if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-1023"]="Preface I. In Chancery II. In Fashion III. A Progress IV. Telescopic Philanthropy V. A Morning Adventure VI. Quite at Home VII. The Ghost’s Walk VIII. Covering a Multitude of Sins IX. Signs and Tokens X. The Law-Writer XI. Our Dear Brother XII. On the Watch XIII. Esther’s Narrative XIV. Deportment XV. Bell Yard XVI. Tom-all-Alone’s XVII. Esther’s Narrative XVIII. Lady Dedlock XIX. Moving On XX. A New Lodger XXI. The Smallweed Family XXII. Mr. Bucket XXIII. Esther’s Narrative XXIV. An Appeal Case XXV. Mrs. Snagsby Sees It All XXVI. Sharpshooters XXVII. More Old Soldiers Than One XXVIII. The Ironmaster XXIX. The Young Man XXX. Esther’s Narrative XXXI. Nurse and Patient XXXII. The Appointed Time XXXIII. Interlopers XXXIV. A Turn of the Screw XXXV. Esther’s Narrative XXXVI. Chesney Wold XXXVII. Jarndyce and Jarndyce XXXVIII. A Struggle XXXIX. Attorney and Client XL. National and Domestic XLI. In Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Room XLII. In Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Chambers XLIII. Esther’s Narrative XLIV. The Letter and the Answer XLV. In Trust XLVI. Stop Him! XLVII. Jo’s Will XLVIII. Closing In XLIX. Dutiful Friendship L. Esther’s Narrative LI. Enlightened LII. Obstinacy LIII. The Track LIV. Springing a Mine LV. Flight LVI. Pursuit LVII. Esther’s Narrative LVIII. A Wintry Day and Night LIX. Esther’s Narrative LX. Perspective LXI. A Discovery LXII. Another Discovery LXIII. Steel and Iron LXIV. Esther’s Narrative LXV. Beginning the World LXVI. Down in Lincolnshire LXVII. The Close of Esther’s Narrative\nA Chancery judge once had the kindness to inform me, as one of a company of some hundred and fifty men and women not labouring under any suspicions of lunacy, that the Court of Chancery, though the shining subject of much popular prejudice (at which point I thought the judge’s eye had a cast in my direction), was almost immaculate. There had been, he admitted, a trivial blemish or so in its rate of progress, but this was exaggerated and had been entirely owing to the “parsimony of the public,” which guilty public, it appeared, had been until lately bent in the most determined manner on by no means enlarging the number of Chancery judges appointed—I believe by Richard the Second, but any other king will do as well.\n\nThis seemed to me too profound a joke to be inserted in the body of this book or I should have restored it to Conversation Kenge or to Mr. Vholes, with one or other of whom I think it must have originated. In such mouths I might have coupled it with an apt quotation from one of Shakespeare’s sonnets:\n\n“My nature is subdued To what it works in, like the dyer’s hand: Pity me, then, and wish I were renewed!”\n\nBut as it is wholesome that the parsimonious public should know what has been doing, and still is doing, in this connexion, I mention here that everything set forth in these pages concerning the Court of Chancery is substantially true, and within the truth. The case of Gridley is in no essential altered from one of actual occurrence, made public by a disinterested person who was professionally acquainted with the whole of the monstrous wrong from beginning to end. At the present moment (August, 1853) there is a suit before the court which was commenced nearly twenty years ago, in which from thirty to forty counsel have been known to appear at one time, in which costs have been incurred to the amount of seventy thousand pounds, which is A FRIENDLY SUIT, and which is (I am assured) no nearer to its termination now than when it was begun. There is another well-known suit in Chancery, not yet decided, which was commenced before the close of the last century and in which more than double the amount of seventy thousand pounds has been swallowed up in costs. If I wanted other authorities for Jarndyce and Jarndyce, I could rain them on these pages, to the shame of—a parsimonious public.\n\nThere is only one other point on which I offer a word of remark. The possibility of what is called spontaneous combustion has been denied since the death of Mr. Krook; and my good friend Mr. Lewes (quite mistaken, as he soon found, in supposing the thing to have been abandoned by all authorities) published some ingenious letters to me at the time when that event was chronicled, arguing that spontaneous combustion could not possibly be. I have no need to observe that I do not wilfully or negligently mislead my readers and that before I wrote that description I took pains to investigate the subject. There are about thirty cases on record, of which the most famous, that of the Countess Cornelia de Baudi Cesenate, was minutely investigated and described by Giuseppe Bianchini, a prebendary of Verona, otherwise distinguished in letters, who published an account of it at Verona in 1731, which he afterwards republished at Rome. The appearances, beyond all rational doubt, observed in that case are the appearances observed in Mr. Krook’s case. The next most famous instance happened at Rheims six years earlier, and the historian in that case is Le Cat, one of the most renowned surgeons produced by France. The subject was a woman, whose husband was ignorantly convicted of having murdered her; but on solemn appeal to a higher court, he was acquitted because it was shown upon the evidence that she had died the death of which this name of spontaneous combustion is given. I do not think it necessary to add to these notable facts, and that general reference to the authorities which will be found at page 30, vol. ii.,* the recorded opinions and experiences of distinguished medical professors, French, English, and Scotch, in more modern days, contenting myself with observing that I shall not abandon the facts until there shall have been a considerable spontaneous combustion of the testimony on which human occurrences are usually received.**\n\nIn Bleak House I have purposely dwelt upon the romantic side of familiar things.\n\n*Transcriber’s note. This referred to a specific page in the printed book. In this Project Gutenberg edition the pertinent information is in Chapter XXX, paragraph 90.\n\n** Another case, very clearly described by a dentist, occurred at the town of Columbus, in the United States of America, quite recently. The subject was a German who kept a liquor-shop and was an inveterate drunkard.\n\nIn Chancery\n\nLondon. Michaelmas term lately over, and the Lord Chancellor sitting in Lincoln’s Inn Hall. Implacable November weather. As much mud in the streets as if the waters had but newly retired from the face of the earth, and it would not be wonderful to meet a Megalosaurus, forty feet long or so, waddling like an elephantine lizard up Holborn Hill. Smoke lowering down from chimney-pots, making a soft black drizzle, with flakes of soot in it as big as full-grown snowflakes—gone into mourning, one might imagine, for the death of the sun. Dogs, undistinguishable in mire. Horses, scarcely better; splashed to their very blinkers. Foot passengers, jostling one another’s umbrellas in a general infection of ill temper, and losing their foot-hold at street-corners, where tens of thousands of other foot passengers have been slipping and sliding since the day broke (if this day ever broke), adding new deposits to the crust upon crust of mud, sticking at those points tenaciously to the pavement, and accumulating at compound interest.\n\nFog everywhere. Fog up the river, where it flows among green aits and meadows; fog down the river, where it rolls defiled among the tiers of shipping and the waterside pollutions of a great (and dirty) city. Fog on the Essex marshes, fog on the Kentish heights. Fog creeping into the cabooses of collier-brigs; fog lying out on the yards and hovering in the rigging of great ships; fog drooping on the gunwales of barges and small boats. Fog in the eyes and throats of ancient Greenwich pensioners, wheezing by the firesides of their wards; fog in the stem and bowl of the afternoon pipe of the wrathful skipper, down in his close cabin; fog cruelly pinching the toes and fingers of his shivering little ’prentice boy on deck. Chance people on the bridges peeping over the parapets into a nether sky of fog, with fog all round them, as if they were up in a balloon and hanging in the misty clouds.\n\nGas looming through the fog in divers places in the streets, much as the sun may, from the spongey fields, be seen to loom by husbandman and ploughboy. Most of the shops lighted two hours before their time—as the gas seems to know, for it has a haggard and unwilling look.\n\nThe raw afternoon is rawest, and the dense fog is densest, and the muddy streets are muddiest near that leaden-headed old obstruction, appropriate ornament for the threshold of a leaden-headed old corporation, Temple Bar. And hard by Temple Bar, in Lincoln’s Inn Hall, at the very heart of the fog, sits the Lord High Chancellor in his High Court of Chancery.\n\nNever can there come fog too thick, never can there come mud and mire too deep, to assort with the groping and floundering condition which this High Court of Chancery, most pestilent of hoary sinners, holds this day in the sight of heaven and earth.\n\nOn such an afternoon, if ever, the Lord High Chancellor ought to be sitting here—as here he is—with a foggy glory round his head, softly fenced in with crimson cloth and curtains, addressed by a large advocate with great whiskers, a little voice, and an interminable brief, and outwardly directing his contemplation to the lantern in the roof, where he can see nothing but fog. On such an afternoon some score of members of the High Court of Chancery bar ought to be—as here they are—mistily engaged in one of the ten thousand stages of an endless cause, tripping one another up on slippery precedents, groping knee-deep in technicalities, running their goat-hair and horsehair warded heads against walls of words and making a pretence of equity with serious faces, as players might. On such an afternoon the various solicitors in the cause, some two or three of whom have inherited it from their fathers, who made a fortune by it, ought to be—as are they not?—ranged in a line, in a long matted well (but you might look in vain for truth at the bottom of it) between the registrar’s red table and the silk gowns, with bills, cross-bills, answers, rejoinders, injunctions, affidavits, issues, references to masters, masters’ reports, mountains of costly nonsense, piled before them. Well may the court be dim, with wasting candles here and there; well may the fog hang heavy in it, as if it would never get out; well may the stained-glass windows lose their colour and admit no light of day into the place; well may the uninitiated from the streets, who peep in through the glass panes in the door, be deterred from entrance by its owlish aspect and by the drawl, languidly echoing to the roof from the padded dais where the Lord High Chancellor looks into the lantern that has no light in it and where the attendant wigs are all stuck in a fog-bank! This is the Court of Chancery, which has its decaying houses and its blighted lands in every shire, which has its worn-out lunatic in every madhouse and its dead in every churchyard, which has its ruined suitor with his slipshod heels and threadbare dress borrowing and begging through the round of every man’s acquaintance, which gives to monied might the means abundantly of wearying out the right, which so exhausts finances, patience, courage, hope, so overthrows the brain and breaks the heart, that there is not an honourable man among its practitioners who would not give—who does not often give—the warning, “Suffer any wrong that can be done you rather than come here!”\nWho happen to be in the Lord Chancellor’s court this murky afternoon besides the Lord Chancellor, the counsel in the cause, two or three counsel who are never in any cause, and the well of solicitors before mentioned? There is the registrar below the judge, in wig and gown; and there are two or three maces, or petty-bags, or privy purses, or whatever they may be, in legal court suits. These are all yawning, for no crumb of amusement ever falls from Jarndyce and Jarndyce (the cause in hand), which was squeezed dry years upon years ago. The short-hand writers, the reporters of the court, and the reporters of the newspapers invariably decamp with the rest of the regulars when Jarndyce and Jarndyce comes on. Their places are a blank. Standing on a seat at the side of the hall, the better to peer into the curtained sanctuary, is a little mad old woman in a squeezed bonnet who is always in court, from its sitting to its rising, and always expecting some incomprehensible judgment to be given in her favour. Some say she really is, or was, a party to a suit, but no one knows for certain because no one cares. She carries some small litter in a reticule which she calls her documents, principally consisting of paper matches and dry lavender. A sallow prisoner has come up, in custody, for the half-dozenth time to make a personal application “to purge himself of his contempt,” which, being a solitary surviving executor who has fallen into a state of conglomeration about accounts of which it is not pretended that he had ever any knowledge, he is not at all likely ever to do. In the meantime his prospects in life are ended. Another ruined suitor, who periodically appears from Shropshire and breaks out into efforts to address the Chancellor at the close of the day’s business and who can by no means be made to understand that the Chancellor is legally ignorant of his existence after making it desolate for a quarter of a century, plants himself in a good place and keeps an eye on the judge, ready to call out “My Lord!” in a voice of sonorous complaint on the instant of his rising. A few lawyers’ clerks and others who know this suitor by sight linger on the chance of his furnishing some fun and enlivening the dismal weather a little.\n\nJarndyce and Jarndyce drones on. This scarecrow of a suit has, in course of time, become so complicated that no man alive knows what it means. The parties to it understand it least, but it has been observed that no two Chancery lawyers can talk about it for five minutes without coming to a total disagreement as to all the premises. Innumerable children have been born into the cause; innumerable young people have married into it; innumerable old people have died out of it. Scores of persons have deliriously found themselves made parties in Jarndyce and Jarndyce without knowing how or why; whole families have inherited legendary hatreds with the suit. The little plaintiff or defendant who was promised a new rocking-horse when Jarndyce and Jarndyce should be settled has grown up, possessed himself of a real horse, and trotted away into the other world. Fair wards of court have faded into mothers and grandmothers; a long procession of Chancellors has come in and gone out; the legion of bills in the suit have been transformed into mere bills of mortality; there are not three Jarndyces left upon the earth perhaps since old Tom Jarndyce in despair blew his brains out at a coffee-house in Chancery Lane; but Jarndyce and Jarndyce still drags its dreary length before the court, perennially hopeless.\n\nJarndyce and Jarndyce has passed into a joke. That is the only good that has ever come of it. It has been death to many, but it is a joke in the profession. Every master in Chancery has had a reference out of it. Every Chancellor was “in it,” for somebody or other, when he was counsel at the bar. Good things have been said about it by blue-nosed, bulbous-shoed old benchers in select port-wine committee after dinner in hall. Articled clerks have been in the habit of fleshing their legal wit upon it. The last Lord Chancellor handled it neatly, when, correcting Mr. Blowers, the eminent silk gown who said that such a thing might happen when the sky rained potatoes, he observed, “or when we get through Jarndyce and Jarndyce, Mr. Blowers”—a pleasantry that particularly tickled the maces, bags, and purses.\n\nHow many people out of the suit Jarndyce and Jarndyce has stretched forth its unwholesome hand to spoil and corrupt would be a very wide question. From the master upon whose impaling files reams of dusty warrants in Jarndyce and Jarndyce have grimly writhed into many shapes, down to the copying-clerk in the Six Clerks’ Office who has copied his tens of thousands of Chancery folio-pages under that eternal heading, no man’s nature has been made better by it. In trickery, evasion, procrastination, spoliation, botheration, under false pretences of all sorts, there are influences that can never come to good. The very solicitors’ boys who have kept the wretched suitors at bay, by protesting time out of mind that Mr. Chizzle, Mizzle, or otherwise was particularly engaged and had appointments until dinner, may have got an extra moral twist and shuffle into themselves out of Jarndyce and Jarndyce. The receiver in the cause has acquired a goodly sum of money by it but has acquired too a distrust of his own mother and a contempt for his own kind. Chizzle, Mizzle, and otherwise have lapsed into a habit of vaguely promising themselves that they will look into that outstanding little matter and see what can be done for Drizzle—who was not well used—when Jarndyce and Jarndyce shall be got out of the office. Shirking and sharking in all their many varieties have been sown broadcast by the ill-fated cause; and even those who have contemplated its history from the outermost circle of such evil have been insensibly tempted into a loose way of letting bad things alone to take their own bad course, and a loose belief that if the world go wrong it was in some off-hand manner never meant to go right.\n\nThus, in the midst of the mud and at the heart of the fog, sits the Lord High Chancellor in his High Court of Chancery.\n\n“Mr. Tangle,” says the Lord High Chancellor, latterly something restless under the eloquence of that learned gentleman.\n\n“Mlud,” says Mr. Tangle. Mr. Tangle knows more of Jarndyce and Jarndyce than anybody. He is famous for it—supposed never to have read anything else since he left school.\n\n“Have you nearly concluded your argument?”\n\n“Mlud, no—variety of points—feel it my duty tsubmit—ludship,” is the reply that slides out of Mr. Tangle.\n\n“Several members of the bar are still to be heard, I believe?” says the Chancellor with a slight smile.\n\nEighteen of Mr. Tangle’s learned friends, each armed with a little summary of eighteen hundred sheets, bob up like eighteen hammers in a pianoforte, make eighteen bows, and drop into their eighteen places of obscurity.\n\n“We will proceed with the hearing on Wednesday fortnight,” says the Chancellor. For the question at issue is only a question of costs, a mere bud on the forest tree of the parent suit, and really will come to a settlement one of these days.\n\nThe Chancellor rises; the bar rises; the prisoner is brought forward in a hurry; the man from Shropshire cries, “My lord!” Maces, bags, and purses indignantly proclaim silence and frown at the man from Shropshire.\n\n“In reference,” proceeds the Chancellor, still on Jarndyce and Jarndyce, “to the young girl—”\n\n“Begludship’s pardon—boy,” says Mr. Tangle prematurely. “In reference,” proceeds the Chancellor with extra distinctness, “to the young girl and boy, the two young people”—Mr. Tangle crushed—“whom I directed to be in attendance to-day and who are now in my private room, I will see them and satisfy myself as to the expediency of making the order for their residing with their uncle.”\n\nMr. Tangle on his legs again. “Begludship’s pardon—dead.”\n\n“With their”—Chancellor looking through his double eye-glass at the papers on his desk—“grandfather.”\n\n“Begludship’s pardon—victim of rash action—brains.”\n\nSuddenly a very little counsel with a terrific bass voice arises, fully inflated, in the back settlements of the fog, and says, “Will your lordship allow me? I appear for him. He is a cousin, several times removed. I am not at the moment prepared to inform the court in what exact remove he is a cousin, but he IS a cousin.”\n\nLeaving this address (delivered like a sepulchral message) ringing in the rafters of the roof, the very little counsel drops, and the fog knows him no more. Everybody looks for him. Nobody can see him.\n\n“I will speak with both the young people,” says the Chancellor anew, “and satisfy myself on the subject of their residing with their cousin. I will mention the matter to-morrow morning when I take my seat.”\n\nThe Chancellor is about to bow to the bar when the prisoner is presented. Nothing can possibly come of the prisoner’s conglomeration but his being sent back to prison, which is soon done. The man from Shropshire ventures another remonstrative “My lord!” but the Chancellor, being aware of him, has dexterously vanished. Everybody else quickly vanishes too. A battery of blue bags is loaded with heavy charges of papers and carried off by clerks; the little mad old woman marches off with her documents; the empty court is locked up. If all the injustice it has committed and all the misery it has caused could only be locked up with it, and the whole burnt away in a great funeral pyre—why so much the better for other parties than the parties in Jarndyce and Jarndyce!\n\nIt is but a glimpse of the world of fashion that we want on this same miry afternoon. It is not so unlike the Court of Chancery but that we may pass from the one scene to the other, as the crow flies. Both the world of fashion and the Court of Chancery are things of precedent and usage: oversleeping Rip Van Winkles who have played at strange games through a deal of thundery weather; sleeping beauties whom the knight will wake one day, when all the stopped spits in the kitchen shall begin to turn prodigiously!\n\nIt is not a large world. Relatively even to this world of ours, which has its limits too (as your Highness shall find when you have made the tour of it and are come to the brink of the void beyond), it is a very little speck. There is much good in it; there are many good and true people in it; it has its appointed place. But the evil of it is that it is a world wrapped up in too much jeweller’s cotton and fine wool, and cannot hear the rushing of the larger worlds, and cannot see them as they circle round the sun. It is a deadened world, and its growth is sometimes unhealthy for want of air.\n\nMy Lady Dedlock has returned to her house in town for a few days previous to her departure for Paris, where her ladyship intends to stay some weeks, after which her movements are uncertain. The fashionable intelligence says so for the comfort of the Parisians, and it knows all fashionable things. To know things otherwise were to be unfashionable. My Lady Dedlock has been down at what she calls, in familiar conversation, her “place” in Lincolnshire. The waters are out in Lincolnshire. An arch of the bridge in the park has been sapped and sopped away. The adjacent low-lying ground for half a mile in breadth is a stagnant river with melancholy trees for islands in it and a surface punctured all over, all day long, with falling rain. My Lady Dedlock’s place has been extremely dreary. The weather for many a day and night has been so wet that the trees seem wet through, and the soft loppings and prunings of the woodman’s axe can make no crash or crackle as they fall. The deer, looking soaked, leave quagmires where they pass. The shot of a rifle loses its sharpness in the moist air, and its smoke moves in a tardy little cloud towards the green rise, coppice-topped, that makes a background for the falling rain. The view from my Lady Dedlock’s own windows is alternately a lead-coloured view and a view in Indian ink. The vases on the stone terrace in the foreground catch the rain all day; and the heavy drops fall—drip, drip, drip—upon the broad flagged pavement, called from old time the Ghost’s Walk, all night. On Sundays the little church in the park is mouldy; the oaken pulpit breaks out into a cold sweat; and there is a general smell and taste as of the ancient Dedlocks in their graves. My Lady Dedlock (who is childless), looking out in the early twilight from her boudoir at a keeper’s lodge and seeing the light of a fire upon the latticed panes, and smoke rising from the chimney, and a child, chased by a woman, running out into the rain to meet the shining figure of a wrapped-up man coming through the gate, has been put quite out of temper. My Lady Dedlock says she has been “bored to death.”\n\nTherefore my Lady Dedlock has come away from the place in Lincolnshire and has left it to the rain, and the crows, and the rabbits, and the deer, and the partridges and pheasants. The pictures of the Dedlocks past and gone have seemed to vanish into the damp walls in mere lowness of spirits, as the housekeeper has passed along the old rooms shutting up the shutters. And when they will next come forth again, the fashionable intelligence—which, like the fiend, is omniscient of the past and present, but not the future—cannot yet undertake to say.\n\nSir Leicester Dedlock is only a baronet, but there is no mightier baronet than he. His family is as old as the hills, and infinitely more respectable. He has a general opinion that the world might get on without hills but would be done up without Dedlocks. He would on the whole admit nature to be a good idea (a little low, perhaps, when not enclosed with a park-fence), but an idea dependent for its execution on your great county families. He is a gentleman of strict conscience, disdainful of all littleness and meanness and ready on the shortest notice to die any death you may please to mention rather than give occasion for the least impeachment of his integrity. He is an honourable, obstinate, truthful, high-spirited, intensely prejudiced, perfectly unreasonable man.\n\nSir Leicester is twenty years, full measure, older than my Lady. He will never see sixty-five again, nor perhaps sixty-six, nor yet sixty-seven. He has a twist of the gout now and then and walks a little stiffly. He is of a worthy presence, with his light-grey hair and whiskers, his fine shirt-frill, his pure-white waistcoat, and his blue coat with bright buttons always buttoned. He is ceremonious, stately, most polite on every occasion to my Lady, and holds her personal attractions in the highest estimation. His gallantry to my Lady, which has never changed since he courted her, is the one little touch of romantic fancy in him.\n\nIndeed, he married her for love. A whisper still goes about that she had not even family; howbeit, Sir Leicester had so much family that perhaps he had enough and could dispense with any more. But she had beauty, pride, ambition, insolent resolve, and sense enough to portion out a legion of fine ladies. Wealth and station, added to these, soon floated her upward, and for years now my Lady Dedlock has been at the centre of the fashionable intelligence and at the top of the fashionable tree.\n\nHow Alexander wept when he had no more worlds to conquer, everybody knows—or has some reason to know by this time, the matter having been rather frequently mentioned. My Lady Dedlock, having conquered HER world, fell not into the melting, but rather into the freezing, mood. An exhausted composure, a worn-out placidity, an equanimity of fatigue not to be ruffled by interest or satisfaction, are the trophies of her victory. She is perfectly well-bred. If she could be translated to heaven to-morrow, she might be expected to ascend without any rapture.\n\nShe has beauty still, and if it be not in its heyday, it is not yet in its autumn. She has a fine face—originally of a character that would be rather called very pretty than handsome, but improved into classicality by the acquired expression of her fashionable state. Her figure is elegant and has the effect of being tall. Not that she is so, but that “the most is made,” as the Honourable Bob Stables has frequently asserted upon oath, “of all her points.” The same authority observes that she is perfectly got up and remarks in commendation of her hair especially that she is the best-groomed woman in the whole stud.\n\nWith all her perfections on her head, my Lady Dedlock has come up from her place in Lincolnshire (hotly pursued by the fashionable intelligence) to pass a few days at her house in town previous to her departure for Paris, where her ladyship intends to stay some weeks, after which her movements are uncertain. And at her house in town, upon this muddy, murky afternoon, presents himself an old-fashioned old gentleman, attorney-at-law and eke solicitor of the High Court of Chancery, who has the honour of acting as legal adviser of the Dedlocks and has as many cast-iron boxes in his office with that name outside as if the present baronet were the coin of the conjuror’s trick and were constantly being juggled through the whole set. Across the hall, and up the stairs, and along the passages, and through the rooms, which are very brilliant in the season and very dismal out of it—fairy-land to visit, but a desert to live in—the old gentleman is conducted by a Mercury in powder to my Lady’s presence.\n\nThe old gentleman is rusty to look at, but is reputed to have made good thrift out of aristocratic marriage settlements and aristocratic wills, and to be very rich. He is surrounded by a mysterious halo of family confidences, of which he is known to be the silent depository. There are noble mausoleums rooted for centuries in retired glades of parks among the growing timber and the fern, which perhaps hold fewer noble secrets than walk abroad among men, shut up in the breast of Mr. Tulkinghorn. He is of what is called the old school—a phrase generally meaning any school that seems never to have been young—and wears knee-breeches tied with ribbons, and gaiters or stockings. One peculiarity of his black clothes and of his black stockings, be they silk or worsted, is that they never shine. Mute, close, irresponsive to any glancing light, his dress is like himself. He never converses when not professionally consulted. He is found sometimes, speechless but quite at home, at corners of dinner-tables in great country houses and near doors of drawing-rooms, concerning which the fashionable intelligence is eloquent, where everybody knows him and where half the Peerage stops to say “How do you do, Mr. Tulkinghorn?” He receives these salutations with gravity and buries them along with the rest of his knowledge.\n\nSir Leicester Dedlock is with my Lady and is happy to see Mr. Tulkinghorn. There is an air of prescription about him which is always agreeable to Sir Leicester; he receives it as a kind of tribute. He likes Mr. Tulkinghorn’s dress; there is a kind of tribute in that too. It is eminently respectable, and likewise, in a general way, retainer-like. It expresses, as it were, the steward of the legal mysteries, the butler of the legal cellar, of the Dedlocks.\n\nHas Mr. Tulkinghorn any idea of this himself? It may be so, or it may not, but there is this remarkable circumstance to be noted in everything associated with my Lady Dedlock as one of a class—as one of the leaders and representatives of her little world. She supposes herself to be an inscrutable Being, quite out of the reach and ken of ordinary mortals—seeing herself in her glass, where indeed she looks so. Yet every dim little star revolving about her, from her maid to the manager of the Italian Opera, knows her weaknesses, prejudices, follies, haughtinesses, and caprices and lives upon as accurate a calculation and as nice a measure of her moral nature as her dressmaker takes of her physical proportions. Is a new dress, a new custom, a new singer, a new dancer, a new form of jewellery, a new dwarf or giant, a new chapel, a new anything, to be set up? There are deferential people in a dozen callings whom my Lady Dedlock suspects of nothing but prostration before her, who can tell you how to manage her as if she were a baby, who do nothing but nurse her all their lives, who, humbly affecting to follow with profound subservience, lead her and her whole troop after them; who, in hooking one, hook all and bear them off as Lemuel Gulliver bore away the stately fleet of the majestic Lilliput. “If you want to address our people, sir,” say Blaze and Sparkle, the jewellers—meaning by our people Lady Dedlock and the rest—“you must remember that you are not dealing with the general public; you must hit our people in their weakest place, and their weakest place is such a place.” “To make this article go down, gentlemen,” say Sheen and Gloss, the mercers, to their friends the manufacturers, “you must come to us, because we know where to have the fashionable people, and we can make it fashionable.” “If you want to get this print upon the tables of my high connexion, sir,” says Mr. Sladdery, the librarian, “or if you want to get this dwarf or giant into the houses of my high connexion, sir, or if you want to secure to this entertainment the patronage of my high connexion, sir, you must leave it, if you please, to me, for I have been accustomed to study the leaders of my high connexion, sir, and I may tell you without vanity that I can turn them round my finger”—in which Mr. Sladdery, who is an honest man, does not exaggerate at all.\n\nTherefore, while Mr. Tulkinghorn may not know what is passing in the Dedlock mind at present, it is very possible that he may.\n\n“My Lady’s cause has been again before the Chancellor, has it, Mr. Tulkinghorn?” says Sir Leicester, giving him his hand.\n\n“Yes. It has been on again to-day,” Mr. Tulkinghorn replies, making one of his quiet bows to my Lady, who is on a sofa near the fire, shading her face with a hand-screen.\n\n“It would be useless to ask,” says my Lady with the dreariness of the place in Lincolnshire still upon her, “whether anything has been done.”\n\n“Nothing that YOU would call anything has been done to-day,” replies Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n“Nor ever will be,” says my Lady.\n\nSir Leicester has no objection to an interminable Chancery suit. It is a slow, expensive, British, constitutional kind of thing. To be sure, he has not a vital interest in the suit in question, her part in which was the only property my Lady brought him; and he has a shadowy impression that for his name—the name of Dedlock—to be in a cause, and not in the title of that cause, is a most ridiculous accident. But he regards the Court of Chancery, even if it should involve an occasional delay of justice and a trifling amount of confusion, as a something devised in conjunction with a variety of other somethings by the perfection of human wisdom for the eternal settlement (humanly speaking) of everything. And he is upon the whole of a fixed opinion that to give the sanction of his countenance to any complaints respecting it would be to encourage some person in the lower classes to rise up somewhere—like Wat Tyler.\n\n“As a few fresh affidavits have been put upon the file,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “and as they are short, and as I proceed upon the troublesome principle of begging leave to possess my clients with any new proceedings in a cause”—cautious man Mr. Tulkinghorn, taking no more responsibility than necessary—“and further, as I see you are going to Paris, I have brought them in my pocket.”\n\n(Sir Leicester was going to Paris too, by the by, but the delight of the fashionable intelligence was in his Lady.)\nMr. Tulkinghorn takes out his papers, asks permission to place them on a golden talisman of a table at my Lady’s elbow, puts on his spectacles, and begins to read by the light of a shaded lamp.\n\n“‘In Chancery. Between John Jarndyce—’”\nMy Lady interrupts, requesting him to miss as many of the formal horrors as he can.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn glances over his spectacles and begins again lower down. My Lady carelessly and scornfully abstracts her attention. Sir Leicester in a great chair looks at the file and appears to have a stately liking for the legal repetitions and prolixities as ranging among the national bulwarks. It happens that the fire is hot where my Lady sits and that the hand-screen is more beautiful than useful, being priceless but small. My Lady, changing her position, sees the papers on the table—looks at them nearer—looks at them nearer still—asks impulsively, “Who copied that?”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn stops short, surprised by my Lady’s animation and her unusual tone.\n“Is it what you people call law-hand?” she asks, looking full at him in her careless way again and toying with her screen.\n\n“Not quite. Probably”—Mr. Tulkinghorn examines it as he speaks—“the legal character which it has was acquired after the original hand was formed. Why do you ask?”\n“Anything to vary this detestable monotony. Oh, go on, do!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn reads again. The heat is greater; my Lady screens her face. Sir Leicester dozes, starts up suddenly, and cries, “Eh? What do you say?”\n“I say I am afraid,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, who had risen hastily, “that Lady Dedlock is ill.”\n\n“Faint,” my Lady murmurs with white lips, “only that; but it is like the faintness of death. Don’t speak to me. Ring, and take me to my room!”\nMr. Tulkinghorn retires into another chamber; bells ring, feet shuffle and patter, silence ensues. Mercury at last begs Mr. Tulkinghorn to return.\n\n“Better now,” quoth Sir Leicester, motioning the lawyer to sit down and read to him alone. “I have been quite alarmed. I never knew my Lady swoon before. But the weather is extremely trying, and she really has been bored to death down at our place in Lincolnshire.”\n\nI have a great deal of difficulty in beginning to write my portion of these pages, for I know I am not clever. I always knew that. I can remember, when I was a very little girl indeed, I used to say to my doll when we were alone together, “Now, Dolly, I am not clever, you know very well, and you must be patient with me, like a dear!” And so she used to sit propped up in a great arm-chair, with her beautiful complexion and rosy lips, staring at me—or not so much at me, I think, as at nothing—while I busily stitched away and told her every one of my secrets.\n\nMy dear old doll! I was such a shy little thing that I seldom dared to open my lips, and never dared to open my heart, to anybody else. It almost makes me cry to think what a relief it used to be to me when I came home from school of a day to run upstairs to my room and say, “Oh, you dear faithful Dolly, I knew you would be expecting me!” and then to sit down on the floor, leaning on the elbow of her great chair, and tell her all I had noticed since we parted. I had always rather a noticing way—not a quick way, oh, no!—a silent way of noticing what passed before me and thinking I should like to understand it better. I have not by any means a quick understanding. When I love a person very tenderly indeed, it seems to brighten. But even that may be my vanity.\n\nI was brought up, from my earliest remembrance—like some of the princesses in the fairy stories, only I was not charming—by my godmother. At least, I only knew her as such. She was a good, good woman! She went to church three times every Sunday, and to morning prayers on Wednesdays and Fridays, and to lectures whenever there were lectures; and never missed. She was handsome; and if she had ever smiled, would have been (I used to think) like an angel—but she never smiled. She was always grave and strict. She was so very good herself, I thought, that the badness of other people made her frown all her life. I felt so different from her, even making every allowance for the differences between a child and a woman; I felt so poor, so trifling, and so far off that I never could be unrestrained with her—no, could never even love her as I wished. It made me very sorry to consider how good she was and how unworthy of her I was, and I used ardently to hope that I might have a better heart; and I talked it over very often with the dear old doll, but I never loved my godmother as I ought to have loved her and as I felt I must have loved her if I had been a better girl.\n\nThis made me, I dare say, more timid and retiring than I naturally was and cast me upon Dolly as the only friend with whom I felt at ease. But something happened when I was still quite a little thing that helped it very much.\n\nI had never heard my mama spoken of. I had never heard of my papa either, but I felt more interested about my mama. I had never worn a black frock, that I could recollect. I had never been shown my mama’s grave. I had never been told where it was. Yet I had never been taught to pray for any relation but my godmother. I had more than once approached this subject of my thoughts with Mrs. Rachael, our only servant, who took my light away when I was in bed (another very good woman, but austere to me), and she had only said, “Esther, good night!” and gone away and left me.\n\nAlthough there were seven girls at the neighbouring school where I was a day boarder, and although they called me little Esther Summerson, I knew none of them at home. All of them were older than I, to be sure (I was the youngest there by a good deal), but there seemed to be some other separation between us besides that, and besides their being far more clever than I was and knowing much more than I did. One of them in the first week of my going to the school (I remember it very well) invited me home to a little party, to my great joy. But my godmother wrote a stiff letter declining for me, and I never went. I never went out at all.\n\nIt was my birthday. There were holidays at school on other birthdays—none on mine. There were rejoicings at home on other birthdays, as I knew from what I heard the girls relate to one another—there were none on mine. My birthday was the most melancholy day at home in the whole year.\n\nI have mentioned that unless my vanity should deceive me (as I know it may, for I may be very vain without suspecting it, though indeed I don’t), my comprehension is quickened when my affection is. My disposition is very affectionate, and perhaps I might still feel such a wound if such a wound could be received more than once with the quickness of that birthday.\n\nDinner was over, and my godmother and I were sitting at the table before the fire. The clock ticked, the fire clicked; not another sound had been heard in the room or in the house for I don’t know how long. I happened to look timidly up from my stitching, across the table at my godmother, and I saw in her face, looking gloomily at me, “It would have been far better, little Esther, that you had had no birthday, that you had never been born!”\n\nI broke out crying and sobbing, and I said, “Oh, dear godmother, tell me, pray do tell me, did Mama die on my birthday?”\n\n“No,” she returned. “Ask me no more, child!”\n\n“Oh, do pray tell me something of her. Do now, at last, dear godmother, if you please! What did I do to her? How did I lose her? Why am I so different from other children, and why is it my fault, dear godmother? No, no, no, don’t go away. Oh, speak to me!”\n\nI was in a kind of fright beyond my grief, and I caught hold of her dress and was kneeling to her. She had been saying all the while, “Let me go!” But now she stood still.\n\nHer darkened face had such power over me that it stopped me in the midst of my vehemence. I put up my trembling little hand to clasp hers or to beg her pardon with what earnestness I might, but withdrew it as she looked at me, and laid it on my fluttering heart. She raised me, sat in her chair, and standing me before her, said slowly in a cold, low voice—I see her knitted brow and pointed finger—“Your mother, Esther, is your disgrace, and you were hers. The time will come—and soon enough—when you will understand this better and will feel it too, as no one save a woman can. I have forgiven her”—but her face did not relent—“the wrong she did to me, and I say no more of it, though it was greater than you will ever know—than any one will ever know but I, the sufferer. For yourself, unfortunate girl, orphaned and degraded from the first of these evil anniversaries, pray daily that the sins of others be not visited upon your head, according to what is written. Forget your mother and leave all other people to forget her who will do her unhappy child that greatest kindness. Now, go!”\n\nShe checked me, however, as I was about to depart from her—so frozen as I was!—and added this, “Submission, self-denial, diligent work, are the preparations for a life begun with such a shadow on it. You are different from other children, Esther, because you were not born, like them, in common sinfulness and wrath. You are set apart.”\n\nI went up to my room, and crept to bed, and laid my doll’s cheek against mine wet with tears, and holding that solitary friend upon my bosom, cried myself to sleep. Imperfect as my understanding of my sorrow was, I knew that I had brought no joy at any time to anybody’s heart and that I was to no one upon earth what Dolly was to me.\n\nDear, dear, to think how much time we passed alone together afterwards, and how often I repeated to the doll the story of my birthday and confided to her that I would try as hard as ever I could to repair the fault I had been born with (of which I confessedly felt guilty and yet innocent) and would strive as I grew up to be industrious, contented, and kind-hearted and to do some good to some one, and win some love to myself if I could. I hope it is not self-indulgent to shed these tears as I think of it. I am very thankful, I am very cheerful, but I cannot quite help their coming to my eyes.\n\nThere! I have wiped them away now and can go on again properly.\n\nI felt the distance between my godmother and myself so much more after the birthday, and felt so sensible of filling a place in her house which ought to have been empty, that I found her more difficult of approach, though I was fervently grateful to her in my heart, than ever. I felt in the same way towards my school companions; I felt in the same way towards Mrs. Rachael, who was a widow; and oh, towards her daughter, of whom she was proud, who came to see her once a fortnight! I was very retired and quiet, and tried to be very diligent.\n\nOne sunny afternoon when I had come home from school with my books and portfolio, watching my long shadow at my side, and as I was gliding upstairs to my room as usual, my godmother looked out of the parlour-door and called me back. Sitting with her, I found—which was very unusual indeed—a stranger. A portly, important-looking gentleman, dressed all in black, with a white cravat, large gold watch seals, a pair of gold eye-glasses, and a large seal-ring upon his little finger.\n\n“This,” said my godmother in an undertone, “is the child.” Then she said in her naturally stern way of speaking, “This is Esther, sir.”\n\nThe gentleman put up his eye-glasses to look at me and said, “Come here, my dear!” He shook hands with me and asked me to take off my bonnet, looking at me all the while. When I had complied, he said, “Ah!” and afterwards “Yes!” And then, taking off his eye-glasses and folding them in a red case, and leaning back in his arm-chair, turning the case about in his two hands, he gave my godmother a nod. Upon that, my godmother said, “You may go upstairs, Esther!” And I made him my curtsy and left him.\n\nIt must have been two years afterwards, and I was almost fourteen, when one dreadful night my godmother and I sat at the fireside. I was reading aloud, and she was listening. I had come down at nine o’clock as I always did to read the Bible to her, and was reading from St. John how our Saviour stooped down, writing with his finger in the dust, when they brought the sinful woman to him.\n\n“So when they continued asking him, he lifted up himself and said unto them, ‘He that is without sin among you, let him first cast a stone at her!’”\n\nI was stopped by my godmother’s rising, putting her hand to her head, and crying out in an awful voice from quite another part of the book, “‘Watch ye, therefore, lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping. And what I say unto you, I say unto all, Watch!’”\n\nIn an instant, while she stood before me repeating these words, she fell down on the floor. I had no need to cry out; her voice had sounded through the house and been heard in the street.\n\nShe was laid upon her bed. For more than a week she lay there, little altered outwardly, with her old handsome resolute frown that I so well knew carved upon her face. Many and many a time, in the day and in the night, with my head upon the pillow by her that my whispers might be plainer to her, I kissed her, thanked her, prayed for her, asked her for her blessing and forgiveness, entreated her to give me the least sign that she knew or heard me. No, no, no. Her face was immovable. To the very last, and even afterwards, her frown remained unsoftened.\n\nOn the day after my poor good godmother was buried, the gentleman in black with the white neckcloth reappeared. I was sent for by Mrs. Rachael, and found him in the same place, as if he had never gone away.\n\n“My name is Kenge,” he said; “you may remember it, my child; Kenge and Carboy, Lincoln’s Inn.”\n\nI replied that I remembered to have seen him once before.\n\n“Pray be seated—here near me. Don’t distress yourself; it’s of no use. Mrs. Rachael, I needn’t inform you who were acquainted with the late Miss Barbary’s affairs, that her means die with her and that this young lady, now her aunt is dead—”\n\n“My aunt, sir!”\n\n“It is really of no use carrying on a deception when no object is to be gained by it,” said Mr. Kenge smoothly, “Aunt in fact, though not in law. Don’t distress yourself! Don’t weep! Don’t tremble! Mrs. Rachael, our young friend has no doubt heard of—the—a—Jarndyce and Jarndyce.”\n\n“Never,” said Mrs. Rachael.\n\n“Is it possible,” pursued Mr. Kenge, putting up his eye-glasses, “that our young friend—I BEG you won’t distress yourself!—never heard of Jarndyce and Jarndyce!”\n\nI shook my head, wondering even what it was.\n\n“Not of Jarndyce and Jarndyce?” said Mr. Kenge, looking over his glasses at me and softly turning the case about and about as if he were petting something. “Not of one of the greatest Chancery suits known? Not of Jarndyce and Jarndyce—the—a—in itself a monument of Chancery practice. In which (I would say) every difficulty, every contingency, every masterly fiction, every form of procedure known in that court, is represented over and over again? It is a cause that could not exist out of this free and great country. I should say that the aggregate of costs in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, Mrs. Rachael”—I was afraid he addressed himself to her because I appeared inattentive—“amounts at the present hour to from SIX-ty to SEVEN-ty THOUSAND POUNDS!” said Mr. Kenge, leaning back in his chair.\n\nI felt very ignorant, but what could I do? I was so entirely unacquainted with the subject that I understood nothing about it even then.\n\n“And she really never heard of the cause!” said Mr. Kenge. “Surprising!”\n\n“Miss Barbary, sir,” returned Mrs. Rachael, “who is now among the Seraphim—”\n\n“I hope so, I am sure,” said Mr. Kenge politely.\n\n“—Wished Esther only to know what would be serviceable to her. And she knows, from any teaching she has had here, nothing more.”\n\n“Well!” said Mr. Kenge. “Upon the whole, very proper. Now to the point,” addressing me. “Miss Barbary, your sole relation (in fact that is, for I am bound to observe that in law you had none) being deceased and it naturally not being to be expected that Mrs. Rachael—”\n\n“Oh, dear no!” said Mrs. Rachael quickly.\n\n“Quite so,” assented Mr. Kenge; “—that Mrs. Rachael should charge herself with your maintenance and support (I beg you won’t distress yourself), you are in a position to receive the renewal of an offer which I was instructed to make to Miss Barbary some two years ago and which, though rejected then, was understood to be renewable under the lamentable circumstances that have since occurred. Now, if I avow that I represent, in Jarndyce and Jarndyce and otherwise, a highly humane, but at the same time singular, man, shall I compromise myself by any stretch of my professional caution?” said Mr. Kenge, leaning back in his chair again and looking calmly at us both.\n\nHe appeared to enjoy beyond everything the sound of his own voice. I couldn’t wonder at that, for it was mellow and full and gave great importance to every word he uttered. He listened to himself with obvious satisfaction and sometimes gently beat time to his own music with his head or rounded a sentence with his hand. I was very much impressed by him—even then, before I knew that he formed himself on the model of a great lord who was his client and that he was generally called Conversation Kenge.\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce,” he pursued, “being aware of the—I would say, desolate—position of our young friend, offers to place her at a first-rate establishment where her education shall be completed, where her comfort shall be secured, where her reasonable wants shall be anticipated, where she shall be eminently qualified to discharge her duty in that station of life unto which it has pleased—shall I say Providence?—to call her.”\n\nMy heart was filled so full, both by what he said and by his affecting manner of saying it, that I was not able to speak, though I tried.\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce,” he went on, “makes no condition beyond expressing his expectation that our young friend will not at any time remove herself from the establishment in question without his knowledge and concurrence. That she will faithfully apply herself to the acquisition of those accomplishments, upon the exercise of which she will be ultimately dependent. That she will tread in the paths of virtue and honour, and—the—a——so forth.”\n\nI was still less able to speak than before.\n\n“Now, what does our young friend say?” proceeded Mr. Kenge. “Take time, take time! I pause for her reply. But take time!”\n\nWhat the destitute subject of such an offer tried to say, I need not repeat. What she did say, I could more easily tell, if it were worth the telling. What she felt, and will feel to her dying hour, I could never relate.\n\nThis interview took place at Windsor, where I had passed (as far as I knew) my whole life. On that day week, amply provided with all necessaries, I left it, inside the stagecoach, for Reading.\n\nMrs. Rachael was too good to feel any emotion at parting, but I was not so good, and wept bitterly. I thought that I ought to have known her better after so many years and ought to have made myself enough of a favourite with her to make her sorry then. When she gave me one cold parting kiss upon my forehead, like a thaw-drop from the stone porch—it was a very frosty day—I felt so miserable and self-reproachful that I clung to her and told her it was my fault, I knew, that she could say good-bye so easily!\n\n“No, Esther!” she returned. “It is your misfortune!”\n\nThe coach was at the little lawn-gate—we had not come out until we heard the wheels—and thus I left her, with a sorrowful heart. She went in before my boxes were lifted to the coach-roof and shut the door. As long as I could see the house, I looked back at it from the window through my tears. My godmother had left Mrs. Rachael all the little property she possessed; and there was to be a sale; and an old hearth-rug with roses on it, which always seemed to me the first thing in the world I had ever seen, was hanging outside in the frost and snow. A day or two before, I had wrapped the dear old doll in her own shawl and quietly laid her—I am half ashamed to tell it—in the garden-earth under the tree that shaded my old window. I had no companion left but my bird, and him I carried with me in his cage.\n\nWhen the house was out of sight, I sat, with my bird-cage in the straw at my feet, forward on the low seat to look out of the high window, watching the frosty trees, that were like beautiful pieces of spar, and the fields all smooth and white with last night’s snow, and the sun, so red but yielding so little heat, and the ice, dark like metal where the skaters and sliders had brushed the snow away. There was a gentleman in the coach who sat on the opposite seat and looked very large in a quantity of wrappings, but he sat gazing out of the other window and took no notice of me.\n\nI thought of my dead godmother, of the night when I read to her, of her frowning so fixedly and sternly in her bed, of the strange place I was going to, of the people I should find there, and what they would be like, and what they would say to me, when a voice in the coach gave me a terrible start.\n\nIt said, “What the de-vil are you crying for?”\n\nI was so frightened that I lost my voice and could only answer in a whisper, “Me, sir?” For of course I knew it must have been the gentleman in the quantity of wrappings, though he was still looking out of his window.\n\n“Yes, you,” he said, turning round.\n\n“I didn’t know I was crying, sir,” I faltered.\n\n“But you are!” said the gentleman. “Look here!” He came quite opposite to me from the other corner of the coach, brushed one of his large furry cuffs across my eyes (but without hurting me), and showed me that it was wet.\n\n“There! Now you know you are,” he said. “Don’t you?”\n“Yes, sir,” I said.\n\n“And what are you crying for?” said the gentleman, “Don’t you want to go there?”\n\n“Where, sir?”\n\n“Where? Why, wherever you are going,” said the gentleman.\n\n“I am very glad to go there, sir,” I answered.\n\n“Well, then! Look glad!” said the gentleman.\n\nI thought he was very strange, or at least that what I could see of him was very strange, for he was wrapped up to the chin, and his face was almost hidden in a fur cap with broad fur straps at the side of his head fastened under his chin; but I was composed again, and not afraid of him. So I told him that I thought I must have been crying because of my godmother’s death and because of Mrs. Rachael’s not being sorry to part with me.\n\n“Confound Mrs. Rachael!” said the gentleman. “Let her fly away in a high wind on a broomstick!”\n\nI began to be really afraid of him now and looked at him with the greatest astonishment. But I thought that he had pleasant eyes, although he kept on muttering to himself in an angry manner and calling Mrs. Rachael names.\n\nAfter a little while he opened his outer wrapper, which appeared to me large enough to wrap up the whole coach, and put his arm down into a deep pocket in the side.\n\n“Now, look here!” he said. “In this paper,” which was nicely folded, “is a piece of the best plum-cake that can be got for money—sugar on the outside an inch thick, like fat on mutton chops. Here’s a little pie (a gem this is, both for size and quality), made in France. And what do you suppose it’s made of? Livers of fat geese. There’s a pie! Now let’s see you eat ’em.”\n\n“Thank you, sir,” I replied; “thank you very much indeed, but I hope you won’t be offended—they are too rich for me.”\n\n“Floored again!” said the gentleman, which I didn’t at all understand, and threw them both out of window.\n\nHe did not speak to me any more until he got out of the coach a little way short of Reading, when he advised me to be a good girl and to be studious, and shook hands with me. I must say I was relieved by his departure. We left him at a milestone. I often walked past it afterwards, and never for a long time without thinking of him and half expecting to meet him. But I never did; and so, as time went on, he passed out of my mind.\n\nWhen the coach stopped, a very neat lady looked up at the window and said, “Miss Donny.”\n\n“No, ma’am, Esther Summerson.”\n\n“That is quite right,” said the lady, “Miss Donny.”\n\nI now understood that she introduced herself by that name, and begged Miss Donny’s pardon for my mistake, and pointed out my boxes at her request. Under the direction of a very neat maid, they were put outside a very small green carriage; and then Miss Donny, the maid, and I got inside and were driven away.\n\n“Everything is ready for you, Esther,” said Miss Donny, “and the scheme of your pursuits has been arranged in exact accordance with the wishes of your guardian, Mr. Jarndyce.”\n\n“Of—did you say, ma’am?”\n\n“Of your guardian, Mr. Jarndyce,” said Miss Donny.\n\nI was so bewildered that Miss Donny thought the cold had been too severe for me and lent me her smelling-bottle.\n\n“Do you know my—guardian, Mr. Jarndyce, ma’am?” I asked after a good deal of hesitation.\n\n“Not personally, Esther,” said Miss Donny; “merely through his solicitors, Messrs. Kenge and Carboy, of London. A very superior gentleman, Mr. Kenge. Truly eloquent indeed. Some of his periods quite majestic!”\n\nI felt this to be very true but was too confused to attend to it. Our speedy arrival at our destination, before I had time to recover myself, increased my confusion, and I never shall forget the uncertain and the unreal air of everything at Greenleaf (Miss Donny’s house) that afternoon!\n\nBut I soon became used to it. I was so adapted to the routine of Greenleaf before long that I seemed to have been there a great while and almost to have dreamed rather than really lived my old life at my godmother’s. Nothing could be more precise, exact, and orderly than Greenleaf. There was a time for everything all round the dial of the clock, and everything was done at its appointed moment.\n\nWe were twelve boarders, and there were two Miss Donnys, twins. It was understood that I would have to depend, by and by, on my qualifications as a governess, and I was not only instructed in everything that was taught at Greenleaf, but was very soon engaged in helping to instruct others. Although I was treated in every other respect like the rest of the school, this single difference was made in my case from the first. As I began to know more, I taught more, and so in course of time I had plenty to do, which I was very fond of doing because it made the dear girls fond of me. At last, whenever a new pupil came who was a little downcast and unhappy, she was so sure—indeed I don’t know why—to make a friend of me that all new-comers were confided to my care. They said I was so gentle, but I am sure THEY were! I often thought of the resolution I had made on my birthday to try to be industrious, contented, and true-hearted and to do some good to some one and win some love if I could; and indeed, indeed, I felt almost ashamed to have done so little and have won so much.\n\nI passed at Greenleaf six happy, quiet years. I never saw in any face there, thank heaven, on my birthday, that it would have been better if I had never been born. When the day came round, it brought me so many tokens of affectionate remembrance that my room was beautiful with them from New Year’s Day to Christmas.\n\nIn those six years I had never been away except on visits at holiday time in the neighbourhood. After the first six months or so I had taken Miss Donny’s advice in reference to the propriety of writing to Mr. Kenge to say that I was happy and grateful, and with her approval I had written such a letter. I had received a formal answer acknowledging its receipt and saying, “We note the contents thereof, which shall be duly communicated to our client.” After that I sometimes heard Miss Donny and her sister mention how regular my accounts were paid, and about twice a year I ventured to write a similar letter. I always received by return of post exactly the same answer in the same round hand, with the signature of Kenge and Carboy in another writing, which I supposed to be Mr. Kenge’s.\n\nIt seems so curious to me to be obliged to write all this about myself! As if this narrative were the narrative of MY life! But my little body will soon fall into the background now.\n\nSix quiet years (I find I am saying it for the second time) I had passed at Greenleaf, seeing in those around me, as it might be in a looking-glass, every stage of my own growth and change there, when, one November morning, I received this letter. I omit the date.\n\nOld Square, Lincoln’s Inn\n\nMadam,\n\nJarndyce and Jarndyce\n\nOur clt Mr. Jarndyce being abt to rece into his house, under an Order of the Ct of Chy, a Ward of the Ct in this cause, for whom he wishes to secure an elgble compn, directs us to inform you that he will be glad of your serces in the afsd capacity.\n\nWe have arrngd for your being forded, carriage free, pr eight o’clock coach from Reading, on Monday morning next, to White Horse Cellar, Piccadilly, London, where one of our clks will be in waiting to convey you to our offe as above.\n\nWe are, Madam, Your obedt Servts,\n\nKenge and Carboy\n\nMiss Esther Summerson\n\nOh, never, never, never shall I forget the emotion this letter caused in the house! It was so tender in them to care so much for me, it was so gracious in that father who had not forgotten me to have made my orphan way so smooth and easy and to have inclined so many youthful natures towards me, that I could hardly bear it. Not that I would have had them less sorry—I am afraid not; but the pleasure of it, and the pain of it, and the pride and joy of it, and the humble regret of it were so blended that my heart seemed almost breaking while it was full of rapture.\n\nThe letter gave me only five days’ notice of my removal. When every minute added to the proofs of love and kindness that were given me in those five days, and when at last the morning came and when they took me through all the rooms that I might see them for the last time, and when some cried, “Esther, dear, say good-bye to me here at my bedside, where you first spoke so kindly to me!” and when others asked me only to write their names, “With Esther’s love,” and when they all surrounded me with their parting presents and clung to me weeping and cried, “What shall we do when dear, dear Esther’s gone!” and when I tried to tell them how forbearing and how good they had all been to me and how I blessed and thanked them every one, what a heart I had!\n\nAnd when the two Miss Donnys grieved as much to part with me as the least among them, and when the maids said, “Bless you, miss, wherever you go!” and when the ugly lame old gardener, who I thought had hardly noticed me in all those years, came panting after the coach to give me a little nosegay of geraniums and told me I had been the light of his eyes—indeed the old man said so!—what a heart I had then!\n\nAnd could I help it if with all this, and the coming to the little school, and the unexpected sight of the poor children outside waving their hats and bonnets to me, and of a grey-haired gentleman and lady whose daughter I had helped to teach and at whose house I had visited (who were said to be the proudest people in all that country), caring for nothing but calling out, “Good-bye, Esther. May you be very happy!”—could I help it if I was quite bowed down in the coach by myself and said “Oh, I am so thankful, I am so thankful!” many times over!\n\nBut of course I soon considered that I must not take tears where I was going after all that had been done for me. Therefore, of course, I made myself sob less and persuaded myself to be quiet by saying very often, “Esther, now you really must! This WILL NOT do!” I cheered myself up pretty well at last, though I am afraid I was longer about it than I ought to have been; and when I had cooled my eyes with lavender water, it was time to watch for London.\n\nI was quite persuaded that we were there when we were ten miles off, and when we really were there, that we should never get there. However, when we began to jolt upon a stone pavement, and particularly when every other conveyance seemed to be running into us, and we seemed to be running into every other conveyance, I began to believe that we really were approaching the end of our journey. Very soon afterwards we stopped.\n\nA young gentleman who had inked himself by accident addressed me from the pavement and said, “I am from Kenge and Carboy’s, miss, of Lincoln’s Inn.”\n\n“If you please, sir,” said I.\n\nHe was very obliging, and as he handed me into a fly after superintending the removal of my boxes, I asked him whether there was a great fire anywhere? For the streets were so full of dense brown smoke that scarcely anything was to be seen.\n\n“Oh, dear no, miss,” he said. “This is a London particular.”\n\nI had never heard of such a thing.\n\n“A fog, miss,” said the young gentleman.\n\n“Oh, indeed!” said I.\n\nWe drove slowly through the dirtiest and darkest streets that ever were seen in the world (I thought) and in such a distracting state of confusion that I wondered how the people kept their senses, until we passed into sudden quietude under an old gateway and drove on through a silent square until we came to an odd nook in a corner, where there was an entrance up a steep, broad flight of stairs, like an entrance to a church. And there really was a churchyard outside under some cloisters, for I saw the gravestones from the staircase window.\n\nThis was Kenge and Carboy’s. The young gentleman showed me through an outer office into Mr. Kenge’s room—there was no one in it—and politely put an arm-chair for me by the fire. He then called my attention to a little looking-glass hanging from a nail on one side of the chimney-piece.\n\n“In case you should wish to look at yourself, miss, after the journey, as you’re going before the Chancellor. Not that it’s requisite, I am sure,” said the young gentleman civilly.\n\n“Going before the Chancellor?” I said, startled for a moment.\n\n“Only a matter of form, miss,” returned the young gentleman. “Mr. Kenge is in court now. He left his compliments, and would you partake of some refreshment”—there were biscuits and a decanter of wine on a small table—“and look over the paper,” which the young gentleman gave me as he spoke. He then stirred the fire and left me.\n\nEverything was so strange—the stranger from its being night in the day-time, the candles burning with a white flame, and looking raw and cold—that I read the words in the newspaper without knowing what they meant and found myself reading the same words repeatedly. As it was of no use going on in that way, I put the paper down, took a peep at my bonnet in the glass to see if it was neat, and looked at the room, which was not half lighted, and at the shabby, dusty tables, and at the piles of writings, and at a bookcase full of the most inexpressive-looking books that ever had anything to say for themselves. Then I went on, thinking, thinking, thinking; and the fire went on, burning, burning, burning; and the candles went on flickering and guttering, and there were no snuffers—until the young gentleman by and by brought a very dirty pair—for two hours.\n\nAt last Mr. Kenge came. HE was not altered, but he was surprised to see how altered I was and appeared quite pleased. “As you are going to be the companion of the young lady who is now in the Chancellor’s private room, Miss Summerson,” he said, “we thought it well that you should be in attendance also. You will not be discomposed by the Lord Chancellor, I dare say?”\n\n“No, sir,” I said, “I don’t think I shall,” really not seeing on consideration why I should be.\n\nSo Mr. Kenge gave me his arm and we went round the corner, under a colonnade, and in at a side door. And so we came, along a passage, into a comfortable sort of room where a young lady and a young gentleman were standing near a great, loud-roaring fire. A screen was interposed between them and it, and they were leaning on the screen, talking.\n\nThey both looked up when I came in, and I saw in the young lady, with the fire shining upon her, such a beautiful girl! With such rich golden hair, such soft blue eyes, and such a bright, innocent, trusting face!\n\n“Miss Ada,” said Mr. Kenge, “this is Miss Summerson.”\n\nShe came to meet me with a smile of welcome and her hand extended, but seemed to change her mind in a moment and kissed me. In short, she had such a natural, captivating, winning manner that in a few minutes we were sitting in the window-seat, with the light of the fire upon us, talking together as free and happy as could be.\n\nWhat a load off my mind! It was so delightful to know that she could confide in me and like me! It was so good of her, and so encouraging to me!\n\nThe young gentleman was her distant cousin, she told me, and his name Richard Carstone. He was a handsome youth with an ingenuous face and a most engaging laugh; and after she had called him up to where we sat, he stood by us, in the light of the fire, talking gaily, like a light-hearted boy. He was very young, not more than nineteen then, if quite so much, but nearly two years older than she was. They were both orphans and (what was very unexpected and curious to me) had never met before that day. Our all three coming together for the first time in such an unusual place was a thing to talk about, and we talked about it; and the fire, which had left off roaring, winked its red eyes at us—as Richard said—like a drowsy old Chancery lion.\n\nWe conversed in a low tone because a full-dressed gentleman in a bag wig frequently came in and out, and when he did so, we could hear a drawling sound in the distance, which he said was one of the counsel in our case addressing the Lord Chancellor. He told Mr. Kenge that the Chancellor would be up in five minutes; and presently we heard a bustle and a tread of feet, and Mr. Kenge said that the Court had risen and his lordship was in the next room.\n\nThe gentleman in the bag wig opened the door almost directly and requested Mr. Kenge to come in. Upon that, we all went into the next room, Mr. Kenge first, with my darling—it is so natural to me now that I can’t help writing it; and there, plainly dressed in black and sitting in an arm-chair at a table near the fire, was his lordship, whose robe, trimmed with beautiful gold lace, was thrown upon another chair. He gave us a searching look as we entered, but his manner was both courtly and kind.\n\nThe gentleman in the bag wig laid bundles of papers on his lordship’s table, and his lordship silently selected one and turned over the leaves.\n\n“Miss Clare,” said the Lord Chancellor. “Miss Ada Clare?”\n\nMr. Kenge presented her, and his lordship begged her to sit down near him. That he admired her and was interested by her even I could see in a moment. It touched me that the home of such a beautiful young creature should be represented by that dry, official place. The Lord High Chancellor, at his best, appeared so poor a substitute for the love and pride of parents.\n\n“The Jarndyce in question,” said the Lord Chancellor, still turning over leaves, “is Jarndyce of Bleak House.”\n\n“Jarndyce of Bleak House, my lord,” said Mr. Kenge.\n\n“A dreary name,” said the Lord Chancellor.\n\n“But not a dreary place at present, my lord,” said Mr. Kenge.\n\n“And Bleak House,” said his lordship, “is in—”\n\n“Hertfordshire, my lord.”\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House is not married?” said his lordship.\n\n“He is not, my lord,” said Mr. Kenge.\nA pause.\n\n“Young Mr. Richard Carstone is present?” said the Lord Chancellor, glancing towards him.\n\nRichard bowed and stepped forward.\n\n“Hum!” said the Lord Chancellor, turning over more leaves.\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House, my lord,” Mr. Kenge observed in a low voice, “if I may venture to remind your lordship, provides a suitable companion for—”\n\n“For Mr. Richard Carstone?” I thought (but I am not quite sure) I heard his lordship say in an equally low voice and with a smile.\n\n“For Miss Ada Clare. This is the young lady. Miss Summerson.”\n\nHis lordship gave me an indulgent look and acknowledged my curtsy very graciously.\n\n“Miss Summerson is not related to any party in the cause, I think?”\n\n“No, my lord.”\n\nMr. Kenge leant over before it was quite said and whispered. His lordship, with his eyes upon his papers, listened, nodded twice or thrice, turned over more leaves, and did not look towards me again until we were going away.\n\nMr. Kenge now retired, and Richard with him, to where I was, near the door, leaving my pet (it is so natural to me that again I can’t help it!) sitting near the Lord Chancellor, with whom his lordship spoke a little part, asking her, as she told me afterwards, whether she had well reflected on the proposed arrangement, and if she thought she would be happy under the roof of Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House, and why she thought so? Presently he rose courteously and released her, and then he spoke for a minute or two with Richard Carstone, not seated, but standing, and altogether with more ease and less ceremony, as if he still knew, though he WAS Lord Chancellor, how to go straight to the candour of a boy.\n\n“Very well!” said his lordship aloud. “I shall make the order. Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House has chosen, so far as I may judge,” and this was when he looked at me, “a very good companion for the young lady, and the arrangement altogether seems the best of which the circumstances admit.”\n\nHe dismissed us pleasantly, and we all went out, very much obliged to him for being so affable and polite, by which he had certainly lost no dignity but seemed to us to have gained some.\n\nWhen we got under the colonnade, Mr. Kenge remembered that he must go back for a moment to ask a question and left us in the fog, with the Lord Chancellor’s carriage and servants waiting for him to come out.\n\n“Well!” said Richard Carstone. “THAT’S over! And where do we go next, Miss Summerson?”\n\n“Don’t you know?” I said.\n\n“Not in the least,” said he.\n\n“And don’t YOU know, my love?” I asked Ada.\n\n“No!” said she. “Don’t you?”\n“Not at all!” said I.\n\nWe looked at one another, half laughing at our being like the children in the wood, when a curious little old woman in a squeezed bonnet and carrying a reticule came curtsying and smiling up to us with an air of great ceremony.\n\n“Oh!” said she. “The wards in Jarndyce! Ve-ry happy, I am sure, to have the honour! It is a good omen for youth, and hope, and beauty when they find themselves in this place, and don’t know what’s to come of it.”\n\n“Mad!” whispered Richard, not thinking she could hear him.\n\n“Right! Mad, young gentleman,” she returned so quickly that he was quite abashed. “I was a ward myself. I was not mad at that time,” curtsying low and smiling between every little sentence. “I had youth and hope. I believe, beauty. It matters very little now. Neither of the three served or saved me. I have the honour to attend court regularly. With my documents. I expect a judgment. Shortly. On the Day of Judgment. I have discovered that the sixth seal mentioned in the Revelations is the Great Seal. It has been open a long time! Pray accept my blessing.”\n\nAs Ada was a little frightened, I said, to humour the poor old lady, that we were much obliged to her.\n\n“Ye-es!” she said mincingly. “I imagine so. And here is Conversation Kenge. With HIS documents! How does your honourable worship do?”\n\n“Quite well, quite well! Now don’t be troublesome, that’s a good soul!” said Mr. Kenge, leading the way back.\n\n“By no means,” said the poor old lady, keeping up with Ada and me. “Anything but troublesome. I shall confer estates on both—which is not being troublesome, I trust? I expect a judgment. Shortly. On the Day of Judgment. This is a good omen for you. Accept my blessing!”\n\nShe stopped at the bottom of the steep, broad flight of stairs; but we looked back as we went up, and she was still there, saying, still with a curtsy and a smile between every little sentence, “Youth. And hope. And beauty. And Chancery. And Conversation Kenge! Ha! Pray accept my blessing!”\nTelescopic Philanthropy\n\nWe were to pass the night, Mr. Kenge told us when we arrived in his room, at Mrs. Jellyby’s; and then he turned to me and said he took it for granted I knew who Mrs. Jellyby was.\n\n“I really don’t, sir,” I returned. “Perhaps Mr. Carstone—or Miss Clare—”\n\nBut no, they knew nothing whatever about Mrs. Jellyby. “In-deed! Mrs. Jellyby,” said Mr. Kenge, standing with his back to the fire and casting his eyes over the dusty hearth-rug as if it were Mrs. Jellyby’s biography, “is a lady of very remarkable strength of character who devotes herself entirely to the public. She has devoted herself to an extensive variety of public subjects at various times and is at present (until something else attracts her) devoted to the subject of Africa, with a view to the general cultivation of the coffee berry—AND the natives—and the happy settlement, on the banks of the African rivers, of our superabundant home population. Mr. Jarndyce, who is desirous to aid any work that is considered likely to be a good work and who is much sought after by philanthropists, has, I believe, a very high opinion of Mrs. Jellyby.”\n\nMr. Kenge, adjusting his cravat, then looked at us.\n\n“And Mr. Jellyby, sir?” suggested Richard.\n\n“Ah! Mr. Jellyby,” said Mr. Kenge, “is—a—I don’t know that I can describe him to you better than by saying that he is the husband of Mrs. Jellyby.”\n\n“A nonentity, sir?” said Richard with a droll look.\n\n“I don’t say that,” returned Mr. Kenge gravely. “I can’t say that, indeed, for I know nothing whatever OF Mr. Jellyby. I never, to my knowledge, had the pleasure of seeing Mr. Jellyby. He may be a very superior man, but he is, so to speak, merged—merged—in the more shining qualities of his wife.” Mr. Kenge proceeded to tell us that as the road to Bleak House would have been very long, dark, and tedious on such an evening, and as we had been travelling already, Mr. Jarndyce had himself proposed this arrangement. A carriage would be at Mrs. Jellyby’s to convey us out of town early in the forenoon of to-morrow.\n\nHe then rang a little bell, and the young gentleman came in. Addressing him by the name of Guppy, Mr. Kenge inquired whether Miss Summerson’s boxes and the rest of the baggage had been “sent round.” Mr. Guppy said yes, they had been sent round, and a coach was waiting to take us round too as soon as we pleased.\n\n“Then it only remains,” said Mr. Kenge, shaking hands with us, “for me to express my lively satisfaction in (good day, Miss Clare!) the arrangement this day concluded and my (GOOD-bye to you, Miss Summerson!) lively hope that it will conduce to the happiness, the (glad to have had the honour of making your acquaintance, Mr. Carstone!) welfare, the advantage in all points of view, of all concerned! Guppy, see the party safely there.”\n\n“Where IS ‘there,’ Mr. Guppy?” said Richard as we went downstairs.\n\n“No distance,” said Mr. Guppy; “round in Thavies Inn, you know.”\n\n“I can’t say I know where it is, for I come from Winchester and am strange in London.”\n\n“Only round the corner,” said Mr. Guppy. “We just twist up Chancery Lane, and cut along Holborn, and there we are in four minutes’ time, as near as a toucher. This is about a London particular NOW, ain’t it, miss?” He seemed quite delighted with it on my account.\n\n“The fog is very dense indeed!” said I.\n\n“Not that it affects you, though, I’m sure,” said Mr. Guppy, putting up the steps. “On the contrary, it seems to do you good, miss, judging from your appearance.”\n\nI knew he meant well in paying me this compliment, so I laughed at myself for blushing at it when he had shut the door and got upon the box; and we all three laughed and chatted about our inexperience and the strangeness of London until we turned up under an archway to our destination—a narrow street of high houses like an oblong cistern to hold the fog. There was a confused little crowd of people, principally children, gathered about the house at which we stopped, which had a tarnished brass plate on the door with the inscription JELLYBY.\n\n“Don’t be frightened!” said Mr. Guppy, looking in at the coach-window. “One of the young Jellybys been and got his head through the area railings!”\n\n“Oh, poor child,” said I; “let me out, if you please!”\n\n“Pray be careful of yourself, miss. The young Jellybys are always up to something,” said Mr. Guppy.\n\nI made my way to the poor child, who was one of the dirtiest little unfortunates I ever saw, and found him very hot and frightened and crying loudly, fixed by the neck between two iron railings, while a milkman and a beadle, with the kindest intentions possible, were endeavouring to drag him back by the legs, under a general impression that his skull was compressible by those means. As I found (after pacifying him) that he was a little boy with a naturally large head, I thought that perhaps where his head could go, his body could follow, and mentioned that the best mode of extrication might be to push him forward. This was so favourably received by the milkman and beadle that he would immediately have been pushed into the area if I had not held his pinafore while Richard and Mr. Guppy ran down through the kitchen to catch him when he should be released. At last he was happily got down without any accident, and then he began to beat Mr. Guppy with a hoop-stick in quite a frantic manner.\n\nNobody had appeared belonging to the house except a person in pattens, who had been poking at the child from below with a broom; I don’t know with what object, and I don’t think she did. I therefore supposed that Mrs. Jellyby was not at home, and was quite surprised when the person appeared in the passage without the pattens, and going up to the back room on the first floor before Ada and me, announced us as, “Them two young ladies, Missis Jellyby!” We passed several more children on the way up, whom it was difficult to avoid treading on in the dark; and as we came into Mrs. Jellyby’s presence, one of the poor little things fell downstairs—down a whole flight (as it sounded to me), with a great noise.\n\nMrs. Jellyby, whose face reflected none of the uneasiness which we could not help showing in our own faces as the dear child’s head recorded its passage with a bump on every stair—Richard afterwards said he counted seven, besides one for the landing—received us with perfect equanimity. She was a pretty, very diminutive, plump woman of from forty to fifty, with handsome eyes, though they had a curious habit of seeming to look a long way off. As if—I am quoting Richard again—they could see nothing nearer than Africa!\n\n“I am very glad indeed,” said Mrs. Jellyby in an agreeable voice, “to have the pleasure of receiving you. I have a great respect for Mr. Jarndyce, and no one in whom he is interested can be an object of indifference to me.”\n\nWe expressed our acknowledgments and sat down behind the door, where there was a lame invalid of a sofa. Mrs. Jellyby had very good hair but was too much occupied with her African duties to brush it. The shawl in which she had been loosely muffled dropped onto her chair when she advanced to us; and as she turned to resume her seat, we could not help noticing that her dress didn’t nearly meet up the back and that the open space was railed across with a lattice-work of stay-lace—like a summer-house.\n\nThe room, which was strewn with papers and nearly filled by a great writing-table covered with similar litter, was, I must say, not only very untidy but very dirty. We were obliged to take notice of that with our sense of sight, even while, with our sense of hearing, we followed the poor child who had tumbled downstairs: I think into the back kitchen, where somebody seemed to stifle him.\n\nBut what principally struck us was a jaded and unhealthy-looking though by no means plain girl at the writing-table, who sat biting the feather of her pen and staring at us. I suppose nobody ever was in such a state of ink. And from her tumbled hair to her pretty feet, which were disfigured with frayed and broken satin slippers trodden down at heel, she really seemed to have no article of dress upon her, from a pin upwards, that was in its proper condition or its right place.\n\n“You find me, my dears,” said Mrs. Jellyby, snuffing the two great office candles in tin candlesticks, which made the room taste strongly of hot tallow (the fire had gone out, and there was nothing in the grate but ashes, a bundle of wood, and a poker), “you find me, my dears, as usual, very busy; but that you will excuse. The African project at present employs my whole time. It involves me in correspondence with public bodies and with private individuals anxious for the welfare of their species all over the country. I am happy to say it is advancing. We hope by this time next year to have from a hundred and fifty to two hundred healthy families cultivating coffee and educating the natives of Borrioboola-Gha, on the left bank of the Niger.”\n\nAs Ada said nothing, but looked at me, I said it must be very gratifying.\n\n“It IS gratifying,” said Mrs. Jellyby. “It involves the devotion of all my energies, such as they are; but that is nothing, so that it succeeds; and I am more confident of success every day. Do you know, Miss Summerson, I almost wonder that YOU never turned your thoughts to Africa.”\n\nThis application of the subject was really so unexpected to me that I was quite at a loss how to receive it. I hinted that the climate—\n\n“The finest climate in the world!” said Mrs. Jellyby.\n\n“Indeed, ma’am?”\n\n“Certainly. With precaution,” said Mrs. Jellyby. “You may go into Holborn, without precaution, and be run over. You may go into Holborn, with precaution, and never be run over. Just so with Africa.”\n\nI said, “No doubt.” I meant as to Holborn.\n\n“If you would like,” said Mrs. Jellyby, putting a number of papers towards us, “to look over some remarks on that head, and on the general subject, which have been extensively circulated, while I finish a letter I am now dictating to my eldest daughter, who is my amanuensis—”\n\nThe girl at the table left off biting her pen and made a return to our recognition, which was half bashful and half sulky.\n\n“—I shall then have finished for the present,” proceeded Mrs. Jellyby with a sweet smile, “though my work is never done. Where are you, Caddy?”\n\n“‘Presents her compliments to Mr. Swallow, and begs—’” said Caddy.\n\n“‘And begs,’” said Mrs. Jellyby, dictating, “‘to inform him, in reference to his letter of inquiry on the African project—’ No, Peepy! Not on my account!”\n\nPeepy (so self-named) was the unfortunate child who had fallen downstairs, who now interrupted the correspondence by presenting himself, with a strip of plaster on his forehead, to exhibit his wounded knees, in which Ada and I did not know which to pity most—the bruises or the dirt. Mrs. Jellyby merely added, with the serene composure with which she said everything, “Go along, you naughty Peepy!” and fixed her fine eyes on Africa again.\n\nHowever, as she at once proceeded with her dictation, and as I interrupted nothing by doing it, I ventured quietly to stop poor Peepy as he was going out and to take him up to nurse. He looked very much astonished at it and at Ada’s kissing him, but soon fell fast asleep in my arms, sobbing at longer and longer intervals, until he was quiet. I was so occupied with Peepy that I lost the letter in detail, though I derived such a general impression from it of the momentous importance of Africa, and the utter insignificance of all other places and things, that I felt quite ashamed to have thought so little about it.\n\n“Six o’clock!” said Mrs. Jellyby. “And our dinner hour is nominally (for we dine at all hours) five! Caddy, show Miss Clare and Miss Summerson their rooms. You will like to make some change, perhaps? You will excuse me, I know, being so much occupied. Oh, that very bad child! Pray put him down, Miss Summerson!”\n\nI begged permission to retain him, truly saying that he was not at all troublesome, and carried him upstairs and laid him on my bed. Ada and I had two upper rooms with a door of communication between. They were excessively bare and disorderly, and the curtain to my window was fastened up with a fork.\n\n“You would like some hot water, wouldn’t you?” said Miss Jellyby, looking round for a jug with a handle to it, but looking in vain.\n\n“If it is not being troublesome,” said we.\n\n“Oh, it’s not the trouble,” returned Miss Jellyby; “the question is, if there IS any.”\n\nThe evening was so very cold and the rooms had such a marshy smell that I must confess it was a little miserable, and Ada was half crying. We soon laughed, however, and were busily unpacking when Miss Jellyby came back to say that she was sorry there was no hot water, but they couldn’t find the kettle, and the boiler was out of order.\n\nWe begged her not to mention it and made all the haste we could to get down to the fire again. But all the little children had come up to the landing outside to look at the phenomenon of Peepy lying on my bed, and our attention was distracted by the constant apparition of noses and fingers in situations of danger between the hinges of the doors. It was impossible to shut the door of either room, for my lock, with no knob to it, looked as if it wanted to be wound up; and though the handle of Ada’s went round and round with the greatest smoothness, it was attended with no effect whatever on the door. Therefore I proposed to the children that they should come in and be very good at my table, and I would tell them the story of Little Red Riding Hood while I dressed; which they did, and were as quiet as mice, including Peepy, who awoke opportunely before the appearance of the wolf.\n\nWhen we went downstairs we found a mug with “A Present from Tunbridge Wells” on it lighted up in the staircase window with a floating wick, and a young woman, with a swelled face bound up in a flannel bandage blowing the fire of the drawing-room (now connected by an open door with Mrs. Jellyby’s room) and choking dreadfully. It smoked to that degree, in short, that we all sat coughing and crying with the windows open for half an hour, during which Mrs. Jellyby, with the same sweetness of temper, directed letters about Africa. Her being so employed was, I must say, a great relief to me, for Richard told us that he had washed his hands in a pie-dish and that they had found the kettle on his dressing-table, and he made Ada laugh so that they made me laugh in the most ridiculous manner.\n\nSoon after seven o’clock we went down to dinner, carefully, by Mrs. Jellyby’s advice, for the stair-carpets, besides being very deficient in stair-wires, were so torn as to be absolute traps. We had a fine cod-fish, a piece of roast beef, a dish of cutlets, and a pudding; an excellent dinner, if it had had any cooking to speak of, but it was almost raw. The young woman with the flannel bandage waited, and dropped everything on the table wherever it happened to go, and never moved it again until she put it on the stairs. The person I had seen in pattens, who I suppose to have been the cook, frequently came and skirmished with her at the door, and there appeared to be ill will between them.\n\nAll through dinner—which was long, in consequence of such accidents as the dish of potatoes being mislaid in the coal skuttle and the handle of the corkscrew coming off and striking the young woman in the chin—Mrs. Jellyby preserved the evenness of her disposition. She told us a great deal that was interesting about Borrioboola-Gha and the natives, and received so many letters that Richard, who sat by her, saw four envelopes in the gravy at once. Some of the letters were proceedings of ladies’ committees or resolutions of ladies’ meetings, which she read to us; others were applications from people excited in various ways about the cultivation of coffee, and natives; others required answers, and these she sent her eldest daughter from the table three or four times to write. She was full of business and undoubtedly was, as she had told us, devoted to the cause.\n\nI was a little curious to know who a mild bald gentleman in spectacles was, who dropped into a vacant chair (there was no top or bottom in particular) after the fish was taken away and seemed passively to submit himself to Borrioboola-Gha but not to be actively interested in that settlement. As he never spoke a word, he might have been a native but for his complexion. It was not until we left the table and he remained alone with Richard that the possibility of his being Mr. Jellyby ever entered my head. But he WAS Mr. Jellyby; and a loquacious young man called Mr. Quale, with large shining knobs for temples and his hair all brushed to the back of his head, who came in the evening, and told Ada he was a philanthropist, also informed her that he called the matrimonial alliance of Mrs. Jellyby with Mr. Jellyby the union of mind and matter.\n\nThis young man, besides having a great deal to say for himself about Africa and a project of his for teaching the coffee colonists to teach the natives to turn piano-forte legs and establish an export trade, delighted in drawing Mrs. Jellyby out by saying, “I believe now, Mrs. Jellyby, you have received as many as from one hundred and fifty to two hundred letters respecting Africa in a single day, have you not?” or, “If my memory does not deceive me, Mrs. Jellyby, you once mentioned that you had sent off five thousand circulars from one post-office at one time?”—always repeating Mrs. Jellyby’s answer to us like an interpreter. During the whole evening, Mr. Jellyby sat in a corner with his head against the wall as if he were subject to low spirits. It seemed that he had several times opened his mouth when alone with Richard after dinner, as if he had something on his mind, but had always shut it again, to Richard’s extreme confusion, without saying anything.\n\nMrs. Jellyby, sitting in quite a nest of waste paper, drank coffee all the evening and dictated at intervals to her eldest daughter. She also held a discussion with Mr. Quale, of which the subject seemed to be—if I understood it—the brotherhood of humanity, and gave utterance to some beautiful sentiments. I was not so attentive an auditor as I might have wished to be, however, for Peepy and the other children came flocking about Ada and me in a corner of the drawing-room to ask for another story; so we sat down among them and told them in whispers “Puss in Boots” and I don’t know what else until Mrs. Jellyby, accidentally remembering them, sent them to bed. As Peepy cried for me to take him to bed, I carried him upstairs, where the young woman with the flannel bandage charged into the midst of the little family like a dragon and overturned them into cribs.\n\nAfter that I occupied myself in making our room a little tidy and in coaxing a very cross fire that had been lighted to burn, which at last it did, quite brightly. On my return downstairs, I felt that Mrs. Jellyby looked down upon me rather for being so frivolous, and I was sorry for it, though at the same time I knew that I had no higher pretensions.\n\nIt was nearly midnight before we found an opportunity of going to bed, and even then we left Mrs. Jellyby among her papers drinking coffee and Miss Jellyby biting the feather of her pen.\n\n“What a strange house!” said Ada when we got upstairs. “How curious of my cousin Jarndyce to send us here!”\n\n“My love,” said I, “it quite confuses me. I want to understand it, and I can’t understand it at all.”\n\n“What?” asked Ada with her pretty smile.\n\n“All this, my dear,” said I. “It MUST be very good of Mrs. Jellyby to take such pains about a scheme for the benefit of natives—and yet—Peepy and the housekeeping!”\n\nAda laughed and put her arm about my neck as I stood looking at the fire, and told me I was a quiet, dear, good creature and had won her heart. “You are so thoughtful, Esther,” she said, “and yet so cheerful! And you do so much, so unpretendingly! You would make a home out of even this house.”\n\nMy simple darling! She was quite unconscious that she only praised herself and that it was in the goodness of her own heart that she made so much of me!\n\n“May I ask you a question?” said I when we had sat before the fire a little while.\n\n“Five hundred,” said Ada.\n\n“Your cousin, Mr. Jarndyce. I owe so much to him. Would you mind describing him to me?”\n\nShaking her golden hair, Ada turned her eyes upon me with such laughing wonder that I was full of wonder too, partly at her beauty, partly at her surprise.\n\n“Esther!” she cried.\n\n“My dear!”\n\n“You want a description of my cousin Jarndyce?”\n\n“My dear, I never saw him.”\n\n“And I never saw him!” returned Ada.\n\nWell, to be sure!\n\nNo, she had never seen him. Young as she was when her mama died, she remembered how the tears would come into her eyes when she spoke of him and of the noble generosity of his character, which she had said was to be trusted above all earthly things; and Ada trusted it. Her cousin Jarndyce had written to her a few months ago—“a plain, honest letter,” Ada said—proposing the arrangement we were now to enter on and telling her that “in time it might heal some of the wounds made by the miserable Chancery suit.” She had replied, gratefully accepting his proposal. Richard had received a similar letter and had made a similar response. He HAD seen Mr. Jarndyce once, but only once, five years ago, at Winchester school. He had told Ada, when they were leaning on the screen before the fire where I found them, that he recollected him as “a bluff, rosy fellow.” This was the utmost description Ada could give me.\n\nIt set me thinking so that when Ada was asleep, I still remained before the fire, wondering and wondering about Bleak House, and wondering and wondering that yesterday morning should seem so long ago. I don’t know where my thoughts had wandered when they were recalled by a tap at the door.\n\nI opened it softly and found Miss Jellyby shivering there with a broken candle in a broken candlestick in one hand and an egg-cup in the other.\n\n“Good night!” she said very sulkily.\n\n“Good night!” said I.\n\n“May I come in?” she shortly and unexpectedly asked me in the same sulky way.\n\n“Certainly,” said I. “Don’t wake Miss Clare.”\n\nShe would not sit down, but stood by the fire dipping her inky middle finger in the egg-cup, which contained vinegar, and smearing it over the ink stains on her face, frowning the whole time and looking very gloomy.\n\n“I wish Africa was dead!” she said on a sudden.\n\nI was going to remonstrate.\n\n“I do!” she said “Don’t talk to me, Miss Summerson. I hate it and detest it. It’s a beast!”\n\nI told her she was tired, and I was sorry. I put my hand upon her head, and touched her forehead, and said it was hot now but would be cool to-morrow. She still stood pouting and frowning at me, but presently put down her egg-cup and turned softly towards the bed where Ada lay.\n\n“She is very pretty!” she said with the same knitted brow and in the same uncivil manner.\n\nI assented with a smile.\n\n“An orphan. Ain’t she?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But knows a quantity, I suppose? Can dance, and play music, and sing? She can talk French, I suppose, and do geography, and globes, and needlework, and everything?”\n\n“No doubt,” said I.\n\n“I can’t,” she returned. “I can’t do anything hardly, except write. I’m always writing for Ma. I wonder you two were not ashamed of yourselves to come in this afternoon and see me able to do nothing else. It was like your ill nature. Yet you think yourselves very fine, I dare say!”\n\nI could see that the poor girl was near crying, and I resumed my chair without speaking and looked at her (I hope) as mildly as I felt towards her.\n\n“It’s disgraceful,” she said. “You know it is. The whole house is disgraceful. The children are disgraceful. I’M disgraceful. Pa’s miserable, and no wonder! Priscilla drinks—she’s always drinking. It’s a great shame and a great story of you if you say you didn’t smell her to-day. It was as bad as a public-house, waiting at dinner; you know it was!”\n\n“My dear, I don’t know it,” said I.\n\n“You do,” she said very shortly. “You shan’t say you don’t. You do!”\n\n“Oh, my dear!” said I. “If you won’t let me speak—”\n\n“You’re speaking now. You know you are. Don’t tell stories, Miss Summerson.”\n\n“My dear,” said I, “as long as you won’t hear me out—”\n\n“I don’t want to hear you out.”\n\n“Oh, yes, I think you do,” said I, “because that would be so very unreasonable. I did not know what you tell me because the servant did not come near me at dinner; but I don’t doubt what you tell me, and I am sorry to hear it.”\n\n“You needn’t make a merit of that,” said she.\n\n“No, my dear,” said I. “That would be very foolish.”\n\nShe was still standing by the bed, and now stooped down (but still with the same discontented face) and kissed Ada. That done, she came softly back and stood by the side of my chair. Her bosom was heaving in a distressful manner that I greatly pitied, but I thought it better not to speak.\n\n“I wish I was dead!” she broke out. “I wish we were all dead. It would be a great deal better for us.”\n\nIn a moment afterwards, she knelt on the ground at my side, hid her face in my dress, passionately begged my pardon, and wept. I comforted her and would have raised her, but she cried no, no; she wanted to stay there!\n\n“You used to teach girls,” she said, “If you could only have taught me, I could have learnt from you! I am so very miserable, and I like you so much!”\n\nI could not persuade her to sit by me or to do anything but move a ragged stool to where she was kneeling, and take that, and still hold my dress in the same manner. By degrees the poor tired girl fell asleep, and then I contrived to raise her head so that it should rest on my lap, and to cover us both with shawls. The fire went out, and all night long she slumbered thus before the ashy grate. At first I was painfully awake and vainly tried to lose myself, with my eyes closed, among the scenes of the day. At length, by slow degrees, they became indistinct and mingled. I began to lose the identity of the sleeper resting on me. Now it was Ada, now one of my old Reading friends from whom I could not believe I had so recently parted. Now it was the little mad woman worn out with curtsying and smiling, now some one in authority at Bleak House. Lastly, it was no one, and I was no one.\n\nThe purblind day was feebly struggling with the fog when I opened my eyes to encounter those of a dirty-faced little spectre fixed upon me. Peepy had scaled his crib, and crept down in his bed-gown and cap, and was so cold that his teeth were chattering as if he had cut them all.\n\nA Morning Adventure\nAlthough the morning was raw, and although the fog still seemed heavy—I say seemed, for the windows were so encrusted with dirt that they would have made midsummer sunshine dim—I was sufficiently forewarned of the discomfort within doors at that early hour and sufficiently curious about London to think it a good idea on the part of Miss Jellyby when she proposed that we should go out for a walk.\n\n“Ma won’t be down for ever so long,” she said, “and then it’s a chance if breakfast’s ready for an hour afterwards, they dawdle so. As to Pa, he gets what he can and goes to the office. He never has what you would call a regular breakfast. Priscilla leaves him out the loaf and some milk, when there is any, overnight. Sometimes there isn’t any milk, and sometimes the cat drinks it. But I’m afraid you must be tired, Miss Summerson, and perhaps you would rather go to bed.”\n\n“I am not at all tired, my dear,” said I, “and would much prefer to go out.”\n\n“If you’re sure you would,” returned Miss Jellyby, “I’ll get my things on.”\n\nAda said she would go too, and was soon astir. I made a proposal to Peepy, in default of being able to do anything better for him, that he should let me wash him and afterwards lay him down on my bed again. To this he submitted with the best grace possible, staring at me during the whole operation as if he never had been, and never could again be, so astonished in his life—looking very miserable also, certainly, but making no complaint, and going snugly to sleep as soon as it was over. At first I was in two minds about taking such a liberty, but I soon reflected that nobody in the house was likely to notice it.\n\nWhat with the bustle of dispatching Peepy and the bustle of getting myself ready and helping Ada, I was soon quite in a glow. We found Miss Jellyby trying to warm herself at the fire in the writing-room, which Priscilla was then lighting with a smutty parlour candlestick, throwing the candle in to make it burn better. Everything was just as we had left it last night and was evidently intended to remain so. Below-stairs the dinner-cloth had not been taken away, but had been left ready for breakfast. Crumbs, dust, and waste-paper were all over the house. Some pewter pots and a milk-can hung on the area railings; the door stood open; and we met the cook round the corner coming out of a public-house, wiping her mouth. She mentioned, as she passed us, that she had been to see what o’clock it was.\n\nBut before we met the cook, we met Richard, who was dancing up and down Thavies Inn to warm his feet. He was agreeably surprised to see us stirring so soon and said he would gladly share our walk. So he took care of Ada, and Miss Jellyby and I went first. I may mention that Miss Jellyby had relapsed into her sulky manner and that I really should not have thought she liked me much unless she had told me so.\n\n“Where would you wish to go?” she asked.\n\n“Anywhere, my dear,” I replied.\n\n“Anywhere’s nowhere,” said Miss Jellyby, stopping perversely.\n\n“Let us go somewhere at any rate,” said I.\n\nShe then walked me on very fast.\n\n“I don’t care!” she said. “Now, you are my witness, Miss Summerson, I say I don’t care—but if he was to come to our house with his great, shining, lumpy forehead night after night till he was as old as Methuselah, I wouldn’t have anything to say to him. Such ASSES as he and Ma make of themselves!”\n\n“My dear!” I remonstrated, in allusion to the epithet and the vigorous emphasis Miss Jellyby set upon it. “Your duty as a child—”\n\n“Oh! Don’t talk of duty as a child, Miss Summerson; where’s Ma’s duty as a parent? All made over to the public and Africa, I suppose! Then let the public and Africa show duty as a child; it’s much more their affair than mine. You are shocked, I dare say! Very well, so am I shocked too; so we are both shocked, and there’s an end of it!”\n\nShe walked me on faster yet.\n\n“But for all that, I say again, he may come, and come, and come, and I won’t have anything to say to him. I can’t bear him. If there’s any stuff in the world that I hate and detest, it’s the stuff he and Ma talk. I wonder the very paving-stones opposite our house can have the patience to stay there and be a witness of such inconsistencies and contradictions as all that sounding nonsense, and Ma’s management!”\n\nI could not but understand her to refer to Mr. Quale, the young gentleman who had appeared after dinner yesterday. I was saved the disagreeable necessity of pursuing the subject by Richard and Ada coming up at a round pace, laughing and asking us if we meant to run a race. Thus interrupted, Miss Jellyby became silent and walked moodily on at my side while I admired the long successions and varieties of streets, the quantity of people already going to and fro, the number of vehicles passing and repassing, the busy preparations in the setting forth of shop windows and the sweeping out of shops, and the extraordinary creatures in rags secretly groping among the swept-out rubbish for pins and other refuse.\n\n“So, cousin,” said the cheerful voice of Richard to Ada behind me. “We are never to get out of Chancery! We have come by another way to our place of meeting yesterday, and—by the Great Seal, here’s the old lady again!”\n\nTruly, there she was, immediately in front of us, curtsying, and smiling, and saying with her yesterday’s air of patronage, “The wards in Jarndyce! Ve-ry happy, I am sure!”\n\n“You are out early, ma’am,” said I as she curtsied to me.\n\n“Ye-es! I usually walk here early. Before the court sits. It’s retired. I collect my thoughts here for the business of the day,” said the old lady mincingly. “The business of the day requires a great deal of thought. Chancery justice is so ve-ry difficult to follow.”\n\n“Who’s this, Miss Summerson?” whispered Miss Jellyby, drawing my arm tighter through her own.\n\nThe little old lady’s hearing was remarkably quick. She answered for herself directly.\n\n“A suitor, my child. At your service. I have the honour to attend court regularly. With my documents. Have I the pleasure of addressing another of the youthful parties in Jarndyce?” said the old lady, recovering herself, with her head on one side, from a very low curtsy.\n\nRichard, anxious to atone for his thoughtlessness of yesterday, good-naturedly explained that Miss Jellyby was not connected with the suit.\n\n“Ha!” said the old lady. “She does not expect a judgment? She will still grow old. But not so old. Oh, dear, no! This is the garden of Lincoln’s Inn. I call it my garden. It is quite a bower in the summer-time. Where the birds sing melodiously. I pass the greater part of the long vacation here. In contemplation. You find the long vacation exceedingly long, don’t you?”\n\nWe said yes, as she seemed to expect us to say so.\n\n“When the leaves are falling from the trees and there are no more flowers in bloom to make up into nosegays for the Lord Chancellor’s court,” said the old lady, “the vacation is fulfilled and the sixth seal, mentioned in the Revelations, again prevails. Pray come and see my lodging. It will be a good omen for me. Youth, and hope, and beauty are very seldom there. It is a long, long time since I had a visit from either.”\n\nShe had taken my hand, and leading me and Miss Jellyby away, beckoned Richard and Ada to come too. I did not know how to excuse myself and looked to Richard for aid. As he was half amused and half curious and all in doubt how to get rid of the old lady without offence, she continued to lead us away, and he and Ada continued to follow, our strange conductress informing us all the time, with much smiling condescension, that she lived close by.\n\nIt was quite true, as it soon appeared. She lived so close by that we had not time to have done humouring her for a few moments before she was at home. Slipping us out at a little side gate, the old lady stopped most unexpectedly in a narrow back street, part of some courts and lanes immediately outside the wall of the inn, and said, “This is my lodging. Pray walk up!”\n\nShe had stopped at a shop over which was written KROOK, RAG AND BOTTLE WAREHOUSE. Also, in long thin letters, KROOK, DEALER IN MARINE STORES. In one part of the window was a picture of a red paper mill at which a cart was unloading a quantity of sacks of old rags. In another was the inscription BONES BOUGHT. In another, KITCHEN-STUFF BOUGHT. In another, OLD IRON BOUGHT. In another, WASTE-PAPER BOUGHT. In another, LADIES’ AND GENTLEMEN’S WARDROBES BOUGHT. Everything seemed to be bought and nothing to be sold there. In all parts of the window were quantities of dirty bottles—blacking bottles, medicine bottles, ginger-beer and soda-water bottles, pickle bottles, wine bottles, ink bottles; I am reminded by mentioning the latter that the shop had in several little particulars the air of being in a legal neighbourhood and of being, as it were, a dirty hanger-on and disowned relation of the law. There were a great many ink bottles. There was a little tottering bench of shabby old volumes outside the door, labelled “Law Books, all at 9d.” Some of the inscriptions I have enumerated were written in law-hand, like the papers I had seen in Kenge and Carboy’s office and the letters I had so long received from the firm. Among them was one, in the same writing, having nothing to do with the business of the shop, but announcing that a respectable man aged forty-five wanted engrossing or copying to execute with neatness and dispatch: Address to Nemo, care of Mr. Krook, within. There were several second-hand bags, blue and red, hanging up. A little way within the shop-door lay heaps of old crackled parchment scrolls and discoloured and dog’s-eared law-papers. I could have fancied that all the rusty keys, of which there must have been hundreds huddled together as old iron, had once belonged to doors of rooms or strong chests in lawyers’ offices. The litter of rags tumbled partly into and partly out of a one-legged wooden scale, hanging without any counterpoise from a beam, might have been counsellors’ bands and gowns torn up. One had only to fancy, as Richard whispered to Ada and me while we all stood looking in, that yonder bones in a corner, piled together and picked very clean, were the bones of clients, to make the picture complete.\n\nAs it was still foggy and dark, and as the shop was blinded besides by the wall of Lincoln’s Inn, intercepting the light within a couple of yards, we should not have seen so much but for a lighted lantern that an old man in spectacles and a hairy cap was carrying about in the shop. Turning towards the door, he now caught sight of us. He was short, cadaverous, and withered, with his head sunk sideways between his shoulders and the breath issuing in visible smoke from his mouth as if he were on fire within. His throat, chin, and eyebrows were so frosted with white hairs and so gnarled with veins and puckered skin that he looked from his breast upward like some old root in a fall of snow.\n\n“Hi, hi!” said the old man, coming to the door. “Have you anything to sell?”\n\nWe naturally drew back and glanced at our conductress, who had been trying to open the house-door with a key she had taken from her pocket, and to whom Richard now said that as we had had the pleasure of seeing where she lived, we would leave her, being pressed for time. But she was not to be so easily left. She became so fantastically and pressingly earnest in her entreaties that we would walk up and see her apartment for an instant, and was so bent, in her harmless way, on leading me in, as part of the good omen she desired, that I (whatever the others might do) saw nothing for it but to comply. I suppose we were all more or less curious; at any rate, when the old man added his persuasions to hers and said, “Aye, aye! Please her! It won’t take a minute! Come in, come in! Come in through the shop if t’other door’s out of order!” we all went in, stimulated by Richard’s laughing encouragement and relying on his protection.\n\n“My landlord, Krook,” said the little old lady, condescending to him from her lofty station as she presented him to us. “He is called among the neighbours the Lord Chancellor. His shop is called the Court of Chancery. He is a very eccentric person. He is very odd. Oh, I assure you he is very odd!”\n\nShe shook her head a great many times and tapped her forehead with her finger to express to us that we must have the goodness to excuse him, “For he is a little—you know—M!” said the old lady with great stateliness. The old man overheard, and laughed.\n\n“It’s true enough,” he said, going before us with the lantern, “that they call me the Lord Chancellor and call my shop Chancery. And why do you think they call me the Lord Chancellor and my shop Chancery?”\n\n“I don’t know, I am sure!” said Richard rather carelessly.\n\n“You see,” said the old man, stopping and turning round, “they—Hi! Here’s lovely hair! I have got three sacks of ladies’ hair below, but none so beautiful and fine as this. What colour, and what texture!”\n\n“That’ll do, my good friend!” said Richard, strongly disapproving of his having drawn one of Ada’s tresses through his yellow hand. “You can admire as the rest of us do without taking that liberty.”\n\nThe old man darted at him a sudden look which even called my attention from Ada, who, startled and blushing, was so remarkably beautiful that she seemed to fix the wandering attention of the little old lady herself. But as Ada interposed and laughingly said she could only feel proud of such genuine admiration, Mr. Krook shrunk into his former self as suddenly as he had leaped out of it.\n\n“You see, I have so many things here,” he resumed, holding up the lantern, “of so many kinds, and all as the neighbours think (but THEY know nothing), wasting away and going to rack and ruin, that that’s why they have given me and my place a christening. And I have so many old parchmentses and papers in my stock. And I have a liking for rust and must and cobwebs. And all’s fish that comes to my net. And I can’t abear to part with anything I once lay hold of (or so my neighbours think, but what do THEY know?) or to alter anything, or to have any sweeping, nor scouring, nor cleaning, nor repairing going on about me. That’s the way I’ve got the ill name of Chancery. I don’t mind. I go to see my noble and learned brother pretty well every day, when he sits in the Inn. He don’t notice me, but I notice him. There’s no great odds betwixt us. We both grub on in a muddle. Hi, Lady Jane!”\n\nA large grey cat leaped from some neighbouring shelf on his shoulder and startled us all.\n\n“Hi! Show ’em how you scratch. Hi! Tear, my lady!” said her master.\n\nThe cat leaped down and ripped at a bundle of rags with her tigerish claws, with a sound that it set my teeth on edge to hear.\n\n“She’d do as much for any one I was to set her on,” said the old man. “I deal in cat-skins among other general matters, and hers was offered to me. It’s a very fine skin, as you may see, but I didn’t have it stripped off! THAT warn’t like Chancery practice though, says you!”\n\nHe had by this time led us across the shop, and now opened a door in the back part of it, leading to the house-entry. As he stood with his hand upon the lock, the little old lady graciously observed to him before passing out, “That will do, Krook. You mean well, but are tiresome. My young friends are pressed for time. I have none to spare myself, having to attend court very soon. My young friends are the wards in Jarndyce.”\n\n“Jarndyce!” said the old man with a start.\n\n“Jarndyce and Jarndyce. The great suit, Krook,” returned his lodger.\n\n“Hi!” exclaimed the old man in a tone of thoughtful amazement and with a wider stare than before. “Think of it!”\n\nHe seemed so rapt all in a moment and looked so curiously at us that Richard said, “Why, you appear to trouble yourself a good deal about the causes before your noble and learned brother, the other Chancellor!”\n\n“Yes,” said the old man abstractedly. “Sure! YOUR name now will be—”\n\n“Richard Carstone.”\n\n“Carstone,” he repeated, slowly checking off that name upon his forefinger; and each of the others he went on to mention upon a separate finger. “Yes. There was the name of Barbary, and the name of Clare, and the name of Dedlock, too, I think.”\n\n“He knows as much of the cause as the real salaried Chancellor!” said Richard, quite astonished, to Ada and me.\n\n“Aye!” said the old man, coming slowly out of his abstraction. “Yes! Tom Jarndyce—you’ll excuse me, being related; but he was never known about court by any other name, and was as well known there as—she is now,” nodding slightly at his lodger. “Tom Jarndyce was often in here. He got into a restless habit of strolling about when the cause was on, or expected, talking to the little shopkeepers and telling ’em to keep out of Chancery, whatever they did. ‘For,’ says he, ‘it’s being ground to bits in a slow mill; it’s being roasted at a slow fire; it’s being stung to death by single bees; it’s being drowned by drops; it’s going mad by grains.’ He was as near making away with himself, just where the young lady stands, as near could be.”\n\nWe listened with horror.\n\n“He come in at the door,” said the old man, slowly pointing an imaginary track along the shop, “on the day he did it—the whole neighbourhood had said for months before that he would do it, of a certainty sooner or later—he come in at the door that day, and walked along there, and sat himself on a bench that stood there, and asked me (you’ll judge I was a mortal sight younger then) to fetch him a pint of wine. ‘For,’ says he, ‘Krook, I am much depressed; my cause is on again, and I think I’m nearer judgment than I ever was.’ I hadn’t a mind to leave him alone; and I persuaded him to go to the tavern over the way there, t’other side my lane (I mean Chancery Lane); and I followed and looked in at the window, and saw him, comfortable as I thought, in the arm-chair by the fire, and company with him. I hadn’t hardly got back here when I heard a shot go echoing and rattling right away into the inn. I ran out—neighbours ran out—twenty of us cried at once, ‘Tom Jarndyce!’”\n\nThe old man stopped, looked hard at us, looked down into the lantern, blew the light out, and shut the lantern up.\n\n“We were right, I needn’t tell the present hearers. Hi! To be sure, how the neighbourhood poured into court that afternoon while the cause was on! How my noble and learned brother, and all the rest of ’em, grubbed and muddled away as usual and tried to look as if they hadn’t heard a word of the last fact in the case or as if they had—Oh, dear me!—nothing at all to do with it if they had heard of it by any chance!”\n\nAda’s colour had entirely left her, and Richard was scarcely less pale. Nor could I wonder, judging even from my emotions, and I was no party in the suit, that to hearts so untried and fresh it was a shock to come into the inheritance of a protracted misery, attended in the minds of many people with such dreadful recollections. I had another uneasiness, in the application of the painful story to the poor half-witted creature who had brought us there; but, to my surprise, she seemed perfectly unconscious of that and only led the way upstairs again, informing us with the toleration of a superior creature for the infirmities of a common mortal that her landlord was “a little—M—, you know!”\n\nShe lived at the top of the house, in a pretty large room, from which she had a glimpse of Lincoln’s Inn Hall. This seemed to have been her principal inducement, originally, for taking up her residence there. She could look at it, she said, in the night, especially in the moonshine. Her room was clean, but very, very bare. I noticed the scantiest necessaries in the way of furniture; a few old prints from books, of Chancellors and barristers, wafered against the wall; and some half-dozen reticles and work-bags, “containing documents,” as she informed us. There were neither coals nor ashes in the grate, and I saw no articles of clothing anywhere, nor any kind of food. Upon a shelf in an open cupboard were a plate or two, a cup or two, and so forth, but all dry and empty. There was a more affecting meaning in her pinched appearance, I thought as I looked round, than I had understood before.\n\n“Extremely honoured, I am sure,” said our poor hostess with the greatest suavity, “by this visit from the wards in Jarndyce. And very much indebted for the omen. It is a retired situation. Considering. I am limited as to situation. In consequence of the necessity of attending on the Chancellor. I have lived here many years. I pass my days in court, my evenings and my nights here. I find the nights long, for I sleep but little and think much. That is, of course, unavoidable, being in Chancery. I am sorry I cannot offer chocolate. I expect a judgment shortly and shall then place my establishment on a superior footing. At present, I don’t mind confessing to the wards in Jarndyce (in strict confidence) that I sometimes find it difficult to keep up a genteel appearance. I have felt the cold here. I have felt something sharper than cold. It matters very little. Pray excuse the introduction of such mean topics.”\n\nShe partly drew aside the curtain of the long, low garret window and called our attention to a number of bird-cages hanging there, some containing several birds. There were larks, linnets, and goldfinches—I should think at least twenty.\n\n“I began to keep the little creatures,” she said, “with an object that the wards will readily comprehend. With the intention of restoring them to liberty. When my judgment should be given. Ye-es! They die in prison, though. Their lives, poor silly things, are so short in comparison with Chancery proceedings that, one by one, the whole collection has died over and over again. I doubt, do you know, whether one of these, though they are all young, will live to be free! Ve-ry mortifying, is it not?”\n\nAlthough she sometimes asked a question, she never seemed to expect a reply, but rambled on as if she were in the habit of doing so when no one but herself was present.\n\n“Indeed,” she pursued, “I positively doubt sometimes, I do assure you, whether while matters are still unsettled, and the sixth or Great Seal still prevails, I may not one day be found lying stark and senseless here, as I have found so many birds!”\n\nRichard, answering what he saw in Ada’s compassionate eyes, took the opportunity of laying some money, softly and unobserved, on the chimney-piece. We all drew nearer to the cages, feigning to examine the birds.\n\n“I can’t allow them to sing much,” said the little old lady, “for (you’ll think this curious) I find my mind confused by the idea that they are singing while I am following the arguments in court. And my mind requires to be so very clear, you know! Another time, I’ll tell you their names. Not at present. On a day of such good omen, they shall sing as much as they like. In honour of youth,” a smile and curtsy, “hope,” a smile and curtsy, “and beauty,” a smile and curtsy. “There! We’ll let in the full light.”\n\nThe birds began to stir and chirp.\n\n“I cannot admit the air freely,” said the little old lady—the room was close, and would have been the better for it—“because the cat you saw downstairs, called Lady Jane, is greedy for their lives. She crouches on the parapet outside for hours and hours. I have discovered,” whispering mysteriously, “that her natural cruelty is sharpened by a jealous fear of their regaining their liberty. In consequence of the judgment I expect being shortly given. She is sly and full of malice. I half believe, sometimes, that she is no cat, but the wolf of the old saying. It is so very difficult to keep her from the door.”\n\nSome neighbouring bells, reminding the poor soul that it was half-past nine, did more for us in the way of bringing our visit to an end than we could easily have done for ourselves. She hurriedly took up her little bag of documents, which she had laid upon the table on coming in, and asked if we were also going into court. On our answering no, and that we would on no account detain her, she opened the door to attend us downstairs.\n\n“With such an omen, it is even more necessary than usual that I should be there before the Chancellor comes in,” said she, “for he might mention my case the first thing. I have a presentiment that he WILL mention it the first thing this morning.”\n\nShe stopped to tell us in a whisper as we were going down that the whole house was filled with strange lumber which her landlord had bought piecemeal and had no wish to sell, in consequence of being a little M. This was on the first floor. But she had made a previous stoppage on the second floor and had silently pointed at a dark door there.\n\n“The only other lodger,” she now whispered in explanation, “a law-writer. The children in the lanes here say he has sold himself to the devil. I don’t know what he can have done with the money. Hush!”\n\nShe appeared to mistrust that the lodger might hear her even there, and repeating “Hush!” went before us on tiptoe as though even the sound of her footsteps might reveal to him what she had said.\n\nPassing through the shop on our way out, as we had passed through it on our way in, we found the old man storing a quantity of packets of waste-paper in a kind of well in the floor. He seemed to be working hard, with the perspiration standing on his forehead, and had a piece of chalk by him, with which, as he put each separate package or bundle down, he made a crooked mark on the panelling of the wall.\n\nRichard and Ada, and Miss Jellyby, and the little old lady had gone by him, and I was going when he touched me on the arm to stay me, and chalked the letter J upon the wall—in a very curious manner, beginning with the end of the letter and shaping it backward. It was a capital letter, not a printed one, but just such a letter as any clerk in Messrs. Kenge and Carboy’s office would have made.\n\n“Can you read it?” he asked me with a keen glance.\n\n“Surely,” said I. “It’s very plain.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“J.”\nWith another glance at me, and a glance at the door, he rubbed it out and turned an “a” in its place (not a capital letter this time), and said, “What’s that?”\n\nI told him. He then rubbed that out and turned the letter “r,” and asked me the same question. He went on quickly until he had formed in the same curious manner, beginning at the ends and bottoms of the letters, the word Jarndyce, without once leaving two letters on the wall together.\n\n“What does that spell?” he asked me.\n\nWhen I told him, he laughed. In the same odd way, yet with the same rapidity, he then produced singly, and rubbed out singly, the letters forming the words Bleak House. These, in some astonishment, I also read; and he laughed again.\n\n“Hi!” said the old man, laying aside the chalk. “I have a turn for copying from memory, you see, miss, though I can neither read nor write.”\n\nHe looked so disagreeable and his cat looked so wickedly at me, as if I were a blood-relation of the birds upstairs, that I was quite relieved by Richard’s appearing at the door and saying, “Miss Summerson, I hope you are not bargaining for the sale of your hair. Don’t be tempted. Three sacks below are quite enough for Mr. Krook!”\n\nI lost no time in wishing Mr. Krook good morning and joining my friends outside, where we parted with the little old lady, who gave us her blessing with great ceremony and renewed her assurance of yesterday in reference to her intention of settling estates on Ada and me. Before we finally turned out of those lanes, we looked back and saw Mr. Krook standing at his shop-door, in his spectacles, looking after us, with his cat upon his shoulder, and her tail sticking up on one side of his hairy cap like a tall feather.\n\n“Quite an adventure for a morning in London!” said Richard with a sigh. “Ah, cousin, cousin, it’s a weary word this Chancery!”\n\n“It is to me, and has been ever since I can remember,” returned Ada. “I am grieved that I should be the enemy—as I suppose I am—of a great number of relations and others, and that they should be my enemies—as I suppose they are—and that we should all be ruining one another without knowing how or why and be in constant doubt and discord all our lives. It seems very strange, as there must be right somewhere, that an honest judge in real earnest has not been able to find out through all these years where it is.”\n\n“Ah, cousin!” said Richard. “Strange, indeed! All this wasteful, wanton chess-playing IS very strange. To see that composed court yesterday jogging on so serenely and to think of the wretchedness of the pieces on the board gave me the headache and the heartache both together. My head ached with wondering how it happened, if men were neither fools nor rascals; and my heart ached to think they could possibly be either. But at all events, Ada—I may call you Ada?”\n\n“Of course you may, cousin Richard.”\n\n“At all events, Chancery will work none of its bad influences on US. We have happily been brought together, thanks to our good kinsman, and it can’t divide us now!”\n\n“Never, I hope, cousin Richard!” said Ada gently.\n\nMiss Jellyby gave my arm a squeeze and me a very significant look. I smiled in return, and we made the rest of the way back very pleasantly.\n\nIn half an hour after our arrival, Mrs. Jellyby appeared; and in the course of an hour the various things necessary for breakfast straggled one by one into the dining-room. I do not doubt that Mrs. Jellyby had gone to bed and got up in the usual manner, but she presented no appearance of having changed her dress. She was greatly occupied during breakfast, for the morning’s post brought a heavy correspondence relative to Borrioboola-Gha, which would occasion her (she said) to pass a busy day. The children tumbled about, and notched memoranda of their accidents in their legs, which were perfect little calendars of distress; and Peepy was lost for an hour and a half, and brought home from Newgate market by a policeman. The equable manner in which Mrs. Jellyby sustained both his absence and his restoration to the family circle surprised us all.\n\nShe was by that time perseveringly dictating to Caddy, and Caddy was fast relapsing into the inky condition in which we had found her. At one o’clock an open carriage arrived for us, and a cart for our luggage. Mrs. Jellyby charged us with many remembrances to her good friend Mr. Jarndyce; Caddy left her desk to see us depart, kissed me in the passage, and stood biting her pen and sobbing on the steps; Peepy, I am happy to say, was asleep and spared the pain of separation (I was not without misgivings that he had gone to Newgate market in search of me); and all the other children got up behind the barouche and fell off, and we saw them, with great concern, scattered over the surface of Thavies Inn as we rolled out of its precincts.\n\nQuite at Home\nThe day had brightened very much, and still brightened as we went westward. We went our way through the sunshine and the fresh air, wondering more and more at the extent of the streets, the brilliancy of the shops, the great traffic, and the crowds of people whom the pleasanter weather seemed to have brought out like many-coloured flowers. By and by we began to leave the wonderful city and to proceed through suburbs which, of themselves, would have made a pretty large town in my eyes; and at last we got into a real country road again, with windmills, rick-yards, milestones, farmers’ waggons, scents of old hay, swinging signs, and horse troughs: trees, fields, and hedge-rows. It was delightful to see the green landscape before us and the immense metropolis behind; and when a waggon with a train of beautiful horses, furnished with red trappings and clear-sounding bells, came by us with its music, I believe we could all three have sung to the bells, so cheerful were the influences around.\n\n“The whole road has been reminding me of my namesake Whittington,” said Richard, “and that waggon is the finishing touch. Halloa! What’s the matter?”\n\nWe had stopped, and the waggon had stopped too. Its music changed as the horses came to a stand, and subsided to a gentle tinkling, except when a horse tossed his head or shook himself and sprinkled off a little shower of bell-ringing.\n\n“Our postilion is looking after the waggoner,” said Richard, “and the waggoner is coming back after us. Good day, friend!” The waggoner was at our coach-door. “Why, here’s an extraordinary thing!” added Richard, looking closely at the man. “He has got your name, Ada, in his hat!”\n\nHe had all our names in his hat. Tucked within the band were three small notes—one addressed to Ada, one to Richard, one to me. These the waggoner delivered to each of us respectively, reading the name aloud first. In answer to Richard’s inquiry from whom they came, he briefly answered, “Master, sir, if you please”; and putting on his hat again (which was like a soft bowl), cracked his whip, re-awakened his music, and went melodiously away.\n\n“Is that Mr. Jarndyce’s waggon?” said Richard, calling to our post-boy.\n\n“Yes, sir,” he replied. “Going to London.”\n\nWe opened the notes. Each was a counterpart of the other and contained these words in a solid, plain hand.\n\nI look forward, my dear, to our meeting easily and without constraint on either side. I therefore have to propose that we meet as old friends and take the past for granted. It will be a relief to you possibly, and to me certainly, and so my love to you.\n\nJohn Jarndyce\n\nI had perhaps less reason to be surprised than either of my companions, having never yet enjoyed an opportunity of thanking one who had been my benefactor and sole earthly dependence through so many years. I had not considered how I could thank him, my gratitude lying too deep in my heart for that; but I now began to consider how I could meet him without thanking him, and felt it would be very difficult indeed.\n\nThe notes revived in Richard and Ada a general impression that they both had, without quite knowing how they came by it, that their cousin Jarndyce could never bear acknowledgments for any kindness he performed and that sooner than receive any he would resort to the most singular expedients and evasions or would even run away. Ada dimly remembered to have heard her mother tell, when she was a very little child, that he had once done her an act of uncommon generosity and that on her going to his house to thank him, he happened to see her through a window coming to the door, and immediately escaped by the back gate, and was not heard of for three months. This discourse led to a great deal more on the same theme, and indeed it lasted us all day, and we talked of scarcely anything else. If we did by any chance diverge into another subject, we soon returned to this, and wondered what the house would be like, and when we should get there, and whether we should see Mr. Jarndyce as soon as we arrived or after a delay, and what he would say to us, and what we should say to him. All of which we wondered about, over and over again.\n\nThe roads were very heavy for the horses, but the pathway was generally good, so we alighted and walked up all the hills, and liked it so well that we prolonged our walk on the level ground when we got to the top. At Barnet there were other horses waiting for us, but as they had only just been fed, we had to wait for them too, and got a long fresh walk over a common and an old battle-field before the carriage came up. These delays so protracted the journey that the short day was spent and the long night had closed in before we came to St. Albans, near to which town Bleak House was, we knew.\n\nBy that time we were so anxious and nervous that even Richard confessed, as we rattled over the stones of the old street, to feeling an irrational desire to drive back again. As to Ada and me, whom he had wrapped up with great care, the night being sharp and frosty, we trembled from head to foot. When we turned out of the town, round a corner, and Richard told us that the post-boy, who had for a long time sympathized with our heightened expectation, was looking back and nodding, we both stood up in the carriage (Richard holding Ada lest she should be jolted down) and gazed round upon the open country and the starlight night for our destination. There was a light sparkling on the top of a hill before us, and the driver, pointing to it with his whip and crying, “That’s Bleak House!” put his horses into a canter and took us forward at such a rate, uphill though it was, that the wheels sent the road drift flying about our heads like spray from a water-mill. Presently we lost the light, presently saw it, presently lost it, presently saw it, and turned into an avenue of trees and cantered up towards where it was beaming brightly. It was in a window of what seemed to be an old-fashioned house with three peaks in the roof in front and a circular sweep leading to the porch. A bell was rung as we drew up, and amidst the sound of its deep voice in the still air, and the distant barking of some dogs, and a gush of light from the opened door, and the smoking and steaming of the heated horses, and the quickened beating of our own hearts, we alighted in no inconsiderable confusion.\n\n“Ada, my love, Esther, my dear, you are welcome. I rejoice to see you! Rick, if I had a hand to spare at present, I would give it you!”\n\nThe gentleman who said these words in a clear, bright, hospitable voice had one of his arms round Ada’s waist and the other round mine, and kissed us both in a fatherly way, and bore us across the hall into a ruddy little room, all in a glow with a blazing fire. Here he kissed us again, and opening his arms, made us sit down side by side on a sofa ready drawn out near the hearth. I felt that if we had been at all demonstrative, he would have run away in a moment.\n\n“Now, Rick!” said he. “I have a hand at liberty. A word in earnest is as good as a speech. I am heartily glad to see you. You are at home. Warm yourself!”\n\nRichard shook him by both hands with an intuitive mixture of respect and frankness, and only saying (though with an earnestness that rather alarmed me, I was so afraid of Mr. Jarndyce’s suddenly disappearing), “You are very kind, sir! We are very much obliged to you!” laid aside his hat and coat and came up to the fire.\n\n“And how did you like the ride? And how did you like Mrs. Jellyby, my dear?” said Mr. Jarndyce to Ada.\n\nWhile Ada was speaking to him in reply, I glanced (I need not say with how much interest) at his face. It was a handsome, lively, quick face, full of change and motion; and his hair was a silvered iron-grey. I took him to be nearer sixty than fifty, but he was upright, hearty, and robust. From the moment of his first speaking to us his voice had connected itself with an association in my mind that I could not define; but now, all at once, a something sudden in his manner and a pleasant expression in his eyes recalled the gentleman in the stagecoach six years ago on the memorable day of my journey to Reading. I was certain it was he. I never was so frightened in my life as when I made the discovery, for he caught my glance, and appearing to read my thoughts, gave such a look at the door that I thought we had lost him.\n\nHowever, I am happy to say he remained where he was, and asked me what I thought of Mrs. Jellyby.\n\n“She exerts herself very much for Africa, sir,” I said.\n\n“Nobly!” returned Mr. Jarndyce. “But you answer like Ada.” Whom I had not heard. “You all think something else, I see.”\n\n“We rather thought,” said I, glancing at Richard and Ada, who entreated me with their eyes to speak, “that perhaps she was a little unmindful of her home.”\n\n“Floored!” cried Mr. Jarndyce.\n\nI was rather alarmed again.\n\n“Well! I want to know your real thoughts, my dear. I may have sent you there on purpose.”\n\n“We thought that, perhaps,” said I, hesitating, “it is right to begin with the obligations of home, sir; and that, perhaps, while those are overlooked and neglected, no other duties can possibly be substituted for them.”\n\n“The little Jellybys,” said Richard, coming to my relief, “are really—I can’t help expressing myself strongly, sir—in a devil of a state.”\n\n“She means well,” said Mr. Jarndyce hastily. “The wind’s in the east.”\n\n“It was in the north, sir, as we came down,” observed Richard.\n\n“My dear Rick,” said Mr. Jarndyce, poking the fire, “I’ll take an oath it’s either in the east or going to be. I am always conscious of an uncomfortable sensation now and then when the wind is blowing in the east.”\n\n“Rheumatism, sir?” said Richard.\n\n“I dare say it is, Rick. I believe it is. And so the little Jell—I had my doubts about ’em—are in a—oh, Lord, yes, it’s easterly!” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\nHe had taken two or three undecided turns up and down while uttering these broken sentences, retaining the poker in one hand and rubbing his hair with the other, with a good-natured vexation at once so whimsical and so lovable that I am sure we were more delighted with him than we could possibly have expressed in any words. He gave an arm to Ada and an arm to me, and bidding Richard bring a candle, was leading the way out when he suddenly turned us all back again.\n\n“Those little Jellybys. Couldn’t you—didn’t you—now, if it had rained sugar-plums, or three-cornered raspberry tarts, or anything of that sort!” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Oh, cousin—” Ada hastily began.\n\n“Good, my pretty pet. I like cousin. Cousin John, perhaps, is better.”\n\n“Then, cousin John—” Ada laughingly began again.\n\n“Ha, ha! Very good indeed!” said Mr. Jarndyce with great enjoyment. “Sounds uncommonly natural. Yes, my dear?”\n\n“It did better than that. It rained Esther.”\n\n“Aye?” said Mr. Jarndyce. “What did Esther do?”\n\n“Why, cousin John,” said Ada, clasping her hands upon his arm and shaking her head at me across him—for I wanted her to be quiet—“Esther was their friend directly. Esther nursed them, coaxed them to sleep, washed and dressed them, told them stories, kept them quiet, bought them keepsakes”—My dear girl! I had only gone out with Peepy after he was found and given him a little, tiny horse!—“and, cousin John, she softened poor Caroline, the eldest one, so much and was so thoughtful for me and so amiable! No, no, I won’t be contradicted, Esther dear! You know, you know, it’s true!”\n\nThe warm-hearted darling leaned across her cousin John and kissed me, and then looking up in his face, boldly said, “At all events, cousin John, I WILL thank you for the companion you have given me.” I felt as if she challenged him to run away. But he didn’t.\n\n“Where did you say the wind was, Rick?” asked Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“In the north as we came down, sir.”\n\n“You are right. There’s no east in it. A mistake of mine. Come, girls, come and see your home!”\n\nIt was one of those delightfully irregular houses where you go up and down steps out of one room into another, and where you come upon more rooms when you think you have seen all there are, and where there is a bountiful provision of little halls and passages, and where you find still older cottage-rooms in unexpected places with lattice windows and green growth pressing through them. Mine, which we entered first, was of this kind, with an up-and-down roof that had more corners in it than I ever counted afterwards and a chimney (there was a wood fire on the hearth) paved all around with pure white tiles, in every one of which a bright miniature of the fire was blazing. Out of this room, you went down two steps into a charming little sitting-room looking down upon a flower-garden, which room was henceforth to belong to Ada and me. Out of this you went up three steps into Ada’s bedroom, which had a fine broad window commanding a beautiful view (we saw a great expanse of darkness lying underneath the stars), to which there was a hollow window-seat, in which, with a spring-lock, three dear Adas might have been lost at once. Out of this room you passed into a little gallery, with which the other best rooms (only two) communicated, and so, by a little staircase of shallow steps with a number of corner stairs in it, considering its length, down into the hall. But if instead of going out at Ada’s door you came back into my room, and went out at the door by which you had entered it, and turned up a few crooked steps that branched off in an unexpected manner from the stairs, you lost yourself in passages, with mangles in them, and three-cornered tables, and a native Hindu chair, which was also a sofa, a box, and a bedstead, and looked in every form something between a bamboo skeleton and a great bird-cage, and had been brought from India nobody knew by whom or when. From these you came on Richard’s room, which was part library, part sitting-room, part bedroom, and seemed indeed a comfortable compound of many rooms. Out of that you went straight, with a little interval of passage, to the plain room where Mr. Jarndyce slept, all the year round, with his window open, his bedstead without any furniture standing in the middle of the floor for more air, and his cold bath gaping for him in a smaller room adjoining. Out of that you came into another passage, where there were back-stairs and where you could hear the horses being rubbed down outside the stable and being told to “Hold up” and “Get over,” as they slipped about very much on the uneven stones. Or you might, if you came out at another door (every room had at least two doors), go straight down to the hall again by half-a-dozen steps and a low archway, wondering how you got back there or had ever got out of it.\n\nThe furniture, old-fashioned rather than old, like the house, was as pleasantly irregular. Ada’s sleeping-room was all flowers—in chintz and paper, in velvet, in needlework, in the brocade of two stiff courtly chairs which stood, each attended by a little page of a stool for greater state, on either side of the fire-place. Our sitting-room was green and had framed and glazed upon the walls numbers of surprising and surprised birds, staring out of pictures at a real trout in a case, as brown and shining as if it had been served with gravy; at the death of Captain Cook; and at the whole process of preparing tea in China, as depicted by Chinese artists. In my room there were oval engravings of the months—ladies haymaking in short waists and large hats tied under the chin, for June; smooth-legged noblemen pointing with cocked-hats to village steeples, for October. Half-length portraits in crayons abounded all through the house, but were so dispersed that I found the brother of a youthful officer of mine in the china-closet and the grey old age of my pretty young bride, with a flower in her bodice, in the breakfast-room. As substitutes, I had four angels, of Queen Anne’s reign, taking a complacent gentleman to heaven, in festoons, with some difficulty; and a composition in needlework representing fruit, a kettle, and an alphabet. All the movables, from the wardrobes to the chairs and tables, hangings, glasses, even to the pincushions and scent-bottles on the dressing-tables, displayed the same quaint variety. They agreed in nothing but their perfect neatness, their display of the whitest linen, and their storing-up, wheresoever the existence of a drawer, small or large, rendered it possible, of quantities of rose-leaves and sweet lavender. Such, with its illuminated windows, softened here and there by shadows of curtains, shining out upon the starlight night; with its light, and warmth, and comfort; with its hospitable jingle, at a distance, of preparations for dinner; with the face of its generous master brightening everything we saw; and just wind enough without to sound a low accompaniment to everything we heard, were our first impressions of Bleak House.\n\n“I am glad you like it,” said Mr. Jarndyce when he had brought us round again to Ada’s sitting-room. “It makes no pretensions, but it is a comfortable little place, I hope, and will be more so with such bright young looks in it. You have barely half an hour before dinner. There’s no one here but the finest creature upon earth—a child.”\n\n“More children, Esther!” said Ada.\n\n“I don’t mean literally a child,” pursued Mr. Jarndyce; “not a child in years. He is grown up—he is at least as old as I am—but in simplicity, and freshness, and enthusiasm, and a fine guileless inaptitude for all worldly affairs, he is a perfect child.”\n\nWe felt that he must be very interesting.\n\n“He knows Mrs. Jellyby,” said Mr. Jarndyce. “He is a musical man, an amateur, but might have been a professional. He is an artist too, an amateur, but might have been a professional. He is a man of attainments and of captivating manners. He has been unfortunate in his affairs, and unfortunate in his pursuits, and unfortunate in his family; but he don’t care—he’s a child!”\n\n“Did you imply that he has children of his own, sir?” inquired Richard.\n\n“Yes, Rick! Half-a-dozen. More! Nearer a dozen, I should think. But he has never looked after them. How could he? He wanted somebody to look after HIM. He is a child, you know!” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“And have the children looked after themselves at all, sir?” inquired Richard.\n\n“Why, just as you may suppose,” said Mr. Jarndyce, his countenance suddenly falling. “It is said that the children of the very poor are not brought up, but dragged up. Harold Skimpole’s children have tumbled up somehow or other. The wind’s getting round again, I am afraid. I feel it rather!”\n\nRichard observed that the situation was exposed on a sharp night.\n\n“It IS exposed,” said Mr. Jarndyce. “No doubt that’s the cause. Bleak House has an exposed sound. But you are coming my way. Come along!”\n\nOur luggage having arrived and being all at hand, I was dressed in a few minutes and engaged in putting my worldly goods away when a maid (not the one in attendance upon Ada, but another, whom I had not seen) brought a basket into my room with two bunches of keys in it, all labelled.\n\n“For you, miss, if you please,” said she.\n“For me?” said I.\n\n“The housekeeping keys, miss.”\n\nI showed my surprise, for she added with some little surprise on her own part, “I was told to bring them as soon as you was alone, miss. Miss Summerson, if I don’t deceive myself?”\n\n“Yes,” said I. “That is my name.”\n\n“The large bunch is the housekeeping, and the little bunch is the cellars, miss. Any time you was pleased to appoint to-morrow morning, I was to show you the presses and things they belong to.”\n\nI said I would be ready at half-past six, and after she was gone, stood looking at the basket, quite lost in the magnitude of my trust. Ada found me thus and had such a delightful confidence in me when I showed her the keys and told her about them that it would have been insensibility and ingratitude not to feel encouraged. I knew, to be sure, that it was the dear girl’s kindness, but I liked to be so pleasantly cheated.\n\nWhen we went downstairs, we were presented to Mr. Skimpole, who was standing before the fire telling Richard how fond he used to be, in his school-time, of football. He was a little bright creature with a rather large head, but a delicate face and a sweet voice, and there was a perfect charm in him. All he said was so free from effort and spontaneous and was said with such a captivating gaiety that it was fascinating to hear him talk. Being of a more slender figure than Mr. Jarndyce and having a richer complexion, with browner hair, he looked younger. Indeed, he had more the appearance in all respects of a damaged young man than a well-preserved elderly one. There was an easy negligence in his manner and even in his dress (his hair carelessly disposed, and his neck-kerchief loose and flowing, as I have seen artists paint their own portraits) which I could not separate from the idea of a romantic youth who had undergone some unique process of depreciation. It struck me as being not at all like the manner or appearance of a man who had advanced in life by the usual road of years, cares, and experiences.\n\nI gathered from the conversation that Mr. Skimpole had been educated for the medical profession and had once lived, in his professional capacity, in the household of a German prince. He told us, however, that as he had always been a mere child in point of weights and measures and had never known anything about them (except that they disgusted him), he had never been able to prescribe with the requisite accuracy of detail. In fact, he said, he had no head for detail. And he told us, with great humour, that when he was wanted to bleed the prince or physic any of his people, he was generally found lying on his back in bed, reading the newspapers or making fancy-sketches in pencil, and couldn’t come. The prince, at last, objecting to this, “in which,” said Mr. Skimpole, in the frankest manner, “he was perfectly right,” the engagement terminated, and Mr. Skimpole having (as he added with delightful gaiety) “nothing to live upon but love, fell in love, and married, and surrounded himself with rosy cheeks.” His good friend Jarndyce and some other of his good friends then helped him, in quicker or slower succession, to several openings in life, but to no purpose, for he must confess to two of the oldest infirmities in the world: one was that he had no idea of time, the other that he had no idea of money. In consequence of which he never kept an appointment, never could transact any business, and never knew the value of anything! Well! So he had got on in life, and here he was! He was very fond of reading the papers, very fond of making fancy-sketches with a pencil, very fond of nature, very fond of art. All he asked of society was to let him live. THAT wasn’t much. His wants were few. Give him the papers, conversation, music, mutton, coffee, landscape, fruit in the season, a few sheets of Bristol-board, and a little claret, and he asked no more. He was a mere child in the world, but he didn’t cry for the moon. He said to the world, “Go your several ways in peace! Wear red coats, blue coats, lawn sleeves; put pens behind your ears, wear aprons; go after glory, holiness, commerce, trade, any object you prefer; only—let Harold Skimpole live!”\n\nAll this and a great deal more he told us, not only with the utmost brilliancy and enjoyment, but with a certain vivacious candour—speaking of himself as if he were not at all his own affair, as if Skimpole were a third person, as if he knew that Skimpole had his singularities but still had his claims too, which were the general business of the community and must not be slighted. He was quite enchanting. If I felt at all confused at that early time in endeavouring to reconcile anything he said with anything I had thought about the duties and accountabilities of life (which I am far from sure of), I was confused by not exactly understanding why he was free of them. That he WAS free of them, I scarcely doubted; he was so very clear about it himself.\n\n“I covet nothing,” said Mr. Skimpole in the same light way. “Possession is nothing to me. Here is my friend Jarndyce’s excellent house. I feel obliged to him for possessing it. I can sketch it and alter it. I can set it to music. When I am here, I have sufficient possession of it and have neither trouble, cost, nor responsibility. My steward’s name, in short, is Jarndyce, and he can’t cheat me. We have been mentioning Mrs. Jellyby. There is a bright-eyed woman, of a strong will and immense power of business detail, who throws herself into objects with surprising ardour! I don’t regret that I have not a strong will and an immense power of business detail to throw myself into objects with surprising ardour. I can admire her without envy. I can sympathize with the objects. I can dream of them. I can lie down on the grass—in fine weather—and float along an African river, embracing all the natives I meet, as sensible of the deep silence and sketching the dense overhanging tropical growth as accurately as if I were there. I don’t know that it’s of any direct use my doing so, but it’s all I can do, and I do it thoroughly. Then, for heaven’s sake, having Harold Skimpole, a confiding child, petitioning you, the world, an agglomeration of practical people of business habits, to let him live and admire the human family, do it somehow or other, like good souls, and suffer him to ride his rocking-horse!”\n\nIt was plain enough that Mr. Jarndyce had not been neglectful of the adjuration. Mr. Skimpole’s general position there would have rendered it so without the addition of what he presently said.\n\n“It’s only you, the generous creatures, whom I envy,” said Mr. Skimpole, addressing us, his new friends, in an impersonal manner. “I envy you your power of doing what you do. It is what I should revel in myself. I don’t feel any vulgar gratitude to you. I almost feel as if YOU ought to be grateful to ME for giving you the opportunity of enjoying the luxury of generosity. I know you like it. For anything I can tell, I may have come into the world expressly for the purpose of increasing your stock of happiness. I may have been born to be a benefactor to you by sometimes giving you an opportunity of assisting me in my little perplexities. Why should I regret my incapacity for details and worldly affairs when it leads to such pleasant consequences? I don’t regret it therefore.”\n\nOf all his playful speeches (playful, yet always fully meaning what they expressed) none seemed to be more to the taste of Mr. Jarndyce than this. I had often new temptations, afterwards, to wonder whether it was really singular, or only singular to me, that he, who was probably the most grateful of mankind upon the least occasion, should so desire to escape the gratitude of others.\n\nWe were all enchanted. I felt it a merited tribute to the engaging qualities of Ada and Richard that Mr. Skimpole, seeing them for the first time, should be so unreserved and should lay himself out to be so exquisitely agreeable. They (and especially Richard) were naturally pleased, for similar reasons, and considered it no common privilege to be so freely confided in by such an attractive man. The more we listened, the more gaily Mr. Skimpole talked. And what with his fine hilarious manner and his engaging candour and his genial way of lightly tossing his own weaknesses about, as if he had said, “I am a child, you know! You are designing people compared with me” (he really made me consider myself in that light) “but I am gay and innocent; forget your worldly arts and play with me!” the effect was absolutely dazzling.\n\nHe was so full of feeling too and had such a delicate sentiment for what was beautiful or tender that he could have won a heart by that alone. In the evening, when I was preparing to make tea and Ada was touching the piano in the adjoining room and softly humming a tune to her cousin Richard, which they had happened to mention, he came and sat down on the sofa near me and so spoke of Ada that I almost loved him.\n\n“She is like the morning,” he said. “With that golden hair, those blue eyes, and that fresh bloom on her cheek, she is like the summer morning. The birds here will mistake her for it. We will not call such a lovely young creature as that, who is a joy to all mankind, an orphan. She is the child of the universe.”\n\nMr. Jarndyce, I found, was standing near us with his hands behind him and an attentive smile upon his face.\n\n“The universe,” he observed, “makes rather an indifferent parent, I am afraid.”\n\n“Oh! I don’t know!” cried Mr. Skimpole buoyantly.\n\n“I think I do know,” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Well!” cried Mr. Skimpole. “You know the world (which in your sense is the universe), and I know nothing of it, so you shall have your way. But if I had mine,” glancing at the cousins, “there should be no brambles of sordid realities in such a path as that. It should be strewn with roses; it should lie through bowers, where there was no spring, autumn, nor winter, but perpetual summer. Age or change should never wither it. The base word money should never be breathed near it!”\n\nMr. Jarndyce patted him on the head with a smile, as if he had been really a child, and passing a step or two on, and stopping a moment, glanced at the young cousins. His look was thoughtful, but had a benignant expression in it which I often (how often!) saw again, which has long been engraven on my heart. The room in which they were, communicating with that in which he stood, was only lighted by the fire. Ada sat at the piano; Richard stood beside her, bending down. Upon the wall, their shadows blended together, surrounded by strange forms, not without a ghostly motion caught from the unsteady fire, though reflecting from motionless objects. Ada touched the notes so softly and sang so low that the wind, sighing away to the distant hills, was as audible as the music. The mystery of the future and the little clue afforded to it by the voice of the present seemed expressed in the whole picture.\n\nBut it is not to recall this fancy, well as I remember it, that I recall the scene. First, I was not quite unconscious of the contrast in respect of meaning and intention between the silent look directed that way and the flow of words that had preceded it. Secondly, though Mr. Jarndyce’s glance as he withdrew it rested for but a moment on me, I felt as if in that moment he confided to me—and knew that he confided to me and that I received the confidence—his hope that Ada and Richard might one day enter on a dearer relationship.\n\nMr. Skimpole could play on the piano and the violoncello, and he was a composer—had composed half an opera once, but got tired of it—and played what he composed with taste. After tea we had quite a little concert, in which Richard—who was enthralled by Ada’s singing and told me that she seemed to know all the songs that ever were written—and Mr. Jarndyce, and I were the audience. After a little while I missed first Mr. Skimpole and afterwards Richard, and while I was thinking how could Richard stay away so long and lose so much, the maid who had given me the keys looked in at the door, saying, “If you please, miss, could you spare a minute?”\n\nWhen I was shut out with her in the hall, she said, holding up her hands, “Oh, if you please, miss, Mr. Carstone says would you come upstairs to Mr. Skimpole’s room. He has been took, miss!”\n\n“Took?” said I.\n\n“Took, miss. Sudden,” said the maid.\n\nI was apprehensive that his illness might be of a dangerous kind, but of course I begged her to be quiet and not disturb any one and collected myself, as I followed her quickly upstairs, sufficiently to consider what were the best remedies to be applied if it should prove to be a fit. She threw open a door and I went into a chamber, where, to my unspeakable surprise, instead of finding Mr. Skimpole stretched upon the bed or prostrate on the floor, I found him standing before the fire smiling at Richard, while Richard, with a face of great embarrassment, looked at a person on the sofa, in a white great-coat, with smooth hair upon his head and not much of it, which he was wiping smoother and making less of with a pocket-handkerchief.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” said Richard hurriedly, “I am glad you are come. You will be able to advise us. Our friend Mr. Skimpole—don’t be alarmed!—is arrested for debt.”\n\n“And really, my dear Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Skimpole with his agreeable candour, “I never was in a situation in which that excellent sense and quiet habit of method and usefulness, which anybody must observe in you who has the happiness of being a quarter of an hour in your society, was more needed.”\n\nThe person on the sofa, who appeared to have a cold in his head, gave such a very loud snort that he startled me.\n\n“Are you arrested for much, sir?” I inquired of Mr. Skimpole.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson,” said he, shaking his head pleasantly, “I don’t know. Some pounds, odd shillings, and halfpence, I think, were mentioned.”\n\n“It’s twenty-four pound, sixteen, and sevenpence ha’penny,” observed the stranger. “That’s wot it is.”\n\n“And it sounds—somehow it sounds,” said Mr. Skimpole, “like a small sum?”\n\nThe strange man said nothing but made another snort. It was such a powerful one that it seemed quite to lift him out of his seat.\n\n“Mr. Skimpole,” said Richard to me, “has a delicacy in applying to my cousin Jarndyce because he has lately—I think, sir, I understood you that you had lately—”\n\n“Oh, yes!” returned Mr. Skimpole, smiling. “Though I forgot how much it was and when it was. Jarndyce would readily do it again, but I have the epicure-like feeling that I would prefer a novelty in help, that I would rather,” and he looked at Richard and me, “develop generosity in a new soil and in a new form of flower.”\n\n“What do you think will be best, Miss Summerson?” said Richard, aside.\n\nI ventured to inquire, generally, before replying, what would happen if the money were not produced.\n\n“Jail,” said the strange man, coolly putting his handkerchief into his hat, which was on the floor at his feet. “Or Coavinses.”\n\n“May I ask, sir, what is—”\n\n“Coavinses?” said the strange man. “A ’ouse.”\n\nRichard and I looked at one another again. It was a most singular thing that the arrest was our embarrassment and not Mr. Skimpole’s. He observed us with a genial interest, but there seemed, if I may venture on such a contradiction, nothing selfish in it. He had entirely washed his hands of the difficulty, and it had become ours.\n\n“I thought,” he suggested, as if good-naturedly to help us out, “that being parties in a Chancery suit concerning (as people say) a large amount of property, Mr. Richard or his beautiful cousin, or both, could sign something, or make over something, or give some sort of undertaking, or pledge, or bond? I don’t know what the business name of it may be, but I suppose there is some instrument within their power that would settle this?”\n\n“Not a bit on it,” said the strange man.\n\n“Really?” returned Mr. Skimpole. “That seems odd, now, to one who is no judge of these things!”\n\n“Odd or even,” said the stranger gruffly, “I tell you, not a bit on it!”\n\n“Keep your temper, my good fellow, keep your temper!” Mr. Skimpole gently reasoned with him as he made a little drawing of his head on the fly-leaf of a book. “Don’t be ruffled by your occupation. We can separate you from your office; we can separate the individual from the pursuit. We are not so prejudiced as to suppose that in private life you are otherwise than a very estimable man, with a great deal of poetry in your nature, of which you may not be conscious.”\n\nThe stranger only answered with another violent snort, whether in acceptance of the poetry-tribute or in disdainful rejection of it, he did not express to me.\n\n“Now, my dear Miss Summerson, and my dear Mr. Richard,” said Mr. Skimpole gaily, innocently, and confidingly as he looked at his drawing with his head on one side, “here you see me utterly incapable of helping myself, and entirely in your hands! I only ask to be free. The butterflies are free. Mankind will surely not deny to Harold Skimpole what it concedes to the butterflies!”\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson,” said Richard in a whisper, “I have ten pounds that I received from Mr. Kenge. I must try what that will do.”\n\nI possessed fifteen pounds, odd shillings, which I had saved from my quarterly allowance during several years. I had always thought that some accident might happen which would throw me suddenly, without any relation or any property, on the world and had always tried to keep some little money by me that I might not be quite penniless. I told Richard of my having this little store and having no present need of it, and I asked him delicately to inform Mr. Skimpole, while I should be gone to fetch it, that we would have the pleasure of paying his debt.\n\nWhen I came back, Mr. Skimpole kissed my hand and seemed quite touched. Not on his own account (I was again aware of that perplexing and extraordinary contradiction), but on ours, as if personal considerations were impossible with him and the contemplation of our happiness alone affected him. Richard, begging me, for the greater grace of the transaction, as he said, to settle with Coavinses (as Mr. Skimpole now jocularly called him), I counted out the money and received the necessary acknowledgment. This, too, delighted Mr. Skimpole.\n\nHis compliments were so delicately administered that I blushed less than I might have done and settled with the stranger in the white coat without making any mistakes. He put the money in his pocket and shortly said, “Well, then, I’ll wish you a good evening, miss.”\n\n“My friend,” said Mr. Skimpole, standing with his back to the fire after giving up the sketch when it was half finished, “I should like to ask you something, without offence.”\n\nI think the reply was, “Cut away, then!”\n\n“Did you know this morning, now, that you were coming out on this errand?” said Mr. Skimpole.\n\n“Know’d it yes’day aft’noon at tea-time,” said Coavinses.\n\n“It didn’t affect your appetite? Didn’t make you at all uneasy?”\n\n“Not a bit,” said Coavinses. “I know’d if you wos missed to-day, you wouldn’t be missed to-morrow. A day makes no such odds.”\n\n“But when you came down here,” proceeded Mr. Skimpole, “it was a fine day. The sun was shining, the wind was blowing, the lights and shadows were passing across the fields, the birds were singing.”\n\n“Nobody said they warn’t, in MY hearing,” returned Coavinses.\n\n“No,” observed Mr. Skimpole. “But what did you think upon the road?”\n\n“Wot do you mean?” growled Coavinses with an appearance of strong resentment. “Think! I’ve got enough to do, and little enough to get for it without thinking. Thinking!” (with profound contempt).\n\n“Then you didn’t think, at all events,” proceeded Mr. Skimpole, “to this effect: ‘Harold Skimpole loves to see the sun shine, loves to hear the wind blow, loves to watch the changing lights and shadows, loves to hear the birds, those choristers in Nature’s great cathedral. And does it seem to me that I am about to deprive Harold Skimpole of his share in such possessions, which are his only birthright!’ You thought nothing to that effect?”\n\n“I—certainly—did—NOT,” said Coavinses, whose doggedness in utterly renouncing the idea was of that intense kind that he could only give adequate expression to it by putting a long interval between each word, and accompanying the last with a jerk that might have dislocated his neck.\n\n“Very odd and very curious, the mental process is, in you men of business!” said Mr. Skimpole thoughtfully. “Thank you, my friend. Good night.”\n\nAs our absence had been long enough already to seem strange downstairs, I returned at once and found Ada sitting at work by the fireside talking to her cousin John. Mr. Skimpole presently appeared, and Richard shortly after him. I was sufficiently engaged during the remainder of the evening in taking my first lesson in backgammon from Mr. Jarndyce, who was very fond of the game and from whom I wished of course to learn it as quickly as I could in order that I might be of the very small use of being able to play when he had no better adversary. But I thought, occasionally, when Mr. Skimpole played some fragments of his own compositions or when, both at the piano and the violoncello, and at our table, he preserved with an absence of all effort his delightful spirits and his easy flow of conversation, that Richard and I seemed to retain the transferred impression of having been arrested since dinner and that it was very curious altogether.\n\nIt was late before we separated, for when Ada was going at eleven o’clock, Mr. Skimpole went to the piano and rattled hilariously that the best of all ways to lengthen our days was to steal a few hours from night, my dear! It was past twelve before he took his candle and his radiant face out of the room, and I think he might have kept us there, if he had seen fit, until daybreak. Ada and Richard were lingering for a few moments by the fire, wondering whether Mrs. Jellyby had yet finished her dictation for the day, when Mr. Jarndyce, who had been out of the room, returned.\n\n“Oh, dear me, what’s this, what’s this!” he said, rubbing his head and walking about with his good-humoured vexation. “What’s this they tell me? Rick, my boy, Esther, my dear, what have you been doing? Why did you do it? How could you do it? How much apiece was it? The wind’s round again. I feel it all over me!”\n\nWe neither of us quite knew what to answer.\n\n“Come, Rick, come! I must settle this before I sleep. How much are you out of pocket? You two made the money up, you know! Why did you? How could you? Oh, Lord, yes, it’s due east—must be!”\n\n“Really, sir,” said Richard, “I don’t think it would be honourable in me to tell you. Mr. Skimpole relied upon us—”\n\n“Lord bless you, my dear boy! He relies upon everybody!” said Mr. Jarndyce, giving his head a great rub and stopping short.\n\n“Indeed, sir?”\n\n“Everybody! And he’ll be in the same scrape again next week!” said Mr. Jarndyce, walking again at a great pace, with a candle in his hand that had gone out. “He’s always in the same scrape. He was born in the same scrape. I verily believe that the announcement in the newspapers when his mother was confined was ‘On Tuesday last, at her residence in Botheration Buildings, Mrs. Skimpole of a son in difficulties.’”\n\nRichard laughed heartily but added, “Still, sir, I don’t want to shake his confidence or to break his confidence, and if I submit to your better knowledge again, that I ought to keep his secret, I hope you will consider before you press me any more. Of course, if you do press me, sir, I shall know I am wrong and will tell you.”\n\n“Well!” cried Mr. Jarndyce, stopping again, and making several absent endeavours to put his candlestick in his pocket. “I—here! Take it away, my dear. I don’t know what I am about with it; it’s all the wind—invariably has that effect—I won’t press you, Rick; you may be right. But really—to get hold of you and Esther—and to squeeze you like a couple of tender young Saint Michael’s oranges! It’ll blow a gale in the course of the night!”\n\nHe was now alternately putting his hands into his pockets as if he were going to keep them there a long time, and taking them out again and vehemently rubbing them all over his head.\n\nI ventured to take this opportunity of hinting that Mr. Skimpole, being in all such matters quite a child—\n\n“Eh, my dear?” said Mr. Jarndyce, catching at the word.\n\n“Being quite a child, sir,” said I, “and so different from other people—”\n\n“You are right!” said Mr. Jarndyce, brightening. “Your woman’s wit hits the mark. He is a child—an absolute child. I told you he was a child, you know, when I first mentioned him.”\n\nCertainly! Certainly! we said.\n\n“And he IS a child. Now, isn’t he?” asked Mr. Jarndyce, brightening more and more.\n\nHe was indeed, we said.\n\n“When you come to think of it, it’s the height of childishness in you—I mean me—” said Mr. Jarndyce, “to regard him for a moment as a man. You can’t make HIM responsible. The idea of Harold Skimpole with designs or plans, or knowledge of consequences! Ha, ha, ha!”\n\nIt was so delicious to see the clouds about his bright face clearing, and to see him so heartily pleased, and to know, as it was impossible not to know, that the source of his pleasure was the goodness which was tortured by condemning, or mistrusting, or secretly accusing any one, that I saw the tears in Ada’s eyes, while she echoed his laugh, and felt them in my own.\n\n“Why, what a cod’s head and shoulders I am,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “to require reminding of it! The whole business shows the child from beginning to end. Nobody but a child would have thought of singling YOU two out for parties in the affair! Nobody but a child would have thought of YOUR having the money! If it had been a thousand pounds, it would have been just the same!” said Mr. Jarndyce with his whole face in a glow.\n\nWe all confirmed it from our night’s experience.\n\n“To be sure, to be sure!” said Mr. Jarndyce. “However, Rick, Esther, and you too, Ada, for I don’t know that even your little purse is safe from his inexperience—I must have a promise all round that nothing of this sort shall ever be done any more. No advances! Not even sixpences.”\n\nWe all promised faithfully, Richard with a merry glance at me touching his pocket as if to remind me that there was no danger of OUR transgressing.\n\n“As to Skimpole,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “a habitable doll’s house with good board and a few tin people to get into debt with and borrow money of would set the boy up in life. He is in a child’s sleep by this time, I suppose; it’s time I should take my craftier head to my more worldly pillow. Good night, my dears. God bless you!”\n\nHe peeped in again, with a smiling face, before we had lighted our candles, and said, “Oh! I have been looking at the weather-cock. I find it was a false alarm about the wind. It’s in the south!” And went away singing to himself.\n\nAda and I agreed, as we talked together for a little while upstairs, that this caprice about the wind was a fiction and that he used the pretence to account for any disappointment he could not conceal, rather than he would blame the real cause of it or disparage or depreciate any one. We thought this very characteristic of his eccentric gentleness and of the difference between him and those petulant people who make the weather and the winds (particularly that unlucky wind which he had chosen for such a different purpose) the stalking-horses of their splenetic and gloomy humours.\n\nIndeed, so much affection for him had been added in this one evening to my gratitude that I hoped I already began to understand him through that mingled feeling. Any seeming inconsistencies in Mr. Skimpole or in Mrs. Jellyby I could not expect to be able to reconcile, having so little experience or practical knowledge. Neither did I try, for my thoughts were busy when I was alone, with Ada and Richard and with the confidence I had seemed to receive concerning them. My fancy, made a little wild by the wind perhaps, would not consent to be all unselfish, either, though I would have persuaded it to be so if I could. It wandered back to my godmother’s house and came along the intervening track, raising up shadowy speculations which had sometimes trembled there in the dark as to what knowledge Mr. Jarndyce had of my earliest history—even as to the possibility of his being my father, though that idle dream was quite gone now.\n\nIt was all gone now, I remembered, getting up from the fire. It was not for me to muse over bygones, but to act with a cheerful spirit and a grateful heart. So I said to myself, “Esther, Esther, Esther! Duty, my dear!” and gave my little basket of housekeeping keys such a shake that they sounded like little bells and rang me hopefully to bed.\nThe Ghost’s Walk\n\nWhile Esther sleeps, and while Esther wakes, it is still wet weather down at the place in Lincolnshire. The rain is ever falling—drip, drip, drip—by day and night upon the broad flagged terrace-pavement, the Ghost’s Walk. The weather is so very bad down in Lincolnshire that the liveliest imagination can scarcely apprehend its ever being fine again. Not that there is any superabundant life of imagination on the spot, for Sir Leicester is not here (and, truly, even if he were, would not do much for it in that particular), but is in Paris with my Lady; and solitude, with dusky wings, sits brooding upon Chesney Wold.\n\nThere may be some motions of fancy among the lower animals at Chesney Wold. The horses in the stables—the long stables in a barren, red-brick court-yard, where there is a great bell in a turret, and a clock with a large face, which the pigeons who live near it and who love to perch upon its shoulders seem to be always consulting—THEY may contemplate some mental pictures of fine weather on occasions, and may be better artists at them than the grooms. The old roan, so famous for cross-country work, turning his large eyeball to the grated window near his rack, may remember the fresh leaves that glisten there at other times and the scents that stream in, and may have a fine run with the hounds, while the human helper, clearing out the next stall, never stirs beyond his pitchfork and birch-broom. The grey, whose place is opposite the door and who with an impatient rattle of his halter pricks his ears and turns his head so wistfully when it is opened, and to whom the opener says, “Woa grey, then, steady! Noabody wants you to-day!” may know it quite as well as the man. The whole seemingly monotonous and uncompanionable half-dozen, stabled together, may pass the long wet hours when the door is shut in livelier communication than is held in the servants’ hall or at the Dedlock Arms, or may even beguile the time by improving (perhaps corrupting) the pony in the loose-box in the corner.\n\nSo the mastiff, dozing in his kennel in the court-yard with his large head on his paws, may think of the hot sunshine when the shadows of the stable-buildings tire his patience out by changing and leave him at one time of the day no broader refuge than the shadow of his own house, where he sits on end, panting and growling short, and very much wanting something to worry besides himself and his chain. So now, half-waking and all-winking, he may recall the house full of company, the coach-houses full of vehicles, the stables full of horses, and the out-buildings full of attendants upon horses, until he is undecided about the present and comes forth to see how it is. Then, with that impatient shake of himself, he may growl in the spirit, “Rain, rain, rain! Nothing but rain—and no family here!” as he goes in again and lies down with a gloomy yawn.\n\nSo with the dogs in the kennel-buildings across the park, who have their restless fits and whose doleful voices when the wind has been very obstinate have even made it known in the house itself—upstairs, downstairs, and in my Lady’s chamber. They may hunt the whole country-side, while the raindrops are pattering round their inactivity. So the rabbits with their self-betraying tails, frisking in and out of holes at roots of trees, may be lively with ideas of the breezy days when their ears are blown about or of those seasons of interest when there are sweet young plants to gnaw. The turkey in the poultry-yard, always troubled with a class-grievance (probably Christmas), may be reminiscent of that summer morning wrongfully taken from him when he got into the lane among the felled trees, where there was a barn and barley. The discontented goose, who stoops to pass under the old gateway, twenty feet high, may gabble out, if we only knew it, a waddling preference for weather when the gateway casts its shadow on the ground.\n\nBe this as it may, there is not much fancy otherwise stirring at Chesney Wold. If there be a little at any odd moment, it goes, like a little noise in that old echoing place, a long way and usually leads off to ghosts and mystery.\n\nIt has rained so hard and rained so long down in Lincolnshire that Mrs. Rouncewell, the old housekeeper at Chesney Wold, has several times taken off her spectacles and cleaned them to make certain that the drops were not upon the glasses. Mrs. Rouncewell might have been sufficiently assured by hearing the rain, but that she is rather deaf, which nothing will induce her to believe. She is a fine old lady, handsome, stately, wonderfully neat, and has such a back and such a stomacher that if her stays should turn out when she dies to have been a broad old-fashioned family fire-grate, nobody who knows her would have cause to be surprised. Weather affects Mrs. Rouncewell little. The house is there in all weathers, and the house, as she expresses it, “is what she looks at.” She sits in her room (in a side passage on the ground floor, with an arched window commanding a smooth quadrangle, adorned at regular intervals with smooth round trees and smooth round blocks of stone, as if the trees were going to play at bowls with the stones), and the whole house reposes on her mind. She can open it on occasion and be busy and fluttered, but it is shut up now and lies on the breadth of Mrs. Rouncewell’s iron-bound bosom in a majestic sleep.\n\nIt is the next difficult thing to an impossibility to imagine Chesney Wold without Mrs. Rouncewell, but she has only been here fifty years. Ask her how long, this rainy day, and she shall answer “fifty year, three months, and a fortnight, by the blessing of heaven, if I live till Tuesday.” Mr. Rouncewell died some time before the decease of the pretty fashion of pig-tails, and modestly hid his own (if he took it with him) in a corner of the churchyard in the park near the mouldy porch. He was born in the market-town, and so was his young widow. Her progress in the family began in the time of the last Sir Leicester and originated in the still-room.\n\nThe present representative of the Dedlocks is an excellent master. He supposes all his dependents to be utterly bereft of individual characters, intentions, or opinions, and is persuaded that he was born to supersede the necessity of their having any. If he were to make a discovery to the contrary, he would be simply stunned—would never recover himself, most likely, except to gasp and die. But he is an excellent master still, holding it a part of his state to be so. He has a great liking for Mrs. Rouncewell; he says she is a most respectable, creditable woman. He always shakes hands with her when he comes down to Chesney Wold and when he goes away; and if he were very ill, or if he were knocked down by accident, or run over, or placed in any situation expressive of a Dedlock at a disadvantage, he would say if he could speak, “Leave me, and send Mrs. Rouncewell here!” feeling his dignity, at such a pass, safer with her than with anybody else.\n\nMrs. Rouncewell has known trouble. She has had two sons, of whom the younger ran wild, and went for a soldier, and never came back. Even to this hour, Mrs. Rouncewell’s calm hands lose their composure when she speaks of him, and unfolding themselves from her stomacher, hover about her in an agitated manner as she says what a likely lad, what a fine lad, what a gay, good-humoured, clever lad he was! Her second son would have been provided for at Chesney Wold and would have been made steward in due season, but he took, when he was a schoolboy, to constructing steam-engines out of saucepans and setting birds to draw their own water with the least possible amount of labour, so assisting them with artful contrivance of hydraulic pressure that a thirsty canary had only, in a literal sense, to put his shoulder to the wheel and the job was done. This propensity gave Mrs. Rouncewell great uneasiness. She felt it with a mother’s anguish to be a move in the Wat Tyler direction, well knowing that Sir Leicester had that general impression of an aptitude for any art to which smoke and a tall chimney might be considered essential. But the doomed young rebel (otherwise a mild youth, and very persevering), showing no sign of grace as he got older but, on the contrary, constructing a model of a power-loom, she was fain, with many tears, to mention his backslidings to the baronet. “Mrs. Rouncewell,” said Sir Leicester, “I can never consent to argue, as you know, with any one on any subject. You had better get rid of your boy; you had better get him into some Works. The iron country farther north is, I suppose, the congenial direction for a boy with these tendencies.” Farther north he went, and farther north he grew up; and if Sir Leicester Dedlock ever saw him when he came to Chesney Wold to visit his mother, or ever thought of him afterwards, it is certain that he only regarded him as one of a body of some odd thousand conspirators, swarthy and grim, who were in the habit of turning out by torchlight two or three nights in the week for unlawful purposes.\n\nNevertheless, Mrs. Rouncewell’s son has, in the course of nature and art, grown up, and established himself, and married, and called unto him Mrs. Rouncewell’s grandson, who, being out of his apprenticeship, and home from a journey in far countries, whither he was sent to enlarge his knowledge and complete his preparations for the venture of this life, stands leaning against the chimney-piece this very day in Mrs. Rouncewell’s room at Chesney Wold.\n\n“And, again and again, I am glad to see you, Watt! And, once again, I am glad to see you, Watt!” says Mrs. Rouncewell. “You are a fine young fellow. You are like your poor uncle George. Ah!” Mrs. Rouncewell’s hands unquiet, as usual, on this reference.\n\n“They say I am like my father, grandmother.”\n\n“Like him, also, my dear—but most like your poor uncle George! And your dear father.” Mrs. Rouncewell folds her hands again. “He is well?”\n\n“Thriving, grandmother, in every way.”\n\n“I am thankful!” Mrs. Rouncewell is fond of her son but has a plaintive feeling towards him, much as if he were a very honourable soldier who had gone over to the enemy.\n\n“He is quite happy?” says she.\n\n“Quite.”\n\n“I am thankful! So he has brought you up to follow in his ways and has sent you into foreign countries and the like? Well, he knows best. There may be a world beyond Chesney Wold that I don’t understand. Though I am not young, either. And I have seen a quantity of good company too!”\n\n“Grandmother,” says the young man, changing the subject, “what a very pretty girl that was I found with you just now. You called her Rosa?”\n\n“Yes, child. She is daughter of a widow in the village. Maids are so hard to teach, now-a-days, that I have put her about me young. She’s an apt scholar and will do well. She shows the house already, very pretty. She lives with me at my table here.”\n\n“I hope I have not driven her away?”\n\n“She supposes we have family affairs to speak about, I dare say. She is very modest. It is a fine quality in a young woman. And scarcer,” says Mrs. Rouncewell, expanding her stomacher to its utmost limits, “than it formerly was!”\n\nThe young man inclines his head in acknowledgment of the precepts of experience. Mrs. Rouncewell listens.\n\n“Wheels!” says she. They have long been audible to the younger ears of her companion. “What wheels on such a day as this, for gracious sake?”\n\nAfter a short interval, a tap at the door. “Come in!” A dark-eyed, dark-haired, shy, village beauty comes in—so fresh in her rosy and yet delicate bloom that the drops of rain which have beaten on her hair look like the dew upon a flower fresh gathered.\n\n“What company is this, Rosa?” says Mrs. Rouncewell.\n\n“It’s two young men in a gig, ma’am, who want to see the house—yes, and if you please, I told them so!” in quick reply to a gesture of dissent from the housekeeper. “I went to the hall-door and told them it was the wrong day and the wrong hour, but the young man who was driving took off his hat in the wet and begged me to bring this card to you.”\n\n“Read it, my dear Watt,” says the housekeeper.\n\nRosa is so shy as she gives it to him that they drop it between them and almost knock their foreheads together as they pick it up. Rosa is shyer than before.\n\n“Mr. Guppy” is all the information the card yields.\n\n“Guppy!” repeats Mrs. Rouncewell, “MR. Guppy! Nonsense, I never heard of him!”\n\n“If you please, he told ME that!” says Rosa. “But he said that he and the other young gentleman came from London only last night by the mail, on business at the magistrates’ meeting, ten miles off, this morning, and that as their business was soon over, and they had heard a great deal said of Chesney Wold, and really didn’t know what to do with themselves, they had come through the wet to see it. They are lawyers. He says he is not in Mr. Tulkinghorn’s office, but he is sure he may make use of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s name if necessary.” Finding, now she leaves off, that she has been making quite a long speech, Rosa is shyer than ever.\n\nNow, Mr. Tulkinghorn is, in a manner, part and parcel of the place, and besides, is supposed to have made Mrs. Rouncewell’s will. The old lady relaxes, consents to the admission of the visitors as a favour, and dismisses Rosa. The grandson, however, being smitten by a sudden wish to see the house himself, proposes to join the party. The grandmother, who is pleased that he should have that interest, accompanies him—though to do him justice, he is exceedingly unwilling to trouble her.\n\n“Much obliged to you, ma’am!” says Mr. Guppy, divesting himself of his wet dreadnought in the hall. “Us London lawyers don’t often get an out, and when we do, we like to make the most of it, you know.”\n\nThe old housekeeper, with a gracious severity of deportment, waves her hand towards the great staircase. Mr. Guppy and his friend follow Rosa; Mrs. Rouncewell and her grandson follow them; a young gardener goes before to open the shutters.\n\nAs is usually the case with people who go over houses, Mr. Guppy and his friend are dead beat before they have well begun. They straggle about in wrong places, look at wrong things, don’t care for the right things, gape when more rooms are opened, exhibit profound depression of spirits, and are clearly knocked up. In each successive chamber that they enter, Mrs. Rouncewell, who is as upright as the house itself, rests apart in a window-seat or other such nook and listens with stately approval to Rosa’s exposition. Her grandson is so attentive to it that Rosa is shyer than ever—and prettier. Thus they pass on from room to room, raising the pictured Dedlocks for a few brief minutes as the young gardener admits the light, and reconsigning them to their graves as he shuts it out again. It appears to the afflicted Mr. Guppy and his inconsolable friend that there is no end to the Dedlocks, whose family greatness seems to consist in their never having done anything to distinguish themselves for seven hundred years.\n\nEven the long drawing-room of Chesney Wold cannot revive Mr. Guppy’s spirits. He is so low that he droops on the threshold and has hardly strength of mind to enter. But a portrait over the chimney-piece, painted by the fashionable artist of the day, acts upon him like a charm. He recovers in a moment. He stares at it with uncommon interest; he seems to be fixed and fascinated by it.\n\n“Dear me!” says Mr. Guppy. “Who’s that?”\n\n“The picture over the fire-place,” says Rosa, “is the portrait of the present Lady Dedlock. It is considered a perfect likeness, and the best work of the master.”\n\n“Blest,” says Mr. Guppy, staring in a kind of dismay at his friend, “if I can ever have seen her. Yet I know her! Has the picture been engraved, miss?”\n\n“The picture has never been engraved. Sir Leicester has always refused permission.”\n\n“Well!” says Mr. Guppy in a low voice. “I’ll be shot if it ain’t very curious how well I know that picture! So that’s Lady Dedlock, is it!”\n\n“The picture on the right is the present Sir Leicester Dedlock. The picture on the left is his father, the late Sir Leicester.”\n\nMr. Guppy has no eyes for either of these magnates. “It’s unaccountable to me,” he says, still staring at the portrait, “how well I know that picture! I’m dashed,” adds Mr. Guppy, looking round, “if I don’t think I must have had a dream of that picture, you know!”\n\nAs no one present takes any especial interest in Mr. Guppy’s dreams, the probability is not pursued. But he still remains so absorbed by the portrait that he stands immovable before it until the young gardener has closed the shutters, when he comes out of the room in a dazed state that is an odd though a sufficient substitute for interest and follows into the succeeding rooms with a confused stare, as if he were looking everywhere for Lady Dedlock again.\n\nHe sees no more of her. He sees her rooms, which are the last shown, as being very elegant, and he looks out of the windows from which she looked out, not long ago, upon the weather that bored her to death. All things have an end, even houses that people take infinite pains to see and are tired of before they begin to see them. He has come to the end of the sight, and the fresh village beauty to the end of her description; which is always this: “The terrace below is much admired. It is called, from an old story in the family, the Ghost’s Walk.”\n\n“No?” says Mr. Guppy, greedily curious. “What’s the story, miss? Is it anything about a picture?”\n\n“Pray tell us the story,” says Watt in a half whisper.\n\n“I don’t know it, sir.” Rosa is shyer than ever.\n\n“It is not related to visitors; it is almost forgotten,” says the housekeeper, advancing. “It has never been more than a family anecdote.”\n\n“You’ll excuse my asking again if it has anything to do with a picture, ma’am,” observes Mr. Guppy, “because I do assure you that the more I think of that picture the better I know it, without knowing how I know it!”\n\nThe story has nothing to do with a picture; the housekeeper can guarantee that. Mr. Guppy is obliged to her for the information and is, moreover, generally obliged. He retires with his friend, guided down another staircase by the young gardener, and presently is heard to drive away. It is now dusk. Mrs. Rouncewell can trust to the discretion of her two young hearers and may tell THEM how the terrace came to have that ghostly name.\n\nShe seats herself in a large chair by the fast-darkening window and tells them: “In the wicked days, my dears, of King Charles the First—I mean, of course, in the wicked days of the rebels who leagued themselves against that excellent king—Sir Morbury Dedlock was the owner of Chesney Wold. Whether there was any account of a ghost in the family before those days, I can’t say. I should think it very likely indeed.”\n\nMrs. Rouncewell holds this opinion because she considers that a family of such antiquity and importance has a right to a ghost. She regards a ghost as one of the privileges of the upper classes, a genteel distinction to which the common people have no claim.\n\n“Sir Morbury Dedlock,” says Mrs. Rouncewell, “was, I have no occasion to say, on the side of the blessed martyr. But it IS supposed that his Lady, who had none of the family blood in her veins, favoured the bad cause. It is said that she had relations among King Charles’s enemies, that she was in correspondence with them, and that she gave them information. When any of the country gentlemen who followed his Majesty’s cause met here, it is said that my Lady was always nearer to the door of their council-room than they supposed. Do you hear a sound like a footstep passing along the terrace, Watt?”\n\nRosa draws nearer to the housekeeper.\n\n“I hear the rain-drip on the stones,” replies the young man, “and I hear a curious echo—I suppose an echo—which is very like a halting step.”\n\nThe housekeeper gravely nods and continues: “Partly on account of this division between them, and partly on other accounts, Sir Morbury and his Lady led a troubled life. She was a lady of a haughty temper. They were not well suited to each other in age or character, and they had no children to moderate between them. After her favourite brother, a young gentleman, was killed in the civil wars (by Sir Morbury’s near kinsman), her feeling was so violent that she hated the race into which she had married. When the Dedlocks were about to ride out from Chesney Wold in the king’s cause, she is supposed to have more than once stolen down into the stables in the dead of night and lamed their horses; and the story is that once at such an hour, her husband saw her gliding down the stairs and followed her into the stall where his own favourite horse stood. There he seized her by the wrist, and in a struggle or in a fall or through the horse being frightened and lashing out, she was lamed in the hip and from that hour began to pine away.”\n\nThe housekeeper has dropped her voice to a little more than a whisper.\n\n“She had been a lady of a handsome figure and a noble carriage. She never complained of the change; she never spoke to any one of being crippled or of being in pain, but day by day she tried to walk upon the terrace, and with the help of the stone balustrade, went up and down, up and down, up and down, in sun and shadow, with greater difficulty every day. At last, one afternoon her husband (to whom she had never, on any persuasion, opened her lips since that night), standing at the great south window, saw her drop upon the pavement. He hastened down to raise her, but she repulsed him as he bent over her, and looking at him fixedly and coldly, said, ‘I will die here where I have walked. And I will walk here, though I am in my grave. I will walk here until the pride of this house is humbled. And when calamity or when disgrace is coming to it, let the Dedlocks listen for my step!’”\n\nWatt looks at Rosa. Rosa in the deepening gloom looks down upon the ground, half frightened and half shy.\n\n“There and then she died. And from those days,” says Mrs. Rouncewell, “the name has come down—the Ghost’s Walk. If the tread is an echo, it is an echo that is only heard after dark, and is often unheard for a long while together. But it comes back from time to time; and so sure as there is sickness or death in the family, it will be heard then.”\n\n“And disgrace, grandmother—” says Watt.\n\n“Disgrace never comes to Chesney Wold,” returns the housekeeper.\n\nHer grandson apologizes with “True. True.”\n\n“That is the story. Whatever the sound is, it is a worrying sound,” says Mrs. Rouncewell, getting up from her chair; “and what is to be noticed in it is that it MUST BE HEARD. My Lady, who is afraid of nothing, admits that when it is there, it must be heard. You cannot shut it out. Watt, there is a tall French clock behind you (placed there, ’a purpose) that has a loud beat when it is in motion and can play music. You understand how those things are managed?”\n\n“Pretty well, grandmother, I think.”\n\n“Set it a-going.”\n\nWatt sets it a-going—music and all.\n\n“Now, come hither,” says the housekeeper. “Hither, child, towards my Lady’s pillow. I am not sure that it is dark enough yet, but listen! Can you hear the sound upon the terrace, through the music, and the beat, and everything?”\n\n“I certainly can!”\n\n“So my Lady says.”\n\nCovering a Multitude of Sins\nIt was interesting when I dressed before daylight to peep out of window, where my candles were reflected in the black panes like two beacons, and finding all beyond still enshrouded in the indistinctness of last night, to watch how it turned out when the day came on. As the prospect gradually revealed itself and disclosed the scene over which the wind had wandered in the dark, like my memory over my life, I had a pleasure in discovering the unknown objects that had been around me in my sleep. At first they were faintly discernible in the mist, and above them the later stars still glimmered. That pale interval over, the picture began to enlarge and fill up so fast that at every new peep I could have found enough to look at for an hour. Imperceptibly my candles became the only incongruous part of the morning, the dark places in my room all melted away, and the day shone bright upon a cheerful landscape, prominent in which the old Abbey Church, with its massive tower, threw a softer train of shadow on the view than seemed compatible with its rugged character. But so from rough outsides (I hope I have learnt), serene and gentle influences often proceed.\n\nEvery part of the house was in such order, and every one was so attentive to me, that I had no trouble with my two bunches of keys, though what with trying to remember the contents of each little store-room drawer and cupboard; and what with making notes on a slate about jams, and pickles, and preserves, and bottles, and glass, and china, and a great many other things; and what with being generally a methodical, old-maidish sort of foolish little person, I was so busy that I could not believe it was breakfast-time when I heard the bell ring. Away I ran, however, and made tea, as I had already been installed into the responsibility of the tea-pot; and then, as they were all rather late and nobody was down yet, I thought I would take a peep at the garden and get some knowledge of that too. I found it quite a delightful place—in front, the pretty avenue and drive by which we had approached (and where, by the by, we had cut up the gravel so terribly with our wheels that I asked the gardener to roll it); at the back, the flower-garden, with my darling at her window up there, throwing it open to smile out at me, as if she would have kissed me from that distance. Beyond the flower-garden was a kitchen-garden, and then a paddock, and then a snug little rick-yard, and then a dear little farm-yard. As to the house itself, with its three peaks in the roof; its various-shaped windows, some so large, some so small, and all so pretty; its trellis-work, against the south-front for roses and honey-suckle, and its homely, comfortable, welcoming look—it was, as Ada said when she came out to meet me with her arm through that of its master, worthy of her cousin John, a bold thing to say, though he only pinched her dear cheek for it.\n\nMr. Skimpole was as agreeable at breakfast as he had been overnight. There was honey on the table, and it led him into a discourse about bees. He had no objection to honey, he said (and I should think he had not, for he seemed to like it), but he protested against the overweening assumptions of bees. He didn’t at all see why the busy bee should be proposed as a model to him; he supposed the bee liked to make honey, or he wouldn’t do it—nobody asked him. It was not necessary for the bee to make such a merit of his tastes. If every confectioner went buzzing about the world banging against everything that came in his way and egotistically calling upon everybody to take notice that he was going to his work and must not be interrupted, the world would be quite an unsupportable place. Then, after all, it was a ridiculous position to be smoked out of your fortune with brimstone as soon as you had made it. You would have a very mean opinion of a Manchester man if he spun cotton for no other purpose. He must say he thought a drone the embodiment of a pleasanter and wiser idea. The drone said unaffectedly, “You will excuse me; I really cannot attend to the shop! I find myself in a world in which there is so much to see and so short a time to see it in that I must take the liberty of looking about me and begging to be provided for by somebody who doesn’t want to look about him.” This appeared to Mr. Skimpole to be the drone philosophy, and he thought it a very good philosophy, always supposing the drone to be willing to be on good terms with the bee, which, so far as he knew, the easy fellow always was, if the consequential creature would only let him, and not be so conceited about his honey!\n\nHe pursued this fancy with the lightest foot over a variety of ground and made us all merry, though again he seemed to have as serious a meaning in what he said as he was capable of having. I left them still listening to him when I withdrew to attend to my new duties. They had occupied me for some time, and I was passing through the passages on my return with my basket of keys on my arm when Mr. Jarndyce called me into a small room next his bed-chamber, which I found to be in part a little library of books and papers and in part quite a little museum of his boots and shoes and hat-boxes.\n\n“Sit down, my dear,” said Mr. Jarndyce. “This, you must know, is the growlery. When I am out of humour, I come and growl here.”\n\n“You must be here very seldom, sir,” said I.\n\n“Oh, you don’t know me!” he returned. “When I am deceived or disappointed in—the wind, and it’s easterly, I take refuge here. The growlery is the best-used room in the house. You are not aware of half my humours yet. My dear, how you are trembling!”\n\nI could not help it; I tried very hard, but being alone with that benevolent presence, and meeting his kind eyes, and feeling so happy and so honoured there, and my heart so full—I kissed his hand. I don’t know what I said, or even that I spoke. He was disconcerted and walked to the window; I almost believed with an intention of jumping out, until he turned and I was reassured by seeing in his eyes what he had gone there to hide. He gently patted me on the head, and I sat down.\n\n“There! There!” he said. “That’s over. Pooh! Don’t be foolish.”\n\n“It shall not happen again, sir,” I returned, “but at first it is difficult—”\n\n“Nonsense!” he said. “It’s easy, easy. Why not? I hear of a good little orphan girl without a protector, and I take it into my head to be that protector. She grows up, and more than justifies my good opinion, and I remain her guardian and her friend. What is there in all this? So, so! Now, we have cleared off old scores, and I have before me thy pleasant, trusting, trusty face again.”\n\nI said to myself, “Esther, my dear, you surprise me! This really is not what I expected of you!” And it had such a good effect that I folded my hands upon my basket and quite recovered myself. Mr. Jarndyce, expressing his approval in his face, began to talk to me as confidentially as if I had been in the habit of conversing with him every morning for I don’t know how long. I almost felt as if I had.\n\n“Of course, Esther,” he said, “you don’t understand this Chancery business?”\n\nAnd of course I shook my head.\n\n“I don’t know who does,” he returned. “The lawyers have twisted it into such a state of bedevilment that the original merits of the case have long disappeared from the face of the earth. It’s about a will and the trusts under a will—or it was once. It’s about nothing but costs now. We are always appearing, and disappearing, and swearing, and interrogating, and filing, and cross-filing, and arguing, and sealing, and motioning, and referring, and reporting, and revolving about the Lord Chancellor and all his satellites, and equitably waltzing ourselves off to dusty death, about costs. That’s the great question. All the rest, by some extraordinary means, has melted away.”\n\n“But it was, sir,” said I, to bring him back, for he began to rub his head, “about a will?”\n\n“Why, yes, it was about a will when it was about anything,” he returned. “A certain Jarndyce, in an evil hour, made a great fortune, and made a great will. In the question how the trusts under that will are to be administered, the fortune left by the will is squandered away; the legatees under the will are reduced to such a miserable condition that they would be sufficiently punished if they had committed an enormous crime in having money left them, and the will itself is made a dead letter. All through the deplorable cause, everything that everybody in it, except one man, knows already is referred to that only one man who don’t know, it to find out—all through the deplorable cause, everybody must have copies, over and over again, of everything that has accumulated about it in the way of cartloads of papers (or must pay for them without having them, which is the usual course, for nobody wants them) and must go down the middle and up again through such an infernal country-dance of costs and fees and nonsense and corruption as was never dreamed of in the wildest visions of a witch’s Sabbath. Equity sends questions to law, law sends questions back to equity; law finds it can’t do this, equity finds it can’t do that; neither can so much as say it can’t do anything, without this solicitor instructing and this counsel appearing for A, and that solicitor instructing and that counsel appearing for B; and so on through the whole alphabet, like the history of the apple pie. And thus, through years and years, and lives and lives, everything goes on, constantly beginning over and over again, and nothing ever ends. And we can’t get out of the suit on any terms, for we are made parties to it, and MUST BE parties to it, whether we like it or not. But it won’t do to think of it! When my great uncle, poor Tom Jarndyce, began to think of it, it was the beginning of the end!”\n\n“The Mr. Jarndyce, sir, whose story I have heard?”\n\nHe nodded gravely. “I was his heir, and this was his house, Esther. When I came here, it was bleak indeed. He had left the signs of his misery upon it.”\n\n“How changed it must be now!” I said.\n\n“It had been called, before his time, the Peaks. He gave it its present name and lived here shut up, day and night poring over the wicked heaps of papers in the suit and hoping against hope to disentangle it from its mystification and bring it to a close. In the meantime, the place became dilapidated, the wind whistled through the cracked walls, the rain fell through the broken roof, the weeds choked the passage to the rotting door. When I brought what remained of him home here, the brains seemed to me to have been blown out of the house too, it was so shattered and ruined.”\n\nHe walked a little to and fro after saying this to himself with a shudder, and then looked at me, and brightened, and came and sat down again with his hands in his pockets.\n\n“I told you this was the growlery, my dear. Where was I?”\n\nI reminded him, at the hopeful change he had made in Bleak House.\n\n“Bleak House; true. There is, in that city of London there, some property of ours which is much at this day what Bleak House was then; I say property of ours, meaning of the suit’s, but I ought to call it the property of costs, for costs is the only power on earth that will ever get anything out of it now or will ever know it for anything but an eyesore and a heartsore. It is a street of perishing blind houses, with their eyes stoned out, without a pane of glass, without so much as a window-frame, with the bare blank shutters tumbling from their hinges and falling asunder, the iron rails peeling away in flakes of rust, the chimneys sinking in, the stone steps to every door (and every door might be death’s door) turning stagnant green, the very crutches on which the ruins are propped decaying. Although Bleak House was not in Chancery, its master was, and it was stamped with the same seal. These are the Great Seal’s impressions, my dear, all over England—the children know them!”\n\n“How changed it is!” I said again.\n\n“Why, so it is,” he answered much more cheerfully; “and it is wisdom in you to keep me to the bright side of the picture.” (The idea of my wisdom!) “These are things I never talk about or even think about, excepting in the growlery here. If you consider it right to mention them to Rick and Ada,” looking seriously at me, “you can. I leave it to your discretion, Esther.”\n\n“I hope, sir—” said I.\n\n“I think you had better call me guardian, my dear.”\n\nI felt that I was choking again—I taxed myself with it, “Esther, now, you know you are!”—when he feigned to say this slightly, as if it were a whim instead of a thoughtful tenderness. But I gave the housekeeping keys the least shake in the world as a reminder to myself, and folding my hands in a still more determined manner on the basket, looked at him quietly.\n\n“I hope, guardian,” said I, “that you may not trust too much to my discretion. I hope you may not mistake me. I am afraid it will be a disappointment to you to know that I am not clever, but it really is the truth, and you would soon find it out if I had not the honesty to confess it.”\n\nHe did not seem at all disappointed; quite the contrary. He told me, with a smile all over his face, that he knew me very well indeed and that I was quite clever enough for him.\n\n“I hope I may turn out so,” said I, “but I am much afraid of it, guardian.”\n\n“You are clever enough to be the good little woman of our lives here, my dear,” he returned playfully; “the little old woman of the child’s (I don’t mean Skimpole’s) rhyme:\n\n“‘Little old woman, and whither so high?’ ‘To sweep the cobwebs out of the sky.’”\n\n“You will sweep them so neatly out of OUR sky in the course of your housekeeping, Esther, that one of these days we shall have to abandon the growlery and nail up the door.”\n\nThis was the beginning of my being called Old Woman, and Little Old Woman, and Cobweb, and Mrs. Shipton, and Mother Hubbard, and Dame Durden, and so many names of that sort that my own name soon became quite lost among them.\n\n“However,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “to return to our gossip. Here’s Rick, a fine young fellow full of promise. What’s to be done with him?”\n\nOh, my goodness, the idea of asking my advice on such a point!\n\n“Here he is, Esther,” said Mr. Jarndyce, comfortably putting his hands into his pockets and stretching out his legs. “He must have a profession; he must make some choice for himself. There will be a world more wiglomeration about it, I suppose, but it must be done.”\n\n“More what, guardian?” said I.\n\n“More wiglomeration,” said he. “It’s the only name I know for the thing. He is a ward in Chancery, my dear. Kenge and Carboy will have something to say about it; Master Somebody—a sort of ridiculous sexton, digging graves for the merits of causes in a back room at the end of Quality Court, Chancery Lane—will have something to say about it; counsel will have something to say about it; the Chancellor will have something to say about it; the satellites will have something to say about it; they will all have to be handsomely fee’d, all round, about it; the whole thing will be vastly ceremonious, wordy, unsatisfactory, and expensive, and I call it, in general, wiglomeration. How mankind ever came to be afflicted with Wiglomeration, or for whose sins these young people ever fell into a pit of it, I don’t know; so it is.”\n\nHe began to rub his head again and to hint that he felt the wind. But it was a delightful instance of his kindness towards me that whether he rubbed his head, or walked about, or did both, his face was sure to recover its benignant expression as it looked at mine; and he was sure to turn comfortable again and put his hands in his pockets and stretch out his legs.\n\n“Perhaps it would be best, first of all,” said I, “to ask Mr. Richard what he inclines to himself.”\n\n“Exactly so,” he returned. “That’s what I mean! You know, just accustom yourself to talk it over, with your tact and in your quiet way, with him and Ada, and see what you all make of it. We are sure to come at the heart of the matter by your means, little woman.”\n\nI really was frightened at the thought of the importance I was attaining and the number of things that were being confided to me. I had not meant this at all; I had meant that he should speak to Richard. But of course I said nothing in reply except that I would do my best, though I feared (I really felt it necessary to repeat this) that he thought me much more sagacious than I was. At which my guardian only laughed the pleasantest laugh I ever heard.\n\n“Come!” he said, rising and pushing back his chair. “I think we may have done with the growlery for one day! Only a concluding word. Esther, my dear, do you wish to ask me anything?”\n\nHe looked so attentively at me that I looked attentively at him and felt sure I understood him.\n\n“About myself, sir?” said I.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Guardian,” said I, venturing to put my hand, which was suddenly colder than I could have wished, in his, “nothing! I am quite sure that if there were anything I ought to know or had any need to know, I should not have to ask you to tell it to me. If my whole reliance and confidence were not placed in you, I must have a hard heart indeed. I have nothing to ask you, nothing in the world.”\n\nHe drew my hand through his arm and we went away to look for Ada. From that hour I felt quite easy with him, quite unreserved, quite content to know no more, quite happy.\n\nWe lived, at first, rather a busy life at Bleak House, for we had to become acquainted with many residents in and out of the neighbourhood who knew Mr. Jarndyce. It seemed to Ada and me that everybody knew him who wanted to do anything with anybody else’s money. It amazed us when we began to sort his letters and to answer some of them for him in the growlery of a morning to find how the great object of the lives of nearly all his correspondents appeared to be to form themselves into committees for getting in and laying out money. The ladies were as desperate as the gentlemen; indeed, I think they were even more so. They threw themselves into committees in the most impassioned manner and collected subscriptions with a vehemence quite extraordinary. It appeared to us that some of them must pass their whole lives in dealing out subscription-cards to the whole post-office directory—shilling cards, half-crown cards, half-sovereign cards, penny cards. They wanted everything. They wanted wearing apparel, they wanted linen rags, they wanted money, they wanted coals, they wanted soup, they wanted interest, they wanted autographs, they wanted flannel, they wanted whatever Mr. Jarndyce had—or had not. Their objects were as various as their demands. They were going to raise new buildings, they were going to pay off debts on old buildings, they were going to establish in a picturesque building (engraving of proposed west elevation attached) the Sisterhood of Mediaeval Marys, they were going to give a testimonial to Mrs. Jellyby, they were going to have their secretary’s portrait painted and presented to his mother-in-law, whose deep devotion to him was well known, they were going to get up everything, I really believe, from five hundred thousand tracts to an annuity and from a marble monument to a silver tea-pot. They took a multitude of titles. They were the Women of England, the Daughters of Britain, the Sisters of all the cardinal virtues separately, the Females of America, the Ladies of a hundred denominations. They appeared to be always excited about canvassing and electing. They seemed to our poor wits, and according to their own accounts, to be constantly polling people by tens of thousands, yet never bringing their candidates in for anything. It made our heads ache to think, on the whole, what feverish lives they must lead.\n\nAmong the ladies who were most distinguished for this rapacious benevolence (if I may use the expression) was a Mrs. Pardiggle, who seemed, as I judged from the number of her letters to Mr. Jarndyce, to be almost as powerful a correspondent as Mrs. Jellyby herself. We observed that the wind always changed when Mrs. Pardiggle became the subject of conversation and that it invariably interrupted Mr. Jarndyce and prevented his going any farther, when he had remarked that there were two classes of charitable people; one, the people who did a little and made a great deal of noise; the other, the people who did a great deal and made no noise at all. We were therefore curious to see Mrs. Pardiggle, suspecting her to be a type of the former class, and were glad when she called one day with her five young sons.\n\nShe was a formidable style of lady with spectacles, a prominent nose, and a loud voice, who had the effect of wanting a great deal of room. And she really did, for she knocked down little chairs with her skirts that were quite a great way off. As only Ada and I were at home, we received her timidly, for she seemed to come in like cold weather and to make the little Pardiggles blue as they followed.\n\n“These, young ladies,” said Mrs. Pardiggle with great volubility after the first salutations, “are my five boys. You may have seen their names in a printed subscription list (perhaps more than one) in the possession of our esteemed friend Mr. Jarndyce. Egbert, my eldest (twelve), is the boy who sent out his pocket-money, to the amount of five and threepence, to the Tockahoopo Indians. Oswald, my second (ten and a half), is the child who contributed two and nine-pence to the Great National Smithers Testimonial. Francis, my third (nine), one and sixpence halfpenny; Felix, my fourth (seven), eightpence to the Superannuated Widows; Alfred, my youngest (five), has voluntarily enrolled himself in the Infant Bonds of Joy, and is pledged never, through life, to use tobacco in any form.”\n\nWe had never seen such dissatisfied children. It was not merely that they were weazened and shrivelled—though they were certainly that too—but they looked absolutely ferocious with discontent. At the mention of the Tockahoopo Indians, I could really have supposed Egbert to be one of the most baleful members of that tribe, he gave me such a savage frown. The face of each child, as the amount of his contribution was mentioned, darkened in a peculiarly vindictive manner, but his was by far the worst. I must except, however, the little recruit into the Infant Bonds of Joy, who was stolidly and evenly miserable.\n\n“You have been visiting, I understand,” said Mrs. Pardiggle, “at Mrs. Jellyby’s?”\n\nWe said yes, we had passed one night there.\n\n“Mrs. Jellyby,” pursued the lady, always speaking in the same demonstrative, loud, hard tone, so that her voice impressed my fancy as if it had a sort of spectacles on too—and I may take the opportunity of remarking that her spectacles were made the less engaging by her eyes being what Ada called “choking eyes,” meaning very prominent—“Mrs. Jellyby is a benefactor to society and deserves a helping hand. My boys have contributed to the African project—Egbert, one and six, being the entire allowance of nine weeks; Oswald, one and a penny halfpenny, being the same; the rest, according to their little means. Nevertheless, I do not go with Mrs. Jellyby in all things. I do not go with Mrs. Jellyby in her treatment of her young family. It has been noticed. It has been observed that her young family are excluded from participation in the objects to which she is devoted. She may be right, she may be wrong; but, right or wrong, this is not my course with MY young family. I take them everywhere.”\n\nI was afterwards convinced (and so was Ada) that from the ill-conditioned eldest child, these words extorted a sharp yell. He turned it off into a yawn, but it began as a yell.\n\n“They attend matins with me (very prettily done) at half-past six o’clock in the morning all the year round, including of course the depth of winter,” said Mrs. Pardiggle rapidly, “and they are with me during the revolving duties of the day. I am a School lady, I am a Visiting lady, I am a Reading lady, I am a Distributing lady; I am on the local Linen Box Committee and many general committees; and my canvassing alone is very extensive—perhaps no one’s more so. But they are my companions everywhere; and by these means they acquire that knowledge of the poor, and that capacity of doing charitable business in general—in short, that taste for the sort of thing—which will render them in after life a service to their neighbours and a satisfaction to themselves. My young family are not frivolous; they expend the entire amount of their allowance in subscriptions, under my direction; and they have attended as many public meetings and listened to as many lectures, orations, and discussions as generally fall to the lot of few grown people. Alfred (five), who, as I mentioned, has of his own election joined the Infant Bonds of Joy, was one of the very few children who manifested consciousness on that occasion after a fervid address of two hours from the chairman of the evening.”\n\nAlfred glowered at us as if he never could, or would, forgive the injury of that night.\n\n“You may have observed, Miss Summerson,” said Mrs. Pardiggle, “in some of the lists to which I have referred, in the possession of our esteemed friend Mr. Jarndyce, that the names of my young family are concluded with the name of O. A. Pardiggle, F.R.S., one pound. That is their father. We usually observe the same routine. I put down my mite first; then my young family enrol their contributions, according to their ages and their little means; and then Mr. Pardiggle brings up the rear. Mr. Pardiggle is happy to throw in his limited donation, under my direction; and thus things are made not only pleasant to ourselves, but, we trust, improving to others.”\n\nSuppose Mr. Pardiggle were to dine with Mr. Jellyby, and suppose Mr. Jellyby were to relieve his mind after dinner to Mr. Pardiggle, would Mr. Pardiggle, in return, make any confidential communication to Mr. Jellyby? I was quite confused to find myself thinking this, but it came into my head.\n\n“You are very pleasantly situated here!” said Mrs. Pardiggle.\n\nWe were glad to change the subject, and going to the window, pointed out the beauties of the prospect, on which the spectacles appeared to me to rest with curious indifference.\n\n“You know Mr. Gusher?” said our visitor.\n\nWe were obliged to say that we had not the pleasure of Mr. Gusher’s acquaintance.\n\n“The loss is yours, I assure you,” said Mrs. Pardiggle with her commanding deportment. “He is a very fervid, impassioned speaker—full of fire! Stationed in a waggon on this lawn, now, which, from the shape of the land, is naturally adapted to a public meeting, he would improve almost any occasion you could mention for hours and hours! By this time, young ladies,” said Mrs. Pardiggle, moving back to her chair and overturning, as if by invisible agency, a little round table at a considerable distance with my work-basket on it, “by this time you have found me out, I dare say?”\n\nThis was really such a confusing question that Ada looked at me in perfect dismay. As to the guilty nature of my own consciousness after what I had been thinking, it must have been expressed in the colour of my cheeks.\n\n“Found out, I mean,” said Mrs. Pardiggle, “the prominent point in my character. I am aware that it is so prominent as to be discoverable immediately. I lay myself open to detection, I know. Well! I freely admit, I am a woman of business. I love hard work; I enjoy hard work. The excitement does me good. I am so accustomed and inured to hard work that I don’t know what fatigue is.”\n\nWe murmured that it was very astonishing and very gratifying, or something to that effect. I don’t think we knew what it was either, but this is what our politeness expressed.\n\n“I do not understand what it is to be tired; you cannot tire me if you try!” said Mrs. Pardiggle. “The quantity of exertion (which is no exertion to me), the amount of business (which I regard as nothing), that I go through sometimes astonishes myself. I have seen my young family, and Mr. Pardiggle, quite worn out with witnessing it, when I may truly say I have been as fresh as a lark!”\n\nIf that dark-visaged eldest boy could look more malicious than he had already looked, this was the time when he did it. I observed that he doubled his right fist and delivered a secret blow into the crown of his cap, which was under his left arm.\n\n“This gives me a great advantage when I am making my rounds,” said Mrs. Pardiggle. “If I find a person unwilling to hear what I have to say, I tell that person directly, ‘I am incapable of fatigue, my good friend, I am never tired, and I mean to go on until I have done.’ It answers admirably! Miss Summerson, I hope I shall have your assistance in my visiting rounds immediately, and Miss Clare’s very soon.”\n\nAt first I tried to excuse myself for the present on the general ground of having occupations to attend to which I must not neglect. But as this was an ineffectual protest, I then said, more particularly, that I was not sure of my qualifications. That I was inexperienced in the art of adapting my mind to minds very differently situated, and addressing them from suitable points of view. That I had not that delicate knowledge of the heart which must be essential to such a work. That I had much to learn, myself, before I could teach others, and that I could not confide in my good intentions alone. For these reasons I thought it best to be as useful as I could, and to render what kind services I could to those immediately about me, and to try to let that circle of duty gradually and naturally expand itself. All this I said with anything but confidence, because Mrs. Pardiggle was much older than I, and had great experience, and was so very military in her manners.\n\n“You are wrong, Miss Summerson,” said she, “but perhaps you are not equal to hard work or the excitement of it, and that makes a vast difference. If you would like to see how I go through my work, I am now about—with my young family—to visit a brickmaker in the neighbourhood (a very bad character) and shall be glad to take you with me. Miss Clare also, if she will do me the favour.”\n\nAda and I interchanged looks, and as we were going out in any case, accepted the offer. When we hastily returned from putting on our bonnets, we found the young family languishing in a corner and Mrs. Pardiggle sweeping about the room, knocking down nearly all the light objects it contained. Mrs. Pardiggle took possession of Ada, and I followed with the family.\n\nAda told me afterwards that Mrs. Pardiggle talked in the same loud tone (that, indeed, I overheard) all the way to the brickmaker’s about an exciting contest which she had for two or three years waged against another lady relative to the bringing in of their rival candidates for a pension somewhere. There had been a quantity of printing, and promising, and proxying, and polling, and it appeared to have imparted great liveliness to all concerned, except the pensioners—who were not elected yet.\n\nI am very fond of being confided in by children and am happy in being usually favoured in that respect, but on this occasion it gave me great uneasiness. As soon as we were out of doors, Egbert, with the manner of a little footpad, demanded a shilling of me on the ground that his pocket-money was “boned” from him. On my pointing out the great impropriety of the word, especially in connexion with his parent (for he added sulkily “By her!”), he pinched me and said, “Oh, then! Now! Who are you! YOU wouldn’t like it, I think? What does she make a sham for, and pretend to give me money, and take it away again? Why do you call it my allowance, and never let me spend it?” These exasperating questions so inflamed his mind and the minds of Oswald and Francis that they all pinched me at once, and in a dreadfully expert way—screwing up such little pieces of my arms that I could hardly forbear crying out. Felix, at the same time, stamped upon my toes. And the Bond of Joy, who on account of always having the whole of his little income anticipated stood in fact pledged to abstain from cakes as well as tobacco, so swelled with grief and rage when we passed a pastry-cook’s shop that he terrified me by becoming purple. I never underwent so much, both in body and mind, in the course of a walk with young people as from these unnaturally constrained children when they paid me the compliment of being natural.\n\nI was glad when we came to the brickmaker’s house, though it was one of a cluster of wretched hovels in a brick-field, with pigsties close to the broken windows and miserable little gardens before the doors growing nothing but stagnant pools. Here and there an old tub was put to catch the droppings of rain-water from a roof, or they were banked up with mud into a little pond like a large dirt-pie. At the doors and windows some men and women lounged or prowled about, and took little notice of us except to laugh to one another or to say something as we passed about gentlefolks minding their own business and not troubling their heads and muddying their shoes with coming to look after other people’s.\n\nMrs. Pardiggle, leading the way with a great show of moral determination and talking with much volubility about the untidy habits of the people (though I doubted if the best of us could have been tidy in such a place), conducted us into a cottage at the farthest corner, the ground-floor room of which we nearly filled. Besides ourselves, there were in this damp, offensive room a woman with a black eye, nursing a poor little gasping baby by the fire; a man, all stained with clay and mud and looking very dissipated, lying at full length on the ground, smoking a pipe; a powerful young man fastening a collar on a dog; and a bold girl doing some kind of washing in very dirty water. They all looked up at us as we came in, and the woman seemed to turn her face towards the fire as if to hide her bruised eye; nobody gave us any welcome.\n\n“Well, my friends,” said Mrs. Pardiggle, but her voice had not a friendly sound, I thought; it was much too business-like and systematic. “How do you do, all of you? I am here again. I told you, you couldn’t tire me, you know. I am fond of hard work, and am true to my word.”\n\n“There an’t,” growled the man on the floor, whose head rested on his hand as he stared at us, “any more on you to come in, is there?”\n\n“No, my friend,” said Mrs. Pardiggle, seating herself on one stool and knocking down another. “We are all here.”\n\n“Because I thought there warn’t enough of you, perhaps?” said the man, with his pipe between his lips as he looked round upon us.\n\nThe young man and the girl both laughed. Two friends of the young man, whom we had attracted to the doorway and who stood there with their hands in their pockets, echoed the laugh noisily.\n\n“You can’t tire me, good people,” said Mrs. Pardiggle to these latter. “I enjoy hard work, and the harder you make mine, the better I like it.”\n\n“Then make it easy for her!” growled the man upon the floor. “I wants it done, and over. I wants a end of these liberties took with my place. I wants an end of being drawed like a badger. Now you’re a-going to poll-pry and question according to custom—I know what you’re a-going to be up to. Well! You haven’t got no occasion to be up to it. I’ll save you the trouble. Is my daughter a-washin? Yes, she IS a-washin. Look at the water. Smell it! That’s wot we drinks. How do you like it, and what do you think of gin instead! An’t my place dirty? Yes, it is dirty—it’s nat’rally dirty, and it’s nat’rally onwholesome; and we’ve had five dirty and onwholesome children, as is all dead infants, and so much the better for them, and for us besides. Have I read the little book wot you left? No, I an’t read the little book wot you left. There an’t nobody here as knows how to read it; and if there wos, it wouldn’t be suitable to me. It’s a book fit for a babby, and I’m not a babby. If you was to leave me a doll, I shouldn’t nuss it. How have I been conducting of myself? Why, I’ve been drunk for three days; and I’da been drunk four if I’da had the money. Don’t I never mean for to go to church? No, I don’t never mean for to go to church. I shouldn’t be expected there, if I did; the beadle’s too gen-teel for me. And how did my wife get that black eye? Why, I give it her; and if she says I didn’t, she’s a Lie!”\n\nHe had pulled his pipe out of his mouth to say all this, and he now turned over on his other side and smoked again. Mrs. Pardiggle, who had been regarding him through her spectacles with a forcible composure, calculated, I could not help thinking, to increase his antagonism, pulled out a good book as if it were a constable’s staff and took the whole family into custody. I mean into religious custody, of course; but she really did it as if she were an inexorable moral policeman carrying them all off to a station-house.\n\nAda and I were very uncomfortable. We both felt intrusive and out of place, and we both thought that Mrs. Pardiggle would have got on infinitely better if she had not had such a mechanical way of taking possession of people. The children sulked and stared; the family took no notice of us whatever, except when the young man made the dog bark, which he usually did when Mrs. Pardiggle was most emphatic. We both felt painfully sensible that between us and these people there was an iron barrier which could not be removed by our new friend. By whom or how it could be removed, we did not know, but we knew that. Even what she read and said seemed to us to be ill-chosen for such auditors, if it had been imparted ever so modestly and with ever so much tact. As to the little book to which the man on the floor had referred, we acquired a knowledge of it afterwards, and Mr. Jarndyce said he doubted if Robinson Crusoe could have read it, though he had had no other on his desolate island.\n\nWe were much relieved, under these circumstances, when Mrs. Pardiggle left off.\n\nThe man on the floor, then turning his head round again, said morosely, “Well! You’ve done, have you?”\n\n“For to-day, I have, my friend. But I am never fatigued. I shall come to you again in your regular order,” returned Mrs. Pardiggle with demonstrative cheerfulness.\n\n“So long as you goes now,” said he, folding his arms and shutting his eyes with an oath, “you may do wot you like!”\n\nMrs. Pardiggle accordingly rose and made a little vortex in the confined room from which the pipe itself very narrowly escaped. Taking one of her young family in each hand, and telling the others to follow closely, and expressing her hope that the brickmaker and all his house would be improved when she saw them next, she then proceeded to another cottage. I hope it is not unkind in me to say that she certainly did make, in this as in everything else, a show that was not conciliatory of doing charity by wholesale and of dealing in it to a large extent.\n\nShe supposed that we were following her, but as soon as the space was left clear, we approached the woman sitting by the fire to ask if the baby were ill.\n\nShe only looked at it as it lay on her lap. We had observed before that when she looked at it she covered her discoloured eye with her hand, as though she wished to separate any association with noise and violence and ill treatment from the poor little child.\n\nAda, whose gentle heart was moved by its appearance, bent down to touch its little face. As she did so, I saw what happened and drew her back. The child died.\n\n“Oh, Esther!” cried Ada, sinking on her knees beside it. “Look here! Oh, Esther, my love, the little thing! The suffering, quiet, pretty little thing! I am so sorry for it. I am so sorry for the mother. I never saw a sight so pitiful as this before! Oh, baby, baby!”\n\nSuch compassion, such gentleness, as that with which she bent down weeping and put her hand upon the mother’s might have softened any mother’s heart that ever beat. The woman at first gazed at her in astonishment and then burst into tears.\n\nPresently I took the light burden from her lap, did what I could to make the baby’s rest the prettier and gentler, laid it on a shelf, and covered it with my own handkerchief. We tried to comfort the mother, and we whispered to her what Our Saviour said of children. She answered nothing, but sat weeping—weeping very much.\n\nWhen I turned, I found that the young man had taken out the dog and was standing at the door looking in upon us with dry eyes, but quiet. The girl was quiet too and sat in a corner looking on the ground. The man had risen. He still smoked his pipe with an air of defiance, but he was silent.\n\nAn ugly woman, very poorly clothed, hurried in while I was glancing at them, and coming straight up to the mother, said, “Jenny! Jenny!” The mother rose on being so addressed and fell upon the woman’s neck.\n\nShe also had upon her face and arms the marks of ill usage. She had no kind of grace about her, but the grace of sympathy; but when she condoled with the woman, and her own tears fell, she wanted no beauty. I say condoled, but her only words were “Jenny! Jenny!” All the rest was in the tone in which she said them.\n\nI thought it very touching to see these two women, coarse and shabby and beaten, so united; to see what they could be to one another; to see how they felt for one another, how the heart of each to each was softened by the hard trials of their lives. I think the best side of such people is almost hidden from us. What the poor are to the poor is little known, excepting to themselves and God.\n\nWe felt it better to withdraw and leave them uninterrupted. We stole out quietly and without notice from any one except the man. He was leaning against the wall near the door, and finding that there was scarcely room for us to pass, went out before us. He seemed to want to hide that he did this on our account, but we perceived that he did, and thanked him. He made no answer.\n\nAda was so full of grief all the way home, and Richard, whom we found at home, was so distressed to see her in tears (though he said to me, when she was not present, how beautiful it was too!), that we arranged to return at night with some little comforts and repeat our visit at the brick-maker’s house. We said as little as we could to Mr. Jarndyce, but the wind changed directly.\n\nRichard accompanied us at night to the scene of our morning expedition. On our way there, we had to pass a noisy drinking-house, where a number of men were flocking about the door. Among them, and prominent in some dispute, was the father of the little child. At a short distance, we passed the young man and the dog, in congenial company. The sister was standing laughing and talking with some other young women at the corner of the row of cottages, but she seemed ashamed and turned away as we went by.\n\nWe left our escort within sight of the brickmaker’s dwelling and proceeded by ourselves. When we came to the door, we found the woman who had brought such consolation with her standing there looking anxiously out.\n\n“It’s you, young ladies, is it?” she said in a whisper. “I’m a-watching for my master. My heart’s in my mouth. If he was to catch me away from home, he’d pretty near murder me.”\n\n“Do you mean your husband?” said I.\n\n“Yes, miss, my master. Jenny’s asleep, quite worn out. She’s scarcely had the child off her lap, poor thing, these seven days and nights, except when I’ve been able to take it for a minute or two.”\n\nAs she gave way for us, she went softly in and put what we had brought near the miserable bed on which the mother slept. No effort had been made to clean the room—it seemed in its nature almost hopeless of being clean; but the small waxen form from which so much solemnity diffused itself had been composed afresh, and washed, and neatly dressed in some fragments of white linen; and on my handkerchief, which still covered the poor baby, a little bunch of sweet herbs had been laid by the same rough, scarred hands, so lightly, so tenderly!\n\n“May heaven reward you!” we said to her. “You are a good woman.”\n\n“Me, young ladies?” she returned with surprise. “Hush! Jenny, Jenny!”\n\nThe mother had moaned in her sleep and moved. The sound of the familiar voice seemed to calm her again. She was quiet once more.\n\nHow little I thought, when I raised my handkerchief to look upon the tiny sleeper underneath and seemed to see a halo shine around the child through Ada’s drooping hair as her pity bent her head—how little I thought in whose unquiet bosom that handkerchief would come to lie after covering the motionless and peaceful breast! I only thought that perhaps the Angel of the child might not be all unconscious of the woman who replaced it with so compassionate a hand; not all unconscious of her presently, when we had taken leave, and left her at the door, by turns looking, and listening in terror for herself, and saying in her old soothing manner, “Jenny, Jenny!”\nSigns and Tokens\n\nI don’t know how it is I seem to be always writing about myself. I mean all the time to write about other people, and I try to think about myself as little as possible, and I am sure, when I find myself coming into the story again, I am really vexed and say, “Dear, dear, you tiresome little creature, I wish you wouldn’t!” but it is all of no use. I hope any one who may read what I write will understand that if these pages contain a great deal about me, I can only suppose it must be because I have really something to do with them and can’t be kept out.\n\nMy darling and I read together, and worked, and practised, and found so much employment for our time that the winter days flew by us like bright-winged birds. Generally in the afternoons, and always in the evenings, Richard gave us his company. Although he was one of the most restless creatures in the world, he certainly was very fond of our society.\n\nHe was very, very, very fond of Ada. I mean it, and I had better say it at once. I had never seen any young people falling in love before, but I found them out quite soon. I could not say so, of course, or show that I knew anything about it. On the contrary, I was so demure and used to seem so unconscious that sometimes I considered within myself while I was sitting at work whether I was not growing quite deceitful.\n\nBut there was no help for it. All I had to do was to be quiet, and I was as quiet as a mouse. They were as quiet as mice too, so far as any words were concerned, but the innocent manner in which they relied more and more upon me as they took more and more to one another was so charming that I had great difficulty in not showing how it interested me.\n\n“Our dear little old woman is such a capital old woman,” Richard would say, coming up to meet me in the garden early, with his pleasant laugh and perhaps the least tinge of a blush, “that I can’t get on without her. Before I begin my harum-scarum day—grinding away at those books and instruments and then galloping up hill and down dale, all the country round, like a highwayman—it does me so much good to come and have a steady walk with our comfortable friend, that here I am again!”\n\n“You know, Dame Durden, dear,” Ada would say at night, with her head upon my shoulder and the firelight shining in her thoughtful eyes, “I don’t want to talk when we come upstairs here. Only to sit a little while thinking, with your dear face for company, and to hear the wind and remember the poor sailors at sea—”\n\nAh! Perhaps Richard was going to be a sailor. We had talked it over very often now, and there was some talk of gratifying the inclination of his childhood for the sea. Mr. Jarndyce had written to a relation of the family, a great Sir Leicester Dedlock, for his interest in Richard’s favour, generally; and Sir Leicester had replied in a gracious manner that he would be happy to advance the prospects of the young gentleman if it should ever prove to be within his power, which was not at all probable, and that my Lady sent her compliments to the young gentleman (to whom she perfectly remembered that she was allied by remote consanguinity) and trusted that he would ever do his duty in any honourable profession to which he might devote himself.\n\n“So I apprehend it’s pretty clear,” said Richard to me, “that I shall have to work my own way. Never mind! Plenty of people have had to do that before now, and have done it. I only wish I had the command of a clipping privateer to begin with and could carry off the Chancellor and keep him on short allowance until he gave judgment in our cause. He’d find himself growing thin, if he didn’t look sharp!”\n\nWith a buoyancy and hopefulness and a gaiety that hardly ever flagged, Richard had a carelessness in his character that quite perplexed me, principally because he mistook it, in such a very odd way, for prudence. It entered into all his calculations about money in a singular manner which I don’t think I can better explain than by reverting for a moment to our loan to Mr. Skimpole.\n\nMr. Jarndyce had ascertained the amount, either from Mr. Skimpole himself or from Coavinses, and had placed the money in my hands with instructions to me to retain my own part of it and hand the rest to Richard. The number of little acts of thoughtless expenditure which Richard justified by the recovery of his ten pounds, and the number of times he talked to me as if he had saved or realized that amount, would form a sum in simple addition.\n\n“My prudent Mother Hubbard, why not?” he said to me when he wanted, without the least consideration, to bestow five pounds on the brickmaker. “I made ten pounds, clear, out of Coavinses’ business.”\n\n“How was that?” said I.\n\n“Why, I got rid of ten pounds which I was quite content to get rid of and never expected to see any more. You don’t deny that?”\n\n“No,” said I.\n\n“Very well! Then I came into possession of ten pounds—”\n\n“The same ten pounds,” I hinted.\n\n“That has nothing to do with it!” returned Richard. “I have got ten pounds more than I expected to have, and consequently I can afford to spend it without being particular.”\n\nIn exactly the same way, when he was persuaded out of the sacrifice of these five pounds by being convinced that it would do no good, he carried that sum to his credit and drew upon it.\n\n“Let me see!” he would say. “I saved five pounds out of the brickmaker’s affair, so if I have a good rattle to London and back in a post-chaise and put that down at four pounds, I shall have saved one. And it’s a very good thing to save one, let me tell you: a penny saved is a penny got!”\n\nI believe Richard’s was as frank and generous a nature as there possibly can be. He was ardent and brave, and in the midst of all his wild restlessness, was so gentle that I knew him like a brother in a few weeks. His gentleness was natural to him and would have shown itself abundantly even without Ada’s influence; but with it, he became one of the most winning of companions, always so ready to be interested and always so happy, sanguine, and light-hearted. I am sure that I, sitting with them, and walking with them, and talking with them, and noticing from day to day how they went on, falling deeper and deeper in love, and saying nothing about it, and each shyly thinking that this love was the greatest of secrets, perhaps not yet suspected even by the other—I am sure that I was scarcely less enchanted than they were and scarcely less pleased with the pretty dream.\n\nWe were going on in this way, when one morning at breakfast Mr. Jarndyce received a letter, and looking at the superscription, said, “From Boythorn? Aye, aye!” and opened and read it with evident pleasure, announcing to us in a parenthesis when he was about half-way through, that Boythorn was “coming down” on a visit. Now who was Boythorn, we all thought. And I dare say we all thought too—I am sure I did, for one—would Boythorn at all interfere with what was going forward?\n\n“I went to school with this fellow, Lawrence Boythorn,” said Mr. Jarndyce, tapping the letter as he laid it on the table, “more than five and forty years ago. He was then the most impetuous boy in the world, and he is now the most impetuous man. He was then the loudest boy in the world, and he is now the loudest man. He was then the heartiest and sturdiest boy in the world, and he is now the heartiest and sturdiest man. He is a tremendous fellow.”\n\n“In stature, sir?” asked Richard.\n\n“Pretty well, Rick, in that respect,” said Mr. Jarndyce; “being some ten years older than I and a couple of inches taller, with his head thrown back like an old soldier, his stalwart chest squared, his hands like a clean blacksmith’s, and his lungs! There’s no simile for his lungs. Talking, laughing, or snoring, they make the beams of the house shake.”\n\nAs Mr. Jarndyce sat enjoying the image of his friend Boythorn, we observed the favourable omen that there was not the least indication of any change in the wind.\n\n“But it’s the inside of the man, the warm heart of the man, the passion of the man, the fresh blood of the man, Rick—and Ada, and little Cobweb too, for you are all interested in a visitor—that I speak of,” he pursued. “His language is as sounding as his voice. He is always in extremes, perpetually in the superlative degree. In his condemnation he is all ferocity. You might suppose him to be an ogre from what he says, and I believe he has the reputation of one with some people. There! I tell you no more of him beforehand. You must not be surprised to see him take me under his protection, for he has never forgotten that I was a low boy at school and that our friendship began in his knocking two of my head tyrant’s teeth out (he says six) before breakfast. Boythorn and his man,” to me, “will be here this afternoon, my dear.”\n\nI took care that the necessary preparations were made for Mr. Boythorn’s reception, and we looked forward to his arrival with some curiosity. The afternoon wore away, however, and he did not appear. The dinner-hour arrived, and still he did not appear. The dinner was put back an hour, and we were sitting round the fire with no light but the blaze when the hall-door suddenly burst open and the hall resounded with these words, uttered with the greatest vehemence and in a stentorian tone: “We have been misdirected, Jarndyce, by a most abandoned ruffian, who told us to take the turning to the right instead of to the left. He is the most intolerable scoundrel on the face of the earth. His father must have been a most consummate villain, ever to have such a son. I would have had that fellow shot without the least remorse!”\n\n“Did he do it on purpose?” Mr. Jarndyce inquired.\n\n“I have not the slightest doubt that the scoundrel has passed his whole existence in misdirecting travellers!” returned the other. “By my soul, I thought him the worst-looking dog I had ever beheld when he was telling me to take the turning to the right. And yet I stood before that fellow face to face and didn’t knock his brains out!”\n\n“Teeth, you mean?” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Ha, ha, ha!” laughed Mr. Lawrence Boythorn, really making the whole house vibrate. “What, you have not forgotten it yet! Ha, ha, ha! And that was another most consummate vagabond! By my soul, the countenance of that fellow when he was a boy was the blackest image of perfidy, cowardice, and cruelty ever set up as a scarecrow in a field of scoundrels. If I were to meet that most unparalleled despot in the streets to-morrow, I would fell him like a rotten tree!”\n\n“I have no doubt of it,” said Mr. Jarndyce. “Now, will you come upstairs?”\n\n“By my soul, Jarndyce,” returned his guest, who seemed to refer to his watch, “if you had been married, I would have turned back at the garden-gate and gone away to the remotest summits of the Himalaya Mountains sooner than I would have presented myself at this unseasonable hour.”\n\n“Not quite so far, I hope?” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“By my life and honour, yes!” cried the visitor. “I wouldn’t be guilty of the audacious insolence of keeping a lady of the house waiting all this time for any earthly consideration. I would infinitely rather destroy myself—infinitely rather!”\n\nTalking thus, they went upstairs, and presently we heard him in his bedroom thundering “Ha, ha, ha!” and again “Ha, ha, ha!” until the flattest echo in the neighbourhood seemed to catch the contagion and to laugh as enjoyingly as he did or as we did when we heard him laugh.\n\nWe all conceived a prepossession in his favour, for there was a sterling quality in this laugh, and in his vigorous, healthy voice, and in the roundness and fullness with which he uttered every word he spoke, and in the very fury of his superlatives, which seemed to go off like blank cannons and hurt nothing. But we were hardly prepared to have it so confirmed by his appearance when Mr. Jarndyce presented him. He was not only a very handsome old gentleman—upright and stalwart as he had been described to us—with a massive grey head, a fine composure of face when silent, a figure that might have become corpulent but for his being so continually in earnest that he gave it no rest, and a chin that might have subsided into a double chin but for the vehement emphasis in which it was constantly required to assist; but he was such a true gentleman in his manner, so chivalrously polite, his face was lighted by a smile of so much sweetness and tenderness, and it seemed so plain that he had nothing to hide, but showed himself exactly as he was—incapable, as Richard said, of anything on a limited scale, and firing away with those blank great guns because he carried no small arms whatever—that really I could not help looking at him with equal pleasure as he sat at dinner, whether he smilingly conversed with Ada and me, or was led by Mr. Jarndyce into some great volley of superlatives, or threw up his head like a bloodhound and gave out that tremendous “Ha, ha, ha!”\n\n“You have brought your bird with you, I suppose?” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“By heaven, he is the most astonishing bird in Europe!” replied the other. “He IS the most wonderful creature! I wouldn’t take ten thousand guineas for that bird. I have left an annuity for his sole support in case he should outlive me. He is, in sense and attachment, a phenomenon. And his father before him was one of the most astonishing birds that ever lived!”\n\nThe subject of this laudation was a very little canary, who was so tame that he was brought down by Mr. Boythorn’s man, on his forefinger, and after taking a gentle flight round the room, alighted on his master’s head. To hear Mr. Boythorn presently expressing the most implacable and passionate sentiments, with this fragile mite of a creature quietly perched on his forehead, was to have a good illustration of his character, I thought.\n\n“By my soul, Jarndyce,” he said, very gently holding up a bit of bread to the canary to peck at, “if I were in your place I would seize every master in Chancery by the throat to-morrow morning and shake him until his money rolled out of his pockets and his bones rattled in his skin. I would have a settlement out of somebody, by fair means or by foul. If you would empower me to do it, I would do it for you with the greatest satisfaction!” (All this time the very small canary was eating out of his hand.)\n\n“I thank you, Lawrence, but the suit is hardly at such a point at present,” returned Mr. Jarndyce, laughing, “that it would be greatly advanced even by the legal process of shaking the bench and the whole bar.”\n\n“There never was such an infernal cauldron as that Chancery on the face of the earth!” said Mr. Boythorn. “Nothing but a mine below it on a busy day in term time, with all its records, rules, and precedents collected in it and every functionary belonging to it also, high and low, upward and downward, from its son the Accountant-General to its father the Devil, and the whole blown to atoms with ten thousand hundredweight of gunpowder, would reform it in the least!”\n\nIt was impossible not to laugh at the energetic gravity with which he recommended this strong measure of reform. When we laughed, he threw up his head and shook his broad chest, and again the whole country seemed to echo to his “Ha, ha, ha!” It had not the least effect in disturbing the bird, whose sense of security was complete and who hopped about the table with its quick head now on this side and now on that, turning its bright sudden eye on its master as if he were no more than another bird.\n\n“But how do you and your neighbour get on about the disputed right of way?” said Mr. Jarndyce. “You are not free from the toils of the law yourself!”\n\n“The fellow has brought actions against ME for trespass, and I have brought actions against HIM for trespass,” returned Mr. Boythorn. “By heaven, he is the proudest fellow breathing. It is morally impossible that his name can be Sir Leicester. It must be Sir Lucifer.”\n\n“Complimentary to our distant relation!” said my guardian laughingly to Ada and Richard.\n\n“I would beg Miss Clare’s pardon and Mr. Carstone’s pardon,” resumed our visitor, “if I were not reassured by seeing in the fair face of the lady and the smile of the gentleman that it is quite unnecessary and that they keep their distant relation at a comfortable distance.”\n\n“Or he keeps us,” suggested Richard.\n\n“By my soul,” exclaimed Mr. Boythorn, suddenly firing another volley, “that fellow is, and his father was, and his grandfather was, the most stiff-necked, arrogant imbecile, pig-headed numskull, ever, by some inexplicable mistake of Nature, born in any station of life but a walking-stick’s! The whole of that family are the most solemnly conceited and consummate blockheads! But it’s no matter; he should not shut up my path if he were fifty baronets melted into one and living in a hundred Chesney Wolds, one within another, like the ivory balls in a Chinese carving. The fellow, by his agent, or secretary, or somebody, writes to me ‘Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, presents his compliments to Mr. Lawrence Boythorn, and has to call his attention to the fact that the green pathway by the old parsonage-house, now the property of Mr. Lawrence Boythorn, is Sir Leicester’s right of way, being in fact a portion of the park of Chesney Wold, and that Sir Leicester finds it convenient to close up the same.’ I write to the fellow, ‘Mr. Lawrence Boythorn presents his compliments to Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and has to call HIS attention to the fact that he totally denies the whole of Sir Leicester Dedlock’s positions on every possible subject and has to add, in reference to closing up the pathway, that he will be glad to see the man who may undertake to do it.’ The fellow sends a most abandoned villain with one eye to construct a gateway. I play upon that execrable scoundrel with a fire-engine until the breath is nearly driven out of his body. The fellow erects a gate in the night. I chop it down and burn it in the morning. He sends his myrmidons to come over the fence and pass and repass. I catch them in humane man traps, fire split peas at their legs, play upon them with the engine—resolve to free mankind from the insupportable burden of the existence of those lurking ruffians. He brings actions for trespass; I bring actions for trespass. He brings actions for assault and battery; I defend them and continue to assault and batter. Ha, ha, ha!”\n\nTo hear him say all this with unimaginable energy, one might have thought him the angriest of mankind. To see him at the very same time, looking at the bird now perched upon his thumb and softly smoothing its feathers with his forefinger, one might have thought him the gentlest. To hear him laugh and see the broad good nature of his face then, one might have supposed that he had not a care in the world, or a dispute, or a dislike, but that his whole existence was a summer joke.\n\n“No, no,” he said, “no closing up of my paths by any Dedlock! Though I willingly confess,” here he softened in a moment, “that Lady Dedlock is the most accomplished lady in the world, to whom I would do any homage that a plain gentleman, and no baronet with a head seven hundred years thick, may. A man who joined his regiment at twenty and within a week challenged the most imperious and presumptuous coxcomb of a commanding officer that ever drew the breath of life through a tight waist—and got broke for it—is not the man to be walked over by all the Sir Lucifers, dead or alive, locked or unlocked. Ha, ha, ha!”\n\n“Nor the man to allow his junior to be walked over either?” said my guardian.\n\n“Most assuredly not!” said Mr. Boythorn, clapping him on the shoulder with an air of protection that had something serious in it, though he laughed. “He will stand by the low boy, always. Jarndyce, you may rely upon him! But speaking of this trespass—with apologies to Miss Clare and Miss Summerson for the length at which I have pursued so dry a subject—is there nothing for me from your men Kenge and Carboy?”\n\n“I think not, Esther?” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Nothing, guardian.”\n\n“Much obliged!” said Mr. Boythorn. “Had no need to ask, after even my slight experience of Miss Summerson’s forethought for every one about her.” (They all encouraged me; they were determined to do it.) “I inquired because, coming from Lincolnshire, I of course have not yet been in town, and I thought some letters might have been sent down here. I dare say they will report progress to-morrow morning.”\n\nI saw him so often in the course of the evening, which passed very pleasantly, contemplate Richard and Ada with an interest and a satisfaction that made his fine face remarkably agreeable as he sat at a little distance from the piano listening to the music—and he had small occasion to tell us that he was passionately fond of music, for his face showed it—that I asked my guardian as we sat at the backgammon board whether Mr. Boythorn had ever been married.\n\n“No,” said he. “No.”\n“But he meant to be!” said I.\n\n“How did you find out that?” he returned with a smile. “Why, guardian,” I explained, not without reddening a little at hazarding what was in my thoughts, “there is something so tender in his manner, after all, and he is so very courtly and gentle to us, and—”\n\nMr. Jarndyce directed his eyes to where he was sitting as I have just described him.\n\nI said no more.\n\n“You are right, little woman,” he answered. “He was all but married once. Long ago. And once.”\n\n“Did the lady die?”\n\n“No—but she died to him. That time has had its influence on all his later life. Would you suppose him to have a head and a heart full of romance yet?”\n\n“I think, guardian, I might have supposed so. But it is easy to say that when you have told me so.”\n\n“He has never since been what he might have been,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “and now you see him in his age with no one near him but his servant and his little yellow friend. It’s your throw, my dear!”\n\nI felt, from my guardian’s manner, that beyond this point I could not pursue the subject without changing the wind. I therefore forbore to ask any further questions. I was interested, but not curious. I thought a little while about this old love story in the night, when I was awakened by Mr. Boythorn’s lusty snoring; and I tried to do that very difficult thing, imagine old people young again and invested with the graces of youth. But I fell asleep before I had succeeded, and dreamed of the days when I lived in my godmother’s house. I am not sufficiently acquainted with such subjects to know whether it is at all remarkable that I almost always dreamed of that period of my life.\n\nWith the morning there came a letter from Messrs. Kenge and Carboy to Mr. Boythorn informing him that one of their clerks would wait upon him at noon. As it was the day of the week on which I paid the bills, and added up my books, and made all the household affairs as compact as possible, I remained at home while Mr. Jarndyce, Ada, and Richard took advantage of a very fine day to make a little excursion, Mr. Boythorn was to wait for Kenge and Carboy’s clerk and then was to go on foot to meet them on their return.\n\nWell! I was full of business, examining tradesmen’s books, adding up columns, paying money, filing receipts, and I dare say making a great bustle about it when Mr. Guppy was announced and shown in. I had had some idea that the clerk who was to be sent down might be the young gentleman who had met me at the coach-office, and I was glad to see him, because he was associated with my present happiness.\n\nI scarcely knew him again, he was so uncommonly smart. He had an entirely new suit of glossy clothes on, a shining hat, lilac-kid gloves, a neckerchief of a variety of colours, a large hot-house flower in his button-hole, and a thick gold ring on his little finger. Besides which, he quite scented the dining-room with bear’s-grease and other perfumery. He looked at me with an attention that quite confused me when I begged him to take a seat until the servant should return; and as he sat there crossing and uncrossing his legs in a corner, and I asked him if he had had a pleasant ride, and hoped that Mr. Kenge was well, I never looked at him, but I found him looking at me in the same scrutinizing and curious way.\n\nWhen the request was brought to him that he would go upstairs to Mr. Boythorn’s room, I mentioned that he would find lunch prepared for him when he came down, of which Mr. Jarndyce hoped he would partake. He said with some embarrassment, holding the handle of the door, “Shall I have the honour of finding you here, miss?” I replied yes, I should be there; and he went out with a bow and another look.\n\nI thought him only awkward and shy, for he was evidently much embarrassed; and I fancied that the best thing I could do would be to wait until I saw that he had everything he wanted and then to leave him to himself. The lunch was soon brought, but it remained for some time on the table. The interview with Mr. Boythorn was a long one, and a stormy one too, I should think, for although his room was at some distance I heard his loud voice rising every now and then like a high wind, and evidently blowing perfect broadsides of denunciation.\n\nAt last Mr. Guppy came back, looking something the worse for the conference. “My eye, miss,” he said in a low voice, “he’s a Tartar!”\n\n“Pray take some refreshment, sir,” said I.\n\nMr. Guppy sat down at the table and began nervously sharpening the carving-knife on the carving-fork, still looking at me (as I felt quite sure without looking at him) in the same unusual manner. The sharpening lasted so long that at last I felt a kind of obligation on me to raise my eyes in order that I might break the spell under which he seemed to labour, of not being able to leave off.\n\nHe immediately looked at the dish and began to carve.\n\n“What will you take yourself, miss? You’ll take a morsel of something?”\n\n“No, thank you,” said I.\n\n“Shan’t I give you a piece of anything at all, miss?” said Mr. Guppy, hurriedly drinking off a glass of wine.\n\n“Nothing, thank you,” said I. “I have only waited to see that you have everything you want. Is there anything I can order for you?”\n\n“No, I am much obliged to you, miss, I’m sure. I’ve everything that I can require to make me comfortable—at least I—not comfortable—I’m never that.” He drank off two more glasses of wine, one after another.\n\nI thought I had better go.\n\n“I beg your pardon, miss!” said Mr. Guppy, rising when he saw me rise. “But would you allow me the favour of a minute’s private conversation?”\n\nNot knowing what to say, I sat down again.\n\n“What follows is without prejudice, miss?” said Mr. Guppy, anxiously bringing a chair towards my table.\n\n“I don’t understand what you mean,” said I, wondering.\n\n“It’s one of our law terms, miss. You won’t make any use of it to my detriment at Kenge and Carboy’s or elsewhere. If our conversation shouldn’t lead to anything, I am to be as I was and am not to be prejudiced in my situation or worldly prospects. In short, it’s in total confidence.”\n\n“I am at a loss, sir,” said I, “to imagine what you can have to communicate in total confidence to me, whom you have never seen but once; but I should be very sorry to do you any injury.”\n\n“Thank you, miss. I’m sure of it—that’s quite sufficient.” All this time Mr. Guppy was either planing his forehead with his handkerchief or tightly rubbing the palm of his left hand with the palm of his right. “If you would excuse my taking another glass of wine, miss, I think it might assist me in getting on without a continual choke that cannot fail to be mutually unpleasant.”\n\nHe did so, and came back again. I took the opportunity of moving well behind my table.\n\n“You wouldn’t allow me to offer you one, would you miss?” said Mr. Guppy, apparently refreshed.\n\n“Not any,” said I.\n\n“Not half a glass?” said Mr. Guppy. “Quarter? No! Then, to proceed. My present salary, Miss Summerson, at Kenge and Carboy’s, is two pound a week. When I first had the happiness of looking upon you, it was one fifteen, and had stood at that figure for a lengthened period. A rise of five has since taken place, and a further rise of five is guaranteed at the expiration of a term not exceeding twelve months from the present date. My mother has a little property, which takes the form of a small life annuity, upon which she lives in an independent though unassuming manner in the Old Street Road. She is eminently calculated for a mother-in-law. She never interferes, is all for peace, and her disposition easy. She has her failings—as who has not?—but I never knew her do it when company was present, at which time you may freely trust her with wines, spirits, or malt liquors. My own abode is lodgings at Penton Place, Pentonville. It is lowly, but airy, open at the back, and considered one of the ’ealthiest outlets. Miss Summerson! In the mildest language, I adore you. Would you be so kind as to allow me (as I may say) to file a declaration—to make an offer!”\n\nMr. Guppy went down on his knees. I was well behind my table and not much frightened. I said, “Get up from that ridiculous position immediately, sir, or you will oblige me to break my implied promise and ring the bell!”\n\n“Hear me out, miss!” said Mr. Guppy, folding his hands.\n\n“I cannot consent to hear another word, sir,” I returned, “Unless you get up from the carpet directly and go and sit down at the table as you ought to do if you have any sense at all.”\n\nHe looked piteously, but slowly rose and did so.\n\n“Yet what a mockery it is, miss,” he said with his hand upon his heart and shaking his head at me in a melancholy manner over the tray, “to be stationed behind food at such a moment. The soul recoils from food at such a moment, miss.”\n\n“I beg you to conclude,” said I; “you have asked me to hear you out, and I beg you to conclude.”\n\n“I will, miss,” said Mr. Guppy. “As I love and honour, so likewise I obey. Would that I could make thee the subject of that vow before the shrine!”\n\n“That is quite impossible,” said I, “and entirely out of the question.”\n\n“I am aware,” said Mr. Guppy, leaning forward over the tray and regarding me, as I again strangely felt, though my eyes were not directed to him, with his late intent look, “I am aware that in a worldly point of view, according to all appearances, my offer is a poor one. But, Miss Summerson! Angel! No, don’t ring—I have been brought up in a sharp school and am accustomed to a variety of general practice. Though a young man, I have ferreted out evidence, got up cases, and seen lots of life. Blest with your hand, what means might I not find of advancing your interests and pushing your fortunes! What might I not get to know, nearly concerning you? I know nothing now, certainly; but what MIGHT I not if I had your confidence, and you set me on?”\n\nI told him that he addressed my interest or what he supposed to be my interest quite as unsuccessfully as he addressed my inclination, and he would now understand that I requested him, if he pleased, to go away immediately.\n\n“Cruel miss,” said Mr. Guppy, “hear but another word! I think you must have seen that I was struck with those charms on the day when I waited at the Whytorseller. I think you must have remarked that I could not forbear a tribute to those charms when I put up the steps of the ’ackney-coach. It was a feeble tribute to thee, but it was well meant. Thy image has ever since been fixed in my breast. I have walked up and down of an evening opposite Jellyby’s house only to look upon the bricks that once contained thee. This out of to-day, quite an unnecessary out so far as the attendance, which was its pretended object, went, was planned by me alone for thee alone. If I speak of interest, it is only to recommend myself and my respectful wretchedness. Love was before it, and is before it.”\n\n“I should be pained, Mr. Guppy,” said I, rising and putting my hand upon the bell-rope, “to do you or any one who was sincere the injustice of slighting any honest feeling, however disagreeably expressed. If you have really meant to give me a proof of your good opinion, though ill-timed and misplaced, I feel that I ought to thank you. I have very little reason to be proud, and I am not proud. I hope,” I think I added, without very well knowing what I said, “that you will now go away as if you had never been so exceedingly foolish and attend to Messrs. Kenge and Carboy’s business.”\n\n“Half a minute, miss!” cried Mr. Guppy, checking me as I was about to ring. “This has been without prejudice?”\n\n“I will never mention it,” said I, “unless you should give me future occasion to do so.”\n\n“A quarter of a minute, miss! In case you should think better at any time, however distant—THAT’S no consequence, for my feelings can never alter—of anything I have said, particularly what might I not do, Mr. William Guppy, eighty-seven, Penton Place, or if removed, or dead (of blighted hopes or anything of that sort), care of Mrs. Guppy, three hundred and two, Old Street Road, will be sufficient.”\n\nI rang the bell, the servant came, and Mr. Guppy, laying his written card upon the table and making a dejected bow, departed. Raising my eyes as he went out, I once more saw him looking at me after he had passed the door.\n\nI sat there for another hour or more, finishing my books and payments and getting through plenty of business. Then I arranged my desk, and put everything away, and was so composed and cheerful that I thought I had quite dismissed this unexpected incident. But, when I went upstairs to my own room, I surprised myself by beginning to laugh about it and then surprised myself still more by beginning to cry about it. In short, I was in a flutter for a little while and felt as if an old chord had been more coarsely touched than it ever had been since the days of the dear old doll, long buried in the garden.\n\nThe Law-Writer\nOn the eastern borders of Chancery Lane, that is to say, more particularly in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, Mr. Snagsby, law-stationer, pursues his lawful calling. In the shade of Cook’s Court, at most times a shady place, Mr. Snagsby has dealt in all sorts of blank forms of legal process; in skins and rolls of parchment; in paper—foolscap, brief, draft, brown, white, whitey-brown, and blotting; in stamps; in office-quills, pens, ink, India-rubber, pounce, pins, pencils, sealing-wax, and wafers; in red tape and green ferret; in pocket-books, almanacs, diaries, and law lists; in string boxes, rulers, inkstands—glass and leaden—pen-knives, scissors, bodkins, and other small office-cutlery; in short, in articles too numerous to mention, ever since he was out of his time and went into partnership with Peffer. On that occasion, Cook’s Court was in a manner revolutionized by the new inscription in fresh paint, PEFFER AND SNAGSBY, displacing the time-honoured and not easily to be deciphered legend PEFFER only. For smoke, which is the London ivy, had so wreathed itself round Peffer’s name and clung to his dwelling-place that the affectionate parasite quite overpowered the parent tree.\n\nPeffer is never seen in Cook’s Court now. He is not expected there, for he has been recumbent this quarter of a century in the churchyard of St. Andrews, Holborn, with the waggons and hackney-coaches roaring past him all the day and half the night like one great dragon. If he ever steal forth when the dragon is at rest to air himself again in Cook’s Court until admonished to return by the crowing of the sanguine cock in the cellar at the little dairy in Cursitor Street, whose ideas of daylight it would be curious to ascertain, since he knows from his personal observation next to nothing about it—if Peffer ever do revisit the pale glimpses of Cook’s Court, which no law-stationer in the trade can positively deny, he comes invisibly, and no one is the worse or wiser.\n\nIn his lifetime, and likewise in the period of Snagsby’s “time” of seven long years, there dwelt with Peffer in the same law-stationering premises a niece—a short, shrewd niece, something too violently compressed about the waist, and with a sharp nose like a sharp autumn evening, inclining to be frosty towards the end. The Cook’s Courtiers had a rumour flying among them that the mother of this niece did, in her daughter’s childhood, moved by too jealous a solicitude that her figure should approach perfection, lace her up every morning with her maternal foot against the bed-post for a stronger hold and purchase; and further, that she exhibited internally pints of vinegar and lemon-juice, which acids, they held, had mounted to the nose and temper of the patient. With whichsoever of the many tongues of Rumour this frothy report originated, it either never reached or never influenced the ears of young Snagsby, who, having wooed and won its fair subject on his arrival at man’s estate, entered into two partnerships at once. So now, in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, Mr. Snagsby and the niece are one; and the niece still cherishes her figure, which, however tastes may differ, is unquestionably so far precious that there is mighty little of it.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Snagsby are not only one bone and one flesh, but, to the neighbours’ thinking, one voice too. That voice, appearing to proceed from Mrs. Snagsby alone, is heard in Cook’s Court very often. Mr. Snagsby, otherwise than as he finds expression through these dulcet tones, is rarely heard. He is a mild, bald, timid man with a shining head and a scrubby clump of black hair sticking out at the back. He tends to meekness and obesity. As he stands at his door in Cook’s Court in his grey shop-coat and black calico sleeves, looking up at the clouds, or stands behind a desk in his dark shop with a heavy flat ruler, snipping and slicing at sheepskin in company with his two ’prentices, he is emphatically a retiring and unassuming man. From beneath his feet, at such times, as from a shrill ghost unquiet in its grave, there frequently arise complainings and lamentations in the voice already mentioned; and haply, on some occasions when these reach a sharper pitch than usual, Mr. Snagsby mentions to the ’prentices, “I think my little woman is a-giving it to Guster!”\n\nThis proper name, so used by Mr. Snagsby, has before now sharpened the wit of the Cook’s Courtiers to remark that it ought to be the name of Mrs. Snagsby, seeing that she might with great force and expression be termed a Guster, in compliment to her stormy character. It is, however, the possession, and the only possession except fifty shillings per annum and a very small box indifferently filled with clothing, of a lean young woman from a workhouse (by some supposed to have been christened Augusta) who, although she was farmed or contracted for during her growing time by an amiable benefactor of his species resident at Tooting, and cannot fail to have been developed under the most favourable circumstances, “has fits,” which the parish can’t account for.\n\nGuster, really aged three or four and twenty, but looking a round ten years older, goes cheap with this unaccountable drawback of fits, and is so apprehensive of being returned on the hands of her patron saint that except when she is found with her head in the pail, or the sink, or the copper, or the dinner, or anything else that happens to be near her at the time of her seizure, she is always at work. She is a satisfaction to the parents and guardians of the ’prentices, who feel that there is little danger of her inspiring tender emotions in the breast of youth; she is a satisfaction to Mrs. Snagsby, who can always find fault with her; she is a satisfaction to Mr. Snagsby, who thinks it a charity to keep her. The law-stationer’s establishment is, in Guster’s eyes, a temple of plenty and splendour. She believes the little drawing-room upstairs, always kept, as one may say, with its hair in papers and its pinafore on, to be the most elegant apartment in Christendom. The view it commands of Cook’s Court at one end (not to mention a squint into Cursitor Street) and of Coavinses’ the sheriff’s officer’s backyard at the other she regards as a prospect of unequalled beauty. The portraits it displays in oil—and plenty of it too—of Mr. Snagsby looking at Mrs. Snagsby and of Mrs. Snagsby looking at Mr. Snagsby are in her eyes as achievements of Raphael or Titian. Guster has some recompenses for her many privations.\n\nMr. Snagsby refers everything not in the practical mysteries of the business to Mrs. Snagsby. She manages the money, reproaches the tax-gatherers, appoints the times and places of devotion on Sundays, licenses Mr. Snagsby’s entertainments, and acknowledges no responsibility as to what she thinks fit to provide for dinner, insomuch that she is the high standard of comparison among the neighbouring wives a long way down Chancery Lane on both sides, and even out in Holborn, who in any domestic passages of arms habitually call upon their husbands to look at the difference between their (the wives’) position and Mrs. Snagsby’s, and their (the husbands’) behaviour and Mr. Snagsby’s. Rumour, always flying bat-like about Cook’s Court and skimming in and out at everybody’s windows, does say that Mrs. Snagsby is jealous and inquisitive and that Mr. Snagsby is sometimes worried out of house and home, and that if he had the spirit of a mouse he wouldn’t stand it. It is even observed that the wives who quote him to their self-willed husbands as a shining example in reality look down upon him and that nobody does so with greater superciliousness than one particular lady whose lord is more than suspected of laying his umbrella on her as an instrument of correction. But these vague whisperings may arise from Mr. Snagsby’s being in his way rather a meditative and poetical man, loving to walk in Staple Inn in the summer-time and to observe how countrified the sparrows and the leaves are, also to lounge about the Rolls Yard of a Sunday afternoon and to remark (if in good spirits) that there were old times once and that you’d find a stone coffin or two now under that chapel, he’ll be bound, if you was to dig for it. He solaces his imagination, too, by thinking of the many Chancellors and Vices, and Masters of the Rolls who are deceased; and he gets such a flavour of the country out of telling the two ’prentices how he HAS heard say that a brook “as clear as crystal” once ran right down the middle of Holborn, when Turnstile really was a turnstile, leading slap away into the meadows—gets such a flavour of the country out of this that he never wants to go there.\n\nThe day is closing in and the gas is lighted, but is not yet fully effective, for it is not quite dark. Mr. Snagsby standing at his shop-door looking up at the clouds sees a crow who is out late skim westward over the slice of sky belonging to Cook’s Court. The crow flies straight across Chancery Lane and Lincoln’s Inn Garden into Lincoln’s Inn Fields.\n\nHere, in a large house, formerly a house of state, lives Mr. Tulkinghorn. It is let off in sets of chambers now, and in those shrunken fragments of its greatness, lawyers lie like maggots in nuts. But its roomy staircases, passages, and antechambers still remain; and even its painted ceilings, where Allegory, in Roman helmet and celestial linen, sprawls among balustrades and pillars, flowers, clouds, and big-legged boys, and makes the head ache—as would seem to be Allegory’s object always, more or less. Here, among his many boxes labelled with transcendent names, lives Mr. Tulkinghorn, when not speechlessly at home in country-houses where the great ones of the earth are bored to death. Here he is to-day, quiet at his table. An oyster of the old school whom nobody can open.\n\nLike as he is to look at, so is his apartment in the dusk of the present afternoon. Rusty, out of date, withdrawing from attention, able to afford it. Heavy, broad-backed, old-fashioned, mahogany-and-horsehair chairs, not easily lifted; obsolete tables with spindle-legs and dusty baize covers; presentation prints of the holders of great titles in the last generation or the last but one, environ him. A thick and dingy Turkey-carpet muffles the floor where he sits, attended by two candles in old-fashioned silver candlesticks that give a very insufficient light to his large room. The titles on the backs of his books have retired into the binding; everything that can have a lock has got one; no key is visible. Very few loose papers are about. He has some manuscript near him, but is not referring to it. With the round top of an inkstand and two broken bits of sealing-wax he is silently and slowly working out whatever train of indecision is in his mind. Now the inkstand top is in the middle, now the red bit of sealing-wax, now the black bit. That’s not it. Mr. Tulkinghorn must gather them all up and begin again.\n\nHere, beneath the painted ceiling, with foreshortened Allegory staring down at his intrusion as if it meant to swoop upon him, and he cutting it dead, Mr. Tulkinghorn has at once his house and office. He keeps no staff, only one middle-aged man, usually a little out at elbows, who sits in a high pew in the hall and is rarely overburdened with business. Mr. Tulkinghorn is not in a common way. He wants no clerks. He is a great reservoir of confidences, not to be so tapped. His clients want HIM; he is all in all. Drafts that he requires to be drawn are drawn by special-pleaders in the temple on mysterious instructions; fair copies that he requires to be made are made at the stationers’, expense being no consideration. The middle-aged man in the pew knows scarcely more of the affairs of the peerage than any crossing-sweeper in Holborn.\n\nThe red bit, the black bit, the inkstand top, the other inkstand top, the little sand-box. So! You to the middle, you to the right, you to the left. This train of indecision must surely be worked out now or never. Now! Mr. Tulkinghorn gets up, adjusts his spectacles, puts on his hat, puts the manuscript in his pocket, goes out, tells the middle-aged man out at elbows, “I shall be back presently.” Very rarely tells him anything more explicit.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn goes, as the crow came—not quite so straight, but nearly—to Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street. To Snagsby’s, Law-Stationer’s, Deeds engrossed and copied, Law-Writing executed in all its branches, &c., &c., &c.\n\nIt is somewhere about five or six o’clock in the afternoon, and a balmy fragrance of warm tea hovers in Cook’s Court. It hovers about Snagsby’s door. The hours are early there: dinner at half-past one and supper at half-past nine. Mr. Snagsby was about to descend into the subterranean regions to take tea when he looked out of his door just now and saw the crow who was out late.\n\n“Master at home?”\n\nGuster is minding the shop, for the ’prentices take tea in the kitchen with Mr. and Mrs. Snagsby; consequently, the robe-maker’s two daughters, combing their curls at the two glasses in the two second-floor windows of the opposite house, are not driving the two ’prentices to distraction as they fondly suppose, but are merely awakening the unprofitable admiration of Guster, whose hair won’t grow, and never would, and it is confidently thought, never will.\n\n“Master at home?” says Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\nMaster is at home, and Guster will fetch him. Guster disappears, glad to get out of the shop, which she regards with mingled dread and veneration as a storehouse of awful implements of the great torture of the law—a place not to be entered after the gas is turned off.\n\nMr. Snagsby appears, greasy, warm, herbaceous, and chewing. Bolts a bit of bread and butter. Says, “Bless my soul, sir! Mr. Tulkinghorn!”\n\n“I want half a word with you, Snagsby.”\n\n“Certainly, sir! Dear me, sir, why didn’t you send your young man round for me? Pray walk into the back shop, sir.” Snagsby has brightened in a moment.\n\nThe confined room, strong of parchment-grease, is warehouse, counting-house, and copying-office. Mr. Tulkinghorn sits, facing round, on a stool at the desk.\n\n“Jarndyce and Jarndyce, Snagsby.”\n\n“Yes, sir.” Mr. Snagsby turns up the gas and coughs behind his hand, modestly anticipating profit. Mr. Snagsby, as a timid man, is accustomed to cough with a variety of expressions, and so to save words.\n\n“You copied some affidavits in that cause for me lately.”\n\n“Yes, sir, we did.”\n\n“There was one of them,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, carelessly feeling—tight, unopenable oyster of the old school!—in the wrong coat-pocket, “the handwriting of which is peculiar, and I rather like. As I happened to be passing, and thought I had it about me, I looked in to ask you—but I haven’t got it. No matter, any other time will do. Ah! here it is! I looked in to ask you who copied this.”\n\n“Who copied this, sir?” says Mr. Snagsby, taking it, laying it flat on the desk, and separating all the sheets at once with a twirl and a twist of the left hand peculiar to lawstationers. “We gave this out, sir. We were giving out rather a large quantity of work just at that time. I can tell you in a moment who copied it, sir, by referring to my book.”\n\nMr. Snagsby takes his book down from the safe, makes another bolt of the bit of bread and butter which seemed to have stopped short, eyes the affidavit aside, and brings his right forefinger travelling down a page of the book, “Jewby—Packer—Jarndyce.”\n\n“Jarndyce! Here we are, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby. “To be sure! I might have remembered it. This was given out, sir, to a writer who lodges just over on the opposite side of the lane.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn has seen the entry, found it before the law-stationer, read it while the forefinger was coming down the hill.\n\n“WHAT do you call him? Nemo?” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “Nemo, sir. Here it is. Forty-two folio. Given out on the Wednesday night at eight o’clock, brought in on the Thursday morning at half after nine.”\n\n“Nemo!” repeats Mr. Tulkinghorn. “Nemo is Latin for no one.”\n\n“It must be English for some one, sir, I think,” Mr. Snagsby submits with his deferential cough. “It is a person’s name. Here it is, you see, sir! Forty-two folio. Given out Wednesday night, eight o’clock; brought in Thursday morning, half after nine.”\n\nThe tail of Mr. Snagsby’s eye becomes conscious of the head of Mrs. Snagsby looking in at the shop-door to know what he means by deserting his tea. Mr. Snagsby addresses an explanatory cough to Mrs. Snagsby, as who should say, “My dear, a customer!”\n\n“Half after nine, sir,” repeats Mr. Snagsby. “Our law-writers, who live by job-work, are a queer lot; and this may not be his name, but it’s the name he goes by. I remember now, sir, that he gives it in a written advertisement he sticks up down at the Rule Office, and the King’s Bench Office, and the Judges’ Chambers, and so forth. You know the kind of document, sir—wanting employ?”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn glances through the little window at the back of Coavinses’, the sheriff’s officer’s, where lights shine in Coavinses’ windows. Coavinses’ coffee-room is at the back, and the shadows of several gentlemen under a cloud loom cloudily upon the blinds. Mr. Snagsby takes the opportunity of slightly turning his head to glance over his shoulder at his little woman and to make apologetic motions with his mouth to this effect: “Tul-king-horn—rich—in-flu-en-tial!”\n\n“Have you given this man work before?” asks Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\n“Oh, dear, yes, sir! Work of yours.”\n\n“Thinking of more important matters, I forget where you said he lived?”\n\n“Across the lane, sir. In fact, he lodges at a—” Mr. Snagsby makes another bolt, as if the bit of bread and butter were insurmountable “—at a rag and bottle shop.”\n\n“Can you show me the place as I go back?”\n\n“With the greatest pleasure, sir!”\n\nMr. Snagsby pulls off his sleeves and his grey coat, pulls on his black coat, takes his hat from its peg. “Oh! Here is my little woman!” he says aloud. “My dear, will you be so kind as to tell one of the lads to look after the shop while I step across the lane with Mr. Tulkinghorn? Mrs. Snagsby, sir—I shan’t be two minutes, my love!”\n\nMrs. Snagsby bends to the lawyer, retires behind the counter, peeps at them through the window-blind, goes softly into the back office, refers to the entries in the book still lying open. Is evidently curious.\n\n“You will find that the place is rough, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby, walking deferentially in the road and leaving the narrow pavement to the lawyer; “and the party is very rough. But they’re a wild lot in general, sir. The advantage of this particular man is that he never wants sleep. He’ll go at it right on end if you want him to, as long as ever you like.”\n\nIt is quite dark now, and the gas-lamps have acquired their full effect. Jostling against clerks going to post the day’s letters, and against counsel and attorneys going home to dinner, and against plaintiffs and defendants and suitors of all sorts, and against the general crowd, in whose way the forensic wisdom of ages has interposed a million of obstacles to the transaction of the commonest business of life; diving through law and equity, and through that kindred mystery, the street mud, which is made of nobody knows what and collects about us nobody knows whence or how—we only knowing in general that when there is too much of it we find it necessary to shovel it away—the lawyer and the law-stationer come to a rag and bottle shop and general emporium of much disregarded merchandise, lying and being in the shadow of the wall of Lincoln’s Inn, and kept, as is announced in paint, to all whom it may concern, by one Krook.\n\n“This is where he lives, sir,” says the law-stationer.\n\n“This is where he lives, is it?” says the lawyer unconcernedly. “Thank you.”\n\n“Are you not going in, sir?”\n\n“No, thank you, no; I am going on to the Fields at present. Good evening. Thank you!” Mr. Snagsby lifts his hat and returns to his little woman and his tea.\n\nBut Mr. Tulkinghorn does not go on to the Fields at present. He goes a short way, turns back, comes again to the shop of Mr. Krook, and enters it straight. It is dim enough, with a blot-headed candle or so in the windows, and an old man and a cat sitting in the back part by a fire. The old man rises and comes forward, with another blot-headed candle in his hand.\n\n“Pray is your lodger within?”\n\n“Male or female, sir?” says Mr. Krook.\n\n“Male. The person who does copying.”\n\nMr. Krook has eyed his man narrowly. Knows him by sight. Has an indistinct impression of his aristocratic repute.\n\n“Did you wish to see him, sir?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It’s what I seldom do myself,” says Mr. Krook with a grin. “Shall I call him down? But it’s a weak chance if he’d come, sir!”\n\n“I’ll go up to him, then,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\n“Second floor, sir. Take the candle. Up there!” Mr. Krook, with his cat beside him, stands at the bottom of the staircase, looking after Mr. Tulkinghorn. “Hi-hi!” he says when Mr. Tulkinghorn has nearly disappeared. The lawyer looks down over the hand-rail. The cat expands her wicked mouth and snarls at him.\n\n“Order, Lady Jane! Behave yourself to visitors, my lady! You know what they say of my lodger?” whispers Krook, going up a step or two.\n\n“What do they say of him?”\n\n“They say he has sold himself to the enemy, but you and I know better—he don’t buy. I’ll tell you what, though; my lodger is so black-humoured and gloomy that I believe he’d as soon make that bargain as any other. Don’t put him out, sir. That’s my advice!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn with a nod goes on his way. He comes to the dark door on the second floor. He knocks, receives no answer, opens it, and accidentally extinguishes his candle in doing so.\n\nThe air of the room is almost bad enough to have extinguished it if he had not. It is a small room, nearly black with soot, and grease, and dirt. In the rusty skeleton of a grate, pinched at the middle as if poverty had gripped it, a red coke fire burns low. In the corner by the chimney stand a deal table and a broken desk, a wilderness marked with a rain of ink. In another corner a ragged old portmanteau on one of the two chairs serves for cabinet or wardrobe; no larger one is needed, for it collapses like the cheeks of a starved man. The floor is bare, except that one old mat, trodden to shreds of rope-yarn, lies perishing upon the hearth. No curtain veils the darkness of the night, but the discoloured shutters are drawn together, and through the two gaunt holes pierced in them, famine might be staring in—the banshee of the man upon the bed.\n\nFor, on a low bed opposite the fire, a confusion of dirty patchwork, lean-ribbed ticking, and coarse sacking, the lawyer, hesitating just within the doorway, sees a man. He lies there, dressed in shirt and trousers, with bare feet. He has a yellow look in the spectral darkness of a candle that has guttered down until the whole length of its wick (still burning) has doubled over and left a tower of winding-sheet above it. His hair is ragged, mingling with his whiskers and his beard—the latter, ragged too, and grown, like the scum and mist around him, in neglect. Foul and filthy as the room is, foul and filthy as the air is, it is not easy to perceive what fumes those are which most oppress the senses in it; but through the general sickliness and faintness, and the odour of stale tobacco, there comes into the lawyer’s mouth the bitter, vapid taste of opium.\n\n“Hallo, my friend!” he cries, and strikes his iron candlestick against the door.\n\nHe thinks he has awakened his friend. He lies a little turned away, but his eyes are surely open.\n\n“Hallo, my friend!” he cries again. “Hallo! Hallo!”\n\nAs he rattles on the door, the candle which has drooped so long goes out and leaves him in the dark, with the gaunt eyes in the shutters staring down upon the bed.\nOur Dear Brother\n\nA touch on the lawyer’s wrinkled hand as he stands in the dark room, irresolute, makes him start and say, “What’s that?”\n\n“It’s me,” returns the old man of the house, whose breath is in his ear. “Can’t you wake him?”\n“No.”\n\n“What have you done with your candle?”\n\n“It’s gone out. Here it is.”\n\nKrook takes it, goes to the fire, stoops over the red embers, and tries to get a light. The dying ashes have no light to spare, and his endeavours are vain. Muttering, after an ineffectual call to his lodger, that he will go downstairs and bring a lighted candle from the shop, the old man departs. Mr. Tulkinghorn, for some new reason that he has, does not await his return in the room, but on the stairs outside.\n\nThe welcome light soon shines upon the wall, as Krook comes slowly up with his green-eyed cat following at his heels. “Does the man generally sleep like this?” inquired the lawyer in a low voice. “Hi! I don’t know,” says Krook, shaking his head and lifting his eyebrows. “I know next to nothing of his habits except that he keeps himself very close.”\n\nThus whispering, they both go in together. As the light goes in, the great eyes in the shutters, darkening, seem to close. Not so the eyes upon the bed.\n\n“God save us!” exclaims Mr. Tulkinghorn. “He is dead!” Krook drops the heavy hand he has taken up so suddenly that the arm swings over the bedside.\n\nThey look at one another for a moment.\n\n“Send for some doctor! Call for Miss Flite up the stairs, sir. Here’s poison by the bed! Call out for Flite, will you?” says Krook, with his lean hands spread out above the body like a vampire’s wings.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn hurries to the landing and calls, “Miss Flite! Flite! Make haste, here, whoever you are! Flite!” Krook follows him with his eyes, and while he is calling, finds opportunity to steal to the old portmanteau and steal back again.\n\n“Run, Flite, run! The nearest doctor! Run!” So Mr. Krook addresses a crazy little woman who is his female lodger, who appears and vanishes in a breath, who soon returns accompanied by a testy medical man brought from his dinner, with a broad, snuffy upper lip and a broad Scotch tongue.\n\n“Ey! Bless the hearts o’ ye,” says the medical man, looking up at them after a moment’s examination. “He’s just as dead as Phairy!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn (standing by the old portmanteau) inquires if he has been dead any time.\n\n“Any time, sir?” says the medical gentleman. “It’s probable he wull have been dead aboot three hours.”\n\n“About that time, I should say,” observes a dark young man on the other side of the bed.\n\n“Air you in the maydickle prayfession yourself, sir?” inquires the first.\n\nThe dark young man says yes.\n\n“Then I’ll just tak’ my depairture,” replies the other, “for I’m nae gude here!” With which remark he finishes his brief attendance and returns to finish his dinner.\n\nThe dark young surgeon passes the candle across and across the face and carefully examines the law-writer, who has established his pretensions to his name by becoming indeed No one.\n\n“I knew this person by sight very well,” says he. “He has purchased opium of me for the last year and a half. Was anybody present related to him?” glancing round upon the three bystanders.\n\n“I was his landlord,” grimly answers Krook, taking the candle from the surgeon’s outstretched hand. “He told me once I was the nearest relation he had.”\n\n“He has died,” says the surgeon, “of an over-dose of opium, there is no doubt. The room is strongly flavoured with it. There is enough here now,” taking an old tea-pot from Mr. Krook, “to kill a dozen people.”\n\n“Do you think he did it on purpose?” asks Krook.\n\n“Took the over-dose?”\n\n“Yes!” Krook almost smacks his lips with the unction of a horrible interest.\n\n“I can’t say. I should think it unlikely, as he has been in the habit of taking so much. But nobody can tell. He was very poor, I suppose?”\n\n“I suppose he was. His room—don’t look rich,” says Krook, who might have changed eyes with his cat, as he casts his sharp glance around. “But I have never been in it since he had it, and he was too close to name his circumstances to me.”\n\n“Did he owe you any rent?”\n\n“Six weeks.”\n\n“He will never pay it!” says the young man, resuming his examination. “It is beyond a doubt that he is indeed as dead as Pharaoh; and to judge from his appearance and condition, I should think it a happy release. Yet he must have been a good figure when a youth, and I dare say, good-looking.” He says this, not unfeelingly, while sitting on the bedstead’s edge with his face towards that other face and his hand upon the region of the heart. “I recollect once thinking there was something in his manner, uncouth as it was, that denoted a fall in life. Was that so?” he continues, looking round.\n\nKrook replies, “You might as well ask me to describe the ladies whose heads of hair I have got in sacks downstairs. Than that he was my lodger for a year and a half and lived—or didn’t live—by law-writing, I know no more of him.”\n\nDuring this dialogue Mr. Tulkinghorn has stood aloof by the old portmanteau, with his hands behind him, equally removed, to all appearance, from all three kinds of interest exhibited near the bed—from the young surgeon’s professional interest in death, noticeable as being quite apart from his remarks on the deceased as an individual; from the old man’s unction; and the little crazy woman’s awe. His imperturbable face has been as inexpressive as his rusty clothes. One could not even say he has been thinking all this while. He has shown neither patience nor impatience, nor attention nor abstraction. He has shown nothing but his shell. As easily might the tone of a delicate musical instrument be inferred from its case, as the tone of Mr. Tulkinghorn from his case.\n\nHe now interposes, addressing the young surgeon in his unmoved, professional way.\n\n“I looked in here,” he observes, “just before you, with the intention of giving this deceased man, whom I never saw alive, some employment at his trade of copying. I had heard of him from my stationer—Snagsby of Cook’s Court. Since no one here knows anything about him, it might be as well to send for Snagsby. Ah!” to the little crazy woman, who has often seen him in court, and whom he has often seen, and who proposes, in frightened dumb-show, to go for the law-stationer. “Suppose you do!”\n\nWhile she is gone, the surgeon abandons his hopeless investigation and covers its subject with the patchwork counterpane. Mr. Krook and he interchange a word or two. Mr. Tulkinghorn says nothing, but stands, ever, near the old portmanteau.\n\nMr. Snagsby arrives hastily in his grey coat and his black sleeves. “Dear me, dear me,” he says; “and it has come to this, has it! Bless my soul!”\n\n“Can you give the person of the house any information about this unfortunate creature, Snagsby?” inquires Mr. Tulkinghorn. “He was in arrears with his rent, it seems. And he must be buried, you know.”\n\n“Well, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby, coughing his apologetic cough behind his hand, “I really don’t know what advice I could offer, except sending for the beadle.”\n\n“I don’t speak of advice,” returns Mr. Tulkinghorn. “I could advise—”\n\n“No one better, sir, I am sure,” says Mr. Snagsby, with his deferential cough.\n\n“I speak of affording some clue to his connexions, or to where he came from, or to anything concerning him.”\n\n“I assure you, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby after prefacing his reply with his cough of general propitiation, “that I no more know where he came from than I know—”\n\n“Where he has gone to, perhaps,” suggests the surgeon to help him out.\n\nA pause. Mr. Tulkinghorn looking at the law-stationer. Mr. Krook, with his mouth open, looking for somebody to speak next.\n\n“As to his connexions, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby, “if a person was to say to me, ‘Snagsby, here’s twenty thousand pound down, ready for you in the Bank of England if you’ll only name one of ’em,’ I couldn’t do it, sir! About a year and a half ago—to the best of my belief, at the time when he first came to lodge at the present rag and bottle shop—”\n\n“That was the time!” says Krook with a nod.\n\n“About a year and a half ago,” says Mr. Snagsby, strengthened, “he came into our place one morning after breakfast, and finding my little woman (which I name Mrs. Snagsby when I use that appellation) in our shop, produced a specimen of his handwriting and gave her to understand that he was in want of copying work to do and was, not to put too fine a point upon it,” a favourite apology for plain speaking with Mr. Snagsby, which he always offers with a sort of argumentative frankness, “hard up! My little woman is not in general partial to strangers, particular—not to put too fine a point upon it—when they want anything. But she was rather took by something about this person, whether by his being unshaved, or by his hair being in want of attention, or by what other ladies’ reasons, I leave you to judge; and she accepted of the specimen, and likewise of the address. My little woman hasn’t a good ear for names,” proceeds Mr. Snagsby after consulting his cough of consideration behind his hand, “and she considered Nemo equally the same as Nimrod. In consequence of which, she got into a habit of saying to me at meals, ‘Mr. Snagsby, you haven’t found Nimrod any work yet!’ or ‘Mr. Snagsby, why didn’t you give that eight and thirty Chancery folio in Jarndyce to Nimrod?’ or such like. And that is the way he gradually fell into job-work at our place; and that is the most I know of him except that he was a quick hand, and a hand not sparing of night-work, and that if you gave him out, say, five and forty folio on the Wednesday night, you would have it brought in on the Thursday morning. All of which—” Mr. Snagsby concludes by politely motioning with his hat towards the bed, as much as to add, “I have no doubt my honourable friend would confirm if he were in a condition to do it.”\n\n“Hadn’t you better see,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn to Krook, “whether he had any papers that may enlighten you? There will be an inquest, and you will be asked the question. You can read?”\n\n“No, I can’t,” returns the old man with a sudden grin.\n\n“Snagsby,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “look over the room for him. He will get into some trouble or difficulty otherwise. Being here, I’ll wait if you make haste, and then I can testify on his behalf, if it should ever be necessary, that all was fair and right. If you will hold the candle for Mr. Snagsby, my friend, he’ll soon see whether there is anything to help you.”\n\n“In the first place, here’s an old portmanteau, sir,” says Snagsby.\n\nAh, to be sure, so there is! Mr. Tulkinghorn does not appear to have seen it before, though he is standing so close to it, and though there is very little else, heaven knows.\n\nThe marine-store merchant holds the light, and the law-stationer conducts the search. The surgeon leans against the corner of the chimney-piece; Miss Flite peeps and trembles just within the door. The apt old scholar of the old school, with his dull black breeches tied with ribbons at the knees, his large black waistcoat, his long-sleeved black coat, and his wisp of limp white neckerchief tied in the bow the peerage knows so well, stands in exactly the same place and attitude.\n\nThere are some worthless articles of clothing in the old portmanteau; there is a bundle of pawnbrokers’ duplicates, those turnpike tickets on the road of poverty; there is a crumpled paper, smelling of opium, on which are scrawled rough memoranda—as, took, such a day, so many grains; took, such another day, so many more—begun some time ago, as if with the intention of being regularly continued, but soon left off. There are a few dirty scraps of newspapers, all referring to coroners’ inquests; there is nothing else. They search the cupboard and the drawer of the ink-splashed table. There is not a morsel of an old letter or of any other writing in either. The young surgeon examines the dress on the law-writer. A knife and some odd halfpence are all he finds. Mr. Snagsby’s suggestion is the practical suggestion after all, and the beadle must be called in.\n\nSo the little crazy lodger goes for the beadle, and the rest come out of the room. “Don’t leave the cat there!” says the surgeon; “that won’t do!” Mr. Krook therefore drives her out before him, and she goes furtively downstairs, winding her lithe tail and licking her lips.\n\n“Good night!” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, and goes home to Allegory and meditation.\n\nBy this time the news has got into the court. Groups of its inhabitants assemble to discuss the thing, and the outposts of the army of observation (principally boys) are pushed forward to Mr. Krook’s window, which they closely invest. A policeman has already walked up to the room, and walked down again to the door, where he stands like a tower, only condescending to see the boys at his base occasionally; but whenever he does see them, they quail and fall back. Mrs. Perkins, who has not been for some weeks on speaking terms with Mrs. Piper in consequence for an unpleasantness originating in young Perkins’ having “fetched” young Piper “a crack,” renews her friendly intercourse on this auspicious occasion. The potboy at the corner, who is a privileged amateur, as possessing official knowledge of life and having to deal with drunken men occasionally, exchanges confidential communications with the policeman and has the appearance of an impregnable youth, unassailable by truncheons and unconfinable in station-houses. People talk across the court out of window, and bare-headed scouts come hurrying in from Chancery Lane to know what’s the matter. The general feeling seems to be that it’s a blessing Mr. Krook warn’t made away with first, mingled with a little natural disappointment that he was not. In the midst of this sensation, the beadle arrives.\n\nThe beadle, though generally understood in the neighbourhood to be a ridiculous institution, is not without a certain popularity for the moment, if it were only as a man who is going to see the body. The policeman considers him an imbecile civilian, a remnant of the barbarous watchmen times, but gives him admission as something that must be borne with until government shall abolish him. The sensation is heightened as the tidings spread from mouth to mouth that the beadle is on the ground and has gone in.\n\nBy and by the beadle comes out, once more intensifying the sensation, which has rather languished in the interval. He is understood to be in want of witnesses for the inquest to-morrow who can tell the coroner and jury anything whatever respecting the deceased. Is immediately referred to innumerable people who can tell nothing whatever. Is made more imbecile by being constantly informed that Mrs. Green’s son “was a law-writer his-self and knowed him better than anybody,” which son of Mrs. Green’s appears, on inquiry, to be at the present time aboard a vessel bound for China, three months out, but considered accessible by telegraph on application to the Lords of the Admiralty. Beadle goes into various shops and parlours, examining the inhabitants, always shutting the door first, and by exclusion, delay, and general idiotcy exasperating the public. Policeman seen to smile to potboy. Public loses interest and undergoes reaction. Taunts the beadle in shrill youthful voices with having boiled a boy, choruses fragments of a popular song to that effect and importing that the boy was made into soup for the workhouse. Policeman at last finds it necessary to support the law and seize a vocalist, who is released upon the flight of the rest on condition of his getting out of this then, come, and cutting it—a condition he immediately observes. So the sensation dies off for the time; and the unmoved policeman (to whom a little opium, more or less, is nothing), with his shining hat, stiff stock, inflexible great-coat, stout belt and bracelet, and all things fitting, pursues his lounging way with a heavy tread, beating the palms of his white gloves one against the other and stopping now and then at a street-corner to look casually about for anything between a lost child and a murder.\n\nUnder cover of the night, the feeble-minded beadle comes flitting about Chancery Lane with his summonses, in which every juror’s name is wrongly spelt, and nothing rightly spelt but the beadle’s own name, which nobody can read or wants to know. The summonses served and his witnesses forewarned, the beadle goes to Mr. Krook’s to keep a small appointment he has made with certain paupers, who, presently arriving, are conducted upstairs, where they leave the great eyes in the shutter something new to stare at, in that last shape which earthly lodgings take for No one—and for Every one.\n\nAnd all that night the coffin stands ready by the old portmanteau; and the lonely figure on the bed, whose path in life has lain through five and forty years, lies there with no more track behind him that any one can trace than a deserted infant.\n\nNext day the court is all alive—is like a fair, as Mrs. Perkins, more than reconciled to Mrs. Piper, says in amicable conversation with that excellent woman. The coroner is to sit in the first-floor room at the Sol’s Arms, where the Harmonic Meetings take place twice a week and where the chair is filled by a gentleman of professional celebrity, faced by Little Swills, the comic vocalist, who hopes (according to the bill in the window) that his friends will rally round him and support first-rate talent. The Sol’s Arms does a brisk stroke of business all the morning. Even children so require sustaining under the general excitement that a pieman who has established himself for the occasion at the corner of the court says his brandy-balls go off like smoke. What time the beadle, hovering between the door of Mr. Krook’s establishment and the door of the Sol’s Arms, shows the curiosity in his keeping to a few discreet spirits and accepts the compliment of a glass of ale or so in return.\n\nAt the appointed hour arrives the coroner, for whom the jurymen are waiting and who is received with a salute of skittles from the good dry skittle-ground attached to the Sol’s Arms. The coroner frequents more public-houses than any man alive. The smell of sawdust, beer, tobacco-smoke, and spirits is inseparable in his vocation from death in its most awful shapes. He is conducted by the beadle and the landlord to the Harmonic Meeting Room, where he puts his hat on the piano and takes a Windsor-chair at the head of a long table formed of several short tables put together and ornamented with glutinous rings in endless involutions, made by pots and glasses. As many of the jury as can crowd together at the table sit there. The rest get among the spittoons and pipes or lean against the piano. Over the coroner’s head is a small iron garland, the pendant handle of a bell, which rather gives the majesty of the court the appearance of going to be hanged presently.\n\nCall over and swear the jury! While the ceremony is in progress, sensation is created by the entrance of a chubby little man in a large shirt-collar, with a moist eye and an inflamed nose, who modestly takes a position near the door as one of the general public, but seems familiar with the room too. A whisper circulates that this is Little Swills. It is considered not unlikely that he will get up an imitation of the coroner and make it the principal feature of the Harmonic Meeting in the evening.\n\n“Well, gentlemen—” the coroner begins.\n\n“Silence there, will you!” says the beadle. Not to the coroner, though it might appear so.\n\n“Well, gentlemen,” resumes the coroner. “You are impanelled here to inquire into the death of a certain man. Evidence will be given before you as to the circumstances attending that death, and you will give your verdict according to the—skittles; they must be stopped, you know, beadle!—evidence, and not according to anything else. The first thing to be done is to view the body.”\n\n“Make way there!” cries the beadle.\n\nSo they go out in a loose procession, something after the manner of a straggling funeral, and make their inspection in Mr. Krook’s back second floor, from which a few of the jurymen retire pale and precipitately. The beadle is very careful that two gentlemen not very neat about the cuffs and buttons (for whose accommodation he has provided a special little table near the coroner in the Harmonic Meeting Room) should see all that is to be seen. For they are the public chroniclers of such inquiries by the line; and he is not superior to the universal human infirmity, but hopes to read in print what “Mooney, the active and intelligent beadle of the district,” said and did and even aspires to see the name of Mooney as familiarly and patronizingly mentioned as the name of the hangman is, according to the latest examples.\n\nLittle Swills is waiting for the coroner and jury on their return. Mr. Tulkinghorn, also. Mr. Tulkinghorn is received with distinction and seated near the coroner between that high judicial officer, a bagatelle-board, and the coal-box. The inquiry proceeds. The jury learn how the subject of their inquiry died, and learn no more about him. “A very eminent solicitor is in attendance, gentlemen,” says the coroner, “who, I am informed, was accidentally present when discovery of the death was made, but he could only repeat the evidence you have already heard from the surgeon, the landlord, the lodger, and the law-stationer, and it is not necessary to trouble him. Is anybody in attendance who knows anything more?”\n\nMrs. Piper pushed forward by Mrs. Perkins. Mrs. Piper sworn.\n\nAnastasia Piper, gentlemen. Married woman. Now, Mrs. Piper, what have you got to say about this?\n\nWhy, Mrs. Piper has a good deal to say, chiefly in parentheses and without punctuation, but not much to tell. Mrs. Piper lives in the court (which her husband is a cabinet-maker), and it has long been well beknown among the neighbours (counting from the day next but one before the half-baptizing of Alexander James Piper aged eighteen months and four days old on accounts of not being expected to live such was the sufferings gentlemen of that child in his gums) as the plaintive—so Mrs. Piper insists on calling the deceased—was reported to have sold himself. Thinks it was the plaintive’s air in which that report originatinin. See the plaintive often and considered as his air was feariocious and not to be allowed to go about some children being timid (and if doubted hoping Mrs. Perkins may be brought forard for she is here and will do credit to her husband and herself and family). Has seen the plaintive wexed and worrited by the children (for children they will ever be and you cannot expect them specially if of playful dispositions to be Methoozellers which you was not yourself). On accounts of this and his dark looks has often dreamed as she see him take a pick-axe from his pocket and split Johnny’s head (which the child knows not fear and has repeatually called after him close at his eels). Never however see the plaintive take a pick-axe or any other wepping far from it. Has seen him hurry away when run and called after as if not partial to children and never see him speak to neither child nor grown person at any time (excepting the boy that sweeps the crossing down the lane over the way round the corner which if he was here would tell you that he has been seen a-speaking to him frequent).\n\nSays the coroner, is that boy here? Says the beadle, no, sir, he is not here. Says the coroner, go and fetch him then. In the absence of the active and intelligent, the coroner converses with Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\nOh! Here’s the boy, gentlemen!\n\nHere he is, very muddy, very hoarse, very ragged. Now, boy! But stop a minute. Caution. This boy must be put through a few preliminary paces.\n\nName, Jo. Nothing else that he knows on. Don’t know that everybody has two names. Never heerd of sich a think. Don’t know that Jo is short for a longer name. Thinks it long enough for HIM. HE don’t find no fault with it. Spell it? No. HE can’t spell it. No father, no mother, no friends. Never been to school. What’s home? Knows a broom’s a broom, and knows it’s wicked to tell a lie. Don’t recollect who told him about the broom or about the lie, but knows both. Can’t exactly say what’ll be done to him arter he’s dead if he tells a lie to the gentlemen here, but believes it’ll be something wery bad to punish him, and serve him right—and so he’ll tell the truth.\n\n“This won’t do, gentlemen!” says the coroner with a melancholy shake of the head.\n\n“Don’t you think you can receive his evidence, sir?” asks an attentive juryman.\n\n“Out of the question,” says the coroner. “You have heard the boy. ‘Can’t exactly say’ won’t do, you know. We can’t take THAT in a court of justice, gentlemen. It’s terrible depravity. Put the boy aside.”\n\nBoy put aside, to the great edification of the audience, especially of Little Swills, the comic vocalist.\n\nNow. Is there any other witness? No other witness.\n\nVery well, gentlemen! Here’s a man unknown, proved to have been in the habit of taking opium in large quantities for a year and a half, found dead of too much opium. If you think you have any evidence to lead you to the conclusion that he committed suicide, you will come to that conclusion. If you think it is a case of accidental death, you will find a verdict accordingly.\n\nVerdict accordingly. Accidental death. No doubt. Gentlemen, you are discharged. Good afternoon.\n\nWhile the coroner buttons his great-coat, Mr. Tulkinghorn and he give private audience to the rejected witness in a corner.\n\nThat graceless creature only knows that the dead man (whom he recognized just now by his yellow face and black hair) was sometimes hooted and pursued about the streets. That one cold winter night when he, the boy, was shivering in a doorway near his crossing, the man turned to look at him, and came back, and having questioned him and found that he had not a friend in the world, said, “Neither have I. Not one!” and gave him the price of a supper and a night’s lodging. That the man had often spoken to him since and asked him whether he slept sound at night, and how he bore cold and hunger, and whether he ever wished to die, and similar strange questions. That when the man had no money, he would say in passing, “I am as poor as you to-day, Jo,” but that when he had any, he had always (as the boy most heartily believes) been glad to give him some.\n\n“He was wery good to me,” says the boy, wiping his eyes with his wretched sleeve. “Wen I see him a-layin’ so stritched out just now, I wished he could have heerd me tell him so. He wos wery good to me, he wos!”\n\nAs he shuffles downstairs, Mr. Snagsby, lying in wait for him, puts a half-crown in his hand. “If you ever see me coming past your crossing with my little woman—I mean a lady—” says Mr. Snagsby with his finger on his nose, “don’t allude to it!”\n\nFor some little time the jurymen hang about the Sol’s Arms colloquially. In the sequel, half-a-dozen are caught up in a cloud of pipe-smoke that pervades the parlour of the Sol’s Arms; two stroll to Hampstead; and four engage to go half-price to the play at night, and top up with oysters. Little Swills is treated on several hands. Being asked what he thinks of the proceedings, characterizes them (his strength lying in a slangular direction) as “a rummy start.” The landlord of the Sol’s Arms, finding Little Swills so popular, commends him highly to the jurymen and public, observing that for a song in character he don’t know his equal and that that man’s character-wardrobe would fill a cart.\n\nThus, gradually the Sol’s Arms melts into the shadowy night and then flares out of it strong in gas. The Harmonic Meeting hour arriving, the gentleman of professional celebrity takes the chair, is faced (red-faced) by Little Swills; their friends rally round them and support first-rate talent. In the zenith of the evening, Little Swills says, “Gentlemen, if you’ll permit me, I’ll attempt a short description of a scene of real life that came off here to-day.” Is much applauded and encouraged; goes out of the room as Swills; comes in as the coroner (not the least in the world like him); describes the inquest, with recreative intervals of piano-forte accompaniment, to the refrain: With his (the coroner’s) tippy tol li doll, tippy tol lo doll, tippy tol li doll, Dee!\n\nThe jingling piano at last is silent, and the Harmonic friends rally round their pillows. Then there is rest around the lonely figure, now laid in its last earthly habitation; and it is watched by the gaunt eyes in the shutters through some quiet hours of night. If this forlorn man could have been prophetically seen lying here by the mother at whose breast he nestled, a little child, with eyes upraised to her loving face, and soft hand scarcely knowing how to close upon the neck to which it crept, what an impossibility the vision would have seemed! Oh, if in brighter days the now-extinguished fire within him ever burned for one woman who held him in her heart, where is she, while these ashes are above the ground!\n\nIt is anything but a night of rest at Mr. Snagsby’s, in Cook’s Court, where Guster murders sleep by going, as Mr. Snagsby himself allows—not to put too fine a point upon it—out of one fit into twenty. The occasion of this seizure is that Guster has a tender heart and a susceptible something that possibly might have been imagination, but for Tooting and her patron saint. Be it what it may, now, it was so direfully impressed at tea-time by Mr. Snagsby’s account of the inquiry at which he had assisted that at supper-time she projected herself into the kitchen, preceded by a flying Dutch cheese, and fell into a fit of unusual duration, which she only came out of to go into another, and another, and so on through a chain of fits, with short intervals between, of which she has pathetically availed herself by consuming them in entreaties to Mrs. Snagsby not to give her warning “when she quite comes to,” and also in appeals to the whole establishment to lay her down on the stones and go to bed. Hence, Mr. Snagsby, at last hearing the cock at the little dairy in Cursitor Street go into that disinterested ecstasy of his on the subject of daylight, says, drawing a long breath, though the most patient of men, “I thought you was dead, I am sure!”\n\nWhat question this enthusiastic fowl supposes he settles when he strains himself to such an extent, or why he should thus crow (so men crow on various triumphant public occasions, however) about what cannot be of any moment to him, is his affair. It is enough that daylight comes, morning comes, noon comes.\n\nThen the active and intelligent, who has got into the morning papers as such, comes with his pauper company to Mr. Krook’s and bears off the body of our dear brother here departed to a hemmed-in churchyard, pestiferous and obscene, whence malignant diseases are communicated to the bodies of our dear brothers and sisters who have not departed, while our dear brothers and sisters who hang about official back-stairs—would to heaven they HAD departed!—are very complacent and agreeable. Into a beastly scrap of ground which a Turk would reject as a savage abomination and a Caffre would shudder at, they bring our dear brother here departed to receive Christian burial.\n\nWith houses looking on, on every side, save where a reeking little tunnel of a court gives access to the iron gate—with every villainy of life in action close on death, and every poisonous element of death in action close on life—here they lower our dear brother down a foot or two, here sow him in corruption, to be raised in corruption: an avenging ghost at many a sick-bedside, a shameful testimony to future ages how civilization and barbarism walked this boastful island together.\n\nCome night, come darkness, for you cannot come too soon or stay too long by such a place as this! Come, straggling lights into the windows of the ugly houses; and you who do iniquity therein, do it at least with this dread scene shut out! Come, flame of gas, burning so sullenly above the iron gate, on which the poisoned air deposits its witch-ointment slimy to the touch! It is well that you should call to every passerby, “Look here!”\n\nWith the night comes a slouching figure through the tunnel-court to the outside of the iron gate. It holds the gate with its hands and looks in between the bars, stands looking in for a little while.\n\nIt then, with an old broom it carries, softly sweeps the step and makes the archway clean. It does so very busily and trimly, looks in again a little while, and so departs.\n\nJo, is it thou? Well, well! Though a rejected witness, who “can’t exactly say” what will be done to him in greater hands than men’s, thou art not quite in outer darkness. There is something like a distant ray of light in thy muttered reason for this: “He wos wery good to me, he wos!”\nOn the Watch\n\nIt has left off raining down in Lincolnshire at last, and Chesney Wold has taken heart. Mrs. Rouncewell is full of hospitable cares, for Sir Leicester and my Lady are coming home from Paris. The fashionable intelligence has found it out and communicates the glad tidings to benighted England. It has also found out that they will entertain a brilliant and distinguished circle of the ELITE of the BEAU MONDE (the fashionable intelligence is weak in English, but a giant refreshed in French) at the ancient and hospitable family seat in Lincolnshire.\n\nFor the greater honour of the brilliant and distinguished circle, and of Chesney Wold into the bargain, the broken arch of the bridge in the park is mended; and the water, now retired within its proper limits and again spanned gracefully, makes a figure in the prospect from the house. The clear, cold sunshine glances into the brittle woods and approvingly beholds the sharp wind scattering the leaves and drying the moss. It glides over the park after the moving shadows of the clouds, and chases them, and never catches them, all day. It looks in at the windows and touches the ancestral portraits with bars and patches of brightness never contemplated by the painters. Athwart the picture of my Lady, over the great chimney-piece, it throws a broad bend-sinister of light that strikes down crookedly into the hearth and seems to rend it.\n\nThrough the same cold sunshine and the same sharp wind, my Lady and Sir Leicester, in their travelling chariot (my Lady’s woman and Sir Leicester’s man affectionate in the rumble), start for home. With a considerable amount of jingling and whip-cracking, and many plunging demonstrations on the part of two bare-backed horses and two centaurs with glazed hats, jack-boots, and flowing manes and tails, they rattle out of the yard of the Hôtel Bristol in the Place Vendôme and canter between the sun-and-shadow-chequered colonnade of the Rue de Rivoli and the garden of the ill-fated palace of a headless king and queen, off by the Place of Concord, and the Elysian Fields, and the Gate of the Star, out of Paris.\n\nSooth to say, they cannot go away too fast, for even here my Lady Dedlock has been bored to death. Concert, assembly, opera, theatre, drive, nothing is new to my Lady under the worn-out heavens. Only last Sunday, when poor wretches were gay—within the walls playing with children among the clipped trees and the statues in the Palace Garden; walking, a score abreast, in the Elysian Fields, made more Elysian by performing dogs and wooden horses; between whiles filtering (a few) through the gloomy Cathedral of Our Lady to say a word or two at the base of a pillar within flare of a rusty little gridiron-full of gusty little tapers; without the walls encompassing Paris with dancing, love-making, wine-drinking, tobacco-smoking, tomb-visiting, billiard card and domino playing, quack-doctoring, and much murderous refuse, animate and inanimate—only last Sunday, my Lady, in the desolation of Boredom and the clutch of Giant Despair, almost hated her own maid for being in spirits.\n\nShe cannot, therefore, go too fast from Paris. Weariness of soul lies before her, as it lies behind—her Ariel has put a girdle of it round the whole earth, and it cannot be unclasped—but the imperfect remedy is always to fly from the last place where it has been experienced. Fling Paris back into the distance, then, exchanging it for endless avenues and cross-avenues of wintry trees! And, when next beheld, let it be some leagues away, with the Gate of the Star a white speck glittering in the sun, and the city a mere mound in a plain—two dark square towers rising out of it, and light and shadow descending on it aslant, like the angels in Jacob’s dream!\n\nSir Leicester is generally in a complacent state, and rarely bored. When he has nothing else to do, he can always contemplate his own greatness. It is a considerable advantage to a man to have so inexhaustible a subject. After reading his letters, he leans back in his corner of the carriage and generally reviews his importance to society.\n\n“You have an unusual amount of correspondence this morning?” says my Lady after a long time. She is fatigued with reading. Has almost read a page in twenty miles.\n\n“Nothing in it, though. Nothing whatever.”\n\n“I saw one of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s long effusions, I think?”\n\n“You see everything,” says Sir Leicester with admiration.\n\n“Ha!” sighs my Lady. “He is the most tiresome of men!”\n\n“He sends—I really beg your pardon—he sends,” says Sir Leicester, selecting the letter and unfolding it, “a message to you. Our stopping to change horses as I came to his postscript drove it out of my memory. I beg you’ll excuse me. He says—” Sir Leicester is so long in taking out his eye-glass and adjusting it that my Lady looks a little irritated. “He says ‘In the matter of the right of way—’ I beg your pardon, that’s not the place. He says—yes! Here I have it! He says, ‘I beg my respectful compliments to my Lady, who, I hope, has benefited by the change. Will you do me the favour to mention (as it may interest her) that I have something to tell her on her return in reference to the person who copied the affidavit in the Chancery suit, which so powerfully stimulated her curiosity. I have seen him.’”\n\nMy Lady, leaning forward, looks out of her window.\n\n“That’s the message,” observes Sir Leicester.\n\n“I should like to walk a little,” says my Lady, still looking out of her window.\n\n“Walk?” repeats Sir Leicester in a tone of surprise.\n\n“I should like to walk a little,” says my Lady with unmistakable distinctness. “Please to stop the carriage.”\n\nThe carriage is stopped, the affectionate man alights from the rumble, opens the door, and lets down the steps, obedient to an impatient motion of my Lady’s hand. My Lady alights so quickly and walks away so quickly that Sir Leicester, for all his scrupulous politeness, is unable to assist her, and is left behind. A space of a minute or two has elapsed before he comes up with her. She smiles, looks very handsome, takes his arm, lounges with him for a quarter of a mile, is very much bored, and resumes her seat in the carriage.\n\nThe rattle and clatter continue through the greater part of three days, with more or less of bell-jingling and whip-cracking, and more or less plunging of centaurs and bare-backed horses. Their courtly politeness to each other at the hotels where they tarry is the theme of general admiration. Though my Lord IS a little aged for my Lady, says Madame, the hostess of the Golden Ape, and though he might be her amiable father, one can see at a glance that they love each other. One observes my Lord with his white hair, standing, hat in hand, to help my Lady to and from the carriage. One observes my Lady, how recognisant of my Lord’s politeness, with an inclination of her gracious head and the concession of her so-genteel fingers! It is ravishing!\n\nThe sea has no appreciation of great men, but knocks them about like the small fry. It is habitually hard upon Sir Leicester, whose countenance it greenly mottles in the manner of sage-cheese and in whose aristocratic system it effects a dismal revolution. It is the Radical of Nature to him. Nevertheless, his dignity gets over it after stopping to refit, and he goes on with my Lady for Chesney Wold, lying only one night in London on the way to Lincolnshire.\n\nThrough the same cold sunlight, colder as the day declines, and through the same sharp wind, sharper as the separate shadows of bare trees gloom together in the woods, and as the Ghost’s Walk, touched at the western corner by a pile of fire in the sky, resigns itself to coming night, they drive into the park. The rooks, swinging in their lofty houses in the elm-tree avenue, seem to discuss the question of the occupancy of the carriage as it passes underneath, some agreeing that Sir Leicester and my Lady are come down, some arguing with malcontents who won’t admit it, now all consenting to consider the question disposed of, now all breaking out again in violent debate, incited by one obstinate and drowsy bird who will persist in putting in a last contradictory croak. Leaving them to swing and caw, the travelling chariot rolls on to the house, where fires gleam warmly through some of the windows, though not through so many as to give an inhabited expression to the darkening mass of front. But the brilliant and distinguished circle will soon do that.\n\nMrs. Rouncewell is in attendance and receives Sir Leicester’s customary shake of the hand with a profound curtsy.\n\n“How do you do, Mrs. Rouncewell? I am glad to see you.”\n\n“I hope I have the honour of welcoming you in good health, Sir Leicester?”\n\n“In excellent health, Mrs. Rouncewell.”\n\n“My Lady is looking charmingly well,” says Mrs. Rouncewell with another curtsy.\n\nMy Lady signifies, without profuse expenditure of words, that she is as wearily well as she can hope to be.\n\nBut Rosa is in the distance, behind the housekeeper; and my Lady, who has not subdued the quickness of her observation, whatever else she may have conquered, asks, “Who is that girl?”\n\n“A young scholar of mine, my Lady. Rosa.”\n\n“Come here, Rosa!” Lady Dedlock beckons her, with even an appearance of interest. “Why, do you know how pretty you are, child?” she says, touching her shoulder with her two forefingers.\n\nRosa, very much abashed, says, “No, if you please, my Lady!” and glances up, and glances down, and don’t know where to look, but looks all the prettier.\n\n“How old are you?”\n\n“Nineteen, my Lady.”\n\n“Nineteen,” repeats my Lady thoughtfully. “Take care they don’t spoil you by flattery.”\n\n“Yes, my Lady.”\n\nMy Lady taps her dimpled cheek with the same delicate gloved fingers and goes on to the foot of the oak staircase, where Sir Leicester pauses for her as her knightly escort. A staring old Dedlock in a panel, as large as life and as dull, looks as if he didn’t know what to make of it, which was probably his general state of mind in the days of Queen Elizabeth.\n\nThat evening, in the housekeeper’s room, Rosa can do nothing but murmur Lady Dedlock’s praises. She is so affable, so graceful, so beautiful, so elegant; has such a sweet voice and such a thrilling touch that Rosa can feel it yet! Mrs. Rouncewell confirms all this, not without personal pride, reserving only the one point of affability. Mrs. Rouncewell is not quite sure as to that. Heaven forbid that she should say a syllable in dispraise of any member of that excellent family, above all, of my Lady, whom the whole world admires; but if my Lady would only be “a little more free,” not quite so cold and distant, Mrs. Rouncewell thinks she would be more affable.\n\n“’Tis almost a pity,” Mrs. Rouncewell adds—only “almost” because it borders on impiety to suppose that anything could be better than it is, in such an express dispensation as the Dedlock affairs—“that my Lady has no family. If she had had a daughter now, a grown young lady, to interest her, I think she would have had the only kind of excellence she wants.”\n\n“Might not that have made her still more proud, grandmother?” says Watt, who has been home and come back again, he is such a good grandson.\n\n“More and most, my dear,” returns the housekeeper with dignity, “are words it’s not my place to use—nor so much as to hear—applied to any drawback on my Lady.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, grandmother. But she is proud, is she not?”\n\n“If she is, she has reason to be. The Dedlock family have always reason to be.”\n\n“Well,” says Watt, “it’s to be hoped they line out of their prayer-books a certain passage for the common people about pride and vainglory. Forgive me, grandmother! Only a joke!”\n\n“Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, my dear, are not fit subjects for joking.”\n\n“Sir Leicester is no joke by any means,” says Watt, “and I humbly ask his pardon. I suppose, grandmother, that even with the family and their guests down here, there is no objection to my prolonging my stay at the Dedlock Arms for a day or two, as any other traveller might?”\n\n“Surely, none in the world, child.”\n\n“I am glad of that,” says Watt, “because I have an inexpressible desire to extend my knowledge of this beautiful neighbourhood.”\n\nHe happens to glance at Rosa, who looks down and is very shy indeed. But according to the old superstition, it should be Rosa’s ears that burn, and not her fresh bright cheeks, for my Lady’s maid is holding forth about her at this moment with surpassing energy.\n\nMy Lady’s maid is a Frenchwoman of two and thirty, from somewhere in the southern country about Avignon and Marseilles, a large-eyed brown woman with black hair who would be handsome but for a certain feline mouth and general uncomfortable tightness of face, rendering the jaws too eager and the skull too prominent. There is something indefinably keen and wan about her anatomy, and she has a watchful way of looking out of the corners of her eyes without turning her head which could be pleasantly dispensed with, especially when she is in an ill humour and near knives. Through all the good taste of her dress and little adornments, these objections so express themselves that she seems to go about like a very neat she-wolf imperfectly tamed. Besides being accomplished in all the knowledge appertaining to her post, she is almost an Englishwoman in her acquaintance with the language; consequently, she is in no want of words to shower upon Rosa for having attracted my Lady’s attention, and she pours them out with such grim ridicule as she sits at dinner that her companion, the affectionate man, is rather relieved when she arrives at the spoon stage of that performance.\n\nHa, ha, ha! She, Hortense, been in my Lady’s service since five years and always kept at the distance, and this doll, this puppet, caressed—absolutely caressed—by my Lady on the moment of her arriving at the house! Ha, ha, ha! “And do you know how pretty you are, child?” “No, my Lady.” You are right there! “And how old are you, child! And take care they do not spoil you by flattery, child!” Oh, how droll! It is the BEST thing altogether.\n\nIn short, it is such an admirable thing that Mademoiselle Hortense can’t forget it; but at meals for days afterwards, even among her countrywomen and others attached in like capacity to the troop of visitors, relapses into silent enjoyment of the joke—an enjoyment expressed, in her own convivial manner, by an additional tightness of face, thin elongation of compressed lips, and sidewise look, which intense appreciation of humour is frequently reflected in my Lady’s mirrors when my Lady is not among them.\n\nAll the mirrors in the house are brought into action now, many of them after a long blank. They reflect handsome faces, simpering faces, youthful faces, faces of threescore and ten that will not submit to be old; the entire collection of faces that have come to pass a January week or two at Chesney Wold, and which the fashionable intelligence, a mighty hunter before the Lord, hunts with a keen scent, from their breaking cover at the Court of St. James’s to their being run down to death. The place in Lincolnshire is all alive. By day guns and voices are heard ringing in the woods, horsemen and carriages enliven the park roads, servants and hangers-on pervade the village and the Dedlock Arms. Seen by night from distant openings in the trees, the row of windows in the long drawing-room, where my Lady’s picture hangs over the great chimney-piece, is like a row of jewels set in a black frame. On Sunday the chill little church is almost warmed by so much gallant company, and the general flavour of the Dedlock dust is quenched in delicate perfumes.\n\nThe brilliant and distinguished circle comprehends within it no contracted amount of education, sense, courage, honour, beauty, and virtue. Yet there is something a little wrong about it in despite of its immense advantages. What can it be?\n\nDandyism? There is no King George the Fourth now (more the pity) to set the dandy fashion; there are no clear-starched jack-towel neckcloths, no short-waisted coats, no false calves, no stays. There are no caricatures, now, of effeminate exquisites so arrayed, swooning in opera boxes with excess of delight and being revived by other dainty creatures poking long-necked scent-bottles at their noses. There is no beau whom it takes four men at once to shake into his buckskins, or who goes to see all the executions, or who is troubled with the self-reproach of having once consumed a pea. But is there dandyism in the brilliant and distinguished circle notwithstanding, dandyism of a more mischievous sort, that has got below the surface and is doing less harmless things than jack-towelling itself and stopping its own digestion, to which no rational person need particularly object?\n\nWhy, yes. It cannot be disguised. There ARE at Chesney Wold this January week some ladies and gentlemen of the newest fashion, who have set up a dandyism—in religion, for instance. Who in mere lackadaisical want of an emotion have agreed upon a little dandy talk about the vulgar wanting faith in things in general, meaning in the things that have been tried and found wanting, as though a low fellow should unaccountably lose faith in a bad shilling after finding it out! Who would make the vulgar very picturesque and faithful by putting back the hands upon the clock of time and cancelling a few hundred years of history.\n\nThere are also ladies and gentlemen of another fashion, not so new, but very elegant, who have agreed to put a smooth glaze on the world and to keep down all its realities. For whom everything must be languid and pretty. Who have found out the perpetual stoppage. Who are to rejoice at nothing and be sorry for nothing. Who are not to be disturbed by ideas. On whom even the fine arts, attending in powder and walking backward like the Lord Chamberlain, must array themselves in the milliners’ and tailors’ patterns of past generations and be particularly careful not to be in earnest or to receive any impress from the moving age.\n\nThen there is my Lord Boodle, of considerable reputation with his party, who has known what office is and who tells Sir Leicester Dedlock with much gravity, after dinner, that he really does not see to what the present age is tending. A debate is not what a debate used to be; the House is not what the House used to be; even a Cabinet is not what it formerly was. He perceives with astonishment that supposing the present government to be overthrown, the limited choice of the Crown, in the formation of a new ministry, would lie between Lord Coodle and Sir Thomas Doodle—supposing it to be impossible for the Duke of Foodle to act with Goodle, which may be assumed to be the case in consequence of the breach arising out of that affair with Hoodle. Then, giving the Home Department and the leadership of the House of Commons to Joodle, the Exchequer to Koodle, the Colonies to Loodle, and the Foreign Office to Moodle, what are you to do with Noodle? You can’t offer him the Presidency of the Council; that is reserved for Poodle. You can’t put him in the Woods and Forests; that is hardly good enough for Quoodle. What follows? That the country is shipwrecked, lost, and gone to pieces (as is made manifest to the patriotism of Sir Leicester Dedlock) because you can’t provide for Noodle!\n\nOn the other hand, the Right Honourable William Buffy, M.P., contends across the table with some one else that the shipwreck of the country—about which there is no doubt; it is only the manner of it that is in question—is attributable to Cuffy. If you had done with Cuffy what you ought to have done when he first came into Parliament, and had prevented him from going over to Duffy, you would have got him into alliance with Fuffy, you would have had with you the weight attaching as a smart debater to Guffy, you would have brought to bear upon the elections the wealth of Huffy, you would have got in for three counties Juffy, Kuffy, and Luffy, and you would have strengthened your administration by the official knowledge and the business habits of Muffy. All this, instead of being as you now are, dependent on the mere caprice of Puffy!\n\nAs to this point, and as to some minor topics, there are differences of opinion; but it is perfectly clear to the brilliant and distinguished circle, all round, that nobody is in question but Boodle and his retinue, and Buffy and HIS retinue. These are the great actors for whom the stage is reserved. A People there are, no doubt—a certain large number of supernumeraries, who are to be occasionally addressed, and relied upon for shouts and choruses, as on the theatrical stage; but Boodle and Buffy, their followers and families, their heirs, executors, administrators, and assigns, are the born first-actors, managers, and leaders, and no others can appear upon the scene for ever and ever.\n\nIn this, too, there is perhaps more dandyism at Chesney Wold than the brilliant and distinguished circle will find good for itself in the long run. For it is, even with the stillest and politest circles, as with the circle the necromancer draws around him—very strange appearances may be seen in active motion outside. With this difference, that being realities and not phantoms, there is the greater danger of their breaking in.\n\nChesney Wold is quite full anyhow, so full that a burning sense of injury arises in the breasts of ill-lodged ladies’-maids, and is not to be extinguished. Only one room is empty. It is a turret chamber of the third order of merit, plainly but comfortably furnished and having an old-fashioned business air. It is Mr. Tulkinghorn’s room, and is never bestowed on anybody else, for he may come at any time. He is not come yet. It is his quiet habit to walk across the park from the village in fine weather, to drop into this room as if he had never been out of it since he was last seen there, to request a servant to inform Sir Leicester that he is arrived in case he should be wanted, and to appear ten minutes before dinner in the shadow of the library-door. He sleeps in his turret with a complaining flag-staff over his head, and has some leads outside on which, any fine morning when he is down here, his black figure may be seen walking before breakfast like a larger species of rook.\n\nEvery day before dinner, my Lady looks for him in the dusk of the library, but he is not there. Every day at dinner, my Lady glances down the table for the vacant place that would be waiting to receive him if he had just arrived, but there is no vacant place. Every night my Lady casually asks her maid, “Is Mr. Tulkinghorn come?”\n\nEvery night the answer is, “No, my Lady, not yet.”\n\nOne night, while having her hair undressed, my Lady loses herself in deep thought after this reply until she sees her own brooding face in the opposite glass, and a pair of black eyes curiously observing her.\n\n“Be so good as to attend,” says my Lady then, addressing the reflection of Hortense, “to your business. You can contemplate your beauty at another time.”\n\n“Pardon! It was your Ladyship’s beauty.”\n\n“That,” says my Lady, “you needn’t contemplate at all.”\n\nAt length, one afternoon a little before sunset, when the bright groups of figures which have for the last hour or two enlivened the Ghost’s Walk are all dispersed and only Sir Leicester and my Lady remain upon the terrace, Mr. Tulkinghorn appears. He comes towards them at his usual methodical pace, which is never quickened, never slackened. He wears his usual expressionless mask—if it be a mask—and carries family secrets in every limb of his body and every crease of his dress. Whether his whole soul is devoted to the great or whether he yields them nothing beyond the services he sells is his personal secret. He keeps it, as he keeps the secrets of his clients; he is his own client in that matter, and will never betray himself.\n\n“How do you do, Mr. Tulkinghorn?” says Sir Leicester, giving him his hand.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn is quite well. Sir Leicester is quite well. My Lady is quite well. All highly satisfactory. The lawyer, with his hands behind him, walks at Sir Leicester’s side along the terrace. My Lady walks upon the other side.\n\n“We expected you before,” says Sir Leicester. A gracious observation. As much as to say, “Mr. Tulkinghorn, we remember your existence when you are not here to remind us of it by your presence. We bestow a fragment of our minds upon you, sir, you see!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn, comprehending it, inclines his head and says he is much obliged.\n\n“I should have come down sooner,” he explains, “but that I have been much engaged with those matters in the several suits between yourself and Boythorn.”\n\n“A man of a very ill-regulated mind,” observes Sir Leicester with severity. “An extremely dangerous person in any community. A man of a very low character of mind.”\n\n“He is obstinate,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\n“It is natural to such a man to be so,” says Sir Leicester, looking most profoundly obstinate himself. “I am not at all surprised to hear it.”\n\n“The only question is,” pursues the lawyer, “whether you will give up anything.”\n\n“No, sir,” replies Sir Leicester. “Nothing. I give up?”\n\n“I don’t mean anything of importance. That, of course, I know you would not abandon. I mean any minor point.”\n\n“Mr. Tulkinghorn,” returns Sir Leicester, “there can be no minor point between myself and Mr. Boythorn. If I go farther, and observe that I cannot readily conceive how ANY right of mine can be a minor point, I speak not so much in reference to myself as an individual as in reference to the family position I have it in charge to maintain.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn inclines his head again. “I have now my instructions,” he says. “Mr. Boythorn will give us a good deal of trouble—”\n\n“It is the character of such a mind, Mr. Tulkinghorn,” Sir Leicester interrupts him, “TO give trouble. An exceedingly ill-conditioned, levelling person. A person who, fifty years ago, would probably have been tried at the Old Bailey for some demagogue proceeding, and severely punished—if not,” adds Sir Leicester after a moment’s pause, “if not hanged, drawn, and quartered.”\n\nSir Leicester appears to discharge his stately breast of a burden in passing this capital sentence, as if it were the next satisfactory thing to having the sentence executed.\n\n“But night is coming on,” says he, “and my Lady will take cold. My dear, let us go in.”\n\nAs they turn towards the hall-door, Lady Dedlock addresses Mr. Tulkinghorn for the first time.\n\n“You sent me a message respecting the person whose writing I happened to inquire about. It was like you to remember the circumstance; I had quite forgotten it. Your message reminded me of it again. I can’t imagine what association I had with a hand like that, but I surely had some.”\n\n“You had some?” Mr. Tulkinghorn repeats.\n\n“Oh, yes!” returns my Lady carelessly. “I think I must have had some. And did you really take the trouble to find out the writer of that actual thing—what is it!—affidavit?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“How very odd!”\n\nThey pass into a sombre breakfast-room on the ground floor, lighted in the day by two deep windows. It is now twilight. The fire glows brightly on the panelled wall and palely on the window-glass, where, through the cold reflection of the blaze, the colder landscape shudders in the wind and a grey mist creeps along, the only traveller besides the waste of clouds.\n\nMy Lady lounges in a great chair in the chimney-corner, and Sir Leicester takes another great chair opposite. The lawyer stands before the fire with his hand out at arm’s length, shading his face. He looks across his arm at my Lady.\n\n“Yes,” he says, “I inquired about the man, and found him. And, what is very strange, I found him—”\n\n“Not to be any out-of-the-way person, I am afraid!” Lady Dedlock languidly anticipates.\n\n“I found him dead.”\n\n“Oh, dear me!” remonstrated Sir Leicester. Not so much shocked by the fact as by the fact of the fact being mentioned.\n\n“I was directed to his lodging—a miserable, poverty-stricken place—and I found him dead.”\n\n“You will excuse me, Mr. Tulkinghorn,” observes Sir Leicester. “I think the less said—”\n\n“Pray, Sir Leicester, let me hear the story out” (it is my Lady speaking). “It is quite a story for twilight. How very shocking! Dead?”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn re-asserts it by another inclination of his head. “Whether by his own hand—”\n\n“Upon my honour!” cries Sir Leicester. “Really!”\n\n“Do let me hear the story!” says my Lady.\n\n“Whatever you desire, my dear. But, I must say—”\n\n“No, you mustn’t say! Go on, Mr. Tulkinghorn.”\n\nSir Leicester’s gallantry concedes the point, though he still feels that to bring this sort of squalor among the upper classes is really—really—\n\n“I was about to say,” resumes the lawyer with undisturbed calmness, “that whether he had died by his own hand or not, it was beyond my power to tell you. I should amend that phrase, however, by saying that he had unquestionably died of his own act, though whether by his own deliberate intention or by mischance can never certainly be known. The coroner’s jury found that he took the poison accidentally.”\n\n“And what kind of man,” my Lady asks, “was this deplorable creature?”\n\n“Very difficult to say,” returns the lawyer, shaking his head. “He had lived so wretchedly and was so neglected, with his gipsy colour and his wild black hair and beard, that I should have considered him the commonest of the common. The surgeon had a notion that he had once been something better, both in appearance and condition.”\n\n“What did they call the wretched being?”\n\n“They called him what he had called himself, but no one knew his name.”\n\n“Not even any one who had attended on him?”\n\n“No one had attended on him. He was found dead. In fact, I found him.”\n\n“Without any clue to anything more?”\n\n“Without any; there was,” says the lawyer meditatively, “an old portmanteau, but—No, there were no papers.”\n\nDuring the utterance of every word of this short dialogue, Lady Dedlock and Mr. Tulkinghorn, without any other alteration in their customary deportment, have looked very steadily at one another—as was natural, perhaps, in the discussion of so unusual a subject. Sir Leicester has looked at the fire, with the general expression of the Dedlock on the staircase. The story being told, he renews his stately protest, saying that as it is quite clear that no association in my Lady’s mind can possibly be traceable to this poor wretch (unless he was a begging-letter writer), he trusts to hear no more about a subject so far removed from my Lady’s station.\n\n“Certainly, a collection of horrors,” says my Lady, gathering up her mantles and furs, “but they interest one for the moment! Have the kindness, Mr. Tulkinghorn, to open the door for me.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn does so with deference and holds it open while she passes out. She passes close to him, with her usual fatigued manner and insolent grace. They meet again at dinner—again, next day—again, for many days in succession. Lady Dedlock is always the same exhausted deity, surrounded by worshippers, and terribly liable to be bored to death, even while presiding at her own shrine. Mr. Tulkinghorn is always the same speechless repository of noble confidences, so oddly out of place and yet so perfectly at home. They appear to take as little note of one another as any two people enclosed within the same walls could. But whether each evermore watches and suspects the other, evermore mistrustful of some great reservation; whether each is evermore prepared at all points for the other, and never to be taken unawares; what each would give to know how much the other knows—all this is hidden, for the time, in their own hearts.\nEsther’s Narrative\n\nWe held many consultations about what Richard was to be, first without Mr. Jarndyce, as he had requested, and afterwards with him, but it was a long time before we seemed to make progress. Richard said he was ready for anything. When Mr. Jarndyce doubted whether he might not already be too old to enter the Navy, Richard said he had thought of that, and perhaps he was. When Mr. Jarndyce asked him what he thought of the Army, Richard said he had thought of that, too, and it wasn’t a bad idea. When Mr. Jarndyce advised him to try and decide within himself whether his old preference for the sea was an ordinary boyish inclination or a strong impulse, Richard answered, Well he really HAD tried very often, and he couldn’t make out.\n\n“How much of this indecision of character,” Mr. Jarndyce said to me, “is chargeable on that incomprehensible heap of uncertainty and procrastination on which he has been thrown from his birth, I don’t pretend to say; but that Chancery, among its other sins, is responsible for some of it, I can plainly see. It has engendered or confirmed in him a habit of putting off—and trusting to this, that, and the other chance, without knowing what chance—and dismissing everything as unsettled, uncertain, and confused. The character of much older and steadier people may be even changed by the circumstances surrounding them. It would be too much to expect that a boy’s, in its formation, should be the subject of such influences and escape them.”\n\nI felt this to be true; though if I may venture to mention what I thought besides, I thought it much to be regretted that Richard’s education had not counteracted those influences or directed his character. He had been eight years at a public school and had learnt, I understood, to make Latin verses of several sorts in the most admirable manner. But I never heard that it had been anybody’s business to find out what his natural bent was, or where his failings lay, or to adapt any kind of knowledge to HIM. HE had been adapted to the verses and had learnt the art of making them to such perfection that if he had remained at school until he was of age, I suppose he could only have gone on making them over and over again unless he had enlarged his education by forgetting how to do it. Still, although I had no doubt that they were very beautiful, and very improving, and very sufficient for a great many purposes of life, and always remembered all through life, I did doubt whether Richard would not have profited by some one studying him a little, instead of his studying them quite so much.\n\nTo be sure, I knew nothing of the subject and do not even now know whether the young gentlemen of classic Rome or Greece made verses to the same extent—or whether the young gentlemen of any country ever did.\n\n“I haven’t the least idea,” said Richard, musing, “what I had better be. Except that I am quite sure I don’t want to go into the Church, it’s a toss-up.”\n\n“You have no inclination in Mr. Kenge’s way?” suggested Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“I don’t know that, sir!” replied Richard. “I am fond of boating. Articled clerks go a good deal on the water. It’s a capital profession!”\n\n“Surgeon—” suggested Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“That’s the thing, sir!” cried Richard.\n\nI doubt if he had ever once thought of it before.\n\n“That’s the thing, sir,” repeated Richard with the greatest enthusiasm. “We have got it at last. M.R.C.S.!”\n\nHe was not to be laughed out of it, though he laughed at it heartily. He said he had chosen his profession, and the more he thought of it, the more he felt that his destiny was clear; the art of healing was the art of all others for him. Mistrusting that he only came to this conclusion because, having never had much chance of finding out for himself what he was fitted for and having never been guided to the discovery, he was taken by the newest idea and was glad to get rid of the trouble of consideration, I wondered whether the Latin verses often ended in this or whether Richard’s was a solitary case.\n\nMr. Jarndyce took great pains to talk with him seriously and to put it to his good sense not to deceive himself in so important a matter. Richard was a little grave after these interviews, but invariably told Ada and me that it was all right, and then began to talk about something else.\n\n“By heaven!” cried Mr. Boythorn, who interested himself strongly in the subject—though I need not say that, for he could do nothing weakly; “I rejoice to find a young gentleman of spirit and gallantry devoting himself to that noble profession! The more spirit there is in it, the better for mankind and the worse for those mercenary task-masters and low tricksters who delight in putting that illustrious art at a disadvantage in the world. By all that is base and despicable,” cried Mr. Boythorn, “the treatment of surgeons aboard ship is such that I would submit the legs—both legs—of every member of the Admiralty Board to a compound fracture and render it a transportable offence in any qualified practitioner to set them if the system were not wholly changed in eight and forty hours!”\n\n“Wouldn’t you give them a week?” asked Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“No!” cried Mr. Boythorn firmly. “Not on any consideration! Eight and forty hours! As to corporations, parishes, vestry-boards, and similar gatherings of jolter-headed clods who assemble to exchange such speeches that, by heaven, they ought to be worked in quicksilver mines for the short remainder of their miserable existence, if it were only to prevent their detestable English from contaminating a language spoken in the presence of the sun—as to those fellows, who meanly take advantage of the ardour of gentlemen in the pursuit of knowledge to recompense the inestimable services of the best years of their lives, their long study, and their expensive education with pittances too small for the acceptance of clerks, I would have the necks of every one of them wrung and their skulls arranged in Surgeons’ Hall for the contemplation of the whole profession in order that its younger members might understand from actual measurement, in early life, HOW thick skulls may become!”\n\nHe wound up this vehement declaration by looking round upon us with a most agreeable smile and suddenly thundering, “Ha, ha, ha!” over and over again, until anybody else might have been expected to be quite subdued by the exertion.\n\nAs Richard still continued to say that he was fixed in his choice after repeated periods for consideration had been recommended by Mr. Jarndyce and had expired, and he still continued to assure Ada and me in the same final manner that it was “all right,” it became advisable to take Mr. Kenge into council. Mr. Kenge, therefore, came down to dinner one day, and leaned back in his chair, and turned his eye-glasses over and over, and spoke in a sonorous voice, and did exactly what I remembered to have seen him do when I was a little girl.\n\n“Ah!” said Mr. Kenge. “Yes. Well! A very good profession, Mr. Jarndyce, a very good profession.”\n\n“The course of study and preparation requires to be diligently pursued,” observed my guardian with a glance at Richard.\n\n“Oh, no doubt,” said Mr. Kenge. “Diligently.”\n\n“But that being the case, more or less, with all pursuits that are worth much,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “it is not a special consideration which another choice would be likely to escape.”\n\n“Truly,” said Mr. Kenge. “And Mr. Richard Carstone, who has so meritoriously acquitted himself in the—shall I say the classic shades?—in which his youth had been passed, will, no doubt, apply the habits, if not the principles and practice, of versification in that tongue in which a poet was said (unless I mistake) to be born, not made, to the more eminently practical field of action on which he enters.”\n\n“You may rely upon it,” said Richard in his off-hand manner, “that I shall go at it and do my best.”\n\n“Very well, Mr. Jarndyce!” said Mr. Kenge, gently nodding his head. “Really, when we are assured by Mr. Richard that he means to go at it and to do his best,” nodding feelingly and smoothly over those expressions, “I would submit to you that we have only to inquire into the best mode of carrying out the object of his ambition. Now, with reference to placing Mr. Richard with some sufficiently eminent practitioner. Is there any one in view at present?”\n\n“No one, Rick, I think?” said my guardian.\n\n“No one, sir,” said Richard.\n\n“Quite so!” observed Mr. Kenge. “As to situation, now. Is there any particular feeling on that head?”\n\n“N—no,” said Richard.\n\n“Quite so!” observed Mr. Kenge again.\n\n“I should like a little variety,” said Richard; “I mean a good range of experience.”\n\n“Very requisite, no doubt,” returned Mr. Kenge. “I think this may be easily arranged, Mr. Jarndyce? We have only, in the first place, to discover a sufficiently eligible practitioner; and as soon as we make our want—and shall I add, our ability to pay a premium?—known, our only difficulty will be in the selection of one from a large number. We have only, in the second place, to observe those little formalities which are rendered necessary by our time of life and our being under the guardianship of the court. We shall soon be—shall I say, in Mr. Richard’s own light-hearted manner, ‘going at it’—to our heart’s content. It is a coincidence,” said Mr. Kenge with a tinge of melancholy in his smile, “one of those coincidences which may or may not require an explanation beyond our present limited faculties, that I have a cousin in the medical profession. He might be deemed eligible by you and might be disposed to respond to this proposal. I can answer for him as little as for you, but he MIGHT!”\n\nAs this was an opening in the prospect, it was arranged that Mr. Kenge should see his cousin. And as Mr. Jarndyce had before proposed to take us to London for a few weeks, it was settled next day that we should make our visit at once and combine Richard’s business with it.\n\nMr. Boythorn leaving us within a week, we took up our abode at a cheerful lodging near Oxford Street over an upholsterer’s shop. London was a great wonder to us, and we were out for hours and hours at a time, seeing the sights, which appeared to be less capable of exhaustion than we were. We made the round of the principal theatres, too, with great delight, and saw all the plays that were worth seeing. I mention this because it was at the theatre that I began to be made uncomfortable again by Mr. Guppy.\n\nI was sitting in front of the box one night with Ada, and Richard was in the place he liked best, behind Ada’s chair, when, happening to look down into the pit, I saw Mr. Guppy, with his hair flattened down upon his head and woe depicted in his face, looking up at me. I felt all through the performance that he never looked at the actors but constantly looked at me, and always with a carefully prepared expression of the deepest misery and the profoundest dejection.\n\nIt quite spoiled my pleasure for that night because it was so very embarrassing and so very ridiculous. But from that time forth, we never went to the play without my seeing Mr. Guppy in the pit, always with his hair straight and flat, his shirt-collar turned down, and a general feebleness about him. If he were not there when we went in, and I began to hope he would not come and yielded myself for a little while to the interest of the scene, I was certain to encounter his languishing eyes when I least expected it and, from that time, to be quite sure that they were fixed upon me all the evening.\n\nI really cannot express how uneasy this made me. If he would only have brushed up his hair or turned up his collar, it would have been bad enough; but to know that that absurd figure was always gazing at me, and always in that demonstrative state of despondency, put such a constraint upon me that I did not like to laugh at the play, or to cry at it, or to move, or to speak. I seemed able to do nothing naturally. As to escaping Mr. Guppy by going to the back of the box, I could not bear to do that because I knew Richard and Ada relied on having me next them and that they could never have talked together so happily if anybody else had been in my place. So there I sat, not knowing where to look—for wherever I looked, I knew Mr. Guppy’s eyes were following me—and thinking of the dreadful expense to which this young man was putting himself on my account.\n\nSometimes I thought of telling Mr. Jarndyce. Then I feared that the young man would lose his situation and that I might ruin him. Sometimes I thought of confiding in Richard, but was deterred by the possibility of his fighting Mr. Guppy and giving him black eyes. Sometimes I thought, should I frown at him or shake my head. Then I felt I could not do it. Sometimes I considered whether I should write to his mother, but that ended in my being convinced that to open a correspondence would be to make the matter worse. I always came to the conclusion, finally, that I could do nothing. Mr. Guppy’s perseverance, all this time, not only produced him regularly at any theatre to which we went, but caused him to appear in the crowd as we were coming out, and even to get up behind our fly—where I am sure I saw him, two or three times, struggling among the most dreadful spikes. After we got home, he haunted a post opposite our house. The upholsterer’s where we lodged being at the corner of two streets, and my bedroom window being opposite the post, I was afraid to go near the window when I went upstairs, lest I should see him (as I did one moonlight night) leaning against the post and evidently catching cold. If Mr. Guppy had not been, fortunately for me, engaged in the daytime, I really should have had no rest from him.\n\nWhile we were making this round of gaieties, in which Mr. Guppy so extraordinarily participated, the business which had helped to bring us to town was not neglected. Mr. Kenge’s cousin was a Mr. Bayham Badger, who had a good practice at Chelsea and attended a large public institution besides. He was quite willing to receive Richard into his house and to superintend his studies, and as it seemed that those could be pursued advantageously under Mr. Badger’s roof, and Mr. Badger liked Richard, and as Richard said he liked Mr. Badger “well enough,” an agreement was made, the Lord Chancellor’s consent was obtained, and it was all settled.\n\nOn the day when matters were concluded between Richard and Mr. Badger, we were all under engagement to dine at Mr. Badger’s house. We were to be “merely a family party,” Mrs. Badger’s note said; and we found no lady there but Mrs. Badger herself. She was surrounded in the drawing-room by various objects, indicative of her painting a little, playing the piano a little, playing the guitar a little, playing the harp a little, singing a little, working a little, reading a little, writing poetry a little, and botanizing a little. She was a lady of about fifty, I should think, youthfully dressed, and of a very fine complexion. If I add to the little list of her accomplishments that she rouged a little, I do not mean that there was any harm in it.\n\nMr. Bayham Badger himself was a pink, fresh-faced, crisp-looking gentleman with a weak voice, white teeth, light hair, and surprised eyes, some years younger, I should say, than Mrs. Bayham Badger. He admired her exceedingly, but principally, and to begin with, on the curious ground (as it seemed to us) of her having had three husbands. We had barely taken our seats when he said to Mr. Jarndyce quite triumphantly, “You would hardly suppose that I am Mrs. Bayham Badger’s third!”\n\n“Indeed?” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Her third!” said Mr. Badger. “Mrs. Bayham Badger has not the appearance, Miss Summerson, of a lady who has had two former husbands?”\nI said “Not at all!”\n\n“And most remarkable men!” said Mr. Badger in a tone of confidence. “Captain Swosser of the Royal Navy, who was Mrs. Badger’s first husband, was a very distinguished officer indeed. The name of Professor Dingo, my immediate predecessor, is one of European reputation.”\n\nMrs. Badger overheard him and smiled.\n\n“Yes, my dear!” Mr. Badger replied to the smile, “I was observing to Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson that you had had two former husbands—both very distinguished men. And they found it, as people generally do, difficult to believe.”\n\n“I was barely twenty,” said Mrs. Badger, “when I married Captain Swosser of the Royal Navy. I was in the Mediterranean with him; I am quite a sailor. On the twelfth anniversary of my wedding-day, I became the wife of Professor Dingo.”\n\n“Of European reputation,” added Mr. Badger in an undertone.\n\n“And when Mr. Badger and myself were married,” pursued Mrs. Badger, “we were married on the same day of the year. I had become attached to the day.”\n\n“So that Mrs. Badger has been married to three husbands—two of them highly distinguished men,” said Mr. Badger, summing up the facts, “and each time upon the twenty-first of March at eleven in the forenoon!”\n\nWe all expressed our admiration.\n\n“But for Mr. Badger’s modesty,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “I would take leave to correct him and say three distinguished men.”\n\n“Thank you, Mr. Jarndyce! What I always tell him!” observed Mrs. Badger.\n\n“And, my dear,” said Mr. Badger, “what do I always tell you? That without any affectation of disparaging such professional distinction as I may have attained (which our friend Mr. Carstone will have many opportunities of estimating), I am not so weak—no, really,” said Mr. Badger to us generally, “so unreasonable—as to put my reputation on the same footing with such first-rate men as Captain Swosser and Professor Dingo. Perhaps you may be interested, Mr. Jarndyce,” continued Mr. Bayham Badger, leading the way into the next drawing-room, “in this portrait of Captain Swosser. It was taken on his return home from the African station, where he had suffered from the fever of the country. Mrs. Badger considers it too yellow. But it’s a very fine head. A very fine head!”\n\nWe all echoed, “A very fine head!”\n\n“I feel when I look at it,” said Mr. Badger, “‘that’s a man I should like to have seen!’ It strikingly bespeaks the first-class man that Captain Swosser pre-eminently was. On the other side, Professor Dingo. I knew him well—attended him in his last illness—a speaking likeness! Over the piano, Mrs. Bayham Badger when Mrs. Swosser. Over the sofa, Mrs. Bayham Badger when Mrs. Dingo. Of Mrs. Bayham Badger IN ESSE, I possess the original and have no copy.”\n\nDinner was now announced, and we went downstairs. It was a very genteel entertainment, very handsomely served. But the captain and the professor still ran in Mr. Badger’s head, and as Ada and I had the honour of being under his particular care, we had the full benefit of them.\n\n“Water, Miss Summerson? Allow me! Not in that tumbler, pray. Bring me the professor’s goblet, James!”\n\nAda very much admired some artificial flowers under a glass.\n\n“Astonishing how they keep!” said Mr. Badger. “They were presented to Mrs. Bayham Badger when she was in the Mediterranean.”\n\nHe invited Mr. Jarndyce to take a glass of claret.\n\n“Not that claret!” he said. “Excuse me! This is an occasion, and ON an occasion I produce some very special claret I happen to have. (James, Captain Swosser’s wine!) Mr. Jarndyce, this is a wine that was imported by the captain, we will not say how many years ago. You will find it very curious. My dear, I shall be happy to take some of this wine with you. (Captain Swosser’s claret to your mistress, James!) My love, your health!”\n\nAfter dinner, when we ladies retired, we took Mrs. Badger’s first and second husband with us. Mrs. Badger gave us in the drawing-room a biographical sketch of the life and services of Captain Swosser before his marriage and a more minute account of him dating from the time when he fell in love with her at a ball on board the Crippler, given to the officers of that ship when she lay in Plymouth Harbour.\n\n“The dear old Crippler!” said Mrs. Badger, shaking her head. “She was a noble vessel. Trim, ship-shape, all a taunto, as Captain Swosser used to say. You must excuse me if I occasionally introduce a nautical expression; I was quite a sailor once. Captain Swosser loved that craft for my sake. When she was no longer in commission, he frequently said that if he were rich enough to buy her old hulk, he would have an inscription let into the timbers of the quarter-deck where we stood as partners in the dance to mark the spot where he fell—raked fore and aft (Captain Swosser used to say) by the fire from my tops. It was his naval way of mentioning my eyes.”\n\nMrs. Badger shook her head, sighed, and looked in the glass.\n\n“It was a great change from Captain Swosser to Professor Dingo,” she resumed with a plaintive smile. “I felt it a good deal at first. Such an entire revolution in my mode of life! But custom, combined with science—particularly science—inured me to it. Being the professor’s sole companion in his botanical excursions, I almost forgot that I had ever been afloat, and became quite learned. It is singular that the professor was the antipodes of Captain Swosser and that Mr. Badger is not in the least like either!”\n\nWe then passed into a narrative of the deaths of Captain Swosser and Professor Dingo, both of whom seem to have had very bad complaints. In the course of it, Mrs. Badger signified to us that she had never madly loved but once and that the object of that wild affection, never to be recalled in its fresh enthusiasm, was Captain Swosser. The professor was yet dying by inches in the most dismal manner, and Mrs. Badger was giving us imitations of his way of saying, with great difficulty, “Where is Laura? Let Laura give me my toast and water!” when the entrance of the gentlemen consigned him to the tomb.\n\nNow, I observed that evening, as I had observed for some days past, that Ada and Richard were more than ever attached to each other’s society, which was but natural, seeing that they were going to be separated so soon. I was therefore not very much surprised when we got home, and Ada and I retired upstairs, to find Ada more silent than usual, though I was not quite prepared for her coming into my arms and beginning to speak to me, with her face hidden.\n\n“My darling Esther!” murmured Ada. “I have a great secret to tell you!”\n\nA mighty secret, my pretty one, no doubt!\n\n“What is it, Ada?”\n\n“Oh, Esther, you would never guess!”\n\n“Shall I try to guess?” said I.\n\n“Oh, no! Don’t! Pray don’t!” cried Ada, very much startled by the idea of my doing so.\n\n“Now, I wonder who it can be about?” said I, pretending to consider.\n\n“It’s about—” said Ada in a whisper. “It’s about—my cousin Richard!”\n\n“Well, my own!” said I, kissing her bright hair, which was all I could see. “And what about him?”\n\n“Oh, Esther, you would never guess!”\n\nIt was so pretty to have her clinging to me in that way, hiding her face, and to know that she was not crying in sorrow but in a little glow of joy, and pride, and hope, that I would not help her just yet.\n\n“He says—I know it’s very foolish, we are both so young—but he says,” with a burst of tears, “that he loves me dearly, Esther.”\n\n“Does he indeed?” said I. “I never heard of such a thing! Why, my pet of pets, I could have told you that weeks and weeks ago!”\n\nTo see Ada lift up her flushed face in joyful surprise, and hold me round the neck, and laugh, and cry, and blush, was so pleasant!\n\n“Why, my darling,” said I, “what a goose you must take me for! Your cousin Richard has been loving you as plainly as he could for I don’t know how long!”\n\n“And yet you never said a word about it!” cried Ada, kissing me.\n\n“No, my love,” said I. “I waited to be told.”\n\n“But now I have told you, you don’t think it wrong of me, do you?” returned Ada. She might have coaxed me to say no if I had been the hardest-hearted duenna in the world. Not being that yet, I said no very freely.\n\n“And now,” said I, “I know the worst of it.”\n\n“Oh, that’s not quite the worst of it, Esther dear!” cried Ada, holding me tighter and laying down her face again upon my breast.\n\n“No?” said I. “Not even that?”\n\n“No, not even that!” said Ada, shaking her head.\n\n“Why, you never mean to say—” I was beginning in joke.\n\nBut Ada, looking up and smiling through her tears, cried, “Yes, I do! You know, you know I do!” And then sobbed out, “With all my heart I do! With all my whole heart, Esther!”\n\nI told her, laughing, why I had known that, too, just as well as I had known the other! And we sat before the fire, and I had all the talking to myself for a little while (though there was not much of it); and Ada was soon quiet and happy.\n\n“Do you think my cousin John knows, dear Dame Durden?” she asked.\n\n“Unless my cousin John is blind, my pet,” said I, “I should think my cousin John knows pretty well as much as we know.”\n\n“We want to speak to him before Richard goes,” said Ada timidly, “and we wanted you to advise us, and to tell him so. Perhaps you wouldn’t mind Richard’s coming in, Dame Durden?”\n\n“Oh! Richard is outside, is he, my dear?” said I.\n\n“I am not quite certain,” returned Ada with a bashful simplicity that would have won my heart if she had not won it long before, “but I think he’s waiting at the door.”\n\nThere he was, of course. They brought a chair on either side of me, and put me between them, and really seemed to have fallen in love with me instead of one another, they were so confiding, and so trustful, and so fond of me. They went on in their own wild way for a little while—I never stopped them; I enjoyed it too much myself—and then we gradually fell to considering how young they were, and how there must be a lapse of several years before this early love could come to anything, and how it could come to happiness only if it were real and lasting and inspired them with a steady resolution to do their duty to each other, with constancy, fortitude, and perseverance, each always for the other’s sake. Well! Richard said that he would work his fingers to the bone for Ada, and Ada said that she would work her fingers to the bone for Richard, and they called me all sorts of endearing and sensible names, and we sat there, advising and talking, half the night. Finally, before we parted, I gave them my promise to speak to their cousin John to-morrow.\n\nSo, when to-morrow came, I went to my guardian after breakfast, in the room that was our town-substitute for the growlery, and told him that I had it in trust to tell him something.\n\n“Well, little woman,” said he, shutting up his book, “if you have accepted the trust, there can be no harm in it.”\n\n“I hope not, guardian,” said I. “I can guarantee that there is no secrecy in it. For it only happened yesterday.”\n\n“Aye? And what is it, Esther?”\n\n“Guardian,” said I, “you remember the happy night when first we came down to Bleak House? When Ada was singing in the dark room?”\n\nI wished to call to his remembrance the look he had given me then. Unless I am much mistaken, I saw that I did so.\n\n“Because—” said I with a little hesitation.\n\n“Yes, my dear!” said he. “Don’t hurry.”\n\n“Because,” said I, “Ada and Richard have fallen in love. And have told each other so.”\n\n“Already!” cried my guardian, quite astonished.\n\n“Yes!” said I. “And to tell you the truth, guardian, I rather expected it.”\n\n“The deuce you did!” said he.\n\nHe sat considering for a minute or two, with his smile, at once so handsome and so kind, upon his changing face, and then requested me to let them know that he wished to see them. When they came, he encircled Ada with one arm in his fatherly way and addressed himself to Richard with a cheerful gravity.\n\n“Rick,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “I am glad to have won your confidence. I hope to preserve it. When I contemplated these relations between us four which have so brightened my life and so invested it with new interests and pleasures, I certainly did contemplate, afar off, the possibility of you and your pretty cousin here (don’t be shy, Ada, don’t be shy, my dear!) being in a mind to go through life together. I saw, and do see, many reasons to make it desirable. But that was afar off, Rick, afar off!”\n\n“We look afar off, sir,” returned Richard.\n\n“Well!” said Mr. Jarndyce. “That’s rational. Now, hear me, my dears! I might tell you that you don’t know your own minds yet, that a thousand things may happen to divert you from one another, that it is well this chain of flowers you have taken up is very easily broken, or it might become a chain of lead. But I will not do that. Such wisdom will come soon enough, I dare say, if it is to come at all. I will assume that a few years hence you will be in your hearts to one another what you are to-day. All I say before speaking to you according to that assumption is, if you DO change—if you DO come to find that you are more commonplace cousins to each other as man and woman than you were as boy and girl (your manhood will excuse me, Rick!)—don’t be ashamed still to confide in me, for there will be nothing monstrous or uncommon in it. I am only your friend and distant kinsman. I have no power over you whatever. But I wish and hope to retain your confidence if I do nothing to forfeit it.”\n\n“I am very sure, sir,” returned Richard, “that I speak for Ada too when I say that you have the strongest power over us both—rooted in respect, gratitude, and affection—strengthening every day.”\n\n“Dear cousin John,” said Ada, on his shoulder, “my father’s place can never be empty again. All the love and duty I could ever have rendered to him is transferred to you.”\n\n“Come!” said Mr. Jarndyce. “Now for our assumption. Now we lift our eyes up and look hopefully at the distance! Rick, the world is before you; and it is most probable that as you enter it, so it will receive you. Trust in nothing but in Providence and your own efforts. Never separate the two, like the heathen waggoner. Constancy in love is a good thing, but it means nothing, and is nothing, without constancy in every kind of effort. If you had the abilities of all the great men, past and present, you could do nothing well without sincerely meaning it and setting about it. If you entertain the supposition that any real success, in great things or in small, ever was or could be, ever will or can be, wrested from Fortune by fits and starts, leave that wrong idea here or leave your cousin Ada here.”\n\n“I will leave IT here, sir,” replied Richard smiling, “if I brought it here just now (but I hope I did not), and will work my way on to my cousin Ada in the hopeful distance.”\n\n“Right!” said Mr. Jarndyce. “If you are not to make her happy, why should you pursue her?”\n\n“I wouldn’t make her unhappy—no, not even for her love,” retorted Richard proudly.\n\n“Well said!” cried Mr. Jarndyce. “That’s well said! She remains here, in her home with me. Love her, Rick, in your active life, no less than in her home when you revisit it, and all will go well. Otherwise, all will go ill. That’s the end of my preaching. I think you and Ada had better take a walk.”\n\nAda tenderly embraced him, and Richard heartily shook hands with him, and then the cousins went out of the room, looking back again directly, though, to say that they would wait for me.\n\nThe door stood open, and we both followed them with our eyes as they passed down the adjoining room, on which the sun was shining, and out at its farther end. Richard with his head bent, and her hand drawn through his arm, was talking to her very earnestly; and she looked up in his face, listening, and seemed to see nothing else. So young, so beautiful, so full of hope and promise, they went on lightly through the sunlight as their own happy thoughts might then be traversing the years to come and making them all years of brightness. So they passed away into the shadow and were gone. It was only a burst of light that had been so radiant. The room darkened as they went out, and the sun was clouded over.\n\n“Am I right, Esther?” said my guardian when they were gone.\n\nHe was so good and wise to ask ME whether he was right!\n\n“Rick may gain, out of this, the quality he wants. Wants, at the core of so much that is good!” said Mr. Jarndyce, shaking his head. “I have said nothing to Ada, Esther. She has her friend and counsellor always near.” And he laid his hand lovingly upon my head.\n\nI could not help showing that I was a little moved, though I did all I could to conceal it.\n\n“Tut tut!” said he. “But we must take care, too, that our little woman’s life is not all consumed in care for others.”\n\n“Care? My dear guardian, I believe I am the happiest creature in the world!”\n\n“I believe so, too,” said he. “But some one may find out what Esther never will—that the little woman is to be held in remembrance above all other people!”\n\nI have omitted to mention in its place that there was some one else at the family dinner party. It was not a lady. It was a gentleman. It was a gentleman of a dark complexion—a young surgeon. He was rather reserved, but I thought him very sensible and agreeable. At least, Ada asked me if I did not, and I said yes.\n\nDeportment\nRichard left us on the very next evening to begin his new career, and committed Ada to my charge with great love for her and great trust in me. It touched me then to reflect, and it touches me now, more nearly, to remember (having what I have to tell) how they both thought of me, even at that engrossing time. I was a part of all their plans, for the present and the future. I was to write Richard once a week, making my faithful report of Ada, who was to write to him every alternate day. I was to be informed, under his own hand, of all his labours and successes; I was to observe how resolute and persevering he would be; I was to be Ada’s bridesmaid when they were married; I was to live with them afterwards; I was to keep all the keys of their house; I was to be made happy for ever and a day.\n\n“And if the suit SHOULD make us rich, Esther—which it may, you know!” said Richard to crown all.\nA shade crossed Ada’s face.\n\n“My dearest Ada,” asked Richard, “why not?”\n\n“It had better declare us poor at once,” said Ada.\n\n“Oh! I don’t know about that,” returned Richard, “but at all events, it won’t declare anything at once. It hasn’t declared anything in heaven knows how many years.”\n\n“Too true,” said Ada.\n\n“Yes, but,” urged Richard, answering what her look suggested rather than her words, “the longer it goes on, dear cousin, the nearer it must be to a settlement one way or other. Now, is not that reasonable?”\n\n“You know best, Richard. But I am afraid if we trust to it, it will make us unhappy.”\n\n“But, my Ada, we are not going to trust to it!” cried Richard gaily. “We know it better than to trust to it. We only say that if it SHOULD make us rich, we have no constitutional objection to being rich. The court is, by solemn settlement of law, our grim old guardian, and we are to suppose that what it gives us (when it gives us anything) is our right. It is not necessary to quarrel with our right.”\n\n“No,” said Ada, “but it may be better to forget all about it.”\n\n“Well, well,” cried Richard, “then we will forget all about it! We consign the whole thing to oblivion. Dame Durden puts on her approving face, and it’s done!”\n\n“Dame Durden’s approving face,” said I, looking out of the box in which I was packing his books, “was not very visible when you called it by that name; but it does approve, and she thinks you can’t do better.”\n\nSo, Richard said there was an end of it, and immediately began, on no other foundation, to build as many castles in the air as would man the Great Wall of China. He went away in high spirits. Ada and I, prepared to miss him very much, commenced our quieter career.\n\nOn our arrival in London, we had called with Mr. Jarndyce at Mrs. Jellyby’s but had not been so fortunate as to find her at home. It appeared that she had gone somewhere to a tea-drinking and had taken Miss Jellyby with her. Besides the tea-drinking, there was to be some considerable speech-making and letter-writing on the general merits of the cultivation of coffee, conjointly with natives, at the Settlement of Borrioboola-Gha. All this involved, no doubt, sufficient active exercise of pen and ink to make her daughter’s part in the proceedings anything but a holiday.\n\nIt being now beyond the time appointed for Mrs. Jellyby’s return, we called again. She was in town, but not at home, having gone to Mile End directly after breakfast on some Borrioboolan business, arising out of a society called the East London Branch Aid Ramification. As I had not seen Peepy on the occasion of our last call (when he was not to be found anywhere, and when the cook rather thought he must have strolled away with the dustman’s cart), I now inquired for him again. The oyster shells he had been building a house with were still in the passage, but he was nowhere discoverable, and the cook supposed that he had “gone after the sheep.” When we repeated, with some surprise, “The sheep?” she said, Oh, yes, on market days he sometimes followed them quite out of town and came back in such a state as never was!\n\nI was sitting at the window with my guardian on the following morning, and Ada was busy writing—of course to Richard—when Miss Jellyby was announced, and entered, leading the identical Peepy, whom she had made some endeavours to render presentable by wiping the dirt into corners of his face and hands and making his hair very wet and then violently frizzling it with her fingers. Everything the dear child wore was either too large for him or too small. Among his other contradictory decorations he had the hat of a bishop and the little gloves of a baby. His boots were, on a small scale, the boots of a ploughman, while his legs, so crossed and recrossed with scratches that they looked like maps, were bare below a very short pair of plaid drawers finished off with two frills of perfectly different patterns. The deficient buttons on his plaid frock had evidently been supplied from one of Mr. Jellyby’s coats, they were so extremely brazen and so much too large. Most extraordinary specimens of needlework appeared on several parts of his dress, where it had been hastily mended, and I recognized the same hand on Miss Jellyby’s. She was, however, unaccountably improved in her appearance and looked very pretty. She was conscious of poor little Peepy being but a failure after all her trouble, and she showed it as she came in by the way in which she glanced first at him and then at us.\n\n“Oh, dear me!” said my guardian. “Due east!”\n\nAda and I gave her a cordial welcome and presented her to Mr. Jarndyce, to whom she said as she sat down, “Ma’s compliments, and she hopes you’ll excuse her, because she’s correcting proofs of the plan. She’s going to put out five thousand new circulars, and she knows you’ll be interested to hear that. I have brought one of them with me. Ma’s compliments.” With which she presented it sulkily enough.\n\n“Thank you,” said my guardian. “I am much obliged to Mrs. Jellyby. Oh, dear me! This is a very trying wind!”\n\nWe were busy with Peepy, taking off his clerical hat, asking him if he remembered us, and so on. Peepy retired behind his elbow at first, but relented at the sight of sponge-cake and allowed me to take him on my lap, where he sat munching quietly. Mr. Jarndyce then withdrawing into the temporary growlery, Miss Jellyby opened a conversation with her usual abruptness.\n\n“We are going on just as bad as ever in Thavies Inn,” said she. “I have no peace of my life. Talk of Africa! I couldn’t be worse off if I was a what’s-his-name—man and a brother!”\n\nI tried to say something soothing.\n\n“Oh, it’s of no use, Miss Summerson,” exclaimed Miss Jellyby, “though I thank you for the kind intention all the same. I know how I am used, and I am not to be talked over. YOU wouldn’t be talked over if you were used so. Peepy, go and play at Wild Beasts under the piano!”\n\n“I shan’t!” said Peepy.\n\n“Very well, you ungrateful, naughty, hard-hearted boy!” returned Miss Jellyby with tears in her eyes. “I’ll never take pains to dress you any more.”\n\n“Yes, I will go, Caddy!” cried Peepy, who was really a good child and who was so moved by his sister’s vexation that he went at once.\n\n“It seems a little thing to cry about,” said poor Miss Jellyby apologetically, “but I am quite worn out. I was directing the new circulars till two this morning. I detest the whole thing so that that alone makes my head ache till I can’t see out of my eyes. And look at that poor unfortunate child! Was there ever such a fright as he is!”\n\nPeepy, happily unconscious of the defects in his appearance, sat on the carpet behind one of the legs of the piano, looking calmly out of his den at us while he ate his cake.\n\n“I have sent him to the other end of the room,” observed Miss Jellyby, drawing her chair nearer ours, “because I don’t want him to hear the conversation. Those little things are so sharp! I was going to say, we really are going on worse than ever. Pa will be a bankrupt before long, and then I hope Ma will be satisfied. There’ll be nobody but Ma to thank for it.”\n\nWe said we hoped Mr. Jellyby’s affairs were not in so bad a state as that.\n\n“It’s of no use hoping, though it’s very kind of you,” returned Miss Jellyby, shaking her head. “Pa told me only yesterday morning (and dreadfully unhappy he is) that he couldn’t weather the storm. I should be surprised if he could. When all our tradesmen send into our house any stuff they like, and the servants do what they like with it, and I have no time to improve things if I knew how, and Ma don’t care about anything, I should like to make out how Pa is to weather the storm. I declare if I was Pa, I’d run away.”\n\n“My dear!” said I, smiling. “Your papa, no doubt, considers his family.”\n\n“Oh, yes, his family is all very fine, Miss Summerson,” replied Miss Jellyby; “but what comfort is his family to him? His family is nothing but bills, dirt, waste, noise, tumbles downstairs, confusion, and wretchedness. His scrambling home, from week’s end to week’s end, is like one great washing-day—only nothing’s washed!”\n\nMiss Jellyby tapped her foot upon the floor and wiped her eyes.\n\n“I am sure I pity Pa to that degree,” she said, “and am so angry with Ma that I can’t find words to express myself! However, I am not going to bear it, I am determined. I won’t be a slave all my life, and I won’t submit to be proposed to by Mr. Quale. A pretty thing, indeed, to marry a philanthropist. As if I hadn’t had enough of THAT!” said poor Miss Jellyby.\n\nI must confess that I could not help feeling rather angry with Mrs. Jellyby myself, seeing and hearing this neglected girl and knowing how much of bitterly satirical truth there was in what she said.\n\n“If it wasn’t that we had been intimate when you stopped at our house,” pursued Miss Jellyby, “I should have been ashamed to come here to-day, for I know what a figure I must seem to you two. But as it is, I made up my mind to call, especially as I am not likely to see you again the next time you come to town.”\n\nShe said this with such great significance that Ada and I glanced at one another, foreseeing something more.\n\n“No!” said Miss Jellyby, shaking her head. “Not at all likely! I know I may trust you two. I am sure you won’t betray me. I am engaged.”\n\n“Without their knowledge at home?” said I.\n\n“Why, good gracious me, Miss Summerson,” she returned, justifying herself in a fretful but not angry manner, “how can it be otherwise? You know what Ma is—and I needn’t make poor Pa more miserable by telling HIM.”\n\n“But would it not be adding to his unhappiness to marry without his knowledge or consent, my dear?” said I.\n\n“No,” said Miss Jellyby, softening. “I hope not. I should try to make him happy and comfortable when he came to see me, and Peepy and the others should take it in turns to come and stay with me, and they should have some care taken of them then.”\n\nThere was a good deal of affection in poor Caddy. She softened more and more while saying this and cried so much over the unwonted little home-picture she had raised in her mind that Peepy, in his cave under the piano, was touched, and turned himself over on his back with loud lamentations. It was not until I had brought him to kiss his sister, and had restored him to his place on my lap, and had shown him that Caddy was laughing (she laughed expressly for the purpose), that we could recall his peace of mind; even then it was for some time conditional on his taking us in turns by the chin and smoothing our faces all over with his hand. At last, as his spirits were not equal to the piano, we put him on a chair to look out of window; and Miss Jellyby, holding him by one leg, resumed her confidence.\n\n“It began in your coming to our house,” she said.\n\nWe naturally asked how.\n“I felt I was so awkward,” she replied, “that I made up my mind to be improved in that respect at all events and to learn to dance. I told Ma I was ashamed of myself, and I must be taught to dance. Ma looked at me in that provoking way of hers as if I wasn’t in sight, but I was quite determined to be taught to dance, and so I went to Mr. Turveydrop’s Academy in Newman Street.”\n\n“And was it there, my dear—” I began.\n\n“Yes, it was there,” said Caddy, “and I am engaged to Mr. Turveydrop. There are two Mr. Turveydrops, father and son. My Mr. Turveydrop is the son, of course. I only wish I had been better brought up and was likely to make him a better wife, for I am very fond of him.”\n\n“I am sorry to hear this,” said I, “I must confess.”\n\n“I don’t know why you should be sorry,” she retorted a little anxiously, “but I am engaged to Mr. Turveydrop, whether or no, and he is very fond of me. It’s a secret as yet, even on his side, because old Mr. Turveydrop has a share in the connexion and it might break his heart or give him some other shock if he was told of it abruptly. Old Mr. Turveydrop is a very gentlemanly man indeed—very gentlemanly.”\n\n“Does his wife know of it?” asked Ada.\n\n“Old Mr. Turveydrop’s wife, Miss Clare?” returned Miss Jellyby, opening her eyes. “There’s no such person. He is a widower.”\n\nWe were here interrupted by Peepy, whose leg had undergone so much on account of his sister’s unconsciously jerking it like a bell-rope whenever she was emphatic that the afflicted child now bemoaned his sufferings with a very low-spirited noise. As he appealed to me for compassion, and as I was only a listener, I undertook to hold him. Miss Jellyby proceeded, after begging Peepy’s pardon with a kiss and assuring him that she hadn’t meant to do it.\n\n“That’s the state of the case,” said Caddy. “If I ever blame myself, I still think it’s Ma’s fault. We are to be married whenever we can, and then I shall go to Pa at the office and write to Ma. It won’t much agitate Ma; I am only pen and ink to HER. One great comfort is,” said Caddy with a sob, “that I shall never hear of Africa after I am married. Young Mr. Turveydrop hates it for my sake, and if old Mr. Turveydrop knows there is such a place, it’s as much as he does.”\n\n“It was he who was very gentlemanly, I think!” said I.\n\n“Very gentlemanly indeed,” said Caddy. “He is celebrated almost everywhere for his deportment.”\n\n“Does he teach?” asked Ada.\n\n“No, he don’t teach anything in particular,” replied Caddy. “But his deportment is beautiful.”\n\nCaddy went on to say with considerable hesitation and reluctance that there was one thing more she wished us to know, and felt we ought to know, and which she hoped would not offend us. It was that she had improved her acquaintance with Miss Flite, the little crazy old lady, and that she frequently went there early in the morning and met her lover for a few minutes before breakfast—only for a few minutes. “I go there at other times,” said Caddy, “but Prince does not come then. Young Mr. Turveydrop’s name is Prince; I wish it wasn’t, because it sounds like a dog, but of course he didn’t christen himself. Old Mr. Turveydrop had him christened Prince in remembrance of the Prince Regent. Old Mr. Turveydrop adored the Prince Regent on account of his deportment. I hope you won’t think the worse of me for having made these little appointments at Miss Flite’s, where I first went with you, because I like the poor thing for her own sake and I believe she likes me. If you could see young Mr. Turveydrop, I am sure you would think well of him—at least, I am sure you couldn’t possibly think any ill of him. I am going there now for my lesson. I couldn’t ask you to go with me, Miss Summerson; but if you would,” said Caddy, who had said all this earnestly and tremblingly, “I should be very glad—very glad.”\n\nIt happened that we had arranged with my guardian to go to Miss Flite’s that day. We had told him of our former visit, and our account had interested him; but something had always happened to prevent our going there again. As I trusted that I might have sufficient influence with Miss Jellyby to prevent her taking any very rash step if I fully accepted the confidence she was so willing to place in me, poor girl, I proposed that she and I and Peepy should go to the academy and afterwards meet my guardian and Ada at Miss Flite’s, whose name I now learnt for the first time. This was on condition that Miss Jellyby and Peepy should come back with us to dinner. The last article of the agreement being joyfully acceded to by both, we smartened Peepy up a little with the assistance of a few pins, some soap and water, and a hair-brush, and went out, bending our steps towards Newman Street, which was very near.\n\nI found the academy established in a sufficiently dingy house at the corner of an archway, with busts in all the staircase windows. In the same house there were also established, as I gathered from the plates on the door, a drawing-master, a coal-merchant (there was, certainly, no room for his coals), and a lithographic artist. On the plate which, in size and situation, took precedence of all the rest, I read, MR. TURVEYDROP. The door was open, and the hall was blocked up by a grand piano, a harp, and several other musical instruments in cases, all in progress of removal, and all looking rakish in the daylight. Miss Jellyby informed me that the academy had been lent, last night, for a concert.\n\nWe went upstairs—it had been quite a fine house once, when it was anybody’s business to keep it clean and fresh, and nobody’s business to smoke in it all day—and into Mr. Turveydrop’s great room, which was built out into a mews at the back and was lighted by a skylight. It was a bare, resounding room smelling of stables, with cane forms along the walls, and the walls ornamented at regular intervals with painted lyres and little cut-glass branches for candles, which seemed to be shedding their old-fashioned drops as other branches might shed autumn leaves. Several young lady pupils, ranging from thirteen or fourteen years of age to two or three and twenty, were assembled; and I was looking among them for their instructor when Caddy, pinching my arm, repeated the ceremony of introduction. “Miss Summerson, Mr. Prince Turveydrop!”\n\nI curtsied to a little blue-eyed fair man of youthful appearance with flaxen hair parted in the middle and curling at the ends all round his head. He had a little fiddle, which we used to call at school a kit, under his left arm, and its little bow in the same hand. His little dancing-shoes were particularly diminutive, and he had a little innocent, feminine manner which not only appealed to me in an amiable way, but made this singular effect upon me, that I received the impression that he was like his mother and that his mother had not been much considered or well used.\n\n“I am very happy to see Miss Jellyby’s friend,” he said, bowing low to me. “I began to fear,” with timid tenderness, “as it was past the usual time, that Miss Jellyby was not coming.”\n\n“I beg you will have the goodness to attribute that to me, who have detained her, and to receive my excuses, sir,” said I.\n\n“Oh, dear!” said he.\n\n“And pray,” I entreated, “do not allow me to be the cause of any more delay.”\n\nWith that apology I withdrew to a seat between Peepy (who, being well used to it, had already climbed into a corner place) and an old lady of a censorious countenance whose two nieces were in the class and who was very indignant with Peepy’s boots. Prince Turveydrop then tinkled the strings of his kit with his fingers, and the young ladies stood up to dance. Just then there appeared from a side-door old Mr. Turveydrop, in the full lustre of his deportment.\n\nHe was a fat old gentleman with a false complexion, false teeth, false whiskers, and a wig. He had a fur collar, and he had a padded breast to his coat, which only wanted a star or a broad blue ribbon to be complete. He was pinched in, and swelled out, and got up, and strapped down, as much as he could possibly bear. He had such a neckcloth on (puffing his very eyes out of their natural shape), and his chin and even his ears so sunk into it, that it seemed as though he must inevitably double up if it were cast loose. He had under his arm a hat of great size and weight, shelving downward from the crown to the brim, and in his hand a pair of white gloves with which he flapped it as he stood poised on one leg in a high-shouldered, round-elbowed state of elegance not to be surpassed. He had a cane, he had an eye-glass, he had a snuff-box, he had rings, he had wristbands, he had everything but any touch of nature; he was not like youth, he was not like age, he was not like anything in the world but a model of deportment.\n\n“Father! A visitor. Miss Jellyby’s friend, Miss Summerson.”\n\n“Distinguished,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “by Miss Summerson’s presence.” As he bowed to me in that tight state, I almost believe I saw creases come into the whites of his eyes.\n\n“My father,” said the son, aside, to me with quite an affecting belief in him, “is a celebrated character. My father is greatly admired.”\n\n“Go on, Prince! Go on!” said Mr. Turveydrop, standing with his back to the fire and waving his gloves condescendingly. “Go on, my son!”\n\nAt this command, or by this gracious permission, the lesson went on. Prince Turveydrop sometimes played the kit, dancing; sometimes played the piano, standing; sometimes hummed the tune with what little breath he could spare, while he set a pupil right; always conscientiously moved with the least proficient through every step and every part of the figure; and never rested for an instant. His distinguished father did nothing whatever but stand before the fire, a model of deportment.\n\n“And he never does anything else,” said the old lady of the censorious countenance. “Yet would you believe that it’s HIS name on the door-plate?”\n\n“His son’s name is the same, you know,” said I.\n\n“He wouldn’t let his son have any name if he could take it from him,” returned the old lady. “Look at the son’s dress!” It certainly was plain—threadbare—almost shabby. “Yet the father must be garnished and tricked out,” said the old lady, “because of his deportment. I’d deport him! Transport him would be better!”\n\nI felt curious to know more concerning this person. I asked, “Does he give lessons in deportment now?”\n\n“Now!” returned the old lady shortly. “Never did.”\n\nAfter a moment’s consideration, I suggested that perhaps fencing had been his accomplishment.\n\n“I don’t believe he can fence at all, ma’am,” said the old lady.\n\nI looked surprised and inquisitive. The old lady, becoming more and more incensed against the master of deportment as she dwelt upon the subject, gave me some particulars of his career, with strong assurances that they were mildly stated.\n\nHe had married a meek little dancing-mistress, with a tolerable connexion (having never in his life before done anything but deport himself), and had worked her to death, or had, at the best, suffered her to work herself to death, to maintain him in those expenses which were indispensable to his position. At once to exhibit his deportment to the best models and to keep the best models constantly before himself, he had found it necessary to frequent all public places of fashionable and lounging resort, to be seen at Brighton and elsewhere at fashionable times, and to lead an idle life in the very best clothes. To enable him to do this, the affectionate little dancing-mistress had toiled and laboured and would have toiled and laboured to that hour if her strength had lasted so long. For the mainspring of the story was that in spite of the man’s absorbing selfishness, his wife (overpowered by his deportment) had, to the last, believed in him and had, on her death-bed, in the most moving terms, confided him to their son as one who had an inextinguishable claim upon him and whom he could never regard with too much pride and deference. The son, inheriting his mother’s belief, and having the deportment always before him, had lived and grown in the same faith, and now, at thirty years of age, worked for his father twelve hours a day and looked up to him with veneration on the old imaginary pinnacle.\n\n“The airs the fellow gives himself!” said my informant, shaking her head at old Mr. Turveydrop with speechless indignation as he drew on his tight gloves, of course unconscious of the homage she was rendering. “He fully believes he is one of the aristocracy! And he is so condescending to the son he so egregiously deludes that you might suppose him the most virtuous of parents. Oh!” said the old lady, apostrophizing him with infinite vehemence. “I could bite you!”\n\nI could not help being amused, though I heard the old lady out with feelings of real concern. It was difficult to doubt her with the father and son before me. What I might have thought of them without the old lady’s account, or what I might have thought of the old lady’s account without them, I cannot say. There was a fitness of things in the whole that carried conviction with it.\n\nMy eyes were yet wandering, from young Mr. Turveydrop working so hard, to old Mr. Turveydrop deporting himself so beautifully, when the latter came ambling up to me and entered into conversation.\n\nHe asked me, first of all, whether I conferred a charm and a distinction on London by residing in it? I did not think it necessary to reply that I was perfectly aware I should not do that, in any case, but merely told him where I did reside.\n\n“A lady so graceful and accomplished,” he said, kissing his right glove and afterwards extending it towards the pupils, “will look leniently on the deficiencies here. We do our best to polish—polish—polish!”\n\nHe sat down beside me, taking some pains to sit on the form, I thought, in imitation of the print of his illustrious model on the sofa. And really he did look very like it.\n\n“To polish—polish—polish!” he repeated, taking a pinch of snuff and gently fluttering his fingers. “But we are not, if I may say so to one formed to be graceful both by Nature and Art—” with the high-shouldered bow, which it seemed impossible for him to make without lifting up his eyebrows and shutting his eyes “—we are not what we used to be in point of deportment.”\n\n“Are we not, sir?” said I.\n\n“We have degenerated,” he returned, shaking his head, which he could do to a very limited extent in his cravat. “A levelling age is not favourable to deportment. It develops vulgarity. Perhaps I speak with some little partiality. It may not be for me to say that I have been called, for some years now, Gentleman Turveydrop, or that his Royal Highness the Prince Regent did me the honour to inquire, on my removing my hat as he drove out of the Pavilion at Brighton (that fine building), ‘Who is he? Who the devil is he? Why don’t I know him? Why hasn’t he thirty thousand a year?’ But these are little matters of anecdote—the general property, ma’am—still repeated occasionally among the upper classes.”\n\n“Indeed?” said I.\n\nHe replied with the high-shouldered bow. “Where what is left among us of deportment,” he added, “still lingers. England—alas, my country!—has degenerated very much, and is degenerating every day. She has not many gentlemen left. We are few. I see nothing to succeed us but a race of weavers.”\n\n“One might hope that the race of gentlemen would be perpetuated here,” said I.\n\n“You are very good.” He smiled with a high-shouldered bow again. “You flatter me. But, no—no! I have never been able to imbue my poor boy with that part of his art. Heaven forbid that I should disparage my dear child, but he has—no deportment.”\n\n“He appears to be an excellent master,” I observed.\n\n“Understand me, my dear madam, he IS an excellent master. All that can be acquired, he has acquired. All that can be imparted, he can impart. But there ARE things—” He took another pinch of snuff and made the bow again, as if to add, “This kind of thing, for instance.”\n\nI glanced towards the centre of the room, where Miss Jellyby’s lover, now engaged with single pupils, was undergoing greater drudgery than ever.\n\n“My amiable child,” murmured Mr. Turveydrop, adjusting his cravat.\n\n“Your son is indefatigable,” said I.\n\n“It is my reward,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “to hear you say so. In some respects, he treads in the footsteps of his sainted mother. She was a devoted creature. But wooman, lovely wooman,” said Mr. Turveydrop with very disagreeable gallantry, “what a sex you are!”\n\nI rose and joined Miss Jellyby, who was by this time putting on her bonnet. The time allotted to a lesson having fully elapsed, there was a general putting on of bonnets. When Miss Jellyby and the unfortunate Prince found an opportunity to become betrothed I don’t know, but they certainly found none on this occasion to exchange a dozen words.\n\n“My dear,” said Mr. Turveydrop benignly to his son, “do you know the hour?”\n\n“No, father.” The son had no watch. The father had a handsome gold one, which he pulled out with an air that was an example to mankind.\n\n“My son,” said he, “it’s two o’clock. Recollect your school at Kensington at three.”\n\n“That’s time enough for me, father,” said Prince. “I can take a morsel of dinner standing and be off.”\n\n“My dear boy,” returned his father, “you must be very quick. You will find the cold mutton on the table.”\n\n“Thank you, father. Are YOU off now, father?”\n\n“Yes, my dear. I suppose,” said Mr. Turveydrop, shutting his eyes and lifting up his shoulders with modest consciousness, “that I must show myself, as usual, about town.”\n\n“You had better dine out comfortably somewhere,” said his son.\n\n“My dear child, I intend to. I shall take my little meal, I think, at the French house, in the Opera Colonnade.”\n\n“That’s right. Good-bye, father!” said Prince, shaking hands.\n\n“Good-bye, my son. Bless you!”\n\nMr. Turveydrop said this in quite a pious manner, and it seemed to do his son good, who, in parting from him, was so pleased with him, so dutiful to him, and so proud of him that I almost felt as if it were an unkindness to the younger man not to be able to believe implicitly in the elder. The few moments that were occupied by Prince in taking leave of us (and particularly of one of us, as I saw, being in the secret), enhanced my favourable impression of his almost childish character. I felt a liking for him and a compassion for him as he put his little kit in his pocket—and with it his desire to stay a little while with Caddy—and went away good-humouredly to his cold mutton and his school at Kensington, that made me scarcely less irate with his father than the censorious old lady.\n\nThe father opened the room door for us and bowed us out in a manner, I must acknowledge, worthy of his shining original. In the same style he presently passed us on the other side of the street, on his way to the aristocratic part of the town, where he was going to show himself among the few other gentlemen left. For some moments, I was so lost in reconsidering what I had heard and seen in Newman Street that I was quite unable to talk to Caddy or even to fix my attention on what she said to me, especially when I began to inquire in my mind whether there were, or ever had been, any other gentlemen, not in the dancing profession, who lived and founded a reputation entirely on their deportment. This became so bewildering and suggested the possibility of so many Mr. Turveydrops that I said, “Esther, you must make up your mind to abandon this subject altogether and attend to Caddy.” I accordingly did so, and we chatted all the rest of the way to Lincoln’s Inn.\n\nCaddy told me that her lover’s education had been so neglected that it was not always easy to read his notes. She said if he were not so anxious about his spelling and took less pains to make it clear, he would do better; but he put so many unnecessary letters into short words that they sometimes quite lost their English appearance. “He does it with the best intention,” observed Caddy, “but it hasn’t the effect he means, poor fellow!” Caddy then went on to reason, how could he be expected to be a scholar when he had passed his whole life in the dancing-school and had done nothing but teach and fag, fag and teach, morning, noon, and night! And what did it matter? She could write letters enough for both, as she knew to her cost, and it was far better for him to be amiable than learned. “Besides, it’s not as if I was an accomplished girl who had any right to give herself airs,” said Caddy. “I know little enough, I am sure, thanks to Ma!”\n\n“There’s another thing I want to tell you, now we are alone,” continued Caddy, “which I should not have liked to mention unless you had seen Prince, Miss Summerson. You know what a house ours is. It’s of no use my trying to learn anything that it would be useful for Prince’s wife to know in OUR house. We live in such a state of muddle that it’s impossible, and I have only been more disheartened whenever I have tried. So I get a little practice with—who do you think? Poor Miss Flite! Early in the morning I help her to tidy her room and clean her birds, and I make her cup of coffee for her (of course she taught me), and I have learnt to make it so well that Prince says it’s the very best coffee he ever tasted, and would quite delight old Mr. Turveydrop, who is very particular indeed about his coffee. I can make little puddings too; and I know how to buy neck of mutton, and tea, and sugar, and butter, and a good many housekeeping things. I am not clever at my needle, yet,” said Caddy, glancing at the repairs on Peepy’s frock, “but perhaps I shall improve, and since I have been engaged to Prince and have been doing all this, I have felt better-tempered, I hope, and more forgiving to Ma. It rather put me out at first this morning to see you and Miss Clare looking so neat and pretty and to feel ashamed of Peepy and myself too, but on the whole I hope I am better-tempered than I was and more forgiving to Ma.”\n\nThe poor girl, trying so hard, said it from her heart, and touched mine. “Caddy, my love,” I replied, “I begin to have a great affection for you, and I hope we shall become friends.”\n\n“Oh, do you?” cried Caddy. “How happy that would make me!”\n\n“My dear Caddy,” said I, “let us be friends from this time, and let us often have a chat about these matters and try to find the right way through them.” Caddy was overjoyed. I said everything I could in my old-fashioned way to comfort and encourage her, and I would not have objected to old Mr. Turveydrop that day for any smaller consideration than a settlement on his daughter-in-law.\n\nBy this time we were come to Mr. Krook’s, whose private door stood open. There was a bill, pasted on the door-post, announcing a room to let on the second floor. It reminded Caddy to tell me as we proceeded upstairs that there had been a sudden death there and an inquest and that our little friend had been ill of the fright. The door and window of the vacant room being open, we looked in. It was the room with the dark door to which Miss Flite had secretly directed my attention when I was last in the house. A sad and desolate place it was, a gloomy, sorrowful place that gave me a strange sensation of mournfulness and even dread. “You look pale,” said Caddy when we came out, “and cold!” I felt as if the room had chilled me.\n\nWe had walked slowly while we were talking, and my guardian and Ada were here before us. We found them in Miss Flite’s garret. They were looking at the birds, while a medical gentleman who was so good as to attend Miss Flite with much solicitude and compassion spoke with her cheerfully by the fire.\n\n“I have finished my professional visit,” he said, coming forward. “Miss Flite is much better and may appear in court (as her mind is set upon it) to-morrow. She has been greatly missed there, I understand.”\n\nMiss Flite received the compliment with complacency and dropped a general curtsy to us.\n\n“Honoured, indeed,” said she, “by another visit from the wards in Jarndyce! Ve-ry happy to receive Jarndyce of Bleak House beneath my humble roof!” with a special curtsy. “Fitz-Jarndyce, my dear”—she had bestowed that name on Caddy, it appeared, and always called her by it—“a double welcome!”\n\n“Has she been very ill?” asked Mr. Jarndyce of the gentleman whom we had found in attendance on her. She answered for herself directly, though he had put the question in a whisper.\n\n“Oh, decidedly unwell! Oh, very unwell indeed,” she said confidentially. “Not pain, you know—trouble. Not bodily so much as nervous, nervous! The truth is,” in a subdued voice and trembling, “we have had death here. There was poison in the house. I am very susceptible to such horrid things. It frightened me. Only Mr. Woodcourt knows how much. My physician, Mr. Woodcourt!” with great stateliness. “The wards in Jarndyce—Jarndyce of Bleak House—Fitz-Jarndyce!”\n\n“Miss Flite,” said Mr. Woodcourt in a grave kind of voice, as if he were appealing to her while speaking to us, and laying his hand gently on her arm, “Miss Flite describes her illness with her usual accuracy. She was alarmed by an occurrence in the house which might have alarmed a stronger person, and was made ill by the distress and agitation. She brought me here in the first hurry of the discovery, though too late for me to be of any use to the unfortunate man. I have compensated myself for that disappointment by coming here since and being of some small use to her.”\n\n“The kindest physician in the college,” whispered Miss Flite to me. “I expect a judgment. On the day of judgment. And shall then confer estates.”\n\n“She will be as well in a day or two,” said Mr. Woodcourt, looking at her with an observant smile, “as she ever will be. In other words, quite well of course. Have you heard of her good fortune?”\n\n“Most extraordinary!” said Miss Flite, smiling brightly. “You never heard of such a thing, my dear! Every Saturday, Conversation Kenge or Guppy (clerk to Conversation K.) places in my hand a paper of shillings. Shillings. I assure you! Always the same number in the paper. Always one for every day in the week. Now you know, really! So well-timed, is it not? Ye-es! From whence do these papers come, you say? That is the great question. Naturally. Shall I tell you what I think? I think,” said Miss Flite, drawing herself back with a very shrewd look and shaking her right forefinger in a most significant manner, “that the Lord Chancellor, aware of the length of time during which the Great Seal has been open (for it has been open a long time!), forwards them. Until the judgment I expect is given. Now that’s very creditable, you know. To confess in that way that he IS a little slow for human life. So delicate! Attending court the other day—I attend it regularly, with my documents—I taxed him with it, and he almost confessed. That is, I smiled at him from my bench, and HE smiled at me from his bench. But it’s great good fortune, is it not? And Fitz-Jarndyce lays the money out for me to great advantage. Oh, I assure you to the greatest advantage!”\n\nI congratulated her (as she addressed herself to me) upon this fortunate addition to her income and wished her a long continuance of it. I did not speculate upon the source from which it came or wonder whose humanity was so considerate. My guardian stood before me, contemplating the birds, and I had no need to look beyond him.\n\n“And what do you call these little fellows, ma’am?” said he in his pleasant voice. “Have they any names?”\n\n“I can answer for Miss Flite that they have,” said I, “for she promised to tell us what they were. Ada remembers?”\n\nAda remembered very well.\n\n“Did I?” said Miss Flite. “Who’s that at my door? What are you listening at my door for, Krook?”\n\nThe old man of the house, pushing it open before him, appeared there with his fur cap in his hand and his cat at his heels.\n\n“I warn’t listening, Miss Flite,” he said, “I was going to give a rap with my knuckles, only you’re so quick!”\n\n“Make your cat go down. Drive her away!” the old lady angrily exclaimed.\n\n“Bah, bah! There ain’t no danger, gentlefolks,” said Mr. Krook, looking slowly and sharply from one to another until he had looked at all of us; “she’d never offer at the birds when I was here unless I told her to it.”\n\n“You will excuse my landlord,” said the old lady with a dignified air. “M, quite M! What do you want, Krook, when I have company?”\n\n“Hi!” said the old man. “You know I am the Chancellor.”\n\n“Well?” returned Miss Flite. “What of that?”\n\n“For the Chancellor,” said the old man with a chuckle, “not to be acquainted with a Jarndyce is queer, ain’t it, Miss Flite? Mightn’t I take the liberty? Your servant, sir. I know Jarndyce and Jarndyce a’most as well as you do, sir. I knowed old Squire Tom, sir. I never to my knowledge see you afore though, not even in court. Yet, I go there a mortal sight of times in the course of the year, taking one day with another.”\n\n“I never go there,” said Mr. Jarndyce (which he never did on any consideration). “I would sooner go—somewhere else.”\n\n“Would you though?” returned Krook, grinning. “You’re bearing hard upon my noble and learned brother in your meaning, sir, though perhaps it is but nat’ral in a Jarndyce. The burnt child, sir! What, you’re looking at my lodger’s birds, Mr. Jarndyce?” The old man had come by little and little into the room until he now touched my guardian with his elbow and looked close up into his face with his spectacled eyes. “It’s one of her strange ways that she’ll never tell the names of these birds if she can help it, though she named ’em all.” This was in a whisper. “Shall I run ’em over, Flite?” he asked aloud, winking at us and pointing at her as she turned away, affecting to sweep the grate.\n\n“If you like,” she answered hurriedly.\n\nThe old man, looking up at the cages after another look at us, went through the list.\n\n“Hope, Joy, Youth, Peace, Rest, Life, Dust, Ashes, Waste, Want, Ruin, Despair, Madness, Death, Cunning, Folly, Words, Wigs, Rags, Sheepskin, Plunder, Precedent, Jargon, Gammon, and Spinach. That’s the whole collection,” said the old man, “all cooped up together, by my noble and learned brother.”\n\n“This is a bitter wind!” muttered my guardian.\n\n“When my noble and learned brother gives his judgment, they’re to be let go free,” said Krook, winking at us again. “And then,” he added, whispering and grinning, “if that ever was to happen—which it won’t—the birds that have never been caged would kill ’em.”\n\n“If ever the wind was in the east,” said my guardian, pretending to look out of the window for a weathercock, “I think it’s there to-day!”\n\nWe found it very difficult to get away from the house. It was not Miss Flite who detained us; she was as reasonable a little creature in consulting the convenience of others as there possibly could be. It was Mr. Krook. He seemed unable to detach himself from Mr. Jarndyce. If he had been linked to him, he could hardly have attended him more closely. He proposed to show us his Court of Chancery and all the strange medley it contained; during the whole of our inspection (prolonged by himself) he kept close to Mr. Jarndyce and sometimes detained him under one pretence or other until we had passed on, as if he were tormented by an inclination to enter upon some secret subject which he could not make up his mind to approach. I cannot imagine a countenance and manner more singularly expressive of caution and indecision, and a perpetual impulse to do something he could not resolve to venture on, than Mr. Krook’s was that day. His watchfulness of my guardian was incessant. He rarely removed his eyes from his face. If he went on beside him, he observed him with the slyness of an old white fox. If he went before, he looked back. When we stood still, he got opposite to him, and drawing his hand across and across his open mouth with a curious expression of a sense of power, and turning up his eyes, and lowering his grey eyebrows until they appeared to be shut, seemed to scan every lineament of his face.\n\nAt last, having been (always attended by the cat) all over the house and having seen the whole stock of miscellaneous lumber, which was certainly curious, we came into the back part of the shop. Here on the head of an empty barrel stood on end were an ink-bottle, some old stumps of pens, and some dirty playbills; and against the wall were pasted several large printed alphabets in several plain hands.\n\n“What are you doing here?” asked my guardian.\n\n“Trying to learn myself to read and write,” said Krook.\n\n“And how do you get on?”\n\n“Slow. Bad,” returned the old man impatiently. “It’s hard at my time of life.”\n\n“It would be easier to be taught by some one,” said my guardian.\n\n“Aye, but they might teach me wrong!” returned the old man with a wonderfully suspicious flash of his eye. “I don’t know what I may have lost by not being learned afore. I wouldn’t like to lose anything by being learned wrong now.”\n\n“Wrong?” said my guardian with his good-humoured smile. “Who do you suppose would teach you wrong?”\n\n“I don’t know, Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House!” replied the old man, turning up his spectacles on his forehead and rubbing his hands. “I don’t suppose as anybody would, but I’d rather trust my own self than another!”\n\nThese answers and his manner were strange enough to cause my guardian to inquire of Mr. Woodcourt, as we all walked across Lincoln’s Inn together, whether Mr. Krook were really, as his lodger represented him, deranged. The young surgeon replied, no, he had seen no reason to think so. He was exceedingly distrustful, as ignorance usually was, and he was always more or less under the influence of raw gin, of which he drank great quantities and of which he and his back-shop, as we might have observed, smelt strongly; but he did not think him mad as yet.\n\nOn our way home, I so conciliated Peepy’s affections by buying him a windmill and two flour-sacks that he would suffer nobody else to take off his hat and gloves and would sit nowhere at dinner but at my side. Caddy sat upon the other side of me, next to Ada, to whom we imparted the whole history of the engagement as soon as we got back. We made much of Caddy, and Peepy too; and Caddy brightened exceedingly; and my guardian was as merry as we were; and we were all very happy indeed until Caddy went home at night in a hackney-coach, with Peepy fast asleep, but holding tight to the windmill.\n\nI have forgotten to mention—at least I have not mentioned—that Mr. Woodcourt was the same dark young surgeon whom we had met at Mr. Badger’s. Or that Mr. Jarndyce invited him to dinner that day. Or that he came. Or that when they were all gone and I said to Ada, “Now, my darling, let us have a little talk about Richard!” Ada laughed and said—\n\nBut I don’t think it matters what my darling said. She was always merry.\nBell Yard\n\nWhile we were in London Mr. Jarndyce was constantly beset by the crowd of excitable ladies and gentlemen whose proceedings had so much astonished us. Mr. Quale, who presented himself soon after our arrival, was in all such excitements. He seemed to project those two shining knobs of temples of his into everything that went on and to brush his hair farther and farther back, until the very roots were almost ready to fly out of his head in inappeasable philanthropy. All objects were alike to him, but he was always particularly ready for anything in the way of a testimonial to any one. His great power seemed to be his power of indiscriminate admiration. He would sit for any length of time, with the utmost enjoyment, bathing his temples in the light of any order of luminary. Having first seen him perfectly swallowed up in admiration of Mrs. Jellyby, I had supposed her to be the absorbing object of his devotion. I soon discovered my mistake and found him to be train-bearer and organ-blower to a whole procession of people.\n\nMrs. Pardiggle came one day for a subscription to something, and with her, Mr. Quale. Whatever Mrs. Pardiggle said, Mr. Quale repeated to us; and just as he had drawn Mrs. Jellyby out, he drew Mrs. Pardiggle out. Mrs. Pardiggle wrote a letter of introduction to my guardian in behalf of her eloquent friend Mr. Gusher. With Mr. Gusher appeared Mr. Quale again. Mr. Gusher, being a flabby gentleman with a moist surface and eyes so much too small for his moon of a face that they seemed to have been originally made for somebody else, was not at first sight prepossessing; yet he was scarcely seated before Mr. Quale asked Ada and me, not inaudibly, whether he was not a great creature—which he certainly was, flabbily speaking, though Mr. Quale meant in intellectual beauty—and whether we were not struck by his massive configuration of brow. In short, we heard of a great many missions of various sorts among this set of people, but nothing respecting them was half so clear to us as that it was Mr. Quale’s mission to be in ecstasies with everybody else’s mission and that it was the most popular mission of all.\n\nMr. Jarndyce had fallen into this company in the tenderness of his heart and his earnest desire to do all the good in his power; but that he felt it to be too often an unsatisfactory company, where benevolence took spasmodic forms, where charity was assumed as a regular uniform by loud professors and speculators in cheap notoriety, vehement in profession, restless and vain in action, servile in the last degree of meanness to the great, adulatory of one another, and intolerable to those who were anxious quietly to help the weak from failing rather than with a great deal of bluster and self-laudation to raise them up a little way when they were down, he plainly told us. When a testimonial was originated to Mr. Quale by Mr. Gusher (who had already got one, originated by Mr. Quale), and when Mr. Gusher spoke for an hour and a half on the subject to a meeting, including two charity schools of small boys and girls, who were specially reminded of the widow’s mite, and requested to come forward with halfpence and be acceptable sacrifices, I think the wind was in the east for three whole weeks.\n\nI mention this because I am coming to Mr. Skimpole again. It seemed to me that his off-hand professions of childishness and carelessness were a great relief to my guardian, by contrast with such things, and were the more readily believed in since to find one perfectly undesigning and candid man among many opposites could not fail to give him pleasure. I should be sorry to imply that Mr. Skimpole divined this and was politic; I really never understood him well enough to know. What he was to my guardian, he certainly was to the rest of the world.\n\nHe had not been very well; and thus, though he lived in London, we had seen nothing of him until now. He appeared one morning in his usual agreeable way and as full of pleasant spirits as ever.\n\nWell, he said, here he was! He had been bilious, but rich men were often bilious, and therefore he had been persuading himself that he was a man of property. So he was, in a certain point of view—in his expansive intentions. He had been enriching his medical attendant in the most lavish manner. He had always doubled, and sometimes quadrupled, his fees. He had said to the doctor, “Now, my dear doctor, it is quite a delusion on your part to suppose that you attend me for nothing. I am overwhelming you with money—in my expansive intentions—if you only knew it!” And really (he said) he meant it to that degree that he thought it much the same as doing it. If he had had those bits of metal or thin paper to which mankind attached so much importance to put in the doctor’s hand, he would have put them in the doctor’s hand. Not having them, he substituted the will for the deed. Very well! If he really meant it—if his will were genuine and real, which it was—it appeared to him that it was the same as coin, and cancelled the obligation.\n\n“It may be, partly, because I know nothing of the value of money,” said Mr. Skimpole, “but I often feel this. It seems so reasonable! My butcher says to me he wants that little bill. It’s a part of the pleasant unconscious poetry of the man’s nature that he always calls it a ‘little’ bill—to make the payment appear easy to both of us. I reply to the butcher, ‘My good friend, if you knew it, you are paid. You haven’t had the trouble of coming to ask for the little bill. You are paid. I mean it.’”\n\n“But, suppose,” said my guardian, laughing, “he had meant the meat in the bill, instead of providing it?”\n\n“My dear Jarndyce,” he returned, “you surprise me. You take the butcher’s position. A butcher I once dealt with occupied that very ground. Says he, ‘Sir, why did you eat spring lamb at eighteen pence a pound?’ ‘Why did I eat spring lamb at eighteen pence a pound, my honest friend?’ said I, naturally amazed by the question. ‘I like spring lamb!’ This was so far convincing. ‘Well, sir,’ says he, ‘I wish I had meant the lamb as you mean the money!’ ‘My good fellow,’ said I, ‘pray let us reason like intellectual beings. How could that be? It was impossible. You HAD got the lamb, and I have NOT got the money. You couldn’t really mean the lamb without sending it in, whereas I can, and do, really mean the money without paying it!’ He had not a word. There was an end of the subject.”\n\n“Did he take no legal proceedings?” inquired my guardian.\n\n“Yes, he took legal proceedings,” said Mr. Skimpole. “But in that he was influenced by passion, not by reason. Passion reminds me of Boythorn. He writes me that you and the ladies have promised him a short visit at his bachelor-house in Lincolnshire.”\n\n“He is a great favourite with my girls,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “and I have promised for them.”\n\n“Nature forgot to shade him off, I think,” observed Mr. Skimpole to Ada and me. “A little too boisterous—like the sea. A little too vehement—like a bull who has made up his mind to consider every colour scarlet. But I grant a sledge-hammering sort of merit in him!”\n\nI should have been surprised if those two could have thought very highly of one another, Mr. Boythorn attaching so much importance to many things and Mr. Skimpole caring so little for anything. Besides which, I had noticed Mr. Boythorn more than once on the point of breaking out into some strong opinion when Mr. Skimpole was referred to. Of course I merely joined Ada in saying that we had been greatly pleased with him.\n\n“He has invited me,” said Mr. Skimpole; “and if a child may trust himself in such hands—which the present child is encouraged to do, with the united tenderness of two angels to guard him—I shall go. He proposes to frank me down and back again. I suppose it will cost money? Shillings perhaps? Or pounds? Or something of that sort? By the by, Coavinses. You remember our friend Coavinses, Miss Summerson?”\n\nHe asked me as the subject arose in his mind, in his graceful, light-hearted manner and without the least embarrassment.\n\n“Oh, yes!” said I.\n\n“Coavinses has been arrested by the Great Bailiff,” said Mr. Skimpole. “He will never do violence to the sunshine any more.”\n\nIt quite shocked me to hear it, for I had already recalled with anything but a serious association the image of the man sitting on the sofa that night wiping his head.\n\n“His successor informed me of it yesterday,” said Mr. Skimpole. “His successor is in my house now—in possession, I think he calls it. He came yesterday, on my blue-eyed daughter’s birthday. I put it to him, ‘This is unreasonable and inconvenient. If you had a blue-eyed daughter you wouldn’t like ME to come, uninvited, on HER birthday?’ But he stayed.”\n\nMr. Skimpole laughed at the pleasant absurdity and lightly touched the piano by which he was seated.\n\n“And he told me,” he said, playing little chords where I shall put full stops, “The Coavinses had left. Three children. No mother. And that Coavinses’ profession. Being unpopular. The rising Coavinses. Were at a considerable disadvantage.”\n\nMr. Jarndyce got up, rubbing his head, and began to walk about. Mr. Skimpole played the melody of one of Ada’s favourite songs. Ada and I both looked at Mr. Jarndyce, thinking that we knew what was passing in his mind.\n\nAfter walking and stopping, and several times leaving off rubbing his head, and beginning again, my guardian put his hand upon the keys and stopped Mr. Skimpole’s playing. “I don’t like this, Skimpole,” he said thoughtfully.\n\nMr. Skimpole, who had quite forgotten the subject, looked up surprised.\n\n“The man was necessary,” pursued my guardian, walking backward and forward in the very short space between the piano and the end of the room and rubbing his hair up from the back of his head as if a high east wind had blown it into that form. “If we make such men necessary by our faults and follies, or by our want of worldly knowledge, or by our misfortunes, we must not revenge ourselves upon them. There was no harm in his trade. He maintained his children. One would like to know more about this.”\n\n“Oh! Coavinses?” cried Mr. Skimpole, at length perceiving what he meant. “Nothing easier. A walk to Coavinses’ headquarters, and you can know what you will.”\n\nMr. Jarndyce nodded to us, who were only waiting for the signal. “Come! We will walk that way, my dears. Why not that way as soon as another!” We were quickly ready and went out. Mr. Skimpole went with us and quite enjoyed the expedition. It was so new and so refreshing, he said, for him to want Coavinses instead of Coavinses wanting him!\n\nHe took us, first, to Cursitor Street, Chancery Lane, where there was a house with barred windows, which he called Coavinses’ Castle. On our going into the entry and ringing a bell, a very hideous boy came out of a sort of office and looked at us over a spiked wicket.\n\n“Who did you want?” said the boy, fitting two of the spikes into his chin.\n\n“There was a follower, or an officer, or something, here,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “who is dead.”\n\n“Yes?” said the boy. “Well?”\n\n“I want to know his name, if you please?”\n\n“Name of Neckett,” said the boy.\n\n“And his address?”\n\n“Bell Yard,” said the boy. “Chandler’s shop, left hand side, name of Blinder.”\n\n“Was he—I don’t know how to shape the question—” murmured my guardian, “industrious?”\n\n“Was Neckett?” said the boy. “Yes, wery much so. He was never tired of watching. He’d set upon a post at a street corner eight or ten hours at a stretch if he undertook to do it.”\n\n“He might have done worse,” I heard my guardian soliloquize. “He might have undertaken to do it and not done it. Thank you. That’s all I want.”\n\nWe left the boy, with his head on one side and his arms on the gate, fondling and sucking the spikes, and went back to Lincoln’s Inn, where Mr. Skimpole, who had not cared to remain nearer Coavinses, awaited us. Then we all went to Bell Yard, a narrow alley at a very short distance. We soon found the chandler’s shop. In it was a good-natured-looking old woman with a dropsy, or an asthma, or perhaps both.\n\n“Neckett’s children?” said she in reply to my inquiry. “Yes, Surely, miss. Three pair, if you please. Door right opposite the stairs.” And she handed me the key across the counter.\n\nI glanced at the key and glanced at her, but she took it for granted that I knew what to do with it. As it could only be intended for the children’s door, I came out without asking any more questions and led the way up the dark stairs. We went as quietly as we could, but four of us made some noise on the aged boards, and when we came to the second story we found we had disturbed a man who was standing there looking out of his room.\n\n“Is it Gridley that’s wanted?” he said, fixing his eyes on me with an angry stare.\n\n“No, sir,” said I; “I am going higher up.”\n\nHe looked at Ada, and at Mr. Jarndyce, and at Mr. Skimpole, fixing the same angry stare on each in succession as they passed and followed me. Mr. Jarndyce gave him good day. “Good day!” he said abruptly and fiercely. He was a tall, sallow man with a careworn head on which but little hair remained, a deeply lined face, and prominent eyes. He had a combative look and a chafing, irritable manner which, associated with his figure—still large and powerful, though evidently in its decline—rather alarmed me. He had a pen in his hand, and in the glimpse I caught of his room in passing, I saw that it was covered with a litter of papers.\n\nLeaving him standing there, we went up to the top room. I tapped at the door, and a little shrill voice inside said, “We are locked in. Mrs. Blinder’s got the key!”\n\nI applied the key on hearing this and opened the door. In a poor room with a sloping ceiling and containing very little furniture was a mite of a boy, some five or six years old, nursing and hushing a heavy child of eighteen months. There was no fire, though the weather was cold; both children were wrapped in some poor shawls and tippets as a substitute. Their clothing was not so warm, however, but that their noses looked red and pinched and their small figures shrunken as the boy walked up and down nursing and hushing the child with its head on his shoulder.\n\n“Who has locked you up here alone?” we naturally asked.\n\n“Charley,” said the boy, standing still to gaze at us.\n\n“Is Charley your brother?”\n\n“No. She’s my sister, Charlotte. Father called her Charley.”\n\n“Are there any more of you besides Charley?”\n\n“Me,” said the boy, “and Emma,” patting the limp bonnet of the child he was nursing. “And Charley.”\n\n“Where is Charley now?”\n\n“Out a-washing,” said the boy, beginning to walk up and down again and taking the nankeen bonnet much too near the bedstead by trying to gaze at us at the same time.\n\nWe were looking at one another and at these two children when there came into the room a very little girl, childish in figure but shrewd and older-looking in the face—pretty-faced too—wearing a womanly sort of bonnet much too large for her and drying her bare arms on a womanly sort of apron. Her fingers were white and wrinkled with washing, and the soap-suds were yet smoking which she wiped off her arms. But for this, she might have been a child playing at washing and imitating a poor working-woman with a quick observation of the truth.\n\nShe had come running from some place in the neighbourhood and had made all the haste she could. Consequently, though she was very light, she was out of breath and could not speak at first, as she stood panting, and wiping her arms, and looking quietly at us.\n\n“Oh, here’s Charley!” said the boy.\n\nThe child he was nursing stretched forth its arms and cried out to be taken by Charley. The little girl took it, in a womanly sort of manner belonging to the apron and the bonnet, and stood looking at us over the burden that clung to her most affectionately.\n\n“Is it possible,” whispered my guardian as we put a chair for the little creature and got her to sit down with her load, the boy keeping close to her, holding to her apron, “that this child works for the rest? Look at this! For God’s sake, look at this!”\n\nIt was a thing to look at. The three children close together, and two of them relying solely on the third, and the third so young and yet with an air of age and steadiness that sat so strangely on the childish figure.\n\n“Charley, Charley!” said my guardian. “How old are you?”\n\n“Over thirteen, sir,” replied the child.\n\n“Oh! What a great age,” said my guardian. “What a great age, Charley!”\n\nI cannot describe the tenderness with which he spoke to her, half playfully yet all the more compassionately and mournfully.\n\n“And do you live alone here with these babies, Charley?” said my guardian.\n\n“Yes, sir,” returned the child, looking up into his face with perfect confidence, “since father died.”\n\n“And how do you live, Charley? Oh! Charley,” said my guardian, turning his face away for a moment, “how do you live?”\n\n“Since father died, sir, I’ve gone out to work. I’m out washing to-day.”\n\n“God help you, Charley!” said my guardian. “You’re not tall enough to reach the tub!”\n\n“In pattens I am, sir,” she said quickly. “I’ve got a high pair as belonged to mother.”\n\n“And when did mother die? Poor mother!”\n\n“Mother died just after Emma was born,” said the child, glancing at the face upon her bosom. “Then father said I was to be as good a mother to her as I could. And so I tried. And so I worked at home and did cleaning and nursing and washing for a long time before I began to go out. And that’s how I know how; don’t you see, sir?”\n\n“And do you often go out?”\n\n“As often as I can,” said Charley, opening her eyes and smiling, “because of earning sixpences and shillings!”\n\n“And do you always lock the babies up when you go out?”\n\n“To keep ’em safe, sir, don’t you see?” said Charley. “Mrs. Blinder comes up now and then, and Mr. Gridley comes up sometimes, and perhaps I can run in sometimes, and they can play you know, and Tom an’t afraid of being locked up, are you, Tom?”\n\n“No-o!” said Tom stoutly.\n\n“When it comes on dark, the lamps are lighted down in the court, and they show up here quite bright—almost quite bright. Don’t they, Tom?”\n\n“Yes, Charley,” said Tom, “almost quite bright.”\n\n“Then he’s as good as gold,” said the little creature—Oh, in such a motherly, womanly way! “And when Emma’s tired, he puts her to bed. And when he’s tired he goes to bed himself. And when I come home and light the candle and has a bit of supper, he sits up again and has it with me. Don’t you, Tom?”\n\n“Oh, yes, Charley!” said Tom. “That I do!” And either in this glimpse of the great pleasure of his life or in gratitude and love for Charley, who was all in all to him, he laid his face among the scanty folds of her frock and passed from laughing into crying.\n\nIt was the first time since our entry that a tear had been shed among these children. The little orphan girl had spoken of their father and their mother as if all that sorrow were subdued by the necessity of taking courage, and by her childish importance in being able to work, and by her bustling busy way. But now, when Tom cried, although she sat quite tranquil, looking quietly at us, and did not by any movement disturb a hair of the head of either of her little charges, I saw two silent tears fall down her face.\n\nI stood at the window with Ada, pretending to look at the housetops, and the blackened stack of chimneys, and the poor plants, and the birds in little cages belonging to the neighbours, when I found that Mrs. Blinder, from the shop below, had come in (perhaps it had taken her all this time to get upstairs) and was talking to my guardian.\n\n“It’s not much to forgive ’em the rent, sir,” she said; “who could take it from them!”\n\n“Well, well!” said my guardian to us two. “It is enough that the time will come when this good woman will find that it WAS much, and that forasmuch as she did it unto the least of these—This child,” he added after a few moments, “could she possibly continue this?”\n\n“Really, sir, I think she might,” said Mrs. Blinder, getting her heavy breath by painful degrees. “She’s as handy as it’s possible to be. Bless you, sir, the way she tended them two children after the mother died was the talk of the yard! And it was a wonder to see her with him after he was took ill, it really was! ‘Mrs. Blinder,’ he said to me the very last he spoke—he was lying there—‘Mrs. Blinder, whatever my calling may have been, I see a angel sitting in this room last night along with my child, and I trust her to Our Father!’”\n\n“He had no other calling?” said my guardian.\n\n“No, sir,” returned Mrs. Blinder, “he was nothing but a follerers. When he first came to lodge here, I didn’t know what he was, and I confess that when I found out I gave him notice. It wasn’t liked in the yard. It wasn’t approved by the other lodgers. It is NOT a genteel calling,” said Mrs. Blinder, “and most people do object to it. Mr. Gridley objected to it very strong, and he is a good lodger, though his temper has been hard tried.”\n\n“So you gave him notice?” said my guardian.\n\n“So I gave him notice,” said Mrs. Blinder. “But really when the time came, and I knew no other ill of him, I was in doubts. He was punctual and diligent; he did what he had to do, sir,” said Mrs. Blinder, unconsciously fixing Mr. Skimpole with her eye, “and it’s something in this world even to do that.”\n\n“So you kept him after all?”\n\n“Why, I said that if he could arrange with Mr. Gridley, I could arrange it with the other lodgers and should not so much mind its being liked or disliked in the yard. Mr. Gridley gave his consent gruff—but gave it. He was always gruff with him, but he has been kind to the children since. A person is never known till a person is proved.”\n\n“Have many people been kind to the children?” asked Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Upon the whole, not so bad, sir,” said Mrs. Blinder; “but certainly not so many as would have been if their father’s calling had been different. Mr. Coavins gave a guinea, and the follerers made up a little purse. Some neighbours in the yard that had always joked and tapped their shoulders when he went by came forward with a little subscription, and—in general—not so bad. Similarly with Charlotte. Some people won’t employ her because she was a follerer’s child; some people that do employ her cast it at her; some make a merit of having her to work for them, with that and all her draw-backs upon her, and perhaps pay her less and put upon her more. But she’s patienter than others would be, and is clever too, and always willing, up to the full mark of her strength and over. So I should say, in general, not so bad, sir, but might be better.”\n\nMrs. Blinder sat down to give herself a more favourable opportunity of recovering her breath, exhausted anew by so much talking before it was fully restored. Mr. Jarndyce was turning to speak to us when his attention was attracted by the abrupt entrance into the room of the Mr. Gridley who had been mentioned and whom we had seen on our way up.\n\n“I don’t know what you may be doing here, ladies and gentlemen,” he said, as if he resented our presence, “but you’ll excuse my coming in. I don’t come in to stare about me. Well, Charley! Well, Tom! Well, little one! How is it with us all to-day?”\n\nHe bent over the group in a caressing way and clearly was regarded as a friend by the children, though his face retained its stern character and his manner to us was as rude as it could be. My guardian noticed it and respected it.\n\n“No one, surely, would come here to stare about him,” he said mildly.\n\n“May be so, sir, may be so,” returned the other, taking Tom upon his knee and waving him off impatiently. “I don’t want to argue with ladies and gentlemen. I have had enough of arguing to last one man his life.”\n\n“You have sufficient reason, I dare say,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “for being chafed and irritated—”\n\n“There again!” exclaimed the man, becoming violently angry. “I am of a quarrelsome temper. I am irascible. I am not polite!”\n\n“Not very, I think.”\n\n“Sir,” said Gridley, putting down the child and going up to him as if he meant to strike him, “do you know anything of Courts of Equity?”\n\n“Perhaps I do, to my sorrow.”\n\n“To your sorrow?” said the man, pausing in his wrath, “if so, I beg your pardon. I am not polite, I know. I beg your pardon! Sir,” with renewed violence, “I have been dragged for five and twenty years over burning iron, and I have lost the habit of treading upon velvet. Go into the Court of Chancery yonder and ask what is one of the standing jokes that brighten up their business sometimes, and they will tell you that the best joke they have is the man from Shropshire. I,” he said, beating one hand on the other passionately, “am the man from Shropshire.”\n\n“I believe I and my family have also had the honour of furnishing some entertainment in the same grave place,” said my guardian composedly. “You may have heard my name—Jarndyce.”\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce,” said Gridley with a rough sort of salutation, “you bear your wrongs more quietly than I can bear mine. More than that, I tell you—and I tell this gentleman, and these young ladies, if they are friends of yours—that if I took my wrongs in any other way, I should be driven mad! It is only by resenting them, and by revenging them in my mind, and by angrily demanding the justice I never get, that I am able to keep my wits together. It is only that!” he said, speaking in a homely, rustic way and with great vehemence. “You may tell me that I over-excite myself. I answer that it’s in my nature to do it, under wrong, and I must do it. There’s nothing between doing it, and sinking into the smiling state of the poor little mad woman that haunts the court. If I was once to sit down under it, I should become imbecile.”\n\nThe passion and heat in which he was, and the manner in which his face worked, and the violent gestures with which he accompanied what he said, were most painful to see.\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce,” he said, “consider my case. As true as there is a heaven above us, this is my case. I am one of two brothers. My father (a farmer) made a will and left his farm and stock and so forth to my mother for her life. After my mother’s death, all was to come to me except a legacy of three hundred pounds that I was then to pay my brother. My mother died. My brother some time afterwards claimed his legacy. I and some of my relations said that he had had a part of it already in board and lodging and some other things. Now mind! That was the question, and nothing else. No one disputed the will; no one disputed anything but whether part of that three hundred pounds had been already paid or not. To settle that question, my brother filing a bill, I was obliged to go into this accursed Chancery; I was forced there because the law forced me and would let me go nowhere else. Seventeen people were made defendants to that simple suit! It first came on after two years. It was then stopped for another two years while the master (may his head rot off!) inquired whether I was my father’s son, about which there was no dispute at all with any mortal creature. He then found out that there were not defendants enough—remember, there were only seventeen as yet!—but that we must have another who had been left out and must begin all over again. The costs at that time—before the thing was begun!—were three times the legacy. My brother would have given up the legacy, and joyful, to escape more costs. My whole estate, left to me in that will of my father’s, has gone in costs. The suit, still undecided, has fallen into rack, and ruin, and despair, with everything else—and here I stand, this day! Now, Mr. Jarndyce, in your suit there are thousands and thousands involved, where in mine there are hundreds. Is mine less hard to bear or is it harder to bear, when my whole living was in it and has been thus shamefully sucked away?”\n\nMr. Jarndyce said that he condoled with him with all his heart and that he set up no monopoly himself in being unjustly treated by this monstrous system.\n\n“There again!” said Mr. Gridley with no diminution of his rage. “The system! I am told on all hands, it’s the system. I mustn’t look to individuals. It’s the system. I mustn’t go into court and say, ‘My Lord, I beg to know this from you—is this right or wrong? Have you the face to tell me I have received justice and therefore am dismissed?’ My Lord knows nothing of it. He sits there to administer the system. I mustn’t go to Mr. Tulkinghorn, the solicitor in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, and say to him when he makes me furious by being so cool and satisfied—as they all do, for I know they gain by it while I lose, don’t I?—I mustn’t say to him, ‘I will have something out of some one for my ruin, by fair means or foul!’ HE is not responsible. It’s the system. But, if I do no violence to any of them, here—I may! I don’t know what may happen if I am carried beyond myself at last! I will accuse the individual workers of that system against me, face to face, before the great eternal bar!”\n\nHis passion was fearful. I could not have believed in such rage without seeing it.\n\n“I have done!” he said, sitting down and wiping his face. “Mr. Jarndyce, I have done! I am violent, I know. I ought to know it. I have been in prison for contempt of court. I have been in prison for threatening the solicitor. I have been in this trouble, and that trouble, and shall be again. I am the man from Shropshire, and I sometimes go beyond amusing them, though they have found it amusing, too, to see me committed into custody and brought up in custody and all that. It would be better for me, they tell me, if I restrained myself. I tell them that if I did restrain myself I should become imbecile. I was a good-enough-tempered man once, I believe. People in my part of the country say they remember me so, but now I must have this vent under my sense of injury or nothing could hold my wits together. It would be far better for you, Mr. Gridley,’ the Lord Chancellor told me last week, ‘not to waste your time here, and to stay, usefully employed, down in Shropshire.’ ‘My Lord, my Lord, I know it would,’ said I to him, ‘and it would have been far better for me never to have heard the name of your high office, but unhappily for me, I can’t undo the past, and the past drives me here!’ Besides,” he added, breaking fiercely out, “I’ll shame them. To the last, I’ll show myself in that court to its shame. If I knew when I was going to die, and could be carried there, and had a voice to speak with, I would die there, saying, ‘You have brought me here and sent me from here many and many a time. Now send me out feet foremost!’”\n\nHis countenance had, perhaps for years, become so set in its contentious expression that it did not soften, even now when he was quiet.\n\n“I came to take these babies down to my room for an hour,” he said, going to them again, “and let them play about. I didn’t mean to say all this, but it don’t much signify. You’re not afraid of me, Tom, are you?”\n\n“No!” said Tom. “You ain’t angry with ME.”\n\n“You are right, my child. You’re going back, Charley? Aye? Come then, little one!” He took the youngest child on his arm, where she was willing enough to be carried. “I shouldn’t wonder if we found a ginger-bread soldier downstairs. Let’s go and look for him!”\n\nHe made his former rough salutation, which was not deficient in a certain respect, to Mr. Jarndyce, and bowing slightly to us, went downstairs to his room.\n\nUpon that, Mr. Skimpole began to talk, for the first time since our arrival, in his usual gay strain. He said, Well, it was really very pleasant to see how things lazily adapted themselves to purposes. Here was this Mr. Gridley, a man of a robust will and surprising energy—intellectually speaking, a sort of inharmonious blacksmith—and he could easily imagine that there Gridley was, years ago, wandering about in life for something to expend his superfluous combativeness upon—a sort of Young Love among the thorns—when the Court of Chancery came in his way and accommodated him with the exact thing he wanted. There they were, matched, ever afterwards! Otherwise he might have been a great general, blowing up all sorts of towns, or he might have been a great politician, dealing in all sorts of parliamentary rhetoric; but as it was, he and the Court of Chancery had fallen upon each other in the pleasantest way, and nobody was much the worse, and Gridley was, so to speak, from that hour provided for. Then look at Coavinses! How delightfully poor Coavinses (father of these charming children) illustrated the same principle! He, Mr. Skimpole, himself, had sometimes repined at the existence of Coavinses. He had found Coavinses in his way. He could had dispensed with Coavinses. There had been times when, if he had been a sultan, and his grand vizier had said one morning, “What does the Commander of the Faithful require at the hands of his slave?” he might have even gone so far as to reply, “The head of Coavinses!” But what turned out to be the case? That, all that time, he had been giving employment to a most deserving man, that he had been a benefactor to Coavinses, that he had actually been enabling Coavinses to bring up these charming children in this agreeable way, developing these social virtues! Insomuch that his heart had just now swelled and the tears had come into his eyes when he had looked round the room and thought, “I was the great patron of Coavinses, and his little comforts were MY work!”\n\nThere was something so captivating in his light way of touching these fantastic strings, and he was such a mirthful child by the side of the graver childhood we had seen, that he made my guardian smile even as he turned towards us from a little private talk with Mrs. Blinder. We kissed Charley, and took her downstairs with us, and stopped outside the house to see her run away to her work. I don’t know where she was going, but we saw her run, such a little, little creature in her womanly bonnet and apron, through a covered way at the bottom of the court and melt into the city’s strife and sound like a dewdrop in an ocean.\nTom-all-Alone’s\n\nMy Lady Dedlock is restless, very restless. The astonished fashionable intelligence hardly knows where to have her. To-day she is at Chesney Wold; yesterday she was at her house in town; to-morrow she may be abroad, for anything the fashionable intelligence can with confidence predict. Even Sir Leicester’s gallantry has some trouble to keep pace with her. It would have more but that his other faithful ally, for better and for worse—the gout—darts into the old oak bed-chamber at Chesney Wold and grips him by both legs.\n\nSir Leicester receives the gout as a troublesome demon, but still a demon of the patrician order. All the Dedlocks, in the direct male line, through a course of time during and beyond which the memory of man goeth not to the contrary, have had the gout. It can be proved, sir. Other men’s fathers may have died of the rheumatism or may have taken base contagion from the tainted blood of the sick vulgar, but the Dedlock family have communicated something exclusive even to the levelling process of dying by dying of their own family gout. It has come down through the illustrious line like the plate, or the pictures, or the place in Lincolnshire. It is among their dignities. Sir Leicester is perhaps not wholly without an impression, though he has never resolved it into words, that the angel of death in the discharge of his necessary duties may observe to the shades of the aristocracy, “My lords and gentlemen, I have the honour to present to you another Dedlock certified to have arrived per the family gout.”\n\nHence Sir Leicester yields up his family legs to the family disorder as if he held his name and fortune on that feudal tenure. He feels that for a Dedlock to be laid upon his back and spasmodically twitched and stabbed in his extremities is a liberty taken somewhere, but he thinks, “We have all yielded to this; it belongs to us; it has for some hundreds of years been understood that we are not to make the vaults in the park interesting on more ignoble terms; and I submit myself to the compromise.”\n\nAnd a goodly show he makes, lying in a flush of crimson and gold in the midst of the great drawing-room before his favourite picture of my Lady, with broad strips of sunlight shining in, down the long perspective, through the long line of windows, and alternating with soft reliefs of shadow. Outside, the stately oaks, rooted for ages in the green ground which has never known ploughshare, but was still a chase when kings rode to battle with sword and shield and rode a-hunting with bow and arrow, bear witness to his greatness. Inside, his forefathers, looking on him from the walls, say, “Each of us was a passing reality here and left this coloured shadow of himself and melted into remembrance as dreamy as the distant voices of the rooks now lulling you to rest,” and bear their testimony to his greatness too. And he is very great this day. And woe to Boythorn or other daring wight who shall presumptuously contest an inch with him!\n\nMy Lady is at present represented, near Sir Leicester, by her portrait. She has flitted away to town, with no intention of remaining there, and will soon flit hither again, to the confusion of the fashionable intelligence. The house in town is not prepared for her reception. It is muffled and dreary. Only one Mercury in powder gapes disconsolate at the hall-window; and he mentioned last night to another Mercury of his acquaintance, also accustomed to good society, that if that sort of thing was to last—which it couldn’t, for a man of his spirits couldn’t bear it, and a man of his figure couldn’t be expected to bear it—there would be no resource for him, upon his honour, but to cut his throat!\n\nWhat connexion can there be between the place in Lincolnshire, the house in town, the Mercury in powder, and the whereabout of Jo the outlaw with the broom, who had that distant ray of light upon him when he swept the churchyard-step? What connexion can there have been between many people in the innumerable histories of this world who from opposite sides of great gulfs have, nevertheless, been very curiously brought together!\n\nJo sweeps his crossing all day long, unconscious of the link, if any link there be. He sums up his mental condition when asked a question by replying that he “don’t know nothink.” He knows that it’s hard to keep the mud off the crossing in dirty weather, and harder still to live by doing it. Nobody taught him even that much; he found it out.\n\nJo lives—that is to say, Jo has not yet died—in a ruinous place known to the like of him by the name of Tom-all-Alone’s. It is a black, dilapidated street, avoided by all decent people, where the crazy houses were seized upon, when their decay was far advanced, by some bold vagrants who after establishing their own possession took to letting them out in lodgings. Now, these tumbling tenements contain, by night, a swarm of misery. As on the ruined human wretch vermin parasites appear, so these ruined shelters have bred a crowd of foul existence that crawls in and out of gaps in walls and boards; and coils itself to sleep, in maggot numbers, where the rain drips in; and comes and goes, fetching and carrying fever and sowing more evil in its every footprint than Lord Coodle, and Sir Thomas Doodle, and the Duke of Foodle, and all the fine gentlemen in office, down to Zoodle, shall set right in five hundred years—though born expressly to do it.\n\nTwice lately there has been a crash and a cloud of dust, like the springing of a mine, in Tom-all-Alone’s; and each time a house has fallen. These accidents have made a paragraph in the newspapers and have filled a bed or two in the nearest hospital. The gaps remain, and there are not unpopular lodgings among the rubbish. As several more houses are nearly ready to go, the next crash in Tom-all-Alone’s may be expected to be a good one.\n\nThis desirable property is in Chancery, of course. It would be an insult to the discernment of any man with half an eye to tell him so. Whether “Tom” is the popular representative of the original plaintiff or defendant in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, or whether Tom lived here when the suit had laid the street waste, all alone, until other settlers came to join him, or whether the traditional title is a comprehensive name for a retreat cut off from honest company and put out of the pale of hope, perhaps nobody knows. Certainly Jo don’t know.\n\n“For I don’t,” says Jo, “I don’t know nothink.”\n\nIt must be a strange state to be like Jo! To shuffle through the streets, unfamiliar with the shapes, and in utter darkness as to the meaning, of those mysterious symbols, so abundant over the shops, and at the corners of streets, and on the doors, and in the windows! To see people read, and to see people write, and to see the postmen deliver letters, and not to have the least idea of all that language—to be, to every scrap of it, stone blind and dumb! It must be very puzzling to see the good company going to the churches on Sundays, with their books in their hands, and to think (for perhaps Jo DOES think at odd times) what does it all mean, and if it means anything to anybody, how comes it that it means nothing to me? To be hustled, and jostled, and moved on; and really to feel that it would appear to be perfectly true that I have no business here, or there, or anywhere; and yet to be perplexed by the consideration that I AM here somehow, too, and everybody overlooked me until I became the creature that I am! It must be a strange state, not merely to be told that I am scarcely human (as in the case of my offering myself for a witness), but to feel it of my own knowledge all my life! To see the horses, dogs, and cattle go by me and to know that in ignorance I belong to them and not to the superior beings in my shape, whose delicacy I offend! Jo’s ideas of a criminal trial, or a judge, or a bishop, or a government, or that inestimable jewel to him (if he only knew it) the Constitution, should be strange! His whole material and immaterial life is wonderfully strange; his death, the strangest thing of all.\n\nJo comes out of Tom-all-Alone’s, meeting the tardy morning which is always late in getting down there, and munches his dirty bit of bread as he comes along. His way lying through many streets, and the houses not yet being open, he sits down to breakfast on the door-step of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts and gives it a brush when he has finished as an acknowledgment of the accommodation. He admires the size of the edifice and wonders what it’s all about. He has no idea, poor wretch, of the spiritual destitution of a coral reef in the Pacific or what it costs to look up the precious souls among the coco-nuts and bread-fruit.\n\nHe goes to his crossing and begins to lay it out for the day. The town awakes; the great tee-totum is set up for its daily spin and whirl; all that unaccountable reading and writing, which has been suspended for a few hours, recommences. Jo and the other lower animals get on in the unintelligible mess as they can. It is market-day. The blinded oxen, over-goaded, over-driven, never guided, run into wrong places and are beaten out, and plunge red-eyed and foaming at stone walls, and often sorely hurt the innocent, and often sorely hurt themselves. Very like Jo and his order; very, very like!\n\nA band of music comes and plays. Jo listens to it. So does a dog—a drover’s dog, waiting for his master outside a butcher’s shop, and evidently thinking about those sheep he has had upon his mind for some hours and is happily rid of. He seems perplexed respecting three or four, can’t remember where he left them, looks up and down the street as half expecting to see them astray, suddenly pricks up his ears and remembers all about it. A thoroughly vagabond dog, accustomed to low company and public-houses; a terrific dog to sheep, ready at a whistle to scamper over their backs and tear out mouthfuls of their wool; but an educated, improved, developed dog who has been taught his duties and knows how to discharge them. He and Jo listen to the music, probably with much the same amount of animal satisfaction; likewise as to awakened association, aspiration, or regret, melancholy or joyful reference to things beyond the senses, they are probably upon a par. But, otherwise, how far above the human listener is the brute!\n\nTurn that dog’s descendants wild, like Jo, and in a very few years they will so degenerate that they will lose even their bark—but not their bite.\n\nThe day changes as it wears itself away and becomes dark and drizzly. Jo fights it out at his crossing among the mud and wheels, the horses, whips, and umbrellas, and gets but a scanty sum to pay for the unsavoury shelter of Tom-all-Alone’s. Twilight comes on; gas begins to start up in the shops; the lamplighter, with his ladder, runs along the margin of the pavement. A wretched evening is beginning to close in.\n\nIn his chambers Mr. Tulkinghorn sits meditating an application to the nearest magistrate to-morrow morning for a warrant. Gridley, a disappointed suitor, has been here to-day and has been alarming. We are not to be put in bodily fear, and that ill-conditioned fellow shall be held to bail again. From the ceiling, foreshortened Allegory, in the person of one impossible Roman upside down, points with the arm of Samson (out of joint, and an odd one) obtrusively toward the window. Why should Mr. Tulkinghorn, for such no reason, look out of window? Is the hand not always pointing there? So he does not look out of window.\n\nAnd if he did, what would it be to see a woman going by? There are women enough in the world, Mr. Tulkinghorn thinks—too many; they are at the bottom of all that goes wrong in it, though, for the matter of that, they create business for lawyers. What would it be to see a woman going by, even though she were going secretly? They are all secret. Mr. Tulkinghorn knows that very well.\n\nBut they are not all like the woman who now leaves him and his house behind, between whose plain dress and her refined manner there is something exceedingly inconsistent. She should be an upper servant by her attire, yet in her air and step, though both are hurried and assumed—as far as she can assume in the muddy streets, which she treads with an unaccustomed foot—she is a lady. Her face is veiled, and still she sufficiently betrays herself to make more than one of those who pass her look round sharply.\n\nShe never turns her head. Lady or servant, she has a purpose in her and can follow it. She never turns her head until she comes to the crossing where Jo plies with his broom. He crosses with her and begs. Still, she does not turn her head until she has landed on the other side. Then she slightly beckons to him and says, “Come here!”\n\nJo follows her a pace or two into a quiet court.\n\n“Are you the boy I’ve read of in the papers?” she asked behind her veil.\n\n“I don’t know,” says Jo, staring moodily at the veil, “nothink about no papers. I don’t know nothink about nothink at all.”\n\n“Were you examined at an inquest?”\n\n“I don’t know nothink about no—where I was took by the beadle, do you mean?” says Jo. “Was the boy’s name at the inkwhich Jo?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That’s me!” says Jo.\n\n“Come farther up.”\n\n“You mean about the man?” says Jo, following. “Him as wos dead?”\n\n“Hush! Speak in a whisper! Yes. Did he look, when he was living, so very ill and poor?”\n\n“Oh, jist!” says Jo.\n\n“Did he look like—not like YOU?” says the woman with abhorrence.\n\n“Oh, not so bad as me,” says Jo. “I’m a reg’lar one I am! You didn’t know him, did you?”\n\n“How dare you ask me if I knew him?”\n\n“No offence, my lady,” says Jo with much humility, for even he has got at the suspicion of her being a lady.\n\n“I am not a lady. I am a servant.”\n\n“You are a jolly servant!” says Jo without the least idea of saying anything offensive, merely as a tribute of admiration.\n\n“Listen and be silent. Don’t talk to me, and stand farther from me! Can you show me all those places that were spoken of in the account I read? The place he wrote for, the place he died at, the place where you were taken to, and the place where he was buried? Do you know the place where he was buried?”\n\nJo answers with a nod, having also nodded as each other place was mentioned.\n\n“Go before me and show me all those dreadful places. Stop opposite to each, and don’t speak to me unless I speak to you. Don’t look back. Do what I want, and I will pay you well.”\n\nJo attends closely while the words are being spoken; tells them off on his broom-handle, finding them rather hard; pauses to consider their meaning; considers it satisfactory; and nods his ragged head.\n\n“I’m fly,” says Jo. “But fen larks, you know. Stow hooking it!”\n\n“What does the horrible creature mean?” exclaims the servant, recoiling from him.\n\n“Stow cutting away, you know!” says Jo.\n\n“I don’t understand you. Go on before! I will give you more money than you ever had in your life.”\n\nJo screws up his mouth into a whistle, gives his ragged head a rub, takes his broom under his arm, and leads the way, passing deftly with his bare feet over the hard stones and through the mud and mire.\n\nCook’s Court. Jo stops. A pause.\n\n“Who lives here?”\n\n“Him wot give him his writing and give me half a bull,” says Jo in a whisper without looking over his shoulder.\n\n“Go on to the next.”\n\nKrook’s house. Jo stops again. A longer pause.\n\n“Who lives here?”\n\n“HE lived here,” Jo answers as before.\n\nAfter a silence he is asked, “In which room?”\n\n“In the back room up there. You can see the winder from this corner. Up there! That’s where I see him stritched out. This is the public-ouse where I was took to.”\n\n“Go on to the next!”\n\nIt is a longer walk to the next, but Jo, relieved of his first suspicions, sticks to the forms imposed upon him and does not look round. By many devious ways, reeking with offence of many kinds, they come to the little tunnel of a court, and to the gas-lamp (lighted now), and to the iron gate.\n\n“He was put there,” says Jo, holding to the bars and looking in.\n\n“Where? Oh, what a scene of horror!”\n\n“There!” says Jo, pointing. “Over yinder. Among them piles of bones, and close to that there kitchin winder! They put him wery nigh the top. They was obliged to stamp upon it to git it in. I could unkiver it for you with my broom if the gate was open. That’s why they locks it, I s’pose,” giving it a shake. “It’s always locked. Look at the rat!” cries Jo, excited. “Hi! Look! There he goes! Ho! Into the ground!”\n\nThe servant shrinks into a corner, into a corner of that hideous archway, with its deadly stains contaminating her dress; and putting out her two hands and passionately telling him to keep away from her, for he is loathsome to her, so remains for some moments. Jo stands staring and is still staring when she recovers herself.\n\n“Is this place of abomination consecrated ground?”\n\n“I don’t know nothink of consequential ground,” says Jo, still staring.\n\n“Is it blessed?”\n\n“Which?” says Jo, in the last degree amazed.\n\n“Is it blessed?”\n\n“I’m blest if I know,” says Jo, staring more than ever; “but I shouldn’t think it warn’t. Blest?” repeats Jo, something troubled in his mind. “It an’t done it much good if it is. Blest? I should think it was t’othered myself. But I don’t know nothink!”\n\nThe servant takes as little heed of what he says as she seems to take of what she has said herself. She draws off her glove to get some money from her purse. Jo silently notices how white and small her hand is and what a jolly servant she must be to wear such sparkling rings.\n\nShe drops a piece of money in his hand without touching it, and shuddering as their hands approach. “Now,” she adds, “show me the spot again!”\n\nJo thrusts the handle of his broom between the bars of the gate, and with his utmost power of elaboration, points it out. At length, looking aside to see if he has made himself intelligible, he finds that he is alone.\n\nHis first proceeding is to hold the piece of money to the gas-light and to be overpowered at finding that it is yellow—gold. His next is to give it a one-sided bite at the edge as a test of its quality. His next, to put it in his mouth for safety and to sweep the step and passage with great care. His job done, he sets off for Tom-all-Alone’s, stopping in the light of innumerable gas-lamps to produce the piece of gold and give it another one-sided bite as a reassurance of its being genuine.\n\nThe Mercury in powder is in no want of society to-night, for my Lady goes to a grand dinner and three or four balls. Sir Leicester is fidgety down at Chesney Wold, with no better company than the gout; he complains to Mrs. Rouncewell that the rain makes such a monotonous pattering on the terrace that he can’t read the paper even by the fireside in his own snug dressing-room.\n\n“Sir Leicester would have done better to try the other side of the house, my dear,” says Mrs. Rouncewell to Rosa. “His dressing-room is on my Lady’s side. And in all these years I never heard the step upon the Ghost’s Walk more distinct than it is to-night!”\n\nEsther’s Narrative\nRichard very often came to see us while we remained in London (though he soon failed in his letter-writing), and with his quick abilities, his good spirits, his good temper, his gaiety and freshness, was always delightful. But though I liked him more and more the better I knew him, I still felt more and more how much it was to be regretted that he had been educated in no habits of application and concentration. The system which had addressed him in exactly the same manner as it had addressed hundreds of other boys, all varying in character and capacity, had enabled him to dash through his tasks, always with fair credit and often with distinction, but in a fitful, dazzling way that had confirmed his reliance on those very qualities in himself which it had been most desirable to direct and train. They were good qualities, without which no high place can be meritoriously won, but like fire and water, though excellent servants, they were very bad masters. If they had been under Richard’s direction, they would have been his friends; but Richard being under their direction, they became his enemies.\n\nI write down these opinions not because I believe that this or any other thing was so because I thought so, but only because I did think so and I want to be quite candid about all I thought and did. These were my thoughts about Richard. I thought I often observed besides how right my guardian was in what he had said, and that the uncertainties and delays of the Chancery suit had imparted to his nature something of the careless spirit of a gamester who felt that he was part of a great gaming system.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Bayham Badger coming one afternoon when my guardian was not at home, in the course of conversation I naturally inquired after Richard.\n\n“Why, Mr. Carstone,” said Mrs. Badger, “is very well and is, I assure you, a great acquisition to our society. Captain Swosser used to say of me that I was always better than land a-head and a breeze a-starn to the midshipmen’s mess when the purser’s junk had become as tough as the fore-topsel weather earrings. It was his naval way of mentioning generally that I was an acquisition to any society. I may render the same tribute, I am sure, to Mr. Carstone. But I—you won’t think me premature if I mention it?”\n\nI said no, as Mrs. Badger’s insinuating tone seemed to require such an answer.\n\n“Nor Miss Clare?” said Mrs. Bayham Badger sweetly.\n\nAda said no, too, and looked uneasy.\n\n“Why, you see, my dears,” said Mrs. Badger, “—you’ll excuse me calling you my dears?”\n\nWe entreated Mrs. Badger not to mention it.\n\n“Because you really are, if I may take the liberty of saying so,” pursued Mrs. Badger, “so perfectly charming. You see, my dears, that although I am still young—or Mr. Bayham Badger pays me the compliment of saying so—”\n\n“No,” Mr. Badger called out like some one contradicting at a public meeting. “Not at all!”\n\n“Very well,” smiled Mrs. Badger, “we will say still young.”\n\n“Undoubtedly,” said Mr. Badger.\n\n“My dears, though still young, I have had many opportunities of observing young men. There were many such on board the dear old Crippler, I assure you. After that, when I was with Captain Swosser in the Mediterranean, I embraced every opportunity of knowing and befriending the midshipmen under Captain Swosser’s command. YOU never heard them called the young gentlemen, my dears, and probably would not understand allusions to their pipe-claying their weekly accounts, but it is otherwise with me, for blue water has been a second home to me, and I have been quite a sailor. Again, with Professor Dingo.”\n\n“A man of European reputation,” murmured Mr. Badger.\n\n“When I lost my dear first and became the wife of my dear second,” said Mrs. Badger, speaking of her former husbands as if they were parts of a charade, “I still enjoyed opportunities of observing youth. The class attendant on Professor Dingo’s lectures was a large one, and it became my pride, as the wife of an eminent scientific man seeking herself in science the utmost consolation it could impart, to throw our house open to the students as a kind of Scientific Exchange. Every Tuesday evening there was lemonade and a mixed biscuit for all who chose to partake of those refreshments. And there was science to an unlimited extent.”\n\n“Remarkable assemblies those, Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Badger reverentially. “There must have been great intellectual friction going on there under the auspices of such a man!”\n\n“And now,” pursued Mrs. Badger, “now that I am the wife of my dear third, Mr. Badger, I still pursue those habits of observation which were formed during the lifetime of Captain Swosser and adapted to new and unexpected purposes during the lifetime of Professor Dingo. I therefore have not come to the consideration of Mr. Carstone as a neophyte. And yet I am very much of the opinion, my dears, that he has not chosen his profession advisedly.”\n\nAda looked so very anxious now that I asked Mrs. Badger on what she founded her supposition.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson,” she replied, “on Mr. Carstone’s character and conduct. He is of such a very easy disposition that probably he would never think it worth while to mention how he really feels, but he feels languid about the profession. He has not that positive interest in it which makes it his vocation. If he has any decided impression in reference to it, I should say it was that it is a tiresome pursuit. Now, this is not promising. Young men like Mr. Allan Woodcourt who take it from a strong interest in all that it can do will find some reward in it through a great deal of work for a very little money and through years of considerable endurance and disappointment. But I am quite convinced that this would never be the case with Mr. Carstone.”\n\n“Does Mr. Badger think so too?” asked Ada timidly.\n\n“Why,” said Mr. Badger, “to tell the truth, Miss Clare, this view of the matter had not occurred to me until Mrs. Badger mentioned it. But when Mrs. Badger put it in that light, I naturally gave great consideration to it, knowing that Mrs. Badger’s mind, in addition to its natural advantages, has had the rare advantage of being formed by two such very distinguished (I will even say illustrious) public men as Captain Swosser of the Royal Navy and Professor Dingo. The conclusion at which I have arrived is—in short, is Mrs. Badger’s conclusion.”\n\n“It was a maxim of Captain Swosser’s,” said Mrs. Badger, “speaking in his figurative naval manner, that when you make pitch hot, you cannot make it too hot; and that if you only have to swab a plank, you should swab it as if Davy Jones were after you. It appears to me that this maxim is applicable to the medical as well as to the nautical profession.”\n\n“To all professions,” observed Mr. Badger. “It was admirably said by Captain Swosser. Beautifully said.”\n\n“People objected to Professor Dingo when we were staying in the north of Devon after our marriage,” said Mrs. Badger, “that he disfigured some of the houses and other buildings by chipping off fragments of those edifices with his little geological hammer. But the professor replied that he knew of no building save the Temple of Science. The principle is the same, I think?”\n\n“Precisely the same,” said Mr. Badger. “Finely expressed! The professor made the same remark, Miss Summerson, in his last illness, when (his mind wandering) he insisted on keeping his little hammer under the pillow and chipping at the countenances of the attendants. The ruling passion!”\n\nAlthough we could have dispensed with the length at which Mr. and Mrs. Badger pursued the conversation, we both felt that it was disinterested in them to express the opinion they had communicated to us and that there was a great probability of its being sound. We agreed to say nothing to Mr. Jarndyce until we had spoken to Richard; and as he was coming next evening, we resolved to have a very serious talk with him.\n\nSo after he had been a little while with Ada, I went in and found my darling (as I knew she would be) prepared to consider him thoroughly right in whatever he said.\n\n“And how do you get on, Richard?” said I. I always sat down on the other side of him. He made quite a sister of me.\n\n“Oh! Well enough!” said Richard.\n\n“He can’t say better than that, Esther, can he?” cried my pet triumphantly.\n\nI tried to look at my pet in the wisest manner, but of course I couldn’t.\n\n“Well enough?” I repeated.\n\n“Yes,” said Richard, “well enough. It’s rather jog-trotty and humdrum. But it’ll do as well as anything else!”\n\n“Oh! My dear Richard!” I remonstrated.\n\n“What’s the matter?” said Richard.\n\n“Do as well as anything else!”\n\n“I don’t think there’s any harm in that, Dame Durden,” said Ada, looking so confidingly at me across him; “because if it will do as well as anything else, it will do very well, I hope.”\n\n“Oh, yes, I hope so,” returned Richard, carelessly tossing his hair from his forehead. “After all, it may be only a kind of probation till our suit is—I forgot though. I am not to mention the suit. Forbidden ground! Oh, yes, it’s all right enough. Let us talk about something else.”\n\nAda would have done so willingly, and with a full persuasion that we had brought the question to a most satisfactory state. But I thought it would be useless to stop there, so I began again.\n\n“No, but Richard,” said I, “and my dear Ada! Consider how important it is to you both, and what a point of honour it is towards your cousin, that you, Richard, should be quite in earnest without any reservation. I think we had better talk about this, really, Ada. It will be too late very soon.”\n\n“Oh, yes! We must talk about it!” said Ada. “But I think Richard is right.”\n\nWhat was the use of my trying to look wise when she was so pretty, and so engaging, and so fond of him!\n\n“Mr. and Mrs. Badger were here yesterday, Richard,” said I, “and they seemed disposed to think that you had no great liking for the profession.”\n\n“Did they though?” said Richard. “Oh! Well, that rather alters the case, because I had no idea that they thought so, and I should not have liked to disappoint or inconvenience them. The fact is, I don’t care much about it. But, oh, it don’t matter! It’ll do as well as anything else!”\n\n“You hear him, Ada!” said I.\n\n“The fact is,” Richard proceeded, half thoughtfully and half jocosely, “it is not quite in my way. I don’t take to it. And I get too much of Mrs. Bayham Badger’s first and second.”\n\n“I am sure THAT’S very natural!” cried Ada, quite delighted. “The very thing we both said yesterday, Esther!”\n\n“Then,” pursued Richard, “it’s monotonous, and to-day is too like yesterday, and to-morrow is too like to-day.”\n\n“But I am afraid,” said I, “this is an objection to all kinds of application—to life itself, except under some very uncommon circumstances.”\n\n“Do you think so?” returned Richard, still considering. “Perhaps! Ha! Why, then, you know,” he added, suddenly becoming gay again, “we travel outside a circle to what I said just now. It’ll do as well as anything else. Oh, it’s all right enough! Let us talk about something else.”\n\nBut even Ada, with her loving face—and if it had seemed innocent and trusting when I first saw it in that memorable November fog, how much more did it seem now when I knew her innocent and trusting heart—even Ada shook her head at this and looked serious. So I thought it a good opportunity to hint to Richard that if he were sometimes a little careless of himself, I was very sure he never meant to be careless of Ada, and that it was a part of his affectionate consideration for her not to slight the importance of a step that might influence both their lives. This made him almost grave.\n\n“My dear Mother Hubbard,” he said, “that’s the very thing! I have thought of that several times and have been quite angry with myself for meaning to be so much in earnest and—somehow—not exactly being so. I don’t know how it is; I seem to want something or other to stand by. Even you have no idea how fond I am of Ada (my darling cousin, I love you, so much!), but I don’t settle down to constancy in other things. It’s such uphill work, and it takes such a time!” said Richard with an air of vexation.\n\n“That may be,” I suggested, “because you don’t like what you have chosen.”\n\n“Poor fellow!” said Ada. “I am sure I don’t wonder at it!”\n\nNo. It was not of the least use my trying to look wise. I tried again, but how could I do it, or how could it have any effect if I could, while Ada rested her clasped hands upon his shoulder and while he looked at her tender blue eyes, and while they looked at him!\n\n“You see, my precious girl,” said Richard, passing her golden curls through and through his hand, “I was a little hasty perhaps; or I misunderstood my own inclinations perhaps. They don’t seem to lie in that direction. I couldn’t tell till I tried. Now the question is whether it’s worth-while to undo all that has been done. It seems like making a great disturbance about nothing particular.”\n\n“My dear Richard,” said I, “how CAN you say about nothing particular?”\n\n“I don’t mean absolutely that,” he returned. “I mean that it MAY be nothing particular because I may never want it.”\n\nBoth Ada and I urged, in reply, not only that it was decidedly worth-while to undo what had been done, but that it must be undone. I then asked Richard whether he had thought of any more congenial pursuit.\n\n“There, my dear Mrs. Shipton,” said Richard, “you touch me home. Yes, I have. I have been thinking that the law is the boy for me.”\n\n“The law!” repeated Ada as if she were afraid of the name.\n\n“If I went into Kenge’s office,” said Richard, “and if I were placed under articles to Kenge, I should have my eye on the—hum!—the forbidden ground—and should be able to study it, and master it, and to satisfy myself that it was not neglected and was being properly conducted. I should be able to look after Ada’s interests and my own interests (the same thing!); and I should peg away at Blackstone and all those fellows with the most tremendous ardour.”\n\nI was not by any means so sure of that, and I saw how his hankering after the vague things yet to come of those long-deferred hopes cast a shade on Ada’s face. But I thought it best to encourage him in any project of continuous exertion, and only advised him to be quite sure that his mind was made up now.\n\n“My dear Minerva,” said Richard, “I am as steady as you are. I made a mistake; we are all liable to mistakes; I won’t do so any more, and I’ll become such a lawyer as is not often seen. That is, you know,” said Richard, relapsing into doubt, “if it really is worth-while, after all, to make such a disturbance about nothing particular!”\n\nThis led to our saying again, with a great deal of gravity, all that we had said already and to our coming to much the same conclusion afterwards. But we so strongly advised Richard to be frank and open with Mr. Jarndyce, without a moment’s delay, and his disposition was naturally so opposed to concealment that he sought him out at once (taking us with him) and made a full avowal. “Rick,” said my guardian, after hearing him attentively, “we can retreat with honour, and we will. But we must be careful—for our cousin’s sake, Rick, for our cousin’s sake—that we make no more such mistakes. Therefore, in the matter of the law, we will have a good trial before we decide. We will look before we leap, and take plenty of time about it.”\n\nRichard’s energy was of such an impatient and fitful kind that he would have liked nothing better than to have gone to Mr. Kenge’s office in that hour and to have entered into articles with him on the spot. Submitting, however, with a good grace to the caution that we had shown to be so necessary, he contented himself with sitting down among us in his lightest spirits and talking as if his one unvarying purpose in life from childhood had been that one which now held possession of him. My guardian was very kind and cordial with him, but rather grave, enough so to cause Ada, when he had departed and we were going upstairs to bed, to say, “Cousin John, I hope you don’t think the worse of Richard?”\n\n“No, my love,” said he.\n\n“Because it was very natural that Richard should be mistaken in such a difficult case. It is not uncommon.”\n\n“No, no, my love,” said he. “Don’t look unhappy.”\n\n“Oh, I am not unhappy, cousin John!” said Ada, smiling cheerfully, with her hand upon his shoulder, where she had put it in bidding him good night. “But I should be a little so if you thought at all the worse of Richard.”\n\n“My dear,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “I should think the worse of him only if you were ever in the least unhappy through his means. I should be more disposed to quarrel with myself even then, than with poor Rick, for I brought you together. But, tut, all this is nothing! He has time before him, and the race to run. I think the worse of him? Not I, my loving cousin! And not you, I swear!”\n\n“No, indeed, cousin John,” said Ada, “I am sure I could not—I am sure I would not—think any ill of Richard if the whole world did. I could, and I would, think better of him then than at any other time!”\n\nSo quietly and honestly she said it, with her hands upon his shoulders—both hands now—and looking up into his face, like the picture of truth!\n\n“I think,” said my guardian, thoughtfully regarding her, “I think it must be somewhere written that the virtues of the mothers shall occasionally be visited on the children, as well as the sins of the father. Good night, my rosebud. Good night, little woman. Pleasant slumbers! Happy dreams!”\n\nThis was the first time I ever saw him follow Ada with his eyes with something of a shadow on their benevolent expression. I well remembered the look with which he had contemplated her and Richard when she was singing in the firelight; it was but a very little while since he had watched them passing down the room in which the sun was shining, and away into the shade; but his glance was changed, and even the silent look of confidence in me which now followed it once more was not quite so hopeful and untroubled as it had originally been.\n\nAda praised Richard more to me that night than ever she had praised him yet. She went to sleep with a little bracelet he had given her clasped upon her arm. I fancied she was dreaming of him when I kissed her cheek after she had slept an hour and saw how tranquil and happy she looked.\n\nFor I was so little inclined to sleep myself that night that I sat up working. It would not be worth mentioning for its own sake, but I was wakeful and rather low-spirited. I don’t know why. At least I don’t think I know why. At least, perhaps I do, but I don’t think it matters.\n\nAt any rate, I made up my mind to be so dreadfully industrious that I would leave myself not a moment’s leisure to be low-spirited. For I naturally said, “Esther! You to be low-spirited. YOU!” And it really was time to say so, for I—yes, I really did see myself in the glass, almost crying. “As if you had anything to make you unhappy, instead of everything to make you happy, you ungrateful heart!” said I.\n\nIf I could have made myself go to sleep, I would have done it directly, but not being able to do that, I took out of my basket some ornamental work for our house (I mean Bleak House) that I was busy with at that time and sat down to it with great determination. It was necessary to count all the stitches in that work, and I resolved to go on with it until I couldn’t keep my eyes open, and then to go to bed.\n\nI soon found myself very busy. But I had left some silk downstairs in a work-table drawer in the temporary growlery, and coming to a stop for want of it, I took my candle and went softly down to get it. To my great surprise, on going in I found my guardian still there, and sitting looking at the ashes. He was lost in thought, his book lay unheeded by his side, his silvered iron-grey hair was scattered confusedly upon his forehead as though his hand had been wandering among it while his thoughts were elsewhere, and his face looked worn. Almost frightened by coming upon him so unexpectedly, I stood still for a moment and should have retired without speaking had he not, in again passing his hand abstractedly through his hair, seen me and started.\n\n“Esther!”\n\nI told him what I had come for.\n\n“At work so late, my dear?”\n\n“I am working late to-night,” said I, “because I couldn’t sleep and wished to tire myself. But, dear guardian, you are late too, and look weary. You have no trouble, I hope, to keep you waking?”\n\n“None, little woman, that YOU would readily understand,” said he.\n\nHe spoke in a regretful tone so new to me that I inwardly repeated, as if that would help me to his meaning, “That I could readily understand!”\n\n“Remain a moment, Esther,” said he, “You were in my thoughts.”\n\n“I hope I was not the trouble, guardian?”\n\nHe slightly waved his hand and fell into his usual manner. The change was so remarkable, and he appeared to make it by dint of so much self-command, that I found myself again inwardly repeating, “None that I could understand!”\n\n“Little woman,” said my guardian, “I was thinking—that is, I have been thinking since I have been sitting here—that you ought to know of your own history all I know. It is very little. Next to nothing.”\n\n“Dear guardian,” I replied, “when you spoke to me before on that subject—”\n\n“But since then,” he gravely interposed, anticipating what I meant to say, “I have reflected that your having anything to ask me, and my having anything to tell you, are different considerations, Esther. It is perhaps my duty to impart to you the little I know.”\n\n“If you think so, guardian, it is right.”\n\n“I think so,” he returned very gently, and kindly, and very distinctly. “My dear, I think so now. If any real disadvantage can attach to your position in the mind of any man or woman worth a thought, it is right that you at least of all the world should not magnify it to yourself by having vague impressions of its nature.”\n\nI sat down and said after a little effort to be as calm as I ought to be, “One of my earliest remembrances, guardian, is of these words: ‘Your mother, Esther, is your disgrace, and you were hers. The time will come, and soon enough, when you will understand this better, and will feel it too, as no one save a woman can.’” I had covered my face with my hands in repeating the words, but I took them away now with a better kind of shame, I hope, and told him that to him I owed the blessing that I had from my childhood to that hour never, never, never felt it. He put up his hand as if to stop me. I well knew that he was never to be thanked, and said no more.\n\n“Nine years, my dear,” he said after thinking for a little while, “have passed since I received a letter from a lady living in seclusion, written with a stern passion and power that rendered it unlike all other letters I have ever read. It was written to me (as it told me in so many words), perhaps because it was the writer’s idiosyncrasy to put that trust in me, perhaps because it was mine to justify it. It told me of a child, an orphan girl then twelve years old, in some such cruel words as those which live in your remembrance. It told me that the writer had bred her in secrecy from her birth, had blotted out all trace of her existence, and that if the writer were to die before the child became a woman, she would be left entirely friendless, nameless, and unknown. It asked me to consider if I would, in that case, finish what the writer had begun.”\n\nI listened in silence and looked attentively at him.\n\n“Your early recollection, my dear, will supply the gloomy medium through which all this was seen and expressed by the writer, and the distorted religion which clouded her mind with impressions of the need there was for the child to expiate an offence of which she was quite innocent. I felt concerned for the little creature, in her darkened life, and replied to the letter.”\n\nI took his hand and kissed it.\n\n“It laid the injunction on me that I should never propose to see the writer, who had long been estranged from all intercourse with the world, but who would see a confidential agent if I would appoint one. I accredited Mr. Kenge. The lady said, of her own accord and not of his seeking, that her name was an assumed one. That she was, if there were any ties of blood in such a case, the child’s aunt. That more than this she would never (and he was well persuaded of the steadfastness of her resolution) for any human consideration disclose. My dear, I have told you all.”\n\nI held his hand for a little while in mine.\n\n“I saw my ward oftener than she saw me,” he added, cheerily making light of it, “and I always knew she was beloved, useful, and happy. She repays me twenty-thousandfold, and twenty more to that, every hour in every day!”\n\n“And oftener still,” said I, “she blesses the guardian who is a father to her!”\n\nAt the word father, I saw his former trouble come into his face. He subdued it as before, and it was gone in an instant; but it had been there and it had come so swiftly upon my words that I felt as if they had given him a shock. I again inwardly repeated, wondering, “That I could readily understand. None that I could readily understand!” No, it was true. I did not understand it. Not for many and many a day.\n\n“Take a fatherly good night, my dear,” said he, kissing me on the forehead, “and so to rest. These are late hours for working and thinking. You do that for all of us, all day long, little housekeeper!”\n\nI neither worked nor thought any more that night. I opened my grateful heart to heaven in thankfulness for its providence to me and its care of me, and fell asleep.\n\nWe had a visitor next day. Mr. Allan Woodcourt came. He came to take leave of us; he had settled to do so beforehand. He was going to China and to India as a surgeon on board ship. He was to be away a long, long time.\n\nI believe—at least I know—that he was not rich. All his widowed mother could spare had been spent in qualifying him for his profession. It was not lucrative to a young practitioner, with very little influence in London; and although he was, night and day, at the service of numbers of poor people and did wonders of gentleness and skill for them, he gained very little by it in money. He was seven years older than I. Not that I need mention it, for it hardly seems to belong to anything.\n\nI think—I mean, he told us—that he had been in practice three or four years and that if he could have hoped to contend through three or four more, he would not have made the voyage on which he was bound. But he had no fortune or private means, and so he was going away. He had been to see us several times altogether. We thought it a pity he should go away. Because he was distinguished in his art among those who knew it best, and some of the greatest men belonging to it had a high opinion of him.\n\nWhen he came to bid us good-bye, he brought his mother with him for the first time. She was a pretty old lady, with bright black eyes, but she seemed proud. She came from Wales and had had, a long time ago, an eminent person for an ancestor, of the name of Morgan ap-Kerrig—of some place that sounded like Gimlet—who was the most illustrious person that ever was known and all of whose relations were a sort of royal family. He appeared to have passed his life in always getting up into mountains and fighting somebody; and a bard whose name sounded like Crumlinwallinwer had sung his praises in a piece which was called, as nearly as I could catch it, Mewlinnwillinwodd.\n\nMrs. Woodcourt, after expatiating to us on the fame of her great kinsman, said that no doubt wherever her son Allan went he would remember his pedigree and would on no account form an alliance below it. She told him that there were many handsome English ladies in India who went out on speculation, and that there were some to be picked up with property, but that neither charms nor wealth would suffice for the descendant from such a line without birth, which must ever be the first consideration. She talked so much about birth that for a moment I half fancied, and with pain—But what an idle fancy to suppose that she could think or care what MINE was!\n\nMr. Woodcourt seemed a little distressed by her prolixity, but he was too considerate to let her see it and contrived delicately to bring the conversation round to making his acknowledgments to my guardian for his hospitality and for the very happy hours—he called them the very happy hours—he had passed with us. The recollection of them, he said, would go with him wherever he went and would be always treasured. And so we gave him our hands, one after another—at least, they did—and I did; and so he put his lips to Ada’s hand—and to mine; and so he went away upon his long, long voyage!\n\nI was very busy indeed all day and wrote directions home to the servants, and wrote notes for my guardian, and dusted his books and papers, and jingled my housekeeping keys a good deal, one way and another. I was still busy between the lights, singing and working by the window, when who should come in but Caddy, whom I had no expectation of seeing!\n\n“Why, Caddy, my dear,” said I, “what beautiful flowers!”\n\nShe had such an exquisite little nosegay in her hand.\n\n“Indeed, I think so, Esther,” replied Caddy. “They are the loveliest I ever saw.”\n\n“Prince, my dear?” said I in a whisper.\n\n“No,” answered Caddy, shaking her head and holding them to me to smell. “Not Prince.”\n\n“Well, to be sure, Caddy!” said I. “You must have two lovers!”\n\n“What? Do they look like that sort of thing?” said Caddy.\n\n“Do they look like that sort of thing?” I repeated, pinching her cheek.\n\nCaddy only laughed in return, and telling me that she had come for half an hour, at the expiration of which time Prince would be waiting for her at the corner, sat chatting with me and Ada in the window, every now and then handing me the flowers again or trying how they looked against my hair. At last, when she was going, she took me into my room and put them in my dress.\n\n“For me?” said I, surprised.\n\n“For you,” said Caddy with a kiss. “They were left behind by somebody.”\n\n“Left behind?”\n\n“At poor Miss Flite’s,” said Caddy. “Somebody who has been very good to her was hurrying away an hour ago to join a ship and left these flowers behind. No, no! Don’t take them out. Let the pretty little things lie here,” said Caddy, adjusting them with a careful hand, “because I was present myself, and I shouldn’t wonder if somebody left them on purpose!”\n\n“Do they look like that sort of thing?” said Ada, coming laughingly behind me and clasping me merrily round the waist. “Oh, yes, indeed they do, Dame Durden! They look very, very like that sort of thing. Oh, very like it indeed, my dear!”\n\nLady Dedlock\nIt was not so easy as it had appeared at first to arrange for Richard’s making a trial of Mr. Kenge’s office. Richard himself was the chief impediment. As soon as he had it in his power to leave Mr. Badger at any moment, he began to doubt whether he wanted to leave him at all. He didn’t know, he said, really. It wasn’t a bad profession; he couldn’t assert that he disliked it; perhaps he liked it as well as he liked any other—suppose he gave it one more chance! Upon that, he shut himself up for a few weeks with some books and some bones and seemed to acquire a considerable fund of information with great rapidity. His fervour, after lasting about a month, began to cool, and when it was quite cooled, began to grow warm again. His vacillations between law and medicine lasted so long that midsummer arrived before he finally separated from Mr. Badger and entered on an experimental course of Messrs. Kenge and Carboy. For all his waywardness, he took great credit to himself as being determined to be in earnest “this time.” And he was so good-natured throughout, and in such high spirits, and so fond of Ada, that it was very difficult indeed to be otherwise than pleased with him.\n\n“As to Mr. Jarndyce,” who, I may mention, found the wind much given, during this period, to stick in the east; “As to Mr. Jarndyce,” Richard would say to me, “he is the finest fellow in the world, Esther! I must be particularly careful, if it were only for his satisfaction, to take myself well to task and have a regular wind-up of this business now.”\n\nThe idea of his taking himself well to task, with that laughing face and heedless manner and with a fancy that everything could catch and nothing could hold, was ludicrously anomalous. However, he told us between-whiles that he was doing it to such an extent that he wondered his hair didn’t turn grey. His regular wind-up of the business was (as I have said) that he went to Mr. Kenge’s about midsummer to try how he liked it.\n\nAll this time he was, in money affairs, what I have described him in a former illustration—generous, profuse, wildly careless, but fully persuaded that he was rather calculating and prudent. I happened to say to Ada, in his presence, half jestingly, half seriously, about the time of his going to Mr. Kenge’s, that he needed to have Fortunatus’ purse, he made so light of money, which he answered in this way, “My jewel of a dear cousin, you hear this old woman! Why does she say that? Because I gave eight pounds odd (or whatever it was) for a certain neat waistcoat and buttons a few days ago. Now, if I had stayed at Badger’s I should have been obliged to spend twelve pounds at a blow for some heart-breaking lecture-fees. So I make four pounds—in a lump—by the transaction!”\n\nIt was a question much discussed between him and my guardian what arrangements should be made for his living in London while he experimented on the law, for we had long since gone back to Bleak House, and it was too far off to admit of his coming there oftener than once a week. My guardian told me that if Richard were to settle down at Mr. Kenge’s he would take some apartments or chambers where we too could occasionally stay for a few days at a time; “but, little woman,” he added, rubbing his head very significantly, “he hasn’t settled down there yet!” The discussions ended in our hiring for him, by the month, a neat little furnished lodging in a quiet old house near Queen Square. He immediately began to spend all the money he had in buying the oddest little ornaments and luxuries for this lodging; and so often as Ada and I dissuaded him from making any purchase that he had in contemplation which was particularly unnecessary and expensive, he took credit for what it would have cost and made out that to spend anything less on something else was to save the difference.\n\nWhile these affairs were in abeyance, our visit to Mr. Boythorn’s was postponed. At length, Richard having taken possession of his lodging, there was nothing to prevent our departure. He could have gone with us at that time of the year very well, but he was in the full novelty of his new position and was making most energetic attempts to unravel the mysteries of the fatal suit. Consequently we went without him, and my darling was delighted to praise him for being so busy.\n\nWe made a pleasant journey down into Lincolnshire by the coach and had an entertaining companion in Mr. Skimpole. His furniture had been all cleared off, it appeared, by the person who took possession of it on his blue-eyed daughter’s birthday, but he seemed quite relieved to think that it was gone. Chairs and table, he said, were wearisome objects; they were monotonous ideas, they had no variety of expression, they looked you out of countenance, and you looked them out of countenance. How pleasant, then, to be bound to no particular chairs and tables, but to sport like a butterfly among all the furniture on hire, and to flit from rosewood to mahogany, and from mahogany to walnut, and from this shape to that, as the humour took one!\n\n“The oddity of the thing is,” said Mr. Skimpole with a quickened sense of the ludicrous, “that my chairs and tables were not paid for, and yet my landlord walks off with them as composedly as possible. Now, that seems droll! There is something grotesque in it. The chair and table merchant never engaged to pay my landlord my rent. Why should my landlord quarrel with HIM? If I have a pimple on my nose which is disagreeable to my landlord’s peculiar ideas of beauty, my landlord has no business to scratch my chair and table merchant’s nose, which has no pimple on it. His reasoning seems defective!”\n\n“Well,” said my guardian good-humouredly, “it’s pretty clear that whoever became security for those chairs and tables will have to pay for them.”\n\n“Exactly!” returned Mr. Skimpole. “That’s the crowning point of unreason in the business! I said to my landlord, ‘My good man, you are not aware that my excellent friend Jarndyce will have to pay for those things that you are sweeping off in that indelicate manner. Have you no consideration for HIS property?’ He hadn’t the least.”\n\n“And refused all proposals,” said my guardian.\n\n“Refused all proposals,” returned Mr. Skimpole. “I made him business proposals. I had him into my room. I said, ‘You are a man of business, I believe?’ He replied, ‘I am.’ ‘Very well,’ said I, ‘now let us be business-like. Here is an inkstand, here are pens and paper, here are wafers. What do you want? I have occupied your house for a considerable period, I believe to our mutual satisfaction until this unpleasant misunderstanding arose; let us be at once friendly and business-like. What do you want?’ In reply to this, he made use of the figurative expression—which has something Eastern about it—that he had never seen the colour of my money. ‘My amiable friend,’ said I, ‘I never have any money. I never know anything about money.’ ‘Well, sir,’ said he, ‘what do you offer if I give you time?’ ‘My good fellow,’ said I, ‘I have no idea of time; but you say you are a man of business, and whatever you can suggest to be done in a business-like way with pen, and ink, and paper—and wafers—I am ready to do. Don’t pay yourself at another man’s expense (which is foolish), but be business-like!’ However, he wouldn’t be, and there was an end of it.”\n\nIf these were some of the inconveniences of Mr. Skimpole’s childhood, it assuredly possessed its advantages too. On the journey he had a very good appetite for such refreshment as came in our way (including a basket of choice hothouse peaches), but never thought of paying for anything. So when the coachman came round for his fee, he pleasantly asked him what he considered a very good fee indeed, now—a liberal one—and on his replying half a crown for a single passenger, said it was little enough too, all things considered, and left Mr. Jarndyce to give it him.\n\nIt was delightful weather. The green corn waved so beautifully, the larks sang so joyfully, the hedges were so full of wild flowers, the trees were so thickly out in leaf, the bean-fields, with a light wind blowing over them, filled the air with such a delicious fragrance! Late in the afternoon we came to the market-town where we were to alight from the coach—a dull little town with a church-spire, and a marketplace, and a market-cross, and one intensely sunny street, and a pond with an old horse cooling his legs in it, and a very few men sleepily lying and standing about in narrow little bits of shade. After the rustling of the leaves and the waving of the corn all along the road, it looked as still, as hot, as motionless a little town as England could produce.\n\nAt the inn we found Mr. Boythorn on horseback, waiting with an open carriage to take us to his house, which was a few miles off. He was overjoyed to see us and dismounted with great alacrity.\n\n“By heaven!” said he after giving us a courteous greeting. “This a most infamous coach. It is the most flagrant example of an abominable public vehicle that ever encumbered the face of the earth. It is twenty-five minutes after its time this afternoon. The coachman ought to be put to death!”\n\n“IS he after his time?” said Mr. Skimpole, to whom he happened to address himself. “You know my infirmity.”\n\n“Twenty-five minutes! Twenty-six minutes!” replied Mr. Boythorn, referring to his watch. “With two ladies in the coach, this scoundrel has deliberately delayed his arrival six and twenty minutes. Deliberately! It is impossible that it can be accidental! But his father—and his uncle—were the most profligate coachmen that ever sat upon a box.”\n\nWhile he said this in tones of the greatest indignation, he handed us into the little phaeton with the utmost gentleness and was all smiles and pleasure.\n\n“I am sorry, ladies,” he said, standing bare-headed at the carriage-door when all was ready, “that I am obliged to conduct you nearly two miles out of the way. But our direct road lies through Sir Leicester Dedlock’s park, and in that fellow’s property I have sworn never to set foot of mine, or horse’s foot of mine, pending the present relations between us, while I breathe the breath of life!” And here, catching my guardian’s eye, he broke into one of his tremendous laughs, which seemed to shake even the motionless little market-town.\n\n“Are the Dedlocks down here, Lawrence?” said my guardian as we drove along and Mr. Boythorn trotted on the green turf by the roadside.\n\n“Sir Arrogant Numskull is here,” replied Mr. Boythorn. “Ha ha ha! Sir Arrogant is here, and I am glad to say, has been laid by the heels here. My Lady,” in naming whom he always made a courtly gesture as if particularly to exclude her from any part in the quarrel, “is expected, I believe, daily. I am not in the least surprised that she postpones her appearance as long as possible. Whatever can have induced that transcendent woman to marry that effigy and figure-head of a baronet is one of the most impenetrable mysteries that ever baffled human inquiry. Ha ha ha ha!”\n\n“I suppose,” said my guardian, laughing, “WE may set foot in the park while we are here? The prohibition does not extend to us, does it?”\n\n“I can lay no prohibition on my guests,” he said, bending his head to Ada and me with the smiling politeness which sat so gracefully upon him, “except in the matter of their departure. I am only sorry that I cannot have the happiness of being their escort about Chesney Wold, which is a very fine place! But by the light of this summer day, Jarndyce, if you call upon the owner while you stay with me, you are likely to have but a cool reception. He carries himself like an eight-day clock at all times, like one of a race of eight-day clocks in gorgeous cases that never go and never went—Ha ha ha!—but he will have some extra stiffness, I can promise you, for the friends of his friend and neighbour Boythorn!”\n\n“I shall not put him to the proof,” said my guardian. “He is as indifferent to the honour of knowing me, I dare say, as I am to the honour of knowing him. The air of the grounds and perhaps such a view of the house as any other sightseer might get are quite enough for me.”\n\n“Well!” said Mr. Boythorn. “I am glad of it on the whole. It’s in better keeping. I am looked upon about here as a second Ajax defying the lightning. Ha ha ha ha! When I go into our little church on a Sunday, a considerable part of the inconsiderable congregation expect to see me drop, scorched and withered, on the pavement under the Dedlock displeasure. Ha ha ha ha! I have no doubt he is surprised that I don’t. For he is, by heaven, the most self-satisfied, and the shallowest, and the most coxcombical and utterly brainless ass!”\n\nOur coming to the ridge of a hill we had been ascending enabled our friend to point out Chesney Wold itself to us and diverted his attention from its master.\n\nIt was a picturesque old house in a fine park richly wooded. Among the trees and not far from the residence he pointed out the spire of the little church of which he had spoken. Oh, the solemn woods over which the light and shadow travelled swiftly, as if heavenly wings were sweeping on benignant errands through the summer air; the smooth green slopes, the glittering water, the garden where the flowers were so symmetrically arranged in clusters of the richest colours, how beautiful they looked! The house, with gable and chimney, and tower, and turret, and dark doorway, and broad terrace-walk, twining among the balustrades of which, and lying heaped upon the vases, there was one great flush of roses, seemed scarcely real in its light solidity and in the serene and peaceful hush that rested on all around it. To Ada and to me, that above all appeared the pervading influence. On everything, house, garden, terrace, green slopes, water, old oaks, fern, moss, woods again, and far away across the openings in the prospect to the distance lying wide before us with a purple bloom upon it, there seemed to be such undisturbed repose.\n\nWhen we came into the little village and passed a small inn with the sign of the Dedlock Arms swinging over the road in front, Mr. Boythorn interchanged greetings with a young gentleman sitting on a bench outside the inn-door who had some fishing-tackle lying beside him.\n\n“That’s the housekeeper’s grandson, Mr. Rouncewell by name,” said, he, “and he is in love with a pretty girl up at the house. Lady Dedlock has taken a fancy to the pretty girl and is going to keep her about her own fair person—an honour which my young friend himself does not at all appreciate. However, he can’t marry just yet, even if his Rosebud were willing; so he is fain to make the best of it. In the meanwhile, he comes here pretty often for a day or two at a time to—fish. Ha ha ha ha!”\n\n“Are he and the pretty girl engaged, Mr. Boythorn?” asked Ada.\n\n“Why, my dear Miss Clare,” he returned, “I think they may perhaps understand each other; but you will see them soon, I dare say, and I must learn from you on such a point—not you from me.”\n\nAda blushed, and Mr. Boythorn, trotting forward on his comely grey horse, dismounted at his own door and stood ready with extended arm and uncovered head to welcome us when we arrived.\n\nHe lived in a pretty house, formerly the parsonage house, with a lawn in front, a bright flower-garden at the side, and a well-stocked orchard and kitchen-garden in the rear, enclosed with a venerable wall that had of itself a ripened ruddy look. But, indeed, everything about the place wore an aspect of maturity and abundance. The old lime-tree walk was like green cloisters, the very shadows of the cherry-trees and apple-trees were heavy with fruit, the gooseberry-bushes were so laden that their branches arched and rested on the earth, the strawberries and raspberries grew in like profusion, and the peaches basked by the hundred on the wall. Tumbled about among the spread nets and the glass frames sparkling and winking in the sun there were such heaps of drooping pods, and marrows, and cucumbers, that every foot of ground appeared a vegetable treasury, while the smell of sweet herbs and all kinds of wholesome growth (to say nothing of the neighbouring meadows where the hay was carrying) made the whole air a great nosegay. Such stillness and composure reigned within the orderly precincts of the old red wall that even the feathers hung in garlands to scare the birds hardly stirred; and the wall had such a ripening influence that where, here and there high up, a disused nail and scrap of list still clung to it, it was easy to fancy that they had mellowed with the changing seasons and that they had rusted and decayed according to the common fate.\n\nThe house, though a little disorderly in comparison with the garden, was a real old house with settles in the chimney of the brick-floored kitchen and great beams across the ceilings. On one side of it was the terrible piece of ground in dispute, where Mr. Boythorn maintained a sentry in a smock-frock day and night, whose duty was supposed to be, in cases of aggression, immediately to ring a large bell hung up there for the purpose, to unchain a great bull-dog established in a kennel as his ally, and generally to deal destruction on the enemy. Not content with these precautions, Mr. Boythorn had himself composed and posted there, on painted boards to which his name was attached in large letters, the following solemn warnings: “Beware of the bull-dog. He is most ferocious. Lawrence Boythorn.” “The blunderbus is loaded with slugs. Lawrence Boythorn.” “Man-traps and spring-guns are set here at all times of the day and night. Lawrence Boythorn.” “Take notice. That any person or persons audaciously presuming to trespass on this property will be punished with the utmost severity of private chastisement and prosecuted with the utmost rigour of the law. Lawrence Boythorn.” These he showed us from the drawing-room window, while his bird was hopping about his head, and he laughed, “Ha ha ha ha! Ha ha ha ha!” to that extent as he pointed them out that I really thought he would have hurt himself.\n\n“But this is taking a good deal of trouble,” said Mr. Skimpole in his light way, “when you are not in earnest after all.”\n\n“Not in earnest!” returned Mr. Boythorn with unspeakable warmth. “Not in earnest! If I could have hoped to train him, I would have bought a lion instead of that dog and would have turned him loose upon the first intolerable robber who should dare to make an encroachment on my rights. Let Sir Leicester Dedlock consent to come out and decide this question by single combat, and I will meet him with any weapon known to mankind in any age or country. I am that much in earnest. Not more!”\n\nWe arrived at his house on a Saturday. On the Sunday morning we all set forth to walk to the little church in the park. Entering the park, almost immediately by the disputed ground, we pursued a pleasant footpath winding among the verdant turf and the beautiful trees until it brought us to the church-porch.\n\nThe congregation was extremely small and quite a rustic one with the exception of a large muster of servants from the house, some of whom were already in their seats, while others were yet dropping in. There were some stately footmen, and there was a perfect picture of an old coachman, who looked as if he were the official representative of all the pomps and vanities that had ever been put into his coach. There was a very pretty show of young women, and above them, the handsome old face and fine responsible portly figure of the housekeeper towered pre-eminent. The pretty girl of whom Mr. Boythorn had told us was close by her. She was so very pretty that I might have known her by her beauty even if I had not seen how blushingly conscious she was of the eyes of the young fisherman, whom I discovered not far off. One face, and not an agreeable one, though it was handsome, seemed maliciously watchful of this pretty girl, and indeed of every one and everything there. It was a Frenchwoman’s.\n\nAs the bell was yet ringing and the great people were not yet come, I had leisure to glance over the church, which smelt as earthy as a grave, and to think what a shady, ancient, solemn little church it was. The windows, heavily shaded by trees, admitted a subdued light that made the faces around me pale, and darkened the old brasses in the pavement and the time and damp-worn monuments, and rendered the sunshine in the little porch, where a monotonous ringer was working at the bell, inestimably bright. But a stir in that direction, a gathering of reverential awe in the rustic faces, and a blandly ferocious assumption on the part of Mr. Boythorn of being resolutely unconscious of somebody’s existence forewarned me that the great people were come and that the service was going to begin.\n\n“‘Enter not into judgment with thy servant, O Lord, for in thy sight—’”\n\nShall I ever forget the rapid beating at my heart, occasioned by the look I met as I stood up! Shall I ever forget the manner in which those handsome proud eyes seemed to spring out of their languor and to hold mine! It was only a moment before I cast mine down—released again, if I may say so—on my book; but I knew the beautiful face quite well in that short space of time.\n\nAnd, very strangely, there was something quickened within me, associated with the lonely days at my godmother’s; yes, away even to the days when I had stood on tiptoe to dress myself at my little glass after dressing my doll. And this, although I had never seen this lady’s face before in all my life—I was quite sure of it—absolutely certain.\n\nIt was easy to know that the ceremonious, gouty, grey-haired gentleman, the only other occupant of the great pew, was Sir Leicester Dedlock, and that the lady was Lady Dedlock. But why her face should be, in a confused way, like a broken glass to me, in which I saw scraps of old remembrances, and why I should be so fluttered and troubled (for I was still) by having casually met her eyes, I could not think.\n\nI felt it to be an unmeaning weakness in me and tried to overcome it by attending to the words I heard. Then, very strangely, I seemed to hear them, not in the reader’s voice, but in the well-remembered voice of my godmother. This made me think, did Lady Dedlock’s face accidentally resemble my godmother’s? It might be that it did, a little; but the expression was so different, and the stern decision which had worn into my godmother’s face, like weather into rocks, was so completely wanting in the face before me that it could not be that resemblance which had struck me. Neither did I know the loftiness and haughtiness of Lady Dedlock’s face, at all, in any one. And yet I—I, little Esther Summerson, the child who lived a life apart and on whose birthday there was no rejoicing—seemed to arise before my own eyes, evoked out of the past by some power in this fashionable lady, whom I not only entertained no fancy that I had ever seen, but whom I perfectly well knew I had never seen until that hour.\n\nIt made me tremble so to be thrown into this unaccountable agitation that I was conscious of being distressed even by the observation of the French maid, though I knew she had been looking watchfully here, and there, and everywhere, from the moment of her coming into the church. By degrees, though very slowly, I at last overcame my strange emotion. After a long time, I looked towards Lady Dedlock again. It was while they were preparing to sing, before the sermon. She took no heed of me, and the beating at my heart was gone. Neither did it revive for more than a few moments when she once or twice afterwards glanced at Ada or at me through her glass.\n\nThe service being concluded, Sir Leicester gave his arm with much taste and gallantry to Lady Dedlock—though he was obliged to walk by the help of a thick stick—and escorted her out of church to the pony carriage in which they had come. The servants then dispersed, and so did the congregation, whom Sir Leicester had contemplated all along (Mr. Skimpole said to Mr. Boythorn’s infinite delight) as if he were a considerable landed proprietor in heaven.\n\n“He believes he is!” said Mr. Boythorn. “He firmly believes it. So did his father, and his grandfather, and his great-grandfather!”\n\n“Do you know,” pursued Mr. Skimpole very unexpectedly to Mr. Boythorn, “it’s agreeable to me to see a man of that sort.”\n\n“IS it!” said Mr. Boythorn.\n\n“Say that he wants to patronize me,” pursued Mr. Skimpole. “Very well! I don’t object.”\n\n“I do,” said Mr. Boythorn with great vigour.\n\n“Do you really?” returned Mr. Skimpole in his easy light vein. “But that’s taking trouble, surely. And why should you take trouble? Here am I, content to receive things childishly as they fall out, and I never take trouble! I come down here, for instance, and I find a mighty potentate exacting homage. Very well! I say ‘Mighty potentate, here IS my homage! It’s easier to give it than to withhold it. Here it is. If you have anything of an agreeable nature to show me, I shall be happy to see it; if you have anything of an agreeable nature to give me, I shall be happy to accept it.’ Mighty potentate replies in effect, ‘This is a sensible fellow. I find him accord with my digestion and my bilious system. He doesn’t impose upon me the necessity of rolling myself up like a hedgehog with my points outward. I expand, I open, I turn my silver lining outward like Milton’s cloud, and it’s more agreeable to both of us.’ That’s my view of such things, speaking as a child!”\n\n“But suppose you went down somewhere else to-morrow,” said Mr. Boythorn, “where there was the opposite of that fellow—or of this fellow. How then?”\n\n“How then?” said Mr. Skimpole with an appearance of the utmost simplicity and candour. “Just the same then! I should say, ‘My esteemed Boythorn’—to make you the personification of our imaginary friend—‘my esteemed Boythorn, you object to the mighty potentate? Very good. So do I. I take it that my business in the social system is to be agreeable; I take it that everybody’s business in the social system is to be agreeable. It’s a system of harmony, in short. Therefore if you object, I object. Now, excellent Boythorn, let us go to dinner!’”\n\n“But excellent Boythorn might say,” returned our host, swelling and growing very red, “I’ll be—”\n\n“I understand,” said Mr. Skimpole. “Very likely he would.”\n\n“—if I WILL go to dinner!” cried Mr. Boythorn in a violent burst and stopping to strike his stick upon the ground. “And he would probably add, ‘Is there such a thing as principle, Mr. Harold Skimpole?’”\n\n“To which Harold Skimpole would reply, you know,” he returned in his gayest manner and with his most ingenuous smile, “‘Upon my life I have not the least idea! I don’t know what it is you call by that name, or where it is, or who possesses it. If you possess it and find it comfortable, I am quite delighted and congratulate you heartily. But I know nothing about it, I assure you; for I am a mere child, and I lay no claim to it, and I don’t want it!’ So, you see, excellent Boythorn and I would go to dinner after all!”\n\nThis was one of many little dialogues between them which I always expected to end, and which I dare say would have ended under other circumstances, in some violent explosion on the part of our host. But he had so high a sense of his hospitable and responsible position as our entertainer, and my guardian laughed so sincerely at and with Mr. Skimpole, as a child who blew bubbles and broke them all day long, that matters never went beyond this point. Mr. Skimpole, who always seemed quite unconscious of having been on delicate ground, then betook himself to beginning some sketch in the park which he never finished, or to playing fragments of airs on the piano, or to singing scraps of songs, or to lying down on his back under a tree and looking at the sky—which he couldn’t help thinking, he said, was what he was meant for; it suited him so exactly.\n\n“Enterprise and effort,” he would say to us (on his back), “are delightful to me. I believe I am truly cosmopolitan. I have the deepest sympathy with them. I lie in a shady place like this and think of adventurous spirits going to the North Pole or penetrating to the heart of the Torrid Zone with admiration. Mercenary creatures ask, ‘What is the use of a man’s going to the North Pole? What good does it do?’ I can’t say; but, for anything I CAN say, he may go for the purpose—though he don’t know it—of employing my thoughts as I lie here. Take an extreme case. Take the case of the slaves on American plantations. I dare say they are worked hard, I dare say they don’t altogether like it. I dare say theirs is an unpleasant experience on the whole; but they people the landscape for me, they give it a poetry for me, and perhaps that is one of the pleasanter objects of their existence. I am very sensible of it, if it be, and I shouldn’t wonder if it were!”\n\nI always wondered on these occasions whether he ever thought of Mrs. Skimpole and the children, and in what point of view they presented themselves to his cosmopolitan mind. So far as I could understand, they rarely presented themselves at all.\n\nThe week had gone round to the Saturday following that beating of my heart in the church; and every day had been so bright and blue that to ramble in the woods, and to see the light striking down among the transparent leaves and sparkling in the beautiful interlacings of the shadows of the trees, while the birds poured out their songs and the air was drowsy with the hum of insects, had been most delightful. We had one favourite spot, deep in moss and last year’s leaves, where there were some felled trees from which the bark was all stripped off. Seated among these, we looked through a green vista supported by thousands of natural columns, the whitened stems of trees, upon a distant prospect made so radiant by its contrast with the shade in which we sat and made so precious by the arched perspective through which we saw it that it was like a glimpse of the better land. Upon the Saturday we sat here, Mr. Jarndyce, Ada, and I, until we heard thunder muttering in the distance and felt the large raindrops rattle through the leaves.\n\nThe weather had been all the week extremely sultry, but the storm broke so suddenly—upon us, at least, in that sheltered spot—that before we reached the outskirts of the wood the thunder and lightning were frequent and the rain came plunging through the leaves as if every drop were a great leaden bead. As it was not a time for standing among trees, we ran out of the wood, and up and down the moss-grown steps which crossed the plantation-fence like two broad-staved ladders placed back to back, and made for a keeper’s lodge which was close at hand. We had often noticed the dark beauty of this lodge standing in a deep twilight of trees, and how the ivy clustered over it, and how there was a steep hollow near, where we had once seen the keeper’s dog dive down into the fern as if it were water.\n\nThe lodge was so dark within, now the sky was overcast, that we only clearly saw the man who came to the door when we took shelter there and put two chairs for Ada and me. The lattice-windows were all thrown open, and we sat just within the doorway watching the storm. It was grand to see how the wind awoke, and bent the trees, and drove the rain before it like a cloud of smoke; and to hear the solemn thunder and to see the lightning; and while thinking with awe of the tremendous powers by which our little lives are encompassed, to consider how beneficent they are and how upon the smallest flower and leaf there was already a freshness poured from all this seeming rage which seemed to make creation new again.\n\n“Is it not dangerous to sit in so exposed a place?”\n\n“Oh, no, Esther dear!” said Ada quietly.\nAda said it to me, but I had not spoken.\n\nThe beating of my heart came back again. I had never heard the voice, as I had never seen the face, but it affected me in the same strange way. Again, in a moment, there arose before my mind innumerable pictures of myself.\n\nLady Dedlock had taken shelter in the lodge before our arrival there and had come out of the gloom within. She stood behind my chair with her hand upon it. I saw her with her hand close to my shoulder when I turned my head.\n\n“I have frightened you?” she said.\n\nNo. It was not fright. Why should I be frightened!\n\n“I believe,” said Lady Dedlock to my guardian, “I have the pleasure of speaking to Mr. Jarndyce.”\n\n“Your remembrance does me more honour than I had supposed it would, Lady Dedlock,” he returned.\n\n“I recognized you in church on Sunday. I am sorry that any local disputes of Sir Leicester’s—they are not of his seeking, however, I believe—should render it a matter of some absurd difficulty to show you any attention here.”\n\n“I am aware of the circumstances,” returned my guardian with a smile, “and am sufficiently obliged.”\n\nShe had given him her hand in an indifferent way that seemed habitual to her and spoke in a correspondingly indifferent manner, though in a very pleasant voice. She was as graceful as she was beautiful, perfectly self-possessed, and had the air, I thought, of being able to attract and interest any one if she had thought it worth her while. The keeper had brought her a chair on which she sat in the middle of the porch between us.\n\n“Is the young gentleman disposed of whom you wrote to Sir Leicester about and whose wishes Sir Leicester was sorry not to have it in his power to advance in any way?” she said over her shoulder to my guardian.\n\n“I hope so,” said he.\n\nShe seemed to respect him and even to wish to conciliate him. There was something very winning in her haughty manner, and it became more familiar—I was going to say more easy, but that could hardly be—as she spoke to him over her shoulder.\n\n“I presume this is your other ward, Miss Clare?”\n\nHe presented Ada, in form.\n\n“You will lose the disinterested part of your Don Quixote character,” said Lady Dedlock to Mr. Jarndyce over her shoulder again, “if you only redress the wrongs of beauty like this. But present me,” and she turned full upon me, “to this young lady too!”\n\n“Miss Summerson really is my ward,” said Mr. Jarndyce. “I am responsible to no Lord Chancellor in her case.”\n\n“Has Miss Summerson lost both her parents?” said my Lady.\n“Yes.”\n\n“She is very fortunate in her guardian.”\n\nLady Dedlock looked at me, and I looked at her and said I was indeed. All at once she turned from me with a hasty air, almost expressive of displeasure or dislike, and spoke to him over her shoulder again.\n\n“Ages have passed since we were in the habit of meeting, Mr. Jarndyce.”\n\n“A long time. At least I thought it was a long time, until I saw you last Sunday,” he returned.\n\n“What! Even you are a courtier, or think it necessary to become one to me!” she said with some disdain. “I have achieved that reputation, I suppose.”\n\n“You have achieved so much, Lady Dedlock,” said my guardian, “that you pay some little penalty, I dare say. But none to me.”\n\n“So much!” she repeated, slightly laughing. “Yes!”\n\nWith her air of superiority, and power, and fascination, and I know not what, she seemed to regard Ada and me as little more than children. So, as she slightly laughed and afterwards sat looking at the rain, she was as self-possessed and as free to occupy herself with her own thoughts as if she had been alone.\n\n“I think you knew my sister when we were abroad together better than you know me?” she said, looking at him again.\n\n“Yes, we happened to meet oftener,” he returned.\n\n“We went our several ways,” said Lady Dedlock, “and had little in common even before we agreed to differ. It is to be regretted, I suppose, but it could not be helped.”\n\nLady Dedlock again sat looking at the rain. The storm soon began to pass upon its way. The shower greatly abated, the lightning ceased, the thunder rolled among the distant hills, and the sun began to glisten on the wet leaves and the falling rain. As we sat there, silently, we saw a little pony phaeton coming towards us at a merry pace.\n\n“The messenger is coming back, my Lady,” said the keeper, “with the carriage.”\n\nAs it drove up, we saw that there were two people inside. There alighted from it, with some cloaks and wrappers, first the Frenchwoman whom I had seen in church, and secondly the pretty girl, the Frenchwoman with a defiant confidence, the pretty girl confused and hesitating.\n\n“What now?” said Lady Dedlock. “Two!”\n\n“I am your maid, my Lady, at the present,” said the Frenchwoman. “The message was for the attendant.”\n\n“I was afraid you might mean me, my Lady,” said the pretty girl.\n\n“I did mean you, child,” replied her mistress calmly. “Put that shawl on me.”\n\nShe slightly stooped her shoulders to receive it, and the pretty girl lightly dropped it in its place. The Frenchwoman stood unnoticed, looking on with her lips very tightly set.\n\n“I am sorry,” said Lady Dedlock to Mr. Jarndyce, “that we are not likely to renew our former acquaintance. You will allow me to send the carriage back for your two wards. It shall be here directly.”\n\nBut as he would on no account accept this offer, she took a graceful leave of Ada—none of me—and put her hand upon his proffered arm, and got into the carriage, which was a little, low, park carriage with a hood.\n\n“Come in, child,” she said to the pretty girl; “I shall want you. Go on!”\n\nThe carriage rolled away, and the Frenchwoman, with the wrappers she had brought hanging over her arm, remained standing where she had alighted.\n\nI suppose there is nothing pride can so little bear with as pride itself, and that she was punished for her imperious manner. Her retaliation was the most singular I could have imagined. She remained perfectly still until the carriage had turned into the drive, and then, without the least discomposure of countenance, slipped off her shoes, left them on the ground, and walked deliberately in the same direction through the wettest of the wet grass.\n\n“Is that young woman mad?” said my guardian.\n\n“Oh, no, sir!” said the keeper, who, with his wife, was looking after her. “Hortense is not one of that sort. She has as good a head-piece as the best. But she’s mortal high and passionate—powerful high and passionate; and what with having notice to leave, and having others put above her, she don’t take kindly to it.”\n\n“But why should she walk shoeless through all that water?” said my guardian.\n\n“Why, indeed, sir, unless it is to cool her down!” said the man.\n\n“Or unless she fancies it’s blood,” said the woman. “She’d as soon walk through that as anything else, I think, when her own’s up!”\n\nWe passed not far from the house a few minutes afterwards. Peaceful as it had looked when we first saw it, it looked even more so now, with a diamond spray glittering all about it, a light wind blowing, the birds no longer hushed but singing strongly, everything refreshed by the late rain, and the little carriage shining at the doorway like a fairy carriage made of silver. Still, very steadfastly and quietly walking towards it, a peaceful figure too in the landscape, went Mademoiselle Hortense, shoeless, through the wet grass.\n\nMoving On\nIt is the long vacation in the regions of Chancery Lane. The good ships Law and Equity, those teak-built, copper-bottomed, iron-fastened, brazen-faced, and not by any means fast-sailing clippers are laid up in ordinary. The Flying Dutchman, with a crew of ghostly clients imploring all whom they may encounter to peruse their papers, has drifted, for the time being, heaven knows where. The courts are all shut up; the public offices lie in a hot sleep. Westminster Hall itself is a shady solitude where nightingales might sing, and a tenderer class of suitors than is usually found there, walk.\n\nThe Temple, Chancery Lane, Serjeants’ Inn, and Lincoln’s Inn even unto the Fields are like tidal harbours at low water, where stranded proceedings, offices at anchor, idle clerks lounging on lop-sided stools that will not recover their perpendicular until the current of Term sets in, lie high and dry upon the ooze of the long vacation. Outer doors of chambers are shut up by the score, messages and parcels are to be left at the Porter’s Lodge by the bushel. A crop of grass would grow in the chinks of the stone pavement outside Lincoln’s Inn Hall, but that the ticket-porters, who have nothing to do beyond sitting in the shade there, with their white aprons over their heads to keep the flies off, grub it up and eat it thoughtfully.\n\nThere is only one judge in town. Even he only comes twice a week to sit in chambers. If the country folks of those assize towns on his circuit could see him now! No full-bottomed wig, no red petticoats, no fur, no javelin-men, no white wands. Merely a close-shaved gentleman in white trousers and a white hat, with sea-bronze on the judicial countenance, and a strip of bark peeled by the solar rays from the judicial nose, who calls in at the shell-fish shop as he comes along and drinks iced ginger-beer!\n\nThe bar of England is scattered over the face of the earth. How England can get on through four long summer months without its bar—which is its acknowledged refuge in adversity and its only legitimate triumph in prosperity—is beside the question; assuredly that shield and buckler of Britannia are not in present wear. The learned gentleman who is always so tremendously indignant at the unprecedented outrage committed on the feelings of his client by the opposite party that he never seems likely to recover it is doing infinitely better than might be expected in Switzerland. The learned gentleman who does the withering business and who blights all opponents with his gloomy sarcasm is as merry as a grig at a French watering-place. The learned gentleman who weeps by the pint on the smallest provocation has not shed a tear these six weeks. The very learned gentleman who has cooled the natural heat of his gingery complexion in pools and fountains of law until he has become great in knotty arguments for term-time, when he poses the drowsy bench with legal “chaff,” inexplicable to the uninitiated and to most of the initiated too, is roaming, with a characteristic delight in aridity and dust, about Constantinople. Other dispersed fragments of the same great palladium are to be found on the canals of Venice, at the second cataract of the Nile, in the baths of Germany, and sprinkled on the sea-sand all over the English coast. Scarcely one is to be encountered in the deserted region of Chancery Lane. If such a lonely member of the bar do flit across the waste, and come upon a prowling suitor who is unable to leave off haunting the scenes of his anxiety, they frighten one another and retreat into opposite shades.\n\nIt is the hottest long vacation known for many years. All the young clerks are madly in love, and according to their various degrees, pine for bliss with the beloved object, at Margate, Ramsgate, or Gravesend. All the middle-aged clerks think their families too large. All the unowned dogs who stray into the Inns of Court and pant about staircases and other dry places seeking water give short howls of aggravation. All the blind men’s dogs in the streets draw their masters against pumps or trip them over buckets. A shop with a sun-blind, and a watered pavement, and a bowl of gold and silver fish in the window, is a sanctuary. Temple Bar gets so hot that it is, to the adjacent Strand and Fleet Street, what a heater is in an urn, and keeps them simmering all night.\n\nThere are offices about the Inns of Court in which a man might be cool, if any coolness were worth purchasing at such a price in dullness; but the little thoroughfares immediately outside those retirements seem to blaze. In Mr. Krook’s court, it is so hot that the people turn their houses inside out and sit in chairs upon the pavement—Mr. Krook included, who there pursues his studies, with his cat (who never is too hot) by his side. The Sol’s Arms has discontinued the Harmonic Meetings for the season, and Little Swills is engaged at the Pastoral Gardens down the river, where he comes out in quite an innocent manner and sings comic ditties of a juvenile complexion calculated (as the bill says) not to wound the feelings of the most fastidious mind.\n\nOver all the legal neighbourhood there hangs, like some great veil of rust or gigantic cobweb, the idleness and pensiveness of the long vacation. Mr. Snagsby, law-stationer of Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, is sensible of the influence not only in his mind as a sympathetic and contemplative man, but also in his business as a law-stationer aforesaid. He has more leisure for musing in Staple Inn and in the Rolls Yard during the long vacation than at other seasons, and he says to the two ’prentices, what a thing it is in such hot weather to think that you live in an island with the sea a-rolling and a-bowling right round you.\n\nGuster is busy in the little drawing-room on this present afternoon in the long vacation, when Mr. and Mrs. Snagsby have it in contemplation to receive company. The expected guests are rather select than numerous, being Mr. and Mrs. Chadband and no more. From Mr. Chadband’s being much given to describe himself, both verbally and in writing, as a vessel, he is occasionally mistaken by strangers for a gentleman connected with navigation, but he is, as he expresses it, “in the ministry.” Mr. Chadband is attached to no particular denomination and is considered by his persecutors to have nothing so very remarkable to say on the greatest of subjects as to render his volunteering, on his own account, at all incumbent on his conscience; but he has his followers, and Mrs. Snagsby is of the number. Mrs. Snagsby has but recently taken a passage upward by the vessel, Chadband; and her attention was attracted to that Bark A 1, when she was something flushed by the hot weather.\n\n“My little woman,” says Mr. Snagsby to the sparrows in Staple Inn, “likes to have her religion rather sharp, you see!”\n\nSo Guster, much impressed by regarding herself for the time as the handmaid of Chadband, whom she knows to be endowed with the gift of holding forth for four hours at a stretch, prepares the little drawing-room for tea. All the furniture is shaken and dusted, the portraits of Mr. and Mrs. Snagsby are touched up with a wet cloth, the best tea-service is set forth, and there is excellent provision made of dainty new bread, crusty twists, cool fresh butter, thin slices of ham, tongue, and German sausage, and delicate little rows of anchovies nestling in parsley, not to mention new-laid eggs, to be brought up warm in a napkin, and hot buttered toast. For Chadband is rather a consuming vessel—the persecutors say a gorging vessel—and can wield such weapons of the flesh as a knife and fork remarkably well.\n\nMr. Snagsby in his best coat, looking at all the preparations when they are completed and coughing his cough of deference behind his hand, says to Mrs. Snagsby, “At what time did you expect Mr. and Mrs. Chadband, my love?”\n\n“At six,” says Mrs. Snagsby.\n\nMr. Snagsby observes in a mild and casual way that “it’s gone that.”\n\n“Perhaps you’d like to begin without them,” is Mrs. Snagsby’s reproachful remark.\n\nMr. Snagsby does look as if he would like it very much, but he says, with his cough of mildness, “No, my dear, no. I merely named the time.”\n\n“What’s time,” says Mrs. Snagsby, “to eternity?”\n\n“Very true, my dear,” says Mr. Snagsby. “Only when a person lays in victuals for tea, a person does it with a view—perhaps—more to time. And when a time is named for having tea, it’s better to come up to it.”\n\n“To come up to it!” Mrs. Snagsby repeats with severity. “Up to it! As if Mr. Chadband was a fighter!”\n\n“Not at all, my dear,” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\nHere, Guster, who had been looking out of the bedroom window, comes rustling and scratching down the little staircase like a popular ghost, and falling flushed into the drawing-room, announces that Mr. and Mrs. Chadband have appeared in the court. The bell at the inner door in the passage immediately thereafter tinkling, she is admonished by Mrs. Snagsby, on pain of instant reconsignment to her patron saint, not to omit the ceremony of announcement. Much discomposed in her nerves (which were previously in the best order) by this threat, she so fearfully mutilates that point of state as to announce “Mr. and Mrs. Cheeseming, least which, Imeantersay, whatsername!” and retires conscience-stricken from the presence.\n\nMr. Chadband is a large yellow man with a fat smile and a general appearance of having a good deal of train oil in his system. Mrs. Chadband is a stern, severe-looking, silent woman. Mr. Chadband moves softly and cumbrously, not unlike a bear who has been taught to walk upright. He is very much embarrassed about the arms, as if they were inconvenient to him and he wanted to grovel, is very much in a perspiration about the head, and never speaks without first putting up his great hand, as delivering a token to his hearers that he is going to edify them.\n\n“My friends,” says Mr. Chadband, “peace be on this house! On the master thereof, on the mistress thereof, on the young maidens, and on the young men! My friends, why do I wish for peace? What is peace? Is it war? No. Is it strife? No. Is it lovely, and gentle, and beautiful, and pleasant, and serene, and joyful? Oh, yes! Therefore, my friends, I wish for peace, upon you and upon yours.”\n\nIn consequence of Mrs. Snagsby looking deeply edified, Mr. Snagsby thinks it expedient on the whole to say amen, which is well received.\n\n“Now, my friends,” proceeds Mr. Chadband, “since I am upon this theme—”\n\nGuster presents herself. Mrs. Snagsby, in a spectral bass voice and without removing her eyes from Chadband, says with dreadful distinctness, “Go away!”\n\n“Now, my friends,” says Chadband, “since I am upon this theme, and in my lowly path improving it—”\n\nGuster is heard unaccountably to murmur “one thousing seven hundred and eighty-two.” The spectral voice repeats more solemnly, “Go away!”\n\n“Now, my friends,” says Mr. Chadband, “we will inquire in a spirit of love—”\n\nStill Guster reiterates “one thousing seven hundred and eighty-two.”\n\nMr. Chadband, pausing with the resignation of a man accustomed to be persecuted and languidly folding up his chin into his fat smile, says, “Let us hear the maiden! Speak, maiden!”\n\n“One thousing seven hundred and eighty-two, if you please, sir. Which he wish to know what the shilling ware for,” says Guster, breathless.\n\n“For?” returns Mrs. Chadband. “For his fare!”\n\nGuster replied that “he insistes on one and eightpence or on summonsizzing the party.” Mrs. Snagsby and Mrs. Chadband are proceeding to grow shrill in indignation when Mr. Chadband quiets the tumult by lifting up his hand.\n\n“My friends,” says he, “I remember a duty unfulfilled yesterday. It is right that I should be chastened in some penalty. I ought not to murmur. Rachael, pay the eightpence!”\n\nWhile Mrs. Snagsby, drawing her breath, looks hard at Mr. Snagsby, as who should say, “You hear this apostle!” and while Mr. Chadband glows with humility and train oil, Mrs. Chadband pays the money. It is Mr. Chadband’s habit—it is the head and front of his pretensions indeed—to keep this sort of debtor and creditor account in the smallest items and to post it publicly on the most trivial occasions.\n\n“My friends,” says Chadband, “eightpence is not much; it might justly have been one and fourpence; it might justly have been half a crown. O let us be joyful, joyful! O let us be joyful!”\n\nWith which remark, which appears from its sound to be an extract in verse, Mr. Chadband stalks to the table, and before taking a chair, lifts up his admonitory hand.\n\n“My friends,” says he, “what is this which we now behold as being spread before us? Refreshment. Do we need refreshment then, my friends? We do. And why do we need refreshment, my friends? Because we are but mortal, because we are but sinful, because we are but of the earth, because we are not of the air. Can we fly, my friends? We cannot. Why can we not fly, my friends?”\n\nMr. Snagsby, presuming on the success of his last point, ventures to observe in a cheerful and rather knowing tone, “No wings.” But is immediately frowned down by Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“I say, my friends,” pursues Mr. Chadband, utterly rejecting and obliterating Mr. Snagsby’s suggestion, “why can we not fly? Is it because we are calculated to walk? It is. Could we walk, my friends, without strength? We could not. What should we do without strength, my friends? Our legs would refuse to bear us, our knees would double up, our ankles would turn over, and we should come to the ground. Then from whence, my friends, in a human point of view, do we derive the strength that is necessary to our limbs? Is it,” says Chadband, glancing over the table, “from bread in various forms, from butter which is churned from the milk which is yielded unto us by the cow, from the eggs which are laid by the fowl, from ham, from tongue, from sausage, and from such like? It is. Then let us partake of the good things which are set before us!”\n\nThe persecutors denied that there was any particular gift in Mr. Chadband’s piling verbose flights of stairs, one upon another, after this fashion. But this can only be received as a proof of their determination to persecute, since it must be within everybody’s experience that the Chadband style of oratory is widely received and much admired.\n\nMr. Chadband, however, having concluded for the present, sits down at Mr. Snagsby’s table and lays about him prodigiously. The conversion of nutriment of any sort into oil of the quality already mentioned appears to be a process so inseparable from the constitution of this exemplary vessel that in beginning to eat and drink, he may be described as always becoming a kind of considerable oil mills or other large factory for the production of that article on a wholesale scale. On the present evening of the long vacation, in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, he does such a powerful stroke of business that the warehouse appears to be quite full when the works cease.\n\nAt this period of the entertainment, Guster, who has never recovered her first failure, but has neglected no possible or impossible means of bringing the establishment and herself into contempt—among which may be briefly enumerated her unexpectedly performing clashing military music on Mr. Chadband’s head with plates, and afterwards crowning that gentleman with muffins—at which period of the entertainment, Guster whispers Mr. Snagsby that he is wanted.\n\n“And being wanted in the—not to put too fine a point upon it—in the shop,” says Mr. Snagsby, rising, “perhaps this good company will excuse me for half a minute.”\n\nMr. Snagsby descends and finds the two ’prentices intently contemplating a police constable, who holds a ragged boy by the arm.\n\n“Why, bless my heart,” says Mr. Snagsby, “what’s the matter!”\n\n“This boy,” says the constable, “although he’s repeatedly told to, won’t move on—”\n\n“I’m always a-moving on, sar,” cries the boy, wiping away his grimy tears with his arm. “I’ve always been a-moving and a-moving on, ever since I was born. Where can I possibly move to, sir, more nor I do move!”\n\n“He won’t move on,” says the constable calmly, with a slight professional hitch of his neck involving its better settlement in his stiff stock, “although he has been repeatedly cautioned, and therefore I am obliged to take him into custody. He’s as obstinate a young gonoph as I know. He WON’T move on.”\n\n“Oh, my eye! Where can I move to!” cries the boy, clutching quite desperately at his hair and beating his bare feet upon the floor of Mr. Snagsby’s passage.\n\n“Don’t you come none of that or I shall make blessed short work of you!” says the constable, giving him a passionless shake. “My instructions are that you are to move on. I have told you so five hundred times.”\n\n“But where?” cries the boy.\n\n“Well! Really, constable, you know,” says Mr. Snagsby wistfully, and coughing behind his hand his cough of great perplexity and doubt, “really, that does seem a question. Where, you know?”\n\n“My instructions don’t go to that,” replies the constable. “My instructions are that this boy is to move on.”\n\nDo you hear, Jo? It is nothing to you or to any one else that the great lights of the parliamentary sky have failed for some few years in this business to set you the example of moving on. The one grand recipe remains for you—the profound philosophical prescription—the be-all and the end-all of your strange existence upon earth. Move on! You are by no means to move off, Jo, for the great lights can’t at all agree about that. Move on!\n\nMr. Snagsby says nothing to this effect, says nothing at all indeed, but coughs his forlornest cough, expressive of no thoroughfare in any direction. By this time Mr. and Mrs. Chadband and Mrs. Snagsby, hearing the altercation, have appeared upon the stairs. Guster having never left the end of the passage, the whole household are assembled.\n\n“The simple question is, sir,” says the constable, “whether you know this boy. He says you do.”\n\nMrs. Snagsby, from her elevation, instantly cries out, “No he don’t!”\n\n“My lit-tle woman!” says Mr. Snagsby, looking up the staircase. “My love, permit me! Pray have a moment’s patience, my dear. I do know something of this lad, and in what I know of him, I can’t say that there’s any harm; perhaps on the contrary, constable.” To whom the law-stationer relates his Joful and woeful experience, suppressing the half-crown fact.\n\n“Well!” says the constable, “so far, it seems, he had grounds for what he said. When I took him into custody up in Holborn, he said you knew him. Upon that, a young man who was in the crowd said he was acquainted with you, and you were a respectable housekeeper, and if I’d call and make the inquiry, he’d appear. The young man don’t seem inclined to keep his word, but—Oh! Here IS the young man!”\n\nEnter Mr. Guppy, who nods to Mr. Snagsby and touches his hat with the chivalry of clerkship to the ladies on the stairs.\n\n“I was strolling away from the office just now when I found this row going on,” says Mr. Guppy to the law-stationer, “and as your name was mentioned, I thought it was right the thing should be looked into.”\n\n“It was very good-natured of you, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby, “and I am obliged to you.” And Mr. Snagsby again relates his experience, again suppressing the half-crown fact.\n\n“Now, I know where you live,” says the constable, then, to Jo. “You live down in Tom-all-Alone’s. That’s a nice innocent place to live in, ain’t it?”\n\n“I can’t go and live in no nicer place, sir,” replies Jo. “They wouldn’t have nothink to say to me if I wos to go to a nice innocent place fur to live. Who ud go and let a nice innocent lodging to such a reg’lar one as me!”\n\n“You are very poor, ain’t you?” says the constable.\n\n“Yes, I am indeed, sir, wery poor in gin’ral,” replies Jo. “I leave you to judge now! I shook these two half-crowns out of him,” says the constable, producing them to the company, “in only putting my hand upon him!”\n\n“They’re wot’s left, Mr. Snagsby,” says Jo, “out of a sov-ring as wos give me by a lady in a wale as sed she wos a servant and as come to my crossin one night and asked to be showd this ’ere ouse and the ouse wot him as you giv the writin to died at, and the berrin-ground wot he’s berrid in. She ses to me she ses ‘are you the boy at the inkwhich?’ she ses. I ses ‘yes’ I ses. She ses to me she ses ‘can you show me all them places?’ I ses ‘yes I can’ I ses. And she ses to me ‘do it’ and I dun it and she giv me a sov’ring and hooked it. And I an’t had much of the sov’ring neither,” says Jo, with dirty tears, “fur I had to pay five bob, down in Tom-all-Alone’s, afore they’d square it fur to give me change, and then a young man he thieved another five while I was asleep and another boy he thieved ninepence and the landlord he stood drains round with a lot more on it.”\n\n“You don’t expect anybody to believe this, about the lady and the sovereign, do you?” says the constable, eyeing him aside with ineffable disdain.\n\n“I don’t know as I do, sir,” replies Jo. “I don’t expect nothink at all, sir, much, but that’s the true hist’ry on it.”\n\n“You see what he is!” the constable observes to the audience. “Well, Mr. Snagsby, if I don’t lock him up this time, will you engage for his moving on?”\n\n“No!” cries Mrs. Snagsby from the stairs.\n\n“My little woman!” pleads her husband. “Constable, I have no doubt he’ll move on. You know you really must do it,” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\n“I’m everyways agreeable, sir,” says the hapless Jo.\n\n“Do it, then,” observes the constable. “You know what you have got to do. Do it! And recollect you won’t get off so easy next time. Catch hold of your money. Now, the sooner you’re five mile off, the better for all parties.”\n\nWith this farewell hint and pointing generally to the setting sun as a likely place to move on to, the constable bids his auditors good afternoon and makes the echoes of Cook’s Court perform slow music for him as he walks away on the shady side, carrying his iron-bound hat in his hand for a little ventilation.\n\nNow, Jo’s improbable story concerning the lady and the sovereign has awakened more or less the curiosity of all the company. Mr. Guppy, who has an inquiring mind in matters of evidence and who has been suffering severely from the lassitude of the long vacation, takes that interest in the case that he enters on a regular cross-examination of the witness, which is found so interesting by the ladies that Mrs. Snagsby politely invites him to step upstairs and drink a cup of tea, if he will excuse the disarranged state of the tea-table, consequent on their previous exertions. Mr. Guppy yielding his assent to this proposal, Jo is requested to follow into the drawing-room doorway, where Mr. Guppy takes him in hand as a witness, patting him into this shape, that shape, and the other shape like a butterman dealing with so much butter, and worrying him according to the best models. Nor is the examination unlike many such model displays, both in respect of its eliciting nothing and of its being lengthy, for Mr. Guppy is sensible of his talent, and Mrs. Snagsby feels not only that it gratifies her inquisitive disposition, but that it lifts her husband’s establishment higher up in the law. During the progress of this keen encounter, the vessel Chadband, being merely engaged in the oil trade, gets aground and waits to be floated off.\n\n“Well!” says Mr. Guppy. “Either this boy sticks to it like cobbler’s-wax or there is something out of the common here that beats anything that ever came into my way at Kenge and Carboy’s.”\n\nMrs. Chadband whispers Mrs. Snagsby, who exclaims, “You don’t say so!”\n\n“For years!” replied Mrs. Chadband.\n\n“Has known Kenge and Carboy’s office for years,” Mrs. Snagsby triumphantly explains to Mr. Guppy. “Mrs. Chadband—this gentleman’s wife—Reverend Mr. Chadband.”\n\n“Oh, indeed!” says Mr. Guppy.\n\n“Before I married my present husband,” says Mrs. Chadband.\n\n“Was you a party in anything, ma’am?” says Mr. Guppy, transferring his cross-examination.\n\n“No.”\n“NOT a party in anything, ma’am?” says Mr. Guppy.\n\nMrs. Chadband shakes her head.\n\n“Perhaps you were acquainted with somebody who was a party in something, ma’am?” says Mr. Guppy, who likes nothing better than to model his conversation on forensic principles.\n\n“Not exactly that, either,” replies Mrs. Chadband, humouring the joke with a hard-favoured smile.\n\n“Not exactly that, either!” repeats Mr. Guppy. “Very good. Pray, ma’am, was it a lady of your acquaintance who had some transactions (we will not at present say what transactions) with Kenge and Carboy’s office, or was it a gentleman of your acquaintance? Take time, ma’am. We shall come to it presently. Man or woman, ma’am?”\n\n“Neither,” says Mrs. Chadband as before.\n\n“Oh! A child!” says Mr. Guppy, throwing on the admiring Mrs. Snagsby the regular acute professional eye which is thrown on British jurymen. “Now, ma’am, perhaps you’ll have the kindness to tell us WHAT child.”\n\n“You have got it at last, sir,” says Mrs. Chadband with another hard-favoured smile. “Well, sir, it was before your time, most likely, judging from your appearance. I was left in charge of a child named Esther Summerson, who was put out in life by Messrs. Kenge and Carboy.”\n\n“Miss Summerson, ma’am!” cries Mr. Guppy, excited.\n\n“I call her Esther Summerson,” says Mrs. Chadband with austerity. “There was no Miss-ing of the girl in my time. It was Esther. ‘Esther, do this! Esther, do that!’ and she was made to do it.”\n\n“My dear ma’am,” returns Mr. Guppy, moving across the small apartment, “the humble individual who now addresses you received that young lady in London when she first came here from the establishment to which you have alluded. Allow me to have the pleasure of taking you by the hand.”\n\nMr. Chadband, at last seeing his opportunity, makes his accustomed signal and rises with a smoking head, which he dabs with his pocket-handkerchief. Mrs. Snagsby whispers “Hush!”\n\n“My friends,” says Chadband, “we have partaken in moderation” (which was certainly not the case so far as he was concerned) “of the comforts which have been provided for us. May this house live upon the fatness of the land; may corn and wine be plentiful therein; may it grow, may it thrive, may it prosper, may it advance, may it proceed, may it press forward! But, my friends, have we partaken of anything else? We have. My friends, of what else have we partaken? Of spiritual profit? Yes. From whence have we derived that spiritual profit? My young friend, stand forth!”\n\nJo, thus apostrophized, gives a slouch backward, and another slouch forward, and another slouch to each side, and confronts the eloquent Chadband with evident doubts of his intentions.\n\n“My young friend,” says Chadband, “you are to us a pearl, you are to us a diamond, you are to us a gem, you are to us a jewel. And why, my young friend?”\n\n“I don’t know,” replies Jo. “I don’t know nothink.”\n\n“My young friend,” says Chadband, “it is because you know nothing that you are to us a gem and jewel. For what are you, my young friend? Are you a beast of the field? No. A bird of the air? No. A fish of the sea or river? No. You are a human boy, my young friend. A human boy. O glorious to be a human boy! And why glorious, my young friend? Because you are capable of receiving the lessons of wisdom, because you are capable of profiting by this discourse which I now deliver for your good, because you are not a stick, or a staff, or a stock, or a stone, or a post, or a pillar.\n\nO running stream of sparkling joy To be a soaring human boy!\n\nAnd do you cool yourself in that stream now, my young friend? No. Why do you not cool yourself in that stream now? Because you are in a state of darkness, because you are in a state of obscurity, because you are in a state of sinfulness, because you are in a state of bondage. My young friend, what is bondage? Let us, in a spirit of love, inquire.”\n\nAt this threatening stage of the discourse, Jo, who seems to have been gradually going out of his mind, smears his right arm over his face and gives a terrible yawn. Mrs. Snagsby indignantly expresses her belief that he is a limb of the arch-fiend.\n\n“My friends,” says Mr. Chadband with his persecuted chin folding itself into its fat smile again as he looks round, “it is right that I should be humbled, it is right that I should be tried, it is right that I should be mortified, it is right that I should be corrected. I stumbled, on Sabbath last, when I thought with pride of my three hours’ improving. The account is now favourably balanced: my creditor has accepted a composition. O let us be joyful, joyful! O let us be joyful!”\n\nGreat sensation on the part of Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“My friends,” says Chadband, looking round him in conclusion, “I will not proceed with my young friend now. Will you come to-morrow, my young friend, and inquire of this good lady where I am to be found to deliver a discourse unto you, and will you come like the thirsty swallow upon the next day, and upon the day after that, and upon the day after that, and upon many pleasant days, to hear discourses?” (This with a cow-like lightness.)\n\nJo, whose immediate object seems to be to get away on any terms, gives a shuffling nod. Mr. Guppy then throws him a penny, and Mrs. Snagsby calls to Guster to see him safely out of the house. But before he goes downstairs, Mr. Snagsby loads him with some broken meats from the table, which he carries away, hugging in his arms.\n\nSo, Mr. Chadband—of whom the persecutors say that it is no wonder he should go on for any length of time uttering such abominable nonsense, but that the wonder rather is that he should ever leave off, having once the audacity to begin—retires into private life until he invests a little capital of supper in the oil-trade. Jo moves on, through the long vacation, down to Blackfriars Bridge, where he finds a baking stony corner wherein to settle to his repast.\n\nAnd there he sits, munching and gnawing, and looking up at the great cross on the summit of St. Paul’s Cathedral, glittering above a red-and-violet-tinted cloud of smoke. From the boy’s face one might suppose that sacred emblem to be, in his eyes, the crowning confusion of the great, confused city—so golden, so high up, so far out of his reach. There he sits, the sun going down, the river running fast, the crowd flowing by him in two streams—everything moving on to some purpose and to one end—until he is stirred up and told to “move on” too.\n\nA New Lodger\nThe long vacation saunters on towards term-time like an idle river very leisurely strolling down a flat country to the sea. Mr. Guppy saunters along with it congenially. He has blunted the blade of his penknife and broken the point off by sticking that instrument into his desk in every direction. Not that he bears the desk any ill will, but he must do something, and it must be something of an unexciting nature, which will lay neither his physical nor his intellectual energies under too heavy contribution. He finds that nothing agrees with him so well as to make little gyrations on one leg of his stool, and stab his desk, and gape.\n\nKenge and Carboy are out of town, and the articled clerk has taken out a shooting license and gone down to his father’s, and Mr. Guppy’s two fellow-stipendiaries are away on leave. Mr. Guppy and Mr. Richard Carstone divide the dignity of the office. But Mr. Carstone is for the time being established in Kenge’s room, whereat Mr. Guppy chafes. So exceedingly that he with biting sarcasm informs his mother, in the confidential moments when he sups with her off a lobster and lettuce in the Old Street Road, that he is afraid the office is hardly good enough for swells, and that if he had known there was a swell coming, he would have got it painted.\n\nMr. Guppy suspects everybody who enters on the occupation of a stool in Kenge and Carboy’s office of entertaining, as a matter of course, sinister designs upon him. He is clear that every such person wants to depose him. If he be ever asked how, why, when, or wherefore, he shuts up one eye and shakes his head. On the strength of these profound views, he in the most ingenious manner takes infinite pains to counterplot when there is no plot, and plays the deepest games of chess without any adversary.\n\nIt is a source of much gratification to Mr. Guppy, therefore, to find the new-comer constantly poring over the papers in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, for he well knows that nothing but confusion and failure can come of that. His satisfaction communicates itself to a third saunterer through the long vacation in Kenge and Carboy’s office, to wit, Young Smallweed.\n\nWhether Young Smallweed (metaphorically called Small and eke Chick Weed, as it were jocularly to express a fledgling) was ever a boy is much doubted in Lincoln’s Inn. He is now something under fifteen and an old limb of the law. He is facetiously understood to entertain a passion for a lady at a cigar-shop in the neighbourhood of Chancery Lane and for her sake to have broken off a contract with another lady, to whom he had been engaged some years. He is a town-made article, of small stature and weazen features, but may be perceived from a considerable distance by means of his very tall hat. To become a Guppy is the object of his ambition. He dresses at that gentleman (by whom he is patronized), talks at him, walks at him, founds himself entirely on him. He is honoured with Mr. Guppy’s particular confidence and occasionally advises him, from the deep wells of his experience, on difficult points in private life.\n\nMr. Guppy has been lolling out of window all the morning after trying all the stools in succession and finding none of them easy, and after several times putting his head into the iron safe with a notion of cooling it. Mr. Smallweed has been twice dispatched for effervescent drinks, and has twice mixed them in the two official tumblers and stirred them up with the ruler. Mr. Guppy propounds for Mr. Smallweed’s consideration the paradox that the more you drink the thirstier you are and reclines his head upon the window-sill in a state of hopeless languor.\n\nWhile thus looking out into the shade of Old Square, Lincoln’s Inn, surveying the intolerable bricks and mortar, Mr. Guppy becomes conscious of a manly whisker emerging from the cloistered walk below and turning itself up in the direction of his face. At the same time, a low whistle is wafted through the Inn and a suppressed voice cries, “Hip! Gup-py!”\n\n“Why, you don’t mean it!” says Mr. Guppy, aroused. “Small! Here’s Jobling!” Small’s head looks out of window too and nods to Jobling.\n\n“Where have you sprung up from?” inquires Mr. Guppy.\n\n“From the market-gardens down by Deptford. I can’t stand it any longer. I must enlist. I say! I wish you’d lend me half a crown. Upon my soul, I’m hungry.”\n\nJobling looks hungry and also has the appearance of having run to seed in the market-gardens down by Deptford.\n\n“I say! Just throw out half a crown if you have got one to spare. I want to get some dinner.”\n\n“Will you come and dine with me?” says Mr. Guppy, throwing out the coin, which Mr. Jobling catches neatly.\n\n“How long should I have to hold out?” says Jobling.\n\n“Not half an hour. I am only waiting here till the enemy goes,” returns Mr. Guppy, butting inward with his head.\n\n“What enemy?”\n\n“A new one. Going to be articled. Will you wait?”\n\n“Can you give a fellow anything to read in the meantime?” says Mr. Jobling.\n\nSmallweed suggests the law list. But Mr. Jobling declares with much earnestness that he “can’t stand it.”\n\n“You shall have the paper,” says Mr. Guppy. “He shall bring it down. But you had better not be seen about here. Sit on our staircase and read. It’s a quiet place.”\n\nJobling nods intelligence and acquiescence. The sagacious Smallweed supplies him with the newspaper and occasionally drops his eye upon him from the landing as a precaution against his becoming disgusted with waiting and making an untimely departure. At last the enemy retreats, and then Smallweed fetches Mr. Jobling up.\n\n“Well, and how are you?” says Mr. Guppy, shaking hands with him.\n\n“So, so. How are you?”\n\nMr. Guppy replying that he is not much to boast of, Mr. Jobling ventures on the question, “How is SHE?” This Mr. Guppy resents as a liberty, retorting, “Jobling, there ARE chords in the human mind—” Jobling begs pardon.\n\n“Any subject but that!” says Mr. Guppy with a gloomy enjoyment of his injury. “For there ARE chords, Jobling—”\n\nMr. Jobling begs pardon again.\n\nDuring this short colloquy, the active Smallweed, who is of the dinner party, has written in legal characters on a slip of paper, “Return immediately.” This notification to all whom it may concern, he inserts in the letter-box, and then putting on the tall hat at the angle of inclination at which Mr. Guppy wears his, informs his patron that they may now make themselves scarce.\n\nAccordingly they betake themselves to a neighbouring dining-house, of the class known among its frequenters by the denomination slap-bang, where the waitress, a bouncing young female of forty, is supposed to have made some impression on the susceptible Smallweed, of whom it may be remarked that he is a weird changeling to whom years are nothing. He stands precociously possessed of centuries of owlish wisdom. If he ever lay in a cradle, it seems as if he must have lain there in a tail-coat. He has an old, old eye, has Smallweed; and he drinks and smokes in a monkeyish way; and his neck is stiff in his collar; and he is never to be taken in; and he knows all about it, whatever it is. In short, in his bringing up he has been so nursed by Law and Equity that he has become a kind of fossil imp, to account for whose terrestrial existence it is reported at the public offices that his father was John Doe and his mother the only female member of the Roe family, also that his first long-clothes were made from a blue bag.\n\nInto the dining-house, unaffected by the seductive show in the window of artificially whitened cauliflowers and poultry, verdant baskets of peas, coolly blooming cucumbers, and joints ready for the spit, Mr. Smallweed leads the way. They know him there and defer to him. He has his favourite box, he bespeaks all the papers, he is down upon bald patriarchs, who keep them more than ten minutes afterwards. It is of no use trying him with anything less than a full-sized “bread” or proposing to him any joint in cut unless it is in the very best cut. In the matter of gravy he is adamant.\n\nConscious of his elfin power and submitting to his dread experience, Mr. Guppy consults him in the choice of that day’s banquet, turning an appealing look towards him as the waitress repeats the catalogue of viands and saying “What do YOU take, Chick?” Chick, out of the profundity of his artfulness, preferring “veal and ham and French beans—and don’t you forget the stuffing, Polly” (with an unearthly cock of his venerable eye), Mr. Guppy and Mr. Jobling give the like order. Three pint pots of half-and-half are superadded. Quickly the waitress returns bearing what is apparently a model of the Tower of Babel but what is really a pile of plates and flat tin dish-covers. Mr. Smallweed, approving of what is set before him, conveys intelligent benignity into his ancient eye and winks upon her. Then, amid a constant coming in, and going out, and running about, and a clatter of crockery, and a rumbling up and down of the machine which brings the nice cuts from the kitchen, and a shrill crying for more nice cuts down the speaking-pipe, and a shrill reckoning of the cost of nice cuts that have been disposed of, and a general flush and steam of hot joints, cut and uncut, and a considerably heated atmosphere in which the soiled knives and tablecloths seem to break out spontaneously into eruptions of grease and blotches of beer, the legal triumvirate appease their appetites.\n\nMr. Jobling is buttoned up closer than mere adornment might require. His hat presents at the rims a peculiar appearance of a glistening nature, as if it had been a favourite snail-promenade. The same phenomenon is visible on some parts of his coat, and particularly at the seams. He has the faded appearance of a gentleman in embarrassed circumstances; even his light whiskers droop with something of a shabby air.\n\nHis appetite is so vigorous that it suggests spare living for some little time back. He makes such a speedy end of his plate of veal and ham, bringing it to a close while his companions are yet midway in theirs, that Mr. Guppy proposes another. “Thank you, Guppy,” says Mr. Jobling, “I really don’t know but what I WILL take another.”\n\nAnother being brought, he falls to with great goodwill.\n\nMr. Guppy takes silent notice of him at intervals until he is half way through this second plate and stops to take an enjoying pull at his pint pot of half-and-half (also renewed) and stretches out his legs and rubs his hands. Beholding him in which glow of contentment, Mr. Guppy says, “You are a man again, Tony!”\n\n“Well, not quite yet,” says Mr. Jobling. “Say, just born.”\n\n“Will you take any other vegetables? Grass? Peas? Summer cabbage?”\n\n“Thank you, Guppy,” says Mr. Jobling. “I really don’t know but what I WILL take summer cabbage.”\n\nOrder given; with the sarcastic addition (from Mr. Smallweed) of “Without slugs, Polly!” And cabbage produced.\n\n“I am growing up, Guppy,” says Mr. Jobling, plying his knife and fork with a relishing steadiness.\n\n“Glad to hear it.”\n\n“In fact, I have just turned into my teens,” says Mr. Jobling.\n\nHe says no more until he has performed his task, which he achieves as Messrs. Guppy and Smallweed finish theirs, thus getting over the ground in excellent style and beating those two gentlemen easily by a veal and ham and a cabbage.\n\n“Now, Small,” says Mr. Guppy, “what would you recommend about pastry?”\n\n“Marrow puddings,” says Mr. Smallweed instantly.\n\n“Aye, aye!” cries Mr. Jobling with an arch look. “You’re there, are you? Thank you, Mr. Guppy, I don’t know but what I WILL take a marrow pudding.”\n\nThree marrow puddings being produced, Mr. Jobling adds in a pleasant humour that he is coming of age fast. To these succeed, by command of Mr. Smallweed, “three Cheshires,” and to those “three small rums.” This apex of the entertainment happily reached, Mr. Jobling puts up his legs on the carpeted seat (having his own side of the box to himself), leans against the wall, and says, “I am grown up now, Guppy. I have arrived at maturity.”\n\n“What do you think, now,” says Mr. Guppy, “about—you don’t mind Smallweed?”\n\n“Not the least in the world. I have the pleasure of drinking his good health.”\n\n“Sir, to you!” says Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“I was saying, what do you think NOW,” pursues Mr. Guppy, “of enlisting?”\n\n“Why, what I may think after dinner,” returns Mr. Jobling, “is one thing, my dear Guppy, and what I may think before dinner is another thing. Still, even after dinner, I ask myself the question, What am I to do? How am I to live? Ill fo manger, you know,” says Mr. Jobling, pronouncing that word as if he meant a necessary fixture in an English stable. “Ill fo manger. That’s the French saying, and mangering is as necessary to me as it is to a Frenchman. Or more so.”\n\nMr. Smallweed is decidedly of opinion “much more so.”\n\n“If any man had told me,” pursues Jobling, “even so lately as when you and I had the frisk down in Lincolnshire, Guppy, and drove over to see that house at Castle Wold—”\n\nMr. Smallweed corrects him—Chesney Wold.\n\n“Chesney Wold. (I thank my honourable friend for that cheer.) If any man had told me then that I should be as hard up at the present time as I literally find myself, I should have—well, I should have pitched into him,” says Mr. Jobling, taking a little rum-and-water with an air of desperate resignation; “I should have let fly at his head.”\n\n“Still, Tony, you were on the wrong side of the post then,” remonstrates Mr. Guppy. “You were talking about nothing else in the gig.”\n\n“Guppy,” says Mr. Jobling, “I will not deny it. I was on the wrong side of the post. But I trusted to things coming round.”\n\nThat very popular trust in flat things coming round! Not in their being beaten round, or worked round, but in their “coming” round! As though a lunatic should trust in the world’s “coming” triangular!\n\n“I had confident expectations that things would come round and be all square,” says Mr. Jobling with some vagueness of expression and perhaps of meaning too. “But I was disappointed. They never did. And when it came to creditors making rows at the office and to people that the office dealt with making complaints about dirty trifles of borrowed money, why there was an end of that connexion. And of any new professional connexion too, for if I was to give a reference to-morrow, it would be mentioned and would sew me up. Then what’s a fellow to do? I have been keeping out of the way and living cheap down about the market-gardens, but what’s the use of living cheap when you have got no money? You might as well live dear.”\n\n“Better,” Mr. Smallweed thinks.\n\n“Certainly. It’s the fashionable way; and fashion and whiskers have been my weaknesses, and I don’t care who knows it,” says Mr. Jobling. “They are great weaknesses—Damme, sir, they are great. Well,” proceeds Mr. Jobling after a defiant visit to his rum-and-water, “what can a fellow do, I ask you, BUT enlist?”\n\nMr. Guppy comes more fully into the conversation to state what, in his opinion, a fellow can do. His manner is the gravely impressive manner of a man who has not committed himself in life otherwise than as he has become the victim of a tender sorrow of the heart.\n\n“Jobling,” says Mr. Guppy, “myself and our mutual friend Smallweed—”\n\nMr. Smallweed modestly observes, “Gentlemen both!” and drinks.\n\n“—Have had a little conversation on this matter more than once since you—”\n\n“Say, got the sack!” cries Mr. Jobling bitterly. “Say it, Guppy. You mean it.”\n\n“No-o-o! Left the Inn,” Mr. Smallweed delicately suggests.\n\n“Since you left the Inn, Jobling,” says Mr. Guppy; “and I have mentioned to our mutual friend Smallweed a plan I have lately thought of proposing. You know Snagsby the stationer?”\n\n“I know there is such a stationer,” returns Mr. Jobling. “He was not ours, and I am not acquainted with him.”\n\n“He IS ours, Jobling, and I AM acquainted with him,” Mr. Guppy retorts. “Well, sir! I have lately become better acquainted with him through some accidental circumstances that have made me a visitor of his in private life. Those circumstances it is not necessary to offer in argument. They may—or they may not—have some reference to a subject which may—or may not—have cast its shadow on my existence.”\n\nAs it is Mr. Guppy’s perplexing way with boastful misery to tempt his particular friends into this subject, and the moment they touch it, to turn on them with that trenchant severity about the chords in the human mind, both Mr. Jobling and Mr. Smallweed decline the pitfall by remaining silent.\n\n“Such things may be,” repeats Mr. Guppy, “or they may not be. They are no part of the case. It is enough to mention that both Mr. and Mrs. Snagsby are very willing to oblige me and that Snagsby has, in busy times, a good deal of copying work to give out. He has all Tulkinghorn’s, and an excellent business besides. I believe if our mutual friend Smallweed were put into the box, he could prove this?”\n\nMr. Smallweed nods and appears greedy to be sworn.\n\n“Now, gentlemen of the jury,” says Mr. Guppy, “—I mean, now, Jobling—you may say this is a poor prospect of a living. Granted. But it’s better than nothing, and better than enlistment. You want time. There must be time for these late affairs to blow over. You might live through it on much worse terms than by writing for Snagsby.”\n\nMr. Jobling is about to interrupt when the sagacious Smallweed checks him with a dry cough and the words, “Hem! Shakspeare!”\n\n“There are two branches to this subject, Jobling,” says Mr. Guppy. “That is the first. I come to the second. You know Krook, the Chancellor, across the lane. Come, Jobling,” says Mr. Guppy in his encouraging cross-examination-tone, “I think you know Krook, the Chancellor, across the lane?”\n\n“I know him by sight,” says Mr. Jobling.\n\n“You know him by sight. Very well. And you know little Flite?”\n\n“Everybody knows her,” says Mr. Jobling.\n\n“Everybody knows her. VERY well. Now it has been one of my duties of late to pay Flite a certain weekly allowance, deducting from it the amount of her weekly rent, which I have paid (in consequence of instructions I have received) to Krook himself, regularly in her presence. This has brought me into communication with Krook and into a knowledge of his house and his habits. I know he has a room to let. You may live there at a very low charge under any name you like, as quietly as if you were a hundred miles off. He’ll ask no questions and would accept you as a tenant at a word from me—before the clock strikes, if you chose. And I tell you another thing, Jobling,” says Mr. Guppy, who has suddenly lowered his voice and become familiar again, “he’s an extraordinary old chap—always rummaging among a litter of papers and grubbing away at teaching himself to read and write, without getting on a bit, as it seems to me. He is a most extraordinary old chap, sir. I don’t know but what it might be worth a fellow’s while to look him up a bit.”\n\n“You don’t mean—” Mr. Jobling begins.\n\n“I mean,” returns Mr. Guppy, shrugging his shoulders with becoming modesty, “that I can’t make him out. I appeal to our mutual friend Smallweed whether he has or has not heard me remark that I can’t make him out.”\n\nMr. Smallweed bears the concise testimony, “A few!”\n\n“I have seen something of the profession and something of life, Tony,” says Mr. Guppy, “and it’s seldom I can’t make a man out, more or less. But such an old card as this, so deep, so sly, and secret (though I don’t believe he is ever sober), I never came across. Now, he must be precious old, you know, and he has not a soul about him, and he is reported to be immensely rich; and whether he is a smuggler, or a receiver, or an unlicensed pawnbroker, or a money-lender—all of which I have thought likely at different times—it might pay you to knock up a sort of knowledge of him. I don’t see why you shouldn’t go in for it, when everything else suits.”\n\nMr. Jobling, Mr. Guppy, and Mr. Smallweed all lean their elbows on the table and their chins upon their hands, and look at the ceiling. After a time, they all drink, slowly lean back, put their hands in their pockets, and look at one another.\n\n“If I had the energy I once possessed, Tony!” says Mr. Guppy with a sigh. “But there are chords in the human mind—”\n\nExpressing the remainder of the desolate sentiment in rum-and-water, Mr. Guppy concludes by resigning the adventure to Tony Jobling and informing him that during the vacation and while things are slack, his purse, “as far as three or four or even five pound goes,” will be at his disposal. “For never shall it be said,” Mr. Guppy adds with emphasis, “that William Guppy turned his back upon his friend!”\n\nThe latter part of the proposal is so directly to the purpose that Mr. Jobling says with emotion, “Guppy, my trump, your fist!” Mr. Guppy presents it, saying, “Jobling, my boy, there it is!” Mr. Jobling returns, “Guppy, we have been pals now for some years!” Mr. Guppy replies, “Jobling, we have.”\n\nThey then shake hands, and Mr. Jobling adds in a feeling manner, “Thank you, Guppy, I don’t know but what I WILL take another glass for old acquaintance sake.”\n\n“Krook’s last lodger died there,” observes Mr. Guppy in an incidental way.\n\n“Did he though!” says Mr. Jobling.\n\n“There was a verdict. Accidental death. You don’t mind that?”\n\n“No,” says Mr. Jobling, “I don’t mind it; but he might as well have died somewhere else. It’s devilish odd that he need go and die at MY place!” Mr. Jobling quite resents this liberty, several times returning to it with such remarks as, “There are places enough to die in, I should think!” or, “He wouldn’t have liked my dying at HIS place, I dare say!”\n\nHowever, the compact being virtually made, Mr. Guppy proposes to dispatch the trusty Smallweed to ascertain if Mr. Krook is at home, as in that case they may complete the negotiation without delay. Mr. Jobling approving, Smallweed puts himself under the tall hat and conveys it out of the dining-rooms in the Guppy manner. He soon returns with the intelligence that Mr. Krook is at home and that he has seen him through the shop-door, sitting in the back premises, sleeping “like one o’clock.”\n\n“Then I’ll pay,” says Mr. Guppy, “and we’ll go and see him. Small, what will it be?”\n\nMr. Smallweed, compelling the attendance of the waitress with one hitch of his eyelash, instantly replies as follows: “Four veals and hams is three, and four potatoes is three and four, and one summer cabbage is three and six, and three marrows is four and six, and six breads is five, and three Cheshires is five and three, and four half-pints of half-and-half is six and three, and four small rums is eight and three, and three Pollys is eight and six. Eight and six in half a sovereign, Polly, and eighteenpence out!”\n\nNot at all excited by these stupendous calculations, Smallweed dismisses his friends with a cool nod and remains behind to take a little admiring notice of Polly, as opportunity may serve, and to read the daily papers, which are so very large in proportion to himself, shorn of his hat, that when he holds up the Times to run his eye over the columns, he seems to have retired for the night and to have disappeared under the bedclothes.\n\nMr. Guppy and Mr. Jobling repair to the rag and bottle shop, where they find Krook still sleeping like one o’clock, that is to say, breathing stertorously with his chin upon his breast and quite insensible to any external sounds or even to gentle shaking. On the table beside him, among the usual lumber, stand an empty gin-bottle and a glass. The unwholesome air is so stained with this liquor that even the green eyes of the cat upon her shelf, as they open and shut and glimmer on the visitors, look drunk.\n\n“Hold up here!” says Mr. Guppy, giving the relaxed figure of the old man another shake. “Mr. Krook! Halloa, sir!”\n\nBut it would seem as easy to wake a bundle of old clothes with a spirituous heat smouldering in it. “Did you ever see such a stupor as he falls into, between drink and sleep?” says Mr. Guppy.\n\n“If this is his regular sleep,” returns Jobling, rather alarmed, “it’ll last a long time one of these days, I am thinking.”\n\n“It’s always more like a fit than a nap,” says Mr. Guppy, shaking him again. “Halloa, your lordship! Why, he might be robbed fifty times over! Open your eyes!”\n\nAfter much ado, he opens them, but without appearing to see his visitors or any other objects. Though he crosses one leg on another, and folds his hands, and several times closes and opens his parched lips, he seems to all intents and purposes as insensible as before.\n\n“He is alive, at any rate,” says Mr. Guppy. “How are you, my Lord Chancellor. I have brought a friend of mine, sir, on a little matter of business.”\n\nThe old man still sits, often smacking his dry lips without the least consciousness. After some minutes he makes an attempt to rise. They help him up, and he staggers against the wall and stares at them.\n\n“How do you do, Mr. Krook?” says Mr. Guppy in some discomfiture. “How do you do, sir? You are looking charming, Mr. Krook. I hope you are pretty well?”\n\nThe old man, in aiming a purposeless blow at Mr. Guppy, or at nothing, feebly swings himself round and comes with his face against the wall. So he remains for a minute or two, heaped up against it, and then staggers down the shop to the front door. The air, the movement in the court, the lapse of time, or the combination of these things recovers him. He comes back pretty steadily, adjusting his fur cap on his head and looking keenly at them.\n\n“Your servant, gentlemen; I’ve been dozing. Hi! I am hard to wake, odd times.”\n\n“Rather so, indeed, sir,” responds Mr. Guppy.\n\n“What? You’ve been a-trying to do it, have you?” says the suspicious Krook.\n\n“Only a little,” Mr. Guppy explains.\n\nThe old man’s eye resting on the empty bottle, he takes it up, examines it, and slowly tilts it upside down.\n\n“I say!” he cries like the hobgoblin in the story. “Somebody’s been making free here!”\n\n“I assure you we found it so,” says Mr. Guppy. “Would you allow me to get it filled for you?”\n\n“Yes, certainly I would!” cries Krook in high glee. “Certainly I would! Don’t mention it! Get it filled next door—Sol’s Arms—the Lord Chancellor’s fourteenpenny. Bless you, they know ME!”\n\nHe so presses the empty bottle upon Mr. Guppy that that gentleman, with a nod to his friend, accepts the trust and hurries out and hurries in again with the bottle filled. The old man receives it in his arms like a beloved grandchild and pats it tenderly.\n\n“But, I say,” he whispers, with his eyes screwed up, after tasting it, “this ain’t the Lord Chancellor’s fourteenpenny. This is eighteenpenny!”\n\n“I thought you might like that better,” says Mr. Guppy.\n\n“You’re a nobleman, sir,” returns Krook with another taste, and his hot breath seems to come towards them like a flame. “You’re a baron of the land.”\n\nTaking advantage of this auspicious moment, Mr. Guppy presents his friend under the impromptu name of Mr. Weevle and states the object of their visit. Krook, with his bottle under his arm (he never gets beyond a certain point of either drunkenness or sobriety), takes time to survey his proposed lodger and seems to approve of him. “You’d like to see the room, young man?” he says. “Ah! It’s a good room! Been whitewashed. Been cleaned down with soft soap and soda. Hi! It’s worth twice the rent, letting alone my company when you want it and such a cat to keep the mice away.”\n\nCommending the room after this manner, the old man takes them upstairs, where indeed they do find it cleaner than it used to be and also containing some old articles of furniture which he has dug up from his inexhaustible stores. The terms are easily concluded—for the Lord Chancellor cannot be hard on Mr. Guppy, associated as he is with Kenge and Carboy, Jarndyce and Jarndyce, and other famous claims on his professional consideration—and it is agreed that Mr. Weevle shall take possession on the morrow. Mr. Weevle and Mr. Guppy then repair to Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, where the personal introduction of the former to Mr. Snagsby is effected and (more important) the vote and interest of Mrs. Snagsby are secured. They then report progress to the eminent Smallweed, waiting at the office in his tall hat for that purpose, and separate, Mr. Guppy explaining that he would terminate his little entertainment by standing treat at the play but that there are chords in the human mind which would render it a hollow mockery.\n\nOn the morrow, in the dusk of evening, Mr. Weevle modestly appears at Krook’s, by no means incommoded with luggage, and establishes himself in his new lodging, where the two eyes in the shutters stare at him in his sleep, as if they were full of wonder. On the following day Mr. Weevle, who is a handy good-for-nothing kind of young fellow, borrows a needle and thread of Miss Flite and a hammer of his landlord and goes to work devising apologies for window-curtains, and knocking up apologies for shelves, and hanging up his two teacups, milkpot, and crockery sundries on a pennyworth of little hooks, like a shipwrecked sailor making the best of it.\n\nBut what Mr. Weevle prizes most of all his few possessions (next after his light whiskers, for which he has an attachment that only whiskers can awaken in the breast of man) is a choice collection of copper-plate impressions from that truly national work The Divinities of Albion, or Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty, representing ladies of title and fashion in every variety of smirk that art, combined with capital, is capable of producing. With these magnificent portraits, unworthily confined in a band-box during his seclusion among the market-gardens, he decorates his apartment; and as the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty wears every variety of fancy dress, plays every variety of musical instrument, fondles every variety of dog, ogles every variety of prospect, and is backed up by every variety of flower-pot and balustrade, the result is very imposing.\n\nBut fashion is Mr. Weevle’s, as it was Tony Jobling’s, weakness. To borrow yesterday’s paper from the Sol’s Arms of an evening and read about the brilliant and distinguished meteors that are shooting across the fashionable sky in every direction is unspeakable consolation to him. To know what member of what brilliant and distinguished circle accomplished the brilliant and distinguished feat of joining it yesterday or contemplates the no less brilliant and distinguished feat of leaving it to-morrow gives him a thrill of joy. To be informed what the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty is about, and means to be about, and what Galaxy marriages are on the tapis, and what Galaxy rumours are in circulation, is to become acquainted with the most glorious destinies of mankind. Mr. Weevle reverts from this intelligence to the Galaxy portraits implicated, and seems to know the originals, and to be known of them.\n\nFor the rest he is a quiet lodger, full of handy shifts and devices as before mentioned, able to cook and clean for himself as well as to carpenter, and developing social inclinations after the shades of evening have fallen on the court. At those times, when he is not visited by Mr. Guppy or by a small light in his likeness quenched in a dark hat, he comes out of his dull room—where he has inherited the deal wilderness of desk bespattered with a rain of ink—and talks to Krook or is “very free,” as they call it in the court, commendingly, with any one disposed for conversation. Wherefore, Mrs. Piper, who leads the court, is impelled to offer two remarks to Mrs. Perkins: firstly, that if her Johnny was to have whiskers, she could wish ’em to be identically like that young man’s; and secondly, “Mark my words, Mrs. Perkins, ma’am, and don’t you be surprised, Lord bless you, if that young man comes in at last for old Krook’s money!”\nThe Smallweed Family\n\nIn a rather ill-favoured and ill-savoured neighbourhood, though one of its rising grounds bears the name of Mount Pleasant, the Elfin Smallweed, christened Bartholomew and known on the domestic hearth as Bart, passes that limited portion of his time on which the office and its contingencies have no claim. He dwells in a little narrow street, always solitary, shady, and sad, closely bricked in on all sides like a tomb, but where there yet lingers the stump of an old forest tree whose flavour is about as fresh and natural as the Smallweed smack of youth.\n\nThere has been only one child in the Smallweed family for several generations. Little old men and women there have been, but no child, until Mr. Smallweed’s grandmother, now living, became weak in her intellect and fell (for the first time) into a childish state. With such infantine graces as a total want of observation, memory, understanding, and interest, and an eternal disposition to fall asleep over the fire and into it, Mr. Smallweed’s grandmother has undoubtedly brightened the family.\n\nMr. Smallweed’s grandfather is likewise of the party. He is in a helpless condition as to his lower, and nearly so as to his upper, limbs, but his mind is unimpaired. It holds, as well as it ever held, the first four rules of arithmetic and a certain small collection of the hardest facts. In respect of ideality, reverence, wonder, and other such phrenological attributes, it is no worse off than it used to be. Everything that Mr. Smallweed’s grandfather ever put away in his mind was a grub at first, and is a grub at last. In all his life he has never bred a single butterfly.\n\nThe father of this pleasant grandfather, of the neighbourhood of Mount Pleasant, was a horny-skinned, two-legged, money-getting species of spider who spun webs to catch unwary flies and retired into holes until they were entrapped. The name of this old pagan’s god was Compound Interest. He lived for it, married it, died of it. Meeting with a heavy loss in an honest little enterprise in which all the loss was intended to have been on the other side, he broke something—something necessary to his existence, therefore it couldn’t have been his heart—and made an end of his career. As his character was not good, and he had been bred at a charity school in a complete course, according to question and answer, of those ancient people the Amorites and Hittites, he was frequently quoted as an example of the failure of education.\n\nHis spirit shone through his son, to whom he had always preached of “going out” early in life and whom he made a clerk in a sharp scrivener’s office at twelve years old. There the young gentleman improved his mind, which was of a lean and anxious character, and developing the family gifts, gradually elevated himself into the discounting profession. Going out early in life and marrying late, as his father had done before him, he too begat a lean and anxious-minded son, who in his turn, going out early in life and marrying late, became the father of Bartholomew and Judith Smallweed, twins. During the whole time consumed in the slow growth of this family tree, the house of Smallweed, always early to go out and late to marry, has strengthened itself in its practical character, has discarded all amusements, discountenanced all story-books, fairy-tales, fictions, and fables, and banished all levities whatsoever. Hence the gratifying fact that it has had no child born to it and that the complete little men and women whom it has produced have been observed to bear a likeness to old monkeys with something depressing on their minds.\n\nAt the present time, in the dark little parlour certain feet below the level of the street—a grim, hard, uncouth parlour, only ornamented with the coarsest of baize table-covers, and the hardest of sheet-iron tea-trays, and offering in its decorative character no bad allegorical representation of Grandfather Smallweed’s mind—seated in two black horsehair porter’s chairs, one on each side of the fire-place, the superannuated Mr. and Mrs. Smallweed while away the rosy hours. On the stove are a couple of trivets for the pots and kettles which it is Grandfather Smallweed’s usual occupation to watch, and projecting from the chimney-piece between them is a sort of brass gallows for roasting, which he also superintends when it is in action. Under the venerable Mr. Smallweed’s seat and guarded by his spindle legs is a drawer in his chair, reported to contain property to a fabulous amount. Beside him is a spare cushion with which he is always provided in order that he may have something to throw at the venerable partner of his respected age whenever she makes an allusion to money—a subject on which he is particularly sensitive.\n\n“And where’s Bart?” Grandfather Smallweed inquires of Judy, Bart’s twin sister.\n\n“He an’t come in yet,” says Judy.\n\n“It’s his tea-time, isn’t it?”\n“No.”\n\n“How much do you mean to say it wants then?”\n\n“Ten minutes.”\n\n“Hey?”\n\n“Ten minutes.” (Loud on the part of Judy.)\n\n“Ho!” says Grandfather Smallweed. “Ten minutes.”\n\nGrandmother Smallweed, who has been mumbling and shaking her head at the trivets, hearing figures mentioned, connects them with money and screeches like a horrible old parrot without any plumage, “Ten ten-pound notes!”\n\nGrandfather Smallweed immediately throws the cushion at her.\n\n“Drat you, be quiet!” says the good old man.\n\nThe effect of this act of jaculation is twofold. It not only doubles up Mrs. Smallweed’s head against the side of her porter’s chair and causes her to present, when extricated by her granddaughter, a highly unbecoming state of cap, but the necessary exertion recoils on Mr. Smallweed himself, whom it throws back into HIS porter’s chair like a broken puppet. The excellent old gentleman being at these times a mere clothes-bag with a black skull-cap on the top of it, does not present a very animated appearance until he has undergone the two operations at the hands of his granddaughter of being shaken up like a great bottle and poked and punched like a great bolster. Some indication of a neck being developed in him by these means, he and the sharer of his life’s evening again sit fronting one another in their two porter’s chairs, like a couple of sentinels long forgotten on their post by the Black Serjeant, Death.\n\nJudy the twin is worthy company for these associates. She is so indubitably sister to Mr. Smallweed the younger that the two kneaded into one would hardly make a young person of average proportions, while she so happily exemplifies the before-mentioned family likeness to the monkey tribe that attired in a spangled robe and cap she might walk about the table-land on the top of a barrel-organ without exciting much remark as an unusual specimen. Under existing circumstances, however, she is dressed in a plain, spare gown of brown stuff.\n\nJudy never owned a doll, never heard of Cinderella, never played at any game. She once or twice fell into children’s company when she was about ten years old, but the children couldn’t get on with Judy, and Judy couldn’t get on with them. She seemed like an animal of another species, and there was instinctive repugnance on both sides. It is very doubtful whether Judy knows how to laugh. She has so rarely seen the thing done that the probabilities are strong the other way. Of anything like a youthful laugh, she certainly can have no conception. If she were to try one, she would find her teeth in her way, modelling that action of her face, as she has unconsciously modelled all its other expressions, on her pattern of sordid age. Such is Judy.\n\nAnd her twin brother couldn’t wind up a top for his life. He knows no more of Jack the Giant Killer or of Sinbad the Sailor than he knows of the people in the stars. He could as soon play at leap-frog or at cricket as change into a cricket or a frog himself. But he is so much the better off than his sister that on his narrow world of fact an opening has dawned into such broader regions as lie within the ken of Mr. Guppy. Hence his admiration and his emulation of that shining enchanter.\n\nJudy, with a gong-like clash and clatter, sets one of the sheet-iron tea-trays on the table and arranges cups and saucers. The bread she puts on in an iron basket, and the butter (and not much of it) in a small pewter plate. Grandfather Smallweed looks hard after the tea as it is served out and asks Judy where the girl is.\n\n“Charley, do you mean?” says Judy.\n\n“Hey?” from Grandfather Smallweed.\n\n“Charley, do you mean?”\n\nThis touches a spring in Grandmother Smallweed, who, chuckling as usual at the trivets, cries, “Over the water! Charley over the water, Charley over the water, over the water to Charley, Charley over the water, over the water to Charley!” and becomes quite energetic about it. Grandfather looks at the cushion but has not sufficiently recovered his late exertion.\n\n“Ha!” he says when there is silence. “If that’s her name. She eats a deal. It would be better to allow her for her keep.”\n\nJudy, with her brother’s wink, shakes her head and purses up her mouth into no without saying it.\n\n“No?” returns the old man. “Why not?”\n\n“She’d want sixpence a day, and we can do it for less,” says Judy.\n\n“Sure?”\n\nJudy answers with a nod of deepest meaning and calls, as she scrapes the butter on the loaf with every precaution against waste and cuts it into slices, “You, Charley, where are you?” Timidly obedient to the summons, a little girl in a rough apron and a large bonnet, with her hands covered with soap and water and a scrubbing brush in one of them, appears, and curtsys.\n\n“What work are you about now?” says Judy, making an ancient snap at her like a very sharp old beldame.\n\n“I’m a-cleaning the upstairs back room, miss,” replies Charley.\n\n“Mind you do it thoroughly, and don’t loiter. Shirking won’t do for me. Make haste! Go along!” cries Judy with a stamp upon the ground. “You girls are more trouble than you’re worth, by half.”\n\nOn this severe matron, as she returns to her task of scraping the butter and cutting the bread, falls the shadow of her brother, looking in at the window. For whom, knife and loaf in hand, she opens the street-door.\n\n“Aye, aye, Bart!” says Grandfather Smallweed. “Here you are, hey?”\n\n“Here I am,” says Bart.\n\n“Been along with your friend again, Bart?”\n\nSmall nods.\n\n“Dining at his expense, Bart?”\n\nSmall nods again.\n\n“That’s right. Live at his expense as much as you can, and take warning by his foolish example. That’s the use of such a friend. The only use you can put him to,” says the venerable sage.\n\nHis grandson, without receiving this good counsel as dutifully as he might, honours it with all such acceptance as may lie in a slight wink and a nod and takes a chair at the tea-table. The four old faces then hover over teacups like a company of ghastly cherubim, Mrs. Smallweed perpetually twitching her head and chattering at the trivets and Mr. Smallweed requiring to be repeatedly shaken up like a large black draught.\n\n“Yes, yes,” says the good old gentleman, reverting to his lesson of wisdom. “That’s such advice as your father would have given you, Bart. You never saw your father. More’s the pity. He was my true son.” Whether it is intended to be conveyed that he was particularly pleasant to look at, on that account, does not appear.\n\n“He was my true son,” repeats the old gentleman, folding his bread and butter on his knee, “a good accountant, and died fifteen years ago.”\n\nMrs. Smallweed, following her usual instinct, breaks out with “Fifteen hundred pound. Fifteen hundred pound in a black box, fifteen hundred pound locked up, fifteen hundred pound put away and hid!” Her worthy husband, setting aside his bread and butter, immediately discharges the cushion at her, crushes her against the side of her chair, and falls back in his own, overpowered. His appearance, after visiting Mrs. Smallweed with one of these admonitions, is particularly impressive and not wholly prepossessing, firstly because the exertion generally twists his black skull-cap over one eye and gives him an air of goblin rakishness, secondly because he mutters violent imprecations against Mrs. Smallweed, and thirdly because the contrast between those powerful expressions and his powerless figure is suggestive of a baleful old malignant who would be very wicked if he could. All this, however, is so common in the Smallweed family circle that it produces no impression. The old gentleman is merely shaken and has his internal feathers beaten up, the cushion is restored to its usual place beside him, and the old lady, perhaps with her cap adjusted and perhaps not, is planted in her chair again, ready to be bowled down like a ninepin.\n\nSome time elapses in the present instance before the old gentleman is sufficiently cool to resume his discourse, and even then he mixes it up with several edifying expletives addressed to the unconscious partner of his bosom, who holds communication with nothing on earth but the trivets. As thus: “If your father, Bart, had lived longer, he might have been worth a deal of money—you brimstone chatterer!—but just as he was beginning to build up the house that he had been making the foundations for, through many a year—you jade of a magpie, jackdaw, and poll-parrot, what do you mean!—he took ill and died of a low fever, always being a sparing and a spare man, full of business care—I should like to throw a cat at you instead of a cushion, and I will too if you make such a confounded fool of yourself!—and your mother, who was a prudent woman as dry as a chip, just dwindled away like touchwood after you and Judy were born—you are an old pig. You are a brimstone pig. You’re a head of swine!”\n\nJudy, not interested in what she has often heard, begins to collect in a basin various tributary streams of tea, from the bottoms of cups and saucers and from the bottom of the tea-pot for the little charwoman’s evening meal. In like manner she gets together, in the iron bread-basket, as many outside fragments and worn-down heels of loaves as the rigid economy of the house has left in existence.\n\n“But your father and me were partners, Bart,” says the old gentleman, “and when I am gone, you and Judy will have all there is. It’s rare for you both that you went out early in life—Judy to the flower business, and you to the law. You won’t want to spend it. You’ll get your living without it, and put more to it. When I am gone, Judy will go back to the flower business and you’ll still stick to the law.”\n\nOne might infer from Judy’s appearance that her business rather lay with the thorns than the flowers, but she has in her time been apprenticed to the art and mystery of artificial flower-making. A close observer might perhaps detect both in her eye and her brother’s, when their venerable grandsire anticipates his being gone, some little impatience to know when he may be going, and some resentful opinion that it is time he went.\n\n“Now, if everybody has done,” says Judy, completing her preparations, “I’ll have that girl in to her tea. She would never leave off if she took it by herself in the kitchen.”\n\nCharley is accordingly introduced, and under a heavy fire of eyes, sits down to her basin and a Druidical ruin of bread and butter. In the active superintendence of this young person, Judy Smallweed appears to attain a perfectly geological age and to date from the remotest periods. Her systematic manner of flying at her and pouncing on her, with or without pretence, whether or no, is wonderful, evincing an accomplishment in the art of girl-driving seldom reached by the oldest practitioners.\n\n“Now, don’t stare about you all the afternoon,” cries Judy, shaking her head and stamping her foot as she happens to catch the glance which has been previously sounding the basin of tea, “but take your victuals and get back to your work.”\n\n“Yes, miss,” says Charley.\n\n“Don’t say yes,” returns Miss Smallweed, “for I know what you girls are. Do it without saying it, and then I may begin to believe you.”\n\nCharley swallows a great gulp of tea in token of submission and so disperses the Druidical ruins that Miss Smallweed charges her not to gormandize, which “in you girls,” she observes, is disgusting. Charley might find some more difficulty in meeting her views on the general subject of girls but for a knock at the door.\n\n“See who it is, and don’t chew when you open it!” cries Judy.\n\nThe object of her attentions withdrawing for the purpose, Miss Smallweed takes that opportunity of jumbling the remainder of the bread and butter together and launching two or three dirty tea-cups into the ebb-tide of the basin of tea as a hint that she considers the eating and drinking terminated.\n\n“Now! Who is it, and what’s wanted?” says the snappish Judy.\n\nIt is one Mr. George, it appears. Without other announcement or ceremony, Mr. George walks in.\n\n“Whew!” says Mr. George. “You are hot here. Always a fire, eh? Well! Perhaps you do right to get used to one.” Mr. George makes the latter remark to himself as he nods to Grandfather Smallweed.\n\n“Ho! It’s you!” cries the old gentleman. “How de do? How de do?”\n\n“Middling,” replies Mr. George, taking a chair. “Your granddaughter I have had the honour of seeing before; my service to you, miss.”\n\n“This is my grandson,” says Grandfather Smallweed. “You ha’n’t seen him before. He is in the law and not much at home.”\n\n“My service to him, too! He is like his sister. He is very like his sister. He is devilish like his sister,” says Mr. George, laying a great and not altogether complimentary stress on his last adjective.\n\n“And how does the world use you, Mr. George?” Grandfather Smallweed inquires, slowly rubbing his legs.\n\n“Pretty much as usual. Like a football.”\n\nHe is a swarthy brown man of fifty, well made, and good looking, with crisp dark hair, bright eyes, and a broad chest. His sinewy and powerful hands, as sunburnt as his face, have evidently been used to a pretty rough life. What is curious about him is that he sits forward on his chair as if he were, from long habit, allowing space for some dress or accoutrements that he has altogether laid aside. His step too is measured and heavy and would go well with a weighty clash and jingle of spurs. He is close-shaved now, but his mouth is set as if his upper lip had been for years familiar with a great moustache; and his manner of occasionally laying the open palm of his broad brown hand upon it is to the same effect. Altogether one might guess Mr. George to have been a trooper once upon a time.\n\nA special contrast Mr. George makes to the Smallweed family. Trooper was never yet billeted upon a household more unlike him. It is a broadsword to an oyster-knife. His developed figure and their stunted forms, his large manner filling any amount of room and their little narrow pinched ways, his sounding voice and their sharp spare tones, are in the strongest and the strangest opposition. As he sits in the middle of the grim parlour, leaning a little forward, with his hands upon his thighs and his elbows squared, he looks as though, if he remained there long, he would absorb into himself the whole family and the whole four-roomed house, extra little back-kitchen and all.\n\n“Do you rub your legs to rub life into ’em?” he asks of Grandfather Smallweed after looking round the room.\n\n“Why, it’s partly a habit, Mr. George, and—yes—it partly helps the circulation,” he replies.\n\n“The cir-cu-la-tion!” repeats Mr. George, folding his arms upon his chest and seeming to become two sizes larger. “Not much of that, I should think.”\n\n“Truly I’m old, Mr. George,” says Grandfather Smallweed. “But I can carry my years. I’m older than HER,” nodding at his wife, “and see what she is? You’re a brimstone chatterer!” with a sudden revival of his late hostility.\n\n“Unlucky old soul!” says Mr. George, turning his head in that direction. “Don’t scold the old lady. Look at her here, with her poor cap half off her head and her poor hair all in a muddle. Hold up, ma’am. That’s better. There we are! Think of your mother, Mr. Smallweed,” says Mr. George, coming back to his seat from assisting her, “if your wife an’t enough.”\n\n“I suppose you were an excellent son, Mr. George?” the old man hints with a leer.\n\nThe colour of Mr. George’s face rather deepens as he replies, “Why no. I wasn’t.”\n\n“I am astonished at it.”\n\n“So am I. I ought to have been a good son, and I think I meant to have been one. But I wasn’t. I was a thundering bad son, that’s the long and the short of it, and never was a credit to anybody.”\n\n“Surprising!” cries the old man.\n\n“However,” Mr. George resumes, “the less said about it, the better now. Come! You know the agreement. Always a pipe out of the two months’ interest! (Bosh! It’s all correct. You needn’t be afraid to order the pipe. Here’s the new bill, and here’s the two months’ interest-money, and a devil-and-all of a scrape it is to get it together in my business.)”\n\nMr. George sits, with his arms folded, consuming the family and the parlour while Grandfather Smallweed is assisted by Judy to two black leathern cases out of a locked bureau, in one of which he secures the document he has just received, and from the other takes another similar document which he hands to Mr. George, who twists it up for a pipelight. As the old man inspects, through his glasses, every up-stroke and down-stroke of both documents before he releases them from their leathern prison, and as he counts the money three times over and requires Judy to say every word she utters at least twice, and is as tremulously slow of speech and action as it is possible to be, this business is a long time in progress. When it is quite concluded, and not before, he disengages his ravenous eyes and fingers from it and answers Mr. George’s last remark by saying, “Afraid to order the pipe? We are not so mercenary as that, sir. Judy, see directly to the pipe and the glass of cold brandy-and-water for Mr. George.”\n\nThe sportive twins, who have been looking straight before them all this time except when they have been engrossed by the black leathern cases, retire together, generally disdainful of the visitor, but leaving him to the old man as two young cubs might leave a traveller to the parental bear.\n\n“And there you sit, I suppose, all the day long, eh?” says Mr. George with folded arms.\n\n“Just so, just so,” the old man nods.\n\n“And don’t you occupy yourself at all?”\n\n“I watch the fire—and the boiling and the roasting—”\n\n“When there is any,” says Mr. George with great expression.\n\n“Just so. When there is any.”\n\n“Don’t you read or get read to?”\n\nThe old man shakes his head with sharp sly triumph. “No, no. We have never been readers in our family. It don’t pay. Stuff. Idleness. Folly. No, no!”\n\n“There’s not much to choose between your two states,” says the visitor in a key too low for the old man’s dull hearing as he looks from him to the old woman and back again. “I say!” in a louder voice.\n\n“I hear you.”\n\n“You’ll sell me up at last, I suppose, when I am a day in arrear.”\n\n“My dear friend!” cries Grandfather Smallweed, stretching out both hands to embrace him. “Never! Never, my dear friend! But my friend in the city that I got to lend you the money—HE might!”\n\n“Oh! You can’t answer for him?” says Mr. George, finishing the inquiry in his lower key with the words “You lying old rascal!”\n\n“My dear friend, he is not to be depended on. I wouldn’t trust him. He will have his bond, my dear friend.”\n\n“Devil doubt him,” says Mr. George. Charley appearing with a tray, on which are the pipe, a small paper of tobacco, and the brandy-and-water, he asks her, “How do you come here! You haven’t got the family face.”\n\n“I goes out to work, sir,” returns Charley.\n\nThe trooper (if trooper he be or have been) takes her bonnet off, with a light touch for so strong a hand, and pats her on the head. “You give the house almost a wholesome look. It wants a bit of youth as much as it wants fresh air.” Then he dismisses her, lights his pipe, and drinks to Mr. Smallweed’s friend in the city—the one solitary flight of that esteemed old gentleman’s imagination.\n\n“So you think he might be hard upon me, eh?”\n\n“I think he might—I am afraid he would. I have known him do it,” says Grandfather Smallweed incautiously, “twenty times.”\n\nIncautiously, because his stricken better-half, who has been dozing over the fire for some time, is instantly aroused and jabbers “Twenty thousand pounds, twenty twenty-pound notes in a money-box, twenty guineas, twenty million twenty per cent, twenty—” and is then cut short by the flying cushion, which the visitor, to whom this singular experiment appears to be a novelty, snatches from her face as it crushes her in the usual manner.\n\n“You’re a brimstone idiot. You’re a scorpion—a brimstone scorpion! You’re a sweltering toad. You’re a chattering clattering broomstick witch that ought to be burnt!” gasps the old man, prostrate in his chair. “My dear friend, will you shake me up a little?”\n\nMr. George, who has been looking first at one of them and then at the other, as if he were demented, takes his venerable acquaintance by the throat on receiving this request, and dragging him upright in his chair as easily as if he were a doll, appears in two minds whether or no to shake all future power of cushioning out of him and shake him into his grave. Resisting the temptation, but agitating him violently enough to make his head roll like a harlequin’s, he puts him smartly down in his chair again and adjusts his skull-cap with such a rub that the old man winks with both eyes for a minute afterwards.\n\n“O Lord!” gasps Mr. Smallweed. “That’ll do. Thank you, my dear friend, that’ll do. Oh, dear me, I’m out of breath. O Lord!” And Mr. Smallweed says it not without evident apprehensions of his dear friend, who still stands over him looming larger than ever.\n\nThe alarming presence, however, gradually subsides into its chair and falls to smoking in long puffs, consoling itself with the philosophical reflection, “The name of your friend in the city begins with a D, comrade, and you’re about right respecting the bond.”\n\n“Did you speak, Mr. George?” inquires the old man.\n\nThe trooper shakes his head, and leaning forward with his right elbow on his right knee and his pipe supported in that hand, while his other hand, resting on his left leg, squares his left elbow in a martial manner, continues to smoke. Meanwhile he looks at Mr. Smallweed with grave attention and now and then fans the cloud of smoke away in order that he may see him the more clearly.\n\n“I take it,” he says, making just as much and as little change in his position as will enable him to reach the glass to his lips with a round, full action, “that I am the only man alive (or dead either) that gets the value of a pipe out of YOU?”\n\n“Well,” returns the old man, “it’s true that I don’t see company, Mr. George, and that I don’t treat. I can’t afford to it. But as you, in your pleasant way, made your pipe a condition—”\n\n“Why, it’s not for the value of it; that’s no great thing. It was a fancy to get it out of you. To have something in for my money.”\n\n“Ha! You’re prudent, prudent, sir!” cries Grandfather Smallweed, rubbing his legs.\n\n“Very. I always was.” Puff. “It’s a sure sign of my prudence that I ever found the way here.” Puff. “Also, that I am what I am.” Puff. “I am well known to be prudent,” says Mr. George, composedly smoking. “I rose in life that way.”\n\n“Don’t be down-hearted, sir. You may rise yet.”\n\nMr. George laughs and drinks.\n\n“Ha’n’t you no relations, now,” asks Grandfather Smallweed with a twinkle in his eyes, “who would pay off this little principal or who would lend you a good name or two that I could persuade my friend in the city to make you a further advance upon? Two good names would be sufficient for my friend in the city. Ha’n’t you no such relations, Mr. George?”\n\nMr. George, still composedly smoking, replies, “If I had, I shouldn’t trouble them. I have been trouble enough to my belongings in my day. It MAY be a very good sort of penitence in a vagabond, who has wasted the best time of his life, to go back then to decent people that he never was a credit to and live upon them, but it’s not my sort. The best kind of amends then for having gone away is to keep away, in my opinion.”\n\n“But natural affection, Mr. George,” hints Grandfather Smallweed.\n\n“For two good names, hey?” says Mr. George, shaking his head and still composedly smoking. “No. That’s not my sort either.”\n\nGrandfather Smallweed has been gradually sliding down in his chair since his last adjustment and is now a bundle of clothes with a voice in it calling for Judy. That houri, appearing, shakes him up in the usual manner and is charged by the old gentleman to remain near him. For he seems chary of putting his visitor to the trouble of repeating his late attentions.\n\n“Ha!” he observes when he is in trim again. “If you could have traced out the captain, Mr. George, it would have been the making of you. If when you first came here, in consequence of our advertisement in the newspapers—when I say ‘our,’ I’m alluding to the advertisements of my friend in the city, and one or two others who embark their capital in the same way, and are so friendly towards me as sometimes to give me a lift with my little pittance—if at that time you could have helped us, Mr. George, it would have been the making of you.”\n\n“I was willing enough to be ‘made,’ as you call it,” says Mr. George, smoking not quite so placidly as before, for since the entrance of Judy he has been in some measure disturbed by a fascination, not of the admiring kind, which obliges him to look at her as she stands by her grandfather’s chair, “but on the whole, I am glad I wasn’t now.”\n\n“Why, Mr. George? In the name of—of brimstone, why?” says Grandfather Smallweed with a plain appearance of exasperation. (Brimstone apparently suggested by his eye lighting on Mrs. Smallweed in her slumber.)\n\n“For two reasons, comrade.”\n\n“And what two reasons, Mr. George? In the name of the—”\n\n“Of our friend in the city?” suggests Mr. George, composedly drinking.\n\n“Aye, if you like. What two reasons?”\n\n“In the first place,” returns Mr. George, but still looking at Judy as if she being so old and so like her grandfather it is indifferent which of the two he addresses, “you gentlemen took me in. You advertised that Mr. Hawdon (Captain Hawdon, if you hold to the saying ‘Once a captain, always a captain’) was to hear of something to his advantage.”\n\n“Well?” returns the old man shrilly and sharply.\n\n“Well!” says Mr. George, smoking on. “It wouldn’t have been much to his advantage to have been clapped into prison by the whole bill and judgment trade of London.”\n\n“How do you know that? Some of his rich relations might have paid his debts or compounded for ’em. Besides, he had taken US in. He owed us immense sums all round. I would sooner have strangled him than had no return. If I sit here thinking of him,” snarls the old man, holding up his impotent ten fingers, “I want to strangle him now.” And in a sudden access of fury, he throws the cushion at the unoffending Mrs. Smallweed, but it passes harmlessly on one side of her chair.\n\n“I don’t need to be told,” returns the trooper, taking his pipe from his lips for a moment and carrying his eyes back from following the progress of the cushion to the pipe-bowl which is burning low, “that he carried on heavily and went to ruin. I have been at his right hand many a day when he was charging upon ruin full-gallop. I was with him when he was sick and well, rich and poor. I laid this hand upon him after he had run through everything and broken down everything beneath him—when he held a pistol to his head.”\n\n“I wish he had let it off,” says the benevolent old man, “and blown his head into as many pieces as he owed pounds!”\n\n“That would have been a smash indeed,” returns the trooper coolly; “any way, he had been young, hopeful, and handsome in the days gone by, and I am glad I never found him, when he was neither, to lead to a result so much to his advantage. That’s reason number one.”\n\n“I hope number two’s as good?” snarls the old man.\n\n“Why, no. It’s more of a selfish reason. If I had found him, I must have gone to the other world to look. He was there.”\n\n“How do you know he was there?”\n\n“He wasn’t here.”\n\n“How do you know he wasn’t here?”\n\n“Don’t lose your temper as well as your money,” says Mr. George, calmly knocking the ashes out of his pipe. “He was drowned long before. I am convinced of it. He went over a ship’s side. Whether intentionally or accidentally, I don’t know. Perhaps your friend in the city does. Do you know what that tune is, Mr. Smallweed?” he adds after breaking off to whistle one, accompanied on the table with the empty pipe.\n\n“Tune!” replied the old man. “No. We never have tunes here.”\n\n“That’s the Dead March in Saul. They bury soldiers to it, so it’s the natural end of the subject. Now, if your pretty granddaughter—excuse me, miss—will condescend to take care of this pipe for two months, we shall save the cost of one next time. Good evening, Mr. Smallweed!”\n\n“My dear friend!” the old man gives him both his hands.\n\n“So you think your friend in the city will be hard upon me if I fall in a payment?” says the trooper, looking down upon him like a giant.\n\n“My dear friend, I am afraid he will,” returns the old man, looking up at him like a pygmy.\n\nMr. George laughs, and with a glance at Mr. Smallweed and a parting salutation to the scornful Judy, strides out of the parlour, clashing imaginary sabres and other metallic appurtenances as he goes.\n\n“You’re a damned rogue,” says the old gentleman, making a hideous grimace at the door as he shuts it. “But I’ll lime you, you dog, I’ll lime you!”\n\nAfter this amiable remark, his spirit soars into those enchanting regions of reflection which its education and pursuits have opened to it, and again he and Mrs. Smallweed while away the rosy hours, two unrelieved sentinels forgotten as aforesaid by the Black Serjeant.\n\nWhile the twain are faithful to their post, Mr. George strides through the streets with a massive kind of swagger and a grave-enough face. It is eight o’clock now, and the day is fast drawing in. He stops hard by Waterloo Bridge and reads a playbill, decides to go to Astley’s Theatre. Being there, is much delighted with the horses and the feats of strength; looks at the weapons with a critical eye; disapproves of the combats as giving evidences of unskilful swordsmanship; but is touched home by the sentiments. In the last scene, when the Emperor of Tartary gets up into a cart and condescends to bless the united lovers by hovering over them with the Union Jack, his eyelashes are moistened with emotion.\n\nThe theatre over, Mr. George comes across the water again and makes his way to that curious region lying about the Haymarket and Leicester Square which is a centre of attraction to indifferent foreign hotels and indifferent foreigners, racket-courts, fighting-men, swordsmen, footguards, old china, gaming-houses, exhibitions, and a large medley of shabbiness and shrinking out of sight. Penetrating to the heart of this region, he arrives by a court and a long whitewashed passage at a great brick building composed of bare walls, floors, roof-rafters, and skylights, on the front of which, if it can be said to have any front, is painted GEORGE’S SHOOTING GALLERY, &c.\n\nInto George’s Shooting Gallery, &c., he goes; and in it there are gaslights (partly turned off now), and two whitened targets for rifle-shooting, and archery accommodation, and fencing appliances, and all necessaries for the British art of boxing. None of these sports or exercises being pursued in George’s Shooting Gallery to-night, which is so devoid of company that a little grotesque man with a large head has it all to himself and lies asleep upon the floor.\n\nThe little man is dressed something like a gunsmith, in a green-baize apron and cap; and his face and hands are dirty with gunpowder and begrimed with the loading of guns. As he lies in the light before a glaring white target, the black upon him shines again. Not far off is the strong, rough, primitive table with a vice upon it at which he has been working. He is a little man with a face all crushed together, who appears, from a certain blue and speckled appearance that one of his cheeks presents, to have been blown up, in the way of business, at some odd time or times.\n\n“Phil!” says the trooper in a quiet voice.\n\n“All right!” cries Phil, scrambling to his feet.\n\n“Anything been doing?”\n\n“Flat as ever so much swipes,” says Phil. “Five dozen rifle and a dozen pistol. As to aim!” Phil gives a howl at the recollection.\n\n“Shut up shop, Phil!”\n\nAs Phil moves about to execute this order, it appears that he is lame, though able to move very quickly. On the speckled side of his face he has no eyebrow, and on the other side he has a bushy black one, which want of uniformity gives him a very singular and rather sinister appearance. Everything seems to have happened to his hands that could possibly take place consistently with the retention of all the fingers, for they are notched, and seamed, and crumpled all over. He appears to be very strong and lifts heavy benches about as if he had no idea what weight was. He has a curious way of limping round the gallery with his shoulder against the wall and tacking off at objects he wants to lay hold of instead of going straight to them, which has left a smear all round the four walls, conventionally called “Phil’s mark.”\n\nThis custodian of George’s Gallery in George’s absence concludes his proceedings, when he has locked the great doors and turned out all the lights but one, which he leaves to glimmer, by dragging out from a wooden cabin in a corner two mattresses and bedding. These being drawn to opposite ends of the gallery, the trooper makes his own bed and Phil makes his.\n\n“Phil!” says the master, walking towards him without his coat and waistcoat, and looking more soldierly than ever in his braces. “You were found in a doorway, weren’t you?”\n\n“Gutter,” says Phil. “Watchman tumbled over me.”\n\n“Then vagabondizing came natural to YOU from the beginning.”\n\n“As nat’ral as possible,” says Phil.\n\n“Good night!”\n\n“Good night, guv’ner.”\n\nPhil cannot even go straight to bed, but finds it necessary to shoulder round two sides of the gallery and then tack off at his mattress. The trooper, after taking a turn or two in the rifle-distance and looking up at the moon now shining through the skylights, strides to his own mattress by a shorter route and goes to bed too.\n\nMr. Bucket\nAllegory looks pretty cool in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, though the evening is hot, for both Mr. Tulkinghorn’s windows are wide open, and the room is lofty, gusty, and gloomy. These may not be desirable characteristics when November comes with fog and sleet or January with ice and snow, but they have their merits in the sultry long vacation weather. They enable Allegory, though it has cheeks like peaches, and knees like bunches of blossoms, and rosy swellings for calves to its legs and muscles to its arms, to look tolerably cool to-night.\n\nPlenty of dust comes in at Mr. Tulkinghorn’s windows, and plenty more has generated among his furniture and papers. It lies thick everywhere. When a breeze from the country that has lost its way takes fright and makes a blind hurry to rush out again, it flings as much dust in the eyes of Allegory as the law—or Mr. Tulkinghorn, one of its trustiest representatives—may scatter, on occasion, in the eyes of the laity.\n\nIn his lowering magazine of dust, the universal article into which his papers and himself, and all his clients, and all things of earth, animate and inanimate, are resolving, Mr. Tulkinghorn sits at one of the open windows enjoying a bottle of old port. Though a hard-grained man, close, dry, and silent, he can enjoy old wine with the best. He has a priceless bin of port in some artful cellar under the Fields, which is one of his many secrets. When he dines alone in chambers, as he has dined to-day, and has his bit of fish and his steak or chicken brought in from the coffee-house, he descends with a candle to the echoing regions below the deserted mansion, and heralded by a remote reverberation of thundering doors, comes gravely back encircled by an earthy atmosphere and carrying a bottle from which he pours a radiant nectar, two score and ten years old, that blushes in the glass to find itself so famous and fills the whole room with the fragrance of southern grapes.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn, sitting in the twilight by the open window, enjoys his wine. As if it whispered to him of its fifty years of silence and seclusion, it shuts him up the closer. More impenetrable than ever, he sits, and drinks, and mellows as it were in secrecy, pondering at that twilight hour on all the mysteries he knows, associated with darkening woods in the country, and vast blank shut-up houses in town, and perhaps sparing a thought or two for himself, and his family history, and his money, and his will—all a mystery to every one—and that one bachelor friend of his, a man of the same mould and a lawyer too, who lived the same kind of life until he was seventy-five years old, and then suddenly conceiving (as it is supposed) an impression that it was too monotonous, gave his gold watch to his hair-dresser one summer evening and walked leisurely home to the Temple and hanged himself.\n\nBut Mr. Tulkinghorn is not alone to-night to ponder at his usual length. Seated at the same table, though with his chair modestly and uncomfortably drawn a little way from it, sits a bald, mild, shining man who coughs respectfully behind his hand when the lawyer bids him fill his glass.\n\n“Now, Snagsby,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “to go over this odd story again.”\n\n“If you please, sir.”\n\n“You told me when you were so good as to step round here last night—”\n\n“For which I must ask you to excuse me if it was a liberty, sir; but I remember that you had taken a sort of an interest in that person, and I thought it possible that you might—just—wish—to—”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn is not the man to help him to any conclusion or to admit anything as to any possibility concerning himself. So Mr. Snagsby trails off into saying, with an awkward cough, “I must ask you to excuse the liberty, sir, I am sure.”\n\n“Not at all,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “You told me, Snagsby, that you put on your hat and came round without mentioning your intention to your wife. That was prudent I think, because it’s not a matter of such importance that it requires to be mentioned.”\n\n“Well, sir,” returns Mr. Snagsby, “you see, my little woman is—not to put too fine a point upon it—inquisitive. She’s inquisitive. Poor little thing, she’s liable to spasms, and it’s good for her to have her mind employed. In consequence of which she employs it—I should say upon every individual thing she can lay hold of, whether it concerns her or not—especially not. My little woman has a very active mind, sir.”\n\nMr. Snagsby drinks and murmurs with an admiring cough behind his hand, “Dear me, very fine wine indeed!”\n\n“Therefore you kept your visit to yourself last night?” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “And to-night too?”\n\n“Yes, sir, and to-night, too. My little woman is at present in—not to put too fine a point on it—in a pious state, or in what she considers such, and attends the Evening Exertions (which is the name they go by) of a reverend party of the name of Chadband. He has a great deal of eloquence at his command, undoubtedly, but I am not quite favourable to his style myself. That’s neither here nor there. My little woman being engaged in that way made it easier for me to step round in a quiet manner.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn assents. “Fill your glass, Snagsby.”\n\n“Thank you, sir, I am sure,” returns the stationer with his cough of deference. “This is wonderfully fine wine, sir!”\n\n“It is a rare wine now,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “It is fifty years old.”\n\n“Is it indeed, sir? But I am not surprised to hear it, I am sure. It might be—any age almost.” After rendering this general tribute to the port, Mr. Snagsby in his modesty coughs an apology behind his hand for drinking anything so precious.\n\n“Will you run over, once again, what the boy said?” asks Mr. Tulkinghorn, putting his hands into the pockets of his rusty smallclothes and leaning quietly back in his chair.\n\n“With pleasure, sir.”\n\nThen, with fidelity, though with some prolixity, the law-stationer repeats Jo’s statement made to the assembled guests at his house. On coming to the end of his narrative, he gives a great start and breaks off with, “Dear me, sir, I wasn’t aware there was any other gentleman present!”\n\nMr. Snagsby is dismayed to see, standing with an attentive face between himself and the lawyer at a little distance from the table, a person with a hat and stick in his hand who was not there when he himself came in and has not since entered by the door or by either of the windows. There is a press in the room, but its hinges have not creaked, nor has a step been audible upon the floor. Yet this third person stands there with his attentive face, and his hat and stick in his hands, and his hands behind him, a composed and quiet listener. He is a stoutly built, steady-looking, sharp-eyed man in black, of about the middle-age. Except that he looks at Mr. Snagsby as if he were going to take his portrait, there is nothing remarkable about him at first sight but his ghostly manner of appearing.\n\n“Don’t mind this gentleman,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn in his quiet way. “This is only Mr. Bucket.”\n\n“Oh, indeed, sir?” returns the stationer, expressing by a cough that he is quite in the dark as to who Mr. Bucket may be.\n\n“I wanted him to hear this story,” says the lawyer, “because I have half a mind (for a reason) to know more of it, and he is very intelligent in such things. What do you say to this, Bucket?”\n\n“It’s very plain, sir. Since our people have moved this boy on, and he’s not to be found on his old lay, if Mr. Snagsby don’t object to go down with me to Tom-all-Alone’s and point him out, we can have him here in less than a couple of hours’ time. I can do it without Mr. Snagsby, of course, but this is the shortest way.”\n\n“Mr. Bucket is a detective officer, Snagsby,” says the lawyer in explanation.\n\n“Is he indeed, sir?” says Mr. Snagsby with a strong tendency in his clump of hair to stand on end.\n\n“And if you have no real objection to accompany Mr. Bucket to the place in question,” pursues the lawyer, “I shall feel obliged to you if you will do so.”\n\nIn a moment’s hesitation on the part of Mr. Snagsby, Bucket dips down to the bottom of his mind.\n\n“Don’t you be afraid of hurting the boy,” he says. “You won’t do that. It’s all right as far as the boy’s concerned. We shall only bring him here to ask him a question or so I want to put to him, and he’ll be paid for his trouble and sent away again. It’ll be a good job for him. I promise you, as a man, that you shall see the boy sent away all right. Don’t you be afraid of hurting him; you an’t going to do that.”\n\n“Very well, Mr. Tulkinghorn!” cries Mr. Snagsby cheerfully. And reassured, “Since that’s the case—”\n\n“Yes! And lookee here, Mr. Snagsby,” resumes Bucket, taking him aside by the arm, tapping him familiarly on the breast, and speaking in a confidential tone. “You’re a man of the world, you know, and a man of business, and a man of sense. That’s what YOU are.”\n\n“I am sure I am much obliged to you for your good opinion,” returns the stationer with his cough of modesty, “but—”\n\n“That’s what YOU are, you know,” says Bucket. “Now, it an’t necessary to say to a man like you, engaged in your business, which is a business of trust and requires a person to be wide awake and have his senses about him and his head screwed on tight (I had an uncle in your business once)—it an’t necessary to say to a man like you that it’s the best and wisest way to keep little matters like this quiet. Don’t you see? Quiet!”\n\n“Certainly, certainly,” returns the other.\n\n“I don’t mind telling YOU,” says Bucket with an engaging appearance of frankness, “that as far as I can understand it, there seems to be a doubt whether this dead person wasn’t entitled to a little property, and whether this female hasn’t been up to some games respecting that property, don’t you see?”\n\n“Oh!” says Mr. Snagsby, but not appearing to see quite distinctly.\n\n“Now, what YOU want,” pursues Bucket, again tapping Mr. Snagsby on the breast in a comfortable and soothing manner, “is that every person should have their rights according to justice. That’s what YOU want.”\n\n“To be sure,” returns Mr. Snagsby with a nod.\n\n“On account of which, and at the same time to oblige a—do you call it, in your business, customer or client? I forget how my uncle used to call it.”\n\n“Why, I generally say customer myself,” replies Mr. Snagsby.\n\n“You’re right!” returns Mr. Bucket, shaking hands with him quite affectionately. “—On account of which, and at the same time to oblige a real good customer, you mean to go down with me, in confidence, to Tom-all-Alone’s and to keep the whole thing quiet ever afterwards and never mention it to any one. That’s about your intentions, if I understand you?”\n\n“You are right, sir. You are right,” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\n“Then here’s your hat,” returns his new friend, quite as intimate with it as if he had made it; “and if you’re ready, I am.”\n\nThey leave Mr. Tulkinghorn, without a ruffle on the surface of his unfathomable depths, drinking his old wine, and go down into the streets.\n\n“You don’t happen to know a very good sort of person of the name of Gridley, do you?” says Bucket in friendly converse as they descend the stairs.\n\n“No,” says Mr. Snagsby, considering, “I don’t know anybody of that name. Why?”\n\n“Nothing particular,” says Bucket; “only having allowed his temper to get a little the better of him and having been threatening some respectable people, he is keeping out of the way of a warrant I have got against him—which it’s a pity that a man of sense should do.”\n\nAs they walk along, Mr. Snagsby observes, as a novelty, that however quick their pace may be, his companion still seems in some undefinable manner to lurk and lounge; also, that whenever he is going to turn to the right or left, he pretends to have a fixed purpose in his mind of going straight ahead, and wheels off, sharply, at the very last moment. Now and then, when they pass a police-constable on his beat, Mr. Snagsby notices that both the constable and his guide fall into a deep abstraction as they come towards each other, and appear entirely to overlook each other, and to gaze into space. In a few instances, Mr. Bucket, coming behind some under-sized young man with a shining hat on, and his sleek hair twisted into one flat curl on each side of his head, almost without glancing at him touches him with his stick, upon which the young man, looking round, instantly evaporates. For the most part Mr. Bucket notices things in general, with a face as unchanging as the great mourning ring on his little finger or the brooch, composed of not much diamond and a good deal of setting, which he wears in his shirt.\n\nWhen they come at last to Tom-all-Alone’s, Mr. Bucket stops for a moment at the corner and takes a lighted bull’s-eye from the constable on duty there, who then accompanies him with his own particular bull’s-eye at his waist. Between his two conductors, Mr. Snagsby passes along the middle of a villainous street, undrained, unventilated, deep in black mud and corrupt water—though the roads are dry elsewhere—and reeking with such smells and sights that he, who has lived in London all his life, can scarce believe his senses. Branching from this street and its heaps of ruins are other streets and courts so infamous that Mr. Snagsby sickens in body and mind and feels as if he were going every moment deeper down into the infernal gulf.\n\n“Draw off a bit here, Mr. Snagsby,” says Bucket as a kind of shabby palanquin is borne towards them, surrounded by a noisy crowd. “Here’s the fever coming up the street!”\n\nAs the unseen wretch goes by, the crowd, leaving that object of attraction, hovers round the three visitors like a dream of horrible faces and fades away up alleys and into ruins and behind walls, and with occasional cries and shrill whistles of warning, thenceforth flits about them until they leave the place.\n\n“Are those the fever-houses, Darby?” Mr. Bucket coolly asks as he turns his bull’s-eye on a line of stinking ruins.\n\nDarby replies that “all them are,” and further that in all, for months and months, the people “have been down by dozens” and have been carried out dead and dying “like sheep with the rot.” Bucket observing to Mr. Snagsby as they go on again that he looks a little poorly, Mr. Snagsby answers that he feels as if he couldn’t breathe the dreadful air.\n\nThere is inquiry made at various houses for a boy named Jo. As few people are known in Tom-all-Alone’s by any Christian sign, there is much reference to Mr. Snagsby whether he means Carrots, or the Colonel, or Gallows, or Young Chisel, or Terrier Tip, or Lanky, or the Brick. Mr. Snagsby describes over and over again. There are conflicting opinions respecting the original of his picture. Some think it must be Carrots, some say the Brick. The Colonel is produced, but is not at all near the thing. Whenever Mr. Snagsby and his conductors are stationary, the crowd flows round, and from its squalid depths obsequious advice heaves up to Mr. Bucket. Whenever they move, and the angry bull’s-eyes glare, it fades away and flits about them up the alleys, and in the ruins, and behind the walls, as before.\n\nAt last there is a lair found out where Toughy, or the Tough Subject, lays him down at night; and it is thought that the Tough Subject may be Jo. Comparison of notes between Mr. Snagsby and the proprietress of the house—a drunken face tied up in a black bundle, and flaring out of a heap of rags on the floor of a dog-hutch which is her private apartment—leads to the establishment of this conclusion. Toughy has gone to the doctor’s to get a bottle of stuff for a sick woman but will be here anon.\n\n“And who have we got here to-night?” says Mr. Bucket, opening another door and glaring in with his bull’s-eye. “Two drunken men, eh? And two women? The men are sound enough,” turning back each sleeper’s arm from his face to look at him. “Are these your good men, my dears?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” returns one of the women. “They are our husbands.”\n\n“Brickmakers, eh?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“What are you doing here? You don’t belong to London.”\n\n“No, sir. We belong to Hertfordshire.”\n\n“Whereabouts in Hertfordshire?”\n\n“Saint Albans.”\n\n“Come up on the tramp?”\n\n“We walked up yesterday. There’s no work down with us at present, but we have done no good by coming here, and shall do none, I expect.”\n\n“That’s not the way to do much good,” says Mr. Bucket, turning his head in the direction of the unconscious figures on the ground.\n\n“It an’t indeed,” replies the woman with a sigh. “Jenny and me knows it full well.”\n\nThe room, though two or three feet higher than the door, is so low that the head of the tallest of the visitors would touch the blackened ceiling if he stood upright. It is offensive to every sense; even the gross candle burns pale and sickly in the polluted air. There are a couple of benches and a higher bench by way of table. The men lie asleep where they stumbled down, but the women sit by the candle. Lying in the arms of the woman who has spoken is a very young child.\n\n“Why, what age do you call that little creature?” says Bucket. “It looks as if it was born yesterday.” He is not at all rough about it; and as he turns his light gently on the infant, Mr. Snagsby is strangely reminded of another infant, encircled with light, that he has seen in pictures.\n\n“He is not three weeks old yet, sir,” says the woman.\n\n“Is he your child?”\n\nThe other woman, who was bending over it when they came in, stoops down again and kisses it as it lies asleep.\n\n“You seem as fond of it as if you were the mother yourself,” says Mr. Bucket.\n\n“I was the mother of one like it, master, and it died.”\n\n“Ah, Jenny, Jenny!” says the other woman to her. “Better so. Much better to think of dead than alive, Jenny! Much better!”\n\n“Why, you an’t such an unnatural woman, I hope,” returns Bucket sternly, “as to wish your own child dead?”\n\n“God knows you are right, master,” she returns. “I am not. I’d stand between it and death with my own life if I could, as true as any pretty lady.”\n\n“Then don’t talk in that wrong manner,” says Mr. Bucket, mollified again. “Why do you do it?”\n\n“It’s brought into my head, master,” returns the woman, her eyes filling with tears, “when I look down at the child lying so. If it was never to wake no more, you’d think me mad, I should take on so. I know that very well. I was with Jenny when she lost hers—warn’t I, Jenny?—and I know how she grieved. But look around you at this place. Look at them,” glancing at the sleepers on the ground. “Look at the boy you’re waiting for, who’s gone out to do me a good turn. Think of the children that your business lays with often and often, and that YOU see grow up!”\n\n“Well, well,” says Mr. Bucket, “you train him respectable, and he’ll be a comfort to you, and look after you in your old age, you know.”\n\n“I mean to try hard,” she answers, wiping her eyes. “But I have been a-thinking, being over-tired to-night and not well with the ague, of all the many things that’ll come in his way. My master will be against it, and he’ll be beat, and see me beat, and made to fear his home, and perhaps to stray wild. If I work for him ever so much, and ever so hard, there’s no one to help me; and if he should be turned bad ‘spite of all I could do, and the time should come when I should sit by him in his sleep, made hard and changed, an’t it likely I should think of him as he lies in my lap now and wish he had died as Jenny’s child died!”\n\n“There, there!” says Jenny. “Liz, you’re tired and ill. Let me take him.”\n\nIn doing so, she displaces the mother’s dress, but quickly readjusts it over the wounded and bruised bosom where the baby has been lying.\n\n“It’s my dead child,” says Jenny, walking up and down as she nurses, “that makes me love this child so dear, and it’s my dead child that makes her love it so dear too, as even to think of its being taken away from her now. While she thinks that, I think what fortune would I give to have my darling back. But we mean the same thing, if we knew how to say it, us two mothers does in our poor hearts!”\n\nAs Mr. Snagsby blows his nose and coughs his cough of sympathy, a step is heard without. Mr. Bucket throws his light into the doorway and says to Mr. Snagsby, “Now, what do you say to Toughy? Will HE do?”\n\n“That’s Jo,” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\nJo stands amazed in the disk of light, like a ragged figure in a magic-lantern, trembling to think that he has offended against the law in not having moved on far enough. Mr. Snagsby, however, giving him the consolatory assurance, “It’s only a job you will be paid for, Jo,” he recovers; and on being taken outside by Mr. Bucket for a little private confabulation, tells his tale satisfactorily, though out of breath.\n\n“I have squared it with the lad,” says Mr. Bucket, returning, “and it’s all right. Now, Mr. Snagsby, we’re ready for you.”\n\nFirst, Jo has to complete his errand of good nature by handing over the physic he has been to get, which he delivers with the laconic verbal direction that “it’s to be all took d’rectly.” Secondly, Mr. Snagsby has to lay upon the table half a crown, his usual panacea for an immense variety of afflictions. Thirdly, Mr. Bucket has to take Jo by the arm a little above the elbow and walk him on before him, without which observance neither the Tough Subject nor any other Subject could be professionally conducted to Lincoln’s Inn Fields. These arrangements completed, they give the women good night and come out once more into black and foul Tom-all-Alone’s.\n\nBy the noisome ways through which they descended into that pit, they gradually emerge from it, the crowd flitting, and whistling, and skulking about them until they come to the verge, where restoration of the bull’s-eyes is made to Darby. Here the crowd, like a concourse of imprisoned demons, turns back, yelling, and is seen no more. Through the clearer and fresher streets, never so clear and fresh to Mr. Snagsby’s mind as now, they walk and ride until they come to Mr. Tulkinghorn’s gate.\n\nAs they ascend the dim stairs (Mr. Tulkinghorn’s chambers being on the first floor), Mr. Bucket mentions that he has the key of the outer door in his pocket and that there is no need to ring. For a man so expert in most things of that kind, Bucket takes time to open the door and makes some noise too. It may be that he sounds a note of preparation.\n\nHowbeit, they come at last into the hall, where a lamp is burning, and so into Mr. Tulkinghorn’s usual room—the room where he drank his old wine to-night. He is not there, but his two old-fashioned candlesticks are, and the room is tolerably light.\n\nMr. Bucket, still having his professional hold of Jo and appearing to Mr. Snagsby to possess an unlimited number of eyes, makes a little way into this room, when Jo starts and stops.\n\n“What’s the matter?” says Bucket in a whisper.\n\n“There she is!” cries Jo.\n\n“Who!”\n\n“The lady!”\n\nA female figure, closely veiled, stands in the middle of the room, where the light falls upon it. It is quite still and silent. The front of the figure is towards them, but it takes no notice of their entrance and remains like a statue.\n\n“Now, tell me,” says Bucket aloud, “how you know that to be the lady.”\n\n“I know the wale,” replies Jo, staring, “and the bonnet, and the gownd.”\n\n“Be quite sure of what you say, Tough,” returns Bucket, narrowly observant of him. “Look again.”\n\n“I am a-looking as hard as ever I can look,” says Jo with starting eyes, “and that there’s the wale, the bonnet, and the gownd.”\n\n“What about those rings you told me of?” asks Bucket.\n\n“A-sparkling all over here,” says Jo, rubbing the fingers of his left hand on the knuckles of his right without taking his eyes from the figure.\n\nThe figure removes the right-hand glove and shows the hand.\n\n“Now, what do you say to that?” asks Bucket.\n\nJo shakes his head. “Not rings a bit like them. Not a hand like that.”\n\n“What are you talking of?” says Bucket, evidently pleased though, and well pleased too.\n\n“Hand was a deal whiter, a deal delicater, and a deal smaller,” returns Jo.\n\n“Why, you’ll tell me I’m my own mother next,” says Mr. Bucket. “Do you recollect the lady’s voice?”\n\n“I think I does,” says Jo.\n\nThe figure speaks. “Was it at all like this? I will speak as long as you like if you are not sure. Was it this voice, or at all like this voice?”\n\nJo looks aghast at Mr. Bucket. “Not a bit!”\n\n“Then, what,” retorts that worthy, pointing to the figure, “did you say it was the lady for?”\n\n“Cos,” says Jo with a perplexed stare but without being at all shaken in his certainty, “cos that there’s the wale, the bonnet, and the gownd. It is her and it an’t her. It an’t her hand, nor yet her rings, nor yet her woice. But that there’s the wale, the bonnet, and the gownd, and they’re wore the same way wot she wore ’em, and it’s her height wot she wos, and she giv me a sov’ring and hooked it.”\n\n“Well!” says Mr. Bucket slightly, “we haven’t got much good out of YOU. But, however, here’s five shillings for you. Take care how you spend it, and don’t get yourself into trouble.” Bucket stealthily tells the coins from one hand into the other like counters—which is a way he has, his principal use of them being in these games of skill—and then puts them, in a little pile, into the boy’s hand and takes him out to the door, leaving Mr. Snagsby, not by any means comfortable under these mysterious circumstances, alone with the veiled figure. But on Mr. Tulkinghorn’s coming into the room, the veil is raised and a sufficiently good-looking Frenchwoman is revealed, though her expression is something of the intensest.\n\n“Thank you, Mademoiselle Hortense,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn with his usual equanimity. “I will give you no further trouble about this little wager.”\n\n“You will do me the kindness to remember, sir, that I am not at present placed?” says mademoiselle.\n\n“Certainly, certainly!”\n\n“And to confer upon me the favour of your distinguished recommendation?”\n\n“By all means, Mademoiselle Hortense.”\n\n“A word from Mr. Tulkinghorn is so powerful.”\n\n“It shall not be wanting, mademoiselle.”\n\n“Receive the assurance of my devoted gratitude, dear sir.”\n\n“Good night.”\n\nMademoiselle goes out with an air of native gentility; and Mr. Bucket, to whom it is, on an emergency, as natural to be groom of the ceremonies as it is to be anything else, shows her downstairs, not without gallantry.\n\n“Well, Bucket?” quoth Mr. Tulkinghorn on his return.\n\n“It’s all squared, you see, as I squared it myself, sir. There an’t a doubt that it was the other one with this one’s dress on. The boy was exact respecting colours and everything. Mr. Snagsby, I promised you as a man that he should be sent away all right. Don’t say it wasn’t done!”\n\n“You have kept your word, sir,” returns the stationer; “and if I can be of no further use, Mr. Tulkinghorn, I think, as my little woman will be getting anxious—”\n\n“Thank you, Snagsby, no further use,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “I am quite indebted to you for the trouble you have taken already.”\n\n“Not at all, sir. I wish you good night.”\n\n“You see, Mr. Snagsby,” says Mr. Bucket, accompanying him to the door and shaking hands with him over and over again, “what I like in you is that you’re a man it’s of no use pumping; that’s what YOU are. When you know you have done a right thing, you put it away, and it’s done with and gone, and there’s an end of it. That’s what YOU do.”\n\n“That is certainly what I endeavour to do, sir,” returns Mr. Snagsby.\n\n“No, you don’t do yourself justice. It an’t what you endeavour to do,” says Mr. Bucket, shaking hands with him and blessing him in the tenderest manner, “it’s what you DO. That’s what I estimate in a man in your way of business.”\n\nMr. Snagsby makes a suitable response and goes homeward so confused by the events of the evening that he is doubtful of his being awake and out—doubtful of the reality of the streets through which he goes—doubtful of the reality of the moon that shines above him. He is presently reassured on these subjects by the unchallengeable reality of Mrs. Snagsby, sitting up with her head in a perfect beehive of curl-papers and night-cap, who has dispatched Guster to the police-station with official intelligence of her husband’s being made away with, and who within the last two hours has passed through every stage of swooning with the greatest decorum. But as the little woman feelingly says, many thanks she gets for it!\nEsther’s Narrative\n\nWe came home from Mr. Boythorn’s after six pleasant weeks. We were often in the park and in the woods and seldom passed the lodge where we had taken shelter without looking in to speak to the keeper’s wife; but we saw no more of Lady Dedlock, except at church on Sundays. There was company at Chesney Wold; and although several beautiful faces surrounded her, her face retained the same influence on me as at first. I do not quite know even now whether it was painful or pleasurable, whether it drew me towards her or made me shrink from her. I think I admired her with a kind of fear, and I know that in her presence my thoughts always wandered back, as they had done at first, to that old time of my life.\n\nI had a fancy, on more than one of these Sundays, that what this lady so curiously was to me, I was to her—I mean that I disturbed her thoughts as she influenced mine, though in some different way. But when I stole a glance at her and saw her so composed and distant and unapproachable, I felt this to be a foolish weakness. Indeed, I felt the whole state of my mind in reference to her to be weak and unreasonable, and I remonstrated with myself about it as much as I could.\n\nOne incident that occurred before we quitted Mr. Boythorn’s house, I had better mention in this place.\n\nI was walking in the garden with Ada when I was told that some one wished to see me. Going into the breakfast-room where this person was waiting, I found it to be the French maid who had cast off her shoes and walked through the wet grass on the day when it thundered and lightened.\n\n“Mademoiselle,” she began, looking fixedly at me with her too-eager eyes, though otherwise presenting an agreeable appearance and speaking neither with boldness nor servility, “I have taken a great liberty in coming here, but you know how to excuse it, being so amiable, mademoiselle.”\n\n“No excuse is necessary,” I returned, “if you wish to speak to me.”\n\n“That is my desire, mademoiselle. A thousand thanks for the permission. I have your leave to speak. Is it not?” she said in a quick, natural way.\n\n“Certainly,” said I.\n\n“Mademoiselle, you are so amiable! Listen then, if you please. I have left my Lady. We could not agree. My Lady is so high, so very high. Pardon! Mademoiselle, you are right!” Her quickness anticipated what I might have said presently but as yet had only thought. “It is not for me to come here to complain of my Lady. But I say she is so high, so very high. I will not say a word more. All the world knows that.”\n\n“Go on, if you please,” said I.\n\n“Assuredly; mademoiselle, I am thankful for your politeness. Mademoiselle, I have an inexpressible desire to find service with a young lady who is good, accomplished, beautiful. You are good, accomplished, and beautiful as an angel. Ah, could I have the honour of being your domestic!”\n\n“I am sorry—” I began.\n\n“Do not dismiss me so soon, mademoiselle!” she said with an involuntary contraction of her fine black eyebrows. “Let me hope a moment! Mademoiselle, I know this service would be more retired than that which I have quitted. Well! I wish that. I know this service would be less distinguished than that which I have quitted. Well! I wish that, I know that I should win less, as to wages here. Good. I am content.”\n\n“I assure you,” said I, quite embarrassed by the mere idea of having such an attendant, “that I keep no maid—”\n\n“Ah, mademoiselle, but why not? Why not, when you can have one so devoted to you! Who would be enchanted to serve you; who would be so true, so zealous, and so faithful every day! Mademoiselle, I wish with all my heart to serve you. Do not speak of money at present. Take me as I am. For nothing!”\n\nShe was so singularly earnest that I drew back, almost afraid of her. Without appearing to notice it, in her ardour she still pressed herself upon me, speaking in a rapid subdued voice, though always with a certain grace and propriety.\n\n“Mademoiselle, I come from the South country where we are quick and where we like and dislike very strong. My Lady was too high for me; I was too high for her. It is done—past—finished! Receive me as your domestic, and I will serve you well. I will do more for you than you figure to yourself now. Chut! Mademoiselle, I will—no matter, I will do my utmost possible in all things. If you accept my service, you will not repent it. Mademoiselle, you will not repent it, and I will serve you well. You don’t know how well!”\n\nThere was a lowering energy in her face as she stood looking at me while I explained the impossibility of my engaging her (without thinking it necessary to say how very little I desired to do so), which seemed to bring visibly before me some woman from the streets of Paris in the reign of terror.\n\nShe heard me out without interruption and then said with her pretty accent and in her mildest voice, “Hey, mademoiselle, I have received my answer! I am sorry of it. But I must go elsewhere and seek what I have not found here. Will you graciously let me kiss your hand?”\n\nShe looked at me more intently as she took it, and seemed to take note, with her momentary touch, of every vein in it. “I fear I surprised you, mademoiselle, on the day of the storm?” she said with a parting curtsy.\n\nI confessed that she had surprised us all.\n\n“I took an oath, mademoiselle,” she said, smiling, “and I wanted to stamp it on my mind so that I might keep it faithfully. And I will! Adieu, mademoiselle!”\n\nSo ended our conference, which I was very glad to bring to a close. I supposed she went away from the village, for I saw her no more; and nothing else occurred to disturb our tranquil summer pleasures until six weeks were out and we returned home as I began just now by saying.\n\nAt that time, and for a good many weeks after that time, Richard was constant in his visits. Besides coming every Saturday or Sunday and remaining with us until Monday morning, he sometimes rode out on horseback unexpectedly and passed the evening with us and rode back again early next day. He was as vivacious as ever and told us he was very industrious, but I was not easy in my mind about him. It appeared to me that his industry was all misdirected. I could not find that it led to anything but the formation of delusive hopes in connexion with the suit already the pernicious cause of so much sorrow and ruin. He had got at the core of that mystery now, he told us, and nothing could be plainer than that the will under which he and Ada were to take I don’t know how many thousands of pounds must be finally established if there were any sense or justice in the Court of Chancery—but oh, what a great IF that sounded in my ears—and that this happy conclusion could not be much longer delayed. He proved this to himself by all the weary arguments on that side he had read, and every one of them sunk him deeper in the infatuation. He had even begun to haunt the court. He told us how he saw Miss Flite there daily, how they talked together, and how he did her little kindnesses, and how, while he laughed at her, he pitied her from his heart. But he never thought—never, my poor, dear, sanguine Richard, capable of so much happiness then, and with such better things before him—what a fatal link was riveting between his fresh youth and her faded age, between his free hopes and her caged birds, and her hungry garret, and her wandering mind.\n\nAda loved him too well to mistrust him much in anything he said or did, and my guardian, though he frequently complained of the east wind and read more than usual in the growlery, preserved a strict silence on the subject. So I thought one day when I went to London to meet Caddy Jellyby, at her solicitation, I would ask Richard to be in waiting for me at the coach-office, that we might have a little talk together. I found him there when I arrived, and we walked away arm in arm.\n\n“Well, Richard,” said I as soon as I could begin to be grave with him, “are you beginning to feel more settled now?”\n\n“Oh, yes, my dear!” returned Richard. “I’m all right enough.”\n\n“But settled?” said I.\n\n“How do you mean, settled?” returned Richard with his gay laugh.\n\n“Settled in the law,” said I.\n\n“Oh, aye,” replied Richard, “I’m all right enough.”\n\n“You said that before, my dear Richard.”\n\n“And you don’t think it’s an answer, eh? Well! Perhaps it’s not. Settled? You mean, do I feel as if I were settling down?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why, no, I can’t say I am settling down,” said Richard, strongly emphasizing “down,” as if that expressed the difficulty, “because one can’t settle down while this business remains in such an unsettled state. When I say this business, of course I mean the—forbidden subject.”\n\n“Do you think it will ever be in a settled state?” said I.\n\n“Not the least doubt of it,” answered Richard.\n\nWe walked a little way without speaking, and presently Richard addressed me in his frankest and most feeling manner, thus: “My dear Esther, I understand you, and I wish to heaven I were a more constant sort of fellow. I don’t mean constant to Ada, for I love her dearly—better and better every day—but constant to myself. (Somehow, I mean something that I can’t very well express, but you’ll make it out.) If I were a more constant sort of fellow, I should have held on either to Badger or to Kenge and Carboy like grim death, and should have begun to be steady and systematic by this time, and shouldn’t be in debt, and—”\n\n“ARE you in debt, Richard?”\n\n“Yes,” said Richard, “I am a little so, my dear. Also, I have taken rather too much to billiards and that sort of thing. Now the murder’s out; you despise me, Esther, don’t you?”\n\n“You know I don’t,” said I.\n\n“You are kinder to me than I often am to myself,” he returned. “My dear Esther, I am a very unfortunate dog not to be more settled, but how CAN I be more settled? If you lived in an unfinished house, you couldn’t settle down in it; if you were condemned to leave everything you undertook unfinished, you would find it hard to apply yourself to anything; and yet that’s my unhappy case. I was born into this unfinished contention with all its chances and changes, and it began to unsettle me before I quite knew the difference between a suit at law and a suit of clothes; and it has gone on unsettling me ever since; and here I am now, conscious sometimes that I am but a worthless fellow to love my confiding cousin Ada.”\n\nWe were in a solitary place, and he put his hands before his eyes and sobbed as he said the words.\n\n“Oh, Richard!” said I. “Do not be so moved. You have a noble nature, and Ada’s love may make you worthier every day.”\n\n“I know, my dear,” he replied, pressing my arm, “I know all that. You mustn’t mind my being a little soft now, for I have had all this upon my mind for a long time, and have often meant to speak to you, and have sometimes wanted opportunity and sometimes courage. I know what the thought of Ada ought to do for me, but it doesn’t do it. I am too unsettled even for that. I love her most devotedly, and yet I do her wrong, in doing myself wrong, every day and hour. But it can’t last for ever. We shall come on for a final hearing and get judgment in our favour, and then you and Ada shall see what I can really be!”\n\nIt had given me a pang to hear him sob and see the tears start out between his fingers, but that was infinitely less affecting to me than the hopeful animation with which he said these words.\n\n“I have looked well into the papers, Esther. I have been deep in them for months,” he continued, recovering his cheerfulness in a moment, “and you may rely upon it that we shall come out triumphant. As to years of delay, there has been no want of them, heaven knows! And there is the greater probability of our bringing the matter to a speedy close; in fact, it’s on the paper now. It will be all right at last, and then you shall see!”\n\nRecalling how he had just now placed Messrs. Kenge and Carboy in the same category with Mr. Badger, I asked him when he intended to be articled in Lincoln’s Inn.\n\n“There again! I think not at all, Esther,” he returned with an effort. “I fancy I have had enough of it. Having worked at Jarndyce and Jarndyce like a galley slave, I have slaked my thirst for the law and satisfied myself that I shouldn’t like it. Besides, I find it unsettles me more and more to be so constantly upon the scene of action. So what,” continued Richard, confident again by this time, “do I naturally turn my thoughts to?”\n\n“I can’t imagine,” said I.\n\n“Don’t look so serious,” returned Richard, “because it’s the best thing I can do, my dear Esther, I am certain. It’s not as if I wanted a profession for life. These proceedings will come to a termination, and then I am provided for. No. I look upon it as a pursuit which is in its nature more or less unsettled, and therefore suited to my temporary condition—I may say, precisely suited. What is it that I naturally turn my thoughts to?”\n\nI looked at him and shook my head.\n\n“What,” said Richard, in a tone of perfect conviction, “but the army!”\n\n“The army?” said I.\n\n“The army, of course. What I have to do is to get a commission; and—there I am, you know!” said Richard.\n\nAnd then he showed me, proved by elaborate calculations in his pocket-book, that supposing he had contracted, say, two hundred pounds of debt in six months out of the army; and that he contracted no debt at all within a corresponding period in the army—as to which he had quite made up his mind; this step must involve a saving of four hundred pounds in a year, or two thousand pounds in five years, which was a considerable sum. And then he spoke so ingenuously and sincerely of the sacrifice he made in withdrawing himself for a time from Ada, and of the earnestness with which he aspired—as in thought he always did, I know full well—to repay her love, and to ensure her happiness, and to conquer what was amiss in himself, and to acquire the very soul of decision, that he made my heart ache keenly, sorely. For, I thought, how would this end, how could this end, when so soon and so surely all his manly qualities were touched by the fatal blight that ruined everything it rested on!\n\nI spoke to Richard with all the earnestness I felt, and all the hope I could not quite feel then, and implored him for Ada’s sake not to put any trust in Chancery. To all I said, Richard readily assented, riding over the court and everything else in his easy way and drawing the brightest pictures of the character he was to settle into—alas, when the grievous suit should loose its hold upon him! We had a long talk, but it always came back to that, in substance.\n\nAt last we came to Soho Square, where Caddy Jellyby had appointed to wait for me, as a quiet place in the neighbourhood of Newman Street. Caddy was in the garden in the centre and hurried out as soon as I appeared. After a few cheerful words, Richard left us together.\n\n“Prince has a pupil over the way, Esther,” said Caddy, “and got the key for us. So if you will walk round and round here with me, we can lock ourselves in and I can tell you comfortably what I wanted to see your dear good face about.”\n\n“Very well, my dear,” said I. “Nothing could be better.” So Caddy, after affectionately squeezing the dear good face as she called it, locked the gate, and took my arm, and we began to walk round the garden very cosily.\n\n“You see, Esther,” said Caddy, who thoroughly enjoyed a little confidence, “after you spoke to me about its being wrong to marry without Ma’s knowledge, or even to keep Ma long in the dark respecting our engagement—though I don’t believe Ma cares much for me, I must say—I thought it right to mention your opinions to Prince. In the first place because I want to profit by everything you tell me, and in the second place because I have no secrets from Prince.”\n\n“I hope he approved, Caddy?”\n\n“Oh, my dear! I assure you he would approve of anything you could say. You have no idea what an opinion he has of you!”\n\n“Indeed!”\n\n“Esther, it’s enough to make anybody but me jealous,” said Caddy, laughing and shaking her head; “but it only makes me joyful, for you are the first friend I ever had, and the best friend I ever can have, and nobody can respect and love you too much to please me.”\n\n“Upon my word, Caddy,” said I, “you are in the general conspiracy to keep me in a good humour. Well, my dear?”\n\n“Well! I am going to tell you,” replied Caddy, crossing her hands confidentially upon my arm. “So we talked a good deal about it, and so I said to Prince, ‘Prince, as Miss Summerson—’”\n\n“I hope you didn’t say ‘Miss Summerson’?”\n\n“No. I didn’t!” cried Caddy, greatly pleased and with the brightest of faces. “I said, ‘Esther.’ I said to Prince, ‘As Esther is decidedly of that opinion, Prince, and has expressed it to me, and always hints it when she writes those kind notes, which you are so fond of hearing me read to you, I am prepared to disclose the truth to Ma whenever you think proper. And I think, Prince,’ said I, ‘that Esther thinks that I should be in a better, and truer, and more honourable position altogether if you did the same to your papa.’”\n\n“Yes, my dear,” said I. “Esther certainly does think so.”\n\n“So I was right, you see!” exclaimed Caddy. “Well! This troubled Prince a good deal, not because he had the least doubt about it, but because he is so considerate of the feelings of old Mr. Turveydrop; and he had his apprehensions that old Mr. Turveydrop might break his heart, or faint away, or be very much overcome in some affecting manner or other if he made such an announcement. He feared old Mr. Turveydrop might consider it undutiful and might receive too great a shock. For old Mr. Turveydrop’s deportment is very beautiful, you know, Esther,” said Caddy, “and his feelings are extremely sensitive.”\n\n“Are they, my dear?”\n\n“Oh, extremely sensitive. Prince says so. Now, this has caused my darling child—I didn’t mean to use the expression to you, Esther,” Caddy apologized, her face suffused with blushes, “but I generally call Prince my darling child.”\n\nI laughed; and Caddy laughed and blushed, and went on.\n\n“This has caused him, Esther—”\n\n“Caused whom, my dear?”\n\n“Oh, you tiresome thing!” said Caddy, laughing, with her pretty face on fire. “My darling child, if you insist upon it! This has caused him weeks of uneasiness and has made him delay, from day to day, in a very anxious manner. At last he said to me, ‘Caddy, if Miss Summerson, who is a great favourite with my father, could be prevailed upon to be present when I broke the subject, I think I could do it.’ So I promised I would ask you. And I made up my mind, besides,” said Caddy, looking at me hopefully but timidly, “that if you consented, I would ask you afterwards to come with me to Ma. This is what I meant when I said in my note that I had a great favour and a great assistance to beg of you. And if you thought you could grant it, Esther, we should both be very grateful.”\n\n“Let me see, Caddy,” said I, pretending to consider. “Really, I think I could do a greater thing than that if the need were pressing. I am at your service and the darling child’s, my dear, whenever you like.”\n\nCaddy was quite transported by this reply of mine, being, I believe, as susceptible to the least kindness or encouragement as any tender heart that ever beat in this world; and after another turn or two round the garden, during which she put on an entirely new pair of gloves and made herself as resplendent as possible that she might do no avoidable discredit to the Master of Deportment, we went to Newman Street direct.\n\nPrince was teaching, of course. We found him engaged with a not very hopeful pupil—a stubborn little girl with a sulky forehead, a deep voice, and an inanimate, dissatisfied mama—whose case was certainly not rendered more hopeful by the confusion into which we threw her preceptor. The lesson at last came to an end, after proceeding as discordantly as possible; and when the little girl had changed her shoes and had had her white muslin extinguished in shawls, she was taken away. After a few words of preparation, we then went in search of Mr. Turveydrop, whom we found, grouped with his hat and gloves, as a model of deportment, on the sofa in his private apartment—the only comfortable room in the house. He appeared to have dressed at his leisure in the intervals of a light collation, and his dressing-case, brushes, and so forth, all of quite an elegant kind, lay about.\n\n“Father, Miss Summerson; Miss Jellyby.”\n\n“Charmed! Enchanted!” said Mr. Turveydrop, rising with his high-shouldered bow. “Permit me!” Handing chairs. “Be seated!” Kissing the tips of his left fingers. “Overjoyed!” Shutting his eyes and rolling. “My little retreat is made a paradise.” Recomposing himself on the sofa like the second gentleman in Europe.\n\n“Again you find us, Miss Summerson,” said he, “using our little arts to polish, polish! Again the sex stimulates us and rewards us by the condescension of its lovely presence. It is much in these times (and we have made an awfully degenerating business of it since the days of his Royal Highness the Prince Regent—my patron, if I may presume to say so) to experience that deportment is not wholly trodden under foot by mechanics. That it can yet bask in the smile of beauty, my dear madam.”\n\nI said nothing, which I thought a suitable reply; and he took a pinch of snuff.\n\n“My dear son,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “you have four schools this afternoon. I would recommend a hasty sandwich.”\n\n“Thank you, father,” returned Prince, “I will be sure to be punctual. My dear father, may I beg you to prepare your mind for what I am going to say?”\n\n“Good heaven!” exclaimed the model, pale and aghast as Prince and Caddy, hand in hand, bent down before him. “What is this? Is this lunacy! Or what is this?”\n\n“Father,” returned Prince with great submission, “I love this young lady, and we are engaged.”\n\n“Engaged!” cried Mr. Turveydrop, reclining on the sofa and shutting out the sight with his hand. “An arrow launched at my brain by my own child!”\n\n“We have been engaged for some time, father,” faltered Prince, “and Miss Summerson, hearing of it, advised that we should declare the fact to you and was so very kind as to attend on the present occasion. Miss Jellyby is a young lady who deeply respects you, father.”\n\nMr. Turveydrop uttered a groan.\n\n“No, pray don’t! Pray don’t, father,” urged his son. “Miss Jellyby is a young lady who deeply respects you, and our first desire is to consider your comfort.”\n\nMr. Turveydrop sobbed.\n\n“No, pray don’t, father!” cried his son.\n\n“Boy,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “it is well that your sainted mother is spared this pang. Strike deep, and spare not. Strike home, sir, strike home!”\n\n“Pray don’t say so, father,” implored Prince, in tears. “It goes to my heart. I do assure you, father, that our first wish and intention is to consider your comfort. Caroline and I do not forget our duty—what is my duty is Caroline’s, as we have often said together—and with your approval and consent, father, we will devote ourselves to making your life agreeable.”\n\n“Strike home,” murmured Mr. Turveydrop. “Strike home!” But he seemed to listen, I thought, too.\n\n“My dear father,” returned Prince, “we well know what little comforts you are accustomed to and have a right to, and it will always be our study and our pride to provide those before anything. If you will bless us with your approval and consent, father, we shall not think of being married until it is quite agreeable to you; and when we ARE married, we shall always make you—of course—our first consideration. You must ever be the head and master here, father; and we feel how truly unnatural it would be in us if we failed to know it or if we failed to exert ourselves in every possible way to please you.”\n\nMr. Turveydrop underwent a severe internal struggle and came upright on the sofa again with his cheeks puffing over his stiff cravat, a perfect model of parental deportment.\n\n“My son!” said Mr. Turveydrop. “My children! I cannot resist your prayer. Be happy!”\n\nHis benignity as he raised his future daughter-in-law and stretched out his hand to his son (who kissed it with affectionate respect and gratitude) was the most confusing sight I ever saw.\n\n“My children,” said Mr. Turveydrop, paternally encircling Caddy with his left arm as she sat beside him, and putting his right hand gracefully on his hip. “My son and daughter, your happiness shall be my care. I will watch over you. You shall always live with me”—meaning, of course, I will always live with you—“this house is henceforth as much yours as mine; consider it your home. May you long live to share it with me!”\n\nThe power of his deportment was such that they really were as much overcome with thankfulness as if, instead of quartering himself upon them for the rest of his life, he were making some munificent sacrifice in their favour.\n\n“For myself, my children,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “I am falling into the sear and yellow leaf, and it is impossible to say how long the last feeble traces of gentlemanly deportment may linger in this weaving and spinning age. But, so long, I will do my duty to society and will show myself, as usual, about town. My wants are few and simple. My little apartment here, my few essentials for the toilet, my frugal morning meal, and my little dinner will suffice. I charge your dutiful affection with the supply of these requirements, and I charge myself with all the rest.”\n\nThey were overpowered afresh by his uncommon generosity.\n\n“My son,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “for those little points in which you are deficient—points of deportment, which are born with a man, which may be improved by cultivation, but can never be originated—you may still rely on me. I have been faithful to my post since the days of his Royal Highness the Prince Regent, and I will not desert it now. No, my son. If you have ever contemplated your father’s poor position with a feeling of pride, you may rest assured that he will do nothing to tarnish it. For yourself, Prince, whose character is different (we cannot be all alike, nor is it advisable that we should), work, be industrious, earn money, and extend the connexion as much as possible.”\n\n“That you may depend I will do, dear father, with all my heart,” replied Prince.\n\n“I have no doubt of it,” said Mr. Turveydrop. “Your qualities are not shining, my dear child, but they are steady and useful. And to both of you, my children, I would merely observe, in the spirit of a sainted wooman on whose path I had the happiness of casting, I believe, SOME ray of light, take care of the establishment, take care of my simple wants, and bless you both!”\n\nOld Mr. Turveydrop then became so very gallant, in honour of the occasion, that I told Caddy we must really go to Thavies Inn at once if we were to go at all that day. So we took our departure after a very loving farewell between Caddy and her betrothed, and during our walk she was so happy and so full of old Mr. Turveydrop’s praises that I would not have said a word in his disparagement for any consideration.\n\nThe house in Thavies Inn had bills in the windows announcing that it was to let, and it looked dirtier and gloomier and ghastlier than ever. The name of poor Mr. Jellyby had appeared in the list of bankrupts but a day or two before, and he was shut up in the dining-room with two gentlemen and a heap of blue bags, account-books, and papers, making the most desperate endeavours to understand his affairs. They appeared to me to be quite beyond his comprehension, for when Caddy took me into the dining-room by mistake and we came upon Mr. Jellyby in his spectacles, forlornly fenced into a corner by the great dining-table and the two gentlemen, he seemed to have given up the whole thing and to be speechless and insensible.\n\nGoing upstairs to Mrs. Jellyby’s room (the children were all screaming in the kitchen, and there was no servant to be seen), we found that lady in the midst of a voluminous correspondence, opening, reading, and sorting letters, with a great accumulation of torn covers on the floor. She was so preoccupied that at first she did not know me, though she sat looking at me with that curious, bright-eyed, far-off look of hers.\n\n“Ah! Miss Summerson!” she said at last. “I was thinking of something so different! I hope you are well. I am happy to see you. Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Clare quite well?”\n\nI hoped in return that Mr. Jellyby was quite well.\n\n“Why, not quite, my dear,” said Mrs. Jellyby in the calmest manner. “He has been unfortunate in his affairs and is a little out of spirits. Happily for me, I am so much engaged that I have no time to think about it. We have, at the present moment, one hundred and seventy families, Miss Summerson, averaging five persons in each, either gone or going to the left bank of the Niger.”\n\nI thought of the one family so near us who were neither gone nor going to the left bank of the Niger, and wondered how she could be so placid.\n\n“You have brought Caddy back, I see,” observed Mrs. Jellyby with a glance at her daughter. “It has become quite a novelty to see her here. She has almost deserted her old employment and in fact obliges me to employ a boy.”\n\n“I am sure, Ma—” began Caddy.\n\n“Now you know, Caddy,” her mother mildly interposed, “that I DO employ a boy, who is now at his dinner. What is the use of your contradicting?”\n\n“I was not going to contradict, Ma,” returned Caddy. “I was only going to say that surely you wouldn’t have me be a mere drudge all my life.”\n\n“I believe, my dear,” said Mrs. Jellyby, still opening her letters, casting her bright eyes smilingly over them, and sorting them as she spoke, “that you have a business example before you in your mother. Besides. A mere drudge? If you had any sympathy with the destinies of the human race, it would raise you high above any such idea. But you have none. I have often told you, Caddy, you have no such sympathy.”\n\n“Not if it’s Africa, Ma, I have not.”\n\n“Of course you have not. Now, if I were not happily so much engaged, Miss Summerson,” said Mrs. Jellyby, sweetly casting her eyes for a moment on me and considering where to put the particular letter she had just opened, “this would distress and disappoint me. But I have so much to think of, in connexion with Borrioboola-Gha and it is so necessary I should concentrate myself that there is my remedy, you see.”\n\nAs Caddy gave me a glance of entreaty, and as Mrs. Jellyby was looking far away into Africa straight through my bonnet and head, I thought it a good opportunity to come to the subject of my visit and to attract Mrs. Jellyby’s attention.\n\n“Perhaps,” I began, “you will wonder what has brought me here to interrupt you.”\n\n“I am always delighted to see Miss Summerson,” said Mrs. Jellyby, pursuing her employment with a placid smile. “Though I wish,” and she shook her head, “she was more interested in the Borrioboolan project.”\n\n“I have come with Caddy,” said I, “because Caddy justly thinks she ought not to have a secret from her mother and fancies I shall encourage and aid her (though I am sure I don’t know how) in imparting one.”\n\n“Caddy,” said Mrs. Jellyby, pausing for a moment in her occupation and then serenely pursuing it after shaking her head, “you are going to tell me some nonsense.”\n\nCaddy untied the strings of her bonnet, took her bonnet off, and letting it dangle on the floor by the strings, and crying heartily, said, “Ma, I am engaged.”\n\n“Oh, you ridiculous child!” observed Mrs. Jellyby with an abstracted air as she looked over the dispatch last opened; “what a goose you are!”\n\n“I am engaged, Ma,” sobbed Caddy, “to young Mr. Turveydrop, at the academy; and old Mr. Turveydrop (who is a very gentlemanly man indeed) has given his consent, and I beg and pray you’ll give us yours, Ma, because I never could be happy without it. I never, never could!” sobbed Caddy, quite forgetful of her general complainings and of everything but her natural affection.\n\n“You see again, Miss Summerson,” observed Mrs. Jellyby serenely, “what a happiness it is to be so much occupied as I am and to have this necessity for self-concentration that I have. Here is Caddy engaged to a dancing-master’s son—mixed up with people who have no more sympathy with the destinies of the human race than she has herself! This, too, when Mr. Quale, one of the first philanthropists of our time, has mentioned to me that he was really disposed to be interested in her!”\n\n“Ma, I always hated and detested Mr. Quale!” sobbed Caddy.\n\n“Caddy, Caddy!” returned Mrs. Jellyby, opening another letter with the greatest complacency. “I have no doubt you did. How could you do otherwise, being totally destitute of the sympathies with which he overflows! Now, if my public duties were not a favourite child to me, if I were not occupied with large measures on a vast scale, these petty details might grieve me very much, Miss Summerson. But can I permit the film of a silly proceeding on the part of Caddy (from whom I expect nothing else) to interpose between me and the great African continent? No. No,” repeated Mrs. Jellyby in a calm clear voice, and with an agreeable smile, as she opened more letters and sorted them. “No, indeed.”\n\nI was so unprepared for the perfect coolness of this reception, though I might have expected it, that I did not know what to say. Caddy seemed equally at a loss. Mrs. Jellyby continued to open and sort letters and to repeat occasionally in quite a charming tone of voice and with a smile of perfect composure, “No, indeed.”\n\n“I hope, Ma,” sobbed poor Caddy at last, “you are not angry?”\n\n“Oh, Caddy, you really are an absurd girl,” returned Mrs. Jellyby, “to ask such questions after what I have said of the preoccupation of my mind.”\n\n“And I hope, Ma, you give us your consent and wish us well?” said Caddy.\n\n“You are a nonsensical child to have done anything of this kind,” said Mrs. Jellyby; “and a degenerate child, when you might have devoted yourself to the great public measure. But the step is taken, and I have engaged a boy, and there is no more to be said. Now, pray, Caddy,” said Mrs. Jellyby, for Caddy was kissing her, “don’t delay me in my work, but let me clear off this heavy batch of papers before the afternoon post comes in!”\n\nI thought I could not do better than take my leave; I was detained for a moment by Caddy’s saying, “You won’t object to my bringing him to see you, Ma?”\n\n“Oh, dear me, Caddy,” cried Mrs. Jellyby, who had relapsed into that distant contemplation, “have you begun again? Bring whom?”\n\n“Him, Ma.”\n\n“Caddy, Caddy!” said Mrs. Jellyby, quite weary of such little matters. “Then you must bring him some evening which is not a Parent Society night, or a Branch night, or a Ramification night. You must accommodate the visit to the demands upon my time. My dear Miss Summerson, it was very kind of you to come here to help out this silly chit. Good-bye! When I tell you that I have fifty-eight new letters from manufacturing families anxious to understand the details of the native and coffee-cultivation question this morning, I need not apologize for having very little leisure.”\n\nI was not surprised by Caddy’s being in low spirits when we went downstairs, or by her sobbing afresh on my neck, or by her saying she would far rather have been scolded than treated with such indifference, or by her confiding to me that she was so poor in clothes that how she was ever to be married creditably she didn’t know. I gradually cheered her up by dwelling on the many things she would do for her unfortunate father and for Peepy when she had a home of her own; and finally we went downstairs into the damp dark kitchen, where Peepy and his little brothers and sisters were grovelling on the stone floor and where we had such a game of play with them that to prevent myself from being quite torn to pieces I was obliged to fall back on my fairy-tales. From time to time I heard loud voices in the parlour overhead, and occasionally a violent tumbling about of the furniture. The last effect I am afraid was caused by poor Mr. Jellyby’s breaking away from the dining-table and making rushes at the window with the intention of throwing himself into the area whenever he made any new attempt to understand his affairs.\n\nAs I rode quietly home at night after the day’s bustle, I thought a good deal of Caddy’s engagement and felt confirmed in my hopes (in spite of the elder Mr. Turveydrop) that she would be the happier and better for it. And if there seemed to be but a slender chance of her and her husband ever finding out what the model of deportment really was, why that was all for the best too, and who would wish them to be wiser? I did not wish them to be any wiser and indeed was half ashamed of not entirely believing in him myself. And I looked up at the stars, and thought about travellers in distant countries and the stars THEY saw, and hoped I might always be so blest and happy as to be useful to some one in my small way.\n\nThey were so glad to see me when I got home, as they always were, that I could have sat down and cried for joy if that had not been a method of making myself disagreeable. Everybody in the house, from the lowest to the highest, showed me such a bright face of welcome, and spoke so cheerily, and was so happy to do anything for me, that I suppose there never was such a fortunate little creature in the world.\n\nWe got into such a chatty state that night, through Ada and my guardian drawing me out to tell them all about Caddy, that I went on prose, prose, prosing for a length of time. At last I got up to my own room, quite red to think how I had been holding forth, and then I heard a soft tap at my door. So I said, “Come in!” and there came in a pretty little girl, neatly dressed in mourning, who dropped a curtsy.\n\n“If you please, miss,” said the little girl in a soft voice, “I am Charley.”\n\n“Why, so you are,” said I, stooping down in astonishment and giving her a kiss. “How glad am I to see you, Charley!”\n\n“If you please, miss,” pursued Charley in the same soft voice, “I’m your maid.”\n\n“Charley?”\n\n“If you please, miss, I’m a present to you, with Mr. Jarndyce’s love.”\n\nI sat down with my hand on Charley’s neck and looked at Charley.\n\n“And oh, miss,” says Charley, clapping her hands, with the tears starting down her dimpled cheeks, “Tom’s at school, if you please, and learning so good! And little Emma, she’s with Mrs. Blinder, miss, a-being took such care of! And Tom, he would have been at school—and Emma, she would have been left with Mrs. Blinder—and me, I should have been here—all a deal sooner, miss; only Mr. Jarndyce thought that Tom and Emma and me had better get a little used to parting first, we was so small. Don’t cry, if you please, miss!”\n\n“I can’t help it, Charley.”\n\n“No, miss, nor I can’t help it,” says Charley. “And if you please, miss, Mr. Jarndyce’s love, and he thinks you’ll like to teach me now and then. And if you please, Tom and Emma and me is to see each other once a month. And I’m so happy and so thankful, miss,” cried Charley with a heaving heart, “and I’ll try to be such a good maid!”\n\n“Oh, Charley dear, never forget who did all this!”\n\n“No, miss, I never will. Nor Tom won’t. Nor yet Emma. It was all you, miss.”\n\n“I have known nothing of it. It was Mr. Jarndyce, Charley.”\n\n“Yes, miss, but it was all done for the love of you and that you might be my mistress. If you please, miss, I am a little present with his love, and it was all done for the love of you. Me and Tom was to be sure to remember it.”\n\nCharley dried her eyes and entered on her functions, going in her matronly little way about and about the room and folding up everything she could lay her hands upon. Presently Charley came creeping back to my side and said, “Oh, don’t cry, if you please, miss.”\n\nAnd I said again, “I can’t help it, Charley.”\n\nAnd Charley said again, “No, miss, nor I can’t help it.” And so, after all, I did cry for joy indeed, and so did she.\nAn Appeal Case\n\nAs soon as Richard and I had held the conversation of which I have given an account, Richard communicated the state of his mind to Mr. Jarndyce. I doubt if my guardian were altogether taken by surprise when he received the representation, though it caused him much uneasiness and disappointment. He and Richard were often closeted together, late at night and early in the morning, and passed whole days in London, and had innumerable appointments with Mr. Kenge, and laboured through a quantity of disagreeable business. While they were thus employed, my guardian, though he underwent considerable inconvenience from the state of the wind and rubbed his head so constantly that not a single hair upon it ever rested in its right place, was as genial with Ada and me as at any other time, but maintained a steady reserve on these matters. And as our utmost endeavours could only elicit from Richard himself sweeping assurances that everything was going on capitally and that it really was all right at last, our anxiety was not much relieved by him.\n\nWe learnt, however, as the time went on, that a new application was made to the Lord Chancellor on Richard’s behalf as an infant and a ward, and I don’t know what, and that there was a quantity of talking, and that the Lord Chancellor described him in open court as a vexatious and capricious infant, and that the matter was adjourned and readjourned, and referred, and reported on, and petitioned about until Richard began to doubt (as he told us) whether, if he entered the army at all, it would not be as a veteran of seventy or eighty years of age. At last an appointment was made for him to see the Lord Chancellor again in his private room, and there the Lord Chancellor very seriously reproved him for trifling with time and not knowing his mind—“a pretty good joke, I think,” said Richard, “from that quarter!”—and at last it was settled that his application should be granted. His name was entered at the Horse Guards as an applicant for an ensign’s commission; the purchase-money was deposited at an agent’s; and Richard, in his usual characteristic way, plunged into a violent course of military study and got up at five o’clock every morning to practise the broadsword exercise.\n\nThus, vacation succeeded term, and term succeeded vacation. We sometimes heard of Jarndyce and Jarndyce as being in the paper or out of the paper, or as being to be mentioned, or as being to be spoken to; and it came on, and it went off. Richard, who was now in a professor’s house in London, was able to be with us less frequently than before; my guardian still maintained the same reserve; and so time passed until the commission was obtained and Richard received directions with it to join a regiment in Ireland.\n\nHe arrived post-haste with the intelligence one evening, and had a long conference with my guardian. Upwards of an hour elapsed before my guardian put his head into the room where Ada and I were sitting and said, “Come in, my dears!” We went in and found Richard, whom we had last seen in high spirits, leaning on the chimney-piece looking mortified and angry.\n\n“Rick and I, Ada,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “are not quite of one mind. Come, come, Rick, put a brighter face upon it!”\n\n“You are very hard with me, sir,” said Richard. “The harder because you have been so considerate to me in all other respects and have done me kindnesses that I can never acknowledge. I never could have been set right without you, sir.”\n\n“Well, well!” said Mr. Jarndyce. “I want to set you more right yet. I want to set you more right with yourself.”\n\n“I hope you will excuse my saying, sir,” returned Richard in a fiery way, but yet respectfully, “that I think I am the best judge about myself.”\n\n“I hope you will excuse my saying, my dear Rick,” observed Mr. Jarndyce with the sweetest cheerfulness and good humour, “that it’s quite natural in you to think so, but I don’t think so. I must do my duty, Rick, or you could never care for me in cool blood; and I hope you will always care for me, cool and hot.”\n\nAda had turned so pale that he made her sit down in his reading-chair and sat beside her.\n\n“It’s nothing, my dear,” he said, “it’s nothing. Rick and I have only had a friendly difference, which we must state to you, for you are the theme. Now you are afraid of what’s coming.”\n\n“I am not indeed, cousin John,” replied Ada with a smile, “if it is to come from you.”\n\n“Thank you, my dear. Do you give me a minute’s calm attention, without looking at Rick. And, little woman, do you likewise. My dear girl,” putting his hand on hers as it lay on the side of the easy-chair, “you recollect the talk we had, we four when the little woman told me of a little love affair?”\n\n“It is not likely that either Richard or I can ever forget your kindness that day, cousin John.”\n\n“I can never forget it,” said Richard.\n\n“And I can never forget it,” said Ada.\n\n“So much the easier what I have to say, and so much the easier for us to agree,” returned my guardian, his face irradiated by the gentleness and honour of his heart. “Ada, my bird, you should know that Rick has now chosen his profession for the last time. All that he has of certainty will be expended when he is fully equipped. He has exhausted his resources and is bound henceforward to the tree he has planted.”\n\n“Quite true that I have exhausted my present resources, and I am quite content to know it. But what I have of certainty, sir,” said Richard, “is not all I have.”\n\n“Rick, Rick!” cried my guardian with a sudden terror in his manner, and in an altered voice, and putting up his hands as if he would have stopped his ears. “For the love of God, don’t found a hope or expectation on the family curse! Whatever you do on this side the grave, never give one lingering glance towards the horrible phantom that has haunted us so many years. Better to borrow, better to beg, better to die!”\n\nWe were all startled by the fervour of this warning. Richard bit his lip and held his breath, and glanced at me as if he felt, and knew that I felt too, how much he needed it.\n\n“Ada, my dear,” said Mr. Jarndyce, recovering his cheerfulness, “these are strong words of advice, but I live in Bleak House and have seen a sight here. Enough of that. All Richard had to start him in the race of life is ventured. I recommend to him and you, for his sake and your own, that he should depart from us with the understanding that there is no sort of contract between you. I must go further. I will be plain with you both. You were to confide freely in me, and I will confide freely in you. I ask you wholly to relinquish, for the present, any tie but your relationship.”\n\n“Better to say at once, sir,” returned Richard, “that you renounce all confidence in me and that you advise Ada to do the same.”\n\n“Better to say nothing of the sort, Rick, because I don’t mean it.”\n\n“You think I have begun ill, sir,” retorted Richard. “I HAVE, I know.”\n\n“How I hoped you would begin, and how go on, I told you when we spoke of these things last,” said Mr. Jarndyce in a cordial and encouraging manner. “You have not made that beginning yet, but there is a time for all things, and yours is not gone by; rather, it is just now fully come. Make a clear beginning altogether. You two (very young, my dears) are cousins. As yet, you are nothing more. What more may come must come of being worked out, Rick, and no sooner.”\n\n“You are very hard with me, sir,” said Richard. “Harder than I could have supposed you would be.”\n\n“My dear boy,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “I am harder with myself when I do anything that gives you pain. You have your remedy in your own hands. Ada, it is better for him that he should be free and that there should be no youthful engagement between you. Rick, it is better for her, much better; you owe it to her. Come! Each of you will do what is best for the other, if not what is best for yourselves.”\n\n“Why is it best, sir?” returned Richard hastily. “It was not when we opened our hearts to you. You did not say so then.”\n\n“I have had experience since. I don’t blame you, Rick, but I have had experience since.”\n\n“You mean of me, sir.”\n\n“Well! Yes, of both of you,” said Mr. Jarndyce kindly. “The time is not come for your standing pledged to one another. It is not right, and I must not recognize it. Come, come, my young cousins, begin afresh! Bygones shall be bygones, and a new page turned for you to write your lives in.”\n\nRichard gave an anxious glance at Ada but said nothing.\n\n“I have avoided saying one word to either of you or to Esther,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “until now, in order that we might be open as the day, and all on equal terms. I now affectionately advise, I now most earnestly entreat, you two to part as you came here. Leave all else to time, truth, and steadfastness. If you do otherwise, you will do wrong, and you will have made me do wrong in ever bringing you together.”\n\nA long silence succeeded.\n\n“Cousin Richard,” said Ada then, raising her blue eyes tenderly to his face, “after what our cousin John has said, I think no choice is left us. Your mind may be quite at ease about me, for you will leave me here under his care and will be sure that I can have nothing to wish for—quite sure if I guide myself by his advice. I—I don’t doubt, cousin Richard,” said Ada, a little confused, “that you are very fond of me, and I—I don’t think you will fall in love with anybody else. But I should like you to consider well about it too, as I should like you to be in all things very happy. You may trust in me, cousin Richard. I am not at all changeable; but I am not unreasonable, and should never blame you. Even cousins may be sorry to part; and in truth I am very, very sorry, Richard, though I know it’s for your welfare. I shall always think of you affectionately, and often talk of you with Esther, and—and perhaps you will sometimes think a little of me, cousin Richard. So now,” said Ada, going up to him and giving him her trembling hand, “we are only cousins again, Richard—for the time perhaps—and I pray for a blessing on my dear cousin, wherever he goes!”\n\nIt was strange to me that Richard should not be able to forgive my guardian for entertaining the very same opinion of him which he himself had expressed of himself in much stronger terms to me. But it was certainly the case. I observed with great regret that from this hour he never was as free and open with Mr. Jarndyce as he had been before. He had every reason given him to be so, but he was not; and solely on his side, an estrangement began to arise between them.\n\nIn the business of preparation and equipment he soon lost himself, and even his grief at parting from Ada, who remained in Hertfordshire while he, Mr. Jarndyce, and I went up to London for a week. He remembered her by fits and starts, even with bursts of tears, and at such times would confide to me the heaviest self-reproaches. But in a few minutes he would recklessly conjure up some undefinable means by which they were both to be made rich and happy for ever, and would become as gay as possible.\n\nIt was a busy time, and I trotted about with him all day long, buying a variety of things of which he stood in need. Of the things he would have bought if he had been left to his own ways I say nothing. He was perfectly confidential with me, and often talked so sensibly and feelingly about his faults and his vigorous resolutions, and dwelt so much upon the encouragement he derived from these conversations that I could never have been tired if I had tried.\n\nThere used, in that week, to come backward and forward to our lodging to fence with Richard a person who had formerly been a cavalry soldier; he was a fine bluff-looking man, of a frank free bearing, with whom Richard had practised for some months. I heard so much about him, not only from Richard, but from my guardian too, that I was purposely in the room with my work one morning after breakfast when he came.\n\n“Good morning, Mr. George,” said my guardian, who happened to be alone with me. “Mr. Carstone will be here directly. Meanwhile, Miss Summerson is very happy to see you, I know. Sit down.”\n\nHe sat down, a little disconcerted by my presence, I thought, and without looking at me, drew his heavy sunburnt hand across and across his upper lip.\n\n“You are as punctual as the sun,” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Military time, sir,” he replied. “Force of habit. A mere habit in me, sir. I am not at all business-like.”\n\n“Yet you have a large establishment, too, I am told?” said Mr. Jarndyce.\n\n“Not much of a one, sir. I keep a shooting gallery, but not much of a one.”\n\n“And what kind of a shot and what kind of a swordsman do you make of Mr. Carstone?” said my guardian.\n\n“Pretty good, sir,” he replied, folding his arms upon his broad chest and looking very large. “If Mr. Carstone was to give his full mind to it, he would come out very good.”\n\n“But he don’t, I suppose?” said my guardian.\n\n“He did at first, sir, but not afterwards. Not his full mind. Perhaps he has something else upon it—some young lady, perhaps.” His bright dark eyes glanced at me for the first time.\n\n“He has not me upon his mind, I assure you, Mr. George,” said I, laughing, “though you seem to suspect me.”\n\nHe reddened a little through his brown and made me a trooper’s bow. “No offence, I hope, miss. I am one of the roughs.”\n\n“Not at all,” said I. “I take it as a compliment.”\n\nIf he had not looked at me before, he looked at me now in three or four quick successive glances. “I beg your pardon, sir,” he said to my guardian with a manly kind of diffidence, “but you did me the honour to mention the young lady’s name—”\n\n“Miss Summerson.”\n\n“Miss Summerson,” he repeated, and looked at me again.\n\n“Do you know the name?” I asked.\n\n“No, miss. To my knowledge I never heard it. I thought I had seen you somewhere.”\n\n“I think not,” I returned, raising my head from my work to look at him; and there was something so genuine in his speech and manner that I was glad of the opportunity. “I remember faces very well.”\n\n“So do I, miss!” he returned, meeting my look with the fullness of his dark eyes and broad forehead. “Humph! What set me off, now, upon that!”\n\nHis once more reddening through his brown and being disconcerted by his efforts to remember the association brought my guardian to his relief.\n\n“Have you many pupils, Mr. George?”\n\n“They vary in their number, sir. Mostly they’re but a small lot to live by.”\n\n“And what classes of chance people come to practise at your gallery?”\n\n“All sorts, sir. Natives and foreigners. From gentlemen to ’prentices. I have had Frenchwomen come, before now, and show themselves dabs at pistol-shooting. Mad people out of number, of course, but THEY go everywhere where the doors stand open.”\n\n“People don’t come with grudges and schemes of finishing their practice with live targets, I hope?” said my guardian, smiling.\n\n“Not much of that, sir, though that HAS happened. Mostly they come for skill—or idleness. Six of one, and half-a-dozen of the other. I beg your pardon,” said Mr. George, sitting stiffly upright and squaring an elbow on each knee, “but I believe you’re a Chancery suitor, if I have heard correct?”\n\n“I am sorry to say I am.”\n\n“I have had one of YOUR compatriots in my time, sir.”\n\n“A Chancery suitor?” returned my guardian. “How was that?”\n\n“Why, the man was so badgered and worried and tortured by being knocked about from post to pillar, and from pillar to post,” said Mr. George, “that he got out of sorts. I don’t believe he had any idea of taking aim at anybody, but he was in that condition of resentment and violence that he would come and pay for fifty shots and fire away till he was red hot. One day I said to him when there was nobody by and he had been talking to me angrily about his wrongs, ‘If this practice is a safety-valve, comrade, well and good; but I don’t altogether like your being so bent upon it in your present state of mind; I’d rather you took to something else.’ I was on my guard for a blow, he was that passionate; but he received it in very good part and left off directly. We shook hands and struck up a sort of friendship.”\n\n“What was that man?” asked my guardian in a new tone of interest.\n\n“Why, he began by being a small Shropshire farmer before they made a baited bull of him,” said Mr. George.\n\n“Was his name Gridley?”\n\n“It was, sir.”\n\nMr. George directed another succession of quick bright glances at me as my guardian and I exchanged a word or two of surprise at the coincidence, and I therefore explained to him how we knew the name. He made me another of his soldierly bows in acknowledgment of what he called my condescension.\n\n“I don’t know,” he said as he looked at me, “what it is that sets me off again—but—bosh! What’s my head running against!” He passed one of his heavy hands over his crisp dark hair as if to sweep the broken thoughts out of his mind and sat a little forward, with one arm akimbo and the other resting on his leg, looking in a brown study at the ground.\n\n“I am sorry to learn that the same state of mind has got this Gridley into new troubles and that he is in hiding,” said my guardian.\n\n“So I am told, sir,” returned Mr. George, still musing and looking on the ground. “So I am told.”\n\n“You don’t know where?”\n\n“No, sir,” returned the trooper, lifting up his eyes and coming out of his reverie. “I can’t say anything about him. He will be worn out soon, I expect. You may file a strong man’s heart away for a good many years, but it will tell all of a sudden at last.”\n\nRichard’s entrance stopped the conversation. Mr. George rose, made me another of his soldierly bows, wished my guardian a good day, and strode heavily out of the room.\n\nThis was the morning of the day appointed for Richard’s departure. We had no more purchases to make now; I had completed all his packing early in the afternoon; and our time was disengaged until night, when he was to go to Liverpool for Holyhead. Jarndyce and Jarndyce being again expected to come on that day, Richard proposed to me that we should go down to the court and hear what passed. As it was his last day, and he was eager to go, and I had never been there, I gave my consent and we walked down to Westminster, where the court was then sitting. We beguiled the way with arrangements concerning the letters that Richard was to write to me and the letters that I was to write to him and with a great many hopeful projects. My guardian knew where we were going and therefore was not with us.\n\nWhen we came to the court, there was the Lord Chancellor—the same whom I had seen in his private room in Lincoln’s Inn—sitting in great state and gravity on the bench, with the mace and seals on a red table below him and an immense flat nosegay, like a little garden, which scented the whole court. Below the table, again, was a long row of solicitors, with bundles of papers on the matting at their feet; and then there were the gentlemen of the bar in wigs and gowns—some awake and some asleep, and one talking, and nobody paying much attention to what he said. The Lord Chancellor leaned back in his very easy chair with his elbow on the cushioned arm and his forehead resting on his hand; some of those who were present dozed; some read the newspapers; some walked about or whispered in groups: all seemed perfectly at their ease, by no means in a hurry, very unconcerned, and extremely comfortable.\n\nTo see everything going on so smoothly and to think of the roughness of the suitors’ lives and deaths; to see all that full dress and ceremony and to think of the waste, and want, and beggared misery it represented; to consider that while the sickness of hope deferred was raging in so many hearts this polite show went calmly on from day to day, and year to year, in such good order and composure; to behold the Lord Chancellor and the whole array of practitioners under him looking at one another and at the spectators as if nobody had ever heard that all over England the name in which they were assembled was a bitter jest, was held in universal horror, contempt, and indignation, was known for something so flagrant and bad that little short of a miracle could bring any good out of it to any one—this was so curious and self-contradictory to me, who had no experience of it, that it was at first incredible, and I could not comprehend it. I sat where Richard put me, and tried to listen, and looked about me; but there seemed to be no reality in the whole scene except poor little Miss Flite, the madwoman, standing on a bench and nodding at it.\n\nMiss Flite soon espied us and came to where we sat. She gave me a gracious welcome to her domain and indicated, with much gratification and pride, its principal attractions. Mr. Kenge also came to speak to us and did the honours of the place in much the same way, with the bland modesty of a proprietor. It was not a very good day for a visit, he said; he would have preferred the first day of term; but it was imposing, it was imposing.\n\nWhen we had been there half an hour or so, the case in progress—if I may use a phrase so ridiculous in such a connexion—seemed to die out of its own vapidity, without coming, or being by anybody expected to come, to any result. The Lord Chancellor then threw down a bundle of papers from his desk to the gentlemen below him, and somebody said, “Jarndyce and Jarndyce.” Upon this there was a buzz, and a laugh, and a general withdrawal of the bystanders, and a bringing in of great heaps, and piles, and bags and bags full of papers.\n\nI think it came on “for further directions”—about some bill of costs, to the best of my understanding, which was confused enough. But I counted twenty-three gentlemen in wigs who said they were “in it,” and none of them appeared to understand it much better than I. They chatted about it with the Lord Chancellor, and contradicted and explained among themselves, and some of them said it was this way, and some of them said it was that way, and some of them jocosely proposed to read huge volumes of affidavits, and there was more buzzing and laughing, and everybody concerned was in a state of idle entertainment, and nothing could be made of it by anybody. After an hour or so of this, and a good many speeches being begun and cut short, it was “referred back for the present,” as Mr. Kenge said, and the papers were bundled up again before the clerks had finished bringing them in.\n\nI glanced at Richard on the termination of these hopeless proceedings and was shocked to see the worn look of his handsome young face. “It can’t last for ever, Dame Durden. Better luck next time!” was all he said.\n\nI had seen Mr. Guppy bringing in papers and arranging them for Mr. Kenge; and he had seen me and made me a forlorn bow, which rendered me desirous to get out of the court. Richard had given me his arm and was taking me away when Mr. Guppy came up.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr. Carstone,” said he in a whisper, “and Miss Summerson’s also, but there’s a lady here, a friend of mine, who knows her and wishes to have the pleasure of shaking hands.” As he spoke, I saw before me, as if she had started into bodily shape from my remembrance, Mrs. Rachael of my godmother’s house.\n\n“How do you do, Esther?” said she. “Do you recollect me?”\n\nI gave her my hand and told her yes and that she was very little altered.\n\n“I wonder you remember those times, Esther,” she returned with her old asperity. “They are changed now. Well! I am glad to see you, and glad you are not too proud to know me.” But indeed she seemed disappointed that I was not.\n\n“Proud, Mrs. Rachael!” I remonstrated.\n\n“I am married, Esther,” she returned, coldly correcting me, “and am Mrs. Chadband. Well! I wish you good day, and I hope you’ll do well.”\n\nMr. Guppy, who had been attentive to this short dialogue, heaved a sigh in my ear and elbowed his own and Mrs. Rachael’s way through the confused little crowd of people coming in and going out, which we were in the midst of and which the change in the business had brought together. Richard and I were making our way through it, and I was yet in the first chill of the late unexpected recognition when I saw, coming towards us, but not seeing us, no less a person than Mr. George. He made nothing of the people about him as he tramped on, staring over their heads into the body of the court.\n\n“George!” said Richard as I called his attention to him.\n\n“You are well met, sir,” he returned. “And you, miss. Could you point a person out for me, I want? I don’t understand these places.”\n\nTurning as he spoke and making an easy way for us, he stopped when we were out of the press in a corner behind a great red curtain.\n\n“There’s a little cracked old woman,” he began, “that—”\n\nI put up my finger, for Miss Flite was close by me, having kept beside me all the time and having called the attention of several of her legal acquaintance to me (as I had overheard to my confusion) by whispering in their ears, “Hush! Fitz Jarndyce on my left!”\n\n“Hem!” said Mr. George. “You remember, miss, that we passed some conversation on a certain man this morning? Gridley,” in a low whisper behind his hand.\n\n“Yes,” said I.\n\n“He is hiding at my place. I couldn’t mention it. Hadn’t his authority. He is on his last march, miss, and has a whim to see her. He says they can feel for one another, and she has been almost as good as a friend to him here. I came down to look for her, for when I sat by Gridley this afternoon, I seemed to hear the roll of the muffled drums.”\n\n“Shall I tell her?” said I.\n\n“Would you be so good?” he returned with a glance of something like apprehension at Miss Flite. “It’s a providence I met you, miss; I doubt if I should have known how to get on with that lady.” And he put one hand in his breast and stood upright in a martial attitude as I informed little Miss Flite, in her ear, of the purport of his kind errand.\n\n“My angry friend from Shropshire! Almost as celebrated as myself!” she exclaimed. “Now really! My dear, I will wait upon him with the greatest pleasure.”\n\n“He is living concealed at Mr. George’s,” said I. “Hush! This is Mr. George.”\n\n“In—deed!” returned Miss Flite. “Very proud to have the honour! A military man, my dear. You know, a perfect general!” she whispered to me.\n\nPoor Miss Flite deemed it necessary to be so courtly and polite, as a mark of her respect for the army, and to curtsy so very often that it was no easy matter to get her out of the court. When this was at last done, and addressing Mr. George as “General,” she gave him her arm, to the great entertainment of some idlers who were looking on, he was so discomposed and begged me so respectfully “not to desert him” that I could not make up my mind to do it, especially as Miss Flite was always tractable with me and as she too said, “Fitz Jarndyce, my dear, you will accompany us, of course.” As Richard seemed quite willing, and even anxious, that we should see them safely to their destination, we agreed to do so. And as Mr. George informed us that Gridley’s mind had run on Mr. Jarndyce all the afternoon after hearing of their interview in the morning, I wrote a hasty note in pencil to my guardian to say where we were gone and why. Mr. George sealed it at a coffee-house, that it might lead to no discovery, and we sent it off by a ticket-porter.\n\nWe then took a hackney-coach and drove away to the neighbourhood of Leicester Square. We walked through some narrow courts, for which Mr. George apologized, and soon came to the shooting gallery, the door of which was closed. As he pulled a bell-handle which hung by a chain to the door-post, a very respectable old gentleman with grey hair, wearing spectacles, and dressed in a black spencer and gaiters and a broad-brimmed hat, and carrying a large gold-beaded cane, addressed him.\n\n“I ask your pardon, my good friend,” said he, “but is this George’s Shooting Gallery?”\n\n“It is, sir,” returned Mr. George, glancing up at the great letters in which that inscription was painted on the whitewashed wall.\n\n“Oh! To be sure!” said the old gentleman, following his eyes. “Thank you. Have you rung the bell?”\n\n“My name is George, sir, and I have rung the bell.”\n\n“Oh, indeed?” said the old gentleman. “Your name is George? Then I am here as soon as you, you see. You came for me, no doubt?”\n\n“No, sir. You have the advantage of me.”\n\n“Oh, indeed?” said the old gentleman. “Then it was your young man who came for me. I am a physician and was requested—five minutes ago—to come and visit a sick man at George’s Shooting Gallery.”\n\n“The muffled drums,” said Mr. George, turning to Richard and me and gravely shaking his head. “It’s quite correct, sir. Will you please to walk in.”\n\nThe door being at that moment opened by a very singular-looking little man in a green-baize cap and apron, whose face and hands and dress were blackened all over, we passed along a dreary passage into a large building with bare brick walls where there were targets, and guns, and swords, and other things of that kind. When we had all arrived here, the physician stopped, and taking off his hat, appeared to vanish by magic and to leave another and quite a different man in his place.\n\n“Now lookee here, George,” said the man, turning quickly round upon him and tapping him on the breast with a large forefinger. “You know me, and I know you. You’re a man of the world, and I’m a man of the world. My name’s Bucket, as you are aware, and I have got a peace-warrant against Gridley. You have kept him out of the way a long time, and you have been artful in it, and it does you credit.”\n\nMr. George, looking hard at him, bit his lip and shook his head.\n\n“Now, George,” said the other, keeping close to him, “you’re a sensible man and a well-conducted man; that’s what YOU are, beyond a doubt. And mind you, I don’t talk to you as a common character, because you have served your country and you know that when duty calls we must obey. Consequently you’re very far from wanting to give trouble. If I required assistance, you’d assist me; that’s what YOU’D do. Phil Squod, don’t you go a-sidling round the gallery like that”—the dirty little man was shuffling about with his shoulder against the wall, and his eyes on the intruder, in a manner that looked threatening—“because I know you and won’t have it.”\n\n“Phil!” said Mr. George.\n\n“Yes, guv’ner.”\n\n“Be quiet.”\n\nThe little man, with a low growl, stood still.\n\n“Ladies and gentlemen,” said Mr. Bucket, “you’ll excuse anything that may appear to be disagreeable in this, for my name’s Inspector Bucket of the Detective, and I have a duty to perform. George, I know where my man is because I was on the roof last night and saw him through the skylight, and you along with him. He is in there, you know,” pointing; “that’s where HE is—on a sofy. Now I must see my man, and I must tell my man to consider himself in custody; but you know me, and you know I don’t want to take any uncomfortable measures. You give me your word, as from one man to another (and an old soldier, mind you, likewise), that it’s honourable between us two, and I’ll accommodate you to the utmost of my power.”\n\n“I give it,” was the reply. “But it wasn’t handsome in you, Mr. Bucket.”\n\n“Gammon, George! Not handsome?” said Mr. Bucket, tapping him on his broad breast again and shaking hands with him. “I don’t say it wasn’t handsome in you to keep my man so close, do I? Be equally good-tempered to me, old boy! Old William Tell, Old Shaw, the Life Guardsman! Why, he’s a model of the whole British army in himself, ladies and gentlemen. I’d give a fifty-pun’ note to be such a figure of a man!”\n\nThe affair being brought to this head, Mr. George, after a little consideration, proposed to go in first to his comrade (as he called him), taking Miss Flite with him. Mr. Bucket agreeing, they went away to the further end of the gallery, leaving us sitting and standing by a table covered with guns. Mr. Bucket took this opportunity of entering into a little light conversation, asking me if I were afraid of fire-arms, as most young ladies were; asking Richard if he were a good shot; asking Phil Squod which he considered the best of those rifles and what it might be worth first-hand, telling him in return that it was a pity he ever gave way to his temper, for he was naturally so amiable that he might have been a young woman, and making himself generally agreeable.\n\nAfter a time he followed us to the further end of the gallery, and Richard and I were going quietly away when Mr. George came after us. He said that if we had no objection to see his comrade, he would take a visit from us very kindly. The words had hardly passed his lips when the bell was rung and my guardian appeared, “on the chance,” he slightly observed, “of being able to do any little thing for a poor fellow involved in the same misfortune as himself.” We all four went back together and went into the place where Gridley was.\n\nIt was a bare room, partitioned off from the gallery with unpainted wood. As the screening was not more than eight or ten feet high and only enclosed the sides, not the top, the rafters of the high gallery roof were overhead, and the skylight through which Mr. Bucket had looked down. The sun was low—near setting—and its light came redly in above, without descending to the ground. Upon a plain canvas-covered sofa lay the man from Shropshire, dressed much as we had seen him last, but so changed that at first I recognized no likeness in his colourless face to what I recollected.\n\nHe had been still writing in his hiding-place, and still dwelling on his grievances, hour after hour. A table and some shelves were covered with manuscript papers and with worn pens and a medley of such tokens. Touchingly and awfully drawn together, he and the little mad woman were side by side and, as it were, alone. She sat on a chair holding his hand, and none of us went close to them.\n\nHis voice had faded, with the old expression of his face, with his strength, with his anger, with his resistance to the wrongs that had at last subdued him. The faintest shadow of an object full of form and colour is such a picture of it as he was of the man from Shropshire whom we had spoken with before.\n\nHe inclined his head to Richard and me and spoke to my guardian.\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce, it is very kind of you to come to see me. I am not long to be seen, I think. I am very glad to take your hand, sir. You are a good man, superior to injustice, and God knows I honour you.”\n\nThey shook hands earnestly, and my guardian said some words of comfort to him.\n\n“It may seem strange to you, sir,” returned Gridley; “I should not have liked to see you if this had been the first time of our meeting. But you know I made a fight for it, you know I stood up with my single hand against them all, you know I told them the truth to the last, and told them what they were, and what they had done to me; so I don’t mind your seeing me, this wreck.”\n\n“You have been courageous with them many and many a time,” returned my guardian.\n\n“Sir, I have been,” with a faint smile. “I told you what would come of it when I ceased to be so, and see here! Look at us—look at us!” He drew the hand Miss Flite held through her arm and brought her something nearer to him.\n\n“This ends it. Of all my old associations, of all my old pursuits and hopes, of all the living and the dead world, this one poor soul alone comes natural to me, and I am fit for. There is a tie of many suffering years between us two, and it is the only tie I ever had on earth that Chancery has not broken.”\n\n“Accept my blessing, Gridley,” said Miss Flite in tears. “Accept my blessing!”\n\n“I thought, boastfully, that they never could break my heart, Mr. Jarndyce. I was resolved that they should not. I did believe that I could, and would, charge them with being the mockery they were until I died of some bodily disorder. But I am worn out. How long I have been wearing out, I don’t know; I seemed to break down in an hour. I hope they may never come to hear of it. I hope everybody here will lead them to believe that I died defying them, consistently and perseveringly, as I did through so many years.”\n\nHere Mr. Bucket, who was sitting in a corner by the door, good-naturedly offered such consolation as he could administer.\n\n“Come, come!” he said from his corner. “Don’t go on in that way, Mr. Gridley. You are only a little low. We are all of us a little low sometimes. I am. Hold up, hold up! You’ll lose your temper with the whole round of ’em, again and again; and I shall take you on a score of warrants yet, if I have luck.”\n\nHe only shook his head.\n\n“Don’t shake your head,” said Mr. Bucket. “Nod it; that’s what I want to see you do. Why, Lord bless your soul, what times we have had together! Haven’t I seen you in the Fleet over and over again for contempt? Haven’t I come into court, twenty afternoons for no other purpose than to see you pin the Chancellor like a bull-dog? Don’t you remember when you first began to threaten the lawyers, and the peace was sworn against you two or three times a week? Ask the little old lady there; she has been always present. Hold up, Mr. Gridley, hold up, sir!”\n\n“What are you going to do about him?” asked George in a low voice.\n\n“I don’t know yet,” said Bucket in the same tone. Then resuming his encouragement, he pursued aloud: “Worn out, Mr. Gridley? After dodging me for all these weeks and forcing me to climb the roof here like a tom cat and to come to see you as a doctor? That ain’t like being worn out. I should think not! Now I tell you what you want. You want excitement, you know, to keep YOU up; that’s what YOU want. You’re used to it, and you can’t do without it. I couldn’t myself. Very well, then; here’s this warrant got by Mr. Tulkinghorn of Lincoln’s Inn Fields, and backed into half-a-dozen counties since. What do you say to coming along with me, upon this warrant, and having a good angry argument before the magistrates? It’ll do you good; it’ll freshen you up and get you into training for another turn at the Chancellor. Give in? Why, I am surprised to hear a man of your energy talk of giving in. You mustn’t do that. You’re half the fun of the fair in the Court of Chancery. George, you lend Mr. Gridley a hand, and let’s see now whether he won’t be better up than down.”\n\n“He is very weak,” said the trooper in a low voice.\n\n“Is he?” returned Bucket anxiously. “I only want to rouse him. I don’t like to see an old acquaintance giving in like this. It would cheer him up more than anything if I could make him a little waxy with me. He’s welcome to drop into me, right and left, if he likes. I shall never take advantage of it.”\n\nThe roof rang with a scream from Miss Flite, which still rings in my ears.\n\n“Oh, no, Gridley!” she cried as he fell heavily and calmly back from before her. “Not without my blessing. After so many years!”\n\nThe sun was down, the light had gradually stolen from the roof, and the shadow had crept upward. But to me the shadow of that pair, one living and one dead, fell heavier on Richard’s departure than the darkness of the darkest night. And through Richard’s farewell words I heard it echoed: “Of all my old associations, of all my old pursuits and hopes, of all the living and the dead world, this one poor soul alone comes natural to me, and I am fit for. There is a tie of many suffering years between us two, and it is the only tie I ever had on earth that Chancery has not broken!”\nMrs. Snagsby Sees It All\n\nThere is disquietude in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street. Black suspicion hides in that peaceful region. The mass of Cook’s Courtiers are in their usual state of mind, no better and no worse; but Mr. Snagsby is changed, and his little woman knows it.\n\nFor Tom-all-Alone’s and Lincoln’s Inn Fields persist in harnessing themselves, a pair of ungovernable coursers, to the chariot of Mr. Snagsby’s imagination; and Mr. Bucket drives; and the passengers are Jo and Mr. Tulkinghorn; and the complete equipage whirls though the law-stationery business at wild speed all round the clock. Even in the little front kitchen where the family meals are taken, it rattles away at a smoking pace from the dinner-table, when Mr. Snagsby pauses in carving the first slice of the leg of mutton baked with potatoes and stares at the kitchen wall.\n\nMr. Snagsby cannot make out what it is that he has had to do with. Something is wrong somewhere, but what something, what may come of it, to whom, when, and from which unthought of and unheard of quarter is the puzzle of his life. His remote impressions of the robes and coronets, the stars and garters, that sparkle through the surface-dust of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s chambers; his veneration for the mysteries presided over by that best and closest of his customers, whom all the Inns of Court, all Chancery Lane, and all the legal neighbourhood agree to hold in awe; his remembrance of Detective Mr. Bucket with his forefinger and his confidential manner, impossible to be evaded or declined, persuade him that he is a party to some dangerous secret without knowing what it is. And it is the fearful peculiarity of this condition that, at any hour of his daily life, at any opening of the shop-door, at any pull of the bell, at any entrance of a messenger, or any delivery of a letter, the secret may take air and fire, explode, and blow up—Mr. Bucket only knows whom.\n\nFor which reason, whenever a man unknown comes into the shop (as many men unknown do) and says, “Is Mr. Snagsby in?” or words to that innocent effect, Mr. Snagsby’s heart knocks hard at his guilty breast. He undergoes so much from such inquiries that when they are made by boys he revenges himself by flipping at their ears over the counter and asking the young dogs what they mean by it and why they can’t speak out at once? More impracticable men and boys persist in walking into Mr. Snagsby’s sleep and terrifying him with unaccountable questions, so that often when the cock at the little dairy in Cursitor Street breaks out in his usual absurd way about the morning, Mr. Snagsby finds himself in a crisis of nightmare, with his little woman shaking him and saying “What’s the matter with the man!”\n\nThe little woman herself is not the least item in his difficulty. To know that he is always keeping a secret from her, that he has under all circumstances to conceal and hold fast a tender double tooth, which her sharpness is ever ready to twist out of his head, gives Mr. Snagsby, in her dentistical presence, much of the air of a dog who has a reservation from his master and will look anywhere rather than meet his eye.\n\nThese various signs and tokens, marked by the little woman, are not lost upon her. They impel her to say, “Snagsby has something on his mind!” And thus suspicion gets into Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street. From suspicion to jealousy, Mrs. Snagsby finds the road as natural and short as from Cook’s Court to Chancery Lane. And thus jealousy gets into Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street. Once there (and it was always lurking thereabout), it is very active and nimble in Mrs. Snagsby’s breast, prompting her to nocturnal examinations of Mr. Snagsby’s pockets; to secret perusals of Mr. Snagsby’s letters; to private researches in the day book and ledger, till, cash-box, and iron safe; to watchings at windows, listenings behind doors, and a general putting of this and that together by the wrong end.\n\nMrs. Snagsby is so perpetually on the alert that the house becomes ghostly with creaking boards and rustling garments. The ’prentices think somebody may have been murdered there in bygone times. Guster holds certain loose atoms of an idea (picked up at Tooting, where they were found floating among the orphans) that there is buried money underneath the cellar, guarded by an old man with a white beard, who cannot get out for seven thousand years because he said the Lord’s Prayer backwards.\n\n“Who was Nimrod?” Mrs. Snagsby repeatedly inquires of herself. “Who was that lady—that creature? And who is that boy?” Now, Nimrod being as dead as the mighty hunter whose name Mrs. Snagsby has appropriated, and the lady being unproducible, she directs her mental eye, for the present, with redoubled vigilance to the boy. “And who,” quoth Mrs. Snagsby for the thousand and first time, “is that boy? Who is that—!” And there Mrs. Snagsby is seized with an inspiration.\n\nHe has no respect for Mr. Chadband. No, to be sure, and he wouldn’t have, of course. Naturally he wouldn’t, under those contagious circumstances. He was invited and appointed by Mr. Chadband—why, Mrs. Snagsby heard it herself with her own ears!—to come back, and be told where he was to go, to be addressed by Mr. Chadband; and he never came! Why did he never come? Because he was told not to come. Who told him not to come? Who? Ha, ha! Mrs. Snagsby sees it all.\n\nBut happily (and Mrs. Snagsby tightly shakes her head and tightly smiles) that boy was met by Mr. Chadband yesterday in the streets; and that boy, as affording a subject which Mr. Chadband desires to improve for the spiritual delight of a select congregation, was seized by Mr. Chadband and threatened with being delivered over to the police unless he showed the reverend gentleman where he lived and unless he entered into, and fulfilled, an undertaking to appear in Cook’s Court to-morrow night, “to—mor—row—night,” Mrs. Snagsby repeats for mere emphasis with another tight smile and another tight shake of her head; and to-morrow night that boy will be here, and to-morrow night Mrs. Snagsby will have her eye upon him and upon some one else; and oh, you may walk a long while in your secret ways (says Mrs. Snagsby with haughtiness and scorn), but you can’t blind ME!\n\nMrs. Snagsby sounds no timbrel in anybody’s ears, but holds her purpose quietly, and keeps her counsel. To-morrow comes, the savoury preparations for the Oil Trade come, the evening comes. Comes Mr. Snagsby in his black coat; come the Chadbands; come (when the gorging vessel is replete) the ’prentices and Guster, to be edified; comes at last, with his slouching head, and his shuffle backward, and his shuffle forward, and his shuffle to the right, and his shuffle to the left, and his bit of fur cap in his muddy hand, which he picks as if it were some mangy bird he had caught and was plucking before eating raw, Jo, the very, very tough subject Mr. Chadband is to improve.\n\nMrs. Snagsby screws a watchful glance on Jo as he is brought into the little drawing-room by Guster. He looks at Mr. Snagsby the moment he comes in. Aha! Why does he look at Mr. Snagsby? Mr. Snagsby looks at him. Why should he do that, but that Mrs. Snagsby sees it all? Why else should that look pass between them, why else should Mr. Snagsby be confused and cough a signal cough behind his hand? It is as clear as crystal that Mr. Snagsby is that boy’s father.\n\n“Peace, my friends,” says Chadband, rising and wiping the oily exudations from his reverend visage. “Peace be with us! My friends, why with us? Because,” with his fat smile, “it cannot be against us, because it must be for us; because it is not hardening, because it is softening; because it does not make war like the hawk, but comes home unto us like the dove. Therefore, my friends, peace be with us! My human boy, come forward!”\n\nStretching forth his flabby paw, Mr. Chadband lays the same on Jo’s arm and considers where to station him. Jo, very doubtful of his reverend friend’s intentions and not at all clear but that something practical and painful is going to be done to him, mutters, “You let me alone. I never said nothink to you. You let me alone.”\n\n“No, my young friend,” says Chadband smoothly, “I will not let you alone. And why? Because I am a harvest-labourer, because I am a toiler and a moiler, because you are delivered over unto me and are become as a precious instrument in my hands. My friends, may I so employ this instrument as to use it to your advantage, to your profit, to your gain, to your welfare, to your enrichment! My young friend, sit upon this stool.”\n\nJo, apparently possessed by an impression that the reverend gentleman wants to cut his hair, shields his head with both arms and is got into the required position with great difficulty and every possible manifestation of reluctance.\n\nWhen he is at last adjusted like a lay-figure, Mr. Chadband, retiring behind the table, holds up his bear’s-paw and says, “My friends!” This is the signal for a general settlement of the audience. The ’prentices giggle internally and nudge each other. Guster falls into a staring and vacant state, compounded of a stunned admiration of Mr. Chadband and pity for the friendless outcast whose condition touches her nearly. Mrs. Snagsby silently lays trains of gunpowder. Mrs. Chadband composes herself grimly by the fire and warms her knees, finding that sensation favourable to the reception of eloquence.\n\nIt happens that Mr. Chadband has a pulpit habit of fixing some member of his congregation with his eye and fatly arguing his points with that particular person, who is understood to be expected to be moved to an occasional grunt, groan, gasp, or other audible expression of inward working, which expression of inward working, being echoed by some elderly lady in the next pew and so communicated like a game of forfeits through a circle of the more fermentable sinners present, serves the purpose of parliamentary cheering and gets Mr. Chadband’s steam up. From mere force of habit, Mr. Chadband in saying “My friends!” has rested his eye on Mr. Snagsby and proceeds to make that ill-starred stationer, already sufficiently confused, the immediate recipient of his discourse.\n\n“We have here among us, my friends,” says Chadband, “a Gentile and a heathen, a dweller in the tents of Tom-all-Alone’s and a mover-on upon the surface of the earth. We have here among us, my friends,” and Mr. Chadband, untwisting the point with his dirty thumb-nail, bestows an oily smile on Mr. Snagsby, signifying that he will throw him an argumentative back-fall presently if he be not already down, “a brother and a boy. Devoid of parents, devoid of relations, devoid of flocks and herds, devoid of gold and silver and of precious stones. Now, my friends, why do I say he is devoid of these possessions? Why? Why is he?” Mr. Chadband states the question as if he were propounding an entirely new riddle of much ingenuity and merit to Mr. Snagsby and entreating him not to give it up.\n\nMr. Snagsby, greatly perplexed by the mysterious look he received just now from his little woman—at about the period when Mr. Chadband mentioned the word parents—is tempted into modestly remarking, “I don’t know, I’m sure, sir.” On which interruption Mrs. Chadband glares and Mrs. Snagsby says, “For shame!”\n\n“I hear a voice,” says Chadband; “is it a still small voice, my friends? I fear not, though I fain would hope so—”\n\n“Ah—h!” from Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“Which says, ‘I don’t know.’ Then I will tell you why. I say this brother present here among us is devoid of parents, devoid of relations, devoid of flocks and herds, devoid of gold, of silver, and of precious stones because he is devoid of the light that shines in upon some of us. What is that light? What is it? I ask you, what is that light?”\n\nMr. Chadband draws back his head and pauses, but Mr. Snagsby is not to be lured on to his destruction again. Mr. Chadband, leaning forward over the table, pierces what he has got to follow directly into Mr. Snagsby with the thumb-nail already mentioned.\n\n“It is,” says Chadband, “the ray of rays, the sun of suns, the moon of moons, the star of stars. It is the light of Terewth.”\n\nMr. Chadband draws himself up again and looks triumphantly at Mr. Snagsby as if he would be glad to know how he feels after that.\n\n“Of Terewth,” says Mr. Chadband, hitting him again. “Say not to me that it is NOT the lamp of lamps. I say to you it is. I say to you, a million of times over, it is. It is! I say to you that I will proclaim it to you, whether you like it or not; nay, that the less you like it, the more I will proclaim it to you. With a speaking-trumpet! I say to you that if you rear yourself against it, you shall fall, you shall be bruised, you shall be battered, you shall be flawed, you shall be smashed.”\n\nThe present effect of this flight of oratory—much admired for its general power by Mr. Chadband’s followers—being not only to make Mr. Chadband unpleasantly warm, but to represent the innocent Mr. Snagsby in the light of a determined enemy to virtue, with a forehead of brass and a heart of adamant, that unfortunate tradesman becomes yet more disconcerted and is in a very advanced state of low spirits and false position when Mr. Chadband accidentally finishes him.\n\n“My friends,” he resumes after dabbing his fat head for some time—and it smokes to such an extent that he seems to light his pocket-handkerchief at it, which smokes, too, after every dab—“to pursue the subject we are endeavouring with our lowly gifts to improve, let us in a spirit of love inquire what is that Terewth to which I have alluded. For, my young friends,” suddenly addressing the ’prentices and Guster, to their consternation, “if I am told by the doctor that calomel or castor-oil is good for me, I may naturally ask what is calomel, and what is castor-oil. I may wish to be informed of that before I dose myself with either or with both. Now, my young friends, what is this Terewth then? Firstly (in a spirit of love), what is the common sort of Terewth—the working clothes—the every-day wear, my young friends? Is it deception?”\n\n“Ah—h!” from Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“Is it suppression?”\n\nA shiver in the negative from Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“Is it reservation?”\n\nA shake of the head from Mrs. Snagsby—very long and very tight.\n\n“No, my friends, it is neither of these. Neither of these names belongs to it. When this young heathen now among us—who is now, my friends, asleep, the seal of indifference and perdition being set upon his eyelids; but do not wake him, for it is right that I should have to wrestle, and to combat and to struggle, and to conquer, for his sake—when this young hardened heathen told us a story of a cock, and of a bull, and of a lady, and of a sovereign, was THAT the Terewth? No. Or if it was partly, was it wholly and entirely? No, my friends, no!”\n\nIf Mr. Snagsby could withstand his little woman’s look as it enters at his eyes, the windows of his soul, and searches the whole tenement, he were other than the man he is. He cowers and droops.\n\n“Or, my juvenile friends,” says Chadband, descending to the level of their comprehension with a very obtrusive demonstration in his greasily meek smile of coming a long way downstairs for the purpose, “if the master of this house was to go forth into the city and there see an eel, and was to come back, and was to call unto him the mistress of this house, and was to say, ‘Sarah, rejoice with me, for I have seen an elephant!’ would THAT be Terewth?”\n\nMrs. Snagsby in tears.\n\n“Or put it, my juvenile friends, that he saw an elephant, and returning said ‘Lo, the city is barren, I have seen but an eel,’ would THAT be Terewth?”\n\nMrs. Snagsby sobbing loudly.\n\n“Or put it, my juvenile friends,” said Chadband, stimulated by the sound, “that the unnatural parents of this slumbering heathen—for parents he had, my juvenile friends, beyond a doubt—after casting him forth to the wolves and the vultures, and the wild dogs and the young gazelles, and the serpents, went back to their dwellings and had their pipes, and their pots, and their flutings and their dancings, and their malt liquors, and their butcher’s meat and poultry, would THAT be Terewth?”\n\nMrs. Snagsby replies by delivering herself a prey to spasms, not an unresisting prey, but a crying and a tearing one, so that Cook’s Court re-echoes with her shrieks. Finally, becoming cataleptic, she has to be carried up the narrow staircase like a grand piano. After unspeakable suffering, productive of the utmost consternation, she is pronounced, by expresses from the bedroom, free from pain, though much exhausted, in which state of affairs Mr. Snagsby, trampled and crushed in the piano-forte removal, and extremely timid and feeble, ventures to come out from behind the door in the drawing-room.\n\nAll this time Jo has been standing on the spot where he woke up, ever picking his cap and putting bits of fur in his mouth. He spits them out with a remorseful air, for he feels that it is in his nature to be an unimprovable reprobate and that it’s no good HIS trying to keep awake, for HE won’t never know nothink. Though it may be, Jo, that there is a history so interesting and affecting even to minds as near the brutes as thine, recording deeds done on this earth for common men, that if the Chadbands, removing their own persons from the light, would but show it thee in simple reverence, would but leave it unimproved, would but regard it as being eloquent enough without their modest aid—it might hold thee awake, and thou might learn from it yet!\n\nJo never heard of any such book. Its compilers and the Reverend Chadband are all one to him, except that he knows the Reverend Chadband and would rather run away from him for an hour than hear him talk for five minutes. “It an’t no good my waiting here no longer,” thinks Jo. “Mr. Snagsby an’t a-going to say nothink to me to-night.” And downstairs he shuffles.\n\nBut downstairs is the charitable Guster, holding by the handrail of the kitchen stairs and warding off a fit, as yet doubtfully, the same having been induced by Mrs. Snagsby’s screaming. She has her own supper of bread and cheese to hand to Jo, with whom she ventures to interchange a word or so for the first time.\n\n“Here’s something to eat, poor boy,” says Guster.\n\n“Thank’ee, mum,” says Jo.\n\n“Are you hungry?”\n\n“Jist!” says Jo.\n\n“What’s gone of your father and your mother, eh?”\n\nJo stops in the middle of a bite and looks petrified. For this orphan charge of the Christian saint whose shrine was at Tooting has patted him on the shoulder, and it is the first time in his life that any decent hand has been so laid upon him.\n\n“I never know’d nothink about ’em,” says Jo.\n\n“No more didn’t I of mine,” cries Guster. She is repressing symptoms favourable to the fit when she seems to take alarm at something and vanishes down the stairs.\n\n“Jo,” whispers the law-stationer softly as the boy lingers on the step.\n\n“Here I am, Mr. Snagsby!”\n\n“I didn’t know you were gone—there’s another half-crown, Jo. It was quite right of you to say nothing about the lady the other night when we were out together. It would breed trouble. You can’t be too quiet, Jo.”\n\n“I am fly, master!”\n\nAnd so, good night.\n\nA ghostly shade, frilled and night-capped, follows the law-stationer to the room he came from and glides higher up. And henceforth he begins, go where he will, to be attended by another shadow than his own, hardly less constant than his own, hardly less quiet than his own. And into whatsoever atmosphere of secrecy his own shadow may pass, let all concerned in the secrecy beware! For the watchful Mrs. Snagsby is there too—bone of his bone, flesh of his flesh, shadow of his shadow.\n\nSharpshooters\nWintry morning, looking with dull eyes and sallow face upon the neighbourhood of Leicester Square, finds its inhabitants unwilling to get out of bed. Many of them are not early risers at the brightest of times, being birds of night who roost when the sun is high and are wide awake and keen for prey when the stars shine out. Behind dingy blind and curtain, in upper story and garret, skulking more or less under false names, false hair, false titles, false jewellery, and false histories, a colony of brigands lie in their first sleep. Gentlemen of the green-baize road who could discourse from personal experience of foreign galleys and home treadmills; spies of strong governments that eternally quake with weakness and miserable fear, broken traitors, cowards, bullies, gamesters, shufflers, swindlers, and false witnesses; some not unmarked by the branding-iron beneath their dirty braid; all with more cruelty in them than was in Nero, and more crime than is in Newgate. For howsoever bad the devil can be in fustian or smock-frock (and he can be very bad in both), he is a more designing, callous, and intolerable devil when he sticks a pin in his shirt-front, calls himself a gentleman, backs a card or colour, plays a game or so of billiards, and knows a little about bills and promissory notes, than in any other form he wears. And in such form Mr. Bucket shall find him, when he will, still pervading the tributary channels of Leicester Square.\n\nBut the wintry morning wants him not and wakes him not. It wakes Mr. George of the shooting gallery and his familiar. They arise, roll up and stow away their mattresses. Mr. George, having shaved himself before a looking-glass of minute proportions, then marches out, bare-headed and bare-chested, to the pump in the little yard and anon comes back shining with yellow soap, friction, drifting rain, and exceedingly cold water. As he rubs himself upon a large jack-towel, blowing like a military sort of diver just come up, his hair curling tighter and tighter on his sunburnt temples the more he rubs it so that it looks as if it never could be loosened by any less coercive instrument than an iron rake or a curry-comb—as he rubs, and puffs, and polishes, and blows, turning his head from side to side the more conveniently to excoriate his throat, and standing with his body well bent forward to keep the wet from his martial legs, Phil, on his knees lighting a fire, looks round as if it were enough washing for him to see all that done, and sufficient renovation for one day to take in the superfluous health his master throws off.\n\nWhen Mr. George is dry, he goes to work to brush his head with two hard brushes at once, to that unmerciful degree that Phil, shouldering his way round the gallery in the act of sweeping it, winks with sympathy. This chafing over, the ornamental part of Mr. George’s toilet is soon performed. He fills his pipe, lights it, and marches up and down smoking, as his custom is, while Phil, raising a powerful odour of hot rolls and coffee, prepares breakfast. He smokes gravely and marches in slow time. Perhaps this morning’s pipe is devoted to the memory of Gridley in his grave.\n\n“And so, Phil,” says George of the shooting gallery after several turns in silence, “you were dreaming of the country last night?”\n\nPhil, by the by, said as much in a tone of surprise as he scrambled out of bed.\n\n“Yes, guv’ner.”\n\n“What was it like?”\n\n“I hardly know what it was like, guv’ner,” said Phil, considering.\n\n“How did you know it was the country?”\n\n“On account of the grass, I think. And the swans upon it,” says Phil after further consideration.\n\n“What were the swans doing on the grass?”\n\n“They was a-eating of it, I expect,” says Phil.\n\nThe master resumes his march, and the man resumes his preparation of breakfast. It is not necessarily a lengthened preparation, being limited to the setting forth of very simple breakfast requisites for two and the broiling of a rasher of bacon at the fire in the rusty grate; but as Phil has to sidle round a considerable part of the gallery for every object he wants, and never brings two objects at once, it takes time under the circumstances. At length the breakfast is ready. Phil announcing it, Mr. George knocks the ashes out of his pipe on the hob, stands his pipe itself in the chimney corner, and sits down to the meal. When he has helped himself, Phil follows suit, sitting at the extreme end of the little oblong table and taking his plate on his knees. Either in humility, or to hide his blackened hands, or because it is his natural manner of eating.\n\n“The country,” says Mr. George, plying his knife and fork; “why, I suppose you never clapped your eyes on the country, Phil?”\n\n“I see the marshes once,” says Phil, contentedly eating his breakfast.\n\n“What marshes?”\n\n“THE marshes, commander,” returns Phil.\n\n“Where are they?”\n\n“I don’t know where they are,” says Phil; “but I see ’em, guv’ner. They was flat. And miste.”\n\nGovernor and commander are interchangeable terms with Phil, expressive of the same respect and deference and applicable to nobody but Mr. George.\n\n“I was born in the country, Phil.”\n\n“Was you indeed, commander?”\n\n“Yes. And bred there.”\n\nPhil elevates his one eyebrow, and after respectfully staring at his master to express interest, swallows a great gulp of coffee, still staring at him.\n\n“There’s not a bird’s note that I don’t know,” says Mr. George. “Not many an English leaf or berry that I couldn’t name. Not many a tree that I couldn’t climb yet if I was put to it. I was a real country boy, once. My good mother lived in the country.”\n\n“She must have been a fine old lady, guv’ner,” Phil observes.\n\n“Aye! And not so old either, five and thirty years ago,” says Mr. George. “But I’ll wager that at ninety she would be near as upright as me, and near as broad across the shoulders.”\n\n“Did she die at ninety, guv’ner?” inquires Phil.\n\n“No. Bosh! Let her rest in peace, God bless her!” says the trooper. “What set me on about country boys, and runaways, and good-for-nothings? You, to be sure! So you never clapped your eyes upon the country—marshes and dreams excepted. Eh?”\n\nPhil shakes his head.\n\n“Do you want to see it?”\n\n“N-no, I don’t know as I do, particular,” says Phil.\n\n“The town’s enough for you, eh?”\n\n“Why, you see, commander,” says Phil, “I ain’t acquainted with anythink else, and I doubt if I ain’t a-getting too old to take to novelties.”\n\n“How old ARE you, Phil?” asks the trooper, pausing as he conveys his smoking saucer to his lips.\n\n“I’m something with a eight in it,” says Phil. “It can’t be eighty. Nor yet eighteen. It’s betwixt ’em, somewheres.”\n\nMr. George, slowly putting down his saucer without tasting its contents, is laughingly beginning, “Why, what the deuce, Phil—” when he stops, seeing that Phil is counting on his dirty fingers.\n\n“I was just eight,” says Phil, “agreeable to the parish calculation, when I went with the tinker. I was sent on a errand, and I see him a-sittin under a old buildin with a fire all to himself wery comfortable, and he says, ‘Would you like to come along a me, my man?’ I says ‘Yes,’ and him and me and the fire goes home to Clerkenwell together. That was April Fool Day. I was able to count up to ten; and when April Fool Day come round again, I says to myself, ‘Now, old chap, you’re one and a eight in it.’ April Fool Day after that, I says, ‘Now, old chap, you’re two and a eight in it.’ In course of time, I come to ten and a eight in it; two tens and a eight in it. When it got so high, it got the upper hand of me, but this is how I always know there’s a eight in it.”\n\n“Ah!” says Mr. George, resuming his breakfast. “And where’s the tinker?”\n\n“Drink put him in the hospital, guv’ner, and the hospital put him—in a glass-case, I HAVE heerd,” Phil replies mysteriously.\n\n“By that means you got promotion? Took the business, Phil?”\n\n“Yes, commander, I took the business. Such as it was. It wasn’t much of a beat—round Saffron Hill, Hatton Garden, Clerkenwell, Smiffeld, and there—poor neighbourhood, where they uses up the kettles till they’re past mending. Most of the tramping tinkers used to come and lodge at our place; that was the best part of my master’s earnings. But they didn’t come to me. I warn’t like him. He could sing ’em a good song. I couldn’t! He could play ’em a tune on any sort of pot you please, so as it was iron or block tin. I never could do nothing with a pot but mend it or bile it—never had a note of music in me. Besides, I was too ill-looking, and their wives complained of me.”\n\n“They were mighty particular. You would pass muster in a crowd, Phil!” says the trooper with a pleasant smile.\n\n“No, guv’ner,” returns Phil, shaking his head. “No, I shouldn’t. I was passable enough when I went with the tinker, though nothing to boast of then; but what with blowing the fire with my mouth when I was young, and spileing my complexion, and singeing my hair off, and swallering the smoke, and what with being nat’rally unfort’nate in the way of running against hot metal and marking myself by sich means, and what with having turn-ups with the tinker as I got older, almost whenever he was too far gone in drink—which was almost always—my beauty was queer, wery queer, even at that time. As to since, what with a dozen years in a dark forge where the men was given to larking, and what with being scorched in a accident at a gas-works, and what with being blowed out of winder case-filling at the firework business, I am ugly enough to be made a show on!”\n\nResigning himself to which condition with a perfectly satisfied manner, Phil begs the favour of another cup of coffee. While drinking it, he says, “It was after the case-filling blow-up when I first see you, commander. You remember?”\n\n“I remember, Phil. You were walking along in the sun.”\n\n“Crawling, guv’ner, again a wall—”\n\n“True, Phil—shouldering your way on—”\n\n“In a night-cap!” exclaims Phil, excited.\n\n“In a night-cap—”\n\n“And hobbling with a couple of sticks!” cries Phil, still more excited.\n\n“With a couple of sticks. When—”\n\n“When you stops, you know,” cries Phil, putting down his cup and saucer and hastily removing his plate from his knees, “and says to me, ‘What, comrade! You have been in the wars!’ I didn’t say much to you, commander, then, for I was took by surprise that a person so strong and healthy and bold as you was should stop to speak to such a limping bag of bones as I was. But you says to me, says you, delivering it out of your chest as hearty as possible, so that it was like a glass of something hot, ‘What accident have you met with? You have been badly hurt. What’s amiss, old boy? Cheer up, and tell us about it!’ Cheer up! I was cheered already! I says as much to you, you says more to me, I says more to you, you says more to me, and here I am, commander! Here I am, commander!” cries Phil, who has started from his chair and unaccountably begun to sidle away. “If a mark’s wanted, or if it will improve the business, let the customers take aim at me. They can’t spoil MY beauty. I’M all right. Come on! If they want a man to box at, let ’em box at me. Let ’em knock me well about the head. I don’t mind. If they want a light-weight to be throwed for practice, Cornwall, Devonshire, or Lancashire, let ’em throw me. They won’t hurt ME. I have been throwed, all sorts of styles, all my life!”\n\nWith this unexpected speech, energetically delivered and accompanied by action illustrative of the various exercises referred to, Phil Squod shoulders his way round three sides of the gallery, and abruptly tacking off at his commander, makes a butt at him with his head, intended to express devotion to his service. He then begins to clear away the breakfast.\n\nMr. George, after laughing cheerfully and clapping him on the shoulder, assists in these arrangements and helps to get the gallery into business order. That done, he takes a turn at the dumb-bells, and afterwards weighing himself and opining that he is getting “too fleshy,” engages with great gravity in solitary broadsword practice. Meanwhile Phil has fallen to work at his usual table, where he screws and unscrews, and cleans, and files, and whistles into small apertures, and blackens himself more and more, and seems to do and undo everything that can be done and undone about a gun.\n\nMaster and man are at length disturbed by footsteps in the passage, where they make an unusual sound, denoting the arrival of unusual company. These steps, advancing nearer and nearer to the gallery, bring into it a group at first sight scarcely reconcilable with any day in the year but the fifth of November.\n\nIt consists of a limp and ugly figure carried in a chair by two bearers and attended by a lean female with a face like a pinched mask, who might be expected immediately to recite the popular verses commemorative of the time when they did contrive to blow Old England up alive but for her keeping her lips tightly and defiantly closed as the chair is put down. At which point the figure in it gasping, “O Lord! Oh, dear me! I am shaken!” adds, “How de do, my dear friend, how de do?” Mr. George then descries, in the procession, the venerable Mr. Smallweed out for an airing, attended by his granddaughter Judy as body-guard.\n\n“Mr. George, my dear friend,” says Grandfather Smallweed, removing his right arm from the neck of one of his bearers, whom he has nearly throttled coming along, “how de do? You’re surprised to see me, my dear friend.”\n\n“I should hardly have been more surprised to have seen your friend in the city,” returns Mr. George.\n\n“I am very seldom out,” pants Mr. Smallweed. “I haven’t been out for many months. It’s inconvenient—and it comes expensive. But I longed so much to see you, my dear Mr. George. How de do, sir?”\n\n“I am well enough,” says Mr. George. “I hope you are the same.”\n\n“You can’t be too well, my dear friend.” Mr. Smallweed takes him by both hands. “I have brought my granddaughter Judy. I couldn’t keep her away. She longed so much to see you.”\n\n“Hum! She bears it calmly!” mutters Mr. George.\n\n“So we got a hackney-cab, and put a chair in it, and just round the corner they lifted me out of the cab and into the chair, and carried me here that I might see my dear friend in his own establishment! This,” says Grandfather Smallweed, alluding to the bearer, who has been in danger of strangulation and who withdraws adjusting his windpipe, “is the driver of the cab. He has nothing extra. It is by agreement included in his fare. This person,” the other bearer, “we engaged in the street outside for a pint of beer. Which is twopence. Judy, give the person twopence. I was not sure you had a workman of your own here, my dear friend, or we needn’t have employed this person.”\n\nGrandfather Smallweed refers to Phil with a glance of considerable terror and a half-subdued “O Lord! Oh, dear me!” Nor in his apprehension, on the surface of things, without some reason, for Phil, who has never beheld the apparition in the black-velvet cap before, has stopped short with a gun in his hand with much of the air of a dead shot intent on picking Mr. Smallweed off as an ugly old bird of the crow species.\n\n“Judy, my child,” says Grandfather Smallweed, “give the person his twopence. It’s a great deal for what he has done.”\n\nThe person, who is one of those extraordinary specimens of human fungus that spring up spontaneously in the western streets of London, ready dressed in an old red jacket, with a “mission” for holding horses and calling coaches, received his twopence with anything but transport, tosses the money into the air, catches it over-handed, and retires.\n\n“My dear Mr. George,” says Grandfather Smallweed, “would you be so kind as help to carry me to the fire? I am accustomed to a fire, and I am an old man, and I soon chill. Oh, dear me!”\n\nHis closing exclamation is jerked out of the venerable gentleman by the suddenness with which Mr. Squod, like a genie, catches him up, chair and all, and deposits him on the hearth-stone.\n\n“O Lord!” says Mr. Smallweed, panting. “Oh, dear me! Oh, my stars! My dear friend, your workman is very strong—and very prompt. O Lord, he is very prompt! Judy, draw me back a little. I’m being scorched in the legs,” which indeed is testified to the noses of all present by the smell of his worsted stockings.\n\nThe gentle Judy, having backed her grandfather a little way from the fire, and having shaken him up as usual, and having released his overshadowed eye from its black-velvet extinguisher, Mr. Smallweed again says, “Oh, dear me! O Lord!” and looking about and meeting Mr. George’s glance, again stretches out both hands.\n\n“My dear friend! So happy in this meeting! And this is your establishment? It’s a delightful place. It’s a picture! You never find that anything goes off here accidentally, do you, my dear friend?” adds Grandfather Smallweed, very ill at ease.\n\n“No, no. No fear of that.”\n\n“And your workman. He—Oh, dear me!—he never lets anything off without meaning it, does he, my dear friend?”\n\n“He has never hurt anybody but himself,” says Mr. George, smiling.\n\n“But he might, you know. He seems to have hurt himself a good deal, and he might hurt somebody else,” the old gentleman returns. “He mightn’t mean it—or he even might. Mr. George, will you order him to leave his infernal fire-arms alone and go away?”\n\nObedient to a nod from the trooper, Phil retires, empty-handed, to the other end of the gallery. Mr. Smallweed, reassured, falls to rubbing his legs.\n\n“And you’re doing well, Mr. George?” he says to the trooper, squarely standing faced about towards him with his broadsword in his hand. “You are prospering, please the Powers?”\n\nMr. George answers with a cool nod, adding, “Go on. You have not come to say that, I know.”\n\n“You are so sprightly, Mr. George,” returns the venerable grandfather. “You are such good company.”\n\n“Ha ha! Go on!” says Mr. George.\n\n“My dear friend! But that sword looks awful gleaming and sharp. It might cut somebody, by accident. It makes me shiver, Mr. George. Curse him!” says the excellent old gentleman apart to Judy as the trooper takes a step or two away to lay it aside. “He owes me money, and might think of paying off old scores in this murdering place. I wish your brimstone grandmother was here, and he’d shave her head off.”\n\nMr. George, returning, folds his arms, and looking down at the old man, sliding every moment lower and lower in his chair, says quietly, “Now for it!”\n\n“Ho!” cries Mr. Smallweed, rubbing his hands with an artful chuckle. “Yes. Now for it. Now for what, my dear friend?”\n\n“For a pipe,” says Mr. George, who with great composure sets his chair in the chimney-corner, takes his pipe from the grate, fills it and lights it, and falls to smoking peacefully.\n\nThis tends to the discomfiture of Mr. Smallweed, who finds it so difficult to resume his object, whatever it may be, that he becomes exasperated and secretly claws the air with an impotent vindictiveness expressive of an intense desire to tear and rend the visage of Mr. George. As the excellent old gentleman’s nails are long and leaden, and his hands lean and veinous, and his eyes green and watery; and, over and above this, as he continues, while he claws, to slide down in his chair and to collapse into a shapeless bundle, he becomes such a ghastly spectacle, even in the accustomed eyes of Judy, that that young virgin pounces at him with something more than the ardour of affection and so shakes him up and pats and pokes him in divers parts of his body, but particularly in that part which the science of self-defence would call his wind, that in his grievous distress he utters enforced sounds like a paviour’s rammer.\n\nWhen Judy has by these means set him up again in his chair, with a white face and a frosty nose (but still clawing), she stretches out her weazen forefinger and gives Mr. George one poke in the back. The trooper raising his head, she makes another poke at her esteemed grandfather, and having thus brought them together, stares rigidly at the fire.\n\n“Aye, aye! Ho, ho! U—u—u—ugh!” chatters Grandfather Smallweed, swallowing his rage. “My dear friend!” (still clawing).\n\n“I tell you what,” says Mr. George. “If you want to converse with me, you must speak out. I am one of the roughs, and I can’t go about and about. I haven’t the art to do it. I am not clever enough. It don’t suit me. When you go winding round and round me,” says the trooper, putting his pipe between his lips again, “damme, if I don’t feel as if I was being smothered!”\n\nAnd he inflates his broad chest to its utmost extent as if to assure himself that he is not smothered yet.\n\n“If you have come to give me a friendly call,” continues Mr. George, “I am obliged to you; how are you? If you have come to see whether there’s any property on the premises, look about you; you are welcome. If you want to out with something, out with it!”\n\nThe blooming Judy, without removing her gaze from the fire, gives her grandfather one ghostly poke.\n\n“You see! It’s her opinion too. And why the devil that young woman won’t sit down like a Christian,” says Mr. George with his eyes musingly fixed on Judy, “I can’t comprehend.”\n\n“She keeps at my side to attend to me, sir,” says Grandfather Smallweed. “I am an old man, my dear Mr. George, and I need some attention. I can carry my years; I am not a brimstone poll-parrot” (snarling and looking unconsciously for the cushion), “but I need attention, my dear friend.”\n\n“Well!” returns the trooper, wheeling his chair to face the old man. “Now then?”\n\n“My friend in the city, Mr. George, has done a little business with a pupil of yours.”\n\n“Has he?” says Mr. George. “I am sorry to hear it.”\n\n“Yes, sir.” Grandfather Smallweed rubs his legs. “He is a fine young soldier now, Mr. George, by the name of Carstone. Friends came forward and paid it all up, honourable.”\n\n“Did they?” returns Mr. George. “Do you think your friend in the city would like a piece of advice?”\n\n“I think he would, my dear friend. From you.”\n\n“I advise him, then, to do no more business in that quarter. There’s no more to be got by it. The young gentleman, to my knowledge, is brought to a dead halt.”\n\n“No, no, my dear friend. No, no, Mr. George. No, no, no, sir,” remonstrates Grandfather Smallweed, cunningly rubbing his spare legs. “Not quite a dead halt, I think. He has good friends, and he is good for his pay, and he is good for the selling price of his commission, and he is good for his chance in a lawsuit, and he is good for his chance in a wife, and—oh, do you know, Mr. George, I think my friend would consider the young gentleman good for something yet?” says Grandfather Smallweed, turning up his velvet cap and scratching his ear like a monkey.\n\nMr. George, who has put aside his pipe and sits with an arm on his chair-back, beats a tattoo on the ground with his right foot as if he were not particularly pleased with the turn the conversation has taken.\n\n“But to pass from one subject to another,” resumes Mr. Smallweed. “‘To promote the conversation,’ as a joker might say. To pass, Mr. George, from the ensign to the captain.”\n\n“What are you up to, now?” asks Mr. George, pausing with a frown in stroking the recollection of his moustache. “What captain?”\n\n“Our captain. The captain we know of. Captain Hawdon.”\n\n“Oh! That’s it, is it?” says Mr. George with a low whistle as he sees both grandfather and granddaughter looking hard at him. “You are there! Well? What about it? Come, I won’t be smothered any more. Speak!”\n\n“My dear friend,” returns the old man, “I was applied—Judy, shake me up a little!—I was applied to yesterday about the captain, and my opinion still is that the captain is not dead.”\n\n“Bosh!” observes Mr. George.\n\n“What was your remark, my dear friend?” inquires the old man with his hand to his ear.\n\n“Bosh!”\n“Ho!” says Grandfather Smallweed. “Mr. George, of my opinion you can judge for yourself according to the questions asked of me and the reasons given for asking ’em. Now, what do you think the lawyer making the inquiries wants?”\n\n“A job,” says Mr. George.\n\n“Nothing of the kind!”\n\n“Can’t be a lawyer, then,” says Mr. George, folding his arms with an air of confirmed resolution.\n\n“My dear friend, he is a lawyer, and a famous one. He wants to see some fragment in Captain Hawdon’s writing. He don’t want to keep it. He only wants to see it and compare it with a writing in his possession.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well, Mr. George. Happening to remember the advertisement concerning Captain Hawdon and any information that could be given respecting him, he looked it up and came to me—just as you did, my dear friend. WILL you shake hands? So glad you came that day! I should have missed forming such a friendship if you hadn’t come!”\n\n“Well, Mr. Smallweed?” says Mr. George again after going through the ceremony with some stiffness.\n\n“I had no such thing. I have nothing but his signature. Plague pestilence and famine, battle murder and sudden death upon him,” says the old man, making a curse out of one of his few remembrances of a prayer and squeezing up his velvet cap between his angry hands, “I have half a million of his signatures, I think! But you,” breathlessly recovering his mildness of speech as Judy re-adjusts the cap on his skittle-ball of a head, “you, my dear Mr. George, are likely to have some letter or paper that would suit the purpose. Anything would suit the purpose, written in the hand.”\n\n“Some writing in that hand,” says the trooper, pondering; “may be, I have.”\n\n“My dearest friend!”\n\n“May be, I have not.”\n\n“Ho!” says Grandfather Smallweed, crest-fallen.\n\n“But if I had bushels of it, I would not show as much as would make a cartridge without knowing why.”\n\n“Sir, I have told you why. My dear Mr. George, I have told you why.”\n\n“Not enough,” says the trooper, shaking his head. “I must know more, and approve it.”\n\n“Then, will you come to the lawyer? My dear friend, will you come and see the gentleman?” urges Grandfather Smallweed, pulling out a lean old silver watch with hands like the leg of a skeleton. “I told him it was probable I might call upon him between ten and eleven this forenoon, and it’s now half after ten. Will you come and see the gentleman, Mr. George?”\n\n“Hum!” says he gravely. “I don’t mind that. Though why this should concern you so much, I don’t know.”\n\n“Everything concerns me that has a chance in it of bringing anything to light about him. Didn’t he take us all in? Didn’t he owe us immense sums, all round? Concern me? Who can anything about him concern more than me? Not, my dear friend,” says Grandfather Smallweed, lowering his tone, “that I want YOU to betray anything. Far from it. Are you ready to come, my dear friend?”\n\n“Aye! I’ll come in a moment. I promise nothing, you know.”\n\n“No, my dear Mr. George; no.”\n\n“And you mean to say you’re going to give me a lift to this place, wherever it is, without charging for it?” Mr. George inquires, getting his hat and thick wash-leather gloves.\n\nThis pleasantry so tickles Mr. Smallweed that he laughs, long and low, before the fire. But ever while he laughs, he glances over his paralytic shoulder at Mr. George and eagerly watches him as he unlocks the padlock of a homely cupboard at the distant end of the gallery, looks here and there upon the higher shelves, and ultimately takes something out with a rustling of paper, folds it, and puts it in his breast. Then Judy pokes Mr. Smallweed once, and Mr. Smallweed pokes Judy once.\n\n“I am ready,” says the trooper, coming back. “Phil, you can carry this old gentleman to his coach, and make nothing of him.”\n\n“Oh, dear me! O Lord! Stop a moment!” says Mr. Smallweed. “He’s so very prompt! Are you sure you can do it carefully, my worthy man?”\n\nPhil makes no reply, but seizing the chair and its load, sidles away, tightly hugged by the now speechless Mr. Smallweed, and bolts along the passage as if he had an acceptable commission to carry the old gentleman to the nearest volcano. His shorter trust, however, terminating at the cab, he deposits him there; and the fair Judy takes her place beside him, and the chair embellishes the roof, and Mr. George takes the vacant place upon the box.\n\nMr. George is quite confounded by the spectacle he beholds from time to time as he peeps into the cab through the window behind him, where the grim Judy is always motionless, and the old gentleman with his cap over one eye is always sliding off the seat into the straw and looking upward at him out of his other eye with a helpless expression of being jolted in the back.\nMore Old Soldiers Than One\n\nMr. George has not far to ride with folded arms upon the box, for their destination is Lincoln’s Inn Fields. When the driver stops his horses, Mr. George alights, and looking in at the window, says, “What, Mr. Tulkinghorn’s your man, is he?”\n\n“Yes, my dear friend. Do you know him, Mr. George?”\n\n“Why, I have heard of him—seen him too, I think. But I don’t know him, and he don’t know me.”\n\nThere ensues the carrying of Mr. Smallweed upstairs, which is done to perfection with the trooper’s help. He is borne into Mr. Tulkinghorn’s great room and deposited on the Turkey rug before the fire. Mr. Tulkinghorn is not within at the present moment but will be back directly. The occupant of the pew in the hall, having said thus much, stirs the fire and leaves the triumvirate to warm themselves.\n\nMr. George is mightily curious in respect of the room. He looks up at the painted ceiling, looks round at the old law-books, contemplates the portraits of the great clients, reads aloud the names on the boxes.\n\n“‘Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,’” Mr. George reads thoughtfully. “Ha! ‘Manor of Chesney Wold.’ Humph!” Mr. George stands looking at these boxes a long while—as if they were pictures—and comes back to the fire repeating, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and Manor of Chesney Wold, hey?”\n\n“Worth a mint of money, Mr. George!” whispers Grandfather Smallweed, rubbing his legs. “Powerfully rich!”\n\n“Who do you mean? This old gentleman, or the Baronet?”\n\n“This gentleman, this gentleman.”\n\n“So I have heard; and knows a thing or two, I’ll hold a wager. Not bad quarters, either,” says Mr. George, looking round again. “See the strong-box yonder!”\n\nThis reply is cut short by Mr. Tulkinghorn’s arrival. There is no change in him, of course. Rustily drest, with his spectacles in his hand, and their very case worn threadbare. In manner, close and dry. In voice, husky and low. In face, watchful behind a blind; habitually not uncensorious and contemptuous perhaps. The peerage may have warmer worshippers and faithfuller believers than Mr. Tulkinghorn, after all, if everything were known.\n\n“Good morning, Mr. Smallweed, good morning!” he says as he comes in. “You have brought the sergeant, I see. Sit down, sergeant.”\n\nAs Mr. Tulkinghorn takes off his gloves and puts them in his hat, he looks with half-closed eyes across the room to where the trooper stands and says within himself perchance, “You’ll do, my friend!”\n\n“Sit down, sergeant,” he repeats as he comes to his table, which is set on one side of the fire, and takes his easy-chair. “Cold and raw this morning, cold and raw!” Mr. Tulkinghorn warms before the bars, alternately, the palms and knuckles of his hands and looks (from behind that blind which is always down) at the trio sitting in a little semicircle before him.\n\n“Now, I can feel what I am about” (as perhaps he can in two senses), “Mr. Smallweed.” The old gentleman is newly shaken up by Judy to bear his part in the conversation. “You have brought our good friend the sergeant, I see.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” returns Mr. Smallweed, very servile to the lawyer’s wealth and influence.\n\n“And what does the sergeant say about this business?”\n\n“Mr. George,” says Grandfather Smallweed with a tremulous wave of his shrivelled hand, “this is the gentleman, sir.”\n\nMr. George salutes the gentleman but otherwise sits bolt upright and profoundly silent—very forward in his chair, as if the full complement of regulation appendages for a field-day hung about him.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn proceeds, “Well, George—I believe your name is George?”\n\n“It is so, Sir.”\n\n“What do you say, George?”\n\n“I ask your pardon, sir,” returns the trooper, “but I should wish to know what YOU say?”\n\n“Do you mean in point of reward?”\n\n“I mean in point of everything, sir.”\n\nThis is so very trying to Mr. Smallweed’s temper that he suddenly breaks out with “You’re a brimstone beast!” and as suddenly asks pardon of Mr. Tulkinghorn, excusing himself for this slip of the tongue by saying to Judy, “I was thinking of your grandmother, my dear.”\n\n“I supposed, sergeant,” Mr. Tulkinghorn resumes as he leans on one side of his chair and crosses his legs, “that Mr. Smallweed might have sufficiently explained the matter. It lies in the smallest compass, however. You served under Captain Hawdon at one time, and were his attendant in illness, and rendered him many little services, and were rather in his confidence, I am told. That is so, is it not?”\n\n“Yes, sir, that is so,” says Mr. George with military brevity.\n\n“Therefore you may happen to have in your possession something—anything, no matter what; accounts, instructions, orders, a letter, anything—in Captain Hawdon’s writing. I wish to compare his writing with some that I have. If you can give me the opportunity, you shall be rewarded for your trouble. Three, four, five, guineas, you would consider handsome, I dare say.”\n\n“Noble, my dear friend!” cries Grandfather Smallweed, screwing up his eyes.\n\n“If not, say how much more, in your conscience as a soldier, you can demand. There is no need for you to part with the writing, against your inclination—though I should prefer to have it.”\n\nMr. George sits squared in exactly the same attitude, looks at the painted ceiling, and says never a word. The irascible Mr. Smallweed scratches the air.\n\n“The question is,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn in his methodical, subdued, uninterested way, “first, whether you have any of Captain Hawdon’s writing?”\n\n“First, whether I have any of Captain Hawdon’s writing, sir,” repeats Mr. George.\n\n“Secondly, what will satisfy you for the trouble of producing it?”\n\n“Secondly, what will satisfy me for the trouble of producing it, sir,” repeats Mr. George.\n\n“Thirdly, you can judge for yourself whether it is at all like that,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, suddenly handing him some sheets of written paper tied together.\n\n“Whether it is at all like that, sir. Just so,” repeats Mr. George.\n\nAll three repetitions Mr. George pronounces in a mechanical manner, looking straight at Mr. Tulkinghorn; nor does he so much as glance at the affidavit in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, that has been given to him for his inspection (though he still holds it in his hand), but continues to look at the lawyer with an air of troubled meditation.\n\n“Well?” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “What do you say?”\n\n“Well, sir,” replies Mr. George, rising erect and looking immense, “I would rather, if you’ll excuse me, have nothing to do with this.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn, outwardly quite undisturbed, demands, “Why not?”\n\n“Why, sir,” returns the trooper. “Except on military compulsion, I am not a man of business. Among civilians I am what they call in Scotland a ne’er-do-weel. I have no head for papers, sir. I can stand any fire better than a fire of cross questions. I mentioned to Mr. Smallweed, only an hour or so ago, that when I come into things of this kind I feel as if I was being smothered. And that is my sensation,” says Mr. George, looking round upon the company, “at the present moment.”\n\nWith that, he takes three strides forward to replace the papers on the lawyer’s table and three strides backward to resume his former station, where he stands perfectly upright, now looking at the ground and now at the painted ceiling, with his hands behind him as if to prevent himself from accepting any other document whatever.\n\nUnder this provocation, Mr. Smallweed’s favourite adjective of disparagement is so close to his tongue that he begins the words “my dear friend” with the monosyllable “brim,” thus converting the possessive pronoun into brimmy and appearing to have an impediment in his speech. Once past this difficulty, however, he exhorts his dear friend in the tenderest manner not to be rash, but to do what so eminent a gentleman requires, and to do it with a good grace, confident that it must be unobjectionable as well as profitable. Mr. Tulkinghorn merely utters an occasional sentence, as, “You are the best judge of your own interest, sergeant.” “Take care you do no harm by this.” “Please yourself, please yourself.” “If you know what you mean, that’s quite enough.” These he utters with an appearance of perfect indifference as he looks over the papers on his table and prepares to write a letter.\n\nMr. George looks distrustfully from the painted ceiling to the ground, from the ground to Mr. Smallweed, from Mr. Smallweed to Mr. Tulkinghorn, and from Mr. Tulkinghorn to the painted ceiling again, often in his perplexity changing the leg on which he rests.\n\n“I do assure you, sir,” says Mr. George, “not to say it offensively, that between you and Mr. Smallweed here, I really am being smothered fifty times over. I really am, sir. I am not a match for you gentlemen. Will you allow me to ask why you want to see the captain’s hand, in the case that I could find any specimen of it?”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn quietly shakes his head. “No. If you were a man of business, sergeant, you would not need to be informed that there are confidential reasons, very harmless in themselves, for many such wants in the profession to which I belong. But if you are afraid of doing any injury to Captain Hawdon, you may set your mind at rest about that.”\n\n“Aye! He is dead, sir.”\n\n“IS he?” Mr. Tulkinghorn quietly sits down to write.\n\n“Well, sir,” says the trooper, looking into his hat after another disconcerted pause, “I am sorry not to have given you more satisfaction. If it would be any satisfaction to any one that I should be confirmed in my judgment that I would rather have nothing to do with this by a friend of mine who has a better head for business than I have, and who is an old soldier, I am willing to consult with him. I—I really am so completely smothered myself at present,” says Mr. George, passing his hand hopelessly across his brow, “that I don’t know but what it might be a satisfaction to me.”\n\nMr. Smallweed, hearing that this authority is an old soldier, so strongly inculcates the expediency of the trooper’s taking counsel with him, and particularly informing him of its being a question of five guineas or more, that Mr. George engages to go and see him. Mr. Tulkinghorn says nothing either way.\n\n“I’ll consult my friend, then, by your leave, sir,” says the trooper, “and I’ll take the liberty of looking in again with the final answer in the course of the day. Mr. Smallweed, if you wish to be carried downstairs—”\n\n“In a moment, my dear friend, in a moment. Will you first let me speak half a word with this gentleman in private?”\n\n“Certainly, sir. Don’t hurry yourself on my account.” The trooper retires to a distant part of the room and resumes his curious inspection of the boxes, strong and otherwise.\n\n“If I wasn’t as weak as a brimstone baby, sir,” whispers Grandfather Smallweed, drawing the lawyer down to his level by the lapel of his coat and flashing some half-quenched green fire out of his angry eyes, “I’d tear the writing away from him. He’s got it buttoned in his breast. I saw him put it there. Judy saw him put it there. Speak up, you crabbed image for the sign of a walking-stick shop, and say you saw him put it there!”\nThis vehement conjuration the old gentleman accompanies with such a thrust at his granddaughter that it is too much for his strength, and he slips away out of his chair, drawing Mr. Tulkinghorn with him, until he is arrested by Judy, and well shaken.\n\n“Violence will not do for me, my friend,” Mr. Tulkinghorn then remarks coolly.\n\n“No, no, I know, I know, sir. But it’s chafing and galling—it’s—it’s worse than your smattering chattering magpie of a grandmother,” to the imperturbable Judy, who only looks at the fire, “to know he has got what’s wanted and won’t give it up. He, not to give it up! HE! A vagabond! But never mind, sir, never mind. At the most, he has only his own way for a little while. I have him periodically in a vice. I’ll twist him, sir. I’ll screw him, sir. If he won’t do it with a good grace, I’ll make him do it with a bad one, sir! Now, my dear Mr. George,” says Grandfather Smallweed, winking at the lawyer hideously as he releases him, “I am ready for your kind assistance, my excellent friend!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn, with some shadowy sign of amusement manifesting itself through his self-possession, stands on the hearth-rug with his back to the fire, watching the disappearance of Mr. Smallweed and acknowledging the trooper’s parting salute with one slight nod.\n\nIt is more difficult to get rid of the old gentleman, Mr. George finds, than to bear a hand in carrying him downstairs, for when he is replaced in his conveyance, he is so loquacious on the subject of the guineas and retains such an affectionate hold of his button—having, in truth, a secret longing to rip his coat open and rob him—that some degree of force is necessary on the trooper’s part to effect a separation. It is accomplished at last, and he proceeds alone in quest of his adviser.\n\nBy the cloisterly Temple, and by Whitefriars (there, not without a glance at Hanging-Sword Alley, which would seem to be something in his way), and by Blackfriars Bridge, and Blackfriars Road, Mr. George sedately marches to a street of little shops lying somewhere in that ganglion of roads from Kent and Surrey, and of streets from the bridges of London, centring in the far-famed elephant who has lost his castle formed of a thousand four-horse coaches to a stronger iron monster than he, ready to chop him into mince-meat any day he dares. To one of the little shops in this street, which is a musician’s shop, having a few fiddles in the window, and some Pan’s pipes and a tambourine, and a triangle, and certain elongated scraps of music, Mr. George directs his massive tread. And halting at a few paces from it, as he sees a soldierly looking woman, with her outer skirts tucked up, come forth with a small wooden tub, and in that tub commence a-whisking and a-splashing on the margin of the pavement, Mr. George says to himself, “She’s as usual, washing greens. I never saw her, except upon a baggage-waggon, when she wasn’t washing greens!”\n\nThe subject of this reflection is at all events so occupied in washing greens at present that she remains unsuspicious of Mr. George’s approach until, lifting up herself and her tub together when she has poured the water off into the gutter, she finds him standing near her. Her reception of him is not flattering.\n\n“George, I never see you but I wish you was a hundred mile away!”\n\nThe trooper, without remarking on this welcome, follows into the musical-instrument shop, where the lady places her tub of greens upon the counter, and having shaken hands with him, rests her arms upon it.\n\n“I never,” she says, “George, consider Matthew Bagnet safe a minute when you’re near him. You are that restless and that roving—”\n\n“Yes! I know I am, Mrs. Bagnet. I know I am.”\n\n“You know you are!” says Mrs. Bagnet. “What’s the use of that? WHY are you?”\n\n“The nature of the animal, I suppose,” returns the trooper good-humouredly.\n\n“Ah!” cries Mrs. Bagnet, something shrilly. “But what satisfaction will the nature of the animal be to me when the animal shall have tempted my Mat away from the musical business to New Zealand or Australey?”\n\nMrs. Bagnet is not at all an ill-looking woman. Rather large-boned, a little coarse in the grain, and freckled by the sun and wind which have tanned her hair upon the forehead, but healthy, wholesome, and bright-eyed. A strong, busy, active, honest-faced woman of from forty-five to fifty. Clean, hardy, and so economically dressed (though substantially) that the only article of ornament of which she stands possessed appear’s to be her wedding-ring, around which her finger has grown to be so large since it was put on that it will never come off again until it shall mingle with Mrs. Bagnet’s dust.\n\n“Mrs. Bagnet,” says the trooper, “I am on my parole with you. Mat will get no harm from me. You may trust me so far.”\n\n“Well, I think I may. But the very looks of you are unsettling,” Mrs. Bagnet rejoins. “Ah, George, George! If you had only settled down and married Joe Pouch’s widow when he died in North America, SHE’D have combed your hair for you.”\n\n“It was a chance for me, certainly,” returns the trooper half laughingly, half seriously, “but I shall never settle down into a respectable man now. Joe Pouch’s widow might have done me good—there was something in her, and something of her—but I couldn’t make up my mind to it. If I had had the luck to meet with such a wife as Mat found!”\n\nMrs. Bagnet, who seems in a virtuous way to be under little reserve with a good sort of fellow, but to be another good sort of fellow herself for that matter, receives this compliment by flicking Mr. George in the face with a head of greens and taking her tub into the little room behind the shop.\n\n“Why, Quebec, my poppet,” says George, following, on invitation, into that department. “And little Malta, too! Come and kiss your Bluffy!”\n\nThese young ladies—not supposed to have been actually christened by the names applied to them, though always so called in the family from the places of their birth in barracks—are respectively employed on three-legged stools, the younger (some five or six years old) in learning her letters out of a penny primer, the elder (eight or nine perhaps) in teaching her and sewing with great assiduity. Both hail Mr. George with acclamations as an old friend and after some kissing and romping plant their stools beside him.\n\n“And how’s young Woolwich?” says Mr. George.\n\n“Ah! There now!” cries Mrs. Bagnet, turning about from her saucepans (for she is cooking dinner) with a bright flush on her face. “Would you believe it? Got an engagement at the theayter, with his father, to play the fife in a military piece.”\n\n“Well done, my godson!” cries Mr. George, slapping his thigh.\n\n“I believe you!” says Mrs. Bagnet. “He’s a Briton. That’s what Woolwich is. A Briton!”\n\n“And Mat blows away at his bassoon, and you’re respectable civilians one and all,” says Mr. George. “Family people. Children growing up. Mat’s old mother in Scotland, and your old father somewhere else, corresponded with, and helped a little, and—well, well! To be sure, I don’t know why I shouldn’t be wished a hundred mile away, for I have not much to do with all this!”\n\nMr. George is becoming thoughtful, sitting before the fire in the whitewashed room, which has a sanded floor and a barrack smell and contains nothing superfluous and has not a visible speck of dirt or dust in it, from the faces of Quebec and Malta to the bright tin pots and pannikins upon the dresser shelves—Mr. George is becoming thoughtful, sitting here while Mrs. Bagnet is busy, when Mr. Bagnet and young Woolwich opportunely come home. Mr. Bagnet is an ex-artilleryman, tall and upright, with shaggy eyebrows and whiskers like the fibres of a coco-nut, not a hair upon his head, and a torrid complexion. His voice, short, deep, and resonant, is not at all unlike the tones of the instrument to which he is devoted. Indeed there may be generally observed in him an unbending, unyielding, brass-bound air, as if he were himself the bassoon of the human orchestra. Young Woolwich is the type and model of a young drummer.\n\nBoth father and son salute the trooper heartily. He saying, in due season, that he has come to advise with Mr. Bagnet, Mr. Bagnet hospitably declares that he will hear of no business until after dinner and that his friend shall not partake of his counsel without first partaking of boiled pork and greens. The trooper yielding to this invitation, he and Mr. Bagnet, not to embarrass the domestic preparations, go forth to take a turn up and down the little street, which they promenade with measured tread and folded arms, as if it were a rampart.\n\n“George,” says Mr. Bagnet. “You know me. It’s my old girl that advises. She has the head. But I never own to it before her. Discipline must be maintained. Wait till the greens is off her mind. Then we’ll consult. Whatever the old girl says, do—do it!”\n\n“I intend to, Mat,” replies the other. “I would sooner take her opinion than that of a college.”\n\n“College,” returns Mr. Bagnet in short sentences, bassoon-like. “What college could you leave—in another quarter of the world—with nothing but a grey cloak and an umbrella—to make its way home to Europe? The old girl would do it to-morrow. Did it once!”\n\n“You are right,” says Mr. George.\n\n“What college,” pursues Bagnet, “could you set up in life—with two penn’orth of white lime—a penn’orth of fuller’s earth—a ha’porth of sand—and the rest of the change out of sixpence in money? That’s what the old girl started on. In the present business.”\n\n“I am rejoiced to hear it’s thriving, Mat.”\n\n“The old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet, acquiescing, “saves. Has a stocking somewhere. With money in it. I never saw it. But I know she’s got it. Wait till the greens is off her mind. Then she’ll set you up.”\n\n“She is a treasure!” exclaims Mr. George.\n\n“She’s more. But I never own to it before her. Discipline must be maintained. It was the old girl that brought out my musical abilities. I should have been in the artillery now but for the old girl. Six years I hammered at the fiddle. Ten at the flute. The old girl said it wouldn’t do; intention good, but want of flexibility; try the bassoon. The old girl borrowed a bassoon from the bandmaster of the Rifle Regiment. I practised in the trenches. Got on, got another, get a living by it!”\n\nGeorge remarks that she looks as fresh as a rose and as sound as an apple.\n\n“The old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet in reply, “is a thoroughly fine woman. Consequently she is like a thoroughly fine day. Gets finer as she gets on. I never saw the old girl’s equal. But I never own to it before her. Discipline must be maintained!”\n\nProceeding to converse on indifferent matters, they walk up and down the little street, keeping step and time, until summoned by Quebec and Malta to do justice to the pork and greens, over which Mrs. Bagnet, like a military chaplain, says a short grace. In the distribution of these comestibles, as in every other household duty, Mrs. Bagnet develops an exact system, sitting with every dish before her, allotting to every portion of pork its own portion of pot-liquor, greens, potatoes, and even mustard, and serving it out complete. Having likewise served out the beer from a can and thus supplied the mess with all things necessary, Mrs. Bagnet proceeds to satisfy her own hunger, which is in a healthy state. The kit of the mess, if the table furniture may be so denominated, is chiefly composed of utensils of horn and tin that have done duty in several parts of the world. Young Woolwich’s knife, in particular, which is of the oyster kind, with the additional feature of a strong shutting-up movement which frequently balks the appetite of that young musician, is mentioned as having gone in various hands the complete round of foreign service.\n\nThe dinner done, Mrs. Bagnet, assisted by the younger branches (who polish their own cups and platters, knives and forks), makes all the dinner garniture shine as brightly as before and puts it all away, first sweeping the hearth, to the end that Mr. Bagnet and the visitor may not be retarded in the smoking of their pipes. These household cares involve much pattening and counter-pattening in the backyard and considerable use of a pail, which is finally so happy as to assist in the ablutions of Mrs. Bagnet herself. That old girl reappearing by and by, quite fresh, and sitting down to her needlework, then and only then—the greens being only then to be considered as entirely off her mind—Mr. Bagnet requests the trooper to state his case.\n\nThis Mr. George does with great discretion, appearing to address himself to Mr. Bagnet, but having an eye solely on the old girl all the time, as Bagnet has himself. She, equally discreet, busies herself with her needlework. The case fully stated, Mr. Bagnet resorts to his standard artifice for the maintenance of discipline.\n\n“That’s the whole of it, is it, George?” says he.\n\n“That’s the whole of it.”\n\n“You act according to my opinion?”\n\n“I shall be guided,” replies George, “entirely by it.”\n\n“Old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet, “give him my opinion. You know it. Tell him what it is.”\n\nIt is that he cannot have too little to do with people who are too deep for him and cannot be too careful of interference with matters he does not understand—that the plain rule is to do nothing in the dark, to be a party to nothing underhanded or mysterious, and never to put his foot where he cannot see the ground. This, in effect, is Mr. Bagnet’s opinion, as delivered through the old girl, and it so relieves Mr. George’s mind by confirming his own opinion and banishing his doubts that he composes himself to smoke another pipe on that exceptional occasion and to have a talk over old times with the whole Bagnet family, according to their various ranges of experience.\n\nThrough these means it comes to pass that Mr. George does not again rise to his full height in that parlour until the time is drawing on when the bassoon and fife are expected by a British public at the theatre; and as it takes time even then for Mr. George, in his domestic character of Bluffy, to take leave of Quebec and Malta and insinuate a sponsorial shilling into the pocket of his godson with felicitations on his success in life, it is dark when Mr. George again turns his face towards Lincoln’s Inn Fields.\n\n“A family home,” he ruminates as he marches along, “however small it is, makes a man like me look lonely. But it’s well I never made that evolution of matrimony. I shouldn’t have been fit for it. I am such a vagabond still, even at my present time of life, that I couldn’t hold to the gallery a month together if it was a regular pursuit or if I didn’t camp there, gipsy fashion. Come! I disgrace nobody and cumber nobody; that’s something. I have not done that for many a long year!”\n\nSo he whistles it off and marches on.\n\nArrived in Lincoln’s Inn Fields and mounting Mr. Tulkinghorn’s stair, he finds the outer door closed and the chambers shut, but the trooper not knowing much about outer doors, and the staircase being dark besides, he is yet fumbling and groping about, hoping to discover a bell-handle or to open the door for himself, when Mr. Tulkinghorn comes up the stairs (quietly, of course) and angrily asks, “Who is that? What are you doing there?”\n\n“I ask your pardon, sir. It’s George. The sergeant.”\n\n“And couldn’t George, the sergeant, see that my door was locked?”\n\n“Why, no, sir, I couldn’t. At any rate, I didn’t,” says the trooper, rather nettled.\n\n“Have you changed your mind? Or are you in the same mind?” Mr. Tulkinghorn demands. But he knows well enough at a glance.\n\n“In the same mind, sir.”\n\n“I thought so. That’s sufficient. You can go. So you are the man,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, opening his door with the key, “in whose hiding-place Mr. Gridley was found?”\n\n“Yes, I AM the man,” says the trooper, stopping two or three stairs down. “What then, sir?”\n\n“What then? I don’t like your associates. You should not have seen the inside of my door this morning if I had thought of your being that man. Gridley? A threatening, murderous, dangerous fellow.”\n\nWith these words, spoken in an unusually high tone for him, the lawyer goes into his rooms and shuts the door with a thundering noise.\n\nMr. George takes his dismissal in great dudgeon, the greater because a clerk coming up the stairs has heard the last words of all and evidently applies them to him. “A pretty character to bear,” the trooper growls with a hasty oath as he strides downstairs. “A threatening, murderous, dangerous fellow!” And looking up, he sees the clerk looking down at him and marking him as he passes a lamp. This so intensifies his dudgeon that for five minutes he is in an ill humour. But he whistles that off like the rest of it and marches home to the shooting gallery.\n\nThe Ironmaster\nSir Leicester Dedlock has got the better, for the time being, of the family gout and is once more, in a literal no less than in a figurative point of view, upon his legs. He is at his place in Lincolnshire; but the waters are out again on the low-lying grounds, and the cold and damp steal into Chesney Wold, though well defended, and eke into Sir Leicester’s bones. The blazing fires of faggot and coal—Dedlock timber and antediluvian forest—that blaze upon the broad wide hearths and wink in the twilight on the frowning woods, sullen to see how trees are sacrificed, do not exclude the enemy. The hot-water pipes that trail themselves all over the house, the cushioned doors and windows, and the screens and curtains fail to supply the fires’ deficiencies and to satisfy Sir Leicester’s need. Hence the fashionable intelligence proclaims one morning to the listening earth that Lady Dedlock is expected shortly to return to town for a few weeks.\n\nIt is a melancholy truth that even great men have their poor relations. Indeed great men have often more than their fair share of poor relations, inasmuch as very red blood of the superior quality, like inferior blood unlawfully shed, WILL cry aloud and WILL be heard. Sir Leicester’s cousins, in the remotest degree, are so many Murders in the respect that they “will out.” Among whom there are cousins who are so poor that one might almost dare to think it would have been the happier for them never to have been plated links upon the Dedlock chain of gold, but to have been made of common iron at first and done base service.\n\nService, however (with a few limited reservations, genteel but not profitable), they may not do, being of the Dedlock dignity. So they visit their richer cousins, and get into debt when they can, and live but shabbily when they can’t, and find—the women no husbands, and the men no wives—and ride in borrowed carriages, and sit at feasts that are never of their own making, and so go through high life. The rich family sum has been divided by so many figures, and they are the something over that nobody knows what to do with.\n\nEverybody on Sir Leicester Dedlock’s side of the question and of his way of thinking would appear to be his cousin more or less. From my Lord Boodle, through the Duke of Foodle, down to Noodle, Sir Leicester, like a glorious spider, stretches his threads of relationship. But while he is stately in the cousinship of the Everybodys, he is a kind and generous man, according to his dignified way, in the cousinship of the Nobodys; and at the present time, in despite of the damp, he stays out the visit of several such cousins at Chesney Wold with the constancy of a martyr.\n\nOf these, foremost in the front rank stands Volumnia Dedlock, a young lady (of sixty) who is doubly highly related, having the honour to be a poor relation, by the mother’s side, to another great family. Miss Volumnia, displaying in early life a pretty talent for cutting ornaments out of coloured paper, and also for singing to the guitar in the Spanish tongue, and propounding French conundrums in country houses, passed the twenty years of her existence between twenty and forty in a sufficiently agreeable manner. Lapsing then out of date and being considered to bore mankind by her vocal performances in the Spanish language, she retired to Bath, where she lives slenderly on an annual present from Sir Leicester and whence she makes occasional resurrections in the country houses of her cousins. She has an extensive acquaintance at Bath among appalling old gentlemen with thin legs and nankeen trousers, and is of high standing in that dreary city. But she is a little dreaded elsewhere in consequence of an indiscreet profusion in the article of rouge and persistency in an obsolete pearl necklace like a rosary of little bird’s-eggs.\n\nIn any country in a wholesome state, Volumnia would be a clear case for the pension list. Efforts have been made to get her on it, and when William Buffy came in, it was fully expected that her name would be put down for a couple of hundred a year. But William Buffy somehow discovered, contrary to all expectation, that these were not the times when it could be done, and this was the first clear indication Sir Leicester Dedlock had conveyed to him that the country was going to pieces.\n\nThere is likewise the Honourable Bob Stables, who can make warm mashes with the skill of a veterinary surgeon and is a better shot than most gamekeepers. He has been for some time particularly desirous to serve his country in a post of good emoluments, unaccompanied by any trouble or responsibility. In a well-regulated body politic this natural desire on the part of a spirited young gentleman so highly connected would be speedily recognized, but somehow William Buffy found when he came in that these were not times in which he could manage that little matter either, and this was the second indication Sir Leicester Dedlock had conveyed to him that the country was going to pieces.\n\nThe rest of the cousins are ladies and gentlemen of various ages and capacities, the major part amiable and sensible and likely to have done well enough in life if they could have overcome their cousinship; as it is, they are almost all a little worsted by it, and lounge in purposeless and listless paths, and seem to be quite as much at a loss how to dispose of themselves as anybody else can be how to dispose of them.\n\nIn this society, and where not, my Lady Dedlock reigns supreme. Beautiful, elegant, accomplished, and powerful in her little world (for the world of fashion does not stretch ALL the way from pole to pole), her influence in Sir Leicester’s house, however haughty and indifferent her manner, is greatly to improve it and refine it. The cousins, even those older cousins who were paralysed when Sir Leicester married her, do her feudal homage; and the Honourable Bob Stables daily repeats to some chosen person between breakfast and lunch his favourite original remark, that she is the best-groomed woman in the whole stud.\n\nSuch the guests in the long drawing-room at Chesney Wold this dismal night when the step on the Ghost’s Walk (inaudible here, however) might be the step of a deceased cousin shut out in the cold. It is near bed-time. Bedroom fires blaze brightly all over the house, raising ghosts of grim furniture on wall and ceiling. Bedroom candlesticks bristle on the distant table by the door, and cousins yawn on ottomans. Cousins at the piano, cousins at the soda-water tray, cousins rising from the card-table, cousins gathered round the fire. Standing on one side of his own peculiar fire (for there are two), Sir Leicester. On the opposite side of the broad hearth, my Lady at her table. Volumnia, as one of the more privileged cousins, in a luxurious chair between them. Sir Leicester glancing, with magnificent displeasure, at the rouge and the pearl necklace.\n\n“I occasionally meet on my staircase here,” drawls Volumnia, whose thoughts perhaps are already hopping up it to bed, after a long evening of very desultory talk, “one of the prettiest girls, I think, that I ever saw in my life.”\n\n“A _protégée_ of my Lady’s,” observes Sir Leicester.\n\n“I thought so. I felt sure that some uncommon eye must have picked that girl out. She really is a marvel. A dolly sort of beauty perhaps,” says Miss Volumnia, reserving her own sort, “but in its way, perfect; such bloom I never saw!”\n\nSir Leicester, with his magnificent glance of displeasure at the rouge, appears to say so too.\n\n“Indeed,” remarks my Lady languidly, “if there is any uncommon eye in the case, it is Mrs. Rouncewell’s, and not mine. Rosa is her discovery.”\n\n“Your maid, I suppose?”\n\n“No. My anything; pet—secretary—messenger—I don’t know what.”\n\n“You like to have her about you, as you would like to have a flower, or a bird, or a picture, or a poodle—no, not a poodle, though—or anything else that was equally pretty?” says Volumnia, sympathizing. “Yes, how charming now! And how well that delightful old soul Mrs. Rouncewell is looking. She must be an immense age, and yet she is as active and handsome! She is the dearest friend I have, positively!”\n\nSir Leicester feels it to be right and fitting that the housekeeper of Chesney Wold should be a remarkable person. Apart from that, he has a real regard for Mrs. Rouncewell and likes to hear her praised. So he says, “You are right, Volumnia,” which Volumnia is extremely glad to hear.\n\n“She has no daughter of her own, has she?”\n\n“Mrs. Rouncewell? No, Volumnia. She has a son. Indeed, she had two.”\n\nMy Lady, whose chronic malady of boredom has been sadly aggravated by Volumnia this evening, glances wearily towards the candlesticks and heaves a noiseless sigh.\n\n“And it is a remarkable example of the confusion into which the present age has fallen; of the obliteration of landmarks, the opening of floodgates, and the uprooting of distinctions,” says Sir Leicester with stately gloom, “that I have been informed by Mr. Tulkinghorn that Mrs. Rouncewell’s son has been invited to go into Parliament.”\n\nMiss Volumnia utters a little sharp scream.\n\n“Yes, indeed,” repeats Sir Leicester. “Into Parliament.”\n\n“I never heard of such a thing! Good gracious, what is the man?” exclaims Volumnia.\n\n“He is called, I believe—an—ironmaster.” Sir Leicester says it slowly and with gravity and doubt, as not being sure but that he is called a lead-mistress or that the right word may be some other word expressive of some other relationship to some other metal.\n\nVolumnia utters another little scream.\n\n“He has declined the proposal, if my information from Mr. Tulkinghorn be correct, as I have no doubt it is. Mr. Tulkinghorn being always correct and exact; still that does not,” says Sir Leicester, “that does not lessen the anomaly, which is fraught with strange considerations—startling considerations, as it appears to me.”\n\nMiss Volumnia rising with a look candlestick-wards, Sir Leicester politely performs the grand tour of the drawing-room, brings one, and lights it at my Lady’s shaded lamp.\n\n“I must beg you, my Lady,” he says while doing so, “to remain a few moments, for this individual of whom I speak arrived this evening shortly before dinner and requested in a very becoming note”—Sir Leicester, with his habitual regard to truth, dwells upon it—“I am bound to say, in a very becoming and well-expressed note, the favour of a short interview with yourself and MYself on the subject of this young girl. As it appeared that he wished to depart to-night, I replied that we would see him before retiring.”\n\nMiss Volumnia with a third little scream takes flight, wishing her hosts—O Lud!—well rid of the—what is it?—ironmaster!\n\nThe other cousins soon disperse, to the last cousin there. Sir Leicester rings the bell, “Make my compliments to Mr. Rouncewell, in the housekeeper’s apartments, and say I can receive him now.”\n\nMy Lady, who has heard all this with slight attention outwardly, looks towards Mr. Rouncewell as he comes in. He is a little over fifty perhaps, of a good figure, like his mother, and has a clear voice, a broad forehead from which his dark hair has retired, and a shrewd though open face. He is a responsible-looking gentleman dressed in black, portly enough, but strong and active. Has a perfectly natural and easy air and is not in the least embarrassed by the great presence into which he comes.\n\n“Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, as I have already apologized for intruding on you, I cannot do better than be very brief. I thank you, Sir Leicester.”\n\nThe head of the Dedlocks has motioned towards a sofa between himself and my Lady. Mr. Rouncewell quietly takes his seat there.\n\n“In these busy times, when so many great undertakings are in progress, people like myself have so many workmen in so many places that we are always on the flight.”\n\nSir Leicester is content enough that the ironmaster should feel that there is no hurry there; there, in that ancient house, rooted in that quiet park, where the ivy and the moss have had time to mature, and the gnarled and warted elms and the umbrageous oaks stand deep in the fern and leaves of a hundred years; and where the sun-dial on the terrace has dumbly recorded for centuries that time which was as much the property of every Dedlock—while he lasted—as the house and lands. Sir Leicester sits down in an easy-chair, opposing his repose and that of Chesney Wold to the restless flights of ironmasters.\n\n“Lady Dedlock has been so kind,” proceeds Mr. Rouncewell with a respectful glance and a bow that way, “as to place near her a young beauty of the name of Rosa. Now, my son has fallen in love with Rosa and has asked my consent to his proposing marriage to her and to their becoming engaged if she will take him—which I suppose she will. I have never seen Rosa until to-day, but I have some confidence in my son’s good sense—even in love. I find her what he represents her, to the best of my judgment; and my mother speaks of her with great commendation.”\n\n“She in all respects deserves it,” says my Lady.\n\n“I am happy, Lady Dedlock, that you say so, and I need not comment on the value to me of your kind opinion of her.”\n\n“That,” observes Sir Leicester with unspeakable grandeur, for he thinks the ironmaster a little too glib, “must be quite unnecessary.”\n\n“Quite unnecessary, Sir Leicester. Now, my son is a very young man, and Rosa is a very young woman. As I made my way, so my son must make his; and his being married at present is out of the question. But supposing I gave my consent to his engaging himself to this pretty girl, if this pretty girl will engage herself to him, I think it a piece of candour to say at once—I am sure, Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, you will understand and excuse me—I should make it a condition that she did not remain at Chesney Wold. Therefore, before communicating further with my son, I take the liberty of saying that if her removal would be in any way inconvenient or objectionable, I will hold the matter over with him for any reasonable time and leave it precisely where it is.”\n\nNot remain at Chesney Wold! Make it a condition! All Sir Leicester’s old misgivings relative to Wat Tyler and the people in the iron districts who do nothing but turn out by torchlight come in a shower upon his head, the fine grey hair of which, as well as of his whiskers, actually stirs with indignation.\n\n“Am I to understand, sir,” says Sir Leicester, “and is my Lady to understand”—he brings her in thus specially, first as a point of gallantry, and next as a point of prudence, having great reliance on her sense—“am I to understand, Mr. Rouncewell, and is my Lady to understand, sir, that you consider this young woman too good for Chesney Wold or likely to be injured by remaining here?”\n\n“Certainly not, Sir Leicester,”\n\n“I am glad to hear it.” Sir Leicester very lofty indeed.\n\n“Pray, Mr. Rouncewell,” says my Lady, warning Sir Leicester off with the slightest gesture of her pretty hand, as if he were a fly, “explain to me what you mean.”\n\n“Willingly, Lady Dedlock. There is nothing I could desire more.”\n\nAddressing her composed face, whose intelligence, however, is too quick and active to be concealed by any studied impassiveness, however habitual, to the strong Saxon face of the visitor, a picture of resolution and perseverance, my Lady listens with attention, occasionally slightly bending her head.\n\n“I am the son of your housekeeper, Lady Dedlock, and passed my childhood about this house. My mother has lived here half a century and will die here I have no doubt. She is one of those examples—perhaps as good a one as there is—of love, and attachment, and fidelity in such a nation, which England may well be proud of, but of which no order can appropriate the whole pride or the whole merit, because such an instance bespeaks high worth on two sides—on the great side assuredly, on the small one no less assuredly.”\n\nSir Leicester snorts a little to hear the law laid down in this way, but in his honour and his love of truth, he freely, though silently, admits the justice of the ironmaster’s proposition.\n\n“Pardon me for saying what is so obvious, but I wouldn’t have it hastily supposed,” with the least turn of his eyes towards Sir Leicester, “that I am ashamed of my mother’s position here, or wanting in all just respect for Chesney Wold and the family. I certainly may have desired—I certainly have desired, Lady Dedlock—that my mother should retire after so many years and end her days with me. But as I have found that to sever this strong bond would be to break her heart, I have long abandoned that idea.”\n\nSir Leicester very magnificent again at the notion of Mrs. Rouncewell being spirited off from her natural home to end her days with an ironmaster.\n\n“I have been,” proceeds the visitor in a modest, clear way, “an apprentice and a workman. I have lived on workman’s wages, years and years, and beyond a certain point have had to educate myself. My wife was a foreman’s daughter, and plainly brought up. We have three daughters besides this son of whom I have spoken, and being fortunately able to give them greater advantages than we have had ourselves, we have educated them well, very well. It has been one of our great cares and pleasures to make them worthy of any station.”\n\nA little boastfulness in his fatherly tone here, as if he added in his heart, “even of the Chesney Wold station.” Not a little more magnificence, therefore, on the part of Sir Leicester.\n\n“All this is so frequent, Lady Dedlock, where I live, and among the class to which I belong, that what would be generally called unequal marriages are not of such rare occurrence with us as elsewhere. A son will sometimes make it known to his father that he has fallen in love, say, with a young woman in the factory. The father, who once worked in a factory himself, will be a little disappointed at first very possibly. It may be that he had other views for his son. However, the chances are that having ascertained the young woman to be of unblemished character, he will say to his son, ‘I must be quite sure you are in earnest here. This is a serious matter for both of you. Therefore I shall have this girl educated for two years,’ or it may be, ‘I shall place this girl at the same school with your sisters for such a time, during which you will give me your word and honour to see her only so often. If at the expiration of that time, when she has so far profited by her advantages as that you may be upon a fair equality, you are both in the same mind, I will do my part to make you happy.’ I know of several cases such as I describe, my Lady, and I think they indicate to me my own course now.”\n\nSir Leicester’s magnificence explodes. Calmly, but terribly.\n\n“Mr. Rouncewell,” says Sir Leicester with his right hand in the breast of his blue coat, the attitude of state in which he is painted in the gallery, “do you draw a parallel between Chesney Wold and a—” Here he resists a disposition to choke, “a factory?”\n\n“I need not reply, Sir Leicester, that the two places are very different; but for the purposes of this case, I think a parallel may be justly drawn between them.”\n\nSir Leicester directs his majestic glance down one side of the long drawing-room and up the other before he can believe that he is awake.\n\n“Are you aware, sir, that this young woman whom my Lady—my Lady—has placed near her person was brought up at the village school outside the gates?”\n\n“Sir Leicester, I am quite aware of it. A very good school it is, and handsomely supported by this family.”\n\n“Then, Mr. Rouncewell,” returns Sir Leicester, “the application of what you have said is, to me, incomprehensible.”\n\n“Will it be more comprehensible, Sir Leicester, if I say,” the ironmaster is reddening a little, “that I do not regard the village school as teaching everything desirable to be known by my son’s wife?”\n\nFrom the village school of Chesney Wold, intact as it is this minute, to the whole framework of society; from the whole framework of society, to the aforesaid framework receiving tremendous cracks in consequence of people (iron-masters, lead-mistresses, and what not) not minding their catechism, and getting out of the station unto which they are called—necessarily and for ever, according to Sir Leicester’s rapid logic, the first station in which they happen to find themselves; and from that, to their educating other people out of THEIR stations, and so obliterating the landmarks, and opening the floodgates, and all the rest of it; this is the swift progress of the Dedlock mind.\n\n“My Lady, I beg your pardon. Permit me, for one moment!” She has given a faint indication of intending to speak. “Mr. Rouncewell, our views of duty, and our views of station, and our views of education, and our views of—in short, ALL our views—are so diametrically opposed, that to prolong this discussion must be repellent to your feelings and repellent to my own. This young woman is honoured with my Lady’s notice and favour. If she wishes to withdraw herself from that notice and favour or if she chooses to place herself under the influence of any one who may in his peculiar opinions—you will allow me to say, in his peculiar opinions, though I readily admit that he is not accountable for them to me—who may, in his peculiar opinions, withdraw her from that notice and favour, she is at any time at liberty to do so. We are obliged to you for the plainness with which you have spoken. It will have no effect of itself, one way or other, on the young woman’s position here. Beyond this, we can make no terms; and here we beg—if you will be so good—to leave the subject.”\n\nThe visitor pauses a moment to give my Lady an opportunity, but she says nothing. He then rises and replies, “Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, allow me to thank you for your attention and only to observe that I shall very seriously recommend my son to conquer his present inclinations. Good night!”\n\n“Mr. Rouncewell,” says Sir Leicester with all the nature of a gentleman shining in him, “it is late, and the roads are dark. I hope your time is not so precious but that you will allow my Lady and myself to offer you the hospitality of Chesney Wold, for to-night at least.”\n\n“I hope so,” adds my Lady.\n\n“I am much obliged to you, but I have to travel all night in order to reach a distant part of the country punctually at an appointed time in the morning.”\n\nTherewith the ironmaster takes his departure, Sir Leicester ringing the bell and my Lady rising as he leaves the room.\n\nWhen my Lady goes to her boudoir, she sits down thoughtfully by the fire, and inattentive to the Ghost’s Walk, looks at Rosa, writing in an inner room. Presently my Lady calls her.\n\n“Come to me, child. Tell me the truth. Are you in love?”\n\n“Oh! My Lady!”\n\nMy Lady, looking at the downcast and blushing face, says smiling, “Who is it? Is it Mrs. Rouncewell’s grandson?”\n\n“Yes, if you please, my Lady. But I don’t know that I am in love with him—yet.”\n\n“Yet, you silly little thing! Do you know that he loves YOU, yet?”\n\n“I think he likes me a little, my Lady.” And Rosa bursts into tears.\n\nIs this Lady Dedlock standing beside the village beauty, smoothing her dark hair with that motherly touch, and watching her with eyes so full of musing interest? Aye, indeed it is!\n\n“Listen to me, child. You are young and true, and I believe you are attached to me.”\n\n“Indeed I am, my Lady. Indeed there is nothing in the world I wouldn’t do to show how much.”\n\n“And I don’t think you would wish to leave me just yet, Rosa, even for a lover?”\n\n“No, my Lady! Oh, no!” Rosa looks up for the first time, quite frightened at the thought.\n\n“Confide in me, my child. Don’t fear me. I wish you to be happy, and will make you so—if I can make anybody happy on this earth.”\n\nRosa, with fresh tears, kneels at her feet and kisses her hand. My Lady takes the hand with which she has caught it, and standing with her eyes fixed on the fire, puts it about and about between her own two hands, and gradually lets it fall. Seeing her so absorbed, Rosa softly withdraws; but still my Lady’s eyes are on the fire.\n\nIn search of what? Of any hand that is no more, of any hand that never was, of any touch that might have magically changed her life? Or does she listen to the Ghost’s Walk and think what step does it most resemble? A man’s? A woman’s? The pattering of a little child’s feet, ever coming on—on—on? Some melancholy influence is upon her, or why should so proud a lady close the doors and sit alone upon the hearth so desolate?\n\nVolumnia is away next day, and all the cousins are scattered before dinner. Not a cousin of the batch but is amazed to hear from Sir Leicester at breakfast-time of the obliteration of landmarks, and opening of floodgates, and cracking of the framework of society, manifested through Mrs. Rouncewell’s son. Not a cousin of the batch but is really indignant, and connects it with the feebleness of William Buffy when in office, and really does feel deprived of a stake in the country—or the pension list—or something—by fraud and wrong. As to Volumnia, she is handed down the great staircase by Sir Leicester, as eloquent upon the theme as if there were a general rising in the north of England to obtain her rouge-pot and pearl necklace. And thus, with a clatter of maids and valets—for it is one appurtenance of their cousinship that however difficult they may find it to keep themselves, they MUST keep maids and valets—the cousins disperse to the four winds of heaven; and the one wintry wind that blows to-day shakes a shower from the trees near the deserted house, as if all the cousins had been changed into leaves.\nThe Young Man\n\nChesney Wold is shut up, carpets are rolled into great scrolls in corners of comfortless rooms, bright damask does penance in brown holland, carving and gilding puts on mortification, and the Dedlock ancestors retire from the light of day again. Around and around the house the leaves fall thick, but never fast, for they come circling down with a dead lightness that is sombre and slow. Let the gardener sweep and sweep the turf as he will, and press the leaves into full barrows, and wheel them off, still they lie ankle-deep. Howls the shrill wind round Chesney Wold; the sharp rain beats, the windows rattle, and the chimneys growl. Mists hide in the avenues, veil the points of view, and move in funeral-wise across the rising grounds. On all the house there is a cold, blank smell like the smell of a little church, though something dryer, suggesting that the dead and buried Dedlocks walk there in the long nights and leave the flavour of their graves behind them.\n\nBut the house in town, which is rarely in the same mind as Chesney Wold at the same time, seldom rejoicing when it rejoices or mourning when it mourns, excepting when a Dedlock dies—the house in town shines out awakened. As warm and bright as so much state may be, as delicately redolent of pleasant scents that bear no trace of winter as hothouse flowers can make it, soft and hushed so that the ticking of the clocks and the crisp burning of the fires alone disturb the stillness in the rooms, it seems to wrap those chilled bones of Sir Leicester’s in rainbow-coloured wool. And Sir Leicester is glad to repose in dignified contentment before the great fire in the library, condescendingly perusing the backs of his books or honouring the fine arts with a glance of approbation. For he has his pictures, ancient and modern. Some of the Fancy Ball School in which art occasionally condescends to become a master, which would be best catalogued like the miscellaneous articles in a sale. As “Three high-backed chairs, a table and cover, long-necked bottle (containing wine), one flask, one Spanish female’s costume, three-quarter face portrait of Miss Jogg the model, and a suit of armour containing Don Quixote.” Or “One stone terrace (cracked), one gondola in distance, one Venetian senator’s dress complete, richly embroidered white satin costume with profile portrait of Miss Jogg the model, one Scimitar superbly mounted in gold with jewelled handle, elaborate Moorish dress (very rare), and Othello.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn comes and goes pretty often, there being estate business to do, leases to be renewed, and so on. He sees my Lady pretty often, too; and he and she are as composed, and as indifferent, and take as little heed of one another, as ever. Yet it may be that my Lady fears this Mr. Tulkinghorn and that he knows it. It may be that he pursues her doggedly and steadily, with no touch of compunction, remorse, or pity. It may be that her beauty and all the state and brilliancy surrounding her only gives him the greater zest for what he is set upon and makes him the more inflexible in it. Whether he be cold and cruel, whether immovable in what he has made his duty, whether absorbed in love of power, whether determined to have nothing hidden from him in ground where he has burrowed among secrets all his life, whether he in his heart despises the splendour of which he is a distant beam, whether he is always treasuring up slights and offences in the affability of his gorgeous clients—whether he be any of this, or all of this, it may be that my Lady had better have five thousand pairs of fashionable eyes upon her, in distrustful vigilance, than the two eyes of this rusty lawyer with his wisp of neckcloth and his dull black breeches tied with ribbons at the knees.\n\nSir Leicester sits in my Lady’s room—that room in which Mr. Tulkinghorn read the affidavit in Jarndyce and Jarndyce—particularly complacent. My Lady, as on that day, sits before the fire with her screen in her hand. Sir Leicester is particularly complacent because he has found in his newspaper some congenial remarks bearing directly on the floodgates and the framework of society. They apply so happily to the late case that Sir Leicester has come from the library to my Lady’s room expressly to read them aloud. “The man who wrote this article,” he observes by way of preface, nodding at the fire as if he were nodding down at the man from a mount, “has a well-balanced mind.”\n\nThe man’s mind is not so well balanced but that he bores my Lady, who, after a languid effort to listen, or rather a languid resignation of herself to a show of listening, becomes distraught and falls into a contemplation of the fire as if it were her fire at Chesney Wold, and she had never left it. Sir Leicester, quite unconscious, reads on through his double eye-glass, occasionally stopping to remove his glass and express approval, as “Very true indeed,” “Very properly put,” “I have frequently made the same remark myself,” invariably losing his place after each observation, and going up and down the column to find it again.\n\nSir Leicester is reading with infinite gravity and state when the door opens, and the Mercury in powder makes this strange announcement, “The young man, my Lady, of the name of Guppy.”\n\nSir Leicester pauses, stares, repeats in a killing voice, “The young man of the name of Guppy?”\n\nLooking round, he beholds the young man of the name of Guppy, much discomfited and not presenting a very impressive letter of introduction in his manner and appearance.\n\n“Pray,” says Sir Leicester to Mercury, “what do you mean by announcing with this abruptness a young man of the name of Guppy?”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir Leicester, but my Lady said she would see the young man whenever he called. I was not aware that you were here, Sir Leicester.”\n\nWith this apology, Mercury directs a scornful and indignant look at the young man of the name of Guppy which plainly says, “What do you come calling here for and getting ME into a row?”\n\n“It’s quite right. I gave him those directions,” says my Lady. “Let the young man wait.”\n\n“By no means, my Lady. Since he has your orders to come, I will not interrupt you.” Sir Leicester in his gallantry retires, rather declining to accept a bow from the young man as he goes out and majestically supposing him to be some shoemaker of intrusive appearance.\n\nLady Dedlock looks imperiously at her visitor when the servant has left the room, casting her eyes over him from head to foot. She suffers him to stand by the door and asks him what he wants.\n\n“That your ladyship would have the kindness to oblige me with a little conversation,” returns Mr. Guppy, embarrassed.\n\n“You are, of course, the person who has written me so many letters?”\n\n“Several, your ladyship. Several before your ladyship condescended to favour me with an answer.”\n\n“And could you not take the same means of rendering a Conversation unnecessary? Can you not still?”\n\nMr. Guppy screws his mouth into a silent “No!” and shakes his head.\n\n“You have been strangely importunate. If it should appear, after all, that what you have to say does not concern me—and I don’t know how it can, and don’t expect that it will—you will allow me to cut you short with but little ceremony. Say what you have to say, if you please.”\n\nMy Lady, with a careless toss of her screen, turns herself towards the fire again, sitting almost with her back to the young man of the name of Guppy.\n\n“With your ladyship’s permission, then,” says the young man, “I will now enter on my business. Hem! I am, as I told your ladyship in my first letter, in the law. Being in the law, I have learnt the habit of not committing myself in writing, and therefore I did not mention to your ladyship the name of the firm with which I am connected and in which my standing—and I may add income—is tolerably good. I may now state to your ladyship, in confidence, that the name of that firm is Kenge and Carboy, of Lincoln’s Inn, which may not be altogether unknown to your ladyship in connexion with the case in Chancery of Jarndyce and Jarndyce.”\n\nMy Lady’s figure begins to be expressive of some attention. She has ceased to toss the screen and holds it as if she were listening.\n\n“Now, I may say to your ladyship at once,” says Mr. Guppy, a little emboldened, “it is no matter arising out of Jarndyce and Jarndyce that made me so desirous to speak to your ladyship, which conduct I have no doubt did appear, and does appear, obtrusive—in fact, almost blackguardly.”\n\nAfter waiting for a moment to receive some assurance to the contrary, and not receiving any, Mr. Guppy proceeds, “If it had been Jarndyce and Jarndyce, I should have gone at once to your ladyship’s solicitor, Mr. Tulkinghorn, of the Fields. I have the pleasure of being acquainted with Mr. Tulkinghorn—at least we move when we meet one another—and if it had been any business of that sort, I should have gone to him.”\n\nMy Lady turns a little round and says, “You had better sit down.”\n\n“Thank your ladyship.” Mr. Guppy does so. “Now, your ladyship”—Mr. Guppy refers to a little slip of paper on which he has made small notes of his line of argument and which seems to involve him in the densest obscurity whenever he looks at it—“I—Oh, yes!—I place myself entirely in your ladyship’s hands. If your ladyship was to make any complaint to Kenge and Carboy or to Mr. Tulkinghorn of the present visit, I should be placed in a very disagreeable situation. That, I openly admit. Consequently, I rely upon your ladyship’s honour.”\n\nMy Lady, with a disdainful gesture of the hand that holds the screen, assures him of his being worth no complaint from her.\n\n“Thank your ladyship,” says Mr. Guppy; “quite satisfactory. Now—I—dash it!—The fact is that I put down a head or two here of the order of the points I thought of touching upon, and they’re written short, and I can’t quite make out what they mean. If your ladyship will excuse me taking it to the window half a moment, I—”\n\nMr. Guppy, going to the window, tumbles into a pair of love-birds, to whom he says in his confusion, “I beg your pardon, I am sure.” This does not tend to the greater legibility of his notes. He murmurs, growing warm and red and holding the slip of paper now close to his eyes, now a long way off, “C.S. What’s C.S. for? Oh! C.S.! Oh, I know! Yes, to be sure!” And comes back enlightened.\n\n“I am not aware,” says Mr. Guppy, standing midway between my Lady and his chair, “whether your ladyship ever happened to hear of, or to see, a young lady of the name of Miss Esther Summerson.”\n\nMy Lady’s eyes look at him full. “I saw a young lady of that name not long ago. This past autumn.”\n\n“Now, did it strike your ladyship that she was like anybody?” asks Mr. Guppy, crossing his arms, holding his head on one side, and scratching the corner of his mouth with his memoranda.\n\nMy Lady removes her eyes from him no more.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Not like your ladyship’s family?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“I think your ladyship,” says Mr. Guppy, “can hardly remember Miss Summerson’s face?”\n\n“I remember the young lady very well. What has this to do with me?”\n\n“Your ladyship, I do assure you that having Miss Summerson’s image imprinted on my ’eart—which I mention in confidence—I found, when I had the honour of going over your ladyship’s mansion of Chesney Wold while on a short out in the county of Lincolnshire with a friend, such a resemblance between Miss Esther Summerson and your ladyship’s own portrait that it completely knocked me over, so much so that I didn’t at the moment even know what it WAS that knocked me over. And now I have the honour of beholding your ladyship near (I have often, since that, taken the liberty of looking at your ladyship in your carriage in the park, when I dare say you was not aware of me, but I never saw your ladyship so near), it’s really more surprising than I thought it.”\n\nYoung man of the name of Guppy! There have been times, when ladies lived in strongholds and had unscrupulous attendants within call, when that poor life of yours would NOT have been worth a minute’s purchase, with those beautiful eyes looking at you as they look at this moment.\n\nMy Lady, slowly using her little hand-screen as a fan, asks him again what he supposes that his taste for likenesses has to do with her.\n\n“Your ladyship,” replies Mr. Guppy, again referring to his paper, “I am coming to that. Dash these notes! Oh! ‘Mrs. Chadband.’ Yes.” Mr. Guppy draws his chair a little forward and seats himself again. My Lady reclines in her chair composedly, though with a trifle less of graceful ease than usual perhaps, and never falters in her steady gaze. “A—stop a minute, though!” Mr. Guppy refers again. “E.S. twice? Oh, yes! Yes, I see my way now, right on.”\n\nRolling up the slip of paper as an instrument to point his speech with, Mr. Guppy proceeds.\n\n“Your ladyship, there is a mystery about Miss Esther Summerson’s birth and bringing up. I am informed of that fact because—which I mention in confidence—I know it in the way of my profession at Kenge and Carboy’s. Now, as I have already mentioned to your ladyship, Miss Summerson’s image is imprinted on my ’eart. If I could clear this mystery for her, or prove her to be well related, or find that having the honour to be a remote branch of your ladyship’s family she had a right to be made a party in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, why, I might make a sort of a claim upon Miss Summerson to look with an eye of more dedicated favour on my proposals than she has exactly done as yet. In fact, as yet she hasn’t favoured them at all.”\n\nA kind of angry smile just dawns upon my Lady’s face.\n\n“Now, it’s a very singular circumstance, your ladyship,” says Mr. Guppy, “though one of those circumstances that do fall in the way of us professional men—which I may call myself, for though not admitted, yet I have had a present of my articles made to me by Kenge and Carboy, on my mother’s advancing from the principal of her little income the money for the stamp, which comes heavy—that I have encountered the person who lived as servant with the lady who brought Miss Summerson up before Mr. Jarndyce took charge of her. That lady was a Miss Barbary, your ladyship.”\n\nIs the dead colour on my Lady’s face reflected from the screen which has a green silk ground and which she holds in her raised hand as if she had forgotten it, or is it a dreadful paleness that has fallen on her?\n\n“Did your ladyship,” says Mr. Guppy, “ever happen to hear of Miss Barbary?”\n\n“I don’t know. I think so. Yes.”\n\n“Was Miss Barbary at all connected with your ladyship’s family?”\n\nMy Lady’s lips move, but they utter nothing. She shakes her head.\n\n“NOT connected?” says Mr. Guppy. “Oh! Not to your ladyship’s knowledge, perhaps? Ah! But might be? Yes.” After each of these interrogatories, she has inclined her head. “Very good! Now, this Miss Barbary was extremely close—seems to have been extraordinarily close for a female, females being generally (in common life at least) rather given to conversation—and my witness never had an idea whether she possessed a single relative. On one occasion, and only one, she seems to have been confidential to my witness on a single point, and she then told her that the little girl’s real name was not Esther Summerson, but Esther Hawdon.”\n\n“My God!”\n\nMr. Guppy stares. Lady Dedlock sits before him looking him through, with the same dark shade upon her face, in the same attitude even to the holding of the screen, with her lips a little apart, her brow a little contracted, but for the moment dead. He sees her consciousness return, sees a tremor pass across her frame like a ripple over water, sees her lips shake, sees her compose them by a great effort, sees her force herself back to the knowledge of his presence and of what he has said. All this, so quickly, that her exclamation and her dead condition seem to have passed away like the features of those long-preserved dead bodies sometimes opened up in tombs, which, struck by the air like lightning, vanish in a breath.\n\n“Your ladyship is acquainted with the name of Hawdon?”\n\n“I have heard it before.”\n\n“Name of any collateral or remote branch of your ladyship’s family?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Now, your ladyship,” says Mr. Guppy, “I come to the last point of the case, so far as I have got it up. It’s going on, and I shall gather it up closer and closer as it goes on. Your ladyship must know—if your ladyship don’t happen, by any chance, to know already—that there was found dead at the house of a person named Krook, near Chancery Lane, some time ago, a law-writer in great distress. Upon which law-writer there was an inquest, and which law-writer was an anonymous character, his name being unknown. But, your ladyship, I have discovered very lately that that law-writer’s name was Hawdon.”\n\n“And what is THAT to me?”\n\n“Aye, your ladyship, that’s the question! Now, your ladyship, a queer thing happened after that man’s death. A lady started up, a disguised lady, your ladyship, who went to look at the scene of action and went to look at his grave. She hired a crossing-sweeping boy to show it her. If your ladyship would wish to have the boy produced in corroboration of this statement, I can lay my hand upon him at any time.”\n\nThe wretched boy is nothing to my Lady, and she does NOT wish to have him produced.\n\n“Oh, I assure your ladyship it’s a very queer start indeed,” says Mr. Guppy. “If you was to hear him tell about the rings that sparkled on her fingers when she took her glove off, you’d think it quite romantic.”\n\nThere are diamonds glittering on the hand that holds the screen. My Lady trifles with the screen and makes them glitter more, again with that expression which in other times might have been so dangerous to the young man of the name of Guppy.\n\n“It was supposed, your ladyship, that he left no rag or scrap behind him by which he could be possibly identified. But he did. He left a bundle of old letters.”\n\nThe screen still goes, as before. All this time her eyes never once release him.\n\n“They were taken and secreted. And to-morrow night, your ladyship, they will come into my possession.”\n\n“Still I ask you, what is this to me?”\n\n“Your ladyship, I conclude with that.” Mr. Guppy rises. “If you think there’s enough in this chain of circumstances put together—in the undoubted strong likeness of this young lady to your ladyship, which is a positive fact for a jury; in her having been brought up by Miss Barbary; in Miss Barbary stating Miss Summerson’s real name to be Hawdon; in your ladyship’s knowing both these names VERY WELL; and in Hawdon’s dying as he did—to give your ladyship a family interest in going further into the case, I will bring these papers here. I don’t know what they are, except that they are old letters: I have never had them in my possession yet. I will bring those papers here as soon as I get them and go over them for the first time with your ladyship. I have told your ladyship my object. I have told your ladyship that I should be placed in a very disagreeable situation if any complaint was made, and all is in strict confidence.”\n\nIs this the full purpose of the young man of the name of Guppy, or has he any other? Do his words disclose the length, breadth, depth, of his object and suspicion in coming here; or if not, what do they hide? He is a match for my Lady there. She may look at him, but he can look at the table and keep that witness-box face of his from telling anything.\n\n“You may bring the letters,” says my Lady, “if you choose.”\n\n“Your ladyship is not very encouraging, upon my word and honour,” says Mr. Guppy, a little injured.\n\n“You may bring the letters,” she repeats in the same tone, “if you—please.”\n\n“It shall be done. I wish your ladyship good day.”\n\nOn a table near her is a rich bauble of a casket, barred and clasped like an old strong-chest. She, looking at him still, takes it to her and unlocks it.\n\n“Oh! I assure your ladyship I am not actuated by any motives of that sort,” says Mr. Guppy, “and I couldn’t accept anything of the kind. I wish your ladyship good day, and am much obliged to you all the same.”\n\nSo the young man makes his bow and goes downstairs, where the supercilious Mercury does not consider himself called upon to leave his Olympus by the hall-fire to let the young man out.\n\nAs Sir Leicester basks in his library and dozes over his newspaper, is there no influence in the house to startle him, not to say to make the very trees at Chesney Wold fling up their knotted arms, the very portraits frown, the very armour stir?\n\nNo. Words, sobs, and cries are but air, and air is so shut in and shut out throughout the house in town that sounds need be uttered trumpet-tongued indeed by my Lady in her chamber to carry any faint vibration to Sir Leicester’s ears; and yet this cry is in the house, going upward from a wild figure on its knees.\n\n“O my child, my child! Not dead in the first hours of her life, as my cruel sister told me, but sternly nurtured by her, after she had renounced me and my name! O my child, O my child!”\n\nEsther’s Narrative\nRichard had been gone away some time when a visitor came to pass a few days with us. It was an elderly lady. It was Mrs. Woodcourt, who, having come from Wales to stay with Mrs. Bayham Badger and having written to my guardian, “by her son Allan’s desire,” to report that she had heard from him and that he was well “and sent his kind remembrances to all of us,” had been invited by my guardian to make a visit to Bleak House. She stayed with us nearly three weeks. She took very kindly to me and was extremely confidential, so much so that sometimes she almost made me uncomfortable. I had no right, I knew very well, to be uncomfortable because she confided in me, and I felt it was unreasonable; still, with all I could do, I could not quite help it.\n\nShe was such a sharp little lady and used to sit with her hands folded in each other looking so very watchful while she talked to me that perhaps I found that rather irksome. Or perhaps it was her being so upright and trim, though I don’t think it was that, because I thought that quaintly pleasant. Nor can it have been the general expression of her face, which was very sparkling and pretty for an old lady. I don’t know what it was. Or at least if I do now, I thought I did not then. Or at least—but it don’t matter.\n\nOf a night when I was going upstairs to bed, she would invite me into her room, where she sat before the fire in a great chair; and, dear me, she would tell me about Morgan ap-Kerrig until I was quite low-spirited! Sometimes she recited a few verses from Crumlinwallinwer and the Mewlinnwillinwodd (if those are the right names, which I dare say they are not), and would become quite fiery with the sentiments they expressed. Though I never knew what they were (being in Welsh), further than that they were highly eulogistic of the lineage of Morgan ap-Kerrig.\n\n“So, Miss Summerson,” she would say to me with stately triumph, “this, you see, is the fortune inherited by my son. Wherever my son goes, he can claim kindred with Ap-Kerrig. He may not have money, but he always has what is much better—family, my dear.”\n\nI had my doubts of their caring so very much for Morgan ap-Kerrig in India and China, but of course I never expressed them. I used to say it was a great thing to be so highly connected.\n\n“It IS, my dear, a great thing,” Mrs. Woodcourt would reply. “It has its disadvantages; my son’s choice of a wife, for instance, is limited by it, but the matrimonial choice of the royal family is limited in much the same manner.”\n\nThen she would pat me on the arm and smooth my dress, as much as to assure me that she had a good opinion of me, the distance between us notwithstanding.\n\n“Poor Mr. Woodcourt, my dear,” she would say, and always with some emotion, for with her lofty pedigree she had a very affectionate heart, “was descended from a great Highland family, the MacCoorts of MacCoort. He served his king and country as an officer in the Royal Highlanders, and he died on the field. My son is one of the last representatives of two old families. With the blessing of heaven he will set them up again and unite them with another old family.”\n\nIt was in vain for me to try to change the subject, as I used to try, only for the sake of novelty or perhaps because—but I need not be so particular. Mrs. Woodcourt never would let me change it.\n\n“My dear,” she said one night, “you have so much sense and you look at the world in a quiet manner so superior to your time of life that it is a comfort to me to talk to you about these family matters of mine. You don’t know much of my son, my dear; but you know enough of him, I dare say, to recollect him?”\n\n“Yes, ma’am. I recollect him.”\n\n“Yes, my dear. Now, my dear, I think you are a judge of character, and I should like to have your opinion of him.”\n\n“Oh, Mrs. Woodcourt,” said I, “that is so difficult!”\n\n“Why is it so difficult, my dear?” she returned. “I don’t see it myself.”\n\n“To give an opinion—”\n\n“On so slight an acquaintance, my dear. THAT’S true.”\n\nI didn’t mean that, because Mr. Woodcourt had been at our house a good deal altogether and had become quite intimate with my guardian. I said so, and added that he seemed to be very clever in his profession—we thought—and that his kindness and gentleness to Miss Flite were above all praise.\n\n“You do him justice!” said Mrs. Woodcourt, pressing my hand. “You define him exactly. Allan is a dear fellow, and in his profession faultless. I say it, though I am his mother. Still, I must confess he is not without faults, love.”\n“None of us are,” said I.\n\n“Ah! But his really are faults that he might correct, and ought to correct,” returned the sharp old lady, sharply shaking her head. “I am so much attached to you that I may confide in you, my dear, as a third party wholly disinterested, that he is fickleness itself.”\n\nI said I should have thought it hardly possible that he could have been otherwise than constant to his profession and zealous in the pursuit of it, judging from the reputation he had earned.\n\n“You are right again, my dear,” the old lady retorted, “but I don’t refer to his profession, look you.”\n\n“Oh!” said I.\n\n“No,” said she. “I refer, my dear, to his social conduct. He is always paying trivial attentions to young ladies, and always has been, ever since he was eighteen. Now, my dear, he has never really cared for any one of them and has never meant in doing this to do any harm or to express anything but politeness and good nature. Still, it’s not right, you know; is it?”\n\n“No,” said I, as she seemed to wait for me.\n\n“And it might lead to mistaken notions, you see, my dear.”\n\nI supposed it might.\n\n“Therefore, I have told him many times that he really should be more careful, both in justice to himself and in justice to others. And he has always said, ‘Mother, I will be; but you know me better than anybody else does, and you know I mean no harm—in short, mean nothing.’ All of which is very true, my dear, but is no justification. However, as he is now gone so far away and for an indefinite time, and as he will have good opportunities and introductions, we may consider this past and gone. And you, my dear,” said the old lady, who was now all nods and smiles, “regarding your dear self, my love?”\n\n“Me, Mrs. Woodcourt?”\n\n“Not to be always selfish, talking of my son, who has gone to seek his fortune and to find a wife—when do you mean to seek YOUR fortune and to find a husband, Miss Summerson? Hey, look you! Now you blush!”\n\nI don’t think I did blush—at all events, it was not important if I did—and I said my present fortune perfectly contented me and I had no wish to change it.\n\n“Shall I tell you what I always think of you and the fortune yet to come for you, my love?” said Mrs. Woodcourt.\n\n“If you believe you are a good prophet,” said I.\n\n“Why, then, it is that you will marry some one very rich and very worthy, much older—five and twenty years, perhaps—than yourself. And you will be an excellent wife, and much beloved, and very happy.”\n\n“That is a good fortune,” said I. “But why is it to be mine?”\n\n“My dear,” she returned, “there’s suitability in it—you are so busy, and so neat, and so peculiarly situated altogether that there’s suitability in it, and it will come to pass. And nobody, my love, will congratulate you more sincerely on such a marriage than I shall.”\n\nIt was curious that this should make me uncomfortable, but I think it did. I know it did. It made me for some part of that night uncomfortable. I was so ashamed of my folly that I did not like to confess it even to Ada, and that made me more uncomfortable still. I would have given anything not to have been so much in the bright old lady’s confidence if I could have possibly declined it. It gave me the most inconsistent opinions of her. At one time I thought she was a story-teller, and at another time that she was the pink of truth. Now I suspected that she was very cunning, next moment I believed her honest Welsh heart to be perfectly innocent and simple. And after all, what did it matter to me, and why did it matter to me? Why could not I, going up to bed with my basket of keys, stop to sit down by her fire and accommodate myself for a little while to her, at least as well as to anybody else, and not trouble myself about the harmless things she said to me? Impelled towards her, as I certainly was, for I was very anxious that she should like me and was very glad indeed that she did, why should I harp afterwards, with actual distress and pain, on every word she said and weigh it over and over again in twenty scales? Why was it so worrying to me to have her in our house, and confidential to me every night, when I yet felt that it was better and safer somehow that she should be there than anywhere else? These were perplexities and contradictions that I could not account for. At least, if I could—but I shall come to all that by and by, and it is mere idleness to go on about it now.\n\nSo when Mrs. Woodcourt went away, I was sorry to lose her but was relieved too. And then Caddy Jellyby came down, and Caddy brought such a packet of domestic news that it gave us abundant occupation.\n\nFirst Caddy declared (and would at first declare nothing else) that I was the best adviser that ever was known. This, my pet said, was no news at all; and this, I said, of course, was nonsense. Then Caddy told us that she was going to be married in a month and that if Ada and I would be her bridesmaids, she was the happiest girl in the world. To be sure, this was news indeed; and I thought we never should have done talking about it, we had so much to say to Caddy, and Caddy had so much to say to us.\n\nIt seemed that Caddy’s unfortunate papa had got over his bankruptcy—“gone through the Gazette,” was the expression Caddy used, as if it were a tunnel—with the general clemency and commiseration of his creditors, and had got rid of his affairs in some blessed manner without succeeding in understanding them, and had given up everything he possessed (which was not worth much, I should think, to judge from the state of the furniture), and had satisfied every one concerned that he could do no more, poor man. So, he had been honourably dismissed to “the office” to begin the world again. What he did at the office, I never knew; Caddy said he was a “custom-house and general agent,” and the only thing I ever understood about that business was that when he wanted money more than usual he went to the docks to look for it, and hardly ever found it.\n\nAs soon as her papa had tranquillized his mind by becoming this shorn lamb, and they had removed to a furnished lodging in Hatton Garden (where I found the children, when I afterwards went there, cutting the horse hair out of the seats of the chairs and choking themselves with it), Caddy had brought about a meeting between him and old Mr. Turveydrop; and poor Mr. Jellyby, being very humble and meek, had deferred to Mr. Turveydrop’s deportment so submissively that they had become excellent friends. By degrees, old Mr. Turveydrop, thus familiarized with the idea of his son’s marriage, had worked up his parental feelings to the height of contemplating that event as being near at hand and had given his gracious consent to the young couple commencing housekeeping at the academy in Newman Street when they would.\n\n“And your papa, Caddy. What did he say?”\n\n“Oh! Poor Pa,” said Caddy, “only cried and said he hoped we might get on better than he and Ma had got on. He didn’t say so before Prince, he only said so to me. And he said, ‘My poor girl, you have not been very well taught how to make a home for your husband, but unless you mean with all your heart to strive to do it, you had better murder him than marry him—if you really love him.’”\n\n“And how did you reassure him, Caddy?”\n\n“Why, it was very distressing, you know, to see poor Pa so low and hear him say such terrible things, and I couldn’t help crying myself. But I told him that I DID mean it with all my heart and that I hoped our house would be a place for him to come and find some comfort in of an evening and that I hoped and thought I could be a better daughter to him there than at home. Then I mentioned Peepy’s coming to stay with me, and then Pa began to cry again and said the children were Indians.”\n\n“Indians, Caddy?”\n\n“Yes,” said Caddy, “wild Indians. And Pa said”—here she began to sob, poor girl, not at all like the happiest girl in the world—“that he was sensible the best thing that could happen to them was their being all tomahawked together.”\n\nAda suggested that it was comfortable to know that Mr. Jellyby did not mean these destructive sentiments.\n\n“No, of course I know Pa wouldn’t like his family to be weltering in their blood,” said Caddy, “but he means that they are very unfortunate in being Ma’s children and that he is very unfortunate in being Ma’s husband; and I am sure that’s true, though it seems unnatural to say so.”\n\nI asked Caddy if Mrs. Jellyby knew that her wedding-day was fixed.\n\n“Oh! You know what Ma is, Esther,” she returned. “It’s impossible to say whether she knows it or not. She has been told it often enough; and when she IS told it, she only gives me a placid look, as if I was I don’t know what—a steeple in the distance,” said Caddy with a sudden idea; “and then she shakes her head and says ‘Oh, Caddy, Caddy, what a tease you are!’ and goes on with the Borrioboola letters.”\n\n“And about your wardrobe, Caddy?” said I. For she was under no restraint with us.\n\n“Well, my dear Esther,” she returned, drying her eyes, “I must do the best I can and trust to my dear Prince never to have an unkind remembrance of my coming so shabbily to him. If the question concerned an outfit for Borrioboola, Ma would know all about it and would be quite excited. Being what it is, she neither knows nor cares.”\n\nCaddy was not at all deficient in natural affection for her mother, but mentioned this with tears as an undeniable fact, which I am afraid it was. We were sorry for the poor dear girl and found so much to admire in the good disposition which had survived under such discouragement that we both at once (I mean Ada and I) proposed a little scheme that made her perfectly joyful. This was her staying with us for three weeks, my staying with her for one, and our all three contriving and cutting out, and repairing, and sewing, and saving, and doing the very best we could think of to make the most of her stock. My guardian being as pleased with the idea as Caddy was, we took her home next day to arrange the matter and brought her out again in triumph with her boxes and all the purchases that could be squeezed out of a ten-pound note, which Mr. Jellyby had found in the docks I suppose, but which he at all events gave her. What my guardian would not have given her if we had encouraged him, it would be difficult to say, but we thought it right to compound for no more than her wedding-dress and bonnet. He agreed to this compromise, and if Caddy had ever been happy in her life, she was happy when we sat down to work.\n\nShe was clumsy enough with her needle, poor girl, and pricked her fingers as much as she had been used to ink them. She could not help reddening a little now and then, partly with the smart and partly with vexation at being able to do no better, but she soon got over that and began to improve rapidly. So day after day she, and my darling, and my little maid Charley, and a milliner out of the town, and I, sat hard at work, as pleasantly as possible.\n\nOver and above this, Caddy was very anxious “to learn housekeeping,” as she said. Now, mercy upon us! The idea of her learning housekeeping of a person of my vast experience was such a joke that I laughed, and coloured up, and fell into a comical confusion when she proposed it. However, I said, “Caddy, I am sure you are very welcome to learn anything that you can learn of ME, my dear,” and I showed her all my books and methods and all my fidgety ways. You would have supposed that I was showing her some wonderful inventions, by her study of them; and if you had seen her, whenever I jingled my housekeeping keys, get up and attend me, certainly you might have thought that there never was a greater imposter than I with a blinder follower than Caddy Jellyby.\n\nSo what with working and housekeeping, and lessons to Charley, and backgammon in the evening with my guardian, and duets with Ada, the three weeks slipped fast away. Then I went home with Caddy to see what could be done there, and Ada and Charley remained behind to take care of my guardian.\n\nWhen I say I went home with Caddy, I mean to the furnished lodging in Hatton Garden. We went to Newman Street two or three times, where preparations were in progress too—a good many, I observed, for enhancing the comforts of old Mr. Turveydrop, and a few for putting the newly married couple away cheaply at the top of the house—but our great point was to make the furnished lodging decent for the wedding-breakfast and to imbue Mrs. Jellyby beforehand with some faint sense of the occasion.\n\nThe latter was the more difficult thing of the two because Mrs. Jellyby and an unwholesome boy occupied the front sitting-room (the back one was a mere closet), and it was littered down with waste-paper and Borrioboolan documents, as an untidy stable might be littered with straw. Mrs. Jellyby sat there all day drinking strong coffee, dictating, and holding Borrioboolan interviews by appointment. The unwholesome boy, who seemed to me to be going into a decline, took his meals out of the house. When Mr. Jellyby came home, he usually groaned and went down into the kitchen. There he got something to eat if the servant would give him anything, and then, feeling that he was in the way, went out and walked about Hatton Garden in the wet. The poor children scrambled up and tumbled down the house as they had always been accustomed to do.\n\nThe production of these devoted little sacrifices in any presentable condition being quite out of the question at a week’s notice, I proposed to Caddy that we should make them as happy as we could on her marriage morning in the attic where they all slept, and should confine our greatest efforts to her mama and her mama’s room, and a clean breakfast. In truth Mrs. Jellyby required a good deal of attention, the lattice-work up her back having widened considerably since I first knew her and her hair looking like the mane of a dustman’s horse.\n\nThinking that the display of Caddy’s wardrobe would be the best means of approaching the subject, I invited Mrs. Jellyby to come and look at it spread out on Caddy’s bed in the evening after the unwholesome boy was gone.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson,” said she, rising from her desk with her usual sweetness of temper, “these are really ridiculous preparations, though your assisting them is a proof of your kindness. There is something so inexpressibly absurd to me in the idea of Caddy being married! Oh, Caddy, you silly, silly, silly puss!”\n\nShe came upstairs with us notwithstanding and looked at the clothes in her customary far-off manner. They suggested one distinct idea to her, for she said with her placid smile, and shaking her head, “My good Miss Summerson, at half the cost, this weak child might have been equipped for Africa!”\n\nOn our going downstairs again, Mrs. Jellyby asked me whether this troublesome business was really to take place next Wednesday. And on my replying yes, she said, “Will my room be required, my dear Miss Summerson? For it’s quite impossible that I can put my papers away.”\n\nI took the liberty of saying that the room would certainly be wanted and that I thought we must put the papers away somewhere. “Well, my dear Miss Summerson,” said Mrs. Jellyby, “you know best, I dare say. But by obliging me to employ a boy, Caddy has embarrassed me to that extent, overwhelmed as I am with public business, that I don’t know which way to turn. We have a Ramification meeting, too, on Wednesday afternoon, and the inconvenience is very serious.”\n\n“It is not likely to occur again,” said I, smiling. “Caddy will be married but once, probably.”\n\n“That’s true,” Mrs. Jellyby replied; “that’s true, my dear. I suppose we must make the best of it!”\n\nThe next question was how Mrs. Jellyby should be dressed on the occasion. I thought it very curious to see her looking on serenely from her writing-table while Caddy and I discussed it, occasionally shaking her head at us with a half-reproachful smile like a superior spirit who could just bear with our trifling.\n\nThe state in which her dresses were, and the extraordinary confusion in which she kept them, added not a little to our difficulty; but at length we devised something not very unlike what a common-place mother might wear on such an occasion. The abstracted manner in which Mrs. Jellyby would deliver herself up to having this attire tried on by the dressmaker, and the sweetness with which she would then observe to me how sorry she was that I had not turned my thoughts to Africa, were consistent with the rest of her behaviour.\n\nThe lodging was rather confined as to space, but I fancied that if Mrs. Jellyby’s household had been the only lodgers in Saint Paul’s or Saint Peter’s, the sole advantage they would have found in the size of the building would have been its affording a great deal of room to be dirty in. I believe that nothing belonging to the family which it had been possible to break was unbroken at the time of those preparations for Caddy’s marriage, that nothing which it had been possible to spoil in any way was unspoilt, and that no domestic object which was capable of collecting dirt, from a dear child’s knee to the door-plate, was without as much dirt as could well accumulate upon it.\n\nPoor Mr. Jellyby, who very seldom spoke and almost always sat when he was at home with his head against the wall, became interested when he saw that Caddy and I were attempting to establish some order among all this waste and ruin and took off his coat to help. But such wonderful things came tumbling out of the closets when they were opened—bits of mouldy pie, sour bottles, Mrs. Jellyby’s caps, letters, tea, forks, odd boots and shoes of children, firewood, wafers, saucepan-lids, damp sugar in odds and ends of paper bags, footstools, blacklead brushes, bread, Mrs. Jellyby’s bonnets, books with butter sticking to the binding, guttered candle ends put out by being turned upside down in broken candlesticks, nutshells, heads and tails of shrimps, dinner-mats, gloves, coffee-grounds, umbrellas—that he looked frightened, and left off again. But he came regularly every evening and sat without his coat, with his head against the wall, as though he would have helped us if he had known how.\n\n“Poor Pa!” said Caddy to me on the night before the great day, when we really had got things a little to rights. “It seems unkind to leave him, Esther. But what could I do if I stayed! Since I first knew you, I have tidied and tidied over and over again, but it’s useless. Ma and Africa, together, upset the whole house directly. We never have a servant who don’t drink. Ma’s ruinous to everything.”\n\nMr. Jellyby could not hear what she said, but he seemed very low indeed and shed tears, I thought.\n\n“My heart aches for him; that it does!” sobbed Caddy. “I can’t help thinking to-night, Esther, how dearly I hope to be happy with Prince, and how dearly Pa hoped, I dare say, to be happy with Ma. What a disappointed life!”\n\n“My dear Caddy!” said Mr. Jellyby, looking slowly round from the wail. It was the first time, I think, I ever heard him say three words together.\n\n“Yes, Pa!” cried Caddy, going to him and embracing him affectionately.\n\n“My dear Caddy,” said Mr. Jellyby. “Never have—”\n\n“Not Prince, Pa?” faltered Caddy. “Not have Prince?”\n\n“Yes, my dear,” said Mr. Jellyby. “Have him, certainly. But, never have—”\n\nI mentioned in my account of our first visit in Thavies Inn that Richard described Mr. Jellyby as frequently opening his mouth after dinner without saying anything. It was a habit of his. He opened his mouth now a great many times and shook his head in a melancholy manner.\n\n“What do you wish me not to have? Don’t have what, dear Pa?” asked Caddy, coaxing him, with her arms round his neck.\n\n“Never have a mission, my dear child.”\n\nMr. Jellyby groaned and laid his head against the wall again, and this was the only time I ever heard him make any approach to expressing his sentiments on the Borrioboolan question. I suppose he had been more talkative and lively once, but he seemed to have been completely exhausted long before I knew him.\n\nI thought Mrs. Jellyby never would have left off serenely looking over her papers and drinking coffee that night. It was twelve o’clock before we could obtain possession of the room, and the clearance it required then was so discouraging that Caddy, who was almost tired out, sat down in the middle of the dust and cried. But she soon cheered up, and we did wonders with it before we went to bed.\n\nIn the morning it looked, by the aid of a few flowers and a quantity of soap and water and a little arrangement, quite gay. The plain breakfast made a cheerful show, and Caddy was perfectly charming. But when my darling came, I thought—and I think now—that I never had seen such a dear face as my beautiful pet’s.\n\nWe made a little feast for the children upstairs, and we put Peepy at the head of the table, and we showed them Caddy in her bridal dress, and they clapped their hands and hurrahed, and Caddy cried to think that she was going away from them and hugged them over and over again until we brought Prince up to fetch her away—when, I am sorry to say, Peepy bit him. Then there was old Mr. Turveydrop downstairs, in a state of deportment not to be expressed, benignly blessing Caddy and giving my guardian to understand that his son’s happiness was his own parental work and that he sacrificed personal considerations to ensure it. “My dear sir,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “these young people will live with me; my house is large enough for their accommodation, and they shall not want the shelter of my roof. I could have wished—you will understand the allusion, Mr. Jarndyce, for you remember my illustrious patron the Prince Regent—I could have wished that my son had married into a family where there was more deportment, but the will of heaven be done!”\n\nMr. and Mrs. Pardiggle were of the party—Mr. Pardiggle, an obstinate-looking man with a large waistcoat and stubbly hair, who was always talking in a loud bass voice about his mite, or Mrs. Pardiggle’s mite, or their five boys’ mites. Mr. Quale, with his hair brushed back as usual and his knobs of temples shining very much, was also there, not in the character of a disappointed lover, but as the accepted of a young—at least, an unmarried—lady, a Miss Wisk, who was also there. Miss Wisk’s mission, my guardian said, was to show the world that woman’s mission was man’s mission and that the only genuine mission of both man and woman was to be always moving declaratory resolutions about things in general at public meetings. The guests were few, but were, as one might expect at Mrs. Jellyby’s, all devoted to public objects only. Besides those I have mentioned, there was an extremely dirty lady with her bonnet all awry and the ticketed price of her dress still sticking on it, whose neglected home, Caddy told me, was like a filthy wilderness, but whose church was like a fancy fair. A very contentious gentleman, who said it was his mission to be everybody’s brother but who appeared to be on terms of coolness with the whole of his large family, completed the party.\n\nA party, having less in common with such an occasion, could hardly have been got together by any ingenuity. Such a mean mission as the domestic mission was the very last thing to be endured among them; indeed, Miss Wisk informed us, with great indignation, before we sat down to breakfast, that the idea of woman’s mission lying chiefly in the narrow sphere of home was an outrageous slander on the part of her tyrant, man. One other singularity was that nobody with a mission—except Mr. Quale, whose mission, as I think I have formerly said, was to be in ecstasies with everybody’s mission—cared at all for anybody’s mission. Mrs. Pardiggle being as clear that the only one infallible course was her course of pouncing upon the poor and applying benevolence to them like a strait-waistcoat; as Miss Wisk was that the only practical thing for the world was the emancipation of woman from the thraldom of her tyrant, man. Mrs. Jellyby, all the while, sat smiling at the limited vision that could see anything but Borrioboola-Gha.\n\nBut I am anticipating now the purport of our conversation on the ride home instead of first marrying Caddy. We all went to church, and Mr. Jellyby gave her away. Of the air with which old Mr. Turveydrop, with his hat under his left arm (the inside presented at the clergyman like a cannon) and his eyes creasing themselves up into his wig, stood stiff and high-shouldered behind us bridesmaids during the ceremony, and afterwards saluted us, I could never say enough to do it justice. Miss Wisk, whom I cannot report as prepossessing in appearance, and whose manner was grim, listened to the proceedings, as part of woman’s wrongs, with a disdainful face. Mrs. Jellyby, with her calm smile and her bright eyes, looked the least concerned of all the company.\n\nWe duly came back to breakfast, and Mrs. Jellyby sat at the head of the table and Mr. Jellyby at the foot. Caddy had previously stolen upstairs to hug the children again and tell them that her name was Turveydrop. But this piece of information, instead of being an agreeable surprise to Peepy, threw him on his back in such transports of kicking grief that I could do nothing on being sent for but accede to the proposal that he should be admitted to the breakfast table. So he came down and sat in my lap; and Mrs. Jellyby, after saying, in reference to the state of his pinafore, “Oh, you naughty Peepy, what a shocking little pig you are!” was not at all discomposed. He was very good except that he brought down Noah with him (out of an ark I had given him before we went to church) and WOULD dip him head first into the wine-glasses and then put him in his mouth.\n\nMy guardian, with his sweet temper and his quick perception and his amiable face, made something agreeable even out of the ungenial company. None of them seemed able to talk about anything but his, or her, own one subject, and none of them seemed able to talk about even that as part of a world in which there was anything else; but my guardian turned it all to the merry encouragement of Caddy and the honour of the occasion, and brought us through the breakfast nobly. What we should have done without him, I am afraid to think, for all the company despising the bride and bridegroom and old Mr. Turveydrop—and old Mr. Thurveydrop, in virtue of his deportment, considering himself vastly superior to all the company—it was a very unpromising case.\n\nAt last the time came when poor Caddy was to go and when all her property was packed on the hired coach and pair that was to take her and her husband to Gravesend. It affected us to see Caddy clinging, then, to her deplorable home and hanging on her mother’s neck with the greatest tenderness.\n\n“I am very sorry I couldn’t go on writing from dictation, Ma,” sobbed Caddy. “I hope you forgive me now.”\n\n“Oh, Caddy, Caddy!” said Mrs. Jellyby. “I have told you over and over again that I have engaged a boy, and there’s an end of it.”\n\n“You are sure you are not the least angry with me, Ma? Say you are sure before I go away, Ma?”\n\n“You foolish Caddy,” returned Mrs. Jellyby, “do I look angry, or have I inclination to be angry, or time to be angry? How CAN you?”\n\n“Take a little care of Pa while I am gone, Mama!”\n\nMrs. Jellyby positively laughed at the fancy. “You romantic child,” said she, lightly patting Caddy’s back. “Go along. I am excellent friends with you. Now, good-bye, Caddy, and be very happy!”\n\nThen Caddy hung upon her father and nursed his cheek against hers as if he were some poor dull child in pain. All this took place in the hall. Her father released her, took out his pocket handkerchief, and sat down on the stairs with his head against the wall. I hope he found some consolation in walls. I almost think he did.\n\nAnd then Prince took her arm in his and turned with great emotion and respect to his father, whose deportment at that moment was overwhelming.\n\n“Thank you over and over again, father!” said Prince, kissing his hand. “I am very grateful for all your kindness and consideration regarding our marriage, and so, I can assure you, is Caddy.”\n\n“Very,” sobbed Caddy. “Ve-ry!”\n\n“My dear son,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “and dear daughter, I have done my duty. If the spirit of a sainted wooman hovers above us and looks down on the occasion, that, and your constant affection, will be my recompense. You will not fail in YOUR duty, my son and daughter, I believe?”\n\n“Dear father, never!” cried Prince.\n\n“Never, never, dear Mr. Turveydrop!” said Caddy.\n\n“This,” returned Mr. Turveydrop, “is as it should be. My children, my home is yours, my heart is yours, my all is yours. I will never leave you; nothing but death shall part us. My dear son, you contemplate an absence of a week, I think?”\n\n“A week, dear father. We shall return home this day week.”\n\n“My dear child,” said Mr. Turveydrop, “let me, even under the present exceptional circumstances, recommend strict punctuality. It is highly important to keep the connexion together; and schools, if at all neglected, are apt to take offence.”\n\n“This day week, father, we shall be sure to be home to dinner.”\n\n“Good!” said Mr. Turveydrop. “You will find fires, my dear Caroline, in your own room, and dinner prepared in my apartment. Yes, yes, Prince!” anticipating some self-denying objection on his son’s part with a great air. “You and our Caroline will be strange in the upper part of the premises and will, therefore, dine that day in my apartment. Now, bless ye!”\n\nThey drove away, and whether I wondered most at Mrs. Jellyby or at Mr. Turveydrop, I did not know. Ada and my guardian were in the same condition when we came to talk it over. But before we drove away too, I received a most unexpected and eloquent compliment from Mr. Jellyby. He came up to me in the hall, took both my hands, pressed them earnestly, and opened his mouth twice. I was so sure of his meaning that I said, quite flurried, “You are very welcome, sir. Pray don’t mention it!”\n\n“I hope this marriage is for the best, guardian,” said I when we three were on our road home.\n\n“I hope it is, little woman. Patience. We shall see.”\n\n“Is the wind in the east to-day?” I ventured to ask him.\n\nHe laughed heartily and answered, “No.”\n\n“But it must have been this morning, I think,” said I.\n\nHe answered “No” again, and this time my dear girl confidently answered “No” too and shook the lovely head which, with its blooming flowers against the golden hair, was like the very spring. “Much YOU know of east winds, my ugly darling,” said I, kissing her in my admiration—I couldn’t help it.\n\nWell! It was only their love for me, I know very well, and it is a long time ago. I must write it even if I rub it out again, because it gives me so much pleasure. They said there could be no east wind where Somebody was; they said that wherever Dame Durden went, there was sunshine and summer air.\n\nNurse and Patient\nI had not been at home again many days when one evening I went upstairs into my own room to take a peep over Charley’s shoulder and see how she was getting on with her copy-book. Writing was a trying business to Charley, who seemed to have no natural power over a pen, but in whose hand every pen appeared to become perversely animated, and to go wrong and crooked, and to stop, and splash, and sidle into corners like a saddle-donkey. It was very odd to see what old letters Charley’s young hand had made, they so wrinkled, and shrivelled, and tottering, it so plump and round. Yet Charley was uncommonly expert at other things and had as nimble little fingers as I ever watched.\n\n“Well, Charley,” said I, looking over a copy of the letter O in which it was represented as square, triangular, pear-shaped, and collapsed in all kinds of ways, “we are improving. If we only get to make it round, we shall be perfect, Charley.”\n\nThen I made one, and Charley made one, and the pen wouldn’t join Charley’s neatly, but twisted it up into a knot.\n\n“Never mind, Charley. We shall do it in time.”\n\nCharley laid down her pen, the copy being finished, opened and shut her cramped little hand, looked gravely at the page, half in pride and half in doubt, and got up, and dropped me a curtsy.\n\n“Thank you, miss. If you please, miss, did you know a poor person of the name of Jenny?”\n\n“A brickmaker’s wife, Charley? Yes.”\n\n“She came and spoke to me when I was out a little while ago, and said you knew her, miss. She asked me if I wasn’t the young lady’s little maid—meaning you for the young lady, miss—and I said yes, miss.”\n\n“I thought she had left this neighbourhood altogether, Charley.”\n\n“So she had, miss, but she’s come back again to where she used to live—she and Liz. Did you know another poor person of the name of Liz, miss?”\n\n“I think I do, Charley, though not by name.”\n\n“That’s what she said!” returned Charley. “They have both come back, miss, and have been tramping high and low.”\n\n“Tramping high and low, have they, Charley?”\n\n“Yes, miss.” If Charley could only have made the letters in her copy as round as the eyes with which she looked into my face, they would have been excellent. “And this poor person came about the house three or four days, hoping to get a glimpse of you, miss—all she wanted, she said—but you were away. That was when she saw me. She saw me a-going about, miss,” said Charley with a short laugh of the greatest delight and pride, “and she thought I looked like your maid!”\n\n“Did she though, really, Charley?”\n\n“Yes, miss!” said Charley. “Really and truly.” And Charley, with another short laugh of the purest glee, made her eyes very round again and looked as serious as became my maid. I was never tired of seeing Charley in the full enjoyment of that great dignity, standing before me with her youthful face and figure, and her steady manner, and her childish exultation breaking through it now and then in the pleasantest way.\n\n“And where did you see her, Charley?” said I.\n\nMy little maid’s countenance fell as she replied, “By the doctor’s shop, miss.” For Charley wore her black frock yet.\n\nI asked if the brickmaker’s wife were ill, but Charley said no. It was some one else. Some one in her cottage who had tramped down to Saint Albans and was tramping he didn’t know where. A poor boy, Charley said. No father, no mother, no any one. “Like as Tom might have been, miss, if Emma and me had died after father,” said Charley, her round eyes filling with tears.\n\n“And she was getting medicine for him, Charley?”\n\n“She said, miss,” returned Charley, “how that he had once done as much for her.”\n\nMy little maid’s face was so eager and her quiet hands were folded so closely in one another as she stood looking at me that I had no great difficulty in reading her thoughts. “Well, Charley,” said I, “it appears to me that you and I can do no better than go round to Jenny’s and see what’s the matter.”\n\nThe alacrity with which Charley brought my bonnet and veil, and having dressed me, quaintly pinned herself into her warm shawl and made herself look like a little old woman, sufficiently expressed her readiness. So Charley and I, without saying anything to any one, went out.\n\nIt was a cold, wild night, and the trees shuddered in the wind. The rain had been thick and heavy all day, and with little intermission for many days. None was falling just then, however. The sky had partly cleared, but was very gloomy—even above us, where a few stars were shining. In the north and north-west, where the sun had set three hours before, there was a pale dead light both beautiful and awful; and into it long sullen lines of cloud waved up like a sea stricken immovable as it was heaving. Towards London a lurid glare overhung the whole dark waste, and the contrast between these two lights, and the fancy which the redder light engendered of an unearthly fire, gleaming on all the unseen buildings of the city and on all the faces of its many thousands of wondering inhabitants, was as solemn as might be.\n\nI had no thought that night—none, I am quite sure—of what was soon to happen to me. But I have always remembered since that when we had stopped at the garden-gate to look up at the sky, and when we went upon our way, I had for a moment an undefinable impression of myself as being something different from what I then was. I know it was then and there that I had it. I have ever since connected the feeling with that spot and time and with everything associated with that spot and time, to the distant voices in the town, the barking of a dog, and the sound of wheels coming down the miry hill.\n\nIt was Saturday night, and most of the people belonging to the place where we were going were drinking elsewhere. We found it quieter than I had previously seen it, though quite as miserable. The kilns were burning, and a stifling vapour set towards us with a pale-blue glare.\n\nWe came to the cottage, where there was a feeble candle in the patched window. We tapped at the door and went in. The mother of the little child who had died was sitting in a chair on one side of the poor fire by the bed; and opposite to her, a wretched boy, supported by the chimney-piece, was cowering on the floor. He held under his arm, like a little bundle, a fragment of a fur cap; and as he tried to warm himself, he shook until the crazy door and window shook. The place was closer than before and had an unhealthy and a very peculiar smell.\n\nI had not lifted my veil when I first spoke to the woman, which was at the moment of our going in. The boy staggered up instantly and stared at me with a remarkable expression of surprise and terror.\n\nHis action was so quick and my being the cause of it was so evident that I stood still instead of advancing nearer.\n\n“I won’t go no more to the berryin ground,” muttered the boy; “I ain’t a-going there, so I tell you!”\n\nI lifted my veil and spoke to the woman. She said to me in a low voice, “Don’t mind him, ma’am. He’ll soon come back to his head,” and said to him, “Jo, Jo, what’s the matter?”\n\n“I know wot she’s come for!” cried the boy.\n\n“Who?”\n\n“The lady there. She’s come to get me to go along with her to the berryin ground. I won’t go to the berryin ground. I don’t like the name on it. She might go a-berryin ME.” His shivering came on again, and as he leaned against the wall, he shook the hovel.\n\n“He has been talking off and on about such like all day, ma’am,” said Jenny softly. “Why, how you stare! This is MY lady, Jo.”\n\n“Is it?” returned the boy doubtfully, and surveying me with his arm held out above his burning eyes. “She looks to me the t’other one. It ain’t the bonnet, nor yet it ain’t the gownd, but she looks to me the t’other one.”\n\nMy little Charley, with her premature experience of illness and trouble, had pulled off her bonnet and shawl and now went quietly up to him with a chair and sat him down in it like an old sick nurse. Except that no such attendant could have shown him Charley’s youthful face, which seemed to engage his confidence.\n\n“I say!” said the boy. “YOU tell me. Ain’t the lady the t’other lady?”\n\nCharley shook her head as she methodically drew his rags about him and made him as warm as she could.\n\n“Oh!” the boy muttered. “Then I s’pose she ain’t.”\n\n“I came to see if I could do you any good,” said I. “What is the matter with you?”\n\n“I’m a-being froze,” returned the boy hoarsely, with his haggard gaze wandering about me, “and then burnt up, and then froze, and then burnt up, ever so many times in a hour. And my head’s all sleepy, and all a-going mad-like—and I’m so dry—and my bones isn’t half so much bones as pain.”\n\n“When did he come here?” I asked the woman.\n\n“This morning, ma’am, I found him at the corner of the town. I had known him up in London yonder. Hadn’t I, Jo?”\n\n“Tom-all-Alone’s,” the boy replied.\n\nWhenever he fixed his attention or his eyes, it was only for a very little while. He soon began to droop his head again, and roll it heavily, and speak as if he were half awake.\n\n“When did he come from London?” I asked.\n\n“I come from London yes’day,” said the boy himself, now flushed and hot. “I’m a-going somewheres.”\n\n“Where is he going?” I asked.\n\n“Somewheres,” repeated the boy in a louder tone. “I have been moved on, and moved on, more nor ever I was afore, since the t’other one give me the sov’ring. Mrs. Snagsby, she’s always a-watching, and a-driving of me—what have I done to her?—and they’re all a-watching and a-driving of me. Every one of ’em’s doing of it, from the time when I don’t get up, to the time when I don’t go to bed. And I’m a-going somewheres. That’s where I’m a-going. She told me, down in Tom-all-Alone’s, as she came from Stolbuns, and so I took the Stolbuns Road. It’s as good as another.”\n\nHe always concluded by addressing Charley.\n\n“What is to be done with him?” said I, taking the woman aside. “He could not travel in this state even if he had a purpose and knew where he was going!”\n\n“I know no more, ma’am, than the dead,” she replied, glancing compassionately at him. “Perhaps the dead know better, if they could only tell us. I’ve kept him here all day for pity’s sake, and I’ve given him broth and physic, and Liz has gone to try if any one will take him in (here’s my pretty in the bed—her child, but I call it mine); but I can’t keep him long, for if my husband was to come home and find him here, he’d be rough in putting him out and might do him a hurt. Hark! Here comes Liz back!”\n\nThe other woman came hurriedly in as she spoke, and the boy got up with a half-obscured sense that he was expected to be going. When the little child awoke, and when and how Charley got at it, took it out of bed, and began to walk about hushing it, I don’t know. There she was, doing all this in a quiet motherly manner as if she were living in Mrs. Blinder’s attic with Tom and Emma again.\n\nThe friend had been here and there, and had been played about from hand to hand, and had come back as she went. At first it was too early for the boy to be received into the proper refuge, and at last it was too late. One official sent her to another, and the other sent her back again to the first, and so backward and forward, until it appeared to me as if both must have been appointed for their skill in evading their duties instead of performing them. And now, after all, she said, breathing quickly, for she had been running and was frightened too, “Jenny, your master’s on the road home, and mine’s not far behind, and the Lord help the boy, for we can do no more for him!” They put a few halfpence together and hurried them into his hand, and so, in an oblivious, half-thankful, half-insensible way, he shuffled out of the house.\n\n“Give me the child, my dear,” said its mother to Charley, “and thank you kindly too! Jenny, woman dear, good night! Young lady, if my master don’t fall out with me, I’ll look down by the kiln by and by, where the boy will be most like, and again in the morning!” She hurried off, and presently we passed her hushing and singing to her child at her own door and looking anxiously along the road for her drunken husband.\n\nI was afraid of staying then to speak to either woman, lest I should bring her into trouble. But I said to Charley that we must not leave the boy to die. Charley, who knew what to do much better than I did, and whose quickness equalled her presence of mind, glided on before me, and presently we came up with Jo, just short of the brick-kiln.\n\nI think he must have begun his journey with some small bundle under his arm and must have had it stolen or lost it. For he still carried his wretched fragment of fur cap like a bundle, though he went bare-headed through the rain, which now fell fast. He stopped when we called to him and again showed a dread of me when I came up, standing with his lustrous eyes fixed upon me, and even arrested in his shivering fit.\n\nI asked him to come with us, and we would take care that he had some shelter for the night.\n\n“I don’t want no shelter,” he said; “I can lay amongst the warm bricks.”\n\n“But don’t you know that people die there?” replied Charley.\n\n“They dies everywheres,” said the boy. “They dies in their lodgings—she knows where; I showed her—and they dies down in Tom-all-Alone’s in heaps. They dies more than they lives, according to what I see.” Then he hoarsely whispered Charley, “If she ain’t the t’other one, she ain’t the forrenner. Is there THREE of ’em then?”\n\nCharley looked at me a little frightened. I felt half frightened at myself when the boy glared on me so.\n\nBut he turned and followed when I beckoned to him, and finding that he acknowledged that influence in me, I led the way straight home. It was not far, only at the summit of the hill. We passed but one man. I doubted if we should have got home without assistance, the boy’s steps were so uncertain and tremulous. He made no complaint, however, and was strangely unconcerned about himself, if I may say so strange a thing.\n\nLeaving him in the hall for a moment, shrunk into the corner of the window-seat and staring with an indifference that scarcely could be called wonder at the comfort and brightness about him, I went into the drawing-room to speak to my guardian. There I found Mr. Skimpole, who had come down by the coach, as he frequently did without notice, and never bringing any clothes with him, but always borrowing everything he wanted.\n\nThey came out with me directly to look at the boy. The servants had gathered in the hall too, and he shivered in the window-seat with Charley standing by him, like some wounded animal that had been found in a ditch.\n\n“This is a sorrowful case,” said my guardian after asking him a question or two and touching him and examining his eyes. “What do you say, Harold?”\n\n“You had better turn him out,” said Mr. Skimpole.\n\n“What do you mean?” inquired my guardian, almost sternly.\n\n“My dear Jarndyce,” said Mr. Skimpole, “you know what I am: I am a child. Be cross to me if I deserve it. But I have a constitutional objection to this sort of thing. I always had, when I was a medical man. He’s not safe, you know. There’s a very bad sort of fever about him.”\n\nMr. Skimpole had retreated from the hall to the drawing-room again and said this in his airy way, seated on the music-stool as we stood by.\n\n“You’ll say it’s childish,” observed Mr. Skimpole, looking gaily at us. “Well, I dare say it may be; but I AM a child, and I never pretend to be anything else. If you put him out in the road, you only put him where he was before. He will be no worse off than he was, you know. Even make him better off, if you like. Give him sixpence, or five shillings, or five pound ten—you are arithmeticians, and I am not—and get rid of him!”\n\n“And what is he to do then?” asked my guardian.\n\n“Upon my life,” said Mr. Skimpole, shrugging his shoulders with his engaging smile, “I have not the least idea what he is to do then. But I have no doubt he’ll do it.”\n\n“Now, is it not a horrible reflection,” said my guardian, to whom I had hastily explained the unavailing efforts of the two women, “is it not a horrible reflection,” walking up and down and rumpling his hair, “that if this wretched creature were a convicted prisoner, his hospital would be wide open to him, and he would be as well taken care of as any sick boy in the kingdom?”\n\n“My dear Jarndyce,” returned Mr. Skimpole, “you’ll pardon the simplicity of the question, coming as it does from a creature who is perfectly simple in worldly matters, but why ISN’T he a prisoner then?”\n\nMy guardian stopped and looked at him with a whimsical mixture of amusement and indignation in his face.\n\n“Our young friend is not to be suspected of any delicacy, I should imagine,” said Mr. Skimpole, unabashed and candid. “It seems to me that it would be wiser, as well as in a certain kind of way more respectable, if he showed some misdirected energy that got him into prison. There would be more of an adventurous spirit in it, and consequently more of a certain sort of poetry.”\n\n“I believe,” returned my guardian, resuming his uneasy walk, “that there is not such another child on earth as yourself.”\n\n“Do you really?” said Mr. Skimpole. “I dare say! But I confess I don’t see why our young friend, in his degree, should not seek to invest himself with such poetry as is open to him. He is no doubt born with an appetite—probably, when he is in a safer state of health, he has an excellent appetite. Very well. At our young friend’s natural dinner hour, most likely about noon, our young friend says in effect to society, ‘I am hungry; will you have the goodness to produce your spoon and feed me?’ Society, which has taken upon itself the general arrangement of the whole system of spoons and professes to have a spoon for our young friend, does NOT produce that spoon; and our young friend, therefore, says ‘You really must excuse me if I seize it.’ Now, this appears to me a case of misdirected energy, which has a certain amount of reason in it and a certain amount of romance; and I don’t know but what I should be more interested in our young friend, as an illustration of such a case, than merely as a poor vagabond—which any one can be.”\n\n“In the meantime,” I ventured to observe, “he is getting worse.”\n\n“In the meantime,” said Mr. Skimpole cheerfully, “as Miss Summerson, with her practical good sense, observes, he is getting worse. Therefore I recommend your turning him out before he gets still worse.”\n\nThe amiable face with which he said it, I think I shall never forget.\n\n“Of course, little woman,” observed my guardian, turning to me, “I can ensure his admission into the proper place by merely going there to enforce it, though it’s a bad state of things when, in his condition, that is necessary. But it’s growing late, and is a very bad night, and the boy is worn out already. There is a bed in the wholesome loft-room by the stable; we had better keep him there till morning, when he can be wrapped up and removed. We’ll do that.”\n\n“Oh!” said Mr. Skimpole, with his hands upon the keys of the piano as we moved away. “Are you going back to our young friend?”\n\n“Yes,” said my guardian.\n\n“How I envy you your constitution, Jarndyce!” returned Mr. Skimpole with playful admiration. “You don’t mind these things; neither does Miss Summerson. You are ready at all times to go anywhere, and do anything. Such is will! I have no will at all—and no won’t—simply can’t.”\n\n“You can’t recommend anything for the boy, I suppose?” said my guardian, looking back over his shoulder half angrily; only half angrily, for he never seemed to consider Mr. Skimpole an accountable being.\n\n“My dear Jarndyce, I observed a bottle of cooling medicine in his pocket, and it’s impossible for him to do better than take it. You can tell them to sprinkle a little vinegar about the place where he sleeps and to keep it moderately cool and him moderately warm. But it is mere impertinence in me to offer any recommendation. Miss Summerson has such a knowledge of detail and such a capacity for the administration of detail that she knows all about it.”\n\nWe went back into the hall and explained to Jo what we proposed to do, which Charley explained to him again and which he received with the languid unconcern I had already noticed, wearily looking on at what was done as if it were for somebody else. The servants compassionating his miserable state and being very anxious to help, we soon got the loft-room ready; and some of the men about the house carried him across the wet yard, well wrapped up. It was pleasant to observe how kind they were to him and how there appeared to be a general impression among them that frequently calling him “Old Chap” was likely to revive his spirits. Charley directed the operations and went to and fro between the loft-room and the house with such little stimulants and comforts as we thought it safe to give him. My guardian himself saw him before he was left for the night and reported to me when he returned to the growlery to write a letter on the boy’s behalf, which a messenger was charged to deliver at day-light in the morning, that he seemed easier and inclined to sleep. They had fastened his door on the outside, he said, in case of his being delirious, but had so arranged that he could not make any noise without being heard.\n\nAda being in our room with a cold, Mr. Skimpole was left alone all this time and entertained himself by playing snatches of pathetic airs and sometimes singing to them (as we heard at a distance) with great expression and feeling. When we rejoined him in the drawing-room he said he would give us a little ballad which had come into his head “apropos of our young friend,” and he sang one about a peasant boy,\n\n“Thrown on the wide world, doomed to wander and roam, Bereft of his parents, bereft of a home.”\n\nquite exquisitely. It was a song that always made him cry, he told us.\n\nHe was extremely gay all the rest of the evening, for he absolutely chirped—those were his delighted words—when he thought by what a happy talent for business he was surrounded. He gave us, in his glass of negus, “Better health to our young friend!” and supposed and gaily pursued the case of his being reserved like Whittington to become Lord Mayor of London. In that event, no doubt, he would establish the Jarndyce Institution and the Summerson Almshouses, and a little annual Corporation Pilgrimage to St. Albans. He had no doubt, he said, that our young friend was an excellent boy in his way, but his way was not the Harold Skimpole way; what Harold Skimpole was, Harold Skimpole had found himself, to his considerable surprise, when he first made his own acquaintance; he had accepted himself with all his failings and had thought it sound philosophy to make the best of the bargain; and he hoped we would do the same.\n\nCharley’s last report was that the boy was quiet. I could see, from my window, the lantern they had left him burning quietly; and I went to bed very happy to think that he was sheltered.\n\nThere was more movement and more talking than usual a little before daybreak, and it awoke me. As I was dressing, I looked out of my window and asked one of our men who had been among the active sympathizers last night whether there was anything wrong about the house. The lantern was still burning in the loft-window.\n\n“It’s the boy, miss,” said he.\n\n“Is he worse?” I inquired.\n\n“Gone, miss.”\n\n“Dead!”\n“Dead, miss? No. Gone clean off.”\n\nAt what time of the night he had gone, or how, or why, it seemed hopeless ever to divine. The door remaining as it had been left, and the lantern standing in the window, it could only be supposed that he had got out by a trap in the floor which communicated with an empty cart-house below. But he had shut it down again, if that were so; and it looked as if it had not been raised. Nothing of any kind was missing. On this fact being clearly ascertained, we all yielded to the painful belief that delirium had come upon him in the night and that, allured by some imaginary object or pursued by some imaginary horror, he had strayed away in that worse than helpless state; all of us, that is to say, but Mr. Skimpole, who repeatedly suggested, in his usual easy light style, that it had occurred to our young friend that he was not a safe inmate, having a bad kind of fever upon him, and that he had with great natural politeness taken himself off.\n\nEvery possible inquiry was made, and every place was searched. The brick-kilns were examined, the cottages were visited, the two women were particularly questioned, but they knew nothing of him, and nobody could doubt that their wonder was genuine. The weather had for some time been too wet and the night itself had been too wet to admit of any tracing by footsteps. Hedge and ditch, and wall, and rick and stack, were examined by our men for a long distance round, lest the boy should be lying in such a place insensible or dead; but nothing was seen to indicate that he had ever been near. From the time when he was left in the loft-room, he vanished.\n\nThe search continued for five days. I do not mean that it ceased even then, but that my attention was then diverted into a current very memorable to me.\n\nAs Charley was at her writing again in my room in the evening, and as I sat opposite to her at work, I felt the table tremble. Looking up, I saw my little maid shivering from head to foot.\n\n“Charley,” said I, “are you so cold?”\n\n“I think I am, miss,” she replied. “I don’t know what it is. I can’t hold myself still. I felt so yesterday at about this same time, miss. Don’t be uneasy, I think I’m ill.”\n\nI heard Ada’s voice outside, and I hurried to the door of communication between my room and our pretty sitting-room, and locked it. Just in time, for she tapped at it while my hand was yet upon the key.\n\nAda called to me to let her in, but I said, “Not now, my dearest. Go away. There’s nothing the matter; I will come to you presently.” Ah! It was a long, long time before my darling girl and I were companions again.\n\nCharley fell ill. In twelve hours she was very ill. I moved her to my room, and laid her in my bed, and sat down quietly to nurse her. I told my guardian all about it, and why I felt it was necessary that I should seclude myself, and my reason for not seeing my darling above all. At first she came very often to the door, and called to me, and even reproached me with sobs and tears; but I wrote her a long letter saying that she made me anxious and unhappy and imploring her, as she loved me and wished my mind to be at peace, to come no nearer than the garden. After that she came beneath the window even oftener than she had come to the door, and if I had learnt to love her dear sweet voice before when we were hardly ever apart, how did I learn to love it then, when I stood behind the window-curtain listening and replying, but not so much as looking out! How did I learn to love it afterwards, when the harder time came!\n\nThey put a bed for me in our sitting-room; and by keeping the door wide open, I turned the two rooms into one, now that Ada had vacated that part of the house, and kept them always fresh and airy. There was not a servant in or about the house but was so good that they would all most gladly have come to me at any hour of the day or night without the least fear or unwillingness, but I thought it best to choose one worthy woman who was never to see Ada and whom I could trust to come and go with all precaution. Through her means I got out to take the air with my guardian when there was no fear of meeting Ada, and wanted for nothing in the way of attendance, any more than in any other respect.\n\nAnd thus poor Charley sickened and grew worse, and fell into heavy danger of death, and lay severely ill for many a long round of day and night. So patient she was, so uncomplaining, and inspired by such a gentle fortitude that very often as I sat by Charley holding her head in my arms—repose would come to her, so, when it would come to her in no other attitude—I silently prayed to our Father in heaven that I might not forget the lesson which this little sister taught me.\n\nI was very sorrowful to think that Charley’s pretty looks would change and be disfigured, even if she recovered—she was such a child with her dimpled face—but that thought was, for the greater part, lost in her greater peril. When she was at the worst, and her mind rambled again to the cares of her father’s sick bed and the little children, she still knew me so far as that she would be quiet in my arms when she could lie quiet nowhere else, and murmur out the wanderings of her mind less restlessly. At those times I used to think, how should I ever tell the two remaining babies that the baby who had learned of her faithful heart to be a mother to them in their need was dead!\n\nThere were other times when Charley knew me well and talked to me, telling me that she sent her love to Tom and Emma and that she was sure Tom would grow up to be a good man. At those times Charley would speak to me of what she had read to her father as well as she could to comfort him, of that young man carried out to be buried who was the only son of his mother and she was a widow, of the ruler’s daughter raised up by the gracious hand upon her bed of death. And Charley told me that when her father died she had kneeled down and prayed in her first sorrow that he likewise might be raised up and given back to his poor children, and that if she should never get better and should die too, she thought it likely that it might come into Tom’s mind to offer the same prayer for her. Then would I show Tom how these people of old days had been brought back to life on earth, only that we might know our hope to be restored to heaven!\n\nBut of all the various times there were in Charley’s illness, there was not one when she lost the gentle qualities I have spoken of. And there were many, many when I thought in the night of the last high belief in the watching angel, and the last higher trust in God, on the part of her poor despised father.\n\nAnd Charley did not die. She flutteringly and slowly turned the dangerous point, after long lingering there, and then began to mend. The hope that never had been given, from the first, of Charley being in outward appearance Charley any more soon began to be encouraged; and even that prospered, and I saw her growing into her old childish likeness again.\n\nIt was a great morning when I could tell Ada all this as she stood out in the garden; and it was a great evening when Charley and I at last took tea together in the next room. But on that same evening, I felt that I was stricken cold.\n\nHappily for both of us, it was not until Charley was safe in bed again and placidly asleep that I began to think the contagion of her illness was upon me. I had been able easily to hide what I felt at tea-time, but I was past that already now, and I knew that I was rapidly following in Charley’s steps.\n\nI was well enough, however, to be up early in the morning, and to return my darling’s cheerful blessing from the garden, and to talk with her as long as usual. But I was not free from an impression that I had been walking about the two rooms in the night, a little beside myself, though knowing where I was; and I felt confused at times—with a curious sense of fullness, as if I were becoming too large altogether.\n\nIn the evening I was so much worse that I resolved to prepare Charley, with which view I said, “You’re getting quite strong, Charley, are you not?”\n\n“Oh, quite!” said Charley.\n\n“Strong enough to be told a secret, I think, Charley?”\n\n“Quite strong enough for that, miss!” cried Charley. But Charley’s face fell in the height of her delight, for she saw the secret in MY face; and she came out of the great chair, and fell upon my bosom, and said “Oh, miss, it’s my doing! It’s my doing!” and a great deal more out of the fullness of her grateful heart.\n\n“Now, Charley,” said I after letting her go on for a little while, “if I am to be ill, my great trust, humanly speaking, is in you. And unless you are as quiet and composed for me as you always were for yourself, you can never fulfil it, Charley.”\n\n“If you’ll let me cry a little longer, miss,” said Charley. “Oh, my dear, my dear! If you’ll only let me cry a little longer. Oh, my dear!”—how affectionately and devotedly she poured this out as she clung to my neck, I never can remember without tears—“I’ll be good.”\n\nSo I let Charley cry a little longer, and it did us both good.\n\n“Trust in me now, if you please, miss,” said Charley quietly. “I am listening to everything you say.”\n\n“It’s very little at present, Charley. I shall tell your doctor to-night that I don’t think I am well and that you are going to nurse me.”\n\nFor that the poor child thanked me with her whole heart. “And in the morning, when you hear Miss Ada in the garden, if I should not be quite able to go to the window-curtain as usual, do you go, Charley, and say I am asleep—that I have rather tired myself, and am asleep. At all times keep the room as I have kept it, Charley, and let no one come.”\n\nCharley promised, and I lay down, for I was very heavy. I saw the doctor that night and asked the favour of him that I wished to ask relative to his saying nothing of my illness in the house as yet. I have a very indistinct remembrance of that night melting into day, and of day melting into night again; but I was just able on the first morning to get to the window and speak to my darling.\n\nOn the second morning I heard her dear voice—Oh, how dear now!—outside; and I asked Charley, with some difficulty (speech being painful to me), to go and say I was asleep. I heard her answer softly, “Don’t disturb her, Charley, for the world!”\n\n“How does my own Pride look, Charley?” I inquired.\n\n“Disappointed, miss,” said Charley, peeping through the curtain.\n\n“But I know she is very beautiful this morning.”\n\n“She is indeed, miss,” answered Charley, peeping. “Still looking up at the window.”\n\nWith her blue clear eyes, God bless them, always loveliest when raised like that!\n\nI called Charley to me and gave her her last charge.\n\n“Now, Charley, when she knows I am ill, she will try to make her way into the room. Keep her out, Charley, if you love me truly, to the last! Charley, if you let her in but once, only to look upon me for one moment as I lie here, I shall die.”\n\n“I never will! I never will!” she promised me.\n\n“I believe it, my dear Charley. And now come and sit beside me for a little while, and touch me with your hand. For I cannot see you, Charley; I am blind.”\n\nThe Appointed Time\nIt is night in Lincoln’s Inn—perplexed and troublous valley of the shadow of the law, where suitors generally find but little day—and fat candles are snuffed out in offices, and clerks have rattled down the crazy wooden stairs and dispersed. The bell that rings at nine o’clock has ceased its doleful clangour about nothing; the gates are shut; and the night-porter, a solemn warder with a mighty power of sleep, keeps guard in his lodge. From tiers of staircase windows clogged lamps like the eyes of Equity, bleared Argus with a fathomless pocket for every eye and an eye upon it, dimly blink at the stars. In dirty upper casements, here and there, hazy little patches of candlelight reveal where some wise draughtsman and conveyancer yet toils for the entanglement of real estate in meshes of sheep-skin, in the average ratio of about a dozen of sheep to an acre of land. Over which bee-like industry these benefactors of their species linger yet, though office-hours be past, that they may give, for every day, some good account at last.\n\nIn the neighbouring court, where the Lord Chancellor of the rag and bottle shop dwells, there is a general tendency towards beer and supper. Mrs. Piper and Mrs. Perkins, whose respective sons, engaged with a circle of acquaintance in the game of hide and seek, have been lying in ambush about the by-ways of Chancery Lane for some hours and scouring the plain of the same thoroughfare to the confusion of passengers—Mrs. Piper and Mrs. Perkins have but now exchanged congratulations on the children being abed, and they still linger on a door-step over a few parting words. Mr. Krook and his lodger, and the fact of Mr. Krook’s being “continually in liquor,” and the testamentary prospects of the young man are, as usual, the staple of their conversation. But they have something to say, likewise, of the Harmonic Meeting at the Sol’s Arms, where the sound of the piano through the partly opened windows jingles out into the court, and where Little Swills, after keeping the lovers of harmony in a roar like a very Yorick, may now be heard taking the gruff line in a concerted piece and sentimentally adjuring his friends and patrons to “Listen, listen, listen, tew the wa-ter fall!” Mrs. Perkins and Mrs. Piper compare opinions on the subject of the young lady of professional celebrity who assists at the Harmonic Meetings and who has a space to herself in the manuscript announcement in the window, Mrs. Perkins possessing information that she has been married a year and a half, though announced as Miss M. Melvilleson, the noted siren, and that her baby is clandestinely conveyed to the Sol’s Arms every night to receive its natural nourishment during the entertainments. “Sooner than which, myself,” says Mrs. Perkins, “I would get my living by selling lucifers.” Mrs. Piper, as in duty bound, is of the same opinion, holding that a private station is better than public applause, and thanking heaven for her own (and, by implication, Mrs. Perkins’) respectability. By this time the pot-boy of the Sol’s Arms appearing with her supper-pint well frothed, Mrs. Piper accepts that tankard and retires indoors, first giving a fair good night to Mrs. Perkins, who has had her own pint in her hand ever since it was fetched from the same hostelry by young Perkins before he was sent to bed. Now there is a sound of putting up shop-shutters in the court and a smell as of the smoking of pipes; and shooting stars are seen in upper windows, further indicating retirement to rest. Now, too, the policeman begins to push at doors; to try fastenings; to be suspicious of bundles; and to administer his beat, on the hypothesis that every one is either robbing or being robbed.\n\nIt is a close night, though the damp cold is searching too, and there is a laggard mist a little way up in the air. It is a fine steaming night to turn the slaughter-houses, the unwholesome trades, the sewerage, bad water, and burial-grounds to account, and give the registrar of deaths some extra business. It may be something in the air—there is plenty in it—or it may be something in himself that is in fault; but Mr. Weevle, otherwise Jobling, is very ill at ease. He comes and goes between his own room and the open street door twenty times an hour. He has been doing so ever since it fell dark. Since the Chancellor shut up his shop, which he did very early to-night, Mr. Weevle has been down and up, and down and up (with a cheap tight velvet skull-cap on his head, making his whiskers look out of all proportion), oftener than before.\n\nIt is no phenomenon that Mr. Snagsby should be ill at ease too, for he always is so, more or less, under the oppressive influence of the secret that is upon him. Impelled by the mystery of which he is a partaker and yet in which he is not a sharer, Mr. Snagsby haunts what seems to be its fountain-head—the rag and bottle shop in the court. It has an irresistible attraction for him. Even now, coming round by the Sol’s Arms with the intention of passing down the court, and out at the Chancery Lane end, and so terminating his unpremeditated after-supper stroll of ten minutes’ long from his own door and back again, Mr. Snagsby approaches.\n\n“What, Mr. Weevle?” says the stationer, stopping to speak. “Are YOU there?”\n\n“Aye!” says Weevle, “Here I am, Mr. Snagsby.”\n\n“Airing yourself, as I am doing, before you go to bed?” the stationer inquires.\n\n“Why, there’s not much air to be got here; and what there is, is not very freshening,” Weevle answers, glancing up and down the court.\n\n“Very true, sir. Don’t you observe,” says Mr. Snagsby, pausing to sniff and taste the air a little, “don’t you observe, Mr. Weevle, that you’re—not to put too fine a point upon it—that you’re rather greasy here, sir?”\n\n“Why, I have noticed myself that there is a queer kind of flavour in the place to-night,” Mr. Weevle rejoins. “I suppose it’s chops at the Sol’s Arms.”\n\n“Chops, do you think? Oh! Chops, eh?” Mr. Snagsby sniffs and tastes again. “Well, sir, I suppose it is. But I should say their cook at the Sol wanted a little looking after. She has been burning ’em, sir! And I don’t think”—Mr. Snagsby sniffs and tastes again and then spits and wipes his mouth—“I don’t think—not to put too fine a point upon it—that they were quite fresh when they were shown the gridiron.”\n\n“That’s very likely. It’s a tainting sort of weather.”\n\n“It IS a tainting sort of weather,” says Mr. Snagsby, “and I find it sinking to the spirits.”\n\n“By George! I find it gives me the horrors,” returns Mr. Weevle.\n\n“Then, you see, you live in a lonesome way, and in a lonesome room, with a black circumstance hanging over it,” says Mr. Snagsby, looking in past the other’s shoulder along the dark passage and then falling back a step to look up at the house. “I couldn’t live in that room alone, as you do, sir. I should get so fidgety and worried of an evening, sometimes, that I should be driven to come to the door and stand here sooner than sit there. But then it’s very true that you didn’t see, in your room, what I saw there. That makes a difference.”\n\n“I know quite enough about it,” returns Tony.\n\n“It’s not agreeable, is it?” pursues Mr. Snagsby, coughing his cough of mild persuasion behind his hand. “Mr. Krook ought to consider it in the rent. I hope he does, I am sure.”\n\n“I hope he does,” says Tony. “But I doubt it.”\n\n“You find the rent too high, do you, sir?” returns the stationer. “Rents ARE high about here. I don’t know how it is exactly, but the law seems to put things up in price. Not,” adds Mr. Snagsby with his apologetic cough, “that I mean to say a word against the profession I get my living by.”\n\nMr. Weevle again glances up and down the court and then looks at the stationer. Mr. Snagsby, blankly catching his eye, looks upward for a star or so and coughs a cough expressive of not exactly seeing his way out of this conversation.\n\n“It’s a curious fact, sir,” he observes, slowly rubbing his hands, “that he should have been—”\n\n“Who’s he?” interrupts Mr. Weevle.\n\n“The deceased, you know,” says Mr. Snagsby, twitching his head and right eyebrow towards the staircase and tapping his acquaintance on the button.\n\n“Ah, to be sure!” returns the other as if he were not over-fond of the subject. “I thought we had done with him.”\n\n“I was only going to say it’s a curious fact, sir, that he should have come and lived here, and been one of my writers, and then that you should come and live here, and be one of my writers too. Which there is nothing derogatory, but far from it in the appellation,” says Mr. Snagsby, breaking off with a mistrust that he may have unpolitely asserted a kind of proprietorship in Mr. Weevle, “because I have known writers that have gone into brewers’ houses and done really very respectable indeed. Eminently respectable, sir,” adds Mr. Snagsby with a misgiving that he has not improved the matter.\n\n“It’s a curious coincidence, as you say,” answers Weevle, once more glancing up and down the court.\n\n“Seems a fate in it, don’t there?” suggests the stationer.\n\n“There does.”\n\n“Just so,” observes the stationer with his confirmatory cough. “Quite a fate in it. Quite a fate. Well, Mr. Weevle, I am afraid I must bid you good night”—Mr. Snagsby speaks as if it made him desolate to go, though he has been casting about for any means of escape ever since he stopped to speak—“my little woman will be looking for me else. Good night, sir!”\n\nIf Mr. Snagsby hastens home to save his little woman the trouble of looking for him, he might set his mind at rest on that score. His little woman has had her eye upon him round the Sol’s Arms all this time and now glides after him with a pocket handkerchief wrapped over her head, honouring Mr. Weevle and his doorway with a searching glance as she goes past.\n\n“You’ll know me again, ma’am, at all events,” says Mr. Weevle to himself; “and I can’t compliment you on your appearance, whoever you are, with your head tied up in a bundle. Is this fellow NEVER coming!”\n\nThis fellow approaches as he speaks. Mr. Weevle softly holds up his finger, and draws him into the passage, and closes the street door. Then they go upstairs, Mr. Weevle heavily, and Mr. Guppy (for it is he) very lightly indeed. When they are shut into the back room, they speak low.\n\n“I thought you had gone to Jericho at least instead of coming here,” says Tony.\n\n“Why, I said about ten.”\n\n“You said about ten,” Tony repeats. “Yes, so you did say about ten. But according to my count, it’s ten times ten—it’s a hundred o’clock. I never had such a night in my life!”\n\n“What has been the matter?”\n\n“That’s it!” says Tony. “Nothing has been the matter. But here have I been stewing and fuming in this jolly old crib till I have had the horrors falling on me as thick as hail. THERE’S a blessed-looking candle!” says Tony, pointing to the heavily burning taper on his table with a great cabbage head and a long winding-sheet.\n\n“That’s easily improved,” Mr. Guppy observes as he takes the snuffers in hand.\n\n“IS it?” returns his friend. “Not so easily as you think. It has been smouldering like that ever since it was lighted.”\n\n“Why, what’s the matter with you, Tony?” inquires Mr. Guppy, looking at him, snuffers in hand, as he sits down with his elbow on the table.\n\n“William Guppy,” replies the other, “I am in the downs. It’s this unbearably dull, suicidal room—and old Boguey downstairs, I suppose.” Mr. Weevle moodily pushes the snuffers-tray from him with his elbow, leans his head on his hand, puts his feet on the fender, and looks at the fire. Mr. Guppy, observing him, slightly tosses his head and sits down on the other side of the table in an easy attitude.\n\n“Wasn’t that Snagsby talking to you, Tony?”\n\n“Yes, and he—yes, it was Snagsby,” said Mr. Weevle, altering the construction of his sentence.\n\n“On business?”\n\n“No. No business. He was only sauntering by and stopped to prose.”\n\n“I thought it was Snagsby,” says Mr. Guppy, “and thought it as well that he shouldn’t see me, so I waited till he was gone.”\n\n“There we go again, William G.!” cried Tony, looking up for an instant. “So mysterious and secret! By George, if we were going to commit a murder, we couldn’t have more mystery about it!”\n\nMr. Guppy affects to smile, and with the view of changing the conversation, looks with an admiration, real or pretended, round the room at the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty, terminating his survey with the portrait of Lady Dedlock over the mantelshelf, in which she is represented on a terrace, with a pedestal upon the terrace, and a vase upon the pedestal, and her shawl upon the vase, and a prodigious piece of fur upon the shawl, and her arm on the prodigious piece of fur, and a bracelet on her arm.\n\n“That’s very like Lady Dedlock,” says Mr. Guppy. “It’s a speaking likeness.”\n\n“I wish it was,” growls Tony, without changing his position. “I should have some fashionable conversation, here, then.”\n\nFinding by this time that his friend is not to be wheedled into a more sociable humour, Mr. Guppy puts about upon the ill-used tack and remonstrates with him.\n\n“Tony,” says he, “I can make allowances for lowness of spirits, for no man knows what it is when it does come upon a man better than I do, and no man perhaps has a better right to know it than a man who has an unrequited image imprinted on his ’eart. But there are bounds to these things when an unoffending party is in question, and I will acknowledge to you, Tony, that I don’t think your manner on the present occasion is hospitable or quite gentlemanly.”\n\n“This is strong language, William Guppy,” returns Mr. Weevle.\n\n“Sir, it may be,” retorts Mr. William Guppy, “but I feel strongly when I use it.”\n\nMr. Weevle admits that he has been wrong and begs Mr. William Guppy to think no more about it. Mr. William Guppy, however, having got the advantage, cannot quite release it without a little more injured remonstrance.\n\n“No! Dash it, Tony,” says that gentleman, “you really ought to be careful how you wound the feelings of a man who has an unrequited image imprinted on his ’eart and who is NOT altogether happy in those chords which vibrate to the tenderest emotions. You, Tony, possess in yourself all that is calculated to charm the eye and allure the taste. It is not—happily for you, perhaps, and I may wish that I could say the same—it is not your character to hover around one flower. The ole garden is open to you, and your airy pinions carry you through it. Still, Tony, far be it from me, I am sure, to wound even your feelings without a cause!”\n\nTony again entreats that the subject may be no longer pursued, saying emphatically, “William Guppy, drop it!” Mr. Guppy acquiesces, with the reply, “I never should have taken it up, Tony, of my own accord.”\n\n“And now,” says Tony, stirring the fire, “touching this same bundle of letters. Isn’t it an extraordinary thing of Krook to have appointed twelve o’clock to-night to hand ’em over to me?”\n\n“Very. What did he do it for?”\n\n“What does he do anything for? HE don’t know. Said to-day was his birthday and he’d hand ’em over to-night at twelve o’clock. He’ll have drunk himself blind by that time. He has been at it all day.”\n\n“He hasn’t forgotten the appointment, I hope?”\n\n“Forgotten? Trust him for that. He never forgets anything. I saw him to-night, about eight—helped him to shut up his shop—and he had got the letters then in his hairy cap. He pulled it off and showed ’em me. When the shop was closed, he took them out of his cap, hung his cap on the chair-back, and stood turning them over before the fire. I heard him a little while afterwards, through the floor here, humming like the wind, the only song he knows—about Bibo, and old Charon, and Bibo being drunk when he died, or something or other. He has been as quiet since as an old rat asleep in his hole.”\n\n“And you are to go down at twelve?”\n\n“At twelve. And as I tell you, when you came it seemed to me a hundred.”\n\n“Tony,” says Mr. Guppy after considering a little with his legs crossed, “he can’t read yet, can he?”\n\n“Read! He’ll never read. He can make all the letters separately, and he knows most of them separately when he sees them; he has got on that much, under me; but he can’t put them together. He’s too old to acquire the knack of it now—and too drunk.”\n\n“Tony,” says Mr. Guppy, uncrossing and recrossing his legs, “how do you suppose he spelt out that name of Hawdon?”\n\n“He never spelt it out. You know what a curious power of eye he has and how he has been used to employ himself in copying things by eye alone. He imitated it, evidently from the direction of a letter, and asked me what it meant.”\n\n“Tony,” says Mr. Guppy, uncrossing and recrossing his legs again, “should you say that the original was a man’s writing or a woman’s?”\n\n“A woman’s. Fifty to one a lady’s—slopes a good deal, and the end of the letter ‘n,’ long and hasty.”\n\nMr. Guppy has been biting his thumb-nail during this dialogue, generally changing the thumb when he has changed the cross leg. As he is going to do so again, he happens to look at his coat-sleeve. It takes his attention. He stares at it, aghast.\n\n“Why, Tony, what on earth is going on in this house to-night? Is there a chimney on fire?”\n\n“Chimney on fire!”\n\n“Ah!” returns Mr. Guppy. “See how the soot’s falling. See here, on my arm! See again, on the table here! Confound the stuff, it won’t blow off—smears like black fat!”\n\nThey look at one another, and Tony goes listening to the door, and a little way upstairs, and a little way downstairs. Comes back and says it’s all right and all quiet, and quotes the remark he lately made to Mr. Snagsby about their cooking chops at the Sol’s Arms.\n\n“And it was then,” resumes Mr. Guppy, still glancing with remarkable aversion at the coat-sleeve, as they pursue their conversation before the fire, leaning on opposite sides of the table, with their heads very near together, “that he told you of his having taken the bundle of letters from his lodger’s portmanteau?”\n\n“That was the time, sir,” answers Tony, faintly adjusting his whiskers. “Whereupon I wrote a line to my dear boy, the Honourable William Guppy, informing him of the appointment for to-night and advising him not to call before, Boguey being a slyboots.”\n\nThe light vivacious tone of fashionable life which is usually assumed by Mr. Weevle sits so ill upon him to-night that he abandons that and his whiskers together, and after looking over his shoulder, appears to yield himself up a prey to the horrors again.\n\n“You are to bring the letters to your room to read and compare, and to get yourself into a position to tell him all about them. That’s the arrangement, isn’t it, Tony?” asks Mr. Guppy, anxiously biting his thumb-nail.\n\n“You can’t speak too low. Yes. That’s what he and I agreed.”\n\n“I tell you what, Tony—”\n\n“You can’t speak too low,” says Tony once more. Mr. Guppy nods his sagacious head, advances it yet closer, and drops into a whisper.\n\n“I tell you what. The first thing to be done is to make another packet like the real one so that if he should ask to see the real one while it’s in my possession, you can show him the dummy.”\n\n“And suppose he detects the dummy as soon as he sees it, which with his biting screw of an eye is about five hundred times more likely than not,” suggests Tony.\n\n“Then we’ll face it out. They don’t belong to him, and they never did. You found that, and you placed them in my hands—a legal friend of yours—for security. If he forces us to it, they’ll be producible, won’t they?”\n\n“Ye-es,” is Mr. Weevle’s reluctant admission.\n\n“Why, Tony,” remonstrates his friend, “how you look! You don’t doubt William Guppy? You don’t suspect any harm?”\n\n“I don’t suspect anything more than I know, William,” returns the other gravely.\n\n“And what do you know?” urges Mr. Guppy, raising his voice a little; but on his friend’s once more warning him, “I tell you, you can’t speak too low,” he repeats his question without any sound at all, forming with his lips only the words, “What do you know?”\n\n“I know three things. First, I know that here we are whispering in secrecy, a pair of conspirators.”\n\n“Well!” says Mr. Guppy. “And we had better be that than a pair of noodles, which we should be if we were doing anything else, for it’s the only way of doing what we want to do. Secondly?”\n\n“Secondly, it’s not made out to me how it’s likely to be profitable, after all.”\n\nMr. Guppy casts up his eyes at the portrait of Lady Dedlock over the mantelshelf and replies, “Tony, you are asked to leave that to the honour of your friend. Besides its being calculated to serve that friend in those chords of the human mind which—which need not be called into agonizing vibration on the present occasion—your friend is no fool. What’s that?”\n\n“It’s eleven o’clock striking by the bell of Saint Paul’s. Listen and you’ll hear all the bells in the city jangling.”\n\nBoth sit silent, listening to the metal voices, near and distant, resounding from towers of various heights, in tones more various than their situations. When these at length cease, all seems more mysterious and quiet than before. One disagreeable result of whispering is that it seems to evoke an atmosphere of silence, haunted by the ghosts of sound—strange cracks and tickings, the rustling of garments that have no substance in them, and the tread of dreadful feet that would leave no mark on the sea-sand or the winter snow. So sensitive the two friends happen to be that the air is full of these phantoms, and the two look over their shoulders by one consent to see that the door is shut.\n\n“Yes, Tony?” says Mr. Guppy, drawing nearer to the fire and biting his unsteady thumb-nail. “You were going to say, thirdly?”\n\n“It’s far from a pleasant thing to be plotting about a dead man in the room where he died, especially when you happen to live in it.”\n\n“But we are plotting nothing against him, Tony.”\n\n“May be not, still I don’t like it. Live here by yourself and see how YOU like it.”\n\n“As to dead men, Tony,” proceeds Mr. Guppy, evading this proposal, “there have been dead men in most rooms.”\n\n“I know there have, but in most rooms you let them alone, and—and they let you alone,” Tony answers.\n\nThe two look at each other again. Mr. Guppy makes a hurried remark to the effect that they may be doing the deceased a service, that he hopes so. There is an oppressive blank until Mr. Weevle, by stirring the fire suddenly, makes Mr. Guppy start as if his heart had been stirred instead.\n\n“Fah! Here’s more of this hateful soot hanging about,” says he. “Let us open the window a bit and get a mouthful of air. It’s too close.”\n\nHe raises the sash, and they both rest on the window-sill, half in and half out of the room. The neighbouring houses are too near to admit of their seeing any sky without craning their necks and looking up, but lights in frowsy windows here and there, and the rolling of distant carriages, and the new expression that there is of the stir of men, they find to be comfortable. Mr. Guppy, noiselessly tapping on the window-sill, resumes his whispering in quite a light-comedy tone.\n\n“By the by, Tony, don’t forget old Smallweed,” meaning the younger of that name. “I have not let him into this, you know. That grandfather of his is too keen by half. It runs in the family.”\n\n“I remember,” says Tony. “I am up to all that.”\n\n“And as to Krook,” resumes Mr. Guppy. “Now, do you suppose he really has got hold of any other papers of importance, as he has boasted to you, since you have been such allies?”\n\nTony shakes his head. “I don’t know. Can’t imagine. If we get through this business without rousing his suspicions, I shall be better informed, no doubt. How can I know without seeing them, when he don’t know himself? He is always spelling out words from them, and chalking them over the table and the shop-wall, and asking what this is and what that is; but his whole stock from beginning to end may easily be the waste-paper he bought it as, for anything I can say. It’s a monomania with him to think he is possessed of documents. He has been going to learn to read them this last quarter of a century, I should judge, from what he tells me.”\n\n“How did he first come by that idea, though? That’s the question,” Mr. Guppy suggests with one eye shut, after a little forensic meditation. “He may have found papers in something he bought, where papers were not supposed to be, and may have got it into his shrewd head from the manner and place of their concealment that they are worth something.”\n\n“Or he may have been taken in, in some pretended bargain. Or he may have been muddled altogether by long staring at whatever he HAS got, and by drink, and by hanging about the Lord Chancellor’s Court and hearing of documents for ever,” returns Mr. Weevle.\n\nMr. Guppy sitting on the window-sill, nodding his head and balancing all these possibilities in his mind, continues thoughtfully to tap it, and clasp it, and measure it with his hand, until he hastily draws his hand away.\n\n“What, in the devil’s name,” he says, “is this! Look at my fingers!”\n\nA thick, yellow liquor defiles them, which is offensive to the touch and sight and more offensive to the smell. A stagnant, sickening oil with some natural repulsion in it that makes them both shudder.\n\n“What have you been doing here? What have you been pouring out of window?”\n\n“I pouring out of window! Nothing, I swear! Never, since I have been here!” cries the lodger.\n\nAnd yet look here—and look here! When he brings the candle here, from the corner of the window-sill, it slowly drips and creeps away down the bricks, here lies in a little thick nauseous pool.\n\n“This is a horrible house,” says Mr. Guppy, shutting down the window. “Give me some water or I shall cut my hand off.”\n\nHe so washes, and rubs, and scrubs, and smells, and washes, that he has not long restored himself with a glass of brandy and stood silently before the fire when Saint Paul’s bell strikes twelve and all those other bells strike twelve from their towers of various heights in the dark air, and in their many tones. When all is quiet again, the lodger says, “It’s the appointed time at last. Shall I go?”\n\nMr. Guppy nods and gives him a “lucky touch” on the back, but not with the washed hand, though it is his right hand.\n\nHe goes downstairs, and Mr. Guppy tries to compose himself before the fire for waiting a long time. But in no more than a minute or two the stairs creak and Tony comes swiftly back.\n\n“Have you got them?”\n\n“Got them! No. The old man’s not there.”\n\nHe has been so horribly frightened in the short interval that his terror seizes the other, who makes a rush at him and asks loudly, “What’s the matter?”\n\n“I couldn’t make him hear, and I softly opened the door and looked in. And the burning smell is there—and the soot is there, and the oil is there—and he is not there!” Tony ends this with a groan.\n\nMr. Guppy takes the light. They go down, more dead than alive, and holding one another, push open the door of the back shop. The cat has retreated close to it and stands snarling, not at them, at something on the ground before the fire. There is a very little fire left in the grate, but there is a smouldering, suffocating vapour in the room and a dark, greasy coating on the walls and ceiling. The chairs and table, and the bottle so rarely absent from the table, all stand as usual. On one chair-back hang the old man’s hairy cap and coat.\n\n“Look!” whispers the lodger, pointing his friend’s attention to these objects with a trembling finger. “I told you so. When I saw him last, he took his cap off, took out the little bundle of old letters, hung his cap on the back of the chair—his coat was there already, for he had pulled that off before he went to put the shutters up—and I left him turning the letters over in his hand, standing just where that crumbled black thing is upon the floor.”\n\nIs he hanging somewhere? They look up. No.\n\n“See!” whispers Tony. “At the foot of the same chair there lies a dirty bit of thin red cord that they tie up pens with. That went round the letters. He undid it slowly, leering and laughing at me, before he began to turn them over, and threw it there. I saw it fall.”\n\n“What’s the matter with the cat?” says Mr. Guppy. “Look at her!”\n\n“Mad, I think. And no wonder in this evil place.”\n\nThey advance slowly, looking at all these things. The cat remains where they found her, still snarling at the something on the ground before the fire and between the two chairs. What is it? Hold up the light.\n\nHere is a small burnt patch of flooring; here is the tinder from a little bundle of burnt paper, but not so light as usual, seeming to be steeped in something; and here is—is it the cinder of a small charred and broken log of wood sprinkled with white ashes, or is it coal? Oh, horror, he IS here! And this from which we run away, striking out the light and overturning one another into the street, is all that represents him.\n\nHelp, help, help! Come into this house for heaven’s sake! Plenty will come in, but none can help. The Lord Chancellor of that court, true to his title in his last act, has died the death of all lord chancellors in all courts and of all authorities in all places under all names soever, where false pretences are made, and where injustice is done. Call the death by any name your Highness will, attribute it to whom you will, or say it might have been prevented how you will, it is the same death eternally—inborn, inbred, engendered in the corrupted humours of the vicious body itself, and that only—spontaneous combustion, and none other of all the deaths that can be died.\n\nInterlopers\nNow do those two gentlemen not very neat about the cuffs and buttons who attended the last coroner’s inquest at the Sol’s Arms reappear in the precincts with surprising swiftness (being, in fact, breathlessly fetched by the active and intelligent beadle), and institute perquisitions through the court, and dive into the Sol’s parlour, and write with ravenous little pens on tissue-paper. Now do they note down, in the watches of the night, how the neighbourhood of Chancery Lane was yesterday, at about midnight, thrown into a state of the most intense agitation and excitement by the following alarming and horrible discovery. Now do they set forth how it will doubtless be remembered that some time back a painful sensation was created in the public mind by a case of mysterious death from opium occurring in the first floor of the house occupied as a rag, bottle, and general marine store shop, by an eccentric individual of intemperate habits, far advanced in life, named Krook; and how, by a remarkable coincidence, Krook was examined at the inquest, which it may be recollected was held on that occasion at the Sol’s Arms, a well-conducted tavern immediately adjoining the premises in question on the west side and licensed to a highly respectable landlord, Mr. James George Bogsby. Now do they show (in as many words as possible) how during some hours of yesterday evening a very peculiar smell was observed by the inhabitants of the court, in which the tragical occurrence which forms the subject of that present account transpired; and which odour was at one time so powerful that Mr. Swills, a comic vocalist professionally engaged by Mr. J. G. Bogsby, has himself stated to our reporter that he mentioned to Miss M. Melvilleson, a lady of some pretensions to musical ability, likewise engaged by Mr. J. G. Bogsby to sing at a series of concerts called Harmonic Assemblies, or Meetings, which it would appear are held at the Sol’s Arms under Mr. Bogsby’s direction pursuant to the Act of George the Second, that he (Mr. Swills) found his voice seriously affected by the impure state of the atmosphere, his jocose expression at the time being that he was like an empty post-office, for he hadn’t a single note in him. How this account of Mr. Swills is entirely corroborated by two intelligent married females residing in the same court and known respectively by the names of Mrs. Piper and Mrs. Perkins, both of whom observed the foetid effluvia and regarded them as being emitted from the premises in the occupation of Krook, the unfortunate deceased. All this and a great deal more the two gentlemen who have formed an amicable partnership in the melancholy catastrophe write down on the spot; and the boy population of the court (out of bed in a moment) swarm up the shutters of the Sol’s Arms parlour, to behold the tops of their heads while they are about it.\n\nThe whole court, adult as well as boy, is sleepless for that night, and can do nothing but wrap up its many heads, and talk of the ill-fated house, and look at it. Miss Flite has been bravely rescued from her chamber, as if it were in flames, and accommodated with a bed at the Sol’s Arms. The Sol neither turns off its gas nor shuts its door all night, for any kind of public excitement makes good for the Sol and causes the court to stand in need of comfort. The house has not done so much in the stomachic article of cloves or in brandy-and-water warm since the inquest. The moment the pot-boy heard what had happened, he rolled up his shirt-sleeves tight to his shoulders and said, “There’ll be a run upon us!” In the first outcry, young Piper dashed off for the fire-engines and returned in triumph at a jolting gallop perched up aloft on the Phoenix and holding on to that fabulous creature with all his might in the midst of helmets and torches. One helmet remains behind after careful investigation of all chinks and crannies and slowly paces up and down before the house in company with one of the two policemen who have likewise been left in charge thereof. To this trio everybody in the court possessed of sixpence has an insatiate desire to exhibit hospitality in a liquid form.\n\nMr. Weevle and his friend Mr. Guppy are within the bar at the Sol and are worth anything to the Sol that the bar contains if they will only stay there. “This is not a time,” says Mr. Bogsby, “to haggle about money,” though he looks something sharply after it, over the counter; “give your orders, you two gentlemen, and you’re welcome to whatever you put a name to.”\n\nThus entreated, the two gentlemen (Mr. Weevle especially) put names to so many things that in course of time they find it difficult to put a name to anything quite distinctly, though they still relate to all new-comers some version of the night they have had of it, and of what they said, and what they thought, and what they saw. Meanwhile, one or other of the policemen often flits about the door, and pushing it open a little way at the full length of his arm, looks in from outer gloom. Not that he has any suspicions, but that he may as well know what they are up to in there.\n\nThus night pursues its leaden course, finding the court still out of bed through the unwonted hours, still treating and being treated, still conducting itself similarly to a court that has had a little money left it unexpectedly. Thus night at length with slow-retreating steps departs, and the lamp-lighter going his rounds, like an executioner to a despotic king, strikes off the little heads of fire that have aspired to lessen the darkness. Thus the day cometh, whether or no.\n\nAnd the day may discern, even with its dim London eye, that the court has been up all night. Over and above the faces that have fallen drowsily on tables and the heels that lie prone on hard floors instead of beds, the brick and mortar physiognomy of the very court itself looks worn and jaded. And now the neighbourhood, waking up and beginning to hear of what has happened, comes streaming in, half dressed, to ask questions; and the two policemen and the helmet (who are far less impressible externally than the court) have enough to do to keep the door.\n\n“Good gracious, gentlemen!” says Mr. Snagsby, coming up. “What’s this I hear!”\n\n“Why, it’s true,” returns one of the policemen. “That’s what it is. Now move on here, come!”\n\n“Why, good gracious, gentlemen,” says Mr. Snagsby, somewhat promptly backed away, “I was at this door last night betwixt ten and eleven o’clock in conversation with the young man who lodges here.”\n\n“Indeed?” returns the policeman. “You will find the young man next door then. Now move on here, some of you.”\n\n“Not hurt, I hope?” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\n“Hurt? No. What’s to hurt him!”\n\nMr. Snagsby, wholly unable to answer this or any question in his troubled mind, repairs to the Sol’s Arms and finds Mr. Weevle languishing over tea and toast with a considerable expression on him of exhausted excitement and exhausted tobacco-smoke.\n\n“And Mr. Guppy likewise!” quoth Mr. Snagsby. “Dear, dear, dear! What a fate there seems in all this! And my lit—”\n\nMr. Snagsby’s power of speech deserts him in the formation of the words “my little woman.” For to see that injured female walk into the Sol’s Arms at that hour of the morning and stand before the beer-engine, with her eyes fixed upon him like an accusing spirit, strikes him dumb.\n\n“My dear,” says Mr. Snagsby when his tongue is loosened, “will you take anything? A little—not to put too fine a point upon it—drop of shrub?”\n\n“No,” says Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“My love, you know these two gentlemen?”\n\n“Yes!” says Mrs. Snagsby, and in a rigid manner acknowledges their presence, still fixing Mr. Snagsby with her eye.\n\nThe devoted Mr. Snagsby cannot bear this treatment. He takes Mrs. Snagsby by the hand and leads her aside to an adjacent cask.\n\n“My little woman, why do you look at me in that way? Pray don’t do it.”\n\n“I can’t help my looks,” says Mrs. Snagsby, “and if I could I wouldn’t.”\n\nMr. Snagsby, with his cough of meekness, rejoins, “Wouldn’t you really, my dear?” and meditates. Then coughs his cough of trouble and says, “This is a dreadful mystery, my love!” still fearfully disconcerted by Mrs. Snagsby’s eye.\n\n“It IS,” returns Mrs. Snagsby, shaking her head, “a dreadful mystery.”\n\n“My little woman,” urges Mr. Snagsby in a piteous manner, “don’t for goodness’ sake speak to me with that bitter expression and look at me in that searching way! I beg and entreat of you not to do it. Good Lord, you don’t suppose that I would go spontaneously combusting any person, my dear?”\n\n“I can’t say,” returns Mrs. Snagsby.\n\nOn a hasty review of his unfortunate position, Mr. Snagsby “can’t say” either. He is not prepared positively to deny that he may have had something to do with it. He has had something—he don’t know what—to do with so much in this connexion that is mysterious that it is possible he may even be implicated, without knowing it, in the present transaction. He faintly wipes his forehead with his handkerchief and gasps.\n\n“My life,” says the unhappy stationer, “would you have any objections to mention why, being in general so delicately circumspect in your conduct, you come into a wine-vaults before breakfast?”\n\n“Why do YOU come here?” inquires Mrs. Snagsby.\n\n“My dear, merely to know the rights of the fatal accident which has happened to the venerable party who has been—combusted.” Mr. Snagsby has made a pause to suppress a groan. “I should then have related them to you, my love, over your French roll.”\n\n“I dare say you would! You relate everything to me, Mr. Snagsby.”\n\n“Every—my lit—”\n\n“I should be glad,” says Mrs. Snagsby after contemplating his increased confusion with a severe and sinister smile, “if you would come home with me; I think you may be safer there, Mr. Snagsby, than anywhere else.”\n\n“My love, I don’t know but what I may be, I am sure. I am ready to go.”\n\nMr. Snagsby casts his eye forlornly round the bar, gives Messrs. Weevle and Guppy good morning, assures them of the satisfaction with which he sees them uninjured, and accompanies Mrs. Snagsby from the Sol’s Arms. Before night his doubt whether he may not be responsible for some inconceivable part in the catastrophe which is the talk of the whole neighbourhood is almost resolved into certainty by Mrs. Snagsby’s pertinacity in that fixed gaze. His mental sufferings are so great that he entertains wandering ideas of delivering himself up to justice and requiring to be cleared if innocent and punished with the utmost rigour of the law if guilty.\n\nMr. Weevle and Mr. Guppy, having taken their breakfast, step into Lincoln’s Inn to take a little walk about the square and clear as many of the dark cobwebs out of their brains as a little walk may.\n\n“There can be no more favourable time than the present, Tony,” says Mr. Guppy after they have broodingly made out the four sides of the square, “for a word or two between us upon a point on which we must, with very little delay, come to an understanding.”\n\n“Now, I tell you what, William G.!” returns the other, eyeing his companion with a bloodshot eye. “If it’s a point of conspiracy, you needn’t take the trouble to mention it. I have had enough of that, and I ain’t going to have any more. We shall have YOU taking fire next or blowing up with a bang.”\n\nThis supposititious phenomenon is so very disagreeable to Mr. Guppy that his voice quakes as he says in a moral way, “Tony, I should have thought that what we went through last night would have been a lesson to you never to be personal any more as long as you lived.” To which Mr. Weevle returns, “William, I should have thought it would have been a lesson to YOU never to conspire any more as long as you lived.” To which Mr. Guppy says, “Who’s conspiring?” To which Mr. Jobling replies, “Why, YOU are!” To which Mr. Guppy retorts, “No, I am not.” To which Mr. Jobling retorts again, “Yes, you are!” To which Mr. Guppy retorts, “Who says so?” To which Mr. Jobling retorts, “I say so!” To which Mr. Guppy retorts, “Oh, indeed?” To which Mr. Jobling retorts, “Yes, indeed!” And both being now in a heated state, they walk on silently for a while to cool down again.\n\n“Tony,” says Mr. Guppy then, “if you heard your friend out instead of flying at him, you wouldn’t fall into mistakes. But your temper is hasty and you are not considerate. Possessing in yourself, Tony, all that is calculated to charm the eye—”\n\n“Oh! Blow the eye!” cries Mr. Weevle, cutting him short. “Say what you have got to say!”\n\nFinding his friend in this morose and material condition, Mr. Guppy only expresses the finer feelings of his soul through the tone of injury in which he recommences, “Tony, when I say there is a point on which we must come to an understanding pretty soon, I say so quite apart from any kind of conspiring, however innocent. You know it is professionally arranged beforehand in all cases that are tried what facts the witnesses are to prove. Is it or is it not desirable that we should know what facts we are to prove on the inquiry into the death of this unfortunate old mo—gentleman?” (Mr. Guppy was going to say “mogul,” but thinks “gentleman” better suited to the circumstances.)\n\n“What facts? THE facts.”\n\n“The facts bearing on that inquiry. Those are”—Mr. Guppy tells them off on his fingers—“what we knew of his habits, when you saw him last, what his condition was then, the discovery that we made, and how we made it.”\n\n“Yes,” says Mr. Weevle. “Those are about the facts.”\n\n“We made the discovery in consequence of his having, in his eccentric way, an appointment with you at twelve o’clock at night, when you were to explain some writing to him as you had often done before on account of his not being able to read. I, spending the evening with you, was called down—and so forth. The inquiry being only into the circumstances touching the death of the deceased, it’s not necessary to go beyond these facts, I suppose you’ll agree?”\n\n“No!” returns Mr. Weevle. “I suppose not.”\n\n“And this is not a conspiracy, perhaps?” says the injured Guppy.\n\n“No,” returns his friend; “if it’s nothing worse than this, I withdraw the observation.”\n\n“Now, Tony,” says Mr. Guppy, taking his arm again and walking him slowly on, “I should like to know, in a friendly way, whether you have yet thought over the many advantages of your continuing to live at that place?”\n\n“What do you mean?” says Tony, stopping.\n\n“Whether you have yet thought over the many advantages of your continuing to live at that place?” repeats Mr. Guppy, walking him on again.\n\n“At what place? THAT place?” pointing in the direction of the rag and bottle shop.\n\nMr. Guppy nods.\n\n“Why, I wouldn’t pass another night there for any consideration that you could offer me,” says Mr. Weevle, haggardly staring.\n\n“Do you mean it though, Tony?”\n\n“Mean it! Do I look as if I mean it? I feel as if I do; I know that,” says Mr. Weevle with a very genuine shudder.\n\n“Then the possibility or probability—for such it must be considered—of your never being disturbed in possession of those effects lately belonging to a lone old man who seemed to have no relation in the world, and the certainty of your being able to find out what he really had got stored up there, don’t weigh with you at all against last night, Tony, if I understand you?” says Mr. Guppy, biting his thumb with the appetite of vexation.\n\n“Certainly not. Talk in that cool way of a fellow’s living there?” cries Mr. Weevle indignantly. “Go and live there yourself.”\n\n“Oh! I, Tony!” says Mr. Guppy, soothing him. “I have never lived there and couldn’t get a lodging there now, whereas you have got one.”\n\n“You are welcome to it,” rejoins his friend, “and—ugh!—you may make yourself at home in it.”\n\n“Then you really and truly at this point,” says Mr. Guppy, “give up the whole thing, if I understand you, Tony?”\n\n“You never,” returns Tony with a most convincing steadfastness, “said a truer word in all your life. I do!”\n\nWhile they are so conversing, a hackney-coach drives into the square, on the box of which vehicle a very tall hat makes itself manifest to the public. Inside the coach, and consequently not so manifest to the multitude, though sufficiently so to the two friends, for the coach stops almost at their feet, are the venerable Mr. Smallweed and Mrs. Smallweed, accompanied by their granddaughter Judy.\n\nAn air of haste and excitement pervades the party, and as the tall hat (surmounting Mr. Smallweed the younger) alights, Mr. Smallweed the elder pokes his head out of window and bawls to Mr. Guppy, “How de do, sir! How de do!”\n\n“What do Chick and his family want here at this time of the morning, I wonder!” says Mr. Guppy, nodding to his familiar.\n\n“My dear sir,” cries Grandfather Smallweed, “would you do me a favour? Would you and your friend be so very obleeging as to carry me into the public-house in the court, while Bart and his sister bring their grandmother along? Would you do an old man that good turn, sir?”\n\nMr. Guppy looks at his friend, repeating inquiringly, “The public-house in the court?” And they prepare to bear the venerable burden to the Sol’s Arms.\n\n“There’s your fare!” says the patriarch to the coachman with a fierce grin and shaking his incapable fist at him. “Ask me for a penny more, and I’ll have my lawful revenge upon you. My dear young men, be easy with me, if you please. Allow me to catch you round the neck. I won’t squeeze you tighter than I can help. Oh, Lord! Oh, dear me! Oh, my bones!”\n\nIt is well that the Sol is not far off, for Mr. Weevle presents an apoplectic appearance before half the distance is accomplished. With no worse aggravation of his symptoms, however, than the utterance of divers croaking sounds expressive of obstructed respiration, he fulfils his share of the porterage and the benevolent old gentleman is deposited by his own desire in the parlour of the Sol’s Arms.\n\n“Oh, Lord!” gasps Mr. Smallweed, looking about him, breathless, from an arm-chair. “Oh, dear me! Oh, my bones and back! Oh, my aches and pains! Sit down, you dancing, prancing, shambling, scrambling poll-parrot! Sit down!”\n\nThis little apostrophe to Mrs. Smallweed is occasioned by a propensity on the part of that unlucky old lady whenever she finds herself on her feet to amble about and “set” to inanimate objects, accompanying herself with a chattering noise, as in a witch dance. A nervous affection has probably as much to do with these demonstrations as any imbecile intention in the poor old woman, but on the present occasion they are so particularly lively in connexion with the Windsor arm-chair, fellow to that in which Mr. Smallweed is seated, that she only quite desists when her grandchildren have held her down in it, her lord in the meanwhile bestowing upon her, with great volubility, the endearing epithet of “a pig-headed jackdaw,” repeated a surprising number of times.\n\n“My dear sir,” Grandfather Smallweed then proceeds, addressing Mr. Guppy, “there has been a calamity here. Have you heard of it, either of you?”\n\n“Heard of it, sir! Why, we discovered it.”\n\n“You discovered it. You two discovered it! Bart, THEY discovered it!”\n\nThe two discoverers stare at the Smallweeds, who return the compliment.\n\n“My dear friends,” whines Grandfather Smallweed, putting out both his hands, “I owe you a thousand thanks for discharging the melancholy office of discovering the ashes of Mrs. Smallweed’s brother.”\n\n“Eh?” says Mr. Guppy.\n\n“Mrs. Smallweed’s brother, my dear friend—her only relation. We were not on terms, which is to be deplored now, but he never WOULD be on terms. He was not fond of us. He was eccentric—he was very eccentric. Unless he has left a will (which is not at all likely) I shall take out letters of administration. I have come down to look after the property; it must be sealed up, it must be protected. I have come down,” repeats Grandfather Smallweed, hooking the air towards him with all his ten fingers at once, “to look after the property.”\n\n“I think, Small,” says the disconsolate Mr. Guppy, “you might have mentioned that the old man was your uncle.”\n\n“You two were so close about him that I thought you would like me to be the same,” returns that old bird with a secretly glistening eye. “Besides, I wasn’t proud of him.”\n\n“Besides which, it was nothing to you, you know, whether he was or not,” says Judy. Also with a secretly glistening eye.\n\n“He never saw me in his life to know me,” observed Small; “I don’t know why I should introduce HIM, I am sure!”\n\n“No, he never communicated with us, which is to be deplored,” the old gentleman strikes in, “but I have come to look after the property—to look over the papers, and to look after the property. We shall make good our title. It is in the hands of my solicitor. Mr. Tulkinghorn, of Lincoln’s Inn Fields, over the way there, is so good as to act as my solicitor; and grass don’t grow under HIS feet, I can tell ye. Krook was Mrs. Smallweed’s only brother; she had no relation but Krook, and Krook had no relation but Mrs. Smallweed. I am speaking of your brother, you brimstone black-beetle, that was seventy-six years of age.”\n\nMrs. Smallweed instantly begins to shake her head and pipe up, “Seventy-six pound seven and sevenpence! Seventy-six thousand bags of money! Seventy-six hundred thousand million of parcels of bank-notes!”\n\n“Will somebody give me a quart pot?” exclaims her exasperated husband, looking helplessly about him and finding no missile within his reach. “Will somebody obleege me with a spittoon? Will somebody hand me anything hard and bruising to pelt at her? You hag, you cat, you dog, you brimstone barker!” Here Mr. Smallweed, wrought up to the highest pitch by his own eloquence, actually throws Judy at her grandmother in default of anything else, by butting that young virgin at the old lady with such force as he can muster and then dropping into his chair in a heap.\n\n“Shake me up, somebody, if you’ll be so good,” says the voice from within the faintly struggling bundle into which he has collapsed. “I have come to look after the property. Shake me up, and call in the police on duty at the next house to be explained to about the property. My solicitor will be here presently to protect the property. Transportation or the gallows for anybody who shall touch the property!” As his dutiful grandchildren set him up, panting, and putting him through the usual restorative process of shaking and punching, he still repeats like an echo, “The—the property! The property! Property!”\n\nMr. Weevle and Mr. Guppy look at each other, the former as having relinquished the whole affair, the latter with a discomfited countenance as having entertained some lingering expectations yet. But there is nothing to be done in opposition to the Smallweed interest. Mr. Tulkinghorn’s clerk comes down from his official pew in the chambers to mention to the police that Mr. Tulkinghorn is answerable for its being all correct about the next of kin and that the papers and effects will be formally taken possession of in due time and course. Mr. Smallweed is at once permitted so far to assert his supremacy as to be carried on a visit of sentiment into the next house and upstairs into Miss Flite’s deserted room, where he looks like a hideous bird of prey newly added to her aviary.\n\nThe arrival of this unexpected heir soon taking wind in the court still makes good for the Sol and keeps the court upon its mettle. Mrs. Piper and Mrs. Perkins think it hard upon the young man if there really is no will, and consider that a handsome present ought to be made him out of the estate. Young Piper and young Perkins, as members of that restless juvenile circle which is the terror of the foot-passengers in Chancery Lane, crumble into ashes behind the pump and under the archway all day long, where wild yells and hootings take place over their remains. Little Swills and Miss M. Melvilleson enter into affable conversation with their patrons, feeling that these unusual occurrences level the barriers between professionals and non-professionals. Mr. Bogsby puts up “The popular song of King Death, with chorus by the whole strength of the company,” as the great Harmonic feature of the week and announces in the bill that “J. G. B. is induced to do so at a considerable extra expense in consequence of a wish which has been very generally expressed at the bar by a large body of respectable individuals and in homage to a late melancholy event which has aroused so much sensation.” There is one point connected with the deceased upon which the court is particularly anxious, namely, that the fiction of a full-sized coffin should be preserved, though there is so little to put in it. Upon the undertaker’s stating in the Sol’s bar in the course of the day that he has received orders to construct “a six-footer,” the general solicitude is much relieved, and it is considered that Mr. Smallweed’s conduct does him great honour.\n\nOut of the court, and a long way out of it, there is considerable excitement too, for men of science and philosophy come to look, and carriages set down doctors at the corner who arrive with the same intent, and there is more learned talk about inflammable gases and phosphuretted hydrogen than the court has ever imagined. Some of these authorities (of course the wisest) hold with indignation that the deceased had no business to die in the alleged manner; and being reminded by other authorities of a certain inquiry into the evidence for such deaths reprinted in the sixth volume of the Philosophical Transactions; and also of a book not quite unknown on English medical jurisprudence; and likewise of the Italian case of the Countess Cornelia Baudi as set forth in detail by one Bianchini, prebendary of Verona, who wrote a scholarly work or so and was occasionally heard of in his time as having gleams of reason in him; and also of the testimony of Messrs. Fodere and Mere, two pestilent Frenchmen who WOULD investigate the subject; and further, of the corroborative testimony of Monsieur Le Cat, a rather celebrated French surgeon once upon a time, who had the unpoliteness to live in a house where such a case occurred and even to write an account of it—still they regard the late Mr. Krook’s obstinacy in going out of the world by any such by-way as wholly unjustifiable and personally offensive. The less the court understands of all this, the more the court likes it, and the greater enjoyment it has in the stock in trade of the Sol’s Arms. Then there comes the artist of a picture newspaper, with a foreground and figures ready drawn for anything from a wreck on the Cornish coast to a review in Hyde Park or a meeting in Manchester, and in Mrs. Perkins’ own room, memorable evermore, he then and there throws in upon the block Mr. Krook’s house, as large as life; in fact, considerably larger, making a very temple of it. Similarly, being permitted to look in at the door of the fatal chamber, he depicts that apartment as three-quarters of a mile long by fifty yards high, at which the court is particularly charmed. All this time the two gentlemen before mentioned pop in and out of every house and assist at the philosophical disputations—go everywhere and listen to everybody—and yet are always diving into the Sol’s parlour and writing with the ravenous little pens on the tissue-paper.\n\nAt last come the coroner and his inquiry, like as before, except that the coroner cherishes this case as being out of the common way and tells the gentlemen of the jury, in his private capacity, that “that would seem to be an unlucky house next door, gentlemen, a destined house; but so we sometimes find it, and these are mysteries we can’t account for!” After which the six-footer comes into action and is much admired.\n\nIn all these proceedings Mr. Guppy has so slight a part, except when he gives his evidence, that he is moved on like a private individual and can only haunt the secret house on the outside, where he has the mortification of seeing Mr. Smallweed padlocking the door, and of bitterly knowing himself to be shut out. But before these proceedings draw to a close, that is to say, on the night next after the catastrophe, Mr. Guppy has a thing to say that must be said to Lady Dedlock.\n\nFor which reason, with a sinking heart and with that hang-dog sense of guilt upon him which dread and watching enfolded in the Sol’s Arms have produced, the young man of the name of Guppy presents himself at the town mansion at about seven o’clock in the evening and requests to see her ladyship. Mercury replies that she is going out to dinner; don’t he see the carriage at the door? Yes, he does see the carriage at the door; but he wants to see my Lady too.\n\nMercury is disposed, as he will presently declare to a fellow-gentleman in waiting, “to pitch into the young man”; but his instructions are positive. Therefore he sulkily supposes that the young man must come up into the library. There he leaves the young man in a large room, not over-light, while he makes report of him.\n\nMr. Guppy looks into the shade in all directions, discovering everywhere a certain charred and whitened little heap of coal or wood. Presently he hears a rustling. Is it—? No, it’s no ghost, but fair flesh and blood, most brilliantly dressed.\n\n“I have to beg your ladyship’s pardon,” Mr. Guppy stammers, very downcast. “This is an inconvenient time—”\n\n“I told you, you could come at any time.” She takes a chair, looking straight at him as on the last occasion.\n\n“Thank your ladyship. Your ladyship is very affable.”\n\n“You can sit down.” There is not much affability in her tone.\n\n“I don’t know, your ladyship, that it’s worth while my sitting down and detaining you, for I—I have not got the letters that I mentioned when I had the honour of waiting on your ladyship.”\n\n“Have you come merely to say so?”\n\n“Merely to say so, your ladyship.” Mr. Guppy besides being depressed, disappointed, and uneasy, is put at a further disadvantage by the splendour and beauty of her appearance.\n\nShe knows its influence perfectly, has studied it too well to miss a grain of its effect on any one. As she looks at him so steadily and coldly, he not only feels conscious that he has no guide in the least perception of what is really the complexion of her thoughts, but also that he is being every moment, as it were, removed further and further from her.\n\nShe will not speak, it is plain. So he must.\n\n“In short, your ladyship,” says Mr. Guppy like a meanly penitent thief, “the person I was to have had the letters of, has come to a sudden end, and—” He stops. Lady Dedlock calmly finishes the sentence.\n\n“And the letters are destroyed with the person?”\n\nMr. Guppy would say no if he could—as he is unable to hide.\n\n“I believe so, your ladyship.”\n\nIf he could see the least sparkle of relief in her face now? No, he could see no such thing, even if that brave outside did not utterly put him away, and he were not looking beyond it and about it.\n\nHe falters an awkward excuse or two for his failure.\n\n“Is this all you have to say?” inquires Lady Dedlock, having heard him out—or as nearly out as he can stumble.\n\nMr. Guppy thinks that’s all.\n\n“You had better be sure that you wish to say nothing more to me, this being the last time you will have the opportunity.”\n\nMr. Guppy is quite sure. And indeed he has no such wish at present, by any means.\n\n“That is enough. I will dispense with excuses. Good evening to you!” And she rings for Mercury to show the young man of the name of Guppy out.\n\nBut in that house, in that same moment, there happens to be an old man of the name of Tulkinghorn. And that old man, coming with his quiet footstep to the library, has his hand at that moment on the handle of the door—comes in—and comes face to face with the young man as he is leaving the room.\n\nOne glance between the old man and the lady, and for an instant the blind that is always down flies up. Suspicion, eager and sharp, looks out. Another instant, close again.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Lady Dedlock. I beg your pardon a thousand times. It is so very unusual to find you here at this hour. I supposed the room was empty. I beg your pardon!”\n\n“Stay!” She negligently calls him back. “Remain here, I beg. I am going out to dinner. I have nothing more to say to this young man!”\n\nThe disconcerted young man bows, as he goes out, and cringingly hopes that Mr. Tulkinghorn of the Fields is well.\n\n“Aye, aye?” says the lawyer, looking at him from under his bent brows, though he has no need to look again—not he. “From Kenge and Carboy’s, surely?”\n\n“Kenge and Carboy’s, Mr. Tulkinghorn. Name of Guppy, sir.”\n\n“To be sure. Why, thank you, Mr. Guppy, I am very well!”\n\n“Happy to hear it, sir. You can’t be too well, sir, for the credit of the profession.”\n\n“Thank you, Mr. Guppy!”\n\nMr. Guppy sneaks away. Mr. Tulkinghorn, such a foil in his old-fashioned rusty black to Lady Dedlock’s brightness, hands her down the staircase to her carriage. He returns rubbing his chin, and rubs it a good deal in the course of the evening.\nA Turn of the Screw\n\n“Now, what,” says Mr. George, “may this be? Is it blank cartridge or ball? A flash in the pan or a shot?”\n\nAn open letter is the subject of the trooper’s speculations, and it seems to perplex him mightily. He looks at it at arm’s length, brings it close to him, holds it in his right hand, holds it in his left hand, reads it with his head on this side, with his head on that side, contracts his eyebrows, elevates them, still cannot satisfy himself. He smooths it out upon the table with his heavy palm, and thoughtfully walking up and down the gallery, makes a halt before it every now and then to come upon it with a fresh eye. Even that won’t do. “Is it,” Mr. George still muses, “blank cartridge or ball?”\n\nPhil Squod, with the aid of a brush and paint-pot, is employed in the distance whitening the targets, softly whistling in quick-march time and in drum-and-fife manner that he must and will go back again to the girl he left behind him.\n\n“Phil!” The trooper beckons as he calls him.\n\nPhil approaches in his usual way, sidling off at first as if he were going anywhere else and then bearing down upon his commander like a bayonet-charge. Certain splashes of white show in high relief upon his dirty face, and he scrapes his one eyebrow with the handle of the brush.\n\n“Attention, Phil! Listen to this.”\n\n“Steady, commander, steady.”\n\n“‘Sir. Allow me to remind you (though there is no legal necessity for my doing so, as you are aware) that the bill at two months’ date drawn on yourself by Mr. Matthew Bagnet, and by you accepted, for the sum of ninety-seven pounds four shillings and ninepence, will become due to-morrow, when you will please be prepared to take up the same on presentation. Yours, Joshua Smallweed.’ What do you make of that, Phil?”\n\n“Mischief, guv’ner.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“I think,” replies Phil after pensively tracing out a cross-wrinkle in his forehead with the brush-handle, “that mischeevious consequences is always meant when money’s asked for.”\n\n“Lookye, Phil,” says the trooper, sitting on the table. “First and last, I have paid, I may say, half as much again as this principal in interest and one thing and another.”\n\nPhil intimates by sidling back a pace or two, with a very unaccountable wrench of his wry face, that he does not regard the transaction as being made more promising by this incident.\n\n“And lookye further, Phil,” says the trooper, staying his premature conclusions with a wave of his hand. “There has always been an understanding that this bill was to be what they call renewed. And it has been renewed no end of times. What do you say now?”\n\n“I say that I think the times is come to a end at last.”\n\n“You do? Humph! I am much of the same mind myself.”\n\n“Joshua Smallweed is him that was brought here in a chair?”\n\n“The same.”\n\n“Guv’ner,” says Phil with exceeding gravity, “he’s a leech in his dispositions, he’s a screw and a wice in his actions, a snake in his twistings, and a lobster in his claws.”\n\nHaving thus expressively uttered his sentiments, Mr. Squod, after waiting a little to ascertain if any further remark be expected of him, gets back by his usual series of movements to the target he has in hand and vigorously signifies through his former musical medium that he must and he will return to that ideal young lady. George, having folded the letter, walks in that direction.\n\n“There IS a way, commander,” says Phil, looking cunningly at him, “of settling this.”\n\n“Paying the money, I suppose? I wish I could.”\n\nPhil shakes his head. “No, guv’ner, no; not so bad as that. There IS a way,” says Phil with a highly artistic turn of his brush; “what I’m a-doing at present.”\n\n“Whitewashing.”\n\n“A pretty way that would be! Do you know what would become of the Bagnets in that case? Do you know they would be ruined to pay off my old scores? YOU’RE a moral character,” says the trooper, eyeing him in his large way with no small indignation; “upon my life you are, Phil!”\n\nPhil, on one knee at the target, is in course of protesting earnestly, though not without many allegorical scoops of his brush and smoothings of the white surface round the rim with his thumb, that he had forgotten the Bagnet responsibility and would not so much as injure a hair of the head of any member of that worthy family when steps are audible in the long passage without, and a cheerful voice is heard to wonder whether George is at home. Phil, with a look at his master, hobbles up, saying, “Here’s the guv’ner, Mrs. Bagnet! Here he is!” and the old girl herself, accompanied by Mr. Bagnet, appears.\n\nThe old girl never appears in walking trim, in any season of the year, without a grey cloth cloak, coarse and much worn but very clean, which is, undoubtedly, the identical garment rendered so interesting to Mr. Bagnet by having made its way home to Europe from another quarter of the globe in company with Mrs. Bagnet and an umbrella. The latter faithful appendage is also invariably a part of the old girl’s presence out of doors. It is of no colour known in this life and has a corrugated wooden crook for a handle, with a metallic object let into its prow, or beak, resembling a little model of a fanlight over a street door or one of the oval glasses out of a pair of spectacles, which ornamental object has not that tenacious capacity of sticking to its post that might be desired in an article long associated with the British army. The old girl’s umbrella is of a flabby habit of waist and seems to be in need of stays—an appearance that is possibly referable to its having served through a series of years at home as a cupboard and on journeys as a carpet bag. She never puts it up, having the greatest reliance on her well-proved cloak with its capacious hood, but generally uses the instrument as a wand with which to point out joints of meat or bunches of greens in marketing or to arrest the attention of tradesmen by a friendly poke. Without her market-basket, which is a sort of wicker well with two flapping lids, she never stirs abroad. Attended by these her trusty companions, therefore, her honest sunburnt face looking cheerily out of a rough straw bonnet, Mrs. Bagnet now arrives, fresh-coloured and bright, in George’s Shooting Gallery.\n\n“Well, George, old fellow,” says she, “and how do YOU do, this sunshiny morning?”\n\nGiving him a friendly shake of the hand, Mrs. Bagnet draws a long breath after her walk and sits down to enjoy a rest. Having a faculty, matured on the tops of baggage-waggons and in other such positions, of resting easily anywhere, she perches on a rough bench, unties her bonnet-strings, pushes back her bonnet, crosses her arms, and looks perfectly comfortable.\n\nMr. Bagnet in the meantime has shaken hands with his old comrade and with Phil, on whom Mrs. Bagnet likewise bestows a good-humoured nod and smile.\n\n“Now, George,” said Mrs. Bagnet briskly, “here we are, Lignum and myself”—she often speaks of her husband by this appellation, on account, as it is supposed, of Lignum Vitae having been his old regimental nickname when they first became acquainted, in compliment to the extreme hardness and toughness of his physiognomy—“just looked in, we have, to make it all correct as usual about that security. Give him the new bill to sign, George, and he’ll sign it like a man.”\n\n“I was coming to you this morning,” observes the trooper reluctantly.\n\n“Yes, we thought you’d come to us this morning, but we turned out early and left Woolwich, the best of boys, to mind his sisters and came to you instead—as you see! For Lignum, he’s tied so close now, and gets so little exercise, that a walk does him good. But what’s the matter, George?” asks Mrs. Bagnet, stopping in her cheerful talk. “You don’t look yourself.”\n\n“I am not quite myself,” returns the trooper; “I have been a little put out, Mrs. Bagnet.”\n\nHer bright quick eye catches the truth directly. “George!” holding up her forefinger. “Don’t tell me there’s anything wrong about that security of Lignum’s! Don’t do it, George, on account of the children!”\n\nThe trooper looks at her with a troubled visage.\n\n“George,” says Mrs. Bagnet, using both her arms for emphasis and occasionally bringing down her open hands upon her knees. “If you have allowed anything wrong to come to that security of Lignum’s, and if you have let him in for it, and if you have put us in danger of being sold up—and I see sold up in your face, George, as plain as print—you have done a shameful action and have deceived us cruelly. I tell you, cruelly, George. There!”\n\nMr. Bagnet, otherwise as immovable as a pump or a lamp-post, puts his large right hand on the top of his bald head as if to defend it from a shower-bath and looks with great uneasiness at Mrs. Bagnet.\n\n“George,” says that old girl, “I wonder at you! George, I am ashamed of you! George, I couldn’t have believed you would have done it! I always knew you to be a rolling stone that gathered no moss, but I never thought you would have taken away what little moss there was for Bagnet and the children to lie upon. You know what a hard-working, steady-going chap he is. You know what Quebec and Malta and Woolwich are, and I never did think you would, or could, have had the heart to serve us so. Oh, George!” Mrs. Bagnet gathers up her cloak to wipe her eyes on in a very genuine manner, “How could you do it?”\n\nMrs. Bagnet ceasing, Mr. Bagnet removes his hand from his head as if the shower-bath were over and looks disconsolately at Mr. George, who has turned quite white and looks distressfully at the grey cloak and straw bonnet.\n\n“Mat,” says the trooper in a subdued voice, addressing him but still looking at his wife, “I am sorry you take it so much to heart, because I do hope it’s not so bad as that comes to. I certainly have, this morning, received this letter”—which he reads aloud—“but I hope it may be set right yet. As to a rolling stone, why, what you say is true. I AM a rolling stone, and I never rolled in anybody’s way, I fully believe, that I rolled the least good to. But it’s impossible for an old vagabond comrade to like your wife and family better than I like ’em, Mat, and I trust you’ll look upon me as forgivingly as you can. Don’t think I’ve kept anything from you. I haven’t had the letter more than a quarter of an hour.”\n\n“Old girl,” murmurs Mr. Bagnet after a short silence, “will you tell him my opinion?”\n\n“Oh! Why didn’t he marry,” Mrs. Bagnet answers, half laughing and half crying, “Joe Pouch’s widder in North America? Then he wouldn’t have got himself into these troubles.”\n\n“The old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet, “puts it correct—why didn’t you?”\n\n“Well, she has a better husband by this time, I hope,” returns the trooper. “Anyhow, here I stand, this present day, NOT married to Joe Pouch’s widder. What shall I do? You see all I have got about me. It’s not mine; it’s yours. Give the word, and I’ll sell off every morsel. If I could have hoped it would have brought in nearly the sum wanted, I’d have sold all long ago. Don’t believe that I’ll leave you or yours in the lurch, Mat. I’d sell myself first. I only wish,” says the trooper, giving himself a disparaging blow in the chest, “that I knew of any one who’d buy such a second-hand piece of old stores.”\n\n“Old girl,” murmurs Mr. Bagnet, “give him another bit of my mind.”\n\n“George,” says the old girl, “you are not so much to be blamed, on full consideration, except for ever taking this business without the means.”\n\n“And that was like me!” observes the penitent trooper, shaking his head. “Like me, I know.”\n\n“Silence! The old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet, “is correct—in her way of giving my opinions—hear me out!”\n\n“That was when you never ought to have asked for the security, George, and when you never ought to have got it, all things considered. But what’s done can’t be undone. You are always an honourable and straightforward fellow, as far as lays in your power, though a little flighty. On the other hand, you can’t admit but what it’s natural in us to be anxious with such a thing hanging over our heads. So forget and forgive all round, George. Come! Forget and forgive all round!”\n\nMrs. Bagnet, giving him one of her honest hands and giving her husband the other, Mr. George gives each of them one of his and holds them while he speaks.\n\n“I do assure you both, there’s nothing I wouldn’t do to discharge this obligation. But whatever I have been able to scrape together has gone every two months in keeping it up. We have lived plainly enough here, Phil and I. But the gallery don’t quite do what was expected of it, and it’s not—in short, it’s not the mint. It was wrong in me to take it? Well, so it was. But I was in a manner drawn into that step, and I thought it might steady me, and set me up, and you’ll try to overlook my having such expectations, and upon my soul, I am very much obliged to you, and very much ashamed of myself.” With these concluding words, Mr. George gives a shake to each of the hands he holds, and relinquishing them, backs a pace or two in a broad-chested, upright attitude, as if he had made a final confession and were immediately going to be shot with all military honours.\n\n“George, hear me out!” says Mr. Bagnet, glancing at his wife. “Old girl, go on!”\n\nMr. Bagnet, being in this singular manner heard out, has merely to observe that the letter must be attended to without any delay, that it is advisable that George and he should immediately wait on Mr. Smallweed in person, and that the primary object is to save and hold harmless Mr. Bagnet, who had none of the money. Mr. George, entirely assenting, puts on his hat and prepares to march with Mr. Bagnet to the enemy’s camp.\n\n“Don’t you mind a woman’s hasty word, George,” says Mrs. Bagnet, patting him on the shoulder. “I trust my old Lignum to you, and I am sure you’ll bring him through it.”\n\nThe trooper returns that this is kindly said and that he WILL bring Lignum through it somehow. Upon which Mrs. Bagnet, with her cloak, basket, and umbrella, goes home, bright-eyed again, to the rest of her family, and the comrades sally forth on the hopeful errand of mollifying Mr. Smallweed.\n\nWhether there are two people in England less likely to come satisfactorily out of any negotiation with Mr. Smallweed than Mr. George and Mr. Matthew Bagnet may be very reasonably questioned. Also, notwithstanding their martial appearance, broad square shoulders, and heavy tread, whether there are within the same limits two more simple and unaccustomed children in all the Smallweedy affairs of life. As they proceed with great gravity through the streets towards the region of Mount Pleasant, Mr. Bagnet, observing his companion to be thoughtful, considers it a friendly part to refer to Mrs. Bagnet’s late sally.\n\n“George, you know the old girl—she’s as sweet and as mild as milk. But touch her on the children—or myself—and she’s off like gunpowder.”\n\n“It does her credit, Mat!”\n\n“George,” says Mr. Bagnet, looking straight before him, “the old girl—can’t do anything—that don’t do her credit. More or less. I never say so. Discipline must be maintained.”\n\n“She’s worth her weight in gold,” says the trooper.\n\n“In gold?” says Mr. Bagnet. “I’ll tell you what. The old girl’s weight—is twelve stone six. Would I take that weight—in any metal—for the old girl? No. Why not? Because the old girl’s metal is far more precious—than the preciousest metal. And she’s ALL metal!”\n\n“You are right, Mat!”\n\n“When she took me—and accepted of the ring—she ’listed under me and the children—heart and head; for life. She’s that earnest,” says Mr. Bagnet, “and true to her colours—that, touch us with a finger—and she turns out—and stands to her arms. If the old girl fires wide—once in a way—at the call of duty—look over it, George. For she’s loyal!”\n\n“Why, bless her, Mat,” returns the trooper, “I think the higher of her for it!”\n\n“You are right!” says Mr. Bagnet with the warmest enthusiasm, though without relaxing the rigidity of a single muscle. “Think as high of the old girl—as the rock of Gibraltar—and still you’ll be thinking low—of such merits. But I never own to it before her. Discipline must be maintained.”\n\nThese encomiums bring them to Mount Pleasant and to Grandfather Smallweed’s house. The door is opened by the perennial Judy, who, having surveyed them from top to toe with no particular favour, but indeed with a malignant sneer, leaves them standing there while she consults the oracle as to their admission. The oracle may be inferred to give consent from the circumstance of her returning with the words on her honey lips that they can come in if they want to it. Thus privileged, they come in and find Mr. Smallweed with his feet in the drawer of his chair as if it were a paper foot-bath and Mrs. Smallweed obscured with the cushion like a bird that is not to sing.\n\n“My dear friend,” says Grandfather Smallweed with those two lean affectionate arms of his stretched forth. “How de do? How de do? Who is our friend, my dear friend?”\n\n“Why this,” returns George, not able to be very conciliatory at first, “is Matthew Bagnet, who has obliged me in that matter of ours, you know.”\n\n“Oh! Mr. Bagnet? Surely!” The old man looks at him under his hand.\n\n“Hope you’re well, Mr. Bagnet? Fine man, Mr. George! Military air, sir!”\n\nNo chairs being offered, Mr. George brings one forward for Bagnet and one for himself. They sit down, Mr. Bagnet as if he had no power of bending himself, except at the hips, for that purpose.\n\n“Judy,” says Mr. Smallweed, “bring the pipe.”\n\n“Why, I don’t know,” Mr. George interposes, “that the young woman need give herself that trouble, for to tell you the truth, I am not inclined to smoke it to-day.”\n\n“Ain’t you?” returns the old man. “Judy, bring the pipe.”\n\n“The fact is, Mr. Smallweed,” proceeds George, “that I find myself in rather an unpleasant state of mind. It appears to me, sir, that your friend in the city has been playing tricks.”\n\n“Oh, dear no!” says Grandfather Smallweed. “He never does that!”\n\n“Don’t he? Well, I am glad to hear it, because I thought it might be HIS doing. This, you know, I am speaking of. This letter.”\n\nGrandfather Smallweed smiles in a very ugly way in recognition of the letter.\n\n“What does it mean?” asks Mr. George.\n\n“Judy,” says the old man. “Have you got the pipe? Give it to me. Did you say what does it mean, my good friend?”\n\n“Aye! Now, come, come, you know, Mr. Smallweed,” urges the trooper, constraining himself to speak as smoothly and confidentially as he can, holding the open letter in one hand and resting the broad knuckles of the other on his thigh, “a good lot of money has passed between us, and we are face to face at the present moment, and are both well aware of the understanding there has always been. I am prepared to do the usual thing which I have done regularly and to keep this matter going. I never got a letter like this from you before, and I have been a little put about by it this morning, because here’s my friend Matthew Bagnet, who, you know, had none of the money—”\n\n“I DON’T know it, you know,” says the old man quietly.\n\n“Why, con-found you—it, I mean—I tell you so, don’t I?”\n\n“Oh, yes, you tell me so,” returns Grandfather Smallweed. “But I don’t know it.”\n\n“Well!” says the trooper, swallowing his fire. “I know it.”\n\nMr. Smallweed replies with excellent temper, “Ah! That’s quite another thing!” And adds, “But it don’t matter. Mr. Bagnet’s situation is all one, whether or no.”\n\nThe unfortunate George makes a great effort to arrange the affair comfortably and to propitiate Mr. Smallweed by taking him upon his own terms.\n\n“That’s just what I mean. As you say, Mr. Smallweed, here’s Matthew Bagnet liable to be fixed whether or no. Now, you see, that makes his good lady very uneasy in her mind, and me too, for whereas I’m a harum-scarum sort of a good-for-nought that more kicks than halfpence come natural to, why he’s a steady family man, don’t you see? Now, Mr. Smallweed,” says the trooper, gaining confidence as he proceeds in his soldierly mode of doing business, “although you and I are good friends enough in a certain sort of a way, I am well aware that I can’t ask you to let my friend Bagnet off entirely.”\n\n“Oh, dear, you are too modest. You can ASK me anything, Mr. George.” (There is an ogreish kind of jocularity in Grandfather Smallweed to-day.)\n\n“And you can refuse, you mean, eh? Or not you so much, perhaps, as your friend in the city? Ha ha ha!”\n\n“Ha ha ha!” echoes Grandfather Smallweed. In such a very hard manner and with eyes so particularly green that Mr. Bagnet’s natural gravity is much deepened by the contemplation of that venerable man.\n\n“Come!” says the sanguine George. “I am glad to find we can be pleasant, because I want to arrange this pleasantly. Here’s my friend Bagnet, and here am I. We’ll settle the matter on the spot, if you please, Mr. Smallweed, in the usual way. And you’ll ease my friend Bagnet’s mind, and his family’s mind, a good deal if you’ll just mention to him what our understanding is.”\n\nHere some shrill spectre cries out in a mocking manner, “Oh, good gracious! Oh!” Unless, indeed, it be the sportive Judy, who is found to be silent when the startled visitors look round, but whose chin has received a recent toss, expressive of derision and contempt. Mr. Bagnet’s gravity becomes yet more profound.\n\n“But I think you asked me, Mr. George”—old Smallweed, who all this time has had the pipe in his hand, is the speaker now—“I think you asked me, what did the letter mean?”\n\n“Why, yes, I did,” returns the trooper in his off-hand way, “but I don’t care to know particularly, if it’s all correct and pleasant.”\n\nMr. Smallweed, purposely balking himself in an aim at the trooper’s head, throws the pipe on the ground and breaks it to pieces.\n\n“That’s what it means, my dear friend. I’ll smash you. I’ll crumble you. I’ll powder you. Go to the devil!”\n\nThe two friends rise and look at one another. Mr. Bagnet’s gravity has now attained its profoundest point.\n\n“Go to the devil!” repeats the old man. “I’ll have no more of your pipe-smokings and swaggerings. What? You’re an independent dragoon, too! Go to my lawyer (you remember where; you have been there before) and show your independence now, will you? Come, my dear friend, there’s a chance for you. Open the street door, Judy; put these blusterers out! Call in help if they don’t go. Put ’em out!”\n\nHe vociferates this so loudly that Mr. Bagnet, laying his hands on the shoulders of his comrade before the latter can recover from his amazement, gets him on the outside of the street door, which is instantly slammed by the triumphant Judy. Utterly confounded, Mr. George awhile stands looking at the knocker. Mr. Bagnet, in a perfect abyss of gravity, walks up and down before the little parlour window like a sentry and looks in every time he passes, apparently revolving something in his mind.\n\n“Come, Mat,” says Mr. George when he has recovered himself, “we must try the lawyer. Now, what do you think of this rascal?”\n\nMr. Bagnet, stopping to take a farewell look into the parlour, replies with one shake of his head directed at the interior, “If my old girl had been here—I’d have told him!” Having so discharged himself of the subject of his cogitations, he falls into step and marches off with the trooper, shoulder to shoulder.\n\nWhen they present themselves in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, Mr. Tulkinghorn is engaged and not to be seen. He is not at all willing to see them, for when they have waited a full hour, and the clerk, on his bell being rung, takes the opportunity of mentioning as much, he brings forth no more encouraging message than that Mr. Tulkinghorn has nothing to say to them and they had better not wait. They do wait, however, with the perseverance of military tactics, and at last the bell rings again and the client in possession comes out of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s room.\n\nThe client is a handsome old lady, no other than Mrs. Rouncewell, housekeeper at Chesney Wold. She comes out of the sanctuary with a fair old-fashioned curtsy and softly shuts the door. She is treated with some distinction there, for the clerk steps out of his pew to show her through the outer office and to let her out. The old lady is thanking him for his attention when she observes the comrades in waiting.\n\n“I beg your pardon, sir, but I think those gentlemen are military?”\n\nThe clerk referring the question to them with his eye, and Mr. George not turning round from the almanac over the fire-place. Mr. Bagnet takes upon himself to reply, “Yes, ma’am. Formerly.”\n\n“I thought so. I was sure of it. My heart warms, gentlemen, at the sight of you. It always does at the sight of such. God bless you, gentlemen! You’ll excuse an old woman, but I had a son once who went for a soldier. A fine handsome youth he was, and good in his bold way, though some people did disparage him to his poor mother. I ask your pardon for troubling you, sir. God bless you, gentlemen!”\n\n“Same to you, ma’am!” returns Mr. Bagnet with right good will.\n\nThere is something very touching in the earnestness of the old lady’s voice and in the tremble that goes through her quaint old figure. But Mr. George is so occupied with the almanac over the fire-place (calculating the coming months by it perhaps) that he does not look round until she has gone away and the door is closed upon her.\n\n“George,” Mr. Bagnet gruffly whispers when he does turn from the almanac at last. “Don’t be cast down! ‘Why, soldiers, why—should we be melancholy, boys?’ Cheer up, my hearty!”\n\nThe clerk having now again gone in to say that they are still there and Mr. Tulkinghorn being heard to return with some irascibility, “Let ’em come in then!” they pass into the great room with the painted ceiling and find him standing before the fire.\n\n“Now, you men, what do you want? Sergeant, I told you the last time I saw you that I don’t desire your company here.”\n\nSergeant replies—dashed within the last few minutes as to his usual manner of speech, and even as to his usual carriage—that he has received this letter, has been to Mr. Smallweed about it, and has been referred there.\n\n“I have nothing to say to you,” rejoins Mr. Tulkinghorn. “If you get into debt, you must pay your debts or take the consequences. You have no occasion to come here to learn that, I suppose?”\n\nSergeant is sorry to say that he is not prepared with the money.\n\n“Very well! Then the other man—this man, if this is he—must pay it for you.”\n\nSergeant is sorry to add that the other man is not prepared with the money either.\n\n“Very well! Then you must pay it between you or you must both be sued for it and both suffer. You have had the money and must refund it. You are not to pocket other people’s pounds, shillings, and pence and escape scot-free.”\n\nThe lawyer sits down in his easy-chair and stirs the fire. Mr. George hopes he will have the goodness to—“I tell you, sergeant, I have nothing to say to you. I don’t like your associates and don’t want you here. This matter is not at all in my course of practice and is not in my office. Mr. Smallweed is good enough to offer these affairs to me, but they are not in my way. You must go to Melchisedech’s in Clifford’s Inn.”\n\n“I must make an apology to you, sir,” says Mr. George, “for pressing myself upon you with so little encouragement—which is almost as unpleasant to me as it can be to you—but would you let me say a private word to you?”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn rises with his hands in his pockets and walks into one of the window recesses. “Now! I have no time to waste.” In the midst of his perfect assumption of indifference, he directs a sharp look at the trooper, taking care to stand with his own back to the light and to have the other with his face towards it.\n\n“Well, sir,” says Mr. George, “this man with me is the other party implicated in this unfortunate affair—nominally, only nominally—and my sole object is to prevent his getting into trouble on my account. He is a most respectable man with a wife and family, formerly in the Royal Artillery—”\n\n“My friend, I don’t care a pinch of snuff for the whole Royal Artillery establishment—officers, men, tumbrils, waggons, horses, guns, and ammunition.”\n\n“’Tis likely, sir. But I care a good deal for Bagnet and his wife and family being injured on my account. And if I could bring them through this matter, I should have no help for it but to give up without any other consideration what you wanted of me the other day.”\n\n“Have you got it here?”\n\n“I have got it here, sir.”\n\n“Sergeant,” the lawyer proceeds in his dry passionless manner, far more hopeless in the dealing with than any amount of vehemence, “make up your mind while I speak to you, for this is final. After I have finished speaking I have closed the subject, and I won’t re-open it. Understand that. You can leave here, for a few days, what you say you have brought here if you choose; you can take it away at once if you choose. In case you choose to leave it here, I can do this for you—I can replace this matter on its old footing, and I can go so far besides as to give you a written undertaking that this man Bagnet shall never be troubled in any way until you have been proceeded against to the utmost, that your means shall be exhausted before the creditor looks to his. This is in fact all but freeing him. Have you decided?”\n\nThe trooper puts his hand into his breast and answers with a long breath, “I must do it, sir.”\n\nSo Mr. Tulkinghorn, putting on his spectacles, sits down and writes the undertaking, which he slowly reads and explains to Bagnet, who has all this time been staring at the ceiling and who puts his hand on his bald head again, under this new verbal shower-bath, and seems exceedingly in need of the old girl through whom to express his sentiments. The trooper then takes from his breast-pocket a folded paper, which he lays with an unwilling hand at the lawyer’s elbow. “’Tis only a letter of instructions, sir. The last I ever had from him.”\n\nLook at a millstone, Mr. George, for some change in its expression, and you will find it quite as soon as in the face of Mr. Tulkinghorn when he opens and reads the letter! He refolds it and lays it in his desk with a countenance as unperturbable as death.\n\nNor has he anything more to say or do but to nod once in the same frigid and discourteous manner and to say briefly, “You can go. Show these men out, there!” Being shown out, they repair to Mr. Bagnet’s residence to dine.\n\nBoiled beef and greens constitute the day’s variety on the former repast of boiled pork and greens, and Mrs. Bagnet serves out the meal in the same way and seasons it with the best of temper, being that rare sort of old girl that she receives Good to her arms without a hint that it might be Better and catches light from any little spot of darkness near her. The spot on this occasion is the darkened brow of Mr. George; he is unusually thoughtful and depressed. At first Mrs. Bagnet trusts to the combined endearments of Quebec and Malta to restore him, but finding those young ladies sensible that their existing Bluffy is not the Bluffy of their usual frolicsome acquaintance, she winks off the light infantry and leaves him to deploy at leisure on the open ground of the domestic hearth.\n\nBut he does not. He remains in close order, clouded and depressed. During the lengthy cleaning up and pattening process, when he and Mr. Bagnet are supplied with their pipes, he is no better than he was at dinner. He forgets to smoke, looks at the fire and ponders, lets his pipe out, fills the breast of Mr. Bagnet with perturbation and dismay by showing that he has no enjoyment of tobacco.\n\nTherefore when Mrs. Bagnet at last appears, rosy from the invigorating pail, and sits down to her work, Mr. Bagnet growls, “Old girl!” and winks monitions to her to find out what’s the matter.\n\n“Why, George!” says Mrs. Bagnet, quietly threading her needle. “How low you are!”\n\n“Am I? Not good company? Well, I am afraid I am not.”\n\n“He ain’t at all like Bluffy, mother!” cries little Malta.\n\n“Because he ain’t well, I think, mother,” adds Quebec.\n\n“Sure that’s a bad sign not to be like Bluffy, too!” returns the trooper, kissing the young damsels. “But it’s true,” with a sigh, “true, I am afraid. These little ones are always right!”\n\n“George,” says Mrs. Bagnet, working busily, “if I thought you cross enough to think of anything that a shrill old soldier’s wife—who could have bitten her tongue off afterwards and ought to have done it almost—said this morning, I don’t know what I shouldn’t say to you now.”\n\n“My kind soul of a darling,” returns the trooper. “Not a morsel of it.”\n\n“Because really and truly, George, what I said and meant to say was that I trusted Lignum to you and was sure you’d bring him through it. And you HAVE brought him through it, noble!”\n\n“Thankee, my dear!” says George. “I am glad of your good opinion.”\n\nIn giving Mrs. Bagnet’s hand, with her work in it, a friendly shake—for she took her seat beside him—the trooper’s attention is attracted to her face. After looking at it for a little while as she plies her needle, he looks to young Woolwich, sitting on his stool in the corner, and beckons that fifer to him.\n\n“See there, my boy,” says George, very gently smoothing the mother’s hair with his hand, “there’s a good loving forehead for you! All bright with love of you, my boy. A little touched by the sun and the weather through following your father about and taking care of you, but as fresh and wholesome as a ripe apple on a tree.”\n\nMr. Bagnet’s face expresses, so far as in its wooden material lies, the highest approbation and acquiescence.\n\n“The time will come, my boy,” pursues the trooper, “when this hair of your mother’s will be grey, and this forehead all crossed and re-crossed with wrinkles, and a fine old lady she’ll be then. Take care, while you are young, that you can think in those days, ‘I never whitened a hair of her dear head—I never marked a sorrowful line in her face!’ For of all the many things that you can think of when you are a man, you had better have THAT by you, Woolwich!”\n\nMr. George concludes by rising from his chair, seating the boy beside his mother in it, and saying, with something of a hurry about him, that he’ll smoke his pipe in the street a bit.\n\nEsther’s Narrative\nI lay ill through several weeks, and the usual tenor of my life became like an old remembrance. But this was not the effect of time so much as of the change in all my habits made by the helplessness and inaction of a sick-room. Before I had been confined to it many days, everything else seemed to have retired into a remote distance where there was little or no separation between the various stages of my life which had been really divided by years. In falling ill, I seemed to have crossed a dark lake and to have left all my experiences, mingled together by the great distance, on the healthy shore.\n\nMy housekeeping duties, though at first it caused me great anxiety to think that they were unperformed, were soon as far off as the oldest of the old duties at Greenleaf or the summer afternoons when I went home from school with my portfolio under my arm, and my childish shadow at my side, to my godmother’s house. I had never known before how short life really was and into how small a space the mind could put it.\n\nWhile I was very ill, the way in which these divisions of time became confused with one another distressed my mind exceedingly. At once a child, an elder girl, and the little woman I had been so happy as, I was not only oppressed by cares and difficulties adapted to each station, but by the great perplexity of endlessly trying to reconcile them. I suppose that few who have not been in such a condition can quite understand what I mean or what painful unrest arose from this source.\n\nFor the same reason I am almost afraid to hint at that time in my disorder—it seemed one long night, but I believe there were both nights and days in it—when I laboured up colossal staircases, ever striving to reach the top, and ever turned, as I have seen a worm in a garden path, by some obstruction, and labouring again. I knew perfectly at intervals, and I think vaguely at most times, that I was in my bed; and I talked with Charley, and felt her touch, and knew her very well; yet I would find myself complaining, “Oh, more of these never-ending stairs, Charley—more and more—piled up to the sky’, I think!” and labouring on again.\n\nDare I hint at that worse time when, strung together somewhere in great black space, there was a flaming necklace, or ring, or starry circle of some kind, of which I was one of the beads! And when my only prayer was to be taken off from the rest and when it was such inexplicable agony and misery to be a part of the dreadful thing?\n\nPerhaps the less I say of these sick experiences, the less tedious and the more intelligible I shall be. I do not recall them to make others unhappy or because I am now the least unhappy in remembering them. It may be that if we knew more of such strange afflictions we might be the better able to alleviate their intensity.\n\nThe repose that succeeded, the long delicious sleep, the blissful rest, when in my weakness I was too calm to have any care for myself and could have heard (or so I think now) that I was dying, with no other emotion than with a pitying love for those I left behind—this state can be perhaps more widely understood. I was in this state when I first shrunk from the light as it twinkled on me once more, and knew with a boundless joy for which no words are rapturous enough that I should see again.\n\nI had heard my Ada crying at the door, day and night; I had heard her calling to me that I was cruel and did not love her; I had heard her praying and imploring to be let in to nurse and comfort me and to leave my bedside no more; but I had only said, when I could speak, “Never, my sweet girl, never!” and I had over and over again reminded Charley that she was to keep my darling from the room whether I lived or died. Charley had been true to me in that time of need, and with her little hand and her great heart had kept the door fast.\n\nBut now, my sight strengthening and the glorious light coming every day more fully and brightly on me, I could read the letters that my dear wrote to me every morning and evening and could put them to my lips and lay my cheek upon them with no fear of hurting her. I could see my little maid, so tender and so careful, going about the two rooms setting everything in order and speaking cheerfully to Ada from the open window again. I could understand the stillness in the house and the thoughtfulness it expressed on the part of all those who had always been so good to me. I could weep in the exquisite felicity of my heart and be as happy in my weakness as ever I had been in my strength.\n\nBy and by my strength began to be restored. Instead of lying, with so strange a calmness, watching what was done for me, as if it were done for some one else whom I was quietly sorry for, I helped it a little, and so on to a little more and much more, until I became useful to myself, and interested, and attached to life again.\n\nHow well I remember the pleasant afternoon when I was raised in bed with pillows for the first time to enjoy a great tea-drinking with Charley! The little creature—sent into the world, surely, to minister to the weak and sick—was so happy, and so busy, and stopped so often in her preparations to lay her head upon my bosom, and fondle me, and cry with joyful tears she was so glad, she was so glad, that I was obliged to say, “Charley, if you go on in this way, I must lie down again, my darling, for I am weaker than I thought I was!” So Charley became as quiet as a mouse and took her bright face here and there across and across the two rooms, out of the shade into the divine sunshine, and out of the sunshine into the shade, while I watched her peacefully. When all her preparations were concluded and the pretty tea-table with its little delicacies to tempt me, and its white cloth, and its flowers, and everything so lovingly and beautifully arranged for me by Ada downstairs, was ready at the bedside, I felt sure I was steady enough to say something to Charley that was not new to my thoughts.\n\nFirst I complimented Charley on the room, and indeed it was so fresh and airy, so spotless and neat, that I could scarce believe I had been lying there so long. This delighted Charley, and her face was brighter than before.\n\n“Yet, Charley,” said I, looking round, “I miss something, surely, that I am accustomed to?”\n\nPoor little Charley looked round too and pretended to shake her head as if there were nothing absent.\n\n“Are the pictures all as they used to be?” I asked her.\n\n“Every one of them, miss,” said Charley.\n\n“And the furniture, Charley?”\n\n“Except where I have moved it about to make more room, miss.”\n\n“And yet,” said I, “I miss some familiar object. Ah, I know what it is, Charley! It’s the looking-glass.”\n\nCharley got up from the table, making as if she had forgotten something, and went into the next room; and I heard her sob there.\n\nI had thought of this very often. I was now certain of it. I could thank God that it was not a shock to me now. I called Charley back, and when she came—at first pretending to smile, but as she drew nearer to me, looking grieved—I took her in my arms and said, “It matters very little, Charley. I hope I can do without my old face very well.”\n\nI was presently so far advanced as to be able to sit up in a great chair and even giddily to walk into the adjoining room, leaning on Charley. The mirror was gone from its usual place in that room too, but what I had to bear was none the harder to bear for that.\n\nMy guardian had throughout been earnest to visit me, and there was now no good reason why I should deny myself that happiness. He came one morning, and when he first came in, could only hold me in his embrace and say, “My dear, dear girl!” I had long known—who could know better?—what a deep fountain of affection and generosity his heart was; and was it not worth my trivial suffering and change to fill such a place in it? “Oh, yes!” I thought. “He has seen me, and he loves me better than he did; he has seen me and is even fonder of me than he was before; and what have I to mourn for!”\n\nHe sat down by me on the sofa, supporting me with his arm. For a little while he sat with his hand over his face, but when he removed it, fell into his usual manner. There never can have been, there never can be, a pleasanter manner.\n\n“My little woman,” said he, “what a sad time this has been. Such an inflexible little woman, too, through all!”\n\n“Only for the best, guardian,” said I.\n\n“For the best?” he repeated tenderly. “Of course, for the best. But here have Ada and I been perfectly forlorn and miserable; here has your friend Caddy been coming and going late and early; here has every one about the house been utterly lost and dejected; here has even poor Rick been writing—to ME too—in his anxiety for you!”\n\nI had read of Caddy in Ada’s letters, but not of Richard. I told him so.\n\n“Why, no, my dear,” he replied. “I have thought it better not to mention it to her.”\n\n“And you speak of his writing to YOU,” said I, repeating his emphasis. “As if it were not natural for him to do so, guardian; as if he could write to a better friend!”\n\n“He thinks he could, my love,” returned my guardian, “and to many a better. The truth is, he wrote to me under a sort of protest while unable to write to you with any hope of an answer—wrote coldly, haughtily, distantly, resentfully. Well, dearest little woman, we must look forbearingly on it. He is not to blame. Jarndyce and Jarndyce has warped him out of himself and perverted me in his eyes. I have known it do as bad deeds, and worse, many a time. If two angels could be concerned in it, I believe it would change their nature.”\n\n“It has not changed yours, guardian.”\n\n“Oh, yes, it has, my dear,” he said laughingly. “It has made the south wind easterly, I don’t know how often. Rick mistrusts and suspects me—goes to lawyers, and is taught to mistrust and suspect me. Hears I have conflicting interests, claims clashing against his and what not. Whereas, heaven knows that if I could get out of the mountains of Wiglomeration on which my unfortunate name has been so long bestowed (which I can’t) or could level them by the extinction of my own original right (which I can’t either, and no human power ever can, anyhow, I believe, to such a pass have we got), I would do it this hour. I would rather restore to poor Rick his proper nature than be endowed with all the money that dead suitors, broken, heart and soul, upon the wheel of Chancery, have left unclaimed with the Accountant-General—and that’s money enough, my dear, to be cast into a pyramid, in memory of Chancery’s transcendent wickedness.”\n\n“IS it possible, guardian,” I asked, amazed, “that Richard can be suspicious of you?”\n\n“Ah, my love, my love,” he said, “it is in the subtle poison of such abuses to breed such diseases. His blood is infected, and objects lose their natural aspects in his sight. It is not HIS fault.”\n\n“But it is a terrible misfortune, guardian.”\n\n“It is a terrible misfortune, little woman, to be ever drawn within the influences of Jarndyce and Jarndyce. I know none greater. By little and little he has been induced to trust in that rotten reed, and it communicates some portion of its rottenness to everything around him. But again I say with all my soul, we must be patient with poor Rick and not blame him. What a troop of fine fresh hearts like his have I seen in my time turned by the same means!”\n\nI could not help expressing something of my wonder and regret that his benevolent, disinterested intentions had prospered so little.\n\n“We must not say so, Dame Durden,” he cheerfully replied; “Ada is the happier, I hope, and that is much. I did think that I and both these young creatures might be friends instead of distrustful foes and that we might so far counter-act the suit and prove too strong for it. But it was too much to expect. Jarndyce and Jarndyce was the curtain of Rick’s cradle.”\n\n“But, guardian, may we not hope that a little experience will teach him what a false and wretched thing it is?”\n\n“We WILL hope so, my Esther,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “and that it may not teach him so too late. In any case we must not be hard on him. There are not many grown and matured men living while we speak, good men too, who if they were thrown into this same court as suitors would not be vitally changed and depreciated within three years—within two—within one. How can we stand amazed at poor Rick? A young man so unfortunate,” here he fell into a lower tone, as if he were thinking aloud, “cannot at first believe (who could?) that Chancery is what it is. He looks to it, flushed and fitfully, to do something with his interests and bring them to some settlement. It procrastinates, disappoints, tries, tortures him; wears out his sanguine hopes and patience, thread by thread; but he still looks to it, and hankers after it, and finds his whole world treacherous and hollow. Well, well, well! Enough of this, my dear!”\n\nHe had supported me, as at first, all this time, and his tenderness was so precious to me that I leaned my head upon his shoulder and loved him as if he had been my father. I resolved in my own mind in this little pause, by some means, to see Richard when I grew strong and try to set him right.\n\n“There are better subjects than these,” said my guardian, “for such a joyful time as the time of our dear girl’s recovery. And I had a commission to broach one of them as soon as I should begin to talk. When shall Ada come to see you, my love?”\n\nI had been thinking of that too. A little in connexion with the absent mirrors, but not much, for I knew my loving girl would be changed by no change in my looks.\n\n“Dear guardian,” said I, “as I have shut her out so long—though indeed, indeed, she is like the light to me—”\n\n“I know it well, Dame Durden, well.”\n\nHe was so good, his touch expressed such endearing compassion and affection, and the tone of his voice carried such comfort into my heart that I stopped for a little while, quite unable to go on. “Yes, yes, you are tired,” said he. “Rest a little.”\n\n“As I have kept Ada out so long,” I began afresh after a short while, “I think I should like to have my own way a little longer, guardian. It would be best to be away from here before I see her. If Charley and I were to go to some country lodging as soon as I can move, and if I had a week there in which to grow stronger and to be revived by the sweet air and to look forward to the happiness of having Ada with me again, I think it would be better for us.”\n\nI hope it was not a poor thing in me to wish to be a little more used to my altered self before I met the eyes of the dear girl I longed so ardently to see, but it is the truth. I did. He understood me, I was sure; but I was not afraid of that. If it were a poor thing, I knew he would pass it over.\n\n“Our spoilt little woman,” said my guardian, “shall have her own way even in her inflexibility, though at the price, I know, of tears downstairs. And see here! Here is Boythorn, heart of chivalry, breathing such ferocious vows as never were breathed on paper before, that if you don’t go and occupy his whole house, he having already turned out of it expressly for that purpose, by heaven and by earth he’ll pull it down and not leave one brick standing on another!”\n\nAnd my guardian put a letter in my hand, without any ordinary beginning such as “My dear Jarndyce,” but rushing at once into the words, “I swear if Miss Summerson do not come down and take possession of my house, which I vacate for her this day at one o’clock, P.M.,” and then with the utmost seriousness, and in the most emphatic terms, going on to make the extraordinary declaration he had quoted. We did not appreciate the writer the less for laughing heartily over it, and we settled that I should send him a letter of thanks on the morrow and accept his offer. It was a most agreeable one to me, for all the places I could have thought of, I should have liked to go to none so well as Chesney Wold.\n\n“Now, little housewife,” said my guardian, looking at his watch, “I was strictly timed before I came upstairs, for you must not be tired too soon; and my time has waned away to the last minute. I have one other petition. Little Miss Flite, hearing a rumour that you were ill, made nothing of walking down here—twenty miles, poor soul, in a pair of dancing shoes—to inquire. It was heaven’s mercy we were at home, or she would have walked back again.”\n\nThe old conspiracy to make me happy! Everybody seemed to be in it!\n\n“Now, pet,” said my guardian, “if it would not be irksome to you to admit the harmless little creature one afternoon before you save Boythorn’s otherwise devoted house from demolition, I believe you would make her prouder and better pleased with herself than I—though my eminent name is Jarndyce—could do in a lifetime.”\n\nI have no doubt he knew there would be something in the simple image of the poor afflicted creature that would fall like a gentle lesson on my mind at that time. I felt it as he spoke to me. I could not tell him heartily enough how ready I was to receive her. I had always pitied her, never so much as now. I had always been glad of my little power to soothe her under her calamity, but never, never, half so glad before.\n\nWe arranged a time for Miss Flite to come out by the coach and share my early dinner. When my guardian left me, I turned my face away upon my couch and prayed to be forgiven if I, surrounded by such blessings, had magnified to myself the little trial that I had to undergo. The childish prayer of that old birthday when I had aspired to be industrious, contented, and true-hearted and to do good to some one and win some love to myself if I could came back into my mind with a reproachful sense of all the happiness I had since enjoyed and all the affectionate hearts that had been turned towards me. If I were weak now, what had I profited by those mercies? I repeated the old childish prayer in its old childish words and found that its old peace had not departed from it.\n\nMy guardian now came every day. In a week or so more I could walk about our rooms and hold long talks with Ada from behind the window-curtain. Yet I never saw her, for I had not as yet the courage to look at the dear face, though I could have done so easily without her seeing me.\n\nOn the appointed day Miss Flite arrived. The poor little creature ran into my room quite forgetful of her usual dignity, and crying from her very heart of hearts, “My dear Fitz Jarndyce!” fell upon my neck and kissed me twenty times.\n\n“Dear me!” said she, putting her hand into her reticule, “I have nothing here but documents, my dear Fitz Jarndyce; I must borrow a pocket handkerchief.”\n\nCharley gave her one, and the good creature certainly made use of it, for she held it to her eyes with both hands and sat so, shedding tears for the next ten minutes.\n\n“With pleasure, my dear Fitz Jarndyce,” she was careful to explain. “Not the least pain. Pleasure to see you well again. Pleasure at having the honour of being admitted to see you. I am so much fonder of you, my love, than of the Chancellor. Though I DO attend court regularly. By the by, my dear, mentioning pocket handkerchiefs—”\n\nMiss Flite here looked at Charley, who had been to meet her at the place where the coach stopped. Charley glanced at me and looked unwilling to pursue the suggestion.\n\n“Ve-ry right!” said Miss Flite, “Ve-ry correct. Truly! Highly indiscreet of me to mention it; but my dear Miss Fitz Jarndyce, I am afraid I am at times (between ourselves, you wouldn’t think it) a little—rambling you know,” said Miss Flite, touching her forehead. “Nothing more.”\n\n“What were you going to tell me?” said I, smiling, for I saw she wanted to go on. “You have roused my curiosity, and now you must gratify it.”\n\nMiss Flite looked at Charley for advice in this important crisis, who said, “If you please, ma’am, you had better tell then,” and therein gratified Miss Flite beyond measure.\n\n“So sagacious, our young friend,” said she to me in her mysterious way. “Diminutive. But ve-ry sagacious! Well, my dear, it’s a pretty anecdote. Nothing more. Still I think it charming. Who should follow us down the road from the coach, my dear, but a poor person in a very ungenteel bonnet—”\n\n“Jenny, if you please, miss,” said Charley.\n\n“Just so!” Miss Flite acquiesced with the greatest suavity. “Jenny. Ye-es! And what does she tell our young friend but that there has been a lady with a veil inquiring at her cottage after my dear Fitz Jarndyce’s health and taking a handkerchief away with her as a little keepsake merely because it was my amiable Fitz Jarndyce’s! Now, you know, so very prepossessing in the lady with the veil!”\n\n“If you please, miss,” said Charley, to whom I looked in some astonishment, “Jenny says that when her baby died, you left a handkerchief there, and that she put it away and kept it with the baby’s little things. I think, if you please, partly because it was yours, miss, and partly because it had covered the baby.”\n\n“Diminutive,” whispered Miss Flite, making a variety of motions about her own forehead to express intellect in Charley. “But exceedingly sagacious! And so dear! My love, she’s clearer than any counsel I ever heard!”\n\n“Yes, Charley,” I returned. “I remember it. Well?”\n\n“Well, miss,” said Charley, “and that’s the handkerchief the lady took. And Jenny wants you to know that she wouldn’t have made away with it herself for a heap of money but that the lady took it and left some money instead. Jenny don’t know her at all, if you please, miss!”\n\n“Why, who can she be?” said I.\n\n“My love,” Miss Flite suggested, advancing her lips to my ear with her most mysterious look, “in MY opinion—don’t mention this to our diminutive friend—she’s the Lord Chancellor’s wife. He’s married, you know. And I understand she leads him a terrible life. Throws his lordship’s papers into the fire, my dear, if he won’t pay the jeweller!”\n\nI did not think very much about this lady then, for I had an impression that it might be Caddy. Besides, my attention was diverted by my visitor, who was cold after her ride and looked hungry and who, our dinner being brought in, required some little assistance in arraying herself with great satisfaction in a pitiable old scarf and a much-worn and often-mended pair of gloves, which she had brought down in a paper parcel. I had to preside, too, over the entertainment, consisting of a dish of fish, a roast fowl, a sweetbread, vegetables, pudding, and Madeira; and it was so pleasant to see how she enjoyed it, and with what state and ceremony she did honour to it, that I was soon thinking of nothing else.\n\nWhen we had finished and had our little dessert before us, embellished by the hands of my dear, who would yield the superintendence of everything prepared for me to no one, Miss Flite was so very chatty and happy that I thought I would lead her to her own history, as she was always pleased to talk about herself. I began by saying “You have attended on the Lord Chancellor many years, Miss Flite?”\n\n“Oh, many, many, many years, my dear. But I expect a judgment. Shortly.”\n\nThere was an anxiety even in her hopefulness that made me doubtful if I had done right in approaching the subject. I thought I would say no more about it.\n\n“My father expected a judgment,” said Miss Flite. “My brother. My sister. They all expected a judgment. The same that I expect.”\n\n“They are all—”\n\n“Ye-es. Dead of course, my dear,” said she.\n\nAs I saw she would go on, I thought it best to try to be serviceable to her by meeting the theme rather than avoiding it.\n\n“Would it not be wiser,” said I, “to expect this judgment no more?”\n\n“Why, my dear,” she answered promptly, “of course it would!”\n\n“And to attend the court no more?”\n\n“Equally of course,” said she. “Very wearing to be always in expectation of what never comes, my dear Fitz Jarndyce! Wearing, I assure you, to the bone!”\n\nShe slightly showed me her arm, and it was fearfully thin indeed.\n\n“But, my dear,” she went on in her mysterious way, “there’s a dreadful attraction in the place. Hush! Don’t mention it to our diminutive friend when she comes in. Or it may frighten her. With good reason. There’s a cruel attraction in the place. You CAN’T leave it. And you MUST expect.”\n\nI tried to assure her that this was not so. She heard me patiently and smilingly, but was ready with her own answer.\n\n“Aye, aye, aye! You think so because I am a little rambling. Ve-ry absurd, to be a little rambling, is it not? Ve-ry confusing, too. To the head. I find it so. But, my dear, I have been there many years, and I have noticed. It’s the mace and seal upon the table.”\n\nWhat could they do, did she think? I mildly asked her.\n\n“Draw,” returned Miss Flite. “Draw people on, my dear. Draw peace out of them. Sense out of them. Good looks out of them. Good qualities out of them. I have felt them even drawing my rest away in the night. Cold and glittering devils!”\n\nShe tapped me several times upon the arm and nodded good-humouredly as if she were anxious I should understand that I had no cause to fear her, though she spoke so gloomily, and confided these awful secrets to me.\n\n“Let me see,” said she. “I’ll tell you my own case. Before they ever drew me—before I had ever seen them—what was it I used to do? Tambourine playing? No. Tambour work. I and my sister worked at tambour work. Our father and our brother had a builder’s business. We all lived together. Ve-ry respectably, my dear! First, our father was drawn—slowly. Home was drawn with him. In a few years he was a fierce, sour, angry bankrupt without a kind word or a kind look for any one. He had been so different, Fitz Jarndyce. He was drawn to a debtors’ prison. There he died. Then our brother was drawn—swiftly—to drunkenness. And rags. And death. Then my sister was drawn. Hush! Never ask to what! Then I was ill and in misery, and heard, as I had often heard before, that this was all the work of Chancery. When I got better, I went to look at the monster. And then I found out how it was, and I was drawn to stay there.”\n\nHaving got over her own short narrative, in the delivery of which she had spoken in a low, strained voice, as if the shock were fresh upon her, she gradually resumed her usual air of amiable importance.\n\n“You don’t quite credit me, my dear! Well, well! You will, some day. I am a little rambling. But I have noticed. I have seen many new faces come, unsuspicious, within the influence of the mace and seal in these many years. As my father’s came there. As my brother’s. As my sister’s. As my own. I hear Conversation Kenge and the rest of them say to the new faces, ‘Here’s little Miss Flite. Oh, you are new here; and you must come and be presented to little Miss Flite!’ Ve-ry good. Proud I am sure to have the honour! And we all laugh. But, Fitz Jarndyce, I know what will happen. I know, far better than they do, when the attraction has begun. I know the signs, my dear. I saw them begin in Gridley. And I saw them end. Fitz Jarndyce, my love,” speaking low again, “I saw them beginning in our friend the ward in Jarndyce. Let some one hold him back. Or he’ll be drawn to ruin.”\n\nShe looked at me in silence for some moments, with her face gradually softening into a smile. Seeming to fear that she had been too gloomy, and seeming also to lose the connexion in her mind, she said politely as she sipped her glass of wine, “Yes, my dear, as I was saying, I expect a judgment shortly. Then I shall release my birds, you know, and confer estates.”\n\nI was much impressed by her allusion to Richard and by the sad meaning, so sadly illustrated in her poor pinched form, that made its way through all her incoherence. But happily for her, she was quite complacent again now and beamed with nods and smiles.\n\n“But, my dear,” she said, gaily, reaching another hand to put it upon mine. “You have not congratulated me on my physician. Positively not once, yet!”\n\nI was obliged to confess that I did not quite know what she meant.\n\n“My physician, Mr. Woodcourt, my dear, who was so exceedingly attentive to me. Though his services were rendered quite gratuitously. Until the Day of Judgment. I mean THE judgment that will dissolve the spell upon me of the mace and seal.”\n\n“Mr. Woodcourt is so far away, now,” said I, “that I thought the time for such congratulation was past, Miss Flite.”\n\n“But, my child,” she returned, “is it possible that you don’t know what has happened?”\n\n“No,” said I.\n\n“Not what everybody has been talking of, my beloved Fitz Jarndyce!”\n\n“No,” said I. “You forget how long I have been here.”\n\n“True! My dear, for the moment—true. I blame myself. But my memory has been drawn out of me, with everything else, by what I mentioned. Ve-ry strong influence, is it not? Well, my dear, there has been a terrible shipwreck over in those East Indian seas.”\n\n“Mr. Woodcourt shipwrecked!”\n\n“Don’t be agitated, my dear. He is safe. An awful scene. Death in all shapes. Hundreds of dead and dying. Fire, storm, and darkness. Numbers of the drowning thrown upon a rock. There, and through it all, my dear physician was a hero. Calm and brave through everything. Saved many lives, never complained in hunger and thirst, wrapped naked people in his spare clothes, took the lead, showed them what to do, governed them, tended the sick, buried the dead, and brought the poor survivors safely off at last! My dear, the poor emaciated creatures all but worshipped him. They fell down at his feet when they got to the land and blessed him. The whole country rings with it. Stay! Where’s my bag of documents? I have got it there, and you shall read it, you shall read it!”\n\nAnd I DID read all the noble history, though very slowly and imperfectly then, for my eyes were so dimmed that I could not see the words, and I cried so much that I was many times obliged to lay down the long account she had cut out of the newspaper. I felt so triumphant ever to have known the man who had done such generous and gallant deeds, I felt such glowing exultation in his renown, I so admired and loved what he had done, that I envied the storm-worn people who had fallen at his feet and blessed him as their preserver. I could myself have kneeled down then, so far away, and blessed him in my rapture that he should be so truly good and brave. I felt that no one—mother, sister, wife—could honour him more than I. I did, indeed!\n\nMy poor little visitor made me a present of the account, and when as the evening began to close in she rose to take her leave, lest she should miss the coach by which she was to return, she was still full of the shipwreck, which I had not yet sufficiently composed myself to understand in all its details.\n\n“My dear,” said she as she carefully folded up her scarf and gloves, “my brave physician ought to have a title bestowed upon him. And no doubt he will. You are of that opinion?”\n\nThat he well deserved one, yes. That he would ever have one, no.\n\n“Why not, Fitz Jarndyce?” she asked rather sharply.\n\nI said it was not the custom in England to confer titles on men distinguished by peaceful services, however good and great, unless occasionally when they consisted of the accumulation of some very large amount of money.\n\n“Why, good gracious,” said Miss Flite, “how can you say that? Surely you know, my dear, that all the greatest ornaments of England in knowledge, imagination, active humanity, and improvement of every sort are added to its nobility! Look round you, my dear, and consider. YOU must be rambling a little now, I think, if you don’t know that this is the great reason why titles will always last in the land!”\n\nI am afraid she believed what she said, for there were moments when she was very mad indeed.\n\nAnd now I must part with the little secret I have thus far tried to keep. I had thought, sometimes, that Mr. Woodcourt loved me and that if he had been richer he would perhaps have told me that he loved me before he went away. I had thought, sometimes, that if he had done so, I should have been glad of it. But how much better it was now that this had never happened! What should I have suffered if I had had to write to him and tell him that the poor face he had known as mine was quite gone from me and that I freely released him from his bondage to one whom he had never seen!\n\nOh, it was so much better as it was! With a great pang mercifully spared me, I could take back to my heart my childish prayer to be all he had so brightly shown himself; and there was nothing to be undone: no chain for me to break or for him to drag; and I could go, please God, my lowly way along the path of duty, and he could go his nobler way upon its broader road; and though we were apart upon the journey, I might aspire to meet him, unselfishly, innocently, better far than he had thought me when I found some favour in his eyes, at the journey’s end.\nChesney Wold\n\nCharley and I did not set off alone upon our expedition into Lincolnshire. My guardian had made up his mind not to lose sight of me until I was safe in Mr. Boythorn’s house, so he accompanied us, and we were two days upon the road. I found every breath of air, and every scent, and every flower and leaf and blade of grass, and every passing cloud, and everything in nature, more beautiful and wonderful to me than I had ever found it yet. This was my first gain from my illness. How little I had lost, when the wide world was so full of delight for me.\n\nMy guardian intending to go back immediately, we appointed, on our way down, a day when my dear girl should come. I wrote her a letter, of which he took charge, and he left us within half an hour of our arrival at our destination, on a delightful evening in the early summer-time.\n\nIf a good fairy had built the house for me with a wave of her wand, and I had been a princess and her favoured god-child, I could not have been more considered in it. So many preparations were made for me and such an endearing remembrance was shown of all my little tastes and likings that I could have sat down, overcome, a dozen times before I had revisited half the rooms. I did better than that, however, by showing them all to Charley instead. Charley’s delight calmed mine; and after we had had a walk in the garden, and Charley had exhausted her whole vocabulary of admiring expressions, I was as tranquilly happy as I ought to have been. It was a great comfort to be able to say to myself after tea, “Esther, my dear, I think you are quite sensible enough to sit down now and write a note of thanks to your host.” He had left a note of welcome for me, as sunny as his own face, and had confided his bird to my care, which I knew to be his highest mark of confidence. Accordingly I wrote a little note to him in London, telling him how all his favourite plants and trees were looking, and how the most astonishing of birds had chirped the honours of the house to me in the most hospitable manner, and how, after singing on my shoulder, to the inconceivable rapture of my little maid, he was then at roost in the usual corner of his cage, but whether dreaming or no I could not report. My note finished and sent off to the post, I made myself very busy in unpacking and arranging; and I sent Charley to bed in good time and told her I should want her no more that night.\n\nFor I had not yet looked in the glass and had never asked to have my own restored to me. I knew this to be a weakness which must be overcome, but I had always said to myself that I would begin afresh when I got to where I now was. Therefore I had wanted to be alone, and therefore I said, now alone, in my own room, “Esther, if you are to be happy, if you are to have any right to pray to be true-hearted, you must keep your word, my dear.” I was quite resolved to keep it, but I sat down for a little while first to reflect upon all my blessings. And then I said my prayers and thought a little more.\n\nMy hair had not been cut off, though it had been in danger more than once. It was long and thick. I let it down, and shook it out, and went up to the glass upon the dressing-table. There was a little muslin curtain drawn across it. I drew it back and stood for a moment looking through such a veil of my own hair that I could see nothing else. Then I put my hair aside and looked at the reflection in the mirror, encouraged by seeing how placidly it looked at me. I was very much changed—oh, very, very much. At first my face was so strange to me that I think I should have put my hands before it and started back but for the encouragement I have mentioned. Very soon it became more familiar, and then I knew the extent of the alteration in it better than I had done at first. It was not like what I had expected, but I had expected nothing definite, and I dare say anything definite would have surprised me.\n\nI had never been a beauty and had never thought myself one, but I had been very different from this. It was all gone now. Heaven was so good to me that I could let it go with a few not bitter tears and could stand there arranging my hair for the night quite thankfully.\n\nOne thing troubled me, and I considered it for a long time before I went to sleep. I had kept Mr. Woodcourt’s flowers. When they were withered I had dried them and put them in a book that I was fond of. Nobody knew this, not even Ada. I was doubtful whether I had a right to preserve what he had sent to one so different—whether it was generous towards him to do it. I wished to be generous to him, even in the secret depths of my heart, which he would never know, because I could have loved him—could have been devoted to him. At last I came to the conclusion that I might keep them if I treasured them only as a remembrance of what was irrevocably past and gone, never to be looked back on any more, in any other light. I hope this may not seem trivial. I was very much in earnest.\n\nI took care to be up early in the morning and to be before the glass when Charley came in on tiptoe.\n\n“Dear, dear, miss!” cried Charley, starting. “Is that you?”\n\n“Yes, Charley,” said I, quietly putting up my hair. “And I am very well indeed, and very happy.”\n\nI saw it was a weight off Charley’s mind, but it was a greater weight off mine. I knew the worst now and was composed to it. I shall not conceal, as I go on, the weaknesses I could not quite conquer, but they always passed from me soon and the happier frame of mind stayed by me faithfully.\n\nWishing to be fully re-established in my strength and my good spirits before Ada came, I now laid down a little series of plans with Charley for being in the fresh air all day long. We were to be out before breakfast, and were to dine early, and were to be out again before and after dinner, and were to talk in the garden after tea, and were to go to rest betimes, and were to climb every hill and explore every road, lane, and field in the neighbourhood. As to restoratives and strengthening delicacies, Mr. Boythorn’s good housekeeper was for ever trotting about with something to eat or drink in her hand; I could not even be heard of as resting in the park but she would come trotting after me with a basket, her cheerful face shining with a lecture on the importance of frequent nourishment. Then there was a pony expressly for my riding, a chubby pony with a short neck and a mane all over his eyes who could canter—when he would—so easily and quietly that he was a treasure. In a very few days he would come to me in the paddock when I called him, and eat out of my hand, and follow me about. We arrived at such a capital understanding that when he was jogging with me lazily, and rather obstinately, down some shady lane, if I patted his neck and said, “Stubbs, I am surprised you don’t canter when you know how much I like it; and I think you might oblige me, for you are only getting stupid and going to sleep,” he would give his head a comical shake or two and set off directly, while Charley would stand still and laugh with such enjoyment that her laughter was like music. I don’t know who had given Stubbs his name, but it seemed to belong to him as naturally as his rough coat. Once we put him in a little chaise and drove him triumphantly through the green lanes for five miles; but all at once, as we were extolling him to the skies, he seemed to take it ill that he should have been accompanied so far by the circle of tantalizing little gnats that had been hovering round and round his ears the whole way without appearing to advance an inch, and stopped to think about it. I suppose he came to the decision that it was not to be borne, for he steadily refused to move until I gave the reins to Charley and got out and walked, when he followed me with a sturdy sort of good humour, putting his head under my arm and rubbing his ear against my sleeve. It was in vain for me to say, “Now, Stubbs, I feel quite sure from what I know of you that you will go on if I ride a little while,” for the moment I left him, he stood stock still again. Consequently I was obliged to lead the way, as before; and in this order we returned home, to the great delight of the village.\n\nCharley and I had reason to call it the most friendly of villages, I am sure, for in a week’s time the people were so glad to see us go by, though ever so frequently in the course of a day, that there were faces of greeting in every cottage. I had known many of the grown people before and almost all the children, but now the very steeple began to wear a familiar and affectionate look. Among my new friends was an old old woman who lived in such a little thatched and whitewashed dwelling that when the outside shutter was turned up on its hinges, it shut up the whole house-front. This old lady had a grandson who was a sailor, and I wrote a letter to him for her and drew at the top of it the chimney-corner in which she had brought him up and where his old stool yet occupied its old place. This was considered by the whole village the most wonderful achievement in the world, but when an answer came back all the way from Plymouth, in which he mentioned that he was going to take the picture all the way to America, and from America would write again, I got all the credit that ought to have been given to the post-office and was invested with the merit of the whole system.\n\nThus, what with being so much in the air, playing with so many children, gossiping with so many people, sitting on invitation in so many cottages, going on with Charley’s education, and writing long letters to Ada every day, I had scarcely any time to think about that little loss of mine and was almost always cheerful. If I did think of it at odd moments now and then, I had only to be busy and forget it. I felt it more than I had hoped I should once when a child said, “Mother, why is the lady not a pretty lady now like she used to be?” But when I found the child was not less fond of me, and drew its soft hand over my face with a kind of pitying protection in its touch, that soon set me up again. There were many little occurrences which suggested to me, with great consolation, how natural it is to gentle hearts to be considerate and delicate towards any inferiority. One of these particularly touched me. I happened to stroll into the little church when a marriage was just concluded, and the young couple had to sign the register.\n\nThe bridegroom, to whom the pen was handed first, made a rude cross for his mark; the bride, who came next, did the same. Now, I had known the bride when I was last there, not only as the prettiest girl in the place, but as having quite distinguished herself in the school, and I could not help looking at her with some surprise. She came aside and whispered to me, while tears of honest love and admiration stood in her bright eyes, “He’s a dear good fellow, miss; but he can’t write yet—he’s going to learn of me—and I wouldn’t shame him for the world!” Why, what had I to fear, I thought, when there was this nobility in the soul of a labouring man’s daughter!\n\nThe air blew as freshly and revivingly upon me as it had ever blown, and the healthy colour came into my new face as it had come into my old one. Charley was wonderful to see, she was so radiant and so rosy; and we both enjoyed the whole day and slept soundly the whole night.\n\nThere was a favourite spot of mine in the park-woods of Chesney Wold where a seat had been erected commanding a lovely view. The wood had been cleared and opened to improve this point of sight, and the bright sunny landscape beyond was so beautiful that I rested there at least once every day. A picturesque part of the Hall, called the Ghost’s Walk, was seen to advantage from this higher ground; and the startling name, and the old legend in the Dedlock family which I had heard from Mr. Boythorn accounting for it, mingled with the view and gave it something of a mysterious interest in addition to its real charms. There was a bank here, too, which was a famous one for violets; and as it was a daily delight of Charley’s to gather wild flowers, she took as much to the spot as I did.\n\nIt would be idle to inquire now why I never went close to the house or never went inside it. The family were not there, I had heard on my arrival, and were not expected. I was far from being incurious or uninterested about the building; on the contrary, I often sat in this place wondering how the rooms ranged and whether any echo like a footstep really did resound at times, as the story said, upon the lonely Ghost’s Walk. The indefinable feeling with which Lady Dedlock had impressed me may have had some influence in keeping me from the house even when she was absent. I am not sure. Her face and figure were associated with it, naturally; but I cannot say that they repelled me from it, though something did. For whatever reason or no reason, I had never once gone near it, down to the day at which my story now arrives.\n\nI was resting at my favourite point after a long ramble, and Charley was gathering violets at a little distance from me. I had been looking at the Ghost’s Walk lying in a deep shade of masonry afar off and picturing to myself the female shape that was said to haunt it when I became aware of a figure approaching through the wood. The perspective was so long and so darkened by leaves, and the shadows of the branches on the ground made it so much more intricate to the eye, that at first I could not discern what figure it was. By little and little it revealed itself to be a woman’s—a lady’s—Lady Dedlock’s. She was alone and coming to where I sat with a much quicker step, I observed to my surprise, than was usual with her.\n\nI was fluttered by her being unexpectedly so near (she was almost within speaking distance before I knew her) and would have risen to continue my walk. But I could not. I was rendered motionless. Not so much by her hurried gesture of entreaty, not so much by her quick advance and outstretched hands, not so much by the great change in her manner and the absence of her haughty self-restraint, as by a something in her face that I had pined for and dreamed of when I was a little child, something I had never seen in any face, something I had never seen in hers before.\n\nA dread and faintness fell upon me, and I called to Charley. Lady Dedlock stopped upon the instant and changed back almost to what I had known her.\n\n“Miss Summerson, I am afraid I have startled you,” she said, now advancing slowly. “You can scarcely be strong yet. You have been very ill, I know. I have been much concerned to hear it.”\n\nI could no more have removed my eyes from her pale face than I could have stirred from the bench on which I sat. She gave me her hand, and its deadly coldness, so at variance with the enforced composure of her features, deepened the fascination that overpowered me. I cannot say what was in my whirling thoughts.\n\n“You are recovering again?” she asked kindly.\n\n“I was quite well but a moment ago, Lady Dedlock.”\n\n“Is this your young attendant?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Will you send her on before and walk towards your house with me?”\n\n“Charley,” said I, “take your flowers home, and I will follow you directly.”\n\nCharley, with her best curtsy, blushingly tied on her bonnet and went her way. When she was gone, Lady Dedlock sat down on the seat beside me.\n\nI cannot tell in any words what the state of my mind was when I saw in her hand my handkerchief with which I had covered the dead baby.\n\nI looked at her, but I could not see her, I could not hear her, I could not draw my breath. The beating of my heart was so violent and wild that I felt as if my life were breaking from me. But when she caught me to her breast, kissed me, wept over me, compassionated me, and called me back to myself; when she fell down on her knees and cried to me, “Oh, my child, my child, I am your wicked and unhappy mother! Oh, try to forgive me!”—when I saw her at my feet on the bare earth in her great agony of mind, I felt, through all my tumult of emotion, a burst of gratitude to the providence of God that I was so changed as that I never could disgrace her by any trace of likeness, as that nobody could ever now look at me and look at her and remotely think of any near tie between us.\n\nI raised my mother up, praying and beseeching her not to stoop before me in such affliction and humiliation. I did so in broken, incoherent words, for besides the trouble I was in, it frightened me to see her at MY feet. I told her—or I tried to tell her—that if it were for me, her child, under any circumstances to take upon me to forgive her, I did it, and had done it, many, many years. I told her that my heart overflowed with love for her, that it was natural love which nothing in the past had changed or could change. That it was not for me, then resting for the first time on my mother’s bosom, to take her to account for having given me life, but that my duty was to bless her and receive her, though the whole world turned from her, and that I only asked her leave to do it. I held my mother in my embrace, and she held me in hers, and among the still woods in the silence of the summer day there seemed to be nothing but our two troubled minds that was not at peace.\n\n“To bless and receive me,” groaned my mother, “it is far too late. I must travel my dark road alone, and it will lead me where it will. From day to day, sometimes from hour to hour, I do not see the way before my guilty feet. This is the earthly punishment I have brought upon myself. I bear it, and I hide it.”\n\nEven in the thinking of her endurance, she drew her habitual air of proud indifference about her like a veil, though she soon cast it off again.\n\n“I must keep this secret, if by any means it can be kept, not wholly for myself. I have a husband, wretched and dishonouring creature that I am!”\n\nThese words she uttered with a suppressed cry of despair, more terrible in its sound than any shriek. Covering her face with her hands, she shrank down in my embrace as if she were unwilling that I should touch her; nor could I, by my utmost persuasions or by any endearments I could use, prevail upon her to rise. She said, no, no, no, she could only speak to me so; she must be proud and disdainful everywhere else; she would be humbled and ashamed there, in the only natural moments of her life.\n\nMy unhappy mother told me that in my illness she had been nearly frantic. She had but then known that her child was living. She could not have suspected me to be that child before. She had followed me down here to speak to me but once in all her life. We never could associate, never could communicate, never probably from that time forth could interchange another word on earth. She put into my hands a letter she had written for my reading only and said when I had read it and destroyed it—but not so much for her sake, since she asked nothing, as for her husband’s and my own—I must evermore consider her as dead. If I could believe that she loved me, in this agony in which I saw her, with a mother’s love, she asked me to do that, for then I might think of her with a greater pity, imagining what she suffered. She had put herself beyond all hope and beyond all help. Whether she preserved her secret until death or it came to be discovered and she brought dishonour and disgrace upon the name she had taken, it was her solitary struggle always; and no affection could come near her, and no human creature could render her any aid.\n\n“But is the secret safe so far?” I asked. “Is it safe now, dearest mother?”\n\n“No,” replied my mother. “It has been very near discovery. It was saved by an accident. It may be lost by another accident—to-morrow, any day.”\n\n“Do you dread a particular person?”\n\n“Hush! Do not tremble and cry so much for me. I am not worthy of these tears,” said my mother, kissing my hands. “I dread one person very much.”\n\n“An enemy?”\n\n“Not a friend. One who is too passionless to be either. He is Sir Leicester Dedlock’s lawyer, mechanically faithful without attachment, and very jealous of the profit, privilege, and reputation of being master of the mysteries of great houses.”\n\n“Has he any suspicions?”\n\n“Many.”\n\n“Not of you?” I said alarmed.\n\n“Yes! He is always vigilant and always near me. I may keep him at a standstill, but I can never shake him off.”\n\n“Has he so little pity or compunction?”\n\n“He has none, and no anger. He is indifferent to everything but his calling. His calling is the acquisition of secrets and the holding possession of such power as they give him, with no sharer or opponent in it.”\n\n“Could you trust in him?”\n\n“I shall never try. The dark road I have trodden for so many years will end where it will. I follow it alone to the end, whatever the end be. It may be near, it may be distant; while the road lasts, nothing turns me.”\n\n“Dear mother, are you so resolved?”\n\n“I AM resolved. I have long outbidden folly with folly, pride with pride, scorn with scorn, insolence with insolence, and have outlived many vanities with many more. I will outlive this danger, and outdie it, if I can. It has closed around me almost as awfully as if these woods of Chesney Wold had closed around the house, but my course through it is the same. I have but one; I can have but one.”\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce—” I was beginning when my mother hurriedly inquired, “Does HE suspect?”\n\n“No,” said I. “No, indeed! Be assured that he does not!” And I told her what he had related to me as his knowledge of my story. “But he is so good and sensible,” said I, “that perhaps if he knew—”\n\nMy mother, who until this time had made no change in her position, raised her hand up to my lips and stopped me.\n\n“Confide fully in him,” she said after a little while. “You have my free consent—a small gift from such a mother to her injured child!—but do not tell me of it. Some pride is left in me even yet.”\n\nI explained, as nearly as I could then, or can recall now—for my agitation and distress throughout were so great that I scarcely understood myself, though every word that was uttered in the mother’s voice, so unfamiliar and so melancholy to me, which in my childhood I had never learned to love and recognize, had never been sung to sleep with, had never heard a blessing from, had never had a hope inspired by, made an enduring impression on my memory—I say I explained, or tried to do it, how I had only hoped that Mr. Jarndyce, who had been the best of fathers to me, might be able to afford some counsel and support to her. But my mother answered no, it was impossible; no one could help her. Through the desert that lay before her, she must go alone.\n\n“My child, my child!” she said. “For the last time! These kisses for the last time! These arms upon my neck for the last time! We shall meet no more. To hope to do what I seek to do, I must be what I have been so long. Such is my reward and doom. If you hear of Lady Dedlock, brilliant, prosperous, and flattered, think of your wretched mother, conscience-stricken, underneath that mask! Think that the reality is in her suffering, in her useless remorse, in her murdering within her breast the only love and truth of which it is capable! And then forgive her if you can, and cry to heaven to forgive her, which it never can!”\n\nWe held one another for a little space yet, but she was so firm that she took my hands away, and put them back against my breast, and with a last kiss as she held them there, released them, and went from me into the wood. I was alone, and calm and quiet below me in the sun and shade lay the old house, with its terraces and turrets, on which there had seemed to me to be such complete repose when I first saw it, but which now looked like the obdurate and unpitying watcher of my mother’s misery.\n\nStunned as I was, as weak and helpless at first as I had ever been in my sick chamber, the necessity of guarding against the danger of discovery, or even of the remotest suspicion, did me service. I took such precautions as I could to hide from Charley that I had been crying, and I constrained myself to think of every sacred obligation that there was upon me to be careful and collected. It was not a little while before I could succeed or could even restrain bursts of grief, but after an hour or so I was better and felt that I might return. I went home very slowly and told Charley, whom I found at the gate looking for me, that I had been tempted to extend my walk after Lady Dedlock had left me and that I was over-tired and would lie down. Safe in my own room, I read the letter. I clearly derived from it—and that was much then—that I had not been abandoned by my mother. Her elder and only sister, the godmother of my childhood, discovering signs of life in me when I had been laid aside as dead, had in her stern sense of duty, with no desire or willingness that I should live, reared me in rigid secrecy and had never again beheld my mother’s face from within a few hours of my birth. So strangely did I hold my place in this world that until within a short time back I had never, to my own mother’s knowledge, breathed—had been buried—had never been endowed with life—had never borne a name. When she had first seen me in the church she had been startled and had thought of what would have been like me if it had ever lived, and had lived on, but that was all then.\n\nWhat more the letter told me needs not to be repeated here. It has its own times and places in my story.\n\nMy first care was to burn what my mother had written and to consume even its ashes. I hope it may not appear very unnatural or bad in me that I then became heavily sorrowful to think I had ever been reared. That I felt as if I knew it would have been better and happier for many people if indeed I had never breathed. That I had a terror of myself as the danger and the possible disgrace of my own mother and of a proud family name. That I was so confused and shaken as to be possessed by a belief that it was right and had been intended that I should die in my birth, and that it was wrong and not intended that I should be then alive.\n\nThese are the real feelings that I had. I fell asleep worn out, and when I awoke I cried afresh to think that I was back in the world with my load of trouble for others. I was more than ever frightened of myself, thinking anew of her against whom I was a witness, of the owner of Chesney Wold, of the new and terrible meaning of the old words now moaning in my ear like a surge upon the shore, “Your mother, Esther, was your disgrace, and you are hers. The time will come—and soon enough—when you will understand this better, and will feel it too, as no one save a woman can.” With them, those other words returned, “Pray daily that the sins of others be not visited upon your head.” I could not disentangle all that was about me, and I felt as if the blame and the shame were all in me, and the visitation had come down.\n\nThe day waned into a gloomy evening, overcast and sad, and I still contended with the same distress. I went out alone, and after walking a little in the park, watching the dark shades falling on the trees and the fitful flight of the bats, which sometimes almost touched me, was attracted to the house for the first time. Perhaps I might not have gone near it if I had been in a stronger frame of mind. As it was, I took the path that led close by it.\n\nI did not dare to linger or to look up, but I passed before the terrace garden with its fragrant odours, and its broad walks, and its well-kept beds and smooth turf; and I saw how beautiful and grave it was, and how the old stone balustrades and parapets, and wide flights of shallow steps, were seamed by time and weather; and how the trained moss and ivy grew about them, and around the old stone pedestal of the sun-dial; and I heard the fountain falling. Then the way went by long lines of dark windows diversified by turreted towers and porches of eccentric shapes, where old stone lions and grotesque monsters bristled outside dens of shadow and snarled at the evening gloom over the escutcheons they held in their grip. Thence the path wound underneath a gateway, and through a court-yard where the principal entrance was (I hurried quickly on), and by the stables where none but deep voices seemed to be, whether in the murmuring of the wind through the strong mass of ivy holding to a high red wall, or in the low complaining of the weathercock, or in the barking of the dogs, or in the slow striking of a clock. So, encountering presently a sweet smell of limes, whose rustling I could hear, I turned with the turning of the path to the south front, and there above me were the balustrades of the Ghost’s Walk and one lighted window that might be my mother’s.\n\nThe way was paved here, like the terrace overhead, and my footsteps from being noiseless made an echoing sound upon the flags. Stopping to look at nothing, but seeing all I did see as I went, I was passing quickly on, and in a few moments should have passed the lighted window, when my echoing footsteps brought it suddenly into my mind that there was a dreadful truth in the legend of the Ghost’s Walk, that it was I who was to bring calamity upon the stately house and that my warning feet were haunting it even then. Seized with an augmented terror of myself which turned me cold, I ran from myself and everything, retraced the way by which I had come, and never paused until I had gained the lodge-gate, and the park lay sullen and black behind me.\n\nNot before I was alone in my own room for the night and had again been dejected and unhappy there did I begin to know how wrong and thankless this state was. But from my darling who was coming on the morrow, I found a joyful letter, full of such loving anticipation that I must have been of marble if it had not moved me; from my guardian, too, I found another letter, asking me to tell Dame Durden, if I should see that little woman anywhere, that they had moped most pitiably without her, that the housekeeping was going to rack and ruin, that nobody else could manage the keys, and that everybody in and about the house declared it was not the same house and was becoming rebellious for her return. Two such letters together made me think how far beyond my deserts I was beloved and how happy I ought to be. That made me think of all my past life; and that brought me, as it ought to have done before, into a better condition.\n\nFor I saw very well that I could not have been intended to die, or I should never have lived; not to say should never have been reserved for such a happy life. I saw very well how many things had worked together for my welfare, and that if the sins of the fathers were sometimes visited upon the children, the phrase did not mean what I had in the morning feared it meant. I knew I was as innocent of my birth as a queen of hers and that before my Heavenly Father I should not be punished for birth nor a queen rewarded for it. I had had experience, in the shock of that very day, that I could, even thus soon, find comforting reconcilements to the change that had fallen on me. I renewed my resolutions and prayed to be strengthened in them, pouring out my heart for myself and for my unhappy mother and feeling that the darkness of the morning was passing away. It was not upon my sleep; and when the next day’s light awoke me, it was gone.\n\nMy dear girl was to arrive at five o’clock in the afternoon. How to help myself through the intermediate time better than by taking a long walk along the road by which she was to come, I did not know; so Charley and I and Stubbs—Stubbs saddled, for we never drove him after the one great occasion—made a long expedition along that road and back. On our return, we held a great review of the house and garden and saw that everything was in its prettiest condition, and had the bird out ready as an important part of the establishment.\n\nThere were more than two full hours yet to elapse before she could come, and in that interval, which seemed a long one, I must confess I was nervously anxious about my altered looks. I loved my darling so well that I was more concerned for their effect on her than on any one. I was not in this slight distress because I at all repined—I am quite certain I did not, that day—but, I thought, would she be wholly prepared? When she first saw me, might she not be a little shocked and disappointed? Might it not prove a little worse than she expected? Might she not look for her old Esther and not find her? Might she not have to grow used to me and to begin all over again?\n\nI knew the various expressions of my sweet girl’s face so well, and it was such an honest face in its loveliness, that I was sure beforehand she could not hide that first look from me. And I considered whether, if it should signify any one of these meanings, which was so very likely, could I quite answer for myself?\n\nWell, I thought I could. After last night, I thought I could. But to wait and wait, and expect and expect, and think and think, was such bad preparation that I resolved to go along the road again and meet her.\n\nSo I said to Charley, “Charley, I will go by myself and walk along the road until she comes.” Charley highly approving of anything that pleased me, I went and left her at home.\n\nBut before I got to the second milestone, I had been in so many palpitations from seeing dust in the distance (though I knew it was not, and could not, be the coach yet) that I resolved to turn back and go home again. And when I had turned, I was in such fear of the coach coming up behind me (though I still knew that it neither would, nor could, do any such thing) that I ran the greater part of the way to avoid being overtaken.\n\nThen, I considered, when I had got safe back again, this was a nice thing to have done! Now I was hot and had made the worst of it instead of the best.\n\nAt last, when I believed there was at least a quarter of an hour more yet, Charley all at once cried out to me as I was trembling in the garden, “Here she comes, miss! Here she is!”\n\nI did not mean to do it, but I ran upstairs into my room and hid myself behind the door. There I stood trembling, even when I heard my darling calling as she came upstairs, “Esther, my dear, my love, where are you? Little woman, dear Dame Durden!”\n\nShe ran in, and was running out again when she saw me. Ah, my angel girl! The old dear look, all love, all fondness, all affection. Nothing else in it—no, nothing, nothing!\n\nOh, how happy I was, down upon the floor, with my sweet beautiful girl down upon the floor too, holding my scarred face to her lovely cheek, bathing it with tears and kisses, rocking me to and fro like a child, calling me by every tender name that she could think of, and pressing me to her faithful heart.\n\nJarndyce and Jarndyce\nIf the secret I had to keep had been mine, I must have confided it to Ada before we had been long together. But it was not mine, and I did not feel that I had a right to tell it, even to my guardian, unless some great emergency arose. It was a weight to bear alone; still my present duty appeared to be plain, and blest in the attachment of my dear, I did not want an impulse and encouragement to do it. Though often when she was asleep and all was quiet, the remembrance of my mother kept me waking and made the night sorrowful, I did not yield to it at another time; and Ada found me what I used to be—except, of course, in that particular of which I have said enough and which I have no intention of mentioning any more just now, if I can help it.\n\nThe difficulty that I felt in being quite composed that first evening when Ada asked me, over our work, if the family were at the house, and when I was obliged to answer yes, I believed so, for Lady Dedlock had spoken to me in the woods the day before yesterday, was great. Greater still when Ada asked me what she had said, and when I replied that she had been kind and interested, and when Ada, while admitting her beauty and elegance, remarked upon her proud manner and her imperious chilling air. But Charley helped me through, unconsciously, by telling us that Lady Dedlock had only stayed at the house two nights on her way from London to visit at some other great house in the next county and that she had left early on the morning after we had seen her at our view, as we called it. Charley verified the adage about little pitchers, I am sure, for she heard of more sayings and doings in a day than would have come to my ears in a month.\n\nWe were to stay a month at Mr. Boythorn’s. My pet had scarcely been there a bright week, as I recollect the time, when one evening after we had finished helping the gardener in watering his flowers, and just as the candles were lighted, Charley, appearing with a very important air behind Ada’s chair, beckoned me mysteriously out of the room.\n\n“Oh! If you please, miss,” said Charley in a whisper, with her eyes at their roundest and largest. “You’re wanted at the Dedlock Arms.”\n\n“Why, Charley,” said I, “who can possibly want me at the public-house?”\n\n“I don’t know, miss,” returned Charley, putting her head forward and folding her hands tight upon the band of her little apron, which she always did in the enjoyment of anything mysterious or confidential, “but it’s a gentleman, miss, and his compliments, and will you please to come without saying anything about it.”\n\n“Whose compliments, Charley?”\n\n“His’n, miss,” returned Charley, whose grammatical education was advancing, but not very rapidly.\n\n“And how do you come to be the messenger, Charley?”\n\n“I am not the messenger, if you please, miss,” returned my little maid. “It was W. Grubble, miss.”\n\n“And who is W. Grubble, Charley?”\n\n“Mister Grubble, miss,” returned Charley. “Don’t you know, miss? The Dedlock Arms, by W. Grubble,” which Charley delivered as if she were slowly spelling out the sign.\n\n“Aye? The landlord, Charley?”\n\n“Yes, miss. If you please, miss, his wife is a beautiful woman, but she broke her ankle, and it never joined. And her brother’s the sawyer that was put in the cage, miss, and they expect he’ll drink himself to death entirely on beer,” said Charley.\n\nNot knowing what might be the matter, and being easily apprehensive now, I thought it best to go to this place by myself. I bade Charley be quick with my bonnet and veil and my shawl, and having put them on, went away down the little hilly street, where I was as much at home as in Mr. Boythorn’s garden.\n\nMr. Grubble was standing in his shirt-sleeves at the door of his very clean little tavern waiting for me. He lifted off his hat with both hands when he saw me coming, and carrying it so, as if it were an iron vessel (it looked as heavy), preceded me along the sanded passage to his best parlour, a neat carpeted room with more plants in it than were quite convenient, a coloured print of Queen Caroline, several shells, a good many tea-trays, two stuffed and dried fish in glass cases, and either a curious egg or a curious pumpkin (but I don’t know which, and I doubt if many people did) hanging from his ceiling. I knew Mr. Grubble very well by sight, from his often standing at his door. A pleasant-looking, stoutish, middle-aged man who never seemed to consider himself cozily dressed for his own fire-side without his hat and top-boots, but who never wore a coat except at church.\n\nHe snuffed the candle, and backing away a little to see how it looked, backed out of the room—unexpectedly to me, for I was going to ask him by whom he had been sent. The door of the opposite parlour being then opened, I heard some voices, familiar in my ears I thought, which stopped. A quick light step approached the room in which I was, and who should stand before me but Richard!\n\n“My dear Esther!” he said. “My best friend!” And he really was so warm-hearted and earnest that in the first surprise and pleasure of his brotherly greeting I could scarcely find breath to tell him that Ada was well.\n\n“Answering my very thoughts—always the same dear girl!” said Richard, leading me to a chair and seating himself beside me.\n\nI put my veil up, but not quite.\n\n“Always the same dear girl!” said Richard just as heartily as before.\n\nI put up my veil altogether, and laying my hand on Richard’s sleeve and looking in his face, told him how much I thanked him for his kind welcome and how greatly I rejoiced to see him, the more so because of the determination I had made in my illness, which I now conveyed to him.\n\n“My love,” said Richard, “there is no one with whom I have a greater wish to talk than you, for I want you to understand me.”\n\n“And I want you, Richard,” said I, shaking my head, “to understand some one else.”\n\n“Since you refer so immediately to John Jarndyce,” said Richard, “—I suppose you mean him?”\n\n“Of course I do.”\n\n“Then I may say at once that I am glad of it, because it is on that subject that I am anxious to be understood. By you, mind—you, my dear! I am not accountable to Mr. Jarndyce or Mr. Anybody.”\n\nI was pained to find him taking this tone, and he observed it.\n\n“Well, well, my dear,” said Richard, “we won’t go into that now. I want to appear quietly in your country-house here, with you under my arm, and give my charming cousin a surprise. I suppose your loyalty to John Jarndyce will allow that?”\n\n“My dear Richard,” I returned, “you know you would be heartily welcome at his house—your home, if you will but consider it so; and you are as heartily welcome here!”\n\n“Spoken like the best of little women!” cried Richard gaily.\n\nI asked him how he liked his profession.\n\n“Oh, I like it well enough!” said Richard. “It’s all right. It does as well as anything else, for a time. I don’t know that I shall care about it when I come to be settled, but I can sell out then and—however, never mind all that botheration at present.”\n\nSo young and handsome, and in all respects so perfectly the opposite of Miss Flite! And yet, in the clouded, eager, seeking look that passed over him, so dreadfully like her!\n\n“I am in town on leave just now,” said Richard.\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“Yes. I have run over to look after my—my Chancery interests before the long vacation,” said Richard, forcing a careless laugh. “We are beginning to spin along with that old suit at last, I promise you.”\n\nNo wonder that I shook my head!\n\n“As you say, it’s not a pleasant subject.” Richard spoke with the same shade crossing his face as before. “Let it go to the four winds for to-night. Puff! Gone! Who do you suppose is with me?”\n\n“Was it Mr. Skimpole’s voice I heard?”\n\n“That’s the man! He does me more good than anybody. What a fascinating child it is!”\n\nI asked Richard if any one knew of their coming down together. He answered, no, nobody. He had been to call upon the dear old infant—so he called Mr. Skimpole—and the dear old infant had told him where we were, and he had told the dear old infant he was bent on coming to see us, and the dear old infant had directly wanted to come too; and so he had brought him. “And he is worth—not to say his sordid expenses—but thrice his weight in gold,” said Richard. “He is such a cheery fellow. No worldliness about him. Fresh and green-hearted!”\n\nI certainly did not see the proof of Mr. Skimpole’s worldliness in his having his expenses paid by Richard, but I made no remark about that. Indeed, he came in and turned our conversation. He was charmed to see me, said he had been shedding delicious tears of joy and sympathy at intervals for six weeks on my account, had never been so happy as in hearing of my progress, began to understand the mixture of good and evil in the world now, felt that he appreciated health the more when somebody else was ill, didn’t know but what it might be in the scheme of things that A should squint to make B happier in looking straight or that C should carry a wooden leg to make D better satisfied with his flesh and blood in a silk stocking.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson, here is our friend Richard,” said Mr. Skimpole, “full of the brightest visions of the future, which he evokes out of the darkness of Chancery. Now that’s delightful, that’s inspiriting, that’s full of poetry! In old times the woods and solitudes were made joyous to the shepherd by the imaginary piping and dancing of Pan and the nymphs. This present shepherd, our pastoral Richard, brightens the dull Inns of Court by making Fortune and her train sport through them to the melodious notes of a judgment from the bench. That’s very pleasant, you know! Some ill-conditioned growling fellow may say to me, ‘What’s the use of these legal and equitable abuses? How do you defend them?’ I reply, ‘My growling friend, I DON’T defend them, but they are very agreeable to me. There is a shepherd—youth, a friend of mine, who transmutes them into something highly fascinating to my simplicity. I don’t say it is for this that they exist—for I am a child among you worldly grumblers, and not called upon to account to you or myself for anything—but it may be so.’”\n\nI began seriously to think that Richard could scarcely have found a worse friend than this. It made me uneasy that at such a time when he most required some right principle and purpose he should have this captivating looseness and putting-off of everything, this airy dispensing with all principle and purpose, at his elbow. I thought I could understand how such a nature as my guardian’s, experienced in the world and forced to contemplate the miserable evasions and contentions of the family misfortune, found an immense relief in Mr. Skimpole’s avowal of his weaknesses and display of guileless candour; but I could not satisfy myself that it was as artless as it seemed or that it did not serve Mr. Skimpole’s idle turn quite as well as any other part, and with less trouble.\n\nThey both walked back with me, and Mr. Skimpole leaving us at the gate, I walked softly in with Richard and said, “Ada, my love, I have brought a gentleman to visit you.” It was not difficult to read the blushing, startled face. She loved him dearly, and he knew it, and I knew it. It was a very transparent business, that meeting as cousins only.\n\nI almost mistrusted myself as growing quite wicked in my suspicions, but I was not so sure that Richard loved her dearly. He admired her very much—any one must have done that—and I dare say would have renewed their youthful engagement with great pride and ardour but that he knew how she would respect her promise to my guardian. Still I had a tormenting idea that the influence upon him extended even here, that he was postponing his best truth and earnestness in this as in all things until Jarndyce and Jarndyce should be off his mind. Ah me! What Richard would have been without that blight, I never shall know now!\n\nHe told Ada, in his most ingenuous way, that he had not come to make any secret inroad on the terms she had accepted (rather too implicitly and confidingly, he thought) from Mr. Jarndyce, that he had come openly to see her and to see me and to justify himself for the present terms on which he stood with Mr. Jarndyce. As the dear old infant would be with us directly, he begged that I would make an appointment for the morning, when he might set himself right through the means of an unreserved conversation with me. I proposed to walk with him in the park at seven o’clock, and this was arranged. Mr. Skimpole soon afterwards appeared and made us merry for an hour. He particularly requested to see little Coavinses (meaning Charley) and told her, with a patriarchal air, that he had given her late father all the business in his power and that if one of her little brothers would make haste to get set up in the same profession, he hoped he should still be able to put a good deal of employment in his way.\n\n“For I am constantly being taken in these nets,” said Mr. Skimpole, looking beamingly at us over a glass of wine-and-water, “and am constantly being bailed out—like a boat. Or paid off—like a ship’s company. Somebody always does it for me. I can’t do it, you know, for I never have any money. But somebody does it. I get out by somebody’s means; I am not like the starling; I get out. If you were to ask me who somebody is, upon my word I couldn’t tell you. Let us drink to somebody. God bless him!”\n\nRichard was a little late in the morning, but I had not to wait for him long, and we turned into the park. The air was bright and dewy and the sky without a cloud. The birds sang delightfully; the sparkles in the fern, the grass, and trees, were exquisite to see; the richness of the woods seemed to have increased twenty-fold since yesterday, as if, in the still night when they had looked so massively hushed in sleep, Nature, through all the minute details of every wonderful leaf, had been more wakeful than usual for the glory of that day.\n\n“This is a lovely place,” said Richard, looking round. “None of the jar and discord of law-suits here!”\n\nBut there was other trouble.\n\n“I tell you what, my dear girl,” said Richard, “when I get affairs in general settled, I shall come down here, I think, and rest.”\n\n“Would it not be better to rest now?” I asked.\n\n“Oh, as to resting NOW,” said Richard, “or as to doing anything very definite NOW, that’s not easy. In short, it can’t be done; I can’t do it at least.”\n\n“Why not?” said I.\n\n“You know why not, Esther. If you were living in an unfinished house, liable to have the roof put on or taken off—to be from top to bottom pulled down or built up—to-morrow, next day, next week, next month, next year—you would find it hard to rest or settle. So do I. Now? There’s no now for us suitors.”\n\nI could almost have believed in the attraction on which my poor little wandering friend had expatiated when I saw again the darkened look of last night. Terrible to think it had in it also a shade of that unfortunate man who had died.\n\n“My dear Richard,” said I, “this is a bad beginning of our conversation.”\n\n“I knew you would tell me so, Dame Durden.”\n\n“And not I alone, dear Richard. It was not I who cautioned you once never to found a hope or expectation on the family curse.”\n\n“There you come back to John Jarndyce!” said Richard impatiently. “Well! We must approach him sooner or later, for he is the staple of what I have to say, and it’s as well at once. My dear Esther, how can you be so blind? Don’t you see that he is an interested party and that it may be very well for him to wish me to know nothing of the suit, and care nothing about it, but that it may not be quite so well for me?”\n\n“Oh, Richard,” I remonstrated, “is it possible that you can ever have seen him and heard him, that you can ever have lived under his roof and known him, and can yet breathe, even to me in this solitary place where there is no one to hear us, such unworthy suspicions?”\n\nHe reddened deeply, as if his natural generosity felt a pang of reproach. He was silent for a little while before he replied in a subdued voice, “Esther, I am sure you know that I am not a mean fellow and that I have some sense of suspicion and distrust being poor qualities in one of my years.”\n\n“I know it very well,” said I. “I am not more sure of anything.”\n\n“That’s a dear girl,” retorted Richard, “and like you, because it gives me comfort. I had need to get some scrap of comfort out of all this business, for it’s a bad one at the best, as I have no occasion to tell you.”\n\n“I know perfectly,” said I. “I know as well, Richard—what shall I say? as well as you do—that such misconstructions are foreign to your nature. And I know, as well as you know, what so changes it.”\n\n“Come, sister, come,” said Richard a little more gaily, “you will be fair with me at all events. If I have the misfortune to be under that influence, so has he. If it has a little twisted me, it may have a little twisted him too. I don’t say that he is not an honourable man, out of all this complication and uncertainty; I am sure he is. But it taints everybody. You know it taints everybody. You have heard him say so fifty times. Then why should HE escape?”\n\n“Because,” said I, “his is an uncommon character, and he has resolutely kept himself outside the circle, Richard.”\n\n“Oh, because and because!” replied Richard in his vivacious way. “I am not sure, my dear girl, but that it may be wise and specious to preserve that outward indifference. It may cause other parties interested to become lax about their interests; and people may die off, and points may drag themselves out of memory, and many things may smoothly happen that are convenient enough.”\n\nI was so touched with pity for Richard that I could not reproach him any more, even by a look. I remembered my guardian’s gentleness towards his errors and with what perfect freedom from resentment he had spoken of them.\n\n“Esther,” Richard resumed, “you are not to suppose that I have come here to make underhanded charges against John Jarndyce. I have only come to justify myself. What I say is, it was all very well and we got on very well while I was a boy, utterly regardless of this same suit; but as soon as I began to take an interest in it and to look into it, then it was quite another thing. Then John Jarndyce discovers that Ada and I must break off and that if I don’t amend that very objectionable course, I am not fit for her. Now, Esther, I don’t mean to amend that very objectionable course: I will not hold John Jarndyce’s favour on those unfair terms of compromise, which he has no right to dictate. Whether it pleases him or displeases him, I must maintain my rights and Ada’s. I have been thinking about it a good deal, and this is the conclusion I have come to.”\n\nPoor dear Richard! He had indeed been thinking about it a good deal. His face, his voice, his manner, all showed that too plainly.\n\n“So I tell him honourably (you are to know I have written to him about all this) that we are at issue and that we had better be at issue openly than covertly. I thank him for his goodwill and his protection, and he goes his road, and I go mine. The fact is, our roads are not the same. Under one of the wills in dispute, I should take much more than he. I don’t mean to say that it is the one to be established, but there it is, and it has its chance.”\n\n“I have not to learn from you, my dear Richard,” said I, “of your letter. I had heard of it already without an offended or angry word.”\n\n“Indeed?” replied Richard, softening. “I am glad I said he was an honourable man, out of all this wretched affair. But I always say that and have never doubted it. Now, my dear Esther, I know these views of mine appear extremely harsh to you, and will to Ada when you tell her what has passed between us. But if you had gone into the case as I have, if you had only applied yourself to the papers as I did when I was at Kenge’s, if you only knew what an accumulation of charges and counter-charges, and suspicions and cross-suspicions, they involve, you would think me moderate in comparison.”\n\n“Perhaps so,” said I. “But do you think that, among those many papers, there is much truth and justice, Richard?”\n\n“There is truth and justice somewhere in the case, Esther—”\n\n“Or was once, long ago,” said I.\n\n“Is—is—must be somewhere,” pursued Richard impetuously, “and must be brought out. To allow Ada to be made a bribe and hush-money of is not the way to bring it out. You say the suit is changing me; John Jarndyce says it changes, has changed, and will change everybody who has any share in it. Then the greater right I have on my side when I resolve to do all I can to bring it to an end.”\n\n“All you can, Richard! Do you think that in these many years no others have done all they could? Has the difficulty grown easier because of so many failures?”\n\n“It can’t last for ever,” returned Richard with a fierceness kindling in him which again presented to me that last sad reminder. “I am young and earnest, and energy and determination have done wonders many a time. Others have only half thrown themselves into it. I devote myself to it. I make it the object of my life.”\n\n“Oh, Richard, my dear, so much the worse, so much the worse!”\n\n“No, no, no, don’t you be afraid for me,” he returned affectionately. “You’re a dear, good, wise, quiet, blessed girl; but you have your prepossessions. So I come round to John Jarndyce. I tell you, my good Esther, when he and I were on those terms which he found so convenient, we were not on natural terms.”\n\n“Are division and animosity your natural terms, Richard?”\n\n“No, I don’t say that. I mean that all this business puts us on unnatural terms, with which natural relations are incompatible. See another reason for urging it on! I may find out when it’s over that I have been mistaken in John Jarndyce. My head may be clearer when I am free of it, and I may then agree with what you say to-day. Very well. Then I shall acknowledge it and make him reparation.”\n\nEverything postponed to that imaginary time! Everything held in confusion and indecision until then!\n\n“Now, my best of confidantes,” said Richard, “I want my cousin Ada to understand that I am not captious, fickle, and wilful about John Jarndyce, but that I have this purpose and reason at my back. I wish to represent myself to her through you, because she has a great esteem and respect for her cousin John; and I know you will soften the course I take, even though you disapprove of it; and—and in short,” said Richard, who had been hesitating through these words, “I—I don’t like to represent myself in this litigious, contentious, doubting character to a confiding girl like Ada.”\n\nI told him that he was more like himself in those latter words than in anything he had said yet.\n\n“Why,” acknowledged Richard, “that may be true enough, my love. I rather feel it to be so. But I shall be able to give myself fair-play by and by. I shall come all right again, then, don’t you be afraid.”\n\nI asked him if this were all he wished me to tell Ada.\n\n“Not quite,” said Richard. “I am bound not to withhold from her that John Jarndyce answered my letter in his usual manner, addressing me as ‘My dear Rick,’ trying to argue me out of my opinions, and telling me that they should make no difference in him. (All very well of course, but not altering the case.) I also want Ada to know that if I see her seldom just now, I am looking after her interests as well as my own—we two being in the same boat exactly—and that I hope she will not suppose from any flying rumours she may hear that I am at all light-headed or imprudent; on the contrary, I am always looking forward to the termination of the suit, and always planning in that direction. Being of age now and having taken the step I have taken, I consider myself free from any accountability to John Jarndyce; but Ada being still a ward of the court, I don’t yet ask her to renew our engagement. When she is free to act for herself, I shall be myself once more and we shall both be in very different worldly circumstances, I believe. If you tell her all this with the advantage of your considerate way, you will do me a very great and a very kind service, my dear Esther; and I shall knock Jarndyce and Jarndyce on the head with greater vigour. Of course I ask for no secrecy at Bleak House.”\n\n“Richard,” said I, “you place great confidence in me, but I fear you will not take advice from me?”\n\n“It’s impossible that I can on this subject, my dear girl. On any other, readily.”\n\nAs if there were any other in his life! As if his whole career and character were not being dyed one colour!\n\n“But I may ask you a question, Richard?”\n\n“I think so,” said he, laughing. “I don’t know who may not, if you may not.”\n\n“You say, yourself, you are not leading a very settled life.”\n\n“How can I, my dear Esther, with nothing settled!”\n\n“Are you in debt again?”\n\n“Why, of course I am,” said Richard, astonished at my simplicity.\n\n“Is it of course?”\n\n“My dear child, certainly. I can’t throw myself into an object so completely without expense. You forget, or perhaps you don’t know, that under either of the wills Ada and I take something. It’s only a question between the larger sum and the smaller. I shall be within the mark any way. Bless your heart, my excellent girl,” said Richard, quite amused with me, “I shall be all right! I shall pull through, my dear!”\n\nI felt so deeply sensible of the danger in which he stood that I tried, in Ada’s name, in my guardian’s, in my own, by every fervent means that I could think of, to warn him of it and to show him some of his mistakes. He received everything I said with patience and gentleness, but it all rebounded from him without taking the least effect. I could not wonder at this after the reception his preoccupied mind had given to my guardian’s letter, but I determined to try Ada’s influence yet.\n\nSo when our walk brought us round to the village again, and I went home to breakfast, I prepared Ada for the account I was going to give her and told her exactly what reason we had to dread that Richard was losing himself and scattering his whole life to the winds. It made her very unhappy, of course, though she had a far, far greater reliance on his correcting his errors than I could have—which was so natural and loving in my dear!—and she presently wrote him this little letter:\n\nMy dearest cousin,\n\nEsther has told me all you said to her this morning. I write this to repeat most earnestly for myself all that she said to you and to let you know how sure I am that you will sooner or later find our cousin John a pattern of truth, sincerity, and goodness, when you will deeply, deeply grieve to have done him (without intending it) so much wrong.\n\nI do not quite know how to write what I wish to say next, but I trust you will understand it as I mean it. I have some fears, my dearest cousin, that it may be partly for my sake you are now laying up so much unhappiness for yourself—and if for yourself, for me. In case this should be so, or in case you should entertain much thought of me in what you are doing, I most earnestly entreat and beg you to desist. You can do nothing for my sake that will make me half so happy as for ever turning your back upon the shadow in which we both were born. Do not be angry with me for saying this. Pray, pray, dear Richard, for my sake, and for your own, and in a natural repugnance for that source of trouble which had its share in making us both orphans when we were very young, pray, pray, let it go for ever. We have reason to know by this time that there is no good in it and no hope, that there is nothing to be got from it but sorrow.\n\nMy dearest cousin, it is needless for me to say that you are quite free and that it is very likely you may find some one whom you will love much better than your first fancy. I am quite sure, if you will let me say so, that the object of your choice would greatly prefer to follow your fortunes far and wide, however moderate or poor, and see you happy, doing your duty and pursuing your chosen way, than to have the hope of being, or even to be, very rich with you (if such a thing were possible) at the cost of dragging years of procrastination and anxiety and of your indifference to other aims. You may wonder at my saying this so confidently with so little knowledge or experience, but I know it for a certainty from my own heart.\n\nEver, my dearest cousin, your most affectionate\n\nAda\n\nThis note brought Richard to us very soon, but it made little change in him if any. We would fairly try, he said, who was right and who was wrong—he would show us—we should see! He was animated and glowing, as if Ada’s tenderness had gratified him; but I could only hope, with a sigh, that the letter might have some stronger effect upon his mind on re-perusal than it assuredly had then.\n\nAs they were to remain with us that day and had taken their places to return by the coach next morning, I sought an opportunity of speaking to Mr. Skimpole. Our out-of-door life easily threw one in my way, and I delicately said that there was a responsibility in encouraging Richard.\n\n“Responsibility, my dear Miss Summerson?” he repeated, catching at the word with the pleasantest smile. “I am the last man in the world for such a thing. I never was responsible in my life—I can’t be.”\n\n“I am afraid everybody is obliged to be,” said I timidly enough, he being so much older and more clever than I.\n\n“No, really?” said Mr. Skimpole, receiving this new light with a most agreeable jocularity of surprise. “But every man’s not obliged to be solvent? I am not. I never was. See, my dear Miss Summerson,” he took a handful of loose silver and halfpence from his pocket, “there’s so much money. I have not an idea how much. I have not the power of counting. Call it four and ninepence—call it four pound nine. They tell me I owe more than that. I dare say I do. I dare say I owe as much as good-natured people will let me owe. If they don’t stop, why should I? There you have Harold Skimpole in little. If that’s responsibility, I am responsible.”\n\nThe perfect ease of manner with which he put the money up again and looked at me with a smile on his refined face, as if he had been mentioning a curious little fact about somebody else, almost made me feel as if he really had nothing to do with it.\n\n“Now, when you mention responsibility,” he resumed, “I am disposed to say that I never had the happiness of knowing any one whom I should consider so refreshingly responsible as yourself. You appear to me to be the very touchstone of responsibility. When I see you, my dear Miss Summerson, intent upon the perfect working of the whole little orderly system of which you are the centre, I feel inclined to say to myself—in fact I do say to myself very often—THAT’S responsibility!”\n\nIt was difficult, after this, to explain what I meant; but I persisted so far as to say that we all hoped he would check and not confirm Richard in the sanguine views he entertained just then.\n\n“Most willingly,” he retorted, “if I could. But, my dear Miss Summerson, I have no art, no disguise. If he takes me by the hand and leads me through Westminster Hall in an airy procession after fortune, I must go. If he says, ‘Skimpole, join the dance!’ I must join it. Common sense wouldn’t, I know, but I have NO common sense.”\n\nIt was very unfortunate for Richard, I said.\n\n“Do you think so!” returned Mr. Skimpole. “Don’t say that, don’t say that. Let us suppose him keeping company with Common Sense—an excellent man—a good deal wrinkled—dreadfully practical—change for a ten-pound note in every pocket—ruled account-book in his hand—say, upon the whole, resembling a tax-gatherer. Our dear Richard, sanguine, ardent, overleaping obstacles, bursting with poetry like a young bud, says to this highly respectable companion, ‘I see a golden prospect before me; it’s very bright, it’s very beautiful, it’s very joyous; here I go, bounding over the landscape to come at it!’ The respectable companion instantly knocks him down with the ruled account-book; tells him in a literal, prosaic way that he sees no such thing; shows him it’s nothing but fees, fraud, horsehair wigs, and black gowns. Now you know that’s a painful change—sensible in the last degree, I have no doubt, but disagreeable. I can’t do it. I haven’t got the ruled account-book, I have none of the tax-gathering elements in my composition, I am not at all respectable, and I don’t want to be. Odd perhaps, but so it is!”\n\nIt was idle to say more, so I proposed that we should join Ada and Richard, who were a little in advance, and I gave up Mr. Skimpole in despair. He had been over the Hall in the course of the morning and whimsically described the family pictures as we walked. There were such portentous shepherdesses among the Ladies Dedlock dead and gone, he told us, that peaceful crooks became weapons of assault in their hands. They tended their flocks severely in buckram and powder and put their sticking-plaster patches on to terrify commoners as the chiefs of some other tribes put on their war-paint. There was a Sir Somebody Dedlock, with a battle, a sprung-mine, volumes of smoke, flashes of lightning, a town on fire, and a stormed fort, all in full action between his horse’s two hind legs, showing, he supposed, how little a Dedlock made of such trifles. The whole race he represented as having evidently been, in life, what he called “stuffed people”—a large collection, glassy eyed, set up in the most approved manner on their various twigs and perches, very correct, perfectly free from animation, and always in glass cases.\n\nI was not so easy now during any reference to the name but that I felt it a relief when Richard, with an exclamation of surprise, hurried away to meet a stranger whom he first descried coming slowly towards us.\n\n“Dear me!” said Mr. Skimpole. “Vholes!”\n\nWe asked if that were a friend of Richard’s.\n\n“Friend and legal adviser,” said Mr. Skimpole. “Now, my dear Miss Summerson, if you want common sense, responsibility, and respectability, all united—if you want an exemplary man—Vholes is THE man.”\n\nWe had not known, we said, that Richard was assisted by any gentleman of that name.\n\n“When he emerged from legal infancy,” returned Mr. Skimpole, “he parted from our conversational friend Kenge and took up, I believe, with Vholes. Indeed, I know he did, because I introduced him to Vholes.”\n\n“Had you known him long?” asked Ada.\n\n“Vholes? My dear Miss Clare, I had had that kind of acquaintance with him which I have had with several gentlemen of his profession. He had done something or other in a very agreeable, civil manner—taken proceedings, I think, is the expression—which ended in the proceeding of his taking ME. Somebody was so good as to step in and pay the money—something and fourpence was the amount; I forget the pounds and shillings, but I know it ended with fourpence, because it struck me at the time as being so odd that I could owe anybody fourpence—and after that I brought them together. Vholes asked me for the introduction, and I gave it. Now I come to think of it,” he looked inquiringly at us with his frankest smile as he made the discovery, “Vholes bribed me, perhaps? He gave me something and called it commission. Was it a five-pound note? Do you know, I think it MUST have been a five-pound note!”\n\nHis further consideration of the point was prevented by Richard’s coming back to us in an excited state and hastily representing Mr. Vholes—a sallow man with pinched lips that looked as if they were cold, a red eruption here and there upon his face, tall and thin, about fifty years of age, high-shouldered, and stooping. Dressed in black, black-gloved, and buttoned to the chin, there was nothing so remarkable in him as a lifeless manner and a slow, fixed way he had of looking at Richard.\n\n“I hope I don’t disturb you, ladies,” said Mr. Vholes, and now I observed that he was further remarkable for an inward manner of speaking. “I arranged with Mr. Carstone that he should always know when his cause was in the Chancellor’s paper, and being informed by one of my clerks last night after post time that it stood, rather unexpectedly, in the paper for to-morrow, I put myself into the coach early this morning and came down to confer with him.”\n\n“Yes,” said Richard, flushed, and looking triumphantly at Ada and me, “we don’t do these things in the old slow way now. We spin along now! Mr. Vholes, we must hire something to get over to the post town in, and catch the mail to-night, and go up by it!”\n\n“Anything you please, sir,” returned Mr. Vholes. “I am quite at your service.”\n\n“Let me see,” said Richard, looking at his watch. “If I run down to the Dedlock, and get my portmanteau fastened up, and order a gig, or a chaise, or whatever’s to be got, we shall have an hour then before starting. I’ll come back to tea. Cousin Ada, will you and Esther take care of Mr. Vholes when I am gone?”\n\nHe was away directly, in his heat and hurry, and was soon lost in the dusk of evening. We who were left walked on towards the house.\n\n“Is Mr. Carstone’s presence necessary to-morrow, Sir?” said I. “Can it do any good?”\n\n“No, miss,” Mr. Vholes replied. “I am not aware that it can.”\n\nBoth Ada and I expressed our regret that he should go, then, only to be disappointed.\n\n“Mr. Carstone has laid down the principle of watching his own interests,” said Mr. Vholes, “and when a client lays down his own principle, and it is not immoral, it devolves upon me to carry it out. I wish in business to be exact and open. I am a widower with three daughters—Emma, Jane, and Caroline—and my desire is so to discharge the duties of life as to leave them a good name. This appears to be a pleasant spot, miss.”\n\nThe remark being made to me in consequence of my being next him as we walked, I assented and enumerated its chief attractions.\n\n“Indeed?” said Mr. Vholes. “I have the privilege of supporting an aged father in the Vale of Taunton—his native place—and I admire that country very much. I had no idea there was anything so attractive here.”\n\nTo keep up the conversation, I asked Mr. Vholes if he would like to live altogether in the country.\n\n“There, miss,” said he, “you touch me on a tender string. My health is not good (my digestion being much impaired), and if I had only myself to consider, I should take refuge in rural habits, especially as the cares of business have prevented me from ever coming much into contact with general society, and particularly with ladies’ society, which I have most wished to mix in. But with my three daughters, Emma, Jane, and Caroline—and my aged father—I cannot afford to be selfish. It is true I have no longer to maintain a dear grandmother who died in her hundred and second year, but enough remains to render it indispensable that the mill should be always going.”\n\nIt required some attention to hear him on account of his inward speaking and his lifeless manner.\n\n“You will excuse my having mentioned my daughters,” he said. “They are my weak point. I wish to leave the poor girls some little independence, as well as a good name.”\n\nWe now arrived at Mr. Boythorn’s house, where the tea-table, all prepared, was awaiting us. Richard came in restless and hurried shortly afterwards, and leaning over Mr. Vholes’s chair, whispered something in his ear. Mr. Vholes replied aloud—or as nearly aloud I suppose as he had ever replied to anything—“You will drive me, will you, sir? It is all the same to me, sir. Anything you please. I am quite at your service.”\n\nWe understood from what followed that Mr. Skimpole was to be left until the morning to occupy the two places which had been already paid for. As Ada and I were both in low spirits concerning Richard and very sorry so to part with him, we made it as plain as we politely could that we should leave Mr. Skimpole to the Dedlock Arms and retire when the night-travellers were gone.\n\nRichard’s high spirits carrying everything before them, we all went out together to the top of the hill above the village, where he had ordered a gig to wait and where we found a man with a lantern standing at the head of the gaunt pale horse that had been harnessed to it.\n\nI never shall forget those two seated side by side in the lantern’s light, Richard all flush and fire and laughter, with the reins in his hand; Mr. Vholes quite still, black-gloved, and buttoned up, looking at him as if he were looking at his prey and charming it. I have before me the whole picture of the warm dark night, the summer lightning, the dusty track of road closed in by hedgerows and high trees, the gaunt pale horse with his ears pricked up, and the driving away at speed to Jarndyce and Jarndyce.\n\nMy dear girl told me that night how Richard’s being thereafter prosperous or ruined, befriended or deserted, could only make this difference to her, that the more he needed love from one unchanging heart, the more love that unchanging heart would have to give him; how he thought of her through his present errors, and she would think of him at all times—never of herself if she could devote herself to him, never of her own delights if she could minister to his.\n\nAnd she kept her word?\n\nI look along the road before me, where the distance already shortens and the journey’s end is growing visible; and true and good above the dead sea of the Chancery suit and all the ashy fruit it cast ashore, I think I see my darling.\nA Struggle\n\nWhen our time came for returning to Bleak House again, we were punctual to the day and were received with an overpowering welcome. I was perfectly restored to health and strength, and finding my housekeeping keys laid ready for me in my room, rang myself in as if I had been a new year, with a merry little peal. “Once more, duty, duty, Esther,” said I; “and if you are not overjoyed to do it, more than cheerfully and contentedly, through anything and everything, you ought to be. That’s all I have to say to you, my dear!”\n\nThe first few mornings were mornings of so much bustle and business, devoted to such settlements of accounts, such repeated journeys to and fro between the growlery and all other parts of the house, so many rearrangements of drawers and presses, and such a general new beginning altogether, that I had not a moment’s leisure. But when these arrangements were completed and everything was in order, I paid a visit of a few hours to London, which something in the letter I had destroyed at Chesney Wold had induced me to decide upon in my own mind.\n\nI made Caddy Jellyby—her maiden name was so natural to me that I always called her by it—the pretext for this visit and wrote her a note previously asking the favour of her company on a little business expedition. Leaving home very early in the morning, I got to London by stage-coach in such good time that I got to Newman Street with the day before me.\n\nCaddy, who had not seen me since her wedding-day, was so glad and so affectionate that I was half inclined to fear I should make her husband jealous. But he was, in his way, just as bad—I mean as good; and in short it was the old story, and nobody would leave me any possibility of doing anything meritorious.\n\nThe elder Mr. Turveydrop was in bed, I found, and Caddy was milling his chocolate, which a melancholy little boy who was an apprentice—it seemed such a curious thing to be apprenticed to the trade of dancing—was waiting to carry upstairs. Her father-in-law was extremely kind and considerate, Caddy told me, and they lived most happily together. (When she spoke of their living together, she meant that the old gentleman had all the good things and all the good lodging, while she and her husband had what they could get, and were poked into two corner rooms over the Mews.)\n\n“And how is your mama, Caddy?” said I.\n\n“Why, I hear of her, Esther,” replied Caddy, “through Pa, but I see very little of her. We are good friends, I am glad to say, but Ma thinks there is something absurd in my having married a dancing-master, and she is rather afraid of its extending to her.”\n\nIt struck me that if Mrs. Jellyby had discharged her own natural duties and obligations before she swept the horizon with a telescope in search of others, she would have taken the best precautions against becoming absurd, but I need scarcely observe that I kept this to myself.\n\n“And your papa, Caddy?”\n\n“He comes here every evening,” returned Caddy, “and is so fond of sitting in the corner there that it’s a treat to see him.”\n\nLooking at the corner, I plainly perceived the mark of Mr. Jellyby’s head against the wall. It was consolatory to know that he had found such a resting-place for it.\n\n“And you, Caddy,” said I, “you are always busy, I’ll be bound?”\n\n“Well, my dear,” returned Caddy, “I am indeed, for to tell you a grand secret, I am qualifying myself to give lessons. Prince’s health is not strong, and I want to be able to assist him. What with schools, and classes here, and private pupils, AND the apprentices, he really has too much to do, poor fellow!”\n\nThe notion of the apprentices was still so odd to me that I asked Caddy if there were many of them.\n\n“Four,” said Caddy. “One in-door, and three out. They are very good children; only when they get together they WILL play—children-like—instead of attending to their work. So the little boy you saw just now waltzes by himself in the empty kitchen, and we distribute the others over the house as well as we can.”\n\n“That is only for their steps, of course?” said I.\n\n“Only for their steps,” said Caddy. “In that way they practise, so many hours at a time, whatever steps they happen to be upon. They dance in the academy, and at this time of year we do figures at five every morning.”\n\n“Why, what a laborious life!” I exclaimed.\n\n“I assure you, my dear,” returned Caddy, smiling, “when the out-door apprentices ring us up in the morning (the bell rings into our room, not to disturb old Mr. Turveydrop), and when I put up the window and see them standing on the door-step with their little pumps under their arms, I am actually reminded of the Sweeps.”\n\nAll this presented the art to me in a singular light, to be sure. Caddy enjoyed the effect of her communication and cheerfully recounted the particulars of her own studies.\n\n“You see, my dear, to save expense I ought to know something of the piano, and I ought to know something of the kit too, and consequently I have to practise those two instruments as well as the details of our profession. If Ma had been like anybody else, I might have had some little musical knowledge to begin upon. However, I hadn’t any; and that part of the work is, at first, a little discouraging, I must allow. But I have a very good ear, and I am used to drudgery—I have to thank Ma for that, at all events—and where there’s a will there’s a way, you know, Esther, the world over.” Saying these words, Caddy laughingly sat down at a little jingling square piano and really rattled off a quadrille with great spirit. Then she good-humouredly and blushingly got up again, and while she still laughed herself, said, “Don’t laugh at me, please; that’s a dear girl!”\n\nI would sooner have cried, but I did neither. I encouraged her and praised her with all my heart. For I conscientiously believed, dancing-master’s wife though she was, and dancing-mistress though in her limited ambition she aspired to be, she had struck out a natural, wholesome, loving course of industry and perseverance that was quite as good as a mission.\n\n“My dear,” said Caddy, delighted, “you can’t think how you cheer me. I shall owe you, you don’t know how much. What changes, Esther, even in my small world! You recollect that first night, when I was so unpolite and inky? Who would have thought, then, of my ever teaching people to dance, of all other possibilities and impossibilities!”\n\nHer husband, who had left us while we had this chat, now coming back, preparatory to exercising the apprentices in the ball-room, Caddy informed me she was quite at my disposal. But it was not my time yet, I was glad to tell her, for I should have been vexed to take her away then. Therefore we three adjourned to the apprentices together, and I made one in the dance.\n\nThe apprentices were the queerest little people. Besides the melancholy boy, who, I hoped, had not been made so by waltzing alone in the empty kitchen, there were two other boys and one dirty little limp girl in a gauzy dress. Such a precocious little girl, with such a dowdy bonnet on (that, too, of a gauzy texture), who brought her sandalled shoes in an old threadbare velvet reticule. Such mean little boys, when they were not dancing, with string, and marbles, and cramp-bones in their pockets, and the most untidy legs and feet—and heels particularly.\n\nI asked Caddy what had made their parents choose this profession for them. Caddy said she didn’t know; perhaps they were designed for teachers, perhaps for the stage. They were all people in humble circumstances, and the melancholy boy’s mother kept a ginger-beer shop.\n\nWe danced for an hour with great gravity, the melancholy child doing wonders with his lower extremities, in which there appeared to be some sense of enjoyment though it never rose above his waist. Caddy, while she was observant of her husband and was evidently founded upon him, had acquired a grace and self-possession of her own, which, united to her pretty face and figure, was uncommonly agreeable. She already relieved him of much of the instruction of these young people, and he seldom interfered except to walk his part in the figure if he had anything to do in it. He always played the tune. The affectation of the gauzy child, and her condescension to the boys, was a sight. And thus we danced an hour by the clock.\n\nWhen the practice was concluded, Caddy’s husband made himself ready to go out of town to a school, and Caddy ran away to get ready to go out with me. I sat in the ball-room in the interval, contemplating the apprentices. The two out-door boys went upon the staircase to put on their half-boots and pull the in-door boy’s hair, as I judged from the nature of his objections. Returning with their jackets buttoned and their pumps stuck in them, they then produced packets of cold bread and meat and bivouacked under a painted lyre on the wall. The little gauzy child, having whisked her sandals into the reticule and put on a trodden-down pair of shoes, shook her head into the dowdy bonnet at one shake, and answering my inquiry whether she liked dancing by replying, “Not with boys,” tied it across her chin, and went home contemptuous.\n\n“Old Mr. Turveydrop is so sorry,” said Caddy, “that he has not finished dressing yet and cannot have the pleasure of seeing you before you go. You are such a favourite of his, Esther.”\n\nI expressed myself much obliged to him, but did not think it necessary to add that I readily dispensed with this attention.\n\n“It takes him a long time to dress,” said Caddy, “because he is very much looked up to in such things, you know, and has a reputation to support. You can’t think how kind he is to Pa. He talks to Pa of an evening about the Prince Regent, and I never saw Pa so interested.”\n\nThere was something in the picture of Mr. Turveydrop bestowing his deportment on Mr. Jellyby that quite took my fancy. I asked Caddy if he brought her papa out much.\n\n“No,” said Caddy, “I don’t know that he does that, but he talks to Pa, and Pa greatly admires him, and listens, and likes it. Of course I am aware that Pa has hardly any claims to deportment, but they get on together delightfully. You can’t think what good companions they make. I never saw Pa take snuff before in my life, but he takes one pinch out of Mr. Turveydrop’s box regularly and keeps putting it to his nose and taking it away again all the evening.”\n\nThat old Mr. Turveydrop should ever, in the chances and changes of life, have come to the rescue of Mr. Jellyby from Borrioboola-Gha appeared to me to be one of the pleasantest of oddities.\n\n“As to Peepy,” said Caddy with a little hesitation, “whom I was most afraid of—next to having any family of my own, Esther—as an inconvenience to Mr. Turveydrop, the kindness of the old gentleman to that child is beyond everything. He asks to see him, my dear! He lets him take the newspaper up to him in bed; he gives him the crusts of his toast to eat; he sends him on little errands about the house; he tells him to come to me for sixpences. In short,” said Caddy cheerily, “and not to prose, I am a very fortunate girl and ought to be very grateful. Where are we going, Esther?”\n\n“To the Old Street Road,” said I, “where I have a few words to say to the solicitor’s clerk who was sent to meet me at the coach-office on the very day when I came to London and first saw you, my dear. Now I think of it, the gentleman who brought us to your house.”\n\n“Then, indeed, I seem to be naturally the person to go with you,” returned Caddy.\n\nTo the Old Street Road we went and there inquired at Mrs. Guppy’s residence for Mrs. Guppy. Mrs. Guppy, occupying the parlours and having indeed been visibly in danger of cracking herself like a nut in the front-parlour door by peeping out before she was asked for, immediately presented herself and requested us to walk in. She was an old lady in a large cap, with rather a red nose and rather an unsteady eye, but smiling all over. Her close little sitting-room was prepared for a visit, and there was a portrait of her son in it which, I had almost written here, was more like than life: it insisted upon him with such obstinacy, and was so determined not to let him off.\n\nNot only was the portrait there, but we found the original there too. He was dressed in a great many colours and was discovered at a table reading law-papers with his forefinger to his forehead.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Guppy, rising, “this is indeed an oasis. Mother, will you be so good as to put a chair for the other lady and get out of the gangway.”\n\nMrs. Guppy, whose incessant smiling gave her quite a waggish appearance, did as her son requested and then sat down in a corner, holding her pocket handkerchief to her chest, like a fomentation, with both hands.\n\nI presented Caddy, and Mr. Guppy said that any friend of mine was more than welcome. I then proceeded to the object of my visit.\n\n“I took the liberty of sending you a note, sir,” said I.\n\nMr. Guppy acknowledged the receipt by taking it out of his breast-pocket, putting it to his lips, and returning it to his pocket with a bow. Mr. Guppy’s mother was so diverted that she rolled her head as she smiled and made a silent appeal to Caddy with her elbow.\n\n“Could I speak to you alone for a moment?” said I.\n\nAnything like the jocoseness of Mr. Guppy’s mother just now, I think I never saw. She made no sound of laughter, but she rolled her head, and shook it, and put her handkerchief to her mouth, and appealed to Caddy with her elbow, and her hand, and her shoulder, and was so unspeakably entertained altogether that it was with some difficulty she could marshal Caddy through the little folding-door into her bedroom adjoining.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Guppy, “you will excuse the waywardness of a parent ever mindful of a son’s appiness. My mother, though highly exasperating to the feelings, is actuated by maternal dictates.”\n\nI could hardly have believed that anybody could in a moment have turned so red or changed so much as Mr. Guppy did when I now put up my veil.\n\n“I asked the favour of seeing you for a few moments here,” said I, “in preference to calling at Mr. Kenge’s because, remembering what you said on an occasion when you spoke to me in confidence, I feared I might otherwise cause you some embarrassment, Mr. Guppy.”\n\nI caused him embarrassment enough as it was, I am sure. I never saw such faltering, such confusion, such amazement and apprehension.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” stammered Mr. Guppy, “I—I—beg your pardon, but in our profession—we—we—find it necessary to be explicit. You have referred to an occasion, miss, when I—when I did myself the honour of making a declaration which—”\n\nSomething seemed to rise in his throat that he could not possibly swallow. He put his hand there, coughed, made faces, tried again to swallow it, coughed again, made faces again, looked all round the room, and fluttered his papers.\n\n“A kind of giddy sensation has come upon me, miss,” he explained, “which rather knocks me over. I—er—a little subject to this sort of thing—er—by George!”\n\nI gave him a little time to recover. He consumed it in putting his hand to his forehead and taking it away again, and in backing his chair into the corner behind him.\n\n“My intention was to remark, miss,” said Mr. Guppy, “dear me—something bronchial, I think—hem!—to remark that you was so good on that occasion as to repel and repudiate that declaration. You—you wouldn’t perhaps object to admit that? Though no witnesses are present, it might be a satisfaction to—to your mind—if you was to put in that admission.”\n\n“There can be no doubt,” said I, “that I declined your proposal without any reservation or qualification whatever, Mr. Guppy.”\n\n“Thank you, miss,” he returned, measuring the table with his troubled hands. “So far that’s satisfactory, and it does you credit. Er—this is certainly bronchial!—must be in the tubes—er—you wouldn’t perhaps be offended if I was to mention—not that it’s necessary, for your own good sense or any person’s sense must show ’em that—if I was to mention that such declaration on my part was final, and there terminated?”\n\n“I quite understand that,” said I.\n\n“Perhaps—er—it may not be worth the form, but it might be a satisfaction to your mind—perhaps you wouldn’t object to admit that, miss?” said Mr. Guppy.\n\n“I admit it most fully and freely,” said I.\n\n“Thank you,” returned Mr. Guppy. “Very honourable, I am sure. I regret that my arrangements in life, combined with circumstances over which I have no control, will put it out of my power ever to fall back upon that offer or to renew it in any shape or form whatever, but it will ever be a retrospect entwined—er—with friendship’s bowers.” Mr. Guppy’s bronchitis came to his relief and stopped his measurement of the table.\n\n“I may now perhaps mention what I wished to say to you?” I began.\n\n“I shall be honoured, I am sure,” said Mr. Guppy. “I am so persuaded that your own good sense and right feeling, miss, will—will keep you as square as possible—that I can have nothing but pleasure, I am sure, in hearing any observations you may wish to offer.”\n\n“You were so good as to imply, on that occasion—”\n\n“Excuse me, miss,” said Mr. Guppy, “but we had better not travel out of the record into implication. I cannot admit that I implied anything.”\n\n“You said on that occasion,” I recommenced, “that you might possibly have the means of advancing my interests and promoting my fortunes by making discoveries of which I should be the subject. I presume that you founded that belief upon your general knowledge of my being an orphan girl, indebted for everything to the benevolence of Mr. Jarndyce. Now, the beginning and the end of what I have come to beg of you is, Mr. Guppy, that you will have the kindness to relinquish all idea of so serving me. I have thought of this sometimes, and I have thought of it most lately—since I have been ill. At length I have decided, in case you should at any time recall that purpose and act upon it in any way, to come to you and assure you that you are altogether mistaken. You could make no discovery in reference to me that would do me the least service or give me the least pleasure. I am acquainted with my personal history, and I have it in my power to assure you that you never can advance my welfare by such means. You may, perhaps, have abandoned this project a long time. If so, excuse my giving you unnecessary trouble. If not, I entreat you, on the assurance I have given you, henceforth to lay it aside. I beg you to do this, for my peace.”\n\n“I am bound to confess,” said Mr. Guppy, “that you express yourself, miss, with that good sense and right feeling for which I gave you credit. Nothing can be more satisfactory than such right feeling, and if I mistook any intentions on your part just now, I am prepared to tender a full apology. I should wish to be understood, miss, as hereby offering that apology—limiting it, as your own good sense and right feeling will point out the necessity of, to the present proceedings.”\n\nI must say for Mr. Guppy that the snuffling manner he had had upon him improved very much. He seemed truly glad to be able to do something I asked, and he looked ashamed.\n\n“If you will allow me to finish what I have to say at once so that I may have no occasion to resume,” I went on, seeing him about to speak, “you will do me a kindness, sir. I come to you as privately as possible because you announced this impression of yours to me in a confidence which I have really wished to respect—and which I always have respected, as you remember. I have mentioned my illness. There really is no reason why I should hesitate to say that I know very well that any little delicacy I might have had in making a request to you is quite removed. Therefore I make the entreaty I have now preferred, and I hope you will have sufficient consideration for me to accede to it.”\n\nI must do Mr. Guppy the further justice of saying that he had looked more and more ashamed and that he looked most ashamed and very earnest when he now replied with a burning face, “Upon my word and honour, upon my life, upon my soul, Miss Summerson, as I am a living man, I’ll act according to your wish! I’ll never go another step in opposition to it. I’ll take my oath to it if it will be any satisfaction to you. In what I promise at this present time touching the matters now in question,” continued Mr. Guppy rapidly, as if he were repeating a familiar form of words, “I speak the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth, so—”\n\n“I am quite satisfied,” said I, rising at this point, “and I thank you very much. Caddy, my dear, I am ready!”\n\nMr. Guppy’s mother returned with Caddy (now making me the recipient of her silent laughter and her nudges), and we took our leave. Mr. Guppy saw us to the door with the air of one who was either imperfectly awake or walking in his sleep; and we left him there, staring.\n\nBut in a minute he came after us down the street without any hat, and with his long hair all blown about, and stopped us, saying fervently, “Miss Summerson, upon my honour and soul, you may depend upon me!”\n\n“I do,” said I, “quite confidently.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, miss,” said Mr. Guppy, going with one leg and staying with the other, “but this lady being present—your own witness—it might be a satisfaction to your mind (which I should wish to set at rest) if you was to repeat those admissions.”\n\n“Well, Caddy,” said I, turning to her, “perhaps you will not be surprised when I tell you, my dear, that there never has been any engagement—”\n\n“No proposal or promise of marriage whatsoever,” suggested Mr. Guppy.\n\n“No proposal or promise of marriage whatsoever,” said I, “between this gentleman—”\n\n“William Guppy, of Penton Place, Pentonville, in the county of Middlesex,” he murmured.\n\n“Between this gentleman, Mr. William Guppy, of Penton Place, Pentonville, in the county of Middlesex, and myself.”\n\n“Thank you, miss,” said Mr. Guppy. “Very full—er—excuse me—lady’s name, Christian and surname both?”\nI gave them.\n\n“Married woman, I believe?” said Mr. Guppy. “Married woman. Thank you. Formerly Caroline Jellyby, spinster, then of Thavies Inn, within the city of London, but extra-parochial; now of Newman Street, Oxford Street. Much obliged.”\n\nHe ran home and came running back again.\n\n“Touching that matter, you know, I really and truly am very sorry that my arrangements in life, combined with circumstances over which I have no control, should prevent a renewal of what was wholly terminated some time back,” said Mr. Guppy to me forlornly and despondently, “but it couldn’t be. Now COULD it, you know! I only put it to you.”\n\nI replied it certainly could not. The subject did not admit of a doubt. He thanked me and ran to his mother’s again—and back again.\n\n“It’s very honourable of you, miss, I am sure,” said Mr. Guppy. “If an altar could be erected in the bowers of friendship—but, upon my soul, you may rely upon me in every respect save and except the tender passion only!”\n\nThe struggle in Mr. Guppy’s breast and the numerous oscillations it occasioned him between his mother’s door and us were sufficiently conspicuous in the windy street (particularly as his hair wanted cutting) to make us hurry away. I did so with a lightened heart; but when we last looked back, Mr. Guppy was still oscillating in the same troubled state of mind.\n\nAttorney and Client\nThe name of Mr. Vholes, preceded by the legend Ground-Floor, is inscribed upon a door-post in Symond’s Inn, Chancery Lane—a little, pale, wall-eyed, woebegone inn like a large dust-binn of two compartments and a sifter. It looks as if Symond were a sparing man in his way and constructed his inn of old building materials which took kindly to the dry rot and to dirt and all things decaying and dismal, and perpetuated Symond’s memory with congenial shabbiness. Quartered in this dingy hatchment commemorative of Symond are the legal bearings of Mr. Vholes.\n\nMr. Vholes’s office, in disposition retiring and in situation retired, is squeezed up in a corner and blinks at a dead wall. Three feet of knotty-floored dark passage bring the client to Mr. Vholes’s jet-black door, in an angle profoundly dark on the brightest midsummer morning and encumbered by a black bulk-head of cellarage staircase against which belated civilians generally strike their brows. Mr. Vholes’s chambers are on so small a scale that one clerk can open the door without getting off his stool, while the other who elbows him at the same desk has equal facilities for poking the fire. A smell as of unwholesome sheep blending with the smell of must and dust is referable to the nightly (and often daily) consumption of mutton fat in candles and to the fretting of parchment forms and skins in greasy drawers. The atmosphere is otherwise stale and close. The place was last painted or whitewashed beyond the memory of man, and the two chimneys smoke, and there is a loose outer surface of soot everywhere, and the dull cracked windows in their heavy frames have but one piece of character in them, which is a determination to be always dirty and always shut unless coerced. This accounts for the phenomenon of the weaker of the two usually having a bundle of firewood thrust between its jaws in hot weather.\n\nMr. Vholes is a very respectable man. He has not a large business, but he is a very respectable man. He is allowed by the greater attorneys who have made good fortunes or are making them to be a most respectable man. He never misses a chance in his practice, which is a mark of respectability. He never takes any pleasure, which is another mark of respectability. He is reserved and serious, which is another mark of respectability. His digestion is impaired, which is highly respectable. And he is making hay of the grass which is flesh, for his three daughters. And his father is dependent on him in the Vale of Taunton.\n\nThe one great principle of the English law is to make business for itself. There is no other principle distinctly, certainly, and consistently maintained through all its narrow turnings. Viewed by this light it becomes a coherent scheme and not the monstrous maze the laity are apt to think it. Let them but once clearly perceive that its grand principle is to make business for itself at their expense, and surely they will cease to grumble.\n\nBut not perceiving this quite plainly—only seeing it by halves in a confused way—the laity sometimes suffer in peace and pocket, with a bad grace, and DO grumble very much. Then this respectability of Mr. Vholes is brought into powerful play against them. “Repeal this statute, my good sir?” says Mr. Kenge to a smarting client. “Repeal it, my dear sir? Never, with my consent. Alter this law, sir, and what will be the effect of your rash proceeding on a class of practitioners very worthily represented, allow me to say to you, by the opposite attorney in the case, Mr. Vholes? Sir, that class of practitioners would be swept from the face of the earth. Now you cannot afford—I will say, the social system cannot afford—to lose an order of men like Mr. Vholes. Diligent, persevering, steady, acute in business. My dear sir, I understand your present feelings against the existing state of things, which I grant to be a little hard in your case; but I can never raise my voice for the demolition of a class of men like Mr. Vholes.” The respectability of Mr. Vholes has even been cited with crushing effect before Parliamentary committees, as in the following blue minutes of a distinguished attorney’s evidence. “Question (number five hundred and seventeen thousand eight hundred and sixty-nine): If I understand you, these forms of practice indisputably occasion delay? Answer: Yes, some delay. Question: And great expense? Answer: Most assuredly they cannot be gone through for nothing. Question: And unspeakable vexation? Answer: I am not prepared to say that. They have never given ME any vexation; quite the contrary. Question: But you think that their abolition would damage a class of practitioners? Answer: I have no doubt of it. Question: Can you instance any type of that class? Answer: Yes. I would unhesitatingly mention Mr. Vholes. He would be ruined. Question: Mr. Vholes is considered, in the profession, a respectable man? Answer:”—which proved fatal to the inquiry for ten years—“Mr. Vholes is considered, in the profession, a MOST respectable man.”\n\nSo in familiar conversation, private authorities no less disinterested will remark that they don’t know what this age is coming to, that we are plunging down precipices, that now here is something else gone, that these changes are death to people like Vholes—a man of undoubted respectability, with a father in the Vale of Taunton, and three daughters at home. Take a few steps more in this direction, say they, and what is to become of Vholes’s father? Is he to perish? And of Vholes’s daughters? Are they to be shirt-makers, or governesses? As though, Mr. Vholes and his relations being minor cannibal chiefs and it being proposed to abolish cannibalism, indignant champions were to put the case thus: Make man-eating unlawful, and you starve the Vholeses!\n\nIn a word, Mr. Vholes, with his three daughters and his father in the Vale of Taunton, is continually doing duty, like a piece of timber, to shore up some decayed foundation that has become a pitfall and a nuisance. And with a great many people in a great many instances, the question is never one of a change from wrong to right (which is quite an extraneous consideration), but is always one of injury or advantage to that eminently respectable legion, Vholes.\n\nThe Chancellor is, within these ten minutes, “up” for the long vacation. Mr. Vholes, and his young client, and several blue bags hastily stuffed out of all regularity of form, as the larger sort of serpents are in their first gorged state, have returned to the official den. Mr. Vholes, quiet and unmoved, as a man of so much respectability ought to be, takes off his close black gloves as if he were skinning his hands, lifts off his tight hat as if he were scalping himself, and sits down at his desk. The client throws his hat and gloves upon the ground—tosses them anywhere, without looking after them or caring where they go; flings himself into a chair, half sighing and half groaning; rests his aching head upon his hand and looks the portrait of young despair.\n\n“Again nothing done!” says Richard. “Nothing, nothing done!”\n\n“Don’t say nothing done, sir,” returns the placid Vholes. “That is scarcely fair, sir, scarcely fair!”\n\n“Why, what IS done?” says Richard, turning gloomily upon him.\n\n“That may not be the whole question,” returns Vholes, “The question may branch off into what is doing, what is doing?”\n\n“And what is doing?” asks the moody client.\n\nVholes, sitting with his arms on the desk, quietly bringing the tips of his five right fingers to meet the tips of his five left fingers, and quietly separating them again, and fixedly and slowly looking at his client, replies, “A good deal is doing, sir. We have put our shoulders to the wheel, Mr. Carstone, and the wheel is going round.”\n\n“Yes, with Ixion on it. How am I to get through the next four or five accursed months?” exclaims the young man, rising from his chair and walking about the room.\n\n“Mr. C.,” returns Vholes, following him close with his eyes wherever he goes, “your spirits are hasty, and I am sorry for it on your account. Excuse me if I recommend you not to chafe so much, not to be so impetuous, not to wear yourself out so. You should have more patience. You should sustain yourself better.”\n\n“I ought to imitate you, in fact, Mr. Vholes?” says Richard, sitting down again with an impatient laugh and beating the devil’s tattoo with his boot on the patternless carpet.\n\n“Sir,” returns Vholes, always looking at the client as if he were making a lingering meal of him with his eyes as well as with his professional appetite. “Sir,” returns Vholes with his inward manner of speech and his bloodless quietude, “I should not have had the presumption to propose myself as a model for your imitation or any man’s. Let me but leave the good name to my three daughters, and that is enough for me; I am not a self-seeker. But since you mention me so pointedly, I will acknowledge that I should like to impart to you a little of my—come, sir, you are disposed to call it insensibility, and I am sure I have no objection—say insensibility—a little of my insensibility.”\n\n“Mr. Vholes,” explains the client, somewhat abashed, “I had no intention to accuse you of insensibility.”\n\n“I think you had, sir, without knowing it,” returns the equable Vholes. “Very naturally. It is my duty to attend to your interests with a cool head, and I can quite understand that to your excited feelings I may appear, at such times as the present, insensible. My daughters may know me better; my aged father may know me better. But they have known me much longer than you have, and the confiding eye of affection is not the distrustful eye of business. Not that I complain, sir, of the eye of business being distrustful; quite the contrary. In attending to your interests, I wish to have all possible checks upon me; it is right that I should have them; I court inquiry. But your interests demand that I should be cool and methodical, Mr. Carstone; and I cannot be otherwise—no, sir, not even to please you.”\n\nMr. Vholes, after glancing at the official cat who is patiently watching a mouse’s hole, fixes his charmed gaze again on his young client and proceeds in his buttoned-up, half-audible voice as if there were an unclean spirit in him that will neither come out nor speak out, “What are you to do, sir, you inquire, during the vacation. I should hope you gentlemen of the army may find many means of amusing yourselves if you give your minds to it. If you had asked me what I was to do during the vacation, I could have answered you more readily. I am to attend to your interests. I am to be found here, day by day, attending to your interests. That is my duty, Mr. C., and term-time or vacation makes no difference to me. If you wish to consult me as to your interests, you will find me here at all times alike. Other professional men go out of town. I don’t. Not that I blame them for going; I merely say I don’t go. This desk is your rock, sir!”\n\nMr. Vholes gives it a rap, and it sounds as hollow as a coffin. Not to Richard, though. There is encouragement in the sound to him. Perhaps Mr. Vholes knows there is.\n\n“I am perfectly aware, Mr. Vholes,” says Richard, more familiarly and good-humouredly, “that you are the most reliable fellow in the world and that to have to do with you is to have to do with a man of business who is not to be hoodwinked. But put yourself in my case, dragging on this dislocated life, sinking deeper and deeper into difficulty every day, continually hoping and continually disappointed, conscious of change upon change for the worse in myself, and of no change for the better in anything else, and you will find it a dark-looking case sometimes, as I do.”\n\n“You know,” says Mr. Vholes, “that I never give hopes, sir. I told you from the first, Mr. C., that I never give hopes. Particularly in a case like this, where the greater part of the costs comes out of the estate, I should not be considerate of my good name if I gave hopes. It might seem as if costs were my object. Still, when you say there is no change for the better, I must, as a bare matter of fact, deny that.”\n\n“Aye?” returns Richard, brightening. “But how do you make it out?”\n\n“Mr. Carstone, you are represented by—”\n\n“You said just now—a rock.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” says Mr. Vholes, gently shaking his head and rapping the hollow desk, with a sound as if ashes were falling on ashes, and dust on dust, “a rock. That’s something. You are separately represented, and no longer hidden and lost in the interests of others. THAT’S something. The suit does not sleep; we wake it up, we air it, we walk it about. THAT’S something. It’s not all Jarndyce, in fact as well as in name. THAT’S something. Nobody has it all his own way now, sir. And THAT’S something, surely.”\n\nRichard, his face flushing suddenly, strikes the desk with his clenched hand.\n\n“Mr. Vholes! If any man had told me when I first went to John Jarndyce’s house that he was anything but the disinterested friend he seemed—that he was what he has gradually turned out to be—I could have found no words strong enough to repel the slander; I could not have defended him too ardently. So little did I know of the world! Whereas now I do declare to you that he becomes to me the embodiment of the suit; that in place of its being an abstraction, it is John Jarndyce; that the more I suffer, the more indignant I am with him; that every new delay and every new disappointment is only a new injury from John Jarndyce’s hand.”\n\n“No, no,” says Vholes. “Don’t say so. We ought to have patience, all of us. Besides, I never disparage, sir. I never disparage.”\n\n“Mr. Vholes,” returns the angry client. “You know as well as I that he would have strangled the suit if he could.”\n\n“He was not active in it,” Mr. Vholes admits with an appearance of reluctance. “He certainly was not active in it. But however, but however, he might have had amiable intentions. Who can read the heart, Mr. C.!”\n\n“You can,” returns Richard.\n\n“Well enough to know what his intentions were. Are or are not our interests conflicting? Tell—me—that!” says Richard, accompanying his last three words with three raps on his rock of trust.\n\n“Mr. C.,” returns Vholes, immovable in attitude and never winking his hungry eyes, “I should be wanting in my duty as your professional adviser, I should be departing from my fidelity to your interests, if I represented those interests as identical with the interests of Mr. Jarndyce. They are no such thing, sir. I never impute motives; I both have and am a father, and I never impute motives. But I must not shrink from a professional duty, even if it sows dissensions in families. I understand you to be now consulting me professionally as to your interests? You are so? I reply, then, they are not identical with those of Mr. Jarndyce.”\n\n“Of course they are not!” cries Richard. “You found that out long ago.”\n\n“Mr. C.,” returns Vholes, “I wish to say no more of any third party than is necessary. I wish to leave my good name unsullied, together with any little property of which I may become possessed through industry and perseverance, to my daughters Emma, Jane, and Caroline. I also desire to live in amity with my professional brethren. When Mr. Skimpole did me the honour, sir—I will not say the very high honour, for I never stoop to flattery—of bringing us together in this room, I mentioned to you that I could offer no opinion or advice as to your interests while those interests were entrusted to another member of the profession. And I spoke in such terms as I was bound to speak of Kenge and Carboy’s office, which stands high. You, sir, thought fit to withdraw your interests from that keeping nevertheless and to offer them to me. You brought them with clean hands, sir, and I accepted them with clean hands. Those interests are now paramount in this office. My digestive functions, as you may have heard me mention, are not in a good state, and rest might improve them; but I shall not rest, sir, while I am your representative. Whenever you want me, you will find me here. Summon me anywhere, and I will come. During the long vacation, sir, I shall devote my leisure to studying your interests more and more closely and to making arrangements for moving heaven and earth (including, of course, the Chancellor) after Michaelmas term; and when I ultimately congratulate you, sir,” says Mr. Vholes with the severity of a determined man, “when I ultimately congratulate you, sir, with all my heart, on your accession to fortune—which, but that I never give hopes, I might say something further about—you will owe me nothing beyond whatever little balance may be then outstanding of the costs as between solicitor and client not included in the taxed costs allowed out of the estate. I pretend to no claim upon you, Mr. C., but for the zealous and active discharge—not the languid and routine discharge, sir: that much credit I stipulate for—of my professional duty. My duty prosperously ended, all between us is ended.”\n\nVholes finally adds, by way of rider to this declaration of his principles, that as Mr. Carstone is about to rejoin his regiment, perhaps Mr. C. will favour him with an order on his agent for twenty pounds on account.\n\n“For there have been many little consultations and attendances of late, sir,” observes Vholes, turning over the leaves of his diary, “and these things mount up, and I don’t profess to be a man of capital. When we first entered on our present relations I stated to you openly—it is a principle of mine that there never can be too much openness between solicitor and client—that I was not a man of capital and that if capital was your object you had better leave your papers in Kenge’s office. No, Mr. C., you will find none of the advantages or disadvantages of capital here, sir. This,” Vholes gives the desk one hollow blow again, “is your rock; it pretends to be nothing more.”\n\nThe client, with his dejection insensibly relieved and his vague hopes rekindled, takes pen and ink and writes the draft, not without perplexed consideration and calculation of the date it may bear, implying scant effects in the agent’s hands. All the while, Vholes, buttoned up in body and mind, looks at him attentively. All the while, Vholes’s official cat watches the mouse’s hole.\n\nLastly, the client, shaking hands, beseeches Mr. Vholes, for heaven’s sake and earth’s sake, to do his utmost to “pull him through” the Court of Chancery. Mr. Vholes, who never gives hopes, lays his palm upon the client’s shoulder and answers with a smile, “Always here, sir. Personally, or by letter, you will always find me here, sir, with my shoulder to the wheel.” Thus they part, and Vholes, left alone, employs himself in carrying sundry little matters out of his diary into his draft bill book for the ultimate behoof of his three daughters. So might an industrious fox or bear make up his account of chickens or stray travellers with an eye to his cubs, not to disparage by that word the three raw-visaged, lank, and buttoned-up maidens who dwell with the parent Vholes in an earthy cottage situated in a damp garden at Kennington.\n\nRichard, emerging from the heavy shade of Symond’s Inn into the sunshine of Chancery Lane—for there happens to be sunshine there to-day—walks thoughtfully on, and turns into Lincoln’s Inn, and passes under the shadow of the Lincoln’s Inn trees. On many such loungers have the speckled shadows of those trees often fallen; on the like bent head, the bitten nail, the lowering eye, the lingering step, the purposeless and dreamy air, the good consuming and consumed, the life turned sour. This lounger is not shabby yet, but that may come. Chancery, which knows no wisdom but in precedent, is very rich in such precedents; and why should one be different from ten thousand?\n\nYet the time is so short since his depreciation began that as he saunters away, reluctant to leave the spot for some long months together, though he hates it, Richard himself may feel his own case as if it were a startling one. While his heart is heavy with corroding care, suspense, distrust, and doubt, it may have room for some sorrowful wonder when he recalls how different his first visit there, how different he, how different all the colours of his mind. But injustice breeds injustice; the fighting with shadows and being defeated by them necessitates the setting up of substances to combat; from the impalpable suit which no man alive can understand, the time for that being long gone by, it has become a gloomy relief to turn to the palpable figure of the friend who would have saved him from this ruin and make HIM his enemy. Richard has told Vholes the truth. Is he in a hardened or a softened mood, he still lays his injuries equally at that door; he was thwarted, in that quarter, of a set purpose, and that purpose could only originate in the one subject that is resolving his existence into itself; besides, it is a justification to him in his own eyes to have an embodied antagonist and oppressor.\n\nIs Richard a monster in all this, or would Chancery be found rich in such precedents too if they could be got for citation from the Recording Angel?\n\nTwo pairs of eyes not unused to such people look after him, as, biting his nails and brooding, he crosses the square and is swallowed up by the shadow of the southern gateway. Mr. Guppy and Mr. Weevle are the possessors of those eyes, and they have been leaning in conversation against the low stone parapet under the trees. He passes close by them, seeing nothing but the ground.\n\n“William,” says Mr. Weevle, adjusting his whiskers, “there’s combustion going on there! It’s not a case of spontaneous, but it’s smouldering combustion it is.”\n\n“Ah!” says Mr. Guppy. “He wouldn’t keep out of Jarndyce, and I suppose he’s over head and ears in debt. I never knew much of him. He was as high as the monument when he was on trial at our place. A good riddance to me, whether as clerk or client! Well, Tony, that as I was mentioning is what they’re up to.”\n\nMr. Guppy, refolding his arms, resettles himself against the parapet, as resuming a conversation of interest.\n\n“They are still up to it, sir,” says Mr. Guppy, “still taking stock, still examining papers, still going over the heaps and heaps of rubbish. At this rate they’ll be at it these seven years.”\n\n“And Small is helping?”\n\n“Small left us at a week’s notice. Told Kenge his grandfather’s business was too much for the old gentleman and he could better himself by undertaking it. There had been a coolness between myself and Small on account of his being so close. But he said you and I began it, and as he had me there—for we did—I put our acquaintance on the old footing. That’s how I come to know what they’re up to.”\n\n“You haven’t looked in at all?”\n\n“Tony,” says Mr. Guppy, a little disconcerted, “to be unreserved with you, I don’t greatly relish the house, except in your company, and therefore I have not; and therefore I proposed this little appointment for our fetching away your things. There goes the hour by the clock! Tony”—Mr. Guppy becomes mysteriously and tenderly eloquent—“it is necessary that I should impress upon your mind once more that circumstances over which I have no control have made a melancholy alteration in my most cherished plans and in that unrequited image which I formerly mentioned to you as a friend. That image is shattered, and that idol is laid low. My only wish now in connexion with the objects which I had an idea of carrying out in the court with your aid as a friend is to let ’em alone and bury ’em in oblivion. Do you think it possible, do you think it at all likely (I put it to you, Tony, as a friend), from your knowledge of that capricious and deep old character who fell a prey to the—spontaneous element, do you, Tony, think it at all likely that on second thoughts he put those letters away anywhere, after you saw him alive, and that they were not destroyed that night?”\n\nMr. Weevle reflects for some time. Shakes his head. Decidedly thinks not.\n\n“Tony,” says Mr. Guppy as they walk towards the court, “once again understand me, as a friend. Without entering into further explanations, I may repeat that the idol is down. I have no purpose to serve now but burial in oblivion. To that I have pledged myself. I owe it to myself, and I owe it to the shattered image, as also to the circumstances over which I have no control. If you was to express to me by a gesture, by a wink, that you saw lying anywhere in your late lodgings any papers that so much as looked like the papers in question, I would pitch them into the fire, sir, on my own responsibility.”\n\nMr. Weevle nods. Mr. Guppy, much elevated in his own opinion by having delivered these observations, with an air in part forensic and in part romantic—this gentleman having a passion for conducting anything in the form of an examination, or delivering anything in the form of a summing up or a speech—accompanies his friend with dignity to the court.\n\nNever since it has been a court has it had such a Fortunatus’ purse of gossip as in the proceedings at the rag and bottle shop. Regularly, every morning at eight, is the elder Mr. Smallweed brought down to the corner and carried in, accompanied by Mrs. Smallweed, Judy, and Bart; and regularly, all day, do they all remain there until nine at night, solaced by gipsy dinners, not abundant in quantity, from the cook’s shop, rummaging and searching, digging, delving, and diving among the treasures of the late lamented. What those treasures are they keep so secret that the court is maddened. In its delirium it imagines guineas pouring out of tea-pots, crown-pieces overflowing punch-bowls, old chairs and mattresses stuffed with Bank of England notes. It possesses itself of the sixpenny history (with highly coloured folding frontispiece) of Mr. Daniel Dancer and his sister, and also of Mr. Elwes, of Suffolk, and transfers all the facts from those authentic narratives to Mr. Krook. Twice when the dustman is called in to carry off a cartload of old paper, ashes, and broken bottles, the whole court assembles and pries into the baskets as they come forth. Many times the two gentlemen who write with the ravenous little pens on the tissue-paper are seen prowling in the neighbourhood—shy of each other, their late partnership being dissolved. The Sol skilfully carries a vein of the prevailing interest through the Harmonic nights. Little Swills, in what are professionally known as “patter” allusions to the subject, is received with loud applause; and the same vocalist “gags” in the regular business like a man inspired. Even Miss M. Melvilleson, in the revived Caledonian melody of “We’re a-Nodding,” points the sentiment that “the dogs love broo” (whatever the nature of that refreshment may be) with such archness and such a turn of the head towards next door that she is immediately understood to mean Mr. Smallweed loves to find money, and is nightly honoured with a double encore. For all this, the court discovers nothing; and as Mrs. Piper and Mrs. Perkins now communicate to the late lodger whose appearance is the signal for a general rally, it is in one continual ferment to discover everything, and more.\n\nMr. Weevle and Mr. Guppy, with every eye in the court’s head upon them, knock at the closed door of the late lamented’s house, in a high state of popularity. But being contrary to the court’s expectation admitted, they immediately become unpopular and are considered to mean no good.\n\nThe shutters are more or less closed all over the house, and the ground-floor is sufficiently dark to require candles. Introduced into the back shop by Mr. Smallweed the younger, they, fresh from the sunlight, can at first see nothing save darkness and shadows; but they gradually discern the elder Mr. Smallweed seated in his chair upon the brink of a well or grave of waste-paper, the virtuous Judy groping therein like a female sexton, and Mrs. Smallweed on the level ground in the vicinity snowed up in a heap of paper fragments, print, and manuscript which would appear to be the accumulated compliments that have been sent flying at her in the course of the day. The whole party, Small included, are blackened with dust and dirt and present a fiendish appearance not relieved by the general aspect of the room. There is more litter and lumber in it than of old, and it is dirtier if possible; likewise, it is ghostly with traces of its dead inhabitant and even with his chalked writing on the wall.\n\nOn the entrance of visitors, Mr. Smallweed and Judy simultaneously fold their arms and stop in their researches.\n\n“Aha!” croaks the old gentleman. “How de do, gentlemen, how de do! Come to fetch your property, Mr. Weevle? That’s well, that’s well. Ha! Ha! We should have been forced to sell you up, sir, to pay your warehouse room if you had left it here much longer. You feel quite at home here again, I dare say? Glad to see you, glad to see you!”\n\nMr. Weevle, thanking him, casts an eye about. Mr. Guppy’s eye follows Mr. Weevle’s eye. Mr. Weevle’s eye comes back without any new intelligence in it. Mr. Guppy’s eye comes back and meets Mr. Smallweed’s eye. That engaging old gentleman is still murmuring, like some wound-up instrument running down, “How de do, sir—how de—how—” And then having run down, he lapses into grinning silence, as Mr. Guppy starts at seeing Mr. Tulkinghorn standing in the darkness opposite with his hands behind him.\n\n“Gentleman so kind as to act as my solicitor,” says Grandfather Smallweed. “I am not the sort of client for a gentleman of such note, but he is so good!”\n\nMr. Guppy, slightly nudging his friend to take another look, makes a shuffling bow to Mr. Tulkinghorn, who returns it with an easy nod. Mr. Tulkinghorn is looking on as if he had nothing else to do and were rather amused by the novelty.\n\n“A good deal of property here, sir, I should say,” Mr. Guppy observes to Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“Principally rags and rubbish, my dear friend! Rags and rubbish! Me and Bart and my granddaughter Judy are endeavouring to make out an inventory of what’s worth anything to sell. But we haven’t come to much as yet; we—haven’t—come—to—hah!”\n\nMr. Smallweed has run down again, while Mr. Weevle’s eye, attended by Mr. Guppy’s eye, has again gone round the room and come back.\n\n“Well, sir,” says Mr. Weevle. “We won’t intrude any longer if you’ll allow us to go upstairs.”\n\n“Anywhere, my dear sir, anywhere! You’re at home. Make yourself so, pray!”\n\nAs they go upstairs, Mr. Guppy lifts his eyebrows inquiringly and looks at Tony. Tony shakes his head. They find the old room very dull and dismal, with the ashes of the fire that was burning on that memorable night yet in the discoloured grate. They have a great disinclination to touch any object, and carefully blow the dust from it first. Nor are they desirous to prolong their visit, packing the few movables with all possible speed and never speaking above a whisper.\n\n“Look here,” says Tony, recoiling. “Here’s that horrible cat coming in!”\n\nMr. Guppy retreats behind a chair. “Small told me of her. She went leaping and bounding and tearing about that night like a dragon, and got out on the house-top, and roamed about up there for a fortnight, and then came tumbling down the chimney very thin. Did you ever see such a brute? Looks as if she knew all about it, don’t she? Almost looks as if she was Krook. Shoohoo! Get out, you goblin!”\n\nLady Jane, in the doorway, with her tiger snarl from ear to ear and her club of a tail, shows no intention of obeying; but Mr. Tulkinghorn stumbling over her, she spits at his rusty legs, and swearing wrathfully, takes her arched back upstairs. Possibly to roam the house-tops again and return by the chimney.\n\n“Mr. Guppy,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “could I have a word with you?”\n\nMr. Guppy is engaged in collecting the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty from the wall and depositing those works of art in their old ignoble band-box. “Sir,” he returns, reddening, “I wish to act with courtesy towards every member of the profession, and especially, I am sure, towards a member of it so well known as yourself—I will truly add, sir, so distinguished as yourself. Still, Mr. Tulkinghorn, sir, I must stipulate that if you have any word with me, that word is spoken in the presence of my friend.”\n\n“Oh, indeed?” says Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\n“Yes, sir. My reasons are not of a personal nature at all, but they are amply sufficient for myself.”\n\n“No doubt, no doubt.” Mr. Tulkinghorn is as imperturbable as the hearthstone to which he has quietly walked. “The matter is not of that consequence that I need put you to the trouble of making any conditions, Mr. Guppy.” He pauses here to smile, and his smile is as dull and rusty as his pantaloons. “You are to be congratulated, Mr. Guppy; you are a fortunate young man, sir.”\n\n“Pretty well so, Mr. Tulkinghorn; I don’t complain.”\n\n“Complain? High friends, free admission to great houses, and access to elegant ladies! Why, Mr. Guppy, there are people in London who would give their ears to be you.”\n\nMr. Guppy, looking as if he would give his own reddening and still reddening ears to be one of those people at present instead of himself, replies, “Sir, if I attend to my profession and do what is right by Kenge and Carboy, my friends and acquaintances are of no consequence to them nor to any member of the profession, not excepting Mr. Tulkinghorn of the Fields. I am not under any obligation to explain myself further; and with all respect for you, sir, and without offence—I repeat, without offence—”\n\n“Oh, certainly!”\n\n“—I don’t intend to do it.”\n\n“Quite so,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn with a calm nod. “Very good; I see by these portraits that you take a strong interest in the fashionable great, sir?”\n\nHe addresses this to the astounded Tony, who admits the soft impeachment.\n\n“A virtue in which few Englishmen are deficient,” observes Mr. Tulkinghorn. He has been standing on the hearthstone with his back to the smoked chimney-piece, and now turns round with his glasses to his eyes. “Who is this? ‘Lady Dedlock.’ Ha! A very good likeness in its way, but it wants force of character. Good day to you, gentlemen; good day!”\n\nWhen he has walked out, Mr. Guppy, in a great perspiration, nerves himself to the hasty completion of the taking down of the Galaxy Gallery, concluding with Lady Dedlock.\n\n“Tony,” he says hurriedly to his astonished companion, “let us be quick in putting the things together and in getting out of this place. It were in vain longer to conceal from you, Tony, that between myself and one of the members of a swan-like aristocracy whom I now hold in my hand, there has been undivulged communication and association. The time might have been when I might have revealed it to you. It never will be more. It is due alike to the oath I have taken, alike to the shattered idol, and alike to circumstances over which I have no control, that the whole should be buried in oblivion. I charge you as a friend, by the interest you have ever testified in the fashionable intelligence, and by any little advances with which I may have been able to accommodate you, so to bury it without a word of inquiry!”\n\nThis charge Mr. Guppy delivers in a state little short of forensic lunacy, while his friend shows a dazed mind in his whole head of hair and even in his cultivated whiskers.\n\nNational and Domestic\nEngland has been in a dreadful state for some weeks. Lord Coodle would go out, Sir Thomas Doodle wouldn’t come in, and there being nobody in Great Britain (to speak of) except Coodle and Doodle, there has been no government. It is a mercy that the hostile meeting between those two great men, which at one time seemed inevitable, did not come off, because if both pistols had taken effect, and Coodle and Doodle had killed each other, it is to be presumed that England must have waited to be governed until young Coodle and young Doodle, now in frocks and long stockings, were grown up. This stupendous national calamity, however, was averted by Lord Coodle’s making the timely discovery that if in the heat of debate he had said that he scorned and despised the whole ignoble career of Sir Thomas Doodle, he had merely meant to say that party differences should never induce him to withhold from it the tribute of his warmest admiration; while it as opportunely turned out, on the other hand, that Sir Thomas Doodle had in his own bosom expressly booked Lord Coodle to go down to posterity as the mirror of virtue and honour. Still England has been some weeks in the dismal strait of having no pilot (as was well observed by Sir Leicester Dedlock) to weather the storm; and the marvellous part of the matter is that England has not appeared to care very much about it, but has gone on eating and drinking and marrying and giving in marriage as the old world did in the days before the flood. But Coodle knew the danger, and Doodle knew the danger, and all their followers and hangers-on had the clearest possible perception of the danger. At last Sir Thomas Doodle has not only condescended to come in, but has done it handsomely, bringing in with him all his nephews, all his male cousins, and all his brothers-in-law. So there is hope for the old ship yet.\n\nDoodle has found that he must throw himself upon the country, chiefly in the form of sovereigns and beer. In this metamorphosed state he is available in a good many places simultaneously and can throw himself upon a considerable portion of the country at one time. Britannia being much occupied in pocketing Doodle in the form of sovereigns, and swallowing Doodle in the form of beer, and in swearing herself black in the face that she does neither—plainly to the advancement of her glory and morality—the London season comes to a sudden end, through all the Doodleites and Coodleites dispersing to assist Britannia in those religious exercises.\n\nHence Mrs. Rouncewell, housekeeper at Chesney Wold, foresees, though no instructions have yet come down, that the family may shortly be expected, together with a pretty large accession of cousins and others who can in any way assist the great Constitutional work. And hence the stately old dame, taking Time by the forelock, leads him up and down the staircases, and along the galleries and passages, and through the rooms, to witness before he grows any older that everything is ready, that floors are rubbed bright, carpets spread, curtains shaken out, beds puffed and patted, still-room and kitchen cleared for action—all things prepared as beseems the Dedlock dignity.\n\nThis present summer evening, as the sun goes down, the preparations are complete. Dreary and solemn the old house looks, with so many appliances of habitation and with no inhabitants except the pictured forms upon the walls. So did these come and go, a Dedlock in possession might have ruminated passing along; so did they see this gallery hushed and quiet, as I see it now; so think, as I think, of the gap that they would make in this domain when they were gone; so find it, as I find it, difficult to believe that it could be without them; so pass from my world, as I pass from theirs, now closing the reverberating door; so leave no blank to miss them, and so die.\n\nThrough some of the fiery windows beautiful from without, and set, at this sunset hour, not in dull-grey stone but in a glorious house of gold, the light excluded at other windows pours in rich, lavish, overflowing like the summer plenty in the land. Then do the frozen Dedlocks thaw. Strange movements come upon their features as the shadows of leaves play there. A dense justice in a corner is beguiled into a wink. A staring baronet, with a truncheon, gets a dimple in his chin. Down into the bosom of a stony shepherdess there steals a fleck of light and warmth that would have done it good a hundred years ago. One ancestress of Volumnia, in high-heeled shoes, very like her—casting the shadow of that virgin event before her full two centuries—shoots out into a halo and becomes a saint. A maid of honour of the court of Charles the Second, with large round eyes (and other charms to correspond), seems to bathe in glowing water, and it ripples as it glows.\n\nBut the fire of the sun is dying. Even now the floor is dusky, and shadow slowly mounts the walls, bringing the Dedlocks down like age and death. And now, upon my Lady’s picture over the great chimney-piece, a weird shade falls from some old tree, that turns it pale, and flutters it, and looks as if a great arm held a veil or hood, watching an opportunity to draw it over her. Higher and darker rises shadow on the wall—now a red gloom on the ceiling—now the fire is out.\n\nAll that prospect, which from the terrace looked so near, has moved solemnly away and changed—not the first nor the last of beautiful things that look so near and will so change—into a distant phantom. Light mists arise, and the dew falls, and all the sweet scents in the garden are heavy in the air. Now the woods settle into great masses as if they were each one profound tree. And now the moon rises to separate them, and to glimmer here and there in horizontal lines behind their stems, and to make the avenue a pavement of light among high cathedral arches fantastically broken.\n\nNow the moon is high; and the great house, needing habitation more than ever, is like a body without life. Now it is even awful, stealing through it, to think of the live people who have slept in the solitary bedrooms, to say nothing of the dead. Now is the time for shadow, when every corner is a cavern and every downward step a pit, when the stained glass is reflected in pale and faded hues upon the floors, when anything and everything can be made of the heavy staircase beams excepting their own proper shapes, when the armour has dull lights upon it not easily to be distinguished from stealthy movement, and when barred helmets are frightfully suggestive of heads inside. But of all the shadows in Chesney Wold, the shadow in the long drawing-room upon my Lady’s picture is the first to come, the last to be disturbed. At this hour and by this light it changes into threatening hands raised up and menacing the handsome face with every breath that stirs.\n\n“She is not well, ma’am,” says a groom in Mrs. Rouncewell’s audience-chamber.\n\n“My Lady not well! What’s the matter?”\n\n“Why, my Lady has been but poorly, ma’am, since she was last here—I don’t mean with the family, ma’am, but when she was here as a bird of passage like. My Lady has not been out much, for her, and has kept her room a good deal.”\n\n“Chesney Wold, Thomas,” rejoins the housekeeper with proud complacency, “will set my Lady up! There is no finer air and no healthier soil in the world!”\n\nThomas may have his own personal opinions on this subject, probably hints them in his manner of smoothing his sleek head from the nape of his neck to his temples, but he forbears to express them further and retires to the servants’ hall to regale on cold meat-pie and ale.\n\nThis groom is the pilot-fish before the nobler shark. Next evening, down come Sir Leicester and my Lady with their largest retinue, and down come the cousins and others from all the points of the compass. Thenceforth for some weeks backward and forward rush mysterious men with no names, who fly about all those particular parts of the country on which Doodle is at present throwing himself in an auriferous and malty shower, but who are merely persons of a restless disposition and never do anything anywhere.\n\nOn these national occasions Sir Leicester finds the cousins useful. A better man than the Honourable Bob Stables to meet the Hunt at dinner, there could not possibly be. Better got up gentlemen than the other cousins to ride over to polling-booths and hustings here and there, and show themselves on the side of England, it would be hard to find. Volumnia is a little dim, but she is of the true descent; and there are many who appreciate her sprightly conversation, her French conundrums so old as to have become in the cycles of time almost new again, the honour of taking the fair Dedlock in to dinner, or even the privilege of her hand in the dance. On these national occasions dancing may be a patriotic service, and Volumnia is constantly seen hopping about for the good of an ungrateful and unpensioning country.\n\nMy Lady takes no great pains to entertain the numerous guests, and being still unwell, rarely appears until late in the day. But at all the dismal dinners, leaden lunches, basilisk balls, and other melancholy pageants, her mere appearance is a relief. As to Sir Leicester, he conceives it utterly impossible that anything can be wanting, in any direction, by any one who has the good fortune to be received under that roof; and in a state of sublime satisfaction, he moves among the company, a magnificent refrigerator.\n\nDaily the cousins trot through dust and canter over roadside turf, away to hustings and polling-booths (with leather gloves and hunting-whips for the counties and kid gloves and riding-canes for the boroughs), and daily bring back reports on which Sir Leicester holds forth after dinner. Daily the restless men who have no occupation in life present the appearance of being rather busy. Daily Volumnia has a little cousinly talk with Sir Leicester on the state of the nation, from which Sir Leicester is disposed to conclude that Volumnia is a more reflecting woman than he had thought her.\n\n“How are we getting on?” says Miss Volumnia, clasping her hands. “ARE we safe?”\n\nThe mighty business is nearly over by this time, and Doodle will throw himself off the country in a few days more. Sir Leicester has just appeared in the long drawing-room after dinner, a bright particular star surrounded by clouds of cousins.\n\n“Volumnia,” replies Sir Leicester, who has a list in his hand, “we are doing tolerably.”\n\n“Only tolerably!”\n\nAlthough it is summer weather, Sir Leicester always has his own particular fire in the evening. He takes his usual screened seat near it and repeats with much firmness and a little displeasure, as who should say, I am not a common man, and when I say tolerably, it must not be understood as a common expression, “Volumnia, we are doing tolerably.”\n\n“At least there is no opposition to YOU,” Volumnia asserts with confidence.\n\n“No, Volumnia. This distracted country has lost its senses in many respects, I grieve to say, but—”\n\n“It is not so mad as that. I am glad to hear it!”\n\nVolumnia’s finishing the sentence restores her to favour. Sir Leicester, with a gracious inclination of his head, seems to say to himself, “A sensible woman this, on the whole, though occasionally precipitate.”\n\nIn fact, as to this question of opposition, the fair Dedlock’s observation was superfluous, Sir Leicester on these occasions always delivering in his own candidateship, as a kind of handsome wholesale order to be promptly executed. Two other little seats that belong to him he treats as retail orders of less importance, merely sending down the men and signifying to the tradespeople, “You will have the goodness to make these materials into two members of Parliament and to send them home when done.”\n\n“I regret to say, Volumnia, that in many places the people have shown a bad spirit, and that this opposition to the government has been of a most determined and most implacable description.”\n\n“W-r-retches!” says Volumnia.\n\n“Even,” proceeds Sir Leicester, glancing at the circumjacent cousins on sofas and ottomans, “even in many—in fact, in most—of those places in which the government has carried it against a faction—”\n\n(Note, by the way, that the Coodleites are always a faction with the Doodleites, and that the Doodleites occupy exactly the same position towards the Coodleites.)\n\n“—Even in them I am shocked, for the credit of Englishmen, to be constrained to inform you that the party has not triumphed without being put to an enormous expense. Hundreds,” says Sir Leicester, eyeing the cousins with increasing dignity and swelling indignation, “hundreds of thousands of pounds!”\n\nIf Volumnia have a fault, it is the fault of being a trifle too innocent, seeing that the innocence which would go extremely well with a sash and tucker is a little out of keeping with the rouge and pearl necklace. Howbeit, impelled by innocence, she asks, “What for?”\n\n“Volumnia,” remonstrates Sir Leicester with his utmost severity. “Volumnia!”\n\n“No, no, I don’t mean what for,” cries Volumnia with her favourite little scream. “How stupid I am! I mean what a pity!”\n\n“I am glad,” returns Sir Leicester, “that you do mean what a pity.”\n\nVolumnia hastens to express her opinion that the shocking people ought to be tried as traitors and made to support the party.\n\n“I am glad, Volumnia,” repeats Sir Leicester, unmindful of these mollifying sentiments, “that you do mean what a pity. It is disgraceful to the electors. But as you, though inadvertently and without intending so unreasonable a question, asked me ‘what for?’ let me reply to you. For necessary expenses. And I trust to your good sense, Volumnia, not to pursue the subject, here or elsewhere.”\n\nSir Leicester feels it incumbent on him to observe a crushing aspect towards Volumnia because it is whispered abroad that these necessary expenses will, in some two hundred election petitions, be unpleasantly connected with the word bribery, and because some graceless jokers have consequently suggested the omission from the Church service of the ordinary supplication in behalf of the High Court of Parliament and have recommended instead that the prayers of the congregation be requested for six hundred and fifty-eight gentlemen in a very unhealthy state.\n\n“I suppose,” observes Volumnia, having taken a little time to recover her spirits after her late castigation, “I suppose Mr. Tulkinghorn has been worked to death.”\n\n“I don’t know,” says Sir Leicester, opening his eyes, “why Mr. Tulkinghorn should be worked to death. I don’t know what Mr. Tulkinghorn’s engagements may be. He is not a candidate.”\n\nVolumnia had thought he might have been employed. Sir Leicester could desire to know by whom, and what for. Volumnia, abashed again, suggests, by somebody—to advise and make arrangements. Sir Leicester is not aware that any client of Mr. Tulkinghorn has been in need of his assistance.\n\nLady Dedlock, seated at an open window with her arm upon its cushioned ledge and looking out at the evening shadows falling on the park, has seemed to attend since the lawyer’s name was mentioned.\n\nA languid cousin with a moustache in a state of extreme debility now observes from his couch that man told him ya’as’dy that Tulkinghorn had gone down t’ that iron place t’ give legal ’pinion ’bout something, and that contest being over t’ day, ’twould be highly jawlly thing if Tulkinghorn should ’pear with news that Coodle man was floored.\n\nMercury in attendance with coffee informs Sir Leicester, hereupon, that Mr. Tulkinghorn has arrived and is taking dinner. My Lady turns her head inward for the moment, then looks out again as before.\n\nVolumnia is charmed to hear that her delight is come. He is so original, such a stolid creature, such an immense being for knowing all sorts of things and never telling them! Volumnia is persuaded that he must be a Freemason. Is sure he is at the head of a lodge, and wears short aprons, and is made a perfect idol of with candlesticks and trowels. These lively remarks the fair Dedlock delivers in her youthful manner, while making a purse.\n\n“He has not been here once,” she adds, “since I came. I really had some thoughts of breaking my heart for the inconstant creature. I had almost made up my mind that he was dead.”\n\nIt may be the gathering gloom of evening, or it may be the darker gloom within herself, but a shade is on my Lady’s face, as if she thought, “I would he were!”\n\n“Mr. Tulkinghorn,” says Sir Leicester, “is always welcome here and always discreet wheresoever he is. A very valuable person, and deservedly respected.”\n\nThe debilitated cousin supposes he is “’normously rich fler.”\n\n“He has a stake in the country,” says Sir Leicester, “I have no doubt. He is, of course, handsomely paid, and he associates almost on a footing of equality with the highest society.”\n\nEverybody starts. For a gun is fired close by.\n\n“Good gracious, what’s that?” cries Volumnia with her little withered scream.\n\n“A rat,” says my Lady. “And they have shot him.”\n\nEnter Mr. Tulkinghorn, followed by Mercuries with lamps and candles.\n\n“No, no,” says Sir Leicester, “I think not. My Lady, do you object to the twilight?”\n\nOn the contrary, my Lady prefers it.\n\n“Volumnia?”\n\nOh! Nothing is so delicious to Volumnia as to sit and talk in the dark.\n\n“Then take them away,” says Sir Leicester. “Tulkinghorn, I beg your pardon. How do you do?”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn with his usual leisurely ease advances, renders his passing homage to my Lady, shakes Sir Leicester’s hand, and subsides into the chair proper to him when he has anything to communicate, on the opposite side of the Baronet’s little newspaper-table. Sir Leicester is apprehensive that my Lady, not being very well, will take cold at that open window. My Lady is obliged to him, but would rather sit there for the air. Sir Leicester rises, adjusts her scarf about her, and returns to his seat. Mr. Tulkinghorn in the meanwhile takes a pinch of snuff.\n\n“Now,” says Sir Leicester. “How has that contest gone?”\n\n“Oh, hollow from the beginning. Not a chance. They have brought in both their people. You are beaten out of all reason. Three to one.”\n\nIt is a part of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s policy and mastery to have no political opinions; indeed, NO opinions. Therefore he says “you” are beaten, and not “we.”\n\nSir Leicester is majestically wroth. Volumnia never heard of such a thing. ‘The debilitated cousin holds that it’s sort of thing that’s sure tapn slongs votes—giv’n—Mob.\n\n“It’s the place, you know,” Mr. Tulkinghorn goes on to say in the fast-increasing darkness when there is silence again, “where they wanted to put up Mrs. Rouncewell’s son.”\n\n“A proposal which, as you correctly informed me at the time, he had the becoming taste and perception,” observes Sir Leicester, “to decline. I cannot say that I by any means approve of the sentiments expressed by Mr. Rouncewell when he was here for some half-hour in this room, but there was a sense of propriety in his decision which I am glad to acknowledge.”\n\n“Ha!” says Mr. Tulkinghorn. “It did not prevent him from being very active in this election, though.”\n\nSir Leicester is distinctly heard to gasp before speaking. “Did I understand you? Did you say that Mr. Rouncewell had been very active in this election?”\n\n“Uncommonly active.”\n“Against—”\n\n“Oh, dear yes, against you. He is a very good speaker. Plain and emphatic. He made a damaging effect, and has great influence. In the business part of the proceedings he carried all before him.”\n\nIt is evident to the whole company, though nobody can see him, that Sir Leicester is staring majestically.\n\n“And he was much assisted,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn as a wind-up, “by his son.”\n\n“By his son, sir?” repeats Sir Leicester with awful politeness.\n\n“By his son.”\n\n“The son who wished to marry the young woman in my Lady’s service?”\n\n“That son. He has but one.”\n\n“Then upon my honour,” says Sir Leicester after a terrific pause during which he has been heard to snort and felt to stare, “then upon my honour, upon my life, upon my reputation and principles, the floodgates of society are burst open, and the waters have—a—obliterated the landmarks of the framework of the cohesion by which things are held together!”\n\nGeneral burst of cousinly indignation. Volumnia thinks it is really high time, you know, for somebody in power to step in and do something strong. Debilitated cousin thinks—country’s going—Dayvle—steeple-chase pace.\n\n“I beg,” says Sir Leicester in a breathless condition, “that we may not comment further on this circumstance. Comment is superfluous. My Lady, let me suggest in reference to that young woman—”\n\n“I have no intention,” observes my Lady from her window in a low but decided tone, “of parting with her.”\n\n“That was not my meaning,” returns Sir Leicester. “I am glad to hear you say so. I would suggest that as you think her worthy of your patronage, you should exert your influence to keep her from these dangerous hands. You might show her what violence would be done in such association to her duties and principles, and you might preserve her for a better fate. You might point out to her that she probably would, in good time, find a husband at Chesney Wold by whom she would not be—” Sir Leicester adds, after a moment’s consideration, “dragged from the altars of her forefathers.”\n\nThese remarks he offers with his unvarying politeness and deference when he addresses himself to his wife. She merely moves her head in reply. The moon is rising, and where she sits there is a little stream of cold pale light, in which her head is seen.\n\n“It is worthy of remark,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “however, that these people are, in their way, very proud.”\n\n“Proud?” Sir Leicester doubts his hearing.\n\n“I should not be surprised if they all voluntarily abandoned the girl—yes, lover and all—instead of her abandoning them, supposing she remained at Chesney Wold under such circumstances.”\n\n“Well!” says Sir Leicester tremulously. “Well! You should know, Mr. Tulkinghorn. You have been among them.”\n\n“Really, Sir Leicester,” returns the lawyer, “I state the fact. Why, I could tell you a story—with Lady Dedlock’s permission.”\n\nHer head concedes it, and Volumnia is enchanted. A story! Oh, he is going to tell something at last! A ghost in it, Volumnia hopes?\n\n“No. Real flesh and blood.” Mr. Tulkinghorn stops for an instant and repeats with some little emphasis grafted upon his usual monotony, “Real flesh and blood, Miss Dedlock. Sir Leicester, these particulars have only lately become known to me. They are very brief. They exemplify what I have said. I suppress names for the present. Lady Dedlock will not think me ill-bred, I hope?”\n\nBy the light of the fire, which is low, he can be seen looking towards the moonlight. By the light of the moon Lady Dedlock can be seen, perfectly still.\n\n“A townsman of this Mr. Rouncewell, a man in exactly parallel circumstances as I am told, had the good fortune to have a daughter who attracted the notice of a great lady. I speak of really a great lady, not merely great to him, but married to a gentleman of your condition, Sir Leicester.”\n\nSir Leicester condescendingly says, “Yes, Mr. Tulkinghorn,” implying that then she must have appeared of very considerable moral dimensions indeed in the eyes of an iron-master.\n\n“The lady was wealthy and beautiful, and had a liking for the girl, and treated her with great kindness, and kept her always near her. Now this lady preserved a secret under all her greatness, which she had preserved for many years. In fact, she had in early life been engaged to marry a young rake—he was a captain in the army—nothing connected with whom came to any good. She never did marry him, but she gave birth to a child of which he was the father.”\n\nBy the light of the fire he can be seen looking towards the moonlight. By the moonlight, Lady Dedlock can be seen in profile, perfectly still.\n\n“The captain in the army being dead, she believed herself safe; but a train of circumstances with which I need not trouble you led to discovery. As I received the story, they began in an imprudence on her own part one day when she was taken by surprise, which shows how difficult it is for the firmest of us (she was very firm) to be always guarded. There was great domestic trouble and amazement, you may suppose; I leave you to imagine, Sir Leicester, the husband’s grief. But that is not the present point. When Mr. Rouncewell’s townsman heard of the disclosure, he no more allowed the girl to be patronized and honoured than he would have suffered her to be trodden underfoot before his eyes. Such was his pride, that he indignantly took her away, as if from reproach and disgrace. He had no sense of the honour done him and his daughter by the lady’s condescension; not the least. He resented the girl’s position, as if the lady had been the commonest of commoners. That is the story. I hope Lady Dedlock will excuse its painful nature.”\n\nThere are various opinions on the merits, more or less conflicting with Volumnia’s. That fair young creature cannot believe there ever was any such lady and rejects the whole history on the threshold. The majority incline to the debilitated cousin’s sentiment, which is in few words—“no business—Rouncewell’s fernal townsman.” Sir Leicester generally refers back in his mind to Wat Tyler and arranges a sequence of events on a plan of his own.\n\nThere is not much conversation in all, for late hours have been kept at Chesney Wold since the necessary expenses elsewhere began, and this is the first night in many on which the family have been alone. It is past ten when Sir Leicester begs Mr. Tulkinghorn to ring for candles. Then the stream of moonlight has swelled into a lake, and then Lady Dedlock for the first time moves, and rises, and comes forward to a table for a glass of water. Winking cousins, bat-like in the candle glare, crowd round to give it; Volumnia (always ready for something better if procurable) takes another, a very mild sip of which contents her; Lady Dedlock, graceful, self-possessed, looked after by admiring eyes, passes away slowly down the long perspective by the side of that nymph, not at all improving her as a question of contrast.\nIn Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Room\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn arrives in his turret-room a little breathed by the journey up, though leisurely performed. There is an expression on his face as if he had discharged his mind of some grave matter and were, in his close way, satisfied. To say of a man so severely and strictly self-repressed that he is triumphant would be to do him as great an injustice as to suppose him troubled with love or sentiment or any romantic weakness. He is sedately satisfied. Perhaps there is a rather increased sense of power upon him as he loosely grasps one of his veinous wrists with his other hand and holding it behind his back walks noiselessly up and down.\n\nThere is a capacious writing-table in the room on which is a pretty large accumulation of papers. The green lamp is lighted, his reading-glasses lie upon the desk, the easy-chair is wheeled up to it, and it would seem as though he had intended to bestow an hour or so upon these claims on his attention before going to bed. But he happens not to be in a business mind. After a glance at the documents awaiting his notice—with his head bent low over the table, the old man’s sight for print or writing being defective at night—he opens the French window and steps out upon the leads. There he again walks slowly up and down in the same attitude, subsiding, if a man so cool may have any need to subside, from the story he has related downstairs.\n\nThe time was once when men as knowing as Mr. Tulkinghorn would walk on turret-tops in the starlight and look up into the sky to read their fortunes there. Hosts of stars are visible to-night, though their brilliancy is eclipsed by the splendour of the moon. If he be seeking his own star as he methodically turns and turns upon the leads, it should be but a pale one to be so rustily represented below. If he be tracing out his destiny, that may be written in other characters nearer to his hand.\n\nAs he paces the leads with his eyes most probably as high above his thoughts as they are high above the earth, he is suddenly stopped in passing the window by two eyes that meet his own. The ceiling of his room is rather low; and the upper part of the door, which is opposite the window, is of glass. There is an inner baize door, too, but the night being warm he did not close it when he came upstairs. These eyes that meet his own are looking in through the glass from the corridor outside. He knows them well. The blood has not flushed into his face so suddenly and redly for many a long year as when he recognizes Lady Dedlock.\n\nHe steps into the room, and she comes in too, closing both the doors behind her. There is a wild disturbance—is it fear or anger?—in her eyes. In her carriage and all else she looks as she looked downstairs two hours ago.\n\nIs it fear or is it anger now? He cannot be sure. Both might be as pale, both as intent.\n\n“Lady Dedlock?”\n\nShe does not speak at first, nor even when she has slowly dropped into the easy-chair by the table. They look at each other, like two pictures.\n\n“Why have you told my story to so many persons?”\n\n“Lady Dedlock, it was necessary for me to inform you that I knew it.”\n\n“How long have you known it?”\n\n“I have suspected it a long while—fully known it a little while.”\n\n“Months?”\n“Days.”\n\nHe stands before her with one hand on a chair-back and the other in his old-fashioned waistcoat and shirt-frill, exactly as he has stood before her at any time since her marriage. The same formal politeness, the same composed deference that might as well be defiance; the whole man the same dark, cold object, at the same distance, which nothing has ever diminished.\n\n“Is this true concerning the poor girl?”\n\nHe slightly inclines and advances his head as not quite understanding the question.\n\n“You know what you related. Is it true? Do her friends know my story also? Is it the town-talk yet? Is it chalked upon the walls and cried in the streets?”\n\nSo! Anger, and fear, and shame. All three contending. What power this woman has to keep these raging passions down! Mr. Tulkinghorn’s thoughts take such form as he looks at her, with his ragged grey eyebrows a hair’s breadth more contracted than usual under her gaze.\n\n“No, Lady Dedlock. That was a hypothetical case, arising out of Sir Leicester’s unconsciously carrying the matter with so high a hand. But it would be a real case if they knew—what we know.”\n\n“Then they do not know it yet?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Can I save the poor girl from injury before they know it?”\n\n“Really, Lady Dedlock,” Mr. Tulkinghorn replies, “I cannot give a satisfactory opinion on that point.”\n\nAnd he thinks, with the interest of attentive curiosity, as he watches the struggle in her breast, “The power and force of this woman are astonishing!”\n\n“Sir,” she says, for the moment obliged to set her lips with all the energy she has, that she may speak distinctly, “I will make it plainer. I do not dispute your hypothetical case. I anticipated it, and felt its truth as strongly as you can do, when I saw Mr. Rouncewell here. I knew very well that if he could have had the power of seeing me as I was, he would consider the poor girl tarnished by having for a moment been, although most innocently, the subject of my great and distinguished patronage. But I have an interest in her, or I should rather say—no longer belonging to this place—I had, and if you can find so much consideration for the woman under your foot as to remember that, she will be very sensible of your mercy.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn, profoundly attentive, throws this off with a shrug of self-depreciation and contracts his eyebrows a little more.\n\n“You have prepared me for my exposure, and I thank you for that too. Is there anything that you require of me? Is there any claim that I can release or any charge or trouble that I can spare my husband in obtaining HIS release by certifying to the exactness of your discovery? I will write anything, here and now, that you will dictate. I am ready to do it.”\n\nAnd she would do it, thinks the lawyer, watchful of the firm hand with which she takes the pen!\n\n“I will not trouble you, Lady Dedlock. Pray spare yourself.”\n\n“I have long expected this, as you know. I neither wish to spare myself nor to be spared. You can do nothing worse to me than you have done. Do what remains now.”\n\n“Lady Dedlock, there is nothing to be done. I will take leave to say a few words when you have finished.”\n\nTheir need for watching one another should be over now, but they do it all this time, and the stars watch them both through the opened window. Away in the moonlight lie the woodland fields at rest, and the wide house is as quiet as the narrow one. The narrow one! Where are the digger and the spade, this peaceful night, destined to add the last great secret to the many secrets of the Tulkinghorn existence? Is the man born yet, is the spade wrought yet? Curious questions to consider, more curious perhaps not to consider, under the watching stars upon a summer night.\n\n“Of repentance or remorse or any feeling of mine,” Lady Dedlock presently proceeds, “I say not a word. If I were not dumb, you would be deaf. Let that go by. It is not for your ears.”\n\nHe makes a feint of offering a protest, but she sweeps it away with her disdainful hand.\n\n“Of other and very different things I come to speak to you. My jewels are all in their proper places of keeping. They will be found there. So, my dresses. So, all the valuables I have. Some ready money I had with me, please to say, but no large amount. I did not wear my own dress, in order that I might avoid observation. I went to be henceforward lost. Make this known. I leave no other charge with you.”\n\n“Excuse me, Lady Dedlock,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, quite unmoved. “I am not sure that I understand you. You want—”\n\n“To be lost to all here. I leave Chesney Wold to-night. I go this hour.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn shakes his head. She rises, but he, without moving hand from chair-back or from old-fashioned waistcoat and shirt-frill, shakes his head.\n\n“What? Not go as I have said?”\n\n“No, Lady Dedlock,” he very calmly replies.\n\n“Do you know the relief that my disappearance will be? Have you forgotten the stain and blot upon this place, and where it is, and who it is?”\n\n“No, Lady Dedlock, not by any means.”\n\nWithout deigning to rejoin, she moves to the inner door and has it in her hand when he says to her, without himself stirring hand or foot or raising his voice, “Lady Dedlock, have the goodness to stop and hear me, or before you reach the staircase I shall ring the alarm-bell and rouse the house. And then I must speak out before every guest and servant, every man and woman, in it.”\n\nHe has conquered her. She falters, trembles, and puts her hand confusedly to her head. Slight tokens these in any one else, but when so practised an eye as Mr. Tulkinghorn’s sees indecision for a moment in such a subject, he thoroughly knows its value.\n\nHe promptly says again, “Have the goodness to hear me, Lady Dedlock,” and motions to the chair from which she has risen. She hesitates, but he motions again, and she sits down.\n\n“The relations between us are of an unfortunate description, Lady Dedlock; but as they are not of my making, I will not apologize for them. The position I hold in reference to Sir Leicester is so well known to you that I can hardly imagine but that I must long have appeared in your eyes the natural person to make this discovery.”\n\n“Sir,” she returns without looking up from the ground on which her eyes are now fixed, “I had better have gone. It would have been far better not to have detained me. I have no more to say.”\n\n“Excuse me, Lady Dedlock, if I add a little more to hear.”\n\n“I wish to hear it at the window, then. I can’t breathe where I am.”\n\nHis jealous glance as she walks that way betrays an instant’s misgiving that she may have it in her thoughts to leap over, and dashing against ledge and cornice, strike her life out upon the terrace below. But a moment’s observation of her figure as she stands in the window without any support, looking out at the stars—not up—gloomily out at those stars which are low in the heavens, reassures him. By facing round as she has moved, he stands a little behind her.\n\n“Lady Dedlock, I have not yet been able to come to a decision satisfactory to myself on the course before me. I am not clear what to do or how to act next. I must request you, in the meantime, to keep your secret as you have kept it so long and not to wonder that I keep it too.”\n\nHe pauses, but she makes no reply.\n\n“Pardon me, Lady Dedlock. This is an important subject. You are honouring me with your attention?”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“Thank you. I might have known it from what I have seen of your strength of character. I ought not to have asked the question, but I have the habit of making sure of my ground, step by step, as I go on. The sole consideration in this unhappy case is Sir Leicester.”\n\n“Then why,” she asks in a low voice and without removing her gloomy look from those distant stars, “do you detain me in his house?”\n\n“Because he IS the consideration. Lady Dedlock, I have no occasion to tell you that Sir Leicester is a very proud man, that his reliance upon you is implicit, that the fall of that moon out of the sky would not amaze him more than your fall from your high position as his wife.”\n\nShe breathes quickly and heavily, but she stands as unflinchingly as ever he has seen her in the midst of her grandest company.\n\n“I declare to you, Lady Dedlock, that with anything short of this case that I have, I would as soon have hoped to root up by means of my own strength and my own hands the oldest tree on this estate as to shake your hold upon Sir Leicester and Sir Leicester’s trust and confidence in you. And even now, with this case, I hesitate. Not that he could doubt (that, even with him, is impossible), but that nothing can prepare him for the blow.”\n\n“Not my flight?” she returned. “Think of it again.”\n\n“Your flight, Lady Dedlock, would spread the whole truth, and a hundred times the whole truth, far and wide. It would be impossible to save the family credit for a day. It is not to be thought of.”\n\nThere is a quiet decision in his reply which admits of no remonstrance.\n\n“When I speak of Sir Leicester being the sole consideration, he and the family credit are one. Sir Leicester and the baronetcy, Sir Leicester and Chesney Wold, Sir Leicester and his ancestors and his patrimony”—Mr. Tulkinghorn very dry here—“are, I need not say to you, Lady Dedlock, inseparable.”\n\n“Go on!”\n\n“Therefore,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, pursuing his case in his jog-trot style, “I have much to consider. This is to be hushed up if it can be. How can it be, if Sir Leicester is driven out of his wits or laid upon a death-bed? If I inflicted this shock upon him to-morrow morning, how could the immediate change in him be accounted for? What could have caused it? What could have divided you? Lady Dedlock, the wall-chalking and the street-crying would come on directly, and you are to remember that it would not affect you merely (whom I cannot at all consider in this business) but your husband, Lady Dedlock, your husband.”\n\nHe gets plainer as he gets on, but not an atom more emphatic or animated.\n\n“There is another point of view,” he continues, “in which the case presents itself. Sir Leicester is devoted to you almost to infatuation. He might not be able to overcome that infatuation, even knowing what we know. I am putting an extreme case, but it might be so. If so, it were better that he knew nothing. Better for common sense, better for him, better for me. I must take all this into account, and it combines to render a decision very difficult.”\n\nShe stands looking out at the same stars without a word. They are beginning to pale, and she looks as if their coldness froze her.\n\n“My experience teaches me,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, who has by this time got his hands in his pockets and is going on in his business consideration of the matter like a machine. “My experience teaches me, Lady Dedlock, that most of the people I know would do far better to leave marriage alone. It is at the bottom of three fourths of their troubles. So I thought when Sir Leicester married, and so I always have thought since. No more about that. I must now be guided by circumstances. In the meanwhile I must beg you to keep your own counsel, and I will keep mine.”\n\n“I am to drag my present life on, holding its pains at your pleasure, day by day?” she asks, still looking at the distant sky.\n\n“Yes, I am afraid so, Lady Dedlock.”\n\n“It is necessary, you think, that I should be so tied to the stake?”\n\n“I am sure that what I recommend is necessary.”\n\n“I am to remain on this gaudy platform on which my miserable deception has been so long acted, and it is to fall beneath me when you give the signal?” she said slowly.\n\n“Not without notice, Lady Dedlock. I shall take no step without forewarning you.”\n\nShe asks all her questions as if she were repeating them from memory or calling them over in her sleep.\n\n“We are to meet as usual?”\n\n“Precisely as usual, if you please.”\n\n“And I am to hide my guilt, as I have done so many years?”\n\n“As you have done so many years. I should not have made that reference myself, Lady Dedlock, but I may now remind you that your secret can be no heavier to you than it was, and is no worse and no better than it was. I know it certainly, but I believe we have never wholly trusted each other.”\n\nShe stands absorbed in the same frozen way for some little time before asking, “Is there anything more to be said to-night?”\n\n“Why,” Mr. Tulkinghorn returns methodically as he softly rubs his hands, “I should like to be assured of your acquiescence in my arrangements, Lady Dedlock.”\n\n“You may be assured of it.”\n\n“Good. And I would wish in conclusion to remind you, as a business precaution, in case it should be necessary to recall the fact in any communication with Sir Leicester, that throughout our interview I have expressly stated my sole consideration to be Sir Leicester’s feelings and honour and the family reputation. I should have been happy to have made Lady Dedlock a prominent consideration, too, if the case had admitted of it; but unfortunately it does not.”\n\n“I can attest your fidelity, sir.”\n\nBoth before and after saying it she remains absorbed, but at length moves, and turns, unshaken in her natural and acquired presence, towards the door. Mr. Tulkinghorn opens both the doors exactly as he would have done yesterday, or as he would have done ten years ago, and makes his old-fashioned bow as she passes out. It is not an ordinary look that he receives from the handsome face as it goes into the darkness, and it is not an ordinary movement, though a very slight one, that acknowledges his courtesy. But as he reflects when he is left alone, the woman has been putting no common constraint upon herself.\n\nHe would know it all the better if he saw the woman pacing her own rooms with her hair wildly thrown from her flung-back face, her hands clasped behind her head, her figure twisted as if by pain. He would think so all the more if he saw the woman thus hurrying up and down for hours, without fatigue, without intermission, followed by the faithful step upon the Ghost’s Walk. But he shuts out the now chilled air, draws the window-curtain, goes to bed, and falls asleep. And truly when the stars go out and the wan day peeps into the turret-chamber, finding him at his oldest, he looks as if the digger and the spade were both commissioned and would soon be digging.\n\nThe same wan day peeps in at Sir Leicester pardoning the repentant country in a majestically condescending dream; and at the cousins entering on various public employments, principally receipt of salary; and at the chaste Volumnia, bestowing a dower of fifty thousand pounds upon a hideous old general with a mouth of false teeth like a pianoforte too full of keys, long the admiration of Bath and the terror of every other community. Also into rooms high in the roof, and into offices in court-yards, and over stables, where humbler ambition dreams of bliss, in keepers’ lodges, and in holy matrimony with Will or Sally. Up comes the bright sun, drawing everything up with it—the Wills and Sallys, the latent vapour in the earth, the drooping leaves and flowers, the birds and beasts and creeping things, the gardeners to sweep the dewy turf and unfold emerald velvet where the roller passes, the smoke of the great kitchen fire wreathing itself straight and high into the lightsome air. Lastly, up comes the flag over Mr. Tulkinghorn’s unconscious head cheerfully proclaiming that Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock are in their happy home and that there is hospitality at the place in Lincolnshire.\nIn Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Chambers\n\nFrom the verdant undulations and the spreading oaks of the Dedlock property, Mr. Tulkinghorn transfers himself to the stale heat and dust of London. His manner of coming and going between the two places is one of his impenetrabilities. He walks into Chesney Wold as if it were next door to his chambers and returns to his chambers as if he had never been out of Lincoln’s Inn Fields. He neither changes his dress before the journey nor talks of it afterwards. He melted out of his turret-room this morning, just as now, in the late twilight, he melts into his own square.\n\nLike a dingy London bird among the birds at roost in these pleasant fields, where the sheep are all made into parchment, the goats into wigs, and the pasture into chaff, the lawyer, smoke-dried and faded, dwelling among mankind but not consorting with them, aged without experience of genial youth, and so long used to make his cramped nest in holes and corners of human nature that he has forgotten its broader and better range, comes sauntering home. In the oven made by the hot pavements and hot buildings, he has baked himself dryer than usual; and he has in his thirsty mind his mellowed port-wine half a century old.\n\nThe lamplighter is skipping up and down his ladder on Mr. Tulkinghorn’s side of the Fields when that high-priest of noble mysteries arrives at his own dull court-yard. He ascends the door-steps and is gliding into the dusky hall when he encounters, on the top step, a bowing and propitiatory little man.\n\n“Is that Snagsby?”\n\n“Yes, sir. I hope you are well, sir. I was just giving you up, sir, and going home.”\n\n“Aye? What is it? What do you want with me?”\n\n“Well, sir,” says Mr. Snagsby, holding his hat at the side of his head in his deference towards his best customer, “I was wishful to say a word to you, sir.”\n\n“Can you say it here?”\n\n“Perfectly, sir.”\n\n“Say it then.” The lawyer turns, leans his arms on the iron railing at the top of the steps, and looks at the lamplighter lighting the court-yard.\n\n“It is relating,” says Mr. Snagsby in a mysterious low voice, “it is relating—not to put too fine a point upon it—to the foreigner, sir!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn eyes him with some surprise. “What foreigner?”\n\n“The foreign female, sir. French, if I don’t mistake? I am not acquainted with that language myself, but I should judge from her manners and appearance that she was French; anyways, certainly foreign. Her that was upstairs, sir, when Mr. Bucket and me had the honour of waiting upon you with the sweeping-boy that night.”\n\n“Oh! Yes, yes. Mademoiselle Hortense.”\n\n“Indeed, sir?” Mr. Snagsby coughs his cough of submission behind his hat. “I am not acquainted myself with the names of foreigners in general, but I have no doubt it WOULD be that.” Mr. Snagsby appears to have set out in this reply with some desperate design of repeating the name, but on reflection coughs again to excuse himself.\n\n“And what can you have to say, Snagsby,” demands Mr. Tulkinghorn, “about her?”\n\n“Well, sir,” returns the stationer, shading his communication with his hat, “it falls a little hard upon me. My domestic happiness is very great—at least, it’s as great as can be expected, I’m sure—but my little woman is rather given to jealousy. Not to put too fine a point upon it, she is very much given to jealousy. And you see, a foreign female of that genteel appearance coming into the shop, and hovering—I should be the last to make use of a strong expression if I could avoid it, but hovering, sir—in the court—you know it is—now ain’t it? I only put it to yourself, sir.”\n\nMr. Snagsby, having said this in a very plaintive manner, throws in a cough of general application to fill up all the blanks.\n\n“Why, what do you mean?” asks Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\n“Just so, sir,” returns Mr. Snagsby; “I was sure you would feel it yourself and would excuse the reasonableness of MY feelings when coupled with the known excitableness of my little woman. You see, the foreign female—which you mentioned her name just now, with quite a native sound I am sure—caught up the word Snagsby that night, being uncommon quick, and made inquiry, and got the direction and come at dinner-time. Now Guster, our young woman, is timid and has fits, and she, taking fright at the foreigner’s looks—which are fierce—and at a grinding manner that she has of speaking—which is calculated to alarm a weak mind—gave way to it, instead of bearing up against it, and tumbled down the kitchen stairs out of one into another, such fits as I do sometimes think are never gone into, or come out of, in any house but ours. Consequently there was by good fortune ample occupation for my little woman, and only me to answer the shop. When she DID say that Mr. Tulkinghorn, being always denied to her by his employer (which I had no doubt at the time was a foreign mode of viewing a clerk), she would do herself the pleasure of continually calling at my place until she was let in here. Since then she has been, as I began by saying, hovering, hovering, sir”—Mr. Snagsby repeats the word with pathetic emphasis—“in the court. The effects of which movement it is impossible to calculate. I shouldn’t wonder if it might have already given rise to the painfullest mistakes even in the neighbours’ minds, not mentioning (if such a thing was possible) my little woman. Whereas, goodness knows,” says Mr. Snagsby, shaking his head, “I never had an idea of a foreign female, except as being formerly connected with a bunch of brooms and a baby, or at the present time with a tambourine and earrings. I never had, I do assure you, sir!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn had listened gravely to this complaint and inquires when the stationer has finished, “And that’s all, is it, Snagsby?”\n\n“Why yes, sir, that’s all,” says Mr. Snagsby, ending with a cough that plainly adds, “and it’s enough too—for me.”\n\n“I don’t know what Mademoiselle Hortense may want or mean, unless she is mad,” says the lawyer.\n\n“Even if she was, you know, sir,” Mr. Snagsby pleads, “it wouldn’t be a consolation to have some weapon or another in the form of a foreign dagger planted in the family.”\n\n“No,” says the other. “Well, well! This shall be stopped. I am sorry you have been inconvenienced. If she comes again, send her here.”\n\nMr. Snagsby, with much bowing and short apologetic coughing, takes his leave, lightened in heart. Mr. Tulkinghorn goes upstairs, saying to himself, “These women were created to give trouble the whole earth over. The mistress not being enough to deal with, here’s the maid now! But I will be short with THIS jade at least!”\n\nSo saying, he unlocks his door, gropes his way into his murky rooms, lights his candles, and looks about him. It is too dark to see much of the Allegory overhead there, but that importunate Roman, who is for ever toppling out of the clouds and pointing, is at his old work pretty distinctly. Not honouring him with much attention, Mr. Tulkinghorn takes a small key from his pocket, unlocks a drawer in which there is another key, which unlocks a chest in which there is another, and so comes to the cellar-key, with which he prepares to descend to the regions of old wine. He is going towards the door with a candle in his hand when a knock comes.\n\n“Who’s this? Aye, aye, mistress, it’s you, is it? You appear at a good time. I have just been hearing of you. Now! What do you want?”\n\nHe stands the candle on the chimney-piece in the clerk’s hall and taps his dry cheek with the key as he addresses these words of welcome to Mademoiselle Hortense. That feline personage, with her lips tightly shut and her eyes looking out at him sideways, softly closes the door before replying.\n\n“I have had great deal of trouble to find you, sir.”\n\n“HAVE you!”\n\n“I have been here very often, sir. It has always been said to me, he is not at home, he is engage, he is this and that, he is not for you.”\n\n“Quite right, and quite true.”\n\n“Not true. Lies!”\n\nAt times there is a suddenness in the manner of Mademoiselle Hortense so like a bodily spring upon the subject of it that such subject involuntarily starts and falls back. It is Mr. Tulkinghorn’s case at present, though Mademoiselle Hortense, with her eyes almost shut up (but still looking out sideways), is only smiling contemptuously and shaking her head.\n\n“Now, mistress,” says the lawyer, tapping the key hastily upon the chimney-piece. “If you have anything to say, say it, say it.”\n\n“Sir, you have not use me well. You have been mean and shabby.”\n\n“Mean and shabby, eh?” returns the lawyer, rubbing his nose with the key.\n\n“Yes. What is it that I tell you? You know you have. You have attrapped me—catched me—to give you information; you have asked me to show you the dress of mine my Lady must have wore that night, you have prayed me to come in it here to meet that boy. Say! Is it not?” Mademoiselle Hortense makes another spring.\n\n“You are a vixen, a vixen!” Mr. Tulkinghorn seems to meditate as he looks distrustfully at her, then he replies, “Well, wench, well. I paid you.”\n\n“You paid me!” she repeats with fierce disdain. “Two sovereign! I have not change them, I re-fuse them, I des-pise them, I throw them from me!” Which she literally does, taking them out of her bosom as she speaks and flinging them with such violence on the floor that they jerk up again into the light before they roll away into corners and slowly settle down there after spinning vehemently.\n\n“Now!” says Mademoiselle Hortense, darkening her large eyes again. “You have paid me? Eh, my God, oh yes!”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn rubs his head with the key while she entertains herself with a sarcastic laugh.\n\n“You must be rich, my fair friend,” he composedly observes, “to throw money about in that way!”\n\n“I AM rich,” she returns. “I am very rich in hate. I hate my Lady, of all my heart. You know that.”\n\n“Know it? How should I know it?”\n\n“Because you have known it perfectly before you prayed me to give you that information. Because you have known perfectly that I was en-r-r-r-raged!” It appears impossible for mademoiselle to roll the letter “r” sufficiently in this word, notwithstanding that she assists her energetic delivery by clenching both her hands and setting all her teeth.\n\n“Oh! I knew that, did I?” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, examining the wards of the key.\n\n“Yes, without doubt. I am not blind. You have made sure of me because you knew that. You had reason! I det-est her.” Mademoiselle Hortense folds her arms and throws this last remark at him over one of her shoulders.\n\n“Having said this, have you anything else to say, mademoiselle?”\n\n“I am not yet placed. Place me well. Find me a good condition! If you cannot, or do not choose to do that, employ me to pursue her, to chase her, to disgrace and to dishonour her. I will help you well, and with a good will. It is what YOU do. Do I not know that?”\n\n“You appear to know a good deal,” Mr. Tulkinghorn retorts.\n\n“Do I not? Is it that I am so weak as to believe, like a child, that I come here in that dress to rec-eive that boy only to decide a little bet, a wager? Eh, my God, oh yes!” In this reply, down to the word “wager” inclusive, mademoiselle has been ironically polite and tender, then as suddenly dashed into the bitterest and most defiant scorn, with her black eyes in one and the same moment very nearly shut and staringly wide open.\n\n“Now, let us see,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, tapping his chin with the key and looking imperturbably at her, “how this matter stands.”\n\n“Ah! Let us see,” mademoiselle assents, with many angry and tight nods of her head.\n\n“You come here to make a remarkably modest demand, which you have just stated, and it not being conceded, you will come again.”\n\n“And again,” says mademoiselle with more tight and angry nods. “And yet again. And yet again. And many times again. In effect, for ever!”\n\n“And not only here, but you will go to Mr. Snagsby’s too, perhaps? That visit not succeeding either, you will go again perhaps?”\n\n“And again,” repeats mademoiselle, cataleptic with determination. “And yet again. And yet again. And many times again. In effect, for ever!”\n\n“Very well. Now, Mademoiselle Hortense, let me recommend you to take the candle and pick up that money of yours. I think you will find it behind the clerk’s partition in the corner yonder.”\n\nShe merely throws a laugh over her shoulder and stands her ground with folded arms.\n\n“You will not, eh?”\n\n“No, I will not!”\n\n“So much the poorer you; so much the richer I! Look, mistress, this is the key of my wine-cellar. It is a large key, but the keys of prisons are larger. In this city there are houses of correction (where the treadmills are, for women), the gates of which are very strong and heavy, and no doubt the keys too. I am afraid a lady of your spirit and activity would find it an inconvenience to have one of those keys turned upon her for any length of time. What do you think?”\n\n“I think,” mademoiselle replies without any action and in a clear, obliging voice, “that you are a miserable wretch.”\n\n“Probably,” returns Mr. Tulkinghorn, quietly blowing his nose. “But I don’t ask what you think of myself; I ask what you think of the prison.”\n\n“Nothing. What does it matter to me?”\n\n“Why, it matters this much, mistress,” says the lawyer, deliberately putting away his handkerchief and adjusting his frill; “the law is so despotic here that it interferes to prevent any of our good English citizens from being troubled, even by a lady’s visits against his desire. And on his complaining that he is so troubled, it takes hold of the troublesome lady and shuts her up in prison under hard discipline. Turns the key upon her, mistress.” Illustrating with the cellar-key.\n\n“Truly?” returns mademoiselle in the same pleasant voice. “That is droll! But—my faith!—still what does it matter to me?”\n\n“My fair friend,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “make another visit here, or at Mr. Snagsby’s, and you shall learn.”\n\n“In that case you will send me to the prison, perhaps?”\n\n“Perhaps.”\n\nIt would be contradictory for one in mademoiselle’s state of agreeable jocularity to foam at the mouth, otherwise a tigerish expansion thereabouts might look as if a very little more would make her do it.\n\n“In a word, mistress,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, “I am sorry to be unpolite, but if you ever present yourself uninvited here—or there—again, I will give you over to the police. Their gallantry is great, but they carry troublesome people through the streets in an ignominious manner, strapped down on a board, my good wench.”\n\n“I will prove you,” whispers mademoiselle, stretching out her hand, “I will try if you dare to do it!”\n\n“And if,” pursues the lawyer without minding her, “I place you in that good condition of being locked up in jail, it will be some time before you find yourself at liberty again.”\n\n“I will prove you,” repeats mademoiselle in her former whisper.\n\n“And now,” proceeds the lawyer, still without minding her, “you had better go. Think twice before you come here again.”\n\n“Think you,” she answers, “twice two hundred times!”\n\n“You were dismissed by your lady, you know,” Mr. Tulkinghorn observes, following her out upon the staircase, “as the most implacable and unmanageable of women. Now turn over a new leaf and take warning by what I say to you. For what I say, I mean; and what I threaten, I will do, mistress.”\n\nShe goes down without answering or looking behind her. When she is gone, he goes down too, and returning with his cobweb-covered bottle, devotes himself to a leisurely enjoyment of its contents, now and then, as he throws his head back in his chair, catching sight of the pertinacious Roman pointing from the ceiling.\n\nEsther’s Narrative\nIt matters little now how much I thought of my living mother who had told me evermore to consider her dead. I could not venture to approach her or to communicate with her in writing, for my sense of the peril in which her life was passed was only to be equalled by my fears of increasing it. Knowing that my mere existence as a living creature was an unforeseen danger in her way, I could not always conquer that terror of myself which had seized me when I first knew the secret. At no time did I dare to utter her name. I felt as if I did not even dare to hear it. If the conversation anywhere, when I was present, took that direction, as it sometimes naturally did, I tried not to hear: I mentally counted, repeated something that I knew, or went out of the room. I am conscious now that I often did these things when there can have been no danger of her being spoken of, but I did them in the dread I had of hearing anything that might lead to her betrayal, and to her betrayal through me.\n\nIt matters little now how often I recalled the tones of my mother’s voice, wondered whether I should ever hear it again as I so longed to do, and thought how strange and desolate it was that it should be so new to me. It matters little that I watched for every public mention of my mother’s name; that I passed and repassed the door of her house in town, loving it, but afraid to look at it; that I once sat in the theatre when my mother was there and saw me, and when we were so wide asunder before the great company of all degrees that any link or confidence between us seemed a dream. It is all, all over. My lot has been so blest that I can relate little of myself which is not a story of goodness and generosity in others. I may well pass that little and go on.\n\nWhen we were settled at home again, Ada and I had many conversations with my guardian of which Richard was the theme. My dear girl was deeply grieved that he should do their kind cousin so much wrong, but she was so faithful to Richard that she could not bear to blame him even for that. My guardian was assured of it, and never coupled his name with a word of reproof. “Rick is mistaken, my dear,” he would say to her. “Well, well! We have all been mistaken over and over again. We must trust to you and time to set him right.”\n\nWe knew afterwards what we suspected then, that he did not trust to time until he had often tried to open Richard’s eyes. That he had written to him, gone to him, talked with him, tried every gentle and persuasive art his kindness could devise. Our poor devoted Richard was deaf and blind to all. If he were wrong, he would make amends when the Chancery suit was over. If he were groping in the dark, he could not do better than do his utmost to clear away those clouds in which so much was confused and obscured. Suspicion and misunderstanding were the fault of the suit? Then let him work the suit out and come through it to his right mind. This was his unvarying reply. Jarndyce and Jarndyce had obtained such possession of his whole nature that it was impossible to place any consideration before him which he did not, with a distorted kind of reason, make a new argument in favour of his doing what he did. “So that it is even more mischievous,” said my guardian once to me, “to remonstrate with the poor dear fellow than to leave him alone.”\n\nI took one of these opportunities of mentioning my doubts of Mr. Skimpole as a good adviser for Richard.\n\n“Adviser!” returned my guardian, laughing, “My dear, who would advise with Skimpole?”\n\n“Encourager would perhaps have been a better word,” said I.\n\n“Encourager!” returned my guardian again. “Who could be encouraged by Skimpole?”\n\n“Not Richard?” I asked.\n\n“No,” he replied. “Such an unworldly, uncalculating, gossamer creature is a relief to him and an amusement. But as to advising or encouraging or occupying a serious station towards anybody or anything, it is simply not to be thought of in such a child as Skimpole.”\n\n“Pray, cousin John,” said Ada, who had just joined us and now looked over my shoulder, “what made him such a child?”\n\n“What made him such a child?” inquired my guardian, rubbing his head, a little at a loss.\n\n“Yes, cousin John.”\n\n“Why,” he slowly replied, roughening his head more and more, “he is all sentiment, and—and susceptibility, and—and sensibility, and—and imagination. And these qualities are not regulated in him, somehow. I suppose the people who admired him for them in his youth attached too much importance to them and too little to any training that would have balanced and adjusted them, and so he became what he is. Hey?” said my guardian, stopping short and looking at us hopefully. “What do you think, you two?”\n\nAda, glancing at me, said she thought it was a pity he should be an expense to Richard.\n\n“So it is, so it is,” returned my guardian hurriedly. “That must not be. We must arrange that. I must prevent it. That will never do.”\n\nAnd I said I thought it was to be regretted that he had ever introduced Richard to Mr. Vholes for a present of five pounds.\n\n“Did he?” said my guardian with a passing shade of vexation on his face. “But there you have the man. There you have the man! There is nothing mercenary in that with him. He has no idea of the value of money. He introduces Rick, and then he is good friends with Mr. Vholes and borrows five pounds of him. He means nothing by it and thinks nothing of it. He told you himself, I’ll be bound, my dear?”\n\n“Oh, yes!” said I.\n\n“Exactly!” cried my guardian, quite triumphant. “There you have the man! If he had meant any harm by it or was conscious of any harm in it, he wouldn’t tell it. He tells it as he does it in mere simplicity. But you shall see him in his own home, and then you’ll understand him better. We must pay a visit to Harold Skimpole and caution him on these points. Lord bless you, my dears, an infant, an infant!”\n\nIn pursuance of this plan, we went into London on an early day and presented ourselves at Mr. Skimpole’s door.\n\nHe lived in a place called the Polygon, in Somers Town, where there were at that time a number of poor Spanish refugees walking about in cloaks, smoking little paper cigars. Whether he was a better tenant than one might have supposed, in consequence of his friend Somebody always paying his rent at last, or whether his inaptitude for business rendered it particularly difficult to turn him out, I don’t know; but he had occupied the same house some years. It was in a state of dilapidation quite equal to our expectation. Two or three of the area railings were gone, the water-butt was broken, the knocker was loose, the bell-handle had been pulled off a long time to judge from the rusty state of the wire, and dirty footprints on the steps were the only signs of its being inhabited.\n\nA slatternly full-blown girl who seemed to be bursting out at the rents in her gown and the cracks in her shoes like an over-ripe berry answered our knock by opening the door a very little way and stopping up the gap with her figure. As she knew Mr. Jarndyce (indeed Ada and I both thought that she evidently associated him with the receipt of her wages), she immediately relented and allowed us to pass in. The lock of the door being in a disabled condition, she then applied herself to securing it with the chain, which was not in good action either, and said would we go upstairs?\n\nWe went upstairs to the first floor, still seeing no other furniture than the dirty footprints. Mr. Jarndyce without further ceremony entered a room there, and we followed. It was dingy enough and not at all clean, but furnished with an odd kind of shabby luxury, with a large footstool, a sofa, and plenty of cushions, an easy-chair, and plenty of pillows, a piano, books, drawing materials, music, newspapers, and a few sketches and pictures. A broken pane of glass in one of the dirty windows was papered and wafered over, but there was a little plate of hothouse nectarines on the table, and there was another of grapes, and another of sponge-cakes, and there was a bottle of light wine. Mr. Skimpole himself reclined upon the sofa in a dressing-gown, drinking some fragrant coffee from an old china cup—it was then about mid-day—and looking at a collection of wallflowers in the balcony.\n\nHe was not in the least disconcerted by our appearance, but rose and received us in his usual airy manner.\n\n“Here I am, you see!” he said when we were seated, not without some little difficulty, the greater part of the chairs being broken. “Here I am! This is my frugal breakfast. Some men want legs of beef and mutton for breakfast; I don’t. Give me my peach, my cup of coffee, and my claret; I am content. I don’t want them for themselves, but they remind me of the sun. There’s nothing solar about legs of beef and mutton. Mere animal satisfaction!”\n\n“This is our friend’s consulting-room (or would be, if he ever prescribed), his sanctum, his studio,” said my guardian to us.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Skimpole, turning his bright face about, “this is the bird’s cage. This is where the bird lives and sings. They pluck his feathers now and then and clip his wings, but he sings, he sings!”\n\nHe handed us the grapes, repeating in his radiant way, “He sings! Not an ambitious note, but still he sings.”\n\n“These are very fine,” said my guardian. “A present?”\n\n“No,” he answered. “No! Some amiable gardener sells them. His man wanted to know, when he brought them last evening, whether he should wait for the money. ‘Really, my friend,’ I said, ‘I think not—if your time is of any value to you.’ I suppose it was, for he went away.”\n\nMy guardian looked at us with a smile, as though he asked us, “Is it possible to be worldly with this baby?”\n\n“This is a day,” said Mr. Skimpole, gaily taking a little claret in a tumbler, “that will ever be remembered here. We shall call it Saint Clare and Saint Summerson day. You must see my daughters. I have a blue-eyed daughter who is my Beauty daughter, I have a Sentiment daughter, and I have a Comedy daughter. You must see them all. They’ll be enchanted.”\n\nHe was going to summon them when my guardian interposed and asked him to pause a moment, as he wished to say a word to him first. “My dear Jarndyce,” he cheerfully replied, going back to his sofa, “as many moments as you please. Time is no object here. We never know what o’clock it is, and we never care. Not the way to get on in life, you’ll tell me? Certainly. But we DON’T get on in life. We don’t pretend to do it.”\n\nMy guardian looked at us again, plainly saying, “You hear him?”\n\n“Now, Harold,” he began, “the word I have to say relates to Rick.”\n\n“The dearest friend I have!” returned Mr. Skimpole cordially. “I suppose he ought not to be my dearest friend, as he is not on terms with you. But he is, I can’t help it; he is full of youthful poetry, and I love him. If you don’t like it, I can’t help it. I love him.”\n\nThe engaging frankness with which he made this declaration really had a disinterested appearance and captivated my guardian, if not, for the moment, Ada too.\n\n“You are welcome to love him as much as you like,” returned Mr. Jarndyce, “but we must save his pocket, Harold.”\n\n“Oh!” said Mr. Skimpole. “His pocket? Now you are coming to what I don’t understand.” Taking a little more claret and dipping one of the cakes in it, he shook his head and smiled at Ada and me with an ingenuous foreboding that he never could be made to understand.\n\n“If you go with him here or there,” said my guardian plainly, “you must not let him pay for both.”\n\n“My dear Jarndyce,” returned Mr. Skimpole, his genial face irradiated by the comicality of this idea, “what am I to do? If he takes me anywhere, I must go. And how can I pay? I never have any money. If I had any money, I don’t know anything about it. Suppose I say to a man, how much? Suppose the man says to me seven and sixpence? I know nothing about seven and sixpence. It is impossible for me to pursue the subject with any consideration for the man. I don’t go about asking busy people what seven and sixpence is in Moorish—which I don’t understand. Why should I go about asking them what seven and sixpence is in Money—which I don’t understand?”\n\n“Well,” said my guardian, by no means displeased with this artless reply, “if you come to any kind of journeying with Rick, you must borrow the money of me (never breathing the least allusion to that circumstance), and leave the calculation to him.”\n\n“My dear Jarndyce,” returned Mr. Skimpole, “I will do anything to give you pleasure, but it seems an idle form—a superstition. Besides, I give you my word, Miss Clare and my dear Miss Summerson, I thought Mr. Carstone was immensely rich. I thought he had only to make over something, or to sign a bond, or a draft, or a cheque, or a bill, or to put something on a file somewhere, to bring down a shower of money.”\n\n“Indeed it is not so, sir,” said Ada. “He is poor.”\n\n“No, really?” returned Mr. Skimpole with his bright smile. “You surprise me.”\n\n“And not being the richer for trusting in a rotten reed,” said my guardian, laying his hand emphatically on the sleeve of Mr. Skimpole’s dressing-gown, “be you very careful not to encourage him in that reliance, Harold.”\n\n“My dear good friend,” returned Mr. Skimpole, “and my dear Miss Simmerson, and my dear Miss Clare, how can I do that? It’s business, and I don’t know business. It is he who encourages me. He emerges from great feats of business, presents the brightest prospects before me as their result, and calls upon me to admire them. I do admire them—as bright prospects. But I know no more about them, and I tell him so.”\n\nThe helpless kind of candour with which he presented this before us, the light-hearted manner in which he was amused by his innocence, the fantastic way in which he took himself under his own protection and argued about that curious person, combined with the delightful ease of everything he said exactly to make out my guardian’s case. The more I saw of him, the more unlikely it seemed to me, when he was present, that he could design, conceal, or influence anything; and yet the less likely that appeared when he was not present, and the less agreeable it was to think of his having anything to do with any one for whom I cared.\n\nHearing that his examination (as he called it) was now over, Mr. Skimpole left the room with a radiant face to fetch his daughters (his sons had run away at various times), leaving my guardian quite delighted by the manner in which he had vindicated his childish character. He soon came back, bringing with him the three young ladies and Mrs. Skimpole, who had once been a beauty but was now a delicate high-nosed invalid suffering under a complication of disorders.\n\n“This,” said Mr. Skimpole, “is my Beauty daughter, Arethusa—plays and sings odds and ends like her father. This is my Sentiment daughter, Laura—plays a little but don’t sing. This is my Comedy daughter, Kitty—sings a little but don’t play. We all draw a little and compose a little, and none of us have any idea of time or money.”\n\nMrs. Skimpole sighed, I thought, as if she would have been glad to strike out this item in the family attainments. I also thought that she rather impressed her sigh upon my guardian and that she took every opportunity of throwing in another.\n\n“It is pleasant,” said Mr. Skimpole, turning his sprightly eyes from one to the other of us, “and it is whimsically interesting to trace peculiarities in families. In this family we are all children, and I am the youngest.”\n\nThe daughters, who appeared to be very fond of him, were amused by this droll fact, particularly the Comedy daughter.\n\n“My dears, it is true,” said Mr. Skimpole, “is it not? So it is, and so it must be, because like the dogs in the hymn, ‘it is our nature to.’ Now, here is Miss Summerson with a fine administrative capacity and a knowledge of details perfectly surprising. It will sound very strange in Miss Summerson’s ears, I dare say, that we know nothing about chops in this house. But we don’t, not the least. We can’t cook anything whatever. A needle and thread we don’t know how to use. We admire the people who possess the practical wisdom we want, but we don’t quarrel with them. Then why should they quarrel with us? Live and let live, we say to them. Live upon your practical wisdom, and let us live upon you!”\n\nHe laughed, but as usual seemed quite candid and really to mean what he said.\n\n“We have sympathy, my roses,” said Mr. Skimpole, “sympathy for everything. Have we not?”\n\n“Oh, yes, papa!” cried the three daughters.\n\n“In fact, that is our family department,” said Mr. Skimpole, “in this hurly-burly of life. We are capable of looking on and of being interested, and we DO look on, and we ARE interested. What more can we do? Here is my Beauty daughter, married these three years. Now I dare say her marrying another child, and having two more, was all wrong in point of political economy, but it was very agreeable. We had our little festivities on those occasions and exchanged social ideas. She brought her young husband home one day, and they and their young fledglings have their nest upstairs. I dare say at some time or other Sentiment and Comedy will bring THEIR husbands home and have THEIR nests upstairs too. So we get on, we don’t know how, but somehow.”\n\nShe looked very young indeed to be the mother of two children, and I could not help pitying both her and them. It was evident that the three daughters had grown up as they could and had had just as little haphazard instruction as qualified them to be their father’s playthings in his idlest hours. His pictorial tastes were consulted, I observed, in their respective styles of wearing their hair, the Beauty daughter being in the classic manner, the Sentiment daughter luxuriant and flowing, and the Comedy daughter in the arch style, with a good deal of sprightly forehead, and vivacious little curls dotted about the corners of her eyes. They were dressed to correspond, though in a most untidy and negligent way.\n\nAda and I conversed with these young ladies and found them wonderfully like their father. In the meanwhile Mr. Jarndyce (who had been rubbing his head to a great extent, and hinted at a change in the wind) talked with Mrs. Skimpole in a corner, where we could not help hearing the chink of money. Mr. Skimpole had previously volunteered to go home with us and had withdrawn to dress himself for the purpose.\n\n“My roses,” he said when he came back, “take care of mama. She is poorly to-day. By going home with Mr. Jarndyce for a day or two, I shall hear the larks sing and preserve my amiability. It has been tried, you know, and would be tried again if I remained at home.”\n\n“That bad man!” said the Comedy daughter.\n\n“At the very time when he knew papa was lying ill by his wallflowers, looking at the blue sky,” Laura complained.\n\n“And when the smell of hay was in the air!” said Arethusa.\n\n“It showed a want of poetry in the man,” Mr. Skimpole assented, but with perfect good humour. “It was coarse. There was an absence of the finer touches of humanity in it! My daughters have taken great offence,” he explained to us, “at an honest man—”\n\n“Not honest, papa. Impossible!” they all three protested.\n\n“At a rough kind of fellow—a sort of human hedgehog rolled up,” said Mr. Skimpole, “who is a baker in this neighbourhood and from whom we borrowed a couple of arm-chairs. We wanted a couple of arm-chairs, and we hadn’t got them, and therefore of course we looked to a man who HAD got them, to lend them. Well! This morose person lent them, and we wore them out. When they were worn out, he wanted them back. He had them back. He was contented, you will say. Not at all. He objected to their being worn. I reasoned with him, and pointed out his mistake. I said, ‘Can you, at your time of life, be so headstrong, my friend, as to persist that an arm-chair is a thing to put upon a shelf and look at? That it is an object to contemplate, to survey from a distance, to consider from a point of sight? Don’t you KNOW that these arm-chairs were borrowed to be sat upon?’ He was unreasonable and unpersuadable and used intemperate language. Being as patient as I am at this minute, I addressed another appeal to him. I said, ‘Now, my good man, however our business capacities may vary, we are all children of one great mother, Nature. On this blooming summer morning here you see me’ (I was on the sofa) ‘with flowers before me, fruit upon the table, the cloudless sky above me, the air full of fragrance, contemplating Nature. I entreat you, by our common brotherhood, not to interpose between me and a subject so sublime, the absurd figure of an angry baker!’ But he did,” said Mr. Skimpole, raising his laughing eyes in playful astonishment; “he did interpose that ridiculous figure, and he does, and he will again. And therefore I am very glad to get out of his way and to go home with my friend Jarndyce.”\n\nIt seemed to escape his consideration that Mrs. Skimpole and the daughters remained behind to encounter the baker, but this was so old a story to all of them that it had become a matter of course. He took leave of his family with a tenderness as airy and graceful as any other aspect in which he showed himself and rode away with us in perfect harmony of mind. We had an opportunity of seeing through some open doors, as we went downstairs, that his own apartment was a palace to the rest of the house.\n\nI could have no anticipation, and I had none, that something very startling to me at the moment, and ever memorable to me in what ensued from it, was to happen before this day was out. Our guest was in such spirits on the way home that I could do nothing but listen to him and wonder at him; nor was I alone in this, for Ada yielded to the same fascination. As to my guardian, the wind, which had threatened to become fixed in the east when we left Somers Town, veered completely round before we were a couple of miles from it.\n\nWhether of questionable childishness or not in any other matters, Mr. Skimpole had a child’s enjoyment of change and bright weather. In no way wearied by his sallies on the road, he was in the drawing-room before any of us; and I heard him at the piano while I was yet looking after my housekeeping, singing refrains of barcaroles and drinking songs, Italian and German, by the score.\n\nWe were all assembled shortly before dinner, and he was still at the piano idly picking out in his luxurious way little strains of music, and talking between whiles of finishing some sketches of the ruined old Verulam wall to-morrow, which he had begun a year or two ago and had got tired of, when a card was brought in and my guardian read aloud in a surprised voice, “Sir Leicester Dedlock!”\n\nThe visitor was in the room while it was yet turning round with me and before I had the power to stir. If I had had it, I should have hurried away. I had not even the presence of mind, in my giddiness, to retire to Ada in the window, or to see the window, or to know where it was. I heard my name and found that my guardian was presenting me before I could move to a chair.\n\n“Pray be seated, Sir Leicester.”\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce,” said Sir Leicester in reply as he bowed and seated himself, “I do myself the honour of calling here—”\n\n“You do ME the honour, Sir Leicester.”\n\n“Thank you—of calling here on my road from Lincolnshire to express my regret that any cause of complaint, however strong, that I may have against a gentleman who—who is known to you and has been your host, and to whom therefore I will make no farther reference, should have prevented you, still more ladies under your escort and charge, from seeing whatever little there may be to gratify a polite and refined taste at my house, Chesney Wold.”\n\n“You are exceedingly obliging, Sir Leicester, and on behalf of those ladies (who are present) and for myself, I thank you very much.”\n\n“It is possible, Mr. Jarndyce, that the gentleman to whom, for the reasons I have mentioned, I refrain from making further allusion—it is possible, Mr. Jarndyce, that that gentleman may have done me the honour so far to misapprehend my character as to induce you to believe that you would not have been received by my local establishment in Lincolnshire with that urbanity, that courtesy, which its members are instructed to show to all ladies and gentlemen who present themselves at that house. I merely beg to observe, sir, that the fact is the reverse.”\n\nMy guardian delicately dismissed this remark without making any verbal answer.\n\n“It has given me pain, Mr. Jarndyce,” Sir Leicester weightily proceeded. “I assure you, sir, it has given—me—pain—to learn from the housekeeper at Chesney Wold that a gentleman who was in your company in that part of the county, and who would appear to possess a cultivated taste for the fine arts, was likewise deterred by some such cause from examining the family pictures with that leisure, that attention, that care, which he might have desired to bestow upon them and which some of them might possibly have repaid.” Here he produced a card and read, with much gravity and a little trouble, through his eye-glass, “Mr. Hirrold—Herald—Harold—Skampling—Skumpling—I beg your pardon—Skimpole.”\n\n“This is Mr. Harold Skimpole,” said my guardian, evidently surprised.\n\n“Oh!” exclaimed Sir Leicester, “I am happy to meet Mr. Skimpole and to have the opportunity of tendering my personal regrets. I hope, sir, that when you again find yourself in my part of the county, you will be under no similar sense of restraint.”\n\n“You are very obliging, Sir Leicester Dedlock. So encouraged, I shall certainly give myself the pleasure and advantage of another visit to your beautiful house. The owners of such places as Chesney Wold,” said Mr. Skimpole with his usual happy and easy air, “are public benefactors. They are good enough to maintain a number of delightful objects for the admiration and pleasure of us poor men; and not to reap all the admiration and pleasure that they yield is to be ungrateful to our benefactors.”\n\nSir Leicester seemed to approve of this sentiment highly. “An artist, sir?”\n\n“No,” returned Mr. Skimpole. “A perfectly idle man. A mere amateur.”\n\nSir Leicester seemed to approve of this even more. He hoped he might have the good fortune to be at Chesney Wold when Mr. Skimpole next came down into Lincolnshire. Mr. Skimpole professed himself much flattered and honoured.\n\n“Mr. Skimpole mentioned,” pursued Sir Leicester, addressing himself again to my guardian, “mentioned to the housekeeper, who, as he may have observed, is an old and attached retainer of the family—”\n\n(“That is, when I walked through the house the other day, on the occasion of my going down to visit Miss Summerson and Miss Clare,” Mr. Skimpole airily explained to us.)\n\n“—That the friend with whom he had formerly been staying there was Mr. Jarndyce.” Sir Leicester bowed to the bearer of that name. “And hence I became aware of the circumstance for which I have professed my regret. That this should have occurred to any gentleman, Mr. Jarndyce, but especially a gentleman formerly known to Lady Dedlock, and indeed claiming some distant connexion with her, and for whom (as I learn from my Lady herself) she entertains a high respect, does, I assure you, give—me—pain.”\n\n“Pray say no more about it, Sir Leicester,” returned my guardian. “I am very sensible, as I am sure we all are, of your consideration. Indeed the mistake was mine, and I ought to apologize for it.”\n\nI had not once looked up. I had not seen the visitor and had not even appeared to myself to hear the conversation. It surprises me to find that I can recall it, for it seemed to make no impression on me as it passed. I heard them speaking, but my mind was so confused and my instinctive avoidance of this gentleman made his presence so distressing to me that I thought I understood nothing, through the rushing in my head and the beating of my heart.\n\n“I mentioned the subject to Lady Dedlock,” said Sir Leicester, rising, “and my Lady informed me that she had had the pleasure of exchanging a few words with Mr. Jarndyce and his wards on the occasion of an accidental meeting during their sojourn in the vicinity. Permit me, Mr. Jarndyce, to repeat to yourself, and to these ladies, the assurance I have already tendered to Mr. Skimpole. Circumstances undoubtedly prevent my saying that it would afford me any gratification to hear that Mr. Boythorn had favoured my house with his presence, but those circumstances are confined to that gentleman himself and do not extend beyond him.”\n\n“You know my old opinion of him,” said Mr. Skimpole, lightly appealing to us. “An amiable bull who is determined to make every colour scarlet!”\n\nSir Leicester Dedlock coughed as if he could not possibly hear another word in reference to such an individual and took his leave with great ceremony and politeness. I got to my own room with all possible speed and remained there until I had recovered my self-command. It had been very much disturbed, but I was thankful to find when I went downstairs again that they only rallied me for having been shy and mute before the great Lincolnshire baronet.\n\nBy that time I had made up my mind that the period was come when I must tell my guardian what I knew. The possibility of my being brought into contact with my mother, of my being taken to her house, even of Mr. Skimpole’s, however distantly associated with me, receiving kindnesses and obligations from her husband, was so painful that I felt I could no longer guide myself without his assistance.\n\nWhen we had retired for the night, and Ada and I had had our usual talk in our pretty room, I went out at my door again and sought my guardian among his books. I knew he always read at that hour, and as I drew near I saw the light shining out into the passage from his reading-lamp.\n\n“May I come in, guardian?”\n\n“Surely, little woman. What’s the matter?”\n\n“Nothing is the matter. I thought I would like to take this quiet time of saying a word to you about myself.”\n\nHe put a chair for me, shut his book, and put it by, and turned his kind attentive face towards me. I could not help observing that it wore that curious expression I had observed in it once before—on that night when he had said that he was in no trouble which I could readily understand.\n\n“What concerns you, my dear Esther,” said he, “concerns us all. You cannot be more ready to speak than I am to hear.”\n\n“I know that, guardian. But I have such need of your advice and support. Oh! You don’t know how much need I have to-night.”\n\nHe looked unprepared for my being so earnest, and even a little alarmed.\n\n“Or how anxious I have been to speak to you,” said I, “ever since the visitor was here to-day.”\n\n“The visitor, my dear! Sir Leicester Dedlock?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe folded his arms and sat looking at me with an air of the profoundest astonishment, awaiting what I should say next. I did not know how to prepare him.\n\n“Why, Esther,” said he, breaking into a smile, “our visitor and you are the two last persons on earth I should have thought of connecting together!”\n\n“Oh, yes, guardian, I know it. And I too, but a little while ago.”\n\nThe smile passed from his face, and he became graver than before. He crossed to the door to see that it was shut (but I had seen to that) and resumed his seat before me.\n\n“Guardian,” said I, “do you remember, when we were overtaken by the thunder-storm, Lady Dedlock’s speaking to you of her sister?”\n\n“Of course. Of course I do.”\n\n“And reminding you that she and her sister had differed, had gone their several ways?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“Why did they separate, guardian?”\n\nHis face quite altered as he looked at me. “My child, what questions are these! I never knew. No one but themselves ever did know, I believe. Who could tell what the secrets of those two handsome and proud women were! You have seen Lady Dedlock. If you had ever seen her sister, you would know her to have been as resolute and haughty as she.”\n\n“Oh, guardian, I have seen her many and many a time!”\n\n“Seen her?”\n\nHe paused a little, biting his lip. “Then, Esther, when you spoke to me long ago of Boythorn, and when I told you that he was all but married once, and that the lady did not die, but died to him, and that that time had had its influence on his later life—did you know it all, and know who the lady was?”\n\n“No, guardian,” I returned, fearful of the light that dimly broke upon me. “Nor do I know yet.”\n\n“Lady Dedlock’s sister.”\n\n“And why,” I could scarcely ask him, “why, guardian, pray tell me why were THEY parted?”\n\n“It was her act, and she kept its motives in her inflexible heart. He afterwards did conjecture (but it was mere conjecture) that some injury which her haughty spirit had received in her cause of quarrel with her sister had wounded her beyond all reason, but she wrote him that from the date of that letter she died to him—as in literal truth she did—and that the resolution was exacted from her by her knowledge of his proud temper and his strained sense of honour, which were both her nature too. In consideration for those master points in him, and even in consideration for them in herself, she made the sacrifice, she said, and would live in it and die in it. She did both, I fear; certainly he never saw her, never heard of her from that hour. Nor did any one.”\n\n“Oh, guardian, what have I done!” I cried, giving way to my grief; “what sorrow have I innocently caused!”\n\n“You caused, Esther?”\n\n“Yes, guardian. Innocently, but most surely. That secluded sister is my first remembrance.”\n\n“No, no!” he cried, starting.\n\n“Yes, guardian, yes! And HER sister is my mother!”\n\nI would have told him all my mother’s letter, but he would not hear it then. He spoke so tenderly and wisely to me, and he put so plainly before me all I had myself imperfectly thought and hoped in my better state of mind, that, penetrated as I had been with fervent gratitude towards him through so many years, I believed I had never loved him so dearly, never thanked him in my heart so fully, as I did that night. And when he had taken me to my room and kissed me at the door, and when at last I lay down to sleep, my thought was how could I ever be busy enough, how could I ever be good enough, how in my little way could I ever hope to be forgetful enough of myself, devoted enough to him, and useful enough to others, to show him how I blessed and honoured him.\n\nThe Letter and the Answer\nMy guardian called me into his room next morning, and then I told him what had been left untold on the previous night. There was nothing to be done, he said, but to keep the secret and to avoid another such encounter as that of yesterday. He understood my feeling and entirely shared it. He charged himself even with restraining Mr. Skimpole from improving his opportunity. One person whom he need not name to me, it was not now possible for him to advise or help. He wished it were, but no such thing could be. If her mistrust of the lawyer whom she had mentioned were well-founded, which he scarcely doubted, he dreaded discovery. He knew something of him, both by sight and by reputation, and it was certain that he was a dangerous man. Whatever happened, he repeatedly impressed upon me with anxious affection and kindness, I was as innocent of as himself and as unable to influence.\n\n“Nor do I understand,” said he, “that any doubts tend towards you, my dear. Much suspicion may exist without that connexion.”\n\n“With the lawyer,” I returned. “But two other persons have come into my mind since I have been anxious. Then I told him all about Mr. Guppy, who I feared might have had his vague surmises when I little understood his meaning, but in whose silence after our last interview I expressed perfect confidence.”\n\n“Well,” said my guardian. “Then we may dismiss him for the present. Who is the other?”\n\nI called to his recollection the French maid and the eager offer of herself she had made to me.\n\n“Ha!” he returned thoughtfully. “That is a more alarming person than the clerk. But after all, my dear, it was but seeking for a new service. She had seen you and Ada a little while before, and it was natural that you should come into her head. She merely proposed herself for your maid, you know. She did nothing more.”\n\n“Her manner was strange,” said I.\n\n“Yes, and her manner was strange when she took her shoes off and showed that cool relish for a walk that might have ended in her death-bed,” said my guardian. “It would be useless self-distress and torment to reckon up such chances and possibilities. There are very few harmless circumstances that would not seem full of perilous meaning, so considered. Be hopeful, little woman. You can be nothing better than yourself; be that, through this knowledge, as you were before you had it. It is the best you can do for everybody’s sake. I, sharing the secret with you—”\n\n“And lightening it, guardian, so much,” said I.\n\n“—will be attentive to what passes in that family, so far as I can observe it from my distance. And if the time should come when I can stretch out a hand to render the least service to one whom it is better not to name even here, I will not fail to do it for her dear daughter’s sake.”\n\nI thanked him with my whole heart. What could I ever do but thank him! I was going out at the door when he asked me to stay a moment. Quickly turning round, I saw that same expression on his face again; and all at once, I don’t know how, it flashed upon me as a new and far-off possibility that I understood it.\n\n“My dear Esther,” said my guardian, “I have long had something in my thoughts that I have wished to say to you.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“I have had some difficulty in approaching it, and I still have. I should wish it to be so deliberately said, and so deliberately considered. Would you object to my writing it?”\n\n“Dear guardian, how could I object to your writing anything for ME to read?”\n\n“Then see, my love,” said he with his cheery smile, “am I at this moment quite as plain and easy—do I seem as open, as honest and old-fashioned—as I am at any time?”\n\nI answered in all earnestness, “Quite.” With the strictest truth, for his momentary hesitation was gone (it had not lasted a minute), and his fine, sensible, cordial, sterling manner was restored.\n\n“Do I look as if I suppressed anything, meant anything but what I said, had any reservation at all, no matter what?” said he with his bright clear eyes on mine.\n\nI answered, most assuredly he did not.\n\n“Can you fully trust me, and thoroughly rely on what I profess, Esther?”\n\n“Most thoroughly,” said I with my whole heart.\n\n“My dear girl,” returned my guardian, “give me your hand.”\n\nHe took it in his, holding me lightly with his arm, and looking down into my face with the same genuine freshness and faithfulness of manner—the old protecting manner which had made that house my home in a moment—said, “You have wrought changes in me, little woman, since the winter day in the stage-coach. First and last you have done me a world of good since that time.”\n\n“Ah, guardian, what have you done for me since that time!”\n\n“But,” said he, “that is not to be remembered now.”\n\n“It never can be forgotten.”\n\n“Yes, Esther,” said he with a gentle seriousness, “it is to be forgotten now, to be forgotten for a while. You are only to remember now that nothing can change me as you know me. Can you feel quite assured of that, my dear?”\n\n“I can, and I do,” I said.\n\n“That’s much,” he answered. “That’s everything. But I must not take that at a word. I will not write this something in my thoughts until you have quite resolved within yourself that nothing can change me as you know me. If you doubt that in the least degree, I will never write it. If you are sure of that, on good consideration, send Charley to me this night week—‘for the letter.’ But if you are not quite certain, never send. Mind, I trust to your truth, in this thing as in everything. If you are not quite certain on that one point, never send!”\n\n“Guardian,” said I, “I am already certain, I can no more be changed in that conviction than you can be changed towards me. I shall send Charley for the letter.”\n\nHe shook my hand and said no more. Nor was any more said in reference to this conversation, either by him or me, through the whole week. When the appointed night came, I said to Charley as soon as I was alone, “Go and knock at Mr. Jarndyce’s door, Charley, and say you have come from me—‘for the letter.’” Charley went up the stairs, and down the stairs, and along the passages—the zig-zag way about the old-fashioned house seemed very long in my listening ears that night—and so came back, along the passages, and down the stairs, and up the stairs, and brought the letter. “Lay it on the table, Charley,” said I. So Charley laid it on the table and went to bed, and I sat looking at it without taking it up, thinking of many things.\n\nI began with my overshadowed childhood, and passed through those timid days to the heavy time when my aunt lay dead, with her resolute face so cold and set, and when I was more solitary with Mrs. Rachael than if I had had no one in the world to speak to or to look at. I passed to the altered days when I was so blest as to find friends in all around me, and to be beloved. I came to the time when I first saw my dear girl and was received into that sisterly affection which was the grace and beauty of my life. I recalled the first bright gleam of welcome which had shone out of those very windows upon our expectant faces on that cold bright night, and which had never paled. I lived my happy life there over again, I went through my illness and recovery, I thought of myself so altered and of those around me so unchanged; and all this happiness shone like a light from one central figure, represented before me by the letter on the table.\n\nI opened it and read it. It was so impressive in its love for me, and in the unselfish caution it gave me, and the consideration it showed for me in every word, that my eyes were too often blinded to read much at a time. But I read it through three times before I laid it down. I had thought beforehand that I knew its purport, and I did. It asked me, would I be the mistress of Bleak House.\n\nIt was not a love letter, though it expressed so much love, but was written just as he would at any time have spoken to me. I saw his face, and heard his voice, and felt the influence of his kind protecting manner in every line. It addressed me as if our places were reversed, as if all the good deeds had been mine and all the feelings they had awakened his. It dwelt on my being young, and he past the prime of life; on his having attained a ripe age, while I was a child; on his writing to me with a silvered head, and knowing all this so well as to set it in full before me for mature deliberation. It told me that I would gain nothing by such a marriage and lose nothing by rejecting it, for no new relation could enhance the tenderness in which he held me, and whatever my decision was, he was certain it would be right. But he had considered this step anew since our late confidence and had decided on taking it, if it only served to show me through one poor instance that the whole world would readily unite to falsify the stern prediction of my childhood. I was the last to know what happiness I could bestow upon him, but of that he said no more, for I was always to remember that I owed him nothing and that he was my debtor, and for very much. He had often thought of our future, and foreseeing that the time must come, and fearing that it might come soon, when Ada (now very nearly of age) would leave us, and when our present mode of life must be broken up, had become accustomed to reflect on this proposal. Thus he made it. If I felt that I could ever give him the best right he could have to be my protector, and if I felt that I could happily and justly become the dear companion of his remaining life, superior to all lighter chances and changes than death, even then he could not have me bind myself irrevocably while this letter was yet so new to me, but even then I must have ample time for reconsideration. In that case, or in the opposite case, let him be unchanged in his old relation, in his old manner, in the old name by which I called him. And as to his bright Dame Durden and little housekeeper, she would ever be the same, he knew.\n\nThis was the substance of the letter, written throughout with a justice and a dignity as if he were indeed my responsible guardian impartially representing the proposal of a friend against whom in his integrity he stated the full case.\n\nBut he did not hint to me that when I had been better looking he had had this same proceeding in his thoughts and had refrained from it. That when my old face was gone from me, and I had no attractions, he could love me just as well as in my fairer days. That the discovery of my birth gave him no shock. That his generosity rose above my disfigurement and my inheritance of shame. That the more I stood in need of such fidelity, the more firmly I might trust in him to the last.\n\nBut I knew it, I knew it well now. It came upon me as the close of the benignant history I had been pursuing, and I felt that I had but one thing to do. To devote my life to his happiness was to thank him poorly, and what had I wished for the other night but some new means of thanking him?\n\nStill I cried very much, not only in the fullness of my heart after reading the letter, not only in the strangeness of the prospect—for it was strange though I had expected the contents—but as if something for which there was no name or distinct idea were indefinitely lost to me. I was very happy, very thankful, very hopeful; but I cried very much.\n\nBy and by I went to my old glass. My eyes were red and swollen, and I said, “Oh, Esther, Esther, can that be you!” I am afraid the face in the glass was going to cry again at this reproach, but I held up my finger at it, and it stopped.\n\n“That is more like the composed look you comforted me with, my dear, when you showed me such a change!” said I, beginning to let down my hair. “When you are mistress of Bleak House, you are to be as cheerful as a bird. In fact, you are always to be cheerful; so let us begin for once and for all.”\n\nI went on with my hair now, quite comfortably. I sobbed a little still, but that was because I had been crying, not because I was crying then.\n\n“And so Esther, my dear, you are happy for life. Happy with your best friends, happy in your old home, happy in the power of doing a great deal of good, and happy in the undeserved love of the best of men.”\n\nI thought, all at once, if my guardian had married some one else, how should I have felt, and what should I have done! That would have been a change indeed. It presented my life in such a new and blank form that I rang my housekeeping keys and gave them a kiss before I laid them down in their basket again.\n\nThen I went on to think, as I dressed my hair before the glass, how often had I considered within myself that the deep traces of my illness and the circumstances of my birth were only new reasons why I should be busy, busy, busy—useful, amiable, serviceable, in all honest, unpretending ways. This was a good time, to be sure, to sit down morbidly and cry! As to its seeming at all strange to me at first (if that were any excuse for crying, which it was not) that I was one day to be the mistress of Bleak House, why should it seem strange? Other people had thought of such things, if I had not. “Don’t you remember, my plain dear,” I asked myself, looking at the glass, “what Mrs. Woodcourt said before those scars were there about your marrying—”\n\nPerhaps the name brought them to my remembrance. The dried remains of the flowers. It would be better not to keep them now. They had only been preserved in memory of something wholly past and gone, but it would be better not to keep them now.\n\nThey were in a book, and it happened to be in the next room—our sitting-room, dividing Ada’s chamber from mine. I took a candle and went softly in to fetch it from its shelf. After I had it in my hand, I saw my beautiful darling, through the open door, lying asleep, and I stole in to kiss her.\n\nIt was weak in me, I know, and I could have no reason for crying; but I dropped a tear upon her dear face, and another, and another. Weaker than that, I took the withered flowers out and put them for a moment to her lips. I thought about her love for Richard, though, indeed, the flowers had nothing to do with that. Then I took them into my own room and burned them at the candle, and they were dust in an instant.\n\nOn entering the breakfast-room next morning, I found my guardian just as usual, quite as frank, as open, and free. There being not the least constraint in his manner, there was none (or I think there was none) in mine. I was with him several times in the course of the morning, in and out, when there was no one there, and I thought it not unlikely that he might speak to me about the letter, but he did not say a word.\n\nSo, on the next morning, and the next, and for at least a week, over which time Mr. Skimpole prolonged his stay. I expected, every day, that my guardian might speak to me about the letter, but he never did.\n\nI thought then, growing uneasy, that I ought to write an answer. I tried over and over again in my own room at night, but I could not write an answer that at all began like a good answer, so I thought each night I would wait one more day. And I waited seven more days, and he never said a word.\n\nAt last, Mr. Skimpole having departed, we three were one afternoon going out for a ride; and I, being dressed before Ada and going down, came upon my guardian, with his back towards me, standing at the drawing-room window looking out.\n\nHe turned on my coming in and said, smiling, “Aye, it’s you, little woman, is it?” and looked out again.\n\nI had made up my mind to speak to him now. In short, I had come down on purpose. “Guardian,” I said, rather hesitating and trembling, “when would you like to have the answer to the letter Charley came for?”\n\n“When it’s ready, my dear,” he replied.\n\n“I think it is ready,” said I.\n\n“Is Charley to bring it?” he asked pleasantly.\n\n“No. I have brought it myself, guardian,” I returned.\n\nI put my two arms round his neck and kissed him, and he said was this the mistress of Bleak House, and I said yes; and it made no difference presently, and we all went out together, and I said nothing to my precious pet about it.\nIn Trust\n\nOne morning when I had done jingling about with my baskets of keys, as my beauty and I were walking round and round the garden I happened to turn my eyes towards the house and saw a long thin shadow going in which looked like Mr. Vholes. Ada had been telling me only that morning of her hopes that Richard might exhaust his ardour in the Chancery suit by being so very earnest in it; and therefore, not to damp my dear girl’s spirits, I said nothing about Mr. Vholes’s shadow.\n\nPresently came Charley, lightly winding among the bushes and tripping along the paths, as rosy and pretty as one of Flora’s attendants instead of my maid, saying, “Oh, if you please, miss, would you step and speak to Mr. Jarndyce!”\n\nIt was one of Charley’s peculiarities that whenever she was charged with a message she always began to deliver it as soon as she beheld, at any distance, the person for whom it was intended. Therefore I saw Charley asking me in her usual form of words to “step and speak” to Mr. Jarndyce long before I heard her. And when I did hear her, she had said it so often that she was out of breath.\n\nI told Ada I would make haste back and inquired of Charley as we went in whether there was not a gentleman with Mr. Jarndyce. To which Charley, whose grammar, I confess to my shame, never did any credit to my educational powers, replied, “Yes, miss. Him as come down in the country with Mr. Richard.”\n\nA more complete contrast than my guardian and Mr. Vholes I suppose there could not be. I found them looking at one another across a table, the one so open and the other so close, the one so broad and upright and the other so narrow and stooping, the one giving out what he had to say in such a rich ringing voice and the other keeping it in in such a cold-blooded, gasping, fish-like manner that I thought I never had seen two people so unmatched.\n\n“You know Mr. Vholes, my dear,” said my guardian. Not with the greatest urbanity, I must say.\n\nMr. Vholes rose, gloved and buttoned up as usual, and seated himself again, just as he had seated himself beside Richard in the gig. Not having Richard to look at, he looked straight before him.\n\n“Mr. Vholes,” said my guardian, eyeing his black figure as if he were a bird of ill omen, “has brought an ugly report of our most unfortunate Rick.” Laying a marked emphasis on “most unfortunate” as if the words were rather descriptive of his connexion with Mr. Vholes.\n\nI sat down between them; Mr. Vholes remained immovable, except that he secretly picked at one of the red pimples on his yellow face with his black glove.\n\n“And as Rick and you are happily good friends, I should like to know,” said my guardian, “what you think, my dear. Would you be so good as to—as to speak up, Mr. Vholes?”\n\nDoing anything but that, Mr. Vholes observed, “I have been saying that I have reason to know, Miss Summerson, as Mr. C.’s professional adviser, that Mr. C.’s circumstances are at the present moment in an embarrassed state. Not so much in point of amount as owing to the peculiar and pressing nature of liabilities Mr. C. has incurred and the means he has of liquidating or meeting the same. I have staved off many little matters for Mr. C., but there is a limit to staving off, and we have reached it. I have made some advances out of pocket to accommodate these unpleasantnesses, but I necessarily look to being repaid, for I do not pretend to be a man of capital, and I have a father to support in the Vale of Taunton, besides striving to realize some little independence for three dear girls at home. My apprehension is, Mr. C.’s circumstances being such, lest it should end in his obtaining leave to part with his commission, which at all events is desirable to be made known to his connexions.”\n\nMr. Vholes, who had looked at me while speaking, here emerged into the silence he could hardly be said to have broken, so stifled was his tone, and looked before him again.\n\n“Imagine the poor fellow without even his present resource,” said my guardian to me. “Yet what can I do? You know him, Esther. He would never accept of help from me now. To offer it or hint at it would be to drive him to an extremity, if nothing else did.”\n\nMr. Vholes hereupon addressed me again.\n\n“What Mr. Jarndyce remarks, miss, is no doubt the case, and is the difficulty. I do not see that anything is to be done. I do not say that anything is to be done. Far from it. I merely come down here under the seal of confidence and mention it in order that everything may be openly carried on and that it may not be said afterwards that everything was not openly carried on. My wish is that everything should be openly carried on. I desire to leave a good name behind me. If I consulted merely my own interests with Mr. C., I should not be here. So insurmountable, as you must well know, would be his objections. This is not a professional attendance. This can he charged to nobody. I have no interest in it except as a member of society and a father—AND a son,” said Mr. Vholes, who had nearly forgotten that point.\n\nIt appeared to us that Mr. Vholes said neither more nor less than the truth in intimating that he sought to divide the responsibility, such as it was, of knowing Richard’s situation. I could only suggest that I should go down to Deal, where Richard was then stationed, and see him, and try if it were possible to avert the worst. Without consulting Mr. Vholes on this point, I took my guardian aside to propose it, while Mr. Vholes gauntly stalked to the fire and warmed his funeral gloves.\n\nThe fatigue of the journey formed an immediate objection on my guardian’s part, but as I saw he had no other, and as I was only too happy to go, I got his consent. We had then merely to dispose of Mr. Vholes.\n\n“Well, sir,” said Mr. Jarndyce, “Miss Summerson will communicate with Mr. Carstone, and you can only hope that his position may be yet retrievable. You will allow me to order you lunch after your journey, sir.”\n\n“I thank you, Mr. Jarndyce,” said Mr. Vholes, putting out his long black sleeve to check the ringing of the bell, “not any. I thank you, no, not a morsel. My digestion is much impaired, and I am but a poor knife and fork at any time. If I was to partake of solid food at this period of the day, I don’t know what the consequences might be. Everything having been openly carried on, sir, I will now with your permission take my leave.”\n\n“And I would that you could take your leave, and we could all take our leave, Mr. Vholes,” returned my guardian bitterly, “of a cause you know of.”\n\nMr. Vholes, whose black dye was so deep from head to foot that it had quite steamed before the fire, diffusing a very unpleasant perfume, made a short one-sided inclination of his head from the neck and slowly shook it.\n\n“We whose ambition it is to be looked upon in the light of respectable practitioners, sir, can but put our shoulders to the wheel. We do it, sir. At least, I do it myself; and I wish to think well of my professional brethren, one and all. You are sensible of an obligation not to refer to me, miss, in communicating with Mr. C.?”\n\nI said I would be careful not to do it.\n\n“Just so, miss. Good morning. Mr. Jarndyce, good morning, sir.” Mr. Vholes put his dead glove, which scarcely seemed to have any hand in it, on my fingers, and then on my guardian’s fingers, and took his long thin shadow away. I thought of it on the outside of the coach, passing over all the sunny landscape between us and London, chilling the seed in the ground as it glided along.\n\nOf course it became necessary to tell Ada where I was going and why I was going, and of course she was anxious and distressed. But she was too true to Richard to say anything but words of pity and words of excuse, and in a more loving spirit still—my dear devoted girl!—she wrote him a long letter, of which I took charge.\n\nCharley was to be my travelling companion, though I am sure I wanted none and would willingly have left her at home. We all went to London that afternoon, and finding two places in the mail, secured them. At our usual bed-time, Charley and I were rolling away seaward with the Kentish letters.\n\nIt was a night’s journey in those coach times, but we had the mail to ourselves and did not find the night very tedious. It passed with me as I suppose it would with most people under such circumstances. At one while my journey looked hopeful, and at another hopeless. Now I thought I should do some good, and now I wondered how I could ever have supposed so. Now it seemed one of the most reasonable things in the world that I should have come, and now one of the most unreasonable. In what state I should find Richard, what I should say to him, and what he would say to me occupied my mind by turns with these two states of feeling; and the wheels seemed to play one tune (to which the burden of my guardian’s letter set itself) over and over again all night.\n\nAt last we came into the narrow streets of Deal, and very gloomy they were upon a raw misty morning. The long flat beach, with its little irregular houses, wooden and brick, and its litter of capstans, and great boats, and sheds, and bare upright poles with tackle and blocks, and loose gravelly waste places overgrown with grass and weeds, wore as dull an appearance as any place I ever saw. The sea was heaving under a thick white fog; and nothing else was moving but a few early ropemakers, who, with the yarn twisted round their bodies, looked as if, tired of their present state of existence, they were spinning themselves into cordage.\n\nBut when we got into a warm room in an excellent hotel and sat down, comfortably washed and dressed, to an early breakfast (for it was too late to think of going to bed), Deal began to look more cheerful. Our little room was like a ship’s cabin, and that delighted Charley very much. Then the fog began to rise like a curtain, and numbers of ships that we had had no idea were near appeared. I don’t know how many sail the waiter told us were then lying in the downs. Some of these vessels were of grand size—one was a large Indiaman just come home; and when the sun shone through the clouds, making silvery pools in the dark sea, the way in which these ships brightened, and shadowed, and changed, amid a bustle of boats pulling off from the shore to them and from them to the shore, and a general life and motion in themselves and everything around them, was most beautiful.\n\nThe large Indiaman was our great attraction because she had come into the downs in the night. She was surrounded by boats, and we said how glad the people on board of her must be to come ashore. Charley was curious, too, about the voyage, and about the heat in India, and the serpents and the tigers; and as she picked up such information much faster than grammar, I told her what I knew on those points. I told her, too, how people in such voyages were sometimes wrecked and cast on rocks, where they were saved by the intrepidity and humanity of one man. And Charley asking how that could be, I told her how we knew at home of such a case.\n\nI had thought of sending Richard a note saying I was there, but it seemed so much better to go to him without preparation. As he lived in barracks I was a little doubtful whether this was feasible, but we went out to reconnoitre. Peeping in at the gate of the barrack-yard, we found everything very quiet at that time in the morning, and I asked a sergeant standing on the guardhouse-steps where he lived. He sent a man before to show me, who went up some bare stairs, and knocked with his knuckles at a door, and left us.\n\n“Now then!” cried Richard from within. So I left Charley in the little passage, and going on to the half-open door, said, “Can I come in, Richard? It’s only Dame Durden.”\n\nHe was writing at a table, with a great confusion of clothes, tin cases, books, boots, brushes, and portmanteaus strewn all about the floor. He was only half dressed—in plain clothes, I observed, not in uniform—and his hair was unbrushed, and he looked as wild as his room. All this I saw after he had heartily welcomed me and I was seated near him, for he started upon hearing my voice and caught me in his arms in a moment. Dear Richard! He was ever the same to me. Down to—ah, poor poor fellow!—to the end, he never received me but with something of his old merry boyish manner.\n\n“Good heaven, my dear little woman,” said he, “how do you come here? Who could have thought of seeing you! Nothing the matter? Ada is well?”\n\n“Quite well. Lovelier than ever, Richard!”\n\n“Ah!” he said, leaning back in his chair. “My poor cousin! I was writing to you, Esther.”\n\nSo worn and haggard as he looked, even in the fullness of his handsome youth, leaning back in his chair and crushing the closely written sheet of paper in his hand!\n\n“Have you been at the trouble of writing all that, and am I not to read it after all?” I asked.\n\n“Oh, my dear,” he returned with a hopeless gesture. “You may read it in the whole room. It is all over here.”\n\nI mildly entreated him not to be despondent. I told him that I had heard by chance of his being in difficulty and had come to consult with him what could best be done.\n\n“Like you, Esther, but useless, and so NOT like you!” said he with a melancholy smile. “I am away on leave this day—should have been gone in another hour—and that is to smooth it over, for my selling out. Well! Let bygones be bygones. So this calling follows the rest. I only want to have been in the church to have made the round of all the professions.”\n\n“Richard,” I urged, “it is not so hopeless as that?”\n\n“Esther,” he returned, “it is indeed. I am just so near disgrace as that those who are put in authority over me (as the catechism goes) would far rather be without me than with me. And they are right. Apart from debts and duns and all such drawbacks, I am not fit even for this employment. I have no care, no mind, no heart, no soul, but for one thing. Why, if this bubble hadn’t broken now,” he said, tearing the letter he had written into fragments and moodily casting them away, by driblets, “how could I have gone abroad? I must have been ordered abroad, but how could I have gone? How could I, with my experience of that thing, trust even Vholes unless I was at his back!”\n\nI suppose he knew by my face what I was about to say, but he caught the hand I had laid upon his arm and touched my own lips with it to prevent me from going on.\n\n“No, Dame Durden! Two subjects I forbid—must forbid. The first is John Jarndyce. The second, you know what. Call it madness, and I tell you I can’t help it now, and can’t be sane. But it is no such thing; it is the one object I have to pursue. It is a pity I ever was prevailed upon to turn out of my road for any other. It would be wisdom to abandon it now, after all the time, anxiety, and pains I have bestowed upon it! Oh, yes, true wisdom. It would be very agreeable, too, to some people; but I never will.”\n\nHe was in that mood in which I thought it best not to increase his determination (if anything could increase it) by opposing him. I took out Ada’s letter and put it in his hand.\n\n“Am I to read it now?” he asked.\n\nAs I told him yes, he laid it on the table, and resting his head upon his hand, began. He had not read far when he rested his head upon his two hands—to hide his face from me. In a little while he rose as if the light were bad and went to the window. He finished reading it there, with his back towards me, and after he had finished and had folded it up, stood there for some minutes with the letter in his hand. When he came back to his chair, I saw tears in his eyes.\n\n“Of course, Esther, you know what she says here?” He spoke in a softened voice and kissed the letter as he asked me.\n\n“Yes, Richard.”\n\n“Offers me,” he went on, tapping his foot upon the floor, “the little inheritance she is certain of so soon—just as little and as much as I have wasted—and begs and prays me to take it, set myself right with it, and remain in the service.”\n\n“I know your welfare to be the dearest wish of her heart,” said I. “And, oh, my dear Richard, Ada’s is a noble heart.”\n\n“I am sure it is. I—I wish I was dead!”\n\nHe went back to the window, and laying his arm across it, leaned his head down on his arm. It greatly affected me to see him so, but I hoped he might become more yielding, and I remained silent. My experience was very limited; I was not at all prepared for his rousing himself out of this emotion to a new sense of injury.\n\n“And this is the heart that the same John Jarndyce, who is not otherwise to be mentioned between us, stepped in to estrange from me,” said he indignantly. “And the dear girl makes me this generous offer from under the same John Jarndyce’s roof, and with the same John Jarndyce’s gracious consent and connivance, I dare say, as a new means of buying me off.”\n\n“Richard!” I cried out, rising hastily. “I will not hear you say such shameful words!” I was very angry with him indeed, for the first time in my life, but it only lasted a moment. When I saw his worn young face looking at me as if he were sorry, I put my hand on his shoulder and said, “If you please, my dear Richard, do not speak in such a tone to me. Consider!”\n\nHe blamed himself exceedingly and told me in the most generous manner that he had been very wrong and that he begged my pardon a thousand times. At that I laughed, but trembled a little too, for I was rather fluttered after being so fiery.\n\n“To accept this offer, my dear Esther,” said he, sitting down beside me and resuming our conversation, “—once more, pray, pray forgive me; I am deeply grieved—to accept my dearest cousin’s offer is, I need not say, impossible. Besides, I have letters and papers that I could show you which would convince you it is all over here. I have done with the red coat, believe me. But it is some satisfaction, in the midst of my troubles and perplexities, to know that I am pressing Ada’s interests in pressing my own. Vholes has his shoulder to the wheel, and he cannot help urging it on as much for her as for me, thank God!”\n\nHis sanguine hopes were rising within him and lighting up his features, but they made his face more sad to me than it had been before.\n\n“No, no!” cried Richard exultingly. “If every farthing of Ada’s little fortune were mine, no part of it should be spent in retaining me in what I am not fit for, can take no interest in, and am weary of. It should be devoted to what promises a better return, and should be used where she has a larger stake. Don’t be uneasy for me! I shall now have only one thing on my mind, and Vholes and I will work it. I shall not be without means. Free of my commission, I shall be able to compound with some small usurers who will hear of nothing but their bond now—Vholes says so. I should have a balance in my favour anyway, but that would swell it. Come, come! You shall carry a letter to Ada from me, Esther, and you must both of you be more hopeful of me and not believe that I am quite cast away just yet, my dear.”\n\nI will not repeat what I said to Richard. I know it was tiresome, and nobody is to suppose for a moment that it was at all wise. It only came from my heart. He heard it patiently and feelingly, but I saw that on the two subjects he had reserved it was at present hopeless to make any representation to him. I saw too, and had experienced in this very interview, the sense of my guardian’s remark that it was even more mischievous to use persuasion with him than to leave him as he was.\n\nTherefore I was driven at last to asking Richard if he would mind convincing me that it really was all over there, as he had said, and that it was not his mere impression. He showed me without hesitation a correspondence making it quite plain that his retirement was arranged. I found, from what he told me, that Mr. Vholes had copies of these papers and had been in consultation with him throughout. Beyond ascertaining this, and having been the bearer of Ada’s letter, and being (as I was going to be) Richard’s companion back to London, I had done no good by coming down. Admitting this to myself with a reluctant heart, I said I would return to the hotel and wait until he joined me there, so he threw a cloak over his shoulders and saw me to the gate, and Charley and I went back along the beach.\n\nThere was a concourse of people in one spot, surrounding some naval officers who were landing from a boat, and pressing about them with unusual interest. I said to Charley this would be one of the great Indiaman’s boats now, and we stopped to look.\n\nThe gentlemen came slowly up from the waterside, speaking good-humouredly to each other and to the people around and glancing about them as if they were glad to be in England again. “Charley, Charley,” said I, “come away!” And I hurried on so swiftly that my little maid was surprised.\n\nIt was not until we were shut up in our cabin-room and I had had time to take breath that I began to think why I had made such haste. In one of the sunburnt faces I had recognized Mr. Allan Woodcourt, and I had been afraid of his recognizing me. I had been unwilling that he should see my altered looks. I had been taken by surprise, and my courage had quite failed me.\n\nBut I knew this would not do, and I now said to myself, “My dear, there is no reason—there is and there can be no reason at all—why it should be worse for you now than it ever has been. What you were last month, you are to-day; you are no worse, you are no better. This is not your resolution; call it up, Esther, call it up!” I was in a great tremble—with running—and at first was quite unable to calm myself; but I got better, and I was very glad to know it.\n\nThe party came to the hotel. I heard them speaking on the staircase. I was sure it was the same gentlemen because I knew their voices again—I mean I knew Mr. Woodcourt’s. It would still have been a great relief to me to have gone away without making myself known, but I was determined not to do so. “No, my dear, no. No, no, no!”\n\nI untied my bonnet and put my veil half up—I think I mean half down, but it matters very little—and wrote on one of my cards that I happened to be there with Mr. Richard Carstone, and I sent it in to Mr. Woodcourt. He came immediately. I told him I was rejoiced to be by chance among the first to welcome him home to England. And I saw that he was very sorry for me.\n\n“You have been in shipwreck and peril since you left us, Mr. Woodcourt,” said I, “but we can hardly call that a misfortune which enabled you to be so useful and so brave. We read of it with the truest interest. It first came to my knowledge through your old patient, poor Miss Flite, when I was recovering from my severe illness.”\n\n“Ah! Little Miss Flite!” he said. “She lives the same life yet?”\n\n“Just the same.”\n\nI was so comfortable with myself now as not to mind the veil and to be able to put it aside.\n\n“Her gratitude to you, Mr. Woodcourt, is delightful. She is a most affectionate creature, as I have reason to say.”\n\n“You—you have found her so?” he returned. “I—I am glad of that.” He was so very sorry for me that he could scarcely speak.\n\n“I assure you,” said I, “that I was deeply touched by her sympathy and pleasure at the time I have referred to.”\n\n“I was grieved to hear that you had been very ill.”\n\n“I was very ill.”\n\n“But you have quite recovered?”\n\n“I have quite recovered my health and my cheerfulness,” said I. “You know how good my guardian is and what a happy life we lead, and I have everything to be thankful for and nothing in the world to desire.”\n\nI felt as if he had greater commiseration for me than I had ever had for myself. It inspired me with new fortitude and new calmness to find that it was I who was under the necessity of reassuring him. I spoke to him of his voyage out and home, and of his future plans, and of his probable return to India. He said that was very doubtful. He had not found himself more favoured by fortune there than here. He had gone out a poor ship’s surgeon and had come home nothing better. While we were talking, and when I was glad to believe that I had alleviated (if I may use such a term) the shock he had had in seeing me, Richard came in. He had heard downstairs who was with me, and they met with cordial pleasure.\n\nI saw that after their first greetings were over, and when they spoke of Richard’s career, Mr. Woodcourt had a perception that all was not going well with him. He frequently glanced at his face as if there were something in it that gave him pain, and more than once he looked towards me as though he sought to ascertain whether I knew what the truth was. Yet Richard was in one of his sanguine states and in good spirits and was thoroughly pleased to see Mr. Woodcourt again, whom he had always liked.\n\nRichard proposed that we all should go to London together; but Mr. Woodcourt, having to remain by his ship a little longer, could not join us. He dined with us, however, at an early hour, and became so much more like what he used to be that I was still more at peace to think I had been able to soften his regrets. Yet his mind was not relieved of Richard. When the coach was almost ready and Richard ran down to look after his luggage, he spoke to me about him.\n\nI was not sure that I had a right to lay his whole story open, but I referred in a few words to his estrangement from Mr Jarndyce and to his being entangled in the ill-fated Chancery suit. Mr. Woodcourt listened with interest and expressed his regret.\n\n“I saw you observe him rather closely,” said I, “Do you think him so changed?”\n\n“He is changed,” he returned, shaking his head.\n\nI felt the blood rush into my face for the first time, but it was only an instantaneous emotion. I turned my head aside, and it was gone.\n\n“It is not,” said Mr. Woodcourt, “his being so much younger or older, or thinner or fatter, or paler or ruddier, as there being upon his face such a singular expression. I never saw so remarkable a look in a young person. One cannot say that it is all anxiety or all weariness; yet it is both, and like ungrown despair.”\n\n“You do not think he is ill?” said I.\n\nNo. He looked robust in body.\n\n“That he cannot be at peace in mind, we have too much reason to know,” I proceeded. “Mr. Woodcourt, you are going to London?”\n\n“To-morrow or the next day.”\n\n“There is nothing Richard wants so much as a friend. He always liked you. Pray see him when you get there. Pray help him sometimes with your companionship if you can. You do not know of what service it might be. You cannot think how Ada, and Mr. Jarndyce, and even I—how we should all thank you, Mr. Woodcourt!”\n\n“Miss Summerson,” he said, more moved than he had been from the first, “before heaven, I will be a true friend to him! I will accept him as a trust, and it shall be a sacred one!”\n\n“God bless you!” said I, with my eyes filling fast; but I thought they might, when it was not for myself. “Ada loves him—we all love him, but Ada loves him as we cannot. I will tell her what you say. Thank you, and God bless you, in her name!”\n\nRichard came back as we finished exchanging these hurried words and gave me his arm to take me to the coach.\n\n“Woodcourt,” he said, unconscious with what application, “pray let us meet in London!”\n\n“Meet?” returned the other. “I have scarcely a friend there now but you. Where shall I find you?”\n\n“Why, I must get a lodging of some sort,” said Richard, pondering. “Say at Vholes’s, Symond’s Inn.”\n\n“Good! Without loss of time.”\n\nThey shook hands heartily. When I was seated in the coach and Richard was yet standing in the street, Mr. Woodcourt laid his friendly hand on Richard’s shoulder and looked at me. I understood him and waved mine in thanks.\n\nAnd in his last look as we drove away, I saw that he was very sorry for me. I was glad to see it. I felt for my old self as the dead may feel if they ever revisit these scenes. I was glad to be tenderly remembered, to be gently pitied, not to be quite forgotten.\nStop Him!\n\nDarkness rests upon Tom-All-Alone’s. Dilating and dilating since the sun went down last night, it has gradually swelled until it fills every void in the place. For a time there were some dungeon lights burning, as the lamp of life hums in Tom-all-Alone’s, heavily, heavily, in the nauseous air, and winking—as that lamp, too, winks in Tom-all-Alone’s—at many horrible things. But they are blotted out. The moon has eyed Tom with a dull cold stare, as admitting some puny emulation of herself in his desert region unfit for life and blasted by volcanic fires; but she has passed on and is gone. The blackest nightmare in the infernal stables grazes on Tom-all-Alone’s, and Tom is fast asleep.\n\nMuch mighty speech-making there has been, both in and out of Parliament, concerning Tom, and much wrathful disputation how Tom shall be got right. Whether he shall be put into the main road by constables, or by beadles, or by bell-ringing, or by force of figures, or by correct principles of taste, or by high church, or by low church, or by no church; whether he shall be set to splitting trusses of polemical straws with the crooked knife of his mind or whether he shall be put to stone-breaking instead. In the midst of which dust and noise there is but one thing perfectly clear, to wit, that Tom only may and can, or shall and will, be reclaimed according to somebody’s theory but nobody’s practice. And in the hopeful meantime, Tom goes to perdition head foremost in his old determined spirit.\n\nBut he has his revenge. Even the winds are his messengers, and they serve him in these hours of darkness. There is not a drop of Tom’s corrupted blood but propagates infection and contagion somewhere. It shall pollute, this very night, the choice stream (in which chemists on analysis would find the genuine nobility) of a Norman house, and his Grace shall not be able to say nay to the infamous alliance. There is not an atom of Tom’s slime, not a cubic inch of any pestilential gas in which he lives, not one obscenity or degradation about him, not an ignorance, not a wickedness, not a brutality of his committing, but shall work its retribution through every order of society up to the proudest of the proud and to the highest of the high. Verily, what with tainting, plundering, and spoiling, Tom has his revenge.\n\nIt is a moot point whether Tom-all-Alone’s be uglier by day or by night, but on the argument that the more that is seen of it the more shocking it must be, and that no part of it left to the imagination is at all likely to be made so bad as the reality, day carries it. The day begins to break now; and in truth it might be better for the national glory even that the sun should sometimes set upon the British dominions than that it should ever rise upon so vile a wonder as Tom.\n\nA brown sunburnt gentleman, who appears in some inaptitude for sleep to be wandering abroad rather than counting the hours on a restless pillow, strolls hitherward at this quiet time. Attracted by curiosity, he often pauses and looks about him, up and down the miserable by-ways. Nor is he merely curious, for in his bright dark eye there is compassionate interest; and as he looks here and there, he seems to understand such wretchedness and to have studied it before.\n\nOn the banks of the stagnant channel of mud which is the main street of Tom-all-Alone’s, nothing is to be seen but the crazy houses, shut up and silent. No waking creature save himself appears except in one direction, where he sees the solitary figure of a woman sitting on a door-step. He walks that way. Approaching, he observes that she has journeyed a long distance and is footsore and travel-stained. She sits on the door-step in the manner of one who is waiting, with her elbow on her knee and her head upon her hand. Beside her is a canvas bag, or bundle, she has carried. She is dozing probably, for she gives no heed to his steps as he comes toward her.\n\nThe broken footway is so narrow that when Allan Woodcourt comes to where the woman sits, he has to turn into the road to pass her. Looking down at her face, his eye meets hers, and he stops.\n\n“What is the matter?”\n\n“Nothing, sir.”\n\n“Can’t you make them hear? Do you want to be let in?”\n\n“I’m waiting till they get up at another house—a lodging-house—not here,” the woman patiently returns. “I’m waiting here because there will be sun here presently to warm me.”\n\n“I am afraid you are tired. I am sorry to see you sitting in the street.”\n\n“Thank you, sir. It don’t matter.”\n\nA habit in him of speaking to the poor and of avoiding patronage or condescension or childishness (which is the favourite device, many people deeming it quite a subtlety to talk to them like little spelling books) has put him on good terms with the woman easily.\n\n“Let me look at your forehead,” he says, bending down. “I am a doctor. Don’t be afraid. I wouldn’t hurt you for the world.”\n\nHe knows that by touching her with his skilful and accustomed hand he can soothe her yet more readily. She makes a slight objection, saying, “It’s nothing”; but he has scarcely laid his fingers on the wounded place when she lifts it up to the light.\n\n“Aye! A bad bruise, and the skin sadly broken. This must be very sore.”\n\n“It do ache a little, sir,” returns the woman with a started tear upon her cheek.\n\n“Let me try to make it more comfortable. My handkerchief won’t hurt you.”\n\n“Oh, dear no, sir, I’m sure of that!”\n\nHe cleanses the injured place and dries it, and having carefully examined it and gently pressed it with the palm of his hand, takes a small case from his pocket, dresses it, and binds it up. While he is thus employed, he says, after laughing at his establishing a surgery in the street, “And so your husband is a brickmaker?”\n\n“How do you know that, sir?” asks the woman, astonished.\n\n“Why, I suppose so from the colour of the clay upon your bag and on your dress. And I know brickmakers go about working at piecework in different places. And I am sorry to say I have known them cruel to their wives too.”\n\nThe woman hastily lifts up her eyes as if she would deny that her injury is referable to such a cause. But feeling the hand upon her forehead, and seeing his busy and composed face, she quietly drops them again.\n\n“Where is he now?” asks the surgeon.\n\n“He got into trouble last night, sir; but he’ll look for me at the lodging-house.”\n\n“He will get into worse trouble if he often misuses his large and heavy hand as he has misused it here. But you forgive him, brutal as he is, and I say no more of him, except that I wish he deserved it. You have no young child?”\n\nThe woman shakes her head. “One as I calls mine, sir, but it’s Liz’s.”\n\n“Your own is dead. I see! Poor little thing!”\n\nBy this time he has finished and is putting up his case. “I suppose you have some settled home. Is it far from here?” he asks, good-humouredly making light of what he has done as she gets up and curtsys.\n\n“It’s a good two or three and twenty mile from here, sir. At Saint Albans. You know Saint Albans, sir? I thought you gave a start like, as if you did.”\n\n“Yes, I know something of it. And now I will ask you a question in return. Have you money for your lodging?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” she says, “really and truly.” And she shows it. He tells her, in acknowledgment of her many subdued thanks, that she is very welcome, gives her good day, and walks away. Tom-all-Alone’s is still asleep, and nothing is astir.\n\nYes, something is! As he retraces his way to the point from which he descried the woman at a distance sitting on the step, he sees a ragged figure coming very cautiously along, crouching close to the soiled walls—which the wretchedest figure might as well avoid—and furtively thrusting a hand before it. It is the figure of a youth whose face is hollow and whose eyes have an emaciated glare. He is so intent on getting along unseen that even the apparition of a stranger in whole garments does not tempt him to look back. He shades his face with his ragged elbow as he passes on the other side of the way, and goes shrinking and creeping on with his anxious hand before him and his shapeless clothes hanging in shreds. Clothes made for what purpose, or of what material, it would be impossible to say. They look, in colour and in substance, like a bundle of rank leaves of swampy growth that rotted long ago.\n\nAllan Woodcourt pauses to look after him and note all this, with a shadowy belief that he has seen the boy before. He cannot recall how or where, but there is some association in his mind with such a form. He imagines that he must have seen it in some hospital or refuge, still, cannot make out why it comes with any special force on his remembrance.\n\nHe is gradually emerging from Tom-all-Alone’s in the morning light, thinking about it, when he hears running feet behind him, and looking round, sees the boy scouring towards him at great speed, followed by the woman.\n\n“Stop him, stop him!” cries the woman, almost breathless. “Stop him, sir!”\n\nHe darts across the road into the boy’s path, but the boy is quicker than he, makes a curve, ducks, dives under his hands, comes up half-a-dozen yards beyond him, and scours away again. Still the woman follows, crying, “Stop him, sir, pray stop him!” Allan, not knowing but that he has just robbed her of her money, follows in chase and runs so hard that he runs the boy down a dozen times, but each time he repeats the curve, the duck, the dive, and scours away again. To strike at him on any of these occasions would be to fell and disable him, but the pursuer cannot resolve to do that, and so the grimly ridiculous pursuit continues. At last the fugitive, hard-pressed, takes to a narrow passage and a court which has no thoroughfare. Here, against a hoarding of decaying timber, he is brought to bay and tumbles down, lying gasping at his pursuer, who stands and gasps at him until the woman comes up.\n\n“Oh, you, Jo!” cries the woman. “What? I have found you at last!”\n\n“Jo,” repeats Allan, looking at him with attention, “Jo! Stay. To be sure! I recollect this lad some time ago being brought before the coroner.”\n\n“Yes, I see you once afore at the inkwhich,” whimpers Jo. “What of that? Can’t you never let such an unfortnet as me alone? An’t I unfortnet enough for you yet? How unfortnet do you want me fur to be? I’ve been a-chivied and a-chivied, fust by one on you and nixt by another on you, till I’m worritted to skins and bones. The inkwhich warn’t MY fault. I done nothink. He wos wery good to me, he wos; he wos the only one I knowed to speak to, as ever come across my crossing. It ain’t wery likely I should want him to be inkwhiched. I only wish I wos, myself. I don’t know why I don’t go and make a hole in the water, I’m sure I don’t.”\n\nHe says it with such a pitiable air, and his grimy tears appear so real, and he lies in the corner up against the hoarding so like a growth of fungus or any unwholesome excrescence produced there in neglect and impurity, that Allan Woodcourt is softened towards him. He says to the woman, “Miserable creature, what has he done?”\n\nTo which she only replies, shaking her head at the prostrate figure more amazedly than angrily, “Oh, you Jo, you Jo. I have found you at last!”\n\n“What has he done?” says Allan. “Has he robbed you?”\n\n“No, sir, no. Robbed me? He did nothing but what was kind-hearted by me, and that’s the wonder of it.”\n\nAllan looks from Jo to the woman, and from the woman to Jo, waiting for one of them to unravel the riddle.\n\n“But he was along with me, sir,” says the woman. “Oh, you Jo! He was along with me, sir, down at Saint Albans, ill, and a young lady, Lord bless her for a good friend to me, took pity on him when I durstn’t, and took him home—”\n\nAllan shrinks back from him with a sudden horror.\n\n“Yes, sir, yes. Took him home, and made him comfortable, and like a thankless monster he ran away in the night and never has been seen or heard of since till I set eyes on him just now. And that young lady that was such a pretty dear caught his illness, lost her beautiful looks, and wouldn’t hardly be known for the same young lady now if it wasn’t for her angel temper, and her pretty shape, and her sweet voice. Do you know it? You ungrateful wretch, do you know that this is all along of you and of her goodness to you?” demands the woman, beginning to rage at him as she recalls it and breaking into passionate tears.\n\nThe boy, in rough sort stunned by what he hears, falls to smearing his dirty forehead with his dirty palm, and to staring at the ground, and to shaking from head to foot until the crazy hoarding against which he leans rattles.\n\nAllan restrains the woman, merely by a quiet gesture, but effectually.\n\n“Richard told me—” He falters. “I mean, I have heard of this—don’t mind me for a moment, I will speak presently.”\n\nHe turns away and stands for a while looking out at the covered passage. When he comes back, he has recovered his composure, except that he contends against an avoidance of the boy, which is so very remarkable that it absorbs the woman’s attention.\n\n“You hear what she says. But get up, get up!”\n\nJo, shaking and chattering, slowly rises and stands, after the manner of his tribe in a difficulty, sideways against the hoarding, resting one of his high shoulders against it and covertly rubbing his right hand over his left and his left foot over his right.\n\n“You hear what she says, and I know it’s true. Have you been here ever since?”\n\n“Wishermaydie if I seen Tom-all-Alone’s till this blessed morning,” replies Jo hoarsely.\n\n“Why have you come here now?”\n\nJo looks all round the confined court, looks at his questioner no higher than the knees, and finally answers, “I don’t know how to do nothink, and I can’t get nothink to do. I’m wery poor and ill, and I thought I’d come back here when there warn’t nobody about, and lay down and hide somewheres as I knows on till arter dark, and then go and beg a trifle of Mr. Snagsby. He wos allus willin fur to give me somethink he wos, though Mrs. Snagsby she was allus a-chivying on me—like everybody everywheres.”\n\n“Where have you come from?”\n\nJo looks all round the court again, looks at his questioner’s knees again, and concludes by laying his profile against the hoarding in a sort of resignation.\n\n“Did you hear me ask you where you have come from?”\n\n“Tramp then,” says Jo.\n\n“Now tell me,” proceeds Allan, making a strong effort to overcome his repugnance, going very near to him, and leaning over him with an expression of confidence, “tell me how it came about that you left that house when the good young lady had been so unfortunate as to pity you and take you home.”\n\nJo suddenly comes out of his resignation and excitedly declares, addressing the woman, that he never known about the young lady, that he never heern about it, that he never went fur to hurt her, that he would sooner have hurt his own self, that he’d sooner have had his unfortnet ed chopped off than ever gone a-nigh her, and that she wos wery good to him, she wos. Conducting himself throughout as if in his poor fashion he really meant it, and winding up with some very miserable sobs.\n\nAllan Woodcourt sees that this is not a sham. He constrains himself to touch him. “Come, Jo. Tell me.”\n\n“No. I dustn’t,” says Jo, relapsing into the profile state. “I dustn’t, or I would.”\n\n“But I must know,” returns the other, “all the same. Come, Jo.”\n\nAfter two or three such adjurations, Jo lifts up his head again, looks round the court again, and says in a low voice, “Well, I’ll tell you something. I was took away. There!”\n\n“Took away? In the night?”\n\n“Ah!” Very apprehensive of being overheard, Jo looks about him and even glances up some ten feet at the top of the hoarding and through the cracks in it lest the object of his distrust should be looking over or hidden on the other side.\n\n“Who took you away?”\n\n“I dustn’t name him,” says Jo. “I dustn’t do it, sir.\n\n“But I want, in the young lady’s name, to know. You may trust me. No one else shall hear.”\n\n“Ah, but I don’t know,” replies Jo, shaking his head fearfully, “as he DON’T hear.”\n\n“Why, he is not in this place.”\n\n“Oh, ain’t he though?” says Jo. “He’s in all manner of places, all at wanst.”\n\nAllan looks at him in perplexity, but discovers some real meaning and good faith at the bottom of this bewildering reply. He patiently awaits an explicit answer; and Jo, more baffled by his patience than by anything else, at last desperately whispers a name in his ear.\n\n“Aye!” says Allan. “Why, what had you been doing?”\n\n“Nothink, sir. Never done nothink to get myself into no trouble, ’sept in not moving on and the inkwhich. But I’m a-moving on now. I’m a-moving on to the berryin ground—that’s the move as I’m up to.”\n\n“No, no, we will try to prevent that. But what did he do with you?”\n\n“Put me in a horsepittle,” replied Jo, whispering, “till I was discharged, then giv me a little money—four half-bulls, wot you may call half-crowns—and ses ‘Hook it! Nobody wants you here,’ he ses. ‘You hook it. You go and tramp,’ he ses. ‘You move on,’ he ses. ‘Don’t let me ever see you nowheres within forty mile of London, or you’ll repent it.’ So I shall, if ever he doos see me, and he’ll see me if I’m above ground,” concludes Jo, nervously repeating all his former precautions and investigations.\n\nAllan considers a little, then remarks, turning to the woman but keeping an encouraging eye on Jo, “He is not so ungrateful as you supposed. He had a reason for going away, though it was an insufficient one.”\n\n“Thankee, sir, thankee!” exclaims Jo. “There now! See how hard you wos upon me. But ony you tell the young lady wot the genlmn ses, and it’s all right. For YOU wos wery good to me too, and I knows it.”\n\n“Now, Jo,” says Allan, keeping his eye upon him, “come with me and I will find you a better place than this to lie down and hide in. If I take one side of the way and you the other to avoid observation, you will not run away, I know very well, if you make me a promise.”\n\n“I won’t, not unless I wos to see HIM a-coming, sir.”\n\n“Very well. I take your word. Half the town is getting up by this time, and the whole town will be broad awake in another hour. Come along. Good day again, my good woman.”\n\n“Good day again, sir, and I thank you kindly many times again.”\n\nShe has been sitting on her bag, deeply attentive, and now rises and takes it up. Jo, repeating, “Ony you tell the young lady as I never went fur to hurt her and wot the genlmn ses!” nods and shambles and shivers, and smears and blinks, and half laughs and half cries, a farewell to her, and takes his creeping way along after Allan Woodcourt, close to the houses on the opposite side of the street. In this order, the two come up out of Tom-all-Alone’s into the broad rays of the sunlight and the purer air.\n\nJo’s Will\nAs Allan Woodcourt and Jo proceed along the streets where the high church spires and the distances are so near and clear in the morning light that the city itself seems renewed by rest, Allan revolves in his mind how and where he shall bestow his companion. “It surely is a strange fact,” he considers, “that in the heart of a civilized world this creature in human form should be more difficult to dispose of than an unowned dog.” But it is none the less a fact because of its strangeness, and the difficulty remains.\n\nAt first he looks behind him often to assure himself that Jo is still really following. But look where he will, he still beholds him close to the opposite houses, making his way with his wary hand from brick to brick and from door to door, and often, as he creeps along, glancing over at him watchfully. Soon satisfied that the last thing in his thoughts is to give him the slip, Allan goes on, considering with a less divided attention what he shall do.\n\nA breakfast-stall at a street-corner suggests the first thing to be done. He stops there, looks round, and beckons Jo. Jo crosses and comes halting and shuffling up, slowly scooping the knuckles of his right hand round and round in the hollowed palm of his left, kneading dirt with a natural pestle and mortar. What is a dainty repast to Jo is then set before him, and he begins to gulp the coffee and to gnaw the bread and butter, looking anxiously about him in all directions as he eats and drinks, like a scared animal.\n\nBut he is so sick and miserable that even hunger has abandoned him. “I thought I was amost a-starvin, sir,” says Jo, soon putting down his food, “but I don’t know nothink—not even that. I don’t care for eating wittles nor yet for drinking on ’em.” And Jo stands shivering and looking at the breakfast wonderingly.\n\nAllan Woodcourt lays his hand upon his pulse and on his chest. “Draw breath, Jo!” “It draws,” says Jo, “as heavy as a cart.” He might add, “And rattles like it,” but he only mutters, “I’m a-moving on, sir.”\n\nAllan looks about for an apothecary’s shop. There is none at hand, but a tavern does as well or better. He obtains a little measure of wine and gives the lad a portion of it very carefully. He begins to revive almost as soon as it passes his lips. “We may repeat that dose, Jo,” observes Allan after watching him with his attentive face. “So! Now we will take five minutes’ rest, and then go on again.”\n\nLeaving the boy sitting on the bench of the breakfast-stall, with his back against an iron railing, Allan Woodcourt paces up and down in the early sunshine, casting an occasional look towards him without appearing to watch him. It requires no discernment to perceive that he is warmed and refreshed. If a face so shaded can brighten, his face brightens somewhat; and by little and little he eats the slice of bread he had so hopelessly laid down. Observant of these signs of improvement, Allan engages him in conversation and elicits to his no small wonder the adventure of the lady in the veil, with all its consequences. Jo slowly munches as he slowly tells it. When he has finished his story and his bread, they go on again.\n\nIntending to refer his difficulty in finding a temporary place of refuge for the boy to his old patient, zealous little Miss Flite, Allan leads the way to the court where he and Jo first foregathered. But all is changed at the rag and bottle shop; Miss Flite no longer lodges there; it is shut up; and a hard-featured female, much obscured by dust, whose age is a problem, but who is indeed no other than the interesting Judy, is tart and spare in her replies. These sufficing, however, to inform the visitor that Miss Flite and her birds are domiciled with a Mrs. Blinder, in Bell Yard, he repairs to that neighbouring place, where Miss Flite (who rises early that she may be punctual at the divan of justice held by her excellent friend the Chancellor) comes running downstairs with tears of welcome and with open arms.\n\n“My dear physician!” cries Miss Flite. “My meritorious, distinguished, honourable officer!” She uses some odd expressions, but is as cordial and full of heart as sanity itself can be—more so than it often is. Allan, very patient with her, waits until she has no more raptures to express, then points out Jo, trembling in a doorway, and tells her how he comes there.\n\n“Where can I lodge him hereabouts for the present? Now, you have a fund of knowledge and good sense and can advise me.”\n\nMiss Flite, mighty proud of the compliment, sets herself to consider; but it is long before a bright thought occurs to her. Mrs. Blinder is entirely let, and she herself occupies poor Gridley’s room. “Gridley!” exclaims Miss Flite, clapping her hands after a twentieth repetition of this remark. “Gridley! To be sure! Of course! My dear physician! General George will help us out.”\n\nIt is hopeless to ask for any information about General George, and would be, though Miss Flite had not already run upstairs to put on her pinched bonnet and her poor little shawl and to arm herself with her reticule of documents. But as she informs her physician in her disjointed manner on coming down in full array that General George, whom she often calls upon, knows her dear Fitz Jarndyce and takes a great interest in all connected with her, Allan is induced to think that they may be in the right way. So he tells Jo, for his encouragement, that this walking about will soon be over now; and they repair to the general’s. Fortunately it is not far.\n\nFrom the exterior of George’s Shooting Gallery, and the long entry, and the bare perspective beyond it, Allan Woodcourt augurs well. He also descries promise in the figure of Mr. George himself, striding towards them in his morning exercise with his pipe in his mouth, no stock on, and his muscular arms, developed by broadsword and dumbbell, weightily asserting themselves through his light shirt-sleeves.\n\n“Your servant, sir,” says Mr. George with a military salute. Good-humouredly smiling all over his broad forehead up into his crisp hair, he then defers to Miss Flite, as, with great stateliness, and at some length, she performs the courtly ceremony of presentation. He winds it up with another “Your servant, sir!” and another salute.\n\n“Excuse me, sir. A sailor, I believe?” says Mr. George.\n\n“I am proud to find I have the air of one,” returns Allan; “but I am only a sea-going doctor.”\n\n“Indeed, sir! I should have thought you was a regular blue-jacket myself.”\n\nAllan hopes Mr. George will forgive his intrusion the more readily on that account, and particularly that he will not lay aside his pipe, which, in his politeness, he has testified some intention of doing. “You are very good, sir,” returns the trooper. “As I know by experience that it’s not disagreeable to Miss Flite, and since it’s equally agreeable to yourself—” and finishes the sentence by putting it between his lips again. Allan proceeds to tell him all he knows about Jo, unto which the trooper listens with a grave face.\n\n“And that’s the lad, sir, is it?” he inquires, looking along the entry to where Jo stands staring up at the great letters on the whitewashed front, which have no meaning in his eyes.\n\n“That’s he,” says Allan. “And, Mr. George, I am in this difficulty about him. I am unwilling to place him in a hospital, even if I could procure him immediate admission, because I foresee that he would not stay there many hours if he could be so much as got there. The same objection applies to a workhouse, supposing I had the patience to be evaded and shirked, and handed about from post to pillar in trying to get him into one, which is a system that I don’t take kindly to.”\n\n“No man does, sir,” returns Mr. George.\n\n“I am convinced that he would not remain in either place, because he is possessed by an extraordinary terror of this person who ordered him to keep out of the way; in his ignorance, he believes this person to be everywhere, and cognizant of everything.”\n\n“I ask your pardon, sir,” says Mr. George. “But you have not mentioned that party’s name. Is it a secret, sir?”\n\n“The boy makes it one. But his name is Bucket.”\n\n“Bucket the detective, sir?”\n\n“The same man.”\n\n“The man is known to me, sir,” returns the trooper after blowing out a cloud of smoke and squaring his chest, “and the boy is so far correct that he undoubtedly is a—rum customer.” Mr. George smokes with a profound meaning after this and surveys Miss Flite in silence.\n\n“Now, I wish Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson at least to know that this Jo, who tells so strange a story, has reappeared, and to have it in their power to speak with him if they should desire to do so. Therefore I want to get him, for the present moment, into any poor lodging kept by decent people where he would be admitted. Decent people and Jo, Mr. George,” says Allan, following the direction of the trooper’s eyes along the entry, “have not been much acquainted, as you see. Hence the difficulty. Do you happen to know any one in this neighbourhood who would receive him for a while on my paying for him beforehand?”\n\nAs he puts the question, he becomes aware of a dirty-faced little man standing at the trooper’s elbow and looking up, with an oddly twisted figure and countenance, into the trooper’s face. After a few more puffs at his pipe, the trooper looks down askant at the little man, and the little man winks up at the trooper.\n\n“Well, sir,” says Mr. George, “I can assure you that I would willingly be knocked on the head at any time if it would be at all agreeable to Miss Summerson, and consequently I esteem it a privilege to do that young lady any service, however small. We are naturally in the vagabond way here, sir, both myself and Phil. You see what the place is. You are welcome to a quiet corner of it for the boy if the same would meet your views. No charge made, except for rations. We are not in a flourishing state of circumstances here, sir. We are liable to be tumbled out neck and crop at a moment’s notice. However, sir, such as the place is, and so long as it lasts, here it is at your service.”\n\nWith a comprehensive wave of his pipe, Mr. George places the whole building at his visitor’s disposal.\n\n“I take it for granted, sir,” he adds, “you being one of the medical staff, that there is no present infection about this unfortunate subject?”\n\nAllan is quite sure of it.\n\n“Because, sir,” says Mr. George, shaking his head sorrowfully, “we have had enough of that.”\n\nHis tone is no less sorrowfully echoed by his new acquaintance. “Still I am bound to tell you,” observes Allan after repeating his former assurance, “that the boy is deplorably low and reduced and that he may be—I do not say that he is—too far gone to recover.”\n\n“Do you consider him in present danger, sir?” inquires the trooper.\n\n“Yes, I fear so.”\n“Then, sir,” returns the trooper in a decisive manner, “it appears to me—being naturally in the vagabond way myself—that the sooner he comes out of the street, the better. You, Phil! Bring him in!”\n\nMr. Squod tacks out, all on one side, to execute the word of command; and the trooper, having smoked his pipe, lays it by. Jo is brought in. He is not one of Mrs. Pardiggle’s Tockahoopo Indians; he is not one of Mrs. Jellyby’s lambs, being wholly unconnected with Borrioboola-Gha; he is not softened by distance and unfamiliarity; he is not a genuine foreign-grown savage; he is the ordinary home-made article. Dirty, ugly, disagreeable to all the senses, in body a common creature of the common streets, only in soul a heathen. Homely filth begrimes him, homely parasites devour him, homely sores are in him, homely rags are on him; native ignorance, the growth of English soil and climate, sinks his immortal nature lower than the beasts that perish. Stand forth, Jo, in uncompromising colours! From the sole of thy foot to the crown of thy head, there is nothing interesting about thee.\n\nHe shuffles slowly into Mr. George’s gallery and stands huddled together in a bundle, looking all about the floor. He seems to know that they have an inclination to shrink from him, partly for what he is and partly for what he has caused. He, too, shrinks from them. He is not of the same order of things, not of the same place in creation. He is of no order and no place, neither of the beasts nor of humanity.\n\n“Look here, Jo!” says Allan. “This is Mr. George.”\n\nJo searches the floor for some time longer, then looks up for a moment, and then down again.\n\n“He is a kind friend to you, for he is going to give you lodging room here.”\n\nJo makes a scoop with one hand, which is supposed to be a bow. After a little more consideration and some backing and changing of the foot on which he rests, he mutters that he is “wery thankful.”\n\n“You are quite safe here. All you have to do at present is to be obedient and to get strong. And mind you tell us the truth here, whatever you do, Jo.”\n\n“Wishermaydie if I don’t, sir,” says Jo, reverting to his favourite declaration. “I never done nothink yit, but wot you knows on, to get myself into no trouble. I never was in no other trouble at all, sir, ’sept not knowin’ nothink and starwation.”\n\n“I believe it, now attend to Mr. George. I see he is going to speak to you.”\n\n“My intention merely was, sir,” observes Mr. George, amazingly broad and upright, “to point out to him where he can lie down and get a thorough good dose of sleep. Now, look here.” As the trooper speaks, he conducts them to the other end of the gallery and opens one of the little cabins. “There you are, you see! Here is a mattress, and here you may rest, on good behaviour, as long as Mr., I ask your pardon, sir”—he refers apologetically to the card Allan has given him—“Mr. Woodcourt pleases. Don’t you be alarmed if you hear shots; they’ll be aimed at the target, and not you. Now, there’s another thing I would recommend, sir,” says the trooper, turning to his visitor. “Phil, come here!”\n\nPhil bears down upon them according to his usual tactics. “Here is a man, sir, who was found, when a baby, in the gutter. Consequently, it is to be expected that he takes a natural interest in this poor creature. You do, don’t you, Phil?”\n\n“Certainly and surely I do, guv’ner,” is Phil’s reply.\n\n“Now I was thinking, sir,” says Mr. George in a martial sort of confidence, as if he were giving his opinion in a council of war at a drum-head, “that if this man was to take him to a bath and was to lay out a few shillings in getting him one or two coarse articles—”\n\n“Mr. George, my considerate friend,” returns Allan, taking out his purse, “it is the very favour I would have asked.”\n\nPhil Squod and Jo are sent out immediately on this work of improvement. Miss Flite, quite enraptured by her success, makes the best of her way to court, having great fears that otherwise her friend the Chancellor may be uneasy about her or may give the judgment she has so long expected in her absence, and observing “which you know, my dear physician, and general, after so many years, would be too absurdly unfortunate!” Allan takes the opportunity of going out to procure some restorative medicines, and obtaining them near at hand, soon returns to find the trooper walking up and down the gallery, and to fall into step and walk with him.\n\n“I take it, sir,” says Mr. George, “that you know Miss Summerson pretty well?”\n\nYes, it appears.\n\n“Not related to her, sir?”\n\nNo, it appears.\n\n“Excuse the apparent curiosity,” says Mr. George. “It seemed to me probable that you might take more than a common interest in this poor creature because Miss Summerson had taken that unfortunate interest in him. ’Tis MY case, sir, I assure you.”\n\n“And mine, Mr. George.”\n\nThe trooper looks sideways at Allan’s sunburnt cheek and bright dark eye, rapidly measures his height and build, and seems to approve of him.\n\n“Since you have been out, sir, I have been thinking that I unquestionably know the rooms in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, where Bucket took the lad, according to his account. Though he is not acquainted with the name, I can help you to it. It’s Tulkinghorn. That’s what it is.”\n\nAllan looks at him inquiringly, repeating the name.\n\n“Tulkinghorn. That’s the name, sir. I know the man, and know him to have been in communication with Bucket before, respecting a deceased person who had given him offence. I know the man, sir. To my sorrow.”\n\nAllan naturally asks what kind of man he is.\n\n“What kind of man! Do you mean to look at?”\n\n“I think I know that much of him. I mean to deal with. Generally, what kind of man?”\n\n“Why, then I’ll tell you, sir,” returns the trooper, stopping short and folding his arms on his square chest so angrily that his face fires and flushes all over; “he is a confoundedly bad kind of man. He is a slow-torturing kind of man. He is no more like flesh and blood than a rusty old carbine is. He is a kind of man—by George!—that has caused me more restlessness, and more uneasiness, and more dissatisfaction with myself than all other men put together. That’s the kind of man Mr. Tulkinghorn is!”\n\n“I am sorry,” says Allan, “to have touched so sore a place.”\n\n“Sore?” The trooper plants his legs wider apart, wets the palm of his broad right hand, and lays it on the imaginary moustache. “It’s no fault of yours, sir; but you shall judge. He has got a power over me. He is the man I spoke of just now as being able to tumble me out of this place neck and crop. He keeps me on a constant see-saw. He won’t hold off, and he won’t come on. If I have a payment to make him, or time to ask him for, or anything to go to him about, he don’t see me, don’t hear me—passes me on to Melchisedech’s in Clifford’s Inn, Melchisedech’s in Clifford’s Inn passes me back again to him—he keeps me prowling and dangling about him as if I was made of the same stone as himself. Why, I spend half my life now, pretty well, loitering and dodging about his door. What does he care? Nothing. Just as much as the rusty old carbine I have compared him to. He chafes and goads me till—Bah! Nonsense! I am forgetting myself. Mr. Woodcourt,” the trooper resumes his march, “all I say is, he is an old man; but I am glad I shall never have the chance of setting spurs to my horse and riding at him in a fair field. For if I had that chance, in one of the humours he drives me into—he’d go down, sir!”\n\nMr. George has been so excited that he finds it necessary to wipe his forehead on his shirt-sleeve. Even while he whistles his impetuosity away with the national anthem, some involuntary shakings of his head and heavings of his chest still linger behind, not to mention an occasional hasty adjustment with both hands of his open shirt-collar, as if it were scarcely open enough to prevent his being troubled by a choking sensation. In short, Allan Woodcourt has not much doubt about the going down of Mr. Tulkinghorn on the field referred to.\n\nJo and his conductor presently return, and Jo is assisted to his mattress by the careful Phil, to whom, after due administration of medicine by his own hands, Allan confides all needful means and instructions. The morning is by this time getting on apace. He repairs to his lodgings to dress and breakfast, and then, without seeking rest, goes away to Mr. Jarndyce to communicate his discovery.\n\nWith him Mr. Jarndyce returns alone, confidentially telling him that there are reasons for keeping this matter very quiet indeed and showing a serious interest in it. To Mr. Jarndyce, Jo repeats in substance what he said in the morning, without any material variation. Only that cart of his is heavier to draw, and draws with a hollower sound.\n\n“Let me lay here quiet and not be chivied no more,” falters Jo, “and be so kind any person as is a-passin nigh where I used fur to sleep, as jist to say to Mr. Sangsby that Jo, wot he known once, is a-moving on right forards with his duty, and I’ll be wery thankful. I’d be more thankful than I am aready if it wos any ways possible for an unfortnet to be it.”\n\nHe makes so many of these references to the law-stationer in the course of a day or two that Allan, after conferring with Mr. Jarndyce, good-naturedly resolves to call in Cook’s Court, the rather, as the cart seems to be breaking down.\n\nTo Cook’s Court, therefore, he repairs. Mr. Snagsby is behind his counter in his grey coat and sleeves, inspecting an indenture of several skins which has just come in from the engrosser’s, an immense desert of law-hand and parchment, with here and there a resting-place of a few large letters to break the awful monotony and save the traveller from despair. Mr Snagsby puts up at one of these inky wells and greets the stranger with his cough of general preparation for business.\n\n“You don’t remember me, Mr. Snagsby?”\n\nThe stationer’s heart begins to thump heavily, for his old apprehensions have never abated. It is as much as he can do to answer, “No, sir, I can’t say I do. I should have considered—not to put too fine a point upon it—that I never saw you before, sir.”\n\n“Twice before,” says Allan Woodcourt. “Once at a poor bedside, and once—”\n\n“It’s come at last!” thinks the afflicted stationer, as recollection breaks upon him. “It’s got to a head now and is going to burst!” But he has sufficient presence of mind to conduct his visitor into the little counting-house and to shut the door.\n\n“Are you a married man, sir?”\n\n“No, I am not.”\n\n“Would you make the attempt, though single,” says Mr. Snagsby in a melancholy whisper, “to speak as low as you can? For my little woman is a-listening somewheres, or I’ll forfeit the business and five hundred pound!”\n\nIn deep dejection Mr. Snagsby sits down on his stool, with his back against his desk, protesting, “I never had a secret of my own, sir. I can’t charge my memory with ever having once attempted to deceive my little woman on my own account since she named the day. I wouldn’t have done it, sir. Not to put too fine a point upon it, I couldn’t have done it, I dursn’t have done it. Whereas, and nevertheless, I find myself wrapped round with secrecy and mystery, till my life is a burden to me.”\n\nHis visitor professes his regret to hear it and asks him does he remember Jo. Mr. Snagsby answers with a suppressed groan, oh, don’t he!\n\n“You couldn’t name an individual human being—except myself—that my little woman is more set and determined against than Jo,” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\nAllan asks why.\n\n“Why?” repeats Mr. Snagsby, in his desperation clutching at the clump of hair at the back of his bald head. “How should I know why? But you are a single person, sir, and may you long be spared to ask a married person such a question!”\n\nWith this beneficent wish, Mr. Snagsby coughs a cough of dismal resignation and submits himself to hear what the visitor has to communicate.\n\n“There again!” says Mr. Snagsby, who, between the earnestness of his feelings and the suppressed tones of his voice is discoloured in the face. “At it again, in a new direction! A certain person charges me, in the solemnest way, not to talk of Jo to any one, even my little woman. Then comes another certain person, in the person of yourself, and charges me, in an equally solemn way, not to mention Jo to that other certain person above all other persons. Why, this is a private asylum! Why, not to put too fine a point upon it, this is Bedlam, sir!” says Mr. Snagsby.\n\nBut it is better than he expected after all, being no explosion of the mine below him or deepening of the pit into which he has fallen. And being tender-hearted and affected by the account he hears of Jo’s condition, he readily engages to “look round” as early in the evening as he can manage it quietly. He looks round very quietly when the evening comes, but it may turn out that Mrs. Snagsby is as quiet a manager as he.\n\nJo is very glad to see his old friend and says, when they are left alone, that he takes it uncommon kind as Mr. Sangsby should come so far out of his way on accounts of sich as him. Mr. Snagsby, touched by the spectacle before him, immediately lays upon the table half a crown, that magic balsam of his for all kinds of wounds.\n\n“And how do you find yourself, my poor lad?” inquires the stationer with his cough of sympathy.\n\n“I am in luck, Mr. Sangsby, I am,” returns Jo, “and don’t want for nothink. I’m more cumfbler nor you can’t think. Mr. Sangsby! I’m wery sorry that I done it, but I didn’t go fur to do it, sir.”\n\nThe stationer softly lays down another half-crown and asks him what it is that he is sorry for having done.\n\n“Mr. Sangsby,” says Jo, “I went and giv a illness to the lady as wos and yit as warn’t the t’other lady, and none of ’em never says nothink to me for having done it, on accounts of their being ser good and my having been s’unfortnet. The lady come herself and see me yesday, and she ses, ‘Ah, Jo!’ she ses. ‘We thought we’d lost you, Jo!’ she ses. And she sits down a-smilin so quiet, and don’t pass a word nor yit a look upon me for having done it, she don’t, and I turns agin the wall, I doos, Mr. Sangsby. And Mr. Jarnders, I see him a-forced to turn away his own self. And Mr. Woodcot, he come fur to giv me somethink fur to ease me, wot he’s allus a-doin’ on day and night, and wen he come a-bending over me and a-speakin up so bold, I see his tears a-fallin, Mr. Sangsby.”\n\nThe softened stationer deposits another half-crown on the table. Nothing less than a repetition of that infallible remedy will relieve his feelings.\n\n“Wot I was a-thinkin on, Mr. Sangsby,” proceeds Jo, “wos, as you wos able to write wery large, p’raps?”\n\n“Yes, Jo, please God,” returns the stationer.\n\n“Uncommon precious large, p’raps?” says Jo with eagerness.\n\n“Yes, my poor boy.”\n\nJo laughs with pleasure. “Wot I wos a-thinking on then, Mr. Sangsby, wos, that when I wos moved on as fur as ever I could go and couldn’t be moved no furder, whether you might be so good p’raps as to write out, wery large so that any one could see it anywheres, as that I wos wery truly hearty sorry that I done it and that I never went fur to do it, and that though I didn’t know nothink at all, I knowd as Mr. Woodcot once cried over it and wos allus grieved over it, and that I hoped as he’d be able to forgive me in his mind. If the writin could be made to say it wery large, he might.”\n\n“It shall say it, Jo. Very large.”\n\nJo laughs again. “Thankee, Mr. Sangsby. It’s wery kind of you, sir, and it makes me more cumfbler nor I was afore.”\n\nThe meek little stationer, with a broken and unfinished cough, slips down his fourth half-crown—he has never been so close to a case requiring so many—and is fain to depart. And Jo and he, upon this little earth, shall meet no more. No more.\n\nFor the cart so hard to draw is near its journey’s end and drags over stony ground. All round the clock it labours up the broken steps, shattered and worn. Not many times can the sun rise and behold it still upon its weary road.\n\nPhil Squod, with his smoky gunpowder visage, at once acts as nurse and works as armourer at his little table in a corner, often looking round and saying with a nod of his green-baize cap and an encouraging elevation of his one eyebrow, “Hold up, my boy! Hold up!” There, too, is Mr. Jarndyce many a time, and Allan Woodcourt almost always, both thinking, much, how strangely fate has entangled this rough outcast in the web of very different lives. There, too, the trooper is a frequent visitor, filling the doorway with his athletic figure and, from his superfluity of life and strength, seeming to shed down temporary vigour upon Jo, who never fails to speak more robustly in answer to his cheerful words.\n\nJo is in a sleep or in a stupor to-day, and Allan Woodcourt, newly arrived, stands by him, looking down upon his wasted form. After a while he softly seats himself upon the bedside with his face towards him—just as he sat in the law-writer’s room—and touches his chest and heart. The cart had very nearly given up, but labours on a little more.\n\nThe trooper stands in the doorway, still and silent. Phil has stopped in a low clinking noise, with his little hammer in his hand. Mr. Woodcourt looks round with that grave professional interest and attention on his face, and glancing significantly at the trooper, signs to Phil to carry his table out. When the little hammer is next used, there will be a speck of rust upon it.\n\n“Well, Jo! What is the matter? Don’t be frightened.”\n\n“I thought,” says Jo, who has started and is looking round, “I thought I was in Tom-all-Alone’s agin. Ain’t there nobody here but you, Mr. Woodcot?”\n\n“Nobody.”\n“And I ain’t took back to Tom-all-Alone’s. Am I, sir?”\n\n“No.” Jo closes his eyes, muttering, “I’m wery thankful.”\n\nAfter watching him closely a little while, Allan puts his mouth very near his ear and says to him in a low, distinct voice, “Jo! Did you ever know a prayer?”\n\n“Never knowd nothink, sir.”\n\n“Not so much as one short prayer?”\n\n“No, sir. Nothink at all. Mr. Chadbands he wos a-prayin wunst at Mr. Sangsby’s and I heerd him, but he sounded as if he wos a-speakin’ to hisself, and not to me. He prayed a lot, but I couldn’t make out nothink on it. Different times there was other genlmen come down Tom-all-Alone’s a-prayin, but they all mostly sed as the t’other ’wuns prayed wrong, and all mostly sounded to be a-talking to theirselves, or a-passing blame on the t’others, and not a-talkin to us. WE never knowd nothink. I never knowd what it wos all about.”\n\nIt takes him a long time to say this, and few but an experienced and attentive listener could hear, or, hearing, understand him. After a short relapse into sleep or stupor, he makes, of a sudden, a strong effort to get out of bed.\n\n“Stay, Jo! What now?”\n\n“It’s time for me to go to that there berryin ground, sir,” he returns with a wild look.\n\n“Lie down, and tell me. What burying ground, Jo?”\n\n“Where they laid him as wos wery good to me, wery good to me indeed, he wos. It’s time fur me to go down to that there berryin ground, sir, and ask to be put along with him. I wants to go there and be berried. He used fur to say to me, ‘I am as poor as you to-day, Jo,’ he ses. I wants to tell him that I am as poor as him now and have come there to be laid along with him.”\n\n“By and by, Jo. By and by.”\n\n“Ah! P’raps they wouldn’t do it if I wos to go myself. But will you promise to have me took there, sir, and laid along with him?”\n\n“I will, indeed.”\n\n“Thankee, sir. Thankee, sir. They’ll have to get the key of the gate afore they can take me in, for it’s allus locked. And there’s a step there, as I used for to clean with my broom. It’s turned wery dark, sir. Is there any light a-comin?”\n\n“It is coming fast, Jo.”\n\nFast. The cart is shaken all to pieces, and the rugged road is very near its end.\n\n“Jo, my poor fellow!”\n\n“I hear you, sir, in the dark, but I’m a-gropin—a-gropin—let me catch hold of your hand.”\n\n“Jo, can you say what I say?”\n\n“I’ll say anythink as you say, sir, for I knows it’s good.”\n\n“Our Father.”\n“Our Father! Yes, that’s wery good, sir.”\n\n“Which art in heaven.”\n\n“Art in heaven—is the light a-comin, sir?”\n\n“It is close at hand. Hallowed be thy name!”\n\n“Hallowed be—thy—”\n\nThe light is come upon the dark benighted way. Dead!\n\nDead, your Majesty. Dead, my lords and gentlemen. Dead, right reverends and wrong reverends of every order. Dead, men and women, born with heavenly compassion in your hearts. And dying thus around us every day.\n\nClosing In\nThe place in Lincolnshire has shut its many eyes again, and the house in town is awake. In Lincolnshire the Dedlocks of the past doze in their picture-frames, and the low wind murmurs through the long drawing-room as if they were breathing pretty regularly. In town the Dedlocks of the present rattle in their fire-eyed carriages through the darkness of the night, and the Dedlock Mercuries, with ashes (or hair-powder) on their heads, symptomatic of their great humility, loll away the drowsy mornings in the little windows of the hall. The fashionable world—tremendous orb, nearly five miles round—is in full swing, and the solar system works respectfully at its appointed distances.\n\nWhere the throng is thickest, where the lights are brightest, where all the senses are ministered to with the greatest delicacy and refinement, Lady Dedlock is. From the shining heights she has scaled and taken, she is never absent. Though the belief she of old reposed in herself as one able to reserve whatsoever she would under her mantle of pride is beaten down, though she has no assurance that what she is to those around her she will remain another day, it is not in her nature when envious eyes are looking on to yield or to droop. They say of her that she has lately grown more handsome and more haughty. The debilitated cousin says of her that she’s beauty nough—tsetup shopofwomen—but rather larming kind—remindingmanfact—inconvenient woman—who WILL getoutofbedandbawthstahlishment—Shakespeare.\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn says nothing, looks nothing. Now, as heretofore, he is to be found in doorways of rooms, with his limp white cravat loosely twisted into its old-fashioned tie, receiving patronage from the peerage and making no sign. Of all men he is still the last who might be supposed to have any influence upon my Lady. Of all women she is still the last who might be supposed to have any dread of him.\n\nOne thing has been much on her mind since their late interview in his turret-room at Chesney Wold. She is now decided, and prepared to throw it off.\n\nIt is morning in the great world, afternoon according to the little sun. The Mercuries, exhausted by looking out of window, are reposing in the hall and hang their heavy heads, the gorgeous creatures, like overblown sunflowers. Like them, too, they seem to run to a deal of seed in their tags and trimmings. Sir Leicester, in the library, has fallen asleep for the good of the country over the report of a Parliamentary committee. My Lady sits in the room in which she gave audience to the young man of the name of Guppy. Rosa is with her and has been writing for her and reading to her. Rosa is now at work upon embroidery or some such pretty thing, and as she bends her head over it, my Lady watches her in silence. Not for the first time to-day.\n\n“Rosa.”\n\nThe pretty village face looks brightly up. Then, seeing how serious my Lady is, looks puzzled and surprised.\n\n“See to the door. Is it shut?”\n\nYes. She goes to it and returns, and looks yet more surprised.\n\n“I am about to place confidence in you, child, for I know I may trust your attachment, if not your judgment. In what I am going to do, I will not disguise myself to you at least. But I confide in you. Say nothing to any one of what passes between us.”\n\nThe timid little beauty promises in all earnestness to be trustworthy.\n\n“Do you know,” Lady Dedlock asks her, signing to her to bring her chair nearer, “do you know, Rosa, that I am different to you from what I am to any one?”\n\n“Yes, my Lady. Much kinder. But then I often think I know you as you really are.”\n\n“You often think you know me as I really am? Poor child, poor child!”\n\nShe says it with a kind of scorn—though not of Rosa—and sits brooding, looking dreamily at her.\n\n“Do you think, Rosa, you are any relief or comfort to me? Do you suppose your being young and natural, and fond of me and grateful to me, makes it any pleasure to me to have you near me?”\n\n“I don’t know, my Lady; I can scarcely hope so. But with all my heart, I wish it was so.”\n\n“It is so, little one.”\n\nThe pretty face is checked in its flush of pleasure by the dark expression on the handsome face before it. It looks timidly for an explanation.\n\n“And if I were to say to-day, ‘Go! Leave me!’ I should say what would give me great pain and disquiet, child, and what would leave me very solitary.”\n\n“My Lady! Have I offended you?”\n\n“In nothing. Come here.”\n\nRosa bends down on the footstool at my Lady’s feet. My Lady, with that motherly touch of the famous ironmaster night, lays her hand upon her dark hair and gently keeps it there.\n\n“I told you, Rosa, that I wished you to be happy and that I would make you so if I could make anybody happy on this earth. I cannot. There are reasons now known to me, reasons in which you have no part, rendering it far better for you that you should not remain here. You must not remain here. I have determined that you shall not. I have written to the father of your lover, and he will be here to-day. All this I have done for your sake.”\n\nThe weeping girl covers her hand with kisses and says what shall she do, what shall she do, when they are separated! Her mistress kisses her on the cheek and makes no other answer.\n\n“Now, be happy, child, under better circumstances. Be beloved and happy!”\n\n“Ah, my Lady, I have sometimes thought—forgive my being so free—that YOU are not happy.”\n“I!”\n\n“Will you be more so when you have sent me away? Pray, pray, think again. Let me stay a little while!”\n\n“I have said, my child, that what I do, I do for your sake, not my own. It is done. What I am towards you, Rosa, is what I am now—not what I shall be a little while hence. Remember this, and keep my confidence. Do so much for my sake, and thus all ends between us!”\n\nShe detaches herself from her simple-hearted companion and leaves the room. Late in the afternoon, when she next appears upon the staircase, she is in her haughtiest and coldest state. As indifferent as if all passion, feeling, and interest had been worn out in the earlier ages of the world and had perished from its surface with its other departed monsters.\n\nMercury has announced Mr. Rouncewell, which is the cause of her appearance. Mr. Rouncewell is not in the library, but she repairs to the library. Sir Leicester is there, and she wishes to speak to him first.\n\n“Sir Leicester, I am desirous—but you are engaged.”\n\nOh, dear no! Not at all. Only Mr. Tulkinghorn.\n\nAlways at hand. Haunting every place. No relief or security from him for a moment.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Lady Dedlock. Will you allow me to retire?”\n\nWith a look that plainly says, “You know you have the power to remain if you will,” she tells him it is not necessary and moves towards a chair. Mr. Tulkinghorn brings it a little forward for her with his clumsy bow and retires into a window opposite. Interposed between her and the fading light of day in the now quiet street, his shadow falls upon her, and he darkens all before her. Even so does he darken her life.\n\nIt is a dull street under the best conditions, where the two long rows of houses stare at each other with that severity that half-a-dozen of its greatest mansions seem to have been slowly stared into stone rather than originally built in that material. It is a street of such dismal grandeur, so determined not to condescend to liveliness, that the doors and windows hold a gloomy state of their own in black paint and dust, and the echoing mews behind have a dry and massive appearance, as if they were reserved to stable the stone chargers of noble statues. Complicated garnish of iron-work entwines itself over the flights of steps in this awful street, and from these petrified bowers, extinguishers for obsolete flambeaux gasp at the upstart gas. Here and there a weak little iron hoop, through which bold boys aspire to throw their friends’ caps (its only present use), retains its place among the rusty foliage, sacred to the memory of departed oil. Nay, even oil itself, yet lingering at long intervals in a little absurd glass pot, with a knob in the bottom like an oyster, blinks and sulks at newer lights every night, like its high and dry master in the House of Lords.\n\nTherefore there is not much that Lady Dedlock, seated in her chair, could wish to see through the window in which Mr. Tulkinghorn stands. And yet—and yet—she sends a look in that direction as if it were her heart’s desire to have that figure moved out of the way.\n\nSir Leicester begs his Lady’s pardon. She was about to say?\n\n“Only that Mr. Rouncewell is here (he has called by my appointment) and that we had better make an end of the question of that girl. I am tired to death of the matter.”\n\n“What can I do—to—assist?” demands Sir Leicester in some considerable doubt.\n\n“Let us see him here and have done with it. Will you tell them to send him up?”\n\n“Mr. Tulkinghorn, be so good as to ring. Thank you. Request,” says Sir Leicester to Mercury, not immediately remembering the business term, “request the iron gentleman to walk this way.”\n\nMercury departs in search of the iron gentleman, finds, and produces him. Sir Leicester receives that ferruginous person graciously.\n\n“I hope you are well, Mr. Rouncewell. Be seated. (My solicitor, Mr. Tulkinghorn.) My Lady was desirous, Mr. Rouncewell,” Sir Leicester skilfully transfers him with a solemn wave of his hand, “was desirous to speak with you. Hem!”\n\n“I shall be very happy,” returns the iron gentleman, “to give my best attention to anything Lady Dedlock does me the honour to say.”\n\nAs he turns towards her, he finds that the impression she makes upon him is less agreeable than on the former occasion. A distant supercilious air makes a cold atmosphere about her, and there is nothing in her bearing, as there was before, to encourage openness.\n\n“Pray, sir,” says Lady Dedlock listlessly, “may I be allowed to inquire whether anything has passed between you and your son respecting your son’s fancy?”\n\nIt is almost too troublesome to her languid eyes to bestow a look upon him as she asks this question.\n\n“If my memory serves me, Lady Dedlock, I said, when I had the pleasure of seeing you before, that I should seriously advise my son to conquer that—fancy.” The ironmaster repeats her expression with a little emphasis.\n\n“And did you?”\n\n“Oh! Of course I did.”\n\nSir Leicester gives a nod, approving and confirmatory. Very proper. The iron gentleman, having said that he would do it, was bound to do it. No difference in this respect between the base metals and the precious. Highly proper.\n\n“And pray has he done so?”\n\n“Really, Lady Dedlock, I cannot make you a definite reply. I fear not. Probably not yet. In our condition of life, we sometimes couple an intention with our—our fancies which renders them not altogether easy to throw off. I think it is rather our way to be in earnest.”\n\nSir Leicester has a misgiving that there may be a hidden Wat Tylerish meaning in this expression, and fumes a little. Mr. Rouncewell is perfectly good-humoured and polite, but within such limits, evidently adapts his tone to his reception.\n\n“Because,” proceeds my Lady, “I have been thinking of the subject, which is tiresome to me.”\n\n“I am very sorry, I am sure.”\n\n“And also of what Sir Leicester said upon it, in which I quite concur”—Sir Leicester flattered—“and if you cannot give us the assurance that this fancy is at an end, I have come to the conclusion that the girl had better leave me.”\n\n“I can give no such assurance, Lady Dedlock. Nothing of the kind.”\n\n“Then she had better go.”\n\n“Excuse me, my Lady,” Sir Leicester considerately interposes, “but perhaps this may be doing an injury to the young woman which she has not merited. Here is a young woman,” says Sir Leicester, magnificently laying out the matter with his right hand like a service of plate, “whose good fortune it is to have attracted the notice and favour of an eminent lady and to live, under the protection of that eminent lady, surrounded by the various advantages which such a position confers, and which are unquestionably very great—I believe unquestionably very great, sir—for a young woman in that station of life. The question then arises, should that young woman be deprived of these many advantages and that good fortune simply because she has”—Sir Leicester, with an apologetic but dignified inclination of his head towards the ironmaster, winds up his sentence—“has attracted the notice of Mr Rouncewell’s son? Now, has she deserved this punishment? Is this just towards her? Is this our previous understanding?”\n\n“I beg your pardon,” interposes Mr. Rouncewell’s son’s father. “Sir Leicester, will you allow me? I think I may shorten the subject. Pray dismiss that from your consideration. If you remember anything so unimportant—which is not to be expected—you would recollect that my first thought in the affair was directly opposed to her remaining here.”\n\nDismiss the Dedlock patronage from consideration? Oh! Sir Leicester is bound to believe a pair of ears that have been handed down to him through such a family, or he really might have mistrusted their report of the iron gentleman’s observations.\n\n“It is not necessary,” observes my Lady in her coldest manner before he can do anything but breathe amazedly, “to enter into these matters on either side. The girl is a very good girl; I have nothing whatever to say against her, but she is so far insensible to her many advantages and her good fortune that she is in love—or supposes she is, poor little fool—and unable to appreciate them.”\n\nSir Leicester begs to observe that wholly alters the case. He might have been sure that my Lady had the best grounds and reasons in support of her view. He entirely agrees with my Lady. The young woman had better go.\n\n“As Sir Leicester observed, Mr. Rouncewell, on the last occasion when we were fatigued by this business,” Lady Dedlock languidly proceeds, “we cannot make conditions with you. Without conditions, and under present circumstances, the girl is quite misplaced here and had better go. I have told her so. Would you wish to have her sent back to the village, or would you like to take her with you, or what would you prefer?”\n\n“Lady Dedlock, if I may speak plainly—”\n\n“By all means.”\n\n“—I should prefer the course which will the soonest relieve you of the incumbrance and remove her from her present position.”\n\n“And to speak as plainly,” she returns with the same studied carelessness, “so should I. Do I understand that you will take her with you?”\n\nThe iron gentleman makes an iron bow.\n\n“Sir Leicester, will you ring?” Mr. Tulkinghorn steps forward from his window and pulls the bell. “I had forgotten you. Thank you.” He makes his usual bow and goes quietly back again. Mercury, swift-responsive, appears, receives instructions whom to produce, skims away, produces the aforesaid, and departs.\n\nRosa has been crying and is yet in distress. On her coming in, the ironmaster leaves his chair, takes her arm in his, and remains with her near the door ready to depart.\n\n“You are taken charge of, you see,” says my Lady in her weary manner, “and are going away well protected. I have mentioned that you are a very good girl, and you have nothing to cry for.”\n\n“She seems after all,” observes Mr. Tulkinghorn, loitering a little forward with his hands behind him, “as if she were crying at going away.”\n\n“Why, she is not well-bred, you see,” returns Mr. Rouncewell with some quickness in his manner, as if he were glad to have the lawyer to retort upon, “and she is an inexperienced little thing and knows no better. If she had remained here, sir, she would have improved, no doubt.”\n\n“No doubt,” is Mr. Tulkinghorn’s composed reply.\n\nRosa sobs out that she is very sorry to leave my Lady, and that she was happy at Chesney Wold, and has been happy with my Lady, and that she thanks my Lady over and over again. “Out, you silly little puss!” says the ironmaster, checking her in a low voice, though not angrily. “Have a spirit, if you’re fond of Watt!” My Lady merely waves her off with indifference, saying, “There, there, child! You are a good girl. Go away!” Sir Leicester has magnificently disengaged himself from the subject and retired into the sanctuary of his blue coat. Mr. Tulkinghorn, an indistinct form against the dark street now dotted with lamps, looms in my Lady’s view, bigger and blacker than before.\n\n“Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock,” says Mr. Rouncewell after a pause of a few moments, “I beg to take my leave, with an apology for having again troubled you, though not of my own act, on this tiresome subject. I can very well understand, I assure you, how tiresome so small a matter must have become to Lady Dedlock. If I am doubtful of my dealing with it, it is only because I did not at first quietly exert my influence to take my young friend here away without troubling you at all. But it appeared to me—I dare say magnifying the importance of the thing—that it was respectful to explain to you how the matter stood and candid to consult your wishes and convenience. I hope you will excuse my want of acquaintance with the polite world.”\n\nSir Leicester considers himself evoked out of the sanctuary by these remarks. “Mr. Rouncewell,” he returns, “do not mention it. Justifications are unnecessary, I hope, on either side.”\n\n“I am glad to hear it, Sir Leicester; and if I may, by way of a last word, revert to what I said before of my mother’s long connexion with the family and the worth it bespeaks on both sides, I would point out this little instance here on my arm who shows herself so affectionate and faithful in parting and in whom my mother, I dare say, has done something to awaken such feelings—though of course Lady Dedlock, by her heartfelt interest and her genial condescension, has done much more.”\n\nIf he mean this ironically, it may be truer than he thinks. He points it, however, by no deviation from his straightforward manner of speech, though in saying it he turns towards that part of the dim room where my Lady sits. Sir Leicester stands to return his parting salutation, Mr. Tulkinghorn again rings, Mercury takes another flight, and Mr. Rouncewell and Rosa leave the house.\n\nThen lights are brought in, discovering Mr. Tulkinghorn still standing in his window with his hands behind him and my Lady still sitting with his figure before her, closing up her view of the night as well as of the day. She is very pale. Mr. Tulkinghorn, observing it as she rises to retire, thinks, “Well she may be! The power of this woman is astonishing. She has been acting a part the whole time.” But he can act a part too—his one unchanging character—and as he holds the door open for this woman, fifty pairs of eyes, each fifty times sharper than Sir Leicester’s pair, should find no flaw in him.\n\nLady Dedlock dines alone in her own room to-day. Sir Leicester is whipped in to the rescue of the Doodle Party and the discomfiture of the Coodle Faction. Lady Dedlock asks on sitting down to dinner, still deadly pale (and quite an illustration of the debilitated cousin’s text), whether he is gone out? Yes. Whether Mr. Tulkinghorn is gone yet? No. Presently she asks again, is he gone YET? No. What is he doing? Mercury thinks he is writing letters in the library. Would my Lady wish to see him? Anything but that.\n\nBut he wishes to see my Lady. Within a few more minutes he is reported as sending his respects, and could my Lady please to receive him for a word or two after her dinner? My Lady will receive him now. He comes now, apologizing for intruding, even by her permission, while she is at table. When they are alone, my Lady waves her hand to dispense with such mockeries.\n\n“What do you want, sir?”\n\n“Why, Lady Dedlock,” says the lawyer, taking a chair at a little distance from her and slowly rubbing his rusty legs up and down, up and down, up and down, “I am rather surprised by the course you have taken.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“Yes, decidedly. I was not prepared for it. I consider it a departure from our agreement and your promise. It puts us in a new position, Lady Dedlock. I feel myself under the necessity of saying that I don’t approve of it.”\n\nHe stops in his rubbing and looks at her, with his hands on his knees. Imperturbable and unchangeable as he is, there is still an indefinable freedom in his manner which is new and which does not escape this woman’s observation.\n\n“I do not quite understand you.”\n\n“Oh, yes you do, I think. I think you do. Come, come, Lady Dedlock, we must not fence and parry now. You know you like this girl.”\n\n“Well, sir?”\n\n“And you know—and I know—that you have not sent her away for the reasons you have assigned, but for the purpose of separating her as much as possible from—excuse my mentioning it as a matter of business—any reproach and exposure that impend over yourself.”\n\n“Well, sir?”\n\n“Well, Lady Dedlock,” returns the lawyer, crossing his legs and nursing the uppermost knee. “I object to that. I consider that a dangerous proceeding. I know it to be unnecessary and calculated to awaken speculation, doubt, rumour, I don’t know what, in the house. Besides, it is a violation of our agreement. You were to be exactly what you were before. Whereas, it must be evident to yourself, as it is to me, that you have been this evening very different from what you were before. Why, bless my soul, Lady Dedlock, transparently so!”\n\n“If, sir,” she begins, “in my knowledge of my secret—” But he interrupts her.\n\n“Now, Lady Dedlock, this is a matter of business, and in a matter of business the ground cannot be kept too clear. It is no longer your secret. Excuse me. That is just the mistake. It is my secret, in trust for Sir Leicester and the family. If it were your secret, Lady Dedlock, we should not be here holding this conversation.”\n\n“That is very true. If in my knowledge of THE secret I do what I can to spare an innocent girl (especially, remembering your own reference to her when you told my story to the assembled guests at Chesney Wold) from the taint of my impending shame, I act upon a resolution I have taken. Nothing in the world, and no one in the world, could shake it or could move me.” This she says with great deliberation and distinctness and with no more outward passion than himself. As for him, he methodically discusses his matter of business as if she were any insensible instrument used in business.\n\n“Really? Then you see, Lady Dedlock,” he returns, “you are not to be trusted. You have put the case in a perfectly plain way, and according to the literal fact; and that being the case, you are not to be trusted.”\n\n“Perhaps you may remember that I expressed some anxiety on this same point when we spoke at night at Chesney Wold?”\n\n“Yes,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, coolly getting up and standing on the hearth. “Yes. I recollect, Lady Dedlock, that you certainly referred to the girl, but that was before we came to our arrangement, and both the letter and the spirit of our arrangement altogether precluded any action on your part founded upon my discovery. There can be no doubt about that. As to sparing the girl, of what importance or value is she? Spare! Lady Dedlock, here is a family name compromised. One might have supposed that the course was straight on—over everything, neither to the right nor to the left, regardless of all considerations in the way, sparing nothing, treading everything under foot.”\n\nShe has been looking at the table. She lifts up her eyes and looks at him. There is a stern expression on her face and a part of her lower lip is compressed under her teeth. “This woman understands me,” Mr. Tulkinghorn thinks as she lets her glance fall again. “SHE cannot be spared. Why should she spare others?”\n\nFor a little while they are silent. Lady Dedlock has eaten no dinner, but has twice or thrice poured out water with a steady hand and drunk it. She rises from table, takes a lounging-chair, and reclines in it, shading her face. There is nothing in her manner to express weakness or excite compassion. It is thoughtful, gloomy, concentrated. “This woman,” thinks Mr. Tulkinghorn, standing on the hearth, again a dark object closing up her view, “is a study.”\n\nHe studies her at his leisure, not speaking for a time. She too studies something at her leisure. She is not the first to speak, appearing indeed so unlikely to be so, though he stood there until midnight, that even he is driven upon breaking silence.\n\n“Lady Dedlock, the most disagreeable part of this business interview remains, but it is business. Our agreement is broken. A lady of your sense and strength of character will be prepared for my now declaring it void and taking my own course.”\n\n“I am quite prepared.”\n\nMr. Tulkinghorn inclines his head. “That is all I have to trouble you with, Lady Dedlock.”\n\nShe stops him as he is moving out of the room by asking, “This is the notice I was to receive? I wish not to misapprehend you.”\n\n“Not exactly the notice you were to receive, Lady Dedlock, because the contemplated notice supposed the agreement to have been observed. But virtually the same, virtually the same. The difference is merely in a lawyer’s mind.”\n\n“You intend to give me no other notice?”\n\n“You are right. No.”\n\n“Do you contemplate undeceiving Sir Leicester to-night?”\n\n“A home question!” says Mr. Tulkinghorn with a slight smile and cautiously shaking his head at the shaded face. “No, not to-night.”\n\n“To-morrow?”\n\n“All things considered, I had better decline answering that question, Lady Dedlock. If I were to say I don’t know when, exactly, you would not believe me, and it would answer no purpose. It may be to-morrow. I would rather say no more. You are prepared, and I hold out no expectations which circumstances might fail to justify. I wish you good evening.”\n\nShe removes her hand, turns her pale face towards him as he walks silently to the door, and stops him once again as he is about to open it.\n\n“Do you intend to remain in the house any time? I heard you were writing in the library. Are you going to return there?”\n\n“Only for my hat. I am going home.”\n\nShe bows her eyes rather than her head, the movement is so slight and curious, and he withdraws. Clear of the room he looks at his watch but is inclined to doubt it by a minute or thereabouts. There is a splendid clock upon the staircase, famous, as splendid clocks not often are, for its accuracy. “And what do YOU say,” Mr. Tulkinghorn inquires, referring to it. “What do you say?”\n\nIf it said now, “Don’t go home!” What a famous clock, hereafter, if it said to-night of all the nights that it has counted off, to this old man of all the young and old men who have ever stood before it, “Don’t go home!” With its sharp clear bell it strikes three quarters after seven and ticks on again. “Why, you are worse than I thought you,” says Mr. Tulkinghorn, muttering reproof to his watch. “Two minutes wrong? At this rate you won’t last my time.” What a watch to return good for evil if it ticked in answer, “Don’t go home!”\n\nHe passes out into the streets and walks on, with his hands behind him, under the shadow of the lofty houses, many of whose mysteries, difficulties, mortgages, delicate affairs of all kinds, are treasured up within his old black satin waistcoat. He is in the confidence of the very bricks and mortar. The high chimney-stacks telegraph family secrets to him. Yet there is not a voice in a mile of them to whisper, “Don’t go home!”\n\nThrough the stir and motion of the commoner streets; through the roar and jar of many vehicles, many feet, many voices; with the blazing shop-lights lighting him on, the west wind blowing him on, and the crowd pressing him on, he is pitilessly urged upon his way, and nothing meets him murmuring, “Don’t go home!” Arrived at last in his dull room to light his candles, and look round and up, and see the Roman pointing from the ceiling, there is no new significance in the Roman’s hand to-night or in the flutter of the attendant groups to give him the late warning, “Don’t come here!”\n\nIt is a moonlight night, but the moon, being past the full, is only now rising over the great wilderness of London. The stars are shining as they shone above the turret-leads at Chesney Wold. This woman, as he has of late been so accustomed to call her, looks out upon them. Her soul is turbulent within her; she is sick at heart and restless. The large rooms are too cramped and close. She cannot endure their restraint and will walk alone in a neighbouring garden.\n\nToo capricious and imperious in all she does to be the cause of much surprise in those about her as to anything she does, this woman, loosely muffled, goes out into the moonlight. Mercury attends with the key. Having opened the garden-gate, he delivers the key into his Lady’s hands at her request and is bidden to go back. She will walk there some time to ease her aching head. She may be an hour, she may be more. She needs no further escort. The gate shuts upon its spring with a clash, and he leaves her passing on into the dark shade of some trees.\n\nA fine night, and a bright large moon, and multitudes of stars. Mr. Tulkinghorn, in repairing to his cellar and in opening and shutting those resounding doors, has to cross a little prison-like yard. He looks up casually, thinking what a fine night, what a bright large moon, what multitudes of stars! A quiet night, too.\n\nA very quiet night. When the moon shines very brilliantly, a solitude and stillness seem to proceed from her that influence even crowded places full of life. Not only is it a still night on dusty high roads and on hill-summits, whence a wide expanse of country may be seen in repose, quieter and quieter as it spreads away into a fringe of trees against the sky with the grey ghost of a bloom upon them; not only is it a still night in gardens and in woods, and on the river where the water-meadows are fresh and green, and the stream sparkles on among pleasant islands, murmuring weirs, and whispering rushes; not only does the stillness attend it as it flows where houses cluster thick, where many bridges are reflected in it, where wharves and shipping make it black and awful, where it winds from these disfigurements through marshes whose grim beacons stand like skeletons washed ashore, where it expands through the bolder region of rising grounds, rich in cornfield wind-mill and steeple, and where it mingles with the ever-heaving sea; not only is it a still night on the deep, and on the shore where the watcher stands to see the ship with her spread wings cross the path of light that appears to be presented to only him; but even on this stranger’s wilderness of London there is some rest. Its steeples and towers and its one great dome grow more ethereal; its smoky house-tops lose their grossness in the pale effulgence; the noises that arise from the streets are fewer and are softened, and the footsteps on the pavements pass more tranquilly away. In these fields of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s inhabiting, where the shepherds play on Chancery pipes that have no stop, and keep their sheep in the fold by hook and by crook until they have shorn them exceeding close, every noise is merged, this moonlight night, into a distant ringing hum, as if the city were a vast glass, vibrating.\n\nWhat’s that? Who fired a gun or pistol? Where was it?\n\nThe few foot-passengers start, stop, and stare about them. Some windows and doors are opened, and people come out to look. It was a loud report and echoed and rattled heavily. It shook one house, or so a man says who was passing. It has aroused all the dogs in the neighbourhood, who bark vehemently. Terrified cats scamper across the road. While the dogs are yet barking and howling—there is one dog howling like a demon—the church-clocks, as if they were startled too, begin to strike. The hum from the streets, likewise, seems to swell into a shout. But it is soon over. Before the last clock begins to strike ten, there is a lull. When it has ceased, the fine night, the bright large moon, and multitudes of stars, are left at peace again.\n\nHas Mr. Tulkinghorn been disturbed? His windows are dark and quiet, and his door is shut. It must be something unusual indeed to bring him out of his shell. Nothing is heard of him, nothing is seen of him. What power of cannon might it take to shake that rusty old man out of his immovable composure?\n\nFor many years the persistent Roman has been pointing, with no particular meaning, from that ceiling. It is not likely that he has any new meaning in him to-night. Once pointing, always pointing—like any Roman, or even Briton, with a single idea. There he is, no doubt, in his impossible attitude, pointing, unavailingly, all night long. Moonlight, darkness, dawn, sunrise, day. There he is still, eagerly pointing, and no one minds him.\n\nBut a little after the coming of the day come people to clean the rooms. And either the Roman has some new meaning in him, not expressed before, or the foremost of them goes wild, for looking up at his outstretched hand and looking down at what is below it, that person shrieks and flies. The others, looking in as the first one looked, shriek and fly too, and there is an alarm in the street.\n\nWhat does it mean? No light is admitted into the darkened chamber, and people unaccustomed to it enter, and treading softly but heavily, carry a weight into the bedroom and lay it down. There is whispering and wondering all day, strict search of every corner, careful tracing of steps, and careful noting of the disposition of every article of furniture. All eyes look up at the Roman, and all voices murmur, “If he could only tell what he saw!”\n\nHe is pointing at a table with a bottle (nearly full of wine) and a glass upon it and two candles that were blown out suddenly soon after being lighted. He is pointing at an empty chair and at a stain upon the ground before it that might be almost covered with a hand. These objects lie directly within his range. An excited imagination might suppose that there was something in them so terrific as to drive the rest of the composition, not only the attendant big-legged boys, but the clouds and flowers and pillars too—in short, the very body and soul of Allegory, and all the brains it has—stark mad. It happens surely that every one who comes into the darkened room and looks at these things looks up at the Roman and that he is invested in all eyes with mystery and awe, as if he were a paralysed dumb witness.\n\nSo it shall happen surely, through many years to come, that ghostly stories shall be told of the stain upon the floor, so easy to be covered, so hard to be got out, and that the Roman, pointing from the ceiling shall point, so long as dust and damp and spiders spare him, with far greater significance than he ever had in Mr. Tulkinghorn’s time, and with a deadly meaning. For Mr. Tulkinghorn’s time is over for evermore, and the Roman pointed at the murderous hand uplifted against his life, and pointed helplessly at him, from night to morning, lying face downward on the floor, shot through the heart.\nDutiful Friendship\n\nA great annual occasion has come round in the establishment of Mr. Matthew Bagnet, otherwise Lignum Vitae, ex-artilleryman and present bassoon-player. An occasion of feasting and festival. The celebration of a birthday in the family.\n\nIt is not Mr. Bagnet’s birthday. Mr. Bagnet merely distinguishes that epoch in the musical instrument business by kissing the children with an extra smack before breakfast, smoking an additional pipe after dinner, and wondering towards evening what his poor old mother is thinking about it—a subject of infinite speculation, and rendered so by his mother having departed this life twenty years. Some men rarely revert to their father, but seem, in the bank-books of their remembrance, to have transferred all the stock of filial affection into their mother’s name. Mr. Bagnet is one of these. Perhaps his exalted appreciation of the merits of the old girl causes him usually to make the noun-substantive “goodness” of the feminine gender.\n\nIt is not the birthday of one of the three children. Those occasions are kept with some marks of distinction, but they rarely overleap the bounds of happy returns and a pudding. On young Woolwich’s last birthday, Mr. Bagnet certainly did, after observing on his growth and general advancement, proceed, in a moment of profound reflection on the changes wrought by time, to examine him in the catechism, accomplishing with extreme accuracy the questions number one and two, “What is your name?” and “Who gave you that name?” but there failing in the exact precision of his memory and substituting for number three the question “And how do you like that name?” which he propounded with a sense of its importance, in itself so edifying and improving as to give it quite an orthodox air. This, however, was a speciality on that particular birthday, and not a general solemnity.\n\nIt is the old girl’s birthday, and that is the greatest holiday and reddest-letter day in Mr. Bagnet’s calendar. The auspicious event is always commemorated according to certain forms settled and prescribed by Mr. Bagnet some years since. Mr. Bagnet, being deeply convinced that to have a pair of fowls for dinner is to attain the highest pitch of imperial luxury, invariably goes forth himself very early in the morning of this day to buy a pair; he is, as invariably, taken in by the vendor and installed in the possession of the oldest inhabitants of any coop in Europe. Returning with these triumphs of toughness tied up in a clean blue and white cotton handkerchief (essential to the arrangements), he in a casual manner invites Mrs. Bagnet to declare at breakfast what she would like for dinner. Mrs. Bagnet, by a coincidence never known to fail, replying fowls, Mr. Bagnet instantly produces his bundle from a place of concealment amidst general amazement and rejoicing. He further requires that the old girl shall do nothing all day long but sit in her very best gown and be served by himself and the young people. As he is not illustrious for his cookery, this may be supposed to be a matter of state rather than enjoyment on the old girl’s part, but she keeps her state with all imaginable cheerfulness.\n\nOn this present birthday, Mr. Bagnet has accomplished the usual preliminaries. He has bought two specimens of poultry, which, if there be any truth in adages, were certainly not caught with chaff, to be prepared for the spit; he has amazed and rejoiced the family by their unlooked-for production; he is himself directing the roasting of the poultry; and Mrs. Bagnet, with her wholesome brown fingers itching to prevent what she sees going wrong, sits in her gown of ceremony, an honoured guest.\n\nQuebec and Malta lay the cloth for dinner, while Woolwich, serving, as beseems him, under his father, keeps the fowls revolving. To these young scullions Mrs. Bagnet occasionally imparts a wink, or a shake of the head, or a crooked face, as they made mistakes.\n\n“At half after one.” Says Mr. Bagnet. “To the minute. They’ll be done.”\n\nMrs. Bagnet, with anguish, beholds one of them at a standstill before the fire and beginning to burn.\n\n“You shall have a dinner, old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet. “Fit for a queen.”\n\nMrs. Bagnet shows her white teeth cheerfully, but to the perception of her son, betrays so much uneasiness of spirit that he is impelled by the dictates of affection to ask her, with his eyes, what is the matter, thus standing, with his eyes wide open, more oblivious of the fowls than before, and not affording the least hope of a return to consciousness. Fortunately his elder sister perceives the cause of the agitation in Mrs. Bagnet’s breast and with an admonitory poke recalls him. The stopped fowls going round again, Mrs. Bagnet closes her eyes in the intensity of her relief.\n\n“George will look us up,” says Mr. Bagnet. “At half after four. To the moment. How many years, old girl. Has George looked us up. This afternoon?”\n\n“Ah, Lignum, Lignum, as many as make an old woman of a young one, I begin to think. Just about that, and no less,” returns Mrs. Bagnet, laughing and shaking her head.\n\n“Old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet, “never mind. You’d be as young as ever you was. If you wasn’t younger. Which you are. As everybody knows.”\n\nQuebec and Malta here exclaim, with clapping of hands, that Bluffy is sure to bring mother something, and begin to speculate on what it will be.\n\n“Do you know, Lignum,” says Mrs. Bagnet, casting a glance on the table-cloth, and winking “salt!” at Malta with her right eye, and shaking the pepper away from Quebec with her head, “I begin to think George is in the roving way again.”\n\n“George,” returns Mr. Bagnet, “will never desert. And leave his old comrade. In the lurch. Don’t be afraid of it.”\n\n“No, Lignum. No. I don’t say he will. I don’t think he will. But if he could get over this money trouble of his, I believe he would be off.”\n\nMr. Bagnet asks why.\n\n“Well,” returns his wife, considering, “George seems to me to be getting not a little impatient and restless. I don’t say but what he’s as free as ever. Of course he must be free or he wouldn’t be George, but he smarts and seems put out.”\n\n“He’s extra-drilled,” says Mr. Bagnet. “By a lawyer. Who would put the devil out.”\n\n“There’s something in that,” his wife assents; “but so it is, Lignum.”\n\nFurther conversation is prevented, for the time, by the necessity under which Mr. Bagnet finds himself of directing the whole force of his mind to the dinner, which is a little endangered by the dry humour of the fowls in not yielding any gravy, and also by the made gravy acquiring no flavour and turning out of a flaxen complexion. With a similar perverseness, the potatoes crumble off forks in the process of peeling, upheaving from their centres in every direction, as if they were subject to earthquakes. The legs of the fowls, too, are longer than could be desired, and extremely scaly. Overcoming these disadvantages to the best of his ability, Mr. Bagnet at last dishes and they sit down at table, Mrs. Bagnet occupying the guest’s place at his right hand.\n\nIt is well for the old girl that she has but one birthday in a year, for two such indulgences in poultry might be injurious. Every kind of finer tendon and ligament that is in the nature of poultry to possess is developed in these specimens in the singular form of guitar-strings. Their limbs appear to have struck roots into their breasts and bodies, as aged trees strike roots into the earth. Their legs are so hard as to encourage the idea that they must have devoted the greater part of their long and arduous lives to pedestrian exercises and the walking of matches. But Mr. Bagnet, unconscious of these little defects, sets his heart on Mrs. Bagnet eating a most severe quantity of the delicacies before her; and as that good old girl would not cause him a moment’s disappointment on any day, least of all on such a day, for any consideration, she imperils her digestion fearfully. How young Woolwich cleans the drum-sticks without being of ostrich descent, his anxious mother is at a loss to understand.\n\nThe old girl has another trial to undergo after the conclusion of the repast in sitting in state to see the room cleared, the hearth swept, and the dinner-service washed up and polished in the backyard. The great delight and energy with which the two young ladies apply themselves to these duties, turning up their skirts in imitation of their mother and skating in and out on little scaffolds of pattens, inspire the highest hopes for the future, but some anxiety for the present. The same causes lead to confusion of tongues, a clattering of crockery, a rattling of tin mugs, a whisking of brooms, and an expenditure of water, all in excess, while the saturation of the young ladies themselves is almost too moving a spectacle for Mrs. Bagnet to look upon with the calmness proper to her position. At last the various cleansing processes are triumphantly completed; Quebec and Malta appear in fresh attire, smiling and dry; pipes, tobacco, and something to drink are placed upon the table; and the old girl enjoys the first peace of mind she ever knows on the day of this delightful entertainment.\n\nWhen Mr. Bagnet takes his usual seat, the hands of the clock are very near to half-past four; as they mark it accurately, Mr. Bagnet announces, “George! Military time.”\n\nIt is George, and he has hearty congratulations for the old girl (whom he kisses on the great occasion), and for the children, and for Mr. Bagnet. “Happy returns to all!” says Mr. George.\n\n“But, George, old man!” cries Mrs. Bagnet, looking at him curiously. “What’s come to you?”\n\n“Come to me?”\n\n“Ah! You are so white, George—for you—and look so shocked. Now don’t he, Lignum?”\n\n“George,” says Mr. Bagnet, “tell the old girl. What’s the matter.”\n\n“I didn’t know I looked white,” says the trooper, passing his hand over his brow, “and I didn’t know I looked shocked, and I’m sorry I do. But the truth is, that boy who was taken in at my place died yesterday afternoon, and it has rather knocked me over.”\n\n“Poor creetur!” says Mrs. Bagnet with a mother’s pity. “Is he gone? Dear, dear!”\n\n“I didn’t mean to say anything about it, for it’s not birthday talk, but you have got it out of me, you see, before I sit down. I should have roused up in a minute,” says the trooper, making himself speak more gaily, “but you’re so quick, Mrs. Bagnet.”\n\n“You’re right. The old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet. “Is as quick. As powder.”\n\n“And what’s more, she’s the subject of the day, and we’ll stick to her,” cries Mr. George. “See here, I have brought a little brooch along with me. It’s a poor thing, you know, but it’s a keepsake. That’s all the good it is, Mrs. Bagnet.”\n\nMr. George produces his present, which is greeted with admiring leapings and clappings by the young family, and with a species of reverential admiration by Mr. Bagnet. “Old girl,” says Mr. Bagnet. “Tell him my opinion of it.”\n\n“Why, it’s a wonder, George!” Mrs. Bagnet exclaims. “It’s the beautifullest thing that ever was seen!”\n\n“Good!” says Mr. Bagnet. “My opinion.”\n\n“It’s so pretty, George,” cries Mrs. Bagnet, turning it on all sides and holding it out at arm’s length, “that it seems too choice for me.”\n\n“Bad!” says Mr. Bagnet. “Not my opinion.”\n\n“But whatever it is, a hundred thousand thanks, old fellow,” says Mrs. Bagnet, her eyes sparkling with pleasure and her hand stretched out to him; “and though I have been a crossgrained soldier’s wife to you sometimes, George, we are as strong friends, I am sure, in reality, as ever can be. Now you shall fasten it on yourself, for good luck, if you will, George.”\n\nThe children close up to see it done, and Mr. Bagnet looks over young Woolwich’s head to see it done with an interest so maturely wooden, yet pleasantly childish, that Mrs. Bagnet cannot help laughing in her airy way and saying, “Oh, Lignum, Lignum, what a precious old chap you are!” But the trooper fails to fasten the brooch. His hand shakes, he is nervous, and it falls off. “Would any one believe this?” says he, catching it as it drops and looking round. “I am so out of sorts that I bungle at an easy job like this!”\n\nMrs. Bagnet concludes that for such a case there is no remedy like a pipe, and fastening the brooch herself in a twinkling, causes the trooper to be inducted into his usual snug place and the pipes to be got into action. “If that don’t bring you round, George,” says she, “just throw your eye across here at your present now and then, and the two together MUST do it.”\n\n“You ought to do it of yourself,” George answers; “I know that very well, Mrs. Bagnet. I’ll tell you how, one way and another, the blues have got to be too many for me. Here was this poor lad. ’Twas dull work to see him dying as he did, and not be able to help him.”\n\n“What do you mean, George? You did help him. You took him under your roof.”\n\n“I helped him so far, but that’s little. I mean, Mrs. Bagnet, there he was, dying without ever having been taught much more than to know his right hand from his left. And he was too far gone to be helped out of that.”\n\n“Ah, poor creetur!” says Mrs. Bagnet.\n\n“Then,” says the trooper, not yet lighting his pipe, and passing his heavy hand over his hair, “that brought up Gridley in a man’s mind. His was a bad case too, in a different way. Then the two got mixed up in a man’s mind with a flinty old rascal who had to do with both. And to think of that rusty carbine, stock and barrel, standing up on end in his corner, hard, indifferent, taking everything so evenly—it made flesh and blood tingle, I do assure you.”\n\n“My advice to you,” returns Mrs. Bagnet, “is to light your pipe and tingle that way. It’s wholesomer and comfortabler, and better for the health altogether.”\n\n“You’re right,” says the trooper, “and I’ll do it.”\n\nSo he does it, though still with an indignant gravity that impresses the young Bagnets, and even causes Mr. Bagnet to defer the ceremony of drinking Mrs. Bagnet’s health, always given by himself on these occasions in a speech of exemplary terseness. But the young ladies having composed what Mr. Bagnet is in the habit of calling “the mixtur,” and George’s pipe being now in a glow, Mr. Bagnet considers it his duty to proceed to the toast of the evening. He addresses the assembled company in the following terms.\n\n“George. Woolwich. Quebec. Malta. This is her birthday. Take a day’s march. And you won’t find such another. Here’s towards her!”\n\nThe toast having been drunk with enthusiasm, Mrs. Bagnet returns thanks in a neat address of corresponding brevity. This model composition is limited to the three words “And wishing yours!” which the old girl follows up with a nod at everybody in succession and a well-regulated swig of the mixture. This she again follows up, on the present occasion, by the wholly unexpected exclamation, “Here’s a man!”\n\nHere IS a man, much to the astonishment of the little company, looking in at the parlour-door. He is a sharp-eyed man—a quick keen man—and he takes in everybody’s look at him, all at once, individually and collectively, in a manner that stamps him a remarkable man.\n\n“George,” says the man, nodding, “how do you find yourself?”\n\n“Why, it’s Bucket!” cries Mr. George.\n\n“Yes,” says the man, coming in and closing the door. “I was going down the street here when I happened to stop and look in at the musical instruments in the shop-window—a friend of mine is in want of a second-hand wiolinceller of a good tone—and I saw a party enjoying themselves, and I thought it was you in the corner; I thought I couldn’t be mistaken. How goes the world with you, George, at the present moment? Pretty smooth? And with you, ma’am? And with you, governor? And Lord,” says Mr. Bucket, opening his arms, “here’s children too! You may do anything with me if you only show me children. Give us a kiss, my pets. No occasion to inquire who YOUR father and mother is. Never saw such a likeness in my life!”\n\nMr. Bucket, not unwelcome, has sat himself down next to Mr. George and taken Quebec and Malta on his knees. “You pretty dears,” says Mr. Bucket, “give us another kiss; it’s the only thing I’m greedy in. Lord bless you, how healthy you look! And what may be the ages of these two, ma’am? I should put ’em down at the figures of about eight and ten.”\n\n“You’re very near, sir,” says Mrs. Bagnet.\n\n“I generally am near,” returns Mr. Bucket, “being so fond of children. A friend of mine has had nineteen of ’em, ma’am, all by one mother, and she’s still as fresh and rosy as the morning. Not so much so as yourself, but, upon my soul, she comes near you! And what do you call these, my darling?” pursues Mr. Bucket, pinching Malta’s cheeks. “These are peaches, these are. Bless your heart! And what do you think about father? Do you think father could recommend a second-hand wiolinceller of a good tone for Mr. Bucket’s friend, my dear? My name’s Bucket. Ain’t that a funny name?”\n\nThese blandishments have entirely won the family heart. Mrs. Bagnet forgets the day to the extent of filling a pipe and a glass for Mr. Bucket and waiting upon him hospitably. She would be glad to receive so pleasant a character under any circumstances, but she tells him that as a friend of George’s she is particularly glad to see him this evening, for George has not been in his usual spirits.\n\n“Not in his usual spirits?” exclaims Mr. Bucket. “Why, I never heard of such a thing! What’s the matter, George? You don’t intend to tell me you’ve been out of spirits. What should you be out of spirits for? You haven’t got anything on your mind, you know.”\n\n“Nothing particular,” returns the trooper.\n\n“I should think not,” rejoins Mr. Bucket. “What could you have on your mind, you know! And have these pets got anything on THEIR minds, eh? Not they, but they’ll be upon the minds of some of the young fellows, some of these days, and make ’em precious low-spirited. I ain’t much of a prophet, but I can tell you that, ma’am.”\n\nMrs. Bagnet, quite charmed, hopes Mr. Bucket has a family of his own.\n\n“There, ma’am!” says Mr. Bucket. “Would you believe it? No, I haven’t. My wife and a lodger constitute my family. Mrs. Bucket is as fond of children as myself and as wishful to have ’em, but no. So it is. Worldly goods are divided unequally, and man must not repine. What a very nice backyard, ma’am! Any way out of that yard, now?”\n\nThere is no way out of that yard.\n\n“Ain’t there really?” says Mr. Bucket. “I should have thought there might have been. Well, I don’t know as I ever saw a backyard that took my fancy more. Would you allow me to look at it? Thank you. No, I see there’s no way out. But what a very good-proportioned yard it is!”\n\nHaving cast his sharp eye all about it, Mr. Bucket returns to his chair next his friend Mr. George and pats Mr. George affectionately on the shoulder.\n\n“How are your spirits now, George?”\n\n“All right now,” returns the trooper.\n\n“That’s your sort!” says Mr. Bucket. “Why should you ever have been otherwise? A man of your fine figure and constitution has no right to be out of spirits. That ain’t a chest to be out of spirits, is it, ma’am? And you haven’t got anything on your mind, you know, George; what could you have on your mind!”\n\nSomewhat harping on this phrase, considering the extent and variety of his conversational powers, Mr. Bucket twice or thrice repeats it to the pipe he lights, and with a listening face that is particularly his own. But the sun of his sociality soon recovers from this brief eclipse and shines again.\n\n“And this is brother, is it, my dears?” says Mr. Bucket, referring to Quebec and Malta for information on the subject of young Woolwich. “And a nice brother he is—half-brother I mean to say. For he’s too old to be your boy, ma’am.”\n\n“I can certify at all events that he is not anybody else’s,” returns Mrs. Bagnet, laughing.\n\n“Well, you do surprise me! Yet he’s like you, there’s no denying. Lord, he’s wonderfully like you! But about what you may call the brow, you know, THERE his father comes out!” Mr. Bucket compares the faces with one eye shut up, while Mr. Bagnet smokes in stolid satisfaction.\n\nThis is an opportunity for Mrs. Bagnet to inform him that the boy is George’s godson.\n\n“George’s godson, is he?” rejoins Mr. Bucket with extreme cordiality. “I must shake hands over again with George’s godson. Godfather and godson do credit to one another. And what do you intend to make of him, ma’am? Does he show any turn for any musical instrument?”\n\nMr. Bagnet suddenly interposes, “Plays the fife. Beautiful.”\n\n“Would you believe it, governor,” says Mr. Bucket, struck by the coincidence, “that when I was a boy I played the fife myself? Not in a scientific way, as I expect he does, but by ear. Lord bless you! ‘British Grenadiers’—there’s a tune to warm an Englishman up! COULD you give us ‘British Grenadiers,’ my fine fellow?”\n\nNothing could be more acceptable to the little circle than this call upon young Woolwich, who immediately fetches his fife and performs the stirring melody, during which performance Mr. Bucket, much enlivened, beats time and never fails to come in sharp with the burden, “British Gra-a-anadeers!” In short, he shows so much musical taste that Mr. Bagnet actually takes his pipe from his lips to express his conviction that he is a singer. Mr. Bucket receives the harmonious impeachment so modestly, confessing how that he did once chaunt a little, for the expression of the feelings of his own bosom, and with no presumptuous idea of entertaining his friends, that he is asked to sing. Not to be behindhand in the sociality of the evening, he complies and gives them “Believe Me, if All Those Endearing Young Charms.” This ballad, he informs Mrs. Bagnet, he considers to have been his most powerful ally in moving the heart of Mrs. Bucket when a maiden, and inducing her to approach the altar—Mr. Bucket’s own words are “to come up to the scratch.”\n\nThis sparkling stranger is such a new and agreeable feature in the evening that Mr. George, who testified no great emotions of pleasure on his entrance, begins, in spite of himself, to be rather proud of him. He is so friendly, is a man of so many resources, and so easy to get on with, that it is something to have made him known there. Mr. Bagnet becomes, after another pipe, so sensible of the value of his acquaintance that he solicits the honour of his company on the old girl’s next birthday. If anything can more closely cement and consolidate the esteem which Mr. Bucket has formed for the family, it is the discovery of the nature of the occasion. He drinks to Mrs. Bagnet with a warmth approaching to rapture, engages himself for that day twelvemonth more than thankfully, makes a memorandum of the day in a large black pocket-book with a girdle to it, and breathes a hope that Mrs. Bucket and Mrs. Bagnet may before then become, in a manner, sisters. As he says himself, what is public life without private ties? He is in his humble way a public man, but it is not in that sphere that he finds happiness. No, it must be sought within the confines of domestic bliss.\n\nIt is natural, under these circumstances, that he, in his turn, should remember the friend to whom he is indebted for so promising an acquaintance. And he does. He keeps very close to him. Whatever the subject of the conversation, he keeps a tender eye upon him. He waits to walk home with him. He is interested in his very boots and observes even them attentively as Mr. George sits smoking cross-legged in the chimney-corner.\n\nAt length Mr. George rises to depart. At the same moment Mr. Bucket, with the secret sympathy of friendship, also rises. He dotes upon the children to the last and remembers the commission he has undertaken for an absent friend.\n\n“Respecting that second-hand wiolinceller, governor—could you recommend me such a thing?”\n\n“Scores,” says Mr. Bagnet.\n\n“I am obliged to you,” returns Mr. Bucket, squeezing his hand. “You’re a friend in need. A good tone, mind you! My friend is a regular dab at it. Ecod, he saws away at Mozart and Handel and the rest of the big-wigs like a thorough workman. And you needn’t,” says Mr. Bucket in a considerate and private voice, “you needn’t commit yourself to too low a figure, governor. I don’t want to pay too large a price for my friend, but I want you to have your proper percentage and be remunerated for your loss of time. That is but fair. Every man must live, and ought to it.”\n\nMr. Bagnet shakes his head at the old girl to the effect that they have found a jewel of price.\n\n“Suppose I was to give you a look in, say, at half arter ten to-morrow morning. Perhaps you could name the figures of a few wiolincellers of a good tone?” says Mr. Bucket.\n\nNothing easier. Mr. and Mrs. Bagnet both engage to have the requisite information ready and even hint to each other at the practicability of having a small stock collected there for approval.\n\n“Thank you,” says Mr. Bucket, “thank you. Good night, ma’am. Good night, governor. Good night, darlings. I am much obliged to you for one of the pleasantest evenings I ever spent in my life.”\n\nThey, on the contrary, are much obliged to him for the pleasure he has given them in his company; and so they part with many expressions of goodwill on both sides. “Now George, old boy,” says Mr. Bucket, taking his arm at the shop-door, “come along!” As they go down the little street and the Bagnets pause for a minute looking after them, Mrs. Bagnet remarks to the worthy Lignum that Mr. Bucket “almost clings to George like, and seems to be really fond of him.”\n\nThe neighbouring streets being narrow and ill-paved, it is a little inconvenient to walk there two abreast and arm in arm. Mr. George therefore soon proposes to walk singly. But Mr. Bucket, who cannot make up his mind to relinquish his friendly hold, replies, “Wait half a minute, George. I should wish to speak to you first.” Immediately afterwards, he twists him into a public-house and into a parlour, where he confronts him and claps his own back against the door.\n\n“Now, George,” says Mr. Bucket, “duty is duty, and friendship is friendship. I never want the two to clash if I can help it. I have endeavoured to make things pleasant to-night, and I put it to you whether I have done it or not. You must consider yourself in custody, George.”\n\n“Custody? What for?” returns the trooper, thunderstruck.\n\n“Now, George,” says Mr. Bucket, urging a sensible view of the case upon him with his fat forefinger, “duty, as you know very well, is one thing, and conversation is another. It’s my duty to inform you that any observations you may make will be liable to be used against you. Therefore, George, be careful what you say. You don’t happen to have heard of a murder?”\n\n“Murder!”\n\n“Now, George,” says Mr. Bucket, keeping his forefinger in an impressive state of action, “bear in mind what I’ve said to you. I ask you nothing. You’ve been in low spirits this afternoon. I say, you don’t happen to have heard of a murder?”\n\n“No. Where has there been a murder?”\n\n“Now, George,” says Mr. Bucket, “don’t you go and commit yourself. I’m a-going to tell you what I want you for. There has been a murder in Lincoln’s Inn Fields—gentleman of the name of Tulkinghorn. He was shot last night. I want you for that.”\n\nThe trooper sinks upon a seat behind him, and great drops start out upon his forehead, and a deadly pallor overspreads his face.\n\n“Bucket! It’s not possible that Mr. Tulkinghorn has been killed and that you suspect ME?”\n\n“George,” returns Mr. Bucket, keeping his forefinger going, “it is certainly possible, because it’s the case. This deed was done last night at ten o’clock. Now, you know where you were last night at ten o’clock, and you’ll be able to prove it, no doubt.”\n\n“Last night! Last night?” repeats the trooper thoughtfully. Then it flashes upon him. “Why, great heaven, I was there last night!”\n\n“So I have understood, George,” returns Mr. Bucket with great deliberation. “So I have understood. Likewise you’ve been very often there. You’ve been seen hanging about the place, and you’ve been heard more than once in a wrangle with him, and it’s possible—I don’t say it’s certainly so, mind you, but it’s possible—that he may have been heard to call you a threatening, murdering, dangerous fellow.”\n\nThe trooper gasps as if he would admit it all if he could speak.\n\n“Now, George,” continues Mr. Bucket, putting his hat upon the table with an air of business rather in the upholstery way than otherwise, “my wish is, as it has been all the evening, to make things pleasant. I tell you plainly there’s a reward out, of a hundred guineas, offered by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. You and me have always been pleasant together; but I have got a duty to discharge; and if that hundred guineas is to be made, it may as well be made by me as any other man. On all of which accounts, I should hope it was clear to you that I must have you, and that I’m damned if I don’t have you. Am I to call in any assistance, or is the trick done?”\n\nMr. George has recovered himself and stands up like a soldier. “Come,” he says; “I am ready.”\n\n“George,” continues Mr. Bucket, “wait a bit!” With his upholsterer manner, as if the trooper were a window to be fitted up, he takes from his pocket a pair of handcuffs. “This is a serious charge, George, and such is my duty.”\n\nThe trooper flushes angrily and hesitates a moment, but holds out his two hands, clasped together, and says, “There! Put them on!”\n\nMr. Bucket adjusts them in a moment. “How do you find them? Are they comfortable? If not, say so, for I wish to make things as pleasant as is consistent with my duty, and I’ve got another pair in my pocket.” This remark he offers like a most respectable tradesman anxious to execute an order neatly and to the perfect satisfaction of his customer. “They’ll do as they are? Very well! Now, you see, George”—he takes a cloak from a corner and begins adjusting it about the trooper’s neck—“I was mindful of your feelings when I come out, and brought this on purpose. There! Who’s the wiser?”\n\n“Only I,” returns the trooper, “but as I know it, do me one more good turn and pull my hat over my eyes.”\n\n“Really, though! Do you mean it? Ain’t it a pity? It looks so.”\n\n“I can’t look chance men in the face with these things on,” Mr. George hurriedly replies. “Do, for God’s sake, pull my hat forward.”\n\nSo strongly entreated, Mr. Bucket complies, puts his own hat on, and conducts his prize into the streets, the trooper marching on as steadily as usual, though with his head less erect, and Mr. Bucket steering him with his elbow over the crossings and up the turnings.\n\nEsther’s Narrative\nIt happened that when I came home from Deal I found a note from Caddy Jellyby (as we always continued to call her), informing me that her health, which had been for some time very delicate, was worse and that she would be more glad than she could tell me if I would go to see her. It was a note of a few lines, written from the couch on which she lay and enclosed to me in another from her husband, in which he seconded her entreaty with much solicitude. Caddy was now the mother, and I the godmother, of such a poor little baby—such a tiny old-faced mite, with a countenance that seemed to be scarcely anything but cap-border, and a little lean, long-fingered hand, always clenched under its chin. It would lie in this attitude all day, with its bright specks of eyes open, wondering (as I used to imagine) how it came to be so small and weak. Whenever it was moved it cried, but at all other times it was so patient that the sole desire of its life appeared to be to lie quiet and think. It had curious little dark veins in its face and curious little dark marks under its eyes like faint remembrances of poor Caddy’s inky days, and altogether, to those who were not used to it, it was quite a piteous little sight.\n\nBut it was enough for Caddy that SHE was used to it. The projects with which she beguiled her illness, for little Esther’s education, and little Esther’s marriage, and even for her own old age as the grandmother of little Esther’s little Esthers, was so prettily expressive of devotion to this pride of her life that I should be tempted to recall some of them but for the timely remembrance that I am getting on irregularly as it is.\n\nTo return to the letter. Caddy had a superstition about me which had been strengthening in her mind ever since that night long ago when she had lain asleep with her head in my lap. She almost—I think I must say quite—believed that I did her good whenever I was near her. Now although this was such a fancy of the affectionate girl’s that I am almost ashamed to mention it, still it might have all the force of a fact when she was really ill. Therefore I set off to Caddy, with my guardian’s consent, post-haste; and she and Prince made so much of me that there never was anything like it.\n\nNext day I went again to sit with her, and next day I went again. It was a very easy journey, for I had only to rise a little earlier in the morning, and keep my accounts, and attend to housekeeping matters before leaving home.\n\nBut when I had made these three visits, my guardian said to me, on my return at night, “Now, little woman, little woman, this will never do. Constant dropping will wear away a stone, and constant coaching will wear out a Dame Durden. We will go to London for a while and take possession of our old lodgings.”\n\n“Not for me, dear guardian,” said I, “for I never feel tired,” which was strictly true. I was only too happy to be in such request.\n\n“For me then,” returned my guardian, “or for Ada, or for both of us. It is somebody’s birthday to-morrow, I think.”\n\n“Truly I think it is,” said I, kissing my darling, who would be twenty-one to-morrow.\n\n“Well,” observed my guardian, half pleasantly, half seriously, “that’s a great occasion and will give my fair cousin some necessary business to transact in assertion of her independence, and will make London a more convenient place for all of us. So to London we will go. That being settled, there is another thing—how have you left Caddy?”\n\n“Very unwell, guardian. I fear it will be some time before she regains her health and strength.”\n\n“What do you call some time, now?” asked my guardian thoughtfully.\n\n“Some weeks, I am afraid.”\n\n“Ah!” He began to walk about the room with his hands in his pockets, showing that he had been thinking as much. “Now, what do you say about her doctor? Is he a good doctor, my love?”\n\nI felt obliged to confess that I knew nothing to the contrary but that Prince and I had agreed only that evening that we would like his opinion to be confirmed by some one.\n\n“Well, you know,” returned my guardian quickly, “there’s Woodcourt.”\n\nI had not meant that, and was rather taken by surprise. For a moment all that I had had in my mind in connexion with Mr. Woodcourt seemed to come back and confuse me.\n\n“You don’t object to him, little woman?”\n\n“Object to him, guardian? Oh no!”\n\n“And you don’t think the patient would object to him?”\n\nSo far from that, I had no doubt of her being prepared to have a great reliance on him and to like him very much. I said that he was no stranger to her personally, for she had seen him often in his kind attendance on Miss Flite.\n\n“Very good,” said my guardian. “He has been here to-day, my dear, and I will see him about it to-morrow.”\n\nI felt in this short conversation—though I did not know how, for she was quiet, and we interchanged no look—that my dear girl well remembered how merrily she had clasped me round the waist when no other hands than Caddy’s had brought me the little parting token. This caused me to feel that I ought to tell her, and Caddy too, that I was going to be the mistress of Bleak House and that if I avoided that disclosure any longer I might become less worthy in my own eyes of its master’s love. Therefore, when we went upstairs and had waited listening until the clock struck twelve in order that only I might be the first to wish my darling all good wishes on her birthday and to take her to my heart, I set before her, just as I had set before myself, the goodness and honour of her cousin John and the happy life that was in store for me. If ever my darling were fonder of me at one time than another in all our intercourse, she was surely fondest of me that night. And I was so rejoiced to know it and so comforted by the sense of having done right in casting this last idle reservation away that I was ten times happier than I had been before. I had scarcely thought it a reservation a few hours ago, but now that it was gone I felt as if I understood its nature better.\n\nNext day we went to London. We found our old lodging vacant, and in half an hour were quietly established there, as if we had never gone away. Mr. Woodcourt dined with us to celebrate my darling’s birthday, and we were as pleasant as we could be with the great blank among us that Richard’s absence naturally made on such an occasion. After that day I was for some weeks—eight or nine as I remember—very much with Caddy, and thus it fell out that I saw less of Ada at this time than any other since we had first come together, except the time of my own illness. She often came to Caddy’s, but our function there was to amuse and cheer her, and we did not talk in our usual confidential manner. Whenever I went home at night we were together, but Caddy’s rest was broken by pain, and I often remained to nurse her.\n\nWith her husband and her poor little mite of a baby to love and their home to strive for, what a good creature Caddy was! So self-denying, so uncomplaining, so anxious to get well on their account, so afraid of giving trouble, and so thoughtful of the unassisted labours of her husband and the comforts of old Mr. Turveydrop; I had never known the best of her until now. And it seemed so curious that her pale face and helpless figure should be lying there day after day where dancing was the business of life, where the kit and the apprentices began early every morning in the ball-room, and where the untidy little boy waltzed by himself in the kitchen all the afternoon.\n\nAt Caddy’s request I took the supreme direction of her apartment, trimmed it up, and pushed her, couch and all, into a lighter and more airy and more cheerful corner than she had yet occupied; then, every day, when we were in our neatest array, I used to lay my small small namesake in her arms and sit down to chat or work or read to her. It was at one of the first of these quiet times that I told Caddy about Bleak House.\n\nWe had other visitors besides Ada. First of all we had Prince, who in his hurried intervals of teaching used to come softly in and sit softly down, with a face of loving anxiety for Caddy and the very little child. Whatever Caddy’s condition really was, she never failed to declare to Prince that she was all but well—which I, heaven forgive me, never failed to confirm. This would put Prince in such good spirits that he would sometimes take the kit from his pocket and play a chord or two to astonish the baby, which I never knew it to do in the least degree, for my tiny namesake never noticed it at all.\n\nThen there was Mrs. Jellyby. She would come occasionally, with her usual distraught manner, and sit calmly looking miles beyond her grandchild as if her attention were absorbed by a young Borrioboolan on its native shores. As bright-eyed as ever, as serene, and as untidy, she would say, “Well, Caddy, child, and how do you do to-day?” And then would sit amiably smiling and taking no notice of the reply or would sweetly glide off into a calculation of the number of letters she had lately received and answered or of the coffee-bearing power of Borrioboola-Gha. This she would always do with a serene contempt for our limited sphere of action, not to be disguised.\n\nThen there was old Mr. Turveydrop, who was from morning to night and from night to morning the subject of innumerable precautions. If the baby cried, it was nearly stifled lest the noise should make him uncomfortable. If the fire wanted stirring in the night, it was surreptitiously done lest his rest should be broken. If Caddy required any little comfort that the house contained, she first carefully discussed whether he was likely to require it too. In return for this consideration he would come into the room once a day, all but blessing it—showing a condescension, and a patronage, and a grace of manner in dispensing the light of his high-shouldered presence from which I might have supposed him (if I had not known better) to have been the benefactor of Caddy’s life.\n\n“My Caroline,” he would say, making the nearest approach that he could to bending over her. “Tell me that you are better to-day.”\n\n“Oh, much better, thank you, Mr. Turveydrop,” Caddy would reply.\n\n“Delighted! Enchanted! And our dear Miss Summerson. She is not quite prostrated by fatigue?” Here he would crease up his eyelids and kiss his fingers to me, though I am happy to say he had ceased to be particular in his attentions since I had been so altered.\n\n“Not at all,” I would assure him.\n\n“Charming! We must take care of our dear Caroline, Miss Summerson. We must spare nothing that will restore her. We must nourish her. My dear Caroline”—he would turn to his daughter-in-law with infinite generosity and protection—“want for nothing, my love. Frame a wish and gratify it, my daughter. Everything this house contains, everything my room contains, is at your service, my dear. Do not,” he would sometimes add in a burst of deportment, “even allow my simple requirements to be considered if they should at any time interfere with your own, my Caroline. Your necessities are greater than mine.”\n\nHe had established such a long prescriptive right to this deportment (his son’s inheritance from his mother) that I several times knew both Caddy and her husband to be melted to tears by these affectionate self-sacrifices.\n\n“Nay, my dears,” he would remonstrate; and when I saw Caddy’s thin arm about his fat neck as he said it, I would be melted too, though not by the same process. “Nay, nay! I have promised never to leave ye. Be dutiful and affectionate towards me, and I ask no other return. Now, bless ye! I am going to the Park.”\n\nHe would take the air there presently and get an appetite for his hotel dinner. I hope I do old Mr. Turveydrop no wrong, but I never saw any better traits in him than these I faithfully record, except that he certainly conceived a liking for Peepy and would take the child out walking with great pomp, always on those occasions sending him home before he went to dinner himself, and occasionally with a halfpenny in his pocket. But even this disinterestedness was attended with no inconsiderable cost, to my knowledge, for before Peepy was sufficiently decorated to walk hand in hand with the professor of deportment, he had to be newly dressed, at the expense of Caddy and her husband, from top to toe.\n\nLast of our visitors, there was Mr. Jellyby. Really when he used to come in of an evening, and ask Caddy in his meek voice how she was, and then sit down with his head against the wall, and make no attempt to say anything more, I liked him very much. If he found me bustling about doing any little thing, he sometimes half took his coat off, as if with an intention of helping by a great exertion; but he never got any further. His sole occupation was to sit with his head against the wall, looking hard at the thoughtful baby; and I could not quite divest my mind of a fancy that they understood one another.\n\nI have not counted Mr. Woodcourt among our visitors because he was now Caddy’s regular attendant. She soon began to improve under his care, but he was so gentle, so skilful, so unwearying in the pains he took that it is not to be wondered at, I am sure. I saw a good deal of Mr. Woodcourt during this time, though not so much as might be supposed, for knowing Caddy to be safe in his hands, I often slipped home at about the hours when he was expected. We frequently met, notwithstanding. I was quite reconciled to myself now, but I still felt glad to think that he was sorry for me, and he still WAS sorry for me I believed. He helped Mr. Badger in his professional engagements, which were numerous, and had as yet no settled projects for the future.\n\nIt was when Caddy began to recover that I began to notice a change in my dear girl. I cannot say how it first presented itself to me, because I observed it in many slight particulars which were nothing in themselves and only became something when they were pieced together. But I made it out, by putting them together, that Ada was not so frankly cheerful with me as she used to be. Her tenderness for me was as loving and true as ever; I did not for a moment doubt that; but there was a quiet sorrow about her which she did not confide to me, and in which I traced some hidden regret.\n\nNow, I could not understand this, and I was so anxious for the happiness of my own pet that it caused me some uneasiness and set me thinking often. At length, feeling sure that Ada suppressed this something from me lest it should make me unhappy too, it came into my head that she was a little grieved—for me—by what I had told her about Bleak House.\n\nHow I persuaded myself that this was likely, I don’t know. I had no idea that there was any selfish reference in my doing so. I was not grieved for myself: I was quite contented and quite happy. Still, that Ada might be thinking—for me, though I had abandoned all such thoughts—of what once was, but was now all changed, seemed so easy to believe that I believed it.\n\nWhat could I do to reassure my darling (I considered then) and show her that I had no such feelings? Well! I could only be as brisk and busy as possible, and that I had tried to be all along. However, as Caddy’s illness had certainly interfered, more or less, with my home duties—though I had always been there in the morning to make my guardian’s breakfast, and he had a hundred times laughed and said there must be two little women, for his little woman was never missing—I resolved to be doubly diligent and gay. So I went about the house humming all the tunes I knew, and I sat working and working in a desperate manner, and I talked and talked, morning, noon, and night.\n\nAnd still there was the same shade between me and my darling.\n\n“So, Dame Trot,” observed my guardian, shutting up his book one night when we were all three together, “so Woodcourt has restored Caddy Jellyby to the full enjoyment of life again?”\n\n“Yes,” I said; “and to be repaid by such gratitude as hers is to be made rich, guardian.”\n\n“I wish it was,” he returned, “with all my heart.”\n\nSo did I too, for that matter. I said so.\n\n“Aye! We would make him as rich as a Jew if we knew how. Would we not, little woman?”\n\nI laughed as I worked and replied that I was not sure about that, for it might spoil him, and he might not be so useful, and there might be many who could ill spare him. As Miss Flite, and Caddy herself, and many others.\n\n“True,” said my guardian. “I had forgotten that. But we would agree to make him rich enough to live, I suppose? Rich enough to work with tolerable peace of mind? Rich enough to have his own happy home and his own household gods—and household goddess, too, perhaps?”\n\nThat was quite another thing, I said. We must all agree in that.\n\n“To be sure,” said my guardian. “All of us. I have a great regard for Woodcourt, a high esteem for him; and I have been sounding him delicately about his plans. It is difficult to offer aid to an independent man with that just kind of pride which he possesses. And yet I would be glad to do it if I might or if I knew how. He seems half inclined for another voyage. But that appears like casting such a man away.”\n\n“It might open a new world to him,” said I.\n\n“So it might, little woman,” my guardian assented. “I doubt if he expects much of the old world. Do you know I have fancied that he sometimes feels some particular disappointment or misfortune encountered in it. You never heard of anything of that sort?”\n\nI shook my head.\n\n“Humph,” said my guardian. “I am mistaken, I dare say.” As there was a little pause here, which I thought, for my dear girl’s satisfaction, had better be filled up, I hummed an air as I worked which was a favourite with my guardian.\n\n“And do you think Mr. Woodcourt will make another voyage?” I asked him when I had hummed it quietly all through.\n\n“I don’t quite know what to think, my dear, but I should say it was likely at present that he will give a long trip to another country.”\n\n“I am sure he will take the best wishes of all our hearts with him wherever he goes,” said I; “and though they are not riches, he will never be the poorer for them, guardian, at least.”\n\n“Never, little woman,” he replied.\n\nI was sitting in my usual place, which was now beside my guardian’s chair. That had not been my usual place before the letter, but it was now. I looked up to Ada, who was sitting opposite, and I saw, as she looked at me, that her eyes were filled with tears and that tears were falling down her face. I felt that I had only to be placid and merry once for all to undeceive my dear and set her loving heart at rest. I really was so, and I had nothing to do but to be myself.\n\nSo I made my sweet girl lean upon my shoulder—how little thinking what was heavy on her mind!—and I said she was not quite well, and put my arm about her, and took her upstairs. When we were in our own room, and when she might perhaps have told me what I was so unprepared to hear, I gave her no encouragement to confide in me; I never thought she stood in need of it.\n\n“Oh, my dear good Esther,” said Ada, “if I could only make up my mind to speak to you and my cousin John when you are together!”\n\n“Why, my love!” I remonstrated. “Ada, why should you not speak to us!”\n\nAda only dropped her head and pressed me closer to her heart.\n\n“You surely don’t forget, my beauty,” said I, smiling, “what quiet, old-fashioned people we are and how I have settled down to be the discreetest of dames? You don’t forget how happily and peacefully my life is all marked out for me, and by whom? I am certain that you don’t forget by what a noble character, Ada. That can never be.”\n\n“No, never, Esther.”\n\n“Why then, my dear,” said I, “there can be nothing amiss—and why should you not speak to us?”\n\n“Nothing amiss, Esther?” returned Ada. “Oh, when I think of all these years, and of his fatherly care and kindness, and of the old relations among us, and of you, what shall I do, what shall I do!”\n\nI looked at my child in some wonder, but I thought it better not to answer otherwise than by cheering her, and so I turned off into many little recollections of our life together and prevented her from saying more. When she lay down to sleep, and not before, I returned to my guardian to say good night, and then I came back to Ada and sat near her for a little while.\n\nShe was asleep, and I thought as I looked at her that she was a little changed. I had thought so more than once lately. I could not decide, even looking at her while she was unconscious, how she was changed, but something in the familiar beauty of her face looked different to me. My guardian’s old hopes of her and Richard arose sorrowfully in my mind, and I said to myself, “She has been anxious about him,” and I wondered how that love would end.\n\nWhen I had come home from Caddy’s while she was ill, I had often found Ada at work, and she had always put her work away, and I had never known what it was. Some of it now lay in a drawer near her, which was not quite closed. I did not open the drawer, but I still rather wondered what the work could be, for it was evidently nothing for herself.\n\nAnd I noticed as I kissed my dear that she lay with one hand under her pillow so that it was hidden.\n\nHow much less amiable I must have been than they thought me, how much less amiable than I thought myself, to be so preoccupied with my own cheerfulness and contentment as to think that it only rested with me to put my dear girl right and set her mind at peace!\n\nBut I lay down, self-deceived, in that belief. And I awoke in it next day to find that there was still the same shade between me and my darling.\nEnlightened\n\nWhen Mr. Woodcourt arrived in London, he went, that very same day, to Mr. Vholes’s in Symond’s Inn. For he never once, from the moment when I entreated him to be a friend to Richard, neglected or forgot his promise. He had told me that he accepted the charge as a sacred trust, and he was ever true to it in that spirit.\n\nHe found Mr. Vholes in his office and informed Mr. Vholes of his agreement with Richard that he should call there to learn his address.\n\n“Just so, sir,” said Mr. Vholes. “Mr. C.’s address is not a hundred miles from here, sir, Mr. C.’s address is not a hundred miles from here. Would you take a seat, sir?”\n\nMr. Woodcourt thanked Mr. Vholes, but he had no business with him beyond what he had mentioned.\n\n“Just so, sir. I believe, sir,” said Mr. Vholes, still quietly insisting on the seat by not giving the address, “that you have influence with Mr. C. Indeed I am aware that you have.”\n\n“I was not aware of it myself,” returned Mr. Woodcourt; “but I suppose you know best.”\n\n“Sir,” rejoined Mr. Vholes, self-contained as usual, voice and all, “it is a part of my professional duty to know best. It is a part of my professional duty to study and to understand a gentleman who confides his interests to me. In my professional duty I shall not be wanting, sir, if I know it. I may, with the best intentions, be wanting in it without knowing it; but not if I know it, sir.”\n\nMr. Woodcourt again mentioned the address.\n\n“Give me leave, sir,” said Mr. Vholes. “Bear with me for a moment. Sir, Mr. C. is playing for a considerable stake, and cannot play without—need I say what?”\n\n“Money, I presume?”\n\n“Sir,” said Mr. Vholes, “to be honest with you (honesty being my golden rule, whether I gain by it or lose, and I find that I generally lose), money is the word. Now, sir, upon the chances of Mr. C.’s game I express to you no opinion, NO opinion. It might be highly impolitic in Mr. C., after playing so long and so high, to leave off; it might be the reverse; I say nothing. No, sir,” said Mr. Vholes, bringing his hand flat down upon his desk in a positive manner, “nothing.”\n\n“You seem to forget,” returned Mr. Woodcourt, “that I ask you to say nothing and have no interest in anything you say.”\n\n“Pardon me, sir!” retorted Mr. Vholes. “You do yourself an injustice. No, sir! Pardon me! You shall not—shall not in my office, if I know it—do yourself an injustice. You are interested in anything, and in everything, that relates to your friend. I know human nature much better, sir, than to admit for an instant that a gentleman of your appearance is not interested in whatever concerns his friend.”\n\n“Well,” replied Mr. Woodcourt, “that may be. I am particularly interested in his address.”\n\n“The number, sir,” said Mr. Vholes parenthetically, “I believe I have already mentioned. If Mr. C. is to continue to play for this considerable stake, sir, he must have funds. Understand me! There are funds in hand at present. I ask for nothing; there are funds in hand. But for the onward play, more funds must be provided, unless Mr. C. is to throw away what he has already ventured, which is wholly and solely a point for his consideration. This, sir, I take the opportunity of stating openly to you as the friend of Mr. C. Without funds I shall always be happy to appear and act for Mr. C. to the extent of all such costs as are safe to be allowed out of the estate, not beyond that. I could not go beyond that, sir, without wronging some one. I must either wrong my three dear girls or my venerable father, who is entirely dependent on me, in the Vale of Taunton; or some one. Whereas, sir, my resolution is (call it weakness or folly if you please) to wrong no one.”\n\nMr. Woodcourt rather sternly rejoined that he was glad to hear it.\n\n“I wish, sir,” said Mr. Vholes, “to leave a good name behind me. Therefore I take every opportunity of openly stating to a friend of Mr. C. how Mr. C. is situated. As to myself, sir, the labourer is worthy of his hire. If I undertake to put my shoulder to the wheel, I do it, and I earn what I get. I am here for that purpose. My name is painted on the door outside, with that object.”\n\n“And Mr. Carstone’s address, Mr. Vholes?”\n\n“Sir,” returned Mr. Vholes, “as I believe I have already mentioned, it is next door. On the second story you will find Mr. C.’s apartments. Mr. C. desires to be near his professional adviser, and I am far from objecting, for I court inquiry.”\n\nUpon this Mr. Woodcourt wished Mr. Vholes good day and went in search of Richard, the change in whose appearance he began to understand now but too well.\n\nHe found him in a dull room, fadedly furnished, much as I had found him in his barrack-room but a little while before, except that he was not writing but was sitting with a book before him, from which his eyes and thoughts were far astray. As the door chanced to be standing open, Mr. Woodcourt was in his presence for some moments without being perceived, and he told me that he never could forget the haggardness of his face and the dejection of his manner before he was aroused from his dream.\n\n“Woodcourt, my dear fellow,” cried Richard, starting up with extended hands, “you come upon my vision like a ghost.”\n\n“A friendly one,” he replied, “and only waiting, as they say ghosts do, to be addressed. How does the mortal world go?” They were seated now, near together.\n\n“Badly enough, and slowly enough,” said Richard, “speaking at least for my part of it.”\n\n“What part is that?”\n\n“The Chancery part.”\n\n“I never heard,” returned Mr. Woodcourt, shaking his head, “of its going well yet.”\n\n“Nor I,” said Richard moodily. “Who ever did?” He brightened again in a moment and said with his natural openness, “Woodcourt, I should be sorry to be misunderstood by you, even if I gained by it in your estimation. You must know that I have done no good this long time. I have not intended to do much harm, but I seem to have been capable of nothing else. It may be that I should have done better by keeping out of the net into which my destiny has worked me, but I think not, though I dare say you will soon hear, if you have not already heard, a very different opinion. To make short of a long story, I am afraid I have wanted an object; but I have an object now—or it has me—and it is too late to discuss it. Take me as I am, and make the best of me.”\n\n“A bargain,” said Mr. Woodcourt. “Do as much by me in return.”\n\n“Oh! You,” returned Richard, “you can pursue your art for its own sake, and can put your hand upon the plough and never turn, and can strike a purpose out of anything. You and I are very different creatures.”\n\nHe spoke regretfully and lapsed for a moment into his weary condition.\n\n“Well, well!” he cried, shaking it off. “Everything has an end. We shall see! So you will take me as I am, and make the best of me?”\n\n“Aye! Indeed I will.” They shook hands upon it laughingly, but in deep earnestness. I can answer for one of them with my heart of hearts.\n\n“You come as a godsend,” said Richard, “for I have seen nobody here yet but Vholes. Woodcourt, there is one subject I should like to mention, for once and for all, in the beginning of our treaty. You can hardly make the best of me if I don’t. You know, I dare say, that I have an attachment to my cousin Ada?”\n\nMr. Woodcourt replied that I had hinted as much to him. “Now pray,” returned Richard, “don’t think me a heap of selfishness. Don’t suppose that I am splitting my head and half breaking my heart over this miserable Chancery suit for my own rights and interests alone. Ada’s are bound up with mine; they can’t be separated; Vholes works for both of us. Do think of that!”\n\nHe was so very solicitous on this head that Mr. Woodcourt gave him the strongest assurances that he did him no injustice.\n\n“You see,” said Richard, with something pathetic in his manner of lingering on the point, though it was off-hand and unstudied, “to an upright fellow like you, bringing a friendly face like yours here, I cannot bear the thought of appearing selfish and mean. I want to see Ada righted, Woodcourt, as well as myself; I want to do my utmost to right her, as well as myself; I venture what I can scrape together to extricate her, as well as myself. Do, I beseech you, think of that!”\n\nAfterwards, when Mr. Woodcourt came to reflect on what had passed, he was so very much impressed by the strength of Richard’s anxiety on this point that in telling me generally of his first visit to Symond’s Inn he particularly dwelt upon it. It revived a fear I had had before that my dear girl’s little property would be absorbed by Mr. Vholes and that Richard’s justification to himself would be sincerely this. It was just as I began to take care of Caddy that the interview took place, and I now return to the time when Caddy had recovered and the shade was still between me and my darling.\n\nI proposed to Ada that morning that we should go and see Richard. It a little surprised me to find that she hesitated and was not so radiantly willing as I had expected.\n\n“My dear,” said I, “you have not had any difference with Richard since I have been so much away?”\n\n“No, Esther.”\n\n“Not heard of him, perhaps?” said I.\n\n“Yes, I have heard of him,” said Ada.\n\nSuch tears in her eyes, and such love in her face. I could not make my darling out. Should I go to Richard’s by myself? I said. No, Ada thought I had better not go by myself. Would she go with me? Yes, Ada thought she had better go with me. Should we go now? Yes, let us go now. Well, I could not understand my darling, with the tears in her eyes and the love in her face!\n\nWe were soon equipped and went out. It was a sombre day, and drops of chill rain fell at intervals. It was one of those colourless days when everything looks heavy and harsh. The houses frowned at us, the dust rose at us, the smoke swooped at us, nothing made any compromise about itself or wore a softened aspect. I fancied my beautiful girl quite out of place in the rugged streets, and I thought there were more funerals passing along the dismal pavements than I had ever seen before.\n\nWe had first to find out Symond’s Inn. We were going to inquire in a shop when Ada said she thought it was near Chancery Lane. “We are not likely to be far out, my love, if we go in that direction,” said I. So to Chancery Lane we went, and there, sure enough, we saw it written up. Symond’s Inn.\n\nWe had next to find out the number. “Or Mr. Vholes’s office will do,” I recollected, “for Mr. Vholes’s office is next door.” Upon which Ada said, perhaps that was Mr. Vholes’s office in the corner there. And it really was.\n\nThen came the question, which of the two next doors? I was going for the one, and my darling was going for the other; and my darling was right again. So up we went to the second story, when we came to Richard’s name in great white letters on a hearse-like panel.\n\nI should have knocked, but Ada said perhaps we had better turn the handle and go in. Thus we came to Richard, poring over a table covered with dusty bundles of papers which seemed to me like dusty mirrors reflecting his own mind. Wherever I looked I saw the ominous words that ran in it repeated. Jarndyce and Jarndyce.\n\nHe received us very affectionately, and we sat down. “If you had come a little earlier,” he said, “you would have found Woodcourt here. There never was such a good fellow as Woodcourt is. He finds time to look in between-whiles, when anybody else with half his work to do would be thinking about not being able to come. And he is so cheery, so fresh, so sensible, so earnest, so—everything that I am not, that the place brightens whenever he comes, and darkens whenever he goes again.”\n\n“God bless him,” I thought, “for his truth to me!”\n\n“He is not so sanguine, Ada,” continued Richard, casting his dejected look over the bundles of papers, “as Vholes and I are usually, but he is only an outsider and is not in the mysteries. We have gone into them, and he has not. He can’t be expected to know much of such a labyrinth.”\n\nAs his look wandered over the papers again and he passed his two hands over his head, I noticed how sunken and how large his eyes appeared, how dry his lips were, and how his finger-nails were all bitten away.\n\n“Is this a healthy place to live in, Richard, do you think?” said I.\n\n“Why, my dear Minerva,” answered Richard with his old gay laugh, “it is neither a rural nor a cheerful place; and when the sun shines here, you may lay a pretty heavy wager that it is shining brightly in an open spot. But it’s well enough for the time. It’s near the offices and near Vholes.”\n\n“Perhaps,” I hinted, “a change from both—”\n\n“Might do me good?” said Richard, forcing a laugh as he finished the sentence. “I shouldn’t wonder! But it can only come in one way now—in one of two ways, I should rather say. Either the suit must be ended, Esther, or the suitor. But it shall be the suit, my dear girl, the suit, my dear girl!”\n\nThese latter words were addressed to Ada, who was sitting nearest to him. Her face being turned away from me and towards him, I could not see it.\n\n“We are doing very well,” pursued Richard. “Vholes will tell you so. We are really spinning along. Ask Vholes. We are giving them no rest. Vholes knows all their windings and turnings, and we are upon them everywhere. We have astonished them already. We shall rouse up that nest of sleepers, mark my words!”\n\nHis hopefulness had long been more painful to me than his despondency; it was so unlike hopefulness, had something so fierce in its determination to be it, was so hungry and eager, and yet so conscious of being forced and unsustainable that it had long touched me to the heart. But the commentary upon it now indelibly written in his handsome face made it far more distressing than it used to be. I say indelibly, for I felt persuaded that if the fatal cause could have been for ever terminated, according to his brightest visions, in that same hour, the traces of the premature anxiety, self-reproach, and disappointment it had occasioned him would have remained upon his features to the hour of his death.\n\n“The sight of our dear little woman,” said Richard, Ada still remaining silent and quiet, “is so natural to me, and her compassionate face is so like the face of old days—”\n\nAh! No, no. I smiled and shook my head.\n\n“—So exactly like the face of old days,” said Richard in his cordial voice, and taking my hand with the brotherly regard which nothing ever changed, “that I can’t make pretences with her. I fluctuate a little; that’s the truth. Sometimes I hope, my dear, and sometimes I—don’t quite despair, but nearly. I get,” said Richard, relinquishing my hand gently and walking across the room, “so tired!”\n\nHe took a few turns up and down and sunk upon the sofa. “I get,” he repeated gloomily, “so tired. It is such weary, weary work!”\n\nHe was leaning on his arm saying these words in a meditative voice and looking at the ground when my darling rose, put off her bonnet, kneeled down beside him with her golden hair falling like sunlight on his head, clasped her two arms round his neck, and turned her face to me. Oh, what a loving and devoted face I saw!\n\n“Esther, dear,” she said very quietly, “I am not going home again.”\n\nA light shone in upon me all at once.\n\n“Never any more. I am going to stay with my dear husband. We have been married above two months. Go home without me, my own Esther; I shall never go home any more!” With those words my darling drew his head down on her breast and held it there. And if ever in my life I saw a love that nothing but death could change, I saw it then before me.\n\n“Speak to Esther, my dearest,” said Richard, breaking the silence presently. “Tell her how it was.”\n\nI met her before she could come to me and folded her in my arms. We neither of us spoke, but with her cheek against my own I wanted to hear nothing. “My pet,” said I. “My love. My poor, poor girl!” I pitied her so much. I was very fond of Richard, but the impulse that I had upon me was to pity her so much.\n\n“Esther, will you forgive me? Will my cousin John forgive me?”\n\n“My dear,” said I, “to doubt it for a moment is to do him a great wrong. And as to me!” Why, as to me, what had I to forgive!\n\nI dried my sobbing darling’s eyes and sat beside her on the sofa, and Richard sat on my other side; and while I was reminded of that so different night when they had first taken me into their confidence and had gone on in their own wild happy way, they told me between them how it was.\n\n“All I had was Richard’s,” Ada said; “and Richard would not take it, Esther, and what could I do but be his wife when I loved him dearly!”\n\n“And you were so fully and so kindly occupied, excellent Dame Durden,” said Richard, “that how could we speak to you at such a time! And besides, it was not a long-considered step. We went out one morning and were married.”\n\n“And when it was done, Esther,” said my darling, “I was always thinking how to tell you and what to do for the best. And sometimes I thought you ought to know it directly, and sometimes I thought you ought not to know it and keep it from my cousin John; and I could not tell what to do, and I fretted very much.”\n\nHow selfish I must have been not to have thought of this before! I don’t know what I said now. I was so sorry, and yet I was so fond of them and so glad that they were fond of me; I pitied them so much, and yet I felt a kind of pride in their loving one another. I never had experienced such painful and pleasurable emotion at one time, and in my own heart I did not know which predominated. But I was not there to darken their way; I did not do that.\n\nWhen I was less foolish and more composed, my darling took her wedding-ring from her bosom, and kissed it, and put it on. Then I remembered last night and told Richard that ever since her marriage she had worn it at night when there was no one to see. Then Ada blushingly asked me how did I know that, my dear. Then I told Ada how I had seen her hand concealed under her pillow and had little thought why, my dear. Then they began telling me how it was all over again, and I began to be sorry and glad again, and foolish again, and to hide my plain old face as much as I could lest I should put them out of heart.\n\nThus the time went on until it became necessary for me to think of returning. When that time arrived it was the worst of all, for then my darling completely broke down. She clung round my neck, calling me by every dear name she could think of and saying what should she do without me! Nor was Richard much better; and as for me, I should have been the worst of the three if I had not severely said to myself, “Now Esther, if you do, I’ll never speak to you again!”\n\n“Why, I declare,” said I, “I never saw such a wife. I don’t think she loves her husband at all. Here, Richard, take my child, for goodness’ sake.” But I held her tight all the while, and could have wept over her I don’t know how long.\n\n“I give this dear young couple notice,” said I, “that I am only going away to come back to-morrow and that I shall be always coming backwards and forwards until Symond’s Inn is tired of the sight of me. So I shall not say good-bye, Richard. For what would be the use of that, you know, when I am coming back so soon!”\n\nI had given my darling to him now, and I meant to go; but I lingered for one more look of the precious face which it seemed to rive my heart to turn from.\n\nSo I said (in a merry bustling manner) that unless they gave me some encouragement to come back, I was not sure that I could take that liberty, upon which my dear girl looked up, faintly smiling through her tears, and I folded her lovely face between my hands, and gave it one last kiss, and laughed, and ran away.\n\nAnd when I got downstairs, oh, how I cried! It almost seemed to me that I had lost my Ada for ever. I was so lonely and so blank without her, and it was so desolate to be going home with no hope of seeing her there, that I could get no comfort for a little while as I walked up and down in a dim corner sobbing and crying.\n\nI came to myself by and by, after a little scolding, and took a coach home. The poor boy whom I had found at St. Albans had reappeared a short time before and was lying at the point of death; indeed, was then dead, though I did not know it. My guardian had gone out to inquire about him and did not return to dinner. Being quite alone, I cried a little again, though on the whole I don’t think I behaved so very, very ill.\n\nIt was only natural that I should not be quite accustomed to the loss of my darling yet. Three or four hours were not a long time after years. But my mind dwelt so much upon the uncongenial scene in which I had left her, and I pictured it as such an overshadowed stony-hearted one, and I so longed to be near her and taking some sort of care of her, that I determined to go back in the evening only to look up at her windows.\n\nIt was foolish, I dare say, but it did not then seem at all so to me, and it does not seem quite so even now. I took Charley into my confidence, and we went out at dusk. It was dark when we came to the new strange home of my dear girl, and there was a light behind the yellow blinds. We walked past cautiously three or four times, looking up, and narrowly missed encountering Mr. Vholes, who came out of his office while we were there and turned his head to look up too before going home. The sight of his lank black figure and the lonesome air of that nook in the dark were favourable to the state of my mind. I thought of the youth and love and beauty of my dear girl, shut up in such an ill-assorted refuge, almost as if it were a cruel place.\n\nIt was very solitary and very dull, and I did not doubt that I might safely steal upstairs. I left Charley below and went up with a light foot, not distressed by any glare from the feeble oil lanterns on the way. I listened for a few moments, and in the musty rotting silence of the house believed that I could hear the murmur of their young voices. I put my lips to the hearse-like panel of the door as a kiss for my dear and came quietly down again, thinking that one of these days I would confess to the visit.\n\nAnd it really did me good, for though nobody but Charley and I knew anything about it, I somehow felt as if it had diminished the separation between Ada and me and had brought us together again for those moments. I went back, not quite accustomed yet to the change, but all the better for that hovering about my darling.\n\nMy guardian had come home and was standing thoughtfully by the dark window. When I went in, his face cleared and he came to his seat, but he caught the light upon my face as I took mine.\n\n“Little woman,” said he, “You have been crying.”\n\n“Why, yes, guardian,” said I, “I am afraid I have been, a little. Ada has been in such distress, and is so very sorry, guardian.”\n\nI put my arm on the back of his chair, and I saw in his glance that my words and my look at her empty place had prepared him.\n\n“Is she married, my dear?”\n\nI told him all about it and how her first entreaties had referred to his forgiveness.\n\n“She has no need of it,” said he. “Heaven bless her and her husband!” But just as my first impulse had been to pity her, so was his. “Poor girl, poor girl! Poor Rick! Poor Ada!”\n\nNeither of us spoke after that, until he said with a sigh, “Well, well, my dear! Bleak House is thinning fast.”\n\n“But its mistress remains, guardian.” Though I was timid about saying it, I ventured because of the sorrowful tone in which he had spoken. “She will do all she can to make it happy,” said I.\n\n“She will succeed, my love!”\n\nThe letter had made no difference between us except that the seat by his side had come to be mine; it made none now. He turned his old bright fatherly look upon me, laid his hand on my hand in his old way, and said again, “She will succeed, my dear. Nevertheless, Bleak House is thinning fast, O little woman!”\n\nI was sorry presently that this was all we said about that. I was rather disappointed. I feared I might not quite have been all I had meant to be since the letter and the answer.\n\nObstinacy\nBut one other day had intervened when, early in the morning as we were going to breakfast, Mr. Woodcourt came in haste with the astounding news that a terrible murder had been committed for which Mr. George had been apprehended and was in custody. When he told us that a large reward was offered by Sir Leicester Dedlock for the murderer’s apprehension, I did not in my first consternation understand why; but a few more words explained to me that the murdered person was Sir Leicester’s lawyer, and immediately my mother’s dread of him rushed into my remembrance.\n\nThis unforeseen and violent removal of one whom she had long watched and distrusted and who had long watched and distrusted her, one for whom she could have had few intervals of kindness, always dreading in him a dangerous and secret enemy, appeared so awful that my first thoughts were of her. How appalling to hear of such a death and be able to feel no pity! How dreadful to remember, perhaps, that she had sometimes even wished the old man away who was so swiftly hurried out of life!\n\nSuch crowding reflections, increasing the distress and fear I always felt when the name was mentioned, made me so agitated that I could scarcely hold my place at the table. I was quite unable to follow the conversation until I had had a little time to recover. But when I came to myself and saw how shocked my guardian was and found that they were earnestly speaking of the suspected man and recalling every favourable impression we had formed of him out of the good we had known of him, my interest and my fears were so strongly aroused in his behalf that I was quite set up again.\n\n“Guardian, you don’t think it possible that he is justly accused?”\n\n“My dear, I CAN’T think so. This man whom we have seen so open-hearted and compassionate, who with the might of a giant has the gentleness of a child, who looks as brave a fellow as ever lived and is so simple and quiet with it, this man justly accused of such a crime? I can’t believe it. It’s not that I don’t or I won’t. I can’t!”\n\n“And I can’t,” said Mr. Woodcourt. “Still, whatever we believe or know of him, we had better not forget that some appearances are against him. He bore an animosity towards the deceased gentleman. He has openly mentioned it in many places. He is said to have expressed himself violently towards him, and he certainly did about him, to my knowledge. He admits that he was alone on the scene of the murder within a few minutes of its commission. I sincerely believe him to be as innocent of any participation in it as I am, but these are all reasons for suspicion falling upon him.”\n\n“True,” said my guardian. And he added, turning to me, “It would be doing him a very bad service, my dear, to shut our eyes to the truth in any of these respects.”\n\nI felt, of course, that we must admit, not only to ourselves but to others, the full force of the circumstances against him. Yet I knew withal (I could not help saying) that their weight would not induce us to desert him in his need.\n\n“Heaven forbid!” returned my guardian. “We will stand by him, as he himself stood by the two poor creatures who are gone.” He meant Mr. Gridley and the boy, to both of whom Mr. George had given shelter.\n\nMr. Woodcourt then told us that the trooper’s man had been with him before day, after wandering about the streets all night like a distracted creature. That one of the trooper’s first anxieties was that we should not suppose him guilty. That he had charged his messenger to represent his perfect innocence with every solemn assurance he could send us. That Mr. Woodcourt had only quieted the man by undertaking to come to our house very early in the morning with these representations. He added that he was now upon his way to see the prisoner himself.\n\nMy guardian said directly he would go too. Now, besides that I liked the retired soldier very much and that he liked me, I had that secret interest in what had happened which was only known to my guardian. I felt as if it came close and near to me. It seemed to become personally important to myself that the truth should be discovered and that no innocent people should be suspected, for suspicion, once run wild, might run wilder.\n\nIn a word, I felt as if it were my duty and obligation to go with them. My guardian did not seek to dissuade me, and I went.\n\nIt was a large prison with many courts and passages so like one another and so uniformly paved that I seemed to gain a new comprehension, as I passed along, of the fondness that solitary prisoners, shut up among the same staring walls from year to year, have had—as I have read—for a weed or a stray blade of grass. In an arched room by himself, like a cellar upstairs, with walls so glaringly white that they made the massive iron window-bars and iron-bound door even more profoundly black than they were, we found the trooper standing in a corner. He had been sitting on a bench there and had risen when he heard the locks and bolts turn.\n\nWhen he saw us, he came forward a step with his usual heavy tread, and there stopped and made a slight bow. But as I still advanced, putting out my hand to him, he understood us in a moment.\n\n“This is a load off my mind, I do assure you, miss and gentlemen,” said he, saluting us with great heartiness and drawing a long breath. “And now I don’t so much care how it ends.”\n\nHe scarcely seemed to be the prisoner. What with his coolness and his soldierly bearing, he looked far more like the prison guard.\n\n“This is even a rougher place than my gallery to receive a lady in,” said Mr. George, “but I know Miss Summerson will make the best of it.” As he handed me to the bench on which he had been sitting, I sat down, which seemed to give him great satisfaction.\n\n“I thank you, miss,” said he.\n\n“Now, George,” observed my guardian, “as we require no new assurances on your part, so I believe we need give you none on ours.”\n\n“Not at all, sir. I thank you with all my heart. If I was not innocent of this crime, I couldn’t look at you and keep my secret to myself under the condescension of the present visit. I feel the present visit very much. I am not one of the eloquent sort, but I feel it, Miss Summerson and gentlemen, deeply.”\n\nHe laid his hand for a moment on his broad chest and bent his head to us. Although he squared himself again directly, he expressed a great amount of natural emotion by these simple means.\n\n“First,” said my guardian, “can we do anything for your personal comfort, George?”\n\n“For which, sir?” he inquired, clearing his throat.\n\n“For your personal comfort. Is there anything you want that would lessen the hardship of this confinement?”\n\n“Well, sir,” replied George, after a little cogitation, “I am equally obliged to you, but tobacco being against the rules, I can’t say that there is.”\n\n“You will think of many little things perhaps, by and by. Whenever you do, George, let us know.”\n\n“Thank you, sir. Howsoever,” observed Mr. George with one of his sunburnt smiles, “a man who has been knocking about the world in a vagabond kind of a way as long as I have gets on well enough in a place like the present, so far as that goes.”\n\n“Next, as to your case,” observed my guardian.\n\n“Exactly so, sir,” returned Mr. George, folding his arms upon his breast with perfect self-possession and a little curiosity.\n\n“How does it stand now?”\n\n“Why, sir, it is under remand at present. Bucket gives me to understand that he will probably apply for a series of remands from time to time until the case is more complete. How it is to be made more complete I don’t myself see, but I dare say Bucket will manage it somehow.”\n\n“Why, heaven save us, man,” exclaimed my guardian, surprised into his old oddity and vehemence, “you talk of yourself as if you were somebody else!”\n\n“No offence, sir,” said Mr. George. “I am very sensible of your kindness. But I don’t see how an innocent man is to make up his mind to this kind of thing without knocking his head against the walls unless he takes it in that point of view.”\n\n“That is true enough to a certain extent,” returned my guardian, softened. “But my good fellow, even an innocent man must take ordinary precautions to defend himself.”\n\n“Certainly, sir. And I have done so. I have stated to the magistrates, ‘Gentlemen, I am as innocent of this charge as yourselves; what has been stated against me in the way of facts is perfectly true; I know no more about it.’ I intend to continue stating that, sir. What more can I do? It’s the truth.”\n\n“But the mere truth won’t do,” rejoined my guardian.\n\n“Won’t it indeed, sir? Rather a bad look-out for me!” Mr. George good-humouredly observed.\n\n“You must have a lawyer,” pursued my guardian. “We must engage a good one for you.”\n\n“I ask your pardon, sir,” said Mr. George with a step backward. “I am equally obliged. But I must decidedly beg to be excused from anything of that sort.”\n\n“You won’t have a lawyer?”\n\n“No, sir.” Mr. George shook his head in the most emphatic manner. “I thank you all the same, sir, but—no lawyer!”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“I don’t take kindly to the breed,” said Mr. George. “Gridley didn’t. And—if you’ll excuse my saying so much—I should hardly have thought you did yourself, sir.”\n\n“That’s equity,” my guardian explained, a little at a loss; “that’s equity, George.”\n\n“Is it, indeed, sir?” returned the trooper in his off-hand manner. “I am not acquainted with those shades of names myself, but in a general way I object to the breed.”\n\nUnfolding his arms and changing his position, he stood with one massive hand upon the table and the other on his hip, as complete a picture of a man who was not to be moved from a fixed purpose as ever I saw. It was in vain that we all three talked to him and endeavoured to persuade him; he listened with that gentleness which went so well with his bluff bearing, but was evidently no more shaken by our representations that his place of confinement was.\n\n“Pray think, once more, Mr. George,” said I. “Have you no wish in reference to your case?”\n\n“I certainly could wish it to be tried, miss,” he returned, “by court-martial; but that is out of the question, as I am well aware. If you will be so good as to favour me with your attention for a couple of minutes, miss, not more, I’ll endeavour to explain myself as clearly as I can.”\n\nHe looked at us all three in turn, shook his head a little as if he were adjusting it in the stock and collar of a tight uniform, and after a moment’s reflection went on.\n\n“You see, miss, I have been handcuffed and taken into custody and brought here. I am a marked and disgraced man, and here I am. My shooting gallery is rummaged, high and low, by Bucket; such property as I have—’tis small—is turned this way and that till it don’t know itself; and (as aforesaid) here I am! I don’t particular complain of that. Though I am in these present quarters through no immediately preceding fault of mine, I can very well understand that if I hadn’t gone into the vagabond way in my youth, this wouldn’t have happened. It HAS happened. Then comes the question how to meet it.”\n\nHe rubbed his swarthy forehead for a moment with a good-humoured look and said apologetically, “I am such a short-winded talker that I must think a bit.” Having thought a bit, he looked up again and resumed.\n\n“How to meet it. Now, the unfortunate deceased was himself a lawyer and had a pretty tight hold of me. I don’t wish to rake up his ashes, but he had, what I should call if he was living, a devil of a tight hold of me. I don’t like his trade the better for that. If I had kept clear of his trade, I should have kept outside this place. But that’s not what I mean. Now, suppose I had killed him. Suppose I really had discharged into his body any one of those pistols recently fired off that Bucket has found at my place, and dear me, might have found there any day since it has been my place. What should I have done as soon as I was hard and fast here? Got a lawyer.”\n\nHe stopped on hearing some one at the locks and bolts and did not resume until the door had been opened and was shut again. For what purpose opened, I will mention presently.\n\n“I should have got a lawyer, and he would have said (as I have often read in the newspapers), ‘My client says nothing, my client reserves his defence’: my client this, that, and t’other. Well, ’tis not the custom of that breed to go straight, according to my opinion, or to think that other men do. Say I am innocent and I get a lawyer. He would be as likely to believe me guilty as not; perhaps more. What would he do, whether or not? Act as if I was—shut my mouth up, tell me not to commit myself, keep circumstances back, chop the evidence small, quibble, and get me off perhaps! But, Miss Summerson, do I care for getting off in that way; or would I rather be hanged in my own way—if you’ll excuse my mentioning anything so disagreeable to a lady?”\n\nHe had warmed into his subject now, and was under no further necessity to wait a bit.\n\n“I would rather be hanged in my own way. And I mean to be! I don’t intend to say,” looking round upon us with his powerful arms akimbo and his dark eyebrows raised, “that I am more partial to being hanged than another man. What I say is, I must come off clear and full or not at all. Therefore, when I hear stated against me what is true, I say it’s true; and when they tell me, ‘whatever you say will be used,’ I tell them I don’t mind that; I mean it to be used. If they can’t make me innocent out of the whole truth, they are not likely to do it out of anything less, or anything else. And if they are, it’s worth nothing to me.”\n\nTaking a pace or two over the stone floor, he came back to the table and finished what he had to say.\n\n“I thank you, miss and gentlemen both, many times for your attention, and many times more for your interest. That’s the plain state of the matter as it points itself out to a mere trooper with a blunt broadsword kind of a mind. I have never done well in life beyond my duty as a soldier, and if the worst comes after all, I shall reap pretty much as I have sown. When I got over the first crash of being seized as a murderer—it don’t take a rover who has knocked about so much as myself so very long to recover from a crash—I worked my way round to what you find me now. As such I shall remain. No relations will be disgraced by me or made unhappy for me, and—and that’s all I’ve got to say.”\n\nThe door had been opened to admit another soldier-looking man of less prepossessing appearance at first sight and a weather-tanned, bright-eyed wholesome woman with a basket, who, from her entrance, had been exceedingly attentive to all Mr. George had said. Mr. George had received them with a familiar nod and a friendly look, but without any more particular greeting in the midst of his address. He now shook them cordially by the hand and said, “Miss Summerson and gentlemen, this is an old comrade of mine, Matthew Bagnet. And this is his wife, Mrs. Bagnet.”\n\nMr. Bagnet made us a stiff military bow, and Mrs. Bagnet dropped us a curtsy.\n\n“Real good friends of mine, they are,” said Mr. George. “It was at their house I was taken.”\n\n“With a second-hand wiolinceller,” Mr. Bagnet put in, twitching his head angrily. “Of a good tone. For a friend. That money was no object to.”\n\n“Mat,” said Mr. George, “you have heard pretty well all I have been saying to this lady and these two gentlemen. I know it meets your approval?”\n\nMr. Bagnet, after considering, referred the point to his wife. “Old girl,” said he. “Tell him. Whether or not. It meets my approval.”\n\n“Why, George,” exclaimed Mrs. Bagnet, who had been unpacking her basket, in which there was a piece of cold pickled pork, a little tea and sugar, and a brown loaf, “you ought to know it don’t. You ought to know it’s enough to drive a person wild to hear you. You won’t be got off this way, and you won’t be got off that way—what do you mean by such picking and choosing? It’s stuff and nonsense, George.”\n\n“Don’t be severe upon me in my misfortunes, Mrs. Bagnet,” said the trooper lightly.\n\n“Oh! Bother your misfortunes,” cried Mrs. Bagnet, “if they don’t make you more reasonable than that comes to. I never was so ashamed in my life to hear a man talk folly as I have been to hear you talk this day to the present company. Lawyers? Why, what but too many cooks should hinder you from having a dozen lawyers if the gentleman recommended them to you.”\n\n“This is a very sensible woman,” said my guardian. “I hope you will persuade him, Mrs. Bagnet.”\n\n“Persuade him, sir?” she returned. “Lord bless you, no. You don’t know George. Now, there!” Mrs. Bagnet left her basket to point him out with both her bare brown hands. “There he stands! As self-willed and as determined a man, in the wrong way, as ever put a human creature under heaven out of patience! You could as soon take up and shoulder an eight and forty pounder by your own strength as turn that man when he has got a thing into his head and fixed it there. Why, don’t I know him!” cried Mrs. Bagnet. “Don’t I know you, George! You don’t mean to set up for a new character with ME after all these years, I hope?”\n\nHer friendly indignation had an exemplary effect upon her husband, who shook his head at the trooper several times as a silent recommendation to him to yield. Between whiles, Mrs. Bagnet looked at me; and I understood from the play of her eyes that she wished me to do something, though I did not comprehend what.\n\n“But I have given up talking to you, old fellow, years and years,” said Mrs. Bagnet as she blew a little dust off the pickled pork, looking at me again; “and when ladies and gentlemen know you as well as I do, they’ll give up talking to you too. If you are not too headstrong to accept of a bit of dinner, here it is.”\n\n“I accept it with many thanks,” returned the trooper.\n\n“Do you though, indeed?” said Mrs. Bagnet, continuing to grumble on good-humouredly. “I’m sure I’m surprised at that. I wonder you don’t starve in your own way also. It would only be like you. Perhaps you’ll set your mind upon THAT next.” Here she again looked at me, and I now perceived from her glances at the door and at me, by turns, that she wished us to retire and to await her following us outside the prison. Communicating this by similar means to my guardian and Mr. Woodcourt, I rose.\n\n“We hope you will think better of it, Mr. George,” said I, “and we shall come to see you again, trusting to find you more reasonable.”\n\n“More grateful, Miss Summerson, you can’t find me,” he returned.\n\n“But more persuadable we can, I hope,” said I. “And let me entreat you to consider that the clearing up of this mystery and the discovery of the real perpetrator of this deed may be of the last importance to others besides yourself.”\n\nHe heard me respectfully but without much heeding these words, which I spoke a little turned from him, already on my way to the door; he was observing (this they afterwards told me) my height and figure, which seemed to catch his attention all at once.\n\n“’Tis curious,” said he. “And yet I thought so at the time!”\n\nMy guardian asked him what he meant.\n\n“Why, sir,” he answered, “when my ill fortune took me to the dead man’s staircase on the night of his murder, I saw a shape so like Miss Summerson’s go by me in the dark that I had half a mind to speak to it.”\n\nFor an instant I felt such a shudder as I never felt before or since and hope I shall never feel again.\n\n“It came downstairs as I went up,” said the trooper, “and crossed the moonlighted window with a loose black mantle on; I noticed a deep fringe to it. However, it has nothing to do with the present subject, excepting that Miss Summerson looked so like it at the moment that it came into my head.”\n\nI cannot separate and define the feelings that arose in me after this; it is enough that the vague duty and obligation I had felt upon me from the first of following the investigation was, without my distinctly daring to ask myself any question, increased, and that I was indignantly sure of there being no possibility of a reason for my being afraid.\n\nWe three went out of the prison and walked up and down at some short distance from the gate, which was in a retired place. We had not waited long when Mr. and Mrs. Bagnet came out too and quickly joined us.\n\nThere was a tear in each of Mrs. Bagnet’s eyes, and her face was flushed and hurried. “I didn’t let George see what I thought about it, you know, miss,” was her first remark when she came up, “but he’s in a bad way, poor old fellow!”\n\n“Not with care and prudence and good help,” said my guardian.\n\n“A gentleman like you ought to know best, sir,” returned Mrs. Bagnet, hurriedly drying her eyes on the hem of her grey cloak, “but I am uneasy for him. He has been so careless and said so much that he never meant. The gentlemen of the juries might not understand him as Lignum and me do. And then such a number of circumstances have happened bad for him, and such a number of people will be brought forward to speak against him, and Bucket is so deep.”\n\n“With a second-hand wiolinceller. And said he played the fife. When a boy,” Mr. Bagnet added with great solemnity.\n\n“Now, I tell you, miss,” said Mrs. Bagnet; “and when I say miss, I mean all! Just come into the corner of the wall and I’ll tell you!”\n\nMrs. Bagnet hurried us into a more secluded place and was at first too breathless to proceed, occasioning Mr. Bagnet to say, “Old girl! Tell ’em!”\n\n“Why, then, miss,” the old girl proceeded, untying the strings of her bonnet for more air, “you could as soon move Dover Castle as move George on this point unless you had got a new power to move him with. And I have got it!”\n\n“You are a jewel of a woman,” said my guardian. “Go on!”\n\n“Now, I tell you, miss,” she proceeded, clapping her hands in her hurry and agitation a dozen times in every sentence, “that what he says concerning no relations is all bosh. They don’t know of him, but he does know of them. He has said more to me at odd times than to anybody else, and it warn’t for nothing that he once spoke to my Woolwich about whitening and wrinkling mothers’ heads. For fifty pounds he had seen his mother that day. She’s alive and must be brought here straight!”\n\nInstantly Mrs. Bagnet put some pins into her mouth and began pinning up her skirts all round a little higher than the level of her grey cloak, which she accomplished with surpassing dispatch and dexterity.\n\n“Lignum,” said Mrs. Bagnet, “you take care of the children, old man, and give me the umbrella! I’m away to Lincolnshire to bring that old lady here.”\n\n“But, bless the woman,” cried my guardian with his hand in his pocket, “how is she going? What money has she got?”\n\nMrs. Bagnet made another application to her skirts and brought forth a leathern purse in which she hastily counted over a few shillings and which she then shut up with perfect satisfaction.\n\n“Never you mind for me, miss. I’m a soldier’s wife and accustomed to travel my own way. Lignum, old boy,” kissing him, “one for yourself, three for the children. Now I’m away into Lincolnshire after George’s mother!”\n\nAnd she actually set off while we three stood looking at one another lost in amazement. She actually trudged away in her grey cloak at a sturdy pace, and turned the corner, and was gone.\n\n“Mr. Bagnet,” said my guardian. “Do you mean to let her go in that way?”\n\n“Can’t help it,” he returned. “Made her way home once from another quarter of the world. With the same grey cloak. And same umbrella. Whatever the old girl says, do. Do it! Whenever the old girl says, I’LL do it. She does it.”\n\n“Then she is as honest and genuine as she looks,” rejoined my guardian, “and it is impossible to say more for her.”\n\n“She’s Colour-Sergeant of the Nonpareil battalion,” said Mr. Bagnet, looking at us over his shoulder as he went his way also. “And there’s not such another. But I never own to it before her. Discipline must be maintained.”\n\nThe Track\nMr. Bucket and his fat forefinger are much in consultation together under existing circumstances. When Mr. Bucket has a matter of this pressing interest under his consideration, the fat forefinger seems to rise, to the dignity of a familiar demon. He puts it to his ears, and it whispers information; he puts it to his lips, and it enjoins him to secrecy; he rubs it over his nose, and it sharpens his scent; he shakes it before a guilty man, and it charms him to his destruction. The Augurs of the Detective Temple invariably predict that when Mr. Bucket and that finger are in much conference, a terrible avenger will be heard of before long.\n\nOtherwise mildly studious in his observation of human nature, on the whole a benignant philosopher not disposed to be severe upon the follies of mankind, Mr. Bucket pervades a vast number of houses and strolls about an infinity of streets, to outward appearance rather languishing for want of an object. He is in the friendliest condition towards his species and will drink with most of them. He is free with his money, affable in his manners, innocent in his conversation—but through the placid stream of his life there glides an under-current of forefinger.\n\nTime and place cannot bind Mr. Bucket. Like man in the abstract, he is here to-day and gone to-morrow—but, very unlike man indeed, he is here again the next day. This evening he will be casually looking into the iron extinguishers at the door of Sir Leicester Dedlock’s house in town; and to-morrow morning he will be walking on the leads at Chesney Wold, where erst the old man walked whose ghost is propitiated with a hundred guineas. Drawers, desks, pockets, all things belonging to him, Mr. Bucket examines. A few hours afterwards, he and the Roman will be alone together comparing forefingers.\n\nIt is likely that these occupations are irreconcilable with home enjoyment, but it is certain that Mr. Bucket at present does not go home. Though in general he highly appreciates the society of Mrs. Bucket—a lady of a natural detective genius, which if it had been improved by professional exercise, might have done great things, but which has paused at the level of a clever amateur—he holds himself aloof from that dear solace. Mrs. Bucket is dependent on their lodger (fortunately an amiable lady in whom she takes an interest) for companionship and conversation.\n\nA great crowd assembles in Lincoln’s Inn Fields on the day of the funeral. Sir Leicester Dedlock attends the ceremony in person; strictly speaking, there are only three other human followers, that is to say, Lord Doodle, William Buffy, and the debilitated cousin (thrown in as a make-weight), but the amount of inconsolable carriages is immense. The peerage contributes more four-wheeled affliction than has ever been seen in that neighbourhood. Such is the assemblage of armorial bearings on coach panels that the Herald’s College might be supposed to have lost its father and mother at a blow. The Duke of Foodle sends a splendid pile of dust and ashes, with silver wheel-boxes, patent axles, all the last improvements, and three bereaved worms, six feet high, holding on behind, in a bunch of woe. All the state coachmen in London seem plunged into mourning; and if that dead old man of the rusty garb be not beyond a taste in horseflesh (which appears impossible), it must be highly gratified this day.\n\nQuiet among the undertakers and the equipages and the calves of so many legs all steeped in grief, Mr. Bucket sits concealed in one of the inconsolable carriages and at his ease surveys the crowd through the lattice blinds. He has a keen eye for a crowd—as for what not?—and looking here and there, now from this side of the carriage, now from the other, now up at the house windows, now along the people’s heads, nothing escapes him.\n\n“And there you are, my partner, eh?” says Mr. Bucket to himself, apostrophizing Mrs. Bucket, stationed, by his favour, on the steps of the deceased’s house. “And so you are. And so you are! And very well indeed you are looking, Mrs. Bucket!”\n\nThe procession has not started yet, but is waiting for the cause of its assemblage to be brought out. Mr. Bucket, in the foremost emblazoned carriage, uses his two fat forefingers to hold the lattice a hair’s breadth open while he looks.\n\nAnd it says a great deal for his attachment, as a husband, that he is still occupied with Mrs. B. “There you are, my partner, eh?” he murmuringly repeats. “And our lodger with you. I’m taking notice of you, Mrs. Bucket; I hope you’re all right in your health, my dear!”\n\nNot another word does Mr. Bucket say, but sits with most attentive eyes until the sacked depository of noble secrets is brought down—Where are all those secrets now? Does he keep them yet? Did they fly with him on that sudden journey?—and until the procession moves, and Mr. Bucket’s view is changed. After which he composes himself for an easy ride and takes note of the fittings of the carriage in case he should ever find such knowledge useful.\n\nContrast enough between Mr. Tulkinghorn shut up in his dark carriage and Mr. Bucket shut up in HIS. Between the immeasurable track of space beyond the little wound that has thrown the one into the fixed sleep which jolts so heavily over the stones of the streets, and the narrow track of blood which keeps the other in the watchful state expressed in every hair of his head! But it is all one to both; neither is troubled about that.\n\nMr. Bucket sits out the procession in his own easy manner and glides from the carriage when the opportunity he has settled with himself arrives. He makes for Sir Leicester Dedlock’s, which is at present a sort of home to him, where he comes and goes as he likes at all hours, where he is always welcome and made much of, where he knows the whole establishment, and walks in an atmosphere of mysterious greatness.\n\nNo knocking or ringing for Mr. Bucket. He has caused himself to be provided with a key and can pass in at his pleasure. As he is crossing the hall, Mercury informs him, “Here’s another letter for you, Mr. Bucket, come by post,” and gives it him.\n\n“Another one, eh?” says Mr. Bucket.\n\nIf Mercury should chance to be possessed by any lingering curiosity as to Mr. Bucket’s letters, that wary person is not the man to gratify it. Mr. Bucket looks at him as if his face were a vista of some miles in length and he were leisurely contemplating the same.\n\n“Do you happen to carry a box?” says Mr. Bucket.\n\nUnfortunately Mercury is no snuff-taker.\n\n“Could you fetch me a pinch from anywheres?” says Mr. Bucket. “Thankee. It don’t matter what it is; I’m not particular as to the kind. Thankee!”\n\nHaving leisurely helped himself from a canister borrowed from somebody downstairs for the purpose, and having made a considerable show of tasting it, first with one side of his nose and then with the other, Mr. Bucket, with much deliberation, pronounces it of the right sort and goes on, letter in hand.\n\nNow although Mr. Bucket walks upstairs to the little library within the larger one with the face of a man who receives some scores of letters every day, it happens that much correspondence is not incidental to his life. He is no great scribe, rather handling his pen like the pocket-staff he carries about with him always convenient to his grasp, and discourages correspondence with himself in others as being too artless and direct a way of doing delicate business. Further, he often sees damaging letters produced in evidence and has occasion to reflect that it was a green thing to write them. For these reasons he has very little to do with letters, either as sender or receiver. And yet he has received a round half-dozen within the last twenty-four hours.\n\n“And this,” says Mr. Bucket, spreading it out on the table, “is in the same hand, and consists of the same two words.”\n\nWhat two words?\n\nHe turns the key in the door, ungirdles his black pocket-book (book of fate to many), lays another letter by it, and reads, boldly written in each, “Lady Dedlock.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” says Mr. Bucket. “But I could have made the money without this anonymous information.”\n\nHaving put the letters in his book of fate and girdled it up again, he unlocks the door just in time to admit his dinner, which is brought upon a goodly tray with a decanter of sherry. Mr. Bucket frequently observes, in friendly circles where there is no restraint, that he likes a toothful of your fine old brown East Inder sherry better than anything you can offer him. Consequently he fills and empties his glass with a smack of his lips and is proceeding with his refreshment when an idea enters his mind.\n\nMr. Bucket softly opens the door of communication between that room and the next and looks in. The library is deserted, and the fire is sinking low. Mr. Bucket’s eye, after taking a pigeon-flight round the room, alights upon a table where letters are usually put as they arrive. Several letters for Sir Leicester are upon it. Mr. Bucket draws near and examines the directions. “No,” he says, “there’s none in that hand. It’s only me as is written to. I can break it to Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to-morrow.”\n\nWith that he returns to finish his dinner with a good appetite, and after a light nap, is summoned into the drawing-room. Sir Leicester has received him there these several evenings past to know whether he has anything to report. The debilitated cousin (much exhausted by the funeral) and Volumnia are in attendance.\n\nMr. Bucket makes three distinctly different bows to these three people. A bow of homage to Sir Leicester, a bow of gallantry to Volumnia, and a bow of recognition to the debilitated Cousin, to whom it airily says, “You are a swell about town, and you know me, and I know you.” Having distributed these little specimens of his tact, Mr. Bucket rubs his hands.\n\n“Have you anything new to communicate, officer?” inquires Sir Leicester. “Do you wish to hold any conversation with me in private?”\n\n“Why—not to-night, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”\n\n“Because my time,” pursues Sir Leicester, “is wholly at your disposal with a view to the vindication of the outraged majesty of the law.”\n\nMr. Bucket coughs and glances at Volumnia, rouged and necklaced, as though he would respectfully observe, “I do assure you, you’re a pretty creetur. I’ve seen hundreds worse looking at your time of life, I have indeed.”\n\nThe fair Volumnia, not quite unconscious perhaps of the humanizing influence of her charms, pauses in the writing of cocked-hat notes and meditatively adjusts the pearl necklace. Mr. Bucket prices that decoration in his mind and thinks it as likely as not that Volumnia is writing poetry.\n\n“If I have not,” pursues Sir Leicester, “in the most emphatic manner, adjured you, officer, to exercise your utmost skill in this atrocious case, I particularly desire to take the present opportunity of rectifying any omission I may have made. Let no expense be a consideration. I am prepared to defray all charges. You can incur none in pursuit of the object you have undertaken that I shall hesitate for a moment to bear.”\n\nMr. Bucket made Sir Leicester’s bow again as a response to this liberality.\n\n“My mind,” Sir Leicester adds with a generous warmth, “has not, as may be easily supposed, recovered its tone since the late diabolical occurrence. It is not likely ever to recover its tone. But it is full of indignation to-night after undergoing the ordeal of consigning to the tomb the remains of a faithful, a zealous, a devoted adherent.”\n\nSir Leicester’s voice trembles and his grey hair stirs upon his head. Tears are in his eyes; the best part of his nature is aroused.\n\n“I declare,” he says, “I solemnly declare that until this crime is discovered and, in the course of justice, punished, I almost feel as if there were a stain upon my name. A gentleman who has devoted a large portion of his life to me, a gentleman who has devoted the last day of his life to me, a gentleman who has constantly sat at my table and slept under my roof, goes from my house to his own, and is struck down within an hour of his leaving my house. I cannot say but that he may have been followed from my house, watched at my house, even first marked because of his association with my house—which may have suggested his possessing greater wealth and being altogether of greater importance than his own retiring demeanour would have indicated. If I cannot with my means and influence and my position bring all the perpetrators of such a crime to light, I fail in the assertion of my respect for that gentleman’s memory and of my fidelity towards one who was ever faithful to me.”\n\nWhile he makes this protestation with great emotion and earnestness, looking round the room as if he were addressing an assembly, Mr. Bucket glances at him with an observant gravity in which there might be, but for the audacity of the thought, a touch of compassion.\n\n“The ceremony of to-day,” continues Sir Leicester, “strikingly illustrative of the respect in which my deceased friend”—he lays a stress upon the word, for death levels all distinctions—“was held by the flower of the land, has, I say, aggravated the shock I have received from this most horrible and audacious crime. If it were my brother who had committed it, I would not spare him.”\n\nMr. Bucket looks very grave. Volumnia remarks of the deceased that he was the trustiest and dearest person!\n\n“You must feel it as a deprivation to you, miss,” replies Mr. Bucket soothingly, “no doubt. He was calculated to BE a deprivation, I’m sure he was.”\n\nVolumnia gives Mr. Bucket to understand, in reply, that her sensitive mind is fully made up never to get the better of it as long as she lives, that her nerves are unstrung for ever, and that she has not the least expectation of ever smiling again. Meanwhile she folds up a cocked hat for that redoubtable old general at Bath, descriptive of her melancholy condition.\n\n“It gives a start to a delicate female,” says Mr. Bucket sympathetically, “but it’ll wear off.”\n\nVolumnia wishes of all things to know what is doing? Whether they are going to convict, or whatever it is, that dreadful soldier? Whether he had any accomplices, or whatever the thing is called in the law? And a great deal more to the like artless purpose.\n\n“Why you see, miss,” returns Mr. Bucket, bringing the finger into persuasive action—and such is his natural gallantry that he had almost said “my dear”—“it ain’t easy to answer those questions at the present moment. Not at the present moment. I’ve kept myself on this case, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” whom Mr. Bucket takes into the conversation in right of his importance, “morning, noon, and night. But for a glass or two of sherry, I don’t think I could have had my mind so much upon the stretch as it has been. I COULD answer your questions, miss, but duty forbids it. Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, will very soon be made acquainted with all that has been traced. And I hope that he may find it”—Mr. Bucket again looks grave—“to his satisfaction.”\n\nThe debilitated cousin only hopes some fler’ll be executed—zample. Thinks more interest’s wanted—get man hanged presentime—than get man place ten thousand a year. Hasn’t a doubt—zample—far better hang wrong fler than no fler.\n\n“YOU know life, you know, sir,” says Mr. Bucket with a complimentary twinkle of his eye and crook of his finger, “and you can confirm what I’ve mentioned to this lady. YOU don’t want to be told that from information I have received I have gone to work. You’re up to what a lady can’t be expected to be up to. Lord! Especially in your elevated station of society, miss,” says Mr. Bucket, quite reddening at another narrow escape from “my dear.”\n\n“The officer, Volumnia,” observes Sir Leicester, “is faithful to his duty, and perfectly right.”\n\nMr. Bucket murmurs, “Glad to have the honour of your approbation, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”\n\n“In fact, Volumnia,” proceeds Sir Leicester, “it is not holding up a good model for imitation to ask the officer any such questions as you have put to him. He is the best judge of his own responsibility; he acts upon his responsibility. And it does not become us, who assist in making the laws, to impede or interfere with those who carry them into execution. Or,” says Sir Leicester somewhat sternly, for Volumnia was going to cut in before he had rounded his sentence, “or who vindicate their outraged majesty.”\n\nVolumnia with all humility explains that she had not merely the plea of curiosity to urge (in common with the giddy youth of her sex in general) but that she is perfectly dying with regret and interest for the darling man whose loss they all deplore.\n\n“Very well, Volumnia,” returns Sir Leicester. “Then you cannot be too discreet.”\n\nMr. Bucket takes the opportunity of a pause to be heard again.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I have no objections to telling this lady, with your leave and among ourselves, that I look upon the case as pretty well complete. It is a beautiful case—a beautiful case—and what little is wanting to complete it, I expect to be able to supply in a few hours.”\n\n“I am very glad indeed to hear it,” says Sir Leicester. “Highly creditable to you.”\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” returns Mr. Bucket very seriously, “I hope it may at one and the same time do me credit and prove satisfactory to all. When I depict it as a beautiful case, you see, miss,” Mr. Bucket goes on, glancing gravely at Sir Leicester, “I mean from my point of view. As considered from other points of view, such cases will always involve more or less unpleasantness. Very strange things comes to our knowledge in families, miss; bless your heart, what you would think to be phenomenons, quite.”\n\nVolumnia, with her innocent little scream, supposes so.\n\n“Aye, and even in gen-teel families, in high families, in great families,” says Mr. Bucket, again gravely eyeing Sir Leicester aside. “I have had the honour of being employed in high families before, and you have no idea—come, I’ll go so far as to say not even YOU have any idea, sir,” this to the debilitated cousin, “what games goes on!”\n\nThe cousin, who has been casting sofa-pillows on his head, in a prostration of boredom yawns, “Vayli,” being the used-up for “very likely.”\n\nSir Leicester, deeming it time to dismiss the officer, here majestically interposes with the words, “Very good. Thank you!” and also with a wave of his hand, implying not only that there is an end of the discourse, but that if high families fall into low habits they must take the consequences. “You will not forget, officer,” he adds with condescension, “that I am at your disposal when you please.”\n\nMr. Bucket (still grave) inquires if to-morrow morning, now, would suit, in case he should be as for’ard as he expects to be. Sir Leicester replies, “All times are alike to me.” Mr. Bucket makes his three bows and is withdrawing when a forgotten point occurs to him.\n\n“Might I ask, by the by,” he says in a low voice, cautiously returning, “who posted the reward-bill on the staircase.”\n\n“I ordered it to be put up there,” replies Sir Leicester.\n\n“Would it be considered a liberty, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, if I was to ask you why?”\n\n“Not at all. I chose it as a conspicuous part of the house. I think it cannot be too prominently kept before the whole establishment. I wish my people to be impressed with the enormity of the crime, the determination to punish it, and the hopelessness of escape. At the same time, officer, if you in your better knowledge of the subject see any objection—”\n\nMr. Bucket sees none now; the bill having been put up, had better not be taken down. Repeating his three bows he withdraws, closing the door on Volumnia’s little scream, which is a preliminary to her remarking that that charmingly horrible person is a perfect Blue Chamber.\n\nIn his fondness for society and his adaptability to all grades, Mr. Bucket is presently standing before the hall-fire—bright and warm on the early winter night—admiring Mercury.\n\n“Why, you’re six foot two, I suppose?” says Mr. Bucket.\n\n“Three,” says Mercury.\n\n“Are you so much? But then, you see, you’re broad in proportion and don’t look it. You’re not one of the weak-legged ones, you ain’t. Was you ever modelled now?” Mr. Bucket asks, conveying the expression of an artist into the turn of his eye and head.\n\nMercury never was modelled.\n\n“Then you ought to be, you know,” says Mr. Bucket; “and a friend of mine that you’ll hear of one day as a Royal Academy sculptor would stand something handsome to make a drawing of your proportions for the marble. My Lady’s out, ain’t she?”\n\n“Out to dinner.”\n\n“Goes out pretty well every day, don’t she?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Not to be wondered at!” says Mr. Bucket. “Such a fine woman as her, so handsome and so graceful and so elegant, is like a fresh lemon on a dinner-table, ornamental wherever she goes. Was your father in the same way of life as yourself?”\n\nAnswer in the negative.\n\n“Mine was,” says Mr. Bucket. “My father was first a page, then a footman, then a butler, then a steward, then an inn-keeper. Lived universally respected, and died lamented. Said with his last breath that he considered service the most honourable part of his career, and so it was. I’ve a brother in service, AND a brother-in-law. My Lady a good temper?”\n\nMercury replies, “As good as you can expect.”\n\n“Ah!” says Mr. Bucket. “A little spoilt? A little capricious? Lord! What can you anticipate when they’re so handsome as that? And we like ’em all the better for it, don’t we?”\n\nMercury, with his hands in the pockets of his bright peach-blossom small-clothes, stretches his symmetrical silk legs with the air of a man of gallantry and can’t deny it. Come the roll of wheels and a violent ringing at the bell. “Talk of the angels,” says Mr. Bucket. “Here she is!”\n\nThe doors are thrown open, and she passes through the hall. Still very pale, she is dressed in slight mourning and wears two beautiful bracelets. Either their beauty or the beauty of her arms is particularly attractive to Mr. Bucket. He looks at them with an eager eye and rattles something in his pocket—halfpence perhaps.\n\nNoticing him at his distance, she turns an inquiring look on the other Mercury who has brought her home.\n\n“Mr. Bucket, my Lady.”\n\nMr. Bucket makes a leg and comes forward, passing his familiar demon over the region of his mouth.\n\n“Are you waiting to see Sir Leicester?”\n\n“No, my Lady, I’ve seen him!”\n\n“Have you anything to say to me?”\n\n“Not just at present, my Lady.”\n\n“Have you made any new discoveries?”\n\n“A few, my Lady.”\n\nThis is merely in passing. She scarcely makes a stop, and sweeps upstairs alone. Mr. Bucket, moving towards the staircase-foot, watches her as she goes up the steps the old man came down to his grave, past murderous groups of statuary repeated with their shadowy weapons on the wall, past the printed bill, which she looks at going by, out of view.\n\n“She’s a lovely woman, too, she really is,” says Mr. Bucket, coming back to Mercury. “Don’t look quite healthy though.”\n\nIs not quite healthy, Mercury informs him. Suffers much from headaches.\n\nReally? That’s a pity! Walking, Mr. Bucket would recommend for that. Well, she tries walking, Mercury rejoins. Walks sometimes for two hours when she has them bad. By night, too.\n\n“Are you sure you’re quite so much as six foot three?” asks Mr. Bucket. “Begging your pardon for interrupting you a moment?”\n\nNot a doubt about it.\n\n“You’re so well put together that I shouldn’t have thought it. But the household troops, though considered fine men, are built so straggling. Walks by night, does she? When it’s moonlight, though?”\n\nOh, yes. When it’s moonlight! Of course. Oh, of course! Conversational and acquiescent on both sides.\n\n“I suppose you ain’t in the habit of walking yourself?” says Mr. Bucket. “Not much time for it, I should say?”\n\nBesides which, Mercury don’t like it. Prefers carriage exercise.\n\n“To be sure,” says Mr. Bucket. “That makes a difference. Now I think of it,” says Mr. Bucket, warming his hands and looking pleasantly at the blaze, “she went out walking the very night of this business.”\n\n“To be sure she did! I let her into the garden over the way.”\n\n“And left her there. Certainly you did. I saw you doing it.”\n\n“I didn’t see YOU,” says Mercury.\n\n“I was rather in a hurry,” returns Mr. Bucket, “for I was going to visit a aunt of mine that lives at Chelsea—next door but two to the old original Bun House—ninety year old the old lady is, a single woman, and got a little property. Yes, I chanced to be passing at the time. Let’s see. What time might it be? It wasn’t ten.”\n\n“Half-past nine.”\n\n“You’re right. So it was. And if I don’t deceive myself, my Lady was muffled in a loose black mantle, with a deep fringe to it?”\n\n“Of course she was.”\n\nOf course she was. Mr. Bucket must return to a little work he has to get on with upstairs, but he must shake hands with Mercury in acknowledgment of his agreeable conversation, and will he—this is all he asks—will he, when he has a leisure half-hour, think of bestowing it on that Royal Academy sculptor, for the advantage of both parties?\n\nSpringing a Mine\nRefreshed by sleep, Mr. Bucket rises betimes in the morning and prepares for a field-day. Smartened up by the aid of a clean shirt and a wet hairbrush, with which instrument, on occasions of ceremony, he lubricates such thin locks as remain to him after his life of severe study, Mr. Bucket lays in a breakfast of two mutton chops as a foundation to work upon, together with tea, eggs, toast, and marmalade on a corresponding scale. Having much enjoyed these strengthening matters and having held subtle conference with his familiar demon, he confidently instructs Mercury “just to mention quietly to Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, that whenever he’s ready for me, I’m ready for him.” A gracious message being returned that Sir Leicester will expedite his dressing and join Mr. Bucket in the library within ten minutes, Mr. Bucket repairs to that apartment and stands before the fire with his finger on his chin, looking at the blazing coals.\n\nThoughtful Mr. Bucket is, as a man may be with weighty work to do, but composed, sure, confident. From the expression of his face he might be a famous whist-player for a large stake—say a hundred guineas certain—with the game in his hand, but with a high reputation involved in his playing his hand out to the last card in a masterly way. Not in the least anxious or disturbed is Mr. Bucket when Sir Leicester appears, but he eyes the baronet aside as he comes slowly to his easy-chair with that observant gravity of yesterday in which there might have been yesterday, but for the audacity of the idea, a touch of compassion.\n\n“I am sorry to have kept you waiting, officer, but I am rather later than my usual hour this morning. I am not well. The agitation and the indignation from which I have recently suffered have been too much for me. I am subject to—gout”—Sir Leicester was going to say indisposition and would have said it to anybody else, but Mr. Bucket palpably knows all about it—“and recent circumstances have brought it on.”\n\nAs he takes his seat with some difficulty and with an air of pain, Mr. Bucket draws a little nearer, standing with one of his large hands on the library-table.\n\n“I am not aware, officer,” Sir Leicester observes; raising his eyes to his face, “whether you wish us to be alone, but that is entirely as you please. If you do, well and good. If not, Miss Dedlock would be interested—”\n\n“Why, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” returns Mr. Bucket with his head persuasively on one side and his forefinger pendant at one ear like an earring, “we can’t be too private just at present. You will presently see that we can’t be too private. A lady, under the circumstances, and especially in Miss Dedlock’s elevated station of society, can’t but be agreeable to me, but speaking without a view to myself, I will take the liberty of assuring you that I know we can’t be too private.”\n\n“That is enough.”\n\n“So much so, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” Mr. Bucket resumes, “that I was on the point of asking your permission to turn the key in the door.”\n\n“By all means.” Mr. Bucket skilfully and softly takes that precaution, stooping on his knee for a moment from mere force of habit so to adjust the key in the lock as that no one shall peep in from the outerside.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I mentioned yesterday evening that I wanted but a very little to complete this case. I have now completed it and collected proof against the person who did this crime.”\n\n“Against the soldier?”\n\n“No, Sir Leicester Dedlock; not the soldier.”\n\nSir Leicester looks astounded and inquires, “Is the man in custody?”\n\nMr. Bucket tells him, after a pause, “It was a woman.”\n\nSir Leicester leans back in his chair, and breathlessly ejaculates, “Good heaven!”\n\n“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” Mr. Bucket begins, standing over him with one hand spread out on the library-table and the forefinger of the other in impressive use, “it’s my duty to prepare you for a train of circumstances that may, and I go so far as to say that will, give you a shock. But Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, you are a gentleman, and I know what a gentleman is and what a gentleman is capable of. A gentleman can bear a shock when it must come, boldly and steadily. A gentleman can make up his mind to stand up against almost any blow. Why, take yourself, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. If there’s a blow to be inflicted on you, you naturally think of your family. You ask yourself, how would all them ancestors of yours, away to Julius Caesar—not to go beyond him at present—have borne that blow; you remember scores of them that would have borne it well; and you bear it well on their accounts, and to maintain the family credit. That’s the way you argue, and that’s the way you act, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”\n\nSir Leicester, leaning back in his chair and grasping the elbows, sits looking at him with a stony face.\n\n“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock,” proceeds Mr. Bucket, “thus preparing you, let me beg of you not to trouble your mind for a moment as to anything having come to MY knowledge. I know so much about so many characters, high and low, that a piece of information more or less don’t signify a straw. I don’t suppose there’s a move on the board that would surprise ME, and as to this or that move having taken place, why my knowing it is no odds at all, any possible move whatever (provided it’s in a wrong direction) being a probable move according to my experience. Therefore, what I say to you, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, is, don’t you go and let yourself be put out of the way because of my knowing anything of your family affairs.”\n\n“I thank you for your preparation,” returns Sir Leicester after a silence, without moving hand, foot, or feature, “which I hope is not necessary; though I give it credit for being well intended. Be so good as to go on. Also”—Sir Leicester seems to shrink in the shadow of his figure—“also, to take a seat, if you have no objection.”\n\nNone at all. Mr. Bucket brings a chair and diminishes his shadow. “Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, with this short preface I come to the point. Lady Dedlock—”\n\nSir Leicester raises himself in his seat and stares at him fiercely. Mr. Bucket brings the finger into play as an emollient.\n\n“Lady Dedlock, you see she’s universally admired. That’s what her ladyship is; she’s universally admired,” says Mr. Bucket.\n\n“I would greatly prefer, officer,” Sir Leicester returns stiffly, “my Lady’s name being entirely omitted from this discussion.”\n\n“So would I, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, but—it’s impossible.”\n\n“Impossible?”\n\nMr. Bucket shakes his relentless head.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, it’s altogether impossible. What I have got to say is about her ladyship. She is the pivot it all turns on.”\n\n“Officer,” retorts Sir Leicester with a fiery eye and a quivering lip, “you know your duty. Do your duty, but be careful not to overstep it. I would not suffer it. I would not endure it. You bring my Lady’s name into this communication upon your responsibility—upon your responsibility. My Lady’s name is not a name for common persons to trifle with!”\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I say what I must say, and no more.”\n\n“I hope it may prove so. Very well. Go on. Go on, sir!” Glancing at the angry eyes which now avoid him and at the angry figure trembling from head to foot, yet striving to be still, Mr. Bucket feels his way with his forefinger and in a low voice proceeds.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, it becomes my duty to tell you that the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn long entertained mistrusts and suspicions of Lady Dedlock.”\n\n“If he had dared to breathe them to me, sir—which he never did—I would have killed him myself!” exclaims Sir Leicester, striking his hand upon the table. But in the very heat and fury of the act he stops, fixed by the knowing eyes of Mr. Bucket, whose forefinger is slowly going and who, with mingled confidence and patience, shakes his head.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn was deep and close, and what he fully had in his mind in the very beginning I can’t quite take upon myself to say. But I know from his lips that he long ago suspected Lady Dedlock of having discovered, through the sight of some handwriting—in this very house, and when you yourself, Sir Leicester Dedlock, were present—the existence, in great poverty, of a certain person who had been her lover before you courted her and who ought to have been her husband.” Mr. Bucket stops and deliberately repeats, “Ought to have been her husband, not a doubt about it. I know from his lips that when that person soon afterwards died, he suspected Lady Dedlock of visiting his wretched lodging and his wretched grave, alone and in secret. I know from my own inquiries and through my eyes and ears that Lady Dedlock did make such visit in the dress of her own maid, for the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn employed me to reckon up her ladyship—if you’ll excuse my making use of the term we commonly employ—and I reckoned her up, so far, completely. I confronted the maid in the chambers in Lincoln’s Inn Fields with a witness who had been Lady Dedlock’s guide, and there couldn’t be the shadow of a doubt that she had worn the young woman’s dress, unknown to her. Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I did endeavour to pave the way a little towards these unpleasant disclosures yesterday by saying that very strange things happened even in high families sometimes. All this, and more, has happened in your own family, and to and through your own Lady. It’s my belief that the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn followed up these inquiries to the hour of his death and that he and Lady Dedlock even had bad blood between them upon the matter that very night. Now, only you put that to Lady Dedlock, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and ask her ladyship whether, even after he had left here, she didn’t go down to his chambers with the intention of saying something further to him, dressed in a loose black mantle with a deep fringe to it.”\n\nSir Leicester sits like a statue, gazing at the cruel finger that is probing the life-blood of his heart.\n\n“You put that to her ladyship, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, from me, Inspector Bucket of the Detective. And if her ladyship makes any difficulty about admitting of it, you tell her that it’s no use, that Inspector Bucket knows it and knows that she passed the soldier as you called him (though he’s not in the army now) and knows that she knows she passed him on the staircase. Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, why do I relate all this?”\n\nSir Leicester, who has covered his face with his hands, uttering a single groan, requests him to pause for a moment. By and by he takes his hands away, and so preserves his dignity and outward calmness, though there is no more colour in his face than in his white hair, that Mr. Bucket is a little awed by him. Something frozen and fixed is upon his manner, over and above its usual shell of haughtiness, and Mr. Bucket soon detects an unusual slowness in his speech, with now and then a curious trouble in beginning, which occasions him to utter inarticulate sounds. With such sounds he now breaks silence, soon, however, controlling himself to say that he does not comprehend why a gentleman so faithful and zealous as the late Mr. Tulkinghorn should have communicated to him nothing of this painful, this distressing, this unlooked-for, this overwhelming, this incredible intelligence.\n\n“Again, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” returns Mr. Bucket, “put it to her ladyship to clear that up. Put it to her ladyship, if you think it right, from Inspector Bucket of the Detective. You’ll find, or I’m much mistaken, that the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn had the intention of communicating the whole to you as soon as he considered it ripe, and further, that he had given her ladyship so to understand. Why, he might have been going to reveal it the very morning when I examined the body! You don’t know what I’m going to say and do five minutes from this present time, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet; and supposing I was to be picked off now, you might wonder why I hadn’t done it, don’t you see?”\n\nTrue. Sir Leicester, avoiding, with some trouble those obtrusive sounds, says, “True.” At this juncture a considerable noise of voices is heard in the hall. Mr. Bucket, after listening, goes to the library-door, softly unlocks and opens it, and listens again. Then he draws in his head and whispers hurriedly but composedly, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, this unfortunate family affair has taken air, as I expected it might, the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn being cut down so sudden. The chance to hush it is to let in these people now in a wrangle with your footmen. Would you mind sitting quiet—on the family account—while I reckon ’em up? And would you just throw in a nod when I seem to ask you for it?”\n\nSir Leicester indistinctly answers, “Officer. The best you can, the best you can!” and Mr. Bucket, with a nod and a sagacious crook of the forefinger, slips down into the hall, where the voices quickly die away. He is not long in returning; a few paces ahead of Mercury and a brother deity also powdered and in peach-blossomed smalls, who bear between them a chair in which is an incapable old man. Another man and two women come behind. Directing the pitching of the chair in an affable and easy manner, Mr. Bucket dismisses the Mercuries and locks the door again. Sir Leicester looks on at this invasion of the sacred precincts with an icy stare.\n\n“Now, perhaps you may know me, ladies and gentlemen,” says Mr. Bucket in a confidential voice. “I am Inspector Bucket of the Detective, I am; and this,” producing the tip of his convenient little staff from his breast-pocket, “is my authority. Now, you wanted to see Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. Well! You do see him, and mind you, it ain’t every one as is admitted to that honour. Your name, old gentleman, is Smallweed; that’s what your name is; I know it well.”\n\n“Well, and you never heard any harm of it!” cries Mr. Smallweed in a shrill loud voice.\n\n“You don’t happen to know why they killed the pig, do you?” retorts Mr. Bucket with a steadfast look, but without loss of temper.\n\n“No!”\n\n“Why, they killed him,” says Mr. Bucket, “on account of his having so much cheek. Don’t YOU get into the same position, because it isn’t worthy of you. You ain’t in the habit of conversing with a deaf person, are you?”\n\n“Yes,” snarls Mr. Smallweed, “my wife’s deaf.”\n\n“That accounts for your pitching your voice so high. But as she ain’t here; just pitch it an octave or two lower, will you, and I’ll not only be obliged to you, but it’ll do you more credit,” says Mr. Bucket. “This other gentleman is in the preaching line, I think?”\n\n“Name of Chadband,” Mr. Smallweed puts in, speaking henceforth in a much lower key.\n\n“Once had a friend and brother serjeant of the same name,” says Mr. Bucket, offering his hand, “and consequently feel a liking for it. Mrs. Chadband, no doubt?”\n\n“And Mrs. Snagsby,” Mr. Smallweed introduces.\n\n“Husband a law-stationer and a friend of my own,” says Mr. Bucket. “Love him like a brother! Now, what’s up?”\n\n“Do you mean what business have we come upon?” Mr. Smallweed asks, a little dashed by the suddenness of this turn.\n\n“Ah! You know what I mean. Let us hear what it’s all about in presence of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. Come.”\n\nMr. Smallweed, beckoning Mr. Chadband, takes a moment’s counsel with him in a whisper. Mr. Chadband, expressing a considerable amount of oil from the pores of his forehead and the palms of his hands, says aloud, “Yes. You first!” and retires to his former place.\n\n“I was the client and friend of Mr. Tulkinghorn,” pipes Grandfather Smallweed then; “I did business with him. I was useful to him, and he was useful to me. Krook, dead and gone, was my brother-in-law. He was own brother to a brimstone magpie—leastways Mrs. Smallweed. I come into Krook’s property. I examined all his papers and all his effects. They was all dug out under my eyes. There was a bundle of letters belonging to a dead and gone lodger as was hid away at the back of a shelf in the side of Lady Jane’s bed—his cat’s bed. He hid all manner of things away, everywheres. Mr. Tulkinghorn wanted ’em and got ’em, but I looked ’em over first. I’m a man of business, and I took a squint at ’em. They was letters from the lodger’s sweetheart, and she signed Honoria. Dear me, that’s not a common name, Honoria, is it? There’s no lady in this house that signs Honoria is there? Oh, no, I don’t think so! Oh, no, I don’t think so! And not in the same hand, perhaps? Oh, no, I don’t think so!”\n\nHere Mr. Smallweed, seized with a fit of coughing in the midst of his triumph, breaks off to ejaculate, “Oh, dear me! Oh, Lord! I’m shaken all to pieces!”\n\n“Now, when you’re ready,” says Mr. Bucket after awaiting his recovery, “to come to anything that concerns Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, here the gentleman sits, you know.”\n\n“Haven’t I come to it, Mr. Bucket?” cries Grandfather Smallweed. “Isn’t the gentleman concerned yet? Not with Captain Hawdon, and his ever affectionate Honoria, and their child into the bargain? Come, then, I want to know where those letters are. That concerns me, if it don’t concern Sir Leicester Dedlock. I will know where they are. I won’t have ’em disappear so quietly. I handed ’em over to my friend and solicitor, Mr. Tulkinghorn, not to anybody else.”\n\n“Why, he paid you for them, you know, and handsome too,” says Mr. Bucket.\n\n“I don’t care for that. I want to know who’s got ’em. And I tell you what we want—what we all here want, Mr. Bucket. We want more painstaking and search-making into this murder. We know where the interest and the motive was, and you have not done enough. If George the vagabond dragoon had any hand in it, he was only an accomplice, and was set on. You know what I mean as well as any man.”\n\n“Now I tell you what,” says Mr. Bucket, instantaneously altering his manner, coming close to him, and communicating an extraordinary fascination to the forefinger, “I am damned if I am a-going to have my case spoilt, or interfered with, or anticipated by so much as half a second of time by any human being in creation. YOU want more painstaking and search-making! YOU do? Do you see this hand, and do you think that I don’t know the right time to stretch it out and put it on the arm that fired that shot?”\n\nSuch is the dread power of the man, and so terribly evident it is that he makes no idle boast, that Mr. Smallweed begins to apologize. Mr. Bucket, dismissing his sudden anger, checks him.\n\n“The advice I give you is, don’t you trouble your head about the murder. That’s my affair. You keep half an eye on the newspapers, and I shouldn’t wonder if you was to read something about it before long, if you look sharp. I know my business, and that’s all I’ve got to say to you on that subject. Now about those letters. You want to know who’s got ’em. I don’t mind telling you. I have got ’em. Is that the packet?”\n\nMr. Smallweed looks, with greedy eyes, at the little bundle Mr. Bucket produces from a mysterious part of his coat, and identifies it as the same.\n\n“What have you got to say next?” asks Mr. Bucket. “Now, don’t open your mouth too wide, because you don’t look handsome when you do it.”\n\n“I want five hundred pound.”\n\n“No, you don’t; you mean fifty,” says Mr. Bucket humorously.\n\nIt appears, however, that Mr. Smallweed means five hundred.\n\n“That is, I am deputed by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to consider (without admitting or promising anything) this bit of business,” says Mr. Bucket—Sir Leicester mechanically bows his head—“and you ask me to consider a proposal of five hundred pounds. Why, it’s an unreasonable proposal! Two fifty would be bad enough, but better than that. Hadn’t you better say two fifty?”\n\nMr. Smallweed is quite clear that he had better not.\n\n“Then,” says Mr. Bucket, “let’s hear Mr. Chadband. Lord! Many a time I’ve heard my old fellow-serjeant of that name; and a moderate man he was in all respects, as ever I come across!”\n\nThus invited, Mr. Chadband steps forth, and after a little sleek smiling and a little oil-grinding with the palms of his hands, delivers himself as follows, “My friends, we are now—Rachael, my wife, and I—in the mansions of the rich and great. Why are we now in the mansions of the rich and great, my friends? Is it because we are invited? Because we are bidden to feast with them, because we are bidden to rejoice with them, because we are bidden to play the lute with them, because we are bidden to dance with them? No. Then why are we here, my friends? Air we in possession of a sinful secret, and do we require corn, and wine, and oil, or what is much the same thing, money, for the keeping thereof? Probably so, my friends.”\n\n“You’re a man of business, you are,” returns Mr. Bucket, very attentive, “and consequently you’re going on to mention what the nature of your secret is. You are right. You couldn’t do better.”\n\n“Let us then, my brother, in a spirit of love,” says Mr. Chadband with a cunning eye, “proceed unto it. Rachael, my wife, advance!”\n\nMrs. Chadband, more than ready, so advances as to jostle her husband into the background and confronts Mr. Bucket with a hard, frowning smile.\n\n“Since you want to know what we know,” says she, “I’ll tell you. I helped to bring up Miss Hawdon, her ladyship’s daughter. I was in the service of her ladyship’s sister, who was very sensitive to the disgrace her ladyship brought upon her, and gave out, even to her ladyship, that the child was dead—she WAS very nearly so—when she was born. But she’s alive, and I know her.” With these words, and a laugh, and laying a bitter stress on the word “ladyship,” Mrs. Chadband folds her arms and looks implacably at Mr. Bucket.\n\n“I suppose now,” returns that officer, “YOU will be expecting a twenty-pound note or a present of about that figure?”\n\nMrs. Chadband merely laughs and contemptuously tells him he can “offer” twenty pence.\n\n“My friend the law-stationer’s good lady, over there,” says Mr. Bucket, luring Mrs. Snagsby forward with the finger. “What may YOUR game be, ma’am?”\n\nMrs. Snagsby is at first prevented, by tears and lamentations, from stating the nature of her game, but by degrees it confusedly comes to light that she is a woman overwhelmed with injuries and wrongs, whom Mr. Snagsby has habitually deceived, abandoned, and sought to keep in darkness, and whose chief comfort, under her afflictions, has been the sympathy of the late Mr. Tulkinghorn, who showed so much commiseration for her on one occasion of his calling in Cook’s Court in the absence of her perjured husband that she has of late habitually carried to him all her woes. Everybody it appears, the present company excepted, has plotted against Mrs. Snagsby’s peace. There is Mr. Guppy, clerk to Kenge and Carboy, who was at first as open as the sun at noon, but who suddenly shut up as close as midnight, under the influence—no doubt—of Mr. Snagsby’s suborning and tampering. There is Mr. Weevle, friend of Mr. Guppy, who lived mysteriously up a court, owing to the like coherent causes. There was Krook, deceased; there was Nimrod, deceased; and there was Jo, deceased; and they were “all in it.” In what, Mrs. Snagsby does not with particularity express, but she knows that Jo was Mr. Snagsby’s son, “as well as if a trumpet had spoken it,” and she followed Mr. Snagsby when he went on his last visit to the boy, and if he was not his son why did he go? The one occupation of her life has been, for some time back, to follow Mr. Snagsby to and fro, and up and down, and to piece suspicious circumstances together—and every circumstance that has happened has been most suspicious; and in this way she has pursued her object of detecting and confounding her false husband, night and day. Thus did it come to pass that she brought the Chadbands and Mr. Tulkinghorn together, and conferred with Mr. Tulkinghorn on the change in Mr. Guppy, and helped to turn up the circumstances in which the present company are interested, casually, by the wayside, being still and ever on the great high road that is to terminate in Mr. Snagsby’s full exposure and a matrimonial separation. All this, Mrs. Snagsby, as an injured woman, and the friend of Mrs. Chadband, and the follower of Mr. Chadband, and the mourner of the late Mr. Tulkinghorn, is here to certify under the seal of confidence, with every possible confusion and involvement possible and impossible, having no pecuniary motive whatever, no scheme or project but the one mentioned, and bringing here, and taking everywhere, her own dense atmosphere of dust, arising from the ceaseless working of her mill of jealousy.\n\nWhile this exordium is in hand—and it takes some time—Mr. Bucket, who has seen through the transparency of Mrs. Snagsby’s vinegar at a glance, confers with his familiar demon and bestows his shrewd attention on the Chadbands and Mr. Smallweed. Sir Leicester Dedlock remains immovable, with the same icy surface upon him, except that he once or twice looks towards Mr. Bucket, as relying on that officer alone of all mankind.\n\n“Very good,” says Mr. Bucket. “Now I understand you, you know, and being deputed by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to look into this little matter,” again Sir Leicester mechanically bows in confirmation of the statement, “can give it my fair and full attention. Now I won’t allude to conspiring to extort money or anything of that sort, because we are men and women of the world here, and our object is to make things pleasant. But I tell you what I DO wonder at; I am surprised that you should think of making a noise below in the hall. It was so opposed to your interests. That’s what I look at.”\n\n“We wanted to get in,” pleads Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“Why, of course you wanted to get in,” Mr. Bucket asserts with cheerfulness; “but for a old gentleman at your time of life—what I call truly venerable, mind you!—with his wits sharpened, as I have no doubt they are, by the loss of the use of his limbs, which occasions all his animation to mount up into his head, not to consider that if he don’t keep such a business as the present as close as possible it can’t be worth a mag to him, is so curious! You see your temper got the better of you; that’s where you lost ground,” says Mr. Bucket in an argumentative and friendly way.\n\n“I only said I wouldn’t go without one of the servants came up to Sir Leicester Dedlock,” returns Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“That’s it! That’s where your temper got the better of you. Now, you keep it under another time and you’ll make money by it. Shall I ring for them to carry you down?”\n\n“When are we to hear more of this?” Mrs. Chadband sternly demands.\n\n“Bless your heart for a true woman! Always curious, your delightful sex is!” replies Mr. Bucket with gallantry. “I shall have the pleasure of giving you a call to-morrow or next day—not forgetting Mr. Smallweed and his proposal of two fifty.”\n\n“Five hundred!” exclaims Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“All right! Nominally five hundred.” Mr. Bucket has his hand on the bell-rope. “SHALL I wish you good day for the present on the part of myself and the gentleman of the house?” he asks in an insinuating tone.\n\nNobody having the hardihood to object to his doing so, he does it, and the party retire as they came up. Mr. Bucket follows them to the door, and returning, says with an air of serious business, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, it’s for you to consider whether or not to buy this up. I should recommend, on the whole, it’s being bought up myself; and I think it may be bought pretty cheap. You see, that little pickled cowcumber of a Mrs. Snagsby has been used by all sides of the speculation and has done a deal more harm in bringing odds and ends together than if she had meant it. Mr. Tulkinghorn, deceased, he held all these horses in his hand and could have drove ’em his own way, I haven’t a doubt; but he was fetched off the box head-foremost, and now they have got their legs over the traces, and are all dragging and pulling their own ways. So it is, and such is life. The cat’s away, and the mice they play; the frost breaks up, and the water runs. Now, with regard to the party to be apprehended.”\n\nSir Leicester seems to wake, though his eyes have been wide open, and he looks intently at Mr. Bucket as Mr. Bucket refers to his watch.\n\n“The party to be apprehended is now in this house,” proceeds Mr. Bucket, putting up his watch with a steady hand and with rising spirits, “and I’m about to take her into custody in your presence. Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, don’t you say a word nor yet stir. There’ll be no noise and no disturbance at all. I’ll come back in the course of the evening, if agreeable to you, and endeavour to meet your wishes respecting this unfortunate family matter and the nobbiest way of keeping it quiet. Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, don’t you be nervous on account of the apprehension at present coming off. You shall see the whole case clear, from first to last.”\n\nMr. Bucket rings, goes to the door, briefly whispers Mercury, shuts the door, and stands behind it with his arms folded. After a suspense of a minute or two the door slowly opens and a Frenchwoman enters. Mademoiselle Hortense.\n\nThe moment she is in the room Mr. Bucket claps the door to and puts his back against it. The suddenness of the noise occasions her to turn, and then for the first time she sees Sir Leicester Dedlock in his chair.\n\n“I ask you pardon,” she mutters hurriedly. “They tell me there was no one here.”\n\nHer step towards the door brings her front to front with Mr. Bucket. Suddenly a spasm shoots across her face and she turns deadly pale.\n\n“This is my lodger, Sir Leicester Dedlock,” says Mr. Bucket, nodding at her. “This foreign young woman has been my lodger for some weeks back.”\n\n“What do Sir Leicester care for that, you think, my angel?” returns mademoiselle in a jocular strain.\n\n“Why, my angel,” returns Mr. Bucket, “we shall see.”\n\nMademoiselle Hortense eyes him with a scowl upon her tight face, which gradually changes into a smile of scorn, “You are very mysterieuse. Are you drunk?”\n\n“Tolerable sober, my angel,” returns Mr. Bucket.\n\n“I come from arriving at this so detestable house with your wife. Your wife have left me since some minutes. They tell me downstairs that your wife is here. I come here, and your wife is not here. What is the intention of this fool’s play, say then?” mademoiselle demands, with her arms composedly crossed, but with something in her dark cheek beating like a clock.\n\nMr. Bucket merely shakes the finger at her.\n\n“Ah, my God, you are an unhappy idiot!” cries mademoiselle with a toss of her head and a laugh. “Leave me to pass downstairs, great pig.” With a stamp of her foot and a menace.\n\n“Now, mademoiselle,” says Mr. Bucket in a cool determined way, “you go and sit down upon that sofy.”\n\n“I will not sit down upon nothing,” she replies with a shower of nods.\n\n“Now, mademoiselle,” repeats Mr. Bucket, making no demonstration except with the finger, “you sit down upon that sofy.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I take you into custody on a charge of murder, and you don’t need to be told it. Now, I want to be polite to one of your sex and a foreigner if I can. If I can’t, I must be rough, and there’s rougher ones outside. What I am to be depends on you. So I recommend you, as a friend, afore another half a blessed moment has passed over your head, to go and sit down upon that sofy.”\n\nMademoiselle complies, saying in a concentrated voice while that something in her cheek beats fast and hard, “You are a devil.”\n\n“Now, you see,” Mr. Bucket proceeds approvingly, “you’re comfortable and conducting yourself as I should expect a foreign young woman of your sense to do. So I’ll give you a piece of advice, and it’s this, don’t you talk too much. You’re not expected to say anything here, and you can’t keep too quiet a tongue in your head. In short, the less you PARLAY, the better, you know.” Mr. Bucket is very complacent over this French explanation.\n\nMademoiselle, with that tigerish expansion of the mouth and her black eyes darting fire upon him, sits upright on the sofa in a rigid state, with her hands clenched—and her feet too, one might suppose—muttering, “Oh, you Bucket, you are a devil!”\n\n“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” says Mr. Bucket, and from this time forth the finger never rests, “this young woman, my lodger, was her ladyship’s maid at the time I have mentioned to you; and this young woman, besides being extraordinary vehement and passionate against her ladyship after being discharged—”\n\n“Lie!” cries mademoiselle. “I discharge myself.”\n\n“Now, why don’t you take my advice?” returns Mr. Bucket in an impressive, almost in an imploring, tone. “I’m surprised at the indiscreetness you commit. You’ll say something that’ll be used against you, you know. You’re sure to come to it. Never you mind what I say till it’s given in evidence. It is not addressed to you.”\n\n“Discharge, too,” cries mademoiselle furiously, “by her ladyship! Eh, my faith, a pretty ladyship! Why, I r-r-r-ruin my character by remaining with a ladyship so infame!”\n\n“Upon my soul I wonder at you!” Mr. Bucket remonstrates. “I thought the French were a polite nation, I did, really. Yet to hear a female going on like that before Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet!”\n\n“He is a poor abused!” cries mademoiselle. “I spit upon his house, upon his name, upon his imbecility,” all of which she makes the carpet represent. “Oh, that he is a great man! Oh, yes, superb! Oh, heaven! Bah!”\n\n“Well, Sir Leicester Dedlock,” proceeds Mr. Bucket, “this intemperate foreigner also angrily took it into her head that she had established a claim upon Mr. Tulkinghorn, deceased, by attending on the occasion I told you of at his chambers, though she was liberally paid for her time and trouble.”\n\n“Lie!” cries mademoiselle. “I ref-use his money all togezzer.”\n\n“If you WILL PARLAY, you know,” says Mr. Bucket parenthetically, “you must take the consequences. Now, whether she became my lodger, Sir Leicester Dedlock, with any deliberate intention then of doing this deed and blinding me, I give no opinion on; but she lived in my house in that capacity at the time that she was hovering about the chambers of the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn with a view to a wrangle, and likewise persecuting and half frightening the life out of an unfortunate stationer.”\n\n“Lie!” cries mademoiselle. “All lie!”\n\n“The murder was committed, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and you know under what circumstances. Now, I beg of you to follow me close with your attention for a minute or two. I was sent for, and the case was entrusted to me. I examined the place, and the body, and the papers, and everything. From information I received (from a clerk in the same house) I took George into custody as having been seen hanging about there on the night, and at very nigh the time of the murder, also as having been overheard in high words with the deceased on former occasions—even threatening him, as the witness made out. If you ask me, Sir Leicester Dedlock, whether from the first I believed George to be the murderer, I tell you candidly no, but he might be, notwithstanding, and there was enough against him to make it my duty to take him and get him kept under remand. Now, observe!”\n\nAs Mr. Bucket bends forward in some excitement—for him—and inaugurates what he is going to say with one ghostly beat of his forefinger in the air, Mademoiselle Hortense fixes her black eyes upon him with a dark frown and sets her dry lips closely and firmly together.\n\n“I went home, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, at night and found this young woman having supper with my wife, Mrs. Bucket. She had made a mighty show of being fond of Mrs. Bucket from her first offering herself as our lodger, but that night she made more than ever—in fact, overdid it. Likewise she overdid her respect, and all that, for the lamented memory of the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn. By the living Lord it flashed upon me, as I sat opposite to her at the table and saw her with a knife in her hand, that she had done it!”\n\nMademoiselle is hardly audible in straining through her teeth and lips the words, “You are a devil.”\n\n“Now where,” pursues Mr. Bucket, “had she been on the night of the murder? She had been to the theayter. (She really was there, I have since found, both before the deed and after it.) I knew I had an artful customer to deal with and that proof would be very difficult; and I laid a trap for her—such a trap as I never laid yet, and such a venture as I never made yet. I worked it out in my mind while I was talking to her at supper. When I went upstairs to bed, our house being small and this young woman’s ears sharp, I stuffed the sheet into Mrs. Bucket’s mouth that she shouldn’t say a word of surprise and told her all about it. My dear, don’t you give your mind to that again, or I shall link your feet together at the ankles.” Mr. Bucket, breaking off, has made a noiseless descent upon mademoiselle and laid his heavy hand upon her shoulder.\n\n“What is the matter with you now?” she asks him.\n\n“Don’t you think any more,” returns Mr. Bucket with admonitory finger, “of throwing yourself out of window. That’s what’s the matter with me. Come! Just take my arm. You needn’t get up; I’ll sit down by you. Now take my arm, will you? I’m a married man, you know; you’re acquainted with my wife. Just take my arm.”\n\nVainly endeavouring to moisten those dry lips, with a painful sound she struggles with herself and complies.\n\n“Now we’re all right again. Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, this case could never have been the case it is but for Mrs. Bucket, who is a woman in fifty thousand—in a hundred and fifty thousand! To throw this young woman off her guard, I have never set foot in our house since, though I’ve communicated with Mrs. Bucket in the baker’s loaves and in the milk as often as required. My whispered words to Mrs. Bucket when she had the sheet in her mouth were, ‘My dear, can you throw her off continually with natural accounts of my suspicions against George, and this, and that, and t’other? Can you do without rest and keep watch upon her night and day? Can you undertake to say, ‘She shall do nothing without my knowledge, she shall be my prisoner without suspecting it, she shall no more escape from me than from death, and her life shall be my life, and her soul my soul, till I have got her, if she did this murder?’ Mrs. Bucket says to me, as well as she could speak on account of the sheet, ‘Bucket, I can!’ And she has acted up to it glorious!”\n\n“Lies!” mademoiselle interposes. “All lies, my friend!”\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, how did my calculations come out under these circumstances? When I calculated that this impetuous young woman would overdo it in new directions, was I wrong or right? I was right. What does she try to do? Don’t let it give you a turn? To throw the murder on her ladyship.”\n\nSir Leicester rises from his chair and staggers down again.\n\n“And she got encouragement in it from hearing that I was always here, which was done a-purpose. Now, open that pocket-book of mine, Sir Leicester Dedlock, if I may take the liberty of throwing it towards you, and look at the letters sent to me, each with the two words ‘Lady Dedlock’ in it. Open the one directed to yourself, which I stopped this very morning, and read the three words ‘Lady Dedlock, Murderess’ in it. These letters have been falling about like a shower of lady-birds. What do you say now to Mrs. Bucket, from her spy-place having seen them all written by this young woman? What do you say to Mrs. Bucket having, within this half-hour, secured the corresponding ink and paper, fellow half-sheets and what not? What do you say to Mrs. Bucket having watched the posting of ’em every one by this young woman, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet?” Mr. Bucket asks, triumphant in his admiration of his lady’s genius.\n\nTwo things are especially observable as Mr. Bucket proceeds to a conclusion. First, that he seems imperceptibly to establish a dreadful right of property in mademoiselle. Secondly, that the very atmosphere she breathes seems to narrow and contract about her as if a close net or a pall were being drawn nearer and yet nearer around her breathless figure.\n\n“There is no doubt that her ladyship was on the spot at the eventful period,” says Mr. Bucket, “and my foreign friend here saw her, I believe, from the upper part of the staircase. Her ladyship and George and my foreign friend were all pretty close on one another’s heels. But that don’t signify any more, so I’ll not go into it. I found the wadding of the pistol with which the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn was shot. It was a bit of the printed description of your house at Chesney Wold. Not much in that, you’ll say, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. No. But when my foreign friend here is so thoroughly off her guard as to think it a safe time to tear up the rest of that leaf, and when Mrs. Bucket puts the pieces together and finds the wadding wanting, it begins to look like Queer Street.”\n\n“These are very long lies,” mademoiselle interposes. “You prose great deal. Is it that you have almost all finished, or are you speaking always?”\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” proceeds Mr. Bucket, who delights in a full title and does violence to himself when he dispenses with any fragment of it, “the last point in the case which I am now going to mention shows the necessity of patience in our business, and never doing a thing in a hurry. I watched this young woman yesterday without her knowledge when she was looking at the funeral, in company with my wife, who planned to take her there; and I had so much to convict her, and I saw such an expression in her face, and my mind so rose against her malice towards her ladyship, and the time was altogether such a time for bringing down what you may call retribution upon her, that if I had been a younger hand with less experience, I should have taken her, certain. Equally, last night, when her ladyship, as is so universally admired I am sure, come home looking—why, Lord, a man might almost say like Venus rising from the ocean—it was so unpleasant and inconsistent to think of her being charged with a murder of which she was innocent that I felt quite to want to put an end to the job. What should I have lost? Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I should have lost the weapon. My prisoner here proposed to Mrs. Bucket, after the departure of the funeral, that they should go per bus a little ways into the country and take tea at a very decent house of entertainment. Now, near that house of entertainment there’s a piece of water. At tea, my prisoner got up to fetch her pocket-handkercher from the bedroom where the bonnets was; she was rather a long time gone and came back a little out of wind. As soon as they came home this was reported to me by Mrs. Bucket, along with her observations and suspicions. I had the piece of water dragged by moonlight, in presence of a couple of our men, and the pocket pistol was brought up before it had been there half-a-dozen hours. Now, my dear, put your arm a little further through mine, and hold it steady, and I shan’t hurt you!”\n\nIn a trice Mr. Bucket snaps a handcuff on her wrist. “That’s one,” says Mr. Bucket. “Now the other, darling. Two, and all told!”\n\nHe rises; she rises too. “Where,” she asks him, darkening her large eyes until their drooping lids almost conceal them—and yet they stare, “where is your false, your treacherous, and cursed wife?”\n\n“She’s gone forrard to the Police Office,” returns Mr. Bucket. “You’ll see her there, my dear.”\n\n“I would like to kiss her!” exclaims Mademoiselle Hortense, panting tigress-like.\n\n“You’d bite her, I suspect,” says Mr. Bucket.\n\n“I would!” making her eyes very large. “I would love to tear her limb from limb.”\n\n“Bless you, darling,” says Mr. Bucket with the greatest composure, “I’m fully prepared to hear that. Your sex have such a surprising animosity against one another when you do differ. You don’t mind me half so much, do you?”\n\n“No. Though you are a devil still.”\n\n“Angel and devil by turns, eh?” cries Mr. Bucket. “But I am in my regular employment, you must consider. Let me put your shawl tidy. I’ve been lady’s maid to a good many before now. Anything wanting to the bonnet? There’s a cab at the door.”\n\nMademoiselle Hortense, casting an indignant eye at the glass, shakes herself perfectly neat in one shake and looks, to do her justice, uncommonly genteel.\n\n“Listen then, my angel,” says she after several sarcastic nods. “You are very spiritual. But can you restore him back to life?”\n\nMr. Bucket answers, “Not exactly.”\n\n“That is droll. Listen yet one time. You are very spiritual. Can you make a honourable lady of her?”\n\n“Don’t be so malicious,” says Mr. Bucket.\n\n“Or a haughty gentleman of HIM?” cries mademoiselle, referring to Sir Leicester with ineffable disdain. “Eh! Oh, then regard him! The poor infant! Ha! Ha! Ha!”\n\n“Come, come, why this is worse PARLAYING than the other,” says Mr. Bucket. “Come along!”\n\n“You cannot do these things? Then you can do as you please with me. It is but the death, it is all the same. Let us go, my angel. Adieu, you old man, grey. I pity you, and I despise you!”\n\nWith these last words she snaps her teeth together as if her mouth closed with a spring. It is impossible to describe how Mr. Bucket gets her out, but he accomplishes that feat in a manner so peculiar to himself, enfolding and pervading her like a cloud, and hovering away with her as if he were a homely Jupiter and she the object of his affections.\n\nSir Leicester, left alone, remains in the same attitude, as though he were still listening and his attention were still occupied. At length he gazes round the empty room, and finding it deserted, rises unsteadily to his feet, pushes back his chair, and walks a few steps, supporting himself by the table. Then he stops, and with more of those inarticulate sounds, lifts up his eyes and seems to stare at something.\n\nHeaven knows what he sees. The green, green woods of Chesney Wold, the noble house, the pictures of his forefathers, strangers defacing them, officers of police coarsely handling his most precious heirlooms, thousands of fingers pointing at him, thousands of faces sneering at him. But if such shadows flit before him to his bewilderment, there is one other shadow which he can name with something like distinctness even yet and to which alone he addresses his tearing of his white hair and his extended arms.\n\nIt is she in association with whom, saving that she has been for years a main fibre of the root of his dignity and pride, he has never had a selfish thought. It is she whom he has loved, admired, honoured, and set up for the world to respect. It is she who, at the core of all the constrained formalities and conventionalities of his life, has been a stock of living tenderness and love, susceptible as nothing else is of being struck with the agony he feels. He sees her, almost to the exclusion of himself, and cannot bear to look upon her cast down from the high place she has graced so well.\n\nAnd even to the point of his sinking on the ground, oblivious of his suffering, he can yet pronounce her name with something like distinctness in the midst of those intrusive sounds, and in a tone of mourning and compassion rather than reproach.\n\nFlight\nInspector Bucket of the Detective has not yet struck his great blow, as just now chronicled, but is yet refreshing himself with sleep preparatory to his field-day, when through the night and along the freezing wintry roads a chaise and pair comes out of Lincolnshire, making its way towards London.\n\nRailroads shall soon traverse all this country, and with a rattle and a glare the engine and train shall shoot like a meteor over the wide night-landscape, turning the moon paler; but as yet such things are non-existent in these parts, though not wholly unexpected. Preparations are afoot, measurements are made, ground is staked out. Bridges are begun, and their not yet united piers desolately look at one another over roads and streams like brick and mortar couples with an obstacle to their union; fragments of embankments are thrown up and left as precipices with torrents of rusty carts and barrows tumbling over them; tripods of tall poles appear on hilltops, where there are rumours of tunnels; everything looks chaotic and abandoned in full hopelessness. Along the freezing roads, and through the night, the post-chaise makes its way without a railroad on its mind.\n\nMrs. Rouncewell, so many years housekeeper at Chesney Wold, sits within the chaise; and by her side sits Mrs. Bagnet with her grey cloak and umbrella. The old girl would prefer the bar in front, as being exposed to the weather and a primitive sort of perch more in accordance with her usual course of travelling, but Mrs. Rouncewell is too thoughtful of her comfort to admit of her proposing it. The old lady cannot make enough of the old girl. She sits, in her stately manner, holding her hand, and regardless of its roughness, puts it often to her lips. “You are a mother, my dear soul,” says she many times, “and you found out my George’s mother!”\n\n“Why, George,” returns Mrs. Bagnet, “was always free with me, ma’am, and when he said at our house to my Woolwich that of all the things my Woolwich could have to think of when he grew to be a man, the comfortablest would be that he had never brought a sorrowful line into his mother’s face or turned a hair of her head grey, then I felt sure, from his way, that something fresh had brought his own mother into his mind. I had often known him say to me, in past times, that he had behaved bad to her.”\n\n“Never, my dear!” returns Mrs. Rouncewell, bursting into tears. “My blessing on him, never! He was always fond of me, and loving to me, was my George! But he had a bold spirit, and he ran a little wild and went for a soldier. And I know he waited at first, in letting us know about himself, till he should rise to be an officer; and when he didn’t rise, I know he considered himself beneath us, and wouldn’t be a disgrace to us. For he had a lion heart, had my George, always from a baby!”\n\nThe old lady’s hands stray about her as of yore, while she recalls, all in a tremble, what a likely lad, what a fine lad, what a gay good-humoured clever lad he was; how they all took to him down at Chesney Wold; how Sir Leicester took to him when he was a young gentleman; how the dogs took to him; how even the people who had been angry with him forgave him the moment he was gone, poor boy. And now to see him after all, and in a prison too! And the broad stomacher heaves, and the quaint upright old-fashioned figure bends under its load of affectionate distress.\n\nMrs. Bagnet, with the instinctive skill of a good warm heart, leaves the old housekeeper to her emotions for a little while—not without passing the back of her hand across her own motherly eyes—and presently chirps up in her cheery manner, “So I says to George when I goes to call him in to tea (he pretended to be smoking his pipe outside), ‘What ails you this afternoon, George, for gracious sake? I have seen all sorts, and I have seen you pretty often in season and out of season, abroad and at home, and I never see you so melancholy penitent.’ ‘Why, Mrs. Bagnet,’ says George, ‘it’s because I AM melancholy and penitent both, this afternoon, that you see me so.’ ‘What have you done, old fellow?’ I says. ‘Why, Mrs. Bagnet,’ says George, shaking his head, ‘what I have done has been done this many a long year, and is best not tried to be undone now. If I ever get to heaven it won’t be for being a good son to a widowed mother; I say no more.’ Now, ma’am, when George says to me that it’s best not tried to be undone now, I have my thoughts as I have often had before, and I draw it out of George how he comes to have such things on him that afternoon. Then George tells me that he has seen by chance, at the lawyer’s office, a fine old lady that has brought his mother plain before him, and he runs on about that old lady till he quite forgets himself and paints her picture to me as she used to be, years upon years back. So I says to George when he has done, who is this old lady he has seen? And George tells me it’s Mrs. Rouncewell, housekeeper for more than half a century to the Dedlock family down at Chesney Wold in Lincolnshire. George has frequently told me before that he’s a Lincolnshire man, and I says to my old Lignum that night, ‘Lignum, that’s his mother for five and for-ty pound!’”\n\nAll this Mrs. Bagnet now relates for the twentieth time at least within the last four hours. Trilling it out like a kind of bird, with a pretty high note, that it may be audible to the old lady above the hum of the wheels.\n\n“Bless you, and thank you,” says Mrs. Rouncewell. “Bless you, and thank you, my worthy soul!”\n\n“Dear heart!” cries Mrs. Bagnet in the most natural manner. “No thanks to me, I am sure. Thanks to yourself, ma’am, for being so ready to pay ’em! And mind once more, ma’am, what you had best do on finding George to be your own son is to make him—for your sake—have every sort of help to put himself in the right and clear himself of a charge of which he is as innocent as you or me. It won’t do to have truth and justice on his side; he must have law and lawyers,” exclaims the old girl, apparently persuaded that the latter form a separate establishment and have dissolved partnership with truth and justice for ever and a day.\n\n“He shall have,” says Mrs. Rouncewell, “all the help that can be got for him in the world, my dear. I will spend all I have, and thankfully, to procure it. Sir Leicester will do his best, the whole family will do their best. I—I know something, my dear; and will make my own appeal, as his mother parted from him all these years, and finding him in a jail at last.”\n\nThe extreme disquietude of the old housekeeper’s manner in saying this, her broken words, and her wringing of her hands make a powerful impression on Mrs. Bagnet and would astonish her but that she refers them all to her sorrow for her son’s condition. And yet Mrs. Bagnet wonders too why Mrs. Rouncewell should murmur so distractedly, “My Lady, my Lady, my Lady!” over and over again.\n\nThe frosty night wears away, and the dawn breaks, and the post-chaise comes rolling on through the early mist like the ghost of a chaise departed. It has plenty of spectral company in ghosts of trees and hedges, slowly vanishing and giving place to the realities of day. London reached, the travellers alight, the old housekeeper in great tribulation and confusion, Mrs. Bagnet quite fresh and collected—as she would be if her next point, with no new equipage and outfit, were the Cape of Good Hope, the Island of Ascension, Hong Kong, or any other military station.\n\nBut when they set out for the prison where the trooper is confined, the old lady has managed to draw about her, with her lavender-coloured dress, much of the staid calmness which is its usual accompaniment. A wonderfully grave, precise, and handsome piece of old china she looks, though her heart beats fast and her stomacher is ruffled more than even the remembrance of this wayward son has ruffled it these many years.\n\nApproaching the cell, they find the door opening and a warder in the act of coming out. The old girl promptly makes a sign of entreaty to him to say nothing; assenting with a nod, he suffers them to enter as he shuts the door.\n\nSo George, who is writing at his table, supposing himself to be alone, does not raise his eyes, but remains absorbed. The old housekeeper looks at him, and those wandering hands of hers are quite enough for Mrs. Bagnet’s confirmation, even if she could see the mother and the son together, knowing what she knows, and doubt their relationship.\n\nNot a rustle of the housekeeper’s dress, not a gesture, not a word betrays her. She stands looking at him as he writes on, all unconscious, and only her fluttering hands give utterance to her emotions. But they are very eloquent, very, very eloquent. Mrs. Bagnet understands them. They speak of gratitude, of joy, of grief, of hope; of inextinguishable affection, cherished with no return since this stalwart man was a stripling; of a better son loved less, and this son loved so fondly and so proudly; and they speak in such touching language that Mrs. Bagnet’s eyes brim up with tears and they run glistening down her sun-brown face.\n\n“George Rouncewell! Oh, my dear child, turn and look at me!”\n\nThe trooper starts up, clasps his mother round the neck, and falls down on his knees before her. Whether in a late repentance, whether in the first association that comes back upon him, he puts his hands together as a child does when it says its prayers, and raising them towards her breast, bows down his head, and cries.\n\n“My George, my dearest son! Always my favourite, and my favourite still, where have you been these cruel years and years? Grown such a man too, grown such a fine strong man. Grown so like what I knew he must be, if it pleased God he was alive!”\n\nShe can ask, and he can answer, nothing connected for a time. All that time the old girl, turned away, leans one arm against the whitened wall, leans her honest forehead upon it, wipes her eyes with her serviceable grey cloak, and quite enjoys herself like the best of old girls as she is.\n\n“Mother,” says the trooper when they are more composed, “forgive me first of all, for I know my need of it.”\n\nForgive him! She does it with all her heart and soul. She always has done it. She tells him how she has had it written in her will, these many years, that he was her beloved son George. She has never believed any ill of him, never. If she had died without this happiness—and she is an old woman now and can’t look to live very long—she would have blessed him with her last breath, if she had had her senses, as her beloved son George.\n\n“Mother, I have been an undutiful trouble to you, and I have my reward; but of late years I have had a kind of glimmering of a purpose in me too. When I left home I didn’t care much, mother—I am afraid not a great deal—for leaving; and went away and ’listed, harum-scarum, making believe to think that I cared for nobody, no not I, and that nobody cared for me.”\n\nThe trooper has dried his eyes and put away his handkerchief, but there is an extraordinary contrast between his habitual manner of expressing himself and carrying himself and the softened tone in which he speaks, interrupted occasionally by a half-stifled sob.\n\n“So I wrote a line home, mother, as you too well know, to say I had ’listed under another name, and I went abroad. Abroad, at one time I thought I would write home next year, when I might be better off; and when that year was out, I thought I would write home next year, when I might be better off; and when that year was out again, perhaps I didn’t think much about it. So on, from year to year, through a service of ten years, till I began to get older, and to ask myself why should I ever write.”\n\n“I don’t find any fault, child—but not to ease my mind, George? Not a word to your loving mother, who was growing older too?”\n\nThis almost overturns the trooper afresh, but he sets himself up with a great, rough, sounding clearance of his throat.\n\n“Heaven forgive me, mother, but I thought there would be small consolation then in hearing anything about me. There were you, respected and esteemed. There was my brother, as I read in chance North Country papers now and then, rising to be prosperous and famous. There was I a dragoon, roving, unsettled, not self-made like him, but self-unmade—all my earlier advantages thrown away, all my little learning unlearnt, nothing picked up but what unfitted me for most things that I could think of. What business had I to make myself known? After letting all that time go by me, what good could come of it? The worst was past with you, mother. I knew by that time (being a man) how you had mourned for me, and wept for me, and prayed for me; and the pain was over, or was softened down, and I was better in your mind as it was.”\n\nThe old lady sorrowfully shakes her head, and taking one of his powerful hands, lays it lovingly upon her shoulder.\n\n“No, I don’t say that it was so, mother, but that I made it out to be so. I said just now, what good could come of it? Well, my dear mother, some good might have come of it to myself—and there was the meanness of it. You would have sought me out; you would have purchased my discharge; you would have taken me down to Chesney Wold; you would have brought me and my brother and my brother’s family together; you would all have considered anxiously how to do something for me and set me up as a respectable civilian. But how could any of you feel sure of me when I couldn’t so much as feel sure of myself? How could you help regarding as an incumbrance and a discredit to you an idle dragooning chap who was an incumbrance and a discredit to himself, excepting under discipline? How could I look my brother’s children in the face and pretend to set them an example—I, the vagabond boy who had run away from home and been the grief and unhappiness of my mother’s life? ‘No, George.’ Such were my words, mother, when I passed this in review before me: ‘You have made your bed. Now, lie upon it.’”\n\nMrs. Rouncewell, drawing up her stately form, shakes her head at the old girl with a swelling pride upon her, as much as to say, “I told you so!” The old girl relieves her feelings and testifies her interest in the conversation by giving the trooper a great poke between the shoulders with her umbrella; this action she afterwards repeats, at intervals, in a species of affectionate lunacy, never failing, after the administration of each of these remonstrances, to resort to the whitened wall and the grey cloak again.\n\n“This was the way I brought myself to think, mother, that my best amends was to lie upon that bed I had made, and die upon it. And I should have done it (though I have been to see you more than once down at Chesney Wold, when you little thought of me) but for my old comrade’s wife here, who I find has been too many for me. But I thank her for it. I thank you for it, Mrs. Bagnet, with all my heart and might.”\n\nTo which Mrs. Bagnet responds with two pokes.\n\nAnd now the old lady impresses upon her son George, her own dear recovered boy, her joy and pride, the light of her eyes, the happy close of her life, and every fond name she can think of, that he must be governed by the best advice obtainable by money and influence, that he must yield up his case to the greatest lawyers that can be got, that he must act in this serious plight as he shall be advised to act and must not be self-willed, however right, but must promise to think only of his poor old mother’s anxiety and suffering until he is released, or he will break her heart.\n\n“Mother, ’tis little enough to consent to,” returns the trooper, stopping her with a kiss; “tell me what I shall do, and I’ll make a late beginning and do it. Mrs. Bagnet, you’ll take care of my mother, I know?”\n\nA very hard poke from the old girl’s umbrella.\n\n“If you’ll bring her acquainted with Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson, she will find them of her way of thinking, and they will give her the best advice and assistance.”\n\n“And, George,” says the old lady, “we must send with all haste for your brother. He is a sensible sound man as they tell me—out in the world beyond Chesney Wold, my dear, though I don’t know much of it myself—and will be of great service.”\n\n“Mother,” returns the trooper, “is it too soon to ask a favour?”\n\n“Surely not, my dear.”\n\n“Then grant me this one great favour. Don’t let my brother know.”\n\n“Not know what, my dear?”\n\n“Not know of me. In fact, mother, I can’t bear it; I can’t make up my mind to it. He has proved himself so different from me and has done so much to raise himself while I’ve been soldiering that I haven’t brass enough in my composition to see him in this place and under this charge. How could a man like him be expected to have any pleasure in such a discovery? It’s impossible. No, keep my secret from him, mother; do me a greater kindness than I deserve and keep my secret from my brother, of all men.”\n\n“But not always, dear George?”\n\n“Why, mother, perhaps not for good and all—though I may come to ask that too—but keep it now, I do entreat you. If it’s ever broke to him that his rip of a brother has turned up, I could wish,” says the trooper, shaking his head very doubtfully, “to break it myself and be governed as to advancing or retreating by the way in which he seems to take it.”\n\nAs he evidently has a rooted feeling on this point, and as the depth of it is recognized in Mrs. Bagnet’s face, his mother yields her implicit assent to what he asks. For this he thanks her kindly.\n\n“In all other respects, my dear mother, I’ll be as tractable and obedient as you can wish; on this one alone, I stand out. So now I am ready even for the lawyers. I have been drawing up,” he glances at his writing on the table, “an exact account of what I knew of the deceased and how I came to be involved in this unfortunate affair. It’s entered, plain and regular, like an orderly-book; not a word in it but what’s wanted for the facts. I did intend to read it, straight on end, whensoever I was called upon to say anything in my defence. I hope I may be let to do it still; but I have no longer a will of my own in this case, and whatever is said or done, I give my promise not to have any.”\n\nMatters being brought to this so far satisfactory pass, and time being on the wane, Mrs. Bagnet proposes a departure. Again and again the old lady hangs upon her son’s neck, and again and again the trooper holds her to his broad chest.\n\n“Where are you going to take my mother, Mrs. Bagnet?”\n\n“I am going to the town house, my dear, the family house. I have some business there that must be looked to directly,” Mrs. Rouncewell answers.\n\n“Will you see my mother safe there in a coach, Mrs. Bagnet? But of course I know you will. Why should I ask it!”\n\nWhy indeed, Mrs. Bagnet expresses with the umbrella.\n\n“Take her, my old friend, and take my gratitude along with you. Kisses to Quebec and Malta, love to my godson, a hearty shake of the hand to Lignum, and this for yourself, and I wish it was ten thousand pound in gold, my dear!” So saying, the trooper puts his lips to the old girl’s tanned forehead, and the door shuts upon him in his cell.\n\nNo entreaties on the part of the good old housekeeper will induce Mrs. Bagnet to retain the coach for her own conveyance home. Jumping out cheerfully at the door of the Dedlock mansion and handing Mrs. Rouncewell up the steps, the old girl shakes hands and trudges off, arriving soon afterwards in the bosom of the Bagnet family and falling to washing the greens as if nothing had happened.\n\nMy Lady is in that room in which she held her last conference with the murdered man, and is sitting where she sat that night, and is looking at the spot where he stood upon the hearth studying her so leisurely, when a tap comes at the door. Who is it? Mrs. Rouncewell. What has brought Mrs. Rouncewell to town so unexpectedly?\n\n“Trouble, my Lady. Sad trouble. Oh, my Lady, may I beg a word with you?”\n\nWhat new occurrence is it that makes this tranquil old woman tremble so? Far happier than her Lady, as her Lady has often thought, why does she falter in this manner and look at her with such strange mistrust?\n\n“What is the matter? Sit down and take your breath.”\n\n“Oh, my Lady, my Lady. I have found my son—my youngest, who went away for a soldier so long ago. And he is in prison.”\n\n“For debt?”\n\n“Oh, no, my Lady; I would have paid any debt, and joyful.”\n\n“For what is he in prison then?”\n\n“Charged with a murder, my Lady, of which he is as innocent as—as I am. Accused of the murder of Mr. Tulkinghorn.”\n\nWhat does she mean by this look and this imploring gesture? Why does she come so close? What is the letter that she holds?\n\n“Lady Dedlock, my dear Lady, my good Lady, my kind Lady! You must have a heart to feel for me, you must have a heart to forgive me. I was in this family before you were born. I am devoted to it. But think of my dear son wrongfully accused.”\n\n“I do not accuse him.”\n\n“No, my Lady, no. But others do, and he is in prison and in danger. Oh, Lady Dedlock, if you can say but a word to help to clear him, say it!”\n\nWhat delusion can this be? What power does she suppose is in the person she petitions to avert this unjust suspicion, if it be unjust? Her Lady’s handsome eyes regard her with astonishment, almost with fear.\n\n“My Lady, I came away last night from Chesney Wold to find my son in my old age, and the step upon the Ghost’s Walk was so constant and so solemn that I never heard the like in all these years. Night after night, as it has fallen dark, the sound has echoed through your rooms, but last night it was awfullest. And as it fell dark last night, my Lady, I got this letter.”\n\n“What letter is it?”\n\n“Hush! Hush!” The housekeeper looks round and answers in a frightened whisper, “My Lady, I have not breathed a word of it, I don’t believe what’s written in it, I know it can’t be true, I am sure and certain that it is not true. But my son is in danger, and you must have a heart to pity me. If you know of anything that is not known to others, if you have any suspicion, if you have any clue at all, and any reason for keeping it in your own breast, oh, my dear Lady, think of me, and conquer that reason, and let it be known! This is the most I consider possible. I know you are not a hard lady, but you go your own way always without help, and you are not familiar with your friends; and all who admire you—and all do—as a beautiful and elegant lady, know you to be one far away from themselves who can’t be approached close. My Lady, you may have some proud or angry reasons for disdaining to utter something that you know; if so, pray, oh, pray, think of a faithful servant whose whole life has been passed in this family which she dearly loves, and relent, and help to clear my son! My Lady, my good Lady,” the old housekeeper pleads with genuine simplicity, “I am so humble in my place and you are by nature so high and distant that you may not think what I feel for my child, but I feel so much that I have come here to make so bold as to beg and pray you not to be scornful of us if you can do us any right or justice at this fearful time!”\n\nLady Dedlock raises her without one word, until she takes the letter from her hand.\n\n“Am I to read this?”\n\n“When I am gone, my Lady, if you please, and then remembering the most that I consider possible.”\n\n“I know of nothing I can do. I know of nothing I reserve that can affect your son. I have never accused him.”\n\n“My Lady, you may pity him the more under a false accusation after reading the letter.”\n\nThe old housekeeper leaves her with the letter in her hand. In truth she is not a hard lady naturally, and the time has been when the sight of the venerable figure suing to her with such strong earnestness would have moved her to great compassion. But so long accustomed to suppress emotion and keep down reality, so long schooled for her own purposes in that destructive school which shuts up the natural feelings of the heart like flies in amber and spreads one uniform and dreary gloss over the good and bad, the feeling and the unfeeling, the sensible and the senseless, she had subdued even her wonder until now.\n\nShe opens the letter. Spread out upon the paper is a printed account of the discovery of the body as it lay face downward on the floor, shot through the heart; and underneath is written her own name, with the word “murderess” attached.\n\nIt falls out of her hand. How long it may have lain upon the ground she knows not, but it lies where it fell when a servant stands before her announcing the young man of the name of Guppy. The words have probably been repeated several times, for they are ringing in her head before she begins to understand them.\n\n“Let him come in!”\n\nHe comes in. Holding the letter in her hand, which she has taken from the floor, she tries to collect her thoughts. In the eyes of Mr. Guppy she is the same Lady Dedlock, holding the same prepared, proud, chilling state.\n\n“Your ladyship may not be at first disposed to excuse this visit from one who has never been welcome to your ladyship”—which he don’t complain of, for he is bound to confess that there never has been any particular reason on the face of things why he should be—“but I hope when I mention my motives to your ladyship you will not find fault with me,” says Mr. Guppy.\n\n“Do so.”\n\n“Thank your ladyship. I ought first to explain to your ladyship,” Mr. Guppy sits on the edge of a chair and puts his hat on the carpet at his feet, “that Miss Summerson, whose image, as I formerly mentioned to your ladyship, was at one period of my life imprinted on my ’eart until erased by circumstances over which I had no control, communicated to me, after I had the pleasure of waiting on your ladyship last, that she particularly wished me to take no steps whatever in any manner at all relating to her. And Miss Summerson’s wishes being to me a law (except as connected with circumstances over which I have no control), I consequently never expected to have the distinguished honour of waiting on your ladyship again.”\n\nAnd yet he is here now, Lady Dedlock moodily reminds him.\n\n“And yet I am here now,” Mr. Guppy admits. “My object being to communicate to your ladyship, under the seal of confidence, why I am here.”\n\nHe cannot do so, she tells him, too plainly or too briefly. “Nor can I,” Mr. Guppy returns with a sense of injury upon him, “too particularly request your ladyship to take particular notice that it’s no personal affair of mine that brings me here. I have no interested views of my own to serve in coming here. If it was not for my promise to Miss Summerson and my keeping of it sacred—I, in point of fact, shouldn’t have darkened these doors again, but should have seen ’em further first.”\n\nMr. Guppy considers this a favourable moment for sticking up his hair with both hands.\n\n“Your ladyship will remember when I mention it that the last time I was here I run against a party very eminent in our profession and whose loss we all deplore. That party certainly did from that time apply himself to cutting in against me in a way that I will call sharp practice, and did make it, at every turn and point, extremely difficult for me to be sure that I hadn’t inadvertently led up to something contrary to Miss Summerson’s wishes. Self-praise is no recommendation, but I may say for myself that I am not so bad a man of business neither.”\n\nLady Dedlock looks at him in stern inquiry. Mr. Guppy immediately withdraws his eyes from her face and looks anywhere else.\n\n“Indeed, it has been made so hard,” he goes on, “to have any idea what that party was up to in combination with others that until the loss which we all deplore I was gravelled—an expression which your ladyship, moving in the higher circles, will be so good as to consider tantamount to knocked over. Small likewise—a name by which I refer to another party, a friend of mine that your ladyship is not acquainted with—got to be so close and double-faced that at times it wasn’t easy to keep one’s hands off his ’ead. However, what with the exertion of my humble abilities, and what with the help of a mutual friend by the name of Mr. Tony Weevle (who is of a high aristocratic turn and has your ladyship’s portrait always hanging up in his room), I have now reasons for an apprehension as to which I come to put your ladyship upon your guard. First, will your ladyship allow me to ask you whether you have had any strange visitors this morning? I don’t mean fashionable visitors, but such visitors, for instance, as Miss Barbary’s old servant, or as a person without the use of his lower extremities, carried upstairs similarly to a guy?”\n“No!”\n\n“Then I assure your ladyship that such visitors have been here and have been received here. Because I saw them at the door, and waited at the corner of the square till they came out, and took half an hour’s turn afterwards to avoid them.”\n\n“What have I to do with that, or what have you? I do not understand you. What do you mean?”\n\n“Your ladyship, I come to put you on your guard. There may be no occasion for it. Very well. Then I have only done my best to keep my promise to Miss Summerson. I strongly suspect (from what Small has dropped, and from what we have corkscrewed out of him) that those letters I was to have brought to your ladyship were not destroyed when I supposed they were. That if there was anything to be blown upon, it IS blown upon. That the visitors I have alluded to have been here this morning to make money of it. And that the money is made, or making.”\n\nMr. Guppy picks up his hat and rises.\n\n“Your ladyship, you know best whether there’s anything in what I say or whether there’s nothing. Something or nothing, I have acted up to Miss Summerson’s wishes in letting things alone and in undoing what I had begun to do, as far as possible; that’s sufficient for me. In case I should be taking a liberty in putting your ladyship on your guard when there’s no necessity for it, you will endeavour, I should hope, to outlive my presumption, and I shall endeavour to outlive your disapprobation. I now take my farewell of your ladyship, and assure you that there’s no danger of your ever being waited on by me again.”\n\nShe scarcely acknowledges these parting words by any look, but when he has been gone a little while, she rings her bell.\n\n“Where is Sir Leicester?”\n\nMercury reports that he is at present shut up in the library alone.\n\n“Has Sir Leicester had any visitors this morning?”\n\nSeveral, on business. Mercury proceeds to a description of them, which has been anticipated by Mr. Guppy. Enough; he may go.\n\nSo! All is broken down. Her name is in these many mouths, her husband knows his wrongs, her shame will be published—may be spreading while she thinks about it—and in addition to the thunderbolt so long foreseen by her, so unforeseen by him, she is denounced by an invisible accuser as the murderess of her enemy.\n\nHer enemy he was, and she has often, often, often wished him dead. Her enemy he is, even in his grave. This dreadful accusation comes upon her like a new torment at his lifeless hand. And when she recalls how she was secretly at his door that night, and how she may be represented to have sent her favourite girl away so soon before merely to release herself from observation, she shudders as if the hangman’s hands were at her neck.\n\nShe has thrown herself upon the floor and lies with her hair all wildly scattered and her face buried in the cushions of a couch. She rises up, hurries to and fro, flings herself down again, and rocks and moans. The horror that is upon her is unutterable. If she really were the murderess, it could hardly be, for the moment, more intense.\n\nFor as her murderous perspective, before the doing of the deed, however subtle the precautions for its commission, would have been closed up by a gigantic dilatation of the hateful figure, preventing her from seeing any consequences beyond it; and as those consequences would have rushed in, in an unimagined flood, the moment the figure was laid low—which always happens when a murder is done; so, now she sees that when he used to be on the watch before her, and she used to think, “if some mortal stroke would but fall on this old man and take him from my way!” it was but wishing that all he held against her in his hand might be flung to the winds and chance-sown in many places. So, too, with the wicked relief she has felt in his death. What was his death but the key-stone of a gloomy arch removed, and now the arch begins to fall in a thousand fragments, each crushing and mangling piecemeal!\n\nThus, a terrible impression steals upon and overshadows her that from this pursuer, living or dead—obdurate and imperturbable before her in his well-remembered shape, or not more obdurate and imperturbable in his coffin-bed—there is no escape but in death. Hunted, she flies. The complication of her shame, her dread, remorse, and misery, overwhelms her at its height; and even her strength of self-reliance is overturned and whirled away like a leaf before a mighty wind.\n\nShe hurriedly addresses these lines to her husband, seals, and leaves them on her table:\n\nIf I am sought for, or accused of, his murder, believe that I am wholly innocent. Believe no other good of me, for I am innocent of nothing else that you have heard, or will hear, laid to my charge. He prepared me, on that fatal night, for his disclosure of my guilt to you. After he had left me, I went out on pretence of walking in the garden where I sometimes walk, but really to follow him and make one last petition that he would not protract the dreadful suspense on which I have been racked by him, you do not know how long, but would mercifully strike next morning.\n\nI found his house dark and silent. I rang twice at his door, but there was no reply, and I came home.\n\nI have no home left. I will encumber you no more. May you, in your just resentment, be able to forget the unworthy woman on whom you have wasted a most generous devotion—who avoids you only with a deeper shame than that with which she hurries from herself—and who writes this last adieu.\n\nShe veils and dresses quickly, leaves all her jewels and her money, listens, goes downstairs at a moment when the hall is empty, opens and shuts the great door, flutters away in the shrill frosty wind.\n\nPursuit\nImpassive, as behoves its high breeding, the Dedlock town house stares at the other houses in the street of dismal grandeur and gives no outward sign of anything going wrong within. Carriages rattle, doors are battered at, the world exchanges calls; ancient charmers with skeleton throats, and peachy cheeks that have a rather ghastly bloom upon them seen by daylight, when indeed these fascinating creatures look like Death and the Lady fused together, dazzle the eyes of men. Forth from the frigid mews come easily swinging carriages guided by short-legged coachmen in flaxen wigs, deep sunk into downy hammercloths, and up behind mount luscious Mercuries bearing sticks of state and wearing cocked hats broadwise, a spectacle for the angels.\n\nThe Dedlock town house changes not externally, and hours pass before its exalted dullness is disturbed within. But Volumnia the fair, being subject to the prevalent complaint of boredom and finding that disorder attacking her spirits with some virulence, ventures at length to repair to the library for change of scene. Her gentle tapping at the door producing no response, she opens it and peeps in; seeing no one there, takes possession.\n\nThe sprightly Dedlock is reputed, in that grass-grown city of the ancients, Bath, to be stimulated by an urgent curiosity which impels her on all convenient and inconvenient occasions to sidle about with a golden glass at her eye, peering into objects of every description. Certain it is that she avails herself of the present opportunity of hovering over her kinsman’s letters and papers like a bird, taking a short peck at this document and a blink with her head on one side at that document, and hopping about from table to table with her glass at her eye in an inquisitive and restless manner. In the course of these researches she stumbles over something, and turning her glass in that direction, sees her kinsman lying on the ground like a felled tree.\n\nVolumnia’s pet little scream acquires a considerable augmentation of reality from this surprise, and the house is quickly in commotion. Servants tear up and down stairs, bells are violently rung, doctors are sent for, and Lady Dedlock is sought in all directions, but not found. Nobody has seen or heard her since she last rang her bell. Her letter to Sir Leicester is discovered on her table, but it is doubtful yet whether he has not received another missive from another world requiring to be personally answered, and all the living languages, and all the dead, are as one to him.\n\nThey lay him down upon his bed, and chafe, and rub, and fan, and put ice to his head, and try every means of restoration. Howbeit, the day has ebbed away, and it is night in his room before his stertorous breathing lulls or his fixed eyes show any consciousness of the candle that is occasionally passed before them. But when this change begins, it goes on; and by and by he nods or moves his eyes or even his hand in token that he hears and comprehends.\n\nHe fell down, this morning, a handsome stately gentleman, somewhat infirm, but of a fine presence, and with a well-filled face. He lies upon his bed, an aged man with sunken cheeks, the decrepit shadow of himself. His voice was rich and mellow and he had so long been thoroughly persuaded of the weight and import to mankind of any word he said that his words really had come to sound as if there were something in them. But now he can only whisper, and what he whispers sounds like what it is—mere jumble and jargon.\n\nHis favourite and faithful housekeeper stands at his bedside. It is the first act he notices, and he clearly derives pleasure from it. After vainly trying to make himself understood in speech, he makes signs for a pencil. So inexpressively that they cannot at first understand him; it is his old housekeeper who makes out what he wants and brings in a slate.\n\nAfter pausing for some time, he slowly scrawls upon it in a hand that is not his, “Chesney Wold?”\n\nNo, she tells him; he is in London. He was taken ill in the library this morning. Right thankful she is that she happened to come to London and is able to attend upon him.\n\n“It is not an illness of any serious consequence, Sir Leicester. You will be much better to-morrow, Sir Leicester. All the gentlemen say so.” This, with the tears coursing down her fair old face.\n\nAfter making a survey of the room and looking with particular attention all round the bed where the doctors stand, he writes, “My Lady.”\n\n“My Lady went out, Sir Leicester, before you were taken ill, and don’t know of your illness yet.”\n\nHe points again, in great agitation, at the two words. They all try to quiet him, but he points again with increased agitation. On their looking at one another, not knowing what to say, he takes the slate once more and writes “My Lady. For God’s sake, where?” And makes an imploring moan.\n\nIt is thought better that his old housekeeper should give him Lady Dedlock’s letter, the contents of which no one knows or can surmise. She opens it for him and puts it out for his perusal. Having read it twice by a great effort, he turns it down so that it shall not be seen and lies moaning. He passes into a kind of relapse or into a swoon, and it is an hour before he opens his eyes, reclining on his faithful and attached old servant’s arm. The doctors know that he is best with her, and when not actively engaged about him, stand aloof.\n\nThe slate comes into requisition again, but the word he wants to write he cannot remember. His anxiety, his eagerness, and affliction at this pass are pitiable to behold. It seems as if he must go mad in the necessity he feels for haste and the inability under which he labours of expressing to do what or to fetch whom. He has written the letter B, and there stopped. Of a sudden, in the height of his misery, he puts Mr. before it. The old housekeeper suggests Bucket. Thank heaven! That’s his meaning.\n\nMr. Bucket is found to be downstairs, by appointment. Shall he come up?\n\nThere is no possibility of misconstruing Sir Leicester’s burning wish to see him or the desire he signifies to have the room cleared of every one but the housekeeper. It is speedily done, and Mr. Bucket appears. Of all men upon earth, Sir Leicester seems fallen from his high estate to place his sole trust and reliance upon this man.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I’m sorry to see you like this. I hope you’ll cheer up. I’m sure you will, on account of the family credit.”\n\nSir Leicester puts her letter in his hands and looks intently in his face while he reads it. A new intelligence comes into Mr. Bucket’s eye as he reads on; with one hook of his finger, while that eye is still glancing over the words, he indicates, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I understand you.”\n\nSir Leicester writes upon the slate. “Full forgiveness. Find—” Mr. Bucket stops his hand.\n\n“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I’ll find her. But my search after her must be begun out of hand. Not a minute must be lost.”\n\nWith the quickness of thought, he follows Sir Leicester Dedlock’s look towards a little box upon a table.\n\n“Bring it here, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet? Certainly. Open it with one of these here keys? Certainly. The littlest key? TO be sure. Take the notes out? So I will. Count ’em? That’s soon done. Twenty and thirty’s fifty, and twenty’s seventy, and fifty’s one twenty, and forty’s one sixty. Take ’em for expenses? That I’ll do, and render an account of course. Don’t spare money? No I won’t.”\n\nThe velocity and certainty of Mr. Bucket’s interpretation on all these heads is little short of miraculous. Mrs. Rouncewell, who holds the light, is giddy with the swiftness of his eyes and hands as he starts up, furnished for his journey.\n\n“You’re George’s mother, old lady; that’s about what you are, I believe?” says Mr. Bucket aside, with his hat already on and buttoning his coat.\n\n“Yes, sir, I am his distressed mother.”\n\n“So I thought, according to what he mentioned to me just now. Well, then, I’ll tell you something. You needn’t be distressed no more. Your son’s all right. Now, don’t you begin a-crying, because what you’ve got to do is to take care of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and you won’t do that by crying. As to your son, he’s all right, I tell you; and he sends his loving duty, and hoping you’re the same. He’s discharged honourable; that’s about what HE is; with no more imputation on his character than there is on yours, and yours is a tidy one, I’LL bet a pound. You may trust me, for I took your son. He conducted himself in a game way, too, on that occasion; and he’s a fine-made man, and you’re a fine-made old lady, and you’re a mother and son, the pair of you, as might be showed for models in a caravan. Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, what you’ve trusted to me I’ll go through with. Don’t you be afraid of my turning out of my way, right or left, or taking a sleep, or a wash, or a shave till I have found what I go in search of. Say everything as is kind and forgiving on your part? Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I will. And I wish you better, and these family affairs smoothed over—as, Lord, many other family affairs equally has been, and equally will be, to the end of time.”\n\nWith this peroration, Mr. Bucket, buttoned up, goes quietly out, looking steadily before him as if he were already piercing the night in quest of the fugitive.\n\nHis first step is to take himself to Lady Dedlock’s rooms and look all over them for any trifling indication that may help him. The rooms are in darkness now; and to see Mr. Bucket with a wax-light in his hand, holding it above his head and taking a sharp mental inventory of the many delicate objects so curiously at variance with himself, would be to see a sight—which nobody DOES see, as he is particular to lock himself in.\n\n“A spicy boudoir, this,” says Mr. Bucket, who feels in a manner furbished up in his French by the blow of the morning. “Must have cost a sight of money. Rum articles to cut away from, these; she must have been hard put to it!”\n\nOpening and shutting table-drawers and looking into caskets and jewel-cases, he sees the reflection of himself in various mirrors, and moralizes thereon.\n\n“One might suppose I was a-moving in the fashionable circles and getting myself up for Almack’s,” says Mr. Bucket. “I begin to think I must be a swell in the Guards without knowing it.”\n\nEver looking about, he has opened a dainty little chest in an inner drawer. His great hand, turning over some gloves which it can scarcely feel, they are so light and soft within it, comes upon a white handkerchief.\n\n“Hum! Let’s have a look at YOU,” says Mr. Bucket, putting down the light. “What should YOU be kept by yourself for? What’s YOUR motive? Are you her ladyship’s property, or somebody else’s? You’ve got a mark upon you somewheres or another, I suppose?”\n\nHe finds it as he speaks, “Esther Summerson.”\n\n“Oh!” says Mr. Bucket, pausing, with his finger at his ear. “Come, I’ll take YOU.”\n\nHe completes his observations as quietly and carefully as he has carried them on, leaves everything else precisely as he found it, glides away after some five minutes in all, and passes into the street. With a glance upward at the dimly lighted windows of Sir Leicester’s room, he sets off, full-swing, to the nearest coach-stand, picks out the horse for his money, and directs to be driven to the shooting gallery. Mr. Bucket does not claim to be a scientific judge of horses, but he lays out a little money on the principal events in that line, and generally sums up his knowledge of the subject in the remark that when he sees a horse as can go, he knows him.\n\nHis knowledge is not at fault in the present instance. Clattering over the stones at a dangerous pace, yet thoughtfully bringing his keen eyes to bear on every slinking creature whom he passes in the midnight streets, and even on the lights in upper windows where people are going or gone to bed, and on all the turnings that he rattles by, and alike on the heavy sky, and on the earth where the snow lies thin—for something may present itself to assist him, anywhere—he dashes to his destination at such a speed that when he stops the horse half smothers him in a cloud of steam.\n\n“Unbear him half a moment to freshen him up, and I’ll be back.”\n\nHe runs up the long wooden entry and finds the trooper smoking his pipe.\n\n“I thought I should, George, after what you have gone through, my lad. I haven’t a word to spare. Now, honour! All to save a woman. Miss Summerson that was here when Gridley died—that was the name, I know—all right—where does she live?”\n\nThe trooper has just come from there and gives him the address, near Oxford Street.\n\n“You won’t repent it, George. Good night!”\n\nHe is off again, with an impression of having seen Phil sitting by the frosty fire staring at him open-mouthed, and gallops away again, and gets out in a cloud of steam again.\n\nMr. Jarndyce, the only person up in the house, is just going to bed, rises from his book on hearing the rapid ringing at the bell, and comes down to the door in his dressing-gown.\n\n“Don’t be alarmed, sir.” In a moment his visitor is confidential with him in the hall, has shut the door, and stands with his hand upon the lock. “I’ve had the pleasure of seeing you before. Inspector Bucket. Look at that handkerchief, sir, Miss Esther Summerson’s. Found it myself put away in a drawer of Lady Dedlock’s, quarter of an hour ago. Not a moment to lose. Matter of life or death. You know Lady Dedlock?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“There has been a discovery there to-day. Family affairs have come out. Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, has had a fit—apoplexy or paralysis—and couldn’t be brought to, and precious time has been lost. Lady Dedlock disappeared this afternoon and left a letter for him that looks bad. Run your eye over it. Here it is!”\n\nMr. Jarndyce, having read it, asks him what he thinks.\n\n“I don’t know. It looks like suicide. Anyways, there’s more and more danger, every minute, of its drawing to that. I’d give a hundred pound an hour to have got the start of the present time. Now, Mr. Jarndyce, I am employed by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to follow her and find her, to save her and take her his forgiveness. I have money and full power, but I want something else. I want Miss Summerson.”\n\nMr. Jarndyce in a troubled voice repeats, “Miss Summerson?”\n\n“Now, Mr. Jarndyce”—Mr. Bucket has read his face with the greatest attention all along—“I speak to you as a gentleman of a humane heart, and under such pressing circumstances as don’t often happen. If ever delay was dangerous, it’s dangerous now; and if ever you couldn’t afterwards forgive yourself for causing it, this is the time. Eight or ten hours, worth, as I tell you, a hundred pound apiece at least, have been lost since Lady Dedlock disappeared. I am charged to find her. I am Inspector Bucket. Besides all the rest that’s heavy on her, she has upon her, as she believes, suspicion of murder. If I follow her alone, she, being in ignorance of what Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, has communicated to me, may be driven to desperation. But if I follow her in company with a young lady, answering to the description of a young lady that she has a tenderness for—I ask no question, and I say no more than that—she will give me credit for being friendly. Let me come up with her and be able to have the hold upon her of putting that young lady for’ard, and I’ll save her and prevail with her if she is alive. Let me come up with her alone—a hard matter—and I’ll do my best, but I don’t answer for what the best may be. Time flies; it’s getting on for one o’clock. When one strikes, there’s another hour gone, and it’s worth a thousand pound now instead of a hundred.”\n\nThis is all true, and the pressing nature of the case cannot be questioned. Mr. Jarndyce begs him to remain there while he speaks to Miss Summerson. Mr. Bucket says he will, but acting on his usual principle, does no such thing, following upstairs instead and keeping his man in sight. So he remains, dodging and lurking about in the gloom of the staircase while they confer. In a very little time Mr. Jarndyce comes down and tells him that Miss Summerson will join him directly and place herself under his protection to accompany him where he pleases. Mr. Bucket, satisfied, expresses high approval and awaits her coming at the door.\n\nThere he mounts a high tower in his mind and looks out far and wide. Many solitary figures he perceives creeping through the streets; many solitary figures out on heaths, and roads, and lying under haystacks. But the figure that he seeks is not among them. Other solitaries he perceives, in nooks of bridges, looking over; and in shadowed places down by the river’s level; and a dark, dark, shapeless object drifting with the tide, more solitary than all, clings with a drowning hold on his attention.\n\nWhere is she? Living or dead, where is she? If, as he folds the handkerchief and carefully puts it up, it were able with an enchanted power to bring before him the place where she found it and the night-landscape near the cottage where it covered the little child, would he descry her there? On the waste where the brick-kilns are burning with a pale blue flare, where the straw-roofs of the wretched huts in which the bricks are made are being scattered by the wind, where the clay and water are hard frozen and the mill in which the gaunt blind horse goes round all day looks like an instrument of human torture—traversing this deserted, blighted spot there is a lonely figure with the sad world to itself, pelted by the snow and driven by the wind, and cast out, it would seem, from all companionship. It is the figure of a woman, too; but it is miserably dressed, and no such clothes ever came through the hall and out at the great door of the Dedlock mansion.\nEsther’s Narrative\n\nI had gone to bed and fallen asleep when my guardian knocked at the door of my room and begged me to get up directly. On my hurrying to speak to him and learn what had happened, he told me, after a word or two of preparation, that there had been a discovery at Sir Leicester Dedlock’s. That my mother had fled, that a person was now at our door who was empowered to convey to her the fullest assurances of affectionate protection and forgiveness if he could possibly find her, and that I was sought for to accompany him in the hope that my entreaties might prevail upon her if his failed. Something to this general purpose I made out, but I was thrown into such a tumult of alarm, and hurry and distress, that in spite of every effort I could make to subdue my agitation, I did not seem, to myself, fully to recover my right mind until hours had passed.\n\nBut I dressed and wrapped up expeditiously without waking Charley or any one and went down to Mr. Bucket, who was the person entrusted with the secret. In taking me to him my guardian told me this, and also explained how it was that he had come to think of me. Mr. Bucket, in a low voice, by the light of my guardian’s candle, read to me in the hall a letter that my mother had left upon her table; and I suppose within ten minutes of my having been aroused I was sitting beside him, rolling swiftly through the streets.\n\nHis manner was very keen, and yet considerate when he explained to me that a great deal might depend on my being able to answer, without confusion, a few questions that he wished to ask me. These were, chiefly, whether I had had much communication with my mother (to whom he only referred as Lady Dedlock), when and where I had spoken with her last, and how she had become possessed of my handkerchief. When I had satisfied him on these points, he asked me particularly to consider—taking time to think—whether within my knowledge there was any one, no matter where, in whom she might be at all likely to confide under circumstances of the last necessity. I could think of no one but my guardian. But by and by I mentioned Mr. Boythorn. He came into my mind as connected with his old chivalrous manner of mentioning my mother’s name and with what my guardian had informed me of his engagement to her sister and his unconscious connexion with her unhappy story.\n\nMy companion had stopped the driver while we held this conversation, that we might the better hear each other. He now told him to go on again and said to me, after considering within himself for a few moments, that he had made up his mind how to proceed. He was quite willing to tell me what his plan was, but I did not feel clear enough to understand it.\n\nWe had not driven very far from our lodgings when we stopped in a by-street at a public-looking place lighted up with gas. Mr. Bucket took me in and sat me in an arm-chair by a bright fire. It was now past one, as I saw by the clock against the wall. Two police officers, looking in their perfectly neat uniform not at all like people who were up all night, were quietly writing at a desk; and the place seemed very quiet altogether, except for some beating and calling out at distant doors underground, to which nobody paid any attention.\n\nA third man in uniform, whom Mr. Bucket called and to whom he whispered his instructions, went out; and then the two others advised together while one wrote from Mr. Bucket’s subdued dictation. It was a description of my mother that they were busy with, for Mr. Bucket brought it to me when it was done and read it in a whisper. It was very accurate indeed.\n\nThe second officer, who had attended to it closely, then copied it out and called in another man in uniform (there were several in an outer room), who took it up and went away with it. All this was done with the greatest dispatch and without the waste of a moment; yet nobody was at all hurried. As soon as the paper was sent out upon its travels, the two officers resumed their former quiet work of writing with neatness and care. Mr. Bucket thoughtfully came and warmed the soles of his boots, first one and then the other, at the fire.\n\n“Are you well wrapped up, Miss Summerson?” he asked me as his eyes met mine. “It’s a desperate sharp night for a young lady to be out in.”\n\nI told him I cared for no weather and was warmly clothed.\n\n“It may be a long job,” he observed; “but so that it ends well, never mind, miss.”\n\n“I pray to heaven it may end well!” said I.\n\nHe nodded comfortingly. “You see, whatever you do, don’t you go and fret yourself. You keep yourself cool and equal for anything that may happen, and it’ll be the better for you, the better for me, the better for Lady Dedlock, and the better for Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”\n\nHe was really very kind and gentle, and as he stood before the fire warming his boots and rubbing his face with his forefinger, I felt a confidence in his sagacity which reassured me. It was not yet a quarter to two when I heard horses’ feet and wheels outside. “Now, Miss Summerson,” said he, “we are off, if you please!”\n\nHe gave me his arm, and the two officers courteously bowed me out, and we found at the door a phaeton or barouche with a postilion and post horses. Mr. Bucket handed me in and took his own seat on the box. The man in uniform whom he had sent to fetch this equipage then handed him up a dark lantern at his request, and when he had given a few directions to the driver, we rattled away.\n\nI was far from sure that I was not in a dream. We rattled with great rapidity through such a labyrinth of streets that I soon lost all idea where we were, except that we had crossed and re-crossed the river, and still seemed to be traversing a low-lying, waterside, dense neighbourhood of narrow thoroughfares chequered by docks and basins, high piles of warehouses, swing-bridges, and masts of ships. At length we stopped at the corner of a little slimy turning, which the wind from the river, rushing up it, did not purify; and I saw my companion, by the light of his lantern, in conference with several men who looked like a mixture of police and sailors. Against the mouldering wall by which they stood, there was a bill, on which I could discern the words, “Found Drowned”; and this and an inscription about drags possessed me with the awful suspicion shadowed forth in our visit to that place.\n\nI had no need to remind myself that I was not there by the indulgence of any feeling of mine to increase the difficulties of the search, or to lessen its hopes, or enhance its delays. I remained quiet, but what I suffered in that dreadful spot I never can forget. And still it was like the horror of a dream. A man yet dark and muddy, in long swollen sodden boots and a hat like them, was called out of a boat and whispered with Mr. Bucket, who went away with him down some slippery steps—as if to look at something secret that he had to show. They came back, wiping their hands upon their coats, after turning over something wet; but thank God it was not what I feared!\n\nAfter some further conference, Mr. Bucket (whom everybody seemed to know and defer to) went in with the others at a door and left me in the carriage, while the driver walked up and down by his horses to warm himself. The tide was coming in, as I judged from the sound it made, and I could hear it break at the end of the alley with a little rush towards me. It never did so—and I thought it did so, hundreds of times, in what can have been at the most a quarter of an hour, and probably was less—but the thought shuddered through me that it would cast my mother at the horses’ feet.\n\nMr. Bucket came out again, exhorting the others to be vigilant, darkened his lantern, and once more took his seat. “Don’t you be alarmed, Miss Summerson, on account of our coming down here,” he said, turning to me. “I only want to have everything in train and to know that it is in train by looking after it myself. Get on, my lad!”\n\nWe appeared to retrace the way we had come. Not that I had taken note of any particular objects in my perturbed state of mind, but judging from the general character of the streets. We called at another office or station for a minute and crossed the river again. During the whole of this time, and during the whole search, my companion, wrapped up on the box, never relaxed in his vigilance a single moment; but when we crossed the bridge he seemed, if possible, to be more on the alert than before. He stood up to look over the parapet, he alighted and went back after a shadowy female figure that flitted past us, and he gazed into the profound black pit of water with a face that made my heart die within me. The river had a fearful look, so overcast and secret, creeping away so fast between the low flat lines of shore—so heavy with indistinct and awful shapes, both of substance and shadow; so death-like and mysterious. I have seen it many times since then, by sunlight and by moonlight, but never free from the impressions of that journey. In my memory the lights upon the bridge are always burning dim, the cutting wind is eddying round the homeless woman whom we pass, the monotonous wheels are whirling on, and the light of the carriage-lamps reflected back looks palely in upon me—a face rising out of the dreaded water.\n\nClattering and clattering through the empty streets, we came at length from the pavement on to dark smooth roads and began to leave the houses behind us. After a while I recognized the familiar way to Saint Albans. At Barnet fresh horses were ready for us, and we changed and went on. It was very cold indeed, and the open country was white with snow, though none was falling then.\n\n“An old acquaintance of yours, this road, Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Bucket cheerfully.\n\n“Yes,” I returned. “Have you gathered any intelligence?”\n\n“None that can be quite depended on as yet,” he answered, “but it’s early times as yet.”\n\nHe had gone into every late or early public-house where there was a light (they were not a few at that time, the road being then much frequented by drovers) and had got down to talk to the turnpike-keepers. I had heard him ordering drink, and chinking money, and making himself agreeable and merry everywhere; but whenever he took his seat upon the box again, his face resumed its watchful steady look, and he always said to the driver in the same business tone, “Get on, my lad!”\n\nWith all these stoppages, it was between five and six o’clock and we were yet a few miles short of Saint Albans when he came out of one of these houses and handed me in a cup of tea.\n\n“Drink it, Miss Summerson, it’ll do you good. You’re beginning to get more yourself now, ain’t you?”\n\nI thanked him and said I hoped so.\n\n“You was what you may call stunned at first,” he returned; “and Lord, no wonder! Don’t speak loud, my dear. It’s all right. She’s on ahead.”\n\nI don’t know what joyful exclamation I made or was going to make, but he put up his finger and I stopped myself.\n\n“Passed through here on foot this evening about eight or nine. I heard of her first at the archway toll, over at Highgate, but couldn’t make quite sure. Traced her all along, on and off. Picked her up at one place, and dropped her at another; but she’s before us now, safe. Take hold of this cup and saucer, ostler. Now, if you wasn’t brought up to the butter trade, look out and see if you can catch half a crown in your t’other hand. One, two, three, and there you are! Now, my lad, try a gallop!”\n\nWe were soon in Saint Albans and alighted a little before day, when I was just beginning to arrange and comprehend the occurrences of the night and really to believe that they were not a dream. Leaving the carriage at the posting-house and ordering fresh horses to be ready, my companion gave me his arm, and we went towards home.\n\n“As this is your regular abode, Miss Summerson, you see,” he observed, “I should like to know whether you’ve been asked for by any stranger answering the description, or whether Mr. Jarndyce has. I don’t much expect it, but it might be.”\n\nAs we ascended the hill, he looked about him with a sharp eye—the day was now breaking—and reminded me that I had come down it one night, as I had reason for remembering, with my little servant and poor Jo, whom he called Toughey.\n\nI wondered how he knew that.\n\n“When you passed a man upon the road, just yonder, you know,” said Mr. Bucket.\n\nYes, I remembered that too, very well.\n\n“That was me,” said Mr. Bucket.\n\nSeeing my surprise, he went on, “I drove down in a gig that afternoon to look after that boy. You might have heard my wheels when you came out to look after him yourself, for I was aware of you and your little maid going up when I was walking the horse down. Making an inquiry or two about him in the town, I soon heard what company he was in and was coming among the brick-fields to look for him when I observed you bringing him home here.”\n\n“Had he committed any crime?” I asked.\n\n“None was charged against him,” said Mr. Bucket, coolly lifting off his hat, “but I suppose he wasn’t over-particular. No. What I wanted him for was in connexion with keeping this very matter of Lady Dedlock quiet. He had been making his tongue more free than welcome as to a small accidental service he had been paid for by the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn; and it wouldn’t do, at any sort of price, to have him playing those games. So having warned him out of London, I made an afternoon of it to warn him to keep out of it now he WAS away, and go farther from it, and maintain a bright look-out that I didn’t catch him coming back again.”\n\n“Poor creature!” said I.\n\n“Poor enough,” assented Mr. Bucket, “and trouble enough, and well enough away from London, or anywhere else. I was regularly turned on my back when I found him taken up by your establishment, I do assure you.”\n\nI asked him why. “Why, my dear?” said Mr. Bucket. “Naturally there was no end to his tongue then. He might as well have been born with a yard and a half of it, and a remnant over.”\n\nAlthough I remember this conversation now, my head was in confusion at the time, and my power of attention hardly did more than enable me to understand that he entered into these particulars to divert me. With the same kind intention, manifestly, he often spoke to me of indifferent things, while his face was busy with the one object that we had in view. He still pursued this subject as we turned in at the garden-gate.\n\n“Ah!” said Mr. Bucket. “Here we are, and a nice retired place it is. Puts a man in mind of the country house in the Woodpecker-tapping, that was known by the smoke which so gracefully curled. They’re early with the kitchen fire, and that denotes good servants. But what you’ve always got to be careful of with servants is who comes to see ’em; you never know what they’re up to if you don’t know that. And another thing, my dear. Whenever you find a young man behind the kitchen-door, you give that young man in charge on suspicion of being secreted in a dwelling-house with an unlawful purpose.”\n\nWe were now in front of the house; he looked attentively and closely at the gravel for footprints before he raised his eyes to the windows.\n\n“Do you generally put that elderly young gentleman in the same room when he’s on a visit here, Miss Summerson?” he inquired, glancing at Mr. Skimpole’s usual chamber.\n\n“You know Mr. Skimpole!” said I.\n\n“What do you call him again?” returned Mr. Bucket, bending down his ear. “Skimpole, is it? I’ve often wondered what his name might be. Skimpole. Not John, I should say, nor yet Jacob?”\n\n“Harold,” I told him.\n\n“Harold. Yes. He’s a queer bird is Harold,” said Mr. Bucket, eyeing me with great expression.\n\n“He is a singular character,” said I.\n\n“No idea of money,” observed Mr. Bucket. “He takes it, though!”\n\nI involuntarily returned for answer that I perceived Mr. Bucket knew him.\n\n“Why, now I’ll tell you, Miss Summerson,” he replied. “Your mind will be all the better for not running on one point too continually, and I’ll tell you for a change. It was him as pointed out to me where Toughey was. I made up my mind that night to come to the door and ask for Toughey, if that was all; but willing to try a move or so first, if any such was on the board, I just pitched up a morsel of gravel at that window where I saw a shadow. As soon as Harold opens it and I have had a look at him, thinks I, you’re the man for me. So I smoothed him down a bit about not wanting to disturb the family after they was gone to bed and about its being a thing to be regretted that charitable young ladies should harbour vagrants; and then, when I pretty well understood his ways, I said I should consider a fypunnote well bestowed if I could relieve the premises of Toughey without causing any noise or trouble. Then says he, lifting up his eyebrows in the gayest way, ‘It’s no use mentioning a fypunnote to me, my friend, because I’m a mere child in such matters and have no idea of money.’ Of course I understood what his taking it so easy meant; and being now quite sure he was the man for me, I wrapped the note round a little stone and threw it up to him. Well! He laughs and beams, and looks as innocent as you like, and says, ‘But I don’t know the value of these things. What am I to DO with this?’ ‘Spend it, sir,’ says I. ‘But I shall be taken in,’ he says, ‘they won’t give me the right change, I shall lose it, it’s no use to me.’ Lord, you never saw such a face as he carried it with! Of course he told me where to find Toughey, and I found him.”\n\nI regarded this as very treacherous on the part of Mr. Skimpole towards my guardian and as passing the usual bounds of his childish innocence.\n\n“Bounds, my dear?” returned Mr. Bucket. “Bounds? Now, Miss Summerson, I’ll give you a piece of advice that your husband will find useful when you are happily married and have got a family about you. Whenever a person says to you that they are as innocent as can be in all concerning money, look well after your own money, for they are dead certain to collar it if they can. Whenever a person proclaims to you ‘In worldly matters I’m a child,’ you consider that that person is only a-crying off from being held accountable and that you have got that person’s number, and it’s Number One. Now, I am not a poetical man myself, except in a vocal way when it goes round a company, but I’m a practical one, and that’s my experience. So’s this rule. Fast and loose in one thing, fast and loose in everything. I never knew it fail. No more will you. Nor no one. With which caution to the unwary, my dear, I take the liberty of pulling this here bell, and so go back to our business.”\n\nI believe it had not been for a moment out of his mind, any more than it had been out of my mind, or out of his face. The whole household were amazed to see me, without any notice, at that time in the morning, and so accompanied; and their surprise was not diminished by my inquiries. No one, however, had been there. It could not be doubted that this was the truth.\n\n“Then, Miss Summerson,” said my companion, “we can’t be too soon at the cottage where those brickmakers are to be found. Most inquiries there I leave to you, if you’ll be so good as to make ’em. The naturalest way is the best way, and the naturalest way is your own way.”\n\nWe set off again immediately. On arriving at the cottage, we found it shut up and apparently deserted, but one of the neighbours who knew me and who came out when I was trying to make some one hear informed me that the two women and their husbands now lived together in another house, made of loose rough bricks, which stood on the margin of the piece of ground where the kilns were and where the long rows of bricks were drying. We lost no time in repairing to this place, which was within a few hundred yards; and as the door stood ajar, I pushed it open.\n\nThere were only three of them sitting at breakfast, the child lying asleep on a bed in the corner. It was Jenny, the mother of the dead child, who was absent. The other woman rose on seeing me; and the men, though they were, as usual, sulky and silent, each gave me a morose nod of recognition. A look passed between them when Mr. Bucket followed me in, and I was surprised to see that the woman evidently knew him.\n\nI had asked leave to enter of course. Liz (the only name by which I knew her) rose to give me her own chair, but I sat down on a stool near the fire, and Mr. Bucket took a corner of the bedstead. Now that I had to speak and was among people with whom I was not familiar, I became conscious of being hurried and giddy. It was very difficult to begin, and I could not help bursting into tears.\n\n“Liz,” said I, “I have come a long way in the night and through the snow to inquire after a lady—”\n\n“Who has been here, you know,” Mr. Bucket struck in, addressing the whole group with a composed propitiatory face; “that’s the lady the young lady means. The lady that was here last night, you know.”\n\n“And who told YOU as there was anybody here?” inquired Jenny’s husband, who had made a surly stop in his eating to listen and now measured him with his eye.\n\n“A person of the name of Michael Jackson, with a blue welveteen waistcoat with a double row of mother of pearl buttons,” Mr. Bucket immediately answered.\n\n“He had as good mind his own business, whoever he is,” growled the man.\n\n“He’s out of employment, I believe,” said Mr. Bucket apologetically for Michael Jackson, “and so gets talking.”\n\nThe woman had not resumed her chair, but stood faltering with her hand upon its broken back, looking at me. I thought she would have spoken to me privately if she had dared. She was still in this attitude of uncertainty when her husband, who was eating with a lump of bread and fat in one hand and his clasp-knife in the other, struck the handle of his knife violently on the table and told her with an oath to mind HER own business at any rate and sit down.\n\n“I should like to have seen Jenny very much,” said I, “for I am sure she would have told me all she could about this lady, whom I am very anxious indeed—you cannot think how anxious—to overtake. Will Jenny be here soon? Where is she?”\n\nThe woman had a great desire to answer, but the man, with another oath, openly kicked at her foot with his heavy boot. He left it to Jenny’s husband to say what he chose, and after a dogged silence the latter turned his shaggy head towards me.\n\n“I’m not partial to gentlefolks coming into my place, as you’ve heerd me say afore now, I think, miss. I let their places be, and it’s curious they can’t let my place be. There’d be a pretty shine made if I was to go a-wisitin THEM, I think. Howsoever, I don’t so much complain of you as of some others, and I’m agreeable to make you a civil answer, though I give notice that I’m not a-going to be drawed like a badger. Will Jenny be here soon? No she won’t. Where is she? She’s gone up to Lunnun.”\n\n“Did she go last night?” I asked.\n\n“Did she go last night? Ah! She went last night,” he answered with a sulky jerk of his head.\n\n“But was she here when the lady came? And what did the lady say to her? And where is the lady gone? I beg and pray you to be so kind as to tell me,” said I, “for I am in great distress to know.”\n\n“If my master would let me speak, and not say a word of harm—” the woman timidly began.\n\n“Your master,” said her husband, muttering an imprecation with slow emphasis, “will break your neck if you meddle with wot don’t concern you.”\n\nAfter another silence, the husband of the absent woman, turning to me again, answered me with his usual grumbling unwillingness.\n\n“Wos Jenny here when the lady come? Yes, she wos here when the lady come. Wot did the lady say to her? Well, I’ll tell you wot the lady said to her. She said, ‘You remember me as come one time to talk to you about the young lady as had been a-wisiting of you? You remember me as give you somethink handsome for a handkercher wot she had left?’ Ah, she remembered. So we all did. Well, then, wos that young lady up at the house now? No, she warn’t up at the house now. Well, then, lookee here. The lady was upon a journey all alone, strange as we might think it, and could she rest herself where you’re a setten for a hour or so. Yes she could, and so she did. Then she went—it might be at twenty minutes past eleven, and it might be at twenty minutes past twelve; we ain’t got no watches here to know the time by, nor yet clocks. Where did she go? I don’t know where she go’d. She went one way, and Jenny went another; one went right to Lunnun, and t’other went right from it. That’s all about it. Ask this man. He heerd it all, and see it all. He knows.”\n\nThe other man repeated, “That’s all about it.”\n\n“Was the lady crying?” I inquired.\n\n“Devil a bit,” returned the first man. “Her shoes was the worse, and her clothes was the worse, but she warn’t—not as I see.”\n\nThe woman sat with her arms crossed and her eyes upon the ground. Her husband had turned his seat a little so as to face her and kept his hammer-like hand upon the table as if it were in readiness to execute his threat if she disobeyed him.\n\n“I hope you will not object to my asking your wife,” said I, “how the lady looked.”\n\n“Come, then!” he gruffly cried to her. “You hear what she says. Cut it short and tell her.”\n\n“Bad,” replied the woman. “Pale and exhausted. Very bad.”\n\n“Did she speak much?”\n\n“Not much, but her voice was hoarse.”\n\nShe answered, looking all the while at her husband for leave.\n\n“Was she faint?” said I. “Did she eat or drink here?”\n\n“Go on!” said the husband in answer to her look. “Tell her and cut it short.”\n\n“She had a little water, miss, and Jenny fetched her some bread and tea. But she hardly touched it.”\n\n“And when she went from here,” I was proceeding, when Jenny’s husband impatiently took me up.\n\n“When she went from here, she went right away nor’ard by the high road. Ask on the road if you doubt me, and see if it warn’t so. Now, there’s the end. That’s all about it.”\n\nI glanced at my companion, and finding that he had already risen and was ready to depart, thanked them for what they had told me, and took my leave. The woman looked full at Mr. Bucket as he went out, and he looked full at her.\n\n“Now, Miss Summerson,” he said to me as we walked quickly away. “They’ve got her ladyship’s watch among ’em. That’s a positive fact.”\n\n“You saw it?” I exclaimed.\n\n“Just as good as saw it,” he returned. “Else why should he talk about his ‘twenty minutes past’ and about his having no watch to tell the time by? Twenty minutes! He don’t usually cut his time so fine as that. If he comes to half-hours, it’s as much as HE does. Now, you see, either her ladyship gave him that watch or he took it. I think she gave it him. Now, what should she give it him for? What should she give it him for?”\n\nHe repeated this question to himself several times as we hurried on, appearing to balance between a variety of answers that arose in his mind.\n\n“If time could be spared,” said Mr. Bucket, “which is the only thing that can’t be spared in this case, I might get it out of that woman; but it’s too doubtful a chance to trust to under present circumstances. They are up to keeping a close eye upon her, and any fool knows that a poor creetur like her, beaten and kicked and scarred and bruised from head to foot, will stand by the husband that ill uses her through thick and thin. There’s something kept back. It’s a pity but what we had seen the other woman.”\n\nI regretted it exceedingly, for she was very grateful, and I felt sure would have resisted no entreaty of mine.\n\n“It’s possible, Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Bucket, pondering on it, “that her ladyship sent her up to London with some word for you, and it’s possible that her husband got the watch to let her go. It don’t come out altogether so plain as to please me, but it’s on the cards. Now, I don’t take kindly to laying out the money of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, on these roughs, and I don’t see my way to the usefulness of it at present. No! So far our road, Miss Summerson, is for’ard—straight ahead—and keeping everything quiet!”\n\nWe called at home once more that I might send a hasty note to my guardian, and then we hurried back to where we had left the carriage. The horses were brought out as soon as we were seen coming, and we were on the road again in a few minutes.\n\nIt had set in snowing at daybreak, and it now snowed hard. The air was so thick with the darkness of the day and the density of the fall that we could see but a very little way in any direction. Although it was extremely cold, the snow was but partially frozen, and it churned—with a sound as if it were a beach of small shells—under the hoofs of the horses into mire and water. They sometimes slipped and floundered for a mile together, and we were obliged to come to a standstill to rest them. One horse fell three times in this first stage, and trembled so and was so shaken that the driver had to dismount from his saddle and lead him at last.\n\nI could eat nothing and could not sleep, and I grew so nervous under those delays and the slow pace at which we travelled that I had an unreasonable desire upon me to get out and walk. Yielding to my companion’s better sense, however, I remained where I was. All this time, kept fresh by a certain enjoyment of the work in which he was engaged, he was up and down at every house we came to, addressing people whom he had never beheld before as old acquaintances, running in to warm himself at every fire he saw, talking and drinking and shaking hands at every bar and tap, friendly with every waggoner, wheelwright, blacksmith, and toll-taker, yet never seeming to lose time, and always mounting to the box again with his watchful, steady face and his business-like “Get on, my lad!”\n\nWhen we were changing horses the next time, he came from the stable-yard, with the wet snow encrusted upon him and dropping off him—plashing and crashing through it to his wet knees as he had been doing frequently since we left Saint Albans—and spoke to me at the carriage side.\n\n“Keep up your spirits. It’s certainly true that she came on here, Miss Summerson. There’s not a doubt of the dress by this time, and the dress has been seen here.”\n\n“Still on foot?” said I.\n\n“Still on foot. I think the gentleman you mentioned must be the point she’s aiming at, and yet I don’t like his living down in her own part of the country neither.”\n\n“I know so little,” said I. “There may be some one else nearer here, of whom I never heard.”\n\n“That’s true. But whatever you do, don’t you fall a-crying, my dear; and don’t you worry yourself no more than you can help. Get on, my lad!”\n\nThe sleet fell all that day unceasingly, a thick mist came on early, and it never rose or lightened for a moment. Such roads I had never seen. I sometimes feared we had missed the way and got into the ploughed grounds or the marshes. If I ever thought of the time I had been out, it presented itself as an indefinite period of great duration, and I seemed, in a strange way, never to have been free from the anxiety under which I then laboured.\n\nAs we advanced, I began to feel misgivings that my companion lost confidence. He was the same as before with all the roadside people, but he looked graver when he sat by himself on the box. I saw his finger uneasily going across and across his mouth during the whole of one long weary stage. I overheard that he began to ask the drivers of coaches and other vehicles coming towards us what passengers they had seen in other coaches and vehicles that were in advance. Their replies did not encourage him. He always gave me a reassuring beck of his finger and lift of his eyelid as he got upon the box again, but he seemed perplexed now when he said, “Get on, my lad!”\n\nAt last, when we were changing, he told me that he had lost the track of the dress so long that he began to be surprised. It was nothing, he said, to lose such a track for one while, and to take it up for another while, and so on; but it had disappeared here in an unaccountable manner, and we had not come upon it since. This corroborated the apprehensions I had formed, when he began to look at direction-posts, and to leave the carriage at cross roads for a quarter of an hour at a time while he explored them. But I was not to be down-hearted, he told me, for it was as likely as not that the next stage might set us right again.\n\nThe next stage, however, ended as that one ended; we had no new clue. There was a spacious inn here, solitary, but a comfortable substantial building, and as we drove in under a large gateway before I knew it, where a landlady and her pretty daughters came to the carriage-door, entreating me to alight and refresh myself while the horses were making ready, I thought it would be uncharitable to refuse. They took me upstairs to a warm room and left me there.\n\nIt was at the corner of the house, I remember, looking two ways. On one side to a stable-yard open to a by-road, where the ostlers were unharnessing the splashed and tired horses from the muddy carriage, and beyond that to the by-road itself, across which the sign was heavily swinging; on the other side to a wood of dark pine-trees. Their branches were encumbered with snow, and it silently dropped off in wet heaps while I stood at the window. Night was setting in, and its bleakness was enhanced by the contrast of the pictured fire glowing and gleaming in the window-pane. As I looked among the stems of the trees and followed the discoloured marks in the snow where the thaw was sinking into it and undermining it, I thought of the motherly face brightly set off by daughters that had just now welcomed me and of MY mother lying down in such a wood to die.\n\nI was frightened when I found them all about me, but I remembered that before I fainted I tried very hard not to do it; and that was some little comfort. They cushioned me up on a large sofa by the fire, and then the comely landlady told me that I must travel no further to-night, but must go to bed. But this put me into such a tremble lest they should detain me there that she soon recalled her words and compromised for a rest of half an hour.\n\nA good endearing creature she was. She and her three fair girls, all so busy about me. I was to take hot soup and broiled fowl, while Mr. Bucket dried himself and dined elsewhere; but I could not do it when a snug round table was presently spread by the fireside, though I was very unwilling to disappoint them. However, I could take some toast and some hot negus; and as I really enjoyed that refreshment, it made some recompense.\n\nPunctual to the time, at the half-hour’s end the carriage came rumbling under the gateway, and they took me down, warmed, refreshed, comforted by kindness, and safe (I assured them) not to faint any more. After I had got in and had taken a grateful leave of them all, the youngest daughter—a blooming girl of nineteen, who was to be the first married, they had told me—got upon the carriage step, reached in, and kissed me. I have never seen her, from that hour, but I think of her to this hour as my friend.\n\nThe transparent windows with the fire and light, looking so bright and warm from the cold darkness out of doors, were soon gone, and again we were crushing and churning the loose snow. We went on with toil enough, but the dismal roads were not much worse than they had been, and the stage was only nine miles. My companion smoking on the box—I had thought at the last inn of begging him to do so when I saw him standing at a great fire in a comfortable cloud of tobacco—was as vigilant as ever and as quickly down and up again when we came to any human abode or any human creature. He had lighted his little dark lantern, which seemed to be a favourite with him, for we had lamps to the carriage; and every now and then he turned it upon me to see that I was doing well. There was a folding-window to the carriage-head, but I never closed it, for it seemed like shutting out hope.\n\nWe came to the end of the stage, and still the lost trace was not recovered. I looked at him anxiously when we stopped to change, but I knew by his yet graver face as he stood watching the ostlers that he had heard nothing. Almost in an instant afterwards, as I leaned back in my seat, he looked in, with his lighted lantern in his hand, an excited and quite different man.\n\n“What is it?” said I, starting. “Is she here?”\n\n“No, no. Don’t deceive yourself, my dear. Nobody’s here. But I’ve got it!”\n\nThe crystallized snow was in his eyelashes, in his hair, lying in ridges on his dress. He had to shake it from his face and get his breath before he spoke to me.\n\n“Now, Miss Summerson,” said he, beating his finger on the apron, “don’t you be disappointed at what I’m a-going to do. You know me. I’m Inspector Bucket, and you can trust me. We’ve come a long way; never mind. Four horses out there for the next stage up! Quick!”\n\nThere was a commotion in the yard, and a man came running out of the stables to know if he meant up or down.\n\n“Up, I tell you! Up! Ain’t it English? Up!”\n\n“Up?” said I, astonished. “To London! Are we going back?”\n\n“Miss Summerson,” he answered, “back. Straight back as a die. You know me. Don’t be afraid. I’ll follow the other, by G——”\n\n“The other?” I repeated. “Who?”\n\n“You called her Jenny, didn’t you? I’ll follow her. Bring those two pair out here for a crown a man. Wake up, some of you!”\n\n“You will not desert this lady we are in search of; you will not abandon her on such a night and in such a state of mind as I know her to be in!” said I, in an agony, and grasping his hand.\n\n“You are right, my dear, I won’t. But I’ll follow the other. Look alive here with them horses. Send a man for’ard in the saddle to the next stage, and let him send another for’ard again, and order four on, up, right through. My darling, don’t you be afraid!”\n\nThese orders and the way in which he ran about the yard urging them caused a general excitement that was scarcely less bewildering to me than the sudden change. But in the height of the confusion, a mounted man galloped away to order the relays, and our horses were put to with great speed.\n\n“My dear,” said Mr. Bucket, jumping to his seat and looking in again, “—you’ll excuse me if I’m too familiar—don’t you fret and worry yourself no more than you can help. I say nothing else at present; but you know me, my dear; now, don’t you?”\n\nI endeavoured to say that I knew he was far more capable than I of deciding what we ought to do, but was he sure that this was right? Could I not go forward by myself in search of—I grasped his hand again in my distress and whispered it to him—of my own mother.\n\n“My dear,” he answered, “I know, I know, and would I put you wrong, do you think? Inspector Bucket. Now you know me, don’t you?”\n\nWhat could I say but yes!\n\n“Then you keep up as good a heart as you can, and you rely upon me for standing by you, no less than by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. Now, are you right there?”\n\n“All right, sir!”\n\n“Off she goes, then. And get on, my lads!”\n\nWe were again upon the melancholy road by which we had come, tearing up the miry sleet and thawing snow as if they were torn up by a waterwheel.\n\nA Wintry Day and Night\nStill impassive, as behoves its breeding, the Dedlock town house carries itself as usual towards the street of dismal grandeur. There are powdered heads from time to time in the little windows of the hall, looking out at the untaxed powder falling all day from the sky; and in the same conservatory there is peach blossom turning itself exotically to the great hall fire from the nipping weather out of doors. It is given out that my Lady has gone down into Lincolnshire, but is expected to return presently.\n\nRumour, busy overmuch, however, will not go down into Lincolnshire. It persists in flitting and chattering about town. It knows that that poor unfortunate man, Sir Leicester, has been sadly used. It hears, my dear child, all sorts of shocking things. It makes the world of five miles round quite merry. Not to know that there is something wrong at the Dedlocks’ is to augur yourself unknown. One of the peachy-cheeked charmers with the skeleton throats is already apprised of all the principal circumstances that will come out before the Lords on Sir Leicester’s application for a bill of divorce.\n\nAt Blaze and Sparkle’s the jewellers and at Sheen and Gloss’s the mercers, it is and will be for several hours the topic of the age, the feature of the century. The patronesses of those establishments, albeit so loftily inscrutable, being as nicely weighed and measured there as any other article of the stock-in-trade, are perfectly understood in this new fashion by the rawest hand behind the counter. “Our people, Mr. Jones,” said Blaze and Sparkle to the hand in question on engaging him, “our people, sir, are sheep—mere sheep. Where two or three marked ones go, all the rest follow. Keep those two or three in your eye, Mr. Jones, and you have the flock.” So, likewise, Sheen and Gloss to THEIR Jones, in reference to knowing where to have the fashionable people and how to bring what they (Sheen and Gloss) choose into fashion. On similar unerring principles, Mr. Sladdery the librarian, and indeed the great farmer of gorgeous sheep, admits this very day, “Why yes, sir, there certainly ARE reports concerning Lady Dedlock, very current indeed among my high connexion, sir. You see, my high connexion must talk about something, sir; and it’s only to get a subject into vogue with one or two ladies I could name to make it go down with the whole. Just what I should have done with those ladies, sir, in the case of any novelty you had left to me to bring in, they have done of themselves in this case through knowing Lady Dedlock and being perhaps a little innocently jealous of her too, sir. You’ll find, sir, that this topic will be very popular among my high connexion. If it had been a speculation, sir, it would have brought money. And when I say so, you may trust to my being right, sir, for I have made it my business to study my high connexion and to be able to wind it up like a clock, sir.”\n\nThus rumour thrives in the capital, and will not go down into Lincolnshire. By half-past five, post meridian, Horse Guards’ time, it has even elicited a new remark from the Honourable Mr. Stables, which bids fair to outshine the old one, on which he has so long rested his colloquial reputation. This sparkling sally is to the effect that although he always knew she was the best-groomed woman in the stud, he had no idea she was a bolter. It is immensely received in turf-circles.\n\nAt feasts and festivals also, in firmaments she has often graced, and among constellations she outshone but yesterday, she is still the prevalent subject. What is it? Who is it? When was it? Where was it? How was it? She is discussed by her dear friends with all the genteelest slang in vogue, with the last new word, the last new manner, the last new drawl, and the perfection of polite indifference. A remarkable feature of the theme is that it is found to be so inspiring that several people come out upon it who never came out before—positively say things! William Buffy carries one of these smartnesses from the place where he dines down to the House, where the Whip for his party hands it about with his snuff-box to keep men together who want to be off, with such effect that the Speaker (who has had it privately insinuated into his own ear under the corner of his wig) cries, “Order at the bar!” three times without making an impression.\n\nAnd not the least amazing circumstance connected with her being vaguely the town talk is that people hovering on the confines of Mr. Sladdery’s high connexion, people who know nothing and ever did know nothing about her, think it essential to their reputation to pretend that she is their topic too, and to retail her at second-hand with the last new word and the last new manner, and the last new drawl, and the last new polite indifference, and all the rest of it, all at second-hand but considered equal to new in inferior systems and to fainter stars. If there be any man of letters, art, or science among these little dealers, how noble in him to support the feeble sisters on such majestic crutches!\n\nSo goes the wintry day outside the Dedlock mansion. How within it?\n\nSir Leicester, lying in his bed, can speak a little, though with difficulty and indistinctness. He is enjoined to silence and to rest, and they have given him some opiate to lull his pain, for his old enemy is very hard with him. He is never asleep, though sometimes he seems to fall into a dull waking doze. He caused his bedstead to be moved out nearer to the window when he heard it was such inclement weather, and his head to be so adjusted that he could see the driving snow and sleet. He watches it as it falls, throughout the whole wintry day.\n\nUpon the least noise in the house, which is kept hushed, his hand is at the pencil. The old housekeeper, sitting by him, knows what he would write and whispers, “No, he has not come back yet, Sir Leicester. It was late last night when he went. He has been but a little time gone yet.”\n\nHe withdraws his hand and falls to looking at the sleet and snow again until they seem, by being long looked at, to fall so thick and fast that he is obliged to close his eyes for a minute on the giddy whirl of white flakes and icy blots.\n\nHe began to look at them as soon as it was light. The day is not yet far spent when he conceives it to be necessary that her rooms should be prepared for her. It is very cold and wet. Let there be good fires. Let them know that she is expected. Please see to it yourself. He writes to this purpose on his slate, and Mrs. Rouncewell with a heavy heart obeys.\n\n“For I dread, George,” the old lady says to her son, who waits below to keep her company when she has a little leisure, “I dread, my dear, that my Lady will never more set foot within these walls.”\n\n“That’s a bad presentiment, mother.”\n\n“Nor yet within the walls of Chesney Wold, my dear.”\n\n“That’s worse. But why, mother?”\n\n“When I saw my Lady yesterday, George, she looked to me—and I may say at me too—as if the step on the Ghost’s Walk had almost walked her down.”\n\n“Come, come! You alarm yourself with old-story fears, mother.”\n\n“No I don’t, my dear. No I don’t. It’s going on for sixty year that I have been in this family, and I never had any fears for it before. But it’s breaking up, my dear; the great old Dedlock family is breaking up.”\n\n“I hope not, mother.”\n\n“I am thankful I have lived long enough to be with Sir Leicester in this illness and trouble, for I know I am not too old nor too useless to be a welcomer sight to him than anybody else in my place would be. But the step on the Ghost’s Walk will walk my Lady down, George; it has been many a day behind her, and now it will pass her and go on.”\n\n“Well, mother dear, I say again, I hope not.”\n\n“Ah, so do I, George,” the old lady returns, shaking her head and parting her folded hands. “But if my fears come true, and he has to know it, who will tell him!”\n\n“Are these her rooms?”\n\n“These are my Lady’s rooms, just as she left them.”\n\n“Why, now,” says the trooper, glancing round him and speaking in a lower voice, “I begin to understand how you come to think as you do think, mother. Rooms get an awful look about them when they are fitted up, like these, for one person you are used to see in them, and that person is away under any shadow, let alone being God knows where.”\n\nHe is not far out. As all partings foreshadow the great final one, so, empty rooms, bereft of a familiar presence, mournfully whisper what your room and what mine must one day be. My Lady’s state has a hollow look, thus gloomy and abandoned; and in the inner apartment, where Mr. Bucket last night made his secret perquisition, the traces of her dresses and her ornaments, even the mirrors accustomed to reflect them when they were a portion of herself, have a desolate and vacant air. Dark and cold as the wintry day is, it is darker and colder in these deserted chambers than in many a hut that will barely exclude the weather; and though the servants heap fires in the grates and set the couches and the chairs within the warm glass screens that let their ruddy light shoot through to the furthest corners, there is a heavy cloud upon the rooms which no light will dispel.\n\nThe old housekeeper and her son remain until the preparations are complete, and then she returns upstairs. Volumnia has taken Mrs. Rouncewell’s place in the meantime, though pearl necklaces and rouge pots, however calculated to embellish Bath, are but indifferent comforts to the invalid under present circumstances. Volumnia, not being supposed to know (and indeed not knowing) what is the matter, has found it a ticklish task to offer appropriate observations and consequently has supplied their place with distracting smoothings of the bed-linen, elaborate locomotion on tiptoe, vigilant peeping at her kinsman’s eyes, and one exasperating whisper to herself of, “He is asleep.” In disproof of which superfluous remark Sir Leicester has indignantly written on the slate, “I am not.”\n\nYielding, therefore, the chair at the bedside to the quaint old housekeeper, Volumnia sits at a table a little removed, sympathetically sighing. Sir Leicester watches the sleet and snow and listens for the returning steps that he expects. In the ears of his old servant, looking as if she had stepped out of an old picture-frame to attend a summoned Dedlock to another world, the silence is fraught with echoes of her own words, “Who will tell him!”\n\nHe has been under his valet’s hands this morning to be made presentable and is as well got up as the circumstances will allow. He is propped with pillows, his grey hair is brushed in its usual manner, his linen is arranged to a nicety, and he is wrapped in a responsible dressing-gown. His eye-glass and his watch are ready to his hand. It is necessary—less to his own dignity now perhaps than for her sake—that he should be seen as little disturbed and as much himself as may be. Women will talk, and Volumnia, though a Dedlock, is no exceptional case. He keeps her here, there is little doubt, to prevent her talking somewhere else. He is very ill, but he makes his present stand against distress of mind and body most courageously.\n\nThe fair Volumnia, being one of those sprightly girls who cannot long continue silent without imminent peril of seizure by the dragon Boredom, soon indicates the approach of that monster with a series of undisguisable yawns. Finding it impossible to suppress those yawns by any other process than conversation, she compliments Mrs. Rouncewell on her son, declaring that he positively is one of the finest figures she ever saw and as soldierly a looking person, she should think, as what’s his name, her favourite Life Guardsman—the man she dotes on, the dearest of creatures—who was killed at Waterloo.\n\nSir Leicester hears this tribute with so much surprise and stares about him in such a confused way that Mrs. Rouncewell feels it necessary to explain.\n\n“Miss Dedlock don’t speak of my eldest son, Sir Leicester, but my youngest. I have found him. He has come home.”\n\nSir Leicester breaks silence with a harsh cry. “George? Your son George come home, Mrs. Rouncewell?”\n\nThe old housekeeper wipes her eyes. “Thank God. Yes, Sir Leicester.”\n\nDoes this discovery of some one lost, this return of some one so long gone, come upon him as a strong confirmation of his hopes? Does he think, “Shall I not, with the aid I have, recall her safely after this, there being fewer hours in her case than there are years in his?”\n\nIt is of no use entreating him; he is determined to speak now, and he does. In a thick crowd of sounds, but still intelligibly enough to be understood.\n\n“Why did you not tell me, Mrs. Rouncewell?”\n\n“It happened only yesterday, Sir Leicester, and I doubted your being well enough to be talked to of such things.”\n\nBesides, the giddy Volumnia now remembers with her little scream that nobody was to have known of his being Mrs. Rouncewell’s son and that she was not to have told. But Mrs. Rouncewell protests, with warmth enough to swell the stomacher, that of course she would have told Sir Leicester as soon as he got better.\n\n“Where is your son George, Mrs. Rouncewell?” asks Sir Leicester,\n\nMrs. Rouncewell, not a little alarmed by his disregard of the doctor’s injunctions, replies, in London.\n\n“Where in London?”\n\nMrs. Rouncewell is constrained to admit that he is in the house.\n\n“Bring him here to my room. Bring him directly.”\n\nThe old lady can do nothing but go in search of him. Sir Leicester, with such power of movement as he has, arranges himself a little to receive him. When he has done so, he looks out again at the falling sleet and snow and listens again for the returning steps. A quantity of straw has been tumbled down in the street to deaden the noises there, and she might be driven to the door perhaps without his hearing wheels.\n\nHe is lying thus, apparently forgetful of his newer and minor surprise, when the housekeeper returns, accompanied by her trooper son. Mr. George approaches softly to the bedside, makes his bow, squares his chest, and stands, with his face flushed, very heartily ashamed of himself.\n\n“Good heaven, and it is really George Rouncewell!” exclaims Sir Leicester. “Do you remember me, George?”\n\nThe trooper needs to look at him and to separate this sound from that sound before he knows what he has said, but doing this and being a little helped by his mother, he replies, “I must have a very bad memory, indeed, Sir Leicester, if I failed to remember you.”\n\n“When I look at you, George Rouncewell,” Sir Leicester observes with difficulty, “I see something of a boy at Chesney Wold—I remember well—very well.”\n\nHe looks at the trooper until tears come into his eyes, and then he looks at the sleet and snow again.\n\n“I ask your pardon, Sir Leicester,” says the trooper, “but would you accept of my arms to raise you up? You would lie easier, Sir Leicester, if you would allow me to move you.”\n\n“If you please, George Rouncewell; if you will be so good.”\n\nThe trooper takes him in his arms like a child, lightly raises him, and turns him with his face more towards the window. “Thank you. You have your mother’s gentleness,” returns Sir Leicester, “and your own strength. Thank you.”\n\nHe signs to him with his hand not to go away. George quietly remains at the bedside, waiting to be spoken to.\n\n“Why did you wish for secrecy?” It takes Sir Leicester some time to ask this.\n\n“Truly I am not much to boast of, Sir Leicester, and I—I should still, Sir Leicester, if you was not so indisposed—which I hope you will not be long—I should still hope for the favour of being allowed to remain unknown in general. That involves explanations not very hard to be guessed at, not very well timed here, and not very creditable to myself. However opinions may differ on a variety of subjects, I should think it would be universally agreed, Sir Leicester, that I am not much to boast of.”\n\n“You have been a soldier,” observes Sir Leicester, “and a faithful one.”\n\nGeorge makes his military bow. “As far as that goes, Sir Leicester, I have done my duty under discipline, and it was the least I could do.”\n\n“You find me,” says Sir Leicester, whose eyes are much attracted towards him, “far from well, George Rouncewell.”\n\n“I am very sorry both to hear it and to see it, Sir Leicester.”\n\n“I am sure you are. No. In addition to my older malady, I have had a sudden and bad attack. Something that deadens,” making an endeavour to pass one hand down one side, “and confuses,” touching his lips.\n\nGeorge, with a look of assent and sympathy, makes another bow. The different times when they were both young men (the trooper much the younger of the two) and looked at one another down at Chesney Wold arise before them both and soften both.\n\nSir Leicester, evidently with a great determination to say, in his own manner, something that is on his mind before relapsing into silence, tries to raise himself among his pillows a little more. George, observant of the action, takes him in his arms again and places him as he desires to be. “Thank you, George. You are another self to me. You have often carried my spare gun at Chesney Wold, George. You are familiar to me in these strange circumstances, very familiar.” He has put Sir Leicester’s sounder arm over his shoulder in lifting him up, and Sir Leicester is slow in drawing it away again as he says these words.\n\n“I was about to add,” he presently goes on, “I was about to add, respecting this attack, that it was unfortunately simultaneous with a slight misunderstanding between my Lady and myself. I do not mean that there was any difference between us (for there has been none), but that there was a misunderstanding of certain circumstances important only to ourselves, which deprives me, for a little while, of my Lady’s society. She has found it necessary to make a journey—I trust will shortly return. Volumnia, do I make myself intelligible? The words are not quite under my command in the manner of pronouncing them.”\n\nVolumnia understands him perfectly, and in truth he delivers himself with far greater plainness than could have been supposed possible a minute ago. The effort by which he does so is written in the anxious and labouring expression of his face. Nothing but the strength of his purpose enables him to make it.\n\n“Therefore, Volumnia, I desire to say in your presence—and in the presence of my old retainer and friend, Mrs. Rouncewell, whose truth and fidelity no one can question, and in the presence of her son George, who comes back like a familiar recollection of my youth in the home of my ancestors at Chesney Wold—in case I should relapse, in case I should not recover, in case I should lose both my speech and the power of writing, though I hope for better things—”\n\nThe old housekeeper weeping silently; Volumnia in the greatest agitation, with the freshest bloom on her cheeks; the trooper with his arms folded and his head a little bent, respectfully attentive.\n\n“Therefore I desire to say, and to call you all to witness—beginning, Volumnia, with yourself, most solemnly—that I am on unaltered terms with Lady Dedlock. That I assert no cause whatever of complaint against her. That I have ever had the strongest affection for her, and that I retain it undiminished. Say this to herself, and to every one. If you ever say less than this, you will be guilty of deliberate falsehood to me.”\n\nVolumnia tremblingly protests that she will observe his injunctions to the letter.\n\n“My Lady is too high in position, too handsome, too accomplished, too superior in most respects to the best of those by whom she is surrounded, not to have her enemies and traducers, I dare say. Let it be known to them, as I make it known to you, that being of sound mind, memory, and understanding, I revoke no disposition I have made in her favour. I abridge nothing I have ever bestowed upon her. I am on unaltered terms with her, and I recall—having the full power to do it if I were so disposed, as you see—no act I have done for her advantage and happiness.”\n\nHis formal array of words might have at any other time, as it has often had, something ludicrous in it, but at this time it is serious and affecting. His noble earnestness, his fidelity, his gallant shielding of her, his generous conquest of his own wrong and his own pride for her sake, are simply honourable, manly, and true. Nothing less worthy can be seen through the lustre of such qualities in the commonest mechanic, nothing less worthy can be seen in the best-born gentleman. In such a light both aspire alike, both rise alike, both children of the dust shine equally.\n\nOverpowered by his exertions, he lays his head back on his pillows and closes his eyes for not more than a minute, when he again resumes his watching of the weather and his attention to the muffled sounds. In the rendering of those little services, and in the manner of their acceptance, the trooper has become installed as necessary to him. Nothing has been said, but it is quite understood. He falls a step or two backward to be out of sight and mounts guard a little behind his mother’s chair.\n\nThe day is now beginning to decline. The mist and the sleet into which the snow has all resolved itself are darker, and the blaze begins to tell more vividly upon the room walls and furniture. The gloom augments; the bright gas springs up in the streets; and the pertinacious oil lamps which yet hold their ground there, with their source of life half frozen and half thawed, twinkle gaspingly like fiery fish out of water—as they are. The world, which has been rumbling over the straw and pulling at the bell, “to inquire,” begins to go home, begins to dress, to dine, to discuss its dear friend with all the last new modes, as already mentioned.\n\nNow does Sir Leicester become worse, restless, uneasy, and in great pain. Volumnia, lighting a candle (with a predestined aptitude for doing something objectionable), is bidden to put it out again, for it is not yet dark enough. Yet it is very dark too, as dark as it will be all night. By and by she tries again. No! Put it out. It is not dark enough yet.\n\nHis old housekeeper is the first to understand that he is striving to uphold the fiction with himself that it is not growing late.\n\n“Dear Sir Leicester, my honoured master,” she softly whispers, “I must, for your own good, and my duty, take the freedom of begging and praying that you will not lie here in the lone darkness watching and waiting and dragging through the time. Let me draw the curtains, and light the candles, and make things more comfortable about you. The church-clocks will strike the hours just the same, Sir Leicester, and the night will pass away just the same. My Lady will come back, just the same.”\n\n“I know it, Mrs. Rouncewell, but I am weak—and she has been so long gone.”\n\n“Not so very long, Sir Leicester. Not twenty-four hours yet.”\n\n“But that is a long time. Oh, it is a long time!”\n\nHe says it with a groan that wrings her heart.\n\nShe knows that this is not a period for bringing the rough light upon him; she thinks his tears too sacred to be seen, even by her. Therefore she sits in the darkness for a while without a word, then gently begins to move about, now stirring the fire, now standing at the dark window looking out. Finally he tells her, with recovered self-command, “As you say, Mrs. Rouncewell, it is no worse for being confessed. It is getting late, and they are not come. Light the room!” When it is lighted and the weather shut out, it is only left to him to listen.\n\nBut they find that however dejected and ill he is, he brightens when a quiet pretence is made of looking at the fires in her rooms and being sure that everything is ready to receive her. Poor pretence as it is, these allusions to her being expected keep up hope within him.\n\nMidnight comes, and with it the same blank. The carriages in the streets are few, and other late sounds in that neighbourhood there are none, unless a man so very nomadically drunk as to stray into the frigid zone goes brawling and bellowing along the pavement. Upon this wintry night it is so still that listening to the intense silence is like looking at intense darkness. If any distant sound be audible in this case, it departs through the gloom like a feeble light in that, and all is heavier than before.\n\nThe corporation of servants are dismissed to bed (not unwilling to go, for they were up all last night), and only Mrs. Rouncewell and George keep watch in Sir Leicester’s room. As the night lags tardily on—or rather when it seems to stop altogether, at between two and three o’clock—they find a restless craving on him to know more about the weather, now he cannot see it. Hence George, patrolling regularly every half-hour to the rooms so carefully looked after, extends his march to the hall-door, looks about him, and brings back the best report he can make of the worst of nights, the sleet still falling and even the stone footways lying ankle-deep in icy sludge.\n\nVolumnia, in her room up a retired landing on the staircase—the second turning past the end of the carving and gilding, a cousinly room containing a fearful abortion of a portrait of Sir Leicester banished for its crimes, and commanding in the day a solemn yard planted with dried-up shrubs like antediluvian specimens of black tea—is a prey to horrors of many kinds. Not last nor least among them, possibly, is a horror of what may befall her little income in the event, as she expresses it, “of anything happening” to Sir Leicester. Anything, in this sense, meaning one thing only; and that the last thing that can happen to the consciousness of any baronet in the known world.\n\nAn effect of these horrors is that Volumnia finds she cannot go to bed in her own room or sit by the fire in her own room, but must come forth with her fair head tied up in a profusion of shawl, and her fair form enrobed in drapery, and parade the mansion like a ghost, particularly haunting the rooms, warm and luxurious, prepared for one who still does not return. Solitude under such circumstances being not to be thought of, Volumnia is attended by her maid, who, impressed from her own bed for that purpose, extremely cold, very sleepy, and generally an injured maid as condemned by circumstances to take office with a cousin, when she had resolved to be maid to nothing less than ten thousand a year, has not a sweet expression of countenance.\n\nThe periodical visits of the trooper to these rooms, however, in the course of his patrolling is an assurance of protection and company both to mistress and maid, which renders them very acceptable in the small hours of the night. Whenever he is heard advancing, they both make some little decorative preparation to receive him; at other times they divide their watches into short scraps of oblivion and dialogues not wholly free from acerbity, as to whether Miss Dedlock, sitting with her feet upon the fender, was or was not falling into the fire when rescued (to her great displeasure) by her guardian genius the maid.\n\n“How is Sir Leicester now, Mr. George?” inquires Volumnia, adjusting her cowl over her head.\n\n“Why, Sir Leicester is much the same, miss. He is very low and ill, and he even wanders a little sometimes.”\n\n“Has he asked for me?” inquires Volumnia tenderly.\n\n“Why, no, I can’t say he has, miss. Not within my hearing, that is to say.”\n\n“This is a truly sad time, Mr. George.”\n\n“It is indeed, miss. Hadn’t you better go to bed?”\n\n“You had a deal better go to bed, Miss Dedlock,” quoth the maid sharply.\n\nBut Volumnia answers No! No! She may be asked for, she may be wanted at a moment’s notice. She never should forgive herself “if anything was to happen” and she was not on the spot. She declines to enter on the question, mooted by the maid, how the spot comes to be there, and not in her room (which is nearer to Sir Leicester’s), but staunchly declares that on the spot she will remain. Volumnia further makes a merit of not having “closed an eye”—as if she had twenty or thirty—though it is hard to reconcile this statement with her having most indisputably opened two within five minutes.\n\nBut when it comes to four o’clock, and still the same blank, Volumnia’s constancy begins to fail her, or rather it begins to strengthen, for she now considers that it is her duty to be ready for the morrow, when much may be expected of her, that, in fact, howsoever anxious to remain upon the spot, it may be required of her, as an act of self-devotion, to desert the spot. So when the trooper reappears with his, “Hadn’t you better go to bed, miss?” and when the maid protests, more sharply than before, “You had a deal better go to bed, Miss Dedlock!” she meekly rises and says, “Do with me what you think best!”\n\nMr. George undoubtedly thinks it best to escort her on his arm to the door of her cousinly chamber, and the maid as undoubtedly thinks it best to hustle her into bed with mighty little ceremony. Accordingly, these steps are taken; and now the trooper, in his rounds, has the house to himself.\n\nThere is no improvement in the weather. From the portico, from the eaves, from the parapet, from every ledge and post and pillar, drips the thawed snow. It has crept, as if for shelter, into the lintels of the great door—under it, into the corners of the windows, into every chink and crevice of retreat, and there wastes and dies. It is falling still; upon the roof, upon the skylight, even through the skylight, and drip, drip, drip, with the regularity of the Ghost’s Walk, on the stone floor below.\n\nThe trooper, his old recollections awakened by the solitary grandeur of a great house—no novelty to him once at Chesney Wold—goes up the stairs and through the chief rooms, holding up his light at arm’s length. Thinking of his varied fortunes within the last few weeks, and of his rustic boyhood, and of the two periods of his life so strangely brought together across the wide intermediate space; thinking of the murdered man whose image is fresh in his mind; thinking of the lady who has disappeared from these very rooms and the tokens of whose recent presence are all here; thinking of the master of the house upstairs and of the foreboding, “Who will tell him!” he looks here and looks there, and reflects how he MIGHT see something now, which it would tax his boldness to walk up to, lay his hand upon, and prove to be a fancy. But it is all blank, blank as the darkness above and below, while he goes up the great staircase again, blank as the oppressive silence.\n\n“All is still in readiness, George Rouncewell?”\n\n“Quite orderly and right, Sir Leicester.”\n\n“No word of any kind?”\n\nThe trooper shakes his head.\n\n“No letter that can possibly have been overlooked?”\n\nBut he knows there is no such hope as that and lays his head down without looking for an answer.\n\nVery familiar to him, as he said himself some hours ago, George Rouncewell lifts him into easier positions through the long remainder of the blank wintry night, and equally familiar with his unexpressed wish, extinguishes the light and undraws the curtains at the first late break of day. The day comes like a phantom. Cold, colourless, and vague, it sends a warning streak before it of a deathlike hue, as if it cried out, “Look what I am bringing you who watch there! Who will tell him!”\n\nEsther’s Narrative\nIt was three o’clock in the morning when the houses outside London did at last begin to exclude the country and to close us in with streets. We had made our way along roads in a far worse condition than when we had traversed them by daylight, both the fall and the thaw having lasted ever since; but the energy of my companion never slackened. It had only been, as I thought, of less assistance than the horses in getting us on, and it had often aided them. They had stopped exhausted half-way up hills, they had been driven through streams of turbulent water, they had slipped down and become entangled with the harness; but he and his little lantern had been always ready, and when the mishap was set right, I had never heard any variation in his cool, “Get on, my lads!”\n\nThe steadiness and confidence with which he had directed our journey back I could not account for. Never wavering, he never even stopped to make an inquiry until we were within a few miles of London. A very few words, here and there, were then enough for him; and thus we came, at between three and four o’clock in the morning, into Islington.\n\nI will not dwell on the suspense and anxiety with which I reflected all this time that we were leaving my mother farther and farther behind every minute. I think I had some strong hope that he must be right and could not fail to have a satisfactory object in following this woman, but I tormented myself with questioning it and discussing it during the whole journey. What was to ensue when we found her and what could compensate us for this loss of time were questions also that I could not possibly dismiss; my mind was quite tortured by long dwelling on such reflections when we stopped.\n\nWe stopped in a high-street where there was a coach-stand. My companion paid our two drivers, who were as completely covered with splashes as if they had been dragged along the roads like the carriage itself, and giving them some brief direction where to take it, lifted me out of it and into a hackney-coach he had chosen from the rest.\n\n“Why, my dear!” he said as he did this. “How wet you are!”\n\nI had not been conscious of it. But the melted snow had found its way into the carriage, and I had got out two or three times when a fallen horse was plunging and had to be got up, and the wet had penetrated my dress. I assured him it was no matter, but the driver, who knew him, would not be dissuaded by me from running down the street to his stable, whence he brought an armful of clean dry straw. They shook it out and strewed it well about me, and I found it warm and comfortable.\n\n“Now, my dear,” said Mr. Bucket, with his head in at the window after I was shut up. “We’re a-going to mark this person down. It may take a little time, but you don’t mind that. You’re pretty sure that I’ve got a motive. Ain’t you?”\n\nI little thought what it was, little thought in how short a time I should understand it better, but I assured him that I had confidence in him.\n\n“So you may have, my dear,” he returned. “And I tell you what! If you only repose half as much confidence in me as I repose in you after what I’ve experienced of you, that’ll do. Lord! You’re no trouble at all. I never see a young woman in any station of society—and I’ve seen many elevated ones too—conduct herself like you have conducted yourself since you was called out of your bed. You’re a pattern, you know, that’s what you are,” said Mr. Bucket warmly; “you’re a pattern.”\n\nI told him I was very glad, as indeed I was, to have been no hindrance to him, and that I hoped I should be none now.\n\n“My dear,” he returned, “when a young lady is as mild as she’s game, and as game as she’s mild, that’s all I ask, and more than I expect. She then becomes a queen, and that’s about what you are yourself.”\n\nWith these encouraging words—they really were encouraging to me under those lonely and anxious circumstances—he got upon the box, and we once more drove away. Where we drove I neither knew then nor have ever known since, but we appeared to seek out the narrowest and worst streets in London. Whenever I saw him directing the driver, I was prepared for our descending into a deeper complication of such streets, and we never failed to do so.\n\nSometimes we emerged upon a wider thoroughfare or came to a larger building than the generality, well lighted. Then we stopped at offices like those we had visited when we began our journey, and I saw him in consultation with others. Sometimes he would get down by an archway or at a street corner and mysteriously show the light of his little lantern. This would attract similar lights from various dark quarters, like so many insects, and a fresh consultation would be held. By degrees we appeared to contract our search within narrower and easier limits. Single police-officers on duty could now tell Mr. Bucket what he wanted to know and point to him where to go. At last we stopped for a rather long conversation between him and one of these men, which I supposed to be satisfactory from his manner of nodding from time to time. When it was finished he came to me looking very busy and very attentive.\n\n“Now, Miss Summerson,” he said to me, “you won’t be alarmed whatever comes off, I know. It’s not necessary for me to give you any further caution than to tell you that we have marked this person down and that you may be of use to me before I know it myself. I don’t like to ask such a thing, my dear, but would you walk a little way?”\n\nOf course I got out directly and took his arm.\n\n“It ain’t so easy to keep your feet,” said Mr. Bucket, “but take time.”\n\nAlthough I looked about me confusedly and hurriedly as we crossed the street, I thought I knew the place. “Are we in Holborn?” I asked him.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Bucket. “Do you know this turning?”\n\n“It looks like Chancery Lane.”\n\n“And was christened so, my dear,” said Mr. Bucket.\n\nWe turned down it, and as we went shuffling through the sleet, I heard the clocks strike half-past five. We passed on in silence and as quickly as we could with such a foot-hold, when some one coming towards us on the narrow pavement, wrapped in a cloak, stopped and stood aside to give me room. In the same moment I heard an exclamation of wonder and my own name from Mr. Woodcourt. I knew his voice very well.\n\nIt was so unexpected and so—I don’t know what to call it, whether pleasant or painful—to come upon it after my feverish wandering journey, and in the midst of the night, that I could not keep back the tears from my eyes. It was like hearing his voice in a strange country.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson, that you should be out at this hour, and in such weather!”\n\nHe had heard from my guardian of my having been called away on some uncommon business and said so to dispense with any explanation. I told him that we had but just left a coach and were going—but then I was obliged to look at my companion.\n\n“Why, you see, Mr. Woodcourt”—he had caught the name from me—“we are a-going at present into the next street. Inspector Bucket.”\n\nMr. Woodcourt, disregarding my remonstrances, had hurriedly taken off his cloak and was putting it about me. “That’s a good move, too,” said Mr. Bucket, assisting, “a very good move.”\n\n“May I go with you?” said Mr. Woodcourt. I don’t know whether to me or to my companion.\n\n“Why, Lord!” exclaimed Mr. Bucket, taking the answer on himself. “Of course you may.”\n\nIt was all said in a moment, and they took me between them, wrapped in the cloak.\n\n“I have just left Richard,” said Mr. Woodcourt. “I have been sitting with him since ten o’clock last night.”\n\n“Oh, dear me, he is ill!”\n\n“No, no, believe me; not ill, but not quite well. He was depressed and faint—you know he gets so worried and so worn sometimes—and Ada sent to me of course; and when I came home I found her note and came straight here. Well! Richard revived so much after a little while, and Ada was so happy and so convinced of its being my doing, though God knows I had little enough to do with it, that I remained with him until he had been fast asleep some hours. As fast asleep as she is now, I hope!”\n\nHis friendly and familiar way of speaking of them, his unaffected devotion to them, the grateful confidence with which I knew he had inspired my darling, and the comfort he was to her; could I separate all this from his promise to me? How thankless I must have been if it had not recalled the words he said to me when he was so moved by the change in my appearance: “I will accept him as a trust, and it shall be a sacred one!”\n\nWe now turned into another narrow street. “Mr. Woodcourt,” said Mr. Bucket, who had eyed him closely as we came along, “our business takes us to a law-stationer’s here, a certain Mr. Snagsby’s. What, you know him, do you?” He was so quick that he saw it in an instant.\n\n“Yes, I know a little of him and have called upon him at this place.”\n\n“Indeed, sir?” said Mr. Bucket. “Then you will be so good as to let me leave Miss Summerson with you for a moment while I go and have half a word with him?”\n\nThe last police-officer with whom he had conferred was standing silently behind us. I was not aware of it until he struck in on my saying I heard some one crying.\n\n“Don’t be alarmed, miss,” he returned. “It’s Snagsby’s servant.”\n\n“Why, you see,” said Mr. Bucket, “the girl’s subject to fits, and has ’em bad upon her to-night. A most contrary circumstance it is, for I want certain information out of that girl, and she must be brought to reason somehow.”\n\n“At all events, they wouldn’t be up yet if it wasn’t for her, Mr. Bucket,” said the other man. “She’s been at it pretty well all night, sir.”\n\n“Well, that’s true,” he returned. “My light’s burnt out. Show yours a moment.”\n\nAll this passed in a whisper a door or two from the house in which I could faintly hear crying and moaning. In the little round of light produced for the purpose, Mr. Bucket went up to the door and knocked. The door was opened after he had knocked twice, and he went in, leaving us standing in the street.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Woodcourt, “if without obtruding myself on your confidence I may remain near you, pray let me do so.”\n\n“You are truly kind,” I answered. “I need wish to keep no secret of my own from you; if I keep any, it is another’s.”\n\n“I quite understand. Trust me, I will remain near you only so long as I can fully respect it.”\n\n“I trust implicitly to you,” I said. “I know and deeply feel how sacredly you keep your promise.”\n\nAfter a short time the little round of light shone out again, and Mr. Bucket advanced towards us in it with his earnest face. “Please to come in, Miss Summerson,” he said, “and sit down by the fire. Mr. Woodcourt, from information I have received I understand you are a medical man. Would you look to this girl and see if anything can be done to bring her round. She has a letter somewhere that I particularly want. It’s not in her box, and I think it must be about her; but she is so twisted and clenched up that she is difficult to handle without hurting.”\n\nWe all three went into the house together; although it was cold and raw, it smelt close too from being up all night. In the passage behind the door stood a scared, sorrowful-looking little man in a grey coat who seemed to have a naturally polite manner and spoke meekly.\n\n“Downstairs, if you please, Mr. Bucket,” said he. “The lady will excuse the front kitchen; we use it as our workaday sitting-room. The back is Guster’s bedroom, and in it she’s a-carrying on, poor thing, to a frightful extent!”\n\nWe went downstairs, followed by Mr. Snagsby, as I soon found the little man to be. In the front kitchen, sitting by the fire, was Mrs. Snagsby, with very red eyes and a very severe expression of face.\n\n“My little woman,” said Mr. Snagsby, entering behind us, “to wave—not to put too fine a point upon it, my dear—hostilities for one single moment in the course of this prolonged night, here is Inspector Bucket, Mr. Woodcourt, and a lady.”\n\nShe looked very much astonished, as she had reason for doing, and looked particularly hard at me.\n\n“My little woman,” said Mr. Snagsby, sitting down in the remotest corner by the door, as if he were taking a liberty, “it is not unlikely that you may inquire of me why Inspector Bucket, Mr. Woodcourt, and a lady call upon us in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, at the present hour. I don’t know. I have not the least idea. If I was to be informed, I should despair of understanding, and I’d rather not be told.”\n\nHe appeared so miserable, sitting with his head upon his hand, and I appeared so unwelcome, that I was going to offer an apology when Mr. Bucket took the matter on himself.\n\n“Now, Mr. Snagsby,” said he, “the best thing you can do is to go along with Mr. Woodcourt to look after your Guster—”\n\n“My Guster, Mr. Bucket!” cried Mr. Snagsby. “Go on, sir, go on. I shall be charged with that next.”\n\n“And to hold the candle,” pursued Mr. Bucket without correcting himself, “or hold her, or make yourself useful in any way you’re asked. Which there’s not a man alive more ready to do, for you’re a man of urbanity and suavity, you know, and you’ve got the sort of heart that can feel for another. Mr. Woodcourt, would you be so good as see to her, and if you can get that letter from her, to let me have it as soon as ever you can?”\n\nAs they went out, Mr. Bucket made me sit down in a corner by the fire and take off my wet shoes, which he turned up to dry upon the fender, talking all the time.\n\n“Don’t you be at all put out, miss, by the want of a hospitable look from Mrs. Snagsby there, because she’s under a mistake altogether. She’ll find that out sooner than will be agreeable to a lady of her generally correct manner of forming her thoughts, because I’m a-going to explain it to her.” Here, standing on the hearth with his wet hat and shawls in his hand, himself a pile of wet, he turned to Mrs. Snagsby. “Now, the first thing that I say to you, as a married woman possessing what you may call charms, you know—‘Believe Me, if All Those Endearing,’ and cetrer—you’re well acquainted with the song, because it’s in vain for you to tell me that you and good society are strangers—charms—attractions, mind you, that ought to give you confidence in yourself—is, that you’ve done it.”\n\nMrs. Snagsby looked rather alarmed, relented a little and faltered, what did Mr. Bucket mean.\n\n“What does Mr. Bucket mean?” he repeated, and I saw by his face that all the time he talked he was listening for the discovery of the letter, to my own great agitation, for I knew then how important it must be; “I’ll tell you what he means, ma’am. Go and see Othello acted. That’s the tragedy for you.”\n\nMrs. Snagsby consciously asked why.\n\n“Why?” said Mr. Bucket. “Because you’ll come to that if you don’t look out. Why, at the very moment while I speak, I know what your mind’s not wholly free from respecting this young lady. But shall I tell you who this young lady is? Now, come, you’re what I call an intellectual woman—with your soul too large for your body, if you come to that, and chafing it—and you know me, and you recollect where you saw me last, and what was talked of in that circle. Don’t you? Yes! Very well. This young lady is that young lady.”\n\nMrs. Snagsby appeared to understand the reference better than I did at the time.\n\n“And Toughey—him as you call Jo—was mixed up in the same business, and no other; and the law-writer that you know of was mixed up in the same business, and no other; and your husband, with no more knowledge of it than your great grandfather, was mixed up (by Mr. Tulkinghorn, deceased, his best customer) in the same business, and no other; and the whole bileing of people was mixed up in the same business, and no other. And yet a married woman, possessing your attractions, shuts her eyes (and sparklers too), and goes and runs her delicate-formed head against a wall. Why, I am ashamed of you! (I expected Mr. Woodcourt might have got it by this time.)”\n\nMrs. Snagsby shook her head and put her handkerchief to her eyes.\n\n“Is that all?” said Mr. Bucket excitedly. “No. See what happens. Another person mixed up in that business and no other, a person in a wretched state, comes here to-night and is seen a-speaking to your maid-servant; and between her and your maid-servant there passes a paper that I would give a hundred pound for, down. What do you do? You hide and you watch ’em, and you pounce upon that maid-servant—knowing what she’s subject to and what a little thing will bring ’em on—in that surprising manner and with that severity that, by the Lord, she goes off and keeps off, when a life may be hanging upon that girl’s words!”\n\nHe so thoroughly meant what he said now that I involuntarily clasped my hands and felt the room turning away from me. But it stopped. Mr. Woodcourt came in, put a paper into his hand, and went away again.\n\n“Now, Mrs. Snagsby, the only amends you can make,” said Mr. Bucket, rapidly glancing at it, “is to let me speak a word to this young lady in private here. And if you know of any help that you can give to that gentleman in the next kitchen there or can think of any one thing that’s likelier than another to bring the girl round, do your swiftest and best!” In an instant she was gone, and he had shut the door. “Now my dear, you’re steady and quite sure of yourself?”\n\n“Quite,” said I.\n\n“Whose writing is that?”\n\nIt was my mother’s. A pencil-writing, on a crushed and torn piece of paper, blotted with wet. Folded roughly like a letter, and directed to me at my guardian’s.\n\n“You know the hand,” he said, “and if you are firm enough to read it to me, do! But be particular to a word.”\n\nIt had been written in portions, at different times. I read what follows:\n\nI came to the cottage with two objects. First, to see the dear one, if I could, once more—but only to see her—not to speak to her or let her know that I was near. The other object, to elude pursuit and to be lost. Do not blame the mother for her share. The assistance that she rendered me, she rendered on my strongest assurance that it was for the dear one’s good. You remember her dead child. The men’s consent I bought, but her help was freely given.\n\n“‘I came.’ That was written,” said my companion, “when she rested there. It bears out what I made of it. I was right.”\n\nThe next was written at another time:\n\nI have wandered a long distance, and for many hours, and I know that I must soon die. These streets! I have no purpose but to die. When I left, I had a worse, but I am saved from adding that guilt to the rest. Cold, wet, and fatigue are sufficient causes for my being found dead, but I shall die of others, though I suffer from these. It was right that all that had sustained me should give way at once and that I should die of terror and my conscience.\n\n“Take courage,” said Mr. Bucket. “There’s only a few words more.”\n\nThose, too, were written at another time. To all appearance, almost in the dark:\n\nI have done all I could do to be lost. I shall be soon forgotten so, and shall disgrace him least. I have nothing about me by which I can be recognized. This paper I part with now. The place where I shall lie down, if I can get so far, has been often in my mind. Farewell. Forgive.\n\nMr. Bucket, supporting me with his arm, lowered me gently into my chair. “Cheer up! Don’t think me hard with you, my dear, but as soon as ever you feel equal to it, get your shoes on and be ready.”\n\nI did as he required, but I was left there a long time, praying for my unhappy mother. They were all occupied with the poor girl, and I heard Mr. Woodcourt directing them and speaking to her often. At length he came in with Mr. Bucket and said that as it was important to address her gently, he thought it best that I should ask her for whatever information we desired to obtain. There was no doubt that she could now reply to questions if she were soothed and not alarmed. The questions, Mr. Bucket said, were how she came by the letter, what passed between her and the person who gave her the letter, and where the person went. Holding my mind as steadily as I could to these points, I went into the next room with them. Mr. Woodcourt would have remained outside, but at my solicitation went in with us.\n\nThe poor girl was sitting on the floor where they had laid her down. They stood around her, though at a little distance, that she might have air. She was not pretty and looked weak and poor, but she had a plaintive and a good face, though it was still a little wild. I kneeled on the ground beside her and put her poor head upon my shoulder, whereupon she drew her arm round my neck and burst into tears.\n\n“My poor girl,” said I, laying my face against her forehead, for indeed I was crying too, and trembling, “it seems cruel to trouble you now, but more depends on our knowing something about this letter than I could tell you in an hour.”\n\nShe began piteously declaring that she didn’t mean any harm, she didn’t mean any harm, Mrs. Snagsby!\n\n“We are all sure of that,” said I. “But pray tell me how you got it.”\n\n“Yes, dear lady, I will, and tell you true. I’ll tell true, indeed, Mrs. Snagsby.”\n\n“I am sure of that,” said I. “And how was it?”\n\n“I had been out on an errand, dear lady—long after it was dark—quite late; and when I came home, I found a common-looking person, all wet and muddy, looking up at our house. When she saw me coming in at the door, she called me back and said did I live here. And I said yes, and she said she knew only one or two places about here, but had lost her way and couldn’t find them. Oh, what shall I do, what shall I do! They won’t believe me! She didn’t say any harm to me, and I didn’t say any harm to her, indeed, Mrs. Snagsby!”\n\nIt was necessary for her mistress to comfort her—which she did, I must say, with a good deal of contrition—before she could be got beyond this.\n\n“She could not find those places,” said I.\n\n“No!” cried the girl, shaking her head. “No! Couldn’t find them. And she was so faint, and lame, and miserable, Oh so wretched, that if you had seen her, Mr. Snagsby, you’d have given her half a crown, I know!”\n\n“Well, Guster, my girl,” said he, at first not knowing what to say. “I hope I should.”\n\n“And yet she was so well spoken,” said the girl, looking at me with wide open eyes, “that it made a person’s heart bleed. And so she said to me, did I know the way to the burying ground? And I asked her which burying ground. And she said, the poor burying ground. And so I told her I had been a poor child myself, and it was according to parishes. But she said she meant a poor burying ground not very far from here, where there was an archway, and a step, and an iron gate.”\n\nAs I watched her face and soothed her to go on, I saw that Mr. Bucket received this with a look which I could not separate from one of alarm.\n\n“Oh, dear, dear!” cried the girl, pressing her hair back with her hands. “What shall I do, what shall I do! She meant the burying ground where the man was buried that took the sleeping-stuff—that you came home and told us of, Mr. Snagsby—that frightened me so, Mrs. Snagsby. Oh, I am frightened again. Hold me!”\n\n“You are so much better now,” said I. “Pray, pray tell me more.”\n\n“Yes I will, yes I will! But don’t be angry with me, that’s a dear lady, because I have been so ill.”\n\nAngry with her, poor soul!\n\n“There! Now I will, now I will. So she said, could I tell her how to find it, and I said yes, and I told her; and she looked at me with eyes like almost as if she was blind, and herself all waving back. And so she took out the letter, and showed it me, and said if she was to put that in the post-office, it would be rubbed out and not minded and never sent; and would I take it from her, and send it, and the messenger would be paid at the house. And so I said yes, if it was no harm, and she said no—no harm. And so I took it from her, and she said she had nothing to give me, and I said I was poor myself and consequently wanted nothing. And so she said God bless you, and went.”\n\n“And did she go—”\n\n“Yes,” cried the girl, anticipating the inquiry. “Yes! She went the way I had shown her. Then I came in, and Mrs. Snagsby came behind me from somewhere and laid hold of me, and I was frightened.”\n\nMr. Woodcourt took her kindly from me. Mr. Bucket wrapped me up, and immediately we were in the street. Mr. Woodcourt hesitated, but I said, “Don’t leave me now!” and Mr. Bucket added, “You’ll be better with us, we may want you; don’t lose time!”\n\nI have the most confused impressions of that walk. I recollect that it was neither night nor day, that morning was dawning but the street-lamps were not yet put out, that the sleet was still falling and that all the ways were deep with it. I recollect a few chilled people passing in the streets. I recollect the wet house-tops, the clogged and bursting gutters and water-spouts, the mounds of blackened ice and snow over which we passed, the narrowness of the courts by which we went. At the same time I remember that the poor girl seemed to be yet telling her story audibly and plainly in my hearing, that I could feel her resting on my arm, that the stained house-fronts put on human shapes and looked at me, that great water-gates seemed to be opening and closing in my head or in the air, and that the unreal things were more substantial than the real.\n\nAt last we stood under a dark and miserable covered way, where one lamp was burning over an iron gate and where the morning faintly struggled in. The gate was closed. Beyond it was a burial ground—a dreadful spot in which the night was very slowly stirring, but where I could dimly see heaps of dishonoured graves and stones, hemmed in by filthy houses with a few dull lights in their windows and on whose walls a thick humidity broke out like a disease. On the step at the gate, drenched in the fearful wet of such a place, which oozed and splashed down everywhere, I saw, with a cry of pity and horror, a woman lying—Jenny, the mother of the dead child.\n\nI ran forward, but they stopped me, and Mr. Woodcourt entreated me with the greatest earnestness, even with tears, before I went up to the figure to listen for an instant to what Mr. Bucket said. I did so, as I thought. I did so, as I am sure.\n\n“Miss Summerson, you’ll understand me, if you think a moment. They changed clothes at the cottage.”\n\nThey changed clothes at the cottage. I could repeat the words in my mind, and I knew what they meant of themselves, but I attached no meaning to them in any other connexion.\n\n“And one returned,” said Mr. Bucket, “and one went on. And the one that went on only went on a certain way agreed upon to deceive and then turned across country and went home. Think a moment!”\n\nI could repeat this in my mind too, but I had not the least idea what it meant. I saw before me, lying on the step, the mother of the dead child. She lay there with one arm creeping round a bar of the iron gate and seeming to embrace it. She lay there, who had so lately spoken to my mother. She lay there, a distressed, unsheltered, senseless creature. She who had brought my mother’s letter, who could give me the only clue to where my mother was; she, who was to guide us to rescue and save her whom we had sought so far, who had come to this condition by some means connected with my mother that I could not follow, and might be passing beyond our reach and help at that moment; she lay there, and they stopped me! I saw but did not comprehend the solemn and compassionate look in Mr. Woodcourt’s face. I saw but did not comprehend his touching the other on the breast to keep him back. I saw him stand uncovered in the bitter air, with a reverence for something. But my understanding for all this was gone.\n\nI even heard it said between them, “Shall she go?”\n\n“She had better go. Her hands should be the first to touch her. They have a higher right than ours.”\n\nI passed on to the gate and stooped down. I lifted the heavy head, put the long dank hair aside, and turned the face. And it was my mother, cold and dead.\nPerspective\n\nI proceed to other passages of my narrative. From the goodness of all about me I derived such consolation as I can never think of unmoved. I have already said so much of myself, and so much still remains, that I will not dwell upon my sorrow. I had an illness, but it was not a long one; and I would avoid even this mention of it if I could quite keep down the recollection of their sympathy.\n\nI proceed to other passages of my narrative.\n\nDuring the time of my illness, we were still in London, where Mrs. Woodcourt had come, on my guardian’s invitation, to stay with us. When my guardian thought me well and cheerful enough to talk with him in our old way—though I could have done that sooner if he would have believed me—I resumed my work and my chair beside his. He had appointed the time himself, and we were alone.\n\n“Dame Trot,” said he, receiving me with a kiss, “welcome to the growlery again, my dear. I have a scheme to develop, little woman. I propose to remain here, perhaps for six months, perhaps for a longer time—as it may be. Quite to settle here for a while, in short.”\n\n“And in the meanwhile leave Bleak House?” said I.\n\n“Aye, my dear? Bleak House,” he returned, “must learn to take care of itself.”\n\nI thought his tone sounded sorrowful, but looking at him, I saw his kind face lighted up by its pleasantest smile.\n\n“Bleak House,” he repeated—and his tone did NOT sound sorrowful, I found—“must learn to take care of itself. It is a long way from Ada, my dear, and Ada stands much in need of you.”\n\n“It’s like you, guardian,” said I, “to have been taking that into consideration for a happy surprise to both of us.”\n\n“Not so disinterested either, my dear, if you mean to extol me for that virtue, since if you were generally on the road, you could be seldom with me. And besides, I wish to hear as much and as often of Ada as I can in this condition of estrangement from poor Rick. Not of her alone, but of him too, poor fellow.”\n\n“Have you seen Mr. Woodcourt, this morning, guardian?”\n\n“I see Mr. Woodcourt every morning, Dame Durden.”\n\n“Does he still say the same of Richard?”\n\n“Just the same. He knows of no direct bodily illness that he has; on the contrary, he believes that he has none. Yet he is not easy about him; who CAN be?”\n\nMy dear girl had been to see us lately every day, some times twice in a day. But we had foreseen, all along, that this would only last until I was quite myself. We knew full well that her fervent heart was as full of affection and gratitude towards her cousin John as it had ever been, and we acquitted Richard of laying any injunctions upon her to stay away; but we knew on the other hand that she felt it a part of her duty to him to be sparing of her visits at our house. My guardian’s delicacy had soon perceived this and had tried to convey to her that he thought she was right.\n\n“Dear, unfortunate, mistaken Richard,” said I. “When will he awake from his delusion!”\n\n“He is not in the way to do so now, my dear,” replied my guardian. “The more he suffers, the more averse he will be to me, having made me the principal representative of the great occasion of his suffering.”\n\nI could not help adding, “So unreasonably!”\n\n“Ah, Dame Trot, Dame Trot,” returned my guardian, “what shall we find reasonable in Jarndyce and Jarndyce! Unreason and injustice at the top, unreason and injustice at the heart and at the bottom, unreason and injustice from beginning to end—if it ever has an end—how should poor Rick, always hovering near it, pluck reason out of it? He no more gathers grapes from thorns or figs from thistles than older men did in old times.”\n\nHis gentleness and consideration for Richard whenever we spoke of him touched me so that I was always silent on this subject very soon.\n\n“I suppose the Lord Chancellor, and the Vice Chancellors, and the whole Chancery battery of great guns would be infinitely astonished by such unreason and injustice in one of their suitors,” pursued my guardian. “When those learned gentlemen begin to raise moss-roses from the powder they sow in their wigs, I shall begin to be astonished too!”\n\nHe checked himself in glancing towards the window to look where the wind was and leaned on the back of my chair instead.\n\n“Well, well, little woman! To go on, my dear. This rock we must leave to time, chance, and hopeful circumstance. We must not shipwreck Ada upon it. She cannot afford, and he cannot afford, the remotest chance of another separation from a friend. Therefore I have particularly begged of Woodcourt, and I now particularly beg of you, my dear, not to move this subject with Rick. Let it rest. Next week, next month, next year, sooner or later, he will see me with clearer eyes. I can wait.”\n\nBut I had already discussed it with him, I confessed; and so, I thought, had Mr. Woodcourt.\n\n“So he tells me,” returned my guardian. “Very good. He has made his protest, and Dame Durden has made hers, and there is nothing more to be said about it. Now I come to Mrs. Woodcourt. How do you like her, my dear?”\n\nIn answer to this question, which was oddly abrupt, I said I liked her very much and thought she was more agreeable than she used to be.\n\n“I think so too,” said my guardian. “Less pedigree? Not so much of Morgan ap—what’s his name?”\n\nThat was what I meant, I acknowledged, though he was a very harmless person, even when we had had more of him.\n\n“Still, upon the whole, he is as well in his native mountains,” said my guardian. “I agree with you. Then, little woman, can I do better for a time than retain Mrs. Woodcourt here?”\n\nNo. And yet—\n\nMy guardian looked at me, waiting for what I had to say.\n\nI had nothing to say. At least I had nothing in my mind that I could say. I had an undefined impression that it might have been better if we had had some other inmate, but I could hardly have explained why even to myself. Or, if to myself, certainly not to anybody else.\n\n“You see,” said my guardian, “our neighbourhood is in Woodcourt’s way, and he can come here to see her as often as he likes, which is agreeable to them both; and she is familiar to us and fond of you.”\n\nYes. That was undeniable. I had nothing to say against it. I could not have suggested a better arrangement, but I was not quite easy in my mind. Esther, Esther, why not? Esther, think!\n\n“It is a very good plan indeed, dear guardian, and we could not do better.”\n\n“Sure, little woman?”\n\nQuite sure. I had had a moment’s time to think, since I had urged that duty on myself, and I was quite sure.\n\n“Good,” said my guardian. “It shall be done. Carried unanimously.”\n\n“Carried unanimously,” I repeated, going on with my work.\n\nIt was a cover for his book-table that I happened to be ornamenting. It had been laid by on the night preceding my sad journey and never resumed. I showed it to him now, and he admired it highly. After I had explained the pattern to him and all the great effects that were to come out by and by, I thought I would go back to our last theme.\n\n“You said, dear guardian, when we spoke of Mr. Woodcourt before Ada left us, that you thought he would give a long trial to another country. Have you been advising him since?”\n\n“Yes, little woman, pretty often.”\n\n“Has he decided to do so?”\n\n“I rather think not.”\n\n“Some other prospect has opened to him, perhaps?” said I.\n\n“Why—yes—perhaps,” returned my guardian, beginning his answer in a very deliberate manner. “About half a year hence or so, there is a medical attendant for the poor to be appointed at a certain place in Yorkshire. It is a thriving place, pleasantly situated—streams and streets, town and country, mill and moor—and seems to present an opening for such a man. I mean a man whose hopes and aims may sometimes lie (as most men’s sometimes do, I dare say) above the ordinary level, but to whom the ordinary level will be high enough after all if it should prove to be a way of usefulness and good service leading to no other. All generous spirits are ambitious, I suppose, but the ambition that calmly trusts itself to such a road, instead of spasmodically trying to fly over it, is of the kind I care for. It is Woodcourt’s kind.”\n\n“And will he get this appointment?” I asked.\n\n“Why, little woman,” returned my guardian, smiling, “not being an oracle, I cannot confidently say, but I think so. His reputation stands very high; there were people from that part of the country in the shipwreck; and strange to say, I believe the best man has the best chance. You must not suppose it to be a fine endowment. It is a very, very commonplace affair, my dear, an appointment to a great amount of work and a small amount of pay; but better things will gather about it, it may be fairly hoped.”\n\n“The poor of that place will have reason to bless the choice if it falls on Mr. Woodcourt, guardian.”\n\n“You are right, little woman; that I am sure they will.”\n\nWe said no more about it, nor did he say a word about the future of Bleak House. But it was the first time I had taken my seat at his side in my mourning dress, and that accounted for it, I considered.\n\nI now began to visit my dear girl every day in the dull dark corner where she lived. The morning was my usual time, but whenever I found I had an hour or so to spare, I put on my bonnet and bustled off to Chancery Lane. They were both so glad to see me at all hours, and used to brighten up so when they heard me opening the door and coming in (being quite at home, I never knocked), that I had no fear of becoming troublesome just yet.\n\nOn these occasions I frequently found Richard absent. At other times he would be writing or reading papers in the cause at that table of his, so covered with papers, which was never disturbed. Sometimes I would come upon him lingering at the door of Mr. Vholes’s office. Sometimes I would meet him in the neighbourhood lounging about and biting his nails. I often met him wandering in Lincoln’s Inn, near the place where I had first seen him, oh how different, how different!\n\nThat the money Ada brought him was melting away with the candles I used to see burning after dark in Mr. Vholes’s office I knew very well. It was not a large amount in the beginning, he had married in debt, and I could not fail to understand, by this time, what was meant by Mr. Vholes’s shoulder being at the wheel—as I still heard it was. My dear made the best of housekeepers and tried hard to save, but I knew that they were getting poorer and poorer every day.\n\nShe shone in the miserable corner like a beautiful star. She adorned and graced it so that it became another place. Paler than she had been at home, and a little quieter than I had thought natural when she was yet so cheerful and hopeful, her face was so unshadowed that I half believed she was blinded by her love for Richard to his ruinous career.\n\nI went one day to dine with them while I was under this impression. As I turned into Symond’s Inn, I met little Miss Flite coming out. She had been to make a stately call upon the wards in Jarndyce, as she still called them, and had derived the highest gratification from that ceremony. Ada had already told me that she called every Monday at five o’clock, with one little extra white bow in her bonnet, which never appeared there at any other time, and with her largest reticule of documents on her arm.\n\n“My dear!” she began. “So delighted! How do you do! So glad to see you. And you are going to visit our interesting Jarndyce wards? TO be sure! Our beauty is at home, my dear, and will be charmed to see you.”\n\n“Then Richard is not come in yet?” said I. “I am glad of that, for I was afraid of being a little late.”\n\n“No, he is not come in,” returned Miss Flite. “He has had a long day in court. I left him there with Vholes. You don’t like Vholes, I hope? DON’T like Vholes. Dan-gerous man!”\n\n“I am afraid you see Richard oftener than ever now,” said I.\n\n“My dearest,” returned Miss Flite, “daily and hourly. You know what I told you of the attraction on the Chancellor’s table? My dear, next to myself he is the most constant suitor in court. He begins quite to amuse our little party. Ve-ry friendly little party, are we not?”\n\nIt was miserable to hear this from her poor mad lips, though it was no surprise.\n\n“In short, my valued friend,” pursued Miss Flite, advancing her lips to my ear with an air of equal patronage and mystery, “I must tell you a secret. I have made him my executor. Nominated, constituted, and appointed him. In my will. Ye-es.”\n\n“Indeed?” said I.\n\n“Ye-es,” repeated Miss Flite in her most genteel accents, “my executor, administrator, and assign. (Our Chancery phrases, my love.) I have reflected that if I should wear out, he will be able to watch that judgment. Being so very regular in his attendance.”\n\nIt made me sigh to think of him.\n\n“I did at one time mean,” said Miss Flite, echoing the sigh, “to nominate, constitute, and appoint poor Gridley. Also very regular, my charming girl. I assure you, most exemplary! But he wore out, poor man, so I have appointed his successor. Don’t mention it. This is in confidence.”\n\nShe carefully opened her reticule a little way and showed me a folded piece of paper inside as the appointment of which she spoke.\n\n“Another secret, my dear. I have added to my collection of birds.”\n\n“Really, Miss Flite?” said I, knowing how it pleased her to have her confidence received with an appearance of interest.\n\nShe nodded several times, and her face became overcast and gloomy. “Two more. I call them the Wards in Jarndyce. They are caged up with all the others. With Hope, Joy, Youth, Peace, Rest, Life, Dust, Ashes, Waste, Want, Ruin, Despair, Madness, Death, Cunning, Folly, Words, Wigs, Rags, Sheepskin, Plunder, Precedent, Jargon, Gammon, and Spinach!”\n\nThe poor soul kissed me with the most troubled look I had ever seen in her and went her way. Her manner of running over the names of her birds, as if she were afraid of hearing them even from her own lips, quite chilled me.\n\nThis was not a cheering preparation for my visit, and I could have dispensed with the company of Mr. Vholes, when Richard (who arrived within a minute or two after me) brought him to share our dinner. Although it was a very plain one, Ada and Richard were for some minutes both out of the room together helping to get ready what we were to eat and drink. Mr. Vholes took that opportunity of holding a little conversation in a low voice with me. He came to the window where I was sitting and began upon Symond’s Inn.\n\n“A dull place, Miss Summerson, for a life that is not an official one,” said Mr. Vholes, smearing the glass with his black glove to make it clearer for me.\n\n“There is not much to see here,” said I.\n\n“Nor to hear, miss,” returned Mr. Vholes. “A little music does occasionally stray in, but we are not musical in the law and soon eject it. I hope Mr. Jarndyce is as well as his friends could wish him?”\n\nI thanked Mr. Vholes and said he was quite well.\n\n“I have not the pleasure to be admitted among the number of his friends myself,” said Mr. Vholes, “and I am aware that the gentlemen of our profession are sometimes regarded in such quarters with an unfavourable eye. Our plain course, however, under good report and evil report, and all kinds of prejudice (we are the victims of prejudice), is to have everything openly carried on. How do you find Mr. C. looking, Miss Summerson?”\n\n“He looks very ill. Dreadfully anxious.”\n\n“Just so,” said Mr. Vholes.\n\nHe stood behind me with his long black figure reaching nearly to the ceiling of those low rooms, feeling the pimples on his face as if they were ornaments and speaking inwardly and evenly as though there were not a human passion or emotion in his nature.\n\n“Mr. Woodcourt is in attendance upon Mr. C., I believe?” he resumed.\n\n“Mr. Woodcourt is his disinterested friend,” I answered.\n\n“But I mean in professional attendance, medical attendance.”\n\n“That can do little for an unhappy mind,” said I.\n\n“Just so,” said Mr. Vholes.\n\nSo slow, so eager, so bloodless and gaunt, I felt as if Richard were wasting away beneath the eyes of this adviser and there were something of the vampire in him.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Vholes, very slowly rubbing his gloved hands, as if, to his cold sense of touch, they were much the same in black kid or out of it, “this was an ill-advised marriage of Mr. C.’s.”\n\nI begged he would excuse me from discussing it. They had been engaged when they were both very young, I told him (a little indignantly) and when the prospect before them was much fairer and brighter. When Richard had not yielded himself to the unhappy influence which now darkened his life.\n\n“Just so,” assented Mr. Vholes again. “Still, with a view to everything being openly carried on, I will, with your permission, Miss Summerson, observe to you that I consider this a very ill-advised marriage indeed. I owe the opinion not only to Mr. C.’s connexions, against whom I should naturally wish to protect myself, but also to my own reputation—dear to myself as a professional man aiming to keep respectable; dear to my three girls at home, for whom I am striving to realize some little independence; dear, I will even say, to my aged father, whom it is my privilege to support.”\n\n“It would become a very different marriage, a much happier and better marriage, another marriage altogether, Mr. Vholes,” said I, “if Richard were persuaded to turn his back on the fatal pursuit in which you are engaged with him.”\n\nMr. Vholes, with a noiseless cough—or rather gasp—into one of his black gloves, inclined his head as if he did not wholly dispute even that.\n\n“Miss Summerson,” he said, “it may be so; and I freely admit that the young lady who has taken Mr. C.’s name upon herself in so ill-advised a manner—you will I am sure not quarrel with me for throwing out that remark again, as a duty I owe to Mr. C.’s connexions—is a highly genteel young lady. Business has prevented me from mixing much with general society in any but a professional character; still I trust I am competent to perceive that she is a highly genteel young lady. As to beauty, I am not a judge of that myself, and I never did give much attention to it from a boy, but I dare say the young lady is equally eligible in that point of view. She is considered so (I have heard) among the clerks in the Inn, and it is a point more in their way than in mine. In reference to Mr. C.’s pursuit of his interests—”\n\n“Oh! His interests, Mr. Vholes!”\n\n“Pardon me,” returned Mr. Vholes, going on in exactly the same inward and dispassionate manner. “Mr. C. takes certain interests under certain wills disputed in the suit. It is a term we use. In reference to Mr. C,’s pursuit of his interests, I mentioned to you, Miss Summerson, the first time I had the pleasure of seeing you, in my desire that everything should be openly carried on—I used those words, for I happened afterwards to note them in my diary, which is producible at any time—I mentioned to you that Mr. C. had laid down the principle of watching his own interests, and that when a client of mine laid down a principle which was not of an immoral (that is to say, unlawful) nature, it devolved upon me to carry it out. I HAVE carried it out; I do carry it out. But I will not smooth things over to any connexion of Mr. C.’s on any account. As open as I was to Mr. Jarndyce, I am to you. I regard it in the light of a professional duty to be so, though it can be charged to no one. I openly say, unpalatable as it may be, that I consider Mr. C.’s affairs in a very bad way, that I consider Mr. C. himself in a very bad way, and that I regard this as an exceedingly ill-advised marriage. Am I here, sir? Yes, I thank you; I am here, Mr. C., and enjoying the pleasure of some agreeable conversation with Miss Summerson, for which I have to thank you very much, sir!”\n\nHe broke off thus in answer to Richard, who addressed him as he came into the room. By this time I too well understood Mr. Vholes’s scrupulous way of saving himself and his respectability not to feel that our worst fears did but keep pace with his client’s progress.\n\nWe sat down to dinner, and I had an opportunity of observing Richard, anxiously. I was not disturbed by Mr. Vholes (who took off his gloves to dine), though he sat opposite to me at the small table, for I doubt if, looking up at all, he once removed his eyes from his host’s face. I found Richard thin and languid, slovenly in his dress, abstracted in his manner, forcing his spirits now and then, and at other intervals relapsing into a dull thoughtfulness. About his large bright eyes that used to be so merry there was a wanness and a restlessness that changed them altogether. I cannot use the expression that he looked old. There is a ruin of youth which is not like age, and into such a ruin Richard’s youth and youthful beauty had all fallen away.\n\nHe ate little and seemed indifferent what it was, showed himself to be much more impatient than he used to be, and was quick even with Ada. I thought at first that his old light-hearted manner was all gone, but it shone out of him sometimes as I had occasionally known little momentary glimpses of my own old face to look out upon me from the glass. His laugh had not quite left him either, but it was like the echo of a joyful sound, and that is always sorrowful.\n\nYet he was as glad as ever, in his old affectionate way, to have me there, and we talked of the old times pleasantly. These did not appear to be interesting to Mr. Vholes, though he occasionally made a gasp which I believe was his smile. He rose shortly after dinner and said that with the permission of the ladies he would retire to his office.\n\n“Always devoted to business, Vholes!” cried Richard.\n\n“Yes, Mr. C.,” he returned, “the interests of clients are never to be neglected, sir. They are paramount in the thoughts of a professional man like myself, who wishes to preserve a good name among his fellow-practitioners and society at large. My denying myself the pleasure of the present agreeable conversation may not be wholly irrespective of your own interests, Mr. C.”\n\nRichard expressed himself quite sure of that and lighted Mr. Vholes out. On his return he told us, more than once, that Vholes was a good fellow, a safe fellow, a man who did what he pretended to do, a very good fellow indeed! He was so defiant about it that it struck me he had begun to doubt Mr. Vholes.\n\nThen he threw himself on the sofa, tired out; and Ada and I put things to rights, for they had no other servant than the woman who attended to the chambers. My dear girl had a cottage piano there and quietly sat down to sing some of Richard’s favourites, the lamp being first removed into the next room, as he complained of its hurting his eyes.\n\nI sat between them, at my dear girl’s side, and felt very melancholy listening to her sweet voice. I think Richard did too; I think he darkened the room for that reason. She had been singing some time, rising between whiles to bend over him and speak to him, when Mr. Woodcourt came in. Then he sat down by Richard and half playfully, half earnestly, quite naturally and easily, found out how he felt and where he had been all day. Presently he proposed to accompany him in a short walk on one of the bridges, as it was a moonlight airy night; and Richard readily consenting, they went out together.\n\nThey left my dear girl still sitting at the piano and me still sitting beside her. When they were gone out, I drew my arm round her waist. She put her left hand in mine (I was sitting on that side), but kept her right upon the keys, going over and over them without striking any note.\n\n“Esther, my dearest,” she said, breaking silence, “Richard is never so well and I am never so easy about him as when he is with Allan Woodcourt. We have to thank you for that.”\n\nI pointed out to my darling how this could scarcely be, because Mr. Woodcourt had come to her cousin John’s house and had known us all there, and because he had always liked Richard, and Richard had always liked him, and—and so forth.\n\n“All true,” said Ada, “but that he is such a devoted friend to us we owe to you.”\n\nI thought it best to let my dear girl have her way and to say no more about it. So I said as much. I said it lightly, because I felt her trembling.\n\n“Esther, my dearest, I want to be a good wife, a very, very good wife indeed. You shall teach me.”\n\nI teach! I said no more, for I noticed the hand that was fluttering over the keys, and I knew that it was not I who ought to speak, that it was she who had something to say to me.\n\n“When I married Richard I was not insensible to what was before him. I had been perfectly happy for a long time with you, and I had never known any trouble or anxiety, so loved and cared for, but I understood the danger he was in, dear Esther.”\n\n“I know, I know, my darling.”\n\n“When we were married I had some little hope that I might be able to convince him of his mistake, that he might come to regard it in a new way as my husband and not pursue it all the more desperately for my sake—as he does. But if I had not had that hope, I would have married him just the same, Esther. Just the same!”\n\nIn the momentary firmness of the hand that was never still—a firmness inspired by the utterance of these last words, and dying away with them—I saw the confirmation of her earnest tones.\n\n“You are not to think, my dearest Esther, that I fail to see what you see and fear what you fear. No one can understand him better than I do. The greatest wisdom that ever lived in the world could scarcely know Richard better than my love does.”\n\nShe spoke so modestly and softly and her trembling hand expressed such agitation as it moved to and fro upon the silent notes! My dear, dear girl!\n\n“I see him at his worst every day. I watch him in his sleep. I know every change of his face. But when I married Richard I was quite determined, Esther, if heaven would help me, never to show him that I grieved for what he did and so to make him more unhappy. I want him, when he comes home, to find no trouble in my face. I want him, when he looks at me, to see what he loved in me. I married him to do this, and this supports me.”\n\nI felt her trembling more. I waited for what was yet to come, and I now thought I began to know what it was.\n\n“And something else supports me, Esther.”\n\nShe stopped a minute. Stopped speaking only; her hand was still in motion.\n\n“I look forward a little while, and I don’t know what great aid may come to me. When Richard turns his eyes upon me then, there may be something lying on my breast more eloquent than I have been, with greater power than mine to show him his true course and win him back.”\n\nHer hand stopped now. She clasped me in her arms, and I clasped her in mine.\n\n“If that little creature should fail too, Esther, I still look forward. I look forward a long while, through years and years, and think that then, when I am growing old, or when I am dead perhaps, a beautiful woman, his daughter, happily married, may be proud of him and a blessing to him. Or that a generous brave man, as handsome as he used to be, as hopeful, and far more happy, may walk in the sunshine with him, honouring his grey head and saying to himself, ‘I thank God this is my father! Ruined by a fatal inheritance, and restored through me!’”\n\nOh, my sweet girl, what a heart was that which beat so fast against me!\n\n“These hopes uphold me, my dear Esther, and I know they will. Though sometimes even they depart from me before a dread that arises when I look at Richard.”\n\nI tried to cheer my darling, and asked her what it was. Sobbing and weeping, she replied, “That he may not live to see his child.”\nA Discovery\n\nThe days when I frequented that miserable corner which my dear girl brightened can never fade in my remembrance. I never see it, and I never wish to see it now; I have been there only once since, but in my memory there is a mournful glory shining on the place which will shine for ever.\n\nNot a day passed without my going there, of course. At first I found Mr. Skimpole there, on two or three occasions, idly playing the piano and talking in his usual vivacious strain. Now, besides my very much mistrusting the probability of his being there without making Richard poorer, I felt as if there were something in his careless gaiety too inconsistent with what I knew of the depths of Ada’s life. I clearly perceived, too, that Ada shared my feelings. I therefore resolved, after much thinking of it, to make a private visit to Mr. Skimpole and try delicately to explain myself. My dear girl was the great consideration that made me bold.\n\nI set off one morning, accompanied by Charley, for Somers Town. As I approached the house, I was strongly inclined to turn back, for I felt what a desperate attempt it was to make an impression on Mr. Skimpole and how extremely likely it was that he would signally defeat me. However, I thought that being there, I would go through with it. I knocked with a trembling hand at Mr. Skimpole’s door—literally with a hand, for the knocker was gone—and after a long parley gained admission from an Irishwoman, who was in the area when I knocked, breaking up the lid of a water-butt with a poker to light the fire with.\n\nMr. Skimpole, lying on the sofa in his room, playing the flute a little, was enchanted to see me. Now, who should receive me, he asked. Who would I prefer for mistress of the ceremonies? Would I have his Comedy daughter, his Beauty daughter, or his Sentiment daughter? Or would I have all the daughters at once in a perfect nosegay?\n\nI replied, half defeated already, that I wished to speak to himself only if he would give me leave.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson, most joyfully! Of course,” he said, bringing his chair nearer mine and breaking into his fascinating smile, “of course it’s not business. Then it’s pleasure!”\n\nI said it certainly was not business that I came upon, but it was not quite a pleasant matter.\n\n“Then, my dear Miss Summerson,” said he with the frankest gaiety, “don’t allude to it. Why should you allude to anything that is NOT a pleasant matter? I never do. And you are a much pleasanter creature, in every point of view, than I. You are perfectly pleasant; I am imperfectly pleasant; then, if I never allude to an unpleasant matter, how much less should you! So that’s disposed of, and we will talk of something else.”\n\nAlthough I was embarrassed, I took courage to intimate that I still wished to pursue the subject.\n\n“I should think it a mistake,” said Mr. Skimpole with his airy laugh, “if I thought Miss Summerson capable of making one. But I don’t!”\n\n“Mr. Skimpole,” said I, raising my eyes to his, “I have so often heard you say that you are unacquainted with the common affairs of life—”\n\n“Meaning our three banking-house friends, L, S, and who’s the junior partner? D?” said Mr. Skimpole, brightly. “Not an idea of them!”\n\n“—That perhaps,” I went on, “you will excuse my boldness on that account. I think you ought most seriously to know that Richard is poorer than he was.”\n\n“Dear me!” said Mr. Skimpole. “So am I, they tell me.”\n\n“And in very embarrassed circumstances.”\n\n“Parallel case, exactly!” said Mr. Skimpole with a delighted countenance.\n\n“This at present naturally causes Ada much secret anxiety, and as I think she is less anxious when no claims are made upon her by visitors, and as Richard has one uneasiness always heavy on his mind, it has occurred to me to take the liberty of saying that—if you would—not—”\n\nI was coming to the point with great difficulty when he took me by both hands and with a radiant face and in the liveliest way anticipated it.\n\n“Not go there? Certainly not, my dear Miss Summerson, most assuredly not. Why SHOULD I go there? When I go anywhere, I go for pleasure. I don’t go anywhere for pain, because I was made for pleasure. Pain comes to ME when it wants me. Now, I have had very little pleasure at our dear Richard’s lately, and your practical sagacity demonstrates why. Our young friends, losing the youthful poetry which was once so captivating in them, begin to think, ‘This is a man who wants pounds.’ So I am; I always want pounds; not for myself, but because tradespeople always want them of me. Next, our young friends begin to think, becoming mercenary, ‘This is the man who HAD pounds, who borrowed them,’ which I did. I always borrow pounds. So our young friends, reduced to prose (which is much to be regretted), degenerate in their power of imparting pleasure to me. Why should I go to see them, therefore? Absurd!”\n\nThrough the beaming smile with which he regarded me as he reasoned thus, there now broke forth a look of disinterested benevolence quite astonishing.\n\n“Besides,” he said, pursuing his argument in his tone of light-hearted conviction, “if I don’t go anywhere for pain—which would be a perversion of the intention of my being, and a monstrous thing to do—why should I go anywhere to be the cause of pain? If I went to see our young friends in their present ill-regulated state of mind, I should give them pain. The associations with me would be disagreeable. They might say, ‘This is the man who had pounds and who can’t pay pounds,’ which I can’t, of course; nothing could be more out of the question! Then kindness requires that I shouldn’t go near them—and I won’t.”\n\nHe finished by genially kissing my hand and thanking me. Nothing but Miss Summerson’s fine tact, he said, would have found this out for him.\n\nI was much disconcerted, but I reflected that if the main point were gained, it mattered little how strangely he perverted everything leading to it. I had determined to mention something else, however, and I thought I was not to be put off in that.\n\n“Mr. Skimpole,” said I, “I must take the liberty of saying before I conclude my visit that I was much surprised to learn, on the best authority, some little time ago, that you knew with whom that poor boy left Bleak House and that you accepted a present on that occasion. I have not mentioned it to my guardian, for I fear it would hurt him unnecessarily; but I may say to you that I was much surprised.”\n\n“No? Really surprised, my dear Miss Summerson?” he returned inquiringly, raising his pleasant eyebrows.\n\n“Greatly surprised.”\n\nHe thought about it for a little while with a highly agreeable and whimsical expression of face, then quite gave it up and said in his most engaging manner, “You know what a child I am. Why surprised?”\n\nI was reluctant to enter minutely into that question, but as he begged I would, for he was really curious to know, I gave him to understand in the gentlest words I could use that his conduct seemed to involve a disregard of several moral obligations. He was much amused and interested when he heard this and said, “No, really?” with ingenuous simplicity.\n\n“You know I don’t intend to be responsible. I never could do it. Responsibility is a thing that has always been above me—or below me,” said Mr. Skimpole. “I don’t even know which; but as I understand the way in which my dear Miss Summerson (always remarkable for her practical good sense and clearness) puts this case, I should imagine it was chiefly a question of money, do you know?”\n\nI incautiously gave a qualified assent to this.\n\n“Ah! Then you see,” said Mr. Skimpole, shaking his head, “I am hopeless of understanding it.”\n\nI suggested, as I rose to go, that it was not right to betray my guardian’s confidence for a bribe.\n\n“My dear Miss Summerson,” he returned with a candid hilarity that was all his own, “I can’t be bribed.”\n\n“Not by Mr. Bucket?” said I.\n\n“No,” said he. “Not by anybody. I don’t attach any value to money. I don’t care about it, I don’t know about it, I don’t want it, I don’t keep it—it goes away from me directly. How can I be bribed?”\n\nI showed that I was of a different opinion, though I had not the capacity for arguing the question.\n\n“On the contrary,” said Mr. Skimpole, “I am exactly the man to be placed in a superior position in such a case as that. I am above the rest of mankind in such a case as that. I can act with philosophy in such a case as that. I am not warped by prejudices, as an Italian baby is by bandages. I am as free as the air. I feel myself as far above suspicion as Caesar’s wife.”\n\nAnything to equal the lightness of his manner and the playful impartiality with which he seemed to convince himself, as he tossed the matter about like a ball of feathers, was surely never seen in anybody else!\n\n“Observe the case, my dear Miss Summerson. Here is a boy received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to. The boy being in bed, a man arrives—like the house that Jack built. Here is the man who demands the boy who is received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to. Here is a bank-note produced by the man who demands the boy who is received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to. Here is the Skimpole who accepts the bank-note produced by the man who demands the boy who is received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to. Those are the facts. Very well. Should the Skimpole have refused the note? WHY should the Skimpole have refused the note? Skimpole protests to Bucket, ‘What’s this for? I don’t understand it, it is of no use to me, take it away.’ Bucket still entreats Skimpole to accept it. Are there reasons why Skimpole, not being warped by prejudices, should accept it? Yes. Skimpole perceives them. What are they? Skimpole reasons with himself, this is a tamed lynx, an active police-officer, an intelligent man, a person of a peculiarly directed energy and great subtlety both of conception and execution, who discovers our friends and enemies for us when they run away, recovers our property for us when we are robbed, avenges us comfortably when we are murdered. This active police-officer and intelligent man has acquired, in the exercise of his art, a strong faith in money; he finds it very useful to him, and he makes it very useful to society. Shall I shake that faith in Bucket because I want it myself; shall I deliberately blunt one of Bucket’s weapons; shall I positively paralyse Bucket in his next detective operation? And again. If it is blameable in Skimpole to take the note, it is blameable in Bucket to offer the note—much more blameable in Bucket, because he is the knowing man. Now, Skimpole wishes to think well of Bucket; Skimpole deems it essential, in its little place, to the general cohesion of things, that he SHOULD think well of Bucket. The state expressly asks him to trust to Bucket. And he does. And that’s all he does!”\n\nI had nothing to offer in reply to this exposition and therefore took my leave. Mr. Skimpole, however, who was in excellent spirits, would not hear of my returning home attended only by “Little Coavinses,” and accompanied me himself. He entertained me on the way with a variety of delightful conversation and assured me, at parting, that he should never forget the fine tact with which I had found that out for him about our young friends.\n\nAs it so happened that I never saw Mr. Skimpole again, I may at once finish what I know of his history. A coolness arose between him and my guardian, based principally on the foregoing grounds and on his having heartlessly disregarded my guardian’s entreaties (as we afterwards learned from Ada) in reference to Richard. His being heavily in my guardian’s debt had nothing to do with their separation. He died some five years afterwards and left a diary behind him, with letters and other materials towards his life, which was published and which showed him to have been the victim of a combination on the part of mankind against an amiable child. It was considered very pleasant reading, but I never read more of it myself than the sentence on which I chanced to light on opening the book. It was this: “Jarndyce, in common with most other men I have known, is the incarnation of selfishness.”\n\nAnd now I come to a part of my story touching myself very nearly indeed, and for which I was quite unprepared when the circumstance occurred. Whatever little lingerings may have now and then revived in my mind associated with my poor old face had only revived as belonging to a part of my life that was gone—gone like my infancy or my childhood. I have suppressed none of my many weaknesses on that subject, but have written them as faithfully as my memory has recalled them. And I hope to do, and mean to do, the same down to the last words of these pages, which I see now not so very far before me.\n\nThe months were gliding away, and my dear girl, sustained by the hopes she had confided in me, was the same beautiful star in the miserable corner. Richard, more worn and haggard, haunted the court day after day, listlessly sat there the whole day long when he knew there was no remote chance of the suit being mentioned, and became one of the stock sights of the place. I wonder whether any of the gentlemen remembered him as he was when he first went there.\n\nSo completely was he absorbed in his fixed idea that he used to avow in his cheerful moments that he should never have breathed the fresh air now “but for Woodcourt.” It was only Mr. Woodcourt who could occasionally divert his attention for a few hours at a time and rouse him, even when he sunk into a lethargy of mind and body that alarmed us greatly, and the returns of which became more frequent as the months went on. My dear girl was right in saying that he only pursued his errors the more desperately for her sake. I have no doubt that his desire to retrieve what he had lost was rendered the more intense by his grief for his young wife, and became like the madness of a gamester.\n\nI was there, as I have mentioned, at all hours. When I was there at night, I generally went home with Charley in a coach; sometimes my guardian would meet me in the neighbourhood, and we would walk home together. One evening he had arranged to meet me at eight o’clock. I could not leave, as I usually did, quite punctually at the time, for I was working for my dear girl and had a few stitches more to do to finish what I was about; but it was within a few minutes of the hour when I bundled up my little work-basket, gave my darling my last kiss for the night, and hurried downstairs. Mr. Woodcourt went with me, as it was dusk.\n\nWhen we came to the usual place of meeting—it was close by, and Mr. Woodcourt had often accompanied me before—my guardian was not there. We waited half an hour, walking up and down, but there were no signs of him. We agreed that he was either prevented from coming or that he had come and gone away, and Mr. Woodcourt proposed to walk home with me.\n\nIt was the first walk we had ever taken together, except that very short one to the usual place of meeting. We spoke of Richard and Ada the whole way. I did not thank him in words for what he had done—my appreciation of it had risen above all words then—but I hoped he might not be without some understanding of what I felt so strongly.\n\nArriving at home and going upstairs, we found that my guardian was out and that Mrs. Woodcourt was out too. We were in the very same room into which I had brought my blushing girl when her youthful lover, now her so altered husband, was the choice of her young heart, the very same room from which my guardian and I had watched them going away through the sunlight in the fresh bloom of their hope and promise.\n\nWe were standing by the opened window looking down into the street when Mr. Woodcourt spoke to me. I learned in a moment that he loved me. I learned in a moment that my scarred face was all unchanged to him. I learned in a moment that what I had thought was pity and compassion was devoted, generous, faithful love. Oh, too late to know it now, too late, too late. That was the first ungrateful thought I had. Too late.\n\n“When I returned,” he told me, “when I came back, no richer than when I went away, and found you newly risen from a sick bed, yet so inspired by sweet consideration for others and so free from a selfish thought—”\n\n“Oh, Mr. Woodcourt, forbear, forbear!” I entreated him. “I do not deserve your high praise. I had many selfish thoughts at that time, many!”\n\n“Heaven knows, beloved of my life,” said he, “that my praise is not a lover’s praise, but the truth. You do not know what all around you see in Esther Summerson, how many hearts she touches and awakens, what sacred admiration and what love she wins.”\n\n“Oh, Mr. Woodcourt,” cried I, “it is a great thing to win love, it is a great thing to win love! I am proud of it, and honoured by it; and the hearing of it causes me to shed these tears of mingled joy and sorrow—joy that I have won it, sorrow that I have not deserved it better; but I am not free to think of yours.”\n\nI said it with a stronger heart, for when he praised me thus and when I heard his voice thrill with his belief that what he said was true, I aspired to be more worthy of it. It was not too late for that. Although I closed this unforeseen page in my life to-night, I could be worthier of it all through my life. And it was a comfort to me, and an impulse to me, and I felt a dignity rise up within me that was derived from him when I thought so.\n\nHe broke the silence.\n\n“I should poorly show the trust that I have in the dear one who will evermore be as dear to me as now”—and the deep earnestness with which he said it at once strengthened me and made me weep—“if, after her assurance that she is not free to think of my love, I urged it. Dear Esther, let me only tell you that the fond idea of you which I took abroad was exalted to the heavens when I came home. I have always hoped, in the first hour when I seemed to stand in any ray of good fortune, to tell you this. I have always feared that I should tell it you in vain. My hopes and fears are both fulfilled to-night. I distress you. I have said enough.”\n\nSomething seemed to pass into my place that was like the angel he thought me, and I felt so sorrowful for the loss he had sustained! I wished to help him in his trouble, as I had wished to do when he showed that first commiseration for me.\n\n“Dear Mr. Woodcourt,” said I, “before we part to-night, something is left for me to say. I never could say it as I wish—I never shall—but—”\n\nI had to think again of being more deserving of his love and his affliction before I could go on.\n\n“—I am deeply sensible of your generosity, and I shall treasure its remembrance to my dying hour. I know full well how changed I am, I know you are not unacquainted with my history, and I know what a noble love that is which is so faithful. What you have said to me could have affected me so much from no other lips, for there are none that could give it such a value to me. It shall not be lost. It shall make me better.”\n\nHe covered his eyes with his hand and turned away his head. How could I ever be worthy of those tears?\n\n“If, in the unchanged intercourse we shall have together—in tending Richard and Ada, and I hope in many happier scenes of life—you ever find anything in me which you can honestly think is better than it used to be, believe that it will have sprung up from to-night and that I shall owe it to you. And never believe, dear dear Mr. Woodcourt, never believe that I forget this night or that while my heart beats it can be insensible to the pride and joy of having been beloved by you.”\n\nHe took my hand and kissed it. He was like himself again, and I felt still more encouraged.\n\n“I am induced by what you said just now,” said I, “to hope that you have succeeded in your endeavour.”\n\n“I have,” he answered. “With such help from Mr. Jarndyce as you who know him so well can imagine him to have rendered me, I have succeeded.”\n\n“Heaven bless him for it,” said I, giving him my hand; “and heaven bless you in all you do!”\n\n“I shall do it better for the wish,” he answered; “it will make me enter on these new duties as on another sacred trust from you.”\n\n“Ah! Richard!” I exclaimed involuntarily, “What will he do when you are gone!”\n\n“I am not required to go yet; I would not desert him, dear Miss Summerson, even if I were.”\n\nOne other thing I felt it needful to touch upon before he left me. I knew that I should not be worthier of the love I could not take if I reserved it.\n\n“Mr. Woodcourt,” said I, “you will be glad to know from my lips before I say good night that in the future, which is clear and bright before me, I am most happy, most fortunate, have nothing to regret or desire.”\n\nIt was indeed a glad hearing to him, he replied.\n\n“From my childhood I have been,” said I, “the object of the untiring goodness of the best of human beings, to whom I am so bound by every tie of attachment, gratitude, and love, that nothing I could do in the compass of a life could express the feelings of a single day.”\n\n“I share those feelings,” he returned. “You speak of Mr. Jarndyce.”\n\n“You know his virtues well,” said I, “but few can know the greatness of his character as I know it. All its highest and best qualities have been revealed to me in nothing more brightly than in the shaping out of that future in which I am so happy. And if your highest homage and respect had not been his already—which I know they are—they would have been his, I think, on this assurance and in the feeling it would have awakened in you towards him for my sake.”\n\nHe fervently replied that indeed indeed they would have been. I gave him my hand again.\n\n“Good night,” I said, “Good-bye.”\n\n“The first until we meet to-morrow, the second as a farewell to this theme between us for ever.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Good night; good-bye.”\n\nHe left me, and I stood at the dark window watching the street. His love, in all its constancy and generosity, had come so suddenly upon me that he had not left me a minute when my fortitude gave way again and the street was blotted out by my rushing tears.\n\nBut they were not tears of regret and sorrow. No. He had called me the beloved of his life and had said I would be evermore as dear to him as I was then, and I felt as if my heart would not hold the triumph of having heard those words. My first wild thought had died away. It was not too late to hear them, for it was not too late to be animated by them to be good, true, grateful, and contented. How easy my path, how much easier than his!\nAnother Discovery\n\nI had not the courage to see any one that night. I had not even the courage to see myself, for I was afraid that my tears might a little reproach me. I went up to my room in the dark, and prayed in the dark, and lay down in the dark to sleep. I had no need of any light to read my guardian’s letter by, for I knew it by heart. I took it from the place where I kept it, and repeated its contents by its own clear light of integrity and love, and went to sleep with it on my pillow.\n\nI was up very early in the morning and called Charley to come for a walk. We bought flowers for the breakfast-table, and came back and arranged them, and were as busy as possible. We were so early that I had a good time still for Charley’s lesson before breakfast; Charley (who was not in the least improved in the old defective article of grammar) came through it with great applause; and we were altogether very notable. When my guardian appeared he said, “Why, little woman, you look fresher than your flowers!” And Mrs. Woodcourt repeated and translated a passage from the Mewlinnwillinwodd expressive of my being like a mountain with the sun upon it.\n\nThis was all so pleasant that I hope it made me still more like the mountain than I had been before. After breakfast I waited my opportunity and peeped about a little until I saw my guardian in his own room—the room of last night—by himself. Then I made an excuse to go in with my housekeeping keys, shutting the door after me.\n\n“Well, Dame Durden?” said my guardian; the post had brought him several letters, and he was writing. “You want money?”\n\n“No, indeed, I have plenty in hand.”\n\n“There never was such a Dame Durden,” said my guardian, “for making money last.”\n\nHe had laid down his pen and leaned back in his chair looking at me. I have often spoken of his bright face, but I thought I had never seen it look so bright and good. There was a high happiness upon it which made me think, “He has been doing some great kindness this morning.”\n\n“There never was,” said my guardian, musing as he smiled upon me, “such a Dame Durden for making money last.”\n\nHe had never yet altered his old manner. I loved it and him so much that when I now went up to him and took my usual chair, which was always put at his side—for sometimes I read to him, and sometimes I talked to him, and sometimes I silently worked by him—I hardly liked to disturb it by laying my hand on his breast. But I found I did not disturb it at all.\n\n“Dear guardian,” said I, “I want to speak to you. Have I been remiss in anything?”\n\n“Remiss in anything, my dear!”\n\n“Have I not been what I have meant to be since—I brought the answer to your letter, guardian?”\n\n“You have been everything I could desire, my love.”\n\n“I am very glad indeed to hear that,” I returned. “You know, you said to me, was this the mistress of Bleak House. And I said, yes.”\n\n“Yes,” said my guardian, nodding his head. He had put his arm about me as if there were something to protect me from and looked in my face, smiling.\n\n“Since then,” said I, “we have never spoken on the subject except once.”\n\n“And then I said Bleak House was thinning fast; and so it was, my dear.”\n\n“And I said,” I timidly reminded him, “but its mistress remained.”\n\nHe still held me in the same protecting manner and with the same bright goodness in his face.\n\n“Dear guardian,” said I, “I know how you have felt all that has happened, and how considerate you have been. As so much time has passed, and as you spoke only this morning of my being so well again, perhaps you expect me to renew the subject. Perhaps I ought to do so. I will be the mistress of Bleak House when you please.”\n\n“See,” he returned gaily, “what a sympathy there must be between us! I have had nothing else, poor Rick excepted—it’s a large exception—in my mind. When you came in, I was full of it. When shall we give Bleak House its mistress, little woman?”\n\n“When you please.”\n\n“Next month?”\n\n“Next month, dear guardian.”\n\n“The day on which I take the happiest and best step of my life—the day on which I shall be a man more exulting and more enviable than any other man in the world—the day on which I give Bleak House its little mistress—shall be next month then,” said my guardian.\n\nI put my arms round his neck and kissed him just as I had done on the day when I brought my answer.\n\nA servant came to the door to announce Mr. Bucket, which was quite unnecessary, for Mr. Bucket was already looking in over the servant’s shoulder. “Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson,” said he, rather out of breath, “with all apologies for intruding, WILL you allow me to order up a person that’s on the stairs and that objects to being left there in case of becoming the subject of observations in his absence? Thank you. Be so good as chair that there member in this direction, will you?” said Mr. Bucket, beckoning over the banisters.\n\nThis singular request produced an old man in a black skull-cap, unable to walk, who was carried up by a couple of bearers and deposited in the room near the door. Mr. Bucket immediately got rid of the bearers, mysteriously shut the door, and bolted it.\n\n“Now you see, Mr. Jarndyce,” he then began, putting down his hat and opening his subject with a flourish of his well-remembered finger, “you know me, and Miss Summerson knows me. This gentleman likewise knows me, and his name is Smallweed. The discounting line is his line principally, and he’s what you may call a dealer in bills. That’s about what YOU are, you know, ain’t you?” said Mr. Bucket, stopping a little to address the gentleman in question, who was exceedingly suspicious of him.\n\nHe seemed about to dispute this designation of himself when he was seized with a violent fit of coughing.\n\n“Now, moral, you know!” said Mr. Bucket, improving the accident. “Don’t you contradict when there ain’t no occasion, and you won’t be took in that way. Now, Mr. Jarndyce, I address myself to you. I’ve been negotiating with this gentleman on behalf of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and one way and another I’ve been in and out and about his premises a deal. His premises are the premises formerly occupied by Krook, marine store dealer—a relation of this gentleman’s that you saw in his lifetime if I don’t mistake?”\n\nMy guardian replied, “Yes.”\n\n“Well! You are to understand,” said Mr. Bucket, “that this gentleman he come into Krook’s property, and a good deal of magpie property there was. Vast lots of waste-paper among the rest. Lord bless you, of no use to nobody!”\n\nThe cunning of Mr. Bucket’s eye and the masterly manner in which he contrived, without a look or a word against which his watchful auditor could protest, to let us know that he stated the case according to previous agreement and could say much more of Mr. Smallweed if he thought it advisable, deprived us of any merit in quite understanding him. His difficulty was increased by Mr. Smallweed’s being deaf as well as suspicious and watching his face with the closest attention.\n\n“Among them odd heaps of old papers, this gentleman, when he comes into the property, naturally begins to rummage, don’t you see?” said Mr. Bucket.\n\n“To which? Say that again,” cried Mr. Smallweed in a shrill, sharp voice.\n\n“To rummage,” repeated Mr. Bucket. “Being a prudent man and accustomed to take care of your own affairs, you begin to rummage among the papers as you have come into; don’t you?”\n\n“Of course I do,” cried Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“Of course you do,” said Mr. Bucket conversationally, “and much to blame you would be if you didn’t. And so you chance to find, you know,” Mr. Bucket went on, stooping over him with an air of cheerful raillery which Mr. Smallweed by no means reciprocated, “and so you chance to find, you know, a paper with the signature of Jarndyce to it. Don’t you?”\n\nMr. Smallweed glanced with a troubled eye at us and grudgingly nodded assent.\n\n“And coming to look at that paper at your full leisure and convenience—all in good time, for you’re not curious to read it, and why should you be?—what do you find it to be but a will, you see. That’s the drollery of it,” said Mr. Bucket with the same lively air of recalling a joke for the enjoyment of Mr. Smallweed, who still had the same crest-fallen appearance of not enjoying it at all; “what do you find it to be but a will?”\n\n“I don’t know that it’s good as a will or as anything else,” snarled Mr. Smallweed.\n\nMr. Bucket eyed the old man for a moment—he had slipped and shrunk down in his chair into a mere bundle—as if he were much disposed to pounce upon him; nevertheless, he continued to bend over him with the same agreeable air, keeping the corner of one of his eyes upon us.\n\n“Notwithstanding which,” said Mr. Bucket, “you get a little doubtful and uncomfortable in your mind about it, having a very tender mind of your own.”\n\n“Eh? What do you say I have got of my own?” asked Mr. Smallweed with his hand to his ear.\n\n“A very tender mind.”\n\n“Ho! Well, go on,” said Mr. Smallweed.\n\n“And as you’ve heard a good deal mentioned regarding a celebrated Chancery will case of the same name, and as you know what a card Krook was for buying all manner of old pieces of furniter, and books, and papers, and what not, and never liking to part with ’em, and always a-going to teach himself to read, you begin to think—and you never was more correct in your born days—‘Ecod, if I don’t look about me, I may get into trouble regarding this will.’”\n\n“Now, mind how you put it, Bucket,” cried the old man anxiously with his hand at his ear. “Speak up; none of your brimstone tricks. Pick me up; I want to hear better. Oh, Lord, I am shaken to bits!”\n\nMr. Bucket had certainly picked him up at a dart. However, as soon as he could be heard through Mr. Smallweed’s coughing and his vicious ejaculations of “Oh, my bones! Oh, dear! I’ve no breath in my body! I’m worse than the chattering, clattering, brimstone pig at home!” Mr. Bucket proceeded in the same convivial manner as before.\n\n“So, as I happen to be in the habit of coming about your premises, you take me into your confidence, don’t you?”\n\nI think it would be impossible to make an admission with more ill will and a worse grace than Mr. Smallweed displayed when he admitted this, rendering it perfectly evident that Mr. Bucket was the very last person he would have thought of taking into his confidence if he could by any possibility have kept him out of it.\n\n“And I go into the business with you—very pleasant we are over it; and I confirm you in your well-founded fears that you will get yourself into a most precious line if you don’t come out with that there will,” said Mr. Bucket emphatically; “and accordingly you arrange with me that it shall be delivered up to this present Mr. Jarndyce, on no conditions. If it should prove to be valuable, you trusting yourself to him for your reward; that’s about where it is, ain’t it?”\n\n“That’s what was agreed,” Mr. Smallweed assented with the same bad grace.\n\n“In consequence of which,” said Mr. Bucket, dismissing his agreeable manner all at once and becoming strictly business-like, “you’ve got that will upon your person at the present time, and the only thing that remains for you to do is just to out with it!”\n\nHaving given us one glance out of the watching corner of his eye, and having given his nose one triumphant rub with his forefinger, Mr. Bucket stood with his eyes fastened on his confidential friend and his hand stretched forth ready to take the paper and present it to my guardian. It was not produced without much reluctance and many declarations on the part of Mr. Smallweed that he was a poor industrious man and that he left it to Mr. Jarndyce’s honour not to let him lose by his honesty. Little by little he very slowly took from a breast-pocket a stained, discoloured paper which was much singed upon the outside and a little burnt at the edges, as if it had long ago been thrown upon a fire and hastily snatched off again. Mr. Bucket lost no time in transferring this paper, with the dexterity of a conjuror, from Mr. Smallweed to Mr. Jarndyce. As he gave it to my guardian, he whispered behind his fingers, “Hadn’t settled how to make their market of it. Quarrelled and hinted about it. I laid out twenty pound upon it. First the avaricious grandchildren split upon him on account of their objections to his living so unreasonably long, and then they split on one another. Lord! There ain’t one of the family that wouldn’t sell the other for a pound or two, except the old lady—and she’s only out of it because she’s too weak in her mind to drive a bargain.”\n\n“Mr Bucket,” said my guardian aloud, “whatever the worth of this paper may be to any one, my obligations are great to you; and if it be of any worth, I hold myself bound to see Mr. Smallweed remunerated accordingly.”\n\n“Not according to your merits, you know,” said Mr. Bucket in friendly explanation to Mr. Smallweed. “Don’t you be afraid of that. According to its value.”\n\n“That is what I mean,” said my guardian. “You may observe, Mr. Bucket, that I abstain from examining this paper myself. The plain truth is, I have forsworn and abjured the whole business these many years, and my soul is sick of it. But Miss Summerson and I will immediately place the paper in the hands of my solicitor in the cause, and its existence shall be made known without delay to all other parties interested.”\n\n“Mr. Jarndyce can’t say fairer than that, you understand,” observed Mr. Bucket to his fellow-visitor. “And it being now made clear to you that nobody’s a-going to be wronged—which must be a great relief to YOUR mind—we may proceed with the ceremony of chairing you home again.”\n\nHe unbolted the door, called in the bearers, wished us good morning, and with a look full of meaning and a crook of his finger at parting went his way.\n\nWe went our way too, which was to Lincoln’s Inn, as quickly as possible. Mr. Kenge was disengaged, and we found him at his table in his dusty room with the inexpressive-looking books and the piles of papers. Chairs having been placed for us by Mr. Guppy, Mr. Kenge expressed the surprise and gratification he felt at the unusual sight of Mr. Jarndyce in his office. He turned over his double eye-glass as he spoke and was more Conversation Kenge than ever.\n\n“I hope,” said Mr. Kenge, “that the genial influence of Miss Summerson,” he bowed to me, “may have induced Mr. Jarndyce,” he bowed to him, “to forego some little of his animosity towards a cause and towards a court which are—shall I say, which take their place in the stately vista of the pillars of our profession?”\n\n“I am inclined to think,” returned my guardian, “that Miss Summerson has seen too much of the effects of the court and the cause to exert any influence in their favour. Nevertheless, they are a part of the occasion of my being here. Mr. Kenge, before I lay this paper on your desk and have done with it, let me tell you how it has come into my hands.”\n\nHe did so shortly and distinctly.\n\n“It could not, sir,” said Mr. Kenge, “have been stated more plainly and to the purpose if it had been a case at law.”\n\n“Did you ever know English law, or equity either, plain and to the purpose?” said my guardian.\n\n“Oh, fie!” said Mr. Kenge.\n\nAt first he had not seemed to attach much importance to the paper, but when he saw it he appeared more interested, and when he had opened and read a little of it through his eye-glass, he became amazed. “Mr. Jarndyce,” he said, looking off it, “you have perused this?”\n\n“Not I!” returned my guardian.\n\n“But, my dear sir,” said Mr. Kenge, “it is a will of later date than any in the suit. It appears to be all in the testator’s handwriting. It is duly executed and attested. And even if intended to be cancelled, as might possibly be supposed to be denoted by these marks of fire, it is NOT cancelled. Here it is, a perfect instrument!”\n\n“Well!” said my guardian. “What is that to me?”\n\n“Mr. Guppy!” cried Mr. Kenge, raising his voice. “I beg your pardon, Mr. Jarndyce.”\n\n“Sir.”\n“Mr. Vholes of Symond’s Inn. My compliments. Jarndyce and Jarndyce. Glad to speak with him.”\n\nMr. Guppy disappeared.\n\n“You ask me what is this to you, Mr. Jarndyce. If you had perused this document, you would have seen that it reduces your interest considerably, though still leaving it a very handsome one, still leaving it a very handsome one,” said Mr. Kenge, waving his hand persuasively and blandly. “You would further have seen that the interests of Mr. Richard Carstone and of Miss Ada Clare, now Mrs. Richard Carstone, are very materially advanced by it.”\n\n“Kenge,” said my guardian, “if all the flourishing wealth that the suit brought into this vile court of Chancery could fall to my two young cousins, I should be well contented. But do you ask ME to believe that any good is to come of Jarndyce and Jarndyce?”\n\n“Oh, really, Mr. Jarndyce! Prejudice, prejudice. My dear sir, this is a very great country, a very great country. Its system of equity is a very great system, a very great system. Really, really!”\n\nMy guardian said no more, and Mr. Vholes arrived. He was modestly impressed by Mr. Kenge’s professional eminence.\n\n“How do you do, Mr. Vholes? Will you be so good as to take a chair here by me and look over this paper?”\n\nMr. Vholes did as he was asked and seemed to read it every word. He was not excited by it, but he was not excited by anything. When he had well examined it, he retired with Mr. Kenge into a window, and shading his mouth with his black glove, spoke to him at some length. I was not surprised to observe Mr. Kenge inclined to dispute what he said before he had said much, for I knew that no two people ever did agree about anything in Jarndyce and Jarndyce. But he seemed to get the better of Mr. Kenge too in a conversation that sounded as if it were almost composed of the words “Receiver-General,” “Accountant-General,” “report,” “estate,” and “costs.” When they had finished, they came back to Mr. Kenge’s table and spoke aloud.\n\n“Well! But this is a very remarkable document, Mr. Vholes,” said Mr. Kenge.\n\nMr. Vholes said, “Very much so.”\n\n“And a very important document, Mr. Vholes,” said Mr. Kenge.\n\nAgain Mr. Vholes said, “Very much so.”\n\n“And as you say, Mr. Vholes, when the cause is in the paper next term, this document will be an unexpected and interesting feature in it,” said Mr. Kenge, looking loftily at my guardian.\n\nMr. Vholes was gratified, as a smaller practitioner striving to keep respectable, to be confirmed in any opinion of his own by such an authority.\n\n“And when,” asked my guardian, rising after a pause, during which Mr. Kenge had rattled his money and Mr. Vholes had picked his pimples, “when is next term?”\n\n“Next term, Mr. Jarndyce, will be next month,” said Mr. Kenge. “Of course we shall at once proceed to do what is necessary with this document and to collect the necessary evidence concerning it; and of course you will receive our usual notification of the cause being in the paper.”\n\n“To which I shall pay, of course, my usual attention.”\n\n“Still bent, my dear sir,” said Mr. Kenge, showing us through the outer office to the door, “still bent, even with your enlarged mind, on echoing a popular prejudice? We are a prosperous community, Mr. Jarndyce, a very prosperous community. We are a great country, Mr. Jarndyce, we are a very great country. This is a great system, Mr. Jarndyce, and would you wish a great country to have a little system? Now, really, really!”\n\nHe said this at the stair-head, gently moving his right hand as if it were a silver trowel with which to spread the cement of his words on the structure of the system and consolidate it for a thousand ages.\n\nSteel and Iron\nGeorge’s Shooting Gallery is to let, and the stock is sold off, and George himself is at Chesney Wold attending on Sir Leicester in his rides and riding very near his bridle-rein because of the uncertain hand with which he guides his horse. But not to-day is George so occupied. He is journeying to-day into the iron country farther north to look about him.\n\nAs he comes into the iron country farther north, such fresh green woods as those of Chesney Wold are left behind; and coal pits and ashes, high chimneys and red bricks, blighted verdure, scorching fires, and a heavy never-lightening cloud of smoke become the features of the scenery. Among such objects rides the trooper, looking about him and always looking for something he has come to find.\n\nAt last, on the black canal bridge of a busy town, with a clang of iron in it, and more fires and more smoke than he has seen yet, the trooper, swart with the dust of the coal roads, checks his horse and asks a workman does he know the name of Rouncewell thereabouts.\n\n“Why, master,” quoth the workman, “do I know my own name?”\n\n“’Tis so well known here, is it, comrade?” asks the trooper.\n\n“Rouncewell’s? Ah! You’re right.”\n\n“And where might it be now?” asks the trooper with a glance before him.\n\n“The bank, the factory, or the house?” the workman wants to know.\n\n“Hum! Rouncewell’s is so great apparently,” mutters the trooper, stroking his chin, “that I have as good as half a mind to go back again. Why, I don’t know which I want. Should I find Mr. Rouncewell at the factory, do you think?”\n\n“Tain’t easy to say where you’d find him—at this time of the day you might find either him or his son there, if he’s in town; but his contracts take him away.”\n\nAnd which is the factory? Why, he sees those chimneys—the tallest ones! Yes, he sees THEM. Well! Let him keep his eye on those chimneys, going on as straight as ever he can, and presently he’ll see ’em down a turning on the left, shut in by a great brick wall which forms one side of the street. That’s Rouncewell’s.\n\nThe trooper thanks his informant and rides slowly on, looking about him. He does not turn back, but puts up his horse (and is much disposed to groom him too) at a public-house where some of Rouncewell’s hands are dining, as the ostler tells him. Some of Rouncewell’s hands have just knocked off for dinner-time and seem to be invading the whole town. They are very sinewy and strong, are Rouncewell’s hands—a little sooty too.\n\nHe comes to a gateway in the brick wall, looks in, and sees a great perplexity of iron lying about in every stage and in a vast variety of shapes—in bars, in wedges, in sheets; in tanks, in boilers, in axles, in wheels, in cogs, in cranks, in rails; twisted and wrenched into eccentric and perverse forms as separate parts of machinery; mountains of it broken up, and rusty in its age; distant furnaces of it glowing and bubbling in its youth; bright fireworks of it showering about under the blows of the steam-hammer; red-hot iron, white-hot iron, cold-black iron; an iron taste, an iron smell, and a Babel of iron sounds.\n\n“This is a place to make a man’s head ache too!” says the trooper, looking about him for a counting-house. “Who comes here? This is very like me before I was set up. This ought to be my nephew, if likenesses run in families. Your servant, sir.”\n\n“Yours, sir. Are you looking for any one?”\n\n“Excuse me. Young Mr. Rouncewell, I believe?”\n\n“Yes.”\n“I was looking for your father, sir. I wish to have a word with him.”\n\nThe young man, telling him he is fortunate in his choice of a time, for his father is there, leads the way to the office where he is to be found. “Very like me before I was set up—devilish like me!” thinks the trooper as he follows. They come to a building in the yard with an office on an upper floor. At sight of the gentleman in the office, Mr. George turns very red.\n\n“What name shall I say to my father?” asks the young man.\n\nGeorge, full of the idea of iron, in desperation answers “Steel,” and is so presented. He is left alone with the gentleman in the office, who sits at a table with account-books before him and some sheets of paper blotted with hosts of figures and drawings of cunning shapes. It is a bare office, with bare windows, looking on the iron view below. Tumbled together on the table are some pieces of iron, purposely broken to be tested at various periods of their service, in various capacities. There is iron-dust on everything; and the smoke is seen through the windows rolling heavily out of the tall chimneys to mingle with the smoke from a vaporous Babylon of other chimneys.\n\n“I am at your service, Mr. Steel,” says the gentleman when his visitor has taken a rusty chair.\n\n“Well, Mr. Rouncewell,” George replies, leaning forward with his left arm on his knee and his hat in his hand, and very chary of meeting his brother’s eye, “I am not without my expectations that in the present visit I may prove to be more free than welcome. I have served as a dragoon in my day, and a comrade of mine that I was once rather partial to was, if I don’t deceive myself, a brother of yours. I believe you had a brother who gave his family some trouble, and ran away, and never did any good but in keeping away?”\n\n“Are you quite sure,” returns the ironmaster in an altered voice, “that your name is Steel?”\n\nThe trooper falters and looks at him. His brother starts up, calls him by his name, and grasps him by both hands.\n\n“You are too quick for me!” cries the trooper with the tears springing out of his eyes. “How do you do, my dear old fellow? I never could have thought you would have been half so glad to see me as all this. How do you do, my dear old fellow, how do you do!”\n\nThey shake hands and embrace each other over and over again, the trooper still coupling his “How do you do, my dear old fellow!” with his protestation that he never thought his brother would have been half so glad to see him as all this!\n\n“So far from it,” he declares at the end of a full account of what has preceded his arrival there, “I had very little idea of making myself known. I thought if you took by any means forgivingly to my name I might gradually get myself up to the point of writing a letter. But I should not have been surprised, brother, if you had considered it anything but welcome news to hear of me.”\n\n“We will show you at home what kind of news we think it, George,” returns his brother. “This is a great day at home, and you could not have arrived, you bronzed old soldier, on a better. I make an agreement with my son Watt to-day that on this day twelvemonth he shall marry as pretty and as good a girl as you have seen in all your travels. She goes to Germany to-morrow with one of your nieces for a little polishing up in her education. We make a feast of the event, and you will be made the hero of it.”\n\nMr. George is so entirely overcome at first by this prospect that he resists the proposed honour with great earnestness. Being overborne, however, by his brother and his nephew—concerning whom he renews his protestations that he never could have thought they would have been half so glad to see him—he is taken home to an elegant house in all the arrangements of which there is to be observed a pleasant mixture of the originally simple habits of the father and mother with such as are suited to their altered station and the higher fortunes of their children. Here Mr. George is much dismayed by the graces and accomplishments of his nieces that are and by the beauty of Rosa, his niece that is to be, and by the affectionate salutations of these young ladies, which he receives in a sort of dream. He is sorely taken aback, too, by the dutiful behaviour of his nephew and has a woeful consciousness upon him of being a scapegrace. However, there is great rejoicing and a very hearty company and infinite enjoyment, and Mr. George comes bluff and martial through it all, and his pledge to be present at the marriage and give away the bride is received with universal favour. A whirling head has Mr. George that night when he lies down in the state-bed of his brother’s house to think of all these things and to see the images of his nieces (awful all the evening in their floating muslins) waltzing, after the German manner, over his counterpane.\n\nThe brothers are closeted next morning in the ironmaster’s room, where the elder is proceeding, in his clear sensible way, to show how he thinks he may best dispose of George in his business, when George squeezes his hand and stops him.\n\n“Brother, I thank you a million times for your more than brotherly welcome, and a million times more to that for your more than brotherly intentions. But my plans are made. Before I say a word as to them, I wish to consult you upon one family point. How,” says the trooper, folding his arms and looking with indomitable firmness at his brother, “how is my mother to be got to scratch me?”\n\n“I am not sure that I understand you, George,” replies the ironmaster.\n\n“I say, brother, how is my mother to be got to scratch me? She must be got to do it somehow.”\n\n“Scratch you out of her will, I think you mean?”\n\n“Of course I do. In short,” says the trooper, folding his arms more resolutely yet, “I mean—TO—scratch me!”\n\n“My dear George,” returns his brother, “is it so indispensable that you should undergo that process?”\n\n“Quite! Absolutely! I couldn’t be guilty of the meanness of coming back without it. I should never be safe not to be off again. I have not sneaked home to rob your children, if not yourself, brother, of your rights. I, who forfeited mine long ago! If I am to remain and hold up my head, I must be scratched. Come. You are a man of celebrated penetration and intelligence, and you can tell me how it’s to be brought about.”\n\n“I can tell you, George,” replies the ironmaster deliberately, “how it is not to be brought about, which I hope may answer the purpose as well. Look at our mother, think of her, recall her emotion when she recovered you. Do you believe there is a consideration in the world that would induce her to take such a step against her favourite son? Do you believe there is any chance of her consent, to balance against the outrage it would be to her (loving dear old lady!) to propose it? If you do, you are wrong. No, George! You must make up your mind to remain UNscratched, I think.” There is an amused smile on the ironmaster’s face as he watches his brother, who is pondering, deeply disappointed. “I think you may manage almost as well as if the thing were done, though.”\n\n“How, brother?”\n\n“Being bent upon it, you can dispose by will of anything you have the misfortune to inherit in any way you like, you know.”\n\n“That’s true!” says the trooper, pondering again. Then he wistfully asks, with his hand on his brother’s, “Would you mind mentioning that, brother, to your wife and family?”\n“Not at all.”\n\n“Thank you. You wouldn’t object to say, perhaps, that although an undoubted vagabond, I am a vagabond of the harum-scarum order, and not of the mean sort?”\n\nThe ironmaster, repressing his amused smile, assents.\n\n“Thank you. Thank you. It’s a weight off my mind,” says the trooper with a heave of his chest as he unfolds his arms and puts a hand on each leg, “though I had set my heart on being scratched, too!”\n\nThe brothers are very like each other, sitting face to face; but a certain massive simplicity and absence of usage in the ways of the world is all on the trooper’s side.\n\n“Well,” he proceeds, throwing off his disappointment, “next and last, those plans of mine. You have been so brotherly as to propose to me to fall in here and take my place among the products of your perseverance and sense. I thank you heartily. It’s more than brotherly, as I said before, and I thank you heartily for it,” shaking him a long time by the hand. “But the truth is, brother, I am a—I am a kind of a weed, and it’s too late to plant me in a regular garden.”\n\n“My dear George,” returns the elder, concentrating his strong steady brow upon him and smiling confidently, “leave that to me, and let me try.”\n\nGeorge shakes his head. “You could do it, I have not a doubt, if anybody could; but it’s not to be done. Not to be done, sir! Whereas it so falls out, on the other hand, that I am able to be of some trifle of use to Sir Leicester Dedlock since his illness—brought on by family sorrows—and that he would rather have that help from our mother’s son than from anybody else.”\n\n“Well, my dear George,” returns the other with a very slight shade upon his open face, “if you prefer to serve in Sir Leicester Dedlock’s household brigade—”\n\n“There it is, brother,” cries the trooper, checking him, with his hand upon his knee again; “there it is! You don’t take kindly to that idea; I don’t mind it. You are not used to being officered; I am. Everything about you is in perfect order and discipline; everything about me requires to be kept so. We are not accustomed to carry things with the same hand or to look at ’em from the same point. I don’t say much about my garrison manners because I found myself pretty well at my ease last night, and they wouldn’t be noticed here, I dare say, once and away. But I shall get on best at Chesney Wold, where there’s more room for a weed than there is here; and the dear old lady will be made happy besides. Therefore I accept of Sir Leicester Dedlock’s proposals. When I come over next year to give away the bride, or whenever I come, I shall have the sense to keep the household brigade in ambuscade and not to manoeuvre it on your ground. I thank you heartily again and am proud to think of the Rouncewells as they’ll be founded by you.”\n\n“You know yourself, George,” says the elder brother, returning the grip of his hand, “and perhaps you know me better than I know myself. Take your way. So that we don’t quite lose one another again, take your way.”\n\n“No fear of that!” returns the trooper. “Now, before I turn my horse’s head homewards, brother, I will ask you—if you’ll be so good—to look over a letter for me. I brought it with me to send from these parts, as Chesney Wold might be a painful name just now to the person it’s written to. I am not much accustomed to correspondence myself, and I am particular respecting this present letter because I want it to be both straightforward and delicate.”\n\nHerewith he hands a letter, closely written in somewhat pale ink but in a neat round hand, to the ironmaster, who reads as follows:\n\nMiss Esther Summerson,\n\nA communication having been made to me by Inspector Bucket of a letter to myself being found among the papers of a certain person, I take the liberty to make known to you that it was but a few lines of instruction from abroad, when, where, and how to deliver an enclosed letter to a young and beautiful lady, then unmarried, in England. I duly observed the same.\n\nI further take the liberty to make known to you that it was got from me as a proof of handwriting only and that otherwise I would not have given it up, as appearing to be the most harmless in my possession, without being previously shot through the heart.\n\nI further take the liberty to mention that if I could have supposed a certain unfortunate gentleman to have been in existence, I never could and never would have rested until I had discovered his retreat and shared my last farthing with him, as my duty and my inclination would have equally been. But he was (officially) reported drowned, and assuredly went over the side of a transport-ship at night in an Irish harbour within a few hours of her arrival from the West Indies, as I have myself heard both from officers and men on board, and know to have been (officially) confirmed.\n\nI further take the liberty to state that in my humble quality as one of the rank and file, I am, and shall ever continue to be, your thoroughly devoted and admiring servant and that I esteem the qualities you possess above all others far beyond the limits of the present dispatch.\n\nI have the honour to be,\n\n“A little formal,” observes the elder brother, refolding it with a puzzled face.\n\n“But nothing that might not be sent to a pattern young lady?” asks the younger.\n\n“Nothing at all.”\n\nTherefore it is sealed and deposited for posting among the iron correspondence of the day. This done, Mr. George takes a hearty farewell of the family party and prepares to saddle and mount. His brother, however, unwilling to part with him so soon, proposes to ride with him in a light open carriage to the place where he will bait for the night, and there remain with him until morning, a servant riding for so much of the journey on the thoroughbred old grey from Chesney Wold. The offer, being gladly accepted, is followed by a pleasant ride, a pleasant dinner, and a pleasant breakfast, all in brotherly communion. Then they once more shake hands long and heartily and part, the ironmaster turning his face to the smoke and fires, and the trooper to the green country. Early in the afternoon the subdued sound of his heavy military trot is heard on the turf in the avenue as he rides on with imaginary clank and jingle of accoutrements under the old elm-trees.\nEsther’s Narrative\n\nSoon after I had that conversation with my guardian, he put a sealed paper in my hand one morning and said, “This is for next month, my dear.” I found in it two hundred pounds.\n\nI now began very quietly to make such preparations as I thought were necessary. Regulating my purchases by my guardian’s taste, which I knew very well of course, I arranged my wardrobe to please him and hoped I should be highly successful. I did it all so quietly because I was not quite free from my old apprehension that Ada would be rather sorry and because my guardian was so quiet himself. I had no doubt that under all the circumstances we should be married in the most private and simple manner. Perhaps I should only have to say to Ada, “Would you like to come and see me married to-morrow, my pet?” Perhaps our wedding might even be as unpretending as her own, and I might not find it necessary to say anything about it until it was over. I thought that if I were to choose, I would like this best.\n\nThe only exception I made was Mrs. Woodcourt. I told her that I was going to be married to my guardian and that we had been engaged some time. She highly approved. She could never do enough for me and was remarkably softened now in comparison with what she had been when we first knew her. There was no trouble she would not have taken to have been of use to me, but I need hardly say that I only allowed her to take as little as gratified her kindness without tasking it.\n\nOf course this was not a time to neglect my guardian, and of course it was not a time for neglecting my darling. So I had plenty of occupation, which I was glad of; and as to Charley, she was absolutely not to be seen for needlework. To surround herself with great heaps of it—baskets full and tables full—and do a little, and spend a great deal of time in staring with her round eyes at what there was to do, and persuade herself that she was going to do it, were Charley’s great dignities and delights.\n\nMeanwhile, I must say, I could not agree with my guardian on the subject of the will, and I had some sanguine hopes of Jarndyce and Jarndyce. Which of us was right will soon appear, but I certainly did encourage expectations. In Richard, the discovery gave occasion for a burst of business and agitation that buoyed him up for a little time, but he had lost the elasticity even of hope now and seemed to me to retain only its feverish anxieties. From something my guardian said one day when we were talking about this, I understood that my marriage would not take place until after the term-time we had been told to look forward to; and I thought the more, for that, how rejoiced I should be if I could be married when Richard and Ada were a little more prosperous.\n\nThe term was very near indeed when my guardian was called out of town and went down into Yorkshire on Mr. Woodcourt’s business. He had told me beforehand that his presence there would be necessary. I had just come in one night from my dear girl’s and was sitting in the midst of all my new clothes, looking at them all around me and thinking, when a letter from my guardian was brought to me. It asked me to join him in the country and mentioned by what stage-coach my place was taken and at what time in the morning I should have to leave town. It added in a postscript that I would not be many hours from Ada.\n\nI expected few things less than a journey at that time, but I was ready for it in half an hour and set off as appointed early next morning. I travelled all day, wondering all day what I could be wanted for at such a distance; now I thought it might be for this purpose, and now I thought it might be for that purpose, but I was never, never, never near the truth.\n\nIt was night when I came to my journey’s end and found my guardian waiting for me. This was a great relief, for towards evening I had begun to fear (the more so as his letter was a very short one) that he might be ill. However, there he was, as well as it was possible to be; and when I saw his genial face again at its brightest and best, I said to myself, he has been doing some other great kindness. Not that it required much penetration to say that, because I knew that his being there at all was an act of kindness.\n\nSupper was ready at the hotel, and when we were alone at table he said, “Full of curiosity, no doubt, little woman, to know why I have brought you here?”\n\n“Well, guardian,” said I, “without thinking myself a Fatima or you a Blue Beard, I am a little curious about it.”\n\n“Then to ensure your night’s rest, my love,” he returned gaily, “I won’t wait until to-morrow to tell you. I have very much wished to express to Woodcourt, somehow, my sense of his humanity to poor unfortunate Jo, his inestimable services to my young cousins, and his value to us all. When it was decided that he should settle here, it came into my head that I might ask his acceptance of some unpretending and suitable little place to lay his own head in. I therefore caused such a place to be looked out for, and such a place was found on very easy terms, and I have been touching it up for him and making it habitable. However, when I walked over it the day before yesterday and it was reported ready, I found that I was not housekeeper enough to know whether things were all as they ought to be. So I sent off for the best little housekeeper that could possibly be got to come and give me her advice and opinion. And here she is,” said my guardian, “laughing and crying both together!”\n\nBecause he was so dear, so good, so admirable. I tried to tell him what I thought of him, but I could not articulate a word.\n\n“Tut, tut!” said my guardian. “You make too much of it, little woman. Why, how you sob, Dame Durden, how you sob!”\n\n“It is with exquisite pleasure, guardian—with a heart full of thanks.”\n\n“Well, well,” said he. “I am delighted that you approve. I thought you would. I meant it as a pleasant surprise for the little mistress of Bleak House.”\n\nI kissed him and dried my eyes. “I know now!” said I. “I have seen this in your face a long while.”\n\n“No; have you really, my dear?” said he. “What a Dame Durden it is to read a face!”\n\nHe was so quaintly cheerful that I could not long be otherwise, and was almost ashamed of having been otherwise at all. When I went to bed, I cried. I am bound to confess that I cried; but I hope it was with pleasure, though I am not quite sure it was with pleasure. I repeated every word of the letter twice over.\n\nA most beautiful summer morning succeeded, and after breakfast we went out arm in arm to see the house of which I was to give my mighty housekeeping opinion. We entered a flower-garden by a gate in a side wall, of which he had the key, and the first thing I saw was that the beds and flowers were all laid out according to the manner of my beds and flowers at home.\n\n“You see, my dear,” observed my guardian, standing still with a delighted face to watch my looks, “knowing there could be no better plan, I borrowed yours.”\n\nWe went on by a pretty little orchard, where the cherries were nestling among the green leaves and the shadows of the apple-trees were sporting on the grass, to the house itself—a cottage, quite a rustic cottage of doll’s rooms; but such a lovely place, so tranquil and so beautiful, with such a rich and smiling country spread around it; with water sparkling away into the distance, here all overhung with summer-growth, there turning a humming mill; at its nearest point glancing through a meadow by the cheerful town, where cricket-players were assembling in bright groups and a flag was flying from a white tent that rippled in the sweet west wind. And still, as we went through the pretty rooms, out at the little rustic verandah doors, and underneath the tiny wooden colonnades garlanded with woodbine, jasmine, and honey-suckle, I saw in the papering on the walls, in the colours of the furniture, in the arrangement of all the pretty objects, MY little tastes and fancies, MY little methods and inventions which they used to laugh at while they praised them, my odd ways everywhere.\n\nI could not say enough in admiration of what was all so beautiful, but one secret doubt arose in my mind when I saw this, I thought, oh, would he be the happier for it! Would it not have been better for his peace that I should not have been so brought before him? Because although I was not what he thought me, still he loved me very dearly, and it might remind him mournfully of what be believed he had lost. I did not wish him to forget me—perhaps he might not have done so, without these aids to his memory—but my way was easier than his, and I could have reconciled myself even to that so that he had been the happier for it.\n\n“And now, little woman,” said my guardian, whom I had never seen so proud and joyful as in showing me these things and watching my appreciation of them, “now, last of all, for the name of this house.”\n\n“What is it called, dear guardian?”\n\n“My child,” said he, “come and see,”\n\nHe took me to the porch, which he had hitherto avoided, and said, pausing before we went out, “My dear child, don’t you guess the name?”\n\n“No!” said I.\nWe went out of the porch and he showed me written over it, Bleak House.\n\nHe led me to a seat among the leaves close by, and sitting down beside me and taking my hand in his, spoke to me thus, “My darling girl, in what there has been between us, I have, I hope, been really solicitous for your happiness. When I wrote you the letter to which you brought the answer,” smiling as he referred to it, “I had my own too much in view; but I had yours too. Whether, under different circumstances, I might ever have renewed the old dream I sometimes dreamed when you were very young, of making you my wife one day, I need not ask myself. I did renew it, and I wrote my letter, and you brought your answer. You are following what I say, my child?”\n\nI was cold, and I trembled violently, but not a word he uttered was lost. As I sat looking fixedly at him and the sun’s rays descended, softly shining through the leaves upon his bare head, I felt as if the brightness on him must be like the brightness of the angels.\n\n“Hear me, my love, but do not speak. It is for me to speak now. When it was that I began to doubt whether what I had done would really make you happy is no matter. Woodcourt came home, and I soon had no doubt at all.”\n\nI clasped him round the neck and hung my head upon his breast and wept. “Lie lightly, confidently here, my child,” said he, pressing me gently to him. “I am your guardian and your father now. Rest confidently here.”\n\nSoothingly, like the gentle rustling of the leaves; and genially, like the ripening weather; and radiantly and beneficently, like the sunshine, he went on.\n\n“Understand me, my dear girl. I had no doubt of your being contented and happy with me, being so dutiful and so devoted; but I saw with whom you would be happier. That I penetrated his secret when Dame Durden was blind to it is no wonder, for I knew the good that could never change in her better far than she did. Well! I have long been in Allan Woodcourt’s confidence, although he was not, until yesterday, a few hours before you came here, in mine. But I would not have my Esther’s bright example lost; I would not have a jot of my dear girl’s virtues unobserved and unhonoured; I would not have her admitted on sufferance into the line of Morgan ap-Kerrig, no, not for the weight in gold of all the mountains in Wales!”\n\nHe stopped to kiss me on the forehead, and I sobbed and wept afresh. For I felt as if I could not bear the painful delight of his praise.\n\n“Hush, little woman! Don’t cry; this is to be a day of joy. I have looked forward to it,” he said exultingly, “for months on months! A few words more, Dame Trot, and I have said my say. Determined not to throw away one atom of my Esther’s worth, I took Mrs. Woodcourt into a separate confidence. ‘Now, madam,’ said I, ‘I clearly perceive—and indeed I know, to boot—that your son loves my ward. I am further very sure that my ward loves your son, but will sacrifice her love to a sense of duty and affection, and will sacrifice it so completely, so entirely, so religiously, that you should never suspect it though you watched her night and day.’ Then I told her all our story—ours—yours and mine. ‘Now, madam,’ said I, ‘come you, knowing this, and live with us. Come you, and see my child from hour to hour; set what you see against her pedigree, which is this, and this’—for I scorned to mince it—‘and tell me what is the true legitimacy when you shall have quite made up your mind on that subject.’ Why, honour to her old Welsh blood, my dear,” cried my guardian with enthusiasm, “I believe the heart it animates beats no less warmly, no less admiringly, no less lovingly, towards Dame Durden than my own!”\n\nHe tenderly raised my head, and as I clung to him, kissed me in his old fatherly way again and again. What a light, now, on the protecting manner I had thought about!\n\n“One more last word. When Allan Woodcourt spoke to you, my dear, he spoke with my knowledge and consent—but I gave him no encouragement, not I, for these surprises were my great reward, and I was too miserly to part with a scrap of it. He was to come and tell me all that passed, and he did. I have no more to say. My dearest, Allan Woodcourt stood beside your father when he lay dead—stood beside your mother. This is Bleak House. This day I give this house its little mistress; and before God, it is the brightest day in all my life!”\n\nHe rose and raised me with him. We were no longer alone. My husband—I have called him by that name full seven happy years now—stood at my side.\n\n“Allan,” said my guardian, “take from me a willing gift, the best wife that ever man had. What more can I say for you than that I know you deserve her! Take with her the little home she brings you. You know what she will make it, Allan; you know what she has made its namesake. Let me share its felicity sometimes, and what do I sacrifice? Nothing, nothing.”\n\nHe kissed me once again, and now the tears were in his eyes as he said more softly, “Esther, my dearest, after so many years, there is a kind of parting in this too. I know that my mistake has caused you some distress. Forgive your old guardian, in restoring him to his old place in your affections; and blot it out of your memory. Allan, take my dear.”\n\nHe moved away from under the green roof of leaves, and stopping in the sunlight outside and turning cheerfully towards us, said, “I shall be found about here somewhere. It’s a west wind, little woman, due west! Let no one thank me any more, for I am going to revert to my bachelor habits, and if anybody disregards this warning, I’ll run away and never come back!”\n\nWhat happiness was ours that day, what joy, what rest, what hope, what gratitude, what bliss! We were to be married before the month was out, but when we were to come and take possession of our own house was to depend on Richard and Ada.\n\nWe all three went home together next day. As soon as we arrived in town, Allan went straight to see Richard and to carry our joyful news to him and my darling. Late as it was, I meant to go to her for a few minutes before lying down to sleep, but I went home with my guardian first to make his tea for him and to occupy the old chair by his side, for I did not like to think of its being empty so soon.\n\nWhen we came home we found that a young man had called three times in the course of that one day to see me and that having been told on the occasion of his third call that I was not expected to return before ten o’clock at night, he had left word that he would call about then. He had left his card three times. Mr. Guppy.\n\nAs I naturally speculated on the object of these visits, and as I always associated something ludicrous with the visitor, it fell out that in laughing about Mr. Guppy I told my guardian of his old proposal and his subsequent retraction. “After that,” said my guardian, “we will certainly receive this hero.” So instructions were given that Mr. Guppy should be shown in when he came again, and they were scarcely given when he did come again.\n\nHe was embarrassed when he found my guardian with me, but recovered himself and said, “How de do, sir?”\n\n“How do you do, sir?” returned my guardian.\n\n“Thank you, sir, I am tolerable,” returned Mr. Guppy. “Will you allow me to introduce my mother, Mrs. Guppy of the Old Street Road, and my particular friend, Mr. Weevle. That is to say, my friend has gone by the name of Weevle, but his name is really and truly Jobling.”\n\nMy guardian begged them to be seated, and they all sat down.\n\n“Tony,” said Mr. Guppy to his friend after an awkward silence. “Will you open the case?”\n\n“Do it yourself,” returned the friend rather tartly.\n\n“Well, Mr. Jarndyce, sir,” Mr. Guppy, after a moment’s consideration, began, to the great diversion of his mother, which she displayed by nudging Mr. Jobling with her elbow and winking at me in a most remarkable manner, “I had an idea that I should see Miss Summerson by herself and was not quite prepared for your esteemed presence. But Miss Summerson has mentioned to you, perhaps, that something has passed between us on former occasions?”\n\n“Miss Summerson,” returned my guardian, smiling, “has made a communication to that effect to me.”\n\n“That,” said Mr. Guppy, “makes matters easier. Sir, I have come out of my articles at Kenge and Carboy’s, and I believe with satisfaction to all parties. I am now admitted (after undergoing an examination that’s enough to badger a man blue, touching a pack of nonsense that he don’t want to know) on the roll of attorneys and have taken out my certificate, if it would be any satisfaction to you to see it.”\n\n“Thank you, Mr. Guppy,” returned my guardian. “I am quite willing—I believe I use a legal phrase—to admit the certificate.”\n\nMr. Guppy therefore desisted from taking something out of his pocket and proceeded without it.\n\n“I have no capital myself, but my mother has a little property which takes the form of an annuity”—here Mr. Guppy’s mother rolled her head as if she never could sufficiently enjoy the observation, and put her handkerchief to her mouth, and again winked at me—“and a few pounds for expenses out of pocket in conducting business will never be wanting, free of interest, which is an advantage, you know,” said Mr. Guppy feelingly.\n\n“Certainly an advantage,” returned my guardian.\n\n“I HAVE some connexion,” pursued Mr. Guppy, “and it lays in the direction of Walcot Square, Lambeth. I have therefore taken a ’ouse in that locality, which, in the opinion of my friends, is a hollow bargain (taxes ridiculous, and use of fixtures included in the rent), and intend setting up professionally for myself there forthwith.”\n\nHere Mr. Guppy’s mother fell into an extraordinary passion of rolling her head and smiling waggishly at anybody who would look at her.\n\n“It’s a six-roomer, exclusive of kitchens,” said Mr. Guppy, “and in the opinion of my friends, a commodious tenement. When I mention my friends, I refer principally to my friend Jobling, who I believe has known me,” Mr. Guppy looked at him with a sentimental air, “from boyhood’s hour.”\n\nMr. Jobling confirmed this with a sliding movement of his legs.\n\n“My friend Jobling will render me his assistance in the capacity of clerk and will live in the ’ouse,” said Mr. Guppy. “My mother will likewise live in the ’ouse when her present quarter in the Old Street Road shall have ceased and expired; and consequently there will be no want of society. My friend Jobling is naturally aristocratic by taste, and besides being acquainted with the movements of the upper circles, fully backs me in the intentions I am now developing.”\n\nMr. Jobling said “Certainly” and withdrew a little from the elbow of Mr Guppy’s mother.\n\n“Now, I have no occasion to mention to you, sir, you being in the confidence of Miss Summerson,” said Mr. Guppy, “(mother, I wish you’d be so good as to keep still), that Miss Summerson’s image was formerly imprinted on my ’eart and that I made her a proposal of marriage.”\n\n“That I have heard,” returned my guardian.\n\n“Circumstances,” pursued Mr. Guppy, “over which I had no control, but quite the contrary, weakened the impression of that image for a time. At which time Miss Summerson’s conduct was highly genteel; I may even add, magnanimous.”\n\nMy guardian patted me on the shoulder and seemed much amused.\n\n“Now, sir,” said Mr. Guppy, “I have got into that state of mind myself that I wish for a reciprocity of magnanimous behaviour. I wish to prove to Miss Summerson that I can rise to a heighth of which perhaps she hardly thought me capable. I find that the image which I did suppose had been eradicated from my ’eart is NOT eradicated. Its influence over me is still tremenjous, and yielding to it, I am willing to overlook the circumstances over which none of us have had any control and to renew those proposals to Miss Summerson which I had the honour to make at a former period. I beg to lay the ’ouse in Walcot Square, the business, and myself before Miss Summerson for her acceptance.”\n\n“Very magnanimous indeed, sir,” observed my guardian.\n\n“Well, sir,” replied Mr. Guppy with candour, “my wish is to BE magnanimous. I do not consider that in making this offer to Miss Summerson I am by any means throwing myself away; neither is that the opinion of my friends. Still, there are circumstances which I submit may be taken into account as a set off against any little drawbacks of mine, and so a fair and equitable balance arrived at.”\n\n“I take upon myself, sir,” said my guardian, laughing as he rang the bell, “to reply to your proposals on behalf of Miss Summerson. She is very sensible of your handsome intentions, and wishes you good evening, and wishes you well.”\n\n“Oh!” said Mr. Guppy with a blank look. “Is that tantamount, sir, to acceptance, or rejection, or consideration?”\n\n“To decided rejection, if you please,” returned my guardian.\n\nMr. Guppy looked incredulously at his friend, and at his mother, who suddenly turned very angry, and at the floor, and at the ceiling.\n\n“Indeed?” said he. “Then, Jobling, if you was the friend you represent yourself, I should think you might hand my mother out of the gangway instead of allowing her to remain where she ain’t wanted.”\n\nBut Mrs. Guppy positively refused to come out of the gangway. She wouldn’t hear of it. “Why, get along with you,” said she to my guardian, “what do you mean? Ain’t my son good enough for you? You ought to be ashamed of yourself. Get out with you!”\n\n“My good lady,” returned my guardian, “it is hardly reasonable to ask me to get out of my own room.”\n\n“I don’t care for that,” said Mrs. Guppy. “Get out with you. If we ain’t good enough for you, go and procure somebody that is good enough. Go along and find ’em.”\n\nI was quite unprepared for the rapid manner in which Mrs. Guppy’s power of jocularity merged into a power of taking the profoundest offence.\n\n“Go along and find somebody that’s good enough for you,” repeated Mrs. Guppy. “Get out!” Nothing seemed to astonish Mr. Guppy’s mother so much and to make her so very indignant as our not getting out. “Why don’t you get out?” said Mrs. Guppy. “What are you stopping here for?”\n\n“Mother,” interposed her son, always getting before her and pushing her back with one shoulder as she sidled at my guardian, “WILL you hold your tongue?”\n\n“No, William,” she returned, “I won’t! Not unless he gets out, I won’t!”\n\nHowever, Mr. Guppy and Mr. Jobling together closed on Mr. Guppy’s mother (who began to be quite abusive) and took her, very much against her will, downstairs, her voice rising a stair higher every time her figure got a stair lower, and insisting that we should immediately go and find somebody who was good enough for us, and above all things that we should get out.\n\nBeginning the World\nThe term had commenced, and my guardian found an intimation from Mr. Kenge that the cause would come on in two days. As I had sufficient hopes of the will to be in a flutter about it, Allan and I agreed to go down to the court that morning. Richard was extremely agitated and was so weak and low, though his illness was still of the mind, that my dear girl indeed had sore occasion to be supported. But she looked forward—a very little way now—to the help that was to come to her, and never drooped.\n\nIt was at Westminster that the cause was to come on. It had come on there, I dare say, a hundred times before, but I could not divest myself of an idea that it MIGHT lead to some result now. We left home directly after breakfast to be at Westminster Hall in good time and walked down there through the lively streets—so happily and strangely it seemed!—together.\n\nAs we were going along, planning what we should do for Richard and Ada, I heard somebody calling “Esther! My dear Esther! Esther!” And there was Caddy Jellyby, with her head out of the window of a little carriage which she hired now to go about in to her pupils (she had so many), as if she wanted to embrace me at a hundred yards’ distance. I had written her a note to tell her of all that my guardian had done, but had not had a moment to go and see her. Of course we turned back, and the affectionate girl was in that state of rapture, and was so overjoyed to talk about the night when she brought me the flowers, and was so determined to squeeze my face (bonnet and all) between her hands, and go on in a wild manner altogether, calling me all kinds of precious names, and telling Allan I had done I don’t know what for her, that I was just obliged to get into the little carriage and calm her down by letting her say and do exactly what she liked. Allan, standing at the window, was as pleased as Caddy; and I was as pleased as either of them; and I wonder that I got away as I did, rather than that I came off laughing, and red, and anything but tidy, and looking after Caddy, who looked after us out of the coach-window as long as she could see us.\n\nThis made us some quarter of an hour late, and when we came to Westminster Hall we found that the day’s business was begun. Worse than that, we found such an unusual crowd in the Court of Chancery that it was full to the door, and we could neither see nor hear what was passing within. It appeared to be something droll, for occasionally there was a laugh and a cry of “Silence!” It appeared to be something interesting, for every one was pushing and striving to get nearer. It appeared to be something that made the professional gentlemen very merry, for there were several young counsellors in wigs and whiskers on the outside of the crowd, and when one of them told the others about it, they put their hands in their pockets, and quite doubled themselves up with laughter, and went stamping about the pavement of the Hall.\n\nWe asked a gentleman by us if he knew what cause was on. He told us Jarndyce and Jarndyce. We asked him if he knew what was doing in it. He said really, no he did not, nobody ever did, but as well as he could make out, it was over. Over for the day? we asked him. No, he said, over for good.\n\nOver for good!\n\nWhen we heard this unaccountable answer, we looked at one another quite lost in amazement. Could it be possible that the will had set things right at last and that Richard and Ada were going to be rich? It seemed too good to be true. Alas it was!\n\nOur suspense was short, for a break-up soon took place in the crowd, and the people came streaming out looking flushed and hot and bringing a quantity of bad air with them. Still they were all exceedingly amused and were more like people coming out from a farce or a juggler than from a court of justice. We stood aside, watching for any countenance we knew, and presently great bundles of paper began to be carried out—bundles in bags, bundles too large to be got into any bags, immense masses of papers of all shapes and no shapes, which the bearers staggered under, and threw down for the time being, anyhow, on the Hall pavement, while they went back to bring out more. Even these clerks were laughing. We glanced at the papers, and seeing Jarndyce and Jarndyce everywhere, asked an official-looking person who was standing in the midst of them whether the cause was over. Yes, he said, it was all up with it at last, and burst out laughing too.\n\nAt this juncture we perceived Mr. Kenge coming out of court with an affable dignity upon him, listening to Mr. Vholes, who was deferential and carried his own bag. Mr. Vholes was the first to see us. “Here is Miss Summerson, sir,” he said. “And Mr. Woodcourt.”\n\n“Oh, indeed! Yes. Truly!” said Mr. Kenge, raising his hat to me with polished politeness. “How do you do? Glad to see you. Mr. Jarndyce is not here?”\n\nNo. He never came there, I reminded him.\n\n“Really,” returned Mr. Kenge, “it is as well that he is NOT here to-day, for his—shall I say, in my good friend’s absence, his indomitable singularity of opinion?—might have been strengthened, perhaps; not reasonably, but might have been strengthened.”\n\n“Pray what has been done to-day?” asked Allan.\n\n“I beg your pardon?” said Mr. Kenge with excessive urbanity.\n\n“What has been done to-day?”\n\n“What has been done,” repeated Mr. Kenge. “Quite so. Yes. Why, not much has been done; not much. We have been checked—brought up suddenly, I would say—upon the—shall I term it threshold?”\n\n“Is this will considered a genuine document, sir?” said Allan. “Will you tell us that?”\n\n“Most certainly, if I could,” said Mr. Kenge; “but we have not gone into that, we have not gone into that.”\n\n“We have not gone into that,” repeated Mr. Vholes as if his low inward voice were an echo.\n\n“You are to reflect, Mr. Woodcourt,” observed Mr. Kenge, using his silver trowel persuasively and smoothingly, “that this has been a great cause, that this has been a protracted cause, that this has been a complex cause. Jarndyce and Jarndyce has been termed, not inaptly, a monument of Chancery practice.”\n\n“And patience has sat upon it a long time,” said Allan.\n\n“Very well indeed, sir,” returned Mr. Kenge with a certain condescending laugh he had. “Very well! You are further to reflect, Mr. Woodcourt,” becoming dignified almost to severity, “that on the numerous difficulties, contingencies, masterly fictions, and forms of procedure in this great cause, there has been expended study, ability, eloquence, knowledge, intellect, Mr. Woodcourt, high intellect. For many years, the—a—I would say the flower of the bar, and the—a—I would presume to add, the matured autumnal fruits of the woolsack—have been lavished upon Jarndyce and Jarndyce. If the public have the benefit, and if the country have the adornment, of this great grasp, it must be paid for in money or money’s worth, sir.”\n\n“Mr. Kenge,” said Allan, appearing enlightened all in a moment. “Excuse me, our time presses. Do I understand that the whole estate is found to have been absorbed in costs?”\n\n“Hem! I believe so,” returned Mr. Kenge. “Mr. Vholes, what do YOU say?”\n\n“I believe so,” said Mr. Vholes.\n\n“And that thus the suit lapses and melts away?”\n\n“Probably,” returned Mr. Kenge. “Mr. Vholes?”\n\n“Probably,” said Mr. Vholes.\n\n“My dearest life,” whispered Allan, “this will break Richard’s heart!”\n\nThere was such a shock of apprehension in his face, and he knew Richard so perfectly, and I too had seen so much of his gradual decay, that what my dear girl had said to me in the fullness of her foreboding love sounded like a knell in my ears.\n\n“In case you should be wanting Mr. C., sir,” said Mr. Vholes, coming after us, “you’ll find him in court. I left him there resting himself a little. Good day, sir; good day, Miss Summerson.” As he gave me that slowly devouring look of his, while twisting up the strings of his bag before he hastened with it after Mr. Kenge, the benignant shadow of whose conversational presence he seemed afraid to leave, he gave one gasp as if he had swallowed the last morsel of his client, and his black buttoned-up unwholesome figure glided away to the low door at the end of the Hall.\n\n“My dear love,” said Allan, “leave to me, for a little while, the charge you gave me. Go home with this intelligence and come to Ada’s by and by!”\n\nI would not let him take me to a coach, but entreated him to go to Richard without a moment’s delay and leave me to do as he wished. Hurrying home, I found my guardian and told him gradually with what news I had returned. “Little woman,” said he, quite unmoved for himself, “to have done with the suit on any terms is a greater blessing than I had looked for. But my poor young cousins!”\n\nWe talked about them all the morning and discussed what it was possible to do. In the afternoon my guardian walked with me to Symond’s Inn and left me at the door. I went upstairs. When my darling heard my footsteps, she came out into the small passage and threw her arms round my neck, but she composed herself directly and said that Richard had asked for me several times. Allan had found him sitting in the corner of the court, she told me, like a stone figure. On being roused, he had broken away and made as if he would have spoken in a fierce voice to the judge. He was stopped by his mouth being full of blood, and Allan had brought him home.\n\nHe was lying on a sofa with his eyes closed when I went in. There were restoratives on the table; the room was made as airy as possible, and was darkened, and was very orderly and quiet. Allan stood behind him watching him gravely. His face appeared to me to be quite destitute of colour, and now that I saw him without his seeing me, I fully saw, for the first time, how worn away he was. But he looked handsomer than I had seen him look for many a day.\n\nI sat down by his side in silence. Opening his eyes by and by, he said in a weak voice, but with his old smile, “Dame Durden, kiss me, my dear!”\n\nIt was a great comfort and surprise to me to find him in his low state cheerful and looking forward. He was happier, he said, in our intended marriage than he could find words to tell me. My husband had been a guardian angel to him and Ada, and he blessed us both and wished us all the joy that life could yield us. I almost felt as if my own heart would have broken when I saw him take my husband’s hand and hold it to his breast.\n\nWe spoke of the future as much as possible, and he said several times that he must be present at our marriage if he could stand upon his feet. Ada would contrive to take him, somehow, he said. “Yes, surely, dearest Richard!” But as my darling answered him thus hopefully, so serene and beautiful, with the help that was to come to her so near—I knew—I knew!\n\nIt was not good for him to talk too much, and when he was silent, we were silent too. Sitting beside him, I made a pretence of working for my dear, as he had always been used to joke about my being busy. Ada leaned upon his pillow, holding his head upon her arm. He dozed often, and whenever he awoke without seeing him, said first of all, “Where is Woodcourt?”\n\nEvening had come on when I lifted up my eyes and saw my guardian standing in the little hall. “Who is that, Dame Durden?” Richard asked me. The door was behind him, but he had observed in my face that some one was there.\n\nI looked to Allan for advice, and as he nodded “Yes,” bent over Richard and told him. My guardian saw what passed, came softly by me in a moment, and laid his hand on Richard’s. “Oh, sir,” said Richard, “you are a good man, you are a good man!” and burst into tears for the first time.\n\nMy guardian, the picture of a good man, sat down in my place, keeping his hand on Richard’s.\n\n“My dear Rick,” said he, “the clouds have cleared away, and it is bright now. We can see now. We were all bewildered, Rick, more or less. What matters! And how are you, my dear boy?”\n\n“I am very weak, sir, but I hope I shall be stronger. I have to begin the world.”\n\n“Aye, truly; well said!” cried my guardian.\n\n“I will not begin it in the old way now,” said Richard with a sad smile. “I have learned a lesson now, sir. It was a hard one, but you shall be assured, indeed, that I have learned it.”\n\n“Well, well,” said my guardian, comforting him; “well, well, well, dear boy!”\n\n“I was thinking, sir,” resumed Richard, “that there is nothing on earth I should so much like to see as their house—Dame Durden’s and Woodcourt’s house. If I could be removed there when I begin to recover my strength, I feel as if I should get well there sooner than anywhere.”\n\n“Why, so have I been thinking too, Rick,” said my guardian, “and our little woman likewise; she and I have been talking of it this very day. I dare say her husband won’t object. What do you think?”\n\nRichard smiled and lifted up his arm to touch him as he stood behind the head of the couch.\n\n“I say nothing of Ada,” said Richard, “but I think of her, and have thought of her very much. Look at her! See her here, sir, bending over this pillow when she has so much need to rest upon it herself, my dear love, my poor girl!”\n\nHe clasped her in his arms, and none of us spoke. He gradually released her, and she looked upon us, and looked up to heaven, and moved her lips.\n\n“When I get down to Bleak House,” said Richard, “I shall have much to tell you, sir, and you will have much to show me. You will go, won’t you?”\n\n“Undoubtedly, dear Rick.”\n\n“Thank you; like you, like you,” said Richard. “But it’s all like you. They have been telling me how you planned it and how you remembered all Esther’s familiar tastes and ways. It will be like coming to the old Bleak House again.”\n\n“And you will come there too, I hope, Rick. I am a solitary man now, you know, and it will be a charity to come to me. A charity to come to me, my love!” he repeated to Ada as he gently passed his hand over her golden hair and put a lock of it to his lips. (I think he vowed within himself to cherish her if she were left alone.)\n\n“It was a troubled dream?” said Richard, clasping both my guardian’s hands eagerly.\n\n“Nothing more, Rick; nothing more.”\n\n“And you, being a good man, can pass it as such, and forgive and pity the dreamer, and be lenient and encouraging when he wakes?”\n\n“Indeed I can. What am I but another dreamer, Rick?”\n\n“I will begin the world!” said Richard with a light in his eyes.\n\nMy husband drew a little nearer towards Ada, and I saw him solemnly lift up his hand to warn my guardian.\n\n“When shall I go from this place to that pleasant country where the old times are, where I shall have strength to tell what Ada has been to me, where I shall be able to recall my many faults and blindnesses, where I shall prepare myself to be a guide to my unborn child?” said Richard. “When shall I go?”\n\n“Dear Rick, when you are strong enough,” returned my guardian.\n\n“Ada, my darling!”\n\nHe sought to raise himself a little. Allan raised him so that she could hold him on her bosom, which was what he wanted.\n\n“I have done you many wrongs, my own. I have fallen like a poor stray shadow on your way, I have married you to poverty and trouble, I have scattered your means to the winds. You will forgive me all this, my Ada, before I begin the world?”\n\nA smile irradiated his face as she bent to kiss him. He slowly laid his face down upon her bosom, drew his arms closer round her neck, and with one parting sob began the world. Not this world, oh, not this! The world that sets this right.\n\nWhen all was still, at a late hour, poor crazed Miss Flite came weeping to me and told me she had given her birds their liberty.\n\nDown in Lincolnshire\nThere is a hush upon Chesney Wold in these altered days, as there is upon a portion of the family history. The story goes that Sir Leicester paid some who could have spoken out to hold their peace; but it is a lame story, feebly whispering and creeping about, and any brighter spark of life it shows soon dies away. It is known for certain that the handsome Lady Dedlock lies in the mausoleum in the park, where the trees arch darkly overhead, and the owl is heard at night making the woods ring; but whence she was brought home to be laid among the echoes of that solitary place, or how she died, is all mystery. Some of her old friends, principally to be found among the peachy-cheeked charmers with the skeleton throats, did once occasionally say, as they toyed in a ghastly manner with large fans—like charmers reduced to flirting with grim death, after losing all their other beaux—did once occasionally say, when the world assembled together, that they wondered the ashes of the Dedlocks, entombed in the mausoleum, never rose against the profanation of her company. But the dead-and-gone Dedlocks take it very calmly and have never been known to object.\n\nUp from among the fern in the hollow, and winding by the bridle-road among the trees, comes sometimes to this lonely spot the sound of horses’ hoofs. Then may be seen Sir Leicester—invalided, bent, and almost blind, but of worthy presence yet—riding with a stalwart man beside him, constant to his bridle-rein. When they come to a certain spot before the mausoleum-door, Sir Leicester’s accustomed horse stops of his own accord, and Sir Leicester, pulling off his hat, is still for a few moments before they ride away.\n\nWar rages yet with the audacious Boythorn, though at uncertain intervals, and now hotly, and now coolly, flickering like an unsteady fire. The truth is said to be that when Sir Leicester came down to Lincolnshire for good, Mr. Boythorn showed a manifest desire to abandon his right of way and do whatever Sir Leicester would, which Sir Leicester, conceiving to be a condescension to his illness or misfortune, took in such high dudgeon, and was so magnificently aggrieved by, that Mr. Boythorn found himself under the necessity of committing a flagrant trespass to restore his neighbour to himself. Similarly, Mr. Boythorn continues to post tremendous placards on the disputed thoroughfare and (with his bird upon his head) to hold forth vehemently against Sir Leicester in the sanctuary of his own home; similarly, also, he defies him as of old in the little church by testifying a bland unconsciousness of his existence. But it is whispered that when he is most ferocious towards his old foe, he is really most considerate, and that Sir Leicester, in the dignity of being implacable, little supposes how much he is humoured. As little does he think how near together he and his antagonist have suffered in the fortunes of two sisters, and his antagonist, who knows it now, is not the man to tell him. So the quarrel goes on to the satisfaction of both.\n\nIn one of the lodges of the park—that lodge within sight of the house where, once upon a time, when the waters were out down in Lincolnshire, my Lady used to see the keeper’s child—the stalwart man, the trooper formerly, is housed. Some relics of his old calling hang upon the walls, and these it is the chosen recreation of a little lame man about the stable-yard to keep gleaming bright. A busy little man he always is, in the polishing at harness-house doors, of stirrup-irons, bits, curb-chains, harness bosses, anything in the way of a stable-yard that will take a polish, leading a life of friction. A shaggy little damaged man, withal, not unlike an old dog of some mongrel breed, who has been considerably knocked about. He answers to the name of Phil.\n\nA goodly sight it is to see the grand old housekeeper (harder of hearing now) going to church on the arm of her son and to observe—which few do, for the house is scant of company in these times—the relations of both towards Sir Leicester, and his towards them. They have visitors in the high summer weather, when a grey cloak and umbrella, unknown to Chesney Wold at other periods, are seen among the leaves; when two young ladies are occasionally found gambolling in sequestered saw-pits and such nooks of the park; and when the smoke of two pipes wreathes away into the fragrant evening air from the trooper’s door. Then is a fife heard trolling within the lodge on the inspiring topic of the “British Grenadiers”; and as the evening closes in, a gruff inflexible voice is heard to say, while two men pace together up and down, “But I never own to it before the old girl. Discipline must be maintained.”\n\nThe greater part of the house is shut up, and it is a show-house no longer; yet Sir Leicester holds his shrunken state in the long drawing-room for all that, and reposes in his old place before my Lady’s picture. Closed in by night with broad screens, and illumined only in that part, the light of the drawing-room seems gradually contracting and dwindling until it shall be no more. A little more, in truth, and it will be all extinguished for Sir Leicester; and the damp door in the mausoleum which shuts so tight, and looks so obdurate, will have opened and received him.\n\nVolumnia, growing with the flight of time pinker as to the red in her face, and yellower as to the white, reads to Sir Leicester in the long evenings and is driven to various artifices to conceal her yawns, of which the chief and most efficacious is the insertion of the pearl necklace between her rosy lips. Long-winded treatises on the Buffy and Boodle question, showing how Buffy is immaculate and Boodle villainous, and how the country is lost by being all Boodle and no Buffy, or saved by being all Buffy and no Boodle (it must be one of the two, and cannot be anything else), are the staple of her reading. Sir Leicester is not particular what it is and does not appear to follow it very closely, further than that he always comes broad awake the moment Volumnia ventures to leave off, and sonorously repeating her last words, begs with some displeasure to know if she finds herself fatigued. However, Volumnia, in the course of her bird-like hopping about and pecking at papers, has alighted on a memorandum concerning herself in the event of “anything happening” to her kinsman, which is handsome compensation for an extensive course of reading and holds even the dragon Boredom at bay.\n\nThe cousins generally are rather shy of Chesney Wold in its dullness, but take to it a little in the shooting season, when guns are heard in the plantations, and a few scattered beaters and keepers wait at the old places of appointment for low-spirited twos and threes of cousins. The debilitated cousin, more debilitated by the dreariness of the place, gets into a fearful state of depression, groaning under penitential sofa-pillows in his gunless hours and protesting that such fernal old jail’s—nough t’sew fler up—frever.\n\nThe only great occasions for Volumnia in this changed aspect of the place in Lincolnshire are those occasions, rare and widely separated, when something is to be done for the county or the country in the way of gracing a public ball. Then, indeed, does the tuckered sylph come out in fairy form and proceed with joy under cousinly escort to the exhausted old assembly-room, fourteen heavy miles off, which, during three hundred and sixty-four days and nights of every ordinary year, is a kind of antipodean lumber-room full of old chairs and tables upside down. Then, indeed, does she captivate all hearts by her condescension, by her girlish vivacity, and by her skipping about as in the days when the hideous old general with the mouth too full of teeth had not cut one of them at two guineas each. Then does she twirl and twine, a pastoral nymph of good family, through the mazes of the dance. Then do the swains appear with tea, with lemonade, with sandwiches, with homage. Then is she kind and cruel, stately and unassuming, various, beautifully wilful. Then is there a singular kind of parallel between her and the little glass chandeliers of another age embellishing that assembly-room, which, with their meagre stems, their spare little drops, their disappointing knobs where no drops are, their bare little stalks from which knobs and drops have both departed, and their little feeble prismatic twinkling, all seem Volumnias.\n\nFor the rest, Lincolnshire life to Volumnia is a vast blank of overgrown house looking out upon trees, sighing, wringing their hands, bowing their heads, and casting their tears upon the window-panes in monotonous depressions. A labyrinth of grandeur, less the property of an old family of human beings and their ghostly likenesses than of an old family of echoings and thunderings which start out of their hundred graves at every sound and go resounding through the building. A waste of unused passages and staircases in which to drop a comb upon a bedroom floor at night is to send a stealthy footfall on an errand through the house. A place where few people care to go about alone, where a maid screams if an ash drops from the fire, takes to crying at all times and seasons, becomes the victim of a low disorder of the spirits, and gives warning and departs.\n\nThus Chesney Wold. With so much of itself abandoned to darkness and vacancy; with so little change under the summer shining or the wintry lowering; so sombre and motionless always—no flag flying now by day, no rows of lights sparkling by night; with no family to come and go, no visitors to be the souls of pale cold shapes of rooms, no stir of life about it—passion and pride, even to the stranger’s eye, have died away from the place in Lincolnshire and yielded it to dull repose.\nThe Close of Esther’s Narrative\n\nFull seven happy years I have been the mistress of Bleak House. The few words that I have to add to what I have written are soon penned; then I and the unknown friend to whom I write will part for ever. Not without much dear remembrance on my side. Not without some, I hope, on his or hers.\n\nThey gave my darling into my arms, and through many weeks I never left her. The little child who was to have done so much was born before the turf was planted on its father’s grave. It was a boy; and I, my husband, and my guardian gave him his father’s name.\n\nThe help that my dear counted on did come to her, though it came, in the eternal wisdom, for another purpose. Though to bless and restore his mother, not his father, was the errand of this baby, its power was mighty to do it. When I saw the strength of the weak little hand and how its touch could heal my darling’s heart and raised hope within her, I felt a new sense of the goodness and the tenderness of God.\n\nThey throve, and by degrees I saw my dear girl pass into my country garden and walk there with her infant in her arms. I was married then. I was the happiest of the happy.\n\nIt was at this time that my guardian joined us and asked Ada when she would come home.\n\n“Both houses are your home, my dear,” said he, “but the older Bleak House claims priority. When you and my boy are strong enough to do it, come and take possession of your home.”\n\nAda called him “her dearest cousin, John.” But he said, no, it must be guardian now. He was her guardian henceforth, and the boy’s; and he had an old association with the name. So she called him guardian, and has called him guardian ever since. The children know him by no other name. I say the children; I have two little daughters.\n\nIt is difficult to believe that Charley (round-eyed still, and not at all grammatical) is married to a miller in our neighbourhood; yet so it is; and even now, looking up from my desk as I write early in the morning at my summer window, I see the very mill beginning to go round. I hope the miller will not spoil Charley; but he is very fond of her, and Charley is rather vain of such a match, for he is well to do and was in great request. So far as my small maid is concerned, I might suppose time to have stood for seven years as still as the mill did half an hour ago, since little Emma, Charley’s sister, is exactly what Charley used to be. As to Tom, Charley’s brother, I am really afraid to say what he did at school in ciphering, but I think it was decimals. He is apprenticed to the miller, whatever it was, and is a good bashful fellow, always falling in love with somebody and being ashamed of it.\n\nCaddy Jellyby passed her very last holidays with us and was a dearer creature than ever, perpetually dancing in and out of the house with the children as if she had never given a dancing-lesson in her life. Caddy keeps her own little carriage now instead of hiring one, and lives full two miles further westward than Newman Street. She works very hard, her husband (an excellent one) being lame and able to do very little. Still, she is more than contented and does all she has to do with all her heart. Mr. Jellyby spends his evenings at her new house with his head against the wall as he used to do in her old one. I have heard that Mrs. Jellyby was understood to suffer great mortification from her daughter’s ignoble marriage and pursuits, but I hope she got over it in time. She has been disappointed in Borrioboola-Gha, which turned out a failure in consequence of the king of Borrioboola wanting to sell everybody—who survived the climate—for rum, but she has taken up with the rights of women to sit in Parliament, and Caddy tells me it is a mission involving more correspondence than the old one. I had almost forgotten Caddy’s poor little girl. She is not such a mite now, but she is deaf and dumb. I believe there never was a better mother than Caddy, who learns, in her scanty intervals of leisure, innumerable deaf and dumb arts to soften the affliction of her child.\n\nAs if I were never to have done with Caddy, I am reminded here of Peepy and old Mr. Turveydrop. Peepy is in the Custom House, and doing extremely well. Old Mr. Turveydrop, very apoplectic, still exhibits his deportment about town, still enjoys himself in the old manner, is still believed in in the old way. He is constant in his patronage of Peepy and is understood to have bequeathed him a favourite French clock in his dressing-room—which is not his property.\n\nWith the first money we saved at home, we added to our pretty house by throwing out a little growlery expressly for my guardian, which we inaugurated with great splendour the next time he came down to see us. I try to write all this lightly, because my heart is full in drawing to an end, but when I write of him, my tears will have their way.\n\nI never look at him but I hear our poor dear Richard calling him a good man. To Ada and her pretty boy, he is the fondest father; to me he is what he has ever been, and what name can I give to that? He is my husband’s best and dearest friend, he is our children’s darling, he is the object of our deepest love and veneration. Yet while I feel towards him as if he were a superior being, I am so familiar with him and so easy with him that I almost wonder at myself. I have never lost my old names, nor has he lost his; nor do I ever, when he is with us, sit in any other place than in my old chair at his side, Dame Trot, Dame Durden, Little Woman—all just the same as ever; and I answer, “Yes, dear guardian!” just the same.\n\nI have never known the wind to be in the east for a single moment since the day when he took me to the porch to read the name. I remarked to him once that the wind seemed never in the east now, and he said, no, truly; it had finally departed from that quarter on that very day.\n\nI think my darling girl is more beautiful than ever. The sorrow that has been in her face—for it is not there now—seems to have purified even its innocent expression and to have given it a diviner quality. Sometimes when I raise my eyes and see her in the black dress that she still wears, teaching my Richard, I feel—it is difficult to express—as if it were so good to know that she remembers her dear Esther in her prayers.\n\nI call him my Richard! But he says that he has two mamas, and I am one.\n\nWe are not rich in the bank, but we have always prospered, and we have quite enough. I never walk out with my husband but I hear the people bless him. I never go into a house of any degree but I hear his praises or see them in grateful eyes. I never lie down at night but I know that in the course of that day he has alleviated pain and soothed some fellow-creature in the time of need. I know that from the beds of those who were past recovery, thanks have often, often gone up, in the last hour, for his patient ministration. Is not this to be rich?\n\nThe people even praise me as the doctor’s wife. The people even like me as I go about, and make so much of me that I am quite abashed. I owe it all to him, my love, my pride! They like me for his sake, as I do everything I do in life for his sake.\n\nA night or two ago, after bustling about preparing for my darling and my guardian and little Richard, who are coming to-morrow, I was sitting out in the porch of all places, that dearly memorable porch, when Allan came home. So he said, “My precious little woman, what are you doing here?” And I said, “The moon is shining so brightly, Allan, and the night is so delicious, that I have been sitting here thinking.”\n\n“What have you been thinking about, my dear?” said Allan then.\n\n“How curious you are!” said I. “I am almost ashamed to tell you, but I will. I have been thinking about my old looks—such as they were.”\n\n“And what have you been thinking about THEM, my busy bee?” said Allan.\n\n“I have been thinking that I thought it was impossible that you COULD have loved me any better, even if I had retained them.”\n\n“‘Such as they were’?” said Allan, laughing.\n\n“Such as they were, of course.”\n\n“My dear Dame Durden,” said Allan, drawing my arm through his, “do you ever look in the glass?”\n\n“You know I do; you see me do it.”\n\n“And don’t you know that you are prettier than you ever were?”\n\nI did not know that; I am not certain that I know it now. But I know that my dearest little pets are very pretty, and that my darling is very beautiful, and that my husband is very handsome, and that my guardian has the brightest and most benevolent face that ever was seen, and that they can very well do without much beauty in me—even supposing—.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-1023"]="7. 유령의 길\n\n8. 숱한 죄악을 덮으며\n9. 징조와 조짐\n\n10. 법률 필경사\n11. 우리 형제\n\n12. 망보며\n13. 에스더의 이야기\n\n14. 몸가짐\n15. 벨 야드\n\n16. 톰 혼자 사는 곳\n17. 에스더의 이야기\n\n18. 데드록 마님\n19. 비키시오\n\n20. 새 하숙인\n21. 스몰위드 일가\n\n22. 버킷 씨\n\n23. 에스더의 이야기\n24. 항소 사건\n\n25. 스내그스비 부인은 다 알아챈다\n26. 명사수들\n\n27. 늙은 군인이 하나만은 아니다\n28. 철물 제조업자\n\n29. 그 젊은이\n30. 에스더의 이야기\n\n31. 간병인과 환자\n32. 약속한 시각\n\n33. 끼어든 자들\n\n34. 나사를 한 번 더 조이며\n35. 에스더의 이야기\n\n36. 체스니 월드\n37. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스\n\n38. 어떤 분투\n39. 변호사와 의뢰인\n\n40. 나라와 가정\n41. 토킹혼 씨의 방에서\n\n42. 토킹혼 씨의 사무실에서\n43. 에스더의 이야기\n\n44. 편지와 답장\n45. 위임받아\n\n46. 저자를 잡아라!\n47. 조의 유언\n\n48. 좁혀 들며\n49. 충직한 우정\n\n50. 에스더의 이야기\n51. 깨우침\n\n52. 고집\n53. 자취\n\n54. 지뢰를 터뜨리며\n55. 도주\n\n56. 추격\n57. 에스더의 이야기\n\n58. 겨울 낮과 밤\n59. 에스더의 이야기\n\n60. 전망\n61. 어떤 발견\n\n62. 또 다른 발견\n63. 강철과 무쇠\n\n64. 에스더의 이야기\n65. 세상을 시작하며\n\n66. 링컨셔의 시골에서\n67. 에스더 이야기의 끝맺음\n\n언젠가 어느 대법원 판사가 친절하게도, 광기를 의심받을 만한 구석이라곤 전혀 없는 백오십 명 남짓한 남녀 무리 가운데 한 사람인 나에게 이렇게 알려준 적이 있다. 대법원은 비록 세간의 편견이 화려하게 들끓는 대상이긴 하지만(이 대목에서 나는 판사의 눈길이 내 쪽으로 흘끔 향했다고 느꼈다), 거의 흠 하나 없이 깨끗하다는 것이다. 판사는 진행 속도에 사소한 결점이 한둘 있긴 했다고 인정했으나, 그것마저 과장된 데다 전적으로 \"대중의 인색함\" 탓이라고 했다. 그 죄 많은 대중이라는 것이, 알고 보니 최근까지도 더없이 단호한 태도로 대법원 판사의 정원을 결코 늘리지 않으려 작심해왔다는 것이었다. 그 정원이 정해진 건 내 생각에 리처드 이세 때이지만, 다른 어느 왕이라고 해도 상관없겠다.\n\n이 농담은 책 본문에 끼워 넣기에는 너무 심오한 듯해서, 그러지 않았다면 나는 이 말을 입심 좋은 켄지 씨나 볼스 씨에게 되돌려주었을 것이다. 둘 중 한 사람 입에서 나온 말이 분명하다고 보기 때문이다. 그런 입에 올렸다면 셰익스피어 소네트 한 구절을 적절히 짝지어 붙였을지도 모른다.\n\"내 천성은 길들었네\n\n염색공의 손처럼, 제 일하는 것에 물들어서.\n그러니 나를 가엾이 여기고, 내가 새로워지기를 빌어주오!\"\n\n그러나 인색한 대중이 이 문제와 관련해 그동안 어떤 일이 벌어졌고 지금도 벌어지고 있는지를 아는 것이 유익하기에, 나는 여기서 밝혀둔다. 이 책에 적힌 대법원에 관한 모든 내용은 실질적으로 진실이며, 진실의 범위 안에 있다. 그리들리의 사건은 본질에서 실제로 일어난 한 사건과 조금도 다르지 않으니, 그 끔찍한 부정을 처음부터 끝까지 직무상 속속들이 아는 어느 이해관계 없는 인사가 세상에 공개한 일이다. 바로 지금 이 순간(1853년 8월) 이 법정에는 거의 이십 년 전에 시작된 소송이 하나 걸려 있는데, 한자리에 변호인이 서른에서 마흔 명까지 나선 적이 있고, 발생한 소송 비용이 칠만 파운드에 이르며, 이것이 우호적 소송인데도, (장담하건대) 시작될 때나 지금이나 종결에 한 발짝도 가까워지지 않았다. 또 다른 유명한 대법원 소송이 하나 있는데 아직 판결이 나지 않았으니, 지난 세기가 저물기 전에 시작되어 칠만 파운드의 곱절이 넘는 금액이 소송 비용으로 삼켜졌다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에 다른 근거가 더 필요하다면, 나는 이 지면에 그것을 비처럼 쏟아부을 수 있으니, 그건 어느 인색한 대중의 수치다.\n딱 한 가지만 더 한마디 덧붙이려 한다. 이른바 자연발화의 가능성은 크룩 씨가 죽은 뒤로 부정당해왔다. 나의 좋은 벗 루이스 씨는(그 일이 모든 권위자에게 폐기되었다고 여긴 것이 곧 알게 되듯 완전한 착각이었다) 그 사건이 기록되던 무렵 나에게 기발한 편지 몇 통을 공개하여, 자연발화란 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이라고 주장했다. 내가 독자를 고의로든 부주의로든 오도하지 않으며, 그 장면을 쓰기 전에 이 주제를 애써 조사했다는 점은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다. 기록에 남은 사례가 서른 건쯤 되는데, 그 가운데 가장 유명한 코르넬리아 데 바우디 체세나테 백작 부인의 사건은 베로나의 참사회원 주세페 비안키니가 세밀하게 조사하고 기술했다. 그는 문필로도 이름난 인물로, 1731년 베로나에서 그 기록을 펴냈고 나중에 로마에서 다시 펴냈다. 그 사건에서 관찰된 정황은, 합리적인 의심을 모두 넘어, 크룩 씨의 사건에서 관찰된 정황과 똑같다. 그다음으로 유명한 사례는 그보다 육 년 앞서 랭스에서 일어났는데, 이 사건의 기록자는 프랑스가 배출한 가장 이름난 외과의 가운데 한 사람인 르 카다. 그 대상은 한 여인이었고, 그 남편은 아무것도 모르는 채 아내를 살해했다는 죄로 유죄 판결을 받았다. 그러나 상급 법원에 엄숙히 항소한 결과, 그녀가 자연발화라는 이름으로 불리는 바로 그 죽음을 맞았다는 사실이 증거로 드러나 무죄가 선고되었다. 나는 이 주목할 만한 사실들과, 제2권 30면에서 찾아볼 수 있는 권위자들에 대한 일반적 언급, 곧 더 근래에 활동한 프랑스, 영국, 스코틀랜드의 저명한 의학 교수들의 기록된 견해와 경험을 굳이 더 보탤 필요는 없다고 본다. 다만 한마디만 덧붙이자면, 인간의 사건이 으레 받아들여지는 그 증언이 상당량 자연발화로 사라지기 전까지는, 나는 이 사실들을 버리지 않으리라.\n\n* 옮긴이 주: 이 대목은 인쇄본의 특정 면을 가리킨다. 이 프로젝트 구텐베르크 판본에서는 관련 내용이 제30장 90번째 문단에 있다.\n* 또 한 가지 사례는 어느 치과의가 아주 또렷이 기술한 것으로, 비교적 최근 아메리카 합중국 콜럼버스라는 도시에서 일어났다. 그 대상은 술집을 운영하던 독일인이었으며, 구제 불능의 술꾼이었다.\n\n「황폐한 집」에서 나는 친숙한 것들의 낭만적인 면을 일부러 곱씹어 다루었다.\n1853년\n\n런던. 미카엘 축일 개정 기간이 막 끝났고, 대법원장은 링컨 법학원 회관에 자리 잡았다. 무자비한 십일월 날씨. 거리에는 진흙이 어찌나 두껍게 깔렸는지 마치 물이 이제 막 대지 표면에서 물러난 것만 같으니, 사십 척쯤 되는 거대공룡 한 마리가 코끼리만 한 도마뱀처럼 뒤뚱뒤뚱 홀본 언덕을 기어오르는 광경을 마주친대도 그리 놀랄 일은 아니겠다. 굴뚝 꼭대기에서 연기가 내려앉아 부드러운 검은 이슬비를 이루고, 그 안에는 다 자란 눈송이만큼 굵은 검댕 조각이 섞여 있다. 마치 태양의 죽음을 애도해 상복을 차려입은 듯하다. 진창에 뒤섞여 분간조차 안 되는 개들. 그보다 나을 것도 없는 말들. 눈가리개까지 흙탕물이 튀었다. 걸어 다니는 행인들은 너 나 할 것 없이 심술이라는 전염병에 옮은 듯 서로 우산을 부딪쳐대고, 동틀 녘부터(이날에 동이 트기는 했다면) 수만 명의 다른 행인이 미끄러지고 자빠진 길모퉁이에서 발을 헛디딘다. 켜켜이 쌓인 진흙 더께 위에 새로운 침전물을 보태고, 그 지점마다 포도에 끈질기게 들러붙어, 복리로 불어난다.\n사방이 안개투성이다. 강 위쪽도 안개, 푸른 모래톱과 풀밭 사이를 흐르는 그곳도 안개다. 강 아래쪽도 안개, 빽빽이 늘어선 선박들과 거대하고 (지저분한) 도시의 강변 오염물 사이를 더럽게 굴러다니는 그곳도 안개다. 에식스 습지에도 안개, 켄트의 고지대에도 안개. 안개는 석탄 운반선의 취사간으로 기어들고, 큰 배의 활대 위에 드러눕고 삭구 사이를 맴돈다. 안개는 거룻배와 작은 배의 뱃전에 축 늘어진다. 안개는 병동 난롯가에서 쌕쌕거리는 늙은 그리니치 연금 수령자들의 눈과 목구멍에도, 비좁은 선실 아래서 성난 선장이 빨아 대는 오후 담뱃대의 대통과 통에도 깃들고, 갑판 위에서 덜덜 떠는 어린 견습생의 발가락과 손가락을 잔인하게 꼬집는다. 다리 위를 지나던 사람들은 난간 너머로 안개로 이루어진 발아래 하늘을 들여다보는데, 사방이 온통 안개여서 마치 자기가 기구를 타고 올라가 자욱한 구름 속에 매달린 것만 같다.\n\n가스등이 거리 곳곳에서 안개를 뚫고 어렴풋이 빛난다. 마치 흠뻑 젖은 들판에서 농부와 쟁기질하는 소년이 어렴풋이 떠오르는 태양을 바라보듯이. 가게는 거의 다 평소보다 두 시간이나 일찍 불을 밝혔다. 가스등도 그 사실을 아는지, 야위고 마지못한 듯한 표정이다.\n쌀쌀한 오후가 가장 쌀쌀하고, 짙은 안개가 가장 짙으며, 진흙투성이 거리가 가장 진흙투성이인 곳은, 저 둔하기 짝이 없는 늙은 장애물 근처다. 둔하기 짝이 없는 늙은 법인의 문턱에 딱 어울리는 장식물, 바로 템플 바다. 그리고 템플 바 바로 곁, 안개의 한복판에 있는 링컨 법학원 회관에, 대법원장이 자신의 대법원에 앉아 있다.\n\n이 대법원이, 케케묵은 죄인들 가운데 가장 해로운 그것이, 오늘 하늘과 땅이 지켜보는 가운데 더듬거리고 허우적대는 꼴과 맞아떨어지려면, 아무리 짙은 안개라도 모자라고 아무리 깊은 진흙과 진창이라도 모자랄 것이다.\n이런 오후라면, 다른 어느 때보다도, 대법원장은 바로 여기에 앉아 있어야 마땅하다. 실제로 여기 앉아 있다. 머리 둘레에는 안개의 후광을 두르고, 진홍빛 천과 휘장으로 폭신하게 둘러싸인 채로, 큼직한 구레나룻에 자그마한 목소리, 끝도 없이 이어지는 변론서를 든 거구의 변호사가 그에게 말을 늘어놓고, 그는 겉으로는 지붕의 채광창 쪽으로 시선을 두지만 거기서 보이는 것이라곤 안개뿐이다. 이런 오후라면 대법원 법정 변호사단의 스무 명 남짓한 사람들이, 실제로 여기 그러고 있듯이, 끝없는 소송의 만 가지 단계 가운데 하나에 안개 속에서 매달려, 미끄러운 판례를 두고 서로 발을 걸어 넘어뜨리고, 무릎까지 빠지는 전문 용어 속을 더듬거리며, 염소 털과 말총으로 만든 가발 쓴 머리를 말의 벽에 들이받고, 마치 배우들이 그러듯 정색한 얼굴로 형평을 가장한다. 이런 오후라면 그 소송에 관여한 갖가지 사무 변호사들이, 그중 두셋은 그것으로 한밑천 잡은 아버지에게서 소송을 물려받았는데, 실제로 그러지 않은가, 한 줄로 늘어서 있을 것이다. 등기관의 붉은 탁자와 비단 법복 사이, 길게 깔개를 깐 우물 같은 자리에 (다만 그 바닥에서 진실을 찾으려 들면 헛수고일 테지만), 소장과 반소장, 답변서, 재항변서, 강제명령서, 진술서, 쟁점, 심리관에게 보내는 회부서, 심리관의 보고서, 값비싼 헛소리의 산더미를 앞에 쌓아 놓고서. 법정이 흐릿한 것도 당연하다, 여기저기서 양초가 닳아 가니. 안개가 거기 무겁게 걸려 영영 빠져나가지 못할 듯한 것도 당연하다. 스테인드글라스 창이 색을 잃고 한 줄기 햇빛도 들이지 못하는 것도 당연하다. 거리에서 문의 유리창으로 안을 들여다보는 문외한들이 그 부엉이 같은 음산한 분위기에, 그리고 폭신한 단상에서 빛이라곤 없는 채광창을 들여다보는 대법원장 쪽에서 지붕까지 나른하게 메아리치며 늘어지는 말소리에 들어설 엄두를 못 내는 것도 당연하다, 단상에서는 시중드는 가발들이 죄다 안개 둑에 처박혀 있으니! 이것이 바로 대법원이다. 모든 주마다 무너져 가는 집과 황폐해진 땅을 거느리고, 모든 정신병원마다 진이 빠진 미치광이를, 모든 교회 묘지마다 죽은 자를 거느린 곳. 닳아빠진 신발 뒤축에 해진 옷을 걸친 파산한 소송인이 아는 사람마다 찾아다니며 빌리고 구걸하게 만드는 곳. 돈의 위세에는 정당한 권리를 지치게 만들 수단을 차고 넘치게 쥐여 주는 곳. 재산과 인내와 용기와 희망을 어찌나 바닥내고, 머리를 무너뜨리고 가슴을 찢어 놓는지, 그 종사자 가운데 명예로운 사람이라면 누구나 이런 경고를 던지지 않을 수 없는, 실제로 자주 던지는 곳. \"여기 오느니 차라리 당할 수 있는 부당함은 다 당하시오!\"\n\n이 음침한 오후, 대법원장의 법정에 자리한 이는, 대법원장과 그 소송의 변호인, 어느 소송에도 끼지 않는 변호인 두셋, 그리고 앞서 말한 사무 변호사들의 우물 말고 또 누가 있을까? 판사 아래쪽에는 가발에 법복을 갖춘 등기관이 있다. 그리고 법정용 정장을 차려입고 권표나 소송 문서 가방이나 옥새 주머니나 뭐 그런 것을 든 사람이 두셋 있다. 이들은 죄다 하품을 한다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송(지금 다루는 그 사건)에서는 재미 부스러기 하나 떨어지는 법이 없으니, 그것은 오래오래전에 진작 바싹 짜내어졌다. 속기사와 법정 기록원, 신문 기자들은 잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 시작되면 어김없이 다른 단골들과 함께 자리를 뜬다. 그들의 자리는 텅 비어 있다. 휘장을 친 성소를 더 잘 들여다보려고 회관 한쪽 좌석에 올라선 사람이 있으니, 찌그러진 보닛을 쓴 자그마한 미친 노파다. 그녀는 법정이 열릴 때부터 파할 때까지 늘 자리를 지키며, 도무지 알 수 없는 어떤 판결이 자기에게 유리하게 내려지기를 언제나 기다린다. 어떤 이는 그녀가 정말 어느 소송의 당사자이거나 그랬다고 하지만, 아무도 확실히는 모른다, 아무도 신경 쓰지 않으니까. 그녀는 망태기에 자질구레한 쓰레기를 넣고 다니며 그것을 자기 서류라고 부르는데, 주로 종이 성냥과 마른 라벤더로 이루어져 있다. 누렇게 뜬 죄수 하나가 구금 상태로 끌려 나왔으니, 벌써 여섯 번째로 \"자기 모욕죄를 씻겠다\"는 본인 신청을 하러 온 것이다. 그러나 그는 단 하나 살아남은 유언 집행인으로, 자기가 알았다고는 누구도 주장하지 않는 회계 장부 문제로 뒤죽박죽 빠져든 처지라, 그 일을 끝내기란 도무지 가망 없는 노릇이다. 그러는 사이 그의 인생 전망은 끝장나 버렸다. 또 다른 파산한 소송인이 있는데, 그는 슈롭셔에서 주기적으로 나타나 하루 업무가 끝날 무렵 대법원장에게 한마디 하려고 발버둥 치는 사람이다. 대법원장이 사반세기 동안 자기 삶을 황폐하게 만들어 놓고도 법적으로는 자기 존재를 모른다는 사실을 도무지 납득하지 못하는 그는, 좋은 자리를 차지하고 판사를 노려보다가, 판사가 일어서는 순간 낭랑하게 울리는 항의의 목소리로 \"각하!\" 하고 외칠 채비를 한다. 이 소송인의 얼굴을 아는 변호사 사무원 몇과 다른 이들은, 그가 무슨 볼거리를 제공해 이 음울한 날씨를 조금이나마 활기차게 해 줄까 싶어 어슬렁대며 남는다.\n잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 단조롭게 웅웅거리며 이어진다. 이 허수아비 같은 소송은 세월이 흐르면서 어찌나 복잡해졌는지 살아 있는 누구도 그 뜻을 모른다. 그 당사자들이 가장 모르는데, 대법원 변호사 둘이 그 소송을 두고 오 분만 이야기해도 모든 전제를 놓고 완전히 엇갈리지 않는 법이 없다는 것이 지금껏 관찰된 바다. 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 아이가 이 소송 속에서 태어났고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 젊은이가 이 소송 속에서 혼인했으며, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 노인이 이 소송에서 빠져나가듯 죽었다. 수십 명이 영문도 모른 채 어쩌다 자기가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 당사자가 되어 있는 것을 정신없이 발견했고, 온 집안이 이 소송과 함께 전설처럼 내려오는 증오를 물려받았다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 해결되면 새 흔들목마를 사 주겠다는 약속을 받았던 어린 원고나 피고는, 어느덧 자라 진짜 말을 손에 넣고는, 저세상으로 총총히 달려가 버렸다. 어여쁘던 법원의 피후견 처녀들은 시들어 어머니가 되고 할머니가 되었다. 대법원장의 긴 행렬이 들고 났다. 소송에 쌓인 군단 같은 소장들은 그저 사망자 명부로 둔갑했다. 늙은 톰 잔다이스가 절망 끝에 챈서리 거리의 어느 커피점에서 제 머리통을 날려 버린 뒤로, 어쩌면 이 땅에 잔다이스라는 사람은 셋도 남지 않았을 것이다. 그래도 잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 여전히 그 음울한 몸뚱이를 법정 앞에서 질질 끌고 다니니, 영원토록 가망 없는 채로.\n\n잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 웃음거리가 되어 버렸다. 그것이 이 소송에서 나온 단 하나의 좋은 결실이다. 그것은 많은 사람에게 죽음이었지만, 법조계에서는 농담거리다. 대법원의 심리관이라면 누구나 그것에서 회부를 하나쯤 받아 보았다. 대법원장이라면 누구나 변호사 시절 누군가를 위해 \"거기 끼어\" 있었다. 코가 시퍼렇고 구근처럼 부푼 신발을 신은 늙은 법학원 간부들이 회관에서 만찬을 마친 뒤 엄선된 포트와인 위원회 자리에서 그것을 두고 그럴듯한 우스개를 떨었다. 견습 변호사들은 그것에 대고 법률적 재치를 갈고닦는 버릇이 있었다. 지난번 대법원장은 그것을 솜씨 좋게 다루었으니, 저명한 비단 법복의 블로어스 씨가 하늘에서 감자가 비처럼 쏟아진다면 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있겠다고 하자, 그 말을 바로잡으며 \"아니면 우리가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 다 끝마쳤을 때라든가요, 블로어스 씨\" 하고 한마디 한 것인데, 이 익살에 권표며 가방이며 주머니를 든 자들이 유독 간지러워했다.\n\n잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 소송 바깥의 얼마나 많은 사람에게 그 해로운 손을 뻗쳐 망치고 더럽혔는지는 참으로 막막한 질문이겠다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 먼지 쌓인 영장 다발이 무더기로 음산하게 온갖 모양으로 비틀려 꽂힌 꽂이를 둔 심리관부터, 그 영원한 표제 아래 수만 장의 대법원 이절판 면을 베껴 온 육서기과의 필사 서기에 이르기까지, 그것 때문에 천성이 나아진 사람은 하나도 없다. 온갖 종류의 거짓 구실 아래 자행되는 속임수, 회피, 미루기, 약탈, 골치 썩이기 속에는 결코 좋게 끝날 수 없는 영향력이 도사리고 있다. 가엾은 소송인들을 멀찍이 떼어 놓느라 치즐 씨든 미즐 씨든 누구든 특별히 바빠서 만찬 때까지 약속이 꽉 찼다고 까마득한 옛날부터 우겨 온 그 변호사 사무실 심부름꾼 소년들조차, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 덕에 도덕성이 한층 더 뒤틀리고 비뚤어졌을지 모른다. 이 소송의 재산 관리인은 그것으로 제법 짭짤한 돈을 챙겼지만, 그러면서 제 어머니마저 못 믿는 불신과 제 동족을 향한 경멸도 함께 챙겼다. 치즐과 미즐을 비롯한 무리는, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 사무실에서 치워지고 나면 저 미결로 남은 자질구레한 건을 들여다보고 부당한 대우를 받은 드리즐을 위해 무엇을 해 줄 수 있을지 알아보겠노라고 막연히 스스로 다짐하는 버릇에 빠져들었다. 온갖 형태의 직무 태만과 사기 행각이 이 불운한 소송에서 사방으로 뿌려졌다. 이런 악행의 가장 바깥 테두리에서 그 역사를 지켜본 사람들조차, 나쁜 일은 나쁜 길로 제멋대로 가게 내버려 두는 헐거운 태도에, 그리고 세상이 잘못 돌아간다면 그것은 어쩌다 그리된 것일 뿐 애초에 바로 돌아가게 되어 있지도 않았다는 헐거운 믿음에, 자기도 모르게 솔깃해지고 말았다.\n이리하여, 진흙 한복판에, 안개의 심장부에, 대법원장이 자신의 대법원에 앉아 있다.\n\n\"탱글 씨.\" 대법원장이 말한다. 요즈음 들어 그는 그 박식한 신사의 달변에 다소 안절부절못하는 기색이다.\n\"각하.\" 탱글 씨가 말한다. 탱글 씨는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에 관해서라면 누구보다 많이 안다. 그는 그것으로 유명한데, 학교를 떠난 뒤로는 다른 것은 아무것도 읽은 적이 없으리라고들 한다.\n\n\"변론을 거의 마쳤소?\"\n\"각하, 아닙니다, 여러 쟁점이, 소생의 본분이라 사료되어 개진하건대, 각하.\" 탱글 씨에게서 미끄러져 나오는 대답이다.\n\n\"법정 변호사 몇 분의 진술이 아직 남은 듯한데, 그렇소?\" 대법원장이 옅은 미소를 띠며 말한다.\n탱글 씨의 박식한 동료 열여덟 명이, 저마다 천팔백 장짜리 요약서로 무장하고서, 피아노 안의 망치 열여덟 개처럼 불쑥 솟구쳐 열여덟 번 절을 하고는, 자기네 열여덟 개의 흐릿한 자리로 도로 주저앉는다.\n\n\"심리는 이 주 뒤 수요일에 속행하겠소.\" 대법원장이 말한다. 지금 다투는 문제란 그저 소송 비용 문제일 뿐이니, 본 소송이라는 큰 나무에 돋은 한낱 봉오리에 지나지 않으며, 언젠가는 정말로 결판이 나기는 할 것이다.\n대법원장이 일어선다. 법정 변호사들이 일어선다. 죄수가 황급히 앞으로 끌려 나온다. 슈롭셔에서 온 사내가 \"각하!\" 하고 외친다. 권표며 가방이며 주머니를 든 자들이 분개하여 정숙을 외치며 슈롭셔에서 온 사내를 노려본다.\n\n\"그 어린 처녀에 관해서는—\" 대법원장이 여전히 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 이어 간다.\n\"황송하오나 각하, 사내아이입니다.\" 탱글 씨가 성급히 끼어든다. \"그 어린 처녀와 사내아이, 그 두 젊은이에 관해서는—\" 대법원장이 한층 또렷하게 이어 가니 탱글 씨는 찍소리도 못 한다. \"오늘 출석하라고 내가 지시했고 지금 내 집무실에 와 있는 그들을, 내가 만나 보고 그들이 백부와 함께 거주하도록 명령을 내리는 것이 타당한지 직접 판단해 보겠소.\"\n\n탱글 씨가 다시 벌떡 일어선다. \"황송하오나 각하, 작고하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그들의—\" 대법원장이 겹안경 너머로 책상 위 서류들을 훑어본다. \"조부와 함께.\"\n\"황송하오나 각하, 경솔한 행동의 희생자가, 머리통이.\"\n\n별안간 아주 자그마한 변호인 하나가 무시무시한 저음으로, 잔뜩 부풀어, 안개의 뒷자리 변두리에서 일어서더니 말한다. \"각하, 한 말씀 올려도 되겠습니까? 제가 그를 변호합니다. 그는 사촌으로, 몇 다리 건너간 사촌입니다. 정확히 몇 다리 건너간 사촌인지는 지금 당장 법정에 아뢸 채비가 되어 있지 않으나, 사촌인 것만은 분명합니다.\"\n\n이 진술을(마치 무덤에서 보내온 전갈처럼 읊조린) 지붕 서까래에 쟁쟁히 울려 놓은 채, 그 아주 자그마한 변호인이 도로 주저앉으니, 안개는 더는 그를 알아보지 못한다. 모두가 그를 찾는다. 아무도 그를 보지 못한다.\n\n\"내가 두 젊은이 모두와 이야기해 보고,\" 대법원장이 다시 말한다. \"그들이 사촌과 함께 거주하는 문제에 관해 직접 판단해 보겠소. 이 일은 내일 아침 착석할 때 다시 거론하겠소.\"\n\n대법원장이 막 법정 변호사들에게 인사하려는데 죄수가 앞에 세워진다. 그 죄수의 뒤죽박죽 사정에서 나올 수 있는 결과라곤 도로 감옥에 보내지는 것뿐이고, 그 일은 이내 처리된다. 슈롭셔에서 온 사내가 또 한 번 항의 섞인 \"각하!\"를 용감히 외쳐 보지만, 대법원장은 그를 진작 알아챈 터라 잽싸게 자취를 감춘 뒤다. 다른 사람들도 모두 재빨리 사라진다. 파란 가방 부대에 묵직한 서류 탄약이 장전되어 사무원들 손에 실려 나간다. 자그마한 미친 노파가 자기 서류를 들고 행군하듯 떠난다. 텅 빈 법정에 자물쇠가 채워진다. 이 법정이 저지른 모든 부정과 야기한 모든 비참함까지 함께 잠가 버리고, 그 전부를 거대한 화장용 장작더미 위에서 불살라 없앨 수만 있다면, 어쩌면 그것은 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 당사자들을 뺀 다른 모든 이에게 그야말로 더없이 좋은 일이겠다!\n이 진창 같은 오후에 우리가 들여다보고 싶은 것은 유행의 세계를 살짝 엿보는 정도다. 그 세계는 대법원과 그리 다르지 않아서, 까마귀가 곧장 날아가듯 한 장면에서 다른 장면으로 건너갈 수 있다. 유행의 세계도 대법원도 둘 다 전례와 관습으로 굴러가는 것들이다. 천둥 치는 궂은 날씨 속에서 기이한 놀이에 빠져 늘어지게 잠든 립 밴 윙클들이며, 어느 날 기사가 깨워줄 잠자는 미녀들이다. 그날이 오면 부엌에서 멈춰 섰던 꼬챙이가 죄다 어마어마하게 돌아가기 시작하리라!\n\n* 립 밴 윙클: 워싱턴 어빙의 단편 주인공으로, 산에서 술을 마시고 이십 년 동안 잠들었다 깨어난 사내.\n크지 않은 세계다. 그 나름의 한계가 있는 우리 세계에 견주어도(전하께서 그 세계를 한 바퀴 돌아 저편 허무의 벼랑에 다다르면 알게 되시리라) 아주 자그마한 티끌에 지나지 않는다. 그 안에도 좋은 것이 많고, 선하고 진실한 사람도 많으며, 정해진 자리가 있다. 그러나 그 세계의 악은, 보석 세공사가 쓰는 솜과 고운 양털에 너무 겹겹이 싸여 더 큰 세계들이 휘몰아치는 소리를 듣지 못하고, 그것들이 태양을 돌며 도는 모습을 보지 못한다는 데 있다. 감각이 마비된 세계요, 공기가 모자라 그 자람이 이따금 병들곤 하는 세계다.\n\n데드록 마님은 파리로 떠나기 며칠 전에 시내 저택으로 돌아와 있다. 마님은 파리에서 몇 주 머물 작정이며, 그 뒤 행보는 아직 정해지지 않았다. 유행 소식통이 파리 사람들을 안심시키려고 그렇게 전하는데, 그 소식통은 유행에 관한 일이라면 죄다 꿰고 있다. 달리 안다는 것은 곧 유행에 뒤떨어진다는 뜻이다. 데드록 마님은 친근한 대화에서 자신의 \"별장\"이라 부르는 링컨셔의 영지에 내려가 있었다. 링컨셔에는 물이 넘쳐흐른다. 정원에 놓인 다리의 아치 하나가 무너져 물에 잠겼다. 너비 반 마일에 이르는 인근 저지대는 고인 강물이 되어, 음울한 나무들이 그 가운데 섬처럼 솟았고, 수면은 종일 떨어지는 빗방울로 온통 구멍이 뚫린다. 데드록 마님의 별장은 더없이 황량하기 그지없다. 여러 날 여러 밤 날씨가 어찌나 축축한지 나무들이 흠뻑 젖은 듯하고, 나무꾼의 도끼에 베이고 잘려나간 가지들이 떨어질 때조차 우지끈 소리 한 번 내지 못한다. 흠뻑 젖은 사슴들은 지나간 자리마다 진창을 남긴다. 소총 한 발의 날카로운 소리도 눅눅한 공기 속에 무뎌지고, 그 연기는 떨어지는 비를 배경으로 잡목이 우거진 푸른 언덕배기 쪽으로 굼뜬 작은 구름이 되어 흘러간다. 데드록 마님의 창에서 내다보이는 풍경은 어떤 때는 납빛이요 어떤 때는 먹빛이다. 앞뜰 돌 테라스에 놓인 화병들은 종일 비를 받고, 묵직한 빗방울이 뚝뚝뚝 옛날부터 유령의 길이라 불려온 너른 포석 위로 밤새도록 떨어진다. 일요일이면 정원 안 작은 교회에서는 곰팡내가 나고, 떡갈나무 설교단에서 식은땀이 배어 나오며, 무덤 속에 누운 옛 데드록 가문 사람들 같은 냄새와 맛이 사방에 감돈다. 자식이 없는 데드록 마님은 이른 황혼 녘 규방에서 사냥터지기 오두막을 내다보다가, 격자창에 어린 불빛과 굴뚝에서 피어오르는 연기, 그리고 한 여인에게 쫓기는 아이가 빗속으로 달려나가 대문을 들어서는, 옷을 잔뜩 껴입은 사내의 환한 모습을 맞이하는 광경을 보고는 잔뜩 심사가 뒤틀려버렸다. 데드록 마님은 자신이 \"지루해 죽을 뻔했다\"고 말한다.\n\n그리하여 데드록 마님은 링컨셔의 별장을 떠나 비와 까마귀와 토끼와 사슴과 자고새와 꿩에게 그곳을 맡겨버렸다. 가정부가 옛 방들을 차례로 지나며 덧문을 닫는 동안, 가고 없는 옛 데드록 가문 사람들의 초상화는 그저 풀이 죽은 채 축축한 벽 속으로 사라져가는 듯했다. 그 초상화들이 다음번 언제 다시 모습을 드러낼지는, 악마처럼 과거와 현재는 죄다 알아도 미래만은 알지 못하는 유행 소식통도 아직은 장담하지 못한다.\n\n레스터 데드록 경은 한낱 준남작에 지나지 않으나, 그보다 더 위세 당당한 준남작은 없다. 그의 가문은 언덕만큼이나 오래되었고, 그보다 한없이 더 존귀하다. 그는 세상이 언덕 없이도 그럭저럭 굴러갈 수는 있어도 데드록 가문 없이는 끝장이 나리라는 지론을 품고 있다. 그는 자연이라는 것이 대체로 괜찮은 발상이라고(정원 울타리로 둘러싸지 않았을 때는 좀 천박할지 몰라도) 인정은 하지만, 그 발상이 실현되려면 결국 자네 같은 대단한 지방 명문가들에 기대야 한다고 여긴다. 그는 양심이 엄격한 신사로, 온갖 옹졸함과 비루함을 경멸하며, 제 고결함에 티끌만 한 흠집이라도 잡힐 빌미를 줄 바에는 당신이 어떤 죽음을 들먹이든 단 한 마디 통보만으로 기꺼이 그 죽음을 맞을 각오가 되어 있다. 그는 명예롭고, 고집스럽고, 정직하고, 기개가 드높으며, 지독하게 편견에 찌들었고, 더없이 비합리적인 사내다.\n레스터 경은 마님보다 꼬박 스무 살 위다. 그는 예순다섯을 다시는 보지 못할 것이요, 어쩌면 예순여섯도, 예순일곱도 그러할 것이다. 이따금 통풍이 도져서 걸음걸이가 조금 뻣뻣하다. 옅은 잿빛 머리칼과 구레나룻, 고운 셔츠 주름 장식, 새하얀 조끼, 그리고 늘 단단히 채워둔 번쩍이는 단추가 달린 푸른 외투를 갖춘 그는 풍채가 그럴듯하다. 그는 격식을 차리고 위엄이 있으며, 어떤 자리에서든 마님에게 더없이 정중하고, 마님의 자태를 무엇보다 높이 친다. 구애하던 시절 이래 한 번도 변치 않은 마님을 향한 그의 헌신은, 그에게 남은 단 하나의 낭만적 정취다.\n\n사실 그는 사랑해서 마님과 결혼했다. 마님에게는 이렇다 할 가문조차 없었다는 수군거림이 아직도 떠돈다. 그러나 레스터 경은 가문이 차고 넘쳐서 어쩌면 더는 필요 없을 만큼, 얼마든지 그것을 양보할 수 있었던 것이다. 그 대신 마님에게는 미모와 자존심과 야망, 오만한 결단, 그리고 명문가 귀부인 한 무리에게 나눠주고도 남을 만큼의 분별이 있었다. 여기에 재산과 지위가 더해지자 마님은 이내 떠올라, 이제 여러 해째 유행 소식통의 한복판이자 유행이라는 나무의 꼭대기에 자리해왔다.\n\n정복할 세계가 더는 없자 알렉산드로스가 어떻게 울었는지는 누구나 안다. 아니, 이쯤 되면 다들 알 만한 까닭이 있는데, 그 일이 꽤 자주 입에 오르내려온 탓이다. 데드록 마님은 자신의 세계를 정복하고도 녹아내리는 기분이 아니라 차라리 얼어붙는 기분에 잠겨들었다. 기진맥진한 평정, 닳고 닳은 차분함, 흥미나 만족 따위로는 흐트러지지 않는 권태로운 평온이 그녀가 거둔 승리의 전리품이다. 그녀는 더없이 잘 자란 사람이다. 내일 당장 천국으로 옮겨진다 해도, 그녀는 황홀경 하나 없이 올라가리라 짐작할 만하다.\n\n마님에게는 여전히 미모가 있고, 그 절정은 지났을지 몰라도 아직 가을에 접어들지는 않았다. 그녀는 고운 얼굴을 지녔는데, 본디 잘생겼다기보다는 아주 예쁘다고 할 만한 인상이었으나, 유행하는 신분에서 몸에 밴 표정 덕에 고전적인 기품으로 다듬어졌다. 자태는 우아하고 키가 커 보이는 효과를 낸다. 실제로 큰 것은 아니고, 명예로운 밥 스테이블스가 여러 번 단언했다시피 \"그 모든 점이 한껏 살려진\" 까닭이다. 같은 권위자는 그녀가 빈틈없이 차려입었다고 평하며, 특히 머리 모양을 두고 온 마구간을 통틀어 가장 잘 손질된 암말이라고 칭찬한다.\n\n이 모든 완벽함을 머리에 인 채 데드록 마님은 (바짝 뒤쫓는 유행 소식통과 함께) 링컨셔의 별장에서 올라와 파리로 떠나기 전 시내 저택에서 며칠을 보낸다. 마님은 파리에서 몇 주 머물 작정이며, 그 뒤 행보는 아직 정해지지 않았다. 그런데 이 질척하고 음침한 오후, 마님의 시내 저택에 한 구식 노신사가 찾아드는데, 그는 변호사이자 대법원 사무 변호사로, 데드록 가문의 법률 고문을 맡는 영예를 누리고 있다. 그의 사무실에는 그 가문의 이름이 겉에 적힌 무쇠 상자가 어찌나 많은지, 마치 지금의 준남작이 요술쟁이의 동전이라도 되어 그 상자들 사이를 끊임없이 옮겨 다니며 묘기에 부려지는 것만 같다. 노신사는 분을 바른 머큐리의 안내를 받아, 현관을 가로질러 계단을 오르고 복도를 지나, 사교철에는 더없이 휘황찬란하지만 철 지나면 더없이 음산한 방들을 가로질러 마님 앞으로 인도된다. 잠깐 구경하기에는 요정 나라요, 살기에는 사막인 방들이다.\n\n* 머큐리: 로마 신화의 전령신. 여기서는 심부름과 안내를 맡은 하인을 빗댄 표현.\n노신사는 보기에 녹슨 듯 후줄근하지만, 귀족들의 혼인 재산 계약이며 유언장을 다루어 짭짤하게 재산을 모았고 무척 부유하다는 평판이 자자하다. 그를 둘러싸고는 가문의 비밀이라는 신비로운 후광이 감도는데, 그가 그 비밀을 말없이 간직하는 보관소라는 사실은 익히 알려져 있다. 정원의 외진 숲속, 자라나는 재목과 고사리 사이에 수백 년째 뿌리내린 고귀한 능묘들이 있지만, 그 안에 담긴 고귀한 비밀이 차라리 토킹혼 씨의 가슴속에 갇혀 세상을 돌아다니는 비밀보다 적을지도 모른다. 그는 이른바 옛 유파에 속하는 사람인데, 이 말은 대개 한 번도 젊었던 적이 없어 보이는 온갖 유파를 일컫는다. 그는 리본으로 묶은 반바지에 각반이나 긴 양말을 갖춰 입는다. 그의 검은 옷과 검은 양말은, 그것이 비단이든 모직이든, 결코 윤이 나지 않는다는 한 가지 특징이 있다. 말이 없고, 속을 닫아걸었으며, 어떤 스쳐 가는 빛에도 응답하지 않는 그의 옷차림은 꼭 그 사람을 닮았다. 그는 직무상 자문을 구하지 않으면 결코 입을 열지 않는다. 그는 이따금, 말 한마디 없으면서도 더없이 제집처럼 편안한 모습으로, 시골 대저택의 만찬 식탁 구석이나 응접실 문가에 자리해 있곤 하는데, 그런 응접실을 두고 유행 소식통은 입에 침이 마르도록 떠들어댄다. 그곳에서는 누구나 그를 알아보고, 귀족 명부에 오른 사람의 절반이 걸음을 멈추고 \"안녕하시오, 토킹혼 씨?\" 하고 인사를 건넨다. 그는 그 인사들을 진중하게 받아들여, 다른 모든 지식과 함께 깊이 묻어둔다.\n\n레스터 데드록 경은 마님과 함께 있다가 토킹혼 씨를 보고 반가워한다. 그에게는 오랜 관례가 몸에 밴 분위기가 있어, 레스터 경은 늘 그 점을 흡족하게 여기며 일종의 공물로 받아들인다. 그는 토킹혼 씨의 옷차림이 마음에 드는데, 거기에도 일종의 공물이 깃들어 있다. 더없이 점잖으면서, 또한 대체로 가신다운 분위기를 풍긴다. 말하자면 데드록 가문의 법률 비밀을 맡은 집사요, 법률 술 곳간을 지키는 시종 같은 인상을 자아낸다.\n토킹혼 씨 자신은 이런 것을 짐작이나 할까? 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있다. 그러나 한 계급의 일원으로서, 자신의 작은 세계를 이끄는 우두머리이자 대표 가운데 하나로서, 데드록 마님과 관련된 온갖 것에는 주목할 만한 한 가지 사정이 있다. 그녀는 자신을 보통 인간의 손길과 분별이 도무지 닿지 못하는 헤아릴 수 없는 존재라 여긴다. 거울에 비친 자신을 보면 과연 그렇게 보이기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀를 둘러싸고 도는 희미한 작은 별들은, 하녀에서부터 이탈리아 오페라 극장의 지배인에 이르기까지, 하나같이 그녀의 약점과 편견과 어리석음과 오만함과 변덕을 꿰뚫고 있으며, 마치 양재사가 그녀의 몸 치수를 재듯 그녀의 됨됨이를 정확히 계산하고 곱게 가늠하며 살아간다. 새 옷이든, 새 관습이든, 새 가수든, 새 무용수든, 새 보석 양식이든, 새 난쟁이나 거인이든, 새 예배당이든, 그 무엇이든 하나 내세우려는가? 데드록 마님은 그저 자기 앞에 엎드릴 뿐이라 여기는 사람들이 열두 직종에 두루 있는데, 그 굽실대는 자들은 마님을 갓난아기 다루듯 주무르는 법을 일러줄 수 있고, 평생을 오로지 마님 시중드는 일로만 보내며, 한껏 겸손한 척 깊이 머리를 조아리며 따르는 시늉을 하면서 실은 마님과 그 무리 전체를 제 뒤로 줄줄이 끌고 다닌다. 하나를 낚으면 죄다 낚아채어, 레뮤얼 걸리버가 위풍당당한 릴리퍼트의 함대를 통째로 끌고 갔듯 몽땅 채어 가는 것이다. \"저희 손님들께 물건을 권하시려거든, 선생,\" 보석상 블레이즈와 스파클은 이렇게 말한다. 저희 손님이란 데드록 마님과 그 부류를 뜻한다. \"일반 대중을 상대하는 게 아니라는 걸 명심하셔야 합니다. 저희 손님들의 가장 약한 데를 찔러야 하는데, 그 약한 데가 바로 이런 곳이지요.\" \"이 물건을 잘 팔리게 하려면, 여러분,\" 포목상 신과 글로스는 제조업자 친구들에게 이렇게 말한다. \"저희한테 오셔야 합니다. 유행하는 분들을 어디서 잡아야 할지 저희가 알고, 저희가 그걸 유행으로 만들 수 있으니까요.\" \"이 책을 제 고귀한 단골들 탁자에 올리고 싶으시거든, 선생,\" 도서 대여업자 슬래더리 씨는 이렇게 말한다. \"아니면 이 난쟁이나 거인을 제 고귀한 단골들 댁에 들여보내고 싶으시거든, 선생, 또는 이 공연에 제 고귀한 단골들의 후원을 받게 하고 싶으시거든, 선생, 부디 그 일은 저한테 맡기십시오. 저는 제 고귀한 단골들의 우두머리를 연구하는 데 이골이 났으니, 자랑이 아니라 사실대로 말씀드리건대 그분들을 제 손가락 끝에서 마음대로 돌릴 수 있습니다.\" 정직한 사내인 슬래더리 씨의 이 말에는 조금도 과장이 없다.\n\n그러니 토킹혼 씨가 지금 데드록 마님의 마음속을 스쳐 가는 것이 무엇인지 모를 수도 있겠으나, 어쩌면 알고 있을 가능성도 다분하다.\n\"마님의 소송 건이 또 대법관 앞에 올랐다지요, 토킹혼 씨?\" 레스터 경이 손을 내밀며 말한다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 오늘 또 다루어졌지요.\" 토킹혼 씨가 대답하며, 난롯가 소파에 앉아 손부채로 얼굴을 가리고 있는 마님에게 늘 하던 대로 조용히 고개를 숙인다.\n\"무슨 진전이라도 있었느냐고 물어봐야 부질없겠지요.\" 마님이 링컨셔 별장의 그 황량함을 아직 떨치지 못한 채 말한다.\n\n\"마님께서 진전이라 할 만한 일은 오늘 아무것도 없었습니다.\" 토킹혼 씨가 대답한다.\n\"앞으로도 영영 없겠지요.\" 마님이 말한다.\n\n레스터 경은 끝도 없이 늘어지는 대법원 소송에 아무런 불만이 없다. 그것은 느리고, 돈이 많이 들고, 영국적이며, 헌정 질서에 어울리는 그런 부류의 일이다. 물론 그가 문제의 소송에 절실한 이해관계가 있는 것은 아니다. 마님이 그에게 가져온 유일한 재산이 바로 그 소송의 지분이었으니 말이다. 게다가 그는 자기 이름, 곧 데드록이라는 이름이 어느 소송에 끼여 있으면서 정작 그 소송의 명칭에는 오르지 못한다는 것이 더없이 우스꽝스러운 사고라는 어렴풋한 느낌을 품고 있다. 그러나 그는 대법원이, 설령 이따금 정의를 지연시키고 약간의 혼란을 빚는다 하더라도, 인간의 지혜가 다다른 완벽함이 다른 온갖 것들과 더불어 고안해낸 것이라, (인간의 처지에서 말하자면) 만사를 영원히 매듭짓기 위한 무언가라고 여긴다. 그리고 대체로 그는, 대법원을 두고 무슨 불평이든 거기에 자기 얼굴의 인준을 보태는 것은 곧 하층민 가운데 누군가가 어딘가에서 와트 타일러처럼 들고일어나도록 부추기는 일이라는 확고한 지론을 품고 있다.\n* 와트 타일러: 1381년 잉글랜드 농민 봉기를 이끈 지도자.\n\n\"새로운 진술서가 몇 장 서류철에 더해진 데다,\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"그것이 짧고, 또 저는 소송에 새로운 진행이 생기면 송구하게도 의뢰인께 알려드린다는 번거로운 원칙을 따르는 데다\"—토킹혼 씨는 조심스러운 사람이라, 꼭 필요한 만큼만 책임을 진다—\"게다가 마님께서 파리로 가신다기에, 제가 주머니에 넣어 가져왔습니다.\"\n(그런데 레스터 경도 파리로 갈 참이었지만, 유행 소식통의 관심은 온통 마님에게 쏠려 있었다.)\n\n토킹혼 씨가 서류를 꺼내 마님 팔꿈치 곁의 황금 부적 같은 탁자 위에 올려놓아도 되겠느냐고 청한 뒤, 안경을 쓰고 갓을 씌운 등불 빛에 의지해 읽기 시작한다.\n\"'대법원에서. 존 잔다이스와—'\"\n\n마님이 말을 가로막으며, 형식적인 끔찍한 대목은 되도록 많이 건너뛰어 달라고 청한다.\n토킹혼 씨가 안경 너머로 흘끗 보더니, 한참 아랫부분부터 다시 읽기 시작한다. 마님은 무심하고 시큰둥하게 정신을 딴 데로 돌린다. 레스터 경은 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 서류철을 바라보며, 법률 특유의 되풀이와 장황함을 나라의 보루에 드는 것으로 여겨 위엄 있게 흐뭇해하는 듯하다. 마침 마님이 앉은 자리는 불기운이 뜨거운데, 손부채는 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼 귀하나 작아서 쓸모보다 아름다움이 앞선다. 마님이 자세를 바꾸다가 탁자 위의 서류를 본다. 조금 더 가까이 본다. 더 가까이 들여다본다. 그러고는 충동적으로 묻는다. \"이건 누가 베껴 쓴 거지요?\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 마님의 활기와 평소답지 않은 어조에 놀라 말을 뚝 멈춘다.\n\"당신네가 법률체라고 부르는 글씨인가요?\" 마님이 다시 무심한 태도로 그를 정면으로 바라보며, 손부채를 만지작거리며 묻는다.\n\n\"꼭 그렇진 않습니다. 아마도\"—토킹혼 씨가 말하면서 그것을 살핀다—\"본래 글씨체가 잡힌 뒤에 법률체의 특징을 익힌 것 같군요. 왜 물으십니까?\"\n\"이 지긋지긋한 단조로움을 깨뜨릴 거라면 뭐든 좋아서요. 아, 어서 읽어요!\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 다시 읽는다. 열기는 한층 더해진다. 마님은 부채로 얼굴을 가린다. 레스터 경이 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가 별안간 화들짝 깨어 외친다. \"응? 뭐라고 했소?\"\n\"마님께서 편찮으신 듯하여 걱정입니다.\" 황급히 일어선 토킹혼 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"현기증이에요.\" 마님이 핏기 없는 입술로 중얼거린다. \"그뿐이지만, 마치 죽음 같은 아찔함이군요. 말 걸지 마세요. 종을 울려서, 나를 방으로 데려다줘요!\"\n토킹혼 씨가 다른 방으로 물러난다. 종이 울리고, 발소리가 이리저리 부산하게 오가더니, 이윽고 정적이 내려앉는다. 마침내 머큐리가 토킹혼 씨에게 돌아와 달라고 청한다.\n\n\"이제 좀 나아졌소.\" 레스터 경이 변호사에게 앉으라고 손짓하며, 자기 한 사람에게만 읽어달라고 이른다. \"내가 어찌나 놀랐는지. 마님이 정신을 잃는 건 처음 봤소. 허나 날씨가 워낙 사람을 못살게 구는 데다, 링컨셔 우리 별장에서 정말이지 지루해 죽을 뻔했으니 그럴 만도 하지.\"\n이 책에서 내가 맡은 부분을 쓰기 시작하려니 여간 어려운 게 아닙니다. 나는 영리하지 못한 사람임을 알기 때문입니다. 그건 언제나 알고 있었습니다. 아주 어린 소녀였을 때, 나는 인형과 단둘이 있으면 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"있잖아, 인형아, 나는 영리하지 못해. 너도 잘 알지? 그러니까 착한 아이처럼 나를 너그럽게 참아주렴!\" 그러면 인형은 커다란 안락의자에 기대어 앉아, 그 고운 살결과 발그레한 입술로 나를 빤히 바라보았습니다. 아니, 나를 본다기보다는 허공을 보는 것 같았는데, 그동안 나는 부지런히 바느질하며 내 비밀을 하나도 빠짐없이 인형에게 털어놓았습니다.\n\n사랑하는 내 옛 인형! 나는 어찌나 수줍음이 많은 아이였던지, 다른 누구에게도 좀처럼 입을 열 엄두를 내지 못했고, 마음을 열 엄두는 더더욱 내지 못했습니다. 학교에서 집으로 돌아온 날, 내 방으로 한걸음에 뛰어 올라가 \"아, 사랑스럽고 믿음직한 인형아, 네가 나를 기다리고 있을 줄 알았어!\" 하고 말한 뒤, 인형의 커다란 의자 팔걸이에 기대어 바닥에 앉아, 헤어진 뒤로 내가 본 것을 죄다 들려주던 그 시간이 내게 얼마나 큰 위안이었는지 떠올리면 눈물이 날 지경입니다. 나는 늘 무언가를 눈여겨보는 편이었습니다. 빠릿빠릿한 게 아니라, 오, 천만에요! 그저 눈앞을 스쳐 가는 것을 말없이 지켜보며 그걸 더 잘 이해하면 좋겠다고 생각하는 식이었습니다. 나는 결코 이해가 빠른 사람이 아닙니다. 그래도 어떤 사람을 진심으로 다정하게 사랑할 때면 이해력이 환해지는 듯합니다. 하지만 그것마저 내 허영일지도 모릅니다.\n내가 기억하는 가장 어린 시절부터, 나는 마치 동화 속 공주들처럼 대모님 손에 자랐습니다. 다만 나는 사랑스럽지 않았다는 점만 달랐지요. 적어도 나는 그분을 대모로만 알고 있었습니다. 정말, 정말 좋은 분이셨습니다! 일요일이면 세 번씩 교회에 가셨고, 수요일과 금요일에는 아침 기도회에, 설교가 있을 때면 언제든 설교를 들으러 가셨으며, 한 번도 거르지 않으셨습니다. 미인이셨고, 한 번이라도 미소 지으셨다면 (나는 늘 그렇게 생각했습니다) 천사 같으셨을 텐데, 그분은 결코 미소 짓지 않으셨습니다. 늘 엄숙하고 엄격하셨습니다. 그분 자신이 어찌나 선하신지, 다른 사람들의 악함 때문에 평생 미간을 찌푸리고 사신 거라고 나는 생각했습니다. 아이와 어른 사이의 차이를 아무리 너그럽게 감안해도, 나는 그분과 너무나 다르다고 느꼈습니다. 나는 너무나 보잘것없고, 너무나 하찮으며, 너무나 멀리 떨어진 존재 같아서 그분 앞에서는 도무지 마음을 놓을 수 없었습니다. 아니, 바라는 만큼 그분을 사랑할 수조차 없었습니다. 그분이 얼마나 선하신지, 내가 얼마나 그분께 어울리지 않는 존재인지를 생각하면 너무나 슬퍼서, 나는 내게 더 나은 마음씨가 깃들기를 간절히 바라곤 했습니다. 그 이야기를 사랑하는 옛 인형에게 자주자주 털어놓았지만, 나는 마땅히 사랑했어야 할 만큼, 더 나은 아이였다면 분명 사랑했을 만큼 대모님을 사랑하지 못했습니다.\n\n이런 까닭에 나는 타고난 천성보다 더 소심하고 내성적인 아이가 되었던 듯하고, 마음 편히 지낼 수 있는 유일한 친구인 인형에게 더 매달리게 되었습니다. 그런데 아직 아주 어렸을 적, 그런 성향을 한층 더 부추긴 일이 하나 일어났습니다.\n나는 우리 엄마에 관한 이야기를 들어본 적이 없었습니다. 우리 아빠에 관해서도 들어본 적이 없었지만, 엄마 쪽이 더 궁금했습니다. 내 기억으로 나는 검은 상복을 입어본 적이 없었습니다. 엄마 무덤을 본 적도 없었습니다. 그게 어디 있는지 들은 적도 없었습니다. 그런데도 나는 대모님 말고 다른 어떤 친척을 위해 기도하라고 배운 적이 없었습니다. 내가 잠자리에 들면 불을 꺼주던, 우리 집의 유일한 하녀인 레이철 부인에게 나는 마음속 이 문제를 한두 번 꺼낸 적이 있었습니다. 그분도 무척 좋은 분이었지만 내게는 엄격했는데, 그분은 그저 \"에스더, 잘 자거라!\" 하고는 자리를 떠나 나를 혼자 두고 가버렸습니다.\n\n내가 통학생으로 다니던 이웃 학교에는 여자아이가 일곱 명 있었고, 그 애들은 나를 꼬마 에스더 서머슨이라고 불렀지만, 나는 그 애들 가운데 누구와도 집에서 어울리지 못했습니다. 물론 그 애들은 모두 나보다 나이가 많았고(나는 거기서 한참 어린 막내였습니다), 게다가 나보다 훨씬 영리하고 아는 것도 훨씬 많았지만, 그 차이 말고도 우리 사이에는 무언가 또 다른 거리가 있는 것 같았습니다. 그중 한 아이가 내가 학교에 다니기 시작한 첫 주에(아주 또렷이 기억합니다) 자기 집에서 여는 작은 모임에 나를 초대했는데, 나는 무척 기뻤습니다. 하지만 대모님이 나를 대신해 정중히 거절하는 딱딱한 편지를 써 보내셨고, 나는 끝내 가지 못했습니다. 나는 도무지 어디에도 나가지 않았습니다.\n내 생일이었습니다. 다른 아이들의 생일에는 학교에 휴일이 있었지만, 내 생일에는 없었습니다. 다른 아이들의 생일에는 집에서 잔치가 벌어졌다고, 아이들이 서로 주고받는 이야기를 들어 알고 있었습니다. 하지만 내 생일에는 그런 게 없었습니다. 내 생일은 일 년 중 집에서 가장 우울한 날이었습니다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 내 허영이 나를 속이지 않는 한(나도 모르게 무척 허영심이 많을 수 있다는 건 알지만, 정말이지 그렇지는 않다고 믿습니다), 내 이해력은 애정이 깊어질 때 빨라집니다. 나는 천성이 무척 정이 많은 사람이라, 그 생일에 받았던 것 같은 상처를 한 번 이상 그렇게 빠르게 받을 수 있다면 지금도 그런 아픔을 느낄지 모릅니다.\n저녁 식사가 끝나고, 대모님과 나는 난롯가 식탁에 앉아 있었습니다. 시계가 똑딱거리고, 불꽃이 탁탁 튀었습니다. 그밖에 다른 소리는 얼마나 오래도록인지 모르게 방 안에서도 집 안에서도 들리지 않았습니다. 나는 바느질하다 우연히 식탁 너머 대모님 쪽으로 머뭇머뭇 눈을 들었는데, 음울하게 나를 바라보는 그 얼굴에서 이런 말을 읽었습니다. \"꼬마 에스더, 너에게 생일 따위 없었더라면, 네가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면 훨씬 나았을 게다!\"\n\n나는 울음을 터뜨리며 흐느꼈고, 이렇게 말했습니다. \"오, 대모님, 말씀해 주세요, 제발 말씀해 주세요. 제 생일에 엄마가 돌아가셨나요?\"\n\"아니다.\" 대모님이 대답했습니다. \"더는 묻지 마라, 얘야!\"\n\n\"오, 제발 엄마 이야기를 조금이라도 해주세요. 이제라도, 대모님, 부디 그래 주세요! 제가 엄마한테 무슨 짓을 했나요? 어쩌다 엄마를 잃었나요? 왜 저는 다른 아이들과 이렇게 다르고, 또 왜 그게 제 잘못인가요, 대모님? 아니에요, 아니에요, 가지 마세요. 오, 제게 말씀해 주세요!\"\n나는 슬픔을 넘어선 어떤 두려움에 사로잡혀, 대모님의 옷자락을 붙잡고 그분 앞에 무릎을 꿇었습니다. 그러는 내내 대모님은 \"놓아라!\" 하고 말하던 참이었습니다. 하지만 이제 그분은 우뚝 멈춰 섰습니다.\n\n어두워진 그분의 얼굴에는 어찌나 큰 힘이 서려 있던지, 격해진 내 마음을 한복판에서 멈춰 세웠습니다. 나는 떨리는 작은 손을 들어 그분 손을 잡거나 있는 힘껏 간절히 용서를 빌려고 했지만, 그분이 나를 바라보자 손을 거두어 두근거리는 가슴에 갖다 댔습니다. 그분은 나를 일으켜 세우고 의자에 앉은 채 나를 자기 앞에 세워둔 뒤, 차갑고 낮은 목소리로 천천히 말했습니다. 찌푸린 미간과 곧추세운 손가락이 지금도 눈에 선합니다. \"에스더, 네 어머니는 네 수치이고, 너는 네 어머니의 수치였다. 머지않아, 충분히 빠른 때에, 너는 이걸 더 잘 깨닫게 되고, 또 여자만이 느낄 수 있는 방식으로 느끼게 될 게다. 나는 그 사람을 용서했다.\" 하지만 그분의 얼굴은 누그러지지 않았습니다. \"그 사람이 내게 저지른 잘못을 말이다. 그 잘못이 네가 영영 알 수 없을 만큼, 고통받은 나 말고는 그 누구도 영영 알 수 없을 만큼 컸지만, 나는 더는 그 일을 입에 담지 않으마. 너 자신으로 말하자면, 불행한 아이야, 이 사악한 기념일의 첫날부터 고아가 되고 천하게 떨어진 너는, 기록된 말씀대로 다른 이들의 죄가 네 머리 위에 임하지 않도록 날마다 기도하여라. 네 어머니를 잊고, 그 불행한 자식에게 가장 큰 친절을 베풀 사람들, 곧 네 어머니를 잊어줄 모든 이에게 그 사람을 잊도록 내버려 두어라. 자, 가거라!\"\n그런데 그분은 내가 막 떠나려 할 때, 나는 그렇게나 얼어붙어 있었는데, 나를 멈춰 세우고 이 말을 덧붙였습니다. \"순종, 자기 부정, 부지런한 노동, 이것이 그런 그림자를 지고 시작된 삶을 위한 준비란다. 에스더, 너는 다른 아이들과 다르다. 너는 그 애들처럼 흔한 죄와 진노 가운데 태어나지 않았기 때문이다. 너는 따로 떼어 구별된 아이란다.\"\n\n나는 내 방으로 올라가 살그머니 잠자리에 들어, 눈물에 젖은 인형의 뺨을 내 뺨에 갖다 댔고, 그 외로운 친구를 가슴에 꼭 안은 채 울다가 잠이 들었습니다. 내 슬픔을 온전히 이해하지는 못했지만, 나는 그동안 단 한 번도 누군가의 마음에 기쁨을 가져다준 적이 없으며, 인형이 내게 그런 존재인 것처럼 이 세상 누구에게도 그런 존재가 되어주지 못한다는 것만은 알았습니다.\n\n아아, 그 뒤로 우리가 단둘이 얼마나 많은 시간을 함께 보냈는지, 내가 인형에게 내 생일 이야기를 얼마나 자주 되풀이했는지, 그리고 내가 타고난 잘못을(나는 분명 죄가 있으면서도 동시에 죄가 없다고 느꼈습니다) 있는 힘껏 바로잡으려 애쓰겠노라고, 자라면서 부지런하고 만족할 줄 알며 마음씨 고운 사람이 되어 누군가에게 작은 선을 베풀고 할 수만 있다면 내게도 사랑을 얻어내겠노라고 인형에게 얼마나 털어놓았는지를 떠올리면. 그 일을 생각하며 이렇게 눈물 흘리는 것이 자기 위안이 아니기를 바랍니다. 나는 무척 감사하고, 무척 즐겁지만, 눈가에 차오르는 눈물을 어쩔 수 없습니다.\n자! 이제 눈물을 다 닦았으니 다시 제대로 이어갈 수 있습니다.\n\n그 생일 이후 나는 대모님과 나 사이의 거리를 한층 더 절감했고, 비어 있어야 마땅했을 자리를 그분 집에서 차지하고 있다는 사실을 너무나 또렷이 의식해서, 마음속으로는 그분께 뜨겁게 감사하면서도 그분께 다가가기가 그 어느 때보다 더 어렵게 느껴졌습니다. 학교 친구들에게도 같은 마음이었고, 과부인 레이철 부인에게도 같은 마음이었습니다. 그리고 아, 그분이 자랑스러워하던, 보름에 한 번씩 그분을 보러 오던 그 딸에게도 그랬습니다! 나는 무척 내성적이고 조용했으며, 무척 부지런해지려고 애썼습니다.\n어느 화창한 오후, 책과 화첩을 들고 학교에서 돌아와 옆으로 길게 드리운 내 그림자를 바라보며 늘 하던 대로 내 방으로 슬그머니 올라가던 참에, 대모님이 응접실 문밖으로 내다보며 나를 불러 세웠습니다. 그분 곁에는, 정말이지 매우 드문 일이었는데, 낯선 사람이 앉아 있었습니다. 풍채 좋고 위엄 있어 보이는 신사로, 온통 검은 옷차림에 흰 넥타이를 매고, 커다란 금시계 줄에 인장이 여럿 달려 있었으며, 금테 안경을 쓰고, 새끼손가락에는 커다란 인장 반지를 끼고 있었습니다.\n\n\"이 아이가.\" 대모님이 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다. \"그 아이입니다.\" 그러고는 본디 엄한 말투로 말했습니다. \"이 애가 에스더입니다, 선생님.\"\n\n신사는 안경을 들어 올려 나를 보더니 말했습니다. \"이리 오너라, 얘야!\" 그는 나와 악수하고는 보닛을 벗어보라고 했는데, 그러는 내내 나를 바라보았습니다. 내가 시키는 대로 하자 그는 \"아!\" 했고, 이어서 \"그래!\" 했습니다. 그러더니 안경을 벗어 빨간 안경집에 접어 넣고는 안락의자에 등을 기대고 두 손으로 안경집을 빙빙 돌리면서 대모님에게 고개를 까딱였습니다. 그러자 대모님이 말했습니다. \"위층으로 올라가도 좋다, 에스더!\" 나는 그에게 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 자리를 떴습니다.\n그로부터 이 년쯤 지나 내가 거의 열네 살이 되었을 무렵, 어느 끔찍한 밤에 대모님과 나는 난롯가에 앉아 있었습니다. 나는 소리 내어 책을 읽었고 그분은 듣고 있었습니다. 나는 늘 그러듯 아홉 시에 내려와 그분께 성경을 읽어드리던 참이었는데, 마침 요한복음에서 사람들이 죄지은 여인을 우리 구주께 끌고 왔을 때, 구주께서 몸을 굽혀 손가락으로 흙바닥에 글을 쓰셨다는 대목을 읽고 있었습니다.\n\n\"그들이 계속 묻거늘, 예수께서 일어나 그들에게 이르시되 '너희 중에 죄 없는 자가 먼저 그 여자에게 돌을 던지라!'\"\n나는 읽기를 멈췄습니다. 대모님이 자리에서 일어나 손을 머리에 갖다 대더니, 책의 전혀 다른 대목을 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤기 때문입니다. \"'그러므로 너희는 깨어 있으라. 그가 홀연히 와서 너희가 자는 것을 보지 않게 하라. 내가 너희에게 하는 이 말을 모든 사람에게 하노니, 깨어 있으라!'\"\n\n그 말을 되뇌며 내 앞에 서 있던 그분이, 순식간에 바닥에 쓰러졌습니다. 내가 소리칠 필요도 없었습니다. 그분의 목소리는 온 집 안에 울려 퍼졌고 거리에서까지 들렸으니까요.\n그분은 침대에 뉘어졌습니다. 일주일이 넘도록 그분은 거기 누워 있었는데, 겉모습은 거의 그대로였고, 내가 그토록 잘 알던 그 옛 미인의 단호하게 찌푸린 표정이 얼굴에 새겨져 있었습니다. 낮에도 밤에도 몇 번이고, 나는 내 속삭임이 그분께 더 또렷이 들리도록 그분 곁 베개에 머리를 대고, 그분께 입 맞추고, 감사하고, 그분을 위해 기도하고, 축복과 용서를 빌고, 나를 알아보거나 들었다는 작은 표시라도 해달라고 간청했습니다. 아니, 아니, 아니었습니다. 그분의 얼굴은 미동도 없었습니다. 마지막 순간까지, 아니 그 뒤에도, 찌푸린 표정은 끝내 풀리지 않았습니다.\n\n가엾고 선하신 내 대모님이 묻힌 다음 날, 흰 목도리를 두른 검은 옷의 그 신사가 다시 나타났습니다. 레이철 부인이 나를 부르러 보냈고, 나는 그가 전과 똑같은 자리에, 마치 한 번도 떠난 적이 없었던 듯 앉아 있는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n\"내 이름은 켄지란다.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"기억할지도 모르겠구나, 얘야. 링컨스 인의 켄지 앤드 카보이일세.\"\n\n나는 전에 한 번 그를 뵌 기억이 난다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"자, 앉으렴, 내 곁에 가까이. 너무 상심하지 마라. 그래봐야 소용없으니. 레이철 부인, 작고하신 바버리 양의 사정을 잘 아셨으니 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없겠지만, 그분의 재산은 그분과 함께 소멸하였고, 이제 이모가 세상을 떠난 이 아가씨는—\"\n\n\"제 이모라고요, 선생님!\"\n\n\"아무 소득도 없는 마당에 거짓을 이어가봐야 정말 소용없는 일이지.\" 켄지 씨가 부드럽게 말했습니다. \"법적으로는 아니지만, 사실상 이모란다. 너무 상심하지 마라! 울지 말고! 떨지도 말고! 레이철 부인, 우리 어린 친구는 분명—그—저—잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에 대해 들어본 적이 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"전혀요.\" 레이철 부인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그럴 수가 있나요.\" 켄지 씨가 안경을 들어 올리며 말을 이었습니다. \"우리 어린 친구가—제발 상심하지 말라니까!—잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 한 번도 들어본 적이 없다니!\"\n\n나는 그게 대체 무엇인지 의아해하며 고개를 가로저었습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 모른다고?\" 켄지 씨가 안경 너머로 나를 바라보며, 마치 무언가를 어르듯 안경집을 부드럽게 빙빙 돌리면서 말했습니다. \"알려진 가장 위대한 대법원 소송 가운데 하나를 모른다고? 그 자체로 대법원 관행의 기념비인—그—저—잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 말이다. 그 안에는 (말하자면) 그 법정에 알려진 온갖 난제, 온갖 우연한 사정, 온갖 절묘한 의제(擬制), 온갖 절차 형식이 거듭거듭 펼쳐져 있단다. 이건 이 자유롭고 위대한 나라가 아니고서는 존재할 수 없는 소송이지. 내가 보기에 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에 들어간 비용을 다 합치면, 레이철 부인\"—내가 건성으로 듣는 듯 보여서 그분께 말을 돌린 모양이었습니다—\"지금 이 순간 육만에서 칠만 파운드에 이른답니다!\" 켄지 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했습니다.\n\n나는 내가 무척 무지하다고 느꼈지만, 어쩔 도리가 있었겠습니까? 나는 그 주제를 워낙 까맣게 몰라서 그때조차 아무것도 이해하지 못했습니다.\n\n\"그런데 이 아이가 정말로 그 소송을 한 번도 들어본 적이 없다니!\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"놀랍군요!\"\n\n\"바버리 양은요, 선생님.\" 레이철 부인이 대답했습니다. \"지금은 천사들 가운데 계시지만—\"\n\n\"부디 그러기를 바랍니다, 진심으로.\" 켄지 씨가 정중히 말했습니다.\n\n\"—에스더에게 그저 살아가는 데 도움 될 것만 알게 하기를 바라셨습니다. 그래서 이 아이가 여기서 배운 것 말고는 더 아는 게 없습니다.\"\n\n\"음!\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"전체적으로 매우 적절하군요. 자, 이제 본론으로.\" 그가 나를 향해 말했습니다. \"너의 유일한 친척인(사실 그렇다는 거다, 법적으로는 친척이 없었다는 점을 밝혀야 하니까) 바버리 양이 작고하셨고, 또 당연하게도 레이철 부인이—\"\n\n\"아, 천만에요!\" 레이철 부인이 얼른 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지요.\" 켄지 씨가 맞장구쳤습니다. \"—레이철 부인이 너를 부양하고 돌보는 일을 떠맡으리라고는 기대할 수 없으니(제발 상심하지 마라), 너는 이제 한 가지 제안을 새로이 받을 처지가 되었단다. 그건 한 이 년 전 내가 바버리 양께 전하라는 지시를 받았던 제안인데, 그때는 거절당했지만 그 뒤로 벌어진 가슴 아픈 사정 아래 다시 제안할 수 있는 것으로 양해되어 있었지. 자, 내가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에서, 또 그밖의 일에서, 더없이 인정 많으면서도 동시에 별난 한 분을 대리하고 있다고 밝힌다면, 직업상의 신중함을 어느 정도 늘여본들 내 입장을 곤란하게 만들 일이야 없겠지?\" 켄지 씨가 다시 의자에 등을 기대고 우리 둘을 차분히 바라보며 말했습니다.\n\n그는 무엇보다도 자기 목소리를 듣는 일을 즐기는 듯했습니다. 그럴 만도 했던 것이, 그 목소리는 부드럽고 풍성해서 그가 내뱉는 한 마디 한 마디에 대단한 무게를 실어주었으니까요. 그는 만족스러운 기색이 역력한 채로 제 목소리에 귀 기울였고, 때로는 고개를 까딱여 제 가락에 맞춰 부드럽게 박자를 맞추기도 하고, 손짓으로 문장을 둥글게 맺기도 했습니다. 나는 그에게 깊은 인상을 받았습니다. 그가 제 의뢰인인 어느 위대한 귀족을 본떠 자기를 꾸민다는 것도, 그가 흔히 '입심꾼 켄지'라고 불린다는 것도 미처 알기 전인 그때조차 말입니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 씨는.\" 그가 말을 이었습니다. \"우리 어린 친구의—말하자면, 외로운—처지를 알고서, 너를 일류 학교에 보내주겠다고 하신단다. 거기서 네 교육이 완성되고, 네 안락이 보장되며, 네 합당한 필요가 미리 채워지고, 또 거기서 너는—섭리라고 해야 할까?—그 부르심에 따라 마땅히 놓인 인생의 자리에서 네 의무를 훌륭히 다할 수 있는 자질을 갖추게 될 거란다.\"\n\n그가 한 말과 그것을 말하는 감동적인 태도에 내 가슴이 어찌나 가득 차오르던지, 나는 애써 보았지만 입을 열 수가 없었습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 씨는.\" 그가 계속 말했습니다. \"우리 어린 친구가 언제든 그분의 양해와 동의 없이는 그 학교를 떠나지 않으리라는 기대를 밝히는 것 말고는 아무 조건도 달지 않으신단다. 또 우리 친구가 종국에는 그것에 기대어 살아가야 할 여러 소양을 익히는 데 성실히 매진하리라는 것, 미덕과 명예의 길을 걸으리라는 것, 그리고—그—저—기타 등등 말이다.\"\n\n나는 전보다 더더욱 입을 열 수가 없었습니다.\n\n\"자, 우리 어린 친구는 뭐라고 하려나?\" 켄지 씨가 말을 이었습니다. \"천천히, 천천히 하렴! 네 대답을 기다리마. 하지만 천천히 하렴!\"\n\n그런 제안을 받은 가엾고 빈털터리인 아이가 무슨 말을 하려 했는지는 굳이 되풀이할 필요가 없겠습니다. 그 아이가 실제로 무슨 말을 했는지는, 이야기할 가치가 있다면 더 쉽게 전할 수 있겠습니다. 그 아이가 무엇을 느꼈는지, 그리고 죽는 날까지 무엇을 느낄지는 도무지 풀어낼 수가 없습니다.\n\n이 면담은 윈저에서 이루어졌는데, 내가 아는 한 나는 그곳에서 평생을 보낸 셈이었습니다. 그날부터 한 주 뒤, 필요한 것을 넉넉히 챙겨, 나는 역마차 안에 앉아 그곳을 떠나 레딩으로 향했습니다.\n\n레이철 부인은 너무나 선해서 이별에 아무런 감정도 느끼지 않았지만, 나는 그만큼 선하지 못해 서럽게 울었습니다. 그 오랜 세월을 함께했으니 나는 그분을 더 잘 알았어야 했고, 그분이 나와 헤어지는 걸 못내 아쉬워할 만큼 그분께 사랑받는 아이가 되었어야 했다고 생각했습니다. 그분이 차가운 작별의 입맞춤을 내 이마에 한 번 해주었을 때—마치 돌 현관에서 떨어지는 해빙의 물방울 같았는데, 몹시 서리가 내린 날이었습니다—나는 너무나 비참하고 자책감이 들어 그분께 매달리며, 이렇게 쉽게 작별 인사를 하실 수 있는 건 다 제 잘못인 줄 안다고 말했습니다!\n\n\"아니다, 에스더!\" 그분이 대답했습니다. \"그건 네 불행이지!\"\n\n마차는 작은 잔디밭 문 앞에 와 있었습니다. 우리는 바퀴 소리가 들리고서야 나왔던 것입니다. 그렇게 나는 슬픈 마음으로 그분을 떠났습니다. 그분은 내 짐가방이 마차 지붕에 실리기도 전에 안으로 들어가 문을 닫아버렸습니다. 나는 집이 보이는 동안 내내 눈물을 흘리며 창밖으로 그 집을 돌아보았습니다. 대모님은 자신이 가진 자그마한 재산을 모두 레이철 부인에게 남겼고, 곧 매각이 있을 예정이었습니다. 그리고 장미꽃 무늬가 있는, 내가 이 세상에서 가장 처음 본 물건이라고 늘 여기던 낡은 난로 깔개가, 서리와 눈을 맞으며 바깥에 걸려 있었습니다. 하루이틀 전, 나는 사랑하는 옛 인형을 그 애의 숄로 감싸서, 말하기가 좀 부끄럽지만, 내 옛 창문에 그늘을 드리우던 나무 아래 정원 흙 속에 가만히 묻어주었습니다. 이제 내게 남은 동무라곤 새 한 마리뿐이었고, 나는 새장에 든 그 새를 데리고 떠났습니다.\n\n집이 시야에서 사라지자, 나는 발치 짚더미에 새장을 놓고, 높다란 창밖을 내다보려고 낮은 좌석 앞쪽으로 앉아, 아름다운 정동석 조각 같은 서리 맞은 나무들과, 간밤에 내린 눈으로 매끄럽고 하얗게 덮인 들판과, 새빨갛지만 온기는 거의 내주지 않는 해와, 스케이트 타는 사람과 미끄럼 타는 사람이 눈을 쓸어낸 자리마다 쇠붙이처럼 거뭇한 얼음을 바라보았습니다. 마차 안에는 신사 한 명이 맞은편 좌석에 앉아 있었는데, 잔뜩 껴입은 옷가지 때문에 무척 커 보였습니다. 하지만 그는 반대쪽 창밖만 응시할 뿐 나에게는 눈길도 주지 않았습니다.\n\n나는 돌아가신 대모님을, 그분께 책을 읽어드리던 그 밤을, 침상에서 그토록 굳게 또 엄하게 미간을 찌푸리던 그분을, 내가 가고 있는 그 낯선 곳을, 거기서 만날 사람들과 그들이 어떤 모습일지, 그들이 내게 뭐라고 할지를 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 마차 안에서 들려온 목소리가 나를 소스라치게 놀라게 했습니다.\n\n그 목소리가 말했습니다. \"대체 뭣 때문에 우는 거냐?\"\n\n나는 어찌나 놀랐던지 목소리가 나오지 않아 그저 속삭이듯 대답할 수밖에 없었습니다. \"저 말인가요, 선생님?\" 물론 잔뜩 껴입은 그 신사일 수밖에 없다는 건 알고 있었습니다. 그는 여전히 창밖을 내다보고 있었지만요.\n\n\"그래, 너 말이다.\" 그가 몸을 돌리며 말했습니다.\n\n\"저는 울고 있는 줄 몰랐어요, 선생님.\" 나는 더듬거렸습니다.\n\n\"하지만 울고 있잖니!\" 신사가 말했습니다. \"이걸 보렴!\" 그는 마차 다른 구석에서 내 바로 맞은편으로 다가오더니, 커다란 털 소맷부리 하나로 내 눈가를 (아프지 않게) 쓱 닦아내고는, 그게 젖었다는 걸 보여주었습니다.\n\n\"자! 이제 알겠지, 네가 울고 있다는 걸.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\"그래, 뭣 때문에 우는 거냐?\" 신사가 말했습니다. \"거기 가기 싫은 게냐?\"\n\n\"어디요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"어디라니? 아니, 네가 가는 데가 어디든 말이다.\" 신사가 말했습니다.\n\n\"저는 거기 가게 되어 무척 기뻐요, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"그럼 됐지! 기쁜 표정을 지어 보렴!\" 신사가 말했습니다.\n\n나는 그가 무척 이상한 사람이라고, 적어도 내가 볼 수 있는 그의 모습이 무척 이상하다고 생각했습니다. 그는 턱까지 꽁꽁 싸매고 있었고, 얼굴은 양옆에 넓은 털 끈이 달려 턱밑에서 여민 털모자에 거의 가려져 있었으니까요. 하지만 나는 다시 마음을 가라앉혔고, 그가 두렵지 않았습니다. 그래서 나는 대모님의 죽음 때문에, 또 레이철 부인이 나와 헤어지면서도 아쉬워하지 않으셨기 때문에 운 모양이라고 말했습니다.\n\n\"레이철 부인 따위는 빌어먹을!\" 신사가 말했습니다. \"거센 바람 부는 날에 빗자루나 타고 날아가버리라지!\"\n\n나는 이제 정말로 그가 무서워지기 시작해서, 한껏 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았습니다. 그래도 그가 화난 듯 혼자 중얼거리며 레이철 부인을 욕하긴 했지만, 눈매만은 다정해 보인다고 생각했습니다.\n\n잠시 뒤 그는 겉옷을 풀어 헤쳤는데, 내가 보기에 그건 마차 전체를 감싸고도 남을 만큼 큼지막했습니다. 그러고는 옆구리의 깊은 주머니에 손을 쑥 집어넣었습니다.\n\n\"자, 이걸 보렴!\" 그가 말했습니다. \"이 종이 안에는\" 하고 가리킨 것은 깔끔하게 접힌 종이였습니다. \"돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 좋은 건포도 케이크 한 조각이 들어 있단다. 겉에 설탕이 손가락 한 마디나 두껍게 발렸는데, 꼭 양고기 토막에 붙은 비계 같지. 여기 작은 파이도 있다(이건 크기로 보나 질로 보나 보석 같은 물건이야). 프랑스에서 만든 거란다. 이게 뭘로 만든 건지 알겠니? 살진 거위 간이지. 이런 파이라니! 자, 어디 네가 먹는 걸 좀 보자꾸나.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"정말 무척 고맙지만, 부디 언짢게 여기지 말아 주세요. 저한테는 너무 기름진 음식이에요.\"\n\n\"또 한 방 먹었군!\" 신사가 말했는데, 나는 그 말을 도무지 알아들을 수 없었습니다. 그러고는 두 가지를 다 창밖으로 던져버렸습니다.\n\n그는 레딩에 조금 못 미친 곳에서 마차를 내리기 전까지 더는 내게 말을 걸지 않았습니다. 내릴 때 그는 착한 아이가 되고 공부에 힘쓰라고 당부하며 나와 악수했습니다. 솔직히 나는 그가 떠나서 마음이 놓였습니다. 우리는 어느 이정표 앞에서 그를 내려주었습니다. 그 뒤로 나는 자주 그 이정표 곁을 걸어 지났는데, 오랫동안 그곳을 지날 때마다 그를 떠올리며 혹시 그를 만나지 않을까 은근히 기대하곤 했습니다. 하지만 끝내 만나지 못했고, 그렇게 세월이 흐르면서 그는 내 기억에서 사라져갔습니다.\n\n마차가 멈추자, 무척 단정한 부인 한 분이 창문을 올려다보며 말했습니다. \"도니 양이지요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 부인. 에스더 서머슨인데요.\"\n\n\"바로 맞아요.\" 부인이 말했습니다. \"도니 양 말이에요.\"\n\n나는 그제야 그분이 자기를 그 이름으로 소개한 것임을 알아차리고, 착각했던 것을 도니 양께 사과한 뒤, 부탁에 따라 내 짐가방을 가리켜 보였습니다. 무척 단정한 하녀의 지시에 따라 가방은 아주 자그마한 초록색 마차 바깥쪽에 실렸습니다. 그러고 나서 도니 양과 하녀와 나는 안에 올라타 마차를 타고 떠났습니다.\n\n\"너를 위해 모든 게 준비되어 있단다, 에스더.\" 도니 양이 말했습니다. \"네 학업 계획은 네 후견인이신 잔다이스 씨의 뜻에 꼭 맞게 짜여 있어.\"\n\n\"제—뭐라고 하셨어요, 부인?\"\n\n\"네 후견인이신 잔다이스 씨 말이다.\" 도니 양이 말했습니다.\n\n나는 어찌나 어리둥절했던지, 도니 양은 추위가 내게 너무 혹독했던 모양이라 여기고 자기 각성제 병을 빌려주었습니다.\n\n\"제—후견인이신 잔다이스 씨를 아세요, 부인?\" 나는 한참 머뭇거린 끝에 물었습니다.\n\n\"직접 아는 건 아니란다, 에스더.\" 도니 양이 말했습니다. \"그저 런던의 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소를 통해서지. 켄지 씨, 참으로 훌륭한 신사야. 정말이지 언변이 뛰어나지. 그분의 어떤 문장들은 아주 장엄하다니까!\"\n\n나는 그 말이 무척 옳다고 느꼈지만, 너무 혼란스러워 거기에 마음을 둘 수 없었습니다. 미처 정신을 추스를 새도 없이 목적지에 빠르게 도착하는 바람에 나는 더욱 혼란스러워졌고, 그날 오후 그린리프(도니 양의 집)에서 본 모든 것의 그 불확실하고 비현실적인 분위기를 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다!\n\n하지만 나는 곧 거기에 익숙해졌습니다. 오래지 않아 나는 그린리프의 일과에 어찌나 잘 적응했던지, 마치 거기서 아주 오래 지낸 것 같았고, 대모님 댁에서 보낸 지난 삶은 정말로 살았다기보다 차라리 꿈을 꾼 것만 같았습니다. 그린리프만큼 정확하고 빈틈없고 질서정연한 곳은 어디에도 없었습니다. 시계 문자판을 따라 모든 일에는 정해진 때가 있었고, 모든 일이 정해진 순간에 행해졌습니다.\n\n우리는 기숙생이 열두 명이었고, 도니 양은 쌍둥이라 두 분이었습니다. 나는 머지않아 가정교사로서의 자격에 기대어 살아가야 하리라는 점이 양해되어 있었기에, 그린리프에서 가르치는 모든 것을 배웠을 뿐 아니라 곧 다른 아이들을 가르치는 일도 돕게 되었습니다. 다른 모든 면에서는 학교의 나머지 아이들과 똑같이 대우받았지만, 이 한 가지 차이만은 처음부터 내게 적용되었습니다. 내가 더 많이 알게 될수록 나는 더 많이 가르쳤고, 그렇게 시간이 흐르면서 할 일이 잔뜩 생겼습니다. 그리고 나는 그 일을 무척 좋아했는데, 그 덕에 사랑스러운 아이들이 나를 따랐기 때문입니다. 마침내는 풀이 죽고 시무룩한 새 학생이 들어올 때마다, 그 애는 어김없이, 정말이지 왜인지는 모르겠지만, 나를 친구로 삼는 통에 새로 오는 아이들은 모두 내게 맡겨졌습니다. 다들 내가 무척 다정하다고 했지만, 정작 다정한 건 그 애들이었습니다! 나는 생일에 했던 결심을, 곧 부지런하고 만족할 줄 알며 진실한 마음을 지닌 사람이 되어 누군가에게 작은 선을 베풀고 할 수만 있다면 사랑을 얻겠노라던 그 결심을 자주 떠올렸습니다. 그러면서 정말, 정말이지, 나는 그토록 적게 해주고도 그토록 많이 받았다는 게 거의 부끄러울 지경이었습니다.\n\n나는 그린리프에서 행복하고 평온한 여섯 해를 보냈습니다. 하늘에 감사하게도, 내 생일에 거기 어느 얼굴에서도 내가 차라리 태어나지 않았더라면 좋았으리라는 기색을 본 적이 없습니다. 그날이 돌아오면, 어찌나 많은 다정한 추억의 표시들이 안겨지던지, 내 방은 정월 초하루부터 성탄절까지 그것들로 아름다웠습니다.\n\n그 여섯 해 동안 나는 방학 때 이웃을 방문하러 간 것 말고는 한 번도 멀리 떠난 적이 없습니다. 처음 여섯 달쯤 지났을 무렵, 나는 켄지 씨에게 행복하고 감사하다는 편지를 써 보내는 것이 도리에 맞을지 도니 양의 조언을 구했고, 그분의 동의를 얻어 그런 편지를 썼습니다. 나는 그 편지를 잘 받았다는 격식 차린 답장을 받았는데, \"본 서신의 내용을 유념하였으며, 이를 당사 의뢰인에게 적절히 전달하겠습니다.\"라는 내용이었습니다. 그 뒤로 나는 이따금 도니 양과 그 자매가 내 비용이 얼마나 또박또박 치러지는지 이야기하는 걸 들었고, 한 해에 두 번쯤 비슷한 편지를 써 보내는 용기를 냈습니다. 나는 늘 답신 편으로 똑같은 둥근 필체로 쓴 토씨 하나 다르지 않은 답장을 받았는데, 켄지 앤드 카보이라는 서명만은 다른 필체로 되어 있어, 나는 그게 켄지 씨의 글씨려니 짐작했습니다.\n\n나 자신에 관한 이 모든 걸 써야만 한다는 게 어찌나 묘하게 느껴지는지요! 마치 이 이야기가 내 삶의 이야기이기라도 한 양 말입니다! 하지만 이제 곧 내 자그마한 몸은 뒤편으로 물러날 것입니다.\n\n그린리프에서 평온한 여섯 해(보니 두 번째로 같은 말을 하고 있군요)를 보내며, 나는 거기서 자라고 변해가는 내 모습의 모든 단계를, 마치 거울에 비추듯 주위 사람들에게서 보아왔는데, 그러던 어느 십일월 아침에 이런 편지를 받았습니다. 날짜는 생략하겠습니다.\n\n링컨스 인, 올드 스퀘어\n\n아가씨께,\n\n잔다이스 대 잔다이스\n\n당사 의뢰인 잔다이스 씨께서 대법원의 명령에 따라 본 소송의 피후견인 한 분을 자택에 맞아들이려 하시며, 그 피후견인께 알맞은 동무를 마련해 드리고자 하시는바, 아가씨께서 상기한 자격으로 봉사해 주신다면 기쁘겠다는 뜻을 전하라 당사에 지시하셨습니다.\n\n당사는 다가오는 월요일 아침, 레딩에서 출발하는 여덟 시 마차 편으로 아가씨를 무료로 런던 피카딜리의 화이트 호스 셀러까지 모시도록 조처하였으며, 그곳에서 당사 직원 하나가 대기하다가 상기 주소의 당사 사무소로 아가씨를 안내할 것입니다.\n\n아가씨께, 충실한 당사 직원 올림,\n\n켄지 앤드 카보이\n\n에스더 서머슨 양 귀하\n\n오, 이 편지가 그 집에 일으킨 감정을 나는 결코, 결코, 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다! 다들 나를 그토록 살뜰히 아껴주는 것이 너무나 따뜻했고, 나를 잊지 않은 그 아버지 같은 분이 고아인 내 앞길을 그토록 매끄럽고 수월하게 닦아주시고 그토록 많은 어린 마음을 내게로 기울게 해주신 것이 너무나 자애로워, 나는 도무지 견딜 수가 없었습니다. 그렇다고 그들이 덜 슬퍼했으면 좋았겠다는 건 아닙니다. 안타깝게도 그건 아니지요. 다만 그 기쁨과 그 아픔과 그 긍지와 환희와 겸허한 아쉬움이 너무나 뒤섞여, 내 가슴은 환희로 가득 차 있으면서도 거의 터질 듯했습니다.\n\n편지는 떠나기까지 단 닷새의 말미만 주었습니다. 그 닷새 동안 시시각각 사랑과 다정함의 증거가 더해지고, 마침내 그날 아침이 밝아 그들이 마지막으로 둘러볼 수 있게 나를 모든 방으로 데려가고, 누구는 \"에스더, 얘, 네가 처음 내게 그토록 다정하게 말해주었던 여기 내 침대맡에서 작별 인사를 해줘!\" 하고 외치고, 또 누구는 그저 \"에스더의 사랑을 담아\"라고 자기 이름을 적어달라고 부탁하며, 다들 작별 선물을 들고 나를 에워싸 울며 매달려서는 \"사랑하는, 사랑하는 에스더가 가버리면 우리는 어쩌니!\" 하고 외치는데, 내가 그들이 내게 얼마나 너그럽고 선했는지, 또 내가 그들 하나하나를 얼마나 축복하고 고마워하는지 말해주려 애쓸 때, 내 가슴은 어떠했을까요!\n\n또 두 도니 양이 그 누구 못지않게 나와 헤어지는 걸 슬퍼하고, 하녀들이 \"어디를 가시든 복 받으세요, 아가씨!\" 하고 말하며, 그 오랜 세월 나를 거의 눈여겨보지 않았다고 여겼던 못생기고 절름발이인 늙은 정원사가 제라늄 꽃다발을 건네려 헐떡이며 마차를 뒤쫓아 와서는 내가 자기 눈의 빛이었노라고—정말 그 노인이 그렇게 말했답니다!—할 때, 그때 내 가슴은 어떠했을까요!\n\n게다가 이 모든 일에 더해, 작은 학교에 들렀을 때 뜻밖에도 가엾은 아이들이 바깥에서 모자와 보닛을 흔들며 인사하는 모습과, 내가 가르치는 일을 돕고 또 그 집을 방문한 적이 있던 한 딸의 부모인, 흰머리의 신사와 부인이(그 일대에서 가장 콧대 높은 사람들이라고들 했지요) 아무것에도 아랑곳없이 \"잘 가요, 에스더. 부디 아주 행복하기를!\" 하고 외치는 모습을 보았으니, 마차 안에 홀로 앉아 완전히 무너져내려 \"오, 너무나 감사해요, 너무나 감사해요!\" 하고 몇 번이고 되뇐 것을 어찌 어쩌겠습니까!\n\n하지만 물론 나는 그토록 많은 것을 받고서 이제 가는 곳에 눈물을 가져가서는 안 된다고 곧 생각했습니다. 그래서 당연히 흐느낌을 줄이려 애쓰며, \"에스더, 이제 정말 그래야 해! 이러면 안 돼!\" 하고 자꾸자꾸 되뇌어 마음을 다잡았습니다. 마침내 나는 제법 잘 기운을 차렸는데, 마땅한 것보다 시간이 더 걸린 듯하여 부끄럽습니다. 라벤더 물로 눈을 식히고 나니, 어느새 런던을 살필 때가 되어 있었습니다.\n\n나는 십육 킬로미터나 떨어져 있을 때 이미 다 왔다고 철석같이 믿었고, 정작 다 왔을 때는 영영 도착하지 못할 것만 같았습니다. 그러다 돌이 깔린 길에서 마차가 덜컹대기 시작하고, 특히 다른 탈것들이 죄다 우리 쪽으로 달려드는 듯하고 우리도 다른 탈것마다 들이받는 듯하자, 나는 우리가 정말로 여정의 끝에 다가가고 있음을 믿게 되었습니다. 머지않아 우리는 멈춰 섰습니다.\n\n뜻하지 않게 잉크를 묻힌 어느 젊은 신사가 길바닥에서 내게 말을 건넸습니다. \"저는 링컨스 인의 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 왔습니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"잘 부탁드립니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n그는 무척 친절했고, 내 짐가방 옮기는 일을 감독한 뒤 나를 삯마차에 태워주었는데, 나는 그에게 어디에 큰불이라도 났느냐고 물었습니다. 거리가 짙은 갈색 연기로 가득 차 거의 아무것도 보이지 않았기 때문입니다.\n\n\"오, 천만에요, 아가씨.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"이건 런던 명물이랍니다.\"\n\n나는 그런 걸 들어본 적이 없었습니다.\n\n\"안개예요, 아가씨.\" 젊은 신사가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아, 그렇군요!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n우리는 (내 생각에) 이 세상에서 본 적 없을 만큼 가장 더럽고 가장 어두운 거리들을, 사람들이 어떻게 제정신을 지키는지 의아할 만큼 정신 사납고 혼잡한 와중에 천천히 지나갔습니다. 그러다 어느 낡은 대문 아래로 들어서며 문득 고요해졌고, 적막한 광장을 가로질러 나아가다 어느 구석의 야릇한 모퉁이에 이르렀는데, 그곳에는 마치 교회 입구처럼 가파르고 너른 계단을 오르는 출입구가 있었습니다. 그리고 회랑 아래 바깥에는 정말로 교회 묘지가 있었으니, 계단 창문으로 묘비들이 보였기 때문입니다.\n\n이곳이 켄지 앤드 카보이였습니다. 젊은 신사는 나를 바깥 사무실을 지나 켄지 씨의 방으로 안내했는데—거기엔 아무도 없었습니다—난롯가에 안락의자를 정중히 놓아주었습니다. 그러고는 벽난로 선반 한쪽 못에 걸린 자그마한 거울로 내 주의를 끌었습니다.\n\n\"여행 끝에 혹시 거울에 비춰보고 싶으실까 해서요, 아가씨. 대법관님 앞에 나가시는 거니까요. 꼭 그래야 한다는 건 아닙니다, 분명히요.\" 젊은 신사가 공손히 말했습니다.\n\n\"대법관님 앞에 나간다고요?\" 나는 순간 흠칫 놀라 말했습니다.\n\n\"그저 형식적인 절차랍니다, 아가씨.\" 젊은 신사가 대답했습니다. \"켄지 씨는 지금 법정에 계세요. 안부를 전하시며, 요기 좀 하시겠느냐고요\"—작은 탁자에 비스킷과 포도주가 든 유리병이 놓여 있었습니다—\"그리고 신문이라도 보고 계시랍니다.\" 젊은 신사는 말하면서 신문을 건넸습니다. 그러고는 불을 뒤적여 돋운 뒤 나를 두고 나갔습니다.\n\n모든 게 어찌나 낯설던지—대낮인데도 밤 같다는 점, 촛불이 하얀 불꽃을 내며 설익은 듯 차갑게 타오른다는 점에서 더더욱 낯설었습니다—나는 신문 속 글자들을 그 뜻도 모른 채 읽었고, 같은 글자를 거듭거듭 읽고 있는 나를 발견했습니다. 그렇게 계속해봐야 소용없어서, 나는 신문을 내려놓고 거울에 보닛이 단정한지 흘끗 비춰본 뒤, 절반밖에 밝혀지지 않은 방과, 허름하고 먼지 낀 탁자들과, 수북이 쌓인 서류 더미와, 여태껏 무언가 할 말이 있다는 책들 가운데 가장 표정 없어 보이는 책들로 가득 찬 책장을 둘러보았습니다. 그러고는 계속 생각하고, 생각하고, 또 생각했습니다. 그러는 동안 불은 타고, 타고, 또 탔으며, 촛불은 일렁이며 촛농을 흘렸는데, 심지 가위가 없어서—이윽고 젊은 신사가 무척 더러운 가위 한 자루를 가져다줄 때까지—두 시간 동안이나 그러했습니다.\n\n마침내 켄지 씨가 왔습니다. 그분은 전혀 변하지 않았지만, 내가 얼마나 변했는지를 보고는 놀라며 무척 흐뭇해하는 듯했습니다. \"지금 대법관님의 사실(私室)에 계신 아가씨의 동무가 되실 분이니, 서머슨 양, 우리는 아가씨도 함께 그 자리에 계시는 게 좋겠다고 여겼답니다.\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"대법관님 앞이라고 당황하지는 않으시겠지요?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그러지 않을 것 같아요.\" 곰곰 생각해봐도 정말 당황할 이유가 없어 보였습니다.\n\n그래서 켄지 씨가 내게 팔을 내주었고, 우리는 모퉁이를 돌아 주랑(柱廊) 아래를 지나 옆문으로 들어섰습니다. 그렇게 우리는 복도를 따라 어느 아늑한 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기서는 젊은 아가씨와 젊은 신사가 크게 우르릉거리는 난롯가에 서 있었습니다. 그들과 불 사이에는 가리개가 놓여 있었고, 두 사람은 가리개에 기대어 이야기를 나누고 있었습니다.\n\n내가 들어서자 둘 다 고개를 들었는데, 불빛이 비치는 그 젊은 아가씨를 보니 어찌나 아름다운 소녀이던지요! 그토록 풍성한 황금빛 머리칼에, 그토록 부드러운 파란 눈에, 그토록 환하고 순결하며 사람을 믿는 얼굴이라니!\n\n\"에이다 양.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"이쪽은 서머슨 양입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 환영의 미소를 띠고 손을 내밀며 나를 맞으러 다가오다가, 순간 마음을 바꾼 듯 내게 입을 맞추었습니다. 한마디로 그녀에게는 어찌나 자연스럽고 사람을 사로잡는 매력적인 태도가 있던지, 몇 분 만에 우리는 불빛을 받으며 창가 자리에 앉아 더없이 거리낌 없고 행복하게 이야기를 나누었습니다.\n\n내 마음이 어찌나 가뿐해지던지요! 그녀가 나를 믿어주고 좋아해줄 수 있다는 걸 아는 것이 너무나 기뻤습니다! 그녀는 너무나 다정했고, 내게는 너무나 큰 힘이 되었습니다!\n\n그녀는 그 젊은 신사가 자기 먼 친척이라며, 이름은 리처드 카스턴이라고 일러주었습니다. 그는 순박한 얼굴에 더없이 호감 가는 웃음을 지닌 잘생긴 청년이었습니다. 그녀가 우리가 앉은 곳으로 그를 부르자, 그는 불빛을 받으며 우리 곁에 서서, 근심 없는 소년처럼 명랑하게 이야기했습니다. 그는 무척 어려서, 그때 기껏해야 열아홉 살, 그것도 채 안 되었을지 몰랐지만, 그녀보다는 거의 두 살 위였습니다. 둘 다 고아였고, (내게는 무척 뜻밖이고 신기하게도) 그날 이전에는 한 번도 만난 적이 없었습니다. 우리 셋이 이런 별난 곳에서 처음으로 한자리에 모인 것은 이야깃거리가 될 만했고, 우리는 그 이야기를 나누었습니다. 그러자 우르릉거리기를 그친 불이, 리처드의 말마따나, 졸린 늙은 대법원 사자처럼 빨간 눈을 끔벅이며 우리에게 윙크했습니다.\n\n우리는 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누었습니다. 정장 차림에 변호사 가발을 쓴 신사 한 명이 자주 들락거렸는데, 그가 드나들 때면 저 멀리서 느릿느릿 늘어지는 소리가 들렸기 때문입니다. 그건 우리 소송의 변호인 가운데 한 명이 대법관님께 변론하는 소리라고 그가 말했습니다. 그는 켄지 씨에게 대법관님이 오 분 안에 일어나실 거라고 했습니다. 이윽고 우리는 부산한 소리와 발걸음 소리를 들었고, 켄지 씨가 법정이 폐정했으며 대법관님이 옆방에 계신다고 말했습니다.\n\n가발을 쓴 신사가 거의 곧바로 문을 열고는 켄지 씨에게 들어오시라고 청했습니다. 그러자 우리는 모두 옆방으로 들어갔는데, 켄지 씨가 내 사랑하는 그녀와 함께 맨 앞에 섰습니다—이제는 내게 너무나 자연스러워 이렇게 쓰지 않을 수가 없군요. 그리고 거기, 수수한 검은 옷차림으로 난롯가 탁자 옆 안락의자에 앉아 계신 분이 대법관님이었는데, 아름다운 금실 장식을 두른 그분의 법복은 다른 의자 위에 던져져 있었습니다. 우리가 들어서자 그분은 살피는 눈길을 보냈지만, 태도는 기품 있으면서도 다정했습니다.\n\n가발을 쓴 신사가 대법관님 탁자에 서류 뭉치를 내려놓았고, 대법관님은 말없이 그중 하나를 골라 책장을 넘겼습니다.\n\n\"클레어 양.\" 대법관이 말했습니다. \"에이다 클레어 양이지요?\"\n\n켄지 씨가 그녀를 소개하자, 대법관님은 자기 곁에 앉으라고 청했습니다. 그분이 그녀를 흠모하고 그녀에게 마음을 두었다는 건 나조차 한눈에 알 수 있었습니다. 그토록 아름다운 젊은 사람의 집이 저 메마르고 사무적인 곳으로 대신 채워진다는 게 가슴 아팠습니다. 아무리 잘 봐줘도 대법관님은 부모의 사랑과 긍지를 대신하기엔 너무나 초라한 대용품 같았습니다.\n\n\"여기 거론된 잔다이스는,\" 여전히 책장을 넘기며 대법관이 말했습니다. \"황폐한 집의 잔다이스로군.\"\n\n\"황폐한 집의 잔다이스입니다, 대법관님.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"음울한 이름이군.\" 대법관이 말했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 지금은 음울한 곳이 아닙니다, 대법관님.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그리고 황폐한 집은,\" 대법관이 말했습니다. \"어디 있는—\"\n\n\"하트퍼드셔에 있습니다, 대법관님.\"\n\n\"황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨는 결혼하지 않았나?\" 대법관이 말했습니다.\n\n\"하지 않았습니다, 대법관님.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀습니다.\n\"젊은 리처드 카스턴 군은 와 있는가?\" 대법관이 그를 흘끔 보며 말했습니다.\n\n리처드는 고개 숙여 인사하고 앞으로 나섰습니다.\n\n\"흠!\" 대법관이 책장을 더 넘기며 말했습니다.\n\n\"황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨는, 대법관님,\" 켄지 씨가 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다. \"황공하오나 다시금 상기시켜 드리자면, 알맞은 동무를 마련하셨는데, 그분은—\"\n\n\"리처드 카스턴 군의 동무인가?\" 대법관이 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 미소를 띠며 그렇게 말한 것을 나는 들은 것 같았습니다(다만 확실하지는 않습니다).\n\n\"에이다 클레어 양의 동무입니다. 이쪽이 그 젊은 아가씨예요. 서머슨 양입니다.\"\n\n대법관님은 내게 너그러운 눈길을 보내고는 내 인사를 무척 자애롭게 받아주었습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양은 이 소송의 어느 당사자와도 친척이 아닐 텐데, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 대법관님.\"\n\n켄지 씨가 그 말이 미처 끝나기도 전에 몸을 숙여 무언가를 속삭였습니다. 대법관님은 서류에 눈을 둔 채 귀 기울이며 두세 번 고개를 끄덕이고 책장을 더 넘겼는데, 우리가 나갈 때까지 다시는 내 쪽을 보지 않았습니다.\n\n이제 켄지 씨가, 그리고 리처드도 함께, 문가에 있던 내 쪽으로 물러났는데, 내 귀염둥이(이게 너무나 자연스러워 또 어쩔 수가 없군요!)는 대법관님 곁에 남아 앉아 있었습니다. 대법관님은 그녀와 잠깐 이야기를 나누었는데, 나중에 그녀가 말해준 바로는, 제안된 그 합의를 잘 숙고했는지, 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨 지붕 아래서 행복할 것 같은지, 또 어째서 그렇게 생각하는지를 물으셨다고 했습니다. 이윽고 대법관님은 정중히 일어나 그녀를 놓아준 뒤, 일이 분쯤 리처드 카스턴과 이야기를 나누었습니다. 앉아서가 아니라 선 채로, 한결 편하고 격식을 덜 차린 태도로 말입니다. 마치 대법관님이면서도, 소년의 솔직함에 곧장 가닿는 법을 여전히 아는 것처럼요.\n\n\"좋아!\" 대법관이 소리 내어 말했습니다. \"명령을 내리겠네. 내가 판단하기로는, 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨는,\" 그러면서 그분은 나를 바라보았습니다. \"이 젊은 아가씨에게 무척 좋은 동무를 골랐고, 이 합의는 전체적으로 형편이 허락하는 한 가장 나은 것으로 보이네.\"\n\n그분은 우리를 기분 좋게 물러가게 했고, 우리는 모두 그분이 그토록 상냥하고 정중했던 것에 무척 감사해하며 나왔습니다. 그렇게 함으로써 그분은 분명 위엄을 조금도 잃지 않았을뿐더러, 우리가 보기엔 오히려 위엄을 얻은 듯했습니다.\n\n주랑 아래로 나왔을 때, 켄지 씨는 잠깐 돌아가 물어볼 게 있다는 걸 떠올리고는, 대법관님의 마차와 하인들이 그분이 나오기를 기다리는 가운데 우리를 안개 속에 남겨두었습니다.\n\n\"자!\" 리처드 카스턴이 말했습니다. \"이제 끝났네요! 그런데 다음엔 어디로 가는 거죠, 서머슨 양?\"\n\n\"모르세요?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"전혀요.\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그럼 너는 아니, 얘?\" 나는 에이다에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"몰라요!\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"언니는요?\"\n\n\"전혀 모르겠는걸!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n우리는 서로 바라보며, 마치 숲속의 아이들 같은 우리 처지에 반쯤 웃고 있었는데, 그때 찌부러진 보닛을 쓰고 손가방을 든 별난 노파 하나가 무척 거창한 격식을 차린 태도로, 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지으며 우리에게 다가왔습니다.\n\n\"오!\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"잔다이스의 피후견인들이군요! 영광을 누리게 되어 참으로 기쁘답니다! 젊음과 희망과 아름다움이 이런 곳에 와서 앞날이 어찌 될지도 모르고 있다니, 좋은 징조예요.\"\n\n\"미쳤어.\" 리처드가 노파에게 들리지 않으리라 여기고 속삭였습니다.\n\n\"맞아요! 미쳤지요, 젊은 신사 양반.\" 그녀가 어찌나 빨리 받아치던지 그는 무안해졌습니다. \"나도 한때 피후견인이었답니다. 그때는 미치지 않았었지요.\" 짧은 문장 하나하나 사이마다 낮게 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"내게도 젊음과 희망이 있었어요. 아름다움도 있었겠지요. 이제는 다 부질없지만요. 그 셋 가운데 어느 하나도 나를 거들거나 구해주지 못했답니다. 나는 영광스럽게도 꼬박꼬박 법정에 출석한답니다. 내 서류들을 들고서요. 나는 판결을 기다리고 있어요. 머지않아요. 심판의 날에 말이지요. 나는 요한계시록에 언급된 여섯째 봉인이 바로 국새(國璽)임을 밝혀냈답니다. 그게 열린 지 오래되었어요! 부디 내 축복을 받으세요.\"\n\n에이다가 조금 겁먹은 터라, 나는 가엾은 노파의 비위를 맞추려고 무척 고맙다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래—요!\" 그녀가 점잔 빼며 말했습니다. \"그러리라 짐작해요. 그리고 여기 입심꾼 켄지가 오는군요. 그의 서류들을 들고서요! 존경하는 나리, 안녕하신지요?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지냅니다, 아주 잘요! 자, 성가시게 굴지 마세요, 착한 양반이니까!\" 켄지 씨가 앞장서 돌아가며 말했습니다.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 가엾은 노파가 에이다와 나를 따라붙으며 말했습니다. \"성가시게 굴기는커녕요. 나는 두 사람 모두에게 영지를 하사할 거랍니다. 그건 성가신 일이 아니겠지요? 나는 판결을 기다리고 있어요. 머지않아요. 심판의 날에요. 이건 두 사람에게 좋은 징조랍니다. 내 축복을 받으세요!\"\n\n그녀는 가파르고 너른 계단 아래서 걸음을 멈추었습니다. 하지만 우리가 계단을 오르며 돌아보니, 그녀는 여전히 거기 서서, 여전히 짧은 문장 하나하나 사이마다 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지으며 이렇게 말하고 있었습니다. \"젊음. 그리고 희망. 그리고 아름다움. 그리고 대법원. 그리고 입심꾼 켄지! 하! 부디 내 축복을 받으세요!\"\n\n우리는 그날 밤을 젤리비 부인 댁에서 묵기로 했다고, 켄지 씨가 그의 방에 도착한 우리에게 일러주었습니다. 그러더니 나를 돌아보며, 내가 젤리비 부인이 누구인지 당연히 알고 있으리라 여긴다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"정말 모릅니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"혹시 카스톤 씨나, 클레어 양은—\"\n\n하지만 두 사람도 젤리비 부인에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐습니다. \"저런! 젤리비 부인은 말이지요.\" 켄지 씨는 난로를 등지고 서서, 마치 먼지 낀 난로 깔개가 젤리비 부인의 전기라도 되는 양 거기에 눈길을 떨구며 말했습니다. \"성품이 아주 비범하게 강인한 부인으로, 자신을 온전히 공공에 바치는 분입니다. 시기마다 광범위하고 다양한 공공 사안에 헌신해왔고, 지금은 (다른 무언가가 마음을 끌기 전까지는) 아프리카 사안에 헌신하고 있지요. 커피 열매의 전반적인 재배를—그리고 원주민을—더불어 우리의 넘쳐나는 본국 인구를 아프리카 강기슭에 행복하게 정착시키는 것을 목표로 말입니다. 좋은 일이라 여겨지는 모든 사업이라면 돕기를 바라며 자선가들에게 자주 부탁받는 잔다이스 씨도, 내가 알기로는, 젤리비 부인을 무척 높이 평가하고 있습니다.\"\n\n켄지 씨는 넥타이를 매만지고 나서 우리를 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"그러면 젤리비 씨는요, 선생님?\" 리처드가 물었습니다.\n\n\"아! 젤리비 씨로 말하자면,\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"그는—저—젤리비 부인의 남편이라고 말하는 것보다 더 잘 설명할 길이 있을지 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"존재감 없는 분이군요, 선생님?\" 리처드가 익살스러운 표정으로 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇다고는 안 했습니다.\" 켄지 씨가 정색하며 대답했습니다. \"사실 그렇게 말할 수 없지요, 젤리비 씨에 관해서는 도무지 아는 게 없으니까요. 내가 알기로는, 젤리비 씨를 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 적이 한 번도 없습니다. 어쩌면 아주 출중한 분일 수도 있지만, 말하자면, 부인의 더 빛나는 자질 속에 묻혀버린—묻혀버린 분이지요.\" 켄지 씨는 이어서 우리에게, 이런 저녁에 황폐한 집까지 가는 길은 매우 길고 어둡고 지루할 터인 데다 우리가 이미 여행을 한 참이라, 잔다이스 씨가 손수 이런 조처를 제안했다고 말해주었습니다. 내일 오전 일찍 우리를 도시 밖으로 데려갈 마차가 젤리비 부인 댁에 와 있으리라고요.\n\n그러고 나서 그가 작은 종을 울리자 그 젊은 신사가 들어왔습니다. 켄지 씨는 그를 거피라는 이름으로 부르며, 서머슨 양의 상자들과 나머지 짐이 \"보내졌는지\" 물었습니다. 거피 씨는 그렇다고, 짐은 보내졌으며, 우리가 원하면 곧바로 우리도 데려갈 마차가 기다리고 있다고 답했습니다.\n\n\"그러면 이제 남은 일은,\" 켄지 씨가 우리와 악수하며 말했습니다. \"오늘 매듭지어진 이 조처에 (안녕히 가세요, 클레어 양!) 대한 나의 생생한 만족을 표하는 것과, 이 일이 관련된 모든 분의 행복과, (안녕히 가세요, 서머슨 양!) 안녕과, 모든 관점에서의 (알게 되어 영광이었습니다, 카스톤 씨!) 이익에 이바지하기를 바라는 나의 생생한 희망을 표하는 것뿐이로군요! 거피, 일행을 무사히 그곳까지 모셔다드리게.\"\n\n\"'그곳'이 어디입니까, 거피 씨?\" 우리가 계단을 내려가는데 리처드가 물었습니다.\n\n\"멀지 않아요.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"아시다시피, 저쪽 세이비스 여관 근처지요.\"\n\n\"어디 있는지 안다고는 못 하겠군요, 나는 윈체스터에서 와서 런던이 낯설거든요.\"\n\n\"바로 모퉁이만 돌면 됩니다.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"대법원 골목을 휙 꺾어 올라가서 홀본을 따라 질러가면, 그야말로 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 사 분 만에 도착이지요. 이만하면 이게 바로 런던 특유의 안개 아닙니까, 아가씨?\" 그는 나를 봐서 그 안개를 무척 흐뭇해하는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"안개가 정말 짙군요!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 아가씨께는 영향이 없는 게 분명합니다.\" 발판을 올리며 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"오히려, 안색으로 판단하건대, 아가씨께는 좋은 작용을 하는 것 같은데요.\"\n\n나는 그가 좋은 뜻으로 이 칭찬을 건넨 줄 알았기에, 그가 문을 닫고 마부석에 올라탄 뒤에 거기에 얼굴을 붉힌 나 자신을 보고 웃었습니다. 그리고 우리 셋은 우리의 미숙함과 런던의 낯섦에 대해 웃으며 이야기를 나누다가, 어느 아치문 아래로 꺾어 들어 목적지에 이르렀습니다—안개를 가두어두는 길쭉한 물통 같은, 높은 집들이 늘어선 좁은 거리였습니다. 우리가 멈춰 선 집 둘레에는 주로 아이들로 이루어진 작고 어수선한 무리가 모여 있었는데, 그 집 문에는 젤리비라는 글자가 새겨진, 빛바랜 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었습니다.\n\n\"놀라지 마세요!\" 마차 창문을 들여다보며 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"젤리비네 어린것 하나가 지하실 난간 사이에 머리를 들이밀고 끼어버렸네요!\"\n\n\"어머, 가엾어라.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"내려주세요, 부탁이에요!\"\n\n\"부디 조심하세요, 아가씨. 젤리비네 어린것들은 늘 무슨 일을 벌이거든요.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n나는 그 가엾은 아이에게 다가갔는데, 내가 본 가장 꾀죄죄하고 가련한 어린것 가운데 하나였습니다. 아이는 잔뜩 달아오른 채 겁에 질려 큰 소리로 울며, 두 개의 쇠 난간 사이에 목이 끼어 옴짝달싹 못 하고 있었습니다. 우유 배달부와 교구지기가 더없이 친절한 마음으로 아이의 다리를 잡아 끄집어내려 애쓰고 있었는데, 그렇게 하면 두개골이 눌려 빠질 수 있으리라고들 믿는 모양이었습니다. 나는 (아이를 달랜 뒤에) 그 아이가 본디 머리가 큰 사내아이라는 것을 알아채고는, 머리가 들어간 곳이라면 어쩌면 몸도 따라갈 수 있겠다 싶어, 빼내는 가장 좋은 방법은 앞으로 밀어 넣는 것이라고 말했습니다. 우유 배달부와 교구지기가 이 말을 어찌나 흔쾌히 받아들였는지, 리처드와 거피 씨가 부엌을 통해 뛰어 내려가 아이가 풀려나면 받아 안으려 할 동안 내가 아이의 앞치마를 붙들지 않았더라면, 아이는 곧장 지하실로 밀려들어 갔을 것입니다. 마침내 아이는 다친 데 없이 무사히 내려졌고, 그러더니 아주 미친 듯이 굴렁쇠 막대로 거피 씨를 두드려 패기 시작했습니다.\n\n그 집 식구로 보이는 사람은 아무도 나타나지 않았고, 다만 나막신을 신은 한 사람이 아래에서 빗자루로 아이를 쿡쿡 찌르고 있었을 뿐입니다. 무슨 까닭에서인지 나는 모르겠고, 그 사람도 모르는 것 같았습니다. 그래서 나는 젤리비 부인이 집에 없으려니 짐작했는데, 그 사람이 나막신을 벗은 채로 복도에 나타나 에이다와 나보다 앞서 이 층 뒤쪽 방으로 올라가더니 \"젤리비 마님, 그 아가씨 두 분이에요!\"라며 우리를 알리는 바람에 적잖이 놀랐습니다. 올라가는 길에 우리는 어둠 속에서 밟지 않으려 애써야 하는 아이들 몇몇을 더 지나쳤습니다. 그리고 우리가 젤리비 부인 앞에 들어서는데, 그 가엾은 어린것 하나가 계단을 굴러떨어졌습니다—한 층을 통째로 (적어도 내 귀에는 그렇게 들렸습니다), 요란한 소리를 내며 말입니다.\n\n그 사랑스러운 아이의 머리가 계단마다 쿵쿵 부딪히며 떨어진 자취를 새기는데도—리처드가 나중에 말하기를 층계참 것 하나를 빼고도 일곱 번을 세었다고 했습니다—우리는 어쩔 수 없이 얼굴에 드러난 불안을 젤리비 부인의 얼굴에서는 조금도 찾아볼 수 없었습니다. 부인은 더없이 차분하게 우리를 맞이했습니다. 그녀는 마흔에서 쉰 사이의, 곱고 아주 자그마하며 통통한 여인으로, 아름다운 눈을 지녔지만 그 눈에는 한참 먼 곳을 바라보는 듯한 묘한 버릇이 있었습니다. 마치—또 리처드의 말을 빌리자면—아프리카보다 가까운 것은 아무것도 보이지 않는다는 듯이 말입니다!\n\n\"두 분을 맞이하는 기쁨을 누리게 되어 정말 기쁩니다.\" 젤리비 부인이 듣기 좋은 목소리로 말했습니다. \"나는 잔다이스 씨를 무척 존경하고, 그분이 관심을 두는 사람이라면 누구도 내게 무심한 대상이 될 수 없으니까요.\"\n\n우리는 감사의 뜻을 밝히고 문 뒤에 앉았는데, 거기에는 다리 하나가 망가진 환자 같은 소파가 놓여 있었습니다. 젤리비 부인은 머릿결이 무척 고왔지만 아프리카 일에 너무 바쁜 나머지 머리를 빗지 못했습니다. 우리에게 다가올 때 부인이 헐겁게 두르고 있던 숄이 의자 위로 흘러내렸고, 자리로 돌아가려고 몸을 돌리는데, 우리는 어쩔 수 없이 알아채고 말았습니다. 부인의 옷이 등 쪽에서 영 여며지지 않았고, 그 벌어진 자리는 코르셋 끈을 격자무늬로 가로질러 엮어두었다는 것을—마치 정자처럼 말입니다.\n\n종이가 흩어져 있고, 비슷한 잡동사니로 뒤덮인 커다란 책상이 거의 가득 차지하고 있는 그 방은, 솔직히 말해, 무척 어수선할 뿐 아니라 무척 더러웠습니다. 우리는 시각으로 그것을 알아챌 수밖에 없었는데, 그러는 동안에도 청각으로는 계단에서 굴러떨어진 그 가엾은 아이를 뒤쫓고 있었습니다. 아마 뒤편 부엌으로 간 듯했는데, 거기서 누군가가 아이를 입막음하는 것 같았습니다.\n\n하지만 무엇보다 우리 눈길을 끈 것은 책상에 앉아 있는, 지치고 병약해 보이지만 결코 못생기지는 않은 한 소녀였습니다. 소녀는 펜의 깃털을 물어뜯으며 우리를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었습니다. 그토록 잉크 범벅이 된 사람은 일찍이 없었으리라 싶었습니다. 헝클어진 머리부터, 굽이 닳아빠지고 해어져 터진 새틴 슬리퍼로 흉하게 망가진 고운 발끝까지, 핀에서 위로 단 하나도, 제대로 된 상태이거나 제자리에 있는 옷가지가 정말 없는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"보다시피, 얘들아,\" 젤리비 부인이 양철 촛대에 꽂힌 큼직한 사무용 양초 두 개의 심지를 잘랐는데, 그 바람에 방에서 뜨거운 짐승 기름 냄새가 진하게 풍겼습니다 (난로 불은 꺼져 있었고, 화격자 안에는 재와 장작 한 묶음, 부지깽이밖에 없었습니다), \"보다시피, 얘들아, 나는 늘 그렇듯 무척 바쁘단다. 하지만 너희가 이해해주리라 믿어. 지금은 아프리카 사업이 내 온 시간을 잡아먹고 있거든. 그 일로 나는 공공 단체들과, 또 전국 각지에서 자기 동족의 안녕을 간절히 바라는 개인들과 편지를 주고받느라 분주하단다. 일이 진척되고 있다고 말할 수 있어 기쁘구나. 우리는 내년 이맘때까지 백오십에서 이백 가구의 건강한 가족이 니제르강 왼쪽 기슭의 보리오불라가에서 커피를 재배하고 원주민을 가르치게 되기를 바라고 있단다.\"\n* 보리오불라가: 디킨스가 지어낸 가상의 아프리카 지명으로, 헛된 박애주의의 표적을 풍자한다.\n\n에이다가 아무 말 없이 나를 바라보기에, 내가 무척 흐뭇한 일이겠다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"흐뭇한 일이지.\" 젤리비 부인이 말했습니다. \"내 보잘것없는 기력이나마 온통 바쳐야 하는 일이지만, 성공하기만 한다면야 그쯤은 아무것도 아니지. 게다가 나는 날마다 성공을 더 확신하고 있단다. 그런데 말이다, 서머슨 양, 나는 어쩐지 네가 한 번도 아프리카로 생각을 돌려본 적이 없다는 게 의아하구나.\"\n\n이렇게 화제를 내게 들이미는 것이 정말 너무 뜻밖이어서, 나는 그것을 어떻게 받아넘겨야 할지 도무지 몰랐습니다. 나는 기후가—하고 넌지시 말을 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"세상에서 가장 좋은 기후란다!\" 젤리비 부인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"정말요, 부인?\"\n\n\"그렇고말고. 조심만 하면 말이다.\" 젤리비 부인이 말했습니다. \"조심하지 않고 홀본에 나갔다가는 마차에 치일 수도 있어. 조심해서 홀본에 나가면 결코 치이지 않지. 아프리카도 꼭 그렇단다.\"\n\n나는 \"물론 그렇겠지요\"라고 말했습니다. 홀본을 두고 한 말이었습니다.\n\n\"괜찮다면,\" 젤리비 부인이 종이 한 무더기를 우리 쪽으로 밀어주며 말했습니다. \"내가 맏딸—이 아이가 내 필경사거든—에게 지금 받아쓰게 하는 편지를 마저 끝내는 동안, 그 문제와 전반적인 사안에 관해 널리 배포된 소견들을 좀 훑어보려무나—\"\n\n책상에 앉은 소녀는 펜 물어뜯기를 멈추고, 반은 수줍고 반은 뾰로통한 태도로 우리의 인사에 답례했습니다.\n\n\"—그러면 지금으로서는 일이 끝나는 셈이지.\" 젤리비 부인이 상냥한 미소를 띠며 말을 이었습니다. \"하기야 내 일이라는 게 끝이 없지만 말이다. 어디까지 했지, 캐디?\"\n\n\"'스왈로 씨께 안부를 전하며, 삼가—'\" 캐디가 말했습니다.\n\n\"'삼가,'\" 젤리비 부인이 받아쓰게 하며 말했습니다. \"'아프리카 사업에 관한 문의 편지와 관련하여 알려드리고자—' 안 돼, 피피! 나 때문이라면 됐어!\"\n\n피피는 (스스로 그렇게 부르는) 계단에서 굴러떨어진 그 가엾은 아이였는데, 이번에는 이마에 반창고 한 조각을 붙인 채로 다친 무릎을 내보이려고 나타나 편지 받아쓰기를 가로막았습니다. 그 무릎을 보고 에이다와 나는 멍 자국과 때 가운데 어느 쪽을 더 가엾어해야 할지 알 수 없었습니다. 젤리비 부인은 모든 말을 할 때와 똑같은 평온한 침착함으로 그저 한마디 덧붙였습니다. \"저리 가, 이 말썽꾸러기 피피!\" 그러고는 다시 아름다운 눈을 아프리카에 고정했습니다.\n\n하지만 부인이 곧바로 받아쓰기를 이어갔고, 내가 그렇게 한다고 해서 무엇을 가로막는 것도 아니었기에, 나는 가엾은 피피가 나가려는 것을 조용히 붙들어 안아 올렸습니다. 아이는 그것과, 또 에이다가 입을 맞추는 것에 몹시 놀란 듯했지만, 이내 내 품에서 흐느낌의 간격이 점점 길어지더니 조용해질 때까지 곤히 잠들었습니다. 나는 피피에게 정신이 팔려 편지 내용을 세세히 놓치고 말았지만, 그 편지에서 받은 전반적인 인상이라면, 아프리카가 더없이 중대하고 다른 모든 곳과 모든 것은 하나같이 하찮다는 것이어서, 그 일을 그토록 하찮게 여겨온 것이 정말 부끄러울 지경이었습니다.\n\n\"여섯 시구나!\" 젤리비 부인이 말했습니다. \"우리 저녁 시간은 명목상 (우리는 아무 때나 먹으니까) 다섯 시인데 말이야! 캐디, 클레어 양과 서머슨 양께 방을 보여드리렴. 아마 좀 갈아입고 싶을 테니까? 내가 이렇게 바쁜 걸, 알다시피, 두 분이 이해해주겠지. 어머, 저 못된 녀석! 부디 그 아이를 내려놓으렴, 서머슨 양!\"\n\n나는 그 아이가 조금도 성가시지 않다고 진심으로 말하며 계속 안고 있게 해달라고 청한 뒤, 위층으로 데려가 내 침대에 눕혔습니다. 에이다와 나는 위층 방 두 개를 썼는데, 그 사이에는 통하는 문이 하나 있었습니다. 방은 휑하기 짝이 없고 어수선했으며, 내 방 창문 커튼은 포크로 고정되어 있었습니다.\n\n\"뜨거운 물이 좀 필요하시죠?\" 젤리비 양이 손잡이 달린 물주전자를 찾아 둘러보았지만 헛수고였습니다.\n\n\"성가시지 않다면요.\" 우리가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아, 성가신 게 문제가 아니에요.\" 젤리비 양이 대답했습니다. \"물이 있느냐 없느냐가 문제죠.\"\n\n저녁 공기가 너무 차갑고 방에서는 늪 같은 냄새가 풍겨서, 솔직히 고백하건대 다소 비참한 기분이 들었고, 에이다는 반쯤 울먹였습니다. 그러나 우리는 곧 웃었고, 한창 짐을 풀고 있는데 젤리비 양이 돌아와 뜨거운 물이 없어 미안하다고, 주전자를 찾지 못한 데다 보일러도 고장 났다고 했습니다.\n\n우리는 그런 말 마시라며, 서둘러 다시 난롯가로 내려가려고 부랴부랴 채비를 했습니다. 그런데 어린것들이 죄다 바깥 층계참으로 올라와 내 침대에 누운 피피라는 진귀한 광경을 구경하고 있어서, 문 경첩 사이 위태로운 자리에 코와 손가락이 쉴 새 없이 나타나는 통에 우리는 정신이 사나웠습니다. 어느 방이든 문을 닫는 것은 불가능했습니다. 내 방 자물쇠는 손잡이가 없어서 마치 태엽을 감아야 할 물건처럼 보였고, 에이다 방의 손잡이는 더없이 매끄럽게 빙빙 돌아갔지만 문에는 아무런 효력도 미치지 못했으니까요. 그래서 나는 아이들에게, 들어와서 내 탁자 앞에 얌전히 앉아 있으면 내가 옷을 갈아입는 동안 빨간 모자 소녀 이야기를 들려주겠다고 제안했습니다. 아이들은 그대로 따랐고, 늑대가 나타나기 직전에 마침맞게 잠에서 깬 피피까지 포함해서, 다들 생쥐처럼 조용했습니다.\n\n아래층으로 내려가니 계단 창문에는 \"턴브리지웰스에서 온 선물\"이라고 적힌 머그잔이 둥둥 뜬 심지를 켜 밝히고 있었고, 한 젊은 여자가 부어오른 얼굴을 플란넬 붕대로 싸맨 채 응접실 (지금은 열린 문으로 젤리비 부인 방과 이어져 있었습니다) 난롯불을 후후 불며 무섭게 콜록거리고 있었습니다. 한마디로 연기가 어찌나 자욱한지, 우리는 모두 창문을 열어둔 채 반 시간이나 기침하고 눈물을 흘리며 앉아 있었고, 그동안 젤리비 부인은 한결같이 온화한 성미로 아프리카에 관한 편지를 받아쓰게 하고 있었습니다. 솔직히 부인이 그렇게 바쁜 것이 내게는 큰 다행이었는데, 리처드가 우리에게, 자기는 파이 접시에 손을 씻었고 화장대 위에서 주전자를 찾았다고 하면서 에이다를 어찌나 웃기던지, 둘이서 나까지 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없게 웃게 만들었기 때문입니다.\n\n일곱 시가 조금 지나 우리는 저녁을 먹으러 내려갔는데, 젤리비 부인의 충고에 따라 조심조심 내려갔습니다. 계단 양탄자는 고정용 철사가 한참 모자란 데다 어찌나 찢어졌는지 완전히 덫이나 다름없었으니까요. 우리는 좋은 대구 한 마리와 구운 쇠고기 한 덩이, 커틀릿 한 접시와 푸딩을 먹었습니다. 변변히 조리만 했더라면 훌륭한 저녁이었겠지만, 거의 날것이었습니다. 플란넬 붕대를 한 젊은 여자가 시중을 들었는데, 닿는 대로 아무 데나 음식을 식탁에 툭 내려놓고는 그것을 계단에 갖다 둘 때까지 다시는 옮기지 않았습니다. 내가 나막신을 신고 있는 모습을 본 그 사람은, 짐작건대 요리사였던 듯한데, 자주 문간으로 와서 그 여자와 옥신각신했고, 둘 사이에는 악감정이 있는 듯했습니다.\n\n저녁 식사 내내—감자 접시가 석탄통 속에 잘못 놓이고, 코르크 따개 손잡이가 빠져 젊은 여자의 턱을 친 따위의 사고 탓에 식사는 길어졌습니다—젤리비 부인은 한결같은 성미를 지켰습니다. 부인은 보리오불라가와 원주민에 관해 흥미로운 이야기를 잔뜩 들려주었고, 어찌나 많은 편지를 받았던지 그 곁에 앉은 리처드는 한꺼번에 봉투 네 개가 그레이비 소스에 빠져 있는 것을 보았습니다. 어떤 편지는 부인회 회의록이나 부인회 결의문이어서 우리에게 읽어주었고, 어떤 편지는 커피 재배와 원주민 문제로 이런저런 흥분에 빠진 사람들의 청원이었으며, 어떤 편지는 답장이 필요해서 부인은 그것을 쓰라고 식탁에서 맏딸을 서너 번이나 내보냈습니다. 부인은 일에 파묻혀 있었고, 우리에게 말한 대로, 분명 그 대의에 헌신하고 있었습니다.\n\n생선이 치워진 뒤에 빈 의자 하나에 (특별히 상석이나 말석이랄 것도 없었습니다) 슬그머니 앉은, 안경을 쓴 온순하고 머리 벗어진 신사가 누구인지 나는 조금 궁금했습니다. 그는 보리오불라가에 수동적으로 자신을 내맡긴 듯했지만, 그 정착지에 능동적으로 관심을 두지는 않는 듯했습니다. 한마디도 입을 떼지 않아서, 피부색만 아니었다면 원주민이라 해도 믿었을 것입니다. 우리가 식탁을 떠나고 그가 리처드와 단둘이 남았을 때에야 비로소, 그가 젤리비 씨일지도 모른다는 생각이 내 머리에 떠올랐습니다. 그런데 그가 정말 젤리비 씨였습니다. 저녁에 찾아온, 관자놀이가 크고 번들거리는 혹처럼 솟고 머리카락을 모조리 뒤로 빗어 넘긴 퀘일 씨라는 수다스러운 젊은이가, 자신은 자선가라고 에이다에게 말하면서, 젤리비 부인과 젤리비 씨의 혼인 결합을 정신과 물질의 결합이라 부른다고 일러주었으니까요.\n\n이 젊은이는 아프리카에 관해, 그리고 커피 식민지 개척자들에게 원주민이 피아노 다리를 깎는 법을 가르치게 하여 수출업을 일으키겠다는 자신의 사업 계획에 관해 할 말이 잔뜩 있을 뿐 아니라, 젤리비 부인의 말을 끌어내는 데 흥을 느꼈습니다. \"젤리비 부인, 이제 보니 부인께서는 단 하루에 아프리카에 관한 편지를 백오십에서 이백 통까지 받으신 적이 있다지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\" 라거나, \"제 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 젤리비 부인, 한번은 한 우체국에서 한꺼번에 회람 오천 부를 발송하셨다고 말씀하신 적이 있지요?\" 같은 식으로 말입니다—그러고는 늘 통역사처럼 젤리비 부인의 대답을 우리에게 되풀이해주었습니다. 저녁 내내 젤리비 씨는 마치 의기소침에 시달리는 사람처럼 벽에 머리를 기댄 채 구석에 앉아 있었습니다. 저녁 식사 후 리처드와 단둘이 있을 때 그는, 마음에 무슨 사연이라도 있는 듯 여러 번 입을 열었다가, 늘 아무 말도 없이 다시 다물어버려서, 리처드를 몹시 당혹스럽게 했던 모양입니다.\n\n젤리비 부인은 폐지가 그득한 둥지 같은 곳에 앉아 저녁 내내 커피를 마시며 틈틈이 맏딸에게 받아쓰게 했습니다. 부인은 또 퀘일 씨와 토론도 벌였는데, 그 주제는—내가 제대로 이해했다면—인류의 형제애인 듯했고, 아름다운 정서를 몇 마디 토로하기도 했습니다. 하지만 나는 바라던 만큼 귀 기울여 듣는 청자가 되지 못했는데, 피피와 다른 아이들이 응접실 구석에 있는 에이다와 내 곁으로 몰려들어 이야기를 하나 더 해달라고 졸랐기 때문입니다. 그래서 우리는 아이들 틈에 앉아 속삭이며 \"장화 신은 고양이\"며 그 밖에 무엇인지 모를 이야기들을 들려주다가, 젤리비 부인이 문득 아이들을 떠올리고는 잠자리로 보냈습니다. 피피가 나더러 재워달라고 울어대기에 나는 아이를 위층으로 안고 올라갔는데, 거기서 플란넬 붕대를 한 젊은 여자가 용처럼 그 어린 식구들 한복판으로 달려들어 아이들을 아기 침대에 들이뒤집어 놓았습니다.\n\n그 후 나는 우리 방을 조금 치우고, 불을 붙여놓았지만 영 심술궂게 굴던 난로를 어르고 달래 타오르게 하는 일에 매달렸는데, 마침내 불이 아주 환하게 붙었습니다. 다시 아래층으로 내려가면서, 나는 젤리비 부인이 내가 그토록 시시한 일에 매달린 것을 다소 깔보는 듯한 눈길로 본다는 것을 느꼈고, 그것이 안타까웠습니다. 하지만 동시에 나는 나 자신에게 그보다 더 거창한 자부심이 없다는 것도 알고 있었습니다.\n\n잠자리에 들 기회를 잡은 것은 거의 자정이 다 되어서였고, 그때조차 우리는 종이에 파묻혀 커피를 마시는 젤리비 부인과 펜의 깃털을 물어뜯는 젤리비 양을 그대로 두고 올라왔습니다.\n\n\"정말 이상한 집이야!\" 위층에 올라오자 에이다가 말했습니다. \"사촌 잔다이스 씨가 우리를 여기로 보내다니, 어쩜 이렇게 별나실까!\"\n\n\"얘,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"정말 어리둥절해. 이해하고 싶은데, 도무지 이해할 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"뭐가?\" 에이다가 고운 미소를 띠며 물었습니다.\n\n\"이 모든 게 말이야.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"젤리비 부인이 원주민을 위한 계획에 이토록 공을 들이는 건 분명 무척 갸륵한 일인데—그런데도—피피며 살림살이며!\"\n\n에이다는 웃으며, 내가 난롯불을 바라보고 서 있는데 내 목에 팔을 두르고는, 나더러 조용하고 사랑스럽고 착한 사람이라 자기 마음을 사로잡았다고 말했습니다. \"넌 참 사려 깊어, 에스더.\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"그런데도 그렇게 명랑하잖아! 게다가 그렇게나 많은 일을, 조금도 내세우지 않고 해내고! 너라면 이런 집에서도 가정을 일굴 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n순진한 내 사랑스러운 친구! 그녀는 자기가 자신을 칭찬하고 있을 뿐이라는 것도, 자기 마음씨가 착하기에 나를 그토록 추켜세운다는 것도 전혀 깨닫지 못했습니다!\n\n\"하나 물어봐도 될까?\" 우리가 난롯가에 잠시 앉아 있다가 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"오백 개라도.\" 에이다가 말했습니다.\n\n\"네 사촌, 잔다이스 씨 말이야. 내가 그분께 신세를 너무 많이 졌잖아. 그분을 좀 설명해줄 수 있겠니?\"\n\n에이다는 황금빛 머리를 흔들며, 어찌나 웃음 어린 놀라움으로 내게 눈을 돌리는지, 나도 가슴 가득 놀라움이 차올랐습니다. 한편으로는 그녀의 아름다움에, 한편으로는 그녀의 놀라움에 말입니다.\n\n\"에스더!\" 그녀가 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"얘!\"\n\n\"내 사촌 잔다이스 씨를 설명해달라고?\"\n\n\"얘, 난 그분을 본 적이 없잖아.\"\n\n\"나도 그분을 본 적이 없어!\" 에이다가 대답했습니다.\n\n세상에, 정말이지!\n\n그래요, 그녀는 그분을 한 번도 본 적이 없었습니다. 어머니가 돌아가셨을 때 그녀는 어렸지만, 어머니가 그분 이야기와 그분의 고결하고 너그러운 성품—세상 그 무엇보다 믿을 만하다고 어머니가 말한 그 성품—을 말할 때면 눈에 눈물이 차오르던 것을 기억했습니다. 에이다는 그 말을 믿었습니다. 사촌 잔다이스 씨는 몇 달 전에 그녀에게 편지를 보냈는데—\"꾸밈없고 진솔한 편지였어.\" 라고 에이다가 말했습니다—우리가 이제 들어서게 된 이 조처를 제안하면서, \"머지않아 그 비참한 대법원 소송이 낸 상처를 얼마간 아물게 할 수도 있을 거라\"고 적었습니다. 그녀는 고마운 마음으로 그 제안을 받아들이겠다는 답장을 보냈습니다. 리처드도 비슷한 편지를 받고 비슷한 답을 했습니다. 그는 오 년 전 윈체스터 학교에서 잔다이스 씨를 한 번, 딱 한 번 본 적이 있었습니다. 내가 두 사람을 발견했을 때 난롯불 앞 가리개에 기대어 있던 리처드는, 에이다에게 그분을 \"활달하고 혈색 좋은 사람\"으로 기억한다고 말했습니다. 이것이 에이다가 내게 해줄 수 있는 설명의 전부였습니다.\n\n그 일이 어찌나 나를 골똘하게 했는지, 에이다가 잠든 뒤에도 나는 여전히 난롯가에 남아 황폐한 집에 대해 자꾸자꾸 궁금해했고, 어제 아침이 어쩌면 이토록 까마득하게 느껴질까 하고 자꾸자꾸 의아해했습니다. 문 두드리는 소리에 정신이 돌아왔을 때, 내 생각이 어디를 헤매고 있었는지 모르겠습니다.\n\n나는 살그머니 문을 열었고, 젤리비 양이 한 손에는 부러진 촛대에 꽂힌 동강 난 양초를, 다른 손에는 달걀 잔을 들고 거기서 떨고 있는 것을 발견했습니다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요!\" 그녀가 무척 뾰로통하게 말했습니다.\n\n\"잘 자요!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"들어가도 돼요?\" 그녀가 똑같이 뾰로통한 태도로 짧고도 뜻밖에 내게 물었습니다.\n\n\"그럼요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"클레어 양은 깨우지 말고요.\"\n\n그녀는 앉으려 하지 않고 난롯가에 서서, 식초가 담긴 달걀 잔에 잉크 묻은 가운뎃손가락을 적셔 얼굴의 잉크 자국에 문질러대면서, 내내 얼굴을 찌푸리며 무척 침울한 표정을 지었습니다.\n\n\"아프리카 따위 없어졌으면 좋겠어요!\" 그녀가 불쑥 말했습니다.\n\n나는 타이르려 했습니다.\n\n\"정말이에요!\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"내게 아무 말 마세요, 서머슨 양. 난 그게 밉고 끔찍해요. 그건 짐승이에요!\"\n\n나는 그녀에게 피곤한 거라고, 안됐다고 말했습니다. 나는 그녀의 머리에 손을 얹고 이마를 짚으며, 지금은 뜨겁지만 내일이면 식을 거라고 말했습니다. 그녀는 여전히 입을 삐죽이며 나를 노려보고 서 있었지만, 이내 달걀 잔을 내려놓고 에이다가 누운 침대 쪽으로 살며시 몸을 돌렸습니다.\n\n\"참 예뻐요!\" 똑같이 찌푸린 이마로, 똑같이 무뚝뚝한 태도로 그녀가 말했습니다.\n\n나는 미소로 동의했습니다.\n\n\"고아죠. 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"맞아요.\"\n\n\"그래도 아는 게 많겠죠? 춤출 줄도 알고, 악기도 다루고, 노래도 부르고? 프랑스어도 할 줄 알고, 지리며 지구의며 바느질이며 뭐든 다 하겠죠?\"\n\n\"틀림없이요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"난 못 해요.\" 그녀가 대답했습니다. \"쓰는 것 말고는 거의 아무것도 못 해요. 난 늘 엄마 대신 글을 써요. 두 분은 오늘 오후에 들어와서 내가 그것밖에 못 하는 걸 보고도 부끄러운 줄 모르더군요. 꼭 두 분 못된 성미 같았어요. 그래도 두 분은 자기가 아주 잘난 줄 알겠죠, 분명히!\"\n\n나는 그 가엾은 소녀가 금방이라도 울 듯한 것을 알아챘고, 아무 말 없이 다시 의자에 앉아 그녀를 (바라건대) 내 마음만큼이나 부드럽게 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"창피한 일이에요.\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"당신도 알잖아요. 이 집 전체가 창피해요. 아이들도 창피하고요. 나도 창피해요. 아빠는 비참하고, 그럴 만도 하죠! 프리실라는 술을 마셔요—늘 마셔대요. 오늘 그 여자한테서 술 냄새를 못 맡았다고 한다면 그건 큰 흉이고 새빨간 거짓말이에요. 저녁 시중을 들 때 술집이나 다름없었어요. 당신도 알잖아요!\"\n\n\"얘, 난 몰라요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"알아요.\" 그녀가 아주 매몰차게 말했습니다. \"모른다는 말은 하지 말아요. 당신은 알아요!\"\n\n\"어머, 얘!\" 내가 말했습니다. \"말도 못 하게 하면—\"\n\n\"지금 말하고 있잖아요. 당신도 알잖아요. 거짓말하지 마세요, 서머슨 양.\"\n\n\"얘,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"네가 내 말을 끝까지 들어주지 않는 한—\"\n\n\"끝까지 듣고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"아니, 듣고 싶을 거예요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"안 그러면 너무 억지스러우니까. 저녁 식사 때 하인이 내 곁에 오지 않아서 네가 하는 말을 몰랐던 거예요. 하지만 네 말을 의심하지 않고, 그런 얘기를 들으니 안타까워요.\"\n\n\"그걸 가지고 잘난 척할 건 없어요.\" 그녀가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 얘.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그러면 아주 어리석은 짓이겠지.\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 침대 곁에 서 있다가, 이제 (그러나 여전히 똑같이 못마땅한 얼굴로) 몸을 굽혀 에이다에게 입을 맞추었습니다. 그러고는 살며시 돌아와 내 의자 옆에 섰습니다. 그녀의 가슴은 보기 안타까울 만큼 괴롭게 들썩였는데, 나는 그것이 무척 가엾었지만 말하지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"차라리 죽어버렸으면 좋겠어요!\" 그녀가 터뜨렸습니다. \"우리 모두 죽어버렸으면 좋겠어요. 그게 우리한테 훨씬 나을 거예요.\"\n\n잠시 뒤에 그녀는 내 곁 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 내 옷자락에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 격하게 용서를 빌며 울었습니다. 나는 그녀를 달래며 일으키려 했지만, 그녀는 싫다고, 싫다고, 거기 그대로 있고 싶다고 울먹였습니다!\n\n\"당신은 여자아이들을 가르쳤다죠.\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"당신이 나를 가르쳐주기만 했다면, 나도 당신한테 배울 수 있었을 텐데! 난 너무나 비참한데, 당신이 너무 좋아요!\"\n\n나는 그녀를 내 곁에 앉게 하거나 그 무엇이라도 하게끔 달랠 수 없었습니다. 그녀는 그저 너덜너덜한 발판 하나를 자기가 무릎 꿇은 자리로 끌어와 그 위에 앉고는, 여전히 똑같이 내 옷자락을 붙들고 있을 뿐이었습니다. 그러다 그 가엾고 지친 소녀는 차츰 잠이 들었고, 그제야 나는 그녀의 머리가 내 무릎에 놓이도록 들어 올려 우리 둘을 숄로 덮었습니다. 난롯불은 꺼졌고, 그녀는 밤새도록 잿빛으로 식은 화격자 앞에서 그렇게 잠을 잤습니다. 처음에 나는 괴롭도록 말똥말똥해서, 눈을 감은 채 그날의 장면들 속으로 빠져들어 보려 애썼지만 헛수고였습니다. 마침내 그 장면들이 더디고 더디게 흐릿해지더니 뒤섞였습니다. 나는 내게 기댄 잠든 사람이 누구인지 알아볼 수 없게 되었습니다. 어떤 때는 에이다였다가, 어떤 때는 그토록 최근에 헤어졌다는 것이 믿기지 않는 리딩 시절의 옛 친구 가운데 하나였습니다. 어떤 때는 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 짓느라 기진맥진한 그 가엾은 미친 여인이었다가, 어떤 때는 황폐한 집의 권위 있는 누군가였습니다. 마지막에는 아무도 아니었고, 나 또한 아무도 아니었습니다.\n\n눈을 떠 보니, 흐릿한 낮이 안개와 힘없이 씨름하고 있었고, 더러운 얼굴의 작은 유령이 나를 빤히 응시하는 눈과 마주쳤습니다. 피피가 아기 침대를 기어 넘어, 잠옷과 모자 차림으로 내려와 있었던 것입니다. 아이는 어찌나 추웠던지, 마치 이가 죄다 새로 난 것처럼 딱딱 부딪치고 있었습니다.\n\n아침은 쌀쌀했고, 안개도 여전히 자욱한 듯했지만 — 굳이 \"듯했다\"고 적는 까닭은, 창문이 온통 때로 뒤덮여 한여름 햇살조차 침침하게 만들 지경이었기 때문입니다 — 그 이른 시각의 집 안이 얼마나 불편할지 충분히 짐작했고 런던도 무척 궁금했던 터라, 젤리비 양이 함께 산책을 나가자고 했을 때 저는 참 좋은 생각이라고 여겼습니다.\n\n\"엄마는 한참 동안 안 내려오실 거예요.\" 젤리비 양이 말했습니다. \"그러고 나서도 한 시간은 지나야 아침이 차려질까 말까 하고요. 다들 어찌나 꾸물대는지. 아빠로 말하면, 손에 잡히는 대로 챙겨 드시고 사무실로 가요. 제대로 된 아침 식사라고 부를 만한 건 한 번도 못 드세요. 프리실라가 전날 밤에 빵 한 덩이랑, 있으면 우유도 좀 내놓아 두거든요. 그런데 우유가 없을 때도 있고, 어떤 때는 고양이가 마셔버려요. 그래도 서머슨 양, 피곤하실 텐데요. 차라리 주무시는 게 낫지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"전혀 피곤하지 않아요.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"오히려 나가고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"정말 그러시다면,\" 젤리비 양이 대꾸했습니다. \"제 옷가지를 챙겨 입을게요.\"\n\n에이다도 같이 가겠다고 하더니 곧 움직이기 시작했습니다. 저는 피피에게 더 나은 무언가를 해줄 수가 없어서, 일단 씻겨주고 다시 제 침대에 눕혀주겠다고 제안했습니다. 피피는 더없이 얌전하게 응했는데, 씻는 내내 저를 빤히 쳐다보는 것이, 마치 평생 그토록 놀라본 적이 없고 앞으로도 다시는 없을 것 같은 표정이었습니다 — 분명 무척 처량해 보이기도 했지만 불평 한마디 없이, 다 끝나자마자 새근새근 잠들었습니다. 처음에는 그렇게 멋대로 굴어도 될지 마음이 두 갈래로 나뉘었지만, 이 집에서는 그런 일을 눈여겨볼 사람이 아무도 없으리라는 생각이 이내 들었습니다.\n\n피피를 재워 보내느라 부산을 떨고, 또 제 채비를 하고 에이다까지 거드느라 부산을 떠는 사이에 저는 어느새 온몸이 후끈 달아올랐습니다. 우리가 보니 젤리비 양은 글방 난롯불에 몸을 녹이려 애쓰고 있었는데, 그때 프리실라가 그을음투성이 응접실 촛대로 불을 붙이느라 양초를 통째로 던져 넣어 더 잘 타게 하고 있었습니다. 모든 것이 어젯밤 우리가 두고 간 그대로였고, 앞으로도 그대로 둘 작정인 게 분명했습니다. 아래층에는 만찬 식탁보가 그대로 깔린 채 아침상으로 쓰려고 남겨져 있었습니다. 빵 부스러기며 먼지며 휴지가 온 집 안에 널려 있었습니다. 백랍 단지 몇 개와 우유통 하나가 지하층 난간에 걸려 있었고, 문은 활짝 열려 있었습니다. 우리는 모퉁이를 돌다가 술집에서 나오며 입을 닦는 요리사와 마주쳤습니다. 요리사는 우리를 지나치며, 지금 몇 시인지 보러 갔다 오는 길이라고 일러주었습니다.\n\n그러나 요리사와 마주치기 전에, 우리는 발을 녹이려고 세이비스 인 앞을 깡충깡충 오르내리는 리처드와 마주쳤습니다. 리처드는 우리가 이렇게 일찍 움직이는 걸 보고 반갑게 놀라며, 기꺼이 산책에 함께하겠다고 했습니다. 그래서 리처드는 에이다를 맡았고, 젤리비 양과 저는 앞장섰습니다. 한마디 덧붙이자면, 젤리비 양은 다시 그 뾰로통한 태도로 되돌아가 있었고, 본인이 직접 말해주지 않았다면 저를 그리 좋아하는 줄은 정말 몰랐을 겁니다.\n\n\"어디로 가고 싶으세요?\" 젤리비 양이 물었습니다.\n\n\"아무 데나요.\" 제가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"아무 데나는 아무 데도 아니에요.\" 젤리비 양이 심술궂게 걸음을 멈추며 말했습니다.\n\n\"어쨌든 어디로든 가보자고요.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n그러자 젤리비 양은 저를 데리고 아주 빠르게 걸었습니다.\n\n\"난 상관없어요!\" 젤리비 양이 말했습니다. \"자, 서머슨 양이 증인이에요. 난 상관없다고요 — 하지만 그 번들번들하고 울퉁불퉁한 커다란 이마를 들이밀고 밤마다 밤마다 우리 집에 찾아온대도, 므두셀라만큼 늙을 때까지 그런대도, 난 그 사람한테 할 말이 한마디도 없어요. 그 사람이랑 엄마가 무슨 멍청이짓을 하는지 좀 보라고요!\"\n* 므두셀라: 구약성서에 나오는 인물로, 969세까지 살았다는 가장 장수한 사람.\n\n\"얘, 이러면 못써요!\" 젤리비 양이 내뱉은 그 말과, 거기에 실은 거센 강조를 두고 저는 타일렀습니다. \"자식 된 도리로서—\"\n\n\"아! 자식 된 도리 얘기는 꺼내지도 마세요, 서머슨 양. 부모 된 엄마의 도리는 어디 있는데요? 죄다 대중이며 아프리카한테 넘겨버렸겠죠, 뭐! 그럼 대중이랑 아프리카더러 자식 된 도리를 보이라고 하세요. 저보다 그쪽 일이 훨씬 더 크니까요. 충격받으셨겠죠, 짐작해요! 좋아요, 저도 충격받았어요. 그러니 우리 둘 다 충격받은 거고, 그걸로 끝이에요!\"\n\n젤리비 양은 저를 데리고 더더욱 빠르게 걸었습니다.\n\n\"하지만 그래도 다시 말하는데, 그 사람이 오고 또 오고 또 와도, 난 그 사람한테 할 말이 한마디도 없어요. 그 사람을 견딜 수가 없어요. 이 세상에 내가 미워하고 진저리치는 게 하나 있다면, 그건 그 사람이랑 엄마가 떠드는 그 소리예요. 우리 집 맞은편 깔린 돌들은 어쩜 그렇게 참을성 있게 거기 버티고 서서, 그 요란한 헛소리며 엄마의 살림 솜씨 같은, 앞뒤도 안 맞고 모순투성이인 꼴을 다 지켜볼 수 있는지 신기할 따름이에요!\"\n\n저는 젤리비 양이 어제 만찬 뒤에 나타난 그 젊은 신사 퀘일 씨를 두고 하는 말이라고 짐작할 수밖에 없었습니다. 그런데 리처드와 에이다가 깔깔 웃으며 빠른 걸음으로 다가와 우리에게 경주라도 할 셈이냐고 묻는 바람에, 저는 그 거북한 화제를 더 좇아야 하는 곤란을 면했습니다. 이렇게 말이 끊기자 젤리비 양은 입을 다물고 시무룩하게 제 옆을 걸었습니다. 그동안 저는 길게 이어지며 갖가지로 변하는 거리들이며, 벌써 오가는 수많은 사람들이며, 끊임없이 지나가고 다시 지나가는 숱한 탈것들이며, 진열창을 꾸미고 가게 안을 쓸어내는 분주한 채비며, 쓸어낸 쓰레기 더미를 헤집으며 핀이며 다른 부스러기를 슬쩍슬쩍 뒤지는 누더기 차림의 별난 생명체들을 신기하게 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"자, 사촌.\" 제 뒤에서 에이다를 향한 리처드의 명랑한 목소리가 들렸습니다. \"우리는 도무지 대법원에서 벗어날 수가 없나 봐! 어제 만난 그 자리를 다른 길로 돌아왔는데도 — 옥새에 두고 맹세하건대, 여기 또 그 노부인이 계셔!\"\n\n정말로, 노부인이 우리 바로 앞에 있었습니다. 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 미소 짓고는, 어제와 똑같은 후원자 같은 태도로 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이로군요! 정말, 정말 기뻐요!\"\n\n\"이른 시각에 나오셨네요, 부인.\" 노부인이 제게 인사를 건네기에 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그-럼요! 저는 보통 이 시각에 여길 산책한답니다. 법정이 열리기 전에요. 한적하거든요. 저는 여기서 하루 일과를 위해 생각을 가다듬어요.\" 노부인이 점잔을 빼며 말했습니다. \"하루 일과에는 생각이 아주 많이 필요하답니다. 대법원의 정의란 따라가기가 정말, 정말 어렵거든요.\"\n\n\"이분은 누구세요, 서머슨 양?\" 젤리비 양이 제 팔을 자기 팔에 더 바짝 끼며 속삭였습니다.\n\n작은 노부인의 귀는 놀랄 만큼 밝았습니다. 부인은 곧장 직접 대답했습니다.\n\n\"소송 당사자랍니다, 얘야. 잘 부탁해요. 저는 영광스럽게도 법정에 꼬박꼬박 나간답니다. 제 서류들을 들고서요. 또 다른 잔다이스 사건의 젊은 당사자분과 인사를 나누는 영광을 누리는 건가요?\" 노부인은 아주 낮게 무릎을 굽혔다가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 몸을 추스르고 말했습니다.\n\n어제의 경솔함을 만회하려 마음 졸이던 리처드가 싹싹하게, 젤리비 양은 그 소송과 관련이 없다고 설명했습니다.\n\n\"하!\" 노부인이 말했습니다. \"이 아가씨는 판결을 기다리지 않는군요? 그래도 늙어가긴 할 테죠. 하지만 그렇게까지 늙지는 않을 거예요. 오, 저런, 아니고말고요! 여기는 링컨스 인의 정원이랍니다. 저는 제 정원이라고 불러요. 여름철이면 아주 정자 같답니다. 새들이 곱게 노래하는 곳이지요. 저는 긴 휴정기의 대부분을 여기서 보내요. 사색하면서요. 긴 휴정기가 정말 길게 느껴지지요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n노부인이 그렇게 대답하기를 바라는 듯해서, 우리는 그렇다고 했습니다.\n\n\"나무에서 잎이 떨어지고, 대법원장님 법정에 바칠 꽃다발로 엮을 꽃이 더는 피지 않으면,\" 노부인이 말했습니다. \"휴정기가 채워지고, 요한계시록에 나오는 여섯 번째 봉인이 다시 위세를 떨치지요. 부디 와서 제 거처를 봐주세요. 제게 좋은 징조가 될 테니까요. 젊음과 희망과 아름다움이 거기 깃드는 일은 정말 드물거든요. 그 어느 것이라도 저를 찾아온 지 오래, 정말 오래되었답니다.\"\n* 여섯 번째 봉인: 요한계시록에서 어린 양이 일곱 봉인을 차례로 떼는데, 여섯 번째 봉인이 열리자 큰 지진과 함께 해가 검어지고 달이 핏빛으로 변한다.\n\n노부인은 제 손을 잡고서 저와 젤리비 양을 이끌었고, 리처드와 에이다에게도 따라오라고 손짓했습니다. 저는 어떻게 사양해야 할지 몰라 리처드에게 도움을 청하는 눈길을 보냈습니다. 리처드는 반쯤 재미있어하고 반쯤 호기심에 차 있었으며, 노부인의 기분을 상하게 하지 않고 어떻게 떼어낼지 도무지 갈피를 못 잡고 있었기에, 노부인은 계속 우리를 이끌었고 리처드와 에이다도 계속 따라왔습니다. 그동안 이 기묘한 안내자는 줄곧 환하게 겸양 떨며, 자기가 바로 가까이에 산다고 알려주었습니다.\n\n곧 드러났듯이 그 말은 정말이었습니다. 노부인이 어찌나 가까이에 사는지, 우리가 잠시 더 비위를 맞춰줄 겨를도 없이 부인은 벌써 집에 다다랐습니다. 작은 옆문으로 우리를 빠져나가게 한 노부인은 아주 뜻밖에도 좁은 뒷골목에 멈춰 섰는데, 그곳은 여관 담장 바로 바깥으로 이어진 몇몇 마당과 골목 가운데 한 자락이었습니다. 그러고는 말했습니다. \"여기가 제 거처랍니다. 부디 올라오세요!\"\n\n노부인이 멈춰 선 곳은 한 가게 앞이었는데, 그 위에는 이렇게 적혀 있었습니다. 크룩, 넝마와 병 도매상. 또 길고 가느다란 글씨로, 크룩, 선박용품 판매상이라고도 쓰여 있었습니다. 진열창 한쪽에는 빨간 제지 공장 그림이 있었는데, 거기서 짐수레 한 대가 헌 넝마 자루를 잔뜩 부리고 있었습니다. 다른 쪽에는 뼈 삽니다라는 글귀가 있었습니다. 또 다른 쪽에는 부엌 부산물 삽니다. 또 다른 쪽에는 고철 삽니다. 또 다른 쪽에는 휴지 삽니다. 또 다른 쪽에는 신사 숙녀용 의복 삽니다. 거기서는 모든 것을 사들이기만 할 뿐 파는 것은 아무것도 없는 듯했습니다. 진열창 곳곳에는 더러운 병이 잔뜩 있었습니다 — 구두약 병, 약병, 생강 맥주와 소다수 병, 절임 병, 포도주 병, 잉크병. 마지막 것을 언급하다 보니 떠오르는데, 그 가게는 몇 가지 자잘한 점에서 법조 동네에 자리 잡은 듯한, 이를테면 법률이 거느린 더러운 식객이자 의절한 친척 같은 분위기를 풍겼습니다. 잉크병이 아주 많았습니다. 문밖에는 낡고 초라한 책들을 얹은 채 기우뚱거리는 작은 좌판이 있었는데, \"법률 서적, 모두 9펜스\"라고 붙어 있었습니다. 제가 늘어놓은 글귀 가운데 몇몇은 법조 글씨체로 쓰여 있었는데, 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에서 본 서류며, 제가 그 회사로부터 그토록 오래 받아온 편지들과 똑같았습니다. 그 가운데 하나는 같은 글씨체였지만 가게 일과는 아무 상관이 없었으니, 마흔다섯 살의 점잖은 남자가 정서나 필사 일을 깔끔하고 신속하게 해드린다는 내용이었습니다. 연락처는 내부의 크룩 씨를 통해 니모에게. 파랗고 빨간 중고 가방 몇 개도 걸려 있었습니다. 가게 문 안쪽으로 조금 들어간 곳에는 낡고 쩍쩍 갈라진 양피지 두루마리와, 빛바래고 귀퉁이가 접힌 법률 서류가 수북이 쌓여 있었습니다. 고철처럼 한데 뒤엉킨 그 녹슨 열쇠들이 — 분명 수백 개는 되었을 텐데 — 한때 변호사 사무소의 방문이나 튼튼한 금고에 달려 있던 것이리라고 상상해도 좋을 듯했습니다. 받침대도 없이 들보에 매달려, 외다리 나무 저울 한쪽 접시에 얹혔다가 절반쯤 안팎으로 흘러넘친 그 너저분한 넝마 더미는, 갈가리 찢긴 법정 변호사의 띠와 법복일지도 모른다고 여길 만했습니다. 우리 모두가 안을 들여다보며 서 있을 때 리처드가 에이다와 제게 속삭였듯이, 저 구석에 한데 쌓인 채 아주 말끔하게 발라진 뼈들이 의뢰인들의 뼈라고 상상하기만 하면, 그림은 완성되었습니다.\n\n아직 안개가 끼고 어두운 데다, 게다가 가게는 링컨스 인 담장에 가려 빛이 채 두어 야드도 못 들어와 막혀 있었기에, 안경을 쓰고 털모자를 쓴 한 노인이 가게 안에서 들고 다니는 불 켜진 등불이 아니었다면 우리는 그토록 많은 것을 보지 못했을 겁니다. 문 쪽으로 돌아서던 노인이 그제야 우리를 알아보았습니다. 키가 작고 송장처럼 핼쑥하며 쪼그라든 그 노인은, 어깨 사이로 머리를 한쪽으로 푹 파묻은 채, 마치 속에서 불이라도 붙은 듯 입에서 눈에 보이는 연기 같은 숨을 뿜어내고 있었습니다. 목과 턱과 눈썹은 흰 털로 어찌나 서리처럼 뒤덮였고 핏줄과 쪼글쪼글한 살로 어찌나 울툭불툭한지, 가슴께부터 위로는 마치 눈 속에 파묻힌 늙은 나무뿌리 같았습니다.\n\n\"이봐요, 이봐요!\" 노인이 문으로 다가오며 말했습니다. \"뭐 팔 거라도 있소?\"\n\n우리는 자연스레 뒤로 물러나 안내자를 흘끔 보았습니다. 그동안 노부인은 주머니에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 집 문을 열려고 애쓰고 있었는데, 리처드가 부인에게 사는 곳을 보았으니 기쁘고, 시간에 쫓기는 터라 이만 가보겠다고 말했습니다. 그러나 부인은 그리 쉽게 떨쳐낼 상대가 아니었습니다. 부인은 잠깐만 올라와 자기 방을 봐달라고 기상천외하게 매달리며 간절히 졸랐고, 자기가 바라는 좋은 징조의 일부라면서, 해될 것 없는 그 방식으로 저를 기어이 안으로 데려가려고 했기에, 저는(다른 사람들이야 어쩌든) 응하는 것 말고는 도리가 없다고 보았습니다. 아마 우리 모두 다소간 호기심이 일었던 모양입니다. 어쨌든 노인까지 부인의 설득에 가세해, \"그래요, 그래! 비위를 맞춰줘요! 일 분도 안 걸려요! 들어와요, 들어와! 저쪽 문이 고장 났으면 가게로 들어와요!\" 하고 거들자, 우리는 다 같이 안으로 들어갔습니다. 리처드의 웃음 섞인 격려에 부추겨졌고, 그의 보호를 믿었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"제 집주인, 크룩이에요.\" 작은 노부인이 그 드높은 자리에서 노인을 굽어보듯 우리에게 그를 소개하며 말했습니다. \"이웃들 사이에서는 대법원장이라고 불린답니다. 이 가게는 대법원이라고 불리고요. 아주 별난 사람이에요. 정말 괴짜죠. 오, 단언컨대 정말 괴짜라니까요!\"\n\n노부인은 고개를 여러 번 절레절레 흔들고 손가락으로 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드려, 부디 너그러이 봐줘야 한다는 뜻을 우리에게 전했습니다. \"이 사람이 좀—그러니까—머리가 좀!\" 노부인이 자못 위엄을 차리며 말했습니다. 노인이 그 말을 엿듣고 웃었습니다.\n\n\"맞는 말이오.\" 노인이 등불을 들고 앞장서며 말했습니다. \"사람들이 날 대법원장이라 부르고 내 가게를 대법원이라 부르는 게 말이오. 그런데 왜 날 대법원장이라 부르고 내 가게를 대법원이라 부르는지, 짐작이 가시오?\"\n\n\"전혀 모르겠는데요.\" 리처드가 다소 무심하게 말했습니다.\n\n\"보다시피,\" 노인이 멈춰 서서 돌아보며 말했습니다. \"사람들이 말이오—이봐! 여기 곱디고운 머리카락 좀 보게! 아래층에 숙녀들 머리카락이 세 자루나 있지만, 이렇게 곱고 고운 건 하나도 없소. 이 빛깔이며, 이 결이라니!\"\n\n\"이제 됐어요, 영감님!\" 에이다의 머리타래 한 가닥을 자기 누런 손으로 끌어당기는 것을 강하게 못마땅해하며 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그런 무례를 범하지 않고도 우리 모두처럼 그저 감탄하시면 되잖아요.\"\n\n노인이 리처드를 향해 갑작스러운 눈길을 쏘아붙였는데, 그 바람에 에이다에게 쏠려 있던 제 주의마저 그쪽으로 돌아갔습니다. 깜짝 놀라 얼굴을 붉힌 에이다가 어찌나 놀랍도록 아름다웠던지, 그 작은 노부인 자신의 산만한 주의마저 붙들어 매는 듯했습니다. 그러나 에이다가 끼어들어 웃으며 그토록 진심 어린 감탄이라면 오히려 자랑스러울 따름이라고 말하자, 크룩 씨는 펄쩍 튀어나왔을 때만큼이나 별안간 본래의 자기 모습으로 움츠러들었습니다.\n\n\"보다시피, 난 여기 온갖 것을 이렇게나 많이 가지고 있소.\" 노인이 등불을 치켜들며 다시 말을 이었습니다. \"온갖 종류로 말이오. 이웃들 생각엔(하지만 그자들은 아무것도 몰라) 죄다 썩어가고 망가지고 폐허가 되어간다지. 그래서 나하고 내 가게한테 그런 세례명을 붙여준 거요. 게다가 난 묵은 양피지며 서류를 재고로 어찌나 많이 쌓아뒀는지. 또 녹이며 곰팡이며 거미줄을 좋아하지. 내 그물에 걸리는 건 죄다 물고기란 말이오. 게다가 난 한번 손에 쥔 건 무엇이든 내놓지를 못하고(이웃들 생각엔 그렇다지만, 그자들이 뭘 알겠소?), 무엇 하나 바꾸지도 못하고, 내 주위에서 쓸거나 닦거나 청소하거나 수리하는 꼴은 도무지 못 견디지. 그래서 대법원이라는 못된 이름이 붙은 거요. 난 상관없소. 그분이 여관에서 자리에 앉아 계실 때면, 거의 매일 우리 고귀하고 박식하신 형제분을 뵈러 간다오. 그분은 나를 알은체하지 않지만, 난 그분을 알은체하지. 우리 둘 사이엔 별 차이가 없소. 둘 다 뒤죽박죽 속에서 아등바등 살아가니까. 이봐, 레이디 제인!\"\n\n커다란 잿빛 고양이 한 마리가 근처 선반에서 노인의 어깨로 펄쩍 뛰어오르는 바람에 우리 모두 깜짝 놀랐습니다.\n\n\"이봐! 어떻게 할퀴는지 보여줘 봐. 이봐! 찢어버려, 우리 아가씨!\" 주인이 말했습니다.\n\n고양이가 펄쩍 뛰어내리더니 호랑이 같은 발톱으로 넝마 뭉치를 갈가리 찢었는데, 그 소리가 어찌나 듣기에 소름이 돋던지 이가 다 시릴 지경이었습니다.\n\n\"내가 누구한테든 부추기면 이놈은 그만큼 해치울 거요.\" 노인이 말했습니다. \"난 여러 잡다한 것들 중에 고양이 가죽도 다루는데, 이놈 가죽도 내게 들어온 거였소. 보다시피 아주 좋은 가죽이지만, 난 벗겨내진 않았다오! 하긴 그건 대법원 방식하곤 다르다고, 당신들은 말하겠지만!\"\n\n노인은 그동안 우리를 데리고 가게를 가로질러 왔고, 이제 가게 뒤편의 문을 열었는데, 그 문은 집 현관으로 통했습니다. 노인이 손을 자물쇠에 얹은 채 서 있자, 작은 노부인이 나가기 전에 점잖게 한마디 건넸습니다. \"그만하면 됐어요, 크룩. 마음은 고맙지만 사람 피곤하게 하는군요. 제 젊은 친구들은 시간에 쫓기는 몸이랍니다. 저도 내드릴 시간이 없고요. 곧 법정에 나가야 하니까요. 제 젊은 친구들은 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이에요.\"\n\n\"잔다이스!\" 노인이 흠칫 놀라며 말했습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 대 잔다이스. 그 대단한 소송 말이에요, 크룩.\" 세입자가 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 노인이 골똘히 놀란 어조로, 아까보다 더 휘둥그레진 눈으로 외쳤습니다. \"이거 참!\"\n\n노인이 별안간 넋을 잃은 듯 우리를 어찌나 신기하게 바라보던지, 리처드가 말했습니다. \"아니, 영감님은 그 고귀하고 박식하신 형제분, 그러니까 또 다른 대법원장 앞에 걸린 사건들에 꽤나 마음을 쓰시나 보군요!\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 노인이 멍하니 말했습니다. \"그렇고말고! 그래, 당신 이름은—\"\n\n\"리처드 카스톤입니다.\"\n\n\"카스톤.\" 노인이 검지로 그 이름을 천천히 짚어가며 되뇌었고, 이어 다른 사람들의 이름도 손가락 하나씩에 짚어가며 읊었습니다. \"그래. 바버리라는 이름이 있었고, 클레어라는 이름이 있었고, 데드록이라는 이름도 있었지, 아마.\"\n\n\"이 사람은 진짜 봉급 받는 대법원장만큼이나 그 사건을 훤히 아는데!\" 리처드가 깜짝 놀라 에이다와 제게 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래!\" 노인이 천천히 넋을 차리며 말했습니다. \"그래! 톰 잔다이스—친척이라 이렇게 불러도 양해해주시오. 하지만 그분은 법정에서 다른 이름으론 한 번도 불린 적이 없었고, 거기선—지금 이분만큼이나 잘 알려져 있었소.\" 노인이 세입자를 향해 살짝 고갯짓했습니다. \"톰 잔다이스는 여기 자주 들렀소. 사건이 열렸거나 열릴 참이면 안절부절못하고 어슬렁거리는 버릇이 들어서, 자그마한 가게 주인들한테 말을 걸며 무슨 일이 있어도 대법원에는 발 들이지 말라고 일렀지. '왜냐면,' 그분이 그러길, '그건 느린 방앗간에 갈려 산산조각 나는 거요. 약한 불에 천천히 구워지는 거요. 벌 한 마리 한 마리한테 쏘여 죽는 거요. 물방울에 빠져 죽는 거요. 낟알 하나하나에 미쳐가는 거요.' 그분은 바로 저 아가씨가 서 있는 자리에서, 거의 제 손으로 목숨을 끊을 뻔했다오. 정말 아슬아슬했지.\"\n\n우리는 소름이 끼친 채 귀를 기울였습니다.\n\n\"그분이 그 짓을 한 날, 저 문으로 들어왔소.\" 노인이 가게를 가로지르는 상상의 자취를 천천히 가리키며 말했습니다. \"—온 동네가 몇 달 전부터, 그분이 머잖아 분명 그럴 거라고 입을 모았지—그날 그분이 저 문으로 들어와 저쪽으로 걸어와선, 저기 놓인 의자에 앉더니, 내게 부탁하더군(그때 난 죽도록 더 젊었던 걸 짐작하실 거요), 포도주 한 파인트만 가져다 달라고. '왜냐면,' 그분이 그러길, '크룩, 난 무척 우울하오. 내 사건이 또 열렸는데, 그 어느 때보다 판결에 가까워진 것 같소.' 난 그분을 혼자 두고 싶지 않아서, 길 건너편, 우리 골목(대법원 골목 말이오) 맞은편에 있는 술집으로 가자고 설득했소. 그러고 따라가서 창문으로 들여다보니, 그분이 난롯가 안락의자에 앉아 일행과 함께, 내 보기엔 편안하게 있더군. 그런데 여기 채 돌아오기도 전에, 총소리가 메아리치고 덜컹대며 여관 안쪽으로 곧장 울려 퍼지는 소리가 들렸소. 난 뛰쳐나갔고—이웃들도 뛰쳐나왔고—스무 명이 한꺼번에 외쳤지, '톰 잔다이스!'\"\n\n노인은 말을 멈추고 우리를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 등불 속을 들여다보더니 불을 훅 끄고 등불을 닫았습니다.\n\n\"우리 짐작이 맞았소. 여기 계신 분들께 굳이 말할 것도 없지. 이봐! 그날 오후, 사건이 열리는 동안 온 동네가 어찌나 법정으로 몰려들었던지! 우리 고귀하고 박식하신 형제분이며 다른 양반들 모두가 여느 때처럼 아등바등 뒤죽박죽 일을 처리하면서, 사건의 마지막 사실에 대해선 한마디도 못 들은 척, 아니면 설령 어쩌다 들었더라도—오, 저런!—자기들과는 아무 상관 없다는 척하려고 애쓰던 꼴이라니!\"\n\n에이다의 핏기는 완전히 가셨고, 리처드도 그에 못잖게 창백했습니다. 저 자신의 감정으로 미루어 봐도 그럴 만했습니다. 저는 그 소송의 당사자도 아니었건만, 그토록 풋풋하고 시련을 모르는 마음에, 많은 이의 머릿속에 그토록 끔찍한 기억으로 얽힌 그 길고 긴 비참함을 물려받는다는 것이 충격일 수밖에 없었습니다. 저에게는 또 다른 불편함이 있었으니, 우리를 그곳으로 데려온 그 가엾고 얼이 반쯤 나간 사람에게 이 고통스러운 이야기가 들어맞는다는 점이었습니다. 그러나 놀랍게도 부인은 그 점을 전혀 의식하지 못한 채, 그저 다시 위층으로 앞장설 뿐이었고, 평범한 인간의 허물을 너그러이 보는 우월한 존재 같은 태도로, 자기 집주인이 \"머리가 좀—아시잖아요!\" 하고 우리에게 일러주었습니다.\n\n노부인은 집 꼭대기 층, 제법 큰 방에 살았는데, 그곳에서는 링컨스 인 홀이 빠끔히 내다보였습니다. 애초에 부인이 그곳에 거처를 정한 주된 이유가 이것인 듯했습니다. 부인은 밤이면, 특히 달빛이 비치면 그것을 바라볼 수 있다고 했습니다. 방은 깨끗했지만, 정말이지 너무나 휑했습니다. 저는 가구라고는 더없이 빈약한 필수품 몇 가지뿐인 것을 알아챘습니다. 벽에는 책에서 뜯어낸 대법원장들과 법정 변호사들의 낡은 판화 몇 장이 풀로 붙어 있었고, 부인의 말로는 \"서류가 들어 있다\"는 손가방과 작업 가방이 대여섯 개쯤 있었습니다. 벽난로 안에는 석탄도 재도 없었고, 어디에도 옷가지 한 점, 음식 한 조각 보이지 않았습니다. 열린 찬장 선반 위에는 접시 한두 개, 잔 한두 개 따위가 있었지만 죄다 바싹 마른 채 텅 비어 있었습니다. 방 안을 둘러보던 저는, 부인의 그 야윈 행색에 제가 전에 헤아렸던 것보다 한층 더 가슴 아픈 의미가 담겨 있다고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"정말이지 더없는 영광이에요.\" 가엾은 안주인이 더없이 상냥하게 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이 이렇게 찾아와 주시다니요. 또 이 징조에 정말 큰 빚을 졌어요. 한적한 곳이지요. 그러고 보면요. 저는 거처에 제약이 있답니다. 대법원장님 곁을 지켜야 할 필요 때문에요. 여기서 여러 해를 살았어요. 낮은 법정에서, 저녁과 밤은 여기서 보내지요. 밤이 길게 느껴져요. 잠은 거의 못 자고 생각은 많이 하거든요. 그야 물론, 대법원에 얽힌 몸이니 어쩔 수 없는 일이지만요. 초콜릿을 대접하지 못해 죄송해요. 머잖아 판결이 날 거라, 그러면 살림을 더 나은 수준으로 꾸릴 생각이랍니다. 지금으로선, 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들께(아주 은밀히) 털어놓아도 괜찮겠지요. 가끔은 품위 있는 행색을 유지하기가 어렵다는 걸요. 여기서 추위를 느껴왔어요. 추위보다 더 매서운 무언가를 느낀 적도 있고요. 별일 아니랍니다. 이런 누추한 이야기를 꺼낸 걸 부디 용서하세요.\"\n\n노부인은 길고 낮은 다락방 창문의 커튼을 반쯤 젖히고, 거기 걸린 수많은 새장으로 우리의 눈길을 돌렸는데, 몇몇 새장에는 여러 마리가 들어 있었습니다. 종다리, 홍방울새, 황금방울새가 있었으니—적어도 스무 마리는 되어 보였습니다.\n\n\"제가 이 작은 것들을 기르기 시작한 건요,\" 부인이 말했습니다. \"피후견인들이라면 금방 알아들을 만한 목적에서랍니다. 이 아이들을 자유로이 풀어주려는 뜻에서요. 제 판결이 내려지면요. 그-럼요! 그래도 이 아이들은 갇혀서 죽어요. 가엾고 어리석은 것들의 목숨은 대법원 절차에 비하면 어찌나 짧은지, 한 마리 한 마리, 모은 새들이 죄다 거듭거듭 죽어 나갔답니다. 그거 아세요, 저는 의심스러워요. 이 아이들이 다 어리긴 해도, 그중 한 마리라도 자유로워질 때까지 살아 있을지 말이에요! 정말, 정말 비통한 일 아닌가요?\"\n\n노부인은 가끔 질문을 던졌지만, 정작 대답을 기대하는 법은 없었고, 혼자 있을 때 으레 그러던 버릇대로 두서없이 말을 이어갔습니다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 부인이 말을 이었습니다. \"단언컨대, 저는 가끔 진심으로 의심한답니다. 일이 아직 매듭지어지지 않고, 여섯 번째 봉인, 곧 큰 봉인이 여전히 위세를 떨치는 동안, 제가 그 숱한 새들을 발견했듯이, 언젠가 저 자신도 여기 뻣뻣하게 넋을 잃고 누운 채 발견되는 게 아닐까 하고요!\"\n\n리처드는 에이다의 안쓰러운 눈빛에서 읽어낸 마음에 답하듯, 그 틈을 타 아무도 모르게 돈을 살그머니 벽난로 선반 위에 올려놓았습니다. 우리는 다 같이 새를 살펴보는 척하며 새장으로 더 다가갔습니다.\n\n\"이 아이들이 너무 많이 노래하도록 둘 수가 없어요.\" 작은 노부인이 말했습니다. \"왜냐면(이상하게 들리시겠지만), 제가 법정에서 변론을 따라가는 동안 이 아이들이 노래하고 있다고 생각하면 정신이 어지러워지거든요. 제 정신은 정말, 정말 또렷해야 하는데 말이죠! 이름은 다음번에 알려드릴게요. 지금 말고요. 이렇게 좋은 징조가 깃든 날에는, 이 아이들이 노래하고 싶은 만큼 노래해도 좋아요. 젊음을 기려서,\" 미소와 절. \"희망을 기려서,\" 미소와 절. \"그리고 아름다움을 기려서요,\" 미소와 절. \"자! 빛을 듬뿍 들이도록 하지요.\"\n\n새들이 부스럭대며 지저귀기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"바람은 마음껏 들이지 못해요.\" 작은 노부인이 말했습니다—방 안이 답답해서 바람을 들이면 한결 나았을 텐데도요—\"아래층에서 보신 레이디 제인이라는 고양이가 이 아이들 목숨을 노리거든요. 그 녀석이 바깥 난간에 몇 시간이고 웅크리고 앉아 있어요. 제가 알아냈는데요,\" 부인이 알쏭달쏭하게 속삭였습니다. \"그 녀석의 타고난 잔인함은, 이 아이들이 다시 자유를 얻을까 봐 질투 어린 두려움에 더 날카로워진답니다. 제가 기다리는 판결이 머잖아 내려질 테니까요. 그 녀석은 교활하고 악의가 가득해요. 가끔은 그 녀석이 고양이가 아니라 옛말에 나오는 늑대가 아닐까 반쯤 믿어진다니까요. 그 녀석을 문에서 떼어놓기가 정말 어렵거든요.\"\n\n근처 어딘가의 종소리가 그 가엾은 영혼에게 아홉 시 반이 되었음을 일깨워주자, 우리가 스스로 했더라면 쉽지 않았을 일—방문을 끝맺는 일—을 종소리가 대신 해주었습니다. 부인은 들어올 때 탁자에 올려놓았던 작은 서류 가방을 황급히 집어 들고, 우리도 법정에 가는 길이냐고 물었습니다. 우리가 아니라고, 결코 부인을 붙잡지 않겠다고 답하자, 부인은 우리를 배웅하러 아래층까지 가려고 문을 열었습니다.\n\n\"이런 징조가 있는 날에는, 평소보다 더더욱 제가 대법원장님이 들어오시기 전에 그곳에 가 있어야 해요.\" 부인이 말했습니다. \"맨 먼저 제 사건을 언급하실지도 모르거든요. 오늘 아침엔 그분이 맨 먼저 제 사건을 언급하실 거라는 예감이 들어요.\"\n\n부인은 아래층으로 내려가면서, 온 집 안이 집주인이 한 점 한 점 사들였으나 팔 마음이 없는 기묘한 잡동사니로 가득 차 있다고 속삭여 일러주었는데, 그것도 집주인이 머리가 좀 모자란 탓이라고 했습니다. 이것은 이 층에서였습니다. 그런데 부인은 그보다 앞서 삼 층에서 한 번 멈춰 서서, 거기 있는 어두운 문 하나를 말없이 가리켰던 적이 있습니다.\n\n\"단 하나뿐인 다른 세입자예요.\" 이제 부인이 속삭이며 설명했습니다. \"법률 필경사죠. 이 동네 골목 아이들은 그 사람이 악마에게 자기를 팔았다고 한답니다. 그 돈으로 뭘 했는지는 모르겠어요. 쉿!\"\n\n* 삼 층, 이 층: 영국식 층수 표기로, 영국에서는 우리식 일 층을 ground floor, 이 층을 first floor라 부른다. 여기서는 우리식 이 층(원문 first floor), 삼 층(원문 second floor)에 해당한다.\n\n들어올 때 가게를 지나왔듯이 나갈 때도 가게를 지나가는데, 우리가 보니 노인은 바닥에 난 우물 같은 구멍에 휴지 꾸러미를 한 무더기 쟁여 넣고 있었습니다. 이마에 땀이 송골송골 맺힌 채 열심히 일하는 듯했고, 곁에 분필을 한 자루 두고서, 꾸러미나 뭉치를 하나씩 내려놓을 때마다 벽 판자에 삐뚤빼뚤한 표시를 했습니다.\n\n리처드와 에이다, 젤리비 양, 그리고 작은 노부인이 그를 지나쳐 갔고, 저도 막 지나가려는데 노인이 제 팔을 건드려 멈춰 세우더니, 벽에 J라는 글자를 분필로 적었습니다—아주 기묘한 방식으로, 글자의 끝에서 시작해 거꾸로 모양을 잡아가면서요. 그것은 대문자였고, 인쇄체가 아니라, 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소의 어느 사무원이라도 썼을 법한 그런 글자였습니다.\n\n\"이걸 읽을 수 있소?\" 노인이 날카로운 눈길로 제게 물었습니다.\n\n\"물론이죠.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"아주 또렷한데요.\"\n\n\"뭐요?\"\n\"J예요.\"\n\n노인은 제게 다시 한 번 눈길을 던지고, 문 쪽으로도 눈길을 던지더니, 그것을 지우고 그 자리에 \"a\"를 만들어 넣고(이번엔 대문자가 아니었습니다), \"이건 뭐요?\" 하고 물었습니다.\n\n저는 알려주었습니다. 그러자 노인은 그것을 지우고 \"r\"이라는 글자를 만들어 똑같은 질문을 던졌습니다. 노인은 빠르게 이어가며, 똑같이 기묘한 방식으로, 글자의 끝과 아래쪽에서 시작해, 벽에 두 글자를 한꺼번에 남겨두는 일이 한 번도 없이, 잔다이스라는 단어를 완성했습니다.\n\n\"이건 무슨 글자가 되오?\" 노인이 제게 물었습니다.\n\n제가 알려주자 노인은 웃었습니다. 그러고는 그 똑같이 별난 방식으로, 하지만 그 똑같은 빠르기로, 황폐한 집이라는 단어를 이루는 글자들을 하나씩 만들었다가 하나씩 지웠습니다. 저는 다소 놀라며 그것도 읽어냈고, 노인은 다시 웃었습니다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 노인이 분필을 내려놓으며 말했습니다. \"보다시피 난 기억으로 베껴 쓰는 데 재주가 있다오, 아가씨. 읽지도 쓰지도 못하지만 말이오.\"\n\n노인이 어찌나 거슬려 보이고, 그 고양이가 마치 제가 위층 새들의 피붙이라도 되는 양 어찌나 사납게 저를 노려보던지, 리처드가 문간에 나타나 이렇게 말해주었을 때 저는 정말이지 마음이 놓였습니다. \"서머슨 양, 설마 머리카락을 팔려고 흥정하시는 건 아니겠죠. 넘어가지 마세요. 아래층에 세 자루면 크룩 씨한테는 차고 넘쳐요!\"\n\n저는 지체 없이 크룩 씨에게 안녕히 계시라 인사하고 밖에서 친구들과 합류했습니다. 거기서 우리는 작은 노부인과 작별했는데, 부인은 자못 격식을 차려 우리에게 축복을 빌어주고, 에이다와 제게 재산을 물려주겠다던 어제의 다짐을 거듭 일러주었습니다. 마침내 그 골목들을 벗어나기 전에 우리가 뒤를 돌아보니, 크룩 씨가 안경을 쓴 채 어깨에 고양이를 얹고 가게 문간에 서서 우리 뒷모습을 바라보고 있었는데, 고양이 꼬리가 그 털모자 한쪽 옆으로 마치 길쭉한 깃털처럼 곧추서 있었습니다.\n\n\"런던에서 보낸 아침치고는 제법 모험이었어!\" 리처드가 한숨을 쉬며 말했습니다. \"아아, 사촌, 사촌, 대법원이란 정말 지긋지긋한 말이야!\"\n\n\"제게도 그래요. 기억하는 한 줄곧 그랬고요.\" 에이다가 대꾸했습니다. \"수많은 친척이며 다른 사람들의 원수가—제가 그런 모양이듯이—되어야 하고, 그들 또한 제 원수가—그런 모양이듯이—되어야 하고, 어떻게, 왜인지도 모른 채 우리 모두가 서로를 파멸시키며, 평생 끊임없는 의심과 불화 속에 살아가야 한다니 슬퍼요. 어딘가엔 분명 옳은 게 있을 텐데, 정직한 판사가 진심으로 애써도 이 긴 세월 내내 그게 어디 있는지 찾아내지 못했다니, 정말 이상해요.\"\n\n\"아아, 사촌!\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"정말 이상하지! 이 모든 낭비투성이에 제멋대로인 장기 놀음이란 게 정말 이상해. 어제 그 차분한 법정이 그토록 태연하게 굴러가는 걸 보면서, 판 위에 놓인 말들의 비참함을 떠올리니, 머리도 아프고 가슴도 아프더라. 사람들이 바보도 악당도 아니라면 어떻게 이런 일이 벌어지는지 의아해서 머리가 아팠고, 그들이 바보거나 악당일 수도 있다고 생각하니 가슴이 아팠어. 하지만 어쨌든, 에이다—에이다라고 불러도 될까?\"\n\n\"물론이죠, 리처드 사촌.\"\n\n\"하지만 어쨌든, 대법원은 우리한테는 그 나쁜 영향을 조금도 못 미칠 거야. 고마운 친척 어른 덕분에 우린 다행히 한자리에 모였고, 이제 대법원도 우릴 갈라놓진 못해!\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요, 리처드 사촌!\" 에이다가 부드럽게 말했습니다.\n\n젤리비 양이 제 팔을 꼭 쥐며 제게 의미심장한 눈길을 보냈습니다. 저는 미소로 답했고, 우리는 돌아오는 나머지 길을 아주 즐겁게 걸었습니다.\n\n우리가 도착하고 반 시간이 지나서야 젤리비 부인이 나타났고, 한 시간이 흐르는 동안 아침 식사에 필요한 갖가지 물건이 하나씩 하나씩 식당으로 어슬렁어슬렁 흘러들어 왔습니다. 젤리비 부인이 여느 사람처럼 잠자리에 들었다가 일어났음을 저는 의심하지 않지만, 부인은 옷을 갈아입은 기색이라곤 전혀 없었습니다. 부인은 아침 식사 내내 무척 바빴는데, 아침 우편으로 보리오불라가 관련 서신이 잔뜩 도착해, 그 탓에(부인 말로는) 분주한 하루를 보내게 생겼기 때문입니다. 아이들은 여기저기 나뒹굴며 다친 자국을 다리에 흠집처럼 새겼는데, 그 다리는 그야말로 작은 고난의 달력이었습니다. 피피는 한 시간 반 동안 사라졌다가 경관 손에 이끌려 뉴게이트 시장에서 돌아왔습니다. 젤리비 부인이 피피가 사라진 일과 가족의 품으로 돌아온 일을 다 같이 그토록 태연하게 받아들이는 데에는 우리 모두 놀랐습니다.\n\n그때쯤 부인은 끈질기게 캐디에게 받아쓰기를 시키고 있었고, 캐디는 우리가 처음 보았을 때처럼 빠르게 다시 잉크투성이 꼴로 되돌아가고 있었습니다. 한 시에 우리를 태울 무개 마차가 왔고, 짐을 실을 짐수레도 왔습니다. 젤리비 부인은 좋은 친구 잔다이스 씨에게 안부를 잔뜩 전해달라고 우리에게 신신당부했습니다. 캐디는 우리가 떠나는 것을 보려고 책상을 떠나, 복도에서 제게 입을 맞추고는, 펜을 깨물며 계단에 서서 흐느꼈습니다. 다행히도 피피는 잠들어 있어 이별의 아픔을 면했습니다(피피가 저를 찾아 뉴게이트 시장에 갔던 게 아닐까 하는 께름칙한 마음이 들지 않은 건 아니었습니다). 그리고 다른 아이들은 죄다 사륜마차 뒤에 올라탔다가 떨어졌고, 우리가 세이비스 여관 구역을 빠져나가며 굴러갈 때, 아이들이 그 일대에 흩어져 있는 것을 우리는 무척 안쓰러운 마음으로 바라보았습니다.\n\n날이 한결 환하게 갰고, 우리가 서쪽으로 갈수록 더욱 환해졌다. 우리는 햇살과 신선한 바람을 가르며 길을 갔는데, 거리가 끝없이 이어지는 광경에, 상점들이 휘황찬란하게 빛나는 모습에, 어마어마한 마차 행렬에, 그리고 더 좋아진 날씨가 빛깔도 가지가지인 꽃처럼 거리로 불러낸 듯한 사람들 무리에 갈수록 감탄했다. 이윽고 우리는 그 놀라운 도시를 벗어나, 그것만으로도 내 눈에는 제법 큰 도시로 보일 만한 변두리를 지나기 시작했고, 마침내 다시 진짜 시골길로 접어들었다. 풍차가 있고, 짚가리 마당이 있고, 이정표가 있고, 농부들의 짐마차가 있고, 묵은 건초 냄새가 나고, 흔들리는 간판이 있고, 말 물통이 있는 길. 나무와 들판과 산울타리가 있는 길. 눈앞에 펼쳐진 초록빛 풍경과 등 뒤에 자리한 거대한 대도시를 바라보는 것은 즐거웠다. 그러다가 붉은 마구를 두르고 맑은 소리를 내는 방울을 단 아름다운 말들이 줄지어 끄는 짐마차가 그 음악과 함께 우리를 스쳐 지날 때, 나는 우리 셋이 다 그 방울 소리에 맞춰 노래라도 부를 수 있었으리라 믿는다. 주위의 분위기가 그토록 흥겨웠다.\n\n\"이 길 내내 내 이름의 주인공인 휘팅턴이 떠오르더라니.\" 리처드가 말했다. \"그런데 저 짐마차가 마지막 화룡점정이군. 어이! 무슨 일이지?\"\n\n우리가 멈춰 섰는데, 짐마차도 멈춰 섰다. 말들이 서자 그 음악이 바뀌어 잔잔한 딸랑임으로 잦아들었다. 다만 말이 머리를 흔들거나 몸을 떨 때마다 방울 소리가 한차례 소나기처럼 흩뿌려졌다.\n\n\"우리 마부가 짐마차꾼을 부르고 있어.\" 리처드가 말했다. \"그리고 짐마차꾼은 우리 쪽으로 되돌아오는군. 안녕하시오, 친구!\" 짐마차꾼이 우리 마차 문 앞에 와 있었다. \"아니, 이거 참 기막힌 일인데!\" 리처드가 그 사람을 유심히 보며 덧붙였다. \"이 사람 모자에 네 이름이 적혀 있어, 에이다!\"\n\n그의 모자에는 우리 이름이 다 적혀 있었다. 모자 띠 안에 작은 쪽지 세 장이 끼워져 있었는데, 하나는 에이다에게, 하나는 리처드에게, 하나는 나에게 온 것이었다. 짐마당꾼은 먼저 이름을 큰 소리로 읽고 나서 우리 각자에게 그것을 건네주었다. 누가 보낸 거냐는 리처드의 물음에 그는 짤막하게 대답했다. \"주인 어른께서요, 나리, 괜찮으시다면요.\" 그러고는 부드러운 사발 같은 모자를 다시 쓰고, 채찍을 철썩 울리고, 그 음악을 다시 깨우더니, 가락에 실려 멀어져 갔다.\n\n\"저게 잔다이스 씨의 짐마차요?\" 리처드가 우리 마부 소년을 향해 외치며 물었다.\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 그가 대답했다. \"런던으로 가는 길입니다.\"\n\n우리는 쪽지를 펼쳤다. 모두 똑같은 내용으로, 또박또박 꾸밈없는 필체로 이런 말이 적혀 있었다.\n\n나는, 사랑하는 이여, 우리가 어느 쪽에도 거리낌이나 부담 없이 편안하게 만나기를 고대한다. 그래서 우리가 오랜 벗처럼 만나, 지난 일은 당연한 것으로 묻어두자고 제안하는 바이다. 어쩌면 너에게도 홀가분할 것이고, 나에게는 분명히 그러할 것이니, 그럼 너에게 사랑을 전한다.\n\n존 잔다이스\n\n나는 두 동행보다 어쩌면 놀랄 이유가 덜했을 것이다. 그토록 오랜 세월 나의 은인이자 이 세상에서 유일하게 기댈 데였던 분에게 감사를 전할 기회를 아직 한 번도 누리지 못했기 때문이다. 어떻게 감사를 전할지 생각해 본 적도 없었다. 내 고마움이 그러기에는 가슴속에 너무 깊이 자리하고 있었으니까. 그러나 이제 나는 어떻게 하면 감사를 전하지 않으면서 그분을 만날 수 있을까 생각하기 시작했고, 그것이 실로 무척 어려우리라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n그 쪽지는 리처드와 에이다에게도, 어디서 비롯되었는지는 분명히 알지 못한 채 둘 다 막연히 품고 있던 인상을 되살려 놓았다. 그것은 사촌 잔다이스가 자기가 베푼 어떤 친절에 대해서도 감사 인사를 결코 견디지 못하며, 감사를 받느니 차라리 더없이 별난 술수와 회피를 동원하거나 심지어 도망쳐 버리기까지 하리라는 것이었다. 에이다는 아주 어린아이였을 적에 어머니가 들려준 이야기를 어렴풋이 기억했다. 그가 한번은 어머니에게 보기 드문 후한 일을 베풀었는데, 어머니가 감사를 전하러 그의 집에 갔더니, 그가 마침 창문 너머로 어머니가 문간으로 다가오는 것을 보고는 곧장 뒷문으로 달아나 석 달 동안 소식이 없었다는 것이다. 이 이야기는 같은 주제로 한참 더 이어졌고, 사실 하루 종일 이어져서, 우리는 거의 다른 이야기는 하지 않았다. 어쩌다 다른 화제로 빠지더라도 이내 이 이야기로 돌아와, 그 집은 어떻게 생겼을지, 언제 도착할지, 도착하자마자 잔다이스 씨를 뵙게 될지 아니면 좀 기다린 뒤일지, 그분이 우리에게 무슨 말씀을 하실지, 우리는 그분께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 궁금해했다. 이 모든 것을 우리는 거듭거듭 궁금해했다.\n\n길은 말들에게는 몹시 험했지만 보도는 대체로 좋았다. 그래서 우리는 마차에서 내려 언덕은 모조리 걸어 올라갔고, 그게 어찌나 좋았던지 꼭대기에 닿으면 평평한 땅에서도 걸음을 더 이어갔다. 바닛에는 우리를 위한 다른 말들이 기다리고 있었지만, 마침 먹이를 막 먹은 참이라 우리는 또 그 말들을 기다려야 했고, 마차가 따라오기 전에 공유지와 옛 전쟁터를 가로질러 한참 동안 상쾌하게 걸었다. 이런 지체들로 여정이 길어져, 짧은 낮이 다 가고 긴 밤이 내려앉은 뒤에야 우리는 세인트올번스에 닿았다. 우리가 알기로 황폐한 집은 그 마을 근처에 있었다.\n\n그때쯤 우리는 어찌나 마음을 졸이고 안절부절못했던지, 옛 거리의 돌바닥 위를 덜컹거리며 지날 무렵에는 리처드조차 도로 돌아가고 싶은 이치에 닿지 않는 충동을 느꼈노라고 털어놓았다. 그가 밤이 매섭고 서리가 내려 정성껏 몸을 감싸준 에이다와 나로 말하자면, 우리는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨었다. 마을을 벗어나 모퉁이를 돌 때, 한참 전부터 우리의 고조된 기대에 동조해 오던 마부 소년이 뒤를 돌아보며 고개를 끄덕인다고 리처드가 일러주자, 우리는 둘 다 마차 안에서 일어서서(에이다가 흔들려 넘어지지 않도록 리처드가 붙잡아 주었다) 우리의 목적지를 찾아 탁 트인 들판과 별빛 어린 밤하늘을 둘러보았다. 우리 앞 언덕 꼭대기에 불빛 하나가 반짝이고 있었고, 마부가 채찍으로 그것을 가리키며 \"저게 황폐한 집입니다!\"라고 외치더니, 말들을 가벼운 구보로 몰아 비탈길인데도 어찌나 빠른 속도로 우리를 앞으로 데려갔던지, 바퀴가 물레방아에서 튀는 물보라처럼 길의 흙먼지를 우리 머리 위로 흩날려 보냈다. 이내 우리는 그 불빛을 놓쳤고, 이내 다시 보았고, 이내 놓쳤다가, 이내 다시 보더니, 가로수 길로 접어들어 불빛이 환하게 비치는 쪽으로 구보로 올라갔다. 그것은 앞쪽 지붕에 세 개의 뾰족한 봉우리가 솟고 현관까지 둥글게 이어지는 진입로가 딸린, 고풍스러워 보이는 집의 한 창문에서 새어 나오는 불빛이었다. 우리가 다다르자 종이 울렸고, 고요한 공기 속 그 깊은 종소리와, 어디선가 멀리서 개들이 짖는 소리와, 열린 문에서 쏟아지는 불빛과, 달아오른 말들이 내뿜는 김과 연기와, 우리 자신의 빨라진 심장 박동 한가운데서, 우리는 적잖은 혼란 속에 마차에서 내렸다.\n\n\"에이다, 사랑스러운 아이야, 에스더, 귀여운 아이야, 잘 왔다. 너희를 보니 정말 기쁘구나! 릭, 지금 손이 하나라도 남아 있다면 너에게 내밀 텐데!\"\n\n맑고 밝고 정겨운 목소리로 이 말을 한 신사는 한쪽 팔로 에이다의 허리를, 다른 팔로 내 허리를 감싸 안고, 아버지처럼 우리 둘에게 입을 맞춘 뒤, 우리를 홀 너머로 데리고 가, 활활 타오르는 불로 온통 발그레하게 빛나는 자그마한 방으로 안내했다. 여기서 그는 다시 우리에게 입을 맞추고, 두 팔을 벌려 우리를 벽난로 옆에 미리 끌어다 놓은 소파에 나란히 앉게 했다. 나는 우리가 조금이라도 감정을 드러냈다면 그가 당장 달아나 버렸으리라는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"자, 릭!\" 그가 말했다. \"손이 하나 비었구나. 진심 어린 한마디면 일장 연설만큼 값지지. 너를 보니 진심으로 기쁘다. 여기가 네 집이야. 몸 좀 녹여라!\"\n\n리처드는 본능적으로 존경과 솔직함이 뒤섞인 태도로 그의 두 손을 맞잡고 흔들며, 다만 이렇게만 말했다(그런데 그 말투가 어찌나 진지했던지 나는 잔다이스 씨가 갑자기 사라져 버릴까 봐 너무 겁이 나서 오히려 놀랐다). \"정말 친절하십니다, 어르신! 정말 큰 신세를 졌습니다!\" 그러고는 모자와 외투를 벗어 놓고 불가로 다가왔다.\n\n\"그래, 오는 길은 어땠느냐? 그리고 젤리비 부인은 어떻더냐, 얘야?\" 잔다이스 씨가 에이다에게 물었다.\n\n에이다가 그에게 대답을 하는 동안, 나는 (얼마나 큰 관심을 가지고 그랬는지는 말할 것도 없으리라) 그의 얼굴을 흘끗 보았다. 그것은 잘생기고 생기 있고 민첩한 얼굴로, 변화와 움직임이 가득했다. 그리고 그의 머리카락은 은빛이 도는 쇳빛 회색이었다. 나는 그를 쉰보다는 예순에 가깝다고 보았으나, 그는 꼿꼿하고 정정하고 다부졌다. 그가 우리에게 처음 말을 건넨 순간부터 그의 목소리는 내 마음속에서 도무지 짚어낼 수 없는 어떤 기억과 이어졌다. 그런데 이제, 별안간, 그의 태도에서 느껴진 어떤 갑작스러움과 그의 눈에 어린 정다운 표정이, 육 년 전 내가 리딩으로 가던 그 잊지 못할 날에 역마차에서 본 그 신사를 떠올리게 했다. 나는 그가 바로 그 사람임을 확신했다. 그 사실을 깨달았을 때처럼 평생 그토록 겁이 났던 적은 없었으니, 그가 내 눈길을 알아채고 내 생각을 읽기라도 한 듯 문 쪽을 어찌나 쳐다보던지, 나는 우리가 그를 놓쳐 버린 줄 알았다.\n\n그러나 다행스럽게도 그는 그 자리에 그대로 있었고, 나에게 젤리비 부인을 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그분은 아프리카를 위해 무척 애쓰시더군요, 어르신.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"고결하지!\" 잔다이스 씨가 받아쳤다. \"그런데 에이다처럼 대답하는구나.\" 나는 에이다가 그렇게 말하는 걸 듣지 못했다. \"보아하니 너희 모두 다른 생각을 하고 있구나.\"\n\n\"저희는 차라리 이렇게 생각했어요.\" 내가 리처드와 에이다를 흘끗 보며 말했는데, 둘은 눈으로 어서 말하라고 나에게 간청하고 있었다. \"어쩌면 그분이 자기 가정에는 조금 소홀하신 게 아닌가 하고요.\"\n\n\"한 방 먹었군!\" 잔다이스 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n나는 또다시 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n\"좋아! 나는 네 진짜 생각을 알고 싶구나, 얘야. 내가 일부러 너희를 그곳에 보냈을 수도 있으니.\"\n\n\"저희가 생각하기에는, 어쩌면\" 내가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"가정에 대한 의무부터 시작하는 것이 옳고요, 어르신. 그리고 어쩌면, 그 의무가 외면당하고 등한시되는 동안에는 다른 어떤 의무로도 그것을 대신할 수 없는 게 아닌가 합니다.\"\n\n\"그 어린 젤리비 아이들은\" 리처드가 나를 거들어 나서며 말했다. \"정말이지 — 강하게 말하지 않을 수가 없네요, 어르신 — 끔찍한 꼴이더군요.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 뜻은 좋아.\" 잔다이스 씨가 황급히 말했다. \"바람이 동쪽에서 부는구나.\"\n\n\"저희가 내려올 때는 북쪽에서 불던데요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 짚었다.\n\n\"이보게 릭.\" 잔다이스 씨가 불을 쑤석거리며 말했다. \"맹세컨대 동쪽이거나 곧 동쪽이 될 거다. 나는 바람이 동쪽에서 불면 가끔씩 영 거북한 느낌이 들곤 하거든.\"\n\n\"신경통이신가요, 어르신?\" 리처드가 말했다.\n\n\"아마 그럴 거다, 릭. 그렇겠지. 그러니까 그 어린 젤 — 나도 그 애들이 걱정스럽긴 했다만 — 그 애들이 — 아, 이런, 그래, 동쪽이로구나!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 이 끊어진 문장들을 내뱉는 동안 어정쩡하게 두세 번 방 안을 오락가락 거닐었는데, 한 손에는 부지깽이를 쥐고 다른 손으로는 머리를 문지르면서, 그 짜증마저 더없이 사람 좋은 데다 어찌나 엉뚱하고 사랑스러웠던지, 나는 우리가 어떤 말로도 다 표현하지 못할 만큼 그에게 흠뻑 빠졌음을 확신한다. 그는 한쪽 팔은 에이다에게, 다른 팔은 나에게 내밀고, 리처드더러 촛불을 가져오라 이르며 앞장서 나가다가, 별안간 우리 모두를 도로 돌려세웠다.\n\n\"그 어린 젤리비 아이들 말이다. 너희가 — 혹시 — 그러니까, 사탕이라도 비처럼 쏟아졌거나, 세모꼴 산딸기 파이라도, 뭐 그런 것이라도 쏟아졌더라면!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 사촌 오라버니 —\" 에이다가 황급히 입을 열었다.\n\n\"좋아, 내 예쁜 아가. 사촌이라니 마음에 드는구나. 사촌 존, 이라면 더 좋을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그럼, 사촌 존 오라버니 —\" 에이다가 웃으며 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"하, 하! 정말 좋구나!\" 잔다이스 씨가 무척 흐뭇해하며 말했다. \"유난히 자연스럽게 들리는구나. 그래, 얘야?\"\n\n\"그것보다 더 좋은 게 쏟아졌어요. 에스더가 비처럼 쏟아졌으니까요.\"\n\n\"오?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"에스더가 뭘 했는데?\"\n\n\"그게요, 사촌 존 오라버니.\" 에이다가 두 손을 그의 팔에 감고는 그를 사이에 두고 나를 향해 고개를 가로저으며 말했다 — 나는 그 애가 잠자코 있기를 바랐으니까. \"에스더는 단박에 그 애들의 친구가 됐어요. 에스더는 그 애들을 돌보고, 어르고 달래 재우고, 씻기고 옷을 입히고, 이야기를 들려주고, 조용히 시키고, 기념될 선물도 사 주었어요.\" — 사랑스러운 아이! 나는 피피가 발견된 뒤에 그 애를 데리고 잠깐 나가 자그마한 장난감 말 하나를 사 주었을 뿐인데! — \"게다가, 사촌 존 오라버니, 에스더는 가엾은 만이 캐럴라인을 어찌나 부드럽게 다독였는지 몰라요, 그리고 저를 어찌나 살뜰히 챙겨 주고 또 얼마나 상냥했는지! 아니, 아니, 반박하면 안 돼, 에스더야! 알잖아, 너도 알잖아, 다 사실이라는 걸!\"\n\n마음씨 따뜻한 그 사랑스러운 아이가 사촌 존을 사이에 두고 몸을 기울여 나에게 입을 맞추고는, 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 당돌하게 말했다. \"어쨌든, 사촌 존 오라버니, 저는 저에게 짝지어 주신 동무에 대해 꼭 감사드리겠어요.\" 나는 그 애가 마치 그에게 어디 한번 달아나 보라고 도전이라도 하는 것 같았다. 그러나 그는 달아나지 않았다.\n\n\"바람이 어디서 분다고 했지, 릭?\" 잔다이스 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"저희가 내려올 때는 북쪽이었습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"네 말이 맞다. 동쪽 기운은 없구나. 내가 잘못 짚었어. 자, 아가씨들, 와서 너희 집을 둘러보거라!\"\n\n그것은 한 방에서 다른 방으로 가려면 계단을 오르내려야 하고, 방을 다 보았다 싶으면 또 다른 방이 나타나고, 자그마한 홀과 복도가 넉넉하게 갖춰져 있고, 뜻밖의 자리에서 격자창이 달리고 그 사이로 푸른 덩굴이 비집고 들어온, 더 오래된 시골집 같은 방들을 마주치게 되는, 그런 기분 좋게 들쭉날쭉한 집들 가운데 하나였다. 우리가 맨 먼저 들어간 내 방이 바로 그런 곳이었는데, 모서리가 너무 많아 나중에 다 세어 보지도 못한 들쭉날쭉한 지붕에, (벽난로에는 장작불이 타고 있었고) 그 둘레가 온통 새하얀 타일로 깔린 굴뚝이 있어, 그 타일 하나하나마다 불꽃의 작고 환한 모형이 활활 타오르고 있었다. 이 방에서 두 계단을 내려가면 꽃밭이 내려다보이는 사랑스러운 자그마한 거실이 나왔는데, 이 방은 이제부터 에이다와 내 차지가 될 곳이었다. 여기서 세 계단을 올라가면 에이다의 침실이 나왔는데, 아름다운 경치가 한눈에 들어오는 멋지고 널찍한 창문이 있었고(우리는 별빛 아래 펼쳐진 드넓은 어둠을 보았다), 그 아래 움푹한 창가 의자가 딸려 있어, 용수철 자물쇠를 달면 사랑스러운 에이다 셋쯤은 한꺼번에 숨겨 둘 만했다. 이 방에서 빠져나오면 자그마한 회랑으로 통했는데, 거기로 다른 손님방들(둘뿐이었다)이 이어졌고, 그렇게 길이에 비해 모퉁이 계단이 여럿 끼인, 얕은 단의 자그마한 층계를 따라 홀로 내려갈 수 있었다. 그러나 에이다의 문으로 나가는 대신 내 방으로 되돌아와, 들어왔던 문으로 다시 나가서, 계단에서 뜻밖의 모양으로 갈라져 나간 비뚜름한 계단 몇 개를 따라 오르면, 복도 속에서 길을 잃게 되는데, 그 복도에는 빨래 짜는 기계며, 세모꼴 탁자며, 소파이면서 궤짝이면서 침상이기도 한 인도 원주민의 의자가 놓여 있었으니, 그것은 어느 모로 보나 대나무 해골과 커다란 새장 사이의 무언가처럼 생겼고, 누가 언제 인도에서 가져왔는지는 아무도 몰랐다. 거기서 리처드의 방으로 이어졌는데, 그 방은 서재이기도 하고 거실이기도 하고 침실이기도 해서, 정말이지 여러 방을 한데 아늑하게 버무려 놓은 것 같았다. 거기서 곧장, 복도를 조금 지나면, 잔다이스 씨가 잠을 자는 수수한 방이 나왔는데, 그는 일 년 내내 창문을 열어 둔 채, 공기를 더 쐬려고 아무 가구도 딸리지 않은 침대를 방 한가운데에 놓고 잤으며, 옆에 붙은 더 작은 방에서는 찬물 목욕통이 그를 기다리며 입을 벌리고 있었다. 거기서 나오면 또 다른 복도가 나왔는데, 거기에는 뒷계단이 있었고, 마구간 밖에서 말들의 몸을 문질러 주는 소리와, 울퉁불퉁한 돌바닥에서 말들이 자꾸 미끄러지자 \"버텨\", \"넘어가\"라고 이르는 소리가 들렸다. 아니면, 다른 문으로 나가면(방마다 문이 적어도 둘은 있었다), 계단 대여섯 개와 낮은 아치 문을 지나 곧장 홀로 다시 내려갈 수 있었는데, 어떻게 거기로 되돌아왔는지, 아니 애초에 어떻게 거기서 나왔는지 어리둥절해하면서 말이다.\n\n가구는, 집과 마찬가지로, 오래되었다기보다는 고풍스러운 것이었고, 기분 좋게 들쭉날쭉했다. 에이다의 침실은 온통 꽃이었다 — 친츠와 벽지에도, 벨벳에도, 자수에도, 벽난로 양옆에 더 격식을 차리려는 듯 자그마한 발받침 시동을 하나씩 거느린 채 놓인 뻣뻣하고 궁정풍인 두 의자의 브로케이드 천에도. 우리 거실은 초록빛이었고, 벽에는 액자에 끼워 유리를 덧댄 놀랍고도 놀란 표정의 새들이 잔뜩 걸려, 그림 밖으로 응시하고 있었다 — 마치 그레이비라도 끼얹은 듯 갈색으로 반들거리는, 유리 상자 속의 진짜 송어를 향해, 쿡 선장의 죽음을 향해, 그리고 중국 화가들이 그린 대로 중국에서 차를 우려내는 전 과정을 향해. 내 방에는 열두 달을 그린 타원형 판화들이 있었는데 — 짧은 허리선 옷에 턱 밑에서 묶는 커다란 모자를 쓴 채 건초를 만드는 여인들은 유월이었고, 매끈한 다리에 삼각모를 쓰고 마을 첨탑을 가리키는 귀족들은 시월이었다. 크레용으로 그린 반신 초상화가 집 안 곳곳에 넘쳐났는데, 어찌나 흩어져 걸렸던지, 나는 내 젊은 장교 초상화의 형제를 도자기 벽장에서 발견했고, 가슴팍에 꽃 한 송이를 꽂은 어여쁜 젊은 신부가 늙어 백발이 된 모습을 아침 식사 방에서 발견했다. 그 대신 내 방에는, 앤 여왕 시절의 천사 넷이 꽃줄 장식에 매달려 흡족한 표정의 신사 하나를 제법 애를 먹어 가며 천국으로 데려가는 그림이 있었고, 과일과 주전자와 알파벳을 수놓은 자수 작품도 하나 있었다. 옷장에서부터 의자와 탁자, 휘장, 거울, 심지어 화장대 위의 바늘꽂이와 향수병에 이르기까지, 옮길 수 있는 모든 물건이 한결같이 이 별스러운 다채로움을 드러냈다. 그것들이 일치하는 점이라곤 더없이 말끔하다는 것, 더없이 새하얀 아마포를 펼쳐 보인다는 것, 그리고 크든 작든 서랍이 있는 곳이라면 어디든 장미 꽃잎과 향긋한 라벤더를 잔뜩 갈무리해 두었다는 것뿐이었다. 별빛 어린 밤하늘을 향해 환하게 빛나는, 군데군데 커튼 그림자로 부드러워진 불 밝은 창들과, 그 빛과 온기와 안락함과, 멀리서 들려오는 저녁 식사 준비의 정겨운 달그락거림과, 우리가 보는 모든 것을 환하게 밝혀 주는 너그러운 주인의 얼굴과, 우리가 듣는 모든 것에 나직한 반주를 곁들일 만큼만 바깥에서 부는 바람 — 이것이 황폐한 집에 대한 우리의 첫인상이었다.\n\n\"너희 마음에 든다니 기쁘구나.\" 잔다이스 씨가 우리를 다시 에이다의 거실로 데려왔을 때 말했다. \"내세울 건 없어도, 아늑하고 자그마한 곳이라고 믿는다. 그리고 이렇게 밝고 젊은 얼굴들이 깃들면 더 그렇게 되겠지. 저녁 식사까지는 삼십 분이 채 안 남았구나. 여기에는 이 세상에서 가장 멋진 사람 하나밖에 없단다 — 어린아이지.\"\n\n\"아이가 또 있나 봐, 에스더!\" 에이다가 말했다.\n\n\"말 그대로 어린아이라는 건 아니다.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"나이로 따져 아이는 아니야. 그는 다 컸지 — 적어도 나만큼은 나이를 먹었어 — 그러나 천진함과 신선함과 열정에서는, 그리고 온갖 세상사에는 도무지 서투른 그 곱고 순박한 무능함에서는, 그는 완벽한 어린아이란다.\"\n\n우리는 그가 무척 흥미로운 사람임에 틀림없다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"그는 젤리비 부인을 알지.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"음악을 하는 사람인데, 아마추어지만 직업으로 삼았을 수도 있었어. 화가이기도 한데, 역시 아마추어지만 직업으로 삼았을 수도 있었지. 재주가 많고 사람을 끄는 데가 있어. 사업에서도 불운했고, 자기가 좇는 일에서도 불운했고, 집안에서도 불운했지. 그래도 개의치 않아 — 그는 어린아이니까!\"\n\n\"그분에게 자기 자식이 있다는 말씀이신가요, 어르신?\" 리처드가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다, 릭! 여섯이야. 더 되지! 내 생각엔 열둘에 가까울 거다. 그런데 그는 한 번도 그 애들을 돌본 적이 없어. 어떻게 돌보겠느냐? 자기를 돌봐 줄 사람이 필요한 사람인걸. 어린아이라니까!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그 아이들은 그나마 제 앞가림은 했나요, 어르신?\" 리처드가 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 너도 짐작이 가겠지만.\" 잔다이스 씨가 별안간 안색이 어두워지며 말했다. \"더없이 가난한 집 아이들은 길러지는 게 아니라 끌어올려지는 거라는 말이 있지. 해럴드 스킴폴의 아이들은 어찌어찌 굴러서 컸어. 바람이 또 동쪽으로 도는 모양이다. 좀 느껴지는걸!\"\n\n리처드는 이렇게 추운 밤에는 자리가 바람에 휑하니 노출되어 있다고 말했다.\n\n\"노출되어 있고말고.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"분명 그게 원인이겠지. 황폐한 집이라는 이름부터가 휑한 느낌이 들지 않느냐. 그건 그렇고, 너도 내가 가는 쪽으로 가는구나. 자, 가자!\"\n\n우리 짐이 도착해 모두 손 닿는 곳에 있었으므로, 나는 몇 분 만에 옷을 갈아입고 내 세간을 정리하고 있었는데, 그때 하녀 하나가(에이다를 시중드는 그 하녀가 아니라, 내가 본 적 없는 다른 하녀였다) 라벨이 죄 붙은 열쇠 꾸러미 두 개가 담긴 바구니를 들고 내 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"아가씨 것이에요, 괜찮으시다면요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"제 것이라고요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"살림 열쇠예요, 아가씨.\"\n내가 놀라움을 드러내자, 그녀도 약간 놀란 기색으로 덧붙였다. \"아가씨 혼자 계실 때 곧바로 갖다드리라는 분부였어요. 서머슨 아가씨, 맞으시죠?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 제 이름이에요.\"\n\n\"큰 꾸러미는 살림 열쇠고, 작은 꾸러미는 지하 저장실 열쇠예요, 아가씨. 내일 아침 정하시는 시각이면 언제든, 그 열쇠에 맞는 찬장이며 물건들을 보여 드리라고 하셨어요.\"\n\n나는 여섯 시 반에 준비하고 있겠다고 말했고, 그녀가 나간 뒤, 나에게 맡겨진 신임의 무게에 그만 넋이 나간 채 바구니를 바라보며 서 있었다. 에이다가 이런 나를 발견했는데, 내가 그 애에게 열쇠를 보여 주고 그 사연을 들려주자, 그 애가 어찌나 흐뭇하게 나를 믿어 주었던지, 거기서 힘을 얻지 않는다면 그건 무정하고 배은망덕한 짓이었으리라. 물론 그게 사랑스러운 그 애의 다정함에서 나온 것임을 알았지만, 나는 그렇게 기분 좋게 속아 넘어가는 것이 좋았다.\n\n우리가 아래층으로 내려가니, 스킴폴 씨에게 소개되었는데, 그는 불 앞에 서서 리처드에게 자기가 학창 시절에 축구를 얼마나 좋아했는지 이야기하고 있었다. 그는 머리가 제법 크지만 얼굴이 섬세하고 목소리가 감미로운, 자그맣고 환한 사람이었고, 그에게는 더없는 매력이 있었다. 그가 하는 말은 죄다 애쓴 데가 없이 절로 흘러나왔고, 어찌나 사람을 끄는 쾌활함으로 말하던지, 그가 이야기하는 것을 듣고 있노라면 빠져들지 않을 수가 없었다. 잔다이스 씨보다 호리호리한 체구에 안색이 더 불그레하고 머리카락도 더 갈색이어서, 그는 더 젊어 보였다. 사실 그는 모든 면에서 잘 보존된 노년의 신사라기보다는 망가진 청년에 가까운 모습이었다. 그의 태도에는, 그리고 옷차림에까지도(머리카락은 아무렇게나 헝클어져 있었고, 목수건은 화가들이 자기 초상화를 그릴 때 즐겨 그리는 모습 그대로 느슨하게 흘러내려 있었다), 무언가 거리낌 없는 무심함이 배어 있었는데, 나는 그것을 어떤 독특한 퇴락의 과정을 거친 낭만적인 청년이라는 인상과 도무지 떼어 놓을 수 없었다. 그것은 세월과 근심과 경험이라는 흔한 길을 따라 인생을 거쳐 온 사람의 태도나 모습과는 전혀 같지 않다는 느낌을 주었다.\n\n대화에서 나는 스킴폴 씨가 의술을 배웠고, 한때 그 직분으로 어느 독일 군주의 집안에서 살았다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그러나 그는, 자기가 무게와 분량을 따지는 일에는 늘 한낱 어린아이였고 그런 것에 대해서는(자기를 진저리 나게 했다는 것 말고는) 아무것도 모른 채 살았던 터라, 처방에 꼭 필요한 세밀한 정확함을 도무지 갖출 수가 없었다고 우리에게 말했다. 사실 자기는 세부적인 것에는 도무지 머리가 돌아가지 않는다고 그는 말했다. 그러고는 무척 익살스럽게, 군주의 피를 뽑거나 그 집안사람 누군가에게 약을 처방해야 할 때면, 자기는 으레 침대에 등을 대고 누워 신문을 읽거나 연필로 마음 가는 대로 그림이나 끼적이고 있어서 올 수가 없었다고 들려주었다. 마침내 군주가 이를 못마땅해하자 — \"그 점에서는,\" 스킴폴 씨가 더없이 솔직한 태도로 말했다. \"그분이 전적으로 옳았지요.\" — 그 일자리가 끝나 버렸고, 스킴폴 씨는 (그가 흐뭇한 쾌활함으로 덧붙인 대로) \"사랑 말고는 먹고살 게 없었던지라, 사랑에 빠졌고, 결혼했고, 발그레한 볼들에 둘러싸이게 되었\"다. 그러자 그의 좋은 벗 잔다이스와 그 밖의 좋은 벗 몇몇이 그에게, 빠르거나 느린 순서로, 인생의 여러 활로를 마련해 주었으나, 다 헛수고였다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 세상에서 가장 오래된 두 가지 약점이 있음을 고백할 수밖에 없었으니, 하나는 시간 개념이 없다는 것이고, 다른 하나는 돈 개념이 없다는 것이었다. 그 탓에 그는 약속을 지킨 적이 없고, 어떤 사무도 처리할 수 없었으며, 무엇의 값어치도 알지 못했다! 어쨌든! 그렇게 그는 인생을 헤쳐 왔고, 이렇게 여기 있는 것이다! 그는 신문 읽기를 무척 좋아했고, 연필로 마음 가는 대로 그림 그리기를 무척 좋아했고, 자연을 무척 좋아했고, 예술을 무척 좋아했다. 그가 세상에 바라는 것이라곤 자기를 그저 살게 내버려 두라는 것뿐이었다. 그것은 대단한 게 아니었다. 그의 바람은 몇 가지 안 되었다. 신문과 대화와 음악과 양고기와 커피와 풍경과 철 따라 나는 과일과 브리스틀 판지 몇 장과 약간의 클라레만 주면, 그는 더 바라지 않았다. 그는 세상에서 한낱 어린아이일 뿐이었지만, 달을 따 달라고 떼쓰지는 않았다. 그는 세상을 향해 이렇게 말했다. \"각자 평화로이 제 갈 길을 가시오! 붉은 옷을 입든, 푸른 옷을 입든, 성직자의 흰 소매를 달든 마음대로 하시오. 귀 뒤에 펜을 꽂든, 앞치마를 두르든, 영광이든 거룩함이든 상업이든 장사든 무엇이든 마음에 드는 것을 좇으시오. 다만 — 해럴드 스킴폴만은 살게 내버려 두시오!\"\n\n이 모든 것을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을, 그는 더없이 빛나고 흐뭇한 태도로 들려주었을 뿐 아니라, 어딘가 생기 넘치는 솔직함으로 우리에게 이야기했다 — 마치 자기가 자기 자신과는 전혀 상관없는 사람인 양, 스킴폴이 제삼자인 양, 스킴폴에게는 별난 구석이 있긴 하지만 그래도 챙겨야 할 몫이 있어서 그것이 공동체 전체의 일이니 소홀히 해서는 안 된다는 것을 잘 안다는 듯이. 그는 정말이지 사람을 홀렸다. 그 이른 시점에 그가 한 어떤 말을 인생의 의무와 책임에 대해 내가 품어 온 어떤 생각과 화해시켜 보려 애쓰면서 내가 조금이라도 혼란스러웠다면(그랬는지는 전혀 확신할 수 없지만), 그것은 그가 어째서 그 의무에서 자유로운지를 정확히 이해하지 못한 데서 온 혼란이었다. 그가 그 의무에서 자유롭다는 것은 거의 의심하지 않았으니, 그 자신이 그 점에 대해서는 너무도 분명했기 때문이다.\n\n\"나는 아무것도 탐내지 않아요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 여전히 가벼운 투로 말했다. \"소유라는 건 나에게 아무것도 아니에요. 여기 내 벗 잔다이스의 훌륭한 집이 있지요. 나는 그가 이 집을 소유한 것에 대해 그에게 고마움을 느낍니다. 나는 이 집을 스케치할 수도 있고, 고쳐 그릴 수도 있어요. 음악으로 옮길 수도 있고요. 내가 여기 있는 동안에는 나도 이 집을 충분히 소유하고 있는 셈인 데다, 골칫거리도, 비용도, 책임도 없지요. 한마디로 내 집사의 이름이 잔다이스인데, 그가 나를 속일 리도 없고요. 우리가 젤리비 부인 이야기를 하고 있었지요. 눈이 초롱초롱하고, 의지가 강하고, 사무를 세세히 처리하는 능력이 어마어마한 여인, 놀라운 열의로 어떤 일에 몸을 던지는 여인이지요! 나에게 강한 의지와 사무를 세세히 처리하는 어마어마한 능력이 없어서 놀라운 열의로 어떤 일에 몸을 던지지 못한다고 해서 나는 아쉬워하지 않아요. 나는 시샘 없이 그녀를 흠모할 수 있어요. 그녀가 좇는 일에 공감할 수도 있고요. 그것을 꿈꿀 수도 있어요. 나는 풀밭에 누워 — 날씨 좋은 날에 — 아프리카의 강을 따라 떠내려가며, 마주치는 원주민을 죄다 끌어안고, 그 깊은 정적을 또렷이 느끼고, 빽빽이 우거진 열대의 초목을 마치 그곳에 있는 것처럼 정확하게 스케치할 수 있어요. 내가 그렇게 한다고 해서 그게 직접적으로 무슨 쓸모가 있는지는 모르겠지만, 그게 내가 할 수 있는 전부고, 나는 그걸 철저히 해내지요. 그러니 제발, 해럴드 스킴폴이라는, 사람을 믿어 의심치 않는 어린아이가, 당신들에게, 실무에 밝은 사람들이 모인 이 세상에, 자기를 살게 해 주고 인간 가족을 흠모하게 해 달라고 청원하니, 어떻게든, 마음씨 고운 분들답게, 그렇게 해 주시고, 그가 흔들목마를 타도록 내버려 두시오!\"\n\n잔다이스 씨가 그 간청을 소홀히 하지 않았다는 것은 충분히 분명했다. 그가 이내 한 말을 보태지 않더라도, 거기서 스킴폴 씨가 차지한 전반적인 처지만으로도 그 점이 명백했다.\n\n\"내가 부러워하는 건 오직 당신들, 너그러운 분들뿐이에요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 자기의 새 벗인 우리를 향해 마치 남 이야기하듯 말했다. \"나는 당신들이 하는 그 일을 해내는 그 능력이 부럽소. 그건 나 자신이 마음껏 누리고 싶은 거니까요. 나는 당신들에게 속된 고마움 따위는 조금도 느끼지 않아요. 오히려 당신들이 나에게 고마워해야 할 것 같은 기분마저 든다오, 너그러움이라는 호사를 누릴 기회를 내가 당신들에게 주었으니 말이오. 당신들이 그걸 좋아한다는 걸 나는 알아요. 어쩌면 나는 당신들의 행복의 곳간을 불려 주려는 바로 그 목적을 위해 이 세상에 왔는지도 모르지요. 어쩌면 나는, 내 자그마한 곤경에서 나를 거들 기회를 이따금 당신들에게 줌으로써 당신들의 은인이 되도록 태어났는지도 모르오. 그것이 이토록 즐거운 결과로 이어지는데, 내가 세부적인 일과 세상사에 무능하다고 해서 어찌 아쉬워하겠소? 그러니 나는 그것을 아쉬워하지 않는다오.\"\n\n그의 모든 장난스러운 말 가운데(장난스럽지만, 그 표현하는 바를 늘 온전히 진심으로 담고 있었다) 이 말만큼 잔다이스 씨의 취향에 들어맞는 것은 없어 보였다. 나는 나중에, 아주 사소한 일에도 인류 가운데 누구보다 고마워할 줄 알 그가, 어째서 남들의 고마움은 그토록 피하려 하는 것인지가 정말로 별난 일인지, 아니면 나에게만 별난 일로 보이는 것인지, 곱씹어 보고 싶은 유혹을 새삼 자주 느꼈다.\n\n우리는 모두 홀딱 빠졌다. 스킴폴 씨가 에이다와 리처드를 처음 보면서도 그토록 격의 없이, 더없이 살갑게 굴려고 애쓰는 것은, 두 사람의 사람을 끄는 됨됨이에 마땅히 바칠 만한 찬사라고 나는 느꼈다. 두 사람은(특히 리처드는) 같은 이유로 자연히 흐뭇해했고, 그토록 매력 있는 사람에게서 그토록 격의 없는 신뢰를 받는 것을 보통이 아닌 특권으로 여겼다. 우리가 귀를 기울일수록 스킴폴 씨는 더욱 흥겹게 이야기했다. 그리고 그 멋들어진 들뜬 태도와, 사람을 끄는 솔직함과, 자기 약점을 가볍게 툭툭 던지는 정겨운 방식이 어우러져 — 마치 \"나는 어린아이라오! 나에 비하면 당신들은 꿍꿍이가 있는 사람들이지요\"(그는 정말로 나로 하여금 나 자신을 그런 사람으로 여기게 만들었다) \"하지만 나는 명랑하고 순진하니, 당신들의 세속적인 잔재주는 잊어버리고 나와 함께 놀아요!\"라고 말하는 듯이 — 그 효과는 그야말로 눈부셨다.\n\n게다가 그는 감정이 너무도 풍부했고, 아름답거나 다정한 것을 향한 섬세한 감수성이 어찌나 깊었던지, 그것 하나만으로도 사람의 마음을 사로잡을 만했다. 그날 저녁, 내가 차를 우릴 채비를 하고 있고 에이다가 옆방에서 피아노를 짚으며 사촌 리처드에게 — 두 사람이 우연히 이야기에 올린 — 어떤 곡조를 나직이 흥얼거리고 있을 때, 그는 다가와 내 곁 소파에 앉더니 에이다 이야기를 어찌나 하던지, 나는 그를 사랑할 뻔했다.\n\n\"그 아가씨는 아침 같아요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 금빛 머리카락에, 그 파란 눈에, 두 볼의 그 싱그러운 홍조까지, 그녀는 여름날 아침 같지요. 여기 새들이 그녀를 아침으로 착각할 거예요. 온 인류에게 기쁨인 저토록 사랑스러운 젊은 사람을, 우리는 고아라고 부르지 맙시다. 그녀는 우주의 아이예요.\"\n\n문득 보니 잔다이스 씨가 두 손을 등 뒤로 모으고 얼굴에는 귀 기울이는 미소를 띤 채 우리 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"우주란,\" 그가 말했다. \"유감스럽지만 어지간히 무심한 부모일 텐데.\"\n\n\"오! 그건 모르지요!\" 스킴폴 씨가 들떠서 외쳤다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 내가 알 것 같은데.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄!\" 스킴폴 씨가 외쳤다. \"당신은 세상을 알지요(당신이 말하는 의미에서는 그게 우주고요). 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모르니, 당신 뜻대로 하시구려. 그러나 내 뜻대로 한다면,\" 두 사촌을 흘끗 보며 그가 말했다. \"저런 길에는 속된 현실의 가시덤불 따위는 없어야 해요. 장미가 흩뿌려져 있어야 하고요. 봄도, 가을도, 겨울도 없이 영원한 여름만 있는 나무 그늘 사이로 그 길이 뻗어 있어야 하지요. 세월도 변화도 그 길을 결코 시들게 해선 안 돼요. 돈이라는 천박한 말은 그 근처에서 결코 입에 올려서도 안 되고요!\"\n\n잔다이스 씨는 그가 정말 어린아이라도 되는 양 미소를 지으며 그의 머리를 토닥이고는, 한두 걸음 더 나아가 잠시 멈춰 서서 젊은 두 사촌을 바라보았다. 그 눈길은 생각에 잠겨 있었으나, 거기에는 내가 훗날 자주(얼마나 자주였던가!) 다시 보게 될, 오래도록 내 가슴에 새겨진 인자한 표정이 어려 있었다. 두 사람이 있는 방은 그가 서 있는 방과 통해 있었는데, 오직 난롯불로만 밝혀져 있었다. 에이다는 피아노 앞에 앉아 있고, 리처드는 그 곁에 서서 몸을 기울이고 있었다. 벽에는 두 사람의 그림자가 한데 어우러져, 기이한 형상들에 둘러싸인 채, 움직이지 않는 물체에서 비친 것이건만 일렁이는 불길에서 옮아 온 어딘가 유령 같은 움직임마저 깃들어 있었다. 에이다가 어찌나 부드럽게 건반을 짚고 어찌나 나직이 노래하던지, 먼 언덕으로 한숨처럼 잦아드는 바람 소리가 그 음악만큼이나 또렷이 들렸다. 미래의 신비와, 현재의 목소리가 그것에 던져 주는 작은 실마리가, 그 그림 전체에 표현되어 있는 듯했다.\n\n그러나 이 장면을 내가 떠올리는 것은, 이 환상을 — 똑똑히 기억하기는 하지만 — 되살리기 위해서가 아니다. 첫째, 나는 그쪽을 향한 그 말없는 눈길과 그에 앞선 도도한 말의 흐름 사이에 의미와 의도의 면에서 어떤 대조가 있음을 어렴풋이나마 의식하고 있었다. 둘째, 잔다이스 씨가 눈길을 거둘 때 그것이 내게 머문 것은 한순간뿐이었으나, 나는 그 순간 그가 나에게 — 에이다와 리처드가 언젠가 더 애틋한 사이로 맺어지기를 바라는 그 소망을 — 털어놓았고, 자기가 나에게 그것을 털어놓는다는 것을, 그리고 내가 그 속내를 받아들인다는 것을 알고 있다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n스킴폴 씨는 피아노와 첼로를 연주할 줄 알았고, 작곡가이기도 해서 — 한번은 오페라를 반쯤 작곡했다가 싫증이 나 버렸다는데 — 자기가 작곡한 것을 운치 있게 연주했다. 차를 마신 뒤 우리는 자그마한 음악회를 열었는데, 거기서 리처드는 — 에이다의 노래에 홀딱 빠져, 그 애가 세상에 나온 노래란 노래는 죄다 아는 것 같다고 나에게 말했다 — 잔다이스 씨와 나와 함께 청중이 되었다. 얼마쯤 지나 나는 먼저 스킴폴 씨가, 이어 리처드가 보이지 않는 것을 알아챘고, 리처드가 어떻게 이렇게 오래 자리를 비워 이 많은 것을 놓칠 수 있나 생각하고 있는데, 나에게 열쇠를 건네주었던 그 하녀가 문 안으로 얼굴을 들이밀며 말했다. \"괜찮으시면, 아가씨, 잠깐 시간 좀 내주실 수 있으세요?\"\n\n내가 그녀와 함께 홀로 나와 문이 닫히자, 그녀가 두 손을 치켜들며 말했다. \"아, 죄송하지만요, 아가씨, 카스톤 씨가요, 스킴폴 씨 방으로 위층에 올라와 주시겠냐고요. 그분이 잡혔어요, 아가씨!\"\n\n\"잡혔다고요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"잡혔어요, 아가씨. 갑자기요.\" 하녀가 말했다.\n\n나는 그의 병이 위중한 종류일까 봐 겁이 났으나, 물론 그녀에게 조용히 하고 누구도 놀라게 하지 말라고 당부하고는, 그녀를 따라 서둘러 위층으로 올라가면서, 만약 발작이라면 무슨 처치가 가장 좋을지 헤아릴 만큼은 마음을 가다듬었다. 그녀가 문을 활짝 열어젖혀 나는 어느 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기서 — 말로 다 할 수 없이 놀랍게도 — 침대에 뻗어 있거나 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 스킴폴 씨를 발견하는 대신, 나는 그가 불 앞에 서서 리처드를 향해 미소 짓고 있는 것을 발견했다. 한편 리처드는 몹시 난처한 얼굴로, 흰 외투를 걸치고 머리에 머리카락이 매끈하게, 그것도 얼마 없이 붙은 한 사람을 소파에서 바라보고 있었는데, 그 사람은 손수건으로 그 머리카락을 더 매끈하게 쓸어 넘기며 더 적어 보이게 만들고 있었다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 리처드가 다급하게 말했다. \"와 주셔서 다행이에요. 우리에게 조언을 해 주실 수 있겠군요. 우리 벗 스킴폴 씨가 — 놀라지 마세요! — 빚 때문에 체포되었어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 정말이지, 서머슨 양.\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 사람 좋은 솔직함으로 말했다. \"나는 십오 분만 당신 곁에 있어 본 사람이라면 누구나 알아챌, 그 빼어난 분별력과 차분하고 빈틈없으며 쓸모 있는 습성이, 지금처럼 더 절실히 필요했던 적이 없는 처지에 놓인 적이 없다오.\"\n\n소파에 앉은 그 사람은 코감기가 든 듯, 어찌나 요란하게 콧김을 내뿜던지 나는 화들짝 놀랐다.\n\n\"많은 액수에 체포되신 건가요, 어르신?\" 나는 스킴폴 씨에게 물었다.\n\n\"이보세요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 정겹게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"나도 몰라요. 몇 파운드에다 잔돈 몇 실링하고 반 페니가 어쩌고 하던 것 같긴 한데.\"\n\n\"이십사 파운드 십육 실링하고 칠 펜스 반이에요.\" 낯선 사람이 짚었다. \"고게 액수요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그게 — 어쩐지 그게,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했다. \"적은 액수처럼 들리지 않나요?\"\n\n낯선 사람은 아무 말도 하지 않고 또 한차례 콧김을 내뿜었다. 어찌나 힘찬 콧김이었던지 그가 제 자리에서 들썩 솟구치는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"스킴폴 씨는,\" 리처드가 나에게 말했다. \"내 사촌 잔다이스에게 부탁하기를 꺼리고 계세요. 요즘 들어 — 제가 알기로는, 어르신, 요즘 들어 — 라고 말씀하신 것 같은데 —\"\n\n\"아, 그렇소!\" 스킴폴 씨가 미소 지으며 받았다. \"얼마였는지, 또 언제였는지는 잊어버렸지만요. 잔다이스라면 기꺼이 또 해 주겠지만, 나에게는 미식가 같은 기분이 있어서, 도움이라면 좀 색다른 것이 좋겠다, 차라리,\" 그러고는 리처드와 나를 보며 말했다. \"새로운 토양에서, 새로운 모양의 꽃으로 너그러움을 피워 내고 싶다는 마음이 든다오.\"\n\n\"어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을까요, 서머슨 양?\" 리처드가 옆에서 나직이 말했다.\n\n나는 대답하기에 앞서, 두루뭉술하게, 그 돈이 마련되지 않으면 어떻게 되는 것이냐고 감히 물어보았다.\n\n\"감옥이지.\" 낯선 사람이 발치 바닥에 놓인 자기 모자에 손수건을 태연히 집어넣으며 말했다. \"아니면 코빈시스든가.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 어르신, 무엇이 —\"\n\n\"코빈시스 말이오?\" 낯선 사람이 말했다. \"집이오.\"\n\n리처드와 나는 다시 서로를 마주 보았다. 이 체포가 스킴폴 씨가 아니라 우리의 난처함이 되어 버렸다는 것은 더없이 별난 일이었다. 그는 정겨운 관심을 가지고 우리를 지켜보았는데, 이런 모순된 말을 굳이 해도 된다면, 거기에는 이기적인 데라곤 조금도 없어 보였다. 그는 이 곤경에서 완전히 손을 씻어 버렸고, 그것은 우리의 것이 되어 있었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 그가 마치 사람 좋게 우리를 거들어 주려는 듯 넌지시 말했다. \"(사람들이 흔히 말하듯) 거액의 재산이 걸린 대법원 소송의 당사자들이니, 리처드 군이나 그 아름다운 사촌이나, 아니면 둘이서, 뭔가에 서명을 하거나, 뭔가를 양도하거나, 무슨 약정이나 담보나 증서 같은 걸 줄 수 있지 않겠소? 그게 법률 용어로 뭐라 불리는지는 모르겠지만, 이 일을 해결해 줄 무슨 증서 같은 게 두 사람의 권한 안에 있지 않을까 싶은데?\"\n\n\"택도 없소.\" 낯선 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"정말이오?\" 스킴폴 씨가 받았다. \"이런 일은 도통 모르는 사람한테는, 그것참, 이상하게 들리는군요!\"\n\n\"이상하든 멀쩡하든,\" 낯선 사람이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"내 말하지 않았소, 택도 없다고!\"\n\n\"성질 좀 누그러뜨려요, 이보시오, 좀 누그러뜨려요!\" 스킴폴 씨가 책의 속표지에 그 사람의 머리를 자그맣게 그려 넣으며 부드럽게 타일렀다. \"당신 직업 때문에 부아를 내지는 말아요. 우리는 당신을 당신 직책과 떼어 놓을 수 있으니까요. 우리는 개인을 그 생업과 떼어 놓을 수 있어요. 우리는 사사로운 삶에서 당신이 더없이 훌륭한 사람, 본인은 미처 모를지라도 천성에 시정이 가득한 사람이 아니라고 여길 만큼 편견에 사로잡혀 있지 않다오.\"\n\n낯선 사람은 또 한차례 거센 콧김으로만 대꾸했을 뿐, 그것이 그 시정의 헌사를 받아들이는 것인지 아니면 경멸하며 물리치는 것인지는 나에게 내비치지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 이보세요, 서머슨 양, 그리고 리처드 군.\" 스킴폴 씨가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 자기가 그린 그림을 들여다보며 명랑하고 천진하고 미덥게 말했다. \"여기 보다시피 나는 나 자신을 도울 길이 도무지 없고, 온전히 당신들 손에 달려 있어요! 나는 그저 자유로워지기를 바랄 뿐이오. 나비는 자유롭지요. 인류가 설마 나비에게 허락하는 것을 해럴드 스킴폴에게는 거절하지 않겠지요!\"\n\n\"이보세요, 서머슨 양.\" 리처드가 속삭이며 말했다. \"켄지 씨에게서 받은 십 파운드가 있어요. 그걸로 어떻게 되는지 해 봐야겠어요.\"\n\n나에게는 여러 해에 걸쳐 분기마다 받은 용돈에서 모은 십오 파운드 남짓이 있었다. 나는 친척 하나 재산 하나 없이 별안간 세상에 내던져지는 무슨 일이 생길지도 모른다고 늘 생각해 왔고, 아주 빈털터리가 되지는 않도록 늘 얼마간 돈을 곁에 두려고 애써 왔다. 나는 리처드에게 이 작은 비축이 있고 지금 당장은 그것이 필요하지 않다고 말했고, 내가 그것을 가지러 간 사이에, 우리가 기꺼이 그의 빚을 갚아 드리겠다고 스킴폴 씨에게 넌지시 전해 달라고 그에게 조심스레 부탁했다.\n\n내가 돌아오자, 스킴폴 씨는 내 손에 입을 맞추며 무척 감격한 듯했다. 자기 자신 때문이 아니라(나는 그 어리둥절하고 기이한 모순을 다시 한번 느꼈다), 우리 때문이었으니, 마치 그에게는 자기를 위한 헤아림이란 있을 수 없고 오직 우리의 행복을 떠올리는 것만이 그를 움직이는 것 같았다. 리처드가, 그의 말마따나 거래에 더 멋을 더하려고, 코빈시스와(이제 스킴폴 씨는 그를 그렇게 익살스레 불렀다) 셈을 치러 달라고 나에게 청했으므로, 나는 돈을 세어 주고 필요한 영수증을 받았다. 이것 역시 스킴폴 씨를 흐뭇하게 했다.\n\n그의 찬사가 어찌나 섬세하게 베풀어졌던지, 나는 그러지 않았더라면 더 붉어졌을 얼굴을 덜 붉히며, 흰 외투를 입은 낯선 사람과 한 치의 실수도 없이 셈을 치렀다. 그는 돈을 주머니에 넣고는 짤막하게 말했다. \"그럼, 좋은 저녁 보내십쇼, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"이보시오.\" 스킴폴 씨가 절반쯤 그린 스케치를 마저 포기하고 난로에 등을 댄 채 서서 말했다. \"기분 나빠하지 말고, 내 뭐 하나 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n대답은 아마 \"그럼 어디 까놓고 물어보쇼!\"였던 것 같다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 말이오, 당신 오늘 이 일로 나설 거라는 걸 알고 있었소?\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어제 오후 차 마실 때 알았수다.\" 코빈시스가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 당신 입맛에는 영향을 안 줬소? 마음이 조금도 불편하지 않았고?\"\n\n\"눈곱만치도.\" 코빈시스가 말했다. \"오늘 당신을 놓쳐도 내일 놓칠 일은 없을 거 아뉴. 하루쯤 차이야 별것도 아니지.\"\n\n\"그러나 당신이 여기 내려올 때는,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말을 이었다. \"날이 좋았소. 해가 빛나고, 바람이 불고, 빛과 그림자가 들판을 가로질러 지나가고, 새들이 노래하고 있었지요.\"\n\n\"안 그렇다고 한 사람은 내 귀엔 없었수다.\" 코빈시스가 받았다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 오는 길에 무슨 생각을 했소?\"\n\n\"그게 뭔 소리요?\" 코빈시스가 잔뜩 부아가 난 기색으로 으르렁거렸다. \"생각이라! 나는 할 일도 많고, 그래 봤자 손에 쥐는 것도 쥐꼬리만 한데 생각이라니. 생각!\" (깊디깊은 경멸을 담아.)\n\n\"그렇다면 적어도 이런 생각은 하지 않았겠군요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말을 이었다. \"'해럴드 스킴폴은 해가 빛나는 걸 보기 좋아하고, 바람이 부는 소리 듣기를 좋아하고, 변하는 빛과 그림자 지켜보기를 좋아하고, 새들, 저 자연의 대성당에서 노래하는 성가대원들의 소리 듣기를 좋아하지. 그런데 내가 지금 해럴드 스킴폴에게서 그런 소유물의, 그의 유일한 타고난 권리인 그것의 몫을 빼앗으려는 게 아닌가!' 이런 생각은 조금도 하지 않았겠지요?\"\n\n\"나는 — 정말로 — 안 — 했소.\" 코빈시스가 말했는데, 그 생각을 깡그리 부인하는 그 옹고집이 어찌나 지독했던지, 그는 한 단어 한 단어 사이에 긴 틈을 두어야만, 게다가 마지막 단어에는 목이 삐끗할 만한 홱 젖힘까지 곁들여야만 그것을 제대로 표현할 수 있었다.\n\n\"무척 별나고 무척 흥미롭군요, 당신네 사업가들의 머릿속 작용이라는 것이 말이오!\" 스킴폴 씨가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"고맙소, 이보시오. 잘 가시오.\"\n\n우리가 자리를 비운 것이 이미 아래층에서 이상하게 보일 만큼 길어졌으므로, 나는 곧장 돌아왔고, 에이다가 난롯가에서 뜨개질을 하며 사촌 존과 이야기를 나누고 있는 것을 보았다. 이내 스킴폴 씨가 나타났고, 그 뒤를 이어 곧 리처드가 들어왔다. 나는 그날 저녁 남은 동안 잔다이스 씨에게서 주사위놀이의 첫 수업을 받느라 충분히 바빴는데, 그는 그 놀이를 무척 좋아했고, 나로서는 물론 그가 더 나은 상대가 없을 때 그와 한판 둘 수 있는, 그 자그마한 쓸모라도 되고 싶어서 되도록 빨리 그것을 배우고 싶었다. 그러나 나는 이따금, 스킴폴 씨가 자기가 작곡한 곡의 단편을 연주할 때나, 피아노에서든 첼로에서든 또 우리 식탁에서든 그가 아무런 애쓴 기색 없이 그 유쾌한 흥과 막힘없는 입담을 이어 갈 때면, 리처드와 나는 저녁 식사 이후로 체포되어 있던 듯한 그 옮아 온 인상을 여전히 떨치지 못하고 있구나, 그리고 통틀어 보면 참으로 묘한 일이구나 하는 생각이 들었다.\n\n우리가 자리를 파한 것은 밤이 깊어서였으니, 에이다가 열한 시에 일어서려 하자 스킴폴 씨가 피아노로 가서, 우리 나날을 늘리는 가장 좋은 길은 밤에서 몇 시간을 훔쳐 오는 것이라는 곡을 흥겹게 두드려 댔던 것이다, 사랑스러운 이여! 그가 촛불과 그 빛나는 얼굴을 들고 방에서 나간 것은 자정이 지나서였는데, 그가 마음만 먹었다면 우리를 동틀 녘까지 그 자리에 붙들어 두었을 성싶다. 에이다와 리처드가 젤리비 부인이 그날 받아쓰기를 이제 끝마쳤을까 궁금해하며 난롯가에서 잠시 미적거리고 있는데, 방에서 나가 있던 잔다이스 씨가 돌아왔다.\n\n\"아이고, 저런, 이게 무슨, 이게 무슨 일이냐!\" 그가 머리를 문지르며, 그 사람 좋은 짜증과 함께 방 안을 거닐면서 말했다. \"이게 무슨 소리들이냐? 릭, 얘야, 에스더, 귀여운 아이야, 너희가 무슨 짓을 한 거냐? 왜 그런 짓을 했어? 어떻게 그럴 수가 있느냐? 각자 얼마씩이었지? 바람이 또 도는구나. 온몸으로 느껴진다!\"\n\n우리 둘 다 무어라 대답해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"이리 오너라, 릭, 어서! 잠들기 전에 이걸 매듭지어야겠다. 너는 얼마나 손해를 봤느냐? 너희 둘이 그 돈을 모았다며, 그렇지! 왜 그랬느냐? 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어? 아, 이런, 그래, 정동풍이야 — 틀림없어!\"\n\n\"정말이지, 어르신.\" 리처드가 말했다. \"제가 그걸 말씀드리는 건 떳떳하지 못한 일인 것 같습니다. 스킴폴 씨가 저희를 믿고 —\"\n\n\"이런 딱한 녀석을 봤나! 그 사람은 누구든 다 믿어!\" 잔다이스 씨가 머리를 박박 문지르며 우뚝 멈춰 서서 말했다.\n\n\"정말이십니까, 어르신?\"\n\n\"누구든 다지! 그리고 다음 주에도 똑같은 곤경에 빠질 게다!\" 잔다이스 씨가 꺼져 버린 촛불을 손에 든 채 다시 큰 보폭으로 거닐며 말했다. \"그는 늘 똑같은 곤경에 빠져 있어. 그 곤경 속에서 태어났지. 그의 어머니가 몸을 풀었을 때 신문에 난 공고가 분명 이랬을 거라고 나는 정말로 믿는다. '지난 화요일, 골칫거리 빌딩의 자택에서, 스킴폴 부인이 곤경에 빠진 아들을 낳다.'\"\n\n리처드는 한바탕 시원하게 웃고는 덧붙였다. \"그래도, 어르신, 저는 그분의 신뢰를 흔들거나 저버리고 싶지는 않습니다. 그래서 그분의 비밀을 지켜야 한다는, 어르신의 더 나은 판단에 다시 따른다 하더라도, 저를 더 다그치시기 전에 한 번 헤아려 주시길 바랍니다. 물론 어르신이 그래도 다그치신다면, 저는 제가 잘못하고 있음을 알고 말씀드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋다!\" 잔다이스 씨가 다시 걸음을 멈추며, 촛대를 주머니에 넣으려고 멍하니 몇 번 애쓰면서 외쳤다. \"내가 — 여기! 이것 좀 치워 다오, 얘야. 내가 이걸로 뭘 하는지 모르겠구나. 다 바람 탓이야 — 어김없이 이런 식이지 — 너를 다그치지 않으마, 릭. 네 말이 옳을 게다. 그러나 정말이지 — 너하고 에스더를 붙들어서 — 마치 한 쌍의 여린 풋 세인트마이클 오렌지처럼 쥐어짜다니! 밤사이에 강풍이 몰아치겠구나!\"\n\n그는 이제 두 손을 오랫동안 거기 둘 작정인 양 주머니에 찔러 넣었다가, 다시 빼내어 머리를 온통 사납게 문지르기를 번갈아 했다.\n\n나는 이 틈을 타, 스킴폴 씨가 그런 모든 일에는 정말이지 어린아이여서 —라고 넌지시 운을 떼 보았다.\n\n\"응, 얘야?\" 잔다이스 씨가 그 말을 덥석 붙들며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 어린아이여서요, 어르신.\" 내가 말했다. \"여느 사람들과는 너무도 다르니까요 —\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다!\" 잔다이스 씨가 얼굴이 환해지며 말했다. \"네 여자다운 슬기가 핵심을 찌르는구나. 그는 어린아이야 — 영락없는 어린아이지. 내가 그를 처음 입에 올렸을 때, 그가 어린아이라고 너희에게 말했잖느냐.\"\n\n물론이지요! 물론이고말고요! 우리는 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그는 어린아이지. 자, 그렇지 않으냐?\" 잔다이스 씨가 점점 더 얼굴이 환해지며 물었다.\n\n정말 그렇습니다, 우리는 말했다.\n\n\"가만 생각해 보면, 그를 한순간이라도 어른으로 여긴 너희가 — 아니, 내가 — 더없이 유치한 짓을 한 거다.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"그를 책임지게 할 수는 없어. 해럴드 스킴폴이 꿍꿍이나 계략을 품다니, 아니면 결과를 헤아리다니, 가당키나 하냐! 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n그의 환한 얼굴을 둘러싼 먹구름이 걷히는 것을 보는 것이, 그리고 그가 그토록 진심으로 흐뭇해하는 것을 보는 것이, 그리고 그 기쁨의 근원이 누군가를 단죄하거나 의심하거나 남몰래 탓하는 일로 괴로워하는 그 선량함이라는 것을 — 모를 수가 없으니 — 아는 것이, 어찌나 더없이 흐뭇했던지, 나는 에이다가 그의 웃음을 따라 웃으면서 눈에 눈물이 어리는 것을 보았고, 내 눈에서도 그 눈물을 느꼈다.\n\n\"이런, 내가 무슨 대구 대가리에 어깻죽지 같은 멍청이람.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"그런 걸 일깨워 줘야 하다니! 이 일 전체가 처음부터 끝까지 어린아이를 그대로 보여 주는구나. 어린아이가 아니고서야 누가 이 일의 당사자로 하필 너희 둘을 콕 집을 생각을 했겠느냐! 어린아이가 아니고서야 누가 너희한테 그 돈이 있을 거라고 생각했겠어! 그게 천 파운드였다 해도 매한가지였을 거다!\" 잔다이스 씨가 온 얼굴을 환히 빛내며 말했다.\n\n우리는 모두 그날 밤의 경험으로 그 말이 옳다고 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고, 그렇고말고!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 릭, 에스더, 그리고 너도, 에이다, 네 자그마한 지갑이라고 그의 철없음에서 안전한지 모르겠으니 — 나는 다 같이 약속을 받아 두어야겠다, 다시는 이런 일을 결코 하지 않겠다고. 선금은 안 된다! 단돈 육 펜스도 안 돼.\"\n\n우리는 모두 굳게 약속했고, 리처드는 우리야 어길 위험이 없다는 것을 나에게 일깨우려는 듯 나를 향해 흥겨운 눈짓을 보내며 제 주머니를 톡톡 두드렸다.\n\n\"스킴폴로 말하자면,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"사람이 살 만한 인형의 집에다 끼니나 잘 챙겨 주고, 빚을 지거나 돈을 빌릴 양철 인형 몇이나 마련해 주면, 그 애한테는 인생살이로 더없이 그만일 게다. 지금쯤 그는 아이처럼 잠들어 있겠지. 나도 이 더 약은 머리를 더 세속적인 베개에 누일 때가 되었구나. 잘들 자거라, 얘들아. 신의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n우리가 촛불을 켜기도 전에 그가 미소 띤 얼굴로 다시 빼꼼 들여다보며 말했다. \"오! 풍향계를 보고 왔다. 바람에 대해서는 내가 괜한 호들갑을 떤 거더구나. 남쪽이야!\" 그러고는 혼자 흥얼거리며 가 버렸다.\n\n에이다와 나는 위층에서 잠시 이런저런 이야기를 나누다가, 바람에 대한 이 변덕이 꾸며 낸 것이며, 그가 그 핑계를 쓰는 것은, 차마 감추지 못하는 어떤 실망의 진짜 원인을 탓하거나 누군가를 깎아내리거나 낮잡아 보느니, 차라리 그것으로 둘러대기 위해서라는 데 뜻을 모았다. 우리는 이것이 그의 별난 다정함을, 그리고 날씨와 바람을(특히 그가 그토록 다른 목적에 골라 쓴 그 운수 사나운 바람을) 자기네 골나고 침울한 심사의 핑곗거리로 삼는 그 짜증스러운 사람들과 그의 차이를, 참으로 잘 보여 준다고 생각했다.\n\n정말이지, 이 단 하루 저녁에 그에 대한 애정이 내 고마움에 어찌나 많이 더해졌던지, 나는 그 뒤섞인 감정을 통해 벌써 그를 이해하기 시작한 것이기를 바랐다. 스킴폴 씨에게서든 젤리비 부인에게서든 겉으로 드러나는 어떤 모순들을 내가 능히 화해시키리라고는 기대할 수 없었으니, 나에게는 경험도 실제적인 지식도 너무 적었기 때문이다. 화해시키려고 애쓰지도 않았으니, 혼자 있을 때 내 생각은 에이다와 리처드로, 그리고 두 사람에 관해 내가 전해 받은 듯한 그 속내로 분주했기 때문이다. 어쩌면 바람 탓에 조금 들떴는지, 내 공상은 끝까지 온전히 욕심 없는 것이 되기를 좀처럼 받아들이지 않았다, 할 수만 있다면 그러도록 타일렀을 텐데도. 그것은 대모님의 집으로 거슬러 올라가, 그 사이의 자취를 따라 더듬어 오면서, 거기 어둠 속에서 이따금 떨리곤 했던 그 어렴풋한 억측들을 — 잔다이스 씨가 내 가장 어린 시절의 내력에 대해 무엇을 알고 있을까 하는, 심지어 그가 내 아버지일 수도 있지 않을까 하는 억측까지 — 불러일으켰다, 비록 그 부질없는 꿈은 이제 말끔히 사라지고 없었지만.\n\n이제 그것은 다 사라지고 없다고, 나는 난롯가에서 일어서며 떠올렸다. 지나간 일을 곱씹는 것은 내가 할 바가 아니요, 명랑한 마음과 고마운 가슴으로 행동하는 것이 내가 할 바였다. 그래서 나는 스스로에게 말했다. \"에스더, 에스더, 에스더! 본분이야, 얘야!\" 그러고는 살림 열쇠가 담긴 자그마한 바구니를 흔들어, 작은 종소리처럼 짤랑이게 하니, 그 소리가 나를 희망차게 잠자리로 불러들였다.\n\n에스더가 잠들었을 때나, 에스더가 깨어 있을 때나, 링컨셔의 그 저택은 여전히 비에 젖어 있다. 비는 끊임없이 내린다. 뚝, 뚝, 뚝, 밤낮없이 넓적한 판석을 깐 테라스 보도, 그러니까 유령의 길 위로 떨어진다. 링컨셔 일대의 날씨가 어찌나 고약한지, 제아무리 생기 넘치는 상상력이라도 다시 맑게 갤 날을 떠올리기란 좀처럼 어렵다. 그렇다고 그곳에 상상력이 넘쳐흐를 일도 없으니, 레스터 경이 여기 없는 까닭이다(설령 있다 한들, 솔직히 말해 그 방면에서는 별 보탬이 못 되겠지만). 경은 마님과 함께 파리에 가 있고, 적막이 거무스름한 날개를 펼친 채 체스니 월드 위에 웅크리고 앉아 시름에 잠겨 있다.\n\n체스니 월드의 미물들 사이에서는 어쩌면 상상의 움직임이 조금쯤 일렁이고 있을지 모른다. 마구간의 말들 말이다. 휑뎅그렁한 붉은 벽돌 안마당에 늘어선 기다란 마구간, 작은 탑에 커다란 종이 달려 있고, 그 곁에 사는 데다 그 어깨에 즐겨 내려앉는 비둘기들이 늘 들여다보며 묻기라도 하는 듯한, 큼직한 문자판이 달린 시계가 있는 그곳, 그 말들이라면 이따금 맑은 날의 풍경을 머릿속에 그려볼 수도 있겠고, 어쩌면 마부들보다 솜씨 좋은 화가일지도 모른다. 거친 들판을 가로지르는 일로 그토록 이름난 늙은 적갈색 말은, 여물통 옆 쇠창살 창으로 커다란 눈알을 굴리며, 다른 철이면 그곳에서 반짝이던 싱싱한 잎사귀와 솔솔 풍기던 냄새를 떠올리고, 사냥개들과 함께 신나게 내달리고 있을지 모른다. 정작 사람 일꾼은 옆 칸을 치우면서 쇠스랑과 자작나무 빗자루를 손에서 놓지 못하는데도 말이다. 문 맞은편이 제자리인 잿빛 말은, 문이 열릴 때면 안달이 나서 고삐를 절그럭거리며 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 그토록 애타게 고개를 돌리는데, 문을 연 사람이 \"워, 워, 잿빛아, 가만! 오늘은 아무도 너 찾지 않아!\" 하고 일러주는 그 말을, 어쩌면 사람만큼이나 잘 알아듣고 있을지 모른다. 함께 마구간에 매인, 겉보기엔 단조롭고 서로 데면데면한 듯한 여섯 마리는, 문이 닫혀 있는 비 오는 긴 시간 동안, 하인 방이나 데드록 암스에서 오가는 것보다 훨씬 활기찬 대화를 나누고 있을지도 모르고, 심지어 구석 빈 칸에 있는 조랑말을 가르치며(어쩌면 버릇을 망쳐가며) 시간을 달래고 있을지도 모른다.\n\n마찬가지로, 안마당 개집에서 커다란 머리를 앞발 위에 얹고 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 마스티프도 뜨거운 햇볕을 떠올리고 있을지 모른다. 마구간 건물의 그림자가 시시각각 옮겨가며 녀석의 인내심을 바닥내고, 하루 중 어느 때면 제 집 그림자보다 더 너른 피난처도 남겨주지 않을 때, 녀석은 거기 꼿꼿이 앉아 헐떡이며 컹컹 짧게 으르렁대고는, 제 몸뚱이와 제 사슬 말고 물어뜯을 무언가가 몹시도 그리워진다. 그래서 지금도, 반쯤 잠든 채 연신 눈을 끔벅이며, 손님으로 가득 찬 저택, 마차로 가득 찬 마차고, 말로 가득 찬 마구간, 말을 돌보는 일꾼들로 가득 찬 부속 건물을 떠올리다가, 지금이 어떤지 종잡지 못해 밖으로 나와 사정을 살핀다. 그러고는 안달하듯 몸을 부르르 떨며 마음속으로 으르렁댄다. \"비, 비, 비! 비밖에 없잖아, 식구라곤 하나도 없고!\" 그러고는 다시 들어가 침울하게 하품하며 드러눕는다.\n\n공원 건너편 사육장 건물의 개들도 그렇다. 녀석들도 안절부절못하는 발작이 도지고, 바람이 유난히 끈질긴 날이면 그 구슬픈 울음소리가 저택 안까지, 위층에도 아래층에도 마님의 침실에도 알려질 지경이다. 빗방울이 그 게으른 몸뚱이들 둘레에 후드득거리는 동안, 녀석들은 마음속으로 온 동네를 누비며 사냥하고 있을지 모른다. 마찬가지로, 제 꼬리가 제 정체를 일러바치는 토끼들, 나무뿌리 굴을 들락날락 깡충대는 토끼들도, 귀가 바람에 나부끼던 산들바람 부는 날들이나, 갉아먹을 달콤하고 어린 새싹이 돋는 그 흥겨운 철을 떠올리며 생기가 돌지 모른다. 가금 마당의 칠면조는 늘 제 계급에 맺힌 한이 있는데(아마 크리스마스 탓이리라), 베어 넘긴 나무들 사이 오솔길로 빠져나가 헛간과 보리가 있던 곳에 이르렀던, 부당하게 빼앗긴 그 여름 아침을 떠올리고 있을지 모른다. 스무 자 높이 옛 대문 아래를 몸을 숙이고 지나는 불만 많은 거위는, 우리가 알아들을 수만 있다면, 대문이 땅에 그림자를 드리우는 날씨가 더 좋다고 뒤뚱뒤뚱 떠들어댈지도 모른다.\n\n어쨌든, 체스니 월드에서 그 밖에 별달리 일렁이는 상상이란 없다. 어쩌다 한 번 그런 것이 조금 일더라도, 그것은 메아리가 울리는 그 오래된 곳의 자그마한 소리처럼 멀리멀리 퍼져나가다가, 으레 유령과 수수께끼로 이어지고 만다.\n\n링컨셔에는 비가 어찌나 세차게, 어찌나 오래 쏟아졌는지, 체스니 월드의 늙은 가정부 라운스웰 부인은 몇 번이나 안경을 벗어 닦으며 빗방울이 안경알에 묻은 게 아닌지 확인했다. 라운스웰 부인은 빗소리만 들어도 충분히 알 수 있었을 테지만, 귀가 좀 어두운 탓이다. 본인은 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사실을 인정하려 들지 않는다. 부인은 훌륭한 노부인으로, 미모가 있고, 위엄이 있으며, 놀랍도록 단정한 데다, 등허리며 가슴받이가 어찌나 꼿꼿한지, 죽은 뒤 그 코르셋이 알고 보니 구식 집안에서 쓰던 넓적한 무쇠 벽난로 살이었다 한들, 부인을 아는 누구도 놀랄 까닭이 없을 정도다. 날씨는 라운스웰 부인에게 별 영향을 못 미친다. 저택은 어떤 날씨에도 그 자리에 있고, 부인 표현대로 저택이야말로 \"부인이 바라보는 것\"이다. 부인은 자기 방에 앉아 있는데(일층 옆 복도에 있는 그 방은 아치형 창이 매끈한 사각 안뜰을 내려다보고, 그 안뜰에는 매끈하고 둥근 나무들과 매끈하고 둥근 돌덩이들이 일정한 간격으로 늘어서 있어, 마치 나무들이 돌을 가지고 볼링이라도 할 참인 듯하다), 저택 전체가 부인의 마음 위에 고요히 깃들어 있다. 부인은 필요할 때 저택을 열어젖히고 분주히 호들갑을 떨 수도 있지만, 지금은 닫혀 있어, 라운스웰 부인의 무쇠로 두른 듯한 너른 가슴팍 위에서 장엄한 잠에 빠져 있다.\n\n라운스웰 부인 없는 체스니 월드를 떠올린다는 것은 거의 불가능에 가까운 일이지만, 부인이 이곳에 있은 지는 고작 오십 년이다. 비 내리는 오늘 그 세월을 물으면, 부인은 이렇게 답하리라. \"하늘의 가호로 화요일까지 살아 있다면, 오십 년 석 달 하고도 보름이지요.\" 라운스웰 씨는 변발이라는 예쁘장한 유행이 사라지기 얼마 전에 세상을 떠났고, 제 변발은(저세상까지 가지고 갔다면 모를까) 공원 안 묘지의, 이끼 낀 현관 곁 한구석에 다소곳이 묻어두었다. 그는 장이 서는 읍내에서 태어났고, 그의 젊은 미망인도 그러했다. 부인이 이 집안에서 자리를 잡기 시작한 것은 선대 레스터 경 시절이었고, 그 시작은 식료품 저장실이었다.\n\n지금의 데드록가 당주는 더없이 훌륭한 주인이다. 그는 제게 딸린 모든 식솔이 개개의 인격이나 의향이나 의견이라곤 깡그리 없는 존재라 여기며, 자신이 바로 그것들을 가질 필요를 대신하려고 태어난 사람이라 굳게 믿는다. 만에 하나 그와 반대되는 사실을 알게 된다면, 그는 그저 얼이 빠져버릴 것이다. 십중팔구 다시는 정신을 못 차리고, 헐떡이다 숨이나 거두는 것 말고는 회복할 길이 없으리라. 그래도 그는 여전히 훌륭한 주인이며, 그렇게 처신하는 것이 제 신분의 한 몫이라 여긴다. 그는 라운스웰 부인을 매우 아낀다. 부인이야말로 더없이 존경할 만하고 믿음직한 여인이라고 말한다. 그는 체스니 월드에 내려올 때나 떠날 때나 늘 부인과 악수를 나눈다. 그리고 만일 그가 중병에 걸리거나, 사고로 쓰러지거나, 마차에 깔리거나, 데드록가 사람이 불리한 처지에 놓였음을 드러낼 어떤 상황에 빠진다면, 말을 할 수만 있다면 이렇게 말하리라. \"다들 물러가고, 여기 라운스웰 부인을 불러오게!\" 그런 고비에서는 제 위엄이 다른 누구보다 부인 곁에서 더 안전하다고 느끼는 것이다.\n\n라운스웰 부인은 시름을 겪어보았다. 부인에게는 아들이 둘 있었는데, 그중 작은아들은 빗나가서 군인이 되어 떠난 뒤 영영 돌아오지 않았다. 지금 이 순간까지도, 라운스웰 부인의 차분하던 두 손은 그 아들 이야기가 나오면 평정을 잃고, 가슴받이에서 풀려나 안절부절못하며 부인 둘레를 맴돈다. 그러면서 부인은 그 아이가 얼마나 싹수 있는 아이였는지, 얼마나 훤칠한 아이였는지, 얼마나 명랑하고 사람 좋고 영리한 아이였는지 늘어놓는다! 둘째 아들은 체스니 월드에서 앞날을 보장받아 때가 되면 집사가 되었을 터였다. 그러나 그는 학생 시절부터 냄비로 증기기관을 만들고, 새들이 되도록 힘 안 들이고 제가 마실 물을 길어 올리도록 길들이는 데 빠져들었으니, 교묘한 수압 장치로 도와준 덕에 목마른 카나리아는 말 그대로 어깨로 바퀴를 밀기만 하면 일이 끝나는 식이었다. 이런 성벽은 라운스웰 부인의 속을 몹시 태웠다. 부인은 그것이 와트 타일러 쪽으로 한 걸음 내딛는 일이라고 어머니다운 비통함으로 느꼈으니, 레스터 경이 연기와 높다란 굴뚝이 따라붙는 모든 기예에 능한 자들에 대해 그러한 막연한 인상을 품고 있음을 잘 알았던 것이다. 그러나 그 가엾은 어린 반역자는(그 밖으로는 온순한 데다 무척 끈기 있는 젊은이였다) 나이가 들어도 마음을 고쳐먹을 기미를 보이기는커녕, 도리어 역직기 모형을 만들어내자, 부인은 눈물깨나 흘리며 그 비뚤어진 행실을 준남작에게 아뢰지 않을 수 없었다. \"라운스웰 부인,\" 레스터 경이 말했다. \"알다시피 나는 어떤 주제로도 누구와 입씨름하는 데 도무지 응할 수 없는 사람이오. 그 아이는 떼어내는 편이 낫겠소. 어디 공장에라도 들여보내는 게 좋겠소. 이런 기질을 타고난 아이한테는 북쪽으로 더 올라간 철공 지대가 맞춤한 방향일 듯하오.\" 그리하여 그는 더 북쪽으로 갔고, 더 북쪽에서 자라났다. 레스터 데드록 경이 어머니를 뵈러 체스니 월드에 온 그를 행여 마주쳤다거나, 그 뒤로 한 번이라도 그를 떠올린 적이 있다면, 분명 그를 한 무리의 음험하고 험상궂은 음모자 수천 명 가운데 하나로만 여겼으리라. 한 주에 두세 밤씩 횃불을 들고 불법한 목적으로 떼 지어 나서는 무리 말이다.\n\n그래도 라운스웰 부인의 아들은 천성과 기예의 도움으로 무사히 자라나, 제 자리를 잡고, 장가를 들고, 라운스웰 부인의 손자를 낳았다. 그 손자는 견습 과정을 마치고, 견문을 넓혀 세상살이라는 모험을 준비하는 일을 완성하라며 보내진 먼 나라들에서 막 여행을 마치고 돌아와, 바로 오늘 체스니 월드의 라운스웰 부인 방에서 벽난로 선반에 기대어 서 있다.\n\n\"몇 번을 봐도, 와트, 널 보니 반갑구나! 다시 봐도, 와트, 널 보니 반가워!\" 라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"참 훤칠한 젊은이가 됐어. 가엾은 네 삼촌 조지를 빼닮았구나. 아!\" 라운스웰 부인의 손이, 늘 그렇듯 그 이름을 입에 올리자 안절부절못한다.\n\n\"다들 제가 아버지를 닮았다고 하던데요, 할머니.\"\n\n\"아버지도 닮았지, 얘야. 하지만 가엾은 네 삼촌 조지를 가장 닮았어! 그리고 네 어진 아버지도.\" 라운스웰 부인이 다시 두 손을 모은다. \"네 아버지는 잘 있느냐?\"\n\n\"무엇 하나 빠짐없이 번창하고 있어요, 할머니.\"\n\n\"고마운 일이지!\" 라운스웰 부인은 아들을 사랑하지만, 마치 그가 적진으로 넘어가버린 더없이 명예로운 군인이기라도 한 양, 그를 향해 애잔한 마음을 품고 있다.\n\n\"아주 행복하다더냐?\" 부인이 묻는다.\n\n\"아주요.\"\n\n\"고마운 일이야! 그래, 그래서 네 아버지가 너를 제 길을 따르도록 키워서, 외국이며 이런저런 곳으로 보낸 게로구나? 그래, 그 사람이 제일 잘 알겠지. 체스니 월드 너머에 내가 모르는 세상이 있을 수도 있지. 나라고 젊은 것도 아니고. 게다가 나도 훌륭한 분들을 어지간히 많이 겪어봤다만!\"\n\n\"할머니,\" 젊은이가 화제를 돌리며 말한다. \"방금 할머니 곁에서 만난 그 아가씨, 참 어여쁘던데요. 로자라고 부르시던가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야. 마을 어느 미망인의 딸이란다. 요즘은 하녀 가르치기가 어찌나 힘든지, 어려서부터 내 곁에 두었지. 잘 배우는 아이라 앞날이 트일 게다. 벌써 저택 안내도 아주 곱게 해낸단다. 여기 내 식탁에서 나랑 함께 지내지.\"\n\n\"제가 그 아가씨를 쫓아버린 건 아닌가 모르겠네요?\"\n\n\"우리가 집안일을 의논할 거라 여겼겠지, 아마. 무척 얌전한 아이야. 젊은 여자한테는 좋은 자질이지. 게다가 예전보다,\" 라운스웰 부인이 가슴받이를 한껏 부풀리며 말한다. \"더 귀해진 자질이고 말이다!\"\n\n젊은이는 경험에서 우러난 가르침을 받든다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙인다. 라운스웰 부인이 귀를 기울인다.\n\n\"바퀴 소리!\" 부인이 말한다. 곁에 있는 젊은이의 더 젊은 귀에는 진작 들려오던 소리다. \"이런 날에 웬 바퀴 소리람, 세상에?\"\n\n잠깐 사이를 두고 문 두드리는 소리가 난다. \"들어와라!\" 검은 눈에 검은 머리, 수줍은 시골 미인이 들어온다. 발그레하면서도 고운 그 생기가 어찌나 싱싱한지, 머리칼에 들이친 빗방울이 갓 꺾은 꽃에 맺힌 이슬 같다.\n\n\"무슨 손님이냐, 로자?\" 라운스웰 부인이 묻는다.\n\n\"이륜마차에 탄 젊은 분 둘인데, 저택을 둘러보고 싶다고 해요, 마님. 네, 외람되지만, 제가 그렇게 일렀어요!\" 가정부가 안 된다는 손짓을 하자 로자가 얼른 덧붙인다. \"현관문으로 나가서, 날도 안 맞고 시간도 안 맞는다고 했어요. 그랬더니 마차를 몰던 젊은 분이 빗속에서 모자를 벗고는, 이 명함을 마님께 갖다드려 달라고 사정사정하지 뭐예요.\"\n\n\"네가 읽어보렴, 와트야.\" 가정부가 말한다.\n\n로자가 어찌나 수줍어하며 명함을 건네는지, 둘 사이에서 그것을 떨어뜨려, 함께 주우려다 하마터면 이마를 부딪칠 뻔한다. 로자는 아까보다 더 수줍어진다.\n\n명함이 알려주는 정보라고는 \"거피 씨\"가 전부다.\n\n\"거피!\" 라운스웰 부인이 되뇐다. \"거피 씨라! 별일이군, 들어본 적도 없는 사람인데!\"\n\n\"외람되지만, 그분도 저한테 그렇게 말했어요!\" 로자가 말한다. \"그런데 자기랑 다른 젊은 신사분이 어젯밤에야 우편마차로 런던에서 내려왔다고 했어요. 오늘 아침 십 마일 떨어진 데서 열리는 치안판사 회합에 볼일이 있어서요. 그런데 그 볼일이 금세 끝났는데, 체스니 월드 이야기를 익히 들어온 데다 도무지 무얼 하며 시간을 보내야 할지 몰라서, 빗속을 뚫고 구경하러 왔다네요. 변호사들이래요. 자기는 토킹혼 씨 사무소 사람은 아니지만, 필요하다면 토킹혼 씨 이름을 대도 괜찮을 거라고 했어요.\" 말을 마치고 보니 자기가 꽤나 긴 이야기를 늘어놓았음을 깨닫고, 로자는 그 어느 때보다 수줍어진다.\n\n그런데 토킹혼 씨로 말하자면, 어떤 의미에서는 이 저택과 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 사람이며, 게다가 라운스웰 부인의 유언장을 작성해준 사람으로 통한다. 노부인은 마음을 누그러뜨려, 호의를 베푸는 셈치고 방문객들을 들이기로 하고, 로자를 내보낸다. 그런데 손자가 문득 저도 저택을 둘러보고 싶은 마음이 동해, 일행에 끼겠다고 한다. 손자가 그런 관심을 보이는 것이 기쁜 할머니는 그를 따라나선다. 정작 손자는, 공평하게 말하자면, 할머니를 번거롭게 하기를 몹시 꺼리는데도 말이다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 마님!\" 거피 씨가 현관에서 비에 젖은 두꺼운 외투를 벗으며 말한다. \"저희 런던 변호사들이야 바깥나들이가 흔치 않으니, 어쩌다 나오면 본전을 톡톡히 뽑고 싶거든요, 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n늙은 가정부는 점잖으면서도 근엄한 몸가짐으로 거대한 계단을 향해 손짓한다. 거피 씨와 그 친구가 로자를 따른다. 라운스웰 부인과 손자가 그들을 따른다. 젊은 정원사 하나가 앞장서서 덧문을 연다.\n\n저택을 둘러보러 다니는 이들이 으레 그렇듯, 거피 씨와 그 친구는 제대로 시작하기도 전에 녹초가 된다. 둘은 엉뚱한 곳에서 어슬렁대고, 엉뚱한 것을 바라보며, 정작 봐야 할 것에는 무심하고, 새 방이 열릴 때마다 하품을 하고, 깊은 의기소침을 드러내며, 누가 봐도 진이 빠져 있다. 그들이 차례로 들어서는 방마다, 저택 못지않게 꼿꼿한 라운스웰 부인은 창가 자리나 그런 구석에 따로 떨어져 앉아, 로자의 설명에 위엄 있는 만족의 빛을 띠고 귀를 기울인다. 손자가 어찌나 그 설명에 열중하는지, 로자는 그 어느 때보다 수줍어진다. 그리고 그 어느 때보다 어여뻐진다. 그렇게 그들은 방에서 방으로 옮겨 다니며, 젊은 정원사가 빛을 들일 때면 그림 속 데드록가 사람들을 잠깐씩 되살려냈다가, 그가 다시 빛을 가리면 그들을 무덤으로 돌려보낸다. 시달릴 대로 시달린 거피 씨와 위로할 길 없는 그 친구에게는, 데드록가 사람들이 끝도 없이 이어지는 것만 같다. 그 집안의 위대함이란 칠백 년 동안 이렇다 할 일이라곤 도무지 한 적이 없다는 데 있는 듯하다.\n\n체스니 월드의 그 기다란 응접실조차 거피 씨의 기운을 되살리지 못한다. 그는 어찌나 풀이 죽었는지 문턱에서 고개를 떨구고, 안으로 들어설 기력조차 거의 없다. 그런데 벽난로 선반 위에 걸린 초상화 한 점이, 당대의 유행 화가가 그린 그 그림이, 마법처럼 그에게 작용한다. 그는 순식간에 기운을 차린다. 그는 예사롭지 않은 관심으로 그것을 뚫어지게 본다. 그것에 못 박힌 듯, 홀린 듯하다.\n\n\"이런!\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"저분은 누구죠?\"\n\n\"벽난로 위 그림은,\" 로자가 말한다. \"지금의 데드록 마님 초상화예요. 완벽하게 닮았다는 평을 듣고, 화가의 최고 걸작으로 꼽힌답니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사,\" 거피 씨가 일종의 당혹감에 휩싸여 친구를 바라보며 말한다. \"내가 저분을 본 적이 있을 리가 없는데. 그런데도 아는 얼굴이야! 이 그림이 판화로 새겨진 적이 있나요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"그림이 판화로 새겨진 적은 없어요. 레스터 경께서 늘 허락하지 않으셨거든요.\"\n\n\"이거 참!\" 거피 씨가 나직한 목소리로 말한다. \"내가 저 그림을 이렇게 잘 아는 게 어찌나 묘한지, 정말이지 알다가도 모르겠네! 그래, 저분이 데드록 마님이라 이거지!\"\n\n\"오른쪽 그림은 지금의 레스터 데드록 경이세요. 왼쪽 그림은 그분의 아버님, 돌아가신 레스터 경이고요.\"\n\n거피 씨에게는 이 두 대인의 모습이 눈에 들어오지도 않는다. \"도무지 영문을 모르겠어요,\" 그는 여전히 초상화를 응시하며 말한다. \"내가 저 그림을 이토록 잘 알다니! 정말이지,\" 거피 씨가 둘레를 둘러보며 덧붙인다. \"꿈에서 저 그림을 본 게 틀림없는 것 같단 말이죠, 아시겠어요!\"\n\n거피 씨의 꿈 따위에 특별히 관심을 두는 사람이 그 자리에 없으니, 그 추측은 더 캐묻지 않는다. 그러나 그는 여전히 초상화에 어찌나 빠져 있는지, 젊은 정원사가 덧문을 닫을 때까지 그 앞에서 꼼짝도 않고 서 있다. 그러다 멍한 상태로 방을 나서는데, 그 멍한 꼴이 관심이라기엔 묘하지만 그럭저럭 관심을 대신하기는 한다. 그러고는 어리둥절한 눈길로 다음 방들로 따라 들어가는데, 마치 사방에서 데드록 마님을 다시 찾으려는 듯하다.\n\n그는 더는 마님을 보지 못한다. 그는 마지막으로 안내되는 마님의 방들이 무척 우아하다고 보고, 마님이 얼마 전 자신을 죽도록 따분하게 만든 그 날씨를 내다보던 바로 그 창으로 밖을 내다본다. 만물에는 끝이 있는 법, 사람들이 무진 애를 써가며 구경하러 왔다가 채 보기도 전에 싫증을 내는 저택조차 그러하다. 그는 구경의 끝에 다다랐고, 싱싱한 시골 미인은 설명의 끝에 다다랐다. 그 끝맺음은 늘 이러하다. \"아래쪽 테라스는 많은 분이 감탄하시는 곳이에요. 집안에 전해 내려오는 옛이야기에서 따와, 유령의 길이라 부른답니다.\"\n\n\"설마?\" 거피 씨가 게걸스러운 호기심으로 묻는다. \"무슨 이야긴데요, 아가씨? 혹시 그림과 관련된 이야긴가요?\"\n\n\"그 이야기 좀 들려주세요.\" 와트가 반쯤 속삭이듯 말한다.\n\n\"저는 모르는걸요, 도련님.\" 로자는 그 어느 때보다 수줍어한다.\n\n\"방문객들에게 들려주는 이야기는 아니랍니다. 이젠 거의 잊혔지요.\" 가정부가 앞으로 나서며 말한다. \"그저 집안에 전해 내려오는 일화에 지나지 않아요.\"\n\n\"거듭 여쭙는 걸 너그러이 봐주십시오만, 혹시 그림과 무슨 관련이 있습니까, 마님?\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"정말이지, 저 그림을 생각하면 할수록 더 잘 아는 것만 같은데, 어떻게 아는지는 도무지 모르겠으니 드리는 말씀입니다!\"\n\n그 이야기는 그림과 아무 관련이 없다. 가정부가 그것은 장담할 수 있다. 거피 씨는 알려주어 고맙다고 하고, 게다가 두루 신세를 졌다고 한다. 그는 친구와 함께 물러나, 젊은 정원사의 안내를 받아 다른 계단으로 내려가고, 이윽고 마차를 몰아 떠나는 소리가 들린다. 어느덧 땅거미가 진다. 라운스웰 부인은 자기 곁 두 젊은 청자의 입이 무겁다는 것을 믿을 수 있으니, 그 테라스가 어쩌다 그 유령 같은 이름을 얻게 되었는지 이들에게 들려줄 수 있다.\n\n부인은 빠르게 어두워지는 창가의 커다란 의자에 자리를 잡고 이야기를 들려준다. \"찰스 일세 임금님의 그 사악한 시절에, 얘들아. 물론 내 말은, 그 훌륭하신 임금님에 맞서 작당한 반역도들의 그 사악한 시절을 두고 하는 말이다만. 모버리 데드록 경이 체스니 월드의 주인이셨지. 그 시절 이전에 이 집안에 유령 이야기가 있었는지는 나도 모르겠구나. 십중팔구 있었을 성싶다만.\"\n\n라운스웰 부인이 이런 생각을 품는 까닭은, 그토록 유서 깊고 지체 높은 집안이라면 마땅히 유령 하나쯤 가질 권리가 있다고 여기기 때문이다. 부인은 유령을 상류층의 특권 가운데 하나로, 평민은 감히 넘볼 수 없는 고상한 표지로 여긴다.\n\n\"모버리 데드록 경께서는,\" 라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"말할 것도 없이 그 거룩한 순교자 편이셨다. 그런데 핏줄에 집안의 피라곤 한 방울도 섞이지 않은 그분의 마님은, 그릇된 편을 두둔하셨다고들 해. 듣자 하니 마님은 찰스 임금님의 적들 가운데 친척이 있어, 그들과 편지를 주고받으며 정보를 넘겼다는 게야. 임금님의 대의를 따르는 이 고장 양반들이 이곳에 모일 때면, 마님이 늘 그분들 생각보다 회합 방문 가까이 다가가 계셨다더구나. 와트야, 테라스를 따라 지나가는 발소리 같은 게 들리지 않니?\"\n\n로자가 가정부 곁으로 바짝 다가선다.\n\n\"빗방울이 돌 위에 떨어지는 소리는 들려요,\" 젊은이가 답한다. \"그리고 묘한 메아리도 들리는데요, 메아리겠지요, 절뚝거리는 발소리랑 꼭 닮았어요.\"\n\n가정부가 무겁게 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 잇는다. \"한편으로는 두 분 사이의 이런 갈라섬 때문에, 또 한편으로는 다른 까닭들로, 모버리 경과 마님은 풍파 많은 삶을 사셨다. 마님은 거만한 성미를 지닌 분이셨지. 두 분은 나이로 보나 성품으로 보나 잘 어울리지 못했고, 사이를 누그러뜨려줄 자식도 없었어. 마님이 끔찍이 아끼던 오라버니, 젊은 양반이 내전에서 목숨을 잃은 뒤로(모버리 경의 가까운 친척 손에 그리되었지), 마님의 원한이 어찌나 사무쳤던지 시집온 그 가문 자체를 미워하셨다. 데드록가 사람들이 임금님의 대의를 받들어 체스니 월드를 떠나 말을 달리려 할 때면, 마님이 한 번 이상 한밤중에 마구간으로 몰래 내려가 말들의 다리를 절게 만들었다더구나. 전해 오는 이야기로는, 한번은 그런 시각에 남편 되시는 분이 마님이 계단을 미끄러지듯 내려가는 모습을 보고, 자기가 아끼는 말이 있는 칸까지 뒤따라 들어갔다는 게야. 거기서 그분이 마님의 손목을 붙잡았는데, 실랑이 끝이었는지, 넘어지면서였는지, 아니면 놀란 말이 발길질을 해댄 탓이었는지, 마님은 엉덩이를 다쳐 절게 되셨고, 그 시각부터 시름시름 야위어가기 시작하셨단다.\"\n\n가정부가 목소리를 속삭임보다 조금 큰 정도로 낮춘다.\n\n\"마님은 자태가 고운 데다 거동도 기품 있는 분이셨다. 그런데 그렇게 변해버린 것을 한 번도 탓하지 않으셨어. 절름발이가 됐다거나 아프다거나, 누구에게도 입에 올리지 않으셨지. 그저 날마다 테라스를 걸으려 애쓰셨다. 돌난간에 의지해서, 오르락내리락, 오르락내리락, 오르락내리락, 볕에서도 그늘에서도, 날이 갈수록 더 힘겹게 걸으셨어. 그러던 어느 날 오후, 그날 밤 이후로 무슨 말로 구슬려도 마님이 한마디도 입을 열지 않으셨던 그 남편 되시는 분이, 커다란 남쪽 창가에 서 계시다가 마님이 보도 위로 쓰러지는 것을 보셨단다. 그분이 황급히 내려가 부축하려 하셨지만, 마님은 몸을 굽히는 그분을 밀쳐내고는, 그분을 차갑게 똑바로 쏘아보며 말씀하셨지. '나는 내가 걷던 이곳에서 죽겠어요. 그리고 무덤에 들어가서도 이곳을 걷겠어요. 이 집안의 오만이 꺾일 때까지 나는 이곳을 걸을 거예요. 그리고 이 집에 재앙이 닥치거나 치욕이 닥칠 때면, 데드록가 사람들은 내 발소리에 귀를 기울이게 될 거예요!'\"\n\n와트가 로자를 바라본다. 짙어가는 어스름 속에서 로자는 반쯤은 겁먹고 반쯤은 수줍어, 바닥을 내려다본다.\n\n\"마님은 그 자리에서, 그 순간에 숨을 거두셨다. 그리고 그날 이후로,\" 라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"그 이름이 전해 내려온 게야. 유령의 길이라고. 그 발소리가 메아리라면, 그것은 어두워진 뒤에야 들리는 메아리이고, 오랫동안 한 번도 안 들릴 때도 많아. 하지만 이따금 다시 돌아오지. 그리고 집안에 병이나 죽음이 있을 때면, 어김없이 그 소리가 들린단다.\"\n\n\"치욕이 있을 때도요, 할머니.\" 와트가 말한다.\n\n\"치욕이란 체스니 월드에 닥치는 법이 없다.\" 가정부가 받는다.\n\n손자가 \"맞아요, 맞는 말씀이에요.\" 하며 사과한다.\n\n\"그게 그 이야기란다. 그 소리가 무엇이든, 사람 마음을 졸이게 하는 소리지.\" 라운스웰 부인이 의자에서 일어서며 말한다. \"그런데 그 소리에서 눈여겨볼 점은, 그것이 반드시 들리고야 만다는 거야. 아무것도 두려워하지 않으시는 마님조차, 그 소리가 났다 하면 반드시 들리고 만다고 인정하시지. 막아낼 도리가 없어. 와트야, 네 뒤에 키 큰 프랑스 시계가 있지(일부러 거기 놓아둔 게다), 움직일 때면 소리가 크게 울리고 음악도 연주하는 시계 말이다. 그런 물건이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알겠니?\"\n\n\"제법 알아요, 할머니, 그런 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"그걸 한번 돌려보렴.\"\n\n와트가 시계를 돌린다. 음악이며 무엇이며 다 함께.\n\n\"자, 이리 오너라.\" 가정부가 말한다. \"이리, 얘야, 마님 베개 쪽으로. 아직 충분히 어두워졌는지는 모르겠다만, 들어보렴! 이 음악이며, 박자며, 그 모든 것 너머로, 테라스에서 나는 소리가 들리니?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 들려요!\"\n\n\"마님도 그러시지.\"\n\n날이 밝기도 전에 옷을 차려입고 창밖을 내다보는 일은 흥미로웠습니다. 새카만 유리창에 촛불 두 개가 봉화처럼 비치고, 그 너머는 온통 간밤의 어슴푸레함에 싸여 있어 도무지 분간이 가지 않았지요. 그렇게 날이 밝아 오면서 풍경이 어떻게 드러나는지 지켜보는 일 말입니다. 어둠 속을 헤매던 바람이 — 마치 내 기억이 내 삶 위를 헤매듯 — 휩쓸고 지나간 그 풍경이 차츰차츰 모습을 드러내자, 나는 잠든 동안 내 주위를 둘러싸고 있던 낯선 사물들을 하나하나 발견하는 즐거움을 누렸습니다. 처음에는 안개 속에 희미하게 어른거릴 뿐이었고, 그 위로는 새벽에 뒤처진 별들이 아직 가물가물 빛났습니다. 그 창백한 막간이 지나자 그림은 빠르게 커지고 채워져, 새로 한 번 내다볼 때마다 한 시간은 족히 바라볼 거리가 생길 정도였습니다. 어느새 내 촛불만이 아침과 어울리지 않는 유일한 부분이 되었고, 방 안의 어두운 구석들은 모두 녹아 없어졌으며, 햇살이 명랑한 풍경 위로 환하게 빛났습니다. 그 풍경에서 유독 도드라진 것은 묵직한 탑을 인 옛 수도원 교회였는데, 그 거칠고 투박한 성격과는 도무지 어울리지 않게 한결 부드러운 그림자 자락을 풍경 위에 드리우고 있었습니다. 하지만 그렇게 거친 겉모습에서도 평온하고 온화한 기운이 흘러나오는 일이 흔하다는 것을 (부디 내가 깨우쳤기를 바랍니다) 그때 배웠습니다.\n\n집안 구석구석이 어찌나 잘 정돈되어 있고 모두가 어찌나 나를 살뜰히 챙겨 주는지, 열쇠 꾸러미 두 묶음을 들고도 아무런 어려움이 없었습니다. 그래도 작은 곳간의 서랍이며 찬장마다 무엇이 들었는지 기억해 내려 애쓰고, 잼이며 절임이며 설탕절임이며 병이며 유리잔이며 도자기며 그 밖에 수많은 것을 석판에 적어 두느라, 게다가 워낙 매사 꼼꼼하고 노처녀 같은 어수룩한 자그마한 사람이다 보니, 어찌나 바빴던지 종이 울리는 소리를 듣고도 벌써 아침 식사 시간이라는 게 믿기지 않을 정도였습니다. 그래도 나는 부리나케 달려가 차를 우렸지요. 이미 찻주전자를 맡는 책임이 내게 주어졌으니까요. 그러고 나니 다들 좀 늦은 데다 아직 아무도 내려오지 않았기에, 나는 정원도 한번 들여다보고 그쪽도 좀 알아 두는 게 좋겠다 싶었습니다. 정원은 정말 기분 좋은 곳이었습니다. 앞쪽으로는 우리가 들어올 때 지나온 예쁜 가로수 길과 마찻길이 있었는데 (그러고 보니 우리가 마차 바퀴로 자갈을 어찌나 험하게 파헤쳐 놓았던지, 나는 정원사에게 롤러로 좀 다져 달라고 부탁했습니다), 뒤쪽으로는 화원이 펼쳐져 있었고, 저 위 창가에는 내 사랑하는 사람이 창을 활짝 열어젖히고 나를 향해 미소 지으며, 그 먼 거리에서도 내게 입맞춤이라도 보내려는 듯했습니다. 화원 너머로는 채마밭이 있었고, 그다음엔 작은 방목장, 그다음엔 아늑한 낟가리 마당, 그다음엔 사랑스러운 작은 농장 마당이 있었습니다. 집 자체로 말하자면, 지붕에 솟은 세 개의 뾰족한 봉우리며, 어떤 건 그리도 크고 어떤 건 그리도 작은, 하지만 하나같이 어찌나 예쁜지 모를 갖가지 모양의 창문들이며, 남쪽 정면에 장미와 인동덩굴을 올리려고 친 격자 시렁이며, 그 정겹고 아늑하고 반겨 주는 듯한 모습이며 — 에이다가 집주인의 팔짱을 끼고 나를 맞으러 나오면서 한 말마따나, 그 사촌 오라버니 존에게 어울리는 집이었습니다. 대담한 말이긴 했지만, 그는 그 말에 그저 그녀의 어여쁜 뺨을 살짝 꼬집어 줄 뿐이었지요.\n\n스킴폴 씨는 아침 식사 자리에서도 간밤만큼이나 유쾌했습니다. 식탁에 꿀이 놓여 있었는데, 그것이 그를 꿀벌에 관한 이야기로 이끌었지요. 그는 꿀에는 아무 이의가 없다고 했습니다(아마 그랬을 겁니다, 꿀을 좋아하는 듯했으니까요). 다만 꿀벌의 그 잘난 척하는 우쭐댐에는 항의한다는 거였습니다. 그는 어째서 부지런한 꿀벌이 자기더러 본받으라는 모범으로 제시되어야 하는지 도무지 이해할 수 없다고 했습니다. 꿀벌은 꿀 만들기를 좋아하니까 만드는 것일 테고, 안 그러면 만들지 않을 텐데, 아무도 시키지도 않았다는 거지요. 꿀벌이 제 취향을 가지고 그렇게까지 공치사할 필요는 없다는 겁니다. 만약 제과업자가 죄다 세상을 윙윙거리며 돌아다니면서 앞길에 걸리는 것마다 쾅쾅 부딪고, 잘난 척하며 모두에게 \"내가 지금 일하러 가니 방해 말라\"고 떠들어 댄다면, 세상은 도무지 견딜 수 없는 곳이 되고 말 거라는 거였습니다. 게다가 결국, 애써 재산을 모으자마자 유황 연기에 그을려 쫓겨나는 신세라니, 이런 우스꽝스러운 처지가 어디 있느냐는 겁니다. 맨체스터의 어떤 사람이 그런 목적밖에 없이 면사를 잣는다면 다들 그를 아주 한심하게 여길 거라고요. 그는 수벌이야말로 훨씬 유쾌하고 현명한 발상을 구현한 존재라고 말하지 않을 수 없다고 했습니다. 수벌은 짐짓 꾸밈없이 이렇게 말한다는 거지요. \"실례지만, 나는 정말 가게 일에 신경 쓸 수가 없네! 볼 것은 이리도 많은데 볼 시간은 이리도 짧은 세상에 와 있고 보니, 나는 마음껏 주위를 둘러보는 자유를 누리고, 둘러볼 마음이 없는 누군가가 나를 좀 먹여 살려 주십사 부탁드릴 수밖에 없네.\" 스킴폴 씨에게는 이것이 수벌의 철학으로 보였고, 그는 이를 아주 훌륭한 철학이라 여겼습니다. 물론 수벌이 꿀벌과 사이좋게 지낼 마음만 있다는 전제하에서요. 그런데 그가 아는 한, 그 느긋한 친구는 늘 그럴 마음이 있다는 겁니다. 저 거들먹거리는 녀석이 가만 놔두기만 한다면, 제 꿀을 가지고 그렇게 우쭐대지만 않는다면 말이지요!\n\n그는 이 공상을 더없이 가벼운 발걸음으로 온갖 화제를 넘나들며 펼쳐 우리 모두를 즐겁게 했습니다. 다만 이번에도 그가 하는 말에는 그가 품을 수 있는 한껏 진지한 뜻이 담겨 있는 듯했지요. 나는 새 임무를 보러 자리를 뜰 때까지도 다들 그의 이야기에 귀 기울이고 있는 모습을 두고 나왔습니다. 한동안 일에 매달렸다가, 열쇠 바구니를 팔에 걸고 복도를 지나 돌아오는데, 잔다이스 씨가 자기 침실 옆 작은 방으로 나를 불렀습니다. 가 보니 한쪽은 책과 서류가 쌓인 작은 서재였고, 다른 한쪽은 그의 장화며 구두며 모자 상자가 들어찬 그야말로 작은 박물관이었습니다.\n\n\"앉으렴, 얘야.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"여기는 말이지, 투덜방이란다. 기분이 언짢을 때면 나는 여기 와서 투덜거리지.\"\n\n\"여기 오실 일이 아주 드물겠네요, 어르신.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"오, 넌 나를 모르는구나!\" 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"내가 무언가에 — 바람에 속거나 실망할 때, 그리고 바람이 동쪽에서 불어올 때, 나는 여기로 피신한단다. 투덜방은 이 집에서 가장 자주 쓰는 방이야. 너는 아직 내 변덕의 절반도 모르지. 얘야, 너 어쩜 그렇게 떨고 있니!\"\n\n어쩔 수가 없었습니다. 무던히 애를 썼지만, 그 자비로운 분과 단둘이 있으면서, 그 다정한 눈길과 마주치고, 그곳에 있다는 것이 그토록 행복하고 영광스러워, 가슴이 벅차오르다 못해 — 나는 그의 손에 입을 맞추고 말았습니다. 내가 무슨 말을 했는지, 아니 말을 하기는 했는지조차 모르겠습니다. 그는 당황하여 창가로 걸어갔습니다. 거의 창밖으로 뛰어내릴 작정인가 싶을 정도였는데, 그가 돌아서자 그 눈 속에서 숨기려고 그리로 갔던 것을 보고서야 나는 안심했습니다. 그는 내 머리를 가만히 토닥여 주었고, 나는 자리에 앉았습니다.\n\n\"자! 자!\" 그가 말했습니다. \"다 끝났다. 흥! 어리석게 굴지 말려무나.\"\n\n\"다시는 이러지 않겠습니다, 어르신.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 처음이라 어렵네요—\"\n\n\"별소릴!\" 그가 말했습니다. \"쉬운 일이야, 쉬워. 안 될 게 뭐냐? 보호자도 없는 착한 고아 소녀가 있다는 소식을 듣고, 내가 그 보호자가 되기로 마음먹은 것뿐이다. 그 아이가 자라나 내 좋은 평가를 넘치도록 입증해 주었고, 나는 그 아이의 후견인이자 벗으로 남았지. 이 모든 일에 무슨 거창한 게 있단 말이냐? 자, 자! 이제 묵은 셈은 다 정리되었고, 내 앞에는 다시 너의 그 다정하고, 믿음직하고, 미더운 얼굴이 있구나.\"\n\n나는 속으로 말했습니다. \"에스더, 얘야, 너 정말 놀랍구나! 이건 정말 너답지 않잖니!\" 그리고 그것이 어찌나 효과가 좋았던지, 나는 바구니 위에 두 손을 모으고 완전히 마음을 추슬렀습니다. 잔다이스 씨는 얼굴 가득 흡족한 빛을 띠며, 마치 내가 언제부턴지도 모를 만큼 오랫동안 매일 아침 그와 이야기를 나눠 온 사이라도 되는 양 허물없이 말을 건네기 시작했습니다. 나도 거의 그래 온 듯한 기분이 들었지요.\n\n\"물론, 에스더,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"너는 이 대법원 일을 이해하지 못하겠지?\"\n\n물론 나는 고개를 저었습니다.\n\n\"누가 이해하는지 나도 모르겠다.\" 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"변호사들이 사건을 어찌나 비비 꼬아 마귀 들린 꼴로 만들어 놓았는지, 사건의 본래 시비는 진작에 이 땅 위에서 사라져 버렸지. 본디 어떤 유언과 그 유언에 딸린 신탁에 관한 거였단다 — 아니, 한때는 그랬지. 지금은 오로지 소송 비용에 관한 것뿐이야. 우리는 끊임없이 출두하고, 퇴장하고, 선서하고, 심문하고, 서류를 제출하고, 또 맞제출하고, 변론하고, 봉인하고, 신청하고, 위탁하고, 보고하고, 대법관과 그의 위성들 주위를 빙글빙글 돌면서, 소송 비용을 두고 형평법답게 왈츠를 추며 먼지투성이 죽음 속으로 흘러간단다. 그게 가장 큰 문제지. 나머지는 죄다, 어떤 기막힌 수작으로, 녹아 사라져 버렸어.\"\n\n\"하지만 본디는, 어르신,\" 내가 그를 본론으로 되돌리려고 말했습니다. 그가 머리를 긁기 시작했거든요. \"어떤 유언에 관한 거였다고요?\"\n\n\"음, 그래, 무언가에 관한 것이었을 때는 유언에 관한 거였지.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"어떤 잔다이스라는 자가, 불운한 시각에, 막대한 재산을 모으고 막대한 유언장을 남겼단다. 그 유언에 딸린 신탁을 어떻게 운영하느냐는 문제로 다투는 사이에, 유언으로 남긴 그 재산은 흥청망청 탕진되고 있지. 유언으로 유산을 받게 된 사람들은 어찌나 비참한 처지로 떨어졌는지, 돈을 물려받았다는 게 무슨 엄청난 죄라도 되어 그 벌을 받는다 친대도 충분할 지경이고, 유언장 자체는 사문서가 되어 버렸어. 이 통탄할 소송 내내, 그 안에 있는 사람이라면 한 사람만 빼고 누구나 이미 다 아는 일을, 하필 모르는 그 한 사람한테 알아내라고 떠넘기지. 이 통탄할 소송 내내, 모두가 그 사건을 둘러싸고 산더미처럼 쌓인 — 수레로 실어 나를 만한 — 서류 사본을 거듭거듭 받아 가야만 한단다(아니면 받지도 못한 채 값을 치러야 하는데, 이게 보통이지. 아무도 그걸 원하지 않으니까). 그러고는 마녀의 안식일이라는 가장 광란에 찬 환상 속에서도 꿈꾼 적 없을 만큼 지독한, 소송 비용과 수수료와 헛소리와 부패로 짜인 지옥 같은 춤판을 가운데로 내려갔다가 다시 올라와야만 해. 형평법원은 보통법원에 질문을 보내고, 보통법원은 형평법원에 질문을 되돌려 보내지. 보통법원은 이걸 할 수 없다 하고, 형평법원은 저걸 할 수 없다 하고, 어느 쪽도 변호사 갑이 갑을 대리해 사무 처리를 맡기고 갑의 변호인이 출두하고, 또 다른 변호사 을이 을을 대리해 사무 처리를 맡기고 을의 변호인이 출두하지 않고서는 아무것도 할 수 없다는 말조차 못 하지. 그렇게 알파벳 전부를 거쳐 가는 게, 꼭 '사과 파이의 역사'라는 노래 같단다. 그리하여 여러 해를 거듭하고, 여러 생애를 거듭하면서, 모든 것이 끊임없이 처음부터 다시 시작될 뿐, 끝나는 것은 하나도 없어. 그리고 우리는 어떤 조건으로도 그 소송에서 빠져나올 수가 없단다. 우리는 당사자로 엮였고, 좋든 싫든 당사자가 될 수밖에 없으니까. 하지만 그걸 자꾸 생각해선 안 돼! 내 종조부, 가엾은 톰 잔다이스가 그걸 곱씹기 시작했을 때, 그게 바로 종말의 시작이었거든!\"\n\n\"제가 그 이야기를 들었던 그 잔다이스 씨 말씀인가요, 어르신?\"\n\n그는 무겁게 고개를 끄덕였습니다. \"내가 그분의 상속인이었고, 이 집이 바로 그분의 집이었단다, 에스더. 내가 여기 왔을 때, 정말이지 황량하기 그지없었지. 그분은 자신의 비참함의 흔적을 이 집에 고스란히 남겨 놓으셨거든.\"\n\n\"지금은 얼마나 달라졌을까요!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그분 시절 전에는 이 집을 '봉우리집'이라 불렀단다. 그분이 지금의 이름을 붙이고, 여기 틀어박혀, 밤낮없이 소송에 얽힌 그 사악한 서류 더미를 들여다보며, 그 미궁에서 사건을 풀어내 끝장을 보겠노라고 헛된 희망에 매달려 사셨지. 그러는 동안 집은 황폐해져, 갈라진 벽 틈으로 바람이 휘파람을 불었고, 부서진 지붕으로 비가 새어 들었고, 잡초가 썩어 가는 문으로 통하는 길을 뒤덮었어. 내가 그분의 남은 유해를 이 집으로 모셔 왔을 때, 이 집에서도 머릿골이 송두리째 날아가 버린 것만 같았단다. 그만큼 부서지고 무너져 있었지.\"\n\n이렇게 혼잣말처럼 내뱉고 몸서리를 친 그는, 잠시 이리저리 거닐다가 나를 바라보더니, 얼굴이 환해지면서 다시 와 앉아 두 손을 호주머니에 넣었습니다.\n\n\"여기가 투덜방이라고 했지, 얘야. 어디까지 얘기했더라?\"\n\n나는 그가 황폐한 집에 가져온 그 희망찬 변화 대목을 일깨워 주었습니다.\n\n\"황폐한 집, 그래. 저기 런던이라는 도시에 우리 소유의 재산이 하나 있는데, 그게 오늘날 딱 그 시절의 황폐한 집 같은 꼴이란다. 우리 소유라고 했지만 소송의 소유라는 뜻이고, 사실은 소송 비용의 소유라고 불러야 옳을 거야. 이제 그 재산에서 무엇이든 뽑아낼 힘은, 또 그것을 흉물이자 골칫거리 말고 무엇으로든 알아줄 힘은, 이 세상에 소송 비용밖에 없으니까. 그건 죽어 가는 눈먼 집들이 늘어선 거리란다. 눈은 돌멩이에 짓이겨졌고, 유리 한 장 남지 않았고, 창틀조차 없으며, 휑한 덧문은 경첩에서 떨어져 나가 산산이 부서지고, 쇠 난간은 녹이 슬어 비늘처럼 벗겨지고, 굴뚝은 주저앉고, 문마다 놓인 돌계단은(그 문 하나하나가 죽음의 문이라 해도 좋을 만큼) 시퍼런 이끼로 썩어 들고, 폐허를 떠받친 그 버팀목들마저 삭아 가고 있지. 황폐한 집은 대법원에 걸리지 않았지만, 그 주인은 걸려 있었고, 집에도 똑같은 봉인이 찍혔던 거야. 이게 바로 국새의 자국이란다, 얘야, 잉글랜드 방방곡곡에 찍혀 있지 — 아이들도 다 알아본다니까!\"\n\n\"정말 많이 달라졌네요!\" 나는 또 말했습니다.\n\n\"음, 그렇지.\" 그가 한결 명랑하게 대답했습니다. \"그리고 나를 그림의 밝은 쪽으로만 붙들어 두다니, 너 참 현명하구나.\" (내가 현명하다니요!) \"이런 것들은 내가 좀처럼 입에 올리지도, 떠올리지도 않는단다. 여기 투덜방에서가 아니면 말이지. 이걸 릭과 에이다에게 말하는 게 옳다고 여긴다면,\" 그가 나를 진지하게 바라보며 말했습니다. \"말해도 좋아. 네 판단에 맡기마, 에스더.\"\n\n\"바라옵건대, 어르신—\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"너는 나를 후견인이라 부르는 게 좋겠다, 얘야.\"\n\n또다시 목이 메어 오는 것이 느껴졌습니다 — 나는 스스로를 다그쳤지요. \"에스더, 자, 너도 알잖니!\" 그가 마치 사려 깊은 다정함이 아니라 한낱 변덕인 양 가볍게 던지는 말투로 그렇게 말했을 때 말입니다. 하지만 나는 스스로 다잡으려고 살림 열쇠를 세상에서 가장 작게 한 번 흔들고는, 두 손을 바구니 위에 더더욱 단단히 모은 채 그를 차분히 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"바라옵건대, 후견인님,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"제 판단을 너무 믿지 말아 주세요. 저를 잘못 보지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 제가 영리하지 못하다는 걸 알면 실망하실까 봐 두렵지만, 그것이 정말 사실이며, 제가 정직하게 고백하지 않더라도 머지않아 알아차리실 거예요.\"\n\n그는 조금도 실망한 기색이 아니었습니다. 오히려 그 반대였지요. 그는 얼굴 가득 미소를 지으며, 자기는 나를 아주 잘 알고 있으며, 나는 자기에게는 충분히 영리한 사람이라고 했습니다.\n\n\"그렇게 드러나면 좋겠습니다만,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"정말 그럴까 두렵네요, 후견인님.\"\n\n\"너는 여기 우리 삶의 착하고 자그마한 안주인이 될 만큼 충분히 영리하단다, 얘야.\" 그가 장난스레 대꾸했습니다. \"동요에 나오는 그 자그마한 할머니 말이다 (스킴폴 동요가 아니란다):\n\n\"'자그마한 할머니, 그리 높이 어디로 가시오?' '하늘의 거미줄을 쓸어 내러 간다오.'\"\n\n\"네가 살림을 살아 나가는 동안 우리 하늘의 거미줄을 어찌나 말끔히 쓸어 낼지, 머지않아 우리는 투덜방을 버리고 그 문을 못질해 닫아야 할 거야, 에스더.\"\n\n이것이 내가 노파니, 자그마한 노파니, 거미줄이니, 십턴 부인이니, 허버드 아주머니니, 더든 부인이니 하는 갖가지 이름으로 불리게 된 시작이었고, 그런 이름이 어찌나 많았던지 내 본래 이름이 곧 그 사이에 파묻혀 버릴 지경이었습니다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"우리 잡담으로 돌아가 보자. 여기 릭이라는 녀석이 있지, 앞날이 창창한 멋진 젊은이 말이다. 저 아이를 어떻게 해야 할까?\"\n\n아이고 맙소사, 그런 문제에 내 조언을 구하시다니요!\n\n\"릭으로 말하자면, 에스더,\" 잔다이스 씨가 편안히 두 손을 호주머니에 찌르고 다리를 쭉 뻗으며 말했습니다. \"직업을 가져야 해. 스스로 어떤 선택을 해야 하지. 그 일에도 가발법석이 한바탕 더 벌어지겠지만, 아무튼 해야 할 일이야.\"\n\n\"무슨 법석이라고요, 후견인님?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"가발법석 말이다.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그 물건을 부를 이름은 그것밖에 모르겠구나. 그 아이는 대법원의 피후견인이란다, 얘야. 켄지와 카보이가 한마디 거들 테고, 누구누구 보좌관 나리 — 챈서리 레인 끝자락 퀄리티 코트 뒷방에서 사건의 시비를 위한 무덤이나 파는, 우스꽝스러운 묘지기 같은 작자 — 가 한마디 거들 테고, 변호인이 한마디 거들 테고, 대법관이 한마디 거들 테고, 그 위성들이 한마디 거들 테지. 그 일로 죄다 두루두루 두둑이 수임료를 챙길 테고, 모든 절차가 엄청나게 격식을 차리고, 말만 많고, 만족스럽지 못하고, 돈만 잡아먹을 테니, 나는 그걸 통틀어 가발법석이라 부른단다. 인류가 어쩌다 가발법석이라는 재앙에 걸려들었는지, 또 이 젊은이들이 누구의 죄 때문에 그 구덩이에 빠지게 되었는지, 나는 도무지 모르겠다. 어쨌든 그런 거야.\"\n\n그는 다시 머리를 긁기 시작하더니 바람이 느껴진다고 넌지시 비쳤습니다. 그래도 나를 향한 그의 다정함을 보여 주는 흐뭇한 본보기였던 것은, 그가 머리를 긁든, 이리저리 거닐든, 둘 다 하든 간에, 내 얼굴을 바라볼 때면 그 얼굴이 어김없이 자애로운 표정을 되찾았고, 어김없이 다시 편안해져서 두 손을 호주머니에 넣고 다리를 쭉 뻗는다는 점이었습니다.\n\n\"무엇보다 먼저,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"리처드 씨한테 스스로 어느 쪽에 마음이 기우는지 물어보는 게 가장 좋겠네요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"내 말이 그 말이지! 알겠니, 너의 그 눈치와 그 차분한 방식으로 그 아이며 에이다와 함께 이 일을 의논하는 데 익숙해지렴. 그러고 너희끼리 어떤 결론을 내는지 보자꾸나. 네 손을 거치면 우리는 틀림없이 이 일의 핵심에 다다를 거야, 자그마한 아가씨.\"\n\n내가 떠맡게 된 비중과 내게 맡겨지는 일들의 수를 생각하니 정말이지 겁이 더럭 났습니다. 나는 결코 이런 걸 뜻한 게 아니었거든요. 그가 리처드에게 직접 말해 주기를 바랐던 거였지요. 하지만 물론 나는 최선을 다하겠노라는 말 외엔 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았습니다. 다만 그가 나를 실제보다 훨씬 더 슬기로운 사람으로 여기는 게 아닐까 두렵다고 (정말이지 이 말은 거듭해야 할 것 같았습니다) 덧붙였지요. 그 말에 후견인은 그저 내가 들어 본 중에 가장 유쾌한 웃음을 터뜨릴 뿐이었습니다.\n\n\"자!\" 그가 일어서며 의자를 뒤로 밀고 말했습니다. \"오늘은 투덜방을 이만 끝내도 될 것 같구나! 마지막으로 한마디만. 에스더, 얘야, 나한테 뭐 물어보고 싶은 게 있니?\"\n\n그가 어찌나 골똘히 나를 바라보던지, 나도 그를 골똘히 바라보았고, 그분의 뜻을 알아차렸다는 확신이 들었습니다.\n\n\"제 자신에 관해서요, 어르신?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"후견인님,\" 나는 어느새 바라던 것보다 차가워진 손을 그의 손에 살며시 얹어 보며 말했습니다. \"아무것도요! 만약 제가 마땅히 알아야 할 게 있거나 알 필요가 있는 게 있다면, 제가 알려 달라고 청할 필요도 없으리라는 걸 저는 확신해요. 제 모든 의지와 믿음을 어르신께 두지 않는다면, 저는 정말이지 모진 마음을 지닌 사람일 거예요. 저는 여쭤 볼 게 없어요, 세상에 아무것도 없답니다.\"\n\n그는 내 손을 끌어다 자기 팔짱을 끼게 하고는, 우리는 함께 에이다를 찾으러 갔습니다. 그 순간부터 나는 그와 함께 있는 것이 더없이 편안했고, 더없이 거리낌 없었고, 더 알 것이 없다는 데 더없이 만족했고, 더없이 행복했습니다.\n\n처음에 우리는 황폐한 집에서 제법 분주하게 지냈습니다. 잔다이스 씨를 아는, 동네 안팎의 수많은 주민들과 안면을 터야 했거든요. 에이다와 내가 보기에는, 남의 돈으로 무슨 일이든 해 보려는 사람이라면 누구나 그를 아는 듯했습니다. 아침에 투덜방에서 그의 편지를 분류하고 그 가운데 몇 통에 대신 답장을 쓰기 시작하면서, 그와 편지를 주고받는 거의 모든 이들의 삶에서 가장 큰 목적이라는 게, 돈을 거둬들이고 또 뿌리기 위한 위원회를 꾸리는 일인 듯하다는 것을 알게 되어 우리는 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 부인들도 신사들 못지않게 필사적이었지요. 아니, 오히려 더 필사적이었던 것 같습니다. 그분들은 더없이 격정적인 태도로 위원회에 뛰어들었고, 그야말로 유별난 열의로 기부금을 거뒀습니다. 우리가 보기에 그들 가운데 어떤 이들은 우체국 주소록에 실린 모든 사람에게 기부 카드를 나눠 주느라 평생을 다 보내는 게 틀림없는 듯했습니다 — 1실링짜리 카드, 반 크라운짜리 카드, 반 소브린짜리 카드, 1페니짜리 카드 말이지요. 그들은 모든 걸 원했습니다. 입을 옷가지를 원했고, 아마포 헝겊을 원했고, 돈을 원했고, 석탄을 원했고, 수프를 원했고, 이자를 원했고, 친필 서명을 원했고, 플란넬을 원했고, 잔다이스 씨가 가진 것은 — 또는 가지지 않은 것은 — 무엇이든 원했습니다. 그들의 목적도 그 요구만큼이나 가지가지였습니다. 새 건물을 세우려 했고, 옛 건물의 빚을 갚으려 했고, 그림처럼 아름다운 건물(서쪽 정면 예정도 동판화 첨부)에 '중세 마리아회'를 세우려 했고, 젤리비 부인에게 공로패를 바치려 했고, 자기네 간사의 초상화를 그려 그를 향한 깊은 헌신으로 잘 알려진 그의 장모에게 선사하려 했고, 정말이지 제 생각엔 오십만 부의 소책자에서부터 연금에 이르기까지, 또 대리석 기념비에서부터 은제 찻주전자에 이르기까지 온갖 것을 마련하려 했습니다. 그들은 수많은 명칭을 내걸었습니다. 잉글랜드의 여인회, 브리튼의 딸들회, 일곱 가지 추요 덕목을 저마다 따로 내건 자매회, 아메리카의 여성회, 백 가지 교파의 부인회였지요. 그들은 늘 유세를 벌이고 선출하는 일로 흥분에 차 있는 듯했습니다. 우리의 변변찮은 머리로 보기엔, 또 그들 스스로 떠벌리는 바로도, 그들은 끊임없이 수만 명을 상대로 투표를 벌이면서도 정작 자기네 후보를 무엇 하나로도 당선시키는 일은 없는 듯했습니다. 통틀어 그들이 얼마나 열에 들뜬 삶을 살아가야 하는지를 생각하면 우리는 머리가 다 지끈거렸습니다.\n\n이 탐욕스러운 자선(이런 표현을 써도 된다면)으로 가장 두드러진 부인들 가운데 파디글 부인이라는 분이 있었습니다. 잔다이스 씨에게 보낸 편지의 수로 미루어 짐작건대, 그분은 젤리비 부인 못지않게 강력한 편지 상대인 듯했지요. 우리는 파디글 부인이 대화의 화제로 오를 때면 어김없이 바람이 바뀐다는 것을, 그리고 자선하는 사람에는 두 부류가 있는데 — 하나는 하는 일은 보잘것없으면서 요란하게 떠드는 사람들이고, 다른 하나는 하는 일은 막대하면서 전혀 소리를 내지 않는 사람들이라는 — 그 말을 잔다이스 씨가 꺼내려 할 때면 그 바람이 어김없이 그의 말을 끊어 더 나아가지 못하게 한다는 것을 알아차렸습니다. 그래서 우리는 파디글 부인을 한번 보고 싶어졌고, 그분이 전자에 속하는 본보기이리라 짐작했는데, 어느 날 그분이 다섯 어린 아들들을 데리고 찾아왔을 때 반가웠습니다.\n\n그분은 안경을 끼고, 코가 우뚝하고, 목소리가 우렁찬, 어딘가 위압적인 유형의 부인이었는데, 자리를 한껏 많이 차지하고 싶어 하는 듯한 인상을 주었습니다. 실제로도 그랬지요. 한참 멀찍이 떨어져 있는 작은 의자들까지 치맛자락으로 넘어뜨렸으니까요. 마침 에이다와 나만 집에 있던 터라, 우리는 머뭇거리며 그분을 맞았습니다. 그분이 찬 날씨처럼 들어오는 듯했고, 뒤따르는 어린 파디글들을 추위에 새파랗게 질리게 만드는 듯했거든요.\n\n\"이 아이들은요, 아가씨들,\" 첫인사를 마친 뒤 파디글 부인이 청산유수로 말했습니다. \"제 다섯 아들입니다. 우리의 존경하는 벗 잔다이스 씨가 가지고 계신 인쇄된 기부자 명단(아마 한 장이 아니라 여러 장일 거예요)에서 이 아이들 이름을 보셨을지도 모르겠네요. 맏이 에그버트(열두 살)는 토커후포 인디언들에게 자기 용돈 5실링 3펜스를 죄다 부친 아이고요. 둘째 오즈월드(열 살 반)는 위대한 국민 스미더스 기념기금에 2실링 9펜스를 보탠 아이예요. 셋째 프랜시스(아홉 살)는 1실링 6펜스 반페니를, 넷째 펠릭스(일곱 살)는 노령 과부회에 8펜스를 냈고요. 막내 앨프리드(다섯 살)는 제 발로 '유아 환희 서약단'에 가입해서, 평생토록 어떤 형태로든 담배를 입에 대지 않기로 서약했답니다.\"\n\n우리는 그렇게 불만스러운 아이들을 본 적이 없었습니다. 그저 비쩍 마르고 쪼그라들었다는 것만이 아니라 — 물론 그렇기도 했지만 — 아이들은 불만에 차서 그야말로 사납기 짝이 없어 보였습니다. 토커후포 인디언 이야기가 나왔을 때, 에그버트가 어찌나 무지막지하게 인상을 찌푸리던지, 나는 정말로 그 아이가 그 부족 가운데 가장 흉포한 일원이라 여길 뻔했습니다. 아이들은 저마다 자기 기부 금액이 언급될 때마다 유독 앙심에 찬 표정으로 얼굴이 어두워졌는데, 그중에서도 에그버트가 단연 제일 심했습니다. 다만 '유아 환희 서약단'에 막 입단한 막내만은 예외로 둬야겠는데, 그 아이는 둔하게, 그러나 한결같이 비참한 표정이었습니다.\n\n\"두 분이서 젤리비 부인 댁에 다녀오셨다고요?\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다.\n\n우리는 그렇다고, 거기서 하룻밤을 묵었다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"젤리비 부인은요,\" 그 부인이 줄곧 똑같이 과시적이고 우렁차고 딱딱한 어조로 말을 이었는데, 어찌나 그러던지 그 목소리에마저 일종의 안경이 끼워져 있는 듯한 인상을 받았습니다. 그러고 보니 이 자리를 빌려 한마디 하자면, 그분의 안경이 한결 덜 매력적으로 보였던 까닭은 그 눈이 에이다가 '숨 막히는 눈'이라 부른, 그러니까 몹시 튀어나온 눈이었기 때문입니다. \"젤리비 부인은 사회의 은인이고 도와드릴 만한 분이지요. 우리 아이들도 그 아프리카 사업에 기부를 했어요 — 에그버트가 1실링 6펜스, 이건 아홉 주치 용돈을 통째로 낸 거고요. 오즈월드가 1실링 1펜스 반페니, 역시 똑같고요. 나머지 아이들도 제 형편껏 냈답니다. 그렇긴 해도, 저는 젤리비 부인과 모든 점에서 뜻이 같진 않아요. 저는 젤리비 부인이 어린 자식들을 대하는 방식에는 동의하지 않습니다. 사람들이 지적했지요. 그 부인의 어린 자식들이 그분이 헌신하는 사업에 참여하지 못한다는 게 사람들 눈에 띄었던 거예요. 그분이 옳을 수도, 그를 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 옳든 그르든, 그건 제 어린 자식들에 대한 제 방식은 아니랍니다. 저는 이 아이들을 어디든 데리고 다녀요.\"\n\n그 말에 나는 (그리고 에이다도) 나중에 확신하게 되었지만, 저 성질 사나운 맏이에게서 날카로운 비명이 터져 나오려던 것을, 그 아이가 하품으로 둘러댄 거였습니다. 하지만 시작은 분명 비명이었지요.\n\n\"이 아이들은 일 년 내내, 물론 한겨울에도, 아침 여섯 시 반에 저와 함께 조과에 참석한답니다(아주 예쁘게 한답니다).\" 파디글 부인이 빠르게 말했습니다. \"그리고 하루 종일 돌아가며 이어지는 제 일과 내내 저와 함께하지요. 저는 학교 부인이고, 방문 부인이고, 낭독 부인이고, 배급 부인이에요. 지역 아마포 상자 위원회를 비롯해 여러 일반 위원회에도 들어 있고요. 제가 벌이는 유세만 해도 매우 광범위해서 — 아마 저만 한 사람이 없을 거예요. 하지만 어디든 이 아이들이 저와 동무가 되어 주고, 그렇게 함으로써 가난한 이들에 대한 그 지식을, 또 자선 사업 전반을 해 나가는 그 역량을 — 한마디로 그런 일에 대한 취향을 — 익히는 거지요. 그게 훗날 이 아이들을 이웃에게 보탬이 되고 자기 자신에게 흡족함이 되는 사람으로 만들어 줄 거예요. 제 어린 자식들은 경박하지 않아요. 받은 용돈을 제 지도에 따라 죄다 기부에 쓰지요. 그리고 이 아이들은 보통 어른도 몇 안 되는 사람만이 겪는 만큼 많은 공개 집회에 참석하고, 그만큼 많은 강연과 연설과 토론을 들어 왔답니다. 앞서 말씀드린, 제 발로 '유아 환희 서약단'에 가입한 앨프리드(다섯 살)는 그날 저녁 사회자가 두 시간에 걸쳐 열변을 토한 뒤에도 의식을 유지한 몇 안 되는 아이들 가운데 하나였어요.\"\n\n앨프리드는 그날 밤 입은 상처를 결코 용서할 수 없다는, 아니 용서하지 않겠다는 듯이 우리를 노려보았습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양도 보셨을지 모르겠네요.\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다. \"우리의 존경하는 벗 잔다이스 씨가 가지고 계신, 아까 말씀드린 그 명단들 가운데 어떤 것을 보면, 제 어린 자식들 이름 끝에 'O. A. 파디글, 왕립학회 회원, 1파운드'라고 적혀 있을 거예요. 이게 그 아이들 아버지랍니다. 우리는 으레 똑같은 순서를 따르지요. 제가 먼저 푼돈을 적어 넣고, 그다음 제 어린 자식들이 나이와 형편껏 기부를 올리고, 그러고 나면 파디글 씨가 마지막을 장식해요. 파디글 씨는 제 지도에 따라 자기 몫의 소소한 기부를 보태는 걸 기쁘게 여긴답니다. 이렇게 해서 우리 자신에게 유쾌할 뿐 아니라, 부디 남들에게도 보탬이 되리라 믿는 거지요.\"\n\n만약 파디글 씨가 젤리비 씨와 함께 만찬을 든다면, 그리고 젤리비 씨가 만찬 뒤에 파디글 씨에게 속내를 털어놓는다면, 파디글 씨도 그 답례로 젤리비 씨에게 무슨 은밀한 이야기를 건넬까? 내가 이런 생각을 하고 있다는 걸 깨닫고 나는 무척 당황했지만, 그래도 그 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐습니다.\n\n\"여기는 참 기분 좋은 곳에 자리 잡으셨네요!\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다.\n\n우리는 화제를 바꾸게 되어 반가웠고, 창가로 가서 풍경의 아름다움을 가리켜 보였는데, 그 안경은 내가 보기에 묘하게 무심한 태도로 그 위에 머무는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"거셔 씨를 아세요?\" 우리의 손님이 말했습니다.\n\n우리는 거셔 씨를 알게 되는 기쁨은 누리지 못했다고 말할 수밖에 없었습니다.\n\n\"손해는 두 분 몫이에요, 정말이지.\" 파디글 부인이 그 위압적인 거동으로 말했습니다. \"그분은 아주 열정적이고 격정적인 연설가랍니다 — 불꽃이 넘쳐요! 지금 이 잔디밭에 마차 한 대 세워 두면, 이 땅의 모양새가 자연스레 공개 집회에 딱 맞으니, 두 분이 떠올릴 수 있는 거의 어떤 자리든 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 일깨워 줄 거예요! 자, 그런데 아가씨들,\" 파디글 부인이 자기 의자로 물러가면서, 마치 보이지 않는 손에라도 이끌린 듯 제법 멀찍이 놓인, 내 반짇고리가 얹힌 작고 둥근 탁자를 넘어뜨리며 말했습니다. \"이쯤 되면 두 분도 저를 간파하셨겠지요?\"\n\n이건 정말이지 어찌나 헷갈리는 질문이던지, 에이다가 어쩔 줄 모르고 나를 쳐다보았습니다. 내가 방금 했던 생각 탓에 마음 한구석이 켕겼던 것으로 말하자면, 분명 내 뺨의 빛깔에 고스란히 드러났을 겁니다.\n\n\"간파하셨다는 건, 제 말은,\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다. \"제 성격의 두드러진 점 말이에요. 그게 워낙 두드러져서 단박에 알아차리실 수 있다는 걸 저도 압니다. 제가 간파당하기 좋게 스스로를 드러내 놓고 있다는 걸 알지요. 뭐! 솔직히 인정하건대, 저는 일하는 여자예요. 저는 고된 일을 사랑하고, 고된 일을 즐깁니다. 그 흥분이 제겐 보약이지요. 고된 일에 어찌나 길들고 단련되었는지, 저는 피로가 뭔지를 모른답니다.\"\n\n우리는 그것 참 놀랍고도 참 흐뭇한 일이라거나, 뭐 그런 비슷한 말을 웅얼거렸습니다. 그게 무슨 말인지 우리도 잘 몰랐던 것 같지만, 우리의 예의가 표현한 것은 그런 거였지요.\n\n\"저는 피곤하다는 게 뭔지 모릅니다. 아무리 애를 써도 저를 지치게 할 순 없어요!\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다. \"제가 때때로 치러 내는 그 노력의 양(저에게는 노력 축에도 못 들지만)과 그 일의 분량(저는 아무것도 아니라 여기는)을 보면 저 스스로도 깜짝 놀랄 정도랍니다. 제 어린 자식들과 파디글 씨가 그걸 지켜보는 것만으로 녹초가 되어 버린 걸 본 적도 있어요. 그때 제가 종달새처럼 팔팔했다고 한들 거짓이 아니지요!\"\n\n그 거무튀튀한 얼굴의 맏이가 이미 보여 준 것보다 더 악의에 찬 표정을 지을 수 있었다면, 바로 그 순간이 그랬습니다. 나는 그 아이가 오른쪽 주먹을 불끈 쥐고, 왼쪽 겨드랑이에 끼고 있던 제 모자 꼭대기에 은밀히 한 방 먹이는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n\"이게 순회를 돌 때 제겐 큰 강점이 되지요.\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다. \"제 이야기를 듣기 싫어하는 사람을 만나면, 저는 그 사람한테 곧장 이렇게 말해요. '여보세요, 저는 피로를 모르는 사람이라, 절대 지치지 않고, 다 마칠 때까지 계속할 작정이랍니다.' 그러면 기막히게 통한다니까요! 서머슨 양, 제 방문 순회에 어서 양의 도움을 받을 수 있길 바라고요, 클레어 양의 도움도 머지않아 받았으면 해요.\"\n\n처음에 나는 소홀히 해선 안 될 일거리가 있다는 일반적인 핑계로 지금은 사양하려고 했습니다. 하지만 그것이 먹히지 않는 항변이 되자, 그다음엔 좀 더 구체적으로, 내가 그 자질을 갖추었는지 확신이 서지 않는다고 말했습니다. 처지가 사뭇 다른 사람들의 마음에 내 마음을 맞추고, 적절한 관점에서 그들에게 말을 건네는 기술에는 내가 미숙하다고요. 그런 일에 반드시 갖추어야 할, 사람 마음에 대한 그 섬세한 통찰이 내겐 없다고요. 남을 가르치기에 앞서 나부터 배워야 할 것이 많고, 내 선한 의도만 믿고 나설 수는 없다고요. 이런 까닭에, 나는 내가 할 수 있는 한 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고, 바로 곁에 있는 사람들에게 내가 할 수 있는 친절을 베풀며, 그 의무의 테두리가 차츰 자연스레 넓어지도록 두는 게 가장 좋겠다고 여긴다고요. 이 모든 말을, 나는 자신감이라곤 도무지 없이 했습니다. 파디글 부인이 나보다 한참 연상인 데다, 경험이 많고, 거동이 어찌나 군대식이던지요.\n\n\"그건 양이 틀렸어요, 서머슨 양.\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"하지만 어쩌면 양은 고된 일이나 그 흥분을 감당할 그릇이 못 되나 보군요. 그렇다면야 사정이 영 다르지요. 제가 어떻게 일을 해내는지 보고 싶다면, 마침 지금 제가 — 어린 자식들과 함께 — 이 동네의 어떤 벽돌공(아주 나쁜 위인이지요)을 방문하러 가는 길이니, 양을 데려가면 기쁘겠어요. 클레어 양도 청을 들어주신다면요.\"\n\n에이다와 나는 눈빛을 주고받았고, 어차피 나가려던 참이었으므로 그 제안을 받아들였습니다. 우리가 보닛을 챙겨 쓰고 부랴부랴 돌아와 보니, 어린 자식들은 한구석에서 풀이 죽어 늘어져 있었고, 파디글 부인은 방 안을 휩쓸고 다니며 그 안에 있던 가벼운 물건들을 거의 죄다 넘어뜨리고 있었습니다. 파디글 부인이 에이다를 차지했고, 나는 그 아이들을 거느리고 뒤를 따랐습니다.\n\n나중에 에이다가 들려준 바로는, 파디글 부인은 벽돌공의 집에 이르는 내내 똑같이 우렁찬 어조로(사실 나도 어쩌다 엿들었지요) 어떤 흥분되는 다툼 이야기를 늘어놓았다고 합니다. 어딘가의 연금을 두고 자기네 경쟁 후보를 내세우는 문제로 다른 부인과 이삼 년 동안 벌여 온 다툼이었지요. 그동안 어마어마한 양의 인쇄물과 약속과 위임장과 투표가 오갔고, 그 일은 관련된 모든 이에게 큰 활기를 불어넣은 듯했습니다 — 정작 그 연금 수급자들만 빼고요. 그들은 아직 선출되지도 않았으니까요.\n\n나는 아이들이 내게 속내를 털어놓는 것을 무척 좋아하고, 대개 그런 호의를 누리는 것을 기쁘게 여깁니다만, 이번만은 그것이 나를 몹시 불편하게 했습니다. 우리가 집 밖으로 나오자마자 에그버트가 꼬마 노상강도 같은 태도로 나한테 1실링을 내놓으라고 졸랐는데, 자기 용돈을 \"뺏겼다\"는 게 그 이유였습니다. 내가 그 말이 몹시 부적절하다고, 더구나 제 부모와 관련해서는 더더욱(아이가 시무룩하게 \"엄마한테!\"라고 덧붙였거든요) 그렇다고 일러 주자, 그 아이는 나를 꼬집으며 말했습니다. \"오, 그럼! 어디! 누나가 뭔데! 누나라도 싫을걸, 안 그래? 엄마는 뭣 하러 속임수를 써서 돈을 주는 척하고는 다시 빼앗아 가는 거야? 왜 그걸 내 용돈이라고 부르면서 한 번도 못 쓰게 하느냐고?\" 이 약 올리는 질문들에 그 아이의 마음과 오즈월드, 프랜시스의 마음까지 어찌나 달아올랐던지, 셋이 한꺼번에 나를 꼬집었는데, 그 솜씨가 어찌나 능수능란하던지 — 내 팔의 살점을 어찌나 야무지게 비틀어 대던지, 나는 비명을 참기 힘들 지경이었습니다. 펠릭스는 그와 동시에 내 발가락을 짓밟았고요. 그리고 늘 제 쥐꼬리만 한 수입을 죄다 미리 당겨 써 버린 탓에 사실상 담배뿐 아니라 과자까지 끊기로 서약한 신세인 그 환희의 서약단원은, 우리가 어느 제과점 앞을 지날 때 슬픔과 분노로 어찌나 부풀어 오르던지 얼굴이 시뻘게져서 나를 겁에 질리게 했습니다. 나는 평생 젊은이들과 산책하면서, 이렇게 부자연스럽게 옥죄어진 아이들이 자연스러워졌답시고 내게 베푼 그 호의에서만큼 몸과 마음으로 그토록 시달려 본 적이 없습니다.\n\n벽돌공의 집에 다다랐을 때, 나는 반가웠습니다. 비록 그 집이 벽돌 밭에 다닥다닥 모여 선 비참한 오두막 무리 가운데 하나로, 깨진 창문 바로 옆에 돼지우리가 붙어 있고, 문 앞 초라한 텃밭에는 고인 물웅덩이밖에 자라는 게 없었지만요. 여기저기 낡은 통을 놓아 지붕에서 떨어지는 빗물을 받기도 했고, 흙으로 둑을 쌓아 큼지막한 진흙 파이 같은 작은 웅덩이를 만들어 두기도 했습니다. 문가와 창가에서 남자며 여자 몇이 어슬렁거리거나 서성였는데, 우리에겐 별다른 관심을 두지 않은 채 그저 자기들끼리 웃거나, 우리가 지나가는 동안 점잖은 양반들이 제 일이나 챙길 것이지 쓸데없이 머리 싸매고 신발에 진흙 묻혀 가며 남의 일이나 들여다보러 온다는 둥 무어라 떠들 뿐이었습니다.\n\n파디글 부인은 도덕적 결의를 한껏 드러내며 앞장을 섰고, 사람들의 지저분한 생활 습관에 대해 청산유수로 떠들면서(하기야 그런 곳에서야 우리 가운데 누군들 깔끔할 수 있었을까 싶었지만), 우리를 가장 외진 구석의 오두막으로 이끌었습니다. 그 일층 방을 우리는 거의 가득 채우다시피 했지요. 우리 말고도 이 눅눅하고 역겨운 방 안에는, 눈에 멍이 든 여자가 불 곁에서 숨을 헐떡이는 가엾은 갓난아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었고, 진흙과 흙투성이에 몹시 방탕해 보이는 남자가 바닥에 큰대자로 누워 파이프를 빨고 있었으며, 우람한 젊은 사내가 개에게 목줄을 채우고 있었고, 당돌해 보이는 처녀가 몹시 더러운 물로 빨래 같은 걸 하고 있었습니다. 우리가 들어서자 그들은 모두 우리를 올려다보았고, 그 여자는 멍든 눈을 가리려는 듯 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 돌리는 듯했습니다. 아무도 우리를 반겨 주지 않았습니다.\n\n\"자, 여러분.\" 파디글 부인이 말했는데, 그 목소리에는 다정한 구석이라곤 없는 듯했습니다. 내 생각엔 너무 사무적이고 체계적이었지요. \"다들 어떻게 지내세요? 제가 또 왔답니다. 저를 지치게 할 순 없다고 말씀드렸지요. 저는 고된 일을 좋아하고, 한번 한 말은 꼭 지키는 사람이거든요.\"\n\n\"더 들어올 사람은,\" 바닥에 누운 남자가 한 손으로 머리를 괴고 우리를 빤히 노려보며 으르렁거렸습니다. \"없는 거요, 응?\"\n\n\"없어요, 여보세요.\" 파디글 부인이 의자 하나에 앉으면서 다른 하나를 넘어뜨리며 말했습니다. \"우리가 다예요.\"\n\n\"하기야 당신네 머릿수가 좀 모자라지 않나 싶었거든.\" 남자가 입에 파이프를 문 채 우리를 둘러보며 말했습니다.\n\n젊은 사내와 처녀가 둘 다 웃었습니다. 우리가 문간으로 끌어들였던, 호주머니에 손을 찌른 채 거기 서 있던 그 젊은 사내의 친구 둘도 요란하게 따라 웃었습니다.\n\n\"여러분은 저를 지치게 할 수 없어요, 착한 분들.\" 파디글 부인이 그 친구들에게 말했습니다. \"저는 고된 일을 즐기고, 여러분이 제 일을 고되게 만들수록 저는 더 좋답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 부인 일이나 수월하게 해 주쇼!\" 바닥에 누운 남자가 으르렁거렸습니다. \"난 그게 끝나길, 다 끝나길 바란단 말이오. 내 집에서 이런 멋대로 굴기가 끝나길 바라요. 오소리처럼 끌려 다니는 신세가 끝나길 바란단 말이오. 자, 이제 당신은 늘 하던 대로 투표 캐고 꼬치꼬치 캐물을 작정이겠지 — 무슨 수작을 부릴지 다 안다고. 흥! 그럴 거 없어요. 내가 수고를 덜어 주지. 내 딸이 빨래 중이냐고? 그래, 빨래 중이오. 저 물 좀 보쇼. 냄새도 맡아 보고! 저게 우리가 마시는 물이오. 어떻소, 마음에 드쇼, 차라리 진을 마시는 게 어떨 것 같소! 내 집이 더럽지 않으냐고? 그래, 더럽소 — 본래부터 더럽고, 본래부터 몸에 안 좋아요. 그리고 우린 더럽고 몸에 안 좋은 애를 다섯이나 뒀는데, 죄다 갓난쟁이로 죽었지. 그게 그 애들한텐, 그리고 우리한테도 차라리 나아요. 당신이 두고 간 그 작은 책 읽었느냐고? 아니, 당신이 두고 간 작은 책 안 읽었소. 여긴 그걸 읽을 줄 아는 사람이 아무도 없고, 설령 있다 한들 나한텐 안 맞아요. 그건 갓난쟁이한테나 맞는 책인데, 난 갓난쟁이가 아니거든. 당신이 나한테 인형을 두고 간들, 난 그걸 어르지 않을 거요. 내가 어떻게 처신해 왔느냐고? 흥, 사흘 동안 곤드레만드레 취해 있었지. 돈만 있었으면 나흘은 취해 있었을 거요. 교회에 갈 생각은 도통 없느냐고? 그래, 교회에 갈 생각 따윈 도통 없소. 간들 거기서 날 반기지도 않을 거고. 교회지기가 나한텐 너무 점잖으셔서 말이오. 그래, 내 마누라가 저 멍든 눈은 어쩌다 그리됐느냐고? 흥, 내가 그랬소. 마누라가 아니라고 한다면, 그건 거짓말이지!\"\n\n이 말을 죄다 쏟아내려고 그는 입에서 파이프를 빼냈다가, 이제 반대쪽으로 돌아누우며 다시 파이프를 빨았습니다. 파디글 부인은 안경 너머로 그를 바라보며, 내 생각엔 그의 적개심을 한층 부추기기에 딱 알맞은 강압적인 침착함을 짓고 있다가, 마치 순경의 곤봉이라도 되는 양 좋은 책 한 권을 꺼내 들고는 온 식구를 구금했습니다. 물론 종교적 구금이라는 뜻이지만, 정말이지 그분의 거동은 마치 무자비한 도덕 경찰이 그들을 죄다 유치장으로 끌고 가는 것만 같았습니다.\n\n에이다와 나는 몹시 거북했습니다. 우리는 둘 다 주제넘게 끼어든 데다 자리에 어울리지 않는다고 느꼈고, 둘 다 파디글 부인이 사람을 그렇게 기계적으로 휘어잡는 방식만 아니었다면 한없이 일이 더 잘 풀렸으리라 생각했습니다. 아이들은 시무룩하게 노려보았고, 그 식구들은 우리에겐 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았습니다. 다만 그 젊은 사내가 개를 짖게 만들 때만 빼고요. 그는 파디글 부인이 가장 힘주어 말할 때면 으레 그렇게 하곤 했습니다. 우리는 둘 다 우리와 이 사람들 사이에 우리의 새 벗이 결코 치울 수 없는 쇠 장벽이 가로놓여 있음을 가슴 아프게 절감했습니다. 그것이 누구의 손으로 어떻게 치워질 수 있는지는 알 수 없었지만, 그것만은 알았지요. 그분이 읽고 말한 내용조차, 설령 아무리 겸손하게, 아무리 큰 눈치를 가지고 전했다 한들, 우리가 보기엔 그런 청중에게는 영 잘못 고른 것 같았습니다. 바닥에 누운 남자가 말한 그 작은 책으로 말하자면, 우리는 나중에 그것에 대해 알게 되었는데, 잔다이스 씨는 로빈슨 크루소가 그 외딴섬에 다른 책이라곤 그것뿐이었다 한들 과연 그 책을 읽었을지 의심스럽다고 했습니다.\n\n이런 형편에서, 파디글 부인이 입을 다물었을 때 우리는 무척 안도했습니다.\n\n그러자 바닥에 누운 남자가 다시 고개를 돌리고는 퉁명스럽게 말했습니다. \"흥! 다 끝났소, 응?\"\n\n\"오늘은 끝났어요, 여보세요. 하지만 저는 결코 지치는 법이 없답니다. 정해진 순서에 따라 다시 찾아오지요.\" 파디글 부인이 과시적인 명랑함으로 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"지금 가 주기만 한다면야,\" 그가 팔짱을 끼고 욕설과 함께 눈을 감으며 말했습니다. \"당신 맘대로 하쇼!\"\n\n그러자 파디글 부인이 일어서며 그 비좁은 방 안에 작은 소용돌이를 일으켰는데, 그 통에 파이프조차 가까스로 화를 면했습니다. 어린 자식들을 양손에 하나씩 잡고, 나머지에게는 바싹 따라오라 이르며, 다음에 찾아올 때는 벽돌공과 그 온 식구가 나아져 있기를 바란다는 말을 남기고, 그분은 또 다른 오두막으로 향했습니다. 내가 이런 말을 하는 게 박정한 짓이 아니길 바랍니다만, 그분은 이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 분명, 자선을 도매로 베풀고 그것을 대규모로 거래한다는, 결코 사람의 마음을 누그러뜨리지 못하는 그런 과시를 한 것은 사실입니다.\n\n그분은 우리가 따라오는 줄 알았지만, 공간이 비자마자 우리는 불 곁에 앉은 여자에게 다가가 아기가 아픈지 물어보았습니다.\n\n그 여자는 무릎에 누운 아기를 그저 바라보기만 했습니다. 우리는 앞서, 그 여자가 아기를 바라볼 때면 멍든 눈을 손으로 가린다는 걸 눈여겨보았지요. 마치 그 가엾은 어린것에게서, 소란과 폭력과 학대에 얽힌 일체의 기억을 떼어 놓고 싶다는 듯이 말입니다.\n\n그 모습에 다정한 마음이 움직인 에이다가 허리를 굽혀 아기의 작은 얼굴을 만지려 했습니다. 그러는 순간, 나는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보고 그녀를 끌어당겼습니다. 아기가 숨을 거둔 것이었습니다.\n\n\"오, 에스더!\" 에이다가 아기 곁에 무릎을 꿇으며 외쳤습니다. \"이것 좀 봐! 오, 에스더, 내 사랑, 이 가엾은 것! 이 고생하던, 얌전한, 어여쁜 작은 것! 너무 가여워. 저 어머니도 너무 가여워. 나는 평생 이토록 애처로운 광경을 본 적이 없어! 오, 아기야, 아기야!\"\n\n그렇게 울며 허리를 굽혀 어머니의 손 위에 제 손을 얹는 그 연민, 그 다정함은, 일찍이 뛰어 본 그 어떤 어머니의 마음이라도 누그러뜨릴 만했습니다. 그 여자는 처음엔 놀라 그녀를 바라보더니, 이내 울음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n이윽고 나는 그 여자의 무릎에서 가벼운 짐을 받아 들고, 아기의 안식을 한결 더 어여쁘고 평온하게 해 주려고 할 수 있는 일을 한 다음, 선반 위에 눕히고 내 손수건으로 덮어 주었습니다. 우리는 어머니를 위로하려 애쓰며, 우리 구주께서 어린아이들에 대해 하신 말씀을 그 여자에게 속삭였습니다. 여자는 아무 대답도 없이 그저 앉아 울기만 했습니다 — 하염없이 울었습니다.\n\n내가 돌아보니, 그 젊은 사내가 개를 데리고 나갔다가 문가에 서서 마른 눈으로, 그러나 차분히 우리를 들여다보고 있었습니다. 처녀도 차분히 한구석에 앉아 바닥을 내려다보고 있었습니다. 남자는 일어나 있었습니다. 그는 여전히 반항하는 기색으로 파이프를 빨고 있었지만, 입은 다물고 있었지요.\n\n내가 그들을 둘러보는 사이, 행색이 몹시 초라한 못생긴 여자 하나가 황급히 들어오더니, 곧장 그 어머니에게 다가가 말했습니다. \"제니! 제니!\" 그렇게 불린 어머니가 일어나 그 여자의 목에 매달려 안겼습니다.\n\n이 여자 역시 얼굴과 팔에 학대당한 자국이 있었습니다. 그녀에게는 동정심이라는 우아함 말고는 아무런 우아함도 없었습니다. 하지만 그녀가 그 어머니를 위로하며 제 눈에서도 눈물이 떨어질 때, 그녀에게는 더 이상 아름다움이 필요치 않았습니다. 위로했다고는 했지만, 그녀가 한 말이라곤 \"제니! 제니!\"뿐이었습니다. 나머지는 모두 그 말을 내뱉는 어조에 담겨 있었지요.\n\n거칠고 남루하고 매 맞은 이 두 여자가 그토록 하나로 묶여 있는 모습을, 서로에게 어떤 존재가 될 수 있는지를, 서로를 어떻게 헤아리는지를, 모진 시련이 저마다의 마음을 어떻게 서로에게 누그러뜨려 놓았는지를 보는 일은, 나는 무척 가슴 뭉클하다고 생각했습니다. 나는 이런 사람들의 가장 선한 면은 우리에게 거의 가려져 있다고 생각합니다. 가난한 이가 가난한 이에게 어떤 존재인지는, 그들 자신과 하느님 말고는 거의 알려져 있지 않으니까요.\n\n우리는 물러나 그들을 방해하지 않고 두는 게 낫겠다고 느꼈습니다. 우리는 그 남자 말고는 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않은 채 조용히 빠져나왔습니다. 그는 문 가까운 벽에 기대어 있다가, 우리가 지나갈 자리가 거의 없는 걸 보더니 우리보다 먼저 밖으로 나갔습니다. 그는 자기가 우리를 위해 그런다는 걸 감추고 싶어 하는 듯했지만, 우리는 그가 그러는 걸 알아채고 고맙다고 했습니다. 그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n에이다는 집으로 돌아오는 내내 슬픔에 잠겨 있었고, 집에서 우리를 맞은 리처드는 그녀가 눈물짓는 모습에 어찌나 마음 아파하던지(그러면서도 그녀가 없을 때 나한테는, 그 모습이 또 얼마나 아름답던지 모른다고 했지만요), 우리는 밤에 자그마한 위안거리들을 챙겨 다시 가서 벽돌공의 집을 한 번 더 찾아보기로 했습니다. 잔다이스 씨에게는 되도록 말을 아꼈지만, 그래도 바람은 곧장 방향을 바꿨습니다.\n\n리처드는 밤에 우리가 아침에 다녀온 그곳까지 동행했습니다. 가는 길에 우리는 시끌벅적한 술집 하나를 지나야 했는데, 그 문 앞에 사내들이 잔뜩 몰려 있었습니다. 그들 사이에서, 무슨 다툼을 벌이며 두드러져 보이는 이가, 바로 그 어린아이의 아버지였습니다. 조금 떨어진 곳에서 우리는 그 젓은 사내와 개가 죽이 맞는 패거리와 어울려 있는 것을 지나쳤습니다. 누이는 오두막들이 늘어선 줄 모퉁이에서 다른 젊은 여자들과 웃고 떠들며 서 있었지만, 우리가 지나가자 부끄러운 듯 고개를 돌렸습니다.\n\n우리는 벽돌공의 집이 보이는 곳에 호위병을 남겨 두고 우리끼리 나아갔습니다. 문 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그토록 큰 위안을 가져다주었던 그 여자가 거기 서서 불안하게 밖을 내다보고 있는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n\"아가씨들이시군요?\" 그녀가 속삭이듯 말했습니다. \"제 남편을 망보고 있는 중이에요. 가슴이 입 밖으로 튀어나올 것만 같네요. 제가 집을 비운 걸 들키기라도 하면, 절 거의 죽여 놓을 거예요.\"\n\n\"남편이라고요?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 제 남편이요. 제니는 자고 있어요, 완전히 녹초가 됐거든요. 가엾게도 이레 낮밤을, 제가 잠깐씩 안아 줄 수 있었을 때 말고는 그 애를 무릎에서 거의 내려놓지도 못했답니다.\"\n\n그녀는 우리에게 자리를 내주면서 가만히 안으로 들어가, 우리가 가져온 것을 어머니가 잠든 그 초라한 침상 가까이에 놓았습니다. 방을 치우려는 노력은 전혀 없었습니다 — 본디 깨끗해지기를 바랄 수 없을 만큼 가망 없는 곳인 듯했지요. 하지만 그토록 많은 엄숙함을 풍기던 그 작은 밀랍 같은 형체는 다시 가지런히 뉘어졌고, 씻겨졌고, 흰 아마포 조각으로 단정하게 감싸여 있었습니다. 그리고 가엾은 아기를 여전히 덮고 있던 내 손수건 위에는, 바로 그 거칠고 흉터진 손이, 어찌나 가볍게, 어찌나 다정하게, 향긋한 풀 한 다발을 얹어 놓았던지요!\n\n\"하늘이 당신에게 갚아 주시길!\" 우리는 그녀에게 말했습니다. \"당신은 착한 여인이에요.\"\n\n\"제가요, 아가씨들?\" 그녀가 놀라며 대꾸했습니다. \"쉿! 제니, 제니!\"\n\n어머니가 잠결에 신음하며 몸을 뒤척였던 것입니다. 그 낯익은 목소리가 그녀를 다시 가라앉히는 듯했습니다. 그녀는 다시 잠잠해졌습니다.\n\n내가 손수건을 들어 그 아래 잠든 작은 이를 들여다보며, 가엾게 여겨 고개 숙인 에이다의 드리워진 머리카락 사이로 아기 둘레에 후광이 비치는 것을 본 듯했던 그때, 나는 얼마나 까맣게 몰랐던지요 — 미동도 없이 평온한 그 가슴을 덮었던 이 손수건이 훗날 누구의 편치 못한 가슴 위에 놓이게 될지를 말입니다! 다만 나는 그저 이렇게만 생각했습니다. 어쩌면 그 아이의 천사가, 그토록 가여워하는 손길로 손수건을 다시 덮어 준 그 여자를 아주 모르지는 않으리라고. 우리가 작별을 고하고 그녀를 문가에 남겨 두었을 때, 번갈아 가며 제 처지가 두려워 밖을 살피고 귀 기울이면서도, 예의 그 다독이는 말투로 \"제니, 제니!\" 하고 되뇌던 그녀를, 그 천사가 그때에도 아주 모르지는 않으리라고요.\n\n어쩌다 보니 저는 늘 제 이야기만 쓰는 것 같습니다. 마음으로는 줄곧 다른 사람들 이야기를 쓰려고 하고, 저 자신은 되도록 적게 생각하려 애쓰며, 정말이지 이야기 속에 또 제가 끼어드는 걸 발견할 때마다 속이 상해서 \"어머, 어머, 이 성가신 것아, 제발 그러지 좀 마!\"라고 말합니다만, 다 소용없는 일입니다. 제가 쓰는 글을 읽으실 분이 계시다면, 이 지면에 저에 관한 이야기가 잔뜩 들어 있더라도 그건 제가 정말로 그 일들과 얽혀 있어서 도저히 빠질 수 없기 때문이라고 짐작하실 수밖에 없다는 점을 이해해주셨으면 합니다.\n\n사랑스러운 그 애와 저는 함께 책을 읽고, 일하고, 연습하며, 시간을 그토록 알차게 채운 덕분에 겨울날들은 밝은 날개를 단 새처럼 곁을 스쳐 날아갔습니다. 대개 오후에, 그리고 저녁에는 언제나 리처드가 우리와 함께 있어주었습니다. 그는 세상에서 가장 가만있지 못하는 사람 가운데 하나였지만, 우리와 어울리는 걸 무척이나 좋아한 게 분명했습니다.\n\n그는 에이다를 아주, 아주, 아주 좋아했습니다. 정말입니다, 그러니 곧바로 말해두는 편이 낫겠습니다. 저는 그때까지 젊은 사람들이 사랑에 빠지는 모습을 본 적이 없었지만, 곧 두 사람의 마음을 알아챘습니다. 물론 입 밖에 낼 수도, 제가 뭔가 안다는 내색을 할 수도 없었습니다. 오히려 너무 얌전을 빼며 아무것도 모르는 척하다 보니, 일감을 들고 앉아 있다가 가끔은 내가 영 음흉한 사람이 되어가는 건 아닐까 하고 속으로 헤아려보기도 했습니다.\n\n그렇지만 어쩔 도리가 없었습니다. 제가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 잠자코 있는 것뿐이었고, 저는 생쥐처럼 조용했습니다. 두 사람도 말로 따지자면 생쥐처럼 조용했지만, 서로에게 점점 더 빠져들수록 점점 더 저에게 기대오는 그 천진한 태도가 어찌나 사랑스러웠던지, 제가 얼마나 관심을 두고 있는지 내색하지 않기가 무척 힘들었습니다.\n\n\"우리 귀여운 꼬마 할머니는 정말 둘도 없는 할머니라니까요.\" 리처드는 아침 일찍 정원으로 저를 맞으러 나오며, 기분 좋은 웃음과 어쩌면 아주 살짝 붉어진 뺨으로 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"이분 없이는 도무지 못 견디겠어요. 정신없는 하루를 시작하기 전에—그 책들이며 도구들과 씨름하다가 노상강도처럼 온 동네를 언덕 위로 골짜기 아래로 말 달려 누비기 전에—나와서 우리의 마음 편한 친구와 차분하게 한 바퀴 걷는 게 얼마나 좋은지, 그래서 또 이렇게 나왔지 뭐예요!\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 더든 아주머니.\" 에이다는 밤이면 제 어깨에 머리를 기대고, 사색에 잠긴 눈에 난롯불빛을 담은 채 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"여기 위층에 올라오면 말하고 싶지가 않아요. 그저 잠깐 앉아서, 언니의 사랑스러운 얼굴을 벗 삼아 생각에 잠기고, 바람 소리를 들으며 바다에 나간 가엾은 뱃사람들을 떠올리고 싶을 뿐이에요—\"\n\n아! 어쩌면 리처드는 뱃사람이 될 참이었습니다. 우리는 이제 그 얘기를 자주 나누었고, 어릴 적부터 품어온 바다에 대한 그의 마음을 채워주자는 말도 오갔습니다. 잔다이스 씨는 집안의 친척인 위대한 레스터 데드록 경에게 편지를 써서, 리처드에게 두루 마음을 써달라고 부탁했습니다. 레스터 경은 정중하게 답장을 보내, 자기 힘이 닿는 일이라면—그럴 가능성은 도무지 없지만—그 젊은이의 앞날을 기꺼이 밀어주겠노라고, 그리고 마님께서도 그 젊은이(먼 핏줄로 인척이 된다는 사실을 또렷이 기억한다는)에게 안부를 전하며, 그가 어떤 명예로운 직업에 몸담든 늘 제 본분을 다하기를 바란다고 했습니다.\n\n\"그러니 내가 보기엔 꽤 분명해요.\" 리처드가 저에게 말했습니다. \"내 길은 내 힘으로 헤쳐가야 한다는 거죠. 상관없어요! 지금까지도 그렇게 해야 했던 사람은 숱하게 많았고, 다들 해냈으니까. 나야 그저 처음부터 날쌘 사략선 한 척만 손에 쥘 수 있다면, 대법관을 납치해서 우리 소송에 판결을 내릴 때까지 굶기다시피 가둬두고 싶을 뿐이에요. 정신 바짝 안 차리면 점점 야위어가는 자기 꼴을 보게 될걸요!\"\n\n좀처럼 사그라지지 않는 활기와 희망과 명랑함을 지녔으면서도, 리처드의 성격에는 저를 무척 당혹스럽게 하는 무심함이 깃들어 있었습니다. 무엇보다 그가 그 무심함을 참으로 묘한 방식으로 신중함이라 착각했기 때문입니다. 그것은 돈에 관한 그의 모든 셈에 유별난 방식으로 끼어들었는데, 잠시 스킴폴 씨에게 돈을 빌려준 일로 되돌아가는 것보다 더 잘 설명할 길은 없을 듯합니다.\n\n잔다이스 씨는 스킴폴 씨 본인에게서든 코빈시즈에게서든 그 액수를 알아내, 제 손에 그 돈을 쥐여주며 제 몫은 챙기고 나머지는 리처드에게 건네라고 일렀습니다. 리처드가 자기 십 파운드를 도로 찾았다는 명목으로 정당화한 자잘한 충동적 지출의 횟수와, 마치 그 액수를 아끼거나 벌어들이기라도 한 것처럼 저에게 말한 횟수를 더하면 그저 단순한 덧셈 문제가 될 것입니다.\n\n\"신중한 우리 허버드 아주머니, 안 될 게 뭐 있어요?\" 그는 조금도 망설이지 않고 벽돌공에게 오 파운드를 주려 했을 때 저에게 이렇게 말했습니다. \"코빈시즈 일로 십 파운드를 고스란히 벌었잖아요.\"\n\n\"그게 어떻게 그래요?\" 제가 물었습니다.\n\n\"아니, 나는 기꺼이 떠나보낼 셈이었고 다시는 못 볼 줄 알았던 십 파운드를 떼였잖아요. 그건 아니라고 못 하겠죠?\"\n\n\"네.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"좋아요! 그러고 나서 나는 십 파운드를 손에 넣었어요—\"\n\n\"같은 십 파운드잖아요.\" 제가 넌지시 일렀습니다.\n\n\"그건 상관없어요!\" 리처드가 받아쳤습니다. \"나는 기대했던 것보다 십 파운드를 더 갖게 됐고, 따라서 까다롭게 굴 것 없이 그 돈을 쓸 여유가 있는 거예요.\"\n\n꼭 마찬가지로, 그 오 파운드를 내놓아봐야 아무 소용없다는 걸 깨닫고 그 지출을 단념했을 때도, 그는 그 액수를 자기 잔고에 적어 넣고 거기서 끌어다 썼습니다.\n\n\"어디 보자!\" 그는 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"벽돌공 일에서 오 파운드를 아꼈으니까, 역마차로 런던을 신나게 한 번 다녀오면서 그 비용을 사 파운드로 잡으면, 일 파운드를 아낀 셈이지. 일 파운드를 아끼는 건 아주 좋은 일이거든, 내 말 들어봐요. 한 푼 아끼면 한 푼 버는 거니까!\"\n\n리처드의 천성은 그보다 더할 수 없을 만큼 솔직하고 너그러웠다고 저는 믿습니다. 그는 열정적이고 용감했으며, 그 거친 들뜸 한복판에서도 어찌나 부드러웠던지 몇 주 만에 저는 그를 친오빠처럼 여기게 되었습니다. 그 부드러움은 그에게 타고난 것이어서 에이다의 영향이 없었더라도 넘치도록 드러났겠지만, 거기에 더해 그는 더없이 사람을 끄는 벗이 되었으니, 늘 무엇에든 흔쾌히 관심을 보였고 늘 그토록 행복하고, 낙천적이고, 마음이 가벼웠습니다. 두 사람과 함께 앉아 있고, 함께 걷고, 함께 이야기하며, 날마다 두 사람이 어떻게 점점 더 깊이 사랑에 빠지면서도 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고, 저마다 이 사랑이야말로 더없이 큰 비밀이며 어쩌면 상대조차 아직 눈치채지 못했으리라 수줍게 여기는 모습을 지켜보던 저는—저 또한 두 사람 못지않게 마음을 빼앗겼고, 그 어여쁜 꿈에 두 사람 못지않게 기뻐했다고 확신합니다.\n\n이렇게 지내던 어느 날 아침, 식사 자리에서 잔다이스 씨가 편지 한 통을 받았습니다. 겉봉을 보더니 그가 말했습니다. \"보이손한테서 온 건가? 그래, 그렇군!\" 그러고는 분명 흐뭇한 기색으로 편지를 뜯어 읽었는데, 절반쯤 읽었을 무렵 보이손이 \"내려와\" 방문할 거라고 우리에게 한마디 끼워 알렸습니다. 자, 보이손이 누굴까, 우리는 모두 그렇게 생각했습니다. 그리고 우리 모두—적어도 저는 분명히 그랬습니다—이런 생각도 했을 것입니다. 보이손이 지금 한창 무르익어가는 일에 조금이라도 끼어들지는 않을까?\n\n\"이 친구, 로런스 보이손하고는 같이 학교를 다녔지.\" 잔다이스 씨가 편지를 탁자에 내려놓으며 그것을 톡톡 두드렸습니다. \"사십오 년도 더 전에 말이야. 그땐 세상에서 가장 충동적인 소년이었는데, 지금은 세상에서 가장 충동적인 사내야. 그땐 세상에서 가장 목소리 큰 소년이었는데, 지금은 세상에서 가장 목소리 큰 사내고. 그땐 세상에서 가장 기운차고 다부진 소년이었는데, 지금은 세상에서 가장 기운차고 다부진 사내지. 엄청난 친구라네.\"\n\n\"덩치가 말입니까, 어르신?\" 리처드가 물었습니다.\n\n\"그쪽으로도 꽤 그렇지, 릭.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"나보다 열 살쯤 위에 키는 두어 인치 더 크고, 늙은 군인처럼 고개를 뒤로 젖히고, 떡 벌어진 가슴팍을 네모지게 펴고, 손은 깨끗한 대장장이 손 같고, 게다가 그 허파라니! 그 허파에 견줄 만한 게 없어. 말을 하든, 웃든, 코를 골든, 집 들보가 흔들릴 지경이거든.\"\n\n잔다이스 씨가 친구 보이손의 모습을 떠올리며 즐거워하는 동안, 우리는 바람 방향이 바뀔 기미가 조금도 없다는 길조를 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 말하려는 건 그 사람의 속, 그 사람의 따뜻한 마음, 그 사람의 열정, 그 사람의 펄펄 끓는 피야, 릭—그리고 에이다, 또 우리 꼬마 거미줄, 손님 얘기니 너희도 다 관심 있겠지.\" 그가 말을 이었습니다. \"그 사람 말투는 목소리만큼이나 쩌렁쩌렁하지. 늘 극단으로 치닫고, 노상 최상급으로만 말한다네. 비난할 땐 온통 사납기 그지없어. 그가 하는 말만 들으면 식인 괴물쯤으로 여길 만하고, 실제로 어떤 사람들 사이에선 그런 평판도 있는 모양이야. 자! 미리 더 일러주진 않겠네. 그가 나를 제 보호 아래 두려 하더라도 놀라지들 말게. 그는 내가 학교에서 약한 아이였다는 것과, 우리 우정이 그가 나를 괴롭히던 우두머리의 이를 아침 식사 전에 두 대(본인은 여섯 대라고 하지만) 부러뜨린 데서 시작됐다는 걸 결코 잊지 않았거든. 보이손과 그 하인은,\" 그가 저를 향해 말했습니다. \"오늘 오후에 도착할 거야, 얘야.\"\n\n저는 보이손 씨를 맞을 채비를 빈틈없이 갖추도록 신경 썼고, 우리는 다소 궁금한 마음으로 그의 도착을 기다렸습니다. 그러나 오후가 다 지나도록 그는 나타나지 않았습니다. 저녁 식사 시각이 되어도 여전히 나타나지 않았습니다. 식사를 한 시간 미루고, 우리가 불빛이라곤 난롯불뿐인 가운데 둘러앉아 있을 때, 현관문이 느닷없이 벌컥 열리더니 더없이 격렬하게, 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 이런 말이 현관에 울려 퍼졌습니다. \"길을 잘못 들었지 뭔가, 잔다이스, 더없이 막돼먹은 망나니 놈 탓이야. 왼쪽으로 꺾으랬어야 할 걸 오른쪽으로 꺾으라고 했단 말이지. 지구상에 그놈보다 더 못 봐줄 악당은 없을 거야. 그런 자식을 두다니 그 아비도 분명 둘도 없는 악한이었을 게야. 그놈은 눈곱만큼의 후회도 없이 총살시켜버렸어야 했어!\"\n\n\"그자가 일부러 그랬답니까?\" 잔다이스 씨가 물었습니다.\n\n\"그 악당이 평생 나그네들 길을 엉뚱하게 일러주며 살아왔다는 데 나는 한 점 의심도 없어!\" 상대가 받아쳤습니다. \"맹세컨대, 오른쪽으로 꺾으라고 일러줄 때 보니 내 평생 본 것 중에 가장 흉악하게 생긴 개 같은 놈이더군. 그런데도 그놈을 코앞에 마주 보고 서서 골통을 박살내지 않았다니!\"\n\n\"이 말이겠지?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"하, 하, 하!\" 로런스 보이손 씨가 웃음을 터뜨리니, 정말로 온 집안이 다 울렸습니다. \"아니, 아직도 그걸 안 잊었군! 하, 하, 하! 그놈도 또 하나의 둘도 없는 부랑자였지! 맹세컨대, 그놈이 소년이었을 때의 그 낯짝은 악당들 밭에 세워둔 허수아비 중에서도 가장 시커먼 배신과 비겁과 잔인의 화신이었어. 내일 길에서 그 비할 데 없는 폭군을 만난다면, 썩은 나무처럼 단번에 베어 넘어뜨려 줄 텐데!\"\n\n\"그러시겠지요.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"자, 이제 위층으로 올라가시겠소?\"\n\n\"맹세컨대, 잔다이스.\" 손님이 시계를 보는 듯하더니 받아 말했습니다. \"자네가 결혼한 몸이었다면, 이런 당치 않은 시각에 나타나느니 차라리 정원 문에서 발길을 돌려 히말라야산맥 가장 외진 봉우리까지라도 가버렸을 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇게까지 멀리는 안 가셨겠지요?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"내 목숨과 명예를 걸고, 갔을 거야!\" 손님이 외쳤습니다. \"세상 그 무엇을 위해서라도, 안주인을 이토록 오래 기다리게 하는 무엄하고 발칙한 죄는 짓지 않았을 거야. 차라리 나 자신을 끝장내는 편이 한없이 낫지—한없이 낫고말고!\"\n\n이렇게 이야기를 나누며 두 사람은 위층으로 올라갔고, 이윽고 우리는 그가 침실에서 \"하, 하, 하!\" 또 \"하, 하, 하!\" 우레처럼 웃어대는 소리를 들었습니다. 마침내 동네에서 가장 무덤덤한 메아리마저 그 웃음에 전염된 듯, 그가 웃는 만큼이나, 또 우리가 그 웃음을 듣고 웃는 만큼이나 신나게 따라 웃는 것 같았습니다.\n\n우리는 모두 그에게 호감을 품게 되었습니다. 그 웃음에도, 우렁차고 건강한 목소리에도, 한마디 한마디를 둥글고 꽉 차게 내뱉는 그 말투에도, 마치 공포탄처럼 펑펑 터지되 아무것도 다치게 하지 않는 그 최상급의 격렬함 자체에도 진국 같은 무언가가 있었기 때문입니다. 그러나 잔다이스 씨가 그를 소개했을 때, 그 호감이 외모로 이토록 굳어질 줄은 우리도 미처 예상하지 못했습니다. 그는 단지 아주 잘생긴 노신사—듣던 대로 꼿꼿하고 다부진—였을 뿐 아니라, 묵직한 잿빛 머리에, 입을 다물면 더없이 차분한 얼굴에, 워낙 늘 진지함에 골몰해 한순간도 쉴 틈을 주지 않은 탓에 비대해질 법도 했으나 그러지 못한 체격에, 끊임없이 그 격렬한 강조를 거들어야 했던 까닭에 겹턱으로 처질 법도 했으나 그러지 못한 턱을 지녔습니다. 게다가 그는 태도가 어찌나 참된 신사인지, 기사처럼 정중했고, 그 얼굴은 더없이 다정하고 따뜻한 미소로 환했으며, 숨길 것이라곤 하나도 없이 있는 그대로의 자기를 고스란히 내보이는 게 너무도 분명해 보였습니다—리처드의 말마따나 어떤 일도 자잘한 규모로는 못 하는 사람, 자잘한 무기라곤 아예 지니지 않아 저 공포탄 같은 거포만 펑펑 쏘아대는 사람이었습니다—그래서 정말이지 저는 식사 자리에 앉은 그를 한결같은 즐거움으로 바라보지 않을 수 없었습니다. 그가 에이다와 저에게 미소 지으며 말을 건넬 때도, 잔다이스 씨에게 이끌려 최상급 어휘를 한바탕 쏟아낼 때도, 사냥개처럼 고개를 쳐들고 그 어마어마한 \"하, 하, 하!\"를 터뜨릴 때도 말입니다.\n\n\"새도 데려오셨겠지요?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"맹세코, 그놈은 유럽에서 가장 놀라운 새야!\" 상대가 답했습니다. \"정말 둘도 없이 신통한 녀석이지! 만 기니를 준대도 그 새는 안 내놓을 거야. 그놈이 나보다 오래 살 경우를 대비해 그놈 부양만을 위한 연금까지 따로 마련해뒀다네. 영리함으로 보나 정으로 보나 그놈은 경이 그 자체야. 그리고 그 아비 새도 여태껏 살았던 새 중에 가장 놀라운 녀석이었지!\"\n\n이 칭송의 대상은 아주 자그마한 카나리아였는데, 어찌나 길이 잘 들었던지 보이손 씨의 하인이 집게손가락에 앉혀 데리고 내려왔고, 그놈은 방 안을 한 바퀴 살랑살랑 날더니 주인의 머리 위에 내려앉았습니다. 이 가냘프고 자그마한 미물이 제 이마에 가만히 앉아 있는 채로, 보이손 씨가 더없이 무자비하고 격정적인 소회를 토해내는 모습을 듣고 있자니, 그의 성격을 잘 보여주는 좋은 본보기라는 생각이 들었습니다.\n\n\"맹세컨대, 잔다이스.\" 그가 카나리아가 쪼아 먹도록 빵 한 조각을 아주 부드럽게 들어 올리며 말했습니다. \"내가 자네 처지라면 내일 아침 당장 대법원의 사무관들 멱살을 하나하나 거머쥐고, 주머니에서 돈이 쏟아져 나오고 가죽 속에서 뼈다귀가 덜그럭거릴 때까지 흔들어대겠어. 정당한 수단으로든 부정한 수단으로든 누군가에게서 합의를 받아내고 말겠어. 자네가 나한테 그럴 권한만 준다면, 더없이 흡족한 마음으로 자네를 위해 해주겠네!\" (이러는 내내 그 아주 자그마한 카나리아는 그의 손에서 먹이를 쪼아 먹고 있었습니다.)\n\n\"고맙네, 로런스. 하지만 그 소송은 지금 그런 단계라고 보긴 어렵다네.\" 잔다이스 씨가 웃으며 받았습니다. \"판사석과 변호인단 전부를 흔들어대는 법적 절차를 거친다 한들 크게 진척될 만한 처지가 아니거든.\"\n\n\"이 지구상에 그 대법원만큼 지독한 가마솥은 또 없을 거야!\" 보이손 씨가 말했습니다. \"개정 기간 한창 바쁜 날 그 밑에 지뢰를 묻고, 거기 딸린 기록이며 규칙이며 판례를 죄다 그러모으고, 거기 매인 관리란 관리는 높든 낮든, 위든 아래든, 그 아들뻘인 회계감사관에서부터 그 아비뻘인 악마에 이르기까지 죄다 한데 모아, 화약 만 헌드레드웨이트로 통째 산산조각 내어 날려버리는 것 말고는, 그걸 눈곱만큼이라도 뜯어고칠 길이 없어!\"\n\n이토록 기운찬 진지함으로 그가 권하는 이 강력한 개혁책에 웃지 않기란 불가능했습니다. 우리가 웃자 그는 고개를 쳐들고 떡 벌어진 가슴팍을 들썩였으며, 또다시 온 동네가 그의 \"하, 하, 하!\"에 메아리치는 듯했습니다. 그래도 그 새를 흩트리는 데에는 조금의 효과도 없었으니, 안전하다는 확신에 가득 찬 그놈은 탁자 위를 폴짝폴짝 돌아다니며 잰 고개를 이쪽저쪽으로 갸웃거리고, 마치 주인이 별것 아닌 또 한 마리 새이기라도 한 양 그 밝고 재빠른 눈을 그에게 돌리곤 했습니다.\n\n\"그런데 통행권 다툼을 두고 이웃과는 어찌 지내시오?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"당신도 법의 굴레에서 자유롭지는 못하니 말이오!\"\n\n\"그놈은 무단침입으로 나한테 소송을 걸었고, 나도 무단침입으로 그놈한테 소송을 걸었네.\" 보이손 씨가 받았습니다. \"맹세코, 그놈은 숨 쉬는 사람 중에 가장 오만한 작자야. 그 이름이 레스터 경일 리가 도덕적으로 도무지 없어. 분명 루시퍼 경일 거야.\"\n\n\"우리 먼 친척에 대한 칭찬이로군요!\" 후견인이 에이다와 리처드에게 웃으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"클레어 양과 카스톤 씨에게 용서를 빌어야 마땅하겠지.\" 손님이 다시 말을 이었습니다. \"다만 아가씨의 고운 얼굴과 신사분의 미소에서, 그럴 필요가 조금도 없으며 두 분이 그 먼 친척을 적당히 편안한 거리에 두고 계시다는 걸 읽고 안심하지 않았더라면 말이야.\"\n\n\"아니면 그쪽이 우리를 두는 거겠지요.\" 리처드가 거들었습니다.\n\n\"맹세코,\" 보이손 씨가 별안간 또 한바탕 포문을 열며 외쳤습니다. \"그놈도, 그 아비도, 그 할아비도, 자연이 도무지 설명할 수 없는 실수를 저질러 지팡이 신세 말고는 어떤 처지로도 태어나지 말았어야 할, 둘도 없이 목이 뻣뻣하고 거만한 멍청이, 고집불통 돌대가리들이었어! 그 집안 전체가 더없이 엄숙하게 자만에 찬, 둘도 없는 얼간이들이라고! 하지만 상관없네. 그놈이 쉰 명의 준남작을 하나로 녹여 빚은 자라 한들, 그래서 중국 조각의 상아 공처럼 하나가 다른 하나를 품은 체스니 월드 백 채에 산다 한들, 내 길은 막지 못해. 그놈이 제 대리인인지, 비서인지, 누군가를 시켜 나한테 이렇게 써 보낸단 말이지. '준남작 레스터 데드록 경이 로런스 보이손 씨에게 안부를 전하며, 현재 로런스 보이손 씨의 소유인 옛 사제관 옆 푸른 오솔길은 사실상 체스니 월드 정원의 일부로서 레스터 경의 통행권에 속하는바, 레스터 경이 이를 폐쇄함이 편리하다고 보는 사실에 주의를 환기하고자 한다.' 그래서 나는 그놈한테 이렇게 써 보내지. '로런스 보이손 씨가 준남작 레스터 데드록 경에게 안부를 전하며, 가능한 모든 사안에 걸쳐 레스터 데드록 경의 입장을 통째로 전면 부정하는 사실에 그의 주의를 환기하고자 하며, 오솔길 폐쇄와 관련해서는 그 일을 맡아 해보겠다는 자를 한번 만나 뵙기를 고대한다고 덧붙이는 바이다.' 그러자 그놈이 애꾸눈에 더없이 막돼먹은 악당을 보내 문을 세우려 들더군. 나는 그 천하의 악당한테 소방 펌프를 들이대서 숨이 거의 다 빠져나갈 지경으로 물을 퍼부어줬지. 그놈은 한밤중에 문을 세워. 나는 아침에 그걸 찍어 넘어뜨리고 불태워버려. 그놈이 제 졸개들을 보내 울타리를 넘어 들락날락하게 하면, 나는 사람 다치지 않는 덫으로 그놈들을 잡고, 다리에 콩알을 쏘아대고, 펌프 물을 퍼부어대지—그렇게 숨어 다니는 망나니들의 존재라는 견딜 수 없는 짐에서 인류를 해방하기로 작정한 거야. 그놈이 무단침입으로 소송을 걸면, 나도 무단침입으로 소송을 걸어. 그놈이 폭행과 구타로 소송을 걸면, 나는 그걸 방어하면서 계속 폭행하고 구타하지. 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n상상도 못 할 기세로 이 모든 말을 쏟아내는 그를 듣고 있노라면, 인류 중에 가장 성난 사람이라 여길 만했습니다. 바로 그 순간 엄지에 앉은 새를 들여다보며 집게손가락으로 그 깃털을 살살 쓰다듬는 그를 보고 있노라면, 더없이 온순한 사람이라 여길 만했습니다. 그가 웃는 소리를 듣고 그때 그 얼굴에 드넓게 번지는 호인다움을 보고 있노라면, 세상에 근심이라곤, 다툼이라곤, 미워하는 것이라곤 하나도 없이, 그의 온 생애가 여름날의 농담 한 자락이라 짐작할 만했습니다.\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"데드록 따위가 내 길을 막다니, 어림도 없지! 물론 기꺼이 인정하건대,\" 여기서 그는 한순간에 누그러졌습니다. \"데드록 마님은 세상에서 가장 기품 넘치는 부인이며, 일곱 세기나 묵은 골통을 인 준남작 따위가 아니라 한낱 평범한 신사가 바칠 수 있는 어떤 경의라도 나는 그분께 바치겠어. 스무 살에 연대에 들어가 일주일도 안 되어, 꽉 졸라맨 허리로 숨을 쉬는 인간 중에 가장 거만하고 건방진 멋쟁이 지휘관에게 결투를 신청하고—그 때문에 강등까지 당한—사내가, 죽었든 살았든, 잠겼든 열렸든, 루시퍼 경 따위 누구한테도 짓밟힐 사내는 아니지. 하, 하, 하!\"\n\n\"제 후배가 짓밟히는 것 또한 두고 볼 사내가 아니고요?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"두말하면 잔소리지!\" 보이손 씨가 그의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했습니다. 웃고는 있었지만 그 보호하는 듯한 기색에는 어딘가 진지한 데가 있었습니다. \"그는 늘 약한 아이 편에 설 거야. 잔다이스, 자네는 그를 믿어도 좋아! 그런데 이 무단침입 얘기 말인데—이토록 메마른 주제를 길게도 늘어놓은 데 대해 클레어 양과 서머슨 양께 사과를 드리며—자네 사람들, 켄지와 카보이한테서 나한테 온 건 없나?\"\n\n\"없는 것 같지, 에스더?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"없어요, 후견인님.\"\n\n\"고맙군요!\" 보이손 씨가 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양께서 주변 모두를 얼마나 살뜰히 챙기시는지 잠깐 겪어본 것만으로도, 물어볼 필요조차 없었는데 말입니다.\" (다들 저를 추켜세웠습니다. 작정하고 그러는 것이었습니다.) \"내가 물어본 건, 링컨셔에서 온 터라 아직 시내에 들르지 못해, 혹 여기로 편지가 내려왔을까 싶어서였어요. 내일 아침이면 진척 상황을 알려오겠지요.\"\n\n매우 즐겁게 흘러간 그 저녁 내내, 저는 그가 피아노에서 조금 떨어져 앉아 음악에 귀 기울이며 리처드와 에이다를 바라보는 모습을 자주 보았습니다. 그 관심과 흐뭇함이 어찌나 그 멋진 얼굴을 유난히 보기 좋게 했던지—음악을 열렬히 좋아한다는 말을 굳이 우리에게 할 필요도 없었으니, 얼굴에 그대로 드러났던 것입니다—저는 우리가 주사위 놀이판 앞에 앉았을 때 후견인에게 보이손 씨가 결혼한 적이 있느냐고 물었습니다.\n\"아니.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 하려고는 하셨죠!\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그건 어떻게 알았지?\" 그가 미소 지으며 받았습니다. \"그게요, 후견인님.\" 저는 마음속에 떠오른 생각을 무릅쓰고 말하려니 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 설명했습니다. \"어쨌든 저분 태도에는 어딘가 무척 다정한 데가 있고, 우리에게 그토록 정중하고 부드럽게 대해주시고, 또—\"\n\n잔다이스 씨는 제가 방금 묘사한 대로 그가 앉아 있는 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸습니다.\n\n저는 더 말하지 않았습니다.\n\n\"네 말이 맞아, 꼬마 아가씨.\" 그가 답했습니다. \"그는 한 번, 거의 결혼할 뻔했지. 아주 오래전에. 딱 한 번.\"\n\n\"그 여인이 세상을 떠났나요?\"\n\n\"아니—다만 그에게는 죽은 거나 마찬가지였어. 그때 일이 그 뒤로 그의 온 삶에 그늘을 드리웠지. 그가 아직도 머리와 가슴에 낭만을 가득 품고 있으리라 짐작이나 했겠니?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 후견인님, 어쩌면 짐작했을지도 모르겠어요. 하지만 그렇게 말씀해주시고 나니 그렇게 말하기야 쉽지요.\"\n\n\"그는 그 뒤로 한 번도 그가 될 수 있었던 사람이 되지 못했어.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그래서 이제 그 나이가 되도록, 곁에 둔 거라곤 하인과 저 자그마한 노란 친구뿐인 모습을 너도 보는 거지. 네 차례다, 얘야!\"\n\n저는 후견인의 태도로 미루어, 이 이상은 바람의 방향을 바꾸지 않고서는 이 이야기를 더 캐물을 수 없겠다고 느꼈습니다. 그래서 더는 질문을 삼갔습니다. 마음이 가긴 했지만 캐고 싶지는 않았습니다. 밤중에 보이손 씨의 우렁찬 코 고는 소리에 잠이 깼을 때, 저는 이 오래된 사랑 이야기를 잠시 곱씹어보았습니다. 그리고 무척이나 어려운 일, 곧 노인들을 다시 젊게, 청춘의 우아함을 두른 모습으로 그려보려 애썼습니다. 그러나 그 일을 이루기도 전에 잠이 들어, 대모님 댁에 살던 시절의 꿈을 꾸었습니다. 저는 이런 일에 대해 잘 알지 못해, 제가 거의 늘 제 삶의 그 시절을 꿈꾸는 것이 조금이라도 별스러운 일인지 어떤지는 모르겠습니다.\n\n아침이 되자 켄지 카보이 법률사무소에서 보이손 씨에게 편지가 와서, 정오에 사무원 하나가 찾아뵐 거라고 알렸습니다. 그날은 제가 청구서를 치르고, 장부를 결산하고, 집안 살림을 되도록 빈틈없이 추스르는 요일이었던지라, 잔다이스 씨와 에이다와 리처드가 더없이 맑은 날을 틈타 잠깐 소풍을 떠나는 동안 저는 집에 남았고, 보이손 씨는 켄지 카보이의 사무원을 기다렸다가 걸어서 나가 돌아오는 그들을 마중하기로 했습니다.\n\n자! 저는 상인들의 장부를 살피고, 숫자 칸을 합산하고, 돈을 치르고, 영수증을 철하느라 일에 파묻혀, 분명 부산을 떨고 있었습니다. 그때 거피 씨가 왔다는 전갈과 함께 그가 안으로 안내되어 들어왔습니다. 내려보낸다는 그 사무원이 마차 사무소에서 저를 맞아주었던 그 젊은이일지도 모른다는 생각이 어렴풋이 들던 참이라, 저는 그를 보고 반가웠습니다. 그는 지금의 제 행복과 얽혀 있는 사람이었으니까요.\n\n그가 어찌나 유난히 말쑥하던지 저는 그를 한눈에 알아보지도 못했습니다. 윤기 흐르는 새 옷을 한 벌 빼입고, 번쩍이는 모자에, 연보라색 새끼염소 가죽 장갑에, 갖가지 색이 어우러진 목도리에, 단춧구멍에는 커다란 온실 꽃을 꽂고, 새끼손가락에는 두툼한 금반지를 끼고 있었습니다. 게다가 곰 기름이며 다른 향수 냄새로 식당을 온통 진동시켰습니다. 하인이 돌아올 때까지 자리에 앉으시라 권하자, 그가 저를 어찌나 빤히 보는지 저는 무척 당황했습니다. 그가 구석에 앉아 다리를 꼬았다 풀었다 하는 동안, 제가 오는 길이 편하셨느냐고, 켄지 씨는 안녕하시냐고 물었지만, 그를 볼 때마다 그 역시 똑같이 꼬치꼬치 캐는 듯 호기심 어린 눈으로 저를 바라보고 있는 것이었습니다.\n\n위층 보이손 씨 방으로 올라와달라는 청이 그에게 전해지자, 저는 내려오면 점심이 차려져 있을 테니 잔다이스 씨께서 들기를 바라신다고 일러두었습니다. 그는 다소 어색한 기색으로 문손잡이를 잡은 채 말했습니다. \"내려오면 여기서 아가씨를 뵙는 영광을 누릴 수 있을까요?\" 저는 네, 여기 있겠다고 답했고, 그는 인사를 꾸벅하고 한 번 더 저를 보고는 나갔습니다.\n\n저는 그가 그저 서투르고 수줍은 사람이라고만 여겼습니다. 무척 당황한 게 분명했으니까요. 그래서 제가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은 그에게 필요한 것이 다 갖춰졌는지 확인할 때까지 기다렸다가 혼자 두는 것이라고 생각했습니다. 점심은 곧 차려졌지만, 한동안 탁자 위에 그대로 놓여 있었습니다. 보이손 씨와의 면담은 길었고, 또 제 짐작엔 험악하기까지 했던 모양입니다. 그 방이 좀 떨어져 있는데도 이따금 거센 바람처럼 치솟는 그의 우렁찬 목소리가 들렸고, 분명 비난의 일제포격을 완벽하게 퍼붓고 있었으니까요.\n\n마침내 거피 씨가 그 면담으로 다소 시달린 기색으로 돌아왔습니다. \"이런, 아가씨.\" 그가 나직이 말했습니다. \"그분 정말 무서운 양반이네요!\"\n\n\"좀 드시지요, 선생님.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n거피 씨는 탁자에 앉아 고기 써는 칼을 고기 포크에 대고 초조하게 갈기 시작하면서도, (제가 그를 보지 않고도 똑똑히 느꼈듯이) 여전히 그 예사롭지 않은 눈초리로 저를 바라보았습니다. 칼 가는 일이 어찌나 오래 이어지던지, 마침내 저는 멈추지 못하고 끌려가는 듯한 그의 그 주문을 깨주어야 할 것 같은 일종의 의무감에 눈을 들었습니다.\n\n그러자 그는 곧장 접시로 눈을 돌리고 고기를 썰기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"아가씨는 뭘 좀 드시겠어요? 한 점이라도 드시지요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"정말 아무것도 한 점 안 드릴까요, 아가씨?\" 거피 씨가 포도주 한 잔을 황급히 들이켜며 말했습니다.\n\n\"아무것도요, 고맙습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"저는 그저 선생님께 필요한 것이 다 갖춰졌는지 확인하려고 기다린 것뿐입니다. 제가 시켜드릴 것이라도 있을까요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 정말 감사합니다, 아가씨. 편히 지내는 데 필요한 건 다 있습니다—적어도 저는—편하진 않습니다—저는 한 번도 편한 적이 없으니까요.\" 그는 포도주를 잇따라 두 잔 더 들이켰습니다.\n\n저는 자리를 뜨는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 아가씨!\" 제가 일어서는 걸 보고 거피 씨가 따라 일어서며 말했습니다. \"그런데 잠깐 단둘이 말씀 나누는 호의를 베풀어주실 수 있을까요?\"\n\n뭐라 해야 할지 몰라 저는 다시 앉았습니다.\n\n\"지금부터 드릴 말씀은 불리하게 작용하지 않는 거지요, 아가씨?\" 거피 씨가 의자를 제 탁자 쪽으로 끌어오며 조마조마하게 말했습니다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\" 의아해하며 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"저희 쪽 법률 용어 가운데 하나입니다, 아가씨. 켄지 카보이에서든 다른 어디서든 이 말을 제게 불리하게 쓰지 않으신다는 거지요. 우리 대화가 아무것도 낳지 못하더라도, 저는 본래대로 남고, 제 처지나 세상 앞날에 불리함을 입지 않는다는 겁니다. 한마디로, 완전한 비밀이라는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"저는 도무지 헤아리기 어렵습니다, 선생님.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"딱 한 번 뵈었을 뿐인 저에게 무슨 완전한 비밀을 털어놓으실 게 있는지 말입니다. 다만 선생님께 어떤 해라도 끼치게 된다면 무척 안타까울 것입니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아가씨. 그러시리라 믿습니다—그것으로 충분합니다.\" 이러는 내내 거피 씨는 손수건으로 이마를 문지르거나, 오른 손바닥으로 왼 손바닥을 꽉꽉 비벼대고 있었습니다. \"포도주를 한 잔 더 드는 걸 너그러이 봐주신다면, 아가씨, 그게 서로에게 불쾌할 수밖에 없는 그 끊임없는 목멤 없이 말을 이어가는 데 도움이 될 것 같습니다.\"\n\n그는 그렇게 하고는 다시 돌아왔습니다. 저는 그 틈을 타 탁자 뒤로 한참 물러섰습니다.\n\n\"아가씨는 한 잔 권해도 안 받으시겠지요?\" 거피 씨가 한결 기운을 차린 듯 말했습니다.\n\n\"안 받겠습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"반 잔도요?\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"사분의 일은요? 안 되겠군요! 그러면, 본론으로 들어가겠습니다. 켄지 카보이에서 제가 지금 받는 봉급은, 서머슨 양, 주급 이 파운드입니다. 처음 아가씨를 뵙는 행복을 누렸을 때는 일 파운드 십오 실링이었고, 그 액수에 오래도록 머물러 있었습니다. 그 뒤로 오 실링이 올랐고, 지금부터 열두 달을 넘지 않는 기한이 끝날 때 다시 오 실링 인상이 보장되어 있습니다. 제 어머니는 자그마한 재산이 있는데, 소액 종신 연금의 형태여서, 그것으로 올드 스트리트 로드에서 검소하나마 자립해 사십니다. 어머니는 시어머니 자리에 더없이 어울리는 분입니다. 결코 참견하지 않으시고, 매사 평화를 좋아하시며, 성품이 무던하십니다. 흠이야 있지요—흠 없는 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?—하지만 손님이 계실 땐 그러시는 걸 한 번도 본 적이 없으니, 그럴 땐 포도주든 독주든 맥주든 마음 놓고 맡기셔도 됩니다. 저 자신의 거처는 펜턴빌의 펜턴 플레이스에 있는 셋방입니다. 누추하나 바람이 잘 통하고, 뒤가 트여 있어, 가장 '건강한' 외곽으로 꼽힙니다. 서머슨 양! 더없이 점잖은 말로 표현하자면, 저는 아가씨를 흠모합니다. 부디 제가 (말하자면) 선언장을 제출하는—청혼을 올리는 호의를 허락해주시겠습니까!\"\n\n거피 씨가 무릎을 꿇었습니다. 저는 탁자 뒤로 한참 물러나 있던 터라 그리 겁나지는 않았습니다. 저는 말했습니다. \"그 우스꽝스러운 자세에서 당장 일어나세요, 선생님. 안 그러면 제가 한 암묵적인 약속을 깨고 종을 울릴 수밖에 없습니다!\"\n\n\"끝까지 들어주십시오, 아가씨!\" 거피 씨가 두 손을 모으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"한마디도 더 들을 수 없습니다, 선생님.\" 제가 받았습니다. \"조금이라도 분별이 있으시다면 마땅히 그러셔야 하듯, 당장 양탄자에서 일어나 탁자로 가 앉지 않으시는 한 말입니다.\"\n\n그는 가엾은 표정을 지으면서도 천천히 일어나 그렇게 했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 이 무슨 조롱입니까, 아가씨.\" 그가 가슴에 손을 얹고 쟁반 너머로 우울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했습니다. \"이런 순간에 음식 뒤에 세워지다니요. 이런 순간에 영혼은 음식을 물리치는 법입니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"부디 끝맺어주십시오.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"끝까지 들어달라 하셨으니, 부디 끝맺어주십시오.\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다, 아가씨.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"제가 사랑하고 공경하듯, 또한 그렇게 따르겠습니다. 제단 앞에서 그대를 그 서약의 대상으로 삼을 수만 있다면!\"\n\n\"그건 전혀 불가능합니다.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"전적으로 말도 안 되는 일이고요.\"\n\n\"저도 압니다.\" 거피 씨가 쟁반 위로 몸을 기울이며, 비록 제 눈은 그에게 향하지 않았으나 또다시 묘하게 느껴지듯, 아까 그 골똘한 눈초리로 저를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"세상 잣대로 보자면, 겉으로 드러나는 모든 정황으로 보자면, 제 청혼이 보잘것없다는 걸 압니다. 하지만, 서머슨 양! 천사여! 아니, 종은 울리지 마십시오—저는 모진 곳에서 자라 온갖 잡다한 실무에 익숙합니다. 젊은 사람이지만, 증거를 캐내고, 사건을 꾸미고, 세상사를 숱하게 보아왔습니다. 그대의 손을 얻는 복을 누린다면, 그대의 이익을 키우고 그대의 운수를 밀어주는 데 무슨 수단인들 못 찾겠습니까! 그대와 깊이 관련된 무슨 일인들 알아내지 못하겠습니까! 지금 당장은 분명 아무것도 모릅니다. 하지만 그대의 믿음을 얻고 그대가 저를 부추겨주신다면, 무슨 일인들 못 알아내겠습니까?\"\n\n저는 그가 제 마음에 호소한 것 못지않게 제 이익이라는 것에—그가 제 이익이라 여기는 것에—호소한 일도 전혀 성공하지 못했다고, 그러니 괜찮으시다면 당장 떠나주십사 청한다는 걸 이제 알아들으셨으면 한다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"매정한 아가씨.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"다만 한마디만 더 들어주십시오! 제가 '하얀 말 셀러'에서 시중들던 날 그 매력에 마음을 빼앗긴 걸 분명 보셨을 줄 압니다. 제가 삯마차 발판을 접어 올릴 때 그 매력에 경의를 표하지 않을 수 없었던 걸 분명 알아채셨을 줄 압니다. 그대에게 바친 변변찮은 경의였지만, 진심에서 우러난 것이었습니다. 그날 이후로 그대의 모습이 줄곧 제 가슴에 박혀 있습니다. 저녁이면 젤리비 댁 맞은편을 오르내리며, 한때 그대를 품었던 벽돌이나마 바라보았습니다. 오늘 이 나들이는, 핑계로 내세운 업무로 따지면 전혀 필요 없는 나들이였으나, 오로지 그대만을 위해 저 혼자 꾸민 것이었습니다. 제가 이익을 말한 것도, 그저 저 자신과 저의 공손한 비참함을 천거하려는 것일 따름입니다. 사랑이 그보다 앞섰고, 지금도 앞섭니다.\"\n\n\"저는 마음이 아플 것입니다, 거피 씨.\" 제가 일어나 종 줄에 손을 얹으며 말했습니다. \"선생님께든, 진심을 다하는 누구에게든, 아무리 거북하게 표현된 것일지라도 그 정직한 마음을 가벼이 여겨 부당하게 대하는 일은 말입니다. 비록 때와 자리가 어긋났을지언정 정말로 저에게 호의의 증표를 보이려 하신 거라면, 저는 마땅히 감사를 드려야 한다고 느낍니다. 저는 우쭐할 까닭이 거의 없으며, 우쭐하지도 않습니다. 바라건대,\" 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 이렇게 덧붙인 듯합니다. \"이제 마치 이토록 어리석은 짓은 한 적도 없는 양 떠나시어, 켄지 카보이의 업무에 마음 쓰셨으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 아가씨!\" 제가 막 종을 울리려는데 거피 씨가 저를 멈춰 세우며 외쳤습니다. \"이건 불리하게 작용하지 않는 거지요?\"\n\n\"결코 입에 올리지 않겠습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"선생님께서 앞으로 제게 그럴 빌미를 주지 않으시는 한 말입니다.\"\n\n\"십오 초만요, 아가씨! 혹 언젠가, 아무리 먼 훗날이라도—그건 상관없습니다, 제 마음은 결코 변할 리 없으니까요—제가 드린 어떤 말씀에 대해, 특히 제가 무슨 일인들 못 하겠느냐던 그 대목에 대해 마음을 고쳐 잡으시거든, 펜턴 플레이스 팔십칠번지 윌리엄 거피, 혹 이사를 갔거나, (꺾인 희망이니 뭐니 그런 까닭으로) 죽었거든 올드 스트리트 로드 삼백이번지 거피 부인 앞으로, 그것이면 충분합니다.\"\n\n저는 종을 울렸고, 하인이 들어왔으며, 거피 씨는 자기 명함을 탁자에 내려놓고 풀 죽은 인사를 꾸벅하고는 떠났습니다. 그가 나갈 때 눈을 들어보니, 문을 지난 뒤에도 다시 한번 저를 바라보고 있었습니다.\n\n저는 그 자리에 한 시간 남짓 더 앉아, 장부와 지불을 마무리하고 일을 잔뜩 해치웠습니다. 그러고는 책상을 정리하고, 모든 걸 치워두었으며, 어찌나 마음이 가라앉고 명랑해졌던지 이 뜻밖의 일을 완전히 떨쳐버린 줄 알았습니다. 하지만 제 방으로 올라가서는, 그 일을 두고 웃기 시작하는 저 자신에 깜짝 놀랐고, 이어 그 일을 두고 울기 시작하는 저 자신에 더욱 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 한마디로, 저는 잠시 마음이 술렁였고, 오래전 정원에 묻힌 그 사랑스러운 낡은 인형 시절 이후로 한 번도 건드려진 적 없던 옛 가락의 줄이, 그때보다 더 거칠게 튕겨진 듯한 기분이 들었습니다.\n\n챈서리 레인 동쪽 변두리, 더 정확히 말하면 커시터 거리의 쿡스 코트에서, 법률 문구상 스내그스비 씨가 합법적인 생업에 종사한다. 대체로 그늘진 곳인 쿡스 코트의 그늘 아래에서, 스내그스비 씨는 온갖 종류의 법률 절차용 빈 서식이며, 양피지 가죽과 두루마리며, 종이—대판지, 약식 서류용지, 초안용지, 갈색지, 백색지, 누르스름한 백색지, 압지—며, 인지며, 사무용 깃펜, 펜, 잉크, 지우개, 가루 분말, 핀, 연필, 봉랍, 봉함지며, 빨간 끈과 초록 끈이며, 수첩, 달력, 일기장, 법률 인명록이며, 노끈 상자, 자, 잉크병—유리와 납으로 된 것—펜나이프, 가위, 송곳, 그 밖의 자잘한 사무용 칼붙이며, 한마디로 일일이 열거하기엔 너무 많은 물품을 다뤄왔으니, 그가 도제 생활을 마치고 페퍼와 동업에 들어선 이래로 줄곧 그래왔다. 그 무렵 쿡스 코트는 갓 칠한 새 간판, 곧 오래되어 알아보기도 어려운 ‘페퍼’라는 글자만 적힌 옛 명패를 밀어내고 들어선 ‘페퍼 앤드 스내그스비’ 덕분에 어떤 의미에서 혁명을 맞은 셈이다. 런던의 담쟁이덩굴이라 할 매연이 페퍼의 이름을 칭칭 휘감고 그의 거처에 들러붙은 나머지, 그 정 깊은 기생식물이 모체인 나무를 완전히 짓눌러버렸기 때문이다.\n\n페퍼는 이제 쿡스 코트에서 보이지 않는다. 그가 이곳에 나타나리라 기대하는 사람도 없으니, 그는 지난 사반세기 동안 홀본의 세인트 앤드루스 묘지에 누워 있고, 짐마차와 삯마차가 하루 종일, 또 밤의 절반 동안 한 마리 거대한 용처럼 그의 곁을 우르릉거리며 지나다닌다. 만약 그 용이 잠잠해진 틈에 그가 슬그머니 빠져나와 다시 쿡스 코트에서 바람을 쐰다면—커시터 거리의 작은 우유 가게 지하실에서 핏빛처럼 붉은 수탉이 울어 그만 돌아가라고 일러줄 때까지 말이다. 그 수탉이 낮이란 걸 어떻게 알고 있는지 알아내는 일도 흥미로울 텐데, 제 눈으로 직접 본 것이라곤 거의 없는 처지이니—만약 페퍼가 쿡스 코트의 희미한 빛 줄기를 정말로 다시 찾는다면, 이 바닥의 어느 법률 문구상도 단호히 부정하지 못할 일이거니와, 그는 보이지 않게 찾아오므로 누구도 더 손해 볼 일이 없고 더 알게 될 일도 없다.\n\n페퍼가 살아 있을 적, 또한 스내그스비가 칠 년이라는 긴 도제 ‘기간’을 보내던 시절, 같은 법률 문구상 건물에서 페퍼와 함께 조카딸 하나가 살았다—키 작고 약삭빠른 조카딸로, 허리는 좀 지나치다 싶을 만큼 졸라매여 있었고, 가을 저녁처럼 뾰족한 코는 끝으로 갈수록 서리가 내릴 듯 쌀쌀맞았다. 쿡스 코트 주민들 사이에는 소문 하나가 떠돌았는데, 이 조카딸의 어머니가 딸이 어릴 적, 딸의 몸매가 완벽에 가까워지기를 너무도 질투심 어린 정성으로 바란 나머지, 매일 아침 더 단단히 힘을 주려고 침대 기둥에 어머니의 발을 받친 채 딸을 동여맸다는 것이며, 게다가 식초와 레몬즙을 몇 파인트씩 속으로 들이부었는데, 그 산성이 환자의 코와 성미로 치솟아 올랐다는 것이다. 소문이라는 여러 혀 가운데 어느 것에서 이 거품 같은 이야기가 비롯되었든, 그것은 젊은 스내그스비의 귀에 끝내 닿지 않았거나 닿았어도 아무 영향을 미치지 못했으니, 그는 성년에 이르자마자 그 어여쁜 주인공에게 구애해 손에 넣었고, 단번에 두 가지 동업 관계에 발을 들였다. 그리하여 이제 커시터 거리의 쿡스 코트에서는, 스내그스비 씨와 그 조카딸이 한 몸이다. 조카딸은 여전히 제 몸매를 애지중지하는데, 취향이야 제각각이겠으나, 그 몸매가 워낙 얼마 되지도 않는다는 점에서만큼은 의심할 여지 없이 귀하다.\n\n스내그스비 부부는 한 뼈에 한 살일 뿐 아니라, 이웃들이 보기엔 한 목소리이기도 하다. 오직 스내그스비 부인에게서만 흘러나오는 듯한 그 목소리는 쿡스 코트에서 아주 자주 들린다. 스내그스비 씨는 그 감미로운 가락을 통하지 않고서는 좀처럼 목소리를 내는 일이 없다. 그는 온순하고 머리가 벗어진, 소심한 남자로, 번들거리는 머리통 뒤쪽에 짧고 뻣뻣한 검은 머리카락 한 줌이 삐죽 솟아 있다. 그는 유순함과 비만으로 기우는 편이다. 회색 가게용 외투에 검은 옥양목 토시를 끼고 쿡스 코트의 문간에 서서 구름을 올려다보거나, 어두운 가게 안쪽 책상 뒤에 서서 묵직한 평자를 들고 두 도제와 함께 양피지를 자르고 베어낼 때면, 그는 두드러지게 내성적이고 겸손한 사람이다. 그럴 때면 그의 발밑에서, 마치 무덤 속에서 안식하지 못하는 날카로운 유령에게서 나오듯, 앞서 말한 그 목소리로 푸념과 탄식이 자주 솟아오른다. 그러다 어쩌다 그 소리가 평소보다 한층 날카로운 음으로 치달을 때면, 스내그스비 씨는 도제들에게 이렇게 말한다. “우리 마누라가 거스터를 닦달하는 모양이군!”\n\n스내그스비 씨가 이렇게 쓰는 이 고유명사는, 일찍부터 쿡스 코트 주민들의 재치를 자극해, 그것이야말로 스내그스비 부인의 이름이어야 마땅하다는 말을 끌어내곤 했다. 부인의 폭풍 같은 성격에 빗대면 ‘거스터’라 불러도 더없이 힘차고 표현력 있게 들어맞으리라는 것이다. 그러나 그것은, 연봉 오십 실링과 옷가지가 어중간하게 채워진 아주 작은 상자 하나를 빼고는, 구빈원 출신의 비쩍 마른 젊은 여자가 가진 유일한 소유물이자 이름이다. (어떤 이들은 그 여자가 오거스타라는 이름으로 세례를 받았으리라 짐작한다.) 그 여자는 자라는 동안 투팅에 사는, 동족을 사랑하는 어느 자애로운 자선가에게 위탁되거나 계약되어 길러졌으니, 더없이 좋은 환경에서 자라났음에 틀림없을 텐데도, “발작을 일으키고”, 그 까닭은 교구로서도 도무지 설명할 길이 없다.\n\n거스터는 실제 나이가 스물서넛쯤이지만 족히 열 살은 더 들어 보이는데, 그 영문 모를 발작이라는 흠 때문에 헐값에 부려진다. 게다가 제 수호성인의 손에 도로 떠넘겨질까 너무도 겁을 내는 통에, 발작이 일어난 순간 마침 곁에 있던 물통이나 개수대, 구리솥, 저녁 음식, 그 밖의 무언가에 머리를 처박은 채 발견될 때를 빼고는, 늘 일하는 중이다. 그 여자는 도제들의 부모와 보호자들에게는 만족스러운 존재이니, 그 가슴에 풋풋한 연정을 불러일으킬 위험이 거의 없다고들 여기기 때문이다. 또 늘 트집 잡을 거리를 찾을 수 있는 스내그스비 부인에게도 만족스러운 존재이고, 그 여자를 거두는 것이 자선이라고 여기는 스내그스비 씨에게도 만족스러운 존재다. 거스터의 눈에 이 법률 문구상의 가게는 풍요와 화려함의 전당이다. 그 여자는 위층의 작은 응접실—말하자면 늘 머리에 종이를 말고 앞치마를 두른 듯 단정히 지켜지는 그 방이 기독교 세계에서 가장 우아한 방이라 믿는다. 한쪽 끝으로는 쿡스 코트가 (커시터 거리를 곁눈질하는 것은 말할 것도 없고) 내다보이고, 다른 쪽 끝으로는 집달리 코아빈스의 뒷마당이 내다보이는 그 전망을, 그 여자는 견줄 데 없는 아름다움의 풍경이라 여긴다. 그 방에 걸린 유화 초상화들—그것도 잔뜩 걸려 있다—곧 스내그스비 부인을 바라보는 스내그스비 씨의 초상과, 스내그스비 씨를 바라보는 스내그스비 부인의 초상은, 그 여자의 눈에는 라파엘이나 티치아노의 걸작이나 다름없다. 거스터는 그 숱한 결핍에 대해 나름의 보상을 누리는 셈이다.\n\n스내그스비 씨는 가게 운영의 실무적 비법에 속하지 않는 모든 일을 스내그스비 부인에게 맡긴다. 부인은 돈을 관리하고, 세금 징수원을 나무라고, 일요일의 예배 시간과 장소를 정하고, 스내그스비 씨의 여흥을 허가하며, 저녁 식탁에 무엇을 올릴지 자기 마음대로 정하는 일에 대해서는 아무런 책임도 인정하지 않는다. 그리하여 부인은 챈서리 레인 양편으로 멀리까지, 심지어 홀본 바깥에 이르는 동네 아낙들 사이에서 비교의 드높은 기준이 되어, 집안싸움이 벌어질 때면 아낙들은 으레 남편들에게 자기들(아낙들)의 처지와 스내그스비 부인의 처지를 견주어보라, 또 자기들(남편들)의 행실과 스내그스비 씨의 행실이 얼마나 다른지 보라고 들이댄다. 늘 박쥐처럼 쿡스 코트를 누비며 누구네 창이든 들락날락 스치고 다니는 소문은 이렇게 말한다. 스내그스비 부인은 질투가 심하고 캐묻기 좋아하며, 스내그스비 씨는 때때로 제 집에서조차 시달려 쫓겨날 지경인데, 만약 그에게 쥐만 한 기개라도 있다면 그런 꼴을 참지 않으리라고. 심지어 제 고집 센 남편들에게 그를 빛나는 본보기랍시고 들먹이는 아낙들이 실은 그를 깔본다는 말도 있으니, 그 누구보다 거만하게 그러는 이는 어느 부인 하나로, 그 부인의 남편으로 말할 것 같으면 우산을 교정 도구로 삼아 아내에게 휘두른다는 의심을 단단히 받고 있다. 그러나 이런 어렴풋한 수군거림은 스내그스비 씨가 나름대로 사색을 즐기고 시심이 깊은 사람이라는 데서 비롯된 것인지도 모른다. 그는 여름철이면 스테이플 인을 거닐며 참새와 나뭇잎이 어쩌면 그리도 시골스러운지 살피기를 좋아하고, 일요일 오후면 롤스 야드를 어슬렁거리며 (기분이 좋을 때면) 옛 시절도 있었지 하면서, 저 예배당 밑을 파보면 분명 돌 관 한두 개쯤은 나올 거라고 장담하기를 좋아한다. 또 그는 세상을 떠난 그 많은 대법원장과 부원장과 기록보관장관을 떠올리며 상상에 위안을 얻는다. 그리고 두 도제에게, 턴스타일이 진짜 회전문이던 시절, “수정처럼 맑은” 시냇물이 홀본 한복판을 곧장 흘러 풀밭으로 곧바로 빠져나갔다더라는 이야기를 들려주며 어찌나 시골 정취를 듬뿍 우려내는지—그렇게 시골 정취를 듬뿍 우려내는 통에, 정작 그곳에 갈 마음은 영영 들지 않는다.\n\n날이 저물어가고 가스등이 켜졌지만, 아직 완전히 어둡지는 않은 터라 그 빛이 온전히 위력을 발휘하지는 못한다. 가게 문간에 서서 구름을 올려다보던 스내그스비 씨는, 늦도록 나돌던 까마귀 한 마리가 쿡스 코트 몫의 하늘 조각 위를 서쪽으로 스쳐 가는 것을 본다. 까마귀는 챈서리 레인과 링컨스 인 가든을 곧장 가로질러 링컨스 인 필즈로 날아든다.\n\n이곳, 한때 권세가의 저택이던 어느 큰 집에 토킹혼 씨가 산다. 지금은 여러 채의 셋방으로 쪼개져, 그 위세가 쪼그라든 조각들 속에서 변호사들이 호두 속 구더기처럼 들어앉아 있다. 그러나 그 널찍한 계단과 복도와 대기실은 여전히 남아 있고, 채색된 천장마저 그대로다. 그 천장에서는 로마식 투구를 쓰고 천상의 아마포를 두른 우의(寓意)가 난간과 기둥과 꽃과 구름과 다리 굵은 소년들 사이에 큰대자로 뻗어, 보는 이의 머리를 지끈거리게 한다—언제나 머리를 아프게 하는 것이야말로 우의의 목적인 듯싶다, 어느 정도는 말이다. 이곳, 거창한 이름이 적힌 그 숱한 상자들 틈에 토킹혼 씨가 사는데, 세상의 권세가들이 따분해 죽을 지경에 빠져 있는 시골 저택에서 말없이 머무르지 않을 때면 그렇다. 오늘도 그는 이곳에서 책상 앞에 조용히 앉아 있다. 누구도 까지 못하는 구식 굴 한 마리다.\n\n생김새가 그러하듯, 오늘 오후의 땅거미 속 그의 방도 그러하다. 녹슬고, 시대에 뒤떨어졌고, 시선에서 물러나 있으나, 그럴 만한 여유가 있다. 묵직하고 등판 넓은 구식의, 들기조차 쉽지 않은 마호가니와 말총 의자들이며, 가락처럼 가느다란 다리에 먼지 앉은 모직 천이 덮인 한물간 탁자들이며, 지난 세대나 그 전 세대의 거창한 작위를 지녔던 이들이 증정한 판화들이 그를 에워싼다. 두껍고 칙칙한 터키 양탄자가 그가 앉은 바닥의 소리를 잠재우고, 구식 은 촛대에 꽂힌 양초 두 자루가 그의 큰 방에 아주 모자란 빛을 던진다. 책등에 적힌 제목들은 제본 속으로 물러나 숨었고, 자물쇠를 채울 수 있는 것은 죄다 자물쇠가 채워져 있으며, 열쇠는 하나도 보이지 않는다. 낱장 서류는 거의 흩어져 있지 않다. 그의 곁에는 필사본이 하나 있지만, 그는 그것을 들여다보지 않는다. 잉크병의 둥근 뚜껑과 봉랍 부스러기 두 조각으로, 그는 제 마음속에 자리한 망설임의 갈피를 잡으려 말없이 천천히 궁리해본다. 이제 잉크병 뚜껑이 한가운데에, 이제 빨간 봉랍 조각이, 이제 검은 조각이. 그게 아니다. 토킹혼 씨는 그것들을 죄다 그러모아 다시 시작해야 한다.\n\n이곳, 채색된 천장 아래, 짧게 줄어든 우의가 마치 그에게 덮쳐들 작정인 듯 그의 침입을 내려다보고, 그는 그것을 본 척도 않은 채, 토킹혼 씨는 집과 사무실을 한데 두고 있다. 그는 직원을 두지 않고, 다만 중년 남자 하나를 둘 뿐인데, 대개 팔꿈치가 좀 해진 차림으로 현관의 높은 칸막이 자리에 앉아 있고, 좀처럼 일에 치이는 법이 없다. 토킹혼 씨는 흔한 방식의 사람이 아니다. 그에게 서기 따위는 필요 없다. 그는 비밀이 고인 거대한 저수지이니, 그렇게 함부로 길어낼 수 없다. 그의 의뢰인들은 바로 그를 원한다. 그가 전부인 것이다. 그가 작성을 원하는 초안은 신비로운 지시에 따라 템플의 특별 변론가들이 작성하고, 그가 원하는 정서본은 비용을 따지지 않고 문구상에서 만들어진다. 칸막이 자리의 그 중년 남자는 홀본의 어느 길거리 청소부보다도 귀족 사회의 일에 대해 거의 더 아는 바가 없다.\n\n빨간 조각, 검은 조각, 잉크병 뚜껑, 다른 잉크병 뚜껑, 작은 모래통. 자! 너는 가운데로, 너는 오른쪽으로, 너는 왼쪽으로. 이 망설임의 갈피는 이제 풀어내든지 영영 못 풀든지 해야 한다. 지금이다! 토킹혼 씨가 일어나 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 모자를 쓰고 필사본을 주머니에 넣은 다음 밖으로 나가며, 팔꿈치 해진 중년 남자에게 “곧 돌아오겠네”라고 이른다. 이보다 더 분명한 말을 그에게 건네는 일은 좀처럼 없다.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 까마귀가 왔던 길을 따라—그만큼 곧장은 아니지만 거의 비슷하게—커시터 거리의 쿡스 코트로 간다. 스내그스비 법률 문구상으로, 곧 ‘증서 정서 및 복사, 모든 분야의 법률 필사 시행, 기타 등등, 기타 등등, 기타 등등’이라 적힌 곳으로.\n\n지금은 오후 다섯 시나 여섯 시쯤이고, 따뜻한 차의 향긋한 내음이 쿡스 코트에 감돈다. 그 내음은 스내그스비의 문간을 맴돈다. 이곳의 시간표는 이른 편이라, 점심은 한 시 반, 저녁은 아홉 시 반이다. 스내그스비 씨는 차를 마시려고 지하 영역으로 내려가려던 참이었는데, 방금 문밖을 내다보다가 늦도록 나돌던 그 까마귀를 본 것이다.\n\n“주인장 계신가?”\n\n가게는 거스터가 지키고 있는데, 도제들이 스내그스비 부부와 함께 부엌에서 차를 마시는 까닭이다. 따라서 맞은편 집 이층 두 창문에서 두 거울에 비추어 곱슬머리를 빗는 의상 제작자의 두 딸은, 저희가 흐뭇이 짐작하는 대로 두 도제를 애간장 녹게 하는 것이 아니라, 그저 거스터의 보람 없는 동경을 일깨우고 있을 뿐이다. 거스터의 머리카락은 자라지 않으며, 여태 한 번도 자란 적이 없고, 앞으로도 영영 자라지 않으리라고 다들 굳게 믿는다.\n\n“주인장 계신가?” 토킹혼 씨가 말한다.\n\n주인장은 안에 있고, 거스터가 그를 부르러 간다. 거스터는 가게에서 벗어나게 된 것이 반가워 사라진다. 그 여자는 가게를, 법이라는 거대한 고문에 쓰일 무시무시한 도구들이 가득한 창고로, 두려움과 외경이 뒤섞인 마음으로 바라보니, 가스 불을 끈 뒤에는 결코 들어가서는 안 될 곳이다.\n\n스내그스비 씨가 번들거리고 따뜻하고 풀 냄새 풍기는 차림으로 음식을 씹으며 나타난다. 버터 바른 빵 한 조각을 꿀꺽 삼킨다. 이렇게 말한다. “아이고, 이런! 토킹혼 씨 아니십니까!”\n\n“자네에게 잠깐 한마디 하려고 왔네, 스내그스비.”\n\n“여부가 있겠습니까, 선생님! 아이고 이런, 선생님, 어찌 사람을 시켜 저를 부르지 않으셨습니까? 부디 안쪽 가게로 드시지요.” 스내그스비는 순식간에 환해진다.\n\n비좁은 그 방은 양피지 기름 냄새가 짙게 배어, 창고이자 회계실이자 필사실이다. 토킹혼 씨가 몸을 돌려 책상 앞 걸상에 앉는다.\n\n“잔다이스 대 잔다이스 건일세, 스내그스비.”\n\n“예, 선생님.” 스내그스비 씨가 가스 불을 돋우고 손으로 입을 가린 채 기침을 하며, 이문이 생길 것을 점잖게 미리 기대한다. 소심한 사람인 스내그스비 씨는 갖가지 뜻을 담아 기침함으로써 말을 아끼는 데 익숙하다.\n\n“요전에 그 사건의 진술서 몇 장을 자네가 베껴주었지.”\n\n“예, 선생님, 그렇게 했지요.”\n\n“그중 하나가 말일세,” 토킹혼 씨가 무심히—굳게 닫혀 열리지 않는 구식 굴이여!—엉뚱한 외투 주머니를 더듬으며 말한다. “필체가 독특해서 내 마음에 제법 들었거든. 마침 지나는 길이고, 가지고 있는 줄 알아서, 들러 자네에게 물어보려던 참인데—없군. 상관없네, 다음에 아무 때나 하지. 아! 여기 있군! 이걸 누가 베꼈는지 물어보려고 들렀네.”\n\n“이걸 누가 베꼈느냐고요, 선생님?” 스내그스비 씨가 그것을 받아 책상 위에 평평히 펼쳐놓고, 법률 문구상 특유의 왼손 손놀림으로 휙 비틀어 단번에 모든 장을 갈라놓으며 말한다. “이건 저희가 하청을 주었습니다, 선생님. 그때 마침 일감을 제법 많이 내보내던 참이었지요. 제 장부를 들춰보면 누가 베꼈는지 금세 말씀드릴 수 있습니다, 선생님.”\n\n스내그스비 씨가 금고에서 장부를 꺼내 내리고, 어딘가에 걸린 듯하던 버터 바른 빵 조각을 다시 한번 꿀꺽 삼키더니, 진술서를 곁눈질하면서 오른쪽 집게손가락을 장부의 한 면을 따라 죽 미끄러뜨린다. “주비—패커—잔다이스.”\n\n“잔다이스! 여기 있군요, 선생님,” 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. “그렇고말고요! 진작 떠올렸어야 했는데. 이건 바로 길 건너편에 하숙하는 필경사에게 맡긴 것입니다, 선생님.”\n\n토킹혼 씨는 이미 그 기재 항목을 보았으니, 법률 문구상보다 먼저 찾아냈고, 그 집게손가락이 비탈을 내려오는 동안 다 읽어버렸다.\n\n“그자를 뭐라고 부르나? 네모?” 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. “네모입니다, 선생님. 여기 있네요. 사절판 마흔두 장. 수요일 밤 여덟 시에 내주어, 목요일 아침 아홉 시 반에 들여왔습니다.”\n\n“네모!” 토킹혼 씨가 되뇐다. “네모는 라틴어로 ‘아무도 아닌 사람’이라는 뜻이지.”\n\n“제 생각엔 그게 영어로는 ‘어떤 사람’이라는 뜻이지 싶습니다, 선생님,” 스내그스비 씨가 공손한 기침과 함께 의견을 내놓는다. “사람 이름이니까요. 보십시오, 여기 있지 않습니까, 선생님! 사절판 마흔두 장. 수요일 밤 여덟 시에 내주고, 목요일 아침 아홉 시 반에 들여왔습니다.”\n\n스내그스비 씨의 곁눈에, 그가 어쩌자고 차를 내팽개쳤는지 알아보려고 가게 문간을 들여다보는 스내그스비 부인의 머리가 들어온다. 스내그스비 씨가 스내그스비 부인을 향해 설명조의 기침을 건네는데, 마치 “여보, 손님이라오!”라고 말하는 듯하다.\n\n“아홉 시 반입니다, 선생님,” 스내그스비 씨가 되뇐다. “삯일로 먹고사는 저희 법률 필경사들은 묘한 족속이라, 이게 그자의 본명이 아닐지도 모르지만, 어쨌든 그가 행세하는 이름입니다. 이제 생각났는데요, 선생님, 그자가 규칙 사무소며 왕좌 재판소 사무소며 판사 집무실이며 그런 데에 손수 써서 내붙이는 광고에 그 이름을 적습니다. 어떤 종류의 문서인지 아시지요, 선생님—일감을 구한다는 것 말입니다.”\n\n토킹혼 씨가 집달리 코아빈스의 뒤편 작은 창을 통해 흘끔 내다보니, 코아빈스의 창문에 불빛이 비친다. 코아빈스의 커피실은 뒤쪽에 있는데, 어두컴컴한 처지의 신사 여럿이 드리운 그림자가 차양막 위로 어렴풋이 어른거린다. 스내그스비 씨는 그 틈을 타 살짝 고개를 돌려 어깨 너머로 제 마누라를 흘끗 보고는, 입을 놀려 이런 뜻으로 변명하는 시늉을 한다. “토킹—혼—부자—세도가!”\n\n“이 사람에게 전에도 일을 준 적이 있나?” 토킹혼 씨가 묻는다.\n\n“아이고, 그럼요, 선생님! 선생님 일감이었지요.”\n\n“더 중요한 일을 생각하느라, 어디 산다고 했는지 잊었네만?”\n\n“길 건너편입니다, 선생님. 실은 그자가 하숙하는 데가—” 스내그스비 씨가 버터 바른 빵 조각이 도무지 넘어가지 않는다는 듯 또 한 번 꿀꺽 삼킨다 “—넝마와 병을 다루는 가게입니다.”\n\n“돌아가는 길에 그곳을 보여줄 수 있겠나?”\n\n“더없는 기쁨이지요, 선생님!”\n\n스내그스비 씨가 토시를 벗고 회색 외투를 벗더니, 검은 외투를 걸치고 못에서 모자를 집어 든다. “아! 여기 우리 마누라가 있군요!” 그가 큰 소리로 말한다. “여보, 내가 토킹혼 씨와 함께 잠깐 길 건너에 다녀오는 동안 누구 한 녀석더러 가게를 봐달라고 일러주겠소? 제 아내입니다, 선생님—이 분도 안 걸려요, 여보!”\n\n스내그스비 부인이 변호사에게 허리를 굽히고는 계산대 뒤로 물러나, 차양막 너머로 두 사람을 엿보다가, 살그머니 안쪽 사무실로 들어가, 아직 펼쳐진 채 놓인 장부의 기재 항목을 들여다본다. 호기심이 일었음이 분명하다.\n\n“그곳이 거칠다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다, 선생님,” 스내그스비 씨가 좁은 인도를 변호사에게 양보하고 자기는 공손히 차도로 걸으며 말한다. “그 작자도 아주 거칠고요. 하기야 대개가 거친 족속들입니다, 선생님. 이 사람의 좋은 점이라면, 도통 잠을 자려 들지 않는다는 겁니다. 원하시면 끝장을 볼 때까지, 원하시는 만큼 얼마든지 곧장 일에 매달리지요.”\n\n이제는 완전히 어두워졌고, 가스등은 온전히 위력을 발휘하게 되었다. 그날의 편지를 부치러 가는 서기들이며, 저녁을 먹으러 집으로 향하는 변호사며 사무 변호사며, 원고와 피고와 온갖 종류의 소송 당사자들이며, 살아가며 마주하는 가장 흔한 일조차 처리하는 데 오랜 세월의 법조계 지혜가 백만 가지 장애물을 끼워 넣어둔 길 위의 일반 군중과 부딪치고 떠밀리며—보통법과 형평법을 헤치고, 또 그것과 한 핏줄인 신비, 곧 거리의 진창을 헤치며—그 진창은 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 아무도 모르고, 어디서 어떻게 우리 둘레에 쌓이는지도 아무도 모르며, 다만 그게 너무 많아지면 삽으로 퍼내야 한다는 것만 대충 알 뿐이다—변호사와 법률 문구상은 넝마와 병을 다루는 가게이자 푸대접받는 온갖 물건이 거래되는 잡화점에 이른다. 그 가게는 링컨스 인 담장의 그늘에 자리 잡고 있으며, 페인트로 적혀 알려진 대로, 관련된 모든 이를 위해 크룩이라는 자가 운영한다.\n\n“여기가 그자가 사는 곳입니다, 선생님,” 법률 문구상이 말한다.\n\n“여기가 그자가 사는 곳이란 말이지?” 변호사가 무심히 말한다. “고맙네.”\n\n“들어가지 않으십니까, 선생님?”\n\n“아니, 됐네, 고맙네. 지금은 필즈로 가는 길이라서 말이야. 잘 가게. 고마웠네!” 스내그스비 씨가 모자를 들어 보이고는 제 마누라와 차가 있는 곳으로 돌아간다.\n\n그러나 토킹혼 씨는 지금 필즈로 가지 않는다. 그는 잠깐 걷다가 발길을 되돌려 다시 크룩 씨의 가게로 와서는 곧장 안으로 들어선다. 가게 안은 창가에 심지 끝이 뭉툭한 양초가 한두 개 켜져 있을 뿐 어둑하고, 안쪽에 노인 하나와 고양이 한 마리가 불가에 앉아 있다. 노인이 일어나 또 다른 심지 끝 뭉툭한 양초를 손에 들고 앞으로 나온다.\n\n“실례하오만, 하숙인이 안에 있소?”\n\n“남자입니까, 여자입니까, 선생님?” 크룩 씨가 말한다.\n\n“남자요. 필사 일을 하는 사람 말이오.”\n\n크룩 씨가 상대를 찬찬히 뜯어본다. 얼굴은 안다. 그가 귀족 사회에서 명망 있다는 것도 어렴풋이 알고 있다.\n\n“그분을 만나보려고요, 선생님?”\n\n“그렇소.”\n\n“저야 좀처럼 안 하는 일입니다만,” 크룩 씨가 히죽 웃으며 말한다. “불러 내려오라 할까요? 하지만 내려올 가망은 별로 없습니다, 선생님!”\n\n“그럼 내가 올라가겠소,” 토킹혼 씨가 말한다.\n\n“삼층입니다, 선생님. 양초를 가져가시지요. 저 위쪽입니다!” 크룩 씨가 고양이를 곁에 둔 채 계단 아래에 서서 토킹혼 씨의 뒷모습을 지켜본다. “이봐요, 이봐요!” 토킹혼 씨가 거의 사라질 즈음 그가 말한다. 변호사가 난간 너머로 내려다본다. 고양이가 사악한 입을 쩍 벌리고 그를 향해 으르렁거린다.\n\n“조용히, 레이디 제인! 손님에게 예의를 지켜라, 아가씨! 저 양반, 우리 하숙인을 두고 사람들이 뭐라 하는지 아시오?” 크룩이 한두 계단 올라서며 속삭인다.\n\n“그를 두고 뭐라 하던가?”\n\n“그자가 제 영혼을 원수에게 팔아넘겼다고들 합니다만, 당신이나 나는 그게 아니란 걸 알지요—그 원수는 사들이는 법이 없으니까요. 하지만 이건 말씀드리지요. 우리 하숙인은 어찌나 음울하고 침침한지, 그런 거래라면 어떤 거래든 못지않게 선뜻 응할 위인이라고 나는 믿습니다. 그자를 자극하지 마십시오, 선생님. 그게 제 충곱니다!”\n\n토킹혼 씨가 고개를 끄덕이고는 가던 길을 간다. 그는 삼층의 어두운 문 앞에 이른다. 문을 두드리지만 아무 대답이 없어, 문을 여는데, 그러는 사이에 그만 양초를 꺼뜨리고 만다.\n\n만약 그가 꺼뜨리지 않았더라도, 방의 공기가 거의 그것을 꺼뜨릴 만큼 고약하다. 작은 방으로, 검댕과 기름과 때로 거의 새카맣다. 마치 가난이 움켜쥔 듯 한가운데가 잘록한, 녹슨 해골 같은 벽난로 틀 안에서 빨간 코크스 불이 사위어간다. 굴뚝 옆 구석에는 전나무 탁자 하나와 부서진 책상 하나가 서 있는데, 잉크가 비처럼 쏟아진 자국으로 얼룩진 황무지 같다. 다른 구석에는 두 개뿐인 의자 가운데 하나에 너덜너덜한 낡은 여행 가방이 놓여 옷장이나 의류함 노릇을 하는데, 더 큰 것은 필요치도 않으니, 그것은 굶주린 사람의 두 볼처럼 푹 꺼져 있다. 바닥에는 아무것도 깔려 있지 않고, 다만 밟혀 노끈 가닥으로 너덜너덜해진 낡은 깔개 하나가 난로 앞에서 삭아가고 있을 뿐이다. 밤의 어둠을 가려줄 휘장은 없고, 다만 색 바랜 덧창이 닫혀 있는데, 거기 뚫린 앙상한 구멍 두 개를 통해 기근이 안을 노려보고 있는지도 모른다—침대에 누운 사내를 곡하는 밴시처럼.\n\n벽난로 맞은편의 낮은 침대, 곧 더러운 누비이불과 갈빗대처럼 앙상한 침대보와 거친 마대가 뒤엉킨 그 위에서, 문간 안쪽에 멈칫 선 변호사는 사내 하나를 본다. 그는 셔츠와 바지 차림에 맨발로 거기 누워 있다. 사위어 내려 심지 전체가 (아직 타고 있는 채로) 둘로 접히고 그 위에 수의 같은 탑이 솟은 양초의 유령 같은 어둠 속에서, 그의 얼굴은 누렇게 비친다. 머리카락은 헝클어져 구레나룻과 턱수염에 뒤섞이고—턱수염 역시 헝클어진 채, 그를 둘러싼 더께와 안개처럼 방치 속에 자라 있다. 방이 더럽고 추악한 만큼, 공기 또한 더럽고 추악하여, 그 안에서 어떤 연기가 감각을 가장 짓누르는지는 가려내기 쉽지 않다. 그러나 전반적인 메스꺼움과 아득함, 그리고 묵은 담배 냄새를 뚫고, 변호사의 입안으로 아편의 쓰고 들척지근한 맛이 스며든다.\n\n“이보게, 친구!” 그가 외치며 쇠 촛대로 문을 친다.\n\n그는 친구를 깨웠으리라 생각한다. 사내는 약간 돌아누운 채이지만, 두 눈은 분명히 뜨고 있다.\n\n“이보게, 친구!” 그가 다시 외친다. “이봐! 이봐!”\n\n그가 문을 두드려대는 사이, 그토록 오래 사위어가던 양초가 꺼져 그를 어둠 속에 남기고, 덧창의 앙상한 두 눈이 침대를 내려다보며 노려본다.\n\n어두운 방에 서서 갈피를 못 잡고 머뭇거리던 변호사는 누군가 주름진 손을 건드리자 흠칫 놀라며 묻는다. \"뭐냐, 이게?\"\n\n\"나요.\" 집주인 노인이 대답하는데, 그 숨결이 변호사 귓전에 닿는다. \"저 사람 못 깨우겠소?\"\n\n\"못 깨우겠소.\"\n\"촛불은 어떻게 했소?\"\n\n\"꺼졌소. 여기 있소.\"\n\n크룩이 초를 받아 들고 난로로 가서, 벌건 잿불 위로 몸을 숙이고 불을 붙여보려 한다. 사그라드는 재에는 나눠줄 불씨가 없어 그 애씀이 헛수고다. 하숙인을 한 번 더 불러봐도 소용없자, 아래층으로 내려가 가게에서 불 켠 초를 가져오겠다고 중얼거리며 노인은 자리를 뜬다. 토킹혼 씨는 무슨 새로운 속셈이 생겼는지 방 안에서 노인을 기다리지 않고 바깥 계단에서 기다린다.\n\n크룩이 초록 눈의 고양이를 발치에 거느리고 천천히 올라오자, 반가운 불빛이 곧 벽을 비춘다. \"저 사람은 늘 이렇게 자나?\" 변호사가 나직한 목소리로 묻는다. \"에이! 나도 모르오.\" 크룩이 고개를 가로젓고 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말한다. \"저 사람 습관이라곤 워낙 자기를 꽁꽁 감추고 산다는 것 말고는 거의 아는 게 없소.\"\n\n이렇게 소곤거리며 둘이 함께 들어선다. 불빛이 들어가자, 덧문에 그려진 커다란 눈들이 어두워지며 감기는 듯하다. 침대 위의 두 눈은 그렇지 않다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 토킹혼 씨가 외친다. \"죽었어!\" 크룩이 들어 올렸던 그 묵직한 손을 어찌나 갑자기 놓아버리는지, 팔이 침대 옆으로 휙 늘어진다.\n\n둘은 한순간 서로를 마주 본다.\n\n\"의사를 불러요! 계단 위로 가서 플라이트 양을 부르시오, 선생. 침대 옆에 독약이 있소! 플라이트를 좀 불러주겠소?\" 크룩이 흡혈귀의 날개처럼 깡마른 두 손을 시신 위로 펼친 채 말한다.\n\n토킹혼 씨가 층계참으로 서둘러 가서 외친다. \"플라이트 양! 플라이트! 빨리 좀, 여보시오, 거기 누구든! 플라이트!\" 크룩은 그를 눈으로 좇다가, 그가 부르는 사이 낡은 여행 가방으로 슬그머니 다가갔다가 다시 슬그머니 돌아올 틈을 잡는다.\n\n\"달려요, 플라이트, 달려! 제일 가까운 의사한테로! 달려요!\" 크룩 씨가 이렇게 외친 상대는 그의 여자 하숙인인 정신 나간 자그마한 노파로, 그녀는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 나타났다 사라지더니, 저녁 식사 도중에 끌려 나온 성마른 의사를 데리고 곧 돌아온다. 그 의사는 윗입술이 넓적하고 코담배 자국이 묻었으며 스코틀랜드 사투리가 걸쭉하다.\n\n\"어이! 다들 복 받으시오.\" 의사가 잠깐 살펴보고 나서 고개를 들며 말한다. \"이 양반 영락없이 죽었구먼, 파라오처럼!\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가(낡은 여행 가방 옆에 선 채) 죽은 지 얼마나 됐느냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"얼마나 됐냐고요, 선생?\" 의사 양반이 말한다. \"죽은 지 한 세 시간쯤 됐을 게요.\"\n\n\"그쯤 됐을 겁니다.\" 침대 건너편에 선 거무스름한 젊은이가 한마디 보탠다.\n\n\"선생도 의술 하시는 분이오?\" 먼저 온 의사가 묻는다.\n\n거무스름한 젊은이가 그렇다고 답한다.\n\n\"그럼 난 그냥 물러가겠소.\" 의사가 대꾸한다. \"여기선 내가 쓸모가 없으니!\" 이 말과 함께 그는 짧은 진료를 마치고 저녁 식사를 마저 끝내러 돌아간다.\n\n거무스름한 젊은 외과의가 죽은 자의 얼굴 위로 촛불을 이리저리 옮겨가며 그 법률 필경사를 찬찬히 살핀다. 필경사는 정말로 '아무도 아닌 자'가 됨으로써 그 이름값을 입증한 셈이다.\n* 아무도 아닌 자: 죽은 필경사는 '네모(Nemo)'라는 이름을 썼는데, 라틴어로 '아무도 아닌 사람'이라는 뜻이다.\n\n\"이 사람 얼굴은 아주 잘 압니다.\" 그가 말한다. \"지난 일 년 반 동안 나한테서 아편을 사 갔지요. 여기 이 사람 친척 되는 분 계십니까?\" 그가 둘러서 있는 세 사람을 휘둘러보며 말한다.\n\n\"내가 이 사람 집주인이오.\" 크룩이 외과의가 내민 손에서 초를 받아 들며 무뚝뚝하게 대답한다. \"이 사람이 한번은 나더러 제일 가까운 친척이라고 한 적이 있소.\"\n\n\"이 사람은 아편 과다 복용으로 죽었습니다. 의심할 여지가 없어요.\" 외과의가 말한다. \"방 안에 냄새가 진동하는군요. 지금 여기 있는 것만 해도,\" 크룩 씨에게서 낡은 찻주전자를 받아 들며 말한다. \"열 사람은 너끈히 죽일 분량입니다.\"\n\n\"일부러 그랬다고 보시오?\" 크룩이 묻는다.\n\n\"과다 복용 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소!\" 크룩이 끔찍한 호기심에 입맛을 다실 듯한 기색이다.\n\n\"뭐라 말씀 못 드리겠습니다. 워낙 많이 복용해온 사람이니 그럴 것 같진 않습니다만. 하지만 누가 알겠습니까. 이 사람 무척 가난했지요, 아마?\"\n\n\"가난했을 거요. 이 사람 방을 보면, 부유해 보이진 않으니까.\" 크룩이 날카로운 눈길을 둘러보며 말하는데, 그 고양이와 눈을 맞바꿨다 해도 믿길 만하다. \"그래도 이 사람이 든 뒤로 난 한 번도 들어와 본 적이 없소. 형편을 나한테 말할 만큼 속내를 트지도 않았고.\"\n\n\"집세는 밀린 게 있소?\"\n\n\"여섯 주치요.\"\n\n\"이제 영영 못 받겠군요!\" 젊은이가 다시 살피기 시작하며 말한다. \"이 사람이 영락없이 파라오처럼 죽었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그리고 행색과 형편으로 미루어 보면, 차라리 잘 떠난 거라고 봐야겠지요. 그래도 젊었을 적엔 풍채가 좋았을 겁니다. 잘생겼을 거고요.\" 그가 침대 모서리에 걸터앉아 저쪽 얼굴을 마주 보며 한 손을 심장께에 올린 채, 매정하지 않은 어조로 이렇게 말한다. \"한번은 이런 생각을 한 적이 있습니다. 이 사람의 거동에, 비록 거칠긴 해도, 한때 영락한 신세를 짐작케 하는 무언가가 있다고 말입니다. 그랬습니까?\" 그가 둘러보며 말을 잇는다.\n\n크룩이 대꾸한다. \"그건 나더러 아래층 자루에 든 머리카락 임자였던 부인들을 묘사해보라는 것과 마찬가지요. 일 년 반 동안 내 하숙인이었고, 필경 일로 먹고살았다는 것 말고는, 아니 먹고살지 못했다는 것 말고는, 나도 더는 아는 게 없소.\"\n\n이런 대화가 오가는 동안 토킹혼 씨는 낡은 여행 가방 옆에 뒷짐을 진 채 멀찍이 서서, 침대 가까이서 드러난 세 가지 관심 — 죽은 자를 한 개인으로 두고 한 말과는 사뭇 동떨어진, 죽음을 향한 젊은 외과의의 직업적 관심과, 노인의 게걸스러운 흥미와, 정신 나간 자그마한 노파의 두려움 — 그 어느 쪽과도 똑같이 동떨어져 보인다. 그의 태연한 얼굴은 낡은 옷만큼이나 아무 표정이 없다. 그가 이러는 내내 무언가 생각하고 있었다고조차 말할 수 없을 정도다. 그는 참을성도 조바심도, 주의도 무심함도 드러내지 않았다. 그가 드러낸 것이라곤 껍데기뿐이다. 섬세한 악기의 음색을 그 상자만 보고 짐작할 수 없듯, 토킹혼 씨의 음색도 그 껍데기만 보고는 짐작할 수 없다.\n\n이제 그가 흔들림 없는 직업적 태도로 젊은 외과의에게 말을 건네며 끼어든다.\n\n\"내가 이 방을 들여다본 건,\" 그가 말한다. \"바로 선생보다 조금 앞선 일인데, 살아 있는 모습은 한 번도 본 적 없는 이 죽은 사람에게 필사 일거리를 좀 맡기려는 생각에서였소. 쿡스 코트의 문구상 스내그스비한테서 이 사람 얘길 들었지요. 여기 아무도 이 사람에 관해 아는 게 없으니, 스내그스비를 부르는 게 좋겠소. 아!\" 법정에서 자기를 자주 보았고 자기도 자주 본 그 정신 나간 자그마한 노파를 향해, 그녀가 겁먹은 몸짓으로 법률 문구상을 데려오겠다는 시늉을 하자 하는 말이다. \"그래주시오!\"\n\n그녀가 가 있는 동안 외과의는 가망 없는 조사를 그만두고, 조각보 침대보로 그 대상을 덮는다. 크룩 씨와 외과의가 한두 마디 주고받는다. 토킹혼 씨는 아무 말 없이, 여전히 낡은 여행 가방 곁에 서 있다.\n\n스내그스비 씨가 잿빛 외투에 검은 토시 차림으로 황급히 도착한다. \"이런, 이런.\" 그가 말한다. \"결국 이 지경이 됐군요, 그래! 맙소사!\"\n\n\"이 가엾은 사람에 관해 집주인한테 뭐라도 알려줄 게 있소, 스내그스비?\" 토킹혼 씨가 묻는다. \"집세를 밀렸던 모양인데. 게다가 묻어주기는 해야 하지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 선생.\" 스내그스비 씨가 손으로 입을 가리고 미안한 듯 기침을 하며 말한다. \"저로선 교구 직원을 부르는 것 말고는 무슨 조언을 드릴 수 있을지 정말 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"조언을 말하는 게 아니오.\" 토킹혼 씨가 대꾸한다. \"조언이라면 내가 할 수도—\"\n\n\"선생만큼 잘하실 분이야 없지요, 암요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 공손한 기침과 함께 말한다.\n\n\"이 사람의 연고지나, 어디서 왔는지나, 그에 관한 무엇이라도 실마리가 될 만한 걸 말하는 거요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드립니다만, 선생.\" 스내그스비 씨가 만사를 무마하는 기침으로 대답을 떼며 말한다. \"저는 이 사람이 어디서 왔는지 도무지 모릅니다, 마치—\"\n\n\"어디로 갔는지를 모르는 만큼이나, 아마.\" 외과의가 거들어주려고 말을 보탠다.\n\n잠시 침묵. 토킹혼 씨가 법률 문구상을 바라본다. 크룩 씨는 입을 벌린 채 다음에 입을 뗄 사람을 찾는다.\n\n\"이 사람 연고지로 말하자면, 선생.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"누가 저더러 '스내그스비, 여기 이만 파운드를 잉글랜드 은행에 당장 준비해뒀으니 그 연고자 한 사람만 대보시오' 한다 해도, 저는 못 댑니다, 선생! 한 일 년 반 전쯤에 — 제가 기억하기로는, 이 사람이 지금 이 넝마와 병 가게에 처음 하숙하러 들어왔을 무렵에—\"\n\n\"바로 그때였소!\" 크룩이 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다.\n\n\"한 일 년 반 전쯤에,\" 힘을 얻은 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"이 사람이 어느 날 아침 식사가 끝난 뒤 저희 가게에 들어왔는데, 가게에 있던 제 안사람이(이렇게 부를 땐 스내그스비 부인이라고 합니다) 마침 거기 있었지요. 그러더니 자기 필체 견본을 내보이며, 필사 일거리를 구한다고, 그리고 너무 까놓고 말하긴 뭣합니다만,\" 이건 스내그스비 씨가 솔직하게 말할 때 즐겨 쓰는 변명으로, 그는 늘 따지듯 거리낌 없는 투로 이 말을 내놓는다. \"쪼들린다고 하더군요! 제 안사람이 원래 낯선 사람을, 더구나 — 너무 까놓고 말하긴 뭣합니다만 — 무얼 바라고 오는 사람을 그리 살갑게 대하는 편이 아닙니다. 그런데 이 사람한테는 어쩐지 마음이 좀 끌렸던 모양이에요. 수염을 안 깎아서였는지, 머리가 손질이 안 돼서였는지, 아니면 여자들만 아는 무슨 이유에서였는지는 선생 판단에 맡깁니다만. 어쨌든 안사람이 그 견본을 받았고, 주소도 받아뒀습니다. 제 안사람이 이름을 잘 못 알아듣거든요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 손으로 입을 가리고 신중을 기하는 기침을 한 뒤 말을 잇는다. \"그래서 네모를 님로드랑 똑같은 걸로 여겼지 뭡니까. 그 바람에 식사 때면 저한테 이러는 버릇이 들었어요. '스내그스비, 당신 아직 님로드한테 일거리 안 줬구려!' 아니면 '스내그스비, 잔다이스 건 그 서른여덟 장짜리 대법원 폴리오를 왜 님로드한테 안 줬어요?' 뭐 그런 식으로요. 그렇게 해서 이 사람이 차츰 저희 가게 삯일에 발을 들였습니다. 손이 빠르고 밤일도 마다하지 않는 사람이라는 것, 가령 수요일 밤에 폴리오 마흔다섯 장쯤 맡기면 목요일 아침엔 다 해서 들고 온다는 것 말고는, 제가 이 사람에 관해 아는 건 그게 전부입니다. 그 모든 게—\" 스내그스비 씨가 침대 쪽으로 정중하게 모자를 까딱해 보이며 말을 맺는데, 마치 \"여기 제 점잖은 친구분도 그럴 형편만 됐다면 두말없이 확인해주셨을 겁니다\" 하고 덧붙이는 듯하다.\n* 폴리오: 법률 문서의 분량 단위로, 일정한 낱말 수를 한 장으로 세었다. * 님로드: 성경에 나오는 사냥꾼이자 용맹한 인물. 스내그스비 부인이 '네모'를 비슷한 어감의 '님로드'로 잘못 알아들었다는 뜻이다.\n\n\"가서 살펴보는 게 낫지 않겠소,\" 토킹혼 씨가 크룩에게 말한다. \"뭐든 단서가 될 만한 서류라도 있는지 말이오. 검시가 있을 텐데, 그때 그런 걸 물을 거요. 글은 읽을 줄 아시오?\"\n\n\"아니, 못 읽소.\" 노인이 불쑥 씩 웃으며 대꾸한다.\n\n\"스내그스비.\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"당신이 대신 방을 좀 살펴봐 주시오. 안 그러면 이 사람이 무슨 곤란이나 시비에 휘말릴 거요. 마침 내가 여기 있으니, 서둘러주면 기다리겠소. 그러면 혹시라도 필요할 때 모든 게 정당하고 떳떳했다고 내가 이 사람을 위해 증언해줄 수 있을 테니. 여보시오, 당신이 스내그스비 씨한테 초를 들어주면, 단서가 될 만한 게 있는지 곧 알 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"우선, 여기 낡은 여행 가방이 있군요, 선생.\" 스내그스비가 말한다.\n\n아, 그렇지, 정말 거기 있구나! 토킹혼 씨는 그 가방 코앞에 서 있는데도, 또 하늘이 알다시피 그 방엔 그것 말고 별것도 없는데도, 전에 본 적이 없다는 기색이다.\n\n해묵은 잡동사니 장수가 불을 들고, 법률 문구상이 수색을 이끈다. 외과의는 벽난로 선반 모서리에 기대 있고, 플라이트 양은 문간 안쪽에서 빠끔히 들여다보며 떨고 있다. 옛 학파의 노련한 노학자는 무릎께를 리본으로 묶은 칙칙한 검은 반바지에, 큼지막한 검은 조끼, 긴 소매 검은 외투, 그리고 귀족 사회가 익히 아는 그 매듭으로 묶은 한 가닥 흐물흐물한 흰 목수건 차림으로, 똑같은 자리에 똑같은 자세로 서 있다.\n\n낡은 여행 가방 안에는 쓸모없는 옷가지 몇 점이 들어 있다. 전당포 전표 뭉치가 있는데, 이는 가난이라는 길 위의 통행료 영수증이다. 아편 냄새가 나는 구겨진 종이 한 장도 있는데, 거기엔 거친 메모가 휘갈겨져 있다 — 어느 날 몇 그레인을 복용했다, 또 어느 날 몇 그레인을 더 복용했다, 하는 식으로, 한동안 꼬박꼬박 이어 적을 작정으로 시작한 듯하나 이내 그만둔 모양이다. 죄다 검시 보도와 관련된 더러운 신문 조각이 몇 점 있고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 없다. 그들은 찬장과 잉크 얼룩진 탁자 서랍을 뒤진다. 어느 쪽에도 묵은 편지 한 조각, 다른 어떤 글씨 한 조각 없다. 젊은 외과의가 법률 필경사가 입은 옷을 살핀다. 찾아낸 것이라곤 칼 한 자루와 동전 몇 닢이 전부다. 결국 스내그스비 씨의 제안이 가장 실속 있는 제안이었던 셈이니, 교구 직원을 불러들여야 한다.\n* 그레인: 약 0.0648그램에 해당하는 무게 단위로, 약물의 분량을 잴 때 썼다.\n\n그리하여 정신 나간 자그마한 하숙인이 교구 직원을 부르러 가고, 나머지는 방에서 나온다. \"고양이를 거기 두지 마시오!\" 외과의가 말한다. \"그건 안 될 일이오!\" 그래서 크룩 씨가 고양이를 앞세워 내몬다. 고양이는 날렵한 꼬리를 휘감고 입맛을 다시며 슬그머니 아래층으로 내려간다.\n\n\"안녕히!\" 토킹혼 씨가 말하고는, '우의'와 명상이 있는 자기 집으로 돌아간다.\n\n* 우의: 토킹혼 씨 방 천장에 그려진 알레고리 벽화를 가리킨다. 손가락으로 무언가를 가리키는 형상으로, 작품 내내 상징적으로 등장한다.\n이쯤 되자 그 소식이 골목 안에 퍼진다. 동네 사람들이 떼 지어 모여 이 일을 떠들어대고, 관찰병 부대의 전초병들(주로 사내아이들)이 크룩 씨네 창문 앞으로 밀고 나가 그 창을 바짝 에워싼다. 경관 하나가 벌써 방으로 올라갔다가 다시 문간으로 내려와서는, 탑처럼 버티고 선 채 이따금 제 발치의 아이들을 흘끗 굽어볼 따름이다. 하지만 그가 한 번이라도 눈길을 주면 아이들은 움찔하며 뒷걸음친다. 퍼킨스 부인은 몇 주째 파이퍼 부인과 말도 안 섞고 지냈는데 — 어린 퍼킨스가 어린 파이퍼에게 \"한 대 먹인\" 데서 비롯된 불쾌한 일 탓이었다 — 이 경사스러운 기회에 다정한 왕래를 새로 튼다. 길모퉁이 술집 사환 녀석은 인생을 공식적으로 안다 할 만큼 술 취한 자들을 더러 상대해본 처지라 특권을 누리는 풋내기인데, 경관과 은밀한 이야기를 주고받으며 곤봉으로도 못 건드리고 유치장에도 못 가둘 무적의 청년인 양 으스댄다. 사람들이 창 너머로 골목을 가로질러 떠들고, 맨머리 정찰병들이 무슨 일인가 알아보려고 챈서리 레인에서 부랴부랴 달려든다. 분위기로 보아 크룩 씨가 먼저 당하지 않은 게 천만다행이라는 기색인데, 그렇지 못한 데 대한 자연스러운 실망감도 조금 섞여 있는 듯하다. 이 소동 한복판으로 교구 직원이 당도한다.\n\n교구 직원이라는 자리는 동네에서 대체로 우스꽝스러운 직책으로 통하지만, 이 순간만큼은 시신을 보러 들어갈 사람이라는 이유 하나만으로도 어느 정도 인기가 없지 않다. 경관은 그를 미개한 야경꾼 시절의 유물 같은 얼간이 민간인으로 여기지만, 정부가 이 자리를 없애기 전까지는 견뎌야 할 존재로 치고 들여보내 준다. 교구 직원이 현장에 와서 안으로 들어갔다는 소식이 입에서 입으로 퍼지면서 분위기가 한층 달아오른다.\n\n이윽고 교구 직원이 나오자, 그새 좀 시들해졌던 분위기가 다시금 후끈 달아오른다. 알고 보니 그는 내일 검시에 쓸 증인을, 그러니까 죽은 자에 관해 검시관과 배심에게 뭐라도 말해줄 수 있는 증인을 찾고 있다. 그러자 대뜸 아무것도 모르는 사람들이 무수히 그에게 떠밀려 온다. 게다가 그린 부인네 아들이 \"지가 직접 법률 필경사였고 누구보다 그 사람을 잘 알았다\"는 말을 거듭 듣게 되면서 한층 더 얼간이가 되고 마는데, 알아보니 그린 부인네 그 아들은 지금 중국행 배를 타고 떠난 지 석 달째라, 다만 해군성 경들에게 신청하면 전보로 연락이 닿으리라는 것이다. 교구 직원은 이 가게 저 응접실로 들어가 사람들을 캐묻는데, 늘 문부터 닫고는, 사람들을 따돌리고 늑장 부리고 전반적으로 멍청하게 굴어 대중의 약을 올린다. 경관이 술집 사환을 보며 빙긋 웃는 모습이 보인다. 대중은 흥미를 잃고 반작용을 일으킨다. 아이들이 새된 목소리로, 교구 직원이 사내애 하나를 삶아 먹었다고 빈정대며 그 내용을 담은 유행가 가락을 한 토막씩 합창하는데, 그 애가 구빈원 수프 거리가 되었다는 노래다. 마침내 경관은 법을 떠받칠 필요를 느껴 노래꾼 하나를 붙잡는데, 나머지가 줄행랑을 치자 그놈도 당장 여기서 꺼지겠다는, 자, 어서 사라지겠다는 조건으로 풀어주니 — 그놈은 즉시 그 조건을 지킨다. 그리하여 소동은 한동안 잦아든다. 그리고 (아편이 좀 많고 적고는 아무것도 아닌) 태연한 경관은, 번쩍이는 모자에 빳빳한 목깃, 뻣뻣한 외투, 튼튼한 허리띠와 팔찌, 그 밖에 빠짐없이 갖춘 차림으로, 흰 장갑 낀 손바닥을 마주 두드리며 무거운 발걸음으로 어슬렁어슬렁 제 길을 가다가, 이따금 길모퉁이에 멈춰 서서 미아부터 살인 사건에 이르기까지 아무거나 무심히 둘러본다.\n\n밤의 장막을 틈타, 모자란 머리의 교구 직원이 소환장을 들고 챈서리 레인 일대를 종종거리며 돌아다닌다. 그 소환장에는 배심원들 이름이 죄다 틀리게 적혀 있고, 제대로 적힌 것이라곤 교구 직원 자기 이름뿐인데, 그건 아무도 읽을 수 없고 알고 싶지도 않은 이름이다. 소환장을 다 돌리고 증인들에게 미리 일러둔 뒤, 교구 직원은 크룩 씨네로 가서, 미리 약속해둔 빈민 몇과의 자그마한 약속을 지킨다. 이내 도착한 빈민들은 위층으로 안내되는데, 거기서 그들은 덧문에 그려진 커다란 눈들에게 새로 노려볼 거리를 남긴다 — '아무도 아닌 자'에게, 또 '모든 사람'에게 이승의 거처가 마지막으로 취하는 바로 그 모습을.\n\n그리고 그날 밤 내내 관이 낡은 여행 가방 옆에 채비를 갖춘 채 놓여 있다. 마흔다섯 해 동안 인생길을 걸어온 침대 위의 외로운 형체는, 버려진 갓난아이만큼이나 누구도 더듬어볼 자취 하나 남기지 못한 채 거기 누워 있다.\n\n이튿날, 골목 안은 온통 활기에 넘쳐 — 파이퍼 부인과 진작에 화해한 퍼킨스 부인이 그 훌륭한 여인과 다정한 대화를 나누며 말하듯 — 영락없는 장날 같다. 검시관은 솔스 암스 이층 방에서 자리를 펼 예정인데, 거기선 일주일에 두 번 화음 모임이 열리고, 그 모임의 좌장은 직업적으로 이름난 신사가 맡되, 그 맞은편엔 희극 노래꾼 리틀 스윌스가 앉아 (창문에 붙은 광고에 따르면) 친구들이 자기 곁에 모여 일류 재능을 응원해주기를 바란다고 적혀 있다. 솔스 암스는 아침 내내 장사가 신바람 난다. 아이들조차 이 들뜬 분위기에 기운을 차릴 게 필요한지, 이 일을 노리고 골목 모퉁이에 자리 잡은 파이 장수가 자기네 브랜디 사탕이 연기처럼 팔려나간다고 말할 정도다. 그러는 사이 교구 직원은 크룩 씨네 가게 문과 솔스 암스 문 사이를 어슬렁거리며, 자기가 맡아둔 구경거리를 입 무거운 몇몇에게 보여주고는 그 대가로 맥주 한두 잔의 호의를 받아 마신다.\n\n정해진 시각에 검시관이 도착하는데, 배심원들이 그를 기다리고 있고, 솔스 암스에 딸린 잘 마른 스키틀 마당에서 스키틀 소리가 그를 인사하듯 맞는다. 검시관은 살아 있는 그 누구보다도 술집을 많이 드나드는 사람이다. 그의 직업에서 톱밥과 맥주, 담배 연기와 독한 술 냄새는 가장 무시무시한 모습의 죽음과 떼려야 뗄 수 없다. 그는 교구 직원과 술집 주인의 안내로 화음 모임방에 들어가, 모자를 피아노 위에 올려놓고, 짧은 탁자 여럿을 붙여 만든 긴 탁자 상석에 윈저 의자를 놓고 앉는다. 그 탁자엔 술잔과 단지가 남긴 끈적한 동그라미 자국이 끝없이 뒤엉켜 새겨져 있다. 배심원들은 탁자에 다닥다닥 붙어 앉을 수 있는 만큼 거기 앉고, 나머지는 가래받이와 담뱃대 사이에 끼어 있거나 피아노에 기대선다. 검시관 머리 위엔 자그마한 무쇠 화환이, 곧 종을 매단 손잡이가 드리워져 있어, 법정의 위엄이 머잖아 교수형에라도 처해질 것 같은 꼴이다.\n\n* 스키틀: 나무 핀을 공으로 쓰러뜨리는 영국식 볼링 놀이.\n배심원 호명하고 선서시켜라! 그 절차가 진행되는 동안, 큼지막한 셔츠 깃에 눈가가 촉촉하고 코가 벌건 토실토실한 자그마한 사내가 들어와 한바탕 술렁임을 일으킨다. 그는 그저 일반 방청객인 양 점잖게 문간 가까이 자리를 잡지만, 이 방을 꽤 잘 아는 눈치다. 저 사람이 리틀 스윌스라는 소곤거림이 돈다. 저녁 화음 모임에서 그가 검시관 흉내를 한바탕 꾸며 그날의 백미로 삼으리라는 게 다들 점치는 바다.\n\n\"자, 여러분—\" 검시관이 입을 뗀다.\n\n\"거기 조용히, 좀!\" 교구 직원이 말한다. 그렇게 들릴 법도 하지만, 검시관더러 한 말은 아니다.\n\n\"자, 여러분.\" 검시관이 말을 잇는다. \"여러분은 어떤 사람의 죽음을 조사하고자 여기 배심으로 소집되었습니다. 그 죽음에 얽힌 정황에 관한 증거가 여러분 앞에 제시될 것이고, 여러분은 그 — 스키틀 좀, 저거 멈추게 해야겠소, 교구 직원! — 증거에 따라 평결을 내리되, 그 밖에 다른 무엇에도 따르지 마십시오. 가장 먼저 할 일은 시신을 검안하는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"길 좀 비키시오!\" 교구 직원이 외친다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 흐트러진 행렬을 지어 나가는데, 어쩐지 뿔뿔이 흩어진 장례 행렬 같은 모양새다. 크룩 씨네 뒤편 삼층에서 검안을 하는데, 배심원 몇은 새파랗게 질려 허둥지둥 물러난다. 교구 직원은 소맷부리와 단추가 별로 단정치 못한 두 신사가(이들을 위해 그는 화음 모임방의 검시관 곁에 특별히 작은 탁자 하나를 마련해두었다) 볼 만한 건 죄다 보도록 무척 신경 쓴다. 이들이야말로 이런 종류의 조사를 줄줄이 기록해 세상에 알리는 공인된 연대기 작가들이기 때문이다. 게다가 그도 인간 보편의 약점에서 벗어난 사람은 아니라, \"이 지역의 민첩하고 영민한 교구 직원 무니\"가 무어라 말하고 무얼 했다는 내용을 활자로 읽기를 바라며, 더 나아가 요즘 사례에 비추어 사형 집행인의 이름만큼이나 무니라는 이름이 친근하고 너그러이 거론되는 걸 보기를 열망한다.\n\n검시관과 배심이 돌아오기를 리틀 스윌스가 기다리고 있다. 토킹혼 씨도. 토킹혼 씨는 정중히 영접을 받아, 그 높으신 사법관과 바가텔 판, 석탄통 사이에서 검시관 곁에 앉는다. 조사가 진행된다. 배심은 조사 대상이 어떻게 죽었는지는 알게 되지만, 그에 관해서는 그 이상 아무것도 알지 못한다. \"아주 저명한 사무변호사 한 분이 자리해 계십니다, 여러분.\" 검시관이 말한다. \"들으니 이분이 시신 발견 당시 우연히 그 자리에 계셨다 하나, 여러분이 외과의와 집주인, 하숙인과 법률 문구상한테서 이미 들으신 증언을 되풀이하실 따름이니, 굳이 번거롭게 해드릴 필요는 없겠습니다. 이보다 더 아는 게 있는 분, 자리에 계십니까?\"\n* 바가텔: 작은 공을 막대로 쳐서 구멍에 넣는 당구 비슷한 실내 놀이.\n\n퍼킨스 부인이 파이퍼 부인을 앞으로 떠민다. 파이퍼 부인 선서.\n\n배심원 여러분, 어내스테이저 파이퍼입니다. 유부녀이고요. 자, 파이퍼 부인, 이 일에 관해 하실 말씀이 무엇입니까?\n\n웬걸, 파이퍼 부인은 할 말이 잔뜩인데, 대개 군더더기 삽입구뿐이요 구두점도 없는 데다, 정작 들려줄 만한 알맹이는 별로 없다. 파이퍼 부인은 이 골목에 사는데(남편이 가구장이라) 동네에선 진작에 잘 알려진 일이라(알렉산더 제임스 파이퍼가 생후 십팔 개월 나흘 적에 잇몸 때문에 어찌나 고생을 했던지 살아남지 못할 줄 알고 약식으로 세례를 준 그 전전날부터 치자면) 그 원고가 — 파이퍼 부인은 죽은 자를 한사코 이렇게 부른다 — 자기 몸을 팔아넘겼다는 소문이 돌았다는 것이다. 그 소문이 시작된 건 그 원고의 인상 때문인 것 같다고 한다. 그 원고를 자주 보았는데, 그 인상이 흉포해 보여서, 어떤 아이들은 겁이 많으니 함부로 돌아다니게 둬선 안 될 사람이라 여겼다고 한다(미덥지 않으시면 퍼킨스 부인을 불러주시기 바라는데 그분이 여기 와 있고 남편과 자신과 가족의 체면을 세워줄 것이라고). 그 원고가 아이들한테 약 오르고 시달리는 걸 본 적이 있다고 한다(애들은 어차피 애들이라, 더구나 장난기 많은 애들이라면, 무드셀라처럼 점잖기를 바랄 수 없잖아요, 그쪽도 그러지 못했으면서). 이 때문에 또 그 사람 어두운 낯빛 때문에, 그가 주머니에서 곡괭이를 꺼내 조니 머리를 쪼개는 꿈을 자주 꿨다고 한다(그 애가 겁이라곤 없어서 바짝 따라붙으며 자꾸 그 사람한테 소리를 질러댔거든요). 그렇지만 그 원고가 곡괭이는커녕 다른 어떤 흉기를 꺼내는 건 전혀 본 적이 없다고 한다. 아이들이 뒤쫓으며 소리치면 그가 황급히 달아나는 건 봤는데, 마치 아이들을 좋아하지 않는 듯했다고, 그리고 그가 아이든 어른이든 누구한테 말 거는 건 한 번도 못 봤다고 한다(저 길 건너 모퉁이 돌아 골목 아래 횡단로를 쓰는 그 애만 빼고요, 그 애가 여기 있다면 그 사람이 자기한테 자주 말 거는 걸 봤다고 일러드릴 텐데요).\n* 무드셀라: 성경에서 969세까지 살았다는 인물 므두셀라. 여기선 '점잖고 늙수그레한 사람'을 비꼬는 비유다.\n\n검시관이 묻는다, 그 애가 여기 있소? 교구 직원이 답한다, 아닙니다, 선생, 여기 없습니다. 검시관이 말한다, 그럼 가서 데려오시오. 민첩하고 영민한 직원이 자리를 비운 사이, 검시관은 토킹혼 씨와 담소를 나눈다.\n\n오! 그 애가 왔습니다, 여러분!\n\n여기 그 애가 왔다, 진흙투성이에, 목이 잔뜩 쉬고, 누더기 차림이다. 자, 얘야! 하지만 잠깐. 주의할 것. 이 아이는 먼저 몇 가지 예비 절차를 거쳐야 한다.\n\n이름, 조. 그 밖엔 아는 게 없다. 사람마다 이름이 둘인 줄도 모른다. 그런 건 들어본 적도 없다. 조가 더 긴 이름의 줄임말인 줄도 모른다. 자기한테는 그것만으로도 충분히 길다고 여긴다. 그걸로 트집 잡을 생각도 없다. 철자를 대보라고? 아니. 철자는 못 댄다. 아버지도 어머니도 친구도 없다. 학교라곤 가본 적이 없다. 집이 뭔데? 빗자루가 빗자루인 건 알고, 거짓말하는 게 나쁘다는 것도 안다. 빗자루며 거짓말이며 누가 가르쳐줬는지는 기억 못 하지만, 둘 다 알기는 안다. 여기 신사분들한테 거짓말을 하면 죽은 뒤에 자기한테 무슨 일이 벌어질지 콕 집어 말하진 못하겠는데, 벌을 받으려고 아주 고약한 일이 닥칠 거고 그래 싸다고 믿는다 — 그러니 사실대로 말하겠다.\n\n\"이래선 안 되겠습니다, 여러분!\" 검시관이 침울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말한다.\n\n\"이 아이 증언을 받아들이실 수 없겠습니까, 선생?\" 주의 깊은 배심원 하나가 묻는다.\n\n\"어림없는 일입니다.\" 검시관이 말한다. \"방금 그 아이 말을 들으셨잖습니까. '콕 집어 말하진 못하겠다'는 건 안 됩니다, 아시다시피. 우리는 그런 걸 사법 법정에서 받아들일 수 없어요, 여러분. 끔찍한 타락이지요. 저 아이는 물려두시오.\"\n\n아이는 한쪽으로 물려지고, 방청객들, 특히 희극 노래꾼 리틀 스윌스는 이를 더없이 유익한 구경거리로 여긴다.\n\n자, 다른 증인이 또 있소? 다른 증인은 없다.\n\n좋습니다, 여러분! 여기 신원 미상의 남자가 있고, 일 년 반 동안 아편을 다량 복용해온 것으로 입증되었으며, 아편 과다로 숨진 채 발견되었습니다. 그가 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 결론으로 이끌 만한 증거가 있다고 보시면, 그 결론에 이르시면 됩니다. 사고사라고 보시면, 그에 맞춰 평결을 내리시면 됩니다.\n\n그에 맞춰 평결. 사고사. 의심할 여지 없음. 여러분, 해산하셔도 됩니다. 안녕히 가십시오.\n\n검시관이 외투 단추를 잠그는 동안, 토킹혼 씨와 그는 구석에서 퇴짜 맞은 증인을 따로 만난다.\n\n그 보잘것없는 녀석이 아는 것이라곤, 죽은 사내가(방금 누런 얼굴과 검은 머리로 알아본 그 사람이) 이따금 길거리에서 야유받고 쫓겨 다녔다는 것뿐이다. 어느 추운 겨울밤, 그 애가, 그러니까 조가, 제 횡단로 근처 어느 문간에서 덜덜 떨고 있을 때, 그 사내가 돌아서서 자기를 보더니, 되돌아와서, 이것저것 묻고는 이 애한테 세상에 친구 하나 없다는 걸 알자, \"나도 없단다. 하나도!\" 하고는 저녁 한 끼와 하룻밤 잠자리 값을 쥐여주었다는 것이다. 그 뒤로도 사내가 자주 말을 걸어, 밤에 잠은 푹 자는지, 추위와 굶주림은 어떻게 견디는지, 죽고 싶은 적은 없는지, 그런 이상한 것들을 묻곤 했다는 것이다. 사내는 돈이 없을 땐 지나치며 \"나도 오늘은 너만큼이나 가난하구나, 조\" 하곤 했지만, 돈이 좀 있을 땐 늘(이 애가 진심으로 믿기로는) 기꺼이 얼마쯤 나눠주었다는 것이다.\n\n\"그분은 저한테 참 잘해주셨어요.\" 아이가 다 해진 소매로 눈을 훔치며 말한다. \"방금 그분이 그렇게 쭉 뻗어 누운 걸 보니, 제가 이 말을 하는 걸 그분이 들으셨더라면 싶었어요. 그분은 저한테 참 잘해주셨어요, 정말요!\"\n\n아이가 발을 끌며 아래층으로 내려갈 때, 그를 기다리고 있던 스내그스비 씨가 그 손에 반 크라운을 쥐여준다. \"혹시라도 내가 우리 안사람을 — 아니, 어느 부인을 데리고 네 횡단로 앞을 지나가는 걸 보거든,\" 스내그스비 씨가 코에 손가락을 갖다 대고 말한다. \"그 일은 모른 척해다오!\"\n\n한동안 배심원들이 솔스 암스 언저리에 모여 두런두런 이야기를 나눈다. 그 끝에, 예닐곱은 솔스 암스 응접실에 자욱한 담배 연기 구름 속에 휩싸이고, 둘은 햄스테드로 어슬렁어슬렁 떠나며, 넷은 밤에 반값으로 연극을 보고 굴 안주로 마무리하기로 약속한다. 리틀 스윌스는 여러 손에서 한턱을 얻어먹는다. 이 절차를 어떻게 보느냐는 물음에, 그는(그의 장기가 속어 쪽에 있는지라) 그것을 \"별난 한판\"이라 평한다. 솔스 암스 주인은 리틀 스윌스가 이리 인기 있는 걸 보고는, 배심원들과 손님들에게 그를 한껏 추켜세우며, 배역을 살린 노래로 말하자면 그에 견줄 자를 모르겠다고, 그자의 분장 의상만 해도 짐수레 하나를 가득 채우고도 남을 거라고 한다.\n\n이렇게 솔스 암스는 차츰 어스름한 밤 속으로 녹아들었다가, 이내 가스 불빛으로 환히 타오르며 다시 솟아난다. 화음 모임 시각이 닥치자, 직업적으로 이름난 신사가 좌장을 맡고, 그 맞은편엔 (벌건 얼굴의) 리틀 스윌스가 앉는다. 친구들이 그들 곁에 모여들어 일류 재능을 응원한다. 저녁이 무르익을 무렵, 리틀 스윌스가 말한다. \"여러분, 허락해주신다면, 오늘 이곳에서 벌어진 실제 삶의 한 장면을 짧게 묘사해보겠습니다.\" 갈채와 격려가 쏟아진다. 그는 스윌스로 방을 나갔다가, 검시관이 되어 들어온다(실은 눈곱만큼도 닮지 않았다). 그러고는 피아노 반주를 곁들인 흥겨운 막간을 사이사이 끼워가며 검시를 묘사하는데, 후렴은 이렇다. 그분의(검시관의) 티피 톨 리 돌, 티피 톨 로 돌, 티피 톨 리 돌, 디!\n\n땡땡거리던 피아노가 마침내 잠잠해지고, 화음 모임 친구들은 저마다 제 베개로 모여든다. 그러자 이제 마지막 이승의 거처에 뉘인 그 외로운 형체 둘레에 고요가 깃들고, 밤의 적막한 몇 시간 동안 덧문에 그려진 앙상한 눈들이 그를 지켜본다. 만일 이 가엾은 사내가 여기 이렇게 누워 있는 모습을, 한때 자기 품에 안겨 사랑 어린 얼굴을 우러러보던 갓난아이의 보드라운 손이 어찌 감싸 쥐어야 할지도 모른 채 그 목덜미로 기어오르던 — 그 젖을 물리던 어머니가 예언처럼 미리 보았더라면, 그 광경이 얼마나 있을 수 없는 일로 보였겠는가! 오, 더 환했던 시절에, 이제는 꺼져버린 그의 가슴속 불길이 그를 마음에 품은 어느 여인을 향해 단 한 번이라도 타올랐다면, 이 잿더미가 땅 위에 놓인 지금 그녀는 어디 있는가!\n\n쿡스 코트 스내그스비 씨네는 도무지 안식의 밤이 아닌데, 거스터가 — 스내그스비 씨 본인이 인정하듯, 너무 까놓고 말하긴 뭣하지만 — 발작을 한 번에서 스무 번으로 줄줄이 일으키며 잠을 죽이고 있기 때문이다. 이 발작의 빌미는 거스터에게 여린 마음과 더불어 어쩌면 상상력이라 할 만한 어떤 예민한 기질이 있다는 데 있다 — 투팅과 그녀의 수호성인만 아니었더라면 말이다. 그게 무엇이든, 어쨌든 그것이 차 마시는 시각에 스내그스비 씨가 들려준, 자기가 거든 그 조사 이야기에 어찌나 끔찍하게 사로잡혔던지, 저녁 식사 때 그녀는 둥근 네덜란드 치즈 하나를 앞세워 날리며 부엌으로 나동그라지더니 유난히 긴 발작에 빠졌고, 거기서 겨우 빠져나오자마자 또 다른 발작으로, 또 다른 발작으로, 그렇게 짧은 틈을 사이에 둔 발작의 사슬을 줄줄이 이어갔다. 그녀는 그 짧은 틈마저 가련하게도, 자기가 \"아주 정신이 들거든\" 내보내지 말아 달라고 스내그스비 부인에게 애원하는 데, 또 자기를 돌바닥에 뉘어놓고 다들 자러 가라고 온 식구에게 호소하는 데 써먹는다. 그리하여 마침내 커시터 스트리트 자그마한 낙농장의 수탉이 동틀 녘을 두고 그 사심 없는 황홀경에 빠져드는 소리를 듣고서, 스내그스비 씨는 더없이 참을성 많은 사람인데도 긴 한숨을 내쉬며 말한다. \"난 정말이지 네가 죽은 줄로만 알았다!\"\n* 투팅: 런던 외곽의 빈민 위탁 양육 시설이 있던 곳. 거스터가 그곳 출신임을 암시하며, 열악한 환경 탓에 상상력이라 할 만한 것이 제대로 자라지 못했다는 풍자다.\n\n이 신바람 난 닭이 그토록 기를 쓸 때 대체 무슨 문제를 매듭짓는다고 여기는지, 아니면 제게 조금도 중할 게 없는 일을 두고 어찌 이리 울어대는지는(하기야 사람도 갖가지 의기양양한 공식 자리에서 이렇게 울어대긴 한다) 그놈 사정이다. 어쨌든 동이 트고, 아침이 오고, 정오가 온다는 것으로 족하다.\n\n그러자 그 민첩하고 영민한 자가 — 그렇게 조간신문에 실린 그자가 — 빈민 일행을 거느리고 크룩 씨네로 와서, 여기 떠나간 우리 형제의 시신을 둘러메고 사방이 꽉 막힌 어느 묘지로 향한다. 역병이 들끓고 추악한 그곳에서는 떠나가지 않은 우리 형제자매의 몸으로 악성 질병이 옮겨 가는 한편, 관청 뒷계단을 어슬렁대는 우리 형제자매는 — 차라리 그들이 떠나갔더라면, 오, 그랬더라면! — 더없이 흡족하고 흐뭇해한다. 터키인이라면 야만스러운 가증으로 여겨 마다하고 카프레인이라면 몸서리쳤을 짐승 우리 같은 한 뙈기 땅으로, 그들은 여기 떠나간 우리 형제를 데려와 기독교식 장례를 치러준다.\n* 카프레: 남아프리카의 한 부족을 일컫던 옛말로, 당시 영국인이 '미개인'의 본보기로 흔히 들먹이던 표현이다.\n\n사방으로 집들이 내려다보는 가운데 — 다만 김이 무럭무럭 오르는 자그마한 굴 같은 골목 하나만이 무쇠 대문으로 통하는 곳에서 — 삶의 온갖 악행이 죽음에 바짝 붙어 벌어지고, 죽음의 온갖 독한 기운이 삶에 바짝 붙어 벌어지는 이곳에서, 그들은 여기 떠나간 우리 형제를 한두 자쯤 내려놓는다. 여기 그를 부패 속에 씨 뿌려, 부패 속에서 일으켜 세워지게 한다. 숱한 병상 머리맡을 떠도는 복수의 유령으로, 또 문명과 야만이 이 잘난 척하는 섬나라를 어떻게 나란히 걸어왔는지를 후세에 증언하는 부끄러운 증거로.\n\n오라, 밤이여, 오라, 어둠이여, 이런 곳에는 아무리 빨리 와도 이르지 않고 아무리 오래 머물러도 길지 않으니! 오라, 흉한 집들의 창마다 듬성듬성 깃드는 불빛이여. 그리고 그 안에서 못된 짓을 저지르는 그대들이여, 최소한 이 끔찍한 광경만은 가려둔 채 저지르라! 오라, 무쇠 대문 위에서 그토록 음울하게 타오르는 가스 불길이여, 그 대문에는 독한 공기가 손에 닿으면 끈적이는 마녀의 고약을 발라놓는구나! 그대가 지나는 행인마다 붙잡고 \"여기를 보시오!\" 하고 외쳐줌이 마땅하리라.\n\n밤과 더불어, 구부정한 형체 하나가 굴 같은 골목을 지나 무쇠 대문 바깥으로 다가온다. 그것은 두 손으로 대문을 붙잡고 창살 사이로 안을 들여다보며, 한동안 그렇게 서서 들여다본다.\n\n그러다가 들고 온 낡은 빗자루로, 그것은 가만가만 계단을 쓸어 아치 길을 깨끗이 한다. 그것은 더없이 부지런하고 야무지게 그렇게 하고는, 다시 한동안 안을 들여다보더니, 이내 자리를 뜬다.\n\n조, 너로구나? 그래, 그래! 비록 퇴짜 맞은 증인이요, 사람의 손보다 더 큰 손이 자기한테 무슨 일을 하실지 \"콕 집어 말하진 못하\"는 처지일망정, 너는 아주 캄캄한 바깥 어둠 속에 있는 건 아니로구나. 네가 중얼거린 이 까닭 속에 어딘가 머나먼 빛줄기 같은 것이 깃들어 있으니. \"그분은 저한테 참 잘해주셨어요, 정말요!\"\n\n링컨셔에 마침내 비가 그쳤고, 체스니 월드는 다시 생기를 찾았다. 라운스웰 부인은 손님맞이 채비로 분주하니, 레스터 경과 마님이 파리에서 돌아오기 때문이다. 유행 소식통은 이 사실을 알아내어 어둠에 잠긴 잉글랜드에 그 기쁜 소식을 전한다. 또한 소식통은 그분들이 링컨셔의 유서 깊고 인심 후한 가문의 저택에서 사교계 최고 명사들로 이루어진 화려하고 고귀한 모임을 베풀 거라는 사실까지 알아냈다(유행 소식통은 영어에는 약하지만 프랑스어 앞에서는 새 기운을 얻은 거인이다).\n\n화려하고 고귀한 모임의 영예를 더하고, 덤으로 체스니 월드의 영예까지 더하기 위해, 정원에 놓인 다리의 부서진 아치가 손질되었다. 이제 제 경계 안으로 물러나 다시 우아하게 다리 밑을 흐르는 물줄기가 저택에서 바라보는 풍경에 한몫한다. 맑고 차가운 햇살이 메마른 숲속으로 비스듬히 스며들어, 매서운 바람이 낙엽을 흩날리고 이끼를 말리는 광경을 흡족하게 굽어본다. 햇살은 정원을 가로질러 구름의 움직이는 그림자를 뒤쫓다가, 끝내 붙잡지 못한 채 온종일 뒤만 쫓는다. 창문을 들여다보고는 조상들의 초상화에 화가가 미처 생각지도 못한 빛줄기와 빛무리를 드리운다. 큰 벽난로 위에 걸린 마님의 초상화를 비스듬히 가로질러, 햇살은 널찍한 사선 띠 같은 빛을 던지는데, 그 빛은 비뚤어진 채 난로 바닥으로 내리꽂혀 그것을 찢어발기는 듯하다.\n\n바로 그 차가운 햇살과 바로 그 매서운 바람을 뚫고, 마님과 레스터 경은 여행용 마차를 타고(마님의 하녀와 레스터 경의 하인은 뒷자리에서 다정하게 앉아) 집으로 향한다. 방울 소리며 채찍 휘두르는 소리가 요란하게 울리고, 안장 없는 말 두 마리와 번쩍이는 모자에 장화를 신고 갈기와 꼬리를 휘날리는 켄타우로스 같은 마부 두 사람이 거세게 날뛰는 가운데, 마차는 방돔 광장에 자리한 호텔 브리스톨의 마당을 덜컹덜컹 빠져나가, 햇빛과 그늘이 바둑판처럼 얼룩진 리볼리 거리의 회랑과 머리 잘린 왕과 왕비의 불운한 궁전 정원 사이를 달려, 콩코르드 광장과 엘리제 들판과 별의 문을 지나, 파리를 벗어난다.\n\n사실대로 말하자면, 아무리 빨리 떠나도 모자랄 지경이니, 이곳에서마저 데드록 마님은 지루해 죽을 지경이었기 때문이다. 음악회며 모임이며 오페라며 연극이며 마차 산책이며, 닳고 닳은 하늘 아래 마님에게 새로운 것이라곤 하나도 없다. 바로 지난 일요일만 해도, 가엾은 가난뱅이들이 흥겨워하던 그날—성벽 안에서는 궁전 정원의 다듬은 나무와 조각상들 사이에서 아이들과 놀았고, 묘기 부리는 개와 목마 덕분에 한층 더 낙원다워진 엘리제 들판을 스무 명씩 나란히 줄지어 거닐었으며, 틈틈이 (몇몇은) 침침한 노트르담 대성당으로 스며들어, 바람에 흔들리는 작은 양초가 가득 꽂힌 녹슨 자그마한 촛대의 불빛 아래 기둥 발치에서 두어 마디 기도를 올렸고, 성벽 밖에서는 춤추고 사랑을 나누고 술을 마시고 담배를 피우고 무덤을 찾고 당구와 카드와 도미노를 치고 돌팔이 의술을 펴는 등 살아 있는 것이든 죽은 것이든 살벌한 온갖 찌꺼기로 파리를 에워싸던 그날—바로 지난 일요일에도, 마님은 권태의 황량함 속에서 절망 거인의 손아귀에 붙들린 채, 자기 하녀가 활기차다는 이유만으로 하마터면 미워할 뻔했다.\n\n그러니 마님은 파리에서 아무리 빨리 떠나도 모자란다. 영혼의 권태가 뒤에 깔린 만큼 앞에도 깔려 있으니—마님의 요정 아리엘이 그 권태로 온 땅을 한 바퀴 띠처럼 둘러놓았고, 그 띠는 풀 길이 없다—그래도 한 가지 불완전한 처방이라면, 그것을 맛본 마지막 장소에서 언제나 달아나는 것이다. 그러니 파리를 멀리 뒤로 내던지고, 겨울 나무가 끝없이 늘어선 가로수길과 교차로로 바꿔치자! 그리고 다음번에 파리를 바라볼 때는, 별의 문이 햇빛에 반짝이는 흰 점으로 보일 만큼, 도시가 들판에 솟은 한낱 흙무더기로 보일 만큼 몇 리는 떨어진 곳에서 보도록 하자—그 흙무더기에서 두 개의 검은 사각 탑이 솟아오르고, 빛과 그림자가 야곱의 꿈에 나온 천사들처럼 비스듬히 그 위로 내려앉도록!\n\n레스터 경은 대체로 흡족한 상태이고, 좀처럼 지루해하는 법이 없다. 달리 할 일이 없을 때면 언제든 자기 자신의 위대함을 곱씹어볼 수 있기 때문이다. 그토록 무궁무진한 화제를 지녔다는 것은 사람에게 상당한 이점이다. 편지를 다 읽고 나면, 그는 마차 구석에 등을 기대고 대체로 사회에서 자기가 차지하는 비중을 되새겨본다.\n\n\"오늘 아침엔 편지가 유난히 많이 왔나 봐요?\" 한참 만에 마님이 말한다. 책 읽기에 지친 참이다. 이십 마일을 오는 동안 겨우 한 쪽을 읽었다.\n\n\"아무것도 없는 거요, 그래도. 정말 아무것도.\"\n\n\"토킹혼 씨의 긴 편지가 한 장 있던 것 같던데요?\"\n\n\"당신은 뭐든 다 보는군.\" 레스터 경이 감탄하며 말한다.\n\n\"하!\" 마님이 한숨짓는다. \"사람치고 제일 지긋지긋한 양반이지요!\"\n\n\"그가—이거 정말 미안하구려—그가,\" 레스터 경이 편지를 골라 펼치며 말한다, \"당신에게 전갈을 보냈소. 추신에 이르렀을 때 마침 말을 바꾸느라 멈춰서 그만 까맣게 잊었구려. 부디 용서하시오. 그가 말하길—\" 레스터 경이 안경을 꺼내 맞추는 데 어찌나 오래 걸리는지 마님이 살짝 짜증스러운 기색을 보인다. \"그가 말하길 '통행권 문제에 관하여—' 미안하오, 그 대목이 아니구려. 그가 말하길—그래! 여기 있구려! 그가 말하길, '마님께 정중히 안부를 전합니다. 부디 환경을 바꾸시어 효험을 보셨기를 바랍니다. 마님께서 흥미를 가지실 만한 일이라 여쭙건대, 마님께서 그토록 강하게 호기심을 보이셨던 대법원 소송의 진술서를 베껴 쓴 그 사람과 관련하여, 돌아오시면 말씀드릴 것이 있다고 전해 주시겠습니까. 그자를 만났습니다.'\"\n\n마님이 몸을 앞으로 기울여 창밖을 내다본다.\n\n\"이게 그 전갈이오.\" 레스터 경이 말한다.\n\n\"좀 걷고 싶어요.\" 여전히 창밖을 내다보며 마님이 말한다.\n\n\"걷겠다고?\" 레스터 경이 놀란 어조로 되묻는다.\n\n\"좀 걷고 싶어요.\" 마님이 또렷하기 그지없게 말한다. \"마차를 세워 주세요.\"\n\n마차가 멈추고, 다정한 하인이 뒷자리에서 내려와 문을 열고 발판을 내리니, 마님이 손을 조급하게 움직여 보인 명령에 따른 것이다. 마님이 어찌나 잽싸게 내려 어찌나 잽싸게 걸어가는지, 레스터 경은 그 깍듯한 예의에도 불구하고 그녀를 거들지 못한 채 뒤에 처진다. 일이 분쯤 지나서야 그는 그녀를 따라잡는다. 마님은 미소 짓고, 무척 아름다워 보이고, 그의 팔을 잡고, 사분의 일 마일쯤 그와 함께 어슬렁거리다가, 몹시 지루해하더니, 다시 마차 자리로 돌아온다.\n\n덜컹거림과 달그락거림은 사흘 가운데 거의 내내 이어지고, 방울 소리와 채찍 소리도 더하거나 덜한 채 따르며, 켄타우로스와 안장 없는 말이 날뛰는 것도 더하거나 덜한 채 이어진다. 묵어가는 호텔마다 두 사람이 서로에게 보이는 궁정식 예의는 두루 감탄을 자아내는 화젯거리다. 황금 원숭이 여관의 안주인 마담 말로는, 나리께서 마님보다는 좀 연세가 있고 그 다정한 아버지뻘은 됨직하지만, 두 분이 서로 사랑한다는 건 한눈에 알아볼 수 있단다. 백발이 성성한 나리께서 모자를 손에 들고 서서 마님이 마차에 오르고 내리는 걸 거드는 모습을 보라. 나리의 예의에 그토록 고마워하며 우아한 고개를 살짝 숙이고 그토록 고상한 손가락을 내미는 마님을 보라! 황홀할 지경이다!\n\n바다는 위대한 인물을 알아보는 안목 따위는 없어서, 그들을 송사리처럼 함부로 흔들어댄다. 바다는 레스터 경에게 늘 모질게 굴어, 그의 얼굴을 세이지 치즈처럼 푸르뎅뎅하게 얼룩지게 하고, 그의 귀족적 체질에 음울한 혁명을 일으킨다. 바다는 그에게 자연이 낳은 급진파다. 그래도 그의 위엄은 멈춰 추스른 뒤 그 고비를 넘기고, 그는 마님과 함께 체스니 월드로 향하는 길에 런던에서 단 하룻밤만 묵으며 링컨셔로 나아간다.\n\n바로 그 차가운 햇살을 뚫고—날이 저물수록 더 차가워지는 그 햇살을, 또 바로 그 매서운 바람을 뚫고—헐벗은 나무들의 따로따로 드리운 그림자가 숲속에서 한데 어우러져 어두워지고, 서쪽 모퉁이가 하늘에 쌓인 불더미에 물든 유령의 길이 다가오는 밤에 몸을 맡기는 가운데, 두 사람은 마차를 타고 정원으로 들어선다. 느릅나무 가로수길 높다란 둥지에서 흔들리던 떼까마귀들은 마차가 그 밑을 지날 때 마차에 누가 탔는지를 두고 갑론을박하는 듯하니, 어떤 놈들은 레스터 경과 마님이 내려오셨다고 동의하고, 어떤 놈들은 그걸 인정하지 않으려는 불평꾼들과 논쟁을 벌이다가, 이제 다들 그 문제를 다 매듭지었다고 여기는가 싶더니, 또다시 격렬한 논쟁으로 와르르 터져 나오는데, 끝까지 마지막으로 반박하는 까악 소리를 기어이 보태려 드는 고집스럽고 졸음에 겨운 한 마리가 부추긴 탓이다. 까마귀들이 흔들리고 까악거리도록 내버려둔 채, 여행용 마차는 저택으로 굴러가니, 그곳 창문 몇몇으로는 난롯불이 따뜻하게 비치지만, 그 수가 많지 않아 어둑해지는 저택 정면의 거대한 덩어리에 사람 사는 집다운 표정을 주기에는 모자란다. 하지만 화려하고 고귀한 모임이 곧 그 일을 해줄 것이다.\n\n라운스웰 부인이 시중을 들러 나와, 레스터 경이 늘 그러듯 내미는 악수를 깊이 무릎 굽혀 인사하며 받는다.\n\n\"잘 지냈소, 라운스웰 부인? 보니 반갑구려.\"\n\n\"건강한 모습으로 맞이하는 영예를 누리게 되었으면 합니다, 레스터 경?\"\n\n\"아주 건강하다오, 라운스웰 부인.\"\n\n\"마님께서도 더없이 어여쁘고 건강해 보이십니다.\" 라운스웰 부인이 또 한 번 무릎 굽혀 인사하며 말한다.\n\n마님은 말을 헤프게 쓰지 않고, 바랄 수 있는 만큼은 지쳐도 건강하다는 뜻을 내비친다.\n\n그런데 가정부 뒤편 멀찍이 로사가 서 있다. 다른 건 다 정복했을지 몰라도 관찰의 민첩함만은 누그러뜨리지 못한 마님이 묻는다. \"저 아가씨는 누구지?\"\n\n\"제가 가르치는 어린 제자입니다, 마님. 로사라고 합니다.\"\n\n\"이리 오너라, 로사!\" 데드록 마님은 흥미를 보이는 기색까지 띠며 손짓해 부른다. \"얘야, 네가 얼마나 예쁜지 알고 있느냐?\" 두 집게손가락으로 그녀의 어깨를 만지며 마님이 말한다.\n\n로사는 무척 부끄러워하며 말한다. \"아니요, 마님!\" 그러고는 눈길을 올렸다 내렸다 어디를 봐야 할지 모르는데, 그럴수록 더 예뻐 보인다.\n\n\"몇 살이지?\"\n\n\"열아홉입니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"열아홉이라.\" 마님이 생각에 잠겨 되뇐다. \"아첨에 휘둘려 사람 버리지 않게 조심하거라.\"\n\n\"네, 마님.\"\n\n마님은 보조개 팬 그녀의 뺨을 그 섬세한 장갑 낀 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리고는 떡갈나무 계단 발치로 걸어가니, 그곳에서 레스터 경이 기사다운 호위 노릇을 하려고 그녀를 기다린다. 벽판에 그려진, 실물 크기에 실물만큼이나 따분한 데드록 가문의 한 노인이 멀거니 내려다보는데, 이를 어찌 받아들여야 할지 모르겠다는 표정이니, 이는 아마 엘리자베스 여왕 시절 그의 평소 마음 상태였으리라.\n\n그날 저녁, 가정부 방에서 로사는 데드록 마님을 칭송하는 말밖에 할 줄 모른다. 그분은 어찌나 상냥하고, 어찌나 우아하고, 어찌나 아름답고, 어찌나 기품 있는지, 목소리가 어찌나 달콤하고 손길이 어찌나 짜릿한지, 로사는 아직도 그 감촉이 느껴질 정도다! 라운스웰 부인은 이 모든 말을 확인해 주는데, 거기에 개인적인 자부심도 없지 않으나, 단 한 가지 상냥함이라는 점만은 유보한다. 라운스웰 부인은 그 점에 대해서는 썩 확신이 서지 않는다. 그 훌륭한 가문의 어느 누구를, 무엇보다 온 세상이 흠모하는 마님을 헐뜯는 말은 한마디도 하지 않으련다. 하지만 마님이 조금만 더 \"스스럼없이\" 대해 주신다면, 그리 차갑고 거리를 두지만 않으신다면, 더 상냥해 보이실 거라고 라운스웰 부인은 생각한다.\n\n\"거의 안타까울 지경이지요.\" 라운스웰 부인이 덧붙인다—데드록 가문의 일이라는 명백한 섭리 안에서 무엇이 지금보다 더 나을 수 있으리라 여기는 것 자체가 불경에 가깝기에 \"거의\"라고만 한 것이다—\"마님께 가족이 없다는 게 말이다. 만약 따님이 하나 있어서, 다 자란 아가씨가 있어서 마님 마음을 사로잡았더라면, 마님께 모자란 단 한 가지 미덕을 채우셨을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그랬다간 마님이 더더욱 거만해지셨을지도 모르잖아요, 할머니?\" 와트가 말하는데, 워낙 착한 손자라 집에 갔다가 다시 돌아온 참이다.\n\n\"더더욱이라느니 하는 말은, 얘야,\" 가정부가 위엄 있게 대꾸한다, \"마님을 두고 어떤 흠이든 갖다 붙이는 데 내가 입에 담을 말도 아니고—귀에 담을 말도 아니다.\"\n\n\"죄송해요, 할머니. 하지만 마님은 거만하시잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"거만하시다면, 거만하실 만한 이유가 있어서다. 데드록 가문은 늘 그럴 만한 이유가 있단다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 와트가 말한다. \"그렇다면 평민들 보라고 기도서에 적힌, 거만과 헛된 영광에 관한 어떤 구절은 빼버리시는 게 좋겠네요. 용서하세요, 할머니! 그냥 농담이에요!\"\n\n\"레스터 경과 데드록 마님은 농담 삼을 분들이 아니다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"레스터 경은 결코 농담거리가 아니지요.\" 와트가 말한다. \"그분께는 삼가 용서를 빕니다. 그런데 할머니, 가문 어른들과 손님들이 다 내려와 계셔도, 여느 나그네처럼 제가 데드록 암스 여관에 하루 이틀 더 머무는 데에는 별 거리낄 게 없겠지요?\"\n\n\"그야 세상에 거리낄 게 뭐가 있겠니, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그거 다행이네요.\" 와트가 말한다. \"이 아름다운 동네를 더 알고 싶은 마음이 이루 말할 수 없거든요.\"\n\n그는 마침 로사를 흘끗 보는데, 로사는 눈을 내리깔고 정말이지 무척 수줍어한다. 하지만 옛 미신대로라면 화끈거려야 할 것은 로사의 싱그럽고 발그레한 뺨이 아니라 두 귀일 터이니, 지금 이 순간 마님의 하녀가 로사에 대해 유난한 기세로 떠들어대고 있기 때문이다.\n\n마님의 하녀는 서른두 살 난 프랑스 여자로, 아비뇽과 마르세유 부근 남쪽 어디 출신이다. 검은 머리에 눈이 큰 갈색 피부의 여자인데, 고양이 같은 입매와 얼굴 전체에 어딘가 거북하게 팽팽한 긴장감만 아니라면 미인일 텐데, 그 탓에 턱이 너무 사납고 두개골이 너무 도드라진다. 그 골격에는 어딘가 형언하기 어려운 예리함과 핼쑥함이 감돌고, 고개를 돌리지 않은 채 눈꼬리로 곁눈질하며 살피는 버릇이 있으니, 그 버릇은—특히 기분이 언짢고 칼붙이가 가까이 있을 때면—없는 편이 차라리 나을 것이다. 옷차림과 자잘한 장신구가 취향 좋게 갖춰져 있어도, 이런 거슬리는 점들이 스스로 드러나서, 그녀는 어설프게 길든 아주 단정한 암늑대처럼 돌아다니는 듯하다. 제 직분에 걸맞은 온갖 지식을 두루 갖춘 데다, 영어 실력까지 거의 영국 여자에 맞먹어, 마님의 관심을 끌었다는 이유로 로사에게 퍼부을 말이 모자랄 리 없으니, 저녁 식탁에 앉아 그 말을 어찌나 음산한 조롱을 담아 쏟아내는지, 함께 앉은 그 다정한 하인은 그녀가 마침내 그 식사의 숟가락 단계에 이르자 차라리 안도할 정도다.\n\n하, 하, 하! 그녀, 오르탕스는 마님을 모신 지 오 년인데도 늘 거리를 두고 부려졌는데, 이 인형 같은, 이 꼭두각시 같은 계집애가 집에 도착하자마자 마님의 귀여움을 받다니—그야말로 귀여움을 받다니! 하, 하, 하! \"얘야, 네가 얼마나 예쁜지 알고 있느냐?\" \"아니요, 마님.\" 그건 옳은 대답이지! \"그래 몇 살이냐, 얘야! 아첨에 휘둘려 사람 버리지 않게 조심하거라, 얘야!\" 오, 어찌나 우스운지! 무엇보다 최고의 구경거리다.\n\n요컨대 어찌나 대단한 구경거리인지 오르탕스 양은 그 일을 잊지 못하니, 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 식사 자리에서, 같은 처지로 손님 무리를 따라온 동향 여자들이나 다른 이들과 함께 있을 때조차도, 그 농담을 떠올리며 말없이 즐기는 데 빠져든다—그 즐거움은 그녀 특유의 흥겨운 방식으로, 곧 한층 더 팽팽해진 얼굴과, 꾹 다물어 가늘게 늘어난 입술과, 곁눈질로 드러나는데, 이 유머에 대한 강렬한 음미는, 마님이 곁에 없을 때면 마님의 거울들에 곧잘 비친다.\n\n이제 집 안의 거울이란 거울은 죄다 동원되었으니, 그중 많은 것은 오랫동안 텅 비어 있던 끝이다. 거울은 잘생긴 얼굴, 새치름하게 웃는 얼굴, 젊은 얼굴, 늙기를 마다하는 일흔 줄 얼굴을 비춘다. 체스니 월드에서 일이 주의 일월을 보내러 온 얼굴들의 총집합인데, 주님 앞의 막강한 사냥꾼 같은 유행 소식통은 그들이 세인트 제임스 궁정에서 은신처를 박차고 나오는 순간부터 끝내 사냥당해 죽는 순간까지 예리한 후각으로 뒤쫓는다. 링컨셔의 이 저택은 온통 활기가 넘친다. 낮이면 총소리와 사람 목소리가 숲속에 울려 퍼지고, 말 탄 이들과 마차들이 정원 길을 활기차게 만들며, 하인들과 빌붙어 사는 자들이 마을과 데드록 암스 여관을 누빈다. 밤에 멀리 떨어진 나무 사이 틈으로 바라보면, 큰 벽난로 위에 마님의 초상화가 걸린 기다란 응접실의 한 줄로 늘어선 창문은 검은 액자에 박힌 한 줄의 보석 같다. 일요일이면 으슬으슬 추운 작은 교회가 그 많은 화사한 손님들 덕에 거의 따뜻해질 지경이고, 데드록 가문에 감도는 곰팡내는 은은한 향수에 묻혀버린다.\n\n화려하고 고귀한 모임은 그 안에 적잖은 양의 교양, 분별, 용기, 명예, 미모, 미덕을 품고 있다. 그런데도 그 엄청난 장점들에도 불구하고 어딘가 조금 잘못된 데가 있다. 대체 무엇일까?\n\n멋 부림일까? 멋쟁이 유행을 퍼뜨릴 조지 사세는 이제 없다(더더욱 안타깝다). 빳빳이 풀 먹인 행주 같은 목수건도, 허리 짧은 외투도, 가짜 종아리도, 코르셋도 없다. 그렇게 차려입고 오페라 좌석에서 기쁨에 겨워 까무러쳤다가 다른 곱상한 것들이 목 긴 향수병을 코에 들이대 깨어나는 여자 같은 멋쟁이들을 그린 풍자화도 이제 없다. 가죽 바지에 몸을 욱여넣는 데 장정 넷이 한꺼번에 달려들어 흔들어야 하는 멋쟁이도, 처형이란 처형은 죄다 구경 가는 멋쟁이도, 완두콩 한 알 먹어치웠다고 자책에 시달리는 멋쟁이도 없다. 그렇다면 화려하고 고귀한 모임에는 그럼에도 멋 부림이 있는 것일까, 그것도 좀 더 해로운 종류의, 겉으로 드러나지 않게 표면 아래로 파고들어, 행주처럼 빳빳이 굴거나 제 소화나 망치는 따위—분별 있는 이라면 굳이 트집 잡을 것도 없는 그런 짓보다 덜 무해한 일을 저지르는 멋 부림이?\n\n그렇다, 있다. 감출 수 없다. 체스니 월드에는 이 일월 한 주 동안, 이를테면 종교에서 멋 부림을 차린 최신 유행의 신사 숙녀들이 있다. 그저 감정거리가 권태롭게 그리워서, 평민들이 대체로 만물에 대한 믿음을 잃었다는—그러니까 이미 시험해 보았다가 모자란다고 판명 난 것들에 대한 믿음을 잃었다는 자그마한 멋쟁이식 잡담에 의기투합한 자들 말이다, 마치 천박한 작자가 가짜 실링을 알아보고 나서 영문 모를 이유로 그 가짜 실링에 대한 믿음을 잃기라도 한 양! 시간의 시계 바늘을 거꾸로 돌려 수백 년의 역사를 지워버림으로써 평민들을 무척 그림 같고 신실하게 만들려는 자들 말이다.\n\n또 그만큼 새롭지는 않으나 무척 우아한, 다른 유행의 신사 숙녀들도 있다. 세상에 매끄러운 광택을 입혀 온갖 현실을 짓눌러두기로 의기투합한 자들이다. 이들에게는 모든 것이 나른하고 예뻐야 한다. 영원한 정지를 발견한 자들이다. 어떤 일에도 기뻐하지 않고 어떤 일에도 슬퍼하지 않으려는 자들이다. 어떤 사상에도 흔들리지 않으려는 자들이다. 이들에게는 분을 바르고 시종장처럼 뒷걸음질 치며 시중드는 순수 예술마저도, 지난 세대 모자장이와 재단사의 본을 따라 차려입어야 하고, 무엇보다 진지해지지 않도록, 그리고 흘러가는 시대로부터 어떤 자취도 새겨 받지 않도록 각별히 조심해야 한다.\n\n그다음으로는 자기 당내에서 상당한 명망을 누리며 관직이 무엇인지 익히 아는 부들 경이 있는데, 만찬 뒤에 그는 레스터 데드록 경에게 자못 진지하게, 정말이지 요즘 세상이 어디로 가는지 도무지 모르겠다고 말한다. 토론은 예전의 그 토론이 아니고, 의회는 예전의 그 의회가 아니며, 심지어 내각조차도 예전의 그 내각이 아니다. 그는 놀랍게도, 현 정부가 무너진다고 가정할 때, 새 내각을 꾸리는 데 왕실의 제한된 선택지가 쿠들 경과 토머스 두들 경 사이에 놓일 거라고 본다—단, 푸들 공작이 구들과 손잡는 게 불가능하다고 가정한다면 말인데, 후들과의 그 사건에서 비롯된 불화 탓에 그게 사실이라고 봐도 무방하다. 그러고 나서 내무부와 하원 원내총무 자리는 주들에게, 재무부는 쿠들에게, 식민부는 루들에게, 외무부는 무들에게 준다면, 누들은 대체 어쩔 셈인가? 그에게 추밀원 의장 자리를 줄 수는 없다. 그건 푸들 몫으로 잡아두었으니까. 그를 산림부에 앉힐 수도 없다. 그건 쿠들에게도 좀처럼 과분하니까. 그러면 어찌 되는가? 누들 자리를 마련해 주지 못하기 때문에 나라가 난파하고, 침몰하고, 산산조각 난다는 것이다(이는 레스터 데드록 경의 애국심에 또렷이 드러난 사실이다)!\n\n다른 한편으로, 하원 의원이자 우경 윌리엄 버피는 식탁 너머로 다른 누군가와 맞서, 나라의 난파는—그건 의심할 여지가 없고, 다만 그 방식만이 문제다—커피 탓이라고 주장한다. 커피가 처음 의회에 들어왔을 때 마땅히 했어야 할 처치를 그에게 했더라면, 그래서 그가 더피에게 넘어가지 못하게 막았더라면, 그를 푸피와 동맹시킬 수 있었을 것이고, 노련한 토론가로서 구피에게 따라붙는 무게를 자기편에 둘 수 있었을 것이고, 후피의 재력을 선거에 활용할 수 있었을 것이고, 세 개 주에서 주피와 쿠피와 루피를 당선시킬 수 있었을 것이고, 무피의 실무 지식과 일머리로 정부를 강화할 수 있었을 것이다. 이 모든 게 지금처럼 한낱 푸피의 변덕에 매달리는 대신에 말이다!\n\n이 점에 관해서, 그리고 몇몇 사소한 화제에 관해서는 의견이 갈린다. 하지만 화려하고 고귀한 모임에는 어느 모로 보나 부들과 그 무리, 그리고 버피와 그 무리 외에는 거론할 자가 없다는 것이 더없이 분명하다. 이들이 무대를 독차지한 위대한 배우들이다. 물론 민중이라는 것도 있다—이따금 말을 걸어주고, 연극 무대에서처럼 함성과 합창을 맡겨 의지할 어느 정도 큰 무리의 단역들 말이다. 하지만 부들과 버피, 그들의 추종자와 가족, 그들의 상속자, 유언 집행인, 유산 관리인, 양수인이야말로 타고난 주연이자 감독이자 지도자이며, 다른 누구도 그 무대에 영영 등장할 수 없다.\n\n이 점에서도 어쩌면 체스니 월드에는, 화려하고 고귀한 모임이 길게 보아 제게 이로우리라 여길 만한 양보다 더 많은 멋 부림이 깃들어 있는지 모른다. 왜냐하면 아무리 고요하고 점잖은 모임이라 해도, 마법사가 제 둘레에 그려놓은 원처럼—원 바깥에서는 무척 기이한 형상들이 활발히 움직이는 게 보이게 마련이기 때문이다. 다만 다른 점이라면, 그것들이 환영이 아니라 현실인 만큼, 안으로 쳐들어올 위험이 그만큼 더 크다는 것이다.\n\n어쨌거나 체스니 월드는 발 디딜 틈 없이 꽉 차서, 변변찮은 방을 받은 하녀들 가슴에 활활 타오르는 억울함이 솟구치니, 도무지 꺼질 줄 모른다. 빈방이라곤 하나뿐이다. 세 번째 등급에 드는 작은 탑 방인데, 소박하지만 안락하게 꾸며져 있고 어딘가 구식 사무실 같은 분위기를 풍긴다. 토킹혼 씨의 방으로, 그가 언제든 들이닥칠 수 있어 다른 누구에게도 결코 내주지 않는 방이다. 그는 아직 오지 않았다. 날씨가 좋을 때면 마을에서 정원을 가로질러 걸어와, 마치 지난번 본 이래 한 번도 자리를 비운 적 없었다는 듯 이 방으로 슬며시 들어와서는, 혹시 자기를 찾을지 모르니 레스터 경에게 자기가 도착했다고 알려달라고 하인에게 청하고, 만찬 십 분 전에 서재 문 그늘에 모습을 드러내는 것이 그의 조용한 버릇이다. 그는 머리 위에서 깃대가 끽끽 우는 탑 방에서 잠들고, 바깥에는 납지붕이 좀 있어서, 이 아래 내려와 있을 때면 날씨 좋은 아침마다 그의 검은 형체가 아침 식사 전에 그 위를 거니는 것을, 떼까마귀의 좀 더 큰 변종처럼 거니는 것을 볼 수 있다.\n\n날마다 만찬 전이면 마님은 어둑한 서재에서 그를 찾지만, 그는 거기 없다. 날마다 만찬 자리에서 마님은 식탁을 죽 훑어 그가 막 도착했다면 맞이하려고 비워두었을 빈자리를 찾지만, 빈자리는 없다. 밤마다 마님은 무심한 척 하녀에게 묻는다. \"토킹혼 씨는 오셨느냐?\"\n\n밤마다 대답은 한결같다. \"아니요, 마님, 아직입니다.\"\n\n어느 밤, 머리를 풀어 내리던 마님은 이 대답을 듣고 깊은 생각에 잠겨 자신을 잊고 있다가, 맞은편 거울에 비친 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 제 얼굴을 발견하고, 또 자기를 호기심 어린 눈초리로 살피는 검은 두 눈을 발견한다.\n\n\"이거 좀 신경 써 다오.\" 그러자 마님이 거울에 비친 오르탕스를 향해 말한다. \"네 할 일에 말이다. 네 미모는 다른 때에 감상하면 되잖느냐.\"\n\n\"송구합니다! 마님의 미모를 본 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"그건,\" 마님이 말한다, \"전혀 감상할 필요 없느니라.\"\n\n마침내 어느 오후, 해 지기 조금 전, 지난 한두 시간 유령의 길을 활기차게 만들던 화사한 무리들이 죄다 흩어지고 테라스에는 레스터 경과 마님만 남았을 때, 토킹혼 씨가 모습을 드러낸다. 그는 늘 그러듯 한결같이 절도 있는 걸음으로 두 사람에게 다가오는데, 그 걸음은 빨라지는 법도 느려지는 법도 없다. 그는 늘 그러듯 무표정한 가면을—그것이 가면이라면—쓰고, 몸의 마디마디와 옷의 주름마다 가문의 비밀을 지니고 다닌다. 그가 온 영혼을 그 귀하신 분들에게 바치는지, 아니면 자기가 파는 봉사 이상은 아무것도 내주지 않는지는 그만의 개인적 비밀이다. 그는 의뢰인들의 비밀을 지키듯 그 비밀을 지킨다. 그 일에서는 그 자신이 제 의뢰인이니, 결코 스스로를 저버리지 않는다.\n\n\"잘 지냈소, 토킹혼 씨?\" 레스터 경이 손을 내밀며 말한다.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 아주 잘 지낸다. 레스터 경도 아주 잘 지낸다. 마님도 아주 잘 지낸다. 모두 더없이 흡족하다. 변호사는 뒷짐을 진 채 레스터 경 옆에 서서 테라스를 따라 걷는다. 마님은 반대편에서 걷는다.\n\n\"좀 더 일찍 올 줄 알았소만.\" 레스터 경이 말한다. 너그러운 한마디다. 이렇게 말하는 셈이다. \"토킹혼 씨, 당신이 여기 있어 그 존재를 일깨워주지 않을 때조차 우리는 당신의 존재를 기억하고 있다오. 우리는 당신에게 우리 마음 한 조각을 베푸는 거요, 보시다시피!\"\n\n그 뜻을 알아챈 토킹혼 씨는 고개를 숙이며 무척 황송하다고 말한다.\n\n\"좀 더 일찍 내려왔어야 했습니다만,\" 그가 해명한다, \"어르신과 보이손 사이의 여러 소송 건에 워낙 매여 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"심성이 아주 고약하게 비뚤어진 작자요.\" 레스터 경이 매섭게 평한다. \"어느 사회에 있든 더없이 위험한 인물이지. 심성이 무척 천박한 작자란 말이오.\"\n\n\"고집이 셉니다.\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"그런 작자에겐 그게 천성이지.\" 레스터 경 자신이 무척이나 고집스러워 보이는 표정으로 말한다. \"그런 말 들어도 조금도 놀랍지 않소.\"\n\n\"문제는 단 하나입니다.\" 변호사가 말을 잇는다. \"어르신께서 뭐든 양보하실 의향이 있으신지요.\"\n\n\"없소, 토킹혼 씨.\" 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"아무것도. 내가 양보를 한다고?\"\n\n\"중요한 것을 두고 드리는 말씀은 아닙니다. 그야 물론 어르신께서 포기하지 않으시리라는 걸 압니다. 사소한 점이라도 말입니다.\"\n\n\"토킹혼 씨,\" 레스터 경이 받는다. \"나와 보이손 씨 사이에 사소한 점이란 있을 수 없소. 한 걸음 더 나아가, 내 권리 가운데 어떤 것이 어찌 사소한 점이 될 수 있을지 도무지 떠올릴 수 없다고 말한다면, 이는 한 개인으로서의 나 자신을 두고 하는 말이라기보다 내가 지킬 책임을 맡은 가문의 지위를 두고 하는 말이오.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 다시 고개를 숙인다. \"이제 지시를 받들었습니다.\" 그가 말한다. \"보이손 씨가 우리를 적잖이 성가시게 할 텐데—\"\n\n\"그런 심성을 지닌 자의 본성이 바로 그것이오, 토킹혼 씨,\" 레스터 경이 그의 말을 가로막는다. \"성가시게 구는 것 말이오. 더없이 심성 고약한, 평등 타령이나 하는 작자요. 오십 년 전이었다면 십중팔구 무슨 선동가 짓을 벌인 죄로 올드 베일리 법정에 끌려가 호되게 벌을 받았을, 아니,\" 레스터 경이 잠시 뜸을 들이고 덧붙인다, \"아니, 교수형에 처해져 내장이 끄집어내지고 사지가 갈가리 찢겼을 작자요.\"\n\n레스터 경은 이 사형 선고를 내리며 위풍당당한 가슴에서 짐을 덜어내는 듯하니, 마치 그 선고를 실제로 집행하는 것 다음으로 흡족한 일이라는 듯하다.\n\n\"하지만 밤이 다가오는구려.\" 그가 말한다. \"마님이 감기 들겠소. 여보, 안으로 들어갑시다.\"\n\n두 사람이 현관 쪽으로 돌아설 때, 데드록 마님이 처음으로 토킹혼 씨에게 말을 건넨다.\n\n\"제가 우연히 필체를 물어본 그 사람에 관해 전갈을 보내셨더군요. 그 일을 기억하시다니 과연 당신답네요. 저는 까맣게 잊고 있었거든요. 당신 전갈 덕에 다시 떠올렸어요. 제가 그런 필체를 두고 무슨 연상을 했던 건지 도무지 모르겠지만, 분명 뭔가 있긴 했어요.\"\n\n\"뭔가 있으셨다고요?\" 토킹혼 씨가 되뇐다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요!\" 마님이 무심하게 대꾸한다. \"분명 뭔가 있었을 거예요. 그런데 정말로 수고를 들여, 그 실제 물건을—그게 뭐였더라!—진술서를 베껴 쓴 사람을 찾아내셨다고요?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"참 묘한 일이네요!\"\n\n두 사람은 일층의 음침한 아침 식사실로 들어서는데, 낮에는 깊숙한 두 개의 창으로 빛이 든다. 지금은 황혼 무렵이다. 난롯불이 벽판에는 밝게, 창유리에는 희미하게 일렁이는데, 그 창유리에는 불길의 차가운 반사 너머로 더 차가운 풍경이 바람에 떨고, 잿빛 안개가 기어가니, 떠도는 구름 떼 말고는 그 안개만이 유일한 나그네다.\n\n마님은 벽난로 모퉁이의 커다란 의자에 나른히 기대앉고, 레스터 경은 맞은편의 또 다른 커다란 의자에 앉는다. 변호사는 난롯불 앞에 서서 한 손을 팔 길이만큼 쭉 뻗어 제 얼굴을 가린다. 그는 그 팔 너머로 마님을 바라본다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 그가 말한다. \"그자에 대해 알아보았고, 찾아냈습니다. 한데 참으로 기이하게도, 찾고 보니 그자가—\"\n\n\"별 특별할 것 없는 사람이었나 보군요!\" 데드록 마님이 나른하게 앞질러 말한다.\n\n\"죽어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"아이고, 저런!\" 레스터 경이 못마땅한 듯 한마디 한다. 그 사실 자체에 충격받았다기보다 그 사실이 입에 오른다는 사실에 충격받은 투다.\n\n\"그자의 하숙으로 안내받아 갔는데—비참하고 가난에 찌든 곳이었지요—찾고 보니 죽어 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"이거 양해해 주시오, 토킹혼 씨.\" 레스터 경이 한마디 한다. \"내 생각엔 말을 아끼는 편이—\"\n\n\"부디, 레스터 경, 이야기를 끝까지 듣게 해줘요\"(마님이 하는 말이다). \"황혼 무렵에 딱 어울리는 이야기네요. 어찌나 충격적인지! 죽었다고요?\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 다시 한번 고개를 숙여 그렇다고 거듭 못 박는다. \"스스로 목숨을 끊은 건지—\"\n\n\"맹세코!\" 레스터 경이 외친다. \"원, 정말이지!\"\n\n\"제발 그 이야기 좀 듣게 해줘요!\" 마님이 말한다.\n\n\"당신이 원하는 거라면 무엇이든, 여보. 하지만 한마디 해야겠는데—\"\n\n\"아니요, 하지 마세요! 계속하세요, 토킹혼 씨.\"\n\n레스터 경의 신사도가 그 점을 양보하지만, 그래도 그는 이런 종류의 누추함을 상류층 사이로 끌어들이는 게 정말이지—정말이지—싶다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드리려던 건,\" 변호사가 흔들림 없는 침착함으로 말을 잇는다, \"그자가 스스로 목숨을 끊었는지 아닌지는 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 범위를 넘어선다는 거였습니다. 다만 그 표현은 이렇게 고쳐야겠지요. 그자가 제 행위로 죽었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없으나, 그것이 스스로의 계획적인 의도에 의한 것인지 아니면 사고에 의한 것인지는 결코 확실히 알 수 없다고 말입니다. 검시 배심은 그자가 우연히 독을 먹었다고 판정했습니다.\"\n\n\"그래 어떤 부류의 사람이었나요,\" 마님이 묻는다, \"그 가엾은 자는?\"\n\n\"말씀드리기가 무척 어렵습니다.\" 변호사가 고개를 저으며 대답한다. \"어찌나 비참하게 살았고 어찌나 방치되었던지, 집시처럼 거뭇한 낯빛에 헝클어진 검은 머리와 수염을 한 꼴이, 천한 자들 중에서도 가장 천한 자로 여겼을 정도입니다. 외과의는 그자가 한때는 겉모습으로 보나 처지로 보나 좀 더 나은 사람이었으리라는 견해를 내놓더군요.\"\n\n\"그 가엾은 자를 뭐라고 불렀나요?\"\n\n\"그자가 스스로 부르던 이름으로 불렀습니다만, 아무도 그자의 이름을 알지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"그자를 돌보던 사람조차도요?\"\n\n\"그자를 돌본 사람은 아무도 없었습니다. 그자는 죽은 채 발견되었지요. 사실, 제가 그자를 발견했습니다.\"\n\n\"더 알아낼 단서라곤 전혀 없었고요?\"\n\n\"전혀요. 다만,\" 변호사가 생각에 잠겨 말한다, \"낡은 여행 가방이 하나 있긴 했습니다만—아니요, 서류 같은 건 없었습니다.\"\n\n이 짧은 대화의 한마디 한마디가 오가는 동안, 데드록 마님과 토킹혼 씨는 평소의 몸가짐에 다른 변화 하나 없이 서로를 무척 빤히 바라보았다—하긴 이토록 별난 주제를 논하다 보면 자연스러운 일이었으리라. 레스터 경은 계단에 걸린 데드록 가문 사람의 그 표정 그대로, 난롯불을 바라보았다. 이야기가 끝나자 그는 위엄 어린 항변을 거듭하며 말하길, 마님 마음속의 어떤 연상도 이 가엾은 작자와는 도저히 이어질 수 없음이 명백하니(그자가 구걸 편지나 써대던 자가 아니라면 말이지만), 마님의 신분과는 그토록 동떨어진 주제에 관해 더는 듣지 않기를 바란다고 한다.\n\n\"확실히, 끔찍한 일들을 한데 모아놓은 셈이네요.\" 마님이 망토와 모피를 추슬러 모으며 말한다. \"하지만 그 순간만큼은 흥미롭긴 하지요! 토킹혼 씨, 미안하지만 제게 문 좀 열어 주세요.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 정중하게 문을 열고는 그녀가 지나가도록 잡아준다. 그녀는 늘 그러듯 지친 듯한 태도와 오만한 우아함을 지닌 채 그의 곁을 바싹 스쳐 지나간다. 두 사람은 만찬에서 다시 마주친다—다음 날 또—여러 날 잇따라 또. 데드록 마님은 언제나 한결같이 지친 여신이니, 숭배자들에게 둘러싸여, 제 신전을 손수 주관하는 와중에도 지루해 죽을 지경에 무섭도록 곧잘 빠진다. 토킹혼 씨는 언제나 한결같이 고귀한 비밀을 담아두는 말없는 보관소이니, 어찌나 묘하게 어울리지 않으면서도 어찌나 완벽하게 제집처럼 편안한지. 두 사람은 같은 벽 안에 갇힌 어느 두 사람만큼이나 서로에게 거의 눈길도 주지 않는 듯하다. 하지만 둘이 영영 서로를 살피고 의심하는지, 영영 무슨 커다란 감춤이 있지 않을까 서로를 못 미더워하는지, 둘이 영영 어느 면에서나 서로를 맞상대할 채비를 갖춘 채 결코 허를 잡히지 않으려 하는지, 상대가 얼마나 많이 아는지를 알아내기 위해 둘 다 무엇이든 내놓으려 하는지—이 모든 것은, 당분간, 각자의 가슴속에 감춰져 있다.\n\n리처드를 무엇으로 키울지를 두고 우리는 여러 차례 상의했습니다. 처음에는 잔다이스 씨가 그러길 바라서 그분 없이 의논했고, 나중에는 그분과 함께 의논했지만, 좀처럼 진전이 보이지 않았습니다. 리처드는 무엇이든 좋다고 했습니다. 잔다이스 씨가 해군에 들어가기엔 이미 나이가 너무 많지 않겠느냐고 의문을 던지자, 리처드는 그 생각도 해봤다며 아마 그럴지도 모르겠다고 했습니다. 잔다이스 씨가 육군은 어떻게 생각하느냐고 묻자, 리처드는 그것도 생각해봤는데 나쁜 생각은 아니라고 했습니다. 잔다이스 씨가 바다를 좋아하던 그 오랜 마음이 그저 흔한 소년다운 끌림인지 아니면 강한 충동인지 스스로 결정해보라고 권하자, 리처드는 정말이지 여러 번 애써봤는데 도무지 알 수가 없다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"이렇게 우유부단한 성격이,\" 잔다이스 씨가 나에게 말했습니다. \"그 애가 태어나면서부터 떠밀려온, 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 불확실과 미루기의 더미 탓이 얼마나 되는지는 내가 함부로 말하지 않겠네. 하지만 대법원이 다른 숱한 죄악과 더불어 그 일부에 책임이 있다는 건 똑똑히 알 수 있어. 대법원이 그 애한테 미루는 버릇을 심어주었거나 굳혀준 거지. 무슨 가망인지도 모르면서 이런저런 가망에 기대고, 모든 걸 미결, 불확실, 혼란으로 치워버리는 버릇 말일세. 훨씬 나이 들고 한결 진중한 사람의 성격도 둘러싼 환경에 따라 바뀔 수 있어. 한창 자라나는 소년의 성격이 그런 영향에 노출되고도 거기서 벗어나기를 바라는 건 지나친 욕심이지.\"\n\n나는 이 말이 옳다고 느꼈습니다. 다만 내 생각을 감히 보태도 된다면, 리처드의 교육이 그런 영향을 막아내거나 그의 성격을 바로잡아주지 못한 것이 무척 안타까웠습니다. 그는 팔 년 동안 퍼블릭 스쿨에 다니며, 내가 듣기로는 여러 종류의 라틴어 시를 더없이 훌륭하게 짓는 법을 배웠다고 합니다. 그러나 그의 타고난 성향이 무엇인지, 약점이 어디 있는지를 알아내거나, 어떤 종류의 지식이든 그에게 맞추는 일이 누군가의 책무였다는 말은 들어본 적이 없습니다. 정작 그가 시에 맞춰졌고, 시 짓는 솜씨를 어찌나 완벽하게 익혔던지, 성년이 될 때까지 학교에 남아 있었다면 짓는 법을 까먹어서 교육의 폭을 넓히기라도 하지 않는 한, 그저 같은 시를 짓고 또 짓는 일밖에 못 했으리라 싶습니다. 그래도 그 시들이 무척 아름답고, 무척 사람을 키워주고, 인생의 숱한 목적에 무척 쓸모가 있으며, 평생토록 늘 기억에 남으리라는 점은 의심하지 않았지만, 리처드가 시를 그토록 열심히 공부하는 대신 누군가가 그를 조금 공부해주었더라면 더 득을 보지 않았을까 하는 의심은 들었습니다.\n\n물론 나는 이 분야를 전혀 몰랐고, 고전 시대 로마나 그리스의 젊은 신사들이 똑같은 정도로 시를 지었는지, 아니 어느 나라의 젊은 신사들이라도 그런 적이 있었는지조차 지금도 모릅니다.\n\n\"내가 뭐가 되는 게 좋을지,\" 리처드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했습니다. \"도무지 모르겠어요. 성직자가 되긴 정말 싫다는 것만 분명하고, 나머지는 동전 던지기예요.\"\n\n\"켄지 씨 쪽으로는 마음이 안 가니?\" 잔다이스 씨가 넌지시 물었습니다.\n\n\"그건 모르겠어요, 어르신!\" 리처드가 대답했습니다. \"전 보트 타기를 좋아하잖아요. 견습 서기들은 물 위에서 꽤 많이 지내니까요. 굉장한 직업이에요!\"\n\n\"외과의는—\" 잔다이스 씨가 넌지시 권했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요, 어르신!\" 리처드가 외쳤습니다.\n\n나는 그가 그 전에 한 번이라도 그 생각을 해본 적이 있는지 의심스러웠습니다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 더없이 열을 올리며 거듭 말했습니다. \"드디어 찾았어요. 왕립외과의사회 회원이요!\"\n\n그는 진심으로 껄껄 웃었지만, 웃음으로 그 마음을 떨쳐낼 수는 없었습니다. 그는 직업을 정했다고 했고, 생각하면 생각할수록 자신의 운명이 분명하게 느껴진다고 했습니다. 치유하는 기술이야말로 다른 무엇보다 자기에게 딱 맞는 기술이라는 것이었습니다. 그가 무엇에 맞는지 스스로 알아낼 기회가 거의 없었고, 그것을 발견하도록 이끌어준 적도 없었기에, 그저 가장 새로운 생각에 사로잡혀 고민의 수고를 덜게 된 것이 기뻐서 이런 결론에 이른 것은 아닐까 미심쩍었던 나는, 라틴어 시가 흔히 이렇게 끝나는 것인지, 아니면 리처드만 유독 그런 경우인지 궁금했습니다.\n\n잔다이스 씨는 그와 진지하게 이야기를 나누고, 이렇게 중요한 일에서 스스로를 속이지 말라고 그의 분별심에 호소하느라 무척 애를 썼습니다. 리처드는 이런 대화를 마치고 나면 조금 심각해졌지만, 언제나 에이다와 나에게는 다 괜찮다고 말한 다음 곧 다른 이야기를 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 보이손 씨가 외쳤습니다. 그는 이 문제에 강하게 관심을 보였는데—하긴 굳이 말할 필요도 없는 것이, 그는 무엇이든 미적지근하게 하는 법이 없었으니까요. \"기개와 패기가 넘치는 젊은 신사가 그 고귀한 직업에 몸을 바치려 한다니 정말 기쁘구먼! 거기 기개가 넘칠수록 인류에게는 더 좋고, 그 빛나는 기술을 세상에서 불리한 처지로 몰아넣고 즐거워하는 그 돈만 밝히는 부역꾼들과 비열한 사기꾼들에게는 더 나쁜 일이지. 비열하고 천박한 모든 것에 걸고 말하건대,\" 보이손 씨가 외쳤습니다. \"배 위에서 외과의를 다루는 꼴이라니, 해군성 위원회 위원이란 위원은 죄다 두 다리를—두 다리를 다—복합 골절로 만들어버린 다음, 마흔여덟 시간 안에 그 제도를 통째로 바꾸지 않는 한, 자격 있는 의사가 그 다리를 맞추기만 해도 유배형에 처할 죄로 삼고 싶을 지경이라네!\"\n\n\"일주일은 주지 않겠소?\" 잔다이스 씨가 물었습니다.\n\n\"안 되네!\" 보이손 씨가 단호하게 외쳤습니다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 안 돼! 마흔여덟 시간이야! 자치체니 교구니 교구 위원회니, 그 비슷하게 모여서 그런 연설이나 주고받는 멍청한 얼간이 무리로 말하자면, 맙소사, 그자들은 그 비참한 삶의 짧은 여생을 수은 광산에서 일하게 해야 마땅하지. 그자들의 역겨운 영어가 해 아래서 쓰이는 언어를 오염시키지 못하게 하려고만 해도 그래야 하네. 그런 작자들로 말하자면, 지식을 좇는 신사들의 열정을 비열하게 이용해먹고는, 그 인생의 가장 좋은 시절과 오랜 학업과 값비싼 교육이 베푼 헤아릴 수 없는 봉사에 대한 보답으로, 서기조차 받아들이지 않을 만큼 쥐꼬리만 한 보수를 쥐여주는 자들이지. 그런 놈은 하나하나 목을 비틀어버리고, 그 두개골을 외과의사 회관에 진열해서 온 의료계가 들여다보게 하고 싶네. 젊은 회원들이 어려서부터 실제로 재어보고, 두개골이 얼마나 두꺼워질 수 있는지를 깨닫도록 말일세!\"\n\n그는 이 격렬한 선언을 마무리하면서, 더없이 유쾌한 미소로 우리를 둘러보더니, 느닷없이 \"하, 하, 하!\" 하고 천둥처럼 거듭 웃어젖혔습니다. 다른 사람 같았으면 그 힘 빼는 일에 완전히 기진맥진했을 만큼 말입니다.\n\n잔다이스 씨가 권한 거듭된 숙고의 기간이 지나고도 리처드가 여전히 선택을 굳혔다고 말하고, 여전히 에이다와 나에게 똑같이 단호한 태도로 \"다 괜찮다\"고 장담하자, 켄지 씨를 불러 의논하는 편이 좋겠다 싶었습니다. 그리하여 어느 날 켄지 씨가 만찬에 내려와, 의자에 기대앉아, 안경을 빙글빙글 돌리고, 낭랑한 목소리로 말했는데, 내가 어린 소녀였을 때 보았던 모습과 한 치도 다르지 않았습니다.\n\n\"아!\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"그렇지요. 그렇고말고요! 아주 훌륭한 직업입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 아주 훌륭한 직업이지요.\"\n\n\"공부와 준비 과정을 부지런히 밟아야 하지요.\" 후견인이 리처드를 흘긋 보며 말했습니다.\n\n\"오, 그야 두말할 것 없지요.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"부지런히요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 정도의 차이는 있어도 제법 값어치 있는 모든 일에 다 해당하니,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"다른 선택을 했다고 해서 피해 갈 만한 특별한 사정은 아니지요.\"\n\n\"참으로 그렇습니다.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"또 리처드 카스톤 군은—그 고전의 그늘이라고 할까요?—젊은 시절을 보낸 그곳에서 그토록 훌륭하게 처신했으니, 시인은 (내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면) 만들어지는 것이 아니라 태어나는 것이라고들 하는 그 언어로 시를 짓던 습관을, 원리와 실제까지는 아니더라도, 이제 발을 들이려는 그 한층 두드러지게 실용적인 행동의 장에 틀림없이 적용할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"믿으셔도 됩니다.\" 리처드가 거침없는 태도로 말했습니다. \"제가 달려들어서 최선을 다할 거예요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다, 잔다이스 씨!\" 켄지 씨가 부드럽게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했습니다. \"정말이지, 리처드 군이 달려들어서 최선을 다하겠다고 우리에게 장담하니,\" 그 표현들을 음미하듯 부드럽게 고개를 끄덕이면서. \"이제 우리는 그의 포부를 이룰 가장 좋은 방법만 알아보면 되겠다고 말씀드리고 싶습니다. 자, 리처드 군을 충분히 저명한 어느 의사에게 맡기는 일에 관해서요. 지금 염두에 둔 분이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"없는 것 같은데, 릭?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"없어요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇군요!\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"그럼 장소에 관해서는 어떨까요. 그 점에 무슨 특별한 바람이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"아—아니요.\" 리처드가 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇군요!\" 켄지 씨가 다시 말했습니다.\n\n\"전 변화가 좀 있었으면 좋겠어요.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그러니까, 폭넓은 경험을 쌓고 싶다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"두말할 것 없이 꼭 필요한 일이지요.\" 켄지 씨가 응답했습니다. \"이건 쉽게 마련될 것 같습니다, 잔다이스 씨? 우선 충분히 적합한 의사를 찾기만 하면 됩니다. 우리의 바람을—거기다 사례금을 낼 능력까지 있다고 덧붙일까요?—알리는 즉시, 우리의 유일한 어려움은 수많은 의사 가운데 하나를 고르는 일이 되겠지요. 둘째로는, 우리의 나이와 법원의 후견을 받는 처지 때문에 필요해진 그 자질구레한 절차들만 지키면 됩니다. 그러면 머잖아—리처드 군 특유의 명랑한 말투를 빌려 '달려든다'고 할까요—마음껏 일을 추진하게 될 겁니다. 묘한 우연이지만,\" 켄지 씨가 미소에 한 줄기 우수를 띠며 말했습니다. \"우리의 현재 한정된 능력으로는 설명이 필요할 수도, 필요 없을 수도 있는 그런 우연 가운데 하나인데요, 마침 제 사촌 하나가 의료계에 몸담고 있습니다. 그분이라면 보시기에 적합할 수도 있고, 이 제안에 응할 마음이 있을 수도 있겠지요. 어르신을 두고 장담 못 하듯 그분을 두고도 장담은 못 하지만, 어쩌면 그럴 수도 있습니다!\"\n\n이것이 앞날에 트인 길이었기에, 켄지 씨가 자기 사촌을 만나보기로 정해졌습니다. 그리고 잔다이스 씨가 이미 우리를 몇 주 동안 런던에 데려가겠다고 했던 터라, 이튿날 우리는 곧장 그곳을 방문하면서 리처드의 일도 함께 처리하기로 정했습니다.\n\n보이손 씨가 일주일 안에 우리 곁을 떠났고, 우리는 옥스퍼드 거리 근처, 가구상 위층의 쾌적한 하숙에 거처를 잡았습니다. 런던은 우리에게 커다란 경이였습니다. 우리는 몇 시간이고 거리를 쏘다니며 구경거리를 보았는데, 그 구경거리는 우리보다 동이 날 줄을 모르는 듯했습니다. 우리는 큰 극장들도 더없이 즐겁게 두루 돌며 볼 만한 연극은 죄다 보았습니다. 이 이야기를 꺼내는 까닭은, 바로 그 극장에서 다시금 거피 씨 때문에 거북해지기 시작했기 때문입니다.\n\n어느 날 밤 나는 에이다와 함께 특별석 앞쪽에 앉아 있었고, 리처드는 자기가 가장 좋아하는 자리, 에이다의 의자 뒤에 있었는데, 마침 일층 평석을 내려다보다가 거피 씨를 발견했습니다. 그는 머리카락을 머리에 납작하게 붙이고, 얼굴에는 비탄을 가득 담은 채 나를 올려다보고 있었습니다. 공연 내내 나는 그가 배우들은 한 번도 보지 않고 줄곧 나만 바라본다는 것을 느꼈습니다. 그것도 언제나 더없이 깊은 비참과 가장 심오한 낙담을 정성껏 빚어낸 표정으로 말입니다.\n\n그날 밤 내 즐거움은 완전히 망가지고 말았습니다. 너무도 거북하고 너무도 우스꽝스러웠으니까요. 하지만 그때부터 우리는 연극을 보러 갈 때마다 반드시 일층 평석에서 거피 씨를 보았습니다. 그는 언제나 머리카락을 곧게 펴 납작하게 붙이고, 셔츠 깃을 접어 내리고, 어딘가 전반적으로 기운 없는 모습이었습니다. 우리가 들어갈 때 그가 거기 없으면, 나는 그가 오지 않기를 바라기 시작해 잠시나마 무대의 재미에 빠져들었지만, 그러다가 가장 뜻밖의 순간에 영락없이 그 애처로운 눈길과 마주쳤고, 그때부터는 저녁 내내 그 눈길이 나에게 고정되어 있다는 것을 확신하게 되었습니다.\n\n이것이 나를 얼마나 불편하게 했는지 정말이지 이루 말할 수 없습니다. 그가 머리카락이라도 빗어 세우거나 깃이라도 올렸다면 그것만으로도 충분히 끔찍했을 텐데, 그 우스꽝스러운 모습이 늘 나를 응시하고 있다는 것을, 그것도 언제나 그렇게 노골적인 낙담의 상태로 그러고 있다는 것을 안다는 사실이 어찌나 나를 옥죄던지, 나는 연극을 보며 웃지도, 울지도, 움직이지도, 말하지도 못했습니다. 무엇 하나 자연스럽게 할 수가 없는 것 같았습니다. 특별석 뒤쪽으로 가서 거피 씨를 피한다는 것은, 도저히 그럴 수 없었습니다. 리처드와 에이다가 나를 자기들 옆에 두고 싶어 한다는 것을, 그리고 내 자리에 다른 누가 있었다면 그 둘이 그토록 즐겁게 이야기를 나누지 못했으리라는 것을 알았으니까요. 그래서 나는 어디를 봐야 할지 모른 채 그 자리에 앉아 있었습니다. 어디를 보든 거피 씨의 눈이 나를 좇고 있다는 것을 알았으니까요. 그러면서 이 젊은이가 나 때문에 치르고 있는 끔찍한 비용을 생각했습니다.\n\n이따금 잔다이스 씨에게 말할까 하는 생각도 들었습니다. 그러다가는 그 젊은이가 일자리를 잃을지도 모르고, 그러면 내가 그를 망치게 될까 봐 두려웠습니다. 이따금 리처드에게 털어놓을까 하는 생각도 들었지만, 그가 거피 씨와 싸워 눈에 멍이라도 들게 할 가능성 때문에 그만두었습니다. 이따금 그에게 눈살을 찌푸리거나 고개를 저어볼까 생각도 했습니다. 그러다가는 그럴 수 없다는 것을 깨달았습니다. 이따금 그의 어머니에게 편지를 써볼까 따져보기도 했지만, 그것은 편지 왕래를 트는 일이 문제를 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이라는 확신으로 끝나고 말았습니다. 나는 끝내 늘 아무것도 할 수 없다는 결론에 이르렀습니다. 이러는 내내 거피 씨의 끈기는 우리가 가는 어느 극장에든 그를 어김없이 나타나게 했을 뿐 아니라, 우리가 나올 때면 인파 속에 모습을 드러내게 했고, 심지어 우리 마차 뒤에 올라타게까지 했습니다. 거기서 나는 그가 그 무시무시한 쇠창살들 사이에서 두세 번 버둥거리는 것을 분명히 보았습니다. 우리가 집에 돌아온 뒤에는 그가 우리 집 맞은편 기둥 곁을 맴돌았습니다. 우리가 묵던 가구상이 두 거리가 만나는 모퉁이에 있었고, 내 침실 창문이 그 기둥 맞은편이었던 탓에, 나는 위층에 올라갈 때면 창가에 다가가기가 두려웠습니다. (어느 달밤에 실제로 보았듯이) 그가 기둥에 기댄 채 뻔히 감기에 걸려가는 모습을 보게 될까 봐서요. 다행히 거피 씨가 낮에는 일에 매여 있지 않았더라면, 정말이지 나는 그에게서 한순간도 쉴 틈이 없었을 겁니다.\n\n거피 씨가 그토록 유별나게 끼어든 이 흥겨운 나들이를 이어가는 동안에도, 우리를 런던으로 데려오는 데 한몫한 그 일은 소홀히 하지 않았습니다. 켄지 씨의 사촌은 베이엄 배저 씨로, 첼시에서 진료를 잘하고 있었고 그 밖에도 큰 공공 기관에 출근하고 있었습니다. 그는 리처드를 집에 받아들여 학업을 지도하는 데 기꺼이 응했고, 그 학업을 배저 씨의 집에서 유익하게 밟을 수 있을 듯했으며, 배저 씨는 리처드를 마음에 들어 했고, 리처드도 배저 씨가 \"그런대로 마음에 든다\"고 해서, 합의가 이루어지고 대법원장의 동의도 얻어, 모든 것이 정해졌습니다.\n\n리처드와 배저 씨 사이의 일이 마무리된 날, 우리는 모두 배저 씨 댁에서 만찬을 들기로 약속이 되어 있었습니다. 배저 부인의 쪽지에 적힌 대로 우리는 \"그저 가족끼리의 모임\"이 될 참이었고, 거기 모인 부인이라곤 배저 부인 본인뿐이었습니다. 그녀는 응접실에서 갖가지 물건에 둘러싸여 있었는데, 그것들은 그녀가 그림을 조금 그리고, 피아노를 조금 치고, 기타를 조금 치고, 하프를 조금 타고, 노래를 조금 부르고, 바느질을 조금 하고, 책을 조금 읽고, 시를 조금 쓰고, 식물 채집을 조금 한다는 것을 말해주고 있었습니다. 그녀는 내 짐작에 쉰 살쯤 된 부인으로, 젊게 차려입었고 안색이 무척 고왔습니다. 그녀의 자질구레한 재주 목록에 연지를 조금 바른다는 것까지 보탠다 해도, 거기에 무슨 흠이 있다는 뜻은 아닙니다.\n\n베이엄 배저 씨 본인은 혈색이 발그레하고 얼굴이 싱싱하며 산뜻한 인상의 신사로, 목소리는 가냘프고, 치아는 희고, 머리카락은 옅고, 눈은 놀란 듯했는데, 내 짐작에 베이엄 배저 부인보다 몇 살은 아래였습니다. 그는 그녀를 대단히 흠모했지만, 그 주된 까닭은, 그리고 무엇보다 첫째 까닭은, (우리가 보기엔) 그녀가 남편을 셋이나 두었다는 묘한 점이었습니다. 우리가 자리에 앉기가 무섭게 그는 자못 의기양양하게 잔다이스 씨에게 말했습니다. \"제가 베이엄 배저 부인의 세 번째 남편이라곤 좀처럼 짐작 못 하실 겁니다!\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"세 번째입니다!\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"베이엄 배저 부인은 전남편을 둘이나 두었던 부인 같아 보이지 않지요, 서머슨 양?\"\n\n나는 \"전혀 그렇지 않네요!\" 하고 말했습니다.\n\"게다가 더없이 비범한 인물들이었습니다!\" 배저 씨가 은밀한 어조로 말했습니다. \"배저 부인의 첫 남편이었던 왕립 해군의 스워서 함장은 참으로 걸출한 장교였지요. 제 바로 전임자인 딩고 교수의 이름은 유럽 전역에 명성이 자자합니다.\"\n\n배저 부인이 그 말을 듣고 미소 지었습니다.\n\n\"그래요, 여보!\" 배저 씨가 그 미소에 응답했습니다. \"당신이 전남편을 둘이나—둘 다 더없이 걸출한 인물을—두었다고 잔다이스 씨와 서머슨 양께 말씀드리던 참이오. 그런데 두 분도 사람들이 으레 그러듯 믿기 어려워하시더군.\"\n\n\"제가 왕립 해군의 스워서 함장과 결혼했을 때는 겨우 스무 살이었어요.\" 배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"그이와 함께 지중해에 있었으니, 저는 영락없는 뱃사람이지요. 결혼 열두 돌이 되던 날, 저는 딩고 교수의 아내가 되었어요.\"\n\n\"유럽 전역에 명성이 자자한 분이지요.\" 배저 씨가 나직이 덧붙였습니다.\n\n\"그리고 배저 씨와 제가 결혼했을 때도,\" 배저 부인이 말을 이었습니다. \"한 해 중 같은 날에 했답니다. 제가 그 날짜에 정이 들었거든요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 배저 부인은 남편을 셋 두었고—그중 둘은 더없이 걸출한 인물이었으며,\" 배저 씨가 사실들을 정리하며 말했습니다. \"그것도 매번 삼월 이십일일 오전 열한 시에 결혼한 셈이지요!\"\n\n우리는 모두 감탄을 표했습니다.\n\n\"배저 씨의 겸손함만 아니라면,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"외람되지만 그분 말씀을 바로잡아 걸출한 인물이 셋이라고 하고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 잔다이스 씨! 제가 늘 그이한테 하는 말이에요!\" 배저 부인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그런데 여보,\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"내가 당신한테 늘 하는 말이 뭐요? 내가 이룬 의사로서의 명성이 (이건 우리 카스톤 군이 앞으로 가늠해볼 기회가 숱하게 있을 테고) 어떻든, 그걸 깎아내리는 척하려는 게 아니라, 나는 내 명성을 스워서 함장이나 딩고 교수 같은 일류 인물들과 같은 반열에 올려놓을 만큼 그렇게 어리석지—아니, 정말이지,\" 배저 씨가 우리 모두를 향해 말했습니다. \"그렇게 분별없는 사람은 아니라는 거지요. 어쩌면 흥미로우실지도 모르겠습니다, 잔다이스 씨.\" 베이엄 배저 씨가 옆 응접실로 앞장서 들어가며 말을 이었습니다. \"이게 스워서 함장의 초상화입니다. 아프리카 주둔지에서 그곳 열병에 시달리다 귀국했을 때 그린 것이지요. 배저 부인은 너무 누렇다고 합니다만, 참으로 훌륭한 두상이에요. 참으로 훌륭한 두상이지요!\"\n\n우리는 모두 따라 외쳤습니다. \"참으로 훌륭한 두상이네요!\"\n\n\"이걸 볼 때마다 이런 느낌이 듭니다.\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"'이 사람은 내가 직접 만나봤으면 싶은 인물이야!' 스워서 함장이 단연 일류 인물이었다는 것을 인상 깊게 말해주지요. 반대편은 딩고 교수입니다. 제가 잘 알았지요—그분 임종 때 진료도 했고요—꼭 닮았지요! 피아노 위쪽은 스워서 부인이었을 적의 베이엄 배저 부인입니다. 소파 위쪽은 딩고 부인이었을 적의 베이엄 배저 부인이고요. 현재의 베이엄 배저 부인으로 말하면, 제가 원본을 소장하고 있으니 모사본은 없습니다.\"\n\n이제 만찬이 차려졌다는 전갈이 와서 우리는 아래층으로 내려갔습니다. 무척 품격 있는 자리였고, 차림새도 무척 훌륭했습니다. 그러나 함장과 교수는 여전히 배저 씨의 머릿속을 떠나지 않았고, 에이다와 나는 그의 각별한 보살핌을 받는 영광을 누린 터라, 그 두 사람의 덕을 톡톡히 보았습니다.\n\n\"물 드시겠습니까, 서머슨 양? 제가 따라드리지요! 그 잔에는 말고요, 부디. 제임스, 교수의 술잔을 가져오게!\"\n\n에이다가 유리갓 아래 놓인 조화 몇 송이를 무척 감탄하며 보았습니다.\n\n\"이게 어찌나 잘 보존되었는지 놀랍지요!\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"베이엄 배저 부인이 지중해에 있을 때 선물받은 거랍니다.\"\n\n그는 잔다이스 씨에게 클라레 한 잔을 들기를 권했습니다.\n\n\"그 클라레 말고요!\" 그가 말했습니다. \"죄송합니다만! 오늘은 특별한 날이고, 특별한 날에는 제가 마침 가지고 있는 아주 특별한 클라레를 내놓지요. (제임스, 스워서 함장의 포도주를!) 잔다이스 씨, 이건 함장이 들여온 포도주인데, 몇 년 전인지는 굳이 말하지 않겠습니다. 무척 진귀하다 여기실 겁니다. 여보, 당신과 이 포도주를 함께 들 수 있다니 기쁘구려. (제임스, 스워서 함장의 클라레를 마님께!) 여보, 당신의 건강을 위하여!\"\n\n만찬이 끝나고 우리 부인네들이 물러날 때, 우리는 배저 부인의 첫 남편과 둘째 남편을 함께 데리고 갔습니다. 배저 부인은 응접실에서 스워서 함장의 결혼 전 생애와 복무에 관한 전기적 개관을, 이어서 그가 플리머스 항에 정박해 있던 크리플러호 장교들을 위해 그 배 위에서 열린 무도회에서 자기에게 반한 때부터의 한층 자세한 이야기를 우리에게 들려주었습니다.\n\n\"그리운 옛 크리플러호!\" 배저 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했습니다. \"고귀한 배였지요. 단정하고, 말끔하고, 스워서 함장 말마따나 더없이 빈틈없었어요. 제가 가끔 항해 용어를 끼워 넣더라도 양해해주세요. 한때는 영락없는 뱃사람이었으니까요. 스워서 함장은 저 때문에 그 배를 사랑했답니다. 그 배가 더는 현역으로 쓰이지 않게 되었을 때, 그이는 자기가 그 낡은 선체를 살 만큼 부자라면, 우리가 춤 짝으로 서 있던 후갑판 목재에 글귀를 새겨 넣어 자기가 쓰러진 자리를 표시하겠다고 자주 말하곤 했어요—제 돛대 끝에서 쏟아진 포화에 뱃머리부터 고물까지 휩쓸려서 말이지요(스워서 함장의 표현이었어요). 그건 제 두 눈을 두고 그이가 해군식으로 한 말이었답니다.\"\n\n배저 부인은 고개를 젓고, 한숨을 쉬고, 거울을 들여다보았습니다.\n\n\"스워서 함장에서 딩고 교수로 바뀐 것은 엄청난 변화였어요.\" 그녀가 애잔한 미소를 지으며 말을 이었습니다. \"처음엔 무척 실감이 났지요. 제 생활 방식이 통째로 뒤바뀌었으니까요! 하지만 습관에 학문이—특히 학문이—더해지니 거기에 익숙해지더군요. 교수님의 식물 채집 여행에 유일한 동반자로 따라다니다 보니, 제가 한때 배를 탔다는 사실조차 거의 잊어버리고, 제법 박식해졌어요. 묘하게도 그 교수님은 스워서 함장과 정반대였고, 배저 씨는 또 그 둘 중 누구와도 조금도 닮지 않았지요!\"\n\n이어서 우리는 스워서 함장과 딩고 교수의 죽음에 관한 이야기로 넘어갔는데, 두 사람 다 아주 고약한 병을 앓았던 모양이었습니다. 그 이야기 도중 배저 부인은 자기가 미친 듯이 사랑한 것은 평생 단 한 번뿐이었으며, 그 싱그러운 열정으로는 결코 되돌릴 수 없는 그 격렬한 애정의 대상이 바로 스워서 함장이었다고 우리에게 일러주었습니다. 교수는 더없이 처량한 모습으로 한 치 한 치 죽어가고 있던 참이었고, 배저 부인이 그가 몹시 힘겹게 \"로라는 어디 있나? 로라가 내 토스트와 물을 가져오게 해!\" 하고 말하던 흉내를 우리에게 내 보이고 있는데, 마침 신사들이 들어오는 바람에 그를 무덤으로 보내고 말았습니다.\n\n그런데 나는 그날 저녁, 지난 며칠 동안 그래왔듯이, 에이다와 리처드가 그 어느 때보다 서로 함께 있기를 좋아한다는 것을 알아챘습니다. 머잖아 헤어질 처지였으니 그것은 당연한 일이었습니다. 그래서 집에 돌아와 에이다와 내가 위층으로 물러났을 때, 에이다가 평소보다 말이 없는 것을 보고도 나는 그리 놀라지 않았습니다. 다만 그녀가 내 품에 안겨 얼굴을 묻은 채 말을 꺼내기 시작하리라고는 미처 예상하지 못했습니다.\n\n\"사랑하는 에스더!\" 에이다가 속삭였습니다. \"너한테 들려줄 굉장한 비밀이 있어!\"\n\n엄청난 비밀이겠지, 우리 귀여운 아이야, 두말할 것 없이!\n\n\"그게 뭔데, 에이다?\"\n\n\"오, 에스더, 넌 절대 못 맞힐 거야!\"\n\n\"내가 한번 맞혀볼까?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"오, 안 돼! 그러지 마! 제발 그러지 마!\" 에이다가 내가 그러겠다는 생각에 몹시 놀라 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"자, 누구에 관한 걸까?\" 내가 곰곰이 생각하는 척하며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그건 바로—\" 에이다가 속삭였습니다. \"그건 바로—내 사촌 리처드에 관한 거야!\"\n\n\"그래, 우리 아가!\" 나는 그녀의 빛나는 머리카락에 입을 맞추며 말했습니다. 그것이 내가 볼 수 있는 전부였으니까요. \"그 사람이 어쨌는데?\"\n\n\"오, 에스더, 넌 절대 못 맞힐 거야!\"\n\n그녀가 그렇게 나에게 매달려 얼굴을 묻고 있는 모습이 어찌나 사랑스럽던지, 또 그녀가 슬퍼서가 아니라 기쁨과 자랑과 희망의 작은 설렘으로 우는 줄을 알기에, 나는 아직 그녀를 도와주지 않았습니다.\n\n\"그이가 그러는데—나도 알아, 너무 어리석다는 거, 우린 둘 다 너무 어리니까—하지만 그이가 그러는데,\" 그녀가 눈물을 왈칵 쏟으며 말했습니다. \"날 무척 사랑한대, 에스더.\"\n\n\"정말 그래?\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그런 얘긴 처음 듣는걸! 아니, 우리 아가 중의 아가야, 그건 내가 몇 주, 아니 몇 주도 더 전에 너한테 말해줄 수 있었어!\"\n\n에이다가 기쁜 놀라움에 발그레한 얼굴을 들고, 내 목을 끌어안고, 웃고, 울고, 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 보니 어찌나 흐뭇하던지요!\n\n\"아니, 우리 아가,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"넌 나를 어지간한 바보로 여겼나 보구나! 네 사촌 리처드는 언제부터인지도 모를 만큼 오래도록 너를 더없이 빤히 사랑해왔는데!\"\n\n\"그런데도 넌 한마디도 안 했잖아!\" 에이다가 나에게 입을 맞추며 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 아가.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"난 네가 말해주기를 기다린 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 이제 내가 말했으니, 내가 잘못했다곤 생각 안 하지, 그렇지?\" 에이다가 응답했습니다. 내가 세상에서 가장 모진 보호자였다 해도 그녀는 나를 어르고 달래어 아니라는 말을 끌어낼 수 있었을 겁니다. 아직 그런 사람은 아니었기에, 나는 거리낌 없이 아니라고 했습니다.\n\n\"자 이제,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"최악의 사정까지 다 알았네.\"\n\n\"오, 그게 아주 최악은 아니야, 에스더!\" 에이다가 나를 더 꼭 끌어안고 다시 내 가슴에 얼굴을 묻으며 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"아니라고?\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그것마저도 아니란 말이야?\"\n\n\"응, 그것마저도 아니야!\" 에이다가 고개를 저으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니, 설마 네 말은—\" 내가 농담조로 말을 꺼냈습니다.\n\n그러나 에이다는 눈물 너머로 고개를 들어 미소 지으며 외쳤습니다. \"응, 맞아! 알잖아, 너도 알잖아!\" 그러고는 흐느끼며 토해냈습니다. \"온 마음을 다해 그래! 내 온 마음을 다해서, 에스더!\"\n\n나는 웃으며 그녀에게 말해주었습니다. 그것 역시 다른 사실만큼이나 똑같이 진작에 알고 있었던 까닭을요! 우리는 난롯가에 앉았고, 한동안 이야기는 나 혼자 도맡았습니다(그래봐야 많지도 않았지만). 에이다는 이내 차분해지고 행복해졌습니다.\n\n\"내 사촌 존도 알고 계실까, 더든 부인?\" 그녀가 물었습니다.\n\n\"내 사촌 존이 눈먼 사람이 아닌 다음에야, 우리 아가.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"내 사촌 존은 우리가 아는 만큼은 잘 알고 계실 거야.\"\n\n\"리처드가 떠나기 전에 그분께 말씀드리고 싶어.\" 에이다가 머뭇거리며 말했습니다. \"그래서 네가 우리한테 조언도 해주고, 그분께 그렇게 전해주었으면 했어. 혹시 리처드가 들어와도 괜찮을까, 더든 부인?\"\n\n\"오! 리처드가 밖에 있구나, 그렇지, 우리 아가?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"잘은 모르겠지만,\" 에이다가 수줍은 순진함으로 응답했습니다. 진작에 내 마음을 사로잡지 않았더라면 바로 그 모습에 사로잡혔을 만한 순진함이었지요. \"문간에서 기다리고 있는 것 같아.\"\n\n물론 그는 거기 있었습니다. 두 사람은 내 양옆에 의자를 가져다 놓고 나를 가운데 앉히더니, 정말이지 서로를 사랑하는 대신 나에게 반한 것처럼, 어찌나 나를 믿고 의지하며 좋아하던지요. 두 사람은 한동안 자기들 식으로 마음껏 들떠 떠들었고—나는 그들을 막지 않았습니다. 나도 그것이 너무도 즐거웠으니까요—그러다가 우리는 차츰 그들이 얼마나 어린지, 이 이른 사랑이 무언가로 결실을 맺으려면 몇 해는 흘러야 한다는 것, 그리고 그것이 참되고 변함없으며, 서로를 위해 변함없음과 꿋꿋함과 끈기로 각자의 도리를 다하려는 굳건한 결심을 그들에게 불어넣을 때에야 비로소 행복으로 이어질 수 있다는 것을 헤아리게 되었습니다. 그래요! 리처드는 에이다를 위해 손가락이 뼈만 남도록 일하겠다고 했고, 에이다는 리처드를 위해 손가락이 뼈만 남도록 일하겠다고 했으며, 두 사람은 나를 온갖 다정하고 분별 있는 이름으로 불렀습니다. 우리는 거기 앉아 조언하고 이야기하며 밤의 절반을 보냈습니다. 마침내 헤어지기 전에, 나는 내일 그들의 사촌 존에게 말씀드리겠다고 두 사람에게 약속했습니다.\n\n그리하여 이튿날이 되자, 나는 아침을 먹고 후견인에게 갔습니다. 우리의 시골 투덜방을 대신하는 방이었지요. 거기서 나는 그분께 전해드릴 일을 부탁받았다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 작은 부인,\" 그분이 책을 덮으며 말했습니다. \"자네가 그 부탁을 맡았다면, 거기에 나쁜 일이 있을 리 없겠지.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요, 후견인님.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"거기엔 아무 비밀도 없다고 장담할 수 있어요. 어제서야 일어난 일이거든요.\"\n\n\"그래? 그래서 무슨 일인가, 에스더?\"\n\n\"후견인님,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"우리가 처음 황폐한 집으로 내려가던 그 행복한 밤을 기억하세요? 에이다가 어두운 방에서 노래를 부르던 때요?\"\n\n나는 그분이 그때 나에게 던졌던 그 눈빛을 떠올리게 하고 싶었습니다. 내가 크게 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그렇게 했다는 것을 알아챌 수 있었습니다.\n\n\"그게요—\" 내가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 아가!\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"서두르지 말게.\"\n\n\"그게요,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"에이다와 리처드가 서로 사랑에 빠졌어요. 그리고 서로 그렇다고 말했고요.\"\n\n\"벌써!\" 후견인이 깜짝 놀라 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"네!\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 후견인님, 저는 이미 그럴 줄 짐작하고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 자네가 말인가!\" 그분이 말했습니다.\n\n그분은 변해가는 얼굴에 그토록 잘생기고도 그토록 다정한 미소를 띤 채, 잠시 일이 분 동안 곰곰이 생각하며 앉아 있다가, 두 사람을 보고 싶다는 뜻을 전하라고 나에게 일렀습니다. 두 사람이 오자, 그분은 아버지처럼 한쪽 팔로 에이다를 감싸 안고는 명랑하면서도 진중한 태도로 리처드에게 말했습니다.\n\n\"릭,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"자네 신뢰를 얻었다니 기쁘네. 그 신뢰를 계속 지켜가길 바라네. 내 삶을 그토록 환하게 밝히고, 새로운 흥미와 즐거움으로 채워준 우리 네 사람 사이의 이 관계를 떠올려볼 때, 나는 분명 저 멀리서나마, 자네와 여기 자네의 어여쁜 사촌이 (수줍어 말게, 에이다, 수줍어 말게, 우리 아가!) 평생을 함께 가려는 마음을 품을 가능성을 떠올렸다네. 그게 바람직하다고 여길 만한 이유를 여럿 보았고, 지금도 보고 있어. 하지만 그건 저 멀리 있는 일이었네, 릭, 저 멀리!\"\n\n\"저희도 저 멀리를 바라봅니다, 어르신.\" 리처드가 응답했습니다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그건 이치에 맞는 말이지. 자, 내 말을 들어보게, 우리 아이들! 자네들은 아직 제 마음을 모른다고, 자네들을 서로에게서 갈라놓을 숱한 일이 일어날 수 있다고, 자네들이 집어 든 이 꽃사슬이 아주 쉽게 끊어진다는 게 다행이라고, 안 그러면 그게 납사슬이 될 수도 있다고 말할 수도 있겠지. 하지만 난 그러지 않겠네. 그런 지혜는, 정 올 것이라면 머잖아 충분히 오겠지. 나는 몇 해 뒤에도 자네들이 마음속으로 서로에게 오늘과 똑같으리라 여기겠네. 그 가정에 따라 자네들에게 말하기 전에 한마디만 하자면, 만약 자네들이 정말로 변한다면—소년 소녀였을 때보다 어른 남녀로서 서로에게 더 평범한 사촌일 뿐임을 정말로 알게 된다면 (자네의 성년을 걸고 양해하게, 릭!)—그래도 부끄러워 말고 나에게 털어놓게. 거기엔 끔찍하거나 별난 구석이 전혀 없을 테니까. 나는 그저 자네들의 친구이자 먼 친척일 뿐이야. 자네들에게 아무런 권한도 없어. 다만 내가 그것을 잃을 짓을 하지 않는 한, 자네들의 신뢰를 계속 지니길 바라고 또 희망하네.\"\n\n\"확신합니다, 어르신.\" 리처드가 응답했습니다. \"어르신께서 저희 두 사람 모두에게 가장 강한 힘을 지니셨다고—존경과 감사와 애정에 뿌리내려 날마다 깊어가는 힘을 지니셨다고 말씀드릴 때, 에이다를 대신해서도 말씀드리는 거예요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 사촌 존.\" 에이다가 그분의 어깨에 기대어 말했습니다. \"아버지의 자리가 다시는 빈자리가 되지 않을 거예요. 제가 아버지께 드릴 수 있었던 그 모든 사랑과 도리를 이제 사촌께 옮겨드릴게요.\"\n\n\"자!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"이제 우리의 가정으로 돌아가지. 자, 눈을 들어 희망을 품고 저 멀리를 바라보세! 릭, 세상은 자네 앞에 펼쳐져 있어. 그리고 십중팔구 자네가 세상에 들어서는 대로 세상도 자네를 맞아줄 걸세. 섭리와 자네 자신의 노력 말고는 아무것도 믿지 말게. 그 둘을 결코 갈라놓지 말게나, 저 이교도 마부처럼. 사랑에서의 변함없음은 좋은 것이지만, 온갖 노력에서의 변함없음이 없으면 그건 아무 의미도 없고 아무것도 아니야. 자네가 고금의 위인들이 지닌 온갖 재능을 다 가졌다 해도, 진심으로 마음먹고 착수하지 않으면 무엇 하나 제대로 해낼 수 없네. 크든 작든 어떤 참된 성공이, 들쭉날쭉 변덕으로 운명에게서 비틀어 얻어진 적이 있었거나 있을 수 있다고, 또 앞으로 그러할 수 있거나 그럴 것이라고 여긴다면, 그 그릇된 생각을 여기 두고 가든지, 아니면 자네 사촌 에이다를 여기 두고 가게.\"\n\n\"그건 여기 두고 가겠습니다, 어르신.\" 리처드가 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"혹 제가 방금 그걸 여기 들여왔다면 말이지요 (그러지 않았기를 바랍니다만), 그리고 일해 나아가 저 희망에 찬 먼 곳의 사촌 에이다에게 이르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"옳거니!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그녀를 행복하게 해줄 게 아니라면, 어찌 그녀를 좇겠는가?\"\n\n\"전 그녀를 불행하게 만들지 않겠어요—아니요, 그녀의 사랑을 얻기 위해서라도 그러지 않겠어요.\" 리처드가 당당하게 응수했습니다.\n\n\"잘 말했네!\" 잔다이스 씨가 외쳤습니다. \"참 잘 말했어! 에이다는 여기, 나와 함께 제 집에 남을 거야. 그녀를 사랑하게, 릭, 자네가 돌아와 찾는 이 집에서만큼이나 자네의 분주한 삶 속에서도 사랑하게. 그러면 모든 게 잘될 거야. 그러지 않으면 모든 게 잘못될 테고. 이걸로 내 설교는 끝일세. 자네와 에이다는 산책이나 좀 다녀오는 게 좋겠어.\"\n\n에이다가 그분을 다정하게 끌어안았고, 리처드는 그분과 진심으로 악수를 나누었습니다. 그러고 나서 두 사촌은 방을 나섰는데, 곧장 뒤돌아보며 나를 기다리겠다고 말했습니다.\n\n문이 열려 있어서, 우리 두 사람은 그들이 햇빛이 비쳐드는 옆방을 지나 그 저편 끝으로 나갈 때까지 눈으로 좇았습니다. 리처드는 고개를 숙인 채, 그녀의 손을 자기 팔에 끼우고 그녀에게 무척 진지하게 이야기하고 있었습니다. 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 귀를 기울였고, 다른 것은 아무것도 보이지 않는 듯했습니다. 그토록 젊고, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 희망과 약속으로 가득 찬 두 사람은 햇살 속을 가뿐히 걸어갔습니다. 그들의 행복한 생각이 그때 다가올 세월을 가로질러 그 모든 세월을 환한 빛의 나날로 만들고 있는 것처럼 말이지요. 그렇게 두 사람은 그늘 속으로 사라져 보이지 않게 되었습니다. 그토록 눈부셨던 것은 한 줄기 빛이 터져 나온 것뿐이었습니다. 그들이 나가자 방은 어두워졌고, 해는 구름에 가렸습니다.\n\n\"내가 옳은가, 에스더?\" 두 사람이 가버린 뒤 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n그분은 어찌나 선하고 지혜로운지, 자기가 옳은지를 나에게 다 묻다니요!\n\n\"릭은 이번 일로 자기에게 모자란 자질을 얻을지도 몰라. 그토록 좋은 점이 많은데도, 그 핵심에 모자란 자질을 말일세!\" 잔다이스 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했습니다. \"나는 에이다에게는 아무 말도 하지 않았네, 에스더. 그 아이에겐 벗이자 조언자가 언제나 곁에 있으니.\" 그러고는 사랑스럽게 손을 내 머리에 얹었습니다.\n\n나는 감추려고 온갖 애를 썼지만, 조금 마음이 움직였다는 것을 드러내지 않을 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"쯧쯧!\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"하지만 우리 작은 부인의 삶이 온통 남을 보살피는 데만 쓰이지 않도록 우리도 조심해야 해.\"\n\n\"보살핀다니요? 사랑하는 후견인님, 저는 제가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람이라고 믿는걸요!\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 믿네.\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"하지만 누군가는 에스더가 결코 깨닫지 못할 것을 알아낼 걸세—이 작은 부인이야말로 다른 그 누구보다 더 깊이 기억할 사람이라는 것을 말이야!\"\n\n마땅한 자리에서 언급하지 못한 것이 있는데, 그 가족 만찬 자리에 또 한 사람이 있었습니다. 부인은 아니었습니다. 신사였지요. 피부가 가무잡잡한 신사로—젊은 외과의였습니다. 그는 다소 말수가 적었지만, 나는 그가 무척 분별 있고 호감 가는 사람이라고 생각했습니다. 적어도 에이다가 나에게 그렇게 생각하지 않느냐고 물었고, 나는 그렇다고 했습니다.\n\n리처드는 바로 다음 날 저녁에 새로운 앞날을 시작하러 우리를 떠나면서, 에이다를 향한 깊은 사랑과 나를 향한 깊은 신뢰를 담아 그녀를 내게 맡겼습니다. 그토록 정신없이 바쁜 와중에도 두 사람이 나를 그렇게 생각해 주었다는 것을, 그때도 떠올리면 가슴이 뭉클했지만 지금 (앞으로 들려드릴 이야기를 안고 있는 까닭에) 다시 떠올리면 더욱 절실히 가슴에 사무칩니다. 나는 두 사람의 모든 계획에, 현재와 미래의 모든 계획에 한 자리를 차지하고 있었습니다. 나는 일주일에 한 번씩 리처드에게 편지를 써서 에이다의 소식을 충실히 알려 주기로 했고, 에이다는 하루걸러 한 번씩 그에게 편지를 쓰기로 했습니다. 그가 직접 쓴 편지로 그의 온갖 노고와 성공을 전해 듣기로 했으며, 그가 얼마나 굳세고 끈기 있게 해 나가는지 지켜보기로 했고, 두 사람이 결혼하면 에이다의 신부 들러리가 되고, 그 후에는 둘과 함께 살며, 그들 집의 열쇠를 모두 맡고, 영원히, 그리고 하루 더, 행복하게 지내기로 했습니다.\n\n\"게다가 만에 하나 그 소송이 우리를 부자로 만들어 주기라도 하면 말이야, 에스더 — 그럴 수도 있잖아, 안 그래!\" 리처드가 모든 말을 마무리 짓듯 말했습니다.\n\n에이다의 얼굴에 그늘이 스쳐 지나갔습니다.\n\n\"내 사랑 에이다,\" 리처드가 물었습니다. \"왜 안 된다는 거야?\"\n\n\"차라리 당장 우리를 가난뱅이라고 선고해 주는 편이 나아요.\" 에이다가 말했습니다.\n\"오! 그건 잘 모르겠지만,\" 리처드가 대꾸했습니다. \"어쨌든 당장 무엇 하나 선고해 주진 않을 거야. 도대체 몇 년째 아무것도 선고하지 못하고 있는지 하느님만 아실 노릇이니까.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇죠.\" 에이다가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만,\" 리처드는 그녀의 말보다 그녀의 표정이 암시하는 바에 대답하듯 다그쳤습니다. \"사촌, 오래 끌면 끌수록 이렇든 저렇든 어떻게든 결판에 가까워질 수밖에 없잖아. 자, 그게 이치에 맞지 않아?\"\n\n\"당신이 제일 잘 알겠죠, 리처드. 하지만 우리가 거기 기대를 걸면 그게 우릴 불행하게 만들까 봐 두려워요.\"\n\n\"그렇지만, 내 에이다, 우린 거기 기대를 걸지 않을 거야!\" 리처드가 명랑하게 외쳤습니다. \"거기 기대를 걸 만큼 모르지 않으니까. 우린 그저 만에 하나 그게 우리를 부자로 만들어 준다면, 우리도 부자가 되는 데 천성적으로 반대할 까닭은 없다는 것뿐이야. 그 법정은 엄숙한 법의 결정으로 정해진 우리의 무뚝뚝한 늙은 후견인이고, 그것이 우리에게 주는 것(무엇이든 주기라도 한다면)은 당연히 우리 권리라고 봐야지. 우리 권리와 싸울 필요는 없잖아.\"\n\n\"그렇죠,\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"하지만 그냥 다 잊어버리는 편이 나을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아,\" 리처드가 외쳤습니다. \"그럼 다 잊어버리자고! 깡그리 망각 속에 처박아 버리는 거야. 두든 아주머니가 흡족한 얼굴을 지으면, 그걸로 끝!\"\n\n\"두든 아주머니의 흡족한 얼굴은,\" 그의 책을 챙겨 넣던 상자 너머로 내가 내다보며 말했습니다. \"당신이 그렇게 부를 때는 그다지 보이지 않았지만, 그래도 흡족해하긴 해요. 그리고 이보다 더 잘하긴 어렵다고 생각하고요.\"\n\n그러자 리처드는 이걸로 끝난 일이라 말하고는, 다른 토대라곤 아무것도 없이 곧장 만리장성에 다 늘어세울 만큼 많은 공중누각을 짓기 시작했습니다. 그는 잔뜩 들뜬 기분으로 떠났습니다. 에이다와 나는 그가 무척 그리울 것을 각오하고서, 한결 조용해진 앞날을 시작했습니다.\n\n런던에 도착했을 때 우리는 잔다이스 씨와 함께 젤리비 부인 댁을 찾았지만, 운이 닿지 않아 그녀를 집에서 만나지 못했습니다. 어딘가 차 모임에 가면서 젤리비 양도 데려간 모양이었습니다. 차 모임 외에도, 보리오불라가 정착지에서 원주민들과 더불어 커피를 재배하는 일이 두루 어떤 이로움을 지니는가를 두고 상당한 연설과 편지 쓰기가 예정되어 있다고 했습니다. 이 모든 것이 의심할 바 없이 펜과 잉크의 충분한 노동을 요구했을 테니, 그 절차에서 딸이 맡은 몫이 도무지 휴일과는 거리가 멀었을 것입니다.\n\n이제 젤리비 부인이 돌아오기로 한 시각이 지났기에 우리는 다시 찾아갔습니다. 그녀는 시내에 있었지만 집에는 없었으니, 아침 식사를 마치자마자 동런던 지부 보조 분파라는 단체에서 비롯된 어떤 보리오불라 관련 용무로 마일엔드에 갔다는 것이었습니다. 지난번 방문 때 피피를 보지 못했던 터라(그때 그 아이는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었고, 요리사는 청소부 수레를 따라 어슬렁어슬렁 가버린 게 틀림없다고 했습니다) 나는 다시 그 아이의 안부를 물었습니다. 그가 집을 짓는다고 쌓아 올렸던 굴 껍데기는 여전히 복도에 있었지만, 아이는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었고, 요리사는 그가 \"양 떼를 쫓아갔나 보다\" 했습니다. 우리가 적잖이 놀라 \"양 떼요?\" 하고 되묻자, 요리사는 아, 네, 장날이면 가끔 양 떼를 따라 시 밖까지 나갔다가 말도 못 할 꼴이 되어 돌아온다고 했습니다!\n\n이튿날 아침에 나는 후견인과 함께 창가에 앉아 있었고, 에이다는 편지를 쓰느라 바빴는데 — 물론 리처드에게 보내는 편지였습니다 — 그때 젤리비 양이 왔다는 전갈이 들어오더니, 바로 그 피피의 손을 잡고 들어왔습니다. 젤리비 양은 그 아이를 그럴듯하게 꾸며 보려고 얼마쯤 애를 썼는데, 얼굴과 손에 묻은 때를 한구석으로 문질러 몰아넣고, 머리카락을 흠뻑 적신 뒤 손가락으로 마구 비벼 곱슬곱슬하게 만든 식이었습니다. 그 가엾은 아이가 걸친 것은 하나같이 너무 크거나 너무 작았습니다. 다른 모순된 장식들과 더불어, 그는 주교가 쓸 법한 모자를 쓰고 갓난아기용 작은 장갑을 끼고 있었습니다. 신발은 작은 치수이긴 했으나 영락없는 농사꾼의 장화였고, 긁힌 자국이 이리저리 엇갈려 마치 지도처럼 보이는 두 다리는, 서로 전혀 다른 무늬의 주름 장식 두 개로 끝을 마무리한 아주 짧은 격자무늬 속바지 아래로 맨살이 드러나 있었습니다. 격자무늬 윗도리에 부족한 단추는 젤리비 씨의 외투 가운데 하나에서 떼어다 단 것이 분명했으니, 어찌나 번쩍이고 또 어찌나 큼지막한지요. 옷 곳곳에 황급히 기운 자리마다 보기 드물게 별난 바느질 표본이 나타났는데, 나는 젤리비 양의 옷에서도 같은 손길을 알아보았습니다. 그렇지만 젤리비 양 자신은 어찌 된 영문인지 차림새가 부쩍 나아져 무척 예뻐 보였습니다. 그녀는 그 모든 수고에도 불구하고 가엾은 어린 피피가 끝내 실패작에 그쳤다는 것을 알고 있었고, 들어오면서 먼저 그 아이를 흘끔 보고 다음에 우리를 보는 그 눈길로 그 마음을 내비쳤습니다.\n\n\"이런, 저런!\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"정동풍일세!\"\n\n에이다와 나는 그녀를 다정하게 맞이하고 잔다이스 씨에게 소개했는데, 젤리비 양은 자리에 앉으면서 그에게 이렇게 말했습니다. \"엄마가 안부 전해 달래요, 그리고 양해해 주십사 하고요. 지금 계획서 교정을 보고 계시거든요. 새 회람장 오천 장을 돌릴 거라는데, 선생님도 그 소식을 들으면 흥미로워하실 거라고 하셨어요. 한 장 가지고 왔어요. 엄마가 안부 전해 달래요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 꽤나 뚱한 태도로 그것을 내밀었습니다.\n\n\"고맙군요,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"젤리비 부인께 대단히 감사하다고 전해 주세요. 이런, 저런! 견디기 참 힘든 바람이로군요!\"\n\n우리는 피피에게 매달려 그의 성직자 모자를 벗기고, 우리를 기억하느냐 묻고, 그런저런 일을 하고 있었습니다. 피피는 처음엔 제 팔꿈치 뒤로 숨었지만, 스펀지케이크를 보고는 마음이 풀려 내 무릎에 앉도록 허락했고, 거기서 조용히 우물우물 케이크를 먹으며 앉아 있었습니다. 그러고 나서 잔다이스 씨가 임시 투덜방으로 물러가자, 젤리비 양은 여느 때처럼 불쑥 이야기를 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"세이비스 인은 여전히 형편없이 돌아가고 있어요,\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"사는 게 도무지 편할 날이 없어요. 아프리카는 무슨 아프리카예요! 제가 그 뭐냐 — 사람이자 형제라는 그 흑인이었어도 이보다 더 처지가 나쁘진 않았을 거예요!\"\n\n나는 무언가 달래는 말을 하려고 애썼습니다.\n\n\"오, 소용없어요, 서머슨 양,\" 젤리비 양이 외쳤습니다. \"그래도 그 다정한 뜻은 고마워요. 제가 어떤 취급을 받는지 저도 다 알고, 말로 구워삶을 수 있는 사람이 아니에요. 서머슨 양도 그런 취급을 받았다면 말에 넘어가지 않으셨을걸요. 피피, 피아노 밑에 가서 맹수 놀이나 해!\"\n\n\"싫어!\" 피피가 말했습니다.\n\n\"좋아, 이 은혜도 모르고 못된, 매정한 녀석!\" 젤리비 양이 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 대꾸했습니다. \"다신 너 입혀 주느라 고생 안 할 거야.\"\n\n\"갈게, 갈게, 캐디!\" 피피가 외쳤습니다. 그 아이는 사실 착한 아이여서 누나가 속상해하는 것에 그렇게 마음이 움직여 당장 가버렸습니다.\n\n\"이런 일로 우는 게 별것 아닌 것처럼 보이겠죠,\" 가엾은 젤리비 양이 변명하듯 말했습니다. \"하지만 저는 완전히 녹초가 됐어요. 오늘 새벽 두 시까지 새 회람장 주소를 쓰고 있었거든요. 그 모든 게 어찌나 지긋지긋한지, 그것만으로도 머리가 지끈거려 눈앞이 다 안 보일 지경이에요. 그리고 저 가엾은 불쌍한 아이 좀 보세요! 세상에 저런 흉한 꼴이 또 어디 있겠어요!\"\n\n피피는 다행히도 제 꼴이 어떤지 까맣게 모른 채, 피아노 다리 하나 뒤편 카펫에 앉아 케이크를 먹으면서 제 굴속에서 우리를 차분히 내다보고 있었습니다.\n\n\"방 저쪽 끝으로 보낸 건,\" 젤리비 양이 의자를 우리 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했습니다. \"이야기를 못 듣게 하려고 그런 거예요. 저런 어린것들은 어찌나 약삭빠른지! 아까 하려던 말은, 우리 집이 정말 어느 때보다 더 엉망으로 돌아가고 있다는 거예요. 머잖아 아빠가 파산할 텐데, 그러면 엄마도 만족하시겠죠. 그게 다 엄마 탓이라는 것 말고는 탓할 게 없을 거예요.\"\n\n우리는 젤리비 씨의 형편이 그렇게까지 나쁜 지경은 아니길 바란다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"바라 봤자 소용없어요, 마음은 정말 고맙지만요,\" 젤리비 양이 고개를 저으며 대꾸했습니다. \"아빠가 바로 어제 아침에 (무척 불행해하시면서) 이 풍랑을 헤쳐 나갈 수 없을 거라고 말씀하셨어요. 헤쳐 나가신다면 오히려 제가 놀랄 거예요. 우리 거래처들이 죄다 제 마음대로 아무 물건이나 집에 들이밀고, 하인들은 그걸 또 제멋대로 써버리고, 저는 방법을 안다 해도 형편을 나아지게 할 시간이 없고, 엄마는 아무것에도 관심이 없는데, 도대체 아빠가 무슨 수로 이 풍랑을 헤쳐 나갈지 따져 보고 싶을 정도예요. 정말이지 제가 아빠라면 달아나 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"얘야!\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"네 아버지께서는 분명 가족을 생각하시는 거란다.\"\n\n\"오, 네, 아빠의 가족이라는 게 참으로 훌륭하기도 하죠, 서머슨 양,\" 젤리비 양이 대답했습니다. \"그런데 아빠한테 가족이 무슨 위안이 되겠어요? 아빠의 가족이라곤 청구서, 더러움, 낭비, 소음, 계단에서 굴러떨어지는 일, 난장판, 그리고 비참함뿐인걸요. 한 주가 시작될 때부터 끝날 때까지 아빠가 허둥지둥 돌아오는 그 집은 거대한 빨래하는 날 같아요 — 다만 빨리는 건 아무것도 없죠!\"\n\n젤리비 양은 바닥에 발을 톡톡 구르며 눈물을 훔쳤습니다.\n\n\"정말이지 그 정도로 아빠가 가엾어요,\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"그리고 엄마한테는 어찌나 화가 나는지 말로 다 표현할 수가 없어요! 어쨌든 저는 이걸 참고 견디지 않을 거예요, 단단히 결심했어요. 평생 노예로 살지 않을 거고, 퀘일 씨의 청혼을 받아들이지도 않을 거예요. 박애주의자랑 결혼하는 게 참 대단한 일이겠네요, 정말. 그런 건 이미 지긋지긋하도록 겪지 않았느냐 말이에요!\" 가엾은 젤리비 양이 말했습니다.\n\n고백하건대, 이 방치된 소녀를 보고 그 말을 들으며, 또 그녀가 한 말 속에 얼마나 쓰디쓴 풍자적 진실이 담겨 있는지 알기에, 나 자신도 젤리비 부인에게 적잖이 화가 나는 것을 어쩔 수가 없었습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양이 우리 집에 묵으셨을 때 친하게 지낸 사이가 아니었다면,\" 젤리비 양이 말을 이었습니다. \"오늘 여기 오는 것이 부끄러웠을 거예요. 제가 두 분께 어떤 꼴로 보일지 잘 아니까요. 하지만 그래도 저는 꼭 찾아오기로 마음먹었어요, 더구나 다음에 두 분이 시내에 오실 때는 저를 다시 보기 어려울 테니까요.\"\n\n그녀가 어찌나 의미심장하게 이 말을 했던지, 에이다와 나는 무언가 더 있겠구나 짐작하며 서로 눈길을 주고받았습니다.\n\n\"안 돼요!\" 젤리비 양이 고개를 저으며 말했습니다. \"전혀 어려울 거예요! 두 분은 믿어도 된다는 걸 알아요. 두 분은 절 배신하지 않으실 거예요. 저, 약혼했어요.\"\n\n\"집안 식구들 몰래?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니, 어쩌면 좋아요, 서머슨 양,\" 그녀가 짜증스럽지만 화내는 투는 아니게 스스로를 변호하며 대꾸했습니다. \"어떻게 달리할 수가 있겠어요? 엄마가 어떤 사람인지 아시잖아요 — 그리고 불쌍한 아빠한테 말씀드려서 더 비참하게 만들 필요도 없고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 얘야, 아버지께서 모르시거나 허락하지 않으신 채 결혼하는 것이 오히려 그분의 불행을 더하는 일이 되지 않겠니?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 젤리비 양이 누그러지며 말했습니다. \"그러지 않았으면 해요. 아빠가 저를 보러 오시면 행복하고 편안하게 해 드리려고 애쓸 거예요. 그리고 피피랑 다른 동생들도 번갈아 와서 저랑 지내게 할 거고, 그때는 그 아이들도 돌봄을 좀 받을 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n가엾은 캐디에게는 다정한 마음이 가득했습니다. 이 말을 하면서 그녀는 점점 더 누그러졌고, 마음속에 떠올린 그 낯선 작은 가정의 그림에 어찌나 펑펑 울었던지, 피아노 밑 동굴 속의 피피까지 마음이 움직여 큰 소리로 한탄하며 벌렁 등을 대고 나뒹굴었습니다. 내가 그 아이를 데려와 누나에게 입을 맞추게 하고, 다시 내 무릎 위 제자리에 앉히고, 캐디가 웃고 있다고 보여 주고 나서야(그녀는 일부러 그러려고 웃어 주었습니다) 우리는 그 아이의 마음을 가라앉힐 수 있었습니다. 그러고도 한동안은, 그 아이가 우리 턱을 번갈아 붙들고 손으로 우리 얼굴을 죄다 쓰다듬게 해 준다는 조건에서만 마음이 풀렸습니다. 마침내 그의 기분이 피아노까지 갈 만큼은 되지 않았기에, 우리는 그를 의자에 앉혀 창밖을 내다보게 했습니다. 그리고 젤리비 양은 그의 한쪽 다리를 붙든 채 다시 속내를 털어놓기 시작했습니다.\n\n“그건 당신이 우리 집에 온 일에서 시작됐어요.” 그녀가 말했습니다.\n\n\"제가 너무 어설프다는 걸 느꼈거든요,\" 그녀가 대답했습니다. \"그래서 어쨌든 그 점만이라도 나아져야겠다, 춤을 배워야겠다고 마음먹었어요. 엄마한테 제가 부끄럽다고, 춤을 꼭 배워야겠다고 말했죠. 엄마는 늘 그러시는 그 사람 약 올리는 식으로, 마치 제가 눈에 들어오지도 않는다는 듯이 저를 보셨지만, 저는 춤을 배우기로 단단히 마음먹었고, 그래서 뉴먼 가에 있는 터비드롭 씨의 학원에 갔어요.\"\n\n\"그래서 거기서, 얘야 —\" 내가 말을 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"네, 거기서였어요,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그리고 저는 터비드롭 씨와 약혼했어요. 터비드롭 씨는 두 분이세요, 아버지와 아들이요. 제 터비드롭 씨는 물론 아들이죠. 제가 더 잘 자라서 그 사람한테 더 나은 아내가 될 수 있다면 좋으련만, 저는 그 사람을 정말 좋아하거든요.\"\n\n\"고백하건대, 이 말을 들으니 안타깝구나.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"왜 안타까워하시는지 모르겠어요,\" 그녀가 살짝 불안한 듯 받아쳤습니다. \"하지만 어쨌든 저는 터비드롭 씨와 약혼했고, 그 사람도 저를 무척 좋아해요. 아직은 비밀이에요, 그 사람 쪽에서도요. 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 그 관계에 한몫 끼어 있어서, 갑자기 말씀드리면 그분 가슴이 무너지거나 무슨 다른 충격을 받으실지도 모르거든요. 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 정말 점잖은 신사분이에요 — 아주 점잖으시죠.\"\n\n\"그분 부인은 그 일을 아시니?\" 에이다가 물었습니다.\n\n\"늙은 터비드롭 씨의 부인 말씀이세요, 클레어 양?\" 젤리비 양이 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 대꾸했습니다. \"그런 사람은 없어요. 그분은 홀아비세요.\"\n\n여기서 우리는 피피 때문에 말을 끊어야 했습니다. 누나가 무슨 말을 힘주어 할 때마다 종 끈을 잡아당기듯 무심코 그 다리를 홱홱 잡아당기는 통에 그 다리가 어찌나 시달렸던지, 가엾은 아이가 이제 아주 풀죽은 소리로 제 고초를 한탄했던 것입니다. 그가 내게 동정을 호소했고 나는 그저 듣는 사람일 뿐이었기에, 내가 그를 안고 있기로 했습니다. 젤리비 양은 입을 맞추며 피피에게 미안하다고 빌고 일부러 그런 게 아니라고 안심시킨 뒤 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"사정이 그래요,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"제가 저 자신을 탓하게 될 때도, 그래도 그건 엄마 잘못이라고 생각해요. 우리는 형편이 닿는 대로 결혼할 거고, 그러면 저는 아빠가 계신 사무실로 가서 엄마한테 편지를 쓸 거예요. 엄마는 별로 동요하지도 않으실걸요. 엄마한테 저는 펜과 잉크일 뿐이니까요. 한 가지 큰 위안은,\" 캐디가 흐느끼며 말했습니다. \"결혼하고 나면 다시는 아프리카 소리를 듣지 않아도 된다는 거예요. 젊은 터비드롭 씨는 저를 위해서 그걸 끔찍이 싫어하고, 늙은 터비드롭 씨로 말하면 그런 곳이 있다는 것을 안다 해도 딱 거기까지가 전부예요.\"\n\n\"방금 점잖은 신사라고 했던 그분이 바로 그분이지!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"정말로 아주 점잖으세요,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그분은 그 몸가짐으로 거의 어디서나 유명하세요.\"\n\n\"가르치시는 거니?\" 에이다가 물었습니다.\n\n\"아니요, 딱히 뭘 가르치시진 않아요,\" 캐디가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 그분 몸가짐은 정말 멋져요.\"\n\n캐디는 적잖이 망설이고 머뭇거리면서 말을 이어, 우리가 알아 주었으면 하는, 또 우리가 마땅히 알아야 한다고 느끼는, 그러면서도 우리가 언짢아하지 않기를 바라는 일이 한 가지 더 있다고 했습니다. 그건 그녀가 그 작고 정신이 좀 이상한 노부인 플라이트 양과 알고 지내는 사이가 더 깊어졌다는 것, 그래서 이른 아침에 자주 그곳에 가서 아침 식사 전 잠깐 동안 — 정말 잠깐 동안만 — 연인을 만난다는 것이었습니다. \"다른 때에도 거기 가지만,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그때는 프린스가 오지 않아요. 젊은 터비드롭 씨 이름이 프린스예요. 개 이름 같아서 안 그랬으면 싶지만, 물론 본인이 자기한테 그 이름을 붙인 건 아니죠. 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 섭정 왕세자를 기리며 프린스라는 세례명을 붙여 주셨대요. 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 그 몸가짐 때문에 섭정 왕세자를 흠모하셨거든요. 제가 처음에 두 분과 함께 갔던 플라이트 양 댁에서 이렇게 몰래 약속을 잡았다고 해서 저를 나쁘게 보지는 말아 주세요. 저는 그 가엾은 분을 그분 자체로 좋아하고, 그분도 저를 좋아하신다고 믿어요. 젊은 터비드롭 씨를 보시면, 분명 좋게 생각하실 거예요 — 적어도 절대 나쁘게는 생각하실 수 없을 거예요. 지금 그리로 수업을 받으러 가는 길이에요. 서머슨 양께 같이 가자고 청할 수는 없지만, 혹시 같이 가 주신다면,\" 이 모든 말을 진지하게, 떨리는 목소리로 한 캐디가 말했습니다. \"정말 기쁠 거예요 — 정말 기쁠 거예요.\"\n\n마침 우리는 그날 플라이트 양 댁에 가기로 후견인과 약속해 두었던 참이었습니다. 우리는 그분께 지난번 방문 이야기를 했고, 우리 이야기를 흥미로워하셨지만, 늘 무슨 일이 생겨 다시 가지 못했던 것입니다. 이 가엾은 소녀가 내게 기꺼이 맡기려는 그 신뢰를 내가 온전히 받아들인다면, 그녀가 무모한 짓을 저지르지 않도록 막을 만큼의 영향력은 내게 있으리라 믿었기에, 나는 그녀와 나와 피피가 함께 학원에 갔다가 그 후에 플라이트 양 댁에서 후견인과 에이다를 만나자고 제안했습니다. 플라이트 양의 이름은 이때 처음 알게 되었습니다. 단, 젤리비 양과 피피가 우리와 함께 돌아와 저녁을 먹는다는 조건이었습니다. 약속의 마지막 항목에 둘 다 기쁘게 응했으므로, 우리는 핀 몇 개와 비누와 물, 그리고 머리빗의 도움을 받아 피피를 조금 말끔하게 단장시킨 뒤 길을 나서, 아주 가까운 뉴먼 가로 발걸음을 옮겼습니다.\n\n학원은 아치형 통로 모퉁이의 꽤 칙칙한 집에 자리 잡고 있었고, 계단의 모든 창마다 흉상이 놓여 있었습니다. 문에 붙은 명패로 미루어 보건대, 같은 집에 데생 선생, 석탄 상인(석탄을 둘 자리는 분명 없어 보였습니다만), 그리고 석판 화가도 함께 들어 있었습니다. 크기와 위치로 보아 나머지 모든 것보다 앞자리를 차지한 명패에는 \"터비드롭 씨\"라고 적혀 있었습니다. 문은 열려 있었고, 현관은 그랜드 피아노 한 대와 하프 한 대, 그리고 상자에 든 여러 악기로 가로막혀 있었는데, 모두 옮기는 중이었고 한낮의 빛 속에서 하나같이 방탕한 꼴로 보였습니다. 젤리비 양은 어젯밤 학원을 음악회에 빌려주었다고 알려 주었습니다.\n\n우리는 위층으로 올라갔습니다 — 누군가 깨끗하고 산뜻하게 관리하는 것이 일이고, 온종일 거기서 담배를 피우는 것이 아무의 일도 아니던 시절에는 꽤 훌륭한 집이었습니다 — 그리고 터비드롭 씨의 큰 방으로 들어갔는데, 그 방은 뒤편 마구간 골목 쪽으로 튀어나오게 지어 천창으로 빛이 들어왔습니다. 마구간 냄새가 풍기는 휑하니 울리는 방이었고, 벽을 따라 등나무 의자가 놓여 있었으며, 벽에는 일정한 간격으로 칠한 리라와 양초를 꽂는 작은 컷글라스 가지들이 장식되어 있었는데, 그 가지들은 다른 나뭇가지가 가을 잎을 떨구듯 케케묵은 유리 방울을 흘리고 있는 듯 보였습니다. 열서너 살에서 스물둘셋 살에 이르는 여러 젊은 아가씨 제자들이 모여 있었습니다. 그 사이에서 그들의 선생을 찾고 있을 때, 캐디가 내 팔을 꼬집으며 소개 의식을 되풀이했습니다. \"서머슨 양, 프린스 터비드롭 씨예요!\"\n\n나는 젊어 보이는, 파란 눈에 살결이 흰 자그마한 남자에게 인사를 했습니다. 그는 아마빛 머리카락을 가운데로 가르고 끝을 머리통 둘레로 둥글게 말아 올리고 있었습니다. 왼팔 밑에는 학교에서 우리가 '키트'라고 부르던 작은 바이올린을 끼고, 같은 손에 그 작은 활을 쥐고 있었습니다. 그의 작은 무용화는 유난히 자그마했고, 그에게는 어딘가 순진하고 여성스러운 태도가 있어 호감 가는 방식으로 내 마음을 끌었을 뿐 아니라, 이런 묘한 효과까지 내게 미쳤으니, 그가 제 어머니를 닮았으며 그 어머니는 그다지 존중받지도 좋은 대접을 받지도 못했으리라는 인상을 받게 한 것입니다.\n\n\"젤리비 양의 친구분을 뵙게 되어 정말 기쁩니다,\" 그가 내게 깊이 허리를 숙이며 말했습니다. \"여느 때보다 늦으셔서,\" 수줍은 다정함을 담아 말을 이었습니다. \"젤리비 양이 오시지 않는 줄로 슬슬 걱정하던 참이었어요.\"\n\n\"그 점은 부디 그녀를 붙잡아 둔 저의 탓으로 돌려 주시고, 제 사과를 받아 주세요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"오, 저런!\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그러니 제발,\" 내가 간청했습니다. \"제가 더 이상 지체의 원인이 되지 않도록 해 주세요.\"\n\n그렇게 사과하고서 나는 피피(이런 일에 익숙해 벌써 구석 자리로 기어 올라가 있었습니다)와, 두 조카딸이 그 반에 있는, 비판적인 표정의 한 노부인 사이의 자리로 물러났습니다. 그 노부인은 피피의 장화를 무척 못마땅해했습니다. 그러고 나서 프린스 터비드롭이 손가락으로 자기 키트의 줄을 튕기자, 젊은 아가씨들이 춤을 추려고 일어섰습니다. 바로 그때 옆문에서 늙은 터비드롭 씨가, 그 몸가짐의 광채를 한껏 뽐내며 나타났습니다.\n\n그는 가짜 혈색에 가짜 이, 가짜 구레나룻, 그리고 가발을 한 뚱뚱한 늙은 신사였습니다. 모피 깃을 둘렀고, 외투 가슴에는 솜을 넣어 부풀렸는데, 별 모양 훈장이나 넓은 청색 띠 하나만 더하면 완벽할 지경이었습니다. 그는 견딜 수 있는 한 최대로 조이고, 부풀리고, 차려입고, 졸라매고 있었습니다. 어찌나 큼지막한 목수건을 둘렀는지(눈알이 본래 모양에서 튀어나올 지경이었습니다), 턱은 물론 귀까지 거기 파묻혀, 그것을 풀어 놓으면 영락없이 푹 고꾸라질 것만 같았습니다. 그는 겨드랑이에 크고 무거운, 정수리에서 챙으로 비스듬히 내려간 모자를 끼고, 손에는 흰 장갑 한 켤레를 쥐고서, 한쪽 다리로 균형을 잡은 채 어깨를 치켜올리고 팔꿈치를 둥글린, 더 능가할 수 없는 우아한 자세로 그 모자를 톡톡 쳤습니다. 그는 지팡이가 있었고, 외알 안경이 있었고, 코담뱃갑이 있었고, 반지가 있었고, 소맷부리가 있었고, 무엇이든 다 있었으나 자연스러움이라곤 한 점도 없었습니다. 그는 젊음 같지도 않았고, 늙음 같지도 않았으며, 이 세상 무엇과도 닮지 않았으니, 오직 몸가짐의 본보기일 뿐이었습니다.\n\n\"아버지! 손님이 오셨어요. 젤리비 양의 친구분, 서머슨 양이세요.\"\n\n\"서머슨 양께서 와 주시어,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"영광입니다.\" 그 빳빳한 차림으로 내게 절을 할 때, 나는 그의 흰자위에 주름이 잡히는 것을 거의 보았다고 믿습니다.\n\n\"제 아버지는,\" 아들이 그에 대한 자못 가슴 뭉클한 믿음을 담아 내게 나직이 말했습니다. \"유명한 분이세요. 제 아버지는 크게 존경받으시죠.\"\n\n\"계속하렴, 프린스! 계속해!\" 터비드롭 씨가 난로를 등지고 서서 장갑을 너그러이 흔들며 말했습니다. \"계속하거라, 내 아들아!\"\n\n이 명령에, 아니 이 자비로운 허락에, 수업이 계속되었습니다. 프린스 터비드롭은 때로 키트를 켜며 춤추었고, 때로 선 채로 피아노를 쳤으며, 때로는 제자를 바로잡아 주는 동안 남은 숨을 짜내어 곡조를 흥얼거렸고, 가장 서툰 제자와 더불어 모든 스텝과 춤동작의 모든 부분을 양심껏 함께 움직였으며, 한순간도 쉬지 않았습니다. 그 유명한 그의 아버지는 난로 앞에 선 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았으니, 그저 몸가짐의 본보기였습니다.\n\n\"그리고 저 양반은 절대 다른 건 안 해요,\" 비판적인 표정의 노부인이 말했습니다. \"그런데도 문패에 적힌 게 저 양반 이름이라니, 믿어지세요?\"\n\n\"아들 이름도 똑같잖아요, 아시다시피.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"빼앗을 수만 있다면 아들한테 이름 하나 갖게 두지도 않을 사람이에요,\" 노부인이 대꾸했습니다. \"아들 옷차림 좀 보세요!\" 정말 그 옷차림은 수수하고 — 닳고 — 거의 초라할 지경이었습니다. \"그런데 아비는 치장하고 멋을 부려야 한다는군요,\" 노부인이 말했습니다. \"그 몸가짐 때문에요. 내가 그 작자를 추방해 버리고 싶다니까요! 차라리 유배 보내는 게 낫겠어요!\"\n\n나는 이 사람에 대해 더 알고 싶은 호기심이 일었습니다. 그래서 물었습니다. \"저분이 지금 몸가짐 수업을 하시나요?\"\n\n\"지금이라뇨!\" 노부인이 짧게 대꾸했습니다. \"한 적도 없어요.\"\n\n잠시 생각한 끝에, 나는 어쩌면 펜싱이 그분의 재주였던 게 아니냐고 넌지시 말해 보았습니다.\n\n\"저 양반이 펜싱이라도 할 줄 안다고는 믿기지 않는걸요, 부인.\" 노부인이 말했습니다.\n\n나는 놀라며 캐묻는 표정을 지었습니다. 노부인은 그 화제를 곱씹을수록 점점 더 그 몸가짐의 대가에게 분개하더니, 그의 이력을 몇 가지 들려주면서 이것도 아주 점잖게 말하는 것이라고 거듭 단언했습니다.\n\n그는 그럭저럭 손님이 붙은 온순하고 자그마한 무용 선생과 결혼했고(그는 평생 몸가짐을 차리는 것 말고는 아무 일도 한 적이 없었습니다), 그녀를 일을 시켜 죽음에 이르게 했거나, 잘 봐줘야 그녀가 스스로 일에 치여 죽도록 내버려 둔 것이었으니, 자기 처지에 없어서는 안 될 그 비용을 대게 하려는 것이었습니다. 자기 몸가짐을 최고의 본보기들에게 선보이는 동시에 그 최고의 본보기들을 늘 눈앞에 두기 위해, 그는 유행과 한가로운 사교의 온갖 공공장소를 드나들어야 했고, 철철이 브라이턴이며 여러 곳에 모습을 드러내야 했으며, 더없이 좋은 옷을 차려입고 빈둥거리는 삶을 살아야 했습니다. 그가 이렇게 하도록, 그 다정하고 자그마한 무용 선생은 고되게 일하고 또 일했으니, 기력만 버텨 주었다면 마지막 순간까지도 고되게 일하고 또 일했을 것입니다. 이 이야기의 핵심은, 그 남자의 사무치는 이기심에도 불구하고, 그의 아내가(그 몸가짐에 압도당하여) 끝까지 그를 믿었다는 것, 그리고 임종의 자리에서 더없이 감동적인 말로 그를 아들에게 맡기되, 꺼지지 않는 권리를 아들에게 지닌 분, 아무리 큰 자부심과 공경으로 떠받들어도 지나치지 않을 분으로 당부했다는 것이었습니다. 어머니의 그 믿음을 물려받고, 늘 그 몸가짐을 눈앞에 두어 온 아들은, 똑같은 신앙 속에서 살며 자라났고, 이제 서른 살이 되어, 하루 열두 시간을 아버지를 위해 일하면서 그 케케묵은 가공의 정점에 올라앉은 아버지를 우러러보고 있었습니다.\n\n\"저 작자가 부리는 거드름이라니!\" 내게 이야기를 들려주던 노부인이, 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 빳빳한 장갑을 끼는 모습을 보며 — 물론 그는 그녀가 바치는 그 경의를 까맣게 모르고 있었습니다 — 말없는 분노로 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했습니다. \"저 양반은 자기가 귀족 가운데 하나라고 철석같이 믿고 있어요! 게다가 그렇게 어처구니없이 속여 먹는 아들한테는 어찌나 너그럽게 구는지, 보고 있노라면 세상에서 가장 덕 있는 아비라고 여길 정도라니까요. 오!\" 노부인이 한없는 격정으로 그를 향해 부르짖었습니다. \"콱 물어뜯어 주고 싶어요!\"\n\n나는 진심 어린 걱정으로 노부인의 말을 끝까지 들었지만, 그래도 웃음이 나는 것을 어쩔 수 없었습니다. 아버지와 아들을 눈앞에 두고 보니 그녀의 말을 의심하기란 어려웠습니다. 노부인의 이야기 없이 두 사람을 보았다면 어떻게 생각했을지, 또 두 사람을 보지 않고 노부인의 이야기만 들었다면 어떻게 생각했을지는 알 수 없습니다. 그 전체에는 사물의 들어맞음이 있어 절로 수긍이 가게 했습니다.\n\n젊은 터비드롭 씨가 그토록 고되게 일하는 모습에서, 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 그토록 멋들어지게 몸가짐을 차리는 모습으로 내 눈길이 아직 헤매고 있을 때, 후자가 어슬렁어슬렁 내게 다가와 말을 걸기 시작했습니다.\n\n그는 무엇보다도 먼저, 내가 런던에 거주함으로써 이 도시에 매력과 품격을 더해 주는 것이냐고 물었습니다. 나는 어떤 경우에도 내가 그러지 못하리라는 것을 잘 안다고 대답할 필요는 없다고 보아, 그저 내가 사는 곳이 어디인지만 알려 주었습니다.\n\n\"그토록 우아하고 교양 있는 숙녀분께서는,\" 그가 오른쪽 장갑에 입을 맞추고 나서 그 손을 제자들 쪽으로 뻗으며 말했습니다. \"이곳의 부족함을 너그러이 보아 주시겠지요. 우리는 갈고닦고 — 갈고닦고 — 갈고닦으려 최선을 다하고 있습니다!\"\n\n그는 내 옆에 앉았는데, 소파에 앉은 그 명망 높은 본보기의 판화를 흉내 내느라 의자에 꽤나 공들여 앉는 것 같았습니다. 그리고 정말로 그는 그 판화와 무척 닮아 보였습니다.\n\n\"갈고닦고 — 갈고닦고 — 갈고닦는다!\" 그가 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 손가락을 가볍게 나풀거리고는 거듭 말했습니다. \"하지만, 천성과 기예로 두루 우아하도록 빚어진 분께 이런 말을 해도 된다면 —\" 눈썹을 치켜올리고 눈을 감지 않고서는 도무지 못 할 듯한 그 어깨를 치켜올린 절을 하며 말했습니다. \"— 몸가짐이라는 점에서 우리는 예전 같지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않은가요, 선생님?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"우리는 타락했습니다,\" 목수건 속에서 아주 제한된 정도로나마 고개를 저으며 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"평준화의 시대는 몸가짐에 호의적이지 않지요. 그것은 천박함을 키웁니다. 어쩌면 제가 다소 편파적으로 말하는지도 모르겠습니다. 제가 몇 해 전부터 '신사 터비드롭'이라 불려 왔다거나, 섭정 왕세자 전하께서 브라이턴의 그 멋진 건물 파빌리온에서 마차를 타고 나오실 때 제가 모자를 벗자 황송하게도 '저 사람은 누구인가? 도대체 저자가 누구냔 말이다? 어찌하여 내가 저 사람을 모른단 말인가? 어찌하여 저 사람에게 연 삼만 파운드가 없단 말인가?' 하고 물으셨다는 것을, 제가 말씀드릴 자리는 아닐지도 모릅니다. 하지만 이런 것은 사소한 일화의 부스러기일 뿐 — 누구나 아는 이야기지요, 부인 — 지금도 상류층 사이에서 이따금 회자되고는 합니다.\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n그는 어깨를 치켜올린 절로 대답했습니다. \"우리에게 몸가짐이라 할 만한 것이 남아,\" 그가 덧붙였습니다. \"아직 명맥을 잇는 곳이지요. 영국은 — 아아, 내 조국이여! — 몹시 타락하였고, 나날이 타락해 가고 있습니다. 이제 신사가 그리 많이 남지 않았습니다. 우리는 소수입니다. 우리 뒤를 이을 것이라곤 직조공의 무리 말고는 보이질 않는군요.\"\n\n\"이곳에서 신사의 혈통이 길이 이어지기를 바랄 수도 있겠지요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"참으로 친절하십니다.\" 그는 또다시 어깨를 치켜올린 절과 함께 미소 지었습니다. \"과찬이십니다. 하지만, 아니지요 — 아니에요! 저는 제 가엾은 아들에게 그 기예의 그 부분만큼은 끝내 불어넣어 줄 수가 없었습니다. 제 사랑하는 아이를 깎아내릴 생각은 결코 없습니다만, 그 아이에게는 — 몸가짐이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 선생님이신 듯하던데요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"오해는 마세요, 부인. 그 아이는 훌륭한 선생이 맞습니다. 익힐 수 있는 것은 모두 익혔지요. 전수할 수 있는 것은 모두 전수할 수 있고요. 하지만 그것만으로는 안 되는 것이 — \" 그는 코담배를 또 한 줌 집고는 다시 절을 했으니, 마치 \"이를테면 이런 것 말입니다\" 하고 덧붙이려는 듯했습니다.\n\n나는 방 한가운데로 눈길을 던졌습니다. 거기서 젤리비 양의 연인은 이제 제자들을 한 사람씩 맡아 어느 때보다 더한 고역을 치르고 있었습니다.\n\n\"내 사랑스러운 아이.\" 터비드롭 씨가 목수건을 매만지며 중얼거렸습니다.\n\n\"아드님은 지칠 줄을 모르네요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"그것이 제 보답입니다. 어떤 점에서 그 아이는 성스러운 제 어미의 발자취를 따르고 있지요. 그 사람은 헌신적인 사람이었습니다. 하지만 여인, 사랑스러운 여인이여,\" 터비드롭 씨가 자못 거북한 정중함을 담아 말했습니다. \"그대들은 참으로 대단한 성(性)이로다!\"\n\n나는 일어나 젤리비 양에게 갔는데, 그녀는 이미 보닛을 쓰고 있었습니다. 수업에 할당된 시간이 다 지나, 다들 보닛을 쓰는 참이었습니다. 젤리비 양과 그 불운한 프린스가 언제 약혼할 짬을 냈는지는 모르겠으나, 이날만큼은 분명 열 마디도 주고받을 짬을 내지 못했습니다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 터비드롭 씨가 아들에게 인자하게 말했습니다. \"지금 몇 시인지 아느냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요, 아버지.\" 아들에게는 시계가 없었습니다. 아버지에게는 멋진 금시계가 있어, 그것을 인류의 본보기가 될 만한 태도로 꺼내 들었습니다.\n\n\"아들아,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"두 시구나. 세 시에 켄징턴에서 수업이 있다는 걸 잊지 마라.\"\n\n\"그 정도면 넉넉해요, 아버지,\" 프린스가 말했습니다. \"선 채로 저녁을 한 술 뜨고 곧장 가면 돼요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 아들아,\" 아버지가 대꾸했습니다. \"아주 서둘러야 한다. 식탁에 식은 양고기가 있을 게다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아버지. 이제 나가시는 거예요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야. 아무래도,\" 터비드롭 씨가 눈을 감고 겸손한 자의식과 함께 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했습니다. \"여느 때처럼 시내에 한번 얼굴을 비춰야겠지.\"\n\n\"어디 편한 데서 느긋하게 식사하고 오세요.\" 아들이 말했습니다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 아이야, 그럴 작정이다. 오페라 콜로네이드에 있는 프랑스 식당에서 가볍게 한 끼 들까 한다.\"\n\n\"좋아요. 다녀오세요, 아버지!\" 프린스가 악수하며 말했습니다.\n\n\"잘 있거라, 내 아들아. 신의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n터비드롭 씨가 이 말을 자못 경건하게 했더니, 아들에게 좋은 효험을 주는 듯했습니다. 아들은 아버지와 헤어지며 그를 어찌나 기뻐하고, 그에게 어찌나 효성스럽고, 그를 어찌나 자랑스러워하던지, 이 젊은이를 위해서라도 늙은 아버지를 곧이곧대로 믿어 주지 못하는 것이 그에게 매정한 짓처럼 느껴질 지경이었습니다. 프린스가 우리에게 작별을 고하느라(그리고 내가 보기에 비밀을 아는 처지인 만큼, 특히 우리 중 한 사람에게 작별을 고하느라) 보낸 그 짧은 순간들은, 거의 아이 같은 그의 성품에 대한 내 호의적 인상을 한층 더 짙게 해 주었습니다. 그가 작은 키트를 주머니에 넣으면서 — 캐디와 잠시라도 더 있고 싶은 바람도 그와 함께 거두어 넣으면서 — 식은 양고기와 켄징턴 수업을 향해 싹싹하게 떠나가는 모습을 보니, 나는 그에게 호감과 더불어 연민을 느꼈고, 그 통에 비판적인 노부인 못지않게 그의 아버지에게 화가 났습니다.\n\n아버지는 우리에게 방문을 열어 주고, 솔직히 인정하건대 그 빛나는 원본에 손색없는 태도로 우리를 배웅해 나갔습니다. 같은 태도로 그는 곧 길 건너편에서 우리를 지나쳐, 몇 안 남은 다른 신사들 사이에 모습을 드러내러 시내의 귀족 구역으로 향했습니다. 잠시 나는 뉴먼 가에서 듣고 본 것을 다시 곱씹느라 어찌나 정신이 팔렸던지, 캐디에게 말을 걸기는커녕 그녀가 내게 하는 말에 주의를 모을 수조차 없었습니다. 특히, 무용업에 종사하지 않으면서도 오로지 몸가짐 하나로 살아가며 명성을 쌓은 다른 신사들이 지금 있거나 일찍이 있었던 적이 있을까 마음속으로 헤아리기 시작하면서부터 더욱 그러했습니다. 이런 생각이 어찌나 어지럽고, 또 그토록 많은 터비드롭 씨가 있을 수 있다는 가능성을 떠올리게 했던지, 나는 \"에스더, 이 주제는 죄다 그만 떨쳐 버리고 캐디한테 마음을 쓰기로 단단히 마음먹어야겠다\"라고 다짐했습니다. 그래서 나는 그렇게 했고, 우리는 링컨스인까지 가는 내내 줄곧 이야기를 나누었습니다.\n\n캐디는 자기 연인이 받은 교육이 어찌나 소홀했던지 그의 쪽지를 읽어 내기가 늘 쉽지만은 않다고 말했습니다. 그녀는, 그가 철자에 그렇게 안달하지 않고 그걸 또렷하게 하려고 그토록 애쓰지만 않으면 오히려 더 나을 거라고 했습니다. 그런데 짧은 단어에 쓸데없는 글자를 어찌나 많이 끼워 넣는지, 그것들이 때로는 영어로 보이지도 않을 지경이라는 것이었습니다. \"최선의 뜻으로 그러는 거지만,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그가 바라는 효과는 못 내요, 가엾은 사람!\" 그러고 나서 캐디는 따져 물었습니다. 평생을 무용 학원에서 보내며 가르치고 죽도록 일하고, 죽도록 일하고 가르치는 것 말고는 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 한 게 없는데, 어떻게 그에게 학식을 바랄 수 있겠느냐고요! 그리고 그게 무슨 대수냐고요? 그녀는 — 호되게 겪어 잘 알듯이 — 둘 몫의 편지를 충분히 쓸 수 있고, 그가 학식 있는 것보다 다정한 편이 훨씬 낫다고 했습니다. \"게다가 제가 무슨 교양 있는 처녀라서 거드름을 피울 자격이 있는 것도 아니잖아요,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"엄마 덕분에 정말이지 아는 게 쥐뿔도 없는걸요!\"\n\n\"이제 우리 둘뿐이니 한 가지 더 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있어요,\" 캐디가 말을 이었습니다. \"서머슨 양이 프린스를 보지 않으셨다면 꺼내고 싶지 않았을 얘기예요. 우리 집이 어떤 집인지 아시잖아요. 우리 집에서는 프린스의 아내가 알아 두면 쓸모 있을 만한 걸 뭐 하나 배우려 해도 소용이 없어요. 우리는 그런 난장판 속에서 사니까 불가능하고, 시도할 때마다 더 의기소침해지기만 했어요. 그래서 저는 어디서 조금씩 연습을 하는데 — 어디겠어요? 가엾은 플라이트 양 댁이에요! 이른 아침에 그분 방 정돈하고 새들 돌보는 걸 도와드리고, 커피도 타 드려요(물론 그분이 가르쳐 주셨죠). 그런데 어찌나 잘 타게 됐는지, 프린스는 자기가 마셔 본 커피 중에 단연 최고라면서, 커피에 정말 까다로운 늙은 터비드롭 씨도 아주 흡족해하실 거래요. 저는 작은 푸딩도 만들 줄 알고, 양고기 목살이며 차며 설탕이며 버터며 살림에 필요한 여러 가지를 사는 법도 알아요. 바느질은 아직 솜씨가 없지만요,\" 캐디가 피피의 윗도리에 기운 자리를 흘끔 보며 말했습니다. \"그래도 차차 나아지겠죠. 그리고 프린스와 약혼하고 이런 걸 죄다 하면서, 제 성미도 한결 누그러지고 엄마한테도 더 너그러워진 것 같아요, 그러기를 바라요. 오늘 아침 처음엔 서머슨 양과 클레어 양이 그렇게 단정하고 예쁜 모습이신 걸 보고, 피피와 제 꼴이 부끄러워 좀 속이 상했지만, 그래도 대체로는 제 성미가 예전보다 나아지고 엄마한테도 더 너그러워졌기를 바라요.\"\n\n그토록 애쓰는 이 가엾은 소녀가 진심으로 한 말이라 내 마음을 울렸습니다. \"캐디, 얘야,\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"나는 네게 큰 정이 들기 시작했단다. 우리 친구가 되었으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"오, 정말요?\" 캐디가 외쳤습니다. \"그렇게 되면 얼마나 행복할까요!\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 캐디,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"지금부터 우리 친구가 되자꾸나. 그리고 이런 일들을 자주 함께 이야기하면서 헤쳐 나갈 옳은 길을 찾아보자.\" 캐디는 더없이 기뻐했습니다. 나는 그녀를 위로하고 격려하려고 내 구식대로 할 수 있는 말은 다 했고, 그날만큼은 며느리 앞으로 재산을 떼어 준다는 것보다 못한 어떤 조건으로도 늙은 터비드롭 씨에게 이의를 달고 싶지 않았을 것입니다.\n\n이 무렵 우리는 크룩 씨의 가게에 이르렀는데, 그 개인 출입문이 열려 있었습니다. 문기둥에 한 장의 광고가 붙어, 삼층에 세놓을 방이 있다고 알리고 있었습니다. 그것을 보고 캐디는, 위층으로 올라가면서 내게 들려주기를, 그곳에서 갑작스러운 죽음이 있었고 검시가 있었으며, 우리의 작은 친구가 그 놀란 충격으로 앓아누웠다고 했습니다. 빈방의 문과 창이 열려 있어, 우리는 안을 들여다보았습니다. 지난번 내가 이 집에 왔을 때 플라이트 양이 몰래 내 주의를 끌었던, 그 검은 문이 달린 방이었습니다. 그곳은 슬프고 황량한 곳, 음울하고 비통한 곳이어서, 내게 야릇한 애상감, 아니 두려움마저 안겨 주었습니다. \"안색이 창백해요,\" 우리가 밖으로 나오자 캐디가 말했습니다. \"게다가 추워 보이고요!\" 나는 그 방이 나를 오싹하게 만든 것만 같았습니다.\n\n이야기하느라 우리는 천천히 걸었던 터라, 후견인과 에이다가 우리보다 먼저 와 있었습니다. 우리는 두 사람을 플라이트 양의 다락방에서 만났습니다. 그들은 새들을 들여다보고 있었고, 그동안 깊은 배려와 연민으로 친절히 플라이트 양을 돌보는 한 의사 양반이 난롯가에서 그녀와 명랑하게 이야기를 나누고 있었습니다.\n\n\"왕진을 마쳤습니다,\" 그가 앞으로 나서며 말했습니다. \"플라이트 양은 한결 좋아지셔서, (마음을 그리 굳히셨으니) 내일은 법정에 나가셔도 되겠습니다. 듣자 하니 거기서 그분을 무척 그리워했다더군요.\"\n\n플라이트 양은 그 칭찬을 흐뭇하게 받으며 우리 모두에게 두루 인사를 했습니다.\n\n\"참으로 영광이에요,\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이 또 찾아와 주시다니! 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 어른을 누추한 제 지붕 아래 모시게 되어 무 ─ 척 기쁩니다!\" 하고 특별히 한 번 더 인사했습니다. \"피츠잔다이스, 얘야 ─\" 알고 보니 그녀는 캐디에게 그 이름을 붙여 주고 늘 그렇게 불렀습니다 ─ \"너에게는 두 곱절로 환영을!\"\n\n\"많이 편찮으셨던가요?\" 잔다이스 씨가, 그녀를 돌보고 있던 양반에게 물었습니다. 그가 속삭이듯 물었는데도, 그녀가 곧장 자기 입으로 대답했습니다.\n\n\"오, 확실히 편치 않았지요! 오, 정말 무척 편치 않았어요,\" 그녀가 은밀히 말했습니다. \"아픈 게 아니라, 아시잖아요 ─ 심란한 거예요. 몸이라기보다 신경, 신경이 그래요! 사실은,\" 가라앉은 목소리로 떨면서 말했습니다. \"여기서 죽음이 있었답니다. 이 집에 독이 들었어요. 저는 그런 끔찍한 일에 아주 민감하답니다. 그게 저를 겁에 질리게 했어요. 얼마나 그랬는지는 우드코트 선생님만 아세요. 제 주치의, 우드코트 선생님이세요!\" 자못 위엄을 갖추어 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들 ─ 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 어른 ─ 피츠잔다이스!\"\n\n\"플라이트 양은,\" 우드코트 씨가, 우리에게 말하면서도 그녀에게 호소하는 듯한 진지한 목소리로, 그녀의 팔에 손을 살며시 얹으며 말했습니다. \"여느 때처럼 정확하게 당신의 병환을 설명하십니다. 더 강건한 사람도 놀랐을 법한 이 집의 한 사건에 놀라셨고, 그 비탄과 동요로 병이 나신 겁니다. 그분이 발견 직후의 그 황급한 와중에 저를 이리로 데려오셨는데, 그 가엾은 사내에게는 제가 아무 도움이 될 수 없을 만큼 이미 너무 늦었더군요. 그 실망에 대한 보상으로, 저는 그 후로 이곳을 찾아 그분께 조금이나마 도움이 되어 드리고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"의사회에서 가장 친절한 의사 선생님이세요,\" 플라이트 양이 내게 속삭였습니다. \"저는 판결을 기다린답니다. 최후의 심판 날에요. 그러면 영지를 나눠 드릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"하루 이틀이면 이분도,\" 우드코트 씨가 살피는 미소로 그녀를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"지금껏 늘 그러셨던 만큼은 좋아지실 겁니다. 다시 말해, 물론 아주 건강해지신다는 뜻이지요. 이분의 횡재 소식은 들으셨나요?\"\n\n\"더없이 놀라운 일이지요!\" 플라이트 양이 환하게 웃으며 말했습니다. \"그런 얘긴 들어 본 적도 없으실 거예요, 얘야! 토요일마다 '입심 좋은 켄지', 아니면 (입심 좋은 켄지의 서기인) 거피가 제 손에 실링이 든 종이 꾸러미를 쥐여 준답니다. 실링이에요. 정말이라니까요! 늘 종이 안에 똑같은 개수가 들어 있어요. 늘 한 주의 매일에 하나씩요. 자, 이거 정말이지! 어쩜 그리 때맞춰 오는지, 안 그래요? 그─럼요! 그 꾸러미가 어디서 오느냐고요? 그게 바로 큰 의문이지요. 당연하고말고요. 제 생각을 말씀드릴까요? 제 생각엔,\" 플라이트 양이 자못 영민한 눈빛으로 몸을 뒤로 빼며, 오른쪽 집게손가락을 더없이 의미심장하게 흔들면서 말했습니다. \"대법원장님이 옥새가 그토록 오래 열려 있었던 것을 아시고는(정말로 오래 열려 있었으니까요!), 그걸 보내 주시는 거예요. 제가 기다리는 그 판결이 내려질 때까지요. 자, 이거 참으로 갸륵한 일이지요. 인간의 수명에 비해 자기가 좀 굼뜨다는 걸 그렇게 인정하시다니요. 어쩜 그리 섬세하신지! 저번에 법정에 갔을 때 ─ 저는 제 서류들을 들고 꼬박꼬박 나간답니다 ─ 그 일로 그분을 추궁했더니, 거의 인정하시더라고요. 그러니까, 제 자리에서 제가 그분께 미소 지었고, 그분도 자기 자리에서 제게 미소 지으셨어요. 하지만 이거 큰 횡재지요, 안 그래요? 게다가 피츠잔다이스가 그 돈을 저를 위해 아주 유리하게 굴려 준답니다. 오, 정말이지 더없이 유리하게요!\"\n\n나는 (그녀가 내게 말을 건넸으므로) 수입에 이 다행스러운 보탬이 생긴 것을 축하하고, 그것이 오래도록 이어지기를 빌어 주었습니다. 나는 그 돈이 어디서 오는지 짐작해 보지도, 누구의 인정이 그토록 사려 깊은지 궁금해하지도 않았습니다. 후견인이 내 앞에서 새들을 바라보며 서 있었으니, 나는 그분 너머로 눈길을 둘 필요가 없었습니다.\n\n\"그런데 이 작은 녀석들을 뭐라고 부르시나요, 부인?\" 그분이 그 다정한 목소리로 말했습니다. \"이름이라도 있나요?\"\n\n\"플라이트 양을 대신해서 제가 장담하건대 이름이 있어요,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"이름이 뭔지 말해 주겠다고 약속하셨거든요. 에이다도 기억하지?\"\n\n에이다는 아주 잘 기억하고 있었습니다.\n\n\"제가 그랬던가요?\" 플라이트 양이 말했습니다. \"내 문 앞에 있는 게 누구지? 무엇하러 내 문 앞에서 엿듣는 거예요, 크룩?\"\n\n집주인 노인이 문을 밀어 열며, 모피 모자를 손에 들고 고양이를 발치에 거느린 채 거기 나타났습니다.\n\n\"엿들은 거 아니에요, 플라이트 양,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"주먹으로 문을 똑똑 두드리려던 참인데, 어찌나 잽싸신지!\"\n\n\"그 고양이 내려보내요. 쫓아 버려요!\" 노부인이 화를 내며 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"쯧, 쯧! 위험할 거 없습니다요, 손님들,\" 크룩 씨가 우리 한 사람 한 사람을 다 둘러볼 때까지 천천히 날카롭게 눈길을 옮기며 말했습니다. \"제가 시키지 않는 한, 제가 여기 있을 땐 이놈이 절대 새들한테 덤비는 법이 없어요.\"\n\n\"제 집주인을 양해해 주세요,\" 노부인이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했습니다. \"엠(M)이에요, 완전히 엠이라고요! 무슨 일이에요, 크룩, 내게 손님이 와 계신데?\"\n\n\"하이!\" 노인이 말했습니다. \"내가 대법원장인 거 아시잖수.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 플라이트 양이 대꾸했습니다. \"그게 어쨌다고요?\"\n\n\"대법원장이,\" 노인이 킬킬거리며 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 한 분을 모르고 지낸다는 게 우습잖수, 안 그래요, 플라이트 양? 제가 외람되이 한 말씀 드려도 될까요? 안녕하십니까, 어른. 저는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 사건을 어른 못지않게 훤히 알고 있습죠. 옛날 톰 영주님도 알았고요. 하지만 제가 알기로 어른을 전에 뵌 적은 없어요, 법정에서조차요. 그래도 저는 한 해 동안 어느 날 어느 날 합치면 정말 무던히도 자주 거길 간답니다.\"\n\n\"나는 거기 가는 일이 없소,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다(실제로 그분은 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 가지 않았습니다). \"차라리 ─ 다른 어디라도 가지요.\"\n\n\"그러시다고요?\" 크룩이 히죽거리며 대꾸했습니다. \"어른께서는 그 뜻으로 제 고결하고 학식 높은 형제분을 호되게 나무라시는 셈이지만, 잔다이스 가문 분이시니 어쩌면 당연한 노릇이겠죠. 불에 덴 아이라잖습니까요, 어른! 아니, 제 하숙인 새들을 보고 계셨네요, 잔다이스 어른?\" 노인은 조금씩 조금씩 방 안으로 들어와, 어느새 팔꿈치로 내 후견인을 건드리며 안경 낀 눈으로 그분 얼굴을 바싹 들여다보았습니다. \"저 새들 이름은 자기가 다 지어 놓고도, 어지간하면 절대 안 가르쳐 주려고 하는 게 저분의 별난 버릇 가운데 하나죠.\" 이건 속삭임이었습니다. \"내가 죽 읊어 볼까요, 플라이트?\" 그가 큰 소리로 물으며 우리에게 눈을 찡긋하고는, 모른 척 화덕을 쓰는 시늉을 하며 돌아서는 그녀를 손가락으로 가리켰습니다.\n\n\"좋으실 대로요.\" 그녀가 황급히 대답했습니다.\n\n노인은 우리를 또 한 번 보고 나서 새장들을 올려다보며, 그 목록을 죽 읊었습니다.\n\n\"희망, 환희, 청춘, 평화, 안식, 생명, 먼지, 잿더미, 황폐, 결핍, 파멸, 절망, 광기, 죽음, 교활, 어리석음, 말, 가발, 누더기, 양피지, 노략질, 판례, 횡설수설, 헛소리, 그리고 시금치. 이게 컬렉션 전부예요,\" 노인이 말했습니다. \"제 고결하고 학식 높은 형제분 덕분에 한데 갇혀 있는 신세죠.\"\n\n\"매서운 바람일세!\" 내 후견인이 중얼거렸습니다.\n\n\"제 고결하고 학식 높은 형제분이 판결을 내려 주시면, 저것들을 풀어 줄 거예요,\" 크룩이 다시 우리에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했습니다. \"그러고 나면,\" 그가 속삭이며 히죽거리며 덧붙였습니다. \"그런 일이 정말로 일어난다면 ─ 그럴 리야 없겠지만 ─ 한 번도 갇혀 본 적 없는 새들이 저것들을 물어 죽이겠죠.\"\n\n\"바람이 정녕 동쪽에서 분 적이 있다면,\" 내 후견인이 풍향계를 찾는 척 창밖을 내다보며 말했습니다. \"오늘이 바로 그날인 것 같군!\"\n\n우리는 그 집에서 떠나기가 무척 어려웠습니다. 우리를 붙든 건 플라이트 양이 아니었습니다. 그녀로 말하면 남의 편의를 헤아리는 데서 그보다 더 분별 있을 수 없을 만큼 사리 밝은 작은 사람이었습니다. 붙든 것은 크룩 씨였습니다. 그는 잔다이스 씨에게서 떨어질 줄을 모르는 듯했습니다. 그에게 사슬로 묶여 있었다 한들 그보다 더 바싹 붙어 따라다닐 수는 없었을 것입니다. 그는 자신의 대법원과 그 안에 든 온갖 기묘한 잡동사니를 보여 주겠다고 했습니다. (그 자신이 한껏 늘인) 둘러보는 내내, 그는 잔다이스 씨에게 바싹 붙어 다니며, 때로는 이런저런 핑계로 그분을 붙들어 두었다가 우리가 먼저 지나가게 했으니, 마치 차마 다가갈 결심을 못 하는 어떤 은밀한 화제를 꺼내고 싶은 충동에 시달리는 것만 같았습니다. 그날 크룩 씨의 표정과 태도처럼 신중함과 우유부단함을, 또 무언가 하고 싶으면서도 차마 감행할 결심을 못 하는 끊임없는 충동을, 그토록 유난히 또렷하게 드러내는 얼굴과 몸짓을 나는 달리 떠올릴 수가 없습니다. 내 후견인을 살피는 그의 눈길은 한시도 그치지 않았습니다. 그분 얼굴에서 좀처럼 눈을 떼지 않았던 것입니다. 그분 옆에서 걸을 때면 늙은 흰여우 같은 음흉함으로 그분을 관찰했습니다. 앞장서 갈 때면 뒤를 돌아보았습니다. 우리가 멈춰 서면 그분 맞은편에 자리를 잡고는, 무언가 힘을 쥐었다는 야릇한 표정으로 벌린 입을 손으로 거듭거듭 쓸어내리며, 눈을 치뜨고 잿빛 눈썹을 내려 마치 감긴 듯 보일 때까지 내리누른 채, 그분 얼굴의 모든 윤곽을 샅샅이 뜯어보는 듯했습니다.\n\n마침내, (늘 고양이를 거느리고) 집 안을 죄다 돌아보고, 그 잡다한 폐물 더미를 ─ 분명 신기하긴 했습니다 ─ 모조리 구경한 끝에, 우리는 가게 뒤편으로 들어섰습니다. 거기 거꾸로 세워 둔 빈 통 머리 위에는 잉크병 하나와 닳아빠진 펜 토막 몇 개, 그리고 더러운 공연 전단 몇 장이 놓여 있었고, 벽에는 여러 종류의 단순한 필체로 인쇄한 큼지막한 알파벳 표가 여러 장 붙어 있었습니다.\n\n\"여기서 뭘 하고 계신가요?\" 내 후견인이 물었습니다.\n\n\"혼자 읽고 쓰는 법을 익혀 보려고요.\" 크룩이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 잘 되어 가나요?\"\n\n\"더디지요. 영 신통찮아요,\" 노인이 조급하게 대꾸했습니다. \"이 나이엔 힘든 일이에요.\"\n\n\"누구한테 배우면 더 수월할 텐데요.\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"옳거니, 하지만 잘못 가르칠지도 모르잖수!\" 노인이 놀랍도록 의심 어린 눈빛을 번득이며 대꾸했습니다. \"진작 못 배워서 제가 뭘 손해 봤는지는 모르겠어요. 그렇다고 이제 와 잘못 배워서 뭐라도 손해 보긴 싫거든요.\"\n\n\"잘못 가르친다니요?\" 내 후견인이 그 너그러운 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. \"도대체 누가 당신을 잘못 가르치겠어요?\"\n\n\"모르죠, 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 어른!\" 노인이 안경을 이마 위로 밀어 올리고 두 손을 비비며 대꾸했습니다. \"그럴 사람이야 없겠지만, 저는 남보다 제 자신을 믿는 편이 낫겠어요!\"\n\n이런 대답과 그의 태도가 어찌나 기묘했던지, 내 후견인은 우리 모두가 함께 링컨스인을 가로질러 걷는 동안 우드코트 씨에게, 크룩 씨가 정말로 그 하숙인이 말하듯 정신이 온전치 못한 것이냐고 물었습니다. 젊은 외과의는, 아니라고, 그렇게 여길 만한 근거는 보지 못했다고 대답했습니다. 무지가 으레 그렇듯 그는 지독히도 의심이 많고, 늘 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 독한 진의 기운에 절어 있는데 ─ 그 술을 엄청나게 마셔 대는 통에, 우리도 알아챘다시피 그와 그 가게 뒤편에서 독한 술 냄새가 진동했습니다 ─ 그래도 아직 미쳤다고는 보지 않는다고 했습니다.\n\n집으로 돌아오는 길에, 나는 피피에게 풍차 하나와 밀가루 자루 두 개를 사 주어 그 아이의 호감을 어찌나 단단히 사 두었던지, 그는 다른 누구도 자기 모자와 장갑을 벗기지 못하게 했고, 저녁 식탁에서도 내 곁 말고는 어디에도 앉으려 하지 않았습니다. 캐디는 내 반대편, 에이다 옆에 앉았는데, 우리는 돌아오자마자 에이다에게 약혼의 전말을 죄다 들려주었습니다. 우리는 캐디를, 그리고 피피까지도 한껏 떠받들었고, 캐디는 더없이 환해졌으며, 내 후견인도 우리만큼이나 즐거워했습니다. 밤에 캐디가 삯마차를 타고 집으로 돌아갈 때까지 ─ 피피는 곤히 잠들었지만 풍차만은 꼭 움켜쥔 채로였습니다 ─ 우리 모두는 정말이지 더없이 행복했습니다.\n\n내가 미처 말하지 못한 것이 있는데 ─ 적어도 언급하지 않은 것이 있는데 ─ 우드코트 씨가 바로 우리가 배저 씨 댁에서 만났던 그 가무잡잡한 젊은 외과의였다는 것입니다. 또 잔다이스 씨가 그날 그를 저녁 식사에 초대했다는 것, 그가 왔다는 것도요. 또 다들 돌아가고 나서 내가 에이다에게 \"자, 내 사랑, 우리 리처드 얘기나 좀 해 볼까!\" 하고 말했더니, 에이다가 웃으며 말했다는 것도요 ─\n\n하지만 내 사랑이 무슨 말을 했는지는 중요치 않은 것 같습니다. 그 애는 언제나 명랑했으니까요.\n\n우리가 런던에 머무는 동안 잔다이스 씨는 그 행동거지로 우리를 그토록 놀라게 한 흥분 잘하는 신사 숙녀 무리에게 끊임없이 시달렸습니다. 우리가 도착하고 얼마 안 되어 모습을 드러낸 퀘일 씨도 그런 흥분에는 빠짐없이 끼어 있었습니다. 그는 번들거리는 두 관자놀이 혹을 무슨 일이든 닥치는 대로 들이밀고, 머리카락을 점점 더 뒤로 빗어 넘기는 통에 마침내 그 뿌리가 달랠 길 없는 박애심에 못 이겨 머리에서 튀어나오기 직전처럼 보일 지경이었습니다. 그에게는 무엇이든 다 똑같았지만, 누구에게든 표창장을 바치는 일이라면 언제나 유난히 채비가 되어 있었습니다. 그의 위대한 재능은 무엇이든 가리지 않고 찬탄하는 능력인 듯했습니다. 그는 어느 등급의 빛나는 인물이 발하는 광채든 그 빛에 관자놀이를 적시면서 더없는 즐거움에 잠겨 얼마든지 오래도록 앉아 있을 수 있었습니다. 처음에 그가 젤리비 부인에 대한 찬탄에 완전히 빠져드는 모습을 본 나는, 그녀가 그의 헌신을 통째로 빨아들이는 대상인 줄로만 알았습니다. 나는 곧 내 착각을 깨닫고, 그가 한 무리의 사람들이 줄지어 행진하는 그 모든 행렬에서 옷자락을 받쳐 들고 풍금에 바람을 넣는 사람이라는 사실을 알게 되었습니다.\n\n어느 날 파디글 부인이 무언가에 기부금을 받으러 왔는데, 함께 온 사람이 퀘일 씨였습니다. 파디글 부인이 무슨 말을 하든 퀘일 씨가 우리에게 되풀이했고, 젤리비 부인의 말을 끌어냈듯이 파디글 부인의 말도 끌어냈습니다. 파디글 부인은 자기의 능변가 친구 거셔 씨를 위해 내 후견인에게 소개장을 한 통 써 주었습니다. 거셔 씨가 나타날 때도 퀘일 씨가 또 함께였습니다. 거셔 씨는 표면이 축축하게 늘어진 신사로, 그 둥근 보름달 같은 얼굴에 비해 눈이 턱없이 작아 본래 다른 누군가를 위해 만들어진 것을 가져다 박은 듯이 보였으니, 첫눈에 호감을 주는 인물은 아니었습니다. 그런데도 그가 채 자리에 앉기도 전에 퀘일 씨가 에이다와 나에게, 다 들릴 만한 목소리로, 저분이 위대한 인물이 아니냐고 물었습니다. 늘어진 외양으로 따지자면 분명 그러했지만, 퀘일 씨는 지적인 아름다움을 두고 한 말이었습니다. 그러고는 우리가 저 우람한 이마의 생김새에 감탄하지 않느냐고 물었습니다. 한마디로, 우리는 이 무리 사이에서 갖가지 사명에 관한 이야기를 무척 많이 들었지만, 그 어느 것도 우리에게 절반만큼도 분명하지 않았던 데 비해, 퀘일 씨의 사명이란 다른 모든 사람의 사명에 황홀해하는 것이며 그것이야말로 가장 인기 있는 사명이라는 사실만큼은 더없이 또렷했습니다.\n\n잔다이스 씨가 이 무리에 발을 들인 것은 마음이 여린 데다 자기 힘닿는 데까지 온갖 선을 베풀고자 하는 간절한 바람 때문이었습니다. 그러나 그것이 너무도 자주 만족스럽지 못한 무리라고 그는 우리에게 솔직히 말했습니다. 여기서는 자선이 발작하듯 터져 나오고, 값싼 악명을 노리는 떠벌이꾼과 투기꾼들이 박애를 무슨 제복인 양 걸치고 다니며, 입으로는 격렬하고 행동에서는 들떠 허영에 차 있으며, 권세 있는 자에게는 비굴함의 밑바닥까지 굽실거리고, 서로를 치켜세우면서, 약한 자가 쓰러지지 않도록 조용히 도우려는 이들에게는 견딜 수 없는 존재가 되니, 약한 자가 쓰러진 뒤에야 요란을 떨고 제 자랑을 늘어놓으며 겨우 조금 일으켜 세우는 자들이었습니다. 거셔 씨가 퀘일 씨에게 표창장을 바치고(퀘일 씨가 먼저 거셔 씨에게 표창장을 바친 적이 있었지요), 거셔 씨가 그 일을 두고 한 시간 반 동안 모임에서 연설을 했는데, 그 자리에는 어린 소년 소녀들이 다니는 자선 학교 두 곳도 끼어 있어, 그 아이들에게 과부의 동전 이야기를 특별히 일깨우며 반 페니씩 들고 나와 가상한 제물이 되라고 청했을 때에는, 동풍이 꼬박 삼 주 동안 불었던 것 같습니다.\n\n내가 이런 이야기를 꺼내는 까닭은 다시 스킴폴 씨 이야기로 넘어가기 위해서입니다. 자기는 어린애처럼 철없고 무심할 뿐이라는 그의 거리낌 없는 고백이, 이런 일들과 견주어 보면 내 후견인에게 큰 위안이 되었던 듯하고, 또 그만큼 더 쉽게 믿어졌던 것 같습니다. 정반대인 사람들이 우글거리는 가운데 아무 꿍꿍이 없이 솔직한 사람을 하나라도 발견한다는 것은 그에게 기쁨을 주지 않을 수 없었으니까요. 스킴폴 씨가 이를 알아채고 약삭빠르게 처신했다고 넌지시 말하려는 것은 아닙니다. 나는 정말이지 그를 그만큼 잘 이해한 적이 없어 알 수가 없었습니다. 그가 내 후견인에게 어떤 사람이었든, 세상 나머지 사람들에게도 분명 똑같은 사람이었습니다.\n\n그는 몸이 그리 좋지 않았습니다. 그래서 런던에 살면서도 우리는 이제껏 그를 통 보지 못했습니다. 그가 어느 날 아침, 늘 그렇듯 호감 가는 태도로, 여느 때처럼 유쾌한 기운을 잔뜩 머금고 나타났습니다.\n\n자, 그가 말하기를, 자기가 왔다는 것이었습니다! 담즙이 끓어 속이 좋지 않았는데, 부유한 사람들도 흔히 담즙이 끓는 법이니, 그래서 자기도 재산가라고 스스로 믿어 왔다는 것이었습니다. 어떤 관점에서 보면 그는 정말로 재산가였습니다. 다시 말해 그 너그러운 마음씨에서요. 그는 자기를 돌보는 의사를 더없이 후하게 부유하게 해 주었다고 했습니다. 진료비를 늘 곱절로, 때로는 네 곱절로 쳐 주었다는 것이었습니다. 그는 의사에게 이렇게 말했다고 합니다. \"여보게, 친애하는 의사 선생, 자네가 나를 거저 봐준다고 여긴다면 그건 자네의 착각일세. 나는 자네에게 돈을 퍼붓고 있다네. 내 너그러운 마음속에서 말일세. 자네가 그걸 알기만 한다면!\" 그리고 정말로(그가 말하기를) 그는 그렇게 마음먹은 정도가, 실제로 돈을 주는 것과 거의 다를 바 없다고 여길 만큼이었다는 것이었습니다. 인류가 그토록 중하게 여기는 그 쇳조각이나 얇은 종잇장을 손에 쥐고 있었더라면 의사의 손에 쥐여 주었을 것이고, 그것이 없으니 행위 대신 마음을 내놓았다는 것이었습니다. 좋습니다! 그가 정말로 그렇게 마음먹었다면, 그의 마음이 진짜이고 진실하다면, 사실 그러했으니, 그에게는 그것이 동전과 다름없어 빚을 갚은 셈이 된다는 것이었습니다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그건 부분적으로 내가 돈의 가치라는 걸 도통 모르기 때문일 수도 있겠지요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 나는 자주 이렇게 느낍니다. 너무도 이치에 맞는 것 같거든요! 우리 정육점 주인이 나한테 그 자그마한 청구서를 받았으면 한다고 말합니다. 그가 늘 그걸 '자그마한' 청구서라고 부르는 건 그 사람 천성에 깃든 흐뭇하고 무심한 시심의 한 자락인데, 우리 둘 다에게 지불을 쉬워 보이게 하려는 게지요. 나는 정육점 주인에게 이렇게 대답합니다. '여보게, 자네가 그걸 안다면, 자네는 이미 받은 셈일세. 자그마한 청구서를 받으러 오는 수고도 들지 않았고. 자네는 받은 거야. 진심으로 하는 말일세.'\"\n\n\"하지만 만약에 말이오,\" 내 후견인이 웃으며 말했습니다. \"그자가 고기를 대주는 대신 청구서 속의 고기로 마음만 먹었다고 한다면 어떻겠소?\"\n\n\"이보시오, 친애하는 잔다이스,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"당신은 나를 놀라게 하는구려. 정육점 주인의 입장에 서다니. 한번은 내가 거래하던 정육점 주인이 바로 그 자리에 섰지요. 그가 말합디다. '나리, 어쩌자고 파운드당 십팔 펜스나 하는 봄철 새끼 양고기를 잡수셨습니까?' '내가 어쩌자고 파운드당 십팔 펜스짜리 봄철 새끼 양고기를 먹었느냐고, 여보게 정직한 친구?' 나는 그 질문에 당연히 깜짝 놀라 말했지요. '봄철 새끼 양고기가 좋으니까!' 여기까지는 그럴듯하게 들렸습니다. '그렇다면 나리,' 그가 말합디다. '나리께서 돈을 마음먹으시듯 나도 그 양고기를 마음만 먹었더라면 좋았겠습니다!' '여보게,' 내가 말했지요. '제발 지성을 가진 존재답게 따져 봅세. 어떻게 그럴 수가 있겠나? 그건 불가능하지. 자네는 양고기를 가지고 있었고, 나는 돈을 가지고 있지 않네. 자네는 양고기를 부쳐 주지 않고서야 정말로 마음먹을 수가 없지만, 나는 돈을 치르지 않고서도 정말로 마음먹을 수 있고, 실제로 그러고 있지 않은가!' 그는 한마디도 못 합디다. 그것으로 그 이야기는 끝이었지요.\"\n\n\"그자가 법적 절차는 밟지 않았소?\" 내 후견인이 물었습니다.\n\n\"네, 법적 절차를 밟았습니다.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 그 일에서 그는 이성이 아니라 격정에 휘둘린 거지요. 격정이라니 보이손이 떠오르는군요. 그가 나한테 편지를 보냈는데, 당신과 두 아가씨가 링컨셔에 있는 자기 독신자 저택에 잠깐 들르기로 약속했다더군요.\"\n\n\"그 친구는 우리 아가씨들이 무척 좋아하지요.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그래서 내가 아이들 대신 약속을 했소.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 자연이 그 사람을 빚어낼 때 음영을 넣는 걸 깜빡한 것 같습니다.\" 스킴폴 씨가 에이다와 나를 돌아보며 말했습니다. \"조금 지나치게 시끄럽거든요. 꼭 바다처럼요. 조금 지나치게 격렬하고요. 무슨 색이든 다 진홍빛이라고 단단히 작정한 황소처럼 말이지요. 하지만 그에게 무쇠 망치 같은 미덕이 있다는 건 인정합니다!\"\n\n저 두 사람이 서로를 그리 높이 평가할 수 있다면 나는 오히려 놀랐을 것입니다. 보이손 씨는 수많은 것에 그토록 큰 의미를 두는 사람이고 스킴폴 씨는 무엇에도 그토록 무심한 사람이니까요. 게다가 나는 스킴폴 씨 이야기만 나오면 보이손 씨가 무언가 강한 의견을 터뜨리기 직전까지 가는 모습을 한두 번 본 적이 있었습니다. 물론 나는 그저 에이다와 더불어, 그분이 무척 좋았다고만 말했습니다.\n\n\"그가 나를 초대했지요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그러니 한 아이가 그런 손에 자기 몸을 맡겨도 된다면, 지금 이 아이로 말하자면 두 천사의 다정함이 한데 모여 지켜 주고 있으니 마음 놓고 그럴 수 있겠지요, 나는 가렵니다. 그가 갈 때나 올 때나 비용을 대 주겠다고 합디다. 그게 돈이 들겠지요? 실링쯤일까요? 아니면 파운드쯤? 뭐 그런 거겠지요? 그러고 보니, 코빈시스. 우리 친구 코빈시스를 기억하시지요, 서머슨 양?\"\n\n그는 마음속에 그 화제가 떠오르는 대로, 우아하고 가벼운 태도로, 조금도 거북해하는 기색 없이 나에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"아, 네!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"코빈시스가 위대한 집행관에게 체포되었답니다.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그는 다시는 햇빛에 폭력을 휘두르지 못하게 됐지요.\"\n\n그 말을 들으니 나는 적잖이 충격을 받았습니다. 그날 밤 소파에 앉아 이마를 닦던 그 사람의 모습이 떠올랐는데, 결코 진지하달 수 없는 기분으로 떠올렸던 터였으니까요.\n\n\"그의 후임이 어제 그 소식을 전해 주더군요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그 후임이 지금 우리 집에 와 있어요. 자기 말로는 점유 중이라던가요. 그가 어제 왔는데, 하필이면 내 푸른 눈의 딸 생일이었지요. 내가 그에게 따졌습니다. '이건 도리에 맞지도 않고 곤란한 일이오. 당신에게 푸른 눈의 딸이 있다면, 내가 초대도 받지 않고 그 애 생일에 찾아오는 걸 좋아하지 않을 게 아니오?' 하지만 그는 그대로 눌러앉았습니다.\"\n\n스킴폴 씨는 그 유쾌한 어처구니없음에 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 곁에 앉아 있던 피아노를 가볍게 짚었습니다.\n\n\"그리고 그가 나한테 말하기를,\" 그가 말했는데, 내가 마침표를 찍는 자리마다 작은 화음을 짚었습니다. \"코빈시스가 떠났다고. 아이가 셋. 어머니는 없고. 게다가 코빈시스의 직업이. 인기가 없어서. 자라나는 코빈시스들이. 상당히 불리한 처지에 놓였다고.\"\n\n잔다이스 씨가 머리를 문지르며 일어나 이리저리 거닐기 시작했습니다. 스킴폴 씨는 에이다가 좋아하는 노래 가운데 한 곡의 가락을 연주했습니다. 에이다와 나는 둘 다 잔다이스 씨를 바라보며, 그의 마음속에 무슨 생각이 오가는지 우리가 안다고 여겼습니다.\n\n이리저리 거닐다가 멈추고, 몇 번이고 머리 문지르기를 그만두었다가 다시 시작하던 끝에, 내 후견인이 건반 위에 손을 얹어 스킴폴 씨의 연주를 멈추게 했습니다. \"이건 마음에 들지 않소, 스킴폴.\" 그가 생각에 잠겨 말했습니다.\n\n그 화제를 까맣게 잊고 있던 스킴폴 씨가 놀라며 고개를 들었습니다.\n\n\"그 사람은 꼭 필요한 사람이었소.\" 내 후견인이 말을 이으며, 피아노와 방 끝 사이의 아주 좁은 공간을 앞뒤로 오가면서, 거센 동풍이 그렇게 불어 놓기라도 한 듯 뒤통수 쪽 머리카락을 위로 쓸어 올렸습니다. \"우리가 우리의 잘못과 어리석음으로, 혹은 세상 물정에 어두워서, 혹은 우리의 불행 탓에 그런 사람들을 필요로 만들어 놓고서는, 그들에게 앙갚음을 해서는 안 되오. 그의 직업에는 잘못이 없었소. 그는 제 아이들을 먹여 살렸소. 이 일을 좀 더 알아보고 싶구려.\"\n\n\"아! 코빈시스 말이오?\" 스킴폴 씨가 마침내 그 뜻을 알아채고 소리쳤습니다. \"그보다 쉬운 일이 없지요. 코빈시스의 본거지까지 걸어가면, 당신이 알고 싶은 걸 다 알 수 있습니다.\"\n\n잔다이스 씨가 신호만 기다리던 우리에게 고갯짓을 했습니다. \"자! 그쪽으로 걸어가 보자, 얘들아. 다른 데보다 그쪽으로 가지 못할 까닭이 어디 있겠느냐!\" 우리는 얼른 채비를 하고 밖으로 나섰습니다. 스킴폴 씨도 우리와 함께 가며 그 행차를 무척 즐거워했습니다. 코빈시스가 자기를 찾는 게 아니라 자기가 코빈시스를 찾으니, 너무도 새롭고 너무도 상쾌하다고 그가 말했습니다!\n\n그는 우리를 먼저 챈서리 길의 커시터 거리로 데려갔는데, 거기에는 창살을 두른 창문이 달린 집 한 채가 있었습니다. 그는 그곳을 코빈시스의 성이라고 불렀습니다. 우리가 입구로 들어가 종을 울리자, 사무실 비슷한 곳에서 아주 흉하게 생긴 소년 하나가 나와 끝이 뾰족한 쪽문 너머로 우리를 쳐다보았습니다.\n\n\"누굴 찾으세요?\" 소년이 뾰족한 살 두 개를 제 턱에 끼우며 말했습니다.\n\n\"여기 추심꾼인지, 집행관인지, 뭐 그런 사람이 하나 있었는데,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"죽었다더구나.\"\n\n\"네?\" 소년이 말했습니다. \"그래서요?\"\n\n\"괜찮다면 그 사람 이름을 알고 싶구나.\"\n\n\"네킷이란 사람이에요.\" 소년이 말했습니다.\n\n\"주소는?\"\n\n\"벨 야드요.\" 소년이 말했습니다. \"양초 가게, 왼쪽 편, 블라인더라는 사람.\"\n\n\"그 사람은, 어떻게 물어야 할지 모르겠다만,\" 내 후견인이 중얼거렸습니다. \"부지런했느냐?\"\n\n\"네킷 말이에요?\" 소년이 말했습니다. \"네, 아주 그랬죠. 망보는 데는 지칠 줄을 몰랐어요. 한번 맡으면 길모퉁이 기둥에 여덟 시간이고 열 시간이고 내처 붙어 있었다니까요.\"\n\n\"그보다 못할 수도 있었겠지.\" 내 후견인이 혼잣말하는 것을 나는 들었습니다. \"맡고도 안 할 수도 있었을 테니. 고맙구나. 내가 알고 싶은 건 그게 다란다.\"\n\n우리는 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 두 팔을 문에 걸친 채 뾰족한 살을 어루만지고 빨아 대는 소년을 남겨 두고, 링컨 법학원으로 돌아갔습니다. 코빈시스에 더 가까이 머물고 싶어 하지 않던 스킴폴 씨가 거기서 우리를 기다리고 있었습니다. 그러고 나서 우리는 다 같이 벨 야드로 갔는데, 아주 가까운 거리에 있는 좁은 골목이었습니다. 우리는 곧 양초 가게를 찾아냈습니다. 그 안에는 사람 좋아 보이는 노파가 있었는데, 부종을 앓는지, 천식을 앓는지, 어쩌면 둘 다였습니다.\n\n\"네킷네 아이들이오?\" 노파가 내 물음에 대답했습니다. \"네, 그럼요, 아가씨. 괜찮으시다면 삼 층이오. 계단 바로 맞은편 문이지요.\" 그러고는 계산대 너머로 나에게 열쇠를 건넸습니다.\n\n나는 열쇠를 한 번 보고 노파를 한 번 보았지만, 노파는 내가 그걸 어떻게 쓰는지 안다고 당연하게 여겼습니다. 그것이 아이들 방문에 쓰라는 것일 수밖에 없었으므로, 나는 더 묻지 않고 나와 어두운 계단으로 앞장서 올라갔습니다. 우리는 되도록 조용히 올라갔지만, 넷이나 되다 보니 낡은 마룻널에서 소리가 좀 났고, 이 층에 이르렀을 때는 우리가 자기 방에서 밖을 내다보며 서 있던 한 남자를 방해한 것을 알았습니다.\n\n\"찾는 게 그리들리요?\" 그가 나에게 성난 눈길을 박으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생님.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"저는 더 위로 올라갑니다.\"\n\n그는 에이다를, 잔다이스 씨를, 스킴폴 씨를 차례로 쳐다보았는데, 그들이 나를 지나 뒤따라 올라갈 때마다 똑같은 성난 눈길을 하나하나에게 박았습니다. 잔다이스 씨가 그에게 인사를 건넸습니다. \"안녕하시오!\" 그가 퉁명스럽고 사납게 말했습니다. 그는 키가 크고 누르스름한 남자로, 근심에 찌든 머리에는 머리카락이 얼마 남지 않았고, 얼굴에는 깊은 주름이 잡혀 있었으며, 눈이 툭 불거져 있었습니다. 호전적인 표정과 부글부글 끓어오르는 신경질적인 태도가, 비록 한물간 기색이 역력하지만 여전히 크고 우람한 그 몸집과 어우러져 나를 적잖이 겁먹게 했습니다. 그는 손에 펜을 쥐고 있었고, 지나치며 흘끗 본 그의 방은 어지러이 흩어진 종이 더미로 뒤덮여 있었습니다.\n\n우리는 그를 거기 세워 둔 채 맨 위층 방으로 올라갔습니다. 내가 문을 두드리자, 안에서 작고 새된 목소리가 말했습니다. \"우린 갇혀 있어요. 블라인더 부인이 열쇠를 가지고 있어요!\"\n\n나는 이 말을 듣고 열쇠를 끼워 문을 열었습니다. 천장이 비스듬하게 기울고 가구라곤 거의 없는 초라한 방 안에, 다섯이나 여섯 살쯤 된 자그마한 사내아이 하나가 열여덟 달쯤 된 묵직한 아기를 안고 어르며 달래고 있었습니다. 날씨가 추운데도 불은 피워져 있지 않았습니다. 두 아이는 그 대신 허름한 숄과 목도리를 둘둘 두르고 있었습니다. 그래도 옷이 그리 따뜻하지는 않은지, 사내아이가 아기를 어깨에 기대게 한 채 어르고 달래며 이리저리 걷는 동안, 두 아이의 코는 빨갛게 얼어붙어 보였고 작은 몸은 잔뜩 움츠러들어 있었습니다.\n\n\"누가 너희끼리만 여기 가두어 놓았니?\" 우리는 당연히 물었습니다.\n\n\"찰리요.\" 사내아이가 멈춰 서서 우리를 빤히 쳐다보며 말했습니다.\n\n\"찰리가 네 형이니?\"\n\n\"아니요. 누나예요, 샬럿이요. 아버지가 찰리라고 불렀어요.\"\n\n\"찰리 말고 너희가 또 있니?\"\n\n\"저요.\" 사내아이가 말했습니다. \"그리고 엠마요.\" 안고 있던 아기의 축 늘어진 보닛을 토닥이며 말했습니다. \"그리고 찰리요.\"\n\n\"찰리는 지금 어디 있니?\"\n\n\"빨래하러 나갔어요.\" 사내아이가 다시 이리저리 걷기 시작하며 말했습니다. 동시에 우리를 빤히 보려다 보니 누런 무명 보닛을 침대 틀에 너무 가까이 들이밀었습니다.\n\n우리가 서로를 보고 또 이 두 아이를 보고 있을 때, 아주 어린 여자아이 하나가 방으로 들어왔습니다. 몸집은 어린아이였지만 얼굴은 영악하고 나이 들어 보였습니다. 게다가 얼굴은 예뻤습니다. 자기에게는 너무 큰 어른 여자용 보닛을 쓰고, 어른 여자용 앞치마에다 맨팔을 닦으며 들어왔습니다. 손가락은 빨래로 하얗게 쪼글쪼글했고, 팔에서 닦아 낸 비눗물 거품은 아직도 김이 나고 있었습니다. 그것만 아니었다면, 진실을 빠르게 관찰하여 가난한 일하는 여자를 흉내 내며 빨래 놀이를 하는 어린아이로 보였을 것입니다.\n\n여자아이는 동네 어딘가에서 뛰어와 있는 힘껏 서두른 참이었습니다. 그래서 몸이 무척 가벼운데도 숨이 차서, 처음에는 말도 하지 못한 채 헐떡이며 서서 팔을 닦으며 조용히 우리를 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"아, 찰리가 왔다!\" 사내아이가 말했습니다.\n\n사내아이가 안고 있던 아기가 두 팔을 내밀며 찰리에게 안기겠다고 울어 댔습니다. 여자아이는 앞치마와 보닛에 어울리는 어른 여자 같은 몸짓으로 아기를 받아 안고, 더없이 다정하게 자기에게 매달리는 짐을 안은 채 우리를 바라보며 서 있었습니다.\n\n\"이게 가능한 일이오,\" 우리가 그 작은 아이에게 의자를 갖다 주고 그 짐을 안은 채 앉게 했을 때, 사내아이가 누나 곁에 바짝 붙어 앞치마를 붙들고 있는 가운데, 내 후견인이 속삭였습니다. \"이 아이가 나머지를 위해 일을 한다는 게? 이것 좀 보시오! 제발, 이것 좀 보란 말이오!\"\n\n그것은 정말 볼 만한 광경이었습니다. 세 아이가 바짝 붙어 있고, 그중 둘은 오로지 셋째에게만 의지하며, 그 셋째는 그토록 어리면서도 어린 몸에 너무도 낯설게 어울리는, 어른스럽고 의젓한 분위기를 풍기고 있었습니다.\n\n\"찰리, 찰리야!\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다. \"너 몇 살이니?\"\n\n\"열세 살 넘었어요, 선생님.\" 아이가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"오! 정말 대단한 나이로구나.\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다. \"정말 대단한 나이야, 찰리!\"\n\n그가 아이에게 말하던 그 다정함을, 절반은 장난스러우면서도 그래서 더욱 가엾고 애틋했던 그 어조를 나는 이루 다 그려 낼 수가 없습니다.\n\n\"그래서 너는 이 아기들과 여기서 혼자 사는 거니, 찰리?\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 아이가 더없이 굳은 믿음으로 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 대답했습니다. \"아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 살아가니, 찰리? 오! 찰리야,\" 내 후견인이 잠깐 얼굴을 돌리며 말했습니다. \"어떻게 살아가는 거니?\"\n\n\"아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 일을 나가요, 선생님. 오늘도 빨래하러 나갔어요.\"\n\n\"하느님이 너를 도우시기를, 찰리!\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다. \"넌 키가 작아서 빨래통에도 손이 안 닿을 텐데!\"\n\n\"나막신을 신으면 닿아요, 선생님.\" 아이가 얼른 말했습니다. \"어머니가 신던 굽 높은 나막신이 있거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어머니는 언제 돌아가셨니? 가엾은 어머니!\"\n\n\"어머니는 엠마가 태어나고 얼마 안 돼서 돌아가셨어요.\" 아이가 제 가슴에 안긴 얼굴을 흘끗 보며 말했습니다. \"그러고 나서 아버지가, 내가 할 수 있는 만큼 좋은 엄마 노릇을 해 주라고 하셨어요. 그래서 그러려고 했어요. 그래서 한참 동안 집에서 일하면서 청소하고 아기 보고 빨래하다가, 그러고 나서 밖에 일 나가기 시작했어요. 그래서 할 줄 알아요. 그렇잖아요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"그럼 너는 자주 나가니?\"\n\n\"할 수 있는 만큼 자주요.\" 찰리가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 웃으며 말했습니다. \"육 펜스도 벌고 일 실링도 버니까요!\"\n\n\"그럼 나갈 때는 늘 아기들을 가둬 두니?\"\n\n\"안전하게 지키려고요, 그렇잖아요, 선생님?\" 찰리가 말했습니다. \"블라인더 부인이 가끔 올라와 보시고, 그리들리 아저씨도 가끔 올라오시고, 어쩌면 저도 가끔 잠깐 들를 수 있고, 또 얘들끼리 놀 수도 있고요. 그리고 톰은 갇혀 있는 걸 무서워 안 해요, 그렇지, 톰?\"\n\n\"으응!\" 톰이 씩씩하게 말했습니다.\n\n\"어두워지면 안마당 아래쪽에 등불이 켜지는데, 그게 여기까지 아주 환하게 비춰요. 거의 아주 환하게요. 그렇지, 톰?\"\n\n\"응, 찰리.\" 톰이 말했습니다. \"거의 아주 환해.\"\n\n\"그러면 얘는 정말 착하게 굴어요.\" 그 작은 아이가 말했습니다. 오, 어쩜 그토록 엄마같이, 어른 여자같이 말하던지요! \"그리고 엠마가 졸리면 얘가 재워 줘요. 그리고 자기가 졸리면 스스로 잠자리에 들고요. 그리고 제가 집에 와서 촛불을 켜고 저녁을 좀 먹으면, 얘가 다시 일어나서 저랑 같이 먹어요. 그렇지, 톰?\"\n\n\"응, 찰리!\" 톰이 말했습니다. \"그렇게 해!\" 그러고는 자기 인생의 가장 큰 즐거움을 이렇게 언뜻 떠올린 탓인지, 아니면 자기에게 전부나 다름없는 찰리를 향한 고마움과 사랑 때문인지, 그는 누나 옷자락의 빈약한 주름 사이에 얼굴을 파묻고 웃다가 울음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n우리가 들어온 뒤로 이 아이들 사이에서 눈물이 흘러내린 것은 그때가 처음이었습니다. 어린 고아 소녀는 마치 그 모든 슬픔이 용기를 내야 하는 절박함에, 일을 할 수 있다는 어린아이다운 자부심에, 그리고 부산스레 분주한 제 몸가짐에 가라앉기라도 한 듯 아버지와 어머니 이야기를 했습니다. 그러나 이제 톰이 울자, 비록 그녀는 더없이 차분히 앉아 조용히 우리를 바라보며 작은 두 아이의 머리카락 한 올도 흐트러뜨리지 않았지만, 나는 소리 없는 두 줄기 눈물이 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n나는 에이다와 함께 창가에 서서 지붕들과 시커멓게 그을린 굴뚝 무더기와 가엾은 화초들과 이웃집의 작은 새장에 든 새들을 보는 척하다가, 아래층 가게에서 블라인더 부인이 들어와(아마 계단을 올라오는 데만 이만큼 시간이 걸렸겠지요) 내 후견인과 이야기하고 있는 것을 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"집세를 면해 주는 건 별것 아니에요, 선생님.\" 부인이 말했습니다. \"누가 저 애들한테 집세를 받겠어요!\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요!\" 내 후견인이 우리 둘에게 말했습니다. \"언젠가 이 착한 부인이, 그것이 별것이었음을 깨닫게 될 때가 오리라는 것만으로 충분하오. 그리고 이 보잘것없는 사람 가운데 하나에게 베푼 것이니 곧... 이 아이가,\" 그가 잠시 후 덧붙였습니다. \"정말로 이 일을 계속할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 선생님, 제 생각엔 할 수 있을 것 같아요.\" 블라인더 부인이 가쁜 숨을 괴롭게 한 모금씩 몰아쉬며 말했습니다. \"저렇게 야무진 애가 없어요. 아이고 선생님, 어머니가 죽은 뒤에 저 애가 두 아이를 보살핀 솜씨는 이 골목의 화젯거리였답니다! 그리고 아버지가 병들고 나서 저 애가 아버지를 돌보는 모습은 정말이지 보기에 놀라웠어요, 정말로요! '블라인더 부인,' 그이가 마지막으로 한 말이 이거였어요. 거기 누운 채로요. '블라인더 부인, 내 직업이 무엇이었든, 어젯밤 이 방에 천사가 내 아이와 함께 앉아 있는 걸 보았소. 나는 저 애를 우리 아버지께 맡깁니다!'\"\n\n\"다른 직업은 없었소?\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"없었어요, 선생님.\" 블라인더 부인이 대답했습니다. \"그이는 그저 추심꾼일 뿐이었지요. 처음 여기 세 들어 왔을 때는 그이가 뭐 하는 사람인지 몰랐는데, 솔직히 말씀드리면 알고 나서는 나가라고 통보했답니다. 골목에서 좋아하지들 않았거든요. 다른 세입자들도 못마땅해했고요. 그건 점잖은 직업이 아니라서요.\" 블라인더 부인이 말했습니다. \"사람들 대부분이 싫어하지요. 그리들리 씨가 아주 강하게 싫어했는데, 그래도 그분은 좋은 세입자예요. 성미가 모질게 단련되긴 했지만요.\"\n\n\"그래서 나가라고 통보했소?\" 내 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래서 나가라고 통보했지요.\" 블라인더 부인이 말했습니다. \"그런데 막상 때가 되고 보니, 그이한테 다른 나쁜 점이라곤 알지 못해서 망설여지더군요. 그이는 시간을 잘 지키고 부지런했어요. 자기가 할 일을 했지요, 선생님.\" 블라인더 부인이 자기도 모르게 스킴폴 씨에게 눈길을 박으며 말했습니다. \"이 세상에서 그것만 해도 어디예요.\"\n\n\"그래서 결국 그 사람을 데리고 있었소?\"\n\n\"그러니까요, 제가 그랬지요. 그이가 그리들리 씨하고 잘 풀 수 있다면, 다른 세입자들하고는 내가 잘 처리할 수 있고, 골목에서 좋아하든 싫어하든 그렇게까지 마음 쓰지는 않겠다고요. 그리들리 씨가 퉁명스럽게 허락하긴 했지만, 어쨌든 허락했어요. 그분은 늘 그이한테 퉁명스러웠지만, 그래도 아이들한테는 그 뒤로 다정하게 대해 주셨어요. 사람은 겪어 보기 전에는 도무지 알 수가 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"아이들한테 다정하게 대해 준 사람이 많았소?\" 잔다이스 씨가 물었습니다.\n\n\"대체로 그리 나쁘진 않았어요, 선생님.\" 블라인더 부인이 말했습니다. \"하지만 아버지 직업이 달랐다면 그랬을 만큼 많지는 분명 않았지요. 코빈스 씨가 일 기니를 주었고, 추심꾼들이 푼돈을 모아 주었어요. 그이가 지나갈 때면 늘 농담을 던지고 어깨를 툭툭 치던 골목 이웃 몇몇이 나서서 자그마한 모금을 해 주었고요. 그래서 대체로 그리 나쁘진 않았어요. 샬럿도 마찬가지예요. 어떤 사람들은 추심꾼의 자식이라고 저 애를 안 쓰고, 또 어떤 사람들은 부려 먹으면서도 그걸 들먹이며 면박을 주고, 어떤 사람들은 그 온갖 흠을 다 안은 저 애를 일 시켜 주는 걸 무슨 선심이라도 베푸는 양 굴면서, 어쩌면 품삯은 덜 주고 일은 더 시키지요. 그래도 저 애는 다른 애들보다 참을성이 많고, 영리하기도 하고, 늘 제 힘이 닿는 데까지, 그 이상으로 기꺼이 한답니다. 그러니 대체로 그리 나쁘진 않았다고 하겠지만, 더 나을 수도 있었겠지요, 선생님.\"\n\n블라인더 부인은 채 가라앉지도 않은 숨을 그토록 많은 말로 다시 소진한 터라, 좀 더 편안히 숨을 고를 기회를 가지려 자리에 앉았습니다. 잔다이스 씨가 우리에게 말을 건네려고 돌아서는데, 앞서 이야기에 나왔고 우리가 올라오는 길에 보았던 바로 그 그리들리 씨가 불쑥 방으로 들어오는 바람에 그의 주의가 그쪽으로 쏠렸습니다.\n\n\"여러분이 여기서 무얼 하시는지는 모르겠소만, 신사 숙녀 여러분,\" 그가 우리의 존재가 못마땅하다는 듯 말했습니다. \"내가 들어온 것을 양해해 주시오. 나는 두리번거리며 구경하러 온 게 아니오. 자, 찰리! 자, 톰! 자, 꼬맹아! 오늘 우리 모두 어떻게 지냈누?\"\n\n그는 어루만지듯 그 무리 위로 몸을 숙였고, 아이들에게는 분명 친구로 여겨지고 있었습니다. 비록 그의 얼굴은 여전히 험상궂은 빛을 띠고 우리를 대하는 태도는 더없이 무례했지만요. 내 후견인은 그것을 알아채고 존중했습니다.\n\n\"여기 두리번거리며 구경하러 올 사람은 분명 아무도 없을 게요.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했습니다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요, 선생, 그럴지도 모르지요.\" 상대가 톰을 무릎에 앉혔다가 짜증스레 떨쳐 내며 대답했습니다. \"나는 신사 숙녀들과 입씨름할 마음은 없소. 입씨름이라면 한 사람 평생 몫으로 충분할 만큼 해 봤으니까.\"\n\n\"능히 그럴 만한 까닭이 있겠지요.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그렇게 부글부글 끓고 신경질이 날 만한—\"\n\n\"또 그 소리!\" 남자가 사납게 화를 내며 외쳤습니다. \"나는 싸움질하는 성미요. 화를 잘 내지. 예의 같은 건 없소!\"\n\n\"그리 예의 바르진 않으신 것 같소.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 그리들리가 아이를 내려놓고는 마치 후려치기라도 할 듯 그에게 다가서며 말했습니다. \"선생은 형평법 법정에 대해 뭐라도 아시오?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 알지요, 가슴 아프게도.\"\n\n\"가슴 아프게도?\" 남자가 분노를 멈추며 말했습니다. \"그렇다면 미안하오. 내가 예의가 없다는 건 나도 아오. 용서하시오! 선생,\" 다시 격렬해지며 말했습니다. \"나는 이십오 년을 불타는 쇠 위로 질질 끌려다녔고, 그래서 벨벳 위를 밟는 버릇은 잊어버렸소. 저쪽 대법원에 들어가서, 그들의 사무에 가끔 활기를 불어넣는 단골 농담이 무엇이냐고 물어보시오. 그러면 자기들이 가진 가장 좋은 농담은 슈롭셔에서 온 사람이라고 말해 줄 거요. 내가,\" 그가 한 손으로 다른 손을 격하게 내려치며 말했습니다. \"바로 그 슈롭셔에서 온 사람이오.\"\n\n\"나와 내 가족도 그 똑같은 엄숙한 곳에서 얼마간 웃음거리를 제공하는 영광을 누렸나 보오.\" 내 후견인이 침착하게 말했습니다. \"내 이름을 들어 보셨을지도 모르겠소. 잔다이스요.\"\n\n\"잔다이스 씨.\" 그리들리가 거친 투로 인사를 건네며 말했습니다. \"당신은 나보다 자기 억울함을 더 조용히 견디시는구려. 그뿐 아니라, 당신에게 말하고, 또 이 신사분과 이 젊은 아가씨들에게도, 당신의 벗이라면 말하리다. 내가 만약 내 억울함을 다른 식으로 받아들였다면 나는 미쳐 버렸을 거요! 그것에 분개하고, 마음속으로 거기에 앙갚음하고, 결코 얻지 못하는 정의를 노여워하며 요구하는 것, 오직 그것만으로 나는 제정신을 붙들어 둘 수 있소. 오직 그것뿐이오!\" 그가 투박한 시골 사람 말투로, 무서운 격렬함을 띠고 말했습니다. \"당신은 내가 자신을 너무 흥분시킨다고 말할지 모르오. 나는 대답하리다. 억울함 앞에서 그렇게 하는 게 내 천성이고, 나는 그렇게 해야만 한다고. 그렇게 하든가, 아니면 저 법정에 출몰하는 가엾은 미친 노파처럼 실실 웃는 상태로 가라앉든가, 둘 중 하나뿐이오. 내가 한 번이라도 그 밑에 주저앉는다면, 나는 백치가 되고 말 거요.\"\n\n그가 휩싸인 격정과 열기, 일그러지던 얼굴, 말에 곁들이던 사나운 몸짓은 차마 보기가 더없이 고통스러웠습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 씨.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"내 사정을 들어 보시오. 우리 위에 하늘이 있다는 것이 진실이듯, 이것이 내 사정이오. 나는 두 형제 가운데 하나요. 우리 아버지는(농부였지요) 유언장을 만들어, 농장과 가축과 그 밖의 것들을 어머니에게 살아 계실 동안 남기셨소. 어머니가 돌아가신 뒤에는 모든 것이 나에게 오되, 다만 내가 그때 형에게 줄 삼백 파운드의 유산만은 빼고요. 어머니가 돌아가셨소. 형은 얼마 뒤에 자기 유산을 요구했소. 나와 친척 몇몇은, 형이 이미 식비와 숙박비, 그 밖의 몇 가지로 그 일부를 받았다고 말했소. 자, 잘 들으시오! 그것이 쟁점이었고, 다른 건 아무것도 없었소. 누구도 유언장을 다투지 않았소. 그 삼백 파운드 가운데 일부가 이미 지불되었느냐 아니냐, 오직 그것만 다투었을 뿐이오. 그 문제를 가리려고, 형이 소장을 제출했고, 나는 이 저주받은 대법원으로 들어갈 수밖에 없었소. 법이 나를 거기로 몰아넣고 다른 어디로도 가지 못하게 했으니까. 그 단순한 소송에 피고로 세워진 사람이 열일곱이었소! 이 년 만에 처음 사건이 열렸소. 그러더니 또 이 년 동안 멈췄소. 주심관이(그자의 머리가 썩어 떨어지기를!) 내가 우리 아버지의 아들이 맞는지 조사하는 동안 말이오. 그건 살아 있는 어떤 사람과도 전혀 다툴 여지가 없는 일인데도요. 그러더니 그자는 피고가 충분하지 않다는 걸 알아냈소. 잘 기억하시오, 아직 열일곱뿐이었는데도! 빠진 사람이 하나 더 있으니 그를 마저 넣어야 하고, 모든 걸 처음부터 다시 시작해야 한다는 거였소. 그때까지 든 비용이, 일을 시작하기도 전에! 그 유산의 세 배였소. 형은 더 드는 비용을 피하려고 유산을 기꺼이 포기하려 했소. 우리 아버지의 그 유언장에 나에게 남겨진 내 전 재산이 비용으로 다 날아갔소. 아직도 판결이 나지 않은 그 소송은, 다른 모든 것과 함께 파멸과 폐허와 절망 속으로 빠져들었소. 그리고 나는, 오늘 이렇게 여기 서 있소! 자, 잔다이스 씨, 당신의 소송에는 수천 수만 파운드가 걸려 있지만, 내 소송에는 수백 파운드가 걸려 있을 뿐이오. 내 전 재산이 거기 걸려 있었고 이렇게 부끄럽게 빨려 나갔는데, 내 것은 견디기가 덜 힘들겠소, 아니면 더 힘들겠소?\"\n\n잔다이스 씨는 진심으로 그를 위로하며, 이 괴물 같은 제도에 부당하게 당하는 것을 자기 혼자 독차지하려는 게 아니라고 말했습니다.\n\n\"또 그 소리!\" 그리들리 씨가 노여움을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않고 말했습니다. \"제도라니! 사방에서들 나한테 그러는구려, 그게 제도라고. 개인들을 탓해선 안 된다고. 그게 제도라고. 나는 법정에 들어가서 이렇게 말해선 안 된다는 거요. '판사님, 이것을 여쭙고자 합니다. 이게 옳습니까, 그릅니까? 제가 정의를 받았으니 그러므로 기각이라고 저한테 뻔뻔스레 말씀하실 수 있습니까?' 판사님은 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르오. 그저 거기 앉아 제도를 집행할 뿐이지. 나는 링컨 법학원 광장의 변호사 토킹혼 씨에게 가서, 그자가 그토록 냉정하고 흡족한 꼴로 나를 미치게 만들 때—다들 그러는데, 내가 손해 보는 동안 그자들은 이득을 본다는 걸 내가 아니까, 그렇지 않소?—그자에게 이렇게 말해서도 안 된다는 거요. '나는 내 파멸의 대가를 누군가에게서 받아 내고야 말겠다, 정당한 수단으로든 부정한 수단으로든!' 그자에게는 책임이 없다는 거지. 그게 제도라니까. 하지만 내가 여기서 그자들 누구에게도 폭력을 휘두르지 않는다면, 어쩌면 나도! 마침내 내가 자제력을 잃고 미쳐 날뛰게 되면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 나도 모르겠소! 나는 나에게 맞선 그 제도를 굴리는 개개의 일꾼들을, 얼굴을 맞대고, 위대하고 영원한 심판대 앞에서 고발하겠소!\"\n\n그의 격정은 무시무시했습니다. 직접 보지 않았다면 나는 그런 분노를 믿지 못했을 것입니다.\n\n\"다 했소!\" 그가 자리에 앉아 얼굴을 닦으며 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 씨, 나는 다 했소! 내가 사납다는 건 나도 아오. 알아야 마땅하지. 나는 법정 모욕죄로 감옥에 갔었소. 변호사를 협박한 죄로 감옥에 갔었소. 이런 곤경 저런 곤경을 겪었고, 또 겪을 거요. 나는 슈롭셔에서 온 사람이오. 가끔은 그자들을 즐겁게 해 주는 정도를 넘어서기도 하지요. 비록 그자들은 내가 구금되고 구금된 채 끌려 나오고 하는 그 모든 꼴을 보는 게 즐겁다고들 여기지만요. 내가 자제하면 나한테 더 나을 거라고들 말합니다. 나는 그자들한테, 내가 만약 자제한다면 백치가 되고 말 거라고 대답하오. 한때 나는 성미가 꽤 좋은 사람이었던 것 같소. 우리 고향 사람들은 그렇게 기억한다고들 하지만, 이제 나는 이 억울함 속에서 이렇게 분풀이를 하지 않으면 제정신을 붙들어 둘 수가 없소. '그러느니 당신한테 훨씬 나을 게요, 그리들리 씨,' 지난주에 대법원장이 나한테 그럽디다. '여기서 시간을 허비하지 말고, 슈롭셔로 내려가 쓸모 있게 일하며 지내는 게.' '판사님, 판사님, 그러는 게 나으리란 걸 압니다,' 내가 그에게 말했소. '그리고 판사님의 그 높은 직함을 애당초 듣지 않았더라면 나한테 훨씬 나았겠지요. 하지만 불행히도 나는 지난 일을 되돌릴 수가 없고, 그 지난 일이 나를 여기로 몰아대는 겁니다!' 게다가,\" 그가 사납게 터뜨리며 덧붙였습니다. \"나는 그자들에게 망신을 줄 거요. 끝까지, 나는 그 법정에 나타나 그 수치를 보여 주겠소. 내가 언제 죽을지 알 수 있다면, 그리고 거기로 실려 갈 수 있고, 말할 목소리가 남아 있다면, 나는 거기서 죽으며 이렇게 말하겠소. '너희가 나를 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 여기로 끌고 왔다가 여기서 내보냈다. 이제는 발부터 먼저 내보내거라!'\"\n\n그의 얼굴은, 아마도 여러 해에 걸쳐, 그 싸우려 드는 표정으로 너무도 굳어 버린 탓에, 이렇게 잠잠해진 지금조차 부드러워지지 않았습니다.\n\n\"이 아기들을 한 시간쯤 내 방으로 데려가려고 왔소.\" 그가 다시 아이들에게 다가가며 말했습니다. \"마음껏 놀게 하려고요. 이런 말을 다 할 생각은 아니었소만, 뭐 그리 대수롭잖소. 너는 내가 무섭지 않지, 톰?\"\n\n\"안 무서워요!\" 톰이 말했습니다. \"아저씨는 나한테 화 안 내잖아요.\"\n\n\"옳거니, 우리 꼬맹이. 찰리 너는 돌아가는 거지? 응? 그럼 가자, 꼬맹아!\" 그는 가장 어린 아이를 팔에 안았는데, 아이도 안기는 것을 마다하지 않았습니다. \"아래층에 생강빵 병정이 있을지도 모르겠다. 가서 찾아보자꾸나!\"\n\n그는 아까처럼 거친 인사를, 그래도 어떤 존중이 빠지지 않은 그 인사를 잔다이스 씨에게 건네고, 우리에게는 가볍게 고개 숙여 인사하고는, 아래층 자기 방으로 내려갔습니다.\n\n그러자 스킴폴 씨가, 우리가 도착한 뒤로 처음으로, 여느 때의 그 명랑한 가락으로 이야기하기 시작했습니다. 그는 말했습니다. 자, 세상일이 어찌 그리 게으르게 제 목적에 들어맞아 가는지 보는 것이 참으로 즐겁다고요. 여기 이 그리들리 씨로 말하자면, 의지가 굳세고 정력이 놀라운 사람, 지적으로 말하자면 일종의 조화롭지 못한 대장장이인데, 그가 몇 해 전 인생을 떠돌며 남아도는 호전성을 쏟아부을 무언가를 찾아 헤매던 모습, 가시덤불 속의 어린 큐피드 같은 그 모습이 눈에 선하다는 것이었습니다. 그러다가 대법원이 그의 길에 나타나, 그가 원하던 바로 그것을 제공해 주었다는 것이지요. 그 후로 영영, 둘은 천생연분이었다! 그게 아니었다면 그는 온갖 도시를 폭파하는 위대한 장군이 되었거나, 아니면 온갖 의회식 수사를 늘어놓는 위대한 정치가가 되었을지도 모른다고요. 하지만 사정이 이러하여, 그와 대법원이 더없이 흐뭇한 방식으로 서로에게 맞닥뜨렸으니, 그리 손해 본 사람도 없고, 말하자면 그리들리는 그 시각부터 앞날이 마련된 셈이라는 것이었습니다. 그리고 코빈시스를 보라고요! 가엾은 코빈시스(이 사랑스러운 아이들의 아버지)가 그 똑같은 이치를 얼마나 멋지게 보여 주었는가! 자기, 곧 스킴폴 씨 본인도, 코빈시스의 존재를 가끔 원망했었다고요. 코빈시스가 자기 앞길을 가로막는다고 여겼다고요. 코빈시스 없이도 살 수 있었다고요. 만약 자기가 술탄이고, 어느 날 아침 대재상이 \"충성스러운 신도들의 지휘관께서는 노예의 손에서 무엇을 원하시나이까?\" 하고 물었다면, \"코빈시스의 목을!\" 하고 대답하기까지 했을 만한 때도 있었다고요. 하지만 알고 보니 어찌 된 일이었던가? 그 모든 세월 동안 자기가 더없이 마땅한 한 사람에게 일자리를 주고 있었다는 것, 자기가 코빈시스의 은인이었다는 것, 자기가 실은 코빈시스로 하여금 이 사랑스러운 아이들을 이렇게 흐뭇한 방식으로 길러 내고 이런 사회적 미덕을 키워 내게 해 주고 있었다는 것이었습니다! 그리하여 방금 방 안을 둘러보며, \"내가 코빈시스의 위대한 후원자였고, 그가 누린 자그마한 안락이 내 작품이었구나!\" 하고 생각하니, 가슴이 벅차오르고 눈에 눈물이 고였다는 것이었습니다.\n\n이 환상적인 현(絃)을 가볍게 튕기는 그의 태도에는 너무도 사람을 사로잡는 데가 있었고, 우리가 본 더 의젓한 어린아이 곁에서 그는 어찌나 명랑한 어린아이였던지, 블라인더 부인과 잠시 사사로운 이야기를 나누다 우리에게로 돌아서던 내 후견인마저 그 때문에 미소 짓게 만들었습니다. 우리는 찰리에게 입을 맞추고, 그 애를 데리고 함께 아래층으로 내려가, 그 애가 일하러 뛰어가는 모습을 보려고 집 밖에 멈춰 섰습니다. 그 애가 어디로 가는지는 모르겠지만, 우리는 그 애가 뛰어가는 것을 보았습니다. 어른 여자용 보닛과 앞치마를 두른 그토록 작고 작은 아이가, 안마당 끄트머리의 지붕 덮인 통로를 지나, 도시의 다툼과 소음 속으로, 마치 대양에 떨어진 이슬 한 방울처럼 녹아드는 것을요.\n\n데드록 마님은 안절부절못한다, 도무지 가만있질 못한다. 깜짝 놀란 사교계 소식통은 마님을 어디서 붙잡아야 할지조차 가늠하지 못한다. 오늘은 체스니 월드에 있고, 어제는 시내 저택에 있었으며, 내일은 외국에 가 있을지도 모를 일이라, 사교계 소식통이 무엇 하나 자신 있게 점치지 못한다. 레스터 경의 헌신마저 마님을 뒤따르기가 적잖이 버겁다. 더 버거울 뻔했으나, 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나 한결같은 그의 또 다른 충직한 동맹—통풍—이 체스니 월드의 늙은 떡갈나무 침실로 쏜살같이 달려들어 두 다리를 움켜쥐는 덕에 그나마 덜하다.\n\n레스터 경은 통풍을 성가신 악마로 받아들이되, 그래도 명문가의 반열에 든 악마로 여긴다. 모든 데드록 가문 사람은, 직계 남계로, 사람의 기억이 거슬러 닿지 않는 아득한 세월 동안 그리고 그 너머로, 하나같이 통풍을 앓았다. 증명할 수 있는 일이다, 경. 남의 집 아버지들은 류머티즘으로 죽었을지도 모르고, 병든 천것들의 오염된 피에서 천박한 전염을 옮았을지도 모르지만, 데드록 가문은 제 집안 통풍으로 죽음으로써 죽음이라는 만인 평등의 과정에마저 무언가 독점적인 것을 전했다. 그것은 은식기나, 그림이나, 링컨셔의 영지처럼 빛나는 가문의 혈통을 타고 내려왔다. 그것은 가문의 위엄 가운데 하나다. 레스터 경은, 비록 말로 정리해본 적은 한 번도 없으나, 죽음의 천사가 그 마땅한 직무를 수행하면서 귀족의 망령들에게 이렇게 고하리라는 인상을 어쩌면 전혀 품지 않은 것은 아니다. \"경들과 신사 여러분, 가문 통풍을 타고 도착했음이 확인된 또 한 분의 데드록을 여러분께 소개하는 영광을 누립니다.\"\n\n그리하여 레스터 경은 마치 그 봉건적 보유 조건으로 제 이름과 재산을 지니기라도 한 듯, 가문의 다리를 가문의 질환에 바친다. 그는 데드록 사람이 등을 대고 누워 사지를 발작하듯 씰룩거리고 쑤시며 찔리는 것이 어딘가에서 자행된 무례라고 느끼지만, 이렇게 생각한다. \"우리는 모두 이것에 굴복해왔다. 이것은 우리에게 속한 것이다. 우리가 정원 묘소를 더 비천한 조건으로 흥미롭게 만들지 않으리라는 것은 수백 년 동안 이해되어온 바다. 그러니 나는 그 타협에 순순히 따른다.\"\n\n그가 펼치는 광경은 자못 볼만하다. 진홍과 황금이 불그레하게 번지는 가운데, 큰 응접실 한복판 자신이 가장 아끼는 마님의 초상화 앞에 누워 있고, 긴 창들의 긴 행렬을 지나, 길게 트인 시야를 따라 햇살이 넓은 띠를 이루며 비쳐 들고, 그 햇살이 부드럽게 도드라진 그늘과 번갈아 어우러진다. 바깥에서는, 보습이라곤 한 번도 닿은 적 없는 푸른 땅에 오랜 세월 뿌리를 박은 위풍당당한 떡갈나무들이—왕들이 칼과 방패를 들고 전장으로 말을 달리고 활과 화살을 들고 사냥을 나서던 시절에도 여전히 사냥터였던 그 땅에서—그의 위대함을 증언한다. 안에서는, 벽에서 그를 내려다보는 선조들이 이렇게 말한다. \"우리 하나하나도 여기서 한순간의 실체였고, 자신의 이 채색된 그림자를 남기고는, 지금 그대를 잠들게 하는 까마귀들의 먼 울음소리처럼 아련한 추억 속으로 녹아 사라졌다네.\" 그러며 그들 또한 그의 위대함을 증언한다. 그리고 그는 오늘 자못 위대하다. 그러니 감히 한 치라도 그와 다투려 드는 보이손이나 그 밖의 무모한 자에게 화 있을진저!\n\n마님은 지금 레스터 경 곁에서 초상화로 대신 자리한다. 마님은 그곳에 머물 뜻 없이 시내로 훌쩍 떠나버렸고, 머잖아 다시 이리로 훌쩍 돌아와 사교계 소식통을 혼란에 빠뜨릴 것이다. 시내 저택은 마님을 맞을 채비가 되어 있지 않다. 휘장에 덮여 음울하다. 분을 바른 머큐리 하나만이 현관 창가에서 처량하게 입을 헤벌리고 있다. 어젯밤 그는 자기와 안면 있는, 역시 상류 사교계에 익숙한 또 다른 머큐리에게 이렇게 토로했다. 이런 식의 생활이 계속될 거라면—그럴 리야 없겠지만, 자기 같은 기개를 지닌 사내가 견딜 수도 없고, 자기 같은 풍채를 지닌 사내가 견디리라 기대할 수도 없으니—명예를 걸고 말하건대 자기로서는 목을 그어버리는 것 말고는 다른 방도가 없을 거라고!\n\n링컨셔의 영지와, 시내 저택과, 분 바른 머큐리와, 빗자루를 든 무법자 조—교회 묘지 계단을 쓸 때 저 멀리서 한 줄기 빛이 그에게 닿았던 그 아이—의 행방 사이에 무슨 연관이 있을 수 있단 말인가? 이 세상의 헤아릴 수 없는 사연 속에서, 거대한 심연의 양쪽 기슭에서 갈라져 살던 숱한 사람들이, 그럼에도 참으로 기묘하게 한자리에 엮인 일은 또 얼마나 많았던가!\n\n조는 무슨 연결 고리가 있다 한들 그 고리를 알지 못한 채, 온종일 자기 길목을 쓴다. 무언가 물으면 그는 \"암것도 몰러유\"라고 답하는 것으로 제 정신 상태를 요약한다. 그는 궂은 날씨에 길목에서 진흙을 걷어내기가 힘들고, 그 일로 먹고살기는 더 힘들다는 것을 안다. 그것조차 누가 가르쳐주지 않았다. 스스로 깨쳤다.\n\n조는 톰 혼자 사는 곳이라는 이름으로 그 비슷한 처지의 사람들에게 알려진, 허물어져가는 곳에서 산다—다시 말해, 조는 아직 죽지 않았다. 그곳은 시커멓게 다 쓰러져가는 골목으로, 점잖은 사람이라면 누구나 피해 다니는데, 그 미친 듯이 낡은 집들은 한참 썩어 들어갈 무렵 어느 대담한 부랑자들에게 점거당했고, 그들은 제 소유를 확립한 뒤 그것을 셋방으로 내놓기 시작했다. 이제 이 무너져가는 셋집들은 밤이면 떼 지어 모여드는 비참함을 품는다. 망가진 인간 군상에 기생충이 들끓듯, 이 망가진 거처들도 추잡한 무리를 길러내, 그것이 벽과 판자의 틈으로 기어들고 기어 나오고, 빗물이 새어 드는 곳에 구더기처럼 떼 지어 똬리를 틀고 잠들며, 발길마다 열병을 짊어지고 나르며, 쿠들 경과 토머스 두들 경과 푸들 공작과, 그 아래 주들에 이르기까지 관직에 앉은 그 모든 잘난 신사들이—바로 그러라고 작정하고 태어났음에도—오백 년 걸려도 바로잡지 못할 만큼 많은 악을 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 뿌리며 드나든다.\n\n최근 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서는 마치 지뢰가 터지듯 우지끈 무너지는 소리와 함께 먼지구름이 두 차례나 일었다. 그때마다 집 한 채가 쓰러졌다. 이 사고들은 신문에 한 문단씩 실렸고, 가장 가까운 병원의 병상 한둘을 채웠다. 무너진 자리는 그대로 남았고, 잔해 틈에도 인기 없지 않은 셋방이 들어선다. 여러 채가 더 곧 무너질 듯하니, 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 다음번 무너짐은 제법 볼만하리라 예상해도 좋겠다.\n\n이 탐나는 자산은 물론 대법원에 걸려 있다. 눈치가 반쯤이라도 있는 사람에게 그걸 굳이 일러주는 것은 그 분별력에 대한 모욕이리라. \"톰\"이 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송의 본래 원고나 피고를 두루 일컫는 통칭인지, 아니면 그 소송이 골목을 폐허로 만든 뒤 다른 거주자들이 와서 합류하기 전까지 톰이 여기서 홀로 살았는지, 아니면 그 전해 내려오는 명칭이 정직한 무리에게서 끊겨 나오고 희망의 울타리 밖으로 내쳐진 은신처를 두루 가리키는 이름인지는 어쩌면 아무도 모른다. 조가 모르는 것만은 분명하다.\n\n\"왜냐믄 지는,\" 조가 말한다. \"지는 암것도 몰러유.\"\n\n조처럼 산다는 건 참으로 기이한 상태이리라! 가게마다, 길모퉁이마다, 문짝마다, 창문마다 그토록 넘쳐나는 그 신비로운 기호들의 모양에도 낯설고 그 뜻에도 캄캄한 채 거리를 어슬렁어슬렁 헤맨다는 건! 사람들이 글 읽는 걸 보고, 사람들이 글 쓰는 걸 보고, 우체부가 편지를 배달하는 걸 보면서도, 그 모든 언어를 털끝만큼도 알지 못한다는 건—그 한 조각 한 조각에 돌처럼 눈멀고 벙어리가 된다는 건! 일요일이면 점잖은 사람들이 책을 손에 들고 교회로 가는 걸 보면서, (어쩌면 조도 가끔은 정말 생각하니까) 저게 다 무슨 뜻일까, 누군가에게 무슨 뜻이 있다면 어째서 나에게는 아무 뜻도 없을까 하고 생각한다는 건 참으로 어리둥절하리라. 떠밀리고, 부대끼고, 비키라는 소리를 듣고, 내가 여기도, 저기도, 그 어디에도 있을 자격이 없다는 게 더없이 진실인 듯 정말로 느끼면서도, 그래도 나 또한 어떻게든 여기 있고, 내가 지금 이 꼴이 되기까지 모두가 나를 못 본 척했다는 생각에 어쩔 줄 모른다는 건! 내가 (증인으로 나서겠다 했을 때처럼) 사람이라 하기 어렵다는 말을 듣는 것뿐만 아니라, 평생토록 그것을 스스로 절감하며 산다는 건 참으로 기이한 상태이리라! 말이며 개며 소가 내 곁을 지나가는 걸 보면서, 영문도 모른 채 내가 그것들에게 속할 뿐 나와 같은 모양을 한 우월한 존재들에게는 속하지 않으며, 그들의 고상함을 내가 거스른다는 것을 안다는 건! 형사 재판이나, 판사나, 주교나, 정부나, 혹은 (알기만 한다면) 그에게 더없이 값진 보석인 헌법에 대한 조의 관념이란 분명 기이하리라! 그의 물질적이고 비물질적인 삶 전체가 놀랍도록 기이하다. 그의 죽음은, 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 기이하다.\n\n조는 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 나와, 그곳까지 내려오는 데 늘 굼뜬 아침을 맞으며, 걸어오는 길에 더러운 빵 조각을 우물거린다. 그가 가는 길은 숱한 거리를 지나고, 가게들이 아직 문을 열지 않았기에, 그는 해외선교복음전파협회 문간 계단에 앉아 아침을 먹고, 다 먹은 뒤에는 그 자리를 빌려준 데 대한 답례로 빗자루질을 한 번 해준다. 그는 그 건물의 규모에 감탄하며, 대체 무얼 하는 곳일까 궁금해한다. 가엾은 녀석은 태평양 산호초의 영적 궁핍이 무엇인지, 야자나무와 빵나무 열매 사이에서 그 귀한 영혼들을 찾아내는 데 얼마나 큰 비용이 드는지 도무지 알 길이 없다.\n\n그는 제 길목으로 가 하루치 일을 벌일 채비를 한다. 도시가 깨어난다. 거대한 팽이가 날마다의 회전과 소용돌이를 위해 세워진다. 몇 시간 멈춰 있던 그 영문 모를 읽기와 쓰기가 다시 시작된다. 조와 그 밖의 미천한 짐승들은 그 알아먹을 수 없는 난장판 속에서 그저 제 형편껏 살아간다. 장날이다. 눈먼 황소들이, 너무 들볶이고, 너무 내몰리고, 한 번도 제대로 이끌린 적 없이, 엉뚱한 데로 뛰어들었다가 매를 맞고 쫓겨나, 핏발 선 눈으로 거품을 물고 돌담에 들이박고, 종종 죄 없는 이를 호되게 다치게 하며, 종종 제 몸도 호되게 다친다. 조와 그 족속과 꼭 닮았다. 어쩜 그리도, 그리도 닮았는지!\n\n악대가 와서 연주한다. 조가 그것을 듣는다. 개도 한 마리 듣는다—푸줏간 앞에서 주인을 기다리는 가축몰이꾼의 개로, 몇 시간 동안 마음에 짊어졌다가 다행히 벗어버린 그 양들을 분명 곱씹는 중이다. 서너 마리 때문에 어리둥절한 듯, 어디다 두고 왔는지 떠올리지 못하고, 길을 위아래로 살피며 혹시 그것들이 어슬렁대지 않나 반쯤 기대하다가, 문득 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 모든 걸 기억해낸다. 천것들 무리와 술집에 익숙한, 영락없는 떠돌이 개로, 양에게는 무시무시한 개이며, 휘파람 한 번이면 양 등을 타고 내달려 털을 한 움큼씩 물어뜯을 태세이지만, 제 임무를 배우고 그 임무를 어떻게 다하는지 아는, 교육받고 향상되고 발달한 개다. 그와 조는 음악을 듣는데, 아마 거의 같은 정도의 짐승다운 만족을 누릴 것이다. 마찬가지로, 일깨워지는 연상이나, 동경이나, 후회나, 감각 너머의 것들에 대한 우울하거나 즐거운 떠올림에서도, 둘은 아마 막상막하일 것이다. 그러나 그 밖의 점에서는, 이 짐승이 인간 청중보다 얼마나 까마득히 위에 있는가!\n\n그 개의 후손들을 조처럼 야생으로 풀어놓아 보라. 그러면 몇 해 지나지 않아 어찌나 퇴화하는지 짖는 소리조차 잃어버릴 것이다—허나 무는 버릇만은 잃지 않으리라.\n\n날은 저물어가며 변하여 어두워지고 부슬비가 내린다. 조는 진흙과 바퀴들, 말들, 채찍들, 우산들 틈에서 제 길목을 지키며 싸우지만, 톰 혼자 사는 곳의 그 께름칙한 잠자리 값으로 치를 푼돈밖에 벌지 못한다. 땅거미가 깔린다. 가게마다 가스등이 켜지기 시작한다. 사다리를 든 점등부가 보도 가장자리를 따라 달려간다. 비참한 저녁이 내려앉기 시작한다.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 자기 사무실에 앉아, 내일 아침 가장 가까운 치안판사에게 영장을 신청할 일을 곰곰 생각한다. 소송에 좌절한 그리들리가 오늘 여기 다녀가며 위협을 가한 것이다. 우리가 신변의 위협을 받을 수는 없는 노릇이니, 그 못된 작자를 다시 보석에 묶어두어야 한다. 천장에서는, 거꾸로 매달린 어느 있을 수 없는 로마인의 모습을 한, 단축법으로 그려진 우의화가 삼손의 팔로(관절이 어긋나고, 짝도 맞지 않는 팔로) 창문 쪽을 노골적으로 가리킨다. 토킹혼 씨가 무슨 까닭에, 그런 아무 까닭도 없이 창밖을 내다보겠는가? 저 손은 늘 그쪽을 가리키고 있지 않은가? 그러니 그는 창밖을 내다보지 않는다.\n\n그가 내다본들, 여자 하나가 지나가는 걸 본다 한들 그게 무어란 말인가? 세상에 여자는 넘치도록 많다고 토킹혼 씨는 생각한다—너무 많다. 세상에 잘못되는 모든 일의 밑바닥에는 여자가 있다, 하기야 그 덕에 변호사에게 일거리가 생기기는 하지만. 여자 하나가 지나가는 걸 본다 한들, 설령 그 여자가 몰래 가는 길이라 한들 그게 무어란 말인가? 여자들은 죄다 비밀스럽다. 토킹혼 씨는 그것을 아주 잘 안다.\n\n그러나 여자들이 모두, 지금 그와 그의 집을 뒤로하고 떠나는 저 여자 같지는 않다. 저 여자는 수수한 옷차림과 세련된 몸가짐 사이에 어딘가 몹시 어긋나는 데가 있다. 차림새로 보면 상급 하녀쯤 되어야 마땅한데, 그 분위기와 걸음걸이로 보면—비록 둘 다 서두르며 꾸민 것이고, 익숙지 않은 발로 디디는 진창길에서 꾸밀 수 있는 한껏 꾸민 것이긴 해도—분명 귀부인이다. 얼굴은 베일로 가렸으나, 그래도 충분히 제 본색을 드러내어, 곁을 지나는 이들 가운데 한둘 이상이 날카롭게 돌아보게 한다.\n\n여자는 결코 고개를 돌리지 않는다. 귀부인이든 하녀든, 그녀는 마음에 목적을 품었고 그것을 좇을 줄 안다. 조가 빗자루를 부리는 길목에 이를 때까지 그녀는 결코 고개를 돌리지 않는다. 조가 그녀와 함께 건너며 동냥을 한다. 그래도 그녀는 건너편에 다다를 때까지 고개를 돌리지 않는다. 그러고는 그에게 살짝 손짓하며 말한다. \"이리 와봐!\"\n\n조는 그녀를 따라 한두 걸음, 한적한 안뜰로 들어선다.\n\n\"네가 신문에서 읽은 그 아이니?\" 그녀가 베일 너머에서 물었다.\n\n\"몰러유,\" 조가 시무룩하게 베일을 쳐다보며 말한다. \"신문이고 뭐고 암것도. 지는 암것도, 워떤 것도 통 몰러유.\"\n\n\"검시 심문에서 조사받았니?\"\n\n\"지는 암것도 몰러유—교구 관리한테 끌려갔던 거 말씀이세유?\" 조가 말한다. \"그 검신가 뭔가 하던 데 나온 아이 이름이 조였남유?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그게 저예유!\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"좀 더 위로 와봐.\"\n\n\"그 사람 얘기세유?\" 조가 따라오며 말한다. \"죽은 그 사람유?\"\n\n\"쉿! 속삭여서 말해! 그래. 그 사람은, 살아 있을 때, 그렇게나 아프고 가난해 보였니?\"\n\n\"아유, 똑같았지유!\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"혹시—너랑은 안 닮아 보였니?\" 그 여자가 진저리치며 말한다.\n\n\"아유, 저만큼 형편없진 않았지유,\" 조가 말한다. \"지야 영락없는 거지구만유! 그 사람을 아셨던 건 아니지유, 그쵸?\"\n\n\"감히 네가 그 사람을 내가 알았느냐 묻다니?\"\n\n\"기분 상하셨다면 죄송혀유, 마님,\" 조가 한껏 굽실대며 말한다. 그조차 그녀가 귀부인이 아닐까 의심하기에 이른 것이다.\n\n\"난 귀부인이 아냐. 하녀야.\"\n\n\"참말 좋은 하녀시구먼유!\" 조가 무슨 거슬리는 말을 할 뜻이라곤 조금도 없이, 그저 감탄의 표시로 말한다.\n\n\"잘 듣고 입 다물어. 나한테 말 걸지 말고, 좀 더 떨어져 서! 내가 읽은 그 기사에 나온 곳들을 죄다 보여줄 수 있겠니? 그 사람이 글을 써서 받았던 곳, 그 사람이 죽은 곳, 네가 끌려간 곳, 그리고 그 사람이 묻힌 곳을? 그 사람이 묻힌 곳은 아니?\"\n\n조는 고개를 끄덕여 답하는데, 앞서 다른 곳들이 언급될 때마다도 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"내 앞장서서 그 끔찍한 곳들을 죄다 보여줘. 각 장소 맞은편에 멈춰 서고, 내가 말 걸기 전엔 나한테 말하지 마. 뒤돌아보지 마. 내가 시키는 대로 하면 후하게 값을 쳐주마.\"\n\n조는 말이 이어지는 동안 바짝 귀 기울여 듣는다. 빗자루 자루에 그 말들을 하나하나 짚어가며 헤아리는데, 제법 어려운 모양이다. 잠시 멈춰 그 뜻을 곱씹고, 흡족하다 여기며, 헝클어진 머리를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"알어들었구먼유,\" 조가 말한다. \"근디 장난질은 안 되유, 아시쥬. 내빼기 없기유!\"\n\n\"이 끔찍한 것이 대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거지?\" 하녀가 그에게서 움찔 물러나며 외친다.\n\n\"내빼기 없기라구유, 아시쥬!\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"무슨 소린지 모르겠다. 어서 앞장서! 네가 평생 만져본 적 없는 큰돈을 줄 테니.\"\n\n조는 입을 오므려 휘파람 모양을 짓고, 헝클어진 머리를 한 번 긁고, 빗자루를 옆구리에 끼고는, 맨발로 딱딱한 돌과 진흙과 진창을 날렵하게 밟으며 앞장서 길을 안내한다.\n\n쿡 골목. 조가 멈춘다. 침묵.\n\n\"여기는 누가 살지?\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 글 쓸 거 주고, 지한테는 반 크라운 줬던 양반이 살어유,\" 조가 어깨너머로 돌아보지도 않고 속삭이며 말한다.\n\n\"다음 데로 가.\"\n\n크룩의 집. 조가 다시 멈춘다. 좀 더 긴 침묵.\n\n\"여기는 누가 살지?\"\n\n\"그 사람이 여기 살었어유,\" 조가 앞서처럼 답한다.\n\n한참 침묵하다가 그녀가 묻는다. \"어느 방에서?\"\n\n\"저 위 뒷방이유. 이 모퉁이에서 창문이 보여유. 저기 위! 거기서 지는 그 사람이 뻗어 있는 걸 봤어유. 이건 지가 끌려갔던 그 술집이구유.\"\n\n\"다음 데로 가!\"\n\n다음 장소까지는 좀 더 먼 길이지만, 처음의 의심을 떨쳐버린 조는 자기에게 지워진 절차를 충실히 지키며 돌아보지 않는다. 온갖 종류의 역겨운 냄새가 진동하는 구불구불한 길을 숱하게 지나, 그들은 터널 같은 작은 골목에 이르고, (지금은 켜진) 가스등에 이르고, 쇠문에 이른다.\n\n\"그 사람은 저기 들어갔어유,\" 조가 쇠창살을 붙들고 안을 들여다보며 말한다.\n\n\"어디? 아, 이 무슨 끔찍한 광경이람!\"\n\n\"저기유!\" 조가 가리키며 말한다. \"저짝에유. 저 뼈 무더기들 사이, 저기 부엌 창문 바싹 옆에! 그 사람을 맨 꼭대기 근처에 묻었어유. 집어넣으려고 발로 꾹꾹 밟아 다져야 했어유. 문이 열려 있으면 지가 빗자루로 들춰서 보여드릴 수도 있는디. 그래서 잠가두는 거겄지유, 아마,\" 그가 문을 흔들며 말한다. \"늘 잠겨 있어유. 쥐 좀 봐유!\" 조가 들떠서 소리친다. \"어! 봐유! 저기 가유! 야! 땅속으로!\"\n\n하녀는 한 구석으로 움츠러든다, 죽음의 얼룩이 옷을 더럽히는 그 흉측한 아치 아래 한 구석으로. 그러고는 두 손을 내밀어, 그가 자기에게 역겹기 짝이 없으니 가까이 오지 말라고 격하게 이르며, 한동안 그렇게 머문다. 조는 멀거니 쳐다보며 서 있고, 그녀가 정신을 추스를 때까지도 여전히 쳐다본다.\n\n\"이 끔찍한 곳이 축성된 땅이냐?\"\n\n\"공쩡인지 뭔지 하는 땅은 지가 몰러유,\" 조가 여전히 쳐다보며 말한다.\n\n\"여기가 축복받은 곳이냐?\"\n\n\"뭐가유?\" 조가 더없이 어리둥절해하며 말한다.\n\n\"축복받은 곳이냐고?\"\n\n\"낸들 알 게 뭐유,\" 조가 그 어느 때보다 멀뚱히 쳐다보며 말한다. \"근디 그렇진 않을 성싶은디유. 축복이라구유?\" 조가 마음에 무언가 걸리는 듯 되뇐다. \"그게 그렇다면 별 덕은 못 봤지유. 축복이라구유? 지가 보기엔 외려 저주받은 거 같은디유. 근디 지는 암것도 몰러유!\"\n\n하녀는 그가 하는 말에도, 자기가 한 말에 그러는 것만큼이나 거의 귀를 기울이지 않는다. 그녀가 지갑에서 돈을 꺼내려 장갑을 벗는다. 조는 그녀의 손이 얼마나 희고 작은지, 저렇게 반짝이는 반지를 끼다니 참말 좋은 하녀일 거라고 말없이 눈여겨본다.\n\n그녀는 손이 닿지 않게, 둘의 손이 가까워지자 진저리치며, 동전 한 닢을 그의 손에 떨어뜨린다. \"자,\" 그녀가 덧붙인다. \"그 자리를 다시 보여줘!\"\n\n조는 빗자루 자루를 쇠문 창살 사이로 밀어 넣고, 온 힘을 다해 정성껏 그곳을 가리킨다. 마침내, 제 말이 통했는지 보려고 곁눈질하다가, 그는 자기가 혼자라는 것을 깨닫는다.\n\n그가 맨 먼저 하는 일은 동전을 가스등 불빛에 비춰 보는 것이고, 그것이 노란 빛깔—금이라는 것을 알고는 압도당하는 것이다. 그다음 하는 일은 품질을 확인하려고 동전 가장자리를 한쪽으로 깨물어보는 것이다. 그다음에는 잃어버리지 않으려고 동전을 입에 넣고, 계단과 통로를 아주 정성껏 쓰는 것이다. 일을 마치자, 그는 톰 혼자 사는 곳으로 출발하는데, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 가스등 불빛마다 멈춰 서서 금화를 꺼내, 진짜라는 것을 거듭 확인하려고 또 한 번 한쪽으로 깨물어본다.\n\n분 바른 머큐리는 오늘 밤 말동무가 아쉽지 않으니, 마님이 성대한 만찬에 가고 무도회에도 서너 군데 가기 때문이다. 레스터 경은 통풍 말고는 더 나은 벗도 없이 체스니 월드에서 안절부절못한다. 그는 라운스웰 부인에게, 빗물이 테라스에 어찌나 단조롭게 후두두 떨어지는지 자기 아늑한 옷방 난롯가에서조차 신문을 읽을 수가 없다고 투덜댄다.\n\n\"경께서 차라리 저택 반대편을 써보셨으면 좋았을 텐데, 얘야,\" 라운스웰 부인이 로사에게 말한다. \"경의 옷방이 마님 쪽에 있잖니. 그런데 이 오랜 세월 동안 유령의 길 위로 들리는 그 발소리를 오늘 밤보다 또렷하게 들어본 적이 없구나!\"\n\n리처드는 우리가 런던에 머무는 동안 자주 찾아왔습니다(편지 쓰는 일은 곧 시들해졌지만요). 그리고 그 빠른 재주와 밝은 기운, 너그러운 성품, 명랑함과 신선함으로 언제나 즐거움을 주었습니다. 하지만 알면 알수록 그를 더 좋아하게 되면서도, 끈기 있게 한곳에 집중하는 습관을 그가 전혀 길러오지 못했다는 점이 못내 안타깝게 느껴졌습니다. 성격도 능력도 제각각인 수백 명의 다른 소년에게 똑같은 방식으로 들이댔던 그 교육 제도는, 리처드가 자기 과제들을 늘 그럭저럭 좋은 평가를 받으며, 더러는 두드러진 성적으로 후딱 해치우게 해주긴 했습니다. 그러나 그 방식은 변덕스럽고 번쩍이는 식이어서, 정작 이끌어주고 다듬어주어야 마땅했던 바로 그 자질에 그가 더욱 기대게 만들 뿐이었습니다. 그것들은 좋은 자질이고, 그것 없이는 높은 자리를 제대로 얻을 수 없는 자질이지만, 불과 물처럼 훌륭한 하인이긴 해도 아주 고약한 주인이었습니다. 만약 그 자질들이 리처드의 다스림 아래 있었다면 그의 벗이 되었을 테지만, 리처드가 그 자질들의 다스림 아래 있었기에 그것들은 그의 적이 되고 말았습니다.\n\n내가 이런 생각을 적어두는 건, 내가 그렇게 여겼다고 해서 이것이나 다른 무엇이 실제로 그러했다고 믿어서가 아니라, 다만 내가 정말 그렇게 생각했고, 내가 생각하고 행한 모든 것을 솔직하게 밝히고 싶어서입니다. 이것이 리처드에 관한 내 생각이었습니다. 게다가 나는 후견인이 했던 말이 얼마나 옳았는지, 또 대법원 소송의 불확실함과 지체가 그의 천성에 어떤 기운을 불어넣었는지를 자주 보는 것 같았습니다. 자기가 거대한 도박판의 일부라고 느끼는 노름꾼의 무심한 마음 같은 것을 말입니다.\n* 대법원: 영국 형평법을 다루던 챈서리 법원. 끝없이 늘어지는 소송으로 악명 높았다.\n\n어느 날 오후 후견인이 집에 없을 때 베이엄 배저 부부가 찾아왔는데, 이야기를 나누다 나는 자연스레 리처드의 안부를 물었습니다.\n\n\"아, 카스턴 씨 말이군요.\" 배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"아주 잘 지내고 있고, 장담하건대 우리 모임에 큰 보탬이 되는 분이에요. 스워서 함장은 늘 나를 두고 이렇게 말하곤 했죠. 사관후보생 식당에서 사무장이 내놓는 절인 고기가 앞돛 위 풍향삭처럼 질겨졌을 때, 뱃머리 앞의 육지와 고물 쪽에서 불어오는 산들바람보다도 내가 더 낫다고요. 그건 어떤 모임에든 내가 보탬이 된다는 걸 두루 일컫는 그분의 해군식 표현이었어요. 나는 카스턴 씨에게도 똑같은 찬사를 바칠 수 있다고 확신합니다. 그런데 나는… 내가 이런 말을 꺼내는 게 너무 성급하다고 여기진 않으시겠죠?\"\n\n나는 아니라고 했습니다. 배저 부인의 은근한 말투가 그런 대답을 요구하는 듯했으니까요.\n\n\"클레어 양도 그렇지 않죠?\" 베이엄 배저 부인이 상냥하게 물었습니다.\n\n에이다 역시 아니라고 하면서 불편한 기색을 보였습니다.\n\n\"그러니까 말이에요, 얘들아.\" 배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"─내가 너희를 얘들아 하고 불러도 괜찮겠지?\"\n\n우리는 배저 부인에게 마음 쓰지 마시라고 간곡히 청했습니다.\n\n\"왜냐하면 너희가 정말로, 이런 말 함부로 해도 된다면 말이지,\" 배저 부인이 말을 이었습니다. \"어쩜 그렇게 더할 나위 없이 사랑스러운지. 그래서 말인데, 얘들아, 내가 아직 젊긴 하지만─베이엄 배저 씨가 그렇게 말해주는 영광을 베풀어주긴 하지만─\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 배저 씨가 공개 집회에서 누군가의 말에 반박하듯 큰 소리로 외쳤습니다. \"전혀 아니지요!\"\n\n\"좋아요.\" 배저 부인이 미소 지었습니다. \"아직 젊다고 해두죠.\"\n\n\"두말할 것 없이요.\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"얘들아, 아직 젊긴 해도 나는 젊은 남자들을 관찰할 기회가 많았단다. 그리운 옛 크리플러호에는 그런 젊은이가 많았지, 정말이야. 그 후에 스워서 함장과 함께 지중해에 있을 때는, 함장 휘하의 사관후보생들을 알고 가까이할 기회를 하나도 놓치지 않았어. 너희는 그들이 '젊은 신사분들'이라 불리는 걸 들어본 적도 없을 테고, 그들이 주간 회계 장부를 백토로 닦았다는 식의 비유도 아마 이해하지 못하겠지만, 나는 다르단다. 푸른 바다가 내겐 제2의 고향이었고, 나는 거의 뱃사람이나 다름없었으니까. 그리고 또 딩고 교수와 함께 지낼 때도 그랬지.\"\n\n\"유럽 전역에 명성을 떨친 분이지요.\" 배저 씨가 나직이 덧붙였습니다.\n\n\"내가 사랑하는 첫째를 잃고 사랑하는 둘째의 아내가 되었을 때도,\" 배저 부인은 먼저 떠난 남편들을 마치 수수께끼 놀이의 한 토막처럼 말했습니다. \"나는 여전히 젊은이들을 관찰할 기회를 누렸어. 딩고 교수의 강의를 듣는 수강생이 아주 많았는데, 학문 속에서 학문이 줄 수 있는 최고의 위안을 스스로 구하던 저명한 과학자의 아내로서, 우리 집을 학생들에게 일종의 '과학 교류소'처럼 활짝 열어두는 게 내 자랑거리가 되었지. 화요일 저녁마다 그 다과를 들고 싶어 하는 모든 이를 위해 레모네이드와 갖가지 비스킷이 차려졌어. 그리고 과학이 끝도 없이 펼쳐졌단다.\"\n\n\"참으로 놀라운 모임이었겠습니다, 서머슨 양.\" 배저 씨가 경건하게 말했습니다. \"그런 분의 후원 아래 거기서 얼마나 대단한 지적 마찰이 일어났겠습니까!\"\n\n\"그리고 이제는,\" 배저 부인이 말을 이었습니다. \"이제는 사랑하는 셋째 배저 씨의 아내가 되어서도, 나는 스워서 함장 생전에 몸에 밴, 그리고 딩고 교수 생전에 새롭고 뜻밖의 목적에 맞게 다듬어진 그 관찰 습관을 여전히 이어가고 있어. 그러니 나는 풋내기로서 카스턴 씨를 살피게 된 게 아니란다. 그런데도 나는, 얘들아, 그분이 자기 직업을 신중하게 고른 게 아니라는 쪽으로 생각이 많이 기운단다.\"\n\n이제 에이다가 너무도 근심스러운 얼굴을 하기에, 나는 배저 부인에게 그 짐작의 근거가 무엇인지 물었습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양,\" 그녀가 대답했습니다. \"카스턴 씨의 성격과 행동을 보고 하는 말이에요. 그분은 워낙 느긋한 성미라 자기 진심을 굳이 입에 올릴 가치가 있다고 여기지 않겠지만, 그 직업에 대해서는 흥이 나질 않아요. 그것을 천직으로 삼게 할 만한 적극적인 관심이 없는 거죠. 그것과 관련해 무언가 또렷한 인상을 갖고 있다면, 내가 보기엔 지겨운 일이라는 인상일 거예요. 자, 이건 가망이 없어요. 앨런 우드코트 씨 같은 젊은이는 그 일이 해낼 수 있는 모든 것에 강한 관심을 품고 시작했기에, 쥐꼬리만 한 돈을 받고 엄청난 일을 하면서, 또 여러 해 동안 적잖은 인내와 실망을 겪으면서도 거기서 어떤 보람을 찾아낼 거예요. 하지만 카스턴 씨에게는 결코 그런 일이 없으리라고 나는 확신해요.\"\n\n\"배저 씨도 그렇게 생각하시나요?\" 에이다가 머뭇거리며 물었습니다.\n\n\"음,\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"사실대로 말하자면, 클레어 양, 배저 부인이 말을 꺼내기 전까지 나는 이 일을 그런 식으로 보지 못했습니다. 하지만 배저 부인이 그렇게 비춰주자, 나는 당연히 그것을 깊이 헤아렸지요. 배저 부인의 정신은 타고난 장점에 더해, 영국 해군의 스워서 함장과 딩고 교수라는 두 분의 매우 저명한(아니, 빛나는 분이라고까지 말하겠습니다) 공인에 의해 다듬어지는 드문 이점을 누렸다는 걸 알기 때문입니다. 내가 다다른 결론은─한마디로, 배저 부인의 결론입니다.\"\n\n\"스워서 함장의 좌우명이 하나 있었지.\" 배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"그분 특유의 비유적인 해군식으로 말하자면, 역청을 뜨겁게 데울 때는 아무리 뜨겁게 해도 지나치지 않다, 그리고 갑판 한 장을 닦더라도 데비 존스가 뒤쫓아 오는 양 닦아야 한다는 거였어. 내 보기엔 이 좌우명이 항해뿐 아니라 의료라는 직업에도 똑같이 들어맞는 것 같구나.\"\n\n“어느 직업에나 통하는 말이지요.” 배저 씨가 말했다. “스워서 함장이 참으로 훌륭하게 말씀하셨어요. 멋진 말이었죠.”\n\n\"모든 직업에 그렇지요.\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"스워서 함장께서 참으로 훌륭하게 말씀하셨습니다. 멋진 말씀이지요.\"\n\"우리가 결혼하고 데번 북부에 머물 때 사람들이 딩고 교수를 못마땅해했어.\" 배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"교수가 그 자그마한 지질 망치로 집이며 다른 건물들에서 조각을 떼어내 흉하게 만든다고 말이지. 하지만 교수는 자기가 아는 건물이라곤 오직 '과학의 신전'뿐이라고 대꾸했단다. 이치는 같다고 보는데, 그렇지 않니?\"\n\n\"정확히 같습니다.\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다. \"기막힌 표현이지요! 교수께서는 마지막 병석에서도 똑같은 말씀을 하셨습니다, 서머슨 양. (정신이 흐려진 채로) 그 자그마한 망치를 베개 밑에 두겠다고 고집하시며 곁에서 시중드는 사람들의 얼굴을 쪼아대셨지요. 천성으로 굳은 그 열정이란!\"\n\n배저 부부가 그토록 길게 늘어놓은 대화는 굳이 없어도 좋았겠지만, 우리 둘 다 그들이 사심 없이 우리에게 그 의견을 전해주었으며, 그것이 옳을 가능성이 크다고 느꼈습니다. 우리는 리처드와 이야기하기 전까지는 잔다이스 씨에게 아무 말도 하지 않기로 했고, 그가 다음 날 저녁에 오기로 했기에 그와 아주 진지하게 의논하기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n그래서 그가 에이다와 잠시 함께 있은 뒤 내가 들어가 보니, 내 사랑하는 동무는(내 예상대로) 그가 무슨 말을 하든 옳다고 받아들일 채비가 되어 있었습니다.\n\n\"리처드, 일은 잘돼 가요?\" 내가 말했습니다. 나는 늘 그의 반대편에 앉았습니다. 그는 나를 누이처럼 여겼으니까요.\n\n\"아! 그럭저럭요!\" 리처드가 말했습니다.\n\n\"이보다 더 좋게 말할 순 없잖아, 에스더, 그렇지?\" 내 귀염둥이가 의기양양하게 외쳤습니다.\n\n나는 더없이 현명한 표정으로 내 귀염둥이를 바라보려 했지만, 당연히 그럴 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"그럭저럭이라고요?\" 내가 되물었습니다.\n\n\"네,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그럭저럭이요. 좀 단조롭고 따분해요. 하지만 다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지일 테니!\"\n\n\"아! 리처드도 참!\" 내가 나무랐습니다.\n\n\"왜 그래요?\" 리처드가 말했습니다.\n\n\"다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지라니요!\"\n\n\"그게 잘못된 말은 아니지 않아, 더든 아주머니.\" 에이다가 그를 사이에 두고 나를 그토록 믿음직스럽게 바라보며 말했습니다. \"다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지라면, 잘되리라는 거잖아, 그러길 바라.\"\n\n\"아, 그래, 나도 그러길 바라.\" 리처드가 무심하게 이마에서 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기며 대꾸했습니다. \"결국 우리 소송이 끝날 때까지의 일종의 수습 기간일지도 몰라─아 참, 깜빡했네. 소송 얘긴 하면 안 되지. 금지된 구역이니까! 아, 그래, 다 괜찮아. 다른 얘기나 하자.\"\n\n에이다는 기꺼이 그러려 했고, 우리가 이 문제를 더없이 만족스러운 상태로 매듭지었다고 굳게 믿었습니다. 하지만 나는 거기서 멈추는 게 소용없으리라 여겨 다시 말을 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 리처드,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그리고 사랑하는 에이다! 이게 너희 둘 모두에게 얼마나 중요한 일인지, 또 사촌 오라버니에 대한 명예가 걸린 문제인지 잘 생각해 봐. 리처드, 너는 아무 망설임 없이 정말로 진지해야 해. 정말이지, 이 얘기를 제대로 하는 게 좋겠어, 에이다. 곧 너무 늦어버릴 거야.\"\n\n\"아, 그래! 우린 이 얘길 해야 해!\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"하지만 리처드 말이 옳다고 봐.\"\n\n그 애가 그토록 예쁘고, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 그토록 그를 좋아하는데, 내가 현명한 표정을 지어본들 무슨 소용이 있었겠어요!\n\n\"어제 배저 부부가 다녀갔어요, 리처드,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그분들은 리처드가 그 직업을 별로 좋아하지 않는다고 여기는 눈치였어요.\"\n\n\"그분들이 그랬어요?\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"아! 그럼, 얘기가 좀 달라지는데요. 그분들이 그렇게 생각할 줄은 몰랐고, 그분들을 실망시키거나 불편하게 하고 싶진 않으니까. 사실은요, 나도 그 일에 별 관심이 없어요. 하지만, 뭐, 상관없어요! 다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지일 테니까!\"\n\n\"들었지, 에이다!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"사실은,\" 리처드가 반쯤은 생각에 잠긴 채 반쯤은 농담조로 말을 이었습니다. \"내 적성에 딱 맞지가 않아. 흥미가 안 생겨. 그리고 베이엄 배저 부인의 첫째 남편이며 둘째 남편 얘기를 너무 많이 들어야 하고.\"\n\n\"그야 정말 당연하지!\" 에이다가 무척 기뻐하며 외쳤습니다. \"어제 우리 둘 다 했던 바로 그 말이잖아, 에스더!\"\n\n\"게다가,\" 리처드가 말을 이었습니다. \"단조롭고, 오늘은 어제와 너무 똑같고, 내일은 오늘과 너무 똑같아.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"온갖 끈기 있는 일에, 아니 아주 별난 사정이 아니고서야 삶 자체에 다 해당하는 불만이지 않을까 싶어.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각해요?\" 리처드가 여전히 곰곰이 생각하며 대꾸했습니다. \"아마도요! 하! 그렇다면, 그것 봐요,\" 그가 갑자기 다시 명랑해지며 덧붙였습니다. \"결국 방금 내가 한 말로 빙 돌아오는 셈이네요. 다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지라고요. 아, 다 괜찮아요! 다른 얘기나 하자고요.\"\n\n하지만 그 다정한 얼굴의 에이다조차도─그 잊지 못할 11월 안개 속에서 처음 보았을 때 그 얼굴이 순진하고 미더워 보였다면, 이제 그 애의 순진하고 미더운 마음을 알게 된 지금은 얼마나 더 그래 보였는지요─에이다조차도 이 말에는 고개를 가로저으며 심각한 표정을 지었습니다. 그래서 나는 좋은 기회다 싶어 리처드에게 넌지시 일렀습니다. 가끔 그가 자기 자신에게 좀 무심할 수는 있어도, 에이다에게 무심하려는 마음은 결코 없으리라 내가 확신한다고, 또 두 사람의 인생을 좌우할 수 있는 결정의 중요성을 가볍게 여기지 않는 것이 그 애를 위하는 그의 애정 어린 배려의 한 부분이라고요. 이 말에 그는 거의 진지해졌습니다.\n\n\"사랑하는 허버드 할머니,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"바로 그거예요! 나도 그 생각을 여러 번 했고, 그토록 진지해지려고 마음먹고도─어쩐 일인지─딱히 그러지 못하는 나 자신에게 단단히 화가 났어요. 왜 그런지 모르겠어요. 뭔가 의지할 게 필요한 것 같아요. 내가 에이다를 얼마나 좋아하는지 누나도 짐작 못 할 거예요(사랑하는 사촌, 정말 많이 사랑해!). 하지만 다른 일에는 한결같이 자리를 잡지 못하겠어요. 너무 힘겨운 일이고, 시간도 너무 오래 걸리고!\" 리처드가 짜증스러운 기색으로 말했습니다.\n* 허버드 할머니: 영국 전래 동요 속 인물. 살림을 도맡는 다정한 노파를 빗댄 애칭으로, 리처드가 에스더를 놀리듯 부르는 별명이다.\n\n\"아마도,\" 내가 넌지시 말했습니다. \"네가 고른 일을 좋아하지 않아서일 거야.\"\n\n\"가엾어라!\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"정말 그럴 만도 해!\"\n\n소용없었습니다. 내가 현명한 표정을 지어보려는 건 전혀 소용이 없었어요. 다시 애를 써봤지만, 에이다가 두 손을 맞잡아 그의 어깨에 얹고, 그가 그 부드러운 푸른 눈을 바라보고, 그 눈이 다시 그를 바라보는데, 내가 어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠으며, 설령 그런들 무슨 효과가 있었겠어요!\n\n\"있잖아, 내 소중한 사촌,\" 리처드가 그 애의 금빛 곱슬머리를 손가락 사이로 거듭 흘려보내며 말했습니다. \"내가 좀 성급했는지도 몰라. 아니면 내 성향을 내가 잘못 알았는지도 모르고. 내 마음은 그쪽으로 기우는 것 같지 않아. 해보기 전엔 알 수가 없었지. 이제 문제는 이미 해놓은 일을 죄다 되돌릴 만한 가치가 있느냐는 거야. 별것도 아닌 일로 큰 소동을 벌이는 것 같단 말이지.\"\n\n\"리처드,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"어떻게 별것도 아니라고 말할 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"꼭 그런 뜻은 아니에요.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"내 말은, 어쩌면 영영 그게 필요 없을지도 모르니까 별것 아닐 수도 있다는 거예요.\"\n\n이에 에이다와 나는 한목소리로, 이미 해놓은 일을 되돌리는 게 분명히 가치 있을 뿐 아니라 반드시 되돌려야 한다고 거듭 다그쳤습니다. 그러고서 나는 리처드에게 더 마음에 맞는 일을 생각해 본 적이 있는지 물었습니다.\n\"이런, 우리 십턴 부인,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"정곡을 찌르시네요. 네, 있어요. 법률이야말로 내게 딱 맞는 일이라고 생각해 왔어요.\"\n\n* 십턴 부인: 16세기 영국의 전설적인 예언가. 리처드가 에스더의 통찰력을 빗대 붙인 또 다른 장난스러운 별명이다.\n\n\"법률!\" 에이다가 그 이름이 두려운 듯 되뇌었습니다.\n\n\"내가 켄지 씨 사무소에 들어가서,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"켄지 밑에서 견습 계약을 맺게 되면, 그─음!─금지된 구역에 눈을 둘 수 있고, 그것을 공부하고, 통달하고, 그게 소홀히 다뤄지지 않고 제대로 진행되고 있는지 내 눈으로 확인할 수 있어. 에이다의 이익과 내 이익(같은 거지!)을 챙길 수 있고, 블랙스톤이며 그 모든 법학자를 더없이 맹렬한 열정으로 파고들 수 있을 거야.\"\n* 블랙스톤: 윌리엄 블랙스톤. 그의 『영국법 주해』는 당시 법학도의 필독서였다.\n\n나는 그 점에 도무지 확신이 서질 않았고, 오래 미뤄온 그 희망에서 아직 다가오지 않은 막연한 것들을 향한 그의 갈망이 에이다의 얼굴에 그늘을 드리우는 것을 보았습니다. 하지만 꾸준한 노력이 따르는 계획이라면 무엇이든 북돋아 주는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 다만 이번에는 정말 마음을 굳혔는지 확실히 하라고만 일렀습니다.\n\n\"사랑하는 미네르바,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는 누나만큼이나 굳건해요. 실수를 했지만, 누구나 실수는 하는 법이고, 다시는 그러지 않을 거예요. 좀처럼 보기 드문 그런 법률가가 될 거예요. 그러니까, 알다시피,\" 리처드가 다시 의심에 빠져들며 말했습니다. \"별것도 아닌 일로 그렇게 큰 소동을 벌일 만한 가치가 정말로 있다면 말이에요!\"\n\n* 미네르바: 로마 신화 속 지혜의 여신. 역시 에스더의 분별력을 추켜세우는 별명이다.\n이 때문에 우리는 이미 했던 말을 자못 진지하게 다시 되풀이했고, 결국 거의 같은 결론에 이르렀습니다. 하지만 우리가 리처드에게 한순간도 미루지 말고 잔다이스 씨에게 솔직하고 숨김없이 털어놓으라고 어찌나 강하게 권했던지, 또 그의 천성이 본디 감추는 것을 싫어했던지라, 그는 당장 (우리를 데리고) 후견인을 찾아가 모든 것을 다 털어놓았습니다. \"릭,\" 후견인이 그의 말을 주의 깊게 듣고 나서 말했습니다. \"우리는 명예롭게 물러설 수 있고, 또 그렇게 할 거다. 하지만 조심해야 해─우리 사촌을 위해서, 릭, 우리 사촌을 위해서─다시는 이런 실수를 하지 않도록 말이야. 그러니 법률에 관해서는, 결정하기 전에 충분히 시험해 보기로 하자. 뛰어들기 전에 잘 살피고, 시간을 넉넉히 들이는 거다.\"\n\n리처드의 열정은 어찌나 조급하고 변덕스러운 종류였던지, 그는 그 시각에 당장 켄지 씨 사무소로 달려가 그 자리에서 견습 계약을 맺는 것보다 더 바라는 게 없었을 겁니다. 그래도 그토록 필요하다고 우리가 일러둔 신중함을 흔쾌히 받아들이고는, 더없이 가벼운 기분으로 우리 사이에 앉아, 마치 어릴 적부터 인생의 한결같은 단 하나의 목표가 지금 그를 사로잡은 바로 그것이었다는 듯이 이야기하는 데 만족했습니다. 후견인은 그에게 무척 다정하고 따뜻하게 대했지만, 다소 진지했습니다. 그가 떠나고 우리가 잠자리에 들려고 이층으로 올라갈 때 에이다가 이렇게 말할 만큼은요. \"존 사촌 오라버니, 리처드를 나쁘게 보시는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아니란다, 얘야.\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n\"이렇게 어려운 일에서 리처드가 갈피를 잡지 못한 건 아주 당연한 일이잖아요. 흔히 있는 일이고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 얘야.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그렇게 시무룩한 얼굴 하지 마라.\"\n\n\"아, 저 시무룩하지 않아요, 존 사촌 오라버니!\" 에이다가 잘 자라는 인사를 하며 얹어두었던 손을 그의 어깨에 그대로 둔 채, 명랑하게 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"하지만 오라버니가 리처드를 조금이라도 나쁘게 여기신다면 좀 그럴 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"네가 그 애 때문에 조금이라도 불행해지는 일이 생긴다면, 그때서야 나는 그 애를 나쁘게 볼 거다. 하지만 그때조차도 나는 가엾은 릭보다 차라리 나 자신과 다투려 들겠지. 내가 너희를 맺어주었으니 말이야. 하지만, 쯧, 이런 건 다 아무것도 아니다! 그 앤 아직 시간이 있고, 달려야 할 경주가 남았어. 내가 그 애를 나쁘게 본다고? 천만에, 사랑스러운 내 사촌! 그리고 너도 아니야, 내 장담하지!\"\n\n\"네, 정말이에요, 존 사촌 오라버니,\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"온 세상이 다 그런다 해도 저는 리처드를 나쁘게─결코 나쁘게 생각할 수 없을 거예요. 그땐 오히려 다른 어느 때보다도 그를 더 좋게 생각할 거고, 또 그러고 싶어요!\"\n\n그 애가 두 손을─이제는 두 손 다─그의 어깨에 얹고 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 마치 진실 그 자체를 그린 그림처럼, 어찌나 차분하고 진솔하게 그 말을 하던지요!\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 후견인이 그 애를 생각에 잠겨 바라보며 말했습니다. \"어딘가에 이렇게 적혀 있을 것 같구나. 아비의 죄뿐 아니라 어미의 미덕도 이따금 자식에게 내린다고 말이야. 잘 자거라, 내 장미꽃 봉오리야. 잘 자라, 작은 아가씨. 편히 자거라! 좋은 꿈 꾸고!\"\n\n후견인이 그 인자한 표정에 얼마간 그늘을 드리운 채 에이다를 눈으로 좇는 것을 본 건 그때가 처음이었습니다. 그 애가 난롯불 빛 속에서 노래할 때 그가 그 애와 리처드를 바라보던 그 눈길을 나는 똑똑히 기억하고 있었습니다. 햇살이 비쳐 드는 방을 가로질러 두 사람이 걸어 나가 그늘 속으로 사라지는 모습을 그가 지켜본 것도 아주 잠깐 전의 일이었고요. 하지만 그의 눈빛은 변해 있었고, 이제 다시 나를 향한 그 말없는 신뢰의 눈길조차도 처음만큼 그렇게 희망차고 근심 없어 보이지는 않았습니다.\n\n그날 밤 에이다는 그 어느 때보다도 더 리처드를 칭찬했습니다. 그 애는 그가 준 작은 팔찌를 팔에 찬 채 잠들었습니다. 한 시간쯤 자고 난 그 애의 뺨에 입을 맞추며 그 평온하고 행복해 보이는 얼굴을 들여다보았을 때, 나는 그 애가 그의 꿈을 꾸고 있구나 싶었습니다.\n\n그날 밤 나는 도무지 잠이 오질 않아 일어나 앉아 일을 했으니까요. 그것 자체로야 말할 거리도 못 되지만, 나는 잠이 오지 않았고 마음이 좀 가라앉아 있었습니다. 왜인지는 모르겠습니다. 적어도 안다고는 생각하지 않습니다. 어쩌면 알지도 모르지만, 그게 중요한 것 같지는 않습니다.\n\n아무튼 나는 가라앉은 기분에 빠질 한순간의 틈도 남기지 않도록 지독하게 부지런을 떨기로 마음먹었습니다. 나도 모르게 이렇게 말했으니까요. \"에스더! 네가 기분이 가라앉다니. 네가!\" 그렇게 말할 때가 정말 되긴 했습니다. 나는─그래요, 거울 속에서 거의 울고 있는 나 자신을 정말로 보았으니까요. \"너를 불행하게 할 거라곤 하나도 없고 행복하게 할 것투성이인데도 그러다니, 이 배은망덕한 마음아!\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n스스로 잠들 수만 있었다면 당장 그렇게 했겠지만, 그럴 수가 없어서, 나는 그 무렵 한창 매달려 있던 우리 집(황폐한 집 말이에요)을 위한 장식용 바느질감을 바구니에서 꺼내 굳은 각오로 앉아 일을 시작했습니다. 그 작업은 코를 하나하나 다 세어야 했기에, 나는 눈을 뜨고 있을 수 없을 때까지 계속하다가 그제야 잠자리에 들기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n곧 나는 일에 푹 빠졌습니다. 그런데 임시 투덜방의 작업대 서랍에 명주실을 두고 내려와 일을 멈춘 터라, 나는 촛불을 들고 그걸 가지러 살그머니 아래층으로 내려갔습니다. 그런데 들어가 보니 놀랍게도 후견인이 아직 거기 앉아 잿더미를 들여다보고 있는 게 아니겠어요. 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 펴놓은 책은 옆에 팽개쳐진 채였으며, 은빛이 도는 잿빛 머리카락은 마치 다른 데 정신이 팔린 동안 손이 그 사이를 헤매고 다닌 양 이마 위로 어지럽게 흩어져 있었고, 얼굴은 지쳐 보였습니다. 그렇게 느닷없이 마주친 게 거의 무섭기까지 해서, 나는 잠시 가만히 서 있었습니다. 그가 다시 머리카락 사이로 멍하니 손을 쓸어 넘기다 나를 보고 흠칫하지 않았다면 아무 말 없이 물러났을 겁니다.\n\n\"에스더!\"\n\n나는 무엇을 가지러 왔는지 말했습니다.\n\n\"이렇게 늦게까지 일을 하고 있어, 얘야?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 늦게까지 일하고 있어요,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"잠이 오질 않아서 몸을 좀 지치게 하고 싶었거든요. 그런데 후견인님, 후견인님도 늦으셨고, 또 피곤해 보이세요. 잠 못 이루게 하는 걱정거리가 있으신 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"없단다, 작은 아가씨야. 적어도 네가 쉽게 이해할 만한 건 아니야.\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n그가 내게는 너무도 낯선, 회한 어린 어조로 말했기에, 나는 그 뜻을 헤아리는 데 도움이라도 될 양 속으로 되뇌었습니다. \"내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 거라니!\"\n\n\"잠깐 있어 보렴, 에스더.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"마침 네 생각을 하고 있었거든.\"\n\n\"제가 걱정거리는 아니었겠죠, 후견인님?\"\n\n그가 손을 살짝 내저으며 평소의 모습으로 돌아왔습니다. 그 변화가 어찌나 두드러지던지, 또 그토록 큰 자제력을 들여서야 그렇게 하는 듯 보이던지, 나는 또다시 속으로 되뇌고 있었습니다. \"내가 이해할 만한 건 아니라니!\"\n\n\"작은 아가씨야,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"나는 생각하고 있었단다─그러니까, 여기 앉아 있는 동안 줄곧 생각해 온 건데─네 신상에 관해 내가 아는 모든 걸 너도 알아야 마땅하다고 말이야. 아주 적단다. 거의 없다시피 해.\"\n\n\"후견인님,\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"전에 그 일로 제게 말씀하셨을 때─\"\n\n\"하지만 그 후로,\" 그가 내가 하려던 말을 앞질러 진지하게 끼어들었습니다. \"곰곰이 생각해 봤다. 네가 내게 물을 게 있느냐 하는 것과, 내가 네게 들려줄 게 있느냐 하는 건 별개의 문제더구나, 에스더. 내가 아는 그 적은 것이나마 네게 전하는 게 어쩌면 내 도리일 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하신다면, 후견인님, 그게 옳아요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각한다.\" 그가 아주 부드럽고, 다정하게, 또렷이 대답했습니다. \"얘야, 이제 그렇게 생각해. 생각할 가치가 있는 어떤 남자나 여자의 마음속에서든 네 처지에 어떤 진짜 불리함이 따라붙을 수 있다면, 적어도 너만은, 온 세상 누구보다도 너만은, 그 본질에 대해 막연한 인상을 품은 채 스스로 그것을 부풀려선 안 되니까.\"\n\n나는 자리에 앉아, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 침착해지려 잠시 애쓴 뒤에 말했습니다. \"제 가장 어린 기억 가운데 하나가 이 말이에요, 후견인님. '네 어미는, 에스더, 네 수치고, 너는 네 어미의 수치였다. 머지않아 때가 올 거다. 네가 이걸 더 잘 이해하고, 또 여자만이 느낄 수 있는 식으로 그것을 절감하게 될 때가.'\" 그 말을 되뇌면서 나는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 있었지만, 이제는, 부디 더 나은 종류의 부끄러움이었기를 바라며, 그 손을 내리고 그에게 말했습니다. 내가 어린 시절부터 이 순간까지 결코, 결코, 결코 그것을 느끼지 않았던 그 축복이 다 그분 덕이라고요. 그가 나를 막으려는 듯 손을 들어 올렸습니다. 그에게 고마움을 표해서는 안 된다는 걸 나는 잘 알고 있었기에 더는 말하지 않았습니다.\n\n\"구 년 전이란다, 얘야.\" 그가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했습니다. \"어느 외딴곳에 은거하던 한 부인에게서 편지 한 통을 받은 게. 내가 이제껏 읽어본 그 어떤 편지와도 다르게, 준엄한 격정과 힘으로 쓰인 편지였지. 그 편지는(글에 그렇게 또렷이 쓰여 있었듯) 내게 보내진 거였어. 어쩌면 나를 그렇게 믿는 게 글쓴이의 별난 성정이었기 때문일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그 믿음을 증명하는 게 내 성정이었기 때문일 수도 있겠지. 그 편지는 한 아이에 대해 들려주었어. 그때 열두 살 난 고아 소녀였지. 네 기억 속에 살아 있는 것과 비슷한, 그런 모진 말들로 말이야. 글쓴이는 그 아이를 태어날 때부터 비밀 속에서 길렀고, 그 아이가 존재했다는 흔적을 모조리 지워버렸으며, 만약 그 아이가 여자가 되기 전에 글쓴이가 죽는다면 그 아이는 벗도, 이름도, 신원도 전혀 없이 홀로 남겨질 거라고 했어. 그런 경우 글쓴이가 시작한 일을 내가 마무리해 줄 의향이 있는지 헤아려달라고 청했지.\"\n\n나는 잠자코 들으며 그를 주의 깊게 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"네 어린 시절의 기억이, 글쓴이가 이 모든 것을 보고 표현하던 그 음울한 매개와, 그 아이가 아무 죄도 없는 잘못을 속죄해야 한다는 인상으로 그 마음을 흐려놓던 그 뒤틀린 신앙을 짐작하게 해줄 거다. 나는 어둠에 갇힌 삶을 사는 그 작은 아이가 가여워 그 편지에 답장을 썼단다.\"\n\n나는 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었습니다.\n\n\"그 편지는 내게 하나의 단서를 달았어. 글쓴이를 만나자고 결코 청하지 말라는 거였지. 오래도록 세상과의 모든 왕래를 끊고 지내온 사람이지만, 내가 믿을 만한 대리인을 한 사람 정하면 그 사람은 만나겠다고 했어. 나는 켄지 씨를 보냈단다. 부인은, 그가 묻지도 않았는데 스스로, 자기 이름이 가명이라고 했어. 그리고 만약 그런 경우에 혈연이라는 게 있다면, 자기가 그 아이의 이모라고. 그 이상은 어떤 인간적인 사정을 들어서도 결코 (그리고 그는 그 결심이 얼마나 굳센지 충분히 확신했지) 밝히지 않겠다고 했어. 얘야, 이게 내가 아는 전부란다.\"\n\n나는 그의 손을 잠시 내 두 손에 쥐고 있었습니다.\n\n\"나는 내 피후견인이 나를 보는 것보다 더 자주 그 아이를 보았고,\" 그가 별일 아닌 듯 명랑하게 덧붙였습니다. \"그 아이가 사랑받고, 쓸모 있고, 행복하다는 걸 늘 알고 있었지. 그 아이는 하루하루, 매시간 나에게 이만 배로, 아니 거기에 또 이만 배를 더해 갚아준단다!\"\n\n\"그리고 그보다 더 자주,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그 아이는 자기에게 아버지나 다름없는 후견인에게 축복을 빌어요!\"\n\n아버지라는 말에, 나는 그 얼굴에 아까의 그 근심이 떠오르는 것을 보았습니다. 그는 앞서처럼 그것을 가라앉혔고, 그것은 순식간에 사라졌습니다. 하지만 분명히 거기 있었고, 내 말이 떨어지자마자 어찌나 빠르게 떠올랐던지, 마치 내 말이 그에게 충격을 준 것만 같았습니다. 나는 또다시 의아해하며 속으로 되뇌었습니다. \"내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 거라니. 내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 건 아니라니!\" 그래요, 사실이었습니다. 나는 그것을 이해하지 못했습니다. 그 후로도 오래오래 이해하지 못했습니다.\n\n\"아버지가 하듯 잘 자라는 인사를 받으렴, 얘야.\" 그가 내 이마에 입을 맞추며 말했습니다. \"그리고 가서 쉬어. 일하고 생각하기엔 너무 늦은 시각이구나. 너는 하루 종일 우리 모두를 위해 그러고 있잖니, 작은 살림꾼아!\"\n\n그날 밤 나는 더는 일도 생각도 하지 않았습니다. 나는 나를 향한 하늘의 섭리와 보살핌에 감사하며 고마운 마음을 하늘에 활짝 열어 보이고는 잠이 들었습니다.\n\n다음 날 우리에게 손님이 찾아왔습니다. 앨런 우드코트 씨가 온 것입니다. 그는 우리에게 작별을 고하러 왔는데, 미리 그렇게 하기로 정해둔 터였습니다. 그는 선상 군의관으로 중국과 인도로 떠날 예정이었습니다. 아주, 아주 오랫동안 떠나 있을 참이었습니다.\n\n나는 그가 부유하지 않다고 믿습니다─아니, 알고 있습니다. 홀로 된 어머니가 마련할 수 있는 모든 것이 그의 직업 자격을 갖추는 데 들어갔던 것이지요. 런던에서 별다른 연줄도 없는 젊은 개업의에게 그 일은 벌이가 좋지 않았습니다. 그는 밤낮으로 수많은 가난한 사람들을 위해 헌신하며 그들에게 놀랍도록 다정하고 능숙한 치료를 베풀었지만, 그것으로 돈은 거의 벌지 못했습니다. 그는 나보다 일곱 살 위였습니다. 굳이 말할 필요는 없습니다. 무엇과도 별 상관이 없어 보이니까요.\n\n내 생각에는─그러니까, 그가 우리에게 말하기로는─그가 삼사 년 정도 개업해 있었고, 만약 앞으로 삼사 년을 더 버텨낼 수 있으리라는 희망이 있었다면 그는 이번에 떠나는 항해에 나서지 않았으리라는 것이었습니다. 하지만 그에게는 재산도 사사로운 수입도 없었기에 떠나는 것이었습니다. 그는 그동안 우리를 여러 번 찾아왔었습니다. 우리는 그가 떠나는 게 안타까웠습니다. 그는 그 분야를 가장 잘 아는 이들 사이에서 두각을 나타냈고, 거기에 속한 가장 위대한 인물들 가운데 더러는 그를 높이 평가했으니까요.\n\n작별 인사를 하러 왔을 때, 그는 처음으로 어머니를 모시고 왔습니다. 그녀는 빛나는 검은 눈을 가진 어여쁜 노부인이었지만, 자존심이 강해 보였습니다. 그녀는 웨일스 출신으로, 아주 오래전에 모건 앱케리그라는 이름의 저명한 조상을 두었다는데─김릿 비슷하게 들리는 어느 고장 사람이라고 했습니다─이제껏 알려진 가장 빛나는 인물이었으며 그의 친척이라면 죄다 일종의 왕족 같은 집안이었다고 했습니다. 그분은 평생토록 늘 산에 올라 누군가와 싸우며 보낸 듯했고, 크럼린왈린워라고 들리는 이름의 음유시인이 그의 공적을 기려, 내가 알아들은 바로는 뮬린윌린워드라는 제목의 노래를 지어 불렀다고 했습니다.\n\n우드코트 부인은 그 위대한 친척의 명성을 우리에게 한껏 늘어놓고 나서, 자기 아들 앨런이 어디를 가든 틀림없이 그 가문의 내력을 기억하여 결코 그보다 낮은 혼인을 맺지 않으리라고 말했습니다. 그녀는 아들에게 일렀습니다. 인도에는 한밑천 잡으러 건너간 잘생긴 영국 숙녀가 많고, 그중에는 재산까지 갖춘 이도 더러 줍듯이 얻을 수 있지만, 그런 가문의 후손에게는 미모도 재산도 가문의 혈통 없이는 충분치 않으며, 혈통이야말로 언제나 으뜸으로 따져야 할 것이라고요. 그녀가 어찌나 혈통 얘기를 많이 하던지 한순간 나는 어렴풋이, 그것도 아픈 마음으로 생각했습니다─하지만 그녀가 내 혈통이 어떤지를 생각하거나 마음 쓸 거라고 짐작하다니, 얼마나 부질없는 공상인지요!\n\n우드코트 씨는 어머니의 장황함에 다소 난처해하는 듯했지만, 그것을 그녀에게 내비치기에는 너무도 사려 깊은 사람이어서, 슬며시 화제를 돌려 후견인에게 그 환대와, 우리와 함께 보낸 더없이 행복한 시간─그는 더없이 행복한 시간이라고 했습니다─에 대한 감사의 말을 전하게끔 했습니다. 그 시간들의 기억이 어디를 가든 자기와 함께할 것이며 언제까지나 소중히 간직되리라고 그는 말했습니다. 그래서 우리는 한 사람씩 차례로 그에게 손을 내밀었고─적어도, 그들이 그랬고, 나도 그랬습니다. 그래서 그는 에이다의 손에─그리고 내 손에 입술을 댔습니다. 그러고는 그 아주, 아주 긴 항해를 떠났습니다!\n\n나는 그날 하루 종일 정말 분주하게, 집의 하인들에게 보낼 지시를 적고, 후견인에게 전할 쪽지를 쓰고, 그의 책과 서류를 털고, 이런저런 일로 살림 열쇠 꾸러미를 꽤 짤랑거렸습니다. 해 질 녘 어스름 속에서 창가에서 노래하고 바느질하며 여전히 분주하던 차에, 뜻밖에도 누가 들어왔나 했더니 글쎄 캐디가 아니겠어요. 올 줄은 전혀 몰랐는데요!\n\n\"어머, 캐디,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"이 아름다운 꽃들 좀 봐!\"\n\n그 애 손에는 더없이 어여쁜 작은 꽃다발이 들려 있었습니다.\n\n\"정말 그래, 에스더.\" 캐디가 대답했습니다. \"내가 본 것 중에 제일 사랑스러운 꽃이야.\"\n\n\"프린스가 준 거지, 응?\" 내가 속삭이듯 물었습니다.\n\n\"아니.\" 캐디가 고개를 가로저으며 꽃을 내게 내밀어 향기를 맡게 해주었습니다. \"프린스 아니야.\"\n\n\"어머나, 캐디!\" 내가 말했습니다. \"너 애인이 둘이구나!\"\n\n\"왜? 이게 그런 것처럼 보여?\" 캐디가 말했습니다.\n\n\"이게 그런 것처럼 보이냐고?\" 나는 그 애의 뺨을 꼬집으며 되물었습니다.\n\n캐디는 대답 대신 웃기만 하면서, 삼십 분 정도 있다 가려고 왔고 그 시간이 지나면 프린스가 모퉁이에서 기다리고 있을 거라고 했습니다. 그러고는 나와 에이다와 함께 창가에 앉아 두런두런 이야기를 나누며, 이따금 그 꽃을 내게 다시 건네기도 하고 내 머리에 대보며 어울리는지 살펴보기도 했습니다. 마침내 가려고 할 때, 그 애는 나를 내 방으로 데려가 그 꽃을 내 옷에 꽂아주었습니다.\n\n\"나한테 주는 거야?\" 내가 놀라서 물었습니다.\n\n\"너한테 주는 거야.\" 캐디가 입을 맞추며 말했습니다. \"누가 두고 간 거거든.\"\n\n\"두고 갔다고?\"\n\n\"가엾은 플라이트 양 집에서.\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그분에게 무척 잘해주던 누군가가 한 시간쯤 전에 배를 타러 부랴부랴 떠나면서 이 꽃을 두고 갔어. 안 돼, 안 돼! 빼지 마. 이 예쁜 것들 그냥 여기 두렴.\" 캐디가 조심스러운 손길로 꽃을 매만지며 말했습니다. \"내가 직접 그 자리에 있었는데, 누군가 일부러 두고 갔다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 것 같으니까!\"\n\n\"이게 그런 것처럼 보이냐고?\" 에이다가 웃으며 내 뒤로 다가와 즐겁게 내 허리를 감싸 안으며 말했습니다. \"아, 그래, 정말 그래 보여, 더든 아주머니! 정말, 정말 그런 것처럼 보인다고. 아, 정말이지 똑 그래 보여, 얘!\"\n\n리처드가 켄지 씨 사무실에서 시험 삼아 일하도록 주선하는 일은 처음 보였던 것만큼 쉽지 않았습니다. 가장 큰 걸림돌은 리처드 자신이었습니다. 언제든 마음만 먹으면 배저 씨 곁을 떠날 수 있게 되자, 그는 도리어 정말 떠나고 싶은 건지 의심하기 시작했습니다. 솔직히 잘 모르겠다고 했습니다. 나쁜 직업도 아니고, 싫다고 단언할 수도 없으며, 어쩌면 다른 어떤 일만큼이나 좋아하는지도 모르니, 한 번만 더 기회를 줘보면 어떻겠느냐는 것이었습니다. 그러고는 몇 주 동안 책 몇 권과 뼈 몇 개를 끼고 틀어박혀, 놀라운 속도로 상당한 지식을 쌓아가는 듯했습니다. 그러나 한 달쯤 이어지던 열정은 식어가기 시작했고, 완전히 식고 나자 다시 뜨거워지기 시작했습니다. 법학과 의학 사이를 오가는 그의 갈팡질팡은 어찌나 오래 이어졌던지, 결국 배저 씨와 갈라서서 켄지-카보이 법률사무소의 시험 과정에 들어가기까지 한여름이 와버렸습니다. 그렇게 변덕스러우면서도 리처드는 \"이번에는\" 반드시 진지하게 임하겠노라 결심했다며 스스로를 무척 대견해했습니다. 게다가 줄곧 어찌나 사람 좋고, 기분이 들떠 있고, 에이다를 끔찍이 아꼈던지, 그를 기쁘게 여기지 않기란 정말이지 어려웠습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 아저씨 말이야,\" 그 무렵 잔다이스 아저씨는 바람이 자꾸 동쪽으로 분다고 느끼셨다는 점을 여기서 짚어두겠습니다. \"잔다이스 아저씨 말이야,\" 리처드는 내게 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 분이야, 에스더! 다른 건 다 제쳐두고 아저씨를 만족시켜드리기 위해서라도, 나는 각별히 조심해서 나 자신을 단단히 다잡고 이번 일을 깔끔하게 마무리지어야 해.\"\n\n그토록 잘 웃는 얼굴과 무심한 태도로, 무엇이든 손에 잡되 무엇도 붙들지 못하는 성미로 자기 자신을 단단히 다잡겠다는 발상은, 우습도록 앞뒤가 맞지 않았습니다. 그런데도 그는 이따금, 어찌나 단단히 다잡고 있는지 머리가 세지 않는 게 신기할 지경이라고 우리에게 말하곤 했습니다. 그가 일을 깔끔하게 마무리지은 방식이란 (앞서 말했듯이) 한여름쯤 켄지 씨 사무실로 가서 그 일이 마음에 드는지 시험해보는 것이었습니다.\n\n이 무렵 내내 돈 문제에서 그는 앞서 한 차례 묘사했던 그대로였습니다. 너그럽고, 헤프고, 어처구니없이 무신경하면서도, 자신이 제법 계산적이고 신중하다고 철석같이 믿었습니다. 그가 켄지 씨 사무실로 갈 무렵, 나는 그 앞에서 에이다에게 반쯤 농담조로 반쯤 진담조로, 돈을 저렇게 우습게 아니 포르투나투스의 지갑이라도 가져야겠다고 말한 적이 있습니다. 그러자 그는 이렇게 대꾸했습니다. \"내 보배 같은 사촌 누이야, 이 늙은 할멈 소리 좀 들어봐! 왜 저런 소리를 할까? 내가 며칠 전에 멋진 조끼와 단추를 사느라 팔 파운드 남짓(얼마였든)을 썼다고 저러는 거야. 그런데 말이지, 내가 배저 씨네 그냥 있었다면 가슴 찢어지는 강의료로 단번에 십이 파운드를 써야 했을 거야. 그러니 이 거래로 나는 사 파운드를—한꺼번에—번 셈이지!\"\n* 포르투나투스의 지갑: 중세 설화에 나오는 마법의 지갑으로, 꺼내도 꺼내도 돈이 마르지 않는다.\n\n그가 법학을 시험해보는 동안 런던에서 어떻게 지내게 할지를 두고, 그와 후견인 사이에 의논이 많았습니다. 우리는 진작에 황폐한 집으로 돌아온 터라, 거리가 너무 멀어서 그가 그곳으로 일주일에 한 번 이상 오기란 무리였기 때문입니다. 후견인은 만약 리처드가 켄지 씨 사무실에 자리를 잡는다면 우리도 가끔 며칠씩 묵을 수 있는 셋집이나 사무실을 얻겠다고 하셨습니다. \"하지만 말이다, 꼬마 아가씨,\" 후견인은 의미심장하게 머리를 문지르며 덧붙이셨습니다. \"그 녀석은 아직 거기 자리를 잡지 못했어!\" 의논 끝에 우리는 퀸 광장 근처의 한적한 옛집에 가구가 갖춰진 아담한 셋방을 달치기로 빌려주었습니다. 그는 곧장 가진 돈을 죄다 쏟아부어 이 셋방을 꾸밀 더없이 별난 자잘한 장식품과 사치품을 사들였습니다. 그래서 에이다와 내가, 그가 사려고 마음먹은 물건 가운데 유난히 불필요하고 값비싼 것을 사지 말라고 만류할 때마다, 그는 그 물건에 들었을 비용을 자기 공으로 돌리고는, 다른 데 그보다 적게 쓰면 그 차액을 아끼는 셈이라고 우겼습니다.\n\n이런 일들이 마무리되지 않은 채 미뤄지는 동안, 보이손 씨 댁을 찾는 방문도 늦춰졌습니다. 마침내 리처드가 셋방에 들어가면서, 우리의 출발을 막을 일은 아무것도 없게 되었습니다. 그 계절이면 그도 우리와 함께 충분히 갈 수 있었지만, 새 자리의 신선함에 푹 빠져 그 운명적인 소송의 수수께끼를 풀어보려고 무척 열성을 내던 참이었습니다. 그래서 우리는 그 없이 떠났고, 내 사랑하는 동무는 그가 그렇게 바쁜 것이 기특하다며 기뻐서 칭찬했습니다.\n\n우리는 역마차로 즐거운 여정을 따라 링컨셔로 내려갔고, 스킴폴 씨라는 재미난 길동무도 함께였습니다. 듣자 하니 그의 가구는 그의 파란 눈 딸 생일날 압류해 간 사람이 죄다 실어 가버렸다는데, 그는 가구가 사라졌다고 생각하니 오히려 후련해하는 눈치였습니다. 의자며 탁자며 따분한 물건이라고 그는 말했습니다. 단조로운 관념이고, 표정에 변화라곤 없으며, 사람을 멋쩍게 노려보면 사람도 그것들을 멋쩍게 노려본다는 것이었습니다. 그러니 어떤 특정한 의자와 탁자에 매이지 않고, 빌린 가구들 사이를 나비처럼 날아다니며, 자단목에서 마호가니로, 마호가니에서 호두나무로, 이 모양에서 저 모양으로, 기분 내키는 대로 옮겨 다니니 얼마나 즐거운가!\n\n\"이 일의 우스운 점은 말이지,\" 스킴폴 씨가 더욱 예민해진 익살스러운 감각으로 말했습니다. \"내 의자와 탁자 값은 치르지도 않았는데, 집주인이 그걸 더없이 태연하게 들고 가버린다는 거야. 이거 참 익살맞지 않은가! 어딘가 괴상한 데가 있어. 의자와 탁자 상인은 내 집세를 집주인에게 대신 내주겠다고 약속한 적이 없거든. 그런데 집주인이 어째서 그 사람과 다투는 거지? 만약 내 코에 뾰루지가 났는데 그게 집주인 특유의 미의식에 거슬린다면, 집주인이 뾰루지 하나 없는 의자와 탁자 상인의 코를 긁어댈 까닭이 없잖나. 그 사람 논리는 결함이 있는 것 같아!\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 후견인이 너그럽게 말씀하셨습니다. \"그 의자와 탁자에 보증을 선 사람이 누구든 결국 그 값을 치러야 한다는 건 꽤 명백하지.\"\n\n\"바로 그거예요!\" 스킴폴 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"이 일에서 가장 어처구니없는 대목이 바로 그겁니다! 내가 집주인에게 말했지요. '여보게, 자네 그 물건들을 그렇게 무례하게 쓸어 가지만, 결국 그 값은 내 훌륭한 친구 잔다이스가 치러야 한다는 걸 모르나? 자네는 그분 재산은 안중에도 없는가?' 그런데 손톱만큼도 없더군요.\"\n\n\"그러고는 모든 제안을 거절했고,\" 후견인이 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n\"모든 제안을 거절했어요,\" 스킴폴 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"내가 사무적인 제안을 했거든요. 그자를 내 방으로 불러들였지요. 내가 말했어요. '자네 사업가지, 그렇지?' 그자가 대답하더군요. '그렇소.' '아주 좋아,' 내가 말했지요. '그럼 우리 사업가답게 해보세. 여기 잉크병이 있고, 펜과 종이가 있고, 봉인지가 있네. 자네는 무얼 원하나? 나는 자네 집에 꽤 오랫동안 살았고, 이 불쾌한 오해가 생기기 전까지는 서로 만족스러웠다고 믿네. 그러니 다정하면서도 사업가답게 굴어보세. 자네는 무얼 원하나?' 이에 그자는—어딘가 동방풍이 묻어나는—비유적인 표현을 쓰더군요. 내 돈은 색깔조차 본 적이 없다고요. '나의 정겨운 친구여,' 내가 말했지요. '나는 돈이라곤 한 푼도 없다네. 돈에 관해서는 아무것도 모르고.' '그럼, 선생,' 그자가 말하더군요. '시간을 드리면 무얼 내놓으시겠소?' '여보게,' 내가 말했지요. '나는 시간이란 게 뭔지 도무지 모른다네. 하지만 자네는 사업가라고 하니, 펜과 잉크와 종이로—그리고 봉인지로—사업가답게 처리할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 나는 기꺼이 하겠네. 남의 돈으로 자네 잇속을 챙기지는 말게(그건 어리석은 짓이니), 사업가답게 굴라고!' 그런데도 그자는 그러질 못하더군요. 그걸로 끝이었어요.\"\n\n이런 것들이 스킴폴 씨의 어린아이 같은 천성이 안긴 불편 가운데 하나였다면, 그 천성에는 분명 이점도 있었습니다. 여정 내내 그는 우리 손에 들어온 먹을거리(고급 온실 복숭아 한 바구니까지 포함해서)를 아주 맛있게 즐겼지만, 무엇 하나 값을 치를 생각은 하지 않았습니다. 그래서 마부가 삯을 받으러 다가오자, 그는 마부에게 정말 후한 삯이란, 그러니까 넉넉한 삯이란 얼마라고 생각하느냐고 다정하게 물었고, 승객 한 명당 반 크라운이라는 대답을 듣자, 이것저것 따져보면 그것도 적은 편이라며, 잔다이스 씨가 대신 치르도록 맡겨버렸습니다.\n\n날씨는 더없이 화창했습니다. 푸른 곡식이 어찌나 아름답게 물결치는지, 종다리가 어찌나 즐겁게 노래하는지, 산울타리는 들꽃으로 가득하고, 나무들은 잎이 빽빽하게 돋았으며, 콩밭 위로 산들바람이 불어와 공기를 그토록 향긋한 내음으로 채웠습니다! 오후 늦게 우리는 역마차에서 내려야 할 장날 마을에 다다랐습니다. 교회 첨탑이 있고, 장터가 있고, 장터 십자가가 있고, 햇살이 쨍쨍 내리쬐는 거리 하나가 있고, 늙은 말 한 마리가 다리를 식히는 연못이 있고, 비좁은 그늘 자투리에 졸린 듯 눕거나 서 있는 남자 몇몇이 있는 따분한 작은 마을이었습니다. 길을 따라 줄곧 나뭇잎이 살랑거리고 곡식이 물결치던 풍경을 보다가 오니, 잉글랜드가 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 고요하고, 가장 무덥고, 가장 미동도 없는 작은 마을처럼 보였습니다.\n\n여관에서 우리는 말을 탄 보이손 씨를 만났는데, 그는 몇 마일 떨어진 자기 집으로 우리를 데려가려고 덮개 없는 마차를 대기시켜놓고 기다리던 중이었습니다. 그는 우리를 보고 몹시 기뻐하며 잽싸게 말에서 내렸습니다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 정중하게 인사를 건넨 뒤 그가 말했습니다. \"이건 더없이 형편없는 역마차로군요. 지구상에 짐스럽게 굴러다닌 가증스러운 대중교통수단 가운데서도 가장 노골적인 본보기입니다. 오늘 오후엔 정각보다 이십오 분이나 늦었어요. 저 마부는 사형감이오!\"\n\n\"정각보다 늦었나요?\" 마침 보이손 씨가 말을 건넨 스킴폴 씨가 물었습니다. \"내 약점은 잘 아시잖소.\"\n\n\"이십오 분! 이십육 분!\" 보이손 씨가 회중시계를 보며 대꾸했습니다. \"숙녀 두 분을 태우고서도 저 악당은 일부러 이십육 분이나 늦게 도착한 거요. 일부러! 우연일 리가 없소! 게다가 저자의 아비도—그 삼촌도—마부석에 앉았던 자들 가운데 가장 방탕한 작자들이었지.\"\n\n이렇게 더없이 분개한 어조로 말하면서도, 그는 더없이 부드럽게 우리를 작은 사륜마차에 태웠고, 얼굴엔 온통 웃음과 기쁨이 가득했습니다.\n\n\"미안합니다, 숙녀분들,\" 채비가 다 끝나고 그가 마차 문 옆에 모자를 벗은 채 서서 말했습니다. \"어쩔 수 없이 두 마일 가까이 둘러 가야 하는군요. 하지만 곧장 가는 길은 레스터 데드록 경의 영지를 가로지른답니다. 그자의 땅에는, 지금 우리 사이가 이런 관계로 있는 한, 내 발도 내 말 발도 결코 들이지 않겠다고 살아 숨 쉬는 동안 맹세했지요!\" 그러더니 후견인의 눈길과 마주치자, 그는 그 어마어마한 웃음을 한바탕 터뜨렸는데, 그 웃음은 미동도 없던 작은 장날 마을마저 뒤흔드는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"데드록 부부가 여기 내려와 있나, 로런스?\" 우리가 마차를 달리고 보이손 씨가 길가 푸른 잔디밭을 따라 말을 몰며 나아갈 때 후견인이 물으셨습니다.\n\n\"거만한 멍청이 경께서 여기 와 계시지,\" 보이손 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"하하하! 거만한 경께서 여기 와 계시고, 게다가 다행히도 여기 발이 묶여버리셨어. 마님은,\" 그는 마님이라는 호칭을 부를 때면 늘, 마치 그 다툼에서 그녀만은 각별히 빼놓겠다는 듯 정중한 몸짓을 했습니다. \"내 짐작에 날마다 도착할 거라고 기다리는 모양이더군. 가능한 한 오래 모습을 미루는 게 나로선 조금도 놀랍지 않아. 도대체 무엇이 그 빼어난 여인을 저런 허울뿐인 준남작 인형에게 시집가게 했는지는, 인간의 탐구를 좌절시킨 가장 풀 수 없는 수수께끼 가운데 하나라네. 하하하하!\"\n\n\"그럼,\" 후견인이 웃으며 말씀하셨습니다. \"우리는 여기 있는 동안 영지에 발을 들여도 되겠지? 그 금지령이 우리한테까지 미치진 않겠지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"내 손님들에게는 어떤 금지령도 내릴 수 없습니다,\" 그가 에이다와 내게 고개를 숙이며 그토록 우아하게 어울리는 미소 띤 정중함으로 말했습니다. \"떠나는 일에 관해서만 빼고요. 다만 두 분을 모시고 체스니 월드를 안내하는 기쁨을 누릴 수 없다는 게 안타까울 따름입니다. 정말 멋진 곳이거든요! 하지만 잔다이스, 이 여름날의 햇빛에 두고 맹세하건대, 나와 머무는 동안 그 영지 주인을 찾아간다면 자네는 냉랭한 대접밖에 못 받을 걸세. 그자는 언제나 팔일 태엽 시계처럼 거드름을 피우거든. 한 번도 가지 않았고 앞으로도 가지 않을, 화려한 함에 든 팔일 태엽 시계 가문의 일원처럼 말이야—하하하!—하지만 장담컨대, 자기 친구이자 이웃인 보이손의 벗들에게는 한층 더 뻣뻣하게 굴 거야!\"\n\n\"굳이 그걸 시험해보진 않겠네,\" 후견인이 말씀하셨습니다. \"그자가 나를 알게 되는 영광에 무심한 만큼, 나도 그자를 알게 되는 영광에 무심하다네. 영지의 공기와, 다른 구경꾼이라면 누구나 얻을 법한 저택 풍경 정도면 나로선 충분해.\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 보이손 씨가 말했습니다. \"전체로 보면 그게 다행이지. 그게 더 어울려. 이 근방에서 나는 번개에 맞서는 또 다른 아약스쯤으로 여겨진다네. 하하하하! 일요일에 우리 작은 교회에 들어서면, 그 보잘것없는 신도들 가운데 상당수가, 내가 데드록의 노여움을 사서 포석 위에 그슬리고 시들어 털썩 쓰러지는 꼴을 볼 거라고 기대하지. 하하하하! 내가 그러지 않으니 그자가 놀라는 것도 무리는 아닐 거야. 그자는, 맹세코, 가장 자기만족에 빠지고, 가장 천박하고, 가장 멋이나 부리고, 골이라곤 텅텅 빈 얼간이거든!\"\n\n우리가 오르던 언덕 마루에 다다르자, 우리 벗은 체스니 월드 자체를 가리켜 보일 수 있게 되었고, 그 덕에 그의 관심도 저택 주인에게서 다른 데로 돌아갔습니다.\n\n* 아약스: 그리스 신화의 영웅. 폭풍 속에서 신들의 분노에 맞서 \"신들이 막아도 살아남겠다\"고 외치다 벼락을 맞아 죽었다는 이야기로 전해진다.\n울창한 숲이 우거진 멋진 영지 안에 자리 잡은, 그림 같은 옛 저택이었습니다. 그는 나무들 사이로, 저택에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있는, 앞서 말했던 작은 교회의 첨탑을 가리켜 보였습니다. 아, 빛과 그림자가 빠르게 스쳐 가는 그 엄숙한 숲, 마치 천상의 날개가 여름 공기를 가르며 자비로운 심부름을 하는 듯했습니다. 매끄러운 초록빛 비탈, 반짝이는 물, 더없이 짙은 빛깔의 꽃들이 무리지어 그토록 가지런히 배치된 정원, 그 모든 것이 얼마나 아름다워 보였는지요! 박공과 굴뚝과 탑과 작은 망루와 어두운 출입구와 널찍한 테라스 산책로를 갖추고, 그 난간 사이로 휘감기며 화병 위에 수북이 쌓인 장미가 한바탕 붉게 타오르는 그 저택은, 가벼운 듯 단단한 그 자태와 사방에 깃든 고요하고 평온한 정적 속에서, 도무지 실재하는 것 같지 않았습니다. 에이다와 나에게는, 무엇보다 그 정적이 사방에 스며든 기운으로 다가왔습니다. 모든 것에—저택, 정원, 테라스, 초록빛 비탈, 물, 늙은 떡갈나무, 고사리, 이끼, 다시 숲, 그리고 저 멀리 시야가 트인 사이로 자줏빛 노을이 어린 채 우리 앞에 광활하게 펼쳐진 먼 곳에 이르기까지—흐트러짐 없는 안식이 깃들어 있는 듯했습니다.\n\n작은 마을로 들어서서, 데드록 암스라는 간판이 길 앞에서 흔들리는 작은 여관을 지날 때, 보이손 씨는 여관 문밖 의자에 앉아 있던 한 젊은 신사와 인사를 주고받았습니다. 그 신사 곁에는 낚시 도구가 놓여 있었습니다.\n\n\"저 친구가 가정부의 손자인데, 라운스웰이라는 사람이라네,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그리고 저 친구는 저택에 사는 예쁜 처녀와 사랑에 빠졌지. 데드록 마님이 그 예쁜 처녀를 마음에 들어 해서 자기 곁에 두려 하는데—내 젊은 친구는 그 영광을 도무지 달가워하지 않는다네. 하지만 설령 자기 장미 봉오리가 마음이 있다 해도, 저 친구는 아직 결혼할 처지가 못 되거든. 그러니 그저 형편에 맞춰 지낼 수밖에. 그러는 사이 저 친구는 여기 꽤 자주 와서 하루 이틀씩 머물며—낚시를 하는 거지. 하하하하!\"\n\n\"저 사람과 그 예쁜 처녀는 약혼한 사인가요, 보이손 씨?\" 에이다가 물었습니다.\n\n\"이런, 클레어 양,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"아마 둘이 서로 마음을 통하고 있는지도 모르지요. 하지만 곧 직접 보게 될 테니, 그런 일에 관해서는 내가 아가씨에게 배워야겠지요—아가씨가 나한테 배울 게 아니라.\"\n\n에이다는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 보이손 씨는 잘생긴 회색 말을 몰아 앞으로 나아가 자기 집 문 앞에서 내린 뒤, 우리가 도착하자 팔을 벌리고 모자를 벗은 채 맞이할 채비를 하고 서 있었습니다.\n\n그는 예전에 목사관이었던 아담한 집에 살았습니다. 앞에는 잔디밭이, 옆에는 화사한 꽃밭이, 뒤에는 잘 가꾸어진 과수원과 채소밭이 있었는데, 그 자체로 잘 익은 듯 불그스레한 빛이 도는 유서 깊은 담장으로 둘러싸여 있었습니다. 사실 그곳의 모든 것에는 무르익은 풍요로움이 깃들어 있었습니다. 오래된 보리수 산책로는 초록빛 회랑 같았고, 벚나무와 사과나무의 그림자마저 열매로 묵직했으며, 까치밥나무 덤불은 가지가 휘어져 땅에 닿을 만큼 열매를 가득 매달았고, 딸기와 산딸기도 그에 못지않게 무성하게 자랐으며, 복숭아는 담장가에서 수백 개씩 햇볕을 쬐고 있었습니다. 펼쳐진 그물과 햇살에 반짝거리며 깜박이는 유리 온상틀 사이로, 축 처진 꼬투리며 호박이며 오이가 어찌나 수북이 뒹굴던지, 땅 한 자 한 자가 채소 보물창고 같았고, 향긋한 풀과 온갖 싱싱한 작물의 내음은(건초를 거두어들이는 이웃 풀밭은 말할 것도 없고) 온 공기를 거대한 꽃다발로 만들어주었습니다. 오래된 붉은 담장의 정연한 경내에는 어찌나 고요한 평온이 감돌던지, 새를 쫓으려고 꽃줄처럼 매달아둔 깃털조차 거의 흔들리지 않았습니다. 게다가 담장에는 무르익게 하는 기운이 어찌나 강한지, 군데군데 높이 박힌 쓸모를 잃은 못과 끈 자투리가 아직 매달려 있는 것을 보면, 그것들마저 바뀌는 계절을 따라 무르익었고, 만물의 공통된 운명을 따라 녹슬고 삭아갔다고 상상하기 쉬웠습니다.\n\n집은 정원에 비하면 다소 어수선했지만, 벽돌 깐 부엌의 굴뚝가에 등받이 긴 의자가 놓이고 천장을 가로지르는 큼직한 들보가 있는, 진짜 오래된 집이었습니다. 그 한쪽에는 그 무시무시한 분쟁의 땅이 있었는데, 보이손 씨는 밤낮으로 작업복 차림의 보초를 세워두었고, 그 보초의 임무는 침범이 일어나면 즉시 그러려고 매달아둔 커다란 종을 울리고, 동맹군으로 개집에 둔 큰 불도그의 사슬을 풀고, 대체로 적에게 파멸을 안기는 것이라 했습니다. 이런 대비로도 만족하지 못한 보이손 씨는, 자기 이름을 큼직한 글씨로 박은 칠한 나무판에 다음과 같은 엄숙한 경고문을 손수 지어 그곳에 내걸었습니다. \"불도그를 조심하라. 매우 사나움. 로런스 보이손.\" \"나팔총에 산탄을 장전해두었음. 로런스 보이손.\" \"이곳에는 사람 잡는 덫과 용수철 총이 밤낮을 가리지 않고 설치되어 있음. 로런스 보이손.\" \"유의하라. 누구든 뻔뻔스럽게 이 영지에 무단 침입하는 자는 더없이 가혹한 사적 응징을 받고, 더없이 엄정한 법의 심판을 받을 것임. 로런스 보이손.\" 그는 객실 창문에서 이것들을 우리에게 보여주었는데, 그 사이 그의 새가 그의 머리 위에서 통통 뛰어다녔고, 그는 그것들을 가리키며 \"하하하하! 하하하하!\" 어찌나 웃어대던지, 나는 정말이지 그가 제 몸을 상하게 할까 봐 걱정했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 이건 꽤 수고스러운 일이로군요,\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 특유의 가벼운 투로 말했습니다. \"어차피 진지하게 그러는 것도 아니면서요.\"\n\n\"진지하지 않다니!\" 보이손 씨가 말로 다 못 할 열기로 대꾸했습니다. \"진지하지 않다니! 저 개를 길들일 수 있다고 기대했다면, 나는 저 개 대신 사자를 사들여서, 감히 내 권리를 침범하는 첫 번째 참을 수 없는 도둑놈에게 풀어놓았을 거요. 레스터 데드록 경이 나와서 이 문제를 일대일 결투로 판가름하겠다고만 한다면, 나는 어느 시대 어느 나라의 무기든 인류에게 알려진 무기로 그를 상대하겠소. 나는 그 정도로 진지하오. 그 이상은 아니지만!\"\n\n우리는 토요일에 그의 집에 도착했습니다. 일요일 아침 우리는 모두 영지 안의 작은 교회로 걸어가려고 길을 나섰습니다. 거의 곧장 그 분쟁의 땅을 지나 영지로 들어선 우리는, 푸릇한 잔디와 아름다운 나무들 사이로 굽이도는 기분 좋은 오솔길을 따라가 마침내 교회 현관에 다다랐습니다.\n\n신도들은 무척 적었고, 저택에서 온 하인들이 한 무리 크게 모여 있는 것을 빼면 영락없는 시골 사람들이었습니다. 하인들 가운데 일부는 이미 자리에 앉아 있었고, 더러는 아직 하나둘 들어오고 있었습니다. 위풍당당한 종복들이 몇 있었고, 그림 같은 늙은 마부가 하나 있었는데, 마치 자기 마차에 실린 적이 있는 온갖 허세와 허영의 공식 대표라도 되는 듯한 모습이었습니다. 젊은 여자들도 무척 어여쁘게 늘어서 있었고, 그들 위로 가정부의 잘생긴 늙은 얼굴과 듬직하고 위엄 있는 풍채가 단연 도드라졌습니다. 보이손 씨가 우리에게 말했던 그 예쁜 처녀가 가정부 바로 곁에 있었습니다. 그녀는 어찌나 예쁘던지, 멀지 않은 곳에 있던 젊은 낚시꾼의 시선을 그녀가 얼마나 부끄럽게 의식하는지 보지 못했더라도 그 아름다움만으로 알아볼 수 있었을 것입니다. 한 얼굴이, 잘생기긴 했지만 그리 호감 가지 않는 한 얼굴이, 이 예쁜 처녀를, 아니 사실은 그곳의 모든 사람과 모든 것을 심술궂게 감시하는 듯했습니다. 어느 프랑스 여자의 얼굴이었습니다.\n\n종이 아직 울리고 있고 그 귀하신 분들이 아직 오지 않았기에, 나는 한가로이 교회를 둘러보았습니다. 무덤처럼 흙냄새가 풍기는 교회였는데, 얼마나 그늘지고 오래되고 엄숙한 작은 교회인지 새삼 느꼈습니다. 나무에 짙게 가려진 창문은 은은한 빛만 들이는데, 그 빛이 내 둘레의 얼굴들을 창백하게 만들고, 포석에 박힌 오래된 놋쇠 명패와 세월과 습기에 닳은 기념비를 어둑하게 가렸으며, 단조롭게 종을 치는 사람이 일하고 있는 작은 현관의 햇살을 더없이 눈부시게 만들었습니다. 그러나 그쪽에서 술렁임이 일고, 시골 사람들의 얼굴에 경건한 외경이 차오르고, 보이손 씨가 누군가의 존재를 단호히 모르는 척하겠다는 듯 점잖으면서도 사나운 태도를 짓는 것을 보고, 나는 그 귀하신 분들이 도착했으며 예배가 곧 시작되리라는 것을 미리 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"'주여, 당신의 종을 심판하지 마옵소서, 당신 앞에서는—'\"\n\n자리에서 일어섰을 때 마주친 그 눈길이 내 가슴을 얼마나 빠르게 뛰게 했는지, 내가 과연 잊을 수 있을까요! 그 잘생기고 오만한 두 눈이 나른함을 떨치고 솟구쳐 나와 내 눈을 붙드는 듯하던 그 모양을, 내가 과연 잊을 수 있을까요! 내가 다시 눈을 떨구어—말하자면 다시 풀려나서—책으로 시선을 돌리기까지는 한순간뿐이었지만, 그 짧은 시간에 나는 그 아름다운 얼굴을 아주 또렷이 알아보았습니다.\n\n그리고 무척 이상하게도, 내 안에서 무언가가 깨어났는데, 그것은 대모님 집에서 보낸 외로운 나날과 이어져 있었습니다. 그렇습니다, 인형에게 옷을 입힌 다음 작은 거울 앞에서 발끝으로 서서 내 옷을 입던 그 시절까지 거슬러 올라가는 것이었습니다. 그런데도 나는 이 마님의 얼굴을 평생 단 한 번도 본 적이 없었습니다—나는 그것을 확신했고—완전히 확신했습니다.\n\n격식 차린 그 통풍 걸린 백발의 신사가, 그 커다란 신도석의 또 다른 단 한 사람이, 레스터 데드록 경이고, 그 마님이 데드록 마님이라는 것은 쉽게 알 수 있었습니다. 그런데 어째서 그 얼굴이 내게 깨진 거울처럼, 옛 기억의 조각들이 비치는 그런 것으로 어렴풋이 다가오는지, 또 어째서 우연히 그 눈과 마주친 일만으로 내가 (여전히 그러했듯이) 그토록 가슴이 두근거리고 마음이 어지러운지, 나는 도무지 알 수가 없었습니다.\n\n나는 그것을 까닭 모를 내 약점으로 여기고, 내가 듣고 있는 말씀에 마음을 기울여 그 약점을 이겨내려 했습니다. 그러자, 무척 이상하게도, 그 말씀이 낭독하는 이의 목소리가 아니라 또렷이 기억나는 대모님의 목소리로 들리는 듯했습니다. 그래서 나는 생각했습니다, 데드록 마님의 얼굴이 우연히 대모님의 얼굴과 닮았던 걸까? 어쩌면 조금은 닮았는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 그 표정이 너무나 달랐고, 비바람이 바위에 새겨지듯 대모님의 얼굴에 새겨졌던 그 준엄한 단호함이 내 앞의 얼굴에는 깡그리 없었으니, 나를 사로잡은 것이 그런 닮음일 리는 없었습니다. 게다가 데드록 마님 얼굴의 그 도도함과 거만함은, 내가 아는 그 누구에게서도 본 적이 없었습니다. 그런데도 나는—나, 외따로 떨어진 삶을 살았고 생일이 와도 누구 하나 기뻐해주지 않던 아이, 어린 에스더 서머슨이—이 멋쟁이 마님에게 깃든 어떤 힘에 이끌려 과거에서 불려 나와, 내 눈앞에 떠오르는 듯했습니다. 그 마님을 예전에 본 적이 있다는 환상은 조금도 품지 않았을뿐더러, 그 시각까지 결단코 본 적이 없다는 것을 나는 완벽하게 알고 있었는데도 말입니다.\n\n이 까닭 모를 동요에 빠져 어찌나 떨렸던지, 나는 그 프랑스 하녀의 시선에마저 괴로움을 느꼈습니다. 그녀가 교회에 들어선 순간부터 여기저기, 사방을 경계하듯 살피고 있었다는 것을 알면서도 말입니다. 무척 더디기는 했지만, 차츰차츰 나는 마침내 그 이상한 감정을 이겨냈습니다. 한참이 지나서야 나는 다시 데드록 마님 쪽을 바라보았습니다. 설교 전, 찬송을 부르려고 준비하던 무렵이었습니다. 마님은 내게 아무 관심도 두지 않았고, 내 가슴의 두근거림도 사라졌습니다. 그 뒤로 마님이 한두 번 오페라글라스로 에이다나 나를 흘끗 보았을 때도, 그 두근거림은 잠깐을 넘어 되살아나지 않았습니다.\n\n예배가 끝나자, 레스터 경은 짙은 지팡이에 의지해 걸어야 했는데도, 무척 멋스럽고 정중하게 데드록 마님에게 팔을 내주어 그녀를 교회 밖, 그들이 타고 온 조랑말 마차까지 모셨습니다. 그러자 하인들이 흩어졌고, 신도들도 흩어졌습니다. 레스터 경은 (스킴폴 씨가 보이손 씨가 더없이 좋아하라고 한 말처럼) 마치 자신이 천국의 상당한 토지를 소유한 지주라도 되는 양 줄곧 그 신도들을 굽어보고 있었습니다.\n\n\"저 사람은 정말 그런 줄 안다니까!\" 보이손 씨가 말했습니다. \"철석같이 믿어. 그 아비도 그랬고, 할아비도, 증조할아비도 그랬지!\"\n\n\"그거 아십니까,\" 스킴폴 씨가 보이손 씨에게 무척 뜻밖의 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"나는 저런 부류의 사람을 보는 게 흐뭇하답니다.\"\n\n\"흐뭇하다고요!\" 보이손 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"저자가 나를 너그러이 후원하려 든다고 칩시다,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말을 이었습니다. \"아주 좋아요! 나는 마다하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 마다하겠소,\" 보이손 씨가 무척 기세 좋게 말했습니다.\n\n\"정말로요?\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 느긋하고 가벼운 투로 대꾸했습니다. \"하지만 그건 분명 수고스러운 일입니다. 한데 왜 굳이 수고를 사서 합니까? 보세요, 나는 일이 닥치는 대로 어린아이처럼 받아들이는 데 만족하고, 결코 수고를 사서 하지 않습니다! 이를테면 나는 여기 내려와서, 한 위대한 권세가가 경의를 요구하는 것을 봅니다. 아주 좋아요! 나는 말하지요. '위대한 권세가여, 여기 내 경의를 바치오! 바치는 게 거두는 것보다 쉬우니까요. 자, 여기 있소. 내게 보여줄 흐뭇한 것이 있다면, 나는 기꺼이 보겠소. 내게 줄 흐뭇한 것이 있다면, 나는 기꺼이 받겠소.' 그러면 위대한 권세가는 사실상 이렇게 답하지요. '이자는 분별 있는 친구로군. 내 소화와 담즙 체질에 잘 맞는단 말이야. 이자는 나로 하여금 고슴도치처럼 가시를 밖으로 곤두세워 몸을 둘둘 말 필요를 안기지 않는군. 나는 활짝 펴고, 마음을 열고, 밀턴의 구름처럼 은빛 안감을 밖으로 뒤집어 보이니, 둘 다에게 더 흐뭇하지 않은가.' 그게 그런 일들에 대한 내 견해입니다. 어린아이로서 하는 말이지요!\"\n\n\"하지만 자네가 내일 어디 다른 데로 내려갔는데,\" 보이손 씨가 말했습니다. \"거기엔 저자와—혹은 이 사람과—정반대인 자가 있다고 칩시다. 그럼 어쩌겠나?\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩌겠느냐고요?\" 스킴폴 씨가 더없이 천진하고 솔직한 얼굴로 말했습니다. \"그때도 똑같지요! 나는 이렇게 말할 겁니다. '나의 존경하는 보이손'—자네를 우리의 가상 친구로 삼아서—'나의 존경하는 보이손, 자네는 그 위대한 권세가가 못마땅한가? 아주 좋아. 나도 그렇다네. 사회 체계 안에서 내 할 일은 흐뭇한 사람이 되는 것이라고 나는 생각하네. 사회 체계 안에서 누구나 할 일은 흐뭇한 사람이 되는 것이라고 나는 생각하네. 요컨대 조화의 체계지. 그러니 자네가 못마땅하면, 나도 못마땅하다네. 자, 훌륭한 보이손, 우리 저녁 먹으러 가세!'\"\n\n\"하지만 훌륭한 보이손은 이렇게 말할지도 모르지,\" 우리 주인은 부풀어 오르며 얼굴이 새빨개져서 대꾸했습니다. \"내가 절대—\"\n\n\"알겠습니다,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그럴 법하군요.\"\n\n\"—저녁을 먹으러 갈 줄 아느냐고!\" 보이손 씨가 격하게 터뜨리며 외쳤고, 걸음을 멈추고 지팡이로 땅을 내리쳤습니다. \"그러고는 아마 이렇게 덧붙이겠지. '원칙이라는 게 있긴 한가, 해럴드 스킴폴 씨?'\"\n\n\"거기에 해럴드 스킴폴은 이렇게 대답하겠지요,\" 그는 더없이 명랑한 태도로, 더없이 천연덕스러운 미소를 지으며 대꾸했습니다. \"'맹세코 나는 도무지 모르겠소이다! 당신이 그 이름으로 부르는 것이 무엇인지, 어디 있는지, 누가 가졌는지 나는 모릅니다. 당신이 그것을 가졌고 편안하다면, 나는 더없이 기쁘고 진심으로 축하드립니다. 하지만 장담컨대 나는 그것에 관해 아무것도 모릅니다. 나는 한낱 어린아이이고, 그것을 주장하지도 않으며, 원하지도 않으니까요!' 그러니 보시다시피, 결국 훌륭한 보이손과 나는 저녁을 먹으러 갈 겁니다!\"\n\n이것은 두 사람 사이의 자잘한 대화 가운데 하나였는데, 나는 그 대화가 늘 우리 주인 쪽에서 격렬하게 폭발하는 것으로 끝나리라 예상했고, 다른 상황이었다면 정말 그렇게 끝났으리라 봅니다. 하지만 그는 우리를 대접하는 주인으로서 자신의 후하고 책임 있는 자리를 무척 소중히 여겼고, 후견인은 온종일 거품을 불었다 터뜨리는 어린아이 같은 스킴폴 씨를 두고, 또 그와 함께 어찌나 진심으로 웃었던지, 사태는 결코 이 지점을 넘어서지 않았습니다. 자신이 위태로운 땅을 밟고 있었다는 것을 전혀 의식하지 못하는 듯한 스킴폴 씨는, 그러고 나면 영지에서 끝내 완성하지 못할 어떤 스케치를 시작하거나, 피아노로 곡조의 단편들을 치거나, 노래 조각을 흥얼거리거나, 나무 아래 등을 대고 누워 하늘을 바라보았습니다. 그는 자기가 바로 이러려고 태어난 모양이라고, 이게 자기한테 너무도 꼭 맞는다며 그런 생각을 떨칠 수 없다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"모험과 노력이란,\" 그는 (드러누운 채) 우리에게 말하곤 했습니다. \"내게는 흐뭇한 것들이지요. 나는 내가 진정한 세계주의자라고 믿습니다. 나는 그런 것들에 더없이 깊은 공감을 품고 있어요. 나는 이런 그늘진 곳에 누워서, 북극으로 가거나 열대의 한복판을 파고드는 모험심 넘치는 영혼들을 흠모하며 떠올립니다. 돈벌레 같은 작자들은 묻지요. '사람이 북극에 가는 게 무슨 소용이람? 그게 무슨 득이 돼?' 나는 모릅니다. 하지만 내가 말할 수 있는 한, 그 사람은 어쩌면—본인은 모르겠지만—내가 여기 누워 떠올릴 거리를 마련해주려고 가는 것인지도 모르지요. 극단적인 경우를 들어봅시다. 아메리카 농장의 노예들을 예로 들지요. 분명 그들은 고되게 부려질 테고, 분명 그걸 썩 좋아하진 않을 겁니다. 분명 그들의 처지는 전체로 보면 유쾌하지 못한 경험이겠지요. 하지만 그들은 내게 풍경을 채워주고, 내게 시정을 안겨주니, 어쩌면 그것이 그들 존재의 더 즐거운 목적 가운데 하나인지도 모릅니다. 만약 그렇다면 나는 그것을 무척 잘 느끼는 사람이고, 정말 그렇다 해도 놀랍지 않을 겁니다!\"\n\n이런 자리에서 나는 늘 궁금했습니다. 그가 스킴폴 부인과 아이들을 한 번이라도 떠올린 적이 있을까, 그들이 그의 세계주의적인 머릿속에 어떤 모습으로 나타날까. 내가 이해한 바로는, 그들은 거의 떠오르지 않는 듯했습니다.\n\n교회에서 내 가슴이 그렇게 두근거린 날로부터, 한 주가 돌아 그다음 토요일에 이르렀습니다. 날마다 어찌나 화창하고 푸르렀던지, 숲속을 거닐며 투명한 잎새들 사이로 빛이 내리꽂혀 나무 그림자가 아름답게 얽힌 틈에서 반짝이는 것을 보고, 새들이 노래를 쏟아내고 공기가 벌레들의 윙윙거림으로 나른해지는 것을 느끼는 일은, 더없이 흐뭇했습니다. 우리에게는 즐겨 찾는 한 곳이 있었습니다. 이끼와 지난해 낙엽이 깊이 깔린 곳으로, 껍질이 죄다 벗겨진 채 베어진 나무 몇 그루가 있었습니다. 그 사이에 앉아서, 우리는 수천 개의 자연 기둥, 곧 허옇게 바랜 나무줄기들이 떠받친 초록빛 회랑 너머로, 멀리 펼쳐진 풍경을 바라보았습니다. 그 풍경은 우리가 앉은 그늘과 대비되어 더없이 눈부셨고, 우리가 그것을 바라보는 아치형 원근 너머의 정경이 너무도 귀해서, 마치 더 나은 세상을 언뜻 엿보는 것 같았습니다. 그 토요일에 우리는—잔다이스 씨와 에이다와 나는—멀리서 천둥이 우르릉대는 소리가 들리고 큼직한 빗방울이 잎새를 후드득 때리는 것을 느낄 때까지 그곳에 앉아 있었습니다.\n\n그 한 주 내내 날씨가 무척 후텁지근했는데, 폭풍은 어찌나 갑자기 들이닥쳤던지—적어도 그 아늑한 자리에 있던 우리에게는—숲 가장자리에 채 이르기도 전에 천둥과 번개가 잦아지고, 비가 마치 한 방울 한 방울이 커다란 납구슬이라도 되는 듯 잎새를 뚫고 내리꽂혔습니다. 나무 사이에 서 있을 때가 아니었기에, 우리는 숲에서 뛰쳐나와, 마치 등을 맞댄 살이 넓은 사다리 두 개처럼 농장 울타리를 가로지른 이끼 낀 계단을 오르내려, 바로 가까이에 있던 사냥터지기의 오두막으로 향했습니다. 우리는 나무들의 짙은 어스름 속에 서 있는 이 오두막의 어두운 아름다움을, 담쟁이가 그 위로 휘감긴 모습을, 그리고 근처의 가파른 우묵한 골짜기—언젠가 사냥터지기의 개가 마치 물속으로 뛰어들 듯 고사리 속으로 곤두박질치는 것을 본 적 있는 그곳을—종종 눈여겨보았습니다.\n\n하늘이 잔뜩 흐려진 지금, 오두막 안은 어찌나 어둡던지, 우리가 그곳에 피신하자 문간으로 나와 에이다와 나에게 의자 두 개를 놓아준 남자만이 또렷이 보였습니다. 격자창은 죄다 활짝 열려 있었고, 우리는 문간 바로 안쪽에 앉아 폭풍을 지켜보았습니다. 바람이 깨어나 나무들을 휘고 빗줄기를 연기 구름처럼 몰아가는 모습을 보고, 엄숙한 천둥소리를 듣고 번개를 보는 일은 장엄했습니다. 그리고 우리의 작은 목숨을 둘러싼 그 어마어마한 힘들을 외경에 차 떠올리면서도, 그 힘들이 얼마나 자비로운지를, 또 이 겉보기 광포함에서 쏟아져 나온 싱그러움이 어떻게 가장 작은 꽃과 잎새에마저 벌써 어려, 만물을 다시 새롭게 만드는 듯한지를 헤아리는 일도 장엄했습니다.\n\"이렇게 트인 곳에 앉아 있으면 위험하지 않나요?\"\n\n\"어머, 아니야, 에스더, 우리 에스더!\" 에이다가 조용히 말했습니다.\n\n에이다는 내게 그 말을 건넸지만, 정작 말을 한 건 내가 아니었습니다.\n\n내 가슴의 두근거림이 다시 찾아왔습니다. 그 얼굴을 본 적이 없듯 나는 그 목소리도 들은 적이 없었지만, 그것은 똑같이 이상한 방식으로 나를 흔들었습니다. 또다시, 한순간에, 내 마음속에 나 자신의 무수한 모습이 떠올랐습니다.\n\n데드록 마님은 우리가 도착하기 전에 그 오두막에 피신해 있다가, 안쪽의 어둠 속에서 걸어 나온 것이었습니다. 그녀는 내 의자 뒤에 서서 의자에 손을 얹고 있었습니다. 고개를 돌리자, 그녀의 손이 내 어깨 가까이에 있는 것이 보였습니다.\n\n\"내가 놀라게 했나요?\" 그녀가 말했습니다.\n\n아니었습니다. 놀란 것이 아니었습니다. 내가 왜 놀라겠습니까!\n\n\"제가 지금,\" 데드록 마님이 후견인에게 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 씨와 이야기하는 기쁨을 누리고 있는 듯하군요.\"\n\n\"저를 기억해주시다니, 생각했던 것보다 더한 영광입니다, 데드록 마님,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"일요일에 교회에서 당신을 알아보았어요. 레스터 경의 지역 분쟁이—하긴 그분이 일부러 벌인 일은 아니라고 알고 있습니다만—이곳에서 당신께 조금이라도 예를 갖추는 일을 그토록 우스꽝스럽게 어렵게 만든다니 유감입니다.\"\n\n\"그 사정은 잘 알고 있습니다,\" 후견인이 미소 지으며 대꾸했습니다. \"그리고 그것만으로 충분히 황송합니다.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀었는데, 그녀에게 몸에 밴 듯한 무심한 태도였고, 말투도 그에 어울리게 무심했지만, 목소리는 무척 듣기 좋았습니다. 그녀는 아름다운 만큼 우아했고, 완벽하게 침착했으며, 마음만 먹는다면 누구든 끌어당기고 흥미를 불러일으킬 수 있는 분위기를 지녔다고 나는 생각했습니다. 사냥터지기가 의자를 가져다주자, 그녀는 우리 사이, 현관 한가운데에 앉았습니다.\n\n\"당신이 레스터 경에게 편지로 부탁했던, 그리고 레스터 경이 어떤 식으로도 그 바람을 돕지 못해 안타까워했던 그 젊은이는 자리를 잡았나요?\" 그녀가 어깨너머로 후견인에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"그러길 바랍니다,\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n그녀는 그를 존중하는 듯했고, 심지어 그의 마음을 누그러뜨리고 싶어 하는 것 같았습니다. 그녀의 도도한 태도에는 무척 매혹적인 데가 있었고, 어깨너머로 그에게 말을 건넬수록 그 태도는 한층 친근해졌습니다—한층 편안해졌다고 말하려 했지만, 그건 도무지 어울리지 않는 표현이겠지요.\n\n\"이쪽은 당신의 또 다른 피후견인, 클레어 양인가요?\"\n\n그는 격식을 갖춰 에이다를 소개했습니다.\n\n\"이렇게 아름다운 이의 억울함만 바로잡아주신다면, 당신의 돈키호테다운 면모에서 사심 없는 부분은 잃게 되실 거예요,\" 데드록 마님이 다시 어깨너머로 잔다이스 씨에게 말했습니다. \"하지만 이 아가씨도,\" 그녀가 내게로 온전히 몸을 돌렸습니다. \"제게 소개해주세요!\"\n\n\"서머슨 양은 진짜로 제 피후견인입니다,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 아이에 관해서라면 저는 어떤 대법관에게도 책임질 일이 없지요.\"\n\n\"서머슨 양은 부모를 모두 잃었나요?\" 마님이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"후견인을 참 잘 만났군요.\"\n\n데드록 마님이 나를 바라보았고, 나도 그녀를 바라보며 정말 그렇다고 말했습니다. 그러자 그녀는 거의 못마땅함이나 싫증을 드러내듯 황급한 태도로 내게서 홱 돌아서더니, 다시 어깨너머로 그에게 말을 건넸습니다.\n\n\"우리가 만나곤 하던 때로부터 여러 세월이 흘렀군요, 잔다이스 씨.\"\n\n\"오랜 세월이지요. 적어도 지난 일요일에 당신을 뵙기 전까지는 오랜 세월이라고 여겼습니다,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"아니! 당신마저 아첨꾼이로군요, 아니면 제게 그래야 한다고 여기시거나!\" 그녀가 다소 경멸하듯 말했습니다. \"제가 그런 평판을 얻은 모양이지요, 아마.\"\n\n\"당신은 너무도 많은 것을 이루셨으니, 데드록 마님,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그 대가를 얼마쯤 치르시는 거겠지요, 아마. 하지만 제게 치르실 건 없습니다.\"\n\n\"너무도 많은 것을!\" 그녀가 가볍게 웃으며 되뇌었습니다. \"그렇죠!\"\n\n우월함과 권력과 매혹과, 그밖에 무어라 할 수 없는 분위기로, 그녀는 에이다와 나를 어린아이쯤으로밖에 여기지 않는 듯했습니다. 그래서 그녀는 가볍게 웃은 다음 앉아서 비를 바라보면서, 마치 혼자 있는 양 침착하게, 거리낌 없이 제 생각에 잠겼습니다.\n\n\"우리가 함께 외국에 있을 때, 당신은 저보다 제 언니를 더 잘 알았던 것 같은데요?\" 그녀가 다시 그를 바라보며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 그쪽과는 더 자주 만나게 되었지요,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"우리는 각자의 길을 갔어요,\" 데드록 마님이 말했습니다. \"달리 살기로 마음먹기 전부터도 공통점이 거의 없었죠. 유감스러운 일이겠지만, 어쩔 수 없는 일이었어요.\"\n\n데드록 마님은 다시 앉아 비를 바라보았습니다. 폭풍은 곧 제 갈 길로 지나가기 시작했습니다. 빗줄기가 크게 잦아들고, 번개가 멎고, 천둥이 먼 언덕들 사이로 굴러갔으며, 햇살이 젖은 잎새와 떨어지는 빗방울 위에서 반짝이기 시작했습니다. 우리가 그곳에 말없이 앉아 있는데, 작은 조랑말 마차 한 대가 경쾌한 속도로 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 것이 보였습니다.\n\n\"심부름꾼이 마차를 가지고 돌아오는군요, 마님,\" 사냥터지기가 말했습니다.\n\n마차가 다가오자, 그 안에 두 사람이 타고 있는 것이 보였습니다. 망토며 덮개를 들고 마차에서 내린 것은, 먼저 교회에서 보았던 그 프랑스 여자였고, 다음으로 그 예쁜 처녀였습니다. 프랑스 여자는 도전하듯 당당했고, 예쁜 처녀는 어쩔 줄 몰라 머뭇거렸습니다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이지?\" 데드록 마님이 말했습니다. \"둘이나!\"\n\n\"지금은 제가 마님의 시중을 들고 있습니다, 마님,\" 프랑스 여자가 말했습니다. \"전갈은 시중드는 사람에게 온 것이었어요.\"\n\n\"마님께서 저를 부르시는 줄 알고 걱정했어요, 마님,\" 예쁜 처녀가 말했습니다.\n\n\"내가 부른 게 맞다, 얘야,\" 그녀의 주인이 차분히 대꾸했습니다. \"이 숄을 둘러다오.\"\n\n그녀는 그것을 받으려고 어깨를 살짝 숙였고, 예쁜 처녀가 그것을 가뜬히 제자리에 걸쳐주었습니다. 프랑스 여자는 본체만체 무시당한 채, 입술을 아주 앙다물고 지켜보고 서 있었습니다.\n\n\"우리가 옛 친분을 다시 잇기는 어려울 듯하여 유감입니다,\" 데드록 마님이 잔다이스 씨에게 말했습니다. \"두 피후견인을 위해 마차를 다시 돌려보내드리도록 허락해주세요. 곧장 이리로 보내겠습니다.\"\n\n하지만 그가 결단코 그 제안을 받아들이지 않았기에, 그녀는 에이다에게 우아하게 작별을 고하고—내게는 한마디도 없이—그가 내민 팔에 손을 얹고서, 덮개가 달린 자그마하고 나지막한 영지용 마차에 올라탔습니다.\n\n\"타거라, 얘야,\" 그녀가 예쁜 처녀에게 말했습니다. \"네가 필요할 테니. 출발!\"\n\n마차가 굴러가 버리자, 프랑스 여자는 가져왔던 덮개를 팔에 걸친 채, 내렸던 자리에 그대로 서 있었습니다.\n\n자존심이 가장 견디지 못하는 것은 다름 아닌 자존심 자체인가 봅니다. 그녀는 그 오만한 태도에 대한 벌을 받은 셈이었습니다. 그녀의 보복은 내가 상상할 수 있었던 가장 기이한 것이었습니다. 그녀는 마차가 진입로로 돌아 들어갈 때까지 완벽하게 미동도 없이 서 있다가, 이윽고 얼굴빛 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않은 채, 신발을 벗어 땅에 버려두고는, 흠뻑 젖은 풀밭 가운데서도 가장 젖은 곳을 골라 같은 방향으로 또박또박 걸어갔습니다.\n\n\"저 젊은 여자가 미쳤소?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 나리!\" 아내와 함께 그녀를 지켜보던 사냥터지기가 말했습니다. \"오르탕스는 그런 부류가 아니에요. 머리는 누구 못지않게 좋답니다. 하지만 무섭게 콧대 높고 격정적이지요—지독하게 콧대 높고 격정적이에요. 게다가 나가라는 통보를 받은 데다 남들이 자기 위로 올라서니, 그걸 곱게 받아들이지 못하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 어째서 저 물을 죄다 맨발로 걸어간단 말이오?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리, 머리라도 식히려는 게 아니라면 모르겠습니다요!\" 남자가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니면 저게 피라고 여기는 게지요,\" 아내가 말했습니다. \"성질이 돋으면, 저런 물쯤은 다른 무엇이라도 그렇듯 거침없이 밟고 갈 사람이에요!\"\n\n몇 분 뒤 우리는 저택에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳을 지났습니다. 처음 보았을 때도 평화로웠지만, 지금은 한층 더 평화로워 보였습니다. 다이아몬드 같은 물방울이 사방에서 반짝이고, 산들바람이 불고, 새들은 더는 잠잠하지 않고 힘차게 노래했으며, 모든 것이 막 지나간 비에 싱그러워졌고, 문간의 작은 마차는 은으로 빚은 요정의 마차처럼 빛나고 있었습니다. 그래도, 더없이 한결같고 조용하게 그 마차를 향해 걸어가는, 풍경 속의 또 하나의 평화로운 형상—마드무아젤 오르탕스가, 신발을 벗은 채, 젖은 풀밭을 걸어가고 있었습니다.\n\n챈서리 레인 일대는 장기 휴정에 들어섰다. 보통법과 형평법이라는 두 척의 멋진 배, 티크로 짜고 밑바닥에 구리를 대고 쇠못으로 단단히 박고 뱃머리에 놋쇠를 둘렀으나 결코 빠른 항해와는 거리가 먼 그 쾌속 범선들은 닻을 내린 채 묶여 있다. 마주치는 사람마다 붙들고 제발 자기네 서류 좀 읽어봐 달라고 애원하는 유령 같은 의뢰인들을 태운 방황하는 네덜란드인 유령선은, 당분간 어디로 떠내려갔는지 하늘만이 안다. 법정은 죄다 문을 닫았고, 관청들은 후텁지근한 잠에 빠져 있다. 웨스트민스터 홀마저 나이팅게일이 노래해도 좋을 그늘진 적막이 되어, 그곳에서 흔히 보던 부류보다 한결 부드러운 소송인들이 거니는 곳이 되었다.\n\n템플과 챈서리 레인, 변호사 회관, 링컨 법학원과 그 너머 들판까지, 모두 썰물 때의 항구를 닮았다. 좌초한 소송들과 닻을 내린 사무실들, 개정기의 물살이 밀려들기 전까지는 결코 똑바로 서지 못할 한쪽으로 기우뚱한 의자에 늘어진 채 빈둥대는 서기들이, 장기 휴정의 진창 위에 높직이 마른 채로 뒹군다. 사무실 바깥문은 수십 개씩 잠겨 있고, 전갈과 소포는 수위실에 한 무더기씩 맡겨진다. 링컨 법학원 회관 밖 돌바닥 틈새에서 풀이 한 무더기 자라날 법도 하건만, 그늘에 앉아 파리를 쫓느라 하얀 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓴 것 말고는 할 일이 없는 짐꾼들이 그 풀을 뽑아 골똘히 씹어먹는 통에 그러지도 못한다.\n\n읍내에 판사라곤 단 한 사람뿐이다. 그마저도 일주일에 두 번 사실에 나와 앉을 뿐이다. 그가 순회하는 순회재판 읍의 시골 사람들이 지금 그 모습을 본다면 어떨까! 머리 전체를 덮는 가발도 없고, 붉은 법복도, 모피도, 창을 든 호위병도, 흰 지팡이도 없다. 그저 흰 바지에 흰 모자를 쓴, 말끔히 면도한 신사일 뿐이다. 판사다운 얼굴은 바닷바람에 그을렸고, 판사다운 코에서는 햇볕에 익은 살갗이 한 꺼풀 벗겨졌으며, 오는 길에 조개구이 가게에 들러 얼음 넣은 진저비어를 들이켜는 사람이다!\n\n잉글랜드의 법조계는 온 지상에 흩어졌다. 역경에 처했을 때 공인된 피난처요 번영할 때 유일하게 정당한 승리인 그 법조계 없이, 잉글랜드가 긴 여름철 넉 달을 어떻게 견디는가 하는 것은 따로 따져볼 문제다. 다만 브리타니아의 그 방패와 작은 방패가 지금은 쓰이지 않고 있는 것만은 분명하다. 상대편이 의뢰인의 감정을 전례 없이 짓밟았다며 늘 어마어마하게 분개하여 도무지 회복할 가망이 없어 보이던 그 학식 높은 신사는, 스위스에서 기대 이상으로 훨씬 잘 지내고 있다. 음울한 빈정거림으로 모든 적수를 시들게 하는 일을 도맡던 학식 높은 신사는, 프랑스의 어느 온천 휴양지에서 귀뚜라미처럼 흥겹다. 사소한 자극에도 한 통씩 눈물을 쏟던 학식 높은 신사는, 이 여섯 주 동안 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않았다. 생강빛으로 불그레한 안색의 타고난 열기를 법의 웅덩이와 샘물에 식혀, 개정기에 쓸 까다로운 논변의 대가가 된 그 무척 학식 높은 신사는—문외한은 물론이고 그 길에 들어선 사람조차 대부분 알아듣지 못할 법률 헛소리로 졸음에 겨운 판사석을 곤혹스럽게 만드는 그 사람은—메마름과 먼지에 대한 특유의 취향에 흡족해하며 콘스탄티노플 일대를 떠돈다. 같은 위대한 수호상의 나머지 흩어진 조각들은 베네치아의 운하에서, 나일강의 둘째 폭포에서, 독일의 온천에서, 그리고 잉글랜드 해안 곳곳의 모래밭에 흩뿌려진 채 찾아볼 수 있다. 인적 끊긴 챈서리 레인 지대에서는 그런 사람 하나 마주치기도 어렵다. 그렇게 외로운 법조인 하나가 그 황무지를 가로질러 어른거리다가, 제 불안의 현장을 떠나지 못해 어슬렁대는 소송인과 맞닥뜨리기라도 하면, 둘은 서로 화들짝 놀라 반대편 그늘로 물러나고 만다.\n\n여러 해 만에 가장 무더운 장기 휴정이다. 젊은 서기들은 죄다 미친 듯이 사랑에 빠져, 저마다 정도는 다르나 마게이트에서, 램스게이트에서, 그레이브젠드에서 연인과 누릴 행복을 애타게 그린다. 중년의 서기들은 하나같이 제 식구가 너무 많다고 여긴다. 법학원 안으로 흘러들어 계단이며 물기 없는 구석을 헐떡이며 물을 찾아 헤매는 주인 없는 개들은 죄다 짤막하게 짜증 섞인 울음을 토한다. 거리의 맹인 안내견들은 죄다 주인을 펌프 쪽으로 끌고 가거나 양동이에 걸려 넘어지게 한다. 차양을 치고 보도에 물을 뿌리고 진열창에 금붕어 은붕어 담은 어항을 놓아둔 가게는 하나의 성소다. 템플 바는 어찌나 뜨거운지, 인접한 스트랜드와 플리트 거리에는 마치 끓이는 항아리 속 데우개 같아서, 밤새도록 그곳을 보글보글 끓게 만든다.\n\n법학원 일대에는 둔하기 짝이 없는 그런 대가를 치르고서라도 시원함을 살 만하다면 시원하게 지낼 수 있는 사무실도 더러 있다. 하지만 그 은둔처 바로 바깥의 좁은 골목길들은 활활 타오르는 듯하다. 크룩 씨네 마당은 어찌나 더운지 사람들이 집 안을 통째로 밖으로 뒤집어내고는 보도에 의자를 놓고 앉아 있다—크룩 씨도 그 가운데 하나로, 거기서 (더위라곤 모르는) 고양이를 곁에 두고 연구에 몰두한다. 솔스 암스는 이번 철의 화음 모임을 중단했고, 리틀 스윌스는 강 하류의 목가 정원에 출연 계약을 맺어, 거기서 자못 천진한 태도로 나와서는 (전단지에 적힌 대로) 아무리 까다로운 사람의 감정도 상하지 않게끔 어린아이 같은 우스개 노래를 부른다.\n\n법조계 동네 전체에는, 거대한 녹의 장막이나 어마어마한 거미줄처럼, 장기 휴정의 나른함과 시름이 드리워 있다. 커시터 거리 쿡스 코트의 법률 문방구 주인 스내그스비 씨는, 다정하고 사색을 즐기는 사람으로서의 마음으로도 그렇거니와, 앞서 말한 법률 문방구 주인으로서의 장사로도 그 영향을 절감한다. 그는 장기 휴정 동안 스테이플 법학원과 롤스 야드에서 다른 철보다 한가로이 사색에 잠길 여유가 더 많아져서, 두 도제에게 이런 무더운 날씨에 사방으로 바다가 출렁이며 굽이도는 섬에 살고 있다고 생각하면 참 묘한 일이라고 말한다.\n\n장기 휴정의 어느 오후, 스내그스비 부부가 손님을 맞을 작정인 오늘, 거스터는 자그마한 응접실에서 분주하다. 초대받은 손님은 수가 많다기보다는 자못 엄선된 분들로, 채드밴드 부부 두 사람뿐이다. 채드밴드 씨는 말로도 글로도 자기 자신을 곧잘 그릇이라 칭하는 까닭에, 이따금 낯선 사람들에게 항해와 관련된 신사로 오해받기도 한다. 하지만 그는 자기 표현을 빌리자면 \"성직에 몸담은\" 사람이다. 채드밴드 씨는 어느 특정 교파에도 속해 있지 않으며, 그를 헐뜯는 자들은 그가 자청해서 나설 만큼 양심상 마땅히 그래야 할 정도로 가장 위대한 주제에 관해 그리 대단할 것 없는 이야기를 하는 사람이라고 여긴다. 그래도 그를 따르는 무리가 있으니, 스내그스비 부인도 그 가운데 하나다. 스내그스비 부인은 최근에야 채드밴드라는 배에 올라 위로 향하는 항로에 몸을 실었다. 무더운 날씨에 얼마간 얼굴이 달아올랐을 무렵, 그녀의 주의가 그 일등급 범선에 끌렸던 것이다.\n\n\"우리 마누라는 말이지,\" 스내그스비 씨가 스테이플 법학원의 참새들에게 말한다. \"종교를 좀 칼칼한 걸로 좋아한단 말씀이야, 그렇다니까!\"\n\n그리하여 거스터는, 한 번에 네 시간씩 내리 설교를 늘어놓는 재주를 타고난 줄 익히 아는 채드밴드의 하녀로 잠시 자신을 여기는 일에 자못 감격하여, 차 마실 자그마한 응접실을 채비한다. 가구는 죄다 털고 닦았고, 스내그스비 부부의 초상화는 젖은 천으로 매만졌으며, 가장 좋은 다기 한 벌이 차려졌다. 그리고 보드라운 새 빵, 바삭한 꽈배기 빵, 시원하고 신선한 버터, 얇게 저민 햄과 우설과 독일 소시지, 그리고 파슬리에 폭 파묻힌 멸치젓을 작고 앙증맞게 줄지어 놓은 것까지 푸짐하게 마련해놓았으니, 따뜻한 행주에 싸서 김이 모락모락 올라오게 내올 갓 낳은 달걀과 뜨거운 버터 토스트는 말할 것도 없다. 채드밴드는 어지간히 먹어치우는 그릇이기 때문이다—헐뜯는 자들 말로는 게걸스레 처넣는 그릇이라 한다—나이프와 포크 같은 육신의 무기를 더없이 능란하게 휘두를 줄 안다.\n\n가장 좋은 외투를 차려입은 스내그스비 씨는 채비가 다 끝난 것을 둘러보며 손으로 입을 가리고 공손하게 기침을 한 번 하고는 스내그스비 부인에게 말한다. \"여보, 채드밴드 부부는 몇 시에 오시기로 했소?\"\n\n\"여섯 시예요.\" 스내그스비 부인이 말한다.\n\n스내그스비 씨는 점잖고 무심한 투로 \"그 시간이 지났는데\" 하고 한마디 한다.\n\n\"그분들 없이 먼저 들고 싶으신가 보죠.\" 스내그스비 부인이 나무라듯 한마디 한다.\n\n스내그스비 씨는 정말이지 무척 그러고 싶은 표정이지만, 점잖은 기침을 곁들여 이렇게 말한다. \"아니오, 여보, 아니오. 그냥 시간을 말한 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"시간이라는 게,\" 스내그스비 부인이 말한다. \"영원에 견주면 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"지당한 말씀이오, 여보.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"다만 누가 차 마시려고 먹을 것을 장만할 때는—아무래도—시간을 좀 더 염두에 두고 하는 것 아니겠소. 그리고 차 마실 시간을 정해놓았으면, 거기에 맞춰 오는 편이 낫지 않겠소.\"\n\"맞춰 오다니요!\" 스내그스비 부인이 매섭게 되뇐다. \"맞춰요! 채드밴드 씨가 무슨 권투 선수라도 되는 줄 아세요!\"\n\n* 맞춰 오다: 원문은 'come up to it'으로, 권투에서 시합 시작 전 선수가 가운데 선까지 나와 맞붙는 'come up to scratch'를 떠올리게 하여, 스내그스비 부인이 권투 운운하며 트집 잡는 말장난이다.\n\n\"천만에, 여보.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n이때 침실 창밖을 내다보고 있던 거스터가, 흔해빠진 유령처럼 바스락거리고 긁어대며 좁은 계단을 내려와 얼굴이 벌게진 채 응접실로 굴러들어와서는, 채드밴드 부부가 마당에 나타났다고 알린다. 그 직후 복도 안쪽 문에 달린 종이 딸랑거리자, 스내그스비 부인은 당장 수호성인에게로 도로 보내버리겠다고 으름장을 놓으며 손님 알리는 격식을 빠뜨리지 말라고 단단히 일러둔다. (앞서까지는 더없이 멀쩡하던) 신경이 이 위협에 크게 흐트러진 거스터는, 그 격식의 요점을 어찌나 끔찍이 망가뜨려놓는지 \"치즈밍 씨 부부, 아니 그게, 제 말씀은, 거시기예요!\"라고 알리고는 양심에 찔린 채 그 앞에서 물러난다.\n\n채드밴드 씨는 누런 빛이 도는 덩치 큰 남자로, 기름진 미소를 띠고 있으며, 온몸에 고래기름이 잔뜩 차 있는 듯한 분위기를 풍긴다. 채드밴드 부인은 근엄하고 매서운 인상에 말이 없는 여자다. 채드밴드 씨는 부드러우면서도 둔중하게 움직이는데, 똑바로 걷도록 훈련받은 곰과 그리 다르지 않다. 그는 팔을 무척 거북해하는데, 마치 팔이 불편하기라도 한 양, 차라리 엎드려 기고 싶어 하는 듯하다. 머리에는 땀이 흥건하고, 말을 꺼낼 때마다 먼저 그 커다란 손을 치켜드는데, 이제 듣는 이들을 교화하려 한다는 신호를 보내는 셈이다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"이 집에 평화가 깃들기를! 이 집 주인에게, 이 집 안주인에게, 젊은 처녀들에게, 그리고 젊은이들에게! 여러분, 나는 어찌하여 평화를 바라는가? 평화란 무엇인가? 그것이 전쟁인가? 아니다. 그것이 다툼인가? 아니다. 그것이 사랑스럽고, 온화하고, 아름답고, 즐겁고, 평온하고, 기쁜 것인가? 오, 그렇다! 그러므로 여러분, 나는 여러분에게, 그리고 여러분의 식구에게 평화가 깃들기를 바라노라.\"\n\n스내그스비 부인이 깊이 교화된 표정을 짓는 까닭에, 스내그스비 씨는 통틀어 아멘 하고 말하는 편이 좋겠다고 판단하는데, 이 말은 좋은 반응을 얻는다.\n\n\"자, 여러분,\" 채드밴드 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"내가 이 주제를 다루는 김에—\"\n\n거스터가 모습을 드러낸다. 스내그스비 부인은 채드밴드에게서 눈길을 떼지 않은 채 유령 같은 저음으로 무시무시할 만큼 또렷이 말한다. \"물러가!\"\n\n\"자, 여러분,\" 채드밴드가 말한다. \"내가 이 주제를 다루며, 나의 비천한 길에서 이를 깊이 새기는 김에—\"\n\n거스터가 영문 모를 소리로 \"천칠백팔십이\"라고 중얼거리는 소리가 들린다. 유령 같은 목소리가 한층 엄숙하게 되풀이한다. \"물러가!\"\n\n\"자, 여러분,\" 채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"우리 사랑의 정신으로 물어보기로 하자—\"\n\n그래도 거스터는 \"천칠백팔십이\"를 거듭 읊는다.\n\n박해받는 데 이골이 난 사람의 체념을 보이며 말을 멈춘 채드밴드 씨는, 턱을 나른하게 기름진 미소 속으로 접어 넣으며 말한다. \"처녀의 말을 들어봅시다! 말해보거라, 처녀야!\"\n\n\"천칠백팔십이라는데요, 죄송해요, 나리. 그 일 실링이 뭐 하는 돈인지 알고 싶다는데요.\" 거스터가 숨을 헐떡이며 말한다.\n\n\"뭐 하는 거냐고?\" 채드밴드 부인이 받는다. \"찻삯 말이지!\"\n\n거스터는 \"그 사람이 일 실링 팔 펜스를 내든지, 안 그러면 사람을 소환하겠다고 우긴다\"고 대답한다. 스내그스비 부인과 채드밴드 부인이 분개하여 목소리를 날카롭게 높이려는 참에, 채드밴드 씨가 손을 들어 소란을 가라앉힌다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 말한다. \"어제 이행하지 못한 의무 하나가 떠오르는구려. 내가 얼마간 벌을 받아 마땅하오. 투덜대서는 아니 되지요. 레이철, 그 팔 펜스를 치르시오!\"\n\n스내그스비 부인이 숨을 들이쉬며 마치 \"이 사도님 좀 보세요!\" 하는 듯한 표정으로 스내그스비 씨를 빤히 바라보는 동안, 그리고 채드밴드 씨가 겸허함과 고래기름으로 번들거리는 동안, 채드밴드 부인이 돈을 치른다. 이런 식의 채권 채무 셈을 아무리 사소한 항목까지 따지고, 더없이 하찮은 일에까지 공공연히 드러내는 것이 채드밴드 씨의 버릇이다—사실 그것이 그가 내세우는 허세의 으뜸가는 골자다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 채드밴드가 말한다. \"팔 펜스는 큰돈이 아니오. 일 실링 사 펜스가 될 수도 있었고, 응당 반 크라운이 될 수도 있었소. 오, 기뻐합시다, 기뻐합시다! 오, 기뻐합시다!\"\n\n이 말은—그 가락으로 보아 시구의 한 토막인 듯한데—그 말과 함께 채드밴드 씨는 식탁으로 거드름을 피우며 다가가, 의자에 앉기에 앞서 훈계하는 손을 치켜든다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 말한다. \"지금 우리 앞에 차려진 이것은 무엇이오? 음식이오. 그렇다면 우리에게 음식이 필요하오, 여러분? 그렇소. 그런데 어찌하여 우리에게 음식이 필요하오, 여러분? 우리가 한낱 죽을 목숨인 까닭이오. 우리가 한낱 죄지은 자인 까닭이오. 우리가 한낱 흙에서 난 자요, 공기에서 난 자가 아닌 까닭이오. 우리가 날 수 있소, 여러분? 우리는 날 수 없소. 어찌하여 우리는 날 수 없소, 여러분?\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 조금 전의 성공에 우쭐하여 명랑하고 자못 아는 체하는 투로 \"날개가 없으니까요\"라고 한마디 거든다. 하지만 곧장 스내그스비 부인에게 눈총을 받아 입을 다문다.\n\n\"내 말하노니, 여러분,\" 채드밴드 씨는 스내그스비 씨의 의견을 깡그리 묵살하고 지워버리며 말을 잇는다. \"어찌하여 우리는 날 수 없소? 우리가 걷도록 지어진 까닭이오? 그렇소. 우리가 힘 없이 걸을 수 있소, 여러분? 그럴 수 없소. 힘이 없으면 우리가 어찌 되겠소, 여러분? 다리는 우리를 떠받치기를 마다할 것이고, 무릎은 꺾일 것이며, 발목은 접질리고, 우리는 땅바닥에 쓰러질 것이오. 그렇다면 여러분, 인간의 관점에서 우리 사지에 꼭 필요한 그 힘을 우리는 어디서 얻는 것이오? 그것은,\" 채드밴드가 식탁을 한 번 훑어보며 말한다. \"갖가지 모양의 빵에서, 암소가 우리에게 내어주는 젖을 휘저어 만든 버터에서, 닭이 낳은 달걀에서, 햄에서, 우설에서, 소시지에서, 그리고 그와 같은 것들에서 얻는 것 아니오? 그렇소. 그러니 우리 앞에 차려진 이 좋은 것들을 들기로 합시다!\"\n\n채드밴드 씨가 이런 식으로 장황한 말의 계단을 한 단 한 단 쌓아 올리는 데 무슨 특별한 재능 따위는 없다고 헐뜯는 자들은 부인했다. 하지만 채드밴드식 웅변이 널리 환영받고 크게 칭송받는다는 것은 누구나 겪어봤을 일이 분명하므로, 이는 그저 그들이 기어이 헐뜯고 말겠다는 결심의 증거로 받아들일 수밖에 없다.\n\n어쨌거나 채드밴드 씨는 지금으로서는 말을 맺고, 스내그스비 씨의 식탁에 앉아 어마어마하게 먹어치운다. 어떤 종류의 양분이든 앞서 말한 그 질의 기름으로 바꾸는 일이, 이 본보기 같은 그릇의 체질과 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 과정인 듯하니, 먹고 마시기 시작하면 그는 늘 그 물건을 도매 규모로 만들어내는 일종의 거대한 기름 방앗간이나 다른 큰 공장이 된다고 묘사해도 좋을 정도다. 장기 휴정의 이날 저녁, 커시터 거리 쿡스 코트에서 그가 어찌나 강력하게 한 건 해치우는지, 작업이 끝날 무렵에는 창고가 가득 찬 듯이 보인다.\n\n이 향응이 무르익은 무렵, 첫 실수에서 끝내 헤어나지 못한 거스터는 이 집과 자신을 웃음거리로 만들 가능성이 있는 수단이든 없는 수단이든 하나도 빼놓지 않고 동원했으니—그 가운데 몇 가지만 간추리자면, 느닷없이 접시로 채드밴드 씨의 머리에 요란한 군악을 연주하더니 이어서 그 신사의 머리에 머핀을 씌워 왕관을 만들어준 일이 있다—바로 그 무렵 거스터가 스내그스비 씨에게 손님이 찾는다고 귀띔한다.\n\n\"그러니까 손님이—이렇게 말하면 좀 거시기하지만—가게에서 찾는다 하니,\" 스내그스비 씨가 일어서며 말한다. \"이 좋은 분들께서 잠깐만 양해해주시겠지요.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨가 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 두 도제가 누더기 차림의 소년의 팔을 붙들고 있는 순경 하나를 골똘히 바라보고 있다.\n\n\"아니, 이런 세상에,\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"무슨 일이오!\"\n\n\"이 녀석이 말입니다,\" 순경이 말한다. \"거듭 일렀는데도 비키질 않아서요—\"\n\n\"전 늘 비키고 있다구요, 나리.\" 소년이 팔로 꾀죄죄한 눈물을 닦으며 외친다. \"태어난 뒤로 줄곧 비키고 또 비키고 있다니까요. 지금보다 더 어디로 비키란 말이에요, 나리, 대체 어디로요!\"\n\n\"이 녀석이 비키질 않아서요,\" 순경이 침착하게 말하며, 빳빳한 깃 속으로 목을 더 잘 자리 잡게 하려는 듯 직업적으로 목을 슬쩍 움직인다. \"거듭 주의를 줬는데도 말입니다. 그래서 부득이 잡아넣을 수밖에 없습죠. 제가 아는 놈팡이 중에 이만큼 고집 센 어린 좀도둑도 없습니다. 이 녀석이 도무지 비키질 않아요.\"\n\n\"아이고, 어쩌라구요! 대체 어디로 비키냐구요!\" 소년이 머리카락을 잡아 쥐어뜯고 맨발로 스내그스비 씨네 복도 바닥을 쾅쾅 구르며 자못 필사적으로 외친다.\n\n\"그딴 짓 했다간 내가 아주 짧게 끝장을 내줄 테다!\" 순경이 무덤덤하게 소년을 한 번 흔들며 말한다. \"내가 받은 명령은 네놈더러 비키라는 거다. 오백 번도 더 일렀잖느냐.\"\n\n\"근데 어디로요?\" 소년이 외친다.\n\n\"글쎄, 순경 양반, 그게 말이오,\" 스내그스비 씨가 안쓰러운 듯 말하며, 손으로 입을 가리고 몹시 당혹스럽고 미심쩍은 기침을 한다. \"정말이지 그게 좀 문제 같소만. 어디로, 그러게 말이오?\"\n\n\"제 명령은 거기까지는 아닙니다.\" 순경이 대답한다. \"제가 받은 명령은 이 녀석더러 비키라는 것뿐입죠.\"\n\n알아듣겠느냐, 조? 의회라는 하늘의 거대한 빛들이 이 일에서 몇 해 동안 네게 비키는 본보기를 보여주지 못했다는 것은, 너에게도 다른 누구에게도 아무것도 아니다. 네게는 단 하나의 위대한 처방이 남아 있으니—심오한 철학적 처방—이 땅 위 네 기묘한 존재의 전부이자 궁극이다. 비켜라! 그렇다고 아주 떠나버려서는 결코 아니 된다, 조. 그 점에 대해서는 그 거대한 빛들이 도무지 의견 일치를 보지 못하니까. 비켜라!\n\n스내그스비 씨는 이런 취지로는 아무 말도 하지 않는다. 사실 아무 말도 하지 않고, 어느 방향으로도 길이 없다는 뜻을 담아 더없이 처량한 기침만 할 뿐이다. 이때쯤 말다툼 소리를 들은 채드밴드 부부와 스내그스비 부인이 계단에 모습을 드러냈다. 거스터는 복도 끝을 한 번도 떠난 적이 없으니, 온 식구가 다 모인 셈이다.\n\n\"간단히 여쭙겠습니다, 나리,\" 순경이 말한다. \"이 녀석을 아십니까? 이 녀석이 안다고 합니다만.\"\n\n스내그스비 부인이 높은 곳에서 대뜸 외친다. \"아니, 모르세요!\"\n\n\"여보, 마누라!\" 스내그스비 씨가 계단 위를 올려다보며 말한다. \"여보, 잠깐만! 제발 잠깐만 참아주시오, 여보. 이 아이를 좀 알기는 아오. 그런데 내가 아는 한, 무슨 나쁜 구석이 있다고는 못 하겠소. 순경 양반, 어쩌면 오히려 그 반대일지도 모르오.\" 그러고는 법률 문방구 주인이 순경에게 조와 얽힌 딱하고도 서글픈 사연을 들려주는데, 반 크라운 이야기만은 빼놓는다.\n\n\"글쎄요!\" 순경이 말한다. \"그렇다면 여태까지는 이 녀석이 한 말에 근거가 있었던 모양입니다. 제가 홀본 위쪽에서 이 녀석을 잡았을 때, 나리를 안다고 하더군요. 그러자 군중 속에 있던 어떤 젊은이가 나리와 아는 사이라면서, 나리가 점잖은 가장이시고, 제가 가서 알아보면 자기가 나타나겠다고 했지요. 그 젊은이는 약속을 지킬 마음이 없는 듯합니다만—오! 마침 그 젊은이가 왔군요!\"\n\n거피 씨가 들어와, 스내그스비 씨에게 고갯짓을 하고는 서기다운 정중함으로 계단 위 부인들에게 모자에 손을 갖다 댄다.\n\n\"방금 사무실에서 어슬렁어슬렁 나오다가 이 소동이 벌어지는 걸 봤지 뭡니까,\" 거피 씨가 법률 문방구 주인에게 말한다. \"그런데 선생 성함이 나오기에, 이 일을 좀 알아보는 게 옳겠다 싶었지요.\"\n\n\"참 친절도 하시오, 선생,\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"고맙소이다.\" 그러고는 스내그스비 씨가 다시 자기 사연을 들려주는데, 이번에도 반 크라운 이야기는 빼놓는다.\n\n\"자, 난 네놈이 어디 사는지 안다,\" 순경이 그제야 조에게 말한다. \"톰 혼자 사는 곳에 살지. 살기에 참 좋고 깨끗한 데로구나, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"전 더 좋고 깨끗한 데 가서 살 수가 없어요, 나리.\" 조가 대답한다. \"제가 좋고 깨끗한 데 가서 살려고 해봤자 거기선 거들떠도 안 본다구요. 누가 저 같은 진짜배기한테 좋고 깨끗한 셋방을 내주겠어요!\"\n\n\"넌 무척 가난하지, 안 그러냐?\" 순경이 말한다.\n\n\"네, 정말 그래요, 나리, 대체로 무척 가난하죠.\" 조가 대답한다. \"자, 그럼 직접 판단해보십쇼! 이 녀석한테 손만 한 번 댔는데,\" 순경이 사람들에게 반 크라운 두 닢을 꺼내 보이며 말한다. \"이 반 크라운 두 닢이 떨어져 나왔지 뭡니까!\"\n\n\"그건 남은 거예요, 스내그스비 나리,\" 조가 말한다. \"어떤 마님이 저한테 준 일 파운드 금화에서 남은 거라구요. 너울 쓴 마님이 자기가 하녀라면서 어느 날 밤에 제 길목으로 와서는, 이 집이랑, 나리가 글 써준 그 사람이 죽은 집이랑, 그 사람이 묻힌 묘지를 좀 보여달라고 했어요. 마님이 저한테 그러는 거예요, '네가 그 심리에 나온 애냐?' 하구요. 그래서 제가 '네' 그랬죠. 마님이 또 저한테 그러는 거예요, '그 데를 다 보여줄 수 있겠냐?' 하구요. 그래서 제가 '네, 그럼요' 그랬어요. 그랬더니 마님이 저한테 '그럼 해다오' 하길래, 제가 해드렸더니, 마님이 일 파운드 금화를 주고 휙 가버렸다구요. 근데 그 금화도 그닥 못 챙겼어요,\" 조가 꾀죄죄한 눈물을 흘리며 말한다. \"톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 잔돈으로 바꿔주는 값으로 오 실링을 먼저 줘야 했고, 그담엔 제가 자는 새 어떤 젊은 놈이 또 오 실링을 슬쩍했고, 또 딴 애가 구 펜스를 슬쩍했고, 주인장은 거기에 더 보태서 술을 쫙 돌렸으니까요.\"\n\n\"이 마님이랑 금화 이야기를 누가 곧이듣기라도 할 것 같으냐, 응?\" 순경이 이루 말할 수 없는 경멸을 담아 곁눈질로 소년을 흘겨보며 말한다.\n\n\"믿으실 거라곤 생각 안 해요, 나리.\" 조가 대답한다. \"전 아무것도, 별로 기대 안 해요, 나리. 근데 그게 진짜 있었던 일이에요.\"\n\n\"이 녀석 꼴 좀 보십쇼!\" 순경이 청중에게 한마디 한다. \"자, 스내그스비 나리, 제가 이번엔 이 녀석을 가두지 않는다면, 나리께서 이 녀석이 비키게 책임지시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"안 돼요!\" 계단에서 스내그스비 부인이 외친다.\n\n\"여보, 마누라!\" 남편이 사정한다. \"순경 양반, 이 아이가 비킬 거라고 난 믿어 의심치 않소. 알다시피 자넨 정말 그래야만 하네,\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"전 어떻게든 다 좋아요, 나리.\" 가엾은 조가 말한다.\n\n\"그럼 그렇게 해라,\" 순경이 한마디 한다. \"네가 뭘 해야 하는지 알지. 그렇게 해! 그리고 명심해라, 다음번엔 이렇게 쉽게 넘어가진 못할 거다. 네 돈이나 챙겨라. 자, 어서 오 마일 떨어진 데로 갈수록 모두한테 좋을 거다.\"\n\n이렇게 작별의 충고를 던지고, 비켜갈 만한 곳이라며 지는 해 쪽을 대충 가리킨 순경은, 듣고 있던 사람들에게 작별 인사를 하고는, 약간의 통풍을 위해 쇠 두른 모자를 손에 들고 그늘진 쪽으로 걸어가며, 쿡스 코트의 메아리가 자신을 위해 느린 음악을 연주하게 만든다.\n\n자, 마님과 금화에 관한 조의 믿기 힘든 이야기는 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람의 호기심을 얼마간 일깨워놓았다. 증거 문제에 관해서라면 캐묻기 좋아하는 두뇌를 지녔고 장기 휴정의 권태에 시달려온 거피 씨는, 이 사건에 흥미가 동한 나머지 정식으로 증인을 반대신문하기 시작하는데, 부인들이 어찌나 흥미진진해하는지 스내그스비 부인은 정중하게 거피 씨에게 위층으로 올라와 차 한 잔 들기를 청한다. 앞선 소동 탓에 차상이 어수선해진 것은 양해해달라면서 말이다. 거피 씨가 이 제안에 동의를 표하자, 조는 응접실 문간으로 따라 들어오라는 청을 받는다. 거기서 거피 씨는 마치 버터 장수가 버터 한 덩이를 다루듯, 조를 이 모양 저 모양 또 다른 모양으로 주물럭거리며 증인으로 붙잡고는, 더없이 훌륭한 본보기를 좇아 그를 들들 볶는다. 또한 이 신문은, 아무것도 끄집어내지 못한다는 점에서나 길고 지루하다는 점에서나, 그런 본보기다운 연출과 다를 바 없다. 거피 씨는 제 재능을 절감하고, 스내그스비 부인은 이 일이 자신의 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미를 만족시킬 뿐 아니라 남편의 가게를 법조계에서 한층 더 높이 올려준다고 느끼는 까닭이다. 이 치열한 격돌이 진행되는 동안, 한낱 기름 장사에만 매여 있던 채드밴드라는 배는, 좌초한 채 다시 띄워지기를 기다린다.\n\n\"음!\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"이 녀석이 구두장이 밀랍처럼 끝까지 고집을 부리는 거거나, 아니면 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 내가 겪어본 그 어떤 일도 능가하는 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 게 여기 있는 거로군.\"\n\n채드밴드 부인이 스내그스비 부인에게 귀엣말을 하자, 스내그스비 부인이 외친다. \"어머, 그게 정말이에요!\"\n\n\"여러 해 됐어요!\" 채드밴드 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"켄지 앤드 카보이 사무실을 여러 해 전부터 아셨대요.\" 스내그스비 부인이 의기양양하게 거피 씨에게 설명한다. \"채드밴드 부인이세요—이분 부인이시죠—채드밴드 목사님이요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇습니까!\" 거피 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"지금 남편과 결혼하기 전 일이에요.\" 채드밴드 부인이 말한다.\n\n\"부인께서 무슨 사건의 당사자이셨습니까?\" 거피 씨가 반대신문의 화살을 돌리며 말한다.\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"무슨 사건의 당사자가 아니셨다고요?\" 거피 씨가 말한다.\n\n채드밴드 부인이 고개를 젓는다.\n\n\"그렇다면 혹시 무슨 사건의 당사자인 누군가를 아셨습니까?\" 거피 씨가 말한다. 그는 자기 대화를 법정의 원칙에 따라 본뜨는 것보다 더 좋아하는 일이 없다.\n\n\"꼭 그런 것도 아니에요.\" 채드밴드 부인이 무뚝뚝한 미소로 농에 장단을 맞추며 대답한다.\n\n\"꼭 그런 것도 아니다!\" 거피 씨가 되뇐다. \"아주 좋습니다. 그럼 부인, 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무실과 어떤 거래(지금 무슨 거래인지는 말하지 않기로 합시다)를 한 사람이 부인이 아시는 어떤 여인이었습니까, 아니면 부인이 아시는 어떤 신사였습니까? 천천히 생각하십시오, 부인. 차차 알게 될 테니까요. 남자입니까, 여자입니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"둘 다 아니에요.\" 채드밴드 부인이 아까처럼 말한다.\n\n\"오! 아이로군요!\" 거피 씨가 영국 배심원에게 던지곤 하는 그 날카롭고 직업적인 눈길을 감탄해 마지않는 스내그스비 부인에게 던지며 말한다. \"자, 부인, 그럼 그게 어떤 아이였는지 친절히 일러주시겠습니까.\"\n\n\"드디어 맞히셨네요, 나리.\" 채드밴드 부인이 또 한 번 무뚝뚝한 미소를 지으며 말한다. \"글쎄요, 나리, 보아하니 나리가 태어나기도 전 일일 거예요. 저는 에스더 서머슨이라는 아이를 맡아 길렀는데, 그 아이를 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에서 세상에 내보냈지요.\"\n\n\"서머슨 아가씨요, 부인!\" 거피 씨가 흥분하여 외친다.\n\n\"저는 그 애를 에스더 서머슨이라 부릅니다.\" 채드밴드 부인이 엄숙하게 말한다. \"내가 데리고 있을 적엔 그 계집애한테 '아가씨'라곤 없었어요. 그냥 에스더였죠. '에스더, 이거 해라! 에스더, 저거 해라!' 하면 그 애는 시키는 대로 해야 했지요.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 거피 씨가 그 좁은 방을 가로질러 다가가며 말한다. \"지금 부인께 말씀드리는 이 보잘것없는 인간이, 방금 부인께서 언급하신 그곳에서 그 젊은 아가씨가 런던에 처음 왔을 때 그분을 맞이한 사람입니다. 부디 제가 부인의 손을 잡는 기쁨을 누리게 해주십시오.\"\n\n마침내 기회를 엿본 채드밴드 씨가 늘 하던 신호를 보내며 김이 모락모락 나는 머리를 손수건으로 톡톡 두드리며 일어선다. 스내그스비 부인이 \"쉿!\" 하고 속삭인다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 채드밴드가 말한다. \"우리는 우리를 위해 마련된 위안을 절제하여 들었소\"(적어도 그로 말하자면 결코 그렇지 않았지만) \"이 집이 이 땅의 기름진 것을 누리며 살기를. 이 집에 곡식과 포도주가 넘쳐나기를. 이 집이 자라고, 번창하고, 번영하고, 나아가고, 전진하고, 앞으로 밀고 나아가기를! 그러나 여러분, 우리가 그 밖에 또 무엇을 들었소? 들었소이다. 여러분, 우리가 그 밖에 또 무엇을 들었소? 영적인 유익이오? 그렇소. 우리는 그 영적인 유익을 어디서 얻었소? 나의 젊은 벗이여, 앞으로 나오라!\"\n\n이렇게 불려 나온 조는 뒤로 한 번 구부정하고, 앞으로 또 한 번 구부정하고, 양옆으로 한 번씩 더 구부정하더니, 그 의도를 영 미심쩍어하는 기색으로 달변의 채드밴드와 마주 선다.\n\n\"나의 젊은 벗이여,\" 채드밴드가 말한다. \"그대는 우리에게 진주요, 그대는 우리에게 다이아몬드요, 그대는 우리에게 보석이요, 그대는 우리에게 보배로다. 그런데 어찌하여 그러한가, 나의 젊은 벗이여?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\" 조가 대답한다. \"전 아무것도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"나의 젊은 벗이여,\" 채드밴드가 말한다. \"그대가 아무것도 모르는 까닭에 그대가 우리에게 보석이요 보배인 것이오. 그대는 무엇인가, 나의 젊은 벗이여? 그대는 들의 짐승인가? 아니다. 공중의 새인가? 아니다. 바다나 강의 물고기인가? 아니다. 그대는 사람의 아이로다, 나의 젊은 벗이여. 사람의 아이. 오, 사람의 아이라는 것은 영광스럽도다! 그런데 어찌하여 영광스러운가, 나의 젊은 벗이여? 그대가 지혜의 가르침을 받을 수 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 그대의 유익을 위해 내가 지금 베푸는 이 말씀으로 이로움을 얻을 수 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 막대기도, 지팡이도, 그루터기도, 돌멩이도, 말뚝도, 기둥도 아닌 까닭이오.\n\n오 반짝이는 기쁨의 흐르는 시냇물이여 드높이 솟구치는 사람의 아이가 된다는 것은!\n\n그래, 그대는 지금 그 시냇물에서 몸을 식히고 있는가, 나의 젊은 벗이여? 아니다. 어찌하여 그대는 지금 그 시냇물에서 몸을 식히지 않는가? 그대가 어둠 속에 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 캄캄함 속에 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 죄악 속에 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 속박 속에 있는 까닭이오. 나의 젊은 벗이여, 속박이란 무엇인가? 우리 사랑의 정신으로 물어보기로 하자.\"\n\n설교가 이런 위협적인 단계에 이르자, 점점 정신이 나가는 듯하던 조가 오른팔로 얼굴을 쓱 문지르며 끔찍하게 하품을 한다. 스내그스비 부인은 저 녀석이 마왕의 수족이라 믿는다고 분개하여 표명한다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 채드밴드 씨가 박해받는 턱을 다시 기름진 미소 속으로 접어 넣으며 둘러보고 말한다. \"내가 낮추어지는 것이 마땅하오. 내가 시험받는 것이 마땅하오. 내가 욕을 보는 것이 마땅하오. 내가 바로잡히는 것이 마땅하오. 나는 지난 안식일에 내 세 시간의 교화를 두고 우쭐한 마음을 품었다가 걸려 넘어졌소. 이제 그 셈은 좋게 균형이 맞춰졌으니, 내 채권자가 화해를 받아들였소. 오, 기뻐합시다, 기뻐합시다! 오, 기뻐합시다!\"\n\n스내그스비 부인 쪽에서 커다란 감동이 인다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 채드밴드가 마무리하며 둘러보고 말한다. \"나는 지금 나의 젊은 벗과는 더 나아가지 않으렵니다. 나의 젊은 벗이여, 그대는 내일 와서 이 착한 부인께 내가 그대에게 말씀을 베풀려면 어디로 가야 하는지 여쭤보겠는가? 그리고 그 이튿날에도, 그다음 날에도, 또 그다음 날에도, 그리고 숱한 즐거운 날에, 목마른 제비처럼 말씀을 들으러 오겠는가?\"(이 말은 암소처럼 가뿐하게 한다.)\n\n당장 어떻게든 빠져나가는 것이 목적인 듯한 조는 어물어물 고개를 끄덕인다. 그러자 거피 씨가 그에게 일 페니를 던져주고, 스내그스비 부인은 거스터를 불러 이 녀석을 무사히 집 밖으로 내보내라 한다. 하지만 그가 아래층으로 내려가기 전에 스내그스비 씨가 식탁에서 남은 고기 부스러기를 잔뜩 안겨주고, 조는 그것을 두 팔로 끌어안고 들고 나간다.\n\n이리하여 채드밴드 씨는—헐뜯는 자들 말로는, 그가 그토록 가증스러운 헛소리를 한도 끝도 없이 늘어놓는 것이야 놀랄 일이 아니지만, 일단 뻔뻔하게 시작해놓고 어쩌다 그치기라도 하는 것이 오히려 놀랄 일이라는데—저녁이라는 약간의 밑천을 기름 장사에 투자할 때까지 사생활 속으로 물러난다. 조는 장기 휴정을 가로질러 비키고 또 비켜, 블랙프라이어스 다리까지 내려가서는, 자리 잡고 한 끼 들 만한 뜨끈하게 달궈진 돌 모퉁이를 찾아낸다.\n\n그리고 거기 앉아, 우물우물 씹고 잘근잘근 뜯으며, 붉고 보랏빛으로 물든 연기 구름 위로 반짝이는 세인트 폴 대성당 꼭대기의 거대한 십자가를 올려다본다. 소년의 얼굴을 보면 그 거룩한 상징이 그의 눈에는, 이 거대하고 어수선한 도시에서 으뜸가는 어수선함—그토록 금빛 찬란하고, 그토록 높이 솟았고, 그토록 손이 닿지 않는 것—으로 비치는가 보다. 거기 그가 앉아 있다. 해는 저물고, 강물은 빠르게 흐르고, 군중은 두 갈래 물줄기로 그의 곁을 흘러간다—저마다 어떤 목적을 향해, 하나의 끝을 향해 모두가 비켜 나아간다—마침내 그도 누군가에게 떠밀려 \"비키라\"는 말을 들을 때까지.\n\n장기 휴정기가 개정기를 향해 어슬렁어슬렁 흘러간다. 마치 한가로운 강물이 평평한 들판을 느릿느릿 거닐며 바다로 흘러가듯이. 구피 씨는 그 흐름과 마음 맞게 어슬렁거린다. 그는 주머니칼 날을 무디게 만들었고, 그 칼을 책상에 사방으로 푹푹 찔러대다 끝을 부러뜨렸다. 책상에 무슨 원한이 있어서가 아니라, 무엇이든 해야 하기 때문이고, 그 무엇이라는 것이 육체의 기력도 지적인 기력도 너무 무겁게 끌어다 쓰지 않는, 시시한 종류여야 하기 때문이다. 그는 등받이 없는 의자의 다리 하나로 빙글빙글 도는 것, 책상을 푹푹 찌르는 것, 그리고 하품하는 것만큼 자기에게 잘 맞는 일이 없다고 깨닫는다.\n\n켄지와 카보이는 시내를 떠나 있고, 견습 사무원은 수렵 면허를 따서 아버지 댁으로 내려갔으며, 구피 씨의 동료 봉급생활자 두 명도 휴가를 떠나 부재중이다. 구피 씨와 리처드 카스톤 씨가 사무실의 위엄을 나눠 가진다. 그런데 카스톤 씨가 당분간 켄지의 방에 자리를 잡는 바람에 구피 씨는 속이 부글부글 끓는다. 어찌나 끓는지, 올드 스트리트 로드에서 어머니와 바닷가재와 상추로 저녁을 먹는 은밀한 시간이면 그는 신랄한 빈정거림으로 어머니에게 알린다. 사무실이 멋쟁이 나리한테 어울리기엔 영 변변찮은 게 걱정이라느니, 멋쟁이가 온다는 걸 미리 알았더라면 페인트칠이라도 해뒀을 거라느니 하고.\n\n구피 씨는 켄지와 카보이 사무실에서 의자 하나를 차지하고 들어앉는 사람이라면 누구든, 당연한 일이라는 듯 자기에게 음흉한 속셈을 품고 있으리라고 의심한다. 그런 자들은 하나같이 자기를 끌어내리려 든다고 굳게 믿는다. 어떻게, 왜, 언제, 무슨 까닭으로 그러느냐고 물으면 그는 한쪽 눈을 감고 고개를 절레절레 흔든다. 이 심오한 견해를 밑천 삼아, 그는 음모가 없는데도 더없이 교묘한 방식으로 무한한 공을 들여 맞불 음모를 꾸미고, 상대도 없는 가장 심오한 장기판을 펼친다.\n\n그러니 새로 온 사람이 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 서류를 끊임없이 들여다보는 모습은 구피 씨에게 크나큰 만족의 원천이 된다. 그런 짓에서 나올 것이라곤 혼란과 실패뿐임을 그는 잘 알기 때문이다. 그의 만족은 켄지와 카보이 사무실에서 장기 휴정기를 어슬렁대는 또 한 사람, 즉 어린 스몰위드에게로 옮겨간다.\n\n어린 스몰위드가(은유적으로 스몰이라고 불리고, 또 햇병아리를 익살스레 표현하듯 칙위드라고도 불린다) 과연 소년이었던 적이 있는지는 링컨 법학원에서 크게 의심받는 바다. 그는 지금 열다섯 살이 채 안 되었으나 법률계의 노련한 고참이다. 그가 챈서리 레인 근처 시가 가게의 어느 아가씨에게 연정을 품고 있고, 그녀를 위해 몇 해 전부터 약혼한 사이였던 다른 아가씨와의 약속을 깨버렸다는 것이 우스개처럼 통용된다. 그는 도시에서 빚어진 물건으로, 키가 작고 이목구비가 쪼글쪼글하지만, 유난히 키 큰 모자 덕분에 상당히 먼 거리에서도 알아볼 수 있다. 구피가 되는 것이 그의 야망의 목표다. 그는 그 신사(자기를 후원해주는)를 본떠 옷을 입고, 그를 본떠 말하고, 그를 본떠 걷고, 자기 전부를 온전히 그 사람 위에 세운다. 그는 구피 씨의 각별한 신임을 받는 영광을 누리며, 이따금 자기 경험의 깊은 샘에서 길어 올린 지혜로 사생활의 까다로운 대목에 관해 그에게 조언한다.\n\n구피 씨는 의자란 의자를 차례로 다 앉아보고도 편한 것이 하나도 없자, 또 머리를 식혀볼 생각으로 몇 번이나 철제 금고에 머리를 들이밀어보고도, 아침 내내 창밖으로 몸을 늘어뜨린 채 빈둥거렸다. 스몰위드 씨는 거품 나는 음료를 가지러 두 번 심부름을 다녀왔고, 두 번 다 사무실용 텀블러 두 잔에 그것을 타서 자로 휘저었다. 구피 씨는 마실수록 더 목이 마르다는 역설을 스몰위드 씨에게 깊이 생각해보라고 내놓고는, 가망 없는 나른함에 빠져 창턱에 머리를 기댄다.\n\n그렇게 링컨 법학원 올드 스퀘어의 그늘을 내다보며 견딜 수 없이 지긋지긋한 벽돌과 회반죽을 둘러보다가, 구피 씨는 아래쪽 회랑 보도에서 사내다운 구레나룻 한 쌍이 불쑥 솟아올라 자기 얼굴 쪽으로 치켜드는 것을 알아챈다. 그와 동시에 나직한 휘파람이 법학원 안으로 흘러들고, 억눌린 목소리가 외친다. \"어이! 구-피!\"\n\n\"이런, 설마!\" 구피 씨가 정신이 번쩍 들어 말한다. \"스몰! 조블링이 왔어!\" 스몰의 머리도 창밖으로 나와 조블링에게 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"자넨 어디서 불쑥 솟아난 거야?\" 구피 씨가 묻는다.\n\n\"뎁퍼드 아래쪽 채소밭에서. 더는 못 견디겠어. 입대해야겠어. 이봐! 반 크라운만 좀 빌려주면 좋겠는데. 정말이지, 배가 고파.\"\n\n조블링은 굶주린 행색이고, 게다가 뎁퍼드 아래쪽 채소밭에서 시들어 씨가 다 빠져버린 듯한 모습이다.\n\n\"이봐! 여유가 있으면 반 크라운만 던져줘. 저녁이라도 좀 먹게.\"\n\n\"나랑 같이 저녁 먹지 않을래?\" 구피 씨가 동전을 던지며 말하고, 조블링 씨는 그것을 깔끔하게 받아챈다.\n\n\"얼마나 버텨야 하는데?\" 조블링이 말한다.\n\n\"삼십 분도 안 걸려. 적이 떠날 때까지 여기서 기다리는 중이거든.\" 구피 씨가 머리로 안쪽을 가리키며 대답한다.\n\n\"무슨 적?\"\n\n\"새로 온 적. 견습 들어올 작자. 기다릴 텐가?\"\n\n\"그동안 읽을 거라도 좀 줄 수 있어?\" 조블링 씨가 말한다.\n\n스몰위드가 법률 명부를 권한다. 그러나 조블링 씨는 자못 진지하게 그건 \"도저히 못 견디겠다\"고 단언한다.\n\n\"신문을 줄게.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"얘가 내려다 줄 거야. 그래도 여기서 어슬렁대는 걸 들키지 않는 게 좋겠지. 우리 계단에 앉아서 읽어. 조용한 데니까.\"\n\n조블링은 알아들었다는 듯, 또 그러겠다는 듯 고개를 끄덕인다. 영리한 스몰위드는 그에게 신문을 갖다주고, 그가 기다리다 진력이 나서 때 이른 발걸음을 옮기지 않도록 미리 막느라 이따금 층계참에서 그에게 눈길을 떨군다. 마침내 적이 물러가자, 스몰위드는 조블링 씨를 위층으로 데려온다.\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 지냈나?\" 구피 씨가 그와 악수하며 말한다.\n\n\"그저 그렇지. 자넨 어때?\"\n\n구피 씨가 자랑할 만한 형편은 못 된다고 대답하자, 조블링 씨가 슬쩍 한마디를 던진다. \"그분은 어떠셔?\" 이를 구피 씨는 무례한 참견으로 여겨 받아친다. \"조블링, 인간의 마음에는 건드려선 안 될 현이라는 게 있는 법이야―\" 조블링이 사과한다.\n\n\"그 얘기만 빼고 뭐든!\" 구피 씨가 자기 상처를 음울하게 즐기며 말한다. \"현이라는 게 있단 말일세, 조블링―\"\n\n조블링 씨가 다시 사과한다.\n\n이 짧은 입씨름이 오가는 동안, 저녁 자리에 끼어든 부지런한 스몰위드는 종잇조각에 법률 문서체로 \"즉시 돌아옴\"이라고 적어두었다. 그는 관계자 모두에게 알리는 이 통지문을 우편함에 끼워넣고는, 구피 씨가 쓰는 각도 그대로 키 큰 모자를 비스듬히 눌러쓰고 후원자에게 이제 슬그머니 자리를 떠도 좋다고 알린다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 근처의 식당으로 향한다. 단골들 사이에서 후딱식당이라는 이름으로 통하는 부류의 가게로, 거기서 통통하게 잘 자란 마흔 살 먹은 젊은 여종업원이 감수성 예민한 스몰위드에게 모종의 인상을 남긴 것으로 짐작되는데, 이 스몰위드로 말하자면 세월이라는 것이 아무 의미도 없는 기이한 바꿔친 아이라 할 만하다. 그는 조숙하게도 수백 년에 걸친 부엉이 같은 지혜를 갖춘 채 서 있다. 요람에 누운 적이 있다 한들, 거기 누울 때도 연미복 차림이었을 것만 같다. 스몰위드는 늙고 늙은 눈을 가졌다. 그리고 원숭이처럼 마시고 피운다. 목은 옷깃 속에서 빳빳하다. 결코 속아 넘어가는 법이 없다. 그것이 무엇이든 간에, 그 모든 것을 다 안다. 한마디로, 그는 자라는 동안 법과 형평법의 손에 어찌나 길러졌는지, 일종의 화석이 된 도깨비가 되어버렸다. 그가 이 땅에 존재하게 된 내력을 설명하느라 관청가에서는 그의 아버지가 존 도이고 어머니는 로 일가의 유일한 여성 식구였다는 소문이 돌고, 또 그의 첫 배냇저고리가 파란 서류 가방으로 지어졌다는 소문도 돈다.\n* 존 도와 로: 영국 법률에서 신원 미상의 가상 당사자를 가리키는 통칭. 도깨비 같은 출생 내력을 법률 용어로 빗댄 농담이다.\n\n진열창에 인위적으로 하얗게 만든 콜리플라워와 가금류, 푸릇푸릇한 완두콩 바구니, 시원하게 윤기 도는 오이, 꼬챙이에 꿸 채비를 마친 고깃덩이가 사람을 홀리듯 늘어서 있지만, 스몰위드 씨는 거기에 조금도 흔들리지 않고 식당 안으로 앞장서 들어간다. 거기 사람들은 그를 알아보고 떠받든다. 그에게는 단골 칸막이 자리가 있고, 그는 신문이란 신문을 다 차지하며, 그것을 십 분 넘게 붙들고 있는 대머리 노인네들에게 매섭게 군다. 제대로 부푼 \"빵\" 한 덩이가 아니고서는 그에게 무엇을 내밀어도 소용없고, 부위 또한 최상품이 아니고서는 어떤 고기를 권해도 받지 않는다. 그레이비에 관해서는 그는 한 치도 양보가 없다.\n\n구피 씨는 그 요정 같은 위력을 의식하고 그 무시무시한 경륜에 머리를 조아리며 그날의 만찬을 고르는 일에 그의 의견을 구한다. 여종업원이 음식 목록을 줄줄이 읊는 동안 그에게 매달리는 눈길을 돌리며 묻는다. \"자넨 뭘 들겠나, 칙?\" 칙은 능청맞은 깊은 속에서 \"송아지 고기에 햄에 강낭콩―그리고 속도 잊으면 안 돼, 폴리\" 하고(점잖은 눈을 으스스하게 한쪽 치뜨며) 정하니, 구피 씨와 조블링 씨도 똑같이 주문한다. 거기에 반반 섞은 맥주 한 파인트짜리 단지 셋이 더 보태진다. 곧 여종업원이 겉보기엔 바벨탑 모형 같지만 실은 접시와 납작한 양철 뚜껑을 잔뜩 쌓아 올린 것을 들고 돌아온다. 스몰위드 씨는 자기 앞에 놓인 것을 흡족해하며 그 고색창연한 눈에 영민한 자애로움을 담아 그녀에게 윙크한다. 그러자, 끊임없이 들어오고 나가고 이리저리 뛰어다니는 가운데, 그릇 부딪치는 소리가 요란하고, 부엌에서 좋은 부위를 실어 나르는 기계가 위아래로 덜컹대며 오르내리고, 전성관 아래로 좋은 부위를 더 달라고 날카롭게 외치는 소리와 이미 처분된 좋은 부위의 값을 날카롭게 셈하는 소리가 들리며, 자른 것과 자르지 않은 뜨거운 고깃덩이의 김과 열기가 사방에 자욱하고, 상당히 후끈 달아오른 공기 속에서 더러워진 칼과 식탁보가 절로 기름덩어리와 맥주 얼룩을 발진처럼 터뜨려내는 듯한, 그런 한복판에서 이 법률계의 삼두정치는 식욕을 달랜다.\n\n조블링 씨는 단순한 멋내기에 필요한 정도보다 더 단단히 단추를 채우고 있다. 그의 모자는 챙 가장자리에 윤기 나는 묘한 빛을 띠는데, 마치 거기가 달팽이들의 단골 산책로라도 되었던 것 같다. 같은 현상이 그의 외투 군데군데에서도, 특히 솔기에서 두드러지게 보인다. 그는 곤궁한 처지에 빠진 신사처럼 빛바랜 행색이다. 옅은 빛깔의 구레나룻마저 어딘가 초라한 기운을 띤 채 축 늘어져 있다.\n\n그의 식욕이 어찌나 왕성한지, 한동안 굶주린 살림을 해왔음을 짐작케 한다. 그는 일행이 아직 자기 접시의 절반밖에 못 비웠을 때 송아지 고기와 햄 접시를 후딱 해치워버려, 구피 씨가 한 접시 더 들라고 권한다. \"고맙네, 구피.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다. \"정말이지 한 접시 더 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\"\n\n한 접시가 더 나오자 그는 매우 흔쾌히 달려든다.\n\n구피 씨는 이따금 그를 말없이 지켜본다. 마침내 그가 이 두 번째 접시를 절반쯤 비우고는 잠시 멈춰 반반 섞은 맥주(이것도 새로 채운 것이다) 단지를 흐뭇하게 들이켜고 다리를 쭉 뻗으며 두 손을 비빌 때까지. 그렇게 흡족함으로 발그레해진 그를 바라보며 구피 씨가 말한다. \"다시 사람이 됐군, 토니!\"\n\n\"글쎄, 아직 완전히는 아냐.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다. \"이제 막 태어난 셈이지.\"\n\n\"다른 채소도 좀 들겠나? 풀? 완두콩? 여름배추?\"\n\n\"고맙네, 구피.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다. \"정말이지 여름배추를 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\"\n\n주문이 들어간다. 거기에 스몰위드 씨의 빈정대는 한마디가 덧붙는다. \"민달팽이는 빼고, 폴리!\" 그러자 배추가 나온다.\n\n\"점점 자라는 중이야, 구피.\" 조블링 씨가 맛깔스럽게 한결같이 칼과 포크를 놀리며 말한다.\n\n\"그거 반갑군.\"\n\n\"실은 이제 막 십 대로 접어들었거든.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다.\n\n그는 자기 몫을 다 해치울 때까지 더는 말이 없다. 그 일을 구피 씨와 스몰위드 씨가 끝마칠 무렵에 완수하니, 멋들어진 솜씨로 거리를 따라잡아 송아지 고기와 햄 한 접시에 배추 한 접시 차이로 두 신사를 거뜬히 앞지른다.\n\n\"자, 스몰.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"후식은 뭘 권하겠나?\"\n\n\"골수 푸딩이죠.\" 스몰위드 씨가 즉각 말한다.\n\n\"옳거니, 옳거니!\" 조블링 씨가 짓궂은 표정으로 외친다. \"거기 있었군, 그래? 고맙네, 구피 씨, 골수 푸딩을 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\"\n\n골수 푸딩 셋이 나오자, 조블링 씨는 유쾌한 기분으로 자기가 빠르게 성년이 되어간다고 덧붙인다. 거기에 이어 스몰위드 씨의 분부로 \"체셔 치즈 셋\"이, 또 거기에 이어 \"작은 럼주 셋\"이 나온다. 이 만찬의 정점에 흐뭇하게 다다르자, 조블링 씨는(칸막이 자리 한쪽을 혼자 독차지하고 있던 터라) 양탄자 깔린 좌석에 다리를 올려놓고 벽에 기대어 말한다. \"이제 다 컸어, 구피. 무르익은 나이에 이르렀지.\"\n\n\"이제 어떻게 생각하나,\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"그러니까―스몰위드는 괜찮지?\"\n\n\"전혀 상관없어. 이 친구의 건강을 위해 기꺼이 한잔 들지.\"\n\n\"감사합니다, 선생님!\" 스몰위드 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"내 말은, 이제 어떻게 생각하느냐는 거야,\" 구피 씨가 잇는다. \"입대 말일세?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 저녁을 먹고 나서 드는 생각하고,\" 조블링 씨가 대답한다. \"저녁을 먹기 전에 드는 생각은 또 다른 법이지, 친애하는 구피. 그래도 저녁을 먹고 나서까지도 나는 자문하게 돼. 난 뭘 해야 하나? 어떻게 살아야 하나? 일 포 망제, 알잖아.\" 조블링 씨는 그 말을 마치 영국 마구간에 꼭 필요한 비품이라도 가리키는 양 발음한다. \"일 포 망제. 그게 프랑스 속담이거든. 그리고 망제하는 건 프랑스 사람한테나 나한테나 똑같이 꼭 필요한 일이야. 어쩌면 더하지.\"\n* 일 포 망제: 프랑스어 \"Il faut manger(먹어야 한다)\"를 멋대로 영어식으로 발음한 것. 'manger'를 영국 마구간의 여물통(manger)으로 빗댄 말장난이다.\n\n스몰위드 씨는 단연 \"훨씬 더하다\"는 의견이다.\n\n\"누가 나한테 이런 말을 했더라면,\" 조블링이 잇는다. \"자네하고 내가 링컨셔로 신나게 놀러 가서, 마차를 몰고 캐슬 월드에 있는 그 저택을 보러 갔을 때만 해도 말이야, 구피―\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 바로잡는다―체스니 월드.\n\n\"체스니 월드. (그렇게 고쳐준 내 명예로운 벗에게 감사하네.) 그때 누가 나한테, 내가 지금 처한 이 형편처럼 말 그대로 쪼들리게 되리라고 말했더라면, 나는―글쎄, 나는 그자한테 달려들었을 거야.\" 조블링 씨가 자포자기한 듯 체념한 기색으로 럼주에 물을 탄 것을 조금 들이켜며 말한다. \"그자 머리통을 향해 한 방 날렸을 거라고.\"\n\n\"그래도 그때 자넨 잘못된 쪽에 있었어, 토니.\" 구피 씨가 타이른다. \"마차에서 내내 그 얘기만 했잖나.\"\n\n\"구피,\" 조블링 씨가 말한다. \"부정하진 않겠네. 나는 잘못된 쪽에 있었어. 하지만 일이 잘 풀리리라고 믿었거든.\"\n\n만사가 절로 잘 풀리리라는 그 흔해 빠진 믿음이라니! 두들겨서 풀리거나 애써서 풀리는 게 아니라, 저절로 \"풀려\" 온다는 믿음이라니! 마치 미치광이가 세상이 저절로 \"삼각형이 되어\" 오리라고 믿기라도 하듯이!\n\n\"일이 잘 풀려서 다 맞아떨어지리라고 자신만만하게 기대했지.\" 조블링 씨가 표현이, 어쩌면 의미까지도 다소 흐릿하게 말한다. \"그런데 실망하고 말았어. 영영 풀리지 않았거든. 그러다 채권자들이 사무실에서 소란을 피우고, 사무실이 거래하던 사람들이 빌린 돈이니 하는 지저분한 자질구레한 일로 항의해대기에 이르자, 뭐, 그 인연도 그걸로 끝장이었지. 게다가 새로운 직업상의 인연도 마찬가지야. 내일 누가 신원보증을 부탁해온다 해도 그 일이 거론될 테고, 그러면 난 끝장이거든. 그러니 사내가 뭘 어쩌겠나? 그동안 채소밭 근처에 숨어서 싸게 먹고 살았지만, 돈이 한 푼도 없는데 싸게 사는 게 무슨 소용이야? 차라리 비싸게 사는 거나 마찬가지지.\"\n\n\"그게 낫지.\" 스몰위드 씨가 생각한다.\n\n\"그렇고말고. 그게 유행하는 방식이거든. 그런데 유행하고 구레나룻이 내 약점이라네. 누가 알든 난 상관 안 해.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다. \"그것들은 대단한 약점이야―제기랄, 선생, 정말 대단하다고. 자,\" 조블링 씨가 럼주에 물을 탄 것을 도전적으로 한 번 더 들이켜고 나서 잇는다. \"묻겠는데, 사내가 입대 말고 뭘 할 수 있겠나?\"\n\n구피 씨가 대화에 더 깊이 끼어들어, 자기 생각에 사내가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 밝힌다. 그의 태도는, 마음에 깃든 애틋한 슬픔의 희생양이 되었다는 것 말고는 인생에서 달리 자기 발목을 잡힐 짓을 한 적 없는 사내의, 진중하고 인상적인 태도다.\n\n\"조블링,\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"나하고 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드는―\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 겸손하게 한마디 한다. \"두 분 다 신사시죠!\" 그러고는 마신다.\n\n\"―자네가 그렇게 된 뒤로 이 문제를 두고 한두 번 짧게 이야기를 나눴거든―\"\n\n\"잘렸다고 말해!\" 조블링 씨가 쓰라리게 외친다. \"그렇게 말하라고, 구피. 그 뜻이잖나.\"\n\n\"아니, 아냐! 법학원을 떠났다고요.\" 스몰위드 씨가 점잖게 거든다.\n\n\"자네가 법학원을 떠난 뒤로, 조블링,\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"나는 최근에 한번 제안해볼까 생각해둔 계획을 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드에게 말해뒀네. 문방구상 스내그스비를 알지?\"\n\n\"그런 문방구상이 있다는 건 알지.\" 조블링 씨가 대답한다. \"우리 거래처는 아니었어. 나랑 안면은 없네.\"\n\n\"우리 거래처가 맞고, 나는 그 사람하고 안면이 있다네, 조블링.\" 구피 씨가 받아친다. \"그런데 말일세! 어쩌다 우연한 일들로 그 사람 사생활에까지 드나드는 손님이 되는 바람에, 최근에 더 잘 알게 됐거든. 그 정황은 굳이 늘어놓을 필요 없어. 어쩌면 내 인생에 그림자를 드리웠을지도―아닐지도―모르는 어떤 문제와 관련이 있을지도―아닐지도―모르지만 말일세.\"\n\n자랑스러운 비애에 잠겨 자기 절친한 벗들을 이 화제로 끌어들였다가, 그들이 그 화제를 건드리는 순간 인간의 마음에 깃든 현이 어쩌고 하는 신랄한 준엄함으로 돌변해 그들에게 들이대는 것이 구피 씨의 곤혹스러운 버릇인지라, 조블링 씨도 스몰위드 씨도 입을 다문 채 그 함정을 피한다.\n\n\"그런 일들이 있을 수도,\" 구피 씨가 되뇐다. \"없을 수도 있지. 그건 이 일과 아무 상관 없어. 스내그스비 씨도 그 부인도 기꺼이 나를 도와주려 한다는 점, 그리고 스내그스비가 바쁜 철이면 내줄 필사 일거리가 꽤 많다는 점만 말해두면 충분해. 토킹혼 일을 죄다 맡고 있는 데다, 그 밖에도 장사가 아주 잘되거든. 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드를 증인석에 세우면, 이걸 입증해줄 수 있을 거야. 안 그래?\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 어서 선서하고 싶어 안달하는 듯 보인다.\n\n\"자, 배심원 여러분,\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"―아니, 그러니까, 조블링―자넨 이게 변변찮은 밥벌이 전망이라고 할지도 모르지. 인정해. 하지만 아무것도 안 하는 것보단 낫고, 입대보다도 낫네. 자넨 시간이 필요해. 이 최근의 일들이 가라앉을 시간이 있어야 한단 말이야. 스내그스비를 위해 필사하는 것보다 훨씬 못한 조건으로도 그 시간을 버텨낼 수 있을 텐데.\"\n\n조블링 씨가 막 말을 끊으려 하는데, 영리한 스몰위드가 마른기침과 함께 한마디로 그를 제지한다. \"흠! 셰익스피어!\"\n\n\"이 문제엔 두 가지 갈래가 있어, 조블링.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"그게 첫째지. 둘째로 넘어가겠네. 골목 건너편의 챈슬러, 크룩을 알지. 자, 조블링,\" 구피 씨가 격려하는 반대신문 어조로 말한다. \"골목 건너편의 챈슬러, 크룩을 알 텐데?\"\n\n\"얼굴은 알지.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"얼굴은 안다. 아주 좋아. 그럼 꼬마 플라이트도 알지?\"\n\n\"그 여자야 다들 알지.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"다들 안다. 아주 좋아. 그런데 요즘 내 임무 중 하나가 플라이트에게 매주 일정액의 수당을 주는 일이거든. 거기서 매주 집세를 떼는데, 그 집세는(내가 받은 지시에 따라) 어김없이 그녀가 보는 앞에서 크룩 본인에게 직접 치르지. 그러다 보니 크룩과 왕래하게 됐고, 그 집과 그자의 습성도 알게 됐어. 그가 세놓을 방이 하나 있다는 걸 알아. 거기서라면 자넨 아주 싼 값에, 무슨 이름을 대든, 백 마일은 떨어진 데 있는 것처럼 조용히 지낼 수 있어. 그자는 아무것도 캐묻지 않을 테고, 내 말 한마디면 자넬 세입자로 받아줄 거야―자네가 원하면 시계가 종을 치기도 전에 말이지. 그리고 한 가지 더 말해두겠는데, 조블링,\" 구피 씨가 갑자기 목소리를 낮추고 다시 허물없는 투가 되어 말한다. \"그자는 별난 늙은이야―늘 어수선한 종이 더미를 뒤지고, 읽고 쓰는 법을 혼자 익히겠다고 죽어라 파고드는데, 내가 보기엔 영 진척이 없거든. 정말이지 별난 늙은이야, 선생. 사내라면 그자를 좀 파고들어볼 만하지 않을까 싶기도 하다고.\"\n\n\"설마 자네 말은―\" 조블링 씨가 운을 뗀다.\n\n\"내 말은,\" 구피 씨가 어울리는 겸손으로 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답한다. \"그자를 도무지 알아낼 수가 없다는 거야. 내가 그자를 알아낼 수 없다고 말하는 걸 들었는지 못 들었는지, 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드에게 물어보겠네.\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 간결한 증언을 내놓는다. \"몇 번!\"\n\n\"나도 이 직업을 좀 겪었고 인생도 좀 겪어봤어, 토니.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"그래서 사람을 어느 정도 못 알아보는 일이 좀처럼 없거든. 그런데 이런 늙은 능구렁이는, 이렇게 속이 깊고, 음흉하고, 비밀스러운(하긴 그자가 멀쩡하게 깨어 있던 적이 있을 것 같진 않지만) 작자는, 여태 만나본 적이 없어. 자, 그자는 분명 어마어마하게 늙었어, 알겠나. 곁에 사람 하나 없고, 엄청난 부자라는 소문도 있지. 그자가 밀수꾼인지, 장물아비인지, 무허가 전당포 주인인지, 고리대금업자인지―시기마다 다 그럴싸하다 싶었네만―여하튼 그자에 관한 지식을 쌓아두면 자네한테 득이 될지도 몰라. 다른 게 다 들어맞는 마당에, 한번 해보지 않을 이유가 없잖아.\"\n\n조블링 씨, 구피 씨, 스몰위드 씨가 모두 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 턱을 받친 채 천장을 올려다본다. 잠시 후 그들은 모두 천천히 술을 마시고, 등을 뒤로 기대고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣고는 서로를 바라본다.\n\n\"한때 가졌던 그 기력이 나한테 남아 있다면 말이야, 토니!\" 구피 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말한다. \"하지만 인간의 마음에는 건드려선 안 될 현이라는 게―\"\n\n그 처량한 심정의 나머지를 럼주에 물 탄 것으로 토로하고 나서, 구피 씨는 이 모험을 토니 조블링에게 넘기는 것으로 결말을 짓는다. 그러면서 휴정기 동안 일이 한가할 때는 자기 지갑이 \"삼 파운드든 사 파운드든, 심지어 오 파운드까지\" 그가 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 열려 있으리라고 알린다. \"그도 그럴 것이, 윌리엄 구피가 제 벗에게 등을 돌렸다는 말이,\" 구피 씨가 힘주어 덧붙인다. \"결코 나와선 안 되니까!\"\n\n제안의 뒷부분이 어찌나 곧장 핵심을 찌르는지, 조블링 씨가 감격에 겨워 말한다. \"구피, 이 멋진 친구야, 주먹 한번 내밀어보게!\" 구피 씨가 주먹을 내밀며 말한다. \"조블링, 이 친구야, 여기 있네!\" 조블링 씨가 화답한다. \"구피, 우리가 이제 알고 지낸 지도 여러 해네!\" 구피 씨가 대꾸한다. \"조블링, 그렇지.\"\n\n그러고 나서 둘은 악수하고, 조블링 씨가 감개무량한 투로 덧붙인다. \"고맙네, 구피, 옛정을 봐서 한 잔 더 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\"\n\n\"크룩의 지난번 하숙인은 거기서 죽었지.\" 구피 씨가 지나가는 말처럼 일러둔다.\n\n\"정말 그랬다고!\" 조블링 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"평결이 났어. 사고사라고. 자넨 그런 거 안 꺼리지?\"\n\n\"안 꺼려.\" 조블링 씨가 말한다. \"꺼리진 않네만, 그자도 다른 데 가서 죽었으면 좋았잖아. 굳이 내 방에 와서 죽어야 한다니 지독하게 께름칙하군!\" 조블링 씨는 이 무례를 자못 못마땅해하며, 여러 번 그 얘기로 되돌아가 이런 말을 던진다. \"죽을 데야 차고 넘칠 텐데, 내 참!\"이라느니, \"내가 자기 방에서 죽으면 그자도 안 좋아했을걸, 장담하지!\"라느니 하고.\n\n그래도 약정이 사실상 맺어진 터라, 구피 씨는 미덥은 스몰위드를 보내 크룩 씨가 집에 있는지 알아보자고 제안한다. 그렇다면 지체 없이 흥정을 마무리 지을 수 있을 테니까. 조블링 씨가 찬성하자, 스몰위드는 키 큰 모자 아래에 자기를 욱여넣고 구피식으로 그것을 식당 밖으로 모셔 나간다. 그는 곧 크룩 씨가 집에 있다는 소식과 함께, 가게 문 너머로 안쪽 방에 앉아 \"세상모르고\" 자고 있는 그를 봤다고 알리며 돌아온다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 셈을 치르지.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"가서 그 사람을 만나보세. 스몰, 얼마지?\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 속눈썹을 한 번 까딱여 여종업원을 불러 세우고는 즉시 이렇게 대답한다. \"송아지 고기와 햄 넷이 삼 실링, 감자 넷이 삼 실링 사 펜스, 여름배추 하나가 삼 실링 육 펜스, 골수 푸딩 셋이 사 실링 육 펜스, 빵 여섯이 오 실링, 체셔 치즈 셋이 오 실링 삼 펜스, 반반 섞은 맥주 반 파인트 넷이 육 실링 삼 펜스, 작은 럼주 넷이 팔 실링 삼 펜스, 폴리 셋이 팔 실링 육 펜스. 반 소버린에서 팔 실링 육 펜스 제하면, 폴리, 일 실링 육 펜스 거슬러주면 돼!\"\n\n이 어마어마한 계산에도 조금도 들뜨지 않은 채, 스몰위드는 쌀쌀맞게 고개를 끄덕여 벗들을 배웅하고는, 기회가 닿는 대로 폴리를 흠모하며 지켜보고 일간지를 읽으려고 뒤에 남는다. 모자를 벗고 나니 신문이 그의 몸집에 비해 어찌나 큰지, 그가 타임스를 치켜들고 지면을 훑을 때면 마치 밤을 맞아 잠자리에 들어 이불 밑으로 사라져버린 사람처럼 보인다.\n\n구피 씨와 조블링 씨는 넝마와 병을 파는 가게로 향하는데, 거기서 그들은 크룩이 여전히 세상모르고 자고 있는 것을 발견한다. 다시 말해, 턱을 가슴에 묻은 채 드르렁드르렁 코를 골며, 바깥소리는 물론 살살 흔들어도 도무지 깨어나지 않는 상태로 말이다. 그의 곁 탁자 위에는 늘 그렇듯 잡동사니 틈에 빈 진 병과 잔 하나가 놓여 있다. 건강에 해로운 공기가 그 술 냄새에 어찌나 절어 있는지, 선반 위 고양이의 초록 눈마저 떴다 감았다 하며 방문객들을 향해 번뜩일 때 술에 취한 듯 보인다.\n\n\"여보쇼, 정신 차려요!\" 구피 씨가 늘어진 노인의 몸을 다시 한번 흔들며 말한다. \"크룩 씨! 이봐요, 선생!\"\n\n그러나 술기운이 안에서 후끈 타오르는 헌 옷 뭉치를 깨우는 편이 차라리 쉬워 보인다. \"술이랑 잠 사이에서 이렇게 곯아떨어지는 작자를 본 적 있나?\" 구피 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"이게 이 사람 평소 잠버릇이라면,\" 조블링이 다소 겁먹은 채 대답한다. \"조만간 어느 날엔 영영 안 깨고 오래도록 잘 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"매번 한숨 자는 것보다는 발작에 가까워.\" 구피 씨가 그를 다시 흔들며 말한다. \"이봐요, 각하! 이러다 쉰 번은 털리겠소! 눈 좀 떠봐요!\"\n\n한참 애를 쓴 끝에 그가 눈을 뜨지만, 방문객들도 다른 어떤 것도 보는 기색이 없다. 그는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽에 포개고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 메마른 입술을 여러 번 다물었다 벌렸다 하면서도, 누가 봐도 아까나 다름없이 의식이 없어 보인다.\n\n\"어쨌든 살아는 있군.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"어떻게 지내십니까, 우리 대법관 각하. 작은 볼일이 있어 제 친구를 하나 데려왔습니다, 선생.\"\n\n노인은 여전히 앉은 채, 아무 의식도 없이 마른 입술을 자주 쩝쩝거린다. 몇 분이 지나자 그는 일어서보려 시도한다. 그들이 부축해 일으키니, 그는 벽에 휘청 기대며 그들을 멀거니 바라본다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 크룩 씨?\" 구피 씨가 다소 당황하며 말한다. \"안녕하세요, 선생? 안색이 아주 좋아 보이십니다, 크룩 씨. 그럭저럭 평안하시기를 바랍니다만?\"\n\n노인은 구피 씨를, 아니 허공을 향해 목적 없는 주먹을 휘두르려다, 맥없이 제 몸을 빙 돌려 얼굴부터 벽에 부딪친다. 그렇게 벽에 쌓이듯 기댄 채로 한두 분 가만히 있다가, 비틀비틀 가게를 가로질러 정문으로 걸어 내려간다. 바깥 공기, 마당의 움직임, 흐르는 시간, 아니면 이것들이 한데 어우러진 덕에 그가 정신을 차린다. 그는 머리에 모피 모자를 고쳐 쓰고 그들을 날카롭게 쳐다보며 제법 차분하게 돌아온다.\n\n\"잘들 오셨소, 신사 양반들. 깜빡 졸았구먼. 어허! 가끔 어찌나 깨어나기 힘든지.\"\n\n\"정말 그러신가 봅니다, 선생.\" 구피 씨가 응한다.\n\n\"뭐요? 댁들이 날 깨우려고 애를 썼다, 그거요?\" 의심 많은 크룩이 말한다.\n\n\"조금요.\" 구피 씨가 둘러댄다.\n\n노인의 눈이 빈 병에 가닿자, 그는 그것을 집어 들어 살펴보고는 천천히 거꾸로 기울인다.\n\n\"아니!\" 그가 옛이야기에 나오는 도깨비처럼 외친다. \"누가 여기 손을 댔구먼!\"\n\n\"맹세컨대 저희가 왔을 때 이미 그랬습니다.\" 구피 씨가 말한다. \"제가 채워다 드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 물론 좋고말고!\" 크룩이 크게 기뻐하며 외친다. \"물론이지! 사양할 거 없네! 옆집에서 채워 와―솔스 암스 말이야―대법관표 십사 펜스짜리로. 어이쿠, 거긴 날 다 안다고!\"\n\n그가 어찌나 빈 병을 구피 씨에게 떠안기는지, 그 신사는 친구에게 고개를 한 번 끄덕여 보이고는 그 임무를 받아들여, 서둘러 나갔다가 병을 채워서 서둘러 다시 들어온다. 노인은 그것을 마치 사랑하는 손주라도 되는 양 두 팔로 받아 안고 다정하게 토닥인다.\n\n\"그런데 말이야,\" 그가 한 모금 맛을 보고 나서, 눈을 잔뜩 찡그린 채 속삭인다. \"이건 대법관표 십사 펜스짜리가 아니구먼. 십팔 펜스짜리야!\"\n\n\"그게 더 입에 맞으실 것 같아서요.\" 구피 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"댁은 귀족이구먼, 선생.\" 크룩이 한 모금 더 맛보며 대답하자, 그의 뜨거운 입김이 불꽃처럼 그들 쪽으로 끼쳐오는 듯하다. \"댁은 이 땅의 남작이야.\"\n\n이 상서로운 순간을 틈타, 구피 씨는 즉석에서 위블 씨라는 이름으로 친구를 소개하고 방문 목적을 밝힌다. 크룩은 병을 겨드랑이에 낀 채(그는 취하는 정도든 멀쩡한 정도든 결코 일정한 선을 넘지 못한다) 시간을 들여 세입자가 될 사람을 살펴보고는 그가 마음에 드는 모양이다. \"방을 보고 싶나, 젊은이?\" 그가 말한다. \"아! 좋은 방이지! 회칠도 했어. 물비누에 소다까지 써서 말끔히 닦아냈고. 어허! 집세의 곱절은 값어치를 하지. 게다가 필요할 때 말동무가 되어줄 나도 있고, 쥐를 쫓아줄 저런 고양이도 있는데 말이야.\"\n\n이런 식으로 방을 추켜세우며 노인은 그들을 위층으로 데려가는데, 과연 거기서 그들은 방이 예전보다 깨끗해진 것은 물론, 그가 무궁무진한 창고에서 캐낸 낡은 가구 몇 점이 들어차 있는 것을 발견한다. 조건은 수월하게 합의된다―대법관으로서도 켄지와 카보이, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스, 그 밖에 제 직업적 고려를 요하는 숱한 유명한 청구들과 얽힌 구피 씨에게 모질게 굴 수는 없는 노릇이니―그리하여 위블 씨가 이튿날 입주하기로 정해진다. 그러고 나서 위블 씨와 구피 씨는 커시터 스트리트의 쿡스 코트로 향하는데, 거기서 전자가 스내그스비 씨에게 직접 소개되고, (더 중요하게는) 스내그스비 부인의 찬동과 호의를 확보한다. 이어 그들은 이 일을 위해 사무실에서 키 큰 모자를 쓴 채 기다리던 거물 스몰위드에게 진행 상황을 보고하고 헤어진다. 구피 씨는 이 작은 잔치를 연극 한 편 한턱냄으로써 마무리하고 싶지만 인간의 마음에 깃든 현이 그것을 공허한 흉내로 만들어버릴 것이라고 설명하면서.\n\n이튿날 저물녘 어스름 속에, 위블 씨는 짐이라곤 거의 거추장스러울 것 없이 단출하게 크룩의 집에 점잖이 모습을 드러내고는 새 하숙에 자리를 잡는데, 거기서는 덧창에 난 두 개의 눈이 마치 경이로 가득 찬 양 잠든 그를 빤히 쳐다본다. 그 이튿날, 손재주 좋고 변변찮은 젊은이인 위블 씨는 플라이트 양에게 바늘과 실을, 집주인에게 망치를 빌려와 일에 착수한다. 창 커튼인 척하는 것을 궁리해 만들고, 선반인 척하는 것을 뚝딱 두들겨 세우고, 찻잔 두 개와 우유 단지, 자질구레한 그릇붙이를 한 푼어치 작은 고리에 걸어두는 품이, 난파한 선원이 형편껏 살림을 꾸리는 모양새다.\n\n그러나 위블 씨가 몇 안 되는 소유물 가운데 그 무엇보다 애지중지하는 것은(옅은 빛깔의 구레나룻 다음으로, 그것에 대해서만큼은 사내의 가슴에 오직 구레나룻만이 불러일으킬 수 있는 애착을 품고 있다) 진정 국민적인 작품인 『앨비언의 여신들, 또는 영국 미인 은하 화랑』에서 골라 모은 동판화 인쇄물이다. 자본과 결합한 기예가 빚어낼 수 있는 온갖 갖가지 알랑거리는 미소를 띤, 작위와 유행을 갖춘 귀부인들을 담아낸 작품이다. 채소밭에 칩거하는 동안 종이 상자에 누추하게 갇혀 있던 이 으리으리한 초상화들로 그는 제 방을 장식한다. 그리고 영국 미인 은하 화랑은 온갖 갖가지 화려한 드레스를 걸치고, 온갖 갖가지 악기를 연주하며, 온갖 갖가지 개를 쓰다듬고, 온갖 갖가지 경치에 추파를 던지며, 온갖 갖가지 화분과 난간을 배경으로 두르고 있는 터라, 그 효과는 자못 위풍당당하다.\n\n그러나 유행은 토니 조블링이 그러했듯 위블 씨의 약점이다. 저녁이면 솔스 암스에서 어제 신문을 빌려와, 유행의 하늘을 사방으로 가로지르며 쏟아지는 찬란하고 고귀한 유성들에 관해 읽는 것이 그에게는 이루 말할 수 없는 위안이다. 어느 찬란하고 고귀한 사교계의 어느 일원이 어제 그 사교계에 합류하는 찬란하고 고귀한 위업을 이뤘는지, 또 내일 그것을 떠나는 그에 못지않게 찬란하고 고귀한 위업을 궁리하고 있는지를 아는 것은 그에게 기쁨의 전율을 안긴다. 영국 미인 은하 화랑이 지금 무엇을 하고 있고 무엇을 하려는지, 어떤 은하의 혼사가 도마에 올라 있는지, 어떤 은하의 풍문이 떠도는지를 전해 듣는 것은 인류의 더없이 영광스러운 운명과 친해지는 일이다. 위블 씨는 이 소식에서 거기 얽힌 은하의 초상화로 눈길을 되돌리는데, 마치 그 실물들을 알고 있고 또 그들에게 알려져 있기라도 한 듯하다.\n\n그 밖에 그는 조용한 하숙인으로, 앞서 말했듯 손쉬운 임시변통과 궁리가 넘쳐, 목수 일은 물론 제 손으로 요리하고 청소까지 해낼 줄 알며, 마당에 저녁 어스름이 내려앉은 뒤로는 사교적인 성향을 드러낸다. 그런 때면, 구피 씨가 찾아오거나 검은 모자 속에 꺼져버린 그의 작은 분신 같은 불빛이 찾아오지 않을 적이면, 그는 칙칙한 제 방에서―잉크가 비처럼 흩뿌려진 전나무 책상의 황무지를 물려받은 그 방에서―나와 크룩과 이야기를 나누거나, 마당 사람들이 칭찬조로 부르는 말마따나 대화에 끌리는 누구하고든 \"아주 허물없이\" 군다. 그런 까닭에 마당을 주름잡는 파이퍼 부인은 퍼킨스 부인에게 두 가지 소견을 내놓지 않고는 못 배긴다. 첫째, 만약 자기네 조니가 구레나룻을 기른다면 꼭 저 젊은이 것하고 똑같았으면 좋겠다는 것. 둘째, \"내 말 명심해요, 퍼킨스 부인, 그리고 놀라지 마요, 어이구 맙소사, 저 젊은이가 끝내 크룩 영감의 재산을 차지하게 되더라도 말이에요!\"\n\n생김새도 별로요 풍기는 냄새도 별로인 동네이긴 하지만, 그 가운데 비탈진 언덕 하나가 마운트 플레전트라는 이름을 달고 있는 그곳에서, 요정 같은 스몰위드 — 세례명은 바살러뮤요 집안 화롯가에서는 바트라 불리는 — 는 사무소와 그 잡다한 일거리가 침범할 수 없는 자기만의 한정된 시간을 보낸다. 그는 좁다란 골목길에 사는데, 늘 적막하고 그늘지고 쓸쓸하며, 무덤처럼 사방이 벽돌로 빈틈없이 둘러싸여 있다. 그래도 그곳엔 옛 숲의 고목 그루터기 하나가 아직 남아 있으니, 그 풍취가 신선하고 자연스럽기로는 스몰위드 일가에서 풍기는 젊음의 맛과 엇비슷하다.\n\n스몰위드 집안에는 여러 대에 걸쳐 아이라곤 단 하나뿐이었다. 조그만 늙은이 남녀는 있어도 아이는 없었으니, 스몰위드 씨의 할머니 — 지금도 살아 있는 — 가 정신이 흐려져서 (난생처음) 어린아이 같은 상태로 떨어지기 전까지는 그랬다. 관찰력도 기억력도 이해력도 흥미도 전혀 없는 데다 불 앞에서, 그것도 불 속으로 졸아떨어지는 영원한 성향이라는, 그런 유아적 우아함을 갖춘 덕분에 스몰위드 씨의 할머니는 의심할 바 없이 집안을 환하게 밝혀놓았다.\n\n스몰위드 씨의 할아버지 역시 이 일행에 든다. 그는 하반신은 영 가망 없는 상태이고 상반신도 거의 그러하지만, 정신만은 멀쩡하다. 그 정신은 예나 지금이나 똑같이 산수의 사칙연산과 가장 딱딱한 사실 몇 가지를 그러쥐고 있다. 이상이니 경외니 경탄이니 하는 그 밖의 골상학적 자질로 말하자면, 옛날에 비해 조금도 못해진 데가 없다. 스몰위드 씨의 할아버지가 평생 머릿속에 챙겨 넣은 것은 무엇이든 처음에도 애벌레요 끝에도 애벌레다. 그는 평생토록 나비 한 마리 길러낸 적이 없다.\n\n마운트 플레전트 동네에 사는 이 유쾌한 할아버지의 아버지는, 가죽 같은 살갗에 두 다리로 서서 돈을 긁어모으는 거미 종류였으니, 방심한 파리를 잡으려 거미줄을 치고는 먹이가 걸려들 때까지 구멍 속에 틀어박혀 있었다. 이 늙은 이교도가 섬긴 신의 이름은 복리(複利)였다. 그는 복리를 위해 살았고, 복리와 혼인했으며, 복리 때문에 죽었다. 손실이란 손실은 죄다 상대편이 떠안도록 꾸민, 정직하기 짝이 없는 어떤 자그마한 사업에서 큰 손실을 보게 되자, 그는 무언가가 — 자기 존재에 꼭 필요한 무언가가, 그러니 그게 심장일 리는 없는 무언가가 — 부러져버려 그렇게 생을 마감했다. 그는 품행이 좋지 못한 데다, 자선 학교에서 문답 형식으로 저 옛 백성 아모리 족속과 헷 족속에 관한 과정을 통째로 배우며 자랐던 탓에, 흔히 교육이 실패한 본보기로 인용되곤 했다.\n\n그의 정신은 아들에게로 스며들어 빛을 냈으니, 그는 늘 아들에게 인생 일찍부터 \"세상에 나가라\"고 설교했고 열두 살 적에 약삭빠른 어느 필경사 사무소의 서기로 박아 넣었다. 거기서 그 젊은 양반은 야위고 근심에 찬 성품의 머리를 갈고닦았으며, 집안 내림의 재능을 키워 점차 어음 할인업으로 신분을 끌어올렸다. 아버지가 그랬듯 인생 일찍 세상에 나가 늦게 혼인한 그 역시 야위고 근심에 찬 마음을 가진 아들을 낳았고, 그 아들 또한 인생 일찍 세상에 나가 늦게 혼인하여 쌍둥이 바살러뮤와 주디스 스몰위드의 아비가 되었다. 이 집안 나무가 더디게 자라는 동안 줄곧, 늘 일찍 나가고 늦게 혼인한 스몰위드 가문은 실용적인 기질로 제 몸을 단단히 다져왔으니, 온갖 오락을 내던지고, 온갖 이야기책과 동화와 소설과 우화를 백안시했으며, 어떤 종류의 경박함도 죄다 추방해버렸다. 그리하여, 이 집안에 아이라곤 태어난 적이 없으며 이 집안이 빚어낸 완성된 꼬마 남녀들이 하나같이 마음에 무언가 우울한 걸 얹은 늙은 원숭이를 닮았더라는, 그 흐뭇한 사실이 생겨난 것이다.\n\n지금 이 시각, 길바닥보다 몇 자쯤 아래에 있는 어둑한 작은 거실 — 험상궂고 딱딱하고 볼품없는 거실, 더없이 거친 천으로 만든 식탁보와 더없이 딱딱한 양철 찻쟁반 말고는 아무 장식도 없는, 그 장식적 성격으로 보자면 스몰위드 할아버지의 정신을 우의적으로 표현하기에 손색이 없는 그 거실 — 에서, 벽난로 양쪽에 하나씩 놓인 검은 말총 안락의자에 늙어빠진 스몰위드 노부부가 앉아 장밋빛 시간을 흘려보낸다. 난로 위에는 냄비와 주전자를 얹는 삼발이가 한 쌍 놓여 있는데 그걸 지켜보는 게 스몰위드 할아버지의 일상 소일거리요, 그 사이로 굴뚝 선반에서 튀어나온 것은 고기 굽는 일종의 놋쇠 교수대이니 그것이 작동할 때면 그가 역시 감독한다. 노쇠하신 스몰위드 씨의 자리 밑, 막대기 같은 그의 두 다리가 지키는 곳에는 의자 속 서랍이 하나 있는데, 거기엔 엄청난 액수의 재산이 들었다는 소문이 돈다. 그의 곁에는 여분의 방석이 하나 있어 늘 채워져 있으니, 존경할 만한 나이를 함께 먹은 동반자가 돈 이야기 — 그가 유난히 민감해하는 화제 — 를 들먹일 때마다 던질 무언가가 있어야 하기 때문이다.\n\n\"바트는 어디 있느냐?\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 바트의 쌍둥이 누이 주디에게 묻는다.\n\n\"아직 안 들어왔어요.\" 주디가 말한다.\n\n\"걔 차 마실 시간 아니냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요.\"\n\"그럼 얼마나 남았다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"십 분이요.\"\n\n\"응?\"\n\n\"십 분이라고요.\" (주디가 큰 소리로 말한다.)\n\n\"호!\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다. \"십 분이라.\"\n\n삼발이를 향해 우물거리며 고개를 내젓던 스몰위드 할머니가 숫자 이야기를 듣고는 그걸 돈에 갖다 붙여, 깃털이라곤 하나도 없는 끔찍한 늙은 앵무새처럼 꽥꽥거린다. \"십 파운드짜리 지폐 열 장!\"\n\n스몰위드 할아버지가 즉각 그녀에게 방석을 내던진다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 조용히 좀 못 해!\" 그 착하신 노인이 말한다.\n\n이 투척 행위의 효과는 두 갈래다. 그것은 스몰위드 부인의 머리를 안락의자 옆구리에 처박아 접어버려, 손녀가 끌어내고 보면 그녀의 모자가 영 꼴사납게 흐트러져 있게 만들 뿐 아니라, 거기 드는 힘이 도로 스몰위드 씨 자신에게 튕겨와 그를 부러진 꼭두각시처럼 제 안락의자에 처박는다. 이런 때면 그 훌륭한 노신사는 검은 두건을 꼭대기에 얹은 한낱 옷보따리에 지나지 않으니, 손녀의 손으로 두 차례 시술 — 큰 병처럼 흔들리고, 큰 베개처럼 쿡쿡 찔리고 두들겨 맞는 — 을 거치기 전에는 그다지 생기 있는 모습을 띠지 못한다. 이런 수단으로 목 비슷한 게 그에게서 얼마쯤 돋아나면, 그와 그의 인생 황혼을 함께하는 동반자는 다시 두 안락의자에 마주 앉으니, 마치 검은 하사관 죽음이 초소에 세워놓고 오래도록 잊어버린 두 보초병 같다.\n\n쌍둥이 주디는 이 패거리와 어울리기에 알맞은 동무다. 그녀가 젊은 스몰위드 씨의 누이라는 건 도무지 의심할 여지가 없으니, 둘을 한데 반죽해 하나로 빚어도 평균 체격의 젊은이 하나가 채 되지 않을 지경이요, 게다가 그녀는 앞서 말한 원숭이 족속을 닮은 집안 내림을 어찌나 잘 본보이는지, 번쩍이는 옷과 모자를 차려입혀 손풍금 꼭대기의 평평한 자리를 거닐게 해도 별난 표본이라고 입길에 오르내릴 일이 없을 정도다. 그러나 지금 형편에서는 갈색 천으로 지은 수수하고 빈약한 가운을 걸치고 있다.\n\n주디는 인형을 가져본 적도, 신데렐라 이야기를 들어본 적도, 어떤 놀이를 해본 적도 없다. 열 살 무렵 한두 번 아이들 무리에 끼어든 적은 있으나, 아이들은 주디와 어울리지 못했고 주디도 아이들과 어울리지 못했다. 그녀는 다른 종류의 짐승 같았고, 양쪽 모두 본능적인 거부감을 느꼈다. 주디가 웃을 줄이나 아는지는 자못 의심스럽다. 그녀는 그런 행위를 워낙 본 적이 드물어서, 오히려 모를 가능성이 농후하다. 젊은이다운 웃음 비슷한 것이라면 분명 머릿속에 떠올릴 수조차 없을 터이다. 만일 한번 웃어보려 한다면, 제 이가 거치적거리는 걸 발견하리니, 그 얼굴 동작을, 다른 모든 표정을 무의식중에 본떠왔듯이, 추레한 노년이라는 제 본보기를 따라 빚어낼 테니 말이다. 그런 게 주디다.\n\n그리고 그녀의 쌍둥이 오라비는 죽었다 깨도 팽이 하나 감을 줄 모른다. 그는 거인 잡는 잭이나 뱃사람 신드바드에 대해, 별자리 속 사람들만큼이나 아는 게 없다. 그가 개구리뜀이나 크리켓을 할 줄 아는 정도는, 제 몸이 크리켓 벌레나 개구리로 둔갑할 줄 아는 정도와 매한가지다. 그래도 누이보다는 한결 형편이 나으니, 사실밖에 모르는 그의 좁은 세계에 거피 씨의 식견이 미치는 그런 한층 너른 영역으로 통하는 틈새 하나가 동터왔기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 그 빛나는 마법사를 흠모하고 본받으려 든다.\n\n주디는 양철 찻쟁반 하나를 징을 치듯 쨍그랑 요란하게 식탁에 내려놓고 찻잔과 받침을 늘어놓는다. 빵은 무쇠 바구니에 담고, 버터는 (그나마 얼마 안 되는) 작은 백랍 접시에 담아 올린다. 스몰위드 할아버지는 차가 따라지는 모양을 매섭게 살피더니 주디에게 그 계집애는 어디 있느냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"찰리 말이에요?\" 주디가 말한다.\n\n\"응?\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 되묻는다.\n\n\"찰리 말이냐고요?\"\n\n이 말이 스몰위드 할머니의 어떤 태엽을 건드리니, 여느 때처럼 삼발이를 향해 킬킬거리던 그녀가 외친다. \"물 건너! 물 건너 찰리, 물 건너 찰리, 물 건너 찰리에게로, 물 건너 찰리, 물 건너 찰리에게로!\" 그러고는 그 일에 자못 기운을 낸다. 할아버지가 방석을 쳐다보지만 아까 쓴 기력을 아직 추스르지 못한 터다.\n\n\"하!\" 잠잠해지자 그가 말한다. \"그게 그 애 이름이라면 말이지. 걘 너무 많이 처먹어. 식비를 따로 쳐주는 게 낫겠어.\"\n\n주디는 오라비와 똑같이 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 고개를 젓고는, 입으로 소리는 내지 않은 채 아니라는 모양으로 입술을 오므린다.\n\n\"안 돼?\" 노인이 되묻는다. \"어째서?\"\n\n\"걘 하루에 육 펜스를 달랄 텐데, 우리는 그보다 적게 들이고도 해낼 수 있거든요.\" 주디가 말한다.\n\n\"틀림없냐?\"\n\n주디는 더없이 깊은 뜻을 담아 고개를 끄덕여 답하고는, 낭비될세라 갖은 조심을 다해 빵덩이에 버터를 긁어 펴 바르고 조각으로 썰면서 외친다. \"찰리, 너, 어디 있어?\" 부름에 머뭇머뭇 따르며, 거친 앞치마에 큼직한 보닛을 쓴 어린 계집아이가 두 손에 비눗물을 묻히고 한 손엔 솔을 든 채 나타나 무릎을 굽혀 인사한다.\n\n\"지금 무슨 일 하고 있어?\" 주디가 아주 매서운 늙은 노파처럼 옛 솜씨로 딱 채뜨리듯 묻는다.\n\n\"위층 뒷방 청소하고 있어요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 답한다.\n\n\"꼼꼼히 해, 꾸물대지 말고. 농땡이는 나한테 안 통해. 서둘러! 가봐!\" 주디가 발을 쾅 구르며 외친다. \"너희 계집애들은 쓸모보다 골칫거리가 두 배는 더하다니까.\"\n\n이 엄한 마님이 버터를 긁어 펴 바르고 빵을 써는 일로 되돌아갈 때, 창문으로 들여다보는 오라비의 그림자가 그녀 위로 드리운다. 그 사람을 들이려고 그녀는 칼과 빵덩이를 손에 든 채 거리로 난 문을 연다.\n\n\"오냐, 오냐, 바트!\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다. \"왔구나, 응?\"\n\n\"왔어요.\" 바트가 말한다.\n\n\"또 네 친구랑 같이 있었던 게냐, 바트?\"\n\n스몰위드가 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"걔 돈으로 저녁 얻어먹고, 바트?\"\n\n스몰위드가 또 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"잘했다. 할 수 있는 한 그 녀석 돈으로 살고, 그 어리석은 본보기에서 교훈을 얻어라. 그런 친구가 쓸모 있는 데가 그거지. 네가 그 녀석을 써먹을 수 있는 유일한 데가 그거야.\" 노현인이 말한다.\n\n손자는 이 좋은 충고를 마땅히 받들 만큼 곱게 받들지는 않으나, 한쪽 눈을 살짝 찡긋하고 고개를 끄덕여 보일 만큼의 수긍으로나마 그 충고에 경의를 표하고는 찻상에 의자를 끌어다 앉는다. 그러자 네 늙은 얼굴이 찻잔 위로 모여드니, 마치 섬뜩한 아기 천사 무리 같은데, 스몰위드 부인은 끊임없이 머리를 씰룩이며 삼발이를 향해 종알대고 스몰위드 씨는 큼직한 검은 물약처럼 거듭 흔들어줘야 한다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그 착하신 노신사가 자신의 지혜로운 교훈으로 되돌아가며 말한다. \"그게 바로 네 아비가 너한테 해주었을 충고다, 바트. 넌 네 아비를 본 적이 없지. 더없이 안타까운 일이야. 그 녀석은 내 진짜배기 아들이었어.\" 그렇기에 그 아비가 보기에 특별히 흐뭇한 인물이었다는 뜻을 전하려는 것인지는 드러나지 않는다.\n\n\"그 녀석은 내 진짜배기 아들이었지.\" 노신사가 버터 바른 빵을 무릎 위에서 접으며 되뇐다. \"솜씨 좋은 회계사였고, 십오 년 전에 죽었어.\"\n\n스몰위드 부인은 여느 때 본능을 좇아 불쑥 터뜨린다. \"천오백 파운드. 검은 상자 속에 천오백 파운드, 자물쇠 채운 천오백 파운드, 따로 챙겨 숨겨둔 천오백 파운드!\" 그녀의 훌륭한 남편은 버터 바른 빵을 옆으로 치우고 즉각 그녀에게 방석을 발사해 그녀를 의자 옆구리에 짓뭉개버리고는, 제 의자에 기진하여 나자빠진다. 스몰위드 부인에게 이런 훈계 한 차례를 베풀고 난 그의 몰골은 유난히 인상적이되 그리 호감 가는 꼴은 아니니, 첫째로 그 힘쓰는 통에 검은 두건이 한쪽 눈 위로 비뚜로 돌아가 도깨비 같은 난봉꾼 분위기를 풍기게 만들고, 둘째로 그가 스몰위드 부인을 향해 사나운 저주를 중얼대며, 셋째로 그 우렁찬 표현과 무력한 몸뚱이의 대비가, 할 수만 있다면 한껏 못되게 굴었을 음험하고 늙은 악당을 떠올리게 하기 때문이다. 그러나 이 모든 게 스몰위드 집안에서는 워낙 흔한 일이라 아무런 인상도 남기지 못한다. 노신사는 그저 흔들려 속 깃털을 두들겨 펴질 뿐이요, 방석은 그의 곁 제자리로 돌아가며, 노부인은 모자를 바로 했을 수도 안 했을 수도 있는 채로 다시 의자에 심기니, 구주희 핀처럼 또 한 번 쓰러질 채비를 갖추고서다.\n\n이번 경우엔 노신사가 다시 이야기를 이을 만큼 충분히 식기까지 한참이 걸리고, 그러고도 그는 이야기에 몇 마디 교화적인 욕설을 섞어 넣으니, 그 욕설의 대상은 이 세상 그 무엇과도 — 삼발이 말고는 — 교류하지 않는, 의식 없는 품속 동반자다. 이를테면 이런 식이다. \"네 아비가, 바트, 좀 더 오래 살았더라면 큰돈을 모았을지도 모른다 — 이 유황불 같은 수다쟁이야! — 헌데 그토록 여러 해 기초를 다져온 집을 막 쌓아 올리려던 참에 — 이 까치, 갈까마귀, 앵무새 같은 화냥년아, 대체 무슨 짓이냐! — 병이 들어 미열로 죽고 말았으니, 늘 아끼고 아쉬워하는 사람인 데다 사업 걱정으로 꽉 차 있었거든 — 방석 대신 고양이를 던지고 싶구나, 네가 그렇게 빌어먹을 바보짓을 또 하면 정말로 그러마! — 그리고 네 어미는, 대팻밥처럼 메마른 신중한 여자였는데, 너랑 주디가 태어난 뒤로 부싯깃처럼 그냥 시들어버렸지 — 너는 늙은 돼지야. 유황불 같은 돼지라고. 너는 돼지 우두머리야!\"\n\n주디는 숱하게 들어온 이야기엔 관심이 없는지라, 어린 잡역부 계집아이의 저녁거리로 쓸 차의 갖가지 잔여 줄기를 찻잔과 받침 바닥, 그리고 찻주전자 바닥에서 그러모아 대접 하나에 담기 시작한다. 같은 식으로 그녀는 이 집의 빡빡한 절약이 남겨놓은, 빵 바깥 부스러기와 닳아빠진 빵 꽁다리를 닥치는 대로 무쇠 빵 바구니에 모아 담는다.\n\n\"하지만 네 아비와 나는 동업자였다, 바트.\" 노신사가 말한다. \"그러니 내가 가고 나면 너와 주디가 있는 거 다 차지할 거야. 너희 둘 다 인생 일찍 세상에 나간 게 보기 드물게 잘된 일이지 — 주디는 꽃 장사로, 너는 법 쪽으로. 너희는 그걸 쓰고 싶지 않을 게야. 그거 없이도 벌어먹고, 거기다 더 보탤 테니까. 내가 가고 나면 주디는 꽃 장사로 돌아가고 너는 여전히 법에 들러붙어 있겠지.\"\n\n주디의 생김새로 보자면 그녀가 다루는 건 꽃보다는 가시 쪽이라고 짐작할 법하나, 그래도 한때 그녀는 조화(造花) 만드는 기술과 비법을 도제로 배운 적이 있다. 눈썰미 있는 이라면 어쩌면, 노쇠하신 조부가 자신이 가고 없을 때를 미리 그릴 때 그녀와 그 오라비의 눈에서 다 같이, 그가 대체 언제 가려나 알고 싶어하는 약간의 조바심과, 이제 갈 때도 됐다는 약간의 원망 어린 속내를 읽어낼지도 모른다.\n\n\"자, 다들 먹었으면,\" 주디가 채비를 마무리하며 말한다. \"그 계집애를 들여서 차를 줘야겠어. 부엌에서 혼자 먹게 두면 도무지 끝낼 줄을 모르거든.\"\n\n그리하여 찰리가 안으로 불려 들어와, 쏟아지는 눈길의 맹포화 아래, 제 대접과 드루이드 유적처럼 폐허가 된 버터 바른 빵을 앞에 놓고 앉는다. 이 어린아이를 부산히 감독하는 데서, 주디 스몰위드는 완벽하게 지질학적인 연령에 다다라 까마득한 태곳적부터 비롯된 존재처럼 보인다. 빌미가 있든 없든, 어쩌든 저쩌든, 체계적으로 그 아이에게 달려들어 덮치는 그녀의 솜씨는 놀랍기 그지없으니, 가장 노련한 명인들조차 좀처럼 이르지 못하는 계집애 부리기 기예의 경지를 드러낸다.\n\n\"자, 오후 내내 두리번거리지 말고,\" 차 대접을 미리부터 가늠하고 있던 그 아이의 시선을 마침 붙들고서 주디가 고개를 흔들고 발을 구르며 외친다. \"음식이나 먹고 일하러 돌아가.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 말한다.\n\n\"네라고 하지 마.\" 스몰위드 아가씨가 받는다. \"너희 계집애들이 어떤지 내가 다 아니까. 입으로 떠들지 말고 그냥 해, 그래야 내가 너를 믿기 시작할 테니.\"\n\n찰리는 순종의 표시로 차를 한 모금 크게 들이켜고는 드루이드 유적을 어찌나 해치우는지, 스몰위드 아가씨가 게걸스레 처먹지 말라고 다그치며 \"너희 계집애들\"의 그런 짓은 역겹다고 한마디한다. 계집애라는 전반적 화제에 관한 그녀의 견해를 맞추는 일은 찰리에게 한층 어려웠을지도 모르나, 마침 문 두드리는 소리가 난다.\n\n\"누군지 보고 와, 문 열 때 우물우물 씹지 말고!\" 주디가 외친다.\n\n그녀의 관심을 받는 아이가 그러려고 물러가자, 스몰위드 아가씨는 그 틈을 타 남은 버터 바른 빵을 죄다 한데 뭉뚱그리고 더러운 찻잔 두세 개를 차 대접의 썰물 속으로 던져 넣으니, 먹고 마시는 일은 끝난 걸로 친다는 암시다.\n\n\"자! 누구야, 무슨 일이래?\" 까칠한 주디가 묻는다.\n\n조지 씨라는 사람인 듯하다. 다른 기별이나 격식도 없이 조지 씨가 성큼 걸어 들어온다.\n\n\"휴!\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"여기 덥군요. 늘 불을 피우는군, 응? 글쎄! 하긴 불에 익숙해지는 게 옳을지도 모르겠소.\" 조지 씨는 스몰위드 할아버지에게 고개를 까딱하며 뒷말은 혼잣말로 한다.\n\n\"호! 자네구먼!\" 노신사가 외친다. \"잘 지냈나? 잘 지냈어?\"\n\n\"그럭저럭요.\" 조지 씨가 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 답한다. \"손녀분은 전에 뵌 영광이 있고요. 그럼 안녕하시오, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"이쪽은 내 손자라네.\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다. \"전에 본 적 없겠지. 법 쪽에 있어서 집에 붙어 있는 일이 드물거든.\"\n\n\"이 사람한테도 안부 전하오! 누이를 빼닮았군. 누이랑 어찌나 닮았는지. 누이랑 지독하게 닮았소.\" 조지 씨가 마지막 형용사에 크고도 그다지 칭찬은 아닌 힘을 실어 말한다.\n\n\"그래, 세상살이는 자넬 어떻게 대접하던가, 조지 씨?\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 다리를 천천히 문지르며 묻는다.\n\n\"여느 때랑 거의 똑같죠. 축구공처럼요.\"\n\n그는 쉰 살의 가무잡잡한 갈색 피부 사내로, 체격이 다부지고 잘생겼으며, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리에 빛나는 눈, 떡 벌어진 가슴을 지녔다. 얼굴만큼이나 햇볕에 그을린 그의 힘줄 불거진 억센 두 손은, 한눈에 봐도 꽤나 험한 삶에 익은 손이다. 그에게서 묘한 점은, 마치 오랜 습관 때문에, 이젠 죄 벗어던진 어떤 옷가지나 장구(裝具)를 위해 자리를 비워두기라도 하듯 의자 앞쪽에 걸터앉아 있다는 것이다. 걸음걸이 또한 절도 있고 묵직해서, 박차가 묵직하게 쟁그랑 부딪치는 소리와 잘 어울릴 법하다. 지금은 말끔히 면도했으나, 그의 입매는 마치 윗입술이 여러 해 큼직한 콧수염과 친했던 듯 굳어 있고, 이따금 넓적한 갈색 손바닥을 펴서 그 위에 갖다 대는 버릇도 같은 인상을 준다. 통틀어 보면 조지 씨가 한때 기병이었으리라 짐작할 만하다.\n\n조지 씨는 스몰위드 집안과 유독 대조를 이룬다. 이토록 자기와 안 닮은 가정에 배치된 기병은 일찍이 없었다. 굴 까는 칼에 견준 양손검 격이다. 그의 발달한 체구와 그들의 발육 멈춘 형상, 어떤 공간이든 가득 채우는 그의 큼직한 거동과 그들의 좁다랗고 옹색한 처신, 우렁찬 그의 목소리와 날카롭고 빈약한 그들의 음색은 더없이 강하고도 더없이 기묘한 대립을 이룬다. 험상궂은 거실 한가운데, 약간 앞으로 몸을 숙이고 두 손을 허벅지에 얹고 팔꿈치를 모로 벌린 채 앉아 있는 그를 보면, 저기 오래 앉아 있다가는 온 식구와 방 네 칸짜리 집 전체를, 별채인 자그마한 뒷부엌까지 통째로 제 몸 안에 빨아들일 듯싶다.\n\n\"다리를 문지르는 건 거기 생기를 돌게 하려는 겁니까?\" 그가 방 안을 둘러본 뒤 스몰위드 할아버지에게 묻는다.\n\n\"글쎄, 절반은 버릇이고, 또 — 그래 — 절반은 혈액 순환을 돕는다네.\" 그가 답한다.\n\n\"혈-액-순-환이라!\" 조지 씨가 두 팔을 가슴에 포개며, 몸집이 두 치수쯤 더 커지는 듯 보이며 되뇐다. \"그런 거 별로 없을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"내가 늙긴 늙었지, 조지 씨.\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다. \"그래도 내 나이는 짊어질 만해. 난 저 사람보다 늙었어.\" 아내를 향해 고갯짓하며. \"그런데 저 꼴 좀 보게? 이 유황불 같은 수다쟁이야!\" 아까의 적의를 별안간 되살리며.\n\n\"불쌍한 노인네!\" 조지 씨가 그쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 말한다. \"할머니를 나무라지 마쇼. 여기 이 꼴을 보쇼, 가엾게 모자가 머리에서 반쯤 벗겨지고 머리칼이 죄다 헝클어졌잖소. 자, 똑바로 앉으세요, 마님. 그래야 낫죠. 됐다! 어머니를 생각하쇼, 스몰위드 씨.\" 조지 씨가 할머니를 거든 뒤 제자리로 돌아오며 말한다. \"아내로 모자라거든 말이오.\"\n\n\"자넨 효자였겠지, 조지 씨?\" 노인이 곁눈질하며 넌지시 묻는다.\n\n조지 씨의 얼굴빛이 다소 짙어지며 답한다. \"글쎄, 아니오. 난 안 그랬소.\"\n\n\"그것참 놀랍군.\"\n\n\"나도 놀랍소. 좋은 아들이었어야 했고, 그러려고 마음먹기도 했던 것 같소. 헌데 안 그랬소. 난 천하에 못된 아들이었어, 그게 한마디로 전부요, 그 누구한테도 자랑거리였던 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"놀랍구먼!\" 노인이 외친다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 조지 씨가 다시 입을 연다. \"이젠 그 얘긴 안 할수록 좋겠소. 자! 약속은 아시잖소. 두 달 이자에서 늘 파이프 담배 하나! (헛소리 마쇼! 다 맞소. 파이프 시키는 거 겁낼 거 없소. 여기 새 어음 있고, 여기 두 달 치 이자 돈 있소, 이걸 내 장사로 긁어모으느라 죽을 똥을 쌌소이다.)\"\n\n조지 씨가 두 팔을 포갠 채 앉아 식구와 거실을 집어삼키는 동안, 스몰위드 할아버지는 주디의 도움으로 자물쇠 채운 책상에서 검은 가죽 상자 두 개를 꺼내, 그중 한 상자엔 방금 받은 서류를 챙겨 넣고, 다른 상자에서는 비슷한 서류 하나를 꺼내 조지 씨에게 건네니, 조지 씨는 그걸 비틀어 말아 파이프 불쏘시개로 삼는다. 노인이 두 서류를 가죽 감옥에서 풀어주기 전에 안경 너머로 획 하나하나, 올려 긋고 내려 그은 자국 하나하나를 다 살피고, 돈을 세 번이나 거듭 세고, 주디에게는 무슨 말이든 적어도 두 번씩 하라고 요구하며, 말과 행동이 더할 나위 없이 떨리고 굼뜬 까닭에, 이 일은 한참이나 더디게 진행된다. 일이 완전히 끝나고 나서야, 그 전에는 결코 아니고, 그는 게걸스러운 눈과 손가락을 거기서 떼고 조지 씨의 아까 그 말에 답한다. \"파이프 시키는 걸 겁낸다고? 우리가 그렇게 인색하진 않다네, 이 사람아. 주디야, 당장 파이프하고 조지 씨 드릴 차가운 브랜디 물 한 잔 챙겨라.\"\n\n이 장난기 어린 쌍둥이는 그동안 줄곧 — 검은 가죽 상자에 정신이 팔렸을 때만 빼고 — 똑바로 앞만 바라보고 있었는데, 함께 물러가며 대체로 손님을 업신여기되, 마치 새끼 곰 두 마리가 나그네를 어미 곰에게 떠넘기듯 그를 노인 손에 맡겨둔다.\n\n\"그래서 영감님은 여기 이렇게 온종일 앉아 있는 거요, 응?\" 조지 씨가 두 팔을 포갠 채 말한다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지, 바로 그래.\" 노인이 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"그러고는 아무 일도 안 하고요?\"\n\n\"불을 지켜본다네 — 끓는 것도, 굽는 것도 —\"\n\n\"있을 때 말이죠.\" 조지 씨가 의미심장하게 말한다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지. 있을 때.\"\n\n\"책을 읽거나 누가 읽어주거나 하진 않고요?\"\n\n노인이 매섭고 능청맞은 승리감으로 고개를 젓는다. \"아니, 아니. 우리 집안은 책 읽는 사람이 없었어. 그건 남는 게 없거든. 부질없는 짓. 게으름. 어리석음이지. 아니, 아니!\"\n\n\"그럼 영감 처지나 할멈 처지나 별로 가릴 게 없네.\" 손님이 노인의 둔한 귀에는 안 들릴 만큼 낮은 목소리로, 노인에게서 노부인에게로, 다시 노인에게로 시선을 옮기며 말한다. \"이봐요!\" 좀 더 큰 목소리로.\n\n\"듣고 있네.\"\n\n\"내가 하루라도 밀리면 결국 다 팔아넘기겠죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"이 친애하는 벗이여!\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 그를 끌어안으려고 두 손을 뻗으며 외친다. \"절대! 절대 안 그러지, 친애하는 벗이여! 헌데 자네한테 돈을 빌려주게 해준 시내의 내 친구 — 그 사람은 그럴지도 모르지!\"\n\n\"오! 그 사람은 영감님이 보증 못 한다? '이 거짓말쟁이 늙은 악당아!'\" 조지 씨가 질문을 마저 던지고는 낮은 목소리로 끝맺는다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여, 그 사람은 믿을 수가 없어. 나라도 안 믿을 거야. 그 사람은 제 증서대로 받아낼 게야, 친애하는 벗이여.\"\n\n\"그놈이야 의심할 것도 없겠지.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. 찰리가 파이프와 자그마한 종이 봉지 담배, 브랜디 물을 담은 쟁반을 들고 나타나자, 그가 아이에게 묻는다. \"넌 어쩌다 여기 와 있냐! 이 집안 얼굴은 아닌데.\"\n\n\"전 일하러 나와 있는 거예요, 아저씨.\" 찰리가 답한다.\n\n기병은 (그가 기병이거나 기병이었다면) 그토록 힘센 손치고는 가볍게 아이의 보닛을 벗겨주고 머리를 토닥인다. \"네가 이 집에 그나마 건강한 기색을 좀 보태는구나. 이 집은 신선한 공기만큼이나 젊음이 좀 필요하니까.\" 그러고는 아이를 내보내고, 파이프에 불을 붙이고, 시내의 스몰위드 씨 친구를 위해 — 그 점잖은 노신사 상상력이 펼친 단 하나의 외로운 비상(飛翔)을 위해 — 잔을 든다.\n\n\"그래, 영감님은 그 사람이 나한테 모질게 굴 거라고 보는 거요, 응?\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같아 — 그럴까 봐 걱정이야. 그 사람이 그러는 걸 내가 봤거든.\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 부주의하게 말한다. \"스무 번은.\"\n\n부주의하다 함은, 한동안 불 앞에서 졸던 망가진 그의 반쪽이 즉각 깨어나 지껄여대기 때문이니, \"이만 파운드, 돈궤 속 이십 파운드짜리 지폐 스무 장, 이십 기니, 이천만의 이십 퍼센트, 스물—\" 하다가 날아드는 방석에 말이 뚝 끊긴다. 이 진기한 실험이 처음인 듯싶은 손님이, 여느 때처럼 그녀를 짓뭉개는 그 방석을 그녀 얼굴에서 낚아챈다.\n\n\"이 유황불 같은 멍청이. 넌 전갈이야 — 유황불 같은 전갈! 푹푹 삶기는 두꺼비라고. 화형당해 마땅한, 종알대고 덜그럭대는 빗자루 마녀야!\" 노인이 의자에 널브러진 채 헐떡인다. \"친애하는 벗이여, 날 좀 흔들어 주겠나?\"\n\n조지 씨는, 마치 미친 사람 구경하듯 한 사람을 봤다 또 한 사람을 봤다 하던 차에, 이 부탁을 받자 노쇠한 지인의 멱살을 잡고는, 인형이라도 되는 양 손쉽게 의자에 똑바로 끌어 일으키더니, 다시는 방석 던질 힘이 안 남도록 그를 마저 흔들어 무덤으로 보내버릴까 말까 두 마음을 품은 듯하다. 유혹은 물리치되, 그의 머리가 어릿광대 머리처럼 까딱이도록 사납게 흔들고 나서, 다시 보기 좋게 의자에 앉히고는 두건을 어찌나 박박 문질러 바로잡는지, 노인이 그 뒤로 한참 두 눈을 끔뻑인다.\n\n\"아이고!\" 스몰위드 씨가 헐떡인다. \"그만하면 됐네. 고맙네, 친애하는 벗이여, 그만하면 돼. 아이고, 숨이 다 차는구먼. 아이고!\" 그러고 스몰위드 씨가 이 말을 하는 데는, 여전히 어느 때보다 거대하게 자기 위로 우뚝 솟아 있는 친애하는 벗에 대한 두려움이 역력히 묻어 있다.\n\n그러나 그 위협적인 존재는 점차 제 의자로 가라앉아 길게 뻐끔뻐끔 담배를 피우기 시작하더니, 다음과 같은 철학적 성찰로 스스로를 달랜다. \"시내의 자네 친구 이름이 D로 시작하는구려, 동지, 그리고 증서에 관해선 자네 말이 어지간히 맞소.\"\n\n\"무슨 말 했나, 조지 씨?\" 노인이 묻는다.\n\n기병은 고개를 젓고, 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 오른 무릎에 얹고 그 손으로 파이프를 받친 채 몸을 앞으로 숙이는 한편, 왼다리에 얹은 다른 손으로 왼쪽 팔꿈치를 군인답게 모로 세우고서 계속 담배를 피운다. 그러는 동안 그는 진지한 주의를 기울여 스몰위드 씨를 바라보고, 이따금 그를 더 또렷이 보려고 담배 연기 구름을 부채질해 흩는다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이오,\" 그가 자세를 딱 그만큼만 바꿔 둥글고 묵직한 동작으로 잔을 입에 가져갈 수 있게 하며 말한다. \"영감님한테서 파이프 한 대만큼의 값이라도 뽑아내는 건 산 사람 (죽은 사람 통틀어도) 가운데 나 하나뿐인 것 같소만?\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 노인이 받는다. \"내가 손님을 안 들이고 대접을 안 하는 건 사실이지, 조지 씨. 그럴 형편이 못 되거든. 헌데 자네가, 그 유쾌한 식으로, 파이프를 조건으로 걸었으니—\"\n\n\"뭐, 그 값 때문은 아니오. 그거야 대단한 것도 아니지. 영감님한테서 뭐라도 뜯어내고 싶은 마음이었소. 내 돈값으로 뭐라도 받아두자고.\"\n\n\"하! 자넨 신중하구먼, 신중해, 이 사람아!\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 다리를 문지르며 외친다.\n\n\"무척요. 난 늘 그랬소.\" 뻐끔. \"내가 여기까지 길을 찾아 들어온 것부터가 내 신중함의 확실한 증거지.\" 뻐끔. \"또, 지금 이 모양 이 꼴인 것도.\" 뻐끔. \"난 신중하기로 소문이 자자하오.\" 조지 씨가 태연히 담배를 피우며 말한다. \"그래서 그렇게 출세했지.\"\n\n\"기죽지 말게, 이 사람아. 자넨 아직 출세할 수 있어.\"\n\n조지 씨가 웃고는 술을 마신다.\n\n\"이제, 친척 없나,\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 눈을 반짝이며 묻는다. \"이 자그마한 원금을 갚아주거나, 보증인 이름 한둘을 빌려주면 내가 시내 친구를 설득해 자네한테 돈을 더 빌려주게 할 수 있는 그런 친척 말이야? 시내 친구한텐 좋은 보증인 이름 둘이면 충분하거든. 그런 친척 없나, 조지 씨?\"\n\n조지 씨는 여전히 태연히 담배를 피우며 답한다. \"있어도, 그 사람들을 귀찮게 하진 않겠소. 내 한창때 내 식구들한테 끼친 폐만으로도 차고 넘치니까. 인생의 가장 좋은 시절을 허투루 흘려보낸 부랑자가, 한 번도 자랑거리였던 적 없는 점잖은 사람들한테 돌아가 빌붙어 사는 게 아주 좋은 종류의 참회일지는 모르겠소만, 그건 내 식이 아니오. 떠나버린 데 대한 최선의 보상은 계속 떨어져 있는 거다, 그게 내 생각이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 천륜의 정이란 게 있잖나, 조지 씨.\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 넌지시 떠본다.\n\n\"좋은 보증인 이름 둘 때문에, 응?\" 조지 씨가 고개를 저으며 여전히 태연히 담배를 피운다. \"아니오. 그것도 내 식이 아니오.\"\n\n스몰위드 할아버지는 아까 바로잡힌 뒤로 차츰 의자에서 미끄러져 내려와, 이제는 주디를 부르는 목소리가 담긴 옷보따리 신세다. 그 천녀(天女)가 나타나 여느 때처럼 그를 흔들어 일으키니, 노신사는 그녀에게 제 곁에 머물러 있으라 이른다. 손님에게 아까 그 수고를 또 끼치기는 꺼려지는 모양이다.\n\n\"하!\" 그가 다시 매무새를 추스르고 말한다. \"자네가 그 대위를 찾아냈더라면, 조지 씨, 그게 자네 팔자를 폈을 텐데. 자네가 처음 여기 왔을 때, 우리가 신문에 낸 광고를 보고 말이야 — '우리'라고 할 때 나는 시내 내 친구와, 같은 식으로 제 자본을 굴리며 가끔 내 쥐꼬리만 한 밑천에 보탬을 줄 만큼 나한테 우호적인 한두 사람의 광고를 가리키는 거네만 — 그때 자네가 우릴 도울 수 있었더라면, 조지 씨, 그게 자네 팔자를 폈을 거란 말이야.\"\n\n\"영감님 말대로 '팔자 펴는' 거라면 나야 얼마든지 응할 마음이었소.\" 조지 씨가 아까만큼 평온하게 담배를 피우지는 못하며 말한다. 주디가 들어온 뒤로 어느 정도 마음이 어지러워졌으니, 흠모와는 거리가 먼 어떤 끌림 때문에 그녀가 조부의 의자 곁에 서 있는 모습을 자꾸 바라보지 않을 수 없는 탓이다. \"하지만 다 따지고 보면, 이젠 안 그랬던 게 기쁘오.\"\n\n\"왜, 조지 씨? 대체 — 유황불 같은 무슨 까닭으로, 왜?\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 드러내놓고 분통을 터뜨리며 말한다. (유황불은 아무래도 잠든 스몰위드 부인에게 눈길이 닿은 데서 떠오른 모양이다.)\n\n\"두 가지 이유 때문이오, 동지.\"\n\n\"그래, 무슨 두 가지 이유야, 조지 씨? 대체 그—\"\n\n\"시내 우리 친구의 이름으로?\" 조지 씨가 태연히 술을 마시며 거든다.\n\n\"오냐, 그렇게 하든지. 무슨 두 가지 이유?\"\n\n\"첫째로는,\" 조지 씨가 받지만, 여전히 주디를 바라보면서다. 그녀가 워낙 늙어 조부를 빼닮은 터라 둘 중 누구한테 말을 거는지 아무래도 상관없다는 듯이. \"당신네 양반들이 날 속였소. 당신네가 광고하기를 호든 씨가 — '한 번 대위면 영원히 대위'라는 말을 고집한다면 호든 대위가 — 자기한테 득 될 소식을 듣게 될 거라고 했잖소.\"\n\n\"그래서?\" 노인이 새되고 날카롭게 받는다.\n\n\"그래서!\" 조지 씨가 계속 담배를 피우며 말한다. \"런던의 어음과 판결 장사치 떼한테 몽땅 감옥에 처박히는 게 그 사람한테 그리 득 될 일은 아니었을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그걸 자네가 어찌 아나? 그 사람 부유한 친척 누군가가 빚을 갚아줬거나 화의(和議)로 처리했을지도 모르지. 게다가 그 사람은 우릴 속였어. 사방에 우리한테 어마어마한 빚을 졌단 말이야. 한 푼도 못 받느니 차라리 내가 목을 졸랐을 거야. 여기 앉아 그 사람 생각을 하노라면,\" 노인이 무력한 열 손가락을 들어 올리며 으르렁댄다. \"지금이라도 목을 조르고 싶구먼.\" 그러고는 별안간 치솟는 분노에 죄 없는 스몰위드 부인에게 방석을 던지지만, 그것은 그녀 의자 옆으로 무사히 비껴간다.\n\n\"그 사람이 무겁게 살다 파멸로 치달았다는 거야 굳이 들을 필요도 없소.\" 기병이 잠시 입에서 파이프를 떼고, 방석의 행로를 좇던 눈길을 다 타들어 사그라지는 담뱃대 통으로 도로 거두며 받는다. \"그 사람이 파멸을 향해 전속력으로 돌격할 때 난 여러 날 그 사람 오른편에 있었소. 아플 때나 성할 때나, 부유할 때나 가난할 때나 함께였지. 그 사람이 모든 걸 탕진하고 발밑의 모든 걸 무너뜨린 뒤 — 머리에 권총을 들이댔을 때 — 내가 이 손을 그 사람한테 얹었소.\"\n\n\"그때 방아쇠를 당겼더라면 좋았을걸.\" 그 자비로운 노인이 말한다. \"지가 진 빚 파운드 수만큼 머리통이 산산조각 나도록 말이야!\"\n\n\"그랬으면 정말 박살이 났겠지.\" 기병이 냉정하게 받는다. \"어쨌거나 그 사람도 지난날엔 젊고 희망차고 잘생긴 사람이었소, 그러니 그 사람이 그 무엇도 아니게 됐을 때 그렇게나 '득 될' 결말로 끌고 갈 만큼 끝내 못 찾아낸 게 다행이오. 그게 첫째 이유요.\"\n\n\"둘째 이유도 그만큼 잘난 거겠지?\" 노인이 으르렁댄다.\n\n\"뭐, 아니오. 그건 좀 더 이기적인 이유요. 내가 그 사람을 찾았으려면 저세상까지 찾으러 가야 했을 테니까. 그 사람은 거기 있었소.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 거기 있는 줄 자네가 어찌 아나?\"\n\n\"여기 없었으니까.\"\n\n\"여기 없는 줄을 자네가 어찌 알아?\"\n\n\"돈에다 성질까지 같이 잃지는 마쇼.\" 조지 씨가 침착하게 파이프 재를 털며 말한다. \"그 사람은 한참 전에 물에 빠져 죽었소. 난 그렇게 확신하오. 뱃전 너머로 넘어갔거든. 일부러였는지 사고였는지는 모르오. 어쩌면 시내의 영감님 친구는 알지도 모르고. 이 가락이 무슨 가락인지 아시오, 스몰위드 씨?\" 그가 말을 끊고 빈 파이프로 식탁을 두드려 가락 하나를 휘파람으로 분 뒤 덧붙인다.\n\n\"가락!\" 노인이 답한다. \"아니. 우리 집엔 가락이라곤 없어.\"\n\n\"이게 「사울」에 나오는 장송곡이오. 군인들을 이 가락에 맞춰 묻지요, 그러니 이 화제의 자연스러운 마무리겠소. 자, 영감님의 예쁜 손녀가 — 미안하오, 아가씨 — 황송하게도 이 파이프를 두 달간 보관해준다면, 다음번엔 하나 값을 아끼겠소. 그럼 안녕히 계시오, 스몰위드 씨!\"\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여!\" 노인이 그에게 두 손을 다 내민다.\n\n\"그래서 내가 한 번 갚을 걸 못 내면 시내 영감님 친구가 나한테 모질게 굴 거다, 이거요?\" 기병이 거인처럼 그를 내려다보며 말한다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여, 그럴까 봐 걱정이야.\" 노인이 난쟁이처럼 그를 올려다보며 받는다.\n\n조지 씨가 웃더니, 스몰위드 씨를 한 번 흘끗 보고 멸시하는 주디에게 작별 인사를 던지고는, 거실 밖으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나가며 가는 길에 상상의 군도와 그 밖의 쇠붙이 장구들을 쟁그랑쟁그랑 부딪친다.\n\n\"넌 빌어먹을 악당이야.\" 노신사가 문을 닫으며 문을 향해 흉측한 표정을 짓고 말한다. \"하지만 끈끈이로 너를 잡아주마, 이 개자식, 끈끈이로 잡아주고말고!\"\n\n이 다정한 한마디 뒤로 그의 정신은, 그것의 교육과 추구가 열어준 저 황홀한 사색의 영역으로 솟아오르고, 또다시 그와 스몰위드 부인은 장밋빛 시간을 흘려보내니, 앞서 말한 대로 검은 하사관이 잊어버린 채 내버려둔, 누구의 교대도 받지 못한 두 보초병이다.\n\n이 둘이 제 초소를 충실히 지키는 동안, 조지 씨는 묵직한 호기를 부리며 자못 심각한 얼굴로 거리를 성큼성큼 누빈다. 이제 여덟 시요 날이 빠르게 저문다. 그는 워털루 다리 바로 옆에 멈춰 공연 전단을 읽고 애스틀리 극장에 가기로 마음먹는다. 거기 가서는 말들과 묘기에 무척 즐거워하고, 무기들은 비평가의 눈으로 뜯어보며, 결투 장면은 서툰 검술 솜씨를 드러낸다고 못마땅해하되, 그 정서에는 가슴이 뭉클해진다. 마지막 장면에서, 타타르의 황제가 수레에 올라타 황송하게도 유니언 잭을 든 채 두 연인 위를 맴돌며 그들을 축복하자, 그의 속눈썹이 감격으로 촉촉이 젖는다.\n\n극이 끝나자 조지 씨는 다시 강을 건너, 헤이마켓과 레스터 광장 일대에 펼쳐진 저 기묘한 구역으로 향하니, 거기는 변변찮은 외국인 호텔과 변변찮은 외국인들, 라켓 코트, 격투가, 검객, 보병 근위병, 골동 도자기, 도박장, 흥행물, 그리고 후줄근함과 남의 눈을 피해 움츠러든 것들이 잔뜩 뒤섞여 끌려드는 중심지다. 이 구역의 심장부까지 파고들어, 그는 안마당 하나와 회칠한 긴 통로를 지나 큼직한 벽돌 건물에 다다르니, 건물은 휑한 벽과 마룻바닥, 지붕 서까래, 채광창으로 이루어져 있고, 그 정면에는 — 정면이랄 게 있다 치면 — 「조지의 사격장 외(外)」라고 적혀 있다.\n\n「조지의 사격장 외」 안으로 그가 들어선다. 그 안에는 가스등이 (지금은 일부 꺼져 있다) 켜져 있고, 소총 사격용으로 희게 칠한 표적 두 개와 양궁 시설, 펜싱 기구, 그리고 권투라는 영국 기예에 필요한 일체가 갖춰져 있다. 오늘 밤 「조지의 사격장」에서는 이런 운동이나 연습이 하나도 행해지지 않으니, 워낙 손님이 없어 큰 머리를 한 자그맣고 괴상한 사내 하나가 그곳을 통째로 독차지하고 바닥에 누워 잠들어 있다.\n\n그 자그마한 사내는 총포 만드는 장인처럼 녹색 천 앞치마와 모자를 걸쳤고, 얼굴과 손은 화약으로 더럽고 총에 화약 재는 일로 새카맣게 그을렸다. 눈부시게 흰 표적 앞 불빛 속에 누워 있으니, 그에게 묻은 검댕이 다시 번들거린다. 멀지 않은 곳엔 그가 일하던, 바이스가 물려 있는 튼튼하고 거칠고 투박한 작업대가 있다. 그는 얼굴이 온통 한데 짜부라진 자그마한 사내인데, 한쪽 뺨에 보이는 어떤 푸르스름하고 얼룩덜룩한 모양새로 미루어, 일하다 어쩌다 한두 차례 폭발 사고를 당한 듯하다.\n\n\"필!\" 기병이 조용한 목소리로 부른다.\n\n\"네!\" 필이 허둥지둥 일어서며 외친다.\n\n\"무슨 일이라도 있었나?\"\n\n\"맥주처럼 김 빠진 하루였죠.\" 필이 말한다. \"소총 다섯 다스에 권총 한 다스. 한데 그 조준 솜씨라니!\" 필이 그걸 떠올리며 한 차례 울부짖는다.\n\n\"가게 닫아라, 필!\"\n\n필이 이 명령을 따르려고 움직이는 걸 보면, 그가 절름발이임을 알 수 있다. 그래도 아주 잽싸게 움직이긴 한다. 얼룩진 쪽 얼굴엔 눈썹이 없고, 다른 쪽엔 무성한 검은 눈썹이 있어, 이 좌우의 불균형이 그에게 자못 기묘하고 다소 음흉한 인상을 준다. 그의 두 손엔 손가락을 모조리 보전하는 한도 안에서 일어날 수 있는 일은 죄다 일어난 듯하니, 온통 베이고 갈라지고 쭈글쭈글하다. 그는 무척 힘이 센 듯, 무게가 무언지조차 모르는 양 무거운 의자들을 번쩍번쩍 들어 옮긴다. 그는 사격장을 절뚝거리며 도는 묘한 버릇이 있어, 어깨를 벽에 댄 채 돌다가 붙잡고 싶은 물건이 있으면 곧장 그리로 가지 않고 빗겨 다가가니, 그 때문에 네 벽을 빙 둘러 자국이 하나 남았는데, 흔히들 \"필의 자국\"이라 부른다.\n\n조지가 없는 동안 「조지의 사격장」을 지키는 이 파수꾼은, 큰 문을 잠그고 등불 하나만 남겨 깜박이게 두고 나머지를 모두 끈 다음, 구석의 나무 칸막이 방에서 매트리스 두 장과 침구를 끌어내는 것으로 제 일과를 마무리한다. 이것들을 사격장 양 끝으로 끌어다 놓고, 기병은 제 잠자리를 깔고 필은 제 잠자리를 깐다.\n\n\"필!\" 주인이 외투와 조끼를 벗고 그에게 걸어오며 부르니, 멜빵 차림이라 어느 때보다 군인다워 보인다. \"넌 출입구에 버려져 있었다고 했지?\"\n\n\"개천에요.\" 필이 말한다. \"야경꾼이 절 보고 자빠졌죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 부랑 생활이야 너한텐 처음부터 타고난 거였구나.\"\n\n\"더없이 타고난 거였죠.\" 필이 말한다.\n\n\"잘 자라!\"\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요, 주인님.\"\n\n필은 잠자리에도 곧장 들지 못하고, 사격장 두 면을 어깨로 휘돌고 나서야 제 매트리스로 빗겨 들어야 직성이 풀린다. 기병은 사격 거리를 한두 차례 거닐고 이제 채광창으로 비쳐드는 달을 올려다본 뒤, 더 짧은 길로 제 매트리스로 성큼 다가가 역시 잠자리에 든다.\n\n링컨 법학원 광장에서는 우의화 인물이 제법 시원해 보인다. 저녁 공기는 후텁지근하건만, 토킹혼 씨의 창문 두 짝이 활짝 열려 있는 데다 방 자체가 높다랗고 바람이 잘 통하며 음침하기 때문이다. 안개와 진눈깨비가 몰려드는 십일월이나 얼음과 눈이 쌓이는 일월이 닥치면 이런 특성이 그리 달갑지 않겠지만, 무덥고 긴 휴정기 날씨에는 나름의 미덕이 있다. 그 덕분에, 복숭아 같은 뺨에 꽃송이 다발 같은 무릎, 종아리에는 발그레한 살집이 오르고 팔에는 근육이 붙은 우의화 인물도 오늘 밤은 그런대로 시원해 보이는 것이다.\n\n토킹혼 씨의 창문으로는 먼지가 듬뿍 들어오고, 그의 가구와 서류 틈에서도 먼지가 그득그득 생겨난다. 어디나 먼지가 두텁게 쌓여 있다. 길을 잃은 시골 미풍 한 줄기가 놀라 허둥지둥 다시 빠져나가려 할 때면, 우의화 인물의 두 눈에 먼지를 잔뜩 끼얹는데, 그건 법이—아니 그 법의 가장 믿음직한 대리인 가운데 하나인 토킹혼 씨가—이따금 일반 백성의 눈에 먼지를 뿌리는 양과 어금지금하다.\n\n서류도, 그 자신도, 그의 모든 의뢰인도, 산 것이든 죽은 것이든 세상 만물이 다 같이 녹아들어 가는 그 보편의 물질, 곧 음침하게 쌓인 먼지 창고 한가운데서, 토킹혼 씨는 열린 창문 가에 앉아 묵은 포트와인 한 병을 즐긴다. 깐깐하고 빈틈없고 메마르고 과묵한 사람이지만, 그도 묵은 포도주를 누구 못지않게 즐길 줄 안다. 그에게는 광장 아래 어느 은밀한 지하실에 값을 매길 수 없는 포트와인 저장고가 있는데, 이것이 그의 숱한 비밀 가운데 하나다. 오늘처럼 사무실에서 혼자 식사하며 커피하우스에서 생선 한 토막과 스테이크나 닭고기를 들여올 때면, 그는 촛불을 들고 인적 끊긴 저택 아래 메아리치는 영역으로 내려가, 멀리서 천둥치듯 문 닫히는 소리를 앞세우고는, 흙냄새 풍기는 기운에 둘러싸여 점잖게 돌아온다. 손에 든 병에서 그는 빛나는 신주(神酒)를 따른다. 오십 년 묵은 그 술은 제 명성에 부끄러운 듯 잔 속에서 발그레 물들며 온 방 안을 남쪽 포도 향기로 가득 채운다.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 황혼녘 열린 창가에 앉아 술을 즐긴다. 술이 그 오십 년 침묵과 은둔의 세월을 그에게 속삭이기라도 하는 듯, 그를 한층 더 단단히 닫아 건다. 그 어느 때보다 속을 알 수 없는 사람이 되어, 그는 앉아 마시며, 비밀스레 무르익어 간다고 할까, 그 황혼의 시각에 자기가 아는 온갖 비밀을 곱씹는다. 시골의 어두워 가는 숲과, 도회의 거대하고 텅 빈 채 닫혀 버린 저택들에 얽힌 비밀을. 그러다 어쩌면 자기 자신과, 자기 가문의 내력과, 자기 돈과, 자기 유언을—누구에게나 다 비밀인 그것을—한두 번 떠올릴지도 모른다. 또 자기와 한 부류인, 역시 변호사였던 그 독신 친구 하나를. 그 친구는 일흔다섯이 되도록 똑같은 삶을 살다가, 어느 날 문득 (사람들 짐작으로는) 그 삶이 너무 단조롭다는 생각이 들었던지, 어느 여름 저녁 자기 이발사에게 금시계를 주고는 느긋이 템플의 집으로 걸어와 목을 매달았다.\n\n그러나 오늘 밤 토킹혼 씨는 평소처럼 길게 곱씹을 처지가 못 된다. 같은 탁자에 함께 앉은 사람이 있으니, 의자를 탁자에서 겸손하게 또 어색하게 조금 물려 놓은 채로다. 변호사가 잔을 채우라고 권하면 손으로 입을 가리고 공손하게 기침하는, 대머리에 온순하고 번들거리는 사내다.\n\n\"자, 스내그스비,\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"그 묘한 얘기를 다시 한번 짚어 봅시다.\"\n\n\"예, 그러십시오, 나리.\"\n\n\"어젯밤 친절하게도 이리 들러 주셨을 때 말이오—\"\n\n\"외람된 일이었다면 부디 너그러이 보아 주십사 청해야겠습니다, 나리. 다만 나리께서 그 사람한테 어떤 관심 같은 걸 가지고 계셨던 게 기억나서요, 어쩌면 나리께서—그저—원하실지도—하는 생각이 들어서—\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 상대의 말을 마무리해 줄 사람도, 자기와 관련해 무슨 가능성이든 인정해 줄 사람도 아니다. 그래서 스내그스비 씨는 어색하게 기침하며 말끝을 흐린다. \"외람된 짓을 너그러이 보아 주십사 청해야겠습니다, 정말이지요, 나리.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"스내그스비, 당신은 모자를 쓰고 부인한테는 어디 간다는 말도 없이 이리 왔다고 했지요. 잘하셨소. 굳이 입에 올릴 만큼 중대한 일은 아니니까.\"\n\n\"저, 그게 말입니다, 나리,\" 스내그스비 씨가 대답한다. \"솔직히 말씀드리자면 제 마누라가—호기심이 많습니다. 호기심이 많아요. 가엾게도 그 사람은 발작을 잘 일으키는데, 머리를 어디다 쓰게 하면 좋답니다. 그래서 머리를 굴리는데—말하자면 손에 잡히는 건 무엇이든, 자기와 상관이 있든 없든—특히 상관없는 일에다 굴린다고 해야겠지요. 제 마누라는 머리가 아주 잘 돌아갑니다, 나리.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 술을 마시며 손으로 입을 가리고 감탄 어린 기침을 하고는 중얼거린다. \"이런, 정말 훌륭한 술입니다요!\"\n\n\"그래서 어젯밤 다녀온 일을 혼자만 알고 있었다는 거요?\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"오늘 밤도 그렇고?\"\n\n\"예, 나리, 오늘 밤도 그렇습니다. 제 마누라가 요즘—솔직히 말씀드려—신앙심에 빠진 상태라, 아니 본인이 그렇게 여기는 상태라서, 채드밴드라는 목사 양반의 '저녁 정진'이라는 데(그게 그 모임 이름입니다요)에 나갑니다. 그 양반 말솜씨가 굉장한 건 두말할 나위 없지만, 저는 그 양반 투가 그리 마음에 들지는 않습니다. 뭐 이건 여기서 할 얘기는 아니고요. 마누라가 그 일에 매여 있어서 제가 조용히 다녀오기가 한결 수월했지요.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 고개를 끄덕인다. \"잔 채우시오, 스내그스비.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 정말이지요, 나리.\" 문방구상이 공손한 기침과 함께 대답한다. \"이거 참 기막히게 좋은 술입니다요, 나리!\"\n\n\"이제는 귀한 술이지요.\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"오십 년 묵은 거요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까요, 나리? 하지만 그 말씀을 듣고도 놀랍지 않습니다요, 정말이지요. 이거야—거의 무슨 햇수든 다 될 만하니까요.\" 포트와인에 이렇게 두루뭉술 찬사를 바친 뒤, 스내그스비 씨는 겸손하게도 이토록 귀한 걸 마시는 데 대해 손으로 입을 가리고 사죄하듯 기침한다.\n\n\"그 아이가 한 말을 한 번 더 짚어 주시겠소?\" 토킹혼 씨가 낡은 반바지 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣고 의자에 조용히 기대며 묻는다.\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 나리.\"\n\n그러자 법률 문방구상은 충실하게, 다만 좀 장황하게, 조가 자기 집에 모인 손님들 앞에서 했던 진술을 되풀이한다. 이야기 끝에 다다랐을 때, 그는 화들짝 놀라며 말을 뚝 끊는다. \"아이고, 나리, 여기 다른 신사분이 계신 줄은 미처 몰랐습니다요!\"\n\n스내그스비 씨가 질겁한 것은, 탁자에서 조금 떨어진 곳, 자기와 변호사 사이에 모자와 지팡이를 손에 든 사람 하나가 주의 깊은 얼굴로 서 있는 걸 보았기 때문이다. 자기가 들어왔을 때는 거기 없던 사람이고, 그 뒤로 문으로든 두 창문 어느 쪽으로든 들어온 적도 없는 사람이다. 방 안에 장롱이 하나 있긴 하나 경첩이 삐걱대지도 않았고, 마룻바닥에 발소리 하나 들리지 않았다. 그런데도 이 세 번째 인물은 주의 깊은 얼굴로, 모자와 지팡이를 손에 들고 그 손을 뒤로 한 채, 차분하고 조용한 청자로 거기 서 있다. 검은 옷차림에 다부진 체격, 차분해 보이는 인상, 날카로운 눈매를 한 중년 사내다. 스내그스비 씨를 마치 초상화라도 그릴 듯이 바라본다는 것 말고는, 첫눈에 눈에 띄는 점이라곤 유령처럼 나타난 그 태도뿐이다.\n\n\"이분은 신경 쓰지 마시오.\" 토킹혼 씨가 조용한 투로 말한다. \"그냥 버킷 씨일 뿐이니까.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇습니까요, 나리?\" 문방구상이 대답하면서, 버킷 씨가 대체 누구인지 도무지 알 길이 없다는 뜻을 기침으로 내비친다.\n\n\"이 사람더러 그 얘기를 들으라 했소.\" 변호사가 말한다. \"내가 (그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서) 그걸 좀 더 알고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 있는데, 이 사람이 그런 일에 아주 밝거든. 어떻게 보나, 버킷?\"\n\n\"아주 간단합니다요, 나리. 우리 쪽 사람들이 그 아이를 쫓아내서 늘 있던 자리에 없으니, 스내그스비 씨가 저랑 톰 혼자 사는 곳까지 같이 내려가 그 아이를 짚어 주기만 한다면, 두 시간도 안 돼서 여기 데려올 수 있습니다요. 물론 스내그스비 씨 없이도 할 수 있지만, 이게 제일 빠른 길이지요.\"\n\n\"버킷 씨는 형사요, 스내그스비.\" 변호사가 설명을 덧붙인다.\n\n\"정말입니까요, 나리?\" 스내그스비 씨가 말하는데, 그 텁수룩한 머리칼이 곤두서려는 기색이 역력하다.\n\n\"버킷 씨와 함께 그곳에 가는 데 정말로 거리낄 게 없으시다면,\" 변호사가 말을 잇는다. \"그렇게 해 주시면 신세를 진 걸로 알겠소.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨가 한순간 머뭇거리자, 버킷은 그의 속마음 밑바닥까지 꿰뚫는다.\n\n\"그 아이를 해치게 될까 봐 걱정 마시오.\" 그가 말한다. \"그럴 일 없습니다. 그 아이한테는 아무 탈 없는 일이에요. 우리는 그저 그 아이를 여기 데려와 내가 묻고 싶은 걸 한두 가지 물어볼 뿐이고, 수고비를 줘서 다시 돌려보낼 겁니다. 그 아이한테는 좋은 일이죠. 사내대장부로서 약속하리다. 그 아이가 무사히 돌아가는 걸 당신 눈으로 보게 될 거요. 해치게 될까 봐 걱정 마시오. 그럴 일 없으니까.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 토킹혼 씨!\" 스내그스비 씨가 쾌활하게 외친다. 그러고는 안심한 듯, \"사정이 그렇다면—\"\n\n\"그렇소! 그리고 이것 보시오, 스내그스비 씨.\" 버킷이 그의 팔을 잡아 한쪽으로 데려가며 가슴을 친근하게 톡톡 두드리고는 은밀한 투로 말을 잇는다. \"당신은 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이고, 사업하는 사람이고, 분별 있는 사람이오. 당신이 그런 사람이란 말이지.\"\n\n\"좋게 봐 주셔서 정말 감사합니다요.\" 문방구상이 겸손한 기침과 함께 대답한다. \"하지만—\"\n\n\"당신이 그런 사람이라니까.\" 버킷이 말한다. \"자, 당신처럼 사업하는 사람한테 굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 그게 신용으로 하는 사업이라 사람이 정신을 바짝 차리고 감각을 곤두세우고 머리를 단단히 박고 있어야 하는 일 아니오 (한때 내 삼촌도 당신과 같은 장사를 했지요)—당신 같은 사람한테 굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 이런 자잘한 일은 조용히 묻어 두는 게 가장 현명하고 좋은 길이오. 알겠소? 조용히!\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 물론입니다.\" 상대가 대답한다.\n\n\"당신한테는 말해도 괜찮겠지.\" 버킷이 솔직한 척 사람 끄는 태도로 말한다. \"내가 알기로는, 이 죽은 사람이 작은 재산을 물려받을 권리가 있었던 게 아닌가 하는 의심이 있고, 또 어떤 여자가 그 재산을 두고 무슨 수작을 부린 게 아닌가 싶단 말이오, 알겠소?\"\n\n\"오!\" 스내그스비 씨가 말하지만, 썩 또렷이 알아들은 기색은 아니다.\n\n\"자, 당신이 바라는 건,\" 버킷이 다시 스내그스비 씨의 가슴을 편안하고 달래는 투로 톡톡 두드리며 말을 잇는다. \"정의에 따라 누구나 제 권리를 누리는 것 아니겠소. 당신이 바라는 게 그거지.\"\n\n\"암요, 그렇고말고요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 대답한다.\n\n\"그러니까, 그것 때문에, 그리고 동시에—당신네 장사에서는 손님이라 부르오, 고객이라 부르오? 우리 삼촌이 뭐라 불렀는지 깜빡했네.\"\n\n\"뭐, 저는 보통 손님이라고 합니다요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 대답한다.\n\n\"맞아요!\" 버킷 씨가 그와 아주 다정하게 악수하며 대꾸한다. \"—그것 때문에, 그리고 동시에 정말 좋은 손님 하나 봐주려고, 당신은 나랑 같이 비밀리에 톰 혼자 사는 곳에 내려가서, 그다음엔 이 일을 두고두고 조용히 묻어 두고 누구한테도 말하지 않을 작정이오. 그게 당신 뜻이지요, 내가 제대로 알아들었다면?\"\n\n\"맞습니다요, 나리. 맞습니다요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"그럼 여기 모자요.\" 새로 사귄 친구가 마치 제가 만든 모자라도 되는 양 격의 없이 다루며 대꾸한다. \"당신이 준비됐으면, 나도 됐소.\"\n\n두 사람은 가늠할 길 없는 깊은 속에 물결 하나 일지 않은 채 묵은 술을 마시는 토킹혼 씨를 남겨 두고, 거리로 내려간다.\n\n\"그리들리라는, 아주 괜찮은 양반 하나 혹시 알지 않소?\" 계단을 내려가며 버킷이 다정하게 말을 건넨다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 생각해 보고는 대답한다. \"그런 이름은 모르겠는데요. 왜 그러십니까?\"\n\n\"별것 아니오.\" 버킷이 말한다. \"그저 그 양반이 성질을 못 이겨 점잖은 사람들 몇을 위협한 일이 있어서, 내가 받아 둔 영장을 피해 숨어 다니고 있거든—분별 있는 사람이 그러다니 딱한 노릇이지요.\"\n\n길을 걸어가며 스내그스비 씨는 새삼스러운 일을 하나 알아챈다. 둘의 걸음이 아무리 빨라도, 동행은 여전히 무어라 형용하기 어려운 식으로 어슬렁대며 빈둥거리는 듯 보인다는 것이다. 또 오른쪽이든 왼쪽이든 돌려고 할 때면, 마음속에 곧장 앞으로 갈 작정이 정해진 척하다가 마지막 순간에 홱 방향을 튼다는 것도. 이따금 순찰 중인 순경을 지나칠 때면, 순경도 안내자도 서로 마주 다가오면서 깊은 상념에 잠겨 상대를 까맣게 못 본 척하며 허공을 응시한다는 것을, 스내그스비 씨는 알아챈다. 몇 번인가는, 번들거리는 모자를 쓰고 매끈한 머리칼을 양옆으로 한 가닥씩 납작하게 말아 붙인 작달막한 젊은이 뒤로 다가간 버킷 씨가, 거의 쳐다보지도 않고 지팡이로 툭 건드리자, 젊은이가 돌아보더니 그 자리에서 증발하듯 사라진다. 대체로 버킷 씨는 새끼손가락에 낀 큼직한 추모 반지나, 다이아몬드는 얼마 안 되고 받침 장식만 잔뜩인 셔츠 브로치만큼이나 변함없는 얼굴로 세상사 전반을 둘러본다.\n\n마침내 톰 혼자 사는 곳에 이르자, 버킷 씨는 모퉁이에서 잠시 멈춰 그곳 당직 순경에게 불 켠 등롱을 받아 든다. 순경은 자기 등롱을 허리에 차고 그를 따라나선다. 두 안내자 사이에 끼어, 스내그스비 씨는 흉악한 거리 한가운데를 지나간다. 다른 길은 다 말라 있는데도 이곳은 배수도 안 되고 통풍도 안 되어 시커먼 진흙과 썩은 물이 발목까지 잠기고, 평생 런던에서 살아온 그조차 제 감각을 믿기 어려울 만큼 지독한 냄새와 광경이 진동한다. 이 거리와 무더기진 폐허에서 뻗어 나간 또 다른 거리와 골목들이 어찌나 추악한지, 스내그스비 씨는 몸도 마음도 메스꺼워져 마치 순간순간 지옥의 구렁텅이 속으로 더 깊이 빠져드는 것만 같다.\n\n\"이쪽으로 좀 비키시오, 스내그스비 씨.\" 시끄러운 무리에 둘러싸인 채 허름한 가마 같은 것이 그들 쪽으로 실려 오자 버킷이 말한다. \"열병이 거리를 올라오고 있소!\"\n\n보이지 않는 가엾은 자가 지나가자, 그 구경거리를 버려둔 무리는 끔찍한 얼굴들로 이루어진 한바탕 꿈처럼 세 방문객 주위를 맴돌다가, 골목으로, 폐허 속으로, 담벼락 뒤로 스러져 간다. 그러고는 이따금 고함과 날카로운 경고의 휘파람을 내지르며, 그들이 이곳을 떠날 때까지 줄곧 주변을 어른거린다.\n\n\"저것들이 열병 집이냐, 더비?\" 버킷 씨가 악취 풍기는 폐허의 한 줄로 등롱을 돌리며 태연히 묻는다.\n\n더비는 \"다 그렇습니다\"라고 대답하고, 덧붙여 모든 집에서 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 사람들이 \"수십 명씩 쓰러졌고\" 죽거나 죽어 가는 채로 \"썩어 가는 양들처럼\" 실려 나갔다고 한다. 다시 걸음을 옮기며 버킷이 스내그스비 씨더러 안색이 좀 안 좋아 보인다고 하자, 스내그스비 씨는 이 끔찍한 공기를 도무지 들이쉴 수가 없을 것 같다고 대답한다.\n\n여러 집을 돌며 조라는 아이를 찾아 묻는다. 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서는 제대로 된 세례명으로 알려진 사람이 거의 없는 터라, 스내그스비 씨가 말하는 게 당근인지, 대령인지, 교수대인지, 어린 끌인지, 테리어 팁인지, 키다리인지, 벽돌인지를 두고 자꾸 되묻는다. 스내그스비 씨는 몇 번이고 다시 설명한다. 그가 그린 그림의 원본이 누구인지를 두고 의견이 엇갈린다. 더러는 당근이 분명하다 하고, 더러는 벽돌이라 한다. 대령이 끌려 나오지만 도무지 닮은 데가 없다. 스내그스비 씨와 두 안내자가 멈춰 설 때마다 무리가 주위로 몰려들어, 그 추레한 밑바닥에서 굽실거리는 조언이 버킷 씨에게 솟아오른다. 그들이 움직이고 성난 등롱불이 번득일 때마다, 무리는 아까처럼 골목으로, 폐허 속으로, 담벼락 뒤로 스러졌다가 다시 어른거린다.\n\n마침내 텁수룩이, 곧 텁수룩 양반이 밤에 누워 자는 굴 하나가 밝혀지고, 그 텁수룩 양반이 조일지도 모른다는 생각이 든다. 스내그스비 씨와 그 집 안주인—검은 보따리에 싸인 술 취한 얼굴로, 개집이나 다름없는 제 거처 마룻바닥의 누더기 더미 속에서 번득이며 내다보는 여자—이 서로 정보를 맞춰 본 끝에 이 결론에 다다른다. 텁수룩이는 병든 여자에게 줄 약 한 병을 받으러 의사한테 갔지만 곧 돌아올 거란다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 여기 있는 게 누구누구냐?\" 버킷 씨가 또 다른 문을 열고 등롱으로 안을 비추며 말한다. \"술 취한 사내 둘이라, 응? 그리고 여자 둘이고? 사내들은 잠은 잘 자는구먼.\" 자고 있는 사내들의 팔을 하나씩 얼굴에서 젖혀 보면서. \"이 사람들이 댁들 서방인가, 아주머니들?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 여자 하나가 대답한다. \"우리 남편들이오.\"\n\n\"벽돌공이고?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"여기서 뭣들 하고 있어? 런던 사람들이 아닐 텐데.\"\n\n\"예, 나리. 우리는 허트퍼드셔 사람들이오.\"\n\n\"허트퍼드셔 어디쯤?\"\n\n\"세인트 올번스요.\"\n\n\"걸어서 올라왔나?\"\n\n\"어제 걸어 올라왔소. 지금 우리 동네에는 일이 없는데, 여기 와 봐야 별수 없었고 앞으로도 별수 없을 듯싶소.\"\n\n\"그런다고 별수가 나는 건 아니지.\" 버킷 씨가 바닥에 정신없이 뻗은 사내들 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 말한다.\n\n\"정말 그렇소.\" 여자가 한숨을 쉬며 대답한다. \"제니랑 나는 그걸 뼈저리게 알지요.\"\n\n방은 문보다 두세 자쯤 높긴 하나 천장이 어찌나 낮은지, 방문객 중 가장 키 큰 이가 똑바로 선다면 머리가 시커먼 천장에 닿을 지경이다. 어느 감각에나 역겹기 그지없다. 굵직한 양초마저 더럽혀진 공기 속에서 파리하고 병약하게 타오른다. 긴 의자 두 개와, 탁자 노릇을 하는 좀 더 높은 의자 하나가 있다. 사내들은 비틀거리다 쓰러진 그 자리에 잠들어 있고, 여자들은 촛불 곁에 앉아 있다. 아까 말한 여자의 팔에는 아주 어린 아기가 안겨 있다.\n\n\"아니, 저 작은 것이 몇 살이오?\" 버킷이 말한다. \"어제 태어난 것 같구먼.\" 그 태도가 조금도 거칠지 않다. 그가 등불을 부드럽게 아기 쪽으로 돌리자, 스내그스비 씨는 그림 속에서 본 적 있는, 빛으로 둘러싸인 또 다른 아기가 묘하게 떠오른다.\n\n\"아직 세 주도 안 됐어요, 나리.\" 여자가 말한다.\n\n\"댁의 아기요?\"\n\n\"제 아기지요.\"\n들어왔을 때 아기 위로 몸을 숙이고 있던 다른 여자가, 다시 몸을 굽혀 잠든 아기에게 입을 맞춘다.\n\n\"꼭 댁이 친어머니라도 되는 것처럼 아끼는구먼.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"저도 이만한 아기의 어미였는데, 나리, 그 애가 죽었지요.\"\n\n\"아아, 제니, 제니!\" 다른 여자가 그녀에게 말한다. \"차라리 그게 나아. 산 것보다 죽은 걸 생각하는 게 훨씬 나아, 제니! 훨씬 나아!\"\n\n\"아니, 설마 제 자식이 죽기를 바랄 만큼,\" 버킷이 매섭게 대꾸한다. \"그렇게 모진 여자는 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"하느님은 아시지요, 나리 말씀이 옳다는 걸.\" 그녀가 대답한다. \"그런 건 아니에요. 할 수만 있다면 제 목숨을 던져서라도 그 애와 죽음 사이를 막아설 거예요, 어느 고운 마님 못지않게 진심으로요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그런 그릇된 소리는 마시오.\" 다시 누그러진 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"왜 그런 소리를 하나?\"\n\n\"머릿속에 그런 생각이 떠오르는걸요, 나리.\" 여자가 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해서 대답한다. \"저렇게 누운 아기를 내려다보면요. 만약 다시는 깨어나지 못한다면, 제가 어찌나 미쳐 날뛸지 나리도 절 미쳤다 하실 거예요. 저도 잘 알아요. 제니가 자기 애를 잃었을 때 제가 곁에 있었거든요—그렇지, 제니?—그 애가 얼마나 슬퍼했는지 저는 알아요. 하지만 이곳을 둘러보세요. 저들을 보세요.\" 바닥에 잠든 이들을 흘끔 보며. \"나리가 기다리는 저 아이를 보세요, 저를 도와주려고 나간 그 아이를요. 나리네 일이 흔하디흔하게 엮이고, 또 자라는 걸 나리도 보시는 그 아이들을 생각해 보세요!\"\n\n\"자, 자.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이 애를 번듯하게 키우면, 그 애가 댁한테 위안이 되고 늙어서 댁을 돌봐 줄 거 아니오.\"\n\n\"열심히 애써 볼 작정이에요.\" 그녀가 눈을 훔치며 대답한다. \"하지만 오늘 밤 너무 지치고 학질로 몸도 성치 않다 보니, 이 애 앞길에 닥칠 그 많은 일을 자꾸 생각하게 돼요. 제 남편은 그걸 반대할 테고, 그러면 이 앤 매를 맞고, 제가 맞는 것도 보고, 집을 무서워하게 되고, 어쩌면 거칠게 떠돌게 될 거예요. 제가 이 애를 위해 아무리 일하고 아무리 애써도, 도와줄 사람 하나 없고요. 제가 할 수 있는 걸 다 해도 끝내 이 애가 나쁘게 빗나가서, 모질어지고 딴사람이 되어 버린 이 애 곁에 제가 앉아 그 잠든 모습을 지켜보는 날이 온다면, 지금 제 무릎에 누운 이 애를 떠올리며 차라리 제니의 애처럼 죽어 버렸으면 했더라면 싶지 않겠어요!\"\n\n\"자, 자!\" 제니가 말한다. \"리즈, 넌 지치고 아프잖아. 내가 안을게.\"\n\n그러면서 제니가 어미의 옷을 헤치는데, 아기가 누워 있던 그 상처 나고 멍든 가슴 위로 얼른 옷을 다시 여며 준다.\n\n\"바로 내 죽은 애 때문에,\" 제니가 아기를 안고 어르며 이리저리 거니는데, \"내가 이 애를 이토록 끔찍이 사랑하는 거예요. 그리고 내 죽은 애 때문에, 저 사람도 이 애를 이토록 끔찍이 사랑해서, 지금 이 애를 빼앗긴다는 생각조차 못 견디는 거고요. 저 사람이 그렇게 생각할 때면, 나는 내 귀한 애를 되찾을 수만 있다면 무슨 복이라도 내놓겠다고 생각해요. 하지만 우리 둘은, 말로 옮길 줄만 안다면, 가엾은 마음속으로 똑같은 걸 생각하는 거예요, 어미 된 우리 둘이 말이에요!\"\n\n스내그스비 씨가 코를 풀고 동정의 기침을 하는데, 밖에서 발소리가 들린다. 버킷 씨가 등불을 문간으로 비추며 스내그스비 씨에게 말한다. \"자, 텁수룩이는 어떻소? 저 애면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"저게 조올시다.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n조는 등불 동그라미 속에서, 마술 환등기 속 누더기 형상처럼 어리둥절 서 있다. 충분히 멀리 가지 않아 법을 어긴 게 아닐까 싶어 벌벌 떤다. 그러나 스내그스비 씨가 \"수고하면 돈 받을 일일 뿐이다, 조\" 하고 다독여 안심시키자, 조는 기운을 차린다. 버킷 씨가 조용히 의논하려고 조를 밖으로 데려가자, 조는 숨을 헐떡이면서도 제 이야기를 흡족하게 늘어놓는다.\n\n\"그 애랑 얘기 다 맞췄으니,\" 버킷 씨가 돌아오며 말한다. \"다 됐소. 자, 스내그스비 씨, 당신 차례요.\"\n\n먼저 조는 받아 온 약을 넘겨주어 마음씨 좋은 심부름을 마무리하는데, \"지금 당장 다 먹어야 한다\"는 짤막한 당부와 함께 건넨다. 다음으로 스내그스비 씨가 탁자 위에 반 크라운을 올려놓으니, 이는 그가 온갖 종류의 곤경에 두루 쓰는 만병통치약이다. 셋째로 버킷 씨가 조의 팔을 팔꿈치 조금 위로 잡고 자기 앞에 세워 걷게 하니, 이 절차가 없으면 텁수룩 양반이든 그 어떤 양반이든 격식대로 링컨 법학원 광장까지 인도될 수가 없다. 이 절차가 모두 끝나자, 그들은 여자들에게 잘 자라 인사하고 다시금 시커멓고 더러운 톰 혼자 사는 곳으로 나온다.\n\n그 구렁텅이로 내려갔던 그 역겨운 길을 따라, 그들은 차츰 그곳을 빠져나온다. 무리가 어른거리고 휘파람 불고 슬금슬금 주위를 맴돌다가, 마침내 가장자리에 이르러 더비에게 등롱을 돌려준다. 여기서 무리는 갇혀 있던 악귀 떼처럼 악을 쓰며 돌아서고, 다시는 보이지 않는다. 스내그스비 씨의 마음에 지금처럼 맑고 상쾌해 보인 적 없는 한결 맑고 산뜻한 거리들을 지나, 그들은 걷고 또 타고 하여 토킹혼 씨네 대문에 이른다.\n\n어둑한 계단을 오르며(토킹혼 씨의 사무실은 이 층에 있다), 버킷 씨는 바깥문 열쇠가 주머니에 있으니 초인종을 울릴 필요가 없다고 일러 준다. 그런 일에는 누구보다 능숙한 버킷이건만, 문을 여는 데 한참 시간을 들이고 소리도 제법 낸다. 어쩌면 준비하라는 신호음을 울리는 것인지도 모른다.\n\n아무튼 그들은 마침내 등불 하나 켜진 현관에 들어서고, 이어 토킹혼 씨의 평소 방으로—오늘 밤 그가 묵은 술을 마시던 그 방으로—들어선다. 그는 거기 없지만 그의 구식 촛대 두 개가 놓여 있어, 방은 그런대로 환하다.\n\n여전히 직업적인 솜씨로 조를 붙든 채, 스내그스비 씨가 보기엔 눈이 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 듯한 버킷 씨가 이 방으로 조금 들어서는데, 조가 흠칫 멈춰 선다.\n\n\"왜 그래?\" 버킷이 속삭인다.\n\n\"저기 그 여자가 있어요!\" 조가 외친다.\n\n\"누구?\"\n\n\"그 마님이요!\"\n\n베일을 촘촘히 두른 여자 형상 하나가 방 한가운데, 빛이 떨어지는 자리에 서 있다. 미동도 없고 소리도 없다. 형상의 앞쪽이 그들을 향해 있으나, 그들이 들어선 것에 아랑곳없이 조각상처럼 가만히 있다.\n\n\"자, 말해 봐라.\" 버킷이 큰 소리로 말한다. \"저게 그 마님인 걸 어떻게 아느냐.\"\n\n\"베일을 알아요.\" 조가 빤히 쳐다보며 대답한다. \"그리고 보닛이랑, 가운도요.\"\n\n\"네 말을 똑똑히 확인해라, 텁수룩이.\" 버킷이 그를 면밀히 살피며 대꾸한다. \"다시 봐라.\"\n\n\"있는 힘껏 보고 있는걸요.\" 조가 눈을 부릅뜨며 말한다. \"저게 바로 그 베일이고, 보닛이고, 가운이에요.\"\n\n\"네가 말한 그 반지들은?\" 버킷이 묻는다.\n\n\"여기 온통 반짝거렸어요.\" 조가 형상에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 왼손 손가락으로 오른손 손마디를 문지르며 말한다.\n\n형상이 오른손 장갑을 벗고 손을 보인다.\n\n\"자, 저건 어떠냐?\" 버킷이 묻는다.\n\n조가 고개를 젓는다. \"그것들이랑 조금도 안 닮은 반지예요. 그런 손도 아니고요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야?\" 버킷이 말하는데, 분명 흡족한, 그것도 아주 흡족한 기색이다.\n\n\"손이 훨씬 더 희고, 훨씬 더 곱고, 훨씬 더 작았어요.\" 조가 대답한다.\n\n\"허, 이러다 내가 내 친어미라고 하겠구나.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그 마님 목소리는 기억하느냐?\"\n\n\"기억하는 것 같아요.\" 조가 말한다.\n\n형상이 입을 연다. \"혹시 이런 목소리였니? 확실치 않으면 네가 원하는 만큼 얼마든 말해 주마. 이 목소리였니, 아니면 이 목소리와 비슷하기라도 하니?\"\n\n조가 버킷 씨를 질겁한 얼굴로 본다. \"조금도 안 닮았어요!\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서,\" 그 훌륭한 사내가 형상을 가리키며 되묻는다. \"저게 그 마님이라고 했느냐?\"\n\n\"그건요,\" 조가 어리둥절 빤히 보면서도 확신은 조금도 흔들리지 않은 채 말한다. \"그건 저게 바로 그 베일이고, 보닛이고, 가운이라서요. 저건 그 여자이면서도 그 여자가 아니에요. 그 여자 손도 아니고, 반지도 아니고, 목소리도 아니에요. 그치만 저게 바로 그 베일이고, 보닛이고, 가운이고, 그 여자가 두르던 그대로 둘렀고, 키도 그 여자만 하고, 그 여자는 나한테 일 파운드 금화를 주고 내뺐다고요.\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 버킷 씨가 대수롭잖게 말한다. \"너한테서는 별로 건진 게 없구나. 그래도, 자, 여기 오 실링이다. 어떻게 쓰는지 조심하고, 말썽에 휘말리지 마라.\" 버킷은 동전을 한 손에서 다른 손으로 패 세듯 슬쩍 헤아리는데—이건 그의 버릇으로, 동전을 주로 이런 손재간 놀이에 쓴다—그러고는 작은 무더기로 모아 아이 손에 쥐여 주고 문 밖으로 데리고 나간다. 그 바람에 이 알 수 없는 상황에 도무지 마음이 편치 않은 스내그스비 씨는 베일 두른 형상과 단둘이 남겨진다. 그러나 토킹혼 씨가 방으로 들어오자 베일이 걷히고, 제법 반반한 프랑스 여자가 드러나는데, 그 표정만은 어딘가 더없이 사나운 데가 있다.\n\n\"고맙소, 오르탕스 양.\" 토킹혼 씨가 평소의 침착함으로 말한다. \"이 자그마한 내기 건으로 더는 폐를 끼치지 않으리다.\"\n\n\"제가 지금은 자리가 없는 처지인 걸 부디 잊지 말아 주시겠지요, 나리?\" 마드무아젤이 말한다.\n\n\"물론이오, 물론.\"\n\n\"그리고 나리의 그 빛나는 추천이라는 호의를 제게 베풀어 주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠소, 오르탕스 양.\"\n\n\"토킹혼 씨의 한마디는 그토록 막강하니까요.\"\n\n\"빠짐없이 해 드리리다, 마드무아젤.\"\n\n\"제 한결같은 감사를 받아 주십시오, 친애하는 나리.\"\n\n\"잘 가시오.\"\n\n마드무아젤은 타고난 기품 어린 자태로 나간다. 그리고 버킷 씨는, 비상시에는 의전관 노릇 하는 것이 다른 무엇 하는 것 못지않게 자연스러운 사람인지라, 자못 정중하게 그녀를 아래층까지 배웅한다.\n\n\"그래, 버킷?\" 돌아온 그에게 토킹혼 씨가 묻는다.\n\n\"보시다시피, 제가 직접 맞춰 둔 대로 다 정리됐습니다요, 나리. 그게 이 여자 옷을 입은 그 다른 여자였다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없습니다. 그 애가 빛깔이며 뭐며 다 정확했지요. 스내그스비 씨, 그 애가 무사히 돌아갈 거라고 사내대장부로서 약속드렸는데, 그대로 되지 않았다곤 못 하실 겁니다!\"\n\n\"약속을 지키셨소, 나리.\" 문방구상이 대답한다. \"더 도울 일이 없다면, 토킹혼 씨, 제 마누라가 애를 태우고 있을 듯해서—\"\n\n\"고맙소, 스내그스비, 더 도울 일은 없소.\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"이미 수고해 주신 것만으로도 신세를 톡톡히 졌소.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 나리. 그럼 안녕히 주무십시오.\"\n\n\"있잖소, 스내그스비 씨.\" 버킷 씨가 그를 문까지 배웅하며 거듭거듭 악수하면서 말한다. \"내가 당신한테서 마음에 드는 점은, 당신이 캐물어 봐야 아무 소용없는 사람이란 거요. 당신이 그런 사람이지. 옳은 일을 했다 싶으면 그걸 딱 치워 버리고, 그걸로 끝, 사라져 버리고, 그게 끝이오. 당신이 그러는 사람이지.\"\n\n\"그러려고 애쓰는 건 분명합니다요, 나리.\" 스내그스비 씨가 대답한다.\n\n\"아니, 당신은 자기를 너무 박하게 보는구먼. 애쓰는 정도가 아니라.\" 버킷 씨가 그와 악수하며 더없이 다정하게 축복을 빌어 주듯 말한다. \"당신은 실제로 그렇게 하는 사람이오. 그게 바로 내가 당신 같은 장사하는 사람한테서 높이 사는 점이지.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 알맞은 대꾸를 하고 집으로 향하는데, 저녁의 일들에 어찌나 얼떨떨한지 자기가 깨어 밖에 나와 있는 건지조차 미덥지 않고—지나는 거리가 진짜인지도 미덥지 않고—머리 위에 빛나는 달이 진짜인지도 미덥지 않다. 그러나 이 문제들에 대해 그는 곧 의심할 여지 없는 진짜인 스내그스비 부인 덕에 안심하게 되니, 그녀는 머리에 종이 말이 개와 잠자리 모자를 완벽한 벌집처럼 두르고 안 자고 기다리는 중이다. 그녀는 남편이 해코지를 당했다는 공식 정보를 들려 거스터를 경찰서로 보낸 데다, 지난 두 시간 동안 더없이 점잖은 품위를 갖추고 기절의 온갖 단계를 두루 거쳤더랬다. 그러나 이 자그마한 부인이 절절하게 말하듯, 그렇게 해 준 보답이 참으로 융숭하기도 하지!\n\n여섯 주에 걸친 즐거운 시간을 보낸 뒤, 우리는 보이손 씨 댁에서 집으로 돌아왔다. 우리는 공원과 숲을 자주 거닐었고, 비를 피했던 그 오두막을 지날 때면 으레 안을 들여다보며 관리인의 아내와 말을 나누곤 했다. 하지만 데드록 마님은 일요일 교회에서 말고는 더 보지 못했다. 체스니 월드에는 손님이 모여 있었고, 아름다운 얼굴들이 마님을 에워쌌지만, 마님의 얼굴은 처음과 똑같은 영향을 내게 미쳤다. 그것이 괴로움이었는지 즐거움이었는지, 나를 마님에게로 끌어당겼는지 아니면 움츠러들게 했는지, 나는 지금도 잘 모르겠다. 나는 일종의 두려움을 품고 마님을 흠모했던 것 같고, 마님 앞에 서면 내 생각은 처음 그랬듯이 늘 내 삶의 그 옛 시절로 떠돌아갔다.\n\n이런 일요일이 거듭되는 동안, 나는 한 번 이상 이런 공상에 잠겼다. 이 부인이 그토록 묘하게 나에게 어떤 존재인 것처럼, 나 또한 그분에게 그런 존재가 아닐까 하는 것이었다. 그러니까, 마님이 내 생각을 흔들어 놓듯이, 비록 어딘가 다른 방식이긴 해도 나도 마님의 생각을 어지럽히는 게 아닐까 싶었다. 하지만 슬쩍 곁눈질로 마님을 보면, 그토록 침착하고 멀고 다가갈 수 없는 모습이어서, 나는 이것이 어리석은 나약함이라고 느꼈다. 정말이지, 마님과 관련한 내 마음 전체가 나약하고 분별없다고 느껴졌고, 나는 그 점을 두고 할 수 있는 한 스스로를 타일렀다.\n\n보이손 씨 댁을 떠나기 전에 일어난 한 가지 일을, 여기서 짚고 넘어가는 편이 낫겠다.\n\n내가 에이다와 함께 정원을 거닐고 있을 때, 누군가 나를 만나고 싶어 한다는 전갈이 왔다. 그 사람이 기다리고 있는 아침 식당으로 들어가 보니, 천둥과 번개가 치던 날 신발을 벗어 던지고 젖은 풀밭을 걸어가던 그 프랑스인 하녀였다.\n\n\"마드무아젤,\" 그녀는 너무 간절한 눈빛으로 나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 입을 열었다. 그래도 다른 면에서는 호감 가는 모습이었고, 말투에 당돌함도 비굴함도 없었다. \"이렇게 찾아온 게 큰 결례인 줄 압니다만, 마드무아젤, 워낙 다정하신 분이니 너그러이 봐주실 줄로 알아요.\"\n\n\"용서를 구할 일은 없어요.\" 내가 대답했다. \"내게 할 말이 있다면 말이에요.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 제 바람입니다, 마드무아젤. 허락해 주셔서 천 번 감사드려요. 말씀드려도 되는 거지요. 그렇지요?\" 그녀가 빠르고 자연스러운 말씨로 물었다.\n\n\"물론이에요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"마드무아젤, 정말 다정하세요! 그럼 들어 주세요, 부탁드려요. 저는 마님 곁을 떠났습니다. 우리는 도무지 맞질 않았어요. 마님은 어찌나 도도하신지, 정말이지 너무나 도도하세요. 죄송해요! 마드무아젤, 옳으신 말씀이에요!\" 그녀의 재빠름은 내가 곧 입에 올렸을 법한, 그러나 아직 생각만 하고 있던 말을 앞질렀다. \"여기까지 와서 마님을 험담할 처지는 못 됩니다. 하지만 마님이 너무나 도도하시다는 것만은 말씀드려요. 정말 너무나 도도하세요. 더는 한마디도 하지 않겠어요. 세상이 다 아는 사실이니까요.\"\n\n\"계속하세요, 부탁드려요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요. 마드무아젤, 친절하게 대해 주셔서 감사합니다. 마드무아젤, 저는 착하고, 교양 있고, 아름다운 젊은 아가씨를 모시고 싶은 마음이 이루 말할 수 없습니다. 마드무아젤께서는 천사처럼 착하고, 교양 있고, 아름다우세요. 아, 제가 마드무아젤의 하녀가 되는 영광을 누릴 수만 있다면!\"\n\n\"미안하지만—\" 내가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"그렇게 빨리 물리치지 말아 주세요, 마드무아젤!\" 그녀는 고운 검은 눈썹을 저도 모르게 찌푸리며 말했다. \"잠깐만 희망을 품게 해 주세요! 마드무아젤, 저도 압니다. 이 자리가 제가 떠나온 그 자리보다 훨씬 한적하리라는 걸요. 좋아요! 저는 그걸 바라요. 이 자리가 제가 떠나온 그 자리보다 덜 화려하리라는 것도 압니다. 좋아요! 저는 그걸 바라요. 여기서는 받는 삯이 더 적으리라는 것도 알아요. 좋습니다. 저는 만족해요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말하지만,\" 그런 시중꾼을 둔다는 생각만으로도 무척 당황하여 내가 말했다. \"나는 하녀를 두지 않아요—\"\n\n\"아, 마드무아젤, 그렇지만 왜 안 되나요? 이토록 헌신할 사람을 둘 수 있는데 왜 안 되겠어요! 마드무아젤을 모시는 데 황홀해할 사람을요. 날마다 그토록 진실하고, 그토록 열성적이고, 그토록 충직할 사람을요! 마드무아젤, 저는 진심으로 마드무아젤을 모시고 싶습니다. 지금은 돈 이야기는 하지 마세요. 있는 그대로 저를 받아 주세요. 거저요!\"\n\n그녀가 어찌나 유별나게 간절한지 나는 거의 겁이 나 뒷걸음질을 쳤다. 그런 기색을 알아챈 내색도 없이, 그녀는 열정에 휩싸여 여전히 빠르고 낮은 목소리로 나에게 자꾸만 매달렸다. 그러면서도 늘 어떤 우아함과 단정함을 잃지 않았다.\n\n\"마드무아젤, 저는 남쪽 지방 출신이에요. 그곳 사람들은 성미가 급하고, 좋아하고 싫어하는 게 아주 분명하지요. 마님은 저에게 너무 도도하셨고, 저도 마님에게 너무 도도했어요. 끝난 일이에요—지나간—마무리된 일입니다! 저를 하녀로 받아 주세요. 그럼 잘 모실게요. 마드무아젤이 지금 상상하시는 것보다 더 잘해 드릴 거예요. 쉿! 마드무아젤, 저는—무슨 일이든, 무슨 일이든 제가 할 수 있는 모든 걸 다할 거예요. 제 시중을 받아 주시면 후회하지 않으실 거예요. 마드무아젤, 후회하지 않으실 거예요. 그리고 저는 잘 모실게요. 얼마나 잘 모실지 모르실 거예요!\"\n\n내가 그녀를 들일 수 없는 까닭을 설명하는 동안(들이고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 없는지까지 굳이 말할 필요는 없다고 여겼다), 서서 나를 바라보는 그녀의 얼굴에는 음울하게 도사린 기운이 어려 있었는데, 그것이 공포 정치 시절 파리 거리를 누비던 어떤 여인의 모습을 눈앞에 선연히 떠올리게 했다.\n\n그녀는 내 말을 끊지 않고 끝까지 듣고는, 고운 억양과 더없이 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"어머, 마드무아젤, 답을 들었네요! 유감입니다. 그래도 다른 데로 가서 여기서 못 찾은 것을 찾아봐야지요. 황송하오나 손등에 입을 맞추게 해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n그녀는 내 손을 잡으며 더욱 골똘히 나를 바라보았고, 잠깐 닿는 동안 그 손의 핏줄 하나하나까지 새겨 두려는 듯했다. \"폭풍우가 치던 날, 제가 마드무아젤을 놀라게 했나 봐요?\" 그녀는 작별의 절을 하며 말했다.\n\n나는 우리 모두를 놀라게 했다고 인정했다.\n\n\"저는 맹세를 했답니다, 마드무아젤.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그래서 그 맹세를 충실히 지키려고 마음에 새겨 두고 싶었어요. 꼭 지킬 거예요! 안녕히 계세요, 마드무아젤!\"\n\n이렇게 우리의 면담은 끝났고, 나는 이 자리를 마무리하게 되어 무척 기뻤다. 짐작하건대 그녀는 마을을 떠난 듯했다. 그 뒤로 다시는 보지 못했으니까. 그리고 여섯 주가 다 차서 방금 말한 대로 집으로 돌아올 때까지, 우리의 고요한 여름날의 즐거움을 흩뜨릴 일은 더는 일어나지 않았다.\n\n그 무렵, 그리고 그 뒤로도 여러 주에 걸쳐 리처드는 꾸준히 우리를 찾아왔다. 토요일이나 일요일마다 와서 월요일 아침까지 우리와 함께 지내는 것은 물론, 이따금 불쑥 말을 타고 나와서 저녁을 함께 보낸 뒤 이튿날 일찍 다시 말을 타고 돌아가기도 했다. 그는 여전히 활기가 넘쳤고 자기가 무척 부지런히 일한다고 말했지만, 나는 그가 마음에 걸려 편치 않았다. 내가 보기에 그의 부지런함은 죄다 엉뚱한 데로 쏠려 있었다. 그 부지런함이 이미 그토록 많은 슬픔과 파멸을 불러온 그 해로운 소송과 얽혀 헛된 희망을 키우는 것 말고 무엇으로도 이어지지 않는 듯했다. 그는 이제 그 수수께끼의 핵심을 파고들었다며, 대법원에 조금이라도 분별이나 정의가 남아 있다면 자기와 에이다가 몇천 파운드인지 모를 돈을 받게 될 그 유언장이 끝내 인정받으리라는 것보다 더 명백한 일은 없다고 말했다. 하지만, 아, 그 \"만약\"이라는 말이 내 귀에는 얼마나 거대하게 울렸던가. 그리고 이 행복한 결말이 그리 오래 미뤄지지는 않으리라고 했다. 그는 그쪽 편에 서서 읽은 온갖 지긋지긋한 논거를 들어 이를 스스로에게 입증해 보였는데, 그 하나하나가 그를 망상 속으로 더 깊이 빠뜨렸다. 그는 심지어 법정을 들락거리기 시작했다. 그는 거기서 날마다 플라이트 양을 본다고, 둘이 함께 이야기를 나눈다고, 그녀에게 자잘한 친절을 베푼다고, 그러면서도 그녀를 비웃으면서 한편으로는 진심으로 가엾이 여긴다고 들려주었다. 하지만 그는 결코 생각하지 못했다—결코, 가엾고 다정하고 낙천적인 내 리처드, 그때만 해도 그토록 큰 행복을 누릴 수 있었고 그 앞에 그토록 더 나은 것들이 펼쳐져 있던 그 사람은—그의 싱그러운 젊음과 그녀의 시든 노년 사이에, 그의 자유로운 희망과 그녀의 새장에 갇힌 새들과 그녀의 굶주린 다락방과 그녀의 헤매는 정신 사이에 얼마나 운명적인 사슬이 단단히 채워지고 있는지를.\n\n에이다는 그를 너무나 사랑한지라 그가 하는 말이나 행동을 그다지 의심하지 못했고, 후견인은 동풍이 분다고 자주 투덜대며 으르렁 방에서 평소보다 책을 많이 읽긴 했지만 이 일에 대해서는 굳게 입을 다물었다. 그래서 어느 날 캐디 젤리비의 청을 받고 그녀를 만나러 런던에 가게 되었을 때, 나는 리처드에게 역마차 사무소에서 나를 기다려 달라고 부탁하여 둘이서 잠깐 이야기를 나누어 보아야겠다고 생각했다. 내가 도착하니 그가 거기 있었고, 우리는 팔짱을 끼고 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"리처드,\" 그를 두고 진지해질 만하다 싶자마자 내가 말했다. \"이제 좀 자리가 잡히는 것 같니?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼, 에스더!\" 리처드가 대답했다. \"난 충분히 괜찮아.\"\n\n\"하지만 자리가 잡혔냐고?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자리가 잡힌다는 게 무슨 뜻이야?\" 리처드가 명랑하게 웃으며 되물었다.\n\n\"법조계에 자리가 잡혔냐고.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그래,\" 리처드가 대답했다. \"난 충분히 괜찮아.\"\n\n\"방금도 그렇게 말했잖아, 리처드.\"\n\n\"그게 대답이 안 된다고 생각하는 거지, 응? 그래! 아마 아닐지도 모르지. 자리가 잡혔냐고? 그러니까, 내가 자리를 잡아 가는 것 같으냐는 거지?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 아니지, 자리를 잡아 간다고는 못 하겠어.\" 리처드가 마치 그 말에 어려움이 담겨 있다는 듯 \"잡아\"에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했다. \"이 일이 이렇게 안 잡힌 채로 있는 한 누구도 자리를 잡을 수가 없으니까. 이 일이라고 하면, 물론 그—입에 올리면 안 되는 그 일을 말하는 거야.\"\n\n\"그게 언젠가 매듭이 지어지긴 할 것 같니?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"한 치도 의심하지 않아.\" 리처드가 대답했다.\n\n우리는 말없이 조금 걸었고, 이윽고 리처드가 더없이 솔직하고 진실한 태도로 나에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"에스더, 무슨 말인지 알아. 그리고 내가 좀 더 한결같은 사람이었으면 하고 진심으로 바라. 에이다에게 한결같다는 뜻이 아니야. 난 그 애를 끔찍이 사랑하니까—날마다 점점 더—그게 아니라 나 자신에게 한결같다는 뜻이야. (어쩐지, 잘 표현은 못 하겠지만, 너라면 알아들을 거야.) 내가 좀 더 한결같은 사람이었다면, 배저 선생이든 켄지 앤 카보이든 죽기 살기로 매달렸을 테고, 지금쯤은 차분하고 체계적으로 굴기 시작했을 테고, 빚도 안 졌을 테고, 그리고—\"\n\n\"빚을 졌단 말이니, 리처드?\"\n\n\"응,\" 리처드가 말했다. \"조금 졌어, 에스더. 게다가 당구나 그런 것에도 좀 지나치게 손을 댔어. 자, 이제 죄다 털어놓았다. 너 나를 경멸하는 거지, 에스더, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"내가 그러지 않는 거 알잖아.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 내가 나 자신을 대하는 것보다 훨씬 다정하게 나를 대해 주는구나.\" 그가 대답했다. \"에스더, 나는 자리를 못 잡는 참 불운한 놈이야. 그렇지만 내가 어떻게 자리를 잡을 수 있겠어? 완공되지 않은 집에서 산다면 거기에 정착할 수 없을 거야. 손대는 일마다 끝맺지 못한 채 내버려 두라는 선고를 받는다면, 무슨 일에든 마음 붙이기가 힘들 거야. 그런데 그게 바로 내 불행한 처지야. 나는 온갖 우여곡절을 안은 이 끝나지 않는 분쟁 속으로 태어났고, 그것은 내가 소송과 양복 한 벌의 차이도 채 알기 전부터 나를 들뜨게 흔들어 놓기 시작했어. 그 뒤로 줄곧 나를 흔들어 놓았고, 그래서 지금 나는 이러고 있는 거야. 나를 믿어 주는 사촌 에이다를 사랑하기엔 나야말로 보잘것없는 놈이라고 이따금 절감하면서 말이야.\"\n\n우리는 한적한 곳에 있었고, 그는 이 말을 하면서 두 손으로 눈을 가리고 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"오, 리처드!\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇게 마음 아파하지 마. 너는 고결한 천성을 지녔고, 에이다의 사랑이 날마다 너를 더 훌륭하게 만들어 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"알아, 에스더.\" 그가 내 팔을 꼭 쥐며 대답했다. \"다 알아. 내가 지금 조금 무른 꼴을 보여도 흉보지 마. 이걸 오랫동안 마음에 담아 두고 너한테 말하려고 몇 번이나 마음먹었는데, 어떨 땐 기회가 없고 어떨 땐 용기가 없었어. 에이다를 생각하면 마땅히 내가 어떻게 되어야 하는지 알지만, 그게 그렇게 되질 않아. 나는 그러기에도 너무 들떠 있어. 나는 그 애를 더없이 헌신적으로 사랑하면서도, 날이면 날마다 시간마다 나 자신을 그르치며 그 애에게도 못할 짓을 하고 있어. 하지만 이게 영원히 갈 수는 없어. 우리는 최종 심리에 회부되어 우리에게 유리한 판결을 받을 거야. 그러면 너랑 에이다도 내가 정말 어떤 사람이 될 수 있는지 보게 될 거야!\"\n\n그가 흐느끼는 소리를 듣고 손가락 사이로 눈물이 솟는 것을 보니 가슴이 미어졌지만, 그가 이 말을 하며 보인 희망에 찬 활기는 그보다 비할 데 없이 더 나를 아프게 했다.\n\n\"그 서류들을 잘 들여다봤어, 에스더. 몇 달째 깊이 파고들었지.\" 그는 순식간에 명랑함을 되찾으며 말을 이었다. \"우리가 승리를 거두리라는 걸 믿어도 돼. 세월이 끌린 거로 말하자면, 하늘이 아시듯이 모자란 적이 없었지! 그러니 이 일을 빨리 끝낼 가능성이 그만큼 더 크다는 거야. 사실 지금 심리 일정에 올라 있어. 끝내 다 잘될 거고, 그러면 너도 보게 될 거야!\"\n\n그가 방금 켄지 앤 카보이 변호사 사무소를 배저 선생과 같은 부류로 묶어 말한 것이 떠올라, 나는 언제 링컨 법학원에서 견습 계약을 맺을 생각이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"또 그 얘기로구나! 아예 안 할 것 같아, 에스더.\" 그가 애써 대답했다. \"그건 이제 진절머리가 난 것 같아. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에 노예처럼 매달려 일하다 보니, 법에 대한 갈증은 풀렸고 내가 그걸 좋아하지 않으리라는 걸 알게 됐어. 게다가 이렇게 늘 사건 현장에 붙어 있으니 점점 더 마음이 들뜨더라고. 그러니,\" 리처드는 이때쯤 다시 자신감을 되찾고 말을 이었다. \"내가 자연스레 마음을 두는 게 뭐겠어?\"\n\n\"짐작이 안 가는걸.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 심각한 표정 짓지 마.\" 리처드가 대답했다. \"이게 내가 할 수 있는 최선이거든, 에스더, 확실해. 평생 직업이 필요한 것도 아니잖아. 이 소송 절차는 끝날 테고, 그러면 나는 살길이 마련되는 거니까. 아니야. 나는 그것을 본디 어느 정도 정착되지 않은 일, 그래서 내 임시 처지에 딱 맞는—그래, 그야말로 딱 맞는 일이라고 봐. 내가 자연스레 마음을 두는 게 뭐겠어?\"\n\n나는 그를 바라보며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"군대 말고,\" 리처드가 더없이 확신에 찬 어조로 말했다. \"뭐가 있겠어!\"\n\n\"군대?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"군대지, 당연히. 내가 할 일은 장교 임관장을 따는 거야. 그러면—나는 그걸로 된 거지, 알겠어!\" 리처드가 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 수첩에 적은 정교한 계산을 들이대며 나에게 입증해 보였다. 가령 군대 밖에서 여섯 달 동안 이백 파운드쯤 빚을 졌다 치고, 군대 안에서는 같은 기간에 빚을 전혀 안 진다 하면—이 점은 그가 단단히 마음을 정한 바였다—이 한 걸음으로 한 해에 사백 파운드, 또는 오 년에 이천 파운드를 아끼게 되는 셈이고, 이는 상당한 금액이라는 것이었다. 그러고는 한동안 에이다 곁을 떠나며 치르는 희생에 대해, 또 에이다의 사랑에 보답하고 그녀의 행복을 지켜 주고 자기 안의 잘못된 점을 이겨 내고 결단의 정수 그 자체를 갖추기를—내가 잘 알듯이 그가 늘 마음속으로 갈망하던 대로—얼마나 간절히 바라는지에 대해 어찌나 천진하고 진실하게 말하던지, 내 가슴은 날카롭고 쓰라리게 저며 왔다. 나는 생각했기 때문이다. 이게 어떻게 끝날까, 어떻게 끝날 수 있을까. 이토록 빨리 또 이토록 틀림없이 그의 사내다운 자질이 죄다, 깃드는 것마다 망가뜨리는 그 운명의 마름병에 닿아 버리는데.\n\n나는 내가 느끼는 온갖 간절함을 담아, 그리고 그때는 도무지 품을 수 없던 온갖 희망을 담아 리처드에게 말했고, 에이다를 위해서라도 대법원에 아무 믿음도 걸지 말라고 애원했다. 내가 한 모든 말에 리처드는 선뜻 동의하면서도, 그 특유의 태평한 투로 법정이며 무엇이며 죄다 가뿐히 넘겨 버리고는, 자기가 장차 정착하게 될 인물상을 더없이 밝게 그려 보였다—아, 그 비통한 소송이 그를 옭아맨 손아귀를 풀어 줄 때 말이다! 우리는 오래 이야기를 나누었지만, 결국 알맹이는 늘 그 이야기로 되돌아왔다.\n\n마침내 우리는 소호 광장에 이르렀다. 캐디 젤리비가 뉴먼 거리 근처의 조용한 곳이라며 나를 기다리겠다고 정해 둔 데였다. 캐디는 한가운데 정원에 있다가, 내가 모습을 보이자마자 서둘러 나왔다. 명랑한 인사를 몇 마디 나눈 뒤, 리처드는 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"프린스가 길 건너에서 제자를 가르치고 있어, 에스더.\" 캐디가 말했다. \"우리한테 열쇠를 구해 줬어. 그러니까 나랑 여기를 빙빙 거닐면, 안에 우리끼리만 잠그고 들어앉아서 네 다정하고 착한 얼굴을 보고 싶었던 그 이야기를 마음 편히 들려줄 수 있어.\"\n\n\"좋아, 캐디.\" 내가 말했다. \"그보다 좋을 순 없지.\" 그러자 캐디는 그 다정하고 착한 얼굴이라고 부르던 내 얼굴을 애정 어린 손길로 한번 매만지고는 문을 잠그고 내 팔짱을 끼었고, 우리는 아늑하게 정원을 빙빙 돌기 시작했다.\n\n\"있잖아, 에스더.\" 비밀 이야기를 한껏 즐기던 캐디가 말했다. \"네가 엄마 모르게 결혼하는 건 잘못이고, 우리 약혼을 엄마한테 오래 숨겨 두는 것도 잘못이라고 말해 준 뒤로—솔직히 엄마가 날 그리 마음에 두는 것 같진 않지만—나는 네 의견을 프린스한테 전해 주는 게 옳겠다 싶었어. 첫째로는 네가 해 주는 말은 뭐든 새겨 듣고 싶어서고, 둘째로는 프린스한테는 숨기는 게 하나도 없어서야.\"\n\n\"그가 찬성하던, 캐디?\"\n\n\"오, 얘! 장담하는데 네가 한 말이라면 뭐든 찬성할걸. 그 사람이 너를 얼마나 떠받드는지 넌 모를 거야!\"\n\n\"정말!\"\n\n\"에스더, 나 말고 다른 사람 같으면 누구라도 질투할 만하다니까.\" 캐디가 웃으며 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다. \"그런데 난 그저 기쁘기만 해. 너는 내가 처음으로 사귄 친구이자 앞으로도 사귈 수 있는 가장 좋은 친구니까, 누가 너를 아무리 떠받들고 사랑한대도 난 좋기만 해.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 캐디.\" 내가 말했다. \"너도 나를 기분 좋게 해 주려는 그 합동 음모에 끼어 있구나. 그래서, 얘?\"\n\n\"그래서! 이제 말해 줄게.\" 캐디가 다정하게 두 손을 내 팔에 포개며 대답했다. \"그래서 우리는 그 일을 두고 한참 이야기했고, 그래서 내가 프린스한테 말했어. '프린스, 서머슨 양이—'\"\n\n\"'서머슨 양'이라고 하진 않았겠지?\"\n\n\"응. 안 그랬어!\" 캐디가 더없이 환한 얼굴로 무척 기뻐하며 외쳤다. \"'에스더'라고 했어. 프린스한테 그랬지. '에스더가 분명히 그렇게 생각하고, 그걸 나한테 말해 줬고, 당신이 그렇게 들려 달라고 좋아하는 그 다정한 편지를 쓸 때마다 늘 그 얘기를 넌지시 비추니까, 프린스, 당신이 적당하다고 여길 때면 언제든 엄마한테 사실을 털어놓을 마음의 준비가 돼 있어. 그리고 프린스,' 내가 말했어, '당신도 당신 아버님께 똑같이 한다면, 에스더 생각엔 내 처지가 모든 면에서 더 떳떳하고, 더 진실하고, 더 명예로워질 거래.'\"\n\n\"그래, 캐디.\" 내가 말했다. \"에스더는 분명히 그렇게 생각해.\"\n\n\"그러니까 내가 옳았던 거지!\" 캐디가 외쳤다. \"그런데! 이게 프린스를 꽤나 곤란하게 했어. 그가 거기에 조금이라도 의심을 품어서가 아니라, 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 심정을 워낙 헤아리는 사람이라서야. 그런 말을 꺼내면 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 상심해서 가슴이 미어지거나, 정신을 잃거나, 이런저런 가슴 아픈 모양으로 크게 무너져 내리지 않을까 염려한 거야. 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 그걸 불효라고 여기고 너무 큰 충격을 받지 않을까 걱정한 거지. 알다시피, 에스더, 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 몸가짐은 아주 우아하잖아.\" 캐디가 말했다. \"그리고 감정이 무척 여리고.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘?\"\n\n\"오, 무척 여려. 프린스가 그렇게 말해. 그래서 이게 내 소중한 아가를—너한테 그런 표현을 쓰려던 건 아니었는데, 에스더.\" 캐디가 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올라 사과했다. \"그런데 난 프린스를 으레 내 소중한 아가라고 불러.\"\n\n내가 웃자 캐디도 웃으며 얼굴을 붉혔고, 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이게 그 사람을, 에스더—\"\n\n\"누구를, 얘?\"\n\n\"오, 이 짓궂은 것아!\" 캐디가 고운 얼굴이 불타오르듯 발개져서 웃으며 말했다. \"정 그렇게 우긴다면, 내 소중한 아가를! 이게 그 사람을 몇 주째 마음 졸이게 했고, 날이면 날마다 무척 애를 태우며 미루게 했어. 결국 그가 나한테 말하더라. '캐디, 우리 아버지가 무척 아끼시는 서머슨 양이 내가 그 이야기를 꺼낼 때 함께 있어 주신다면, 내가 해낼 수 있을 것 같아.' 그래서 내가 너한테 부탁해 보겠다고 약속했어. 그리고 게다가 마음먹은 게 있는데,\" 캐디가 희망에 차 있으면서도 머뭇머뭇 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"네가 응해 주면, 그다음엔 나랑 같이 엄마한테 가 달라고 부탁하려고 했어. 편지에 너한테 큰 부탁이자 큰 도움을 청할 일이 있다고 한 게 바로 이거야. 그러니 에스더, 네가 들어줄 수 있겠다 싶으면, 우리 둘 다 정말 고마워할 거야.\"\n\n\"어디 보자, 캐디.\" 내가 곰곰 생각하는 척하며 말했다. \"정말이지, 사정이 급하다면 그보다 더한 일도 해 줄 수 있을 것 같은데. 나는 너와 그 소중한 아가를 위해 언제든 대기하고 있을게, 얘, 네가 원할 때면.\"\n\n캐디는 내 이 대답에 그만 황홀해했다. 이 세상에서 뛰어 본 그 어떤 여린 가슴에도 뒤지지 않을 만큼, 아주 작은 친절이나 격려에도 마음이 움직이는 아이였으니까. 그러고서 정원을 한두 바퀴 더 도는 동안, 그녀는 완전히 새 장갑을 끼고 한껏 눈부시게 꾸몄다. 몸가짐의 대가에게 어쩔 수 없는 망신만은 끼치지 않으려는 마음에서였다. 그런 뒤 우리는 곧장 뉴먼 거리로 갔다.\n\n프린스는 물론 가르치는 중이었다. 우리가 가 보니 그는 그리 가망 없어 보이는 제자—이마를 잔뜩 찌푸린 고집스러운 어린 여자아이로, 목소리가 굵고, 활기 없이 불만스러운 엄마를 둔 아이였다—를 데리고 씨름하고 있었는데, 우리가 끼어들어 선생을 당황하게 만든 통에 그 아이의 가망이 더 밝아졌을 리는 없었다. 수업은 더없이 삐걱대며 이어지다가 마침내 끝났다. 어린 여자아이가 신발을 갈아 신고 흰 모슬린 옷을 숄로 덮어 가린 뒤, 아이는 끌려 나갔다. 우리는 준비할 말을 몇 마디 나눈 다음, 터비드롭 씨를 찾아 나섰다. 그는 집 안에서 유일하게 안락한 방인 자기 거처의 소파에 모자와 장갑을 곁에 갖춘 채, 몸가짐의 본보기처럼 자리하고 있었다. 가벼운 식사 짬짬이 느긋하게 차림을 갖춘 듯했고, 죄다 제법 우아한 화장 도구며 솔 따위가 여기저기 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"아버지, 서머슨 양입니다. 젤리비 양이고요.\"\n\n\"황홀하옵니다! 더없이 기쁩니다!\" 터비드롭 씨가 어깨를 한껏 치켜올린 절을 하며 일어섰다. \"잠깐만요!\" 의자를 내밀며. \"앉으시지요!\" 왼손 손가락 끝에 입을 맞추며. \"더없이 기쁩니다!\" 눈을 감고 몸을 굴리듯 흔들며. \"저의 자그마한 안식처가 낙원이 되었군요.\" 유럽에서 둘째가는 신사라도 되는 양 소파에 다시 자세를 가다듬으며.\n\n\"또 이렇게 저희를 보시는군요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 말했다. \"갈고닦고 또 갈고닦는 저희의 작은 기예를 펼치는 중에 말입니다! 또 한 번 여성이 저희를 북돋우고, 그 사랑스러운 자태로 황송히 곁을 내어 주어 저희에게 보답을 주는군요. 이런 시절에(섭정 왕세자 전하—외람되오나 저의 후원자라 일컬어도 좋다면—의 시대 이래로 저희는 이 일을 참담하게 타락시키고 말았습니다만), 몸가짐이 기능공들의 발밑에 온통 짓밟히지만은 않았음을 실감하는 것은 큰 일입니다. 그것이 아직도 아름다움의 미소 속에서 볕을 쬘 수 있다는 것이, 부인.\"\n\n나는 아무 말도 하지 않았는데, 그게 알맞은 대답이라고 여겼다. 그러자 그는 코담배를 한 줌 집었다.\n\n\"내 아들아,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"너는 오늘 오후에 학교가 네 군데 있구나. 후딱 샌드위치라도 먹어 두기를 권한다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아버지.\" 프린스가 대답했다. \"꼭 시간을 어기지 않겠습니다. 아버지, 제가 드릴 말씀에 마음을 단단히 다잡아 주시기를 부탁드려도 될까요?\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 본보기는 프린스와 캐디가 손을 맞잡고 자기 앞에 몸을 굽히자 새파랗게 질려 아연실색하며 외쳤다. \"이게 무슨 일이냐? 미친 짓이냐! 아니면 이게 대체 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"아버지,\" 프린스가 더없이 공손하게 대답했다. \"저는 이 아가씨를 사랑하고, 우리는 약혼했습니다.\"\n\n\"약혼이라니!\" 터비드롭 씨가 소파에 몸을 기대며 손으로 그 광경을 가려 막고 외쳤다. \"제 자식이 내 머리에 쏘아 보낸 화살이로구나!\"\n\n\"우리는 얼마 전부터 약혼한 사이입니다, 아버지.\" 프린스가 더듬더듬 말했다. \"그리고 그 사실을 듣고 서머슨 양께서 아버지께 이를 알려 드리라고 조언해 주셨고, 더없이 친절하게도 오늘 이 자리에 함께해 주셨습니다. 젤리비 양은 아버지를 깊이 존경하는 아가씨입니다, 아버지.\"\n\n터비드롭 씨가 신음을 토했다.\n\n\"안 됩니다, 제발 그러지 마세요! 제발 그러지 마세요, 아버지.\" 아들이 간곡히 청했다. \"젤리비 양은 아버지를 깊이 존경하는 아가씨이고, 우리의 첫째가는 바람은 아버지의 안락을 헤아리는 것입니다.\"\n\n터비드롭 씨가 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"안 됩니다, 제발 그러지 마세요, 아버지!\" 아들이 외쳤다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"성스러운 네 어미가 이 아픔을 겪지 않게 된 것이 다행이로구나. 깊이 찔러라, 사정 두지 말고. 정통으로 찔러라, 얘야, 정통으로 찔러!\"\n\n\"제발 그런 말씀 마세요, 아버지.\" 프린스가 눈물을 흘리며 애원했다. \"가슴이 미어집니다. 정말이지, 아버지, 우리의 첫째가는 소망이자 작정은 아버지의 안락을 헤아리는 것입니다. 캐롤라인과 저는 우리의 도리를 잊지 않습니다—우리가 늘 함께 이야기했듯이, 제 도리가 곧 캐롤라인의 도리입니다—그러니 아버지께서 승낙하고 허락해 주신다면, 아버지, 우리는 아버지의 삶을 즐겁게 해 드리는 데 온 힘을 바치겠습니다.\"\n\n\"정통으로 찔러라.\" 터비드롭 씨가 중얼거렸다. \"정통으로 찔러!\" 그러면서도 내가 보기엔, 귀를 기울이고 있기도 했다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 프린스가 대답했다. \"우리는 아버지께서 익숙하시고 또 누리실 자격이 있는 자잘한 안락이 무엇인지 잘 알고, 무엇보다 먼저 그것을 마련해 드리는 것이 언제나 우리의 마음 씀이자 자랑이 될 것입니다. 아버지, 아버지께서 승낙과 허락으로 우리를 축복해 주신다면, 우리는 아버지께서 흡족하실 때가 되기 전에는 결혼은 생각조차 하지 않겠습니다. 그리고 결혼한 뒤에도 우리는 늘 아버지를—당연히—맨 먼저 생각하겠습니다. 아버지, 아버지께서는 언제까지나 이곳의 가장이자 주인이셔야 합니다. 만약 우리가 그것을 모르거나, 아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 갖은 애를 다 쏟지 못한다면, 그것이 얼마나 천륜에 어긋나는 일일지 우리는 절감하고 있습니다.\"\n\n터비드롭 씨는 심한 내적 갈등을 치른 끝에 다시 소파에 꼿꼿이 몸을 세웠고, 두 뺨을 빳빳한 크라바트 위로 불룩 부풀린 채, 더없이 완벽한 어버이 몸가짐의 본보기가 되었다.\n\n\"내 아들아!\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"내 아이들아! 너희의 간청을 뿌리칠 수가 없구나. 행복하거라!\"\n\n장차 며느리가 될 아이를 일으켜 세우고 아들에게 손을 내미는(아들은 그 손에 애정 어린 존경과 감사를 담아 입을 맞추었다) 그의 너그러운 모습은, 내가 본 가장 어리둥절한 광경이었다.\n\n\"내 아이들아,\" 터비드롭 씨가 자기 곁에 앉은 캐디를 왼팔로 어버이답게 감싸 안고 오른손을 우아하게 허리에 얹으며 말했다. \"내 아들과 딸아, 너희의 행복을 내가 보살피마. 내가 너희를 지켜보마. 너희는 언제까지나 나와 함께 살아라\"—물론 그 뜻은, 내가 언제까지나 너희와 함께 살겠다는 것이었다—\"이 집은 이제부터 내 것인 만큼이나 너희 것이기도 하니, 너희 집으로 여기거라. 오래도록 살아 나와 함께 이 집을 누리기를!\"\n\n그의 몸가짐의 위력이 어찌나 대단하던지, 그가 남은 평생 그들에게 얹혀살려는 것이 아니라 마치 그들을 위해 무슨 대단한 희생이라도 베푸는 양, 두 사람은 정말로 감사함에 북받쳐 있었다.\n\n\"나로 말하자면, 내 아이들아,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"나는 시들어 누렇게 마른 잎으로 접어들고 있고, 신사다운 몸가짐의 마지막 희미한 자취가 이 베 짜고 실 잣는 시대에 얼마나 더 머물러 줄지는 가늠할 길이 없구나. 하지만 그동안만은 나는 사회에 대한 내 도리를 다할 것이고, 늘 그래 왔듯 시내를 거닐며 내 모습을 보일 것이다. 내가 바라는 바는 적고 소박하다. 여기 이 자그마한 거처, 단장에 쓸 몇 가지 필수품, 검소한 아침 끼니, 그리고 자그마한 저녁 식사면 족하다. 이 자잘한 필요를 채우는 일은 너희의 효심에 맡기고, 나머지는 모두 내가 떠맡으마.\"\n\n두 사람은 그의 남다른 너그러움에 다시금 압도되었다.\n\n\"내 아들아,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"네게 모자란 그 자잘한 점들—타고나는 것이요, 갈고닦아 나아질 수는 있어도 결코 없던 데서 만들어 낼 수는 없는 몸가짐의 점들로 말하자면—그것은 여전히 나를 믿어도 된다. 나는 섭정 왕세자 전하의 시대 이래로 내 자리를 충실히 지켜 왔고, 이제 와서 그것을 저버리지 않으마. 그래, 내 아들아. 네가 일찍이 네 아비의 가난한 처지를 자랑스러운 마음으로 헤아려 본 적이 있다면, 그가 그것을 더럽힐 일은 결코 하지 않으리라 믿어도 좋다. 너로 말하자면, 프린스, 너는 성품이 다르니(우리가 다 같을 수는 없고, 그래야 마땅한 것도 아니지), 일하고, 부지런히 힘쓰고, 돈을 벌고, 인맥을 되도록 넓혀라.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇게 하겠습니다, 아버지, 온 마음을 다해서요.\" 프린스가 대답했다.\n\n\"의심치 않는다.\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"네 자질은 빛나지는 않아도, 내 사랑하는 아이야, 한결같고 쓸모가 있지. 그리고 내 아이들아, 너희 둘에게는 그저 한마디만 일러 두마. 내가 그 길에 한 줄기 빛을 비추는 행복을 누렸다고 믿는, 어느 성스러운 여인의 마음을 담아서 말이다. 이 살림을 잘 건사하고, 나의 소박한 필요를 잘 챙겨 다오. 그리고 너희 둘에게 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n이윽고 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 이 경사를 기리느라 어찌나 한껏 정중하게 굴던지, 나는 캐디에게 오늘 안에 떠브스 법학원에 갈 작정이라면 정말이지 당장 떠나야 한다고 말했다. 그래서 우리는 캐디와 그녀의 약혼자가 더없이 다정하게 작별 인사를 나눈 뒤 길을 나섰다. 걸어가는 내내 그녀는 어찌나 행복해하며 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 입이 닳도록 칭찬하던지, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 그를 깎아내리는 말은 한마디도 하지 않으리라 마음먹었다.\n\n떠브스 법학원의 그 집은 창문마다 세놓는다는 광고가 붙어 있었고, 그 어느 때보다 더 더럽고 더 음울하고 더 으스스해 보였다. 가엾은 젤리비 씨의 이름이 바로 하루 이틀 전에 파산자 명단에 올랐고, 그는 두 신사와 푸른 서류 가방 더미와 장부와 서류에 둘러싸여 식당에 틀어박혀, 자기 사정을 이해해 보려고 갖은 필사적인 애를 다 쓰고 있었다. 내가 보기에 그것은 그의 이해를 한참 벗어나 있는 듯했다. 캐디가 나를 식당으로 잘못 데리고 들어가는 바람에 우리가 안경을 쓴 젤리비 씨와 맞닥뜨렸을 때, 그는 커다란 식탁과 두 신사에게 구석으로 처량하게 둘러막힌 채, 모든 걸 단념하고 말도 못 하고 넋이 나간 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n젤리비 부인의 방으로 올라가 보니(아이들은 죄다 부엌에서 악을 쓰며 울어 댔고, 하인은 그림자도 보이지 않았다), 그 부인은 잔뜩 쌓인 편지더미 한복판에서 편지를 뜯고, 읽고, 분류하고 있었으며, 바닥에는 뜯어낸 봉투가 수북이 쌓여 있었다. 그녀는 어찌나 정신이 팔려 있던지 처음엔 나를 알아보지도 못했다. 그 특유의 묘하고, 눈이 반짝이며, 아득히 먼 곳을 보는 눈길로 나를 바라보며 앉아 있으면서도 말이다.\n\n\"아! 서머슨 양!\" 마침내 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 전혀 다른 걸 생각하고 있었네! 잘 지내지? 만나서 반가워요. 잔다이스 씨와 클레어 양은 아주 잘 지내고?\"\n\n나는 그 답례로 젤리비 씨도 아주 잘 지내시기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그리 잘 지내진 못해, 얘.\" 젤리비 부인이 더없이 태연하게 말했다. \"사업이 잘 안 풀려서 기운이 좀 빠져 있어. 나로서는 다행스럽게도 내가 워낙 바빠서 그걸 생각할 겨를이 없어. 지금 이 순간에도, 서머슨 양, 우리한테는 한 가구당 평균 다섯 명꼴인 백일흔 가구가 니제르 강 왼편으로 떠났거나 떠나려 하고 있어.\"\n\n나는 니제르 강 왼편으로 떠나지도 떠나려 하지도 않은, 우리 바로 곁에 있는 그 한 가구를 떠올리며, 그녀가 어떻게 저토록 평온할 수 있는지 의아했다.\n\n\"캐디를 데려왔구나.\" 젤리비 부인이 딸을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"이 애가 여기 보이는 게 영 새삼스럽네. 옛 일거리는 거의 내팽개치다시피 해서, 사실 나는 사내아이를 하나 써야 할 지경이야.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 엄마—\" 캐디가 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"이제 알겠지, 캐디.\" 어머니가 부드럽게 말을 가로막았다. \"내가 사내아이를 하나 쓰고 있고, 그 애는 지금 밥 먹으러 갔어. 네가 말대꾸해 봐야 무슨 소용이니?\"\n\n\"말대꾸하려던 게 아니에요, 엄마.\" 캐디가 대꾸했다. \"그저, 설마 저더러 평생 한낱 종처럼 부려 먹히라고는 안 하시겠죠 하고 말하려던 거예요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 얘,\" 여전히 편지를 뜯고, 반짝이는 눈으로 미소 지으며 훑어 내리고, 말하면서 분류하는 와중에 젤리비 부인이 말했다. \"네 눈앞에 바로 네 어미라는 사업의 본보기가 있잖니. 게다가, 한낱 종이라니? 네게 인류의 운명에 대한 공감이 조금이라도 있다면, 그런 생각 따위는 훌쩍 넘어설 텐데. 하지만 넌 그게 없어. 내가 누누이 말했잖니, 캐디, 넌 그런 공감이 없다고.\"\n\n\"그게 아프리카 일이라면, 엄마, 저는 없어요.\"\n\n\"당연히 없겠지. 자, 내가 다행히 이렇게 바쁘지 않았다면, 서머슨 양,\" 젤리비 부인이 잠깐 나에게 다정하게 눈길을 던지고는 방금 뜯은 그 편지를 어디에 둘지 가늠하며 말했다. \"이런 일에 속이 상하고 실망했을 거야. 하지만 보리오불라가 일과 관련해 생각할 게 워낙 많고, 거기에 정신을 집중해야 하니, 보다시피 그게 곧 내 처방인 셈이지.\"\n\n캐디가 나에게 애원하는 눈길을 던졌고, 젤리비 부인은 내 보닛과 머리를 그대로 뚫고 아프리카 저 멀리를 바라보고 있었으므로, 나는 지금이야말로 내가 찾아온 용건을 꺼내어 젤리비 부인의 주의를 끌기 좋은 기회라고 생각했다.\n\n\"아마,\" 내가 말을 꺼냈다. \"제가 여기 와서 일을 방해하니 무슨 일인가 궁금하시겠지요.\"\n\n\"나는 서머슨 양을 보면 언제나 반가워요.\" 젤리비 부인이 평온한 미소를 띤 채 하던 일을 이어 가며 말했다. \"다만 바라는 건,\" 그러면서 그녀가 고개를 가로저었다. \"이 애가 보리오불라 사업에 좀 더 관심을 가져 주는 거지.\"\n\n\"제가 캐디와 함께 온 건,\" 내가 말했다. \"캐디가 어머니께 비밀을 두어서는 안 된다고 옳게 생각하면서도, 그걸 털어놓는 데 제가 힘을 보태고 거들어 주리라고(어떻게 할 수 있을지는 저도 정말 모르겠지만) 여겨서예요.\"\n\n\"캐디,\" 젤리비 부인이 하던 일을 잠깐 멈췄다가 고개를 가로저은 뒤 태연히 다시 이어 가며 말했다. \"너 또 무슨 허튼소리를 하려는 게로구나.\"\n\n캐디는 보닛 끈을 풀고 보닛을 벗어, 끈을 잡은 채 바닥에 대롱대롱 늘어뜨리고는, 서럽게 울며 말했다. \"엄마, 저 약혼했어요.\"\n\n\"오, 이 우스꽝스러운 것아!\" 젤리비 부인이 가장 나중에 뜯은 편지를 훑어보며 넋이 나간 듯이 말했다. \"넌 어쩜 이리 멍청하니!\"\n\n\"저 약혼했어요, 엄마.\" 캐디가 흐느꼈다. \"학원의 젊은 터비드롭 씨와요. 그리고 늙은 터비드롭 씨가(정말이지 아주 신사다운 분이세요) 승낙해 주셨어요. 그러니 엄마, 엄마도 우리한테 승낙해 주시기를 빌고 또 빌어요. 저는 그게 없으면 도무지 행복할 수 없을 테니까요. 절대로, 절대로 못 할 거예요!\" 캐디는 평소의 온갖 불평이며 다른 모든 것을 까맣게 잊고 오로지 타고난 정에만 사로잡혀 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"또 보이지요, 서머슨 양.\" 젤리비 부인이 태연히 말했다. \"나처럼 이렇게 바쁘게 지내며, 또 이렇게 정신을 한데 모아야 할 필요를 지닌다는 게 얼마나 다행인지를요. 보세요, 캐디는 무용 선생의 아들과 약혼했어요—저 자신처럼 인류의 운명에 도통 공감이라곤 없는 사람들과 한데 얽혀서요! 게다가 이건, 우리 시대 으뜸가는 박애주의자 가운데 한 분인 퀘일 씨가 이 애한테 정말로 관심을 두고 싶다고 나한테 말씀해 주셨을 때 벌어진 일이라니까요!\"\n\n\"엄마, 저는 퀘일 씨를 늘 미워하고 진저리쳤어요!\" 캐디가 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"캐디, 캐디!\" 젤리비 부인이 더없이 흡족한 얼굴로 또 다른 편지를 뜯으며 대꾸했다. \"그랬을 테지, 의심치 않아. 그분이 넘치도록 지닌 그 공감이 네겐 도무지 없으니, 어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠니! 자, 만약 내 공적인 사명이 내게 애지중지하는 자식 같은 것이 아니었다면, 내가 어마어마한 규모의 큰 사업에 몰두하고 있지 않았다면, 이런 자잘한 일들이 나를 무척 슬프게 했을지도 몰라요, 서머슨 양. 하지만 캐디 따위가 벌인 어리석은 짓의 막 같은 것이(이 애한테는 그 이상 바랄 것도 없지만) 나와 저 위대한 아프리카 대륙 사이를 가로막게 둘 수 있을까요? 아니죠. 아니에요,\" 젤리비 부인이 더 많은 편지를 뜯어 분류하며, 차분하고 또렷한 목소리로 흐뭇한 미소를 띤 채 되풀이했다. \"정말 아니에요.\"\n\n나는 충분히 예상할 수도 있었건만, 이 더없이 냉랭한 응대에 어찌나 마음의 준비가 안 되어 있던지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 캐디도 마찬가지로 어쩔 줄 모르는 듯했다. 젤리비 부인은 계속해서 편지를 뜯고 분류하면서, 이따금 더없이 매력적인 목소리로, 더없이 평온한 미소를 띤 채 \"정말 아니에요\"를 되뇌었다.\n\n\"엄마, 바라건대,\" 가엾은 캐디가 마침내 흐느끼며 말했다. \"화나신 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"오, 캐디, 넌 정말 어처구니없는 애로구나.\" 젤리비 부인이 대꾸했다. \"내 마음이 얼마나 딴 데 가 있는지 그토록 말했는데도 그런 걸 묻다니.\"\n\n\"그리고 엄마, 바라건대, 우리한테 승낙해 주시고 잘되기를 빌어 주시는 거지요?\" 캐디가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 이런 짓을 저지르다니 분별없는 애야.\" 젤리비 부인이 말했다. \"또 위대한 공적 사업에 헌신할 수도 있었을 텐데 그런 걸 보면 못난 애지. 하지만 일은 벌어졌고, 나는 사내아이를 하나 들였으니, 더 할 말도 없어. 자, 제발, 캐디,\" 캐디가 자기에게 입을 맞추고 있었으므로 젤리비 부인이 말했다. \"오후 우편물이 오기 전에 이 묵직한 서류 뭉치를 마저 처리해야 하니, 내 일을 더 붙들고 늘어지지 마라!\"\n\n나는 이만 작별하는 것이 상책이겠다 싶었다. 캐디가 이렇게 말하는 통에 잠깐 발이 묶이긴 했지만. \"엄마, 제가 그분을 데려와서 인사드리게 해도 괜찮죠?\"\n\n\"오, 저런, 캐디.\" 어느새 다시 그 아득한 사색에 잠겨 있던 젤리비 부인이 외쳤다. \"또 시작이니? 누굴 데려온다고?\"\n\n\"그분요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"캐디, 캐디!\" 젤리비 부인이 그런 자잘한 일에 진력이 난 듯 말했다. \"그럼 본부 모임이 있는 밤도, 지부 모임이 있는 밤도, 분회 모임이 있는 밤도 아닌 어느 저녁에 데려오렴. 내 시간이 요구하는 바에 그 방문을 맞춰야 해. 서머슨 양, 이렇게 와서 이 어리석은 계집애를 도와주다니 정말 친절했어요. 잘 가요! 오늘 아침에 원주민과 커피 재배 문제의 세부 사항을 알고 싶어 하는 제조업 가문들한테서 새로 쉰여덟 통이나 편지가 왔다고 하면, 내가 여유가 거의 없는 것도 굳이 변명할 필요가 없겠지요.\"\n\n우리가 아래층으로 내려갈 때 캐디가 풀이 죽어 있어도, 내 목에 매달려 다시 흐느껴도, 차라리 야단을 맞았더라면 이런 무관심을 받느니 훨씬 나았을 거라고 말해도, 옷가지가 워낙 변변찮아서 어떻게 남부끄럽지 않게 결혼할 수 있을지 모르겠다고 나에게 털어놓아도, 나는 놀라지 않았다. 나는 그녀가 자기 집을 갖게 되면 불운한 아버지와 피피를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 숱한 일들을 들려주며 차츰 그녀의 기운을 북돋웠다. 그러고서 마침내 우리는 눅눅하고 어두컴컴한 부엌으로 내려갔는데, 거기서는 피피와 그 어린 동생들이 돌바닥에 엎드려 기어 다니고 있었고, 우리가 그 아이들과 어찌나 신나게 놀았던지, 갈가리 찢기는 꼴을 면하려고 나는 동화 이야기를 꺼내 들 수밖에 없었다. 이따금 위층 거실에서 큰 목소리가 들려왔고, 가끔은 가구가 우당탕 거칠게 나뒹구는 소리도 들렸다. 마지막 소리는, 짐작건대 가엾은 젤리비 씨가 자기 사정을 이해해 보려고 새로 한 번 시도할 때마다 식탁을 박차고 뛰쳐나와 채광창 밖 지하 통로로 몸을 던지려고 창문으로 돌진하는 통에 난 것 같아 걱정스러웠다.\n\n그날의 부산함을 뒤로하고 밤에 조용히 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오면서, 나는 캐디의 약혼을 곰곰 생각하며, (늙은 터비드롭 씨가 마음에 걸리긴 했지만) 그녀가 이 일로 더 행복해지고 더 나아지리라는 내 바람에 확신을 굳혔다. 그리고 그녀와 그녀의 남편이 그 몸가짐의 본보기가 실은 어떤 사람인지 끝내 알아챌 가망이 거의 없어 보인다 한들, 그것 또한 다 잘된 일이었으니, 누가 그들이 더 영리해지기를 바라겠는가? 나는 그들이 조금이라도 더 영리해지기를 바라지 않았고, 사실 나 자신도 그를 온전히 믿지 않는 것이 반쯤 부끄러웠다. 그리고 나는 별들을 올려다보며, 먼 나라의 여행자들과 그들이 바라보는 별들을 생각하면서, 나도 늘 내 작은 방식으로나마 누군가에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되는 복과 행복을 누릴 수 있기를 바랐다.\n\n집에 도착하니 식구들은 늘 그렇듯 나를 보고 어찌나 반가워하던지, 그게 나 자신을 밉살스럽게 만드는 짓만 아니었다면 나는 주저앉아 기쁨의 눈물을 흘릴 뻔했다. 집안의 위아래 모든 사람이 더없이 환한 얼굴로 나를 반기고, 더없이 명랑하게 말을 건네고, 나를 위해서라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 해 주려고 하니, 세상에 나처럼 복 받은 자그마한 것은 일찍이 없었으리라 싶었다.\n\n그날 밤 우리는 어찌나 수다스러운 분위기에 빠져들었던지—에이다와 후견인이 캐디 이야기를 죄다 들려 달라고 나를 꾀어낸 통에—나는 한참 동안 주절주절, 주절주절, 주절주절 늘어놓고 말았다. 마침내 내 방으로 올라가면서, 내가 그토록 떠벌렸다는 생각에 얼굴이 새빨개졌는데, 그때 문을 부드럽게 두드리는 소리가 들렸다. 그래서 내가 \"들어와요!\" 하자, 상복을 단정하게 차려입은 예쁘장한 어린 여자아이가 들어와 무릎을 굽혀 인사했다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 아가씨.\" 어린 여자아이가 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"저는 찰리예요.\"\n\n\"어머나, 정말 그렇구나.\" 나는 깜짝 놀라 몸을 숙이며 그 아이에게 입을 맞추었다. \"찰리, 만나서 어찌나 반가운지!\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 여전히 부드러운 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"저는 아가씨의 하녀예요.\"\n\n\"찰리가?\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 아가씨, 저는 잔다이스 씨가 사랑을 담아 아가씨께 드리는 선물이에요.\"\n\n나는 찰리의 목덜미에 손을 얹고 앉아 찰리를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그리고요, 아가씨,\" 찰리가 보조개 팬 뺨으로 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 두 손을 마주치며 말했다. \"톰은 학교에 다녀요, 그리고 어찌나 잘 배우는지요! 그리고 어린 에마는요, 블라인더 부인 댁에 있는데, 어찌나 살뜰히 보살핌을 받는지요! 그리고 톰은 진작 학교에 갔을 거고요—에마도 진작 블라인더 부인 댁에 맡겨졌을 거고요—저도 진작 여기 와 있었을 거예요, 아가씨. 다만 잔다이스 씨가, 톰이랑 에마랑 저랑 헤어지는 일에 먼저 좀 익숙해지는 게 낫겠다고 여기셨어요. 우리가 워낙 어렸으니까요. 울지 마세요, 아가씨, 제발요!\"\n\n\"어쩔 수가 없구나, 찰리.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 저도 어쩔 수가 없어요.\" 찰리가 말했다. \"그리고요, 아가씨, 잔다이스 씨가 사랑을 전하시며, 아가씨가 이따금 저를 가르쳐 주시면 좋아하실 것 같다고 하셨어요. 그리고요, 톰이랑 에마랑 저는 한 달에 한 번씩 서로 만나기로 했어요. 그래서 저는 너무 행복하고 너무 고마워요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 벅차오르는 가슴으로 외쳤다. \"그리고 저는 정말 좋은 하녀가 되도록 애쓸게요!\"\n\n\"오, 찰리야, 누가 이 모든 걸 해 주었는지 절대 잊지 마라!\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 절대 안 잊을 거예요. 톰도 안 잊을 거고요. 에마도요. 다 아가씨가 해 주신 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 아무것도 몰랐단다. 잔다이스 씨가 하신 거야, 찰리.\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨, 그렇지만 다 아가씨를 사랑하셔서, 또 아가씨가 제 주인님이 되시라고 하신 거예요. 실례지만, 아가씨, 저는 그분이 사랑을 담아 보내신 자그마한 선물이고, 다 아가씨를 사랑하셔서 하신 일이에요. 저랑 톰은 꼭 그걸 기억하기로 했어요.\"\n\n찰리는 눈물을 닦고 자기 소임에 나섰다. 그 의젓한 작은 몸가짐으로 방안을 이리저리 돌며 손에 닿는 것은 죄다 개켜 정리했다. 이윽고 찰리가 슬그머니 내 곁으로 다시 다가와 말했다. \"오, 울지 마세요, 아가씨, 제발요.\"\n\n그래서 나는 다시 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없구나, 찰리.\"\n\n그러자 찰리가 다시 말했다. \"네, 아가씨, 저도 어쩔 수가 없어요.\" 그리하여 결국 나는 정말로 기쁨에 겨워 울고 말았고, 찰리도 그러했다.\n\n리처드와 내가 앞서 적은 그 대화를 나눈 직후, 리처드는 자기 마음 상태를 잔다이스 씨에게 털어놓았습니다. 후견인이 그 이야기를 들었을 때 아주 크게 놀라셨는지는 잘 모르겠지만, 그것이 후견인께 적잖은 불안과 실망을 안긴 것만은 분명합니다. 후견인과 리처드는 밤늦게도, 이른 아침에도 자주 둘이서만 방에 틀어박혔고, 런던에서 온종일 시간을 보냈으며, 켄지 씨와 수없이 약속을 잡고, 골치 아픈 일을 잔뜩 헤치고 나갔습니다. 그렇게 바쁘게 지내는 동안, 후견인은 바람의 상태 때문에 적잖은 불편을 겪으면서 머리를 어찌나 쉴 새 없이 문질러대시던지 머리카락 한 올도 제자리에 가만히 있질 못했지만, 그래도 에이다와 나에게는 여느 때와 다름없이 다정하셨고, 다만 그 일들에 관해서는 한결같이 입을 다무셨습니다. 그리고 우리가 아무리 애를 써봐야 리처드 본인에게서 끌어낼 수 있는 거라곤 모든 일이 더없이 잘 풀리고 있으며 마침내 정말 다 괜찮아졌다는 막연한 장담뿐이었으니, 그를 통해서는 우리 불안이 그다지 가시지 않았습니다.\n\n그러나 시간이 흐르면서 우리도 차츰 알게 되었습니다. 리처드가 미성년자이자 피후견인이라는 둥, 뭐라는 둥의 자격으로 리처드를 위해 대법원장에게 새 신청서가 올라갔다는 것, 말이 무성하게 오갔다는 것, 대법원장이 공개 법정에서 리처드를 두고 성가시고 변덕스러운 미성년자라고 했다는 것, 그 문제가 연기되고 또 연기되고, 다른 데로 회부되고, 보고서가 올라오고, 청원이 거듭된 끝에, 리처드가 (그가 우리에게 말한 대로) 도대체 자기가 군대에 들어가긴 들어가게 된다면 그게 일흔이나 여든 먹은 노병으로서가 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다는 것까지요. 마침내 그가 대법원장을 다시 그 집무실에서 만날 약속이 잡혔고, 거기서 대법원장은 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내고 제 마음 하나 모른다며 그를 아주 엄숙하게 꾸짖었습니다. \"그쪽에서 그런 말이 나오다니, 꽤 그럴듯한 농담 아닌가요.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. 그리고 마침내 그의 신청을 받아들이는 것으로 정해졌습니다. 그의 이름이 기병사령부에 소위 임관 신청자로 등록되었고, 임관에 드는 돈은 대리인에게 예치되었으며, 리처드는 늘 그렇듯 그다운 방식으로 군사 학습이라는 격렬한 과정에 곤두박질치듯 뛰어들어, 날마다 새벽 다섯 시에 일어나 대검 훈련을 익혔습니다.\n\n이렇게 휴정기가 개정기에 이어지고, 개정기가 다시 휴정기에 이어졌습니다. 우리는 이따금 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 사건 목록에 올랐다느니 빠졌다느니, 언급될 예정이라느니, 논의될 거라느니 하는 소식을 들었습니다. 그러다 사건이 다가왔다가는 또 흐지부지 물러가곤 했습니다. 이제 런던의 어느 교관 집에 머물던 리처드는 예전보다 우리와 함께할 시간이 줄었고, 후견인은 여전히 같은 침묵을 지켰습니다. 그렇게 시간이 흘러 마침내 임관이 이루어졌고, 리처드는 그와 함께 아일랜드의 한 연대로 부임하라는 지시를 받았습니다.\n\n어느 날 저녁, 그가 그 소식을 안고 한달음에 달려와서는 후견인과 오랫동안 이야기를 나누었습니다. 한 시간 넘게 지나고서야 후견인이 에이다와 내가 앉아 있는 방으로 머리를 들이밀고 \"얘들아, 들어오너라!\" 하고 부르셨습니다. 우리가 들어가 보니, 우리가 마지막으로 봤을 때만 해도 기운이 펄펄하던 리처드가 벽난로 선반에 기대선 채 굴욕스럽고 분한 얼굴을 하고 있었습니다.\n\n\"에이다야, 릭과 나는,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"마음이 영 하나로 모이질 않는구나. 자, 자, 릭, 좀 더 밝은 얼굴을 해보게!\"\n\n\"어르신은 저한테 너무 모지십니다.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"다른 모든 면에서 저를 그토록 살뜰히 헤아려주시고, 제가 도저히 다 갚지 못할 친절을 베풀어주셨기에 더더욱 모질게 느껴집니다. 어르신이 안 계셨다면 저는 결코 바로 설 수 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"좋아, 좋아!\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"나는 자네를 더 바로 세우고 싶다네. 자네가 자네 자신과의 관계에서 더 바로 서기를 바라는 거야.\"\n\n\"외람되지만 한 말씀 드려도 괜찮을지요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 발끈하면서도 공손하게 대꾸했습니다. \"제 일은 제가 가장 잘 판단한다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"외람되지만 나도 한마디 하지, 릭.\" 잔다이스 씨가 더없이 다정한 쾌활함과 좋은 기분으로 말했습니다. \"자네가 그렇게 생각하는 건 지극히 당연한 일이지만, 나는 그렇게 보지 않는다네. 릭, 나는 내 도리를 다해야 하네. 그러지 않으면 자네가 냉정해진 뒤에 나를 아낄 수가 없을 테니까. 그리고 나는 자네가 늘 나를 아껴주기를 바라네, 냉정할 때나 뜨거울 때나 말일세.\"\n\n에이다의 얼굴이 어찌나 새파래졌던지, 후견인은 그녀를 자신의 독서용 의자에 앉히고 그 곁에 자리를 잡았습니다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니란다, 얘야.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"정말 아무것도 아니야. 릭과 나는 그저 다정한 의견 차이가 있었을 뿐인데, 너에게 말해두어야겠구나. 그 주제가 바로 너거든. 자, 이제 무슨 말이 나올지 겁이 나는 모양이구나.\"\n\n\"그런 거 아니에요, 존 오라버니.\" 에이다가 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"그게 오라버니에게서 나오는 말이라면요.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 얘야. 잠깐만 차분히 내 말에 귀를 기울여주렴, 릭은 보지 말고. 그리고 우리 꼬마 아주머니, 너도 마찬가지다. 얘야,\" 안락의자 팔걸이에 놓인 에이다의 손 위에 자신의 손을 얹으며 후견인이 말을 이었습니다. \"우리 넷이서 나눈 이야기를 기억하지? 이 꼬마 아주머니가 어떤 자그마한 사랑 이야기를 나에게 들려주었을 때 말이다.\"\n\n\"그날 오라버니가 베풀어주신 친절을 리처드도 저도 결코 잊을 리 없어요, 존 오라버니.\"\n\n\"저는 결코 잊지 못합니다.\" 리처드가 말했습니다.\n\n\"저도 결코 잊지 못해요.\" 에이다가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇다면 내가 할 말도 한결 쉬워지고, 우리가 마음을 모으기도 한결 수월해지겠구나.\" 후견인이 대답했습니다. 그 마음의 온화함과 명예로움이 얼굴에 환하게 비쳤습니다. \"에이다, 우리 새야, 너도 알아야 한다. 릭은 이제 마지막으로 제 직업을 정한 거야. 완전히 채비를 갖추고 나면, 그가 확실히 가진 것은 모조리 거기에 들어가게 된다. 그는 자기 재원을 다 써버렸고, 이제부터는 자기가 심은 그 나무에 매여 있게 되는 거야.\"\n\n\"제가 지금 가진 재원을 다 써버린 건 사실이고, 저는 그걸 아는 데 아무 불만 없습니다. 하지만 어르신, 제가 확실히 가진 것이,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"제가 가진 전부는 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"릭, 릭!\" 후견인이 갑자기 겁에 질린 듯한 몸짓으로, 달라진 목소리로, 마치 귀라도 틀어막으려는 듯 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤습니다. \"제발 부탁이니, 그 집안의 저주 위에 희망이나 기대를 세우지 말게! 무덤 이쪽 세상에서 무얼 하든, 그 끔찍한 유령 쪽으로는 한 번도 미련 어린 눈길을 던지지 말게. 그놈은 그토록 오랜 세월 우리를 따라다니며 괴롭혀온 놈이야. 차라리 꾸어 쓰는 게 낫고, 차라리 빌어먹는 게 낫고, 차라리 죽는 게 낫네!\"\n\n이 경고의 격렬함에 우리는 모두 흠칫 놀랐습니다. 리처드는 입술을 깨물고 숨을 죽인 채, 자기가 그 말을 얼마나 절실히 필요로 하는지 스스로 느끼고 있으며 나도 그걸 느끼고 있음을 안다는 듯 나를 흘끗 보았습니다.\n\n\"에이다, 얘야.\" 잔다이스 씨가 쾌활함을 되찾으며 말했습니다. \"이건 너무 센 충고의 말이긴 하다만, 나는 황폐한 집에 살면서 여기서 그런 광경을 직접 보았단다. 그 얘긴 그만하자. 릭이 인생이라는 경주에서 출발하는 데 쓸 밑천은 모조리 걸렸다. 나는 그와 너에게, 그를 위해서나 너 자신을 위해서나 이렇게 권한다. 그가 우리 곁을 떠날 때, 너희 둘 사이에는 그 어떤 약속도 없다는 양해 아래 떠나야 한다고 말이다. 한 걸음 더 나아가야겠구나. 너희 둘 모두에게 솔직히 말하마. 너희는 나를 거리낌 없이 믿어주기로 했으니, 나도 너희를 거리낌 없이 믿고 말하마. 나는 너희에게, 당분간은 혈연이라는 인연을 빼고 다른 모든 끈을 온전히 내려놓으라고 부탁한다.\"\n\n\"차라리 단번에 말씀하시지요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 대꾸했습니다. \"저에 대한 모든 믿음을 거두시고, 에이다에게도 그렇게 하라고 권하시는 거라고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그런 말은 입에도 올리지 않는 게 낫겠네, 릭. 나는 그런 뜻이 아니니까.\"\n\n\"제가 첫발을 잘못 뗐다고 생각하시는 거지요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 받아쳤습니다. \"그건 맞습니다, 저도 압니다.\"\n\n\"내가 자네에게 어떻게 시작하고 어떻게 이어가기를 바랐는지는, 지난번에 이런 일들을 이야기할 때 다 말했네.\" 잔다이스 씨가 진심 어린, 북돋우는 태도로 말했습니다. \"자네는 아직 그 시작을 떼지 못했지만, 모든 일에는 때가 있는 법이고, 자네의 때는 지나간 게 아니라네. 오히려 바로 지금 완전히 무르익은 거지. 모든 걸 깨끗하게 처음부터 시작하게. 너희 둘은 (얘들아, 아직 한참 어리지) 사촌이다. 지금으로선 그 이상은 아무것도 아니야. 그 이상의 무언가가 생긴다면, 그건 노력으로 일궈낸 결과로 와야 하는 것이지, 릭, 그보다 먼저 와서는 안 돼.\"\n\n\"어르신은 저한테 너무 모지십니다.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"제가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 모지셔요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"자네에게 고통을 주는 일을 할 때면, 나는 나 자신에게 더 모질다네. 처방은 자네 손안에 쥐여 있어. 에이다야, 릭이 자유로운 게, 그리고 너희 사이에 어린 나이의 약혼 따위가 없는 게 그에게 더 낫단다. 릭, 그게 에이다에게도 더 나아, 훨씬 더. 자네는 에이다에게 그걸 빚지고 있는 거야. 자! 너희 각자가 서로를 위해 가장 좋은 길을 택하게 될 게다, 설령 자기 자신을 위한 가장 좋은 길은 못 되더라도 말이다.\"\n\n\"어째서 그게 가장 좋다는 겁니까, 어르신?\" 리처드가 황급히 대꾸했습니다. \"저희가 어르신께 마음을 털어놓았을 때는 그게 가장 좋은 길이 아니었잖습니까. 그때는 그런 말씀 안 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"그 뒤로 내가 겪은 게 있어서다. 자네를 탓하는 게 아니야, 릭. 하지만 그 뒤로 내가 겪은 게 있어.\"\n\n\"저를 두고 겪으셨다는 말씀이군요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"음! 그래, 너희 둘 다를 두고 겪었지.\" 잔다이스 씨가 다정하게 말했습니다. \"너희가 서로에게 매여 약속을 지킬 때는 아직 오지 않았다. 그건 옳지 않고, 나는 그걸 인정해서는 안 돼. 자, 자, 우리 어린 사촌들아, 새로 시작하렴! 지나간 일은 지나간 일로 묻고, 너희가 너희 삶을 써내려갈 새 페이지를 펼치는 거야.\"\n\n리처드는 근심 어린 눈길로 에이다를 흘끗 보았지만 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n\"나는 이제껏 너희 둘에게도, 에스더에게도 한마디 입에 올리지 않으려 애써왔다.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"지금 이 순간까지 말이다. 그래야 우리가 대낮처럼 훤히 트인 채로, 모두 똑같은 처지에서 이야기할 수 있을 테니까. 이제 나는 다정한 마음으로 권하고, 더없이 간절히 청한다. 너희 둘, 여기 왔던 그 모습 그대로 헤어지렴. 나머지는 모두 시간과 진실과 한결같음에 맡기고. 만일 그러지 않는다면, 너희는 그릇된 일을 하는 것이고, 너희를 애초에 한자리에 모은 나까지 그릇된 일을 한 사람으로 만드는 것이다.\"\n\n긴 침묵이 뒤따랐습니다.\n\n\"리처드 사촌,\" 이윽고 에이다가 푸른 눈을 다정하게 들어 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했습니다. \"존 오라버니가 하신 말씀을 듣고 나니, 우리에게 다른 선택의 여지는 없는 것 같아요. 나에 대해서는 마음 푹 놓으세요. 사촌은 나를 여기 오라버니의 보살핌 아래 두고 떠나는 거고, 내가 바랄 게 아무것도 없으리라는 걸 분명히 믿어도 돼요. 오라버니의 조언대로 나를 다스린다면 정말 분명히 그럴 테니까요. 나는… 나는 의심하지 않아요, 리처드 사촌.\" 에이다가 조금 당황한 듯 말했습니다. \"사촌이 나를 무척 아낀다는 걸요. 그리고 나는… 나는 사촌이 다른 누군가와 사랑에 빠질 거라고는 생각하지 않아요. 하지만 사촌도 이 일을 잘 헤아려봤으면 좋겠어요. 나는 사촌이 모든 일에서 아주 행복하기를 바라니까요. 나를 믿어도 돼요, 리처드 사촌. 나는 조금도 변덕스럽지 않아요. 그렇다고 막무가내인 사람도 아니어서, 사촌을 결코 탓하지 않을 거예요. 사촌끼리도 헤어지는 게 슬플 수 있잖아요. 그리고 정말이지 나는 너무, 너무 슬퍼요, 리처드. 이게 사촌의 앞날을 위한 일인 줄 알면서도요. 나는 늘 사촌을 다정하게 떠올릴 거고, 에스더와 자주 사촌 이야기를 나눌 거예요. 그리고… 그리고 어쩌면 사촌도 가끔은 나를 조금쯤 떠올려줄지 모르죠, 리처드 사촌. 자, 이제는요,\" 에이다가 그에게 다가가 떨리는 손을 내밀며 말했습니다. \"우리는 다시 사촌일 뿐이에요, 리처드. 어쩌면 한동안만요. 그리고 나는 어디로 가든 내 소중한 사촌에게 축복이 내리기를 기도할게요!\"\n\n리처드가 자기 자신에 대해 나에게 훨씬 더 거센 표현으로 직접 했던 바로 그 평가를, 후견인이 똑같이 품었다는 이유로 그를 용서하지 못한다는 게 나에게는 이상하게 느껴졌습니다. 하지만 분명 그랬습니다. 나는 이 시각 이후로 리처드가 잔다이스 씨에게 예전만큼 거리낌 없이 마음을 열지 못하는 것을 무척 안타까운 마음으로 지켜보았습니다. 그렇게 해도 좋을 온갖 이유가 그에게 주어졌건만, 그는 그러지 않았습니다. 그리고 오로지 그의 쪽에서만, 두 사람 사이에 서먹함이 싹트기 시작했습니다.\n\n리처드는 곧 준비와 채비라는 분주함 속에 빠져들어, 에이다와 헤어지는 슬픔마저 잊어버렸습니다. 에이다는 허트퍼드셔에 남았고, 리처드와 잔다이스 씨와 나는 일주일간 런던으로 올라갔습니다. 그는 발작하듯 불쑥불쑥 에이다를 떠올렸는데, 때로는 울음을 터뜨리기까지 했고, 그럴 때면 더없이 무거운 자책의 말을 나에게 털어놓았습니다. 하지만 몇 분만 지나면 두 사람이 영원히 부유하고 행복하게 될 무슨 막연한 방도를 마구잡이로 꾸며내고는, 더할 나위 없이 들떠 명랑해지곤 했습니다.\n\n분주한 시기였고, 나는 그가 필요로 하는 갖가지 물건을 사들이며 온종일 그와 함께 종종걸음을 쳤습니다. 그가 제멋대로 두었더라면 샀을 법한 물건들에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 그는 나에게 모든 걸 거리낌 없이 털어놓았고, 자기 결점과 굳센 다짐에 관해 자주 그토록 사리 분별 있게, 그토록 절절하게 이야기했으며, 이런 대화에서 자기가 얻는 격려를 어찌나 곱씹던지, 나는 아무리 애를 써도 그와 함께 있는 게 지겨워질 수가 없었습니다.\n\n그 일주일 동안, 리처드와 검술을 겨루러 우리 숙소를 오가던 사람이 하나 있었습니다. 예전에 기병이었던 사람으로, 풍채가 훤하고 우락부락한 외모에, 솔직하고 거침없는 태도를 지닌 남자였습니다. 리처드는 그와 몇 달째 함께 연습해온 터였습니다. 나는 리처드뿐 아니라 후견인에게서도 그에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 어느 날 아침 식사를 마친 뒤 그가 왔을 때, 나는 일부러 일감을 들고 방에 자리를 잡고 있었습니다.\n\n\"좋은 아침이오, 조지 씨.\" 마침 나와 단둘이 있던 후견인이 말했습니다. \"카스턴 군이 곧 올 거요. 그동안, 서머슨 양이 당신을 만나서 무척 반가워한다는 걸 내가 잘 알지요. 앉으시오.\"\n\n그는 자리에 앉았는데, 내가 보기에 내가 있는 게 좀 거북한 듯했고, 나는 쳐다보지도 않은 채 햇볕에 그을린 투박한 손으로 윗입술을 자꾸자꾸 쓸어내렸습니다.\n\n\"당신은 해처럼 시간을 어김없이 지키시는군.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"군대식 시간입니다, 어르신.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"몸에 밴 버릇이지요. 그저 버릇일 뿐입니다, 어르신. 저는 사업가 기질이라곤 도무지 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 제법 큰 사업체를 가지고 있다고 들었는데?\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"대단할 것도 없습니다, 어르신. 사격장을 하나 하긴 합니다만, 변변치 못합니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 카스턴 군은 당신 밑에서 어느 정도 사수가 되고, 어느 정도 검객이 되었소?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"제법 괜찮습니다, 어르신.\" 그가 넓은 가슴 위로 팔짱을 끼고 아주 듬직하게 보이는 자세로 대답했습니다. \"카스턴 군이 온 마음을 쏟기만 한다면, 아주 훌륭해질 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러질 않는단 말이지요?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"처음엔 그랬습니다, 어르신. 그런데 나중엔 안 그러더군요. 온 마음을 쏟지 않아요. 어쩌면 마음에 다른 게 얹혀 있는지도 모르죠. 어떤 아가씨 같은 거요.\" 그의 빛나는 검은 눈이 처음으로 나를 흘끗 보았습니다.\n\n\"단언컨대 그 마음에 얹혀 있는 게 저는 아닙니다, 조지 씨.\" 내가 웃으며 말했습니다. \"저를 의심하시는 것 같지만요.\"\n\n그는 그을린 얼굴이 살짝 붉어지며 나에게 기병식 인사를 꾸벅했습니다. \"기분 상하시지 않았으면 좋겠습니다, 아가씨. 저는 거칠게 자란 사람이라서요.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"칭찬으로 받아들이는걸요.\"\n\n전에는 나를 쳐다보지 않았더라도, 이제 그는 빠르게 서너 차례 연거푸 나를 쳐다보았습니다. \"죄송합니다, 어르신.\" 그가 후견인에게 사내다운 머뭇거림을 담아 말했습니다. \"방금 황송하게도 아가씨의 성함을 말씀해주셨는데요—\"\n\n\"서머슨 양이오.\"\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 그가 되뇌고는 다시 나를 보았습니다.\n\n\"그 이름을 아시나요?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 아가씨. 제가 알기로는 한 번도 들어본 적이 없습니다. 어디선가 뵌 적이 있는 것 같아서요.\"\n\n\"그건 아닐 거예요.\" 나는 그를 보려고 일감에서 고개를 들며 대답했습니다. 그의 말투와 태도에 어딘가 진솔한 데가 있어서 나는 이 기회가 반가웠습니다. \"저는 얼굴을 아주 잘 기억하거든요.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다, 아가씨!\" 그가 검은 눈과 넓은 이마를 가득 들어 내 눈길을 맞받으며 대답했습니다. \"흠! 대체 뭐가 자꾸 나를 이러게 만드는 거지!\"\n\n그가 다시 한번 그을린 얼굴이 붉어지면서, 그 연상을 떠올리려 애쓰다가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하자, 후견인이 그를 구해주려고 나섰습니다.\n\n\"제자가 많소, 조지 씨?\"\n\n\"그날그날 수가 다릅니다, 어르신. 대개는 그걸로 먹고살기엔 빠듯한 수입니다.\"\n\n\"당신 사격장에는 어떤 부류의 손님들이 연습하러 오나요?\"\n\n\"별별 사람이 다 옵니다, 어르신. 내국인도 외국인도요. 신사부터 도제까지요. 전에는 프랑스 여자들이 와서 권총 솜씨가 보통이 아닌 걸 뽐낸 적도 있습니다. 미친 사람들이야 셀 수도 없죠. 하긴 그런 사람들은 문이 열려 있는 데라면 어디든 가니까요.\"\n\n\"앙심을 품고 와서, 산 표적으로 연습을 마무리하겠다는 속셈을 가진 사람은 없겠지요?\" 후견인이 미소 지으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그런 일은 흔치 않습니다, 어르신. 그런 일이 있긴 했지만요. 대개는 솜씨를 키우러 오거나, 빈둥거리러 옵니다. 이거나 저거나 거기서 거기죠. 외람되지만,\" 조지 씨가 뻣뻣하게 허리를 곧추세우고 무릎마다 팔꿈치를 짚어 모으며 말했습니다. \"제가 잘못 들은 게 아니라면, 어르신께서 대법원 소송 당사자라고 하시던데요?\"\n\n\"유감스럽게도 그렇소.\"\n\n\"제가 한창때 어르신과 같은 처지의 사람을 하나 알았습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"대법원 소송 당사자를?\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다. \"어떻게 된 일이오?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 그 사람이 이리저리 떠밀리고 또 떠밀려서, 이 기둥에서 저 기둥으로 두들겨 맞듯 시달리고 들볶이고 고문당하다시피 하다 보니,\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"성미가 비뚤어졌습니다. 저는 그가 누구를 겨눌 생각이 있었다고는 보지 않습니다만, 어찌나 분하고 사나운 상태였던지, 와서 오십 발 쏠 돈을 치르고는 시뻘게질 때까지 쏘아대곤 했죠. 하루는 곁에 아무도 없고 그가 저한테 제 억울함을 화내며 늘어놓길래 제가 그랬습니다. '이 연습이 자네한테 안전판이 되어준다면야, 그거 좋네. 하지만 지금 그런 마음 상태로 여기에 이렇게 매달리는 건 나도 영 마뜩잖아. 자네가 다른 데 마음을 붙이는 게 좋겠어.' 어찌나 격해 있던지 저는 한 대 맞을 각오를 했는데, 그가 아주 순순히 받아들이고 곧장 그만두더군요. 우리는 악수를 나누고 일종의 우정을 맺었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 누구였소?\" 후견인이 새삼 흥미가 묻어나는 어조로 물었습니다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 그가 미끼로 던져진 황소 꼴이 되기 전엔, 슈롭셔의 자그마한 농부로 시작한 사람이었습니다.\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"이름이 그리들리였소?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n후견인과 내가 그 우연에 놀라 몇 마디 주고받는 동안 조지 씨는 또 한 차례 나에게 빠르고 빛나는 눈길을 연거푸 던졌고, 그래서 나는 우리가 어떻게 그 이름을 알게 되었는지 그에게 설명해주었습니다. 그는 자기가 황송하다고 부른 나의 친절에 답례하듯 또 한 번 군인다운 인사를 꾸벅했습니다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다,\" 그가 나를 보며 말했습니다. \"대체 뭐가 또 나를 이러게 만드는 건지—그런데—제기랄! 내 머릿속이 도대체 뭐랑 부딪히고 있는 거람!\" 그는 투박한 손 하나로 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 한 번 쓸어 마치 깨진 생각들을 머릿속에서 쓸어내려는 듯하더니, 한쪽 팔은 허리에 짚고 다른 팔은 다리에 얹은 채 몸을 살짝 앞으로 숙이고 앉아, 깊은 생각에 잠겨 바닥을 들여다보았습니다.\n\n\"그 똑같은 마음 상태가 이 그리들리를 새로운 곤경으로 몰아넣어 지금 몸을 숨기고 있다니, 듣기에 안타깝구려.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇게 들었습니다, 어르신.\" 조지 씨가 여전히 생각에 잠겨 바닥을 내려다보며 대답했습니다. \"그렇게 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"어디 있는지는 모르시오?\"\n\n\"모릅니다, 어르신.\" 기병이 눈을 들어 몽상에서 깨어나며 대답했습니다. \"그에 대해서는 아무 말씀도 드릴 수가 없습니다. 곧 다 닳아 없어질 겁니다, 제 짐작에는요. 굳센 사람의 심장도 여러 해 동안 갈아내다 보면, 결국 어느 순간 단번에 무너지는 법이니까요.\"\n\n리처드가 들어오는 바람에 대화가 끊겼습니다. 조지 씨는 자리에서 일어나 나에게 또 한 번 군인다운 인사를 꾸벅하고, 후견인께 좋은 하루 보내시라 인사한 뒤, 묵직한 걸음으로 성큼성큼 방을 나갔습니다.\n\n이날 아침이 바로 리처드의 출발이 예정된 날이었습니다. 이제 더 살 물건도 없었고, 나는 그날 오후 일찍 그의 짐을 모두 꾸려놓았으며, 밤이 될 때까지 우리 시간은 비어 있었습니다. 그는 밤에 홀리헤드로 가려고 리버풀로 떠날 예정이었습니다. 그날 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 또 거론될 것으로 예상되었기에, 리처드는 우리가 법정에 내려가 무슨 일이 오가는지 들어보자고 제안했습니다. 마지막 날인 데다 그가 가고 싶어 안달이었고, 나는 거기 한 번도 가본 적이 없었기에, 나는 그러자고 했고, 우리는 그때 개정 중이던 웨스트민스터로 걸어 내려갔습니다. 우리는 리처드가 나에게 쓸 편지와 내가 그에게 쓸 편지에 관한 약속을 하고, 또 희망에 찬 온갖 계획을 세우며 길을 즐겁게 보냈습니다. 후견인은 우리가 어디로 가는지 알고 있었기에 함께하지 않았습니다.\n\n우리가 법정에 이르렀을 때, 대법원장이 거기 있었습니다. 내가 링컨스인의 집무실에서 본 바로 그 사람이었는데, 자못 위엄 있고 근엄하게 법대에 앉아 있었고, 그 아래 붉은 탁자에는 권표와 옥새가 놓여 있었으며, 작은 정원 같은 거대하고 납작한 꽃다발이 법정 전체에 향기를 풍기고 있었습니다. 그 탁자 아래로는 또 사무변호사들이 길게 한 줄로 늘어앉아, 발치 깔개 위에 서류 뭉치를 쌓아두고 있었습니다. 그리고 가발과 법복 차림의 법정변호사들이 있었는데, 깨어 있는 이도 있고 잠든 이도 있었으며, 한 사람은 말을 하고 있었지만 그가 하는 말에 별로 귀를 기울이는 사람이 없었습니다. 대법원장은 더없이 편안한 의자에 등을 기대고, 쿠션 댄 팔걸이에 팔꿈치를 올린 채 이마를 손에 받치고 있었습니다. 그 자리에 있는 사람들 가운데 더러는 졸고, 더러는 신문을 읽고, 더러는 이리저리 거닐거나 무리 지어 소곤거렸습니다. 모두가 더없이 느긋해 보였고, 조금도 서두르는 기색 없이, 너무도 태평하고, 지극히 안락했습니다.\n\n모든 일이 그토록 매끄럽게 굴러가는 것을 보면서 소송 당사자들의 거친 삶과 죽음을 떠올리는 일, 그 화려한 정장과 격식을 다 보면서 그것이 표상하는 낭비와 궁핍과 거지꼴이 된 비참을 떠올리는 일, 그토록 많은 가슴속에서 미뤄진 희망의 병이 사납게 들끓는 동안에도 이 점잖은 구경거리가 날마다, 해마다 그토록 질서정연하고 침착하게 태연히 굴러간다는 것을 헤아리는 일, 대법원장과 그 아래 늘어선 모든 법조인이 서로를, 또 방청객들을 바라보면서, 마치 그들이 모여든 그 이름이 잉글랜드 방방곡곡에서 쓰디쓴 농담거리가 되었고, 누구나 진저리치며 경멸하고 분개하는 대상이 되었으며, 너무도 노골적이고 악독해서 거기서 누군가에게 좋은 것이 나오려면 기적이라도 일어나야 할 무언가로 알려져 있다는 것을 일찍이 들어본 적도 없다는 듯 굴고 있는 것을 바라보는 일—이 모든 것이, 그런 경험이라곤 없던 나에게는 너무도 기이하고 자기모순적이어서, 처음에는 도무지 믿기지 않았고, 나는 그것을 이해할 수가 없었습니다. 나는 리처드가 앉혀준 자리에 앉아 들으려고 애쓰며 주위를 둘러보았습니다. 하지만 그 광경 전체에서 현실감이라곤 어디에도 없는 듯했고, 다만 가엾고 자그마한 플라이트 양만은 예외였습니다. 그 미친 노부인은 긴 의자 위에 올라서서 법정을 향해 고개를 끄덕이고 있었습니다.\n\n플라이트 양은 곧 우리를 알아보고 우리가 앉은 곳으로 다가왔습니다. 그녀는 자기 영토에 온 나를 정중히 환영하고는, 한껏 흐뭇해하며 자랑스럽게 그곳의 주요 볼거리를 가리켜 보였습니다. 켄지 씨도 우리에게 말을 건네러 와서, 거의 비슷한 방식으로, 주인다운 부드러운 겸손함을 풍기며 이곳을 안내해주었습니다. 방문하기에 그리 좋은 날은 아니라고 그가 말했습니다. 개정 첫날이었으면 더 좋았을 거라고요. 하지만 그래도 위풍당당하다, 위풍당당하다고 했습니다.\n\n우리가 거기 머문 지 반 시간쯤 지나자, 진행 중이던 사건은—이런 자리에 어울리지도 않는 이 우스꽝스러운 표현을 써도 된다면—아무 결론에도 이르지 않은 채, 누구도 결론에 이르리라 기대하지도 않은 채, 그저 제풀에 김이 빠져 시들어 죽어버리는 듯했습니다. 그러자 대법원장이 자기 책상에서 서류 뭉치 하나를 아래쪽 신사들에게 던졌고, 누군가가 \"잔다이스 대 잔다이스\" 하고 말했습니다. 그러자 웅성거림과 웃음소리가 일고, 구경꾼들이 우르르 빠져나갔으며, 산더미 같은, 첩첩이 쌓인, 자루마다 그득한 서류들이 들어왔습니다.\n\n내가 이해한 바로는—이것 또한 어지간히 뒤죽박죽이었지만—어떤 소송 비용 명세서에 관해 \"추가 지시를 받기 위해\" 사건이 거론된 듯했습니다. 그런데 자기가 \"이 사건에 관여하고 있다\"고 말하는 가발 쓴 신사를 세어보니 스물세 명이었고, 그중 누구도 나보다 이 일을 더 잘 이해하는 것 같지 않았습니다. 그들은 대법원장과 그 일을 두고 잡담을 나누고, 자기들끼리 서로 반박하고 설명하며, 더러는 이쪽이라 하고, 더러는 저쪽이라 했으며, 더러는 진술서를 산더미처럼 읽어보자고 익살스레 제안했고, 또 웅성거림과 웃음소리가 일었으며, 관련된 모든 사람이 한가한 여흥을 즐기는 상태였고, 누구도 그 일로 아무것도 매듭짓지 못했습니다. 한 시간쯤 이러고, 여러 발언이 시작되었다가 도중에 끊긴 끝에, 켄지 씨의 말마따나 그것은 \"당분간 다시 회부\"되었고, 서기들이 미처 서류를 다 들여오기도 전에 서류는 다시 묶여버렸습니다.\n\n이 가망 없는 절차가 끝났을 때 나는 리처드를 흘끗 보았다가, 그 잘생기고 젊은 얼굴에 떠오른 지친 기색을 보고 충격을 받았습니다. \"이게 영원히 갈 리야 없겠지, 더든 아주머니. 다음번엔 운이 더 좋을 거야!\" 그가 한 말은 그게 다였습니다.\n\n나는 거피 씨가 켄지 씨를 위해 서류를 들여와 정리하는 것을 보았습니다. 그도 나를 보고는 처량한 인사를 꾸벅했는데, 그 바람에 나는 어서 법정 밖으로 나가고 싶어졌습니다. 리처드가 나에게 팔을 내주고 나를 데리고 나가려는데 거피 씨가 다가왔습니다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 카스턴 씨.\" 그가 속삭이듯 말했습니다. \"그리고 서머슨 양께도요. 여기 제 친구인 어떤 부인이 계신데, 서머슨 양을 아는 사이라서 악수의 영광을 누리고 싶어 하십니다.\" 그가 그렇게 말하는 사이, 내 앞에 마치 내 기억 속에서 불쑥 몸을 갖추고 튀어나온 듯한 사람이 보였습니다. 대모님 댁에 있던 레이철 부인이었습니다.\n\n\"잘 지냈니, 에스더?\" 그녀가 말했습니다. \"나를 알아보겠니?\"\n\n나는 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 알아본다고, 그녀가 거의 변하지 않았다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"네가 그 시절을 기억하다니 신기하구나, 에스더.\" 그녀가 예전 그대로의 모진 말투로 대꾸했습니다. \"이젠 다 달라졌지. 그래! 너를 보니 반갑고, 네가 너무 거만해져서 나를 모른 체하지 않으니 그것도 반갑구나.\" 하지만 사실 그녀는 내가 거만하지 않다는 걸 오히려 실망스러워하는 눈치였습니다.\n\n\"거만하다니요, 레이철 부인!\" 내가 항변했습니다.\n\n\"나는 결혼했단다, 에스더.\" 그녀가 쌀쌀맞게 나를 바로잡으며 대꾸했습니다. \"이제 채드밴드 부인이야. 그래! 그럼 잘 있거라, 부디 잘 지내기를 바란다.\"\n\n이 짧은 대화에 귀를 기울이고 있던 거피 씨가 내 귓가에 한숨을 한 번 내쉬더니, 우리가 한복판에 끼어 있던, 들고 나는 사람들의 어수선한 작은 무리—사건이 바뀌면서 한데 모인 무리—를 팔꿈치로 헤치며 자기와 레이철 부인의 길을 텄습니다. 리처드와 나는 그 사이를 비집고 나아가는 중이었고, 나는 방금 뜻밖에 누군가를 알아본 첫 한기에서 채 벗어나지도 못한 참이었는데, 우리 쪽으로 다가오는—다만 우리를 보지는 못한 채로—사람을 보았으니, 다름 아닌 조지 씨였습니다. 그는 사람들 머리 너머로 법정 안쪽을 노려보며 성큼성큼 걸어오느라 주위 사람들에는 아랑곳도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n\"조지!\" 내가 그를 가리키자 리처드가 불렀습니다.\n\n\"잘 만났습니다, 카스턴 씨.\" 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"아가씨도요. 제가 찾는 사람을 좀 짚어주실 수 있겠습니까? 저는 이런 데가 도무지 익숙지가 않아서요.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 몸을 돌려 우리에게 길을 터주던 그는, 우리가 인파에서 빠져나와 커다란 붉은 휘장 뒤 한구석에 이르자 걸음을 멈췄습니다.\n\n\"머리가 좀 돈 자그마한 노부인이 하나 있는데요,\" 그가 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"그분이—\"\n\n나는 손가락을 세워 보였습니다. 플라이트 양이 줄곧 내 곁을 떠나지 않고 내내 붙어 있다가, (내가 듣고 당황했듯이) 자기와 안면 있는 법조인 몇몇의 귀에 대고 \"쉿! 내 왼쪽에 잔다이스 후예예요!\" 하고 속삭이며 그들에게 나를 가리켜 보이고 있었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"흠!\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"아가씨, 오늘 아침에 우리가 어떤 사람에 관해 얘기를 좀 나눈 거 기억하시죠? 그리들리요.\" 손으로 입을 가리고 낮게 속삭였습니다.\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그가 제 집에 숨어 있습니다. 그땐 말씀드릴 수가 없었어요. 그의 허락을 못 받았거든요. 그가 마지막 행군 길에 들어섰는데, 아가씨, 저 부인을 보고 싶다고 합니다. 둘이 서로 마음을 헤아려줄 수 있다더군요. 저 부인이 여기서 그에게 거의 친구나 다름없이 잘해주었답니다. 제가 저 부인을 찾으러 내려온 겁니다. 오늘 오후에 그리들리 곁에 앉아 있자니, 천에 싸인 북소리가 울리는 것만 같았거든요.\"\n\n\"제가 부인께 말씀드릴까요?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래 주시겠습니까?\" 그가 플라이트 양 쪽을 보며 무언가 겁먹은 듯한 눈길로 대답했습니다. \"아가씨를 만난 게 천운입니다. 저 부인을 어떻게 상대해야 할지 도무지 몰랐을 텐데요.\" 그러고는 한 손을 가슴에 얹고 군인다운 자세로 꼿꼿이 섰습니다. 나는 자그마한 플라이트 양의 귀에 대고 그의 친절한 심부름의 취지를 일러주었습니다.\n\n\"슈롭셔에서 온 내 성난 친구! 나만큼이나 유명한 사람이지!\" 그녀가 외쳤습니다. \"어머, 정말이지! 얘야, 더없이 기쁜 마음으로 그분을 찾아뵙겠어.\"\n\n\"그분은 조지 씨 댁에 몸을 숨기고 사세요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"쉿! 이분이 조지 씨예요.\"\n\n\"어머나! 정말이지!\" 플라이트 양이 대꾸했습니다. \"이런 영광을 누리다니 무척 자랑스럽군요! 군인이란다, 얘야. 그러니까, 완벽한 장군이지!\" 그녀가 나에게 속삭였습니다.\n\n가엾은 플라이트 양은 군대에 대한 존경의 표시로 이토록 깍듯하고 정중해야 한다고 여겼고, 또 얼마나 자주 무릎 굽혀 인사를 하던지 그녀를 법정 밖으로 데리고 나오는 게 여간 어려운 일이 아니었습니다. 가까스로 그 일이 끝나고, 조지 씨를 \"장군님\"이라 부르며 그에게 팔을 맡기자, 구경하던 몇몇 한량이 무척 재미있어했습니다. 그는 어찌나 어쩔 줄 몰라 하던지, 나에게 \"제발 저를 버리지 말아 달라\"고 어찌나 공손히 청하던지, 나는 차마 그러지 못하겠다는 마음이 들었습니다. 더구나 플라이트 양은 나에게는 늘 고분고분했고, 그녀 또한 \"잔다이스 후예야, 얘야, 너도 당연히 우리와 함께 가야지\" 하고 말했으니까요. 리처드도 우리가 그들을 목적지까지 안전하게 데려다주는 일을 기꺼이, 아니 오히려 바라기까지 하는 눈치여서, 우리는 그러기로 했습니다. 그리고 조지 씨가 알려준 바로는, 그리들리가 오늘 아침 두 사람의 면담 이야기를 들은 뒤 오후 내내 잔다이스 씨를 마음에 두고 있었다고 하기에, 나는 우리가 어디로 가는지, 왜 가는지를 연필로 후견인께 급히 적어 보냈습니다. 조지 씨는 그게 무슨 단서가 되지 않도록 어느 커피점에서 봉인했고, 우리는 그것을 일당 짐꾼 편에 부쳤습니다.\n\n그런 다음 우리는 삯마차를 잡아타고 레스터 광장 근처로 달려갔습니다. 우리는 비좁은 골목 몇 군데를 지나갔는데, 조지 씨가 그 골목에 대해 미안해했고, 이윽고 사격장에 이르렀습니다. 문은 닫혀 있었습니다. 그가 문기둥에 사슬로 매달린 종 손잡이를 잡아당기자, 백발에 안경을 쓰고, 검은 모직 짧은 외투에 각반을 차고, 챙 넓은 모자를 쓴, 큼직한 황금 머리 지팡이를 든 매우 점잖은 노신사가 그에게 말을 걸었습니다.\n\n\"실례하오, 친구.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"여기가 조지 사격장이오?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\" 조지 씨가 회칠한 벽에 그 글귀가 적힌 큼직한 글자들을 올려다보며 대답했습니다.\n\n\"오! 그렇군요!\" 노신사가 그의 눈길을 따라가며 말했습니다. \"고맙소. 종을 울리셨소?\"\n\n\"제 이름이 조지입니다, 어르신. 그리고 종은 제가 울렸습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요?\" 노신사가 말했습니다. \"당신 이름이 조지라고? 그렇다면 보다시피 내가 당신만큼 빨리 온 셈이군. 분명 당신이 나를 부르러 왔겠지요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 어르신. 무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요?\" 노신사가 말했습니다. \"그렇다면 당신의 젊은이가 나를 부르러 온 게로군. 나는 의사인데, 오 분 전에 조지 사격장으로 와서 아픈 사람을 봐달라는 청을 받았소.\"\n\n\"천에 싸인 북소리로군요.\" 조지 씨가 리처드와 나를 돌아보며 무겁게 고개를 가로젓고 말했습니다. \"맞습니다, 어르신. 안으로 드시지요.\"\n\n그 순간 초록색 거친 모직 모자에 앞치마를 두른, 얼굴과 손과 옷이 온통 새카맣게 그을린, 아주 별스러운 외모의 자그마한 사내가 문을 열어주었습니다. 우리는 음산한 복도를 지나, 벽돌이 그대로 드러난 커다란 건물 안으로 들어갔는데, 거기에는 표적과 총과 칼과 그런 종류의 물건들이 있었습니다. 우리가 모두 여기 다다르자 의사가 걸음을 멈추고 모자를 벗었는데, 그러자 그는 마치 마법처럼 사라지고 그 자리에 전혀 다른 사람이 들어선 듯했습니다.\n\n\"자, 여기 좀 보게, 조지.\" 그 사내가 그를 향해 휙 돌아서며 굵은 집게손가락으로 그의 가슴을 톡톡 두드리고 말했습니다. \"자네는 나를 알고, 나도 자네를 알지. 자네는 세상 물정 아는 사람이고, 나도 세상 물정 아는 사람이야. 자네도 알다시피 내 이름은 버킷이고, 나는 그리들리에 대한 평화유지 영장을 가지고 있네. 자네는 그를 오랫동안 빼돌려놓았고, 그 솜씨가 제법 능청스러웠어. 칭찬받을 만하지.\"\n\n조지 씨는 그를 매섭게 노려보며 입술을 깨물고 고개를 가로저었습니다.\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 상대가 그에게 바싹 붙어서 말했습니다. \"자네는 분별 있는 사람이고, 처신이 반듯한 사람이지. 의심할 여지 없이 자네가 바로 그런 사람이야. 그리고 이건 알아두게. 내가 자네를 흔해빠진 작자 대하듯 말하는 게 아니라네. 자네는 나라에 봉사한 사람이고, 의무가 부르면 우리가 따라야 한다는 걸 아는 사람이니까. 그러니 자네가 일을 어렵게 만들 생각이 없다는 건 명백하지. 내가 도움이 필요하면 자네가 나를 도울 거야. 그게 바로 자네라는 사람이지. 필 스쿼드, 그런 식으로 사격장을 슬금슬금 돌아다니지 마라\"—그 꾀죄죄한 자그마한 사내가 어깨를 벽에 붙인 채, 눈은 침입자에게 둔 채로 위협적으로 보이는 태도로 발을 끌며 돌아다니고 있었습니다—\"내가 너를 잘 아니까, 그런 짓은 못 봐주겠다.\"\n\n\"필!\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"네, 주인님.\"\n\n\"가만있어.\"\n\n그 자그마한 사내는 나직이 으르렁대며 그 자리에 멈춰 섰습니다.\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분,\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 일에서 거슬리게 보일 만한 게 있더라도 양해해주시오. 내 이름은 형사부의 버킷 경위이고, 나는 수행할 임무가 있소이다. 조지, 나는 내 사람이 어디 있는지 안다네. 어젯밤 지붕 위에 올라가 채광창으로 그를 봤거든. 자네가 함께 있는 것도 봤고. 그는 저 안에 있어, 알겠나,\" 손으로 가리키며 말했습니다. \"바로 저기 있지. 긴 의자 위에 말이야. 자, 나는 내 사람을 봐야 하고, 내 사람한테 스스로 구금된 몸이라 여기라고 일러줘야 하네. 하지만 자네는 나를 알지. 내가 거북한 수를 쓰고 싶어 하지 않는다는 걸 알 거야. 자네가 나한테 사내 대 사내로서(게다가 명심하게, 자네는 노병이기도 하지) 우리 둘 사이가 떳떳하다고 약속해준다면, 나도 내 힘닿는 데까지 자네 편의를 봐주겠네.\"\n\n\"약속하겠소.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 당신은 점잖지 못했소, 버킷 씨.\"\n\n\"허튼소리, 조지! 점잖지 못했다고?\" 버킷 씨가 다시 그의 넓은 가슴을 톡톡 두드리고 악수를 나누며 말했습니다. \"내가 자네더러, 내 사람을 그렇게 꽁꽁 숨겨둔 게 점잖지 못했다고 했나? 나한테도 똑같이 너그럽게 굴게, 친구! 우리 윌리엄 텔 영감, 쇼 영감, 근위 기병 같으니라고! 아 글쎄, 이 사람 하나가 영국 군대 전체의 본보기라니까요, 신사 숙녀 여러분. 이런 사내대장부 풍채라면 나는 오십 파운드 지폐라도 내놓겠소!\"\n\n일이 이렇게 마무리되자, 조지 씨는 잠시 생각하더니, 자기가 먼저 들어가서 자기 전우(그는 그렇게 불렀습니다)를 만나되 플라이트 양을 데리고 가겠다고 제안했습니다. 버킷 씨가 동의하자, 그들은 사격장 저 안쪽 끝으로 가버렸고, 우리는 총이 잔뜩 놓인 탁자 옆에 앉거나 선 채로 남았습니다. 버킷 씨는 이 틈을 타 가벼운 잡담을 나누었습니다. 나에게는 대개의 젊은 아가씨들처럼 총기가 무섭지 않으냐 물었고, 리처드에게는 사격 솜씨가 좋으냐 물었으며, 필 스쿼드에게는 저 소총들 가운데 어느 게 제일 좋은 것 같으냐, 새것으로 사면 얼마쯤 나가겠느냐 물었고, 그 답례로, 너는 천성이 어찌나 상냥한지 여자로 태어났어도 좋았을 텐데 성질을 못 이겨 그 모양이 됐으니 안타깝다고 말하며, 두루 좋은 분위기를 만들었습니다.\n\n얼마 뒤 그가 우리를 따라 사격장 저 안쪽 끝으로 왔고, 리처드와 내가 조용히 나가려는데 조지 씨가 우리를 뒤따라왔습니다. 그는 우리가 자기 전우를 만나는 데 거리낌이 없다면, 우리가 와주는 걸 무척 고맙게 여기겠다고 말했습니다. 그 말이 그의 입에서 채 떨어지기도 전에 종이 울렸고 후견인이 나타났습니다. \"혹시나,\" 그가 무심한 듯 한마디 했습니다. \"자기와 똑같은 불운에 휘말린 가엾은 사람을 위해 사소한 거라도 도울 수 있을까 해서\" 왔다는 거였습니다. 우리 넷은 함께 되돌아가 그리들리가 있는 곳으로 들어갔습니다.\n\n그곳은 칠하지 않은 나무로 사격장에서 칸막이를 친 텅 빈 방이었습니다. 칸막이가 높이 여덟이나 열 자쯤밖에 안 되고 옆면만 두르고 위는 막지 않았기에, 높다란 사격장 지붕의 서까래가 머리 위로 드러나 있었고, 버킷 씨가 내려다본 그 채광창도 있었습니다. 해가 낮게 걸려 곧 질 무렵이라, 햇빛이 위쪽으로 붉게 들어왔지만 바닥까지는 내려오지 않았습니다. 밋밋한 천을 씌운 긴 의자 위에 슈롭셔에서 온 그 사내가 누워 있었는데, 우리가 지난번 봤을 때와 거의 같은 옷차림이었으나 어찌나 변했던지, 처음에는 그 핏기 없는 얼굴에서 내가 기억하던 모습과 닮은 구석을 알아볼 수가 없었습니다.\n\n그는 그 은신처에서도 여전히 글을 쓰고 있었고, 시간이 흐르고 또 흘러도 여전히 자기 억울함을 곱씹고 있었습니다. 탁자 하나와 선반 몇 개가 원고와 닳은 펜, 그리고 그런 잡다한 흔적들로 뒤덮여 있었습니다. 가슴 아프게도, 또 두렵도록, 한데 모여, 그와 그 자그마한 미친 부인은 나란히, 말하자면 단둘이 있었습니다. 그녀는 의자에 앉아 그의 손을 잡고 있었고, 우리 중 누구도 그들 가까이 다가가지 않았습니다.\n\n그의 목소리는 사그라들어 있었습니다. 예전의 그 얼굴 표정과 함께, 그 힘과 함께, 그 분노와 함께, 마침내 그를 무릎 꿇린 그 부당함에 맞서던 저항과 함께 말입니다. 형태와 색을 가득 머금은 어떤 사물의 가장 희미한 그림자—그것이 그 사물을 보여주는 그림이라고 한다면, 지금의 그는 우리가 전에 이야기를 나눈 그 슈롭셔 사내의 그런 희미한 그림자였습니다.\n\n그는 리처드와 나에게 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 후견인에게 말을 건넸습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 씨, 저를 보러 와주시다니 참 친절하십니다. 제가 보일 날도 이제 얼마 남지 않은 듯합니다. 어르신 손을 잡게 되어 무척 기쁩니다. 어르신은 좋은 분이고, 부당함보다 높이 계신 분이니, 하느님은 아시겠지만 저는 어르신을 존경합니다.\"\n\n그들은 진심을 담아 악수를 나누었고, 후견인은 그에게 위로의 말을 몇 마디 건넸습니다.\n\n\"어르신께는 이상하게 들릴지도 모르겠습니다.\" 그리들리가 대꾸했습니다. \"만일 이번이 우리가 처음 만나는 자리였다면, 저는 이런 모습을 어르신께 보이고 싶지 않았을 겁니다. 하지만 어르신도 아시다시피 저는 끝까지 싸웠습니다. 제 한 손으로 그들 전부에 맞서 일어섰지요. 끝까지 그들에게 진실을 말하고, 그들이 어떤 자들인지, 그들이 저에게 무슨 짓을 했는지 말해주었습니다. 그러니 이 폐허가 된 꼴을 어르신이 보셔도 저는 개의치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 그들에게 수없이, 수없이 용감하게 맞섰소.\" 후견인이 대답했습니다.\n\n\"어르신, 그랬지요.\" 그가 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"제가 더는 그러지 못하게 되면 어떻게 될지 어르신께 말씀드렸지요. 그런데 보십시오! 우리를 좀 보세요, 우리를!\" 그는 플라이트 양이 잡고 있던 손을 그녀의 팔에 꿰어 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 조금 더 끌어당겼습니다.\n\n\"이걸로 끝입니다. 제 모든 옛 인연 가운데, 제 모든 옛 추구와 희망 가운데, 산 자와 죽은 자의 온 세상 가운데, 이 가엾은 영혼 하나만이 저에게 자연스럽게 다가오고, 또 제가 어울릴 만한 사람입니다. 우리 둘 사이에는 여러 해에 걸친 고난의 끈이 있고, 그것이야말로 대법원이 끊어놓지 못한, 제가 이 땅에서 가졌던 유일한 끈입니다.\"\n\n\"내 축복을 받으세요, 그리들리.\" 플라이트 양이 눈물을 흘리며 말했습니다. \"내 축복을 받으세요!\"\n\n\"저는 우쭐대며 생각했습니다. 그들이 결코 제 마음을 꺾지는 못하리라고요, 잔다이스 씨. 그들이 그러지 못하게 하겠노라 굳게 다짐했지요. 저는 정말로 믿었습니다. 제가 무슨 몸의 병으로 죽을 때까지, 그들이 얼마나 우스꽝스러운 자들인지 끝까지 따져 물을 수 있고, 또 그러겠노라고요. 하지만 저는 다 닳아버렸습니다. 얼마나 오래 닳아왔는지는 모르겠습니다. 한 시간 만에 무너져버린 것만 같으니까요. 부디 그들이 이걸 듣게 되지 않기를 바랍니다. 여기 계신 모두가, 제가 그 숱한 세월 그래왔듯 한결같이, 끈질기게 그들에게 맞서다 죽었다고 그들이 믿게끔 해주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n이때 문가 한구석에 앉아 있던 버킷 씨가 인심 좋게도, 자기가 해줄 수 있는 위로를 건넸습니다.\n\n\"이보시오, 이보시오!\" 그가 자기 구석에서 말했습니다. \"그렇게 굴지 마시오, 그리들리 씨. 그저 좀 기운이 빠진 것뿐이오. 우리 모두 때로는 좀 기운이 빠지기 마련이지. 나도 그렇소. 기운 내요, 기운 내! 당신은 또 그자들 전부에게 거듭거듭 성을 낼 거고, 나는 운만 따라준다면 앞으로도 영장을 한 다발은 들고 당신을 잡으러 올 테니까.\"\n\n그는 그저 고개를 가로저을 뿐이었습니다.\n\n\"고개 젓지 마시오.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"끄덕여요. 내가 보고 싶은 게 바로 그거요. 아니, 맙소사, 우리가 함께 보낸 시절이 얼마나 대단했소! 내가 당신을 법정모독죄로 플리트 감옥에서 보고 또 보지 않았소? 당신이 불도그처럼 대법원장에게 달려드는 걸 보겠다는 그 하나의 목적으로, 내가 법정에 스무 번이나 오후마다 나오지 않았소? 당신이 처음 변호사들을 위협하기 시작했을 때, 일주일에 두세 번씩 당신을 상대로 평화유지 선서가 청구되던 일 기억 안 나오? 저기 자그마한 노부인께 여쭤보시오. 늘 그 자리에 계셨으니까. 기운 내요, 그리들리 씨, 기운 내요, 양반!\"\n\n\"이 사람을 어쩌실 작정입니까?\" 조지가 낮은 목소리로 물었습니다.\n\n\"아직 모르겠네.\" 버킷이 같은 어조로 말했습니다. 그러고는 다시 격려를 이어, 큰 소리로 말했습니다. \"다 닳았다고요, 그리들리 씨? 이 몇 주 내내 나를 따돌리고, 나로 하여금 이 지붕을 수고양이처럼 기어오르게 만들고, 의사인 척하고 당신을 보러 오게 만들어놓고서? 그게 어디 다 닳은 사람 같소. 천만에! 자, 내가 당신한테 필요한 게 뭔지 말해주리다. 당신은 자극이 필요해, 알겠소, 기운을 차리려면 말이오. 그게 바로 당신한테 필요한 거요. 당신은 거기 길든 사람이라, 그게 없으면 못 견디는 거지. 나라도 그럴 거요. 자, 그렇다면 여기 링컨스인필즈의 토킹혼 씨가 받아낸 이 영장이 있고, 그 뒤로 대여섯 개 주에 효력이 미치게 되었소. 이 영장으로 나와 함께 가서, 치안판사 앞에서 한바탕 성난 입씨름을 벌이는 게 어떻겠소? 그게 당신한테 약이 될 거요. 당신을 생생하게 해주고, 대법원장과 또 한판 붙을 훈련을 시켜줄 거란 말이오. 항복한다고? 아니, 당신처럼 기운찬 사람이 항복을 입에 올리다니 놀랍구려. 그러면 안 되지. 당신은 대법정에서 벌어지는 구경거리의 절반은 차지하는 사람이오. 조지, 자네가 그리들리 씨를 좀 부축하게, 어디 이분이 누워 있는 것보다 일어나 있는 게 낫지 않은지 봅시다.\"\n\n\"이 사람은 무척 약해졌습니다.\" 기병이 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다.\n\n\"그런가?\" 버킷이 근심스레 대꾸했습니다. \"나는 그저 이 사람 기운을 돋우려는 것뿐이네. 옛 지인이 이렇게 손드는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않아서야. 나한테 좀 발끈하게 만들 수만 있다면, 그게 무엇보다 이 사람 기운을 북돋울 텐데. 좋다면 나한테 닥치는 대로 한 방씩 날려도 좋소. 절대 그걸 빌미 삼지 않을 테니.\"\n\n플라이트 양의 비명이 지붕에 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸고, 그 소리는 지금도 내 귓가에 울립니다.\n\n\"오, 안 돼요, 그리들리!\" 그가 그녀 앞에서 무겁게, 또 고요히 뒤로 쓰러지자 그녀가 울부짖었습니다. \"내 축복도 받지 않고는 안 돼요. 그 숱한 세월이 지났는데!\"\n\n해가 졌고, 빛은 지붕에서 차츰 빠져나갔으며, 그림자는 위쪽으로 슬금슬금 기어올라 갔습니다. 하지만 나에게는, 하나는 산 자요 하나는 죽은 자인 그 한 쌍의 그림자가, 리처드의 출발 위에 가장 캄캄한 밤의 어둠보다도 더 무겁게 드리워졌습니다. 그리고 리처드의 작별 인사 사이로 나는 그 말이 메아리치는 것을 들었습니다. \"제 모든 옛 인연 가운데, 제 모든 옛 추구와 희망 가운데, 산 자와 죽은 자의 온 세상 가운데, 이 가엾은 영혼 하나만이 저에게 자연스럽게 다가오고, 또 제가 어울릴 만한 사람입니다. 우리 둘 사이에는 여러 해에 걸친 고난의 끈이 있고, 그것이야말로 대법원이 끊어놓지 못한, 제가 이 땅에서 가졌던 유일한 끈입니다!\"\n\n커서터가의 쿡스코트가 술렁인다. 시커먼 의혹이 그 평화로운 지역에 도사린다. 쿡스코트 사람들 대부분은 늘 그렇듯 평소 마음 상태 그대로다, 더 낫지도 더 못하지도 않다. 그러나 스내그스비 씨는 달라졌고, 그의 작은 아내는 그것을 안다.\n\n톰 혼자 사는 곳과 링컨스인 광장이 한사코 한 쌍의 길들지 않은 준마가 되어 스내그스비 씨 상상의 마차에 멍에를 메기 때문이다. 마부석에는 버킷 씨가 앉고, 승객은 조와 토킹혼 씨이며, 완벽하게 채비를 갖춘 그 마차는 미친 듯한 속도로 법률 문구점 사업을 가로질러 시계가 한 바퀴 도는 내내 빙글빙글 돈다. 식구들이 끼니를 먹는 작은 앞부엌에서도 마차는 식탁에서 김을 뿜으며 달려 나가는데, 스내그스비 씨가 감자를 곁들여 구운 양고기 다리의 첫 점을 썰다 말고 손을 멈춘 채 부엌 벽을 멍하니 노려보는 바로 그 순간이다.\n\n스내그스비 씨는 자기가 대체 무슨 일에 엮였는지 도무지 종잡지 못한다. 어딘가 뭔가 잘못됐다, 그러나 그 뭔가가 무엇인지, 거기서 무엇이 나올지, 누구에게, 언제, 또 생각지도 듣지도 못한 어느 방향에서 닥칠지가 그의 인생을 사로잡은 수수께끼다. 토킹혼 씨 사무실의 먼지 덮인 표면 너머로 반짝이는 법복과 관모, 훈장과 가터 띠에 대한 그의 어렴풋한 인상, 모든 법학원과 챈서리 레인과 법조계 일대가 입을 모아 경외하는, 가장 훌륭하고 가장 가까운 그 단골 손님이 주관하는 신비에 대한 그의 숭배, 집게손가락을 곧추세우고 은근한 태도를 짓던, 피하거나 거절하기란 도무지 불가능했던 형사 버킷 씨에 대한 그의 기억, 이 모든 것이 그로 하여금 자신이 무언가 위험한 비밀의 당사자이면서도 그게 뭔지조차 모른다고 믿게 만든다. 그리고 이 상태의 무서운 특징은 이것이니, 일상의 어느 시각이든, 가게 문이 열리는 어느 순간이든, 종이 당겨지는 어느 때든, 심부름꾼이 들어오는 어느 때든, 편지가 배달되는 어느 때든, 그 비밀이 바람을 타고 불이 붙어 폭발하고 터져버릴지도 모른다는 것이다. 누구를 날려버릴지는 오직 버킷 씨만 안다.\n\n그런 까닭에 모르는 남자가 가게에 들어와(모르는 남자는 숱하게 들어온다) \"스내그스비 씨 계시오?\"라거나 그와 비슷하게 악의 없는 말을 던질 때마다, 스내그스비 씨의 심장은 죄지은 가슴팍을 쿵쿵 두드린다. 그는 그런 물음에 어찌나 시달리는지, 사내아이가 물어오기라도 하면 계산대 너머로 그 녀석의 귀를 톡 튀기며 분풀이를 하고, 그게 무슨 수작이냐, 어째서 단번에 똑바로 말하지 못하느냐고 따져 묻는다. 더 다루기 힘든 남자들과 아이들이 한사코 스내그스비 씨의 잠 속으로 걸어 들어와 영문 모를 질문으로 그를 공포에 떨게 하는지라, 커서터가의 작은 우유 가게 수탉이 아침이랍시고 늘 하던 그 우스꽝스러운 식으로 홰를 치며 울 무렵이면, 스내그스비 씨는 종종 악몽의 한복판에서 발버둥치고 있고, 그의 작은 아내가 그를 흔들며 \"이 양반이 대체 왜 이래!\" 하고 있다.\n\n그 작은 아내 자신이 그가 겪는 어려움에서 결코 작은 항목이 아니다. 자신이 늘 아내에게 비밀을 숨기고 있다는 것, 어떤 상황에서도 흔들리는 어금니 하나를 감추고 단단히 붙들고 있어야 한다는 것, 그런데 아내의 날카로움은 언제든 그 어금니를 그의 머리에서 비틀어 뽑아낼 채비가 되어 있다는 것, 이런 사정이 스내그스비 씨로 하여금 그 치과의사 같은 아내 앞에서, 주인에게 켕기는 구석이 있어 그 눈길을 마주하느니 차라리 아무 데나 둘러보는 개 같은 꼴을 하게 만든다.\n\n작은 아내가 포착한 이런저런 징표와 조짐은 그녀에게서 흘려보내지지 않는다. 그것들이 그녀를 부추겨 \"스내그스비, 저 양반 마음에 뭔가 있어!\" 하고 말하게 한다. 그리하여 의혹이 커서터가의 쿡스코트에 스며든다. 의혹에서 질투로 가는 길을, 스내그스비 부인은 쿡스코트에서 챈서리 레인으로 가는 길만큼이나 자연스럽고도 가깝게 여긴다. 그리하여 질투가 커서터가의 쿡스코트에 스며든다. 일단 거기 들어서면(사실 늘 그 언저리에 도사리고 있었지만), 그것은 스내그스비 부인의 가슴속에서 무척이나 활발하고 잰싸게 움직여, 그녀로 하여금 밤이면 스내그스비 씨의 주머니를 뒤지게 하고, 스내그스비 씨의 편지를 몰래 정독하게 하고, 낮이면 매상 장부와 원장과 돈서랍과 무쇠 금고를 은밀히 조사하게 하고, 창가에서 망보고 문 뒤에서 엿듣고, 이것저것을 죄다 엉뚱한 쪽으로 끼워 맞추게 한다.\n\n스내그스비 부인이 어찌나 줄곧 촉을 곤두세우고 있는지, 집안은 삐걱대는 마룻장과 바스락대는 옷자락으로 유령이 나올 것만 같다. 견습공들은 옛날 옛적에 누가 여기서 살해당한 건 아닐까 생각한다. 거스터는 한 가지 막연한 생각의 흩어진 조각들을 품고 있는데(투팅에서 주워들은 것으로, 그곳 고아들 사이에 떠돌던 이야기다), 지하실 밑에 돈이 묻혀 있고 흰 수염을 기른 노인이 그것을 지키는데, 주기도문을 거꾸로 외운 탓에 칠천 년 동안 밖으로 나오지 못한다는 것이다.\n\n\"님로드가 누구였더라?\" 스내그스비 부인이 거듭 자문한다. \"그 여자가 누구였지, 그 계집이? 그리고 저 사내애는 또 누구야?\" 그런데 님로드는 스내그스비 부인이 그 이름을 빌려온 저 위대한 사냥꾼만큼이나 깡그리 죽어버렸고, 그 여자는 끄집어낼 길이 없으니, 그녀는 지금으로서는 갑절로 경계심을 곤두세워 마음의 눈길을 사내애에게 돌린다. \"그리고 저 애는,\" 스내그스비 부인이 천 번하고도 한 번째로 되뇐다. \"저 애는 누구야? 저자가 누구냐고!\" 그리고 바로 거기서 스내그스비 부인은 한 줄기 영감에 사로잡힌다.\n\n저 애는 채드밴드 씨를 존경하지 않는다. 그래, 정말 그렇다, 물론 존경할 리가 없다. 당연히 그렇지, 그 전염되기 쉬운 사정을 생각하면. 저 애는 채드밴드 씨한테 초대받고 지시받았다, 어머, 스내그스비 부인이 제 두 귀로 똑똑히 들었다고! 다시 돌아오라고, 그러면 어디로 가야 할지 일러주겠노라고, 채드밴드 씨가 말씀을 베풀어주겠노라고. 그런데 저 애는 결코 오지 않았다! 어째서 결코 오지 않았을까? 오지 말라는 말을 들었으니까. 누가 오지 말라고 했을까? 누가? 하, 하! 스내그스비 부인은 이제 죄다 알아챘다.\n\n하지만 다행히도(스내그스비 부인은 야무지게 고개를 흔들고 야무지게 미소 짓는다) 그 애를 채드밴드 씨가 어제 거리에서 마주쳤다. 그리고 그 애는, 가려 뽑은 신도들의 영적 기쁨을 위해 채드밴드 씨가 갈고닦아 활용하고자 하는 소재를 제공하는지라, 채드밴드 씨에게 붙들렸고, 자기가 어디 사는지 그 목사님께 보여주지 않으면, 또 내일 밤 쿡스코트에 나타나겠다는 약속을 맺고 이행하지 않으면 경찰에 넘기겠다는 위협을 받았으니, \"내—일—밤,\" 스내그스비 부인이 그저 강조하느라 또 한 번 야무지게 미소 짓고 또 한 번 야무지게 고개를 흔들며 되풀이한다. 그리고 내일 밤 그 애가 이리로 올 것이고, 내일 밤 스내그스비 부인은 그 애에게서, 그리고 또 다른 누군가에게서 눈을 떼지 않을 것이며, 오, 너희야 너희 은밀한 길을 오래도록 걷고 다닐 수 있겠지(스내그스비 부인이 거만과 경멸을 담아 말한다), 하지만 이 몸의 눈은 못 가려!\n\n스내그스비 부인은 누구의 귀에도 작은북을 울리지 않고, 제 속셈을 조용히 간직하며 입을 다문다. 내일이 온다, 유지업을 위한 구수한 채비도 차려진다, 저녁이 온다. 검은 외투를 걸친 스내그스비 씨가 온다, 채드밴드 부부가 온다, (게걸스러운 그릇이 가득 차고 나면) 가르침을 받으러 견습공들과 거스터가 온다, 마침내 그가 온다, 푹 수그린 머리에, 뒤로 질질, 앞으로 질질, 오른쪽으로 질질, 왼쪽으로 질질 걸으며, 진흙투성이 손에 털모자 조각을 쥔 채, 마치 자기가 잡아온 옴 오른 새라도 되는 양 날로 먹기 전에 깃털을 쥐어뜯듯 그것을 쥐어뜯으며, 조가 온다, 채드밴드 씨가 갈고닦아야 할 그 지독하게도 뻣뻣한 소재가.\n\n스내그스비 부인은 거스터에게 이끌려 작은 응접실로 들어오는 조에게 감시의 눈초리를 날카롭게 고정한다. 그는 들어오자마자 스내그스비 씨를 쳐다본다. 아하! 어째서 저 애가 스내그스비 씨를 쳐다보지? 스내그스비 씨도 그를 쳐다본다. 스내그스비 부인이 이미 죄다 알아챈 까닭이 아니라면 어째서 저 양반이 저럴까? 그렇지 않다면 어째서 저런 눈길이 둘 사이를 오갈까, 어째서 스내그스비 씨가 당황해서 손으로 입을 가리고 신호 같은 헛기침을 할까? 스내그스비 씨가 저 애의 아비라는 건 수정처럼 명백하다.\n\n\"평안하시기를, 친애하는 벗들이여,\" 채드밴드 씨가 일어서며 거룩한 얼굴에서 배어 나온 기름기를 닦으며 말한다. \"우리에게 평안이 있기를! 친애하는 벗들이여, 어찌하여 우리에게입니까? 무릇,\" 그는 기름진 미소를 띤다. \"평안은 우리에게 맞서지 못하기에, 무릇 평안은 우리를 위한 것이어야 하기에, 무릇 평안은 굳히지 않고 무르게 하기에, 무릇 평안은 매처럼 전쟁을 일으키지 않고 비둘기처럼 우리에게로 돌아오기에. 그러므로 친애하는 벗들이여, 우리에게 평안이 있기를! 인간 소년아, 앞으로 나오너라!\"\n\n채드밴드 씨가 물렁한 손바닥을 쭉 내밀어 그것을 조의 팔에 얹고 어디다 세워둘지 궁리한다. 조는 그 거룩하신 벗의 의도를 무척 의심스러워하며, 무언가 실질적이고 고통스러운 일이 자기에게 가해지려는 게 아닌가 영 확신이 안 서서 중얼거린다. \"냅둬요. 난 아저씨한테 아무 말도 안 했어요. 냅두라구요.\"\n\n\"아니다, 젊은 벗이여,\" 채드밴드 씨가 매끄럽게 말한다. \"나는 너를 내버려두지 않으련다. 어찌하여? 무릇 나는 추수하는 일꾼이기에, 무릇 나는 애쓰고 땀 흘리는 자이기에, 무릇 너는 내게 맡겨져 내 손안의 귀중한 도구가 되었기에. 친애하는 벗들이여, 부디 내가 이 도구를 그대들의 유익을 위하여, 그대들의 이득을 위하여, 그대들의 소득을 위하여, 그대들의 복리를 위하여, 그대들의 풍요를 위하여 부려쓰게 하소서! 젊은 벗이여, 이 걸상에 앉아라.\"\n\n조는 그 거룩하신 양반이 자기 머리카락을 자르려는 줄로 단단히 믿은 모양인지 두 팔로 머리를 감싸고, 온갖 거부의 몸짓을 다 보이며 갖은 애를 먹인 끝에야 요구받은 자세로 앉혀진다.\n\n마침내 마네킹처럼 자리가 잡히자, 채드밴드 씨는 탁자 뒤로 물러나 곰 발바닥 같은 손을 치켜들고 말한다. \"친애하는 벗들이여!\" 이것이 청중이 두루 자리를 잡으라는 신호다. 견습공들은 속으로 낄낄대며 서로 팔꿈치로 쿡쿡 찌른다. 거스터는 멍하니 넋을 놓은 상태에 빠지는데, 채드밴드 씨에 대한 얼떨떨한 경탄과, 제 처지가 절절히 와닿는 저 의지가지없는 버림받은 아이에 대한 연민이 뒤섞인 것이다. 스내그스비 부인은 말없이 화약 도화선을 깐다. 채드밴드 부인은 난롯가에서 험상궂게 몸을 추스르고 무릎을 덥히는데, 그 감각이 웅변을 받아들이기에 알맞다고 여겨서다.\n\n마침 채드밴드 씨에게는 설교단의 버릇이 하나 있으니, 신도 가운데 한 사람을 눈으로 콕 찍어 붙들고 그 특정인을 상대로 제 논점을 기름지게 펼치는 것이다. 그러면 그 사람은 이따금 끙, 끄응, 헉 하는 소리나, 속이 움직이는 그밖의 들릴 만한 표현으로 화답하리라 기대되며, 속이 움직이는 그 표현은 옆 좌석의 어느 나이 든 부인에게 메아리쳐, 거기 모인 더 발효되기 쉬운 죄인들의 무리로 벌금 놀이처럼 번져나가, 의회의 환호 같은 구실을 하며 채드밴드 씨의 증기를 끌어올린다. 그저 버릇의 힘으로, 채드밴드 씨는 \"친애하는 벗들이여!\" 하고 말하면서 눈길을 스내그스비 씨에게 얹어두었고, 이제 그 불운한 문구상을, 이미 충분히 당황한 사람을, 제 설교의 직통 수신자로 삼는다.\n\n\"우리 가운데 여기, 친애하는 벗들이여,\" 채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"한 이방인이자 한 이교도가 있으니, 톰 혼자 사는 곳의 천막에 깃든 자요, 이 땅 거죽 위를 떠도는 자라. 우리 가운데 여기, 친애하는 벗들이여,\" 그러면서 채드밴드 씨는 더러운 엄지손톱으로 논점을 비틀어 풀어내며, 스내그스비 씨에게 기름진 미소를 베푸는데, 이는 그가 아직 쓰러지지 않았다면 곧 논변의 뒤집기 한판으로 메다꽂아주겠다는 뜻이다. \"한 형제이자 한 소년이 있으니. 부모도 없고, 친척도 없고, 양 떼와 소 떼도 없고, 금도 은도 보석도 없는 자라. 자, 친애하는 벗들이여, 어찌하여 내가 그에게 이런 소유가 없다고 말하는가? 어찌하여? 어찌하여 그러한가?\" 채드밴드 씨는 마치 무척 기발하고 훌륭한, 아주 새로운 수수께끼를 스내그스비 씨에게 내놓으며 부디 포기하지 말라고 간청하기라도 하듯 그 물음을 던진다.\n\n스내그스비 씨는 방금 작은 아내에게서 받은 그 알 수 없는 눈길에 크게 당황한 터라—채드밴드 씨가 부모라는 단어를 입에 올린 바로 그 무렵의 일이었다—조심스레 한마디 던지고 만다. \"글쎄요, 정말 모르겠습니다, 목사님.\" 이렇게 끼어들자 채드밴드 부인은 눈을 부라리고 스내그스비 부인은 \"창피한 줄 아세요!\" 하고 말한다.\n\n\"내게 한 음성이 들리는구나,\" 채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"그것이 고요하고 작은 음성인가, 친애하는 벗들이여? 그러기를 간절히 바라나, 아무래도 아닌 듯하구나—\"\n\n\"아—!\" 스내그스비 부인에게서 새어 나온다.\n\n\"그 음성이 이르기를, '나는 모르겠다' 하는구나. 그러면 내가 그대들에게 어찌하여인지 일러주리라. 내 말하노니, 지금 우리 가운데 있는 이 형제는 부모도 없고, 친척도 없고, 양 떼와 소 떼도 없고, 금도 은도 보석도 없으니, 이는 우리 가운데 어떤 이들에게 비쳐드는 빛이 그에게는 없는 까닭이라. 그 빛이 무엇인가? 그것이 무엇인가? 내 그대들에게 묻노니, 그 빛이 무엇인가?\"\n\n채드밴드 씨는 고개를 뒤로 빼고 잠시 멈추나, 스내그스비 씨는 다시 제 파멸로 꾀어들지 않는다. 채드밴드 씨는 탁자 위로 몸을 기울여, 앞서 말한 그 엄지손톱으로 곧바로 이어질 말을 스내그스비 씨 속으로 콕 찔러넣는다.\n\n\"그것은,\" 채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"빛 중의 빛이요, 해 중의 해요, 달 중의 달이요, 별 중의 별이라. 그것은 진리의 빛이라.\"\n\n채드밴드 씨는 다시 몸을 곧추세우고는, 마치 그 말을 듣고 어떤 심정일지 알고 싶어 못 견디겠다는 듯 의기양양하게 스내그스비 씨를 바라본다.\n\n\"진리의,\" 채드밴드 씨가 또 한 방 먹인다. \"그것이 등불 중의 등불이 아니라고 내게 말하지 말라. 내 그대에게 이르노니 그것이 바로 그것이라. 내 그대에게 백만 번이고 거듭 이르노니 그것이 바로 그것이라. 그것이라! 내 그대에게 이르노니, 그대가 좋든 싫든 내가 그대에게 선포하리라, 아니, 그대가 싫어할수록 더욱더 그대에게 선포하리라. 나팔을 불어가며! 내 그대에게 이르노니, 그대가 그것에 맞서 일어선다면, 그대는 쓰러지리라, 그대는 짓이겨지리라, 그대는 두들겨 맞으리라, 그대는 금이 가리라, 그대는 박살나리라.\"\n\n이 웅변의 비상이—채드밴드 씨의 추종자들이 그 두루 막강한 위력으로 크게 떠받드는 솜씨다—지금 빚어낸 효과는, 채드밴드 씨를 불쾌하리만치 후끈 달아오르게 했을 뿐 아니라, 죄 없는 스내그스비 씨를 놋쇠 같은 이마와 금강석 같은 심장을 지닌 미덕의 단호한 원수처럼 보이게 만든 것이라, 그 불운한 장사꾼은 더욱더 낭패스러워져, 채드밴드 씨가 어쩌다 그에게 마지막 일격을 가할 무렵엔 풀 죽고 처지가 거북해진 상태가 한참 깊어진다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗들이여,\" 그는 한동안 기름진 머리를 톡톡 두드린 뒤에 다시 이어간다—그런데 그 머리에서 어찌나 김이 모락모락 솟는지 거기다 손수건에 불을 붙이는 것만 같고, 두드릴 때마다 손수건마저 김을 뿜는 듯하다—\"우리가 보잘것없는 재능으로나마 갈고닦아 보려 애쓰는 이 주제를 좇아, 사랑의 마음으로 물어보자, 내가 언급한 그 진리가 무엇인가를. 무릇, 젊은 벗들이여,\" 그가 견습공들과 거스터를 별안간 지목하니 그들이 화들짝 놀란다. \"의사가 내게 감홍이나 피마자유가 몸에 좋다고 일러준다면, 나는 당연히 감홍이 무엇이고 피마자유가 무엇인지 물을 수 있으리라. 그 둘 중 하나로, 혹은 둘 다로 내 몸을 다스리기 전에 그것을 알고 싶을 수 있으리라. 자, 젊은 벗들이여, 그렇다면 이 진리란 무엇인가? 첫째로(사랑의 마음으로), 흔히 말하는 종류의 진리—일하는 옷—날마다 걸치는 그 진리란 무엇인가, 젊은 벗들이여? 그것은 속임인가?\"\n\n\"아—!\" 스내그스비 부인에게서.\n\n\"그것은 숨김인가?\"\n\n스내그스비 부인에게서 아니라는 떨림.\n\n\"그것은 감춤인가?\"\n\n스내그스비 부인에게서 고개를 가로젓는 몸짓—아주 길고 아주 야무진.\n\n\"아니다, 친애하는 벗들이여, 그 어느 것도 아니다. 그 이름 중 어느 것도 그것에 어울리지 않는다. 지금 우리 가운데 있는 이 젊은 이교도가—그는 지금, 친애하는 벗들이여, 잠들어 있고, 그 눈꺼풀에는 무관심과 멸망의 봉인이 찍혀 있다, 그러나 그를 깨우지 말라, 그를 위하여 내가 씨름하고, 맞붙어 싸우고, 분투하고, 이겨내야 마땅하기 때문이라—이 젊고 완고한 이교도가 우리에게 수탉이며, 황소며, 한 부인이며, 한 군주에 관한 허튼소리를 늘어놓았을 때, 그것이 진리였는가? 아니다. 혹 일부라도 그러했다 한들, 그것이 온전히 전부 그러했는가? 아니다, 친애하는 벗들이여, 아니다!\"\n\n스내그스비 씨가 제 영혼의 창인 두 눈으로 들어와 온 집안을 샅샅이 뒤지는 작은 아내의 눈길을 견뎌낼 수 있다면, 그는 지금의 그가 아닌 딴사람일 것이다. 그는 움츠러들고 시들어든다.\n\n\"혹은, 어린 벗들이여,\" 채드밴드 씨가 그들의 이해 수준으로 내려서며 말하는데, 그 기름지고 온순한 미소에는 일부러 이를 위해 계단을 한참 내려왔노라는 매우 노골적인 과시가 어려 있다. \"이 집의 주인이 도성으로 나아가 거기서 뱀장어 한 마리를 보고는, 돌아와, 이 집의 안주인을 제게로 불러, '세라여, 나와 더불어 기뻐하라, 내가 코끼리를 보았노라!' 하고 말한다면, 그것이 진리이겠는가?\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 눈물짓는다.\n\n\"혹은 이렇게 해보자, 어린 벗들이여, 그가 코끼리를 보고도, 돌아와 '보라, 도성은 황량하도다, 나는 뱀장어 한 마리밖에 보지 못하였노라' 하고 말한다면, 그것이 진리이겠는가?\"\n\n스내그스비 부인이 큰 소리로 흐느낀다.\n\n\"혹은 이렇게 해보자, 어린 벗들이여,\" 채드밴드 씨가 그 소리에 자극받아 말한다. \"이 잠든 이교도의 인륜을 저버린 부모가—그에게도 부모가 있었다, 어린 벗들이여, 의심할 여지없이—그를 이리 떼와 독수리 떼와, 들개 떼와 어린 영양 떼와, 뱀들에게 내던져버린 뒤, 제 거처로 돌아가 곰방대를 빨고, 술단지를 기울이고, 피리를 불고 춤을 추고, 맥주를 들이켜고, 푸줏간 고기와 가금을 먹었다면, 그것이 진리이겠는가?\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 발작의 먹이로 제 몸을 내던지는 것으로 화답하는데, 순순한 먹이가 아니라 울부짖고 쥐어뜯는 먹이라, 쿡스코트가 그녀의 비명으로 메아리친다. 마침내 강경증에 빠진 그녀를 그랜드피아노 옮기듯 좁은 계단 위로 떠메고 올라가야 한다. 이루 말할 수 없는 고초 끝에, 더없는 낭패를 빚어낸 끝에, 침실에서 보내온 급보로 그녀는 통증은 가셨으나 몹시 지쳤노라고 선언되는데, 그런 와중에 피아노포르테 이송 작업에서 짓밟히고 깔린 스내그스비 씨가, 몹시 겁먹고 기진한 채로, 응접실 문 뒤에서 빠끔히 나와 본다.\n\n이 모든 동안 조는 자기가 잠에서 깬 그 자리에 줄곧 서서, 내내 모자를 쥐어뜯어 털 부스러기를 입에 넣고 있었다. 그는 뉘우치는 기색으로 그것을 뱉어낸다, 자기는 천성이 도무지 개선될 수 없는 죄인이며, 자기가 깨어 있으려 애써봐야 헛일이라고, 자기는 결코 아무것도 모르고 말 거라고 느끼기 때문이다. 그러나 조여, 어쩌면 너의 마음처럼 짐승에 가까운 마음에게마저 너무도 흥미롭고 가슴 저민, 이 땅에서 평범한 사람들을 위해 행해진 행적을 기록한 이야기가 하나 있을지도 모른다, 만일 채드밴드 무리가 제 몸뚱이를 그 빛에서 비켜주고, 그저 소박한 경외로 그것을 네게 보여주기만 한다면, 그저 그것을 갈고닦지 않은 채로 내버려두기만 한다면, 그저 그것이 그들의 가당찮은 도움 없이도 충분히 웅변적임을 알아주기만 한다면—그 이야기가 너를 깨어 있게 붙들고, 너는 거기서 아직 무언가를 배울 수 있을지도 모른다!\n\n조는 그런 책에 대해 들어본 적이 없다. 그것을 엮은 이들이나 채드밴드 목사나 그에게는 다 한가지인데, 다만 채드밴드 목사는 알고 있고, 그가 오 분 떠드는 걸 듣느니 차라리 한 시간 동안 그한테서 달아나는 편이 낫겠다고 여길 뿐이다. \"여기서 더 기다려봐야 소용없겠어,\" 조는 생각한다. \"스내그스비 아저씨는 오늘 밤 나한테 아무 말도 안 할 거야.\" 그리고 그는 질질 끌며 아래층으로 내려간다.\n\n그런데 아래층에는 마음씨 고운 거스터가, 부엌 계단 난간을 붙들고 발작을 막아내고 있는데, 아직은 막아낼지 어떨지 미덥지가 않다, 스내그스비 부인의 비명에 유발된 발작이기 때문이다. 그녀는 제 몫의 빵과 치즈 야참을 조에게 건네줄 참이고, 처음으로 그와 한두 마디 말을 주고받아 본다.\n\n\"이거 먹어, 가엾은 애야,\" 거스터가 말한다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 누나,\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"배고프니?\"\n\n\"딱 그래요!\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"네 아빠랑 엄마는 어떻게 됐어, 응?\"\n\n조는 한 입 베어 물다 말고 돌처럼 굳어버린다. 투팅에 사당을 둔 그 기독교 성인이 거두는 고아 가운데 하나인 이 처녀가 조의 어깨를 토닥였는데, 그의 평생에 어떤 점잖은 손길이 그에게 그렇게 얹힌 것은 이번이 처음이기 때문이다.\n\n\"난 그런 거 하나도 몰라요,\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"나도 우리 엄마 아빠는 그래,\" 거스터가 외친다. 그녀는 발작에 유리한 증세를 억누르고 있다가, 무언가에 놀란 듯하더니 계단 아래로 사라진다.\n\n\"조,\" 그 아이가 계단에서 머뭇거리자 법률 문구상이 부드럽게 속삭인다.\n\n\"여기 있어요, 스내그스비 아저씨!\"\n\n\"난 네가 간 줄 몰랐다—여기 반 크라운 더 있다, 조. 일전에 우리 둘이 함께 나갔던 그날 밤, 그 부인에 대해 아무 말 안 한 건 정말 잘한 일이었어. 괜한 말썽을 부를 테니까. 너는 아무리 조용히 굴어도 지나치지 않다, 조.\"\n\n\"잘 알아요, 아저씨!\"\n\n그러고서, 잘 자거라.\n\n주름 장식을 두르고 나이트캡을 쓴 유령 같은 그림자 하나가 법률 문구상을 따라 그가 나온 방으로 가더니 더 위층으로 스르르 올라간다. 그리고 이제부터 그는, 어디를 가든, 제 그림자가 아닌 또 다른 그림자의 시중을 받기 시작하는데, 제 그림자에 못지않게 한결같고, 제 그림자에 못지않게 조용한 그림자다. 그리고 그의 그림자가 어떤 비밀의 기운 속으로 들어가든, 그 비밀에 얽힌 모든 이들은 조심할지어다! 경계를 늦추지 않는 스내그스비 부인 또한 거기 있을 테니—그의 뼈 중의 뼈요, 그의 살 중의 살이요, 그의 그림자 중의 그림자가.\n\n겨울 아침이 흐릿한 눈과 누르스름한 얼굴로 레스터 광장 일대를 굽어보는데, 그곳 주민들은 좀체 잠자리에서 빠져나오려 하지 않는다. 그들 가운데 상당수는 아무리 환한 시각에도 일찍 일어나는 법이 없으니, 해가 높이 뜨면 둥지에 깃들고 별이 반짝일 때면 두 눈 부릅뜨고 먹잇감을 노리는 밤의 새들인 까닭이다. 거무튀튀한 차양과 커튼 뒤, 위층과 다락방에서, 가짜 이름과 가짜 머리카락과 가짜 작위와 가짜 보석과 가짜 내력 아래 어지간히 몸을 숨긴 채, 한 무리의 산적 떼가 첫잠에 빠져 있다. 외국의 갤리선과 본국의 디딜방아를 몸소 겪은 경험담을 풀어놓을 만한 노름판의 신사들, 허약함과 비참한 두려움에 영영 떠는 강성 정부의 첩자들, 망가진 반역자와 겁쟁이와 깡패와 노름꾼과 사기꾼과 협잡꾼과 위증꾼, 그중 더러는 더러운 장식끈 밑에 낙인의 자국이 또렷하다. 모두가 네로보다 더한 잔혹을 품었고, 뉴게이트 감옥보다 더한 죄악을 품었다. 무명베나 작업복 차림의 악마가 제아무리 악할 수 있다 해도(둘 다 입어도 무척 악할 수 있다), 셔츠 앞섶에 핀을 꽂고 자신을 신사라 일컫고 카드나 패에 돈을 걸고 당구 한두 판을 치고 어음과 약속어음을 조금 아는 그런 악마야말로, 다른 어떤 모습으로 나타날 때보다 더 음흉하고 더 냉혹하고 더 견딜 수 없는 악마다. 버킷 씨는 그런 모습으로, 마음만 먹으면 언제든, 레스터 광장으로 흘러드는 곁가지 물길에 여전히 스며 있는 그를 찾아낼 것이다.\n\n그러나 겨울 아침은 그를 원하지 않고 그를 깨우지도 않는다. 겨울 아침은 사격장의 조지 씨와 그의 단짝을 깨운다. 둘은 일어나 매트리스를 둘둘 말아 치워 둔다. 조지 씨는 자그마한 거울 앞에서 면도를 마친 뒤, 맨머리에 맨가슴으로 작은 마당의 펌프로 성큼성큼 걸어 나가고, 이윽고 노란 비누와 마찰과 흩날리는 빗방울과 더없이 차가운 물로 번들거리며 돌아온다. 큼직한 거친 수건에 몸을 문지르며, 방금 물 위로 올라온 군대식 잠수부처럼 푸푸 숨을 내뿜으며, 문지를수록 햇볕에 그을린 관자놀이의 머리칼이 점점 더 단단히 곱슬거려서 쇠갈퀴나 빗솔 같은 억센 도구가 아니고서는 도무지 풀어질 성싶지 않은 그 머리칼을 문지르며 — 문지르고, 헐떡이고, 닦고, 숨을 내뿜으며, 목을 더 편하게 박박 문지르려고 고개를 이쪽저쪽으로 돌리며, 군인다운 다리에 물이 튀지 않게 몸을 잔뜩 앞으로 굽힌 채 서 있는 동안, 무릎을 꿇고 불을 붙이던 필은 마치 그 광경을 보는 것만으로 제 몸 씻기는 충분하다는 듯, 그리고 제 주인이 내던지는 남아도는 건강을 받아들이는 것만으로 하루치 활력 회복은 넉넉하다는 듯 둘레를 돌아본다.\n\n조지 씨는 몸이 마르면 단단한 솔 두 개로 한꺼번에 머리를 빗어 대는데, 그 모습이 어찌나 가차 없는지, 사격장을 쓸며 어깨로 비집고 돌아다니던 필이 동정 어린 눈을 찡긋한다. 이 박박 비비기가 끝나면 조지 씨의 몸단장에서 멋 내는 부분은 금세 마무리된다. 그는 파이프에 담배를 채워 불을 붙이고, 늘 그러듯 담배를 피우며 위아래로 성큼성큼 거니는데, 그동안 필은 뜨거운 빵과 커피의 짙은 냄새를 풍기며 아침을 준비한다. 그는 진지하게 담배를 피우며 느린 박자로 걷는다. 어쩌면 오늘 아침의 파이프는 무덤 속 그리들리를 추모하는 데 바쳐졌는지도 모른다.\n\n\"그래서 말이지, 필,\" 한참을 말없이 거닐던 사격장의 조지가 입을 연다. \"어젯밤에 시골 꿈을 꿨다고?\"\n\n그러고 보니 필은 잠자리에서 허둥지둥 기어 나오면서 놀란 어조로 그렇게 말했었다.\n\n\"네, 두목님.\"\n\n\"어떤 모습이었나?\"\n\n\"어떤 모습이었는지는 저도 잘 모르겠습니다, 두목님.\" 필이 곰곰이 생각하며 말한다.\n\n\"그럼 시골인 줄은 어떻게 알았어?\"\n\n\"풀밭 때문인 것 같습니다. 그리고 그 위에 있던 백조들 때문이고요.\" 한참 더 생각한 끝에 필이 말한다.\n\n\"백조들이 풀밭에서 뭘 하고 있었는데?\"\n\n\"풀을 뜯어 먹고 있었던 것 같습니다.\" 필이 말한다.\n\n주인은 다시 걷기 시작하고, 부하는 다시 아침 준비에 매달린다. 그것이 꼭 긴 준비라야 하는 건 아니니, 두 사람 몫의 아주 단출한 아침 식사 도구를 차려 놓고 녹슨 화격자의 불에 베이컨 한 조각을 굽는 일로 그치기 때문이다. 하지만 필은 필요한 물건 하나하나를 가지러 사격장을 거의 한 바퀴씩 옆걸음으로 돌아야 하고, 한 번에 두 가지를 결코 가져오는 법이 없으니, 사정이 이런지라 시간이 걸린다. 마침내 아침이 차려진다. 필이 그것을 알리자, 조지 씨는 화로 선반에 파이프를 두드려 재를 털고, 파이프 자체는 굴뚝 모퉁이에 세워 둔 다음, 식사 자리에 앉는다. 그가 제 몫을 덜고 나면, 필도 그를 따라 작은 직사각형 식탁의 맨 끝에 앉아 접시를 무릎 위에 올려놓는다. 겸손해서인지, 아니면 새까매진 손을 감추려는 것인지, 아니면 그것이 그가 밥 먹는 타고난 방식이어서인지.\n\n\"시골이라.\" 나이프와 포크를 놀리며 조지 씨가 말한다. \"이봐, 자넨 시골이라곤 한 번도 구경한 적이 없을 것 같은데, 필?\"\n\n\"습지는 한 번 봤습니다.\" 필이 흡족하게 아침을 먹으며 말한다.\n\n\"무슨 습지?\"\n\n\"그 습지요, 사령관님.\" 필이 대답한다.\n\n\"그게 어디 있는데?\"\n\n\"어디 있는지는 모릅니다.\" 필이 말한다. \"하지만 봤습니다, 두목님. 평평했어요. 그리고 안개가 자욱했고요.\"\n\n두목이니 사령관이니 하는 말은 필에게 서로 바꿔 쓸 수 있는 호칭으로, 똑같은 존경과 공경을 나타내며 조지 씨 말고는 그 누구에게도 쓰이지 않는다.\n\n\"난 시골에서 태어났어, 필.\"\n\n\"정말이십니까, 사령관님?\"\n\n\"그래. 거기서 자랐지.\"\n\n필은 한쪽 눈썹을 치켜올리고, 관심을 드러내려 공손히 주인을 빤히 쳐다본 다음, 여전히 그를 응시하며 커피를 크게 한 모금 꿀꺽 들이켠다.\n\n\"내가 모르는 새소리란 없지.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"이름을 못 댈 영국 나뭇잎이나 열매도 별로 없고. 마음만 먹으면 지금도 못 오를 나무가 별로 없어. 한때 난 진짜 시골 아이였어. 우리 착한 어머니가 시골에 사셨거든.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 노부인이셨겠습니다, 두목님.\" 필이 한마디 한다.\n\n\"암! 그것도 삼십오 년 전엔 그리 늙으시지도 않았고.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"하지만 장담컨대 아흔이 되셔도 나만큼이나 꼿꼿하고, 나만큼이나 어깨가 떡 벌어지셨을 거야.\"\n\n\"아흔에 돌아가셨습니까, 두목님?\" 필이 묻는다.\n\n\"아니. 헛소리! 부디 편히 잠드시기를, 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\" 기병이 말한다. \"대체 뭣 때문에 시골 아이니 가출이니 쓸모없는 놈들이니 하는 얘기를 꺼냈을까? 바로 자네 때문이지, 암! 그래서 자넨 습지하고 꿈 빼고는 시골이라곤 한 번도 구경 못 했단 말이지. 응?\"\n\n필이 고개를 가로젓는다.\n\n\"보고 싶나?\"\n\n\"아-아니요, 딱히 그런 건 모르겠습니다.\" 필이 말한다.\n\n\"도시면 충분하다 이거지, 응?\"\n\n\"그게, 사령관님,\" 필이 말한다. \"저는 다른 건 아는 게 없고, 게다가 새로운 걸 익히기엔 너무 늙어 가는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"자넨 대체 몇 살인가, 필?\" 김이 나는 받침 접시를 입으로 가져가다 말고 기병이 묻는다.\n\n\"여덟이 들어가는 어떤 숫자입니다.\" 필이 말한다. \"여든은 아니고요. 그렇다고 열여덟도 아니고. 그 사이 어디쯤입니다.\"\n\n조지 씨는 받침 접시를 맛도 보지 않은 채 천천히 내려놓으며 웃음 섞인 목소리로 \"이런, 대체 무슨 소리야, 필—\" 하고 입을 떼다가, 필이 더러운 손가락을 꼽으며 셈하는 것을 보고 멈춘다.\n\n\"제가 땜장이를 따라갔을 때, 교구 셈법으로 딱 여덟이었습니다.\" 필이 말한다. \"심부름을 가던 길에, 그 사람이 낡은 건물 밑에서 혼자 불을 쬐며 아주 편안하게 앉아 있는 걸 봤죠. 그가 '이봐 젊은이, 나하고 같이 갈 텐가?' 하길래 제가 '예' 했고, 그 사람하고 저하고 그 불하고 함께 클러큰웰의 집으로 갔습니다. 그게 만우절이었어요. 저는 열까지 셀 줄 알았는데, 만우절이 다시 돌아왔을 때 속으로 그랬죠. '자, 이 친구야, 넌 이제 하나에 여덟이 들어가는 나이다.' 그다음 만우절엔 그랬고요. '자, 이 친구야, 넌 이제 둘에 여덟이 들어가는 나이다.' 그러다 보니 열에 여덟이 들어가는 나이가 됐고, 두 번의 열에 여덟이 들어가는 나이가 됐죠. 그게 너무 높이 올라가니까 제 머리로 감당이 안 됐는데, 어쨌든 그래서 여덟이 들어간다는 건 늘 압니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 조지 씨가 아침 식사를 다시 시작하며 말한다. \"그래, 그 땜장이는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"술 때문에 병원에 들어갔고, 병원이 그 사람을—유리 상자에 넣었다고, 그렇게 들었습니다.\" 필이 알쏭달쏭하게 대답한다.\n\n\"그래서 자넨 승진했군? 사업을 물려받았나, 필?\"\n\n\"네, 사령관님, 사업을 물려받았죠. 뭐 그런 게 사업이라면요. 그리 대단한 구역도 아니었습니다—새프런 힐, 해튼 가든, 클러큰웰, 스미스필드 그 일대로, 가난한 동네라 다 떨어질 때까지 주전자를 쓰는 곳이었죠. 떠돌이 땜장이 대부분이 우리 집에 와서 묵었는데, 그게 우리 주인 벌이의 가장 좋은 부분이었습니다. 그런데 그 사람들이 저한테는 안 왔어요. 저는 그 사람 같지가 않았거든요. 그 사람은 그들한테 노래를 멋들어지게 불러 줄 수 있었지만, 저는 못 했죠! 그 사람은 쇠나 양철로 된 거라면 무슨 냄비든 곡조를 뽑아냈지만, 저는 냄비로는 때우거나 물 끓이는 것 말고는 아무것도 못 했어요—음악적 재주라곤 타고난 게 없었으니까요. 게다가 저는 너무 못생겨서, 그 사람들 아내들이 저를 두고 불평했습니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람들 참 까다롭군. 군중 속에선 자네도 충분히 무난히 통할 텐데, 필!\" 기병이 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 말한다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 두목님.\" 필이 고개를 저으며 대답한다. \"아닙니다, 그렇지 않을 겁니다. 땜장이를 따라갔을 땐 그럭저럭 봐줄 만했죠, 뭐 그때도 자랑할 정도는 아니었지만요. 그런데 어릴 적에 입으로 불을 불어 대다 얼굴빛을 망치고, 머리카락을 그슬리고, 연기를 들이마시고, 게다가 뜨거운 쇠붙이에 부딪쳐 그 자국을 내고 다니는 게 타고난 불운이었고, 또 나이가 좀 들면서 땜장이가 술에 너무 곯아떨어졌을 때마다—거의 늘 그랬지만—주먹다짐을 벌이고 한 탓에, 그때부터 이미 제 미모가 묘했죠, 아주 묘했어요. 그 뒤로는 또 어떻고요. 사내들이 장난질을 일삼던 어두운 대장간에서 십수 년을 보낸 데다, 가스 공장 사고에서 그슬리고, 또 불꽃놀이 사업에서 화약 채우다 창밖으로 나가떨어진 통에, 저는 구경거리로 내세워도 될 만큼 추하게 됐습니다!\"\n\n그런 처지에 더없이 만족한 태도로 체념하며, 필은 커피 한 잔을 더 청한다. 그것을 마시며 그가 말한다. \"화약 채우다 터진 사고가 있고 나서, 제가 사령관님을 처음 봤죠. 기억하십니까?\"\n\n\"기억하지, 필. 자넨 햇빛 속을 걸어가고 있었어.\"\n\n\"기어가고 있었습니다, 두목님, 벽에 붙어서—\"\n\n\"맞아, 필—어깨로 비집으며 나아가고 있었지—\"\n\n\"잠자리 모자를 쓰고요!\" 필이 흥분하여 외친다.\n\n\"잠자리 모자를 쓰고—\"\n\n\"그리고 지팡이 두 개로 절뚝거리며요!\" 필이 더욱 흥분하여 소리친다.\n\n\"지팡이 두 개로. 그때—\"\n\n\"그때 사령관님이 멈춰 서시더니,\" 필이 컵과 받침 접시를 내려놓고 무릎의 접시를 황급히 치우며 외친다. \"저한테 이러셨죠. '이런, 전우여! 자네 전쟁터에 다녀왔구먼!' 저는 그때 사령관님한테 별말 못 했습니다, 사령관님처럼 그렇게 튼튼하고 건강하고 대담한 분이 저 같은 절뚝거리는 뼈다귀 자루한테 말을 걸려고 멈춰 서다니 깜짝 놀랐거든요. 그런데 사령관님은 가슴에서 우러나오듯 더없이 다정하게, 마치 뜨거운 무언가 한 잔처럼 이렇게 말씀하셨죠. '무슨 사고를 당한 건가? 심하게 다쳤구먼. 어찌 된 일이야, 이 친구야? 기운 내고 어디 얘기해 보게!' 기운 내라니요! 저는 이미 기운이 났습니다! 제가 사령관님한테 그만큼 말했고, 사령관님이 저한테 더 말씀하셨고, 제가 사령관님한테 더 말했고, 사령관님이 저한테 더 말씀하셨고, 그래서 제가 여기 있습니다, 사령관님! 제가 여기 있습니다, 사령관님!\" 필은 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 영문 모를 옆걸음질을 치며 외친다. \"과녁이 필요하다면, 아니면 그게 사업에 도움이 된다면, 손님들더러 저를 겨냥하라고 하십쇼. 그들이 제 미모는 망치지 못합니다. 저는 끄떡없어요. 자, 덤벼라! 권투 상대가 필요하다면, 저한테 권투하라고 하십쇼. 제 머리통을 실컷 갈겨도 됩니다. 전 상관없어요. 콘월식이든 데번셔식이든 랭커셔식이든, 연습용으로 메다꽂을 가벼운 상대가 필요하다면, 저를 던지라고 하십쇼. 저는 안 다칩니다. 저는 평생토록 온갖 방식으로 메다꽂혀 봤거든요!\"\n\n이 뜻밖의 연설을 기운차게 늘어놓으며, 언급한 갖가지 운동을 흉내 내는 동작까지 곁들이며, 필 스쿼드는 사격장의 세 면을 어깨로 비집고 돌더니, 느닷없이 사령관 쪽으로 방향을 틀어 머리로 그를 들이받는 시늉을 하는데, 그건 충성을 표하려는 것이다. 그러고는 아침상을 치우기 시작한다.\n\n조지 씨는 유쾌하게 웃으며 그의 어깨를 두드려 준 다음, 정리를 거들고 사격장을 영업할 수 있게 갖추도록 돕는다. 그것이 끝나면 그는 아령을 한 차례 들고, 이어 체중을 재 보고는 자신이 \"너무 살이 찌고 있다\"고 진단하며 더없이 진지하게 혼자 검술 연습에 몰두한다. 그동안 필은 늘 쓰는 작업대에 들러붙어 나사를 조였다 풀었다 하고, 닦고, 줄질하고, 작은 구멍에 휘파람을 불어 넣고, 점점 더 새까매지며, 총에 관해 할 수 있는 일과 되돌릴 수 있는 일이라면 죄다 하고 또 되돌리는 듯하다.\n\n마침내 주인과 부하는 복도에서 들려오는 발소리에 방해받는데, 그 소리는 예사롭지 않게 들려 예사롭지 않은 손님의 도착을 알린다. 점점 사격장에 가까워지는 그 발소리는, 첫눈에 보기에 일 년 중 십일월 오일* 말고는 도무지 어울리지 않을 한 무리를 끌고 들어온다.\n* 십일월 오일: '가이 포크스의 날'. 1605년 의회 폭파 음모(화약 음모 사건)가 발각된 것을 기념해 영국에서 모닥불을 피우고 불꽃놀이를 하며 가이 포크스의 인형을 태우는 날.\n\n그 무리는 가마꾼 둘이 의자에 메고 온 흐물흐물하고 추한 형체 하나와, 그를 시중드는, 쪼그라든 가면 같은 얼굴의 깡마른 여자 하나로 이루어져 있다. 그 여자는 이제라도 당장, 그들이 늙은 잉글랜드를 산 채로 날려 버리려 꾸몄던 그 시절을 기념하는 그 유명한 시구를 읊을 듯하건만, 의자가 내려놓이는 순간까지 입술을 굳게 다물고 도전적으로 다물고 있어 그러질 않는다. 그 대목에서 의자에 앉은 형체가 헐떡인다. \"오, 맙소사! 아이고, 이런! 흔들려서 죽겠네!\" 그러고는 덧붙인다. \"잘 지냈나, 친애하는 벗이여, 잘 지냈어?\" 그제야 조지 씨는 그 행렬 속에서, 바람 쐬러 나온 그 존귀하신 스몰위드 씨와, 그의 호위병 격으로 시중드는 손녀 주디를 알아본다.\n\n\"조지 씨, 친애하는 벗이여,\" 오는 길에 거의 목을 졸라 댄 가마꾼 하나의 목에서 오른팔을 떼며 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"잘 지냈나? 나를 보니 놀랐지, 친애하는 벗이여.\"\n\n\"댁의 그 친구를 도심에서 만났대도 이보다 더 놀라진 않았겠습니다.\" 조지 씨가 대답한다.\n\n\"나는 좀처럼 밖에 안 나오지.\" 스몰위드 씨가 헐떡인다. \"몇 달째 나오질 않았어. 불편하기도 하고—비용도 들고. 하지만 자네가 어찌나 보고 싶던지, 친애하는 조지 씨. 잘 지냈나, 선생?\"\n\n\"그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"당신도 마찬가지이길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"자넨 아무리 잘 지내도 모자라지, 친애하는 벗이여.\" 스몰위드 씨가 그의 두 손을 맞잡는다. \"내 손녀 주디를 데려왔네. 도저히 떼어 놓을 수가 있어야지. 자네가 어찌나 보고 싶다던지.\"\n\n\"흠! 무던하게도 잘 견디는군!\" 조지 씨가 중얼거린다.\n\n\"그래서 우리는 삯마차를 한 대 잡아 거기에 의자를 싣고, 모퉁이를 막 돌아서 나를 마차에서 들어내 의자에 앉히고, 친애하는 벗을 그의 일터에서 볼 수 있도록 여기까지 메고 왔지! 이 사람은,\" 목이 졸릴 뻔하여 숨통을 가다듬으며 물러나는 가마꾼을 가리키며 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"마차 마부야. 따로 줄 건 없어. 약속대로 삯에 포함된 거니까. 이 사람은,\" 다른 가마꾼이다. \"맥주 한 파인트 값으로 길거리에서 고용했네. 그게 이 펜스지. 주디, 이 사람한테 이 펜스 주거라. 친애하는 벗이여, 여기 자네 일꾼이 있는지 몰라서 그랬어, 알았더라면 이 사람을 쓸 필요도 없었을 텐데.\"\n\n스몰위드 영감은 상당히 겁에 질린 눈길과 반쯤 억눌린 \"오, 맙소사! 아이고, 이런!\"을 곁들여 필을 가리킨다. 겉으로 보면 그가 겁먹은 데에 까닭이 없는 것도 아니니, 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 이 유령 같은 형체를 난생처음 본 필이, 손에 총을 든 채 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 까마귀 종류의 추한 늙은 새 한 마리 잡아 떨어뜨리겠다고 작정한 명사수의 분위기를 한껏 풍기고 있는 까닭이다.\n\n\"주디, 얘야,\" 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"이 사람한테 이 펜스 주거라. 그가 한 일에 비하면 후한 거다.\"\n\n그 사람은 런던 서쪽 거리에서 절로 솟아나는 인간 곰팡이의 그 유별난 표본 가운데 하나로, 낡은 빨간 윗도리를 차려입고 말을 잡아 주거나 마차를 불러 주는 \"사명\"을 띤 자인데, 이 펜스를 도무지 기쁜 기색이라곤 없이 받아 들더니, 동전을 공중에 휙 던졌다가 손등으로 낚아챈 다음 물러난다.\n\n\"친애하는 조지 씨,\" 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"나를 불 가까이로 메고 가도록 좀 도와주겠나? 나는 불에 익숙하고, 이렇게 늙은 몸이라 금세 으슬으슬해지거든. 아이고, 이런!\"\n\n존귀하신 신사의 이 끝맺음 감탄사는, 스쿼드 씨가 마치 마신처럼 별안간 그를 의자째 번쩍 들어 화롯돌 위에 내려놓는 바람에 그의 입에서 튀어나온 것이다.\n\n\"오, 맙소사!\" 스몰위드 씨가 헐떡이며 말한다. \"아이고, 이런! 아이고, 별들이여! 친애하는 벗이여, 자네 일꾼은 참 힘이 세군—게다가 아주 잽싸고. 오, 맙소사, 정말 잽싸구먼! 주디, 나를 좀 뒤로 빼다오. 다리가 그슬리고 있어.\" 과연 그의 털양말 타는 냄새가 그 자리에 있는 모두의 코에 그것을 입증한다.\n\n상냥한 주디가 할아버지를 불에서 조금 뒤로 빼고, 늘 그러듯 그를 흔들어 추슬러 주고, 검은 벨벳 모자라는 끄개에 가려 그늘진 눈을 풀어 주자, 스몰위드 씨는 다시 \"아이고, 이런! 오, 맙소사!\" 하더니 둘레를 둘러보다 조지 씨의 눈길과 마주치자 다시 두 손을 내민다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여! 이렇게 만나다니 정말 기쁘군! 그래, 이게 자네 일터인가? 멋진 곳이야. 그림 같구먼. 여기선 뭐가 우발적으로 발사되는 일은 결코 없겠지, 안 그런가, 친애하는 벗이여?\" 스몰위드 영감이 몹시 불안해하며 덧붙인다.\n\n\"없죠, 없습니다. 그럴 염려는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 자네 일꾼 말이야. 그가—아이고, 이런!—마음먹지 않고서 뭘 발사하는 일은 결코 없겠지, 안 그런가, 친애하는 벗이여?\"\n\n\"그는 자기 자신 말고는 누굴 다치게 한 적이 없습니다.\" 조지 씨가 미소 지으며 말한다.\n\n\"하지만 다치게 할 수도 있지 않나. 자기 몸은 꽤나 많이 다친 것 같으니, 남도 다치게 할 수 있겠어.\" 노신사가 대꾸한다. \"마음먹지 않고서도—아니, 마음먹고서라도 말이야. 조지 씨, 그더러 그 망할 총기는 좀 내버려 두고 저리 가 있으라고 명령해 주겠나?\"\n\n기병의 고갯짓에 따라, 필은 빈손으로 사격장 저쪽 끝으로 물러난다. 안심한 스몰위드 씨는 다리를 문지르기 시작한다.\n\n\"그래, 조지 씨, 일은 잘되고 있나?\" 그가 손에 폭넓은 검을 든 채 자기 쪽으로 똑바로 돌아서 있는 기병에게 말한다. \"신의 가호로, 번창하고 있나?\"\n\n조지 씨는 냉랭하게 고갯짓으로 대답하며 덧붙인다. \"어서 말하시죠. 그 말 하러 오신 게 아닌 줄은 압니다.\"\n\n\"자넨 참 활기가 넘쳐, 조지 씨.\" 존귀하신 할아버지가 대답한다. \"자넨 정말 함께 있기 좋은 사람이야.\"\n\n\"하하! 어서 말하시죠!\" 조지 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여! 그런데 그 검은 무섭도록 번쩍이고 날카로워 보이는군. 우발적으로 누굴 벨 수도 있겠어. 오싹해지는구먼, 조지 씨. 빌어먹을 놈!\" 기병이 검을 치워 두려고 한두 걸음 물러서자, 이 훌륭하신 노신사가 주디에게 따로 속삭인다. \"저놈은 나한테 빚이 있어서, 이 살벌한 데서 묵은 셈을 갚을 생각을 할지도 모른다. 네 그 망할 할미가 여기 있었으면, 저놈이 그 할미 머리를 밀어 버릴 텐데.\"\n\n조지 씨는 돌아와 팔짱을 끼고, 의자에서 시시각각 점점 더 아래로 미끄러져 내리는 노인을 내려다보며 조용히 말한다. \"자, 시작해 보시죠!\"\n\n\"호!\" 스몰위드 씨가 교활하게 킥킥거리며 두 손을 비빈다. \"그래. 시작해 보자고. 뭘 시작해 보자는 거지, 친애하는 벗이여?\"\n\n\"파이프를요.\" 조지 씨가 말하더니, 더없이 침착하게 의자를 굴뚝 모퉁이에 놓고, 화격자에서 파이프를 집어 담배를 채워 불을 붙이고는, 평온하게 담배를 피우기 시작한다.\n\n이것은 스몰위드 씨를 당혹스럽게 만드는 데 보탬이 되는데, 그는 제 목적이 무엇이든 그것을 다시 꺼내기가 어찌나 어려운지, 약이 바짝 올라, 조지 씨의 얼굴을 갈가리 찢어발기고 싶은 강렬한 욕구를 드러내며 무력한 앙심으로 몰래 허공을 할퀸다. 이 훌륭하신 노신사의 손톱은 길고 납빛이며, 손은 비쩍 마르고 핏줄이 불거졌고, 눈은 푸르스름하고 축축한 데다, 그 위에 더해 그가 할퀴는 동안에도 의자에서 계속 미끄러져 내려 형체 없는 보따리로 폭삭 주저앉는 통에, 늘 보아 온 주디의 눈에도 어찌나 소름 끼치는 광경이 되는지, 그 젊은 처녀는 애정 이상의 무언가를 담은 열정으로 그에게 달려들어 그를 흔들어 추스르고, 몸 여기저기를, 특히 호신술에서 명치라 부를 그 부위를 두드리고 쑤셔 대니, 그가 비참한 고통 속에 도로 다지는 기계 같은 소리를 억지로 토해 낸다.\n\n이런 방법으로 주디가 그를 의자에 다시 똑바로 앉혀 놓자, 얼굴은 하얗게 코는 서리 맞은 듯한(그러면서도 여전히 할퀴는) 그가 보이고, 그녀는 시든 집게손가락을 내밀어 조지 씨의 등을 한 번 쿡 찌른다. 기병이 고개를 들자, 그녀는 존경하는 할아버지를 또 한 번 쿡 찌르더니, 이렇게 둘을 마주 보게 해 놓고는, 불을 뻣뻣하게 응시한다.\n\n\"그래, 그래! 호, 호! 으—으—으—윽!\" 스몰위드 영감이 분을 삼키며 이를 딱딱거린다. \"친애하는 벗이여!\" (여전히 할퀴며.)\n\n\"내 말해 두겠는데,\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"나하고 대화하고 싶으면 똑바로 말해야 합니다. 나는 거친 사람이라 빙빙 둘러서 말할 줄을 몰라요. 그럴 재주가 없습니다. 그만큼 영리하지가 못해요. 나한텐 안 맞습니다. 당신이 나를 빙글빙글 둘러쌀 때면,\" 기병이 다시 파이프를 입에 물며 말한다. \"젠장, 마치 숨이 막혀 죽을 것 같단 말입니다!\"\n\n그러고는 자신이 아직 숨 막혀 죽지 않았음을 확인하려는 듯, 넓은 가슴을 한껏 부풀린다.\n\n\"친근하게 한번 들러 보려고 오신 거라면,\" 조지 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"감사합니다. 잘 지내십니까? 이 안에 무슨 재산거리가 있나 보러 오신 거라면, 둘러보시죠. 환영입니다. 무언가 털어놓을 게 있으시면, 그냥 털어놓으시죠!\"\n\n활짝 핀 주디는 불에서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 할아버지를 유령처럼 한 번 쿡 찌른다.\n\n\"보십쇼! 저 애 생각도 같지 않습니까. 그런데 대체 저 처녀가 왜 사람답게 앉질 않는지,\" 조지 씨가 생각에 잠긴 눈을 주디에게 고정한 채 말한다. \"당최 알 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"저 애는 나를 시중들려고 내 곁을 지키는 거야, 선생.\" 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"나는 늙은이라네, 친애하는 조지 씨, 그래서 시중이 좀 필요해. 나이는 견딜 만하지. 나는 망할 앵무새가 아니거든\" (으르렁대며 무의식중에 방석을 찾으며) \"하지만 시중이 필요하다네, 친애하는 벗이여.\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 기병이 의자를 돌려 노인을 마주하며 대답한다. \"자, 그래서요?\"\n\n\"도심에 있는 내 친구가 말이야, 조지 씨, 자네 제자 하나하고 작은 거래를 했어.\"\n\n\"그랬습니까?\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"유감이군요.\"\n\n\"그렇다네, 선생.\" 스몰위드 영감이 다리를 문지른다. \"이제 어엿한 젊은 군인이지, 조지 씨, 카스톤이라는 이름의. 친구들이 나서서 명예롭게 죄다 갚아 줬어.\"\n\n\"그랬습니까?\" 조지 씨가 대꾸한다. \"도심에 계신 그 친구분께서 조언 한마디 들으실 마음이 있으실까요?\"\n\n\"있고말고, 친애하는 벗이여. 자네한테서라면.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분께 조언드리죠. 그쪽으로는 더 이상 거래하지 마시라고요. 거기선 더 뜯어낼 게 없습니다. 내가 아는 한, 그 젊은 신사는 완전히 멈춰 섰어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 친애하는 벗이여. 아니야, 아니, 조지 씨. 아니, 아니, 아니야, 선생.\" 스몰위드 영감이 비쩍 마른 다리를 교활하게 문지르며 항변한다. \"완전히 멈춰 선 건 아니라고 봐. 그 사람한텐 좋은 친구들이 있고, 봉급도 받을 만하고, 임관 자리를 팔면 받을 값도 있고, 소송에서 한몫 잡을 가망도 있고, 아내 덕 볼 가망도 있고—아, 그러고 보니, 조지 씨, 내 친구는 그 젊은 신사가 아직 뭔가 건질 만하다고 여길 것 같은데?\" 스몰위드 영감이 벨벳 모자를 추켜올리고 원숭이처럼 귀를 긁으며 말한다.\n\n파이프를 치워 두고 한 팔을 의자 등받이에 걸친 채 앉아 있던 조지 씨가, 대화가 흘러가는 방향이 영 마뜩잖다는 듯 오른발로 바닥을 통통 두드린다.\n\n\"한데 한 화제에서 다른 화제로 넘어가 보세.\" 스몰위드 씨가 다시 말한다. \"'대화에 활기를 더하기 위해' 라고, 익살꾼이라면 그러겠지. 넘어가 보자고, 조지 씨, 그 소위에서 그 대위로 말이야.\"\n\n\"이제 또 무슨 꿍꿍이입니까?\" 조지 씨가 콧수염을 기억하듯 쓰다듬다 말고 눈살을 찌푸리며 묻는다. \"무슨 대위 말입니까?\"\n\n\"우리 대위 말이야. 우리가 아는 그 대위. 호던 대위.\"\n\n\"오! 그거였군요?\" 조지 씨가 할아버지와 손녀가 둘 다 자신을 뚫어지게 바라보는 것을 보며 나직이 휘파람을 불며 말한다. \"거기 있었군요! 그래서요? 그게 뭐 어떻다는 겁니까? 자, 나는 더 이상 숨 막히긴 싫습니다. 말씀하시죠!\"\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여,\" 노인이 대답한다. \"어제 누가 나한테 의뢰를 했어—주디, 나를 좀 흔들어 다오!—어제 누가 그 대위에 관해 나한테 의뢰를 했는데, 내 생각엔 여전히 그 대위가 죽지 않았다는 거야.\"\n\n\"헛소리.\" 조지 씨가 한마디 한다.\n\n\"방금 뭐라고 했나, 친애하는 벗이여?\" 노인이 귀에 손을 갖다 대며 묻는다.\n\"헛소리라고요!\"\n\n\"호!\" 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"조지 씨, 내 생각이 어떤지는, 나한테 던져진 질문들과 그걸 묻는 이유들로 미루어 자네 스스로 판단할 수 있을 걸세. 자, 그 조회하는 변호사가 뭘 원할 것 같나?\"\n\n\"일감이겠죠.\" 조지 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"천만에!\"\n\n\"그럼 변호사일 리가 없죠.\" 조지 씨가 확고한 결심의 분위기로 팔짱을 끼며 말한다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여, 그는 변호사야, 그것도 유명한. 그는 호던 대위 필체의 글 조각을 좀 보고 싶어 해. 그걸 가지려는 게 아니야. 그저 보고서, 자기가 가진 어떤 글씨와 대조해 보려는 거지.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서, 조지 씨. 그가 마침 호던 대위에 관한, 그리고 그에 관해 줄 수 있는 어떤 정보에 관한 그 광고를 기억해 내고, 그걸 찾아본 다음 나를 찾아왔어—꼭 자네가 그랬듯이 말이야, 친애하는 벗이여. 악수 좀 하겠나? 그날 자네가 와 줘서 어찌나 기쁘던지! 자네가 안 왔더라면 이런 우정을 맺을 기회를 놓쳤을 거야!\"\n\n\"그래서요, 스몰위드 씨?\" 다소 뻣뻣하게 그 의식을 치르고 나서 조지 씨가 다시 말한다.\n\n\"나한테는 그런 게 없었어. 그 사람 서명 말고는 가진 게 없거든. 그놈한테 역병과 기근, 전쟁과 살인과 급사가 떨어지기를,\" 노인은 몇 안 되는 기도 기억 가운데 하나를 저주로 둔갑시키며, 성난 두 손으로 벨벳 모자를 우그러뜨리며 말한다. \"그놈 서명이라면 오십만 장은 될 거야, 아마! 그런데 자네는,\" 주디가 그의 그 볼링공 같은 머리에 모자를 다시 바로 씌워 주자, 그는 숨 가쁘게 부드러운 말투를 되찾으며 말한다. \"자네는, 친애하는 조지 씨, 그 목적에 맞을 편지나 서류를 갖고 있을 법하단 말이지. 그 필체로 쓰인 거라면 뭐든 그 목적에 맞을 거야.\"\n\n\"그 필체로 쓰인 글이라.\" 기병이 곰곰이 생각하며 말한다. \"어쩌면, 있을지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"더없이 친애하는 벗이여!\"\n\n\"어쩌면, 없을지도 모르고요.\"\n\n\"호!\" 스몰위드 영감이 풀이 죽어 말한다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 한 말이나 있다 해도, 영문을 모르고서야 탄약 하나 만들 만큼도 내보이지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"선생, 영문은 말해 줬잖나. 친애하는 조지 씨, 영문은 말해 줬다고.\"\n\n\"충분치 않습니다.\" 기병이 고개를 저으며 말한다. \"나는 더 알아야겠고, 그게 납득이 가야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 변호사한테 가 보겠나? 친애하는 벗이여, 가서 그 신사를 만나 보겠나?\" 스몰위드 영감이 해골 다리 같은 바늘이 달린 비쩍 마른 낡은 은시계를 꺼내며 재촉한다. \"내가 그 사람한테, 오늘 오전 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 들를 것 같다고 말해 뒀는데, 지금이 열 시 반이야. 가서 그 신사를 만나 보겠나, 조지 씨?\"\n\n\"흠!\" 그가 진지하게 말한다. \"그건 상관없습니다. 다만 이게 어째서 당신한테 그토록 중대한 일인지는 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"그에 관해 뭔가를 밝혀낼 가망이 있는 일이라면 죄다 나한테 중대하지. 그자가 우리 모두를 속이지 않았나? 우리 모두한테 어마어마한 돈을 빚지지 않았어? 나한테 중대하냐고? 그자에 관한 일이 나보다 누구한테 더 중대할 수 있겠나? 그렇다고,\" 스몰위드 영감이 목소리를 낮추며 말한다. \"친애하는 벗이여, 자네더러 뭘 누설하라는 건 아니야. 천만에. 갈 채비가 됐나, 친애하는 벗이여?\"\n\n\"예! 곧 가죠. 아무것도 약속하진 않습니다, 아시겠지만.\"\n\n\"그럼, 친애하는 조지 씨, 그럼.\"\n\n\"그리고 그게 어디든 그 장소까지 나를 공짜로 태워 주겠다는 말씀입니까?\" 조지 씨가 모자와 두툼한 무두질 가죽 장갑을 챙기며 묻는다.\n\n이 농담이 어찌나 스몰위드 씨의 비위를 간질이는지, 그는 불 앞에서 길고 나직하게 웃는다. 그러면서도 웃는 내내, 그는 마비된 어깨 너머로 조지 씨를 흘끔거리며, 그가 사격장 저쪽 끝의 수수한 찬장 자물쇠를 열고, 위쪽 선반들을 여기저기 둘러보다가, 마침내 종이 부스럭거리는 소리와 함께 무언가를 꺼내 접어서 품에 넣는 것을 열심히 지켜본다. 그러자 주디가 스몰위드 씨를 한 번 쿡 찌르고, 스몰위드 씨가 주디를 한 번 쿡 찌른다.\n\n\"준비됐습니다.\" 기병이 돌아오며 말한다. \"필, 이 노신사를 마차까지 메다 드려라, 가뿐히 다루고.\"\n\n\"오, 이런! 오, 맙소사! 잠깐 멈추게!\" 스몰위드 씨가 말한다. \"이 사람 너무 잽싸단 말이야! 조심해서 할 수 있겠나, 친애하는 양반?\"\n\n필은 대꾸 없이 의자와 그 짐을 움켜잡고 옆걸음으로 가는데, 이제 말문이 막힌 스몰위드 씨를 꽉 끌어안고서, 마치 이 노신사를 가장 가까운 화산까지 메고 가라는 흡족한 임무라도 받은 양 복도를 따라 내달린다. 하지만 그 짧은 위탁이 마차에서 끝나니, 그는 거기에 영감을 내려놓는다. 그러자 어여쁜 주디가 그 곁에 자리를 잡고, 의자는 지붕을 장식하며, 조지 씨는 마부석의 빈자리에 앉는다.\n\n조지 씨는 이따금 뒤쪽 창문으로 마차 안을 들여다볼 때 보이는 광경에 어안이 벙벙해지는데, 그 안에서는 험상궂은 주디가 늘 미동도 없고, 모자가 한쪽 눈을 덮은 노신사가 늘 자리에서 짚 더미 위로 미끄러져 내리며, 나머지 한쪽 눈으로 등이 덜컹거려 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 표정으로 그를 올려다보고 있다.\n\n조지 씨가 팔짱을 끼고 마부석에 앉아 가야 할 거리는 멀지 않다. 목적지가 링컨스인필즈이기 때문이다. 마부가 말을 세우자 조지 씨는 내려서 창 안을 들여다보며 말한다. \"아니, 토킹혼 씨가 당신 사람이라는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇다네, 친애하는 벗이여. 그 사람을 아시나, 조지 씨?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 소문은 들었소—본 적도 있는 것 같고. 하지만 난 그 사람을 모르고, 그 사람도 나를 모르오.\"\n\n이제 스몰위드 씨를 위층으로 옮기는 일이 이어지는데, 기병의 도움으로 완벽하게 처리된다. 그는 토킹혼 씨의 널찍한 방으로 옮겨져 벽난로 앞 터키 양탄자 위에 내려진다. 토킹혼 씨는 지금 자리에 없지만 곧 돌아올 것이다. 현관 칸막이 자리를 지키는 사내가 이 정도 말을 전한 다음 불을 휘저어 돋우고는, 세 사람이 몸을 녹이도록 내버려두고 떠난다.\n\n조지 씨는 그 방에 대해 자못 호기심이 인다. 그는 그림이 그려진 천장을 올려다보고, 낡은 법률 서적들을 둘러보고, 거물 의뢰인들의 초상화를 찬찬히 바라보고, 상자에 적힌 이름들을 소리 내어 읽는다.\n\n\"'레스터 데드록 경, 준남작.'\" 조지 씨가 골똘히 읽는다. \"허! '체스니 월드 영지.' 흠!\" 조지 씨는 마치 그것들이 그림이라도 되는 양 한참 동안 그 상자들을 바라보고 서 있다가, 벽난로 쪽으로 돌아오며 거듭 중얼거린다. \"레스터 데드록 경, 준남작에다 체스니 월드 영지라, 응?\"\n\n\"엄청난 돈줄이지요, 조지 씨!\" 스몰위드 영감이 다리를 문지르며 속삭인다. \"어마어마한 부자랍니다!\"\n\n\"누구 말이오? 이 늙은 양반이오, 아니면 그 준남작이오?\"\n\n\"이 양반 말입니다, 이 양반.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 들었소. 게다가 한두 가지쯤은 아는 사람이지, 내기를 걸어도 좋소. 거처도 나쁘지 않고.\" 조지 씨가 다시 둘러보며 말한다. \"저기 저 금고 좀 보시오!\"\n\n이 대답은 토킹혼 씨가 들어서면서 끊긴다. 물론 그에게는 아무런 변화가 없다. 낡아빠진 옷차림에, 안경을 손에 들었는데 그 안경집조차 닳고 닳아 올이 다 드러났다. 태도는 입이 무겁고 메마르다. 목소리는 쉰 듯 낮다. 얼굴은 가림막 뒤에서 살피는 듯한데, 평소에 비난과 경멸의 기색이 없다고는 할 수 없을 것이다. 모든 것이 다 알려진다면, 토킹혼 씨보다 귀족 계급을 더 뜨겁게 숭배하고 더 충실히 믿는 자들이 어쩌면 따로 있을지도 모른다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 스몰위드 씨, 안녕하시오!\" 그가 들어오며 말한다. \"하사관을 데려오셨군. 앉으시오, 하사관.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 장갑을 벗어 모자 안에 넣으면서, 반쯤 감은 눈으로 방 건너편에 서 있는 기병을 바라보며 어쩌면 속으로 이렇게 말하는지도 모른다. \"자네라면 쓸 만하겠어, 친구!\"\n\n\"앉으시오, 하사관.\" 그는 벽난로 한쪽에 놓인 탁자로 다가가 안락의자에 앉으며 다시 말한다. \"오늘 아침은 춥고 을씨년스럽구먼, 춥고 을씨년스러워!\" 토킹혼 씨는 난로 살창 앞에서 두 손바닥과 손등을 번갈아 녹이며, (언제나 내려져 있는 그 가림막 뒤에서) 자기 앞에 작은 반원을 그리며 앉은 세 사람을 바라본다.\n\n\"자, 이제 내가 무슨 일을 하는지 느낄 수 있겠소\" (어쩌면 두 가지 의미에서 그럴지도 모른다), \"스몰위드 씨.\" 노인은 대화에 한몫 끼도록 주디에게 다시 한 번 흔들려 추슬러진다. \"우리 좋은 벗 하사관을 데려오셨군그래.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\" 스몰위드 씨가 변호사의 재산과 권세에 한껏 굽실거리며 대답한다.\n\n\"그래, 하사관은 이 일에 대해 뭐라고 하오?\"\n\n\"조지 씨라는데,\" 스몰위드 영감이 쪼그라든 손을 떨며 흔들어 보이며 말한다. \"이 양반이 그 사람입니다, 어르신.\"\n\n조지 씨는 그 신사에게 경례를 붙이지만 그 밖에는 꼿꼿이 똑바로 앉아 깊이 침묵을 지킨다—마치 사열식에 필요한 규정 장비 일습을 온통 몸에 두르고 있기라도 한 듯, 의자에 잔뜩 당겨 앉은 채로.\n\n토킹혼 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"자, 조지—이름이 조지가 맞지?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"어떻게 하겠소, 조지?\"\n\n\"실례지만, 어르신,\" 기병이 대꾸한다. \"저야말로 어르신께서 어떻게 하실 작정인지 알고 싶습니다만?\"\n\n\"보상 문제를 말하는 거요?\"\n\n\"모든 문제를 말하는 겁니다, 어르신.\"\n\n이 말이 스몰위드 씨의 비위를 어찌나 거스르는지, 그는 별안간 \"이 유황 같은 짐승 놈아!\" 하고 터뜨리고는 또 별안간 토킹혼 씨에게 용서를 구하더니, 이 말실수를 변명한답시고 주디에게 이렇게 말한다. \"네 할미를 생각하고 있었느니라, 얘야.\"\n\n\"내 생각에는, 하사관,\" 토킹혼 씨가 의자 한쪽에 기대어 다리를 꼬며 말을 잇는다. \"스몰위드 씨가 그 일을 충분히 설명해 두었을 듯한데. 어쨌든 아주 간단한 일이오. 자네는 한때 호든 대위 밑에서 복무했고, 그가 병들었을 때 시중을 들었고, 자잘한 일을 여러 가지 도와주었고, 그의 신임을 꽤 받았다고 들었소. 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 어르신, 그렇습니다.\" 조지 씨가 군인답게 짤막하게 말한다.\n\n\"그렇다면 자네가 무언가를 가지고 있을 법한데—무엇이든, 뭐든 상관없소. 장부든, 지시서든, 명령서든, 편지든, 무엇이든—호든 대위가 직접 쓴 것 말이오. 나는 그의 필적을 내가 가진 어떤 것과 대조해 보고 싶소. 그 기회를 줄 수 있다면, 수고에 대해 보상하리다. 삼, 사, 오 기니쯤이면 후하다고 여기겠지, 아마.\"\n\n\"통이 크시군요, 친애하는 벗이여!\" 스몰위드 영감이 두 눈을 가늘게 모으며 외친다.\n\n\"그게 아니라면, 군인의 양심을 걸고 얼마를 더 요구할 수 있는지 말해 보시오. 내키지 않는다면 그 필적을 굳이 넘기지 않아도 좋소—나로서야 받는 편이 더 좋겠지만.\"\n\n조지 씨는 똑같은 자세 그대로 네모반듯하게 앉아 그림 그려진 천장을 바라보며 한마디도 하지 않는다. 성마른 스몰위드 씨가 허공을 긁어댄다.\n\n\"문제는 말이오,\" 토킹혼 씨가 그 조리 있고 가라앉은, 무심한 투로 말한다. \"첫째, 자네가 호든 대위의 필적을 조금이라도 가지고 있느냐 하는 거요.\"\n\n\"첫째, 제가 호든 대위의 필적을 조금이라도 가지고 있느냐, 어르신.\" 조지 씨가 따라 말한다.\n\n\"둘째, 그것을 내놓는 수고의 대가로 무엇이면 만족하겠소?\"\n\n\"둘째, 그것을 내놓는 수고의 대가로 무엇이면 만족하겠느냐, 어르신.\" 조지 씨가 따라 말한다.\n\n\"셋째, 이게 그것과 조금이라도 비슷한지는 자네 스스로 판단할 수 있을 거요.\" 토킹혼 씨가 별안간 한데 묶은 종이 몇 장을 그에게 건네며 말한다.\n\n\"이게 그것과 조금이라도 비슷한지, 어르신. 바로 그렇군요.\" 조지 씨가 따라 말한다.\n\n이 세 번의 따라 말하기를 조지 씨는 토킹혼 씨를 똑바로 쳐다보며 기계적인 투로 내뱉는다. 검토하라고 그에게 건네진 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송의 진술서를 (여전히 손에 들고 있으면서도) 흘끗 보지도 않은 채, 그저 곤혹스럽게 골똘한 기색으로 변호사를 계속 바라보기만 한다.\n\n\"그래서?\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"어떻게 하겠소?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 어르신,\" 조지 씨가 똑바로 일어서서 거대해 보이는 모습으로 대답한다. \"외람되지만, 저는 차라리 이 일에 손대지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 겉으로는 조금도 흔들림 없이 묻는다. \"어째서?\"\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 어르신,\" 기병이 대꾸한다. \"군의 강제 명령이라면 모를까, 저는 사무에 밝은 사람이 아닙니다. 민간인들 사이에서 저는 스코틀랜드 말로 이른바 건달이올시다. 저는 서류라면 머리가 안 돌아갑니다, 어르신. 추궁의 포화보다는 그 어떤 포화 속이라도 더 잘 견딜 수 있습니다. 한 시간쯤 전에 스몰위드 씨에게도 말씀드렸는데, 저는 이런 일에 끼어들면 숨이 막히는 것만 같습니다. 그리고 그게 바로 지금 이 순간 제 심정입니다.\" 조지 씨가 좌중을 둘러보며 말한다.\n\n그러고는 성큼성큼 세 걸음 앞으로 나아가 그 서류를 변호사의 탁자에 도로 놓고, 다시 성큼성큼 세 걸음 뒤로 물러나 원래 자리로 돌아가서, 거기에 완벽하게 똑바로 서서, 다른 어떤 서류든 받아들이지 못하게 막으려는 듯 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채, 한 번은 바닥을, 한 번은 그림 그려진 천장을 번갈아 바라본다.\n\n이런 약 올림에 스몰위드 씨가 즐겨 쓰는 멸시의 형용사가 혀끝까지 어찌나 바싹 차올랐는지, 그는 \"친애하는 벗이여\"라는 말을 \"유\" 한 음절로 시작하는 바람에 소유의 호칭을 \"유애하는\"으로 바꿔 버려, 말을 더듬는 것처럼 보인다. 그러나 이 난관을 넘기고 나자, 그는 더없이 다정한 투로 친애하는 벗에게 경솔하게 굴지 말고, 저토록 훌륭한 양반이 요구하는 바를, 그것도 흔쾌한 태도로 들어주라고 권하면서, 그것이 분명 흠잡을 데 없는 데다 돈벌이도 될 것이라고 호언한다. 토킹혼 씨는 이따금 한 마디씩만 던질 뿐이다. 이를테면, \"자기 이익이야 자네가 제일 잘 판단할 테지, 하사관.\" \"이 일로 해를 입지 않도록 조심하시오.\" \"마음대로 하시오, 마음대로.\" \"자네 말뜻을 자네가 안다면, 그걸로 충분하오.\" 이런 말들을, 그는 탁자 위의 서류를 들여다보며 편지를 쓸 채비를 하면서, 더없이 무심한 기색으로 내뱉는다.\n\n조지 씨는 그림 그려진 천장에서 바닥으로, 바닥에서 스몰위드 씨에게로, 스몰위드 씨에게서 토킹혼 씨에게로, 토킹혼 씨에게서 다시 그림 그려진 천장으로 미심쩍은 눈길을 옮기며, 곤혹스러운 나머지 몸을 지탱하는 다리를 자주 바꾼다.\n\n\"정말이지 말씀드리는데, 어르신,\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"기분 상하시라고 드리는 말은 아닙니다만, 어르신과 여기 이 스몰위드 씨 사이에서 저는 정말 쉰 번도 더 숨이 막히는 것 같습니다. 정말입니다, 어르신. 저는 두 분 같은 양반들의 상대가 못 됩니다. 한 가지 여쭤도 될까요? 제가 그 대위의 필적을 무어라도 찾아낸다 치고, 대체 그걸 어디에 쓰시려는 겁니까?\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 조용히 고개를 가로젓는다. \"안 되오. 자네가 사무에 밝은 사람이라면, 내가 속한 직업에서는 그 자체로는 지극히 무해하지만 비밀로 해야 하는 사정이 그런 요구에 흔히 따른다는 걸 굳이 일러줄 필요도 없을 거요. 하지만 호든 대위에게 무슨 해라도 끼칠까 두려운 거라면, 그 점은 마음 놓아도 좋소.\"\n\n\"아! 그분은 돌아가셨습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 토킹혼 씨가 조용히 자리에 앉아 글을 쓴다.\n\n\"그런데 말입니다, 어르신,\" 기병이 또 한 번 당황스러운 침묵 끝에 자기 모자 속을 들여다보며 말한다. \"더 흡족하게 해 드리지 못해 송구합니다. 만약 누구라도, 제가 이 일에 손대지 않는 편이 낫다는 제 판단을—저보다 사무에 밝고 게다가 늙은 군인인 제 벗 하나에게서—확인받는 것이 흡족하다면야, 기꺼이 그와 의논해 보겠습니다. 저는—저는 정말이지 지금 이렇게 완전히 숨이 막혀서,\" 조지 씨가 절망스럽게 이마를 손으로 쓸어내리며 말한다. \"그게 저로서도 흡족할지 어떨지조차 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n이 권위자라는 사람이 늙은 군인이라는 말을 듣자, 스몰위드 씨는 기병이 그와 의논하는 것이 마땅하다고 어찌나 강하게 주입하는지, 더구나 이것이 오 기니, 아니 그 이상이 걸린 문제라는 점을 특별히 일러주는 통에, 조지 씨는 그를 만나러 가겠노라 약속한다. 토킹혼 씨는 이러쿵저러쿵 한마디도 하지 않는다.\n\n\"그럼 어르신의 허락을 얻어 제 벗과 의논해 보겠습니다.\" 기병이 말한다. \"그리고 오늘 중으로 최종 답변을 가지고 외람되나마 다시 들르겠습니다. 스몰위드 씨, 아래층으로 옮겨 드릴까요—\"\n\n\"잠깐만, 친애하는 벗이여, 잠깐만. 우선 이 양반과 단둘이 반 마디만 나누게 해 주겠소?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 어르신. 저 때문에 서두르지 마시지요.\" 기병은 방 저편 멀찍한 곳으로 물러나 튼튼한 것이든 아니든 그 상자들을 다시 호기심 어린 눈으로 살펴본다.\n\n\"제가 유황 갓난애처럼 이렇게 기운이 없지만 않다면 말입니다, 어르신,\" 스몰위드 영감이 변호사의 외투 깃을 잡고 제 눈높이까지 끌어내리며, 성난 두 눈에서 반쯤 꺼진 녹색 불꽃을 번득이며 속삭인다. \"그자한테서 그 필적을 뜯어내고 말 텐데. 그자는 그걸 가슴팍에 단추로 채워 넣어 두었어요. 내가 거기 넣는 걸 봤습니다. 주디도 거기 넣는 걸 봤고요. 어디 말해 보아라, 이 지팡이 가게 간판으로나 쓸 심술궂은 형상아, 그자가 거기 넣는 걸 봤다고 말이다!\" 노인은 이 격렬한 호소에 손녀를 어찌나 세게 떠미는지, 그 힘이 제 몸에 부쳐 의자에서 미끄러져 내리며 토킹혼 씨까지 함께 끌고 내려가다가, 주디에게 붙들려 호되게 흔들리고서야 멈춘다.\n\n\"폭력은 나한테 안 통하오, 벗이여.\" 그러자 토킹혼 씨가 차갑게 한마디 한다.\n\n\"네, 네, 압니다, 알지요, 어르신. 하지만 속이 타고 부아가 치밀어서—그러니까—그자가 필요한 걸 가지고도 안 내놓는다는 걸 아는 게, 떠벌이며 지껄여대는 까치 같은 당신네 할미보다도 더 고약하단 말이오,\" 그는 그저 불만 바라보고 있는 태연한 주디에게 이렇게 쏘아붙이고는 말을 잇는다. \"그걸 안 내놔! 그자가! 그놈이! 부랑자 주제에! 하지만 괜찮습니다, 어르신, 괜찮아요. 길어 봤자 그자는 잠깐 제멋대로 굴 뿐이지. 내가 주기적으로 그놈을 죔쇠에 물려 둔 셈이니까. 비틀어 버리겠소, 어르신. 나사처럼 조여 버리겠소, 어르신. 흔쾌히 안 하겠다면, 마지못해서라도 하게 만들고 말겠소, 어르신! 자, 친애하는 조지 씨,\" 스몰위드 영감이 변호사를 놓아주며 그에게 흉측하게 윙크를 보내고 말한다. \"이제 자네의 친절한 도움을 받을 준비가 됐네, 나의 훌륭한 벗이여!\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 침착함 사이로 어렴풋한 즐거움의 기색을 비치며, 벽난로 깔개 위에 불을 등지고 서서, 스몰위드 씨가 사라지는 것을 지켜보며, 기병이 작별하며 붙이는 경례에 가벼운 목례 한 번으로 답한다.\n\n조지 씨가 보니, 이 노인을 떼어내는 일이 그를 아래층으로 옮기는 일을 거드는 것보다 더 까다롭다. 운반 도구에 도로 앉혀 놓자 노인은 기니 이야기를 어찌나 수다스럽게 늘어놓고 또 그의 단추를 어찌나 애틋하게 붙잡고 늘어지는지—사실은 그의 외투를 찢어 열고 털어내고픈 은밀한 갈망을 품은 채—기병으로서는 어느 정도 힘을 써야만 떼어낼 수 있을 지경이다. 마침내 그 일을 해내고, 그는 홀로 자기 조언자를 찾아 나선다.\n\n회랑이 늘어선 템플을 지나고, 화이트프라이어스를 지나(거기서 행잉소드 골목을 한번 흘끗 보는데, 그 골목이 그의 진로상 무언가 의미가 있는 듯하다), 블랙프라이어스 다리와 블랙프라이어스 길을 지나, 조지 씨는 묵직한 발걸음으로 차분히 행진해 자그마한 가게들이 늘어선 어느 거리로 간다. 그 거리는 켄트와 서리에서 오는 길들과 런던의 다리들에서 오는 거리들이 한데 얽혀드는 그 길목 어딘가에 있는데, 그 한복판에는 그 유명한 코끼리가 버티고 있다. 한때 사두마차 천 대로 이루어진 제 성채를 자랑하던 그 코끼리는, 이제 저보다 더 힘센 무쇠 괴물에게 그 성채를 빼앗기고 말았으니, 그 괴물은 코끼리가 감히 덤비기만 하면 언제든 잘게 다져 버릴 태세다. 이 거리에 늘어선 작은 가게들 가운데 하나, 곧 진열창에 바이올린 몇 대와 팬파이프 몇 개, 탬버린 하나, 트라이앵글 하나, 그리고 길쭉길쭉한 악보 조각 몇 점을 늘어놓은 어느 악기점으로, 조지 씨는 그 묵직한 발걸음을 옮긴다. 그러고는 거기서 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에 멈춰 서는데, 군인다워 보이는 한 여인이 겉치마를 걷어 올린 채 작은 나무통을 들고 나와, 보도 가장자리에서 그 통에 든 것을 휘젓고 첨벙거리기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 조지 씨가 혼잣말을 한다. \"여전하군, 푸성귀를 씻고 있어. 짐마차 위에 있을 때 빼고는, 저 사람이 푸성귀 안 씻는 꼴을 본 적이 없다니까!\"\n\n이 상념의 주인공은 어쨌거나 지금 푸성귀 씻는 일에 어찌나 골몰해 있는지, 조지 씨가 다가오는 것을 전혀 눈치채지 못하다가, 물을 도랑에 따라 버리고 통과 함께 몸을 일으키면서야 그가 가까이 서 있는 것을 알아챈다. 그를 맞이하는 태도는 살가운 구석이 없다.\n\n\"조지, 난 당신을 볼 때마다 차라리 백 마일은 멀리 떨어져 있었으면 싶다니까!\"\n\n기병은 이 인사에 대해 아무런 토를 달지 않고 그녀를 따라 악기점 안으로 들어가는데, 부인은 푸성귀 통을 계산대 위에 올려놓고 그와 악수를 나눈 다음 그 위에 두 팔을 괸다.\n\n\"난 말이야, 조지,\" 그녀가 말한다. \"당신이 곁에 있으면 단 일 분도 매슈 배그닛이 안전하다고 여겨지질 않아. 당신이 그렇게나 좀이 쑤시고 떠돌이 기질이라서—\"\n\n\"맞소! 나도 알아요, 배그닛 부인. 나도 알지요.\"\n\n\"안다면서!\" 배그닛 부인이 말한다. \"그게 무슨 소용이람? 대체 왜 그런 거야?\"\n\n\"타고난 짐승 본성이겠지요, 아마.\" 기병이 너스레 떨며 대꾸한다.\n\n\"흥!\" 배그닛 부인이 다소 날카롭게 외친다. \"그 짐승이 내 매트를 꾀어내서 악기 장사를 때려치우고 뉴질랜드니 오스트레일리아니로 데려가 버린다면, 그 짐승 본성이라는 게 나한테 무슨 위안이 되겠어?\"\n\n배그닛 부인은 결코 못생긴 여자가 아니다. 골격이 다소 굵고, 살결이 조금 거칠고, 햇볕과 바람에 그을려 주근깨가 돋고 이마께 머리칼이 누렇게 탔지만, 건강하고 정정하고 눈매가 또랑또랑하다. 마흔다섯에서 쉰쯤 된, 야무지고 부지런하고 활기차고 정직한 얼굴의 여인이다. 깔끔하고 다부지고, 또 어찌나 알뜰하게(그러면서도 야무지게) 차려입었는지, 그녀가 지닌 장신구라곤 결혼반지 하나뿐인 듯한데, 그것을 끼운 뒤로 손가락이 어찌나 굵어졌는지 이제 그 반지는 배그닛 부인의 유골과 뒤섞일 때까지 결코 빠지지 않을 것이다.\n\n\"배그닛 부인,\" 기병이 말한다. \"나는 당신에게 맹세코 약속하리다. 매트는 나 때문에 아무 해도 입지 않을 거요. 그 점만은 나를 믿어도 좋소.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 믿어도 될 것 같긴 해. 하지만 당신은 보고만 있어도 마음이 들뜬다니까.\" 배그닛 부인이 받아친다. \"아, 조지, 조지! 당신이 북아메리카에서 조 파우치가 죽었을 때 그 과부랑 결혼해서 진작 자리나 잡았더라면, 그 여자가 당신 머리를 곱게 빗겨 줬을 텐데.\"\n\n\"확실히 나한테 기회이긴 했지요.\" 기병이 반은 웃으며 반은 진지하게 대꾸한다. \"하지만 난 이제 점잖은 사내로 자리 잡을 일은 영영 없을 거요. 조 파우치의 과부가 나한테 잘해 줬을지도 모르지요—그 여자한테 뭔가가 있었고, 또 그 여자다운 무언가도 있었으니까—하지만 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없었소. 내가 매트가 만난 것 같은 아내를 만나는 행운이라도 누렸더라면 모를까!\"\n\n배그닛 부인은 사람 좋은 사내한테는 순박한 식으로 별로 격을 차리지 않는 듯하지만, 그 점에서는 자기야말로 또 한 명의 사람 좋은 사내인지라, 이 칭찬에 답한답시고 푸성귀 한 포기로 조지 씨의 얼굴을 탁 치고는 통을 들고 가게 뒤편의 작은 방으로 들어간다.\n\n\"이런, 퀘벡, 우리 귀염둥이,\" 조지가 청을 받고 그 방으로 따라 들어가며 말한다. \"그리고 우리 꼬마 몰타도! 이리 와서 너희 블러피한테 뽀뽀해 주렴!\"\n\n이 어린 아가씨들은—실제로 그렇게 세례명을 받은 건 아닌 듯하지만, 막사에서 태어난 곳을 따 집안에서는 늘 그렇게 불린다—각각 다리 셋 달린 걸상에 앉아 있는데, 동생은(대여섯 살쯤) 한 푼짜리 입문서로 글자를 배우는 중이고, 언니는(아마 여덟이나 아홉 살) 동생을 가르치며 부지런히 바느질을 하는 중이다. 둘 다 조지 씨를 오랜 벗으로 반기며 환호하고, 한바탕 뽀뽀하고 뒹굴며 놀고 나서는 걸상을 그의 곁에 갖다 놓는다.\n\n\"그래, 어린 울위치는 어떻게 지내요?\" 조지 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"아! 그러게 말이야!\" 배그닛 부인이 (저녁을 짓고 있던 터라) 냄비들에서 몸을 돌리며, 얼굴에 환한 홍조를 띠고 외친다. \"믿어지겠어? 글쎄 극장에서 제 아비랑 함께 군악극에서 횡적을 부는 일자리를 얻었지 뭐야.\"\n\n\"잘했구나, 내 대자!\" 조지 씨가 제 허벅지를 탁 치며 외친다.\n\n\"내 말이 그 말이라니까!\" 배그닛 부인이 말한다. \"걘 영국인이야. 울위치가 바로 그런 애지. 영국인!\"\n\n\"그리고 매트는 바순을 불어대고, 당신네는 너나없이 점잖은 민간인들이고,\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"어엿한 가정을 이루고. 아이들은 무럭무럭 자라고. 스코틀랜드에 있는 매트의 노모와, 어딘가에 있는 당신네 노부와도 편지를 주고받고 조금씩 도와드리고, 그리고—그래, 그래! 정말이지, 내가 백 마일 멀리 떨어져 있었으면 좋겠다는 소리를 들어도 할 말이 없지, 난 이 모든 것과는 별 인연이 없으니까!\"\n\n조지 씨는 점점 생각에 잠겨든다. 모래를 깔아 놓은 바닥에 막사 냄새가 풍기고, 군더더기라곤 하나도 없으며, 퀘벡과 몰타의 얼굴부터 찬장 선반 위의 반들거리는 양철 냄비며 작은 통에 이르기까지 눈에 띄는 먼지나 티끌 한 점 없는, 회칠한 그 방에서 벽난로 앞에 앉아—조지 씨는 점점 생각에 잠겨든다. 배그닛 부인이 바쁘게 일하는 동안 그가 여기 앉아 있을 때, 배그닛 씨와 어린 울위치가 마침맞게 집으로 돌아온다. 배그닛 씨는 퇴역 포병으로, 키가 크고 꼿꼿하며, 텁수룩한 눈썹에 야자열매 섬유 같은 구레나룻을 길렀고, 머리에는 머리카락 한 올 없으며, 얼굴빛이 이글거리듯 검붉다. 그의 목소리는 짧고 굵고 우렁찬데, 그가 애지중지하는 악기 소리와 조금도 다르지 않다. 실로 그에게서는 대체로 강직하고 굽힐 줄 모르는, 놋쇠로 둘러싼 듯한 분위기가 풍기는데, 마치 그 자신이 인간 관현악단의 바순이기라도 한 것 같다. 어린 울위치는 어린 고수의 전형이요 본보기다.\n\n아비와 아들 둘 다 기병을 진심으로 반긴다. 그가 때맞춰 배그닛 씨와 의논하러 왔다고 말하자, 배그닛 씨는 저녁 식사 전에는 무슨 일이든 듣지 않겠노라, 또 자기 벗이 삶은 돼지고기와 푸성귀부터 먹지 않고서는 자기 조언도 들을 수 없노라 손님 대접하듯 선언한다. 기병이 이 청에 응하자, 그와 배그닛 씨는 살림 채비에 방해되지 않도록 그 자그마한 거리를 한 바퀴 오르내리러 나가서, 마치 거기가 성벽이라도 되는 양 팔짱을 끼고 절도 있는 걸음걸이로 거닌다.\n\n\"조지,\" 배그닛 씨가 말한다. \"자네는 날 알지. 조언은 내 마누라가 하는 거야. 머리는 그 사람이 돌아가. 하지만 난 그 사람 앞에서는 절대 그걸 인정 안 해. 규율은 지켜야 하니까. 푸성귀가 그 사람 머릿속에서 떠날 때까지 기다리게. 그런 다음에 의논하지. 마누라가 뭐라고 하든, 따르게—그대로 따라!\"\n\n\"그럴 작정이야, 매트.\" 상대가 대답한다. \"난 대학교 의견보다 그 사람 의견을 더 따르고 싶으니까.\"\n\n\"대학교라.\" 배그닛 씨가 바순처럼 짧은 문장으로 대꾸한다. \"어떤 대학교가—세상 반대편 어느 구석에서—회색 외투 한 벌과 우산 하나만 달랑 들고—제 힘으로 유럽 집까지 찾아올 수 있겠나? 우리 마누라라면 당장 내일이라도 해낼걸. 실제로 한 번 했고!\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞아.\" 조지 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"어떤 대학교가,\" 배그닛이 말을 잇는다. \"사람 인생을 일으켜 세울 수 있겠나—두 푼어치 백회 가루랑—한 푼어치 풀러스 흙이랑—반 푼어치 모래랑—그러고도 육 펜스에서 남은 잔돈만 가지고? 우리 마누라가 그걸로 시작했어. 지금 이 장사를.\"\n\n\"잘되고 있다니 기쁜걸, 매트.\"\n\n\"우리 마누라는,\" 배그닛 씨가 수긍하며 말한다. \"저축을 해. 어딘가에 양말짝을 하나 두고 있어. 그 안에 돈이 들었지. 난 본 적 없어. 하지만 그게 있다는 건 알지. 푸성귀가 그 사람 머릿속에서 떠날 때까지 기다리게. 그런 다음에 자넬 일으켜 줄 테니.\"\n\n\"보물 같은 분이로군!\" 조지 씨가 외친다.\n\n\"그 이상이지. 하지만 난 그 사람 앞에서는 절대 인정 안 해. 규율은 지켜야 하니까. 내 음악 재주를 끌어내 준 게 우리 마누라야. 마누라가 아니었으면 난 지금도 포병대에 있었을걸. 육 년을 바이올린에 매달렸어. 십 년을 플루트에. 마누라가 그건 안 되겠다더군. 마음은 좋은데 융통성이 부족하다고. 바순을 해 보라더라고. 마누라가 소총 연대 군악대장한테서 바순을 빌려 왔어. 난 참호 속에서 연습했지. 늘었고, 또 한 대 더 구하고, 그걸로 먹고살게 됐어!\"\n\n조지가 부인은 장미처럼 싱싱하고 사과처럼 탱탱해 보인다고 한마디 한다.\n\n\"우리 마누라는,\" 배그닛 씨가 답으로 말한다. \"철두철미 훌륭한 여자야. 그러니 철두철미 화창한 날 같지. 나이 들수록 더 화창해져. 우리 마누라만 한 사람은 본 적이 없어. 하지만 난 그 사람 앞에서는 절대 인정 안 해. 규율은 지켜야 하니까!\"\n\n이런저런 시답잖은 일들을 이야기하며 그들은 보조와 박자를 맞춰 그 자그마한 거리를 오르내리다가, 마침내 퀘벡과 몰타에게 불려 돼지고기와 푸성귀를 정당하게 대접받으러 가는데, 그 음식을 앞에 두고 배그닛 부인은 군목처럼 짤막한 식전 기도를 올린다. 이 음식을 나누어 줄 때도, 다른 모든 집안일에서와 마찬가지로, 배그닛 부인은 빈틈없는 체계를 발휘하여, 모든 접시를 제 앞에 늘어놓고, 돼지고기 한 토막 한 토막에 각자 몫의 육수와 푸성귀와 감자, 심지어 겨자까지 배정하여 완전하게 차려서 내놓는다. 마찬가지로 깡통에서 맥주까지 따라 돌려 식구들에게 필요한 모든 것을 갖추어 준 다음에야, 배그닛 부인은 비로소 제 허기를 채우는데, 그 허기는 건강한 상태다. 식탁 살림이라 부를 만한 것들은—식탁에 차린 살림이 그렇게 불릴 수 있다면—주로 뿔과 양철로 만든 도구들로, 세계 여러 곳에서 한몫씩 해 온 물건들이다. 특히 어린 울위치의 칼은 굴 까는 종류에다 한 가지 특징이 더해진 것인데, 강하게 접히는 동작 때문에 그 어린 악사의 식욕을 자주 좌절시키는 그 칼은, 여러 손을 거쳐 외국 복무지를 빠짐없이 돌고 돌아온 물건이라는 이야기가 나온다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나자, 배그닛 부인은 어린 가지들의 (제 잔과 접시며 칼과 포크를 직접 닦는) 도움을 받아, 식탁의 모든 장식거리를 전과 다름없이 환하게 반짝이게 닦아 죄다 치워 놓는데, 배그닛 씨와 손님이 파이프 피우는 일에 지장이 없도록 먼저 벽난로 앞부터 쓸어낸다. 이런 집안일에는 뒷마당에서 나막신을 신고 오가는 일이 잔뜩 따르고 물통도 상당히 쓰이는데, 그 물통은 마침내 배그닛 부인 자신의 몸단장을 거드는 영광까지 누린다. 그 마누라가 이윽고 아주 산뜻한 모습으로 다시 나타나 바느질거리를 들고 자리에 앉고서야, 바로 그제야—그제야 비로소 푸성귀가 그녀의 머릿속에서 완전히 떠났다고 여길 수 있으므로—배그닛 씨는 기병에게 사정을 말해 보라고 청한다.\n\n이 일을 조지 씨는 무척 신중하게 해내는데, 겉으로는 배그닛 씨에게 말하는 척하면서도, 배그닛 씨 자신이 그러하듯, 내내 오직 그 마누라에게만 눈을 두고 있다. 그녀 역시 똑같이 신중하게 제 바느질거리에 골몰한다. 사정을 다 말하자, 배그닛 씨는 규율 유지를 위한 자기 특유의 술책에 의지한다.\n\n\"그게 전부인가, 조지?\" 그가 말한다.\n\n\"그게 전부일세.\"\n\n\"내 의견대로 하겠다는 거지?\"\n\n\"전적으로 그것에 따르겠네.\" 조지가 대답한다.\n\n\"마누라,\" 배그닛 씨가 말한다. \"내 의견을 이 친구한테 말해 줘. 당신도 알잖아. 그게 뭔지 말해 주라고.\"\n\n그 의견인즉, 자기로서는 도무지 속을 헤아릴 수 없는 사람들과는 되도록 엮이지 않을수록 좋고, 자기가 이해하지 못하는 일에 끼어드는 데는 아무리 조심해도 지나치지 않다는 것—요컨대 어둠 속에서는 아무 일도 하지 말고, 음흉하거나 알 수 없는 일에는 한 패가 되지 말며, 땅이 보이지 않는 곳에는 결코 발을 들이지 말라는 것이 명백한 원칙이라는 것이다. 이것이, 그 마누라의 입을 통해 전해진 배그닛 씨의 의견의 골자인데, 이 의견은 조지 씨 자신의 생각을 뒷받침하고 그의 의구심을 몰아냄으로써 그의 마음을 어찌나 놓이게 하는지, 그는 이 특별한 자리를 빌려 파이프를 한 대 더 피우며 마음을 가라앉히고, 배그닛 가족 전원과 저마다의 경험 폭에 따라 옛이야기를 나눈다.\n\n이런 까닭에, 조지 씨가 그 거실에서 다시 한껏 키를 펴고 일어서는 것은, 영국 대중이 극장에서 바순과 횡적을 기다릴 시각이 다가올 무렵에 이르러서다. 더구나 그렇게 되어서도, 조지 씨가 블러피라는 가정 안의 인물로서 퀘벡과 몰타에게 작별을 고하고, 인생의 성공을 축하하며 대자의 주머니에 대부로서 일 실링을 슬쩍 찔러 넣는 데에도 시간이 걸리는 터라, 조지 씨가 다시 링컨스인필즈로 발길을 돌릴 때에는 이미 날이 저물어 있다.\n\n\"가정이라는 건,\" 그가 걸어가며 곰곰이 생각한다. \"아무리 자그마해도, 나 같은 사람을 외로워 보이게 만든단 말이지. 하지만 내가 그 결혼이라는 전술 변환을 끝내 안 한 건 잘한 일이야. 난 그런 데 어울리는 위인이 못 됐을 테니. 이 나이가 되어서도 여전히 이렇게나 떠돌이 신세라, 사격 연습장 일이 정규 생업이거나, 거기서 집시처럼 천막을 치고 살지 않는다면야, 한 달도 붙어 있질 못할 거야. 자! 난 누구한테 망신을 주지도 않고 누구한테 짐이 되지도 않아. 그것만 해도 어디냐. 오랜 세월 그것조차 못 해 봤으니!\"\n\n그래서 그는 그것을 휘파람으로 떨쳐 버리고 계속 행진한다.\n\n링컨스인필즈에 다다라 토킹혼 씨의 계단을 오르니, 바깥문이 닫혀 있고 사무실도 잠겨 있다. 그러나 기병은 바깥문에 대해 잘 모르는 데다 계단마저 어두운 터라, 초인종 손잡이라도 찾아내거나 제 손으로 문을 열어 볼까 싶어 여태 더듬거리며 헤매고 있는데, 토킹혼 씨가 (물론 조용히) 계단을 올라와 성난 목소리로 묻는다. \"거 누구요? 거기서 뭐 하는 거요?\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 어르신. 조지입니다. 하사관 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 하사관 조지는 내 문이 잠긴 것도 못 봤단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그게, 네, 못 봤습니다, 어르신. 어쨌든, 못 봤습니다.\" 기병이 다소 부아가 나서 말한다.\n\n\"마음을 바꿨소? 아니면 그대로요?\" 토킹혼 씨가 묻는다. 하지만 그는 한눈에 충분히 알고 있다.\n\n\"그대롭니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았소. 그걸로 됐소. 가도 좋소. 그래, 자네가 바로 그 사람이로군,\" 토킹혼 씨가 열쇠로 문을 열며 말한다. \"자네 은신처에서 그리들리가 발견됐다지?\"\n\n\"네, 제가 바로 그 사람입니다.\" 기병이 두세 계단 아래에서 멈춰 서며 말한다. \"그래서 어쨌다는 겁니까, 어르신?\"\n\n\"어쨌다고? 난 자네 패거리가 마음에 안 드네. 자네가 그자라는 걸 진작 알았더라면, 오늘 아침 내 문 안쪽은 구경도 못 했을 거요. 그리들리라니? 위협이나 일삼는, 살기등등한, 위험한 작자.\"\n\n평소답지 않게 한껏 높인 목소리로 이 말을 내뱉고, 변호사는 자기 방으로 들어가 천둥 같은 소리를 내며 문을 닫는다.\n\n조지 씨는 몹시 언짢게 이 축객령을 받아들이는데, 계단을 올라오던 서기 하나가 그 마지막 말을 죄다 듣고 그것을 분명히 자기한테 적용하는 통에 더욱 언짢다. \"참 듣기 좋은 평판일세.\" 기병이 황급히 욕을 한마디 내뱉으며 성큼성큼 계단을 내려가며 으르렁댄다. \"위협이나 일삼는, 살기등등한, 위험한 작자라니!\" 그러고는 고개를 들어 보니, 그 서기가 그를 내려다보며, 그가 등불을 지나칠 때 그 모습을 눈여겨 새겨 두고 있다. 이 때문에 그의 언짢음이 어찌나 심해지는지, 그는 오 분쯤 기분이 잡친 채로 있다. 그러나 그는 나머지 것들처럼 그것도 휘파람으로 떨쳐 버리고 사격 연습장 집으로 행진해 간다.\n\n레스터 데드록 경은 가문의 통풍을 이번만큼은 이겨내고, 비유적으로뿐 아니라 글자 그대로의 의미에서도 다시금 제 두 다리로 일어섰다. 그는 링컨셔의 영지에 머물고 있다. 그러나 저지대 땅에는 또다시 물이 차오르고, 추위와 습기가 야무지게 방비한 체스니 월드에까지 스며들며, 덤으로 레스터 경의 뼛속까지 파고든다. 데드록 가문의 목재와 태곳적 숲에서 나온 장작과 석탄이 넓디넓은 난로에서 활활 타오르며, 나무가 어떻게 희생되는지 못마땅하게 지켜보는 음울한 숲을 향해 황혼녘에 깜박이지만, 그래도 적을 막아내지는 못한다. 집안 구석구석을 휘감으며 뻗어 나간 온수관도, 쿠션을 댄 문과 창문도, 칸막이와 커튼도, 난롯불의 부족분을 채우거나 레스터 경의 필요를 만족시키지는 못한다. 그리하여 어느 날 아침 상류층 소식통은 귀 기울이는 온 세상에 데드록 마님이 몇 주 묵을 요량으로 곧 도시로 돌아올 예정이라고 알린다.\n\n위대한 인물에게도 가난한 친척이 있다는 것은 서글픈 진실이다. 사실 위대한 인물은 가난한 친척을 분에 넘치게 많이 거느린 경우가 흔하다. 상등품의 새빨간 피라는 것이, 불법으로 흘린 하등의 피와 마찬가지로, 기어이 큰소리로 울부짖고 기어이 누군가의 귀에 들리고야 마는 까닭이다. 레스터 경의 친척은, 아무리 먼 촌수라도, \"들통나고야 마는\" 살인 사건처럼 수도 없이 많다. 그 가운데에는 너무도 가난한 친척들이 있어서, 차라리 데드록 가문의 황금 사슬에 끼워진 도금 고리가 되지 말고, 애당초 흔해 빠진 무쇠로 만들어져 천한 일이나 하며 살았더라면 더 행복했으리라고 거의 단언하고 싶을 지경이다.\n\n그러나 일이라는 것은(점잖되 돈벌이는 되지 않는 몇 가지 제한된 예외를 빼면) 데드록 가문의 품위 때문에 그들이 손댈 수 없는 노릇이다. 그래서 그들은 더 부유한 친척을 찾아다니고, 가능할 때는 빚을 지며, 그러지 못할 때는 초라하게 산다. 그리하여 여자들은 남편을 못 찾고 남자들은 아내를 못 찾으며, 빌린 마차를 타고, 제 손으로는 결코 차린 적 없는 잔칫상에 앉아, 그렇게 상류 생활을 헤쳐 나간다. 부유한 가문의 합계가 너무 많은 숫자로 나뉜 탓에, 그들은 아무도 처치할 줄 모르는 나머지 우수리다.\n\n레스터 데드록 경 편에 서서 그와 같은 방식으로 생각하는 사람이라면 누구나 다소간 그의 친척인 듯하다. 부들 경부터 푸들 공작을 거쳐 누들에 이르기까지, 레스터 경은 찬란한 거미처럼 친척 관계의 실을 사방으로 뻗는다. 그러나 그는 누구나 다 아는 거물 친척들 앞에서는 위엄을 차리면서도, 아무도 모르는 보잘것없는 친척들에게는 자기 나름의 점잖은 방식으로 친절하고 너그러운 사람이다. 그래서 지금도 그는 습기에도 아랑곳없이, 체스니 월드에 찾아온 그런 친척 몇몇의 방문을 순교자 같은 인내심으로 끝까지 견뎌낸다.\n\n그 가운데 맨 앞줄 선두에 서는 인물이 볼룸니아 데드록이다. 또 다른 명문가에 외가 쪽으로 가난한 친척이 되는 영예를 누리니, 이중으로 고귀한 혈통을 지닌 (예순 살의) 젊은 아가씨다. 볼룸니아 양은 어린 시절부터 색종이를 오려 장식품 만드는 데 제법 솜씨를 보였고, 기타에 맞춰 스페인 말로 노래를 부르거나 시골 저택에서 프랑스어 수수께끼를 내는 재주도 있어서, 스무 살에서 마흔 살 사이의 이십 년 세월을 그런대로 즐겁게 보냈다. 그러다 한물가 버리고, 스페인 말 노래 솜씨로 사람들을 지겹게 한다는 평을 듣게 되자, 그녀는 바스로 물러나 레스터 경이 해마다 보내주는 선물에 의지해 빠듯하게 살며, 이따금 친척들의 시골 저택에서 부활하곤 한다. 그녀는 바스에서 다리가 가늘고 무명 바지를 입은 소름 끼치는 노신사들 사이에 폭넓은 친분을 쌓아, 그 음산한 도시에서 높은 지위를 누린다. 그러나 다른 곳에서는 다소 꺼림칙한 존재인데, 연지를 분별없이 잔뜩 처바르는 데다, 작은 새알을 꿴 묵주 같은 한물간 진주 목걸이를 고집스레 걸고 다니는 탓이다.\n\n건전한 상태에 놓인 나라라면 어디서든 볼룸니아는 두말없이 연금 대상자가 되었을 것이다. 그녀를 연금 명단에 올리려는 노력이 있었고, 윌리엄 버피가 입각했을 때는 그녀의 이름이 연 이백 파운드짜리로 적힐 거라고 다들 기대했다. 그러나 윌리엄 버피는 어쩐 일인지, 모두의 기대와 달리, 지금이 그런 일을 할 수 있는 시절이 아님을 알아냈으니, 이것이 레스터 데드록 경에게 나라가 망조에 들었다고 전한 첫 번째 명백한 징조였다.\n\n마찬가지로 명예로운 밥 스테이블스라는 인물도 있는데, 수의사 못지않은 솜씨로 따뜻한 사료죽을 쑬 줄 알고, 웬만한 사냥터지기보다 명사수다. 그는 한동안 골치 아픈 일이나 책임이 따르지 않으면서도 보수가 두둑한 자리에서 나라에 봉사하기를 유독 갈망해왔다. 잘 굴러가는 정치 조직이라면, 이토록 고귀한 연줄을 지닌 패기만만한 젊은 신사의 이 자연스러운 소망이 곧장 인정받았을 텐데, 어쩐 일인지 윌리엄 버피는 입각하고서 지금이 그 사소한 일조차 처리할 수 있는 시절이 아님을 알아냈으니, 이것이 레스터 데드록 경에게 나라가 망조에 들었다고 전한 두 번째 징조였다.\n\n나머지 친척들은 나이도 능력도 제각각인 신사 숙녀로, 대부분은 상냥하고 분별 있으며, 친척이라는 굴레만 극복할 수 있었다면 인생을 그럭저럭 잘 꾸려나갔을 사람들이다. 사정이 그렇다 보니 그들은 거의 다 그 굴레에 조금씩 휘둘려, 목적도 의욕도 없는 길에서 빈둥대며, 남들이 그들을 어떻게 처치해야 할지 몰라 난감해하는 만큼이나 자기 자신을 어찌해야 할지 몰라 갈피를 못 잡는 듯하다.\n\n이런 무리 가운데서, 아니 어디서든, 데드록 마님은 최고로 군림한다. 아름답고 우아하고 교양 있고, 자기만의 작은 세계에서 막강한 그녀이고 보니(유행의 세계라는 것이 극에서 극까지 죄다 뻗어 있는 건 아니니까), 비록 그 태도는 도도하고 무심할지언정, 레스터 경의 저택에서 그녀가 끼치는 영향은 그 집을 크게 끌어올리고 세련되게 한다. 친척들은, 심지어 레스터 경이 그녀와 결혼했을 때 충격으로 몸이 마비될 지경이던 손위 친척들마저, 그녀에게 봉건시대의 신하처럼 예를 갖춘다. 그리고 명예로운 밥 스테이블스는 날마다 아침과 점심 사이에 누군가 골라잡은 사람에게 자기가 가장 아끼는 독창적인 말을 되풀이하니, 그녀가 온 마구간을 통틀어 가장 잘 손질된 암말이라는 것이다.\n\n유령의 길에 울리는 발소리가(하기야 여기서는 들리지도 않지만) 추위에 쫓겨난 고인 친척의 발소리일지도 모를 이 음울한 밤, 체스니 월드의 기다란 응접실에 모인 손님들이 바로 그런 무리다. 잠자리에 들 시각이 가깝다. 침실마다 난롯불이 환하게 타오르며, 벽과 천장에 음산한 가구의 유령을 일으켜 세운다. 문가의 먼 탁자에는 침실용 촛대들이 빽빽이 늘어서 있고, 친척들은 오토만에 앉아 하품을 한다. 피아노 앞에 친척, 탄산수 쟁반 곁에 친척, 카드 탁자에서 일어서는 친척, 난롯가에 둘러앉은 친척. 자기 전용 난로(난로가 둘이 있다) 한쪽에 서 있는 레스터 경. 넓은 난로의 맞은편에 자기 탁자 앞에 앉은 마님. 더 특권을 누리는 친척의 하나로서 두 사람 사이의 호화로운 의자에 앉은 볼룸니아. 그 연지와 진주 목걸이를 더없이 못마땅한 눈길로 흘끔거리는 레스터 경.\n\n\"이 집 층계에서 가끔 마주치는데,\" 매우 두서없는 이야기로 긴 저녁을 보낸 터라 생각이 벌써 층계를 콩콩 뛰어올라 잠자리로 향하는 듯한 볼룸니아가 느릿느릿 말한다. \"내 평생 본 가운데 가장 예쁜 축에 드는 아가씨를 말이에요.\"\n\n\"마님께서 거두신 아이지요.\" 레스터 경이 한마디 한다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았어요. 그 아이를 골라낸 건 분명 보통 안목이 아니라고 확신했지요. 정말이지 경이로운 아이예요. 인형 같은 미모랄까,\" 볼룸니아 양은 자기 취향은 따로 남겨두며 말한다. \"하지만 그 나름으로는 완벽하죠. 그렇게 싱그러운 혈색은 정말 본 적이 없어요!\"\n\n연지를 더없이 못마땅한 눈길로 흘끔거리는 레스터 경도 그렇다고 말하는 듯하다.\n\n\"실은,\" 마님이 나른하게 말한다. \"이 일에 보통 안목이 끼어 있다면, 그건 내가 아니라 라운스웰 부인의 안목이에요. 로자는 그이가 찾아낸 아이지요.\"\n\n\"마님의 하녀인가 보군요?\"\n\n\"아니에요. 뭐든 다 해주는 아이죠. 애완동물이자, 비서이자, 심부름꾼이자, 글쎄 뭐라 해야 할지.\"\n\n\"마치 꽃이나 새나 그림이나 푸들을 곁에 두고 싶은 것처럼, 아니, 푸들은 아니고, 아무튼 그만큼 예쁜 무언가를 곁에 두고 싶어 하시는 거군요?\" 볼룸니아가 맞장구친다. \"그래요, 정말 사랑스럽잖아요! 그리고 그 사랑스러운 노인네 라운스웰 부인은 어쩜 그리 정정한지요. 어마어마한 연세일 텐데도, 그렇게 활기차고 멋지다니! 정말이지 내겐 둘도 없이 소중한 벗이에요!\"\n\n레스터 경은 체스니 월드의 가정부라면 마땅히 비범한 인물이어야 한다고 여긴다. 그것과 별개로, 그는 라운스웰 부인을 진심으로 존중하며 그녀를 칭찬하는 말을 듣기 좋아한다. 그래서 그는 말한다. \"옳은 말이오, 볼룸니아.\" 볼룸니아는 그 말을 듣고 무척 기뻐한다.\n\n\"그이에게 딸은 없지요?\"\n\n\"라운스웰 부인 말이오? 없소, 볼룸니아. 아들이 있지. 사실 둘이 있었소.\"\n\n오늘 저녁 볼룸니아 때문에 만성적인 권태가 한심하게 더 도진 마님은, 촛대 쪽으로 지친 눈길을 던지며 소리 없는 한숨을 내쉰다.\n\n\"라운스웰 부인의 아들이 의회에 들어오라는 권유를 받았다는 이야기를 토킹혼 씨에게서 들었는데, 이건 지금 시대가 빠져든 혼란의 주목할 만한 본보기요. 경계표가 지워지고, 수문이 열리고, 신분의 구별이 뿌리째 뽑혀 나간 본보기란 말이오.\" 레스터 경이 위엄 있는 침울함으로 말한다.\n\n볼룸니아 양이 작게 날카로운 비명을 지른다.\n\n\"그렇소, 정말이지.\" 레스터 경이 거듭 말한다. \"의회로 말이오.\"\n\n\"그런 일은 들어본 적도 없어요! 세상에, 그 사람은 대체 뭐 하는 사람인데요?\" 볼룸니아가 소리친다.\n\n\"내 알기로 그자를, 음, 철물 제조업자라고 부르는 모양이오.\" 레스터 경은 그것을 천천히, 무겁게, 미심쩍어하며 말한다. 혹시 납 제조업자라고 부르는 게 아닌지, 아니면 다른 어떤 금속과 다른 어떤 관계를 나타내는 다른 어떤 말이 맞는 게 아닌지 확신이 서지 않는다는 투다.\n\n볼룸니아가 또 한 번 작게 비명을 지른다.\n\n\"토킹혼 씨에게서 들은 내 정보가 정확하다면, 분명 그럴 텐데, 토킹혼 씨야 늘 정확하고 빈틈없으니 말이오, 그자가 그 제안을 거절했다고 하오. 그렇다고 해도,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"그렇다고 해도 이 변칙이 줄어드는 건 아니오. 그 안에는 기묘한 고려거리가, 내가 보기에 소스라치게 놀라운 고려거리가 가득 차 있단 말이오.\"\n\n볼룸니아 양이 촛대 쪽으로 눈길을 던지며 일어서자, 레스터 경은 정중하게 응접실을 한 바퀴 거창하게 돌아 촛대를 하나 가져와서는 마님의 갓 씌운 등불에 불을 붙인다.\n\n\"부탁이오, 마님.\" 그가 불을 붙이며 말한다. \"잠깐만 더 있어 주시오. 내가 말하는 이 작자가 오늘 저녁 식사 직전에 도착해, 아주 격식 있는 쪽지로 청해왔소.\" 진실을 향한 평소의 존중에 따라 레스터 경은 이 대목을 곱씹는다. \"이건 인정해야겠소만, 아주 격식 있고 잘 표현된 쪽지로, 그 어린 처녀의 일을 두고 당신과 나를 잠깐 만나 뵙는 호의를 베풀어달라고 청해왔단 말이오. 오늘 밤 떠나기를 바라는 듯하기에, 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 만나주겠다고 답했소.\"\n\n볼룸니아 양은 세 번째로 작게 비명을 지르며 줄행랑을 치는데, 부디 주인 부부가, 아이고 맙소사, 그 뭐라더라, 철물 제조업자인가 하는 작자를 무사히 떨쳐내기를 바란다면서다.\n\n다른 친척들도 곧 흩어져, 마지막 한 사람까지 자리를 뜬다. 레스터 경이 종을 울린다. \"라운스웰 씨가 가정부 거처에 있으니 내 안부를 전하고, 지금 만나주겠다고 일러라.\"\n\n이 모든 이야기를 겉으로는 건성으로 듣고 있던 마님이, 라운스웰 씨가 들어오자 그쪽을 바라본다. 그는 쉰을 조금 넘긴 듯하고, 어머니를 닮아 풍채가 좋으며, 또랑또랑한 목소리에, 검은 머리가 물러난 넓은 이마를 지녔고, 빈틈없으면서도 솔직한 얼굴이다. 검은 옷을 입은 믿음직스러운 인상의 신사로, 몸집이 제법 듬직하면서도 다부지고 활기차다. 더없이 자연스럽고 편안한 태도이며, 자기가 들어선 위풍당당한 자리에 조금도 주눅 들지 않는다.\n\n\"레스터 경과 데드록 마님, 폐를 끼쳐 죄송하다는 사과를 이미 드린 마당이니, 최대한 짧게 말씀드리는 것이 상책이겠지요. 감사합니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n데드록 가문의 수장이 자기와 마님 사이의 소파를 손짓으로 가리킨다. 라운스웰 씨가 조용히 거기에 앉는다.\n\n\"이렇게 바쁜 시절에, 큰 사업이 한꺼번에 그렇게 많이 벌어지다 보니, 저 같은 사람은 곳곳에 일꾼이 많아서 늘 이리저리 날아다니듯 분주합니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 철물 제조업자가 이곳에서는 서두를 필요가 없음을 느낀다면 그것으로 흡족하다. 담쟁이와 이끼가 무르익을 만큼 세월이 흐른 이 고요한 정원에 뿌리내린 이 유서 깊은 저택에서는, 옹이지고 혹투성이인 느릅나무와 그늘 짙은 떡갈나무가 백 년 묵은 양치류와 낙엽 속에 깊이 잠겨 서 있고, 테라스의 해시계는 수백 년 동안 묵묵히 시간을 기록해왔으니, 그 시간이란 살아 있는 동안 데드록 가문 사람마다 저택이며 영지만큼이나 자기 소유였던 것이다. 레스터 경은 안락의자에 앉아, 자기와 체스니 월드의 안식을 철물 제조업자들의 쉴 새 없는 비행에 맞세운다.\n\n\"데드록 마님께서,\" 라운스웰 씨가 그쪽으로 공손한 눈길을 던지며 고개를 숙이고 말을 잇는다. \"친절하게도 로자라는 이름의 어린 미인을 곁에 두셨지요. 그런데 제 아들이 로자에게 반해서, 그 아이에게 청혼하고 만일 그 아이가 받아준다면, 받아주리라 봅니다만, 약혼하고 싶다며 제 허락을 구했습니다. 저는 오늘에야 로자를 처음 봤습니다만, 제 아들의 분별력은, 사랑에 빠진 와중이라 해도 어느 정도 믿습니다. 제 판단으로는 그 아이가 아들이 말한 그대로이고, 제 어머니도 그 아이를 매우 칭찬하시더군요.\"\n\n\"그 아이는 모든 면에서 칭찬받을 만하지요.\" 마님이 말한다.\n\n\"그리 말씀해주시니 기쁩니다, 데드록 마님. 그 아이에 대한 마님의 호의 어린 평가가 제게 얼마나 값진지는 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"그건,\" 레스터 경이 이루 말할 수 없이 위엄을 차리며 한마디 한다. 그는 철물 제조업자가 입심이 좀 지나치다고 여기는 터다. \"전혀 필요 없는 말일 것이오.\"\n\n\"전혀 필요 없는 말이지요, 레스터 경. 그런데 제 아들은 아주 젊은 청년이고, 로자도 아주 젊은 처녀입니다. 제가 제 길을 헤쳐 왔듯, 제 아들도 제 길을 헤쳐 나가야 하니, 지금 결혼시키는 것은 어림없는 일입니다. 다만 이 예쁜 처녀가 제 아들과 약혼하겠다면, 제 아들이 이 예쁜 처녀와 약혼하도록 허락한다고 칠 때, 이건 솔직히 당장 말씀드리는 게 도리겠지요. 레스터 경과 데드록 마님, 분명 이해하고 너그러이 봐주시리라 믿습니다만, 저는 그 아이가 체스니 월드에 남지 않는다는 것을 조건으로 내걸겠습니다. 그러니 제 아들과 더 이야기를 나누기 전에, 외람되게 말씀드리자면, 만일 그 아이를 데려가는 일이 어떤 식으로든 불편하거나 마뜩잖으시다면, 합당한 기간만큼 아들과 이 문제를 보류하고 지금 이대로 그냥 놔두겠습니다.\"\n\n체스니 월드에 남지 않는다니! 그것을 조건으로 걸다니! 와트 타일러며, 횃불을 밝히고 죄다 들고일어나는 짓이나 일삼는 철공 지대 사람들에 대해 레스터 경이 품어온 해묵은 불안이 그의 머리 위로 우수수 쏟아져, 그 고운 잿빛 머리칼은 물론 구레나룻까지 정말로 분노에 곤두선다.\n\n\"내가 이렇게 이해해도 되겠소, 선생,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"그리고 마님도 이렇게 이해해도 되겠소,\" 이렇게 마님을 특별히 끌어들이는데, 첫째로는 정중함의 표시이고, 둘째로는 그녀의 분별력을 크게 믿는 터라 신중함의 표시다. \"내가 이렇게 이해해도 되겠소, 라운스웰 씨, 그리고 마님도, 선생, 그대가 이 어린 처녀를 체스니 월드에 두기엔 너무 아깝다고 여기거나, 이곳에 남으면 그 아이가 해를 입으리라고 여긴다는 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"결코 그렇지 않습니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구려.\" 레스터 경, 더없이 거만하다.\n\n\"라운스웰 씨,\" 마님이 레스터 경을 파리라도 되는 양 예쁜 손으로 살짝 손짓해 물러나게 하며 말한다. \"무슨 뜻인지 내게 설명해주세요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 데드록 마님. 저로서는 더 바랄 게 없습니다.\"\n\n침착한 마님의 얼굴, 하지만 그 안의 총기는 아무리 몸에 밴 무표정으로도 감출 수 없을 만큼 빠르고 활달한 그 얼굴을, 결의와 끈기의 표상인 손님의 강인한 색슨족 얼굴을 향하고서, 마님은 이따금 고개를 살짝 숙이며 주의 깊게 듣는다.\n\n\"저는 마님의 가정부의 아들이고, 이 집 언저리에서 어린 시절을 보냈습니다, 데드록 마님. 제 어머니는 이곳에서 반세기를 사셨고, 틀림없이 이곳에서 돌아가시겠지요. 어머니는 그런 본보기, 아마 둘도 없는 본보기 가운데 하나입니다. 영국이 자랑스러워해도 좋을 사랑과 헌신과 충성의 본보기 말이지요. 하지만 어느 계급도 그 자랑이나 그 공로를 통째로 차지할 수는 없습니다. 그런 본보기란 양편 모두의 높은 인품을 증언하니까요. 큰 쪽의 인품을 분명히 증언하지만, 작은 쪽의 인품도 그에 못지않게 분명히 증언하지요.\"\n\n레스터 경은 이런 식으로 펼쳐지는 논리에 콧방귀를 살짝 뀌지만, 명예심과 진실에 대한 사랑에서, 비록 말은 없으나, 철물 제조업자의 명제가 옳음을 선선히 인정한다.\n\n\"이렇게 뻔한 말씀을 드려 송구합니다만, 제가 이곳에서 어머니가 처한 처지를 부끄러워한다거나, 체스니 월드와 가문에 마땅히 표해야 할 존경이 모자란다고,\" 레스터 경 쪽으로 눈길을 아주 살짝 돌리며, \"성급히 넘겨짚으시는 건 원치 않습니다. 저는 분명 바랐을 겁니다, 정말이지 바랐습니다, 데드록 마님, 그 오랜 세월이 지났으니 제 어머니가 물러나 저와 함께 여생을 보내시기를요. 하지만 이 굳건한 인연을 끊는 것이 곧 어머니의 가슴을 무너뜨리는 일임을 깨닫고는, 진작에 그 생각을 접었습니다.\"\n\n라운스웰 부인이 타고난 보금자리에서 채여 나가 철물 제조업자와 여생을 보낸다는 발상에, 레스터 경은 다시금 더없이 위엄을 차린다.\n\n\"저는,\" 손님이 겸손하고 또렷한 어조로 말을 잇는다. \"도제였고 일꾼이었습니다. 여러 해, 여러 해 동안 일꾼의 품삯으로 살았고, 어느 선까지는 독학으로 저를 가르쳐야 했지요. 제 아내는 작업반장의 딸로, 수수하게 자랐습니다. 방금 말씀드린 아들 말고도 우리에겐 딸이 셋 있는데, 다행히 우리가 누리지 못한 더 큰 혜택을 그 아이들에게 줄 수 있었던 터라, 잘, 아주 잘 가르쳤습니다. 그 아이들을 어떤 지위에도 어울릴 만한 사람으로 키우는 것이 우리의 크나큰 근심거리이자 기쁨의 하나였지요.\"\n\n여기 그 아버지다운 어조에는 자랑기가 좀 묻어나니, 마치 마음속으로 \"체스니 월드의 지위에마저 어울리도록\"이라고 덧붙이는 것만 같다. 그러니 레스터 경 쪽에서는 위엄이 한층 더할밖에.\n\n\"이런 일은 제가 사는 곳에서, 또 제가 속한 계급 안에서 워낙 흔해서, 흔히들 격이 안 맞는 결혼이라 부를 만한 일이 우리에게는 다른 데만큼 드물지 않습니다, 데드록 마님. 아들이 가끔 아버지에게 털어놓지요, 이를테면 공장의 어떤 처녀에게 반했다고요. 한때 자기도 공장에서 일했던 아버지는 처음엔 십중팔구 좀 실망할 겁니다. 아들에게 다른 뜻을 품고 있었을 수도 있으니까요. 그래도 십중팔구는, 그 처녀가 흠 없는 품성이라는 것을 확인하고 나면, 아들에게 이렇게 말하겠지요. '네가 정말 진심인지 확실히 해둬야겠다. 이건 너희 둘 다에게 중대한 일이니까. 그러니 내가 이 아가씨를 두 해 동안 가르치게 하마.' 아니면 이렇게도 말하겠지요. '내가 이 아가씨를 네 누이들과 같은 학교에 얼마간 보낼 테니, 그동안 너는 명예를 걸고 가끔씩만 그 아이를 만나겠다고 내게 약속해라. 그 기간이 끝났을 때, 그 아이가 혜택을 누려 너와 어지간히 대등해지고, 너희 둘의 마음이 그대로라면, 내가 너희를 행복하게 해주는 데 내 몫을 다하마.' 제가 말씀드린 것 같은 사례를 여럿 알고 있습니다, 마님. 그리고 그것이 지금 제가 가야 할 길을 일러주는 듯합니다.\"\n\n레스터 경의 위엄이 터져 나온다. 차분하게, 그러나 무시무시하게.\n\n\"라운스웰 씨,\" 레스터 경이 푸른 외투 가슴팍에 오른손을 찔러 넣은 채 말한다. 회랑에 걸린 초상화 속 그 위풍당당한 자세 그대로다. \"그대는 체스니 월드와 어떤, \" 여기서 그는 목이 멜 것 같은 충동을 억누르며, \"공장을 나란히 견준단 말이오?\"\n\n\"두 곳이 매우 다르다는 것은 굳이 답할 필요도 없겠지요, 레스터 경. 그러나 이번 경우에 한해서는, 두 곳을 나란히 견주는 것이 온당하다고 봅니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 자기가 정녕 깨어 있는 것인지 믿어지지 않아, 위엄 있는 눈길을 기다란 응접실의 한쪽 끝으로 내리뜨렸다가 다른 쪽 끝으로 올려뜬다.\n\n\"선생, 마님이, 마님께서 곁에 두신 이 어린 처녀가 성문 밖 마을 학교에서 자랐다는 것을 알고 있소?\"\n\n\"레스터 경, 잘 알고 있습니다. 아주 훌륭한 학교이고, 이 가문이 후하게 지원하고 있지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 라운스웰 씨,\" 레스터 경이 받아친다. \"그대가 한 말을 어디에 빗대는 건지 나로서는 도무지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"이렇게 말씀드리면 좀 더 이해가 되시겠습니까, 레스터 경,\" 철물 제조업자가 얼굴을 살짝 붉히며 말한다. \"저는 마을 학교가 제 며느릿감이 마땅히 알아야 할 모든 것을 가르친다고 보지는 않습니다.\"\n\n체스니 월드의 마을 학교에서, 이 순간에도 멀쩡한 그 학교에서, 사회 전체의 골격으로. 사회 전체의 골격에서, 앞서 말한 그 골격이 사람들이(철물 제조업자며, 납 제조업자며, 뭐며 하는 자들이) 교리문답은 거들떠보지도 않고, 자기가 부름받은 신분을 벗어나려는 탓에 무시무시하게 쩍쩍 금이 가는 데로. 그 신분이란, 레스터 경의 잽싼 논리에 따르면, 반드시 그리고 영원히, 그들이 어쩌다 처음 몸담은 바로 그 신분이다. 그리고 거기서 다시, 그들이 다른 사람들마저 그 사람들 본래의 신분에서 벗어나도록 가르쳐, 그리하여 경계표를 지우고, 수문을 열어젖히고, 운운하는 데로. 이것이 데드록 정신의 잽싼 진행이다.\n\n\"마님, 죄송합니다. 잠깐만 양해해주시오!\" 마님이 말하려는 기색을 희미하게 내비쳤던 것이다. \"라운스웰 씨, 의무에 대한 우리의 견해도, 신분에 대한 우리의 견해도, 교육에 대한 우리의 견해도, 그리고 한마디로 우리의 모든 견해가 너무도 정반대라, 이 논의를 더 끄는 것은 그대의 감정에도 거슬리고 나 자신의 감정에도 거슬릴 것이오. 이 어린 처녀는 마님의 관심과 총애를 받는 영예를 누리고 있소. 만일 그 아이가 그 관심과 총애에서 스스로 물러나기를 바라거나, 자기 나름의 유별난 견해로, 유별난 견해라고 말하는 걸 양해하시오, 비록 그가 그 견해를 내게 해명할 책임이 없다는 건 선뜻 인정하오만, 아무튼 자기 나름의 유별난 견해로 그 아이를 그 관심과 총애에서 떼어내려는 누군가의 영향 아래 스스로 들어가기로 한다면, 언제든 그렇게 할 자유가 있소. 그대가 솔직하게 말해준 데 대해서는 고맙게 생각하오. 그것만으로는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 이곳에서의 그 처녀의 처지에 아무런 영향도 미치지 못할 것이오. 그 이상으로 우리가 어떤 조건도 맺을 수는 없소. 그러니 부디 그대가 너그러이, 이 문제는 여기서 접어주기를 청하오.\"\n\n손님은 마님에게 기회를 주려고 잠시 멈추지만, 그녀는 아무 말이 없다. 그러자 그는 일어서서 대답한다. \"레스터 경과 데드록 마님, 경청해주셔서 감사드립니다. 그리고 한 말씀만 드리자면, 제 아들에게 지금의 마음을 누르라고 아주 진지하게 권하겠습니다. 안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n\"라운스웰 씨,\" 레스터 경이 신사의 천성을 온전히 빛내며 말한다. \"시각도 늦었고 길도 어둡소. 그대의 시간이 그토록 귀하지만은 않아, 적어도 오늘 밤만은 마님과 내가 체스니 월드의 후의를 베풀도록 허락해주기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"그랬으면 합니다.\" 마님이 거든다.\n\n\"매우 감사합니다만, 아침 정해진 시각에 맞춰 먼 고장에 닿으려면 밤새 길을 가야 합니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 철물 제조업자는 떠나고, 레스터 경은 종을 울리며, 마님은 그가 방을 나설 때 일어선다.\n\n마님은 자기 내실로 가서 난롯가에 깊은 생각에 잠겨 앉아, 유령의 길에는 마음을 두지 않은 채, 안쪽 방에서 글을 쓰고 있는 로자를 바라본다. 이윽고 마님이 그 아이를 부른다.\n\n\"이리 오너라, 얘야. 솔직히 말해보렴. 너 사랑에 빠졌니?\"\n\n\"어머! 마님!\"\n\n마님은 수줍게 고개 숙여 발그레해진 얼굴을 보며 미소 띠고 말한다. \"그게 누구냐? 라운스웰 부인의 손자니?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 마님. 하지만 제가 그분을 사랑하는지는, 아직 잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"아직이라니, 이 어리석은 것아! 그럼 그가 너를 사랑한다는 건 아직도 모르니?\"\n\n\"저를 조금 좋아하시는 것 같아요, 마님.\" 그러고는 로자가 울음을 터뜨린다.\n\n마을의 미인 곁에 서서, 어머니 같은 손길로 그 아이의 검은 머리를 쓸어내리며, 사색에 잠긴 관심이 가득 어린 눈으로 그 아이를 지켜보는 이 사람이 정녕 데드록 마님인가? 그렇다, 정말이지 그렇다!\n\n\"내 말을 듣거라, 얘야. 너는 젊고 진실하지. 게다가 너는 나를 따른다고 나는 믿는다.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다, 마님. 정말이지 제가 얼마나 마님을 따르는지 보여드릴 수만 있다면 이 세상에 못 할 일이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 너는, 로자, 사랑하는 사람을 위해서라도, 아직은 나를 떠나고 싶지 않겠지?\"\n\n\"안 떠나요, 마님! 아, 안 떠나요!\" 로자는 그 생각에 잔뜩 놀라 처음으로 고개를 든다.\n\n\"내게 털어놓으렴, 얘야. 나를 두려워 말고. 나는 네가 행복하기를 바라고, 너를 행복하게 해주마. 내가 이 세상에서 누군가를 행복하게 해줄 수 있다면 말이다.\"\n\n로자는 새로 눈물을 쏟으며 그녀의 발치에 무릎 꿇고 손에 입을 맞춘다. 마님은 그 아이가 붙든 손을 거두어, 난롯불에 눈길을 박은 채 서서, 그것을 두 손 사이에 이리저리 넣고 굴리다가, 차츰 떨어뜨린다. 마님이 그토록 골똘히 잠긴 것을 보고 로자는 살며시 물러나지만, 마님의 눈길은 여전히 난롯불에 머문다.\n\n무엇을 찾는 걸까? 이제는 없는 어떤 손을, 애당초 없었던 어떤 손을, 마법처럼 그녀의 삶을 바꿔놓았을지도 모를 어떤 손길을? 아니면 유령의 길에 귀 기울이며 그것이 어떤 발소리를 가장 닮았는지 헤아리는 걸까? 사내의 발소리? 여인의 발소리? 자꾸만 다가오고 또 다가오고 또 다가오는 어린아이의 자그마한 발자국 소리? 어떤 우울한 기운이 그녀를 짓누르는 게 아니라면, 어찌하여 이토록 도도한 마님이 문을 닫아걸고 그토록 쓸쓸하게 홀로 난롯가에 앉아 있는 걸까?\n\n이튿날 볼룸니아는 떠나고, 친척들도 모두 저녁 식사 전에 흩어진다. 라운스웰 부인의 아들을 통해 드러난 경계표의 지워짐과, 수문의 열림과, 사회 골격의 쩍쩍 갈라짐에 대해, 아침 식사 자리에서 레스터 경에게 듣고 기겁하지 않는 친척이 그 무리 가운데 하나도 없다. 정말로 분개하지 않는 친척, 그것을 윌리엄 버피의 재임 시절 나약함과 결부 짓지 않는 친척, 사기와 부정 탓에 자기가 나라의, 아니 연금 명단의, 아니 무언가의 한몫을 빼앗긴 기분을 정말로 느끼지 않는 친척이 그 무리 가운데 하나도 없다. 볼룸니아로 말하자면, 레스터 경이 큰 층계를 따라 그녀를 손수 부축해 내려가는데, 마치 그녀의 연지 단지와 진주 목걸이를 차지하려고 잉글랜드 북부에서 대대적인 봉기라도 일어난 양 그 주제를 두고 열변을 토한다. 그리하여 하녀들과 시종들이 우당탕거리는 가운데, 제 한 몸 건사하기가 아무리 빠듯해도 하녀와 시종만은 반드시 두어야 하는 것이 그들 친척 됨의 한 가지 부속물인지라, 친척들은 하늘의 사방 바람을 향해 뿔뿔이 흩어진다. 그리고 오늘 부는 겨울바람 한 줄기가 인적 끊긴 저택 근처의 나무들에서 우수수 잎을 떨구니, 마치 친척들이 죄다 나뭇잎으로 변해버린 것만 같다.\n\n체스니 월드는 굳게 닫혔다. 양탄자는 살풍경한 방구석에 둘둘 말려 거대한 두루마리가 되었고, 화사한 다마스크 천은 칙칙한 갈색 마포를 뒤집어쓴 채 속죄하고, 조각과 금박은 고행하듯 빛을 죽였으며, 데드록 가문의 조상들은 다시 한번 햇빛에서 물러난다. 집을 빙빙 에워싸며 나뭇잎이 두툼하게 떨어지는데, 결코 빠르지는 않다. 음울하고 느릿하게, 죽은 듯한 가벼움으로 빙글빙글 맴돌아 내려오기 때문이다. 정원사가 아무리 잔디를 쓸고 또 쓸어 나뭇잎을 손수레 가득 눌러 담아 실어 날라도, 나뭇잎은 여전히 발목까지 쌓여 있다. 날카로운 바람이 체스니 월드를 휘돌며 울부짖고, 매서운 빗줄기가 들이치고, 창문은 덜컹대고, 굴뚝은 으르렁댄다. 안개는 가로수 길에 몸을 숨기고, 전망을 가리며, 장례 행렬처럼 비탈진 땅을 가로질러 흐른다. 집 전체에 작은 교회에서 나는 냄새 같은 차갑고 공허한 냄새가, 다만 그보다 조금 더 메마른 냄새가 감돈다. 마치 죽어 묻힌 데드록 조상들이 긴긴밤마다 그곳을 거닐며 자기 무덤의 향취를 남겨두고 가는 듯하다.\n\n그러나 도시의 저택은, 좀처럼 체스니 월드와 같은 시기에 같은 기분이 되는 법이 없어, 한쪽이 기뻐할 때 함께 기뻐하거나 한쪽이 슬퍼할 때 함께 슬퍼하는 일이 드물고, 다만 데드록 가문 사람이 죽었을 때만 예외인데, 그 도시의 저택은 잠에서 깨어나 환히 빛난다. 위풍당당함이 자아낼 수 있는 한껏 따스하고 환하게, 온실 꽃이 빚어낼 수 있는 한껏 겨울 기색이라곤 한 점 없는 향긋한 내음을 섬세하게 풍기며, 시계의 똑딱임과 벽난로 불의 바삭한 타는 소리만이 방 안의 고요를 깨뜨릴 만큼 부드럽고 잔잔하게, 그 저택은 레스터 경의 그 시린 뼈마디를 무지갯빛 양모로 감싸주는 듯하다. 레스터 경은 서재의 커다란 난롯불 앞에서 위엄 있는 만족감에 잠겨 쉬는 것을 흐뭇해하며, 황송하다는 듯 책등을 훑어보거나 미술품에 흡족한 눈길을 보내 영예를 베푼다. 그에게는 고금의 그림들이 있으니 말이다. 더러는 가장무도회파에 속하는 것들로, 미술이 이따금 황송하게도 거장 노릇을 해주는 부류인데, 이런 것들은 경매 잡화 목록처럼 정리하는 편이 제일 어울린다. 이를테면 \"등받이 높은 의자 셋, 탁자 하나와 덮개 하나, 목 긴 병 하나(포도주 들었음), 플라스크 하나, 스페인 여인 의상 하나, 모델 조그 양의 사분의 삼 측면 초상, 그리고 돈키호테가 든 갑옷 한 벌.\" 또는 \"돌 테라스 하나(금 갔음), 멀리 보이는 곤돌라 하나, 베네치아 원로원 의원 의상 한 벌 완비, 모델 조그 양의 옆얼굴 초상이 곁든 화려한 자수 흰 새틴 의상 하나, 보석 박은 손잡이에 금으로 호화롭게 장식한 신월도 하나, 정교한 무어인 의상(아주 희귀함) 하나, 그리고 오셀로.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 영지 업무라든가 임대차 계약 갱신 따위가 있어 꽤 자주 드나든다. 그는 마님도 꽤 자주 본다. 그리고 그와 그녀는 여전히 차분하고, 여전히 무심하며, 여전히 서로에게 거의 마음을 쓰지 않는다. 그러나 어쩌면 마님은 이 토킹혼 씨를 두려워하고, 그는 그것을 알고 있는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그는 가책도, 후회도, 연민도 한 점 없이 끈질기고 꾸준하게 그녀를 뒤쫓는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그녀의 아름다움과 그녀를 둘러싼 온갖 위풍과 광채가 도리어 그가 작정한 일에 더 큰 흥취를 돋우고, 그 일에 한결 더 완강해지게 만드는지도 모른다. 그가 차갑고 잔인하든, 자기 의무로 삼은 일에 흔들림이 없든, 권력욕에 빠져 있든, 평생 비밀 사이를 파고들어 온 영역에서 어느 것 하나 자기 모르게 숨겨지는 것을 용납지 않으려 작정했든, 자기가 한낱 머나먼 빛줄기에 지나지 않는 그 화려함을 마음속으로 멸시하든, 화려한 의뢰인들의 살가운 태도 속에 받은 멸시와 모욕을 늘 차곡차곡 쌓아두든, 이 가운데 어느 하나이든 혹은 전부이든, 어쩌면 마님으로서는 목에 헝겊 조각 같은 넥타이를 두르고 무릎에 리본을 묶은 칙칙한 검정 반바지를 입은 이 녹슨 변호사의 두 눈보다, 차라리 유행을 좇는 눈동자 오천 쌍이 미덥지 못한 경계심으로 자기를 지켜보는 편이 나을지도 모른다.\n\n레스터 경이 마님의 방에 앉아 있다. 토킹혼 씨가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송의 진술서를 읽었던 바로 그 방이다. 유난히 흡족한 표정이다. 마님은 그날과 마찬가지로 손에 가리개를 든 채 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있다. 레스터 경이 유난히 흡족한 까닭은 신문에서 사회의 둑과 골격을 곧장 겨냥한 마음에 쏙 드는 논평을 찾아냈기 때문이다. 그 논평이 요전번 사건에 어찌나 절묘하게 들어맞는지, 레스터 경은 일부러 그것을 소리 내어 읽어주려고 서재에서 마님의 방까지 건너왔다. \"이 기사를 쓴 사람은 말이오,\" 그는 서두를 떼며, 마치 높은 산봉우리에서 그 사람을 내려다보며 고개를 끄덕이듯 난롯불을 향해 끄덕이며 말한다. \"균형 잡힌 정신을 지녔어.\"\n\n그 사람의 정신이 그리 균형 잡혀 있다고 보기는 어렵지만, 어쨌든 그 사람은 마님을 지루하게 만든다. 마님은 나른하게 귀 기울여 보려 애쓰다가, 아니 차라리 귀 기울이는 시늉이나마 나른하게 받아들여 보려다가, 어느새 정신이 딴 데로 흩어져 난롯불을 골똘히 바라본다. 마치 그것이 체스니 월드의 자기 난롯불이고, 한 번도 그곳을 떠난 적이 없는 듯이. 레스터 경은 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 코안경 너머로 계속 읽어 내려가다가, 이따금 멈춰 안경을 벗고는 \"참으로 옳은 말이야,\" \"참 적절하게 짚었어,\" \"나도 종종 똑같은 말을 하곤 했지\" 하고 찬동을 표하는데, 매번 그렇게 한마디 하고 나면 어김없이 읽던 자리를 잃어버려 다시 찾으려 단을 위아래로 더듬는다.\n\n레스터 경이 더없이 진지하고 위엄 있게 읽고 있는데 문이 열리더니, 분을 뒤집어쓴 머큐리가 이 기묘한 통고를 한다. \"마님, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이가 왔습니다.\"\n\n레스터 경이 멈칫하고, 빤히 쳐다보다가, 사람 잡을 듯한 목소리로 되뇐다. \"거피라는 이름의 젊은이라고?\"\n\n둘러보니, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이가, 잔뜩 주눅 든 채, 태도와 행색으로 보아 그리 인상적인 소개장이 못 되는 모습으로 서 있다.\n* 머큐리: 로마 신화의 전령신. 디킨스가 분 바른 가발을 쓰고 시중드는 하인을 빈정대어 부르는 별칭이다.\n\n\"그래,\" 레스터 경이 머큐리에게 말한다. \"거피라는 이름의 젊은이가 왔다고 이렇게 불쑥 알리다니, 도대체 무슨 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"송구합니다, 레스터 경. 다만 마님께서 그 젊은이가 찾아오면 언제든 만나겠다고 말씀하셨기에. 레스터 경께서 여기 계신 줄은 미처 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n이렇게 변명하고 나서 머큐리는 거피라는 이름의 젊은이에게 경멸과 분노가 어린 눈길을 던지는데, 그 눈빛은 분명 이렇게 말하고 있다. \"대체 뭐 하러 여기 찾아와서 나를 곤경에 빠뜨리는 거야?\"\n\n\"잘했다. 내가 그 사람한테 그렇게 일러두었어,\" 마님이 말한다. \"젊은이를 기다리게 하렴.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 부인. 당신이 오라고 분부했다니 내가 방해하지 않으리다.\" 레스터 경은 정중하게 물러가는데, 나가는 길에 젊은이의 인사를 받아주기를 사양하다시피 하며, 위풍당당하게도 그를 주제넘게 찾아온 구두장이쯤으로 짐작한다.\n\n하인이 방을 나가자, 데드록 마님은 손님을 도도하게 바라보며 머리끝에서 발끝까지 훑는다. 그를 문가에 세워둔 채, 무슨 용건이냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"마님께서 친절을 베푸시어 잠시 대화를 허락해주셨으면 합니다,\" 거피 씨가 쩔쩔매며 대꾸한다.\n\n\"네가 그렇게나 숱한 편지를 보낸 그 사람이렷다?\"\n\n\"몇 통이었습니다, 마님. 마님께서 황송하게도 답장을 내려주시기 전까지 몇 통 보냈지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 같은 방식으로, 그러니까 편지로 이 대화를 불필요하게 만들 수는 없었느냐? 지금이라도 그럴 수 없느냐?\"\n\n거피 씨는 입을 오므려 소리 없이 \"안 됩니다!\" 하는 모양을 짓고는 고개를 젓는다.\n\n\"넌 기이하리만치 끈질겼다. 결국 네가 할 말이 나와는 아무 상관도 없는 것으로 드러난다면, 어떻게 상관이 있을지도 모르겠고 그러리라 기대도 않지만, 그럴 때는 별 예의 차리지 않고 네 말을 끊어버릴 테니 그리 알아라. 할 말이 있거든 어서 하렴.\"\n\n마님은 가리개를 무심히 한 번 까딱하고는 다시 난롯불 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이에게 거의 등을 돌리다시피 하고 앉는다.\n\n\"그렇다면 마님의 허락을 얻어,\" 젊은이가 말한다. \"이제 용건으로 들어가겠습니다. 흠! 저는 첫 편지에서 마님께 말씀드린 대로 법조계에 몸담고 있습니다. 법조계에 있다 보니 글로는 함부로 발을 들이지 않는 습관을 익혔습지요. 그래서 제가 몸담은 회사 이름, 거기서 제 지위가, 덧붙이자면 수입도 제법 괜찮은 그 회사의 이름은 마님께 적어 보내지 않았습니다. 이제 마님께 은밀히 아뢰자면, 그 회사 이름은 링컨 법학원의 켄지 앤드 카보이로, 어쩌면 마님께서도 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 대법원 소송과 관련하여 영 모르시지는 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n마님의 자태에 얼마간 주의를 기울이는 기색이 비치기 시작한다. 마님은 가리개를 까딱이던 손길을 멈추고, 귀를 기울이는 양 그것을 든 채로 있다.\n\n\"자, 마님께 곧바로 말씀드리겠습니다만,\" 거피 씨가 조금 대담해져서 말한다. \"제가 이토록 마님과 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한 까닭은 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서 비롯된 일 때문이 아닙니다. 물론 이런 행동이 주제넘어 보였고 또 지금도 그렇게 보이리라는 건, 사실 거의 불한당 같다는 것도 의심치 않습니다만.\"\n\n그렇지 않다고 안심시켜 주는 말을 잠시 기다렸으나 아무 말도 듣지 못하자, 거피 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"만일 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 건이었다면, 저는 곧장 마님의 변호사인 더 필즈의 토킹혼 씨께 갔을 겁니다. 저는 토킹혼 씨와 안면이 있는 영광을 누리고 있습니다. 적어도 서로 마주치면 목례 정도는 나누지요. 그런 종류의 일이었다면 저는 그분께 갔을 겁니다.\"\n\n마님이 몸을 조금 돌리고 말한다. \"앉는 게 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"감사합니다, 마님.\" 거피 씨가 자리에 앉는다. \"자, 마님,\" 거피 씨는 자기 논지를 작게 메모해둔 쪽지를 들여다보는데, 그것을 볼 때마다 외려 더없이 짙은 안갯속으로 빠져드는 듯하다. \"저는, 아 그렇지! 저는 전적으로 마님 손에 저 자신을 맡기겠습니다. 만일 마님께서 오늘 이 방문을 두고 켄지 앤드 카보이나 토킹혼 씨께 무어라 항의하신다면, 저는 아주 난처한 처지에 놓이게 됩니다. 그건 솔직히 인정합니다. 그러니 저는 마님의 명예를 믿겠습니다.\"\n\n마님은 가리개를 든 손으로 경멸하는 몸짓을 지어, 그가 자기 항의를 받을 만한 가치도 없는 자임을 일러준다.\n\n\"감사합니다, 마님,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"더없이 만족스럽습니다. 자, 저는, 에라 모르겠다! 실은 제가 짚으려던 요점의 순서를 한두 가지 여기 적어두었는데, 짧게 줄여 쓴 데다 무슨 뜻인지 도통 알아먹질 못하겠습니다. 마님께서 잠깐만 창가로 가져가 보는 걸 양해해주신다면, 저는…\"\n\n거피 씨는 창가로 가다가 잉꼬 한 쌍에게 부딪치고는, 당황하여 그들에게 말한다. \"아이고 죄송합니다, 정말로.\" 이래서야 메모가 더 잘 읽힐 리 없다. 그는 얼굴이 달아올라 벌게진 채, 쪽지를 눈에 바싹 댔다가 다시 멀찍이 떼었다가 하며 중얼거린다. \"씨에스. 씨에스가 뭐였더라? 아! 씨에스! 아, 알겠다! 그래, 그렇고말고!\" 그러고는 깨달음을 얻어 돌아온다.\n\n\"마님께서,\" 거피 씨가 마님과 자기 의자 중간쯤에 서서 말한다. \"혹시라도 에스더 서머슨 양이라는 이름의 젊은 아가씨에 대해 들어보셨거나 만나보신 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n마님의 두 눈이 그를 똑바로 바라본다. \"그런 이름의 젊은 아가씨를 얼마 전에 보았다. 지난 가을이었지.\"\n\n\"그런데 마님께서 보시기에 그 아가씨가 누군가와 닮았다는 생각이 들지 않으셨습니까?\" 거피 씨가 팔짱을 끼고 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 메모지로 입가를 긁적이며 묻는다.\n\n마님은 그에게서 더는 눈을 떼지 않는다.\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"마님의 가문 사람들과 닮지 않았던가요?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"제 생각에 마님께서는,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"서머슨 양의 얼굴을 잘 기억하지 못하시겠지요?\"\n\n\"그 젊은 아가씨를 아주 잘 기억한다. 한데 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐?\"\n\n\"마님, 정말로 장담컨대 저는 서머슨 양의 모습을 제 심장에 아로새겨 두고 있는데, 이건 은밀히 말씀드리는 겁니다만, 어느 벗과 함께 링컨셔 지방에 잠깐 나들이 갔을 때 마님의 저택 체스니 월드를 둘러보는 영광을 누리다가, 에스더 서머슨 양과 마님의 초상화가 어찌나 닮았는지 그만 완전히 나가떨어지고 말았습니다. 어찌나 심하게 나가떨어졌던지 그 순간에는 도대체 무엇이 저를 그렇게 나가떨어지게 했는지조차 몰랐을 정도였습니다. 그리고 이제 마님을 가까이서 뵙는 영광을 누리고 보니, 그 뒤로도 공원에서 마차에 타신 마님을 종종 외람되게 훔쳐보긴 했습니다만, 그땐 아마 마님께서 저를 알아채지 못하셨겠지요, 어쨌든 마님을 이렇게 가까이서 뵌 적은 없었는데, 정말이지 제가 생각했던 것보다 한층 더 놀랍습니다.\"\n\n거피라는 이름의 젊은이여! 귀부인들이 견고한 성채에 살며 부르면 달려올 거리에 양심이라곤 없는 시종들을 거느리던 시절이 있었으니, 그런 때라면 지금 이 순간 그대를 바라보는 저 아름다운 두 눈 앞에서, 그대의 가련한 목숨은 단 일 분의 값어치도 못 되었으리라.\n\n마님은 작은 손가리개를 부채 삼아 느릿느릿 부치며, 닮은꼴을 향한 그의 취미가 도대체 자기와 무슨 상관이 있다고 여기느냐고 다시 묻는다.\n\n\"마님,\" 거피 씨가 다시 자기 쪽지를 들여다보며 대답한다. \"이제 그 얘기로 들어갑니다. 에라, 이놈의 메모! 아! '채드밴드 부인.' 그렇지.\" 거피 씨는 의자를 조금 앞으로 당겨 다시 자리에 앉는다. 마님은 의자에 차분히 기대 있는데, 어쩌면 평소보다 우아한 여유는 약간 덜할지 몰라도, 그 흔들림 없는 응시만은 조금도 흐트러지지 않는다. \"아, 잠깐만요!\" 거피 씨가 또 들여다본다. \"이에스가 두 번? 아, 그렇지! 그래, 이제 길이 보입니다, 곧장 가면 돼요.\"\n\n거피 씨는 자기 말을 강조하는 도구로 삼으려 쪽지를 둘둘 말아 들고는 말을 잇는다.\n\n\"마님, 에스더 서머슨 양의 출생과 양육에는 수수께끼가 있습니다. 제가 그 사실을 아는 건, 이것도 은밀히 말씀드리는 겁니다만, 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 직업상 알게 되었기 때문입니다. 자, 이미 마님께 말씀드린 대로 서머슨 양의 모습은 제 심장에 아로새겨져 있습니다. 만일 제가 그녀를 위해 이 수수께끼를 풀어준다든가, 그녀가 좋은 가문 출신임을 입증한다든가, 혹은 그녀가 마님 가문의 먼 갈래에 드는 영광을 지녀 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 당사자로 들어갈 권리가 있음을 알아낸다든가 한다면, 저는 서머슨 양에게 그동안 정확히 보여주지 않았던 것보다 더 헌신적인 호의의 눈길로 제 청혼을 봐달라고 일종의 요구를 할 수 있을 겁니다. 사실 그녀는 지금껏 제 청혼에 도통 호의를 보인 적이 없거든요.\"\n\n마님의 얼굴에 일종의 노여운 미소가 막 어린다.\n\n\"자, 이건 참으로 기묘한 정황입니다, 마님,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"그래도 저희 법조인의 길에 곧잘 끼어드는 정황 가운데 하나이긴 합니다만, 저도 법조인이라 부를 만한 게, 정식 자격은 못 얻었어도 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 견습 계약서를 선물로 받았거든요. 인지대가 만만치 않은데 그걸 어머니가 얼마 안 되는 수입의 원금에서 떼어 대주셨지요. 어쨌든 그 기묘한 정황이란, 제가 잔다이스 씨가 서머슨 양을 맡기 전에 그녀를 길러준 부인 곁에서 하녀로 살았던 사람을 만났다는 겁니다. 그 부인은 바버리 양이라는 분이었습니다, 마님.\"\n\n마님의 얼굴에 어린 송장 같은 빛깔은, 초록 비단 바탕의 가리개에서 비친 것인가, 그녀가 까맣게 잊은 듯 치켜든 손에 든 그 가리개에서 비친 것인가, 아니면 그녀에게 내려앉은 끔찍한 창백함인가?\n\n\"마님께서는,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"혹시 바버리 양에 대해 들어보신 적이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"모르겠다. 그런 것도 같고. 그래, 들어봤다.\"\n\n\"바버리 양이 마님 가문과 무슨 관련이라도 있었습니까?\"\n\n마님의 입술이 움직이지만, 아무 말도 나오지 않는다. 그녀가 고개를 젓는다.\n\n\"관련이 없다고요?\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"아! 어쩌면 마님께서 아시는 한에서는 없다는 거겠지요? 아하! 그래도 있을 수는 있겠지요? 그렇군요.\" 이렇게 물을 때마다 그녀는 고개를 숙여 보였다. \"아주 좋습니다! 자, 이 바버리 양은 입이 무척 무거운 분이었습니다. 여자치고는 유난히 입이 무거웠던 모양인데, 여자란 대개 적어도 일상에서는 말하기를 꽤 즐기는 법이잖습니까. 어쨌든 제 증인은 그분에게 친척이 한 명이라도 있는지조차 도무지 짐작할 수 없었답니다. 한번, 딱 한 번, 그분이 단 한 가지 점에 대해서만 제 증인에게 속을 털어놓은 적이 있는데, 그때 그분은 그 어린 여자아이의 진짜 이름이 에스더 서머슨이 아니라 에스더 혼이라고 말했답니다.\"\n\n\"세상에!\"\n\n거피 씨가 빤히 쳐다본다. 데드록 마님은 그의 앞에 앉아, 얼굴에 그 똑같은 검은 그늘을 드리운 채, 가리개 든 손짓까지 그대로인 자세로, 입술을 조금 벌리고, 이마를 조금 찌푸린 채, 그를 꿰뚫어 보는데, 순간 송장처럼 굳어 있다. 그는 그녀에게 의식이 돌아오는 것을, 수면에 이는 잔물결처럼 그녀의 온몸을 떨림이 스쳐 지나가는 것을, 그녀의 입술이 떨리는 것을, 그녀가 안간힘을 다해 그것을 다잡는 것을, 그의 존재와 그가 한 말에 대한 인식으로 자신을 억지로 되돌리는 것을 본다. 이 모든 것이 어찌나 빠르게 지나갔던지, 그녀의 탄성과 그 송장 같던 상태는 마치 무덤에서 이따금 파헤쳐지는 오래 보존된 시신들의 모습처럼, 바깥 공기가 번개처럼 들이치자 한순간 숨결에 사라져버린 듯하다.\n\n\"마님께서는 혼이라는 이름을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"전에 들어본 적 있다.\"\n\n\"마님 가문의 방계나 먼 갈래에 드는 누군가의 이름인가요?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"자, 마님,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"이제 제가 알아낸 한도 안에서, 이 사건의 마지막 대목으로 들어갑니다. 사건은 지금도 진행 중이고, 진행될수록 저는 더 바싹바싹 추려 모을 작정입니다. 마님께서도 아셔야 할 게, 혹시라도 이미 알고 계시지 않다면 말입니다만, 얼마 전 챈서리 레인 부근의 크룩이라는 사람의 집에서 한 법률 필경사가 몹시 곤궁한 처지로 죽은 채 발견되었습니다. 그 법률 필경사를 두고 검시가 열렸는데, 이 필경사는 이름도 알려지지 않은 무명의 인물이었습니다. 그런데 마님, 제가 아주 최근에 알아낸 바로는, 그 법률 필경사의 이름이 혼이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐?\"\n\n\"아, 마님, 바로 그게 문제입니다! 자, 마님, 그 사람이 죽은 뒤에 기묘한 일이 벌어졌습니다. 한 부인이 나타났는데, 변장한 부인이었습니다, 마님, 그 부인이 사건 현장을 보러 가고 그의 무덤도 보러 갔지요. 그 부인은 길거리 청소부 소년을 사서 무덤을 안내받았습니다. 마님께서 이 진술을 뒷받침할 증거로 그 소년을 데려오기를 바라신다면, 저는 언제든 그 아이를 잡아올 수 있습니다.\"\n\n그 가련한 소년은 마님에게 아무것도 아니며, 마님은 그를 데려오기를 바라지 않는다.\n\n\"아, 정말이지 마님, 그건 참으로 기묘한 일이었습니다,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"그 부인이 장갑을 벗었을 때 손가락에 반짝이던 반지들 이야기를 그 아이한테서 들으시면, 마님께서도 어지간히 낭만적이라 여기실 겁니다.\"\n\n가리개를 든 손에 다이아몬드가 반짝이고 있다. 마님은 가리개를 만지작거려 그것을 더 반짝이게 하는데, 또다시 그 표정을 짓는다. 다른 시절이었다면 거피라는 이름의 젊은이에게 자못 위험했을 그 표정을.\n\n\"마님, 그 사람이 신원을 가려낼 만한 천 조각 하나, 부스러기 하나 남기지 않았으리라 여겨졌습니다. 하지만 남겼지요. 그는 오래된 편지 한 뭉치를 남겼습니다.\"\n\n가리개는 여전히 아까처럼 움직인다. 이 내내 그녀의 두 눈은 단 한 번도 그를 놓아주지 않는다.\n\n\"그 편지들은 누군가 가져가 감춰두었습니다. 그런데 내일 밤이면, 마님, 그것이 제 손에 들어옵니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 다시 묻겠다, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐?\"\n\n\"마님, 그 말씀으로 마무리하겠습니다.\" 거피 씨가 일어선다. \"한데 짜 맞춰진 이 정황의 사슬에, 그러니까 이 젊은 아가씨가 마님과 의심할 바 없이 강하게 닮았다는 점, 이건 배심원 앞에서는 확실한 사실입니다만, 그 아가씨가 바버리 양 손에 자랐다는 점, 바버리 양이 서머슨 양의 진짜 이름을 혼이라 했다는 점, 마님께서 이 두 이름을 다 아주 잘 아신다는 점, 그리고 혼이 그렇게 죽었다는 점, 이 모든 것에 마님께서 이 사건을 더 깊이 파고들 만한 가문의 이해관계를 부여하는 무언가가 충분히 들어 있다고 여기신다면, 저는 그 서류들을 이리로 가져오겠습니다. 그게 무엇인지는, 오래된 편지라는 것 말고는 저도 모릅니다. 아직 제 손에 넣어본 적이 없으니까요. 그것을 손에 넣는 대로 이리로 가져와, 마님과 함께 처음으로 펼쳐 보겠습니다. 제 목적은 마님께 말씀드렸습니다. 어떤 항의라도 들어오면 제가 아주 난처한 처지에 놓인다는 것도 말씀드렸고요. 모든 게 철저히 비밀입니다.\"\n\n이것이 거피라는 이름의 젊은이의 온전한 속셈일까, 아니면 다른 무엇이 더 있는 걸까? 그의 말은 여기 찾아온 그의 목적과 의심의 길이며 너비며 깊이를 다 드러내고 있는 걸까, 아니라면 무엇을 감추고 있는 걸까? 그 점에서는 그도 마님에게 만만찮은 상대다. 마님은 그를 바라볼 수 있어도, 그는 탁자를 바라보며 그 증인석 같은 얼굴이 아무것도 발설하지 않도록 지킬 수 있으니 말이다.\n\n\"편지를 가져와도 좋다,\" 마님이 말한다. \"네가 원한다면.\"\n\n\"마님께서는 정말이지 별로 격려가 되어주지 않으시는군요, 맹세코,\" 거피 씨가 조금 서운한 듯 말한다.\n\n\"편지를 가져와도 좋다,\" 그녀가 같은 어조로 되풀이한다. \"네가, 원한다면.\"\n\n\"그리하겠습니다. 마님, 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n그녀 곁의 탁자에는 오래된 금고처럼 빗장과 잠금쇠가 채워진 호화로운 보석함 하나가 놓여 있다. 그녀는 여전히 그를 바라보면서 그것을 끌어당겨 자물쇠를 연다.\n\n\"아! 마님, 단언컨대 저는 그런 따위 동기로 움직이는 게 아닙니다,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"그런 것은 도저히 받을 수 없습니다. 마님, 안녕히 계십시오. 그래도 정말 감사합니다.\"\n\n이렇게 젊은이는 인사를 하고 아래층으로 내려가는데, 거기서 거만한 머큐리는 젊은이를 내보내주려고 현관 난롯불 옆 자기 올림포스를 떠날 필요는 없다고 여긴다.\n\n레스터 경이 서재에서 느긋이 쉬며 신문 위로 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있는 동안, 이 집 안에는 그를 소스라치게 할 기운이라곤 없는 걸까? 하물며 체스니 월드의 나무들이 옹이진 팔을 치켜들게 하고, 초상화들이 눈살을 찌푸리게 하고, 갑옷들이 들썩이게 할 기운이 없는 걸까?\n\n없다. 말도, 흐느낌도, 울부짖음도 한낱 공기일 뿐이요, 이 도시의 저택은 어디고 공기가 어찌나 꽁꽁 갇히고 막혀 있는지, 마님이 자기 방에서 나팔 같은 목청으로 외쳐야 비로소 어렴풋한 떨림이나마 레스터 경의 귀에 가닿을 만하다. 그런데도 이 울부짖음은 집 안에 있어, 무릎 꿇은 어떤 광란의 형상에게서 위로 솟구쳐 오른다.\n\n\"오 내 아이, 내 아이! 잔인한 언니가 내게 말했듯 태어나 첫 시간에 죽은 게 아니라, 언니가 나와 내 이름을 저버린 뒤로 그 손에 모질게 길러졌구나! 오 내 아이, 오 내 아이!\"\n\n리처드가 떠난 지 얼마쯤 지났을 때, 며칠 묵으러 손님이 찾아왔습니다. 나이 지긋한 부인이었지요. 바로 우드코트 부인으로, 웨일스에서 와 베이엄 배저 부인 댁에 머물던 차에, \"아들 앨런의 부탁으로\" 후견인에게 편지를 보내 아들 소식을 들었으며 아들이 잘 지내고 \"우리 모두에게 다정한 안부를 전한다\"라고 알렸더니, 후견인이 황폐한 집을 한번 방문하라고 청한 것이었습니다. 부인은 우리와 거의 삼 주를 함께 지냈습니다. 나를 무척 다정하게 대했고 흉허물 없이 속내를 털어놓았는데, 어찌나 그러던지 때로는 나를 거의 불편하게 만들 정도였습니다. 부인이 나를 믿고 속을 털어놓는다 해서 내가 불편해할 권리가 없다는 건 나도 잘 알았고, 그게 이치에 맞지 않는다고도 느꼈습니다. 그래도 아무리 애를 써도 그 마음을 영 떨치지 못했습니다.\n\n부인은 어찌나 영민한 작은 분인지, 나에게 이야기하는 동안 두 손을 맞잡은 채 앉아 무척이나 살피는 눈빛으로 바라보곤 했는데, 어쩌면 나는 그게 다소 거북했던 것 같습니다. 아니면 그토록 꼿꼿하고 단정한 자세 탓이었을지도 모르지만, 그건 아닌 듯합니다. 그건 어딘가 예스럽게 정겹다고 여겼으니까요. 노부인치고는 무척 반짝이고 고운, 그 얼굴 전체에서 풍기는 표정 때문도 아닐 겁니다. 무엇 때문이었는지 모르겠습니다. 아니면 적어도 지금은 안다 해도, 그때는 몰랐다고 여겼습니다. 아니면 적어도 — 하지만 그건 중요치 않습니다.\n\n밤에 내가 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갈 때면, 부인은 나를 자기 방으로 청해 들였는데, 그 방에서 부인은 커다란 의자에 앉아 난롯불 앞을 지키고 있었습니다. 그러고는, 세상에, 내가 아주 풀이 죽을 때까지 모건 앱케리그 이야기를 늘어놓는 것이었습니다! 가끔은 크럼린왈린워와 뮬린윌린워드(이게 맞는 이름이라면 말이지만, 분명 아닐 겁니다)에서 시구를 몇 줄 읊었고, 거기 담긴 정취에 자못 열을 올리곤 했습니다. 그것들이 (웨일스 말이라) 무슨 뜻인지 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없었지만, 모건 앱케리그 가문을 더없이 드높이 기리는 내용이라는 것만은 알았습니다.\n\n\"그러니까, 서머슨 양,\" 부인은 위엄에 찬 의기양양한 투로 나에게 말하곤 했습니다. \"이게 바로 내 아들이 물려받은 재산이라우. 내 아들이 어딜 가든 앱케리그와 한 핏줄이라고 내세울 수 있지요. 돈은 없을지언정, 그보다 훨씬 나은 걸 늘 지니고 있으니 — 바로 가문이에요, 그래요.\"\n\n인도며 중국에서 사람들이 모건 앱케리그를 그토록 끔찍이 여길까 싶어 나는 의구심이 들었지만, 물론 그런 말은 한 번도 입 밖에 내지 않았습니다. 그렇게 명문가와 이어져 있다는 건 대단한 일이라고 말하곤 했지요.\n\n\"대단한 일이라우, 그래요,\" 우드코트 부인은 대답하곤 했습니다. \"불리한 점도 있긴 하지요. 이를테면 내 아들이 아내를 고르는 폭이 그 때문에 좁아진다든가. 하지만 왕가의 혼인 상대 선택도 꼭 그런 식으로 좁혀지는 법이라우.\"\n\n그러고 나면 내 팔을 토닥이고 옷매무새를 매만지곤 했는데, 우리 사이에 놓인 거리에도 불구하고 나를 좋게 본다는 걸 새삼 확인시켜 주려는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"가엾은 우드코트 씨는 말이에요,\" 부인은 늘 어떤 감회에 젖어 말하곤 했는데, 그토록 드높은 혈통을 지녔으면서도 무척 다정한 마음을 지녔으니까요. \"하일랜드의 대단한 가문, 매쿠어트 가의 매쿠어트 출신이었답니다. 왕립 하일랜더 연대 장교로 임금과 나라를 섬기다가 전장에서 숨을 거두었지요. 내 아들은 두 오래된 가문의 마지막 후손 가운데 하나라우. 하늘의 축복으로 그 가문을 다시 일으켜 또 다른 오래된 가문과 맺어줄 거예요.\"\n\n화제를 바꿔보려고 애써도 소용없었습니다. 그저 새로운 이야기를 해보고 싶어서, 아니면 어쩌면 — 하지만 그렇게 시시콜콜 따질 필요는 없겠지요. 우드코트 부인은 결코 화제를 바꾸도록 내버려 두지 않았습니다.\n\n\"이봐요,\" 어느 날 밤 부인이 말했습니다. \"당신은 분별이 무척 깊고, 나이에 비해 한참 높은 데서 세상을 차분히 바라보니, 내 집안 일을 이렇게 당신에게 이야기하는 게 나로선 위안이 된다우. 당신은 내 아들을 잘 모르지요. 하지만 그래도, 분명, 떠올릴 만큼은 알지요?\"\n\n\"네, 부인. 그분을 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"그래요. 자, 이봐요, 당신은 사람 됨됨이를 볼 줄 안다고 생각해요. 그러니 그 애에 대한 당신 의견을 듣고 싶구려.\"\n\n\"어머, 우드코트 부인,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그건 너무 어려운데요!\"\n\n\"왜 그게 그리 어렵다는 거예요?\" 부인이 되물었습니다. \"나는 잘 모르겠는데.\"\n\n\"의견을 말씀드린다는 게 —\"\n\n\"이렇게 친분이 얕은데 말이지요. 그래요, 맞아요.\"\n\n내 말은 그런 뜻이 아니었습니다. 우드코트 씨는 우리 집에 두루 자주 드나들었고 후견인과도 꽤 가까운 사이가 되었으니까요. 나는 그렇게 말하고, 그분이 자기 직업에서 무척 유능해 보인다고 — 우리 생각에 그렇다고 — 덧붙였으며, 플라이트 양에게 베푸는 친절과 다정함은 무어라 칭찬해도 모자란다고 했습니다.\n\n\"제대로 보았네요!\" 우드코트 부인이 내 손을 꼭 누르며 말했습니다. \"그 애를 정확히 짚어냈어요. 앨런은 사랑스러운 녀석이고, 제 일에서는 나무랄 데가 없지요. 어미인 내가 하는 말이지만요. 그래도 솔직히 말하자면, 그 애에게 흠이 없는 건 아니라우.\"\n\n\"우리 가운데 누구도 흠이 없는 사람은 없지요,\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\"아! 하지만 그 애 흠은 정말이지 고칠 수 있고, 마땅히 고쳐야 할 흠이라우,\" 영민한 노부인이 매섭게 고개를 저으며 대꾸했습니다. \"당신에게 무척 정이 들었으니, 아무 이해관계 없는 제삼자에게 하듯 당신에게 털어놓을 수 있겠네요. 그 애는 변덕 그 자체예요.\"\n\n그분이 쌓은 명성으로 미루어 보면, 자기 직업에 한결같고 그 길을 좇는 데 열성을 다하지 않았으리라고는 도무지 생각하기 어렵겠다고 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"그것도 옳은 말이에요,\" 노부인이 받아쳤습니다. \"하지만 내가 말하는 건 그 애 직업이 아니라우, 봐요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 부인이 말했습니다. \"내가 말하는 건, 이봐요, 그 애의 사교 처신이라우. 그 애는 늘 젊은 아가씨들에게 시시한 관심을 기울이고, 열여덟 살 적부터 줄곧 그래왔지요. 그런데 말이에요, 그 애는 그중 누구도 진심으로 마음에 둔 적이 없고, 그러면서 무슨 해를 끼치거나 예의와 호의 말고 다른 무엇을 드러내려던 적도 없어요. 그래도 옳지 않은 일이지요. 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"네,\" 부인이 내 대답을 기다리는 듯해 나는 그렇게 말했습니다.\n\n\"그러다 보면 오해를 살 수도 있잖아요, 봐요, 이봐요.\"\n\n그럴 수도 있겠다고 나는 짐작했습니다.\n\n\"그래서 나는 그 애한테 여러 번 일렀어요. 정말 좀 더 조심해야 한다고, 자기를 위해서나 남을 위해서나 말이지요. 그러면 그 애는 늘 이러더군요. '어머니, 그러겠어요. 하지만 어머니는 누구보다 저를 잘 아시잖아요. 제가 아무 해도 끼칠 뜻이 없다는 걸 — 한마디로, 아무 뜻도 없다는 걸 아시잖아요.' 다 옳은 말이지요, 이봐요. 하지만 변명은 못 되지요. 하지만 이제 그 애가 그렇게 멀리, 기약 없이 떠났고, 좋은 기회와 소개도 많이 받을 테니, 이건 다 지나간 일로 칠 수 있겠어요. 그런데 당신은 말이에요, 이봐요,\" 어느새 온통 고갯짓과 미소뿐인 노부인이 말했습니다. \"당신 자신은 어떻게 할 거예요?\"\n\n\"저 말씀인가요, 우드코트 부인?\"\n\n\"제 운을 찾아 떠나고 아내를 구하러 간 아들 이야기만 늘어놓으며 늘 그렇게 제 식구 일만 챙길 순 없지요 — 당신은 언제 당신 운을 찾고 남편을 구할 생각이에요, 서머슨 양? 어머나, 봐요! 이제 얼굴이 다 붉어지네요!\"\n\n나는 얼굴을 붉히지 않은 것 같습니다 — 설령 붉혔다 해도 그건 중요한 일이 아니었고요 — 그래서 나는 지금의 내 처지가 더없이 만족스러우며 그것을 바꿀 마음이 없다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"내가 당신과, 당신에게 앞으로 다가올 운에 대해 늘 어떻게 생각하는지 말해줄까요?\" 우드코트 부인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"부인께서 스스로 예언을 잘하신다고 믿으신다면요,\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 당신은 무척 부유하고 무척 훌륭하며 당신보다 한참 — 어쩌면 스물다섯 살쯤 — 나이 많은 누군가와 결혼할 거예요. 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 아내가 되어, 많은 사랑을 받고, 무척 행복할 거예요.\"\n\n\"좋은 운이네요,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"하지만 왜 그게 제 운이어야 하나요?\"\n\n\"이봐요,\" 부인이 대꾸했습니다. \"거기엔 어울림이 있어요 — 당신은 그토록 부지런하고, 그토록 야무지며, 처지가 그토록 유달리 남다르니, 거기엔 어울림이 있고, 그러니 그렇게 될 거예요. 그리고 당신이 그런 혼인을 한다면, 이봐요, 나만큼 진심으로 축하해줄 사람도 없을 거예요.\"\n\n이런 말이 나를 불편하게 만들다니 묘한 일이었지만, 정말 그랬던 것 같습니다. 분명 그랬습니다. 그 때문에 나는 그날 밤 한동안 불편했습니다. 내 어리석음이 어찌나 부끄럽던지 에이다에게조차 털어놓고 싶지 않았고, 그게 나를 더더욱 불편하게 만들었습니다. 어떻게든 거절할 수만 있었다면, 그 명랑한 노부인의 속내 이야기를 그토록 깊이 듣지 않기 위해 무엇이든 다 내놓았을 겁니다. 그 때문에 나는 부인에 대해 더없이 앞뒤가 안 맞는 생각을 품게 되었습니다. 어느 순간에는 부인이 이야기를 꾸며대는 사람이라 여겼고, 또 어느 순간에는 진실의 본보기라 여겼습니다. 지금은 부인이 무척 교활하다고 의심하다가, 다음 순간에는 그 정직한 웨일스 마음이 더없이 천진하고 단순하다고 믿었습니다. 그리고 따지고 보면, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이며, 왜 나에게 상관이 되었을까요? 왜 나는 열쇠 바구니를 들고 잠자리에 들러 올라가다가, 부인의 난롯불 곁에 잠시 앉아 적어도 다른 누구에게 그러듯 부인에게 잠깐 맞춰주며, 부인이 나에게 건넨 해롭지 않은 말들로 마음을 졸이지 않을 수 없었을까요? 분명 나는 부인에게 마음이 끌렸으니 — 부인이 나를 좋아해 주길 무척 바랐고 정말로 그래주어 더없이 기뻤으니 — 왜 그러고 나서 그 말 한마디 한마디를, 진짜 괴로움과 아픔에 사로잡혀 곱씹고, 스무 개 저울에 올려 거듭거듭 달아보았을까요? 부인이 우리 집에 머물며 밤마다 나에게 속을 터놓는 게 왜 나에게 그토록 마음 쓰이는 일이었을까요? 그러면서도 부인이 다른 어느 곳보다 여기 있는 편이 어쩐지 더 낫고 더 안전하다고 느꼈으면서요. 이것들은 내가 설명할 수 없는 곤혹스러움이요 모순이었습니다. 적어도, 설명할 수 있다 해도 — 하지만 그 모든 건 차차 이야기할 테니, 지금 늘어놓는 건 그저 부질없는 짓일 뿐입니다.\n\n그래서 우드코트 부인이 떠났을 때, 나는 부인을 잃는 게 서운했지만 한편으론 마음이 놓이기도 했습니다. 그러고 나서 캐디 젤리비가 내려왔는데, 캐디가 어찌나 집안 소식 보따리를 잔뜩 가져왔던지 우리는 할 일이 넘쳐났습니다.\n\n먼저 캐디는 (처음에는 다른 말은 한사코 입에 올리지 않으며) 내가 세상에 둘도 없는 더없이 훌륭한 조언자라고 단언했습니다. 내 귀염둥이는 그건 전혀 새삼스러운 소식이 아니라고 했고, 나는 물론 그건 터무니없는 소리라고 했습니다. 그러고 나서 캐디는 한 달 뒤에 결혼하게 되었다고, 에이다와 내가 들러리를 서준다면 자기는 세상에서 가장 행복한 처녀가 되겠다고 말했습니다. 정말이지 이건 그야말로 소식이었지요. 우리는 캐디에게 할 말이 너무 많고 캐디도 우리에게 할 말이 너무 많아, 그 이야기는 도무지 끝날 줄 몰랐습니다.\n\n알고 보니 캐디의 불운한 아빠는 파산을 헤쳐 나온 모양이었습니다 — 캐디가 쓴 말로는 \"관보를 통과했다\"라고 했는데, 마치 무슨 터널이라도 되는 양 — 채권자들의 너그러운 자비와 동정 덕에 어찌어찌 복받은 방식으로 일을 정리했지만 정작 그 내막은 끝내 이해하지 못했고, 가진 것을 죄다 내놓았으며(가구 상태로 미루어 보건대 그리 값나가는 것도 아니었을 듯합니다), 더는 어쩔 도리가 없다는 걸 관계자 모두에게 납득시켰으니, 가엾은 양반이었지요. 그렇게 해서 명예롭게 풀려나 \"사무실\"로 가 다시 처음부터 세상을 시작하게 되었습니다. 그 사무실에서 무슨 일을 하는지는 나도 끝내 알지 못했습니다. 캐디는 아빠가 \"세관 겸 일반 대리업자\"라고 했는데, 그 일에 대해 내가 알아들은 건 단 하나, 평소보다 돈이 더 궁할 때면 아빠가 부두로 그것을 찾으러 가지만 좀처럼 찾지 못한다는 것뿐이었습니다.\n\n이렇게 털 깎인 어린양 신세가 되어 아빠가 마음을 추스르고, 식구들이 해턴 가든의 가구 딸린 셋방으로 옮겨 가자(나중에 그곳에 가보니 아이들이 의자 방석에서 말총을 뽑아내 그걸로 목이 메도록 입에 물고 있었습니다), 캐디는 아빠와 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 만나는 자리를 마련했습니다. 그러자 가엾은 젤리비 씨가 워낙 겸손하고 온순한 분이라 터비드롭 씨의 풍채에 어찌나 고분고분 굽실거렸던지, 둘은 더없이 좋은 벗이 되었습니다. 차츰 늙은 터비드롭 씨도 그렇게 아들의 결혼이라는 생각에 익숙해지면서, 그 일이 코앞에 닥쳤음을 헤아릴 만큼 부정을 한껏 끌어올렸고, 젊은 부부가 원할 때 뉴먼 거리의 학원에서 살림을 시작해도 좋다고 너그러이 승낙했습니다.\n\n\"그래서 너희 아빠는, 캐디. 뭐라고 하셨어?\"\n\n\"아! 가엾은 아빠,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그저 우시기만 하면서, 우리가 아빠랑 엄마보다는 더 잘 지내길 바란다고만 하셨어. 프린스 앞에서는 그러지 않으시고, 나한테만 그러셨지. 그러고는 이러셨어. '가엾은 내 딸아, 너는 남편을 위해 가정을 꾸리는 법을 제대로 배우지 못했다. 하지만 진심으로 온 마음을 다해 그러려고 애쓸 작정이 아니라면 — 그 사람을 정말로 사랑한다면 — 결혼하느니 차라리 그를 죽이는 게 낫다.'\"\n\n\"그래서 너는 아빠를 어떻게 안심시켰어, 캐디?\"\n\n\"그게, 너도 알겠지만, 가엾은 아빠가 그렇게 풀이 죽어 그런 끔찍한 말씀을 하시는 걸 보니 무척 마음이 아파서, 나도 울지 않을 수 없었어. 하지만 나는 정말 온 마음을 다할 작정이라고, 우리 집이 아빠가 저녁이면 찾아와 위안을 얻을 수 있는 곳이 되길 바란다고, 거기서는 집에 있을 때보다 더 나은 딸이 될 수 있길 바라고 또 그러리라 생각한다고 말씀드렸어. 그러고 나서 피피가 나랑 같이 와서 지내게 될 거라고 했더니, 아빠가 또 우시면서 그 애들은 인디언이라고 하셨어.\"\n\n\"인디언이라고, 캐디?\"\n\n\"응,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"사나운 인디언이래. 그러고는 아빠가 이러셨어\" — 여기서 그 애는 흐느끼기 시작했는데, 가엾게도 세상에서 가장 행복한 처녀와는 영 딴판이었지요 — \"그 애들한테 일어날 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은 다 같이 토마호크에 찍히는 거라는 걸 자기도 잘 안다고 하셨어.\"\n\n에이다는 젤리비 씨가 그런 파괴적인 말을 진심으로 한 게 아니라는 걸 아니 다행이라고 했습니다.\n\n\"응, 물론 아빠가 식구들이 피투성이가 되어 나뒹구는 걸 바라지 않으신다는 건 나도 알아,\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"하지만 그 애들이 엄마의 자식인 게 무척 불운하고, 아빠가 엄마의 남편인 게 무척 불운하다는 뜻이지. 이런 말 하는 게 도리에 어긋나는 것 같긴 하지만, 그게 사실인 건 분명해.\"\n\n나는 캐디에게 젤리비 부인이 결혼 날짜가 잡힌 걸 아느냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"아! 너도 엄마가 어떤지 알잖아, 에스더,\" 그 애가 대답했습니다. \"아시는지 모르시는지 도무지 말할 수가 없어. 여러 번 말씀드렸지. 그런데 말씀드려도 그저 나를 차분한 눈빛으로 바라보실 뿐인데, 마치 내가 뭐랄까 — 저 멀리 있는 첨탑쯤 되는 것처럼 말이야,\" 캐디가 문득 떠오른 생각을 말했습니다. \"그러고는 고개를 저으면서 '아, 캐디, 캐디, 넌 어쩜 그렇게 사람을 못살게 구니!' 하시고는 보리오불라 편지를 계속 쓰셔.\"\n\n\"그럼 네 혼수는, 캐디?\" 내가 말했습니다. 그 애는 우리 앞에서 조금도 거리낌이 없었으니까요.\n\n\"글쎄, 에스더,\" 그 애가 눈물을 닦으며 대답했습니다. \"할 수 있는 데까지 최선을 다하고, 내가 이렇게 초라한 차림으로 가더라도 사랑하는 프린스가 결코 서운하게 여기지 않으리라 믿는 수밖에. 보리오불라로 보낼 채비에 관한 문제라면 엄마가 죄다 알고 한껏 들떠 하시겠지. 그게 아니니, 알지도 못하고 신경도 안 쓰셔.\"\n\n캐디는 어머니에 대한 자연스러운 애정이 조금도 모자라지 않았지만, 부정할 수 없는 사실로서 이 말을 눈물과 함께 꺼냈고, 안타깝게도 그건 사실이었습니다. 우리는 가엾은 그 애가 딱했고, 그토록 풀 죽을 만한 처지에서도 살아남은 그 착한 천성에 탄복할 점이 어찌나 많던지, 우리 둘은(에이다와 나 말입니다) 그 자리에서 그 애를 더없이 기쁘게 할 작은 계획을 내놓았습니다. 그건 캐디가 우리 집에서 삼 주를 지내고, 내가 캐디네서 일주일을 지내며, 우리 셋이 머리를 짜내 마름질하고, 손보고, 바느질하고, 아껴서, 그 애가 가진 것을 최대한 활용하도록 우리가 생각해낼 수 있는 최선을 다하는 것이었습니다. 후견인도 캐디만큼이나 그 생각을 반겨서, 우리는 이튿날 캐디를 집에 데려가 일을 마련하고, 다시 의기양양하게 데리고 나왔습니다. 상자들과, 십 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장에서 짜낼 수 있는 모든 물건까지 함께 말입니다. 그 지폐는 젤리비 씨가 부두에서 찾아낸 것이지 싶은데, 어쨌든 그가 그 애에게 준 것이었습니다. 우리가 부추겼다면 후견인이 그 애에게 무엇을 더 주지 않았을지 가늠하기 어렵겠지만, 우리는 결혼 예복과 보닛 이상은 받지 않기로 매듭짓는 게 옳다고 여겼습니다. 후견인도 이 타협에 동의했고, 캐디가 평생 한 번이라도 행복했던 적이 있다면, 우리가 일감을 펼쳐놓고 앉았을 때 행복했습니다.\n\n가엾게도 그 애는 바늘 솜씨가 영 서툴러서, 예전에 손에 잉크를 묻히던 만큼이나 손가락을 찔러댔습니다. 이따금 얼굴이 약간 붉어지는 걸 어쩌지 못했는데, 따끔거려서 그렇기도 하고 더 잘 못하는 게 분해서 그렇기도 했지요. 하지만 곧 그것을 이겨내고 부쩍 솜씨가 늘기 시작했습니다. 그렇게 날마다 그 애와, 내 귀염둥이와, 내 어린 하녀 찰리와, 시내에서 온 모자 만드는 여자와, 내가 더없이 즐겁게 일에 몰두했습니다.\n\n이뿐 아니라 캐디는 자기 말대로 \"살림을 배우고\" 싶어 무척 안달이었습니다. 그런데, 세상에! 어마어마한 경륜을 지닌 내게서 살림을 배운다는 발상이 어찌나 우스웠던지, 그 애가 그러자고 했을 때 나는 웃음을 터뜨리고, 얼굴이 붉어지고, 우스꽝스러운 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 꼴에 빠지고 말았습니다. 그래도 나는 \"캐디, 나한테서 배울 수 있는 거라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 가르쳐줄게\" 하고는, 내 장부며 방식이며 내 까탈스러운 버릇을 죄다 보여주었습니다. 그 애가 그것들을 들여다보는 모습을 보면, 내가 무슨 놀라운 발명품이라도 보여주는 줄 알았을 겁니다. 그리고 내가 살림 열쇠를 짤랑거릴 때마다 그 애가 벌떡 일어나 시중을 드는 걸 보았다면, 캐디 젤리비처럼 눈먼 추종자를 거느린 나처럼 대단한 사기꾼은 세상에 없으리라 여겼을 게 분명합니다.\n\n그렇게 일하고 살림하고, 찰리에게 글을 가르치고, 저녁이면 후견인과 주사위 놀이를 하고, 에이다와 이중창을 부르는 사이 삼 주가 후딱 지나갔습니다. 그러고 나서 나는 캐디와 함께 그 애 집으로 가 거기서 무엇을 할 수 있을지 보았고, 에이다와 찰리는 후견인을 보살피러 남았습니다.\n\n캐디와 함께 그 애 집으로 갔다고 했지만, 해턴 가든의 가구 딸린 셋방을 말하는 겁니다. 우리는 뉴먼 거리에도 두세 번 갔는데, 거기서도 채비가 진행 중이었습니다 — 보아하니 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 안락을 더하기 위한 것이 꽤 많았고, 갓 결혼한 부부를 집 맨 위층에 값싸게 들이기 위한 것은 몇 가지뿐이었습니다 — 하지만 우리의 큰 목표는 가구 딸린 셋방을 결혼식 아침 식사에 어울리게 그럴듯하게 꾸미고, 젤리비 부인에게 미리 그날의 행사에 대한 어렴풋한 감각이나마 불어넣는 것이었습니다.\n\n둘 중에 후자가 더 어려운 일이었습니다. 젤리비 부인과 병약한 사내아이가 앞쪽 거실을 차지하고 있었는데(뒤쪽 방은 그저 벽장이나 다름없었지요), 그곳은 어수선한 마구간에 짚이 깔리듯 폐지와 보리오불라 서류들이 어지러이 널려 있었습니다. 젤리비 부인은 온종일 거기 앉아 진한 커피를 들이켜고, 받아쓰기를 시키고, 약속을 잡아 보리오불라 면담을 했습니다. 병약한 사내아이는 내가 보기에 시름시름 야위어가는 듯했는데, 끼니는 집 밖에서 해결했습니다. 젤리비 씨는 집에 돌아오면 으레 끙 앓는 소리를 내며 부엌으로 내려갔습니다. 거기서 하녀가 무어라도 내주면 요기를 했고, 그러고 나면 자기가 거치적거린다는 생각에 밖으로 나가 비를 맞으며 해턴 가든을 거닐었습니다. 가엾은 아이들은 늘 하던 대로 집을 기어오르다 굴러떨어지곤 했지요.\n\n일주일이라는 짧은 말미에 이 가엾은 어린 희생양들을 봐줄 만한 꼴로 꾸며낸다는 건 도무지 어림없는 일이었으므로, 나는 캐디에게 제안하기를, 결혼식 날 아침 그 애들이 다 같이 자는 다락방에서 할 수 있는 만큼 즐겁게 해주고, 우리의 가장 큰 노력은 그 애 엄마와 엄마 방, 그리고 깔끔한 아침 식사에 쏟자고 했습니다. 사실 젤리비 부인은 손이 꽤 많이 가는 분이었습니다. 등의 격자 모양 끈은 내가 처음 알았을 적보다 한참 벌어져 있었고, 머리카락은 청소부 말의 갈기 같았으니까요.\n\n캐디의 혼수를 펼쳐 보이는 것이 이 화제로 다가서는 가장 좋은 방편이겠다 싶어, 나는 병약한 사내아이가 돌아간 뒤 저녁에 젤리비 부인을 청해, 침대에 펼쳐놓은 그것을 보러 오게 했습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양,\" 부인이 평소의 더없이 온화한 기색으로 책상에서 일어나며 말했습니다. \"이건 정말이지 우스꽝스러운 채비예요. 그래도 당신이 거드는 건 당신의 친절을 보여주는 증표지요. 캐디가 결혼한다는 생각만 하면 어쩐지 말로 다 못 할 만큼 우습기 짝이 없다우! 아, 캐디, 이 바보, 바보, 바보 같은 것아!\"\n\n그래도 부인은 우리와 함께 위층으로 올라와 늘 그러듯 동떨어진 태도로 옷가지를 들여다보았습니다. 그것들은 부인에게 한 가지 또렷한 생각을 일깨운 모양이었습니다. 부인이 차분한 미소를 띤 채 고개를 저으며 말했으니까요. \"착한 서머슨 양, 이 반값이면 이 가냘픈 아이를 아프리카로 보낼 채비를 갖출 수 있었을 텐데!\"\n\n다시 아래층으로 내려가자, 젤리비 부인은 이 성가신 일이 정말로 다음 수요일에 벌어지는 게 맞느냐고 나에게 물었습니다. 그렇다고 대답하자 부인은 이렇게 말했습니다. \"내 방이 필요한가요, 서머슨 양? 내 서류를 치운다는 건 도무지 불가능한 일이라서요.\"\n\n나는 외람되게도 그 방이 분명 필요할 거라고, 그러니 서류를 어딘가에 치워두어야 할 것 같다고 말했습니다. \"글쎄요, 서머슨 양,\" 젤리비 부인이 말했습니다. \"당신이 제일 잘 알겠지요. 하지만 캐디가 나를 사내아이까지 부리도록 떠밀어, 가뜩이나 공무에 파묻혀 사는 나를 어찌나 곤란하게 만드는지, 도무지 어느 쪽으로 돌아서야 할지 모르겠어요. 게다가 수요일 오후에는 지부 회의도 있어서, 여간 불편한 게 아니랍니다.\"\n\n\"이런 일이 또 일어나진 않겠지요,\" 나는 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"캐디는 아마 결혼을 딱 한 번만 할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 맞아요,\" 젤리비 부인이 대답했습니다. \"맞는 말이에요. 어떻게든 잘 넘겨야겠지요!\"\n\n다음 문제는 그날 젤리비 부인이 무엇을 입을 것인가였습니다. 캐디와 내가 그 일을 의논하는 동안 부인이 글쓰는 탁자에서 태연히 지켜보다가, 우리의 하찮은 짓을 겨우 견뎌주는 우월한 영혼처럼 절반쯤 나무라는 미소를 띠고 이따금 고개를 젓는 걸 보니 무척 묘했습니다.\n\n부인의 옷가지가 어떤 상태인지, 또 그것을 얼마나 유별나게 어질러놓고 사는지가 우리의 어려움에 적잖이 보탬이 되었습니다. 하지만 마침내 우리는 그런 자리에 평범한 어머니가 입을 법한 것과 그리 다르지 않은 무언가를 마련해냈습니다. 옷 만드는 여자가 그 차림을 입혀보는 동안 젤리비 부인이 넋을 놓고 몸을 내맡기던 모습이며, 그러고 나서 내가 아프리카로 생각을 돌리지 않은 게 못내 안타깝다고 더없이 온화하게 나에게 말하던 모습은, 부인의 다른 처신과 한결같았습니다.\n\n그 셋방은 공간이 다소 비좁았지만, 나는 이런 상상을 했습니다. 설령 젤리비 부인의 식솔이 세인트폴 대성당이나 세인트피터 대성당의 유일한 세입자였다 한들, 그 건물의 크기에서 찾아낼 수 있는 단 하나의 이점은 더럽힐 자리가 넉넉하다는 것뿐이었으리라고요. 캐디의 결혼을 위한 그 채비가 한창일 무렵, 그 집안에 속한 것 가운데 깨뜨릴 수 있었던 것치고 깨지지 않은 게 없었고, 어떤 식으로든 망가뜨릴 수 있었던 것치고 망가지지 않은 게 없었으며, 사랑스러운 아이의 무릎부터 문패에 이르기까지 먼지가 쌓일 만한 살림살이치고 쌓일 수 있는 만큼 먼지가 잔뜩 끼지 않은 게 없었다고 나는 믿습니다.\n\n좀처럼 말이 없고 집에 있을 때면 거의 늘 머리를 벽에 기댄 채 앉아 있던 가엾은 젤리비 씨는, 캐디와 내가 이 온갖 폐물과 잔해 속에서 얼마간 질서를 세워보려 애쓰는 걸 보더니 관심이 동했는지, 윗옷을 벗고 거들었습니다. 그런데 벽장을 열자 어찌나 놀라운 것들이 우르르 쏟아져 나오던지 — 곰팡이 슨 파이 조각, 시어버린 병들, 젤리비 부인의 모자들, 편지들, 차, 포크들, 짝 안 맞는 아이들 장화와 신발, 땔감, 봉인지, 냄비 뚜껑들, 여기저기 종이봉지 자투리에 든 눅눅한 설탕, 발받침들, 흑연 솔들, 빵, 젤리비 부인의 보닛들, 제본에 버터가 들러붙은 책들, 부러진 촛대에 거꾸로 처박혀 꺼진 흘러내린 양초 토막들, 호두 껍데기들, 새우 대가리와 꼬리들, 식탁 깔개들, 장갑들, 커피 찌꺼기, 우산들 — 그는 겁먹은 얼굴이 되어 다시 손을 놓았습니다. 그래도 그는 저녁마다 어김없이 와서, 윗옷도 벗어둔 채 머리를 벽에 기대고 앉아, 마치 방법만 안다면 우리를 거들기라도 할 것처럼 있었습니다.\n\n\"가엾은 아빠!\" 큰 날을 하루 앞둔 밤, 정말로 어지간히 정돈이 되었을 무렵 캐디가 나에게 말했습니다. \"아빠를 두고 떠나는 게 매정한 일 같아, 에스더. 하지만 내가 남는다고 무얼 할 수 있겠어! 너를 처음 알게 된 뒤로 치우고 또 치우고 거듭 치워봤지만, 다 소용없는 일이야. 엄마랑 아프리카가 합세하면 온 집을 대번에 뒤집어놓거든. 우리 집엔 술 안 마시는 하녀라곤 한 명도 없어. 엄마는 모든 걸 망쳐놓고 마셔.\"\n\n젤리비 씨는 그 애가 한 말을 들을 수 없었지만, 무척이나 풀이 죽어 보였고, 내 보기에 눈물을 흘렸습니다.\n\n\"아빠 생각만 하면 가슴이 아파. 정말이야!\" 캐디가 흐느꼈습니다. \"오늘 밤 자꾸만 이런 생각이 들어, 에스더. 내가 얼마나 간절히 프린스와 행복하길 바라는지, 그리고 아빠도 분명 얼마나 간절히 엄마와 행복하길 바라셨을지. 얼마나 좌절뿐인 인생인지!\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 캐디!\" 젤리비 씨가 벽에서 천천히 고개를 돌리며 말했습니다. 그가 단어 세 개를 잇달아 말하는 걸 들은 건 그때가 처음이었지 싶습니다.\n\n\"네, 아빠!\" 캐디가 외치며 다가가 다정하게 그를 끌어안았습니다.\n\n\"사랑하는 캐디,\" 젤리비 씨가 말했습니다. \"절대 갖지 마—\"\n\n\"프린스 말고요, 아빠?\" 캐디가 더듬거렸습니다. \"프린스를 갖지 말라고요?\"\n\n\"아니다, 얘야,\" 젤리비 씨가 말했습니다. \"그 애는 당연히 가져야지. 하지만, 절대 갖지 마—\"\n\n타비스 인에서의 첫 방문을 이야기할 때, 리처드가 젤리비 씨를 두고 저녁 식사 뒤에 아무 말도 없이 입만 자주 벌리던 사람으로 묘사했다고 적었습니다. 그건 그분의 버릇이었지요. 그는 지금도 여러 번 입을 벌리고는 서글픈 기색으로 고개를 저었습니다.\n\n\"제가 무얼 갖지 말기를 바라세요? 무얼 갖지 말라는 거예요, 사랑하는 아빠?\" 캐디가 그의 목에 팔을 두른 채 어르며 물었습니다.\n\n\"절대 사명을 갖지 마라, 사랑하는 내 딸아.\"\n\n젤리비 씨는 끙 앓는 소리를 내며 다시 벽에 머리를 기댔는데, 그가 보리오불라 문제에 대한 심경을 표현하려는 기색을 조금이라도 내비치는 걸 내가 들은 건 그때가 유일했습니다. 한때는 그도 더 말이 많고 활기찼으리라 짐작하지만, 내가 알기 한참 전에 이미 완전히 기진맥진해버린 듯했습니다.\n\n그날 밤 젤리비 부인은 태연히 서류를 들여다보고 커피를 마시는 일을 도무지 그만둘 줄 모를 것 같았습니다. 우리가 그 방을 차지하게 된 건 열두 시가 되어서였는데, 그때 치워야 할 것이 어찌나 막막하던지 거의 녹초가 된 캐디는 먼지 한가운데 주저앉아 울었습니다. 하지만 곧 기운을 차렸고, 우리는 잠자리에 들기 전에 그 방을 놀랍도록 바꿔놓았습니다.\n\n아침이 되자 그 방은 꽃 몇 송이와 넉넉한 비누 물, 약간의 정리 덕에 제법 화사해 보였습니다. 소박한 아침 식탁은 흥겨운 광경을 자아냈고, 캐디는 더없이 사랑스러웠습니다. 하지만 내 귀염둥이가 왔을 때, 나는 생각했습니다 — 지금도 생각합니다 — 내 아름다운 귀염둥이만큼 사랑스러운 얼굴은 한 번도 본 적이 없다고요.\n\n우리는 위층에서 아이들에게 작은 잔치를 베풀어주고, 피피를 식탁 상석에 앉히고, 신부 차림을 한 캐디를 보여주었더니, 아이들은 손뼉을 치며 만세를 불렀습니다. 캐디는 그 애들을 떠난다는 생각에 울며 몇 번이고 끌어안았고, 마침내 우리가 프린스를 데려와 그 애를 데려가게 했을 때 — 안타깝게도, 피피가 그를 깨물었습니다. 그러고 나서 아래층에는 이루 말할 수 없는 풍채를 갖춘 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 있었는데, 캐디에게 자애로이 축복을 내리고, 아들의 행복은 다름 아닌 자기가 부모로서 이룬 일이며 그것을 보장하려고 개인적인 사정을 희생했다고 후견인에게 넌지시 알렸습니다. \"이보십시오,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 젊은이들은 저와 함께 살 겁니다. 제 집은 그들을 들이기에 넉넉하니, 제 지붕의 보살핌을 마다하게 하지 않을 겁니다. 저는 바랐습니다만 — 빗댄 뜻을 아시겠지요, 잔다이스 씨, 제 고귀한 후원자이신 섭정 왕세자 전하를 기억하실 테니 — 제 아들이 풍채가 더 갖춰진 가문과 혼인하기를 바랐습니다만, 하늘의 뜻대로 되었지요!\"\n\n파디글 부부도 한자리에 있었습니다 — 파디글 씨는 고집스러운 인상에 커다란 조끼를 걸치고 뻣뻣한 머리를 한 사내로, 늘 굵직한 저음으로 자기 헌금이며, 파디글 부인의 헌금이며, 다섯 아들의 헌금에 대해 떠들어댔습니다. 퀘일 씨도 와 있었는데, 늘 그렇듯 머리를 뒤로 빗어 넘기고 관자놀이 혹이 번들번들 빛나는 채였습니다. 그는 실연당한 연인이 아니라, 그 자리에 함께 온 어느 젊은 — 적어도 미혼인 — 숙녀, 위스크 양에게 받아들여진 사람으로 와 있었습니다. 위스크 양의 사명은, 후견인 말로는, 여자의 사명이 곧 남자의 사명임을, 그리고 남자와 여자 모두의 유일하게 참된 사명이란 공개 모임에서 온갖 일에 대해 늘 선언적 결의안을 발의하는 것임을 세상에 보여주는 것이었습니다. 손님은 적었지만, 젤리비 부인 댁에서 으레 그러하듯, 하나같이 오로지 공적인 대의에만 헌신하는 이들이었습니다. 앞서 말한 이들 말고도, 보닛을 온통 비뚜로 쓰고 옷에 가격표가 그대로 달려 있는 무척 지저분한 부인이 있었는데, 캐디 말로는 그 부인이 방치한 집은 더러운 황야 같지만 그 부인의 교회는 자선 바자회 같다고 했습니다. 자기 사명은 모든 사람의 형제가 되는 것이라면서도 정작 자기 대가족 전체와는 냉랭한 사이로 보이는, 무척 시비조인 신사 한 명이 그 무리를 완성했습니다.\n\n이런 행사와 이토록 어울리지 않는 무리를 어떤 재간을 부려도 좀처럼 그러모으기 어려웠을 겁니다. 가정의 사명처럼 하찮은 사명은 그들 사이에서 도무지 참아낼 수 없는 것이었습니다. 사실, 위스크 양은 아침 식사 자리에 앉기도 전에 우리에게 잔뜩 분개하며 알려주기를, 여자의 사명이 주로 가정이라는 좁은 영역에 있다는 발상은 폭군인 남자가 저지른 터무니없는 비방이라고 했습니다. 또 한 가지 별난 점은, 사명을 지닌 이들 가운데 누구도 — 앞서 말한 것 같은데, 모든 사람의 사명에 황홀해하는 게 사명인 퀘일 씨만 빼고 — 남의 사명에는 도통 관심이 없다는 것이었습니다. 파디글 부인은 유일하게 그르치지 않는 길이란 가난한 이들을 덮쳐 구속복을 입히듯 자선을 들이붓는 자기 방식뿐이라고 확신했고, 위스크 양은 세상에 유일하게 실질적인 일이란 폭군 남자의 굴레에서 여자를 해방하는 것이라고 확신했습니다. 그러는 내내 젤리비 부인은 보리오불라가 말고는 무엇이든 눈에 담을 수 있는 그 좁은 시야를 향해 미소를 지으며 앉아 있었습니다.\n\n하지만 지금 나는 캐디부터 결혼시키지 않고 집으로 돌아오는 마차 안에서 나눈 대화의 요지를 앞당겨 말하고 있군요. 우리는 모두 교회로 갔고, 젤리비 씨가 그 애를 신랑에게 건넸습니다. 식이 진행되는 동안 늙은 터비드롭 씨가, 왼팔 아래에 모자를 끼고(그 안쪽이 마치 대포처럼 목사를 향했지요) 두 눈을 가발 속으로 잔뜩 찡그린 채, 빳빳하게 높은 어깨를 하고 우리 들러리들 뒤에 서 있다가 식이 끝난 뒤 우리에게 인사하던 그 태도란, 아무리 말해도 제대로 옮기기 어려웠습니다. 외모가 호감을 준다고는 도무지 전할 수 없고 태도도 험상궂은 위스크 양은, 그 절차를 여자가 당하는 부당함의 한 대목으로 여기며 경멸 어린 얼굴로 지켜보았습니다. 젤리비 부인은 차분한 미소와 초롱초롱한 눈으로, 그 자리에서 가장 무심한 사람처럼 보였습니다.\n\n우리는 어김없이 아침 식사를 하러 돌아왔고, 젤리비 부인이 식탁 상석에, 젤리비 씨가 말석에 앉았습니다. 캐디는 앞서 위층으로 살그머니 올라가 아이들을 다시 한번 끌어안고는 이제 자기 이름이 터비드롭이라고 일러주었습니다. 그런데 이 소식은 피피에게 반가운 놀라움이 되기는커녕, 그 애를 발버둥 치는 슬픔의 발작에 빠뜨려 벌렁 자빠뜨렸으니, 불려간 나로서는 그 애를 아침 식탁에 끼워주자는 제안에 응하는 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었습니다. 그래서 그 애가 내려와 내 무릎에 앉았고, 젤리비 부인은 그 애 턱받이 꼴을 두고 \"어머, 이 못된 피피야, 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍한 새끼돼지람!\" 하고는 조금도 마음이 흐트러지지 않았습니다. 그 애는 무척 얌전했는데, 다만 노아를 들고 내려와(교회에 가기 전에 내가 준 방주에서 꺼낸 것이었지요) 기어이 노아를 거꾸로 포도주 잔에 담갔다가 입에 넣곤 했습니다.\n\n후견인은 그 다정한 성품과 빠른 눈치, 그리고 상냥한 얼굴로, 이 살가울 데 없는 무리에서마저 무언가 흥겨운 것을 빚어냈습니다. 그들 가운데 누구도 자기만의 단 하나의 주제 말고는 도무지 이야기할 줄 모르는 듯했고, 더구나 그 주제마저도 다른 무언가가 있는 세상의 일부로서 이야기할 줄은 도무지 모르는 듯했습니다. 하지만 후견인은 그 모든 것을 캐디를 흥겹게 북돋우고 그날의 영예를 기리는 쪽으로 돌려, 우리를 훌륭하게 아침 식사 자리에서 이끌어냈습니다. 그가 없었다면 우리가 어떻게 했을지 생각만 해도 두렵습니다. 손님 모두가 신랑신부와 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 깔보고 — 게다가 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 자기 풍채에 힘입어 자기가 손님 모두보다 한참 우월하다고 여겼으니 — 여간 막막한 형편이 아니었으니까요.\n\n마침내 가엾은 캐디가 떠나야 할 때가 왔고, 그 애와 남편을 그레이브젠드로 데려갈 세낸 마차와 두 마리 말에 그 애 짐이 죄다 실렸습니다. 그때 캐디가 그 한심한 집에 매달려, 더없는 애틋함으로 어머니 목에 안기는 걸 보니 우리는 마음이 뭉클했습니다.\n\n\"받아쓰기를 계속 못 해드려서 정말 죄송해요, 엄마,\" 캐디가 흐느꼈습니다. \"이제 저를 용서해주셨으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"아, 캐디, 캐디!\" 젤리비 부인이 말했습니다. \"내가 너한테 거듭거듭 말하지 않았니, 사내아이를 들였다고. 그걸로 끝난 일이야.\"\n\n\"엄마, 정말 저한테 조금도 화 안 나신 거 맞죠? 가기 전에 화 안 났다고 말씀해주세요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"이 어리석은 캐디야,\" 젤리비 부인이 대답했습니다. \"내가 화난 얼굴이니, 화낼 마음이라도 있니, 화낼 짬이라도 있니? 어쩜 그러니?\"\n\n\"제가 없는 동안 아빠를 좀 챙겨주세요, 엄마!\"\n\n젤리비 부인은 그 발상에 영락없이 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다. \"이 공상에 빠진 아이야,\" 부인이 캐디의 등을 가볍게 토닥이며 말했습니다. \"어서 가렴. 나는 너랑 더없이 좋은 사이란다. 자, 잘 가, 캐디, 부디 행복하렴!\"\n\n그러고 나서 캐디는 아버지에게 매달려, 마치 그가 아픔에 잠긴 가엾고 멍한 아이라도 되는 양 그의 뺨을 제 뺨에 어루만졌습니다. 이 모든 일이 현관에서 벌어졌습니다. 그 애 아버지는 그 애를 놓아주고, 손수건을 꺼내, 머리를 벽에 기댄 채 계단에 주저앉았습니다. 그가 벽에서 얼마간의 위안을 얻었기를 바랍니다. 정말 그랬을 것 같기도 합니다.\n\n그러고 나서 프린스가 그 애 팔짱을 끼고는, 더없는 감동과 존경을 담아 아버지에게로 돌아섰는데, 그 순간 아버지의 풍채는 압도적이었습니다.\n\n\"거듭거듭 감사드립니다, 아버지!\" 프린스가 아버지의 손에 입 맞추며 말했습니다. \"우리 결혼에 베풀어주신 온갖 친절과 배려에 정말 깊이 감사드립니다. 분명 말씀드리지만, 캐디도 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"정말로요,\" 캐디가 흐느꼈습니다. \"정-말로요!\"\n\n\"사랑하는 내 아들아,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"그리고 사랑하는 내 딸아, 나는 내 도리를 다했다. 만일 성스러운 여인의 영혼이 우리 머리 위를 맴돌며 이 자리를 굽어본다면, 그것과 너희의 한결같은 애정이 나의 보답이 되리라. 너희도 너희 도리를 저버리지 않으리라 믿는다, 내 아들아, 내 딸아?\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아버지, 결코 그러지 않겠습니다!\" 프린스가 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"결코, 결코 그러지 않겠어요, 사랑하는 터비드롭 씨!\" 캐디가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래야 마땅하지,\" 터비드롭 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"내 아이들아, 내 집은 너희 것이고, 내 마음도 너희 것이며, 내 모든 것이 너희 것이다. 나는 결코 너희를 떠나지 않으리라. 오직 죽음만이 우리를 갈라놓으리라. 사랑하는 내 아들아, 일주일쯤 자리를 비울 작정인 게지?\"\n\n\"일주일이요, 사랑하는 아버지. 오늘로부터 일주일째 되는 날 집으로 돌아오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 내 아이야,\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"지금 이 예외적인 상황에서라도, 엄정하게 시간을 지키기를 당부하마. 거래처를 한데 묶어두는 게 무척 중요하니라. 학원이란 조금이라도 소홀히 하면 토라지기 십상이거든.\"\n\n\"오늘로부터 일주일째 되는 날, 아버지, 저희는 꼭 저녁 식사에 맞춰 집에 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"사랑하는 캐럴라인, 네 방에는 난롯불이 피워져 있을 것이고, 저녁 식사는 내 거처에 차려져 있을 게다. 그래, 그래, 프린스!\" 무언가 사양하는 반론을 펴려는 아들을 거창한 기색으로 앞질러 막으며 말했습니다. \"너와 우리 캐럴라인은 위층 살림이 낯설 테니, 그날은 내 거처에서 저녁을 들도록 해라. 자, 너희에게 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n그들은 마차를 타고 떠났고, 내가 젤리비 부인에게 더 놀랐는지 터비드롭 씨에게 더 놀랐는지 나로서는 알 수 없었습니다. 우리가 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나눌 때 에이다와 후견인도 같은 처지였습니다. 하지만 우리도 마차를 타고 떠나기 전에, 나는 젤리비 씨에게서 더없이 뜻밖이면서도 절절한 찬사를 받았습니다. 그는 현관에서 나에게 다가와 내 두 손을 잡고는, 간절하게 꼭 누르며, 입을 두 번 벌렸습니다. 나는 그분의 뜻을 어찌나 확신했던지, 무척 허둥대며 이렇게 말했습니다. \"천만에요, 선생님. 부디 마음 쓰지 마세요!\"\n\n\"이 결혼이 잘되는 거였으면 좋겠어요, 후견인,\" 우리 셋이 집으로 가는 길에 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그러길 바라야지, 꼬마 아가씨. 인내심을 갖자꾸나. 두고 보면 알겠지.\"\n\n\"오늘은 바람이 동쪽에서 부나요?\" 나는 외람되이 그에게 물어보았습니다.\n\n그는 호탕하게 웃으며 대답했습니다. \"아니.\"\n\n\"하지만 오늘 아침엔 분명 그랬던 것 같아요,\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n그는 다시 \"아니\"라고 대답했고, 이번에는 내 사랑하는 아이도 자신만만하게 \"아니\"라고 대답하며, 금빛 머리카락에 활짝 핀 꽃을 단, 영락없는 봄날 같은 그 어여쁜 머리를 흔들었습니다. \"동풍에 대해 참 많이도 아는구나, 못난 내 귀염둥이야,\" 나는 탄복한 나머지 그 애에게 입을 맞추며 말했습니다 — 도무지 그러지 않을 수 없었으니까요.\n\n그래요! 그건 그저 나를 향한 그들의 사랑이었을 뿐임을 나는 잘 알며, 한참 전 일입니다. 다시 지워버릴지언정 나는 이것을 적어두어야겠습니다. 적어두면 나에게 큰 기쁨이 되니까요. 그들은 누군가가 있는 곳에는 동풍이 불 리 없다고 했습니다. 더든 아주머니가 가는 곳에는 어디든 햇살과 여름 공기가 있다고 했습니다.\n\n집에 다시 돌아온 지 며칠 지나지 않은 어느 날 저녁, 나는 위층 내 방으로 올라가 찰리의 어깨너머로 글씨 연습장을 들여다보며 얼마나 늘었는지 살펴보았습니다. 찰리에게 글씨 쓰기는 여간 힘겨운 일이 아니었습니다. 펜을 다루는 타고난 재주가 도무지 없는 듯해서, 찰리 손에 들리기만 하면 펜이라는 펜은 죄다 심술궂게 살아나 비뚤배뚤 엇나가고, 멈췄다가 잉크를 튀기고, 안장 얹은 당나귀처럼 모퉁이로 슬금슬금 비켜갔습니다. 찰리의 어린 손이 어찌나 늙은 글자를 만들어내는지 보고 있으면 참 이상했습니다. 글자는 주름지고 쪼그라들고 비틀거리는데, 손은 통통하고 동그스름했으니 말입니다. 그래도 찰리는 다른 일에는 유난히 재주가 빼어났고, 내가 본 어느 누구보다 손놀림이 야무지고 잽쌌습니다.\n\n\"자, 찰리.\" 나는 네모나기도 하고 세모나기도 하고 배 모양으로 늘어지기도 하고 갖가지로 찌그러진 'O' 자가 늘어선 연습장을 들여다보며 말했습니다. \"점점 나아지는구나. 이걸 동그랗게만 쓸 수 있으면 완벽해지겠다, 찰리.\"\n\n그러고서 내가 하나 써 보이고 찰리도 하나 써 보았는데, 펜은 찰리의 글자를 말끔하게 잇지 못하고 매듭처럼 배배 꼬아놓고 말았습니다.\n\n\"괜찮아, 찰리. 시간이 지나면 되게 마련이야.\"\n\n연습이 끝나자 찰리는 펜을 내려놓고, 뻣뻣하게 굳은 작은 손을 폈다 쥐었다 하더니, 절반은 자랑스럽고 절반은 미덥지 못한 표정으로 연습장을 진지하게 들여다본 뒤, 일어나서 나에게 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 인사했습니다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아가씨. 그런데 아가씨, 혹시 제니라는 가엾은 여자분을 아세요?\"\n\n\"벽돌공 아내 말이니, 찰리? 그래, 알지.\"\n\n\"조금 전에 제가 바깥에 나갔을 때 와서 말을 걸었어요. 아가씨가 자기를 안다고 하던데요. 제가 그 아가씨네 어린 하녀가 아니냐고 묻기에—그 아가씨가 바로 아가씨를 두고 한 말이에요, 아가씨—제가 그렇다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"난 그 여자가 이 동네를 아예 떠난 줄 알았는데, 찰리.\"\n\n\"그랬대요, 아가씨. 그런데 예전에 살던 데로 다시 돌아왔대요—리즈하고 같이요. 아가씨, 혹시 리즈라는 다른 가엾은 여자분도 아세요?\"\n\n\"알 것 같구나, 찰리. 이름은 몰라도 말이야.\"\n\n\"그 여자분도 바로 그렇게 말했어요!\" 찰리가 받았습니다. \"둘 다 돌아왔는데, 아가씨, 여기저기 정처 없이 떠돌아다녔대요.\"\n\n\"여기저기 떠돌아다녔다고, 찰리?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 내 얼굴을 들여다보는 그 동그란 눈만큼 연습장 글자를 동그랗게 쓸 수 있었다면 더없이 훌륭했을 것입니다. \"그 가엾은 여자분이 아가씨를 한 번이라도 뵙고 싶어서 사나흘 동안 집 근처를 서성였대요—그것만 바랐다고요—그런데 아가씨가 안 계셨던 거예요. 그때 저를 본 거고요. 제가 돌아다니는 걸 봤대요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 더없이 기쁘고 자랑스러운 듯 짧게 웃으며 말했습니다. \"그러고는 제가 아가씨 하녀처럼 보인다고 생각했대요!\"\n\n\"정말 그랬다니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨!\" 찰리가 말했습니다. \"정말로, 진짜로요.\" 그러고서 찰리는 더없이 순수한 환희로 다시 짧게 웃고는, 눈을 또 동그랗게 뜨고 내 하녀답게 자못 진지한 표정을 지었습니다. 나는 찰리가 그 대단한 위엄을 한껏 누리며 내 앞에 서 있는 모습을, 그 앳된 얼굴과 자태와 의젓한 몸가짐, 그리고 그 사이로 이따금 더없이 사랑스럽게 터져 나오는 어린아이 같은 환희를 보고 또 보아도 도무지 싫증이 나지 않았습니다.\n\n\"그래서 그 여자를 어디서 봤니, 찰리?\" 나는 물었습니다.\n\n내 작은 하녀의 얼굴빛이 어두워지며 대답했습니다. \"약방 앞에서요, 아가씨.\" 찰리는 아직 검은 옷을 입고 있었으니까요.\n\n나는 벽돌공 아내가 아픈 거냐고 물었지만, 찰리는 아니라고 했습니다. 다른 사람이라는 거였습니다. 그 오두막에 머무는 누군가가 세인트올번스까지 걸어 내려왔는데, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 떠돌고 있다고 했습니다. 가엾은 사내아이라고, 찰리가 말했습니다. 아버지도 어머니도, 아무도 없다고요. \"톰도, 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤에 엠마하고 제가 죽었더라면 저렇게 됐을지도 몰라요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 말하는데 동그란 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐습니다.\n\n\"그래서 그 아이한테 줄 약을 사고 있었니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"그 아이가 예전에 자기한테 그만큼 해준 적이 있다고 했어요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 대답했습니다.\n\n내 작은 하녀의 얼굴은 어찌나 간절한지, 또 나를 바라보며 서 있는 그 얌전한 두 손을 어찌나 꼭 맞잡고 있는지, 나는 그 마음을 읽어내기가 그리 어렵지 않았습니다. \"그래, 찰리.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"내가 보기엔 너와 내가 제니네 집에 가서 무슨 일인지 알아보는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없겠구나.\"\n\n찰리가 내 모자와 베일을 가져와 나에게 씌워주고는, 자기도 따뜻한 숄을 옛스럽게 핀으로 여며 작은 노파처럼 차려입는 그 날렵함만으로도 채비가 다 됐음을 충분히 알 수 있었습니다. 그렇게 찰리와 나는 아무에게도 말하지 않고 집을 나섰습니다.\n\n춥고 사나운 밤이었고, 나무들이 바람에 부르르 떨었습니다. 비가 하루 종일 굵고 세차게 내렸고, 여러 날 동안 거의 그치지 않고 쏟아졌습니다. 그래도 그 순간만큼은 비가 내리지 않았습니다. 하늘은 어느 정도 개었지만 무척 음울했습니다—몇 개의 별이 반짝이는 머리 위쪽조차 그러했습니다. 세 시간 전에 해가 진 북쪽과 북서쪽에는 아름다우면서도 무시무시한 창백한 죽은 빛이 깔려 있었고, 그 속으로 길게 뻗은 음울한 구름 줄기가 일렁이다 그대로 굳어버린 바다처럼 너울거리며 솟구쳐 올랐습니다. 런던 쪽으로는 시뻘건 불빛이 그 어두운 황야 전체를 뒤덮었는데, 이 두 빛이 빚어내는 대조와, 그 붉은 빛이 불러일으키는 환상—이 세상 것이 아닌 불길이 도시의 보이지 않는 온갖 건물과 경탄에 잠긴 수천수만 주민의 얼굴 위에서 번득이는 듯한 환상은—이루 말할 수 없이 장엄했습니다.\n\n그날 밤 나에게는 곧 닥쳐올 일에 대한 어떤 예감도 없었습니다—전혀, 정말 단언하건대 없었습니다. 그러나 그 뒤로 늘 기억합니다. 정원 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 하늘을 올려다보았을 때, 그리고 다시 길을 나섰을 때, 나는 한순간 내가 그때의 나와는 무언가 다른 존재라는, 뭐라 규정할 수 없는 느낌에 사로잡혔습니다. 바로 그때 그 자리에서 그 느낌을 받았다는 것을 압니다. 그 뒤로 나는 그 느낌을 그 장소와 그 시간에, 그리고 그 장소와 그 시간에 얽힌 모든 것—마을에서 들려오던 아득한 목소리들, 개 짖는 소리, 진창 언덕을 내려오던 수레바퀴 소리—에 늘 결부시키게 되었습니다.\n\n토요일 밤이었고, 우리가 가려는 곳에 사는 사람들은 대부분 다른 데서 술을 마시고 있었습니다. 그곳은 전에 봤을 때보다 조용했지만, 여전히 참담하기는 마찬가지였습니다. 가마들이 불타오르며 숨 막히는 수증기가 창백한 푸른빛을 띠고 우리 쪽으로 밀려왔습니다.\n\n우리는 그 오두막에 이르렀습니다. 누덕누덕 기운 창문에 희미한 촛불 하나가 켜져 있었습니다. 우리는 문을 두드리고 안으로 들어갔습니다. 죽은 어린아이의 어머니가 침대 옆 변변찮은 난롯가 한쪽에 의자를 놓고 앉아 있었고, 그 맞은편에는 비참한 행색의 사내아이 하나가 벽난로 선반에 몸을 기댄 채 바닥에 웅크리고 있었습니다. 아이는 작은 보따리라도 되는 양 모피 모자 조각 하나를 팔 밑에 끼고 있었습니다. 몸을 녹이려 애쓰면서도 어찌나 떨어대는지 다 망가진 문과 창문까지 덜컹덜컹 흔들렸습니다. 그곳은 전보다 더 후텁지근했고, 건강에 해로운 아주 묘한 냄새가 났습니다.\n\n처음 그 여자에게 말을 걸었을 때, 그러니까 우리가 막 들어선 그 순간에, 나는 아직 베일을 걷지 않았습니다. 사내아이는 곧장 비틀거리며 일어나더니, 놀라움과 공포가 뒤섞인 야릇한 표정으로 나를 빤히 쳐다보았습니다.\n\n아이의 동작이 어찌나 빠르고, 또 내가 그 원인이라는 게 어찌나 분명했던지, 나는 가까이 다가가는 대신 그 자리에 가만히 멈춰 섰습니다.\n\n\"무덤 구덩이엔 다신 안 가.\" 아이가 중얼거렸습니다. \"거긴 안 갈 거야, 그러니까 말해두는데!\"\n\n나는 베일을 걷고 여자에게 말을 건넸습니다. 여자는 낮은 목소리로 나에게 말했습니다. \"신경 쓰지 마세요, 마님. 곧 제정신이 돌아올 거예요.\" 그러고는 아이에게 말했습니다. \"조, 조, 왜 그래?\"\n\n\"저 여자가 뭐 하러 왔는지 다 안다고!\" 아이가 소리쳤습니다.\n\n\"누가?\"\n\n\"저기 저 마님 말이야. 나를 데리고 무덤 구덩이로 가려고 온 거야. 난 무덤 구덩이엔 안 가. 그 이름부터가 싫어. 나까지 파묻어버릴지도 몰라.\" 다시 오한이 도지자, 아이는 벽에 기댄 채 그 누추한 오두막을 흔들어댔습니다.\n\n\"하루 종일 저런 소리를 띄엄띄엄 해댔어요, 마님.\" 제니가 부드럽게 말했습니다. \"아니, 왜 그렇게 빤히 봐! 이분이 우리 마님이야, 조.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 아이가 미심쩍은 듯 받으며, 이글거리는 두 눈 위로 팔을 들어 가리고 나를 살폈습니다. \"내 눈엔 딴 사람으로 보이는데. 모자도 아니고 옷도 아닌데, 내 눈엔 딴 사람으로 보여.\"\n\n병과 고생을 일찍부터 겪어온 나의 어린 찰리는 모자와 숄을 벗어놓고, 이제 의자 하나를 들고 조용히 아이에게 다가가, 늙은 간병인처럼 아이를 의자에 앉혔습니다. 다만 그런 간병인이라면 아이에게 보여줄 수 없었을 찰리의 앳된 얼굴이, 아이의 마음을 사로잡는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"저기!\" 아이가 말했습니다. \"네가 말해봐. 저 마님이 딴 마님 아니야?\"\n\n찰리는 고개를 가로저으며 아이의 누더기를 차근차근 여며주고 할 수 있는 한 따뜻하게 해주었습니다.\n\n\"아!\" 아이가 중얼거렸습니다. \"그럼 딴 사람이 아닌가 보네.\"\n\n\"네가 좀 나아지게 도울 수 있을까 해서 왔단다.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"어디가 아프니?\"\n\n\"꽁꽁 얼었다가.\" 아이가 쉰 목소리로 받으며, 초췌한 눈길을 내 주위로 헤매게 했습니다. \"그다음엔 활활 타고, 또 얼었다가 또 타고, 한 시간에도 몇 번씩 그래. 머리는 온통 졸리고, 미쳐 돌아가는 것 같고—목은 바싹바싹 마르고—뼈마디는 뼈라기보다 통증 덩어리야.\"\n\n\"이 아이가 언제 여기 왔지요?\" 나는 여자에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 마을 모퉁이에서 발견했어요, 마님. 저 위 런던에서 알던 아이거든요. 그렇지, 조?\"\n\n\"톰 혼자 사는 곳.\" 아이가 대답했습니다.\n\n아이는 주의를 모으거나 눈을 한곳에 두어도, 그건 아주 잠깐뿐이었습니다. 곧 다시 고개를 떨구고는 무겁게 가누다가, 반쯤 잠든 사람처럼 말하기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"이 아이가 런던에서 언제 왔지요?\" 나는 물었습니다.\n\n\"난 어제 런던에서 왔어.\" 이제 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 아이가 직접 말했습니다. \"어디론가 가는 길이야.\"\n\n\"이 아이가 어디로 가는데요?\" 나는 물었습니다.\n\n\"어디론가.\" 아이가 더 큰 소리로 되풀이했습니다. \"그 딴 사람이 나한테 금화를 준 뒤로, 전보다 더 쫓겨 다니고 또 쫓겨 다녔어. 스내그스비 부인은 늘 나를 감시하면서 쫓아내—내가 그 여자한테 뭘 어쨌다고?—다들 나를 감시하고 쫓아내. 하나같이 그래, 내가 일어나지 못하는 때부터 잠자리에 들지 못하는 때까지. 그래서 난 어디론가 가는 거야. 그게 내가 가는 데야. 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 그 사람이 나한테 그랬어, 스톨번스에서 왔다고. 그래서 나도 스톨번스 길로 잡았어. 다른 데나 매한가지니까.\"\n\n아이는 늘 마지막엔 찰리에게 말을 건넸습니다.\n\n\"이 아이를 어떻게 하면 좋지?\" 나는 여자를 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했습니다. \"설령 목적지가 있고 어디로 가는지 안다 해도 이런 상태로는 길을 갈 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"저도 도무지 모르겠어요, 마님. 죽은 사람이나 다름없죠.\" 여자가 가엾다는 듯 아이를 흘끔 보며 대답했습니다. \"차라리 죽은 사람들이 더 잘 알지도 모르죠, 말만 해줄 수 있다면요. 가엾어서 하루 종일 여기 데리고 있으면서 죽도 먹이고 약도 줬어요. 리즈는 누가 받아줄지 알아보러 나갔고요(여기 이 침대에 누운 내 예쁜이는—저이 아이지만 내 아이라고 부른답니다). 그래도 오래는 못 데리고 있어요. 우리 남편이 집에 와서 이 애가 있는 걸 보면 거칠게 내쫓을 테고, 그러다 다치게 할지도 모르거든요. 들어보세요! 리즈가 돌아오네요!\"\n\n여자가 말하는 사이 다른 여자가 황급히 들어왔고, 이제 가야 하나 보다 하는 어렴풋한 느낌으로 아이가 일어섰습니다. 어린아이가 언제 깨어났는지, 또 찰리가 언제 어떻게 다가가 아이를 침대에서 안아 올려 어르면서 거닐기 시작했는지, 나는 모릅니다. 어느새 찰리는 그 모든 일을 조용하고 자애로운 손길로 해내고 있었습니다. 마치 블라인더 부인네 다락방에서 톰과 엠마를 다시 돌보고 있기라도 한 듯이요.\n\n그 친구는 이리저리 돌아다니며 이 손 저 손으로 떠밀리다, 떠날 때와 똑같은 빈손으로 돌아왔습니다. 처음엔 너무 일러서 아이를 제대로 된 보호소에 받아줄 수 없었고, 나중엔 너무 늦었던 것입니다. 한 관리가 그 여자를 다른 관리에게 보내고, 그 관리가 다시 처음 관리에게 돌려보내고, 그렇게 앞으로 뒤로 떠밀려 다닌 끝에, 나에게는 두 관리 모두 직무를 수행하라고가 아니라 회피하는 솜씨가 뛰어나다고 임명받은 게 아닌가 싶을 지경이었습니다. 그러고도 결국, 여자는 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했습니다—내내 뛰어다닌 데다 겁까지 먹은 탓이었습니다—\"제니, 자네 남편이 집에 오는 길이고, 우리 집 양반도 멀지 않았어. 하느님이 이 애를 도우시길, 우린 더는 해줄 게 없으니!\" 두 사람은 반 페니짜리 동전 몇 닢을 모아 아이 손에 황급히 쥐여주었고, 그렇게 아이는 멍하니, 절반은 고마워하고 절반은 아무 감각도 없는 채로, 발을 질질 끌며 집을 나섰습니다.\n\n\"아이를 이리 줘요, 새댁.\" 어린아이의 어머니가 찰리에게 말했습니다. \"고마워요, 정말로! 제니, 잘 자요, 우리 어진 아주머니! 아가씨, 우리 집 양반이랑 틀어지지만 않으면, 조금 있다 가마 쪽으로 내려가 볼게요. 그 애가 거기 있을 가능성이 제일 크니까요. 아침에 또 가보고요!\" 여자는 황급히 떠났고, 이내 우리는 그 여자가 제 집 문간에서 아이를 어르고 노래를 불러주며, 술 취한 남편이 오는지 길을 따라 애타게 살피는 곁을 지나쳤습니다.\n\n그때 나는 두 여자 중 누구와도 말을 나누려고 머물렀다가는 곤경에 빠뜨릴까 봐 두려웠습니다. 하지만 나는 찰리에게, 그 아이를 그냥 죽게 내버려둘 수는 없다고 말했습니다. 나보다 무엇을 해야 할지 훨씬 잘 알고, 침착함 못지않게 행동도 잰 찰리가 내 앞으로 미끄러지듯 나아갔고, 우리는 이내 벽돌 가마 바로 못 미친 곳에서 조를 따라잡았습니다.\n\n생각건대 아이는 작은 보따리를 팔 밑에 끼고 길을 나섰다가 그걸 도둑맞았거나 잃어버렸던 모양입니다. 이제 빠르게 쏟아지는 빗속을 맨머리로 걸으면서도, 그 비참한 모피 모자 조각을 여전히 보따리처럼 들고 있었으니까요. 우리가 부르자 아이는 멈춰 섰고, 내가 다가가자 다시 나를 두려워하며, 빛나는 두 눈을 나에게 고정한 채, 떨리던 발작까지 멎은 듯 우뚝 섰습니다.\n\n나는 아이에게 우리와 함께 가자고, 그러면 오늘 밤 묵을 곳을 마련해 주겠다고 했습니다.\n\n\"잘 데 따위 필요 없어.\" 아이가 말했습니다. \"따뜻한 벽돌 틈에 누우면 돼.\"\n\n\"하지만 거기서 사람들이 죽는 거 몰라?\" 찰리가 받았습니다.\n\n\"사람들은 어디서나 죽어.\" 아이가 말했습니다. \"셋방에서도 죽고—이 여자가 알아, 내가 보여줬으니까—톰 혼자 사는 곳에선 무더기로 죽어. 내가 보기엔 사는 사람보다 죽는 사람이 더 많아.\" 그러고는 쉰 목소리로 찰리에게 속삭였습니다. \"저 마님이 딴 마님이 아니면, 그 외국 여자도 아니네. 그럼 셋이나 되는 거야?\"\n\n찰리가 약간 겁먹은 얼굴로 나를 바라보았습니다. 아이가 나를 그렇게 노려보니 나 역시 내 자신이 조금 두려워졌습니다.\n\n그러나 내가 손짓하자 아이는 돌아서서 따라왔고, 아이가 나에게 그런 힘이 있음을 인정하는 것을 보고, 나는 곧장 집으로 앞장섰습니다. 멀지 않았습니다, 언덕 꼭대기였으니까요. 우리는 사람을 딱 한 명 마주쳤습니다. 아이의 발걸음이 어찌나 불안하고 후들거리는지, 도움 없이 집에 닿을 수 있을지 의문이었습니다. 그래도 아이는 아무 불평이 없었고, 이렇게 이상한 표현을 써도 된다면, 제 몸에 대해 이상하리만치 무심했습니다.\n\n나는 잠시 아이를 현관에 남겨두었는데, 아이는 창가 자리 구석에 잔뜩 웅크린 채, 주위의 안락함과 환함을 보면서도 경이로움이라고도 할 수 없는 무관심한 눈으로 빤히 바라보았습니다. 나는 후견인에게 말씀드리러 응접실로 들어갔습니다. 거기에는 스킴폴 씨가 와 있었는데, 자주 그러듯 기별도 없이 역마차를 타고 내려온 것이었습니다. 옷가지 하나 가져오는 법 없이, 필요한 건 무엇이든 늘 빌려 쓰는 사람이었습니다.\n\n두 사람은 곧장 나와 함께 아이를 보러 나갔습니다. 하인들도 현관에 모여 있었고, 아이는 도랑에서 발견된 상처 입은 짐승처럼 창가 자리에서 떨고 있었으며, 찰리가 그 곁에 서 있었습니다.\n\n\"딱한 경우로구나.\" 후견인이 아이에게 한두 마디 물어보고, 손을 짚어보고, 눈을 살펴본 뒤에 말했습니다. \"자넨 어떻게 생각하나, 해럴드?\"\n\n\"내쫓는 게 좋겠소.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 말인가?\" 후견인이 거의 준엄하다 싶게 물었습니다.\n\n\"이보게 잔다이스.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"내가 어떤 사람인지 자네도 알지 않나. 나는 어린애일세. 내가 그럴 만하면 나한테 화를 내게. 하지만 나는 이런 일에는 천성적으로 거부감이 있어. 의사 노릇을 할 때부터 늘 그랬지. 저 아이는 위험해, 알겠나. 몹시 고약한 열병에 걸려 있거든.\"\n\n스킴폴 씨는 현관에서 다시 응접실로 물러나, 우리가 곁에 선 가운데 피아노 의자에 앉아 그 특유의 가벼운 투로 이렇게 말했습니다.\n\n\"유치한 소리라고 하겠지.\" 스킴폴 씨가 우리를 명랑하게 바라보며 말했습니다. \"글쎄, 그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 나는 어린애고, 다른 무엇인 척한 적도 없네. 저 아이를 길바닥에 내놓는다 해도, 결국 원래 있던 자리에 도로 갖다 놓는 것뿐이야. 전보다 더 나빠질 것도 없어, 알겠나. 자네가 원한다면 더 나아지게 할 수도 있지. 저 애한테 6펜스를 주든, 5실링을 주든, 5파운드 10실링을 주든—자네들은 셈에 밝고 나는 그렇지 않으니—그렇게 떼어내 버리게!\"\n\n\"그럼 그러고 나서 저 아인 어쩌란 말인가?\" 후견인이 물었습니다.\n\n\"맹세코.\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 매력적인 미소를 띠고 어깨를 으쓱이며 말했습니다. \"그러고 나서 저 애가 어쩔지는 나도 도무지 모르겠네. 하지만 어떻게든 하리란 건 의심치 않아.\"\n\n\"그런데 말일세, 이거 끔찍한 노릇 아닌가.\" 내가 후견인에게 두 여자의 헛수고를 황급히 설명해 드리자 후견인이 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리며 방 안을 오가며 말했습니다. \"이거 끔찍한 노릇 아니냐고. 만약 이 가엾은 아이가 형이 확정된 죄수라면, 병원 문이 활짝 열려 있을 테고, 이 나라 어느 병든 아이 못지않게 잘 보살핌을 받을 게 아닌가?\"\n\n\"이보게 잔다이스.\" 스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다. \"세상일에는 더없이 단순한 인간이 던지는 단순한 질문이니 너그러이 봐주게. 그렇다면 저 애는 왜 죄수가 아닌가?\"\n\n후견인은 걸음을 멈추고, 즐거움과 분노가 묘하게 뒤섞인 얼굴로 그를 바라보았습니다.\n\n\"우리 어린 친구한테 무슨 섬세한 감정 따위가 있다고 의심할 순 없을 것 같네만.\" 스킴폴 씨가 주눅 들지 않고 솔직하게 말했습니다. \"내가 보기엔, 저 애가 감옥에 가게 할 만한 엉뚱한 기운이라도 좀 부려주는 편이 더 현명하고, 또 어떤 면에선 더 떳떳하지 않겠나. 그러면 모험심이라도 좀 깃들 테고, 따라서 어떤 종류의 시정(詩情)도 더 생길 거란 말이지.\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔 말일세.\" 후견인이 다시 불안한 걸음을 옮기며 받았습니다. \"이 땅 위에 자네 같은 어린애는 또 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"정말 그런가?\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그럴 만도 하지! 하지만 솔직히 말해, 우리 어린 친구가 제 처지에 맞게나마, 제게 열린 시정을 제 몸에 두르려 들지 않을 이유를 모르겠어. 저 애도 분명 식욕을 타고났을 거야—건강이 좀 더 멀쩡할 땐, 아마 식욕이 아주 왕성하겠지. 좋아. 우리 어린 친구의 자연스러운 저녁 식사 시간, 그러니까 한낮쯤에, 우리 어린 친구가 사회에 대고 사실상 이렇게 말하는 거야. '나는 배가 고픕니다. 부디 그 숟가락을 꺼내 나를 먹여주시겠습니까?' 그런데 숟가락 체계 전체를 도맡아 관리하노라 자처하고, 우리 어린 친구 몫의 숟가락도 있노라 공언하는 사회가, 정작 그 숟가락을 내놓지 않는단 말이지. 그래서 우리 어린 친구가 이렇게 말하는 거야. '그렇다면 내가 그걸 낚아채더라도 양해해 주셔야겠습니다.' 자, 이건 내가 보기에 엉뚱한 데로 쏠린 기운의 한 사례인데, 거기엔 어느 정도 이치도 있고 어느 정도 낭만도 있어. 그래서 나로선 우리 어린 친구를, 그저 떠돌이 거지로—그거야 누구나 될 수 있는 거고—볼 때보다, 그런 사례의 본보기로 볼 때 더 흥미를 느낄 것 같단 말일세.\"\n\n\"그러는 사이에.\" 나는 감히 한마디 했습니다. \"저 아이는 점점 나빠지고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그러는 사이에.\" 스킴폴 씨가 쾌활하게 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양이 그 실용적인 양식으로 짚었듯, 저 애는 점점 나빠지고 있지. 그러니 더 나빠지기 전에 내쫓으라고 권하는 걸세.\"\n\n그 말을 하면서 짓던 그 상냥한 얼굴을, 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것 같습니다.\n\n\"물론이지, 우리 꼬마 아주머니.\" 후견인이 나를 돌아보며 말했습니다. \"내가 그곳에 직접 가서 강제로 밀어붙이면 저 애를 마땅한 데 들여보낼 수야 있지. 저 애 상태에 그런 짓까지 해야 한다는 게 한심한 노릇이긴 하다만. 하지만 밤이 깊어가고, 날씨도 몹시 사납고, 저 애는 이미 기진맥진했어. 마구간 옆 위생적인 다락방에 침대가 하나 있으니, 아침까지 거기 재우는 게 낫겠다. 아침이 되면 잘 싸매서 옮기면 되고. 그렇게 하자꾸나.\"\n\n\"아!\" 우리가 자리를 뜨려는데 스킴폴 씨가 피아노 건반에 손을 얹으며 말했습니다. \"우리 어린 친구한테 돌아가는 건가?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"자네 그 체질이 얼마나 부러운지 모르겠네, 잔다이스!\" 스킴폴 씨가 장난스러운 감탄을 담아 받았습니다. \"자넨 이런 일에 개의치 않잖아. 서머슨 양도 마찬가지고. 자네들은 언제든 어디든 가고 무슨 일이든 할 채비가 돼 있어. 그게 바로 의지란 거지! 나는 의지라곤 통 없어—'싫다'는 것도 없어—그저 못 할 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"저 아이한테 뭐 권할 만한 게 없겠나?\" 후견인이 어깨너머로 반쯤 화난 듯 돌아보며 말했습니다. 반쯤만 화난 것이었는데, 그는 스킴폴 씨를 책임을 물을 만한 존재로 여기는 법이 결코 없었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"이보게 잔다이스, 저 애 주머니에 열을 식히는 약병이 하나 있는 걸 봤네. 그걸 먹는 것보다 더 나은 방도는 없을 거야. 저 애가 자는 자리 둘레에 식초를 좀 뿌리고, 자리는 적당히 시원하게, 애는 적당히 따뜻하게 해주라고 일러두게. 하지만 내가 이런 권고를 늘어놓는다는 게 그저 주제넘은 짓이지. 서머슨 양이 워낙 세세한 일을 잘 알고 또 세세한 일을 척척 처리하는 능력이 있으니, 다 알아서 할 걸세.\"\n\n우리는 다시 현관으로 돌아가 조에게 우리가 하려는 바를 설명했고, 찰리가 다시 한번 일러주었습니다. 아이는 내가 이미 눈여겨본 그 나른한 무관심으로 받아들이며, 마치 남의 일이기라도 한 양 진행되는 일을 지친 눈으로 바라보았습니다. 하인들이 그 비참한 처지를 가엾이 여겨 무척 거들고 싶어 했으므로, 우리는 이내 다락방을 준비했고, 집안 남자들 몇이 아이를 잘 싸매어 젖은 마당을 가로질러 옮겼습니다. 그들이 아이에게 얼마나 친절한지, 또 그를 자꾸 \"어이, 친구\" 하고 부르면 기운이 살아나리라는 생각이 그들 사이에 두루 퍼져 있는 듯한 것이 보기 좋았습니다. 찰리가 일을 지휘하며, 안전하다 싶은 약간의 자양분과 위안거리를 들고 다락방과 본채 사이를 오갔습니다. 후견인도 밤새 아이만 남겨두기 전에 직접 살펴보고, 아침 동틀 무렵 전령을 시켜 전하기로 한 편지를 아이를 위해 쓰러 으르렁 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 아이가 한결 편안해진 듯하고 잠들 기색이라고 나에게 일러주었습니다. 혹시라도 헛소리를 할까 봐 문은 바깥에서 잠가두었지만, 아이가 무슨 소리라도 내면 들리도록 해두었다고도 했습니다.\n\n에이다는 감기에 걸려 우리 방에 있었으므로, 스킴폴 씨는 그동안 내내 혼자 남아, 애처로운 가락을 토막토막 치고 이따금 거기에 맞춰 (멀리서 우리에게 들려오듯) 풍부한 감정과 표현을 담아 노래를 부르며 시간을 보냈습니다. 우리가 응접실에서 다시 그와 합류하자, 그는 \"우리 어린 친구에게 어울리는\" 짤막한 발라드 하나가 떠올랐다며, 어느 시골 소년에 관한 노래를 불러주었습니다.\n\n\"넓은 세상에 내던져져, 정처 없이 떠돌 운명, 부모도 여의고, 집도 여의었네.\"\n\n참으로 절묘하게 불렀습니다. 그는 이 노래를 부르면 늘 눈물이 난다고 우리에게 말했습니다.\n\n그는 그날 저녁 내내 더없이 흥겨워했습니다. 자기가 일에 그토록 행복한 재능을 가진 이들에게 둘러싸여 있다고 생각하니, 그야말로 \"재잘재잘 흥이 났다\"—그 자신의 들뜬 표현이었습니다. 그는 니거스 술잔을 들고 \"우리 어린 친구의 쾌차를 위하여!\"라고 우리에게 건배하며, 그 아이가 휘팅턴처럼 훗날 런던 시장이 되도록 점지된 게 아니겠느냐며 그 가상의 경우를 흥겹게 펼쳐 보였습니다. 그렇게 되면 분명 그 애는 잔다이스 재단과 서머슨 양로원을 세우고, 해마다 세인트올번스로 향하는 소규모 시 순례단도 꾸리겠지요. 우리 어린 친구가 제 나름으로는 훌륭한 아이임을 의심치 않지만, 그 나름이라는 게 해럴드 스킴폴식은 아니라고 그는 말했습니다. 해럴드 스킴폴이 어떤 사람인지는, 해럴드 스킴폴이 처음 제 자신과 알게 되었을 때 적잖이 놀라며 스스로 깨달았다고요. 그는 온갖 결점까지 다 안고 제 자신을 받아들였으며, 그 거래에서 최선을 끌어내는 것이 건전한 철학이라 여겨왔다고, 그리고 우리도 그렇게 하기를 바란다고 했습니다.\n\n찰리의 마지막 보고는 아이가 잠잠하다는 것이었습니다. 나는 내 방 창문에서, 그들이 아이에게 남겨준 등불이 조용히 타오르는 것을 볼 수 있었습니다. 그래서 아이가 비바람을 피해 보호받고 있다는 생각에 무척 행복한 마음으로 잠자리에 들었습니다.\n\n동트기 조금 전, 여느 때보다 부산한 움직임과 말소리가 일어 나를 깨웠습니다. 옷을 입으며 창밖을 내다보다, 간밤에 적극적으로 거들던 우리 집 남자 하나에게 집에 무슨 일이라도 있느냐고 물었습니다. 다락방 창에는 아직 등불이 타고 있었습니다.\n\n\"그 아이 때문입니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n\"더 나빠졌나요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"사라졌어요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"죽었느냐고요, 아가씨? 아닙니다. 감쪽같이 사라졌어요.\"\n\n밤 몇 시에 사라졌는지, 어떻게, 왜 사라졌는지는 아무리 해도 끝내 알아낼 길이 없을 듯했습니다. 문은 잠가둔 그대로였고 등불은 창에 그대로 놓여 있었으니, 아래쪽 빈 수레 창고로 통하는 바닥의 들창으로 빠져나갔다고밖에는 짐작할 수 없었습니다. 하지만 그렇다 해도 아이는 그걸 도로 닫아두었고, 들어 올린 흔적조차 없어 보였습니다. 없어진 물건은 아무것도 없었습니다. 이 사실이 분명히 밝혀지자, 우리는 모두 가슴 아픈 결론에 이를 수밖에 없었습니다. 밤사이 아이에게 섬망이 찾아왔고, 무슨 환영에 홀렸거나 무슨 환상의 공포에 쫓겨, 무력하다는 말로도 모자랄 그 상태로 어디론가 헤매 나갔으리라는 것이었습니다. 다만 스킴폴 씨만은 예외였는데, 그는 평소의 그 느긋하고 가벼운 투로 거듭 이렇게 넌지시 말했습니다. 우리 어린 친구가 고약한 열병에 걸린 자기는 묵어도 안전치 못한 손님이라는 생각이 들어, 타고난 예의를 한껏 발휘해 제 발로 떠났을 거라고요.\n\n할 수 있는 모든 수소문을 했고, 곳곳을 다 뒤졌습니다. 벽돌 가마를 살피고, 오두막들을 찾아가고, 특히 두 여자에게 캐물었지만, 그들은 아이에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그 놀라움이 진심임을 누구도 의심할 수 없었습니다. 한동안 날씨가 너무 궂었고 그날 밤 역시 너무 궂어서 발자국으로 행방을 좇을 수는 없었습니다. 혹시 아이가 어딘가에 의식을 잃거나 죽어 쓰러져 있을까 봐, 우리 집 남자들은 멀리까지 산울타리와 도랑과 담장, 짚단과 낟가리를 다 뒤졌지만, 아이가 근처에라도 왔었다는 흔적은 하나도 보이지 않았습니다. 다락방에 남겨진 그때부터, 아이는 자취도 없이 사라진 것이었습니다.\n\n수색은 닷새 동안 이어졌습니다. 그때 수색이 그쳤다는 말은 아니고, 다만 그때 내 주의가 나로서는 무척이나 잊지 못할 다른 흐름으로 쏠렸다는 뜻입니다.\n\n저녁에 찰리가 내 방에서 또 글씨 연습을 하고, 나는 그 맞은편에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었는데, 탁자가 흔들리는 것이 느껴졌습니다. 고개를 들어보니 내 어린 하녀가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있었습니다.\n\n\"찰리.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"그렇게 춥니?\"\n\n\"그런가 봐요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 대답했습니다. \"왜 이러는지 모르겠어요. 가만히 있을 수가 없어요. 어제도 이맘때쯤 이랬어요, 아가씨. 걱정 마세요, 제가 아픈가 봐요.\"\n\n밖에서 에이다의 목소리가 들리기에, 나는 서둘러 내 방과 우리의 예쁜 거실 사이의 연결문으로 가서 잠갔습니다. 아슬아슬하게 때맞춰서요. 내 손이 아직 열쇠에 닿아 있을 때 에이다가 문을 두드렸으니까요.\n\n에이다가 들여보내 달라고 불렀지만, 나는 말했습니다. \"지금은 안 돼, 내 사랑. 저리 가 있어. 별일 아니야. 이따 너한테 갈게.\" 아! 사랑하는 그 아이와 내가 다시 벗이 되기까지는 참으로, 참으로 오랜 시간이 걸렸습니다.\n\n찰리가 병이 났습니다. 열두 시간 만에 매우 위중해졌습니다. 나는 찰리를 내 방으로 옮겨 내 침대에 눕히고, 조용히 곁에 앉아 간호했습니다. 나는 후견인에게 자초지종을 다 말씀드렸습니다. 내가 왜 스스로를 가두어야 한다고 여기는지, 무엇보다 사랑하는 그 아이를 만나지 않으려는 까닭이 무엇인지를요. 처음에 에이다는 자주 문 앞에 와서 나를 부르고, 흐느끼며 눈물로 나를 원망하기까지 했습니다. 하지만 나는 긴 편지를 써서, 네가 나를 불안하고 괴롭게 만든다고, 그러니 나를 사랑하고 내 마음이 평온하기를 바란다면 정원보다 더 가까이는 오지 말아달라고 간곡히 빌었습니다. 그 뒤로 에이다는 문 앞에 오던 것보다 더 자주 창 아래로 왔는데, 우리가 거의 떨어져 지낸 적이 없던 전에도 그 다정하고 고운 목소리를 사랑하는 법을 배웠다면, 창 커튼 뒤에 서서 귀 기울여 대답만 할 뿐 내다보지조차 못하던 그때, 나는 그 목소리를 사랑하는 법을 얼마나 더 깊이 배웠던지요! 그 뒤 더 힘겨운 때가 닥쳤을 때, 또 나는 그 목소리를 사랑하는 법을 얼마나 더 깊이 배웠던지요!\n\n사람들이 우리 거실에 내 침대를 놓아주었습니다. 이제 에이다가 그쪽을 비웠으니, 나는 문을 활짝 열어둔 채 두 방을 하나로 트고, 늘 신선하고 통풍이 잘되게 해두었습니다. 집 안팎의 하인이라면 누구 하나 빠짐없이 어찌나 착한지, 낮이든 밤이든 어느 때라도 조금의 두려움이나 꺼리는 기색 없이 더없이 기꺼이 나에게 와주었을 것입니다. 하지만 나는 에이다와는 결코 마주치지 않을, 그리고 빈틈없이 조심해서 드나들 거라 믿을 수 있는 점잖은 여인 하나를 고르는 것이 가장 낫겠다고 생각했습니다. 그 여인 덕분에 나는 에이다와 마주칠 염려가 없을 때 후견인과 함께 나가 바람을 쐴 수 있었고, 다른 어떤 면에서와 마찬가지로 시중드는 일에서도 아쉬울 것이 없었습니다.\n\n그렇게 가엾은 찰리는 점점 더 앓고 더 나빠져, 죽음의 위험에 깊이 빠져들었고, 길고 긴 낮과 밤이 거듭되는 동안 위중하게 누워 있었습니다. 그토록 잘 참고, 그토록 불평이 없고, 그토록 온화한 꿋꿋함으로 버티는 모습에, 나는 자주 찰리 곁에 앉아 그 머리를 두 팔로 안고서—다른 어떤 자세로도 쉬지 못하던 아이가 그렇게 하면 쉬곤 했습니다—이 어린 누이가 나에게 가르쳐준 교훈을 잊지 않게 해달라고 하늘에 계신 우리 아버지께 말없이 기도했습니다.\n\n설령 회복한다 해도 찰리의 예쁜 얼굴이 흉하게 망가질 거라는 생각에 나는 무척 슬펐습니다—보조개 진 얼굴이 아직 그토록 어린아이였으니까요—하지만 그 생각은 대체로 그 아이의 더 큰 위험 앞에 묻혀버렸습니다. 가장 위중하던 때, 정신이 다시 아버지의 병상과 어린 동생들 걱정으로 헤맬 때조차, 그 아이는 다른 어디서도 가만히 누워 있지 못하면서 내 품에서는 잠잠해질 만큼은 나를 알아보았고, 헤매는 정신의 헛소리도 덜 불안하게 흘려냈습니다. 그럴 때면 나는 이런 생각을 하곤 했습니다. 남은 두 어린 동생에게, 그 충실한 마음으로 어려울 때 그들에게 어머니 노릇 하는 법을 깨우친 그 아이가 죽었다는 걸 내가 어떻게 말한단 말인가!\n\n찰리가 나를 또렷이 알아보고 나에게 말을 거는 다른 때도 있었습니다. 톰과 엠마에게 사랑을 전한다고, 톰은 분명 자라서 훌륭한 사람이 될 거라고 말했습니다. 그럴 때면 찰리는 아버지를 위로하려고 자기 딴에는 최선을 다해 읽어드렸던 이야기들을 들려주었습니다. 죽어 묻히러 실려 나가던 청년 이야기, 그 청년은 어느 어머니의 외아들이었고 그 어머니는 과부였다는 이야기, 그리고 죽음의 자리에 누운 회당장의 딸이 자비로운 손길로 일으켜진 이야기를요. 그러고서 찰리는, 아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 첫 슬픔에 무릎 꿇고 기도했다고 했습니다. 아버지도 그처럼 일으켜져 가엾은 자식들에게 돌려보내지기를요. 그리고 자기가 끝내 낫지 못하고 죽게 된다면, 톰의 마음에도 자기를 위해 똑같은 기도를 올릴 생각이 떠오를 것 같다고 했습니다. 그러면 나는 톰에게 보여주리라고 생각했습니다. 옛 시절의 이 사람들이 이 땅에서 되살아난 것은, 오로지 우리가 하늘로 회복되리라는 우리의 희망을 알게 하기 위함이었다고 말입니다!\n\n그러나 찰리가 앓는 그 온갖 때를 통틀어, 내가 말한 그 온화한 품성을 그 아이가 잃은 적은 한 번도 없었습니다. 그리고 밤이면 나는 여러 번, 참으로 여러 번, 그 가엾고 멸시받던 아버지가 마지막으로 지녔던 그 드높은 믿음—지켜보는 천사에 대한 믿음, 그리고 하느님에 대한 한층 더 드높은 신뢰—을 떠올렸습니다.\n\n그리고 찰리는 죽지 않았습니다. 위험한 고비에서 오래도록 머뭇거린 끝에, 가물가물 천천히 그 고비를 넘어섰고, 그러고 나서 차도를 보이기 시작했습니다. 처음부터 도무지 가질 수 없던 희망—찰리가 겉모습에서나마 예전의 찰리로 남으리라는 희망—이 이내 살아나기 시작했습니다. 그것마저 잘 풀려, 나는 그 아이가 옛 어린아이 모습으로 다시 자라나는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n에이다가 정원에 서 있을 때 이 모든 이야기를 전해줄 수 있었던 그날 아침은 참으로 벅찬 아침이었습니다. 그리고 찰리와 내가 마침내 옆방에서 함께 차를 마신 그날 저녁은 참으로 벅찬 저녁이었습니다. 그러나 바로 그날 저녁, 나는 한기가 온몸을 덮치는 것을 느꼈습니다.\n\n우리 둘에게는 다행스럽게도, 찰리가 다시 무사히 침대에 누워 평온히 잠든 뒤에야 비로소 나는 그 아이의 병이 나에게 옮았다는 생각이 들기 시작했습니다. 차를 마실 때만 해도 느끼는 바를 쉽게 감출 수 있었지만, 이제는 이미 그 단계를 지나 있었고, 나는 내가 빠르게 찰리의 전철을 밟고 있음을 알았습니다.\n\n그래도 나는 아침 일찍 일어나, 정원에서 들려오는 사랑하는 아이의 명랑한 축복에 화답하고, 여느 때처럼 오래 이야기를 나눌 만큼은 괜찮았습니다. 하지만 간밤에 내가 어디 있는지는 알면서도 약간 정신이 나간 채 두 방을 서성였다는 느낌만은 떨칠 수가 없었습니다. 그리고 이따금 정신이 어지러웠는데—몸이 온통 너무 커지기라도 하는 듯, 묘하게 부풀어 오르는 느낌이 들었습니다.\n\n저녁이 되자 훨씬 더 나빠져서, 나는 찰리에게 마음의 준비를 시키기로 마음먹고 이렇게 말했습니다. \"이제 아주 튼튼해졌지, 찰리, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"네, 아주요!\" 찰리가 말했습니다.\n\n\"비밀 하나 들어도 될 만큼 튼튼한 것 같니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"그러기엔 충분히 튼튼해요, 아가씨!\" 찰리가 외쳤습니다. 그러나 찰리의 얼굴은 그 기쁨이 한껏 차오른 순간에 무너지고 말았습니다. 내 얼굴에서 그 비밀을 읽었으니까요. 찰리는 그 큰 의자에서 빠져나와 내 가슴에 와락 안기며 말했습니다. \"오, 아가씨, 다 제 탓이에요! 다 제 탓이에요!\" 그러고는 고마움으로 가득 찬 마음에서 우러난 말을 한참이나 더 쏟아냈습니다.\n\n\"자, 찰리.\" 나는 한동안 그렇게 하도록 내버려둔 뒤에 말했습니다. \"내가 앓게 된다면, 사람의 힘으로 말하건대, 내가 가장 크게 믿는 건 너야. 그런데 네가 늘 너 자신을 위해 그랬던 것처럼 나를 위해서도 차분하고 침착하지 않으면, 그 일을 결코 해낼 수 없어, 찰리.\"\n\n\"조금만 더 울게 해주세요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 말했습니다. \"오, 아가씨, 아가씨! 조금만 더 울게 해주세요. 오, 아가씨!\"—내 목에 매달려 이 말을 얼마나 다정하고 헌신적으로 쏟아냈던지, 나는 지금도 눈물 없이는 떠올릴 수가 없습니다—\"착하게 굴게요.\"\n\n그래서 나는 찰리가 조금 더 울도록 두었고, 그것은 우리 둘 모두에게 좋았습니다.\n\n\"이제 저를 믿어주세요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 차분하게 말했습니다. \"아가씨가 하시는 말씀 하나하나 다 듣고 있어요.\"\n\n\"지금은 아주 사소한 거야, 찰리. 오늘 밤 네 의사 선생님께 내가 몸이 좋지 않은 것 같다고, 그리고 네가 나를 간호할 거라고 말씀드릴 거야.\"\n\n그 말에 가엾은 아이는 진심을 다해 나에게 고마워했습니다. \"그리고 아침에 에이다 아가씨가 정원에 온 소리가 들릴 때, 내가 여느 때처럼 창 커튼으로 갈 수 없거든, 찰리야, 네가 가서 내가 자고 있다고 말해다오—내가 좀 피곤해서 자고 있다고 말이야. 언제든 방은 내가 해온 그대로 두고, 찰리, 아무도 들이지 마라.\"\n\n찰리는 약속했고, 나는 자리에 누웠습니다. 몸이 무척 무거웠으니까요. 그날 밤 의사를 만나, 내 병에 대해 아직은 집안에 아무 말도 하지 말아달라고 청하고 싶던 부탁을 그에게 드렸습니다. 그날 밤이 낮으로 녹아들고, 낮이 다시 밤으로 녹아들던 일은 무척 흐릿하게만 기억납니다. 다만 첫날 아침에는 가까스로 창가로 가서 사랑하는 아이에게 말을 건넬 수 있었습니다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 나는 그 다정한 목소리를—오, 이제 얼마나 다정한지요!—밖에서 들었고, 찰리에게 (말하는 것이 고통스러워) 어렵사리, 가서 내가 자고 있다고 전해달라고 했습니다. 찰리가 나직이 대답하는 소리가 들렸습니다. \"아가씨를 깨우지 마세요, 찰리, 무슨 일이 있어도요!\"\n\n\"내 자랑둥이는 어떻게 보이니, 찰리?\" 나는 물었습니다.\n\n\"실망하셨어요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 커튼 사이로 엿보며 말했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 오늘 아침 무척 아름다울 거라는 건 알아.\"\n\n\"정말 그러세요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 엿보며 대답했습니다. \"아직도 창을 올려다보고 계세요.\"\n\n그 맑고 푸른 두 눈, 하느님의 축복이 깃들기를, 그렇게 들어 올려질 때 늘 가장 사랑스러운 그 눈이여!\n\n나는 찰리를 곁으로 불러 마지막 당부를 했습니다.\n\n\"자, 찰리, 내가 아프다는 걸 에이다가 알면, 어떻게든 이 방으로 들어오려 할 거야. 막아다오, 찰리, 나를 진정 사랑한다면, 끝까지 막아다오! 찰리, 단 한 번이라도 에이다를 들여보내, 내가 여기 누운 모습을 한순간이라도 보게 하면, 나는 죽을 거야.\"\n\n\"절대 안 그럴게요! 절대로요!\" 찰리가 나에게 약속했습니다.\n\n\"믿는다, 사랑하는 찰리. 자, 이제 잠시 내 곁에 와 앉아서, 네 손으로 나를 만져다오. 너를 볼 수가 없구나, 찰리. 나는 앞이 보이지 않아.\"\n\n링컨법학원에 밤이 내린다. 법의 그림자가 드리운 골짜기, 미궁처럼 얽혀 시름만 가득하고, 소송 당사자들이 햇빛이라곤 좀처럼 보기 어려운 곳이다. 사무실마다 굵은 양초가 꺼지고, 서기들은 삐걱대는 나무 계단을 우당탕 내려와 뿔뿔이 흩어졌다. 아홉 시에 울리는 종은 아무 의미도 없이 구슬프게 떨던 소리를 멈추었고, 출입문은 닫혔으며, 야간 수위는 무시무시한 잠 솜씨를 자랑하는 근엄한 파수꾼답게 수위실에서 보초를 선다. 층층이 늘어선 계단 창문에서, 형평법의 눈동자처럼 때 낀 등불이, 눈마다 깊이를 알 수 없는 호주머니를 달고 그 위에 또 눈 하나를 얹은 흐릿한 아르고스처럼, 별을 향해 가물가물 깜박인다. 여기저기 지저분한 위층 여닫이창에서는, 자그마한 촛불 얼룩이 부옇게 어른거리며, 어느 영리한 서류 작성인과 부동산 양도 전문가가 아직도 부동산을 양피지 그물에 옭아매느라 골몰하는 곳을 드러낸다. 그 비율은 한 에이커당 양 열두 마리꼴이다. 이 꿀벌 같은 근면함 위에서, 자기 종족의 은인이라 할 이들은 근무 시간이 지났는데도 여전히 미적거리니, 그것은 하루하루 마침내 무언가 좋은 셈을 치르기 위함이다.\n\n이웃한 마당, 헌 옷과 빈 병을 사고파는 가게의 대법관이 사는 그곳에는, 맥주와 야식 쪽으로 기우는 전반적인 분위기가 감돈다. 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인, 두 사람의 아들들은 한 무리의 동무들과 어울려 숨바꼭질을 하느라 몇 시간째 챈서리 거리 샛길마다 매복하고, 같은 큰길의 평원을 누비며 행인들을 어수선하게 만들었는데, 그 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인이 이제 막 아이들이 잠자리에 든 것을 두고 서로 축하를 나누고는, 아직도 어느 문간에서 작별 인사 몇 마디를 주고받으며 미적거린다. 크룩 씨와 그의 하숙인, 그리고 크룩 씨가 \"노상 술에 절어 있다\"는 사실, 또 그 젊은이의 유산 상속 전망이, 여느 때처럼 두 사람 대화의 주된 재료다. 그러나 두 사람은 솔즈 암스에서 열리는 화음 모임에 대해서도 할 말이 있으니, 반쯤 열린 창문 너머로 피아노 소리가 짤랑짤랑 마당까지 흘러나오고, 거기서 꼬마 스윌스가 영락없는 요릭처럼 화음 애호가들을 웃음바다에 빠뜨린 끝에, 이제는 합창곡에서 굵직한 저음을 맡아 친구와 후원자들을 향해 애절하게 호소하는 소리가 들린다. \"들어요, 들어요, 들어요, 저 푸-울 떨어지는 소리를!\" 퍼킨스 부인과 파이퍼 부인은 화음 모임을 거드는, 직업적으로 이름난 젊은 아가씨를 두고 의견을 견준다. 그 아가씨는 창문에 붙은 손글씨 안내문에 자기만의 자리를 차지하고 있는데, 퍼킨스 부인이 알아낸 바로는, 이름난 사이렌 'M. 멜빌슨 양'으로 광고되긴 하지만 결혼한 지 일 년 반이나 되었고, 그 아기는 매일 밤 공연이 펼쳐지는 동안 자연스러운 영양을 공급받으려고 몰래 솔즈 암스로 옮겨진다는 것이다. \"그렇게 사느니 차라리 나라면,\" 퍼킨스 부인이 말한다, \"성냥팔이로 벌어먹고 살겠어요.\" 파이퍼 부인도 마땅히 그래야 한다는 듯 같은 의견이라, 사사로운 처지가 대중의 갈채보다 낫다고 여기며, 자신의(그리고 은연중에 퍼킨스 부인의) 떳떳함을 하늘에 감사한다. 그쯤 되자 솔즈 암스의 술집 심부름꾼이 거품이 잘 인 야식용 한 파인트를 들고 나타나니, 파이퍼 부인은 그 잔을 받아 들고는, 같은 술집에서 어린 퍼킨스가 잠자리에 들기 전에 받아 온 이래 줄곧 자기 한 파인트를 손에 쥐고 있던 퍼킨스 부인에게 정답게 작별 인사부터 건넨 뒤 안으로 물러난다. 이제 마당에는 가게 덧문을 다는 소리가 들리고, 담배 연기 같은 냄새가 풍긴다. 위층 창문에서는 별똥별이 보이니, 다들 잠자리에 드는 참임을 한층 또렷이 알려준다. 이제는 또, 순경이 문마다 밀어보고, 빗장을 살피고, 보따리를 수상쩍어하고, 누구나 도둑질을 하거나 도둑맞는 중이라는 가정 아래 자기 순찰 구역을 돌기 시작한다.\n\n후텁지근한 밤이지만, 축축한 냉기 또한 살갗을 파고들고, 공기 중에는 한 발쯤 위로 굼뜬 안개가 떠 있다. 도축장과 해로운 업종, 하수와 더러운 물, 그리고 매장지를 한껏 부려먹어 사망 등록관에게 일거리를 좀 더 안겨주기에 딱 좋은, 김이 무럭무럭 오르는 밤이다. 공기 중에 무언가 있는 탓일 수도 있고—거기엔 별의별 게 다 있으니—아니면 그 자신 속에 무언가 잘못된 게 있는 탓일 수도 있겠지만, 위블 씨, 다른 이름으로는 조블링은, 영 마음이 편치 않다. 그는 자기 방과 활짝 열린 거리 쪽 문 사이를 한 시간에 스무 번씩 오간다. 날이 어두워진 뒤로 줄곧 그러고 있다. 대법관이 가게 문을 닫은 뒤로—오늘 밤은 아주 일찌감치 닫았다—위블 씨는 내려갔다 올라가고, 또 내려갔다 올라가기를(머리에 싸구려 벨벳 챙모자를 바짝 눌러쓴 탓에 구레나룻이 도무지 어울리지 않게 삐죽 튀어나온 채로) 전보다 잦게 되풀이한다.\n\n스내그스비 씨 역시 마음이 편치 않은 것은 별스러운 일이 아니니, 그는 자기에게 얹힌 비밀의 압박 아래 늘 어느 정도는 그러하기 때문이다. 자기도 한몫 끼었으면서 정작 그 속내는 나눠 받지 못한 그 수수께끼에 떠밀려, 스내그스비 씨는 그 발원지로 보이는 곳—마당의 헌 옷과 빈 병 가게—주위를 맴돈다. 그곳은 그에게 거부할 수 없는 끌림을 지녔다. 지금도, 솔즈 암스를 돌아 마당을 지나 챈서리 거리 쪽 끝으로 빠져나가, 제 집 문에서 나왔다가 다시 돌아오는, 미처 작정도 없이 시작한 십 분짜리 야식 후 산책을 마무리할 작정으로, 스내그스비 씨가 다가온다.\n\n\"아니, 위블 씨?\" 문구상이 발걸음을 멈추고 말을 건넨다. \"거기 계셨소?\"\n\n\"예!\" 위블이 말한다. \"여기 있습니다, 스내그스비 씨.\"\n\n\"나처럼 잠자리에 들기 전에 바람 좀 쐬는 거요?\" 문구상이 묻는다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 여기선 쐴 바람이랄 게 별로 없고, 있는 것도 그리 상쾌하진 않네요.\" 위블이 마당을 위아래로 훑어보며 대답한다.\n\n\"지당한 말이오. 못 느끼셨소,\" 스내그스비 씨가 잠시 멈춰 공기를 좀 킁킁대며 맛보더니 말한다, \"위블 씨, 못 느끼셨소, 여기가—에둘러 말할 것도 없이—여기가 좀 느끼하다는 걸 말이오?\"\n\n\"실은 저도 오늘 밤 이곳에 묘한 냄새가 떠도는 걸 눈치챘습니다,\" 위블 씨가 대꾸한다. \"솔즈 암스에서 굽는 고기 토막인가 보죠.\"\n\n\"고기 토막이라, 그렇게 생각하시오? 오! 고기 토막, 그렇소?\" 스내그스비 씨가 다시 킁킁대며 맛본다. \"글쎄, 그렇겠죠. 한데 내 보기에 솔의 그 요리사는 좀 단속이 필요하겠소. 고기를 태우고 있구먼! 게다가 내 생각엔\"—스내그스비 씨가 또다시 킁킁대며 맛보더니 침을 뱉고 입을 훔친다—\"내 생각엔—에둘러 말할 것도 없이—그것들이 석쇠에 올려질 때 그리 싱싱하지도 않았던 것 같소.\"\n\n\"그럴 법하군요. 뭐든 상하기 좋은 날씨니까요.\"\n\n\"상하기 좋은 날씨고말고,\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다, \"게다가 마음까지 가라앉히는 것 같소.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 저는 소름이 다 끼치는걸요.\" 위블 씨가 대답한다.\n\n\"하긴, 보시오, 댁은 외딴 식으로, 외딴 방에서, 시커먼 사연이 머리 위에 드리운 채 사니까요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 상대의 어깨 너머로 어두운 통로 안쪽을 들여다보더니 한 발짝 물러서서 집을 올려다보며 말한다. \"나라면 댁처럼 저 방에서 혼자는 못 살겠소. 어떤 저녁이면 어찌나 안절부절못하고 마음이 졸아드는지, 거기 앉아 있느니 차라리 문간에 나와 이렇게 서 있고 싶어질 거요. 하긴, 댁은 그 방에서 내가 거기서 본 걸 못 봤으니, 그건 사실이오. 그게 차이를 만들지요.\"\n\n\"그것에 대해선 저도 알 만큼 압니다.\" 토니가 대답한다.\n\n\"기분 좋은 일은 아니지요, 그렇소?\" 스내그스비 씨가 손바닥으로 입을 가린 채 부드럽게 구슬리는 기침을 하며 말을 잇는다. \"크룩 씨가 집세에서 그걸 좀 헤아려줘야 마땅하오. 부디 그러길 바라오, 정말로.\"\n\n\"저도 그러길 바랍니다,\" 토니가 말한다. \"하지만 글쎄요.\"\n\n\"집세가 너무 비싸다, 그 말이오?\" 문구상이 되묻는다. \"이 근방 집세가 비싸긴 하죠. 어찌 된 영문인지 정확히는 모르지만, 법이라는 게 물건값을 올려놓는 모양이오. 그렇다고,\" 스내그스비 씨가 변명하는 기침을 덧붙이며 말한다, \"내가 벌어먹고 사는 그 업종을 험담하려는 건 아니오만.\"\n\n위블 씨가 다시 마당을 위아래로 훑어보더니 문구상을 바라본다. 스내그스비 씨는 멍하니 그 눈길과 마주치자, 별이라도 하나 찾는 양 위를 올려다보고는, 이 대화에서 어떻게 빠져나갈지 도무지 길이 안 보인다는 투의 기침을 한다.\n\n\"참 묘한 일이오, 선생,\" 그가 천천히 두 손을 비비며 말한다, \"그 사람이 하필—\"\n\n\"그 사람이라니 누구요?\" 위블 씨가 말을 자른다.\n\n\"왜, 그 죽은 사람 말이오.\" 스내그스비 씨가 계단 쪽으로 고개와 오른쪽 눈썹을 씰룩이며, 아는 이의 단추를 톡톡 건드린다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지!\" 상대가 그 화제를 그리 달가워하지 않는 양 대꾸한다. \"그 얘긴 끝낸 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"내 말은 그저, 참 묘한 일이라는 거요, 선생. 그 사람이 여기 와서 살았고 내 필경사 가운데 하나였는데, 이번엔 또 댁이 와서 살면서 역시 내 필경사가 되었으니 말이오. 그렇다고 그 호칭에 얕잡는 뜻이 있다는 건 아니오, 천만의 말이오,\" 스내그스비 씨가 자기가 위블 씨에게 어떤 소유주 행세를 무례하게 내세운 건 아닐까 하는 미심쩍음에 말을 끊더니 잇는다, \"왜냐하면 양조업자 집에 들어가서 정말 아주 점잖게 잘 지낸 필경사들도 내가 알기 때문이오. 더없이 점잖게 말이오, 선생,\" 스내그스비 씨가 외려 일을 그르친 게 아닐까 하는 께름칙함에 덧붙인다.\n\n\"말씀대로 묘한 우연이군요.\" 위블이 또 한 번 마당을 위아래로 훑어보며 대답한다.\n\n\"무슨 운명 같은 게 깃든 것 같지 않소?\" 문구상이 넌지시 말한다.\n\n\"그러네요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거요.\" 문구상이 자기 말을 굳히는 기침과 함께 말한다. \"아주 운명적이오. 영락없는 운명. 자, 위블 씨, 미안하지만 이만 작별 인사를 드려야겠소\"—스내그스비 씨는 말을 멈추고 입을 연 그 순간부터 줄곧 빠져나갈 핑계를 찾고 있었으면서도, 마치 떠나기가 못내 서운하다는 투로 말한다—\"안 그러면 우리 마누라가 날 찾아 나설 테니. 안녕히 계시오, 선생!\"\n\n스내그스비 씨가 마누라더러 자기를 찾아 나서는 수고를 덜어주려고 부랴부랴 집으로 향하는 것이라면, 그 점은 마음을 푹 놓아도 좋다. 그의 마누라는 줄곧 솔즈 암스 언저리에서 그를 눈여겨보고 있었으니, 이제 손수건을 머리에 둘러쓴 채 그를 뒤따라 미끄러지듯 다가오며, 지나는 길에 위블 씨와 그의 문간에 캐묻는 듯한 눈길을 베푼다.\n\n\"여하튼 부인은 날 다시 알아보겠구먼,\" 위블 씨가 혼잣말을 한다. \"한데 머리를 보따리처럼 동여맨 그 꼴을 보니, 당신이 누구든 간에 그 행색에 대해선 칭찬할 말이 없네요. 이 친구는 대체 왜 안 오는 거야!\"\n\n그가 말하는 사이 그 친구가 다가온다. 위블 씨는 살그머니 손가락을 들어 보이며 그를 통로 안으로 끌어들이고는 거리 쪽 문을 닫는다. 그러고 나서 두 사람이 위층으로 올라가는데, 위블 씨는 묵직하게, 거피 씨는(그가 바로 그 친구다) 정말이지 아주 가뿐하게 올라간다. 안쪽 방에 들어가 문을 닫자, 두 사람은 낮은 목소리로 말한다.\n\n\"여기 오는 대신 적어도 머나먼 예리코까지 간 줄 알았네.\" 토니가 말한다.\n\n\"아니, 열 시쯤이라고 했잖아.\"\n\n\"열 시쯤이라고 했지,\" 토니가 되받는다. \"그래, 열 시쯤이라고 하긴 했어. 한데 내 셈으론 열 곱하기 열이야—백 시쯤이라고. 내 평생 이런 밤은 처음일세!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었는데?\"\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 토니가 말한다. \"아무 일도 없었어. 한데 이 멋들어진 옛날 굴속에서 끓고 부글대고 하다 보니, 소름이 우박처럼 마구 쏟아져 내리더라고. 저기 저 복받은 꼴의 촛불 좀 보게!\" 토니가 탁자 위에서 묵직하게 타들어가는, 양배추통만 한 심지에 길게 늘어진 수의 같은 촛농을 늘어뜨린 양초를 가리키며 말한다.\n\n\"그건 쉽게 고칠 수 있지.\" 거피 씨가 심지 가위를 집어 들며 말한다.\n\n\"그래?\" 친구가 대꾸한다. \"자네 생각만큼 쉽지 않아. 불을 붙인 뒤로 줄곧 저렇게 거무튀튀하게 타고 있다고.\"\n\n\"아니, 자네 대체 무슨 일이야, 토니?\" 거피 씨가 심지 가위를 손에 든 채, 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉은 그를 바라보며 묻는다.\n\n\"윌리엄 거피,\" 상대가 대답한다, \"나 영 가라앉았어. 이 견딜 수 없이 음침하고, 자살이라도 부를 것 같은 방에다—아래층의 그 늙은 도깨비 영감 탓이겠지.\" 위블 씨가 시무룩하게 팔꿈치로 심지 가위 받침을 밀쳐내고, 손에 머리를 기대고, 두 발을 난로 가리개에 얹은 채 불을 바라본다. 거피 씨는 그를 살피며 가볍게 고개를 까딱이고는, 탁자 맞은편에 느긋한 자세로 앉는다.\n\n\"방금 자네한테 말 걸던 게 스내그스비 아니었나, 토니?\"\n\n\"맞아, 그리고 그가—그래, 스내그스비였어.\" 위블 씨가 문장의 짜임새를 바꿔가며 말한다.\n\n\"무슨 볼일로?\"\n\n\"아니. 볼일은 없어. 그냥 어슬렁대며 지나가다 멈춰서 주절댄 거지.\"\n\n\"스내그스비인 줄 알았어,\" 거피 씨가 말한다, \"그래서 그가 날 못 보는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 그가 갈 때까지 기다린 거야.\"\n\n\"또 시작이군, 윌리엄 G.!\" 토니가 잠깐 고개를 들고 소리친다. \"어찌나 수상쩍고 은밀한지! 맙소사, 우리가 살인이라도 저지르려는 참이라면 이보다 더 음험하게 굴 수도 없겠다!\"\n\n거피 씨는 짐짓 미소 짓는 척하며, 화제를 돌릴 요량으로, 방 안에 걸린 '영국 미인 은하 화랑'을 진짜든 가짜든 감탄 어린 눈길로 둘러보다가, 벽난로 선반 위에 걸린 데드록 마님의 초상화에서 그 둘러봄을 끝맺는다. 그림 속 그녀는 테라스에 서 있고, 테라스 위에는 받침대가, 받침대 위에는 항아리가, 항아리 위에는 그녀의 숄이, 숄 위에는 엄청난 모피 한 덩이가, 그 엄청난 모피 위에는 그녀의 팔이, 그 팔에는 팔찌가 얹혀 있다.\n\n\"저거 데드록 마님이랑 꼭 닮았네.\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"말이라도 할 것 같은 닮은꼴이야.\"\n\n\"닮은꼴이면 좋으련만.\" 토니가 자세도 바꾸지 않은 채 으르렁대듯 말한다. \"그럼 나도 여기서 상류층 대화나 좀 나눌 텐데.\"\n\n이쯤 되자 친구를 어르고 달랜다고 해서 좀 더 붙임성 있는 기분으로 끌어낼 수 없음을 알아채고, 거피 씨는 박대당한 자의 방향으로 뱃머리를 돌려 그를 나무란다.\n\n\"토니,\" 그가 말한다, \"기분이 가라앉는 건 내가 봐줄 수 있어. 그게 사람한테 닥쳤을 때 어떤 건지, 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람도 없고, 가슴에 짝사랑의 형상을 새긴 사람보다 그걸 더 잘 알 권리가 있는 사람도 아마 없을 테니까. 하지만 죄 없는 사람이 끼어 있을 땐, 이런 일에도 한도가 있는 법이야. 그래서 자네한테 인정하건대, 토니, 이번 경우에 자네 태도는 손님을 대접하는 것도, 신사다운 것도 아니라고 봐.\"\n\n\"말이 좀 세군, 윌리엄 거피.\" 위블 씨가 받아친다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지,\" 윌리엄 거피 씨가 되받는다, \"하지만 이 말을 할 땐 마음이 진짜 거센걸.\"\n\n위블 씨는 자기가 잘못했음을 인정하고, 윌리엄 거피 씨에게 더는 마음에 두지 말아 달라고 청한다. 그러나 윌리엄 거피 씨는 이미 우위를 잡은 터라, 상처받은 자의 책망을 조금 더 늘어놓지 않고는 도무지 그 우위를 놓아주지 못한다.\n\n\"아니! 제기랄, 토니,\" 그분이 말한다, \"자네 정말이지 조심해야 해. 가슴에 짝사랑의 형상을 새긴 데다, 가장 여린 감정에 떨리는 그 심금이 영 온전치 못한 사람의 마음을 어떻게 상하게 하는지 말이야. 자네는, 토니, 눈을 호리고 취향을 꾈 만한 모든 걸 다 갖췄어. 자네는—아마 자네한텐 다행스럽게도, 나도 똑같이 말할 수 있다면 좋겠다만—한 송이 꽃 둘레만 맴도는 그런 성격이 아니지. 정원 전체가 자네한테 활짝 열려 있고, 자네의 가뿐한 날개가 자넬 그 정원 곳곳으로 실어 나르거든. 그래도 토니, 정말이지, 까닭도 없이 자네 마음마저 상하게 할 마음은 내게 결코 없다네!\"\n\n토니는 다시금 그 얘긴 더 이어가지 말아 달라 간청하며, 힘주어 말한다, \"윌리엄 거피, 그만 좀 해!\" 거피 씨가 수긍하며 대답한다, \"내 발로 먼저 꺼낼 얘긴 절대 아니었어, 토니.\"\n\n\"자, 이제,\" 토니가 불을 쑤시며 말한다, \"그 편지 꾸러미 얘긴데. 크룩이 그걸 나한테 넘기겠다고 오늘 밤 열두 시로 시각을 정한 거, 별난 일 아닌가?\"\n\n\"별나지. 왜 그런 거래?\"\n\n\"그 양반이 뭘 하든 무슨 까닭이 있겠나? 자기도 몰라. 오늘이 자기 생일이라면서, 오늘 밤 열두 시에 넘겨주겠다더군. 그때쯤이면 곤드레만드레가 돼 있겠지. 온종일 퍼마셨으니까.\"\n\n\"약속을 잊진 않았겠지?\"\n\n\"잊어? 그 양반은 절대 안 그래. 뭐든 잊는 법이 없거든. 오늘 밤 여덟 시쯤 그 양반을 봤어—가게 문 닫는 걸 거들었지—그때 이미 그 편지들을 털북숭이 모자 속에 넣어두고 있더라고. 모자를 벗어서 나한테 보여줬어. 가게 문을 닫고 나선, 모자에서 그걸 꺼내, 모자는 의자 등받이에 걸어두고, 불 앞에 서서 그걸 뒤적였어. 조금 있다가 여기 마룻바닥 너머로 들리던데, 바람처럼 흥얼대더라고. 그 양반이 아는 유일한 노래—비보랑 늙은 카론에 관한, 비보가 죽을 때 취해 있었다나 뭐라나 하는 노래 말이야. 그 뒤론 제 굴속에서 잠든 늙은 쥐처럼 쥐 죽은 듯 조용해.\"\n\n\"그래서 자넨 열두 시에 내려가는 거고?\"\n\n\"열두 시에. 그러니 말했듯이, 자네가 왔을 땐 나한텐 백 시쯤으로 느껴졌다고.\"\n\n\"토니,\" 거피 씨가 다리를 꼰 채 잠깐 곰곰이 생각하더니 말한다, \"그 양반 아직 글을 못 읽지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"읽어! 평생 못 읽어. 글자를 하나하나 따로 쓸 줄은 알고, 대부분은 보면 따로따로 알아보긴 해. 거기까진 내 밑에서 익혔지. 하지만 그걸 한데 엮질 못해. 이제 그 요령을 익히기엔 너무 늙었어—게다가 너무 취해 있고.\"\n\n\"토니,\" 거피 씨가 다리를 풀었다가 다시 꼬며 말한다, \"그 양반이 호든이라는 그 이름을 어떻게 떠듬떠듬 읽어냈을 것 같아?\"\n\n\"떠듬떠듬 읽은 게 아니야. 그 양반 눈썰미가 얼마나 유별난지, 또 오로지 눈으로만 베껴 그리는 일에 얼마나 이골이 났는지 자네도 알잖아. 분명 어떤 편지 겉봉을 보고 그걸 그대로 본떠 그린 다음, 나한테 무슨 뜻이냐고 물은 거지.\"\n\n\"토니,\" 거피 씨가 다시 다리를 풀었다가 또 꼬며 말한다, \"그 원본이 남자 글씨라고 보나, 여자 글씨라고 보나?\"\n\n\"여자 글씨야. 십중팔구 귀부인 거지—꽤나 비스듬히 기울었고, 글자 'n'의 끝이 길고 황급하거든.\"\n\n거피 씨는 이 대화가 오가는 내내 엄지손톱을 물어뜯고 있었으니, 으레 꼰 다리를 바꿀 때마다 엄지도 바꿔 물었다. 또 그러려는 참에, 우연히 자기 외투 소맷부리를 보게 된다. 그게 그의 눈길을 사로잡는다. 그가 기겁하여 그것을 빤히 본다.\n\n\"아니, 토니, 오늘 밤 이 집에서 대체 무슨 일이 벌어지는 거야? 굴뚝에 불이라도 났나?\"\n\n\"굴뚝에 불이라니!\"\n\n\"이거 봐!\" 거피 씨가 대꾸한다. \"그을음이 어떻게 떨어지는지 좀 봐. 여기, 내 팔 위에! 또 여기, 이 탁자 위에도! 이 망할 것이, 불어도 안 떨어지네—시커먼 기름처럼 번져!\"\n\n두 사람은 서로를 바라보고, 토니는 문가에 가서 귀를 기울이고, 위층으로 조금, 아래층으로 조금 가본다. 돌아와서는 다 멀쩡하고 다 조용하다 말하며, 조금 전 솔즈 암스에서 고기 토막을 굽는다고 스내그스비 씨에게 했던 말을 다시 읊는다.\n\n\"그러니까 그때였단 말이지,\" 거피 씨가 외투 소맷부리를 여전히 몹시 꺼림칙한 눈길로 흘끔거리며 다시 말을 잇는다. 두 사람은 불 앞에서 탁자를 사이에 두고 양쪽에 기대 머리를 바싹 맞댄 채 대화를 이어간다, \"그 양반이 자기 하숙인의 여행 가방에서 편지 꾸러미를 꺼냈다고 자네한테 말한 게?\"\n\n\"바로 그때였지, 친구.\" 토니가 구레나룻을 힘없이 매만지며 대답한다. \"그래서 내가 친애하는 친구, 명예로우신 윌리엄 거피 군에게 한 줄 적어 보냈잖아. 오늘 밤 약속을 알리면서, 그 도깨비 영감이 워낙 음흉한 자라 그전엔 들르지 말라고 일러준 거지.\"\n\n위블 씨가 으레 꾸며 보이는 상류 사교계의 가볍고 활기찬 말투가 오늘 밤은 어찌나 그에게 어울리지 않는지, 그는 그 말투를 구레나룻과 함께 내던지고는, 어깨 너머를 돌아본 뒤, 다시금 그 소름의 먹잇감으로 자신을 내맡긴 듯 보인다.\n\n\"자넨 그 편지들을 자네 방으로 가져와 읽고 견주어, 그 양반한테 그것들에 대해 죄다 일러줄 수 있는 처지가 되어야 하고. 그게 약속이지, 안 그래, 토니?\" 거피 씨가 초조하게 엄지손톱을 물어뜯으며 묻는다.\n\n\"아무리 낮춰 말해도 지나치지 않아. 그래. 그 양반이랑 내가 그렇게 합의했어.\"\n\n\"내 말 좀 들어봐, 토니—\"\n\n\"아무리 낮춰 말해도 지나치지 않다니까.\" 토니가 한 번 더 말한다. 거피 씨가 영민한 고개를 끄덕이고는, 그것을 한층 더 가까이 들이밀더니, 속삭임으로 잦아든다.\n\n\"내 말 들어봐. 제일 먼저 할 일은 진짜랑 똑같은 꾸러미를 하나 더 만드는 거야. 그래야 진짜가 내 손에 있는 동안 그 양반이 진짜를 보자고 하면, 자네가 가짜를 보여줄 수 있잖아.\"\n\n\"그 양반이 보자마자 가짜인 걸 알아채면 어쩌고. 그 깨물 듯 파고드는 눈초리로 봐선, 못 알아챌 가능성보다 알아챌 가능성이 오백 배쯤 더 높을 텐데.\" 토니가 넌지시 말한다.\n\n\"그럼 우린 정면으로 맞서는 거야. 그건 그 양반 것이 아니고, 애당초 그 양반 것이었던 적도 없어. 자네가 그걸 발견했고, 안전하게 보관하라고—자네의 법률가 친구인—내 손에 맡긴 거지. 그 양반이 우릴 거기까지 몰아붙이면, 내놓으면 될 거 아냐, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그-렇지.\" 위블 씨가 마지못해 인정한다.\n\n\"아니, 토니,\" 친구가 나무란다, \"그 표정은 또 뭐야! 자네 이 윌리엄 거피를 의심하는 거야? 무슨 나쁜 일이라도 있을까 봐 그래?\"\n\n\"난 내가 아는 것 이상은 의심하지 않아, 윌리엄.\" 상대가 진지하게 대답한다.\n\n\"그래서 자네가 아는 게 뭔데?\" 거피 씨가 목소리를 조금 높이며 다그치지만, 친구가 또 한 번 \"낮춰 말해도 지나치지 않다니까\" 하고 주의를 주자, 아무런 소리도 없이 입술 모양만으로 \"자네가 아는 게 뭔데?\"라는 말을 지어 보이며 그 물음을 되풀이한다.\n\n\"난 세 가지를 알아. 첫째, 지금 우리 둘이 음모꾼 한 쌍처럼 은밀하게 속삭이고 있다는 거.\"\n\n\"그래서!\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"그래도 멍청이 한 쌍보다는 그 편이 낫지. 다른 짓을 하고 있다면 우린 영락없는 멍청이 꼴일 테니까. 우리가 하려는 일을 해내는 길이 이것뿐이거든. 둘째는?\"\n\n\"둘째, 이게 결국 어떻게 돈벌이가 될지가 나한텐 도무지 안 잡혀.\"\n\n거피 씨는 벽난로 선반 위 데드록 마님의 초상화로 눈을 치켜뜨고는 대답한다, \"토니, 그건 자네 친구의 명예에 맡겨두라고 부탁받는 거야. 게다가 그게 그 친구한테, 인간 마음의 그 심금에서—이번 경우에 굳이 고통스럽게 떨리도록 불러낼 것까진 없는 그 심금에서—도움이 되도록 계산된 일이라는 점은 차치하더라도, 자네 친구는 바보가 아니거든. 저게 무슨 소리지?\"\n\n\"성 바울 성당 종이 열한 시를 치는 거야. 귀 기울여 봐. 도시의 온 종이 어수선하게 뒤섞여 울리는 게 들릴 테니.\"\n\n두 사람 다 말없이 앉아, 가까이서 또 멀리서, 높이가 제각각인 종탑에서 울려 퍼지는 그 쇠붙이 목소리들에 귀 기울인다. 그 음색은 종탑들의 위치보다도 한결 제각각이다. 마침내 그 소리들이 그치자, 모든 것이 전보다 더 으스스하고 고요해 보인다. 속삭임의 한 가지 고약한 결과는, 그것이 침묵의 분위기를 불러내는 듯하다는 것이니, 그 침묵에는 소리의 유령들—기이한 삐걱임과 똑딱임, 알맹이라곤 없는 옷자락이 스치는 소리, 바닷가 모래나 겨울 눈 위에 아무 자국도 남기지 않을 무시무시한 발걸음 소리—이 깃든다. 마침 두 친구가 어찌나 예민해진 상태인지, 공기가 이런 허깨비들로 가득 차고, 두 사람은 누가 먼저랄 것도 없이 어깨 너머를 돌아보며 문이 닫혀 있는지 확인한다.\n\n\"그래서, 토니?\" 거피 씨가 불 쪽으로 더 가까이 다가앉으며, 떨리는 엄지손톱을 물어뜯는다. \"셋째를 말하려던 참이었지?\"\n\n\"죽은 사람을 두고, 그 사람이 죽은 방에서 음모를 꾸미는 건 결코 유쾌한 일이 아니야. 더구나 자네가 하필 그 방에 살고 있다면 더더욱.\"\n\n\"하지만 우리가 그 양반한테 못된 짓을 꾸미는 건 아니잖아, 토니.\"\n\n\"아닐지도 모르지. 그래도 난 영 싫어. 자네가 여기서 혼자 살아보고 자넨 얼마나 좋은지 한번 봐.\"\n\n\"죽은 사람들 얘기라면, 토니,\" 거피 씨가 그 제안을 슬쩍 피하며 말을 잇는다, \"대개의 방마다 죽은 사람이 있었어.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 알아. 하지만 대개의 방에선 그들을 가만 내버려두고, 그러면—그러면 그들도 자넬 가만 내버려두지.\" 토니가 대답한다.\n\n두 사람은 다시 서로를 바라본다. 거피 씨가 어쩌면 우리가 죽은 이에게 도움을 주는 셈일지도 모른다고, 부디 그러길 바란다고 황급히 한마디 던진다. 짓누르는 듯한 적막이 흐르다가, 위블 씨가 느닷없이 불을 쑤시는 바람에, 거피 씨는 마치 자기 심장이 쑤셔진 양 흠칫 놀란다.\n\n\"우엑! 이 지긋지긋한 그을음이 또 떠다니네.\" 그가 말한다. \"창문 좀 열어서 바람이라도 한 모금 들이마시자. 너무 후텁지근해.\"\n\n그가 창틀을 올리자, 두 사람은 방 안에 반쯤, 밖에 반쯤 걸친 채 창턱에 기댄다. 이웃한 집들이 너무 가까워서 목을 길게 빼고 올려다보지 않고는 하늘 한 조각 볼 수 없지만, 여기저기 꾀죄죄한 창문의 불빛이며, 멀리서 굴러가는 마차 소리며, 사람들의 부산함이 자아내는 그 새로운 기색이, 두 사람에겐 위안이 된다. 거피 씨가 소리 없이 창턱을 톡톡 두드리며, 자못 가벼운 희극조로 다시 속삭이기 시작한다.\n\n\"참, 그건 그렇고, 토니, 그 늙은 스몰위드 잊지 말게.\" 그 집안의 젊은 쪽을 가리키는 말이다. \"내가 그 친군 이 일에 안 끼워줬거든, 알지. 그 할아비라는 작자가 도가 지나치게 빈틈없어서 말이야. 그게 집안 내력이거든.\"\n\n\"기억하고 있어,\" 토니가 말한다. \"그건 죄다 꿰고 있다고.\"\n\n\"그리고 크룩 얘긴데,\" 거피 씨가 다시 말을 잇는다. \"자, 그 양반이 자네한테 큰소리친 대로, 자네들이 그렇게 동맹을 맺은 뒤로 정말 다른 중요한 서류를 손에 넣은 것 같아?\"\n\n토니가 고개를 젓는다. \"모르겠어. 짐작도 안 가. 그 양반 의심을 사지 않고 이 일을 잘 마치면, 틀림없이 더 알게 되겠지. 그 양반 자신도 모르는데, 내가 그걸 보지도 않고 어찌 알겠나? 노상 거기서 단어들을 떠듬떠듬 읽어내선, 탁자랑 가게 벽에다 분필로 끄적이고, 이건 뭐고 저건 뭐냐고 물어대지. 하지만 내가 보기엔, 처음부터 끝까지 그 양반 재고라는 게, 애당초 그 양반이 사들일 때 그랬던 그대로 그냥 휴지 뭉치일 수도 있어. 자기가 문서를 잔뜩 지녔다고 여기는 게 그 양반의 외곬 망상이거든. 그 양반이 나한테 한 말로 미루어 보건대, 지난 사반세기 동안 줄곧 그걸 읽는 법을 배우려고 들었던 것 같아.\"\n\n\"그래도 애초에 그 생각을 어떻게 품게 된 걸까? 그게 문제야.\" 거피 씨가 한쪽 눈을 감고 잠시 법정에 선 듯 골똘히 궁리하더니 넌지시 말한다. \"어쩌면 뭘 사들였는데 그 안에서, 서류가 있으리라곤 생각지도 못한 데서 서류를 발견했을 수도 있어. 그래서 그걸 감춰둔 방식이며 장소로 미루어, 그게 뭔가 값나가는 거라고 그 약삭빠른 머리에 박아 넣었을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"아니면 무슨 사기 흥정에 속아 넘어갔을 수도 있고. 아니면 자기가 가진 게 뭐든 간에 하도 오래 노려보고, 술에 절고, 대법관 법정 언저리를 어슬렁대며 노상 문서 얘길 들어대다가 죄다 머리가 뒤죽박죽되었는지도 모르지.\" 위블 씨가 대답한다.\n\n거피 씨는 창턱에 걸터앉아, 고개를 끄덕이며 이 모든 가능성을 머릿속에서 저울질하면서, 골똘히 그 창턱을 톡톡 두드리고, 손으로 움켜쥐고, 가늠해보기를 이어가다가, 화들짝 손을 떼어낸다.\n\n\"이게 대체,\" 그가 말한다, \"이게 뭐야! 내 손가락 좀 봐!\"\n\n걸쭉하고 누런 진액이 그 손가락을 더럽히는데, 손에도 눈에도 거슬리고, 냄새는 한층 더 역하다. 무언가 본능적인 거부감이 깃든, 고여서 메스꺼운 기름이라 두 사람 다 진저리를 친다.\n\n\"자네 여기서 뭘 한 거야? 창밖으로 뭘 쏟아부은 거냐고?\"\n\n\"내가 창밖으로 쏟아붓다니! 아무것도 안 했어, 맹세코! 여기 온 뒤로 단 한 번도!\" 하숙인이 소리친다.\n\n그런데도 여기를 봐라—또 여기를! 그가 창턱 모퉁이에서 촛불을 가져오자, 그것이 벽돌을 타고 천천히 똑똑 떨어지며 기어 내려가, 여기 걸쭉하고 메스꺼운 작은 웅덩이를 이루고 있다.\n\n\"끔찍한 집이야.\" 거피 씨가 창문을 쾅 내리며 말한다. \"물 좀 줘, 안 그러면 이 손을 잘라버리겠어.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 씻고, 문지르고, 벅벅 닦고, 냄새 맡고, 또 씻어대는지, 브랜디 한 잔으로 겨우 정신을 추스르고 불 앞에 말없이 선 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 성 바울 성당 종이 열두 시를 치고, 다른 그 모든 종도 높이가 제각각인 종탑에서, 어두운 공기 속으로, 저마다의 음색으로 열두 시를 친다. 다시 모든 것이 고요해지자, 하숙인이 말한다. \"드디어 약속한 시각이군. 내려가 볼까?\"\n\n거피 씨가 고개를 끄덕이고는 그의 등에 \"행운의 손길\"을 얹어주는데, 오른손이긴 해도 그 씻은 손은 아니다.\n\n그가 아래층으로 내려가고, 거피 씨는 오래 기다릴 마음으로 불 앞에서 마음을 가다듬어 보려 한다. 그러나 일이 분도 채 못 되어 계단이 삐걱대더니 토니가 부랴부랴 돌아온다.\n\n\"받아 왔어?\"\n\n\"받아 와! 아니. 영감이 거기 없어.\"\n\n그가 그 짧은 사이에 어찌나 무섭게 질겁했는지, 그 공포가 상대마저 사로잡는다. 상대가 그에게 와락 달려들며 큰 소리로 묻는다, \"무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"아무리 불러도 못 듣더라고. 그래서 살그머니 문을 열고 안을 들여다봤어. 거기 그 타는 냄새가 있고—그을음이 있고, 기름이 있는데—정작 그 양반은 없어!\" 토니가 신음과 함께 말을 맺는다.\n\n거피 씨가 촛불을 집어 든다. 두 사람은 죽은 거나 진배없는 몰골로 서로를 부둥켜안은 채 아래로 내려가, 안쪽 가게 문을 밀어 연다. 고양이가 그 문에 바싹 붙어 물러나 있다가, 두 사람을 향해서가 아니라 불 앞 바닥에 놓인 무언가를 향해 으르렁대며 서 있다. 화로에는 불기가 거의 남아 있지 않은데, 방 안에는 자욱하게 숨 막히는 김이 떠돌고, 벽과 천장에는 거무튀튀한 기름때가 끼어 있다. 의자와 탁자, 그리고 탁자에서 좀처럼 사라지는 법이 없는 그 술병이, 모두 여느 때처럼 놓여 있다. 의자 등받이 하나에는 영감의 털북숭이 모자와 외투가 걸려 있다.\n\n\"보게!\" 하숙인이 떨리는 손가락으로 친구의 주의를 그 물건들로 이끌며 속삭인다. \"내가 말했잖아. 마지막으로 그 양반을 봤을 때, 모자를 벗어선, 그 오래된 편지 꾸러미를 꺼내고, 모자를 의자 등받이에 걸었어—외투는 이미 거기 걸려 있었지, 덧문 달러 가기 전에 벗어뒀으니까—그리고 그 양반이 손에 든 편지를 뒤적이는 걸 남겨두고 내가 나왔거든. 바로 저기 마룻바닥에 부스러진 시커먼 게 놓인 그 자리에 서서 말이야.\"\n\n어딘가에 목을 매단 걸까? 두 사람이 위를 올려다본다. 아니다.\n\n\"봐!\" 토니가 속삭인다. \"그 똑같은 의자 발치에, 펜을 묶을 때 쓰는 가느다란 붉은 끈 한 토막이 더럽게 떨어져 있어. 그게 편지들을 둘러 묶고 있던 거야. 그 양반이 뒤적이기 전에, 날 보고 히죽거리며 비웃으면서 그걸 천천히 풀어선, 저기로 던졌어. 떨어지는 걸 내가 봤다고.\"\n\n\"고양이가 왜 저래?\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"저것 좀 봐!\"\n\n\"미친 것 같아. 이 사악한 곳에서 그럴 만도 하지.\"\n\n두 사람은 이 모든 것을 살피며 천천히 다가간다. 고양이는 처음 본 그 자리에 그대로 있으면서, 여전히 불 앞, 두 의자 사이 바닥에 놓인 그 무언가를 향해 으르렁댄다. 그게 뭘까? 촛불을 들어 올려라.\n\n여기 마룻바닥이 자그맣게 그슬린 자국이 있고, 여기 한 줌의 불탄 종이 꾸러미에서 나온 부싯깃 같은 것이 있는데, 여느 것처럼 가볍지가 않고 무언가에 흠뻑 젖은 듯하다. 그리고 여기 있는 이것은—희뿌연 재가 뿌려진, 작게 까맣게 타서 부러진 장작개비의 깜부기인가, 아니면 석탄인가? 오, 끔찍하여라, 그가 여기 있다! 그리고 우리가 촛불을 내동댕이치고 서로를 거리로 떠밀어 넘어뜨리며 줄행랑치게 만드는 이것이, 그를 대신하는 전부다.\n\n사람 살려, 사람 살려, 사람 살려! 제발 이 집으로 와 다오! 숱한 사람이 들어오겠지만, 누구도 도울 수 없다. 그 법정의 대법관은, 그 마지막 행위에서마저 제 칭호에 충실하게도, 모든 법정의 모든 대법관이, 또 어떤 이름으로 불리든 거짓된 명분이 꾸며지고 부정이 저질러지는 모든 곳의 모든 권위자가 맞이하는 그 죽음을 맞이했다. 그 죽음을 전하께서 어떤 이름으로 부르시든, 누구의 탓으로 돌리시든, 또 어떻게 막을 수 있었을 거라 말씀하시든, 그것은 영원토록 똑같은 죽음이다—그 사악한 몸뚱이 자체의 썩어빠진 체액 속에서 타고나고, 길러지고, 잉태된 죽음, 오직 그뿐인 죽음—자연발화, 인간이 죽을 수 있는 그 모든 죽음 가운데 다른 어떤 것도 아닌 바로 그 죽음이다.\n\n이번에는 솔즈 암스에서 열린 지난번 검시 심리에 참석했던, 소맷부리와 단추가 그리 단정치 못한 두 신사가 놀라우리만치 잽싸게 그 일대에 다시 나타나(사실 활동적이고 영민한 교구 직원이 숨 가쁘게 데려온 것이다), 골목을 샅샅이 뒤지고 다니며 솔즈 암스의 응접실로 잠수하듯 들어가더니, 게걸스러운 작은 펜으로 박엽지에 갈겨쓴다. 이제 그들은 한밤의 야경 시각에, 어제 자정 무렵 챈서리 거리 일대가 다음과 같은 경악스럽고 끔찍한 발견으로 극도의 흥분과 동요에 휩싸였다고 받아 적는다. 이제 그들은 이렇게 늘어놓는다. 얼마 전 어느 별난 개인—나이가 한참 든 데다 무절제한 습성을 지닌, 크룩이라는 자—이 넝마, 병, 잡다한 선구를 취급하는 가게로 쓰던 집의 이층에서 아편으로 의문스럽게 죽은 사건으로 세간에 고통스러운 충격이 일었던 것은 누구나 틀림없이 기억할 것이다. 그리고 놀라운 우연으로, 크룩이 바로 그 검시 심리에서 신문을 받았다는 사실도 기억날 것이다. 그 심리는 당시 솔즈 암스에서 열렸는데, 그곳은 문제의 건물에 바로 서쪽으로 붙어 있는, 운영이 잘되는 술집이며, 더없이 점잖은 주인 제임스 조지 보그스비 씨가 허가를 받아 운영하는 곳이다. 이제 그들은 (될 수 있는 한 많은 말을 동원해) 보여준다. 어제 저녁 몇 시간 동안 이 골목 주민들이 아주 특이한 냄새를 맡았으며, 그 골목에서 지금 이 기사의 주제가 된 비극적 사건이 일어났다는 것을. 그 악취가 한때 어찌나 지독했던지, 보그스비 씨에게 직업적으로 고용된 희극 가수 스윌스 씨는 본지 기자에게 직접 진술하기를, 자신이 멜빌슨 양에게—음악적 재능을 다소 자처하는 숙녀로, 역시 보그스비 씨에게 고용되어 ‘화음 모임’ 혹은 ‘집회’라 불리는 일련의 음악회에서 노래를 부르는데, 이 모임은 보그스비 씨의 지휘 아래 조지 이세의 법령에 따라 솔즈 암스에서 열리는 듯하다—그 탁한 공기 탓에 자기(스윌스 씨) 목이 심각하게 망가졌다고 말했다는 것이다. 그때 그가 농담조로 한 말이, 자기는 텅 빈 우체국 같다는 것이었으니, 안에 음표라곤 한 장도 없기 때문이라 했다. 스윌스 씨의 이 증언은 같은 골목에 사는 영민한 기혼 부인 둘이 온전히 뒷받침하니, 각각 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인이라는 이름으로 알려진 이들이다. 두 사람 모두 그 고약한 악취를 맡았고, 그것이 불운하게 죽은 크룩이 쓰던 건물에서 흘러나온다고 여겼다. 이 모든 것과 그 밖에 훨씬 더 많은 것을, 이 우울한 참사를 두고 화기애애한 동업 관계를 맺은 두 신사가 현장에서 받아 적는다. 그러는 동안 골목의 사내아이들은(순식간에 잠자리에서 빠져나와) 솔즈 암스 응접실의 덧문 위로 떼 지어 기어올라, 그들이 받아 적는 모습 위로 보이는 두 사람 정수리를 구경한다.\n\n골목 전체가, 사내아이는 물론 어른까지, 그날 밤은 한숨도 못 자고, 그저 여러 머리를 싸매고서 그 박복한 집 이야기만 하며 그 집을 바라볼 뿐이다. 플라이트 양은 마치 집에 불이라도 난 것처럼 용감히 자기 방에서 구출되어 솔즈 암스에 잠자리를 얻었다. 솔즈는 밤새 가스등도 끄지 않고 문도 닫지 않으니, 어떤 종류의 대중적 흥분이든 솔즈에는 이득이 되고 골목은 위안을 필요로 하게 만들기 때문이다. 이 집은 검시 심리 이후로 위장을 달래는 정향이나 따뜻한 브랜디 물에서 이만한 매상을 올린 적이 없다. 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 듣자마자 술집 사환은 셔츠 소매를 어깨까지 단단히 걷어붙이며 말했다. \"우리한테 손님이 몰려들겠는걸!\" 처음 소동이 일었을 때 어린 파이퍼는 소방차를 부르러 달려갔다가, 흔들리는 질주 속에 피닉스호 꼭대기에 높이 걸터앉아, 그 전설 속 괴물을 죽어라 붙들고서, 투구와 횃불 한가운데서 의기양양하게 돌아왔다. 모든 틈새와 구석을 꼼꼼히 조사한 끝에 투구 하나가 뒤에 남아, 마찬가지로 그곳 경비를 맡긴 경관 둘 중 한 사람과 짝을 이루어 집 앞을 천천히 오간다. 이 셋에게, 골목에서 육 펜스라도 지닌 자라면 누구나 술이라는 형태로 환대를 베풀고 싶어 안달이다.\n\n위블 씨와 그의 벗 거피 씨는 솔즈의 술집 안에 있으며, 그들이 그 자리에 머물러주기만 한다면 술집에 있는 무엇과도 바꿀 수 없을 만큼 솔즈에 값진 손님이다. \"지금은,\" 보그스비 씨가 말한다. \"돈을 두고 흥정할 때가 아니지요.\" 그러면서도 그는 계산대 너머로 그것을 다소 날카롭게 챙긴다. \"주문하세요, 두 신사분. 이름만 대시면 무엇이든 환영입니다.\"\n\n이렇게 권유받은 두 신사는(특히 위블 씨가) 너무 많은 것에 이름을 대다 보니, 시간이 흐르자 무엇 하나 또렷하게 이름 대기가 어려운 지경에 이른다. 그래도 그들은 새로 오는 사람마다 붙잡고 자기들이 보낸 그 밤 이야기를, 무슨 말을 했고 무슨 생각을 했고 무엇을 보았는지를 이런저런 식으로 들려준다. 그러는 사이 경관 한둘이 곧잘 문가를 어른거리며, 팔을 한껏 뻗어 문을 조금 밀어젖히고는, 바깥 어둠 속에서 안을 들여다본다. 무슨 의심이 있어서가 아니라, 저 안에서 무슨 짓을 하는지 알아두는 편이 낫겠다 싶어서다.\n\n이렇게 밤은 납덩이 같은 걸음으로 흘러가, 골목이 여전히 이례적인 시각까지 잠자리에 들지 않고, 여전히 술을 사고 또 얻어 마시며, 뜻밖에 약간의 돈이 굴러든 골목처럼 굴고 있는 것을 본다. 이렇게 밤은 마침내 천천히 물러가는 발걸음으로 떠나고, 가로등 점등부는 순찰을 돌며, 마치 폭군 왕의 사형 집행인처럼, 어둠을 덜어보겠다고 솟아오른 작은 불꽃 머리들을 쳐낸다. 이렇게 날이 밝아온다, 그러든 말든.\n\n그리고 날은, 흐릿한 런던의 눈으로나마, 이 골목이 밤새 깨어 있었음을 알아본다. 졸음에 겨워 탁자에 엎어진 얼굴들과, 침대 대신 딱딱한 바닥에 큰대자로 뻗은 발뒤꿈치들은 말할 것도 없고, 골목 자체의 벽돌과 회반죽으로 된 인상마저 지치고 핼쑥해 보인다. 이제 동네가 잠에서 깨어 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 듣기 시작하면서, 옷을 반쯤 걸친 채 줄줄이 몰려와 이것저것 캐묻는다. 그리고 두 경관과 투구는(겉보기로는 골목보다 훨씬 덜 동요하는 자들이라) 문을 지키느라 진땀을 뺀다.\n\n\"이런 세상에, 여보게들!\" 스내그스비 씨가 다가오며 말한다. \"이게 무슨 소린가!\"\n\n\"뭐, 사실이오.\" 경관 하나가 대꾸한다. \"그게 다요. 자, 여기서 비키시오, 어서!\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 세상에, 여보게들,\" 스내그스비 씨는 다소 잽싸게 물러서면서 말한다. \"내가 어젯밤 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 바로 이 문 앞에서 여기 하숙하는 젊은이와 이야기를 나눴소.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 경관이 대꾸한다. \"그럼 그 젊은이는 옆집에서 찾을 수 있겠소. 자, 여기 좀 비키시오, 다들.\"\n\n\"다치진 않았겠지요?\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"다쳐요? 아니오. 그 사람이 뭘 다쳐!\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 어수선한 마음으로 이 질문도 다른 어떤 질문도 도무지 답할 수 없어, 솔즈 암스로 발걸음을 옮긴다. 그곳에서 그는 위블 씨가 차와 토스트를 앞에 두고 늘어져 있는 것을 보니, 그 얼굴에는 흥분에 지치고 담배 연기에 지친 기색이 짙게 어려 있다.\n\n\"게다가 거피 씨도 있군!\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"저런, 저런, 저런! 이 모든 일에 무슨 운명 같은 게 깃든 모양이야! 그리고 우리 마—\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 \"우리 마누라\"라는 말을 채 빚어내지 못하고 말문이 막힌다. 그 시간, 그 이른 아침에 자기 마누라가, 그 학대받은 여인이 솔즈 암스로 걸어 들어와 맥주 따르는 기계 앞에 서서, 마치 고발하는 망령처럼 두 눈을 자기에게 못 박고 있는 것을 보니, 그는 그만 입이 떨어지지 않는 것이다.\n\n\"여보,\" 스내그스비 씨가 혀가 풀리자 말한다. \"뭐라도 한잔하겠소? 조금—이렇게 너무 까다롭게 굴 건 없지만—과실주 한 모금이라도?\"\n\n\"싫어요.\" 스내그스비 부인이 말한다.\n\n\"여보, 이 두 신사분을 알지요?\"\n\n\"네!\" 스내그스비 부인은 그렇게 말하며 뻣뻣한 태도로 두 사람의 존재를 인정하되, 여전히 스내그스비 씨에게서 눈을 떼지 않는다.\n\n헌신적인 스내그스비 씨는 이런 대접을 견딜 수 없다. 그는 스내그스비 부인의 손을 잡고 옆쪽 가까운 술통 곁으로 이끈다.\n\n\"여보, 왜 그런 눈으로 날 보는 거요? 제발 그러지 마시오.\"\n\n\"내 눈은 내 맘대로 안 돼요.\" 스내그스비 부인이 말한다. \"되더라도 안 그럴 거예요.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 온순한 기침을 하며 다시 말한다. \"정말 안 그러겠소, 여보?\" 그러고는 곰곰 생각한다. 그러더니 곤혹스러운 기침을 하고서 말한다. \"이건 끔찍한 수수께끼야, 여보!\" 그러면서도 여전히 스내그스비 부인의 눈빛에 무섭도록 당황한다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 스내그스비 부인이 고개를 저으며 대꾸한다. \"끔찍한 수수께끼지요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 스내그스비 씨가 애처롭게 매달리듯 말한다. \"제발 부탁이니 그런 모진 표정으로 내게 말하지 말고, 그렇게 꿰뚫어 보는 눈으로 날 보지도 마시오! 빌고 또 비니 제발 그러지 마시오. 맙소사, 설마 내가 어떤 사람을 자연발화로 태워 죽이고 다닌다고 여기는 건 아니겠지, 여보?\"\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 스내그스비 부인이 대꾸한다.\n\n자기 처지가 얼마나 딱한지 황급히 되짚어 본 스내그스비 씨도 \"글쎄요\" 할 수밖에 없다. 그는 자기가 그 일과 어떤 관련이 있었을지도 모른다는 것을 딱 잘라 부인할 처지가 못 된다. 그는 이 일과 관련된 그 숱한 수수께끼 같은 일들에—무엇인지도 모를 무언가에—관여한 바가 있어서, 자기도 모르는 새 이번 사건에마저 연루되었을 가능성이 있다. 그는 손수건으로 이마를 힘없이 훔치며 숨을 헐떡인다.\n\n\"여보,\" 불행한 문방구상이 말한다. \"당신은 평소 행실이 그토록 섬세하고 신중한 사람인데, 어째서 아침 식사 전에 술 창고에 들어왔는지 그 까닭을 말해주면 안 되겠소?\"\n\n\"당신은 왜 여기 왔는데요?\" 스내그스비 부인이 묻는다.\n\n\"여보, 그저 그—태워진—그 점잖은 양반에게 닥친 끔찍한 사고의 자초지종을 알아보려고.\" 스내그스비 씨는 신음을 억누르느라 잠시 말을 멈췄다. \"그래서 당신에게 프랑스식 빵을 곁들여 그 이야길 들려주려 했지, 여보.\"\n\n\"그러시겠지요! 당신은 내게 뭐든 다 얘기하니까요, 스내그스비 씨.\"\n\n\"뭐든—우리 마—\"\n\n\"기쁘겠어요,\" 스내그스비 부인이 그의 한층 깊어진 당혹을 매섭고 음흉한 미소로 바라보며 말한다. \"나와 함께 집에 가준다면 말이에요. 스내그스비 씨, 당신은 다른 어디보다 거기 있는 편이 더 안전할 거예요.\"\n\n\"여보, 정말 그럴지도 모르겠소, 정말로. 갈 채비가 됐소.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 술집 안을 처량하게 둘러보고, 위블 씨와 거피 씨에게 좋은 아침이라 인사하며 두 사람이 다치지 않은 것을 보니 흡족하다고 다짐하고는, 스내그스비 부인을 따라 솔즈 암스를 나선다. 밤이 되기 전에, 온 동네의 입길에 오르내리는 그 참사에서 자기가 도무지 상상할 수 없는 어떤 역할을 맡지나 않았을까 하는 그의 의혹은, 스내그스비 부인이 그 한결같은 응시를 집요하게 거두지 않는 통에 거의 확신으로 굳어진다. 그의 정신적 고통이 어찌나 큰지, 그는 차라리 자수하여, 무죄라면 풀어달라고, 유죄라면 법의 지엄한 형벌로 처벌해달라고 요구할까 하는 종잡을 수 없는 생각마저 품는다.\n\n위블 씨와 거피 씨는 아침을 먹고 나서, 잠깐 산책하며 머릿속의 거뭇한 거미줄을 산책으로 걷어낼 수 있는 만큼이라도 걷어내려고, 링컨스 인 광장으로 발을 들여놓는다.\n\n\"지금보다 더 좋은 때는 없을 거야, 토니,\" 둘이 침울하게 광장 네 변을 다 돌고 나자 거피 씨가 말한다. \"우리가 머잖아 합의를 봐야 할 한 가지 문제를 두고 둘이서 몇 마디 나누기에 말이야.\"\n\n\"이봐, 내가 한마디 하겠는데, 윌리엄 G.!\" 상대가 충혈된 눈으로 동행을 노려보며 대꾸한다. \"그게 무슨 음모 따위 문제라면 굳이 꺼낼 것도 없어. 난 그런 거 진절머리가 나, 더는 사양이야. 다음엔 자네마저 불붙거나 펑 터지는 꼴을 보게 될 판이라고.\"\n\n이 가상의 현상이 거피 씨에게는 어찌나 거북한지, 그는 목소리를 떨며 점잔 빼는 투로 말한다. \"토니, 어젯밤 우리가 겪은 일이라면, 자넨 살아생전 다시는 인신공격 따윈 하지 말아야 한다는 교훈으로 삼았어야 했어.\" 이에 위블 씨가 대꾸한다. \"윌리엄, 난 그게 자네한테 살아생전 다시는 음모 따윈 꾸미지 말라는 교훈이 됐어야 한다고 생각했는데.\" 이에 거피 씨가 말한다. \"누가 음모를 꾸민다는 거야?\" 이에 조블링 씨가 받는다. \"아니, 자네가 꾸미잖아!\" 이에 거피 씨가 받아친다. \"아니, 난 안 그래.\" 이에 조블링 씨가 또 받아친다. \"아니, 자넨 그래!\" 이에 거피 씨가 받아친다. \"누가 그래?\" 이에 조블링 씨가 받아친다. \"내가 그래!\" 이에 거피 씨가 받아친다. \"오, 그래?\" 이에 조블링 씨가 받아친다. \"그래, 그렇다고!\" 그러고는 둘 다 이제 열이 오를 대로 올라, 한동안 말없이 걸으며 다시 열을 식힌다.\n\n\"토니,\" 그러더니 거피 씨가 말한다. \"벗에게 달려들 게 아니라 끝까지 들어줬다면, 자네도 오해에 빠지진 않았을 거야. 하지만 자넨 성미가 급하고 사려가 부족해. 토니, 자네는 보는 이의 눈을 사로잡을 만한 것을 죄다 갖춘 사람이면서—\"\n\n\"오! 눈은 꺼지라 그래!\" 위블 씨가 그의 말을 잘라먹으며 외친다. \"할 말이나 하라고!\"\n\n벗이 이렇게 시무룩하고 살벌한 상태인 것을 보고, 거피 씨는 자기 영혼의 그 섬세한 감정을 오로지 상처받은 어조로만 드러내며 다시 입을 연다. \"토니, 우리가 머잖아 합의를 봐야 할 문제가 하나 있다고 할 때, 난 그걸 어떤 종류의 음모와도, 아무리 결백한 음모일지언정, 전혀 별개로 말하는 거야. 자네도 알다시피, 재판에 부쳐지는 모든 사건에서는 증인들이 어떤 사실을 입증할 것인지를 직업적으로 미리 정해둬. 이 불운한 노인—신사 양반의 사망에 관한 심리에서 우리가 어떤 사실을 입증해야 하는지, 우리가 알아두는 게 바람직하지 않겠어, 바람직하지 않겠어?\" (거피 씨는 \"노친네\"라고 할 참이었으나, 상황에는 \"신사 양반\"이 더 어울린다고 생각한다.)\n\n\"무슨 사실? 그 사실 말이지.\"\n\n\"그 심리와 관련된 사실들 말이야. 그건—\" 거피 씨가 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 늘어놓는다. \"우리가 그의 습성에 대해 알던 것, 자네가 그를 마지막으로 본 게 언제인지, 그때 그의 상태가 어땠는지, 우리가 한 발견과 그걸 어떻게 했는지야.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 위블 씨가 말한다. \"그게 대충 그 사실들이지.\"\n\n\"우리가 그 발견을 하게 된 건, 그가 그 별난 식대로 자네와 자정에 약속을 잡았기 때문이야. 그가 글을 못 읽으니, 전에도 자네가 종종 그랬듯이 그에게 어떤 글을 설명해주기로 했지. 나는 자네와 저녁을 함께 보내다가 아래층으로 불려 내려갔고—뭐 그런 식이지. 심리는 망자의 사망 정황에 관한 것일 뿐이니, 이 사실들 너머로 갈 필요는 없을 거야, 자네도 동의하겠지?\"\n\n\"아니!\" 위블 씨가 대꾸한다. \"그렇겠지.\"\n\n\"그럼 이건 음모가 아니지, 아마?\" 상처받은 거피가 말한다.\n\n\"그래,\" 그의 벗이 대꾸한다. \"이것보다 더 나쁜 게 아니라면, 그 말은 거두지.\"\n\n\"자, 토니,\" 거피 씨가 다시 그의 팔을 잡고 천천히 걸음을 옮기며 말한다. \"벗으로서 묻고 싶은데, 자네가 그곳에 계속 사는 게 얼마나 이로운지 곰곰 생각해봤나?\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야?\" 토니가 걸음을 멈추며 말한다.\n\n\"자네가 그곳에 계속 사는 게 얼마나 이로운지 곰곰 생각해봤느냐고?\" 거피 씨가 그를 다시 걷게 하며 되풀이한다.\n\n\"어느 곳에? 그곳에?\" 위블 씨가 넝마와 병 가게 쪽을 가리킨다.\n\n거피 씨가 고개를 끄덕인다.\n\n\"흥, 자네가 무슨 대가를 내건다 해도 거기서 하룻밤을 더 보내진 않을 거야.\" 위블 씨가 핼쑥하게 노려보며 말한다.\n\n\"하지만 진심이야, 토니?\"\n\n\"진심이냐고! 내가 진심 아닌 것처럼 보여? 진심인 것 같다고. 그건 내가 알지.\" 위블 씨가 정말로 진저리를 치며 말한다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 세상천지에 일가친척 하나 없어 보이던 외로운 노인의 그 유품을, 자네가 손에 넣고도 아무에게도 방해받지 않을 가능성이나 개연성—그렇게 봐야 마땅하지—그리고 그가 거기에 정말로 무엇을 쟁여두었는지 자네가 알아낼 수 있다는 확실성이, 어젯밤 일과 견주면 자네한텐 아무 무게도 없다는 거지, 내가 자네 말을 제대로 이해한 거라면?\" 거피 씨가 분한 식욕으로 엄지손가락을 깨물며 말한다.\n\n\"당연히 그렇지. 사람이 거기 산다는 걸 그렇게 태평하게 말해?\" 위블 씨가 분개하여 외친다. \"자네가 가서 살든가.\"\n\n\"오! 나야, 토니!\" 거피 씨가 그를 달래며 말한다. \"난 거기 살아본 적도 없고 지금 거기서 하숙을 구할 수도 없어. 반면에 자넨 이미 하나 갖고 있잖아.\"\n\n\"자네 가지라고.\" 그의 벗이 대꾸한다. \"그리고—으윽!—거기서 마음껏 편히 지내든가.\"\n\n\"그럼 자넨 정말이지 이 시점에,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"이 일을 통째로 접겠다는 거군, 내가 자네 말을 제대로 이해한 거라면, 토니?\"\n\n\"자넨,\" 토니가 더없이 확고한 단호함으로 대꾸한다. \"평생 그보다 더 옳은 말을 한 적이 없어. 정말 접을 거야!\"\n\n그들이 그렇게 이야기를 나누는 사이, 삯마차 한 대가 광장으로 들어오는데, 그 마차 마부석 위로 아주 키 큰 모자 하나가 대중 앞에 모습을 드러낸다. 마차 안에는—따라서 군중에게는 그만큼 드러나지 않으나, 마차가 거의 두 벗의 발치에 멈춰 서니 그들에게는 충분히 드러나는데—점잖은 스몰위드 씨와 스몰위드 부인이, 손녀 주디와 함께 타고 있다.\n\n황급하고 들뜬 기색이 일행에 감돈다. 그리고 키 큰 모자가(젊은 스몰위드 씨 머리에 얹혀) 마차에서 내리자, 늙은 스몰위드 씨가 창밖으로 머리를 쑥 내밀고 거피 씨에게 고함친다. \"안녕하시오, 선생! 안녕하시오!\"\n\n\"이런 아침 시각에 칙 영감과 그 식솔이 여기 무슨 볼일이람!\" 거피 씨가 자기 지인에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다.\n\n\"여보게, 선생,\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 외친다. \"부탁 하나 들어주겠나? 자네와 자네 벗이, 바트와 그 누이가 제 할머니를 모셔 오는 동안, 나를 저 골목 안 술집까지 좀 옮겨주는 너그러움을 베풀어줄 수 있겠나? 늙은이한테 그 정도 호의는 베풀어주겠나, 선생?\"\n\n거피 씨가 벗을 바라보며 캐묻듯 되뇐다. \"골목 안 술집?\" 그러고는 둘이서 그 점잖은 짐을 솔즈 암스로 옮길 채비를 한다.\n\n\"이게 자네 삯이야!\" 노인이 마부에게 사납게 이를 드러내며, 말 안 듣는 주먹을 그에게 흔들면서 말한다. \"여기서 한 푼이라도 더 달라 했단, 법대로 복수해주마. 친애하는 젊은이들, 제발 살살 다뤄주게. 자네들 목을 끌어안게 해주게. 어쩔 수 없는 만큼만 꽉 잡을 테니. 오, 맙소사! 오, 이런! 오, 내 뼈마디!\"\n\n솔즈가 멀지 않은 것이 다행이니, 위블 씨는 거리의 절반도 가기 전에 졸도할 듯한 몰골이 되기 때문이다. 그래도 막힌 호흡을 드러내는 갖가지 갈라진 소리를 토해내는 것 말고는 증세가 더 악화되진 않은 채, 그는 자기 몫의 운반을 완수하고, 그 인자한 노신사는 자기 뜻대로 솔즈 암스의 응접실에 부려진다.\n\n\"오, 맙소사!\" 스몰위드 씨가 안락의자에 앉아 숨을 헐떡이며 주위를 둘러보고 말한다. \"오, 이런! 오, 내 뼈와 등짝! 오, 내 쑤시고 결리는 데! 좀 앉아, 이 춤추고 뛰고 비틀거리고 허둥대는 앵무새 같은 년아! 앉으라고!\"\n\n스몰위드 부인에게 던진 이 짤막한 호통은, 그 불운한 노부인이 발을 디디고 설 때마다 어슬렁거리며 돌아다니다 무생물을 향해 \"절\"을 하고, 마치 마녀의 춤이라도 추듯 재잘대는 소리를 곁들이는 버릇 탓에 나온 것이다. 이 가엾은 노파의 이런 거동에는 어떤 얼빠진 의도 못지않게 신경 질환이 작용한 면도 있겠으나, 이번에는 스몰위드 씨가 앉아 있는 것과 짝을 이루는 윈저식 안락의자를 두고 유난히 활발하게 그 짓을 벌이는 통에, 손주들이 그녀를 그 의자에 눌러 앉히고 나서야 비로소 멈춘다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 부군은 그녀에게 \"고집통 갈까마귀\"라는 정겨운 별칭을 어찌나 술술 퍼붓는지, 놀라울 만큼 여러 번 되풀이한다.\n\n\"여보게, 선생,\" 그러더니 스몰위드 할아버지가 거피 씨에게 말을 건넨다. \"여기서 변고가 있었네. 자네들 둘 중 누구라도 그 소식 들었나?\"\n\n\"들었느냐고요, 선생! 아니, 우리가 발견했는걸요.\"\n\n\"자네들이 발견했다. 자네 둘이 발견했어! 바트, 얘들이 발견했다는구나!\"\n\n두 발견자가 스몰위드 일가를 빤히 쳐다보고, 일가도 그 응시를 똑같이 돌려준다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗들,\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 두 손을 내밀며 칭얼대듯 말한다. \"스몰위드 부인의 오라비 유골을 발견하는 그 우울한 소임을 다해주었으니, 자네들에게 천 번의 감사를 빚졌네.\"\n\n\"네?\" 거피 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"스몰위드 부인의 오라비 말이야, 친애하는 벗—그 사람 유일한 혈육이지. 우리는 사이가 좋질 못했어, 이제 와선 한탄스러운 일이지만, 그자가 도무지 사이좋게 지내려 하질 않았어. 우리를 좋아하질 않았지. 별난 사람이었어—아주 별난 사람이었지. 그가 유언장을 남기지 않았다면(전혀 그럴 성싶지 않지만) 내가 재산 관리 인가장을 받아낼 걸세. 재산을 살피러 내려온 거야. 봉인해둬야 해, 보호해둬야 해. 내려온 거야,\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 열 손가락을 한꺼번에 갈고리처럼 굽혀 허공을 제 쪽으로 그러쥐며 되풀이한다. \"재산을 살피러 말이지.\"\n\n\"내 생각에, 스몰,\" 풀이 죽은 거피 씨가 말한다. \"그 노인이 당신 삼촌이라고 진작 말해줬어도 좋았을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"자네들 둘이 그 양반에 대해 어찌나 입을 다물던지, 나도 똑같이 입을 다물어주길 바라겠거니 했지.\" 그 노물이 몰래 번뜩이는 눈으로 대꾸한다. \"게다가 난 그자가 자랑스럽지도 않았고.\"\n\n\"게다가 그 사람이 그렇든 말든 당신과는 상관없는 일이었으니까요.\" 주디가 말한다. 역시 몰래 번뜩이는 눈으로.\n\n\"그자는 살아생전 날 알아볼 만큼 본 적도 없어.\" 스몰이 한마디 거든다. \"내가 왜 그자를 끌어들여야 하느냐고, 정말!\"\n\n\"그래, 그자는 우리와 연락한 적이 없어, 한탄스러운 일이지,\" 노신사가 끼어든다. \"하지만 난 재산을 살피러 온 거야—서류를 검토하고, 재산을 살피러. 우리는 권리를 확립할 거야. 그건 내 변호사 손에 있어. 저기 길 건너 링컨스 인 필즈의 토킹혼 씨가 친절하게도 내 변호사로 나서주었지. 장담하건대, 그 양반 발밑에선 풀도 안 자라. 크룩은 스몰위드 부인의 유일한 오라비였어. 부인한텐 크룩 말곤 혈육이 없었고, 크룩한텐 스몰위드 부인 말곤 혈육이 없었지. 네 오라비 얘길 하는 거다, 이 유황불 검정 딱정벌레야, 일흔여섯 먹은 그 양반 말이야.\"\n\n스몰위드 부인이 즉시 고개를 흔들며 새된 소리로 외기 시작한다. \"일흔여섯 파운드 칠 실링 칠 펜스! 일흔여섯 천 자루의 돈! 일흔여섯 억조 꾸러미의 지폐!\"\n\n\"누가 나한테 일 쿼트짜리 술잔 좀 주겠나?\" 격분한 그녀의 남편이 손닿는 곳에 던질 만한 것을 못 찾고 어쩔 줄 몰라 주위를 둘러보며 외친다. \"누가 나한테 가래통 좀 갖다주는 호의를 베풀겠나? 누가 단단하고 멍이 들 만한 걸 손에 쥐여줘서 저년한테 내던지게 해주겠나? 이 마귀할멈, 이 고양이년, 이 개년, 이 유황불 짖는 개야!\" 이 대목에서 스몰위드 씨는 제 입심에 한껏 격앙된 나머지, 달리 던질 게 없자 정말로 주디를 할머니에게 집어 던지듯, 이 젊은 처녀를 모을 수 있는 온 힘을 다해 노부인 쪽으로 들이받고는, 의자에 푹석 무너져 내린다.\n\n\"누가 좀, 괜찮다면, 나를 일으켜 흔들어주게.\" 그가 폭삭 주저앉아 만든, 힘없이 버둥대는 보따리 속에서 목소리가 들려온다. \"재산을 살피러 온 거야. 나를 흔들어 일으키고, 옆집에 근무하는 경관을 불러서 재산에 대해 설명을 해줘. 내 변호사가 곧 와서 재산을 지켜줄 거야. 재산에 손대는 놈은 누구든 유배형이나 교수형이다!\" 효성 깊은 손주들이 그를 일으켜 세우자, 그는 헐떡이며, 흔들고 두드리는 늘 하던 회복 절차를 받으면서도, 메아리처럼 여전히 되뇐다. \"그—그 재산! 그 재산! 재산!\"\n\n위블 씨와 거피 씨가 서로를 바라본다. 전자는 그 일을 통째로 단념한 자로서, 후자는 아직 어떤 미련 같은 기대를 품었던 자로서 낭패한 얼굴로. 그러나 스몰위드 측 이해관계에 맞서 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없다. 토킹혼 씨의 서기가 법률 사무소의 제 공식 자리에서 내려와 경관에게 일러두기를, 가장 가까운 혈육에 관한 일은 모두 토킹혼 씨가 책임지고 정확히 처리할 것이며, 서류와 유품은 때가 되면 절차에 따라 정식으로 점유될 것이라 한다. 스몰위드 씨는 이내 자기 권위를 그만큼은 내세우도록 허락받아, 감회에 젖은 방문 길에 옆집으로 옮겨져 위층 플라이트 양의 텅 빈 방으로 들어가는데, 그 모습이 마치 그녀의 새장에 새로 들여놓은 흉측한 맹금처럼 보인다.\n\n이 뜻밖의 상속인이 들이닥쳤다는 소문이 골목에 금세 퍼지면서, 여전히 솔즈에는 이득이 되고 골목은 활기를 잃지 않는다. 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인은 정말로 유언장이 없다면 그 젊은이가 딱하다고 여기며, 유산에서 한몫 두둑이 떼어 그에게 선물로 줘야 마땅하다고 본다. 어린 파이퍼와 어린 퍼킨스는, 챈서리 거리 행인들의 공포의 대상인 그 들썩이는 아이 무리의 일원으로서, 하루 종일 펌프 뒤와 아치 길 아래에서 재가 되어 흩어지고, 그 잔해를 두고 거친 비명과 야유가 벌어진다. 꼬마 스윌스와 멜빌슨 양은 단골들과 싹싹하게 담소를 나누는데, 이 이례적인 사태가 직업인과 비직업인 사이의 장벽을 허문다고 느끼기 때문이다. 보그스비 씨는 \"온 출연진의 총력을 다한 합창이 곁들여진 인기곡 ‘죽음의 왕’\"을 그 주의 위대한 화음 모임 주제로 내걸고, 광고지에 이렇게 알린다. \"제이 지 비는 이번에 상당한 추가 비용을 무릅쓰고 이를 마련하니, 이는 술집에서 다수의 점잖은 분들이 두루 표한 바람에 따른 것이며, 또한 그토록 많은 이의 심금을 울린 근래의 어느 우울한 사건에 경의를 표하고자 함입니다.\" 망자와 관련하여 골목이 유난히 마음 졸이는 점이 한 가지 있으니, 곧 안에 채워 넣을 것이 그토록 적은데도 어른 크기 관이라는 허구만은 지켜져야 한다는 것이다. 그날 중에 장의사가 솔즈 술집에서 \"육 척짜리\"를 짜라는 주문을 받았다고 밝히자, 모두의 노심초사가 크게 누그러지고, 스몰위드 씨의 처사가 그에게 큰 명예가 된다고들 여긴다.\n\n골목 밖, 그것도 한참 밖에서도 상당한 흥분이 인다. 과학자와 철학자 들이 구경하러 오고, 마차들이 같은 뜻으로 찾아온 의사들을 모퉁이에 내려놓으며, 가연성 기체니 인화수소니 하는, 골목이 일찍이 상상도 못 한 유식한 이야기가 오간다. 이 권위자들 가운데 일부는(물론 가장 현명한 자들이다) 망자가 그런 식으로 죽었을 리 없다고 분개하며 우긴다. 다른 권위자들이 그들에게 일깨우기를, 그런 죽음의 증거에 관한 어떤 조사가 「철학회보」 제육권에 재수록되어 있고, 또 영국 의학 법리학에 관한 제법 알려진 책 한 권도 있으며, 마찬가지로 베로나의 참사회원 비안키니가—학술서 한두 권을 써냈고 당대에 더러 이성의 번득임이 엿보이던 인물로—세밀히 기술한 이탈리아의 코르넬리아 바우디 백작 부인 사건도 있고, 또 그 주제를 기어이 파고들겠다고 한 골치 아픈 프랑스인 둘, 포데레 씨와 메르 씨의 증언도 있으며, 더 나아가 한때 제법 유명했던 프랑스 외과의 르 카 씨—그런 사건이 일어난 집에 살았던 데다 그에 대한 기록까지 써내는 무례함을 보인 자다—의 뒷받침하는 증언도 있다고 하는데도, 그들은 여전히 고 크룩 씨가 그런 옆길로 세상을 떠나겠다고 고집부린 것을 도무지 정당화할 수 없으며 개인적으로 불쾌하기까지 하다고 여긴다. 골목은 이 모든 것을 알아듣지 못할수록 더 좋아하고, 솔즈 암스에 쟁여둔 상품을 한층 즐겁게 누린다. 이윽고 그림 신문의 화가가, 콘월 해안의 난파선에서부터 하이드 파크의 열병식이나 맨체스터의 집회까지 무엇에든 갖다 붙일 수 있는 배경과 인물을 미리 그려둔 채 찾아온다. 그러고는 영원히 잊지 못할 퍼킨스 부인의 방에서, 거기 그 자리에서 판 위에 크룩 씨의 집을 실물 크기로 그려 넣으니, 사실은 실물보다 한참 크게, 아예 신전 하나로 만들어버린다. 마찬가지로 그 운명의 방 문 안을 들여다보도록 허락받자, 그는 그 방을 길이는 사분의 삼 마일에 높이는 오십 야드로 묘사하는데, 골목은 이를 유난히 마음에 들어 한다. 이 내내 앞서 말한 두 신사는 집집마다 들락거리며 철학 논쟁을 거들고—사방을 쏘다니며 누구의 말이든 귀담아듣고—그러면서도 늘 솔즈의 응접실로 잠수해 들어가 게걸스러운 작은 펜으로 박엽지에 갈겨쓴다.\n\n마침내 검시관과 그의 심리가 전과 똑같이 찾아드는데, 다만 검시관이 이번 사건을 흔치 않은 일로 여겨 애지중지하며, 배심원 신사들에게 사사로이 이르기를, \"여러분, 저 옆집은 어째 불운한 집인 듯합니다, 운명 지어진 집 말입니다. 그래도 우리가 더러 그런 일을 마주치곤 하는데, 이런 건 우리가 설명할 수 없는 수수께끼지요!\" 그러고 나서 육 척짜리가 등장하여 큰 찬사를 받는다.\n\n이 모든 절차에서 거피 씨가 맡은 몫은, 증언할 때를 빼면 어찌나 미미한지, 그는 일개 사인처럼 떠밀려 다니며 그 비밀스러운 집을 바깥에서나 맴돌 따름이다. 거기서 그는 스몰위드 씨가 문에 자물쇠를 채우는 것을 보는 굴욕을 겪고, 자기가 그 집에서 따돌려졌음을 쓰라리게 깨닫는다. 그러나 이 절차들이 마무리되기 전에, 다시 말해 그 참사 이튿날 밤, 거피 씨에게는 반드시 데드록 마님에게 해야 할 말이 한 가지 있다.\n\n그런 까닭에, 가라앉는 가슴과, 솔즈 암스에 갇혀 두려움과 망보기로 빚어진 그 풀 죽은 죄책감을 안고서, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이는 저녁 일곱 시쯤 시내 저택에 모습을 드러내고 마님을 뵙기를 청한다. 머큐리가 마님은 만찬에 나가실 참이라고 답한다. 문 앞에 마차가 있는 게 안 보이느냐는 것이다. 그렇다, 그도 문 앞에 마차가 있는 것을 본다. 하지만 그는 마님도 뵙고 싶다는 것이다.\n\n머큐리는, 잠시 후 대기 중인 동료 신사에게 떠벌리겠지만, \"저 젊은 놈을 한 대 갈겨주고\" 싶은 마음이다. 그러나 그가 받은 지시는 명확하다. 그래서 그는 시무룩하게, 이 젊은이를 서재로 올려보내야겠거니 한다. 그곳, 그리 밝지 않은 큰 방에 젊은이를 남겨두고 그는 보고하러 간다.\n\n거피 씨는 사방의 어둠 속을 들여다보며, 어디서나 거뭇하게 그을리고 희끄무레해진 작은 석탄인지 나무인지의 무더기를 발견한다. 이윽고 그는 사각거리는 소리를 듣는다. 그건—? 아니, 유령이 아니라, 더없이 눈부시게 차려입은, 멀쩡한 살과 피다.\n\n\"마님께 용서를 빌어야겠습니다.\" 거피 씨가 잔뜩 풀이 죽어 더듬거린다. \"불편한 시각에—\"\n\n\"아무 때나 와도 좋다고 했을 텐데.\" 그녀가 의자를 잡으며, 지난번처럼 그를 똑바로 바라본다.\n\n\"감사합니다, 마님. 마님께선 참으로 너그러우십니다.\"\n\n\"앉아도 좋네.\" 그 어조에는 너그러움이 그리 묻어 있지 않다.\n\n\"마님, 제가 앉아서 마님을 붙들어둘 만한 일인지 모르겠습니다. 저는—제가 마님을 뵙는 영광을 누렸을 때 말씀드린 그 편지들을 가져오지 못했으니까요.\"\n\n\"그저 그 말을 하러 온 것인가?\"\n\n\"그저 그 말씀을 드리러 왔습니다, 마님.\" 거피 씨는 풀 죽고 실망하고 마음이 불편한 데다, 그녀의 자태가 뿜는 광채와 미모에 한층 더 기가 꺾인다.\n\n그녀는 그 영향력을 완벽히 알고 있으니, 누구에게든 그 효과가 한 톨도 빗나가지 않도록 너무도 잘 연구해두었다. 그녀가 그토록 한결같고 차갑게 그를 바라보자, 그는 그녀 생각의 빛깔이 정녕 어떠한지를 조금도 가늠할 길잡이가 없음을 의식할 뿐 아니라, 자신이 매 순간 그녀로부터, 말하자면, 점점 더 멀리 밀려나고 있음마저 느낀다.\n\n그녀가 입을 열지 않으리란 건 분명하다. 그러니 그가 입을 열 수밖에.\n\n\"한마디로, 마님,\" 거피 씨가 비굴하게 뉘우치는 도둑처럼 말한다. \"제가 그 편지들을 받기로 했던 사람이 갑작스레 최후를 맞아서—\" 그가 말을 멈춘다. 데드록 마님이 차분히 그 문장을 마무리 짓는다.\n\n\"그래서 편지들도 그 사람과 함께 없어졌다는 건가?\"\n\n거피 씨는 할 수만 있다면 아니라고 하고 싶지만—숨기지 못한다.\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다, 마님.\"\n\n지금 그녀의 얼굴에서 안도의 빛이 조금이라도 번뜩이는 것을 그가 볼 수 있다면? 아니, 그는 그런 것을 볼 수 없으니, 설령 그 의연한 겉모습이 그를 아주 밀쳐내지 않고, 그가 그 너머와 그 언저리를 살피고 있었다 한들 마찬가지다.\n\n그는 자기 실패에 대한 어색한 변명을 한두 마디 더듬거린다.\n\n\"이게 자네가 할 말의 전부인가?\" 데드록 마님이, 그의 말을 끝까지—혹은 그가 더듬거리며 마칠 수 있는 데까지—다 듣고 나서 묻는다.\n\n거피 씨는 그게 전부라고 생각한다.\n\n\"내게 더 할 말이 정말 없는지 잘 헤아려보는 게 좋을 거네. 이번이 자네가 그럴 기회를 갖는 마지막일 테니.\"\n\n거피 씨는 확실하다. 그리고 정녕 지금은 그런 바람이 도무지 없다.\n\n\"그걸로 됐네. 변명은 됐어. 잘 가게!\" 그러고는 그녀가 종을 울려, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이를 배웅하라고 머큐리를 부른다.\n\n그러나 그 집에는, 바로 그 순간, 토킹혼이라는 이름의 노인이 마침 있다. 그리고 그 노인이 조용한 발걸음으로 서재로 다가와, 바로 그 순간 문손잡이에 손을 얹고—들어와서—방을 나서던 젊은이와 정면으로 마주친다.\n\n노인과 마님 사이에 단 한 번 눈길이 오가고, 한순간, 늘 내려져 있던 차양이 휙 올라간다. 의심이, 간절하고도 날카로운 의심이 내다본다. 또 한순간, 다시 닫힌다.\n\n\"실례했습니다, 데드록 마님. 천 번 만 번 실례했습니다. 이 시각에 여기 계시다니 참으로 드문 일이라서요. 방이 비었으려니 했습니다. 실례했습니다!\"\n\n\"잠깐!\" 그녀가 무심하게 그를 불러 세운다. \"여기 계세요, 부탁드려요. 나는 만찬에 나갑니다. 이 젊은이에게 더 할 말은 없어요!\"\n\n당황한 젊은이는 나가면서 인사를 굽실하고, 필즈의 토킹혼 씨가 안녕하시기를 비굴하게 바란다.\n\n\"어, 어?\" 변호사가 구부정한 눈썹 아래로 그를 바라보며 말한다. 다시 볼 필요도 없으면서—그는 그런 사람이 아니지만. \"켄지 앤드 카보이 사람이 분명하지?\"\n\n\"켄지 앤드 카보이입니다, 토킹혼 씨. 이름은 거피입니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고. 어허, 고맙네, 거피 군, 난 아주 잘 있네!\"\n\n\"그 말씀 들으니 기쁩니다, 선생님. 우리 직업의 명예를 위해서라도 선생님은 아무리 건강하셔도 지나치지 않지요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 거피 군!\"\n\n거피 씨가 슬그머니 물러간다. 토킹혼 씨는, 그 구식의 빛바랜 검은 옷차림이 데드록 마님의 광채와 어찌나 대조되는지, 그녀를 부축해 계단 아래 마차까지 모신다. 그는 턱을 문지르며 돌아오고, 그날 저녁 내내 턱을 꽤나 문지른다.\n\n“그래, 이게,” 조지 씨가 말한다. “뭘까? 공포탄인가, 실탄인가? 헛불인가, 진짜 발포인가?”\n\n부대원이 골똘히 살피는 대상은 펼쳐진 편지 한 통인데, 그를 단단히 헷갈리게 만드는 모양이다. 그는 편지를 팔 길이만큼 멀찍이 떼어 들여다보다가, 바짝 끌어당겨 보고, 오른손에 쥐었다가 왼손에 쥐었다가, 고개를 이쪽으로 기울여 읽고 저쪽으로 기울여 읽고, 눈썹을 찌푸렸다가 치켜올렸다가 해도 좀처럼 마음이 놓이지 않는다. 그는 두툼한 손바닥으로 편지를 탁자에 펴 누른 다음, 생각에 잠겨 사격장을 오르락내리락하며 이따금 그 앞에 멈춰 서서 새로운 눈길로 다시 들여다본다. 그래도 소용이 없다. “이게,” 조지 씨는 여전히 곱씹는다. “공포탄인가, 실탄인가?”\n\n필 스쿼드는 저만치서 솔과 페인트 통을 들고 표적에 흰 칠을 하느라 바쁜데, 빠른 행진곡 박자로, 북과 피리 가락처럼, 자기는 두고 온 그 처녀에게 반드시 돌아가야 하고 또 돌아가고 말겠다는 노래를 나직이 휘파람으로 분다.\n\n“필!” 부대원이 부르며 손짓한다.\n\n필은 늘 하던 식으로 다가오는데, 처음에는 마치 딴 데로 가려는 양 비스듬히 빠지다가 이내 총검 돌격하듯 상관에게 들이닥친다. 더러운 얼굴에는 흰 페인트 자국 몇 점이 도드라지게 박혀 있고, 그는 솔 자루로 하나뿐인 눈썹을 긁적인다.\n\n“차렷, 필! 이것 좀 들어봐.”\n\n“각오됐습니다, 대장님, 각오됐습니다.”\n\n“‘귀하. 굳이 상기시켜 드릴 법적 의무는 없습니다만(귀하도 아시다시피), 매슈 배그닛 씨가 귀하 앞으로 발행하고 귀하가 인수한, 금액 구십칠 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스, 만기 두 달짜리 어음이 내일 만기가 됩니다. 제시되는 즉시 결제하실 수 있도록 준비해 주시기 바랍니다. 조슈아 스몰위드 올림.’ 어떻게 생각해, 필?”\n\n“말썽입니다, 두목.”\n\n“왜?”\n\n“제 생각엔요,” 필이 솔 자루로 이마에 가로 주름을 골똘히 그어 보고는 대답한다. “돈을 내놓으라고 할 땐 늘 말썽스러운 일이 벌어지게 마련이거든요.”\n\n“이봐, 필,” 부대원이 탁자에 걸터앉으며 말한다. “처음부터 끝까지 따져보면, 나는 이 원금에다가 이자며 이것저것 해서 그 절반은 더 갚았다고 할 수 있어.”\n\n필은 한두 걸음 비스듬히 물러서며 비뚤어진 얼굴을 도무지 알 수 없게 일그러뜨리는데, 이 일로 거래의 앞날이 한층 밝아졌다고는 보지 않는다는 뜻이다.\n\n“그리고 또 이봐, 필,” 부대원이 손짓으로 그의 성급한 결론을 막으며 말한다. “이 어음은 늘 그쪽 말로 갱신하기로 양해가 돼 있었어. 그리고 셀 수 없이 갱신돼 왔단 말이지. 이제 뭐라고 할 텐가?”\n\n“이번엔 그 셀 수 없는 게 결국 끝장났구나, 그렇게 말하겠습니다.”\n\n“그래? 흠! 나도 거의 같은 생각이야.”\n\n“조슈아 스몰위드라면, 의자에 실려서 여기 왔던 그 양반입니까?”\n\n“바로 그 사람이지.”\n\n“두목,” 필이 더없이 엄숙하게 말한다. “그자는 성미로 보면 거머리고, 하는 짓으로 보면 나사에 죔쇠고, 몸 비트는 걸로 보면 뱀이고, 집게발로 보면 가재입니다.”\n\n이렇게 자기 소견을 절묘하게 토해낸 스쿼드 씨는, 더 할 말을 기다리는지 잠시 살피다가, 늘 하던 일련의 동작으로 손에 잡고 있던 표적으로 돌아가, 아까 그 음악적 수단을 통해 자기는 반드시 그 가상의 아가씨에게 돌아가고 말겠다고 힘차게 알린다. 편지를 접은 조지는 그쪽으로 걸어간다.\n\n“방법이 하나 있긴 합니다, 대장님,” 필이 그를 능청스레 쳐다보며 말한다. “이걸 해결할 방법이요.”\n\n“돈을 갚는 거겠지? 나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠다.”\n\n필은 고개를 젓는다. “아뇨, 두목, 아뇨, 그렇게 고약한 건 아니고요. 방법이 하나 있습니다,” 필이 솔을 아주 예술적으로 한 번 휘두르며 말한다. “지금 제가 하고 있는 거죠.”\n\n“흰 칠 말이냐.”\n\n필이 고개를 끄덕인다.\n“그것참 근사한 방법이겠다! 그러면 배그닛네가 어떻게 되는지 알기나 해? 내 묵은 빚을 갚느라 그 사람들이 거덜 나는 거 몰라? 너 참 도덕적인 인간이다,” 부대원이 적잖은 분노를 담아 그 특유의 너그러운 눈길로 그를 쳐다보며 말한다. “정말이지, 그래, 필!”\n\n표적 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇은 필은, 솔로 비유적인 곡선을 잔뜩 그어대고 엄지로 가장자리 둘레의 흰 면을 매끈하게 다듬으면서도, 배그닛네에 대한 책임을 깜박했을 뿐 그 훌륭한 식구 누구의 머리카락 하나라도 다칠 마음은 없었노라고 진지하게 항변하는 참인데, 그때 바깥 긴 복도에서 발소리가 들리고, 조지가 집에 있는지 궁금해하는 쾌활한 목소리가 들린다. 필은 주인을 한 번 쳐다보고는 절뚝거리며 일어서서 “두목 여기 계십니다, 배그닛 부인! 여기 계세요!” 하고 외치고, 늙은 아낙 본인이 배그닛 씨를 거느리고 나타난다.\n\n이 늙은 아낙은 한 해의 어느 철에든 외출 차림으로 나설 때 회색 천 망토를 두르지 않는 법이 없는데, 그 망토는 거칠고 몹시 닳았으되 아주 깨끗하니, 의심할 여지 없이 배그닛 부인과 우산 한 자루와 함께 지구 반대편에서 유럽 본국까지 흘러들어온 덕분에 배그닛 씨에게 그토록 흥미로운 물건이 된 바로 그 옷이다. 후자인 충실한 부속품 또한 늙은 아낙이 바깥나들이를 할 때면 어김없이 한 부분을 차지한다. 그것은 이승에서 알려진 그 어떤 색도 아니고, 손잡이는 골이 진 나무 갈고리이며, 뱃머리, 곧 부리에 해당하는 부분에는 금속 물건 하나가 박혀 있는데, 길가 문 위에 달린 부채꼴 채광창의 작은 모형이나 안경 한 짝에서 빼낸 타원형 알 하나를 닮았다. 다만 이 장식물은 영국군과 오래 함께한 물건에 마땅히 바랄 법한, 제자리에 끈질기게 붙어 있는 성질은 갖추지 못했다. 늙은 아낙의 우산은 허리께가 흐물흐물한 품새라 코르셋이라도 받쳐줘야 할 형편인데, 이런 꼴이 된 까닭은 아마 여러 해 동안 집에서는 찬장으로, 여행 길에는 손가방으로 봉사해온 탓일 게다. 그녀는 우산을 결코 펴는 법이 없으니, 넉넉한 두건이 달린 자기 믿음직한 망토를 더없이 신뢰하기 때문이라, 대개는 그 도구를 지팡이처럼 써서 장을 볼 때 고깃덩이나 푸성귀 단을 가리키거나 상인들의 주의를 정답게 쿡 찔러 끄는 데 쓴다. 펄럭이는 뚜껑이 둘 달린 일종의 버들 우물 같은 장바구니가 없으면 그녀는 결코 바깥나들이를 하지 않는다. 그리하여 이 믿음직한 길벗들을 거느리고, 거친 밀짚 보닛 밖으로 정직하고 햇볕에 그은 얼굴을 쾌활하게 내민 채, 배그닛 부인은 이제 혈색 좋고 환한 모습으로 조지의 사격장에 들어선다.\n\n“그래, 조지, 이 친구야,” 그녀가 말한다. “이 햇살 좋은 아침에 자네 어떻게 지내?”\n\n배그닛 부인은 그에게 정답게 악수를 건네고는, 걸어온 끝에 긴 숨을 내쉬며 한숨 돌리려고 자리에 앉는다. 짐수레 위며 그런 자리에서 단련된, 어디서든 손쉽게 쉬는 재주가 있는 터라, 그녀는 거친 의자에 걸터앉아 보닛 끈을 풀고 보닛을 뒤로 젖히고 팔짱을 끼고 더없이 편안한 표정을 짓는다.\n\n그사이 배그닛 씨는 오랜 전우와, 또 필과 악수를 나누었는데, 필에게도 배그닛 부인이 마찬가지로 너그러운 고갯짓과 미소를 보낸다.\n\n“자, 조지,” 배그닛 부인이 활기차게 말한다. “우리가 왔다네, 리그넘하고 나하고”—그녀는 남편을 흔히 이 호칭으로 부르는데, 둘이 처음 알게 되었을 무렵 그의 인상이 워낙 단단하고 질긴 데 경의를 표해 ‘리그넘 비테’가 그의 옛 연대 별명이었던 까닭이라고들 한다—“늘 하던 대로 그 담보 건을 깔끔하게 처리하려고 잠깐 들렀어. 새 어음을 그이한테 줘서 서명하게 해, 조지. 그럼 사내답게 척 서명할 테니.”\n\n“오늘 아침 내가 댁으로 찾아가려던 참이었어요.” 부대원이 마지못해 말한다.\n\n“그래, 자네가 오늘 아침 우리한테 올 줄 알았지. 그래도 우리가 일찍 나서서, 그 착한 우리 울리치한테 누이들 보라고 맡기고 울위치를 떠나, 대신 자네한테 온 거야—보다시피! 리그넘이 요즘 워낙 매여 지내고 운동을 못 하니, 걷는 게 몸에 좋거든. 그런데 왜 그래, 조지?” 배그닛 부인이 쾌활한 말끝을 멈추며 묻는다. “자네 평소 같지가 않아.”\n\n“좀 평소 같지가 않습니다,” 부대원이 대답한다. “좀 마음이 어수선해서요, 배그닛 부인.”\n\n그녀의 밝고 재빠른 눈이 당장 진실을 알아챈다. “조지!” 그녀가 집게손가락을 치켜든다. “리그넘의 그 담보에 무슨 문제가 생겼다는 말은 하지 마! 그러지 마, 조지, 애들을 봐서라도!”\n\n부대원은 괴로운 낯빛으로 그녀를 쳐다본다.\n\n“조지,” 배그닛 부인이 강조하려고 두 팔을 다 쓰고 이따금 펼친 손을 무릎에 탁탁 내리치며 말한다. “자네가 리그넘의 그 담보에 무슨 문제가 닥치게 내버려뒀고, 그 사람을 그 일에 엮어 넣었고, 우리를 다 차압당할 위험에 빠뜨렸다면—자네 얼굴에 차압당한다고 활자처럼 또렷이 쓰여 있어, 조지—자네는 부끄러운 짓을 한 거고 우릴 모질게 속인 거야. 정말이지, 모질게, 조지. 자!”\n\n배그닛 씨는 평소 펌프나 가로등 기둥처럼 꿈쩍도 않는 사람이건만, 큼직한 오른손을 벗어진 머리 위에 얹어 마치 물벼락이라도 막으려는 양 하고는 몹시 불안한 눈으로 배그닛 부인을 쳐다본다.\n\n“조지,” 그 늙은 아낙이 말한다. “난 자네가 어이없어! 조지, 난 자네가 부끄러워! 조지, 자네가 이런 짓을 했을 줄은 정말 믿을 수가 없어! 자네가 이끼 한 점 못 모으고 굴러다니는 돌멩이라는 건 늘 알았지만, 배그닛하고 애들이 깔고 누울 그 알량한 이끼마저 가져가 버릴 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어. 그이가 얼마나 부지런하고 착실한 사람인지 자네도 알잖아. 퀘벡하고 몰타하고 울리치가 뭔지 자네도 알면서, 자네가 우리를 이렇게 만들 마음을 먹었거나 먹을 수 있었으리라곤 정말 생각도 못 했어. 아, 조지!” 배그닛 부인이 더없이 진심 어린 몸짓으로 망토 자락을 끌어모아 눈물을 훔친다. “어떻게 그럴 수가 있어?”\n\n배그닛 부인이 말을 그치자, 배그닛 씨는 물벼락이 끝나기라도 한 양 머리에서 손을 떼고 조지 씨를 시무룩하게 바라보는데, 조지 씨는 새하얗게 질려 회색 망토와 밀짚 보닛을 괴로운 눈으로 쳐다본다.\n\n“맷,” 부대원이 가라앉은 목소리로, 그에게 말을 건네면서도 여전히 그의 아내를 바라보며 말한다. “자네가 이렇게까지 마음 아파해서 미안하네. 사실은 그렇게까지 나쁘지는 않기를 바라거든. 분명히 오늘 아침 이 편지를 받긴 했지만”—그는 그것을 소리 내어 읽는다—“그래도 아직 바로잡을 수 있길 바라네. 굴러다니는 돌멩이 얘기로 말하자면, 그래, 자네 말이 옳아. 난 굴러다니는 돌멩이가 맞아. 게다가 정말이지, 난 누구의 길에서도 굴러서 털끝만 한 득이라도 끼친 적이 없는 것 같아. 하지만 이 늙은 떠돌이 전우가 자네 아내며 식구를 내가 좋아하는 것보다 더 좋아할 사람은 없을 거야, 맷. 그러니 부디 최대한 너그럽게 봐주게. 내가 자네한테 뭘 숨겼다곤 생각 말고. 이 편지를 받은 지 십오 분도 채 안 됐으니까.”\n\n“여보,” 배그닛 씨가 잠깐 침묵 끝에 중얼거린다. “저 친구한테 내 생각 좀 전해주겠어?”\n\n“아! 저 사람은 왜,” 배그닛 부인이 반은 웃고 반은 울며 대답한다. “북아메리카에서 조 파우치네 과부랑 결혼하지 않았을까? 그랬으면 이런 고생은 안 했을 텐데.”\n\n“여기 이 사람이,” 배그닛 씨가 말한다. “정확히 짚었네—자넨 왜 그렇게 안 했나?”\n\n“글쎄, 지금쯤 그 과부는 더 나은 남편을 만났겠지, 그러길 바라네,” 부대원이 대답한다. “어쨌든, 오늘 이 자리에 나는 조 파우치네 과부랑 결혼하지 않은 채로 서 있단 말이지. 어떡하면 좋겠나? 내가 가진 건 자네도 다 봐서 알 거야. 내 것이 아니라 자네 거지. 한마디만 하게, 그럼 한 조각 남김없이 다 팔아 치울 테니. 필요한 금액 비슷하게라도 받을 수 있으리라 기대했으면 진작에 다 팔아 버렸을 거야. 자네나 자네 식구를 곤경에 내버려 둘 거라곤 생각 말게, 맷. 차라리 나 자신을 먼저 팔겠어. 다만,” 부대원이 제 가슴을 자조하듯 한 대 치며 말한다. “이런 중고 헌 물건 따위를 사 줄 사람을 누구든 알기만 한다면 좋겠네.”\n\n“여보,” 배그닛 씨가 중얼거린다. “내 생각 한 토막 더 전해줘.”\n\n“조지,” 늙은 아낙이 말한다. “곰곰이 따져보면 자넬 그렇게 탓할 일은 아니야. 다만 밑천도 없이 이 일을 떠맡은 건 빼고.”\n\n“그게 딱 나다웠지!” 뉘우치는 부대원이 고개를 저으며 말한다. “나다워, 나도 알아.”\n\n“조용히! 여기 이 사람은,” 배그닛 씨가 말한다. “정확해—내 생각을 전하는 방식이 말이야—끝까지 들어!”\n\n“바로 그때, 자네는 애당초 담보를 부탁하지 말았어야 했고, 이것저것 다 따져보면 받지도 말았어야 했어, 조지. 하지만 이미 엎질러진 물은 어쩔 수 없지. 자넨 좀 들떠 있긴 해도, 할 수 있는 한 늘 명예롭고 솔직한 사람이야. 그렇지만 이런 일이 머리 위에 매달려 있으면 우리가 불안해하는 게 당연하다는 건 자네도 인정 못 할 리 없겠지. 그러니 다 같이 잊고 용서하자, 조지. 자! 다 같이 잊고 용서하자고!”\n\n배그닛 부인이 한 손은 그에게, 다른 한 손은 남편에게 정직한 손을 내미니, 조지 씨도 각각에게 한 손씩 내밀어 잡고는 말을 이어간다.\n\n“두 분께 정말 장담하는데, 이 빚을 갚을 수만 있다면 못 할 일이 없어요. 하지만 내가 그러모을 수 있었던 건 두 달마다 이걸 유지하는 데 죄다 들어갔단 말입니다. 여기서 필이랑 나는 충분히 검소하게 살았어요. 그래도 이 사격장이 기대한 만큼 벌이가 되질 않아서요, 한마디로—한마디로 돈 찍어내는 조폐국은 아니거든요. 내가 이걸 맡은 게 잘못이었냐고요? 그래요, 잘못이었죠. 하지만 어떻게 보면 그 길로 끌려 들어간 셈이고, 이게 날 안정시키고 자리 잡게 해줄 거라 여겼던 거예요. 내가 그런 기대를 품었던 건 부디 눈감아 주세요. 그리고 정말이지, 두 분께 정말 고맙고, 또 내 자신이 정말 부끄럽습니다.” 이 끝맺는 말과 함께 조지 씨는 잡고 있던 두 손을 각각 한 번씩 흔들고는 놓아주고, 떡 벌어진 가슴을 펴고 꼿꼿이 선 자세로 한두 걸음 물러서는데, 마치 마지막 고백을 마치고 당장 정식 군례를 받으며 총살당할 사람 같다.\n\n“조지, 끝까지 들어!” 배그닛 씨가 아내를 흘끗 보며 말한다. “여보, 계속해!”\n\n이 묘한 방식으로 끝까지 진술하게 된 배그닛 씨가 한 말이라곤, 이 편지는 한시도 지체 없이 처리해야 하고, 조지와 자기가 곧장 스몰위드 씨를 직접 찾아가는 게 좋겠으며, 무엇보다 우선할 일은 돈을 한 푼도 만지지 않은 배그닛 씨를 구제해 손해를 면하게 하는 것이라는 게 전부다. 조지 씨는 전적으로 동의하고는 모자를 쓰고 배그닛 씨와 함께 적진을 향해 행군할 채비를 한다.\n\n“여자가 성급하게 한 말일랑 마음에 두지 마, 조지,” 배그닛 부인이 그의 어깨를 토닥이며 말한다. “내 늙은 리그넘을 자네한테 맡길게. 자네가 이 일을 무사히 넘기게 해주리라 믿어.”\n\n부대원은 참 다정한 말이라며, 어떻게든 리그넘이 이 일을 무사히 넘기게 하겠노라고 대답한다. 그러자 배그닛 부인은 망토와 바구니와 우산을 챙겨, 다시 눈을 반짝이며 나머지 식구에게로 집으로 돌아가고, 두 전우는 스몰위드 씨를 누그러뜨리겠다는 희망찬 심부름 길에 나선다.\n\n스몰위드 씨와의 어떤 협상에서든 조지 씨와 매슈 배그닛 씨보다 만족스럽게 빠져나올 가능성이 더 낮은 두 사람이 잉글랜드에 또 있을지는 매우 합리적으로 의심해 볼 만하다. 또한 군인다운 풍채와 떡 벌어진 네모진 어깨와 묵직한 발걸음에도 불구하고, 스몰위드식 세상사 전반에 관한 한 이 두 사람보다 더 순진하고 서툰 어린애가 그 안에 또 있을지도. 두 사람이 더없이 엄숙한 얼굴로 거리를 지나 마운트 플레전트 쪽으로 나아가는데, 배그닛 씨는 동행이 생각에 잠긴 것을 보고 배그닛 부인이 아까 터뜨린 말에 대해 한마디 하는 것이 벗으로서의 도리라 여긴다.\n\n“조지, 자네도 우리 마누라 알지—우유처럼 곱고 순한 사람이야. 그런데 애들 얘기를—아니면 내 얘기를—건드리면, 화약처럼 터진다네.”\n\n“그게 다 그분이 훌륭한 증거지, 맷!”\n\n“조지,” 배그닛 씨가 똑바로 앞을 보며 말한다. “우리 마누라가—하는 일이라면—훌륭한 증거 아닌 게 없어. 어느 정도든. 난 절대 그렇게 말 안 하지만. 군기는 지켜야 하거든.”\n\n“그분은 황금만큼 값진 분이야.” 부대원이 말한다.\n\n“황금이라?” 배그닛 씨가 말한다. “내 말 들어봐. 우리 마누라 몸무게가—십이 스톤 육 파운드야. 그 무게만큼을—어떤 금속으로 준다 해도—내가 우리 마누라랑 바꾸겠나? 천만에. 왜냐고? 우리 마누라라는 금속은—제일 값진 금속보다—훨씬 더 값지거든. 게다가 그 사람은 통째로 금속이야!”\n\n“자네 말이 맞아, 맷!”\n\n“그 사람이 날 맞아들이고—반지를 받았을 때—나하고 애들 밑에—마음과 머리를 다해, 평생토록 입대한 거야. 그 사람은 어찌나 진지한지,” 배그닛 씨가 말한다. “또 어찌나 제 깃발에 충실한지—누가 손가락 하나로 우릴 건드리면—그 사람이 떨쳐나와—무기를 들고 버틴다네. 우리 마누라가 어쩌다 한 번—임무에 충실하느라—허공에 대고 발포하더라도—못 본 척 넘겨주게, 조지. 그 사람은 충성스러우니까!”\n\n“아니, 그분께 복이 있길, 맷,” 부대원이 대답한다. “난 그 일로 그분을 더 높이 보는걸!”\n\n“자네 말이 맞아!” 배그닛 씨가 더없이 뜨거운 열의로 말하지만, 근육 하나 풀어지는 일 없이 그대로다. “우리 마누라를 지브롤터 바위만큼—높이 본다 해도—그 사람 덕에 비하면 여전히 낮게 보는 거야. 하지만 그 사람 앞에선 절대 그렇다고 인정 안 해. 군기는 지켜야 하거든.”\n\n이런 찬사를 늘어놓는 사이 두 사람은 마운트 플레전트에, 또 스몰위드 영감의 집에 다다른다. 문은 변함없는 주디가 열어주는데, 그녀는 두 사람을 머리끝부터 발끝까지 별 호감 없이, 아니 사실은 악의 가득한 비웃음으로 훑어보고는, 들여보낼지를 신탁에게 묻겠다며 그들을 그 자리에 세워둔 채 들어간다. 그녀가 꿀 같은 입술에 들어오고 싶으면 들어와도 좋다는 말을 달고 돌아오는 정황으로 미루어 신탁이 허락을 내린 모양이다. 이리하여 특권을 얻은 두 사람은 안으로 들어가, 종이 족욕통이라도 되는 양 의자 서랍에 발을 담그고 있는 스몰위드 씨와, 노래하지 못하게 한 새처럼 방석에 가려진 스몰위드 부인을 본다.\n\n“이보게, 친애하는 벗,” 스몰위드 영감이 그 비쩍 마른 다정한 두 팔을 앞으로 뻗으며 말한다. “잘 지냈나? 잘 지냈어? 이 친애하는 벗 양반, 이쪽 우리 벗은 누구신가?”\n\n“그러니까 이쪽은,” 조지가 처음부터 살갑게 굴 수는 없는 터라 대답한다. “매슈 배그닛입니다. 아시다시피 우리 그 건에서 나를 도와준 사람이지요.”\n\n“오! 배그닛 씨? 그렇고말고!” 영감은 손차양을 하고 그를 쳐다본다.\n\n“안녕하신가요, 배그닛 씨? 멋진 분이로군요, 조지 씨! 군인다운 풍채야, 선생!”\n\n의자를 권하는 사람이 없으니 조지 씨는 배그닛에게 하나, 자기에게 하나 의자를 끌어다 놓는다. 두 사람은 앉는데, 배그닛 씨는 그러려고 엉덩이 외에는 몸을 구부릴 줄 모르는 사람 같다.\n\n“주디,” 스몰위드 씨가 말한다. “담뱃대 가져와라.”\n\n“아니, 그럴 것 없습니다,” 조지 씨가 끼어든다. “그 아가씨가 그런 수고까지 할 것 없어요. 사실 오늘은 담배를 피우고 싶은 생각이 없거든요.”\n\n“그래?” 영감이 대꾸한다. “주디, 담뱃대 가져와.”\n\n“실은요, 스몰위드 씨,” 조지가 말을 잇는다. “제가 좀 불쾌한 심정입니다. 제가 보기엔 말이죠, 선생, 시내에 있는 당신 친구가 농간을 부린 것 같아서요.”\n\n“오, 천만에!” 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. “그 친구는 절대 그런 짓 안 해!”\n\n“안 한다고요? 그렇다니 다행이군요. 그 친구 짓일지도 모른다고 생각했거든요. 이거 말입니다. 이 편지요.”\n\n스몰위드 영감은 편지를 알아보고는 아주 흉하게 웃는다.\n\n“이게 무슨 뜻입니까?” 조지 씨가 묻는다.\n\n“주디,” 영감이 말한다. “담뱃대 챙겼느냐? 이리 다오. 이게 무슨 뜻이냐고 했나, 친애하는 벗?”\n\n“그래요! 자, 이거 보세요, 스몰위드 씨,” 부대원이 최대한 부드럽고 다정하게 말하려 애쓰며, 한 손에 펼친 편지를 쥐고 다른 손의 떡 벌어진 주먹을 허벅지에 얹은 채 다그친다. “우리 사이에 적잖은 돈이 오갔고, 지금 이렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 있고, 늘 양해가 있었다는 건 둘 다 잘 알잖아요. 나야 늘 꼬박꼬박 해온 그 통상적인 처리를 할 준비가 돼 있고, 이 건을 계속 이어갈 생각입니다. 당신한테 이런 편지를 받은 건 처음이라, 오늘 아침 좀 어수선했어요. 여기 내 친구 매슈 배그닛은, 아시다시피, 그 돈을 한 푼도—”\n\n“난 그건 모르는데, 알다시피.” 영감이 조용히 말한다.\n\n“아니, 제기—이거 말이죠—내가 그렇다고 말하잖아요, 안 그래요?”\n\n“오, 그래, 자네가 그렇다고 말하지,” 스몰위드 영감이 대꾸한다. “그래도 난 모른다네.”\n\n“좋아요!” 부대원이 치미는 불을 삼키며 말한다. “난 압니다.”\n\n스몰위드 씨가 더없이 좋은 기분으로 대답한다. “아! 그건 또 전혀 다른 얘기지!” 그리고 덧붙인다. “하지만 그건 상관없어. 그렇든 아니든 배그닛 씨 처지는 매한가지니까.”\n\n불운한 조지는 이 일을 원만히 매듭짓고, 스몰위드 씨를 그쪽 조건에 맞춰주어 비위를 맞춰보려 무던히 애쓴다.\n\n“바로 그게 제 말입니다. 당신 말대로요, 스몰위드 씨, 여기 매슈 배그닛은 그렇든 아니든 옭매일 처지지요. 그런데 그게 그이의 착한 아내를 마음 무척 불안하게 하고, 나도 그렇거든요. 나야 발길질이나 받아 마땅한 칠칠치 못한 건달이지만, 이 사람은 착실한 가장이잖아요, 안 그래요? 자, 스몰위드 씨,” 부대원이 자기식 군인다운 일 처리 방식으로 말을 이으며 점점 자신감을 얻는다. “당신하고 나는 어떤 식으로든 꽤 좋은 사이지만, 내 친구 배그닛을 완전히 빼달라고 부탁할 수는 없다는 건 나도 잘 압니다.”\n\n“아이고, 너무 겸손하시네. 자넨 뭐든 부탁해도 돼, 조지 씨.” (오늘 스몰위드 영감에게는 식인귀 같은 익살이 어려 있다.)\n\n“그리고 당신은 거절할 수 있다, 그 말이죠, 응? 아니면 당신보다는, 어쩌면 시내에 있는 당신 친구가? 하하하!”\n\n“하하하!” 스몰위드 영감이 따라 웃는다. 그게 어찌나 모진지, 또 눈이 어찌나 유난히 시퍼런지, 배그닛 씨는 그 존경스러운 노인을 바라보는 통에 타고난 엄숙함이 한층 깊어진다.\n\n“자!” 낙천적인 조지가 말한다. “우리가 유쾌하게 굴 수 있어서 다행입니다. 이걸 유쾌하게 매듭짓고 싶었거든요. 여기 내 친구 배그닛이 있고, 여기 내가 있어요. 괜찮으시면 통상적인 방식으로 그 자리에서 이 건을 해결합시다, 스몰위드 씨. 그리고 우리 양해가 뭔지 이 사람한테 한마디만 해주시면, 내 친구 배그닛의 마음도, 그 식구들 마음도 한결 놓이겠네요.”\n\n이때 어떤 새된 유령이 조롱하는 투로 외친다. “아이고, 맙소사! 아!” 정작 그게 장난기 어린 주디가 아니라면 말이지만, 놀란 손님들이 둘러보니 그녀는 잠자코 있으나, 그 턱이 방금 한 번 치켜올려져 비웃음과 경멸을 드러내고 있다. 배그닛 씨의 엄숙함은 한층 더 깊어진다.\n\n“그런데 자네가 나한테 묻지 않았나, 조지 씨”—이때껏 줄곧 담뱃대를 손에 쥐고 있던 스몰위드 영감이 이번엔 화자다—“이 편지가 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었던 것 같은데?”\n\n“그야, 그랬죠,” 부대원이 무심한 투로 대답한다. “하지만 다 제대로 되고 유쾌하기만 하면, 굳이 자세히 알고 싶진 않아요.”\n\n스몰위드 씨는 부대원의 머리를 겨누다가 일부러 빗맞히는 시늉으로 담뱃대를 바닥에 내던져 산산조각 낸다.\n\n“이게 그 뜻이야, 친애하는 벗. 내가 자넬 박살 낼 거야. 가루로 만들어 줄 테야. 잘게 빻아 줄 테야. 지옥에나 가!”\n\n두 벗은 일어나 서로를 쳐다본다. 배그닛 씨의 엄숙함은 이제 더없이 깊은 경지에 이르렀다.\n\n“지옥에나 가!” 영감이 거듭 말한다. “자네 담뱃대질이며 으스대는 꼴은 이제 더 못 봐주겠어. 뭐? 자넨 독립적인 용기병이기도 하지! 내 변호사한테 가(어딘지 기억하지, 전에도 가봤잖아). 거기서 그 독립을 한번 보여봐, 어때? 자, 친애하는 벗, 자네한테도 기회가 있다네. 길가 문을 열어라, 주디. 이 허풍쟁이들을 내쫓아! 안 나가면 사람을 불러. 내쫓아 버려!”\n\n영감이 어찌나 큰 소리로 악을 쓰는지, 배그닛 씨는 전우가 어안이 벙벙한 데서 채 헤어나기도 전에 그 어깨에 손을 얹어 길가 문 밖으로 끌어내고, 의기양양한 주디가 곧장 그 문을 쾅 닫는다. 완전히 얼이 빠진 조지 씨는 한동안 문고리를 멍하니 바라보고 서 있다. 더없는 엄숙의 심연에 빠진 배그닛 씨는 보초처럼 작은 거실 창문 앞을 오락가락하며 지날 때마다 안을 들여다보는데, 무언가를 머릿속에서 곱씹는 기색이 역력하다.\n\n“가세, 맷,” 조지 씨가 정신을 차리고 말한다. “변호사한테 가봐야겠어. 그래, 저 악당을 어떻게 생각하나?”\n\n배그닛 씨는 거실을 마지막으로 한 번 들여다보려고 멈춰 서서, 안쪽을 향해 고개를 한 번 젓고 대답한다. “우리 마누라가 여기 있었더라면—내가 저자한테 한마디 했을 텐데!” 이렇게 골똘히 곱씹던 주제를 털어놓고 나서, 그는 보조를 맞춰 부대원과 어깨를 나란히 하고 행군해 나간다.\n\n두 사람이 링컨스 인 필즈에 모습을 드러내자, 토킹혼 씨는 일이 있어 만날 수 없다고 한다. 그는 두 사람을 만날 마음이 전혀 없으니, 둘이 꼬박 한 시간을 기다린 끝에 서기가 종이 울리자 그 김에 그들 얘기를 꺼냈을 때도, 토킹혼 씨가 그들에게 할 말이 없으니 더 기다리지 않는 편이 좋겠다는 별로 달갑잖은 전갈밖에 가져오지 않는다. 그래도 두 사람은 군대식 전술의 끈기로 기다리고, 마침내 다시 종이 울리며 안에 있던 의뢰인이 토킹혼 씨의 방에서 나온다.\n\n그 의뢰인은 풍채 고운 노부인으로, 다름 아닌 체스니 월드의 가정부 라운스웰 부인이다. 그녀는 그 성소에서 우아한 옛날식 무릎 인사를 하며 나와 가만히 문을 닫는다. 그녀는 이곳에서 어느 정도 대접을 받으니, 서기가 자기 자리에서 나와 바깥 사무실을 지나 그녀를 배웅하고 문밖으로 내보내 준다. 노부인이 그의 친절에 감사를 표하다가 기다리고 있는 두 전우를 알아본다.\n\n“실례합니다만, 선생님, 저 신사분들은 군인이신 듯한데요?”\n\n서기가 눈짓으로 그 물음을 두 사람에게 넘기는데, 조지 씨는 벽난로 위 달력에서 고개를 돌리지 않는다. 그래서 배그닛 씨가 나서서 대답한다. “네, 부인. 예전에요.”\n\n“그럴 줄 알았어요. 틀림없을 거라 여겼지요. 두 분을 뵈니 마음이 따뜻해지네요, 신사분들. 그런 분들을 뵐 때면 늘 그렇답니다. 신의 가호가 있기를, 신사분들! 늙은이를 너그러이 봐주세요. 저도 한때 군인이 된 아들이 하나 있었거든요. 잘생기고 훌륭한 청년이었어요. 제 방식대로 대담하니 착했지요. 더러 그 가엾은 어미 앞에서 아이를 헐뜯는 사람도 있었지만요. 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다, 선생님. 신의 가호가 있기를, 신사분들!”\n\n“부인께도요!” 배그닛 씨가 진심 어린 호의로 대답한다.\n\n노부인의 목소리에 어린 간절함과, 그 예스러운 자그마한 몸을 타고 흐르는 떨림에는 사람을 몹시 뭉클하게 하는 무언가가 있다. 그러나 조지 씨는 벽난로 위 달력에 어찌나 골몰해 있는지(아마 그것으로 앞으로 다가올 달들을 헤아리고 있는지도) 그녀가 가버리고 등 뒤로 문이 닫힐 때까지 고개를 돌리지 않는다.\n\n“조지,” 배그닛 씨가 마침내 달력에서 돌아서며 무뚝뚝하게 속삭인다. “기죽지 마! ‘아, 병사들이여, 어찌하여—우리가 우울해야 하나, 친구들?’ 기운 내, 이 사람아!”\n\n이제 서기가 다시 들어가 두 사람이 아직 있다고 전하고, 토킹혼 씨가 다소 짜증스럽게 “그럼 들여보내!” 하고 대꾸하는 소리가 들리자, 두 사람은 천장에 그림이 그려진 그 커다란 방으로 들어가, 벽난로 앞에 서 있는 그를 본다.\n\n“자, 자네들, 무슨 일인가? 병장, 지난번 만났을 때 말했지. 여기서 자네 일행과 어울릴 생각이 없다고.”\n\n병장은—지난 몇 분 사이에 평소의 말투며 평소의 몸가짐마저 풀이 죽은 채로—이 편지를 받고 스몰위드 씨에게 갔더니 이리로 가보라 했다고 대답한다.\n\n“난 자네한테 할 말이 없네,” 토킹혼 씨가 응수한다. “빚을 졌으면 빚을 갚든지 그 결과를 감수하든지 해야지. 그걸 배우려고 여기까지 올 일은 없을 텐데, 안 그런가?”\n\n병장은 유감스럽게도 돈을 마련하지 못했다고 말한다.\n\n“좋아! 그럼 다른 사람이—이 사람이 그 사람이라면—대신 갚아야지.”\n\n병장은 유감스럽게도 다른 사람 역시 돈을 마련하지 못했다고 덧붙인다.\n\n“좋아! 그럼 둘이 갚든지, 아니면 둘 다 고소당해 둘 다 곤욕을 치르든지 해야지. 돈을 받았으면 도로 내놔야지. 남의 파운드, 실링, 펜스를 챙겨놓고 무사히 빠져나갈 순 없네.”\n\n변호사는 안락의자에 앉아 불을 휘젓는다. 조지 씨는 부디 그가 선처를 베풀어—“말했잖나, 병장, 자네한테 할 말이 없네. 난 자네 일행이 마음에 안 들고, 자네가 여기 오는 것도 싫어. 이 일은 내 업무 영역에 전혀 들지 않고, 내 사무소 소관도 아니야. 스몰위드 씨가 친절히도 이런 일을 나한테 가져오긴 하지만, 내 일이 아닐세. 클리퍼즈 인의 멜키세덱한테 가보게.”\n\n“선생님께 사과드려야겠습니다,” 조지 씨가 말한다. “이렇게 달가워하지도 않으시는데 들이닥쳐서요—저로서도 선생님 못지않게 거북한 일이지만—단둘이 한마디 드려도 되겠습니까?”\n\n토킹혼 씨는 주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 일어나 창가 우묵한 곳 한 군데로 걸어간다. “자! 낭비할 시간 없네.” 완벽하게 꾸며낸 무관심 속에서도 그는 부대원에게 날카로운 눈길을 던지는데, 자기는 빛을 등지고 서고 상대는 빛을 마주 보게 하려고 빈틈없이 신경 쓴다.\n\n“그러니까, 선생님,” 조지 씨가 말한다. “저와 함께 온 이 사람이 이 딱한 일에 엮인 또 한 당사자입니다—명목상으로요, 그저 명목상으로만요—제 유일한 목적은 저 때문에 이 사람이 곤경에 빠지지 않게 하는 겁니다. 이 사람은 아내와 식구가 있는 더없이 점잖은 사람이고, 예전에 왕립포병대에 있었—”\n\n“이보게, 난 왕립포병대 전체에 코담배 한 자밤만큼의 관심도 없어—장교든, 병사든, 탄약 수레든, 짐마차든, 말이든, 대포든, 탄약이든.”\n\n“그러시겠지요, 선생님. 하지만 저는 저 때문에 배그닛이며 그 아내와 식구가 해를 입는 데는 관심이 아주 큽니다. 그래서 이 일에서 그들을 무사히 빼낼 수만 있다면, 다른 도리 없이, 며칠 전 선생님이 저한테 원하셨던 그것을 아무 조건 없이 내놓겠습니다.”\n\n“그걸 여기 가져왔나?”\n\n“여기 가져왔습니다, 선생님.”\n\n“병장,” 변호사가 그 메마르고 무덤덤한 투로 말을 잇는데, 어떤 격렬함보다도 상대하기에 훨씬 더 가망 없는 어조다. “내가 말하는 동안 마음을 정하게. 이건 최종이니까. 내가 말을 마치고 나면 이 건은 닫힌 거고, 다시 열지 않을 거야. 그것만 명심해. 가져왔다는 그것을 원하면 며칠 여기 맡겨둬도 좋고, 원하면 당장 도로 가져가도 좋아. 여기 맡겨두기로 한다면, 내가 자네를 위해 이렇게 해줄 수 있네—이 건을 예전 상태로 되돌려놓고, 거기에 더해, 자네가 최대한 추궁당하기 전까지는, 즉 채권자가 이 사람한테 손대기 전에 자네 재산이 먼저 바닥나기 전까지는 이 배그닛이라는 사람이 어떤 식으로도 시달리지 않으리라는 각서를 써줄 수 있어. 이건 사실상 이 사람을 풀어주는 거나 다름없네. 결정했나?”\n\n부대원이 가슴팍에 손을 넣고 긴 숨과 함께 대답한다. “그렇게 해야겠지요, 선생님.”\n\n그리하여 토킹혼 씨는 안경을 쓰고 앉아 각서를 작성하고는, 천천히 읽으며 배그닛에게 설명해 주는데, 배그닛은 이때껏 줄곧 천장만 노려보고 있다가 이 새로운 말의 물벼락 아래 또다시 벗어진 머리에 손을 얹고는, 자기 심정을 대신 표현해 줄 늙은 아낙이 몹시도 절실해 보인다. 이윽고 부대원이 가슴 주머니에서 접은 종이 한 장을 꺼내 내키지 않는 손길로 변호사 팔꿈치 곁에 내려놓는다. “그저 지시가 적힌 편지일 뿐입니다, 선생님. 그 사람한테 받은 마지막 편지죠.”\n\n조지 씨여, 맷돌이 표정을 바꾸나 들여다보시오. 토킹혼 씨가 그 편지를 펴서 읽을 때 그 얼굴에서 변화를 찾느니 차라리 맷돌에서 찾는 편이 빠를 테니! 그는 편지를 도로 접어 책상에 넣어두는데, 그 낯빛이 죽음처럼 흔들림이 없다.\n\n그가 더 할 말도, 할 일도 없으니, 다만 그 똑같이 싸늘하고 무례한 투로 한 번 고개를 까딱하고는 짧게 말할 뿐이다. “가도 좋네. 거기, 이 사람들 내보내!” 밖으로 안내되어 나온 두 사람은 저녁을 먹으러 배그닛 씨 댁으로 향한다.\n\n전번의 삶은 돼지고기와 푸성귀에서 이날은 삶은 쇠고기와 푸성귀로 변화를 주었고, 배그닛 부인은 똑같은 방식으로 음식을 떠서 나눠주며 더없이 좋은 기분으로 양념을 친다. 그녀는 더 나을 수도 있다는 내색 없이 ‘좋음’을 두 팔로 끌어안고, 곁에 어떤 자그마한 어둠의 얼룩이 있어도 거기서 빛을 길어 올리는 보기 드문 부류의 늙은 아낙인 것이다. 이번의 그 얼룩은 조지 씨의 어두워진 이마인데, 그는 유난히 생각에 잠기고 풀이 죽어 있다. 처음에 배그닛 부인은 퀘벡과 몰타의 합동 애교에 기대 그를 풀어보려 하지만, 그 어린 아가씨들이 지금 자기들 ‘블러피’가 평소 함께 까불며 놀던 그 블러피가 아님을 눈치챈 것을 보고는, 그 경보병들에게 눈짓으로 물러나라 이르고, 가정의 화로라는 트인 들판에서 그가 마음껏 펼쳐지도록 내버려 둔다.\n\n그러나 그는 그러지 않는다. 그는 밀집 대형 그대로, 흐리고 침울하게 머물러 있다. 길게 이어지는 설거지와 나막신 신는 절차가 진행되는 동안, 그와 배그닛 씨에게 담뱃대가 주어졌을 때도, 그는 저녁 식사 때나 다를 바 없다. 그는 담배 피우는 것을 잊고, 불을 바라보며 골똘히 생각에 잠기고, 담뱃대를 꺼뜨리고, 담배를 도무지 즐기지 못하는 모습을 보여 배그닛 씨의 가슴을 불안과 당혹으로 가득 채운다.\n\n그리하여 배그닛 부인이 마침내 기운을 돋우는 물통에서 발갛게 달아올라 나타나 자기 일감을 들고 자리에 앉자, 배그닛 씨가 으르렁거린다. “여보!” 그러고는 무슨 일인지 알아보라고 눈짓으로 신호를 보낸다.\n\n“아니, 조지!” 배그닛 부인이 바늘에 조용히 실을 꿰며 말한다. “자네 어쩜 그리 기가 죽었어!”\n\n“그런가요? 같이 있기 별로지요? 그래요, 그런 것 같네요.”\n\n“이분 블러피 같지가 않아, 엄마!” 어린 몰타가 외친다.\n\n“몸이 안 좋으셔서 그런 거야, 엄마.” 퀘벡이 덧붙인다.\n\n“블러피 같지 않다는 건 분명 안 좋은 징조지!” 부대원이 어린 아가씨들에게 입을 맞추며 대꾸한다. “하지만 그게 맞아,” 한숨과 함께. “맞아, 안타깝게도. 이 꼬마들은 늘 옳거든!”\n\n“조지,” 배그닛 부인이 바삐 일하며 말한다. “자네가, 오늘 아침 새된 늙은 군인의 아내가—나중에 제 혀를 깨물어 버리고 싶었고 진작에 그랬어야 마땅한—했던 그런 말을 마음에 담아둘 만큼 꼬여 있다고 내가 생각한다면, 지금 자네한테 무슨 말을 못 할지 모르겠어.”\n\n“내 다정한 친구 같은 분,” 부대원이 대답한다. “그런 건 털끝만큼도 없어요.”\n\n“정말이지 진심으로 말하는데, 조지, 내가 한 말이며 하려던 말은 리그넘을 자네한테 맡기고 자네가 그이를 무사히 이 일에서 빼내주리라 믿는다는 거였어. 그리고 자네는 그이를 무사히 빼냈잖아, 훌륭하게!”\n\n“고마워요, 부인!” 조지가 말한다. “좋게 봐주셔서 기쁩니다.”\n\n배그닛 부인의 손을, 일감을 쥔 그 손을 정답게 한 번 흔드는데—그녀가 그의 곁에 앉았으므로—그 순간 부대원의 시선이 그녀의 얼굴로 쏠린다. 그녀가 바늘을 놀리는 동안 잠시 그 얼굴을 바라보던 그는, 구석 걸상에 앉아 있는 어린 울리치 쪽으로 눈길을 돌려 그 피리 부는 아이를 손짓해 부른다.\n\n“저기 좀 봐라, 얘야,” 조지가 손으로 어머니의 머리카락을 아주 부드럽게 쓰다듬으며 말한다. “저기 착하고 사랑이 넘치는 이마가 있구나! 온통 너에 대한 사랑으로 환하지, 얘야. 네 아버지를 따라다니고 너를 돌보느라 햇볕과 비바람에 조금 그을리긴 했지만, 나무에 달린 잘 익은 사과처럼 싱싱하고 건강하단다.”\n\n배그닛 씨의 얼굴은 그 나무토막 같은 재질이 허락하는 한도 내에서 더없는 찬동과 동의를 드러낸다.\n\n“언젠가 그런 날이 올 거야, 얘야,” 부대원이 말을 잇는다. “네 어머니의 이 머리카락이 백발이 되고, 이 이마가 온통 주름으로 가로세로 얽히고, 그러면 멋진 노부인이 되시겠지. 젊을 때 마음을 단단히 먹어두렴. 그날이 오면 ‘난 어머니의 그 소중한 머리카락 한 올도 세게 한 적 없고, 어머니 얼굴에 서글픈 주름 한 줄 새긴 적 없다’고 생각할 수 있도록. 어른이 되어 떠올릴 수 있는 그 숱한 것들 가운데, 그걸 곁에 지니는 편이 가장 나으니까, 울리치!”\n\n조지 씨는 의자에서 일어나 그 아이를 어머니 곁에 앉히고, 어딘가 황급한 기색으로 거리에 나가 담배나 한 대 피우고 오겠다고 말하며 마무리한다.\n\n저는 몇 주 동안이나 앓아누웠고, 그동안 제 삶의 평범한 흐름은 아득한 옛 기억처럼 멀어졌습니다. 그러나 이것은 세월이 흘러서라기보다, 병실의 무력함과 무위(無爲)가 제 모든 습관을 송두리째 바꿔놓은 탓이었습니다. 병실에 갇힌 지 며칠 지나기도 전에, 다른 모든 것이 아득히 먼 곳으로 물러나버린 듯했고, 실제로는 여러 해로 나뉘어 있던 제 인생의 여러 단계들이 그 먼 곳에서는 거의, 아니 전혀 구분되지 않았습니다. 병에 걸리면서 저는 어두운 호수를 건너온 듯했고, 제 모든 경험은 그 까마득한 거리 때문에 한데 뒤섞인 채 건강한 쪽 기슭에 남겨두고 온 듯했습니다.\n\n처음에는 제 살림 일이 그대로 방치되어 있다는 생각에 몹시 마음을 졸였지만, 그 일조차 곧 그린리프 시절의 가장 오래된 옛 임무들만큼이나, 아니면 화첩을 옆구리에 끼고 어린 그림자를 곁에 거느린 채 학교에서 대모님 댁으로 돌아가던 여름날 오후만큼이나 아득히 멀어졌습니다. 저는 그전까지 삶이 정말로 얼마나 짧은지, 그리고 그 삶을 마음이 얼마나 작은 공간 안에 담아둘 수 있는지 미처 몰랐습니다.\n\n몹시 앓는 동안, 이렇게 나뉜 시간의 토막들이 서로 뒤엉키는 그 방식이 제 마음을 한없이 괴롭혔습니다. 어린아이이면서 동시에 좀 더 자란 소녀였고, 또 그토록 행복했던 작은 여인이기도 했던 저는, 각각의 처지에 어울리는 근심과 어려움에 짓눌렸을 뿐 아니라, 그것들을 끝없이 화해시키려 애쓰는 거대한 혼란에까지 시달렸습니다. 그런 상태를 겪어보지 않은 분이라면 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지, 혹은 그것이 어떤 고통스러운 불안을 불러일으켰는지 온전히 이해하기는 어려우리라 생각합니다.\n\n같은 이유로 저는 제 병중의 그 시간을 입에 올리기조차 두렵습니다. 그것은 하나의 긴 밤처럼 느껴졌지만, 실은 그 안에 밤도 낮도 다 들어 있었으리라 믿습니다. 그때 저는 거대한 계단을 힘겹게 기어올랐고, 늘 꼭대기에 닿으려 안간힘을 썼으나, 정원 길에서 본 벌레가 무언가에 가로막히듯 번번이 되돌려져 다시 기어오르기를 거듭했습니다. 이따금 저는 제가 침대에 누워 있다는 사실을 또렷이 알았고, 대체로는 어렴풋이나마 알았다고 생각합니다. 찰리와 이야기를 나누고, 그 아이의 손길을 느꼈으며, 그 아이를 아주 잘 알아보았습니다. 그런데도 어느새 저는 이렇게 하소연하고 있었습니다. \"아, 또 이 끝없는 계단이야, 찰리. 자꾸자꾸 — 하늘까지 쌓아 올린 것 같아!\" 그러고는 다시 힘겹게 기어올랐습니다.\n\n더 끔찍했던 그 시간을 감히 입에 올려도 될까요. 거대하고 새까만 공간 어딘가에 활활 타오르는 목걸이인지, 고리인지, 별처럼 둥근 어떤 원이 꿰여 있었고, 저는 그 구슬 가운데 하나였습니다! 그리고 제 유일한 기도는 그 나머지로부터 떼어내어 달라는 것이었고, 그 무시무시한 것의 일부가 되어 있다는 사실이 이루 말할 수 없는 고통이요 비참이었던 그 시간 말입니다.\n\n이런 병중의 경험은 적게 말할수록 덜 지루하고 더 잘 전해지리라 생각합니다. 제가 이것을 다시 끄집어내는 까닭은 남을 불행하게 만들려는 것도, 지금 그것을 떠올리는 일이 조금이라도 불행해서도 아닙니다. 어쩌면 우리가 이런 기이한 고통을 더 잘 안다면, 그 격렬함을 누그러뜨릴 길도 더 잘 찾을 수 있을지 모르기 때문입니다.\n\n그 뒤에 찾아온 안식, 길고도 달콤한 잠, 더없이 행복한 휴식 — 쇠약해진 제가 너무도 고요하여 제 자신은 조금도 염려하지 않았고, 제가 죽어간다는 말을 들었다 해도(지금 돌이켜보면 그러했으리라 싶습니다) 뒤에 남겨두고 갈 이들을 향한 애틋한 사랑 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 없었으리라 싶은 그 상태 — 이것은 어쩌면 더 널리 이해받을 수 있을 것입니다. 빛이 다시 한번 제 위에서 반짝일 때 제가 처음으로 그 빛을 피해 움츠러들었던 것도 바로 이 상태에서였고, 어떤 말로도 다 표현할 수 없는 끝없는 기쁨 속에서 제가 다시 볼 수 있게 되리라는 것을 알았던 것도 바로 그때였습니다.\n\n저는 에이더가 밤낮으로 문 앞에서 우는 소리를 들었습니다. 제가 모질다고, 저를 사랑하지 않는다고 외치는 소리도 들었습니다. 안으로 들어와 저를 간호하고 위로하게 해달라고, 제 머리맡을 다시는 떠나지 않게 해달라고 기도하고 애원하는 소리도 들었습니다. 그러나 저는 말을 할 수 있을 때면 그저 이렇게만 말했습니다. \"안 돼, 내 사랑하는 아가, 절대 안 돼!\" 그리고 제가 살든 죽든 그 아이를 이 방에 들이지 말라고 찰리에게 거듭거듭 당부했습니다. 찰리는 그 절박한 시간 내내 제게 진실했고, 그 작은 손과 큰 마음으로 문을 굳게 지켰습니다.\n\n그러나 이제 시력이 차츰 돌아오고 그 영광스러운 빛이 날마다 더 충만하고 환하게 제게 비쳐들면서, 저는 사랑하는 그 아이가 아침저녁으로 써 보내는 편지를 읽을 수 있었고, 그것을 입술에 갖다 대고 뺨을 묻으면서도 그 아이에게 해를 끼칠까 두려워하지 않게 되었습니다. 저의 작은 하녀가 어찌나 다정하고 어찌나 세심하게 두 방을 오가며 모든 것을 정돈하고, 다시 열린 창문 너머로 에이더에게 명랑하게 말을 거는지를 볼 수 있었습니다. 집 안의 그 고요함과, 늘 제게 그토록 잘해주던 모든 이들이 그 고요함으로 드러내는 그 깊은 배려를 이해할 수 있었습니다. 더없이 절절한 마음의 행복 속에서 눈물 흘릴 수 있었고, 건강할 때 그러했던 것 못지않게 쇠약한 가운데서도 행복할 수 있었습니다.\n\n머지않아 제 기력이 회복되기 시작했습니다. 저를 위해 행해지는 일들을 마치 제가 조용히 가엾이 여기는 다른 누군가를 위한 일인 양 그렇게 이상한 평온함 속에서 누워 바라보기만 하던 대신, 저는 조금씩 거들기 시작했고, 그러다 좀 더, 또 훨씬 더 거들게 되어, 마침내 제 자신에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되었고, 삶에 다시 관심을 갖고 애착을 느끼게 되었습니다.\n\n처음으로 베개를 받치고 일어나 앉아 찰리와 함께 거나하게 차를 마시던 그 즐거운 오후를 저는 얼마나 또렷이 기억하는지요! 그 작은 아이는 — 분명 약하고 병든 이들을 보살피라고 이 세상에 보내진 것이겠지요 — 어찌나 행복하고 어찌나 분주한지, 차 준비를 하다 말고 자꾸자꾸 멈춰서 제 가슴에 머리를 기대고 저를 어루만지며 기쁨의 눈물을 흘렸습니다. 너무 기뻐서, 너무 기뻐서요. 그래서 저는 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없었습니다. \"찰리, 네가 이렇게 하면 나는 다시 누워야 해, 아가. 내가 생각했던 것보다 더 약하거든!\" 그러자 찰리는 생쥐처럼 조용해져서, 그 환한 얼굴을 두 방을 가로질러 이리저리, 그늘에서 눈부신 햇살 속으로, 다시 햇살에서 그늘 속으로 옮겨 다녔고, 저는 평온히 그 모습을 지켜보았습니다. 마침내 모든 준비가 끝나, 저를 꾀어낼 자그마한 별미들과 하얀 보, 꽃들, 그리고 아래층의 에이더가 저를 위해 그토록 사랑스럽고 아름답게 차려놓은 그 모든 것이 놓인 예쁜 찻상이 침대 곁에 준비되자, 저는 마음속에 오래 품어온, 새삼스럽지 않은 이야기를 찰리에게 꺼낼 만큼 충분히 안정되어 있다는 확신이 들었습니다.\n\n먼저 저는 방에 대해 찰리를 칭찬했는데, 정말이지 방은 어찌나 산뜻하고 바람이 잘 통하며, 어찌나 티 하나 없이 깔끔한지, 제가 그토록 오래 거기 누워 있었다는 것이 믿기지 않을 정도였습니다. 이 말에 찰리는 무척 기뻐했고, 그 얼굴은 전보다 더 환해졌습니다.\n\n\"그런데 찰리,\" 저는 방을 둘러보며 말했습니다. \"내가 익숙하던 뭔가가 분명 없는 것 같은데?\"\n\n가엾은 작은 찰리도 방을 둘러보더니, 없어진 것이라곤 아무것도 없다는 듯 고개를 가로젓는 시늉을 했습니다.\n\n\"그림은 다 예전 그대로니?\" 제가 그 아이에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"하나도 빠짐없이 그대로예요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 말했습니다.\n\n\"가구는, 찰리?\"\n\n\"공간을 더 내려고 제가 옮겨놓은 것만 빼고요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"그런데도,\" 제가 말했습니다. \"낯익은 물건 하나가 없는 것 같아. 아, 뭔지 알겠다, 찰리! 거울이야.\"\n\n찰리는 무언가를 깜빡 잊은 척하며 식탁에서 일어나 옆방으로 들어갔고, 저는 그 아이가 거기서 흐느끼는 소리를 들었습니다.\n\n저는 이것을 아주 여러 번 생각해왔습니다. 이제 확신이 섰습니다. 그것이 지금 제게 충격이 아니라는 사실을 하느님께 감사할 수 있었습니다. 저는 찰리를 다시 불렀고, 그 아이가 왔을 때 — 처음에는 웃는 척했지만 제게 다가올수록 슬픈 표정이 되더군요 — 그 아이를 두 팔로 안고 말했습니다. \"별일 아니야, 찰리. 옛날 내 얼굴 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있을 것 같아.\"\n\n저는 곧 큰 의자에 앉아 있을 수 있을 만큼, 심지어 찰리에게 기대어 어질어질하나마 옆방으로 걸어 들어갈 수 있을 만큼 회복되었습니다. 그 방에서도 거울이 늘 있던 자리에서 사라지고 없었지만, 그렇다고 제가 견뎌야 할 것이 더 견디기 어려워지지는 않았습니다.\n\n후견인은 줄곧 저를 찾아오고 싶어 안달이었고, 이제 제가 그 기쁨을 마다할 까닭은 조금도 없었습니다. 그분은 어느 날 아침에 오셨는데, 처음 들어섰을 때는 그저 저를 품에 안고 \"내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아이야!\"라고만 하실 수 있을 뿐이었습니다. 그분의 마음이 얼마나 깊은 애정과 너그러움의 샘인지 저는 오래전부터 알고 있었습니다. 누가 저보다 더 잘 알겠습니까? 그런 마음속에 그런 자리를 채우는 일이라면, 제 하찮은 고통과 변화쯤이야 값어치가 있지 않겠습니까? \"아, 그래!\" 저는 생각했습니다. \"그분은 나를 보셨고, 전보다 더 나를 사랑하셔. 나를 보시고도 예전보다 더 나를 아끼셔. 그러니 내가 슬퍼할 게 무엇이 있담!\"\n\n그분은 소파에서 제 곁에 앉아 팔로 저를 받쳐주셨습니다. 잠시 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 앉아 계셨지만, 손을 떼었을 때는 평소의 모습으로 돌아오셨습니다. 그보다 더 유쾌한 태도는 일찍이 없었고, 앞으로도 결코 없을 것입니다.\n\n\"내 작은 여인,\" 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. \"참으로 슬픈 시간이었구나. 그 와중에도 어찌나 고집 센 작은 여인이던지!\"\n\n\"다 잘되자고 그런 거예요, 후견인 어른.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"잘되자고?\" 그분이 다정하게 되뇌셨습니다. \"그럼, 다 잘되자고 그랬지. 하지만 그동안 에이더와 나는 완전히 외롭고 비참했단다. 네 친구 캐디는 아침저녁으로 드나들었고, 이 집 사람들은 하나같이 넋이 나가 풀이 죽어 있었지. 심지어 가엾은 릭마저도 너를 걱정해서 편지를 — 그것도 내게! — 보냈단다!\"\n\n저는 에이더의 편지에서 캐디 이야기는 읽었으나 리처드 이야기는 읽지 못했습니다. 저는 그 사실을 그분께 말씀드렸습니다.\n\n\"음, 그래, 얘야.\" 그분이 대답하셨습니다. \"그 애한테는 말하지 않는 게 낫겠다 싶었단다.\"\n\n\"게다가 그 애가 어른께 편지했다고 말씀하시는데,\" 저는 그분이 강조한 말투를 따라 하며 말했습니다. \"마치 그 애가 그러는 게 당연하지 않다는 듯이요, 후견인 어른. 마치 그 애가 더 좋은 친구에게 편지할 수 있기라도 한 것처럼요!\"\n\n\"그 애는 그럴 수 있다고 생각한단다, 얘야.\" 후견인이 대답하셨습니다. \"그것도 훨씬 더 좋은 여러 사람한테 말이지. 실은 그 애가 내게 편지한 건 일종의 마지못함에서였어. 너한테는 답장을 기대할 수 없어 쓰지 못하던 차에 말이다. 차갑게, 거만하게, 서먹하게, 원망 섞어 썼더구나. 그래, 가장 사랑하는 작은 여인아, 우리는 그것을 너그러이 보아야 한다. 그 애 잘못이 아니야. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 그 애를 제 본모습에서 비틀어놓았고, 그 애 눈에 나를 비뚤어진 사람으로 만들어놓은 게지. 그 소송이 그만큼, 아니 그보다 더 못된 짓을 저지르는 걸 나는 여러 번 보아왔단다. 천사 둘이 거기 얽혀든다 해도, 그 본성이 바뀌고 말 거라고 나는 믿는다.\"\n\n\"하지만 어른의 본성은 바꾸지 못했잖아요, 후견인 어른.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇고말고, 바꿔놓았지, 얘야.\" 그분이 웃으며 말씀하셨습니다. \"남풍을 동풍으로 둔갑시킨 게 몇 번인지 모른단다. 릭은 나를 불신하고 의심해. 변호사들을 찾아가서는 나를 불신하고 의심하라고 배워 오지. 내가 이해관계가 충돌한다느니, 제 권리와 부딪치는 청구권이 있다느니 하는 따위의 말을 듣는 게야. 하지만 하늘이 알다시피, 내가 만일 내 불운한 이름이 그토록 오래 얹혀온 그 법률 잡동사니의 산더미에서 빠져나올 수만 있다면(빠져나올 수 없지만), 혹은 내 본래의 권리를 소멸시켜 그 산을 평평하게 만들 수만 있다면(이것 역시 할 수 없고, 어떤 인간의 힘으로도 결코 못 하지. 우리가 이미 그 지경에까지 와버렸다고 나는 믿는다), 나는 당장 이 순간에라도 그렇게 하겠다. 나는 죽은 소송인들이 — 대법원이라는 수레바퀴에 마음도 영혼도 짓이겨진 채로 — 회계감사원장에게 청구하지 못하고 남겨둔 그 모든 돈을 받느니, 차라리 가엾은 릭에게 제 본래의 천성을 되돌려주는 편이 낫다. 얘야, 그 돈은 대법원의 어마어마한 사악함을 기리는 피라미드를 쌓아 올릴 만큼 충분한 액수란다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요, 후견인 어른,\" 저는 깜짝 놀라 물었습니다. \"리처드가 어른을 의심한다는 게 가능한 일인가요?\"\n\n\"아, 얘야, 얘야.\" 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. \"그런 폐단의 교묘한 독은 바로 그런 병을 길러내는 법이란다. 그 애 피는 감염되었고, 그 애 눈에는 사물이 본래 모습을 잃어버린 게지. 그 애 잘못이 아니야.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 끔찍한 불행이에요, 후견인 어른.\"\n\n\"잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 영향권 안에 끌려드는 것은 끔찍한 불행이지, 작은 여인아. 그보다 더한 불행을 나는 알지 못한다. 그 애는 야금야금 그 썩은 갈대에 의지하도록 꾀임을 당했고, 그 갈대는 제 썩은 기운을 그 애 주위 모든 것에 옮겨 넣는 게야. 하지만 다시 한번 진심으로 말하건대, 우리는 가엾은 릭에게 인내심을 갖고, 그 애를 탓하지 말아야 한다. 내가 살아오는 동안 그 애처럼 싱싱하고 멋진 마음들이 똑같은 수법에 무너지는 걸 얼마나 많이 보았던지!\"\n\n저는 그분의 자애롭고 사심 없는 뜻이 그토록 보람을 거두지 못한 데 대한 놀라움과 안타까움을 어느 정도 드러내지 않을 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"그런 말은 하면 못쓴다, 더든 부인.\" 그분이 명랑하게 대답하셨습니다. \"에이더는 더 행복할 게다. 그러길 바라고, 그것만으로도 큰 거란다. 나는 나와 이 두 젊은이가 서로 불신하는 적이 아니라 벗이 될 수 있고, 그리하여 어느 정도는 소송에 맞서 그것을 이겨낼 만큼 강해질 수 있으리라 정말로 생각했지. 하지만 그건 지나친 기대였어. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 릭이 누운 요람의 장막이었으니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 후견인 어른, 약간의 경험만으로도 그 소송이 얼마나 거짓되고 비참한 것인지를 그 애가 깨닫게 되리라 기대할 수는 없을까요?\"\n\n\"우리는 그러길 기대해야지, 내 에스더.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말씀하셨습니다. \"그리고 그 깨달음이 너무 늦게 오지 않기를 바라야지. 어쨌든 우리는 그 애에게 모질게 굴어선 안 된다. 지금 이렇게 말하는 순간에도, 다 자라 성숙한 사내들 중에, 그것도 선량한 사람들 중에, 이 같은 법정에 소송인으로 던져진다면 삼 년 안에 — 이 년 안에 — 아니 일 년 안에 본질이 바뀌고 망가지지 않을 사람이 그리 많지 않단다. 그러니 가엾은 릭을 두고 어찌 놀라겠느냐? 그토록 불운한 젊은이는,\" 여기서 그분은 마치 혼잣말을 하듯 목소리를 낮추셨습니다. \"처음에는 대법원이 지금 모습 그대로의 그것이라는 사실을 믿지 못해(누군들 믿겠느냐?). 얼굴이 붉어진 채 들떠서 그 법정을 바라보며, 제 이권에 무언가 결말을 내고 어떤 해결을 가져다주기를 기대하지. 그런데 법정은 미루고, 실망시키고, 시험하고, 고문하지. 그 애의 들뜬 희망과 인내심을 한 올 한 올 닳아 없애. 그래도 그 애는 여전히 그 법정을 바라보고, 그것을 갈망하며, 제 온 세상이 배신으로 가득하고 텅 비어 있음을 깨닫는단다. 자, 자, 자! 이 이야기는 이만하면 됐다, 얘야!\"\n\n그분은 처음과 마찬가지로 이 모든 시간 내내 저를 받쳐주셨고, 그분의 다정함이 제게 어찌나 소중한지 저는 그분의 어깨에 머리를 기대고 마치 그분이 제 아버지라도 되는 양 그분을 사랑했습니다. 이 잠깐의 멈춤 동안 저는 마음속으로, 어떻게든 기운을 차리면 리처드를 만나 그를 바로잡아주리라 다짐했습니다.\n\n\"이런 것들보다는 더 나은 이야깃거리가 있단다,\" 후견인이 말씀하셨습니다. \"우리 사랑하는 아이가 회복되는 이 기쁜 때에 어울리는 이야기 말이다. 그리고 나는 이야기를 시작하면 곧 꺼내야 할 부탁 한 가지를 맡아 왔지. 에이더가 언제 너를 보러 오면 좋겠느냐, 얘야?\"\n\n저 역시 그 일을 생각하고 있었습니다. 사라진 거울과 약간 관련이 있긴 했지만, 그리 큰 관련은 아니었습니다. 제 사랑하는 그 아이가 제 외모의 어떤 변화에도 흔들리지 않으리라는 것을 저는 알고 있었으니까요.\n\n\"사랑하는 후견인 어른,\" 제가 말했습니다. \"제가 그 애를 그토록 오래 들이지 않았으니 — 정말이지, 정말이지 그 애는 제게 빛과 같은 존재인데도요 —\"\n\n\"잘 안다, 더든 부인, 잘 알아.\"\n\n그분은 어찌나 자상하신지, 그 손길에 그토록 살뜰한 연민과 애정이 배어 있고, 그 음성에 그토록 깊은 위안이 실려 제 마음에 스며들어, 저는 잠시 말을 잇지 못하고 멈추었습니다. \"그래, 그래, 피곤하구나.\" 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. \"조금 쉬렴.\"\n\n\"제가 에이더를 그토록 오래 들이지 않았으니,\" 잠시 뒤 저는 다시 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"제 뜻대로 조금만 더 하게 해주셨으면 좋겠어요, 후견인 어른. 그 애를 보기 전에 여기를 떠나 있는 편이 가장 좋을 것 같아요. 제가 움직일 수 있게 되는 대로 찰리와 함께 어디 시골 하숙으로 가서, 거기서 일주일쯤 묵으며 기력을 차리고 맑은 공기로 되살아나서, 에이더와 다시 함께 지내는 행복을 기다릴 수 있다면, 그게 우리 두 사람한테 더 나을 것 같아요.\"\n\n그토록 간절히 보고 싶었던 사랑하는 그 아이의 눈앞에 서기 전에, 달라진 제 모습에 조금 더 익숙해지고 싶다고 바란 것이 제게 못난 마음이었다고는 생각하고 싶지 않지만, 그것이 진실입니다. 저는 그러기를 바랐습니다. 그분은 저를 이해하셨으리라 확신했지만, 저는 그 점이 두렵지 않았습니다. 설령 그것이 못난 마음이라 해도, 그분이 그것을 너그러이 넘겨주시리라는 것을 알았기 때문입니다.\n\n\"우리 응석받이 작은 여인은,\" 후견인이 말씀하셨습니다. \"그 고집까지도 제 뜻대로 하게 해주마. 비록 아래층에서 누군가 눈물을 쏟는 값을 치르겠지만 말이다. 그리고 이걸 좀 보렴! 여기 기사도의 화신인 보이손이 일찍이 종이에 적힌 적 없는 무시무시한 맹세를 토해놓았구나. 네가 내려가 자기 집을 통째로 차지하지 않으면 — 그자는 벌써 그러려고 일부러 집을 비웠단다 — 하늘과 땅에 맹세코 그 집을 헐어버려 벽돌 한 장도 다른 벽돌 위에 남겨두지 않겠다는 거다!\"\n\n그러고서 후견인은 제 손에 편지 한 통을 쥐여주셨는데, \"친애하는 잔다이스 군에게\" 같은 평범한 첫인사도 없이 곧장 본론으로 돌진하여, \"맹세컨대 서머슨 양이 내려와 내 집을 차지하지 않으면 — 나는 오늘 오후 한 시 정각에 그녀를 위해 집을 비운다 —\"로 시작하더니, 이어서 더없이 진지하게, 더없이 강조하는 어투로 그분이 인용했던 그 기상천외한 선언을 늘어놓았습니다. 우리는 그것을 읽으며 마음껏 웃었지만, 그렇다고 그 편지를 쓴 이를 덜 높이 사지는 않았고, 이튿날 제가 감사 편지를 보내 그의 제안을 받아들이기로 정했습니다. 그것은 제게 더없이 반가운 제안이었으니, 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 어느 곳보다도 저는 체스니 월드만큼 가고 싶은 데가 없었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"자, 작은 살림꾼아,\" 후견인이 시계를 보며 말씀하셨습니다. \"나는 위층으로 올라오기 전에 엄격하게 시간을 정해두었단다. 너를 너무 일찍 지치게 해선 안 되니까. 그런데 그 시간이 이제 마지막 일분까지 다 닳아버렸구나. 부탁이 하나 더 있단다. 작은 플라이트 양이 네가 아프다는 소문을 듣고는, 가엾게도 무도화를 신은 채 — 이십 마일을 — 여기까지 걸어 내려오는 것쯤 아무것도 아니라는 듯 안부를 물으러 왔단다. 우리가 마침 집에 있었던 게 천만다행이지, 안 그랬으면 그이는 또 걸어서 돌아갔을 게다.\"\n\n저를 행복하게 만들려는 그 오랜 음모! 모두가 거기 가담한 듯했습니다!\n\n\"자, 귀염둥이야,\" 후견인이 말씀하셨습니다. \"네가 보이손의, 안 그러면 헐릴 운명인 그 헌신적인 집을 철거에서 구해주기 전에 어느 날 오후 그 해롭잖은 작은 이를 들여보내는 게 성가시지 않다면, 너는 그이를 — 비록 내 이름이 명망 높은 잔다이스라 해도 내가 평생을 들여 해줄 수 있는 것보다 — 더 자랑스럽고 더 흐뭇하게 만들어줄 수 있으리라 믿는다.\"\n\n저는 그분이, 그 가엾고 고통받는 이의 소박한 모습 속에 그 무렵 제 마음에 부드러운 가르침처럼 와닿을 무언가가 있으리라는 것을 알고 계셨으리라 확신합니다. 그분이 제게 말씀하시는 동안 저는 그것을 느꼈습니다. 저는 그이를 맞이할 마음이 얼마나 간절한지 말로는 도무지 다 표현할 수가 없었습니다. 저는 늘 그이를 가엾이 여겨왔지만, 그때만큼 가엾이 여긴 적은 없었습니다. 그이를 그 불행 속에서 달래줄 작은 힘이나마 제게 있다는 것을 늘 기뻐해왔지만, 그때만큼, 그 절반만큼이라도 기뻤던 적은 일찍이 없었습니다.\n\n우리는 플라이트 양이 마차를 타고 나와 제 이른 저녁을 함께 들 시간을 정했습니다. 후견인이 떠나신 뒤, 저는 긴 의자에 얼굴을 돌리고 누워, 만일 제가 이토록 많은 축복에 둘러싸여 있으면서 제가 겪어야 할 그 자그마한 시련을 스스로 부풀렸다면 용서해달라고 기도했습니다. 부지런하고 만족할 줄 알며 진실한 사람이 되어, 누군가에게 선을 베풀고 할 수만 있다면 약간의 사랑이라도 얻고 싶다고 빌던 그 옛 생일날의 어린아이 같은 기도가, 그 뒤로 제가 누려온 그 모든 행복과 제게로 향해온 그 모든 다정한 마음들을 떠올리게 하는 자책감과 함께 다시 마음속에 되살아났습니다. 만일 지금 제가 약해진다면, 그 은혜들로부터 제가 얻은 것이 무엇이겠습니까? 저는 그 옛 어린아이의 기도를 그 옛 어린아이의 말 그대로 되뇌었고, 그 옛 평안이 그 기도에서 떠나지 않았음을 깨달았습니다.\n\n이제 후견인은 날마다 오셨습니다. 한 주쯤 더 지나자 저는 우리 방들을 거닐 수 있었고, 창문 커튼 뒤에서 에이더와 긴 이야기를 나눌 수 있었습니다. 그래도 저는 그 아이를 한 번도 보지 않았는데, 그 아이가 알아채지 못하게 쉽게 볼 수 있었는데도, 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 마주할 용기가 아직 나지 않았기 때문입니다.\n\n정해진 날 플라이트 양이 도착했습니다. 그 가엾은 작은 이는 평소의 위엄도 까맣게 잊은 채 제 방으로 달려 들어와, 진심을 다해 마음속 깊은 곳에서부터 울며 \"내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스!\"라고 외치고는 제 목에 매달려 스무 번이나 입을 맞추었습니다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 그이는 손가방에 손을 넣으며 말했습니다. \"여긴 서류밖에 없네요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스. 손수건을 좀 빌려야겠어요.\"\n\n찰리가 손수건 하나를 건네주었고, 이 선량한 이는 분명 그것을 요긴하게 썼으니, 두 손으로 그것을 눈에 갖다 댄 채 그렇게 앉아 그다음 십 분 동안 내내 눈물을 흘렸습니다.\n\n\"기뻐서 그래요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스.\" 그이는 굳이 설명하려 애썼습니다. \"조금도 괴로워서가 아니에요. 당신이 다시 건강해진 걸 보니 기뻐서요. 당신을 뵙는 영광을 누리게 되어 기뻐서요. 나는 대법관님보다 당신이 훨씬 더 좋답니다, 내 사랑. 비록 내가 법정에는 꼬박꼬박 나가지만요. 그런데 말이 나온 김에, 얘야, 손수건 이야기를 하다 보니 —\"\n\n여기서 플라이트 양은 찰리를 보았는데, 찰리는 마차가 서는 곳까지 그이를 마중 나갔던 참이었습니다. 찰리는 저를 흘끔 보더니 그 말을 더 이어가기를 꺼리는 눈치였습니다.\n\n\"옳지!\" 플라이트 양이 말했습니다. \"옳고말고. 정말이지! 내가 그걸 입에 올리다니 참으로 경솔했네요. 하지만 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스 양, 솔직히 나는 가끔 (우리끼리니까 하는 말인데, 당신은 짐작도 못 하겠지만) 조금 — 횡설수설하거든요.\" 플라이트 양은 제 이마를 가리키며 말했습니다. \"그것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거예요?\" 저는 미소 지으며 말했습니다. 그이가 말을 잇고 싶어 하는 것을 보았기 때문입니다. \"제 호기심을 돋우어놓으셨으니, 이제 풀어주셔야지요.\"\n\n플라이트 양은 이 중대한 고비에서 조언을 구하듯 찰리를 보았고, 찰리는 \"괜찮으시면, 아주머니, 말씀하시는 게 낫겠어요\"라고 말하여, 그로써 플라이트 양을 더없이 흐뭇하게 했습니다.\n\n\"참 영리하지, 우리 어린 친구는.\" 그이는 그 특유의 알 듯 모를 듯한 태도로 제게 말했습니다. \"조그맣지만. 아주아주 영리해요! 자, 얘야, 이건 예쁜 일화랍니다. 그것뿐이에요. 그래도 나는 참 매력적이라고 생각해요. 마차에서 내려 우리를 따라 길을 내려온 게 누구겠어요, 얘야. 아주 촌스러운 보닛을 쓴 가엾은 사람이었답니다 —\"\n\n\"제니예요, 아주머니.\" 찰리가 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지!\" 플라이트 양은 더없이 상냥하게 수긍했습니다. \"제니. 그래요! 그런데 그 사람이 우리 어린 친구에게 뭐라고 했느냐면, 베일을 쓴 어떤 부인이 자기 오두막에 찾아와 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스의 안부를 묻고는, 손수건 하나를 자그마한 기념으로 가져갔다지 뭐예요. 그저 그게 내 사랑스러운 피츠 잔다이스의 것이라는 이유만으로요! 자, 보세요, 그 베일 쓴 부인이 어찌나 마음을 끄는지!\"\n\n\"괜찮으시면, 아가씨,\" 제가 다소 놀라 바라보자 찰리가 말했습니다. \"제니 말로는, 아기가 죽었을 때 아가씨가 거기 손수건 하나를 두고 가셨고, 자기가 그걸 챙겨서 아기의 자그마한 물건들과 함께 간직해두었대요. 제 생각엔, 괜찮으시면, 한편으로는 그게 아가씨 것이어서고, 한편으로는 그게 아기를 덮어준 것이어서 그랬나 봐요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"조그맣지만,\" 플라이트 양은 찰리의 총명함을 표현하느라 제 이마 언저리에 갖가지 손짓을 해 보이며 속삭였습니다. \"엄청나게 영리해요! 게다가 참 사랑스럽고! 얘야, 저 아이는 내가 들어본 그 어떤 변호사보다 더 또렷하답니다!\"\n\n\"그래, 찰리.\" 제가 대답했습니다. \"기억나. 그래서?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨,\" 찰리가 말했습니다. \"그게 바로 그 부인이 가져간 손수건이에요. 그리고 제니는 아가씨가 이걸 알아주셨으면 한대요. 자기는 돈을 산더미처럼 준대도 그걸 절대 내놓지 않았을 텐데, 그 부인이 가져가면서 대신 돈을 좀 두고 갔다고요. 제니는 그 부인을 전혀 모른대요, 괜찮으시면, 아가씨!\"\n\n\"그런데 그 부인이 누구일까?\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 플라이트 양이 더없이 알 듯 모를 듯한 표정으로 입술을 제 귀에 가까이 대며 넌지시 말했습니다. \"내 생각에는 — 이건 우리 조그만 친구한테는 말하지 말아요 — 그 부인은 대법관님의 부인이에요. 그분이 결혼했다는 거 알지요. 그런데 듣자 하니 그 부인이 그분을 못살게 군다더군요. 보석상한테 돈을 안 갚으면 각하의 서류를 불 속에 던져버린대요, 얘야!\"\n\n저는 그때 이 부인에 대해 그리 깊이 생각하지 않았습니다. 그게 캐디일지도 모른다는 느낌이 들었기 때문입니다. 게다가 제 손님이 제 주의를 흩뜨려놓았는데, 그이는 마차를 타고 오느라 몸이 차가워진 데다 시장해 보였고, 저녁이 들어오자 종이 꾸러미에 싸 들고 온 가엾으리만치 낡은 숄과 닳고 닳아 여러 번 기운 장갑을 더없이 만족스럽게 차려입는 데에 약간의 도움을 필요로 했습니다. 게다가 저는 생선 요리와 닭구이, 췌장 요리, 채소, 푸딩, 마데이라주로 이루어진 그 잔치를 주관해야 했는데, 그이가 그것을 얼마나 즐기는지, 또 얼마나 위엄과 격식을 차려 그 음식에 경의를 표하는지를 지켜보는 일이 어찌나 즐거운지, 저는 곧 다른 아무것도 생각하지 않게 되었습니다.\n\n식사를 마치고 우리 앞에 자그마한 후식이 놓였을 때 — 저를 위해 준비된 그 무엇도 다른 누구에게 감독을 맡기려 하지 않는 사랑하는 그 아이의 손으로 곱게 꾸며진 것이었지요 — 플라이트 양은 어찌나 수다스럽고 행복해 보이는지, 저는 그이를 그이 자신의 내력으로 이끌어보아야겠다고 생각했습니다. 그이는 늘 자기 이야기를 하는 걸 좋아했으니까요. 저는 이렇게 운을 뗐습니다. \"대법관님 곁을 여러 해 동안 지키셨지요, 플라이트 양?\"\n\n\"오, 여러, 여러, 여러 해째예요, 얘야. 하지만 나는 판결을 기다리고 있어요. 곧 나올 거예요.\"\n\n그이의 그 희망 속에도 어떤 불안이 깃들어 있어서, 제가 이 화제를 꺼낸 게 잘한 일인지 의심스러워졌습니다. 저는 이에 대해서는 더 말하지 않으려고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"우리 아버지도 판결을 기다리셨어요.\" 플라이트 양이 말했습니다. \"우리 오라버니도. 우리 언니도. 다들 판결을 기다렸지요. 내가 기다리는 것과 똑같은 판결을요.\"\n\n\"그분들은 모두 —\"\n\n\"그래요. 물론 돌아가셨지요, 얘야.\" 그이가 말했습니다.\n\n저는 그이가 말을 계속하리라는 것을 알았기에, 이 화제를 피하기보다 차라리 마주함으로써 그이에게 도움이 되어보는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"이 판결을 더는 기다리지 않는 편이,\" 제가 말했습니다. \"더 현명하지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"아니, 얘야,\" 그이가 선뜻 대답했습니다. \"물론 그게 현명하겠지요!\"\n\n\"그리고 법정에도 더는 나가지 않는 게요?\"\n\n\"그것도 물론이고요.\" 그이가 말했습니다. \"결코 오지 않는 것을 늘 기다린다는 건 참으로 사람을 지치게 해요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스! 정말이지, 뼛속까지 닳게 한다니까요!\"\n\n그이는 제게 자기 팔을 살짝 보여주었는데, 정말이지 무섭도록 야위어 있었습니다.\n\n\"하지만, 얘야,\" 그이가 그 알 듯 모를 듯한 태도로 말을 이었습니다. \"그곳에는 무시무시한 끌어당김이 있어요. 쉿! 우리 조그만 친구가 들어오면 이 얘긴 하지 말아요. 안 그러면 그 애가 겁먹을지 몰라요. 그럴 만도 하지요. 그곳에는 잔인한 끌어당김이 있답니다. 거길 떠날 수가 없어요. 그리고 기다릴 수밖에 없어요.\"\n\n저는 그렇지 않다고 그이를 안심시켜보려 했습니다. 그이는 참을성 있게 미소 지으며 제 말을 들었지만, 자기 나름의 대답을 준비하고 있었습니다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 그래! 내가 조금 횡설수설하니까 그렇게 생각하는 거예요. 조금 횡설수설하다니 참 우습지요, 그렇지 않아요? 머릿속이 아주 어지럽기도 하고요. 나도 그렇게 느껴요. 하지만, 얘야, 나는 거기 여러 해 있었고, 그래서 눈여겨봐 왔어요. 그건 탁자 위의 권표와 옥새 때문이에요.\"\n\n그것들이 무얼 할 수 있다는 거냐고, 그이는 어떻게 생각하느냐고 저는 부드럽게 물었습니다.\n\n\"끌어당기지요.\" 플라이트 양이 대답했습니다. \"사람들을 끌어들여요, 얘야. 그들에게서 평안을 빨아내요. 분별을 빨아내요. 고운 외모를 빨아내요. 좋은 성품을 빨아내요. 나는 그것들이 밤에 내 잠마저 빨아내는 걸 느꼈답니다. 차갑고 번쩍이는 악마들이에요!\"\n\n그이는 제 팔을 여러 번 톡톡 두드리며 호의 어린 표정으로 고개를 끄덕였는데, 마치 그토록 음울하게 말하며 이 무시무시한 비밀들을 제게 털어놓으면서도, 제가 그이를 두려워할 까닭이 없음을 알아주기를 바라는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"가만, 보자.\" 그이가 말했습니다. \"내 경우를 들려드리지요. 그것들이 나를 끌어당기기 전에 — 내가 그것들을 보기도 전에 — 내가 뭘 하던 사람이었더라? 탬버린 연주? 아니. 자수 일이에요. 나와 우리 언니는 자수 일을 했어요. 우리 아버지와 오라버니는 건축업을 했고요. 우리는 다 같이 살았답니다. 아주아주 점잖게요, 얘야! 처음에 우리 아버지가 끌려갔어요 — 천천히. 집안도 아버지와 함께 끌려갔지요. 몇 해 만에 아버지는, 누구에게도 다정한 말 한마디 다정한 눈길 한 번 줄 줄 모르는 사납고 신경질적이고 성난 파산자가 되어버렸어요. 전에는 그렇게도 다른 분이셨는데, 피츠 잔다이스. 아버지는 채무자 감옥으로 끌려갔어요. 거기서 돌아가셨지요. 그다음엔 우리 오라버니가 끌려갔어요 — 순식간에 — 술독으로. 그리고 누더기로. 그리고 죽음으로. 그다음엔 우리 언니가 끌려갔지요. 쉿! 어디로 끌려갔는지는 절대 묻지 말아요! 그러고 나서 나는 병들고 비참해졌고, 전에도 자주 들었듯이, 이 모든 게 대법원의 짓이라는 말을 들었어요. 몸이 나아지자 나는 그 괴물을 보러 갔지요. 그러고서 나는 그게 어찌 된 일인지 알아냈고, 거기 머물도록 끌려들고 말았답니다.\"\n\n자기 자신의 그 짤막한 이야기를 — 마치 그 충격이 아직도 생생하다는 듯 낮고 긴장된 목소리로 풀어놓던 그 이야기를 — 다 끝내고 나자, 그이는 차츰 평소의 상냥하고도 의젓한 태도를 되찾았습니다.\n\n\"내 말을 영 못 믿겠지요, 얘야! 자, 자! 언젠가는 믿게 될 거예요. 내가 조금 횡설수설하긴 해요. 하지만 나는 눈여겨봐 왔답니다. 이 여러 해 동안 의심 없이 권표와 옥새의 영향권 안으로 새로운 얼굴들이 들어오는 걸 많이 봤어요. 우리 아버지가 거기 들어오셨듯이. 우리 오라버니가 그랬듯이. 우리 언니가 그랬듯이. 나 자신이 그랬듯이. 나는 입심 좋은 켄지며 그 밖의 사람들이 새 얼굴들에게 이렇게 말하는 걸 들어요. '여기 작은 플라이트 양이 계시오. 아, 당신은 여기 처음이군요. 그러니 이리 와서 작은 플라이트 양에게 인사드려야지요!' 아주 좋아요. 그 영광을 누리게 되어 영광이고말고요! 그러면 우리는 다 함께 웃지요. 하지만, 피츠 잔다이스, 나는 앞으로 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알아요. 끌어당김이 시작되면, 나는 그들보다 훨씬 더 잘 안답니다. 나는 그 징조를 알아요, 얘야. 그리들리에게서 그게 시작되는 걸 봤어요. 그리고 그게 끝나는 것도 봤지요. 피츠 잔다이스, 내 사랑,\" 다시 목소리를 낮추며 말했습니다. \"나는 그게 우리 친구, 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인에게서 시작되는 걸 봤어요. 누군가 그를 붙들어줘야 해요. 안 그러면 그는 파멸로 끌려들고 말 거예요.\"\n\n그이는 잠시 말없이 저를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴이 차츰 누그러지며 미소로 번졌습니다. 자기가 너무 음울했나 싶어 두려워하는 듯, 또한 머릿속에서 이야기의 갈피를 놓쳐버린 듯, 그이는 포도주잔을 홀짝이며 정중하게 말했습니다. \"그래요, 얘야, 아까 말했듯이, 나는 곧 판결을 기다리고 있어요. 그러면 알다시피 나는 내 새들을 풀어주고, 영지를 나누어줄 거랍니다.\"\n\n저는 그이가 리처드를 두고 한 말에, 그리고 그이의 가엾고 야윈 모습으로 그토록 서글프게 그려진 그 슬픈 의미가 그 모든 두서없음을 뚫고 드러나는 데에 깊은 인상을 받았습니다. 그러나 다행히도 그이는 이제 다시 아주 흡족해져서 끄덕임과 미소로 환히 빛났습니다.\n\n\"그런데, 얘야,\" 그이는 다른 손을 마저 뻗어 제 손 위에 얹으며 명랑하게 말했습니다. \"당신은 내 주치의 일로 나를 축하해주지 않았어요. 정말이지 단 한 번도요!\"\n\n저는 그이가 무슨 말을 하는지 잘 모르겠다고 고백할 수밖에 없었습니다.\n\n\"내 주치의, 우드코트 선생 말이에요, 얘야. 나한테 그렇게도 극진했던 분이지요. 그것도 보수라곤 한 푼 받지 않고서요. 심판의 날까지요. 내가 말하는 건 권표와 옥새가 내게 건 마법을 풀어줄 바로 그 판결이에요.\"\n\n\"우드코트 씨는 지금 그토록 멀리 계셔서,\" 제가 말했습니다. \"그런 축하를 드릴 때는 이미 지난 줄 알았어요, 플라이트 양.\"\n\n\"하지만, 얘야,\" 그이가 대답했습니다. \"무슨 일이 있었는지 모른다는 게 말이 되나요?\"\n\n\"몰라요.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"모두가 입에 올리던 그 일을 말이에요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스!\"\n\n\"몰라요.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"제가 여기 얼마나 오래 있었는지 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n\"맞아요! 얘야, 잠깐 — 맞아요. 내 탓이네요. 하지만 내 기억력은, 다른 모든 것과 함께, 내가 말한 그것에 빨려나가 버렸거든요. 아주 강한 영향력이지요, 그렇지 않아요? 자, 얘야, 저 동인도 바다 건너에서 끔찍한 난파 사고가 있었답니다.\"\n\n\"우드코트 씨가 난파를 당하셨어요!\"\n\n\"동요하지 말아요, 얘야. 그분은 무사해요. 무시무시한 광경이었지요. 온갖 모습의 죽음이. 수백 명의 죽은 자와 죽어가는 자들이. 불길과 폭풍과 어둠이. 숱한 익사자들이 바위에 내던져졌지요. 거기서, 그 모든 것 한가운데서 내 소중한 주치의는 영웅이었답니다. 모든 일을 헤쳐가며 침착하고 용감했어요. 숱한 목숨을 구하고, 굶주리고 목마른 가운데서도 결코 불평하지 않고, 헐벗은 사람들을 자기 여벌 옷으로 감싸주고, 앞장서서 사람들에게 어찌해야 할지 일러주고, 그들을 통솔하고, 병든 자를 돌보고, 죽은 자를 묻어주고, 마침내 그 가엾은 생존자들을 무사히 데리고 빠져나왔지요! 얘야, 그 가엾고 야윈 사람들이 그분을 거의 떠받들다시피 했답니다. 뭍에 닿자 그들은 그분 발치에 엎드려 그분을 축복했어요. 온 나라가 그 이야기로 떠들썩하지요. 가만! 내 서류 가방이 어디 있더라? 거기 가지고 왔으니, 당신이 읽어봐요, 꼭 읽어봐요!\"\n\n그리고 저는 그 고귀한 이야기를 빠짐없이 읽었습니다. 다만 그때는 아주 더디게, 또 온전치 못하게 읽었지요. 제 눈이 어찌나 흐려졌는지 글자가 보이지 않았고, 어찌나 많이 울었는지 그이가 신문에서 오려낸 그 긴 기사를 몇 번이나 내려놓아야 했으니까요. 그토록 너그럽고 용맹한 일을 해낸 그 사람을 알고 지냈다는 사실에 저는 어찌나 의기양양했는지, 그의 명성에 어찌나 뜨거운 환희를 느꼈는지, 그가 한 일을 어찌나 흠모하고 사랑했는지, 그의 발치에 엎드려 그를 자기네 구원자라며 축복한 그 폭풍에 시달린 사람들이 부럽기까지 했습니다. 저 자신도 그때, 그토록 멀리 떨어진 곳에서나마 무릎 꿇고, 그가 그토록 진실로 선하고 용감하다는 사실에 황홀한 마음으로 그를 축복할 수 있었을 것입니다. 어머니든, 누이든, 아내든 — 그 누구도 저보다 더 그를 높일 수는 없으리라 느꼈습니다. 정말로 그러했습니다!\n\n저의 가엾은 작은 손님은 그 기사를 제게 선물로 주었고, 저녁이 저물어들기 시작하여 자기가 타고 돌아갈 마차를 놓칠세라 일어나 작별을 고할 때에도, 여전히 그 난파 이야기로 가득 차 있었습니다. 저는 아직 그 이야기를 세세한 데까지 다 이해할 만큼 마음을 추스르지 못한 채였지요.\n\n\"얘야,\" 그이는 자기 숄과 장갑을 정성껏 개키며 말했습니다. \"내 용감한 주치의는 작위를 받아야 마땅해요. 그리고 틀림없이 받을 거예요. 당신도 그렇게 생각하지요?\"\n\n작위를 받을 만한 분이냐, 그렇습니다. 작위를 받게 되겠느냐, 아닙니다.\n\n\"왜 안 되나요, 피츠 잔다이스?\" 그이가 다소 날카롭게 물었습니다.\n\n저는, 영국에서는 평화로운 공로로 이름을 떨친 사람에게는 그것이 아무리 훌륭하고 위대할지라도, 이따금 그 공로가 어마어마한 거액의 돈을 쌓아 올린 것일 때를 빼고는, 작위를 내리는 관습이 없다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니, 세상에.\" 플라이트 양이 말했습니다. \"어떻게 그런 말을 할 수 있어요? 얘야, 분명 당신도 알지 않나요? 지식, 상상력, 적극적인 인류애, 온갖 종류의 개선에서 영국의 가장 위대한 광채들이 모두 그 귀족 계급에 보태진다는 걸요! 둘러보고 잘 생각해봐요, 얘야. 이걸 모른다면 지금 당신이 조금 횡설수설하는 거예요. 바로 이것이야말로 이 나라에서 작위가 영원히 이어지는 위대한 까닭이라고요!\"\n\n그이가 자기 말을 정말로 믿는 게 아닐까 저는 두렵습니다. 정말로 정신이 온전치 못한 순간들이 있었으니까요.\n\n이제 저는 지금까지 지켜오려 애쓴 그 작은 비밀을 털어놓아야겠습니다. 저는 가끔, 우드코트 씨가 저를 사랑하시고, 만일 그분이 좀 더 부유했더라면 떠나기 전에 어쩌면 저를 사랑한다고 말해주셨을지도 모른다고 생각하곤 했습니다. 또 가끔은, 만일 그분이 그러셨더라면 제가 기뻐했으리라고 생각하기도 했습니다. 그러나 그런 일이 일어나지 않은 것이 지금 얼마나 다행인지요! 만일 제가 그분께 편지를 써서, 그분이 저의 것으로 알고 계셨던 그 가엾은 얼굴이 이제 제게서 완전히 사라졌으며, 한 번도 본 적 없는 한 여인에게 매인 그 속박에서 그분을 기꺼이 풀어드린다고 말해야 했더라면, 저는 얼마나 괴로웠을까요!\n\n오, 이렇게 된 것이 얼마나 다행인지요! 자비롭게도 큰 고통 하나를 면한 채, 저는 그분이 그토록 환히 보여주신 그 모든 것이 되고 싶다는 어린아이 같은 기도를 다시 제 마음으로 거두어들일 수 있었습니다. 그리고 돌이킬 것이라곤 아무것도 없었습니다. 제가 끊어야 할 사슬도, 그분이 끌어야 할 사슬도 없었습니다. 그리하여 저는, 하느님께서 허락하신다면, 의무의 길을 따라 저의 낮은 걸음을 걸어갈 수 있고, 그분은 그분의 더 고귀한 길을 따라 그 더 넓은 길 위를 걸어가실 수 있었습니다. 비록 우리는 그 여정에서 서로 떨어져 있을지라도, 저는 그 여정의 끝에서 그분을 만나기를 바랄 수 있었습니다. 사심 없이, 순결하게, 그분이 제게서 어떤 호의를 발견하셨던 그때 저를 여기셨던 것보다 훨씬 더 나은 사람이 되어서요.\n\n찰리와 나는 링컨셔로 떠나는 여정에 단둘이서만 나선 게 아니었습니다. 후견인은 내가 보이손 씨의 집에 무사히 도착할 때까지는 나에게서 눈을 떼지 않기로 마음을 굳혔던 터라 우리와 동행했고, 우리는 이틀 동안 길 위에 있었습니다. 나는 한 줄기 바람도, 향기 하나하나도, 꽃과 잎과 풀잎 하나하나도, 흘러가는 구름 한 점도, 자연 속의 모든 것이 지금껏 느꼈던 그 어느 때보다 더 아름답고 경이롭게 느껴졌습니다. 이것이 병을 앓고 얻은 나의 첫 수확이었습니다. 넓은 세상이 나에게 이토록 기쁨으로 가득 차 있는데, 내가 잃은 것이 얼마나 사소한지요.\n\n후견인은 곧바로 돌아갈 작정이었으므로, 우리는 내려가는 길에 사랑하는 그 애가 와야 할 날을 정했습니다. 나는 그 애에게 편지를 한 통 썼고, 후견인이 그것을 맡았으며, 초여름의 어느 화창한 저녁, 목적지에 도착한 지 반 시간도 안 되어 그는 우리를 떠났습니다.\n\n어느 착한 요정이 지팡이를 한 번 휘둘러 나를 위해 그 집을 지어주었고 내가 공주이며 요정이 총애하는 대녀였다 한들, 그 집 안에서 이보다 더 극진한 배려를 받을 수는 없었을 것입니다. 나를 위해 어찌나 많은 준비를 해두었던지, 그리고 나의 자질구레한 취향과 기호 하나하나를 어찌나 다정하게 기억해두었던지, 나는 방의 절반도 채 다시 둘러보기 전에 열두 번은 감격에 겨워 주저앉을 뻔했습니다. 하지만 그러는 대신 나는 그 방들을 죄다 찰리에게 보여주는 더 나은 길을 택했습니다. 찰리가 기뻐하니 내 마음도 가라앉았고, 정원을 한 바퀴 거닐며 찰리가 감탄하는 표현이란 표현을 모조리 쏟아내고 나자, 나는 마땅히 그러해야 할 만큼 잔잔하게 행복해졌습니다. 차를 마신 뒤 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것이 큰 위안이었습니다. \"에스더, 얘야, 이제 자리에 앉아 집주인에게 고맙다는 글을 쓸 만큼은 충분히 분별이 든 것 같구나.\" 그는 자기 얼굴만큼이나 환한 환영의 글을 나에게 남겨두었고, 자기 새를 내 손에 맡겼는데, 나는 그것이 그가 베풀 수 있는 가장 높은 신뢰의 표시임을 알았습니다. 그래서 나는 런던에 있는 그에게 짧은 글을 한 통 써서, 그가 아끼는 식물과 나무가 다들 어떤 모습인지, 더없이 놀라운 그 새가 어찌나 손님 대접 잘하는 태도로 나에게 짹짹거리며 이 집의 영예를 노래해주었는지, 또 어린 하녀가 도무지 형언할 수 없을 만큼 황홀해하는 가운데 내 어깨 위에서 노래를 부르고 나서는 새장 한구석 늘 앉던 자리에 자리를 잡았지만 꿈을 꾸는지 어떤지까지는 알릴 수가 없다고 적었습니다. 글을 마무리해 우체국으로 부치고 나서, 나는 짐을 풀고 정리하느라 무척 분주하게 움직였습니다. 그리고 찰리를 일찌감치 잠자리에 들도록 보내며 그날 밤은 더 이상 그 애가 필요하지 않을 거라고 일러두었습니다.\n\n나는 아직 거울을 들여다보지 않았고, 내 거울을 돌려달라고 청한 적도 없었기 때문입니다. 이것이 반드시 극복해야 할 약함임을 알면서도, 나는 지금 이곳에 도착하면 새로 시작하겠다고 늘 스스로에게 다짐해왔습니다. 그래서 혼자 있고 싶었고, 그래서 이제 내 방에 홀로 남아 이렇게 말했습니다. \"에스더, 행복해지려거든, 진실한 마음을 갖게 해달라고 기도할 자격을 조금이라도 가지려거든, 약속을 지켜야 한단다, 얘야.\" 나는 그 약속을 꼭 지키리라 단단히 마음먹었지만, 우선은 잠깐 자리에 앉아 내가 받은 모든 축복을 곱씹어보았습니다. 그러고 나서 기도를 올리고 조금 더 생각에 잠겼습니다.\n\n내 머리카락은 잘리지 않았으니, 한두 번 위태로운 고비를 넘기긴 했지만 말입니다. 머리카락은 길고 숱이 많았습니다. 나는 그것을 풀어 내려 흔들어 헤치고는 화장대 위 거울 앞으로 다가갔습니다. 거울에는 얇은 모슬린 천 한 자락이 가려져 있었습니다. 나는 그것을 젖히고 잠시 서서, 다른 것은 아무것도 보이지 않을 만큼 내 머리카락의 베일을 통해 들여다보았습니다. 그러고 나서 머리카락을 옆으로 넘기고는, 거울이 어찌나 차분하게 나를 바라보는지에 용기를 얻어 비친 모습을 들여다보았습니다. 나는 무척 많이 변해 있었습니다. 아, 정말, 정말 많이요. 처음에는 내 얼굴이 어찌나 낯설던지, 앞서 말한 그 용기가 없었다면 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 뒷걸음질 쳤으리라 싶습니다. 이내 얼굴이 한결 익숙해졌고, 그제야 그 변화의 정도를 처음보다 더 또렷이 알게 되었습니다. 내가 예상했던 것과는 달랐지만, 사실 나는 무엇 하나 뚜렷하게 예상한 것이 없었고, 어쩌면 뚜렷한 무엇이 있었더라면 그것대로 나를 놀라게 했을 것입니다.\n\n나는 한 번도 미인이었던 적이 없고 스스로 미인이라 여겨본 적도 없지만, 이런 모습과는 무척 달랐습니다. 그것이 이제 죄다 사라진 것입니다. 하늘이 나에게 어찌나 자비로우셨던지, 나는 쓰라리다 할 수 없는 눈물 몇 방울과 함께 그것을 떠나보낼 수 있었고, 그 자리에 서서 무척 감사한 마음으로 밤을 위해 머리를 매만질 수 있었습니다.\n\n한 가지가 나를 괴롭혔고, 나는 잠들기 전 오랫동안 그것을 곰곰이 생각했습니다. 나는 우드코트 씨의 꽃을 간직해두었던 것입니다. 그 꽃이 시들자 나는 말려서 내가 아끼는 책 갈피에 끼워두었습니다. 이것은 아무도 몰랐으니, 에이다조차 몰랐습니다. 나는 그가 이토록 달라진 사람에게 보낸 것을 간직할 권리가 나에게 있는지, 그렇게 하는 것이 그에게 너그러운 일인지 의구심이 들었습니다. 나는 그에게 너그럽고 싶었습니다. 그가 결코 알 리 없는 내 마음 깊은 은밀한 곳에서마저도요. 나는 그를 사랑할 수도 있었고, 그에게 헌신할 수도 있었기 때문입니다. 마침내 나는 이런 결론에 이르렀습니다. 돌이킬 수 없이 지나가버려 다시는 다른 어떤 빛으로도 되돌아볼 일이 없는 것에 대한 추억으로만 소중히 간직한다면 그 꽃을 지녀도 되겠다고요. 이것이 하찮게 보이지 않기를 바랍니다. 나는 무척 진지했습니다.\n\n나는 아침 일찍 일어나, 찰리가 발끝으로 살그머니 들어올 때 거울 앞에 있도록 신경을 썼습니다.\n\n\"어머, 어머, 아가씨!\" 찰리가 흠칫 놀라며 소리쳤습니다. \"그게 아가씨예요?\"\n\n\"그래, 찰리.\" 나는 조용히 머리를 매만지며 대답했습니다. \"그리고 나는 정말 건강하고, 정말 행복하단다.\"\n\n나는 그것이 찰리의 마음에서 짐 하나를 덜어주었음을 알았지만, 내 마음에서는 더 큰 짐이 내려갔습니다. 이제 나는 최악을 알았고 거기에 마음을 가다듬었습니다. 앞으로 이야기를 이어가면서, 끝내 완전히 이겨내지 못한 약함들을 숨기지는 않겠습니다. 하지만 그것들은 언제나 곧 나에게서 사라졌고, 한결 행복한 마음가짐이 충실하게 내 곁에 머물러주었습니다.\n\n에이다가 오기 전에 기운과 명랑함을 온전히 되찾고 싶었던 나는, 이제 찰리와 함께 온종일 신선한 공기를 쐬며 지낼 자잘한 계획을 줄줄이 세웠습니다. 우리는 아침 식사 전에 나가야 하고, 일찍 점심을 먹어야 하고, 점심 전후로 다시 나가야 하고, 차를 마신 뒤에는 정원에서 이야기를 나눠야 하고, 일찌감치 잠자리에 들어야 하며, 동네의 언덕이란 언덕은 죄다 올라야 하고 길과 오솔길과 들판이란 들판은 죄다 누벼야 했습니다. 보양식과 기운을 북돋우는 별미로 말하자면, 보이손 씨네 마음씨 좋은 가정부가 늘 손에 먹을 것이나 마실 것을 들고 종종걸음 치며 돌아다녔습니다. 내가 공원에서 쉬고 있다는 말만 들려도 그녀는 어김없이 바구니를 들고 종종걸음으로 쫓아왔고, 그 명랑한 얼굴에는 자주 영양을 보충하는 일이 얼마나 중요한지 늘어놓는 잔소리가 환하게 빛났습니다. 그리고 오로지 내가 타라고 마련해둔 조랑말이 한 마리 있었으니, 목이 짧고 갈기가 두 눈을 온통 덮은 통통한 조랑말로, 마음만 내키면 어찌나 수월하고 잔잔하게 가볍게 달리던지 그야말로 보물이었습니다. 며칠 지나지 않아 그 녀석은 내가 부르면 방목장에서 나에게 다가와 내 손에서 받아먹고 나를 졸졸 따라다녔습니다. 우리는 더없이 훌륭하게 마음이 통해서, 녀석이 어느 그늘진 오솔길을 나를 태운 채 게으르고 다소 고집스럽게 터덜터덜 가고 있을 때, 내가 목을 토닥이며 \"스텁스, 내가 가볍게 달리는 걸 얼마나 좋아하는지 알면서 안 달리다니 놀랍구나. 좀 들어주면 좋으련만, 너는 점점 멍청해져서 졸기만 하니\" 하고 말하면, 녀석은 머리를 한두 번 익살스럽게 흔들고는 곧장 달려나갔고, 그러면 찰리는 가만히 서서 어찌나 신나게 웃던지 그 웃음소리가 음악 같았습니다. 스텁스라는 이름을 누가 지어줬는지는 모르지만, 그 거친 털만큼이나 자연스럽게 녀석에게 어울리는 이름 같았습니다. 한번은 녀석을 작은 이륜마차에 매어 푸른 오솔길을 따라 오 마일을 의기양양하게 몰고 갔습니다. 그런데 우리가 녀석을 하늘 끝까지 치켜세우던 바로 그때, 가는 내내 한 치도 앞으로 나아가는 기색 없이 귓가를 빙글빙글 맴돌며 약을 올리던 그 작은 각다귀 떼를 여기까지 데려와야 했다는 게 영 못마땅한 듯, 녀석이 멈춰 서서 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하는 것이었습니다. 도저히 참을 수 없다는 결론에 이른 모양인지, 녀석은 내가 찰리에게 고삐를 넘기고 내려서 걷기 시작할 때까지 한사코 움직이려 들지 않았습니다. 그러자 녀석은 듬직하게 좋은 기분으로 나를 따라오며, 내 팔 밑에 머리를 들이밀고 내 소매에 귀를 비벼댔습니다. \"자, 스텁스, 내가 너에 대해 아는 바로 미루어 내가 잠깐만 타면 네가 다시 갈 거라고 확신한단다\" 하고 말해봤자 소용없었으니, 내가 녀석에게서 떨어지는 순간 다시 못 박힌 듯 우뚝 서버렸기 때문입니다. 그래서 나는 별수 없이 아까처럼 앞장서야 했고, 이런 행렬로 마을 사람들이 무척 즐거워하는 가운데 집으로 돌아왔습니다.\n\n찰리와 나는 그곳을 더없이 정다운 마을이라 부를 만했으니, 분명히 그랬습니다. 일주일이 지나자 사람들은 우리가 지나가는 걸 어찌나 반가워하던지, 하루에도 수없이 오갔는데도 집집마다 반기는 얼굴이 내다보였습니다. 나는 어른들 가운데 많은 이를, 아이들은 거의 다 전부터 알고 지냈지만, 이제는 교회 첨탑마저 친숙하고 다정한 표정을 띠기 시작했습니다. 새로 사귄 벗들 가운데에는 아주아주 나이 많은 노부인이 한 분 있었는데, 어찌나 자그마한 초가에 회칠한 집에 사는지 바깥 덧문을 돌쩌귀에 걸어 올리면 집 앞면 전체가 닫혀버릴 정도였습니다. 이 노부인에게는 뱃사람인 손자가 하나 있었는데, 나는 그녀를 대신해 손자에게 편지를 써주고 그 맨 위에 그녀가 손자를 키운 벽난로 구석을 그려 넣었습니다. 거기에는 손자의 옛 걸상이 여전히 옛 자리를 차지하고 있었지요. 온 마을은 이것을 세상에서 가장 놀라운 솜씨라 여겼는데, 머나먼 플리머스에서 답장이 와서 손자가 그 그림을 멀리 아메리카까지 가져갈 것이며 아메리카에서 다시 편지하겠다고 적은 것을 보고는, 마땅히 우체국에 돌아가야 할 공로가 죄다 나에게 돌아왔고, 나는 우편 제도 전체를 떠받든 공로까지 떠안게 되었습니다.\n\n이렇게 바깥 공기를 한껏 쐬고, 그토록 많은 아이와 놀고, 그토록 많은 사람과 수다를 떨고, 초대를 받아 그토록 많은 집에 앉아 있고, 찰리의 공부를 계속 봐주고, 날마다 에이다에게 긴 편지를 쓰다 보니, 그 자그마한 상실을 생각할 겨를이 거의 없었고 나는 거의 언제나 명랑했습니다. 어쩌다 틈틈이 그것을 떠올리더라도, 그저 분주히 움직이면 잊혔습니다. 한번은 한 아이가 \"엄마, 저 아가씨는 왜 예전처럼 예쁜 아가씨가 아니에요?\" 하고 말했을 때 내가 바랐던 것보다 더 사무치게 그것을 느꼈습니다. 하지만 그 아이가 나를 조금도 덜 좋아하지 않는다는 걸, 그리고 그 손길에 가엾이 여기며 감싸주는 듯한 마음을 담아 보드라운 손으로 내 얼굴을 쓰다듬는다는 걸 알고는 곧 다시 기운을 차렸습니다. 어떤 열등함에 대해서든 사려 깊고 섬세하게 대하는 것이 다정한 마음에게 얼마나 자연스러운 일인지를, 큰 위로와 함께 일깨워준 자잘한 일이 많았습니다. 그 가운데 하나가 유독 나를 뭉클하게 했습니다. 어느 결혼식이 막 끝나고 젊은 신랑 신부가 혼인 등록부에 서명을 해야 할 때, 나는 마침 그 작은 교회 안으로 거닐어 들어갔습니다.\n\n먼저 펜을 건네받은 신랑은 자기 표시로 서툰 십자가를 그렸고, 다음 차례인 신부도 똑같이 했습니다. 그런데 나는 지난번 그곳에 왔을 때 그 신부를 알았으니, 마을에서 가장 예쁜 처녀일 뿐 아니라 학교에서 꽤 두각을 나타낸 아이였던지라, 나도 모르게 다소 놀란 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았습니다. 그녀는 옆으로 다가와, 진실한 사랑과 흠모의 눈물을 그 빛나는 눈에 가득 담은 채 나에게 속삭였습니다. \"저이는 더없이 착하고 좋은 사람이에요, 아가씨. 하지만 아직 글을 못 써요. 이제 저한테 배울 거예요. 저는 무슨 일이 있어도 저이를 부끄럽게 하고 싶지 않아요!\" 노동자의 딸 영혼 속에 이런 고결함이 깃들어 있는데, 내가 무엇을 두려워하랴, 하고 나는 생각했습니다.\n\n바람은 예나 다름없이 신선하고 생기를 북돋우며 나에게 불어왔고, 건강한 핏기가 옛 얼굴에 돌았던 것처럼 새 얼굴에도 돌았습니다. 찰리는 보기에 경이로울 만큼 환하고 발그레했습니다. 우리 둘은 하루를 온전히 즐겼고 밤새 곤히 잤습니다.\n\n체스니 월드의 공원 숲에는 내가 좋아하는 곳이 한 군데 있었는데, 사랑스러운 경치가 내려다보이는 자리에 벤치 하나가 놓여 있었습니다. 이 조망 지점을 살리려고 숲을 베어내 탁 트이게 해두었고, 그 너머로 펼쳐진 환하고 햇살 가득한 풍경이 어찌나 아름다운지 나는 하루에 적어도 한 번은 그곳에서 쉬었습니다. 유령의 길이라 불리는, 그림처럼 아름다운 저택의 한 부분이 이 높은 곳에서 한눈에 잘 보였습니다. 그 섬뜩한 이름과, 보이손 씨에게서 들은 그 유래를 설명하는 데드록 가문의 오랜 전설이 그 풍경과 뒤섞여, 실제의 매력에 더해 어딘가 신비로운 흥취를 자아냈습니다. 이곳에는 또 제비꽃이 만발하기로 이름난 비탈이 하나 있었습니다. 찰리는 날마다 들꽃을 따는 것을 낙으로 삼았기에 나만큼이나 그 자리를 좋아했습니다.\n\n내가 어째서 그 저택 가까이는 한 번도 가지 않았고 안으로는 한 번도 들어가지 않았는지를 이제 와 따져봐야 부질없는 일입니다. 도착했을 때 들으니 가족은 그곳에 없었고 올 예정도 없다고 했습니다. 나는 그 건물에 대해 호기심이나 관심이 없기는커녕, 오히려 이 자리에 앉아 방들이 어떻게 늘어서 있을지, 또 그 이야기처럼 정말로 이따금 발소리 같은 메아리가 그 쓸쓸한 유령의 길 위로 울려 퍼지는지 궁금해하곤 했습니다. 데드록 마님이 나에게 남긴 그 형언할 수 없는 느낌이, 그녀가 없을 때조차 나를 그 저택에서 멀어지게 한 데 어느 정도 영향을 주었는지도 모릅니다. 잘은 모르겠습니다. 당연히 그녀의 얼굴과 자태가 그 저택과 결부되어 있었습니다. 하지만 그것이 나를 거기서 밀어냈다고는 할 수 없으니, 무언가가 밀어내긴 했지만요. 무슨 이유에선지, 아니면 아무 이유도 없이, 나는 내 이야기가 이제 다다른 그날에 이르기까지 한 번도 그 저택 가까이 가지 않았습니다.\n\n나는 오래 거닌 뒤 내가 좋아하는 그 자리에서 쉬고 있었고, 찰리는 나에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 제비꽃을 따고 있었습니다. 나는 저 멀리 깊은 석조 그늘 속에 누운 유령의 길을 바라보며 그곳을 떠돈다는 그 여인의 모습을 마음속으로 그려보고 있었는데, 그때 숲을 지나 다가오는 한 형체를 알아차렸습니다. 멀어지는 풍경이 어찌나 길고 나뭇잎으로 어둑하던지, 또 땅 위에 드리운 가지 그림자가 눈에 어찌나 어지럽게 얽혀 보이던지, 처음에는 그것이 어떤 형체인지 분간할 수가 없었습니다. 조금씩 조금씩 그것은 여인의 형체임을 드러냈으니, 어느 귀부인의, 데드록 마님의 모습이었습니다. 그녀는 혼자였고, 내가 보기에 놀랍게도 평소의 그녀답지 않은 훨씬 빠른 걸음으로 내가 앉은 곳을 향해 다가왔습니다.\n\n뜻밖에 그녀가 이토록 가까이 와 있는 데 가슴이 두근거려(그녀를 알아보았을 때는 거의 말이 닿을 만한 거리였습니다) 나는 일어나 산책을 계속하려 했습니다. 하지만 그럴 수가 없었습니다. 나는 옴짝달싹할 수 없게 되었습니다. 애원하듯 다급한 그녀의 몸짓 때문도, 빠른 걸음과 내민 두 손 때문도, 태도의 큰 변화와 그 오만한 자제심이 사라진 것 때문도 아니었으니, 그보다는 어린아이 시절 내가 그토록 사무치게 그리워하고 꿈꾸었던 무언가가 그녀의 얼굴에 어려 있었기 때문이었습니다. 내가 그 어떤 얼굴에서도 본 적 없는 무언가, 그녀의 얼굴에서는 일찍이 본 적 없는 무언가였습니다.\n\n두려움과 아찔함이 나를 덮쳤고, 나는 찰리를 불렀습니다. 데드록 마님은 그 순간 멈춰 서서 내가 알던 그 모습으로 거의 되돌아갔습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양, 내가 놀라게 한 모양이군요.\" 그녀가 이제 천천히 다가오며 말했습니다. \"아직 기력이 온전치 않을 텐데요. 무척 앓았다고 들었어요. 그 소식을 듣고 몹시 마음이 쓰였답니다.\"\n\n내가 앉은 벤치에서 몸을 움직일 수 없었던 것만큼이나, 나는 그녀의 창백한 얼굴에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었습니다. 그녀가 손을 내밀었는데, 억지로 가다듬은 표정과는 너무도 어긋나는 그 죽음 같은 차가움이, 나를 사로잡은 그 황홀한 두려움을 한층 깊게 만들었습니다. 어지러이 소용돌이치던 내 머릿속에 무엇이 들어 있었는지 말로는 다 할 수 없습니다.\n\n\"이제 다시 회복되어가나요?\" 그녀가 다정하게 물었습니다.\n\n\"방금 전까지만 해도 멀쩡했습니다, 데드록 마님.\"\n\n\"이 아이가 시중드는 아이인가요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"먼저 보내고 나와 함께 댁 쪽으로 걸어가겠어요?\"\n\n\"찰리.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"꽃을 집에 가져다 두렴. 곧 따라가마.\"\n\n찰리는 더없이 공손하게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 발그레 얼굴을 붉히며 보닛 끈을 매고 제 갈 길을 갔습니다. 그 애가 떠나자 데드록 마님은 내 곁 벤치에 앉았습니다.\n\n죽은 아기를 덮어주었던 내 손수건이 그녀의 손에 들린 것을 보았을 때, 내 마음이 어떤 상태였는지는 어떤 말로도 이를 수 없습니다.\n\n나는 그녀를 바라보았지만 그녀가 보이지 않았고, 그녀의 말이 들리지 않았으며, 숨을 쉴 수도 없었습니다. 심장이 어찌나 격렬하고 사납게 뛰던지 내 목숨이 몸에서 떨어져 나가는 것만 같았습니다. 하지만 그녀가 나를 가슴에 끌어안고, 입맞추고, 나를 굽어보며 울고, 나를 가엾이 여기며, 나를 제정신으로 불러냈을 때, 그녀가 무릎을 꿇고 나에게 부르짖었을 때, \"오, 내 아이, 내 아이, 내가 너의 사악하고 불행한 어미란다! 오, 부디 나를 용서해다오!\" 하고 외쳤을 때, 그녀가 그 큰 마음의 고통 속에 맨땅 위 내 발치에 엎드린 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 격동하는 온갖 감정 속에서도 하느님의 섭리에 대한 한 줄기 감사가 솟구치는 것을 느꼈습니다. 내가 이토록 변해버려서, 닮은 흔적으로 그녀를 욕보일 일이 결코 없으리라는 것, 이제는 누구도 나를 보고 그녀를 보며 우리 사이에 가까운 핏줄이 있으리라고는 어림조차 못 하리라는 것에 대한 감사였습니다.\n\n나는 어머니를 일으키며, 그토록 비통과 굴욕에 잠겨 내 앞에 엎드리지 말아달라고 기도하듯 간청했습니다. 끊기고 두서없는 말로 그렇게 했으니, 내가 겪던 괴로움에 더해, 어머니가 나의 발치에 엎드린 모습을 보는 것이 나를 두렵게 했기 때문입니다. 나는 어머니에게 말했습니다. 아니, 말하려 애썼습니다. 만약 그녀의 아이인 내가 어떤 상황에서든 그녀를 용서하는 일을 떠맡아야 한다면, 나는 그렇게 했고, 또 오래도록 그렇게 해왔노라고요. 나는 내 마음이 그녀를 향한 사랑으로 흘러넘친다고, 그것은 과거의 그 무엇도 바꾸지 않았고 바꿀 수도 없는 천성의 사랑이라고 말했습니다. 그제야 처음으로 어머니의 품에 기댄 내가 나를 낳아주신 것을 두고 그녀를 책망할 일이 아니라, 온 세상이 그녀에게 등을 돌릴지라도 그녀를 축복하고 받아들이는 것이 나의 도리라고, 다만 그렇게 하도록 허락해주기만을 청한다고 말했습니다. 나는 어머니를 품에 안았고 어머니도 나를 품에 안았으니, 고요한 숲속, 여름날의 정적 속에서, 평화롭지 못한 것은 오직 마음을 졸이는 우리 두 사람뿐인 듯했습니다.\n\n\"나를 축복하고 받아들인다니,\" 어머니가 신음하듯 말했습니다. \"너무도 늦었구나. 나는 내 어두운 길을 혼자 가야 하고, 그 길은 이끄는 대로 나를 데려갈 것이다. 하루하루, 때로는 시시각각, 나는 내 죄 많은 발 앞에 놓인 길이 보이지 않는다. 이것이 내가 스스로 불러온 이승의 벌이다. 나는 그것을 견디고, 또 숨긴다.\"\n\n자신의 인내를 생각하는 그 순간에조차, 어머니는 익숙한 오만한 무관심의 분위기를 베일처럼 다시 끌어다 둘렀지만, 이내 그것을 다시 벗어던졌습니다.\n\n\"무슨 수로든 지킬 수 있다면 나는 이 비밀을 지켜야 한다. 온전히 나만을 위해서가 아니란다. 나에게는 남편이 있으니, 비참하고 명예를 더럽히는 내가 말이다!\"\n\n이 말을 어머니는 어떤 비명보다도 그 소리가 더 끔찍한, 짓눌린 절망의 외침으로 토해냈습니다. 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 마치 내가 자기 몸에 손대는 것을 꺼리듯 내 품 안으로 움츠러들었습니다. 나는 온갖 말로 설득하고 내가 쓸 수 있는 온갖 다정한 말을 써봐도 어머니를 일으켜 세울 수 없었습니다. 어머니는 안 된다고, 안 된다고, 안 된다고 했습니다. 자기는 나에게만 이렇게 말할 수 있고, 다른 모든 곳에서는 오만하고 도도해야 하며, 평생 단 한 번 자연스러운 이 순간, 이곳에서만 자신을 낮추고 부끄러워하겠다고요.\n\n불행한 어머니는, 내가 앓는 동안 거의 미칠 지경이었다고 말했습니다. 그제야 비로소 자기 아이가 살아 있음을 알았던 것입니다. 그전에는 내가 그 아이일 줄 의심조차 할 수 없었습니다. 어머니는 평생 단 한 번 나에게 말을 건네려고 이곳까지 나를 따라왔습니다. 우리는 결코 어울릴 수도, 연락을 주고받을 수도 없을 것이며, 아마 그때 이후로는 이 세상에서 다시는 단 한마디도 나눌 수 없으리라 했습니다. 어머니는 오직 나만 읽으라고 쓴 편지 한 통을 내 손에 쥐여주며, 그것을 읽고 없애버린 뒤에는, 자기를 위해서라기보다는, 자기는 아무것도 청하지 않으니, 남편과 나 자신을 위해서, 앞으로 영원히 자기를 죽은 사람으로 여겨야 한다고 말했습니다. 내가 본 그 고뇌 속에서 어머니가 어머니의 사랑으로 나를 사랑한다고 믿어줄 수 있다면, 부디 그렇게 해달라고 청했습니다. 그러면 어머니가 무엇을 견디는지 헤아리며 더 큰 연민으로 자기를 생각할 수 있을 테니까요. 어머니는 자신을 모든 희망 너머, 모든 도움 너머에 두었습니다. 죽을 때까지 비밀을 지키든, 아니면 그것이 발각되어 자기가 취한 이름에 불명예와 치욕을 가져오든, 그것은 언제나 어머니 홀로의 싸움이었습니다. 어떤 애정도 그녀에게 가까이 갈 수 없었고, 어떤 인간도 그녀에게 아무런 도움을 줄 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"하지만 그 비밀이 지금까지는 안전한가요?\" 내가 물었습니다. \"지금은 안전한가요, 사랑하는 어머니?\"\n\n\"아니다.\" 어머니가 대답했습니다. \"발각될 뻔한 적이 있었단다. 우연 덕에 모면했지. 또 다른 우연으로 들통날 수도 있다. 내일이라도, 언제라도.\"\n\n\"특별히 두려운 사람이 있으세요?\"\n\n\"쉿! 나 때문에 그렇게 떨고 그렇게 울지 마라. 나는 그런 눈물을 받을 자격이 없단다.\" 어머니가 내 손에 입맞추며 말했습니다. \"나는 한 사람을 몹시 두려워한단다.\"\n\n\"적인가요?\"\n\n\"벗은 아니다. 둘 중 어느 쪽이라기엔 너무 무정한 사람이지. 그 사람은 레스터 데드록 경의 변호사로, 애정 없이 기계적으로 충직하며, 명문가의 비밀을 쥔 주인 노릇에서 오는 이익과 특권과 명성에 대해 무척 시샘이 많은 자란다.\"\n\n\"의심하는 게 있나요?\"\n\n\"많지.\"\n\n\"어머니를요?\" 내가 놀라 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래! 그자는 늘 빈틈없이 살피며 늘 내 곁에 있단다. 멈춰 세워둘 수는 있어도, 결코 떨쳐낼 수는 없지.\"\n\n\"그자에게는 연민이나 거리낌이 그토록 없나요?\"\n\n\"전혀 없단다. 분노도 없지. 자기 직분 말고는 모든 것에 무관심한 자야. 그자의 직분이란 비밀을 캐내어, 그것이 안겨주는 권력을, 나눌 자도 맞설 자도 없이 거머쥐는 것이지.\"\n\n\"그자를 믿어볼 수는 없을까요?\"\n\n\"결코 시도하지 않을 거다. 내가 그토록 오랜 세월 밟아온 그 어두운 길은 이끄는 곳에서 끝나겠지. 그 끝이 무엇이든 나는 끝까지 혼자 그 길을 간다. 가까울 수도, 멀 수도 있겠지. 길이 이어지는 한, 그 무엇도 나를 돌려세우지 못한다.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 어머니, 그토록 마음을 굳히셨나요?\"\n\n\"굳혔단다. 나는 오랜 세월 어리석음을 어리석음으로, 오만을 오만으로, 멸시를 멸시로, 무례를 무례로 받아쳐왔고, 그보다 더 많은 허영으로 숱한 허영을 묻고 살아왔다. 할 수만 있다면 이 위험도 견뎌 넘기고, 견뎌 죽어 넘기겠다. 마치 이 체스니 월드의 숲이 저 저택을 에워싸듯, 그것이 거의 무시무시하게 나를 옭아왔지만, 그 속을 헤쳐가는 내 길은 한결같다. 나에게는 길이 하나뿐이고, 하나밖에 있을 수 없으니.\"\n\n\"잔다이스 씨가—\" 내가 입을 떼려는데 어머니가 다급히 물었습니다. \"그 사람이 의심하느냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"아니요, 정말로요! 결코 의심하지 않는다고 안심하셔도 돼요!\" 그러고 나서 나는, 내 내력에 관해 그가 안다며 나에게 들려준 이야기를 어머니에게 전했습니다. \"하지만 그분은 워낙 선량하고 분별 있는 분이라,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"어쩌면 그분이 아신다면—\"\n\n그때까지 자세를 조금도 바꾸지 않았던 어머니가, 손을 들어 내 입술에 대고 나를 멈췄습니다.\n\n\"그 사람을 온전히 믿거라.\" 잠시 뒤 어머니가 말했습니다. \"내가 기꺼이 허락하마. 이런 어미가 상처 입힌 제 아이에게 주는 작은 선물이지! 하지만 그 일을 나에게는 말하지 마라. 나에게도 아직은 얼마간 자존심이 남아 있단다.\"\n\n나는 그때 할 수 있는 한, 그리고 지금 떠올릴 수 있는 한 가까이 설명했습니다. 그 내내 나의 동요와 비통이 어찌나 컸던지 나 자신조차 거의 알아차리지 못했지만, 그토록 낯설고 그토록 구슬프던 어머니의 목소리로 발음된 한마디 한마디가, 어린 시절 나는 한 번도 사랑하고 알아보도록 배운 적 없고, 그 목소리에 잠들도록 노래를 들은 적도, 그 목소리에서 축복을 들은 적도, 그 목소리가 희망을 불어넣어준 적도 없던 그 목소리가, 내 기억에 오래도록 새겨졌습니다. 나는 설명했습니다. 아니, 설명하려 했습니다. 나에게 더없이 좋은 아버지가 되어주신 잔다이스 씨가 어머니에게 얼마간의 조언과 의지가 되어줄 수 있기를 바랐을 뿐이라고요. 하지만 어머니는 아니라고, 그건 불가능하다고, 아무도 자기를 도울 수 없다고 대답했습니다. 자기 앞에 놓인 황야를, 어머니는 홀로 지나가야 한다고요.\n\n\"내 아이, 내 아이!\" 어머니가 말했습니다. \"마지막으로! 이 입맞춤을 마지막으로! 내 목을 감은 이 두 팔도 마지막으로! 우리는 다시 만나지 못한다. 내가 하려는 일을 이루기를 바란다면, 나는 그토록 오래 그래온 그대로의 모습이어야 한다. 그것이 나의 보답이요 운명이지. 화려하고 번영하며 떠받들리는 데드록 마님 소식을 듣거든, 그 가면 아래 양심의 가책에 시달리는 너의 비참한 어미를 생각해다오! 진실은 어미의 고통 속에, 부질없는 회한 속에, 제 가슴이 품을 수 있는 단 하나의 사랑과 진실을 제 가슴속에서 죽이는 그 일 속에 있음을 생각해다오! 그러고는, 할 수 있거든 어미를 용서하고, 결코 용서할 수 없을 하늘을 향해 어미를 용서해달라고 부르짖어다오!\"\n\n우리는 잠시 더 서로를 끌어안고 있었지만, 어머니는 어찌나 단호하던지 내 두 손을 떼어내 도로 내 가슴에 갖다 대고는, 거기 그 손을 쥔 채 마지막으로 입맞추며 놓아주고서, 나를 떠나 숲속으로 들어갔습니다. 나는 홀로 남았고, 햇빛과 그늘 속 저 아래에는 그 오랜 저택이 고요하고 잔잔하게 누워 있었습니다. 테라스와 작은 탑을 거느린 그 저택은, 내가 처음 보았을 때는 더없이 완전한 안식이 깃든 듯 보였건만, 이제는 어머니의 비참을 굽어보는 모질고 무자비한 감시자처럼 보였습니다.\n\n처음에는 멍하니 넋이 나가 병상에 누웠을 때만큼이나 약하고 속수무책이었지만, 발각의 위험을, 아니 가장 아득한 의심마저도 막아야 한다는 절박함이 나에게 도움이 되었습니다. 나는 내가 울었다는 것을 찰리에게 숨기려 할 수 있는 만큼 조심했고, 내가 신중하고 침착해야 할 온갖 거룩한 책무를 떠올리도록 스스로를 다잡았습니다. 그렇게 해내기까지, 아니 북받치는 슬픔을 억누르기까지도 적잖은 시간이 걸렸지만, 한 시간쯤 지나자 한결 나아졌고 이제 돌아가도 되겠다 싶었습니다. 나는 무척 천천히 집으로 갔고, 대문에서 나를 기다리던 찰리에게, 데드록 마님이 떠난 뒤 산책을 좀 더 늘리고 싶은 마음이 들었는데 너무 지쳐 좀 누워야겠다고 말했습니다. 내 방에 안전히 들어와서 나는 그 편지를 읽었습니다. 그것에서 나는 분명히 알아냈으니, 그것이 그때로서는 큰 것이었습니다. 내가 어머니에게 버림받은 것이 아니었다는 사실이었습니다. 내가 죽은 줄로 알고 한쪽에 치워두었을 때 내 몸에서 생명의 기색을 발견한 어머니의 손위이자 하나뿐인 언니, 내 어린 시절의 대모는, 내가 살기를 바라거나 그러길 원치도 않으면서, 엄격한 의무감에서 나를 철저한 비밀 속에 길렀고, 내가 태어난 지 몇 시간 만에 어머니의 얼굴을 다시는 보지 않았습니다. 나는 이 세상에서 어찌나 기이하게 자리를 잡고 있었던지, 얼마 전까지도 나 자신의 어머니가 알기로는 한 번도 숨 쉰 적 없고, 묻혀버렸으며, 생명을 부여받은 적도, 이름을 가진 적도 없었던 것입니다. 어머니가 교회에서 나를 처음 보았을 때 흠칫 놀라며, 만약 그 아이가 살았더라면, 그리고 계속 살았더라면 나와 같았으리라고 생각했지만, 그때는 그것이 전부였습니다.\n\n그 편지가 나에게 더 무엇을 말해주었는지는 여기서 되풀이할 필요가 없습니다. 그것에는 내 이야기 속에서 제 나름의 때와 자리가 있습니다.\n\n내가 가장 먼저 한 일은 어머니가 쓴 것을 태우고 그 재마저 없애버리는 것이었습니다. 그러고 나서 내가 이 세상에 길러진 것을 생각하며 마음이 무겁게 슬퍼졌다 한들, 그것이 내 안의 매우 부자연스럽거나 못된 일로 비치지 않기를 바랍니다. 마치 내가 정말 한 번도 숨 쉬지 않았더라면 많은 사람에게 더 낫고 더 행복했으리라는 것을 아는 듯한 심정이었던 것을요. 나 자신을 두려워하여, 내가 나 자신의 어머니와 어느 자랑스러운 가문의 이름에 닥칠 위험이요 가능한 치욕이라 여겼던 것을요. 내가 어찌나 혼란스럽고 흔들렸던지, 내가 태어나며 죽는 것이 옳고 또 그렇게 정해진 일이었으며, 그때 살아 있는 것은 그른 일이고 정해진 바가 아니었다는 믿음에 사로잡혔던 것을요.\n\n이것이 내가 품었던 진짜 감정입니다. 나는 기진하여 잠들었고, 깨어나서는 남들을 위한 근심의 짐을 진 채 이 세상에 돌아와 있다는 생각에 다시 울었습니다. 나는 그 어느 때보다 나 자신이 두려웠으니, 내가 그에 맞서는 증인이 된 그녀를, 체스니 월드의 안주인을, 그리고 이제 바닷가에 밀려드는 파도처럼 내 귓가에 신음하는 그 오랜 말의 새롭고도 끔찍한 뜻을 새삼 떠올렸기 때문입니다. \"에스더, 네 어미는 너의 치욕이었고, 너는 그 어미의 치욕이다. 머지않아, 정말 머지않아 너는 이것을 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이며, 또한 여자만이 느낄 수 있는 방식으로 그것을 느끼게 될 것이다.\" 그 말과 더불어, 그 다른 말도 되돌아왔습니다. \"남들의 죄가 네 머리 위에 떨어지지 않도록 날마다 기도하여라.\" 나는 나를 둘러싼 그 모든 것을 풀어 헤칠 수 없었고, 마치 그 비난과 수치가 죄다 내 안에 있고, 그 응징이 내려온 것만 같았습니다.\n\n낮은 우중충하게 흐려 슬픈 저녁으로 기울었고, 나는 여전히 같은 고뇌와 씨름했습니다. 나는 홀로 밖으로 나가, 공원을 조금 거닐며 나무 위로 떨어지는 짙은 그늘과, 이따금 거의 나에게 닿을 듯 변덕스레 날아다니는 박쥐들을 바라보다가, 난생처음 그 저택에 이끌렸습니다. 마음이 좀 더 굳건했더라면 그 가까이 가지 않았을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 그때의 나는, 그 저택을 바짝 끼고 도는 오솔길로 접어들었습니다.\n\n나는 감히 머뭇거리거나 올려다보지 못했지만, 향긋한 내음과 널찍한 산책길과 잘 가꾼 화단과 매끄러운 잔디를 갖춘 테라스 정원 앞을 지나며, 그것이 얼마나 아름답고 장중한지, 그리고 오래된 돌난간과 흉벽과 얕은 계단의 넓은 층층대가 세월과 풍상에 어떻게 금이 가 있는지, 또 손질된 이끼와 담쟁이가 그 둘레와 해시계의 오래된 돌받침 둘레로 어떻게 자라 있는지를 보았습니다. 그리고 분수가 떨어지는 소리를 들었습니다. 이윽고 길은 작은 탑을 인 망루와 별난 모양의 현관들로 다채로운, 어두운 창들이 길게 늘어선 곳을 지났으니, 그곳에는 오래된 돌사자와 기괴한 괴물들이 그늘진 굴 밖으로 곤두서서, 발톱에 움켜쥔 가문 문장 너머로 저녁 어스름을 향해 으르렁대고 있었습니다. 거기서 길은 한 대문 아래로 굽어들어, 정문이 있는 안마당을 지나(나는 얼른 서둘러 지나쳤습니다), 마구간 옆을 지났는데, 그곳에는 오직 묵직한 소리들만 깃든 듯했습니다. 높다란 붉은 담을 붙들고 우거진 담쟁이의 두꺼운 덩어리 사이로 바람이 속삭이는 소리에서든, 풍향계가 나직이 투덜대는 소리에서든, 개 짖는 소리에서든, 느릿느릿 울리는 시계 종소리에서든 말입니다. 그러다 이내 나뭇잎 바스락거리는 소리가 들리는 보리수의 달콤한 향내를 맞닥뜨리며, 나는 굽은 길을 따라 남쪽 정면으로 돌아섰고, 거기 내 위로 유령의 길 난간과, 어쩌면 어머니의 방일지도 모를 불 켜진 창 하나가 있었습니다.\n\n이곳도 머리 위 테라스처럼 포석이 깔려 있어, 소리 없던 내 발걸음이 돌바닥 위에서 메아리치는 소리를 냈습니다. 무엇 하나 멈춰 서서 바라보지 않으면서도 지나치며 보이는 것은 죄다 보면서, 나는 빠르게 지나가고 있었고 몇 발짝만 더 가면 불 켜진 창을 지나쳤을 터였습니다. 그때 메아리치는 내 발소리가 문득 마음속에 한 생각을 불러일으켰으니, 유령의 길 전설에는 무시무시한 진실이 깃들어 있다는 것, 이 위풍당당한 저택에 재앙을 불러올 자가 바로 나라는 것, 그리고 불길함을 알리는 내 발이 바로 그 순간 그곳을 떠돌고 있다는 것이었습니다. 나는 나 자신에 대한 한층 커진 두려움에 사로잡혀 온몸이 싸늘해졌고, 나 자신과 모든 것에서 달아나, 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔으며, 정문 수위실에 다다라 공원이 내 뒤로 시무룩하게 시커멓게 누울 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았습니다.\n\n그 밤 내 방에 홀로 들어와 다시 거기서 풀이 죽고 불행해진 뒤에야, 비로소 나는 이런 마음 상태가 얼마나 그르고 배은망덕한지 깨닫기 시작했습니다. 그러나 이튿날 올 사랑하는 그 애에게서 기쁨에 찬 편지가 와 있었으니, 다정한 기대로 가득한 그 편지는 내가 대리석이 아닌 다음에야 마음이 움직일 수밖에 없는 것이었습니다. 후견인에게서도 또 한 통의 편지가 와 있었는데, 만약 어디서든 그 자그마한 사람을 보거든 더든 아주머니에게 전해달라며, 그녀가 없으니 다들 가엾을 만큼 풀이 죽었고, 살림은 엉망진창이 되어가고, 그 누구도 열쇠를 건사하지 못하며, 집 안팎의 모든 이가 이 집은 예전 그 집이 아니라고 입을 모으며 그녀가 돌아오기를 바라며 반란을 일으킬 지경이라고 적혀 있었습니다. 이런 편지 두 통이 한꺼번에 오니, 내가 받을 자격을 한참 넘어 사랑받고 있다는 것과, 내가 얼마나 행복해야 마땅한지를 생각하게 되었습니다. 그것은 나의 지난 삶을 죄다 떠올리게 했고, 마땅히 진작 그랬어야 하듯, 나를 한결 나은 상태로 이끌어주었습니다.\n\n나는 내가 죽도록 정해진 게 아니었음을 똑똑히 보았으니, 그랬다면 애초에 살지도 못했을 것이고, 더구나 이토록 행복한 삶을 위해 남겨지지도 않았을 테니까요. 얼마나 많은 일들이 함께 어우러져 나의 안녕을 위해 작용해왔는지를, 또 설령 아비들의 죄가 때로 자식에게 떨어진다 한들 그 말이 내가 아침에 두려워했던 그런 뜻은 아니라는 것을 똑똑히 보았습니다. 나는 내 출생에 대해 여왕이 자신의 출생에 결백한 것만큼이나 결백하며, 하늘에 계신 아버지 앞에서 출생을 두고 벌받지도, 여왕이 출생을 두고 상받지도 않으리라는 것을 알았습니다. 나는 바로 그날의 충격 속에서, 이토록 빠르게도, 나에게 닥친 변화에 대해 위안이 되는 화해를 찾아낼 수 있다는 것을 경험했습니다. 나는 결심을 새로이 다지며 그 결심 안에서 굳건해지기를 기도했고, 나 자신을 위해, 또 나의 불행한 어머니를 위해 마음을 쏟아내며, 아침의 어둠이 걷혀가고 있음을 느꼈습니다. 그 어둠은 잠 속까지는 따라오지 않았고, 이튿날의 빛이 나를 깨웠을 때 그것은 사라지고 없었습니다.\n\n사랑하는 그 애는 오후 다섯 시에 도착할 예정이었습니다. 그 애가 올 길을 따라 멀리 산책을 다녀오는 것 말고는 그 사이의 시간을 어떻게 더 잘 보낼지 알 수 없었습니다. 그래서 찰리와 나와 스텁스가—그 한 번의 큰 일이 있은 뒤로는 다시 마차를 메우지 않았으므로 스텁스에는 안장을 얹어—그 길을 따라 멀리 다녀왔습니다. 돌아와서 우리는 집과 정원을 거창하게 점검하며 모든 것이 더없이 예쁜 상태인지 살폈고, 이 살림살이의 중요한 일원으로 새도 꺼내 채비를 갖춰두었습니다.\n\n그 애가 올 때까지는 아직 꼬박 두 시간이 넘게 남아 있었는데, 길게만 느껴지던 그 사이에, 고백하건대 나는 달라진 내 모습 때문에 안절부절못하며 마음을 졸였습니다. 나는 사랑하는 그 애를 어찌나 끔찍이 사랑했던지, 내 모습이 누구보다도 그 애에게 어떤 영향을 줄지가 가장 마음 쓰였습니다. 내가 이런 자잘한 괴로움에 빠진 것은 결코 한탄해서가 아니었으니—그날만큼은 정말 한탄하지 않았다고 확신합니다—다만 나는 생각했습니다. 그 애가 온전히 마음의 준비가 되어 있을까? 나를 처음 보았을 때, 좀 충격을 받고 실망하지 않을까? 예상보다 조금 더 나쁘게 드러나지 않을까? 옛 에스더를 찾다가 못 찾는 게 아닐까? 나에게 다시 익숙해지느라 처음부터 모든 걸 새로 시작해야 하지 않을까?\n\n나는 사랑스러운 그 애의 얼굴에 어리는 갖가지 표정을 너무도 잘 알았고, 그 사랑스러움 속의 얼굴이 어찌나 정직한지, 그 애가 그 첫 표정을 나에게 숨기지 못하리라는 것을 미리부터 확신했습니다. 그래서 나는 곰곰이 헤아려보았습니다. 만약 그 표정이 이런 뜻들 가운데 어느 하나라도 담는다면, 그럴 가능성이 무척 높은데, 과연 내가 나 자신을 온전히 책임질 수 있을까?\n\n뭐, 할 수 있으리라 생각했습니다. 지난밤을 겪고 나니 할 수 있으리라 생각했습니다. 하지만 기다리고 또 기다리고, 고대하고 또 고대하고, 생각하고 또 생각하는 것은 어찌나 나쁜 준비인지, 나는 그 길을 다시 따라가 그 애를 맞으러 가기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n그래서 나는 찰리에게 말했습니다. \"찰리, 나 혼자 나가서 그 애가 올 때까지 길을 따라 걸을게.\" 찰리는 나를 기쁘게 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 크게 찬성했으므로, 나는 그 애를 집에 남겨두고 나섰습니다.\n\n하지만 두 번째 이정표에 닿기도 전에, 나는 저 멀리 흙먼지가 이는 것을 보고(아직 마차일 리도 없고 그럴 수도 없다는 걸 알면서도) 어찌나 가슴이 두근거렸던지, 발길을 돌려 다시 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었습니다. 그리고 발길을 돌리고 나니, 이번에는 마차가 내 뒤로 따라붙을까 봐 어찌나 두렵던지(그런 일이 일어나지도, 일어날 수도 없다는 걸 여전히 알면서도) 따라잡히지 않으려고 그 길의 거의 절반을 뛰었습니다.\n\n그러고 나서, 무사히 다시 돌아와서 생각하니, 이거 참 잘한 짓이로구나 싶었습니다! 이제 나는 몸이 후끈 달아올랐고, 최선이 아니라 최악으로 만들어버린 것이었습니다.\n\n마침내, 적어도 십오 분은 더 남았으리라 여기고 있을 때, 정원에서 떨고 있던 나에게 찰리가 느닷없이 소리쳤습니다. \"저기 오세요, 아가씨! 오셨어요!\"\n\n그럴 마음은 아니었는데, 나는 위층 내 방으로 뛰어 올라가 문 뒤에 숨었습니다. 거기서 나는, 사랑하는 그 애가 계단을 올라오며 \"에스더, 얘, 내 사랑, 어디 있어? 자그마한 사람아, 사랑하는 더든 아주머니!\" 하고 부르는 소리가 들려올 때조차, 부들부들 떨며 서 있었습니다.\n\n그 애는 뛰어 들어왔다가, 나를 보고는 다시 뛰어나가려던 참이었습니다. 아, 나의 천사 같은 그 애! 그 옛 다정한 눈빛, 온통 사랑이요, 온통 애틋함이요, 온통 정뿐인 눈빛. 그 밖에는 아무것도—아니, 아무것도, 아무것도 없었습니다!\n\n오, 내가 얼마나 행복했던지요. 마룻바닥에 주저앉아, 사랑스럽고 아름다운 그 애도 마룻바닥에 함께 주저앉아, 흉터 진 내 얼굴을 그 사랑스러운 뺨에 갖다 대고, 눈물과 입맞춤으로 그것을 적시고, 어린아이를 어르듯 나를 앞뒤로 흔들며, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 이름으로 나를 부르고, 그 변함없는 가슴에 나를 꼭 끌어안았습니다.\n\n내가 지켜야 할 비밀이 내 것이었다면, 에이다와 함께 지낸 지 얼마 되지 않아 틀림없이 그녀에게 털어놓고 말았을 것입니다. 그러나 그건 내 것이 아니었고, 나는 어떤 중대한 위기가 닥치지 않는 한, 후견인에게조차 그 비밀을 말할 권리가 내게 있다고 느끼지 않았습니다. 혼자 짊어지기에는 무거운 짐이었습니다. 그래도 지금 내가 해야 할 도리는 분명해 보였고, 사랑하는 사람의 애정으로 복을 누리던 나에게는 그 일을 해낼 충동이나 격려가 따로 필요하지 않았습니다. 그녀가 잠들고 사방이 고요해질 때면 어머니에 대한 기억이 나를 깨워두고 밤을 슬픔으로 물들이곤 했지만, 다른 때에는 거기에 굴복하지 않았습니다. 그래서 에이다는 예전 그대로의 나를 보았습니다. 물론, 내가 이미 충분히 말했고 지금으로서는 가능한 한 더는 입에 올리지 않으려는 그 한 가지만은 빼고 말입니다.\n\n에이다가 첫날 저녁 우리가 함께 바느질을 하다가, 그 집안 사람들이 저택에 와 있느냐고 물었을 때, 그리고 그저께 숲에서 데드록 마님이 내게 말을 걸었던 일이 있었기에 그런 것 같다, 그렇게 알고 있다고 대답할 수밖에 없었을 때, 침착함을 유지하기란 무척 힘들었습니다. 에이다가 마님이 뭐라고 했느냐고 물었을 때, 그리고 친절하고 관심을 보였다고 내가 대답했을 때, 또 에이다가 그 미모와 우아함은 인정하면서도 거만한 태도와 사람을 얼어붙게 하는 도도한 분위기를 두고 한마디 했을 때는 더더욱 힘들었습니다. 그러나 찰리가 자기도 모르게 나를 거들어 주었습니다. 데드록 마님은 런던에서 이웃 주의 다른 큰 저택을 방문하러 가는 길에 이 집에서 겨우 이틀 밤만 묵었고, 우리가 이른바 우리의 전망대에서 마님을 본 그 이튿날 아침 일찍 떠났다고 일러준 것입니다. 찰리는 작은 주전자에도 큰 귀가 달렸다는 옛말을 톡톡히 증명해 주었습니다. 내가 한 달 동안 귀에 담을 이야기보다 더 많은 말과 사연을 그 아이는 하루에 주워들었으니까요.\n* 작은 주전자에도 큰 귀가 달렸다: 아이들도 어른 못지않게 잘 듣고 알아챈다는 뜻의 영국 속담.\n\n우리는 보이손 씨 댁에서 한 달을 머물 예정이었습니다. 내 기억으로는, 사랑스러운 에이다가 그곳에서 화창한 한 주를 채 보내기도 전이었습니다. 어느 날 저녁, 정원사가 꽃에 물 주는 일을 거들고 막 촛불이 켜질 무렵, 찰리가 자못 중대한 표정으로 에이다의 의자 뒤에 나타나더니 알 수 없는 눈짓으로 나를 방 밖으로 불러냈습니다.\n\n\"저, 아가씨, 괜찮으시면요.\" 찰리가 눈을 더없이 동그랗고 크게 뜬 채 소곤거렸습니다. \"데드록 암스에서 아가씨를 찾으세요.\"\n\n\"어머, 찰리.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그 술집에서 도대체 누가 나를 찾는다는 거니?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 고개를 앞으로 내밀고는 작은 앞치마 끈 위에 두 손을 꼭 모으며 대답했습니다. 무언가 비밀스럽거나 은밀한 일을 즐길 때면 늘 하는 동작이었습니다. \"그런데 어떤 신사분이세요, 아가씨. 안부를 전하면서, 아무한테도 말하지 마시고 좀 와 주십사 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"누구의 안부라는 거니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"그분의 거요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 대답했습니다. 그 아이의 문법 실력은 늘고는 있었지만, 그리 빠르지는 않았습니다.\n\n\"그런데 어쩌다 네가 심부름꾼이 된 거니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"제가 심부름꾼은 아니에요, 아가씨, 괜찮으시다면요.\" 어린 하녀가 대답했습니다. \"더블유 그러블 씨예요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"더블유 그러블이 누구니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"그러블 씨요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 대답했습니다. \"모르세요, 아가씨? 데드록 암스, 더블유 그러블.\" 찰리는 마치 간판을 한 글자씩 천천히 읽는 것처럼 말했습니다.\n\n\"아? 그 집 주인 말이니, 찰리?\"\n\n\"네, 아가씨. 저, 괜찮으시면요, 아가씨, 그분 부인이 미인이신데요, 발목이 부러진 게 영영 안 붙었대요. 그리고 그 부인 오빠는 가둬 두는 우리에 들어갔던 톱장이인데요, 다들 그 사람이 맥주만 마시다가 아주 죽고 말 거라고 한대요.\" 찰리가 말했습니다.\n\n무슨 일인지 알 수 없는 데다 요즘은 쉽게 불안해지던 터라, 나는 혼자 그곳에 가는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다. 나는 찰리에게 보닛과 베일과 숄을 어서 가져오라 일렀고, 그것들을 걸친 뒤 비탈진 작은 거리를 따라 내려갔습니다. 보이손 씨의 정원만큼이나 이제는 내 집처럼 익숙한 길이었습니다.\n\n그러블 씨는 셔츠 바람으로 자기 가게의 더없이 깨끗한 작은 선술집 문 앞에 서서 나를 기다리고 있었습니다. 내가 오는 것을 보더니 두 손으로 모자를 벗어 들었는데, 마치 무쇠 그릇이라도 다루듯(실제로 그만큼 무거워 보였습니다) 그렇게 들고서, 모래를 뿌린 통로를 앞장서 걸어 가장 좋은 응접실로 안내했습니다. 카펫이 깔린 말끔한 방이었는데, 화초가 알맞다 싶은 정도보다 많이 놓여 있었고, 캐롤라인 왕비의 색판화 한 점, 조개껍데기 몇 개, 찻쟁반이 꽤 여러 개, 유리 상자에 든 박제하여 말린 물고기 두 마리, 그리고 천장에 매달린 기묘한 달걀인지 기묘한 호박인지 모를 것 하나가 있었습니다(어느 쪽인지 나는 모르겠고, 아는 사람도 별로 없었으리라 싶습니다). 나는 그러블 씨가 자주 문 앞에 서 있던 덕분에 그의 얼굴을 잘 알고 있었습니다. 보기 좋게 통통한, 사람 좋아 보이는 중년 사내였는데, 모자와 무릎까지 오는 장화 없이는 제 집 난롯가에서도 도무지 편히 차려입었다는 기분이 들지 않는 듯했지만, 교회에 갈 때 말고는 결코 외투를 걸치지 않는 사람이었습니다.\n\n그는 촛불 심지를 다듬고는 그것이 어떻게 보이는지 살피려고 조금 물러섰다가, 그대로 방 밖으로 물러나 버렸습니다. 나로서는 뜻밖이었으니, 막 그에게 누가 보냈느냐고 물으려던 참이었기 때문입니다. 그때 맞은편 응접실 문이 열리며 어쩐지 귀에 익은 목소리들이 들리다가 뚝 멎었습니다. 빠르고 가벼운 발걸음이 내가 있는 방으로 다가오더니, 내 앞에 선 사람은 다름 아닌 리처드였습니다!\n\n\"사랑하는 에스더!\" 그가 말했습니다. \"내 가장 좋은 벗!\" 그 형제 같은 인사가 어찌나 따뜻하고 진실하던지, 처음의 놀라움과 기쁨에 나는 에이다가 잘 지낸다는 말을 전할 숨조차 겨우 찾았습니다.\n\n\"내가 생각하던 바로 그걸 답해 주는구나. 언제나 변함없는 그 사랑스러운 아가씨야!\" 리처드가 나를 의자로 이끌어 앉히고 제 옆에 앉으며 말했습니다.\n\n나는 베일을 올렸지만, 다 올리지는 않았습니다.\n\n\"언제나 변함없는 그 사랑스러운 아가씨!\" 리처드가 아까처럼 진심을 담아 말했습니다.\n\n나는 베일을 완전히 올린 뒤, 리처드의 소매에 손을 얹고 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며, 따뜻하게 맞아 줘서 얼마나 고마운지, 그를 보게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지 말했습니다. 앓는 동안 내가 굳혔던 결심 때문에 더욱 그러했고, 나는 이제 그 결심을 그에게 전했습니다.\n\n\"에스더.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"내가 너보다 더 이야기를 나누고 싶은 사람은 없어. 네가 나를 이해해 주었으면 하니까.\"\n\n\"그리고 나는, 리처드.\" 내가 고개를 가로저으며 말했습니다. \"네가 다른 어떤 분을 이해해 주었으면 해.\"\n\n\"네가 그렇게 곧장 존 잔다이스 이야기를 꺼내니,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그분을 말하는 거겠지?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 곧바로 말하지만, 나는 그게 다행이라고 생각해. 바로 그 문제에서 내가 이해받기를 간절히 바라거든. 명심해, 다른 누구도 아닌 너에게 말이야, 에스더! 나는 잔다이스 씨에게도, 그 누구에게도 해명할 책임이 없어.\"\n\n그가 이런 어조를 쓰는 것을 보니 마음이 아팠고, 그도 그것을 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"자, 자, 에스더.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그 이야기는 지금 들어가지 말자. 나는 너를 팔짱에 끼고 여기 네 시골집에 조용히 나타나서, 내 사랑스러운 사촌을 놀라게 해 주고 싶어. 존 잔다이스에 대한 너의 충성심도 그 정도는 허락하겠지?\"\n\n\"리처드.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"그분 댁에서 네가 진심으로 환영받으리라는 걸 너도 알잖아. 네가 그렇게 여겨 주기만 한다면 그곳이 바로 네 집이고. 그리고 여기서도 너는 진심으로 환영받아!\"\n\n\"세상에서 가장 좋은 꼬마 아주머니다운 말이로구나!\" 리처드가 명랑하게 외쳤습니다.\n\n나는 그에게 직업이 마음에 드느냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"아, 그럭저럭 마음에 들어!\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"괜찮아. 한동안은 다른 어떤 일 못지않게 잘 맞고. 자리를 잡고 나면 거기에 마음을 쓰게 될지는 모르겠지만, 그때 가서 자리를 팔고 나오면 되니까. 어쨌든, 지금은 그런 골치 아픈 이야기는 다 접어 두자.\"\n\n그토록 젊고 잘생기고, 모든 면에서 플라이트 양과는 완벽히 정반대인데! 그런데도 그를 스쳐 지나가는 그 흐릿하고 조급하고 무언가를 좇는 듯한 눈빛만은, 끔찍하게도 그녀를 빼닮았습니다!\n\n\"나는 지금 휴가를 받아 시내에 와 있어.\" 리처드가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래?\"\n\n\"응. 긴 휴정기에 앞서 내, 내 대법원 사건을 좀 살피려고 잠깐 건너왔어.\" 리처드가 애써 무심한 웃음을 지으며 말했습니다. \"정말이지, 우리도 드디어 그 해묵은 소송에 속도를 내기 시작했거든.\"\n\n내가 고개를 가로저은 것도 무리는 아니었습니다!\n\n\"네 말마따나, 그게 유쾌한 화제는 아니지.\" 리처드가 아까와 같은 그늘을 얼굴에 드리우며 말했습니다. \"오늘 밤만은 사방으로 날려 보내자. 후! 사라졌다! 누가 나랑 같이 왔게?\"\n\n\"내가 들은 게 스킴폴 씨 목소리였니?\"\n\n\"바로 그 사람이야! 그분만큼 나한테 도움이 되는 사람도 없어. 어찌나 매혹적인 아이 같은지!\"\n\n나는 리처드에게 두 사람이 함께 내려온 걸 누가 아느냐고 물었습니다. 그는 아니, 아무도 모른다고 대답했습니다. 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기—그는 스킴폴 씨를 그렇게 불렀습니다—를 찾아갔더니 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기가 우리가 어디 있는지 일러 주었고, 자기는 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기에게 우리를 보러 갈 작정이라고 말했더니, 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기가 곧장 자기도 따라가고 싶어 해서, 그래서 데려왔다는 것이었습니다. \"그분은 충분히 그럴 만한 값어치가 있어. 그분이 쓰는 푼돈 같은 비용은 말할 것도 없고, 제 몸무게의 세 곱절만큼 금값을 해.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그렇게 유쾌한 사람이라니까. 속물근성이라곤 없어. 싱그럽고 풋풋한 마음을 가졌지!\"\n\n스킴폴 씨가 리처드에게 비용을 대게 한다는 데서 그의 속물근성 없음이 증명된다고는 도무지 보이지 않았지만, 나는 그에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다. 마침 그가 들어와 우리 대화를 다른 데로 돌렸으니까요. 그는 나를 보고 더없이 반가워하며, 나 때문에 여섯 주 동안 틈틈이 기쁨과 공감의 달콤한 눈물을 흘렸노라고, 내 회복 소식을 들었을 때만큼 행복한 적이 없었노라고 말했습니다. 그리고 이제 세상에 선과 악이 뒤섞여 있는 까닭을 알 것 같다고, 누군가 아플 때 건강을 더 고맙게 여기게 된다고 느낀다고, 어쩌면 갑이 사팔뜨기가 되어야 을이 똑바로 보는 제 눈을 더 행복하게 여기게 되고, 병이 나무다리를 짚어야 정이 비단 양말 속의 제 살과 피에 더 만족하게 되는 것이 만물의 섭리인지도 모르겠다고 했습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양, 여기 우리 친구 리처드를 보세요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"미래에 대한 더없이 밝은 환상으로 가득 차서, 그걸 대법원의 어둠 속에서 불러내고 있지요. 자, 이거야말로 유쾌하고, 가슴 뛰고, 시정이 넘치는 일 아닙니까! 옛날에는 판과 님프들이 상상 속에서 피리를 불고 춤을 추어 숲과 외딴 들판을 양치기에게 즐거운 곳으로 만들어 주었지요. 우리의 이 목가적인 양치기 리처드는, 법정 판결의 가락에 맞추어 운명의 여신과 그 시종들을 법학원의 따분한 복도 사이로 뛰놀게 함으로써 그 우중충한 곳을 환하게 밝히고 있습니다. 정말 즐거운 일이지요! 성미 고약한 어떤 투덜이가 나에게 이렇게 말할 수도 있겠지요. '이런 법률과 형평법의 폐단이 무슨 소용이오? 그걸 어떻게 옹호하겠소?' 그러면 나는 이렇게 답합니다. '투덜이 친구, 나는 그걸 옹호하지 않소. 다만 나에게는 그게 무척 흐뭇하단 말이오. 내 친구인 어느 양치기 청년이 그걸 내 단순한 마음에 더없이 매혹적인 무언가로 바꿔 주니까. 그것이 존재하는 까닭이 이것이라고는 말하지 않겠소. 나는 당신네 속세의 투덜이들 사이에 끼인 어린아이일 뿐이고, 당신에게나 나 자신에게나 무엇 하나 해명할 의무가 없으니 말이오. 하지만 그럴지도 모르지 않소.'\"\n\n나는 리처드가 이보다 더 나쁜 친구를 만나기란 어려웠으리라고 진지하게 생각하기 시작했습니다. 바른 원칙과 목적이 그에게 가장 절실한 바로 이런 때에, 매사를 흘려 보내고 미뤄 버리는 이 매혹적인 헐거움, 모든 원칙과 목적을 가뿐히 면제해 버리는 이 가벼움이 그의 곁에 있다는 것이 나를 불안하게 했습니다. 세상 물정을 겪을 만큼 겪었고 집안의 불운이 빚어낸 비참한 회피와 다툼을 늘 들여다볼 수밖에 없는 내 후견인 같은 천성이라면, 스킴폴 씨가 제 약점을 스스럼없이 고백하고 천진한 솔직함을 내보이는 데서 엄청난 위안을 얻는다는 것은 나도 이해할 수 있을 것 같았습니다. 그러나 그것이 보이는 만큼 정말로 꾸밈이 없는 것인지, 혹은 그것이 다른 어떤 역할 못지않게, 그것도 더 적은 수고로, 스킴폴 씨의 안일한 목적에 들어맞는 것은 아닌지, 나는 도무지 확신할 수 없었습니다.\n\n두 사람 다 나와 함께 걸어서 돌아왔고, 스킴폴 씨가 대문에서 우리와 헤어진 뒤, 나는 리처드와 함께 조용히 들어가 말했습니다. \"에이다, 손님 한 분을 모셔 왔어.\" 붉어진 채 깜짝 놀란 그 얼굴을 읽기란 어렵지 않았습니다. 그녀는 그를 깊이 사랑했고, 그도 그것을 알았으며, 나도 알았습니다. 그저 사촌으로서의 만남이라니, 참으로 속이 빤히 들여다보이는 일이었습니다.\n\n나는 내 의심이 어지간히 고약해지고 있는 게 아닌가 하여 나 자신마저 미덥지 않을 지경이었지만, 리처드가 그녀를 깊이 사랑한다는 것만은 그리 확신할 수 없었습니다. 그가 그녀를 무척 흠모한다는 것은—누구라도 그러지 않을 수 없었을 테니까요—분명했고, 후견인에게 한 약속을 그녀가 어떻게 지킬지 그가 알지 못했더라면 큰 자부심과 열정으로 어린 시절의 약혼을 다시 맺었으리라는 것도 분명했습니다. 그래도 나에게는 그를 사로잡은 그 영향력이 여기까지 미쳤다는 괴로운 생각이 들었습니다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 그의 마음에서 떨쳐지기 전까지는, 그가 이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서처럼 자신의 가장 참되고 진실한 마음을 뒤로 미루고 있다는 생각 말입니다. 아아! 그 마름병만 없었더라면 리처드가 어떤 사람이 되었을지, 나는 이제 영영 알 수 없겠지요!\n\n그는 더없이 솔직한 태도로 에이다에게 말했습니다. 그녀가 잔다이스 씨로부터 받아들인 조건(그가 보기에는 너무 고분고분하고 미덥게 받아들였다고 했습니다)에 무슨 은밀한 침범을 하러 온 것이 아니라, 떳떳하게 그녀를 보고 나를 보러 왔으며, 잔다이스 씨와 지금 자기가 맺고 있는 관계의 정당함을 밝히러 왔다고 말입니다. 사랑스러운 늙은 아기가 곧 우리에게 올 테니, 자기가 나와 거리낌 없는 대화를 통해 입장을 바로잡을 수 있도록 내일 아침에 약속을 잡아 달라고 청했습니다. 나는 일곱 시에 그와 공원을 거닐자고 제안했고, 그렇게 정해졌습니다. 곧이어 스킴폴 씨가 나타나 한 시간 동안 우리를 즐겁게 해 주었습니다. 그는 특히 어린 코빈시스(찰리를 가리키는 말이었습니다)를 보고 싶다고 하더니, 가부장 같은 태도로 그 아이에게, 자기가 그 아이의 돌아가신 아버지에게 할 수 있는 한 모든 일거리를 주었노라고, 그리고 그 아이의 어린 남동생 하나가 어서 같은 직업에 발을 들인다면 자기가 여전히 일감을 꽤 많이 돌려줄 수 있기를 바란다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"나는 늘 이런 그물에 걸리거든요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 와인을 탄 물 한 잔을 앞에 두고 환하게 웃으며 우리를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"그리고 늘 보석으로 풀려나지요. 마치 배처럼 말입니다. 아니면 정산을 받지요. 배의 선원들처럼요. 늘 누군가가 나를 대신해 해 줘요. 나는 못 하니까요, 알다시피 돈이 통 없거든요. 하지만 누군가가 해 준답니다. 나는 누군가의 손을 빌려 빠져나오지요. 나는 찌르레기 같지 않아요. 나는 빠져나옵니다. 그 누군가가 누구냐고 물으신다면, 정말이지 나도 말씀드릴 수가 없어요. 자, 그 누군가를 위해 건배합시다. 신의 가호가 그에게 있기를!\"\n\n* 찌르레기: 로런스 스턴의 「감상적 여행」에 나오는, 새장에 갇혀 \"나갈 수 없어\"라고 우는 찌르레기를 빗댄 말.\n리처드는 아침에 조금 늦었지만, 그를 오래 기다릴 필요는 없었고, 우리는 공원으로 들어섰습니다. 공기는 맑고 이슬을 머금었으며 하늘에는 구름 한 점 없었습니다. 새들이 즐겁게 지저귀고, 고사리와 풀과 나무에 맺힌 물방울이 눈부시게 반짝였습니다. 숲의 풍요로움은 어제보다 스무 곱절은 짙어진 듯했습니다. 마치 만물이, 그토록 묵직하게 잠에 잠겨 고요하던 지난밤에, 그날의 영광을 위해 온갖 경이로운 잎사귀의 미세한 마디마디까지 평소보다 더 깨어 있기라도 했던 것처럼 말입니다.\n\n\"여긴 참 아름다운 곳이야.\" 리처드가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했습니다. \"여기엔 소송의 그 삐걱거림과 불협화음이 하나도 없어!\"\n\n그러나 다른 근심거리가 있었습니다.\n\n\"있잖아, 에스더.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"전반적인 일들이 다 정리되고 나면, 나는 여기 내려와서 좀 쉬어야겠어.\"\n\n\"지금 쉬는 편이 더 낫지 않을까?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"아, 지금 쉬는 거라든가,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"지금 뭔가 아주 확실한 걸 한다든가, 그건 쉽지 않아. 한마디로, 그건 안 돼. 적어도 나는 못 하지.\"\n\n\"어째서?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"왜 안 되는지 너도 알잖아, 에스더. 만약 네가 미완성인 집에 살고 있는데, 그 지붕이 내일이나 모레, 다음 주, 다음 달, 내년에 올려지거나 걷히거나, 위에서 아래까지 헐리거나 다시 세워질 판이라면, 너도 쉬거나 자리 잡기가 어려울 거야. 나도 그래. 지금이라고? 우리 같은 소송 당사자들에게 지금이란 건 없어.\"\n\n지난밤의 그 어두워진 표정을 다시 보았을 때, 나는 가엾은 작은 떠돌이 친구가 늘어놓던 그 끌림이라는 것을 거의 믿을 뻔했습니다. 거기에 또한 죽어 버린 그 불운한 사람의 그림자까지 어려 있다고 생각하니 끔찍했습니다.\n\n\"리처드.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"우리 대화의 시작이 좋지 못하구나.\"\n\n\"네가 그렇게 말할 줄 알았어, 더든 아주머니.\"\n\n\"나뿐만이 아니야, 리처드. 집안의 저주에 희망이나 기대를 절대로 걸지 말라고 언젠가 너에게 일러 준 사람이 나는 아니었잖아.\"\n\n\"또 존 잔다이스 이야기로 돌아오는구나!\" 리처드가 조바심을 내며 말했습니다. \"좋아! 우린 늦든 빠르든 그분 이야기를 해야만 해. 그분이 내가 하려는 말의 핵심이니까. 차라리 지금 하는 게 낫겠지. 에스더, 어떻게 그렇게 눈이 멀 수가 있어? 그분이 이해관계가 걸린 당사자라는 게 안 보여? 내가 그 소송에 대해 아무것도 모르고 아무 관심도 없기를 그분이 바라는 게 그분으로서는 아주 좋은 일일지 몰라도, 나한테는 영 좋지 않을 수도 있다는 게 안 보이느냐고?\"\n\n\"아, 리처드.\" 내가 항의했습니다. \"네가 그분을 보았고 그분 말을 들었으면서, 그분 지붕 아래서 살며 그분을 알았으면서, 어떻게 아직도, 그것도 우리 말을 들을 사람 하나 없는 이 외딴곳에서 나에게까지, 그런 가당찮은 의심을 입에 담을 수가 있니?\"\n\n그는 타고난 너그러움이 책망의 아픔을 느끼기라도 한 듯 얼굴을 붉혔습니다. 그는 한참 동안 잠잠히 있다가 가라앉은 목소리로 대답했습니다. \"에스더, 내가 비열한 인간이 아니라는 거, 그리고 의심이나 불신 따위가 내 나이대 사람에게 형편없는 자질이라는 걸 내가 안다는 거, 너도 잘 알 거야.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 알지.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"이보다 더 확실히 아는 건 없어.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 사랑스러운 아가씨다운 말이야.\" 리처드가 받았습니다. \"그리고 너답고. 그 말이 나에게 위안이 되거든. 이 모든 일에서 위안 한 조각이라도 건져야겠어. 어차피 잘해 봐야 고약한 일이니까. 굳이 너한테 말할 것도 없지만.\"\n\n\"잘 알아.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"리처드, 나는 잘 알아. 뭐라고 해야 할까, 너만큼이나 잘 알아, 그런 곡해는 너의 천성과 어긋난다는 걸. 그리고 무엇이 너를 그토록 바꿔 놓는지도, 너만큼이나 잘 알고.\"\n\n\"자, 누이야, 자.\" 리처드가 조금 더 명랑하게 말했습니다. \"어쨌든 너는 나에게 공정하게 굴겠지. 내가 불운하게도 그 영향력 아래 놓여 있다면, 그분 역시 그래. 그게 나를 조금 비틀어 놓았다면, 그분도 조금 비틀어 놓았을지 몰라. 이 모든 얽히고설킨 불확실함을 떼어 놓고 보면 그분이 명예로운 사람이 아니라는 게 아니야. 분명 그렇지. 하지만 그건 누구든 다 오염시켜. 누구든 다 오염시킨다는 거, 너도 알잖아. 그분이 그렇게 말하는 걸 너도 쉰 번은 들었을걸. 그런데 어째서 그분만 빠져나가야 하지?\"\n\n\"그건,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그분은 보기 드문 인품을 지녔고, 단호하게 그 굴레 바깥에 자신을 두어 왔기 때문이야, 리처드.\"\n\n\"아, 때문이고 때문이고!\" 리처드가 특유의 활기찬 투로 대꾸했습니다. \"에스더, 나는 잘 모르겠어. 그렇게 겉으로 무관심한 척하는 게 어쩌면 영리하고 그럴듯한 처신일 수도 있지 않을까. 그러면 이해관계가 걸린 다른 당사자들이 제 몫에 느슨해질 수도 있고. 사람들이 하나둘 죽어 나가고, 쟁점들이 기억에서 흐려지고, 입맛에 딱 맞는 여러 일들이 슬그머니 벌어질 수도 있는 거야.\"\n\n나는 리처드가 어찌나 가여운지 더는, 눈빛으로조차도 그를 책망할 수 없었습니다. 나는 후견인이 그의 잘못을 두고 보이던 너그러움을, 그리고 조금의 원망도 없이 그것을 이야기하던 모습을 떠올렸습니다.\n\n\"에스더.\" 리처드가 다시 말을 이었습니다. \"내가 여기 존 잔다이스를 음흉하게 헐뜯으러 온 거라고는 생각하지 마. 나는 그저 내 입장을 밝히러 왔을 뿐이야. 내 말은 이거야. 내가 이 소송에는 통 아랑곳하지 않던 어린 시절에는 만사가 다 좋았고 우리도 사이좋게 지냈어. 하지만 내가 거기에 관심을 갖고 들여다보기 시작하자마자, 사정이 완전히 달라졌지. 그러자 존 잔다이스는 에이다와 내가 헤어져야 한다는 둥, 내가 그 몹시 못마땅한 길을 고치지 않으면 나는 그녀에게 어울리지 않는다는 둥 하기 시작했어. 자, 에스더, 나는 그 몹시 못마땅한 길을 고칠 생각이 없어. 나는 그분이 명령할 권리도 없는 그런 불공정한 타협의 조건으로 존 잔다이스의 호의를 붙들지는 않겠어. 그분 마음에 들든 안 들든, 나는 내 권리와 에이다의 권리를 지켜야 해. 나는 이걸 꽤 오래 생각해 봤고, 이게 내가 내린 결론이야.\"\n\n가엾은 리처드! 그는 정말로 이 문제를 오래 생각해 온 것이었습니다. 그의 얼굴이, 목소리가, 태도가, 하나같이 그것을 너무도 또렷이 보여 주었습니다.\n\n\"그래서 나는 그분에게 떳떳이 말하는 거야(내가 이 모든 걸 편지로 써 보냈다는 걸 너도 알아야 해). 우리는 입장이 갈리니, 차라리 은밀히 갈리느니 대놓고 갈리는 편이 낫다고. 나는 그분의 호의와 보호에 감사를 표하고, 그분은 그분 길을 가고, 나는 내 길을 가는 거야. 사실 우리 길은 같지가 않아. 지금 다투고 있는 유언장 가운데 하나에 따르면, 나는 그분보다 훨씬 더 많이 받게 돼. 그게 인정될 유언장이라는 말은 아니지만, 어쨌든 그게 있고, 그것도 가망이 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"네 편지에 대해서라면, 리처드,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"너에게 처음 듣는 게 아니야. 나는 이미 그 이야기를 들었어. 기분 상했다거나 화난 말은 한마디도 없이 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 리처드가 누그러지며 대답했습니다. \"이 비참한 일을 떼어 놓고 보면 그분이 명예로운 사람이라고 내가 말해 두길 잘했네. 하지만 나는 늘 그렇게 말하고, 한 번도 의심한 적이 없어. 자, 에스더, 이런 내 생각이 너한테는 몹시 모질게 보인다는 거 나도 알아. 우리 사이에 오간 이야기를 네가 에이다에게 전하면 그 애한테도 그렇게 보이겠지. 하지만 네가 나처럼 이 사건을 파고들었다면, 내가 켄지 씨 사무소에 있을 때처럼 서류에만 매달려 봤다면, 거기에 얼마나 많은 고발과 맞고발, 의심과 맞의심이 켜켜이 쌓여 있는지 알기만 했다면, 너는 도리어 나를 점잖은 편이라고 여길 거야.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그런데 리처드, 그 많은 서류들 가운데에 진실과 정의가 많이 들어 있다고 생각하니?\"\n\n\"이 사건 어딘가에는 진실과 정의가 있어, 에스더—\"\n\n\"아니면, 오래전 한때 있었거나.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"있어, 있어, 어딘가에 분명히 있어야 해.\" 리처드가 격하게 말을 이었습니다. \"그리고 그걸 끄집어내야 해. 에이다가 뇌물이나 입막음 돈으로 쓰이게 내버려 두는 건 그걸 끄집어내는 길이 아니야. 너는 그 소송이 나를 바꾸어 놓는다고 하지. 존 잔다이스는 거기에 조금이라도 발을 담근 사람은 누구든 바꾸어 놓고, 바꾸어 놓았고, 또 바꾸어 놓을 거라고 하고. 그렇다면 그걸 끝장내려고 내가 할 수 있는 모든 걸 하기로 마음먹은 데에는 더더욱 정당한 명분이 내 편에 있는 거지.\"\n\n\"네가 할 수 있는 모든 걸, 리처드! 그 오랜 세월 동안 다른 사람들은 할 수 있는 모든 걸 하지 않았다고 생각해? 그 많은 실패 때문에 이 일이 더 쉬워지기라도 했어?\"\n\n\"이게 영원히 갈 수는 없어.\" 리처드가 다시금 나에게 그 마지막 슬픈 징후를 떠올리게 하는 사나운 기운을 그 안에 지핀 채 대답했습니다. \"나는 젊고 진지해. 그리고 열정과 결단은 숱하게 기적을 일으켜 왔어. 다른 사람들은 거기에 절반밖에 몸을 던지지 않았어. 나는 거기에 나를 바칠 거야. 그걸 내 삶의 목표로 삼을 거야.\"\n\n\"아, 리처드, 그럴수록 더 나쁜 거야, 더 나쁜 거라고!\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐, 아냐, 나 때문에 겁먹지 마.\" 그가 다정하게 대답했습니다. \"너는 사랑스럽고, 착하고, 지혜롭고, 차분하고, 복된 아가씨야. 하지만 너한테는 너만의 선입견이 있어. 그래서 다시 존 잔다이스 이야기로 돌아오는 거야. 에스더, 너한테 말하지만, 그분이 그토록 편하게 여기던 그 조건으로 그분과 내가 지내던 때, 우린 자연스러운 사이가 아니었어.\"\n\n\"분열과 적의가 너에게는 자연스러운 사이니, 리처드?\"\n\n\"아니, 그런 말이 아니야. 내 말은, 이 모든 일이 우리를 부자연스러운 사이로 몰아넣었고, 그래서는 자연스러운 관계가 양립할 수 없다는 거야. 그러니 이 일을 어서 밀어붙여야 할 또 하나의 이유가 있는 거지! 이게 다 끝나고 나면, 내가 존 잔다이스를 잘못 봤다는 걸 알게 될지도 몰라. 이 일에서 벗어나면 내 머리가 더 맑아질 수도 있고, 그러면 오늘 네가 한 말에 동의하게 될지도 모르지. 좋아. 그러면 나는 그걸 인정하고 그분에게 보상할 거야.\"\n\n모든 것이 그 상상 속의 때로 미뤄지다니! 그때까지 모든 것이 혼란과 망설임 속에 붙들려 있다니!\n\n\"자, 내 가장 좋은 속내 친구야.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는 사촌 에이다가 이렇게 이해해 줬으면 해. 내가 존 잔다이스를 두고 트집이나 잡고, 변덕스럽고, 제멋대로 구는 게 아니라, 이런 목적과 이유를 등에 업고 있다는 걸 말이야. 나는 너를 통해 그 애한테 내 모습을 전하고 싶어. 그 애는 사촌 존을 무척 존경하고 따르니까. 그리고 네가 비록 내 길을 못마땅해하더라도 그걸 부드럽게 전해 주리라는 걸 나는 아니까. 그리고, 한마디로,\" 이 말들을 더듬더듬 이어 가던 리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는, 나는 에이다처럼 사람을 믿는 아가씨에게 소송이나 일삼고, 다투기나 하고, 의심이나 하는 이런 인간으로 내 모습을 보이고 싶지가 않아.\"\n\n나는 그에게, 지금껏 한 그 어떤 말보다도 그 마지막 말들에서 그가 더 그다워 보인다고 했습니다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 리처드가 인정했습니다. \"그 말이 어지간히 맞을지도 몰라, 에스더. 나도 그런 것 같다는 생각이 들긴 해. 하지만 머지않아 나는 나 자신에게 공정한 기회를 줄 수 있을 거야. 그때 가면 다시 멀쩡해질 거야. 그러니 겁먹지 마.\"\n\n나는 그에게, 에이다에게 전해 달라는 게 이것이 전부냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"꼭 그렇지는 않아.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는 그 애에게 이것만은 숨기지 않아야 해. 존 잔다이스가 늘 하던 식으로 내 편지에 답을 보내왔다는 거 말이야. 나를 '사랑하는 리크'라고 부르면서, 내 생각을 돌려놓으려고 설득하고, 자기는 조금도 달라지지 않을 거라고 하더군.(물론 다 좋은 말이지, 하지만 사정이 달라지는 건 아니야.) 그리고 나는 에이다가 이것도 알아 줬으면 해. 내가 지금 그 애를 좀처럼 만나지 못하더라도, 나는 내 이익뿐 아니라 그 애의 이익도 살피고 있다는 거 말이야. 우리 둘은 정확히 한배를 탄 처지니까. 그리고 그 애가 무슨 떠도는 소문을 듣더라도, 내가 들뜨거나 경솔하게 군다고 짐작하지 않았으면 해. 오히려 나는 늘 소송이 끝날 날을 내다보고, 늘 그 방향으로 궁리하고 있으니까. 이제 나는 성년이 되었고 내가 내디딘 걸음을 내디뎠으니, 존 잔다이스에게 무엇 하나 해명할 책임이 없다고 여겨. 하지만 에이다는 아직 법정의 피후견인이니, 나는 아직 그 애에게 우리 약혼을 다시 맺자고는 청하지 않아. 그 애가 제 뜻대로 행동할 수 있게 되면, 나는 다시 예전의 내가 될 거고, 우리 둘 다 형편이 아주 달라져 있을 거야, 분명히. 네가 그 사려 깊은 방식으로 이 모든 걸 그 애한테 전해 준다면, 너는 나에게 더없이 크고 더없이 고마운 일을 해 주는 거야, 에스더. 그러면 나는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 더욱 힘차게 때려눕힐 수 있을 거고. 물론 황폐한 집에서는 비밀로 해 달라는 게 아니야.\"\n\n\"리처드.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"너는 나를 무척 믿어 주는구나. 하지만 너는 내 충고는 듣지 않으려 하겠지?\"\n\n\"이 문제에서만은 그럴 수가 없어, 에스더. 다른 어떤 일이라면 기꺼이 듣겠지만.\"\n\n마치 그의 삶에 다른 어떤 일이 있기라도 한 듯이! 마치 그의 인생 전부와 인품 전체가 한 가지 색으로 물들고 있지 않기라도 한 듯이!\n\n\"그래도 한 가지는 물어봐도 되지, 리처드?\"\n\n\"되고말고.\" 그가 웃으며 말했습니다. \"네가 안 된다면 누가 되겠어.\"\n\n\"너 스스로 말했잖아, 그리 안정된 생활을 하고 있지 않다고.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그럴 수 있겠어, 에스더, 정해진 게 하나도 없는데!\"\n\n\"또 빚을 졌니?\"\n\n\"당연히 졌지.\" 리처드가 내 순진함에 놀라며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그게 당연한 거니?\"\n\n\"에스더, 그럼. 비용을 들이지 않고서야 한 가지 목표에 이렇게 송두리째 몸을 던질 수가 없잖아. 잊었나 본데, 아니면 모르나 본데, 어느 쪽 유언장에 따르든 에이다와 나는 얼마간은 받게 돼. 그저 더 많은 액수냐 더 적은 액수냐의 문제일 뿐이지. 어느 쪽이든 나는 그 한도 안에 있어. 아이고, 내 착한 아가씨야.\" 리처드가 나를 보며 무척 재미있어하며 말했습니다. \"나는 괜찮을 거야! 잘 헤쳐 나갈 거라고!\"\n\n나는 그가 처한 위험이 어찌나 절실히 느껴지던지, 에이다의 이름으로, 후견인의 이름으로, 나 자신의 이름으로, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 간곡한 방법을 다 써서 그에게 그 위험을 일러 주고 그의 잘못 몇 가지를 보여 주려 애썼습니다. 그는 내가 하는 말을 모두 참을성 있게, 부드럽게 받아들였지만, 그 모든 것이 조금의 효력도 없이 그에게서 튕겨 나가 버렸습니다. 후견인의 편지조차 그 골몰한 마음이 그렇게 받아들였던 터라 이것이 놀랍지는 않았지만, 나는 그래도 에이다의 영향력을 시험해 보기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n그래서 산책길이 마을로 다시 돌아왔을 때, 나는 집으로 돌아가 아침을 먹으며 에이다에게 들려줄 이야기를 준비시켰고, 우리에게 리처드가 자신을 잃고 자기 인생 전부를 바람에 흩날려 버릴까 봐 두려워할 만한 까닭이 무엇인지 그대로 일러 주었습니다. 그것은 물론 그녀를 무척 슬프게 했습니다. 그래도 그녀는 그가 자기 잘못을 바로잡으리라는 믿음을 나로서는 도저히 가질 수 없을 만큼 굳게, 훨씬 더 굳게 품고 있었습니다. 그것이 내 사랑하는 사람다운 너무도 자연스럽고 사랑이 넘치는 모습이었습니다. 그리고 그녀는 곧 그에게 이런 짤막한 편지를 썼습니다.\n\n사랑하는 사촌에게,\n\n오늘 아침 당신이 에스더에게 한 말을 그 애가 전부 들려주었어요. 그 애가 당신에게 한 모든 말을 나 자신을 위해 더없이 간절히 되풀이하고자, 그리고 머지않아 당신이 우리 사촌 존이 진실과 성실과 선함의 본보기임을 알게 되리라고 내가 얼마나 확신하는지 알려 드리고자 이 글을 씁니다. 그때 당신은 (그럴 뜻이 없으셨건만) 그분에게 그토록 큰 잘못을 저질렀음을 깊이, 깊이 가슴 아파하게 될 거예요.\n\n다음에 하려는 말을 어떻게 써야 할지 잘 모르겠지만, 내 뜻 그대로 헤아려 주시리라 믿어요. 사랑하는 사촌, 나는 한 가지가 두려워요. 당신이 지금 스스로에게 이토록 많은 불행을 쌓고 있는 게 어쩌면 얼마쯤은 나를 위해서가 아닐까, 그래서 만약 당신 자신을 위한 것이라면 곧 나를 위한 것이기도 하지 않을까 하는 거예요. 만약 그렇다면, 혹은 당신이 그 일을 하면서 나를 마음에 많이 두고 있다면, 나는 더없이 간절히 당신에게 그만두기를 빌고 또 청합니다. 우리 둘이 함께 태어난 그 그림자에 영영 등을 돌리는 것, 그것 말고는 당신이 나를 위해 무엇을 한들 나를 그 절반만큼도 행복하게 해 줄 수 없을 거예요. 이런 말을 한다고 나에게 노여워하지 마세요. 부디, 부디, 사랑하는 리처드, 나를 위해, 또 당신 자신을 위해, 그리고 우리가 아주 어렸을 때 우리 둘을 고아로 만드는 데 한몫했던 그 괴로움의 근원을 마땅히 미워하는 마음으로, 부디, 부디, 그것을 영원히 놓아 버리세요. 우리는 이제껏 충분히 알게 되었잖아요. 거기엔 아무런 좋은 것도, 아무런 희망도 없고, 슬픔 말고는 얻을 게 아무것도 없다는 걸요.\n\n사랑하는 사촌, 당신이 전혀 매이지 않은 자유의 몸이라는 것, 그리고 당신이 첫사랑보다 훨씬 더 사랑하게 될 사람을 만날 가능성이 무척 크다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요. 그리고 이런 말을 하도록 허락해 주신다면, 나는 확신해요. 당신이 택한 그 사람은, 미루고 또 미루는 세월과 불안을 견디고 당신이 다른 목표에 무심해지는 것을 대가로 치르면서 당신과 함께 (그런 일이 가능하다 한들) 큰 부자가 되기를 바라거나 정말로 부자가 되기보다는, 아무리 변변찮고 가난하더라도 어디든 멀리까지 당신의 운명을 따르며, 당신이 행복하게, 당신의 본분을 다하며, 당신이 택한 길을 걷는 모습을 보기를 훨씬 더 바라리라는 걸요. 아는 것도 겪은 것도 이리 적은 내가 이걸 이토록 자신 있게 말하는 게 의아하실지 모르지만, 나는 내 마음으로 그것을 확실히 알아요.\n\n언제까지나, 사랑하는 사촌, 당신을 가장 깊이 사랑하는\n\n에이다\n\n이 편지에 리처드는 곧 우리에게 왔지만, 그를 바꿔 놓은 것은 있다 한들 미미했습니다. 누가 옳고 누가 그른지 공정하게 가려 보자고, 자기가 보여 주겠다고, 우리가 두고 보게 되리라고 그는 말했습니다. 그는 에이다의 다정함이 흐뭇하기라도 한 듯 생기 넘치고 환했지만, 나는 그저 한숨을 내쉬며, 그 편지가 그때 분명히 발휘한 것보다는 다시 읽을 때 그의 마음에 좀 더 강한 효력을 미치기를 바랄 따름이었습니다.\n\n그들은 그날 우리와 함께 머물다가 이튿날 아침 마차로 돌아가려고 자리를 잡아 둔 터라, 나는 스킴폴 씨와 이야기할 기회를 노렸습니다. 야외에서 지내는 생활 덕에 그런 기회는 쉽게 찾아왔고, 나는 리처드를 부추기는 데에는 책임이 따른다고 조심스레 말했습니다.\n\n\"책임이라니요, 서머슨 양?\" 그가 더없이 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 그 말을 덥석 받았습니다. \"나야말로 세상에서 그런 것과 가장 거리가 먼 사람이지요. 나는 평생 책임이라는 걸 져 본 적이 없어요. 질 수가 없거든요.\"\n\n\"누구나 책임을 져야 하지 않을까요.\" 내가 꽤 머뭇거리며 말했습니다. 그가 나보다 훨씬 나이도 많고 영리했으니까요.\n\n\"그래요, 정말로요?\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 새로운 깨달음을 더없이 흐뭇한 놀라움의 농담조로 받아들이며 말했습니다. \"하지만 누구나 빚을 다 갚아야만 하는 건 아니잖아요? 나는 안 그래요. 한 번도 그런 적이 없고요. 보세요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 주머니에서 은화와 반 페니짜리 동전을 한 줌 꺼냈습니다. \"여기 이만큼 돈이 있어요. 얼마인지는 도무지 모르겠어요. 나는 셈하는 능력이 없거든요. 사 실링 구 펜스라 칩시다. 아니, 사 파운드 구 실링이라 해도 좋고요. 사람들은 내가 그보다 더 많은 빚을 졌다고들 합디다. 그렇겠지요. 사람 좋은 이들이 지게 해 주는 만큼 빚을 졌겠지요. 그들이 멈추지 않는데 내가 왜 멈춰야 합니까? 자, 여기 해럴드 스킴폴이라는 사람이 고스란히 있습니다. 그게 책임이라면, 나도 책임을 지는 셈이지요.\"\n\n그가 더없이 편안한 태도로 돈을 도로 집어넣고는, 마치 다른 누군가에 관한 묘한 사실 한 토막을 이야기하기라도 한 듯, 그 세련된 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 나를 바라보는 통에, 나는 그가 정말로 이 일과는 아무 상관도 없는 사람처럼 느껴질 지경이었습니다.\n\n\"그런데 책임 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데요.\" 그가 다시 입을 열었습니다. \"나는 이렇게 말하고 싶군요. 내가 알고 지낸 사람 가운데 당신만큼 보기만 해도 산뜻하게 책임감이 넘치는 이를 알게 된 행복은 없었다고 말입니다. 당신은 내게 책임감의 시금석 그 자체로 보여요. 서머슨 양, 당신이 자기를 중심으로 돌아가는 그 작고 질서정연한 체계 전체가 완벽히 돌아가도록 골몰하는 모습을 보면, 나는 이렇게 혼잣말을 하고 싶어진답니다. 사실 자주 혼잣말로 그러지요. 저게 바로 책임감이다, 하고요!\"\n\n이쯤 되니 내 뜻을 설명하기란 어려웠지만, 나는 그래도, 그가 그때 리처드가 품고 있던 그 낙관적인 생각을 부추기지 말고 막아 주기를 우리 모두 바란다는 말만은 굽히지 않았습니다.\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요.\" 그가 받아쳤습니다. \"내가 할 수만 있다면요. 하지만 서머슨 양, 나에게는 잔재주도, 가식도 없어요. 그가 내 손을 잡고 운명을 좇는 경쾌한 행렬을 따라 웨스트민스터 홀을 지나가자고 하면, 나는 가야만 합니다. 그가 '스킴폴, 이 춤에 끼어들게!' 하면, 나는 끼어들어야 해요. 상식이 있는 사람이라면 안 그러겠지요, 나도 압니다. 하지만 나에게는 상식이라는 게 도무지 없거든요.\"\n\n리처드에게는 그게 참 안된 일이라고 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각해요!\" 스킴폴 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"그런 말 마세요, 그런 말 마요. 그가 상식—훌륭한 사람이지요, 주름이 잔뜩 잡히고, 끔찍이도 실용적이며, 주머니마다 십 파운드 지폐를 바꿀 잔돈을 넣고, 손에는 칸 그어진 장부를 든, 한마디로 세금 징수원을 빼닮은 그런 사람—과 함께 다닌다고 칩시다. 우리 사랑하는 리처드는 낙관적이고 열정적이며, 장애물을 훌쩍훌쩍 뛰어넘고, 갓 돋은 새싹처럼 시정이 터질 듯 넘쳐서, 이 점잖기 그지없는 길동무에게 말하지요. '내 앞에 황금빛 전망이 보여. 너무도 환하고, 너무도 아름답고, 너무도 즐거워. 자, 저걸 잡으려고 들판을 껑충껑충 뛰어 달려간다!' 그러면 그 점잖은 길동무는 대뜸 칸 그어진 장부로 그를 후려쳐 쓰러뜨리고는, 딱딱하고 멋대가리 없는 투로, 자기 눈엔 그런 게 안 보인다고 말하지요. 그건 수임료와 사기와 말총 가발과 검은 법복일 뿐이라고 보여 주고요. 자, 그건 참 괴로운 변화 아닙니까. 더없이 분별 있는 일이긴 하겠지요, 그건 의심할 바 없어요, 하지만 영 못마땅하지요. 나는 그렇게 못 합니다. 나에게는 칸 그어진 장부도 없고, 세금 징수원 같은 구석이라곤 내 안에 하나도 없으며, 나는 도무지 점잖지가 못하고, 점잖아지고 싶지도 않아요. 이상할지 몰라도, 그게 그래요!\"\n\n더 말해 봐야 부질없는 일이라, 나는 조금 앞서 걷고 있던 에이다와 리처드에게 가자고 제안하고는, 절망 속에 스킴폴 씨를 단념했습니다. 그는 그날 아침 저택을 둘러보았던 터라, 우리가 걷는 동안 익살스럽게 가족 초상화들을 묘사했습니다. 그가 들려준 바로는, 세상을 떠난 역대 데드록 마님들 가운데에는 어찌나 무시무시한 양치기 처녀들이 있는지, 그들 손에 들리면 평화로운 양치기 지팡이마저 공격용 무기가 되더라는 것이었습니다. 그들은 빳빳한 아마포 옷에 분을 바른 채 엄하게 양 떼를 치고, 다른 어떤 부족의 추장들이 전투용 칠을 하듯 평민들을 겁주려고 얼굴에 반창고 같은 애교점을 붙였더랍니다. 또 어느 데드록 경의 초상은 전투며, 폭파된 지뢰며, 자욱한 연기며, 번쩍이는 번개며, 불타는 도시며, 함락된 요새가 죄다 그의 말 두 뒷다리 사이에서 한창 벌어지고 있는데, 그가 보기엔 데드록 가문 사람이 그런 사소한 일쯤은 얼마나 대수롭잖게 여겼는지를 보여 주는 것이라고 했습니다. 그는 그 가문 전체가 살아생전에도 분명 그가 이른바 \"박제된 사람들\"이었다고 평했습니다. 유리알 같은 눈으로, 더없이 인정받는 방식으로 저마다의 잔가지와 횃대에 올려 세워진, 더없이 반듯하고, 생기라곤 조금도 없으며, 늘 유리 상자에 갇혀 있는, 한 무더기의 커다란 수집품이라고 말입니다.\n\n이제는 그 이름이 입에 오를 때마다 마음이 편치 않았던 터라, 리처드가 놀라 소리치며 우리 쪽으로 천천히 다가오는 낯선 사람을 맨 먼저 알아보고 그를 맞으러 황급히 달려갔을 때, 나는 도리어 마음이 놓였습니다.\n\n\"아니!\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"볼스 아닌가!\"\n\n우리는 그가 리처드의 친구냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"친구이자 법률 고문이지요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양, 만약 당신이 상식과 책임감과 점잖음을 한 몸에 갖춘 사람을 찾는다면, 본받을 만한 사람을 찾는다면, 볼스야말로 바로 그 사람입니다.\"\n\n우리는 리처드가 그런 이름의 신사에게 도움을 받고 있는 줄 미처 몰랐다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그가 법률상의 미성년기에서 벗어났을 때,\" 스킴폴 씨가 대답했습니다. \"우리의 말솜씨 좋은 친구 켄지와 갈라서고는, 내 알기로 볼스를 택했지요. 정말이지 그가 그랬다는 걸 나는 압니다. 내가 그를 볼스에게 소개해 줬으니까요.\"\n\n\"그분을 오래 알고 지내셨나요?\" 에이다가 물었습니다.\n\n\"볼스 말이오? 클레어 양, 나는 그와, 그쪽 직업을 가진 여러 신사들과 맺어 본 그런 종류의 친분을 맺고 있었지요. 그가 무언가를 아주 상냥하고 정중한 태도로 했는데, 절차를 밟았다, 라고 하는 게 그 표현일 겁니다. 그게 결국 나를 잡아들이는 절차로 끝났지요. 어떤 분이 친절히도 끼어들어 그 돈을 갚아 줬는데, 얼마얼마에 사 펜스가 그 액수였습니다. 파운드와 실링은 잊어버렸지만, 사 펜스로 끝났다는 건 알아요. 내가 누구에게 사 펜스를 빚질 수 있다는 게 그때 어찌나 묘하게 여겨지던지요. 그 뒤에 내가 그 둘을 맺어 준 겁니다. 볼스가 나에게 소개를 청했고, 나는 해 주었지요. 그러고 보니 말입니다,\" 그가 그 사실을 발견하고는 더없이 솔직한 미소를 띤 채 미심쩍은 눈으로 우리를 보며 말했습니다. \"볼스가 나에게 뇌물을 줬는지도 모르겠군요? 그가 나에게 무언가를 주면서 그걸 수수료라고 불렀거든요. 그게 오 파운드짜리 지폐였던가? 글쎄요, 아무래도 오 파운드짜리 지폐였던 게 분명한 것 같은데요!\"\n\n이 점을 더 곱씹어 보려던 그의 생각은, 리처드가 흥분한 채 우리에게 돌아와 황급히 볼스 씨를 소개하는 바람에 끊겼습니다. 볼스 씨는 추워서 그런 듯한 얇은 입술을 가진 누르께한 사내로, 얼굴 여기저기에 붉은 발진이 돋아 있었고, 키가 크고 마른 데다, 나이는 쉰쯤 되어 보였으며, 어깨가 솟고 등이 굽어 있었습니다. 검은 옷을 입고 검은 장갑을 끼고 턱까지 단추를 채운 그에게서, 생기 없는 태도와 리처드를 바라보는 느릿하고 고정된 그 눈길만큼 두드러진 것은 없었습니다.\n\n\"숙녀분들, 방해가 되지 않았으면 합니다.\" 볼스 씨가 말했고, 이제 나는 그가 또 한 가지, 안으로 삼키듯 말하는 화법이 두드러진다는 것을 알아챘습니다. \"나는 카스톤 씨와, 그분 사건이 대법관의 처리 목록에 오를 때마다 늘 그분이 알도록 하기로 약속해 두었습니다. 그런데 어젯밤 우편 마감 시각이 지난 뒤 서기 하나에게서 그 사건이 뜻밖에도 내일 목록에 올랐다는 보고를 받고는, 오늘 아침 일찍 마차에 몸을 싣고 그분과 의논하러 내려온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 리처드가 상기된 얼굴로 에이다와 나를 의기양양하게 바라보며 말했습니다. \"이제 우리는 예전의 그 굼뜬 방식으로 이런 일을 하지 않아. 이제 우린 속도를 낸다고! 볼스 씨, 우리는 무얼 좀 빌려서 우편 마차가 다니는 읍내까지 건너가, 오늘 밤 우편 마차를 잡아타고 올라가야겠습니다!\"\n\n\"무엇이든 분부대로 하지요, 선생.\" 볼스 씨가 대답했습니다. \"저는 전적으로 선생을 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어디 보자.\" 리처드가 시계를 보며 말했습니다. \"내가 데드록 암스로 달려 내려가서 여행 가방을 단단히 꾸리고, 이륜마차든 사륜마차든 구할 수 있는 걸 부르고 나면, 출발하기까지 한 시간쯤 여유가 있겠군. 차 마실 때 맞춰 돌아올게. 사촌 에이다, 내가 없는 동안 너랑 에스더가 볼스 씨를 잘 보살펴 주겠니?\"\n\n그는 열기와 조바심에 휩싸여 곧장 떠났고, 이내 저물어 가는 어스름 속으로 사라졌습니다. 남은 우리는 집 쪽으로 걸어갔습니다.\n\n\"내일 카스톤 씨가 꼭 가야 하나요?\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그게 무슨 도움이 될까요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아가씨.\" 볼스 씨가 대답했습니다. \"그게 도움이 되리라고는 보지 않습니다.\"\n\n그렇다면 그가 가 봐야 실망만 하게 될 텐데 가는 것이 아쉽다고, 에이다도 나도 안타까운 마음을 내비쳤습니다.\n\n\"카스톤 씨가 자기 이익은 자기가 지킨다는 원칙을 세우셨지요.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"의뢰인이 자기 원칙을 세우고, 그게 부도덕한 것이 아니라면, 그것을 실행에 옮기는 것이 제 본분입니다. 저는 일에서 정확하고 떳떳하기를 바랍니다. 저는 홀아비이고 딸이 셋—에마, 제인, 캐럴라인—있는데, 제 바람은 이 세상의 도리를 다하여 그 아이들에게 좋은 이름을 남겨 주는 것입니다. 여긴 기분 좋은 곳이로군요, 아가씨.\"\n\n걷는 동안 내가 그의 옆에 있던 까닭에 그 말이 나에게 건네진 것이라, 나는 맞장구를 치며 이곳의 주요한 볼거리들을 꼽아 주었습니다.\n\n\"그렇군요?\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"저는 토턴 골짜기에서—그분이 나고 자란 곳이지요—연로하신 아버지를 모시는 영광을 누리고 있는데, 그 고장을 무척 좋아한답니다. 이곳에 이토록 매력적인 데가 있는 줄은 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n대화를 이어 가려고, 나는 볼스 씨에게 아예 시골에서 살고 싶으냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"아가씨가 제 아픈 데를 건드리시는군요. 제 건강이 좋지 못하고(소화가 많이 상해서요), 만약 저 하나만 생각하면 되는 처지라면 시골살이에 몸을 맡길 겁니다. 더구나 일에 매인 탓에 여태껏 일반적인 사교계에, 그중에서도 제가 가장 어울리고 싶었던 숙녀분들의 모임에 좀처럼 끼어 보지 못했으니 더욱 그렇지요. 하지만 제 세 딸 에마, 제인, 캐럴라인이 있고, 또 연로하신 아버지가 계시니, 저는 이기적으로 굴 형편이 못 됩니다. 사실 백둘에 돌아가신 사랑하는 할머니를 이제 더는 모시지 않아도 되긴 하지만, 그래도 방앗간을 늘 돌려야 할 만큼은 식구가 남아 있지요.\"\n\n안으로 삼키듯 말하는 화법과 생기 없는 태도 탓에 그의 말을 알아들으려면 어지간히 신경을 써야 했습니다.\n\n\"제 딸들 이야기를 꺼낸 걸 너그러이 봐주십시오.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그 아이들이 제 약점이거든요. 그 가엾은 것들에게 좋은 이름뿐 아니라 자그마한 독립의 밑천도 남겨 주고 싶답니다.\"\n\n이윽고 우리는 보이손 씨의 집에 다다랐는데, 차상이 죄다 차려진 채 우리를 기다리고 있었습니다. 리처드가 안절부절못하며 서둘러 들어온 것은 그 직후였고, 그는 볼스 씨의 의자 위로 몸을 숙여 그의 귀에 무어라 속삭였습니다. 볼스 씨는 소리 내어—아마 그가 무엇에든 답할 때 낼 수 있는 가장 큰 소리에 가깝게—대답했습니다. \"선생이 저를 태워다 주시겠다는 겁니까? 저야 아무래도 좋습니다, 선생. 무엇이든 분부대로 하지요. 저는 전적으로 선생을 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n이어진 말로 보아, 우리는 스킴폴 씨가 이미 값을 치러 둔 두 자리를 차지하도록 아침까지 남겨 두기로 했다는 것을 알았습니다. 에이다도 나도 리처드 때문에 마음이 가라앉아 있었고 그와 그렇게 헤어지는 것이 무척 안타까웠던 터라, 우리는 정중하게나마 분명히, 스킴폴 씨는 데드록 암스에 맡겨 두고 밤길을 떠나는 이들이 가고 나면 우리는 물러나겠다는 뜻을 밝혔습니다.\n\n리처드의 들뜬 기분이 만사를 휩쓸어 버리는 통에, 우리는 모두 함께 마을 위쪽 언덕 꼭대기로 나갔습니다. 그가 이륜마차를 대기시켜 두라고 일러 둔 곳이었는데, 거기서 우리는 거기 매인 비쩍 마른 창백한 말의 머리맡에 등불을 든 사내 하나가 서 있는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n등불 빛 속에 나란히 앉은 그 두 사람을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다. 리처드는 온통 상기되고, 불타오르고, 웃음을 머금은 채 고삐를 손에 쥐고 있었고, 볼스 씨는 검은 장갑을 끼고 단추를 꼭 채운 채 미동도 없이, 마치 제 먹잇감을 바라보며 홀리기라도 하듯 그를 바라보고 있었습니다. 나는 지금도 그 풍경을 통째로 눈앞에 그릴 수 있습니다. 따뜻하고 어두운 밤, 여름 번개, 산울타리와 키 큰 나무들에 에워싸인 먼지투성이 길, 귀를 쫑긋 세운 비쩍 마른 창백한 말, 그리고 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 향해 쏜살같이 내달려 가던 그 모습을 말입니다.\n\n내 사랑하는 사람은 그날 밤 나에게 이렇게 말했습니다. 앞으로 리처드가 번창하든 파멸하든, 따르는 이가 있든 버림받든, 자기에게는 단 한 가지만 달라질 뿐이라고요. 그가 변치 않는 한 마음의 사랑을 더 필요로 할수록, 그 변치 않는 한 마음이 그에게 줄 사랑은 더욱 커지리라는 것이었습니다. 그는 지금의 잘못 속에서도 그녀를 생각해 주었으니, 그녀는 언제나 그를 생각하겠다고, 그에게 자신을 바칠 수만 있다면 결코 자기 자신은 생각하지 않겠다고, 그를 보살필 수만 있다면 결코 자기만의 기쁨은 생각하지 않겠다고 말입니다.\n\n그리고 그녀는 그 약속을 지켰을까요?\n\n나는 내 앞에 놓인 길을 따라 멀리 바라봅니다. 그 길에서 이미 거리가 좁혀지고 여정의 끝이 보이기 시작합니다. 그리고 대법원 소송이라는 죽음의 바다와 그것이 물가로 밀어낸 온갖 잿빛 열매들 위로, 참되고 선하게 떠오른 내 사랑하는 사람의 모습이 보이는 듯합니다.\n\n우리가 다시 황폐한 집으로 돌아갈 때가 되자, 우리는 정한 날짜를 어김없이 지켰고 압도적인 환영을 받았습니다. 저는 건강과 기운을 완전히 되찾았고, 제 방에 살림 열쇠가 준비되어 놓인 것을 발견하고는, 마치 제가 새로 시작되는 한 해이기라도 한 양 명랑하게 작은 종소리를 울리며 저 자신을 종처럼 울렸습니다. \"다시 한번, 의무, 의무야, 에스더.\" 저는 말했습니다. \"그리고 네가 그 일을 하면서 무슨 일이 있어도 더없이 기쁘고 만족스럽게, 더할 나위 없이 기뻐하며 하지 않는다면, 너는 마땅히 그래야 해. 내가 너에게 할 말은 그게 다란다, 얘야!\"\n\n처음 며칠 아침은 어찌나 부산하고 분주한 아침이었던지, 회계 정산이며, 으르렁 방과 집 안 곳곳을 오가는 거듭된 발걸음이며, 서랍과 장롱을 수없이 다시 정리하는 일이며, 모든 것을 새로 시작하는 전반적인 일에 매달린 나머지 저는 한순간도 한가할 틈이 없었습니다. 그러나 이 정리가 다 끝나고 모든 것이 제자리를 잡자, 저는 몇 시간 동안 런던을 다녀왔는데, 체스니 월드에서 없애버린 그 편지 속의 무언가가 제 마음속에서 그렇게 하기로 결심하게 만들었던 것입니다.\n\n저는 캐디 젤리비를—그녀의 처녀 적 이름이 저에게는 어찌나 자연스럽던지 저는 늘 그 이름으로 그녀를 불렀습니다—이 방문의 구실로 삼아, 미리 그녀에게 쪽지를 써서 작은 볼일 나들이에 동행해주는 호의를 청했습니다. 아침 아주 일찍 집을 떠나 역마차로 런던에 닿았는데, 어찌나 일찍 도착했던지 하루를 통째로 앞에 둔 채 뉴먼 거리에 이르렀습니다.\n\n결혼식 날 이후로 저를 보지 못한 캐디는 어찌나 반가워하고 어찌나 다정하던지, 저는 그녀의 남편을 질투 나게 만드는 게 아닐까 슬그머니 걱정될 지경이었습니다. 그러나 그도 제 나름으로는 꼭 그만큼이나 나빴습니다—제 말은, 그만큼이나 좋았다는 뜻입니다. 한마디로 늘 똑같은 이야기였고, 누구도 제가 무언가 칭찬받을 만한 일을 할 가능성을 남겨주지 않았습니다.\n\n알고 보니 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 침대에 누워 있었고, 캐디는 그의 초콜릿을 휘젓고 있었으며, 견습생인 우울한 어린 소년이—춤을 추는 직업에 견습으로 들어간다는 게 어찌나 묘한 일로 여겨지던지—그것을 위층으로 나르려고 기다리고 있었습니다. 시아버지는 더없이 친절하고 사려 깊으며, 그들은 더할 나위 없이 행복하게 함께 산다고 캐디가 저에게 말했습니다. (그들이 함께 산다고 말할 때, 그녀가 뜻한 바는, 그 노신사가 온갖 좋은 것과 온갖 좋은 거처를 다 차지하고, 그녀와 그녀의 남편은 얻을 수 있는 것만 가지고 마구간 위 구석방 두 칸에 처박혀 지낸다는 것이었습니다.)\n\n\"그래, 어머님은 어떻게 지내시니, 캐디?\" 제가 물었습니다.\n\n\"글쎄, 아버지를 통해 소식은 듣지, 에스더.\" 캐디가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 직접 뵙는 일은 거의 없어. 우리는 사이좋게 지내, 다행스럽게도 말이야. 그래도 엄마는 내가 무용 선생과 결혼한 게 좀 어이없다고 생각하시고, 그게 당신한테까지 옮을까 봐 꽤 두려워하셔.\"\n\n저는 이런 생각이 들었습니다. 만약 젤리비 부인이 망원경으로 지평선을 훑으며 남의 일을 찾아 나서기 전에 자기 본연의 의무와 책임부터 다했더라면, 어이없는 사람이 되는 것에 대한 최선의 예방책을 마련했으리라고 말입니다. 하지만 제가 이 생각을 마음속에만 담아두었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요.\n\n\"그럼 아버님은, 캐디?\"\n\n\"아버지는 저녁마다 여기 오셔.\" 캐디가 대답했습니다. \"저기 저 구석에 앉아 계시는 걸 어찌나 좋아하시는지, 그 모습을 보는 것만으로도 즐거워.\"\n\n구석을 바라보니, 벽에 젤리비 씨의 머리 자국이 또렷하게 박혀 있는 것이 보였습니다. 그가 머리를 기댈 그런 안식처를 찾아냈다는 것을 알게 되어 마음이 위로가 되었습니다.\n\n\"그럼 너는, 캐디.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"분명히 늘 바쁘겠지?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 얘야.\" 캐디가 대답했습니다. \"정말 그래. 너한테 굉장한 비밀 하나를 털어놓자면, 나는 직접 수업을 가르칠 자격을 갖추려고 준비하는 중이거든. 프린스의 건강이 튼튼하질 못해서, 나는 그를 도울 수 있으면 좋겠어. 학교들이며, 여기서 하는 수업이며, 개인 교습이며, 게다가 견습생들까지, 그이는 정말이지 할 일이 너무 많아, 가엾은 사람!\"\n\n견습생이라는 개념이 저에게는 여전히 너무 묘해서, 저는 캐디에게 견습생이 많으냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"넷이야.\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"한 명은 집 안에, 셋은 바깥에. 다들 아주 착한 아이들이야. 다만 함께 모이기만 하면—아이들이 다 그렇듯이—일에 집중하지 않고 놀려고만 들지. 그래서 방금 네가 본 그 어린 소년은 텅 빈 부엌에서 혼자 왈츠를 추고, 나머지는 우리가 할 수 있는 한 집 안 여기저기에 흩어놓아.\"\n\n\"물론 스텝 연습만 하려는 거겠지?\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"스텝 연습만.\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그런 식으로 그 애들은 한 번에 몇 시간씩, 그때그때 배우는 스텝이 무엇이든 연습해. 무용 교습소에서 춤을 추고, 이맘때면 우리는 매일 아침 다섯 시에 군무 동작을 연습하지.\"\n\n\"세상에, 어찌나 고된 삶인지!\" 저는 탄성을 질렀습니다.\n\n\"정말이야, 얘야.\" 캐디가 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"아침에 바깥 견습생들이 우리를 깨우려고 벨을 울리면(벨은 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 방해하지 않도록 우리 방으로 울리게 되어 있어), 내가 창문을 올리고 그 애들이 작은 무용화를 겨드랑이에 끼고 현관 계단에 서 있는 걸 보면, 정말이지 굴뚝 청소부들이 떠올라.\"\n\n이 모든 것이 분명 그 예술을 저에게 묘한 빛으로 비추어 보였습니다. 캐디는 자기 이야기가 일으킨 효과를 즐기며, 자기 공부에 관한 세세한 내용을 명랑하게 늘어놓았습니다.\n\n\"있잖아, 얘야, 비용을 아끼려면 나는 피아노도 좀 알아야 하고, 키트도 좀 알아야 해. 그래서 우리 일의 세세한 부분뿐 아니라 그 두 악기도 연습해야 하지. 엄마가 다른 사람들처럼 평범했더라면, 시작할 때 작은 음악 지식이라도 갖추고 있었을 텐데. 하지만 나는 아무것도 없었어. 그래서 그 부분이 처음에는 좀 맥이 빠진다는 걸 인정해야겠어. 그래도 나는 귀가 아주 좋고, 고된 일에는 익숙하거든—어쨌든 그건 엄마한테 고마워해야 할 일이지—그리고 너도 알다시피, 에스더, 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있는 법이잖아, 온 세상 어디서나.\" 이렇게 말하며 캐디는 웃으면서 작고 딸랑거리는 사각 피아노 앞에 앉더니, 정말로 카드리유 한 곡을 아주 활기차게 우르르 쏟아냈습니다. 그러고는 기분 좋게 얼굴을 붉히며 다시 일어나, 여전히 스스로 웃으면서 말했습니다. \"제발 나를 비웃지 마, 착하지!\"\n\n저는 차라리 울고 싶었지만, 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않았습니다. 저는 진심을 다해 그녀를 격려하고 칭찬했습니다. 그녀가 무용 선생의 아내이고, 또 소박한 야심 속에서 무용 여선생이 되기를 꿈꾼다 하더라도, 그녀는 자연스럽고 건전하며 사랑이 깃든 근면과 끈기의 길을 스스로 개척해냈으며, 그것이 어떤 사명 못지않게 훌륭하다고 저는 양심에 비추어 믿었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"얘야.\" 캐디가 기뻐하며 말했습니다. \"네가 나를 얼마나 북돋아주는지 너는 모를 거야. 너한테 얼마나 신세를 지는지, 너는 짐작도 못 할걸. 어쩜 이렇게 변했을까, 에스더, 내 작은 세상 안에서조차도 말이야! 그 첫날 밤, 내가 그렇게 무례하고 잉크투성이였던 거 기억하지? 그때 누가 생각이나 했겠어, 다른 온갖 가능한 일이며 불가능한 일을 다 제쳐두고 하필이면 내가 사람들에게 춤을 가르치게 될 줄을!\"\n\n우리가 이렇게 담소를 나누는 동안 자리를 떴던 그녀의 남편이, 무도실에서 견습생들을 가르칠 준비를 하러 이제 막 돌아오자, 캐디는 자기가 온전히 제 뜻에 따르겠다고 알렸습니다. 그러나 아직 제 시간은 아니었기에, 저는 그녀에게 그렇게 말할 수 있어 다행이었습니다. 지금 그녀를 데려간다면 마음이 편치 않았을 테니까요. 그래서 우리 셋은 함께 견습생들에게로 옮겨 갔고, 저도 춤에 한몫 끼었습니다.\n\n견습생들은 더없이 기묘한 꼬마들이었습니다. 그 우울한 소년 말고도—텅 빈 부엌에서 혼자 왈츠를 추다가 그렇게 된 건 아니기를 저는 바랐습니다—다른 두 소년과, 얇은 망사 드레스를 입은 지저분하고 축 늘어진 어린 소녀가 하나 있었습니다. 어찌나 조숙한 어린 소녀였던지, 어찌나 촌스러운 보닛을(그것 역시 얇은 망사 천이었습니다) 쓰고 있었던지, 게다가 닳아빠진 낡은 벨벳 손가방에 샌들 신발을 넣어 들고 다녔습니다. 어찌나 보잘것없는 어린 소년들이었던지, 춤을 추지 않을 때는 주머니에 끈이며, 구슬이며, 공기놀이 뼈를 넣어두고, 다리와 발이—특히 발뒤꿈치가—더없이 지저분했습니다.\n\n저는 캐디에게 무엇 때문에 그 아이들의 부모가 이 직업을 골라주었는지 물었습니다. 캐디는 모르겠다고 했습니다. 어쩌면 선생이 되게 하려는 것일 수도, 어쩌면 무대에 세우려는 것일 수도 있다고요. 다들 형편이 변변치 못한 사람들이었고, 그 우울한 소년의 어머니는 생강맥주 가게를 운영했습니다.\n\n우리는 한 시간 동안 자못 엄숙하게 춤을 추었는데, 그 우울한 아이는 제 하반신으로 놀라운 솜씨를 부렸고, 거기에는 어떤 즐거움의 감각이 깃든 듯했으나 그 즐거움이 결코 허리 위로는 올라오지 않았습니다. 캐디는 남편을 눈여겨 살피며 분명히 그를 본보기 삼고 있었지만, 그러면서도 자기만의 우아함과 침착함을 익혀냈고, 그것이 그녀의 예쁜 얼굴과 자태와 어우러져 보기 드물게 흐뭇했습니다. 그녀는 이미 이 어린 사람들을 가르치는 일의 상당 부분을 남편 대신 맡고 있었고, 남편은 군무에서 자기가 할 일이 있을 때 자기 자리를 거니는 것 말고는 좀처럼 끼어들지 않았습니다. 곡은 늘 그가 연주했습니다. 망사 옷을 입은 아이의 거드름과, 소년들을 깔보는 태도란 정말 볼만했습니다. 그렇게 우리는 시계로 한 시간 동안 춤을 추었습니다.\n\n연습이 끝나자, 캐디의 남편은 어느 학교로 나갈 채비를 했고, 캐디는 저와 함께 나갈 준비를 하러 뛰어갔습니다. 저는 그동안 무도실에 앉아 견습생들을 바라보았습니다. 바깥의 두 소년은 계단으로 가서 반장화를 신고는, 그 항의의 성격으로 미루어 짐작하건대 집 안 소년의 머리카락을 잡아당겼습니다. 재킷 단추를 채우고 무용화를 그 안에 찔러 넣은 채 돌아온 그들은, 차가운 빵과 고기 꾸러미를 꺼내 벽에 그려진 리라 그림 아래에 진을 쳤습니다. 망사 옷을 입은 어린 소녀는 샌들을 후딱 손가방 안에 던져 넣고 뒤축이 닳은 신발 한 켤레를 신더니, 머리를 한 번 흔들어 촌스러운 보닛 속으로 집어넣고는, 춤추는 걸 좋아하느냐는 제 물음에 \"남자애들이랑은 싫어요\"라고 대답하면서 보닛을 턱 아래로 매고, 멸시하는 태도로 집에 갔습니다.\n\n\"늙은 터비드롭 씨가 무척 안타까워하셔.\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"아직 옷 단장을 다 못 마치셔서, 네가 가기 전에 뵙는 기쁨을 누리지 못하신다고 말이야. 너는 그분이 그렇게 아끼는 사람이거든, 에스더.\"\n\n저는 그분께 무척 감사하다는 뜻을 표했지만, 이런 배려쯤은 기꺼이 없어도 된다는 말까지 덧붙일 필요는 없다고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"그분은 옷 단장에 오래 걸려.\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"왜냐하면, 너도 알다시피, 그런 일에서는 사람들이 그분을 무척 우러러보고, 그분도 지켜야 할 명성이 있으니까. 그분이 아버지한테 얼마나 친절하신지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야. 저녁이면 아버지한테 섭정 왕세자 이야기를 들려주시는데, 나는 아버지가 그렇게 흥미로워하시는 걸 본 적이 없어.\"\n\n터비드롭 씨가 젤리비 씨에게 자신의 몸가짐을 베푸는 그림에는, 제 마음을 사로잡는 무언가가 있었습니다. 저는 캐디에게 그분이 아버지를 자주 데리고 나가시느냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"아니.\" 캐디가 말했습니다. \"그러시는 것 같지는 않아. 하지만 아버지한테 이야기를 들려주시고, 아버지는 그분을 무척 우러러보며 귀 기울여 들으시고, 또 그걸 좋아하셔. 물론 아버지가 몸가짐에 대해선 거의 자격이 없다는 건 나도 알지만, 두 분은 즐겁게 잘 어울리셔. 두 분이 얼마나 좋은 길동무가 되시는지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야. 나는 평생 아버지가 코담배 피우시는 걸 본 적이 없는데, 이젠 터비드롭 씨의 담뱃갑에서 규칙적으로 한 줌씩 집어 들고는, 저녁 내내 코에 갖다 댔다 뗐다 하셔.\"\n\n늙은 터비드롭 씨가 인생의 온갖 우연과 변천 속에서 보리오불라가에서 온 젤리비 씨를 구해주기에 이르렀다는 것은, 저에게는 더없이 유쾌한 별난 일 중 하나로 여겨졌습니다.\n\n\"피피로 말하자면,\" 캐디가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했습니다. \"내가 제일 걱정했던 게—내 자식을 두는 것 다음으로 말이야, 에스더—그 애가 터비드롭 씨한테 폐가 될까 하는 거였는데, 그 노신사가 그 아이한테 베푸시는 친절은 이루 말할 수 없을 정도야. 그분이 그 애를 보고 싶다고 청하셔, 얘야! 그 애한테 침대로 신문을 가져오게 하시고, 토스트 가장자리 빵 조각을 떼어 먹게 주시고, 집 안 잔심부름을 시키시고, 6펜스를 받으려면 나한테 오라고 일러주셔. 한마디로,\" 캐디가 명랑하게 말했습니다. \"장황하게 굴지 않고 말하자면, 나는 아주 운 좋은 여자고, 무척 고마워해야 마땅하지. 우리 어디로 가는 거야, 에스더?\"\n\n\"올드 스트리트 로드로.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"거기 사는 변호사 사무원에게 몇 마디 할 말이 있어서. 바로 내가 런던에 와서 처음 너를 만나던 날, 마차 사무소로 나를 마중 나왔던 그 사람 말이야. 지금 생각해 보니, 우리를 너희 집까지 데려다준 그 신사였지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 정말이지, 내가 너랑 같이 가는 게 자연스러운 사람인 셈이네.\" 캐디가 대답했습니다.\n\n우리는 올드 스트리트 로드로 가서, 거기 거피 부인의 거처에서 거피 부인을 찾았습니다. 거피 부인은 응접실들을 차지하고 있었는데, 사실 누가 자기를 찾기도 전에 앞쪽 응접실 문틈으로 내다보다가 호두처럼 제 몸을 깨뜨릴 뻔할 정도였고, 곧장 모습을 드러내며 안으로 들어오라고 청했습니다. 그녀는 커다란 모자를 쓴 노부인으로, 코가 꽤 붉고 눈빛이 꽤 불안정했으나, 온통 웃음을 띠고 있었습니다. 그녀의 비좁은 작은 거실은 손님을 맞이할 채비가 되어 있었고, 그 안에는 그녀 아들의 초상화가 한 점 걸려 있었는데, 저는 하마터면 여기에 이렇게 적을 뻔했습니다. 그것이 실물보다 더 닮았다고 말입니다. 어찌나 고집스럽게 그를 우겨댔던지, 그를 결코 놓아주지 않겠다는 듯 단단히 작정한 모습이었습니다.\n\n초상화만 거기 있는 게 아니라, 그 실물도 거기 있었습니다. 그는 갖가지 색깔로 차려입은 채, 탁자 앞에서 집게손가락을 이마에 댄 채 법률 서류를 읽고 있는 것이 눈에 띄었습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 거피 씨가 일어서며 말했습니다. \"이거야말로 정말 오아시스로군요. 어머니, 미안하지만 이쪽 숙녀분께 의자를 놓아드리고 통로에서 좀 비켜주시겠어요.\"\n\n거피 부인은, 끊임없이 짓는 미소 탓에 자못 익살스러운 풍모를 띠고 있었는데, 아들이 청한 대로 하고는 구석에 앉아, 마치 찜질이라도 하듯 두 손으로 주머니 손수건을 가슴에 꼭 댔습니다.\n\n저는 캐디를 소개했고, 거피 씨는 제 친구라면 누구든 더없이 환영한다고 말했습니다. 그러고 나서 저는 방문의 목적으로 들어갔습니다.\n\n\"제가 외람되게 쪽지를 보내드렸지요, 선생님.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n거피 씨는 가슴 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내어 입술에 갖다 댄 다음, 절을 하며 다시 주머니에 넣어둠으로써 그것을 받았음을 알렸습니다. 거피 씨의 어머니는 어찌나 재미있어하던지, 미소 지으며 고개를 이리저리 굴리고는 팔꿈치로 캐디에게 말없이 호소했습니다.\n\n\"잠깐 단둘이서 말씀 좀 나눌 수 있을까요?\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n방금 거피 씨 어머니의 그 익살스러움 같은 것을, 저는 평생 본 적이 없는 것 같습니다. 그녀는 웃음소리는 내지 않았지만, 고개를 굴리고, 흔들고, 손수건을 입에 갖다 대고, 팔꿈치며 손이며 어깨로 캐디에게 호소했고, 도무지 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 재미있어한 나머지, 옆에 붙은 자기 침실로 통하는 작은 접이문 안으로 캐디를 데려가는 데 적잖이 애를 먹었습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"아들의 행복을 늘 마음에 두는 어버이의 변덕을 양해해 주십시오. 제 어머니는 비록 감정적으로는 무척 사람을 화나게 하지만, 모성의 명령에 따라 움직이는 것입니다.\"\n\n제가 이제 베일을 들어 올리자, 거피 씨가 보인 만큼 한순간에 그토록 붉어지거나 그토록 변할 수 있는 사람이 있으리라고는, 저는 도저히 믿기 어려웠습니다.\n\n\"제가 켄지 씨 사무실로 찾아가는 대신 여기서 잠깐 뵙는 호의를 청한 것은,\" 제가 말했습니다. \"전에 선생님께서 저에게 비밀을 털어놓으며 말씀하셨던 그 일을 떠올리니, 그러지 않으면 제가 선생님께 어떤 곤란을 끼칠지도 모른다는 염려가 들었기 때문입니다, 거피 씨.\"\n\n이미 그것만으로도 제가 그분께 충분히 곤란을 끼쳤다는 것은 분명합니다. 저는 그토록 더듬거리고, 그토록 당황하고, 그토록 놀라고 두려워하는 모습을 본 적이 없습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 거피 씨가 말을 더듬었습니다. \"저—저—실례합니다만, 우리 직업에서는—우리—우리는—명확히 말하는 것이 필요하다고 봅니다. 양께서는 어떤 일을 언급하셨는데, 그때 제가—제가 영광스럽게도 어떤 선언을 했던—\"\n\n무언가가 그의 목구멍에서 치밀어 올라 도무지 삼킬 수 없는 듯했습니다. 그는 거기에 손을 갖다 대고, 기침을 하고, 얼굴을 찡그리고, 다시 그것을 삼켜보려 하고, 또 기침을 하고, 또 얼굴을 찡그리고, 방 안을 빙 둘러보고, 자기 서류들을 팔랑거렸습니다.\n\n\"일종의 어지러운 느낌이 저를 덮쳐서요, 양.\" 그가 설명했습니다. \"그게 저를 좀 쓰러뜨립니다. 저는—어—이런 종류의 일에 좀 약해서요—어—맙소사!\"\n\n저는 그가 추스를 시간을 잠시 주었습니다. 그는 그 시간을 손을 이마에 댔다가 다시 떼는 것과, 자기 의자를 뒤쪽 구석으로 물리는 데 다 써버렸습니다.\n\n\"제가 말하려던 것은요, 양.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"이런—기관지 쪽인 것 같습니다—에헴!—그러니까 그때 양께서 친절하게도 그 선언을 물리치고 거부하셨다는 것 말입니다. 양께서—양께서 혹시 그것을 인정하시는 데 반대하지는 않으시겠지요? 증인이 한 사람도 없긴 하지만, 그 인정을 해두신다면—양의—양의 마음에—흡족한 일이 될지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"저는 아무런 유보나 단서도 없이 선생님의 청혼을 거절했지요, 거피 씨.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 양.\" 그가 어쩔 줄 모르는 두 손으로 탁자를 재며 대답했습니다. \"거기까지는 흡족하고, 양께 명예가 되는 일입니다. 어—이건 확실히 기관지예요!—틀림없이 기관에 있는 거예요—어—혹시 제가 한 가지 말씀드린다 해도 기분 나빠하지는 않으시겠지요—꼭 필요해서가 아니라, 양 본인의 분별이나 어느 누구의 분별로 보더라도 그건 자명할 테니까요—제 쪽에서 한 그 선언이 최종적인 것이었고, 거기서 끝났다는 것을 말씀드린다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"혹시—어—그런 형식까지 갖출 가치는 없을지 모르지만, 양의 마음에 흡족한 일이 될지도 모르니까요—혹시 그것을 인정하시는 데 반대하지는 않으시겠지요, 양?\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"더없이 충분히, 또 거리낌 없이 인정합니다.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 거피 씨가 대답했습니다. \"정말 매우 명예로운 일입니다. 제 인생의 처지가, 제가 어쩔 수 없는 여러 사정과 맞물려서, 그 청혼으로 다시 돌아가거나 그것을 어떤 모습이나 형태로든 새로이 하는 것을 영영 제 능력 밖의 일로 만들어버린 것이 유감스럽습니다만, 그것은 언제까지나—어—우정의 정자(亭子)와 얽힌 추억으로 남을 것입니다.\" 거피 씨의 기관지염이 그를 구원하러 와서, 그가 탁자를 재는 것을 멈추게 했습니다.\n\n\"이제 제가 선생님께 말씀드리고 싶었던 것을 말씀드려도 될까요?\" 제가 입을 열었습니다.\n\n\"영광이지요, 정말로.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"양 본인의 훌륭한 분별과 올바른 감정이—양께서 되도록 공정하게 처신하시도록 지켜주리라고 저는 굳게 믿고 있으니—양께서 어떤 말씀을 하시든 그것을 듣는 것이 저에게는 기쁨일 따름입니다, 정말로.\"\n\n\"선생님께서는 그때 친절하게도 이런 뜻을 비치셨지요—\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 양.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 우리는 기록을 벗어나 암시로 흘러가지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 제가 무언가를 암시했다는 것은 인정할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"선생님께서는 그때 말씀하셨지요,\" 제가 다시 시작했습니다. \"저를 대상으로 어떤 사실들을 밝혀냄으로써, 제 이익을 증진하고 제 처지를 나아지게 할 방도를 어쩌면 갖고 계실지도 모른다고요. 짐작건대 선생님께서는 제가 고아 처녀이며, 모든 것을 잔다이스 씨의 선의에 빚지고 있다는 것을 두루 아셨기에 그런 믿음을 품으셨겠지요. 그런데, 제가 선생님께 청하러 온 일의 처음이자 끝은, 거피 씨, 부디 친절을 베푸셔서 그런 식으로 저를 도우시려는 생각을 깨끗이 단념해 주십사 하는 것입니다. 저는 이따금 이 일을 생각해 왔고, 가장 최근에—제가 앓고 난 뒤로—이 일을 생각했습니다. 마침내 저는, 선생님께서 언제고 그 목적을 다시 떠올리시어 어떤 식으로든 실행에 옮기실 경우를 대비해, 선생님을 찾아뵙고 선생님께서 완전히 잘못 짚으셨음을 분명히 말씀드리기로 결심했습니다. 선생님께서는 저와 관련하여, 저에게 조금이라도 도움이 되거나 조금이라도 기쁨을 줄 만한 사실을 결코 밝혀내실 수 없습니다. 저는 제 개인사를 알고 있으며, 선생님께서 그런 수단으로는 결코 제 안녕을 증진하실 수 없다는 것을 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 어쩌면 선생님께서는 이 계획을 오래전에 접으셨을지도 모릅니다. 만일 그렇다면, 공연한 수고를 끼친 것을 용서해 주십시오. 그렇지 않다면, 제가 드린 이 다짐을 믿고, 이제부터는 그 생각을 내려놓아 주십사 간청드립니다. 부디 그렇게 해주시기를 청합니다, 제 마음의 평안을 위해서요.\"\n\n\"고백하지 않을 수 없군요.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"양께서는, 제가 양께 인정해 드렸던 바로 그 훌륭한 분별과 올바른 감정으로 자신을 표현하고 계십니다. 그런 올바른 감정만큼 흡족한 것은 없지요. 그리고 방금 제가 양 쪽의 어떤 의도를 잘못 짚었다면, 저는 충분히 사과를 드릴 준비가 되어 있습니다. 양, 저는 이로써 그 사과를 드리는 것으로 이해되기를 바랍니다—다만, 양 본인의 훌륭한 분별과 올바른 감정이 그 필요성을 짚어주시는 대로, 그것을 지금의 이 일에 한정하면서 말입니다.\"\n\n거피 씨를 위해 한마디 해두어야겠는데, 그가 띠고 있던 그 코맹맹이 같은 태도가 무척 나아졌다는 것입니다. 그는 제가 청한 무언가를 할 수 있게 된 것을 진심으로 기뻐하는 듯했고, 부끄러워하는 기색이었습니다.\n\n\"제가 다시 말을 꺼낼 일이 없도록, 제가 할 말을 지금 한꺼번에 마치게 허락해 주신다면,\" 그가 막 말을 하려는 것을 보고 저는 말을 이었습니다. \"선생님은 저에게 친절을 베푸시는 셈이 됩니다, 선생님. 제가 되도록 은밀하게 선생님을 찾아온 것은, 선생님께서 이 생각을 저에게 알리실 때 비밀로 하셨고, 저는 그 비밀을 진정으로 존중하고 싶었기 때문입니다—그리고 기억하시겠지만, 저는 늘 그것을 존중해 왔습니다. 제가 앓았던 일을 말씀드렸지요. 선생님께 어떤 청을 드리는 데 제가 느꼈을 작은 거리낌이 이제 완전히 사라졌다는 것을 제가 아주 잘 안다고 말하는 데, 사실 망설일 이유가 전혀 없습니다. 그래서 저는 방금 드린 이 간청을 드리는 것이고, 부디 선생님께서 저를 충분히 배려하시어 이를 들어주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n거피 씨에게 한 가지 더 공정하게 말해두어야겠는데, 그가 점점 더 부끄러워하는 기색이었고, 이제 발갛게 달아오른 얼굴로 대답할 때는 더없이 부끄러워하면서도 무척 진지했다는 것입니다. \"맹세코, 명예를 걸고, 제 목숨을 걸고, 제 영혼을 걸고, 서머슨 양, 제가 살아 있는 사람인 한, 저는 양의 뜻에 따라 행동하겠습니다! 그것에 반하는 짓은 한 발짝도 다시 하지 않겠습니다. 양의 마음에 조금이라도 흡족한 일이 된다면, 그것을 두고 맹세라도 하겠습니다. 지금 이 자리에서, 지금 문제가 되고 있는 사안과 관련하여 제가 약속하는 바는,\" 거피 씨가, 마치 익숙한 정형구를 외기라도 하듯 빠르게 이어 말했습니다. \"진실, 오로지 진실, 진실 외에는 아무것도 아닌 것을 말한다는 것이니, 그러므로—\"\n\n\"아주 흡족합니다.\" 이 대목에서 일어서며 제가 말했습니다. \"정말 감사합니다. 캐디, 얘야, 나는 준비됐어!\"\n\n거피 씨의 어머니가 캐디와 함께 돌아왔는데(이제는 자기의 말없는 웃음과 팔꿈치질의 대상으로 저를 삼았습니다), 우리는 작별을 고했습니다. 거피 씨는 잠이 덜 깼거나 잠결에 걷는 사람 같은 모습으로 우리를 문까지 배웅했고, 우리는 멍하니 바라보는 그를 거기 남겨둔 채 떠났습니다.\n\n그러나 잠시 후 그는 모자도 쓰지 않고, 긴 머리카락을 온통 바람에 휘날리며 거리로 우리를 쫓아 내려와, 우리를 멈춰 세우고는 간절하게 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양, 명예와 영혼을 걸고, 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다!\"\n\n\"믿습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"아주 굳게요.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 양.\" 한 다리는 떼고 다른 다리는 멈춘 채로 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 이 숙녀분이 여기 계시니—양 본인의 증인이니—혹시 그 인정의 말을 다시 한번 해주신다면 양의 마음에(제가 안심시켜 드리고 싶은 그 마음에) 흡족한 일이 될지도 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 캐디.\" 그녀를 돌아보며 제가 말했습니다. \"얘야, 내가 이렇게 말하더라도 너는 놀라지 않을 거야. 여태껏 그 어떤 약혼도 없었다고—\"\n\n\"그 어떤 청혼이나 결혼 약속도 일절 없었다고요.\" 거피 씨가 거들었습니다.\n\n\"그 어떤 청혼이나 결혼 약속도 일절 없었다고.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"이 신사분과—\"\n\n\"미들섹스 주 펜턴빌, 펜턴 플레이스에 사는 윌리엄 거피와요.\" 그가 중얼거렸습니다.\n\n\"이 신사분, 미들섹스 주 펜턴빌, 펜턴 플레이스에 사는 윌리엄 거피 씨와 저 사이에 말이야.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 양.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"아주 빠짐없군요—어—실례합니다만—이 숙녀분 성함은, 이름과 성을 다 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n저는 그것을 말해주었습니다.\n\"기혼 여성이시지요?\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"기혼 여성. 고맙습니다. 예전에는 미혼인 캐럴라인 젤리비, 당시 런던 시 안에 있되 어느 교구에도 속하지 않는 데이비스 인에 거주, 현재는 옥스퍼드 거리의 뉴먼 거리에 거주. 대단히 감사합니다.\"\n\n그는 집으로 뛰어갔다가 다시 뛰어 돌아왔습니다.\n\n\"그 일로 말하자면, 아시다시피, 제 인생의 처지가, 제가 어쩔 수 없는 여러 사정과 맞물려서, 얼마 전에 완전히 끝나버린 그 일을 새로이 하는 것을 막아버리는 것이 정말이지 진심으로 무척 유감스럽습니다.\" 거피 씨가 저에게 처량하고 풀 죽은 어조로 말했습니다. \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 정말 어쩔 수가 없었지요, 아시잖습니까! 그저 양께 여쭙는 겁니다.\"\n\n저는 확실히 어쩔 수 없었다고 대답했습니다. 그 문제는 의심의 여지가 없었으니까요. 그는 저에게 고맙다고 하고는 다시 어머니에게로 뛰어갔다가—또다시 뛰어 돌아왔습니다.\n\n\"정말 매우 명예로운 일입니다, 양.\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"우정의 정자 안에 제단을 세울 수 있다면—하지만, 맹세코, 그 애틋한 연정 하나만 빼고는 모든 면에서 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다!\"\n\n거피 씨의 가슴속에서 벌어지는 갈등과, 그 때문에 그가 어머니의 문과 우리 사이를 수없이 오가는 그 진동은, 바람 부는 거리에서(특히 그의 머리카락이 깎을 때가 되었던 만큼) 충분히 눈에 띄어서, 우리를 서둘러 떠나게 만들었습니다. 저는 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 그렇게 했습니다. 그러나 우리가 마지막으로 뒤돌아보았을 때, 거피 씨는 여전히 똑같이 심란한 마음 상태로 진동하고 있었습니다.\n\n볼스 씨의 이름은 그 위에 \"일층\"이라는 표시를 달고서 챈서리 레인의 시먼즈 인에 있는 어느 문기둥에 새겨져 있다. 작고, 핏기 없고, 사팔뜨기에, 비탄에 잠긴 듯한 그 법학원은, 칸막이 둘에 체 하나를 갖춘 커다란 쓰레기통 같다. 마치 시먼드라는 사람이 제 나름으로 인색해서, 마른 썩음병과 먼지, 그리고 썩어가고 음울한 온갖 것들과 죽이 잘 맞는 낡은 건축 자재로 이 법학원을 지어, 어울리는 누추함으로 시먼드의 기억을 영원토록 남긴 듯하다. 시먼드를 기리는 이 칙칙한 문장(紋章) 안에, 볼스 씨의 법조계 문양이 한 칸 차지하고 있다. * 문장(紋章): 본디 가문의 표지를 새긴 장식으로, 여기서는 음울한 건물 자체를 빗댄 표현이다.\n\n볼스 씨의 사무실은 기질로 보나 위치로 보나 외진 곳으로, 한쪽 구석에 처박혀 막다른 벽을 멀거니 바라본다. 옹이투성이 바닥에 어두운 통로 삼 피트를 지나면 의뢰인은 볼스 씨의 칠흑 같은 문에 이르는데, 그 모퉁이는 한여름 가장 환한 아침에도 깊디깊은 어둠에 잠겨 있고, 지하실 계단의 시커먼 칸막이벽이 가로막고 있어 늑장 부린 민사 변호사들이 으레 거기에 이마를 부딪친다. 볼스 씨의 사무실은 규모가 어찌나 작은지, 서기 하나가 걸상에서 일어나지도 않고 문을 열 수 있고, 같은 책상에서 그와 팔꿈치를 부딪치는 다른 서기는 그만큼 손쉽게 불을 쑤석거릴 수 있다. 곰팡내와 먼지내에 뒤섞인, 어딘가 건강에 해로운 양고기 같은 냄새가 풍기는 것은, 밤마다(흔히는 낮에도) 양 기름으로 만든 양초를 태운 탓이요, 기름때 묻은 서랍 속에서 양피지 서식과 가죽이 부스러지는 탓이다. 그 밖의 공기는 케케묵고 답답하다. 이곳에 마지막으로 페인트칠이나 회칠을 한 것은 사람들 기억 너머의 일이고, 굴뚝 두 개는 연기를 내뿜으며, 사방에는 그을음이 헐겁게 표면을 덮고 있고, 묵직한 틀에 끼인 흐리고 금 간 창문들에는 단 하나의 개성이 있으니, 곧 강제로 시키지 않는 한 늘 더럽고 늘 닫혀 있겠다는 결의다. 그래서 둘 중 더 약한 창문이 무더운 날씨에도 그 아가리에 장작 한 묶음을 물고 있는 진풍경이 벌어지는 것이다.\n\n볼스 씨는 매우 존경할 만한 사람이다. 사업 규모는 크지 않지만, 그래도 매우 존경할 만한 사람이다. 큰 재산을 모았거나 모으는 중인 큰 변호사들도 그를 더없이 존경할 만한 사람으로 인정한다. 그는 업무에서 단 한 번의 기회도 놓치는 법이 없는데, 이것이 존경받을 만한 표시다. 그는 어떤 즐거움도 누리지 않는데, 이것이 또 하나의 존경받을 만한 표시다. 그는 과묵하고 진지한데, 이것이 또 하나의 존경받을 만한 표시다. 소화 기능이 망가져 있는데, 이것이 대단히 존경받을 만하다. 그리고 그는 풀, 곧 살덩이가 마르기 전에 베어 거두느라 여념이 없으니, 세 딸을 위해서다. 게다가 그의 아버지는 톤턴 골짜기에서 그에게 기대어 산다. * 풀, 곧 살덩이: 성경 이사야서의 \"모든 육체는 풀이요\"를 빗댄 표현으로, 의뢰인을 풀이자 살덩이로 보고 거두어들인다는 풍자다.\n\n영국 법의 단 하나 위대한 원리는, 제 자신을 위해 일거리를 만드는 것이다. 그 비좁은 모퉁이들을 하나도 빠짐없이 관통하여 또렷하고 확실하게 한결같이 유지되는 다른 원리란 없다. 이 빛에 비추어 보면 법은 일관된 설계가 되니, 보통 사람들이 으레 여기듯 괴물 같은 미로가 아니다. 보통 사람들이 단 한 번만이라도, 법의 그 웅대한 원리란 곧 자기들의 비용으로 법 제 자신의 일거리를 만드는 것임을 똑똑히 깨닫기만 한다면, 분명 투덜대기를 그치리라.\n\n그러나 이것을 아주 또렷이 깨닫지 못한 채—혼란스럽게 절반쯤만 보고 있는 탓에—보통 사람들은 때로 마음 편치 않게 평온과 주머니를 동시에 축내며, 사실 무척 투덜댄다. 그러면 볼스 씨의 이 존경받을 만함이 그들을 향해 강력하게 발휘된다. \"이 법령을 폐지하라고요, 선생?\" 켄지 씨가 속 쓰린 의뢰인에게 말한다. \"그걸 폐지하라고요, 선생? 절대로요, 내 동의로는 안 됩니다. 이 법을 고쳐 보시오, 선생, 그러면 선생의 그 경솔한 처사가, 이 사건에서 상대편 변호사인 볼스 씨가 더없이 훌륭히 대표하는—이렇게 말씀드려도 될지 모르나—한 부류의 법조 실무가들에게 어떤 영향을 끼치겠습니까? 선생, 그 부류의 실무가들은 지상에서 싹 쓸려 사라질 겁니다. 자, 선생은 볼스 씨 같은 인물의 부류를 잃을 여유가 없습니다—아니, 우리 사회 제도가 그럴 여유가 없다고 말씀드리겠습니다. 부지런하고, 끈기 있고, 한결같고, 일에 빈틈없는 사람들 말입니다. 선생, 현 상태에 대한 지금의 심정을 이해합니다, 선생의 경우엔 조금 가혹한 면이 있다는 것도 인정하지요. 하지만 나는 볼스 씨 같은 부류의 사람들을 무너뜨리자고 결코 목소리를 높일 수 없습니다.\" 볼스 씨의 존경받을 만함은 의회 위원회 앞에서도 압도적인 효과를 내며 인용된 바 있으니, 어느 저명한 변호사의 증언을 담은 다음의 청서(靑書) 회의록이 그것이다. \"질문(제51만 7869번): 제가 이해하기로, 이 실무 절차들이 의문의 여지 없이 지연을 일으킵니까? 답변: 그렇습니다, 다소 지연이 있지요. 질문: 그리고 막대한 비용도? 답변: 분명히 공짜로는 거칠 수 없는 일입니다. 질문: 그리고 말로 다 못 할 골칫거리도? 답변: 그건 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 제게는 한 번도 골칫거리가 된 적이 없으니까요. 오히려 정반대지요. 질문: 그러나 그 절차들을 폐지하면 한 부류의 실무가들이 타격을 입으리라고 보십니까? 답변: 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 질문: 그 부류의 본보기를 하나 들어 주시겠습니까? 답변: 그러지요. 주저 없이 볼스 씨를 들겠습니다. 그는 파산할 겁니다. 질문: 볼스 씨는 업계에서 존경받을 만한 사람으로 여겨집니까? 답변:\"—이것이 십 년 동안 그 조사를 결딴낸 한마디였다—\"볼스 씨는 업계에서 더없이 존경받을 만한 사람으로 여겨집니다.\" * 청서: 영국 의회나 추밀원이 펴낸 공식 보고서로, 표지가 푸른빛이라 그렇게 불렀다.\n\n이리하여 격의 없는 잡담 속에서도, 그에 못지않게 사심 없는 사사로운 권위자들이 이렇게 말하곤 한다. 요즘 세상이 어찌 되려는지 모르겠다, 우리는 벼랑 아래로 곤두박질치고 있다, 자 보라 이번에 또 무엇이 사라졌다, 이런 변화는 볼스 같은 이들에게 죽음이나 다름없다—톤턴 골짜기에 아버지를 두고 집에 세 딸을 둔, 의심할 바 없이 존경받을 만한 사람에게 말이다. 이 방향으로 몇 발짝만 더 나아가 보라, 그러면 볼스의 아버지는 어찌 되겠는가? 굶어 죽으란 말인가? 그리고 볼스의 딸들은? 셔츠 짓는 여공이나 가정교사가 되란 말인가? 마치 볼스 씨와 그의 친족이 식인종 소추장들인데 식인 풍습을 폐지하자는 제안이 나오자, 분개한 옹호자들이 이렇게 항변하기라도 하듯이. 사람 잡아먹기를 불법으로 만들어 보라, 그러면 볼스 일가를 굶겨 죽이는 셈이다!\n\n한마디로, 톤턴 골짜기에 둔 세 딸과 아버지를 거느린 볼스 씨는, 함정이자 골칫거리가 되어버린 어느 썩은 토대를 떠받치는 한 토막 들보처럼 끊임없이 제 소임을 다하고 있다. 그리고 수많은 사람들, 수많은 경우에 있어 문제는 결코 그릇됨에서 옳음으로의 변화(이건 전혀 동떨어진 고려 사항이다)가 아니라, 언제나 그 지극히 존경받을 만한 군단, 곧 볼스 일족에게 손해냐 이익이냐 하는 것이다.\n\n대법원장은 십 분 전쯤 장기 휴정을 위해 \"퇴정\"했다. 볼스 씨와 그의 젊은 의뢰인, 그리고 형체가 흐트러질 만큼 황급히 쑤셔 넣은 푸른 서류 가방 몇 개가—마치 큰 뱀들이 처음 잔뜩 삼킨 상태처럼 불룩하게—그 관청의 소굴로 돌아왔다. 볼스 씨는 그만한 존경받을 만한 사람이라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 조용하고 흔들림 없이, 마치 제 손의 가죽을 벗기듯 꽉 끼는 검은 장갑을 벗고, 마치 제 머리 가죽을 벗기듯 꽉 끼는 모자를 들어 올린 뒤, 책상 앞에 앉는다. 의뢰인은 모자와 장갑을 바닥에 내던진다—어디로 가는지 살피지도 신경 쓰지도 않고 아무 데나 내팽개친다. 반쯤 한숨 쉬고 반쯤 신음하며 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고, 욱신거리는 머리를 손에 괴고서, 젊은 절망의 초상화 같은 모습을 보인다.\n\n\"또 아무것도 못 했어!\" 리처드가 말한다. \"아무것도, 아무것도 못 했다고!\"\n\n\"아무것도 못 했다고는 하지 마십시오, 선생.\" 차분한 볼스가 대꾸한다. \"그건 거의 공정치 못한 말입니다, 선생, 거의 공정치 못해요!\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체 뭘 했는데요?\" 리처드가 침울하게 그를 돌아보며 말한다.\n\n\"그게 문제의 전부는 아닐 겁니다.\" 볼스가 대꾸한다. \"문제는 무엇이 진행 중인가, 무엇이 진행 중인가로 갈라질 수 있지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 뭐가 진행 중인데요?\" 시무룩한 의뢰인이 묻는다.\n\n볼스는 두 팔을 책상에 올린 채, 오른손 다섯 손가락 끝을 왼손 다섯 손가락 끝에 가만히 맞붙였다가 다시 가만히 떼며, 뚫어지게 천천히 의뢰인을 바라보며 대답한다. \"상당히 많은 게 진행 중입니다, 선생. 우리는 수레바퀴에 어깨를 들이댔고, 카스톤 씨, 바퀴는 돌고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 익시온을 태우고서 말이죠. 앞으로 닥칠 이 저주받을 너덧 달을 나는 어떻게 견디란 말입니까?\" 젊은이는 의자에서 일어나 방 안을 거닐며 외친다. * 익시온: 그리스 신화 속 인물로, 영원히 돌아가는 불수레에 묶이는 벌을 받았다.\n\n\"카스톤 씨.\" 볼스가 그가 가는 곳마다 두 눈으로 바싹 좇으며 대꾸한다. \"기분이 성급하시군요, 선생을 위해서도 안타깝습니다. 외람되나, 그렇게 안달하지 마시고, 그렇게 조급해하지 마시고, 그렇게 스스로를 닳게 하지 마시라고 권하겠습니다. 좀 더 인내하셔야 합니다. 자신을 좀 더 잘 지탱하셔야지요.\"\n\n\"사실은 당신을 본받으란 말이지요, 볼스 씨?\" 리처드가 짜증 섞인 웃음을 흘리며 다시 앉아, 무늬 없는 양탄자 위에서 장화로 안달스레 발장단을 치며 말한다.\n\n\"선생.\" 볼스가 대꾸하는데, 그는 늘 직업적 식욕뿐 아니라 두 눈으로도 의뢰인을 천천히 한 끼 식사처럼 음미하듯 바라본다. \"선생.\" 볼스가 속으로 삼키는 듯한 말투와 핏기 없는 평온함으로 대꾸한다. \"내가 감히 나 자신을 선생이나 그 누구의 본보기로 내세울 만큼 주제넘지는 못합니다. 그저 내 세 딸에게 좋은 이름이나 남길 수 있다면 그것으로 족하지요. 나는 사욕을 채우는 사람이 아닙니다. 하지만 선생이 나를 그렇게 꼭 집어 말씀하시니, 인정하겠습니다. 내 것을 조금 나눠 드리고 싶긴 합니다, 내—자, 선생, 그걸 무감각이라 부르고 싶으시겠지요, 나도 이의 없습니다—무감각이라 합시다—내 무감각을 조금 말입니다.\"\n\n\"볼스 씨.\" 의뢰인이 다소 무안해하며 해명한다. \"당신을 무감각하다고 비난할 뜻은 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"있으셨다고 봅니다, 선생, 본인도 모르게요.\" 침착한 볼스가 대꾸한다. \"아주 자연스러운 일이지요. 냉철한 머리로 선생의 이익을 챙기는 게 내 의무이고, 흥분한 선생의 감정에는 지금 같은 때에 내가 무감각하게 보일 수 있다는 걸 충분히 이해합니다. 내 딸들은 나를 더 잘 알지도 모르지요. 늙으신 내 아버지도 나를 더 잘 알지도 모르고요. 하지만 그들은 선생보다 나를 훨씬 더 오래 알아 왔고, 애정의 믿음 어린 눈은 사업의 의심 어린 눈과는 다릅니다. 그렇다고 사업의 눈이 의심 어린 것을 탓하는 건 아닙니다, 선생. 오히려 정반대지요. 선생의 이익을 챙기면서, 나는 나에게 가능한 모든 견제가 있기를 바랍니다. 그런 게 있는 것이 마땅하지요. 나는 오히려 조사를 청합니다. 하지만 선생의 이익을 위해서는 내가 냉철하고 빈틈없어야 하며, 카스톤 씨, 나는 달리 될 수가 없습니다—아니요, 선생, 선생을 기쁘게 하기 위해서라도요.\"\n\n볼스 씨는, 생쥐 구멍을 끈기 있게 지켜보는 관청의 고양이를 한번 흘끔 본 뒤, 다시 그 홀린 듯한 시선을 젊은 의뢰인에게 고정하고서, 마치 그 안에 나오지도 못하고 말도 못 하는 부정한 영(靈)이 들어앉은 듯, 단추를 꼭 채운 듯 반쯤만 들리는 목소리로 말을 잇는다. \"선생, 휴정 기간에 무엇을 해야 하느냐고 물으셨지요. 군대에 계신 신사분들이라면 마음만 먹으면 즐길 거리야 얼마든지 찾으실 수 있으리라 봅니다. 만일 휴정 기간에 내가 무엇을 할 거냐고 물으셨다면, 더 선뜻 대답할 수 있었을 겁니다. 나는 선생의 이익을 챙길 겁니다. 날마다 여기서, 선생의 이익을 챙기고 있을 겁니다. 그게 내 의무입니다, 카스톤 씨, 개정 기간이든 휴정 기간이든 내겐 아무 차이가 없습니다. 선생의 이익에 관해 나와 상의하고 싶으시면, 언제든 한결같이 여기서 나를 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다. 다른 법조인들은 시외로 나가지요. 나는 안 나갑니다. 그들이 나가는 걸 탓하는 건 아닙니다. 그저 나는 안 나간다고 할 뿐이지요. 이 책상이 선생의 반석입니다, 선생!\"\n\n볼스 씨가 책상을 똑똑 두드리니, 관처럼 텅 빈 소리가 난다. 그렇지만 리처드에게는 그렇지 않다. 그 소리에는 그를 북돋우는 무언가가 있다. 어쩌면 볼스 씨는 거기에 그런 게 있다는 걸 아는지도 모른다.\n\n\"잘 알고 있습니다, 볼스 씨.\" 리처드가 좀 더 허물없이, 좀 더 너그러운 기분으로 말한다. \"당신이 세상에서 가장 믿을 만한 사람이고, 당신과 함께 일한다는 건 곧 결코 속아 넘어가지 않는 사업가와 함께 일하는 거라는 걸요. 하지만 내 처지에 서 보세요. 이 어그러진 삶을 질질 끌면서, 날마다 점점 더 깊은 곤경으로 가라앉으면서, 끊임없이 바라고 끊임없이 실망하면서, 나 자신은 더 나쁜 쪽으로 거듭거듭 변하는데 다른 어떤 것도 더 나은 쪽으로는 바뀌지 않는 걸 절감하면서요. 그러면 당신도 때로는, 나처럼, 이게 어두컴컴한 사건이라는 걸 알게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아시다시피,\" 볼스 씨가 말한다. \"나는 결코 희망을 주지 않습니다, 선생. 처음부터 말씀드렸지요, 카스톤 씨, 나는 결코 희망을 주지 않는다고요. 특히 비용의 대부분이 그 재산에서 나오는 이런 사건에서는, 내가 희망을 준다면 내 좋은 이름을 소중히 여기지 않는 셈이 됩니다. 비용이 내 목적인 것처럼 보일 수 있으니까요. 그래도 더 나은 쪽으로 바뀐 게 전혀 없다고 하시니, 나는 순전한 사실로서 그건 부인해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 리처드가 밝아지며 대꾸한다. \"그런데 그걸 어떻게 입증하시려고요?\"\n\n\"카스톤 씨, 선생은 이제 대리되고 있습니다—\"\n\n\"방금 말씀하셨지요—반석이라고요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생.\" 볼스 씨가 머리를 가만히 가로저으며, 마치 재 위에 재가, 먼지 위에 먼지가 떨어지는 듯한 소리가 나도록 텅 빈 책상을 똑똑 두드리며 말한다. \"반석이지요. 그건 대단한 겁니다. 선생은 따로 대리되고 있고, 더는 남들의 이해 속에 숨겨지거나 묻혀 있지 않습니다. 그건 대단한 겁니다. 소송은 잠들어 있지 않습니다. 우리가 그걸 깨우고, 바람을 쐬어 주고, 데리고 거닙니다. 그건 대단한 겁니다. 그건 이름으로 보나 실제로 보나 더는 죄다 잔다이스만의 것이 아닙니다. 그건 대단한 겁니다. 이제는 아무도 제 멋대로만 할 수 없습니다, 선생. 그리고 그것도 분명 대단한 겁니다.\"\n\n리처드는 얼굴이 갑자기 확 달아오르며 움켜쥔 주먹으로 책상을 내리친다.\n\n\"볼스 씨! 내가 처음 존 잔다이스 씨의 집에 갔을 때 누군가 나에게, 그가 겉보기와 달리 사심 없는 벗이 아니라고—그가 차츰 드러난 그런 사람이라고—말했다면, 나는 그 중상을 물리칠 만큼 강한 말을 찾지 못했을 겁니다. 아무리 열렬히 그를 변호해도 모자랐을 겁니다. 그만큼 나는 세상을 몰랐던 거지요! 그런데 지금은 당신에게 분명히 선언하건대, 그가 나에게는 이 소송의 화신이 되었습니다. 소송이 하나의 추상이 아니라, 곧 존 잔다이스라는 겁니다. 내가 괴로울수록 그에게 더 분개하게 되고, 새로운 지연이 닥칠 때마다, 새로운 실망이 닥칠 때마다 그건 그저 존 잔다이스의 손에서 나온 새로운 해코지일 뿐이라는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니지요.\" 볼스가 말한다. \"그렇게는 말씀하지 마십시오. 우리 모두 인내해야 합니다. 게다가 나는 결코 남을 헐뜯지 않습니다, 선생. 나는 결코 남을 헐뜯지 않아요.\"\n\n\"볼스 씨.\" 성난 의뢰인이 대꾸한다. \"그가 할 수만 있었다면 이 소송을 목 졸라 죽였으리라는 걸 당신도 나만큼 잘 알지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"그분이 소송에 적극적이지 않았던 건 맞습니다.\" 볼스 씨가 마지못한 기색으로 인정한다. \"분명 적극적이지 않았지요. 하지만 그래도, 그래도, 그분에게 선량한 의도가 있었을지도 모릅니다. 누가 사람의 마음을 읽을 수 있겠습니까, 카스톤 씨!\"\n\n\"당신은 할 수 있지요.\" 리처드가 대꾸한다.\n\n\"그의 의도가 무엇이었는지 알 만큼은요. 우리 이익이 충돌하는 겁니까, 아닙니까? 그걸—나에게—말해 보세요!\" 리처드는 마지막 세 마디를 그 믿음의 반석에 세 번 똑똑 두드리는 것과 함께 내뱉는다.\n\n\"카스톤 씨.\" 볼스가 자세 하나 흐트러지지 않고, 굶주린 두 눈을 한 번도 깜박이지 않은 채 대꾸한다. \"선생의 직업적 조언자로서 내 의무를 저버리는 것이요, 선생의 이익에 대한 내 충실함에서 벗어나는 것이겠지요, 만일 내가 그 이익을 잔다이스 씨의 이익과 동일한 것으로 내세운다면 말입니다. 그것들은 결코 같은 게 아닙니다, 선생. 나는 결코 남의 동기를 함부로 단정하지 않습니다. 나에게는 아버지가 있고 나 또한 아버지인지라, 결코 남의 동기를 함부로 단정하지 않지요. 하지만 그것이 가문에 불화의 씨를 뿌리는 한이 있더라도, 나는 직업적 의무에서 움츠러들어서는 안 됩니다. 선생은 지금 선생의 이익에 관해 직업적으로 나와 상의하고 계신 걸로 압니다만? 그러신 거지요? 그러면 답하겠습니다, 그것들은 잔다이스 씨의 이익과 동일하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"당연히 같지 않지요!\" 리처드가 외친다. \"당신은 그걸 오래전에 알아냈잖아요.\"\n\n\"카스톤 씨.\" 볼스가 대꾸한다. \"어떤 제삼자에 관해서도 나는 필요 이상으로는 말하고 싶지 않습니다. 나는 내 좋은 이름을, 그리고 근면과 끈기로 손에 넣게 될지 모를 보잘것없는 재산을 더럽히지 않고, 내 딸 에마와 제인과 캐롤라인에게 물려주고 싶습니다. 또한 나는 내 직업 형제들과 화목하게 지내고 싶고요. 스킴폴 씨가 나에게 영광을—아주 대단한 영광이라고는 하지 않겠습니다, 나는 결코 아첨에 굽실대지 않으니까요—우리 둘을 이 방에서 만나게 해 주는 영광을 베푸셨을 때, 선생, 나는 선생의 이익이 우리 업계의 다른 인사에게 맡겨져 있는 동안에는 그 이익에 관해 아무 의견도 조언도 드릴 수 없다고 말씀드렸습니다. 그리고 나는 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에 관해 마땅히 해야 할 말을 했지요, 그 사무소는 명망이 높으니까요. 그런데도 선생은 그 보호에서 선생의 이익을 거두어 나에게 맡기는 것이 옳다고 여기셨습니다. 선생은 깨끗한 손으로 그것을 가져오셨고, 선생, 나도 깨끗한 손으로 받았습니다. 그 이익은 이제 이 사무소에서 최우선입니다. 내 소화 기능은, 내가 말씀드린 걸 들으셨을지 모르나, 상태가 좋지 않아서 쉬면 좀 나아지겠지만, 선생, 내가 선생의 대리인인 한 나는 쉬지 않을 겁니다. 선생이 나를 원하실 때면 언제든 여기서 나를 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다. 어디로든 나를 부르시면, 가겠습니다. 장기 휴정 동안, 선생, 나는 짬을 내어 선생의 이익을 점점 더 면밀히 연구하고, 미카엘 축일 개정 기간 이후 천지를(물론 대법원장까지 포함해서) 뒤흔들 채비를 마련하는 데 바치겠습니다. 그리고 내가 마침내 선생을 축하드릴 때면, 선생,\" 볼스 씨가 단단히 결심한 사람다운 엄숙함으로 말한다. \"내가 마침내 진심을 다해 선생의 재산 상속을 축하드릴 때면—그에 관해서는, 내가 결코 희망을 주지 않으니 그렇지, 안 그랬다면 한마디 더 보탤 수도 있겠습니다만—선생은 그때 미결로 남은 약간의 잔액 말고는 나에게 빚진 게 없을 겁니다, 곧 그 재산에서 인정된 사정(査定) 비용에 포함되지 않은, 변호사와 의뢰인 사이의 비용 말입니다. 나는 선생에게 아무 청구권도 주장하지 않습니다, 카스톤 씨, 다만 내 직업적 의무를 열의 있고 능동적으로 이행한 것에 대해서만은—나른하고 판에 박힌 이행이 아니라요, 선생, 그만큼의 공로는 분명히 해 두겠습니다. 내 의무가 순조롭게 끝나면, 우리 사이의 모든 것도 끝납니다.\"\n\n볼스는 마침내 이 신조 선언에 덧붙임처럼 한마디를 더한다. 곧 카스톤 씨가 곧 연대로 복귀할 참이니, 어쩌면 카스톤 씨가 그의 대리인 앞으로 우선 이십 파운드를 지급하라는 지시서를 호의로 써 주시면 어떻겠냐고.\n\n\"근래에 자잘한 상담과 출석이 많았던 데다, 선생,\" 볼스가 자기 일지를 한 장 한 장 넘기며 말한다. \"이런 것들이 쌓이고, 나는 자본이 넉넉한 사람이라고는 자처하지 않으니까요. 우리가 처음 지금의 관계를 맺었을 때 나는 선생에게 솔직히 말씀드렸지요—변호사와 의뢰인 사이에는 아무리 솔직해도 지나치지 않다는 게 내 신조입니다—나는 자본이 넉넉한 사람이 아니며, 만일 자본이 선생의 목적이라면 차라리 서류를 켄지 사무소에 두는 편이 낫다고요. 그렇습니다, 카스톤 씨, 여기서는 자본의 이점도 단점도 찾으실 수 없을 겁니다, 선생. 이것이,\" 볼스가 책상을 다시 한 번 텅 비게 친다. \"선생의 반석입니다. 그것은 그 이상인 척하지 않습니다.\"\n\n의뢰인은, 풀죽었던 마음이 자기도 모르게 누그러지고 막연한 희망이 다시 지펴져, 펜과 잉크를 들어 지시서를 작성하는데, 거기 적을 날짜를 두고 적잖이 골머리를 앓으며 헤아린다, 대리인의 손에 든 자금이 변변치 못함을 넌지시 드러내면서. 그러는 내내 볼스는 몸도 마음도 단추를 꼭 채운 채 그를 유심히 바라본다. 그러는 내내 볼스의 관청 고양이는 생쥐 구멍을 지켜본다.\n\n마지막으로 의뢰인은 악수를 하며, 제발 하늘과 땅을 두고서, 자기를 대법원에서 \"끌어내 구해 달라고\" 볼스 씨에게 온 힘을 다해 달라고 애원한다. 결코 희망을 주지 않는 볼스 씨는 의뢰인의 어깨에 손바닥을 얹고 미소 지으며 대답한다. \"늘 여기 있겠습니다, 선생. 직접이든 편지로든, 늘 여기서 수레바퀴에 어깨를 들이대고 있는 나를 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다, 선생.\" 이렇게 그들은 헤어지고, 혼자 남은 볼스는 자기 일지에서 자질구레한 사항 몇 가지를 청구서 초안 장부로 옮겨 적는 일에 몰두하니, 마침내 세 딸을 이롭게 할 요량에서다. 부지런한 여우나 곰도 제 새끼들을 염두에 두고 잡은 병아리나 길 잃은 나그네의 셈을 그렇게 매기리라—그렇다고 그 말로 케닝턴의 축축한 정원에 들어선 흙냄새 나는 오두막에서 아비 볼스와 함께 사는, 풋낯에 비쩍 마르고 단추를 꼭 채운 세 처녀를 헐뜯으려는 건 아니다.\n\n리처드는 시먼즈 인의 짙은 그늘에서 챈서리 레인의 햇살 속으로—마침 오늘은 그곳에 햇살이 비치고 있다—걸어 나와, 생각에 잠긴 채 걸음을 옮기다가 링컨즈 인으로 접어들어, 링컨즈 인의 나무 그늘 밑을 지난다. 그 나무들의 얼룩진 그림자는 이런 부류의 어슬렁대는 자들 위로 숱하게 드리워졌으리라. 똑같이 수그린 머리, 물어뜯은 손톱, 침울한 눈빛, 머뭇대는 발걸음, 목적도 없이 몽롱한 분위기, 좋은 것을 갉아먹다 갉아먹힌 모습, 시어 버린 인생 위로. 이 어슬렁대는 자는 아직 추레하지는 않으나, 그것도 닥쳐올 수 있다. 선례 말고는 아무 지혜도 모르는 대법원은 그런 선례라면 차고도 넘치게 풍부하니, 어찌 한 사람이 만 명과 다르겠는가?\n\n그러나 그의 몰락이 시작된 지는 너무도 짧은 시간이라, 비록 그곳을 미워하면서도 여러 달 동안 통째로 떠나기를 못내 아쉬워하며 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나가는 동안, 리처드 자신은 제 사정이 무슨 깜짝 놀랄 일이라도 되는 듯 느낄지도 모른다. 그의 마음이 갉아먹는 근심과 초조와 불신과 의혹으로 무거우면서도, 그 안에는 서글픈 의아함이 들어설 자리가 있을지 모른다, 그가 그곳에 처음 찾아갔을 때가 얼마나 달랐던가, 자기 자신이 얼마나 달랐던가, 제 마음의 온갖 빛깔이 얼마나 달랐던가를 떠올릴 때면 말이다. 그러나 부당함은 부당함을 낳으니, 그림자와 싸우다가 그림자에게 패하다 보면 맞서 싸울 실체를 세워야만 한다. 살아 있는 그 누구도 이해할 수 없는—이미 이해할 시기는 한참 지나 버린—만질 수 없는 소송에서, 만질 수 있는 친구의 모습으로, 곧 자기를 이 파멸에서 구해 주려 했을 친구의 모습으로 눈을 돌려 그를 적으로 삼는 것이 음울한 위안이 되어 버린 것이다. 리처드는 볼스에게 진실을 말했다. 마음이 모질어진 상태든 누그러진 상태든, 그는 여전히 자기가 입은 상처를 한결같이 그 문 앞에 내려놓는다. 그는 그쪽에서 어떤 정해진 목적에 의해 좌절당했고, 그 목적이란 그의 존재를 통째로 제 안으로 녹여 들이고 있는 그 단 하나의 주제에서밖에 비롯될 수 없으며, 게다가 형체를 갖춘 적수와 압제자를 두는 것은 그의 눈에 스스로를 정당화하는 일이기도 하다.\n\n이 모든 일에서 리처드는 괴물인가, 아니면 기록 천사로부터 인용해 올 수만 있다면 대법원에 그런 선례들 또한 차고도 넘치게 풍부한 것으로 드러날까? * 기록 천사: 사람들의 행적을 빠짐없이 적어 두는 천사로, 최후 심판 때 그 기록이 증거가 된다고 한다.\n\n이런 부류의 사람들을 보는 데 낯설지 않은 두 쌍의 눈이, 그가 손톱을 물어뜯으며 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 채 광장을 가로질러 남쪽 문의 그림자 속으로 삼켜질 때까지 그의 뒷모습을 좇는다. 그 눈의 임자는 거피 씨와 위블 씨로, 그들은 나무 밑 야트막한 돌 난간에 기대어 이야기를 나누고 있던 참이다. 그는 그들 바로 곁을 지나가지만, 땅바닥 말고는 아무것도 보지 못한다.\n\n\"윌리엄,\" 위블 씨가 구레나룻을 매만지며 말한다. \"저기 안에서 연소(燃燒)가 일어나고 있어! 자연발화는 아니지만, 그래도 속으로 부글부글 타오르는 연소라고.\"\n\n\"아!\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"그 친구는 잔다이스 소송에서 손을 떼려 하지 않았으니, 빚더미에 머리끝까지 잠겨 있겠지. 나는 그 친구를 잘 알지도 못했어. 우리 사무소에서 수습을 받을 때 보면 기념탑처럼 콧대가 높았거든. 서기로든 의뢰인으로든 내겐 시원하게 떠나 준 셈이지! 자, 토니, 내가 말한 게 바로 저들이 꾸미는 일이야.\"\n\n거피 씨는 다시 팔짱을 끼고, 흥미로운 대화를 이어 가려는 듯 난간에 기대어 자세를 고쳐 잡는다.\n\n\"저들은 아직도 그 일에 매달려 있어, 선생,\" 거피 씨가 말한다. \"아직도 재고를 조사하고, 아직도 서류를 살피고, 아직도 산더미 같은 쓰레기를 뒤지고 있지. 이런 속도라면 앞으로 칠 년은 거기 붙어 있겠어.\"\n\n\"그리고 스몰이 거들고 있다고?\"\n\n\"스몰은 일주일 전에 통보하고 우리를 떠났어. 켄지에게 말하기를, 자기 할아버지 사업이 그 노인장에게는 벅차고, 자기가 그걸 맡으면 형편이 나아지겠다고 했다더군. 스몰이 워낙 속을 안 터놓는 통에 나하고는 사이가 좀 서먹했었지. 그런데 그 친구 말이, 그건 너하고 내가 먼저 시작한 일 아니냐는 거야—사실 그렇긴 했으니까—그래서 거기서 내가 한 방 먹은 셈이라, 우리 사이를 예전대로 돌려놨지. 그렇게 해서 내가 저들이 꾸미는 일을 알게 된 거야.\"\n\n\"넌 안에 한 번도 들여다본 적이 없고?\"\n\n\"토니,\" 거피 씨가 조금 멋쩍어하며 말한다. \"너한테 숨김없이 말하자면, 나는 너랑 같이 있을 때 말고는 그 집이 영 마음에 안 들어서, 그래서 안 들여다봤어. 그래서 네 물건을 가지러 가자고 이 작은 약속을 잡은 거고. 자, 시계가 그 시각을 알리는군! 토니\"—거피 씨가 알 수 없게, 또 애틋하게 능변을 늘어놓기 시작한다—\"내가 네 마음에 다시 한 번 새겨 둬야겠어, 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정들이 내 가장 소중한 계획에, 그리고 전에 친구로서 너에게 말했던 그 보답 없는 형상에 서글픈 변화를 일으켰다는 걸 말이야. 그 형상은 산산조각 났고, 그 우상은 무너져 내렸어. 친구로서 네 도움을 받아 그 법정에서 이루어 볼까 생각했던 목적들과 관련해 지금 내 유일한 바람은, 그것들을 그냥 내버려 두고 망각 속에 묻어 버리는 거야. 너는 가능하다고 보냐, 조금이라도 그럴 법하다고 보냐(친구로서 너한테 묻는 거다, 토니), 그—자연발화의—기운에 먹잇감이 된 그 변덕스럽고 속 깊은 늙은이의 성격을 네가 아는 한에서 말이야, 토니, 너는 그자가 다시 생각해서, 네가 살아 있는 그자를 본 뒤에 그 편지들을 어디 다른 데 치워 두고, 그래서 그것들이 그날 밤에 불타 없어지지 않았을 가능성이 조금이라도 있다고 보냐?\"\n\n위블 씨는 한동안 곰곰이 생각한다. 고개를 가로젓는다. 단연코 아니라고 본다.\n\n\"토니,\" 거피 씨가 두 사람이 그 법정 쪽으로 걸어가며 말한다. \"다시 한 번 친구로서 나를 이해해 줘. 더 자세히 설명하지는 않더라도, 우상이 무너졌다는 건 거듭 말할 수 있어. 이제 내가 이루려는 목적은 망각 속에 묻어 버리는 것밖에 없어. 거기에 나는 나 자신을 걸었어. 나 자신을 위해서도, 산산조각 난 그 형상을 위해서도, 또 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정들을 위해서도 마땅히 그래야 하는 일이지. 만일 네가 몸짓으로든 눈짓으로든 나에게, 네가 전에 머물던 그 하숙에 문제의 그 편지들과 조금이라도 비슷해 보이는 서류가 어디 놓여 있는 걸 봤다고 표시해 준다면, 선생, 나는 내 책임으로 그것들을 불 속에 던져 넣겠어.\"\n\n위블 씨가 고개를 끄덕인다. 거피 씨는, 일부는 법정 변론조로 일부는 낭만조로 이 견해들을 늘어놓은 데 스스로 크게 우쭐해져서—이 신사는 무엇이든 심문하는 형식으로 진행하거나, 무엇이든 논고나 변론 형식으로 늘어놓는 데 열정을 품고 있다—위엄 있게 친구를 데리고 그 법정으로 향한다.\n\n그 법정이 법정이 된 이래로, 넝마와 병을 파는 가게에서 벌어지는 일만큼 화수분 같은 잡담거리를 가진 적은 없었다. 어김없이 아침 여덟 시면 노인 스몰위드 씨가 그 모퉁이로 실려 내려와, 스몰위드 부인과 주디, 바트를 거느리고 안으로 옮겨진다. 그리고 어김없이 온종일 그들 모두는 밤 아홉 시까지 거기 머물며, 요릿집에서 가져온, 양으로는 넉넉지 않은 집시 식사로 요기를 달래면서, 고인이 된 그 안타까운 분의 보물들을 뒤지고 캐고, 파헤치고 파고들고 잠긴다. 그 보물들이 무엇인지 그들이 어찌나 꽁꽁 숨기는지 법정 골목 전체가 미칠 지경이다. 그 골목은 정신착란에 빠져, 찻주전자에서 기니 금화가 쏟아지고, 펀치 사발에서 크라운 은화가 넘쳐흐르며, 낡은 의자와 매트리스마다 잉글랜드 은행권이 가득 채워져 있는 광경을 상상한다. 골목은 대니얼 댄서 씨와 그 누이의 6페니짜리 전기(요란하게 채색된 접이식 권두 삽화가 딸린)와, 서퍽의 엘위스 씨의 전기까지 손에 넣고서, 그 믿을 만한 이야기들에 담긴 온갖 사실을 죄다 크룩 씨에게 옮겨 붙인다. 청소부가 헌 종이와 재와 깨진 병을 한 수레 실어 나르러 불려 올 때면 두 번이나 온 골목이 모여들어, 광주리가 나오는 족족 그 안을 들여다본다. 박엽지 위에 게걸스러운 작은 펜으로 글을 쓰던 두 신사가 그 근방을 어슬렁대는 모습이 여러 번 목격되니—동업 관계가 깨진 뒤로 서로를 멀리하는 사이다. 솔 술집은 화음의 밤마다 그 만연한 관심의 광맥을 솜씨 좋게 한 줄기 꿰어 흐르게 한다. 어린 스윌스가, 업계 용어로 \"주절거리기\"라 불리는 방식으로 그 화제를 빗대면 우레 같은 박수를 받고, 같은 가수가 정규 공연에서 영감에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 \"즉흥 익살\"을 부린다. 멜빌슨 양마저, 다시 유행한 칼레도니아 가락 \"우리는 끄덕이고 있어요\"에서 \"개들은 국물을 좋아해\"라는(그 음식의 정체가 무엇이든 간에) 대목을 어찌나 능청맞게, 또 옆집 쪽으로 고개를 어찌나 잘 틀며 강조하는지, 곧바로 스몰위드 씨가 돈 찾기를 좋아한다는 뜻으로 알아들어, 밤마다 두 차례 앙코르의 영예를 받는다. 이 모든 일에도 불구하고 골목은 아무것도 알아내지 못한다. 그리고 이제 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인이, 모습을 드러내기만 하면 사람들이 죄다 우르르 모여드는 그 옛 하숙인에게 알려 주는 바, 골목은 모든 것을, 아니 그 이상을 알아내려 끊임없이 들끓고 있다.\n\n위블 씨와 거피 씨는, 골목의 모든 눈이 자기들에게 쏠린 가운데, 한껏 인기 높은 채로, 고인이 된 그 안타까운 분의 집 닫힌 문을 두드린다. 그러나 골목의 예상과 달리 안으로 들여보내지자, 그들은 곧장 인기를 잃고, 좋은 일을 꾸미는 게 아니라고 여겨진다.\n\n집 안 곳곳의 덧창은 어지간히 다 닫혀 있고, 일층은 촛불이 필요할 만큼 어둡다. 젊은 스몰위드 씨에게 이끌려 뒷가게로 들어선 두 사람은, 햇빛 속에 있다가 막 들어온지라 처음에는 어둠과 그림자밖에 아무것도 보지 못한다. 그러나 차츰 분간하게 되니, 노인 스몰위드 씨는 폐지(廢紙)의 우물인지 무덤인지 그 가장자리에 놓인 의자에 앉아 있고, 갸륵한 주디는 그 안을 여자 묘지기처럼 더듬고 있으며, 스몰위드 부인은 그 옆 평지에서, 하루 동안 그녀에게 날아든 누적된 찬사인 듯한 종잇조각과 인쇄물과 필사본 더미에 눈처럼 파묻혀 있다. 스몰을 포함한 일행 전체가 먼지와 흙으로 시커멓게 그을려, 방의 전반적인 꼴로도 누그러지지 않는 악귀 같은 행색을 하고 있다. 방 안에는 예전보다 잡동사니와 폐물이 더 많고, 가능하다면 더 더러워졌으며, 또한 죽은 거주자의 자취로, 심지어 벽에 분필로 휘갈긴 그의 글씨로까지, 으스스하다.\n\n방문객이 들어서자 스몰위드 씨와 주디는 동시에 팔짱을 끼고 뒤지던 일을 멈춘다.\n\n\"아하!\" 노인이 쉰 목소리로 까옥거린다. \"안녕들 하신가, 신사 양반들, 안녕들 하신가! 자네 물건을 가지러 왔구먼, 위블 씨? 잘됐어, 잘됐어. 하! 하! 자네가 그걸 여기 더 오래 놔뒀더라면, 선생, 우리는 창고 사용료를 받느라 자네 물건을 경매에 부쳐야 했을 거야. 또 이렇게 오니 아주 제집 같지, 아무렴? 반갑네, 반가워!\"\n\n위블 씨는 그에게 고맙다고 하며 방 안을 한번 둘러본다. 거피 씨의 눈이 위블 씨의 눈을 좇는다. 위블 씨의 눈은 아무 새 정보도 담지 못하고 되돌아온다. 거피 씨의 눈은 되돌아오다가 스몰위드 씨의 눈과 마주친다. 그 살가운 노인은 여전히, 태엽이 풀려 가는 어떤 악기처럼 중얼거린다. \"안녕하신가, 선생—안녕—안—\" 그러다 태엽이 다 풀리자, 씩 웃는 침묵에 잠긴다. 그 순간 거피 씨는 맞은편 어둠 속에 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 서 있는 토킹혼 씨를 보고 흠칫 놀란다.\n\n\"이 신사분이 친절하게도 내 변호사 노릇을 해 주신다네.\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다. \"나는 이만한 명망가에게 어울리는 의뢰인이 못 되는데도, 이분이 참으로 좋은 분이라!\"\n\n거피 씨는 친구를 슬쩍 쿡 찔러 한 번 더 보게 하고는, 토킹혼 씨에게 어정쩡하게 허리 굽혀 인사한다. 토킹혼 씨는 가벼운 끄덕임으로 답한다. 토킹혼 씨는 마치 달리 할 일이 없어, 이 새로운 구경거리가 오히려 재미있다는 듯 바라보고 있다.\n\n\"여기 재산이 꽤 많은 모양입니다, 선생.\" 거피 씨가 스몰위드 씨에게 한마디 한다.\n\n\"주로 넝마와 쓰레기일세, 이 사람아! 넝마와 쓰레기! 나하고 바트하고 손녀딸 주디가 팔아서 한 푼이라도 값나갈 만한 것들의 목록을 만들어 보려는 참이지. 하지만 아직 별로 못 추렸어. 우리는—아직—추리지—못—하—\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 또 태엽이 풀렸는데, 그동안 위블 씨의 눈은 거피 씨의 눈을 거느리고 또 한 번 방을 한 바퀴 돌고 되돌아온다.\n\n\"자, 선생.\" 위블 씨가 말한다. \"위층으로 올라가도록 허락해 주신다면, 더는 폐 끼치지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어디든지, 이 사람아, 어디든지! 자네 집이나 다름없으니. 부디 편히 굴게!\"\n\n두 사람이 위층으로 올라가며 거피 씨는 의아한 듯 눈썹을 치켜올리고 토니를 바라본다. 토니는 고개를 가로젓는다. 그 옛 방은 음울하고 침침하기 짝이 없고, 그 잊지 못할 밤에 타오르던 불의 재가 변색된 벽난로 안에 아직 남아 있다. 그들은 어떤 물건에도 손대기를 몹시 꺼려, 무엇이든 먼저 조심스레 먼지를 불어 낸다. 또한 머무는 시간을 늘리고 싶지도 않아, 몇 안 되는 가져갈 물건들을 한껏 서둘러 챙기며, 결코 속삭임보다 높게 말하지 않는다.\n\n\"이것 봐,\" 토니가 흠칫 물러나며 말한다. \"저 끔찍한 고양이가 들어오잖아!\"\n\n거피 씨가 의자 뒤로 물러선다. \"스몰이 저 녀석 얘기를 해 줬어. 그날 밤 용처럼 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰고 날뛰며 사방을 할퀴고 다니다가 지붕 위로 빠져나가, 거기서 보름 동안 어슬렁대다가, 그러고는 굴뚝으로 비쩍 마른 꼴로 굴러떨어졌대. 너 저런 짐승 본 적 있냐? 모든 걸 다 안다는 표정이잖아, 안 그래? 거의 자기가 크룩인 것 같은 꼴이라니까. 쉿쉿! 저리 가, 이 도깨비야!\"\n\n레이디 제인은 문간에서 귀에서 귀까지 호랑이처럼 으르렁대고 곤봉 같은 꼬리를 세운 채, 따를 기색을 전혀 보이지 않는다. 그러나 토킹혼 씨가 그 녀석에 걸려 비틀거리자, 녀석은 그의 녹슨 듯한 다리를 향해 침을 칵 뱉고, 노여워 욕을 하듯 으르렁대며, 등을 활처럼 곤두세우고 위층으로 올라간다. 어쩌면 또 지붕 위를 어슬렁대다가 굴뚝으로 돌아오려는 모양이다.\n\n\"거피 씨.\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"잠깐 한마디 나눌 수 있겠소?\"\n\n거피 씨는 벽에서 영국 미인 화랑 전집을 거두어, 그 예술품들을 예의 볼품없는 모자 상자에 담느라 여념이 없다. \"선생,\" 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸한다. \"나는 같은 업계의 어느 분에게나 예의를 지키고 싶고, 특히 선생처럼 잘 알려진 분에게라면 더더욱 그렇습니다—진심으로 덧붙이건대, 선생, 선생처럼 저명한 분에게라면요. 그렇지만 토킹혼 씨, 선생, 만일 나에게 하실 말씀이 있다면, 그 말씀은 내 친구가 있는 자리에서 하시기를 요구해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇소?\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 선생. 내 이유는 전혀 사사로운 것이 아니지만, 나로서는 충분하고도 남습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지, 그렇겠지.\" 토킹혼 씨는, 그가 조용히 걸어가 선 벽난로 앞 돌처럼 태연하다. \"이 일은 내가 자네에게 어떤 조건을 내거는 수고를 끼칠 만큼 중대하지도 않네, 거피 씨.\" 여기서 그는 잠시 멈추어 미소 짓는데, 그 미소는 그의 바지만큼이나 칙칙하고 녹슬어 있다. \"축하받을 일이야, 거피 씨. 자네는 운 좋은 젊은이일세, 선생.\"\n\n\"그럭저럭 그렇지요, 토킹혼 씨. 불평하진 않습니다.\"\n\n\"불평이라니? 지체 높은 친구들에, 대저택에 자유로이 드나들고, 우아한 귀부인들과 만날 수 있는데! 아니, 거피 씨, 런던에는 자네가 될 수만 있다면 두 귀라도 내놓을 사람들이 있다네.\"\n\n거피 씨는, 지금 자기 대신 그런 사람들 중 하나가 될 수만 있다면 점점 더 붉어지는 제 두 귀라도 기꺼이 내놓겠다는 듯한 표정으로 대꾸한다. \"선생, 내가 내 직무에 충실하고 켄지 앤드 카보이에 옳게 처신하는 한, 내 친구와 지인이 누구든 그 회사에도, 업계의 어느 분에게도, 그 필즈의 토킹혼 씨에게조차도 아무 상관 없는 일입니다. 나는 더 이상 나를 해명할 어떤 의무도 없습니다. 그리고 선생을 온전히 존경하면서, 또 결례 없이—거듭 말하건대, 결례 없이—\"\n\n\"아, 물론이지!\"\n\n\"—나는 그럴 생각이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고.\" 토킹혼 씨가 차분히 끄덕이며 말한다. \"아주 좋아. 이 초상들을 보니 자네는 유행을 선도하는 명사들에게 큰 관심이 있는 모양이군, 선생?\"\n\n그는 이 말을 어리둥절해진 토니에게 건네는데, 토니는 그 부드러운 책망을 인정한다.\n\n\"영국인이라면 누구나 갖춘 미덕이지.\" 토킹혼 씨가 한마디 한다. 그는 그을린 벽난로 선반을 등지고 벽난로 앞 돌 위에 서 있다가, 이제 안경을 눈에 갖다 대고 돌아선다. \"이건 누구지? '데드록 마님'이라. 하! 나름대로 아주 잘 그린 초상이지만, 개성의 힘이 부족하군. 그럼 잘들 있게, 신사 양반들. 잘들 있어!\"\n\n그가 걸어 나가자, 거피 씨는 식은땀을 뻘뻘 흘리며 마음을 다잡고, 미인 화랑을 마저 황급히 떼어 내려, 데드록 마님으로 끝맺는다.\n\n\"토니,\" 그가 어리둥절해하는 동료에게 황급히 말한다. \"어서 물건들을 챙겨서 이 집에서 나가자. 토니, 너에게 더는 숨겨 봐야 소용없겠다, 지금 내 손에 쥔, 백조처럼 우아한 귀족의 한 분과 나 사이에, 밝혀지지 않은 교신과 교제가 있었다는 걸 말이야. 너에게 그걸 털어놓을 때가 있었을지도 몰라. 하지만 이제 다시는 그럴 일이 없을 거야. 내가 한 맹세를 봐서도, 산산조각 난 그 우상을 봐서도, 또 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정들을 봐서도, 그 모든 게 망각 속에 묻혀야 마땅해. 친구로서 너에게 당부한다, 네가 늘 유행계 소식에 보여 온 관심을 봐서도, 또 내가 너에게 변통해 줄 수 있었던 자잘한 가불을 봐서도, 한마디 캐묻지 말고 그걸 그렇게 묻어 다오!\"\n\n이 당부를 거피 씨는 거의 법정 변론의 광기에 가까운 상태로 늘어놓고, 그러는 동안 그의 친구는 온통 헝클어진 머리칼로도, 심지어 공들여 가꾼 구레나룻으로까지도 얼떨떨한 심정을 내비친다.\n\n잉글랜드는 몇 주째 끔찍한 상태에 빠져 있다. 쿠들 경은 물러나려 하고, 토머스 두들 경은 들어오려 하지 않으니, 쿠들과 두들 말고는 그레이트브리튼에 (말할 만한 사람이라곤) 아무도 없는 터라 정부가 없는 셈이다. 한때 불가피해 보이던 그 두 위대한 인물의 결투가 끝내 벌어지지 않은 건 그나마 다행한 일이다. 만약 두 발의 권총이 모두 명중해 쿠들과 두들이 서로를 죽였더라면, 짐작건대 잉글랜드는 지금 짧은 윗옷에 긴 양말 차림인 어린 쿠들과 어린 두들이 다 자랄 때까지 통치받기를 기다려야 했을 테니 말이다. 그러나 이 엄청난 국가적 재난은 쿠들 경의 시의적절한 깨달음 덕에 모면되었다. 토론이 달아오른 와중에 자신이 토머스 두들 경의 그 보잘것없는 평생 행적 전부를 경멸하고 멸시한다고 말하긴 했으나, 그것은 그저 당파의 차이가 결코 자신으로 하여금 그 행적에 대한 가장 따뜻한 찬탄의 헌사를 거두게 하지는 못하리라는 뜻이었을 뿐이라는 깨달음이었다. 한편 그와 똑같이 절묘하게도, 토머스 두들 경 역시 자기 가슴속에 쿠들 경을 미덕과 명예의 거울로 후세에 명백히 등재해두었다는 사실이 밝혀졌다. 그래도 잉글랜드는 (레스터 데드록 경이 잘 짚었듯이) 폭풍을 헤쳐 나갈 키잡이가 없는 음울한 곤경에 몇 주째 처해 있다. 게다가 놀라운 점은, 잉글랜드가 이를 그리 심하게 걱정하는 기색이 없이, 대홍수 이전 옛 세상이 그러했듯 먹고 마시고 장가들고 시집보내기를 그대로 이어간다는 것이다. 하지만 쿠들도 위험을 알았고, 두들도 위험을 알았으며, 그들의 추종자와 빌붙은 무리 모두 더없이 또렷하게 위험을 알아차렸다. 마침내 토머스 두들 경은 들어오기로 응낙했을 뿐 아니라 그 일을 멋들어지게 해냈으니, 자기 조카들 전부와 사촌 형제들 전부와 처남 매부들 전부를 데리고 들어온 것이다. 그러니 이 낡은 배에도 아직 희망이 있다.\n\n두들은 자신을 나라에 내던져야 한다는 것을, 그것도 주로 금화와 맥주의 형태로 그래야 한다는 것을 알아냈다. 이렇게 변신한 상태로 그는 여러 곳에 동시에 출현할 수 있고, 한 번에 나라의 상당한 부분에 자기를 내던질 수 있다. 브리타니아는 금화 형태의 두들을 호주머니에 챙기고, 맥주 형태의 두들을 들이켜며, 자신은 그런 짓을 하지 않는다고 얼굴이 시퍼레지도록 맹세하느라 — 두말할 것 없이 그 영광과 도덕을 드높이기 위해 — 무척 분주한 까닭에, 모든 두들파와 쿠들파가 그 종교적 수행을 거들러 사방으로 흩어지면서 런던 사교철은 별안간 막을 내린다.\n\n그리하여 체스니 월드의 가정부 라운스웰 부인은, 아직 어떠한 지시도 내려오지 않았으나, 머잖아 가족이 돌아오리라 내다본다. 그것도 사촌들과 그 밖에 저 위대한 헌정 사업에 무엇으로든 손을 보탤 수 있는 사람들이 꽤 큼직하게 늘어 함께 오리라고. 그리하여 이 위풍당당한 노부인은 시간의 앞머리채를 움켜쥐고서, 그를 이끌고 층계를 오르내리고 화랑과 복도를 따라 거닐며 방마다 두루 다닌다. 시간이 더 늙기 전에 모든 게 준비되었음을, 마룻바닥은 반들거리게 닦였고 양탄자는 깔렸으며 커튼은 털어 펼쳐졌고 침대는 부풀려 두드려졌으며 식기실과 부엌은 출동 태세를 갖추었음을 — 데드록 가문의 위엄에 걸맞게 만사가 채비되었음을 — 똑똑히 목격하게 하려는 것이다.\n\n지금 이 여름 저녁, 해가 저물 무렵 준비는 끝났다. 거주에 필요한 온갖 도구를 다 갖추었으면서도 벽에 그려진 형상들 말고는 거주자가 하나도 없으니, 이 오래된 저택은 황량하고 엄숙해 보인다. 이 자리에 사는 어느 데드록이 화랑을 거닐며 이렇게 곱씹었을지도 모른다. 저들도 이렇게 왔다 갔구나, 저들도 지금 내가 보는 것처럼 이 화랑이 고요히 잠든 모습을 보았구나, 저들도 지금 내가 생각하듯 자신들이 떠나면 이 영지에 생길 빈자리를 생각했구나, 저들도 지금 내가 그러하듯 이곳이 자기들 없이 존재할 수 있으리라곤 믿기 어려워했구나, 저들도 저 울리는 문을 이제 닫으며 내가 그들의 세상에서 떠나듯 내 세상에서 떠나갔구나, 저들도 그리워할 빈자리 하나 남기지 못한 채 그렇게 죽어갔구나, 라고.\n\n바깥에서 보면 아름다운, 그래서 이 노을 진 시각에는 칙칙한 잿빛 돌이 아니라 찬란한 황금의 집에 박힌 듯한 그 불타는 창문 몇몇으로, 다른 창에서는 막혔던 빛이 풍성하고 아낌없이, 이 땅의 여름 풍요처럼 넘쳐흐르며 쏟아져 든다. 그러면 얼어붙었던 데드록들이 녹는다. 잎새의 그림자가 그 위에서 어른거리자 그들의 이목구비에 기이한 움직임이 인다. 한구석의 근엄한 판사는 어느새 한쪽 눈을 찡긋한다. 곤봉을 든 채 노려보는 어느 준남작은 턱에 보조개가 팬다. 돌로 된 어느 양치기 처녀의 가슴께로 빛과 온기의 얼룩 한 점이 스며드는데, 백 년 전이었다면 그녀에게 좋았을 그런 온기다. 굽 높은 신을 신은, 그녀와 무척 닮은 볼루미니아의 한 여조상은 — 그 처녀의 사건이 드리울 그림자를 꼬박 두 세기나 앞질러 던지며 — 후광을 내쏘며 성녀가 된다. 커다랗고 둥근 눈을 (그리고 거기 어울리는 다른 매력들도) 지닌 찰스 이세 궁정의 한 시녀는 빛나는 물속에 멱을 감는 듯하고, 그 물은 빛나면서 잔물결을 인다.\n\n그러나 태양의 불길은 사위어간다. 어느덧 마룻바닥은 어둑하고, 그림자는 벽을 천천히 기어오르며 데드록들을 늙음과 죽음처럼 끌어내린다. 그리고 이제, 커다란 벽난로 위에 걸린 마님의 초상화 위로 어느 늙은 나무에서 기괴한 그늘이 드리워, 그것을 창백하게 만들고 펄럭이게 하니, 마치 거대한 팔 하나가 베일이나 두건을 든 채 그녀에게 그것을 씌울 기회를 노리는 것만 같다. 벽 위로 그림자는 점점 높고 어둡게 솟아오르고 — 이제 천장에는 붉은 음영이 — 이제 불이 꺼진다.\n\n테라스에서 보면 그토록 가까워 보이던 그 모든 전망이, 엄숙하게 멀어지며 — 그토록 가까워 보이다 그렇게 변해가는 아름다운 것들 중 처음도 마지막도 아닌 채 — 아득한 환영으로 바뀐다. 옅은 안개가 피어오르고 이슬이 내리며 정원의 온갖 향긋한 내음이 공기 속에 무겁게 잠긴다. 이제 숲은 마치 저마다 한 그루 깊디깊은 나무이기라도 한 듯 거대한 덩어리로 가라앉는다. 그리고 이제 달이 떠올라 그 덩어리들을 갈라놓고, 줄기 뒤로 여기저기 가로 선을 그리며 어른거리고, 환상적으로 부서진 높은 대성당 아치 사이로 가로수길을 빛의 포석으로 만든다.\n\n이제 달은 높이 떴고, 그 어느 때보다 사람의 거처가 필요한 그 큰 저택은 생명 없는 몸뚱이 같다. 이제 그 안을 살그머니 지나며, 외로운 침실들에서 잠들었던 산 사람들을 떠올리기만 해도 — 죽은 자들은 말할 것도 없거니와 — 으스스하다. 지금이야말로 그림자의 시간, 구석마다 동굴이 되고 아래로 내딛는 걸음마다 구덩이가 되며, 색유리가 마룻바닥에 창백하게 바랜 빛깔로 비치고, 묵직한 계단 들보가 제 본래 모습만 빼고 무엇이든 다 될 수 있으며, 갑옷에는 슬그머니 움직이는 것과 좀처럼 분간하기 어려운 흐릿한 빛이 어리고, 창살 달린 투구가 그 안에 든 머리를 끔찍하게 떠올리게 하는 시간이다. 그러나 체스니 월드의 온갖 그림자 가운데, 긴 응접실에서 마님의 초상화에 드리우는 그림자야말로 가장 먼저 찾아오고 가장 나중에야 흩어진다. 이 시각 이 빛 아래에서 그것은, 흔들리는 숨결마다 그 아름다운 얼굴을 향해 위협하듯 치켜든 손으로 변한다.\n\n\"마님께서 편찮으십니다, 부인.\" 라운스웰 부인의 접견실에서 한 마부가 말한다.\n\n\"마님이 편찮으시다니! 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"저, 마님께서 지난번에 여기 계셨을 때부터 영 좋질 못하셨습니다, 부인 — 가족분들과 함께 계셨을 때 말씀이 아니라, 그러니까 잠깐 머무는 철새처럼 다녀가셨을 때 말입니다. 마님께서 평소답지 않게 거의 바깥출입을 안 하시고 방에만 꽤 오래 들어앉아 계셨습니다.\"\n\n\"체스니 월드는 말이다, 토머스.\" 가정부가 자랑스러운 흡족함을 담아 받아친다. \"마님을 일으켜 세울 게다! 세상에 이보다 더 좋은 공기도, 이보다 더 건강한 땅도 없느니라!\"\n\n토머스는 이 문제에 나름의 개인적 견해가 있을지도 모르고, 목덜미에서 관자놀이까지 매끈한 머리를 쓸어 넘기는 그 몸짓에 그 견해를 슬쩍 내비치는 듯도 하나, 그 이상 말로 드러내기는 삼간 채 하인 방으로 물러나 차가운 고기 파이와 에일로 배를 채운다.\n\n이 마부는 한결 고귀한 상어에 앞선 길잡이 물고기다. 이튿날 저녁, 레스터 경과 마님이 가장 큰 수행단을 거느리고 내려오고, 사촌들과 그 밖의 사람들이 사방 방위에서 내려온다. 그때부터 몇 주 동안, 이름도 없는 수상쩍은 사내들이 앞으로 뒤로 분주히 오간다. 두들이 지금 황금과 맥아의 소나기를 퍼붓고 있는 바로 그 지역들을 죄다 날아다니지만, 실은 안절부절못하는 기질의 인간들일 뿐 어디서도 아무 일도 하지 않는 자들이다.\n\n이런 국가적 행사에서 레스터 경은 사촌들이 쓸모 있다고 여긴다. 사냥 모임과 만찬을 함께할 사람으로 명예로운 밥 스테이블스보다 나은 자는 도무지 있을 수 없다. 여기저기 투표소며 유세장으로 말을 몰고 가 잉글랜드 편에 선 모습을 보여줄 사람으로, 잘 차려입은 다른 사촌들보다 나은 자를 찾기란 어렵다. 볼루미니아는 약간 흐리멍덩하나 혈통만은 진짜다. 그녀의 발랄한 대화, 너무 낡아서 세월의 순환 속에 도리어 거의 새것이 되어버린 그 프랑스식 수수께끼, 그 어여쁜 데드록을 만찬장에 데리고 들어가는 영광, 심지어 춤에서 그녀의 손을 잡는 특권을 높이 사는 이들이 많다. 이런 국가적 행사에서 춤은 애국적 봉사일 수 있고, 그리하여 볼루미니아는 은혜도 모르고 연금도 주지 않는 조국을 위해 줄곧 폴짝거리는 모습을 보인다.\n\n마님은 그 숱한 손님들을 접대하느라 별로 애쓰지 않으며, 여전히 몸이 좋지 않아 좀처럼 낮 늦게야 모습을 드러낸다. 그러나 음울한 만찬, 납덩이 같은 오찬, 바실리스크처럼 사람을 굳히는 무도회, 그 밖의 우울한 행사 어디서든 그녀가 그저 나타나기만 해도 분위기가 한결 풀린다. 레스터 경으로 말하자면, 그 지붕 아래 영접받는 행운을 누리는 자라면 누구든, 어느 모로 보나 부족할 게 도무지 있을 수 없다고 굳게 믿는다. 그러고는 숭고한 만족에 잠긴 채, 으리으리한 냉장고 한 대처럼 손님들 사이를 누빈다.\n\n날마다 사촌들은 먼지 속을 종종걸음 치고 길가 잔디 위를 가볍게 달려, 유세장과 투표소로 향한다 (주(州)에는 가죽 장갑과 사냥 채찍을 끼고 들고, 자치도시에는 새끼염소 가죽 장갑과 승마 지팡이를 끼고 들고서). 그러고는 날마다 보고를 가지고 돌아오면 레스터 경이 만찬 뒤에 그것을 두고 일장 연설을 늘어놓는다. 날마다 평생 할 일이라곤 없는 그 안절부절못하는 사내들은 자못 바쁜 듯한 행색을 차린다. 날마다 볼루미니아는 나라의 형편을 두고 레스터 경과 사촌끼리의 소소한 담소를 나누는데, 그 덕에 레스터 경은 볼루미니아가 자신이 생각했던 것보다 사려 깊은 여인이라고 결론짓고 싶어진다.\n\n\"우리, 어떻게 돼가나요?\" 볼루미니아 양이 두 손을 맞잡으며 말한다. \"우리, 안전한가요?\"\n\n이 무렵 그 거대한 사업은 거의 끝나가고, 두들은 며칠만 더 지나면 나라에서 자기를 거두어들일 참이다. 레스터 경은 만찬 뒤 막 긴 응접실에 모습을 드러낸 참인데, 사촌들의 구름에 둘러싸인 한 점 찬란한 별이다.\n\n\"볼루미니아.\" 손에 명단을 든 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"그런대로 해나가고 있다네.\"\n\n\"그런대로라니요!\"\n\n비록 여름철이긴 하나, 레스터 경은 저녁이면 늘 자기만의 벽난로를 피운다. 그는 평소처럼 그 곁의 가림막 친 자리에 앉아, 자못 단호하면서도 약간은 언짢은 기색으로 이렇게 되뇐다. 마치 이렇게 말하려는 듯이. 나는 보통 사람이 아니니, 내가 그런대로라고 할 때 그것을 보통의 표현으로 알아들어서는 안 되네. \"볼루미니아, 우리는 그런대로 해나가고 있다네.\"\n\n\"적어도 백부님께는 맞서는 자가 없잖아요.\" 볼루미니아가 자신만만하게 단언한다.\n\n\"없지, 볼루미니아. 이 정신 나간 나라가 여러 면에서 분별을 잃었다는 건, 말하기 가슴 아프지만, 그래도—\"\n\n\"그 정도로 미치지는 않았군요. 듣던 중 반가운 소리예요!\"\n\n볼루미니아가 문장을 마무리해준 덕에 다시 총애를 회복한다. 레스터 경은 우아하게 고개를 끄덕이며 속으로 이렇게 말하는 듯하다. \"전체로 보면 분별 있는 여인이로군, 이따금 성급하긴 해도.\"\n\n사실 이 반대 세력 문제로 말하자면, 그 어여쁜 데드록의 언급은 군더더기였다. 이런 경우 레스터 경은 늘 자기 출마를 일종의 멋들어진 대량 주문처럼 척척 내놓아 즉각 처리되도록 하기 때문이다. 자기 소유인 다른 두 개의 작은 의석은 그보다 덜 중요한 소매 주문쯤으로 취급해, 그저 사람들을 내려보내고는 거래상에게 이렇게 통고할 따름이다. \"이 재료들을 두 명의 국회의원으로 만들어, 다 되거든 집으로 보내주는 친절을 베풀어주시오.\"\n\n\"유감스럽게도 말일세, 볼루미니아, 여러 곳에서 민중이 못된 기질을 드러냈고, 정부에 대한 이 반대가 더없이 결연하고 더없이 가차 없는 양상을 띠었다네.\"\n\n\"고-약-한 것들!\" 볼루미니아가 말한다.\n\n\"심지어 말이지,\" 레스터 경이 소파며 오토만에 둘러앉은 사촌들을 흘끔 보며 말을 잇는다. \"심지어 정부가 한 파벌을 누르고 이긴 그런 곳들 가운데 많은 데서 — 아니, 사실은 대부분에서—\"\n\n(여담이지만 일러두자면, 쿠들파에게 두들파는 늘 한낱 파벌이고, 두들파에게 쿠들파 역시 똑같은 위치에 놓인다는 점을 유념하시라.)\n\n\"—심지어 그런 곳들에서도, 잉글랜드인의 명예를 위해 차마 말하기 충격스러우나, 우리 당이 어마어마한 비용을 치르지 않고는 승리하지 못했다고 자네에게 알리지 않을 수 없네. 수십만,\" 레스터 경이 점점 더한 위엄과 부풀어 오르는 분노로 사촌들을 노려보며 말한다. \"수십만 파운드라네!\"\n\n볼루미니아에게 흠이 하나 있다면, 그것은 약간 지나치게 순진한 게 흠인데, 어깨띠와 가슴 장식에는 더없이 잘 어울릴 그 순진함이 연지와 진주 목걸이에는 다소 어울리지 않는 까닭이다. 어쨌든 그 순진함에 떠밀려 그녀가 묻는다. \"뭐 하느라요?\"\n\n\"볼루미니아.\" 레스터 경이 더없이 준엄하게 나무란다. \"볼루미니아!\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아뇨, 뭐 하느라냐는 게 아니에요.\" 볼루미니아가 평소 즐기는 그 작은 비명을 지르며 외친다. \"내가 이렇게 멍청하다니! 어쩜 그리 딱한 일이냐는 뜻이었어요!\"\n\n\"다행이군.\" 레스터 경이 대꾸한다. \"어쩜 그리 딱한 일이냐는 뜻이었다니 말일세.\"\n\n볼루미니아는 서둘러 자기 의견을 밝히는데, 그 충격적인 자들은 반역자로 재판에 부쳐 당을 떠받치게 만들어야 마땅하다는 것이다.\n\n\"다행이군, 볼루미니아.\" 레스터 경이 이 누그러뜨리는 소감 따위는 아랑곳없이 되풀이한다. \"어쩜 그리 딱한 일이냐는 뜻이었다니. 유권자들에게는 망신스러운 일이지. 하지만 자네가 무심결에, 그토록 당치 않은 질문을 할 뜻은 없이 내게 '뭐 하느라'고 물었으니, 답해주겠네. 필요한 경비라네. 그리고 자네의 분별을 믿으니, 볼루미니아, 여기서든 다른 데서든 이 화제를 더는 캐묻지 말게나.\"\n\n레스터 경은 볼루미니아를 향해 짓누르는 듯한 표정을 짓는 것이 자기 본분이라 여긴다. 그 필요한 경비라는 것이 어림잡아 이백 건의 선거 청원에서 뇌물이라는 낱말과 불쾌하게 엮이리라는 소문이 바깥에 수군수군 돌고 있고, 그 탓에 짓궂은 익살꾼 몇몇이 예배 의식에서 고등 국회를 위한 통상의 기원을 빼버리고, 그 대신 매우 불건전한 상태에 놓인 신사 육백오십팔 명을 위해 회중의 기도를 청하자고 권한 까닭이다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 말이에요,\" 좀 전의 꾸지람에서 기운을 추스를 시간을 잠시 가진 뒤 볼루미니아가 말한다. \"제 생각엔 토킹혼 씨가 죽도록 일에 시달렸을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"내가 모르겠는 건,\" 레스터 경이 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 말한다. \"어찌하여 토킹혼 씨가 죽도록 일에 시달렸어야 하느냐 하는 점일세. 토킹혼 씨가 무슨 일을 맡고 있는지 나는 모르네. 그는 후보자가 아니지 않은가.\"\n\n볼루미니아는 그가 어디 고용되었을지도 모른다고 생각했던 것이다. 레스터 경은 누구한테, 무엇 하러 그랬다는 건지 알고 싶어 했다. 다시 머쓱해진 볼루미니아는, 누군가한테 — 조언을 해주고 일을 처리하라고 그랬을지 모른다고 더듬더듬 내놓는다. 레스터 경은 토킹혼 씨의 어느 의뢰인이 그의 도움을 필요로 했다는 이야기는 들은 바 없다.\n\n방석 깔린 창턱에 팔을 얹고 열린 창가에 앉아 정원에 내리는 저녁 그림자를 내다보던 데드록 마님이, 변호사의 이름이 입에 오른 뒤로 귀를 기울이는 듯하다.\n\n극도로 기운이 없어 보이는 콧수염 난 어느 나른한 사촌이 이제 자기 긴 의자에서 한마디 흘린다. 어떤 사람이 어저께 그에게 말하기를, 토킹혼이 무슨 일에 법률 의견을 내주러 그 철공소 동네로 내려갔다고 했다는 것이다. 그러니 오늘로 그 선거전이 끝났는데, 만약 토킹혼이 쿠들네 사람이 나가떨어졌다는 소식을 들고 나타나기라도 한다면 그거 참 거나하게 즐거운 일일 거라고 한다.\n\n커피를 들고 시중들던 머큐리가 이때 레스터 경에게 토킹혼 씨가 도착해 만찬을 들고 있다고 알린다. 마님은 잠깐 고개를 안쪽으로 돌렸다가, 다시 아까처럼 바깥을 내다본다.\n\n볼루미니아는 자기가 그토록 좋아하는 분이 왔다는 말에 황홀해한다. 그는 어찌나 독창적인지, 어찌나 무뚝뚝한 인간인지, 온갖 것을 다 알면서도 결코 입 밖에 내지 않는 어찌나 대단한 존재인지! 볼루미니아는 그가 틀림없이 프리메이슨일 거라고 믿어 의심치 않는다. 분명 어느 지부의 우두머리여서 짧은 앞치마를 두르고, 촛대와 흙손으로 떠받들어지는 완벽한 우상이 되어 있을 거라고 확신한다. 그 어여쁜 데드록은 지갑을 짜면서 이 활기찬 소감들을 그 앳된 태도로 늘어놓는다.\n\n\"제가 온 뒤로,\" 그녀가 덧붙인다. \"그분이 여기 한 번도 안 오셨어요. 그 변덕쟁이 때문에 정말이지 가슴이 미어질 뻔했다니까요. 그분이 죽었나 보다고 거의 마음을 굳혔을 정도예요.\"\n\n저녁의 짙어가는 어스름 탓일 수도 있고, 그녀 안의 더 어두운 어스름 탓일 수도 있으나, 마님의 얼굴에는 마치 \"차라리 그랬으면!\" 하고 생각하는 듯한 그늘이 깃든다.\n\n\"토킹혼 씨는,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"이곳에 늘 환영받는 사람이고, 어디에 있든 늘 신중한 사람일세. 무척 귀중한 인물이지, 마땅히 존경받을 만하고.\"\n\n기진맥진한 사촌은 그가 \"어-엄청 부자인 친구\"인 모양이라고 짐작한다.\n\n\"그는 이 나라에 이해관계가 걸려 있는 사람이야,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"그건 의심할 여지가 없지. 물론 보수도 후하게 받고, 거의 대등한 처지로 최고 상류사회와 어울리지.\"\n\n다들 흠칫한다. 가까이서 총성이 한 발 울린 까닭이다.\n\n\"세상에, 저게 뭐죠?\" 볼루미니아가 그 시들시들한 작은 비명을 지르며 외친다.\n\n\"쥐예요.\" 마님이 말한다. \"쥐를 쏜 거고요.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 들어오고, 등불과 양초를 든 머큐리들이 그 뒤를 따른다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"내 생각엔 아닐세. 마님, 이 어스름이 거슬리시오?\"\n\n오히려 마님은 이 어스름이 더 좋다고 한다.\n\n\"볼루미니아?\"\n\n오! 어둠 속에 앉아 두런두런 이야기 나누는 것만큼 볼루미니아에게 달콤한 일은 없다.\n\n\"그럼 그것들을 도로 가져가게.\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"토킹혼, 실례했네. 그래, 잘 지내셨는가?\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 여느 때처럼 느긋한 여유로 다가와, 마님에게 지나가는 경의를 표하고, 레스터 경과 악수한 뒤, 무언가 전할 말이 있을 때 으레 차지하는 자기 의자에 — 준남작의 자그마한 신문 탁자 맞은편 자리에 — 내려앉는다. 레스터 경은 몸이 그리 좋지 않은 마님이 저 열린 창가에서 감기라도 들까 봐 염려스럽다. 마님은 그의 마음 씀은 고맙지만, 바람을 쐬려니 차라리 거기 앉아 있겠다고 한다. 레스터 경은 일어나 그녀의 숄을 매무새 고쳐 둘러주고는 제자리로 돌아온다. 그사이 토킹혼 씨는 코담배를 한 줌 집는다.\n\n\"자,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"그 선거전은 어찌 됐나?\"\n\n\"오, 처음부터 허망했지요. 가망이 없었습니다. 저쪽에서 자기네 사람을 둘 다 당선시켰어요. 말도 안 되게 참패하셨습니다. 삼 대 일이었으니까요.\"\n\n정치적 견해를 일절 갖지 않는 것이, 아니 사실은 어떤 견해도 일절 갖지 않는 것이 토킹혼 씨의 처세요 비결이다. 그래서 그는 \"우리가\" 졌다고 하지 않고 \"당신들이\" 졌다고 말한다.\n\n레스터 경은 위엄 있게 격노한다. 볼루미니아는 그런 얘기는 난생처음 들어본다고 한다. 기진맥진한 사촌은 그게 바로 표가 — 주어지는 — 군중한테 — 표를 — 막 뿌려대면 으레 그리되는 일이라고 본다.\n\n\"아시다시피 그곳은,\" 토킹혼 씨가 다시 침묵이 깔린 가운데, 빠르게 짙어가는 어둠 속에서 말을 잇는다. \"저들이 라운스웰 부인의 아들을 내세우려던 바로 그곳입니다.\"\n\n\"그 제안은, 당시 자네가 내게 정확히 알려주었듯이, 그가 그것을 사양할 만한 합당한 안목과 분별을 지녔던 게지,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"그가 여기 이 방에서 삼십 분쯤 머물렀을 때 내비친 그 생각들을 내가 무슨 수로든 옳다고는 도무지 말 못 하겠으나, 그래도 그 결정에는 분수를 아는 마음이 있었으니, 그 점은 기꺼이 인정하는 바일세.\"\n\n\"하!\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"그렇다고 그가 이번 선거에서 무척 열성으로 뛰지 않았던 건 아니지만 말입니다.\"\n\n레스터 경이 말을 꺼내기에 앞서 숨을 헉 들이켜는 소리가 또렷이 들린다. \"내가 제대로 들었는가? 자네 지금 라운스웰 씨가 이번 선거에서 무척 열성으로 뛰었다고 말한 겐가?\"\n\n\"유난스러울 만큼 열성이었지요.\" \"누구에게 맞서서—\"\n\n\"오, 그렇고말고요, 어르신께 맞서서지요. 그는 무척 훌륭한 연설가입니다. 꾸밈없고 힘이 있지요. 타격을 입힐 만한 영향을 끼쳤고, 세력도 막강합니다. 일의 실무적인 대목에서는 모든 걸 자기 뜻대로 휘어잡았어요.\"\n\n비록 아무도 그를 볼 수는 없으나, 레스터 경이 위엄 있게 노려보고 있다는 것은 좌중 전체에 역력하다.\n\n\"게다가 그는,\" 토킹혼 씨가 마무리 짓듯 말한다. \"자기 아들에게서 큰 도움을 받았지요.\"\n\n\"아들에게서라니, 여보게?\" 레스터 경이 소름 끼치는 정중함으로 되묻는다.\n\n\"아들에게서요.\"\n\n\"우리 마님 시중을 드는 그 젊은 여자와 혼인하고 싶어 하던 그 아들 말인가?\"\n\n\"바로 그 아들입니다. 그에게는 아들이 하나뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대,\" 레스터 경이 콧김을 내뿜는 소리가 들리고 노려보는 기운이 느껴지던 그 무시무시한 침묵 끝에 입을 연다. \"그렇다면 내 명예를 걸고, 내 목숨을 걸고, 내 명망과 신조를 걸고 말하건대, 사회의 수문이 활짝 터져버렸고, 그 물이 — 어 — 만물을 한데 붙들어주는 그 결속의 틀, 그 경계표를 죄다 지워버렸도다!\"\n\n사촌들 사이에서 일제히 분노가 터져 나온다. 볼루미니아는, 그러니까 말이에요, 권력 있는 누군가가 나서서 무언가 강력한 조치를 취할 때가 정말로 무르익었다고 생각한다. 기진맥진한 사촌은 — 나라가 — 망조가 — 장애물 경마 속도로 — 라고 생각한다.\n\n\"청컨대,\" 레스터 경이 숨이 가쁜 상태로 말한다. \"이 일에 관해 더는 왈가왈부하지 말도록 하세. 왈가왈부는 군더더기일 따름이지. 마님, 그 젊은 여자와 관련하여 한 가지 제안을 드리고 싶은데—\"\n\n\"저는 그 아이를 내보낼 뜻이,\" 마님이 창가에서 낮으나 단호한 어조로 말한다. \"없습니다.\"\n\n\"내 말은 그게 아니었소,\" 레스터 경이 대꾸한다. \"그리 말해주니 반갑소. 내가 제안하고 싶은 건, 당신이 그 아이를 후원할 만하다고 여기니, 그 위험한 손아귀에서 그 아이를 지키도록 당신의 영향력을 발휘하라는 것이오. 그런 자들과 어울리는 일이 그 아이의 본분과 신조에 어떤 횡포가 될지 일러주고, 더 나은 운명을 위해 그 아이를 지켜낼 수 있을 게요. 머잖아 좋은 때가 오면 체스니 월드에서 남편감을 만날 수 있을 거라고, 그 사람이라면 그 아이를—\" 레스터 경은 잠시 생각하다가 덧붙인다. \"그 아이를 제 조상의 제단에서 끌어내지는 않을 거라고 일러줄 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n이런 말들을 그는 아내에게 건넬 때면 늘 한결같은 정중함과 공손함으로 내놓는다. 그녀는 그저 고개만 살짝 끄덕여 답할 따름이다. 달이 떠오르고, 그녀가 앉은 자리에는 차갑고 창백한 빛이 가느다란 줄기로 흘러들어, 그 속에 그녀의 머리가 비친다.\n\n\"그래도 주목할 만한 점은,\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"그런 자들이 나름대로 무척 자존심이 세다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"자존심이?\" 레스터 경이 제 귀를 의심한다.\n\n\"제 짐작에는, 그런 처지에서 그 처녀가 체스니 월드에 그대로 남는다면, 그 처녀가 그들을 버리는 게 아니라 도리어 그들이 죄다 제 발로 그 처녀를 — 그래요, 연인까지 통째로 — 버린다 해도 저는 놀라지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"허!\" 레스터 경이 떨리는 목소리로 말한다. \"허! 자네야 잘 알겠지, 토킹혼 씨. 자네가 그자들 틈에 있어봤으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 레스터 경,\" 변호사가 대꾸한다. \"저는 그저 사실을 말씀드리는 겁니다. 아니, 한 가지 이야기를 들려드릴 수도 있는데요 — 데드록 마님께서 허락해주신다면 말이지요.\"\n\n마님의 고갯짓이 그것을 허락하고, 볼루미니아는 넋을 잃는다. 이야기라니! 오, 그가 드디어 무언가를 들려주려나 보다! 그 속에 유령이 나오기를, 볼루미니아는 바란다.\n\n\"아니지요. 진짜 살과 피를 가진 사람입니다.\" 토킹혼 씨가 잠깐 멈췄다가, 평소의 단조로움에 약간의 강세를 덧붙여 되뇐다. \"진짜 살과 피를 가진 사람이지요, 데드록 양. 레스터 경, 이 자초지종은 제가 최근에야 알게 된 것입니다. 아주 간략하지요. 제가 앞서 한 말을 잘 보여주는 사례입니다. 이름은 당분간 밝히지 않겠습니다. 데드록 마님께서 저를 무례하다 여기지 않으시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n낮게 사위어가는 벽난로의 불빛으로, 그가 달빛 쪽을 바라보고 있는 모습이 보인다. 달빛으로, 데드록 마님이 미동도 없이 앉아 있는 모습이 보인다.\n\n\"이 라운스웰 씨와 같은 고장 사람이, 듣자 하니 꼭 같은 처지에 놓인 어떤 사내가, 어느 귀부인의 눈길을 끈 딸 하나를 둔 행운을 누렸습니다. 정말로 대단한 귀부인을 두고 하는 말입니다. 그저 그 사내 보기에 대단한 게 아니라, 레스터 경처럼 그런 지체 높은 신사와 혼인한 분이지요.\"\n\n레스터 경이 거들먹거리듯 \"그래, 토킹혼 씨\" 하고 말하는데, 그렇다면 그 부인은 어느 철공소 주인의 눈에는 정말이지 어마어마한 도덕적 크기로 비쳤을 게 틀림없다는 뜻이 담겨 있다.\n\n\"그 부인은 부유하고 아름다웠으며, 그 처녀를 마음에 들어 해 무척 다정하게 대했고, 늘 곁에 두었습니다. 그런데 이 부인은 그 온갖 고귀함 아래에 한 가지 비밀을 간직하고 있었으니, 여러 해 동안 지켜온 비밀이었지요. 사실인즉, 그녀는 젊은 시절 어느 젊은 난봉꾼과 — 그자는 군대의 대위였습니다 — 혼인하기로 약혼한 적이 있었는데, 그자와 얽힌 일치고 좋게 끝난 게 하나도 없었습니다. 그녀는 끝내 그자와 혼인하지 않았으나, 그자를 아비로 둔 아이를 하나 낳았지요.\"\n\n벽난로 불빛으로, 그가 달빛 쪽을 바라보고 있는 모습이 보인다. 달빛으로, 데드록 마님이 옆얼굴을 보인 채 미동도 없이 앉아 있는 모습이 보인다.\n\n\"그 군대의 대위가 죽었으니, 그녀는 자신이 안전하다고 믿었습니다. 하지만 굳이 여러분을 번거롭게 하지 않아도 될 일련의 정황이 발각으로 이어졌지요. 제가 전해 들은 바로는, 그것은 어느 날 그녀가 불시에 허를 찔려 스스로 저지른 한순간의 경솔함에서 비롯되었는데, 이는 우리 가운데 가장 굳센 사람이라도 (그녀는 무척 굳센 사람이었습니다) 언제나 빈틈없이 경계하기란 얼마나 어려운 일인지를 잘 보여줍니다. 짐작하시겠지만 집안에 큰 풍파와 경악이 일었지요. 레스터 경, 그 남편의 비통함은 어떠했을지 상상에 맡기겠습니다. 하지만 그게 지금 요점은 아닙니다. 라운스웰 씨와 같은 고장 사람은 그 사실이 드러났다는 소식을 듣고는, 제 딸이 제 눈앞에서 발에 짓밟히도록 내버려두지 않을 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 제 딸이 후원받고 떠받들어지도록 그냥 두지 않았습니다. 그 자존심이 어찌나 대단했던지, 그는 마치 치욕과 망신에서 끄집어내듯 분연히 딸을 데려가 버렸지요. 부인이 베푼 그 황송한 호의가 자기와 제 딸에게 얼마나 영예로운 일인지는 조금도 헤아리지 못했습니다. 손톱만큼도요. 그는 그 딸의 처지에 분개했으니, 마치 그 부인이 평민 중에서도 가장 천한 평민이기라도 한 것처럼 굴었지요. 이게 그 이야기입니다. 데드록 마님께서 그 고통스러운 내용을 너그러이 봐주시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n그 이야기의 됨됨이를 두고 갖가지 의견이 나오는데, 볼루미니아의 의견과는 다소간 어긋난다. 그 어여쁜 젊은 처녀는 그런 부인이 세상에 있었으리라고는 도무지 믿지 못해, 그 사연 전부를 문턱에서부터 물리쳐버린다. 다수는 기진맥진한 사촌의 소감 쪽으로 기우는데, 그것을 몇 마디로 줄이면 — \"남의 일이지 — 라운스웰네 그 빌어먹을 고장 사람\" 이다. 레스터 경은 대체로 마음속으로 와트 타일러까지 거슬러 올라가서는, 자기 나름의 구상에 따라 일련의 사건을 배열해본다. * 와트 타일러: 1381년 영국 농민 반란을 이끈 지도자. 레스터 경은 모든 하층민의 반항을 이 옛 반란과 한통속으로 본다.\n\n도통 대화랄 게 별로 없다. 다른 데서 그 필요한 경비가 들기 시작한 뒤로 체스니 월드에서는 늦은 시각까지 깨어 지내왔고, 가족만 단출하게 있는 밤은 여러 날 만에 오늘이 처음인 까닭이다. 레스터 경이 토킹혼 씨에게 양초를 가져오라고 종을 울려달라 청할 무렵이면 열 시가 지난 뒤다. 그제야 달빛 줄기는 부풀어 호수를 이루었고, 그제야 데드록 마님이 처음으로 몸을 움직여 일어서더니, 물 한 잔을 마시려 탁자 쪽으로 다가온다. 촛불 빛에 박쥐처럼 눈을 깜빡이는 사촌들이 물을 건네려 우르르 몰려든다. 볼루미니아는 (더 나은 게 있다면 늘 받아들일 채비가 되어 있는지라) 또 한 잔을 받아 아주 조심스레 한 모금 홀짝이는 것으로 흡족해한다. 데드록 마님은, 우아하고 침착하게, 흠모하는 눈길의 배웅을 받으며, 그 요정 같은 처녀 곁을 지나 긴 회랑을 따라 천천히 멀어져 가는데, 대조라는 측면에서 보면 그 처녀를 조금도 돋보이게 해주지 않는다.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 천천히 올라왔는데도 계단을 오르느라 숨이 약간 가빠진 채 탑실에 들어선다. 무언가 중대한 일을 마음에서 털어내고, 자기 특유의 속내 깊은 방식으로 흡족해하는 듯한 기색이 얼굴에 떠올라 있다. 이토록 엄격하고 철저하게 자신을 억누르는 사람을 두고 의기양양하다고 한다면, 그가 사랑이나 감상이나 어떤 낭만적 약점에 시달린다고 짐작하는 것만큼이나 큰 모욕이 되리라. 그는 차분하게 만족스러워한다. 한 손으로 핏줄 불거진 손목을 느슨하게 쥐어 등 뒤로 돌리고 소리 없이 오르내리는 그의 몸짓에는, 어쩌면 평소보다 한층 강해진 권력의 감각이 어려 있는지도 모른다.\n\n방 안에는 널찍한 책상이 놓여 있고, 그 위에는 서류가 꽤 수북이 쌓여 있다. 초록 등이 켜져 있고, 독서용 안경은 책상에 놓여 있으며, 안락의자는 그 앞으로 바싹 당겨져 있다. 잠자리에 들기 전에 한 시간 남짓 이 산적한 일거리에 시간을 쏟을 작정이었던 것처럼 보인다. 그러나 마침 그는 일할 기분이 아니다. 살펴봐 달라고 기다리는 서류를 한 번 흘끗 보고는—책상 위로 머리를 잔뜩 숙인 채, 노인의 눈은 밤이 되면 인쇄물이나 글씨를 보는 데 영 시원찮으니—그는 프랑스식 창문을 열고 옥상 납판 위로 걸어 나간다. 거기서 그는 다시 똑같은 자세로 천천히 오르내리며, 이토록 냉정한 사람에게도 가라앉힐 것이 있다면, 아래층에서 들려준 그 이야기에서 마음을 가라앉힌다.\n\n토킹혼 씨처럼 식견 있는 사람들이 별빛 아래 탑 꼭대기를 거닐며 하늘을 올려다보고 거기서 제 운명을 읽던 시절이 한때 있었다. 오늘 밤 무수한 별이 보이지만, 그 광채는 달의 찬란함에 가려 빛을 잃는다. 그가 옥상 납판 위를 규칙적으로 돌고 또 돌며 제 별을 찾고 있다면, 이렇게 빛바랜 모습으로 지상에 비친 것을 보건대 그건 어지간히 창백한 별이리라. 그가 제 운명을 더듬어 짚고 있다면, 그건 어쩌면 그의 손에 더 가까운 다른 글자로 적혀 있을지도 모른다.\n\n그가 두 눈을 십중팔구 제 생각보다 한참 높은 곳에—땅 위로 높이 솟은 만큼이나 높은 곳에—두고 옥상 납판을 거니는데, 창문 앞을 지나다 제 눈과 마주친 두 눈에 문득 발길이 멎는다. 그의 방 천장은 다소 낮고, 창문 맞은편에 있는 문의 윗부분은 유리로 되어 있다. 안쪽에 나사천을 댄 문도 하나 있지만, 밤 공기가 따뜻해서 그는 위층에 올라올 때 그 문을 닫지 않았다. 그의 눈과 마주친 그 두 눈은 바깥 복도에서 유리 너머로 안을 들여다보고 있다. 그는 그 눈을 잘 안다. 데드록 마님을 알아본 순간만큼, 그의 얼굴에 피가 이토록 갑작스럽고 붉게 솟구친 적은 오랜 세월 없었다.\n\n그가 방 안으로 들어서자 그녀도 따라 들어와, 등 뒤로 두 문을 모두 닫는다. 그녀의 두 눈에는 거친 동요가—두려움인가, 분노인가?—서려 있다. 몸가짐도 그 밖의 모든 것도, 두 시간 전 아래층에서 보던 그 모습 그대로다.\n\n이제 그것은 두려움인가, 분노인가? 그는 확신할 수 없다. 둘 다 그만큼 창백할 수도, 둘 다 그만큼 골똘할 수도 있으니.\n\n\"데드록 마님?\"\n\n그녀는 처음에 입을 열지 않고, 책상 옆 안락의자에 천천히 주저앉고 나서도 말이 없다. 두 사람은 두 폭의 그림처럼 서로를 바라본다.\n\n\"왜 그토록 많은 사람에게 제 이야기를 하셨죠?\"\n\n\"데드록 마님, 제가 그 일을 안다는 것을 마님께 알려드려야 했습니다.\"\n\n\"언제부터 알고 계셨나요?\"\n\n\"오래전부터 의심했고, 확실히 안 것은 얼마 되지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"몇 달 전부터?\"\n\n\"며칠 전부터입니다.\"\n그는 한 손은 의자 등받이에, 다른 손은 구식 조끼와 셔츠 주름 장식에 얹고 그녀 앞에 서 있다. 그녀가 결혼한 뒤로 어느 때든 그녀 앞에 서던 바로 그 모습 그대로다. 똑같은 격식 차린 정중함, 차라리 반항이라 해도 좋을 똑같은 차분한 공손함. 사람 전체가 똑같이 어둡고 차가운 물체로, 똑같은 거리에 있으며, 그 무엇으로도 결코 줄어든 적이 없다.\n\n\"그 가엾은 아가씨에 관한 이야기는 사실인가요?\"\n\n그는 질문을 잘 알아듣지 못한 듯 머리를 살짝 기울여 앞으로 내민다.\n\n\"당신이 들려준 이야기 말이에요. 그게 사실인가요? 그 아이의 친지들도 제 이야기를 아나요? 벌써 온 동네에 소문이 났나요? 담벼락에 분필로 휘갈겨지고 거리에서 떠들어대고 있나요?\"\n\n이런! 분노와 두려움과 수치심. 셋이 한꺼번에 다투고 있다. 이 여인은 이 끓어오르는 격정을 억누르는 데 얼마나 대단한 힘을 지녔는가! 토킹혼 씨는 그녀를 바라보며 이런 생각에 잠기는데, 그녀의 눈길 아래 그의 헝클어진 잿빛 눈썹이 평소보다 머리카락 한 올만큼 더 좁혀진다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 데드록 마님. 그건 가정에서 꾸민 이야기로, 레스터 경께서 무심코 그 문제를 워낙 거만하게 다루신 데서 비롯된 겁니다. 하지만 그들이—우리가 아는 것을—알게 된다면, 그건 실제 일이 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그들은 아직 모르나요?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"그들이 알기 전에 그 가엾은 아이가 다치지 않도록 제가 구할 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드려, 데드록 마님,\" 토킹혼 씨가 대답한다. \"그 점에 관해서는 만족스러운 의견을 드릴 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n그러면서 그는 그녀의 가슴속에서 벌어지는 갈등을 지켜보며 골똘한 호기심에 가득 차 생각한다. \"이 여인의 힘과 기개는 참으로 놀랍구나!\"\n\n\"이보세요,\" 그녀가 말한다. 또렷하게 말하려고 가진 기력을 다해 잠시 입술을 다물어야 했다. \"더 분명히 말씀드리죠. 당신의 가정 자체를 따지려는 게 아니에요. 라운스웰 씨를 이곳에서 보았을 때, 저는 이미 그걸 예감했고 당신만큼이나 그 진실을 절절히 느꼈으니까요. 만약 그가 제 본모습을 꿰뚫어 볼 수만 있었다면, 그 가엾은 아이가 한순간이라도—더없이 순결한 처지였는데도—저의 대단하고 고귀한 후원의 대상이 되었던 것만으로 더럽혀졌다고 여기리라는 걸, 저는 너무도 잘 알았어요. 하지만 저는 그 아이에게 마음을 쓰고 있어요. 아니, 차라리 이렇게 말해야겠군요—이제 이곳에 속한 사람이 아니니—쓰고 있었어요. 당신이 발밑에 짓밟힌 여인에게 그 정도의 배려라도 베풀어 그것을 기억해 주신다면, 그 아이는 당신의 자비를 깊이 새길 거예요.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 깊이 귀를 기울이면서도, 자신을 낮추는 듯 어깨를 한 번 으쓱하며 이 말을 흘려보내고는 눈썹을 조금 더 좁힌다.\n\n\"당신은 제 폭로에 대비하게 해 주셨고, 그것에 대해서도 감사드려요. 제게 요구하실 게 있나요? 당신의 발견이 정확하다고 제가 증명함으로써, 제 남편이 풀려나는 데—그분의 자유를 되찾는 데—제가 풀어드릴 수 있는 권리나, 덜어드릴 수 있는 어떤 책임이나 수고가 있나요? 당신이 불러주는 대로, 지금 이 자리에서 무엇이든 쓰겠어요. 그럴 준비가 되어 있어요.\"\n\n그러고도 남을 여자다, 변호사는 그녀가 펜을 쥔 단호한 손을 지켜보며 생각한다!\n\n\"수고를 끼치지 않겠습니다, 데드록 마님. 부디 그러지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"아시다시피 저는 오래전부터 이걸 예상해 왔어요. 저 자신을 아끼고 싶지도, 누가 아껴주길 바라지도 않아요. 당신이 이미 한 것보다 더 나쁜 짓을 제게 할 수는 없어요. 이제 남은 걸 하세요.\"\n\n\"데드록 마님, 할 일은 아무것도 없습니다. 말씀이 끝나시면 몇 마디 여쭙겠습니다.\"\n\n이제 서로를 살필 필요는 끝났어야 마땅하건만, 두 사람은 그러는 내내 서로를 살피고 있고, 별들은 열린 창문 너머로 두 사람을 모두 지켜본다. 저 멀리 달빛 속에 숲의 들판이 잠잠히 누워 있고, 너른 저택은 비좁은 그곳만큼이나 고요하다. 그 비좁은 곳! 토킹혼이라는 존재의 숱한 비밀에 마지막 큰 비밀 하나를 보탤 운명을 짊어진 무덤 파는 자와 삽은, 이 평화로운 밤 어디에 있는가? 그 사람은 이미 태어났는가, 그 삽은 이미 벼려졌는가? 여름밤 지켜보는 별들 아래서 곱씹어 볼 만한 묘한 질문이요, 어쩌면 곱씹어 보지 않는 편이 더 묘한 질문이다.\n\n\"후회나 가책, 혹은 제 어떤 감정에 관해서는,\" 데드록 마님이 이윽고 말을 잇는다. \"한마디도 하지 않겠어요. 제가 벙어리가 아니라면, 당신은 귀머거리겠죠. 그건 그냥 흘려보내요. 당신의 귀에 들려줄 게 아니니까요.\"\n\n그가 항변하는 시늉을 하지만, 그녀는 경멸하듯 손을 휘저어 그것을 쓸어내 버린다.\n\n\"그것과는 전혀 다른 일을 말씀드리러 왔어요. 제 보석은 모두 제자리에 보관되어 있어요. 거기서 찾으실 거예요. 제 드레스도 그래요. 제가 가진 귀중품도 죄다 그래요. 현금을 조금 몸에 지녔다고 전해 주세요. 그렇지만 큰 금액은 아니에요. 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 제 옷은 입지 않았어요. 이제부터 영영 자취를 감추려고 떠났다고요. 이걸 알려 주세요. 당신께 다른 책임은 남기지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 데드록 마님,\" 토킹혼 씨가 조금도 동요하지 않고 말한다. \"마님 말씀을 제가 제대로 이해했는지 모르겠습니다. 마님께서 원하시는 건—\"\n\n\"이곳 모든 사람에게서 자취를 감추는 거예요. 저는 오늘 밤 체스니 월드를 떠나요. 지금 당장 떠나요.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 고개를 가로젓는다. 그녀가 일어선다. 그러나 그는 의자 등받이에서도, 구식 조끼와 셔츠 주름 장식에서도 손을 떼지 않은 채 고개를 가로젓는다.\n\n\"뭐죠? 제가 말한 대로 떠나지 못한다고요?\"\n\n\"네, 데드록 마님,\" 그가 아주 차분하게 대답한다.\n\n\"제가 사라지면 얼마나 후련해질지 모르세요? 이곳에 드리운 오점과 얼룩을, 그게 어디에 있고 누구인지를 잊으셨나요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 데드록 마님. 결코 그렇지 않습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 대꾸할 가치도 없다는 듯 안쪽 문으로 다가가 손잡이를 잡는데, 그가 손이나 발 하나 움직이지 않고 목소리도 높이지 않은 채 말한다. \"데드록 마님, 부디 멈추시고 제 말을 들으십시오. 그러지 않으면 마님이 계단에 닿기도 전에 제가 비상종을 울려 온 집안을 깨우겠습니다. 그러면 저는 이 집안의 모든 손님과 하인 앞에서, 모든 남자와 여자 앞에서 다 털어놓아야 합니다.\"\n\n그가 그녀를 굴복시켰다. 그녀는 비틀거리고, 떨면서, 어쩔 줄 모르고 손을 머리에 가져다 댄다. 다른 누구라면 사소한 기색이겠으나, 토킹혼 씨처럼 노련한 눈이 이런 인물에게서 한순간의 망설임을 읽어낼 때, 그는 그 가치를 속속들이 안다.\n\n그가 곧바로 다시 말한다. \"부디 제 말을 들으십시오, 데드록 마님.\" 그러면서 그녀가 일어났던 의자를 가리킨다. 그녀가 머뭇거리자 그가 다시 가리키고, 그녀는 자리에 앉는다.\n\n\"우리 사이의 관계가 딱한 사정이라는 건 압니다, 데드록 마님. 하지만 제가 만든 것이 아니니, 그에 대해 사과하지는 않겠습니다. 제가 레스터 경에 대해 어떤 위치에 있는지는 마님께서 워낙 잘 아시니, 진작부터 제가 마님 눈에 이 발견을 할 만한 당연한 사람으로 비쳤으리라고 어렵잖이 짐작합니다.\"\n\n\"이보세요,\" 이제 두 눈을 바닥에 고정한 채 시선을 들지 않고 그녀가 대꾸한다. \"그냥 떠나게 두는 편이 나았을 거예요. 저를 붙잡지 않는 게 훨씬 나았을 거라고요. 더 드릴 말씀은 없어요.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 데드록 마님. 들어 주실 말씀을 조금만 더 보태겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 창가에서 듣겠어요. 여기서는 숨이 막혀서요.\"\n\n그녀가 그쪽으로 걸어가자, 그가 던지는 시샘 어린 눈길에 한순간의 불안이 드러난다. 그녀가 창밖으로 뛰어내려 난간과 처마 돌림띠에 부딪히며 아래쪽 테라스에서 목숨을 끊으려는 생각을 품었을지도 모른다는 불안이다. 그러나 그녀가 아무것에도 기대지 않고 창가에 서서 별을 바라보는—올려다보지 않고, 하늘 낮게 걸린 그 별들을 음울하게 내다보는—모습을 잠시 살피고는 그는 안심한다. 그녀가 움직인 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 그는 그녀의 약간 뒤쪽에 선다.\n\n\"데드록 마님, 저는 제 앞에 놓인 길에 관해 아직 스스로 만족할 만한 결정에 이르지 못했습니다. 무엇을 해야 할지, 다음에 어떻게 처신해야 할지 분명치 않습니다. 그동안에는 부디 마님께서 오랫동안 지켜오신 그 비밀을 그대로 지키시고, 제가 그것을 함께 지키더라도 의아해하지 마시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n그가 말을 멈추지만, 그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않는다.\n\n\"송구합니다, 데드록 마님. 이건 중요한 문제입니다. 제 말에 귀 기울여 주고 계신지요?\"\n\n\"그래요.\"\n\n\"감사합니다. 제가 본 마님의 강인한 성품으로 미루어 진작 알았어야 했습니다. 그 질문은 드리지 말았어야 했는데, 저는 한 걸음 한 걸음 나아가면서 제 발판을 확실히 다지는 버릇이 있어서요. 이 불행한 사건에서 유일하게 고려할 분은 레스터 경입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서,\" 저 먼 별들에서 음울한 시선을 거두지 않은 채 그녀가 나직이 묻는다. \"그분의 집에 저를 붙잡아 두는 거죠?\"\n\n\"바로 그분이 고려할 대상이기 때문입니다. 데드록 마님, 레스터 경께서 대단히 자존심 강한 분이고, 마님께 대한 의지가 절대적이며, 마님이 그분의 아내라는 높은 자리에서 추락하는 것을 보느니 차라리 저 달이 하늘에서 떨어지는 편이 그분을 덜 놀라게 하리라는 것은, 굳이 제가 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요.\"\n\n그녀는 가쁘고 무겁게 숨을 쉬지만, 그가 일찍이 그녀의 가장 성대한 손님들 한복판에서 보았던 그 모습 그대로 한 치도 움츠러들지 않고 서 있다.\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리는데, 데드록 마님, 지금 제가 쥔 이 사건만 한 무언가가 없었다면, 저는 차라리 제 힘과 제 두 손으로 이 영지에서 가장 오래된 나무를 뿌리째 뽑아내기를 바랐을 겁니다. 마님이 레스터 경에게 쥔 영향력과, 마님에 대한 레스터 경의 신뢰와 믿음을 흔드는 것보다는요. 그리고 지금조차도, 이 사건을 손에 쥐고도 저는 망설이고 있습니다. 그분이 의심하실까 봐가 아닙니다(그건 그분에게조차 불가능한 일이지요). 그 어떤 것으로도 그분에게 그 충격을 대비시킬 수 없기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 달아나는 것으로도 안 되나요?\" 그녀가 대꾸한다. \"다시 생각해 보세요.\"\n\n\"마님이 달아나신다면, 데드록 마님, 그 진실 전체가, 아니 진실의 백 배가 사방팔방으로 퍼질 겁니다. 단 하루도 가문의 명예를 지키는 게 불가능할 겁니다. 생각조차 할 수 없는 일입니다.\"\n\n그의 대답에는 어떤 항변도 허용하지 않는 조용한 단호함이 깃들어 있다.\n\n\"제가 레스터 경을 유일한 고려 대상이라고 말씀드릴 때, 그분과 가문의 명예는 하나입니다. 레스터 경과 준남작 작위, 레스터 경과 체스니 월드, 레스터 경과 그분의 조상들과 세습 재산은\"—여기서 토킹혼 씨는 자못 무미건조하게—\"굳이 마님께 말씀드릴 것도 없이, 데드록 마님, 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"계속하세요!\"\n\n\"그러니,\" 토킹혼 씨가 느릿느릿한 특유의 어조로 사건을 짚어가며 말한다. \"고려할 게 많습니다. 가능하다면 이 일은 덮어야 합니다. 그런데 레스터 경이 정신이 나가거나 임종의 자리에 누우신다면, 어떻게 덮을 수 있겠습니까? 제가 내일 아침 그분께 이 충격을 안긴다면, 그분의 그 갑작스러운 변화를 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠습니까? 무엇이 그것을 불러왔다 하겠습니까? 무엇이 두 분을 갈라놓았다 하겠습니까? 데드록 마님, 담벼락의 낙서와 거리의 외침이 곧장 들이닥칠 것이고, 마님께서 기억하셔야 할 것은, 그것이 마님 한 분께만(이 일에서 저는 마님을 도무지 고려할 수 없습니다만) 영향을 미치는 게 아니라 마님의 남편께, 데드록 마님, 마님의 남편께 영향을 미친다는 점입니다.\"\n\n말을 이어갈수록 그는 더 분명해지지만, 한 톨만큼도 더 격앙되거나 활기를 띠지는 않는다.\n\n\"이 사건을 바라볼 또 다른 관점이 있습니다.\" 그가 말을 잇는다. \"레스터 경은 마님께 거의 미혹에 가까울 만큼 헌신적입니다. 우리가 아는 것을 알게 되더라도, 그분은 그 미혹을 떨쳐내지 못하실 수도 있습니다. 극단적인 경우를 들고 있습니다만, 그럴 수도 있다는 겁니다. 만약 그렇다면, 그분은 아무것도 모르시는 편이 낫습니다. 상식적으로도 낫고, 그분께도 낫고, 저에게도 낫지요. 저는 이 모든 것을 헤아려야 하고, 그것들이 한데 얽혀 결정을 무척 어렵게 만듭니다.\"\n\n그녀는 한마디 말도 없이 똑같은 별들을 내다보며 서 있다. 별들이 빛을 잃기 시작하고, 그녀는 마치 그 차가움이 자기를 얼리기라도 하는 듯한 모습이다.\n\n\"제 경험이 가르쳐 주는바,\" 이제 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 기계처럼 사무적으로 그 문제를 따져가며 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"제 경험이 가르쳐 주는바, 데드록 마님, 제가 아는 대부분의 사람들은 결혼 따위 하지 않는 편이 훨씬 나았을 겁니다. 그게 그들이 겪는 고민의 사분의 삼의 뿌리지요. 레스터 경이 결혼하실 때도 저는 그렇게 생각했고, 그 뒤로도 늘 그렇게 생각해 왔습니다. 그 이야기는 그만하지요. 이제 저는 정황에 따라 처신해야 합니다. 그동안 마님께서는 부디 입을 다물어 주시고, 저도 그리하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제 지금의 삶을 그대로 끌고 가면서, 당신 마음 내키는 대로 하루하루 그 고통을 견디라는 건가요?\" 여전히 먼 하늘을 바라보며 그녀가 묻는다.\n\n\"네, 유감스럽지만 그렇습니다, 데드록 마님.\"\n\n\"제가 그렇게 형틀에 묶여 있어야 한다고, 그게 필요하다고 보시나요?\"\n\n\"제가 권하는 것이 필요하다는 건 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"제가 그 비참한 기만을 그토록 오래 연기해 온 이 화려한 무대 위에 그대로 남아 있다가, 당신이 신호를 보내면 그 무대가 제 발밑에서 무너져 내린다는 거군요?\" 그녀가 천천히 말한다.\n\n\"미리 알려드리지 않고 그러지는 않겠습니다, 데드록 마님. 저는 마님께 먼저 경고하지 않고는 어떤 조치도 취하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 마치 외운 것을 되뇌듯, 혹은 잠결에 읊조리듯 모든 질문을 던진다.\n\n\"우리는 여느 때처럼 만나는 건가요?\"\n\n\"여느 때와 똑같이요, 그래 주신다면요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는 그토록 숱한 세월 그래왔듯 제 죄를 숨기는 거고요?\"\n\n\"그토록 숱한 세월 그래오셨듯이요. 제가 먼저 그런 말은 꺼내지 말았어야 했습니다, 데드록 마님. 하지만 이제 한 가지 일러둘 것은, 마님의 비밀이 예전보다 더 무거울 것도 없고, 예전보다 더 나빠지거나 더 나아진 것도 없다는 점입니다. 저는 그것을 확실히 압니다만, 우리는 한 번도 서로를 온전히 믿은 적이 없는 것 같군요.\"\n\n그녀는 얼마간 똑같이 얼어붙은 자세로 골똘히 서 있다가 묻는다. \"오늘 밤 더 하실 말씀이 있나요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 토킹혼 씨가 두 손을 가만히 비비며 차근차근 대답한다. \"제 조치에 마님께서 동의하신다는 걸 확인하고 싶습니다, 데드록 마님.\"\n\n\"확인하셔도 좋아요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 그리고 끝으로, 업무상 신중을 기하는 차원에서 한 가지 일러둘 것은—혹여 레스터 경과의 어떤 대화에서 이 사실을 되짚을 필요가 생길 경우를 대비해서요—이번 면담 내내 제가 유일하게 고려하는 것은 레스터 경의 심정과 명예, 그리고 가문의 평판이라고 분명히 밝혔다는 점입니다. 사정이 허락했다면 저는 데드록 마님 역시 비중 있게 고려하게 되어 기뻤겠습니다만, 안타깝게도 사정이 그러지 못하는군요.\"\n\n\"당신의 충직함은 제가 증언해 드리지요, 이보세요.\"\n\n그 말을 하기 전에도 후에도 그녀는 골똘히 잠겨 있다가, 마침내 몸을 움직여, 타고난 위엄과 몸에 밴 기품 어느 것도 흔들림 없이 문 쪽으로 돌아선다. 토킹혼 씨는 어제 그랬을 법한, 혹은 십 년 전에 그랬을 법한 그대로 두 문을 열고, 그녀가 나갈 때 구식으로 허리 굽혀 인사한다. 어둠 속으로 사라지는 그 아름다운 얼굴에서 그가 받은 눈길은 예사로운 눈길이 아니요, 그의 정중함에 답하는 그 몸짓은 비록 아주 미미할망정 예사로운 몸짓이 아니다. 그러나 혼자 남아 곰곰이 헤아려 보건대, 그 여인은 예사롭지 않은 자제를 자기 자신에게 강요하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n만약 그 여인이 자기 방을 거니는 모습을 보았다면, 그는 그것을 한층 더 잘 알았으리라. 뒤로 젖힌 얼굴에서 머리카락이 거칠게 흩날리고, 두 손은 머리 뒤로 깍지를 끼었으며, 몸은 고통에 비틀린 듯한 그 모습을. 그 여인이 이렇게 몇 시간을, 지치지도 쉬지도 않고 오르내리는 모습을, 그리고 유령의 길 위로 그 충실한 발소리가 뒤따르는 모습을 보았다면, 그는 더더욱 그렇게 생각했으리라. 그러나 그는 이제 싸늘해진 공기를 가두어 막고, 창문 커튼을 치고, 잠자리에 들어 잠든다. 그리고 정녕 별들이 스러지고 핏기 없는 낮이 탑실 안을 들여다보며 더없이 늙어 보이는 그를 발견할 때, 그는 마치 무덤 파는 자와 삽이 둘 다 이미 부름을 받아 곧 흙을 파헤치기라도 할 것 같은 모습이다.\n\n그 똑같은 핏기 없는 낮이, 회개한 백성을 위풍당당하게 거들먹대며 사면하는 꿈에 잠긴 레스터 경을 들여다본다. 또 여러 공직에—주로 봉급을 받는 자리에—올라선 사촌들을 들여다본다. 또 정숙한 볼럼니아가, 건반이 너무 빽빽이 들어찬 피아노 같은 의치를 한 입에 단 흉측한 늙은 장군에게—오랫동안 바스의 찬탄이자 다른 모든 사교계의 공포였던 그 사내에게—오만 파운드의 지참금을 안기는 광경을 들여다본다. 또한 지붕 높이 자리한 방들과, 안마당의 사무실들과, 마구간 위쪽 방들도 들여다본다. 그곳에서는 더 소박한 야심이 행복을 꿈꾸고, 사냥터지기의 오두막에서, 윌이나 샐리와의 거룩한 혼인을 꿈꾼다. 환한 해가 솟아올라 만물을 함께 끌어 올린다—윌들과 샐리들을, 땅속에 숨은 수증기를, 늘어진 잎과 꽃을, 새와 짐승과 기어 다니는 것들을, 이슬 맺힌 잔디를 쓸고 롤러가 지나간 자리마다 에메랄드빛 우단을 펼치는 정원사들을, 그리고 환한 대기 속으로 곧고 높이 똬리 틀며 피어오르는 대형 부엌 화덕의 연기를. 마지막으로, 아무것도 모르고 잠든 토킹혼 씨의 머리 위로 깃발이 솟아올라, 레스터 경과 데드록 마님이 행복한 보금자리에 계시며 이 링컨셔의 저택에 환대가 베풀어진다는 것을 명랑하게 알린다.\n\n데드록 영지의 푸르른 굽이와 넓게 가지를 펼친 떡갈나무 숲에서 토킹혼 씨는 런던의 케케묵은 열기와 먼지 속으로 자리를 옮긴다. 그가 두 곳 사이를 오가는 방식은 그의 헤아릴 수 없는 면모 가운데 하나다. 그는 마치 체스니 월드가 자기 사무실 바로 옆집이라도 되는 양 그곳으로 걸어 들어가고, 마치 링컨스 인 필즈를 한 번도 떠난 적 없다는 듯 사무실로 돌아온다. 떠나기 전에 옷을 갈아입는 법도 없고, 다녀온 뒤에 그 이야기를 입에 올리는 법도 없다. 오늘 아침 탑방에서 슬며시 녹아 사라졌듯, 지금 저무는 어스름 속에서 그는 자기 광장으로 슬며시 녹아든다.\n\n양은 모조리 양피지가 되고, 염소는 가발이 되고, 목초는 여물이 되어버린 이 쾌적한 들판에 깃들인 새들 틈에 끼어든 한 마리 거무튀튀한 런던 새처럼, 연기에 그을려 빛바랜 이 변호사는, 인간들 사이에 살되 어울리지는 않으며, 인정 넘치는 젊음을 겪어본 적도 없이 늙었고, 인간 본성의 구멍과 구석에 비좁은 둥지를 트는 데 너무 오래 익숙해져 그 본성의 더 넓고 더 나은 영역을 잊어버린 채, 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 돌아온다. 뜨거운 포장도로와 뜨거운 건물이 만들어낸 화덕 속에서 그는 평소보다 더 바싹 구워졌고, 갈증 어린 마음속에는 반세기 묵어 그윽하게 익은 포트와인이 들어 있다.\n\n귀족적 비밀을 다루는 이 대제사장이 자기 칙칙한 안마당에 다다랐을 때, 점등부는 필즈의 토킹혼 씨 쪽 사다리를 오르락내리락하고 있다. 그가 현관 계단을 올라 어슴푸레한 복도로 미끄러져 들어가려는 찰나, 맨 위 계단에서 굽실거리며 비위를 맞추는 자그마한 사내와 마주친다.\n\n\"스내그스비 아닌가?\"\n\n\"예, 선생님. 안녕하셨습니까. 막 단념하고 집에 돌아가려던 참이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래? 무슨 일인가? 나한테 무슨 볼일이 있어서?\"\n\n\"저, 선생님.\" 스내그스비 씨가 자신의 가장 소중한 단골에게 보이는 공경의 표시로 모자를 머리 옆에 댄 채 말한다. \"한 말씀 여쭙고 싶었습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"여기서 말해도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그럼 말해보게.\" 변호사는 몸을 돌려 계단 꼭대기의 쇠난간에 팔을 기대고는, 안마당에 불을 밝히는 점등부를 바라본다.\n\n\"실은 관련된 일입니다.\" 스내그스비 씨가 비밀스럽게 낮은 목소리로 말한다. \"그 외국 여자와—이렇게 거두절미하고 말씀드리자면—관련된 일입니다, 선생님!\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 다소 놀란 눈으로 그를 본다. \"무슨 외국 여자?\"\n\n\"그 외국 여자 말입니다, 선생님. 제가 잘못 안 게 아니라면 프랑스 사람이지요? 저야 그 나라 말은 모릅니다만, 거동이며 생김새로 미루어 프랑스 사람일 거라고 짐작합니다. 아무튼 외국인은 분명합니다. 그날 밤 버킷 씨하고 제가 그 청소부 소년을 데리고 선생님을 모시는 영광을 누렸을 때, 위층에 있던 바로 그 여자 말입니다.\"\n\n\"오! 그래, 그래. 오르탕스 양 말이로군.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까, 선생님?\" 스내그스비 씨는 모자 뒤에서 굴종의 기침을 한 번 한다. \"저야 외국인 이름은 통 모릅니다만, 틀림없이 그 사람일 겁니다.\" 스내그스비 씨는 이 대답을 하면서 그 이름을 따라 발음해보겠다는 결사적인 작정을 했던 듯하나, 다시 생각하더니 또 한 번 기침으로 얼버무린다.\n\n\"그래, 그 여자에 대해 무슨 할 말이 있다는 건가, 스내그스비?\" 토킹혼 씨가 캐묻는다.\n\n\"저, 선생님.\" 문구상은 모자로 자신의 이야기를 가리며 대답한다. \"이게 제게는 좀 난처한 일이올시다. 제 가정의 행복이야 더없이 크지요—적어도 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 크다고 봅니다, 정말로—하지만 제 마누라가 좀 질투심이 많은 편이라서요. 거두절미하고 말씀드리자면, 질투심이 아주 심합니다. 그런데 보십시오, 그렇게 점잖아 보이는 외국 여자가 가게로 들어와서는, 어슬렁어슬렁—피할 수만 있다면 저야 그런 강한 표현은 절대 쓰지 않을 사람입니다만, 어슬렁어슬렁, 선생님—골목을 맴도니—그게 어디 보통 일입니까—안 그렇습니까? 그저 선생님 판단에 맡기겠습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n이 말을 무척 애처로운 투로 한 스내그스비 씨는 모든 빈틈을 메우려 두루 쓰이는 기침을 한 번 던져 넣는다.\n\n\"아니, 그게 대체 무슨 소린가?\" 토킹혼 씨가 묻는다.\n\n\"바로 그겁니다, 선생님.\" 스내그스비 씨가 대답한다. \"선생님께서도 직접 느끼시리라 확신했고, 또 제 마누라의 잘 알려진 흥분 기질과 더불어 제 심정의 마땅함도 헤아려주시리라 믿었습니다. 보십시오, 그 외국 여자가—방금 선생님께서 아주 본토박이 같은 발음으로 이름을 말씀하셨지요—그날 밤 워낙 눈치가 빨라 스내그스비라는 말을 알아챘는지, 캐묻고 다녀 주소를 알아내서는 저녁 시간에 찾아왔지 뭡니까. 그런데 우리 집 어린 하녀 거스터가 겁이 많고 발작을 일으키는 아이라, 그 외국 여자의 험상궂은—사나운—얼굴과, 무언가를 가는 듯한 말투에—여린 마음을 놀라게 하기 딱 좋은 말투지요—기겁을 해서는, 견디기는커녕 그만 무너져, 부엌 계단을 데굴데굴 굴러 한 발작에서 또 다른 발작으로 넘어갔는데, 그런 발작은 저희 집 말고는 어느 집에서도 시작되지도 끝나지도 않을 거라고 가끔 생각할 정도입니다. 그러니 다행인지 불행인지 제 마누라가 그 일에 매달릴 거리가 넘쳐났고, 가게는 저 혼자 봐야 했지요. 그 여자가 글쎄, 토킹혼 씨는 고용주 측에서 늘 자기를 따돌린다며(그때는 저도 외국식으로 서기를 보는 방식인가 보다 했습니다만), 여기 들어올 수 있을 때까지 저희 집에 계속 찾아오는 즐거움을 누리겠다는 겁니다. 그 뒤로 줄곧, 처음에 말씀드린 대로, 어슬렁어슬렁 맴돌고 있습니다, 선생님\"—스내그스비 씨는 애처로운 강조를 담아 그 말을 되풀이한다—\"골목에서 말이지요. 그게 어떤 영향을 미칠지 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없습니다. 어쩌면 벌써 이웃 사람들 마음에까지 더없이 고약한 오해를 불러일으켰을지도 모릅니다. 제 마누라야(그럴 수만 있다면) 말할 것도 없고요. 그런데, 하느님 아실 일이지만,\" 스내그스비 씨는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말한다. \"저는 외국 여자라고 하면 예전에는 빗자루 다발에 갓난애를 안은 모습으로, 요새는 탬버린에 귀고리를 단 모습으로밖에 떠올려본 적이 없습니다. 정말이지 그밖엔 없었습니다, 선생님!\"\n\n토킹혼 씨는 이 하소연을 진지하게 듣고는, 문구상이 말을 마치자 묻는다. \"그래서 그게 다인가, 스내그스비?\"\n\n\"예, 그렇습니다, 선생님, 그게 답니다.\" 스내그스비 씨는 \"게다가 이만하면 저로서는 충분하고도 남습니다\"라고 또렷이 덧붙이는 듯한 기침으로 말을 맺는다.\n\n\"오르탕스 양이 뭘 원하는지, 무슨 속셈인지 나로서는 모르겠군. 미친 게 아니라면 말이야.\" 변호사가 말한다.\n\n\"설령 미쳤다 한들 말입니다, 선생님.\" 스내그스비 씨가 통사정한다. \"외국 단검 같은 무기가 우리 집안에 꽂힌다고 해서 그게 위안이 되지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇지.\" 상대가 말한다. \"자, 자! 이 일은 멈추도록 하겠네. 자네가 불편을 겪었다니 안됐군. 그 여자가 또 오거든 이리로 보내게.\"\n\n스내그스비 씨는 연신 굽실거리고 짧게 사과하는 기침을 하며 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 물러간다. 토킹혼 씨는 위층으로 올라가며 혼잣말을 한다. \"이 여자들이란 온 세상에 말썽을 일으키려고 태어난 족속이야. 마님 하나로도 모자라더니, 이번엔 하녀까지! 하지만 이 계집한테만은 단호하게 잘라버리겠어!\"\n\n이렇게 중얼거리며 그는 문을 열고, 어두컴컴한 방을 더듬어 들어가, 촛불을 밝히고 주위를 둘러본다. 머리 위의 우의화는 너무 어두워 잘 보이지 않지만, 늘 구름에서 굴러떨어지며 손가락질하는 그 끈질긴 로마인만은 꽤 또렷이 제 일을 하고 있다. 별로 주의를 기울이지 않은 채 토킹혼 씨는 주머니에서 작은 열쇠를 꺼내 서랍을 여니 그 안에 또 다른 열쇠가 있고, 그 열쇠로 궤를 여니 또 다른 열쇠가 있어, 마침내 지하 저장실 열쇠에 이른다. 그 열쇠로 묵은 포도주가 있는 곳으로 내려갈 채비를 한다. 촛불을 손에 들고 문 쪽으로 가는데 노크 소리가 난다.\n\n\"누구지? 아, 아, 마님이로군, 자네인가? 마침 잘 왔어. 방금 자네 이야기를 듣던 참이거든. 자! 무슨 볼일인가?\"\n\n그는 서기방 벽난로 선반에 촛불을 세워두고, 오르탕스 양에게 이런 환영의 말을 건네며 열쇠로 자신의 메마른 뺨을 톡톡 두드린다. 입술을 꼭 다물고 곁눈질로 그를 노려보는 그 고양이 같은 여자는, 대답하기 전에 살그머니 문을 닫는다.\n\n\"당신을 찾느라 엄청 애먹었어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그랬어!\"\n\n\"여기 아주 자주 왔어요, 선생님. 그때마다 안 계신다, 바쁘시다, 이렇다 저렇다, 당신을 만날 수 없다고만 하더군요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이고, 사실이지.\"\n\n\"사실 아니에요. 거짓말!\"\n\n오르탕스 양의 태도에는 이따금 어찌나 느닷없는 데가 있는지, 마치 상대에게 몸을 날려 덮치는 것 같아 그 상대가 저도 모르게 흠칫 물러설 정도다. 지금 토킹혼 씨가 바로 그 꼴인데, 정작 오르탕스 양은 눈을 거의 감다시피 하고서(여전히 곁눈질로 노려보면서) 그저 경멸하듯 웃으며 고개를 절레절레 흔들 뿐이다.\n\n\"자, 마님.\" 변호사가 벽난로 선반에 열쇠를 황급히 톡톡 두드리며 말한다. \"할 말이 있거든 하게, 어서 하라고.\"\n\n\"선생님, 당신은 나를 함부로 대했어요. 비열하고 추잡하게 굴었다고요.\"\n\n\"비열하고 추잡하게, 그래?\" 변호사가 열쇠로 코를 문지르며 대꾸한다.\n\n\"그래요. 내가 무슨 말 하는지 알잖아요? 당신도 그랬다는 거 알잖아요. 당신은 나를 옭아맸어요—꾀어냈다고요—정보를 얻어내려고. 그날 밤 마님이 입었을 게 분명한 내 옷을 보여달라고 했고, 그 옷을 입고 여기 와서 그 소년을 만나달라고 빌었잖아요. 말해봐요! 안 그래요?\" 오르탕스 양이 또 한 번 몸을 날릴 듯 덤빈다.\n\n\"독한 계집, 독한 계집이로군!\" 토킹혼 씨는 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 곰곰이 따져보는 듯하다가 대답한다. \"그래, 계집, 그래. 그 값은 치렀잖나.\"\n\n\"값을 치렀다고요!\" 그녀가 사나운 멸시를 담아 되받는다. \"고작 이 소버린! 거슬러 받지도 않았고, 거절했고, 경멸하고, 내던져버렸어요!\" 그러면서 그녀는 말하는 그대로 가슴팍에서 동전을 꺼내 바닥에 얼마나 거세게 내던지는지, 동전이 촛불 빛 속으로 다시 튀어 올랐다가 구석으로 굴러가 한참을 격하게 빙글빙글 돌다 천천히 멈춘다.\n\n\"자!\" 오르탕스 양이 큰 눈을 다시 흐리며 말한다. \"당신이 값을 치렀다고요? 어머, 세상에, 오 그래요!\"\n\n그녀가 비웃듯 한바탕 웃어대는 동안 토킹혼 씨는 열쇠로 머리를 긁적인다.\n\n\"부자인가 보군, 곱디고운 친구여.\" 그가 침착하게 말한다. \"돈을 그런 식으로 내던지는 걸 보니!\"\n\n\"부자 맞아요.\" 그녀가 대꾸한다. \"증오라면 아주 차고 넘치게 부자죠. 나는 마님을 미워해요, 진심으로 온 마음을 다해서. 당신도 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"안다고? 내가 그걸 어찌 알겠나?\"\n\n\"그 정보를 얻으려고 나한테 빌기 전부터 당신은 그걸 빤히 알고 있었으니까요. 내가 펄펄 끓어오를 만큼 화났다는 걸 빤히 알고 있었으니까요!\" 오르탕스 양은 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 이를 악물어 격렬한 전달을 돕건만, 이 단어에서 'ㄹ' 소리를 충분히 굴리기란 도무지 불가능해 보인다.\n\n\"오! 내가 그걸 알았다고?\" 토킹혼 씨가 열쇠의 톱니를 살피며 말한다.\n\n\"그래요, 의심할 여지 없이. 나는 눈먼 사람이 아니에요. 그걸 알았으니까 당신은 나를 손에 넣은 거예요. 그럴 만했죠! 나는 그 여자가 진절머리 나게 싫어요.\" 오르탕스 양은 팔짱을 끼고 어깨 너머로 이 마지막 말을 그에게 던진다.\n\n\"그 말을 했으니, 또 할 말이 더 있나, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"나는 아직 자리를 못 잡았어요. 좋은 자리를 마련해줘요. 번듯한 처지를 찾아달라고요! 그게 안 되거나 그럴 마음이 없거든, 나를 시켜 그 여자를 쫓고, 추적하고, 망신 주고, 모욕하게 해요. 내가 톡톡히, 기꺼이 도와줄게요. 당신이 하는 일이 그런 거잖아요. 내가 그걸 모를 줄 알아요?\"\n\n\"꽤 많이 아는 것 같군.\" 토킹혼 씨가 받아친다.\n\n\"안 그래요? 내가 그 옷을 입고 여기 와서 그 소년을 만난 게 고작 사소한 내기, 노름을 가리려고 한 짓이라고, 어린애처럼 믿을 만큼 내가 그렇게 어리석을까요? 어머, 세상에, 오 그래요!\" 이 대답에서 '노름'이라는 말까지는 그녀가 반어적으로 정중하고 다정했다가, 곧장 더없이 신랄하고 도전적인 경멸로 돌변하는데, 검은 두 눈이 한순간에 거의 감기는 동시에 휘둥그레 부릅떠진다.\n\n\"자, 어디 보세.\" 토킹혼 씨가 열쇠로 턱을 톡톡 두드리며 태연히 그녀를 바라보고 말한다. \"이 일이 어떻게 돌아가고 있는지.\"\n\n\"아! 어디 봐요.\" 오르탕스 양이 성난 듯 야무지게 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이며 맞장구친다.\n\n\"자네는 여기 와서 방금 말한 대로 놀랄 만큼 소박한 요구를 하고, 그게 받아들여지지 않으니 또 오겠다 이거지.\"\n\n\"또 오고말고요.\" 오르탕스 양이 한층 더 야무지고 성난 끄덕임으로 말한다. \"또 오고. 또 오고. 또 몇 번이고 오죠. 사실은, 영원히!\"\n\n\"여기뿐 아니라 스내그스비 씨네도 가겠다는 거고, 아마? 그 방문도 성공하지 못하면 또 가겠지, 아마?\"\n\n\"또 가죠.\" 오르탕스 양이 결의로 굳어진 채 되풀이한다. \"또 가고. 또 가고. 또 몇 번이고 가죠. 사실은, 영원히!\"\n\n\"좋아. 자, 오르탕스 양, 한 가지 권하지. 촛불을 들고 자네 돈을 주워가게. 저쪽 구석 서기 칸막이 뒤에 있을 거야.\"\n\n그녀는 그저 어깨 너머로 웃음을 던지고, 팔짱을 낀 채 그 자리에 버티고 선다.\n\n\"안 줍겠다, 그거지?\"\n\n\"네, 안 주워요!\"\n\n\"그럴수록 자네는 더 가난해지고, 나는 더 부자가 되지! 보게, 마님, 이게 내 포도주 저장실 열쇠야. 큰 열쇠지만, 감옥 열쇠는 더 크지. 이 도시에는 교화소가 있어서(여자들이 쓰는 디딜방아가 그곳에 있지), 그 문이 무척 튼튼하고 묵직한데, 틀림없이 열쇠도 그렇겠지. 자네처럼 기개 넘치고 활발한 숙녀가 그런 열쇠를 한참 동안 등 뒤에서 잠그고 지내려면 꽤 불편할 거야. 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 오르탕스 양이 아무런 동작 없이 또랑또랑하고 상냥한 목소리로 대답한다. \"당신은 비참하고 비열한 인간이에요.\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지.\" 토킹혼 씨가 조용히 코를 풀며 대꾸한다. \"하지만 나는 자네가 나를 어찌 생각하는지 묻는 게 아니야. 그 감옥을 어찌 생각하는지 묻는 거지.\"\n\n\"아무 생각 없어요. 그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠?\"\n\n\"왜, 이만큼 상관있지, 마님.\" 변호사가 손수건을 천천히 치우고 가슴 주름 장식을 매만지며 말한다. \"이 나라 법은 어찌나 강압적인지, 우리 선량한 영국 시민이 원치 않는데도 숙녀의 방문 따위로 시달리는 걸 막으려고 끼어들거든. 그리고 그 시민이 그렇게 시달린다고 호소하면, 법은 그 성가신 숙녀를 붙잡아 엄한 규율 아래 감옥에 가둬버려. 그 여자한테 열쇠를 채우는 거지, 마님.\" 저장실 열쇠로 그 시늉을 해 보인다.\n\n\"정말요?\" 오르탕스 양이 여전히 상냥한 목소리로 받는다. \"거참 우습네요! 하지만—어머나!—그래도 그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠?\"\n\n\"곱디고운 친구여,\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"여기든 스내그스비 씨네든 한 번만 더 찾아와보게. 그럼 알게 될 테니.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신이 나를 감옥에 보내겠다, 그거예요, 아마?\"\n\n\"아마도.\"\n\n오르탕스 양처럼 흐뭇하게 농을 즐기는 처지에 입에 거품을 무는 건 앞뒤가 안 맞는 일이겠으나, 그게 아니라면 그 언저리에 번지는 호랑이 같은 표정으로 보아 조금만 더 건드리면 정말 그럴 듯도 하다.\n\n\"한마디로, 마님,\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"무례해서 미안하네만, 자네가 부르지도 않았는데 여기—혹은 거기—다시 나타나는 날엔, 나는 자네를 경찰에 넘기겠어. 경찰이 워낙 정중하긴 하지만, 성가신 자들을 판자에 끈으로 묶어 거리로 끌고 다니는 게 그 꼴이 영 볼품없거든, 착한 계집아.\"\n\n\"어디 두고 봐요.\" 오르탕스 양이 손을 뻗으며 속삭인다. \"당신이 정말 그럴 배짱이 있는지 시험해보겠어요!\"\n\n\"그리고 만일,\" 변호사가 그녀에게 아랑곳없이 말을 잇는다. \"내가 자네를 감옥에 갇히는 그 번듯한 처지에 앉혀준다면, 다시 자유의 몸이 되기까진 꽤 오래 걸릴 거야.\"\n\n\"어디 두고 봐요.\" 오르탕스 양이 아까처럼 속삭여 되풀이한다.\n\n\"그러니 이제,\" 변호사가 여전히 아랑곳없이 이어간다. \"그만 가는 게 좋겠어. 여기 다시 오기 전에 두 번 생각하라고.\"\n\n\"두 번이라고요.\" 그녀가 대답한다. \"두 곱하기 이백 번을 생각해도 모자라요!\"\n\n\"자네 마님이 자네를 내쫓았지, 알다시피.\" 토킹혼 씨가 계단으로 그녀를 따라 나가며 말한다. \"여자 중에서 가장 무자비하고 가장 다루기 힘든 여자라고 말이야. 이제 마음을 고쳐먹고 내 말을 새겨듣게. 내가 한 말은 진심이고, 내가 위협한 건 반드시 행하니까, 마님.\"\n\n그녀는 대답도 없이, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 내려간다. 그녀가 떠나자 그도 내려가, 거미줄로 뒤덮인 술병을 들고 돌아와, 그 안의 내용물을 느긋이 음미하는 데 몰두한다. 이따금 의자에 머리를 젖힐 때면, 천장에서 손가락질하는 그 집요한 로마인이 눈에 들어온다.\n\n이제 와서, 나에게 늘 죽은 사람으로 여기라고 일렀던 살아 있는 어머니를 내가 얼마나 자주 생각했는지는 그리 중요하지 않습니다. 나는 감히 어머니에게 다가가거나 편지로 연락을 취할 엄두를 내지 못했으니, 어머니의 목숨이 처한 위험에 대한 내 감각은 오로지 그 위험을 더 키우게 될까 하는 두려움과 맞먹을 뿐이었기 때문입니다. 살아 숨 쉬는 한 생명체로서 내가 존재한다는 사실 자체가 어머니 앞길에 예기치 못한 위험이라는 것을 알았기에, 그 비밀을 처음 알았을 때 나를 사로잡았던 나 자신에 대한 공포를 나는 늘 이겨내지는 못했습니다. 어느 때든 나는 감히 어머니의 이름을 입에 올리지 못했습니다. 심지어 그 이름을 듣는 것조차 감히 못 할 것만 같았습니다. 내가 있는 자리에서 어디든 대화가 그쪽으로 흘러가면, 때로 자연스레 그러기도 했는데, 나는 듣지 않으려 애썼습니다. 속으로 수를 세거나, 아는 무언가를 되뇌거나, 방을 나가버렸습니다. 지금 와 생각하면 어머니가 화제에 오를 위험이 전혀 없었을 때조차 나는 종종 이런 짓을 했지만, 어머니를 누설로 이끌 만한, 그것도 나를 통한 누설로 이끌 만한 무언가를 듣게 될까 하는 두려움에 그리한 것입니다.\n\n이제 와서, 내가 얼마나 자주 어머니 목소리의 가락을 떠올렸는지, 그토록 간절히 바라던 대로 그 목소리를 다시 들을 날이 올까 궁금해했는지, 그리고 그것이 나에게 이토록 낯설다는 것이 얼마나 이상하고 쓸쓸한 일인지 생각했는지는 그리 중요하지 않습니다. 내가 어머니의 이름이 세상에 거론될 때마다 귀를 곤두세웠다는 것, 도시에 있는 어머니의 집 문 앞을 사랑하는 마음으로 오가면서도 차마 쳐다보지 못했다는 것, 한번은 극장에 앉아 있는데 마침 거기 있던 어머니가 나를 보았고, 온갖 신분의 거대한 무리 앞에서 우리가 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어 우리 사이의 어떤 연결이나 신뢰도 한낱 꿈만 같았다는 것, 이런 것들도 그리 중요하지 않습니다. 그것은 다, 다 지나간 일입니다. 내 운명은 너무도 큰 축복을 받아서, 나 자신에 관해 들려줄 이야기 가운데 남의 선함과 너그러움에 관한 이야기가 아닌 것이 거의 없을 정도입니다. 그러니 그 자그마한 부분은 그냥 지나치고 이야기를 계속하는 것이 좋겠습니다.\n\n우리가 다시 집에 자리를 잡고 나서, 에이다와 나는 후견인과 여러 차례 대화를 나누었는데 그 주제는 리처드였습니다. 사랑하는 내 친구는 리처드가 친절한 사촌에게 그토록 큰 부당함을 저지른다는 사실에 깊이 마음 아파했지만, 워낙 리처드에게 충실한지라 그 일로조차 그를 탓하는 것을 견디지 못했습니다. 후견인도 그것을 잘 알았기에, 리처드의 이름을 단 한마디 질책과도 엮지 않았습니다. \"릭이 잘못 생각하는 거란다, 얘야.\" 후견인은 에이다에게 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"괜찮아, 괜찮아! 우리도 다들 몇 번이고 거듭 잘못 생각해왔으니까. 너와 세월에 맡기고 그 애가 바로잡히기를 기다려야지.\"\n\n당시 우리가 짐작만 하던 것을 나중에 알게 되었는데, 후견인은 리처드의 눈을 뜨게 하려고 여러 번 애쓰기 전까지는 세월에 맡기지 않았다는 사실입니다. 후견인은 리처드에게 편지를 쓰고, 찾아가고, 이야기를 나누고, 그 친절한 마음이 궁리해낼 수 있는 온갖 부드럽고 설득력 있는 방법을 다 써보았습니다. 가엾도록 외곬으로 빠진 우리 리처드는 그 모든 것에 귀먹고 눈멀었습니다. 자기가 틀렸다면 대법원 소송이 끝난 뒤에 보상하겠다는 것이었습니다. 자기가 어둠 속을 더듬고 있다면, 그토록 많은 것이 뒤엉키고 흐려진 그 구름을 걷어내려 온 힘을 다하는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없다는 것이었습니다. 의심과 오해가 소송의 잘못이라고? 그렇다면 소송을 끝까지 밀어붙여 그것을 통과해 제정신으로 돌아오게 하면 된다는 것이었습니다. 이것이 리처드의 한결같은 대답이었습니다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 그의 본성 전체를 어찌나 단단히 사로잡았는지, 그 앞에 어떤 고려 사항을 내놓아도 그는 비뚤어진 논리로 그것을 자기가 하는 일을 정당화하는 새로운 논거로 바꿔버리니, 도무지 어쩔 도리가 없었습니다. \"그러니 그 가엾은 친구에게 따져 묻는 것이,\" 한번은 후견인이 나에게 이렇게 말했습니다. \"그냥 내버려두는 것보다 오히려 더 해롭단 말이지.\"\n\n나는 이런 기회를 빌려, 스킴폴 씨가 리처드에게 좋은 조언자가 될 수 있을지 내 의구심을 털어놓았습니다.\n\n\"조언자!\" 후견인이 웃으며 받았습니다. \"얘야, 스킴폴한테 누가 조언을 구하겠니?\"\n\n\"부추기는 사람이라는 말이 더 나았을지도 모르겠네요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"부추기는 사람이라!\" 후견인이 다시 받았습니다. \"스킴폴한테 부추김을 받을 사람이 누가 있겠니?\"\n\n\"리처드는 아니란 말씀인가요?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"그래.\" 후견인이 대답했습니다. \"그렇게 세상 물정 모르고, 셈할 줄 모르고, 거미줄처럼 허허로운 인간은 그 애한테 위안이고 즐길 거리지. 하지만 누군가에게, 혹은 무언가에 대해 조언을 하거나 부추기거나 진지한 자리를 맡는다는 건, 스킴폴 같은 어린애한테는 도무지 생각조차 할 수 없는 일이란다.\"\n\n\"저, 존 사촌 오라버니.\" 마침 우리 곁에 와서 내 어깨너머로 들여다보던 에이다가 말했습니다. \"그분은 왜 그렇게 어린애가 됐을까요?\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 어린애가 됐느냐고?\" 후견인이 머리를 문지르며, 살짝 난감해하며 되물었습니다.\n\n\"네, 존 사촌 오라버니.\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 후견인은 머리를 점점 더 헝클어뜨리며 천천히 대답했습니다. \"그 친구는 온통 감상이고, 그리고… 감수성이고, 그리고… 정서고, 그리고… 상상력이야. 그런데 그런 자질이 어쩐 일인지 그 안에서 조절이 안 돼. 젊은 시절에 그를 그 자질로 칭찬하던 사람들이 그것에 너무 큰 가치를 두고, 그 자질을 균형 있게 다듬어줄 어떤 훈련에는 너무 적은 가치를 둔 모양이라, 그래서 그가 지금 같은 사람이 된 거겠지. 응?\" 후견인은 말을 뚝 멈추고 기대에 차서 우리를 보았습니다. \"너희 둘은 어떻게 생각하니?\"\n\n에이다는 나를 흘끗 보고는, 그가 리처드에게 비용 부담이 되는 것이 안타깝다고 생각한다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 그렇지, 그렇고말고.\" 후견인이 황급히 받았습니다. \"그래선 안 되지. 그건 우리가 처리해야 해. 내가 막아야겠어. 그건 절대 안 될 일이야.\"\n\n그리고 나는, 그가 오 파운드를 얻으려고 리처드를 볼스 씨에게 소개한 일이 유감스럽다고 생각한다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그가 그랬다고?\" 후견인은 얼굴에 잠깐 언짢은 기색을 스치며 말했습니다. \"하지만 그게 바로 그 사람이야. 그게 바로 그 사람이라고! 그 친구한테 그건 돈 욕심하고는 아무 상관 없는 일이야. 그는 돈의 가치라는 걸 몰라. 릭을 소개해주고는, 그러고 나서 볼스 씨와 좋은 친구가 돼서 그에게 오 파운드를 빌리는 거지. 거기엔 아무 속셈도 없고, 대수롭게 여기지도 않아. 분명 그 친구가 너한테 직접 그 얘길 했겠지, 얘야?\"\n\n\"오, 네!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 후견인이 자못 의기양양하게 외쳤습니다. \"그게 바로 그 사람이라니까! 거기에 무슨 해를 끼칠 속셈이 있었거나 그 안에 어떤 해가 있다는 걸 의식했다면, 그는 말하지 않았을 거야. 그는 그저 천진하게, 행동하는 그대로 말하는 거지. 하지만 너희가 그를 그의 집에서 보면, 그땐 그를 더 잘 이해하게 될 거야. 해럴드 스킴폴을 찾아가서 이런 점들을 주의시켜야겠다. 어이구 얘들아, 갓난애야, 갓난애!\"\n\n이 계획에 따라, 우리는 어느 이른 날 런던으로 들어가 스킴폴 씨의 집 앞에 나타났습니다.\n\n그는 서머스 타운의 폴리곤이라는 곳에 살았는데, 그 무렵 거기엔 가난한 스페인 망명객 여럿이 망토를 두르고 작은 종이 궐련을 피우며 돌아다녔습니다. 그의 친구 누군가가 늘 끝에 가서는 집세를 대신 내준 덕에 그가 생각보다 나은 세입자였던 것인지, 아니면 사업에 도무지 서툰 탓에 그를 내쫓기가 유난히 어려웠던 것인지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그는 같은 집에서 몇 해를 살고 있었습니다. 집은 우리 예상에 꼭 들어맞게 다 허물어져가는 꼴이었습니다. 지하 채광창 난간 두세 개가 사라졌고, 빗물통은 깨졌고, 문고리는 헐거웠고, 초인종 손잡이는 철사가 녹슨 상태로 보아 오래전에 뜯겨나갔으며, 계단에 찍힌 더러운 발자국만이 사람이 산다는 유일한 표시였습니다.\n\n지나치게 익은 산딸기처럼 옷의 터진 틈과 신발의 갈라진 곳으로 살이 비어져 나올 것 같은, 칠칠치 못하게 푸짐한 처녀가 문을 아주 조금만 열고 그 틈을 제 몸으로 틀어막은 채 우리 노크에 응했습니다. 그 처녀는 잔다이스 씨를 알아보았기에(사실 에이다와 나는 둘 다, 처녀가 분명 그를 제 품삯을 받는 일과 연관 짓는다고 생각했습니다), 곧바로 누그러져 우리를 안으로 들였습니다. 문의 자물쇠가 망가진 상태라, 처녀는 그제야 사슬로 문을 잠그려 들었는데 그 사슬도 영 신통치 않았고, 위층으로 올라가시겠느냐고 물었습니다.\n\n우리는 이층으로 올라갔는데, 여전히 더러운 발자국 말고는 다른 가구라고는 보이지 않았습니다. 잔다이스 씨는 더 격식 차릴 것도 없이 거기 있는 방으로 들어갔고, 우리도 뒤따랐습니다. 방은 꽤 칙칙하고 전혀 깨끗하지 않았지만, 묘하게 초라한 호사로 꾸며져 있었습니다. 커다란 발받침과 소파와 잔뜩 쌓인 방석, 안락의자와 잔뜩 쌓인 베개, 피아노, 책, 그림 도구, 악보, 신문, 그리고 스케치와 그림 몇 점이 있었습니다. 더러운 창문 중 하나에 깨진 유리는 종이를 풀로 발라 막아놓았지만, 탁자 위에는 온실에서 키운 천도복숭아가 담긴 작은 접시가 있었고, 포도가 담긴 접시도, 스펀지케이크가 담긴 접시도 또 있었으며, 가벼운 와인 한 병도 있었습니다. 스킴폴 씨는 손수 실내복 차림으로 소파에 비스듬히 기대어, 낡은 도자기 잔에 담긴 향긋한 커피를 마시며—그때가 한낮쯤이었습니다—발코니에 모아둔 계란풀 무더기를 바라보고 있었습니다.\n\n그는 우리가 나타난 것에 조금도 당황하지 않고, 일어나서 평소의 가뿐한 태도로 우리를 맞았습니다.\n\n\"보다시피, 내가 여기 이러고 있습니다!\" 우리가 자리에 앉자 그가 말했는데, 의자 대부분이 부서져 있어 앉기가 적잖이 어려웠습니다. \"내가 이러고 있어요! 이게 내 소박한 아침 식사랍니다. 어떤 사람들은 아침으로 쇠고기나 양고기 다릿살을 찾지요. 나는 안 그래요. 복숭아 한 알, 커피 한 잔, 클라레 한 잔이면 나는 만족합니다. 그것들이 그 자체로 좋아서가 아니라, 그것들이 나에게 태양을 떠올리게 해주니까요. 쇠고기나 양고기 다릿살에는 태양 같은 데가 전혀 없어요. 그저 짐승 같은 만족일 뿐이지요!\"\n\n\"여기가 우리 친구의 진료실이라네(처방이란 걸 한 적이 있다면 말이지만), 그의 성소이자 작업실이지.\" 후견인이 우리에게 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 환한 얼굴을 우리 쪽으로 돌리며 말했습니다. \"여기가 새장입니다. 새가 여기 살면서 노래하는 곳이지요. 사람들이 이따금 새의 깃털을 뽑고 날개를 자르지만, 그래도 새는 노래합니다, 노래해요!\"\n\n그는 우리에게 포도를 건네면서 그 빛나는 어조로 거듭 말했습니다. \"노래한답니다! 야심 찬 가락은 아니지만, 그래도 노래해요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋은 포도로군.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"선물인가?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"아니에요! 어느 상냥한 정원사가 파는 겁니다. 어젯밤에 그 정원사의 하인이 이걸 가져오면서, 돈을 받아 갈 때까지 기다려야 하느냐고 묻더군요. '이보게, 친구,' 내가 말했지요. '안 그러는 게 좋겠네—자네 시간이 자네한테 조금이라도 값어치가 있다면 말이야.' 값어치가 있었던 모양인지, 그는 가버렸어요.\"\n\n후견인은 미소를 띠고 우리를 바라보았는데, 마치 \"이 갓난애를 상대로 세속적일 수가 있겠나?\" 하고 묻는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"오늘은,\" 스킴폴 씨가 유쾌하게 텀블러에 클라레를 조금 따르며 말했습니다. \"이 집에서 길이길이 기억될 날이 될 겁니다. 우리는 이날을 성 클레어와 성 서머슨의 날이라고 부르겠어요. 내 딸들을 꼭 봐야 합니다. 나에겐 파란 눈을 가진 딸이 있는데 내 미녀 딸이고, 감상 딸이 하나 있고, 희극 딸이 하나 있어요. 셋 다 봐야 합니다. 다들 황홀해할 거예요.\"\n\n그가 딸들을 부르려는데 후견인이 가로막으며, 먼저 한마디 할 말이 있으니 잠깐 멈춰달라고 청했습니다. \"이보게 잔다이스,\" 그가 소파로 돌아가며 명랑하게 대답했습니다. \"원하는 만큼 얼마든지 멈추지. 여기선 시간이 아무 문제가 안 돼. 우리는 지금이 몇 시인지도 통 모르고, 신경도 안 쓰니까. 그게 인생에서 성공하는 길은 아니라고 자네는 말하겠지? 물론 그래. 하지만 우린 인생에서 성공하지 않아. 그러는 척도 안 하고.\"\n\n후견인은 다시 우리를 보았는데, 분명히 \"저 친구 말 들었지?\" 하는 표정이었습니다.\n\n\"자, 해럴드,\" 그가 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"내가 할 말은 릭에 관한 거야.\"\n\n\"내가 가진 가장 소중한 친구지!\" 스킴폴 씨가 다정하게 받았습니다. \"그 친구가 자네하고 사이가 안 좋으니, 내 가장 소중한 친구여서는 안 될 텐데 말이야. 하지만 그렇게 됐으니 나도 어쩔 수가 없어. 그 친구는 젊음의 시정으로 가득 차 있고, 나는 그를 사랑하거든. 자네가 싫어해도 나는 어쩔 수가 없어. 나는 그를 사랑한다네.\"\n\n이런 선언을 하는 그 매력적인 솔직함에는 정말로 사심 없어 보이는 구석이 있어서, 잠시나마 에이다는 몰라도 후견인의 마음을 사로잡았습니다.\n\n\"자네가 그 애를 얼마든지 사랑하는 건 괜찮네.\" 잔다이스 씨가 받았습니다. \"하지만 우리는 그 애 주머니를 지켜줘야 해, 해럴드.\"\n\n\"오!\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그 친구 주머니라고? 이제 내가 통 이해 못 하는 데로 들어가는군.\" 클라레를 조금 더 따라 케이크 하나를 거기에 적시면서, 그는 고개를 저으며 자기는 도무지 이해할 수 없으리라는 천진한 예감을 품고 에이다와 나를 보며 미소 지었습니다.\n\n\"자네가 그 애와 함께 여기저기 다니거든,\" 후견인이 솔직하게 말했습니다. \"그 애가 두 사람 몫을 다 내게 하면 안 돼.\"\n\n\"이보게 잔다이스,\" 스킴폴 씨가 받았는데, 이 발상이 우스워서 그 너그러운 얼굴이 환히 빛났습니다. \"그럼 나더러 어쩌라는 건가? 그 친구가 나를 어디로 데려가면, 나는 가야지. 그런데 내가 어떻게 돈을 내? 나는 돈이라곤 한 푼도 없어. 설사 돈이 있다 해도, 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모른다네. 어떤 사람한테 내가 얼마요? 하고 묻는다고 쳐. 그 사람이 나한테 칠 실링 육 펜스요, 한다고 쳐. 나는 칠 실링 육 펜스에 대해 아무것도 모른다네. 그 사람을 조금이라도 배려하면서 그 문제를 따져나갈 수가 없어. 나는 바쁜 사람들을 붙잡고 칠 실링 육 펜스가 무어인 말로 뭐냐고—내가 모르는 그 무어인 말로—묻고 다니지 않아. 그런데 왜 그들을 붙잡고 칠 실링 육 펜스가 돈으로 뭐냐고—내가 모르는 그 돈으로—묻고 다녀야 하지?\"\n\n\"음,\" 후견인이 이 천진한 대답에 조금도 언짢아하지 않고 말했습니다. \"자네가 릭과 무슨 여행 비슷한 걸 하게 되거든, 그 돈은 나한테서 빌리고(그 사정은 결코 조금도 입 밖에 내지 말고), 셈은 그 애한테 맡기게.\"\n\n\"이보게 잔다이스,\" 스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다. \"자네를 기쁘게 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠네만, 그건 부질없는 형식—일종의 미신 같군. 게다가 내 장담하건대, 클레어 양과 친애하는 서머슨 양, 나는 카스톤 씨가 엄청난 부자인 줄 알았답니다. 무언가를 양도하거나, 증서나 어음이나 수표나 환어음에 서명하거나, 어딘가 서류철에 무언가를 끼워 넣기만 하면 돈벼락이 쏟아질 거라고 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"정말로 그렇지 않답니다, 선생님.\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"그분은 가난해요.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말요?\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 환한 미소를 띠고 받았습니다. \"놀랍군요.\"\n\n\"썩은 갈대를 믿어봐야 부유해질 리도 없으니,\" 후견인이 스킴폴 씨의 실내복 소매에 힘주어 손을 얹으며 말했습니다. \"그 애가 그것에 기대도록 부추기는 일은 절대 없도록 각별히 조심하게, 해럴드.\"\n\n\"이보게 친애하는 벗,\" 스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다. \"그리고 친애하는 시머슨 양, 친애하는 클레어 양, 내가 어떻게 그럴 수 있겠나? 그건 사업이고, 나는 사업이란 걸 모른다네. 오히려 나를 부추기는 건 그 친구 쪽이야. 그 친구는 대단한 사업적 위업에서 솟아 나와, 그 결과랍시고 더없이 환한 전망을 내 앞에 펼쳐놓고는, 그걸 감탄하라고 나를 부른다네. 나는 정말 감탄해—환한 전망이라고 말이지. 하지만 나는 그 이상은 아무것도 모르고, 그 친구한테도 그렇게 말한다네.\"\n\n그가 이것을 우리 앞에 내놓는 그 무력한 솔직함, 자기 천진함에 스스로 재미있어하는 그 가벼운 태도, 자기 자신을 제 보호 아래 두고 그 별난 인물에 대해 따지는 그 기상천외한 방식이, 그가 하는 모든 말의 그 유쾌한 거침없음과 어우러져, 후견인의 주장을 꼭 들어맞게 입증해주었습니다. 그를 보면 볼수록, 그가 있는 자리에서는 그가 무언가를 꾸미거나 감추거나 좌우할 수 있다는 것이 더욱 있을 법하지 않게 여겨졌습니다. 그런데도 그가 없는 자리에서는 그것이 더욱 있을 법하게 여겨졌고, 내가 아끼는 누군가와 그가 어떤 관계를 맺는다는 생각이 더욱 달갑지 않았습니다.\n\n자기 심문(그가 그렇게 부른 것입니다)이 이제 끝났다는 말을 듣고, 스킴폴 씨는 환한 얼굴로 딸들을 데리러 방을 나갔는데(아들들은 이런저런 때에 다들 달아나버렸습니다), 후견인은 그가 자기 어린애 같은 성격을 옹호하는 방식에 자못 즐거워하며 남았습니다. 그는 곧 세 아가씨와 스킴폴 부인을 데리고 돌아왔는데, 부인은 한때 미인이었으나 이제는 갖가지 병이 겹쳐 시달리는 콧대 높고 가냘픈 환자였습니다.\n\n\"이쪽은,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"내 미녀 딸 아레투사인데—제 아비처럼 이것저것 연주하고 노래하지요. 이쪽은 내 감상 딸 로라인데—조금 연주하지만 노래는 안 해요. 이쪽은 내 희극 딸 키티인데—조금 노래하지만 연주는 안 하고요. 우린 다들 조금씩 그림을 그리고 조금씩 작곡을 하는데, 누구 하나 시간이나 돈에 대한 개념이 없답니다.\"\n\n스킴폴 부인은, 내 생각에, 이 가족의 재능 목록에서 그 항목만은 지워버릴 수 있었으면 싶은 듯 한숨을 쉬었습니다. 또 내 생각에 부인은 그 한숨을 후견인에게 자못 강하게 들이밀었고, 틈만 나면 한숨을 한 번씩 더 끼워 넣었습니다.\n\n\"가족마다 특이한 점들을 더듬어보는 건,\" 스킴폴 씨가 그 발랄한 눈을 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 옮기며 말했습니다. \"유쾌하기도 하고, 묘하게 흥미롭기도 하지요. 이 가족에선 우리가 다 어린애인데, 내가 제일 어립니다.\"\n\n그를 무척 따르는 듯한 딸들은 이 익살스러운 사실에 즐거워했는데, 특히 희극 딸이 그러했습니다.\n\n\"얘들아, 그게 사실이잖니,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"안 그래? 사실이고, 그럴 수밖에 없는 게, 찬송가에 나오는 개들처럼 '그게 우리 본성'이거든. 자, 여기 서머슨 양은 빼어난 살림 솜씨에다 세세한 부분에 대한 지식이 놀라울 만큼 훌륭하지요. 우리가 이 집에서 양고기 토막에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 게 서머슨 양 귀엔 무척 이상하게 들리겠지요. 하지만 우린 정말 모른답니다, 손톱만큼도. 우린 무어든 요리할 줄을 몰라요. 바늘과 실도 쓸 줄 모르고. 우린 우리한테 없는 실용적 지혜를 가진 사람들을 우러러보지만, 그들과 다투지는 않아요. 그런데 왜 그들이 우리와 다퉈야 하지요? 살고 또 살게 하라, 우린 그들에게 이렇게 말합니다. 당신들은 실용적 지혜로 살고, 우리는 당신들에 기대어 살게 해달라!\"\n\n그는 웃었지만, 늘 그렇듯 자못 솔직했고 정말로 제 말 그대로를 뜻하는 것 같았습니다.\n\n\"우리에겐 공감이 있단다, 내 장미꽃들아,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"모든 것에 대한 공감이지. 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 아빠!\" 세 딸이 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"사실 그게 우리 가족이 맡은 부문이지요,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 아수라장 같은 삶 속에서 말입니다. 우리는 지켜보고 관심을 가질 줄 알고, 또 정말로 지켜보고 정말로 관심을 갖습니다. 그 이상 우리가 무얼 더 할 수 있겠어요? 여기 내 미녀 딸은 결혼한 지 삼 년이 됐어요. 자, 이 애가 또 다른 어린애와 결혼해서 어린것을 둘이나 더 두었으니 정치경제학으로 따지면 다 잘못된 일이라고 나는 말하겠지만, 그래도 무척 흐뭇한 일이었지요. 그럴 때마다 우린 자그마한 잔치를 벌이고 사교적인 생각을 주고받았답니다. 어느 날 이 애가 어린 남편을 집에 데려왔고, 그들과 어린 새끼들은 위층에 둥지를 틀었지요. 감상이와 희극이도 언젠가 제 남편들을 집에 데려와서 위층에 제 둥지를 틀겠지요. 그렇게 우린 살아갑니다, 어떻게 사는지는 모르지만, 그래도 어떻게든 말이에요.\"\n\n그 애는 두 아이의 어머니라기엔 정말이지 무척 어려 보였고, 나는 그 애도 아이들도 가엾게 여기지 않을 수 없었습니다. 세 딸이 되는대로 자라났고, 한가한 시간에 아비의 노리갯감 노릇이나 할 만큼만 어쩌다 손에 잡히는 가르침을 받았다는 것이 분명했습니다. 그의 회화적 취향이, 내가 보기에, 딸들이 머리를 매만진 저마다의 모양새에 반영되어 있었으니, 미녀 딸은 고전적인 모양으로, 감상 딸은 풍성하게 흘러내리는 모양으로, 희극 딸은 발랄한 이마를 한껏 드러내고 생기 있는 작은 곱슬머리를 눈가에 점점이 흩뜨린 짓궂은 모양이었습니다. 옷도 거기에 맞춰 입었는데, 더없이 너저분하고 부주의한 차림새였습니다.\n\n에이다와 나는 이 아가씨들과 이야기를 나누었고, 그들이 놀랍도록 아비를 닮았다는 것을 알았습니다. 그러는 동안 잔다이스 씨는(머리를 한참이나 문지르며 바람의 방향이 바뀐다고 넌지시 비쳤습니다) 한쪽 구석에서 스킴폴 부인과 이야기를 나누었는데, 우리 귀에 돈이 짤랑거리는 소리가 들리지 않을 수 없었습니다. 스킴폴 씨는 앞서 우리와 함께 집에 가겠노라 자청해두었던 터라, 그러려고 옷을 갈아입으러 물러가 있었습니다.\n\n\"내 장미꽃들아,\" 그가 돌아와서 말했습니다. \"엄마를 잘 보살펴드려라. 오늘 엄마가 편찮으시니까. 잔다이스 씨네 집에 하루 이틀 가 있으면, 나는 종달새 노랫소리도 듣고 내 상냥함도 지킬 수 있을 거야. 알다시피 전에 한번 시험에 들었으니, 집에 그냥 있으면 또 시험에 들 테지.\"\n\n\"그 못된 사람!\" 희극 딸이 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 아빠가 계란풀 옆에 누워 앓으면서 파란 하늘을 바라보고 있을 때 말이에요.\" 로라가 하소연했습니다.\n\n\"건초 냄새가 공기에 가득할 때 말이에요!\" 아레투사가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그건 그 사람한테 시정이 없다는 걸 보여준 거지.\" 스킴폴 씨가 맞장구쳤는데, 더없이 좋은 기분이었습니다. \"그건 천박했어. 거기엔 인간미의 섬세한 결이 없었지! 내 딸들이,\" 그가 우리에게 설명했습니다. \"어느 정직한 사람한테 몹시 화가 나 있답니다—\"\n\n\"정직하지 않아요, 아빠. 말도 안 돼요!\" 셋이 모두 항의했습니다.\n\n\"어느 거친 부류의 친구한테 말이지요—말하자면 몸을 둥글게 만 인간 고슴도치 같은.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 동네에서 빵집을 하는 사람인데, 우리가 그한테서 안락의자 두 개를 빌렸어요. 우린 안락의자가 두 개 필요한데 우리한테는 없었으니, 당연히 그게 있는 사람을 찾아 빌리려 한 거지요. 그런데! 이 뚱한 인간이 빌려주긴 했고, 우린 그걸 닳도록 썼습니다. 다 닳자 그는 그걸 돌려받고 싶어 했어요. 그래서 돌려받았지요. 그럼 만족했겠거니 하겠지요. 천만에. 그는 그게 닳았다고 시비를 걸더군요. 내가 그와 이치를 따지며 그의 잘못을 짚어주었습니다. 내가 말했지요. '이보게 친구, 자네 나이쯤 돼서, 안락의자란 선반에 올려놓고 바라보는 물건이라고 끝까지 우길 만큼 그렇게 고집스러울 수가 있나? 그게 관조하고, 멀찍이서 바라보고, 어느 시점에서 가늠해보는 대상이란 말인가? 이 안락의자들을 앉으라고 빌려준 줄 자네는 모른단 말인가?' 그는 도무지 이치도 모르고 설득도 안 되는 데다 막말까지 하더군요. 지금 이 순간만큼이나 인내심을 발휘하며, 나는 그에게 또 한 번 호소했습니다. 내가 말했지요. '이보게 선량한 친구, 우리 사업 능력이 아무리 다르다 해도, 우린 다 한 위대한 어머니, 자연의 자식들 아닌가. 꽃이 만발한 이 여름 아침에 자네는 여기 나를 보고 있네'(나는 소파에 있었습니다) '내 앞엔 꽃이, 탁자엔 과일이, 머리 위엔 구름 한 점 없는 하늘이, 공기엔 향기가 가득한데, 나는 자연을 관조하고 있어. 우리 공통의 형제애를 걸고 부탁하네만, 나와 이토록 숭고한 대상 사이에 화난 빵장수라는 우스꽝스러운 형상을 끼워 넣지 말아주게!' 그런데도 그자는,\" 스킴폴 씨가 웃음 띤 눈을 들어 장난스러운 놀라움을 지으며 말했습니다. \"끼워 넣었어요, 그 가당찮은 형상을, 그리고 지금도 끼워 넣고, 앞으로도 또 끼워 넣을 겁니다. 그래서 나는 그를 피해 내 친구 잔다이스와 함께 집에 가게 되어 정말 기쁘답니다.\"\n\n스킴폴 부인과 딸들이 남아서 빵장수와 맞닥뜨려야 한다는 사실은 그의 고려에서 벗어난 듯했지만, 이것은 그들 모두에게 워낙 케케묵은 이야기인지라 당연한 일이 되어 있었습니다. 그는 그 자신이 보여준 다른 어떤 면 못지않게 가뿐하고 우아한 다정함으로 가족과 작별하고는, 더없이 화목한 마음으로 우리와 함께 말을 타고 떠났습니다. 우리는 아래층으로 내려가면서 열린 문 몇 개 너머로, 그의 방이 집의 나머지에 견주면 궁전이나 다름없다는 것을 엿볼 수 있었습니다.\n\n이날이 저물기 전에, 그 순간 나에게는 무척 놀라운 일이, 그리고 거기에서 비롯된 일로 인해 내내 잊지 못할 일이 일어나리라고는 나로서는 예감할 수도 없었고, 또 예감하지도 못했습니다. 집으로 돌아오는 길에 우리 손님이 어찌나 들떠 있던지, 나는 그저 그의 말에 귀 기울이며 그를 신기해할 수밖에 없었습니다. 게다가 이런 사람이 나만은 아니었으니, 에이다도 똑같은 매혹에 빠져들었습니다. 후견인으로 말하면, 서머스 타운을 떠날 때만 해도 동쪽에 굳어버릴 듯 위협하던 바람이, 거기서 채 이삼 킬로미터도 가기 전에 완전히 방향을 틀었습니다.\n\n다른 어떤 일에서든 그 어린애 같음이 미심쩍은 것이든 아니든, 스킴폴 씨는 변화와 화창한 날씨를 어린애처럼 즐겼습니다. 길에서 늘어놓은 재담에도 조금도 지치지 않은 채, 그는 우리 누구보다 먼저 응접실에 들어가 있었습니다. 그래서 내가 아직 살림을 둘러보는 동안에도, 그가 피아노 앞에 앉아 이탈리아어와 독일어로 뱃노래며 권주가의 후렴을 수없이 부르는 소리가 들렸습니다.\n\n저녁 식사 조금 전에 우리는 다 모였고, 그는 여전히 피아노 앞에서 그 호사스러운 식으로 한가로이 짤막한 가락을 뜯어내며, 한두 해 전에 시작했다가 싫증 났던 옛 베룰람 성벽 폐허 스케치를 내일 마저 끝내겠다는 둥 틈틈이 이야기하고 있었습니다. 그때 명함 한 장이 들어왔고, 후견인이 놀란 목소리로 소리 내어 읽었습니다. \"레스터 데드록 경!\"\n\n방이 아직 나와 함께 빙빙 돌고 있고 내가 채 몸을 움직일 힘도 없는 사이에, 방문객은 이미 방 안에 들어와 있었습니다. 힘이 있었다면 나는 서둘러 빠져나갔을 것입니다. 어지러운 와중에 나는 창가의 에이다에게 물러갈 침착함도, 창문을 볼 침착함도, 창문이 어디 있는지 알아챌 침착함도 없었습니다. 내 이름이 들렸고, 내가 의자 쪽으로 움직이기도 전에 후견인이 나를 소개하고 있다는 것을 알았습니다.\n\n\"앉으시지요, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"잔다이스 씨,\" 레스터 경이 절을 하고 자리에 앉으며 대답했습니다. \"외람되이 이렇게 찾아온 까닭은—\"\n\n\"외람되다니요, 영광입니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다—링컨셔에서 가는 길에 이렇게 들른 까닭은, 내가 어느 신사에게 품은 불만이, 그것이 아무리 강하다 한들, 그 신사로 말하면—그대도 아는 사람이자 그대를 손님으로 맞은 적이 있고, 그러니 더는 거론하지 않겠습니다만—그 불만 때문에 그대가, 더구나 그대의 보호와 인솔을 받는 숙녀분들이 내 집 체스니 월드에서 점잖고 세련된 취향을 만족시킬 만한 자그마한 것들이나마 둘러보지 못하게 된 데 대해 유감을 표하기 위함입니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하십니다, 레스터 경. 여기 계신 저 숙녀들을 대신해서, 또 저 자신을 위해서도, 진심으로 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"잔다이스 씨, 어쩌면 내가 앞서 말한 까닭으로 더는 언급을 삼가는 그 신사가—잔다이스 씨, 어쩌면 그 신사가 그렇게까지 내 됨됨이를 오해하는 무례를 범하여, 그대로 하여금, 그대가 내 링컨셔 영지 사람들로부터 그 집을 찾는 모든 신사 숙녀에게 보이도록 일러둔 그 정중함과 그 예의로 영접받지 못하리라 믿게 만들었을 수도 있겠습니다. 나는 그저, 선생, 실상은 그 반대라는 점을 일러두고자 할 따름입니다.\"\n\n후견인은 한마디 말로도 대답하지 않은 채 이 말을 부드럽게 흘려보냈습니다.\n\n\"이 일은 나에게 고통을 안겼습니다, 잔다이스 씨.\" 레스터 경이 무겁게 말을 이었습니다. \"장담하건대, 선생, 그것은—나에게—고통을—안겼습니다. 그 고장에서 그대와 함께 있던 어느 신사가, 게다가 미술에 대한 교양 있는 취향을 갖춘 듯한 분이, 그 또한 그 비슷한 까닭으로, 본래 들이고 싶었을, 또 그 가운데 몇몇은 어쩌면 그만한 값어치를 했을, 그런 여유와 그런 주의와 그런 정성으로 가문의 그림들을 살피지 못하고 단념했다는 것을 체스니 월드의 가정부에게서 듣고 말입니다.\" 여기서 그는 명함 한 장을 꺼내, 자못 근엄하게, 외알 안경 너머로 약간 더듬으며 읽었습니다. \"히롤드—허럴드—해럴드—스캠플링—스컴플링—실례했습니다—스킴폴 씨.\"\n\n\"이분이 해럴드 스킴폴 씨입니다.\" 후견인이 분명 놀란 기색으로 말했습니다.\n\n\"오!\" 레스터 경이 외쳤습니다. \"스킴폴 씨를 만나, 직접 유감을 표할 기회를 얻게 되어 기쁩니다. 선생, 다음에 다시 내 고장에 오실 때는 부디 그와 같은 거리낌을 느끼지 마시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하십니다, 레스터 데드록 경. 그렇게 격려해주시니, 나는 반드시 그 아름다운 댁을 다시 한번 찾아뵙는 기쁨과 영광을 누리겠습니다. 체스니 월드 같은 곳의 소유주들은,\" 스킴폴 씨가 평소의 흐뭇하고 거침없는 태도로 말했습니다. \"공공의 은인이지요. 그들은 친절하게도, 우리 가난한 사람들이 우러러보고 즐길 수 있도록 숱한 즐거운 것들을 간직해줍니다. 그러니 그것들이 주는 그 모든 감탄과 즐거움을 거두어들이지 않는다는 것은 우리 은인에게 배은망덕한 짓이지요.\"\n\n레스터 경은 이 견해를 대단히 흡족해하는 듯했습니다. \"예술가시오, 선생?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다. \"완전히 한가한 사람이지요. 한낱 애호가일 뿐입니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 이것을 더더욱 흡족해하는 듯했습니다. 그는 스킴폴 씨가 다음에 링컨셔로 내려올 때 자기가 체스니 월드에 있는 행운을 누릴 수 있기를 바란다고 했습니다. 스킴폴 씨는 무척 우쭐하고 영광이라는 뜻을 내비쳤습니다.\n\n\"스킴폴 씨가 언급하기를,\" 레스터 경이 다시 후견인을 향해 말을 이었습니다. \"가정부에게 언급하기를, 그도 알아챘겠지만 그 가정부는 가문의 오래되고 충직한 가신인데—\"\n\n(\"그러니까 며칠 전 서머슨 양과 클레어 양을 찾아뵈러 내려간 김에 그 집을 둘러볼 때 말이지요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 우리에게 가뿐하게 설명했습니다.)\n\n\"—거기서 예전에 함께 머물렀던 친구가 잔다이스 씨라고 언급했다더군요.\" 레스터 경은 그 이름을 지닌 사람에게 고개를 숙였습니다. \"그래서 내가, 앞서 유감을 표한 그 사정을 알게 된 것입니다. 이런 일이 어느 신사에게든 일어났다는 것, 잔다이스 씨, 더구나 예전에 데드록 부인과 알고 지냈고, 실은 부인과 먼 친척뻘이라 하며, 부인이(이건 부인에게 직접 들은 바입니다만) 깊이 존경하는 그런 신사에게 일어났다는 것은, 장담하건대, 나에게—고통을—안깁니다.\"\n\n\"그 일은 더 말씀하지 마시지요, 레스터 경.\" 후견인이 받았습니다. \"저는, 또 우리 모두 분명 그러하듯, 경의 배려에 깊이 감복하고 있습니다. 사실 잘못은 제게 있었으니, 마땅히 제가 사과드려야 합니다.\"\n\n나는 한 번도 고개를 들지 않았습니다. 방문객을 보지도 않았고, 심지어 나 자신에게조차 그 대화가 들리는 것 같지도 않았습니다. 그런데도 그것을 떠올릴 수 있다는 것이 신기한데, 오가는 동안에는 나에게 아무 인상도 남기지 않는 것 같았기 때문입니다. 그들이 말하는 소리가 들리긴 했지만, 내 머릿속은 너무도 혼란스러웠고 이 신사를 본능적으로 피하려는 마음에 그의 존재가 어찌나 고통스러웠는지, 머리가 쿵쿵 울리고 심장이 두근거리는 통에 나는 아무것도 알아듣지 못하는 것만 같았습니다.\n\n\"이 문제를 데드록 부인에게 이야기했더니,\" 레스터 경이 일어서며 말했습니다. \"부인이 알려주기를, 그들이 그 근방에 머무는 동안 우연히 만나, 잔다이스 씨와 그 피후견인들과 몇 마디 나누는 기쁨을 누렸다더군요. 잔다이스 씨, 내가 이미 스킴폴 씨에게 드린 그 다짐을, 그대 자신과 저 숙녀들에게도 거듭 전하게 해주십시오. 사정상 보이손 씨가 내 집에 들러주면 내가 흐뭇하겠다는 말은 분명 할 수 없습니다만, 그 사정은 오직 그 신사 한 사람에게 국한되며 그를 넘어서지는 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람에 대한 내 오랜 생각은 당신도 아시잖습니까.\" 스킴폴 씨가 가볍게 우리에게 호소하며 말했습니다. \"온갖 색을 다 진홍빛으로 만들기로 작심한 상냥한 황소지요!\"\n\n레스터 데드록 경은 그런 인물에 대한 말은 단 한마디도 더 들을 수 없다는 듯 헛기침을 하고는, 자못 격식과 예의를 갖추어 작별했습니다. 나는 되도록 빨리 내 방으로 가서, 마음의 평정을 되찾을 때까지 거기 머물렀습니다. 마음이 무척 흐트러져 있었지만, 다시 아래층으로 내려갔을 때 그들이 그저 내가 링컨셔의 그 위대한 준남작 앞에서 수줍어하며 말 한마디 못 했다고 놀려대기만 하는 것을 보고 나는 안도했습니다.\n\n그 무렵 나는, 내가 아는 것을 후견인에게 털어놓아야 할 시기가 왔다고 마음을 정했습니다. 내가 어머니와 마주치게 될 가능성, 어머니의 집으로 끌려가게 될 가능성, 심지어 나와는 아무리 먼 관계라 해도 스킴폴 씨가 어머니의 남편에게서 친절과 신세를 입게 될 가능성이 너무도 괴로워서, 나는 후견인의 도움 없이는 더 이상 나 자신을 추스를 수 없을 것 같았습니다.\n\n밤이 되어 잠자리에 들 채비를 하고, 에이다와 내가 우리의 예쁜 방에서 늘 하던 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 나는 다시 방문을 나서 책들 사이에 있는 후견인을 찾아갔습니다. 그가 늘 그 시각에 책을 읽는다는 것을 알고 있었는데, 다가가면서 보니 그의 독서등 불빛이 복도로 새어 나오고 있었습니다.\n\n\"들어가도 될까요, 후견인?\"\n\n\"그럼, 들어오렴, 꼬마 아가씨. 무슨 일이니?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 아니에요. 이 조용한 시간에 제 이야기를 좀 드리고 싶어서요.\"\n\n그는 나에게 의자를 내주고, 책을 덮어 옆에 치워두고는, 그 친절하고 주의 깊은 얼굴을 나에게 돌렸습니다. 나는 그 얼굴에, 전에 한 번 본 적 있는 그 묘한 표정이 어려 있는 것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었습니다—그날 밤, 그가 내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 어떤 괴로움도 자기에게는 없다고 말했던 그날 밤에 본 표정이었습니다.\n\n\"너에게 관계되는 일은, 사랑하는 에스더,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"우리 모두에게 관계되는 일이란다. 네가 말하고 싶은 마음보다 내가 듣고 싶은 마음이 결코 덜하지 않아.\"\n\n\"저도 알아요, 후견인. 하지만 저는 후견인의 조언과 힘이 너무도 절실해요. 오! 오늘 밤 제게 그게 얼마나 절실한지 모르실 거예요.\"\n\n그는 내가 이토록 진지한 데 마음의 준비가 안 된 듯했고, 심지어 조금 놀란 기색이었습니다.\n\n\"제가 후견인께 말씀드리려고 얼마나 마음 졸였는지도요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"오늘 그 방문객이 다녀간 뒤로 줄곧 말이에요.\"\n\n\"방문객이라니, 얘야! 레스터 데드록 경 말이냐?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n그는 팔짱을 끼고 앉아 더없이 깊은 놀라움이 어린 얼굴로 나를 바라보며, 내가 다음에 무슨 말을 할지 기다렸습니다. 나는 그에게 어떻게 마음의 준비를 시켜야 할지 알 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"아니, 에스더,\" 그가 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. \"우리 방문객과 너야말로 이 세상에서 내가 가장 마지막으로나 한데 엮어 생각할 두 사람인데!\"\n\n\"오, 네, 후견인, 저도 알아요. 저도 바로 얼마 전까지는 그랬어요.\"\n\n미소가 그의 얼굴에서 사라지고, 그는 전보다 더 진지해졌습니다. 그는 문으로 건너가 닫혔는지 확인하고는(하지만 그건 내가 이미 살펴두었습니다), 다시 내 앞 자리로 돌아왔습니다.\n\n\"후견인,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"우리가 뇌우를 만났을 때, 데드록 부인이 자기 언니 이야기를 하던 것 기억하세요?\"\n\n\"물론이지. 물론 기억한단다.\"\n\n\"그리고 부인과 그 언니가 사이가 틀어져 제각각 길을 갔다고 일러주던 것도요?\"\n\n\"물론.\"\n\n\"그분들은 왜 갈라섰나요, 후견인?\"\n\n나를 바라보는 그의 얼굴이 싹 달라졌습니다. \"얘야, 그게 무슨 질문이냐! 나는 도무지 모른단다. 그들 자신 말고는 아무도 끝내 몰랐을 거야. 그 아름답고 도도한 두 여인의 비밀이 무엇이었는지 누가 알 수 있겠니! 너도 데드록 부인을 보았지. 만일 그 언니를 본 적이 있다면, 그 언니가 동생만큼이나 단호하고 오만했다는 걸 알았을 게다.\"\n\n\"오, 후견인, 저는 그분을 보고 또 보았어요, 수없이요!\"\n\n\"보았다고?\"\n\n그는 잠깐 멈추어 입술을 깨물었습니다. \"그렇다면, 에스더, 네가 오래전 나에게 보이손 이야기를 했을 때, 그리고 내가 너에게 그가 한때 결혼 직전까지 갔으나 그 여인이 죽은 건 아니지만 그에게는 죽은 사람이 되었고, 그 일이 그의 후일에 영향을 미쳤다고 일러주었을 때—너는 그걸 다 알고 있었던 거냐, 그 여인이 누구인지 알고 있었던 거냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 후견인.\" 나는 어렴풋이 밝아오는 그 빛이 두려워 받았습니다. \"지금도 모르고요.\"\n\n\"데드록 부인의 언니란다.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜,\" 나는 차마 그에게 묻기조차 어려웠습니다. \"왜, 후견인, 부디 말씀해주세요, 그분들은 왜 갈라선 건가요?\"\n\n\"그건 그 언니가 한 일이었고, 그 동기는 굽힐 줄 모르는 가슴속에 묻어두었지. 나중에 그가 추측하기로는(그저 추측일 뿐이지만) 동생과 다투던 그 일로 그 오만한 자존심이 어떤 상처를 입어, 이성을 다 잃을 만큼 깊이 베인 거라고 했어. 하지만 그 언니는 그 편지를 쓴 날부터 자기는 그에게 죽은 사람이라고—말 그대로 정말 그렇게 되었지—써 보냈고, 그 결심은 그의 도도한 기질과 팽팽한 명예심을 자기가 잘 알기에 어쩔 수 없이 내린 것이라고 했단다. 그 두 가지는 그 언니의 천성이기도 했지. 그에게 있는 그 핵심을 헤아려서, 또 자기 안에 있는 그것까지 헤아려서, 자기가 그 희생을 치르는 것이며, 그 안에서 살다가 그 안에서 죽겠다고 했어. 그 언니는 그 둘을 다 한 모양이야, 안타깝지만. 분명 그는 그 시각 이후로 두 번 다시 그 언니를 보지도, 소식을 듣지도 못했지. 그 누구도 못 들었고.\"\n\n\"오, 후견인, 제가 무슨 짓을 한 걸까요!\" 나는 슬픔에 못 이겨 외쳤습니다. \"제가 아무것도 모른 채 얼마나 큰 슬픔을 일으킨 걸까요!\"\n\n\"네가 일으켰다고, 에스더?\"\n\n\"네, 후견인. 아무것도 모른 채, 그러나 더없이 분명하게요. 그 외따로 지내던 언니가 제 첫 기억이에요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야!\" 그가 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"그래요, 후견인, 그래요! 그리고 그분의 동생이 제 어머니예요!\"\n\n나는 그에게 어머니의 편지를 다 이야기하려 했지만, 그는 그때 그것을 들으려 하지 않았습니다. 그는 너무도 다정하고 지혜롭게 나에게 말해주었고, 내 마음이 더 나은 상태에 있을 때 나 스스로 어렴풋이 생각하고 바랐던 모든 것을 너무도 또렷이 내 앞에 펼쳐 보여주어서, 그토록 오랜 세월 그에게 뜨거운 감사로 가득 차 있던 나였지만, 그날 밤만큼 그를 깊이 사랑한 적도, 마음속으로 그토록 온전히 그에게 감사한 적도 없었던 것 같았습니다. 그리고 그가 나를 내 방으로 데려다주고 문 앞에서 입 맞추어주었을 때, 마침내 내가 잠자리에 누웠을 때, 내 생각은 이러했습니다. 내가 어떻게 해야 충분히 부지런할 수 있을까, 어떻게 해야 충분히 선해질 수 있을까, 나의 보잘것없는 방식으로나마 어떻게 해야 나 자신을 충분히 잊고, 그에게 충분히 헌신하고, 다른 이들에게 충분히 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어, 내가 그를 얼마나 축복하고 우러르는지 보여드릴 수 있을까.\n\n이튿날 아침 후견인이 나를 방으로 불렀고, 나는 그제야 어젯밤 다 하지 못했던 이야기를 그에게 들려주었습니다. 후견인은 비밀을 지키는 것과 어제 같은 만남을 다시는 겪지 않도록 피하는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없다고 말했습니다. 그는 내 마음을 이해했고 전적으로 함께 느꼈습니다. 심지어 스킴폴 씨가 그 기회를 함부로 이용하지 못하도록 단속하는 일까지 자신이 떠맡았습니다. 나에게 굳이 이름을 댈 필요가 없는 그 한 사람에 대해서는, 이제 그가 조언하거나 도울 길이 없었습니다. 그러기를 바랐지만 그럴 수 없는 일이었습니다. 그녀가 언급했던 변호사를 향한 불신이 근거 있는 것이라면, 그리고 그는 그것을 거의 의심하지 않았는데, 그는 발각을 두려워했습니다. 그는 그 변호사에 대해 얼굴로도 평판으로도 어느 정도 알고 있었고, 그가 위험한 인물이라는 것만큼은 분명했습니다. 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 나는 그 자신과 마찬가지로 결백하며 그 일에 아무런 영향도 미칠 수 없다는 것을, 그는 근심 어린 애정과 다정함으로 거듭 나에게 새겨주었습니다.\n\n\"게다가 내가 알기로는,\" 그가 말했습니다, \"어떤 의심도 네 쪽으로 향하지는 않는단다, 얘야. 그런 연관이 없어도 의심은 얼마든지 있을 수 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"변호사 말고도요,\" 나는 대답했습니다. \"하지만 마음이 불안해진 뒤로 다른 두 사람이 떠올랐어요.\" 그러고 나서 나는 거피 씨에 대한 모든 것을 그에게 털어놓았습니다. 내가 그의 뜻을 거의 알아차리지 못하던 시절에 그가 막연한 추측을 품었을지 모른다는 게 두려웠지만, 지난번 만남 이후 그가 침묵을 지키는 것에는 완전한 신뢰를 보낸다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그렇다면 그 사람은 당분간 제쳐두어도 되겠구나. 다른 한 사람은 누구냐?\"\n\n나는 프랑스인 하녀와, 그녀가 나에게 자신을 써달라며 보였던 그 간절한 제안을 그에게 떠올리게 했습니다.\n\n\"하!\" 그가 생각에 잠겨 대답했습니다. \"그 여자는 서기보다 더 경계해야 할 사람이로구나. 하지만 얘야, 결국 그건 새 일자리를 구하려던 것뿐이었어. 그 여자는 얼마 전에 너와 에이다를 본 적이 있으니, 네 생각이 머릿속에 떠오른 건 자연스러운 일이지. 그저 네 하녀가 되겠다고 제 발로 나선 것뿐이잖니. 그 이상은 아무것도 하지 않았어.\"\n\n\"그 여자의 태도가 이상했어요,\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 그 여자가 신발을 벗어 던지고 제 죽음의 자리로 끝날 수도 있었던 그 걸음을 태연히 즐기겠다고 했을 때도 태도가 이상했지,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그런 가능성과 우연을 일일이 따지는 건 쓸데없이 스스로를 괴롭히고 고통스럽게 하는 일이야. 그렇게 따지고 들면, 위태로운 의미로 가득 차 보이지 않을 무해한 정황이란 거의 없단다. 희망을 갖거라, 우리 작은 아가씨. 너는 너 자신보다 더 나은 무엇이 될 수는 없어. 그러니 이 사실을 알기 전과 똑같이, 이 사실을 알고도 그저 너 자신으로 있으렴. 그것이 모두를 위해 네가 할 수 있는 최선이야. 너와 비밀을 나누어 가진 나로서는—\"\n\n\"게다가 그 비밀을 이렇게나 가볍게 덜어주시는 후견인께서는,\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"—내가 멀리서나마 살필 수 있는 한, 그 집안에서 벌어지는 일에 마음을 쓰마. 그리고 여기서조차 이름을 부르지 않는 게 나은 그분께 손을 내밀어 아주 작은 도움이라도 드릴 수 있는 때가 온다면, 그분의 사랑하는 따님을 위해서라도 나는 반드시 그 일을 하겠다.\"\n\n나는 온 마음을 다해 그에게 고마워했습니다. 고마워하는 것 말고 내가 무엇을 더 할 수 있었겠습니까! 내가 문을 나서려는데 그가 잠깐만 머물라고 했습니다. 얼른 돌아서니, 그의 얼굴에 또다시 그 같은 표정이 떠올라 있는 것이 보였습니다. 그러자 별안간, 어떻게 그랬는지는 모르지만, 그 표정의 의미를 내가 안다는 것이 새롭고도 아득한 가능성으로 머릿속에 번뜩였습니다.\n\n\"사랑하는 에스더야,\" 후견인이 말했습니다, \"너에게 하고 싶은 이야기가 오래전부터 내 마음속에 있었단다.\"\n\n\"정말요?\"\n\n\"그 이야기를 꺼내기가 어려웠고, 지금도 여전히 어렵구나. 그것을 아주 신중하게 말하고 또 아주 신중하게 헤아려보았으면 한다. 내가 그것을 글로 써도 괜찮겠니?\"\n\n\"사랑하는 후견인, 제가 읽을 것을 후견인께서 무엇이든 써주시겠다는데 제가 어떻게 마다하겠어요?\"\n\n\"그럼 보거라, 얘야,\" 그가 명랑한 미소를 지으며 말했습니다, \"지금 이 순간 내가 여느 때처럼 더없이 솔직하고 편안해 보이느냐—내가 평소와 똑같이 열려 있고, 정직하고, 예스럽게 보이느냐?\"\n\n나는 더없이 진지하게 대답했습니다, \"그럼요.\" 그것은 가장 엄밀한 진실이었습니다. 그의 순간적인 망설임은 사라졌고(그것은 일 분도 가지 않았습니다), 그의 훌륭하고 분별 있고 다정하고 진실한 태도가 되돌아와 있었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"내가 무언가를 감추거나, 말한 것과 다른 뜻을 품거나, 무엇이든 조금이라도 숨기는 것이 있어 보이느냐?\" 그가 맑고 밝은 눈으로 내 눈을 들여다보며 말했습니다.\n\n나는 결단코 그렇지 않다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"너는 나를 온전히 믿을 수 있겠느냐, 그리고 내가 다짐하는 바에 철저히 기댈 수 있겠느냐, 에스더?\"\n\n\"더없이 철저히요,\" 나는 온 마음을 담아 말했습니다.\n\n\"착한 아이로구나,\" 후견인이 대답했습니다, \"네 손을 다오.\"\n\n그는 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고, 팔로 나를 가볍게 받친 채, 그 변함없이 진실하고 신선하며 한결같은 태도로—한순간에 그 집을 내 보금자리로 만들어준 그 옛날의 보살피는 태도로—내 얼굴을 내려다보며 말했습니다. \"역마차를 타고 가던 그 겨울날 이후로, 너는 나를 바꾸어놓았단다, 작은 아가씨. 그때부터 줄곧, 처음부터 끝까지, 너는 나에게 한없는 좋은 일을 해주었어.\"\n\n\"아, 후견인, 그때부터 후견인께서 저에게 해주신 게 얼마나 많은데요!\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 그가 말했습니다, \"지금은 그건 떠올릴 일이 아니야.\"\n\n\"결코 잊을 수 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"그래, 에스더,\" 그가 부드러운 진지함으로 말했습니다, \"지금은 그걸 잊어야 한다, 잠시 동안만이라도 잊어야 해. 지금 네가 기억해야 할 건 오직, 네가 아는 그대로의 나를 그 무엇도 바꿀 수 없다는 것뿐이야. 그것만큼은 확실히 믿을 수 있겠느냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"믿을 수 있고, 또 믿어요,\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"그것만으로도 대단한 거야,\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"그게 전부지. 하지만 나는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이지는 않으마. 네가 아는 그대로의 나를 그 무엇도 바꿀 수 없다는 걸 네 마음속에서 온전히 결심하기 전까지는, 내 마음속의 이 이야기를 쓰지 않겠다. 네가 그것을 조금이라도 의심한다면, 나는 결코 그것을 쓰지 않을 거야. 잘 헤아려본 끝에 그것을 확신한다면, 이레 뒤 오늘 밤 찰리를 나에게 보내거라—'편지를 가지러' 말이다. 하지만 네가 온전히 확신하지 못한다면, 절대 보내지 마라. 명심하렴, 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 이 일에서도 나는 네 진실함을 믿는다. 그 한 가지 점에 대해 온전히 확신이 서지 않거든, 절대 보내지 마라!\"\n\n\"후견인,\" 나는 말했습니다, \"저는 이미 확신해요. 후견인께서 저를 향한 마음을 바꿀 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 저 역시 그 믿음을 바꿀 수 없어요. 편지를 가지러 찰리를 보내겠어요.\"\n\n그는 내 손을 흔들고는 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다. 그도 나도, 그 한 주 내내 이 대화에 관해서는 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않았습니다. 약속한 밤이 오자, 나는 혼자가 되자마자 찰리에게 말했습니다, \"잔다이스 씨 방문을 두드리고 가서, 내가 보냈다고 말씀드려—'편지를 가지러' 왔다고.\" 찰리는 계단을 오르고, 계단을 내려가고, 복도를 따라 걸어갔습니다—그 예스러운 집의 구불구불한 길이 그날 밤 귀를 기울이고 있던 나에게는 무척 길게만 느껴졌습니다—그러고는 복도를 따라, 계단을 내려가, 계단을 올라 돌아와서 편지를 가져왔습니다. \"탁자 위에 놓아두렴, 찰리,\" 나는 말했습니다. 찰리는 그것을 탁자 위에 놓고 잠자리에 들었고, 나는 그것을 집어 들지도 않은 채 그저 바라보며 앉아 온갖 것들을 생각했습니다.\n\n나는 그늘에 덮였던 어린 시절에서 시작해, 그 주눅 든 나날들을 거쳐, 이모가 그 단호한 얼굴을 차갑고 굳은 채 죽어 누워 있던 그 무거운 시절에 이르렀습니다. 그때 나는 세상에 말을 건넬 사람도, 바라볼 사람도 하나 없는 것보다 더 외롭게 레이철 부인과 함께 있었습니다. 그러고는 내가 둘러싼 모든 이들에게서 벗을 찾고 사랑을 받는 큰 복을 누렸던, 달라진 나날로 넘어갔습니다. 사랑하는 그 아이를 처음 보고, 내 삶의 우아함이자 아름다움이었던 그 자매 같은 애정 속으로 받아들여진 그때에 이르렀습니다. 그 춥고 맑던 밤, 우리의 기대에 찬 얼굴들 위로 바로 그 창문에서 빛나던, 한 번도 빛바랜 적 없는 환영의 첫 밝은 빛줄기를 떠올렸습니다. 나는 그곳에서 보낸 행복한 삶을 다시 살아냈고, 병과 회복을 다시 겪었으며, 그토록 달라진 나 자신과 그토록 변함없는 내 주위 사람들을 생각했습니다. 그리고 이 모든 행복은 하나의 중심 인물에게서 비치는 빛처럼 빛났으니, 그 인물은 탁자 위의 편지로 내 앞에 놓여 있었습니다.\n\n나는 편지를 열어 읽었습니다. 그것은 나를 향한 사랑에서, 또 나에게 베푼 사심 없는 당부에서, 한 마디 한 마디에 담긴 나를 향한 배려에서 너무도 절절해, 내 눈은 한 번에 많이 읽지 못하도록 자꾸만 흐려졌습니다. 그래도 나는 그것을 내려놓기 전에 세 번 통독했습니다. 미리부터 나는 그 내용을 안다고 생각했고, 정말 알고 있었습니다. 그것은 나에게, 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되어주겠느냐고 묻고 있었습니다.\n\n그것은 그토록 많은 사랑을 담고 있으면서도 연애편지는 아니었으니, 그가 언제든 나에게 말하던 그대로 쓰여 있었습니다. 나는 모든 줄에서 그의 얼굴을 보았고, 그의 목소리를 들었으며, 그 다정하고 보살피는 태도의 영향을 느꼈습니다. 그것은 마치 우리의 처지가 뒤바뀐 듯이, 마치 모든 선행이 나의 것이고 그 선행이 일깨운 모든 감정이 그의 것인 듯이 나에게 말을 건넸습니다. 그것은 내가 젊고 그는 한창때를 지났다는 것, 그가 무르익은 나이에 이른 반면 나는 아이였다는 것, 그가 은빛 머리로 나에게 글을 쓰고 있으며 이 모든 것을 너무도 잘 알기에 신중히 헤아려보라고 빠짐없이 펼쳐 보인다는 것을 거듭 짚었습니다. 그것은, 그런 결혼으로 내가 얻을 것은 아무것도 없고 그것을 거절한다 해도 잃을 것은 아무것도 없으니, 어떤 새로운 관계도 그가 나를 품은 그 다정함을 더 키울 수 없으며, 내 결정이 무엇이든 그것이 옳으리라 그는 확신한다고 했습니다. 다만 그는 우리가 최근에 나눈 속내 이후 이 걸음을 새로이 헤아려보았고, 비록 그것이 단 한 가지 보잘것없는 사례를 통해서라도 온 세상이 내 어린 시절의 그 가혹한 예언을 기꺼이 거짓으로 만들어줄 것임을 나에게 보여주는 데 도움이 된다면, 그 걸음을 내딛기로 마음먹었다고 했습니다. 내가 그에게 어떤 행복을 줄 수 있는지를 가장 늦게 알아차린 건 나였지만, 그것에 대해서는 그가 더 말하지 않았으니, 나는 그에게 빚진 것이 아무것도 없으며 오히려 그가 나에게 빚진 자이고, 그것도 아주 많이 빚졌다는 것을 늘 기억해야 하기 때문이라고 했습니다. 그는 우리의 앞날을 자주 생각해왔는데, (이제 거의 성년이 다 된) 에이다가 우리를 떠나고 지금의 생활 방식이 깨질 수밖에 없는 때가 반드시 오리라는 것을, 게다가 어쩌면 머지않아 닥칠지도 모른다는 것을 내다보고서, 이 제안을 곱씹는 것이 익숙해졌다고 했습니다. 그렇게 해서 그가 이 제안을 한 것이었습니다. 만약 내가 그에게 나의 보호자가 될 더없이 마땅한 권리를 언젠가 줄 수 있다고 느낀다면, 그리고 죽음 말고는 그 어떤 사소한 우연이나 변화도 넘어서서, 그의 남은 삶의 사랑스러운 동반자가 행복하고 떳떳하게 될 수 있다고 느낀다면, 그렇다 해도 이 편지가 아직 이토록 새로운 동안에는 나를 돌이킬 수 없이 옭아매게 할 수 없으며, 그런 경우에도 다시 헤아려볼 넉넉한 시간을 반드시 가져야 한다고 했습니다. 그 경우든 그 반대의 경우든, 그는 옛 관계에서, 옛 태도에서, 내가 그를 부르던 옛 이름에서 변함이 없도록 해달라고 했습니다. 그리고 그의 밝은 더든 아주머니이자 작은 살림꾼에 대해서는, 그녀가 언제까지나 한결같으리라는 것을 자기는 안다고 했습니다.\n\n이것이 편지의 알맹이였습니다. 그것은 처음부터 끝까지, 마치 그가 정말로 나의 책임진 후견인으로서 한 벗의 제안을 공정하게 대변하면서도, 그 청렴함으로 그 벗에게 불리한 사정까지 빠짐없이 진술하기라도 하듯, 그런 공정함과 품위로 쓰여 있었습니다.\n\n그러나 그는, 내가 더 보기 좋았던 시절에도 이와 똑같은 일을 마음에 품었다가 삼갔다는 것은 내비치지 않았습니다. 내 옛 얼굴이 사라지고 내가 아무 매력도 없게 되었어도, 그가 더 곱던 시절과 똑같이 나를 사랑할 수 있다는 것도. 내 출생의 비밀이 밝혀진 일이 그에게 아무런 충격도 주지 않았다는 것도. 그의 너그러움이 내 흉한 모습과 내가 물려받은 수치를 넘어선다는 것도. 내가 그런 신의를 필요로 하면 할수록, 끝까지 그를 더 굳게 믿어도 된다는 것도, 그는 내비치지 않았습니다.\n\n그러나 나는 알았습니다, 이제는 그것을 잘 알았습니다. 그것은 내가 좇아온 그 자애로운 이야기의 마무리로 나에게 다가왔고, 나는 내가 할 일이 단 하나뿐임을 느꼈습니다. 내 삶을 그의 행복에 바치는 것은 그에게 보잘것없이 고마워하는 일에 지나지 않았건만, 며칠 전 그날 밤 내가 바란 것이 바로 그에게 고마워할 어떤 새로운 길이 아니었던가요?\n\n그래도 나는 무척 많이 울었습니다. 편지를 읽은 뒤 벅차오른 가슴 때문만도, 그 앞날의 낯섦 때문만도 아니었습니다—내용을 예상하고 있었는데도 그것은 낯설었지만—이름도 또렷한 형체도 없는 무언가가 어렴풋이 나에게서 사라져버린 것만 같았기 때문이었습니다. 나는 무척 행복했고, 무척 고마웠고, 무척 희망에 차 있었습니다. 그런데도 나는 무척 많이 울었습니다.\n\n이윽고 나는 내 오래된 거울 앞으로 갔습니다. 두 눈이 붉게 부어 있어서 나는 말했습니다, \"오, 에스더, 에스더, 그게 정말 너란 말이니!\" 거울 속 얼굴이 이 책망에 또 울려는 듯했지만, 나는 그것을 향해 손가락을 치켜들었고, 그러자 그것은 멈추었습니다.\n\n\"네가 그토록 달라진 모습을 나에게 보여주며 나를 위로하던 그 차분한 표정에 한결 가깝구나, 얘야!\" 나는 머리를 풀어 내리기 시작하며 말했습니다. \"네가 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되거든 새처럼 명랑해야 한다. 사실은 언제까지나 명랑해야지. 그러니 이번 한 번으로 영원히, 그렇게 시작해보자꾸나.\"\n\n나는 이제 아주 편안하게 머리를 매만져 나갔습니다. 아직 조금 흐느끼긴 했지만, 그건 내가 울었기 때문이었지 그때 울고 있었기 때문은 아니었습니다.\n\n\"그러니 에스더야, 얘야, 너는 평생 행복한 거란다. 더없이 좋은 벗들과 함께 행복하고, 정든 보금자리에서 행복하고, 많은 선을 베풀 힘을 지녀 행복하고, 가장 훌륭한 분의 분에 넘치는 사랑을 받아 행복하지.\"\n\n문득 나는, 후견인이 다른 누군가와 결혼했더라면 나는 어떤 기분이었을지, 또 나는 어떻게 했을지를 생각했습니다! 그것이야말로 정말 큰 변화였을 것입니다. 그것은 내 삶을 너무도 새롭고 텅 빈 모습으로 그려 보여서, 나는 살림 열쇠들을 흔들어 본 뒤 다시 바구니에 내려놓기 전에 그것들에 입을 맞추었습니다.\n\n그러고 나서 거울 앞에서 머리를 매만지며, 나는 계속 생각해 나갔습니다. 내 병의 깊은 자국과 내 출생의 사정이야말로 내가 부지런하고, 부지런하고, 또 부지런해야 할—정직하고 겸손한 온갖 방식으로 쓸모 있고, 상냥하고, 도움이 되어야 할—새로운 이유에 지나지 않는다고, 나는 얼마나 자주 마음속으로 생각해왔던가요. 정말이지, 지금이야말로 병적으로 주저앉아 울기에 딱 좋은 때로군요! 내가 언젠가 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되리라는 것이 처음에 조금이라도 낯설게 느껴졌다는 것(그것이 울 핑계가 된다면 말이지만, 그렇지도 않았습니다)에 대해서라면, 그게 왜 낯설게 느껴져야 하나요? 내가 그런 것을 생각해보지 못했다 해도, 다른 사람들은 그런 일들을 생각해보았으니까요. \"기억나지 않니, 못난 우리 아가씨,\" 나는 거울을 보며 나 자신에게 물었습니다, \"이 흉터들이 생기기 전에 우드코트 부인이 네 결혼에 대해 뭐라고 말했는지—\"\n\n아마 그 이름이 그것들을 내 기억으로 불러왔나 봅니다. 그 꽃의 말라붙은 잔해 말입니다. 이제는 그것을 간직하지 않는 편이 나을 것입니다. 그것은 다만 완전히 지나가 사라진 무언가를 기억하기 위해 보관해온 것이었지만, 이제는 간직하지 않는 편이 나을 것입니다.\n\n그것들은 어느 책 속에 끼워져 있었는데, 마침 그 책은 옆방에—에이다의 침실과 내 침실을 가르는 우리 거실에 있었습니다. 나는 촛불을 들고 살그머니 들어가 그것을 선반에서 가져왔습니다. 그것을 손에 쥐고 나니, 열린 문 너머로 아름다운 내 사랑하는 아이가 잠들어 누워 있는 것이 보여, 나는 살며시 들어가 그 아이에게 입을 맞추었습니다.\n\n내가 마음이 약했다는 걸 압니다, 그리고 울 까닭이라곤 도무지 없었습니다. 그런데도 나는 그 사랑스러운 얼굴 위에 눈물 한 방울을, 또 한 방울을, 또 한 방울을 떨구었습니다. 그보다 더 약하게도, 나는 그 시든 꽃들을 꺼내어 잠깐 그 아이의 입술에 갖다 대었습니다. 나는 리처드를 향한 그 아이의 사랑을 생각했습니다. 사실 그 꽃들은 그것과 아무 상관이 없었지만요. 그러고는 그것들을 내 방으로 가져가 촛불에 태웠고, 그것들은 순식간에 재가 되었습니다.\n\n이튿날 아침 식당으로 들어가 보니, 후견인은 여느 때와 똑같이, 더없이 솔직하고, 열려 있고, 거리낌이 없었습니다. 그의 태도에 조금의 어색함도 없었기에, 내 태도에도 어색함이 없었습니다(아니, 없었다고 나는 생각합니다). 오전 내내 나는 아무도 없을 때 여러 번 그와 드나들며 함께 있었고, 어쩌면 그가 편지에 대해 나에게 말을 꺼낼지도 모르겠다고 생각했지만, 그는 한마디도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n그렇게, 다음 날 아침에도, 그다음 날에도, 스킴폴 씨가 묵어가던 적어도 한 주 내내 그러했습니다. 나는 날마다 후견인이 편지에 대해 말을 꺼낼지도 모른다고 기대했지만, 그는 끝내 말하지 않았습니다.\n\n그러자 나는 마음이 불안해지면서, 내가 답장을 써야 한다고 생각했습니다. 밤마다 내 방에서 몇 번이고 거듭 써보았지만, 좋은 답장처럼 시작이라도 되는 답장을 도무지 쓸 수가 없어, 밤마다 하루만 더 기다리자고 생각했습니다. 그렇게 나는 이레를 더 기다렸지만, 그는 끝내 한마디도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n마침내, 스킴폴 씨가 떠난 뒤 어느 오후, 우리 셋은 말을 타러 나가려던 참이었습니다. 나는 에이다보다 먼저 채비를 마치고 내려가다가, 응접실 창가에 서서 등을 내 쪽으로 돌린 채 밖을 내다보고 있는 후견인과 마주쳤습니다.\n\n내가 들어서자 그는 돌아서며 미소를 지으며 말했습니다, \"오, 너로구나, 작은 아가씨, 그렇지?\" 그러고는 다시 밖을 내다보았습니다.\n\n나는 이제 그에게 말을 하기로 마음을 굳혔습니다. 사실은, 일부러 그러려고 내려온 것이었습니다. \"후견인,\" 나는 조금 망설이며 떨리는 목소리로 말했습니다, \"찰리가 가지러 갔던 그 편지의 답을 언제 받고 싶으세요?\"\n\n\"준비되거든 받지, 얘야,\" 그가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"준비된 것 같은데요,\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"찰리가 가져오느냐?\" 그가 다정하게 물었습니다.\n\n\"아니요. 제가 직접 가져왔어요, 후견인,\" 나는 대답했습니다.\n\n나는 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸 안고 그에게 입을 맞추었습니다. 그러자 그가 이 사람이 황폐한 집의 안주인이냐고 물었고, 나는 그렇다고 말했습니다. 그리고 그것은 곧 아무런 달라짐도 가져오지 않았으니, 우리는 다 함께 밖으로 나갔고, 나는 사랑스러운 그 아이에게는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n어느 날 아침, 내가 열쇠 바구니를 짤랑짤랑 흔들며 정리를 끝낸 뒤, 내 어여쁜 사람과 함께 정원을 빙글빙글 돌며 거닐고 있을 때, 우연히 집 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸다가 길고 가느다란 그림자 하나가 안으로 들어가는 것을 보았는데, 영락없이 볼스 씨 같았다. 에이다는 바로 그날 아침에야, 리처드가 대법원 소송에 그토록 열성을 쏟느라 그 열정을 다 소진하기를 바란다는 희망을 내게 털어놓았던 터라, 나는 사랑하는 그 애의 기운을 꺾지 않으려고 볼스 씨의 그림자에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n이윽고 찰리가 덤불 사이를 가볍게 헤치며 오솔길을 종종걸음으로 달려왔는데, 내 하녀라기보다 플로라 여신을 모시는 시녀들 가운데 하나처럼 발그레하고 어여뻤다. 그러면서 \"아, 아가씨, 괜찮으시면, 잔다이스 씨께 가서 말씀 좀 나눠보시겠어요!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n찰리에게는 별난 버릇이 하나 있었으니, 전갈을 맡기만 하면 아무리 멀리 떨어져 있어도 대상이 눈에 띄는 순간부터 곧장 그것을 전하기 시작한다는 것이었다. 그래서 나는 찰리가 늘 쓰는 말투로 잔다이스 씨께 \"가서 말씀 좀 나눠보시라\"고 청하는 모습을 그 소리가 들리기 한참 전부터 보았다. 그리고 막상 소리가 들렸을 때는, 그 애가 같은 말을 어찌나 여러 번 되뇌었던지 숨이 차 있었다.\n\n나는 에이다에게 얼른 다녀오겠다고 말하고, 안으로 들어가면서 찰리에게 잔다이스 씨와 함께 있는 신사가 있지 않으냐고 물었다. 그러자 찰리는—부끄럽지만 고백하건대, 그 애의 문법은 내 가르치는 능력에 도무지 빛을 보태준 적이 없었다—\"네, 아가씨. 리처드 씨랑 같이 시골에 내려왔던 그분이요\"라고 대답했다.\n\n내 후견인과 볼스 씨보다 더 완벽한 대조는 아마 있을 수 없으리라. 두 사람은 탁자를 사이에 두고 서로를 마주 보고 있었는데, 한 사람은 그토록 활짝 트였고 다른 한 사람은 그토록 꽉 닫혔으며, 한 사람은 그토록 넓고 곧았고 다른 한 사람은 그토록 좁고 구부정했으며, 한 사람은 할 말을 그토록 풍부하게 울리는 목소리로 쏟아내는데 다른 한 사람은 그토록 냉혈하고 헐떡이는 물고기 같은 태도로 그것을 안에 가두고 있어서, 나는 이토록 어울리지 않는 두 사람을 본 적이 없다고 생각했다.\n\n\"너도 볼스 씨를 알지, 얘야.\" 후견인이 말했다. 솔직히 말해, 그다지 정중한 어조는 아니었다.\n\n볼스 씨는 평소처럼 장갑을 끼고 단추를 단단히 채운 채 일어섰다가, 마차에서 리처드 곁에 앉던 그 모습 그대로 다시 자리에 앉았다. 바라볼 리처드가 없으니, 그는 그저 정면을 똑바로 응시했다.\n\n\"볼스 씨가,\" 후견인은 그 검은 형체를 흉조의 새라도 보듯 바라보며 말했다, \"우리의 가엾기 짝이 없는 릭에 대해 흉한 소식을 가지고 왔구나.\" '가엾기 짝이 없는'이라는 말에 또렷이 힘을 주었는데, 마치 그 말이 볼스 씨와 릭의 관계를 묘사하는 표현이라도 되는 듯했다.\n\n나는 두 사람 사이에 앉았다. 볼스 씨는 검은 장갑을 낀 손으로 누런 얼굴에 돋은 붉은 여드름 하나를 몰래 만지작거리는 것 말고는 미동도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"릭과 자네가 다행히 좋은 친구 사이이니, 나는 네 생각을 알고 싶구나, 얘야.\" 후견인이 말했다. \"그러니 좀—좀 터놓고 말씀해 주시겠소, 볼스 씨?\"\n\n볼스 씨는 그것만 빼고 다른 모든 짓을 하면서 입을 열었다. \"저는 카스톤 씨의 법률 고문으로서, 서머슨 양, 카스톤 씨의 형편이 지금 이 순간 곤란한 상태에 놓여 있다는 것을 알 만한 근거가 있다고 말씀드리던 참입니다. 액수가 크다기보다는, 카스톤 씨가 짊어진 부채의 특수하고 절박한 성격과, 그것을 청산하거나 감당할 수단이 마땅찮은 데서 비롯된 곤란이지요. 저는 카스톤 씨를 위해 자잘한 일들을 여럿 막아드렸습니다만, 막아내는 데도 한계가 있는 법이고, 이제 그 한계에 다다랐습니다. 이 불쾌한 일들을 메우려고 제 주머니에서 얼마간 융통해 드리기도 했습니다만, 저도 응당 변제를 바라볼 수밖에 없으니, 제가 무슨 자본가 행세를 하는 것도 아니고, 톤턴 골짜기에서 부양해야 할 아버지가 있는 데다 집에는 어엿한 독립을 마련해 주려 애쓰는 사랑하는 세 딸까지 있는 처지라서요. 제가 우려하는 바는, 카스톤 씨의 형편이 이러하니, 결국 임관 매각 허가를 받는 것으로 끝나지나 않을까 하는 점인데, 어쨌거나 이 일은 그분의 가까운 분들께 알려드리는 게 바람직하다고 보았습니다.\"\n\n말하는 내내 나를 바라보던 볼스 씨는, 여기서 깨뜨렸다고 하기도 어려울 만큼—그의 어조가 워낙 답답하게 막혀 있었으므로—그 침묵 속으로 다시 잠겨 들더니, 또다시 정면을 응시했다.\n\n\"가엾은 저 친구가 지금 가진 그 밑천마저 없어진다고 상상해 보거라.\" 후견인이 내게 말했다. \"하지만 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠느냐? 너도 그 애를 알잖느냐, 에스더. 그 애는 이제 나한테서 어떤 도움도 결코 받지 않으려 할 게다. 도움을 내밀거나 그런 기미만 비쳐도, 다른 무엇이 그 애를 막다른 데로 몰지 않더라도, 바로 그 도움이 그 애를 극단으로 몰아붙일 게야.\"\n\n이에 볼스 씨가 다시 내게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"잔다이스 씨께서 하신 말씀은, 아가씨, 의심할 바 없이 사실이고, 바로 그것이 난관입니다. 저는 무슨 수가 있다고 보지 않습니다. 무슨 수를 써야 한다고 말하는 것도 아닙니다. 천만의 말씀이지요. 저는 그저 비밀을 지킨다는 약속 아래 이곳에 내려와 이 일을 말씀드리는 것뿐이니, 모든 일이 떳떳하게 처리되도록 하고, 나중에 가서 모든 일이 떳떳하게 처리되지 않았다는 소리가 나오지 않도록 하기 위함입니다. 제 바람은 모든 일이 떳떳하게 처리되는 것입니다. 저는 좋은 이름을 남기고 떠나기를 바랍니다. 만약 제가 카스톤 씨와의 일에서 오로지 제 이익만 따졌다면 여기 와 있지도 않았을 겁니다. 아가씨도 잘 아시다시피, 그분의 반대는 그만큼 넘어설 수 없을 만큼 완강할 테니까요. 이것은 업무상 방문이 아닙니다. 누구에게도 청구할 수 없는 일입니다. 저는 사회의 한 구성원이자 한 아버지로서—그리고 한 아들로서 외에는 이 일에 아무런 이해관계가 없습니다.\" 볼스 씨는 하마터면 그 대목을 잊을 뻔했다.\n\n볼스 씨가 리처드의 처지를 안다는 책임—그것이 어떤 것이든—을 나눠 지고자 한다고 넌지시 비친 것은, 우리가 보기에 사실보다 더도 덜도 아닌 말 같았다. 나로서는, 그때 리처드가 주둔하고 있던 딜로 내려가 그를 만나, 최악의 사태를 막을 수 있을지 시도해 보겠다고 제안하는 수밖에 없었다. 이 점에 대해서는 볼스 씨와 상의하지 않고, 후견인을 한쪽으로 데려가 그 뜻을 전했는데, 그동안 볼스 씨는 깡마른 모습으로 벽난로 쪽으로 성큼성큼 걸어가 그 장례식 같은 장갑을 데우고 있었다.\n\n여정의 고단함이 곧바로 후견인 쪽의 반대 이유가 되었지만, 그 밖에 다른 이유는 없는 듯 보였고, 나 또한 가게 되어 더없이 기뻤으므로, 나는 그의 허락을 받아냈다. 그러고 나니 이제 볼스 씨만 처리하면 되었다.\n\n\"그럼, 선생.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"서머슨 양이 카스톤 군과 이야기를 나눌 것이고, 선생으로서는 그의 처지가 아직 만회될 수 있기를 바라는 수밖에 없겠소. 먼 길 오셨으니 점심을 내오라 하겠소, 선생.\"\n\n\"감사합니다, 잔다이스 씨.\" 볼스 씨가 긴 검은 소맷자락을 내밀어 종 울리는 것을 막으며 말했다. \"아무것도 됐습니다. 감사합니다만, 한 입도 됐습니다. 제 소화 기능이 많이 상해서, 평소에도 변변치 못한 식객이거든요. 하루 중 이 시각에 단단한 음식을 먹었다가는 무슨 탈이 날지 모릅니다. 모든 일을 떳떳하게 처리했으니, 선생, 이제 허락하신다면 물러가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"부디 선생이 물러가고, 우리 모두가 물러갈 수 있다면 좋겠소, 볼스 씨.\" 후견인이 씁쓸하게 받았다. \"선생도 잘 아는 그 소송에서 말이오.\"\n\n볼스 씨는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 검은 물이 어찌나 짙게 들었던지 벽난로 앞에서 김이 모락모락 피어오르며 몹시 불쾌한 냄새를 풍겼는데, 목을 까딱하듯 고개를 한쪽으로 짧게 숙였다가 천천히 가로저었다.\n\n\"존경받는 법조인으로 비치는 것을 목표로 삼는 저희 같은 사람들로서는, 선생, 그저 어깨를 바퀴에 들이대고 밀 수밖에 없습니다. 저희는 그렇게 합니다, 선생. 적어도 저 자신은 그렇게 하고 있고, 제 동업자들 하나하나 모두를 좋게 생각하고 싶습니다. 카스톤 씨와 이야기를 나누실 때 저를 언급하지 않으셔야 한다는 점, 아가씨도 유념하고 계시겠지요?\"\n\n나는 그러지 않도록 조심하겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 아가씨. 안녕히 계십시오. 잔다이스 씨, 안녕히 계십시오, 선생.\" 볼스 씨는 안에 손이 들어 있는지조차 의심스러운 그 죽은 장갑을 내 손가락에, 이어서 후견인의 손가락에 갖다 댔고, 그러고는 자신의 길고 가느다란 그림자를 거두어 떠나갔다. 나는 그 그림자가 역마차 바깥쪽에 앉아 우리와 런던 사이에 펼쳐진 햇살 가득한 풍경을 가로질러 가며, 미끄러지는 자리마다 땅속의 씨앗을 얼어붙게 하는 모습을 떠올렸다.\n\n물론 에이다에게 내가 어디로, 무엇 때문에 가는지를 말해야 했고, 물론 그 애는 애를 태우며 괴로워했다. 그러나 그 애는 리처드에게 너무도 진실하여 가엾어하는 말과 두둔하는 말밖에 하지 않았고, 더더욱 사랑이 넘치는 마음으로—나의 헌신적이고 사랑스러운 그 애는!—그에게 긴 편지를 한 통 써서 내게 맡겼다.\n\n찰리가 내 길동무가 되기로 했는데, 사실 길동무 따위는 필요 없었고 기꺼이 집에 두고 갔을 것이다. 우리는 그날 오후 다 함께 런던으로 가, 우편 마차에 두 자리가 비어 있는 것을 발견하고 그것을 잡아두었다. 평소 잠자리에 들 시각에, 찰리와 나는 켄트로 가는 우편물과 함께 바닷가를 향해 덜컹덜컹 굴러가고 있었다.\n\n그 마차 시절에는 밤을 새우는 여정이었지만, 우편 마차를 우리끼리 차지한 데다 밤이 그리 지루하게 느껴지지도 않았다. 그런 처지에 놓인 대부분의 사람이 그러하리라 짐작되는 식으로 밤은 내게 흘러갔다. 어떤 때는 내 여정이 희망차 보였고, 또 어떤 때는 가망 없어 보였다. 어느 순간에는 내가 무언가 도움이 되리라 싶다가도, 다음 순간에는 어떻게 그런 생각을 다 했을까 의아해졌다. 어느 때는 내가 이렇게 온 것이 세상에서 가장 사리에 맞는 일 같다가도, 다음 순간에는 가장 터무니없는 일 같았다. 리처드를 어떤 상태로 보게 될지, 내가 그에게 무슨 말을 할지, 그가 내게 무슨 말을 할지가 이 두 갈래 감정과 더불어 번갈아 내 마음을 사로잡았고, 바퀴는 밤새도록 단 하나의 곡조를(후견인이 보낸 편지의 내용이 그 곡조에 얹혔다) 몇 번이고 되풀이해 연주하는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 우리는 딜의 좁은 거리로 들어섰는데, 차고 안개 낀 아침이라 거리는 무척이나 음산했다. 길고 평평한 해변에는 나무와 벽돌로 지은 작고 들쭉날쭉한 집들과, 어수선하게 널린 캡스턴이며 큰 배들, 헛간들, 도르래와 활차를 매단 헐벗은 기둥들, 그리고 풀과 잡초가 무성하게 뒤덮인 자갈투성이 황무지가 펼쳐져 있었는데, 내가 본 어느 곳 못지않게 음울한 모습이었다. 바다는 짙고 흰 안개 아래서 일렁이고 있었고, 그 밖에 움직이는 것이라곤 일찌감치 일을 나온 밧줄 짜는 일꾼 몇뿐이었는데, 그들은 실타래를 몸에 칭칭 감고 있어, 지금의 자기 처지에 진력이 나서 스스로를 꼬아 밧줄로 만들고 있는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n그러나 우리가 어느 훌륭한 호텔의 따뜻한 방에 들어가, 말끔히 씻고 옷을 갈아입은 뒤 편안히 앉아 이른 아침 식사를(잠자리에 들기엔 이미 너무 늦은 시각이었으므로) 마주하자, 딜은 한결 명랑해 보이기 시작했다. 우리 자그마한 방은 배의 선실 같았는데, 찰리는 그것을 무척이나 좋아했다. 이윽고 안개가 막처럼 걷히기 시작하더니, 근처에 있는 줄도 몰랐던 수많은 배가 모습을 드러냈다. 그때 다운스에 정박해 있는 배가 몇 척이라고 급사가 일러주었는지는 기억나지 않는다. 그 배들 가운데 일부는 어마어마하게 컸는데—한 척은 마침 본국으로 돌아온 큼직한 동인도 무역선이었다—구름 사이로 햇살이 비쳐 들어 어두운 바다에 은빛 웅덩이를 만들 때, 해변에서 배로, 또 배에서 해변으로 노 저어 오가는 보트들의 부산함 속에서, 그리고 배 자체와 그 주위 모든 것의 활기차고 분주한 움직임 속에서, 그 배들이 환히 빛났다가 그늘졌다가 시시각각 달라지는 모습은 더없이 아름다웠다.\n\n그 큼직한 동인도 무역선이 우리의 가장 큰 구경거리였던 것은 그 배가 간밤에 다운스로 들어왔기 때문이다. 배는 보트들에 둘러싸여 있었고, 우리는 배에 탄 사람들이 뭍에 오르게 되어 얼마나 기쁠까 이야기했다. 찰리는 또 그 항해에 대해, 그리고 인도의 더위와 뱀과 호랑이에 대해 궁금해했는데, 그 애는 그런 지식이라면 문법보다 훨씬 빨리 주워 담았으므로, 나는 그런 것들에 대해 아는 바를 일러주었다. 또한 그런 항해를 하다 보면 사람들이 때로 난파를 당해 바위에 떠밀려 올라가기도 하는데, 그럴 때 한 사람의 담대함과 인정으로 목숨을 건지기도 한다는 이야기도 들려주었다. 찰리가 어떻게 그럴 수 있느냐고 묻기에, 나는 우리가 집에서 그런 사례를 알게 된 일이 있다고 일러주었다.\n\n리처드에게 내가 와 있다는 쪽지를 보낼까 생각해 보았지만, 미리 알리지 않고 그냥 찾아가는 편이 훨씬 나을 듯했다. 그가 병영에서 지내고 있었으므로 이것이 가능한 일인지 다소 미심쩍었으나, 우리는 사정을 살피러 밖으로 나갔다. 병영 마당 문 안을 들여다보니, 아침 그 시각에는 모든 것이 무척 고요했고, 나는 위병소 계단에 서 있는 병장에게 그가 어디 사느냐고 물었다. 병장은 길을 안내하도록 한 사람을 앞세워 보냈는데, 그는 휑한 계단을 올라 어느 문을 손가락 마디로 두드리고는 우리를 두고 가버렸다.\n\n\"들어와요!\" 리처드가 안에서 외쳤다. 그래서 나는 찰리를 좁은 통로에 남겨두고, 반쯤 열린 문 쪽으로 다가가 말했다. \"들어가도 될까, 리처드? 더든 아줌마일 뿐이야.\"\n\n그는 탁자에서 무언가를 쓰고 있었는데, 옷가지며 양철 상자, 책, 장화, 솔, 여행 가방이 바닥 여기저기에 어수선하게 널려 있었다. 그는 옷을 절반밖에 걸치지 않은 채였고—군복이 아니라 평상복 차림이라는 것을 나는 알아챘다—머리도 빗질하지 않아, 제 방만큼이나 헝클어진 모습이었다. 이 모든 것은 그가 나를 진심으로 반기고 내가 그 곁에 앉은 뒤에야 눈에 들어왔는데, 그가 내 목소리를 듣더니 화들짝 놀라 순식간에 나를 품에 끌어안았기 때문이다. 사랑하는 리처드! 그는 내게 늘 한결같았다. 그—아, 가엾고 가엾은 친구!—그 끝까지, 그는 옛날의 그 명랑하고 소년 같은 태도 없이 나를 맞이한 적이 한 번도 없었다.\n\n\"세상에, 나의 어여쁜 아가씨,\" 그가 말했다. \"어떻게 여기까지 온 거야? 누가 너를 보게 되리라 생각이나 했겠어! 무슨 일 있는 건 아니지? 에이다는 잘 있고?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 있어. 그 어느 때보다 어여쁘단다, 리처드!\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"내 가엾은 사촌! 마침 너한테 편지를 쓰고 있었어, 에스더.\"\n\n잘생긴 젊음이 한창인 가운데서도, 의자에 등을 기댄 채 빽빽이 글이 적힌 종이를 손에 움켜쥐고 있는 그가 어찌나 지치고 초췌해 보이던지!\n\n\"그렇게 애써 그걸 다 써놓고선, 결국 나는 못 읽게 하는 거야?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 얘,\" 그가 가망 없다는 몸짓을 하며 받았다. \"이 방 전체를 보면 그게 읽혀. 여기선 다 끝났어.\"\n\n나는 부디 낙담하지 말라고 부드럽게 간청했다. 우연히 그가 곤경에 처했다는 소식을 들어, 무엇이 최선일지 함께 의논하러 왔다고 말했다.\n\n\"너답구나, 에스더, 하지만 소용없는 일이고, 그러니 너답지 않아!\" 그가 우울한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"오늘 휴가를 받아 나와 있어—한 시간 더 있으면 떠났어야 했는데—임관을 매각하는 일을 매끄럽게 마무리하기 위해서지. 뭐! 지난 일은 지난 일로 묻어두자. 그래서 이 직업도 나머지 것들과 똑같은 신세가 됐어. 이제 성직에만 몸담아 봤더라면 온갖 직업을 한 바퀴 다 돌아본 셈이 됐을 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"리처드,\" 내가 다그쳤다. \"그렇게 가망 없는 건 아니잖아?\"\n\n\"에스더,\" 그가 받았다. \"정말 그래. 나는 망신살이 뻗치기 직전이라, (교리문답 식으로 말하자면) 내 위에 권한을 가진 자들이 나를 데리고 있느니 차라리 없는 편을 훨씬 바랄 지경이야. 그리고 그들이 옳아. 빚이며 빚쟁이며 그런 온갖 걸림돌은 차치하고라도, 나는 이 일조차 감당할 위인이 못 돼. 나한테는 오직 한 가지 말고는, 마음도, 정신도, 가슴도, 영혼도 없어. 그러니, 만약 이 거품이 지금 터지지 않았더라면,\" 그가 자기가 쓴 편지를 갈가리 찢어 침울하게 한 조각씩 흩뿌리며 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 외국으로 나갈 수 있었겠어? 외국 파병 명령을 받았어야 했겠지만, 어떻게 나갈 수 있었겠느냐고? 그 일에 대한 내 경험으로 미루어, 내가 등 뒤에 바짝 붙어 지키고 있지 않는 한, 어떻게 볼스조차 믿을 수 있었겠어!\"\n\n내 표정을 보고 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 그가 알아챘던 모양인지, 그는 자기 팔 위에 얹어둔 내 손을 잡아 내 입술에 갖다 대어 내가 말을 잇지 못하게 했다.\n\n\"안 돼, 더든 아줌마! 두 가지 화제는 금지야—금지할 수밖에 없어. 첫째는 존 잔다이스. 둘째는, 네가 아는 그것. 이걸 광기라 불러도 좋아. 나는 이제 어쩔 수가 없고, 제정신일 수도 없다고 말할 테니까. 하지만 그런 게 아니야. 이건 내가 좇아야만 하는 단 하나의 목표야. 내가 다른 어떤 것 때문에라도 내 길에서 벗어나도록 설득당했다는 게 한스러울 뿐이야. 그동안 거기에 쏟아부은 그 모든 시간과 노심초사와 노고를 생각하면, 지금 그것을 포기하는 게 현명한 짓이겠지! 아, 그래, 참된 지혜겠지. 어떤 사람들한테는 더없이 흡족한 일이기도 할 테고. 하지만 나는 결코 그러지 않을 거야.\"\n\n그는 어떤 기분에 빠져 있었는데, 나는 그것을 반대해서 (무엇으로든 그것을 더 키울 수 있다면) 그의 결심을 더 굳히지 않는 편이 상책이라고 생각했다. 나는 에이다의 편지를 꺼내 그의 손에 쥐여주었다.\n\n\"지금 읽으라는 거야?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n내가 그렇다고 하자, 그는 그것을 탁자 위에 놓고 한 손으로 머리를 받친 채 읽기 시작했다. 얼마 읽지 않아 그는 두 손으로 머리를 받쳤다—내게서 얼굴을 가리려는 것이었다. 잠시 후 그는 빛이 나쁘다는 듯 일어나 창가로 갔다. 그는 내게 등을 돌린 채 거기서 편지를 다 읽었고, 다 읽고 나서 그것을 접은 뒤에도 편지를 손에 쥐고 몇 분 동안 그 자리에 서 있었다. 그가 다시 의자로 돌아왔을 때, 나는 그의 눈에 어린 눈물을 보았다.\n\n\"물론, 에스더, 너도 그 애가 여기서 뭐라고 하는지 알겠지?\" 그는 한결 부드러워진 목소리로 말하며, 그렇게 물으면서 편지에 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"응, 리처드.\"\n\n\"그 애가 내게 주겠다는 거야,\" 그가 발로 바닥을 톡톡 구르며 말을 이었다. \"머잖아 확실히 받게 될 그 자그마한 유산을—내가 탕진해 버린 만큼이나 적고도 많은 그것을—제발 받아서, 그걸로 처지를 바로잡고, 계속 복무하라고 빌고 또 비는 거야.\"\n\n\"네 잘됨이 그 애 마음에서 가장 간절한 소원이라는 걸 나도 알아.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고, 아, 사랑하는 리처드, 에이다는 고결한 마음을 지녔어.\"\n\n\"나도 그런 줄 알아. 나는—나는 차라리 죽어버렸으면 좋겠어!\"\n\n그는 다시 창가로 가, 창턱에 팔을 가로 얹고 그 팔에 머리를 떨구었다. 그런 그의 모습을 보니 마음이 몹시 아팠지만, 그가 좀 더 누그러질지도 모른다는 희망에 나는 잠자코 있었다. 내 경험은 무척 일천했던 터라, 그가 이런 감정을 떨치고 일어서서 새삼 억울하다는 감정에 휩싸일 줄은 전혀 예상치 못했다.\n\n\"그리고 이게 바로, 우리 사이에서 달리 입에 올릴 것도 없는 바로 그 존 잔다이스가 끼어들어 나한테서 떼어놓으려 한 그 마음이야.\" 그가 분개하여 말했다. \"그리고 그 사랑스러운 아이가 나한테 이 너그러운 제안을 하는 게, 바로 그 존 잔다이스의 지붕 아래서, 그리고 분명코 그 존 잔다이스의 인자한 동의와 묵인 아래서, 나를 새로운 방식으로 돈으로 떼어내려는 수작이라고.\"\n\n\"리처드!\" 나는 황급히 일어서며 소리쳤다. \"그런 부끄러운 말을 하는 걸 듣고 있지 않겠어!\" 나는 평생 처음으로 그에게 정말로 화가 났지만, 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그가 미안해하는 듯한 표정으로 지친 젊은 얼굴을 들어 나를 바라보는 것을 보자, 나는 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다. \"부디, 사랑하는 리처드, 나한테 그런 어조로 말하지 마. 좀 생각해 봐!\"\n\n그는 자신을 몹시 책망하며, 더없이 너그러운 태도로 자기가 크게 잘못했고 천번 만번 용서를 빈다고 내게 말했다. 그 말에 나는 웃었지만, 한편으로는 조금 떨기도 했는데, 그토록 불같이 화를 낸 뒤라 적잖이 마음이 흔들렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"이 제안을 받아들이는 건, 사랑하는 에스더,\" 그가 내 곁에 앉아 우리의 대화를 다시 이어가며 말했다. \"—다시 한번, 제발, 제발 나를 용서해 줘. 나는 마음 깊이 슬프단다—내 더없이 소중한 사촌의 제안을 받아들이는 건, 말할 것도 없이 불가능해. 게다가, 여기선 다 끝났다는 걸 네게 납득시켜 줄 편지며 서류도 보여줄 수 있어. 군복하고는 이제 끝이야, 정말로. 하지만 이 고난과 혼란의 와중에도, 내 이익을 밀어붙이는 게 곧 에이다의 이익을 밀어붙이는 거라는 걸 아니까 그나마 위안이 돼. 볼스가 어깨를 바퀴에 들이대고 밀고 있으니, 나를 위해서나 그 애를 위해서나 똑같이 그것을 힘껏 밀어붙일 수밖에 없겠지, 천만다행으로!\"\n\n희망에 부푼 그의 기대가 마음속에서 솟아올라 그의 이목구비를 환하게 밝혔지만, 그 때문에 그의 얼굴은 내게 이전보다 더 슬프게 보였다.\n\n\"아니, 아니!\" 리처드가 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"에이다의 자그마한 재산이 한 푼도 남김없이 내 것이 된대도, 그중 단 한 푼도 내가 감당할 수도 없고, 아무런 흥미도 못 느끼고, 신물이 난 이 일에 나를 붙들어두는 데 쓰여서는 안 돼. 그건 더 나은 보답을 약속하는 일에 바쳐야 하고, 그 애가 더 큰 몫을 건 곳에 쓰여야 해. 나 때문에 마음 졸이지 마! 이제 나한테는 마음에 둘 일이 단 하나뿐일 테고, 볼스와 내가 그 일을 해낼 거야. 나도 빈털터리는 아닐 거야. 임관에서 벗어나면, 지금 와선 채권 말고는 아무 소리도 들으려 하지 않는 자잘한 고리대금업자들과 합의를 볼 수 있을 거야—볼스가 그렇게 말했어. 어차피 내 쪽에 잔액이 남을 테지만, 그렇게 하면 그 잔액이 더 불어날 거야. 자, 자! 너는 나한테서 에이다한테 보내는 편지를 가져가 줘, 에스더. 그리고 너희 둘 다 나에 대해 좀 더 희망을 품어주고, 내가 아직 완전히 내버려진 신세는 아니라고 믿어줘야 해, 얘야.\"\n\n내가 리처드에게 한 말을 여기 옮기지는 않겠다. 그것이 지루한 소리였다는 걸 알고, 그것이 조금이라도 현명했으리라고는 아무도 한순간도 생각해서는 안 된다. 그것은 그저 내 가슴에서 우러난 말이었다. 그는 그것을 참을성 있게, 그리고 마음을 담아 들어주었지만, 그가 따로 봉인해 둔 그 두 가지 화제에 대해서는 지금으로선 어떤 말을 해도 가망이 없다는 것을 나는 알았다. 또한 나는, 바로 이 면담에서 직접 겪어보고서, 그를 그대로 내버려두는 것보다 설득하려 드는 게 오히려 더 해롭다던 후견인의 말이 무슨 뜻인지를 절감했다.\n\n그리하여 나는 결국, 그가 말한 대로 거기선 정말로 모든 게 끝났으며 그것이 그의 단순한 짐작이 아니라는 점을 내게 납득시켜 주겠느냐고 리처드에게 물을 수밖에 없었다. 그는 망설임 없이, 자신의 퇴역이 마무리되었음을 아주 분명하게 보여주는 서신들을 내게 내보였다. 나는 그가 들려준 이야기로 미루어, 볼스 씨가 이 서류들의 사본을 가지고 있으며 처음부터 끝까지 그와 상의해 왔다는 것을 알았다. 이 점을 확인한 것과, 에이다의 편지를 전한 심부름꾼 노릇을 한 것과, (앞으로 그러기로 했듯이) 리처드와 함께 런던으로 돌아가는 길동무가 되어주는 것 말고는, 내가 내려와서 한 좋은 일은 아무것도 없었다. 못내 아쉬운 마음으로 이 사실을 스스로 인정하며, 나는 호텔로 돌아가 그가 그리로 합류할 때까지 기다리겠다고 말했고, 그러자 그는 어깨에 망토를 두르고 문까지 나를 배웅해 주었으며, 찰리와 나는 해변을 따라 돌아갔다.\n\n한곳에 사람들이 잔뜩 모여, 보트에서 막 내리는 몇몇 해군 장교를 에워싸고는 유난한 관심으로 그들 주위로 몰려들고 있었다. 나는 찰리에게 이것이 그 큰 동인도 무역선의 보트일 거라고 말하고, 우리는 멈춰 서서 구경했다.\n\n신사들은 물가에서 천천히 올라오며, 서로에게, 또 주위 사람들에게 유쾌하게 말을 건네고, 다시 영국에 돌아와 기쁜 듯 사방을 둘러보고 있었다. \"찰리, 찰리,\" 내가 말했다. \"어서 가자!\" 그러고는 어찌나 잽싸게 서둘렀던지 내 어린 하녀가 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n내가 왜 그토록 서둘렀는지 곰곰이 생각해 보기 시작한 것은, 우리가 선실 같은 방에 틀어박혀 내가 숨을 고를 겨를을 얻고 나서였다. 그 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴들 가운데서 나는 앨런 우드코트 씨를 알아보았고, 그가 나를 알아볼까 봐 두려웠던 것이다. 나는 그가 변해버린 내 얼굴을 보는 게 싫었다. 나는 불시에 허를 찔렸고, 내 용기가 완전히 꺾여버렸던 것이다.\n\n그러나 나는 이래서는 안 된다는 것을 알았기에, 이제 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다. \"얘야, 지금이 예전 어느 때보다 너한테 더 나쁠 까닭은 없어—전혀 없고, 있을 수도 없어. 네가 지난달의 너였던 그대로 오늘의 너인 거야. 더 나빠진 것도 없고, 더 나아진 것도 없어. 이건 네 결심이 아니잖니. 결심을 불러내, 에스더, 불러내라고!\" 나는—달리느라—몹시 떨고 있었고, 처음에는 도무지 마음을 가라앉힐 수가 없었지만, 차츰 나아졌고, 그렇게 되었다는 걸 알게 되어 무척 기뻤다.\n\n일행이 호텔로 왔다. 나는 그들이 계단에서 이야기하는 소리를 들었다. 그들의 목소리를 다시 알아들었으므로 그 신사들이 틀림없다고 확신했는데—내 말은, 우드코트 씨의 목소리를 알아들었다는 뜻이다. 정체를 밝히지 않고 그냥 떠나버린다면 여전히 내게는 큰 안도가 되었겠지만, 나는 그러지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었다. \"안 돼, 얘야, 안 돼. 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!\"\n\n나는 보닛 끈을 풀고 베일을 반쯤 올렸는데—반쯤 내렸다는 뜻인 것 같지만, 그건 아무래도 좋다—내 명함 한 장에 마침 리처드 카스톤 씨와 함께 이곳에 와 있다고 적어, 그것을 우드코트 씨에게 들여보냈다. 그는 곧바로 왔다. 나는 우연히도 그가 영국으로 돌아온 것을 가장 먼저 맞이하는 사람들 축에 끼게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다. 그리고 나는 그가 나를 무척 가엾이 여기고 있다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"우리 곁을 떠나신 뒤로 난파와 위험을 겪으셨군요, 우드코트 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 그토록 쓸모 있고 용감하게 처신하실 수 있게 해준 그 일을 두고 불운이라 부르기는 어렵겠지요. 저희는 그 소식을 더없이 진심 어린 관심으로 읽었답니다. 제가 그 일을 처음 알게 된 건, 제가 그 모진 병에서 회복하던 무렵, 당신의 옛 환자였던 가엾은 플라이트 양을 통해서였어요.\"\n\n\"아! 자그마한 플라이트 양!\" 그가 말했다. \"그분은 아직도 그대로 살고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"꼭 그대로예요.\"\n\n이제 나는 스스로에게 무척이나 편안해져서, 베일 따위는 신경 쓰이지 않았고 그것을 옆으로 젖혀둘 수도 있었다.\n\n\"그분이 당신께 품은 고마움은 참 보기 좋더군요, 우드코트 씨. 그분은 더없이 정 많은 분이세요. 제가 그렇게 말할 만한 까닭이 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그분이—그분이 그렇게 정이 많던가요?\" 그가 받았다. \"저는—저는 그것 참 기쁩니다.\" 그는 나를 너무도 가엾이 여겨 말을 거의 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 말씀드린 그 무렵에 그분이 보여준 동정과 기쁨에 저는 깊이 감동했답니다.\"\n\n\"많이 편찮으셨다는 소식을 듣고 마음이 아팠습니다.\"\n\n\"많이 아팠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이제 완전히 회복하셨고요?\"\n\n\"건강도 명랑함도 완전히 되찾았어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"제 후견인이 얼마나 좋은 분인지, 또 저희가 얼마나 행복하게 사는지 아시잖아요. 저는 감사할 일은 다 갖췄고, 세상에 더 바랄 게 아무것도 없답니다.\"\n\n나는 마치 그가 나를, 내가 나 자신을 가엾이 여겨본 그 어느 때보다 더 가엾이 여기는 것처럼 느껴졌다. 그를 안심시켜야 하는 처지에 놓인 사람이 바로 나라는 것을 깨닫자, 그것이 내게 새로운 꿋꿋함과 새로운 평온함을 불어넣었다. 나는 그에게 나갈 때와 돌아올 때의 항해 이야기며, 앞으로의 계획이며, 인도로 다시 돌아갈 가능성에 대해 물었다. 그는 그것이 무척 불확실하다고 했다. 그는 그곳에서도 이곳에서보다 더 운이 따르지는 않았던 것이다. 그는 가난한 선의(船醫)로 나갔다가 조금도 나을 것 없는 처지로 돌아온 것이었다. 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안, 그리고 그가 나를 보고 받은 충격을 내가 (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 누그러뜨렸다고 기쁘게 믿고 있을 때, 리처드가 들어왔다. 그는 아래층에서 누가 나와 함께 있는지 들은 터였고, 두 사람은 진심 어린 반가움으로 만났다.\n\n처음의 인사가 끝나고 리처드의 진로에 관한 이야기가 오갈 때, 나는 우드코트 씨가 그의 일이 잘 풀리지 않고 있음을 알아챘다는 것을 보았다. 그는 마치 리처드의 얼굴에 자신을 아프게 하는 무언가가 있다는 듯 자주 그 얼굴을 흘끔거렸고, 한 번 이상 내 쪽을 바라보았는데, 마치 진실이 무엇인지를 내가 알고 있는지 가늠하려는 듯했다. 그러나 리처드는 희망에 부푼 상태들 가운데 하나에 빠져 기분이 좋았고, 언제나 좋아했던 우드코트 씨를 다시 만나 진심으로 기뻐했다.\n\n리처드는 우리 모두 함께 런던으로 가자고 제안했지만, 우드코트 씨는 자기 배 곁에 좀 더 머물러야 했으므로 우리와 동행할 수 없었다. 그래도 그는 이른 시각에 우리와 함께 식사를 했고, 옛날의 그 모습과 한결 더 비슷해져서, 내가 그의 회한을 누그러뜨릴 수 있었다고 생각하니 나는 더더욱 마음이 놓였다. 그러나 그의 마음은 리처드에 대한 걱정에서 놓여나지 못했다. 마차가 거의 채비를 마치고 리처드가 자기 짐을 살피러 아래층으로 달려 내려갔을 때, 그가 내게 리처드에 관해 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n내게 그의 사연을 통째로 펼쳐 보일 권리가 있는지는 확신할 수 없었지만, 나는 그가 잔다이스 씨와 사이가 틀어진 일과, 그 운수 사나운 대법원 소송에 얽혀든 일에 대해 몇 마디로 언급했다. 우드코트 씨는 관심을 가지고 들으며 안타까움을 드러냈다.\n\n\"당신이 그를 꽤 유심히 살피시는 걸 봤어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그가 그렇게나 변했다고 보세요?\"\n\n\"변했습니다.\" 그가 고개를 가로저으며 받았다.\n\n나는 난생처음으로 얼굴로 피가 확 솟구치는 것을 느꼈지만, 그것은 그저 한순간의 동요였다. 나는 고개를 옆으로 돌렸고, 그것은 사라졌다.\n\n\"그가 훨씬 젊어졌다거나 늙었다거나, 야위었다거나 살쪘다거나, 창백해졌다거나 혈색이 좋아졌다거나 하는 게 아닙니다.\" 우드코트 씨가 말했다. \"그의 얼굴에 그토록 묘한 표정이 어려 있다는 거지요. 저는 젊은 사람한테서 그렇게 두드러진 표정을 본 적이 없습니다. 그게 전적으로 불안 탓이라거나 전적으로 피로 탓이라고 말할 수는 없어요. 하지만 그 둘 다이고, 마치 다 자라지 못한 절망 같은 것이지요.\"\n\n\"그가 병든 것 같지는 않고요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n아니다. 그는 몸은 튼튼해 보였다.\n\n\"그가 마음이 편할 리 없다는 건, 우리도 너무나 잘 알고 있지요.\" 내가 말을 이었다. \"우드코트 씨, 런던으로 가시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"내일이나 모레 갑니다.\"\n\n\"리처드가 무엇보다 절실히 바라는 건 친구예요. 그는 늘 당신을 좋아했어요. 부디 그곳에 가시거든 그를 만나주세요. 부디 가능하다면 이따금 곁에 있어주는 것으로 그를 도와주세요. 그게 얼마나 큰 도움이 될지 당신은 모르실 거예요. 에이다와, 잔다이스 씨와, 그리고 저까지도—우리 모두가 당신께 얼마나 고마워할지, 당신은 짐작도 못 하실 거예요, 우드코트 씨!\"\n\n\"서머슨 양,\" 그가 처음보다 한결 더 마음이 동한 모습으로 말했다. \"하늘에 맹세코, 저는 그에게 참된 친구가 되겠습니다! 그를 제게 맡겨진 사람으로 받아들이겠고, 그것을 신성한 소임으로 삼겠습니다!\"\n\n\"하느님의 은총이 있기를!\" 내가 말했는데, 눈에는 눈물이 빠르게 차올랐다. 하지만 그것이 나 자신을 위한 게 아닐 때는 그래도 괜찮으리라 생각했다. \"에이다가 그를 사랑해요—우리 모두 그를 사랑하지만, 에이다는 우리로서는 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 그를 사랑해요. 당신이 하신 말씀을 그 애에게 전할게요. 그 애의 이름으로, 고맙습니다, 그리고 하느님의 은총이 있기를!\"\n\n우리가 이 황급한 말들을 주고받기를 마칠 무렵 리처드가 돌아와, 나를 마차로 데려가려고 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n\"우드코트,\" 그가 그 말이 어디에 쓰일지도 모른 채 말했다. \"런던에서 꼭 만나자고!\"\n\n\"만나자고?\" 상대가 받았다. \"이제 거기엔 자네 말고는 친구라 할 사람이 거의 없네. 어디로 가면 자넬 찾을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 어디든 하숙을 하나 구해야겠지.\" 리처드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"사이먼즈 인의 볼스 사무소로 연락하게.\"\n\n\"좋아! 지체 없이 그러겠네.\"\n\n두 사람은 진심으로 악수를 나누었다. 내가 마차에 자리를 잡고 리처드가 아직 거리에 서 있을 때, 우드코트 씨가 다정한 손을 리처드의 어깨에 얹고 나를 바라보았다. 나는 그 뜻을 알아채고, 고마움을 담아 손을 흔들었다.\n\n그리고 우리가 마차를 몰아 떠날 때 그가 보낸 마지막 눈길에서, 나는 그가 나를 무척 가엾이 여기고 있다는 것을 보았다. 나는 그것을 보게 되어 기뻤다. 나는 죽은 이들이 만약 이 세상의 장면들을 다시 찾아온다면 느낄 법한 그런 심정으로, 나의 옛 자신을 떠올렸다. 다정하게 기억되고, 부드럽게 가엾이 여겨지고, 아주 잊히지는 않았다는 것이 나는 기뻤다.\n\n톰 혼자 사는 곳에 어둠이 내려앉는다. 어젯밤 해가 진 뒤로 자꾸만 부풀고 또 부풀더니, 어둠은 차츰 불어나 이곳의 빈자리란 빈자리를 죄다 채워버린다. 한동안은 지하 감옥 같은 불빛 몇 점이 타올랐다. 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 생명의 등불이 메스꺼운 공기 속에서 무겁게, 무겁게 윙윙대듯이, 그리고 그 등불도 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 깜빡이듯이, 숱한 끔찍한 것들을 향해 깜빡이듯이. 그러나 그 불빛마저 지워졌다. 달은 둔하고 차가운 눈초리로 톰을 흘끔 보았으니, 화산 불길에 그을려 생명이 깃들 수 없는 그 황량한 땅에서 제 자신의 가냘픈 모방이라도 발견한 듯했다. 그러나 달은 그대로 지나쳐 사라졌다. 지옥의 마구간에서 가장 시커먼 악몽이 톰 혼자 사는 곳을 풀밭처럼 뜯어먹고, 톰은 깊이 잠들어 있다.\n\n톰을 두고 의회 안팎에서 거창한 연설이 무수히 쏟아졌고, 톰을 어떻게 바로잡을지를 둘러싼 노여운 논쟁도 무수히 벌어졌다. 톰을 큰길로 끌어내는 데 순경을 쓸지, 교구 직원을 쓸지, 종을 울려 쫓을지, 숫자의 힘으로 할지, 올바른 취향의 원칙으로 할지, 고교회로 할지, 저교회로 할지, 아니면 아예 교회 없이 할지. 톰의 마음에 깃든 그 구부러진 칼로 논쟁의 지푸라기 다발이나 쪼개게 만들지, 아니면 그 대신 돌을 깨게 시킬지. 그 먼지와 소음 한복판에서 완벽하게 분명한 것은 단 하나뿐이니, 곧 톰은 누군가의 이론에 따라서는 구제될 수도 있고 구제되어야 하고 구제될 테지만, 그 누구의 실천으로도 구제되지 못한다는 것이다. 그리고 희망찬 그동안에, 톰은 예전 그대로의 완강한 기세로 머리부터 먼저 파멸로 곤두박질친다.\n* 고교회·저교회: 영국 국교회 안의 두 흐름. 고교회는 전례와 성직 권위를 중시하고, 저교회는 복음과 개인 신앙을 중시한다.\n\n그러나 톰에게는 복수가 있다. 바람마저 그의 전령이 되어, 이 어둠의 시간에 그를 섬긴다. 톰의 썩은 피 한 방울도 어딘가에 감염과 전염을 퍼뜨리지 않는 것이 없다. 그 피는 바로 오늘 밤, 어느 노르만 가문의 고귀한 핏줄을(화학자가 분석하면 진짜 귀족의 성분을 찾아낼 그 핏줄을) 더럽힐 것이며, 공작 각하조차 그 수치스러운 결합에 아니라고 말하지 못하리라. 톰의 진창에서 떨어진 티끌 하나, 그가 사는 역병의 기체 한 세제곱인치, 그에게 붙은 외설과 타락 하나, 무지 하나, 사악함 하나, 그가 저지른 야만 하나도, 사회의 모든 계층을 거쳐 가장 도도한 자 가운데 도도한 자, 가장 높은 자 가운데 높은 자에게까지 그 응보를 미치지 않는 것이 없다. 진실로, 오염시키고 약탈하고 망쳐놓는 일로, 톰에게는 복수가 있다.\n\n톰 혼자 사는 곳이 낮에 더 흉한지 밤에 더 흉한지는 다툼의 여지가 있는 문제다. 하지만 그곳을 많이 볼수록 더 충격적일 수밖에 없고, 상상에 맡겨진 어느 부분도 실제만큼 끔찍하게 그려지기는 어렵다는 논리로 보면, 낮이 이긴다. 이제 날이 밝아오기 시작한다. 사실 국가의 영광을 위해서라면, 톰처럼 그토록 비루한 진풍경 위로 태양이 떠오르느니, 차라리 대영제국의 영토에 가끔은 해가 지는 편이 나을지도 모른다.\n* 대영제국의 영토에 가끔은 해가 지는: \"대영제국에는 해가 지지 않는다\"라는 당대의 자부심 어린 표현을 뒤집은 반어.\n\n햇볕에 그을린 갈색 얼굴의 신사 하나가, 잠을 청하지 못한 채 뒤척이는 베개 위에서 시간을 헤아리느니 차라리 바깥을 헤매기로 한 듯한 모습으로, 이 고요한 시각에 이쪽으로 거닐어 온다. 호기심에 이끌려 그는 자주 걸음을 멈추고 사방을, 그 비참한 뒷골목 위아래를 둘러본다. 그저 호기심만은 아니다. 빛나는 검은 눈동자에 측은히 여기는 관심이 어려 있으니, 이리저리 둘러보는 그는 그런 비참함을 이해하는 듯하고, 전에도 그것을 살펴본 적이 있는 듯하다.\n\n톰 혼자 사는 곳의 큰길이라 할 진창 고인 도랑 둑에는 미친 듯이 낡아빠진 집들이, 굳게 닫힌 채 말없이 늘어선 것 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는다. 그 자신 말고는 깨어 있는 생명이라곤 한 방향에서밖에 보이지 않으니, 그곳에 한 여인의 외로운 모습이 문간 계단에 앉아 있다. 그는 그쪽으로 걸어간다. 가까이 다가가서 그는, 그녀가 먼 길을 걸어와 발이 부르트고 여행에 찌든 모습임을 알아챈다. 그녀는 무언가를 기다리는 사람처럼 문간 계단에 앉아 있으니, 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 머리를 얹고 있다. 곁에는 그녀가 들고 온 천 가방인지 보따리인지가 놓여 있다. 그가 다가가는 발소리에도 그녀가 아무 기척이 없는 걸 보면, 아마도 졸고 있는 듯하다.\n\n부서진 보도가 너무 좁아서, 앨런 우드코트가 여인이 앉은 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 그녀를 지나치려고 차도로 돌아서야 한다. 그녀의 얼굴을 내려다보다가, 그의 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주치고, 그는 걸음을 멈춘다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 아닙니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"안에서 못 듣는 모양이오? 들어가고 싶은 거요?\"\n\n\"다른 집에서 사람들이 일어나기를 기다리는 거예요. 하숙집인데, 여기는 아니에요.\" 여인이 참을성 있게 대답한다. \"여기서 기다리는 건, 곧 여기에 햇볕이 들어 몸을 녹여줄 테니까요.\"\n\n\"피곤한 모양이오. 길거리에 앉아 있는 걸 보니 마음이 아프오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리. 괜찮아요.\"\n\n가난한 이들에게 말을 건넬 때 생색이나 깔보는 태도나 어린애 다루듯 하는 짓을(많은 사람이 마치 작은 철자 교본을 읽어주듯 그들에게 말하는 걸 무슨 세련된 기술쯤으로 여기는데) 피하는 그의 습관 덕분에, 그는 손쉽게 여인과 친밀해진다.\n\n\"이마 좀 봅시다.\" 그가 몸을 숙이며 말한다. \"나는 의사요. 겁내지 마시오. 결코 당신을 해치지 않을 테니.\"\n\n그는 능숙하고 익숙한 손으로 그녀를 만지면 그녀를 한결 쉽게 다독일 수 있음을 안다. 그녀는 \"아무것도 아니에요\"라며 살짝 마다하지만, 그가 다친 자리에 손가락을 대자마자 그녀는 이마를 빛 쪽으로 들어올린다.\n\n\"이런! 멍이 심하고, 살갗이 안타깝게도 찢어졌군. 이거 무척 아프겠소.\"\n\n\"좀 쑤시긴 해요, 나리.\" 여인이 대답하는데 뺨에 눈물이 한 방울 돋는다.\n\n\"좀 더 편하게 해드리리다. 내 손수건이 당신을 아프게 하진 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 나리, 그건 분명해요!\"\n\n그는 다친 자리를 씻어내고 닦은 뒤, 조심스레 살피고 손바닥으로 부드럽게 눌러본 다음, 주머니에서 작은 약상자를 꺼내 처치하고 붕대를 감는다. 그렇게 일하면서, 길거리에 진료소를 차렸다며 웃고는 말한다. \"그래, 당신 남편은 벽돌공이오?\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아세요, 나리?\" 여인이 놀라 묻는다.\n\n\"아니, 가방이며 옷에 묻은 진흙 빛깔을 보고 짐작했소. 게다가 벽돌공들이 이곳저곳 다니며 삯일을 하는 걸 알고 있고. 그리고 안타깝게도, 그들이 아내에게 모질게 구는 것도 본 적이 있소.\"\n\n여인은 자기가 다친 게 그런 까닭이 아니라고 부인이라도 하려는 듯 황급히 눈을 든다. 그러나 이마에 놓인 손길을 느끼고, 분주하면서도 차분한 그의 얼굴을 보고는, 가만히 다시 눈을 내린다.\n\n\"그자는 지금 어디 있소?\" 외과의가 묻는다.\n\n\"어젯밤 말썽을 일으켰어요, 나리. 그래도 하숙집으로 저를 찾으러 올 거예요.\"\n\n\"여기서 그랬듯이 그 크고 묵직한 손을 자꾸 함부로 휘두르면, 더 큰 말썽에 빠질 거요. 하지만 당신은 그 짐승 같은 자를 용서하는군. 그러니 나도 더는 말하지 않으리다. 다만 그가 용서받을 자격이 있는 사람이면 좋겠소. 어린 자식은 없소?\"\n\n여인이 고개를 젓는다. \"제 아이라고 부르는 애가 하나 있긴 한데, 리즈네 아이예요, 나리.\"\n\n\"당신 아이는 죽었군. 알겠소! 가엾기도 하지!\"\n\n이쯤 되자 그는 일을 마치고 약상자를 챙긴다. \"정착해 사는 집은 있겠지요. 여기서 멉니까?\" 그녀가 일어나 무릎을 굽혀 인사하는 사이, 그는 자기가 한 일을 너스레로 가볍게 넘기며 묻는다.\n\n\"여기서 한 스물두세 마일은 족히 돼요, 나리. 세인트올번스에 있어요. 세인트올번스 아세요, 나리? 마치 아시는 것처럼 흠칫하시는 것 같던데요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 좀 알지. 자, 이제 나도 답례로 하나 물읍시다. 하숙비 낼 돈은 있소?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 그녀가 말한다. \"정말이지 진짜로요.\" 그러면서 돈을 보여준다. 그는 그녀가 거듭 나직이 건네는 감사에 답하여, 천만의 말씀이라 하고는 잘 가라고 인사하고 걸어간다. 톰 혼자 사는 곳은 여전히 잠들어 있고, 움직이는 것이라곤 없다.\n\n아니, 무언가 움직인다! 그가 멀리서 계단에 앉은 여인을 처음 발견한 자리로 되돌아가는데, 누더기를 걸친 형체 하나가 더없이 조심스럽게 다가오는 게 보인다. 더러운 벽에 바짝 웅크려서, 아무리 비참한 형체라도 차라리 피하고 싶을 그 벽에 붙어, 한 손을 슬그머니 앞으로 내밀며 온다. 얼굴이 움푹 꺼지고 두 눈에 야윈 광기가 번득이는 소년의 형체다. 들키지 않고 가려는 데에만 골몰한 나머지, 멀쩡한 옷을 차려입은 낯선 이가 나타나도 돌아볼 생각조차 않는다. 길 건너편을 지나치며 그는 누더기 팔꿈치로 얼굴을 가리고, 불안한 손을 앞에 내민 채, 형체도 알 수 없는 옷을 너덜너덜 늘어뜨린 채로 움츠리며 기어가듯 나아간다. 무슨 용도로 만든 옷인지, 무슨 천으로 만든 옷인지 도무지 말할 수 없다. 빛깔이며 재질이 마치 오래전에 썩어버린 늪지의 무성한 잎사귀 뭉치 같다.\n\n앨런 우드코트는 걸음을 멈추고 그를 좇아 보며 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보는데, 전에 그 아이를 본 적이 있다는 어렴풋한 느낌이 든다. 어떻게, 어디서였는지 떠오르지는 않지만, 그런 모습과 얽힌 무언가가 마음에 걸린다. 어느 병원이나 보호소에서 본 게 틀림없다 싶으면서도, 그것이 왜 이리 유난히 강렬하게 기억에 떠오르는지 도무지 알 수 없다.\n\n아침 햇살 속에서 톰 혼자 사는 곳을 차츰 빠져나오며 그 일을 생각하던 차에, 그는 뒤에서 달려오는 발소리를 듣고 돌아본다. 그 소년이 엄청난 속도로 그를 향해 내달리고, 그 뒤를 여인이 쫓아온다.\n\n\"붙잡아요, 붙잡아요!\" 여인이 숨이 턱에 차서 외친다. \"저 애 좀 붙잡아요, 나리!\"\n\n그는 길을 가로질러 소년의 앞길을 막지만, 소년이 그보다 빠르다. 곡선을 그리며 몸을 낮추고 그의 손 밑으로 빠져나가, 예닐곱 미터 너머에서 솟아올라 다시 내달린다. 여인은 여전히 쫓아오며 외친다. \"저 애 붙잡아요, 나리, 제발 붙잡아주세요!\" 앨런은 소년이 방금 그녀의 돈을 훔쳤는지도 모른다는 생각에 추격에 나서, 어찌나 세차게 내달리는지 열두 번이나 소년을 따라잡지만, 그때마다 소년은 곡선을 그리고 몸을 낮추고 빠져나가, 다시 내달린다. 이럴 때마다 그를 후려치면 쓰러뜨려 꼼짝 못 하게 할 수 있겠지만, 추격자는 차마 그러기로 마음먹지 못하니, 그렇게 음산하고도 우스꽝스러운 추격이 이어진다. 마침내 도망자는 몰릴 대로 몰린 끝에 좁은 골목으로, 막다른 안마당으로 들어선다. 여기 썩어가는 목재 울타리를 등지고 궁지에 몰린 그는 털썩 쓰러져, 숨을 헐떡이며 추격자를 올려다보고 누워 있고, 추격자도 서서 그를 향해 헐떡이다가 마침내 여인이 다가온다.\n\n\"오, 너, 조!\" 여인이 외친다. \"뭐야? 드디어 널 찾았구나!\"\n\n\"조라.\" 앨런이 그를 유심히 보며 되뇐다. \"조! 가만. 그렇지! 이 녀석을 얼마 전에 검시관 앞에 끌려나왔을 때 봤어.\"\n\n\"네, 댁을 그 검시판에서 한 번 봤구먼유.\" 조가 훌쩍이며 말한다. \"그게 뭐 어때서유? 나 같은 불쌍헌 놈 좀 가만 못 놔둬유? 나 아직두 충분히 불쌍허덜 않아유? 얼매나 불쌍해져야 속이 시원허것슈? 댁들헌테 쫓기구 또 쫓기구, 처음엔 이놈헌테 쫓기구 담엔 저놈헌테 쫓기구, 그러다 살가죽이랑 뼈만 남게 시달렸구먼유. 그 검시판은 내 잘못이 아니었슈. 난 아무것두 안 혔슈. 그 사람은 나헌테 잘혀줬슈, 잘혀줬다구유. 내 건널목에 온 사람덜 중에 나헌테 말 걸어준 유일헌 사람이었슈. 내가 그 사람 검시판에 부쳐지길 바랐을 리가 없잖유. 차라리 내가 그렇게 되구 싶구먼유. 어째서 내가 물에 빠져 죽지 않는지 모르것슈, 정말 모르것슈.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 애처로운 꼴로 그 말을 하는지, 그리고 그 꾀죄죄한 눈물이 어찌나 진짜처럼 보이는지, 또 울타리에 기대 구석에 누운 그 모습이 어찌나 방치와 더러움 속에서 자라난 곰팡이나 어떤 해로운 혹덩이 같은지, 앨런 우드코트는 그를 향해 마음이 누그러진다. 그가 여인에게 말한다. \"가엾은 것, 이 애가 뭘 했단 말이오?\"\n\n여인은 그 말에 대꾸 대신, 노여움보다는 어이없다는 표정으로 엎드린 형체를 보며 고개를 흔들 뿐이다. \"오, 너 조, 너 조. 드디어 널 찾았구나!\"\n\n\"이 애가 뭘 했소?\" 앨런이 말한다. \"당신 걸 훔쳤소?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 나리, 아니에요. 제 걸 훔쳐요? 이 애는 저한테 마음씨 고운 짓밖에 한 게 없어요. 바로 그게 놀라운 일이지요.\"\n\n앨런은 조에서 여인으로, 여인에서 조로 번갈아 보며, 둘 중 하나가 이 수수께끼를 풀어주기를 기다린다.\n\n\"이 애가 저랑 함께 있었거든요, 나리.\" 여인이 말한다. \"오, 너 조! 이 애가 저랑 함께 있었어요, 나리, 저 아래 세인트올번스에서, 앓고 있었지요. 그런데 어느 아가씨가, 저한테 좋은 벗이 되어주신 그분께 주님의 축복이 있기를, 제가 차마 못 할 때 이 애를 가엾이 여겨 집으로 데려갔는데요—\"\n\n앨런이 갑작스러운 공포에 그에게서 움찔 물러난다.\n\n\"네, 나리, 네. 집으로 데려가서, 편히 지내게 해줬는데, 이 배은망덕한 괴물 같은 놈이 밤중에 달아나서는, 방금 제 눈에 띌 때까지 코빼기도 안 보이고 소식도 없었어요. 그리고 그렇게 어여쁜 아가씨가 이 애의 병이 옮아서, 그 고운 얼굴을 잃고, 천사 같은 성품이랑 어여쁜 몸매랑 다정한 목소리만 아니었으면 지금은 같은 아가씨라고 알아보기도 힘들 지경이에요. 알기나 해? 이 배은망덕한 놈아, 이게 다 너 때문이고, 너한테 베푼 그분의 친절 때문이라는 걸 알기나 하냐고?\" 여인이 그 일을 떠올리며 그에게 화를 내기 시작하더니 격한 눈물을 터뜨린다.\n\n소년은 자기가 듣는 말에 거칠게 얻어맞은 듯 멍해져서는, 더러운 손바닥으로 더러운 이마를 문질러대고, 땅바닥을 멀거니 쳐다보며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨다 못해 기댄 미친 듯이 낡은 울타리가 덜컹덜컹 흔들린다.\n\n앨런은 그저 조용한 손짓 하나로 여인을 말리는데, 그게 효과가 있다.\n\n\"리처드가 나한테 말하기를—\" 그가 말을 더듬는다. \"내 말은, 이 이야기를 들은 적이 있다는 거요. 잠깐 나한테 신경 쓰지 마시오, 곧 말하리다.\"\n\n그는 돌아서서 한동안 지붕 덮인 골목 바깥을 내다보며 서 있다. 다시 돌아왔을 때 그는 평정을 되찾았다. 다만 소년을 피하려는 마음과 맞서 싸우고 있는데, 그게 어찌나 두드러지는지 여인의 주의를 온통 끌어당긴다.\n\n\"저 사람이 하는 말을 들었지. 어서 일어나라, 일어나!\"\n\n조가 떨면서 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 천천히 일어서더니, 곤경에 처한 제 동족의 방식대로 울타리에 모로 기대서서, 한쪽 솟은 어깨를 거기 받치고는 슬며시 오른손으로 왼손을, 왼발로 오른발을 비빈다.\n\n\"저 사람이 하는 말 들었지. 그게 사실이라는 걸 나도 안다. 줄곧 여기 있었느냐?\"\n\n\"오늘 이 좋은 아침까장 톰 혼자 사는 데를 본 적 없으면 천벌 받지유.\" 조가 쉰 목소리로 대답한다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 지금 여기 왔느냐?\"\n\n조는 비좁은 안마당을 빙 둘러보고, 질문한 이를 무릎 위로는 쳐다보지도 못한 채 마침내 대답한다. \"난 아무것두 헐 줄 모르구, 헐 일두 못 구해유. 난 무척 가난허구 아픈디, 아무두 없을 때 여기루 다시 와서, 어두워질 때까장 내가 아는 어디 한 군데 드러누워 숨어 있다가, 그담에 가서 스내그스비 나리헌테 푼돈이라두 빌어볼까 혔구먼유. 그 나리는 늘 나헌테 뭔가 줄 마음이 있었슈, 그랬다구유. 스내그스비 부인은 늘 날 쫓아내려구 혔지만유, 어디서나 누구나 다 그러듯이유.\"\n\n\"어디서 왔느냐?\"\n\n조는 다시 안마당을 빙 둘러보고, 다시 질문한 이의 무릎을 쳐다보다가, 결국 일종의 체념한 태도로 옆얼굴을 울타리에 갖다 댄다.\n\n\"내가 어디서 왔느냐고 물은 걸 못 들었느냐?\"\n\n\"그냥 떠돌았슈.\" 조가 말한다.\n\n\"자, 말해보아라.\" 앨런이 거부감을 억누르려 안간힘을 쓰며, 그에게 바짝 다가가, 신뢰의 표정으로 그를 굽어보며 말을 잇는다. \"그 착한 아가씨가 너를 가엾이 여겨 집으로 데려가는 불운을 겪었는데, 어쩌다 그 집을 떠나게 됐는지 말해보아라.\"\n\n조가 별안간 체념에서 깨어나 흥분하여, 여인을 향해 단언한다. 자기는 그 아가씨에 대해 전혀 몰랐고, 그런 얘기는 들어본 적도 없으며, 그분을 해칠 마음은 결코 없었고, 차라리 제 몸을 해쳤으면 했지, 그분 곁에 가느니 차라리 제 불쌍한 머리통을 잘렸을 거라고, 그분은 자기한테 무척 잘해줬다고 말한다. 시종일관 그 가엾은 방식으로나마 정말 진심인 듯이 굴다가, 무척 비참한 흐느낌으로 끝맺는다.\n\n앨런 우드코트는 이것이 거짓이 아님을 안다. 그는 억지로 자신을 다잡아 그를 만진다. \"자, 조. 말해다오.\"\n\n\"안 돼유. 그럴 수 없슈.\" 조가 다시 옆얼굴 자세로 돌아가며 말한다. \"그럴 수 없슈, 안 그러면 말허것지만유.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 알아야겠다.\" 다른 이가 대답한다. \"그래도 말이다. 자, 조.\"\n\n이렇게 두세 번 다그치자, 조가 다시 고개를 들고, 다시 안마당을 둘러보더니, 나직한 목소리로 말한다. \"그럼, 한 가지만 말헐게유. 난 끌려갔슈. 자, 됐쥬!\"\n\n\"끌려가? 밤중에?\"\n\n\"아!\" 누가 엿들을까 무척 겁먹은 조는 사방을 둘러보고, 심지어 울타리 꼭대기까지 삼 미터쯤 위로 흘끔 올려다보고는 그 틈새까지 살핀다. 자기가 믿지 못하는 그 대상이 넘겨다보거나 반대편에 숨어 있을까 봐서다.\n\n\"누가 너를 끌고 갔느냐?\"\n\n\"그 이름은 못 대유.\" 조가 말한다. \"못 대유, 나리.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 그 아가씨의 이름으로 알아야겠다. 나를 믿어도 좋다. 다른 누구도 듣지 못할 테니.\"\n\n\"아, 허지만 모르것슈.\" 조가 겁에 질려 고개를 흔들며 대답한다. \"그자가 안 듣는다는 걸 말이유.\"\n\n\"아니, 그자는 여기 없잖느냐.\"\n\n\"오, 없을 것 같쥬?\" 조가 말한다. \"그자는 온갖 데 다 있슈, 한꺼번에 다유.\"\n\n앨런은 어리둥절하여 그를 바라보지만, 이 어리둥절한 대답 밑바닥에 어떤 진짜 의미와 진심이 깔려 있음을 알아챈다. 그는 참을성 있게 명확한 답을 기다린다. 그리고 조는 그 무엇보다 그의 참을성에 더 어쩔 줄 몰라 하다가, 마침내 절박하게 그의 귀에 어떤 이름을 속삭인다.\n\n\"이런!\" 앨런이 말한다. \"아니, 너는 무슨 짓을 했길래?\"\n\n\"아무것두 안 혔슈, 나리. 말썽에 휘말릴 짓은 결코 안 혔슈. 자리를 안 비킨 거랑 그 검시판 말구는유. 허지만 지금은 비키구 있슈. 매장지루 비켜 가구 있슈, 그게 내가 허는 비키기유.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그건 막아보자꾸나. 한데 그자가 너를 어떻게 했느냐?\"\n\n\"날 병원에 처넣었슈.\" 조가 속삭이며 대답한다. \"퇴원헐 때까장유. 그담에 푼돈을, 반 황소 네 닢, 그러니께 댁들이 말허는 반 크라운 네 닢을 주구는, '꺼져! 여긴 아무두 너 따위 필요 없어' 허구는, '꺼지란 말여. 가서 떠돌어' 허구는, '비켜' 허구는, '런던에서 사십 마일 안쪽 어디서두 두 번 다시 내 눈에 띄지 마라, 안 그러면 후회헐 거다' 혔슈. 그러것쥬, 만약 그자가 날 보면유. 내가 땅 위에 살아 있는 한 그자는 날 볼 거구유.\" 조는 신경질적으로 아까의 그 조심성과 살핌을 모두 되풀이하며 끝맺는다.\n* 반 황소: 조의 거리 은어. 반 크라운(은화)을 가리킨다.\n\n앨런은 잠시 생각하더니, 여인 쪽으로 돌아서되 조에게는 격려의 눈길을 거두지 않은 채 말한다. \"이 애는 당신이 생각한 것만큼 배은망덕하진 않소. 떠난 데는 까닭이 있었던 거요. 충분치는 않은 까닭이었지만.\"\n\n\"고맙구먼유, 나리, 고맙구먼유!\" 조가 외친다. \"거 보슈! 댁이 나헌테 얼매나 모질게 굴었는지. 허지만 그저 그 아가씨헌테 이 나리가 허신 말씀만 전혀주면, 다 괜찮유. 댁두 나헌테 무척 잘혀줬으니께유, 나두 알아유.\"\n\n\"자, 조.\" 앨런이 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말한다. \"나랑 같이 가자. 여기보다 누워 숨기에 더 나은 데를 찾아주마. 남들 눈에 안 띄게 나는 길 한쪽으로, 너는 다른 쪽으로 가면, 네가 약속만 한다면 달아나지 않으리란 걸 나는 잘 안다.\"\n\n\"안 달아나유, 그자가 오는 게 보이지 않는 한은유, 나리.\"\n\n\"좋다. 네 말을 믿으마. 이맘때쯤이면 마을 절반이 일어나고, 한 시간만 더 지나면 마을 전체가 완전히 깰 거다. 가자. 잘 가시오, 착한 아주머니.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요, 나리, 거듭거듭 진심으로 고맙습니다.\"\n\n여인은 가방 위에 앉아 깊이 귀 기울이고 있다가, 이제 일어나 그것을 집어 든다. 조는 \"그저 그 아가씨헌테 내가 그분 해칠 맘은 결코 없었다구, 이 나리가 허신 말씀을 꼭 전혀줘유!\" 하고 되뇌며, 고개를 끄덕이고 비척거리고 부르르 떨고, 얼굴을 문지르고 눈을 깜빡이고, 반은 웃고 반은 울며 그녀에게 작별을 고하고는, 앨런 우드코트의 뒤를 따라 길 건너편 집들에 바짝 붙어 기어가듯 길을 나선다. 이런 차림새로 두 사람은 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 빠져나와 환한 햇살과 한결 맑은 공기 속으로 올라온다.\n\n앨런 우드코트와 조가 거리를 따라 걸어간다. 아침 햇살 속에서 우뚝 솟은 교회 첨탑들과 먼 풍경이 어찌나 가깝고 또렷한지 도시 전체가 휴식으로 새로 태어난 듯하다. 앨런은 이 동행을 어디에 어떻게 맡길지 머릿속으로 궁리한다. \"참으로 묘한 일이다.\" 그는 생각한다. \"문명 세계 한복판에서 사람 모습을 한 이 가련한 존재를 처리하는 일이, 주인 없는 개를 처리하는 것보다 더 어렵다니.\" 그러나 묘하다고 해서 사실이 아닌 건 아니며, 어려움은 여전히 남아 있다.\n\n처음에 그는 조가 정말로 따라오고 있는지 확인하려고 뒤를 자주 돌아본다. 그러나 어디를 보든 조는 늘 맞은편 집들에 바싹 붙은 채, 조심스러운 손길로 벽돌에서 벽돌로, 문에서 문으로 더듬으며 길을 헤쳐 나오고, 살금살금 기어오면서도 그를 향해 경계하듯 흘끔거린다. 곧 그를 따돌릴 생각 따위는 조의 머릿속에 전혀 없음을 확인하자, 앨런은 한층 덜 산만한 정신으로 어떻게 해야 할지 궁리하며 걸음을 옮긴다.\n\n길모퉁이의 간이 아침 식사 가판대가 가장 먼저 할 일을 일러준다. 그는 거기 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보고 조에게 손짓한다. 조가 길을 건너 절뚝거리고 발을 끌며 다가온다. 오른손 마디를 왼손 오목한 손바닥에 대고 천천히 빙빙 문지르며, 타고난 절구와 절굿공이로 때를 반죽한다. 조에게는 진수성찬일 음식이 그의 앞에 차려지고, 그는 먹고 마시는 내내 사방을 불안하게 두리번거리며, 겁먹은 짐승처럼 커피를 꿀꺽꿀꺽 삼키고 버터 바른 빵을 갉아 먹기 시작한다.\n\n그러나 그는 너무 아프고 비참해서 허기조차 그를 떠나버렸다. \"거의 굶어 죽을 지경인 줄 알았어요, 나리.\" 조가 곧 음식을 내려놓으며 말한다. \"근데 아무것도 모르겠어요. 그것조차 모르겠어요. 음식을 먹고 싶은 마음도, 마시고 싶은 마음도 없어요.\" 그러고는 조가 부들부들 떨며 의아한 듯 아침 식사를 바라본다.\n\n앨런 우드코트가 그의 맥을 짚고 가슴에 손을 댄다. \"숨을 들이쉬어 봐, 조!\" \"들이쉬어지긴 해요.\" 조가 말한다. \"수레만큼이나 무겁게요.\" \"수레처럼 덜컹거리기도 한다\"고 덧붙일 법한데도, 그는 그저 중얼거릴 뿐이다. \"비키며 가는 중이에요, 나리.\"\n\n앨런이 약방을 찾아 주위를 둘러본다. 가까이엔 없지만 술집이 그만큼, 아니 그보다 더 쓸모가 있다. 그는 포도주를 조금 받아와 아주 조심스럽게 소년에게 한 모금 먹인다. 포도주가 입술을 지나자마자 조는 거의 곧바로 기운을 차리기 시작한다. \"이 약을 한 번 더 줘도 되겠다, 조.\" 앨런이 세심한 얼굴로 그를 지켜본 뒤 말한다. \"자! 이제 오 분만 쉬었다가, 다시 가자.\"\n\n소년을 간이 가판대 의자에 앉히고 등을 쇠난간에 기대게 한 뒤, 앨런 우드코트는 이른 햇살 속을 오르락내리락 거닐며, 지켜보는 티는 내지 않은 채 이따금 그를 향해 눈길을 던진다. 소년이 따뜻해지고 기운을 차렸음을 알아채는 데는 별다른 통찰이 필요하지 않다. 저토록 그늘진 얼굴이 환해질 수 있다면, 그의 얼굴이 다소 환해진다. 그리고 그토록 절망스럽게 내려놓았던 빵 한 조각을 조금씩 조금씩 먹는다. 이렇게 호전되는 기색을 지켜보다가, 앨런이 그에게 말을 붙여, 적잖이 놀랍게도 베일 쓴 부인의 모험담을 그 모든 결말까지 끌어낸다. 조는 느릿느릿 이야기하며 느릿느릿 우물거린다. 이야기와 빵을 다 마치자, 둘은 다시 길을 떠난다.\n\n소년을 잠시 묵게 할 곳을 찾기가 어렵다는 이 난제를, 앨런은 자신의 오랜 환자이자 열성적인 자그마한 플라이트 양에게 상의하려 마음먹고, 그와 조가 처음 마주쳤던 그 뜰로 앞장서 간다. 그러나 넝마와 병을 파는 가게는 모든 게 달라져 있다. 플라이트 양은 더 이상 거기 묵지 않는다. 가게는 닫혀 있다. 먼지를 잔뜩 뒤집어써 나이를 가늠하기 어려운, 그러나 실은 다름 아닌 그 흥미로운 인물 주디인, 모진 인상의 여자가 무뚝뚝하고 인색하게 대꾸한다. 그래도 그 대꾸만으로 플라이트 양이 새들과 함께 벨 야드의 블라인더 부인 댁에 자리 잡았음을 알아낸 그는, 인근의 그곳으로 향한다. 거기서 플라이트 양이(훌륭한 친구인 대법관이 여는 정의의 회의에 늦지 않으려고 일찍 일어나는 그녀가) 환영의 눈물을 글썽이며 두 팔을 벌리고 계단을 뛰어 내려온다.\n\n\"내 친애하는 의사 선생님!\" 플라이트 양이 외친다. \"공로 많고 출중하고 명예로운 장교님!\" 그녀는 이상한 표현을 쓰긴 하지만, 멀쩡한 정신이 보일 수 있는 만큼이나, 아니 그보다도 더 다정하고 마음이 가득하다. 앨런은 그녀에게 무척 인내심을 발휘하며, 그녀가 더 이상 토해낼 황홀경이 남지 않을 때까지 기다린 뒤, 문간에서 떨고 있는 조를 가리키며 어쩌다 그가 거기 오게 되었는지 들려준다.\n\n\"이 근처에서 당분간 이 아이를 어디 묵게 할 수 있을까요? 부인은 지식과 분별이 풍부하니, 제게 조언을 해주실 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n플라이트 양은 그 칭찬에 잔뜩 우쭐해져 골똘히 생각에 잠긴다. 그러나 좋은 생각이 떠오르기까지는 한참이 걸린다. 블라인더 부인 댁은 방이 모조리 나가 있고, 그녀 자신은 가엾은 그리들리의 방을 쓰고 있다. \"그리들리!\" 이 말을 스무 번째쯤 되풀이한 끝에 플라이트 양이 손뼉을 치며 외친다. \"그리들리! 그렇고말고! 물론이지! 내 친애하는 의사 선생님! 조지 장군이 우리를 도와줄 거예요.\"\n\n조지 장군이 누구인지 알아보려고 묻는 건 부질없는 일이고, 설령 플라이트 양이 벌써 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 꼭 끼는 보닛과 초라하고 자그마한 숄을 두르고 서류 가방으로 무장하지 않았더라도 마찬가지였을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 만반의 채비를 하고 내려오면서 두서없는 말투로, 자기가 자주 찾아가는 조지 장군이 친애하는 피츠 잔다이스를 알고 그녀와 관련된 모든 일에 큰 관심을 가지고 있다고 알려주자, 앨런은 둘이 옳은 길을 가고 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 든다. 그래서 그는 조를 격려하느라, 이렇게 돌아다니는 일도 이제 곧 끝날 거라고 말한다. 그러고 둘은 장군의 집으로 향한다. 다행히 멀지 않다.\n\n조지의 사격 연습장 겉모습과, 긴 입구와, 그 너머로 휑하니 펼쳐진 풍경을 보고, 앨런 우드코트는 길조를 점친다. 아침 운동으로 그들을 향해 성큼성큼 다가오는 조지 씨 본인의 모습에서도 그는 희망을 엿본다. 입에는 파이프를 물고, 목에는 깃을 두르지 않았으며, 양날 검과 아령으로 단련한 우람한 두 팔이 얇은 셔츠 소매를 뚫고 묵직하게 그 존재감을 드러낸다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 군대식 경례를 붙이며 말한다. 그러고 너른 이마부터 곱슬머리까지 온통 흐뭇하게 웃다가, 플라이트 양이 위엄을 잔뜩 갖추고 한참에 걸쳐 정중한 소개 의식을 치르는 동안 그녀에게 정중히 양보한다. 그는 또 한 번 \"안녕하십니까, 선생!\"과 또 한 번의 경례로 그것을 마무리한다.\n\n\"실례지만, 선생. 뱃사람이신 듯한데요?\" 조지 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"제게 그런 풍모가 있다니 자랑스럽습니다.\" 앨런이 대꾸한다. \"하지만 저는 그저 배를 타는 의사일 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까, 선생! 저는 정식 해군이신 줄로 알았는데요.\"\n\n앨런은 그래서 더더욱 조지 씨가 자신의 무례한 방문을 너그러이 용서해주기를 바라며, 특히 파이프를 치우지 말아 주기를 바란다고 말한다. 조지 씨가 예의상 파이프를 치울 기색을 보였기 때문이다. \"참 친절하십니다, 선생.\" 기병이 대꾸한다. \"플라이트 양이 싫어하지 않는 줄은 제 경험으로 잘 알고 있고, 선생께도 마찬가지로 괜찮으시다니—\" 그러고는 파이프를 다시 입에 물어 문장을 끝맺는다. 앨런이 이어서 조에 대해 자기가 아는 모든 것을 들려주고, 기병은 진지한 얼굴로 귀를 기울인다.\n\n\"그러니까 저 아이가 그 녀석이로군요, 선생?\" 그가 묻는다. 그러고는 입구를 따라, 회칠한 정면에 큼지막한 글자들이 적혀 있지만 자기 눈에는 아무 뜻도 없는 그것을 멍하니 올려다보고 선 조 쪽으로 시선을 던진다.\n\n\"바로 그 녀석입니다.\" 앨런이 말한다. \"그런데 조지 씨, 저 아이 문제로 제가 곤경에 빠져 있습니다. 저는 저 아이를 병원에 넣고 싶지 않습니다. 당장 입원시킬 수 있다 해도요. 거기 들여보낼 수만 있다 쳐도, 몇 시간을 못 버티고 나와버릴 게 뻔하기 때문입니다. 구빈원도 마찬가지예요. 저 아이를 거기 넣으려고 이리저리 따돌림당하고 외면당하며 이 기둥에서 저 기둥으로 떠넘겨지는 수고를 참아낸다 쳐도 말입니다. 그건 제가 도무지 정이 가지 않는 방식이라서요.\"\n\n\"누군들 정이 가겠습니까, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 대꾸한다.\n\n\"저 아이는 어느 쪽에도 머물지 않으리라고 확신합니다. 자기더러 비켜 있으라고 명령한 사람에 대한 유별난 공포에 사로잡혀 있거든요. 무지한 탓에, 저 아이는 그 사람이 어디에나 있고 모든 것을 다 알고 있다고 믿고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"그자의 이름을 아직 말씀하지 않으셨군요. 비밀입니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"아이가 비밀로 삼고 있지요. 하지만 그 사람 이름은 버킷입니다.\"\n\n\"형사 버킷 말입니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"바로 그 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 아는 사람입니다, 선생.\" 기병이 연기를 한 모금 내뿜고 가슴을 떡 펴며 대꾸한다. \"그리고 그 아이 말이 거기까진 옳아요. 그자는 분명—만만찮은 작자거든요.\" 조지 씨는 이 말 뒤에 깊은 뜻을 담아 담배를 피우며, 말없이 플라이트 양을 바라본다.\n\n\"그래서 저는 적어도 잔다이스 씨와 서머슨 양이 이 사실을, 그러니까 그토록 기이한 이야기를 들려주는 이 조가 다시 나타났다는 사실을 알게 되고, 만약 원한다면 이 아이와 이야기를 나눌 수 있도록 해주고 싶습니다. 그래서 당분간 점잖은 사람들이 운영하는, 이 아이를 받아줄 만한 허름한 거처에라도 넣어두고 싶은 겁니다. 조지 씨, 보시다시피 점잖은 사람들과 조는.\" 앨런이 입구를 따라 향한 기병의 눈길을 좇으며 말한다. \"그다지 가깝게 지낸 사이가 못 됩니다. 그래서 이게 어려운 거지요. 혹시 이 근처에 제가 미리 비용을 치르면 얼마간 이 아이를 받아줄 만한 사람을 아십니까?\"\n\n이 질문을 던지면서 그는 기병의 팔꿈치께에 더러운 얼굴의 자그마한 사내가 서서, 기묘하게 비틀린 몸매와 표정으로 기병의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있음을 알아챈다. 파이프를 몇 모금 더 빨고 나서, 기병이 그 자그마한 사내를 비스듬히 내려다보고, 자그마한 사내가 기병을 향해 눈을 찡긋한다.\n\n\"그러시다면, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"분명히 말씀드리는데, 서머슨 양에게 조금이라도 좋은 일이 된다면 저는 언제든 기꺼이 머리통을 한 대 얻어맞을 수도 있습니다. 그러니 그 아가씨께 아무리 사소한 거라도 도움을 드리는 일을 영광으로 여깁니다. 여기 저랑 필은 천성이 떠돌이 신세라서요, 선생. 보시다시피 이 꼴인 곳입니다. 선생 뜻에 맞으신다면 이곳 조용한 구석을 이 아이에게 기꺼이 내드리지요. 식대 말고는 따로 받을 게 없습니다. 여기 형편이 그리 넉넉지가 못해서요, 선생. 언제 통고 한마디에 멱살 잡혀 쫓겨날지 모르는 신세입니다. 하지만 선생, 이런 곳이나마, 그리고 여기가 버티는 동안은, 얼마든지 쓰시지요.\"\n\n조지 씨가 파이프를 한 바퀴 휘둘러, 건물 전체를 방문객 마음대로 쓰라고 내준다.\n\n\"당연한 얘기겠습니다만, 선생.\" 그가 덧붙인다. \"선생이 의료진의 한 분이시니, 이 가엾은 아이가 당장 옮길 만한 전염병 같은 건 없겠지요?\"\n\n앨런은 확실히 없다고 한다.\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 말한다. \"그런 건 이미 충분히 겪을 만큼 겪었거든요.\"\n\n그의 어조에 못지않게 슬픈 어조로 새로 사귄 지인도 맞장구를 친다. \"그래도 말씀드려야겠습니다.\" 앨런이 아까의 확언을 거듭한 뒤 말한다. \"이 아이는 한심할 만큼 쇠약하고 망가져 있어서, 어쩌면—꼭 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만—회복하기엔 너무 깊이 가버렸을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"지금 위독하다고 보십니까, 선생?\" 기병이 묻는다.\n\"네, 그런 것 같아 두렵습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 선생.\" 기병이 단호한 어조로 대꾸한다. \"제 생각에는—저 자신이 천성이 떠돌이 신세이니 하는 말입니다만—저 아이를 길거리에서 끌어들이는 게 빠르면 빠를수록 좋겠습니다. 야, 필! 데리고 들어와!\"\n\n스쿼드 씨가 한쪽으로 몸을 기울인 채 명령을 수행하러 종종걸음으로 나간다. 기병은 파이프를 다 피우고 한쪽에 내려놓는다. 조가 들어온다. 조는 파디글 부인의 토커후포 인디언이 아니다. 조는 보리오불라가와는 전혀 무관하니, 젤리비 부인의 어린양도 아니다. 조는 거리와 낯섦으로 미화된 존재가 아니다. 조는 진짜 외국에서 자란 야만인이 아니다. 조는 평범한 국산품이다. 더럽고, 추하고, 모든 감각에 거슬리며, 몸뚱이는 흔한 거리의 흔한 짐승이요, 영혼만 이교도다. 토종 오물이 그를 더럽히고, 토종 기생충이 그를 파먹고, 토종 종기가 그의 몸에 돋고, 토종 누더기가 그의 몸에 걸쳐 있다. 영국 땅과 기후에서 자란 토종 무지가 그의 불멸하는 본성을 스러질 짐승보다 더 낮은 데로 가라앉힌다. 조여, 한 점 에누리 없는 본색으로 나서라! 네 발바닥부터 정수리까지, 네게는 흥미로울 것이라곤 하나도 없다.\n\n그는 발을 끌며 천천히 조지 씨의 연습장 안으로 들어와, 보따리처럼 잔뜩 웅크린 채 바닥만 두루 살핀다. 그는 사람들이 자기를 꺼리고 싶어 한다는 걸 아는 듯하다. 자기가 어떤 존재인지 때문이기도 하고, 자기가 무슨 일을 저질렀는지 때문이기도 하다. 그 역시 그들을 꺼린다. 그는 같은 부류에 속하지 않고, 창조의 질서 안에서 같은 자리를 차지하지 않는다. 그는 어떤 부류에도, 어떤 자리에도 속하지 않으니, 짐승에도 인간에도 속하지 않는다.\n\n\"이리 봐라, 조!\" 앨런이 말한다. \"이분은 조지 씨다.\"\n\n조는 한동안 더 바닥을 살피다가, 잠깐 올려다보고, 다시 내려다본다.\n\n\"이분은 네게 친절한 친구다. 너한테 여기 묵을 방을 내주실 거니까.\"\n\n조가 한 손으로 한 번 떠올리는 시늉을 하는데, 그게 절인 모양이다. 잠시 더 궁리하고 발을 이리저리 바꿔 디딘 끝에, 그는 \"정말 고맙습니다\" 하고 중얼거린다.\n\n\"여기선 아주 안전하다. 지금 네가 할 일은 그저 말 잘 듣고 기운을 차리는 것뿐이야. 그리고 명심해라, 조, 무슨 일이 있어도 여기선 우리한테 사실대로 말해야 한다.\"\n\n\"안 그러면 죽어버릴게요, 나리.\" 조가 즐겨 쓰는 맹세로 되돌아가 말한다. \"전 여태껏 나리가 아시는 거 말고는 말썽 부릴 짓을 한 적이 한 번도 없어요. 다른 말썽 같은 건 아예 없었어요, 나리. 아무것도 모르는 거랑 굶주리는 거 말곤요.\"\n\n\"믿는다, 이제 조지 씨 말씀을 들어라. 너한테 하실 말씀이 있으신 것 같다.\"\n\n\"제 뜻은 그저, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 놀랍도록 떡 벌어진 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 말한다. \"이 녀석한테 어디서 누워 흠뻑 한숨 잘 수 있는지 일러주려는 거였습니다. 자, 이리 보거라.\" 기병은 말하면서 그들을 연습장 저쪽 끝으로 데려가, 자그마한 칸막이 방 하나를 연다. \"자, 보거라! 여기 매트리스가 있고, 행실만 바르면, 여기서 쉬어도 된다. 저, 실례지만, 선생—\" 그가 앨런이 건넨 명함을 미안한 듯 들여다본다. \"우드코트 씨가 좋다고 하시는 동안은 말이다. 총소리가 들려도 놀라지 마라. 표적을 겨눈 거지 너를 겨눈 게 아니니까. 자, 한 가지 더 권하고 싶은 게 있습니다, 선생.\" 기병이 방문객 쪽으로 돌아서며 말한다. \"필, 이리 와!\"\n\n필이 늘 하던 식으로 그들을 향해 비스듬히 다가온다. \"여기 이 사람은 말입니다, 선생, 갓난아기 때 시궁창에서 발견된 자입니다. 그러니 이 가엾은 아이한테 자연히 마음이 가는 게 당연하지요. 그렇지, 필?\"\n\n\"확실히, 틀림없이 그렇습니다요, 대장.\" 필이 대답한다.\n\n\"그래서 제 생각엔 말입니다, 선생.\" 마치 북 위에 펼쳐진 군사 회의에서 의견을 내놓듯, 조지 씨가 군인다운 비밀스러운 어조로 말한다. \"이 사람이 저 아이를 목욕탕에 데려가서, 몇 실링쯤 들여 거친 옷가지 한두 벌을 마련해준다면—\"\n\n\"조지 씨, 사려 깊은 친구분.\" 앨런이 지갑을 꺼내며 대꾸한다. \"바로 제가 부탁드리려던 일입니다.\"\n\n필 스쿼드와 조는 이 단장(端裝)의 임무를 띠고 즉시 내보내진다. 자기 성공에 흠뻑 취한 플라이트 양은 부랴부랴 법정으로 향한다. 안 그러면 친구인 대법관이 자기 걱정을 하거나, 자기가 없는 사이에 그토록 오래 기다려온 판결을 내려버릴까 봐 몹시 두려운 것이다. 그러면서 그녀는 한마디 덧붙인다. \"아시다시피, 내 친애하는 의사 선생님이자 장군님, 이렇게 오랜 세월이 흘렀는데 그리되면 너무 어처구니없이 불운한 일이잖아요!\" 앨런은 이 틈을 타 회복제 몇 가지를 사러 나갔다가, 가까운 데서 그것을 구해 곧 돌아와, 기병이 연습장을 오르락내리락 거니는 모습을 보고는 보조를 맞춰 함께 걷는다.\n\n\"제 짐작입니다만, 선생.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"서머슨 양을 꽤 잘 아시지요?\"\n\n그렇다는 것 같다.\n\n\"친척은 아니시고요, 선생?\"\n\n아니라는 것 같다.\n\n\"호기심을 부리는 것 같아 실례입니다.\" 조지 씨가 말한다. \"서머슨 양이 이 가엾은 아이에게 그토록 안타까운 관심을 쏟았으니, 선생도 이 아이한테 보통 이상의 관심을 가지셨을 법하다는 생각이 들어서요. 저야 그렇거든요, 선생, 정말입니다.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다, 조지 씨.\"\n\n기병은 햇볕에 그을린 앨런의 뺨과 빛나는 검은 눈을 곁눈질하고, 그의 키와 체격을 재빨리 가늠하더니, 그가 마음에 드는 모양이다.\n\n\"선생이 나가 계신 동안, 제가 곰곰 생각해보니, 버킷이 그 아이를 데려갔다는 링컨스 인 필즈의 그 방을, 아이 말대로라면, 틀림없이 제가 알고 있습니다. 그 아이는 이름을 모르지만, 제가 알려드릴 수 있어요. 토킹혼입니다. 바로 그자예요.\"\n\n앨런이 미심쩍은 얼굴로 그를 보며 그 이름을 되뇐다.\n\n\"토킹혼. 그 이름입니다, 선생. 제가 그자를 압니다. 게다가 그자가 자기한테 밉보인 어느 고인 일로 전에 버킷과 연락을 주고받은 사이라는 것도 압니다. 제가 그자를 알지요, 선생. 안타깝게도요.\"\n\n앨런은 자연히 그가 어떤 사람이냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"어떤 사람이냐고요! 생김새 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그건 얼추 아는 듯합니다. 상대하기에 어떤 사람인가 하는 거지요. 통틀어서, 어떤 사람입니까?\"\n\n\"그러시다면, 말씀드리지요, 선생.\" 기병이 우뚝 멈춰 서서 떡 벌어진 가슴 위에 팔짱을 끼는데, 어찌나 성이 났는지 얼굴이 온통 벌겋게 달아오른다. \"지긋지긋하게 못된 부류의 인간입니다. 천천히 사람을 고문하는 부류지요. 녹슨 낡은 기병총만큼이나 피도 눈물도 없는 인간입니다. 맹세코, 그자는—다른 모든 인간을 합친 것보다도 저를 더 안절부절못하게, 더 불안하게, 그리고 제 자신에게 더 불만스럽게 만든 부류의 인간입니다. 토킹혼 씨가 바로 그런 인간이에요!\"\n\n\"죄송합니다.\" 앨런이 말한다. \"이렇게 아픈 데를 건드려서요.\"\n\n\"아픈 데라고요?\" 기병이 다리를 더 넓게 벌리고 떡 벌어진 오른손바닥에 침을 적셔, 있지도 않은 콧수염에 갖다 댄다. \"선생 잘못은 아니지요. 하지만 직접 판단해보십시오. 그자는 저를 휘어잡을 힘을 쥐고 있어요. 아까 제가 이곳에서 저를 멱살 잡아 내쫓을 수 있다고 한 그자가 바로 토킹혼입니다. 그자는 저를 끊임없이 시소 위에 올려놓고 흔들어대요. 손을 떼지도 않고, 본격적으로 나서지도 않습니다. 그자에게 갚을 돈이 있거나, 기한을 부탁해야 하거나, 무슨 일로든 찾아가야 하면, 그자는 저를 보지도, 제 말을 듣지도 않은 채—클리퍼드 인의 멜키세덱한테로 떠넘기고, 클리퍼드 인의 멜키세덱은 다시 저를 그자한테로 떠넘기지요. 마치 제가 자기랑 똑같은 돌덩이로 만들어진 양, 저를 자기 주위에서 어슬렁거리고 매달리게 만듭니다. 글쎄, 저는 이제 거의 인생의 절반을 그자의 문간을 서성이고 피해 다니는 데 허비하고 있어요. 그자가 신경이나 쓰겠습니까? 천만에요. 제가 그자를 빗댄 녹슨 낡은 기병총만큼도 신경 안 씁니다. 그자가 저를 어찌나 안달 나게 들들 볶는지—쳇! 헛소리! 제가 정신을 놓고 있었군요. 우드코트 씨.\" 기병이 다시 걸음을 옮긴다. \"제 말은 그저, 그자가 노인이라는 겁니다. 하지만 탁 트인 벌판에서 말에 박차를 가해 그자에게 돌격할 기회가 영영 없으리란 게 다행이에요. 만약 그자가 저를 몰아넣는 그런 격분에 휩싸여 그런 기회를 잡았더라면—그자는 끝장이었을 겁니다, 선생!\"\n\n조지 씨는 어찌나 흥분했는지 셔츠 소매로 이마를 닦아야 할 지경이다. 국가(國歌)를 휘파람으로 불며 격정을 날려 보내는 와중에도, 자기도 모르게 고개를 몇 번 흔들고 가슴을 들썩이는 잔여 동작이 여전히 남아 있고, 더구나 이따금 두 손으로 풀어 헤친 셔츠 깃을 황급히 매만지는데, 마치 질식할 것 같은 답답함을 막기에는 깃이 충분히 풀려 있지 않은 듯하다. 한마디로, 앨런 우드코트는 방금 말한 그 벌판에서 토킹혼 씨가 끝장날 것임을 별로 의심하지 않는다.\n\n조와 그를 데려간 사람이 곧 돌아오고, 조는 꼼꼼한 필의 부축을 받아 매트리스로 옮겨진다. 앨런은 자기 손으로 약을 제대로 먹인 뒤, 필에게 필요한 모든 수단과 지침을 일러준다. 어느덧 아침이 빠르게 깊어간다. 그는 옷을 갈아입고 아침을 먹으러 자기 거처로 돌아갔다가, 쉴 생각도 않고 자신의 발견을 알리러 잔다이스 씨에게 떠난다.\n\n잔다이스 씨가 그와 함께 단둘이 돌아오는데, 이 일을 정말이지 아주 조용히 묻어두어야 할 사정이 있다고 은밀히 일러주며, 이에 진지한 관심을 보인다. 잔다이스 씨에게 조는 아침에 한 이야기를 실질적으로 똑같이, 별다른 차이 없이 되풀이한다. 다만 그 수레가 끌기에 더 무거워졌고, 더 텅 빈 소리를 내며 끌릴 뿐이다.\n\n\"여기 가만히 누워서, 이제 더는 쫓겨 다니지 않게 해주세요.\" 조가 가까스로 말한다. \"그리고 제가 늘 자던 데 근처를 지나가는 누구라도 친절을 베풀어서, 한때 알던 조가 제 할 일을 다하러 똑바로 비키며 가고 있다고 스내그스비 씨한테 그저 한마디만 전해주면, 정말 고맙겠어요. 불쌍한 놈한테 그게 어떻게든 가능하다면, 지금보다 더 고맙겠어요.\"\n\n조가 하루 이틀 사이에 이렇게 그 법률 문구상을 하도 여러 번 들먹이는 통에, 앨런은 잔다이스 씨와 의논한 끝에, 수레가 무너져 내리는 듯하기도 해서, 마음씨 좋게 쿡스 코트에 들르기로 마음먹는다.\n\n그리하여 그는 쿡스 코트로 향한다. 스내그스비 씨는 회색 외투에 토시를 끼고 계산대 뒤에 서서, 정서공한테서 막 들어온 여러 장짜리 증서를 검사하고 있다. 끔찍한 단조로움을 깨고 길손을 절망에서 구해줄 큼지막한 글자 몇 개가 군데군데 쉼터처럼 박혀 있을 뿐, 법률 글씨와 양피지가 광막한 사막처럼 펼쳐져 있다. 스내그스비 씨가 그 잉크 우물 가운데 한 군데서 멈춰 서서, 으레 업무 채비로 짓는 헛기침을 하며 낯선 이를 맞는다.\n\n\"저를 기억 못 하시겠지요, 스내그스비 씨?\"\n\n문구상의 심장이 무겁게 쿵쿵거리기 시작한다. 그 해묵은 불안이 한 번도 가라앉은 적이 없기 때문이다. 그가 겨우 할 수 있는 대답은 이뿐이다. \"아니요, 선생, 기억난다고는 못 하겠는데요. 제 생각엔—너무 까다롭게 따지자는 건 아닙니다만—전에 한 번도 뵌 적이 없는 것 같은데요, 선생.\"\n\n\"두 번 뵈었지요.\" 앨런 우드코트가 말한다. \"한 번은 어느 가엾은 자의 병상 곁에서, 또 한 번은—\"\n\n'드디어 올 게 왔구나!' 시달리는 문구상이 기억이 번뜩 떠오르며 생각한다. '이제 곪을 대로 곪아서 터지려는구나!' 그래도 그는 방문객을 자그마한 회계실로 안내해 문을 닫을 만큼의 정신은 가까스로 붙들고 있다.\n\n\"결혼하셨습니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"아니요, 안 했습니다.\"\n\n\"미혼이시긴 해도, 한번 애써주시겠습니까.\" 스내그스비 씨가 우울한 속삭임으로 말한다. \"되도록 낮은 목소리로 말씀해주시면요. 제 마누라가 어딘가에서 엿듣고 있거든요. 안 그러면 제 가게랑 오백 파운드를 다 내놓아도 좋습니다!\"\n\n깊은 낙심에 빠진 스내그스비 씨는 책상에 등을 기대고 의자에 앉아 항변한다. \"저는 제 비밀이라곤 한 번도 가져본 적이 없습니다, 선생. 마누라가 혼인 날짜를 잡은 이래로, 제 일로 마누라를 속이려 든 적이 단 한 번이라도 있었다고는 도무지 기억이 안 납니다. 그러지 않았을 거예요, 선생. 너무 까다롭게 따지자는 건 아닙니다만, 그럴 수도 없었고, 감히 그럴 엄두도 못 냈을 겁니다. 그런데도, 그럼에도 불구하고, 제 인생이 짐이 될 만큼 저는 비밀과 수수께끼에 칭칭 휘감겨 있는 처지가 되어버렸지요.\"\n\n방문객은 그 말을 들으니 안타깝다고 하고는, 조를 기억하느냐고 묻는다. 스내그스비 씨가 억눌린 신음으로 답한다. 아, 어찌 잊겠습니까!\n\n\"제 마누라가 조만큼—저 자신을 빼고요—이를 갈고 단단히 벼르는 인간은 따로 없을 겁니다, 선생.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n앨런이 까닭을 묻는다.\n\n\"까닭이요?\" 스내그스비 씨가, 절망에 빠진 채 벗어진 뒤통수의 머리카락 한 줌을 움켜쥐며 되뇐다. \"제가 까닭을 어찌 알겠습니까? 하지만 선생은 미혼이시니, 결혼한 사람한테 그런 질문을 던질 수 있는 그 처지가 부디 오래오래 이어지길 빕니다!\"\n\n이 자비로운 축원과 함께, 스내그스비 씨는 음울한 체념의 헛기침을 한 번 하고는, 방문객이 전할 말을 들으려 몸을 맡긴다.\n\n\"또 그거예요!\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. 그는 감정의 절절함과 억누른 목소리 사이에서 얼굴이 잿빛이 되었다. \"또 그거라니까요, 이번엔 새로운 방향에서! 어떤 사람이 더없이 엄숙하게, 조 얘기를 누구한테도, 심지어 제 마누라한테도 하지 말라고 못을 박습니다. 그러고는 또 다른 어떤 사람이, 바로 선생 모습으로 나타나, 똑같이 엄숙하게, 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 그 또 다른 어떤 사람한테는 절대로 조 얘기를 꺼내지 말라고 못을 박는군요. 글쎄, 이건 사설 정신병원이에요! 너무 까다롭게 따지자는 건 아닙니다만, 이건 정신병원이라고요, 선생!\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다.\n\n그래도 막상 일은 그가 예상한 것보다 낫다. 발밑의 지뢰가 터지는 것도, 자기가 빠진 구덩이가 더 깊어지는 것도 아니기 때문이다. 게다가 마음이 여린 그는 조의 처지에 대해 들은 이야기에 마음이 움직여, 되도록 조용히 짬을 낼 수 있는 대로 저녁에 일찍 \"들러보겠다\"고 선뜻 약속한다. 저녁이 오자 그는 아주 조용히 들른다. 다만 스내그스비 부인도 그 못지않게 조용히 일을 꾸미는 사람일지 모르는 노릇이다.\n\n조는 옛 친구를 보고 무척 반가워한다. 그러고는 둘만 남게 되자, 자기 같은 놈 때문에 스내그스비 씨가 이렇게 멀리까지 일부러 걸음을 해주시다니 더없이 고맙다고 말한다. 눈앞의 광경에 마음이 짠해진 스내그스비 씨는 즉시 식탁 위에 반 크라운을 내려놓는다. 온갖 상처에 듣는 그의 마법의 약이다.\n\n\"그래, 몸은 좀 어떠냐, 가엾은 녀석아?\" 문구상이 측은해하는 헛기침을 하며 묻는다.\n\n\"전 운이 좋아요, 스내그스비 씨, 정말요.\" 조가 대답한다. \"아쉬운 게 하나도 없어요. 생각도 못 하실 만큼 편안해요. 스내그스비 씨! 제가 그 일을 저질러서 정말 죄송해요. 하지만 일부러 그러려던 건 아니었어요, 나리.\"\n\n문구상이 가만히 반 크라운을 하나 더 내려놓고는, 저질러서 미안하다는 게 대체 무슨 일이냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"스내그스비 씨.\" 조가 말한다. \"제가 그 부인한테 병을 옮겼어요. 그 다른 부인인 듯하면서도 그 다른 부인은 아니었던 그 부인한테요. 근데 아무도 제가 그 짓을 했다고 저한테 뭐라 하질 않아요. 다들 어찌나 좋은 분들인지, 또 제가 어찌나 불쌍한 놈인지 그래서요. 그 부인이 어제 직접 찾아와서 절 봤어요. 그러고는 '아, 조!' 하고, '우린 널 잃은 줄 알았단다, 조!' 하더라고요. 그러고 어찌나 조용히 미소 지으며 앉던지, 제가 그 짓을 했다고 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번 안 주더라니까요, 정말이에요. 그래서 전 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠어요, 정말로요, 스내그스비 씨. 그리고 잔다이스 씨도, 자기도 모르게 고개를 돌리는 걸 봤어요. 우드코트 씨는 절 편하게 해주려고 뭘 주러 왔는데, 그분은 늘 밤낮으로 그러시거든요, 근데 제 위로 몸을 숙이고 그렇게 씩씩하게 말을 거는데, 눈물이 떨어지는 걸 봤어요, 스내그스비 씨.\"\n\n마음이 누그러진 문구상이 식탁에 반 크라운을 하나 더 내려놓는다. 그 어김없는 명약을 거듭 처방하는 것 말고는 그의 감정을 달랠 길이 없다.\n\n\"제가 생각한 건요, 스내그스비 씨.\" 조가 이어 말한다. \"혹시 글씨를 아주 크게 쓰실 수 있나, 하는 거였어요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 조, 하느님이 도우신다면야.\" 문구상이 대답한다.\n\n\"엄청나게, 굉장히 크게, 어쩌면요?\" 조가 간절하게 묻는다.\n\n\"그럼, 가엾은 녀석아.\"\n\n조가 기뻐서 웃는다. \"그러니까 제가 생각한 건요, 스내그스비 씨, 제가 갈 수 있는 데까지 다 비키며 가서 더는 비킬 수 없게 됐을 때, 혹시 친절을 베풀어서, 누구든 어디서든 볼 수 있게 아주 크게 써주실 수 있나, 하는 거였어요. 제가 그 짓을 한 걸 정말 진심으로 미안해하고, 일부러 그러려던 게 아니었다고요. 그리고 제가 아무것도 모르긴 해도, 우드코트 씨가 한번은 그 일로 울었고 늘 그 일로 가슴 아파했다는 건 알았다고요. 그리고 그분이 마음속으로 절 용서해주실 수 있길 바란다고요. 글씨로 그걸 아주 크게 써주실 수 있으면, 그분이 용서해주실지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"써주마, 조. 아주 크게.\"\n\n조가 다시 웃는다. \"고맙습니다, 스내그스비 씨. 참 친절하세요, 나리. 덕분에 전보다 더 편안해졌어요.\"\n\n온순한 자그마한 문구상은 끊기다 만 어설픈 헛기침과 함께, 네 번째 반 크라운을 슬쩍 내려놓는다—이토록 여러 개를 필요로 하는 경우와 마주한 적은 그로서도 처음이다—그러고는 떠나는 수밖에 없다. 그리고 조와 그는, 이 자그마한 지상에서, 두 번 다시 만나지 못하리라. 두 번 다시.\n\n끌기 그토록 힘든 그 수레가 여정의 끝에 다다라 돌투성이 길 위로 끌려가고 있기 때문이다. 하루 종일, 수레는 부서지고 닳은 채 망가진 계단을 힘겹게 기어오른다. 해가 떠올라 그 수레가 여전히 그 고단한 길 위에 있음을 보게 될 날도 이제 몇 번 남지 않았다.\n\n화약 연기에 그을린 얼굴의 필 스쿼드가, 한쪽 구석 자그마한 작업대에서 총포 수리공으로 일하는 동시에 간병인 노릇을 한다. 이따금 돌아보며 초록빛 방모 모자를 한 번 까딱이고 하나뿐인 눈썹을 격려하듯 치켜올리며 이렇게 말한다. \"기운 내, 얘야! 기운 내!\" 거기엔 잔다이스 씨도 여러 번 와 있고, 앨런 우드코트는 거의 늘 와 있는데, 둘 다 운명이 어쩌면 이토록 기이하게 이 거친 버림받은 아이를 전혀 다른 삶들의 그물 속에 얽어 넣었는지 깊이 곱씹는다. 거기엔 기병도 자주 들르는데, 떡 벌어진 몸으로 문간을 가득 채우고서, 넘쳐나는 생명과 힘으로 조에게 잠시나마 활기를 흘려보내는 듯하다. 그러면 조는 그 쾌활한 말에 어김없이 더 기운찬 목소리로 대답한다.\n\n오늘 조는 잠들었는지 아니면 혼수에 빠졌는지 모르겠고, 막 도착한 앨런 우드코트가 그 곁에 서서 야윈 몸을 내려다본다. 잠시 후 그는 가만히 침대 가에 앉아 조 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌린다—그 법률 필경사의 방에 앉았던 그 모습 그대로—그러고는 그의 가슴과 심장을 만져본다. 수레는 거의 멈출 뻔하다가, 조금 더 힘겹게 나아간다.\n\n기병이 문간에 가만히 말없이 서 있다. 필은 자그마한 망치를 손에 든 채, 낮게 딸각거리던 소리를 멈췄다. 우드코트 씨가 진지하고 직업적인 관심과 주의가 어린 얼굴로 둘러보더니, 기병에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던지고, 필에게 작업대를 들고 나가라고 손짓한다. 그 자그마한 망치를 다음에 쓰게 될 때면, 그 위에 녹이 한 점 슬어 있으리라.\n\n\"자, 조! 왜 그러느냐? 무서워 마라.\"\n\n\"제가요.\" 조가 화들짝 놀라 두리번거리며 말한다. \"톰 혼자 사는 곳에 다시 와 있는 줄 알았어요. 여긴 나리 말고는 아무도 없는 거지요, 우드코트 씨?\"\n\"아무도 없다.\"\n\n\"그리고 전 톰 혼자 사는 곳으로 다시 끌려가는 거 아니지요. 그렇지요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 조가 눈을 감으며 중얼거린다. \"정말 고맙습니다.\"\n\n잠시 그를 가까이서 지켜본 뒤, 앨런이 그의 귀에 입을 바싹 대고, 낮고 또렷한 목소리로 말한다. \"조! 너 기도 하나라도 알았던 적이 있느냐?\"\n\n\"아무것도 몰랐어요, 나리.\"\n\n\"짤막한 기도 하나조차도?\"\n\n\"네, 나리. 아무것도요. 한번은 채드밴드 씨가 스내그스비 씨네서 기도를 하기에 들었는데, 마치 저한테가 아니라 자기 혼자한테 말하는 것 같았어요. 기도를 한참 했는데, 무슨 소린지 통 알 수가 없었어요. 다른 때엔 다른 양반들이 톰 혼자 사는 곳에 기도하러 내려왔는데, 다들 거의 다른 양반들이 기도를 틀리게 한다고 하고, 다들 거의 자기들끼리 떠들거나 다른 양반들 탓을 하는 것 같았지, 우리한테 말하는 것 같진 않았어요. 우린 아무것도 몰랐어요. 전 그게 다 무슨 소린지 도통 몰랐어요.\"\n\n이 말을 하는 데 그는 한참이 걸리고, 노련하고 주의 깊은 청자가 아니고서는 그 말을 들을 수도, 듣는다 해도 알아들을 수도 없으리라. 잠시 다시 잠인지 혼수인지에 빠졌다가, 그는 느닷없이 침대에서 빠져나오려고 안간힘을 쓴다.\n\n\"가만있어라, 조! 이번엔 또 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"저 묘지로 갈 때가 됐어요, 나리.\" 그가 사나운 눈빛으로 대답한다.\n\n\"누워라, 그리고 말해보거라. 무슨 묘지 말이냐, 조?\"\n\n\"저한테 잘해준 사람을 묻은 데요, 저한테 정말 잘해줬어요, 그 사람이요. 저 묘지로 내려가서, 그 사람 곁에 묻어달라고 부탁할 때가 됐어요, 나리. 거기 가서 묻히고 싶어요. 그 사람은 저한테 늘 이러곤 했어요. '난 오늘 너만큼이나 가난하단다, 조.' 이제 저도 그 사람만큼 가난하고, 그 사람 곁에 묻히려고 거기 왔다고 말해주고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"머잖아, 조. 머잖아 그러자.\"\n\n\"아! 제가 직접 가면 그렇게 안 해줄지도 몰라요. 그래도 저를 거기 데려가서 그 사람 곁에 묻어주겠다고 약속해주실래요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그러마, 꼭.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리. 고맙습니다, 나리. 거긴 늘 잠겨 있어서, 절 들이려면 먼저 문 열쇠를 가져와야 할 거예요. 그리고 거기 계단이 하나 있는데, 제가 빗자루로 늘 쓸던 거예요. 무척 어두워졌어요, 나리. 무슨 빛이 오고 있나요?\"\n\n\"빠르게 오고 있다, 조.\"\n\n빠르게. 수레는 산산이 부서져 흔들리고, 험한 길은 이제 그 끝에 무척 가까워졌다.\n\n\"조, 가엾은 녀석아!\"\n\n\"어둠 속에서 나리 목소리가 들려요, 근데 전 더듬거리고 있어요—더듬거려요—나리 손을 잡게 해주세요.\"\n\n\"조, 내가 하는 말을 따라 할 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"나리가 하시는 말은 뭐든 따라 할게요, 좋은 거라는 걸 아니까요.\"\n\"하늘에 계신 우리 아버지.\"\n\n\"하늘에 계신 우리 아버지! 네, 그거 참 좋아요, 나리.\"\n\n\"하늘에 계신.\"\n\n\"하늘에 계시—오며—빛이 오고 있나요, 나리?\"\n\n\"바로 가까이 와 있다. 이름이 거룩히 여김을 받으시오며!\"\n\n\"이름이 거룩히—여김을—\"\n\n빛이 그 어둡고 캄캄한 길 위에 다다랐다. 죽었다!\n\n죽었습니다, 폐하. 죽었습니다, 경들과 신사 여러분. 죽었습니다, 온갖 직위의 옳은 성직자와 그른 성직자 여러분. 죽었습니다, 가슴에 천상의 연민을 품고 태어난 남녀 여러분. 그리고 날마다 우리 주변에서 이렇게 죽어갑니다.\n\n링컨셔의 저택은 다시 그 숱한 눈을 감았고, 도시의 저택은 깨어났다. 링컨셔에서는 지난날의 데드록 가문 사람들이 액자 속에서 졸고, 나직한 바람이 긴 응접실을 따라 웅얼거리니 마치 그들이 제법 규칙적으로 숨 쉬는 듯하다. 도시에서는 오늘날의 데드록 가문 사람들이 불 같은 눈을 단 마차를 타고 한밤의 어둠을 덜컹덜컹 가르며 달리고, 머리에 잿가루(아니면 분가루)를 뒤집어쓰고 한없는 겸손을 드러내는 데드록 가문의 헤르메스들은 현관의 작은 창가에서 졸린 아침나절을 늘어지게 흘려보낸다. 유행을 좇는 세계는—둘레가 거의 다섯 마일에 이르는 거대한 천체는—한창 돌아가고, 태양계는 정해진 거리에서 공손하게 운행한다.\n\n* 헤르메스: 그리스 신화의 전령 신. 여기서는 화려한 제복을 입은 데드록 가문의 하인들을 풍자적으로 가리킨다.\n무리가 가장 빽빽한 곳, 불빛이 가장 환한 곳, 모든 감각이 더없이 섬세하고 세련되게 시중받는 곳, 그곳에 데드록 마님이 있다. 마님은 자기가 기어올라 차지한 그 빛나는 정점에서 한 번도 자리를 비우지 않는다. 무엇이든 제 자존심의 망토 아래 감출 수 있는 사람이라며 오래도록 자신에게 품어온 믿음은 무너졌고, 주위 사람들에게 비치는 지금 모습이 하루만 더 지나도 그대로일지 장담할 수 없게 되었지만, 시샘하는 눈길이 지켜볼 때 굽히거나 처지는 것은 그녀의 천성에 없다. 사람들은 마님이 요즘 들어 더 아름다워지고 더 오만해졌다고 한다. 그 비실거리는 사촌은 마님을 두고 이렇게 말한다. 미모치곤—여자가게차릴—만하지만—좀—섬뜩한—데가있어—사람생각나게하는—거북한여자야—침대에서일어나—집안을발칵—뒤집어놓을—셰익스피어말이야.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 아무 표정도 짓지 않는다. 지금도 예전처럼 그는 방문간에 서서, 흐물거리는 흰 크라바트를 구식 매듭으로 느슨하게 비틀어 맨 채 귀족들의 후원을 받으면서도 아무런 내색을 하지 않는다. 모든 남자 가운데 마님에게 무슨 영향력이라도 가졌으리라 짐작할 수 있는 마지막 사람이 그다. 모든 여자 가운데 그를 조금이라도 두려워하리라 짐작할 수 있는 마지막 사람이 마님이다.\n\n체스니 월드의 첨탑 방에서 둘이 나눈 지난번 면담 이후로 마님의 마음을 줄곧 짓누르는 일이 하나 있다. 마님은 이제 결심했고, 그 일을 떨쳐낼 채비를 마쳤다.\n\n큰 세계에서는 아침이지만, 작은 태양으로 치자면 오후다. 헤르메스들은 창밖을 내다보느라 지친 나머지 현관에서 쉬며 그 화려한 몸뚱이의 무거운 머리를 떨군 모습이, 활짝 피었다 시든 해바라기 같다. 또 그들처럼, 술과 장식 갓끈에 씨앗이 잔뜩 맺힌 것처럼도 보인다. 서재에서는 레스터 경이 나라를 위한답시고 어느 의회 위원회 보고서를 읽다가 잠들었다. 마님은 거피라는 이름의 그 젊은이를 접견했던 바로 그 방에 앉아 있다. 로자가 곁에 있어 마님 대신 글을 적어주고 책을 읽어주었다. 로자는 지금 자수인지 그 비슷한 고운 일감에 매달려 있고, 그녀가 고개를 숙여 그 위로 손을 놀리는 사이 마님은 말없이 지켜본다. 오늘 처음 있는 일이 아니다.\n\n\"로자.\"\n\n그 어여쁜 시골 처녀의 얼굴이 환하게 위를 올려다본다. 그러다가 마님이 얼마나 심각한지 알아채고는 어리둥절하고 놀란 표정이 된다.\n\n\"문을 살펴보아라. 닫혀 있느냐?\"\n\n네. 로자가 문가로 갔다가 돌아오는데, 더욱 놀란 기색이다.\n\n\"내 너에게 속을 털어놓으려 한다, 얘야. 네 판단력은 몰라도 네 충심만은 믿을 수 있음을 알기 때문이다. 내가 이제 하려는 일에서, 적어도 너에게만은 나를 감추지 않겠다. 하지만 너를 믿고 하는 말이다. 우리 둘 사이에 오가는 이야기는 누구에게도 발설하지 마라.\"\n\n겁 많은 작은 미인은 더없이 진지하게 비밀을 지키겠노라 약속한다.\n\n\"너는 아느냐,\" 데드록 마님이 의자를 더 가까이 가져오라고 손짓하며 묻는다. \"로자, 너는 내가 다른 누구를 대할 때와 너를 대할 때가 다르다는 걸 아느냐?\"\n\n\"네, 마님. 훨씬 다정하시지요. 하지만 저는 마님의 참모습을 안다고 자주 생각하곤 해요.\"\n\n\"내 참모습을 안다고 자주 생각한다? 가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!\"\n\n마님은 일종의 경멸을 담아 이 말을 내뱉지만—로자를 향한 것은 아니다—그러고는 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 꿈결처럼 그녀를 바라보며 앉아 있다.\n\n\"로자, 너는 네가 나에게 위안이나 위로가 된다고 생각하느냐? 네가 젊고 꾸밈없으며 나를 따르고 고마워하는 것이, 너를 곁에 두는 일을 나에게 조금이라도 즐거운 일로 만든다고 여기느냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요, 마님. 감히 그러기를 바랄 수도 없고요. 하지만 진심으로, 그랬으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"그렇단다, 얘야.\"\n\n어여쁜 얼굴이 기쁨에 발그레 물들려다가, 눈앞의 그 아름다운 얼굴에 어린 어두운 표정에 가로막힌다. 그 얼굴이 겁먹은 듯 설명을 구한다.\n\n\"그런데 내가 오늘 너에게 '가거라! 나를 떠나라!' 한다면, 그것은 나에게 큰 아픔과 불안을 안기는 말일 테고, 나를 무척 외롭게 만드는 말일 테다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"마님! 제가 마님께 잘못을 저질렀나요?\"\n\n\"전혀 아니란다. 이리 오너라.\"\n\n로자가 마님 발치의 발받침 위로 몸을 굽힌다. 마님은 그날 밤 그 이름난 철물 제조업자가 보여준 어머니 같은 손길로 로자의 검은 머리에 손을 얹고는 가만히 그대로 둔다.\n\n\"로자, 내 너에게 네가 행복하기를 바라며, 이 세상에서 누군가 하나라도 행복하게 해줄 수 있다면 너를 그렇게 해주겠다고 했지. 그런데 그럴 수가 없구나. 지금 내가 아는 어떤 사정들이 있는데, 네가 끼어들 일은 전혀 없는 그 사정들 때문에 네가 여기 머물지 않는 편이 너에게 훨씬 낫다. 너는 여기 머물러서는 안 된다. 그러지 않기로 내가 정했다. 네 연인의 아버지에게 편지를 보냈고, 그분이 오늘 이리로 올 게다. 이 모든 걸 너를 위해 했다.\"\n\n우는 처녀가 마님의 손에 입맞춤을 퍼부으며, 헤어지면 자기는 어찌해야 하나, 어찌해야 하나 한다! 안주인은 그녀의 뺨에 입을 맞출 뿐 다른 대답은 하지 않는다.\n\n\"자, 행복해지거라, 얘야. 더 나은 처지에서. 사랑받고 행복하거라!\"\n\n\"아, 마님, 저는 가끔 이런 생각을 했어요—이렇게 함부로 말하는 걸 용서해 주세요—마님께서 행복하지 않으신 게 아닐까 하고요.\"\n\n\"내가!\"\n\"저를 보내고 나면 더 행복해지실까요? 부디, 부디 다시 생각해 주세요. 조금만 더 머물게 해주세요!\"\n\n\"내 말했지, 얘야. 내가 하는 일은 나 자신이 아니라 너를 위해 하는 것이라고. 이미 정해진 일이다. 로자, 너를 향한 내 마음은 지금 이대로의 나란다—조금만 지나면 어찌 될지 모를 그런 나가 아니라. 이것을 기억하고, 내 비밀을 지켜다오. 그만큼만 나를 위해 해주면, 우리 둘 사이는 이로써 다 끝나는 것이다!\"\n\n마님은 천진한 동무에게서 몸을 떼고 방을 나선다. 오후도 저물 무렵, 마님이 다시 계단에 모습을 드러낼 때 그녀는 더없이 오만하고 차가운 상태다. 마치 온갖 정념과 감정과 관심이 세상의 더 이른 시대에 다 닳아 없어져 다른 사라진 괴물들과 함께 지표면에서 자취를 감추기라도 한 듯, 무심하기 그지없다.\n\n헤르메스가 라운스웰 씨의 도착을 알렸으니, 그것이 마님이 모습을 드러낸 까닭이다. 라운스웰 씨가 서재에 있는 것은 아니지만, 마님은 서재로 향한다. 거기에는 레스터 경이 있고, 마님은 그에게 먼저 이야기하고 싶다.\n\n\"레스터 경, 제가 청이 있는데—한데 손님이 계시네요.\"\n\n오, 천만에! 전혀 아니오. 토킹혼 씨뿐이라오.\n\n언제나 가까이 있다. 어느 자리에나 망령처럼 따라붙는다. 한순간도 그에게서 벗어나거나 안심할 수 없다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 데드록 마님. 제가 물러가도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"마음만 먹으면 남아 있을 힘이 당신에게 있다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소\" 하고 또렷이 말하는 듯한 눈길로, 마님은 그럴 필요 없다고 이르고는 의자 쪽으로 걸음을 옮긴다. 토킹혼 씨가 어설픈 인사와 함께 의자를 그녀 앞으로 조금 끌어다 놓고는 맞은편 창가로 물러난다. 이제 고요해진 거리에 사위어가는 한낮의 빛과 그녀 사이를 가로막고 선 그의 그림자가 그녀 위로 드리워, 그가 그녀 앞의 모든 것을 어둡게 한다. 꼭 그렇게 그는 그녀의 삶을 어둡게 한다.\n\n아무리 좋은 조건에서도 음울한 거리다. 길게 늘어선 두 줄의 집들이 어찌나 매섭게 서로를 노려보는지, 그중 가장 큰 저택 대여섯 채는 본래 돌로 지어졌다기보다 노려보임을 당하다가 서서히 돌로 굳어버린 듯하다. 어찌나 음산하게 웅장한 거리인지, 활기 따위에 굽힐 생각이 도무지 없어서 문과 창은 검은 칠과 먼지로 제 나름의 침울한 위세를 지키고, 뒤편의 메아리치는 마구간 골목은 메마르고 묵직한 모양새가 마치 고귀한 동상들의 돌 군마를 매어둘 자리로 남겨둔 듯하다. 이 끔찍한 거리에서는 정교하게 장식한 철세공이 층계마다 휘감겨 있고, 이 돌로 굳은 정자에서 한물간 횃불용 소화기들이 벼락출세한 가스등을 보고 입을 떡 벌린다. 군데군데 약하고 작은 쇠고리가 녹슨 잎사귀 장식 사이에 자리를 지키며 사라진 기름불의 기억을 모시고 있는데, 대담한 사내아이들이 친구 모자를 던져 넣으려 애쓰는 것이 그것의 유일한 지금 쓰임새다. 아니, 기름불 자체조차 긴 간격을 두고 군데군데, 바닥에 굴 껍데기 같은 혹이 달린 우스꽝스러운 작은 유리 단지에 머뭇거리며 남아, 밤마다 더 새로운 불빛들을 향해 눈을 깜빡이며 부루퉁한 꼴이, 마치 상원에 들어앉은 한물가고 메마른 제 주인을 닮았다.\n\n그러니 의자에 앉은 데드록 마님이 토킹혼 씨가 서 있는 그 창 너머로 보고 싶어 할 만한 것은 별로 없다. 그런데도—그런데도—마님은 저 인물이 길에서 비켜나기를 마음 깊이 바라기라도 하듯 그쪽으로 눈길을 보낸다.\n\n레스터 경이 마님에게 양해를 구한다. 무슨 말을 하려던 참이었소?\n\n\"다름이 아니라 라운스웰 씨가 와 있어요(제가 약속해서 찾아온 거예요). 그 처녀 문제는 이쯤에서 마무리 짓는 게 좋겠어요. 그 일이라면 진절머리가 나도록 지쳤거든요.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 하면—그—도움이 되겠소?\" 레스터 경이 적잖이 미심쩍어하며 묻는다.\n\n\"그를 이리로 들여서 이 일을 끝내버려요. 그를 올려 보내라고 일러주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"토킹혼 씨, 미안하지만 종을 좀 울려주시오. 고맙소. 그—\" 레스터 경이 헤르메스에게 일렀으나 사무적인 호칭이 얼른 떠오르지 않아, \"그 철물 양반더러 이리로 오시라 청하게.\"\n\n헤르메스가 철물 양반을 찾으러 떠나, 그를 찾아내 데려온다. 레스터 경이 그 무쇠 같은 인물을 정중히 맞이한다.\n\n\"강녕하시기 바랍니다, 라운스웰 씨. 앉으시지요. (내 법률 고문, 토킹혼 씨요.) 마님께서, 라운스웰 씨,\" 레스터 경이 한 손을 엄숙하게 내저으며 능숙하게 그를 마님에게 넘긴다. \"당신과 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하셨소. 험!\"\n\n\"데드록 마님께서 황송하게도 말씀하시는 것이라면 무엇이든 정성을 다해 귀 기울이겠습니다.\" 철물 양반이 대답한다.\n\n마님 쪽으로 돌아서며 그는, 마님이 자기에게 주는 인상이 지난번보다 덜 유쾌함을 느낀다. 거리감 있고 거만한 분위기가 그녀 둘레에 싸늘한 기운을 빚어내고, 지난번과 달리 그녀의 거동에는 솔직함을 북돋울 만한 구석이 전혀 없다.\n\n\"여쭙겠는데요, 선생,\" 데드록 마님이 맥없이 말한다. \"선생과 아드님 사이에 아드님의 그 연정에 관해 무슨 이야기라도 오갔는지 물어봐도 될까요?\"\n\n이 질문을 던지며 그에게 눈길 한 번 주는 것조차 그녀의 나른한 눈에는 너무 성가신 일이다.\n\n\"제 기억이 옳다면, 데드록 마님, 지난번 마님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때 제가 말씀드렸지요. 아들에게 그—연정을 이겨내라고 진지하게 충고하겠노라고요.\" 철물 제조업자가 그녀의 표현을 살짝 힘주어 되풀이한다.\n\n\"그래서 그러셨나요?\"\n\n\"오! 물론 그랬지요.\"\n\n레스터 경이 흡족해하며 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕인다. 아주 마땅한 일이다. 철물 양반은 그러겠다고 말했으니 그럴 의무가 있다. 이 점에서는 천한 금속과 귀한 금속 사이에 차이가 없다. 지극히 마땅한 일이다.\n\n\"그래서 아드님이 그리하던가요?\"\n\n\"솔직히, 데드록 마님, 확실한 대답을 드릴 수가 없습니다. 안 그런 듯합니다. 아마 아직은 아니지요. 저희 같은 처지의 사람들은 가끔 우리—우리 연정에 어떤 결심을 결부하는데, 그러면 좀처럼 떨쳐내기가 쉽지 않습니다. 진지하게 구는 것이 저희 방식인가 봅니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 이 표현에 왓 타일러 같은 불순한 속뜻이 숨어 있을지 모른다는 의구심에 약간 부아가 끓는다. 라운스웰 씨는 더없이 쾌활하고 정중하지만, 그런 한도 안에서 분명히 자기를 대하는 태도에 맞춰 어조를 조절한다.\n* 왓 타일러: 1381년 잉글랜드 농민 봉기를 이끈 인물. 레스터 경은 라운스웰의 말에서 신분 질서에 도전하는 평민적 기운을 의심한다.\n\n\"그게 말이죠,\" 마님이 말을 잇는다. \"제가 이 문제를 줄곧 생각해 왔는데, 저로선 지긋지긋한 일이거든요.\"\n\n\"진심으로 송구스럽습니다.\"\n\n\"또 레스터 경이 이 일에 대해 하신 말씀도 생각했고, 저도 전적으로 같은 뜻이에요\"—레스터 경이 우쭐해한다—\"그래서 만일 선생이 이 연정이 끝났다고 장담해 주실 수 없다면, 저는 그 처녀가 제 곁을 떠나는 편이 낫다는 결론에 이르렀어요.\"\n\n\"그런 장담은 드릴 수가 없습니다, 데드록 마님. 그 비슷한 것조차요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 애가 떠나는 게 낫겠군요.\"\n\n\"실례합니다, 마님,\" 레스터 경이 사려 깊게 끼어든다. \"하지만 어쩌면 이것이 그 젊은 여인에게 마땅찮은 해를 입히는 일이 될지도 모르오. 여기 한 젊은 여인이 있소,\" 레스터 경이 한 벌의 은식기라도 펼쳐 보이듯 오른손으로 그 사정을 장엄하게 펼친다. \"어느 고귀한 부인의 눈에 들어 그 총애를 받는 행운을 누리고, 그 고귀한 부인의 보호 아래 그런 지위가 베푸는 갖가지 혜택에 둘러싸여 사는 여인 말이오. 그 혜택이란 의심할 바 없이 대단한 것이오—내 믿건대 의심할 바 없이 대단한 것이오, 선생—그런 신분의 젊은 여인에게는 말이오. 그러면 이런 의문이 생기오. 그저 그 여인이 라운스웰 씨 아드님의—\" 레스터 경이 철물 제조업자를 향해 미안하다는 듯하면서도 위엄 있게 고개를 기울이며 문장을 마무리한다. \"—눈에 들었다는 이유만으로 이 숱한 혜택과 행운을 빼앗아야 하겠소? 한데, 그 여인이 이런 벌을 받아 마땅하오? 이것이 그 여인에게 공정하오? 이것이 우리가 앞서 합의한 바이오?\"\n\n\"송구합니다만,\" 라운스웰 씨 아드님의 아버지가 끼어든다. \"레스터 경, 한 말씀 드려도 되겠습니까? 제가 이 이야기를 간추려도 될 듯합니다. 부디 그건 고려에서 빼주십시오. 그토록 사소한 것을 기억하신다면—기억하시리라 기대할 수도 없지만—이 일에 대한 저의 첫 생각이 그 애가 여기 머무는 것에 곧장 반대하는 쪽이었음을 떠올리실 겁니다.\"\n\n데드록 가문의 후원을 고려에서 뺀다? 오! 레스터 경은 그런 가문을 통해 자기에게 물려 내려온 두 귀를 믿지 않을 수 없으니, 그렇지 않았다면 철물 양반이 한 말을 그 귀가 전한 대로 정말이지 의심했을지도 모른다.\n\n\"이런 문제들은 어느 쪽에서도 굳이 파고들 필요가 없어요.\" 그가 놀라 숨이나 겨우 내쉴 뿐 아무것도 못 하는 사이, 마님이 더없이 차가운 태도로 말한다. \"그 애는 아주 착한 애예요. 저로선 그 애를 두고 나무랄 게 조금도 없어요. 다만 그 애가 자기에게 주어진 숱한 혜택과 행운을 도무지 깨닫지 못한 채 사랑에 빠져 있다는—아니, 빠졌다고 여기는 거겠지요, 가엾고 어리석은 것—그래서 그것들을 고마워할 줄 모른다는 게 문제일 뿐이에요.\"\n\n레스터 경은 그렇다면 사정이 완전히 달라진다고 한마디 보탠다. 마님이 자기 견해를 뒷받침할 더없이 훌륭한 근거와 이유를 갖추셨으리라 짐작했어야 옳았다. 그는 마님과 전적으로 뜻을 같이한다. 그 젊은 여인은 떠나는 편이 낫겠다.\n\n\"지난번 우리가 이 일로 지쳐 있을 때 레스터 경이 말씀하셨듯이, 라운스웰 씨,\" 데드록 마님이 나른하게 말을 잇는다. \"저희는 선생과 조건을 내걸고 흥정할 수가 없어요. 조건 없이, 지금 형편으로 보아 그 애는 여기 도무지 어울리지 않으니 떠나는 게 낫겠어요. 그 애에게도 그리 일러두었고요. 그 애를 마을로 돌려보내기를 원하시나요, 아니면 데려가고 싶으신가요, 어느 쪽이 좋으시겠어요?\"\n\n\"데드록 마님, 솔직히 말씀드려도 된다면—\"\n\n\"얼마든지요.\"\n\n\"—저는 마님께서 그 짐을 가장 빨리 더시고, 또 그 애를 지금 처지에서 가장 빨리 벗어나게 할 방도를 택하고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도 솔직히 말하자면,\" 마님이 똑같이 꾸며낸 무심함으로 받는다. \"저 역시 그래요. 그 애를 데려가시겠다는 말씀으로 알아들으면 되겠지요?\"\n\n철물 양반이 무쇠 같은 인사를 한다.\n\n\"레스터 경, 종을 울려주시겠어요?\" 토킹혼 씨가 창가에서 앞으로 나서며 종을 당긴다. \"당신을 깜빡했군요. 고맙습니다.\" 그가 늘 하던 인사를 하고는 조용히 다시 물러난다. 헤르메스가 잽싸게 응답하여 나타나, 누구를 데려올지 지시받고는 쏜살같이 가서 앞서 말한 그 처녀를 데려온 뒤 떠난다.\n\n로자는 울고 있었고 지금도 마음이 괴롭다. 그녀가 들어서자 철물 제조업자가 의자에서 일어나 그녀의 팔을 끼고는, 떠날 채비를 한 채 문 가까이에서 그녀와 함께 선다.\n\n\"보다시피 네게는 돌봐줄 사람이 있고,\" 마님이 지친 태도로 말한다. \"넌 든든히 보호받으며 떠나는 거란다. 네가 아주 착한 애라고 말해 두었으니, 울 일은 하나도 없다.\"\n\n\"그런데도 이 아인 떠나는 게 슬퍼서 우는 것 같군요.\" 토킹혼 씨가 뒷짐을 진 채 조금 앞으로 어슬렁거리며 한마디 한다.\n\n\"뭐, 보시다시피 이 애가 가정교육을 제대로 못 받았고,\" 라운스웰 씨가 변호사를 받아칠 상대가 생겨 반가운 듯 다소 날쌘 태도로 응수한다. \"세상 물정 모르는 어린것이라 더 나은 처신을 모르는 거지요. 여기 남았더라면 분명 나아졌을 텐데요, 선생.\"\n\n\"분명 그랬겠지요.\" 토킹혼 씨가 침착하게 받는다.\n\n로자가 흐느끼며, 마님 곁을 떠나는 게 너무도 슬프고, 체스니 월드에서 행복했으며 마님과 함께 있어 행복했다고, 마님께 거듭거듭 감사드린다고 한다. \"이런, 어리석은 새끼 고양이 같으니!\" 철물 제조업자가 낮은 소리로, 그러나 성내지 않고 그녀를 다독인다. \"왓을 사랑한다면, 기개를 좀 가져야지!\" 마님은 그저 무심하게 그녀를 물리며 말한다. \"됐다, 됐어, 얘야! 넌 착한 애다. 가거라!\" 레스터 경은 이 문제에서 장엄하게 손을 떼고 제 푸른 외투의 성소로 물러나 있다. 이제 등불이 점점이 박힌 어두운 거리를 배경으로 흐릿한 윤곽이 된 토킹혼 씨가, 전보다 더 크고 더 검게 마님의 시야에 우뚝 솟는다.\n\n\"레스터 경, 데드록 마님,\" 라운스웰 씨가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말한다. \"이제 작별을 고하고자 합니다. 제 뜻으로 한 일은 아닙니다만, 이 성가신 문제로 또 한 번 폐를 끼쳐 송구합니다. 이토록 사소한 일이 데드록 마님께 얼마나 진절머리 나는 일이 되었을지, 정말이지 잘 헤아립니다. 제가 이 일을 다룬 방식에 미심쩍은 데가 있다면, 그건 다만 제가 처음부터 두 분께 폐를 끼치지 않고 조용히 제 영향력을 써서 여기 이 어린 벗을 데려가지 않았기 때문일 뿐입니다. 하지만 제게는—아마 일의 무게를 과장하는 거겠지요—두 분께 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 설명드리는 것이 예의이고, 두 분의 뜻과 편의를 여쭙는 것이 도리로 여겨졌습니다. 사교계 예법에 어두운 점을 부디 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 이 말에 성소에서 불려 나왔다고 느낀다. \"라운스웰 씨,\" 그가 답한다. \"그런 말씀 마시오. 변명은 어느 쪽에서도 필요 없으리라 보오.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 기쁩니다, 레스터 경. 끝으로 한 말씀만 더 드리자면, 앞서 제 어머니가 이 가문과 오래 인연을 맺어온 일과 그것이 양쪽 모두의 값어치를 말해 준다고 했던 그 이야기로 돌아가, 제 팔에 매달린 이 작은 본보기를 가리켜 보이고 싶습니다. 이 애는 헤어지는 마당에 이토록 정 깊고 한결같은 모습을 보이는데, 제 어머니가 이런 마음을 일깨우는 데 얼마간 거들었으리라 짐작합니다—물론 데드록 마님께서 진심 어린 관심과 자애로운 베풂으로 훨씬 더 많이 거드셨겠지만요.\"\n\n그가 이 말을 비꼬아 한 것이라면, 본인 생각보다 더 사실에 가까울지 모른다. 그러나 그는 거침없는 말투에서 조금도 벗어남 없이 이 말을 가리키니, 다만 이 말을 하면서 그 어둑한 방에서 마님이 앉아 있는 쪽으로 몸을 돌릴 뿐이다. 레스터 경이 그의 작별 인사에 답하려 일어서고, 토킹혼 씨가 다시 종을 울리고, 헤르메스가 또 한 번 날아가고, 라운스웰 씨와 로자는 저택을 떠난다.\n\n그러자 등불이 들어오는데, 토킹혼 씨는 여전히 뒷짐을 진 채 창가에 서 있고 마님은 여전히 제 앞에 선 그의 윤곽이 밤도 낮도 가려버린 자리에 그대로 앉아 있다. 마님은 무척 창백하다. 토킹혼 씨가 마님이 물러가려고 일어서는 것을 보며 이렇게 생각한다. \"그럴 만도 하지! 이 여자의 힘은 놀랍군. 줄곧 한 편의 연기를 펼쳐온 거야.\" 하지만 그 역시 연기를 할 줄 안다—한 번도 변치 않는 그 한 가지 배역을—그래서 그가 이 여자를 위해 문을 잡아주는 동안, 레스터 경의 눈보다 저마다 쉰 배는 더 매서운 쉰 쌍의 눈이 들여다본대도 그에게서 어떤 흠도 찾지 못할 것이다.\n\n데드록 마님은 오늘 자기 방에서 홀로 저녁을 든다. 레스터 경은 두들 당을 구하고 쿠들 파를 무너뜨리는 자리로 불려 나갔다. 마님은 여전히 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴로(비실거리는 사촌의 그 표현을 그대로 그려낸 채) 저녁상에 앉으며, 그가 외출했는지 묻는다. 네. 토킹혼 씨는 아직 갔는지 묻는다. 아니요. 잠시 뒤 다시 묻는다, 그가 아직도 안 갔느냐? 아니요. 무얼 하고 있느냐? 서재에서 편지를 쓰고 있는 것 같다고 헤르메스가 답한다. 마님께서 그를 만나기를 원하시는지? 그것만은 절대 아니다.\n* 두들 당과 쿠들 파: 디킨스가 만들어낸 가상의 정파 이름으로, 거기서 거기인 영국 양대 정당을 풍자한다.\n\n그러나 그가 마님을 뵙고자 한다. 몇 분 더 지나자, 그가 정중히 안부를 전하며 저녁 식사 뒤에 마님이 한두 마디 나누는 자리를 허락해 주실 수 있는지 묻는다는 전갈이 온다. 마님은 지금 그를 맞겠다고 한다. 그가 들어오며, 마님이 허락했다 해도 식사 중에 끼어들어 죄송하다고 사죄한다. 단둘이 되자 마님이 그런 입에 발린 인사 따위는 그만두라고 손을 내젓는다.\n\n\"무슨 일이시죠, 선생?\"\n\n\"그게, 데드록 마님,\" 변호사가 그녀에게서 조금 떨어진 자리에 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 녹슨 다리를 위아래로, 위아래로, 위아래로 천천히 문지른다. \"마님이 취하신 처사에 좀 놀랐습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"네, 정말로요. 미처 예상치 못한 일이었거든요. 저는 그것이 우리 합의와 마님의 약속에서 벗어난 것이라고 봅니다. 그것은 우리를 새로운 처지에 놓는 일입니다, 데드록 마님. 저로서는 그것을 찬성할 수 없다고 말씀드리지 않을 수 없군요.\"\n\n그가 문지르던 손을 멈추고 무릎에 손을 얹은 채 그녀를 바라본다. 흔들림 없고 변함없는 사람이지만, 그의 태도에는 여전히 뭐라 꼬집을 수 없는 거리낌 없음이 새로이 배어 있고, 그것이 이 여자의 관찰을 비껴가지 못한다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 잘 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니, 아실 텐데요. 아시리라 봅니다. 자, 자, 데드록 마님, 이제 칼을 막고 찌르는 시늉은 그만둡시다. 마님이 이 처녀를 아끼시는 걸 마님도 아시지요.\"\n\n\"그래서요, 선생?\"\n\n\"그리고 마님도—저도—압니다. 마님이 그 애를 둘러대신 이유 때문이 아니라, 마님 자신에게 닥쳐오는 어떤 비난과 폭로로부터—사무적인 일로 이런 말씀을 드리는 걸 용서하십시오—그 애를 되도록 멀리 떼어놓으려는 속셈으로 그 애를 보내셨다는 것을요.\"\n\n\"그래서요, 선생?\"\n\n\"그게 말이죠, 데드록 마님,\" 변호사가 다리를 꼬고 위에 얹힌 무릎을 감싸 쥐며 받는다. \"저는 그것에 반대합니다. 저는 그것을 위험한 처사로 봅니다. 그것은 불필요하며, 이 집안에 억측과 의심과 소문, 그밖에 뭐가 될지 모를 것들을 불러일으키기 십상이라는 걸 압니다. 게다가 그것은 우리 합의를 어기는 일입니다. 마님은 예전과 똑같으셔야 했습니다. 그런데 마님 자신에게도, 저에게도 분명하듯이, 오늘 저녁 마님은 예전과는 무척 달랐습니다. 아니, 맙소사, 데드록 마님, 빤히 들여다보이게 그러셨지요!\"\n\n\"선생, 만일,\" 그녀가 입을 연다. \"제 비밀을 알고 있는 제가—\" 그러나 그가 그녀의 말을 끊는다.\n\n\"자, 데드록 마님, 이건 사무적인 일이고, 사무적인 일에서는 바탕을 더없이 깨끗이 해두어야 합니다. 그것은 더 이상 마님의 비밀이 아닙니다. 용서하십시오. 바로 그게 잘못 짚으신 겁니다. 그것은 제 비밀입니다, 레스터 경과 이 가문을 위해 맡아둔 것이지요. 만일 그것이 마님의 비밀이었다면, 데드록 마님, 우리가 여기서 이런 대화를 나누고 있지는 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"지당한 말이에요. 그 비밀을 아는 제가, 죄 없는 처녀 하나를 제게 닥쳐올 치욕의 오점에서 지켜주려고 할 수 있는 일을 한다면(더구나 체스니 월드에 모인 손님들에게 당신이 제 이야기를 들려줄 때 그 애를 언급했던 걸 떠올리면 더욱), 그건 제가 굳힌 결심에 따라 하는 거예요. 이 세상 어떤 것도, 이 세상 누구도 그것을 흔들거나 저를 움직이지 못해요.\" 마님은 그 못지않게 겉으로 아무런 정념도 드러내지 않은 채, 신중하고 또렷하게 이 말을 한다. 그로 말하자면, 그녀가 사무에 쓰이는 무슨 감각 없는 도구라도 되는 양 제 사무를 차근차근 논한다.\n\n\"정말입니까? 그렇다면 보십시오, 데드록 마님,\" 그가 받는다. \"마님은 믿을 수가 없는 분이군요. 마님은 사정을 더없이 명료하게, 글자 그대로의 사실대로 말씀하셨고, 그렇다면 마님은 믿을 수가 없는 분입니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 밤에 체스니 월드에서 이야기 나눌 때, 제가 바로 이 점에 대해 얼마간 걱정을 비쳤던 걸 기억하실는지요?\"\n\n\"네,\" 토킹혼 씨가 냉정하게 일어나 벽난로 앞에 서며 말한다. \"네. 기억합니다, 데드록 마님. 마님이 분명 그 처녀를 언급하셨지요. 하지만 그건 우리가 협정에 이르기 전이었고, 우리 협정의 글자와 정신은 제 발견에 기대어 마님이 무슨 행동을 하는 것을 죄다 막아두었습니다. 그건 의심할 여지가 없지요. 그 처녀를 지켜준다는 것은—그 애가 무슨 대수이고 무슨 값어치가 있단 말입니까? 지켜준다고요! 데드록 마님, 여기 한 가문의 이름이 위태로워졌습니다. 그렇다면 길은 곧장—모든 것을 짓밟으며, 오른쪽으로도 왼쪽으로도 비끼지 않고, 앞을 가로막는 온갖 사정 따위는 아랑곳없이, 아무것도 봐주지 않고, 모든 것을 발밑에 짓이기며 나아가는 거라고 봐야 마땅했지요.\"\n\n그녀는 식탁을 바라보고 있었다. 그녀가 눈을 들어 그를 바라본다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 준엄한 표정이 어려 있고, 아랫입술 한쪽이 이 아래에 깨물려 있다. \"이 여자가 내 말을 알아듣는군.\" 그녀가 다시 눈길을 떨구는 사이 토킹혼 씨가 생각한다. \"이 여자는 봐줄 수가 없어. 그러니 왜 이 여자가 남을 봐줘야 하나?\"\n\n잠시 둘은 말이 없다. 데드록 마님은 저녁을 한 술도 들지 않았지만, 흔들림 없는 손으로 두세 번 물을 따라 마셨다. 마님이 식탁에서 일어나 안락의자를 끌어다 거기 기대앉아 얼굴을 가린다. 그녀의 태도에는 나약함을 드러내거나 동정을 자아낼 만한 구석이 전혀 없다. 골똘하고 침울하며 한곳에 모여 있다. \"이 여자는,\" 다시 그녀의 시야를 가로막는 어두운 물체가 되어 벽난로 앞에 선 토킹혼 씨가 생각한다. \"하나의 연구 대상이로군.\"\n\n그는 한동안 말없이 여유롭게 그녀를 연구한다. 그녀 역시 여유롭게 무언가를 연구한다. 그녀는 좀처럼 먼저 입을 열 사람으로 보이지 않으니, 그가 자정까지 거기 서 있는다 해도 그러지 않을 듯하여, 결국 그조차 침묵을 깨지 않을 수 없게 된다.\n\n\"데드록 마님, 이 사무적 면담에서 가장 거북한 대목이 남아 있습니다만, 어쨌든 사무지요. 우리 합의는 깨졌습니다. 마님 같은 분별과 강단을 지닌 분이라면, 제가 이제 그것을 무효라 선언하고 제 나름의 길을 가더라도 마음의 준비가 되어 있으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"충분히 되어 있어요.\"\n\n토킹혼 씨가 고개를 숙인다. \"이것이 제가 마님께 끼칠 폐의 전부입니다, 데드록 마님.\"\n\n그가 방을 나서려는 참에 그녀가 그를 멈춰 세우며 묻는다. \"이것이 제가 받기로 한 그 통고인가요? 잘못 알아듣고 싶지 않아서요.\"\n\n\"마님이 받기로 한 그 통고와 꼭 같지는 않습니다, 데드록 마님. 본디 예정했던 통고는 합의가 지켜졌다는 전제 위에 있었으니까요. 하지만 사실상 같은 것입니다, 사실상 같지요. 차이라야 한낱 변호사의 머릿속에나 있을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"제게 달리 통고는 하지 않으실 생각인가요?\"\n\n\"옳게 보셨습니다. 안 합니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 레스터 경의 눈을 뜨게 하실 작정인가요?\"\n\n\"정곡을 찌르는 질문이군요!\" 토킹혼 씨가 살짝 웃으며, 가려진 얼굴을 향해 조심스레 고개를 가로젓는다. \"아니요, 오늘 밤은 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"내일은요?\"\n\n\"이것저것 따져보면, 그 질문에는 대답하지 않는 편이 낫겠습니다, 데드록 마님. 제가 정확히 언제인지 모른다고 말하면 마님은 믿지 않으실 테고, 그래봐야 아무 소용도 없을 테니까요. 내일이 될 수도 있지요. 그 이상은 말씀드리지 않으렵니다. 마님은 마음의 준비가 되어 있으시고, 저는 형편이 받쳐주지 못할 어떤 기대도 내비치지 않겠습니다. 안녕히 주무십시오.\"\n\n그녀가 손을 치우고 창백한 얼굴을 그에게로 돌리는 사이 그가 말없이 문 쪽으로 걸어가고, 그가 막 문을 열려는 참에 그녀가 다시 한번 그를 멈춰 세운다.\n\n\"이 집에 한동안 더 머무실 작정인가요? 서재에서 글을 쓰고 계셨다고 들었어요. 거기로 돌아가실 건가요?\"\n\n\"모자를 가지러 갈 뿐입니다. 저는 집으로 갑니다.\"\n\n그녀가 고개라기보다 눈을 숙이니, 그 움직임이 어찌나 미미하고 야릇한지, 그가 물러난다. 방을 벗어나 그가 회중시계를 들여다보지만, 일 분쯤 틀린 게 아닌가 의심한다. 계단에는 훌륭한 시계가 하나 있는데, 훌륭한 시계치고는 드물게도 정확하기로 이름났다. \"그래 너는 뭐라 하느냐,\" 토킹혼 씨가 그 시계를 두고 묻는다. \"너는 뭐라 하느냐?\"\n\n만일 그것이 지금 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 말한다면. 만일 그것이 헤아려온 그 숱한 밤 가운데 하필 오늘 밤, 그 앞에 서본 적 있는 그 숱한 젊은이와 늙은이 가운데 하필 이 늙은이에게 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 말한다면, 훗날 얼마나 이름난 시계가 되겠는가! 시계가 날카롭고 또렷한 종소리로 일곱 시 사십오 분을 치고는 다시 똑딱똑딱 간다. \"원, 내가 보던 것보다 더 못쓰겠군.\" 토킹혼 씨가 제 회중시계를 나무라듯 중얼거린다. \"이 분이나 틀려? 이런 식이면 너는 내 수명도 못 채우겠다.\" 만일 그것이 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 똑딱여 답함으로써 악을 선으로 갚는다면, 그 얼마나 갸륵한 회중시계인가!\n\n그가 거리로 나서서, 뒷짐을 진 채 높다란 집들의 그늘 아래로 걸어간다. 그 집들의 숱한 비밀과 골칫거리, 저당, 온갖 미묘한 사정들이 그의 낡은 검은 새틴 조끼 안에 고이 간직되어 있다. 그는 그 벽돌과 회반죽 자체의 속내까지 꿰고 있다. 높다란 굴뚝들이 가문의 비밀을 그에게 전신으로 알린다. 그런데도 그 일 마일 안에는 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 속삭일 목소리가 단 하나도 없다.\n\n더 평범한 거리들의 북적임과 움직임을 가로질러, 숱한 마차와 숱한 발과 숱한 목소리의 우렁찬 소음과 덜컹거림을 헤치며, 환하게 타오르는 상점 불빛이 그의 앞길을 밝히고 서풍이 그를 떠밀고 군중이 그를 밀어붙이는 가운데, 그는 무자비하게 제 갈 길로 내몰리지만, \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 웅얼대며 그를 마주하는 것은 아무것도 없다. 마침내 그의 음울한 방에 이르러 촛불을 밝히고 둘레와 위쪽을 둘러보며 천장에서 손가락질하는 로마인을 올려다보지만, 오늘 밤 그 로마인의 손에도, 시중드는 무리의 펄럭임에도 \"이리 오지 마시오!\" 하는 때늦은 경고를 줄 만한 새로운 의미는 없다.\n\n달밤이지만, 보름을 지난 달은 이제야 런던의 거대한 황야 위로 떠오른다. 별들은 체스니 월드 첨탑의 납지붕 위에서 빛나던 그대로 빛난다. 요즘 그가 줄곧 부르는 호칭대로 하자면 이 여자가, 그 별들을 내다본다. 그녀의 영혼은 안에서 들끓고, 그녀는 가슴이 답답하며 안절부절못한다. 그 큰 방들이 너무도 비좁고 갑갑하다. 그녀는 방의 옥죔을 견딜 수 없어 근처 정원을 홀로 거닐려 한다.\n\n하는 일마다 너무도 변덕스럽고 위세 등등하여 무슨 짓을 하든 주위 사람을 별로 놀라게 하지 않는 이 여자가, 헐겁게 몸을 감싼 채 달빛 속으로 나선다. 헤르메스가 열쇠를 들고 따라간다. 정원 문을 연 뒤 그는 마님의 청에 따라 열쇠를 마님 손에 건네고 돌아가라는 분부를 받는다. 마님은 지끈거리는 머리를 식히려 한동안 거기를 거닐 것이다. 한 시간쯤 걸릴 수도, 더 걸릴 수도 있다. 더는 누가 따라올 필요가 없다. 문이 용수철 소리와 함께 철컥 닫히고, 그는 마님이 어느 나무들의 어두운 그늘 속으로 들어가도록 두고 떠난다.\n\n좋은 밤이고, 밝고 커다란 달이며, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 별이다. 토킹혼 씨는 제 지하 술 저장고로 가서 그 울리는 문들을 여닫느라, 감옥 같은 작은 마당을 가로질러야 한다. 그가 무심코 위를 올려다보며 생각한다, 참 좋은 밤이군, 참 밝고 커다란 달이군, 별이 참 무수하군! 게다가 고요한 밤이군.\n\n참으로 고요한 밤이다. 달이 유난히 찬란하게 빛날 때면, 그 달에서 어떤 적막과 정적이 흘러나와 생기로 북적이는 곳마저 물들이는 듯하다. 먼지 인 큰길 위에도, 또 드넓은 시골 들녘이 잠든 듯 한눈에 들어오는 언덕마루에도 고요한 밤일 뿐 아니라—거기서는 들판이 멀어지며 하늘을 등진 나무들의 띠에 이르고, 그 위로 잿빛 유령 같은 꽃빛이 어리며 점점 더 고요해진다—정원과 숲에도, 물풀밭이 싱그럽게 푸르고 시냇물이 정겨운 섬들 사이로 반짝이며 둑을 넘어 웅얼대고 갈대 사이로 속삭이며 흐르는 강 위에도 고요한 밤일 뿐 아니라, 집들이 빽빽이 들어찬 곳을 지나며, 숱한 다리가 그 위에 비치는 곳을 지나며, 부두와 선박이 그 강을 검고 으스스하게 만드는 곳을 지나며, 이 흉물들을 벗어나 해변에 떠밀려온 해골처럼 음산한 봉화탑이 늘어선 늪지를 굽이쳐 지나며, 옥수수밭과 풍차와 첨탑이 풍성한 더 호방한 구릉지대를 통과해 펼쳐지며, 마침내 쉴 새 없이 일렁이는 바다와 뒤섞이는 그곳까지, 강물이 흐르는 동안 줄곧 적막이 그것을 따른다. 깊은 바다 위에도, 또 펼친 날개를 단 배가 오직 자기에게만 깔린 듯한 빛의 길을 가로지르는 것을 지켜보려 파수꾼이 서 있는 그 해변에도 고요한 밤일 뿐 아니라, 이 이방인의 황야 같은 런던에조차 얼마간의 안식이 깃든다. 그 첨탑과 탑들과 하나뿐인 거대한 둥근 지붕이 한층 영묘해지고, 연기에 그을린 지붕들은 그 창백한 광휘 속에서 거친 티를 벗으며, 거리에서 일어나는 소음은 줄고 누그러지고, 보도 위의 발소리는 한결 잔잔하게 멀어진다. 토킹혼 씨가 거니는 이 들판, 곧 멈춤구멍 하나 없는 대법원 피리를 양치기들이 불어대고, 양들을 멈춤도 굽힘도 없이 우리에 가두어 두었다가 마침내 바싹 털을 깎아버리는 이 들판에서는, 이 달밤에 모든 소음이 한데 녹아들어 멀리서 울리는 웅웅거림이 되니, 마치 도시가 떨고 있는 거대한 유리잔이라도 되는 듯하다.\n\n저게 뭐지? 누가 총인지 권총인지를 쏘았나? 어디였지?\n\n몇 안 되는 보행자들이 흠칫 놀라 멈춰 서서 사방을 두리번거린다. 창문과 문이 더러 열리고, 사람들이 보려고 나온다. 요란한 발포음이었고, 메아리치며 무겁게 덜컹거렸다. 지나가던 어떤 사내의 말로는 집 한 채가 흔들렸다고도 한다. 그 소리에 동네의 개들이 죄다 깨어나 사납게 짖어댄다. 겁먹은 고양이들이 길을 가로질러 줄행랑친다. 개들이 아직 짖고 울부짖는 동안에도—악마처럼 울부짖는 개가 한 마리 있다—교회 종들이 저마다 놀라기라도 한 듯 치기 시작한다. 거리에서 이는 웅웅거림 역시 외침으로 부풀어 오르는 듯하다. 그러나 그것도 곧 잦아든다. 마지막 종이 열 시를 치기 시작하기도 전에 잠잠해진다. 그것이 멎고 나자, 좋은 밤과 밝고 커다란 달과 무수한 별들이 다시 평온히 남는다.\n\n토킹혼 씨는 방해를 받았을까? 그의 창은 어둡고 고요하며, 문은 닫혀 있다. 그를 제 껍데기 밖으로 끌어내려면 정말이지 어지간히 예사롭지 않은 일이라야 한다. 그에 관해서는 아무 소리도 들리지 않고, 아무 모습도 보이지 않는다. 그 녹슨 늙은이를 그 미동 없는 침착함에서 흔들어내려면 대포를 얼마나 쏘아야 할까?\n\n여러 해 동안 그 끈질긴 로마인은 별 뜻 없이 저 천장에서 손가락질해 왔다. 오늘 밤이라 해서 그에게 무슨 새로운 뜻이 생겼을 리 없다. 한번 가리키면 언제나 가리킨다—한 가지 생각밖에 없는 여느 로마인처럼, 아니 브리튼인이라 한들 마찬가지로. 거기 그가, 불가능한 자세로 밤새도록 부질없이 손가락질하며 있는 게 분명하다. 달빛, 어둠, 동틀 녘, 해돋이, 한낮. 거기 그가 여전히 열심히 손가락질하고 있건만, 아무도 그를 거들떠보지 않는다.\n\n그러나 날이 밝고 조금 지나 사람들이 방을 치우러 온다. 그런데 그 로마인에게 전에는 드러나지 않던 무슨 새로운 뜻이 생겼는지, 아니면 그들 가운데 맨 앞에 선 자가 미쳐버린 것인지, 그자가 뻗은 그 손을 올려다보고 그 아래에 놓인 것을 내려다보더니 비명을 지르며 달아난다. 나머지 사람들도 첫 번째 사람이 들여다본 대로 안을 들여다보다가 똑같이 비명을 지르며 달아나고, 거리에 소동이 인다.\n\n이게 무슨 일인가? 어두컴컴한 방에는 빛이 한 줄기도 들지 않고, 익숙지 않은 사람들이 들어가 사뿐하면서도 무겁게 걸으며 무언가 무거운 것을 침실로 옮겨 내려놓는다. 온종일 수군거림과 의아함이 가득하고, 구석구석 샅샅이 뒤지며, 발자국을 조심스레 좇고, 가구 한 점 한 점의 놓인 자리를 꼼꼼히 적어둔다. 모두의 눈이 그 로마인을 올려다보고, 모두의 입이 웅얼거린다. \"저자가 본 것을 말해 줄 수만 있다면!\"\n\n그가 가리키는 것은 (포도주가 거의 가득 찬) 병 하나와 잔 하나가 놓이고, 불을 붙인 지 얼마 안 되어 느닷없이 꺼져버린 촛불 두 자루가 놓인 탁자다. 그가 가리키는 것은 빈 의자 하나와 그 앞 바닥에 진 얼룩 하나니, 손 하나로 거의 가려질 만한 얼룩이다. 이 물건들은 바로 그가 가리키는 범위 안에 놓여 있다. 흥분한 상상력이라면, 거기에 무언가 너무도 끔찍한 것이 있어 그 구성의 나머지를—시중드는 굵은 다리의 사내아이들뿐 아니라 구름과 꽃과 기둥들까지, 한마디로 알레고리의 바로 그 몸과 영혼, 그리고 거기 깃든 온갖 머리까지—깡그리 미치게 했다고 여길지도 모른다. 어두컴컴한 방에 들어와 이 물건들을 보는 사람마다 그 로마인을 올려다보고, 그리하여 그가 마치 마비되어 말 못 하는 증인이라도 되는 양 모든 눈에 신비와 경외에 휩싸이는 일이, 어김없이 벌어진다.\n\n이렇게 어김없이 벌어지리라. 앞으로 숱한 세월에 걸쳐, 그토록 가리기는 쉬워도 지워내기는 그토록 어려운 바닥의 그 얼룩을 두고 으스스한 이야기들이 떠돌리라. 그리고 그 로마인은 천장에서 손가락질하되, 먼지와 습기와 거미가 그를 비껴두는 한, 토킹혼 씨 시절에 지녔던 것보다 훨씬 큰 의미로, 죽음의 뜻을 담아 가리키리라. 토킹혼 씨의 시절은 영영 끝나버렸으니, 그 로마인은 그의 목숨을 노려 치켜든 살인의 손을 가리켰고, 또 가슴을 관통당해 바닥에 얼굴을 묻고 쓰러진 그를, 밤부터 아침까지 속절없이 가리켰던 것이다.\n\n매튜 배그넷 씨, 다른 이름으로는 리그넘 비타이라 불리는, 전직 포병이자 현직 바순 연주자의 가정에 해마다 돌아오는 큰 행사가 또 찾아왔다. 잔치와 축제의 날이다. 가족 가운데 한 사람의 생일을 기리는 자리다.\n\n* 리그넘 비타이: 라틴어로 '생명의 나무'를 뜻하며, 단단하기로 이름난 열대 목재의 이름이기도 하다. 배그넷의 우직하고 굳센 성품을 빗댄 별명이다.\n배그넷 씨의 생일은 아니다. 배그넷 씨는 악기 장사의 그 시기를 그저 아침을 먹기 전에 아이들에게 입을 한 번 더 쪽 맞추고, 저녁을 먹은 뒤 담배를 한 대 더 피우고, 해 질 무렵이면 가엾은 노모가 이걸 어떻게 생각할까 곰곰 떠올리는 것으로 표시할 따름이다. 끝없이 곱씹게 되는 주제인데, 그 어머니가 세상을 떠난 지 이십 년이 되었으니 더욱 그러하다. 더러 어떤 사내들은 아버지는 좀처럼 떠올리지 않으면서, 추억이라는 통장 속 효심의 자산을 몽땅 어머니 이름으로 옮겨놓은 듯하다. 배그넷 씨가 바로 그런 사람이다. 어쩌면 노모의 미덕을 워낙 드높이 받드는 탓에, 그는 으레 \"선함\"이라는 명사를 여성형으로 다루는지도 모른다.\n\n세 아이 가운데 하나의 생일도 아니다. 그런 날들도 어느 정도 특별하게 챙기긴 하지만, 무사히 또 한 살 먹은 것을 축하하며 푸딩을 차리는 경계를 넘어서는 일은 드물다. 어린 울리치의 지난 생일에 배그넷 씨는 아이가 자란 것과 두루 발전한 것을 두고 한마디 한 뒤, 세월이 빚어내는 변화를 깊이 곱씹던 차에 교리문답을 시켜보는 데까지 나아간 게 분명하다. 첫째와 둘째 물음, \"네 이름이 무엇이냐?\"와 \"누가 네게 그 이름을 주었느냐?\"는 더없이 정확하게 해냈으나, 거기서 기억의 정밀함이 어긋나 셋째 물음 자리에 \"그래서 그 이름이 마음에 드느냐?\"라는 물음을 갈아 끼웠으니, 그는 그 질문을 무게감 있게 던졌고, 그 자체로 어찌나 교화적이고 향상에 이로운지 자못 정통적인 느낌마저 풍겼다. 그러나 이건 그 특정 생일에만 있었던 특별 사례일 뿐, 늘 치르는 의식은 아니었다.\n\n노모의 생일이다. 그리고 그날은 배그넷 씨의 달력에서 가장 큰 명절이자 가장 붉은 글자로 적힌 날이다. 이 경사스러운 행사는 몇 해 전 배그넷 씨가 정해 규정해놓은 일정한 격식에 따라 늘 기념한다. 저녁상에 닭 두 마리를 올리는 것이야말로 제왕에 버금가는 사치의 절정에 이르는 일이라고 깊이 믿는 배그넷 씨는, 이날이면 어김없이 몸소 아주 이른 아침에 나가 닭 두 마리를 사 온다. 그리고 어김없이 장사꾼에게 속아서, 유럽의 어느 닭장에서든 가장 오래 묵은 터줏대감을 손에 넣고야 만다. 이 질김의 승리물을 깨끗한 청백 무명 손수건에(이 손수건이 이 행사의 필수품이다) 싸 들고 돌아와서는, 짐짓 무심한 척 배그넷 부인에게 아침상에서 저녁으로 뭘 먹고 싶은지 말해보라고 청한다. 한 번도 어긋난 적 없는 우연으로, 배그넷 부인이 닭이라고 대답하면, 배그넷 씨는 곧장 숨겨둔 곳에서 보따리를 꺼내 들어 온 식구의 경탄과 환호를 자아낸다. 그는 한술 더 떠 노모가 하루 종일 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고 앉아만 있으면서 자기와 아이들에게 시중을 받아야 한다고 못 박는다. 그의 요리 솜씨가 이름난 게 아닌지라, 노모로서는 즐거움이라기보다 격식을 차린 노릇에 가까울 법하지만, 그래도 그녀는 상상할 수 있는 한 더없이 명랑하게 그 격식을 지킨다.\n\n이번 생일에도 배그넷 씨는 늘 하던 준비를 마쳤다. 그는 닭 두 마리를 사 왔는데, 속담에 진실이 조금이라도 담겨 있다면 이놈들은 결코 겨로 꾀어 잡힌 놈들이 아니다. 그놈들을 꼬챙이에 꿸 채비를 시켰고, 뜻밖의 등장으로 식구들을 놀라게 하고 기쁘게 했으며, 손수 닭 굽는 일을 진두지휘하고 있다. 한편 배그넷 부인은 일이 잘못 돌아가는 걸 보고 손이 근질근질해 막고 싶어 하면서도, 격식 차린 옷을 입고 귀빈으로 앉아 있다.\n* 겨로 꾀어 잡힌 놈들이 아니다: \"늙은 새는 겨로 꾀어 잡지 못한다\"는 영어 속담을 빗댄 것으로, 닭들이 그만큼 늙고 노회하다는 풍자.\n\n퀘벡과 몰타가 저녁상에 식탁보를 깔고, 울리치는 제 신분에 걸맞게 아버지 밑에서 거들며 닭을 빙빙 돌린다. 이 어린 일꾼들이 실수를 저지를 때마다 배그넷 부인은 이따금 윙크를 보내거나 고개를 절레절레 젓거나 얼굴을 찡그려 보인다.\n* 퀘벡, 몰타: 배그넷 부부의 두 딸로, 아버지가 군 복무하던 주둔지의 지명을 따서 붙인 이름이다.\n\n\"한 시 반이다.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"일 분도 안 틀리게. 그때면 다 익는다.\"\n\n배그넷 부인은 가슴 졸이며, 닭 한 마리가 불 앞에서 돌기를 멈추고 타기 시작하는 걸 바라본다.\n\n\"임자, 여왕한테나 차릴 만한 저녁상을 받게 될 거야.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다.\n\n배그넷 부인은 하얀 이를 명랑하게 드러내 보이지만, 아들의 눈에는 마음의 불안이 너무 또렷이 비쳐, 아들은 애정에 떠밀려 눈으로 무슨 일이냐고 어머니에게 묻는다. 그렇게 두 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 선 채 아까보다 더 닭을 까맣게 잊어버려, 정신을 차릴 기미라곤 조금도 보이지 않는다. 다행히 누나가 배그넷 부인의 가슴속 동요의 까닭을 알아채고는, 타이르듯 콕 찔러 동생을 제정신으로 돌려놓는다. 멈췄던 닭이 다시 돌아가자 배그넷 부인은 안도가 사무쳐 눈을 감는다.\n\n\"조지가 우릴 찾아올 거야.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"네 시 반에. 어김없이. 임자, 조지가 우릴 찾아온 지가. 몇 해째지. 오늘 오후로?\"\n\n\"아아, 리그넘, 리그넘, 젊은 처녀가 늙은 할멈이 되고도 남을 만큼 됐지, 슬슬 그런 생각이 드네. 딱 그쯤이지, 그보다 적지는 않을 거야.\" 배그넷 부인이 웃으며 고개를 저으며 대답한다.\n\n\"임자.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"괜찮아. 당신은 옛날만큼 젊어. 더 젊어지진 않았다 쳐도. 사실은 더 젊어졌지. 다들 아는 일이야.\"\n\n이 대목에서 퀘벡과 몰타가 손뼉을 치며, 블러피가 분명 엄마한테 뭔가를 가져올 거라고 소리치고는, 그게 뭘까 점치기 시작한다.\n\n* 블러피: 조지를 가리키는 아이들의 애칭.\n\"있잖아요, 리그넘.\" 배그넷 부인이 식탁보에 눈길을 던지며, 오른쪽 눈으로 몰타에게 \"소금!\" 하고 윙크하고, 고개를 까딱여 퀘벡한테서 후추를 치우게 하면서 말한다. \"조지가 또 떠돌 마음이 든 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"조지는.\" 배그넷 씨가 대답한다. \"절대 등 돌리지 않아. 옛 전우를 두고. 곤경에 내버려두는 일은. 그런 걱정은 말아.\"\n\n\"아니, 리그넘. 아니에요. 그가 그럴 거라는 말은 아니에요. 그러리라곤 생각 안 해요. 하지만 이 돈 문제만 넘긴다면, 떠나버릴 것 같아요.\"\n\n배그넷 씨가 왜냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 아내가 곰곰 생각하며 대답한다. \"조지가 영 안달이 나고 안절부절못하는 것 같단 말이에요. 옛날만큼 거리낌 없지 않다는 건 아니에요. 당연히 자유로워야지, 안 그러면 조지가 아니지, 그래도 어딘가 쓰리고 속이 뒤틀린 듯해요.\"\n\n\"변호사한테 호되게 단련당해서 그래.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"악마라도 화나게 할 변호사한테.\"\n\n\"그 말도 일리가 있긴 해요.\" 아내가 수긍한다. \"하지만 사실이 그런걸요, 리그넘.\"\n\n이때 더 이상의 대화는 잠시 가로막힌다. 배그넷 씨가 온 정신력을 저녁 식사에 쏟아부어야 할 처지에 놓인 까닭이다. 닭들이 메마른 익살을 부려 국물 한 방울 내놓지 않는 데다, 따로 만든 그레이비마저 도통 맛이 들지 않고 누리끼리한 빛으로 나오는 탓에 식사가 살짝 위태로워졌다. 비슷하게 심술궂게도, 감자는 껍질을 벗기는 와중에 포크에서 부스러져, 마치 지진이라도 겪는 양 한가운데서 사방으로 갈라져 솟구친다. 닭다리 또한 바라는 것보다 길고, 비늘이 잔뜩 일어 있다. 이 온갖 불리한 점들을 최선을 다해 극복하고서, 배그넷 씨는 마침내 음식을 접시에 담고, 모두 식탁에 둘러앉으니, 배그넷 부인이 그의 오른편 귀빈석을 차지한다.\n\n노모로서는 생일이 일 년에 한 번뿐인 게 다행이다. 이런 닭 호사를 두 번이나 누렸다간 몸에 해로울 수도 있을 테니까. 닭의 본성이 지닐 수 있는 온갖 가느다란 힘줄과 인대가 이 표본들에서는 기타 줄이라는 별난 형태로 발달해 있다. 사지는 마치 늙은 나무가 흙에 뿌리를 뻗듯, 가슴팍과 몸통에 뿌리를 박은 듯하다. 다리는 어찌나 단단한지, 이놈들이 길고 고된 평생의 태반을 도보 운동과 걷기 시합에 바쳤음이 틀림없다는 생각이 들 정도다. 그러나 배그넷 씨는 이런 자잘한 흠은 까맣게 모른 채, 배그넷 부인이 앞에 놓인 진미를 어마어마하게 먹어치우는 데 온 마음을 쏟는다. 그리고 마음씨 고운 노모는 어떤 날에도, 더구나 이런 날에는 무슨 일이 있어도 그를 단 한순간도 실망시키지 않으려는지라, 제 소화력을 무섭도록 위태롭게 한다. 어린 울리치가 타조 핏줄도 아니면서 어떻게 닭다리를 그토록 말끔히 발라먹는지, 노심초사하는 어머니로서는 도무지 알 길이 없다.\n\n식사가 끝난 뒤에도 노모에게는 또 하나의 시련이 남아 있으니, 격식을 차려 앉아서 방이 치워지고 난로가 쓸리고 식기들이 뒷마당에서 씻기고 닦이는 광경을 지켜보는 일이다. 두 어린 아가씨가 이 일들에 매달리는 대단한 즐거움과 기운—어머니를 흉내 내어 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고, 나막신을 받친 작은 발판을 타고 들락날락 미끄러지듯 누비는 모습—은 앞날에는 더없는 희망을, 그러나 당장에는 약간의 불안을 안긴다. 같은 까닭으로 혀가 뒤엉켜 떠들어대고, 사기그릇이 달그락거리고, 양철 컵이 덜그럭거리고, 빗자루가 휙휙 날고, 물이 펑펑 쓰이는데, 죄다 지나치게 과하다. 한편 어린 아가씨들 자신이 흠뻑 젖어버린 꼴은, 배그넷 부인이 제 자리에 어울리는 평정심을 지키며 바라보기에는 거의 너무도 가슴 뭉클한 광경이다. 마침내 갖가지 청소 과정이 의기양양하게 마무리되고, 퀘벡과 몰타가 보송보송하게 마른 새 옷차림으로 생글거리며 나타난다. 식탁에는 담뱃대와 담배와 마실 것이 놓이고, 노모는 이 즐거운 잔칫날에 처음으로 마음의 평온을 맛본다.\n\n배그넷 씨가 늘 앉는 자리에 앉을 때, 시곗바늘은 네 시 반에 거의 다 와 있다. 바늘이 정확히 그 시각을 가리키자, 배그넷 씨가 알린다. \"조지! 군대 시간이군.\"\n\n조지다. 그는 노모에게(이런 큰 날이라 입을 맞춘다), 아이들에게, 배그넷 씨에게 진심 어린 축하를 건넨다. \"모두 또 한 해 잘 맞으시길!\" 조지 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"근데, 조지, 이 사람아!\" 배그넷 부인이 그를 의아한 눈으로 바라보며 외친다. \"무슨 일 있었어?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이라뇨?\"\n\n\"아니! 얼굴이 어찌 그리 핼쑥해, 조지—자네답지 않게—게다가 무슨 충격받은 얼굴이야. 안 그래요, 리그넘?\"\n\n\"조지.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"노모한테 말하게. 무슨 일인지.\"\n\n\"제 얼굴이 핼쑥한 줄은 몰랐네요.\" 기병이 손으로 이마를 쓸며 말한다. \"충격받은 얼굴인 줄도 몰랐고요, 그렇다니 미안합니다. 하지만 사실은, 제 가게에 받아들였던 그 소년이 어제 오후에 죽었어요. 그게 저를 좀 무너뜨렸나 봅니다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것!\" 배그넷 부인이 어머니다운 연민으로 말한다. \"그 애가 갔어? 저런, 저런!\"\n\n\"이 얘긴 꺼낼 생각이 없었어요, 생일에 할 얘긴 아니니까요. 그런데 보세요, 제가 앉기도 전에 부인이 기어이 끌어내셨네요. 금방 기운 차렸을 텐데.\" 기병이 일부러 더 쾌활하게 말한다. \"부인은 워낙 눈치가 빠르셔서요, 배그넷 부인.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이야. 우리 노모는.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"화약처럼. 빠르지.\"\n\n\"게다가 무엇보다, 오늘의 주인공은 부인이시니, 우린 부인께 매달려야지요.\" 조지 씨가 외친다. \"이거 보세요, 작은 브로치를 하나 가져왔어요. 변변찮은 거예요, 그래도 정표지요. 그게 이 물건의 쓸모 전부예요, 배그넷 부인.\"\n\n조지 씨가 선물을 내놓자, 어린 식구들은 감탄하며 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰고 손뼉을 치며 맞이하고, 배그넷 씨는 일종의 경건한 찬탄으로 맞이한다. \"임자.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"이 사람한테 이것에 대한 내 견해를 전해줘.\"\n\n\"세상에, 이거 정말 굉장한 거잖아, 조지!\" 배그넷 부인이 외친다. \"이렇게 아름다운 건 난생처음 봐!\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"그게 내 견해야.\"\n\n\"어찌나 예쁜지, 조지.\" 배그넷 부인이 이리저리 돌려보고 팔을 쭉 뻗어 들어 보며 외친다. \"나한테는 너무 과분할 만큼 곱구먼.\"\n\n\"틀렸어!\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"그건 내 견해가 아니야.\"\n\n\"하지만 어쨌든, 백만 번 고마워, 이 사람아.\" 배그넷 부인이 기쁨에 눈을 반짝이며 그에게 손을 내밀며 말한다. \"그리고 내가 가끔은 자네한테 까칠한 군인 마누라 노릇을 했지만, 조지, 우린 진짜로는 더없이 단단한 친구지, 그건 분명해. 자, 이제 운이 따르라고 자네가 직접 채워주게, 그래줄 거지, 조지.\"\n\n아이들이 그 광경을 보려고 바싹 다가서고, 배그넷 씨도 어린 울리치의 머리 너머로 그 광경을 지켜보는데, 그 관심이 어찌나 다 자란 어른답게 뻣뻣하면서도 흐뭇하게 아이 같은지, 배그넷 부인은 특유의 경쾌한 투로 웃음을 터뜨리며 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없다. \"어머, 리그넘, 리그넘, 당신은 어쩜 그리 귀여운 영감이람!\" 그러나 기병은 브로치를 채우는 데 실패한다. 손이 떨리고, 그는 불안하고, 브로치는 떨어져버린다. \"이거 참, 누가 믿겠어요?\" 떨어지는 브로치를 잡아채며 그가 둘러보며 말한다. \"내가 이렇게 안절부절못해서, 이런 쉬운 일도 서투르게 망치다니!\"\n\n배그넷 부인은 이런 경우에는 담배만 한 약이 없다고 결론짓고는, 눈 깜짝할 새에 브로치를 손수 채운 뒤, 기병을 늘 앉던 아늑한 자리에 모셔 앉히고 담뱃대를 가동시킨다. \"그래도 기운이 안 나거든, 조지.\" 그녀가 말한다. \"이따금 여기 자네 선물 쪽으로 눈을 던져봐, 그럼 둘이 합쳐서 틀림없이 효험을 볼 테니까.\"\n\n\"부인이 직접 채우셔야 마땅했죠.\" 조지가 대답한다. \"저도 그건 잘 알아요, 배그넷 부인. 이런저런 일로 우울이 너무 겹쳐 견디기 힘들어졌다고나 할까요. 그 가엾은 녀석 일이 있었죠. 그 애가 그렇게 죽어가는 걸 지켜보면서도 도와줄 수 없는 게, 참 답답한 노릇이었어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리야, 조지? 자넨 그 애를 도왔잖아. 자네 지붕 아래 거둬줬으니.\"\n\n\"그만큼은 도왔죠, 하지만 그건 사소한 거예요. 제 말은, 배그넷 부인, 그 애가 거기 누워서, 오른손과 왼손을 구별하는 것 말고는 더 배운 것도 별로 없이 죽어갔다는 거예요. 게다가 이미 너무 늦어서, 그 처지에서 건져낼 수도 없었고요.\"\n\n\"아아, 가엾은 것!\" 배그넷 부인이 말한다.\n\n\"그러고 나니.\" 기병이 아직 담뱃대에 불도 붙이지 않은 채 묵직한 손으로 머리칼을 쓸어 넘기며 말한다. \"그리들리가 떠오르더군요. 그 사람 경우도 결이 다르긴 해도 딱한 처지였죠. 그러더니 그 둘이, 둘 다와 얽혔던 어느 매정한 늙은 악당과 머릿속에서 뒤섞이는 거예요. 그리고 그 녹슨 카빈총이, 개머리판이며 총신이며, 그자의 구석에 똑바로 서서, 단단하고 무심하게, 모든 걸 그토록 한결같이 받아넘기는 꼴을 떠올리면—정말이지, 살과 피가 다 찌릿해졌어요.\"\n\n\"내 충고는.\" 배그넷 부인이 대답한다. \"담뱃대에 불을 붙여서 그쪽으로 찌릿하라는 거야. 그게 더 건강에 좋고 마음도 편하고, 어느 모로 보나 몸에 이로우니까.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다.\" 기병이 말한다. \"그렇게 하지요.\"\n\n그래서 그는 그렇게 하지만, 여전히 분이 서린 엄숙함이 어려 있어 어린 배그넷들에게 인상을 남기고, 심지어 배그넷 씨가 배그넷 부인의 건강을 비는 건배 의식을 미루게까지 한다. 이 건배는 이런 자리마다 늘 그가 본보기로 삼을 만큼 간결한 연설로 올려온 것이다. 그러나 어린 아가씨들이 배그넷 씨가 으레 \"혼합주\"라 부르는 것을 다 타고, 조지의 담뱃대도 이제 발갛게 달아오르자, 배그넷 씨는 그날 저녁의 건배사로 나아가는 것을 제 의무로 여긴다. 그는 모인 일행에게 다음과 같은 말로 연설한다.\n\n\"조지. 울리치. 퀘벡. 몰타. 오늘은 이 사람 생일이다. 하루를 꼬박 행군해봐라. 그래도 이만한 사람은 못 찾을 거다. 이 사람을 위하여!\"\n\n건배를 열렬히 들이켠 뒤, 배그넷 부인은 그에 걸맞게 간결한 답사로 감사를 표한다. 이 본보기 같은 명문은 \"그쪽들도 잘되시길!\"이라는 세 마디로 그치고, 노모는 거기에 이어 모두에게 차례차례 고개를 끄덕이고는 혼합주를 절제 있게 한 모금 들이켠다. 그러고는 이날 자리에서, 다시 이어, 전혀 뜻밖의 탄성을 내뱉는다. \"어머, 손님이네!\"\n\n손님이 있다. 작은 모임이 깜짝 놀라게도, 거실 문 안을 들여다보는 한 사내가 있다. 눈매가 날카로운 사내—재빠르고 예리한 사내—인데, 그는 모두가 자기를 바라보는 표정을, 단번에, 개개인 것이고 전체의 것이고 모조리 받아들이는데, 그 솜씨가 그를 비범한 사내임을 새겨두게 한다.\n\n\"조지.\" 사내가 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다. \"어떻게 지내나?\"\n\n\"아니, 버킷이잖나!\" 조지 씨가 외친다.\n\n\"그래.\" 사내가 들어와 문을 닫으며 말한다. \"이 거리를 내려가던 참에 어쩌다 멈춰서 가게 진열창의 악기들을 들여다봤지—친구 하나가 음색 좋은 중고 비올론첼로를 구하고 있어서 말이야—그러다 보니 일행이 즐겁게 어울리는 게 눈에 들어왔고, 저 구석에 있는 게 자네구나 싶었거든. 잘못 봤을 리 없다 싶었지. 요즘 자네는 세상살이가 어떤가, 조지? 그럭저럭 매끄러운가? 부인은요? 주인장은요? 아이고.\" 버킷 씨가 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 말한다. \"여기 아이들도 있네! 나한테는 아이들만 보여주면 무슨 짓이든 하게 만들 수 있어요. 입 좀 맞춰보자, 요 귀염둥이들. 너희 아버지 어머니가 누군지는 물어볼 것도 없겠다. 평생 이렇게 빼닮은 건 본 적이 없어!\"\n\n버킷 씨는, 환영받지 못할 사람도 아닌지라, 조지 씨 옆에 앉아 퀘벡과 몰타를 무릎에 앉혔다. \"요 예쁜 것들.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"한 번 더 뽀뽀해주렴. 내가 욕심내는 건 이것 하나뿐이란다. 아이고, 어쩜 이리 건강해 보일까! 이 둘은 나이가 몇이나 됩니까, 부인? 내가 보기엔 한 여덟 살, 열 살쯤 되겠는데요.\"\n\n\"거의 맞히셨네요, 선생님.\" 배그넷 부인이 말한다.\n\n\"내가 보통 잘 맞히지요.\" 버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"워낙 아이들을 좋아해서요. 친구 하나는 애가 열아홉이나 됩니다, 부인, 다 한 어머니한테서 났는데, 그 어머니가 아직도 아침처럼 싱싱하고 발그레해요. 부인만큼은 못해도, 정말이지 부인 못지않게 가깝게 갑니다! 그래, 이건 뭐라고 부르지, 우리 귀염둥이?\" 버킷 씨가 몰타의 볼을 꼬집으며 잇는다. \"이건 복숭아네, 복숭아야. 아이고 예뻐라! 그래, 아빠는 어떻게 생각하니? 아빠가 버킷 아저씨 친구한테 음색 좋은 중고 비올론첼로를 추천해줄 수 있을 것 같아, 얘야? 내 이름은 버킷이란다. 우습지 않니, 그 이름?\"\n\n이 살가운 너스레가 온 식구의 마음을 완전히 사로잡았다. 배그넷 부인은 오늘이 무슨 날인지도 잊을 만큼, 버킷 씨에게 담뱃대를 채우고 잔을 채워주며 정성껏 대접한다. 그녀는 어떤 사정에서든 이리도 유쾌한 인물을 맞으면 반가웠을 테지만, 조지의 친구이니 오늘 저녁에 만나뵙게 되어 특히 기쁘다고 그에게 말한다. 조지가 여느 때 같은 기분이 아니어서라는 것이다.\n\n\"여느 때 같은 기분이 아니라고요?\" 버킷 씨가 외친다. \"아니, 그런 소린 처음 듣는데요! 무슨 일인가, 조지? 설마 자네가 기분이 처져 있었다는 말은 아니겠지. 자네가 처질 까닭이 어디 있어? 마음에 걸리는 게 있을 리도 없잖나, 안 그래.\"\n\n\"별것 아닐세.\" 기병이 대답한다.\n\n\"그렇겠지.\" 버킷 씨가 받는다. \"자네가 마음에 걸릴 게 뭐가 있겠나! 그래, 이 귀염둥이들은 마음에 걸리는 게 있을까, 응? 없지, 하지만 언젠가는 이 아이들이 어느 젊은 녀석들 마음에 걸려서, 그 녀석들 기분을 아주 푹 가라앉히겠지요. 내가 예언자는 못 돼도, 그건 장담할 수 있습니다, 부인.\"\n\n배그넷 부인은 아주 흐뭇해하며, 버킷 씨도 제 가정이 있기를 바란다고 한다.\n\n\"아이고, 부인!\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"믿어지세요? 없어요, 저는. 아내와 하숙인 하나가 제 식구의 전부랍니다. 버킷 부인도 저만큼이나 아이들을 좋아하고 그만큼 갖고 싶어 하지만, 안 생기더군요. 그게 그래요. 세상의 재물은 고르지 않게 나뉘는 법이고, 사람은 그걸 두고 투덜대선 안 되지요. 참 좋은 뒷마당이네요, 부인! 그런데 저 마당에서 빠져나가는 길이 따로 있나요?\"\n\n저 마당에서 빠져나가는 길은 없다.\n\n\"정말 없어요?\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"있을 법도 한데 말이죠. 글쎄, 이만큼 내 마음에 든 뒷마당은 본 적이 없는 것 같네요. 좀 봐도 될까요? 고맙습니다. 아니, 보니까 빠져나갈 길이 없군요. 그래도 참 균형이 잘 잡힌 마당이에요!\"\n\n마당을 날카로운 눈으로 두루 살펴본 뒤, 버킷 씨는 친구 조지 씨 옆 의자로 돌아와 조지 씨의 어깨를 다정하게 토닥인다.\n\n\"이제 기분이 어떤가, 조지?\"\n\n\"이제 괜찮네.\" 기병이 대답한다.\n\n\"그래야지!\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"애초에 안 좋을 까닭이 어디 있겠나? 자네처럼 훤칠한 체격에 건장한 사내가 기분이 처질 권리는 없어. 이게 기분이 처질 가슴팍이오, 부인? 게다가 자넨 마음에 걸리는 게 없잖나, 조지. 자네가 마음에 걸릴 게 뭐가 있겠어!\"\n\n그 말솜씨의 폭과 다채로움을 생각하면 다소 이 한마디에 자꾸 매달리는 셈인데, 버킷 씨는 불을 붙이는 담뱃대에 대고 그 말을 두세 번 되뇌는데, 그 표정은 무언가에 귀를 기울이는 듯 그만의 독특한 얼굴이다. 그러나 그의 사교성이라는 태양은 이 짧은 일식에서 곧 회복되어 다시 빛난다.\n\n\"그래, 이 애가 오빠로구나, 얘들아?\" 버킷 씨가 어린 울리치에 관한 정보를 얻으려 퀘벡과 몰타에게 물으며 말한다. \"참 멋진 오빠야—그러니까 배다른 오빠 말이다. 부인이 낳은 아들이라기엔 너무 나이가 들었으니까요.\"\n\n\"적어도 남의 아들이 아니라는 것만은 보증할 수 있답니다.\" 배그넷 부인이 웃으며 대답한다.\n\n\"이거 놀랍군요! 하기야 부인을 닮긴 했어요, 그건 부인할 수 없겠네요. 아이고, 어쩜 이리 부인을 빼닮았을까! 그래도 이마라고 부를 만한 데를 보면, 거기서 아버지가 드러납니다!\" 버킷 씨는 한쪽 눈을 감고 두 얼굴을 견주어보고, 한편 배그넷 씨는 무덤덤한 만족 속에 담배를 피운다.\n\n이때야말로 배그넷 부인이 그에게 그 소년이 조지의 대자라는 걸 알릴 기회다.\n\n\"조지의 대자라고요?\" 버킷 씨가 더없이 살갑게 받는다. \"조지의 대자라면 다시 한번 악수를 해야지. 대부와 대자가 서로 빛을 더해주는군요. 그래, 이 아이를 어떻게 키우실 작정입니까, 부인? 무슨 악기에든 소질을 보이나요?\"\n\n배그넷 씨가 불쑥 끼어든다. \"피리를 분다오. 멋지게.\"\n\n\"이거 믿어지십니까, 주인장.\" 그 우연에 마음이 동한 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"제가 어릴 적엔 저도 피리를 불었답니다? 이 아이처럼 정식으로 배운 건 아니고, 그저 귀로 익혔지만요. 아이고! '영국 척탄병'—그건 영국인의 피를 끓게 하는 곡이지요! 우리한테 '영국 척탄병'을 한 곡 불어줄 수 있겠니, 멋진 친구야?\"\n\n이 어린 모임에 어린 울리치를 향한 이 청보다 더 반가운 일은 없을 것이니, 아이는 곧장 피리를 가져와 그 흥겨운 가락을 연주한다. 그 연주 내내 버킷 씨는 한껏 신이 나 박자를 맞추고, 후렴 \"영국 처어어언탄병!\"을 한 번도 놓치지 않고 야무지게 받아넘긴다. 요컨대 그는 음악적 취향을 어찌나 한껏 드러내는지, 배그넷 씨는 정말로 담뱃대를 입에서 떼고는 그가 가수임에 틀림없다는 확신을 표한다. 버킷 씨는 그 가락에 얽힌 혐의를 어찌나 겸손하게 받아들이는지—한때 제 가슴속 감정을 풀어내려 노래를 좀 흥얼거린 적은 있으나 벗들을 즐겁게 해드릴 만하다는 주제넘은 생각은 추호도 없었다고 털어놓으며—급기야 노래를 청받는다. 그날 저녁의 흥겨움에 뒤처지지 않으려고, 그는 응하여 \"그대의 사랑스러운 젊은 매력이 모두 사라진대도 나를 믿어주오\"를 들려준다. 이 발라드야말로 버킷 부인이 처녀였을 적 그녀의 마음을 움직여 그녀를 제단으로 다가오게 하는 데—버킷 씨 본인의 표현으로는 \"각오를 굳히게\" 하는 데—가장 강력한 아군이었다고 그는 배그넷 부인에게 일러준다.\n\n이 반짝이는 손님은 그날 저녁의 새롭고 유쾌한 면모가 되어, 그가 들어설 때만 해도 별다른 기쁨의 기색을 보이지 않던 조지 씨조차 저도 모르게 그를 자못 자랑스러워하기 시작한다. 그는 어찌나 다정하고, 어찌나 재간이 많고, 어찌나 어울리기 편한지, 그를 여기 소개한 것이 보람된 일로 여겨질 정도다. 배그넷 씨는 담배를 한 대 더 피운 뒤, 그를 알게 된 것의 가치를 절실히 깨달아, 노모의 다음 생일에 그가 함께해주는 영광을 청한다. 버킷 씨가 이 가족에게 품게 된 존경을 더욱 단단히 굳히고 다질 만한 것이 있다면, 그건 바로 이 자리가 무슨 날인지 알게 된 것이다. 그는 환희에 가까운 따스함으로 배그넷 부인을 위해 잔을 들고, 일 년 뒤 그날을 위해 더없이 고맙게 약속하고, 허리띠가 달린 큼직한 검정 수첩에 그날을 적어넣고, 그때까지는 버킷 부인과 배그넷 부인이 어찌 보면 자매 같은 사이가 되기를 바란다고 말한다. 그 자신의 말마따나, 사사로운 정 없는 공적인 삶이 다 무엇이란 말인가? 그도 변변찮으나마 공직에 몸담은 사람이지만, 그가 행복을 찾는 곳은 그 영역이 아니다. 아니, 행복은 가정의 단란함이라는 울타리 안에서 찾아야 마땅하다.\n\n이런 상황에서 그가 그렇게 유망한 친분을 맺게 해준 벗을 이번에는 떠올리는 것도 자연스러운 일이다. 그리고 그는 그를 떠올린다. 그는 그의 곁에 바짝 붙어 있다. 대화의 주제가 무엇이든, 그는 그에게 다정한 눈길을 떼지 않는다. 그는 그와 함께 집까지 걸어가려고 기다린다. 그는 그의 장화에까지 관심을 두어, 조지 씨가 굴뚝 옆에서 책상다리를 하고 앉아 담배를 피우는 동안 그 장화마저 유심히 살핀다.\n\n마침내 조지 씨가 떠나려고 일어선다. 같은 순간, 버킷 씨도 우정의 은밀한 공감으로 함께 일어선다. 그는 끝까지 아이들을 어여삐 여기고, 자리에 없는 친구를 위해 맡은 부탁을 잊지 않는다.\n\n\"그 중고 비올론첼로 말인데요, 주인장—그런 걸 하나 추천해주실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"수십 개도 됩니다.\" 배그넷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"감사합니다.\" 버킷 씨가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 대답한다. \"어려울 때 도와주는 친구가 진짜 친구지요. 음색이 좋아야 해요, 명심하시고! 제 친구가 그쪽으로는 보통 솜씨가 아니거든요. 정말이지, 모차르트며 헨델이며 그 거장들을 영락없는 장인처럼 슥슥 켜낸답니다. 그리고,\" 버킷 씨가 사려 깊고 은근한 목소리로 말한다. \"값을 너무 낮게 부르실 필요는 없어요, 주인장. 제 친구한테 너무 큰돈을 치르게 하고 싶진 않지만, 주인장도 마땅한 이문을 챙기고 들인 시간값을 받으셔야지요. 그게 공평한 거니까요. 누구나 먹고살아야 하고, 또 마땅히 그래야지요.\"\n\n배그넷 씨가 노모를 향해 고개를 젓는데, 값진 보배를 하나 찾아냈다는 뜻이다.\n\n\"그럼 제가 가령 내일 아침 열 시 반쯤 들러볼까요? 음색 좋은 비올론첼로 몇 개의 값을 일러주실 수 있겠지요?\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n그보다 쉬운 일은 없다. 배그넷 부부는 둘 다 필요한 정보를 마련해두기로 약속하고, 심지어 검토하시라고 작은 재고를 그쪽에 좀 모아두는 것도 해볼 만하다고 서로 슬쩍 귀띔까지 한다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"고맙습니다. 안녕히 주무세요, 부인. 안녕히 주무세요, 주인장. 잘 자라, 귀염둥이들. 평생 보낸 가장 즐거운 저녁의 하나를 누리게 해주셔서 정말 감사합니다.\"\n\n그들은 그들대로, 그가 함께해준 즐거움에 대해 그에게 큰 신세를 졌다고 한다. 그래서 양쪽 모두 숱한 호의의 말을 주고받으며 헤어진다. \"자, 조지, 이 사람아.\" 가게 문 앞에서 그의 팔을 잡으며 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"가세!\" 그들이 작은 거리를 내려가고, 배그넷 부부가 잠시 멈춰 그 뒷모습을 바라보는 동안, 배그넷 부인은 의젓한 리그넘에게 버킷 씨가 \"조지한테 거의 들러붙다시피 하는 게, 정말로 그를 좋아하는 것 같다\"고 한마디 한다.\n\n근방의 거리들은 좁고 길이 험해서, 둘이 나란히 팔짱을 끼고 걷기에는 좀 불편하다. 그래서 조지 씨가 곧 한 줄로 걷자고 제안한다. 그러나 정다운 팔짱을 놓을 마음이 좀처럼 서지 않는 버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"잠깐만, 조지. 먼저 자네한테 할 말이 있어.\" 그러고는 곧장 그를 비틀어 어느 술집 안으로, 거실 안으로 데려가서는, 그와 마주 선 채 제 등을 문에 척 붙인다.\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"의무는 의무고, 우정은 우정이지. 난 어쩔 수 없는 게 아니라면 이 둘이 부딪치는 걸 결코 바라지 않아. 오늘 저녁 난 일을 즐겁게 만들려고 애썼고, 내가 그렇게 했는지 안 했는지는 자네 판단에 맡기지. 자네는 이제 체포된 몸이라고 여겨야겠네, 조지.\"\n\n\"체포? 무슨 죄로?\" 기병이 벼락 맞은 듯 되묻는다.\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 버킷 씨가 통통한 집게손가락으로 사태를 분별 있게 바라보라고 그를 다그치며 말한다. \"자네도 잘 알다시피, 의무는 의무고 잡담은 잡담이야. 자네가 무슨 말을 하든 그것이 자네한테 불리하게 쓰일 수 있다는 걸 알리는 게 내 의무지. 그러니, 조지, 말조심해. 혹시 살인 사건 얘기를 들은 적 있나?\"\n\n\"살인이라니!\"\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 버킷 씨가 집게손가락을 인상적으로 놀린 채 말한다. \"내가 한 말을 명심해. 난 자네한테 아무것도 묻지 않아. 자네는 오늘 오후 내내 기분이 처져 있었지. 다시 묻는데, 혹시 살인 사건 얘기를 들은 적 있나?\"\n\n\"아니. 어디서 살인이 났는데?\"\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"괜히 불리한 말을 하지 말게. 자네를 왜 찾는지 내가 일러주지. 링컨스 인 필즈에서 살인이 있었어—토킹혼이라는 이름의 신사가. 어젯밤 총에 맞았지. 그 일로 자네를 찾는 거야.\"\n\n기병은 뒤에 있던 의자에 털썩 주저앉고, 이마에는 굵은 땀방울이 솟아나며, 죽은 듯한 창백함이 얼굴을 뒤덮는다.\n\n\"버킷! 토킹혼 씨가 살해당했고, 자네가 나를 의심한다는 게 말이 되나?\"\n\n\"조지.\" 버킷 씨가 집게손가락을 계속 놀리며 대답한다. \"확실히 말이 되지, 실제로 그러니까. 이 일은 어젯밤 열 시에 벌어졌어. 그래, 자네는 어젯밤 열 시에 어디 있었는지 알 테고, 의심할 바 없이 그걸 입증할 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"어젯밤! 어젯밤이라고?\" 기병이 골똘히 되뇐다. 그러더니 퍼뜩 떠오른다. \"이런, 맙소사, 나 어젯밤 거기 있었어!\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 알고 있어, 조지.\" 버킷 씨가 아주 신중하게 대답한다. \"나도 그렇게 알고 있지. 마찬가지로 자네는 거기 자주 갔어. 그 근처를 어슬렁대는 게 목격됐고, 그와 언쟁을 벌이는 게 한두 번 들리지도 않았어. 그리고 어쩌면—분명 그렇다는 말은 아냐, 명심하게, 하지만 어쩌면—그가 자네더러 위협적이고 살기 어린 위험한 놈이라고 부르는 게 들렸을지도 몰라.\"\n\n기병은 말을 할 수만 있다면 그걸 죄다 인정하기라도 할 듯 숨을 헐떡인다.\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 버킷 씨가 모자를 가구상이나 할 법한 사무적인 태도로 탁자에 내려놓으며 잇는다. \"내 바람은, 오늘 저녁 내내 그래왔듯이, 일을 즐겁게 만드는 거야. 솔직히 말하지, 현상금이 걸렸어, 백 기니짜리로, 레스터 데드록 준남작 경이 내건 거지. 자네와 난 늘 즐겁게 지내온 사이지만, 난 다해야 할 의무가 있고, 그 백 기니를 누가 챙겨야 한다면, 다른 누구 못지않게 내가 챙기는 편이 나아. 이 모든 까닭에서, 내가 자네를 잡아야 한다는 게, 그리고 잡지 않으면 천벌을 받을 거라는 게 자네한테도 분명히 보였으면 해. 내가 누구 도움을 부를까, 아니면 일을 곱게 끝낼까?\"\n\n조지 씨는 정신을 가다듬고 군인처럼 일어선다. \"갑시다.\" 그가 말한다. \"준비됐소.\"\n\n\"조지.\" 버킷 씨가 잇는다. \"잠깐만!\" 마치 기병이 손볼 창문이라도 되는 양 그 가구상 같은 태도로, 그는 호주머니에서 수갑 한 벌을 꺼낸다. \"이건 중대한 혐의야, 조지, 그리고 이게 내 의무지.\"\n\n기병은 성이 나 얼굴이 벌게져 잠시 망설이지만, 두 손을 맞붙여 내밀고는 말한다. \"자! 채우게!\"\n\n버킷 씨가 단번에 그것을 채운다. \"어떤가? 편한가? 안 그러면 말하게, 난 내 의무에 어긋나지 않는 선에서 최대한 일을 편하게 만들고 싶고, 호주머니에 한 벌이 더 있으니까.\" 이 말을 그는 주문을 깔끔하게, 손님이 완벽히 만족하도록 처리하려 애쓰는 더없이 점잖은 장사꾼처럼 건넨다. \"이대로 괜찮겠나? 아주 좋아! 자, 보게, 조지\"—그는 구석에서 외투 하나를 가져와 기병의 목둘레에 둘러주기 시작한다—\"내가 나올 때 자네 기분을 헤아려서, 이걸 일부러 가져왔지. 자! 누가 알아채겠나?\"\n\n\"나만 알지.\" 기병이 대답한다. \"하지만 내가 아는 일이니, 한 번만 더 호의를 베풀어서 모자를 눈까지 끌어내려주게.\"\n\n\"이거 참, 정말로? 진심인가? 안됐잖아. 그렇게 보이니까.\"\n\n\"이런 걸 차고는 지나가는 사람들 얼굴을 똑바로 볼 수가 없어.\" 조지 씨가 다급히 대답한다. \"제발, 부디 모자를 앞으로 끌어내려주게.\"\n\n그토록 간곡히 청하니, 버킷 씨는 응하여, 제 모자를 쓰고는 제 노획물을 거리로 인도한다. 기병은 여느 때처럼 꿋꿋이 행진하되 다만 고개는 덜 꼿꼿하고, 버킷 씨는 팔꿈치로 그를 이끌어 길목을 건너고 모퉁이를 돈다.\n\n딜에서 집으로 돌아왔을 때 마침 캐디 젤리비(우리는 늘 그녀를 이렇게 불렀습니다)에게서 쪽지가 한 장 와 있었습니다. 얼마 전부터 몹시 약해진 그녀의 건강이 더 나빠졌으니, 와서 봐준다면 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 기쁘겠다는 내용이었습니다. 몇 줄짜리 쪽지로, 그녀가 누워 있는 긴 의자에서 쓴 것이었고, 남편이 쓴 다른 편지에 동봉되어 있었는데, 남편은 그 안에서 무척 애틋하게 아내의 부탁을 거들었습니다. 캐디는 이제 그토록 가여운 작은 아기의 어머니였고, 나는 그 아기의 대모였습니다. 모자 테두리 말고는 얼굴이라곤 거의 없어 보이는 조그맣고 늙은 티가 나는 핏덩이로, 가늘고 긴 손가락이 달린 작은 손을 늘 턱 아래 움켜쥐고 있었습니다. 아기는 하루 종일 이런 자세로 누워, 반짝이는 점 같은 두 눈을 뜨고서, (나는 늘 이렇게 상상했습니다만) 어쩌다 자기가 이렇게 작고 약하게 태어났는지 궁금해하는 듯했습니다. 자리를 옮길 때마다 울었지만, 그 밖의 모든 순간에는 어찌나 참을성이 많은지, 평생의 유일한 소망이 가만히 누워 생각하는 것인 듯싶었습니다. 얼굴에는 묘한 작은 검은 핏줄이 어렸고, 눈 밑에는 가여운 캐디가 잉크에 절어 지내던 나날의 희미한 기억 같은 묘하고 작은 검은 자국이 있어서, 통틀어 보면 익숙하지 않은 사람에게는 참으로 가엾고 애처로운 광경이었습니다.\n\n하지만 캐디로서는 자신이 그 아기에게 익숙하다는 것으로 충분했습니다. 병석에 누워 그녀가 시름을 달래며 세운 계획들, 어린 에스더의 교육과 어린 에스더의 결혼, 심지어 어린 에스더가 낳을 어린 에스더들의 할머니가 될 자신의 노년에 관한 계획들은, 이 생애의 자랑거리를 향한 헌신을 어찌나 어여쁘게 드러내던지, 나도 그중 몇 가지를 옮겨놓고 싶은 마음이 들 정도였습니다. 다만 안 그래도 이야기가 두서없이 흘러가고 있다는 걸 때맞춰 떠올린 덕분에 그러지 않았을 뿐입니다.\n\n다시 편지 이야기로 돌아가지요. 캐디는 나에 대해 일종의 미신 같은 믿음을 품고 있었는데, 오래전 그날 밤 내 무릎에 머리를 누이고 잠들었던 이래로 그 믿음이 마음속에서 점점 굳어졌습니다. 그녀는 내가 곁에 있기만 하면 자기에게 좋은 일이 생긴다고 거의—사실 완전히라고 해야겠지요—믿었습니다. 이것이 그 다정한 아가씨의 한낱 공상에 지나지 않아 입에 올리기조차 부끄럽긴 하지만, 그녀가 정말로 아플 때라면 그것이 사실만큼의 힘을 발휘할 수도 있는 노릇이었습니다. 그래서 나는 후견인의 허락을 받아 부랴부랴 캐디에게 달려갔고, 그녀와 프린스가 어찌나 나를 반기던지 그런 환대는 어디에도 없을 정도였습니다.\n\n이튿날 나는 다시 그녀 곁을 지키러 갔고, 그다음 날도 또 갔습니다. 아주 수월한 길이었으니, 아침에 조금만 일찍 일어나 장부를 정리하고 집안일을 챙긴 뒤 집을 나서기만 하면 되었기 때문입니다.\n\n그런데 이렇게 세 번째 다녀오자, 밤에 돌아온 나에게 후견인이 말했습니다. \"자, 꼬마 부인, 꼬마 부인, 이래서는 안 되겠어. 낙숫물이 끊임없이 떨어지면 돌도 닳는 법이고, 마차를 끊임없이 타다 보면 더든 아주머니도 닳아 없어지겠구나. 우리 한동안 런던에 가서 옛 하숙집을 차지하고 지내자꾸나.\"\n\n\"저 때문이라면 그러실 것 없어요, 후견인.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"저는 한 번도 피곤한 줄 모르거든요.\" 이건 한 치도 거짓이 아니었습니다. 나는 그렇게 필요한 사람이 된 것이 그저 더없이 기쁠 따름이었습니다.\n\n\"그럼 나를 위해서다.\" 후견인이 받았습니다. \"아니면 에이다를 위해서거나, 우리 둘 다를 위해서지. 내 기억에 내일이 누군가의 생일이지 싶은데.\"\n\n\"정말 그러네요.\" 나는 내일이면 스물한 살이 되는 사랑하는 친구에게 입을 맞추며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래.\" 후견인이 반은 즐겁게, 반은 진지하게 말했습니다. \"그건 대단한 날이지. 내 어여쁜 사촌이 독립을 주장하느라 처리해야 할 일도 좀 생길 테고, 그러면 런던이 우리 모두에게 더 편한 곳이 되겠구나. 그러니 런던으로 가자. 그건 그렇게 정해두고, 또 한 가지가 있다—캐디는 어떤 상태로 두고 왔니?\"\n\n\"몹시 안 좋아요, 후견인. 건강과 기력을 되찾으려면 한참 걸릴까 봐 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"한참이라면 어느 정도를 말하는 거냐?\" 후견인이 생각에 잠겨 물었습니다.\n\n\"몇 주는 될 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 후견인은 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 방 안을 거닐기 시작했습니다. 자기도 그만큼은 생각하고 있었다는 표시였습니다. \"그래, 그 집 의사는 어떠냐? 좋은 의사니, 얘야?\"\n\n나는 달리 흠잡을 만한 건 모르겠다고 인정할 수밖에 없었습니다. 다만 바로 그날 저녁 프린스와 나는, 누군가가 그 의사의 소견을 확인해주면 좋겠다고 의견을 모은 참이라고 했습니다.\n\n\"그래, 알다시피,\" 후견인이 얼른 받았습니다. \"우드코트가 있잖니.\"\n\n나는 그런 뜻으로 한 말이 아니어서 적잖이 놀랐습니다. 한순간 우드코트 씨와 관련해 마음속에 담아두었던 모든 것이 되살아나 나를 어지럽히는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"그 사람한테 무슨 거리낌이라도 있니, 꼬마 부인?\"\n\n\"거리낌이라뇨, 후견인? 아, 그럴 리가요!\"\n\n\"그럼 환자가 그 사람을 꺼리진 않을 것 같으냐?\"\n\n꺼리기는커녕, 나는 그녀가 그를 크게 의지할 채비가 되어 있고 무척 좋아하리라는 것을 의심치 않았습니다. 나는 그가 그녀에게 낯선 사람이 아니라고 말했습니다. 플라이트 양을 친절히 보살피는 모습을 그녀가 여러 번 보았으니까요.\n\n\"아주 좋아.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그 사람이 오늘 여기 다녀갔다, 얘야. 내일 그 일로 만나보마.\"\n\n이 짧은 대화를 나누면서 나는 느꼈습니다—어떻게 느꼈는지는 모르겠습니다. 그녀는 잠자코 있었고, 우리는 눈길도 주고받지 않았으니까요—내 사랑하는 친구가, 캐디의 손이 아니면 아무도 그 작별의 정표를 내게 가져다주지 못하던 그날, 얼마나 즐겁게 내 허리를 끌어안았는지 또렷이 기억하고 있다는 것을요. 그래서 나는 그녀에게도, 또 캐디에게도 내가 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되리라는 것을 알려야 한다고 느꼈습니다. 그 사실을 더 이상 숨긴다면, 그 집 주인의 사랑을 받을 자격이 내 스스로의 눈에 덜한 사람이 될지도 모를 일이었으니까요. 그래서 우리가 위층으로 올라가, 오로지 나만이 사랑하는 친구의 생일을 맨 먼저 축하하고 그녀를 가슴에 품을 수 있도록, 시계가 열두 시를 칠 때까지 귀 기울이며 기다린 뒤, 나는 내가 스스로에게 말했던 그대로 그녀에게도 일러주었습니다. 사촌 존의 선량함과 고결함을, 그리고 나를 기다리는 행복한 삶을 말입니다. 우리가 함께한 모든 시간을 통틀어 사랑하는 친구가 다른 어느 때보다 나를 더 애틋하게 여긴 적이 있다면, 그건 분명 그날 밤이었습니다. 그리고 나는 그것을 알게 되어 너무나 기뻤고, 이 마지막 부질없는 망설임을 떨쳐버린 것이 옳은 일이었다는 생각에 너무나 마음이 놓여서, 전보다 열 배는 더 행복했습니다. 몇 시간 전만 해도 그것을 망설임이라 여기지도 않았건만, 막상 사라지고 나니 나는 그 본질을 더 잘 이해하게 된 듯한 기분이었습니다.\n\n이튿날 우리는 런던으로 갔습니다. 옛 하숙집이 비어 있는 것을 확인하고는, 반 시간 만에 마치 한 번도 떠난 적 없었던 것처럼 조용히 자리를 잡았습니다. 우드코트 씨가 사랑하는 친구의 생일을 축하하러 와서 우리와 함께 저녁을 들었고, 우리는 그런 자리에 리처드가 없으면 으레 생기는 커다란 빈자리를 안고서도 할 수 있는 한 즐겁게 지냈습니다. 그날 이후 몇 주 동안—내 기억으로는 여덟이나 아홉 주쯤—나는 캐디와 거의 붙어 지냈고, 그러다 보니 이 무렵 나는, 우리가 처음 함께한 이래 내가 앓아누웠던 때를 빼면 어느 때보다도 에이다를 적게 보게 되었습니다. 에이다는 캐디네 집에 자주 왔지만, 거기서 우리가 할 일은 그녀를 즐겁게 해주고 기운을 북돋우는 것이었으므로, 평소처럼 속내를 터놓고 이야기하지는 못했습니다. 밤에 집에 돌아가면 우리는 늘 함께였지만, 캐디는 통증 때문에 잠을 깊이 못 잤고, 나는 그녀를 간호하느라 그 집에 남는 일이 잦았습니다.\n\n사랑할 남편과 가여운 핏덩이 같은 아기가 있고, 또 그들을 위해 가꿔야 할 가정이 있으니, 캐디는 참으로 갸륵한 사람이었습니다! 그토록 자신을 희생하고, 그토록 불평이 없고, 그들을 위해 그토록 낫고 싶어 하고, 폐 끼치는 것을 그토록 두려워하고, 도와주는 이 없이 홀로 애쓰는 남편의 노고와 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 안락을 그토록 배려하다니, 나는 지금에서야 비로소 그녀의 가장 좋은 면모를 알게 되었습니다. 그리고 어찌나 묘하던지요. 춤이 곧 생업인 곳, 무도장에서 작은 바이올린과 견습생들이 아침마다 일찍부터 시작하는 곳, 지저분한 어린 사내아이가 오후 내내 부엌에서 혼자 왈츠를 추는 그곳에, 그녀의 창백한 얼굴과 무력한 몸이 날마다 누워 있다는 것이 말입니다.\n\n캐디의 부탁으로 나는 그녀의 방을 도맡아 관리하며 말끔히 정돈했고, 긴 의자째로 그녀를 밀어, 지금껏 차지하고 있던 곳보다 더 밝고 바람 잘 통하고 더 명랑한 구석으로 옮겨주었습니다. 그러고는 날마다 우리가 가장 단정한 차림을 하고 나면, 나는 나와 이름이 같은 그 자그마하고 자그마한 아기를 그녀의 품에 안겨주고서, 곁에 앉아 이야기를 나누거나 바느질을 하거나 책을 읽어주곤 했습니다. 이렇게 조용한 시간 중에서도 거의 첫 무렵에, 나는 캐디에게 황폐한 집 이야기를 들려주었습니다.\n\n에이다 말고도 다른 손님들이 있었습니다. 우선 프린스가 있었지요. 그는 교습 사이사이 짬이 날 때면 살그머니 들어와 살그머니 앉아서는, 캐디와 그 조그마한 아기를 향한 사랑 어린 근심으로 가득한 얼굴을 하고 있었습니다. 캐디의 상태가 실제로 어떻든 간에, 그녀는 프린스에게는 늘 거의 다 나았다고 한결같이 말했습니다—그리고 나도, 하늘이 용서하시길, 한결같이 그 말을 거들었습니다. 그러면 프린스는 어찌나 기분이 좋아지던지, 이따금 주머니에서 작은 바이올린을 꺼내 한두 가락 켜며 아기를 놀라게 해보려 했지만, 내가 보기에 아기가 조금이라도 놀라는 일은 한 번도 없었습니다. 나와 이름이 같은 그 자그마한 아기는 아예 거들떠보지도 않았으니까요.\n\n다음으로 젤리비 부인이 있었습니다. 부인은 이따금, 여느 때처럼 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 태도로 와서는, 마치 멀고 먼 고향 땅의 어린 보리오불라 사람에게 온통 정신이 팔리기라도 한 듯, 손주를 까마득히 지나친 어딘가를 태연히 바라보며 앉아 있곤 했습니다. 여전히 눈을 반짝이며, 여전히 평온하게, 여전히 단정치 못한 차림으로 부인은 말하곤 했습니다. \"그래, 캐디야, 오늘은 좀 어떠니?\" 그러고는 상냥하게 미소 지으며 앉아 대답에는 아랑곳하지 않거나, 아니면 사뿐히 미끄러지듯 화제를 바꿔 요사이 받고 답한 편지가 몇 통이나 되는지, 혹은 보리오불라가의 커피 산출 능력이 어느 정도인지 따져보는 데로 흘러가곤 했습니다. 부인은 늘 우리의 좁디좁은 활동 영역을 향한, 도무지 감출 수 없는 평온한 경멸을 품고 이렇게 했습니다.\n\n다음으로 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 있었습니다. 그는 아침부터 밤까지, 밤부터 아침까지 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 조심거리의 대상이었습니다. 아기가 울면, 그 소리에 그가 불편해질세라 거의 숨이 막힐 지경으로 달랬습니다. 밤중에 불을 뒤적여야 하면, 그의 안식이 깨질세라 몰래 했습니다. 캐디가 집 안에 있는 자그마한 편의품 하나라도 필요로 하면, 먼저 그도 그것을 필요로 할 법한지부터 조심스레 의논했습니다. 이런 배려에 대한 보답으로 그는 하루에 한 번 방에 들어왔는데, 그 방에 거의 축복을 내리다시피 했습니다—어깨가 솟은 자신의 존재에서 흘러나오는 빛을 베풀면서, 어찌나 은혜를 베푸는 듯한 겸양과 후원과 우아한 태도를 보이던지, 내가 (속사정을 몰랐더라면) 그를 캐디의 삶에 은혜를 베푼 사람으로 여겼을 정도였습니다.\n\n\"내 캐롤라인.\" 그는 그녀에게 몸을 굽힌다고 굽혀 그나마 가장 가까이 다가서며 말하곤 했습니다. \"오늘은 좀 나아졌다고 말해주렴.\"\n\n\"아, 훨씬 나아졌어요, 고맙습니다, 터비드롭 씨.\" 캐디가 대답하곤 했습니다.\n\n\"기쁘구나! 더없이 기쁘다! 그리고 우리의 사랑스러운 서머슨 양. 피곤에 아주 지쳐 쓰러진 건 아니겠지?\" 이 대목에서 그는 눈꺼풀을 잔뜩 찡그리며 나에게 손끝으로 입맞춤을 보내곤 했습니다. 그래도 내가 그토록 달라진 뒤로는 나에 대한 살가운 관심을 거두었다는 것을 다행으로 여기며 말씀드립니다.\n\n\"전혀 아니에요.\" 나는 그를 안심시키곤 했습니다.\n\n\"훌륭하구나! 우리의 사랑스러운 캐롤라인을 잘 보살펴야 해요, 서머슨 양. 저 아이를 낫게 하는 일이라면 무엇 하나 아껴서는 안 됩니다. 잘 먹여 기운을 차리게 해야지요. 내 사랑하는 캐롤라인\"—그는 한없는 너그러움과 비호의 태도로 며느리에게로 몸을 돌리곤 했습니다—\"무엇이든 부족함이 없도록 해라, 얘야. 원하는 게 있거든 말하고 그대로 이루렴, 내 딸아. 이 집에 있는 모든 것, 내 방에 있는 모든 것이 다 네 것이란다, 얘야. 행여,\" 그는 이따금 격식이 북받쳐 이렇게 덧붙이곤 했습니다. \"내 소박한 필요가 어느 때든 네 필요와 부딪히거든, 그것일랑 아예 헤아리지도 말려무나, 내 캐롤라인. 네 필요가 내 것보다 더 중하니까.\"\n\n그는 이런 격식 차린 태도(그의 아들이 어머니에게서 물려받은 그 유산)에 대해 어찌나 오래도록 굳어진 권리를 누리고 있던지, 나는 이렇게 애정 어린 자기희생을 늘어놓는 그를 보고 캐디와 그녀의 남편이 눈물을 흘리는 광경을 여러 번 보았습니다.\n\n\"아니란다, 얘들아.\" 그는 만류하곤 했습니다. 그가 그렇게 말할 때 캐디의 가느다란 팔이 그의 살진 목을 감는 것을 보면, 나도 마음이 녹곤 했습니다. 같은 연유는 아니었지만요. \"아니야, 아니야! 나는 결코 너희를 떠나지 않겠다고 약속했어. 나에게 도리를 다하고 정을 베풀어다오, 그 외에 다른 보답은 바라지 않으마. 자, 너희에게 복이 있기를! 나는 공원에 다녀오마.\"\n\n그는 곧 거기서 바람을 쐬며 호텔 저녁 식사를 위한 입맛을 돋우곤 했습니다. 내가 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 부당하게 깎아내리는 일이 없기를 바랍니다만, 나는 여기 충실히 적은 것들 말고는 그에게서 더 나은 면모를 본 적이 없습니다. 다만 한 가지, 그가 피피를 분명히 마음에 들어 해서, 그 아이를 데리고 거창하게 산책을 나가곤 했다는 점은 빼고 말이지요. 그럴 때면 그는 늘 자기가 저녁을 들러 가기 전에 아이를 집으로 돌려보냈고, 이따금 그 아이 주머니에 반 페니쯤 넣어주기도 했습니다. 하지만 이런 무사무욕한 행동조차도, 내가 아는 한 적잖은 비용이 따랐습니다. 피피가 격식의 대가와 손잡고 걸어도 될 만큼 말끔히 차려입으려면, 캐디와 그녀 남편의 돈으로 머리끝부터 발끝까지 새로 입혀야 했으니까요.\n\n우리 손님들 가운데 마지막으로 젤리비 씨가 있었습니다. 정말이지 그가 저녁에 들어와, 온순한 목소리로 캐디에게 좀 어떠냐고 묻고는, 벽에 머리를 기대고 앉아 그 이상 무슨 말을 하려는 기색도 없을 때, 나는 그가 무척 좋았습니다. 내가 부산히 자그마한 일을 하고 있는 것을 보면, 그는 가끔 무슨 대단한 힘이라도 보태려는 듯 외투를 반쯤 벗기도 했습니다. 하지만 그 이상으로는 결코 나아가지 못했지요. 그의 유일한 소일거리는 벽에 머리를 기대고 앉아, 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 아기를 뚫어지게 바라보는 것이었습니다. 그래서 나는 그 둘이 서로 통하는 데가 있다는 공상을 좀처럼 떨쳐버릴 수가 없었습니다.\n\n우드코트 씨는 우리 손님으로 꼽지 않았는데, 이제 그가 캐디의 단골 주치의가 되었기 때문입니다. 그녀는 그의 보살핌 아래 곧 차도를 보이기 시작했는데, 그가 어찌나 다정하고, 어찌나 솜씨가 좋고, 그토록 지치는 기색 없이 정성을 쏟던지, 그건 당연한 일이었지요. 이 무렵 나는 우드코트 씨를 꽤 자주 보았습니다. 다만 사람들이 짐작할 만큼은 아니었는데, 캐디가 그의 손에 맡겨져 안전하다는 것을 알았으므로, 나는 그가 올 시각이 되면 슬그머니 집으로 빠져나가는 일이 잦았기 때문입니다. 그래도 우리는 자주 마주쳤습니다. 이제 나는 나 자신과 충분히 화해한 상태였지만, 그가 나를 가엾이 여긴다고 생각하면 여전히 기뻤고, 그가 여전히 나를 가엾이 여긴다고 나는 믿었습니다. 그는 배저 씨의 진료 일을 거들고 있었는데, 그 일이 워낙 많았습니다. 그리고 앞날에 대해서는 아직 정해진 계획이 없었습니다.\n\n캐디가 회복되기 시작할 무렵, 나는 사랑하는 친구에게서 어떤 변화를 알아차리기 시작했습니다. 그것이 처음에 어떻게 내 눈에 들어왔는지는 말하기 어렵습니다. 그 자체로는 아무것도 아니어서 여럿이 모여야만 비로소 무언가가 되는 자잘한 정황들을 통해 알아차렸기 때문입니다. 하지만 그것들을 하나하나 맞춰본 끝에 나는, 에이다가 예전만큼 나에게 거리낌 없이 명랑하지 않다는 것을 알아냈습니다. 나를 향한 그녀의 다정함은 여전히 애틋하고 진실했습니다. 그것은 한순간도 의심하지 않았습니다. 다만 그녀에게는 나에게 털어놓지 않는 조용한 슬픔이 어려 있었고, 그 속에서 나는 어떤 감춰진 회한의 자취를 더듬을 수 있었습니다.\n\n그런데 나는 이것을 도무지 이해할 수가 없었고, 내 소중한 친구의 행복이 너무나 걱정스러운 나머지, 마음이 적잖이 불편해져 자주 생각에 잠기게 되었습니다. 마침내 에이다가 이 무언가를, 그것이 나까지 불행하게 만들세라 나에게 감추고 있는 것이 틀림없다고 확신하게 되자, 문득 이런 생각이 들었습니다. 그녀가—나를 위해서—내가 황폐한 집에 관해 들려준 이야기 때문에 조금 슬퍼하고 있는 것이라고요.\n\n내가 어떻게 이것을 그럴듯하다고 스스로를 설득했는지는 모르겠습니다. 내가 그렇게 한 데에 어떤 이기적인 속내가 있다고는 생각지 못했습니다. 나는 나 자신 때문에 슬프지 않았으니까요. 나는 더없이 만족스럽고 더없이 행복했습니다. 그래도 에이다가—나를 위해서, 나는 그런 생각을 다 버렸건만—한때는 그러했으나 이제는 모두 달라져버린 것을 떠올리고 있을지도 모른다는 것은, 너무나 믿기 쉬워서 나는 그만 그것을 믿어버렸습니다.\n\n사랑하는 친구를 안심시키고 나에게 그런 마음이 없다는 것을 보여주려면 어떻게 해야 할까(그때 나는 곰곰이 생각했습니다)? 글쎄요! 나는 그저 최대한 활기차고 분주하게 지낼 수밖에 없었고, 그건 줄곧 애써온 일이기도 했습니다. 다만 캐디의 병 때문에 내 집안일이 이러나저러나 다소 방해를 받은 건 분명했지요—그래도 나는 늘 아침이면 거기서 후견인의 아침상을 차렸고, 그는 백 번도 더 웃으며 꼬마 부인이 둘은 있어야겠다고, 자기 꼬마 부인은 한 번도 자리를 비운 적이 없으니 그렇다고 말했지만요. 어쨌든 나는 갑절로 부지런하고 명랑해지기로 마음먹었습니다. 그래서 나는 아는 노래란 노래는 다 흥얼거리며 집 안을 돌아다녔고, 죽기 살기로 바느질을 하고 또 했으며, 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 이야기하고 또 이야기했습니다.\n\n그런데도 나와 사랑하는 친구 사이에는 여전히 똑같은 그늘이 드리워 있었습니다.\n\n\"자, 더든 아주머니.\" 어느 날 밤, 우리 셋이 함께 있을 때 후견인이 읽던 책을 덮으며 말했습니다. \"그래, 우드코트가 캐디 젤리비를 다시 온전히 삶을 누릴 수 있게 되돌려놓았다고?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그리고 그녀 같은 사람의 고마움으로 보답받는다면 그건 부자가 되는 일이지요, 후견인.\"\n\n\"진심으로 그랬으면 좋겠구나.\" 그가 받았습니다.\n\n사실 나도 그랬으면 했습니다. 나도 그렇다고 말했지요.\n\n\"그래! 방법만 안다면 우리가 그를 유대인만큼이나 부자로 만들어줄 텐데. 안 그러냐, 꼬마 부인?\"\n\n나는 바느질을 하며 웃었고, 그건 잘 모르겠다고 대답했습니다. 그러면 그를 망쳐놓을지도 모르고, 그러면 그가 그리 쓸모 있는 사람이 못 될지도 모르며, 그가 없으면 곤란해질 사람도 많을 테니까요. 플라이트 양이며, 캐디 본인이며, 그 밖에도 많은 이들이 말입니다.\n\n\"맞는 말이다.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"내가 그걸 잊었구나. 하지만 그래도 먹고살 만큼은 부자로 만들어주는 데는 다들 동의하겠지? 그럭저럭 마음 편히 일할 만큼은 부자로? 자기만의 행복한 가정과 자기만의 가정의 수호신들을—어쩌면 가정의 여신까지도—거느릴 만큼은 부자로 말이다.\"\n\n그건 전혀 다른 이야기라고 나는 말했습니다. 그건 우리 모두 동의해야 한다고요.\n\n\"물론이지.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"우리 모두 말이다. 나는 우드코트를 무척 아끼고, 그를 높이 평가한단다. 그래서 그의 계획에 대해 조심스레 떠보고 있었지. 그가 지닌 그 올곧은 자존심을 가진 독립적인 사람에게 도움을 건네기란 어려운 일이야. 그래도 할 수만 있다면, 또 방법만 안다면 기꺼이 그러고 싶구나. 그는 또 한 번 항해를 떠날 마음이 반쯤 있는 듯하더라. 하지만 그건 그런 사람을 내쳐버리는 일 같기도 하고.\"\n\n\"새로운 세상이 그에게 열릴지도 모르지요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지, 꼬마 부인.\" 후견인이 동의했습니다. \"그가 이 낡은 세상에 큰 기대를 거는 것 같지도 않고. 너도 아니? 나는 그가 이 세상에서 어떤 각별한 실망이나 불운을 겪은 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들 때가 있단다. 그런 일에 대해 무슨 이야기를 들은 적은 없니?\"\n\n나는 고개를 가로저었습니다.\n\n\"흠.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"내가 잘못 짚었나 보다.\" 여기서 잠시 침묵이 흘렀는데, 나는 사랑하는 친구를 위해서라도 그 틈을 메우는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 바느질을 하면서 후견인이 좋아하는 곡조 하나를 흥얼거렸습니다.\n\n\"그래서 우드코트 씨가 또 항해를 떠날 거라고 보세요?\" 나는 그 곡을 처음부터 끝까지 나직이 흥얼거리고 나서 그에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"뭐라 단정하기는 어렵구나, 얘야. 하지만 지금으로서는 그가 다른 나라로 긴 여행을 떠날 공산이 크다고 봐야겠지.\"\n\n\"그가 어디로 가든 우리 모두의 진심 어린 축원이 함께할 거예요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그게 재물은 아니지만, 그래도 그것 때문에 그가 더 가난해지는 일은 결코 없을 거예요, 후견인. 적어도 그건 분명하지요.\"\n\n\"결코 그럴 일은 없지, 꼬마 부인.\" 그가 대답했습니다.\n\n나는 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었는데, 이제 그곳은 후견인 의자 옆이었습니다. 그 편지가 오기 전까지는 그게 내 늘 앉던 자리가 아니었지만, 이제는 그랬습니다. 나는 맞은편에 앉은 에이다를 올려다보았고, 그녀가 나를 바라볼 때 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 고이고 그 눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 보았습니다. 나는 단번에 차분하고 명랑해지기만 하면 사랑하는 친구의 오해를 풀어주고 그 사랑 가득한 마음을 편안하게 해줄 수 있다고 느꼈습니다. 나는 정말로 그러했으므로, 그저 나 자신답게 있기만 하면 되었습니다.\n\n그래서 나는 사랑스러운 친구를 내 어깨에 기대게 했습니다—그녀의 마음을 짓누르는 것이 무엇인지는 까맣게 모른 채 말이지요!—그러고는 몸이 좀 안 좋은 거라고 하며 그녀를 팔로 감싸 위층으로 데려갔습니다. 우리 둘만의 방에 들어와, 어쩌면 그녀가 내가 도무지 들을 채비가 되어 있지 않은 그 이야기를 털어놓았을지도 모를 그때, 나는 그녀가 내게 속내를 털어놓도록 부추기지 않았습니다. 그녀에게 그럴 필요가 있다고는 생각조차 못 했으니까요.\n\n\"아, 사랑하는 착한 에스더.\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"너랑 사촌 존이 함께 있을 때, 두 사람한테 말할 결심만 설 수 있다면!\"\n\n\"왜 그래, 얘?\" 내가 만류했습니다. \"에이다, 왜 우리한테 말을 못 한다는 거니!\"\n\n에이다는 그저 고개를 떨구고 나를 가슴에 더 꼭 끌어안았습니다.\n\n\"넌 설마 잊지 않았겠지, 내 어여쁜 친구야.\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"우리가 얼마나 조용하고 예스러운 사람들인지, 또 내가 어떻게 더없이 신중한 안주인으로 자리를 잡았는지 말이야. 내 삶이 얼마나 행복하고 평온하게 다 정해져 있는지, 또 누구에 의해 그런지도 잊지 않았겠지? 분명 너는 얼마나 고결한 분에 의해서인지 잊지 않았을 거야, 에이다. 그건 결코 잊을 수 없는 일이지.\"\n\n\"응, 결코 잊지 않았어, 에스더.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 얘야,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"잘못될 일이라곤 아무것도 없잖니—그러니 너는 왜 우리한테 말을 못 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"잘못될 일이 없다고, 에스더?\" 에이다가 받았습니다. \"아, 지난 그 모든 세월을 생각하면, 그분의 아버지 같은 보살핌과 다정함을, 우리 사이의 그 오랜 정을, 또 너를 생각하면, 나는 어떡하면 좋아, 어떡하면 좋아!\"\n\n나는 다소 의아한 눈으로 내 아이를 바라보았지만, 그녀를 위로하는 것 말고는 달리 대답하지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 우리가 함께한 삶의 자잘한 추억들로 화제를 돌려 그녀가 더 말하지 못하게 막았습니다. 그녀가 잠자리에 누운 뒤에야, 그제야 비로소 나는 후견인에게 가서 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸고, 그런 다음 다시 에이다에게 돌아와 잠시 그녀 곁에 앉아 있었습니다.\n\n그녀는 잠들어 있었고, 나는 그녀를 바라보면서 그녀가 조금 달라졌다고 생각했습니다. 요즘 들어 한두 번 그런 생각을 한 게 아니었지요. 그녀가 의식 없이 누워 있는 모습을 들여다보면서도, 나는 그녀가 어떻게 달라졌는지 도무지 가늠할 수가 없었습니다. 다만 낯익은 그 얼굴의 아름다움 속 무언가가 나에게는 달라 보였습니다. 그녀와 리처드에 대한 후견인의 옛 바람이 내 마음속에 서글프게 떠올랐고, 나는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸습니다. \"이 아이가 그 사람 때문에 마음을 졸여왔구나.\" 그리고 나는 그 사랑이 어떻게 끝날지 헤아려보았습니다.\n\n캐디가 앓는 동안 그 집에서 돌아오면, 나는 에이다가 무언가를 바느질하고 있는 것을 자주 보았는데, 그녀는 늘 그 바느질감을 치워버렸고, 그래서 나는 그게 무엇인지 한 번도 알지 못했습니다. 지금 그중 일부가 그녀 곁의 서랍 안에 놓여 있었는데, 서랍이 완전히 닫혀 있지 않았습니다. 나는 서랍을 열지는 않았지만, 그래도 그 바느질감이 대체 무엇일까 적잖이 궁금했습니다. 그것이 그녀 자신을 위한 것이 아님은 분명했으니까요.\n\n그리고 사랑하는 친구에게 입을 맞추면서 나는, 그녀가 한 손을 베개 밑에 넣어 보이지 않게 한 채 누워 있는 것을 알아챘습니다.\n\n내가 그들이 생각한 것보다, 또 내가 스스로 생각한 것보다 얼마나 덜 사랑스러운 사람이었던가요. 내 자신의 명랑함과 만족에만 그토록 골몰한 나머지, 사랑하는 친구를 바로잡고 그 마음을 평안하게 하는 일이 오로지 나에게 달려 있다고 여겼으니 말입니다!\n\n하지만 나는 스스로를 속인 채, 그 믿음 속에 자리에 누웠습니다. 그리고 이튿날에도 그 믿음 속에서 잠을 깼지만, 나와 사랑하는 친구 사이에는 여전히 똑같은 그늘이 드리워 있다는 것을 알게 되었을 뿐입니다.\n\n우드코트 씨는 런던에 도착하자 바로 그날로 사이먼드 법학원에 있는 볼스 씨를 찾아갔습니다. 내가 그에게 리처드의 친구가 되어달라고 간청한 그 순간부터, 그는 단 한 번도 약속을 소홀히 하거나 잊은 적이 없었습니다. 그는 그 부탁을 신성한 책무로 받아들였다고 나에게 말했고, 언제나 그 정신에 충실했습니다.\n\n그는 사무실에서 볼스 씨를 만나, 리처드의 주소를 알아내려고 들르기로 한 약속을 볼스 씨에게 알렸습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"C 씨 주소는 여기서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않았습니다, 선생님. C 씨 주소는 여기서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않았어요. 자리에 앉으시겠습니까, 선생님?\"\n\n우드코트 씨는 볼스 씨에게 고맙다고 했지만, 앞서 말한 것 외에는 그와 볼일이 없었습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생님. 제 생각에 선생님은,\" 볼스 씨가 주소를 알려주지 않은 채 여전히 조용하게 자리를 권하며 말했습니다. \"C 씨에게 영향력이 있는 듯합니다. 사실 저는 선생님께 그런 영향력이 있다는 걸 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"나 자신은 몰랐는데요.\" 우드코트 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"하지만 당신이 제일 잘 안다니 그런가 봅니다.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 볼스 씨가 여느 때처럼 목소리까지 차분하게 다잡으며 받았습니다. \"제일 잘 아는 것이 제 직업적 의무의 한 부분입니다. 자기 이익을 저에게 맡긴 신사를 연구하고 이해하는 것이 제 직업적 의무의 한 부분이지요. 제가 아는 한, 선생님, 저는 직업적 의무를 게을리하지 않습니다. 좋은 뜻을 품고도 모르는 사이에 게을리할 수는 있겠지요. 하지만 제가 안다면, 선생님, 그러지 않습니다.\"\n\n우드코트 씨가 다시 주소를 언급했습니다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 선생님.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"잠시만 참아주십시오. 선생님, C 씨는 상당한 판돈을 걸고 게임을 하고 있는데, 그러려면 빠질 수 없는 것이 있습니다 — 그게 무엇인지 제가 굳이 말해야 할까요?\"\n\n\"돈, 이겠지요?\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"솔직하게 말씀드리면(솔직함은 제 황금률입니다, 그것으로 득을 보든 손해를 보든 말이지요. 그런데 보통은 손해를 보더군요), 돈이 바로 그 말입니다. 자, 선생님, C 씨의 게임이 어찌 될지에 대해 저는 아무 의견도 드리지 않겠습니다, 아무 의견도요. 그토록 오래, 그토록 크게 걸어온 마당에 C 씨가 손을 떼는 것이 대단히 어리석을 수도 있고, 그 반대일 수도 있겠지요. 저는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 그렇습니다, 선생님.\" 볼스 씨가 단호하게 손바닥을 책상에 탁 내리치며 말했습니다. \"아무 말도요.\"\n\n\"잊으신 모양인데요.\" 우드코트 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"나는 당신에게 아무 말도 하지 말라고 부탁했고, 당신이 무슨 말을 하든 관심이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 선생님!\" 볼스 씨가 받아쳤습니다. \"스스로 부당하게 구시는군요. 아니요, 선생님! 실례지만! 제가 아는 한 — 제 사무실에서 — 스스로를 부당하게 대하시게 둘 수 없습니다. 선생님은 친구와 관련된 일이라면 무엇이든, 모든 것에 관심이 있으십니다. 선생님, 저는 인간 본성을 훨씬 더 잘 압니다. 선생님 같은 풍모의 신사가 친구와 관계된 일에 관심이 없다고는 한순간도 인정할 수 없지요.\"\n\n\"뭐,\" 우드코트 씨가 대답했습니다. \"그럴 수도 있겠지요. 나는 특히 그의 주소에 관심이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"호수는, 선생님,\" 볼스 씨가 덧붙이듯 말했습니다. \"이미 말씀드린 것 같습니다. C 씨가 이 상당한 판돈을 걸고 계속 게임을 하려면, 선생님, 자금이 있어야 합니다. 제 말을 이해해주십시오! 지금 당장은 수중에 자금이 있습니다. 저는 아무것도 요구하지 않습니다. 수중에 자금이 있어요. 하지만 게임을 계속하려면 더 많은 자금을 마련해야 합니다. 그러지 않으면 C 씨가 이미 건 것을 다 내던지는 셈이 되지요. 그건 전적으로, 오로지 C 씨가 고려할 문제입니다만. 이 점을, 선생님, 저는 C 씨의 친구이신 선생님께 이렇게 솔직하게 말씀드릴 기회로 삼습니다. 자금이 없어도 저는 언제든 기꺼이 C 씨를 위해 나서고 행동하겠습니다 — 재산에서 안전하게 인정받을 수 있는 비용의 범위까지만요, 그 이상은 아닙니다. 그 이상은, 선생님, 누군가에게 해를 끼치지 않고는 할 수 없습니다. 저는 사랑하는 세 딸이나, 톤턴 골짜기에서 전적으로 저에게 의지하는 노쇠한 아버지나, 아니면 누군가에게 해를 끼쳐야 하지요. 그런데, 선생님, 제 결심은(약하다거나 어리석다고 하셔도 좋습니다) 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않는 것입니다.\"\n\n우드코트 씨는 다소 엄하게, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘다고 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"저는, 선생님,\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"좋은 이름을 남기고 싶습니다. 그래서 C 씨가 어떤 처지에 있는지 그분 친구분께 솔직하게 말씀드릴 기회를 빠짐없이 잡는 것이지요. 저 자신으로 말하자면, 선생님, 일꾼이 품삯을 받는 건 당연합니다. 제가 수레바퀴에 어깨를 들이대기로 했으면 그렇게 하고, 받는 만큼 벌어들입니다. 저는 그 목적으로 여기 있는 것이지요. 바깥문에 제 이름이 칠해져 있는 것도 그 목적에서입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 카스톤 씨 주소는요, 볼스 씨?\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 볼스 씨가 대답했습니다. \"이미 말씀드린 것 같습니다만, 바로 옆집입니다. 이 층에 가시면 C 씨 거처가 있습니다. C 씨가 자기 법률 자문 가까이 있고 싶어 하시는데, 저는 결코 마다하지 않습니다. 저는 오히려 조사받기를 청하니까요.\"\n\n이 말을 듣고 우드코트 씨는 볼스 씨에게 작별 인사를 하고 리처드를 찾아 나섰습니다. 그는 이제 리처드의 달라진 모습을 너무도 잘 이해하기 시작했습니다.\n\n그는 칙칙한 방에서, 빛바랜 가구가 놓인 그 방에서 리처드를 만났습니다. 얼마 전 내가 막사 같은 그의 방에서 보았던 모습과 거의 같았지만, 다만 그는 글을 쓰고 있지 않고 앞에 책을 펼쳐둔 채 앉아 있었습니다. 그 책에서 그의 눈과 생각은 저 멀리 헤매고 있었지요. 마침 문이 열려 있던 터라, 우드코트 씨는 잠시 그에게 들키지 않은 채 그 앞에 서 있었습니다. 그는 리처드가 몽상에서 깨어나기 전 그 수척한 얼굴과 풀 죽은 태도를 결코 잊을 수 없었다고 나에게 말했습니다.\n\n\"우드코트, 이 친구야.\" 리처드가 두 손을 내밀며 벌떡 일어나 외쳤습니다. \"자네는 유령처럼 내 눈앞에 나타나는군.\"\n\n\"친절한 유령이지.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"그리고 유령이 으레 그러듯, 누가 말 걸어주기만 기다리고 있었네. 인간 세상은 어떻게 돌아가나?\" 이제 두 사람은 가까이 마주 앉았습니다.\n\n\"꽤나 나쁘게, 꽤나 더디게 돌아가지.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"적어도 내가 속한 쪽은 말이야.\"\n\n\"그쪽이 어딘데?\"\n\n\"대법원 쪽.\"\n\n\"내 아직 그쪽이,\" 우드코트 씨가 고개를 저으며 대꾸했습니다. \"잘 돌아간다는 소리는 들어본 적이 없네.\"\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 리처드가 시무룩하게 말했습니다. \"그런 소릴 들어본 사람이 있겠나?\" 그는 곧 다시 환해져서 타고난 솔직함으로 말했습니다. \"우드코트, 자네한테 오해받는 건 싫네, 설령 그 덕에 자네 눈에 더 좋게 비친다 해도 말이야. 내가 오랫동안 아무 쓸모 있는 일도 못 했다는 건 자네도 알아야 하네. 큰 해를 끼치려던 건 아니었지만, 그 외엔 아무것도 할 줄 모르는 사람이 된 것 같아. 운명이 나를 몰아넣은 이 그물에서 빠져나와 있었다면 더 나았을지도 모르지. 하지만 그렇게 생각하진 않아. 자네가 곧, 아니 이미 들었을지도 모르지만, 아주 다른 의견을 듣게 되리란 건 분명하지만 말이야. 긴 이야기를 짧게 줄이자면, 내겐 목표가 없었던 것 같네. 하지만 이제는 목표가 있어 — 아니, 그 목표가 나를 붙들고 있다고 해야겠지 — 그리고 그걸 논하기엔 너무 늦었어. 나를 있는 그대로 받아주고, 좋게 봐주게.\"\n\n\"좋네, 약속이야.\" 우드코트 씨가 말했습니다. \"그 대신 자네도 나한테 그래주게.\"\n\n\"오! 자네는,\" 리처드가 대꾸했습니다. \"자네는 예술 그 자체를 위해 자네 길을 추구할 수 있고, 쟁기에 손을 얹으면 결코 돌아서지 않으며, 무엇에서든 목적을 끌어낼 수 있는 사람이지. 자네와 나는 아주 다른 존재야.\"\n\n그는 아쉬운 듯 말하고는 잠시 다시 그 지친 상태로 가라앉았습니다.\n\n\"뭐, 됐어, 됐어!\" 그가 그 기분을 떨쳐내며 외쳤습니다. \"모든 일엔 끝이 있는 법. 두고 보자고! 그래, 자네는 나를 있는 그대로 받아주고, 좋게 봐주겠다는 거지?\"\n\n\"그럼! 정말 그러겠네.\" 두 사람은 웃으며 그 일을 두고 악수를 나누었지만, 그 속엔 깊은 진심이 담겨 있었습니다. 그중 한 사람에 대해서라면 나는 진심을 다해 보증할 수 있습니다.\n\n\"자넨 하늘이 보내준 선물 같아.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"여기서 아직 볼스 말고는 아무도 못 봤거든. 우드코트, 우리 약속을 시작하는 마당에 단 한 번, 영영 못 박아두고 싶은 이야기가 하나 있네. 그러지 않으면 자네가 나를 좋게 봐주기 어려울 테니까. 내가 사촌 에이다에게 마음을 두고 있다는 건 자네도 알 테지?\"\n\n우드코트 씨는 내가 그런 낌새를 비친 적이 있다고 대답했습니다. \"그럼 부탁인데,\" 리처드가 받았습니다. \"나를 이기심 덩어리로 보진 말아주게. 내가 이 비참한 대법원 소송에 머리를 쪼개고 가슴을 반쯤 찢어가며 매달리는 게 오로지 내 권리와 이익만을 위해서라고는 생각지 말게. 에이다의 것이 내 것과 묶여 있어. 떼어낼 수가 없네. 볼스는 우리 둘 모두를 위해 일하는 거야. 그걸 좀 생각해주게!\"\n\n이 대목에 대해 그가 어찌나 간곡하던지, 우드코트 씨는 결코 그를 부당하게 보지 않는다고 가장 강하게 다짐해주었습니다.\n\n\"있잖아,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. 무심하고 꾸밈없는 태도이긴 했지만 그 점에 매달리는 그의 모습엔 어딘가 애처로운 데가 있었지요. \"자네처럼 올곧은 친구가, 자네처럼 다정한 얼굴을 하고 여기까지 와줬는데, 내가 이기적이고 비열하게 보인다는 건 견딜 수가 없네. 우드코트, 나는 나만큼이나 에이다도 정당한 몫을 받는 걸 보고 싶어. 나만큼이나 그녀를 위해서도 최선을 다하고 싶고, 나만큼이나 그녀를 구해내려고 긁어모을 수 있는 걸 다 걸고 있는 거야. 제발, 부탁이니 그걸 좀 생각해주게!\"\n\n나중에 우드코트 씨가 그동안 있었던 일을 곰곰이 되새겨보았을 때, 이 점에 대한 리처드의 불안이 어찌나 강렬했던지 깊은 인상을 받아서, 사이먼드 법학원을 처음 찾아간 일을 나에게 대강 들려주면서도 유독 그 대목에 오래 머물렀습니다. 그것은 전부터 품고 있던 두려움을 되살렸습니다. 사랑하는 에이다의 그 작은 재산이 볼스 씨에게 다 빨려 들어가고, 리처드는 그것을 진심으로 이렇게 정당화하리라는 두려움 말입니다. 이 만남이 있었던 것은 마침 내가 캐디를 돌보기 시작했을 무렵이었습니다. 이제 나는 캐디가 회복되고도 나와 사랑하는 친구 사이에 여전히 그늘이 드리워 있던 그때로 돌아갑니다.\n\n그날 아침 나는 에이다에게 리처드를 보러 가자고 했습니다. 그런데 에이다가 머뭇거리며 내가 예상했던 것만큼 환하게 내켜 하지 않는 걸 보고 조금 놀랐습니다.\n\n\"얘,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"내가 한참 집을 비운 사이에 리처드랑 무슨 다툼이라도 있었던 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"아니야, 에스더.\"\n\n\"그럼 혹시 그이 소식도 못 들었어?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니, 소식은 들었어.\" 에이다가 말했습니다.\n\n그 눈에 그렁그렁한 눈물, 그 얼굴에 어린 사랑. 나는 도무지 사랑하는 친구의 속을 알 수 없었습니다. 내가 혼자 리처드네 갈까? 내가 물었습니다. 아니, 에이다는 내가 혼자 가지 않는 게 낫겠다고 했습니다. 그럼 같이 갈래? 응, 에이다는 나와 함께 가는 게 낫겠다고 했습니다. 지금 갈까? 응, 지금 가자. 글쎄, 나는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁하고 얼굴엔 사랑이 어린 사랑하는 친구의 속을 알 수가 없었습니다!\n\n우리는 곧 채비를 마치고 나섰습니다. 음울한 날이었고, 차가운 빗방울이 이따금 떨어졌습니다. 모든 것이 무겁고 거칠어 보이는, 그런 빛깔 없는 날 가운데 하루였지요. 집들은 우리를 향해 찌푸리고, 먼지는 우리를 향해 일어나고, 연기는 우리를 향해 덮쳐들었으며, 무엇 하나 제 모습에 타협하거나 누그러진 얼굴을 하지 않았습니다. 나는 험한 거리에서 아름다운 친구가 영 어울리지 않는다는 생각이 들었고, 그 음침한 보도 위로 여태 본 것보다 더 많은 장례 행렬이 지나가는 것만 같았습니다.\n\n우리는 먼저 사이먼드 법학원을 찾아내야 했습니다. 어느 가게에 들어가 물어보려는데 에이다가 챈서리 레인 근처인 것 같다고 했습니다. \"그쪽으로 가면, 얘, 그리 멀리 벗어나진 않겠다.\" 내가 말했습니다. 그래서 우리는 챈서리 레인으로 갔고, 거기서 정말로 그 이름이 적혀 있는 걸 보았습니다. 사이먼드 법학원.\n\n다음으로는 호수를 찾아야 했습니다. \"아니면 볼스 씨 사무실도 괜찮겠다.\" 나는 떠올렸습니다. \"볼스 씨 사무실이 바로 옆집이니까.\" 그러자 에이다가 저기 모퉁이에 있는 게 볼스 씨 사무실인지도 모르겠다고 했습니다. 그리고 정말 그랬습니다.\n\n그다음엔 옆집 둘 중 어느 쪽이냐 하는 문제가 남았습니다. 나는 이쪽으로 가려 했고 사랑하는 친구는 저쪽으로 가려 했는데, 이번에도 사랑하는 친구가 옳았습니다. 그래서 우리는 삼 층으로 올라갔고, 거기서 영구차 같은 검은 판자에 큼직한 흰 글씨로 적힌 리처드의 이름과 마주쳤습니다.\n\n내가 문을 두드리려 했지만, 에이다가 그냥 손잡이를 돌리고 들어가는 게 낫겠다고 했습니다. 그렇게 우리는 리처드에게 이르렀습니다. 그는 먼지투성이 서류 뭉치로 뒤덮인 책상을 골똘히 들여다보고 있었는데, 그 서류들은 마치 그의 마음을 비추는 먼지투성이 거울처럼 보였습니다. 어디를 보든 그 속에 흐르는 불길한 글자가 되풀이되어 보였지요. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스.\n\n그는 우리를 아주 다정하게 맞았고, 우리는 자리에 앉았습니다. \"조금만 더 일찍 왔으면,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"우드코트를 봤을 텐데. 우드코트 같은 좋은 친구는 세상에 다시없어. 다른 사람 같으면 그 절반의 일만 있어도 도저히 못 온다고 생각할 텐데, 그 친구는 짬짬이 들여다볼 시간을 내거든. 게다가 어찌나 쾌활하고, 어찌나 생기 넘치고, 어찌나 분별 있고, 어찌나 진실하고, 어찌나 — 내가 못 가진 모든 걸 다 갖췄는지, 그 친구가 오면 이곳이 환해지고, 다시 가버리면 어두워진다네.\"\n\n\"그가 나에게 보여준 진심을 봐서라도, 하느님께서 그를 축복하시기를.\" 나는 생각했습니다.\n\n\"그 친구는 에이다, 볼스나 나만큼 낙관하진 않아.\" 리처드가 서류 뭉치 위로 풀 죽은 시선을 던지며 말을 이었습니다. \"하지만 그 친구는 그저 외부인이고 그 비밀 속에 들어와 있지 않으니까. 우린 그 속으로 들어갔지만 그 친구는 안 그랬거든. 이런 미궁을 그 친구가 많이 알 거라고 기대할 수야 없지.\"\n\n그의 시선이 다시 서류 위를 헤매고 두 손으로 머리를 쓸어 넘기는 동안, 나는 그의 눈이 얼마나 퀭하고 커 보이는지, 입술이 얼마나 메말랐는지, 손톱이 죄다 물어뜯겨 사라진 것을 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"리처드, 여기가 살기에 건강한 곳이라고 생각해?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"아니, 내 사랑스러운 미네르바,\" 리처드가 예전의 명랑한 웃음을 띠며 대답했습니다. \"전원도 아니고 유쾌한 곳도 아니지. 여기 해가 비치면, 어디 탁 트인 곳엔 해가 쨍쨍하게 비치고 있을 거라고 꽤 큰돈을 걸어도 좋아. 하지만 당분간은 그런대로 괜찮아. 사무소들이 가깝고 볼스가 가깝거든.\"\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 내가 넌지시 말했습니다. \"둘 다에서 좀 떨어지는 게—\"\n\n\"나한테 좋을 거란 말이지?\" 리처드가 그 문장을 끝맺으며 억지로 웃었습니다. \"그럴 만도 하지! 하지만 이젠 한 가지 길로만 그렇게 될 수 있어 — 두 가지 길 중 하나라고 해야겠군. 에스더, 소송이 끝나거나, 아니면 소송인이 끝나거나 둘 중 하나야. 하지만 끝나는 건 소송일 거야, 내 사랑스러운 친구야, 소송 말이야, 내 사랑스러운 친구야!\"\n\n이 마지막 말은 그에게 가장 가까이 앉아 있던 에이다에게 한 것이었습니다. 에이다의 얼굴이 내게서 돌려져 그를 향하고 있어서, 나는 그 표정을 볼 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"우린 아주 잘하고 있어.\" 리처드가 말을 이었습니다. \"볼스가 그렇게 말해줄 거야. 우린 정말 술술 나아가고 있다고. 볼스한테 물어봐. 우린 저들에게 쉴 틈을 안 줘. 볼스는 저들의 온갖 굽이와 길목을 다 알고 있고, 우린 어디서든 저들을 바짝 쫓고 있어. 우린 벌써 저들을 깜짝 놀라게 했어. 저 잠꾸러기 둥지를 발칵 뒤집어놓고 말 테니, 내 말 명심해!\"\n\n그의 희망찬 모습은 이미 오래전부터 그의 절망보다도 나를 더 아프게 했습니다. 그것은 희망과는 너무도 달랐고, 기어이 희망이고자 하는 결의에 어딘가 사납기까지 했으며, 너무도 굶주리고 간절하면서도, 그러면서도 그것이 억지로 짜낸 것이고 지탱될 수 없는 것임을 너무도 잘 알고 있어서, 오래도록 내 가슴을 저몄습니다. 그러나 이제 그 잘생긴 얼굴에 지울 수 없이 새겨진 그 주석은, 그것을 예전보다 훨씬 더 가슴 아프게 만들었습니다. 지울 수 없다고 말하는 까닭은, 설령 그 운명의 소송이 그가 가장 환하게 그리던 대로 바로 그 시각에 영영 끝맺어진다 해도, 그것이 그에게 안겨준 때 이른 불안과 자책과 실망의 흔적은 죽는 순간까지 그 얼굴에 남아 있으리라고 나는 굳게 믿었기 때문입니다.\n\n\"우리 사랑스러운 꼬마 아주머니를 보고 있으면,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. 에이다는 여전히 말없이 가만히 있었지요. \"어찌나 마음이 편한지, 그 가엾어하는 얼굴이 옛날 그 얼굴과 어찌나 똑같은지—\"\n\n아! 아니, 아니에요. 나는 미소 지으며 고개를 저었습니다.\n\n\"—옛날 그 얼굴과 어찌나 꼭 같은지,\" 리처드가 무엇으로도 변치 않는 형제 같은 정으로 내 손을 잡으며 다정한 목소리로 말했습니다. \"이 아주머니한테는 꾸며댈 수가 없어. 솔직히 말하면 난 좀 흔들려. 어떤 땐 희망을 품다가, 어떤 땐 — 아주 절망하진 않지만, 거의 그래. 난 말이야,\" 리처드가 내 손을 가만히 놓고 방을 가로질러 걸으며 말했습니다. \"너무 지쳐!\"\n\n그는 방 안을 몇 번 오가다가 소파에 털썩 주저앉았습니다. \"난 너무,\" 그가 침울하게 되뇌었습니다. \"지쳤어. 이건 정말 고되고 고된 일이야!\"\n\n그가 팔에 기댄 채 생각에 잠긴 목소리로 이 말을 하며 바닥을 내려다보고 있을 때, 사랑하는 친구가 일어나 모자를 벗고는 그의 곁에 무릎을 꿇었습니다. 황금빛 머리카락이 햇살처럼 그의 머리 위로 흘러내렸지요. 그녀는 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸고 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 돌렸습니다. 오, 내가 본 그 얼굴은 얼마나 사랑이 가득하고 헌신적이던지!\n\n\"에스더, 얘.\" 그녀가 아주 조용히 말했습니다. \"나, 다시는 집에 안 갈 거야.\"\n\n문득 한 줄기 빛이 내 안으로 비쳐 들었습니다.\n\n\"이제 두 번 다시. 나는 사랑하는 남편과 함께 있을 거야. 우린 결혼한 지 두 달이 넘었어. 나 없이 집에 가, 내 에스더. 나는 두 번 다시 집에 안 갈 거야!\" 이 말과 함께 사랑하는 친구는 그의 머리를 자기 가슴으로 끌어당겨 거기 꼭 안았습니다. 그리고 내 평생 죽음 말고는 무엇으로도 변할 수 없는 사랑을 본 적이 있다면, 바로 그때 내 눈앞에서 그것을 보았습니다.\n\n\"에스더한테 말해, 여보.\" 리처드가 이윽고 침묵을 깨며 말했습니다. \"어떻게 된 일인지 들려줘.\"\n\n그녀가 내게 오기도 전에 내가 먼저 다가가 두 팔로 끌어안았습니다. 우리 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 그녀의 뺨이 내 뺨에 닿아 있으니 나는 아무 말도 듣고 싶지 않았습니다. \"우리 아가.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"내 사랑. 가엾은, 가엾은 우리 아가!\" 나는 그녀가 너무도 가여웠습니다. 나는 리처드를 무척 아꼈지만, 그 순간 나를 사로잡은 충동은 그녀가 너무도 가엾다는 것이었습니다.\n\n\"에스더, 날 용서해줄래? 사촌 존도 날 용서해줄까?\"\n\n\"얘,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그걸 한순간이라도 의심하는 건 그분께 큰 잘못을 저지르는 거야. 그리고 나로 말하면!\" 아니, 나로 말하면, 내가 뭘 용서할 게 있다고!\n\n나는 흐느끼는 사랑하는 친구의 눈물을 닦아주고 소파에서 그녀 곁에 앉았으며, 리처드는 내 다른 쪽에 앉았습니다. 그리고 그토록 다른 그날 밤이 떠올랐지요. 두 사람이 처음 나에게 속내를 털어놓고 그들 특유의 분방하고 행복한 방식으로 이야기를 이어가던 그 밤 말입니다. 두 사람은 번갈아가며 어떻게 된 일인지 들려주었습니다.\n\n\"내가 가진 건 다 리처드 거였어.\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"그런데 리처드가 받으려 하지 않았어, 에스더. 그토록 사랑하는데, 그이의 아내가 되는 것 말고 내가 뭘 할 수 있었겠어!\"\n\n\"게다가 당신은 그토록 알차고 그토록 친절하게 바빴잖아, 훌륭한 더든 아주머니.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"그런 때에 어떻게 당신한테 말을 꺼낼 수 있었겠어! 게다가 오래 고민한 일도 아니었어. 우린 어느 날 아침 나가서 결혼해버렸거든.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 일을 치르고 나서, 에스더.\" 사랑하는 친구가 말했습니다. \"나는 늘 너한테 어떻게 말할까, 어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을까 고민했어. 어떤 땐 네가 당장 알아야 한다고 생각했고, 어떤 땐 네가 몰라야 하고 사촌 존한테도 숨겨야 한다고 생각했어. 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수가 없어서 무척 애를 태웠지.\"\n\n이걸 진작 생각지 못했다니 나는 얼마나 이기적이었던 걸까요! 그때 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 모르겠습니다. 나는 너무도 미안했지만, 그러면서도 두 사람이 너무도 좋았고 그들이 나를 좋아해줘서 너무도 기뻤습니다. 두 사람이 너무도 가여웠지만, 그러면서도 그들이 서로 사랑한다는 데 일종의 자랑스러움을 느꼈지요. 한꺼번에 그토록 괴로우면서도 즐거운 감정을 느껴본 적은 없었고, 내 마음속에서도 어느 쪽이 더 큰지 알 수 없었습니다. 하지만 나는 그들의 앞길을 어둡게 하려고 거기 있는 게 아니었습니다. 나는 그러지 않았습니다.\n\n내가 덜 어리석어지고 좀 더 차분해지자, 사랑하는 친구가 가슴에서 결혼반지를 꺼내 입을 맞추고는 손가락에 끼웠습니다. 그제야 나는 지난밤이 떠올라, 리처드에게 그녀가 결혼한 뒤로 줄곧 아무도 보지 않는 밤이면 그 반지를 끼고 있었다고 말했습니다. 그러자 에이다가 얼굴을 붉히며, 얘, 그걸 어떻게 알았느냐고 물었습니다. 그래서 나는 그녀가 베개 밑에 손을 감추고 있는 걸 보고도, 얘, 왜인지는 거의 짐작도 못 했다고 말했지요. 그러자 두 사람은 다시 처음부터 어떻게 된 일인지 들려주기 시작했고, 나는 다시 미안하고 또 기뻤다가, 다시 어리석어졌고, 두 사람의 기를 꺾을세라 못생긴 늙은 내 얼굴을 한껏 감추려 했습니다.\n\n그렇게 시간이 흘러 마침내 나는 돌아갈 일을 생각해야 했습니다. 그때가 닥치니 무엇보다도 견디기 힘들었습니다. 사랑하는 친구가 완전히 무너져버렸거든요. 그녀는 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 이름으로 나를 부르며 내 목에 매달려, 나 없이 어떻게 살겠느냐고 했습니다! 리처드도 별반 낫지 않았지요. 그리고 나로 말하면, 만약 내가 스스로에게 엄하게 \"자, 에스더, 그러면 너랑 다시는 말도 안 할 거야!\" 하고 다그치지 않았다면 셋 중 가장 형편없었을 겁니다.\n\n\"아니, 세상에.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"이런 아내는 처음 봐. 남편을 도무지 사랑하지 않는 것 같다니까. 자, 리처드, 제발 이 애를 좀 데려가요.\" 하지만 나는 내내 그녀를 꼭 끌어안고 있었고, 얼마나 오래인지 모르게 그녀를 붙들고 울 수도 있었습니다.\n\n\"이 사랑스러운 젊은 부부에게 일러두는데,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"나는 그저 내일 다시 오려고 가는 거고, 사이먼드 법학원이 내 꼴을 지겨워할 때까지 늘 들락날락할 거예요. 그러니 잘 있으라는 말은 안 할래, 리처드. 이렇게 곧 돌아올 텐데 그런 말이 무슨 소용이겠어!\"\n\n이제 나는 사랑하는 친구를 그에게 넘겨주었고, 그만 가려 했습니다. 하지만 등을 돌리려니 가슴이 찢어지는 것만 같은 그 소중한 얼굴을 한 번 더 보려고 머뭇거렸습니다.\n\n그래서 나는 (명랑하게 부산을 떨며) 돌아오라고 좀 부추겨주지 않으면 내가 그렇게 마음대로 굴어도 될지 모르겠다고 말했고, 그러자 사랑하는 친구가 눈물 사이로 희미하게 미소 지으며 올려다보았습니다. 나는 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 두 손으로 감싸고 마지막으로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 웃으며 달려나왔습니다.\n\n그리고 아래층에 내려와서는, 오, 얼마나 울었던지! 마치 내 에이다를 영영 잃어버린 것만 같았습니다. 그녀가 없으니 너무도 외롭고 텅 빈 것 같았고, 거기서 그녀를 볼 희망도 없이 집으로 돌아간다는 게 너무도 쓸쓸해서, 어둑한 구석을 오가며 흐느껴 우는 동안 한동안은 도무지 마음을 달랠 수 없었습니다.\n\n이윽고 나는 스스로를 조금 나무라고 나서 정신을 차렸고, 삯마차를 타고 집으로 갔습니다. 세인트 올번스에서 만났던 그 가엾은 소년이 얼마 전 다시 나타나 죽음의 문턱에 누워 있었지요. 실은 그때 이미 죽었지만, 나는 그걸 몰랐습니다. 후견인은 그 아이 소식을 알아보러 나가서 저녁 식사 때까지 돌아오지 않았습니다. 혼자가 되니 나는 또 조금 울었지만, 대체로 보면 그렇게 아주아주 못나게 군 것 같지는 않습니다.\n\n사랑하는 친구를 잃은 일에 아직 도무지 익숙해지지 못한 건 당연한 노릇이었지요. 여러 해를 함께한 뒤의 서너 시간은 긴 시간이 아니었으니까요. 하지만 내 마음은 내가 그녀를 두고 온 그 정 없는 풍경에 너무도 자꾸 머물렀고, 나는 그곳을 그토록 그늘지고 매정한 곳으로 그려보았으며, 그녀 곁에서 어떻게든 그녀를 돌봐주고 싶은 마음이 너무도 간절해서, 저녁에 그저 그녀의 창문이라도 올려다보러 다시 가기로 마음먹었습니다.\n\n어리석은 짓이었겠지요. 하지만 그때는 전혀 그렇게 여겨지지 않았고, 지금도 그리 어리석게만 여겨지지는 않습니다. 나는 찰리에게만 속내를 털어놓고, 우리는 땅거미 질 무렵 나섰습니다. 사랑하는 친구의 낯설고 새로운 보금자리에 이르렀을 때는 어두웠고, 노란 차양 뒤로 불빛이 비쳤습니다. 우리는 올려다보며 서너 차례 조심스레 그 앞을 지나쳤는데, 하마터면 볼스 씨와 마주칠 뻔했습니다. 우리가 거기 있는 동안 그가 사무실에서 나와, 집으로 가기 전에 그 역시 고개를 돌려 위를 올려다보았거든요. 그 깡마른 검은 형체와 어둠 속 그 후미진 곳의 쓸쓸한 분위기는 내 마음 상태에 들어맞았습니다. 나는 그토록 어울리지 않는 피난처에 갇힌 사랑하는 친구의 젊음과 사랑과 아름다움을 떠올렸는데, 거의 그곳이 잔인한 곳이기라도 한 것만 같았습니다.\n\n거기는 무척 외지고 무척 칙칙했고, 나는 들키지 않고 위층으로 살그머니 올라가도 되겠다고 의심치 않았습니다. 나는 찰리를 아래에 두고 가벼운 발걸음으로 올라갔는데, 오는 길에 희미한 기름 등불의 불빛이 비쳐도 거북하지 않았습니다. 잠시 귀를 기울이니, 그 집의 곰팡내 나는 썩어가는 정적 속에서 두 사람의 젊은 목소리가 두런대는 소리가 들리는 듯했습니다. 나는 사랑하는 이에게 보내는 입맞춤으로 영구차 같은 문 판자에 입술을 댄 뒤, 조용히 다시 내려왔습니다. 언젠가 이 방문을 고백하리라 생각하면서요.\n\n그리고 그 일은 정말로 나에게 위안이 되었습니다. 찰리와 나 말고는 아무도 그 일을 몰랐지만, 그래도 어쩐지 그것이 에이다와 나 사이의 거리를 좁혀주고, 그 잠깐 동안만이라도 우리를 다시 이어준 것만 같았거든요. 나는 그 변화에 아직 도무지 익숙해지지 못한 채 돌아왔지만, 그래도 사랑하는 친구의 곁을 그렇게 맴돌고 나니 한결 나았습니다.\n\n후견인은 집에 돌아와 어두운 창가에 생각에 잠겨 서 있었습니다. 내가 들어가자 그의 얼굴이 밝아지며 그는 자기 자리로 왔지만, 내가 자리에 앉을 때 내 얼굴에 비친 빛을 보았습니다.\n\n\"꼬마 아주머니,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"울었구나.\"\n\n\"네, 그게, 후견인.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"조금 운 것 같아요. 에이다가 너무 괴로워하고, 너무 미안해해서요, 후견인.\"\n\n나는 그의 의자 등받이에 팔을 얹었고, 내 말과 그녀의 빈자리를 바라보는 내 눈길이 그를 미리 마음의 준비를 시켰음을 그의 눈빛에서 보았습니다.\n\n\"그 애가 결혼했니, 얘야?\"\n\n나는 그 모든 일과, 그녀가 맨 먼저 간청한 것이 그의 용서에 관한 것이었음을 들려주었습니다.\n\n\"용서받을 일이 없지.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"하늘이 그 애와 그 남편을 축복하시기를!\" 하지만 내 첫 충동이 그녀를 가여워하는 것이었듯, 그의 충동도 그러했습니다. \"가엾은 것, 가엾은 것! 가엾은 릭! 가엾은 에이다!\"\n\n그 뒤로 우리 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 그가 한숨을 쉬며 말했습니다. \"그래, 그래, 얘야! 황폐한 집이 빠르게 비어가는구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 안주인은 남아 있어요, 후견인.\" 그렇게 말하기가 조심스러웠지만, 그가 말한 슬픈 어조 때문에 용기를 냈습니다. \"안주인이 이 집을 행복하게 만들려고 할 수 있는 건 다 할 거예요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그 애는 해낼 거다, 내 사랑!\"\n\n그 편지는 우리 사이에 아무것도 달라지게 하지 않았습니다. 다만 그의 곁자리가 내 자리가 되었다는 것뿐이었지요. 지금도 달라진 건 없었습니다. 그는 예전의 그 환하고 자애로운 눈길을 내게 돌리고, 예전 그대로 내 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 다시 말했습니다. \"그 애는 해낼 거다, 얘야. 그래도 황폐한 집은 빠르게 비어가는구나, 오, 꼬마 아주머니!\"\n\n이내 나는 그 일에 대해 우리가 나눈 말이 이게 다라는 것이 아쉬웠습니다. 다소 실망스러웠지요. 그 편지와 그 답장 이후로 내가 되고자 했던 그 모든 모습이 되지 못한 건 아닐까 두려웠습니다.\n\n하루를 더 사이에 두고서야, 아침 일찍 우리가 막 아침을 먹으러 가려던 참에, 우드코트 씨가 황급히 들어와 끔찍한 소식을 전했습니다. 무시무시한 살인 사건이 벌어졌고, 그 일로 조지 씨가 붙잡혀 구금되어 있다는 것이었습니다. 레스터 데드록 경이 살인범을 잡으려고 막대한 현상금을 내걸었다는 말을 들었을 때, 처음 놀란 가운데에는 그 까닭을 알지 못했습니다. 그러나 몇 마디 더 듣고서야, 살해된 사람이 레스터 경의 변호사라는 사실이 밝혀졌고, 그 순간 어머니가 그를 두려워했다는 기억이 와락 떠올랐습니다.\n\n오랫동안 어머니가 지켜보며 불신했고 또한 오랫동안 그가 지켜보며 불신했던 사람, 언제나 위험하고 은밀한 적으로 두려워하느라 어머니가 그를 다정하게 대할 틈이라곤 거의 없었을 그 사람이 이토록 뜻밖에 폭력으로 사라졌다는 사실이 너무도 끔찍해서, 제 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 것은 어머니였습니다. 그런 죽음의 소식을 듣고도 한 점 연민을 느낄 수 없다는 것은 얼마나 소름 끼치는 일인지요! 그토록 순식간에 목숨을 빼앗긴 그 노인을, 어머니가 어쩌면 더러는 사라져버렸으면 하고 바란 적이 있었을지도 모른다는 것을 떠올리는 일은 얼마나 무서운지요!\n\n이렇게 한꺼번에 밀려드는 생각이, 그 이름이 입에 오를 때마다 늘 느끼던 괴로움과 두려움을 더욱 키워서, 저는 식탁에 제대로 앉아 있기조차 어려울 만큼 동요했습니다. 잠시 마음을 추스를 틈을 갖기 전까지는 대화를 도무지 따라갈 수가 없었습니다. 그러나 제정신이 돌아와 후견인이 얼마나 충격을 받았는지 보고, 모두가 그 혐의를 받는 사람을 진지하게 이야기하며, 우리가 알던 그의 선한 됨됨이에서 받은 좋은 인상을 하나하나 되짚고 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 그를 위하는 관심과 두려움이 너무도 강하게 일어 저는 다시금 기운을 차렸습니다.\n\n\"후견인, 그분이 정당하게 고발당했을 가능성이 있다고는 생각하지 않으시죠?\"\n\n\"얘야, 나는 그렇게 생각할 수가 없구나. 그토록 마음을 열고 인정 많은 모습을 보여준 사람, 거인 같은 힘에 어린아이 같은 부드러움을 지닌 사람, 둘도 없이 용감한 사내처럼 보이면서도 그토록 소박하고 차분한 사람, 그 사람이 그런 죄로 정당하게 고발당했다고? 나는 믿을 수가 없어. 안 믿는 것도, 안 믿으려는 것도 아니야. 믿을 수가 없는 거야!\"\n\n\"저도 믿을 수가 없습니다.\" 우드코트 씨가 말했습니다. \"그래도 우리가 그분을 어떻게 믿고 어떻게 알든, 몇 가지 정황이 그분에게 불리하다는 것은 잊지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 그분은 고인이 된 그 신사에게 적의를 품고 있었습니다. 여러 곳에서 그것을 공공연히 드러냈고요. 그 사람에게 격하게 말했다고들 하는데, 제가 아는 한 그 사람에 대해서 격하게 말한 것은 분명한 사실입니다. 그분 스스로 살인이 벌어지고 몇 분 안에 그 현장에 혼자 있었다고 인정했습니다. 저는 진심으로 그분이 저만큼이나 이 일에 가담하지 않았다고 믿지만, 이 모든 것이 그분에게 의심이 쏠리는 까닭입니다.\"\n\n\"맞는 말이야.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. 그러고는 제 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였습니다. \"얘야, 이런 점들에서 진실에 눈을 감는다면 그건 그 사람을 아주 못되게 돕는 일이 될 게다.\"\n\n저는 물론, 우리 자신에게뿐만 아니라 다른 사람들에게도 그에게 불리한 정황의 무게를 온전히 인정해야 한다고 느꼈습니다. 그러면서도 (저는 말하지 않을 수 없었습니다만) 그 무게 때문에 곤경에 처한 그를 우리가 저버리는 일은 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었습니다.\n\n\"천만에, 결코 그럴 수 없지!\" 후견인이 대답했습니다. \"그가 세상을 떠난 두 가엾은 사람 곁을 지켰듯이, 우리도 그의 곁을 지킬 게다.\" 그가 말한 두 사람은 그리들리 씨와 그 소년이었으니, 조지 씨가 두 사람 모두에게 거처를 내준 적이 있었습니다.\n\n이어서 우드코트 씨가 들려준 이야기로는, 기병의 하인이 날이 밝기도 전에 그를 찾아왔는데, 밤새도록 정신 나간 사람처럼 거리를 헤매고 다닌 끝이었다고 했습니다. 그 기병이 가장 먼저 걱정한 것 가운데 하나가, 우리가 그를 죄인으로 여기지 않을까 하는 것이었다고 했습니다. 그래서 자기가 보낸 심부름꾼에게, 보낼 수 있는 온갖 엄숙한 다짐을 다해 자신의 완전한 무죄를 전하라고 일렀다고 했습니다. 우드코트 씨는 아침 일찍 우리 집에 와서 이 이야기를 전하겠다고 약속하고서야 겨우 그 사내를 진정시켰다고 했습니다. 그러면서 이제 자기가 직접 그 죄수를 보러 가는 길이라고 덧붙였습니다.\n\n후견인은 곧바로 자기도 가겠다고 했습니다. 그런데 저는 그 퇴역 군인을 무척 좋아했고 그도 저를 좋아했을 뿐 아니라, 벌어진 일에 후견인만이 아는 은밀한 관심을 품고 있었습니다. 마치 그 일이 제게 바싹 가까이 다가온 것처럼 느껴졌습니다. 진실이 밝혀지고 무고한 사람이 의심받지 않는 것이, 저 개인에게도 중요한 일처럼 여겨졌습니다. 한번 걷잡을 수 없이 날뛴 의심은 더욱 사납게 날뛸 수도 있으니까요.\n\n한마디로, 저는 그들과 함께 가는 것이 제 의무이자 책임처럼 느껴졌습니다. 후견인은 저를 말리려 하지 않았고, 저는 따라나섰습니다.\n\n그곳은 큰 감옥이었습니다. 안마당과 통로가 수없이 많은데 하나같이 서로 닮았고 한결같이 똑같은 돌이 깔려 있어서, 그 길을 지나는 동안 저는 새삼 깨닫는 듯한 느낌이 들었습니다. 해가 바뀌고 또 바뀌도록 똑같이 노려보는 벽 사이에 갇혀 지내는 외로운 죄수들이, 책에서 읽었듯이 잡초 한 포기나 어쩌다 돋아난 풀 한 잎에 어째서 그토록 애착을 품게 되는지를요. 그가 홀로 갇힌 곳은 아치형 천장의 방으로, 위층에 있는 지하 저장고 같았는데, 벽이 어찌나 눈부시게 희던지 그 때문에 육중한 무쇠 창살과 무쇠를 두른 문이 실제보다 한층 더 시커멓게 보였습니다. 그 안 한쪽 구석에 기병이 서 있는 것을 우리는 보았습니다. 그는 거기 놓인 의자에 앉아 있다가 자물쇠와 빗장이 돌아가는 소리를 듣고 일어선 참이었습니다.\n\n우리를 보자 그는 여느 때처럼 묵직한 걸음으로 한 발 다가왔다가 멈추어 서서 가볍게 고개를 숙였습니다. 그러나 제가 손을 내밀며 계속 다가가자, 그는 곧바로 우리 마음을 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"이거 정말이지 마음의 짐을 덜었습니다, 아가씨, 그리고 신사 양반들.\" 그가 더없이 진심을 담아 우리에게 인사하며 길게 숨을 내쉬었습니다. \"이제는 이 일이 어떻게 끝나든 그리 마음 쓰지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n그는 도무지 죄수처럼 보이지 않았습니다. 침착한 태도와 군인다운 자세로 보아, 오히려 감옥의 간수에 훨씬 가까워 보였습니다.\n\n\"제 사격장보다도 더 누추한 데서 숙녀분을 맞게 됐습니다만,\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양은 이 정도쯤 너그럽게 봐주시리라 믿습니다.\" 그가 자기가 앉아 있던 의자로 저를 안내하기에 제가 앉자, 그는 무척 흡족해하는 것 같았습니다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 아가씨.\" 그가 말했습니다.\n\n\"자, 조지.\" 후견인이 입을 열었습니다. \"자네 쪽에서 우리에게 새삼 다짐할 필요가 없듯이, 우리 쪽에서도 자네에게 굳이 다짐할 필요는 없으리라 보네.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 나리. 진심으로 감사드립니다. 제가 이 죄에 대해 무고하지 않았다면, 이렇게 황송하게 찾아와 주신 마당에 나리를 똑바로 쳐다보며 제 속을 감출 수는 없었을 겁니다. 이번 방문이 제게는 정말 큰 의미입니다. 제가 말주변이 좋은 축은 아닙니다만, 그래도 절실히 느낍니다, 서머슨 양, 그리고 신사 양반들, 깊이 느낍니다.\"\n\n그는 잠시 한 손을 자기 넓은 가슴에 얹고 우리에게 고개를 숙였습니다. 곧바로 다시 어깨를 펴긴 했지만, 그 소박한 몸짓으로도 그는 자연스러운 감정을 한껏 드러냈습니다.\n\n\"우선,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"자네 개인의 편의를 위해 우리가 해줄 만한 일이 있겠나, 조지?\"\n\n\"무엇 말씀이십니까, 나리?\" 그가 목을 가다듬으며 물었습니다.\n\n\"자네 개인의 편의 말일세. 이렇게 갇혀 지내는 고생을 조금이나마 덜어줄 만한, 뭐 필요한 게 없나?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리.\" 조지가 잠시 생각하더니 대답했습니다. \"그것도 똑같이 고맙습니다만, 담배는 규칙에 어긋나서, 딱히 필요한 건 없다고 해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"앞으로 자질구레한 게 여럿 생각날지도 모르지. 언제든 생각나거든, 조지, 우리에게 알려주게.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리. 어쨌거나,\" 조지 씨가 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴로 웃으며 말했습니다. \"저처럼 오랫동안 떠돌이 신세로 세상을 헤매고 다닌 사람이라면 이런 곳에서도, 그런 면에서는 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다.\"\n\n\"다음으로, 자네 사건 말인데.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 나리.\" 조지 씨가 완전히 침착한 태도로, 약간의 호기심을 띠며 가슴 앞에 팔짱을 끼고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"지금 어떻게 돌아가고 있나?\"\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 나리, 지금은 구류 연장 중입니다. 버킷 말로는, 사건이 더 갖춰질 때까지 때때로 구류 연장을 거듭 신청할 것 같다고 합니다. 어떻게 더 갖춘다는 건지 저로서는 모르겠습니다만, 버킷이 어떻게든 해내겠지요.\"\n\n\"이런, 맙소사, 이 사람아.\" 후견인이 옛날의 그 별스럽고 격한 기질을 못 이겨 소리쳤습니다. \"자네는 마치 자네가 딴 사람인 양 자네 이야기를 하는구먼!\"\n\n\"기분 상하셨다면 죄송합니다, 나리.\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"나리의 친절은 잘 알고 있습니다. 하지만 무고한 사람이 이런 일을 그렇게 받아들이지 않고서야, 벽에 머리를 들이받지 않고 어떻게 마음을 추스를 수 있겠습니까.\"\n\n\"어느 정도는 맞는 말이긴 하네.\" 후견인이 누그러져서 대답했습니다. \"하지만 이보게, 무고한 사람이라도 자기를 지키려면 으레 해야 할 대비는 해야 하는 법일세.\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 나리. 그래서 저도 그렇게 했습니다. 치안판사들께 이렇게 진술했지요. '여러분, 저는 여러분만큼이나 이 혐의에 결백합니다. 사실관계로 저에게 제기된 것은 모두 틀림없는 진실입니다. 저는 그 이상은 아무것도 모릅니다.' 저는 앞으로도 계속 그렇게 진술할 작정입니다, 나리. 그 이상 제가 무얼 할 수 있겠습니까? 그게 진실인걸요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그저 진실만으로는 안 되네.\" 후견인이 받아쳤습니다.\n\n\"정말 안 되는 겁니까, 나리? 저한테는 영 안 좋은 형국이군요!\" 조지 씨가 너스레를 떨며 말했습니다.\n\n\"자네는 변호사를 두어야 하네.\" 후견인이 이어갔습니다. \"우리가 자네를 위해 솜씨 좋은 사람을 하나 선임해줘야겠어.\"\n\n\"송구합니다, 나리.\" 조지 씨가 한 발 물러서며 말했습니다. \"그것도 똑같이 고맙습니다. 하지만 그런 종류의 일은 단호히 사양하게 해주시기를 간청드립니다.\"\n\n\"변호사를 두지 않겠다고?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\" 조지 씨는 더없이 단호하게 고개를 저었습니다. \"그래도 고맙습니다, 나리, 하지만, 변호사는 안 됩니다!\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"저는 그 족속을 곱게 보지 않습니다.\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"그리들리도 그랬고요. 그리고, 이런 말씀 드려 죄송합니다만, 나리께서도 그러시리라고는 좀처럼 생각되지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 형평법일세.\" 후견인이 약간 난처해하며 설명했습니다. \"그건 형평법이야, 조지.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까, 나리?\" 기병이 무심한 투로 대답했습니다. \"저야 그런 이름의 미묘한 갈래까지는 잘 모릅니다만, 대체로 말해 저는 그 족속이 싫습니다.\"\n\n그는 팔짱을 풀고 자세를 바꾸어, 한쪽 육중한 손을 탁자에 얹고 다른 손은 허리에 댄 채 섰습니다. 한번 정한 뜻에서 결코 움직이지 않을 사람의 본보기로, 제가 본 가운데 그토록 완벽한 그림은 없었습니다. 우리 셋이 그에게 이런저런 말로 설득하려 애써봐야 헛일이었습니다. 그는 무뚝뚝한 태도와 그토록 잘 어울리는 부드러움으로 귀를 기울였지만, 그가 갇힌 그 방만큼이나 우리 말에는 조금도 흔들리지 않는 것이 분명했습니다.\n\n\"제발 다시 한번 생각해주세요, 조지 씨.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"당신 사건과 관련해서 바라는 것이 정말 하나도 없나요?\"\n\n\"군법회의에서 재판받을 수 있다면야 그러기를 바라겠지요, 아가씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 그건 어림없는 일이라는 걸 저도 잘 압니다. 아가씨께서 부디 한 이삼 분만, 그 이상은 말고, 제 말에 귀 기울여주신다면, 제가 할 수 있는 한 또렷이 제 뜻을 밝혀보겠습니다.\"\n\n그는 우리 셋을 차례로 바라보고는, 마치 빳빳한 군복의 깃과 목띠에 목을 맞추기라도 하듯 고개를 약간 가다듬은 다음, 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"보십시오, 아가씨, 저는 수갑이 채워진 채 붙들려서 이리로 끌려왔습니다. 저는 낙인찍히고 망신당한 사람으로, 이렇게 여기 있습니다. 제 사격장은 버킷한테 위아래 샅샅이 뒤지어졌고, 제 재산이라고 해봐야 보잘것없지만, 그것도 이리저리 뒤집혀서 제 것인지조차 모를 지경이 되었고, 그래서 (앞서 말씀드렸듯이) 이렇게 여기 있습니다! 그렇다고 그걸 딱히 불평하는 건 아닙니다. 바로 앞선 제 잘못 때문에 이런 곳에 들어온 건 아니라 해도, 제가 젊은 시절에 떠돌이 길로 들어서지 않았더라면 이런 일은 없었으리라는 건 저도 충분히 압니다. 어쨌든 일은 벌어졌습니다. 그러면 이걸 어떻게 마주할 것이냐, 그게 문제지요.\"\n\n그는 잠시 거무스름한 이마를 문지르며 너그러운 표정으로, 변명하듯 말했습니다. \"제가 숨 가쁘게 더듬는 말꾼이라서, 좀 생각을 해야겠습니다.\" 그러고는 잠시 생각하더니 다시 고개를 들고 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"이걸 어떻게 마주할 것이냐. 자, 불운하게도 세상을 떠난 그 사람은 그 자신이 변호사였고, 저를 단단히 옭아매고 있었습니다. 망자의 재를 들쑤시고 싶지는 않습니다만, 그자가 살아 있다면 저는 이렇게 말하겠습니다. 그자는 저를 지독하리만치 옭아매고 있었다고요. 그렇다고 그 직업이 더 좋아진 건 아닙니다. 제가 그 직업을 멀리했더라면, 이런 곳 밖에 있었겠지요. 하지만 그건 제가 하려는 말이 아닙니다. 자, 제가 그자를 죽였다고 쳐봅시다. 버킷이 제 집에서 찾아낸, 최근에 발사된 그 권총 가운데 하나를 제가 정말로 그자 몸에다 쏘았다고 쳐봅시다. 그런데 이런, 그 권총이야 제 집이 된 뒤로는 언제든 거기서 찾아낼 수 있었던 물건이지요. 그렇게 여기 옴짝달싹 못 하게 갇히자마자 제가 무얼 했겠습니까? 변호사를 구했겠지요.\"\n\n자물쇠와 빗장에서 누군가 인기척이 나자 그는 말을 멈추었고, 문이 열렸다가 다시 닫힐 때까지 말을 잇지 않았습니다. 무슨 일로 문이 열렸는지는 곧 말씀드리겠습니다.\n\n\"저는 변호사를 구했을 거고, 그 변호사는 (신문에서 자주 읽었듯이) 이렇게 말했겠지요. '제 의뢰인은 아무 말도 하지 않습니다, 제 의뢰인은 변론을 유보합니다.' 제 의뢰인은 이렇다, 저렇다, 이러쿵저러쿵. 자, 제 생각에 그 족속은 곧이곧대로 가는 게 본성도 아니고, 남들이 그러리라 여기지도 않습니다. 제가 결백하다고 칩시다, 그래서 변호사를 구합니다. 그자는 저를 죄인으로 믿는 쪽이 안 믿는 쪽만큼이나, 어쩌면 그보다 더 그럴 겁니다. 그러면 그자가 어떻게 하겠습니까? 어떻든 간에, 제가 죄인인 양 굴겠지요. 제 입을 틀어막고, 함부로 말하지 말라 이르고, 정황을 감추고, 증거를 잘게 토막 내고, 말꼬리를 잡고 늘어지고, 그래서 어쩌면 저를 빼내주겠지요! 하지만 서머슨 양, 제가 그런 식으로 빠져나오는 것에 마음을 쓰겠습니까? 아니면 차라리 제 방식대로 교수형을 당하는 편을 택하겠습니까? 숙녀분께 이런 거북한 얘기를 꺼내는 걸 너그러이 봐주신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n이제 그는 자기 이야기에 한껏 달아올라, 더는 잠시 멈추어 생각할 필요가 없었습니다.\n\n\"저는 차라리 제 방식대로 교수형을 당하겠습니다. 그러고야 말 작정입니다! 그렇다고 해서,\" 그가 힘센 두 팔을 허리에 짚고 짙은 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 우리를 둘러보며 말했습니다. \"제가 남들보다 교수형 당하는 걸 더 좋아한다는 뜻은 아닙니다. 제가 하는 말은, 저는 깨끗하고 온전하게 풀려나든가, 아니면 아예 안 풀려나든가 둘 중 하나여야 한다는 겁니다. 그러니 제게 불리한 진술이 사실일 때는, 그건 사실이라고 말합니다. 그리고 그들이 '당신이 하는 말은 무엇이든 증거로 쓰일 거요'라고 하면, 저는 상관없다고, 부디 그렇게 쓰이게 하라고 말합니다. 온전한 진실로도 저를 결백하게 만들지 못한다면, 그보다 덜한 것이나 다른 무엇으로 그리해낼 리도 없을 테니까요. 설령 그리해낸들, 제게는 아무 값어치도 없습니다.\"\n\n그는 돌바닥을 한두 걸음 거닐다가 탁자로 돌아와 하려던 말을 마쳤습니다.\n\n\"아가씨, 그리고 신사 양반들 두 분, 귀 기울여주셔서 거듭 감사드리고, 관심 가져주셔서 더더욱 감사드립니다. 무딘 폭검 같은 머리를 가진 일개 기병에게 비치는 대로, 이게 이 일의 있는 그대로의 사정입니다. 저는 군인으로서의 본분 말고는 살면서 변변히 잘해본 적이 없으니, 끝내 최악의 일이 닥친다 해도, 뿌린 대로 거두는 셈이 되겠지요. 살인자로 붙잡히는 첫 충격을 넘기고 나니, 저처럼 그토록 많이 떠돌아다닌 부랑자는 충격에서 벗어나는 데 그리 오래 걸리지 않는 법인지라, 지금 보시는 이 마음가짐에 다다랐습니다. 저는 이대로 있겠습니다. 저 때문에 망신당하거나 불행해질 친척도 없고요, 그리고, 그게 제가 드릴 수 있는 말 전부입니다.\"\n\n문이 열려 들어온 사람은 또 한 사람의 군인 같은 남자였는데, 첫눈에는 그리 호감 가는 인상이 아니었습니다. 그리고 햇볕에 그을리고 눈이 초롱초롱한, 건강한 여인이 바구니를 들고 함께였는데, 그녀는 들어선 순간부터 조지 씨가 하는 말 하나하나에 무척이나 귀를 기울이고 있었습니다. 조지 씨는 두 사람을 허물없는 끄덕임과 다정한 눈길로 맞이했지만, 이야기를 하던 도중이라 그 이상 각별한 인사는 건네지 못했습니다. 이제 그는 두 사람과 정답게 악수를 나누며 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양, 그리고 신사 양반들, 이쪽은 제 오랜 전우 매슈 배그넷입니다. 그리고 이쪽은 그 부인 배그넷 부인이고요.\"\n\n배그넷 씨는 우리에게 뻣뻣하게 군대식으로 절을 했고, 배그넷 부인은 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 했습니다.\n\n\"제 진짜 좋은 벗들입니다.\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"제가 붙잡힌 게 이 사람들 집에서였지요.\"\n\n\"중고 첼로 때문에.\" 배그넷 씨가 화난 듯 고개를 홱 움직이며 끼어들었습니다. \"음색이 좋은 놈으로. 친구한테 주려고. 돈은 아깝지 않은 거였소.\"\n\n\"맷,\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 아가씨하고 이 두 신사분께 내가 한 말을 자네도 얼추 다 들었을 텐데. 자네도 옳다고 여기겠지?\"\n\n배그넷 씨는 잠시 헤아려보더니 그 문제를 아내에게 넘겼습니다. \"여보.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"당신이 말해. 옳다고 보는지 아닌지를. 나는 옳다고 보네.\"\n\n\"이런, 조지.\" 그 사이 바구니를 풀고 있던 배그넷 부인이 소리쳤는데, 바구니 안에는 차가운 절인 돼지고기 한 덩이와 약간의 차와 설탕, 그리고 거무스레한 빵 한 덩이가 들어 있었습니다. \"당신도 알아야지, 안 옳다는 걸. 당신 얘기를 듣고 있자면 사람 속이 다 뒤집힌다는 걸 알아야 한다고. 이렇게도 안 풀려나겠다, 저렇게도 안 풀려나겠다, 그렇게 골라잡고 까탈을 부려서 대체 어쩌자는 거예요? 다 부질없는 헛소리예요, 조지.\"\n\n\"이 곤경에 빠진 사람한테 너무 모질게 굴지 마세요, 배그넷 부인.\" 기병이 가볍게 말했습니다.\n\n\"오! 곤경이고 뭐고,\" 배그넷 부인이 외쳤습니다. \"그 곤경이 당신을 그 모양보다 좀 더 분별 있게 만들어주지 못한다면 무슨 소용이람. 살면서 사내가 어리석은 소리를 하는 걸 듣고 오늘처럼 부끄러웠던 적이 없어요, 이 자리에서 당신이 이분들께 하는 얘기를 들으면서 말이에요. 변호사라뇨? 사공이 많아서 배가 산으로 가는 게 아니고서야, 이 신사분께서 추천해주시는 변호사를 열 명이고 못 둘 까닭이 어디 있어요.\"\n\n\"이분은 참으로 분별 있는 분이로군.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"부디 저 사람을 설득해주시기 바랍니다, 배그넷 부인.\"\n\n\"설득이요, 나리?\" 그녀가 대답했습니다. \"원, 천만에요. 나리는 조지를 몰라서 그래요. 자, 저기!\" 배그넷 부인이 바구니를 내버려 두고 햇볕에 그을린 두 맨손으로 그를 가리켰습니다. \"저기 저렇게 서 있잖아요! 엉뚱한 쪽으로는, 하늘 아래 어떤 사람의 인내심도 바닥나게 만들 만큼 고집스럽고 막무가내인 사람이라고요! 저 사람이 일단 머릿속에 뭘 집어넣고 못 박아버리면, 그걸 돌려놓느니 차라리 제 힘으로 사십팔 파운드짜리 대포를 번쩍 들어 어깨에 메는 게 빠를 거예요. 원, 내가 저 사람을 모를까!\" 배그넷 부인이 소리쳤습니다. \"내가 당신을 모를까, 조지! 이 오랜 세월 끝에 와서 나한테 새삼 딴사람 행세를 하려는 건 아니겠지, 설마?\"\n\n그녀의 다정한 분노는 그 남편에게 본보기 같은 효과를 미쳐서, 그는 기병에게 여러 번 고개를 저어, 그만 굽히라고 말없이 권했습니다. 그 틈틈이 배그넷 부인은 저를 바라보았는데, 저는 그 눈짓에서 그녀가 제게 무언가를 해주기를 바란다는 것을 알아챘지만, 그게 무엇인지는 알지 못했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 당신한테 말하길 그만둔 지가 벌써 몇 년이오, 이 양반아.\" 배그넷 부인이 절인 돼지고기에서 먼지를 조금 후 불어 날리며, 다시 저를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"그리고 신사 숙녀분들도 나만큼 당신을 잘 알게 되면, 당신한테 말하길 그만두실 거예요. 만약 당신이 너무 고집을 부려서 식사 한 술 못 받아먹을 지경만 아니라면, 여기 가져왔어요.\"\n\n\"고맙게 잘 먹겠습니다.\" 기병이 대답했습니다.\n\n\"정말 그러시겠어요, 응?\" 배그넷 부인이 너스레를 떨며 투덜투덜 이어갔습니다. \"참 별일이네요. 당신 식으로라면 굶지 않는 게 신기할 지경인데. 그게 딱 당신다운 짓일 텐데. 어쩌면 다음번엔 그러기로 작정할지도 모르지.\" 여기서 그녀가 다시 저를 바라보았는데, 저는 이제 그녀가 번갈아 문과 저를 흘끔거리는 눈짓에서, 우리가 자리를 뜨고 감옥 밖에서 그녀가 따라 나오기를 기다려주기를 바란다는 것을 알아챘습니다. 비슷한 방법으로 이를 후견인과 우드코트 씨에게 알리고, 저는 일어섰습니다.\n\n\"부디 다시 생각해주시기 바랍니다, 조지 씨.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"그리고 다시 찾아뵙겠습니다, 그때는 좀 더 분별 있어지셨기를 바라면서요.\"\n\n\"저보다 더 고마워하는 사람은 못 만나실 겁니다, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"하지만 더 설득에 응하는 분은 만날 수 있기를 바라요.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"그리고 부디 헤아려주세요. 이 수수께끼를 풀고 이 일을 실제로 저지른 자를 찾아내는 것이, 당신뿐 아니라 다른 사람들에게도 더없이 중대한 일일 수 있다는 것을요.\"\n\n그는 공손하게 제 말을 들었지만 그 말에 그리 마음을 두지는 않았습니다. 저는 이미 문 쪽으로 가던 길이라 몸을 약간 돌린 채 이 말을 했습니다. 그는 (나중에 사람들이 제게 전해준 바로는) 제 키와 몸매를 눈여겨보고 있었는데, 그것이 갑자기 그의 눈길을 사로잡은 모양이었습니다.\n\n\"참 묘하군요.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그때도 그렇게 생각했었는데!\"\n\n후견인이 그게 무슨 말이냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 나리.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"제 불운이 저를 그 사람이 살해되던 밤 그 사람 계단으로 이끌었을 때, 어둠 속에서 서머슨 양과 어찌나 닮은 형체가 제 곁을 지나가던지, 하마터면 말을 걸 뻔했습니다.\"\n\n순간 저는 그때까지 한 번도 느껴본 적 없는, 그리고 그 뒤로도 두 번 다시 느낀 적 없는, 부디 앞으로도 결코 느끼지 않기를 바라는 그런 전율에 휩싸였습니다.\n\n\"제가 올라가는 동안 그건 아래로 내려와서,\" 기병이 말했습니다. \"달빛이 비치는 창문을 가로질러 갔는데, 헐렁한 검은 망토를 두르고 있었지요. 술이 깊게 달린 게 눈에 띄었습니다. 어쨌든 지금 이야기와는 상관없는 일입니다만, 그 순간 서머슨 양이 그것과 어찌나 닮아 보이던지 문득 떠올랐던 겁니다.\"\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 제 안에 일어난 감정들을 저는 따로 가려내어 또렷이 말할 수가 없습니다. 다만 이것만은 분명합니다. 처음부터 수사를 좇아야 한다고 느꼈던 그 막연한 의무와 책임이, 감히 제 자신에게 어떤 물음도 똑똑히 던지지 못한 채로 더욱 커졌다는 것, 그리고 제가 두려워할 만한 까닭이라곤 도무지 있을 수 없다고 분개하듯 확신했다는 것입니다.\n\n우리 셋은 감옥에서 나와, 외진 곳에 있는 정문에서 조금 떨어져 이리저리 거닐었습니다. 오래 기다리지 않아 배그넷 부부도 나와서 곧 우리에게 다가왔습니다.\n\n배그넷 부인의 두 눈에는 저마다 눈물이 맺혀 있었고, 얼굴은 상기되고 다급해 보였습니다. \"조지한테는 내가 어떻게 생각하는지 안 내비쳤지요, 아가씨.\" 다가오면서 그녀가 처음 꺼낸 말이었습니다. \"하지만 저 사람, 가엾게도 영 위태로운 처지예요!\"\n\n\"잘 보살피고 신중을 기하고 든든히 돕는다면 그렇지 않을 겁니다.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"나리 같은 신사분께서 더 잘 아시겠지요, 나리.\" 배그넷 부인이 잿빛 망토 자락으로 황급히 눈물을 닦으며 대답했습니다. \"하지만 저는 저 사람이 걱정돼요. 저렇게 무심하게, 본심도 아닌 말을 그렇게나 많이 했잖아요. 배심원 신사들은 저를, 아니 저 사람을, 리그넘하고 저처럼 그렇게 알아들어 주지 않을지도 몰라요. 게다가 저 사람한테 불리한 정황이 어찌나 많이 일어났는지, 또 저 사람한테 불리하게 증언하려고 나설 사람도 어찌나 많은지, 그리고 버킷은 어찌나 음흉한지요.\"\n\n\"중고 첼로 가지고. 게다가 어릴 때. 피리를 불었다고 말했지.\" 배그넷 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 덧붙였습니다.\n\n\"자, 들어보세요, 아가씨.\" 배그넷 부인이 말했습니다. \"아가씨라고 했지만, 다들 들으시라는 거예요! 이 담벼락 모퉁이로 좀 오세요, 말씀드릴 테니!\"\n\n배그넷 부인이 우리를 좀 더 으슥한 곳으로 급히 끌고 갔는데, 처음엔 너무 숨이 차서 말을 잇지 못했고, 그 바람에 배그넷 씨가 한마디 했습니다. \"여보! 어서 말해줘!\"\n\n\"그러니까요, 아가씨.\" 그녀가 바람을 더 쐬려고 보닛 끈을 풀면서 말을 이었습니다. \"조지를 이 일에서 돌려놓으려면, 저 사람을 움직일 새로운 힘이 없는 한 차라리 도버성을 옮기는 게 빠를 거예요. 그런데 그 힘을 제가 손에 넣었답니다!\"\n\n\"당신은 보석 같은 여인이로군요.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"어서 말해보세요!\"\n\n\"자, 들어보세요, 아가씨.\" 그녀가 한 문장마다 열두 번씩 다급하게, 동요한 듯 두 손을 마주치며 말을 이었습니다. \"친척이 없다는 저 사람 말은 죄다 헛소리예요. 저쪽이 저 사람을 모르는 거지, 저 사람은 저쪽을 안다고요. 저 사람이 다른 누구한테보다 저한테 이따금 말을 더 많이 했는데, 언젠가 우리 울리치한테 어머니 머리를 세게 하고 주름지게 만든다는 얘기를 한 게 괜한 게 아니었어요. 오십 파운드를 걸어도 좋아요, 그날 저 사람은 어머니를 본 거예요. 어머니가 살아 계셔요, 그러니 당장 이리 모셔 와야 해요!\"\n\n배그넷 부인은 곧바로 핀 몇 개를 입에 물고, 잿빛 망토 높이보다 조금 더 위로 치맛자락을 빙 둘러 핀으로 걷어 올리기 시작했는데, 그 일을 비할 데 없이 날쌔고 능란하게 해냈습니다.\n\n\"리그넘,\" 배그넷 부인이 말했습니다. \"당신은 애들을 잘 보고 있어요, 여보, 그리고 나한테 우산을 줘요! 나는 그 노부인을 모셔 오러 링컨셔로 떠날 테니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 부인 좀 보게.\" 후견인이 주머니에 손을 넣으며 외쳤습니다. \"그분이 어떻게 가신다는 겁니까? 돈은 얼마나 있고요?\"\n\n배그넷 부인이 다시 치맛자락을 더듬어 가죽 지갑을 꺼내, 그 안에서 실링 몇 닢을 황급히 세어보고는 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 도로 닫았습니다.\n\n\"제 걱정은 마세요, 아가씨. 저는 군인의 아내라 제 방식대로 여행하는 데 익숙하답니다. 리그넘, 여보,\" 그를 입맞추며 말했습니다. \"이건 당신 거고, 셋은 애들 거예요. 자, 나는 조지 어머니를 찾아 링컨셔로 떠나요!\"\n\n그러고는 우리 셋이 어안이 벙벙해서 서로를 멀거니 바라보고 서 있는 사이에, 그녀는 정말로 길을 나섰습니다. 잿빛 망토를 두르고 씩씩한 걸음으로 정말 터덜터덜 걸어가, 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌습니다.\n\n\"배그넷 씨.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"부인을 저대로 보내실 작정입니까?\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없습니다.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"한번은 세상 저편에서도 혼자 집까지 찾아왔으니까요. 똑같은 잿빛 망토 두르고. 똑같은 우산 들고. 저 사람이 무슨 말을 하든, 따르시오. 그냥 따르시오! 저 사람이 무슨 말을 하든, 나는 따른다오. 저 사람이 해내거든요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 부인은 보기만큼이나 정직하고 진실한 분이로군요.\" 후견인이 받아 말했습니다. \"그 이상 더 좋은 말을 할 수 없겠습니다.\"\n\n\"저 사람은 둘도 없는 부대의 선임 부사관이지요.\" 배그넷 씨가 자기도 길을 나서며 어깨너머로 우리를 돌아보고 말했습니다. \"저런 사람이 또 없습니다. 하지만 저 사람 앞에서는 절대 그런 말 안 합니다. 군기는 지켜야 하니까요.\"\n\n버킷 씨와 그 통통한 집게손가락은 지금 같은 상황에서 자주 머리를 맞대고 의논을 나눈다. 버킷 씨가 이처럼 다급한 문제를 골똘히 궁리할 때면 그 통통한 집게손가락은 마치 친근한 정령의 품격으로 곧추서는 듯하다. 손가락을 귀에 갖다 대면 손가락이 정보를 속삭이고, 입술에 갖다 대면 비밀을 지키라 명령하고, 코를 문지르면 후각을 예리하게 다듬어주고, 죄지은 자 앞에서 흔들면 그자를 홀려 파멸로 이끈다. 형사 신전의 점쟁이들은 한결같이 예언하니, 버킷 씨와 그 손가락이 한참 회의를 벌이면 머지않아 무시무시한 복수자의 소식이 들려오리라는 것이다.\n\n평소에는 인간 본성을 온화하게 탐구하는 편이라, 대체로 인류의 어리석음을 호되게 나무라지 않는 너그러운 철학자인 버킷 씨는, 숱한 집을 두루 누비고 끝도 없는 거리를 어슬렁거리는데, 겉보기엔 마치 목적을 잃고 시들시들 헤매는 사람 같다. 그는 자기 동족을 더없이 다정하게 대하며 거의 누구와도 함께 술잔을 기울인다. 돈을 쓰는 데 인색하지 않고, 태도가 상냥하며, 대화는 천진난만하다—그러나 잔잔히 흐르는 그의 삶 밑바닥에는 집게손가락의 저류가 미끄러져 흐른다.\n\n시간도 장소도 버킷 씨를 묶어두지 못한다. 추상적인 인간처럼 그는 오늘 여기 있고 내일은 사라진다—그러나 인간과는 사뭇 달리, 다음 날이면 다시 여기 와 있다. 오늘 저녁이면 그는 도심에 있는 레스터 데드록 경 저택 문간의 철제 횃불 소화기를 무심한 듯 들여다볼 것이고, 내일 아침이면 체스니 월드의 납지붕 위를 거닐 것이니, 그곳은 옛날 그 노인이 거닐던 곳, 백 기니로 그 유령을 달랜다는 바로 그 자리다. 서랍이며 책상이며 호주머니며, 그자에게 딸린 모든 물건을 버킷 씨는 샅샅이 살핀다. 그러고 몇 시간 뒤면 그와 로마인이 단둘이 마주 앉아 집게손가락을 견주고 있으리라.\n\n* 로마인: 토킹혼 씨 방 천장에 그려진 로마인 그림으로, 아래쪽을 손가락으로 가리키고 있다.\n이런 일들이 가정의 단란함과는 도무지 어울리지 않으리라는 건 짐작할 만하지만, 버킷 씨가 지금 집에 들어가지 않는다는 것만은 분명하다. 버킷 부인의 동무 노릇을 평소엔 그가 높이 사면서도—타고난 형사 천재성을 지닌 부인이라, 직업적 단련으로 갈고닦았다면 큰일을 해냈을 테지만 영리한 아마추어 수준에서 멈춰버린 분이다—그 정겨운 위안만큼은 멀찍이 물려둔다. 버킷 부인은 동무와 대화를 그 집 하숙인(다행히 부인이 마음을 쓰는 상냥한 여인이다)에게 기대고 있다.\n\n장례식 날 링컨스 인 광장에 큰 군중이 모여든다. 레스터 데드록 경이 몸소 의식에 참석한다. 엄밀히 말하면 그 밖에 사람으로 따라온 이는 셋뿐이니, 두들 경과 윌리엄 버피, 그리고 (머릿수 채우려 끼워 넣은) 골골한 친척이다. 그러나 비탄에 잠긴 마차의 수효는 어마어마하다. 귀족 사회가 이 동네에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 네 바퀴 달린 애도를 한껏 쏟아낸다. 마차 측면에 새긴 문장이 어찌나 빼곡한지, 마치 문장원이 하루아침에 부모를 한꺼번에 잃기라도 한 듯하다. 푸들 공작은 은제 바퀴통과 특허받은 차축에 최신 개량을 모조리 갖춘 호화로운 먼지와 재 한 무더기를 보내고, 그 뒤에 키 큰 슬픔의 벌레 셋이 비탄의 다발을 이루어 매달려 있다. 런던의 모든 의전 마부가 죄다 상복에 잠긴 듯하다. 만일 녹슨 옷차림의 그 죽은 노인에게 말 보는 안목이 남아 있다면(그럴 리야 없겠지만), 오늘 같은 날엔 무척이나 흡족하리라.\n\n장의사들과 마차 행렬, 그리고 슬픔에 흠뻑 젖은 그 숱한 종아리 사이에 끼어, 버킷 씨는 비탄에 잠긴 마차 한 대 속에 몸을 숨기고 앉아 격자 차양 너머로 느긋하게 군중을 살핀다. 그는 군중을 보는 눈썰미가 날카롭다—하기야 못 보는 게 어디 있으랴?—이쪽저쪽 둘러보며, 한 번은 마차 이쪽에서, 한 번은 저쪽에서, 한 번은 저택 창문을 올려다보고, 한 번은 사람들 머리 위를 훑으니, 그의 눈을 벗어나는 것이 없다.\n\n\"옳지, 거기 있구먼, 내 짝꿍, 응?\" 버킷 씨는 혼잣말로, 자기가 일러둔 대로 고인의 저택 계단에 자리 잡은 버킷 부인에게 말을 건넨다. \"그래, 거기 있구먼. 거기 있어! 아주 멀쩡해 보이는걸, 버킷 부인!\"\n\n행렬은 아직 출발하지 않고, 모임의 까닭이 된 인물이 운구되어 나오기를 기다리고 있다. 가장 앞쪽 문장 새긴 마차에 탄 버킷 씨는 통통한 집게손가락 두 개로 격자를 머리카락 한 올만큼 벌려 잡고 바깥을 내다본다.\n\n그리고 그가 여전히 버킷 부인에게 골몰해 있다는 것은, 남편으로서 그 정이 얼마나 깊은지를 잘 보여준다. \"거기 있구먼, 내 짝꿍, 응?\" 그는 중얼중얼 되뇐다. \"우리 하숙인도 같이 있고. 내가 자네를 눈여겨보고 있다네, 버킷 부인. 몸성히 잘 있길 바라네, 여보!\"\n\n버킷 씨는 더는 한마디도 하지 않고, 더없이 주의 깊은 눈으로 앉아 있다가, 귀족의 비밀이 가득 담겼던 자루 같은 그 보관소가 운구되어 내려오고—그 모든 비밀은 지금 어디 있을까? 그자가 아직 간직하고 있을까? 그 느닷없는 여정에 함께 날아가버렸을까?—이윽고 행렬이 움직여 버킷 씨의 시야가 바뀔 때까지 그대로 있다. 그러고 나서 그는 편안한 승차에 몸을 맡길 채비를 하고, 혹시 이런 지식이 쓸모 있을 날이 올까 싶어 마차의 비품을 눈여겨봐 둔다.\n\n어두운 마차에 갇힌 토킹혼 씨와 자기 마차에 갇힌 버킷 씨, 그 대비란! 한쪽은 그 작은 상처 하나가 그를 옴짝달싹 못 하는 잠 속으로 던져넣어 거리의 돌바닥 위에서 그토록 무겁게 덜컹대게 하고, 그 상처 너머로는 헤아릴 수 없이 광막한 공간이 펼쳐져 있다. 다른 한쪽은 머리카락 한 올 한 올마다 깃든 그 빈틈없는 경계 상태로 그를 붙잡아두는, 좁다란 핏줄기의 자취가 있을 뿐이다! 그러나 두 사람에겐 어차피 매한가지다. 어느 쪽도 그런 일로 마음 쓰지 않는다.\n\n버킷 씨는 특유의 느긋한 태도로 행렬을 끝까지 지켜보다가, 미리 마음먹어둔 기회가 오자 마차에서 슬그머니 빠져나온다. 그는 레스터 데드록 경의 저택으로 향하는데, 그곳은 지금 그에게 일종의 제집 같은 곳이라, 시간을 가리지 않고 마음대로 드나들며, 늘 환대받고 떠받들어지며, 집안 전체를 훤히 꿰고서, 신비로운 위엄의 분위기 속을 거니는 곳이다.\n\n버킷 씨는 노크도 초인종도 필요 없다. 그는 미리 손써서 열쇠를 마련해두었기에 마음 내키는 대로 들어갈 수 있다. 그가 현관을 가로지르는데 머큐리가 알린다. \"또 편지 한 통 왔습니다, 버킷 씨, 우편으로요.\" 그러면서 편지를 건넨다.\n* 머큐리: 화려한 제복을 입은 하인을 빗댄 표현. 본디 로마 신화의 전령 신.\n\n\"또 한 통이라, 응?\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n행여 머큐리가 버킷 씨의 편지를 두고 무슨 호기심을 조금이라도 품고 있더라도, 그 빈틈없는 인물은 그 호기심을 채워줄 위인이 아니다. 버킷 씨는 마치 머큐리의 얼굴이 몇 마일이나 뻗은 풍경이고 자기는 그것을 한가로이 감상하기라도 하는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라본다.\n\n\"혹시 코담뱃갑 가지고 다니나?\" 버킷 씨가 묻는다.\n\n공교롭게도 머큐리는 코담배를 하지 않는다.\n\n\"어디서든 한 줌 가져다줄 수 있겠나?\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"고맙네. 무슨 종류든 상관없네. 가리지 않으니까. 고맙네!\"\n\n누군가 아래층에서 일부러 빌려온 통에서 느긋하게 한 줌 떠넣고, 한 번은 콧구멍 이쪽으로 한 번은 저쪽으로 제법 거하게 음미하는 시늉을 하고 나서, 버킷 씨는 무척 신중하게 제대로 된 물건이라 선언하고는, 편지를 손에 든 채 걸음을 옮긴다.\n\n그런데 버킷 씨가 큰 서재 안의 작은 서재로 올라가는 그 얼굴은 하루에 편지를 수십 통씩 받는 사람의 얼굴이건만, 사실 그의 삶에 서신 왕래가 흔한 일은 아니다. 그는 글쓰기에 능한 사람이 아니라, 늘 손닿는 곳에 지니고 다니는 호주머니 지팡이를 다루듯 펜을 다루며, 자기에게 편지를 보내는 일을 남들에게 권하지도 않으니, 그것이 미묘한 일을 처리하기엔 너무 어수룩하고 곧이곧대로인 방식이라 여기기 때문이다. 게다가 그는 증거로 제출된 불리한 편지들을 숱하게 봐왔기에, 그런 걸 써댄다는 게 얼마나 풋내기 짓인지 곱씹을 일이 많았다. 이런 까닭에 그는 편지와는, 보내는 쪽이든 받는 쪽이든, 거의 인연이 없다. 그런데도 지난 스물네 시간 동안 무려 여섯 통이나 받았다.\n\n\"그리고 이것은,\" 버킷 씨가 편지를 탁자 위에 펼쳐놓으며 말한다. \"똑같은 필체에다, 똑같은 두 단어로 되어 있군.\"\n\n무슨 두 단어인가?\n\n그는 문에 열쇠를 돌려 잠그고, (숱한 이들에게 운명의 책인) 검은 수첩의 끈을 풀고서, 그 옆에 또 다른 편지 한 통을 나란히 놓고는, 둘 다에 굵직하게 적힌 글씨를 읽는다. \"데드록 마님.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"하지만 이런 익명의 정보가 없었어도 그 돈은 벌 수 있었을걸.\"\n\n편지들을 운명의 책에 도로 넣고 다시 끈을 묶고 나서, 그가 문을 따자 마침 저녁 식사가 들어온다. 셰리주 한 병을 곁들인 그득한 쟁반에 받쳐 들어온다. 버킷 씨는 거리낌 없는 친구들 사이에서 곧잘 말하곤 하니, 잘 익은 갈색 동인도 셰리주를 한 모금 들이켜는 것이 세상 무엇보다 좋다고 한다. 그리하여 그는 입맛을 쩝쩝 다시며 잔을 채웠다 비웠다 하며 다과를 즐기던 중에 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠오른다.\n\n버킷 씨는 그 방과 옆방을 잇는 문을 살며시 열고 안을 들여다본다. 서재는 텅 비어 있고, 난롯불은 잦아들고 있다. 버킷 씨의 눈은 방 안을 비둘기처럼 한 바퀴 휙 돌고 나서, 편지가 도착하면 으레 올려두는 탁자 위에 내려앉는다. 그 위에 레스터 경 앞으로 온 편지 몇 통이 놓여 있다. 버킷 씨는 가까이 다가가 수신인을 살핀다. \"아니로군.\" 그가 말한다. \"그 필체로 된 건 없어. 편지 받는 건 나뿐이군. 레스터 데드록 준남작께는 내일 알려드리면 되겠어.\"\n\n그러고 그는 입맛 좋게 저녁 식사를 마저 끝내러 돌아갔다가, 한숨 가볍게 자고 나서 응접실로 불려간다. 레스터 경은 지난 며칠 저녁 내내 그를 그곳에서 맞아, 보고할 만한 게 있는지 묻곤 했다. (장례식으로 몹시 지친) 골골한 친척과 볼럼니아가 곁에 시중들고 있다.\n\n버킷 씨는 이 세 사람에게 저마다 뚜렷이 다른 절을 세 번 한다. 레스터 경에게는 공경의 절을, 볼럼니아에게는 정중한 흠모의 절을, 골골한 친척에게는 알아본다는 절을 하는데, 그 절은 가볍게 이렇게 말하는 듯하다. \"당신은 도심의 멋쟁이고, 당신은 나를 알고, 나도 당신을 알지요.\" 이렇게 자기 처세술의 작은 표본들을 골고루 나눠주고 나서, 버킷 씨는 두 손을 비빈다.\n\n\"새로 전할 소식이라도 있나, 경관?\" 레스터 경이 묻는다. \"나와 따로 나눌 이야기라도 있는가?\"\n\n\"글쎄요—오늘 밤은 아닙니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 내 시간은,\" 레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"능욕당한 법의 존엄을 되찾는 일이라면 전적으로 자네 마음대로 쓸 수 있으니 말이네.\"\n\n버킷 씨는 헛기침을 하고는 연지를 바르고 목걸이를 두른 볼럼니아를 흘끔 보는데, 마치 정중하게 이렇게 말하려는 듯하다. \"정말이지, 당신은 어여쁜 분이외다. 당신 연배에 당신보다 못한 얼굴을 수백 명은 봤습니다, 정말이지.\"\n\n어여쁜 볼럼니아는 자기 매력이 인간미를 자아내는 힘이 있다는 걸 어쩌면 어렴풋이 알고 있는지, 삼각모 모양으로 접던 쪽지 쓰기를 멈추고 생각에 잠긴 듯 진주 목걸이를 매만진다. 버킷 씨는 속으로 그 장신구의 값을 어림하고는, 볼럼니아가 시를 쓰고 있을 법도 하다고 짐작한다.\n\n\"만일 내가,\" 레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"이 극악무도한 사건에 자네의 온갖 기량을 다 쏟으라고 더없이 단호하게 당부하지 않았다면, 나는 지금 이 자리를 빌려 혹 빠뜨린 것이 있다면 바로잡고자 하네. 비용은 일절 따지지 말게. 모든 경비를 내가 치를 작정이네. 자네가 맡은 목적을 좇느라 쓰는 비용이라면, 그 어떤 것이라도 내가 단 한순간이라도 망설이며 떠맡지 않을 까닭이 없네.\"\n\n버킷 씨는 이 너그러움에 답하여 다시 한번 레스터 경에게 올리던 절을 한다.\n\n\"내 마음은,\" 레스터 경이 너그러운 온정을 담아 덧붙인다. \"쉬 짐작하겠지만, 그 끔찍한 사건 이후로 평정을 되찾지 못했네. 앞으로도 결코 되찾지 못하겠지. 그러나 오늘 밤은, 충직하고 열성스럽고 헌신적이던 한 추종자의 유해를 무덤에 묻는 시련을 겪고 나니, 분노로 가슴이 가득하네.\"\n\n레스터 경의 목소리가 떨리고 잿빛 머리카락이 머리 위에서 흔들린다. 두 눈에 눈물이 고이고, 그의 본성에서 가장 고결한 부분이 일깨워진다.\n\n\"단언하건대,\" 그가 말한다. \"엄숙히 단언하건대, 이 범죄가 밝혀지고 정의의 절차에 따라 처벌되기 전까지는, 내 이름에 오점이 묻은 것만 같은 심정이네. 자기 인생의 큰 부분을 내게 바친 신사, 자기 인생의 마지막 날을 내게 바친 신사, 늘 내 식탁에 앉고 내 지붕 아래 잠들던 신사가, 내 집에서 제집으로 떠났다가, 내 집을 나선 지 한 시간도 안 되어 쓰러졌네. 그가 내 집에서부터 미행당했을 수도, 내 집에서 감시당했을 수도, 심지어 내 집과 관계가 있다는 이유로 처음부터 표적이 되었을 수도 있다는 걸 부정할 수 없네—그 관계 탓에, 그가 제 몸을 사리던 처신으로는 짐작도 못 할 만큼 더 큰 재산을 지녔고 훨씬 더 중요한 인물이라 여겨졌을지도 모르니 말이네. 내가 가진 재력과 영향력과 지위로도 이런 범죄를 저지른 자들을 모조리 밝혀내지 못한다면, 나는 그 신사의 추억을 향한 존경도, 늘 내게 충직하던 이에 대한 신의도 지켜내지 못하는 것이네.\"\n\n그가 큰 감정과 진심을 담아 이렇게 단언하며, 마치 회중을 앞에 두고 연설하듯 방 안을 둘러보는 동안, 버킷 씨는 관찰하는 듯한 진중한 눈으로 그를 흘끔 보는데, 무엄한 생각인지 몰라도 그 눈에는 어쩌면 한 줄기 연민이 어려 있을지도 모른다.\n\n\"오늘의 의식은,\" 레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"고인이 된 내 벗이\"—그는 죽음이 모든 차별을 평등하게 만든다는 듯 그 말에 힘을 준다—\"이 나라의 정화 같은 분들께 어떤 존경을 받았는지를 놀랍도록 잘 보여주었거니와, 그것이, 말하건대, 이 더없이 끔찍하고 대담한 범죄로 내가 받은 충격을 한층 더 키웠네. 설령 이 짓을 저지른 자가 내 형제라 해도, 나는 용서치 않을 것이네.\"\n\n버킷 씨는 자못 진중한 표정을 짓는다. 볼럼니아는 고인이 더없이 믿음직스럽고 더없이 소중한 분이었노라 한마디 한다!\n\n\"아가씨께는 큰 상실로 느껴지시겠지요.\" 버킷 씨가 달래듯 대답한다. \"당연하고말고요. 그분은 상실감을 자아낼 만한 분이었지요, 정말 그러셨고말고요.\"\n\n볼럼니아는 그 대답으로, 자기 섬세한 마음이 평생토록 이 슬픔을 결코 떨쳐내지 못하리라 굳게 마음먹었으며, 신경이 영영 곤두서버렸고, 다시 미소 지을 날이 오리라곤 조금도 기대하지 않는다는 것을 버킷 씨에게 알린다. 그러는 동안에도 그녀는 바스에 사는 그 무시무시한 늙은 장군에게 보낼 삼각모 쪽지를 접으며, 자기의 우울한 처지를 그려 보인다.\n\n\"섬세한 여성분께는 충격이지요.\" 버킷 씨가 공감하며 말한다. \"허나 차차 가실 겁니다.\"\n\n볼럼니아는 무엇보다도 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알고 싶어 한다. 그들이 그 무시무시한 군인을 유죄로 만들려는 것인지, 아니면 뭐라고 부르든 그런 걸 하려는 것인지? 그자에게 공범이 있었는지, 아니면 법률 용어로 뭐라고 하든 그런 게 있었는지? 그러고는 그런 식의 천진한 질문을 한참 더 늘어놓는다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 아가씨.\" 버킷 씨가 그 손가락을 설득하듯 움직이며 대답한다—그리고 타고난 정중함이 어찌나 몸에 뱄는지 하마터면 \"여보\"라고 할 뻔한다—\"지금 이 순간엔 그런 질문에 답하기가 쉽지 않습니다. 지금 이 순간엔 말이지요. 저는 이 사건에 매달려왔습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨는 레스터 경의 중요한 신분에 걸맞게 그를 대화에 끌어들인다. \"아침이고 한낮이고 한밤이고요. 셰리주 한두 잔이 아니었다면, 제 머리를 이토록 팽팽히 당겨두지는 못했을 겁니다. 저는 아가씨의 질문에 답할 수도 있지만, 직무가 그것을 금합니다. 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께서는 머지않아 밝혀진 모든 것을 알게 되실 겁니다. 그리고 부디 그것이\"—버킷 씨가 다시 진중한 표정을 짓는다—\"나리의 마음에 흡족하길 바랍니다.\"\n\n골골한 친척은 그저 누구든 하나 처형되길—본보기로—바랄 뿐이다. 만 파운드짜리 자리에 사람 앉히는 것보다, 사람 하나 당장 목매다는 게 더 어려운 노릇이라고—더 손써야 한다고 본다. 의심할 바 없으니—본보기로—엉뚱한 놈이라도 하나 목매다는 게 아무도 안 매다는 것보다 훨씬 낫다는 것이다.\n\n\"당신은 세상 물정을 아시지요, 안 그렇습니까, 나리.\" 버킷 씨가 칭찬하듯 눈을 반짝이고 손가락을 구부리며 말한다. \"그러니 제가 이 아가씨께 말씀드린 것을 당신이 거들어주실 수 있겠지요. 당신께는 제가 입수한 정보에 따라 일에 착수했다고 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없습니다. 당신은 여성분께 기대할 수 없는 것까지도 꿰뚫고 계시니까요. 원, 당신처럼 높은 사회적 지위에 계신 분이야 더더욱 그렇지요, 아가씨.\" 버킷 씨가 또 한 번 \"여보\" 소리가 아슬아슬하게 새어 나올 뻔한 통에 얼굴이 발그레해지며 말한다.\n\n\"이 경관은, 볼럼니아,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"제 직무에 충실하니, 더없이 옳은 일이지.\"\n\n버킷 씨가 중얼거린다. \"나리의 인정을 받는 영광을 누리니 기쁩니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\"\n\n\"실은, 볼럼니아,\" 레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"이 경관에게 네가 던진 그런 질문을 하는 것은 본받을 만한 본보기가 못 되네. 그는 자기 책임을 누구보다 잘 판단하는 사람이고, 자기 책임으로 행동하는 사람이네. 또 법을 만드는 일을 거드는 우리가, 그 법을 집행하는 이들을 가로막거나 간섭하는 것은 도리가 아니지. 아니면,\" 레스터 경이 다소 엄하게 말하니, 그가 문장을 매듭짓기도 전에 볼럼니아가 끼어들려 했기 때문이다. \"아니면 능욕당한 법의 존엄을 되찾는 이들을 말이네.\"\n\n볼럼니아는 더없이 겸손하게 해명하니, 자기는 (대체로 경박한 또래 여성들과 마찬가지로) 단지 호기심을 핑계로 댄 것이 아니라, 모두가 그 상실을 애통해하는 그 사랑스러운 분을 향한 안타까움과 관심으로 그야말로 애가 타 죽을 지경이라는 것이다.\n\n\"좋아, 볼럼니아.\" 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"그렇다면 아무리 신중해도 지나치지 않으니라.\"\n\n버킷 씨는 말이 끊긴 틈을 타 다시 입을 연다.\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 나리께서 허락하신다면 우리끼리 이 아가씨께 말씀드리는 것이 거리낄 것 없겠습니다만, 저는 이 사건이 거의 다 마무리되었다고 봅니다. 멋진 사건입니다—멋진 사건이지요—그리고 마무리에 모자란 약간의 것들은, 몇 시간 안에 채워넣을 수 있으리라 기대합니다.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 참으로 기쁘네.\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"자네에게 무척 큰 공이 되겠군.\"\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 자못 진지하게 대답한다. \"그것이 제게 공이 되는 동시에 모두에게도 흡족한 일로 드러나길 바랍니다. 제가 이걸 멋진 사건이라고 말씀드릴 때는, 아시겠지만, 아가씨,\" 버킷 씨가 레스터 경을 진중하게 흘끔 보며 말을 잇는다. \"제 관점에서 그렇다는 겁니다. 다른 관점에서 보자면, 이런 사건들은 으레 어느 정도 언짢은 구석이 따르게 마련이지요. 집안 일이란 게 참 별별 희한한 게 다 우리 귀에 들어옵니다, 아가씨. 원, 아가씨가 보시기엔 그야말로 괴이쩍은 일이라 여기실 만한 것들이 말입니다.\"\n\n볼럼니아가 천진하게 가벼운 비명을 지르며 그러리라 짐작한다.\n\n\"예, 게다가 점잖은 집안에서도, 고귀한 집안에서도, 대단한 집안에서도 그렇습니다.\" 버킷 씨가 또 한 번 레스터 경을 곁눈으로 진중하게 살피며 말한다. \"저는 전에도 고귀한 집안에 고용되는 영광을 누려본 적이 있는데, 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다—아니, 당신마저도 짐작 못 하실 거라고까지 말씀드리겠습니다, 나리,\" 이건 골골한 친척에게 하는 말이다. \"어떤 짓거리들이 벌어지는지 말입니다!\"\n\n소파 쿠션을 제 머리 위로 끌어다 덮으며 지루함에 늘어져 있던 친척이 하품하며 \"그렇겠지\"라고 말하는데, 그건 \"아주 그럼직하다\"를 닳고 닳게 줄인 말이다.\n\n레스터 경이 이제 경관을 물러나게 할 때라 여기고, 여기서 위엄 있게 끼어들어 이렇게 말한다. \"잘했네. 고맙네!\" 그리고 손을 한 번 내젓는데, 이는 이야기가 여기서 끝났다는 뜻일 뿐 아니라, 고귀한 집안이 천한 버릇에 빠지면 그 대가를 치러야 마땅하다는 뜻도 담겨 있다. \"잊지 말게, 경관.\" 그가 짐짓 너그럽게 덧붙인다. \"원할 때 언제든 나를 마음대로 쓸 수 있다는 것을.\"\n\n버킷 씨가 (여전히 진중하게) 묻기를, 자기가 예상하는 만큼 일이 진척되어 있다면, 내일 아침이 괜찮으신지 여쭙는다. 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"내게는 어느 때나 매한가지네.\" 버킷 씨가 세 번 절을 하고 물러나려는데 깜빡 잊은 점 하나가 떠오른다.\n\n\"그런데, 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 조심스레 되돌아오며 말한다. \"계단에 그 현상금 벽보를 누가 붙였습니까?\"\n\n\"내가 거기에 붙이라 지시했네.\" 레스터 경이 대답한다.\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 외람된 일이 아니라면, 왜 그러셨는지 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"전혀 외람되지 않네. 나는 집 안에서 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리로 그곳을 골랐네. 집안 전체에 그것을 아무리 도드라지게 내걸어도 지나치지 않다고 보네. 나는 우리 식솔들이 이 범죄의 극악함과, 그것을 기어이 처벌하겠다는 결의와, 도망쳐봐야 가망 없다는 것을 마음 깊이 새기길 바라네. 한편으로는, 경관, 자네가 이 일에 더 밝은 식견으로 무슨 거리낌이라도 본다면—\"\n\n버킷 씨는 이제 아무 거리낌도 없다. 벽보가 이미 붙은 마당이니 떼지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 것이다. 세 번 절을 거듭하고 그가 물러나며 문을 닫는데, 그 뒤로 볼럼니아의 가벼운 비명이 새어 나오니, 그 매력적으로 끔찍한 사람은 영락없는 푸른 방이라는 그녀의 평이 그에 뒤따른다.\n\n* 푸른 방: 동화 「푸른 수염」에서 살해당한 아내들의 시신이 감춰진 금단의 방. 무시무시하면서도 묘하게 끌리는 비밀의 존재를 빗댄 말.\n사교를 좋아하고 어떤 계층에도 능히 어울리는 버킷 씨는, 이내 현관 난롯불—초겨울 밤에 밝고 따스하다—앞에 서서 머큐리를 감탄하며 바라본다.\n\n\"이봐, 자네 키가 육 피트 이 인치쯤 되겠지?\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"삼 인치입니다.\" 머큐리가 말한다.\n\n\"그렇게나 크다고? 하지만 자네는 그만큼 떡 벌어져서 그렇게 안 보이는군. 자네는 다리 약한 축이 아니야, 암 아니지. 본뜬 적 있나, 모델 말이야?\" 버킷 씨가 묻는데, 눈빛과 고갯짓에 예술가 같은 분위기를 실어 보인다.\n\n머큐리는 모델이 되어본 적이 없다.\n\n\"그럼 그래야 해, 알겠나.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"언젠가 왕립 미술원 조각가로 이름 날릴 내 친구가 하나 있는데, 자네 그 균형 잡힌 몸을 대리석에 새기겠다고 데생 한 장 하는 데 두둑이 사례할 걸세. 마님은 외출하셨나, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"만찬에 나가셨습니다.\"\n\n\"거의 날마다 나가시지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"놀랄 일도 아니지!\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그분처럼 고운 여인이, 그리도 아리땁고 그리도 우아하고 그리도 기품 있는 분이, 만찬 식탁 위의 싱싱한 레몬 같아서, 가는 곳마다 좌중을 빛내니 말이야. 자네 부친도 자네처럼 이 일을 하셨나?\"\n\n아니라는 대답.\n\n\"우리 아버지는 그러셨지.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"우리 아버지는 처음엔 시동, 다음엔 하인, 다음엔 집사, 다음엔 가령, 다음엔 여관 주인이셨지. 두루 존경받으며 사시다, 애도 속에 돌아가셨어. 마지막 숨을 거두며 하시는 말씀이, 봉직이야말로 당신 인생에서 가장 명예로운 부분이라 여긴다 하셨는데, 정말 그러했네. 내 형제 하나도 봉직 일을 하고, 매부도 하나 있지. 마님은 성품이 좋으신가?\"\n\n머큐리가 대답한다. \"기대하실 만큼은요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"조금 응석받이시고? 조금 변덕스럽고? 원! 그리도 아리따운 분에게 무얼 더 바라겠나? 그래서 우리는 더 좋아하는 거 아니겠나, 안 그래?\"\n\n머큐리는 환한 복숭아꽃 빛 반바지 호주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채, 균형 잡힌 비단 다리를 한량 같은 분위기로 쭉 뻗으며, 그 말을 부정하지 못한다. 바퀴 구르는 소리가 들려오고 초인종이 거세게 울린다. \"천사 이야기를 하니,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이렇게 나타나는군!\"\n\n문이 활짝 열리고, 그녀가 현관을 가로질러 지나간다. 여전히 무척 창백한 그녀는 가벼운 상복 차림에 아름다운 팔찌 두 개를 끼고 있다. 그 팔찌의 아름다움인지 그녀 팔의 아름다움인지, 어느 쪽이 버킷 씨의 마음을 유난히 끄는 듯하다. 그는 간절한 눈빛으로 그것을 바라보며 호주머니 속에서 무언가를 짤랑인다—아마도 반 페니 동전이리라.\n\n멀찍이 있는 그를 알아채고, 그녀는 자기를 데려다준 또 다른 머큐리에게 묻는 듯한 눈길을 돌린다.\n\n\"버킷 씨입니다, 마님.\"\n\n버킷 씨가 한쪽 다리를 빼며 절을 하고 앞으로 나서며, 그 친근한 정령을 입 언저리에 슬쩍 문지른다.\n\n\"레스터 경을 뵈려고 기다리는 건가요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 마님. 이미 뵈었습니다!\"\n\n\"내게 할 말이라도 있나요?\"\n\n\"지금 당장은 아닙니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"무슨 새로운 사실이라도 알아냈나요?\"\n\n\"몇 가지요, 마님.\"\n\n이건 그저 지나가며 던지는 말일 뿐이다. 그녀는 거의 멈추지 않고, 홀로 계단을 휩쓸 듯 올라간다. 버킷 씨는 계단 발치로 다가가, 그 노인이 무덤으로 가려고 내려왔던 그 계단을 그녀가 올라가는 모습을 지켜본다. 벽 위로 어슴푸레한 무기를 든 채 거듭 새겨진 살벌한 조각상 무리를 지나, 그녀가 지나가며 흘끗 보는 그 인쇄된 벽보를 지나, 시야에서 사라질 때까지.\n\n\"저분도 사랑스러운 여인이야, 정말이지.\" 버킷 씨가 머큐리에게 돌아오며 말한다. \"그래도 그리 건강해 보이진 않는군.\"\n\n그리 건강하지 못하시다고, 머큐리가 일러준다. 두통으로 자주 고생하신다고.\n\n정말로? 그것참 안됐군! 그런 데는 산책이 좋다고 버킷 씨가 권하겠다. 그런데 마님도 산책을 해보신다고 머큐리가 받는다. 두통이 심하면 더러 두 시간씩 걸으신다고. 게다가 밤중에도.\n\n\"자네 정말 키가 육 피트 삼 인치나 되는 거 확실한가?\" 버킷 씨가 묻는다. \"잠깐 말을 끊어 미안하네만?\"\n\n의심할 여지 없다.\n\n\"자네는 워낙 균형이 잘 잡혀서 그렇게는 안 보였네. 하지만 근위병들은 다들 미남으로 치는데도 어찌나 휘청대게들 생겼는지. 밤중에 걸으신다고, 그분이? 그래도 달밤에 말이지?\"\n\n오, 그럼요. 달밤에! 물론이지요. 오, 물론입니다! 양쪽 다 이야기를 주고받으며 맞장구친다.\n\n\"자네야 직접 걸어 다니는 버릇은 없겠지?\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그럴 틈도 별로 없을 테고, 안 그래?\"\n\n게다가 머큐리는 걷는 걸 좋아하지 않는다. 마차 타는 운동을 더 좋아한다.\n\n\"하긴 그렇지.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그게 다르긴 하지. 가만 생각해보니,\" 버킷 씨가 두 손을 녹이며 활활 타는 불길을 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말한다. \"마님이 그 사건이 있던 바로 그날 밤에도 산책을 나가셨더군.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 나가셨고말고요! 제가 길 건너 정원으로 들여보내드렸지요.\"\n\n\"그러고 거기 두고 왔지. 그랬고말고. 자네가 그러는 걸 내가 봤거든.\"\n\n\"저는 당신을 못 봤는데요.\" 머큐리가 말한다.\n\n\"내가 좀 바빴거든.\" 버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"첼시에 사는 우리 이모님을 뵈러 가는 길이었는데—그 오래된 원조 빵집에서 두 집 건너에 사시지—아흔 살 된 노인이고, 독신이고, 자그마한 재산도 좀 있으시지. 그래, 마침 그때 지나가던 길이었거든. 가만있자. 몇 시쯤이었더라? 열 시는 아니었지.\"\n\n\"아홉 시 반이었습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞아. 그랬지. 그리고 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 마님이 술 장식이 깊게 달린 헐렁한 검은 망토를 두르고 계셨지?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그러셨습니다.\"\n\n그럼, 그러셨고말고. 버킷 씨는 위층에서 마저 처리할 자그마한 일이 있어 돌아가야 하지만, 그래도 머큐리와 악수를 나누며 그 유쾌한 대화에 고마움을 표해야겠다 하고는, 부탁하건대—이것 하나만 부탁한다며—한가한 반 시간이 나거든, 양쪽 다 득이 될 테니, 그 왕립 미술원 조각가에게 그 시간을 한번 내어줄 생각을 해보지 않겠느냐 한다.\n\n잠으로 기운을 차린 버킷 씨는 아침 일찍 일어나 결전의 하루를 준비한다. 깨끗한 셔츠와 물 묻힌 머리빗의 도움으로 말끔하게 매무새를 가다듬는데, 의례를 차려야 할 때면 이 도구로 고된 연구의 세월을 보낸 뒤 머리에 남은 몇 가닥 안 되는 머리칼에 윤기를 입힌다. 버킷 씨는 일을 시작할 토대로 양고기 갈비 두 점을 차린 아침을 먹는데, 거기에 차와 달걀과 토스트와 마멀레이드도 그에 걸맞은 규모로 곁들인다. 이 든든한 음식을 흠뻑 즐기고, 제 마음속 친숙한 악마와 은밀한 회의를 거친 뒤, 그는 자신만만하게 시종에게 지시한다. \"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께 그저 조용히 전해주게. 나리께서 준비되시면 저도 준비됐다고 말이야.\" 레스터 경이 옷차림을 서둘러 마치고 십 분 안에 서재에서 버킷 씨를 만나겠다는 정중한 전갈이 돌아오자, 버킷 씨는 그 방으로 가서 손가락을 턱에 댄 채 불 앞에 서서 활활 타오르는 석탄을 바라본다.\n\n버킷 씨는 무거운 일을 앞둔 사람이 으레 그러하듯 생각에 잠겨 있지만, 침착하고, 확신에 차 있고, 자신만만하다. 그 얼굴 표정으로 보자면, 큰돈 — 이를테면 백 기니쯤은 확실히 — 을 걸고 카드 패를 손에 쥔 채 솜씨 좋게 마지막 한 장까지 풀어내는 데 높은 명성이 달려 있는, 이름난 휘스트 노름꾼처럼 보일지도 모른다. 레스터 경이 모습을 드러내도 버킷 씨는 조금도 불안하거나 동요하지 않지만, 준남작이 천천히 안락의자로 다가오는 동안 어제 보였던 그 관찰자다운 진지함으로 그를 곁눈질하는데, 그 속에는 — 그런 생각이 무례하지만 않다면 — 어제와 마찬가지로 한 줄기 연민이 깃들어 있었을지도 모른다.\n\n\"기다리시게 해서 죄송합니다, 형사 양반. 오늘 아침엔 평소 시각보다 좀 늦었군요. 몸이 좋질 않아요. 요즘 겪은 동요와 분노가 내겐 너무 버거웠소. 나는 — 통풍이 있어서요\" — 레스터 경은 몸이 편치 않다고 말하려던 참이었고 다른 누구에게라면 그렇게 말했겠으나, 버킷 씨는 그 일을 빤히 다 알고 있다 — \"최근 사정 때문에 도졌소이다.\"\n\n레스터 경이 다소 힘겹게, 고통스러운 기색으로 자리에 앉자, 버킷 씨는 큼직한 손 하나를 서재 탁자에 올린 채 조금 더 가까이 다가선다.\n\n\"잘 모르겠소만, 형사 양반,\" 레스터 경이 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들며 말한다. \"우리 둘만 있기를 바라는지 어떤지 말이오. 허나 그건 전적으로 그쪽 뜻에 맡기겠소. 그러기를 원한다면 좋소. 아니라면, 데드록 양도 흥미를 가질 텐데—\"\n\n\"그게 말입니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\" 버킷 씨가 머리를 설득하듯 한쪽으로 기울이고 귀고리처럼 집게손가락을 한쪽 귀에 늘어뜨린 채 대답한다. \"지금 이 순간만큼은 아무리 은밀해도 지나치지 않습니다. 곧 아시게 되겠지만 아무리 은밀해도 지나치지 않아요. 이런 상황에서, 더구나 데드록 양처럼 사교계에서 높은 신분에 계신 숙녀분이라면 저로서야 함께해 기쁘기 그지없겠습니다만, 제 사사로운 마음을 떠나서 감히 단언컨대, 우리는 아무리 은밀해도 지나치지 않다는 것을 저는 압니다.\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐소.\"\n\n\"하도 그러해서 말입니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"마침 문에 자물쇠를 채워도 되겠냐고 여쭈려던 참이었습니다.\"\n\n\"얼마든지.\" 버킷 씨는 능숙하고 부드럽게 그 조처를 취하는데, 순전히 습관의 힘으로 잠시 무릎을 굽혀 바깥쪽에서 아무도 들여다보지 못하도록 자물쇠 안에서 열쇠를 맞춘다.\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 어제저녁에 말씀드렸지요. 이 사건을 마무리 짓는 데 아주 조금만 더 필요하다고 말입니다. 이제 그것을 마무리 지었고, 이 범행을 저지른 자에 대한 증거를 모았습니다.\"\n\n\"그 군인 말이오?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 레스터 데드록 나리. 군인이 아닙니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 깜짝 놀란 표정으로 묻는다. \"그자가 구금되었소?\"\n\n버킷 씨는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 그에게 말한다. \"여자였습니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 숨을 멈추고 외친다. \"맙소사!\"\n\n\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 한 손을 서재 탁자에 활짝 펴 짚고 다른 손 집게손가락을 인상 깊게 놀리며 그를 굽어보고 선 채 입을 연다. \"충격을 줄지도 모르는, 아니 감히 말씀드리건대 분명히 충격을 줄 일련의 사정에 대비하시도록 마음의 준비를 시켜드리는 게 제 의무입니다. 허나 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 나리는 신사이시고, 저는 신사가 어떤 분인지, 신사가 무엇을 감당할 수 있는지 압니다. 신사는 충격이 닥칠 수밖에 없을 때 그것을 대담하고 꿋꿋하게 견딥니다. 신사는 거의 어떤 타격에도 맞설 각오를 다질 수 있지요. 가령 나리 자신을 봅시다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 나리께 타격이 가해진다면, 나리는 자연히 가문을 떠올리실 겁니다. 율리우스 카이사르까지 거슬러 올라가는 — 지금은 그 위로는 가지 않겠습니다만 — 그 모든 조상께서 그 타격을 어떻게 견뎌내셨을까 스스로 물으시겠지요. 그것을 잘 견뎌냈을 분이 수십 명은 떠오르실 테고, 나리는 그분들을 위해, 또 가문의 명예를 지키기 위해 그것을 잘 견뎌내시는 겁니다. 그것이 나리께서 판단하시는 방식이고, 행동하시는 방식이지요, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\"\n\n레스터 경은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 팔걸이를 움켜쥐고, 돌처럼 굳은 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 앉아 있다.\n\n\"자, 레스터 데드록 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"이렇게 마음의 준비를 시켜드렸으니, 무엇이 제 귀에 들어왔든 잠시도 마음 쓰지 마시기를 간청드립니다. 저는 높고 낮은 숱한 인물에 대해 워낙 많은 걸 알고 있어서, 정보 한 조각쯤 많거나 적은 건 지푸라기만큼도 대수롭지 않습니다. 판 위에서 어떤 수가 나온들 저를 놀라게 할 건 없을 듯하고, 이런저런 수가 두어졌다 한들 제가 그걸 안다는 게 조금도 이상할 게 없으니, (그릇된 방향이기만 하다면) 그 어떤 수든 제 경험으로 보면 충분히 둘 법한 수이기 때문입니다. 그러니 제가 나리께 드리는 말씀은, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 제가 나리 가문의 일을 좀 안다고 해서 마음을 흩뜨리지 마시라는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"대비시켜준 점은 고맙소,\" 손도 발도 표정도 까딱하지 않은 채 잠시 침묵하던 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"필요 없는 일이기를 바라지만 말이오. 다만 선의에서 나온 것임은 인정하겠소. 부디 계속하시오. 또한—\" 레스터 경은 제 그림자 속에서 움츠러드는 듯이 보인다 \"—또한, 괜찮다면 자리에 앉으시오.\"\n\n조금도 마다할 일이 없다. 버킷 씨는 의자를 가져와 제 그림자를 줄인다. \"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이 짤막한 서론을 끝내고 본론으로 들어가겠습니다. 데드록 마님께서—\"\n\n레스터 경은 자리에서 몸을 일으키며 그를 매섭게 노려본다. 버킷 씨는 누그러뜨리는 약처럼 손가락을 놀린다.\n\n\"데드록 마님은, 보십시오, 만인의 흠모를 받으시지요. 마님이 바로 그런 분입니다. 만인의 흠모를 받는 분이에요,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"형사 양반, 나는 차라리,\" 레스터 경이 뻣뻣하게 대답한다. \"이 논의에서 마님의 이름은 통째로 빼주었으면 하오.\"\n\n\"저도 그러고 싶습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 허나 — 그건 불가능합니다.\"\n\n\"불가능하다고?\"\n\n버킷 씨는 가차 없이 고개를 젓는다.\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 그건 도무지 불가능한 일입니다. 제가 말씀드려야 할 것이 바로 마님에 관한 것이니까요. 마님이 이 모든 일이 돌아가는 축이십니다.\"\n\n\"형사 양반,\" 레스터 경이 불타는 눈과 떨리는 입술로 쏘아붙인다. \"그대는 제 의무를 알고 있소. 의무를 다하시오. 허나 그 선을 넘지 않도록 조심하시오. 나는 용납하지 않을 것이오. 나는 견디지 않을 것이오. 마님의 이름을 이 이야기에 끌어들이는 건 그대의 책임이오 — 그대의 책임이란 말이오. 마님의 이름은 천한 자들이 함부로 입에 올릴 이름이 아니란 말이오!\"\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 저는 말씀드려야 할 것만 말씀드리지, 그 이상은 하지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그러기를 바라오. 좋소. 계속하시오. 계속하란 말이오!\" 이제 그를 피하는 성난 눈과, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서도 가만히 있으려 애쓰는 성난 몸뚱이를 흘끔 보며, 버킷 씨는 집게손가락으로 더듬듯 나직한 목소리로 말을 잇는다.\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨께서 오래전부터 데드록 마님에게 불신과 의심을 품어왔다는 것을 말씀드리는 게 제 의무가 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"감히 그자가 내게 그런 걸 발설했더라면 — 결코 그러진 않았소만 — 내 손으로 그자를 죽였을 것이오!\" 레스터 경이 탁자를 손으로 내리치며 외친다. 그러나 그 행위의 열기와 분노가 한창일 때, 그는 천천히 움직이는 집게손가락과 함께 확신과 인내가 뒤섞인 채 고개를 젓는 버킷 씨의 다 안다는 듯한 눈에 붙들려 멈춘다.\n\n\"레스터 데드록 나리, 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨는 속이 깊고 입이 무거운 분이라, 그분이 맨 처음부터 마음속에 무엇을 온전히 품고 계셨는지는 제가 감히 단정할 수가 없습니다. 허나 그분 입으로 직접 들은 바, 그분은 오래전부터 데드록 마님이 어떤 필체를 보고 — 바로 이 집에서, 그것도 레스터 데드록 나리께서 친히 그 자리에 계실 때 — 나리께서 마님께 구애하시기 전에 그분의 연인이었고 마땅히 그분의 남편이 되었어야 할 어떤 사람이 지독한 가난 속에 살아 있다는 사실을 알아차렸으리라 의심하셨습니다.\" 버킷 씨는 잠시 멈췄다가 신중하게 되뇐다. \"마땅히 그분의 남편이 되었어야 할 사람, 그건 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그분 입으로 들은 바, 그 사람이 얼마 안 가 죽자, 그분은 데드록 마님이 그자의 누추한 하숙집과 누추한 무덤을 홀로 몰래 찾아갔으리라 의심하셨습니다. 또 제 나름의 조사와 제 눈과 귀로 알아낸 바, 데드록 마님은 정말로 자신의 하녀 옷을 입고 그런 방문을 하셨습니다. 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 마님을 — 저희가 흔히 쓰는 표현을 빌리는 걸 양해해주신다면 — '꿰뚫어보라'고 저를 고용하셨고, 저는 거기까지는 마님을 완전히 꿰뚫어보았습니다. 저는 링컨스 인 광장의 그 방에서, 데드록 마님의 길잡이였던 증인과 함께 그 하녀를 대면시켰는데, 마님이 그 젊은 처자의 옷을 그 처자도 모르게 입었다는 데는 한 점의 의심도 있을 수 없었습니다. 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 저는 어제 이런 불쾌한 사실들을 밝히는 데 조금이나마 길을 닦아보려고, 때로는 명문가에서도 아주 기이한 일이 벌어진다고 말씀드렸던 겁니다. 이 모든 일이, 그리고 그 이상이, 나리의 가문에서, 나리의 마님께, 마님을 통해 벌어졌습니다. 제 믿음으로는 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 돌아가시는 그 순간까지 이 조사를 밀어붙이셨고, 바로 그날 밤 그분과 데드록 마님 사이에 그 일을 두고 험악한 기류까지 흘렀습니다. 자, 나리께서 데드록 마님께 그 점을 여쭤보십시오, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 그분이 이곳을 떠나신 뒤에도, 마님께서 깊은 술이 달린 헐렁한 검은 망토를 두르고 그분께 무언가 더 할 말이 있어 그분 방으로 내려가지 않으셨는지를 마님께 여쭤보십시오.\"\n\n레스터 경은 조각상처럼 앉아, 제 심장의 생피를 후벼 파는 그 잔인한 손가락을 응시한다.\n\n\"마님께 그 점을 여쭤보십시오, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 저, 형사반의 버킷 경위가 드리는 말씀이라고 말입니다. 그리고 마님께서 그 일을 인정하기를 곤란해하시거든, 소용없다고 전하십시오. 버킷 경위가 그 일을 알고 있으며, 마님께서 나리가 그 군인이라 부르신 자(지금은 군에 있지 않습니다만)와 스쳐 지나가셨다는 것도, 마님 자신이 계단에서 그자와 스쳐 지나갔음을 알고 계신다는 것도 안다고 말입니다. 자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 제가 왜 이 모든 걸 늘어놓겠습니까?\"\n\n레스터 경은 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 신음을 한 번 토하더니, 잠시 멈춰달라고 청한다. 이윽고 그는 두 손을 내리는데, 얼굴에 핏기라곤 그의 백발만큼도 남아 있지 않으면서도 위엄과 겉으로의 평정을 어찌나 잘 지키는지, 버킷 씨가 그에게 조금은 경외감을 느낄 정도다. 그의 태도에는 평소의 오만이라는 껍질 위에 무언가 얼어붙고 굳어버린 기운이 어려 있고, 버킷 씨는 곧 그의 말투에 평소답지 않은 더딤이 있음을, 이따금 말을 시작하는 데 묘하게 애를 먹어 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 내는 것을 알아챈다. 그는 지금 그런 소리로 침묵을 깨더니, 이내 마음을 다잡고 이렇게 말한다. 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨처럼 그토록 충직하고 열성적인 신사가 어찌하여 이토록 고통스럽고, 이토록 비통하고, 이토록 뜻밖이고, 이토록 압도적이고, 이토록 믿기 힘든 소식을 자신에게 한마디도 전하지 않았는지 도무지 이해가 가지 않는다고.\n\n\"그것도 말입니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"마님께 그 점을 분명히 밝혀달라고 여쭤보십시오. 옳다고 여기신다면, 형사반의 버킷 경위가 드리는 말이라며 마님께 여쭤보십시오. 제가 크게 잘못 짚은 게 아니라면, 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 때가 무르익었다 여기는 즉시 모든 걸 나리께 알릴 작정이셨고, 더 나아가 마님께도 그렇게 알아듣도록 일러두셨다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다. 아니, 어쩌면 제가 시신을 살피던 바로 그 아침에 그걸 밝히려던 참이었을지도 모릅니다! 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 지금 이 순간으로부터 오 분 뒤에 제가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 할지 나리는 모르십니다. 만약 제가 지금 당장 쓰러진다면, 나리는 제가 왜 진작 그 일을 해두지 않았을까 의아해하시겠지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n옳은 말이다. 레스터 경은 그 거슬리는 소리를 다소 힘겹게 억누르며 \"옳소\" 하고 말한다. 이때 현관에서 상당한 말소리가 들려온다. 버킷 씨는 귀를 기울이더니 서재 문으로 가서, 살며시 자물쇠를 풀어 문을 열고 다시 귀를 기울인다. 그러고는 머리를 안으로 들이고 황급하지만 침착하게 속삭인다. \"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이 불행한 집안일이 새어 나가고 말았습니다. 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 그토록 급작스레 쓰러지셨으니 그러리라 짐작은 했지요. 이걸 덮을 기회는, 지금 나리의 하인들과 옥신각신하는 저 사람들을 들이는 데 있습니다. 제가 저들을 꿰뚫어보는 동안 — 집안을 위해서 — 가만히 앉아 계시겠습니까? 그리고 제가 끄덕여달라고 청하는 듯하거든 그냥 고개만 한 번 끄덕여주시겠습니까?\"\n\n레스터 경은 또렷하지 않게 대답한다. \"형사 양반. 할 수 있는 한 잘, 할 수 있는 한 잘 해주시오!\" 그러자 버킷 씨는 고개를 한 번 끄덕이고 집게손가락을 슬기롭게 한 번 구부려 보이며 현관으로 슬쩍 내려가는데, 그곳의 말소리는 이내 잦아든다. 그가 돌아오기까지는 오래 걸리지 않는다. 시종과, 역시 머리에 분을 뿌리고 복숭아꽃빛 반바지를 입은 또 한 명의 신령 같은 자가 그보다 몇 걸음 앞서며, 둘이서 의자 하나를 맞들고 오는데 그 위에는 거동 못 하는 노인이 앉아 있다. 또 다른 남자 하나와 여자 둘이 뒤따른다. 버킷 씨는 상냥하고 느긋한 태도로 의자를 내려놓는 자리를 지시하더니, 두 시종을 물리고 다시 문을 잠근다. 레스터 경은 신성한 영역을 침범당하는 이 광경을 얼음장 같은 눈초리로 지켜본다.\n\n\"자, 어쩌면 여러분은 저를 아실 겁니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분,\" 버킷 씨가 은밀한 목소리로 말한다. \"저는 형사반의 버킷 경위입니다, 그렇고말고요. 그리고 이것이,\" 가슴 주머니에서 편리한 작은 지팡이 끝을 꺼내며 \"제 권한의 증표지요. 자, 여러분은 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리를 뵙고 싶어 하셨습니다. 좋습니다! 지금 나리를 뵙고 계시지요. 명심하십시오, 그런 영광을 누리는 사람이 아무나 있는 게 아닙니다. 노신사 양반, 당신 성함은 스몰위드지요. 그게 당신 성함입니다. 잘 알고 있어요.\"\n\n\"흥, 그래서 그게 어디 나쁜 소리 들은 적이라도 있나!\" 스몰위드 씨가 째지듯 큰 목소리로 외친다.\n\n\"혹시 사람들이 왜 돼지를 잡아 죽였는지 아십니까?\" 버킷 씨가 흔들림 없는 눈빛으로, 그러나 성을 내지는 않고 받아친다.\n\n\"몰라!\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 말입니다,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그놈이 하도 뻔뻔하게 굴어서 잡아 죽인 겁니다. 당신은 그런 처지에 빠지지 마세요. 당신답지 못한 짓이니까요. 당신, 귀먹은 사람하고 대화하는 버릇이 있는 건 아니지요?\"\n\n\"맞아,\" 스몰위드 씨가 으르렁거린다. \"내 마누라가 귀가 먹었어.\"\n\n\"그래서 그렇게 목청을 높이시는군요. 허나 부인은 여기 안 계시니, 한두 옥타브쯤 낮춰주시겠습니까? 그래주시면 저야 고맙기도 하거니와, 당신 체면도 더 서실 겁니다,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이 다른 신사분은 설교하는 분이신 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"이름은 채드밴드요,\" 스몰위드 씨가 끼어드는데, 이때부터는 한결 낮은 음으로 말한다.\n\n\"예전에 같은 이름을 가진 친구이자 동료 부사관이 하나 있었지요,\" 버킷 씨가 손을 내밀며 말한다. \"그래서 그 이름에 정이 갑니다. 채드밴드 부인이시겠지요?\"\n\n\"그리고 스내그스비 부인이오,\" 스몰위드 씨가 소개한다.\n\n\"남편이 법률 문방구상이고 제 친구지요,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그를 형제처럼 아낍니다! 자, 무슨 일이십니까?\"\n\n\"우리가 무슨 용건으로 왔느냐는 말이오?\" 스몰위드 씨가 이 갑작스러운 전환에 다소 기가 꺾여 묻는다.\n\n\"아! 무슨 뜻인지 아시잖습니까. 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리 앞에서 그게 다 무슨 일인지 들어봅시다. 자.\"\n\n스몰위드 씨는 채드밴드 씨를 손짓으로 부르더니, 잠시 속삭이며 그와 상의한다. 채드밴드 씨는 이마와 손바닥 땀구멍에서 상당량의 기름기를 짜내며 큰 소리로 \"그래요. 당신 먼저!\" 하고는 본디 자리로 물러난다.\n\n\"나는 토킹혼 씨의 의뢰인이자 친구였소,\" 이윽고 스몰위드 영감이 새된 소리로 말한다. \"그와 거래를 했지. 나는 그에게 쓸모가 있었고, 그도 내게 쓸모가 있었소. 죽고 없는 크룩은 내 처남이었소. 유황 까치 같은 — 어쨌든 우리 스몰위드 마누라 말이오 — 그 여편네의 친오빠였단 말이오. 내가 크룩의 재산을 물려받았소. 나는 그의 서류며 유품을 죄다 살펴봤지. 다 내 눈앞에서 파헤쳐 나왔소. 죽고 없는 어떤 하숙인의 편지 한 묶음이 있었는데, 레이디 제인 — 그 고양이 잠자리 말이오 — 그 잠자리 옆쪽 선반 뒤에 숨겨져 있었소. 그자는 별별 것을 다, 사방에 숨겨뒀소. 토킹혼 씨가 그걸 원해서 가져갔지만, 내가 먼저 훑어봤지. 나는 사업하는 사람이라, 그것들을 슬쩍 들여다봤소. 그 하숙인의 연인이 보낸 편지였는데, 여자는 '호노리아'라고 서명했더군. 이런, 호노리아라니 흔한 이름이 아니지 않소? 이 집에 호노리아라고 서명하는 마님은 안 계시겠지요? 아, 없겠지, 아무렴! 아, 없겠지, 아무렴! 게다가 같은 필체일 리도 없겠지? 아, 없겠지, 아무렴!\"\n\n이 대목에서 스몰위드 씨는 의기양양해하던 와중에 기침 발작에 사로잡혀, 말을 끊고 외친다. \"아이고 맙소사! 아, 주여! 온몸이 산산이 흔들리는구나!\"\n\n\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리와 관계되는 무언가로,\" 버킷 씨가 그가 회복하기를 기다렸다가 말한다. \"넘어갈 준비가 되시거든, 신사분이 여기 앉아 계시니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아직 거기까지 안 왔다는 거요, 버킷 씨?\" 스몰위드 영감이 외친다. \"신사분이 아직 관계가 없다는 거요? 하든 대위하고, 그자의 변함없이 애틋한 호노리아하고, 게다가 그 둘의 자식까지 있는데도? 자, 그렇다면 나는 그 편지들이 어디 있는지 알고 싶소. 그건 나하고 관계된 일이오, 레스터 데드록 나리하고는 관계가 없더라도 말이오. 나는 그것들이 어디 있는지 알아야겠소. 그렇게 조용히 사라지게 놔두지 않을 테요. 나는 그걸 내 친구이자 사무 변호사인 토킹혼 씨에게 넘겼지, 다른 누구한테 넘긴 게 아니란 말이오.\"\n\n\"아니, 그가 그 값을 치렀잖습니까, 그것도 후하게 말입니다,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"그건 상관없소. 나는 누가 그걸 가졌는지 알고 싶소. 그리고 우리가 뭘 원하는지 말해주지 — 여기 있는 우리 모두가 원하는 걸 말이오, 버킷 씨. 우리는 이 살인 사건을 더 공들여 더 샅샅이 파헤치기를 원하오. 우리는 그 이해관계와 동기가 어디 있었는지 알고 있고, 당신은 아직 충분히 하질 않았소. 그 떠돌이 용기병 조지가 거기 손을 댔다 한들, 그자는 한낱 공범일 뿐이고 누가 시킨 거요. 당신도 누구 못지않게 내 말뜻을 알 것이오.\"\n\n\"자, 내 한마디 하지요,\" 버킷 씨가 순식간에 태도를 바꾸어 그에게 바짝 다가서며, 집게손가락에 야릇한 흡인력을 실어 말한다. \"이 세상 그 어떤 인간이든 단 반 초라도 내 사건을 망치거나, 끼어들거나, 앞질러 가게 한다면 내가 빌어먹을 놈이 될 거요. 당신이 더 공들이고 더 샅샅이 파헤치기를 원한다고? 당신이? 이 손이 보이시오? 그리고 내가 언제 이 손을 뻗어 그 총을 쏜 자의 팔에 얹어야 할지를 모를 것 같소?\"\n\n이 사내의 무시무시한 위세와, 그것이 결코 허튼소리가 아님이 어찌나 끔찍하리만치 분명한지, 스몰위드 씨는 사과를 늘어놓기 시작한다. 버킷 씨는 별안간 치솟은 노여움을 거두며 그를 제지한다.\n\n\"내가 충고하건대, 그 살인 사건은 당신 머릿속에서 지우시오. 그건 내 일이니까. 당신은 신문이나 곁눈으로 봐두시오. 눈을 부릅뜨고 보면 머잖아 그 일에 관한 무언가를 읽게 되더라도 놀랍지 않을 거요. 나는 내 일을 알고 있고, 그 문제에 관해 당신한테 할 말은 그게 다요. 자, 이제 그 편지들 말입니다. 당신은 누가 그걸 가졌는지 알고 싶다고 했지요. 말해주는 거야 상관없소. 내가 가졌소. 이게 그 묶음이오?\"\n\n스몰위드 씨는 버킷 씨가 외투의 알 수 없는 곳에서 꺼내 든 작은 꾸러미를 게걸스러운 눈으로 바라보며, 바로 그것이라고 알아본다.\n\n\"그다음엔 무슨 할 말이 있으십니까?\" 버킷 씨가 묻는다. \"자, 입을 너무 크게 벌리지 마세요. 그러면 보기 흉하니까.\"\n\n\"오백 파운드를 원하오.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럴 리가. 오십 파운드란 말이겠지요,\" 버킷 씨가 익살스레 말한다.\n\n그러나 알고 보니 스몰위드 씨는 정말로 오백을 뜻하는 모양이다.\n\n\"그러니까, 나는 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께 위임받아 (아무것도 인정하거나 약속하는 게 아니라) 이 사소한 거래를 고려해보라는 거지요,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다 — 레스터 경은 기계적으로 고개를 숙인다 — \"그런데 당신은 오백 파운드짜리 제안을 고려해달라는 거고. 아니, 그건 터무니없는 제안이오! 이백오십도 충분히 고약하지만, 그래도 그건 낫지요. 이백오십이라고 하시는 게 낫지 않겠소?\"\n\n스몰위드 씨는 그러지 않는 편이 낫다고 아주 단호하다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"채드밴드 씨 말을 들어봅시다. 이런! 같은 이름을 가진 옛 동료 부사관 얘길 어찌나 자주 들었던지. 내가 만난 사람 중에 매사에 더없이 온건한 사람이었지요!\"\n\n이렇게 청을 받자 채드밴드 씨가 앞으로 나서더니, 매끄럽게 잠깐 미소 짓고 손바닥으로 잠깐 기름을 비벼대고는 다음과 같이 입을 연다. \"벗들이여, 우리는 지금 — 내 아내 레이철과 나는 — 부유하고 위대한 자들의 저택에 와 있습니다. 우리는 어찌하여 지금 부유하고 위대한 자들의 저택에 와 있을까요, 벗들이여? 우리가 초대를 받아서일까요? 그들과 함께 잔치를 즐기라 청함을 받았기에, 그들과 함께 기뻐하라 청함을 받았기에, 그들과 함께 류트를 타라 청함을 받았기에, 그들과 함께 춤추라 청함을 받았기에? 아니지요. 그렇다면 우리는 어찌하여 여기 있을까요, 벗들이여? 우리가 어떤 죄 많은 비밀을 쥐고 있어, 그것을 지키는 대가로 곡식과, 포도주와, 기름, 혹은 그와 거의 매한가지인 돈을 요구하기 때문일까요? 아마 그럴 테지요, 벗들이여.\"\n\n\"당신도 사업하는 사람이로군요,\" 버킷 씨가 아주 주의 깊게 대답한다. \"그러니 이제 그 비밀의 성격이 무엇인지 말씀하시려는 거고요. 옳습니다. 그보다 나은 수가 없지요.\"\n\n\"그러면 우리 형제여, 사랑의 정신으로,\" 채드밴드 씨가 교활한 눈빛으로 말한다. \"그리로 나아갑시다. 레이철, 내 아내여, 앞으로!\"\n\n채드밴드 부인은 진작부터 채비가 되어 있던 터라, 어찌나 앞으로 나서는지 제 남편을 뒤로 밀쳐내고는 딱딱하고 찌푸린 미소로 버킷 씨와 마주 선다.\n\n\"우리가 아는 걸 알고 싶다니,\" 그녀가 말한다. \"내 말해주지요. 나는 하든 양을, 그 마님의 따님을 키우는 걸 거들었소. 나는 그 마님의 언니 밑에서 일했는데, 그분은 마님이 자기에게 끼친 치욕에 몹시 예민하셔서, 마님께까지도 그 아이가 죽었다고 둘러댔지요 — 사실 거의 죽다시피 했고요 — 태어났을 때 말이오. 허나 그 아이는 살아 있고, 나는 그 아이를 알고 있소.\" 이 말과 함께, 한바탕 웃음과 함께, '마님'이라는 말에 신랄한 힘을 주며, 채드밴드 부인은 팔짱을 끼고 버킷 씨를 무자비하게 노려본다.\n\n\"그러니까 짐작건대,\" 그 형사가 대답한다. \"당신은 이십 파운드짜리 지폐, 아니면 그쯤 되는 선물을 기대하시는 거겠지요?\"\n\n채드밴드 부인은 그저 웃기만 하면서, 경멸하듯 그에게 이십 펜스나 '내놓아' 보라고 한다.\n\n\"저기 계신 내 친구 법률 문방구상의 마나님은,\" 버킷 씨가 손가락으로 스내그스비 부인을 앞으로 꾀어내며 말한다. \"당신의 속셈은 무엇입니까, 부인?\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 처음엔 눈물과 한탄에 막혀 제 속셈의 성격을 밝히지 못하지만, 차츰 어수선하게나마 진상이 드러나니, 그녀는 온갖 상처와 부당함에 짓눌린 여인으로, 스내그스비 씨가 늘 자기를 속이고, 저버리고, 어둠 속에 가둬두려 해왔으며, 그 고난 속에서 그녀의 가장 큰 위안은 고인이 된 토킹혼 씨의 동정이었으니, 그 위증쟁이 남편이 자리를 비운 사이 그분이 쿡스 코트에 들렀던 어느 날 그녀에게 어찌나 큰 연민을 보여주었던지, 그녀는 근래 들어 늘 자기의 온갖 시름을 그분께 가져가곤 했다는 것이다. 보아하니 모든 사람이 — 지금 이 자리에 있는 이들만 빼고 — 스내그스비 부인의 평화를 음모로 깨뜨려왔다. 켄지 앤 카보이의 사무원 거피 씨가 있는데, 처음엔 한낮의 태양처럼 활짝 열린 사람이었으나, 의심할 바 없이 스내그스비 씨가 매수하고 농간을 부린 탓에 별안간 한밤중처럼 꽉 닫혀버렸다. 거피 씨의 친구인 위블 씨가 있는데, 비슷하게 앞뒤가 들어맞는 이유로 어느 골목 위층에 수상쩍게 살고 있었다. 죽고 없는 크룩이 있었고, 죽고 없는 님로드가 있었고, 죽고 없는 조가 있었으니, 그들은 모두 \"한패였다.\" 무슨 한패였는지 스내그스비 부인은 콕 집어 말하지 못하지만, 조가 스내그스비 씨의 아들이라는 것은 \"나팔이 울려 알려준 듯이\" 확실히 알고 있고, 스내그스비 씨가 그 아이를 마지막으로 찾아갔을 때 그 뒤를 밟았으며, 그 아이가 그의 아들이 아니라면 어찌하여 그가 갔겠느냐는 것이다. 한동안 그녀의 삶에서 단 하나의 일거리는 스내그스비 씨를 이리저리, 위아래로 따라다니며 수상쩍은 정황들을 끼워 맞추는 것이었고 — 일어난 정황은 죄다 더없이 수상쩍었으며 — 이런 식으로 그녀는 거짓된 남편을 적발하고 망신 주려는 목표를 밤낮으로 쫓아왔다. 그리하여 채드밴드 부부와 토킹혼 씨를 한자리에 모으게 되었고, 거피 씨의 변화에 대해 토킹혼 씨와 의논했으며, 지금 이 자리에 있는 이들이 관심을 둔 정황들을 길가에서 우연히, 그러나 늘 변함없이 스내그스비 씨의 전면 폭로와 부부의 별거로 끝맺을 그 큰길 위에서 들춰내는 일을 거들었다. 이 모든 것을 스내그스비 부인은, 상처받은 여인으로서, 채드밴드 부인의 벗으로서, 채드밴드 씨의 추종자로서, 고인이 된 토킹혼 씨를 애도하는 자로서, 가능한 모든 그리고 불가능한 온갖 혼란과 뒤얽힘을 곁들여, 어떠한 금전적 동기도 없이, 앞서 말한 것 말고는 아무런 책략이나 계획도 없이, 끊임없이 돌아가는 제 질투의 방앗간에서 피어오르는 제 자신의 자욱한 먼지 안개를 여기 끌고 들어와 사방에 퍼뜨리며, 은밀한 봉인 아래 증언하러 이곳에 와 있는 것이다.\n\n이 장황한 서설이 이어지는 동안 — 그것은 제법 시간이 걸린다 — 스내그스비 부인의 식초 같은 속내를 한눈에 꿰뚫어본 버킷 씨는 제 친숙한 악마와 회의를 하며, 채드밴드 부부와 스몰위드 씨에게 빈틈없는 주의를 기울인다. 레스터 데드록 경은 한두 번 버킷 씨 쪽을 바라보는 것 말고는, 온 인류 가운데 오직 그 형사만을 의지하기라도 하듯, 여전히 얼음장 같은 표면을 두른 채 꼼짝 않고 있다.\n\n\"아주 좋습니다,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이제 당신들 속내를 알겠군요. 그리고 나는 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께 위임받아 이 사소한 문제를 들여다보는 자이니,\" 레스터 경은 다시 기계적으로 고개를 숙여 그 진술을 확인해준다. \"공정하고 충분한 주의를 기울일 수 있지요. 자, 돈을 뜯어내려는 공모니 뭐니 하는 따위는 들먹이지 않겠습니다. 여기 있는 우리는 세상 물정에 밝은 남녀이고, 우리 목적은 일을 원만하게 만드는 거니까요. 허나 정말 의아한 게 하나 있는데, 당신들이 아래 현관에서 소란을 피울 생각을 했다는 게 놀랍단 말입니다. 그건 당신들 이익에 정면으로 어긋나는 짓이었어요. 내가 보는 건 바로 그겁니다.\"\n\n\"우리는 안으로 들어오고 싶었던 거요,\" 스몰위드 씨가 변명한다.\n\n\"아니, 물론 안으로 들어오고 싶으셨겠지요,\" 버킷 씨가 쾌활하게 단언한다. \"허나 당신만 한 연세의 노신사가 — 진정 우러러볼 만한 분이라고 했잖습니까, 명심하세요! — 사지를 못 쓰게 된 탓에 — 그래서 모든 활력이 머리로 솟구쳐 의심할 바 없이 머리가 날카로워지셨을 텐데 — 지금 같은 거래는 가능한 한 비밀로 부치지 않으면 한 푼의 값어치도 없으리라는 걸 헤아리지 못하다니, 참으로 기묘하단 말입니다! 보세요, 당신은 성질이 당신을 이겨버린 겁니다. 바로 거기서 발판을 잃은 거예요,\" 버킷 씨가 따지는 듯하면서도 다정한 투로 말한다.\n\n\"나는 그저 하인 한 명이 레스터 데드록 나리께 올라가지 않으면 돌아가지 않겠다고 했을 뿐이오,\" 스몰위드 씨가 대꾸한다.\n\n\"바로 그겁니다! 거기서 성질이 당신을 이겨버린 거지요. 자, 다음번엔 그걸 잘 다스리시오. 그럼 그걸로 돈을 버실 테니까. 사람들을 불러 당신을 내려가게 할까요?\"\n\n\"이 일에 관해선 언제 더 듣게 되는 거죠?\" 채드밴드 부인이 매섭게 따져 묻는다.\n\n\"과연 진정한 여인이시군요, 복 받으세요! 늘 호기심에 차 있는 게 당신들 사랑스러운 성이지요!\" 버킷 씨가 정중하게 대답한다. \"내일이나 모레쯤 당신을 찾아뵙는 기쁨을 누리겠습니다 — 스몰위드 씨와 그의 이백오십 파운드 제안도 잊지 않고요.\"\n\n\"오백이오!\" 스몰위드 씨가 외친다.\n\n\"좋습니다! 명목상 오백이라 합시다.\" 버킷 씨는 종 줄에 손을 얹고 있다. \"그럼 저 자신과 이 댁 신사분을 대신하여, 지금으로선 작별 인사를 드릴까요?\" 그가 슬쩍 구슬리는 투로 묻는다.\n\n아무도 그러지 말라고 우길 만한 배짱이 없으니, 그는 그렇게 하고, 일행은 올라왔던 대로 물러난다. 버킷 씨는 그들을 문까지 따라갔다가 돌아와, 진지한 업무를 보는 태도로 말한다. \"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이걸 사들일지 말지는 나리께서 판단하실 일입니다. 저로서야 대체로 사들이는 편을 권하고 싶고요. 또 제법 싸게 살 수 있을 듯합니다. 보십시오, 저 스내그스비 부인이라는 절인 오이 같은 여편네는 온갖 속셈에 두루 이용당해서, 일부러 그러려 했던 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 해를 끼쳤습니다, 자질구레한 것들을 한데 끌어모으면서 말이지요. 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨는 이 모든 말을 손아귀에 쥐고 제 뜻대로 몰 수 있었을 겁니다, 그건 의심할 바 없지요. 허나 그분이 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 마부석에서 떨어지고 나니, 이제 말들이 줄을 넘어 다리를 걸치고는 저마다 제멋대로 끌고 당기고 있는 겁니다. 그게 그렇고, 사는 게 그렇지요. 고양이가 없으니 쥐들이 노닐고, 서리가 풀리니 물이 흐르는 법입니다. 자, 이제 잡아들일 자에 관해서 말입니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 눈을 줄곧 부릅뜨고 있었으면서도 마치 잠에서 깨어나는 듯하더니, 버킷 씨가 회중시계를 들여다보는 동안 그를 뚫어지게 바라본다.\n\n\"잡아들일 자는 지금 이 집 안에 있습니다,\" 버킷 씨가 흔들림 없는 손으로 시계를 집어넣고 점점 기운이 오른 채 말을 잇는다. \"그리고 저는 나리께서 보시는 앞에서 그 여자를 구금하려 합니다. 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 한마디도 하지 마시고 까딱도 하지 마십시오. 아무런 소동도 소란도 없을 겁니다. 괜찮으시다면 저녁나절에 다시 와서, 이 불행한 집안일과 그것을 가장 말끔하게 덮을 방도에 관한 나리의 뜻을 받들도록 애써보겠습니다. 자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 지금 곧 있을 체포 때문에 마음 졸이지 마십시오. 처음부터 끝까지 이 사건 전모를 환히 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n버킷 씨는 종을 울리고, 문으로 가서, 시종에게 짤막하게 속삭이고는, 문을 닫고, 팔짱을 낀 채 그 뒤에 선다. 한두 분쯤 마음 졸이는 시간이 흐른 뒤 문이 천천히 열리고 한 프랑스 여자가 들어온다. 오르탕스 양이다.\n\n그녀가 방에 들어선 순간 버킷 씨는 문을 탁 닫고 등을 기대 막아선다. 그 소리의 느닷없음에 그녀는 돌아서고, 그제야 비로소 안락의자에 앉은 레스터 데드록 경을 본다.\n\n\"용서하세요,\" 그녀가 황급히 중얼거린다. \"여기 아무도 없다고들 하기에.\"\n\n문 쪽으로 내딛는 걸음이 그녀를 버킷 씨와 정면으로 마주 세운다. 별안간 경련이 그녀의 얼굴을 가로지르더니 그녀는 새파랗게 질린다.\n\n\"이 여자는 제 하숙인입니다, 레스터 데드록 경,\" 버킷 씨가 그녀를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다. \"이 외국인 처자가 몇 주 전부터 제 하숙인이지요.\"\n\n\"레스터 경이 그딴 걸 무슨 상관 하겠어, 안 그래, 내 천사님?\" 오르탕스 양이 농지거리하듯 대꾸한다.\n\n\"글쎄다, 내 천사님,\" 버킷 씨가 대꾸한다. \"두고 봐야지.\"\n\n오르탕스 양은 팽팽하게 굳은 얼굴에 잔뜩 인상을 쓰며 그를 노려보다가, 그 표정이 차츰 경멸의 미소로 바뀐다. \"당신 참 알 수 없는 사람이네. 술 취했어?\"\n\n\"멀쩡하다네, 내 천사님,\" 버킷 씨가 대꾸한다.\n\n\"나는 당신 마누라하고 이 지긋지긋한 집에 막 도착했어. 당신 마누라가 몇 분 전에 날 두고 가버렸어. 아래층에서 당신 마누라가 여기 있다고 하더군. 그래서 올라왔는데, 당신 마누라는 여기 없잖아. 대체 이 바보 같은 장난이 무슨 꿍꿍이야, 어디 말해봐?\" 오르탕스 양이 팔짱을 차분히 낀 채, 그러나 그 거무스름한 뺨 어딘가가 시계처럼 똑딱이는 채로 따져 묻는다.\n\n버킷 씨는 그저 그녀에게 손가락을 흔들어 보일 뿐이다.\n\n\"아, 맙소사, 당신 참 한심한 멍청이로군!\" 오르탕스 양이 고개를 홱 젖히며 웃음과 함께 외친다. \"비키라고, 이 큰 돼지야, 아래층으로 내려가게.\" 발을 쿵 구르고 으름장을 놓으며.\n\n\"자, 아가씨,\" 버킷 씨가 차분하고 단호하게 말한다. \"가서 저 소파에 앉으시오.\"\n\n\"나는 아무 데도 안 앉아,\" 그녀가 고개를 마구 끄덕이며 대꾸한다.\n\n\"자, 아가씨,\" 버킷 씨가 손가락 말고는 아무런 동작도 하지 않은 채 되풀이한다. \"저 소파에 앉으시오.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"살인 혐의로 당신을 구금할 거니까. 굳이 일러줄 것도 없겠지. 자, 나는 당신 같은 여자에게, 그것도 외국인에게 할 수 있는 한 예의를 차리고 싶소. 그게 안 되면 거칠게 굴 수밖에 없고, 밖에는 더 거친 자들이 있소. 내가 어떻게 나갈지는 당신한테 달렸소. 그러니 친구로서 충고하건대, 당신 머리 위로 복된 반 순간이 더 지나가기 전에, 가서 저 소파에 앉으시오.\"\n\n오르탕스 양은 그 뺨 어딘가가 빠르고 세게 똑딱이는 채로, 응축된 목소리로 \"당신은 악마야\" 하고 말하며 따른다.\n\n\"자, 보십시오,\" 버킷 씨가 흡족하게 말을 잇는다. \"이제 편안히, 당신 같은 분별 있는 외국인 처자에게 기대할 만한 대로 처신하시는군. 그래서 충고를 하나 해주겠는데, 다름 아니라, 말을 너무 많이 하지 마시오. 여기서 당신이 무슨 말을 하기를 바라는 사람도 없고, 입을 다물고 있을수록 좋소. 한마디로, 떠벌릴수록 손해라는 거요, 알겠소?\" 버킷 씨는 이 프랑스어 설명을 무척이나 흡족해한다.\n\n오르탕스 양은 입을 호랑이처럼 잔뜩 벌리고 검은 눈에서 그를 향해 불을 뿜으며, 두 손을 — 또 짐작건대 두 발까지도 — 꽉 움켜쥔 채 소파에 뻣뻣하게 곧추앉아 중얼거린다. \"오, 이 버킷, 당신은 악마야!\"\n\n\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 말하는데, 이때부터 그 손가락은 한시도 쉬지 않는다. \"제 하숙인인 이 처자는, 제가 말씀드린 그 무렵 마님의 하녀였습니다. 그리고 이 처자는 해고당한 뒤로 마님에게 유난히 격렬하고 사납게 굴었을 뿐 아니라—\"\n\n\"거짓말!\" 오르탕스 양이 외친다. \"내가 내 발로 그만뒀어.\"\n\n\"자, 왜 내 충고를 안 듣는 거요?\" 버킷 씨가 인상 깊게, 거의 사정하다시피 한 어조로 대꾸한다. \"당신이 저지르는 경솔함에 놀랄 따름이오. 당신은 자기한테 불리하게 쓰일 말을 하고 말 거요, 알겠소. 틀림없이 그렇게 될 거요. 내 말이 증거로 제출되기 전까지는 신경 쓰지 마시오. 그건 당신더러 들으라는 말이 아니니까.\"\n\n\"게다가 해고라니,\" 오르탕스 양이 사납게 외친다. \"마님한테 해고당했다고! 흥, 맙소사, 거룩하기도 한 마님이지! 그렇게 파렴치한 마님 곁에 붙어 있다가 내 신상만 망치고 말 텐데!\"\n\n\"맹세코 당신한테 놀랐소!\" 버킷 씨가 항변한다. \"나는 프랑스인이 정중한 민족인 줄 알았는데 말이오, 정말로요. 헌데 여자가 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리 앞에서 저런 식으로 떠들어대는 꼴을 다 듣다니!\"\n\n\"이 양반은 가엾게 능욕당한 사람이야!\" 오르탕스 양이 외친다. \"나는 이 양반 집에, 이 양반 이름에, 이 양반 멍청함에 침을 뱉어,\" 이 모든 것을 그녀는 양탄자로 대신 표현한다. \"오, 대단한 양반이라지! 오, 그래, 거룩하기도 하셔라! 오, 하느님! 흥!\"\n\n\"자, 레스터 데드록 경,\" 버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"이 무절제한 외국 여자는 또, 제가 말씀드린 그 사건 때 토킹혼 씨의 방에서 시중을 든 일로 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨에게 무슨 권리를 세워놓았다고 성을 내며 멋대로 생각했습니다, 제 시간과 수고에 대해 후하게 값을 치렀는데도 말이지요.\"\n\n\"거짓말!\" 오르탕스 양이 외친다. \"나는 그자 돈 몽땅 거절했어.\"\n\n\"당신이 굳이 떠벌리겠다면 말이오,\" 버킷 씨가 곁들이듯 말한다. \"그 결과를 감수해야 할 거요. 자, 이 여자가 그때 이미 이 짓을 저지르고 저를 속일 작정으로 제 하숙인이 되었는지 어떤지는 제가 의견을 내지 않겠습니다. 허나 이 여자는 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨의 방을 다투려는 속셈으로 어슬렁거리던 무렵, 그리고 어떤 가엾은 문방구상을 들볶고 반쯤 혼을 빼놓던 무렵에 그런 신분으로 제 집에 살았습니다.\"\n\n\"거짓말!\" 오르탕스 양이 외친다. \"다 거짓말이야!\"\n\n\"살인이 저질러졌습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 어떤 정황에서였는지는 나리도 아십니다. 자, 청컨대 일이 분만 제 말을 바짝 따라와 주십시오. 저는 부름을 받았고, 이 사건이 제게 맡겨졌습니다. 저는 현장과, 시신과, 서류와, 모든 것을 살펴봤습니다. (같은 집에 사는 어느 사무원에게서) 제가 입수한 정보에 따라, 저는 조지가 그날 밤, 그것도 살인이 벌어진 바로 그 시각 무렵에 그 근처를 어슬렁대는 게 목격되었고, 또 이전에도 고인과 험한 말을 주고받는 게 — 증인의 진술로는 그를 위협하기까지 한 게 — 엿들렸다는 이유로 그를 구금했습니다. 레스터 데드록 나리, 제가 처음부터 조지를 살인자로 믿었느냐고 물으신다면, 솔직히 아니라고 말씀드리겠습니다. 허나 그럼에도 그자가 범인일 수는 있었고, 그를 잡아 구류시킬 만큼 그에게 불리한 정황이 충분했기에 그러는 게 제 의무였습니다. 자, 잘 보십시오!\"\n\n버킷 씨가 — 그로서는 — 다소 흥분하여 몸을 앞으로 기울이며, 하려는 말의 서두를 공중에 집게손가락을 유령처럼 한 번 까딱이는 것으로 여는 동안, 오르탕스 양은 검은 눈으로 그를 어둡게 노려보며 메마른 입술을 굳게 꾹 다문다.\n\n\"저는 밤에 집으로 갔습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 그런데 이 처자가 제 아내 버킷 부인과 저녁을 먹고 있더군요. 이 여자는 처음 제 하숙인으로 들어오겠다고 했을 때부터 버킷 부인을 끔찍이 좋아하는 척 요란을 떨었습니다만, 그날 밤엔 그 어느 때보다 더했지요 — 실은 도가 지나쳤습니다. 마찬가지로 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨의 애도할 만한 추억에 대한 존경이며 뭐며도 도가 지나쳤고요. 살아 계신 주님께 맹세코, 제가 식탁에서 그 여자 맞은편에 앉아 손에 칼을 든 그 모습을 보았을 때, 이 여자가 그 짓을 했구나 하는 생각이 번뜩 떠올랐습니다!\"\n\n오르탕스 양이 이를 악물고 입술 사이로 짜내는 말소리는 거의 들리지 않는다. \"당신은 악마야.\"\n\n\"자, 살인이 있던 그날 밤,\" 버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"이 여자는 어디 있었을까요? 극장에 갔다고 했습니다. (실은 정말로 거기 있었더군요, 그 짓 전에도 후에도요, 나중에 알아냈습니다.) 저는 제가 상대해야 할 자가 약삭빠른 손님이고 증거를 잡기가 무척 어려우리란 걸 알았습니다. 그래서 저는 그 여자에게 덫을 놓았지요 — 여태껏 한 번도 놓아본 적 없는 그런 덫을, 여태껏 한 번도 해본 적 없는 그런 모험을 말입니다. 저녁을 먹으며 그 여자와 이야기하는 동안 머릿속으로 그걸 짜냈습니다. 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 저희 집이 작은 데다 이 처자의 귀가 밝은지라, 저는 버킷 부인이 놀라서 한마디도 못 내뱉도록 입에 침대보를 틀어막고는 모든 걸 털어놓았지요. 이봐, 또 그딴 생각일랑 마. 안 그러면 당신 두 발목을 한데 묶어버릴 테니.\" 말을 끊은 버킷 씨는 소리 없이 오르탕스 양에게 내려가, 묵직한 손을 그녀의 어깨에 얹은 참이다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐가 문제야?\" 그녀가 그에게 묻는다.\n\n\"창밖으로 몸을 던질 생각일랑,\" 버킷 씨가 타이르듯 손가락을 세우며 대꾸한다. \"더는 마시오. 내가 문제 삼는 건 바로 그거니까. 자! 그냥 내 팔을 잡으시오. 일어설 것 없소. 내가 당신 곁에 앉을 테니. 자, 내 팔을 잡으시겠소? 나는 결혼한 몸이오, 알다시피. 당신은 내 아내도 알지. 그냥 내 팔을 잡으시오.\"\n\n그 메마른 입술을 적셔보려 부질없이 애쓰며, 고통스러운 소리와 함께 그녀는 안간힘으로 자신과 씨름하다 따른다.\n\n\"자, 이제 다시 멀쩡해졌군요. 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이 사건은 버킷 부인이 아니었다면 결코 지금 같은 사건이 되지 못했을 겁니다. 그 사람은 오만 명 가운데 하나 — 아니, 십오만 명 가운데 하나뿐인 여인이지요! 이 처자가 방심하도록, 저는 그 뒤로 한 번도 저희 집에 발을 들이지 않았습니다. 다만 빵집 빵과 우유로 필요할 때마다 버킷 부인과 연락을 주고받았지요. 침대보를 입에 문 아내에게 제가 속삭인 말은 이러했습니다. '여보, 조지에 대한 내 의심이니, 이거니, 저거니, 또 다른 거니 하는 그럴듯한 이야기로 저 여자를 줄곧 방심시킬 수 있겠소? 쉬지 않고 저 여자를 밤낮으로 감시할 수 있겠소? 「저 여자는 내가 모르는 사이에는 아무것도 못 하리라, 저 여자는 자기도 모르게 내 포로가 되리라, 죽음에서 못 벗어나듯 내게서 못 벗어나리라, 저 여자가 이 살인을 저질렀다면 내가 저 여자를 붙잡을 때까지 저 여자의 목숨은 내 목숨이요 저 여자의 영혼은 내 영혼이리라」 하고 단언할 수 있겠소?' 버킷 부인은 침대보 때문에 겨우 입을 떼며 제게 말하더군요. '버킷, 할 수 있어요!' 그리고 그걸 훌륭하게 해냈지요!\"\n\n\"거짓말!\" 오르탕스 양이 끼어든다. \"다 거짓말이야, 이 친구야!\"\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이런 정황에서 제 계산은 어찌 들어맞았을까요? 이 충동적인 처자가 새로운 방향으로 도를 넘으리라 제가 계산했을 때, 제가 틀렸을까요, 옳았을까요? 옳았습니다. 이 여자가 무슨 짓을 하려 들까요? 충격받지 마십시오. 그 살인을 마님께 뒤집어씌우려 했습니다.\"\n\n레스터 경은 의자에서 몸을 일으키다 다시 비틀거리며 주저앉는다.\n\n\"게다가 이 여자는 제가 늘 여기 있다는 말을 듣고 더욱 부추겨졌는데, 그건 일부러 그리한 겁니다. 자, 제 수첩을 펼쳐보십시오, 레스터 데드록 나리. 외람되이 나리께 던져드려도 된다면 말입니다. 그리고 제게 온 편지들을 보십시오, 하나같이 '데드록 마님'이라는 두 단어가 들어 있지요. 바로 오늘 아침 제가 가로챈, 나리 앞으로 온 편지를 펼쳐, 거기 적힌 '데드록 마님, 살인자'라는 세 단어를 읽어보십시오. 이 편지들이 무당벌레 떼처럼 우수수 쏟아졌습니다. 버킷 부인이 제 감시 자리에서 이 처자가 그것들을 죄다 쓰는 걸 보았다면, 이제 뭐라 하시겠습니까? 버킷 부인이 이 반 시간 안에 똑같은 잉크와 종이, 짝이 맞는 반장짜리 종이며 뭐며를 확보했다면, 뭐라 하시겠습니까? 버킷 부인이 이 처자가 그 하나하나를 우편으로 부치는 걸 지켜봤다면 뭐라 하시겠습니까, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리?\" 버킷 씨가 제 아내의 천재성에 대한 감탄으로 의기양양하여 묻는다.\n\n버킷 씨가 결론으로 나아가는 동안 유독 눈에 띄는 것이 두 가지 있다. 첫째, 그가 어느새 오르탕스 양에 대해 무시무시한 소유권을 확립해가는 듯하다는 것. 둘째, 그녀가 들이쉬는 공기 자체가 마치 촘촘한 그물이나 관 덮개가 그 숨 막혀 하는 몸뚱이를 점점 더 바짝 죄어 들어오기라도 하듯, 그녀를 둘러싸고 좁혀들고 오그라드는 듯하다는 것.\n\n\"그 결정적인 시각에 마님이 그 현장에 계셨다는 건 의심할 여지가 없습니다,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그리고 제 짐작으론 여기 이 외국인 친구가 계단 위쪽에서 마님을 봤습니다. 마님과 조지와 이 외국인 친구는 모두 서로 바짝 뒤꿈치를 밟다시피 했지요. 허나 그건 더는 중요하지 않으니 깊이 들어가지 않겠습니다. 저는 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨를 쏜 권총의 메움 솜뭉치를 찾아냈습니다. 그건 나리의 체스니 월드 저택을 인쇄로 설명한 안내문 한 조각이었지요. 그게 뭐 대수냐 하시겠지요, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 그렇습니다. 허나 여기 이 외국인 친구가 그 종잇장의 나머지를 찢어버려도 안전한 때라고 여길 만큼 완전히 방심했을 때, 그리고 버킷 부인이 그 조각들을 맞춰보고 솜뭉치만큼이 빠졌다는 걸 알아냈을 때, 일이 꽤나 수상쩍게 돌아가기 시작하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이거 참 긴 거짓말이로군,\" 오르탕스 양이 끼어든다. \"당신 말 참 길게도 늘어놓네. 거의 다 끝난 거야, 아니면 계속 떠들 거야?\"\n\n\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\" 버킷 씨가 말을 잇는데, 그는 온전한 칭호를 즐기는지라 그 한 조각이라도 생략할 때면 스스로를 억지로 다잡는다. \"이제 말씀드릴 이 사건의 마지막 대목은, 우리 일에는 인내가 필요하며 결코 무슨 일이든 서둘러 하지 말아야 한다는 걸 보여줍니다. 저는 어제 이 처자가 모르게 그녀를 지켜봤는데, 그때 그녀는 제 아내와 함께 장례식을 구경하고 있었지요. 제 아내가 그녀를 거기 데려가려고 꾸민 일이었습니다. 저는 그녀를 옭아맬 거리가 어찌나 많았던지, 그녀 얼굴에 어린 그런 표정을 보았던지, 마님에 대한 그녀의 악의에 제 마음이 어찌나 곤두섰던지, 그리고 때가 그야말로 그녀에게 이른바 응보를 내릴 만한 그런 때였던지라, 제가 경험 적은 풋내기였다면 틀림없이 그녀를 잡아들였을 겁니다. 마찬가지로, 어젯밤 마님이 — 분명 만인의 흠모를 받는 분이지요 — 집에 돌아오시는데 그 모습이 — 아니, 이런, 거의 바다에서 솟아오르는 비너스 같다고 해도 좋을 만큼이라 — 죄 없는 분이 살인 누명을 쓴다고 생각하니 어찌나 불쾌하고 앞뒤가 안 맞던지, 저는 이 일을 당장 끝내버리고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았습니다. 그랬다면 제가 무엇을 잃었을까요? 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 저는 흉기를 잃었을 겁니다. 여기 제 죄수는 장례 행렬이 떠난 뒤 버킷 부인에게, 함께 합승마차로 시골에 좀 나가 아주 점잖은 요릿집에서 차를 마시자고 청했습니다. 그런데 그 요릿집 근처에 물웅덩이가 하나 있지요. 차를 마시다가 제 죄수는 보닛을 벗어둔 침실로 손수건을 가지러 일어났습니다. 그녀는 제법 오래 자리를 비웠다가 다소 숨이 가빠진 채 돌아왔지요. 집에 돌아오자마자 버킷 부인이 자기가 관찰한 바와 의심을 곁들여 이걸 제게 보고했습니다. 저는 우리 사람 둘을 입회시키고 달빛 아래 그 물웅덩이를 훑게 했는데, 그게 거기 잠긴 지 여섯 시간도 안 되어 그 호신용 권총이 건져 올려졌습니다. 자, 이봐, 당신 팔을 내 팔에 좀 더 깊이 끼고, 단단히 붙들고 있으시오. 그럼 다치게 하지 않을 테니!\"\n\n순식간에 버킷 씨는 그녀의 손목에 수갑을 철컥 채운다. \"이게 하나,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이제 나머지 하나요, 아가씨. 둘, 이로써 끝!\"\n\n그가 일어선다. 그녀도 함께 일어선다. \"당신의,\" 그녀가 그에게 묻는데, 큰 두 눈이 어찌나 어두워지는지 처진 눈꺼풀이 그 눈을 거의 가릴 지경이면서도 — 그러면서도 그 눈은 노려본다. \"당신의 거짓되고, 배신을 일삼는, 저주받을 마누라는 어디 있지?\"\n\n\"먼저 경찰서로 갔소,\" 버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"거기서 그 사람을 보게 될 거요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"그 여자한테 입을 맞춰주고 싶어!\" 오르탕스 양이 호랑이처럼 헐떡이며 외친다.\n\n\"물어뜯기야 하겠지, 내 짐작엔,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"그래!\" 두 눈을 한껏 크게 뜨며. \"사지를 갈가리 찢어놓고 싶어 죽겠다고.\"\n\n\"복 받으시오, 아가씨,\" 버킷 씨가 더없이 침착하게 말한다. \"그런 말쯤은 들을 각오가 되어 있소. 당신네 성은 일단 사이가 틀어지면 서로에게 놀랄 만큼 적개심을 품으니 말이오. 나한테는 그 절반만큼도 신경 안 쓰지, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"안 써. 그래도 당신은 여전히 악마야.\"\n\n\"천사였다 악마였다, 그런 거요?\" 버킷 씨가 외친다. \"허나 나는 제 본분에 따라 일하는 중이란 걸 헤아려주시오. 숄을 단정히 매만져드리리다. 나는 전에도 여러 사람의 시중을 들어줬소. 보닛엔 더 손볼 데 없소? 문 앞에 마차가 와 있소.\"\n\n오르탕스 양은 거울에 분개한 눈길을 던지더니, 단번에 몸을 흔들어 완벽하게 매무새를 가다듬는데, 공평하게 말하자면 유달리 기품 있어 보인다.\n\n\"그럼 들어봐, 내 천사님,\" 그녀가 비아냥대듯 여러 번 고개를 끄덕인 뒤 말한다. \"당신 참 영리해. 그런데 그 사람을 도로 살려낼 수 있어?\"\n\n버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"딱히 그렇진 못하지.\"\n\n\"그것참 우습네. 한 번만 더 들어봐. 당신 참 영리해. 저 여자를 명예로운 마님으로 만들 수 있어?\"\n\n\"그렇게 심술부리지 마시오,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"아니면 저 양반을 도도한 신사로 만들든가?\" 오르탕스 양이 형언할 수 없는 경멸을 담아 레스터 경을 가리키며 외친다. \"흥! 오, 저 꼴 좀 봐! 가엾은 갓난애 같으니! 하! 하! 하!\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 이건 아까보다 더 고약한 떠벌림이로군,\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"자, 갑시다!\"\n\n\"당신은 그런 일들을 못 하잖아? 그럼 나야 당신 마음대로 해. 기껏해야 죽음일 뿐이고, 다 매한가지지. 가자고, 내 천사님. 잘 있으시오, 늙고 머리 센 양반. 당신이 가엾고, 또 경멸스러워!\"\n\n이 마지막 말과 함께 그녀는 입이 용수철로 닫히기라도 하듯 이를 딱 맞물린다. 버킷 씨가 어떻게 그녀를 데리고 나가는지는 도무지 형언할 수 없으나, 그는 그 일을 제 나름으로 무척이나 독특한 방식으로 해내니, 구름처럼 그녀를 감싸고 에워싼 채, 마치 그가 수수한 유피테르이고 그녀가 그 사랑의 대상이기라도 한 양 그녀와 더불어 두둥실 떠나간다.\n\n홀로 남은 레스터 경은, 여전히 귀 기울이고 있는 듯, 여전히 정신을 쏟고 있는 듯, 같은 자세로 그대로 있다. 이윽고 그는 텅 빈 방을 둘러보고, 아무도 없다는 걸 알자 비틀비틀 일어나 의자를 뒤로 밀고, 탁자에 몸을 의지한 채 몇 걸음 걷는다. 그러다 멈춰 서서, 그 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 더 내며, 눈을 들어 무언가를 응시하는 듯하다.\n\n그가 무엇을 보는지는 하늘만이 안다. 체스니 월드의 푸르디푸른 숲, 그 고귀한 저택, 조상들의 초상화, 그것을 훼손하는 낯선 자들, 그의 가장 소중한 가보를 거칠게 다루는 경찰관들, 그를 가리키는 수천 개의 손가락, 그를 비웃는 수천 개의 얼굴. 허나 그런 그림자들이 그의 눈앞을 어지러이 스치며 그를 혼란에 빠뜨릴지언정, 그가 지금도 어느 정도 또렷하게 이름 부를 수 있는, 그리하여 오직 그 하나에게만 백발을 쥐어뜯고 두 팔을 뻗어 호소하는 또 다른 그림자가 하나 있다.\n\n그것은 바로, 그녀가 여러 해 동안 그의 위엄과 자부심이라는 뿌리의 중심 줄기였다는 점을 빼면, 그가 더불어 단 한 번도 이기적인 생각을 품어본 적 없는 그 사람이다. 그것은 바로 그가 사랑하고, 흠모하고, 존중하고, 세상이 우러르도록 떠받들어온 그 사람이다. 그것은 바로 그의 삶을 옭아맨 그 모든 격식과 인습의 핵심에서, 살아 숨 쉬는 다정함과 사랑의 원천이 되어준, 다른 무엇도 그러지 못하리만치 지금 그가 느끼는 고통에 함께 찔릴 줄 아는 그 사람이다. 그는 거의 자기 자신은 잊은 채 그녀를 보고 있으며, 그녀가 그토록 잘 빛내온 그 높은 자리에서 굴러떨어지는 모습을 차마 바라보지 못한다.\n\n그리하여 그가 제 고통도 잊은 채 바닥에 쓰러지는 그 순간까지도, 그는 그 비집고 드는 소리들 한가운데서 어느 정도 또렷하게 그녀의 이름을 부를 수 있으니, 그것도 책망이 아니라 차라리 애도와 연민의 어조로.\n\n수사반 버킷 형사는 방금 기록한 대로 아직 큰 한 방을 날리지 않았고, 결전의 날을 앞두고 잠으로 원기를 보충하는 중이다. 그러나 밤을 가로질러, 얼어붙은 겨울 길을 따라, 이두마차 한 대가 링컨셔에서 빠져나와 런던 쪽으로 길을 잡는다.\n\n머지않아 철도가 이 온 시골을 가로지를 것이고, 덜컹거리는 소리와 번쩍이는 불빛 속에 기관차와 객차가 너른 밤 풍경 위로 유성처럼 내달리며 달빛마저 창백하게 만들 것이다. 그러나 아직은 이 일대에 그런 것이 존재하지 않으니, 그렇다고 전혀 예상하지 못할 일도 아니다. 준비는 진행되고, 측량은 끝났으며, 땅에는 말뚝이 박힌다. 다리는 짓기 시작했고, 아직 이어지지 않은 교각들은 길과 개천 너머로 서로를 황량하게 바라보는데, 마치 결합을 가로막는 장애물을 사이에 둔 벽돌과 회반죽의 한 쌍 같다. 둑은 조각조각 쌓다가 그대로 버려져 절벽이 되었고, 그 위로 녹슨 손수레와 외바퀴차가 우르르 굴러떨어진다. 굴이 뚫린다는 소문이 도는 언덕 꼭대기마다 키 큰 장대로 세운 삼각대가 솟아 있다. 모든 것이 혼란스럽고, 완전히 희망 없이 버려진 듯 보인다. 얼어붙은 길을 따라, 밤을 가로질러, 역마차는 철도 따위는 안중에도 없이 길을 잡는다.\n\n여러 해 동안 체스니 월드의 가정부로 지낸 라운스웰 부인이 마차 안에 앉아 있고, 그 곁에는 회색 망토에 우산을 챙긴 배그넷 부인이 앉아 있다. 늙은 여인은 날씨에 그대로 드러나는 데다 평소 여행 방식에 더 어울리는 원시적인 횃대 같은, 앞쪽 좌석을 더 좋아할 테지만, 라운스웰 부인이 그녀의 편안함을 어찌나 헤아리는지 그 제안을 입에 올릴 틈조차 주지 않는다. 늙은 마님은 늙은 여인을 어떻게 대접해야 할지 모를 정도다. 그녀는 위엄 있는 몸가짐으로 앉아 상대의 손을 잡고, 그 거친 살결도 아랑곳하지 않은 채 자주 입술에 가져다 댄다. \"당신은 어머니예요, 고마운 분.\" 그녀가 몇 번이고 말한다. \"그런 당신이 우리 조지의 어머니를 찾아주었어요!\"\n\n\"아, 조지는,\" 배그넷 부인이 대꾸한다. \"저한테 늘 스스럼없었답니다, 부인. 그이가 우리 집에서 우리 울위치한테, 어른이 되어 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것 중에 제일 마음 편한 건 어머니 얼굴에 시름의 주름 한 줄 새긴 적 없고 머리카락 한 올 세게 만든 적 없다는 거라고 했을 때, 저는 그 말투에서 뭔가가 새삼 그이 어머니를 머릿속에 떠올리게 했구나 하고 확신했지요. 지난날에도 그이가 어머니께 못되게 굴었다고 저한테 종종 말하곤 했거든요.\"\n\n\"그런 적 없어요, 부인!\" 라운스웰 부인이 눈물을 터뜨리며 대꾸한다. \"그 애한테 복이 있기를, 절대 그런 적 없어요! 그 애는 늘 나를 좋아했고, 다정했어요, 우리 조지는! 다만 기개가 당찬 애라서, 조금 거칠게 굴다가 군인이 되겠다고 떠난 거예요. 그 애가 처음엔 자기 소식을 알리는 걸 미뤘던 거, 다 알아요. 장교로 올라설 때까지 기다린 거지요. 그러다 올라서지 못하니까 자기가 우리 밑이라고 여기고, 우리한테 수치가 되지 않으려 했던 거예요. 우리 조지는 사자의 심장을 가진 애였거든요, 갓난아기 적부터 늘!\"\n\n늙은 마님의 두 손은 예전처럼 정처 없이 떠돌고, 그러는 사이 그녀는 온몸을 떨며 회상한다. 그 애가 얼마나 싹수 있는 녀석이었는지, 얼마나 훤칠한 녀석이었는지, 얼마나 명랑하고 너그럽고 영리한 녀석이었는지를. 체스니 월드 사람들이 죄다 그 애를 얼마나 따랐는지, 레스터 경이 젊은 신사이던 그 애를 얼마나 아꼈는지, 개들까지 그 애를 얼마나 따랐는지, 그 애한테 화내던 사람들조차 그 애가 떠나자마자 어찌 그리 용서했는지, 가엾은 녀석. 그런데 이제 와서, 그것도 감옥에서 그 애를 보게 되다니! 넓은 가슴받이가 들썩이고, 기묘하게 꼿꼿하고 고풍스러운 자태가 애정 어린 비탄의 무게에 짓눌려 휜다.\n\n배그넷 부인은 따뜻하고 선한 마음에서 우러난 본능적인 솜씨로, 늙은 가정부를 잠시 그 감정에 내버려둔다. 손등으로 제 어미다운 두 눈을 슬쩍 훔치는 일은 빠뜨리지 않으면서. 이윽고 그녀는 명랑한 투로 새가 지저귀듯 이야기를 꺼낸다. \"그래서 차 마시러 들어오라고 조지를 부르러 갔을 때(그이는 밖에서 담배 피우는 척하고 있었지요), 제가 그랬어요. '대체 오늘 오후엔 무슨 일이오, 조지, 맙소사? 별의별 사람을 다 봤고, 당신도 좋을 때 궂을 때, 외국에서 집에서 꽤 자주 봤지만, 이렇게 시름에 잠겨 뉘우치는 꼴은 처음 보네.' '아, 배그넷 부인,' 조지가 그래요. '오늘 오후엔 정말로 시름에 잠겨 뉘우치고 있어서 그렇게 보이는 거랍니다.' '대체 뭘 했길래 그러오, 이 양반아?' 제가 물었지요. '아, 배그넷 부인,' 조지가 고개를 흔들며 그래요. '내가 한 일은 오래전, 아주 오래전에 한 일이라, 이제 와서 되돌리려 해도 안 되는 게 상책입니다. 내가 천국에 가게 된다면, 그건 홀어머니께 효도한 덕분은 아닐 거예요. 더는 말 안 하렵니다.' 자, 부인, 조지가 이제 와서 되돌리려 해도 안 되는 게 상책이라고 하니, 저는 전부터 자주 그랬듯이 짚이는 데가 있어서, 대체 그날 오후에 무슨 일로 그런 생각에 잠기게 됐는지 캐물었지요. 그러자 조지가, 변호사 사무실에서 우연히 어떤 점잖은 노부인을 봤는데 그분이 자기 어머니를 눈앞에 또렷이 떠올리게 했다지 뭐예요. 그러더니 그 노부인 얘기를 줄줄이 늘어놓다가 완전히 넋이 나가서, 수십 년 전 그분 모습까지 저한테 그려 보이더라고요. 그래서 다 말하고 나니까 제가 물었지요, 본 노부인이 누구냐고. 조지가 그러는데, 링컨셔 체스니 월드의 데드록 가문에서 반세기 넘게 가정부로 지낸 라운스웰 부인이라잖아요. 조지가 전에도 자기는 링컨셔 사람이라고 자주 말했거든요. 그래서 제가 그날 밤 우리 리그넘 영감한테 그랬어요. '리그넘, 사십오 파운드를 걸고서라도 저게 그이 어머니야!'\"\n\n이 모든 이야기를 배그넷 부인은 이제 지난 네 시간 동안 적어도 스무 번째로 풀어놓는 중이다. 일종의 새처럼 꽤 높은 음으로 곱게 지저귀어, 바퀴 굴러가는 소리 너머로도 늙은 마님이 들을 수 있도록.\n\n\"고마워요, 정말 고마워요.\" 라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"고맙고 또 고마워요, 갸륵한 분!\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 배그넷 부인이 더없이 자연스러운 투로 외친다. \"저한테 고마워하실 거 없어요, 정말로. 부인 자신한테 고마워하셔야지요, 이리 선뜻 고마워할 줄 아시니! 그리고 부인, 다시 한 번 명심하세요. 조지가 부인의 친아들로 밝혀진 마당에 부인이 하실 일은, 부인을 봐서라도 그이가 온갖 도움을 다 받아 떳떳이 처신하고, 부인이나 저처럼 결백한데도 뒤집어쓴 혐의를 벗게 하는 거예요. 진실과 정의가 그이 편이라고 다 되는 게 아니거든요. 법과 변호사가 있어야 해요.\" 늙은 여인이 외친다. 보아하니 그녀는 법과 변호사라는 게 진실, 정의와는 별개의 살림이라, 그 둘과 영원토록 손을 끊었다고 믿는 모양이다.\n\n\"그 애한테는,\" 라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"세상에서 구할 수 있는 도움을 죄다 받게 해줄 거예요, 부인. 가진 걸 다 쓰겠어요, 기꺼이, 그걸 마련하느라. 레스터 경께서도 최선을 다하실 테고, 온 집안이 최선을 다할 거예요. 나는, 나는 뭔가 좀 아는 게 있어요, 부인. 그래서 나도 나대로 호소할 거예요. 그 애 어미로서, 이 긴 세월 그 애와 헤어져 지내다 끝내 감옥에서 그 애를 찾았으니까요.\"\n\n이 말을 하면서 늙은 가정부가 보이는 극심한 동요, 끊어지는 말투, 두 손을 비트는 모습은 배그넷 부인에게 강한 인상을 남기고, 만일 그 모두를 아들 처지를 슬퍼한 탓으로 돌리지 않았다면 그녀를 깜짝 놀라게 했을 것이다. 그래도 배그넷 부인은 라운스웰 부인이 왜 저리 넋 나간 듯 \"마님, 마님, 마님!\"을 거듭 중얼거리는지 의아하긴 하다.\n\n서리 내린 밤이 저물어가고 동이 트며, 역마차는 이른 안개를 헤치고 굴러간다. 마치 떠나간 마차의 유령처럼. 그 곁에는 나무와 산울타리의 유령이 잔뜩 유령 같은 동행이 되어, 서서히 사라지며 한낮의 실체에 자리를 내준다. 런던에 닿자 길손들이 내린다. 늙은 가정부는 큰 시름과 혼란에 잠겨 있고, 배그넷 부인은 더없이 말끔하고 침착하다. 다음 행선지가 새 마차도 행장도 없이 곧장 희망봉이든 어센션 섬이든 홍콩이든, 그 밖의 어느 주둔지든 마찬가지였을 그런 태도다.\n\n그러나 기병이 갇힌 감옥으로 길을 나설 무렵, 늙은 마님은 라벤더빛 드레스와 더불어 으레 거기 따라붙는 차분한 평정을 한껏 끌어모은 참이다. 놀랍도록 진중하고 단정하고 우아한 옛 도자기처럼 보이지만, 정작 가슴은 빠르게 뛰고 가슴받이는 이 변덕쟁이 아들에 대한 기억이 지난 숱한 세월 헝클어뜨린 것보다도 더 헝클어져 있다.\n\n감방에 다가가니 문이 열리고 간수 하나가 막 나오는 참이다. 늙은 여인이 얼른 아무 말 말라고 간청하는 손짓을 보낸다. 간수는 고개를 끄덕여 응하고는 문을 닫으며 둘을 들여보낸다.\n\n그래서 자기 혼자뿐인 줄 알고 책상에서 글을 쓰던 조지는 눈을 들지 않고 골똘히 빠져 있다. 늙은 가정부가 그를 바라보는데, 정처 없이 떠도는 그 두 손만으로도 배그넷 부인의 확신은 충분하다. 설령 아는 바를 알면서 어미와 아들을 나란히 두고 본다 한들 둘의 핏줄을 의심할 수 있을까마는.\n\n가정부의 옷자락이 바스락거리는 소리도, 몸짓 하나도, 말 한마디도 그녀를 드러내지 않는다. 그녀는 아무것도 모른 채 글을 써내려가는 그를 그저 바라보고 서 있고, 오직 파닥거리는 두 손만이 감정을 토로한다. 그러나 그 손은 무척이나 웅변적이다, 무척이나, 무척이나 웅변적이다. 배그넷 부인은 그 손을 알아듣는다. 그 손은 감사를, 기쁨을, 비탄을, 희망을 말한다. 이 건장한 사내가 풋내기였던 적부터 보답 한 번 받지 못한 채 품어온, 결코 꺼지지 않는 애정을 말한다. 덜 사랑한 더 나은 아들과, 그토록 애틋하고 그토록 자랑스럽게 사랑한 이 아들을 말한다. 그 손은 어찌나 가슴 저미게 말하는지, 배그넷 부인의 두 눈에 눈물이 차올라 볕에 그을린 갈색 얼굴을 타고 반짝이며 흘러내린다.\n\n\"조지 라운스웰! 오, 내 사랑하는 아들아, 돌아서서 나를 봐다오!\"\n\n기병이 펄쩍 일어나 어머니의 목을 끌어안고는 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇는다. 뒤늦은 뉘우침인지, 그에게 되살아난 첫 기억의 끌림인지, 그는 아이가 기도할 때처럼 두 손을 모으고 그것을 어머니의 가슴 쪽으로 들어 올린 채 고개를 숙이고 운다.\n\n\"우리 조지, 내 가장 소중한 아들! 늘 내 귀염둥이였고 지금도 여전히 내 귀염둥이인데, 이 모진 세월 동안 어디 있었니? 이렇게 어른이 되어서, 이렇게 훤칠하고 늠름한 사내가 되어서. 하느님 뜻으로 살아만 있다면 분명 이리 됐으리라고 내가 알던 그 모습 그대로 자랐구나!\"\n\n한동안은 그녀도 무엇 하나 조리 있게 묻지 못하고, 그도 무엇 하나 조리 있게 답하지 못한다. 그동안 내내 늙은 여인은 돌아서서 한쪽 팔을 희게 칠한 벽에 기대고, 그 위에 정직한 이마를 묻고는, 요긴한 회색 망토로 눈물을 훔치며, 더없이 좋은 늙은 여인답게 한껏 그 순간을 즐긴다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 둘이 좀 더 진정했을 때 기병이 말한다. \"무엇보다 먼저 저를 용서해주세요. 제게 그게 필요한 줄 아니까요.\"\n\n용서라니! 그녀는 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 용서한다. 늘 용서해왔다. 그녀는 이 숱한 세월 유언장에 그가 자기 사랑하는 아들 조지라고 적어두었다고 그에게 말한다. 그에 대해 나쁜 소문은 한 번도 믿은 적이 없다고, 한 번도. 만약 이 행복을 누리지 못한 채 죽었더라면 — 이제 그녀는 늙은 여인이라 그리 오래 살 가망도 없다 — 정신만 온전했다면 마지막 숨으로라도 그를 사랑하는 아들 조지로서 축복했을 거라고.\n\n\"어머니, 저는 어머니께 효도 모르는 골칫거리였으니, 이런 벌도 마땅하지요. 하지만 근래 몇 해 동안엔 제게도 무언가 어렴풋한 목적 같은 게 깃들기 시작했답니다. 집을 떠날 적엔 떠나는 것에 별로 마음 쓰지 않았어요, 어머니. 거의 아무렇지도 않았던 것 같아 두렵습니다. 그저 무작정 떠나서 입대했지요, 정신없이. 나는 아무도 신경 안 쓴다고, 암 그렇고말고, 나를 신경 쓸 사람도 없다고 믿는 척하면서요.\"\n\n기병은 눈물을 닦고 손수건을 치웠지만, 평소 그가 말하고 처신하던 투와 지금 그가 말하는 누그러진 음색 사이에는 유난한 대조가 있다. 그 음색은 이따금 반쯤 삼킨 흐느낌에 끊긴다.\n\n\"그래서 집에 편지 한 줄 띄웠지요, 어머니. 어머니도 너무 잘 아시다시피, 다른 이름으로 입대했다고요. 그러고는 외국으로 나갔습니다. 외국에서, 한때는 내년에 형편이 나아지면 집에 편지하리라 생각했어요. 그러다 그 한 해가 지나면 또, 내년에 형편이 나아지면 집에 편지하리라 생각했지요. 그렇게 또 한 해가 지나니, 어쩌면 별로 생각조차 안 하게 됐어요. 그런 식으로 해가 가고 또 가, 십 년을 복무하는 사이 저도 나이가 들기 시작했고, 대체 내가 편지를 쓸 까닭이 무어냐고 스스로에게 묻기에 이르렀지요.\"\n\n\"널 탓하는 게 아니란다, 얘야. 그래도 이 어미 마음 좀 놓아주려고는 안 했니, 조지? 같이 나이 들어가던 이 사랑하는 어미한테 한마디도?\"\n\n이 말에 기병은 다시 무너질 뻔하지만, 크고 거친 헛기침으로 목청을 쿵 가다듬으며 마음을 다잡는다.\n\n\"하느님, 저를 용서하소서. 어머니, 하지만 그땐 제 소식을 들어봤자 작은 위안밖에 안 되리라 여겼답니다. 어머니는 존경받고 우러름 받는 분이셨지요. 형은, 이따금 북부 지방 신문에서 우연히 읽었듯, 번창하고 이름난 사람으로 올라서고 있었고요. 저는 떠돌고 뿌리 없는 기병이었어요. 형처럼 자수성가한 게 아니라 스스로 망가뜨린 신세였지요. 어릴 적 누리던 온갖 이점은 다 내던졌고, 보잘것없는 배움도 다 까먹었고, 떠올릴 수 있는 웬만한 일에 죄다 어울리지 않게 만드는 것 말곤 아무것도 익히지 못했으니까요. 제가 무슨 자격으로 제 존재를 알린단 말입니까? 그 긴 세월을 다 흘려보내고 나서, 그게 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 어머니, 최악은 이미 지나간 뒤였어요. 그때쯤 저도 (어른이 되었으니) 어머니가 저를 어떻게 애도하고, 저를 위해 울고, 저를 위해 기도했는지 알았지요. 그 아픔은 이미 지나갔거나 무뎌졌을 테고, 차라리 그대로 두는 편이 어머니 마음에 낫겠다 싶었던 겁니다.\"\n\n늙은 마님은 서글피 고개를 젓고는, 그의 억센 손 하나를 잡아 사랑스레 제 어깨에 얹는다.\n\n\"아니에요, 정말 그랬다는 게 아니라, 제가 그렇게 짜맞춘 거예요, 어머니. 방금 제가 그랬지요, 그게 무슨 소용이 있겠냐고. 그런데 사랑하는 어머니, 그게 저 자신한테는 무슨 소용이 됐을지도 모르지요. 바로 거기에 제 비열함이 있었던 겁니다. 어머니는 저를 찾아내셨을 테고, 제 제대를 돈 주고 사주셨을 테고, 저를 체스니 월드로 데려가셨을 테지요. 저랑 형이랑 형네 식구들을 한자리에 모으셨을 테고요. 다들 저를 위해 무얼 해줄지, 어떻게 저를 번듯한 시민으로 세워줄지 애태우며 궁리하셨을 거예요. 하지만 제가 저 자신조차 미덥지 않은데 어머니인들 어찌 저를 미덥다 여기시겠어요? 규율 아래에 있을 때 말고는 제 한 몸에도 짐이요 수치인, 빈둥대는 기병 녀석을 어머니인들 어찌 짐이요 수치로 여기지 않으시겠어요? 제가 무슨 낯으로 형의 아이들을 마주 보고 본보기인 척하겠어요? 집에서 도망쳐 나와 어머니 평생의 슬픔이요 불행이던 이 떠돌이 녀석이 말입니다. '안 돼, 조지.' 어머니, 이 모든 걸 눈앞에 죽 떠올렸을 때 제가 한 말이 그거였어요. '네가 잠자리를 깔았으니, 이제 거기 누워라.'\"\n\n라운스웰 부인이 위엄 있는 자태를 곧추세우며, 부풀어 오르는 자긍심을 안고 늙은 여인을 향해 고개를 끄덕인다. \"내가 그랬잖아요!\" 하고 말하듯이. 늙은 여인은 우산으로 기병의 양 어깨 사이를 콱 찔러 제 심정을 풀고 대화에 쏠린 관심을 드러낸다. 이 동작을 그녀는 그 뒤로도 일종의 애정 어린 광기를 부려 틈틈이 되풀이하는데, 매번 이 항의를 가하고 나면 어김없이 다시 희게 칠한 벽과 회색 망토로 돌아간다.\n\n\"이런 식으로 제 마음을 다잡았지요, 어머니. 제가 깔아놓은 그 잠자리에 누워서 거기서 죽는 것이 제 최선의 속죄라고요. 그리고 실제로 그렇게 했을 거예요(어머니는 까맣게 모르셨지만, 체스니 월드로 어머니를 보러 한 번 넘게 내려간 적도 있답니다). 여기 이 옛 전우의 아내만 아니었다면요. 알고 보니 이분이 저보다 한 수 위였더군요. 그래도 이분께 감사합니다. 진심으로, 온 힘을 다해 감사드려요, 배그넷 부인.\"\n\n이에 배그넷 부인은 우산으로 두 번 찌르는 것으로 응한다.\n\n이제 늙은 마님은 아들 조지에게 — 되찾은 자기 사랑하는 아들, 자신의 기쁨이요 자랑이요 눈의 빛이요 생의 행복한 끝맺음이며, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 애틋한 이름의 그 아들에게 — 신신당부한다. 돈과 인맥으로 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 조언을 따라야 한다고, 구할 수 있는 가장 뛰어난 변호사들에게 사건을 맡겨야 한다고, 이 엄중한 곤경에서는 시키는 대로 처신해야 하며, 제아무리 옳더라도 고집을 부려선 안 된다고, 풀려날 때까지는 오직 가엾은 늙은 어미의 근심과 고통만 생각하겠다고 약속해야 한다고, 안 그러면 어미의 가슴이 찢어진다고.\n\n\"어머니, 그쯤 따르는 거야 어렵지 않지요.\" 기병이 입맞춤으로 그녀의 말을 막으며 대꾸한다. \"제가 뭘 하면 될지 일러주세요. 뒤늦게나마 시작해서 그대로 하겠습니다. 배그넷 부인, 제 어머니를 보살펴주시겠지요, 그러시리라 압니다.\"\n\n늙은 여인의 우산이 아주 세게 한 번 찌른다.\n\n\"어머니를 잔다이스 씨와 서머슨 양에게 소개해주시면, 어머니도 그분들이 같은 생각인 걸 아실 테고, 그분들이 가장 좋은 조언과 도움을 주실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 조지,\" 늙은 마님이 말한다. \"한시바삐 네 형을 부르러 사람을 보내야겠다. 사람들 말로는 분별 있고 견실한 사람이라더구나. 체스니 월드 너머 바깥세상에 있어서 나도 잘은 모르지만, 얘야, 큰 도움이 될 게다.\"\n\n\"어머니,\" 기병이 대꾸한다. \"부탁 하나 드리기엔 너무 이른가요?\"\n\n\"이르긴, 절대 아니란다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그럼 이 큰 부탁 하나만 들어주세요. 형한테는 알리지 말아주세요.\"\n\n\"무얼 알리지 말라는 거니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"저에 관한 걸요. 사실은요, 어머니, 견딜 수가 없어요. 도무지 마음을 다잡을 수가 없습니다. 형은 저와는 영 다른 사람임을 증명해 보였고, 제가 군바리 노릇 하는 동안 자기를 그토록 끌어올렸으니, 이런 곳에서 이런 혐의를 뒤집어쓴 제 꼴을 형에게 보일 만한 뱃심이 제겐 없어요. 형 같은 사람이 그런 사실을 알고 무슨 기쁨을 느끼겠어요? 어림없는 일이지요. 안 돼요, 형한테는 제 비밀을 지켜주세요, 어머니. 제가 받을 자격보다 더 큰 은혜를 베풀어주세요. 하고많은 사람 중에 형한테만은 제 비밀을 지켜주세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 영영은 아니지, 조지?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 어머니, 어쩌면 영영 안 알리는 건 아닐 수도 있어요. 저도 언젠가 그렇게 부탁드리게 될지 모르지요. 하지만 지금은 지켜주세요, 간곡히 빕니다. 만약 언젠가 못난 동생 놈이 나타났다는 사실을 형한테 알리게 된다면, 저는 차라리,\" 기병이 영 미덥잖다는 듯 고개를 흔들며 말한다. \"제 입으로 알리고 싶어요. 그래서 형이 그걸 받아들이는 태도를 보고 나아갈지 물러설지 정하고 싶습니다.\"\n\n이 점에 관해 그의 마음이 단단히 뿌리내린 것이 분명하고, 그 깊이가 배그넷 부인의 얼굴에도 읽히는지라, 어머니는 그가 청하는 바에 두말없이 동의한다. 이에 그는 다정하게 감사한다.\n\n\"다른 모든 일에서는, 사랑하는 어머니, 어머니가 바라시는 만큼 고분고분 따르겠어요. 다만 이 하나만은 제 고집을 부립니다. 자, 이제 변호사들을 만날 채비까지 됐어요. 제가 죽은 양반에 대해 아는 바와, 어쩌다 이 불운한 사건에 얽혀들게 됐는지를 정확히 적어두는 중이었거든요.\" 그가 책상 위 자기 글을 흘끔 본다. \"당번병 일지처럼 분명하고 조리 있게 기재해뒀지요. 사실에 필요한 것 말곤 한마디도 안 들어갔어요. 저를 변호하라는 요청이 오면 언제든 처음부터 끝까지 죽 읽어줄 작정이었습니다. 지금도 그렇게 하도록 허락받기를 바라지만, 이 사건에서는 더 이상 제 고집을 부리지 않을 테니, 무슨 말을 듣고 무슨 일을 하든 제 뜻은 내세우지 않겠다고 약속드리지요.\"\n\n일이 이쯤 흡족하게 매듭지어지고 시간도 기울어가자, 배그넷 부인이 떠나자고 제안한다. 늙은 마님은 거듭거듭 아들의 목에 매달리고, 기병은 거듭거듭 어머니를 제 넓은 가슴에 끌어안는다.\n\n\"제 어머니를 어디로 모셔갈 건가요, 배그넷 부인?\"\n\n\"시내 저택으로 가요, 얘야, 그 집안 저택 말이다. 거기 당장 살펴야 할 볼일이 좀 있단다.\" 라운스웰 부인이 답한다.\n\n\"마차로 어머니를 거기까지 무사히 모셔다드리겠지요, 배그넷 부인? 하기야 당연히 그러실 줄 압니다. 제가 왜 그런 걸 묻겠어요!\"\n\n정말이지 왜 묻겠나, 하고 배그넷 부인은 우산으로 표현한다.\n\n\"제 어머니를 모셔다드려요, 오랜 벗이여. 그리고 제 감사도 함께 가져가세요. 퀘벡과 몰타한테 입맞춤을, 제 대자한테 사랑을, 리그넘한테는 진심 어린 악수를, 그리고 이건 부인 몫이에요. 이게 황금 일만 파운드였으면 좋겠습니다, 부인!\" 이렇게 말하며 기병은 늙은 여인의 그을린 이마에 입술을 갖다 댄다. 그러고는 그를 가둔 감방의 문이 닫힌다.\n\n마음씨 좋은 늙은 가정부가 아무리 간청해도, 배그넷 부인은 자기 귀갓길에 마차를 붙들어두려 하지 않는다. 데드록 저택 문 앞에서 명랑하게 폴짝 뛰어내려 라운스웰 부인을 계단까지 부축해 올린 늙은 여인은, 악수를 나누고 터덜터덜 걸어가, 곧 배그넷 가족의 품에 이르러 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 푸성귀를 씻기 시작한다.\n\n마님은 살해된 사내와 마지막으로 담판을 벌였던 바로 그 방에 있다. 그날 밤 앉았던 자리에 앉아, 그가 난롯가에 서서 그토록 한가로이 자신을 살피던 그 자리를 바라보는데, 문에 똑똑 소리가 난다. 누구인가? 라운스웰 부인이다. 무슨 일로 라운스웰 부인이 이리도 뜻밖에 상경했는가?\n\n\"걱정거리예요, 마님. 슬픈 걱정거리지요. 오, 마님, 한 말씀 여쭤도 될까요?\"\n\n이 차분한 늙은 여인을 이토록 떨게 하는 새 사달이 대체 무엇인가? 마님이 늘 생각해왔듯 마님보다 훨씬 행복한 사람이 어찌하여 이리도 머뭇거리며, 그런 이상한 의심의 눈초리로 마님을 바라보는가?\n\n\"무슨 일이오? 앉아서 숨을 좀 돌리시오.\"\n\n\"오, 마님, 마님. 제 아들을 찾았어요. 오래전에 군인이 되겠다고 떠난 막내를요. 그런데 그 애가 감옥에 있답니다.\"\n\n\"빚 때문에?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 마님. 빚이라면 무슨 빚이든 갚았겠지요, 그것도 기쁘게요.\"\n\n\"그럼 무슨 일로 감옥에 있다는 거요?\"\n\n\"살인 혐의예요, 마님. 그 애는 저처럼 결백한데요. 토킹혼 씨를 살해했다고 고발당했답니다.\"\n\n이 눈빛과 이 애원하는 몸짓은 무엇을 뜻하는가? 어찌하여 이리도 가까이 다가오는가? 그녀가 손에 쥔 저 편지는 무엇인가?\n\n\"데드록 마님, 사랑하는 마님, 어지신 마님, 인자하신 마님! 마님께는 저를 가엾이 여길 마음이 있으실 거예요, 저를 용서하실 마음이 있으실 거예요. 저는 마님이 태어나시기 전부터 이 집안에 있었어요. 이 집안에 헌신해왔지요. 하지만 억울하게 고발당한 제 사랑하는 아들을 생각해주세요.\"\n\n\"나는 그를 고발하지 않소.\"\n\n\"네, 마님, 그러시지요. 하지만 다른 이들이 고발했고, 그 애는 감옥에 갇혀 위태롭답니다. 오, 데드록 마님, 그 애의 누명을 벗기는 데 보탬이 될 한마디라도 하실 수 있다면, 부디 해주세요!\"\n\n이게 무슨 미혹이란 말인가? 그녀가 호소하는 사람에게, 만약 이 부당한 의심이 부당한 것이라면, 그것을 막을 무슨 힘이 있다고 여기는가? 마님의 아름다운 두 눈이 놀라움으로, 거의 두려움으로 그녀를 바라본다.\n\n\"마님, 저는 늘그막에 아들을 찾으려고 어젯밤 체스니 월드를 떠나왔는데요, 유령의 길에 울리던 발소리가 어찌나 끊임없고 어찌나 무겁던지 이 숱한 세월 그런 건 들어본 적이 없었답니다. 밤마다, 날이 어두워지면, 그 소리가 마님의 방들에 메아리쳤지만, 어젯밤이 가장 무시무시했어요. 그리고 어젯밤 날이 어두워질 무렵, 마님, 제가 이 편지를 받았지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 편지요?\"\n\n\"쉿! 쉿!\" 가정부가 주위를 둘러보며 겁먹은 속삭임으로 답한다. \"마님, 저는 이 일을 누구한테도 한마디 발설하지 않았어요. 거기 적힌 걸 믿지도 않고요. 사실일 리 없다는 걸 알아요. 사실이 아니라고 확신하고 또 확신해요. 하지만 제 아들이 위태로우니, 마님께는 저를 가엾이 여길 마음이 있으셔야 해요. 만약 마님이 남들은 모르는 무언가를 아신다면, 무슨 의심이라도 품고 계시다면, 무슨 단서라도 갖고 계시다면, 그걸 마님 가슴속에만 묻어둘 무슨 까닭이 있으시다면, 오, 사랑하는 마님, 저를 생각하셔서 그 까닭을 이겨내시고, 부디 그걸 알려주세요! 제가 바랄 수 있는 건 이게 고작이에요. 마님이 모진 분이 아니신 줄은 알아요. 하지만 마님은 늘 도움 없이 혼자 길을 가시고, 벗들과도 허물없이 지내지 않으시지요. 마님을 우러르는 모든 이가 — 다들 우러르지요 — 아름답고 우아한 마님을 자기들과는 동떨어진, 가까이 다가갈 수 없는 분으로 압니다. 마님, 마님께는 아시는 무언가를 입에 올리길 꺼리실 만한 어떤 자존심이나 노여움의 까닭이 있을지도 몰라요. 만약 그렇다면, 부디, 오, 부디, 평생을 이 집안에서 보낸, 이 집안을 진심으로 사랑하는 충직한 하인을 생각하셔서, 마음을 누그러뜨리시고 제 아들의 누명 벗기는 걸 도와주세요! 마님, 어지신 마님,\" 늙은 가정부가 진솔하고 꾸밈없이 간청한다. \"저는 제 자리에서 이토록 미천하고 마님은 천성이 이토록 높고 멀리 계시니, 제가 제 자식을 두고 느끼는 마음을 마님은 헤아리지 못하실지도 몰라요. 하지만 그 마음이 어찌나 큰지, 이 끔찍한 때에 마님이 우리에게 조금이라도 옳고 정당한 일을 베푸실 수 있다면 부디 우리를 업신여기지 말아달라고, 이렇게 외람되이 빌고 청하러 여기까지 온 거랍니다!\"\n\n데드록 마님은 그녀의 손에서 편지를 받아 들 때까지 한마디 말도 없이 그녀를 일으켜 세운다.\n\n\"이걸 읽어야 하오?\"\n\n\"제가 떠난 뒤에요, 마님, 괜찮으시다면요. 그러고 나서 제가 바랄 수 있는 게 고작 그것임을 기억해주세요.\"\n\n\"내가 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 모르오. 당신 아들에게 영향을 줄 만한, 내가 감춘 것도 아무것도 모르고. 나는 한 번도 그를 고발하지 않았소.\"\n\n\"마님, 편지를 읽으시면 거짓 고발을 당한 그 애를 한층 더 가엾이 여기실지도 몰라요.\"\n\n늙은 가정부는 마님의 손에 편지를 쥐여둔 채 떠난다. 사실 마님은 천성이 모진 여인이 아니어서, 한때는 저토록 절절한 진심으로 호소하는 그 존경스러운 모습이 그녀를 크나큰 연민으로 움직였을 때도 있었다. 그러나 너무나 오래 감정을 억누르고 실제를 짓눌러온 데다, 너무나 오래 제 잇속에 따라 그 파괴적인 학교에서 길들여진 탓에 — 마음의 자연스러운 감정을 호박 속 파리처럼 가두고, 선과 악, 정 있는 것과 정 없는 것, 분별 있는 것과 분별없는 것 위에 한결같고 음울한 윤기 하나를 고루 발라버리는 그런 학교에서 — 그녀는 이제껏 놀라움마저 억눌러왔던 것이다.\n\n그녀가 편지를 펼친다. 종이 위에는 시신이 마룻바닥에 엎어진 채, 심장을 관통당해 발견된 정황이 인쇄되어 펼쳐져 있다. 그 아래에는 그녀 자신의 이름이, \"살인자\"라는 단어와 함께 적혀 있다.\n\n편지가 그녀의 손에서 떨어진다. 그것이 얼마나 오래 바닥에 놓여 있었는지 그녀는 알지 못하나, 하인 하나가 그녀 앞에 서서 거피라는 이름의 젊은이가 왔음을 알릴 때까지도 떨어진 자리에 그대로 놓여 있다. 그 말은 아마 여러 번 되풀이됐을 것이다. 그녀가 그 뜻을 알아듣기 전부터 머릿속에 울리고 있으니.\n\n\"들라 하시오!\"\n\n그가 들어온다. 바닥에서 집어 든 편지를 손에 쥔 채, 그녀는 마음을 추스르려 애쓴다. 거피 씨의 눈에 그녀는 여전히 똑같은 데드록 마님이다, 여전히 준비된, 도도하고 싸늘한 태도를 지닌.\n\n\"마님께서는 한 번도 마님의 환영을 받은 적 없는 자가 이리 찾아온 걸 처음엔 너그러이 봐주실 기분이 아니실지도 모르겠습니다.\" — 그는 이를 불평하는 게 아니다, 겉으로 보아 그가 환영받을 만한 무슨 특별한 까닭이 있던 적은 없음을 인정할 수밖에 없으니까 — \"하지만 제가 마님께 그 동기를 말씀드리면, 저를 탓하지 않으시리라 바랍니다.\" 거피 씨가 말한다.\n\n\"말해보시오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 마님. 먼저 마님께 말씀드려야겠는데요,\" 거피 씨가 의자 끄트머리에 앉아 모자를 제 발치 양탄자에 내려놓는다. \"이전에 마님께 말씀드린 대로 한때 제 심장에 그 모습이 새겨졌다가 제가 어찌할 수 없는 사정으로 지워진 서머슨 양이, 지난번 제가 마님을 뵙는 영광을 누린 뒤에, 자기와 관련된 일이라면 어떤 식으로든 무슨 조치도 일절 취하지 말기를 특별히 바란다고 제게 전했습니다. 그리고 서머슨 양의 뜻은 제게 법이나 마찬가지인지라(제가 어찌할 수 없는 사정과 얽힌 경우는 빼고요), 그래서 저는 다시 마님을 뵙는 영예를 누리리라곤 결코 기대하지 않았지요.\"\n\n그런데도 지금 그가 여기 있다고, 데드록 마님이 시무룩하게 일깨운다.\n\n\"그런데도 지금 제가 여기 있지요.\" 거피 씨가 인정한다. \"제 목적이 마님께, 비밀 엄수를 전제로, 제가 왜 여기 왔는지를 알려드리는 것이니까요.\"\n\n그 점이라면 아무리 분명하게, 아무리 간략하게 말해도 부족함이 없겠다고, 그녀가 그에게 이른다. \"저도 그렇습니다.\" 거피 씨가 무언가 억울하다는 기색으로 대꾸한다. \"제가 여기 온 것이 제 개인적인 일 때문이 아니라는 점을 마님께 각별히 유념해주십사 아무리 각별히 청해도 부족함이 없지요. 여기 오는 데 제 사사로운 잇속이라곤 없습니다. 서머슨 양과의 약속, 그리고 그 약속을 신성히 지키려는 마음만 아니었다면, 솔직히 말해 저는 이 문턱을 다시 넘기는커녕 그러느니 차라리 멀찌감치 사라지는 꼴을 봤을 겁니다.\"\n\n거피 씨는 이 대목이 두 손으로 머리카락을 곧추세우기에 좋은 순간이라 여긴다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드리면 마님께서도 기억하시겠지만, 지난번 제가 여기 왔을 때 저는 우리 업계에서 아주 저명한, 그리고 그 죽음을 우리 모두가 애도하는 어떤 양반과 부딪쳤습니다. 그 양반은 그때부터 분명 제게 맞서 끼어들었어요, 제가 악랄한 수법이라 부를 만한 방식으로요. 그러고는 매 길목마다 매 고비마다, 제가 무심코 서머슨 양의 뜻에 어긋나는 무언가로 이어지는 짓을 하지 않았다고 확신하기를 지독히 어렵게 만들었지요. 자화자찬은 권할 게 못 되지만, 저 자신을 두고 한마디 하자면, 저도 그리 못난 사업가는 아니랍니다.\"\n\n데드록 마님이 준엄하게 따지는 눈으로 그를 본다. 거피 씨는 즉시 그녀의 얼굴에서 눈길을 거두어 아무 데로나 돌린다.\n\n\"정말이지, 그 양반이 다른 이들과 짜고 무슨 꿍꿍이를 꾸미는지 도무지 짐작조차 할 수 없게 만들어놔서요.\" 그가 말을 잇는다. \"우리 모두가 애도하는 그 죽음이 있기 전까지 저는 그야말로 막혀버렸지요. 높은 사교계에서 노니시는 마님께서는 너그러이, 그 표현이 나가떨어졌다는 말과 매한가지라고 여겨주십시오. 스몰 — 이건 마님이 모르시는 또 다른 양반, 제 친구를 가리키는 이름인데요 — 도 어찌나 입을 다물고 두 얼굴로 굴던지, 때로는 그놈 머리통에 손을 안 대기가 쉽지 않을 지경이었답니다. 그렇지만 제 변변찮은 재간을 쥐어짜고, 또 토니 위블 씨라는 한 공통의 벗(고매한 귀족 취향을 지닌 분으로, 마님의 초상화를 늘 제 방에 걸어두고 있지요)의 도움을 받아, 이제 저는 마님께 경계하시라 일러드리러 온 어떤 우려의 근거를 갖게 됐습니다. 우선, 마님, 오늘 아침 이상한 방문객이 있었는지 여쭤봐도 될까요? 사교계의 방문객 말고요, 이를테면 바버리 양의 늙은 하인이라든가, 다리를 쓰지 못해서 무슨 허수아비처럼 위층으로 들려 올라가는 사람 같은 그런 방문객 말입니다.\"\n\n\"없었소!\"\n\"그렇다면 단언컨대, 마님, 그런 방문객들이 여기 와서 여기서 맞이를 받았습니다. 제가 문간에서 그들을 봤고, 그들이 나올 때까지 광장 모퉁이에서 기다렸다가, 그들을 피하느라 그 뒤로 삼십 분쯤 더 돌아다녔으니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 나와 무슨 상관이며, 당신과는 또 무슨 상관이오? 무슨 말인지 모르겠소. 무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"마님, 저는 마님께 경계하시라 일러드리러 온 겁니다. 그럴 까닭이 없을 수도 있지요. 좋습니다. 그렇다면 저는 서머슨 양과의 약속을 지키려 최선을 다한 것뿐이고요. 제가 짐작하기로는(스몰이 흘린 말과, 우리가 그놈한테서 비틀어 짜낸 걸로 봐서) 제가 마님께 가져다드리기로 했던 그 편지들이, 제가 그런 줄로만 알았던 것과 달리, 그때 없애지지 않았던 모양입니다. 폭로될 만한 게 있었다면, 이미 폭로됐다는 거지요. 제가 말씀드린 그 방문객들이 오늘 아침 여기 와서 그걸로 돈을 챙기려 한 거고요. 그리고 그 돈은 이미 챙겨졌거나, 챙겨지는 중이라는 겁니다.\"\n\n거피 씨가 모자를 집어 들고 일어선다.\n\n\"마님, 제가 드린 말씀에 뭔가 있는지 아무것도 없는지는 마님이 가장 잘 아시겠지요. 있든 없든, 저는 서머슨 양의 뜻에 따라 일을 가만 내버려두고, 이미 벌여놓은 일을 가능한 한 되물렸으니, 그걸로 저는 족합니다. 혹시 그럴 필요도 없는데 마님께 경계하시라 일러드려 외람을 범한 거라면, 마님께서 부디 제 주제넘음을 너그러이 견뎌내주시기를 바라고, 저도 마님의 못마땅함을 견뎌내도록 애쓰겠습니다. 이제 마님께 작별을 고하며, 다시는 마님이 저한테서 시중받으실 일이 없으리라는 걸 장담드리지요.\"\n\n그녀는 이 작별의 말을 눈빛으로조차 거의 알은체하지 않지만, 그가 떠나고 조금 지나자 종을 울린다.\n\n\"레스터 경은 어디 계시지?\"\n\n머큐리가 지금 서재에 혼자 틀어박혀 계신다고 아뢴다.\n\n\"레스터 경께 오늘 아침 방문객이 있었나?\"\n\n볼일로 몇 사람 있었습니다. 머큐리가 그들을 묘사하기 시작하는데, 그건 거피 씨가 이미 예고한 바다. 됐다, 가도 좋다.\n\n그래! 모든 게 무너졌다. 그녀의 이름이 이 숱한 입에 오르내리고, 남편은 자기가 입은 모욕을 알며, 그녀의 수치가 세상에 알려질 것이다 — 어쩌면 그녀가 이러는 사이에도 퍼지고 있을지 모른다 — 그리고 그녀가 그토록 오래 내다본, 그러나 그는 그토록 내다보지 못한 그 날벼락에 더하여, 그녀는 보이지 않는 고발자에게 제 원수를 죽인 살인자로 지목당했다.\n\n그는 그녀의 원수였고, 그녀는 자주, 자주, 자주 그가 죽기를 바랐다. 그는 여전히 그녀의 원수다, 무덤 속에서까지도. 이 무시무시한 고발이 그 싸늘한 손에서 새로운 고문처럼 그녀에게 들이닥친다. 그리고 그날 밤 자신이 몰래 그의 문 앞에 갔던 일, 단지 남의 눈을 피하려고 직전에 그토록 일찍 아끼는 하녀를 멀리 보냈다고 둘러대질 수 있겠다는 생각이 떠오르자, 그녀는 마치 교수형 집행인의 손이 제 목을 죄는 듯 진저리친다.\n\n그녀는 바닥에 몸을 내던지고, 머리카락을 온통 마구 흩뜨린 채 긴 의자의 방석에 얼굴을 묻고 엎드린다. 일어나 이리저리 서성이다가 다시 몸을 내던지고, 흔들며 신음한다. 그녀를 짓누르는 공포는 이루 말할 수 없다. 그녀가 정말로 살인자라 한들, 지금 이 순간의 그것보다 더 격렬할 수는 없으리라.\n\n범행 전 그녀의 살의 어린 시야가, 그 실행을 위한 방비책이 제아무리 교묘했던들, 그 가증스러운 형상의 거대한 팽창에 가려져 그 너머의 어떤 결과도 보지 못하게 막혔을 것이고 — 살인이 저질러질 때면 늘 그러하듯 — 그 형상이 거꾸러지는 순간 상상조차 못 한 홍수처럼 그 결과들이 밀려들었을 것이듯, 그렇게 그녀는 이제 깨닫는다. 그가 늘 그녀 앞에서 망보던 시절, 그녀가 \"이 늙은이한테 죽음의 일격이라도 떨어져 내 앞길에서 치워준다면!\" 하고 생각하곤 했던 그것이, 실은 그가 손에 쥐고 그녀를 옭아매던 모든 것이 바람결에 흩날려 사방에 우연히 흩뿌려지기를 바란 것에 지나지 않았음을. 그의 죽음에서 그녀가 느낀 그 사악한 안도 또한 마찬가지다. 그의 죽음이란 음울한 아치의 쐐기돌이 빠진 것 말고 무엇이었던가. 이제 그 아치가 천 조각으로 무너져 내리기 시작하며, 그 조각마다 그녀를 짓이기고 갈가리 찢는다!\n\n그리하여 끔찍한 생각 하나가 그녀에게 슬며시 스며들어 그늘을 드리운다. 살았든 죽었든 이 추적자에게서 — 또렷이 기억나는 그 모습으로 그녀 앞에 완강하고 흔들림 없이 서 있든, 관 속 침상에 누워서도 그에 못지않게 완강하고 흔들림 없든 — 죽음 말고는 벗어날 길이 없다는 생각이. 쫓기는 그녀가 달아난다. 그녀의 수치와 두려움, 회한과 비참이 한데 뒤엉켜 절정에서 그녀를 집어삼킨다. 그녀의 자립심마저 거센 바람에 날리는 잎새처럼 뒤집혀 휩쓸려 날아간다.\n\n그녀는 황급히 남편에게 이 몇 줄을 적어 봉인하고는 책상 위에 남긴다.\n\n만약 제가 그분의 살해범으로 수색되거나 고발된다면, 제가 전적으로 결백함을 믿어주세요. 그 밖에 저에 대한 어떤 좋은 점도 믿지 마세요. 당신이 들었거나 앞으로 들으실, 제게 씌워진 다른 어떤 일에 대해서도 저는 결백하지 않으니까요. 그 끔찍한 밤, 그분은 제 죄를 당신께 폭로하겠노라고 저를 다그쳤습니다. 그분이 떠난 뒤, 저는 가끔 거니는 정원을 산책한다는 핑계로 나갔지만, 실은 그분을 뒤따라가, 그분이 저를 옭아매어온 그 끔찍한 마음 졸임을 — 얼마나 오래였는지 당신은 모르세요 — 더는 끌지 말고 자비롭게 이튿날 아침 결판을 내달라고 마지막 한 번 청하기 위해서였습니다.\n\n그분의 집은 어둡고 고요했습니다. 저는 두 번 문 앞에서 종을 울렸지만 아무 응답이 없어 집으로 돌아왔습니다.\n\n이제 제게는 돌아갈 집이 없습니다. 더는 당신께 짐이 되지 않겠습니다. 부디 당신이 정당한 분노 속에서, 더없이 너그러운 헌신을 헛되이 쏟아부은 이 못난 여인을 잊으실 수 있기를 — 제 자신에게서 황급히 달아날 때보다 더 깊은 수치로 당신을 피하는, 그리고 이 마지막 작별을 적는 이 여인을.\n\n그녀는 황급히 너울을 쓰고 옷을 갖춰 입고는 보석과 돈을 죄다 남겨둔 채 귀를 기울이다가, 현관이 텅 빈 틈에 아래층으로 내려가, 큰 문을 열었다 닫고는, 매서운 서리 바람 속으로 훌쩍 사라진다.\n\n높은 가문에 걸맞게 무표정한 데드록 저택은 음울한 위엄이 깃든 거리의 다른 집들을 무심히 바라보며, 안에서 뭔가 잘못되어 간다는 기색을 겉으로 조금도 내비치지 않는다. 마차가 덜컹거리고, 문이 쾅쾅 두들겨지고, 세상은 서로 방문을 주고받는다. 해골 같은 목과, 대낮에 보면 어쩐지 섬뜩한 화색이 도는 복숭앗빛 뺨을 지닌 늙은 미녀들이, 정말이지 이 매혹적인 존재들이 죽음과 귀부인이 한 몸으로 녹아붙은 듯 보이는 그들이, 사내들의 눈을 호린다. 싸늘한 마구간에서는 아마빛 가발을 쓴 짧은 다리의 마부들이 모는 마차가 부드럽게 흔들리며 나오는데, 마부들은 푹신한 마부석 덮개에 깊숙이 파묻혀 있고, 뒤에는 탐스러운 머큐리들이 올라타 권위의 지팡이를 받쳐 들고 삼각모를 옆으로 비스듬히 눌러쓴 채, 천사들이나 볼 법한 장관을 이룬다.\n\n데드록 저택은 겉으로는 조금도 변하지 않고, 그 드높은 권태가 안에서 흐트러지기까지는 몇 시간이 흐른다. 그러나 어여쁜 볼럼니아는 만연한 병폐인 지루함에 시달리고, 그 무질서가 제법 독하게 기분을 덮쳐오는 것을 느끼자, 마침내 기분 전환을 위해 서재로 발걸음을 옮기는 모험을 감행한다. 문을 살며시 두드려도 아무 대답이 없자, 그녀는 문을 열고 안을 들여다본다. 아무도 없는 것을 보고는 안으로 들어가 차지한다.\n\n이 발랄한 데드록 여인은 풀이 무성한 옛 도시 바스에서, 편하든 불편하든 온갖 자리에서 황금 안경을 눈에 갖다 대고 모로 비집고 다니며 온갖 물건을 들여다보게 만드는 그 다급한 호기심에 휘둘린다는 평을 듣는다. 분명한 것은, 그녀가 지금 이 기회를 틈타 친척의 편지와 서류 위를 새처럼 맴돌며, 이 문서를 쪼아보고 저 문서를 고개를 갸웃이 기울인 채 곁눈질하고, 안경을 눈에 댄 채 캐묻듯 안절부절못하며 탁자에서 탁자로 폴짝폴짝 옮겨 다닌다는 사실이다. 이렇게 뒤지던 중에 그녀는 무언가에 걸려 비틀거리고, 그쪽으로 안경을 돌리자, 친척이 베어 넘긴 나무처럼 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 모습을 본다.\n\n볼럼니아가 즐겨 내지르는 자그마한 비명이 이 놀라움에 힘입어 상당히 실감 나는 비명으로 부풀어 오르고, 집안은 순식간에 발칵 뒤집힌다. 하인들이 계단을 정신없이 오르내리고, 종이 격렬하게 울리고, 의사를 부르러 사람이 달려가고, 데드록 마님을 사방으로 찾지만 보이지 않는다. 마지막으로 종을 울린 뒤로 그녀를 보거나 그녀의 기척을 들은 사람이 아무도 없다. 마님이 레스터 경에게 남긴 편지가 탁자 위에서 발견되지만, 정작 그가 또 다른 세상에서 보낸, 몸소 답해야 할 또 다른 전갈을 이미 받은 것은 아닌지 아직 알 수 없으니, 그에게는 살아 있는 온갖 언어도 죽어버린 온갖 언어도 다 매한가지다.\n\n사람들은 그를 침대에 누이고, 문지르고, 비비고, 부채질하고, 머리에 얼음을 대고, 온갖 회복 수단을 시도한다. 그래도 하루가 다 저물고 방 안에 밤이 깃들고 나서야 그의 그렁그렁한 숨소리가 가라앉고, 이따금 눈앞을 스치는 촛불을 향해 그 고정된 두 눈이 어렴풋한 의식을 비친다. 그러나 이 변화가 시작되자 이어진다. 이윽고 그는 고개를 끄덕이거나 눈을 움직이거나 심지어 손을 움직여 듣고 알아듣는다는 표시를 한다.\n\n오늘 아침 쓰러질 때만 해도 그는 다소 노쇠했으나 풍채가 훌륭하고 얼굴에 살이 통통히 오른, 잘생기고 위엄 있는 신사였다. 지금 침대에 누운 그는 뺨이 움푹 꺼진 노인이요, 제 모습의 노쇠한 그림자다. 그의 목소리는 풍부하고 부드러웠고, 자신이 내뱉는 어떤 말이든 인류에게 무게와 의미가 있다고 너무 오래 굳게 확신해온 나머지, 그의 말은 정말로 그 안에 무언가가 들어 있는 양 들리기에 이르렀다. 그러나 이제 그는 속삭일 수밖에 없고, 그 속삭임은 있는 그대로, 그저 뒤죽박죽 횡설수설로 들린다.\n\n그가 아끼는 충직한 가정부가 침대맡에 서 있다. 그가 알아챈 첫 번째 일이 바로 이것이고, 그는 분명히 거기서 위안을 얻는다. 말로 자기 뜻을 전하려다 헛수고로 끝나자, 그는 연필을 달라는 시늉을 한다. 너무도 알아듣기 힘들게 해서 처음에는 그 뜻을 아무도 헤아리지 못한다. 그가 원하는 것을 알아채고 석판을 가져오는 사람은 바로 그 늙은 가정부다.\n\n한참 멈칫거린 뒤, 그는 제 것이 아닌 듯한 글씨로 천천히 그 위에 갈겨쓴다. \"체스니 월드?\"\n\n아니라고, 그녀가 그에게 일러준다. 여기는 런던이라고. 오늘 아침 서재에서 발작을 일으켜 쓰러졌다고. 마침 자기가 런던에 와 있어 그를 돌볼 수 있게 된 것이 정말로 다행이라고.\n\n\"심각한 병이 아니랍니다, 레스터 경. 내일이면 훨씬 나아지실 거예요, 레스터 경. 의사 선생님들이 다 그렇게 말씀하시는걸요.\" 그렇게 말하는 동안 그녀의 고운 늙은 얼굴 위로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내린다.\n\n방 안을 둘러보고, 의사들이 둘러선 침대 주위를 유난히 주의 깊게 살핀 뒤, 그는 적는다. \"마님.\"\n\n\"마님은 외출하셨어요, 레스터 경. 어르신이 쓰러지시기 전에 나가셔서, 아직 어르신 일을 모르신답니다.\"\n\n그는 몹시 동요하며 그 두 글자를 다시 가리킨다. 사람들이 모두 그를 진정시키려 하지만, 그는 더욱 격하게 동요하며 다시 가리킨다. 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 서로 얼굴만 쳐다보자, 그는 다시 한번 석판을 들고 적는다. \"마님. 제발, 어디에?\" 그러고는 애원하듯 신음을 낸다.\n\n그 늙은 가정부가 그에게 데드록 마님의 편지를 건네주는 편이 낫겠다고 판단되는데, 그 내용은 아무도 모르고 짐작조차 못 한다. 그녀가 편지를 펼쳐 그가 읽을 수 있게 내놓는다. 그는 안간힘을 다해 두 번 읽고 나서, 보이지 않도록 편지를 엎어놓고 신음하며 누워 있다. 그는 다시 도지는 듯한 상태로, 혹은 혼절 상태로 빠져들고, 충직하고 정 깊은 늙은 하인의 팔에 기댄 채 다시 눈을 뜨기까지 한 시간이 걸린다. 의사들은 그가 그녀 곁에 있을 때 가장 편안하다는 것을 알기에, 그를 직접 돌볼 일이 없을 때는 멀찍이 물러서 있다.\n\n석판이 다시 쓰임을 받지만, 그가 적고 싶은 말이 떠오르지 않는다. 이 지경에 이르러 그의 불안과 조바심과 고뇌는 차마 보기 딱하다. 서둘러야 한다는 절박함과, 무엇을 하라거나 누구를 데려오라거나 표현해낼 수 없는 무력함 속에서 그는 미쳐버릴 것만 같다. 그는 B라는 글자를 적고는 거기서 멈춘다. 별안간, 고통이 절정에 달한 순간, 그는 그 앞에 '미스터'를 붙인다. 늙은 가정부가 버킷을 떠올려 말한다. 천만다행이다! 바로 그 뜻이다.\n\n버킷 씨는 약속대로 아래층에 있는 것으로 확인된다. 올라오라고 할까요?\n\n그를 보고 싶어 하는 레스터 경의 불타는 소망이나, 가정부 한 사람만 남기고 방 안의 모두를 내보내고 싶어 하는 그의 뜻은 도무지 잘못 해석할 여지가 없다. 그것은 신속히 이루어지고, 버킷 씨가 나타난다. 세상 모든 사람 가운데, 레스터 경은 자신의 드높은 지위에서 굴러떨어져 오직 이 사내에게만 신뢰와 의지를 두는 듯하다.\n\n\"준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 이런 모습을 뵙게 되어 마음이 안 좋습니다. 부디 기운 내시기를 바랍니다. 가문의 명예를 생각하시면 분명 그러시겠지요.\"\n\n레스터 경이 그녀의 편지를 그의 손에 쥐여주고, 그가 읽는 동안 그의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라본다. 버킷 씨가 읽어 내려갈수록 그의 눈에 새로운 깨달음이 어린다. 그 눈이 아직 글자를 훑는 사이, 그는 손가락을 한 번 까딱하여 일러준다. \"준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 무슨 말씀이신지 알겠습니다.\"\n\n레스터 경이 석판에 쓴다. \"전부 용서한다. 찾아—\" 버킷 씨가 그의 손을 멈춘다.\n\n\"준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 제가 마님을 찾아내겠습니다. 다만 수색은 지금 당장 시작해야 합니다. 일 분도 허비해선 안 됩니다.\"\n\n생각만큼 잽싸게, 그는 탁자 위 작은 상자를 향한 레스터 데드록 경의 눈길을 좇는다.\n\n\"이걸 이리 가져올까요, 준남작 레스터 데드록 경? 그러죠. 여기 이 열쇠 중 하나로 열까요? 그러죠. 제일 작은 열쇠로? 알겠습니다. 지폐를 꺼낼까요? 그러겠습니다. 세어볼까요? 금방 끝납니다. 이십에 삼십이면 오십, 다시 이십이면 칠십, 오십이면 백이십, 사십이면 백육십. 경비로 가져가라고요? 그렇게 하고, 물론 나중에 셈을 보고드리겠습니다. 돈을 아끼지 말라고요? 아끼지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n이 모든 대목을 풀어내는 버킷 씨의 속도와 확신은 거의 기적에 가깝다. 불을 들고 있는 라운스웰 부인은 그가 여정에 나설 채비를 갖추고 벌떡 일어서며 보이는 눈과 손의 날렵함에 어질어질해진다.\n\n\"댁이 조지의 어머니로군요, 노부인. 내 짐작에 그렇지 싶습니다만?\" 버킷 씨가 벌써 모자를 쓰고 외투 단추를 채우며 한쪽으로 비켜 말한다.\n\n\"네, 그래요, 제가 그 애의 애끓는 어미랍니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았습니다. 좀 전에 그가 내게 한 말로 보아서요. 그럼, 한 가지 일러드리지요. 더는 애태우실 것 없습니다. 아드님은 아무 문제 없어요. 자, 이제 우시면 안 됩니다. 댁이 해야 할 일은 준남작 레스터 데드록 경을 보살피는 것인데, 운다고 그 일이 되는 게 아니니까요. 아드님으로 말하자면, 아무 문제 없다니까요. 그리고 사랑을 담아 안부를 전하면서, 어머님도 무탈하시기를 바란다더군요. 그는 떳떳하게 풀려났습니다. 바로 그렇습니다. 그의 인품에 흠 잡힐 데가 없는 것이, 댁의 인품에 흠 잡힐 데 없는 것과 똑같지요. 그리고 댁의 인품이 야무지다는 데 일 파운드 걸지요. 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다. 제가 아드님을 잡아들인 사람이니까요. 그때도 그는 사내답게 처신했고요. 그는 잘 빠진 사내고, 댁은 잘 빠진 노부인이니, 두 분 모자는 천막 흥행에 본보기로 내걸어도 될 만합니다. 준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 제게 맡기신 일은 끝까지 해내겠습니다. 제가 좌로든 우로든 길을 벗어나거나, 한숨 자거나, 씻거나, 면도하거나 하는 일은 찾으러 나선 것을 손에 넣기 전까지는 없을 테니 염려 마십시오. 경의 다정하고 너그러운 뜻을 다 전하라고요? 준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 그러겠습니다. 그리고 어서 쾌차하시고 이 집안일도 잘 수습되기를 바랍니다. 아이고, 다른 숱한 집안일이 그렇게 수습되어 왔고, 또 세상 끝날 때까지 그렇게 수습될 테니까요.\"\n\n이 한바탕 연설을 마치고, 단단히 단추를 채운 버킷 씨는 마치 벌써 밤을 꿰뚫고 달아난 여인을 좇기라도 하는 듯 앞만 똑바로 응시하며 조용히 밖으로 나간다.\n\n그가 맨 먼저 한 일은 데드록 마님의 방으로 가서 도움이 될 만한 사소한 단서라도 없는지 구석구석 살피는 것이다. 방은 이제 어둠에 잠겨 있다. 그리고 밀랍 초를 손에 들고 머리 위로 치켜든 채, 자기와는 묘하게 어울리지 않는 그 숱한 섬세한 물건들을 날카롭게 머릿속으로 헤아리는 버킷 씨를 본다면 그것은 진풍경일 텐데, 그가 안에서 문을 잠그는 데 신경을 쓴 까닭에 그 광경은 아무도 보지 못한다.\n\n\"운치 있는 규방이로군.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. 오늘 아침의 충격으로 그의 프랑스어 솜씨가 어쩐지 닦여나간 기분이다. \"돈깨나 들었겠어. 두고 떠나기 아까운 진귀한 물건들이군. 마음 단단히 먹어야 했을 거야!\"\n\n탁자 서랍을 여닫고 작은 함과 보석 상자를 들여다보다가, 그는 여러 거울에 비친 제 모습을 보고는 거기에 빗대어 인생을 곱씹는다.\n\n\"이러다 내가 사교계라도 드나들면서 올맥스 무도회 채비라도 하는 줄 알겠어.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이러다 나도 모르는 새에 근위대 멋쟁이라도 된 줄 알겠는걸.\"\n\n연신 여기저기 둘러보던 그는 안쪽 서랍에서 앙증맞은 작은 함을 열었다. 너무 가볍고 부드러워 손에 잡히는 감조차 거의 없는 장갑 몇 짝을 그의 큼직한 손이 뒤적이다가, 흰 손수건 한 장에 닿는다.\n\n\"흠! 어디 너를 좀 보자.\" 버킷 씨가 불을 내려놓으며 말한다. \"왜 너만 따로 간직했을까? 무슨 까닭이지? 넌 마님 것이냐, 아니면 다른 누구 것이냐? 어딘가에 표시가 박혀 있겠지, 아마?\"\n\n말하는 사이 그는 그것을 찾아낸다. \"에스더 서머슨.\"\n\n\"오!\" 버킷 씨가 손가락을 귀에 댄 채 멈칫하며 말한다. \"그래, 너를 가져가야겠다.\"\n\n그는 시작할 때처럼 조용하고 신중하게 관찰을 마무리하고, 나머지 것들은 발견한 그대로 한 치도 어긋남 없이 두고서, 통틀어 오 분쯤 지나 슬며시 빠져나가 거리로 나선다. 레스터 경의 방, 희미하게 불 켜진 창문을 한번 올려다보고는, 가장 가까운 마차 정류장으로 전속력으로 달려가 자기 돈값에 맞는 말을 골라내고는, 사격장으로 몰아달라고 이른다. 버킷 씨가 말에 관한 전문가를 자처하는 것은 아니지만, 그쪽 주요 경주에 푼돈쯤은 걸어보는 터라, 그 방면의 식견을 대개 한마디로 간추린다. 잘 달리는 말을 보면 그는 그놈을 알아본다는 것이다.\n\n이번 경우에 그의 식견은 틀리지 않는다. 위험천만한 속도로 돌바닥 위를 덜커덩거리며 달리면서도, 자정 거리에서 스쳐 지나가는 살금살금 움직이는 온갖 것들에, 심지어 사람들이 자리에 들고 있거나 이미 든 위층 창의 불빛에, 그리고 덜컹덜컹 지나치는 온갖 길모퉁이에, 또 무거운 하늘에도, 눈이 엷게 깔린 땅바닥에도 그 예리한 눈을 빈틈없이 던지며—어디서든 도움이 될 무언가가 눈에 띌지 모르니—그는 목적지를 향해 어찌나 빠르게 내달리는지, 멈춰 섰을 때 말이 뿜어내는 김 구름에 그가 반쯤 파묻힌다.\n\n\"잠깐만 마구를 풀어 한숨 돌리게 해줘요. 곧 돌아올 테니.\"\n\n그는 길게 뻗은 나무 통로를 뛰어올라가, 파이프 담배를 피우고 있는 기병을 찾아낸다.\n\n\"이럴 줄 알았네, 조지. 자네가 겪은 일을 생각하면 말이야, 이 사람아. 한가하게 말 나눌 틈이 없네. 자, 약속이네! 다 어떤 여인을 살리려는 일일세. 그리들리가 죽을 때 여기 와 있던 미스 서머슨—그게 이름이었지, 나도 알아—맞아—그분이 어디 사나?\"\n\n기병은 마침 그곳에서 막 돌아온 참이라, 옥스퍼드 거리 근처의 주소를 일러준다.\n\n\"후회할 일 없을 걸세, 조지. 잘 있게!\"\n\n그는 입을 헤벌린 채 자기를 멍하니 바라보며 서리 낀 난로 곁에 앉아 있던 필을 본 듯한 인상을 품고서 다시 떠나, 또다시 말을 몰아 달려가, 또다시 김 구름 속에서 내린다.\n\n집 안에서 유일하게 깨어 있던 잔다이스 씨는 막 자리에 들려던 참인데, 급하게 울리는 종소리를 듣고 책에서 눈을 떼 일어나, 잠옷 바람으로 문간까지 내려온다.\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오, 선생.\" 어느새 그의 방문객이 현관에서 그와 은밀히 이야기를 나누며 문을 닫고, 자물쇠에 손을 얹은 채 서 있다. \"전에 한번 뵌 적이 있지요. 버킷 경위입니다. 이 손수건을 보십시오, 선생. 미스 에스더 서머슨의 것입니다. 십오 분 전에 데드록 마님의 서랍에 따로 넣어둔 것을 제가 직접 찾아냈습니다. 한시도 지체할 수 없습니다. 생사가 걸린 일입니다. 데드록 마님을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"압니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 그 댁에서 어떤 사실이 드러났습니다. 집안일이 불거진 거지요. 준남작 레스터 데드록 경이 발작을 일으켰습니다—뇌졸중인지 마비인지—깨어나질 못해서 귀한 시간을 허비했습니다. 데드록 마님은 오늘 오후에 자취를 감추셨는데, 그분에게 좋지 않아 보이는 편지를 한 장 남겼습니다. 한번 훑어보십시오. 여기 있습니다!\"\n\n잔다이스 씨가 그것을 다 읽고는, 어떻게 생각하느냐고 그에게 묻는다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 자살처럼 보입니다. 어쨌든 시간이 갈수록 그쪽으로 치달을 위험이 점점 커집니다. 지금보다 한 시간만 일찍 시작할 수 있었다면 한 시간에 백 파운드라도 내놓겠습니다. 자, 잔다이스 선생, 저는 준남작 레스터 데드록 경의 의뢰로 그분을 뒤쫓아 찾아내, 살려서 그분의 용서를 전하려는 것입니다. 돈도 있고 전권도 있습니다만, 한 가지가 더 필요합니다. 미스 서머슨이 필요합니다.\"\n\n잔다이스 씨가 곤혹스러운 목소리로 되뇐다. \"미스 서머슨이라고요?\"\n\n\"자, 잔다이스 선생\"—버킷 씨는 줄곧 더없이 주의 깊게 그의 얼굴을 읽어왔다—\"인정 많은 마음을 지닌 신사로 알고 말씀드리는 겁니다. 그것도 흔치 않게 다급한 상황이라서요. 지체가 위험했던 적이 언제고 있었다면 바로 지금이 그렇고, 그 지체를 자초한 일로 나중에 자신을 도저히 용서 못 할 때가 언제고 있다면 바로 지금입니다. 데드록 마님이 사라진 뒤로, 한 시간에 적어도 백 파운드씩이라 말씀드린 그 여덟아홉 시간이 이미 허비됐습니다. 저는 그분을 찾으라는 임무를 맡았습니다. 저는 버킷 경위입니다. 그분을 짓누르는 다른 모든 것에 더해, 그분은 자기 생각에 살인 혐의까지 뒤집어쓰고 있습니다. 제가 혼자 뒤쫓으면, 그분은 준남작 레스터 데드록 경이 제게 전한 바를 모르는 터라 절망에 내몰릴지도 모릅니다. 하지만 제가 어떤 젊은 아가씨와 함께, 그분이 애틋하게 여기는 한 젊은 아가씨의 인상과 들어맞는 분과 함께 뒤쫓으면—저는 아무것도 묻지 않고, 그 이상은 말하지 않겠습니다—그분은 저를 우호적인 사람으로 믿어줄 겁니다. 제가 그분을 따라잡아, 그 젊은 아가씨를 앞세워 그분을 붙잡을 수 있게 해주십시오. 그러면 그분이 살아 있는 한, 제가 그분을 구하고 설득해내겠습니다. 저 혼자서 따라잡으라 하시면—어려운 일이지요—최선을 다하겠습니다만, 그 최선이 어떨지는 장담 못 합니다. 시간이 흐릅니다. 한 시가 다 되어 갑니다. 한 시 종이 울리면 또 한 시간이 가는 것이고, 이제 그 한 시간은 백 파운드가 아니라 천 파운드짜리입니다.\"\n\n이 모든 것이 사실이고, 사안의 다급한 성격은 의심할 여지가 없다. 잔다이스 씨는 자기가 미스 서머슨에게 이야기하는 동안 그에게 여기 있어 달라고 청한다. 버킷 씨는 그러겠다고 하지만, 평소 원칙에 따라 그러지 않고, 대신 계단을 따라 올라가 그 사람을 시야에 둔다. 그렇게 그는 두 사람이 의논하는 동안 계단의 어둠 속에서 요리조리 몸을 숨기고 도사린 채 머문다. 얼마 안 가 잔다이스 씨가 내려와, 미스 서머슨이 곧 그와 함께하여 그가 가자는 곳 어디든 동행하도록 그의 보호 아래 몸을 맡기겠다고 일러준다. 만족한 버킷 씨는 크게 찬동을 표하며 문간에서 그녀가 오기를 기다린다.\n\n거기서 그는 마음속으로 높은 망루에 올라 멀리, 넓게 내다본다. 거리를 따라 살금살금 움직이는 외로운 인영을 그는 여럿 알아본다. 황야와 길 위에 나와 있고, 건초더미 밑에 누워 있는 외로운 인영도 여럿이다. 그러나 그가 찾는 인영은 그들 가운데 없다. 다리 후미진 곳에서 아래를 내려다보고 있는, 강물 높이까지 내려간 어둑한 자리에 있는 또 다른 외로운 이들도 그는 알아본다. 그리고 그 무엇보다 외로이, 물살에 떠밀려가는 검고 검은, 형체 없는 물체 하나가 빠져 죽어가는 손길로 그의 주의를 움켜쥔다.\n\n그녀는 어디 있는가? 살았든 죽었든, 그녀는 어디 있는가? 손수건을 접어 조심스레 갈무리하는 지금, 만약 그것이 마법의 힘으로 그녀가 그것을 주운 장소와, 그것으로 어린아이를 덮어주었던 그 오두막 근처의 밤 풍경을 그의 눈앞에 불러올 수 있다면, 그는 거기서 그녀를 알아볼 수 있을까? 벽돌 가마가 파리한 푸른 불꽃으로 타오르고, 벽돌을 빚어내는 초라한 오두막의 짚지붕이 바람에 흩날리고, 진흙과 물이 꽝꽝 얼어붙고, 비쩍 마른 눈먼 말이 온종일 돌리는 방아가 흡사 인간을 고문하는 기구처럼 보이는 그 황무지—이 버려지고 메마른 곳을 가로질러, 슬픈 세상을 홀로 지닌 채 눈발에 두들겨 맞고 바람에 떠밀리며, 모든 동행에게서 내쫓긴 듯한 외로운 인영 하나가 있다. 그것 또한 여인의 모습이다. 그러나 비참한 차림새이니, 그런 옷이 데드록 저택의 현관을 지나 그 큰 문을 나선 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n잠자리에 들어 잠이 들었는데 후견인이 제 방문을 두드리며 곧장 일어나달라고 했습니다. 무슨 일이냐고 여쭙고 까닭을 알아보려 서둘러 나가니, 후견인은 마음의 준비를 시키는 말을 한두 마디 건넨 뒤, 레스터 데드록 경의 댁에서 어떤 사실이 드러났다고 말했습니다. 어머니가 달아났다는 것, 만에 하나라도 찾아낼 수만 있다면 애정 어린 보호와 용서를 더없이 굳건히 다짐하라는 권한을 받은 사람이 지금 우리 집 문 앞에 와 있다는 것, 그리고 그의 간청이 통하지 않을 때 제 간청이라면 어머니의 마음을 돌릴 수 있으리라는 기대로 제가 그를 따라가주기를 바란다는 것이었습니다. 대략 이런 취지의 말을 알아듣긴 했으나, 저는 경악과 다급함과 비탄의 소용돌이에 휩싸여, 동요를 가라앉히려 안간힘을 다 썼는데도, 몇 시간이 지나고 나서야 비로소 온전히 제정신을 되찾은 듯했습니다.\n\n그래도 저는 찰리든 누구든 깨우지 않고 잽싸게 옷을 입고 몸을 둘러싼 뒤, 그 비밀을 맡은 사람인 버킷 씨에게로 내려갔습니다. 후견인은 저를 그에게 데려가며 이 사실을 일러주었고, 어쩌다 제 생각이 떠올랐는지도 설명해주었습니다. 버킷 씨는 후견인의 촛불 빛에 기대어 낮은 목소리로, 어머니가 탁자 위에 남겨두고 간 편지를 현관에서 제게 읽어주었습니다. 그리고 제가 잠에서 깨어난 지 십 분도 채 되지 않아, 저는 그의 곁에 앉아 거리를 빠르게 굴러가고 있었던 것 같습니다.\n\n그의 태도는 무척 날카로우면서도 사려 깊었으니, 자기가 묻고 싶은 몇 가지 질문에 제가 혼란 없이 대답할 수 있느냐에 많은 것이 달려 있을지도 모른다고 설명할 때 그랬습니다. 질문은 주로 이런 것이었습니다. 어머니(그는 데드록 마님이라고만 칭했습니다)와 많은 이야기를 나누었는지, 마지막으로 이야기한 것이 언제 어디서였는지, 어머니가 어떻게 제 손수건을 갖게 되었는지. 이 점들에 대해 제가 그를 흡족하게 해주자, 그는 특별히—시간을 두고 잘 생각해서—제가 아는 사람 가운데 어디에 있든, 막다른 궁지에 몰린 처지에서 어머니가 속마음을 털어놓을 만한 이가 조금이라도 있는지 곰곰이 생각해보라고 했습니다. 후견인 말고는 떠오르는 이가 없었습니다. 하지만 이윽고 저는 보이손 씨를 언급했습니다. 그는 예전에 기사도적인 태도로 어머니의 이름을 입에 올렸던 일, 그리고 후견인이 일러준 그가 어머니의 언니와 약혼했었다는 사실, 또 그가 자기도 모르는 사이에 어머니의 불행한 사연에 얽혀 있다는 사실과 연결되어 제 마음에 떠올랐습니다.\n\n이 대화를 나누는 동안 제 동행은 서로의 말을 더 잘 들으려고 마부를 멈춰 세웠습니다. 이제 그는 다시 가자고 마부에게 이르고는, 잠시 속으로 궁리한 끝에 어떻게 할지 마음을 정했다고 제게 말했습니다. 자기 계획이 무엇인지 기꺼이 알려주겠다고 했으나, 저는 그것을 이해할 만큼 머리가 맑지 않았습니다.\n\n숙소에서 그리 멀리 가지 않아 우리는 가스등으로 환히 밝혀진, 술집처럼 보이는 곳 앞의 골목길에 멈췄습니다. 버킷 씨는 저를 안으로 데리고 들어가 환하게 타오르는 난롯가의 안락의자에 앉혔습니다. 벽에 걸린 시계로 보니 이제 한 시가 지난 시각이었습니다. 더없이 말끔한 제복 차림으로 밤새 깨어 있던 사람들 같지 않은 경찰관 두 명이 책상에서 조용히 무언가를 쓰고 있었습니다. 지하 어딘가의 먼 문에서 두드리고 외치는 소리가 들려왔으나 아무도 거기에 관심을 두지 않았고, 그 소리만 빼면 그곳은 전체적으로 무척 고요해 보였습니다.\n\n버킷 씨가 부르더니 귓속말로 지시를 내린 또 다른 제복 차림의 사내가 밖으로 나갔습니다. 그러자 나머지 두 사람이 함께 상의했고, 그중 하나가 버킷 씨가 나직이 불러주는 대로 받아 적었습니다. 그들이 분주히 매달린 것은 어머니의 인상착의였으니, 버킷 씨가 다 되자 그것을 제게 가져와 속삭이듯 읽어주었기 때문입니다. 정말이지 무척 정확했습니다.\n\n그것을 꼼꼼히 들여다보던 두 번째 경관이 그것을 베껴 적고는, 또 다른 제복 차림의 사내를(바깥방에 여럿이 있었습니다) 불러들였고, 그 사내가 그것을 받아들고 가지고 나갔습니다. 이 모든 일이 한순간도 허비하지 않고 더없이 신속하게 이루어졌습니다. 그런데도 아무도 서두르는 기색이 없었습니다. 그 종이가 제 갈 길을 떠나 보내지자마자 두 경관은 다시 말끔하고 정성스럽게 무언가를 쓰던 아까의 조용한 일로 돌아갔습니다. 버킷 씨는 골똘히 생각하며 다가와 난롯불에 장화 밑창을 한쪽씩 차례로 데웠습니다.\n\n\"몸은 단단히 둘러쌌습니까, 서머슨 양?\" 그의 눈이 제 눈과 마주치자 물었습니다. \"젊은 아가씨가 밖에 나돌기엔 지독하게 매서운 밤이라서요.\"\n\n저는 날씨 따위는 개의치 않으며 따뜻하게 입었다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"긴 일이 될지도 모릅니다.\" 그가 한마디 했습니다. \"하지만 끝만 좋으면 되니, 마음 쓰지 마십시오, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"부디 좋게 끝나기를 하늘에 기도드립니다!\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n그가 위로하듯 고개를 끄덕였습니다. \"보세요, 무슨 일이 있어도 절대 안달하지 마십시오. 무슨 일이 닥치든 침착하고 평온하게 마음을 다잡으세요. 그래야 아가씨한테도 좋고, 저한테도 좋고, 데드록 마님한테도 좋고, 준남작 레스터 데드록 경한테도 좋습니다.\"\n\n그는 정말로 무척 친절하고 다정했고, 난롯불 앞에 서서 장화를 데우며 집게손가락으로 얼굴을 문지르는 그를 보고 있자니, 그의 명민함에 대한 믿음이 솟아 마음이 놓였습니다. 밖에서 말발굽 소리와 바퀴 소리가 들렸을 때는 아직 두 시 십오 분 전도 되지 않은 시각이었습니다. \"자, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"괜찮으시다면, 출발합시다!\"\n\n그가 제게 팔을 내밀었고, 두 경관이 정중히 인사하며 저를 배웅했습니다. 문 앞에는 마부 한 명과 역마(驛馬)가 딸린 페이튼인지 바루슈인지 하는 마차가 있었습니다. 버킷 씨는 저를 마차에 태우고 자기는 마부석에 자리를 잡았습니다. 그가 이 마차를 가져오라고 보냈던 제복 차림의 사내가 그의 청에 따라 그에게 어둑한 등을 건넸고, 마부에게 몇 가지 지시를 내린 뒤, 우리는 덜컹거리며 떠났습니다.\n\n저는 이것이 꿈이 아니라고 도무지 확신할 수가 없었습니다. 우리는 어찌나 빠르게 미로 같은 거리를 덜컹거리며 달렸던지, 강을 건너고 또 건넜다는 것 말고는 우리가 어디 있는지 곧 종잡을 수 없게 되었습니다. 그래도 부두와 선창이 군데군데 박히고 높이 쌓인 창고들과 도개교들과 배의 돛대들이 뒤섞인, 좁은 길들이 얽힌 강가의 낮고 빽빽한 동네를 여전히 가로지르고 있는 듯했습니다. 마침내 우리는 강에서 불어오는 바람이 휩쓸고 올라와도 깨끗해지지 않는, 진창에 미끄러운 작은 골목 모퉁이에 멈췄습니다. 등불 빛에 비친 제 동행은 경찰과 선원이 뒤섞인 듯 보이는 여러 사내들과 의논하고 있었습니다. 그들이 서 있는 허물어져가는 벽에는 벽보가 한 장 붙어 있었는데, 저는 \"익사체 발견\"이라는 글자를 알아볼 수 있었습니다. 이것과 갈고리에 관한 어떤 문구가, 우리가 그곳을 찾아온 까닭에 어렴풋이 드리워진 끔찍한 의심으로 저를 사로잡았습니다.\n\n수색을 어렵게 만들거나, 그 희망을 꺾거나, 그 지체를 늘리려고 제 감정에 휘둘려 그곳에 와 있는 게 아니라는 사실을 굳이 스스로에게 일깨울 필요는 없었습니다. 저는 잠자코 있었지만, 그 끔찍한 곳에서 제가 겪은 고통은 결코 잊을 수 없습니다. 그리고 그것은 여전히 악몽의 공포 같았습니다. 아직 어둡고 진흙투성이인 한 사내가, 길쭉하게 부풀어 물에 젖은 장화를 신고 그와 똑같은 모자를 쓴 채 배에서 불려 나와 버킷 씨와 귓속말을 나누었고, 버킷 씨는 그와 함께 미끄러운 계단을 따라 내려갔습니다—마치 그가 보여줄 어떤 은밀한 것을 살피러 가는 듯이. 그들은 무언가 젖은 것을 뒤집어본 뒤 코트에 손을 닦으며 돌아왔습니다. 하지만 다행히도 그것은 제가 두려워하던 것이 아니었습니다!\n\n좀 더 의논을 나눈 뒤, 버킷 씨는(누구나 그를 알고 그에게 머리를 숙이는 듯했습니다) 다른 이들과 함께 어떤 문 안으로 들어가며 저를 마차에 남겨두었고, 마부는 몸을 녹이려고 말 옆을 오락가락 걸었습니다. 소리로 짐작건대 밀물이 들어오고 있었고, 골목 끝에서 제 쪽으로 자그맣게 밀려들며 물결이 부서지는 소리가 들렸습니다. 길어야 십오 분, 어쩌면 그보다 짧았을 그 시간 동안, 실제로는 한 번도 그러지 않았는데도 저는 수백 번이나 물결이 그렇게 밀려든다고 생각했습니다. 그리고 그 물결이 어머니를 말발굽 앞으로 던져놓을 거라는 생각이 몸서리치며 저를 훑고 지나갔습니다.\n\n버킷 씨가 다시 나와 다른 이들에게 빈틈없이 경계하라고 당부하고는, 등불을 어둡게 가린 뒤 다시 한번 자리에 앉았습니다. \"우리가 여기까지 내려온 일로 놀라지 마십시오, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 제 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했습니다. \"저는 그저 만사를 빈틈없이 준비해두고, 직접 챙겨보아 그게 제대로 진행되고 있음을 확인하고 싶을 뿐입니다. 가자, 이 사람아!\"\n\n우리는 왔던 길을 되짚어가는 듯했습니다. 어지러운 마음에 어떤 특정한 사물을 눈여겨봐서가 아니라, 거리의 전반적인 분위기로 미루어 그렇게 판단했습니다. 우리는 또 다른 사무소인지 초소인지에 잠깐 들렀다가 다시 강을 건넜습니다. 이 시간 내내, 그리고 수색이 이어지는 내내, 마부석에 몸을 둘러싸고 앉은 제 동행은 단 한순간도 경계를 늦추지 않았습니다. 그런데 다리를 건널 때 그는, 가능하다면, 전보다 더 바짝 긴장하는 것 같았습니다. 그는 일어서서 난간 너머를 살폈고, 우리 곁을 휙 스쳐간 어슴푸레한 여자의 모습을 쫓아 마차에서 내려 되돌아갔으며, 시커멓고 깊은 물웅덩이를 들여다볼 때는 제 가슴이 철렁 내려앉게 하는 표정을 지었습니다. 강은 무서운 모습이었으니, 어찌나 어둡게 깔리고 은밀한지, 낮고 평평한 강기슭 사이로 어찌나 빠르게 흘러 빠져나가는지, 실체든 그림자든 흐릿하고 끔찍한 형상들로 어찌나 묵직한지, 죽음 같고 신비스러웠습니다. 그 뒤로 저는 햇빛 아래서도 달빛 아래서도 그 강을 여러 번 보았으나, 그날 밤 여정의 인상에서 결코 벗어난 적이 없습니다. 제 기억 속에서 다리 위의 불빛들은 늘 흐릿하게 타오르고, 우리가 스쳐가는 집 없는 여인 주위로 살을 에는 바람이 소용돌이치고, 단조로운 바퀴들이 빙빙 굴러가며, 마차 등불의 빛이 되비쳐 제게 창백하게 비쳐듭니다—그 두려운 물에서 솟아오르는 얼굴 하나가.\n\n텅 빈 거리를 덜컹덜컹 끝없이 달려, 우리는 마침내 포장도로에서 어둡고 매끄러운 길로 접어들어 집들을 뒤로하기 시작했습니다. 얼마 지나 저는 세인트올번스로 가는 낯익은 길임을 알아보았습니다. 바닛에서는 새 말이 우리를 기다리고 있어, 말을 바꾸어 다시 갔습니다. 정말이지 무척 추웠고, 탁 트인 들판은 눈으로 하얗게 덮여 있었으나, 그때는 눈이 내리지 않았습니다.\n\n\"이 길은 아가씨한테 오랜 벗이로군요, 서머슨 양.\" 버킷 씨가 명랑하게 말했습니다.\n\n\"네.\" 제가 대답했습니다. \"무슨 소식이라도 모으셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아직 온전히 믿을 만한 건 없습니다.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 아직 이른 때이니까요.\"\n\n그는 불빛이 켜진 늦은 시각이나 이른 시각의 술집마다 다 들어가보았고(그 무렵에는 소몰이꾼들이 그 길을 무척 자주 다녀서 술집이 적지 않았습니다), 통행료 징수원들과 이야기하려고 마차에서 내리곤 했습니다. 그가 술을 주문하고, 돈을 짤랑거리고, 어디서나 싹싹하고 흥겹게 구는 소리를 저는 들었습니다. 하지만 다시 마부석에 자리를 잡을 때마다 그의 얼굴은 빈틈없이 한결같은 표정으로 되돌아갔고, 그는 늘 똑같이 일하는 어조로 마부에게 말했습니다. \"가자, 이 사람아!\"\n\n이렇게 자주 멈춰 선 탓에, 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이가 되어서도 우리는 아직 세인트올번스에서 몇 마일 못 미친 지점에 있었는데, 그때 그가 그런 집들 가운데 하나에서 나와 제게 차 한 잔을 건넸습니다.\n\n\"드세요, 서머슨 양, 몸에 좋을 겁니다. 이제 점점 본래의 아가씨로 돌아오고 있지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n저는 고맙다고 인사하며 그러기를 바란다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"처음에는 그야말로 얼이 빠지셨었지요.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"그러니, 세상에, 무리도 아니지요! 큰 소리로 말하지 마십시오, 아가씨. 다 괜찮습니다. 그분은 앞쪽에 계십니다.\"\n\n제가 무슨 기쁨에 찬 탄성을 질렀는지, 아니면 지르려 했는지 모르겠으나, 그가 손가락을 세우자 저는 입을 다물었습니다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁 여덟 시인가 아홉 시쯤 걸어서 이곳을 지나갔습니다. 하이게이트 너머 아치형 통행료 징수소에서 처음 그분 소식을 들었는데, 썩 확실하지는 않았습니다. 끊겼다 이어졌다 하면서 줄곧 자취를 좇았지요. 한 군데서는 자취를 잡았다가 또 한 군데서는 놓쳤지만, 지금은 우리보다 앞에, 무사히 계십니다. 이 찻잔과 받침을 받게나, 마부. 자, 자네가 버터 장사로 자라지 않았다면, 정신 차리고 다른 손으로 반 크라운을 받아챌 수 있는지 보게. 하나, 둘, 셋, 옜네! 자, 이 사람아, 한번 전속력으로 달려보게!\"\n\n우리는 곧 세인트올번스에 들어서서 날이 밝기 조금 전에 내렸는데, 그제야 저는 비로소 밤사이의 일들을 정리하고 이해하며, 그것이 꿈이 아니었음을 정말로 믿기 시작했습니다. 역참에 마차를 두고 새 말을 준비해두라고 일러둔 뒤, 제 동행은 제게 팔을 내밀었고, 우리는 집을 향해 걸어갔습니다.\n\n\"보세요, 여기가 아가씨의 본거지이니까요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 한마디 했습니다. \"그 인상착의에 들어맞는 낯선 사람이 아가씨를 찾아왔는지, 아니면 잔다이스 씨를 찾아왔는지 알고 싶습니다. 그럴 거라고 크게 기대하지는 않습니다만, 그럴 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n언덕을 오르며 그는 날카로운 눈으로 사방을 둘러보았고—이제 날이 밝아오고 있었습니다—제가 어느 날 밤 어린 하녀와 가엾은 조와 함께 이 언덕을 내려갔던 일을 일깨워주었으니, 그는 조를 터피라고 불렀습니다. 그 일을 저는 기억할 만한 까닭이 있었습니다.\n\n저는 그가 그것을 어떻게 알았는지 의아했습니다.\n\n\"저기 바로 저쯤에서, 길에서 한 사내를 지나치셨지요, 기억나시지요.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n네, 그것도 무척 잘 기억하고 있었습니다.\n\n\"그게 저였습니다.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n제가 놀라는 것을 보고 그가 말을 이었습니다. \"그날 오후에 저는 그 아이를 살피러 이륜마차를 몰고 내려왔습니다. 아가씨가 직접 그 아이를 살피러 나오셨을 때 제 바퀴 소리를 들으셨을지도 모릅니다. 제가 말을 끌고 언덕을 내려갈 때 아가씨와 어린 하녀가 올라가는 걸 알아챘으니까요. 마을에서 그 아이에 대해 한두 가지 물어보고, 저는 곧 그 아이가 어떤 무리와 어울리는지 듣고서, 그 아이를 찾으러 벽돌 굽는 들판으로 들어서던 참에, 아가씨가 그 아이를 이리로 데려오는 것을 보았던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 아이가 무슨 죄라도 지었던가요?\" 제가 물었습니다.\n\n\"그 아이한테 씌워진 죄는 없었습니다.\" 버킷 씨가 태연히 모자를 벗으며 말했습니다. \"하지만 그리 깔끔한 처지는 아니었던 것 같습니다. 아니지요. 제가 그 아이를 원한 건 바로 이 데드록 마님 일을 조용히 묻어두는 것과 관련이 있었습니다. 그 아이가 고인이 된 토킹혼 씨한테서 어떤 사소한 우연한 심부름 값을 받은 일에 대해, 달갑잖을 만큼 입을 함부로 놀려대고 있었거든요. 어떤 값을 치르더라도 그 아이가 그런 장난을 치게 둘 수는 없었습니다. 그래서 그 아이한테 런던을 떠나라고 경고해두었다가, 그가 정말로 떠났으니 이제 거기서 멀리 떨어져 지내고, 더 멀리 가고, 다시 돌아오다가 제 눈에 띄지 않도록 정신을 바짝 차리라고 경고하려고 하루 오후를 들였던 겁니다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것!\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"가엾기 짝이 없었지요.\" 버킷 씨가 동의했습니다. \"골칫거리이기도 했고, 런던이든 어디든 떠나 있는 게 잘된 일이었지요. 단언컨대, 그 아이가 아가씨네 집에 거두어졌다는 걸 알았을 때 저는 그야말로 자빠질 뻔했습니다.\"\n\n저는 왜냐고 물었습니다. \"왜냐고요, 아가씨?\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"그렇게 되니 당연히 그 아이 혀끝에 끝이 없어졌으니까요. 그 아이는 한 자 반쯤 되는 혀를 달고, 남은 토막까지 더해서 태어난 거나 다름없었습니다.\"\n\n지금이야 이 대화를 기억하지만, 그때는 머릿속이 어지러웠고, 제 주의력이라곤 그가 저를 다른 데로 돌리려고 이런 자질구레한 이야기를 늘어놓는다는 것을 알아챌 정도밖에 되지 않았습니다. 분명히 같은 친절한 의도로, 그는 우리가 눈앞에 둔 단 하나의 목표에 얼굴을 골몰히 두면서도, 제게 자주 별것 아닌 이야기를 건넸습니다. 정원 문을 들어설 때까지도 그는 여전히 이 화제를 이어갔습니다.\n\n\"아!\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"다 왔군요, 한적하니 참 좋은 곳입니다. 저 우아하게 피어오르는 연기로 알려졌다던 〈딱따구리 두드리네〉 노래 속 시골집을 떠올리게 하는군요. 부엌 불을 일찍 지폈으니, 좋은 하인들이라는 표시지요. 하지만 하인들에 관해 늘 조심해야 할 건 누가 그들을 찾아오느냐 하는 겁니다. 그걸 모르면 그들이 무슨 꿍꿍이인지 절대 알 수 없으니까요. 그리고 또 한 가지, 아가씨. 부엌문 뒤에서 젊은 사내를 발견하거든, 불법한 목적으로 주택 안에 숨어든 혐의로 그 젊은 사내를 잡아두십시오.\"\n\n이제 우리는 집 앞에 이르렀습니다. 그는 창문으로 눈을 들기 전에, 자갈밭에 발자국이 있는지 주의 깊게 찬찬히 살폈습니다.\n\n\"여기 손님으로 오면 저 늙은 젊은 신사분을 늘 같은 방에 묵게 하십니까, 서머슨 양?\" 그가 스킴폴 씨가 늘 쓰는 방을 흘끔 보며 물었습니다.\n\n\"스킴폴 씨를 아세요!\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그분 이름이 뭐라고 하셨지요?\" 버킷 씨가 귀를 기울이며 되물었습니다. \"스킴폴이라고요? 저분 이름이 뭘까 자주 궁금했었습니다. 스킴폴. 존은 아니고, 그렇다고 제이콥도 아닐 텐데?\"\n\n\"해럴드예요.\" 제가 일러주었습니다.\n\n\"해럴드. 그렇군요. 해럴드는 별난 새이지요.\" 버킷 씨가 의미심장한 표정으로 저를 바라보며 말했습니다.\n\n\"독특한 분이지요.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"돈이라곤 도무지 모르고요.\" 버킷 씨가 한마디 했습니다. \"그러면서도 받기는 받지만요!\"\n\n저는 저도 모르게 버킷 씨가 그분을 안다는 걸 알아챘다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"그럼, 자, 말씀드리지요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"한 가지 일에 너무 줄곧 골몰하지 않는 편이 마음에 한결 나을 테니, 기분 전환 삼아 들려드리지요. 터피가 어디 있는지 제게 가리켜준 게 바로 그분입니다. 그날 밤 저는 다른 수가 없으면 그저 문으로 가서 터피를 내달라고 할 작정이었는데, 무슨 수가 있을지 우선 한두 번 움직여보려고, 그림자가 보이는 저 창문에 자갈 한 알을 던져 올렸습니다. 해럴드가 그걸 열자마자 그를 한번 보고는, 옳거니, 당신이 내 사람이로군, 하고 생각했지요. 그래서 가족들이 잠자리에 든 뒤에 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않다는 둥, 자비로운 젊은 아가씨들이 부랑자를 거두는 건 안타까운 일이라는 둥 그를 슬슬 구슬렸습니다. 그러고 나서 그의 됨됨이를 어지간히 파악하고는, 소란이나 말썽 없이 이 집에서 터피를 데려갈 수만 있다면 5파운드짜리 지폐를 잘 쓰는 셈치겠다고 했지요. 그러자 그가 더없이 쾌활하게 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말하더군요. '내게 5파운드짜리 지폐 얘기를 해봐야 소용없네, 이 사람아. 그런 일에 나는 그저 어린아이라 돈이라곤 도무지 모르니까.' 물론 저는 그가 그렇게 태평하게 구는 게 무슨 뜻인지 알아챘지요. 이제 그가 영락없이 내 사람이라고 확신하고서, 저는 지폐를 작은 돌멩이에 둘둘 말아 그에게 던져 올렸습니다. 그랬더니! 그가 웃고 환히 빛나며, 더없이 천진한 얼굴로 말하더군요. '하지만 난 이런 것들의 값어치를 몰라. 이걸 가지고 대체 뭘 하란 말인가?' '쓰십시오, 선생님.' 제가 말했지요. '하지만 난 속아 넘어갈 걸세.' 그가 말하더군요. '거스름돈을 제대로 안 줄 테고, 잃어버릴 테고, 내게는 아무 쓸모가 없어.' 세상에, 그가 그러면서 짓던 그런 얼굴은 본 적이 없을 겁니다! 물론 그는 제게 터피가 어디 있는지 일러주었고, 저는 그를 찾아냈습니다.\"\n\n저는 이것이 후견인에 대한 스킴폴 씨의 무척 배신적인 처사이며, 그의 어린아이 같은 천진함의 통상적인 한도를 넘어선 일이라고 여겼습니다.\n\n\"한도라고요, 아가씨?\" 버킷 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"한도라? 자, 서머슨 양, 아가씨가 행복하게 결혼해서 가족을 거느리게 되었을 때 아가씨 남편한테 쓸모 있을 충고 한마디 드리지요. 돈에 관한 한 자기는 더없이 순진하다고 누가 말하거든, 자기 돈을 단단히 챙기십시오. 그런 자들은 할 수만 있으면 영락없이 그 돈을 가로채니까요. 누가 '세상일에 나는 어린아이라네' 하고 떠벌리거든, 그 사람은 그저 책임지지 않으려고 발뺌하는 것이며, 아가씨가 그 사람의 정체를 꿰뚫었다고 여기십시오. 그 정체는 바로 '제 잇속 일등'입니다. 자, 저야 시 짓는 사람은 아닙니다, 좌중을 돌며 노래 한 곡 뽑을 때 말고는요. 하지만 저는 실용적인 사람이고, 이게 제 경험입니다. 이 법칙도 그렇고요. 한 가지에서 들쭉날쭉하는 자는 만사에 들쭉날쭉합니다. 이게 어긋나는 걸 본 적이 없습니다. 아가씨도 마찬가지일 겁니다. 누구라도 마찬가지일 거고요. 방심하는 이들을 향한 이 경고와 함께, 아가씨, 실례를 무릅쓰고 이 종을 한번 당기고는, 다시 우리 일로 돌아가겠습니다.\"\n\n그 일이 제 마음에서 한순간도 떠나지 않았듯이, 그의 마음에서도, 그의 얼굴에서도 한순간도 떠난 적이 없었던 것 같습니다. 그날 아침 그 시각에, 아무 기별도 없이, 그런 사람과 함께 나타난 저를 보고 온 집안이 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 게다가 제 질문에도 그들의 놀라움은 가시지 않았습니다. 하지만 아무도 거기 오지 않았다고 했습니다. 이것이 사실임은 의심할 여지가 없었습니다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 서머슨 양.\" 제 동행이 말했습니다. \"그 벽돌공들이 있다는 오두막으로 한시바삐 가야겠습니다. 거기서의 질문은 거의 다 아가씨께 맡기지요, 친절히 맡아주신다면요. 가장 자연스러운 방식이 가장 좋은 방식이고, 가장 자연스러운 방식은 곧 아가씨 자신의 방식이니까요.\"\n\n우리는 곧장 다시 길을 나섰습니다. 오두막에 다다라보니 문이 닫혀 있고 버려진 듯했으나, 저를 알아보는 이웃 한 사람이, 제가 안에서 누구든 불러보려고 애쓰는 동안 나와서 일러주기를, 그 두 여자와 남편들은 이제 다른 집에서 함께 산다고 했습니다. 그 집은 가마들이 있고 긴 벽돌 줄이 마르고 있는 땅뙈기의 가장자리에 헐겁게 쌓은 거친 벽돌로 지어진 것이었습니다. 우리는 지체 없이 그곳으로 갔는데, 그곳은 몇백 야드 안에 있었습니다. 문이 빠끔히 열려 있어 저는 그것을 밀어 열었습니다.\n\n안에는 세 사람만이 아침을 먹으며 앉아 있었고, 아이는 구석 침대에 잠들어 누워 있었습니다. 자리에 없는 사람은 죽은 아이의 어머니인 제니였습니다. 다른 여자가 저를 보고 일어섰고, 두 사내는 여느 때처럼 뚱하고 말이 없었으나, 저마다 못마땅한 듯 까닥하며 알은체를 했습니다. 버킷 씨가 저를 따라 들어서자 그들 사이에 눈빛이 오갔고, 저는 그 여자가 분명 그를 안다는 것을 보고 놀랐습니다.\n\n물론 저는 들어가도 되겠느냐고 청했었습니다. 리즈가(제가 아는 그녀의 유일한 이름이었습니다) 자기 의자를 제게 내주려고 일어섰으나, 저는 난롯가의 걸상에 앉았고, 버킷 씨는 침대틀 한 귀퉁이에 앉았습니다. 이제 말을 해야 하는 데다 낯선 사람들 사이에 있게 되자, 저는 다급하고 어질어질해지는 것을 느꼈습니다. 입을 떼기가 무척 어려웠고, 저는 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았습니다.\n\n\"리즈.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"저는 어떤 부인을 찾으러 밤새 눈을 헤치고 먼 길을 왔어요—\"\n\n\"여기 다녀가셨던 분 말입니다.\" 버킷 씨가 침착하고 누그러뜨리는 얼굴로 무리 전체를 향해 끼어들었습니다. \"이 아가씨가 말하는 게 그 부인입니다. 어젯밤 여기 다녀간 그 부인 말이오.\"\n\n\"그리고 여기 누가 있었다고 누가 댁한테 말하던가?\" 제니의 남편이 물었으니, 그는 귀를 기울이느라 먹던 것을 뚱하게 멈추었다가 이제 그를 눈으로 가늠하고 있었습니다.\n\n\"마이클 잭슨이라는 사람인데, 자개단추가 두 줄 달린 파란 골덴 조끼를 입었지요.\" 버킷 씨가 즉시 대답했습니다.\n\n\"그자가 누구든, 제 일에나 신경 쓰는 게 좋을 거요.\" 사내가 으르렁거렸습니다.\n\n\"실직했다더군요.\" 버킷 씨가 마이클 잭슨을 두둔하듯 말했습니다. \"그래서 그렇게 입을 놀리는 거지요.\"\n\n여자는 다시 의자에 앉지 않고, 부서진 등받이에 손을 얹은 채 머뭇거리며 서서 저를 바라보았습니다. 저는 그녀가 감히 그럴 수만 있다면 제게 따로 이야기를 했으리라고 생각했습니다. 그녀가 여전히 이렇게 망설이는 자세로 서 있는데, 한 손에는 빵과 비계 덩어리를, 다른 손에는 접칼을 들고 먹던 그녀의 남편이 칼자루로 식탁을 세게 내리치고는, 욕설을 내뱉으며 어쨌든 네 일에나 신경 쓰고 앉으라고 그녀에게 말했습니다.\n\n\"제니를 꼭 좀 보고 싶었어요.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"이 부인에 대해 아는 걸 죄다 제게 일러주었을 게 분명하니까요. 저는 그분을 따라잡고 싶은 마음이 정말 간절하답니다—얼마나 간절한지 모르실 거예요. 제니가 곧 올까요? 어디 있나요?\"\n\n여자는 무척 대답하고 싶어 했으나, 사내가 또 한 번 욕설을 내뱉으며 묵직한 장화로 대놓고 그녀의 발을 걷어찼습니다. 그는 무어라 말할지를 제니의 남편에게 넘겼고, 고집스러운 침묵 끝에 후자가 텁수룩한 머리를 제 쪽으로 돌렸습니다.\n\n\"전에도 제가 말한 걸로 아는데요, 아가씨, 저는 신사 양반들이 제 집에 들어오는 게 달갑지 않습니다. 저는 그분들 집을 가만 놔두는데, 그분들은 제 집을 가만 못 놔두니 희한한 노릇이지요. 제가 그분들을 찾아가기라도 한다면 어지간히 야단법석이 날 텐데 말입니다. 하기야, 아가씨한테는 다른 몇몇한테만큼 불평은 없으니, 점잖게 대답해드리는 데는 동의하겠습니다. 다만, 오소리처럼 끌려 나올 생각은 없다는 걸 미리 일러둡니다. 제니가 곧 오겠냐고요? 안 옵니다. 어디 있냐고요? 런던으로 올라갔습니다.\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 갔나요?\" 제가 물었습니다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 갔냐고요? 아! 어젯밤에 갔습니다.\" 그가 뚱하게 머리를 홱 젖히며 대답했습니다.\n\n\"그런데 그 부인이 왔을 때 제니가 여기 있었나요? 그리고 그 부인이 제니한테 뭐라고 했나요? 그 부인은 어디로 갔나요? 부디 친절을 베풀어 제게 일러주시기를 빌고 또 청해요.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"저는 그걸 알고 싶어 몹시 애가 타니까요.\"\n\n\"제 주인 양반이 말하게 해주신다면, 해될 말은 한마디도 안 하고—\" 여자가 머뭇머뭇 말을 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"네 주인은,\" 그녀의 남편이 천천히 힘주어 저주를 중얼거리며 말했습니다. \"네가 상관없는 일에 끼어들면 네 목을 부러뜨릴 거다.\"\n\n또 한 번 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 자리에 없는 여자의 남편이 다시 제 쪽으로 돌아서서, 여느 때처럼 투덜대며 마지못해 제게 대답했습니다.\n\n\"그 부인이 왔을 때 제니가 여기 있었냐고요? 네, 그 부인이 왔을 때 여기 있었습니다. 그 부인이 제니한테 뭐라고 했냐고요? 좋습니다, 그 부인이 뭐라고 했는지 일러드리지요. 그분이 말하더군요. '댁을 찾아왔던 젊은 아가씨에 대해 한번 이야기하러 왔던 나를 기억하나요? 그 아가씨가 두고 간 손수건 값으로 후한 것을 댁한테 준 나를 기억하나요?' 아, 그녀는 기억하더군요. 우리 모두 기억했고요. 그래, 그럼, 그 젊은 아가씨가 지금 저 위 집에 있냐고요? 아니요, 지금 저 위 집에 없었습니다. 그래, 그럼, 들어보세요. 그 부인은, 우리야 이상하다 싶었지만, 혼자 길을 가는 중이었는데, 댁이 앉아 있는 거기서 한 시간쯤 쉬어가도 되겠냐고요. 네, 되고말고요, 그래서 그렇게 했습니다. 그러고는 떠났지요—열한 시 이십 분이었을 수도 있고, 열두 시 이십 분이었을 수도 있습니다. 여기는 시간을 알 시계도 없고, 벽시계도 없으니까요. 어디로 갔냐고요? 어디로 갔는지 모릅니다. 그분은 한쪽 길로 갔고, 제니는 다른 쪽 길로 갔지요. 하나는 곧장 런던으로 갔고, 다른 하나는 곧장 그 반대로 갔습니다. 그게 다입니다. 이 사람한테 물어보세요. 다 듣고 다 봤으니까요. 이 사람이 압니다.\"\n\n다른 사내가 되풀이했습니다. \"그게 다요.\"\n\n\"그 부인이 울던가요?\" 제가 물었습니다.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 첫 번째 사내가 대답했습니다. \"신발은 엉망이고 옷도 엉망이었지만, 울지는—내가 본 바로는, 아니었습니다.\"\n\n여자는 팔짱을 끼고 눈을 바닥에 둔 채 앉아 있었습니다. 그녀의 남편은 그녀를 마주 보도록 자리를 조금 틀고는, 그녀가 말을 듣지 않으면 위협을 실행에 옮길 채비라도 된 듯이 망치 같은 손을 식탁에 얹어두고 있었습니다.\n\n\"실례가 안 된다면, 부인께 그 부인이 어떤 모습이었는지 여쭙고 싶은데요.\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"자, 그럼!\" 그가 그녀에게 퉁명스레 외쳤습니다. \"이 아가씨가 하는 말 들었지. 짧게 끊어서 일러줘.\"\n\n\"안 좋았어요.\" 여자가 대답했습니다. \"창백하고 기진맥진하셨지요. 아주 안 좋았어요.\"\n\n\"말은 많이 했나요?\"\n\n\"많지는 않았지만, 목소리가 쉬어 있었어요.\"\n\n그녀는 줄곧 남편에게서 허락을 구하듯 그를 쳐다보며 대답했습니다.\n\n\"기력이 없었나요?\" 제가 말했습니다. \"여기서 뭘 먹거나 마셨나요?\"\n\n\"말해!\" 남편이 그녀의 눈길에 대답해 말했습니다. \"일러주고 짧게 끊어.\"\n\n\"물을 조금 드셨어요, 아가씨. 제니가 빵하고 차를 좀 가져다드렸고요. 하지만 거의 입을 안 대셨어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 부인이 여기서 떠날 때는—\" 제가 말을 이어가는데, 제니의 남편이 참지 못하고 제 말을 가로챘습니다.\n\n\"여기서 떠날 때, 그분은 큰길로 곧장 북쪽으로 갔습니다. 제 말이 못 미덥거든 길에서 물어보세요, 그렇지 않은지. 자, 이게 끝입니다. 그게 다라고요.\"\n\n저는 제 동행을 흘끔 보았는데, 그가 이미 일어서서 떠날 채비를 한 것을 알고, 그들이 일러준 것에 고맙다고 인사하고 자리를 떴습니다. 그가 나갈 때 여자가 버킷 씨를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 그도 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보았습니다.\n\n\"자, 서머슨 양.\" 우리가 빠르게 걸어 멀어지는데 그가 제게 말했습니다. \"저자들이 마님의 시계를 가지고 있습니다. 그건 확실한 사실입니다.\"\n\n\"보셨나요?\" 제가 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"본 거나 다름없지요.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"안 그러면 왜 그자가 '이십 분'이 어쩌고 하면서, 시간을 알 시계가 없다는 둥 떠들었겠습니까? 이십 분이라니! 그자는 보통 시간을 그렇게 잘게 자르지 않습니다. 그자가 반 시간 단위까지 따진다면 그게 고작이지요. 자, 보세요, 마님이 그자한테 그 시계를 주었거나, 아니면 그자가 빼앗은 겁니다. 제 생각엔 마님이 준 것 같습니다. 자, 그럼 마님이 무엇 때문에 그걸 주었을까요? 무엇 때문에 줬을까요?\"\n\n서둘러 가는 동안 그는 이 질문을 스스로에게 여러 번 되풀이했는데, 마음속에 떠오르는 여러 답들 사이에서 저울질하는 듯했습니다.\n\n\"시간을 낼 수만 있다면,\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"—이 사건에서 낼 수 없는 유일한 게 바로 시간이지만요—저 여자한테서 알아낼 수도 있을 겁니다. 하지만 지금 상황에서 믿고 맡기기엔 너무 미덥잖은 기회지요. 저들은 그 여자를 바짝 감시하는 데 능하고, 머리부터 발끝까지 두들겨 맞고 걷어차이고 흉터투성이에 멍투성이인 저 여자 같은 가엾은 것이, 그렇게 자기를 학대하는 남편 편을 무슨 일이 있어도 들 거라는 건 바보라도 압니다. 감춰둔 게 있어요. 다른 여자를 만나지 못한 게 안타깝군요.\"\n\n저는 그것을 몹시 안타까워했으니, 그녀는 무척 고마워했었고, 제 어떤 간청에도 거역하지 않았으리라고 확신했기 때문입니다.\n\n\"이럴 수도 있습니다, 서머슨 양.\" 버킷 씨가 그것을 곰곰이 따지며 말했습니다. \"마님이 아가씨한테 전할 말을 들려서 그 여자를 런던으로 올려보냈고, 남편이 그녀를 보내주는 대가로 시계를 챙긴 것일 수도 있지요. 제 마음에 흡족할 만큼 죄다 명확히 드러나지는 않지만, 그럴 법은 합니다. 자, 저는 준남작 레스터 데드록 경의 돈을 저런 거친 자들한테 뿌리는 게 달갑지 않고, 지금으로선 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있는지도 모르겠습니다. 아니요! 지금까지로 보면 우리가 갈 길은, 서머슨 양, 앞으로—곧장 앞으로—그리고 만사를 조용히 하는 겁니다!\"\n\n우리는 후견인에게 급히 쪽지를 보내려고 다시 한번 집에 들렀다가, 마차를 두고 온 곳으로 서둘러 돌아갔습니다. 우리가 오는 것이 보이자마자 말이 끌려 나왔고, 우리는 몇 분 만에 다시 길에 올랐습니다.\n\n날이 밝을 무렵부터 눈이 내리기 시작했는데, 이제는 거세게 쏟아졌습니다. 낮의 어둠과 쏟아지는 눈의 자욱함으로 공기가 어찌나 빽빽한지, 어느 쪽으로도 아주 조금밖에 보이지 않았습니다. 몹시 추웠는데도 눈은 부분적으로만 얼어, 말발굽에 짓이겨지며—작은 조개껍데기가 깔린 바닷가 같은 소리를 내며—진창과 물이 되었습니다. 말들은 이따금 1마일을 내리 미끄러지고 허우적댔고, 우리는 말을 쉬게 하려고 멈춰 서야 했습니다. 한 마리는 이 첫 구간에서 세 번이나 넘어졌고, 어찌나 떨고 흔들리는지 마부가 결국 안장에서 내려 끌고 가야 했습니다.\n\n저는 아무것도 먹을 수 없었고 잠도 잘 수 없었으며, 그런 지체와 우리가 가는 더딘 속도에 어찌나 초조해졌던지, 마차에서 내려 걷고 싶은 터무니없는 마음이 일었습니다. 하지만 제 동행의 더 나은 분별에 따라 저는 그대로 머물렀습니다. 이 시간 내내 그는 자기가 하는 일에서 어떤 즐거움을 느끼며 생기를 잃지 않은 채, 우리가 다다르는 집마다 오르내리며, 한 번도 본 적 없는 사람들을 오랜 친지인 양 대했고, 보이는 불마다 뛰어들어 몸을 녹였으며, 술집과 선술집마다 이야기하고 마시고 악수하면서, 짐마차꾼이며 수레바퀴장이며 대장장이며 통행료 징수원과 누구하고나 살갑게 굴었지만, 한 번도 시간을 허비하는 법이 없었고, 늘 빈틈없이 한결같은 얼굴과 일하는 듯한 \"가자, 이 사람아!\"와 함께 다시 마부석에 올랐습니다.\n\n다음번에 말을 바꿀 때, 그는 마구간 마당에서 나왔는데, 젖은 눈이 그에게 더께처럼 엉겨 붙었다가 떨어지고 있었고—세인트올번스를 떠난 뒤로 자주 그랬듯이 젖은 무릎까지 눈을 철벅철벅 으스러뜨리며 헤치고 다녔습니다—그가 마차 옆에서 제게 말했습니다.\n\n\"기운 내십시오. 그분이 여기까지 오신 건 분명 사실입니다, 서머슨 양. 이제 옷차림에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없고, 그 옷차림이 여기서 목격되었습니다.\"\n\n\"여전히 걸어서요?\" 제가 말했습니다.\n\n\"여전히 걸어서요. 아가씨가 말씀하신 그 신사분이 그분이 향하는 지점인 것 같긴 합니다만, 그래도 그분이 마님 자신의 고장 쪽에 사는 게 영 마음에 걸립니다.\"\n\n\"제가 아는 게 너무 없어서요.\" 제가 말했습니다. \"이 근처에 제가 들어본 적 없는 다른 누군가가 있을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 합니다. 하지만 무슨 일이 있어도 울지는 마십시오, 아가씨. 그리고 어쩔 수 없는 것 이상으로 애태우지도 마시고요. 가자, 이 사람아!\"\n\n진눈깨비가 그날 온종일 쉴 새 없이 내렸고, 짙은 안개가 일찍 끼어, 한순간도 걷히거나 옅어지지 않았습니다. 그런 길은 본 적이 없었습니다. 저는 이따금 우리가 길을 잘못 들어 갈아엎은 밭이나 늪으로 들어선 게 아닌가 두려웠습니다. 제가 나와 있은 시간을 떠올려보려 하면, 그것은 한없이 길게 이어진 막연한 기간으로 다가왔고, 이상하게도 저는 그때 시달리던 불안에서 한 번도 벗어나본 적이 없는 것만 같았습니다.\n\n앞으로 나아갈수록 저는 제 동행이 자신감을 잃어가는 게 아닌가 하는 불안한 예감이 들기 시작했습니다. 그는 길가의 사람들을 대할 때는 전과 다름없었으나, 마부석에 혼자 앉아 있을 때는 더 심각한 얼굴이었습니다. 길고 지루한 한 구간 내내 그의 손가락이 불안하게 입가를 가로질러 오가는 것을 저는 보았습니다. 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 마차와 다른 탈것의 마부들에게, 앞서가는 다른 마차와 탈것에서 어떤 승객들을 보았는지 묻기 시작하는 것도 엿들었습니다. 그들의 대답은 그를 북돋아주지 못했습니다. 그는 다시 마부석에 오를 때마다 늘 손가락을 까딱이고 눈꺼풀을 들어 올리며 저를 안심시켰으나, 이제는 \"가자, 이 사람아!\" 하고 말할 때 당황한 듯했습니다.\n\n마침내 말을 바꿀 때, 그는 옷차림의 자취를 너무 오래 놓쳐 슬슬 의아해지기 시작했다고 제게 말했습니다. 그런 자취를 한동안 놓쳤다가 또 한동안 잡고, 그렇게 이어가는 거야 별일 아니라고 했습니다. 하지만 여기서는 영문 모르게 자취가 사라졌고, 그 뒤로 다시 잡지 못했다는 것이었습니다. 이것은 그가 이정표를 살펴보기 시작하고, 갈림길마다 한 번에 십오 분씩 마차에서 내려 길을 탐색하기 시작했을 때 제가 품었던 불안을 뒷받침해주었습니다. 하지만 그는 제게 낙심하지 말라고 했으니, 다음 구간에서 다시 제대로 길을 잡게 될 공산이 충분하다는 것이었습니다.\n\n그러나 다음 구간도 그 구간이 끝난 것처럼 끝났습니다. 우리는 새로운 실마리를 얻지 못했습니다. 거기에는 외딴, 그러나 아늑하고 듬직한 건물인 널찍한 여관이 하나 있었습니다. 제가 미처 알아채기도 전에 큰 대문 아래로 마차를 몰고 들어서자, 여주인과 그녀의 예쁜 딸들이 마차 문으로 나와, 말을 준비하는 동안 내려서 몸을 녹이시라고 제게 청했습니다. 저는 거절하는 게 매정한 일일 것 같았습니다. 그들은 저를 따뜻한 방으로 데려가 위층에 남겨두었습니다.\n\n기억하기로 그곳은 집 모퉁이여서 두 방향으로 내다보였습니다. 한쪽으로는 샛길로 트인 마구간 마당이 보였는데, 거기서 마부들이 진흙투성이 마차에서 흙탕물을 뒤집어쓴 지친 말들의 마구를 풀고 있었고, 그 너머로는 샛길 자체가 보였는데 그 위로 간판이 묵직하게 흔들리고 있었습니다. 다른 쪽으로는 어두운 소나무 숲이 보였습니다. 그 가지들은 눈을 잔뜩 이고 있었고, 제가 창가에 서 있는 동안 그 눈이 젖은 무더기로 소리 없이 떨어졌습니다. 밤이 깃들고 있었고, 창유리에 어리어 환히 타오르며 어른거리는 난롯불의 영상과 대비되어 그 황량함이 한층 짙어졌습니다. 나무 줄기들 사이를 바라보며, 눈 속에 스며들어 그것을 무너뜨리는 해빙의 흔적을 따라 변색된 자국들을 좇으며, 저는 방금 저를 맞아준, 딸들 덕에 환하게 빛나던 그 자애로운 얼굴과, 그런 숲에 누워 죽어가는 제 어머니를 떠올렸습니다.\n\n그들이 모두 제 주위에 둘러서 있는 것을 깨닫고 저는 깜짝 놀랐는데, 정신을 잃기 전에 그러지 않으려고 무던히 애썼던 것이 떠올랐습니다. 그것이 조금은 위안이 되었습니다. 그들은 난롯가의 큰 소파에 저를 방석으로 받쳐 앉혔고, 그러고 나서 그 인상 좋은 여주인이 오늘 밤은 더 가지 말고 잠자리에 들어야 한다고 제게 말했습니다. 하지만 이 말에 저는 그들이 저를 거기 붙잡아둘까 봐 어찌나 떨었던지, 그녀는 곧 그 말을 거두고 반 시간 쉬는 것으로 타협을 보았습니다.\n\n참 정겹고 사랑스러운 사람이었습니다. 그녀와 그녀의 예쁜 세 딸이 모두 저를 그토록 분주히 보살폈습니다. 버킷 씨가 몸을 말리며 다른 데서 식사하는 동안 저는 뜨거운 수프와 닭구이를 먹기로 되어 있었습니다. 하지만 이내 난롯가에 아늑하고 둥근 식탁이 차려졌을 때, 저는 그들을 실망시키고 싶지 않았는데도 그것을 먹을 수가 없었습니다. 그래도 토스트 조금과 뜨거운 니거스는 들 수 있었고, 그 음료를 정말로 맛있게 즐겼기에 어느 정도 보답이 되었습니다.\n\n시간에 꼭 맞추어, 반 시간이 다 되었을 때 마차가 대문 아래로 덜컹거리며 다가왔고, 그들은 저를 아래층으로 데려갔습니다. 몸이 따뜻해지고 기운을 차리고 그들의 친절에 위로받은 저는, (다시는 정신을 잃지 않을 것이라고 그들에게 장담하며) 무사히 내려갔습니다. 마차에 올라 그들 모두와 고마운 작별을 나눈 뒤, 막내딸이—열아홉의 활짝 핀 처녀로, 가장 먼저 결혼하게 될 거라고 그들이 일러주었습니다—마차 발판에 올라 안으로 손을 뻗어 제게 입을 맞추었습니다. 그 시각 이후로 저는 그녀를 다시 본 적이 없으나, 지금 이 순간까지도 그녀를 제 벗으로 떠올립니다.\n\n난롯불과 등불이 어린, 바깥의 차가운 어둠에서 그토록 환하고 따뜻해 보이던 그 투명한 창문들은 곧 사라졌고, 우리는 다시 헐거운 눈을 으스러뜨리고 짓이기며 나아갔습니다. 우리는 어지간히 고생하며 갔으나, 그 음울한 길들은 여태까지보다 그리 더 나쁘지는 않았고, 그 구간은 9마일밖에 되지 않았습니다. 마부석에서 담배를 피우는 제 동행은—아까 그 여관에서 그가 아늑한 담배 연기 구름에 싸여 활활 타는 난롯불 앞에 서 있는 것을 보고, 그렇게 피우시라고 청할 생각을 했었습니다—여전히 빈틈없이 경계했고, 우리가 사람 사는 집이나 사람을 마주칠 때마다 여전히 잽싸게 내렸다가 올랐습니다. 그는 자기가 아끼는 듯한 그 작고 어둑한 등에 불을 켜두었는데—마차에는 등불이 따로 있었는데도요—이따금 그것을 제 쪽으로 돌려 제가 괜찮은지 살폈습니다. 마차 덮개에는 접이식 창문이 있었으나, 저는 한 번도 그것을 닫지 않았으니, 그것은 희망을 닫아거는 것만 같았기 때문입니다.\n\n우리는 구간 끝에 다다랐으나, 여전히 잃어버린 자취를 되찾지 못했습니다. 말을 바꾸려고 멈췄을 때 저는 그를 애타게 바라보았으나, 마부들을 지켜보며 서 있는 그의 한층 심각해진 얼굴로 그가 아무것도 듣지 못했음을 알았습니다. 그러고 나서 거의 곧바로, 제가 자리에 등을 기대고 있는데, 그가 켜둔 등불을 손에 들고 들여다보았으니, 흥분한, 사뭇 딴사람 같은 모습이었습니다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 제가 깜짝 놀라 말했습니다. \"그분이 여기 계세요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요. 착각하지 마십시오, 아가씨. 아무도 여기 없습니다. 하지만 알아냈습니다!\"\n\n결정처럼 얼어붙은 눈이 그의 속눈썹에, 머리카락에 박혀 있었고, 옷에는 이랑처럼 쌓여 있었습니다. 그는 제게 말하기 전에 얼굴에서 그것을 털어내고 숨을 돌려야 했습니다.\n\n\"자, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 마차 앞치마를 손가락으로 두드리며 말했습니다. \"제가 이제 하려는 일에 실망하지 마십시오. 아가씨는 저를 아시잖습니까. 저는 버킷 경위이고, 아가씨는 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다. 먼 길을 왔지만, 괜찮습니다. 다음 구간으로 올라갈 말 네 마리 거기 대령하라! 어서!\"\n\n마당에 소동이 일었고, 한 사내가 마구간에서 뛰쳐나와 위로 가시는 거냐 아래로 가시는 거냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"위라고, 이 사람아! 위로! 영어로 못 알아듣나? 위로!\"\n\n\"위로요?\" 제가 놀라서 말했습니다. \"런던으로요! 우리 돌아가는 건가요?\"\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"돌아갑니다. 화살처럼 곧장 되돌아갑니다. 아가씨는 저를 아시잖습니까. 두려워하지 마십시오. 다른 쪽을 쫓겠습니다, 맹세코—\"\n\n\"다른 쪽이요?\" 제가 되물었습니다. \"누구를요?\"\n\n\"그 여자를 제니라고 부르셨지요, 그렇지요? 그 여자를 쫓겠습니다. 저기 그 두 쌍을 한 사람당 1크라운에 끌어내! 정신들 차려, 이 사람들아!\"\n\n\"우리가 찾는 이 부인을 저버리시면 안 됩니다. 제가 아는바 그분이 처한 이런 밤에, 이런 마음 상태에서 그분을 내버리시면 안 됩니다!\" 제가 괴로움에 차서, 그의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했습니다.\n\n\"옳으신 말씀입니다, 아가씨, 저버리지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 다른 쪽을 쫓겠습니다. 여기 말들, 어서 서두르게. 다음 구간까지 안장에 사람 하나를 앞서 보내고, 그 사람한테 또 하나를 앞서 보내라고 해서, 위로, 곧장 내처 네 마리씩 대령하라고 일러. 아가씨, 두려워하지 마십시오!\"\n\n이 지시들과 그가 마당을 뛰어다니며 그들을 다그치는 모습은, 그 갑작스러운 방향 전환 못지않게 저를 어리둥절하게 하는 전반적인 흥분을 불러일으켰습니다. 하지만 한창 혼란이 일던 중에, 말 탄 사내 하나가 갈아탈 말을 준비시키러 달려 나갔고, 우리 말들은 무척 빠르게 매여졌습니다.\n\n\"아가씨.\" 버킷 씨가 자기 자리로 뛰어오르며 다시 들여다보고 말했습니다. \"—너무 허물없이 굴어도 용서하십시오—어쩔 수 없는 것 이상으로 애태우고 걱정하지 마십시오. 지금은 더 아무 말도 않겠습니다. 하지만 아가씨는 저를 아시잖습니까, 그렇지요?\"\n\n저는 우리가 어떻게 해야 할지 결정하는 데 그가 저보다 훨씬 더 능하다는 것은 안다고 말하려 했으나, 정말 이게 옳은지 그가 확신하느냐고, 제가 혼자라도 앞으로 나아가 찾아보면 안 되느냐고—괴로움에 저는 다시 그의 손을 움켜쥐고 그에게 속삭였습니다—제 친어머니를 찾아 나서면 안 되느냐고 했습니다.\n\n\"아가씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"압니다, 압니다. 제가 아가씨를 그르치게 하겠습니까, 그럴 것 같습니까? 버킷 경위입니다. 자, 아가씨는 저를 아시지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n네, 말고 무어라 할 수 있었겠습니까!\n\n\"그러면 되도록 마음을 굳게 다잡으시고, 준남작 레스터 데드록 경 못지않게 아가씨 곁을 지킬 저를 믿으십시오. 자, 거기 괜찮으십니까?\"\n\n\"다 괜찮아요!\"\n\n\"그럼, 출발이다. 그리고 가자, 이 사람들아!\"\n\n우리는 다시 우리가 왔던 그 처량한 길에 올라, 마치 물레방아에 뜯겨 나가듯 진창 진눈깨비와 녹아내리는 눈을 헤집으며 나아갔습니다.\n\n조상 대대로 물려받은 기품이 명하는 대로 여전히 무표정한 채, 데드록 저택은 음울한 위엄으로 가득한 거리를 향해 평소와 다름없는 자태를 유지한다. 현관의 작은 창마다 이따금 분 바른 머리가 내다보이며, 종일 하늘에서 떨어지는 세금 없는 가루눈을 바라본다. 같은 온실 안에서는 복숭아꽃이 바깥의 살을 에는 날씨를 등지고 커다란 홀의 난롯불 쪽으로 이국적인 자태를 돌리고 있다. 마님은 링컨셔로 내려갔으나 머지않아 돌아오리라고 알려져 있다.\n\n그러나 지나치게 부산한 소문은 링컨셔로 내려가려 하지 않는다. 소문은 한사코 도시 안을 날아다니며 재잘거린다. 소문은 그 가엾고 불운한 사내, 레스터 경이 딱하게 봉변당했음을 안다. 소문은, 가엾은 그대여, 온갖 충격적인 이야기를 듣는다. 소문은 반경 오 마일의 세상을 흥겨움으로 들썩이게 한다. 데드록 가에 무언가 잘못된 일이 벌어졌음을 모른다는 것은, 곧 자기 자신이 무명인사임을 떠벌리는 꼴이다. 해골처럼 앙상한 목에 복숭앗빛 뺨을 가진 매혹적인 여인 가운데 하나는, 레스터 경이 이혼 청원서를 내면 상원에서 드러날 주요 정황을 벌써 죄다 꿰고 있다.\n\n보석상 블레이즈앤드스파클에서도, 포목상 신앤드글로스에서도, 이 일은 몇 시간 동안 시대의 화두요 세기의 사건이 되며 또 될 것이다. 그 가게의 후원자인 귀부인들은 비록 그토록 도도하고 속을 알 수 없을지언정, 거기서는 다른 어떤 진열 상품 못지않게 꼼꼼히 무게가 달리고 치수가 재어지는 터라, 계산대 뒤에 선 가장 풋내기 점원까지도 이 새로운 유행을 완벽히 알아챈다. \"우리 손님은 말일세, 존스 군.\" 블레이즈앤드스파클이 그 점원을 채용하면서 일러둔 말이다. \"우리 손님은, 여보게, 양 떼라네, 그저 양 떼야. 표시 박힌 두세 마리가 가는 데로 나머지가 죄다 따라가지. 그 두세 마리만 잘 지켜보게, 존스 군, 그러면 자네는 양 떼를 손에 쥔 거야.\" 신앤드글로스 또한 자기네 존스에게 똑같이 일러둔다. 어디 가야 유행을 좇는 사람들을 붙잡을지, 자기네(신앤드글로스)가 점찍은 물건을 어떻게 유행시킬지를 두고. 똑같이 빗나가는 법 없는 원칙에 따라, 도서관 주인이자 실로 화려한 양 떼를 길러내는 거물 사육사인 슬래더리 씨도 바로 이날 인정한다. \"아, 그렇고말고요, 손님. 데드록 마님에 관한 소문이 확실히 떠돕니다, 제가 거느린 상류층 단골 사이에서 정말 한창이지요. 보십시오, 제 상류층 단골은 무언가 입에 올릴 거리가 있어야 하니까요. 그저 제가 이름 댈 수 있는 부인 한둘 사이에 어떤 화제를 유행시키기만 하면 그게 전체로 퍼져나가는 겁니다. 손님께서 제게 어떤 신상품을 들여놓으라 맡기셨을 때 제가 그 부인들에게 했을 바로 그 일을, 이번 경우엔 그분들이 스스로 해낸 셈이지요. 데드록 마님을 알고 또 그분을 두고 어쩌면 조금은 천진하게 질투도 한 까닭에 말입니다, 손님. 두고 보십시오, 손님, 이 화제는 제 상류층 단골 사이에서 아주 인기를 끌 겁니다. 만약 이게 투기였다면, 손님, 돈을 벌어들였을 겁니다. 제가 이렇게 말씀드리면, 제 말이 옳다고 믿으셔도 됩니다, 손님. 저는 제 상류층 단골을 연구하고 시계 태엽 감듯 마음대로 다루는 것을 본업으로 삼아온 사람이니까요, 손님.\"\n\n이렇게 소문은 수도에서 무성히 번창하며, 링컨셔로는 내려가려 하지 않는다. 근위 기병대 시계로 오후 다섯 시 반쯤에, 소문은 명예로운 스테이블스 씨에게서 새로운 한마디까지 이끌어냈으니, 그가 오랫동안 입담의 명성을 기대온 옛 농담을 능가할 만하다. 이 번뜩이는 재담인즉, 그는 그녀가 마구간에서 가장 손질 잘 된 암말인 줄은 늘 알았지만 도망치는 말일 줄은 미처 몰랐다는 것이다. 경마판에서 엄청난 갈채를 받는다.\n\n연회와 축제에서도, 그녀가 자주 빛내던 창공에서도, 어제만 해도 그녀가 압도하던 별자리들 사이에서도, 그녀는 여전히 단연 화제다. 무슨 일일까? 누구일까? 언제였을까? 어디였을까? 어떻게 된 일일까? 그녀의 다정한 벗들은 한창 유행하는 가장 점잖은 은어로, 가장 최신의 말투로, 가장 최신의 몸짓으로, 가장 최신의 느릿한 어조로, 그리고 완벽하게 세련된 무관심으로 그녀를 입에 올린다. 이 화제의 주목할 만한 특징은, 영감을 어찌나 불어넣는지 여태 한 번도 입을 열지 않던 사람들까지 입을 연다는 것이다. 정말로 한마디씩 한다! 윌리엄 버피는 만찬 자리에서 이런 재담 하나를 들고 하원으로 내려가고, 거기서 그가 속한 당의 원내총무가 자기 코담뱃갑과 함께 그것을 돌려, 자리를 뜨고 싶어 하는 의원들을 붙잡아둔다. 그 효과가 어찌나 대단한지 의장이(가발 한 귀퉁이 밑으로 그 말이 슬며시 제 귀에 들어왔던 터라) \"정숙하시오!\" 하고 세 번을 외쳐도 아무 소용이 없다.\n\n게다가 그녀가 막연히 도시의 화젯거리가 된 것과 관련해 가장 놀라운 정황은 이것이다. 슬래더리 씨가 거느린 상류층 단골의 변두리를 어슬렁대는 사람들, 그녀에 대해 아무것도 모르고 여태 한 번도 안 적이 없는 사람들이, 자기네도 그녀를 화제로 삼는 척하는 것이 제 평판에 필수라고 여겨, 가장 최신의 말투로, 가장 최신의 몸짓으로, 가장 최신의 느릿한 어조로, 가장 최신의 세련된 무관심으로, 그 밖의 모든 것을 곁들여 그녀를 재탕으로 팔아넘긴다는 점이다. 죄다 재탕이지만 더 열등한 체계와 더 희미한 별들 사이에서는 새것이나 진배없이 통한다. 만약 이 하찮은 장사꾼들 가운데 문필가나 예술가나 학자가 하나라도 있다면, 그토록 위풍당당한 목발로 가냘픈 자매들을 떠받쳐주다니 얼마나 고귀한 일인가!\n\n데드록 저택 바깥으로는 겨울날이 이렇게 흘러간다. 안은 어떨까?\n\n침대에 누운 레스터 경은 힘겹고 또렷하지 못할망정 조금은 말을 할 수 있다. 그에게는 침묵과 안정이 처방되었고, 사람들은 고통을 달래려 아편을 조금 먹였으니, 오랜 숙적이 그를 호되게 다루는 까닭이다. 그는 결코 잠들지 않으나, 이따금 멍하게 깨어 있는 선잠에 빠지는 듯도 하다. 이토록 험악한 날씨라는 말을 듣고 그는 침대를 창가로 더 가까이 옮기게 했고, 머리를 휘날리는 눈과 진눈깨비가 보이도록 손보게 했다. 그는 그것이 떨어지는 모습을 겨울날 내내 지켜본다.\n\n쥐죽은 듯 조용히 유지되는 집 안에서 아주 작은 소리만 나도, 그의 손은 연필로 향한다. 곁에 앉은 늙은 가정부는 그가 무엇을 쓰려는지 알기에 속삭인다. \"아닙니다, 그분은 아직 돌아오지 않으셨어요, 레스터 경. 어젯밤 늦게야 떠나셨답니다. 떠난 지 아직 얼마 되지 않았어요.\"\n\n그는 손을 거두고 다시 진눈깨비와 눈을 바라보기 시작하는데, 오래 바라본 탓에 그것이 어찌나 빽빽하고 빠르게 떨어지는 듯하던지, 그는 어지러이 소용돌이치는 흰 눈송이와 얼음 얼룩 앞에서 잠깐 눈을 감을 수밖에 없다.\n\n그는 날이 밝자마자 그것을 바라보기 시작했다. 그녀의 방을 그녀를 위해 준비해두어야겠다는 생각이 들 때만 해도 날은 아직 많이 가지 않았다. 몹시 춥고 축축하다. 난롯불을 잘 피워두어라. 그녀가 돌아오시기로 되어 있음을 모두에게 알려라. 부디 그대가 직접 살펴라. 그는 석판에 이런 뜻을 적고, 라운스웰 부인은 무거운 마음으로 따른다.\n\n\"이런 예감이 드는구나, 조지.\" 늙은 부인이 아들에게 말한다. 그녀가 잠깐 짬이 날 때 곁을 지켜주려고 아래층에서 기다리던 아들이다. \"이런 예감이 들어, 얘야, 마님이 이 벽 안에 다시는 발을 들이지 못하실 것만 같구나.\"\n\n\"불길한 예감이군요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"체스니 월드 벽 안에도 다시는 못 들어오실 게다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그건 더 안 좋네요. 한데 왜 그러세요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"어제 마님을 뵈었을 때 말이다, 조지, 내게는 그렇게 보였어, 아니 나를 보고 그러시는 것도 같았어. 유령의 길에 울리는 발소리가 마님을 거의 짓밟아버린 것만 같았단다.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런! 어머니, 옛이야기에 나오는 두려움으로 공연히 마음을 졸이시네요.\"\n\n\"아니다, 얘야. 아니야. 내가 이 집안에 들어온 지 예순 해가 다 되어가건만, 그동안 한 번도 두려웠던 적이 없었어. 그런데 무너지고 있구나, 얘야. 위대한 데드록 가문이 무너지고 있어.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않기를 바라요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"이 병환과 환난 중에 레스터 경 곁을 지킬 만큼 오래 살았으니 감사할 따름이다. 내 자리에 있을 다른 누구보다 내가 그분께 더 반가운 모습이 될 만큼은, 내가 그리 늙지도 쓸모없지도 않다는 걸 아니까. 하지만 유령의 길에 울리는 발소리가 마님을 짓밟고 말 거야, 조지. 그 소리는 오랜 세월 마님 뒤를 따라왔는데, 이제 마님을 앞질러 지나가버릴 게다.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 어머니, 다시 말씀드리지만, 그렇지 않기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"아, 나도 그렇단다, 조지.\" 늙은 부인이 고개를 가로젓고 맞잡은 두 손을 풀며 대꾸한다. \"하지만 내 두려움이 사실로 드러나, 그분이 그걸 아셔야만 한다면, 누가 그분께 말씀드리겠니!\"\n\n\"이게 마님의 방인가요?\"\n\n\"여기가 마님의 방이란다, 떠나실 때 그대로지.\"\n\n\"이런, 그러고 보니,\" 기병이 주위를 둘러보고 목소리를 낮추어 말한다. \"어머니가 왜 그런 생각을 하시게 됐는지 이제 알 것 같네요, 어머니. 방이란 게 늘 거기서 보던 누군가를 위해 이렇게 꾸며져 있는데, 그 사람이 무슨 그늘 밑으로 떠나버리면 무서운 기운이 감돌게 마련이지요. 어디로 갔는지 하느님만 아시는 처지라면 더 말할 것도 없고요.\"\n\n그의 말은 그리 빗나가지 않았다. 모든 이별이 저 위대한 마지막 이별을 예고하듯, 익숙한 존재를 잃은 텅 빈 방은 그대의 방과 나의 방이 언젠가 어떤 모습이 될지를 구슬프게 속삭인다. 이렇게 음울하고 버려진 채로, 마님의 거처는 휑한 기운을 띤다. 어젯밤 버킷 씨가 은밀히 뒤졌던 안쪽 방에는 그녀의 옷가지와 장신구의 자취가, 심지어 그것들이 그녀의 일부였을 때 비추던 데 익숙한 거울들마저 황량하고 공허한 기운을 풍긴다. 겨울날이 어둡고 추운 만큼, 이 버려진 방은 비바람이나 겨우 막아낼 숱한 오두막보다도 더 어둡고 더 춥다. 하인들이 화로마다 불을 가득 지피고, 발그레한 빛이 가장 먼 구석까지 쏘아 보내지는 따뜻한 유리 가리개 안쪽에 긴 의자와 안락의자를 들여놓아도, 방마다 어떤 빛으로도 걷어낼 수 없는 무거운 먹구름이 드리워 있다.\n\n늙은 가정부와 아들은 준비가 다 끝날 때까지 머물렀다가, 그녀가 위층으로 돌아간다. 그사이 볼럼니아가 라운스웰 부인의 자리를 대신했는데, 진주 목걸이와 연지 단지가 바스 온천을 꾸미는 데는 제격일지언정 지금 같은 처지의 환자에게는 그리 신통한 위안거리가 못 된다. 무슨 일인지 알 도리가 없다고(실은 정말 모른다) 여겨지는 볼럼니아는 적절한 말을 건네기가 영 까다로운 일임을 깨닫고, 그 대신 침대보를 정신없이 매만지고, 발끝으로 요란하게 오가고, 친척의 눈을 빈틈없이 들여다보고, 혼잣말로 \"주무시네.\" 하고 사람 속을 긁는 한마디를 속삭이는 것으로 자리를 메웠다. 이 쓸데없는 말을 반박하듯 레스터 경은 석판에 분개하며 적었다. \"안 잔다.\"\n\n그리하여 볼럼니아는 머리맡 의자를 그 기묘하고 늙은 가정부에게 내어주고, 조금 떨어진 탁자에 앉아 동정 어린 한숨을 내쉰다. 레스터 경은 진눈깨비와 눈을 지켜보며 기다리던 발소리가 돌아오기를 귀 기울인다. 마치 소환된 데드록 가 사람을 저세상으로 안내하려고 낡은 액자에서 걸어 나온 듯한 늙은 하녀의 귀에, 그 침묵은 제 입에서 나왔던 말의 메아리로 가득하다. \"누가 그분께 말씀드리겠니!\"\n\n그는 오늘 아침 시종의 손에 맡겨져 사람들 앞에 내보일 만하게 단장되었고, 형편이 허락하는 한 깔끔하게 차려졌다. 베개로 몸을 받쳤고, 백발은 평소대로 빗질되었으며, 속옷은 흠잡을 데 없이 매만져졌고, 무게 있는 실내복으로 몸을 감쌌다. 외알 안경과 회중시계도 손닿는 데 놓여 있다. 그가 되도록 동요한 기색 없이, 되도록 평소의 자신다운 모습으로 보이는 것은 필요한 일이다. 어쩌면 이제 자기 자신의 위엄을 위해서라기보다 그녀를 위해서. 여자들이란 입을 놀리게 마련이고, 볼럼니아도 데드록 가 사람이지만 예외는 아니다. 그가 그녀를 여기 붙들어두는 것은 다른 데서 입을 놀리지 못하게 하려는 뜻이 분명하다. 그는 몹시 아프지만, 마음과 몸의 고통에 맞서 더없이 의연하게 버텨내고 있다.\n\n어여쁜 볼럼니아는 오래 입을 다물고 있으면 권태라는 용에게 당장 잡아먹힐 위험에 처하는 발랄한 아가씨 가운데 하나인지라, 이내 감추지 못할 하품을 연달아 쏟아내며 그 괴물이 다가옴을 드러낸다. 대화 말고는 그 하품을 억누를 도리가 없음을 깨닫고, 그녀는 라운스웰 부인에게 아들을 칭찬하며, 그가 단연 여태껏 본 가장 멋진 장부 가운데 하나요, 그러니까 이름이 뭐더라, 자기가 끔찍이 좋아하던 그 근위 기병만큼이나 군인다운 풍채라고 떠벌린다. 자기가 애지중지하던 사람, 더없이 사랑스러운 존재, 워털루에서 전사한 그 사내 말이다.\n\n레스터 경은 이 찬사를 어찌나 놀라워하며 듣는지, 어리둥절한 얼굴로 주위를 두리번거려, 라운스웰 부인은 설명할 필요를 느낀다.\n\n\"데드록 아가씨가 말씀하시는 건 제 큰아들이 아니랍니다, 레스터 경. 막내아들이지요. 그 애를 찾았습니다. 집으로 돌아왔어요.\"\n\n레스터 경이 거친 외침으로 침묵을 깬다. \"조지? 그대의 아들 조지가 돌아왔다고, 라운스웰 부인?\"\n\n늙은 가정부가 눈물을 훔친다. \"하느님 감사합니다. 그렇습니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n잃었던 누군가를 다시 찾았다는 이 소식, 그토록 오래 떠나 있던 누군가가 돌아왔다는 이 일이, 그에게는 제 희망을 굳건히 뒷받침하는 증거로 다가오는 것일까? 그는 이렇게 생각하는 것일까. \"내게 주어진 도움으로, 나 또한 이번 일 뒤에 그녀를 무사히 데려올 수 있지 않을까. 그녀의 경우는 이 사내의 세월보다 짧은 시간밖에 흐르지 않았으니?\"\n\n그를 만류해봐야 소용없다. 그는 지금 말하기로 작정했고, 그래서 말한다. 두텁게 뭉뚱그려진 소리 속에서도, 알아들을 만큼은 또렷이.\n\n\"왜 내게 말하지 않았는가, 라운스웰 부인?\"\n\n\"바로 어제 일어난 일입니다, 레스터 경. 그리고 그런 이야기를 들으실 만큼 정정하실지 의심스러웠습니다.\"\n\n게다가 들뜬 볼럼니아가 이제 작게 비명을 지르며 떠올린다. 그가 라운스웰 부인의 아들이라는 사실을 아무도 알아선 안 되었고, 자기도 그걸 발설해선 안 되었다는 것을. 하지만 라운스웰 부인은 가슴받이가 부풀어 오를 만큼 열을 올려, 물론 레스터 경이 차도를 보이는 대로 곧장 말씀드릴 작정이었다고 항변한다.\n\n\"그대의 아들 조지는 어디 있는가, 라운스웰 부인?\" 레스터 경이 묻는다.\n\n라운스웰 부인은 그가 의사의 당부를 아랑곳하지 않는 데 적잖이 놀라며, 런던에 있다고 대답한다.\n\n\"런던 어디에?\"\n\n라운스웰 부인은 그가 이 집 안에 있다고 마지못해 털어놓는다.\n\n\"그 사람을 이 방으로 데려오게. 당장 데려오게.\"\n\n늙은 부인은 아들을 찾으러 가는 수밖에 없다. 레스터 경은 자신에게 남은 만큼의 움직임을 짜내어, 그를 맞으려고 몸을 조금 추스른다. 그러고 나서 그는 다시 떨어지는 진눈깨비와 눈을 내다보며, 돌아오는 발소리를 다시 귀 기울인다. 거리에는 소음을 죽이려고 짚을 잔뜩 깔아두었으니, 그녀가 바퀴 소리도 들리지 않은 채 문 앞에 당도할지도 모를 일이다.\n\n그가 이렇게, 좀 더 사소한 새 놀라움은 잊어버린 듯 누워 있을 때, 가정부가 기병인 아들과 함께 돌아온다. 조지 씨는 살그머니 침대맡으로 다가가, 고개 숙여 인사하고, 가슴을 펴고 서는데, 얼굴이 벌게진 채 몹시도 진심으로 자신을 부끄러워한다.\n\n\"맙소사, 정말로 조지 라운스웰이로구나!\" 레스터 경이 외친다. \"나를 기억하는가, 조지?\"\n\n기병은 그를 한참 바라보고 이 소리와 저 소리를 갈라내고서야 그가 무슨 말을 했는지 알아채는데, 그러고 나서 어머니의 도움도 조금 받아 대답한다. \"제가 레스터 경을 기억하지 못한다면, 그건 정말이지 제 기억력이 형편없다는 뜻이겠지요.\"\n\n\"그대를 보고 있자니, 조지 라운스웰,\" 레스터 경이 힘겹게 말한다. \"체스니 월드의 한 소년이 어렴풋이 떠오르네. 또렷이 기억하지. 아주 또렷이.\"\n\n그는 눈에 눈물이 차오를 때까지 기병을 바라보다가, 다시 진눈깨비와 눈으로 눈길을 돌린다.\n\n\"외람되오나, 레스터 경,\" 기병이 말한다. \"제 팔로 일으켜 드려도 되겠습니까? 제가 옮겨드리도록 허락해주시면 좀 더 편히 누우실 수 있을 겁니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"그래주게, 조지 라운스웰. 그리 친절을 베풀어준다면.\"\n\n기병은 그를 어린아이처럼 두 팔로 안아 가뿐히 들어 올리고, 얼굴이 창 쪽으로 더 향하도록 돌려 눕힌다. \"고맙네. 그대는 어머니의 다정함을,\" 레스터 경이 대꾸한다. \"그리고 제 몫의 힘을 지녔구먼. 고맙네.\"\n\n그는 손짓으로 가지 말라는 뜻을 전한다. 조지는 말이 떨어지기를 기다리며 조용히 침대맡에 머문다.\n\n\"왜 비밀로 하기를 바랐는가?\" 레스터 경이 이 말을 묻기까지 시간이 좀 걸린다.\n\n\"솔직히 저는 내세울 게 별로 없는 사람입니다, 레스터 경. 그리고 저는, 레스터 경, 혹여 이리 편찮으시지만 않다면, 부디 오래가지 않기를 바라옵니다만, 저는 그래도 전반적으로 무명인 채 남게 해주시는 호의를 바라고 싶습니다. 거기엔 짐작하기 그리 어렵지 않은 사연이 얽혀 있는데, 여기서 꺼내기에 그리 적절치도 않거니와, 제게 그리 떳떳한 일도 못 됩니다. 여러 주제를 두고 의견이 제각각일지언정, 제가 내세울 게 별로 없는 사람이라는 데는 만장일치로 동의하시리라 봅니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"그대는 군인이었지,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. \"그것도 충실한 군인이었어.\"\n\n조지가 군인답게 인사한다. \"거기까지야, 레스터 경, 저는 규율 아래 제 본분을 다했을 뿐이고, 그건 제가 할 수 있는 최소한이었습니다.\"\n\n\"보다시피,\" 레스터 경이 말한다. 그의 눈은 자꾸만 기병에게 이끌린다. \"내 형편이 영 좋지 못하네, 조지 라운스웰.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 듣는 것도, 또 직접 뵙는 것도 몹시 안타깝습니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"그럴 테지. 아닐세. 오래된 지병에 더해, 갑작스럽고 고약한 발작을 겪었네. 무언가 마비시키고,\" 한 손으로 한쪽 옆구리를 쓸어내리려 애쓰며, \"또 헝클어뜨리는 것이.\" 제 입술을 짚으며.\n\n조지가 알겠다는, 또 동정 어린 표정으로 다시 고개 숙여 인사한다. 둘 다 젊은이였던 시절(기병이 둘 중 한참 어렸다) 체스니 월드 아래쪽에서 서로를 바라보던 그 다른 시절들이 두 사람 앞에 떠올라, 둘 다의 마음을 누그러뜨린다.\n\n레스터 경은 다시 침묵에 빠져들기 전에, 제 마음에 담긴 무언가를 제 방식대로 기어이 말하겠다는 굳센 결의로, 베개 사이에서 몸을 조금 더 일으키려 한다. 그 동작을 알아챈 조지가 다시 그를 두 팔로 안아 원하는 자세로 눕혀준다. \"고맙네, 조지. 그대는 내게 또 다른 나일세. 그대는 체스니 월드에서 내 여분의 사냥총을 곧잘 들어주었지, 조지. 이 야릇한 처지에서 그대는 내게 낯익은 사람이야, 아주 낯익어.\" 그를 일으키며 레스터 경의 성한 팔을 제 어깨에 둘러주었던 터라, 그가 이 말을 하는 동안 레스터 경은 그 팔을 도로 거두는 데 더디다.\n\n\"내가 덧붙이려던 말은,\" 그가 이내 이어간다. \"이 발작에 관해 덧붙이려던 말은, 공교롭게도 그것이 마님과 나 사이의 사소한 오해와 때를 같이했다는 거였네. 우리 사이에 어떤 불화가 있었다는 뜻이 아닐세(그런 건 전혀 없었어). 다만 우리 둘에게만 중요한 어떤 정황을 두고 오해가 있었고, 그 탓에 잠시 마님과 함께하지 못하게 되었다는 거지. 마님은 여행을 떠나야 할 사정이 생겼는데, 부디 곧 돌아오시리라 믿네. 볼럼니아, 내 말이 알아들을 만한가? 발음하는 데 말이 통 내 뜻대로 듣질 않는구나.\"\n\n볼럼니아는 그의 말을 완벽히 알아듣는데, 실은 그가 일 분 전만 해도 도저히 가능하다 싶지 않았을 만큼 훨씬 또렷하게 말을 풀어낸다. 그렇게 해내는 데 든 안간힘이 그의 불안하고 애쓰는 표정에 고스란히 적혀 있다. 오로지 그 의지의 힘만이 그것을 해내게 한다.\n\n\"그러니, 볼럼니아, 나는 너 앞에서, 그리고 진실됨과 충직함을 누구도 의심할 수 없는 나의 오랜 가신이자 벗인 라운스웰 부인 앞에서, 또 체스니 월드의 조상 대대로 물려받은 집에서 내 젊은 시절의 낯익은 추억처럼 돌아온 그녀의 아들 조지 앞에서 말하고자 하네. 혹여 내가 다시 쓰러지거든, 혹여 내가 회복하지 못하거든, 혹여 내가 말과 글 쓰는 힘을 둘 다 잃거든, 비록 나은 일을 바라고는 있다만.\"\n\n늙은 가정부는 소리 없이 울고, 볼럼니아는 더없이 동요한 채 뺨에 그 어느 때보다 생생한 홍조를 띠고, 기병은 팔짱을 끼고 고개를 조금 숙인 채 공손히 귀를 기울인다.\n\n\"그러니 나는 말하고, 그대들 모두를 증인으로 부르고자 하네. 볼럼니아, 너부터 시작해, 더없이 엄숙하게. 나는 데드록 마님과 변함없는 관계임을. 나는 그녀에게 그 어떤 불평거리도 내세우지 않음을. 나는 늘 그녀에게 더없이 강한 애정을 품어왔고, 그것을 조금도 줄지 않은 채 간직하고 있음을. 이 말을 그녀 본인에게, 또 모두에게 전하라. 만약 그대가 이보다 조금이라도 덜 말한다면, 그것은 나를 향한 고의적인 거짓을 범하는 죄가 될 것이다.\"\n\n볼럼니아는 그의 당부를 글자 하나 어김없이 지키겠노라고 떨면서 다짐한다.\n\n\"마님은 지위가 너무 높고, 너무 아름답고, 너무 교양 있고, 대개의 일에서 둘러싼 이들 가운데 가장 뛰어난 자들보다도 너무 우월하시니, 적과 비방하는 자가 없을 리 없겠지. 내가 그대들에게 알리듯, 그자들에게도 알려라. 나는 온전한 정신과 기억과 분별을 갖춘 채로, 그녀에게 베푼 어떤 처분도 철회하지 않음을. 나는 그녀에게 내린 어떤 것도 줄이지 않음을. 나는 그녀와 변함없는 관계이며, 그러기로 마음먹었다면 그럴 충분한 권한이 있음에도 불구하고, 보다시피, 그녀의 이익과 행복을 위해 내가 한 어떤 행위도 거두지 않음을.\"\n\n그의 격식 차린 말의 나열은 다른 때였다면, 흔히 그러했듯, 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 구석이 있었을지 모르나, 이때만은 진지하고 가슴을 울린다. 그의 고결한 진심, 그의 충직함, 그녀를 향한 그의 의연한 비호, 그녀를 위해 제 억울함과 제 자존심을 너그러이 이겨낸 그 마음은 그저 명예롭고, 사내답고, 참되다. 더없이 흔한 직공에게서든, 더없이 고귀하게 태어난 신사에게서든, 그런 자질의 광채를 뚫고 이보다 못한 것이라곤 찾아볼 수 없다. 그런 빛 속에서 둘은 똑같이 우러르고, 똑같이 일어서며, 똑같이 흙의 자식으로서 똑같이 빛난다.\n\n안간힘에 짓눌린 그는 베개에 머리를 도로 누이고 일 분도 채 안 되게 눈을 감았다가, 다시 날씨를 지켜보며 먹먹한 소리에 귀 기울이기 시작한다. 그 자잘한 시중을 들어주는 모습에서, 또 그것을 받아들이는 태도에서, 기병은 그에게 없어서는 안 될 사람으로 자리 잡았다. 아무 말도 오가지 않았으나, 충분히 이해되었다. 그는 눈에 띄지 않게 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 어머니 의자 조금 뒤쪽에서 보초를 선다.\n\n이제 날이 저물기 시작한다. 눈이 죄다 녹아내려 변한 안개와 진눈깨비가 더 어두워지고, 불길은 방 벽과 가구에 더 또렷이 어른거리기 시작한다. 어스름이 짙어진다. 거리에는 밝은 가스등이 솟아오르고, 아직 그 자리를 지키는 끈질긴 기름 등잔들은, 그 생명의 근원이 절반은 얼고 절반은 녹은 채로, 물 밖에 나온 불붙은 물고기처럼 가쁘게 반짝인다. 실제로 그러하니까. 짚 위로 덜컹거리며 굴러와 종을 잡아당기고 \"안부를 묻던\" 세상은 이제 집으로 돌아가, 옷을 차려입고, 만찬을 들고, 앞서 말했듯 온갖 최신 유행을 곁들여 제 다정한 벗을 입에 올리기 시작한다.\n\n이제 레스터 경은 더 나빠져, 안절부절못하고, 불안해하며, 심한 고통에 시달린다. 볼럼니아가 (꼭 거슬리는 짓을 하도록 점지받은 사람답게) 초에 불을 붙이자, 도로 꺼두라는 명을 받는데, 아직 그만큼 어둡지 않아서다. 그렇지만 사실 무척 어둡기도 하니, 밤새 어두울 만큼 어둡다. 이윽고 그녀가 다시 시도한다. 안 돼! 꺼두어라. 아직 그만큼 어둡지 않다.\n\n날이 늦어가고 있음을 인정하지 않으려고 그가 스스로 그런 허구를 붙들고 버틴다는 것을, 가장 먼저 알아챈 것은 늙은 가정부다.\n\n\"존경하는 주인님, 레스터 경,\" 그녀가 나직이 속삭인다. \"주인님을 위해서, 또 제 본분으로서, 외람되이 빌고 또 비옵니다. 이 외로운 어둠 속에 누워 지켜보고 기다리며 시간을 질질 끌지 마옵소서. 커튼을 치고, 초에 불을 밝히고, 주변을 좀 더 편안하게 해드리도록 해주십시오. 교회 시계는 똑같이 시각을 알릴 것이고, 레스터 경, 밤도 똑같이 지나갈 것입니다. 마님도 똑같이 돌아오실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나도 안다, 라운스웰 부인. 하지만 내가 약해진 데다, 마님이 떠난 지 너무 오래되었어.\"\n\n\"그리 오래된 건 아닙니다, 레스터 경. 아직 스물네 시간도 안 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 긴 시간이야. 아, 긴 시간이지!\"\n\n그가 그녀의 가슴을 후벼파는 신음과 함께 그 말을 한다.\n\n그녀는 지금이 그에게 거친 빛을 비출 때가 아님을 안다. 그의 눈물이 너무 거룩해 자기조차도 보아선 안 된다고 여긴다. 그래서 그녀는 한동안 말없이 어둠 속에 앉아 있다가, 가만히 움직이기 시작해, 불을 들쑤시기도 하고, 어두운 창가에 서서 바깥을 내다보기도 한다. 마침내 그가 추슬러진 자제력으로 그녀에게 말한다. \"그대 말이 맞네, 라운스웰 부인. 인정한다고 더 나빠질 것도 없지. 날이 늦어가는데, 그들은 오지 않았어. 방에 불을 밝히게!\" 방에 불이 밝혀지고 날씨가 가려지자, 그에게 남은 일이라곤 귀 기울이는 것뿐이다.\n\n하지만 사람들은 알아챈다. 그가 아무리 풀이 죽고 병들었어도, 그녀의 방에 켜둔 불을 들여다보며 그녀를 맞을 채비가 다 되었는지 확인하는 조용한 시늉을 해 보이면 그가 환해진다는 것을. 가련한 시늉일망정, 그녀가 돌아오시기로 되어 있다는 이런 언급은 그의 마음속에 희망을 지펴둔다.\n\n자정이 오고, 그와 함께 똑같은 공허가 온다. 거리에 마차는 드물고, 그 동네에 늦은 다른 소리라곤 없다. 어쩌다 너무도 정처 없이 취해 그 혹한 지대로 흘러든 사내가 인도를 따라 악다구니를 쓰고 고래고래 소리치는 것 말고는. 이 겨울밤은 어찌나 고요한지, 그 짙은 침묵에 귀 기울이는 것이 마치 짙은 어둠을 바라보는 것만 같다. 혹여 이런 침묵 속에 먼 소리 하나가 들려온다면, 그것은 저 어둠 속의 희미한 빛처럼 어스름을 가르며 사라지고, 모든 것이 전보다 더 무거워진다.\n\n하인들 무리는 잠자리로 물러가고(어젯밤 내내 깨어 있었으니 마다하지 않는다), 오직 라운스웰 부인과 조지만이 레스터 경의 방을 지킨다. 밤이 더디게 흘러가며, 아니 오히려 두 시와 세 시 사이에 아예 멈춰 선 듯할 무렵, 두 사람은 그가 이제 볼 수 없는 날씨에 대해 더 알고 싶어 안달하는 것을 알아챈다. 그래서 조지는 그토록 정성껏 보살펴둔 방들을 반 시간마다 어김없이 순찰하던 길에, 발걸음을 현관문까지 넓혀, 주위를 둘러보고, 더없이 고약한 밤에 관해 그가 전할 수 있는 가장 나은 보고를 들고 돌아온다. 진눈깨비는 여전히 떨어지고, 심지어 돌로 깐 보도마저 얼음 진창에 발목까지 잠겨 있다고.\n\n볼럼니아는 계단의 외진 층계참 위쪽 자기 방에 있는데, 조각 장식과 금박 입힌 부분의 끝을 지나 두 번째 모퉁이를 돈 곳이다. 친척용 방인 이곳에는 죄를 지어 추방된 레스터 경의 끔찍하게 망쳐진 초상화 한 점이 걸려 있고, 낮에는 태곳적 표본 같은 시커먼 찻잎처럼 바싹 마른 관목들이 심긴 엄숙한 안뜰이 내려다보인다. 거기서 그녀는 온갖 두려움의 먹잇감이 된다. 그 가운데서도 마지막이 아니요 결코 작지 않은 것은, 어쩌면 그녀의 표현대로 레스터 경에게 \"무슨 일이 생길\" 경우 자기 쥐꼬리만 한 수입이 어찌 될까 하는 두려움이다. 이런 맥락에서 무슨 일이란 오직 한 가지만을 뜻하니, 곧 알려진 세상의 어느 준남작에게든 그의 의식에 일어날 수 있는 마지막 일이다.\n\n이 두려움이 빚어낸 결과로, 볼럼니아는 자기 방에서 도무지 잠자리에 들 수도 없고 자기 방 난롯가에 앉아 있을 수도 없음을 깨닫는다. 그래서 어여쁜 머리를 숄로 잔뜩 동여매고 어여쁜 몸을 천으로 휘감고는, 유령처럼 저택을 떠돌아야만 하는데, 특히 따뜻하고 호사스럽게 꾸며진, 여태 돌아오지 않는 이를 위한 방들을 즐겨 맴돈다. 이런 처지에 혼자 있는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없는지라, 볼럼니아는 하녀를 거느리는데, 이 하녀는 그 일을 위해 제 잠자리에서 끌려나온 데다, 몹시 춥고, 무척 졸리고, 게다가 본디 연봉 만 파운드는 족히 되는 사람의 하녀가 되겠다고 마음먹었건만 처지에 떠밀려 친척 신세인 분의 시중을 들게 된 박복한 하녀인지라, 얼굴빛이 그리 곱지 못하다.\n\n그러나 기병이 순찰하는 길에 이 방들을 주기적으로 들르는 것은 마님에게도 하녀에게도 보호와 동행을 보장하는 일이라, 깊은 밤 시각에는 무척 반가운 일이 된다. 그가 다가오는 소리가 들릴 때마다 둘은 그를 맞으려고 조금씩 매무새를 꾸미는 채비를 하고, 그 밖의 시간에는 짤막한 망각의 토막과, 아주 가시 없지는 않은 대화로 보초 시간을 나눈다. 발받침에 두 발을 올리고 앉았던 데드록 아가씨가, 수호신인 하녀에게 (그녀로서는 몹시 못마땅하게도) 구해지던 순간 불 속으로 빠지려던 참이었는지 아닌지를 두고서.\n\n\"레스터 경은 지금 좀 어떠신가요, 조지 씨?\" 볼럼니아가 머리에 두건을 매만지며 묻는다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 레스터 경은 별 차도가 없으십니다, 아가씨. 무척 기운이 없고 편찮으시고, 이따금 정신이 조금 오락가락하시기도 합니다.\"\n\n\"나를 찾으셨나요?\" 볼럼니아가 다정하게 묻는다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 아니요, 그러셨다고는 못 하겠습니다, 아가씨. 그러니까 제가 듣기로는 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 슬픈 때로군요, 조지 씨.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다, 아가씨. 이제 그만 주무시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"잠자리에 드시는 게 훨씬 나을 텐데요, 데드록 아가씨.\" 하녀가 톡 쏘아붙인다.\n\n하지만 볼럼니아는 싫다고, 싫다고 대답한다. 그녀를 찾을지도 모르고, 언제든 당장 필요로 할지도 모른다는 것이다. \"무슨 일이 생기기라도\" 했는데 자기가 그 자리에 없다면 결코 자신을 용서하지 못하리라는 것이다. 그러면서 하녀가 들고나온 물음, 곧 어째서 그 자리가 (레스터 경 방에 더 가까운) 자기 방이 아니라 거기인가 하는 물음에는 답하기를 사양하고, 다만 그 자리에 남겠노라고 꿋꿋이 선언한다. 볼럼니아는 한술 더 떠 \"한숨도 못 잤음\"을 마치 무슨 큰 공처럼 내세우는데, 마치 스무 번이나 서른 번은 잘 수 있었던 양 군다. 하지만 오 분 안에 두 눈을 분명코 떴음을 따져보면, 이 주장과 좀처럼 아귀가 맞지 않는다.\n\n그러나 네 시가 되도록 여전히 똑같은 공허가 이어지자, 볼럼니아의 한결같음이 흔들리기 시작한다. 아니 오히려 굳건해지기 시작한다. 이제 그녀는 많은 일이 그녀에게 기대될 내일을 위해 채비하는 것이 제 본분이라 여기기 때문이다. 실은 아무리 그 자리에 남고 싶을지언정, 자기희생의 행위로서 그 자리를 떠나는 것이 요구될지도 모른다고. 그래서 기병이 다시 나타나 \"이제 그만 주무시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까, 아가씨?\" 하고, 하녀가 전보다 더 톡 쏘아붙이며 \"잠자리에 드시는 게 훨씬 나을 텐데요, 데드록 아가씨!\" 하고 항변하자, 그녀는 얌전히 일어서며 말한다. \"두 사람이 가장 좋다고 여기는 대로 나를 하구려!\"\n\n조지 씨는 그녀를 제 팔에 의지하게 해 그녀의 친척용 방문까지 데려다주는 것이 가장 좋다고 의심 없이 여기고, 하녀는 그녀를 거의 격식 없이 침대로 떠밀어 넣는 것이 가장 좋다고 의심 없이 여긴다. 그리하여 이 절차가 행해지고, 이제 기병은 순찰 도는 동안 저택을 홀로 차지한다.\n\n날씨는 조금도 나아지지 않았다. 현관 차양에서, 처마에서, 난간에서, 모든 턱과 기둥과 지주에서, 녹은 눈이 뚝뚝 떨어진다. 그것은 마치 피난처를 찾듯 커다란 문의 상인방 안으로, 그 밑으로, 창문 구석으로, 물러설 만한 온갖 틈과 갈라진 자리로 기어들어, 거기서 스러지고 죽는다. 눈은 여전히 떨어지고 있다. 지붕 위로, 천창 위로, 심지어 천창을 뚫고, 유령의 길에 울리는 그 규칙적인 발소리처럼 뚝, 뚝, 뚝, 아래쪽 돌바닥 위로.\n\n기병은 큰 저택의 외로운 위용에 옛 기억이 일깨워져, 한때 체스니 월드에서 익히 보았던 터라 그에게는 새로울 것도 없건만, 등불을 팔 길이만큼 치켜들고 계단을 올라 주요 방들을 지나간다. 지난 몇 주 사이 겪은 갖가지 운명을 떠올리고, 시골에서 보낸 제 소년 시절을 떠올리며, 그 사이의 너른 세월을 가로질러 그토록 야릇하게 한데 묶인 제 삶의 두 시절을 떠올리며. 그 기억이 마음에 생생한 살해당한 사내를 떠올리며. 바로 이 방들에서 사라진, 그러나 최근의 자취가 죄다 여기 남은 그 마님을 떠올리며. 위층에 있는 이 집의 주인을, 그리고 \"누가 그분께 말씀드리겠니!\" 하던 그 불길한 예감을 떠올리며. 그는 여기를 보고 저기를 보며 곰곰 생각한다. 지금이라도 무언가가 보일지 모른다고, 다가가서 손을 얹고 그것이 헛것임을 입증하기에는 제 담력이 시험에 들 그런 무언가가. 하지만 위에도 아래에도 온통 공허뿐, 어둠처럼 공허할 뿐이다. 그가 다시 큰 계단을 올라가는 동안, 짓누르는 침묵처럼 공허할 뿐이다.\n\n\"모두 빈틈없이 채비되었는가, 조지 라운스웰?\"\n\n\"아주 가지런하고 빈틈없습니다, 레스터 경.\"\n\n\"무슨 기별도 없고?\"\n\n기병이 고개를 가로젓는다.\n\n\"행여 빠뜨렸을 만한 편지도 없는가?\"\n\n하지만 그는 그런 희망이란 없음을 알기에, 대답을 기다리지도 않고 머리를 도로 누인다.\n\n몇 시간 전 그 자신이 말했듯 그에게 무척 낯익은 조지 라운스웰은, 공허한 겨울밤이 길게 남은 내내 그를 더 편한 자세로 들어 올려주고, 그의 입 밖에 내지 않은 바람에도 똑같이 익숙하여, 늦게야 동이 처음 트자 등불을 끄고 커튼을 걷는다. 날은 유령처럼 다가온다. 차갑고, 빛깔 없고, 어렴풋한 그 날은, 죽음 같은 빛깔의 경고 줄무늬를 앞세워 보내니, 마치 이렇게 외치는 듯하다. \"거기서 지켜보는 그대여, 내가 무엇을 가져오는지 보라! 누가 그분께 말씀드리겠는가!\"\n\n런던 외곽의 집들이 마침내 시골을 밀어내고 우리를 거리로 에워싸기 시작한 것은 새벽 세 시였습니다. 우리는 낮에 지나왔을 때보다 훨씬 험한 길을 헤치며 나아갔습니다. 그날 이후로 눈도 계속 내리고 해빙도 줄곧 이어진 탓이었지요. 하지만 내 동행의 기력은 조금도 수그러들지 않았습니다. 내 생각에 그 기력이 우리를 앞으로 나아가게 하는 데에 말들보다 보탬이 덜 됐던 것은 그저 잠깐이었고, 오히려 말들을 도와준 적이 더 많았습니다. 말들은 비탈 중턱에서 기진해 멈춰 섰고, 거센 물줄기를 헤치며 몰렸으며, 미끄러져 마구에 뒤엉키기도 했지요. 하지만 그와 그의 작은 등불은 언제나 채비를 갖추고 있었고, 사고가 수습되고 나면 나는 그의 침착한 \"가자, 이놈들아!\" 소리에서 한 번도 흐트러짐을 들은 적이 없었습니다.\n\n그가 돌아오는 길을 이끈 그 한결같음과 확신을 나로서는 도무지 설명할 수가 없었습니다. 그는 한 번도 흔들리지 않았고, 우리가 런던에서 몇 마일 안쪽에 들어설 때까지 묻는답시고 멈추는 일조차 없었습니다. 그러고 나서는 여기저기서 짤막한 몇 마디면 충분했지요. 그렇게 해서 우리는 새벽 세 시에서 네 시 사이에 이즐링턴으로 들어섰습니다.\n\n그동안 내내 우리가 매분 매분 어머니를 점점 더 뒤로 멀리 떼어놓고 있다는 생각에 짓눌리던 그 초조와 불안을 여기서 길게 늘어놓지는 않겠습니다. 내 생각엔, 그가 옳을 수밖에 없고 이 여인을 뒤쫓는 데에 분명 흡족한 목적이 있을 거라는 강한 희망을 어느 정도 품고 있었던 것 같습니다. 그러면서도 나는 그것을 의심하고 따져 묻느라 가는 길 내내 스스로를 들볶았지요. 그 여인을 찾아내면 무슨 일이 벌어질까, 이렇게 잃어버린 시간을 무엇으로 메울 수 있을까 하는 물음 또한 도저히 떨쳐낼 수 없었습니다. 우리가 멈춰 섰을 때 내 마음은 그런 생각들에 오래 매달린 탓에 완전히 시달려 있었습니다.\n\n마차 정거장이 있는 어느 큰길에서 우리는 멈췄습니다. 내 동행은 두 마부에게 삯을 치렀는데, 두 사람은 마치 마차처럼 길바닥에 끌려다니기라도 한 양 온몸이 흙탕물 범벅이었지요. 그는 마차를 어디로 가져다 놓으라고 짤막하게 일러준 뒤 나를 그 안에서 들어 올려, 나머지 가운데서 자기가 골라둔 삯마차로 옮겨 태웠습니다.\n\n\"아니, 아가씨!\" 그가 그러면서 말했습니다. \"온통 젖었군요!\"\n\n나는 그걸 의식하지 못하고 있었습니다. 하지만 녹은 눈이 마차 안으로 스며들었고, 게다가 말이 쓰러져 허우적대는 통에 일으켜 세워야 했던 두세 차례 내가 마차에서 내렸던 터라, 물기가 내 옷에 배어들었던 겁니다. 나는 그에게 아무렇지도 않다고 했지만, 그를 아는 마부는 내 만류에도 아랑곳없이 거리를 달려 자기 마구간으로 가더니 거기서 깨끗하고 마른 짚을 한 아름 가져왔습니다. 두 사람은 그것을 털어 내 둘레에 잘 깔아주었고, 나는 그게 따뜻하고 아늑하다는 걸 알게 되었지요.\n\n\"자, 아가씨.\" 버킷 씨가 나를 안에 태우고 문을 닫은 뒤 창문으로 머리를 들이밀며 말했습니다. \"우리는 이제 이 사람을 잡으러 갑니다. 시간이 좀 걸릴지도 모르지만, 아가씨는 그쯤은 개의치 않을 거고요. 내게 다 생각이 있다는 걸 꽤 확실히 믿고 있죠. 안 그래요?\"\n\n그게 무엇인지 나는 거의 짐작도 못 했고, 얼마나 짧은 시간 안에 그걸 더 잘 알게 될지도 거의 몰랐지만, 나는 그를 믿는다고 그에게 다짐했습니다.\n\n\"믿어도 좋아요, 아가씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"그리고 내 말해 두는데! 내가 아가씨를 겪어보고 아가씨한테 두는 만큼의 절반만 아가씨가 나한테 믿음을 두어도, 그걸로 충분합니다. 세상에! 아가씨는 손톱만큼도 폐가 안 돼요. 나는 어느 신분의 젊은 여자든—높으신 분들도 숱하게 봤지만—잠자리에서 불려 나온 뒤로 아가씨처럼 처신하는 사람을 본 적이 없습니다. 본보기예요, 알아요, 아가씨가 바로 그래요.\" 버킷 씨가 따뜻하게 말했습니다. \"본보기라니까요.\"\n\n나는 그에게 폐가 안 되었다니 정말 기쁘다고, 사실 정말로 기뻤으니까요, 그리고 앞으로도 그러지 않기를 바란다고 말했습니다.\n\n\"아가씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"젊은 숙녀가 담대한 만큼 온순하고, 온순한 만큼 담대하다면, 그게 내가 바라는 전부고 기대 이상이지요. 그럼 그 사람은 여왕이 되는 겁니다. 아가씨가 바로 그래요.\"\n\n이런 격려의 말과 함께—그 외롭고 불안한 처지에서 그 말은 정말로 내게 격려가 되었습니다—그는 마부석에 올랐고, 우리는 다시 한번 달려 나갔습니다. 어디로 달렸는지는 그때도 몰랐고 그 뒤로도 끝내 알지 못했지만, 우리는 런던에서 가장 좁고 가장 후미진 거리들만 골라 찾아드는 듯했습니다. 그가 마부에게 길을 일러주는 걸 볼 때마다 나는 그런 거리의 더 깊은 미궁 속으로 내려가리라 짐작했고, 그건 어김없이 들어맞았지요.\n\n이따금 우리는 좀 더 너른 큰길로 빠져나오거나, 여느 건물보다 큼직하고 환히 불을 밝힌 건물에 이르렀습니다. 그러면 우리는 처음 길을 나설 때 들렀던 것 같은 사무소들 앞에 멈췄고, 그가 다른 이들과 의논하는 모습을 보았지요. 더러는 그가 아치 통로 옆이나 길모퉁이에서 내려 작은 등불을 슬쩍 비추어 보였습니다. 그러면 그 불빛이 마치 무수한 벌레들처럼 여기저기 어두운 구석에서 비슷한 불빛들을 불러냈고, 새로운 의논이 벌어졌습니다. 차츰 우리는 수색의 범위를 더 좁고 손쉬운 한계 안으로 좁혀가는 듯했습니다. 이제는 근무 중인 순경 한 사람 한 사람이 버킷 씨가 알고 싶어 하는 걸 일러주고 어디로 가야 할지 가리켜줄 수 있었지요. 마침내 우리는 그가 그런 사람 가운데 하나와 꽤 긴 이야기를 나누느라 멈춰 섰는데, 그가 이따금 고개를 끄덕이는 모양새로 보아 만족스러운 모양이라고 짐작했습니다. 이야기가 끝나자 그는 무척 바쁘고 무척 주의 깊은 얼굴로 내게 다가왔습니다.\n\n\"자, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 내게 말했습니다. \"무슨 일이 벌어지든 아가씨는 놀라지 않으리란 걸 압니다. 더 당부할 것도 없이, 우리가 이 사람을 잡으러 들어간다는 것과, 나 자신이 미처 알기도 전에 아가씨가 내게 도움이 될지도 모른다는 것만 일러두지요. 이런 부탁은 하고 싶지 않지만, 아가씨, 조금만 걸어주시겠어요?\"\n\n물론 나는 곧장 내려서 그의 팔을 잡았습니다.\n\n\"발을 디디기가 쉽지 않을 겁니다.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"천천히 하세요.\"\n\n거리를 건너는 동안 나는 어수선하고 황급하게 주위를 둘러보았지만, 그곳을 아는 것 같았습니다. \"여기 홀본인가요?\" 나는 그에게 물었습니다.\n\n\"맞아요.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 골목길을 아시나요?\"\n\n\"챈서리 레인 같은데요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇게 이름 붙은 곳이지요, 아가씨.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n우리는 그 길로 접어들었고, 진눈깨비를 헤치며 발을 끌며 가는데 시계가 다섯 시 반을 치는 소리가 들렸습니다. 우리는 그런 발판으로 갈 수 있는 한 빠르게, 말없이 나아갔는데, 그때 외투를 두른 누군가가 좁은 보도에서 우리 쪽으로 다가오다가 걸음을 멈추고 비켜서서 내게 길을 내주었습니다. 바로 그 순간 나는 우드코트 씨의 입에서 놀라움의 탄성과 내 이름을 듣게 되었지요. 나는 그의 목소리를 아주 잘 알았습니다.\n\n그토록 뜻밖이라, 그리고 너무도—뭐라 불러야 할지, 반가운 건지 가슴 아픈 건지 모르겠는, 열병처럼 헤매던 그 여정 끝에, 그것도 한밤중에 그를 마주치자, 나는 눈에서 솟는 눈물을 도저히 참을 수가 없었습니다. 마치 낯선 나라에서 그의 목소리를 듣는 것 같았지요.\n\n\"서머슨 양, 이런 시각에, 이런 날씨에 밖에 나와 계시다니요!\"\n\n그는 내가 어떤 심상찮은 일로 불려 나갔다는 걸 내 후견인에게서 들었던지라, 어떤 설명도 면해주려고 그렇게 말했습니다. 나는 우리가 방금 마차에서 내려 어디로 가는 길이라고 말하려다—거기서 내 동행을 쳐다보지 않을 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"아, 그게 말이죠, 우드코트 씨\"—그는 나한테서 그 이름을 알아챈 거였습니다—\"우리는 지금 옆 거리로 가는 참입니다. 나는 버킷 경위라고 합니다.\"\n\n우드코트 씨는 내 만류는 아랑곳하지 않고 황급히 자기 외투를 벗어 내 둘레에 둘러주었습니다. \"그것도 잘하신 겁니다.\" 버킷 씨가 거들며 말했습니다. \"아주 잘하신 거예요.\"\n\n\"같이 가도 되겠습니까?\" 우드코트 씨가 말했습니다. 내게 묻는 건지 내 동행에게 묻는 건지 모르겠더군요.\n\n\"아니, 세상에!\" 버킷 씨가 그 대답을 자기가 떠맡으며 외쳤습니다. \"물론 되고말고요.\"\n\n이 모든 게 순식간에 오갔고, 두 사람은 외투에 싸인 나를 가운데 두고 데려갔습니다.\n\n\"방금 리처드한테서 오는 길입니다.\" 우드코트 씨가 말했습니다. \"어젯밤 열 시부터 그와 함께 앉아 있었지요.\"\n\n\"아, 저런, 그가 아픈가요!\"\n\n\"아니요, 아닙니다, 정말로요. 아픈 건 아니고, 그저 그리 좋지 않을 뿐입니다. 그가 기운이 빠지고 가물가물하기에—아시다시피 그는 이따금 그렇게 애를 태우고 진이 빠지지요—에이다가 당연히 내게 사람을 보냈고요. 집에 돌아와 그 쪽지를 보고 곧장 이리로 온 겁니다. 자! 리처드는 조금 지나자 무척 기운을 차렸고, 에이다는 그게 다 내 덕이라고 어찌나 행복해하고 어찌나 굳게 믿던지요, 하느님은 아시지만 내가 한 일이라곤 거의 없는데도 말입니다. 그래서 나는 그가 몇 시간 푹 잠들 때까지 그와 함께 있었지요. 지금 그녀가 자고 있는 만큼이나 푹 잠들었기를 바랍니다!\"\n\n그들을 두고 그토록 다정하고 허물없이 이야기하는 그 말투며, 꾸밈없는 헌신이며, 그가 내 사랑하는 벗에게 불어넣었음을 내가 아는 그 고마운 믿음이며, 그가 그녀에게 주는 위안이며, 내가 이 모든 것을 그가 내게 한 약속과 떼어 생각할 수 있었을까요? 내 모습이 변한 것에 그토록 마음 아파하며 그가 내게 한 말을 이것이 떠올리게 해주지 않았다면 나는 얼마나 배은망덕했을지요. \"나는 그를 맡겨진 신뢰로 받아들이겠습니다. 그리고 그것은 거룩한 신뢰가 될 겁니다!\"\n\n이제 우리는 또 다른 좁은 거리로 접어들었습니다. \"우드코트 씨.\" 오는 동안 그를 유심히 살피던 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"우리 일은 여기 어느 법률 문구상한테 가는 겁니다. 스내그스비라는 분이지요. 아니, 그를 아십니까?\" 그는 어찌나 눈치가 빠른지 그 자리에서 그걸 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"네, 조금 압니다. 이곳으로 그를 찾아온 적도 있고요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까, 선생?\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"그럼 내가 가서 그와 한두 마디 나누는 동안, 잠깐만 서머슨 양을 선생께 맡겨도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n그가 마지막으로 의논했던 순경이 우리 뒤에 말없이 서 있었습니다. 나는 누군가 우는 소리가 들린다고 말했을 때 그가 끼어들기 전까지는 그를 알아채지 못했지요.\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오, 아가씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"스내그스비네 하녀입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 말이죠.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"그 계집애가 발작 증세가 있는데, 오늘 밤 심하게 도졌어요. 더없이 거북한 상황입니다. 그 애한테서 알아내야 할 게 있는데, 어떻게든 정신을 차리게 해야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"어쨌든, 그 애 때문이 아니었으면 저 사람들도 아직 안 일어났을 겁니다, 버킷 씨.\" 다른 사람이 말했습니다. \"밤새도록 거의 내내 저러고 있었거든요, 선생.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 사실이지.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"내 등불이 다 탔군. 자네 것 좀 잠깐 비춰주게.\"\n\n이 모든 게, 안에서 우는 소리와 신음 소리가 어렴풋이 들려오는 그 집에서 한두 집 떨어진 곳에서 속삭임으로 오갔습니다. 그러려고 만들어진 그 작고 둥근 불빛 속에서 버킷 씨는 문으로 다가가 두드렸습니다. 두 번 두드리자 문이 열렸고, 그는 우리를 거리에 세워둔 채 안으로 들어갔습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 우드코트 씨가 말했습니다. \"당신의 믿음에 주제넘게 끼어들지 않으면서 곁에 있어도 된다면, 부디 그렇게 하게 해주십시오.\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시군요.\" 나는 대답했습니다. \"내 일이라면 당신께 비밀로 할 것이 없습니다. 만약 무엇이든 숨긴다면, 그건 다른 사람의 것이지요.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다. 믿어주십시오, 그것을 온전히 존중할 수 있는 동안만 당신 곁에 있겠습니다.\"\n\n\"전적으로 당신을 믿습니다.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"당신이 얼마나 거룩하게 약속을 지키는지 나는 알고 깊이 느끼고 있어요.\"\n\n잠시 뒤 그 작고 둥근 불빛이 다시 환히 비쳤고, 버킷 씨가 그 빛 속에서 진지한 얼굴로 우리에게 다가왔습니다. \"들어오시지요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그리고 불가에 앉으세요. 우드코트 씨, 내가 들은 바로는 의사라고 하시던데. 이 계집애를 좀 봐주시고, 정신 차리게 할 무슨 방법이 없는지 봐주시겠습니까? 이 애한테 내가 특별히 원하는 편지가 어딘가 있어요. 그 애 상자 안에는 없으니 분명 몸에 지니고 있을 텐데, 어찌나 비틀리고 잔뜩 움켜쥐고 있는지 다치게 하지 않고 다루기가 어렵군요.\"\n\n우리 셋은 함께 집 안으로 들어갔습니다. 춥고 으슬으슬한데도 밤새 사람들이 깨어 있었던 탓에 공기가 텁텁했지요. 문 뒤 복도에는 회색 외투를 입은, 겁먹고 슬퍼 보이는 작은 남자가 서 있었는데, 천성이 공손해 보였고 말투도 온순했습니다.\n\n\"아래층으로 가시지요, 버킷 씨.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"숙녀분께서는 앞쪽 부엌을 양해해 주시겠지요. 거기를 평소 거실로 쓰고 있습니다. 뒤쪽은 거스터의 침실인데, 거기서 저렇게, 가엾게도, 끔찍할 만큼 난리를 치고 있어요!\"\n\n우리는 스내그스비 씨를 뒤따라 아래층으로 내려갔습니다. 그 작은 남자가 곧 그 사람이라는 걸 알게 됐지요. 앞쪽 부엌에서는 스내그스비 부인이 불가에 앉아 있었는데, 두 눈이 몹시 빨갛고 얼굴은 무척이나 매서운 표정이었습니다.\n\n\"여보.\" 스내그스비 씨가 우리 뒤로 들어서며 말했습니다. \"이 기나긴 밤 중에 단 한순간만이라도—너무 까다롭게 굴지 말고, 여보—적대를 거두어 주오. 여기 버킷 경위와 우드코트 씨, 그리고 어느 숙녀분이 오셨소.\"\n\n그녀는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는지라 무척 놀란 표정으로, 유독 나를 매섭게 노려보았습니다.\n\n\"여보.\" 스내그스비 씨가 마치 외람된 짓이라도 하는 양 문 옆 가장 외진 구석에 앉으며 말했습니다. \"어쩌면 당신은 어째서 버킷 경위와 우드코트 씨와 어느 숙녀분이 이 시각에 커시터 가의 쿡스 코트로 우리를 찾아왔느냐고 내게 물을지도 모르겠소. 나는 모르오. 조금도 짐작이 안 가오. 설령 듣게 된다 해도 나는 이해할 가망이 없을 테니, 차라리 듣지 않는 편이 낫겠소.\"\n\n그가 머리를 손에 괴고 앉은 모습이 어찌나 참담해 보이고, 나는 어찌나 달갑잖은 손님처럼 보였는지, 내가 사과를 하려던 참에 버킷 씨가 그 일을 떠맡았습니다.\n\n\"자, 스내그스비 씨.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"당신이 할 수 있는 최선은 우드코트 씨와 함께 가서 댁의 거스터를 돌보는 겁니다—\"\n\n\"내 거스터라니요, 버킷 씨!\" 스내그스비 씨가 외쳤습니다. \"계속하시오, 선생, 계속해요. 이제 그것까지 내 죄로 뒤집어쓰겠군요.\"\n\n\"그리고 촛불을 들어주거나,\" 버킷 씨는 자기 말을 바로잡지도 않고 이어갔습니다. \"그 애를 붙들어주거나, 시키는 대로 무엇이든 거들어주는 겁니다. 그렇게 기꺼이 해줄 사람이 세상에 또 없지요. 당신은 점잖고 상냥한 사람이고, 남을 헤아릴 줄 아는 그런 마음을 지녔으니까요. 우드코트 씨, 저 애를 좀 봐주시겠습니까? 그리고 그 애한테서 편지를 얻어낼 수 있거든, 되도록 빨리 내게 건네주시겠습니까?\"\n\n두 사람이 나가자 버킷 씨는 나를 불가 구석에 앉히고 젖은 신발을 벗기게 했는데, 그 신발을 난로 가리개 위에 거꾸로 얹어 말리면서 내내 이야기를 했습니다.\n\n\"저기 스내그스비 부인이 손님을 반기는 낯빛이 아니라고 조금도 마음 쓰지 마세요, 아가씨. 부인이 완전히 오해하고 있어서 그러니까요. 부인은 그게 오해라는 걸, 평소 생각을 반듯하게 정리하는 숙녀로서는 달갑지 않을 만큼 빨리 깨닫게 될 겁니다. 내가 지금부터 부인한테 설명할 테니까요.\" 그는 젖은 모자와 숄을 손에 든 채, 제 몸이 한 무더기 물기 그 자체인 모습으로 난롯가에 서서 스내그스비 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰습니다. \"자, 내가 부인께 처음으로 드릴 말씀은, 이를테면 매력이라 할 만한 걸 지닌 기혼 여성으로서 말이죠—'믿어주오, 그 모든 사랑스러운' 어쩌고 하는, 그 노래 잘 아시잖아요, 부인이 상류 사회와 남이라고 내게 우긴들 소용없으니까요—매력 말입니다, 자신감을 가질 만한 그런 매력과 끌림을 지닌 분으로서, 부인은 일을 저질렀다는 겁니다.\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 자못 놀란 표정으로, 조금 누그러져 더듬더듬 버킷 씨가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말이냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"버킷 씨가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말이냐고요?\" 그가 되받았습니다. 그가 이야기하는 내내 편지가 발견되기를 귀 기울이고 있다는 걸 그 얼굴에서 알 수 있었지요. 나도 무척 마음이 떨렸습니다. 그게 얼마나 중요한 것인지 그제야 알았으니까요. \"내가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 알려드리지요, 부인. 가서 『오셀로』 공연을 보세요. 그게 부인한테 딱 맞는 비극입니다.\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 뜨끔한 듯 왜냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"왜냐고요?\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"조심하지 않으면 부인도 그 꼴이 될 테니까요. 아니, 내가 이렇게 말하는 바로 이 순간에도 부인 마음이 이 젊은 아가씨에 대해 무엇으로부터 온전히 자유롭지 못한지 나는 압니다. 헌데 이 젊은 아가씨가 누구인지 내가 말씀드릴까요? 자, 보세요, 부인은 내가 지성적인 여성이라 부르는 그런 사람—굳이 따지자면 영혼이 몸에 비해 너무 커서 몸을 비비적댈 만큼이지요—이고, 나를 알고, 나를 마지막으로 어디서 봤는지, 그 자리에서 무슨 이야기가 오갔는지 기억하실 겁니다. 안 그래요? 그렇지요! 아주 좋습니다. 이 젊은 아가씨가 바로 그 젊은 아가씨입니다.\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 그 말뜻을 그때의 나보다 더 잘 알아듣는 것 같았습니다.\n\n\"그리고 터피—부인이 조라고 부르는 그 애—도 바로 그 일에 얽혀 있었고, 다른 일이 아니었습니다. 부인이 아는 그 법률 필경사도 바로 그 일에 얽혀 있었고, 다른 일이 아니었지요. 부인의 남편도, 부인의 증조부만큼이나 아무것도 모른 채, 바로 그 일에(고인이 된 토킹혼 씨, 그의 가장 큰 단골 손님 때문에) 얽혀 있었고, 다른 일이 아니었습니다. 그리고 그 일에 얽힌 사람이라면 모조리 다 바로 그 일이었지, 다른 일이 아니었어요. 그런데도 부인의 그 매력을 지닌 기혼 여성이 눈을(그리고 반짝이는 눈동자까지) 질끈 감고는, 그 곱게 빚은 머리를 벽에다 들이박고 있는 겁니다. 원, 부끄러운 줄 아세요! (나는 이쯤이면 우드코트 씨가 그걸 손에 넣었으리라 생각하고 있었습니다.)\"\n\n스내그스비 부인은 고개를 가로젓고 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔습니다.\n\n\"이게 전부일까요?\" 버킷 씨가 격앙되어 말했습니다. \"아니죠. 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보세요. 바로 그 일에 얽힌, 다른 일이 아닌, 또 한 사람, 비참한 처지에 놓인 사람이 오늘 밤 여기 와서 댁의 하녀와 이야기하는 게 목격됩니다. 그리고 그녀와 댁의 하녀 사이에 종이 한 장이 건네지는데, 내가 당장 백 파운드라도 내놓을 만한 종이지요. 부인은 어떻게 합니까? 숨어서 그들을 지켜보다가 그 하녀를 덮칩니다—그 애가 무슨 증세가 있는지, 사소한 일에도 그게 도진다는 걸 뻔히 알면서요—그것도 그토록 소스라치게, 그토록 매섭게 덮친 통에, 맙소사, 그 애 입에서 나올 말에 한 목숨이 달려 있을지도 모르는데 그 애가 정신을 놓아버리고 그대로 있는 겁니다!\"\n\n이제 그가 하는 말이 어찌나 절절하게 진심이던지, 나는 나도 모르게 두 손을 맞잡았고 방이 내게서 빙그르르 멀어지는 듯했습니다. 하지만 멈췄지요. 우드코트 씨가 들어와 그의 손에 종이 한 장을 쥐여주고는 다시 나갔습니다.\n\n\"자, 스내그스비 부인, 부인이 할 수 있는 유일한 보상은,\" 버킷 씨가 그것을 휙 훑어보며 말했습니다. \"내가 이 젊은 아가씨와 여기서 단둘이 한마디 나누게 해주는 겁니다. 그리고 저 옆 부엌에 있는 저 신사분께 부인이 줄 수 있는 도움이 뭔지 아시거나, 그 애를 정신 차리게 할 만한 무언가가 딱 하나라도 떠오른다면, 부디 가장 빠르고 가장 잘 해주세요!\" 순식간에 그녀는 나갔고, 그는 문을 닫았습니다. \"자, 아가씨, 마음을 다잡고 정신을 꼭 차리고 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"이게 누구 필체죠?\"\n\n어머니의 것이었습니다. 구겨지고 찢긴 종잇조각에 연필로 쓴 글씨였는데, 물기에 얼룩져 있었지요. 편지처럼 대충 접혀, 내 후견인 댁으로 내게 부쳐진 것이었습니다.\n\n\"이 필체를 아는군요.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그리고 내게 읽어줄 만큼 마음이 단단하다면, 그렇게 해주세요! 다만 한 마디 한 마디 정확히 해야 합니다.\"\n\n그것은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 서로 다른 때에 나뉘어 쓴 것이었습니다. 나는 다음을 읽었습니다.\n\n나는 두 가지 목적으로 그 오두막에 갔다. 첫째는, 할 수만 있다면 그 사랑하는 아이를 한 번만 더 보려고—그러나 그저 보기만 하려고—그 애에게 말을 걸거나 내가 가까이 있음을 알리지는 않으려고. 다른 목적은, 추적을 따돌리고 자취를 감추려는 것이었다. 그 어미가 한 몫을 두고 그녀를 탓하지 말아라. 그녀가 내게 베푼 도움은, 이것이 그 사랑하는 아이를 위한 일이라는 나의 가장 굳은 다짐을 듣고서 베푼 것이다. 그녀의 죽은 아이를 기억하겠지. 남자들의 동의는 내가 돈으로 샀지만, 그녀의 도움은 거저 받은 것이다.\n\n\"'나는 갔다.' 이건,\" 내 동행이 말했습니다. \"그녀가 거기서 쉴 때 쓴 겁니다. 내가 짐작했던 걸 뒷받침하는군요. 내가 옳았어요.\"\n\n다음 것은 또 다른 때에 쓴 것이었습니다.\n\n나는 먼 거리를, 여러 시간 동안 헤맸고, 내가 곧 죽으리라는 걸 안다. 이 거리들! 나에게는 죽는 것 말고는 아무 목적도 없다. 떠나올 때 나에게는 더 나쁜 목적이 있었지만, 그 죄까지 나머지에 보태는 것은 면하게 되었다. 추위와 젖음과 피로만으로도 내가 죽은 채 발견될 충분한 까닭이 되겠지만, 나는 이것들로 고통받으면서도 다른 것들로 죽을 것이다. 나를 지탱해 주던 모든 것이 한꺼번에 무너지고, 내가 공포와 양심으로 죽는 것이 마땅했다.\n\n\"기운을 내세요.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"이제 몇 마디 안 남았습니다.\"\n\n그것 역시 또 다른 때에 쓴 것이었습니다. 보아하니, 거의 어둠 속에서 쓴 듯했지요.\n\n나는 자취를 감추려고 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했다. 그렇게 나는 곧 잊힐 것이고, 그분에게 가장 적은 수치만을 끼칠 것이다. 나에게는 나를 알아볼 만한 어떤 것도 지니고 있지 않다. 이 종이를 이제 내려놓는다. 거기까지 갈 수만 있다면, 내가 누울 그 자리는 줄곧 내 마음속에 있었다. 잘 있어라. 용서해다오.\n\n버킷 씨는 팔로 나를 부축하여 의자에 가만히 내려 앉혔습니다. \"기운 내세요! 내가 모질다고 여기지 마세요, 아가씨, 하지만 견딜 만하다 싶거든 곧바로 신발을 신고 채비를 갖추세요.\"\n\n나는 그가 시키는 대로 했지만, 거기서 오랫동안 홀로 남아 불행한 어머니를 위해 기도했습니다. 다들 가엾은 그 애한테 매달려 있었고, 우드코트 씨가 그들에게 이래라저래라 일러주며 그 애에게 자주 말을 거는 소리가 들렸지요. 마침내 그가 버킷 씨와 함께 들어와서, 그 애에게 부드럽게 말을 거는 것이 중요하니 우리가 알아내고자 하는 것이 무엇이든 내가 그 애한테 물어보는 게 가장 좋겠다고 했습니다. 그 애를 다독여 놀라게 하지만 않는다면 이제 질문에 대답할 수 있으리라는 건 의심의 여지가 없었지요. 버킷 씨가 말하기로 질문은, 그 애가 어떻게 편지를 손에 넣었는지, 편지를 준 사람과 그 애 사이에 무슨 일이 오갔는지, 그리고 그 사람이 어디로 갔는지였습니다. 나는 이 점들에 정신을 최대한 단단히 붙들고 그들과 함께 옆방으로 들어갔습니다. 우드코트 씨는 밖에 남아 있으려 했지만, 내가 청하자 우리와 함께 들어갔지요.\n\n가엾은 그 애는 사람들이 눕혀둔 바닥에 앉아 있었습니다. 다들 그 애 곁에 둘러서 있었으나, 그 애가 숨을 쉴 수 있도록 조금 떨어져 있었지요. 그 애는 예쁘지 않았고 약하고 가난해 보였지만, 아직 조금 정신 나간 듯하긴 해도 애처롭고 선한 얼굴을 하고 있었습니다. 나는 그 애 곁 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 그 가엾은 머리를 내 어깨에 기대게 했는데, 그러자 그 애는 한 팔을 내 목에 두르고 와락 울음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n\"가엾은 것.\" 나는 그 애 이마에 얼굴을 갖다 대며 말했습니다. 정말이지 나도 울며 떨고 있었으니까요. \"지금 너를 괴롭히는 게 모질게 보이겠지만, 우리가 이 편지에 관해 무언가를 알아내는 데 달린 것이 한 시간을 들여도 다 말 못 할 만큼 크단다.\"\n\n그 애는 자기는 나쁜 뜻이 없었다고, 나쁜 뜻이 없었다고, 스내그스비 부인! 하고 애처롭게 외치기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"우리 모두 그건 다 안단다.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"하지만 제발 그걸 어떻게 손에 넣었는지 말해다오.\"\n\n\"네, 친절하신 아씨, 그럴게요, 사실대로 말씀드릴게요. 정말로 사실대로 말할게요, 스내그스비 부인.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 안단다.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"그래, 어떻게 된 거니?\"\n\n\"심부름을 나갔었어요, 친절하신 아씨—날이 어두워진 지 한참 뒤에—아주 늦게요. 그러고 집에 돌아오니 흔해 빠진 행색의, 온통 젖고 진흙투성이인 사람이 우리 집을 올려다보고 있더라고요. 그 여자는 내가 문으로 들어가는 걸 보더니 나를 불러 세우고는, 여기 사느냐고 물었어요. 그래서 나는 그렇다고 했고, 그 여자는 이 근처에 한두 군데밖에 모르는데 길을 잃어 찾을 수가 없다고 했어요. 아, 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아! 사람들이 내 말을 안 믿을 거야! 그 여자는 나한테 나쁜 말 하나 안 했고, 나도 그 여자한테 나쁜 말 하나 안 했어요, 정말이에요, 스내그스비 부인!\"\n\n그 애가 이 대목을 넘어서기까지는 안주인이 그 애를 달래줄 필요가 있었는데—꽤나 뉘우치는 태도로 그렇게 해주었다는 걸 나는 말해두어야겠습니다.\n\n\"그 여자가 그곳들을 못 찾았구나.\" 나는 말했습니다.\n\n\"네!\" 그 애가 고개를 가로저으며 외쳤습니다. \"네! 못 찾았어요. 그리고 그 여자는 어찌나 기진하고, 다리를 절고, 비참하던지, 아 어찌나 가엾던지, 그 여자를 보셨더라면, 스내그스비 씨, 분명히 반 크라운은 주셨을 거예요, 정말로요!\"\n\n\"그래, 거스터.\" 그가 처음에는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 하며 말했습니다. \"나도 그랬으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"그런데도 어찌나 말씨가 고운지,\" 그 애가 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 나를 보며 말했습니다. \"듣는 사람 가슴이 미어질 정도였어요. 그러더니 나한테, 매장지로 가는 길을 아느냐고 물었어요. 그래서 어느 매장지냐고 물었지요. 그랬더니 가난한 사람들 매장지라고 했어요. 그래서 나는 나도 가난한 아이였는데 그건 교구마다 다르다고 말해줬어요. 하지만 그 여자는 여기서 그리 멀지 않은 가난한 사람들 매장지를 말하는 거라고, 아치 통로와 계단 하나와 쇠문이 있는 데라고 했어요.\"\n\n내가 그 애 얼굴을 살피며 계속 말하도록 다독이는데, 버킷 씨가 이 말을 받아들이는 표정에서 나는 경악이라고밖에 볼 수 없는 기색을 읽었습니다.\n\n\"아, 어떡해, 어떡해!\" 그 애가 두 손으로 머리카락을 뒤로 쓸어 넘기며 외쳤습니다. \"어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아! 그 여자가 말한 건 그 남자가 묻힌 매장지였어요—수면제를 먹은 그 남자요—스내그스비 씨가 집에 와서 우리한테 얘기해 준 그 사람, 나를 그렇게 무섭게 만든 그 사람이요, 스내그스비 부인. 아, 또 무서워. 나 좀 붙잡아 주세요!\"\n\n\"이제 한결 좋아졌잖니.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"제발, 제발 더 말해다오.\"\n\n\"네, 그럴게요, 그럴게요! 하지만 나한테 화내지 마세요, 친절하신 아씨, 내가 너무 아팠으니까요.\"\n\n그 애한테 화를 내다니, 가엾은 것!\n\n\"자! 이제 말할게요, 말할게요. 그래서 그 여자가 그곳을 찾는 길을 알려줄 수 있느냐고 했고, 나는 그렇다고 하면서 알려줬어요. 그러자 그 여자는 거의 앞을 못 보는 사람처럼 그런 눈으로 나를 보더니, 제 몸이 온통 휘청휘청하더라고요. 그러고는 그 편지를 꺼내 나한테 보여주면서, 이걸 우체국에 넣으면 지워지고 무시당해서 영영 부쳐지지 않을 거라고, 그러니 자기한테서 이걸 받아 부쳐주겠느냐고, 심부름 삯은 그 집에서 치를 거라고 했어요. 그래서 나는 나쁜 일이 아니라면 그러겠다고 했고, 그 여자는 아니라고—나쁜 일이 아니라고 했어요. 그래서 나는 그걸 받았고, 그 여자는 나한테 줄 게 아무것도 없다고 했고, 나는 나도 가난하니 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 했어요. 그러자 그 여자는 신의 가호가 있기를, 하고는 갔어요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 여자가 갔니—\"\n\n\"네.\" 그 애가 질문을 앞질러 외쳤습니다. \"네! 내가 알려준 길로 갔어요. 그러고 나서 나는 들어왔는데, 스내그스비 부인이 어디선가 내 뒤로 와서 나를 붙들었고, 나는 무서웠어요.\"\n\n우드코트 씨가 그 애를 내게서 다정하게 떼어내 맡았습니다. 버킷 씨는 나를 감싸주었고, 우리는 곧바로 거리로 나섰습니다. 우드코트 씨가 머뭇거렸지만, 나는 \"지금 나를 두고 가지 마세요!\" 하고 말했고, 버킷 씨도 \"우리와 함께 있는 게 나을 겁니다, 당신이 필요할지도 모르니까요. 지체하지 맙시다!\" 하고 덧붙였습니다.\n\n그 걸음에 대해 나는 더없이 어수선한 인상만을 간직하고 있습니다. 밤도 낮도 아니었고, 아침이 밝아오고 있었지만 가로등은 아직 꺼지지 않았고, 진눈깨비가 여전히 내리고 있었고 모든 길이 그것으로 깊이 잠겨 있었던 것이 기억납니다. 추위에 떨며 거리를 지나가던 몇몇 사람들이 기억납니다. 젖은 지붕들, 막혀 터질 듯한 도랑과 빗물받이들, 우리가 밟고 지난 거뭇한 얼음과 눈 더미들, 우리가 지나간 안마당의 비좁음이 기억납니다. 그러면서도 가엾은 그 애가 여전히 내 귓가에서 또렷하고 분명하게 자기 이야기를 하고 있는 것 같았고, 그 애가 내 팔에 기대고 있는 것이 느껴졌고, 얼룩진 집들의 정면이 사람의 형상을 띠고 나를 바라보았으며, 거대한 수문들이 내 머릿속에서, 혹은 허공에서 열렸다 닫혔다 하는 것 같았고, 그 허깨비 같은 것들이 현실보다 더 실체 같았던 것이 기억납니다.\n\n마침내 우리는 어둡고 음산한 지붕 덮인 통로 아래 서게 되었는데, 쇠문 위로 등불 하나가 타오르고 아침이 희미하게 비집고 들어오는 곳이었습니다. 문은 닫혀 있었지요. 그 너머는 매장지였습니다—밤이 아주 더디게 꿈틀거리는 끔찍한 곳이었지만, 멸시받은 무덤과 비석 더미가 어렴풋이 보였고, 그것들은 창마다 흐릿한 불빛 몇 개를 단 더러운 집들에 둘러싸여 있었으며, 그 집들의 벽에는 짙은 습기가 병처럼 배어 나오고 있었습니다. 그 문 옆 계단 위, 어디에나 배어 나와 튀어 흐르는 그런 곳의 무서운 물기에 흠뻑 젖은 채, 나는 가엾고 끔찍한 비명과 함께 한 여인이 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다—죽은 아이의 어머니인 제니였습니다.\n\n나는 앞으로 달려갔지만 그들이 나를 막았고, 우드코트 씨는 내가 그 형상에게 다가가기 전에 버킷 씨가 하는 말을 잠깐만 들어달라고 더없이 간절하게, 눈물까지 글썽이며 애원했습니다. 나는 그렇게 했지요, 그랬다고 생각합니다. 그렇게 했습니다, 분명히 그랬습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양, 잠깐만 생각해 보면 내 말을 이해할 겁니다. 그들은 오두막에서 옷을 바꿔 입었어요.\"\n\n그들은 오두막에서 옷을 바꿔 입었다. 나는 그 말을 마음속으로 되뇔 수 있었고, 그 말 자체가 무슨 뜻인지 알았지만, 다른 어떤 연관 속에서도 거기에 아무 의미를 붙이지 못했습니다.\n\n\"그러고는 하나가 돌아왔고,\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"하나는 계속 갔지요. 그리고 계속 간 쪽은 속이려고 합의된 어느 길까지만 가다가, 들판을 가로질러 집으로 돌아갔습니다. 잠깐만 생각해 보세요!\"\n\n이것도 나는 마음속으로 되뇔 수 있었지만, 그게 무슨 뜻인지는 도무지 알 수 없었습니다. 나는 내 앞 계단에 죽은 아이의 어머니가 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다. 그녀는 한 팔을 쇠문 창살에 감아 그것을 끌어안은 듯한 모습으로 거기 누워 있었지요. 그토록 얼마 전에 내 어머니에게 말을 걸었던 그녀가 거기 누워 있었습니다. 비탄에 잠긴, 비바람을 가릴 데 없는, 정신을 놓은 가엾은 것이 거기 누워 있었습니다. 내 어머니의 편지를 가져온 그녀, 어머니가 어디 있는지에 대한 유일한 실마리를 내게 줄 수 있는 그녀, 우리가 그토록 멀리까지 찾아 헤맨 그분을 구하고 살려내도록 우리를 이끌어줄 그녀, 내가 따라잡을 수 없는 어떤 식으로든 내 어머니와 얽힌 까닭에 이런 지경에 이르렀고 바로 그 순간 우리 손이 닿지 않고 도움이 미치지 않는 곳으로 넘어가고 있을지도 모르는 그녀가, 거기 누워 있었는데, 그들이 나를 막았습니다! 나는 우드코트 씨의 얼굴에 어린 엄숙하고 가엾어하는 표정을 보았으나 이해하지 못했습니다. 그가 다른 한 사람의 가슴에 손을 대어 그를 붙드는 것을 보았으나 이해하지 못했습니다. 나는 그가 무언가에 대한 경의로 매서운 공기 속에 모자를 벗고 서 있는 것을 보았습니다. 하지만 이 모든 것에 대한 내 이해력은 사라지고 없었지요.\n\n나는 그들 사이에 이런 말이 오가는 것까지 들었습니다. \"그녀를 가게 할까요?\"\n\n\"가는 게 낫겠지요. 저 여자한테 먼저 손을 대야 할 사람은 그녀입니다. 우리보다 더 높은 권리가 그녀에게 있어요.\"\n\n나는 문으로 다가가 몸을 굽혔습니다. 무거운 머리를 들어 올리고, 길게 늘어진 축축한 머리카락을 옆으로 치우고, 얼굴을 돌렸습니다. 그건 내 어머니였습니다, 차갑게 식어 죽은.\n\n이야기의 다른 대목으로 넘어가겠습니다. 나는 주위 사람들의 다정함에서, 떠올릴 때마다 가슴이 먹먹해지는 위안을 얻었습니다. 나 자신에 관한 이야기는 이미 충분히 했고 앞으로도 할 이야기가 많으니, 내 슬픔만 붙들고 늘어지지는 않겠습니다. 나는 한 차례 앓았습니다만 오래 가지는 않았습니다. 사람들의 따뜻한 마음에 대한 기억을 깨끗이 눌러둘 수만 있다면 이 병 이야기조차 꺼내지 않았을 것입니다.\n\n이야기의 다른 대목으로 넘어가겠습니다.\n\n내가 앓는 동안 우리는 여전히 런던에 머물렀고, 그곳에 우드코트 부인이 후견인의 초대를 받아 우리와 함께 지내러 와 있었습니다. 후견인이 보기에 내가 예전처럼 그와 이야기를 나눌 만큼 건강하고 명랑해졌다고 여겼을 때―그가 내 말을 믿어주기만 했다면 나는 더 일찍 그럴 수 있었을 텐데―나는 다시 일감을 잡고 그의 곁 의자에 앉았습니다. 시간은 그가 직접 정했고, 우리 둘뿐이었습니다.\n\n\"트롯 아주머니.\" 후견인이 입맞춤으로 나를 맞으며 말했습니다. \"투덜방에 다시 온 걸 환영하네, 그래. 펼쳐 보일 계획이 하나 있어, 우리 꼬마 아가씨. 나는 이곳에 머물 작정이야, 어쩌면 여섯 달, 어쩌면 더 길게―형편 닿는 대로. 한마디로, 한동안 여기 아주 눌러앉을 셈이지.\"\n\n\"그럼 그동안 황폐한 집은 떠나는 건가요?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야? 황폐한 집은.\" 그가 대꾸했습니다. \"제 앞가림을 제가 하는 법을 배워야지.\"\n\n그의 목소리가 서글프게 들리는 듯해서 그를 바라보았더니, 그 다정한 얼굴이 더없이 환한 미소로 밝아 있었습니다.\n\n\"황폐한 집은.\" 그가 거듭 말했는데, 이번에는 목소리가 서글프게 들리지 않았습니다. \"제 앞가림을 제가 하는 법을 배워야지. 거기는 에이다한테서 너무 멀어, 그래. 그리고 에이다한테는 네가 무척 필요하단다.\"\n\n\"후견인다우세요.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"우리 둘 다 기쁘게 놀랄 일을 마련하시려고 그런 생각까지 다 하시다니요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 사심 없는 일도 아니야, 얘야, 네가 그 미덕으로 나를 치켜세울 작정이라면 말이지. 네가 줄곧 길 위에 나가 있으면 나랑 함께 있는 일이 드물 테니까. 게다가 나는 가엾은 릭과 이렇게 서먹해진 처지에, 에이다 소식을 되도록 많이, 되도록 자주 듣고 싶구나. 그 애만이 아니라 그 친구 소식도, 가엾은 녀석 말이야.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에 우드코트 씨를 만나셨어요, 후견인?\"\n\n\"우드코트 씨라면 아침마다 만나지, 더든 아주머니.\"\n\n\"그분이 여전히 리처드를 두고 똑같은 말을 하시던가요?\"\n\n\"그대로야. 그 친구한테 무슨 직접적인 몸의 병이 있는 건 모르겠다더구나. 오히려 그런 병은 없다고 믿고 있어. 그런데도 그 친구가 마음에 걸린다는 거야. 안 걸릴 사람이 누가 있겠니?\"\n\n내 사랑하는 아이는 요즈음 날마다, 어떤 때는 하루에 두 번씩 우리를 보러 왔습니다. 하지만 우리는 이것이 내가 완전히 회복할 때까지만 이어지리란 것을 처음부터 내다보고 있었습니다. 그 애의 뜨거운 마음이 사촌 존을 향한 애정과 고마움으로 예전과 다름없이 가득하다는 것을 우리는 잘 알고 있었고, 리처드가 그 애더러 발길을 끊으라고 어떤 명령을 내린 것이 아니라는 점도 우리는 인정했습니다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 그 애가 우리 집 방문을 자제하는 것이 그에 대한 자기 도리의 한 부분이라 여긴다는 것도 우리는 알았습니다. 후견인의 섬세한 마음은 이를 곧 알아채고, 그 애가 옳다고 생각한다는 뜻을 그 애에게 전하려 애썼습니다.\n\n\"가엾고 불운하고 그릇된 리처드.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그 미망에서 언제나 깨어날까요!\"\n\n\"지금은 그럴 처지가 아니야, 얘야.\" 후견인이 대답했습니다. \"고통이 커질수록 그 친구는 나를 더 멀리할 거야. 자기 고통의 그 거대한 빌미를 대표하는 인물로 나를 삼아버렸으니까.\"\n\n나는 한마디 보태지 않을 수 없었습니다. \"그렇게 터무니없이!\"\n\n\"아아, 트롯 아주머니, 트롯 아주머니.\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다. \"잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서 터무니없지 않은 게 어디 있겠니! 꼭대기에도 부조리와 불의, 한복판과 밑바닥에도 부조리와 불의, 처음부터 끝까지―끝이라는 게 있기나 하다면―부조리와 불의뿐인데, 늘 그 언저리를 맴도는 가엾은 릭이 어떻게 거기서 이치를 뽑아내겠니? 그 친구가 가시덤불에서 포도를, 엉겅퀴에서 무화과를 거둘 수 없는 건, 옛 시절 더 나이 든 사람들이 그러지 못한 거나 매한가지지.\"\n\n리처드 이야기가 나올 때마다 보여주는 그의 너그러움과 배려가 어찌나 가슴에 사무치던지, 나는 이 화제에서는 늘 금세 입을 다물고 말았습니다.\n\n\"대법원장님이나, 부원장님들이나, 대법원의 그 거창한 대포들이 죄다 모인다 해도, 자기네 소송인 가운데 한 사람이 이렇게 터무니없고 부당한 꼴을 본다면 한없이 놀랄 테지.\" 후견인이 말을 이었습니다. \"그 학식 높은 양반들이 자기네 가발에 뿌리는 분으로 이끼장미를 키워내기 시작하면, 그때는 나도 놀라기 시작하마!\"\n\n그는 바람이 어느 쪽에서 부는지 보려고 창 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리다 말고, 대신 내 의자 등받이에 기댔습니다.\n\n\"자, 자, 꼬마 아가씨! 계속하자꾸나, 그래. 이 바위는 시간과 운수와 희망 어린 형편에 맡길 수밖에 없어. 우리가 거기에 에이다를 좌초시켜선 안 돼. 그 애도, 그 친구도, 또 한 번 벗과 헤어지는 일이라면 아주 작은 가능성조차 감당할 수 없으니까. 그래서 나는 우드코트한테 특별히 당부했고, 이제 너한테도 특별히 당부하마, 얘야. 릭한테 이 문제를 꺼내지 말아라. 그냥 두렴. 다음 주든, 다음 달이든, 내년이든, 늦든 이르든, 그 친구는 더 맑은 눈으로 나를 보게 될 게다. 나는 기다릴 수 있어.\"\n\n하지만 나는 이미 그와 이 문제를 의논했노라고 털어놓았습니다. 그리고 내 짐작에 우드코트 씨도 그랬을 것이라고 했습니다.\n\n\"그렇다고 하더구나.\" 후견인이 대답했습니다. \"아주 좋아. 그 사람도 자기 뜻을 밝혔고, 더든 아주머니도 자기 뜻을 밝혔으니, 그 이야긴 더 할 게 없겠다. 자, 이제 우드코트 부인 이야기를 해보자. 그분이 마음에 드니, 얘야?\"\n\n뜬금없이 불쑥 튀어나온 이 물음에, 나는 그분이 무척 마음에 들고 예전보다 한결 다정해진 것 같다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각해.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"족보 얘기가 줄어서? 모건 앱―그 이름이 뭐더라?―얘기가 덜해서 말이지?\"\n\n내 말뜻이 바로 그것이었다고 나는 인정했습니다. 비록 그 양반 이야기를 더 많이 들었을 때도 그분은 아주 해롭잖은 인물이긴 했지만요.\n\n\"그래도, 어쨌든, 그 양반은 제 고향 산속에 그냥 있는 편이 낫겠어.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"네 말에 동의한다. 그러면 꼬마 아가씨, 한동안 우드코트 부인을 여기 머물게 하는 것보다 더 나은 방도가 있겠니?\"\n\n없습니다. 그런데도―\n\n후견인은 내가 무슨 말을 할지 기다리며 나를 바라보았습니다.\n\n나는 할 말이 없었습니다. 적어도 입 밖에 낼 만한 말은 마음속에 없었습니다. 다른 누군가가 함께 지냈다면 더 나았을지도 모른다는 어렴풋한 느낌이 들긴 했지만, 그 까닭은 나 자신에게조차 도무지 설명할 수 없었습니다. 설령 나 자신에게는 설명할 수 있다 해도, 다른 누구에게는 분명 그러지 못했을 것입니다.\n\n\"보렴.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"우리 동네는 우드코트가 다니는 길목이니, 그 사람이 마음 내킬 때마다 어머니를 보러 여기 올 수 있어. 두 사람한테 다 반가운 일이지. 게다가 부인은 우리한테 친숙하고 너를 아주 좋아하잖니.\"\n\n그렇습니다. 부정할 수 없는 일이었습니다. 나는 거기에 반대할 말이 없었습니다. 더 나은 방도를 내놓을 수도 없었지만, 마음이 영 편치는 않았습니다. 에스더, 에스더, 왜 그럴까? 에스더, 생각해봐!\n\n\"정말 아주 좋은 계획이에요, 후견인. 이보다 나을 수는 없겠어요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 꼬마 아가씨?\"\n\n정말입니다. 나 자신에게 그 도리를 다그친 뒤로 잠깐 생각할 틈이 있었으니, 나는 정말로 확신했습니다.\n\n\"좋아.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그렇게 하자. 만장일치로 가결.\"\n\n\"만장일치로 가결.\" 나는 다시 일감을 손에 들며 그렇게 되뇌었습니다.\n\n마침 내가 수를 놓고 있던 것은 그의 책상에 덮을 덮개였습니다. 슬픈 여행을 떠나기 전날 밤에 한쪽에 치워두었다가 다시 손대지 못했던 것이었지요. 나는 이제 그것을 그에게 보여주었고, 그는 무척 감탄했습니다. 무늬를 설명하고 차차 드러날 멋진 효과를 죄다 일러준 다음, 나는 우리가 나누던 마지막 화제로 되돌아가기로 했습니다.\n\n\"에이다가 우리를 떠나기 전에 우드코트 씨 이야기를 하실 때, 그분이 다른 나라에서 오래 시험 삼아 일해볼 거라 생각하신다고 하셨잖아요. 그 뒤로 그분께 조언을 해주셨나요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 꼬마 아가씨, 꽤 자주.\"\n\n\"그분이 그렇게 하기로 정하셨대요?\"\n\n\"아닌 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 다른 길이 열린 건가요?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"글쎄―그래―어쩌면.\" 후견인이 아주 신중한 투로 대답을 시작했습니다. \"지금부터 반년쯤 뒤에, 요크셔의 어느 곳에 빈민을 돌볼 의료 담당자를 한 사람 두기로 되어 있어. 번창하는 고장에다 자리도 쾌적하지―시내를 가르는 물줄기와 거리들, 읍내와 들판, 방앗간과 황야가 있는 곳이야. 그런 사람한테 길이 열리는 자리인 듯싶더구나. 내가 말하는 사람이란, 그 희망과 목표가 더러는 (대개 사람들이 그렇듯이 말이야) 보통 수준을 넘어서지만, 그것이 다른 데로 이어지지 않더라도 유익하고 좋은 봉사의 길로 드러나기만 한다면 그 보통 수준만으로도 충분히 높다고 여길 사람을 말해. 너그러운 영혼은 다 야심이 있게 마련이지, 아마. 하지만 그 위로 발작하듯 날아오르려 애쓰는 대신, 그런 길에 차분히 자기를 맡기는 야심―그게 바로 내가 아끼는 종류야. 그게 우드코트 같은 사람의 야심이지.\"\n\n\"그분이 그 자리를 얻으실까요?\" 내가 물었습니다.\n\n\"글쎄다, 꼬마 아가씨.\" 후견인이 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"내가 점쟁이가 아니니 자신 있게 말할 순 없다만, 그러리라 본다. 그 사람 평판은 아주 높아. 그 고장 사람들이 그 난파선에 타고 있었고, 이상하게 들리겠지만, 가장 훌륭한 사람한테 가장 좋은 기회가 온다고 나는 믿는단다. 그게 무슨 대단한 자리라고 생각하면 안 돼. 아주아주 평범한 일이야, 얘야. 일은 산더미인데 보수는 쥐꼬리만 한 자리지. 하지만 거기에 더 나은 것들이 모여들 거라고, 그건 충분히 바랄 만하지.\"\n\n\"우드코트 씨가 뽑힌다면, 그 고장 빈민들은 그 선택을 두고 고마워할 까닭이 있겠네요, 후견인.\"\n\n\"네 말이 옳다, 꼬마 아가씨. 틀림없이 그럴 게야.\"\n\n우리는 그 이야기를 더 하지 않았고, 그도 황폐한 집의 앞날에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았습니다. 하지만 내가 상복을 입고 그의 곁에 자리를 잡은 것은 그때가 처음이었으니, 그 때문이려니 하고 나는 생각했습니다.\n\n이제 나는 날마다 내 사랑하는 아이를, 그 애가 사는 침침하고 어두운 구석으로 찾아가기 시작했습니다. 아침이 으레 가는 때였지만, 한 시간쯤 짬이 난다 싶으면 나는 보닛을 쓰고 부랴부랴 챈서리 거리로 달려갔습니다. 두 사람은 어느 시각에든 나를 보면 어찌나 반가워하던지, 또 내가 문을 열고 들어오는 소리가 나면(완전히 내 집처럼 드나들었기에 나는 노크라곤 하지 않았습니다) 어찌나 얼굴이 환해지던지, 나는 아직은 폐가 되지 않을까 하는 걱정이 들지 않았습니다.\n\n그렇게 갈 때면 나는 리처드가 자리에 없는 경우를 자주 보았습니다. 어떤 때는 서류로 뒤덮여 한 번도 치워진 적 없는 그 책상 앞에서 소송 서류를 쓰거나 읽고 있었습니다. 어떤 때는 볼스 씨 사무실 문가에서 서성이는 그를 마주치기도 했습니다. 어떤 때는 동네에서 빈둥거리며 손톱을 물어뜯는 그와 마주치기도 했습니다. 또 자주, 내가 그를 처음 보았던 곳 근처, 링컨스 인을 헤매는 그와 마주치곤 했는데, 아, 어쩌면 그리도 달라졌던지, 어쩌면 그리도 달라졌던지!\n\n에이다가 그에게 가져다준 돈이, 해 진 뒤 볼스 씨 사무실에서 타오르던 그 촛불과 함께 녹아 사라지고 있다는 것을 나는 잘 알았습니다. 처음부터 큰 액수도 아니었던 데다 그는 빚을 안고 결혼했으니, 볼스 씨가 어깨로 수레바퀴를 떠받친다는―여전히 내 귀에 들려오던―말이 무슨 뜻인지를 이제는 모를 수 없었습니다. 내 사랑하는 아이는 더할 나위 없는 살림꾼이 되어 악착같이 아끼려 애썼지만, 그들이 날마다 점점 더 가난해진다는 것을 나는 알고 있었습니다.\n\n그 애는 그 비참한 구석에서 아름다운 별처럼 빛났습니다. 그 애가 그곳을 꾸미고 빛내니 그곳은 아주 딴 데가 되었습니다. 집에 있을 때보다 핼쑥해지고, 그토록 명랑하고 희망에 차 있을 때 자연스럽다 여겼던 것보다 조금 더 조용해지긴 했지만, 그 애의 얼굴에는 그늘이 어찌나 없던지, 나는 그 애가 리처드를 향한 사랑에 눈이 멀어 그의 파멸로 치닫는 행로를 보지 못하는 게 아닐까 하고 반쯤 믿게 되었습니다.\n\n이런 인상에 사로잡혀 있던 무렵의 어느 날, 나는 그들과 저녁을 함께하러 갔습니다. 사이먼즈 인으로 꺾어 드는데, 마침 나오던 자그마한 플라이트 양과 마주쳤습니다. 그녀는 여전히 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인이라 부르는 두 사람에게 격식 갖춘 방문을 하고 오는 길이었고, 그 의식에서 더없는 만족을 얻은 모양이었습니다. 에이다가 이미 일러주기를, 그녀는 월요일마다 다섯 시에 찾아오는데, 다른 때는 결코 달지 않는 작고 하얀 나비 매듭 하나를 보닛에 더 달고, 서류를 잔뜩 담은 가장 큰 손가방을 팔에 끼고 온다고 했습니다.\n\n\"아, 얘야!\" 그녀가 입을 열었습니다. \"어찌나 반가운지! 안녕하세요! 만나서 정말 기뻐요. 우리 흥미로운 잔다이스 피후견인들을 만나러 가는 길이죠? 그렇고말고요! 우리 미인은 집에 있어요, 얘야, 당신을 보면 황홀해할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 리처드는 아직 안 들어왔나요?\" 내가 물었습니다. \"잘됐네요, 제가 좀 늦은 게 아닐까 걱정했거든요.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아직 안 들어왔어요.\" 플라이트 양이 대답했습니다. \"오늘 법정에서 긴 하루를 보냈답니다. 거기 볼스랑 같이 있는 걸 두고 왔어요. 당신은 볼스를 좋아하지 않으면 좋겠는데요? 볼스를 좋아하지 마세요. 위험한 사람이에요!\"\n\n\"이젠 리처드를 그 어느 때보다 자주 보시겠네요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"내 사랑.\" 플라이트 양이 대답했습니다. \"날마다, 시시각각이지요. 대법원장 책상 위의 그 끌림에 대해 내가 들려준 얘기 기억나죠? 얘야, 나 다음으로 그 사람이 법정에서 가장 한결같은 소송인이에요. 우리 작은 모임에 아주 즐거움을 주기 시작했답니다. 무척 정다운 작은 모임이지요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n가엾고 미친 그 입에서 이런 말을 듣자니 비참했습니다. 비록 놀랄 일은 아니었지만요.\n\n\"한마디로, 나의 소중한 벗.\" 플라이트 양이 후견하는 듯하면서도 은밀한 태도로 입술을 내 귀에 들이대며 말을 이었습니다. \"비밀을 하나 알려줘야겠어요. 나, 그 사람을 내 유언 집행인으로 삼았어요. 지명하고, 선임하고, 임명했답니다. 내 유언장에다요. 그-럼요.\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그-럼요.\" 플라이트 양이 가장 우아한 말씨로 거듭 말했습니다. \"내 유언 집행인, 재산 관리인, 양수인으로요. (우리 대법원 표현이랍니다, 내 사랑.) 내가 닳아 없어진다 해도 그 사람이라면 그 판결을 지켜볼 수 있겠다 싶었지요. 출석이 그토록 한결같으니까요.\"\n\n그를 떠올리니 한숨이 나왔습니다.\n\n\"한때는 가엾은 그리들리를 지명하고, 선임하고, 임명할 작정이었답니다.\" 플라이트 양이 그 한숨에 화답하듯 말했습니다. \"그이도 아주 한결같았거든요, 사랑스러운 아가씨. 정말이지 더없이 모범적이었어요! 하지만 그이는 닳아 없어졌지요, 가엾은 사람. 그래서 그 후임을 임명한 거예요. 발설하지 말아요. 이건 비밀이니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 손가방을 조심스레 조금 열어, 안에 든 접힌 종이 한 장을 자기가 말한 그 임명장이라며 내게 보여주었습니다.\n\n\"또 다른 비밀이 있어요, 얘야. 내 새 수집품을 늘렸답니다.\"\n\n\"정말요, 플라이트 양?\" 내가 말했습니다. 그녀의 속내를 관심 어린 기색으로 받아주는 것이 그녀를 얼마나 기쁘게 하는지 알았기에 그렇게 말한 것이지요.\n\n그녀는 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕였고, 얼굴은 어두워지며 침울해졌습니다. \"두 마리가 더 늘었어요. 나는 그것들을 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인이라 부른답니다. 다른 새들과 함께 다 가둬뒀어요. 희망, 기쁨, 청춘, 평화, 안식, 생명, 먼지, 잿더미, 황폐, 궁핍, 파멸, 절망, 광기, 죽음, 교활, 어리석음, 말, 가발, 누더기, 양피지, 약탈, 판례, 횡설수설, 허튼소리, 시금치와 함께요!\"\n\n가엾은 그녀는 내가 그녀에게서 본 가장 괴로운 표정으로 내게 입을 맞추고 제 갈 길을 갔습니다. 자기 새들의 이름을 죽 외워 내리는 그 모양새가, 마치 제 입에서 나오는 이름조차 듣기가 두려운 듯해서, 나는 온몸이 오싹했습니다.\n\n이것은 방문을 앞둔 마음을 북돋아주는 채비는 못 되었고, 리처드가(나보다 한두 분 뒤에 도착했습니다) 볼스 씨를 데려와 우리 저녁 식사를 함께하게 했을 때 나는 그의 동석을 사양했더라도 좋았을 것입니다. 아주 소박한 식사였는데도, 에이다와 리처드는 몇 분 동안 함께 방을 비우고 우리가 먹고 마실 것을 차리는 일을 거들었습니다. 볼스 씨는 그 틈을 타 나직한 목소리로 나와 잠시 이야기를 나누었습니다. 그는 내가 앉아 있던 창가로 다가와 사이먼즈 인 이야기부터 꺼냈습니다.\n\n\"공무에 매인 인생이 아니라면 따분한 곳이지요, 서머슨 양.\" 볼스 씨가 검은 장갑으로 유리를 문질러 나를 위해 더 맑게 닦아주며 말했습니다.\n\n\"여기는 볼 게 별로 없네요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"들을 것도 없지요, 아가씨.\" 볼스 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"이따금 음악이 좀 흘러들긴 합니다만, 우리 법조계는 음악과는 거리가 멀어서 금세 내쫓아버린답니다. 잔다이스 씨가 친구분들이 바라는 만큼 건강하시길 빕니다만?\"\n\n나는 볼스 씨에게 고맙다고 하며 그분은 아주 건강하시다고 했습니다.\n\n\"나 자신은 그분의 벗들 축에 끼는 영광을 누리지 못합니다.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그리고 우리 업계 사람들이 그런 자리에서는 더러 곱지 않은 눈길로 비치곤 한다는 것도 압니다. 그렇지만 좋은 평이든 나쁜 평이든, 온갖 편견 아래서든(우리는 편견의 희생자랍니다), 우리가 갈 길은 모든 일을 떳떳이 처리하는 것이지요. 시(C) 씨는 안색이 어떻습디까, 서머슨 양?\"\n\n\"무척 안 좋아 보여요. 끔찍하게 초조해하시고요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n그는 그 길고 검은 형상을 그 낮은 방의 천장에 닿을 듯 세운 채 내 뒤에 서서, 제 얼굴의 여드름을 무슨 장식이라도 되는 양 만지작거리며, 천성에 인간다운 정념이나 감정이라곤 하나도 없는 듯 속으로 가라앉은 평탄한 목소리로 말했습니다.\n\n\"우드코트 씨가 시 씨를 돌보고 계신 줄로 압니다만?\" 그가 다시 말했습니다.\n\n\"우드코트 씨는 그분의 사심 없는 벗이에요.\" 내가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"내 말은 직업상의 돌봄, 의료상의 돌봄을 뜻한 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"그것이 불행한 마음에는 별 도움이 못 되지요.\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n어찌나 굼뜨고, 어찌나 게걸스럽고, 어찌나 피 한 방울 없이 수척한지, 나는 리처드가 이 조언자의 눈길 아래서 시들어가고 있고, 그에게는 흡혈귀 같은 데가 있다는 느낌이 들었습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 볼스 씨가, 자기의 무딘 촉감으로는 검은 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼었든 벗었든 마찬가지라는 듯, 장갑 낀 두 손을 아주 천천히 비비며 말했습니다. \"이건 시 씨에게 잘못 권해진 결혼이었습니다.\"\n\n나는 그 이야기는 논하지 않게 해달라고 청했습니다. 두 사람은 아주 어렸을 때 약혼했고(나는 조금 발끈하며 그에게 말했습니다), 그때는 앞날이 훨씬 더 곱고 환했노라고요. 리처드가, 지금은 그의 인생을 어둡게 하는 그 불행한 영향에 자신을 내맡기기 전이었노라고요.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 볼스 씨가 다시 맞장구쳤습니다. \"그래도 모든 일을 떳떳이 처리한다는 견지에서, 허락하신다면, 서머슨 양, 나는 이것이 정말이지 매우 잘못 권해진 결혼이라 본다는 점을 말씀드리겠습니다. 이 의견은 내가 마땅히 나 자신을 지켜야 할 시 씨의 친척들에게 빚지고 있을 뿐 아니라, 내 자신의 평판에도 빚지고 있지요. 그것은 점잖음을 지키려 애쓰는 업계 사람으로서 나에게 소중하고, 내가 약간의 자립을 마련해주려 분투하고 있는 집안의 세 딸에게 소중하며, 감히 말하건대, 내가 부양하는 영예를 누리는 늙으신 아버지에게도 소중합니다.\"\n\n\"그건 아주 다른 결혼, 훨씬 더 행복하고 더 나은 결혼, 완전히 딴 결혼이 될 거예요, 볼스 씨.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"만약 리처드가 당신이 그와 함께 매달려 있는 그 치명적인 추구에 등을 돌리도록 설득될 수만 있다면요.\"\n\n볼스 씨는 검은 장갑 한쪽에 대고 소리 없는 기침을―아니, 헐떡임을―한 뒤, 그것조차 전적으로 부정하지는 않는다는 듯 고개를 숙였습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그럴지도 모르지요. 그리고 시 씨의 이름을 그토록 잘못 권해진 방식으로 짊어진 그 젊은 아가씨가―시 씨의 친척들에게 내가 마땅히 다해야 할 도리로서 그 말을 다시 꺼내더라도 당신은 분명 나를 탓하지 않으시겠지요―더없이 품위 있는 젊은 아가씨라는 점은 나도 선뜻 인정합니다. 업무 탓에 나는 직업상의 성격으로가 아니고는 일반 사교계에 많이 어울려본 적이 없습니다만, 그래도 그분이 더없이 품위 있는 젊은 아가씨라는 것쯤은 알아볼 능력이 있다고 믿습니다. 미모로 말하자면 나 자신은 그것을 가늠하는 사람이 못 되고, 어릴 적부터 거기엔 별로 마음을 쓴 적이 없습니다만, 그분이 그 점에서도 못지않게 합당하리라 짐작은 합니다. 인(Inn)의 서기들 사이에서도 그렇게 여긴다(고 들었습니다)는데, 그건 나보다 그들 쪽에 더 어울리는 사안이지요. 시 씨가 자기 이익을 추구하는 것에 관해서는―\"\n\n\"오! 그분의 이익이라뇨, 볼스 씨!\"\n\n\"실례합니다.\" 볼스 씨가 똑같이 속으로 가라앉은 무덤덤한 투를 그대로 이어가며 대꾸했습니다. \"시 씨는 이 소송에서 다투어지는 어떤 유언장들에 따라 어떤 이익을 취하지요. 우리가 쓰는 용어랍니다. 시 씨가 자기 이익을 추구하는 것에 관해서는, 서머슨 양, 내가 당신을 뵙는 기쁨을 처음 누렸을 때 말씀드린 바 있지요. 모든 일을 떳떳이 처리하고 싶다는 내 바람에서요―나는 바로 그 말을 썼는데, 마침 그 뒤에 그것을 내 일기에 적어두었고 그 일기는 언제든 제시할 수 있습니다―시 씨가 자기 이익을 지켜보겠다는 원칙을 세웠고, 내 의뢰인이 부도덕한(다시 말해 불법적인) 성질이 아닌 원칙을 세웠을 때 그것을 실행하는 것은 나에게 맡겨진 일이라고 말씀드렸지요. 나는 그것을 실행해왔고, 지금도 실행하고 있습니다. 그러나 나는 어떤 경우에도 시 씨의 어느 친척에게도 일을 얼버무리지 않을 것입니다. 잔다이스 씨에게 떳떳했던 것처럼, 당신에게도 그렇습니다. 그렇게 하는 것을 나는 직업상의 도리로 여깁니다. 누구에게 청구할 수 있는 일은 아니지만요. 듣기에 거북할지라도 나는 떳떳이 말합니다. 나는 시 씨의 일이 매우 나쁜 형국에 있다고 보고, 시 씨 자신도 매우 나쁜 처지에 있다고 보며, 이것을 지극히 잘못 권해진 결혼이라 여긴다고요. 제가 여기 있나요, 선생? 예, 고맙습니다. 여기 있습니다, 시 씨. 서머슨 양과 유쾌한 담소를 나누는 기쁨을 누리고 있었지요. 그 점은 당신께 매우 감사드려야겠습니다, 선생!\"\n\n그는 방으로 들어서며 말을 건넨 리처드에게 대답하느라 이렇게 말을 끊었습니다. 이때쯤 나는 볼스 씨가 자기 자신과 자기 점잖음을 지키는 그 빈틈없는 방식을 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에, 우리의 가장 어두운 두려움이 그 의뢰인의 진행과 꼭 발맞추어 나아가고 있을 뿐이라는 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었습니다.\n\n우리는 저녁상에 둘러앉았고, 나는 리처드를 근심스레 살필 기회를 얻었습니다. 볼스 씨가(식사하느라 장갑을 벗었습니다) 그 작은 식탁에서 내 맞은편에 앉았는데도 나는 방해받지 않았으니, 그가 고개를 들기는 했더라도 주인의 얼굴에서 눈을 한 번이라도 뗐을지 의심스러웠기 때문입니다. 리처드는 여위고 기운이 없어 보였고, 옷차림은 단정치 못했으며, 태도는 멍하니 딴 데 가 있었고, 이따금 억지로 기운을 짜내다가도 또 어느 틈엔가 둔중한 골몰 속으로 다시 가라앉았습니다. 그토록 즐겁던 그 크고 빛나던 두 눈가에는, 그 눈을 아주 딴판으로 바꿔놓은 핏기 없음과 안절부절이 어려 있었습니다. 늙어 보였다는 표현은 쓸 수가 없습니다. 노년과는 다른 청춘의 폐허라는 것이 있는데, 리처드의 청춘과 젊은 아름다움은 죄다 바로 그런 폐허로 무너져 내렸던 것입니다.\n\n그는 거의 먹지 않았고 무엇을 먹든 무심해 보였으며, 예전보다 훨씬 더 참을성이 없었고, 에이다에게마저 퉁명스러웠습니다. 처음에는 그 옛날의 명랑한 태도가 죄다 사라진 줄 알았지만, 그것은 이따금씩 그에게서 환히 비쳐 나왔으니, 마치 거울 속에서 내 옛 얼굴이 잠깐잠깐 나를 내다보던 것을 더러 보았던 것처럼요. 그의 웃음도 아주 떠나버린 것은 아니었지만, 그것은 기쁜 소리의 메아리 같았고, 그런 것은 언제나 서글픈 법입니다.\n\n그래도 그는 변함없이 기뻐했습니다. 그 옛날의 다정한 투로 내가 거기 있는 것을 반가워했고, 우리는 옛 시절 이야기를 즐겁게 나누었습니다. 이런 이야기는 볼스 씨에게는 흥미롭지 않은 듯했으나, 그래도 그는 이따금 헐떡임을 내뱉었는데 그게 그의 미소였던 모양입니다. 그는 저녁을 먹은 뒤 이내 일어서더니, 숙녀분들이 허락하신다면 자기 사무실로 물러가겠다고 했습니다.\n\n\"늘 업무에 헌신이로군, 볼스!\" 리처드가 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 시 씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"의뢰인의 이익은 결코 소홀히 해선 안 되지요, 선생. 동업자들과 사회 전반에서 좋은 이름을 지키고자 하는, 나 같은 업계 사람의 머릿속에서는 그것이 가장 우선이랍니다. 지금 이 유쾌한 담소의 기쁨을 스스로 마다하는 것이, 당신 자신의 이익과 전혀 무관하지만은 않을지도 모르지요, 시 씨.\"\n\n리처드는 그 점은 충분히 확신한다고 말하며 볼스 씨를 등불로 비추어 배웅했습니다. 돌아와서는 볼스가 좋은 친구라고, 미덥다고, 하겠다고 한 일은 해내는 사람이라고, 정말이지 아주 좋은 친구라고 우리에게 거듭 말했습니다. 그가 어찌나 도전적으로 그러던지, 나는 그가 볼스 씨를 의심하기 시작한 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들었습니다.\n\n이윽고 그는 녹초가 되어 소파에 몸을 던졌습니다. 에이다와 나는 자질구레한 것들을 정리했는데, 그들에게는 방을 돌봐주는 여자 말고 다른 하인이 없었기 때문입니다. 내 사랑하는 아이는 그곳에 작은 코티지 피아노를 두고 있었는데, 조용히 앉아 리처드가 좋아하는 곡 몇 가지를 부르려 했습니다. 그가 등불이 눈을 아프게 한다고 투덜대서, 먼저 등불을 옆방으로 옮겨놓고요.\n\n나는 두 사람 사이, 내 사랑하는 아이 곁에 앉아, 그 애의 고운 목소리를 들으며 무척 우울해졌습니다. 리처드도 그랬으리라 생각합니다. 그 때문에 그가 방을 어둡게 한 것이라 생각합니다. 그 애는 한동안 노래를 부르다가도 틈틈이 일어서서 그에게 몸을 숙여 말을 건넸는데, 그러던 차에 우드코트 씨가 들어왔습니다. 그는 리처드 곁에 앉아, 반은 장난스럽게 반은 진지하게, 아주 자연스럽고 편안한 태도로, 그의 기분이 어떤지, 하루 종일 어디 있었는지를 알아냈습니다. 이내 그는 달빛 환하고 바람 시원한 밤이니 다리 한 곳을 잠깐 함께 거닐자고 제안했고, 리처드가 선뜻 응하여 둘은 함께 나갔습니다.\n\n그들은 여전히 피아노 앞에 앉은 내 사랑하는 아이와, 여전히 그 곁에 앉은 나를 남겨두고 떠났습니다. 그들이 나가자 나는 그 애의 허리에 팔을 둘렀습니다. 그 애는 왼손을 내 손에 얹었지만(나는 그쪽에 앉아 있었습니다) 오른손은 건반 위에 둔 채, 어떤 음도 누르지 않으면서 그 위를 자꾸자꾸 더듬었습니다.\n\n\"에스더, 내 사랑.\" 그 애가 침묵을 깨고 말했습니다. \"리처드가 가장 편안할 때도, 내 마음이 그이를 두고 가장 놓일 때도, 다 그이가 앨런 우드코트와 함께 있을 때야. 그건 다 너한테 고마운 일이지.\"\n\n나는 사랑하는 아이에게 그럴 리가 없다고 일러주었습니다. 우드코트 씨는 그 애의 사촌 존의 집에 왔던 분이고 거기서 우리 모두를 알게 되었으며, 늘 리처드를 좋아했고 리처드도 늘 그를 좋아했으니―그러니 어쩌고저쩌고 하면서요.\n\n\"다 맞는 말이야.\" 에이다가 말했습니다. \"하지만 그이가 우리한테 그토록 헌신적인 벗인 건, 다 너 덕분이야.\"\n\n나는 사랑하는 아이가 자기 뜻대로 하게 두고 더는 그 이야기를 하지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다. 그래서 그렇게 말했습니다. 가볍게 말했습니다. 그 애가 떨고 있는 것을 느꼈기 때문입니다.\n\n\"에스더, 내 사랑. 나는 좋은 아내가 되고 싶어, 정말이지 아주아주 좋은 아내가. 네가 가르쳐줘야 해.\"\n\n내가 가르치다니! 나는 더 말하지 않았습니다. 건반 위에서 파닥이는 그 손을 보았고, 말해야 할 사람은 내가 아니라, 나에게 할 말이 있는 사람은 그 애라는 것을 알았기 때문입니다.\n\n\"내가 리처드와 결혼할 때, 그이 앞에 놓인 일을 몰랐던 건 아니야. 너랑 함께 오랫동안 더없이 행복했고, 그토록 사랑받고 보살핌을 받으며 근심이나 걱정이라곤 모르고 지냈지만, 그래도 나는 그이가 처한 위험을 알고 있었어, 에스더.\"\n\n\"알아, 알아, 내 사랑.\"\n\n\"우리가 결혼했을 때, 나는 그이의 잘못을 깨우쳐줄 수 있을지도 모른다고, 그이가 내 남편으로서 그 일을 새로운 눈으로 보게 될지도, 그래서 나를 위해 그 일을 더 필사적으로 좇지 않게 될지도 모른다고―지금 그러는 것과는 달리―작은 희망을 품었어. 하지만 그런 희망이 없었더라도, 나는 똑같이 그이와 결혼했을 거야, 에스더. 똑같이!\"\n\n한시도 가만있지 않던 그 손에 잠깐 깃든 단호함―이 마지막 말을 내뱉음으로써 솟아났다가 그 말과 함께 스러진 단호함―속에서, 나는 그 애의 간절한 어조가 진심임을 확인했습니다.\n\n\"내가 보는 것을 내가 못 본다거나, 네가 두려워하는 것을 내가 안 두려워한다고는 생각하지 마, 내 사랑 에스더. 누구도 나만큼 그이를 잘 이해할 순 없어. 세상에 살았던 가장 위대한 지혜라 해도, 내 사랑이 아는 만큼 리처드를 잘 알기는 어려울 거야.\"\n\n그 애는 어찌나 겸손하고 부드럽게 말하던지, 또 그 떨리는 손은 소리 없는 음 위를 오가며 어찌나 큰 동요를 드러내던지! 내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아이!\n\n\"나는 날마다 그이의 가장 나쁜 모습을 봐. 잠든 그이를 지켜보지. 그이 얼굴의 변화를 하나하나 다 알아. 하지만 리처드와 결혼할 때 나는 굳게 다짐했어, 에스더, 하늘이 도와주신다면, 그이가 한 일을 두고 내가 슬퍼한다는 걸 결코 그이한테 보이지 않겠다고, 그래서 그이를 더 불행하게 만들지 않겠다고. 나는 그이가 집에 돌아왔을 때 내 얼굴에서 어떤 근심도 보지 않길 바라. 나를 볼 때, 그이가 내게서 사랑했던 것을 보길 바라. 나는 이걸 하려고 그이와 결혼한 거야, 그리고 이게 나를 버티게 해.\"\n\n나는 그 애가 더 떠는 것을 느꼈습니다. 나는 아직 나올 말을 기다렸고, 이제 그것이 무엇인지 알아차리기 시작한 듯했습니다.\n\n\"그리고 또 한 가지가 나를 버티게 해, 에스더.\"\n\n그 애는 잠시 멈췄습니다. 말만 멈췄을 뿐, 손은 여전히 움직이고 있었습니다.\n\n\"나는 조금 앞을 내다보는데, 어떤 커다란 도움이 내게 올지 모르겠어. 그때 리처드가 나에게 눈길을 돌리면, 내 가슴에 무언가가 안겨 있을지도 몰라. 지금껏 나보다 더 잘 말하는, 나보다 더 큰 힘으로 그이에게 참된 길을 보여주고 그이를 되돌려놓을 무언가가.\"\n\n이제 그 애의 손이 멈췄습니다. 그 애가 두 팔로 나를 끌어안았고, 나도 두 팔로 그 애를 끌어안았습니다.\n\n\"그 작은 생명마저 안 된다면, 에스더, 그래도 나는 앞을 내다봐. 아주 먼 앞을, 여러 해, 또 여러 해 너머를 내다보며 생각해. 그때, 내가 늙어갈 무렵, 어쩌면 내가 죽은 뒤에, 아름다운 여인이, 그이의 딸이, 행복하게 시집가서 그이를 자랑스러워하고 그이에게 축복이 되어줄지도 모른다고. 아니면 너그럽고 용감한 남자가, 그이가 옛날에 그랬듯 잘생기고, 희망에 차 있고, 그이보다 훨씬 더 행복한 그런 남자가, 그이와 함께 햇살 속을 거닐며 그이의 센 머리를 공경하고 속으로 이렇게 말할지도 모른다고. '하느님 감사합니다, 이분이 제 아버지입니다! 치명적인 유산으로 무너졌다가, 저를 통해 회복되셨지요!'\"\n\n오, 나의 다정한 아이여, 내게 그토록 빠르게 뛰던 그 가슴은 어떤 가슴이었던가요!\n\n\"이런 희망들이 나를 떠받쳐, 사랑하는 에스더, 그리고 앞으로도 그러리란 걸 알아. 비록 가끔은 그 희망들마저, 리처드를 바라볼 때 솟아나는 어떤 두려움 앞에서는 나를 떠나버리지만.\"\n\n나는 사랑하는 아이를 달래보려 그것이 무엇이냐고 물었습니다. 그 애는 흐느끼고 울먹이며 대답했습니다. \"그이가 자기 아이를 보지 못하고 죽을지도 모른다는 두려움이야.\"\n\n내 사랑하는 아가씨가 환하게 밝혀주던 그 비참한 구석에서 보낸 나날은 내 기억에서 영영 바래지 않을 것입니다. 이제는 그곳을 다시 보지도 않고, 보고 싶지도 않습니다. 그 뒤로 딱 한 번 가보았을 뿐이지만, 내 기억 속에는 그 자리를 비추는 슬픈 영광이 깃들어 있고, 그 빛은 영원히 사라지지 않을 것입니다.\n\n물론 하루도 거르지 않고 그곳을 찾았습니다. 처음에는 두세 번쯤 스킴폴 씨가 거기 있는 걸 보았는데, 한가로이 피아노를 치며 늘 그렇듯 활기찬 어조로 떠들고 있었습니다. 그런데 그가 거기 있으면서 리처드를 더 가난하게 만들지 않을 리 없다는 점을 몹시 미심쩍게 여기는 데다가, 그의 태평한 명랑함이 내가 아는 에이다의 깊은 속내와 너무나 어긋나는 무언가를 품고 있는 것처럼 느껴졌습니다. 에이다도 나와 같은 심정이라는 걸 똑똑히 알 수 있었습니다. 그래서 한참을 고민한 끝에, 나는 스킴폴 씨를 따로 찾아가 조심스럽게 내 뜻을 설명해보기로 마음먹었습니다. 내가 그렇게 대담해질 수 있었던 건 무엇보다 사랑하는 아가씨를 생각해서였습니다.\n\n어느 날 아침, 나는 찰리를 데리고 서머스 타운으로 나섰습니다. 집이 가까워지자 그냥 돌아가고 싶은 마음이 강하게 들었는데, 스킴폴 씨에게 어떤 인상을 남기려는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 시도인지, 그리고 그가 나를 보기 좋게 꺾어버릴 가능성이 얼마나 큰지 절감했기 때문입니다. 하지만 여기까지 온 마당이니 끝까지 해보자고 생각했습니다. 나는 떨리는 손으로 스킴폴 씨의 문을 두드렸는데—말 그대로 손으로 두드렸으니, 문고리가 떨어져 나가고 없었기 때문입니다—한참을 옥신각신한 끝에 아일랜드 여인의 허락을 받고서야 들어갈 수 있었습니다. 그 여인은 내가 문을 두드릴 때 마당에 나와, 불을 지피려고 부지깽이로 물통 뚜껑을 부수고 있었습니다.\n\n스킴폴 씨는 자기 방 소파에 누워 플루트를 조금 불고 있다가, 나를 보더니 더없이 반가워했습니다. 그러면서 자기를 누가 맞이하면 좋겠느냐고 물었습니다. 진행 역으로 누구를 더 원하느냐는 것이었습니다. 희극 딸을 부를까, 미인 딸을 부를까, 감성 딸을 부를까? 아니면 세 딸을 한꺼번에 한 다발 꽃처럼 부를까?\n\n이미 반쯤 기가 꺾인 채로 나는, 허락만 해주신다면 오직 그분과만 이야기하고 싶다고 대답했습니다.\n\n\"친애하는 서머슨 양, 더없이 기쁘오! 물론이지.\" 그가 의자를 내 쪽으로 바싹 당기며 그 매혹적인 미소를 활짝 지으며 말했습니다. \"물론 용무는 아니겠지. 그럼 즐거운 일이로군!\"\n\n나는 용무로 온 건 분명히 아니지만, 그렇다고 썩 즐거운 일도 아니라고 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 친애하는 서머슨 양,\" 그가 더없이 솔직한 명랑함으로 말했습니다. \"그 얘긴 꺼내지 마시오. 즐겁지 않은 일을 어째서 굳이 입에 올리겠소? 나는 절대 그러지 않소. 게다가 그대는 어느 모로 보나 나보다 훨씬 더 유쾌한 사람이오. 그대는 완벽하게 유쾌하고, 나는 불완전하게 유쾌하지. 그러니 나조차 불쾌한 일을 입에 올리지 않는데, 하물며 그대가 어찌 올리겠소! 자, 이 문제는 이걸로 정리됐으니, 우리 다른 얘기나 합시다.\"\n\n당황스러웠지만, 나는 용기를 내어 그래도 그 이야기를 계속하고 싶다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤습니다.\n\n\"서머슨 양이 실수를 저지를 만한 사람이라고 여겼다면, 나는 이게 실수라고 생각했을 거요.\" 스킴폴 씨가 경쾌하게 웃으며 말했습니다. \"하지만 난 그렇게 생각하지 않소!\"\n\n\"스킴폴 씨,\" 나는 그를 향해 눈을 들며 말했습니다. \"당신은 세상의 흔한 일들엔 도통 어둡다고 자주 말씀하셨지요—\"\n\n\"우리의 은행가 친구 셋, 그러니까 파운드 군과 실링 군, 그리고 막내 동업자가 누구더라? 펜스 군? 그 얘기로군.\" 스킴폴 씨가 환하게 말했습니다. \"그 친구들에 대해선 아무것도 모르오!\"\n\n\"—그래서 그 점을 핑계 삼아 제 무례를 너그럽게 봐주시리라 믿습니다.\" 나는 말을 이었습니다. \"리처드가 예전보다 가난해졌다는 사실을, 부디 진지하게 아셔야 한다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"나도 그렇다고들 하더군.\"\n\n\"그리고 형편이 몹시 곤란해졌고요.\"\n\n\"내 처지랑 똑같구먼!\" 스킴폴 씨가 기쁜 낯빛으로 말했습니다.\n\n\"지금 이 일이 자연히 에이다에게 남모를 근심을 안겨주고 있어요. 게다가 손님들이 찾아와 무언가를 요구하지 않을 때 그 애가 덜 불안해하는 것 같고, 또 리처드는 늘 마음에 무거운 걱정 하나를 짊어지고 있으니—그래서 외람되지만 한 말씀 드리려고요. 만약 당신이—그러지—않으신다면—\"\n\n내가 그 말을 가까스로 꺼내려는 참에, 그가 내 두 손을 덥석 잡고 환한 얼굴로 더없이 발랄하게 내 말을 가로챘습니다.\n\n\"거기 가지 말라고? 물론이오, 친애하는 서머슨 양, 두말할 것 없소. 내가 왜 거길 가겠소? 나는 어딜 가든 즐거우려고 가는 거요. 고통을 맛보려고 어딜 가진 않소. 나는 즐거움을 위해 태어난 사람이니까. 고통은 나를 원할 때 제 발로 찾아오는 법이오. 그런데 요즘 사랑하는 리처드네 집에선 도무지 즐거움을 누리지 못했고, 그대의 실용적인 통찰이 그 까닭을 똑똑히 짚어주는구려. 우리 젊은 친구들이 한때 그토록 사람을 사로잡던 젊은 시의 정취를 잃어버리고는, '이 사람은 파운드를 원하는 자야' 하고 생각하기 시작했소. 사실 그렇소. 나는 늘 파운드를 원하지. 내가 쓰려는 게 아니라, 상인들이 늘 나한테서 파운드를 받으려 하니까. 그다음엔 우리 젊은 친구들이 속물이 되어, '이 사람은 파운드를 가졌던 자, 그걸 빌렸던 자야' 하고 생각하기 시작하지. 정말 그랬소. 나는 늘 파운드를 빌리니까. 그래서 우리 젊은 친구들이 산문으로 전락해버려서는(참으로 안타까운 일이오) 나에게 즐거움을 안겨주는 힘이 퇴화하고 만 거요. 그러니 내가 왜 그들을 보러 가겠소? 어불성설이오!\"\n\n이렇게 따져가며 나를 바라보던 그 환한 미소 사이로, 이제 사심 없는 자비의 표정이 떠올랐는데, 그 모습이 어찌나 놀랍던지요.\n\n\"게다가,\" 그가 가벼운 확신의 어조로 자기 논리를 이어갔습니다. \"고통을 맛보려고 어딜 가지 않는 내가—그건 내 존재의 본뜻을 거스르는 일이고, 끔찍한 짓이지—고통의 원인이 되려고 어딜 가야 할 까닭이 어디 있겠소? 지금처럼 마음이 흐트러진 우리 젊은 친구들을 보러 간다면, 나는 그들에게 고통을 안기게 될 거요. 나와 얽힌 기억들이 그들에겐 불쾌하게 다가올 테지. 그들은 '이 사람은 파운드를 가졌었는데 이제 파운드를 갚지 못하는 자야' 하고 말할지도 모르오. 물론 나는 갚을 수 없소. 그건 애초에 말도 안 되는 얘기지! 그러니 그들 곁에 가지 않는 것이야말로 친절이오—그래서 나는 가지 않겠소.\"\n\n그는 다정하게 내 손에 입을 맞추고 고맙다는 말로 끝을 맺었습니다. 서머슨 양의 그 빼어난 재치가 아니었다면 결코 이 점을 깨닫지 못했을 거라고 했습니다.\n\n나는 무척 당혹스러웠지만, 핵심만 얻어낸다면 그가 거기에 이르는 모든 과정을 아무리 괴상하게 비틀어놓든 별로 중요치 않다고 마음을 다잡았습니다. 그래도 다른 한 가지를 말하기로 작정한 터라, 그 일만큼은 그냥 넘어갈 수 없다고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"스킴폴 씨,\" 나는 말했습니다. \"방문을 마치기 전에 외람되지만 한 말씀 드려야겠습니다. 얼마 전 가장 확실한 소식통에게서, 그 가엾은 소년이 누구와 함께 황폐한 집을 떠났는지 당신이 알고 있었고, 그때 사례를 받았다는 사실을 듣고 몹시 놀랐습니다. 후견인께는 이 일을 말씀드리지 않았어요. 공연히 마음만 상하실까 봐서요. 하지만 당신께는, 제가 무척 놀랐다는 말씀은 드릴 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"아니라고? 정말로 놀랐단 말이오, 친애하는 서머슨 양?\" 그가 그 유쾌한 눈썹을 치켜올리며 캐묻듯 되물었습니다.\n\n\"크게 놀랐습니다.\"\n\n그는 더없이 호감 가고 익살스러운 표정으로 잠시 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하다가, 이내 포기하고 그 누구보다 사람을 끄는 태도로 말했습니다. \"내가 얼마나 어린애 같은 사람인지 알잖소. 왜 놀랐다는 거요?\"\n\n나는 그 문제를 시시콜콜 따지고 들기가 꺼려졌지만, 그가 정말로 궁금해하며 제발 말해달라고 하기에, 내가 쓸 수 있는 가장 부드러운 말로 그의 행동이 여러 도덕적 의무를 저버린 일처럼 보인다는 뜻을 전했습니다. 그는 이 말을 듣고 무척 재미있어하며 흥미를 보였고, 순진할 만큼 천진하게 \"아니, 정말로?\" 하고 말했습니다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 책임을 질 생각이 없는 사람이란 걸 알잖소. 나는 도저히 그런 걸 할 수 없는 사람이오. 책임이란 늘 내 위에 있거나—아니면 내 아래에 있는 것이었소.\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"어느 쪽인지조차 모르겠소. 하지만 친애하는 서머슨 양이(언제나 그 실용적인 양식과 명석함이 돋보이는 분이지) 이 일을 풀어놓는 방식을 헤아려보건대, 결국 주로 돈 문제인 것 같은데, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n나는 경솔하게도 거기에 어정쩡하게 그렇다고 동의하고 말았습니다.\n\n\"아하! 그럼 보시오,\" 스킴폴 씨가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했습니다. \"나는 그런 건 도무지 이해할 가망이 없는 사람이오.\"\n\n나는 자리에서 일어서며, 뇌물을 받고 후견인의 신뢰를 저버리는 건 옳지 않다고 넌지시 일렀습니다.\n\n\"친애하는 서머슨 양,\" 그가 자기만의 그 솔직한 쾌활함으로 되받았습니다. \"나는 뇌물에 넘어갈 수 있는 사람이 아니오.\"\n\n\"버킷 씨에게도요?\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"누구에게도 안 넘어가지. 나는 돈에 아무런 가치를 두지 않으니까. 돈에 관심도 없고, 돈을 알지도 못하고, 원하지도 않고, 가지고 있지도 않소—돈은 내게서 곧장 달아나버린다오. 그러니 내가 어떻게 뇌물에 넘어가겠소?\"\n\n나는 그 문제를 따져 논할 능력은 없었지만, 내 생각은 다르다는 뜻을 내비쳤습니다.\n\n\"오히려 정반대요,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그런 경우라면 나야말로 남보다 우월한 위치에 설 만한 사람이오. 그런 경우라면 나는 나머지 인류보다 높은 데 있소. 그런 경우라면 나는 철학적으로 행동할 수 있소. 나는 이탈리아 갓난아이가 포대기에 묶이듯 편견에 묶여 있지 않소. 나는 공기처럼 자유로운 사람이오. 카이사르의 아내처럼 의심을 훌쩍 뛰어넘은 곳에 있다고 느낀다오.\"\n\n깃털 공을 가지고 놀듯 이 문제를 이리저리 던지면서, 스스로를 설득해 보이는 그 가벼운 태도와 장난스러운 공정함에 견줄 만한 모습은, 다른 누구에게서도 결코 본 적이 없는 것이었습니다!\n\n\"이 일을 잘 보시오, 친애하는 서머슨 양. 여기 한 소년이 집에 받아들여져, 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여졌소. 소년이 침대에 누워 있는데, 한 남자가 들이닥치오—마치 '잭이 지은 집'처럼 말이지. 여기 그 남자가 있소. 집에 받아들여져 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여진 소년을 내놓으라고 요구하는 그 남자 말이오. 여기 은행권 한 장이 있소. 집에 받아들여져 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여진 소년을 내놓으라고 요구하는 그 남자가 내민 은행권이오. 여기 스킴폴이 있소. 집에 받아들여져 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여진 소년을 내놓으라고 요구하는 그 남자가 내민 은행권을 받는 그 스킴폴 말이오. 이것이 사실이오. 좋소. 그래서 스킴폴이 그 돈을 거절했어야 했소? 어째서 스킴폴이 그 돈을 거절했어야 한단 말이오? 스킴폴은 버킷에게 항변하지. '이게 무슨 돈이오? 도통 모르겠소, 나한테는 아무 쓸모가 없으니 도로 가져가시오.' 그래도 버킷은 스킴폴에게 받아달라고 간청하오. 편견에 묶이지 않은 스킴폴이 그걸 받아야 할 이유가 있겠소? 그렇소, 있소. 스킴폴은 그 이유들을 알아채지. 그게 무엇이겠소? 스킴폴은 스스로 따져보오. 이 사람은 길들여진 스라소니, 발 빠른 경찰관, 영리한 사람, 발상과 실행 양쪽에서 비범하게 집중된 정력과 대단한 치밀함을 지닌 인물이라고. 우리 친구와 적이 달아나면 우리를 위해 찾아주고, 우리가 도둑맞으면 재산을 되찾아주고, 우리가 살해당하면 속 시원히 복수해주는 자라고 말이오. 이 발 빠른 경찰관이자 영리한 사람은 자기 일을 해나가면서 돈에 대한 굳은 믿음을 얻었소. 그는 돈이 자기에게 무척 쓸모 있다는 걸 알고, 또 그것을 사회에 무척 쓸모 있게 쓰지. 그런데 내가 그 돈을 직접 원하는 마당에, 버킷의 그 믿음을 흔들어놓아야 하겠소? 내가 일부러 버킷의 무기 하나를 무디게 만들어야 하겠소? 내가 버킷의 다음 수사 작업을 아주 마비시켜놓아야 하겠소? 또 이런 것도 있소. 스킴폴이 그 돈을 받는 게 탓할 일이라면, 버킷이 그 돈을 내미는 것도 탓할 일이오—오히려 버킷 쪽이 훨씬 더 탓할 일이지. 그는 사정을 다 아는 사람이니까. 그런데 스킴폴은 버킷을 좋게 생각하고 싶소. 스킴폴은, 그 자그마한 자리에서나마, 만물이 두루 어우러지는 데에 자기가 버킷을 좋게 생각하는 것이 긴요하다고 여기오. 국가가 그에게 버킷을 믿으라고 명백히 요청하니, 그는 그대로 믿는 것이오. 그가 한 일이라곤 그게 전부요!\"\n\n이 장광설에 나는 도무지 대꾸할 말이 없어서 자리를 떴습니다. 그러나 더없이 기분이 좋아진 스킴폴 씨는, 내가 '꼬마 코빈시스' 하나만 데리고 집으로 돌아간다는 건 말도 안 된다며 직접 나를 바래다주었습니다. 그는 가는 길 내내 갖가지 유쾌한 이야기로 나를 즐겁게 해주었고, 헤어질 때는 우리 젊은 친구들에 관한 그 일을 자기 대신 알아내 준 내 그 빼어난 재치를 결코 잊지 않겠노라고 다짐했습니다.\n\n그 뒤로 스킴폴 씨를 다시 만나는 일이 없었으니, 내가 아는 그의 내력을 여기서 마저 끝맺어도 되겠습니다. 그와 후견인 사이에 냉랭한 기류가 생겼는데, 그 까닭은 주로 앞서 말한 사정들과, 리처드 일을 두고 그가 후견인의 간청을(이건 나중에 에이다에게 들어 알게 된 것입니다) 매정하게 무시한 데에 있었습니다. 그가 후견인에게 큰 빚을 지고 있었다는 점은 두 사람이 갈라선 일과는 아무 상관이 없었습니다. 그는 그로부터 오 년쯤 뒤에 죽으면서 일기를 남겼는데, 거기엔 편지들과 자기 생애를 정리하려는 다른 자료들이 함께 있었고, 그것이 책으로 출간되었습니다. 그 책은 그가 사랑스러운 아이 하나를 겨눈 인류의 합작 음모에 희생된 사람이었던 양 그려놓았습니다. 사람들은 무척 재미난 읽을거리로 여겼지만, 나는 책을 펼쳤다가 우연히 눈에 들어온 한 문장 말고는 더 읽지 않았습니다. 그 문장은 이러했습니다. \"잔다이스는 내가 아는 대다수 사람과 마찬가지로, 이기심의 화신이다.\"\n\n이제 나는 내 이야기에서 나 자신과 참으로 가깝게 맞닿은 대목에 이르렀는데, 정작 그 일이 벌어졌을 때 나는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았습니다. 내 가엾은 옛 얼굴과 얽혀 이따금 마음속에 되살아나곤 하던 자그마한 미련들이 있었지만, 그것들은 이미 지나가 버린 내 삶의 한 부분에 속한 것으로서만 되살아났을 뿐입니다—내 갓난 시절이나 어린 시절처럼 지나가 버린 것으로요. 그 일에 관한 나의 숱한 약한 마음들을 나는 하나도 감추지 않고, 기억이 떠올려주는 그대로 충실히 적어왔습니다. 그리고 이제 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 보이는 이 책의 마지막 구절에 이르기까지, 나는 한결같이 그렇게 하기를 바라고 또 그럴 작정입니다.\n\n세월은 미끄러지듯 흘러갔고, 나에게 털어놓은 희망에 의지하며 버티던 사랑하는 아가씨는, 그 비참한 구석에서 여전히 한결같이 아름다운 별이었습니다. 리처드는 갈수록 야위고 초췌해진 모습으로 날마다 법정을 맴돌았고, 소송이 거론될 가망이라곤 손톱만큼도 없는 줄 알면서도 온종일 멍하니 앉아 있다가, 그곳의 붙박이 구경거리 가운데 하나가 되어버렸습니다. 그곳 신사들 중에 그가 처음 발을 들여놓았을 적 모습을 기억하는 이가 한 사람이라도 있을까 싶었습니다.\n\n그는 자신의 고정관념에 어찌나 완전히 사로잡혀 있던지, 기분이 좋을 때면 \"우드코트가 아니었다면\" 지금 이렇게 맑은 공기를 마시지도 못했을 거라고 털어놓곤 했습니다. 그가 한 번에 몇 시간씩이나마 마음을 다른 데로 돌리고 그를 다시 일으켜 세울 수 있는 사람은 오직 우드코트 씨뿐이었는데, 우리를 몹시 놀라게 한 그 심신의 무기력에 가라앉을 때조차 그러했습니다. 게다가 그 무기력이 도지는 일은 세월이 갈수록 점점 잦아졌습니다. 그가 자신을 위해 오히려 더 필사적으로 잘못된 길을 좇는 거라던 사랑하는 아가씨의 말은 옳았습니다. 잃어버린 것을 되찾으려는 그의 갈망이 젊은 아내에 대한 슬픔으로 한층 더 격렬해져서, 마치 노름꾼의 광기처럼 변해버렸다는 데 나는 의심의 여지가 없습니다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이 나는 시도 때도 없이 그곳에 있었습니다. 밤에 그곳에 있을 때면 대개 찰리와 함께 마차를 타고 집으로 돌아왔습니다. 이따금 후견인이 근처에서 나를 마중 나오면, 우리는 함께 걸어서 집으로 돌아오곤 했습니다. 어느 날 저녁, 그는 여덟 시에 나를 만나기로 약속해두었습니다. 그날은 평소처럼 시간에 딱 맞춰 자리를 뜰 수가 없었는데, 사랑하는 아가씨를 위해 무언가를 만들던 중이라 손대고 있던 것을 마무리하려면 몇 바늘 더 떠야 했기 때문입니다. 하지만 약속 시각을 몇 분 앞두고 나는 자그마한 반짇고리를 챙겨 들고, 그 밤의 마지막 입맞춤을 사랑하는 아가씨에게 남긴 뒤 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔습니다. 땅거미가 진 터라 우드코트 씨가 나와 함께 가주었습니다.\n\n늘 만나기로 한 자리에 다다랐을 때—그곳은 바로 가까이였고, 우드코트 씨도 전에 여러 번 나와 동행한 적이 있었습니다—후견인은 거기 없었습니다. 우리는 삼십 분 동안 이리저리 거닐며 기다렸지만, 그의 자취는 보이지 않았습니다. 우리는 그가 사정이 생겨 오지 못했거나, 아니면 왔다가 그냥 가버렸으리라는 데 뜻을 모았고, 우드코트 씨가 나와 함께 걸어서 집까지 가자고 했습니다.\n\n늘 만나기로 한 자리까지 가던 그 아주 짧은 걸음을 빼면, 우리가 함께 걸어본 것은 그때가 처음이었습니다. 우리는 가는 길 내내 리처드와 에이다 이야기를 나누었습니다. 나는 그가 해준 일에 대해 말로 고마움을 전하지는 않았습니다—그때 그 일에 대한 내 감사의 마음은 어떤 말로도 다 담을 수 없을 만큼 부풀어 있었으니까요—그래도 내가 그토록 강하게 느끼던 마음을 그가 어느 정도는 헤아려주기를 바랐습니다.\n\n집에 다다라 위층으로 올라가 보니, 후견인은 나가고 없었고 우드코트 부인도 외출 중이었습니다. 우리가 들어선 곳은, 그 옛날 풋풋한 연인이었으나 지금은 너무도 달라진 남편이 된 그 사람을 사랑하는 아가씨가 어린 마음으로 택했을 때, 내가 얼굴 붉히던 그 아이를 데리고 들어왔던 바로 그 방이었습니다. 후견인과 내가 햇살을 받으며 떠나가는 두 사람을, 희망과 약속이 갓 피어난 그 모습을 지켜보았던 바로 그 방이었습니다.\n\n우리가 열린 창가에 서서 거리를 내려다보고 있을 때, 우드코트 씨가 나에게 말을 건넸습니다. 나는 순식간에 그가 나를 사랑한다는 걸 알았습니다. 나는 순식간에 흉터로 일그러진 내 얼굴이 그에게는 조금도 달라지지 않았다는 걸 알았습니다. 나는 순식간에 내가 동정이며 연민이라 여겼던 것이, 실은 헌신적이고 너그럽고 변함없는 사랑이었다는 걸 알았습니다. 아, 이제야 그걸 알다니 너무 늦었습니다, 너무 늦었어요, 너무 늦었습니다. 그것이 나에게 처음으로 떠오른, 은혜를 모르는 생각이었습니다. 너무 늦었다고요.\n\n\"내가 돌아왔을 때,\" 그가 나에게 말했습니다. \"떠날 때보다 조금도 더 가멸지 못한 채 돌아와서, 당신이 병상에서 갓 일어난 모습을 보았을 때, 그러면서도 남을 향한 그 다정한 배려로 그토록 빛나고 이기적인 생각이라곤 그토록 없는 모습을 보았을 때—\"\n\n\"오, 우드코트 씨, 그만, 그만하세요!\" 나는 그에게 간청했습니다. \"저는 당신의 그 높은 칭찬을 받을 자격이 없어요. 그때 저는 이기적인 생각을 참으로 많이 했답니다, 아주 많이요!\"\n\n\"하늘이 알다시피, 내 생의 사랑하는 사람이여,\" 그가 말했습니다. \"내 칭찬은 연인의 칭찬이 아니라 진실이오. 당신 주변의 모든 사람이 에스더 서머슨에게서 무엇을 보는지, 그녀가 얼마나 많은 마음을 어루만지고 일깨우는지, 얼마나 거룩한 흠모와 얼마나 큰 사랑을 얻는지, 당신은 모르오.\"\n\n\"오, 우드코트 씨,\" 나는 외쳤습니다. \"사랑을 얻는다는 건 대단한 일이에요, 사랑을 얻는다는 건 정말 대단한 일이에요! 저는 그것이 자랑스럽고, 그것으로 영광스러워요. 그 말을 들으니 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 이 눈물이 흐르네요—그 사랑을 얻었다는 기쁨, 그리고 그것을 더 받을 만하게 살지 못했다는 슬픔이요. 하지만 저는 당신의 사랑을 마음에 둘 처지가 못 됩니다.\"\n\n나는 한층 굳센 마음으로 그 말을 했습니다. 그가 나를 그렇게 칭찬하고, 자기가 한 말이 참이라는 믿음으로 그의 목소리가 떨리는 걸 들으니, 그 칭찬에 더 어울리는 사람이 되고 싶다는 마음이 솟았기 때문입니다. 그것만큼은 너무 늦지 않았습니다. 비록 오늘 밤 내 삶의 이 뜻밖의 한 장을 덮어버린다 해도, 나는 살아가는 내내 그 모든 것에 더 어울리는 사람이 될 수 있었습니다. 그리고 그렇게 생각하니 그것이 내게 위안이 되고 또 힘이 되어, 그에게서 비롯된 어떤 기품이 내 안에서 솟아오르는 것을 느꼈습니다.\n\n그가 침묵을 깼습니다.\n\n\"지금처럼 앞으로도 영원히 소중할 사랑하는 분에 대한 내 믿음을, 내가 너무도 초라하게 보이게 하는 셈이 될 거요\"—그가 그 말을 하던 깊은 진지함은 나를 단번에 굳세게 하면서도 눈물짓게 했습니다—\"내 사랑을 마음에 둘 처지가 못 된다고 그녀가 분명히 말한 뒤에도 내가 그 사랑을 들이댄다면 말이오. 사랑하는 에스더, 다만 이 말만은 하게 해주오. 내가 멀리 떠나며 품고 간 당신에 대한 그 애틋한 마음이, 집으로 돌아오니 하늘에 닿을 만큼 드높아져 있더이다. 나는 행운의 한 줄기 빛 속에 처음 서 있는 듯한 그 순간에 당신에게 이 말을 하리라고 늘 바라왔소. 그러면서도 그 말이 헛되이 끝날까 늘 두려워했소. 내 바람과 두려움이 오늘 밤 둘 다 이루어졌구려. 내가 당신을 괴롭히는군. 이만하면 충분히 말했소.\"\n\n그가 나를 천사라 여겨주던 그 모습 같은 무언가가 내 안으로 스며드는 듯했고, 나는 그가 겪은 그 상실이 너무도 안타깝게 느껴졌습니다! 그가 나에게 처음 그 연민을 내비쳤을 때 그러고 싶었던 것처럼, 나는 그의 괴로움을 덜어주고 싶었습니다.\n\n\"우드코트 씨,\" 나는 말했습니다. \"오늘 밤 우리가 헤어지기 전에, 제가 드려야 할 말씀이 하나 남아 있어요. 제가 바라는 만큼 잘 표현하지는 결코 못할 테고—앞으로도 결코 못하겠지만—그래도—\"\n\n말을 더 잇기 전에, 나는 그의 사랑과 그의 아픔에 더 어울리는 사람이 되어야 한다고 다시 한번 마음을 다잡아야 했습니다.\n\n\"—저는 당신의 너그러움을 마음 깊이 느끼고 있고, 그 기억을 죽는 순간까지 소중히 간직할 거예요. 제가 얼마나 달라졌는지 저는 너무도 잘 알고, 당신이 제 내력을 모르지 않는다는 것도 알고, 그토록 변함없는 그 사랑이 얼마나 고귀한 것인지도 압니다. 당신이 제게 해주신 그 말씀은 다른 어떤 입에서 나왔다면 저를 이토록 깊이 움직이지 못했을 거예요. 저에게 그만한 값어치를 안겨줄 입은 달리 없으니까요. 그 말씀은 헛되이 사라지지 않을 거예요. 그것이 저를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어줄 테니까요.\"\n\n그는 손으로 두 눈을 가리고 고개를 돌렸습니다. 내가 어찌 저 눈물을 받을 만한 사람이 될 수 있을까요?\n\n\"앞으로도 변함없이 함께할 우리의 교제 속에서—리처드와 에이다를 보살피면서, 그리고 부디 더 행복한 삶의 여러 장면 속에서도—혹시 당신이 제게서 예전보다 나아졌다고 진심으로 여길 만한 무언가를 발견하신다면, 그건 오늘 밤에서 싹튼 것이고 제가 당신께 빚진 것이라고 믿어주세요. 그리고 결코 믿지 마세요, 사랑하고 사랑하는 우드코트 씨, 제가 이 밤을 잊으리라고는, 또는 제 심장이 뛰는 한 당신께 사랑받았다는 그 자랑과 기쁨에 무감할 수 있으리라고는 결코 믿지 마세요.\"\n\n그가 내 손을 잡고 입을 맞추었습니다. 그는 다시 예전의 그 사람으로 돌아왔고, 나는 한층 더 용기를 얻었습니다.\n\n\"방금 하신 말씀을 듣고,\" 나는 말했습니다. \"당신이 그 일에 성공하셨기를 바라게 되네요.\"\n\n\"성공했소.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"그분을 잘 아는 당신이라면 능히 짐작할 만한 그런 도움을 잔다이스 씨에게서 받아, 나는 성공했소.\"\n\n\"그분께 하늘의 축복이 있기를.\" 나는 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했습니다. \"그리고 당신이 하시는 모든 일에 하늘의 축복이 함께하기를!\"\n\n\"그 바람 덕분에 나는 그 일을 더 잘 해낼 거요.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"그 덕분에 나는 이 새로운 임무를, 당신이 내게 맡긴 또 하나의 거룩한 소임처럼 받들며 시작하게 될 테니까.\"\n\n\"아! 리처드는요!\" 나도 모르게 외쳤습니다. \"당신이 떠나면 그이는 어떻게 하나요!\"\n\n\"아직은 떠날 필요가 없소. 설령 떠나야 한다 해도, 친애하는 서머슨 양, 나는 그를 저버리지 않을 거요.\"\n\n그가 나를 떠나기 전에, 한 가지 더 짚어두어야 할 일이 있다고 느꼈습니다. 받을 수 없는 그 사랑을, 만일 내가 마음속에 묻어둔다면 그 사랑에 더 어울리는 사람이 되지 못하리라는 걸 알았기 때문입니다.\n\n\"우드코트 씨,\" 나는 말했습니다. \"안녕히 가시라는 인사를 드리기 전에, 제 입으로 직접 들려드리면 당신이 기뻐하실 거예요. 제 앞에 또렷하고 환하게 펼쳐진 미래 속에서, 저는 더없이 행복하고 더없이 복되며, 후회할 것도 바랄 것도 없답니다.\"\n\n그건 정말로 자기에게 기쁜 소식이라고 그가 대답했습니다.\n\n\"저는 어린 시절부터,\" 나는 말했습니다. \"더없이 훌륭한 인간 한 분의 지칠 줄 모르는 자애를 받는 사람이었어요. 애정과 감사와 사랑의 온갖 끈으로 그분께 단단히 묶여 있어서, 제가 한평생을 바쳐 무엇을 한들 단 하루치의 마음조차 다 표현하지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"나도 그 마음을 함께 느끼오.\" 그가 대답했습니다. \"당신은 잔다이스 씨를 말하는 거로군요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그분의 미덕을 잘 알지요.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"하지만 제가 아는 만큼 그분 인품의 위대함을 아는 사람은 드물답니다. 그분의 가장 드높고 가장 훌륭한 자질이, 제가 이토록 행복해하는 그 미래를 빚어내신 일에서보다 더 환하게 드러난 적은 없었어요. 그리고 설령 당신의 그 더없는 경의와 존경이 이미 그분의 것이 아니었더라도—실은 그분의 것임을 저는 알지만—이 다짐과, 또 저를 위해 그것이 당신 마음속에 그분을 향해 불러일으켰을 그 감정으로 인해, 그분의 것이 되었으리라고 저는 생각해요.\"\n\n그는 진정으로, 진정으로 그러했을 거라고 열렬히 대답했습니다. 나는 다시 그에게 손을 내밀었습니다.\n\n\"안녕히 가세요.\" 나는 말했습니다. \"잘 가세요.\"\n\n\"앞엣것은 내일 다시 만날 때까지의 인사, 뒤엣것은 우리 사이에서 이 이야기를 영영 마무리하는 작별 인사로군요.\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"안녕히, 잘 가세요.\"\n\n그는 나를 떠났고, 나는 어두운 창가에 서서 거리를 바라보았습니다. 한결같고도 너그러운 그의 사랑이 너무도 갑작스레 내게 밀려든 터라, 그가 나를 떠난 지 일 분도 채 안 되어 내 굳센 마음은 또다시 무너졌고, 거리는 쏟아지는 눈물에 가려 보이지 않았습니다.\n\n그러나 그것은 후회와 슬픔의 눈물이 아니었습니다. 아니고말고요. 그는 나를 자기 생의 사랑하는 사람이라 불렀고, 지금 그러하듯 앞으로도 영원히 내가 그에게 소중하리라고 말했습니다. 나는 그 말을 들었다는 그 벅찬 기쁨을 내 심장이 다 담아내지 못할 것만 같았습니다. 처음의 그 걷잡을 수 없던 생각은 스러지고 없었습니다. 그 말을 듣기에 너무 늦지 않았으니, 그 말에 힘입어 선하고 참되고 감사하며 만족하는 사람이 되기에도 너무 늦지 않았기 때문입니다. 내 앞길은 얼마나 평탄한지요, 그의 길보다 얼마나 더 평탄한지요!\n\n그날 밤 나는 누구도 마주할 용기가 없었습니다. 심지어 내 모습을 들여다볼 용기조차 없었으니, 내 눈물이 나를 조금이나마 나무랄까 두려웠기 때문입니다. 나는 어둠 속에서 방으로 올라가, 어둠 속에서 기도하고, 어둠 속에 누워 잠을 청했습니다. 후견인의 편지를 읽는 데에는 어떤 불빛도 필요 없었으니, 나는 그 내용을 외우고 있었기 때문입니다. 나는 늘 간직해두는 자리에서 편지를 꺼내, 그 편지가 스스로 발하는 진실과 사랑의 맑은 빛으로 내용을 되뇌었고, 그것을 베개에 둔 채 잠이 들었습니다.\n\n이튿날 아침 나는 아주 일찍 일어나 찰리를 불러 산책을 나섰습니다. 우리는 아침 식탁에 올릴 꽃을 사 와서 꽂아 두었고, 더없이 분주하게 움직였습니다. 어찌나 일찍 일어났는지 아침 식사 전에 찰리에게 공부를 가르칠 시간까지 넉넉히 남았습니다. 찰리는(예전부터 그토록 부실하던 문법 실력은 조금도 나아지지 않은 채로) 큰 박수를 받으며 그 시간을 견뎌냈고, 우리는 온통 대단한 사람들이 된 기분이었습니다. 후견인이 나타나서 말했습니다. \"이런, 꼬마 아씨, 네가 네 꽃들보다 더 싱그러워 보이는구나!\" 그러자 우드코트 부인이 무얼린윌린워드의 한 구절을 되뇌고 번역해주었는데, 내가 햇살을 받은 산과 같다는 뜻이었습니다.\n\n이 모든 것이 너무도 즐거워서, 부디 이 덕분에 내가 전보다 더 그 산을 닮게 되었기를 바랐습니다. 아침 식사를 마친 뒤 나는 기회를 기다리며 이리저리 살짝 엿보다가, 후견인이 자기 방에—어젯밤의 그 방에—혼자 있는 것을 보았습니다. 그래서 살림 열쇠 꾸러미를 들고 들어갈 핑계를 만들어, 들어가서는 등 뒤로 문을 닫았습니다.\n\n\"그래, 더든 아주머니?\" 후견인이 말했습니다. 우편으로 편지가 여러 통 와 있었고, 그는 글을 쓰던 참이었습니다. \"돈이 필요하니?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말로 손에 넉넉히 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"더든 아주머니처럼 돈을 오래 쓰는 사람은 또 없을 거야.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n그는 펜을 내려놓고 의자에 등을 기댄 채 나를 바라보았습니다. 나는 그의 환한 얼굴을 자주 이야기해왔지만, 그날처럼 그렇게 환하고 선해 보인 적은 없는 것 같았습니다. 그 얼굴에는 드높은 행복이 어려 있어서 나는 이렇게 생각했습니다. '오늘 아침 무슨 큰 선행을 베푸셨구나.'\n\n\"더든 아주머니처럼 돈을 오래 쓰는 사람은,\" 후견인이 나를 보고 미소 지으며 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했습니다. \"정말 또 없을 거야.\"\n\n그는 아직도 그 오래된 태도를 한 번도 바꾸지 않았습니다. 나는 그 태도와 그를 너무도 사랑했기에, 이제 그에게 다가가 늘 그의 곁에 놓이는 내 의자에—이따금 나는 그에게 책을 읽어주고, 이따금 이야기를 나누고, 이따금 그의 곁에서 말없이 일을 했습니다—앉으면서, 그의 가슴에 손을 얹어 그 태도를 흩뜨리고 싶지 않았습니다. 하지만 나는 조금도 그것을 흩뜨리지 않았음을 알게 되었습니다.\n\n\"후견인님,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"드릴 말씀이 있어요. 제가 무언가 소홀했던 게 있나요?\"\n\n\"무언가 소홀했다니, 얘야!\"\n\n\"제가—후견인님의 편지에 답을 가져다드린 뒤로—되고자 마음먹은 그 사람이 되지 못한 건가요?\"\n\n\"너는 내가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이었단다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 정말로 무척 기뻐요.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"아시잖아요, 후견인님이 제게 이 사람이 황폐한 집의 안주인이 맞느냐고 물으셨지요. 그리고 제가 그렇다고 했고요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 후견인이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했습니다. 그는 무언가로부터 나를 지켜주려는 듯 내 어깨에 팔을 둘렀고, 미소 지으며 내 얼굴을 들여다보았습니다.\n\n\"그 뒤로,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"우리는 그 이야기를 딱 한 번 말고는 한 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그때 내가 황폐한 집이 점점 비어간다고 했지. 정말 그랬으니까, 얘야.\"\n\n\"그리고 제가 말했어요,\" 나는 머뭇거리며 그에게 일깨워주었습니다. \"그래도 그 안주인은 남아 있다고요.\"\n\n그는 여전히 똑같이 지켜주는 태도로 나를 안은 채, 얼굴에는 여전히 똑같이 환한 선함을 띠고 있었습니다.\n\n\"후견인님,\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그동안 일어난 모든 일에 대해 후견인님이 어떻게 느끼셨는지, 또 얼마나 배려해주셨는지 저는 알아요. 시간이 이렇게 많이 흘렀고, 게다가 바로 오늘 아침 제가 다시 건강해졌다고 말씀하셨으니, 어쩌면 제가 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내기를 기다리시는지도 모르겠어요. 어쩌면 제가 그래야 하는 거겠지요. 후견인님이 원하실 때 저는 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되겠어요.\"\n\n\"보렴,\" 그가 명랑하게 대답했습니다. \"우리 사이에 얼마나 마음이 통하는지! 가엾은 릭을 빼면—그게 큰 예외이긴 하다만—내 머릿속엔 다른 생각이 없었단다. 네가 들어왔을 때 나는 온통 그 생각뿐이었어. 황폐한 집에 언제 안주인을 들일까, 꼬마 아씨?\"\n\n\"원하실 때요.\"\n\n\"다음 달은 어떠니?\"\n\n\"다음 달에요, 후견인님.\"\n\n\"내가 내 평생 가장 행복하고 가장 멋진 한 걸음을 내딛는 날—이 세상 어느 누구보다 더 기뻐하고 더 부러움받을 사람이 되는 날—황폐한 집에 그 어여쁜 안주인을 들이는 날, 그렇다면 그날은 다음 달이로구나.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n나는 그의 목을 두 팔로 감싸 안고, 내가 답을 가져다드린 그날 그랬던 것처럼 그에게 입을 맞추었습니다.\n\n하인 하나가 문가로 와서 버킷 씨의 방문을 알렸는데, 그것은 전혀 필요 없는 일이었습니다. 버킷 씨가 이미 하인의 어깨너머로 안을 들여다보고 있었으니까요. \"잔다이스 씨, 그리고 서머슨 양.\" 그가 다소 숨을 헐떡이며 말했습니다. \"불쑥 들어와 죄송합니다만, 계단에 있는 사람 하나를 올려 보내도록 명해도 되겠습니까? 그 사람이 없는 사이에 자기가 입길에 오를까 봐 거기 혼자 남는 걸 마다하는지라 말입니다. 고맙습니다. 자, 저기 저 양반을 이쪽으로 모셔 오시오, 알겠소?\" 버킷 씨가 난간 너머로 손짓하며 말했습니다.\n\n이 묘한 요청 끝에 검은 두건을 쓴 노인 하나가 나타났는데, 걸을 수 없는 처지라 짐꾼 둘이 들쳐 메고 올라와 문 가까이에 내려놓았습니다. 버킷 씨는 곧장 짐꾼들을 내보내고, 알 수 없는 태도로 문을 닫더니 빗장을 질렀습니다.\n\n\"자, 보십시오, 잔다이스 씨.\" 그가 모자를 내려놓고 그 유명한 손가락을 한 번 멋들어지게 휘두르며 본론을 꺼냈습니다. \"당신은 저를 아시고, 서머슨 양도 저를 아십니다. 이 양반도 마찬가지로 저를 알지요. 이름은 스몰위드입니다. 주로 어음 할인이 이 양반 일이라, 말하자면 어음 장수라고나 할까요. 당신이 하는 게 대체로 그것 아니오, 안 그렇소?\" 버킷 씨는 잠시 멈추고, 자신을 몹시 미심쩍어하는 그 양반에게 말을 건넸습니다.\n\n그는 자신에 대한 이런 호칭에 이의를 제기하려는 듯하더니, 갑자기 격렬한 기침 발작에 사로잡혔습니다.\n\n\"자, 교훈입니다, 아시겠소!\" 버킷 씨가 그 우연을 활용해 말했습니다. \"그럴 일도 아닌데 반박하지 마시오. 그러면 그런 식으로 당하지 않을 테니. 자, 잔다이스 씨, 당신께 말씀드리겠습니다. 저는 준남작 레스터 데드록 경을 대신해 이 양반과 협상을 해왔고, 이래저래 이 양반 거처를 꽤 들락거렸습니다. 그 거처라는 게 예전에 크룩이라는 자가 쓰던 데인데요, 선박 잡화상이었지요—이 양반의 친척으로, 제가 잘못 안 게 아니라면 살아생전에 당신도 본 적이 있는 자입니다만?\"\n\n후견인이 대답했습니다. \"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그렇지요! 이렇게 이해하시면 됩니다.\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 양반이 크룩의 재산을 물려받았는데, 까치가 모아둔 것 같은 잡동사니가 한가득이었지요. 그중에 폐지가 엄청난 더미로 있었습니다. 맙소사, 누구한테도 아무 쓸모없는 것들이었지요!\"\n\n버킷 씨의 눈에 어린 교활함이며, 경계를 늦추지 않는 청자가 트집 잡을 만한 눈짓 하나 말 한마디 없이도, 자기가 사전에 합의한 대로 사건을 진술하는 것이며 마음만 먹으면 스몰위드 씨에 대해 훨씬 많은 것을 말할 수 있다는 사실을 우리에게 슬쩍 알려주는 그 능란한 솜씨 탓에, 우리가 그를 제대로 이해하는 데에는 우리 자신의 공이 한 톨도 들어가지 않았습니다. 스몰위드 씨가 의심이 많은 데다 귀까지 먹어, 버킷 씨의 얼굴을 더없이 바짝 주의 깊게 지켜보는 통에 그의 어려움은 한층 커졌습니다.\n\n\"그 헌 서류 더미 속을, 이 양반이 재산을 물려받았으니 당연히 뒤지기 시작하지요, 안 그렇겠습니까?\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"뭐를 어쩐다고? 다시 말해보시오.\" 스몰위드 씨가 새되고 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"뒤진다고요.\" 버킷 씨가 되풀이했습니다. \"신중한 사람인 데다 제 일을 챙기는 데 익숙하니, 물려받은 서류들 사이를 뒤지기 시작하지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"당연히 그러지.\" 스몰위드 씨가 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"당연히 그러시지요.\" 버킷 씨가 한담하듯 말했습니다. \"안 그러면 오히려 크게 나무랄 일이고요. 그렇게 해서 우연히 발견하게 되지요, 아시겠습니까,\" 버킷 씨가 명랑하게 놀리는 투로—스몰위드 씨는 결코 그 투에 장단을 맞추지 않았습니다만—그에게 몸을 숙이며 말을 이었습니다. \"그렇게 해서 우연히, 잔다이스라는 서명이 든 서류 한 장을 발견하지요. 안 그렇소?\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 불안한 눈으로 우리를 흘끔거리더니, 마지못해 고개를 끄덕여 수긍했습니다.\n\n\"그래서 짬을 내 느긋하게 그 서류를 들여다보는데—천천히요, 당신은 그걸 읽는 데 호기심이 없으니까요, 또 그럴 이유가 어디 있겠습니까?—그게 다름 아닌 유언장이라는 걸 알게 되지요. 그게 우스운 대목입니다.\" 버킷 씨가 스몰위드 씨를 즐겁게 해주려고 농담을 떠올리는 듯 여전히 활기찬 태도로 말했습니다. 스몰위드 씨는 여전히 전혀 즐겁지 않은 듯 풀이 죽은 모습이었습니다. \"그게 다름 아닌 유언장이라는 걸 알게 되지요?\"\n\n\"그게 유언장으로든 뭐로든 효력이 있는지 난 모르오.\" 스몰위드 씨가 으르렁대듯 말했습니다.\n\n버킷 씨는 잠시 그 노인을 노려보았는데—그는 의자 속으로 미끄러지듯 쪼그라들어 그저 한 보따리 같은 꼴이 되어 있었습니다—마치 당장이라도 덮칠 기세였습니다. 그래도 그는 한쪽 눈꼬리로 우리를 살피며, 여전히 상냥한 태도로 그에게 몸을 숙인 채 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"그런데도 말입니다,\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"당신은 그것 때문에 마음이 좀 미심쩍고 불편해집니다. 워낙 여린 마음을 지닌 분이니까요.\"\n\n\"엥? 내가 뭘 지녔다고?\" 스몰위드 씨가 한 손을 귀에 대고 물었습니다.\n\n\"아주 여린 마음 말입니다.\"\n\n\"호! 그래, 계속하시오.\" 스몰위드 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그리고 똑같은 이름의 유명한 대법원 유언 소송에 관해 이런저런 말을 많이 들어왔고, 또 크룩이라는 자가 온갖 헌 가구며 책이며 서류며 별별 것을 다 사들이고는 절대 내놓지 않으려 하고, 늘 제 손으로 글을 깨치겠다고 벼르던 위인이었다는 걸 아니, 당신은 생각하기 시작하지요—그리고 당신 평생 이보다 더 옳았던 적은 없을 겁니다—'이런, 정신 똑바로 차리지 않으면 이 유언장 때문에 곤경에 빠질지도 모르겠군.'\"\n\n\"자, 말조심해서 하시오, 버킷.\" 노인이 한 손을 귀에 댄 채 불안하게 외쳤습니다. \"또렷이 말하시오. 당신 그 유황불 같은 잔꾀는 그만두고. 나를 일으켜 주시오. 더 잘 듣고 싶소. 오, 맙소사, 몸이 다 산산조각 나는구나!\"\n\n버킷 씨는 분명 잽싸게 그를 일으켜 주었습니다. 그러나 스몰위드 씨의 기침과 \"오, 내 뼈야! 오, 이런! 숨이 막혀 죽겠다! 집에서 떠들고 덜그럭거리는 그 유황불 돼지보다 내 꼴이 더 못하구나!\" 하는 사나운 외침을 뚫고 말이 들릴 만해지자, 버킷 씨는 앞서처럼 다정한 태도로 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"그래서, 제가 마침 당신 거처를 들락거리는 처지이다 보니, 당신은 저를 믿고 속내를 털어놓지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n스몰위드 씨가 이것을 인정할 때 드러낸 것보다 더 마지못해하고 더 볼썽사납게 무언가를 시인하기란 불가능하지 싶었습니다. 그 모습을 보면, 버킷 씨를 어떻게든 떼어낼 수만 있었다면 그가 결코 속내를 털어놓을 생각조차 안 했을 마지막 인물이라는 게 더없이 분명히 드러났습니다.\n\n\"그래서 저는 당신과 함께 이 일에 뛰어듭니다—아주 화기애애하게요. 그리고 그 유언장을 내놓지 않으면 더없이 골치 아픈 처지에 빠질 거라는, 근거 있는 당신의 두려움을 제가 굳혀주지요.\" 버킷 씨가 힘주어 말했습니다. \"그래서 당신은 그것을 지금 이 자리에 계신 잔다이스 씨께 아무 조건 없이 넘기기로 저와 약조합니다. 만약 그게 값진 것으로 드러나면, 보상은 잔다이스 씨를 믿고 맡기기로 하고요. 대략 그렇지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그게 합의한 거요.\" 스몰위드 씨가 여전히 볼썽사납게 수긍했습니다.\n\n\"그렇기에,\" 버킷 씨가 그 상냥한 태도를 단번에 거두고 철저히 사무적으로 변하며 말했습니다. \"당신은 지금 그 유언장을 몸에 지니고 있고, 이제 당신이 할 일은 그저 그것을 꺼내는 것뿐입니다!\"\n\n경계하는 눈꼬리로 우리에게 한 번 눈길을 던지고, 집게손가락으로 의기양양하게 코를 한 번 비빈 뒤, 버킷 씨는 그 속내를 나눈 벗에게 시선을 못 박은 채, 서류를 받아 후견인에게 건넬 채비로 한 손을 내밀고 서 있었습니다. 그것은 스몰위드 씨가 자기는 가난하고 부지런한 사람이라느니, 정직하게 굴다가 손해 보지 않게 해주는 일은 잔다이스 씨의 명예에 맡기겠다느니 하는 숱한 항변 끝에야, 그것도 몹시 마지못해 나왔습니다. 그는 조금씩, 아주 천천히 가슴 주머니에서 얼룩지고 빛바랜 서류 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 겉은 심하게 그을렸고 가장자리는 조금 탔으니, 마치 오래전에 불 속에 던져졌다가 황급히 다시 낚아챈 것 같았습니다. 버킷 씨는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 마술사 같은 손놀림으로 그 서류를 스몰위드 씨에게서 잔다이스 씨에게로 옮겼습니다. 그는 그것을 후견인에게 건네며 손가락 뒤로 속삭였습니다. \"이걸로 어떻게 한몫 챙길지 아직 정하지 못한 자들입니다. 서로 다투고 들먹였지요. 제가 이십 파운드를 썼습니다. 처음엔 욕심 사나운 손주들이 저자가 어처구니없이 오래 산다고 못마땅해하며 등을 돌렸고, 그다음엔 저희끼리 등을 돌렸지요. 맙소사! 저 집안엔 일이 파운드에 제 식구를 팔아넘기지 않을 자가 하나도 없습니다. 저 노파만 빼고요—그것도 머리가 너무 흐려져서 흥정을 못 하니 끼지 못하는 것뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"버킷 씨,\" 후견인이 소리 내어 말했습니다. \"이 서류가 누구에게 어떤 값어치가 있든, 나는 당신에게 큰 신세를 졌소. 그리고 만약 이게 조금이라도 값어치가 있다면, 스몰위드 씨가 그에 걸맞게 보상받도록 챙기는 것을 내 의무로 여기겠소.\"\n\n\"당신이 한 만큼이 아니라, 아시겠소,\" 버킷 씨가 스몰위드 씨에게 다정하게 설명하듯 말했습니다. \"그 점은 걱정 마시오. 그 값어치만큼 말입니다.\"\n\n\"내 말이 그 뜻이오.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"버킷 씨, 보다시피 나는 이 서류를 내 손으로 살펴보는 것을 삼가고 있소. 솔직히 말하면, 나는 이 일 전체를 여러 해 동안 끊고 멀리해왔고, 내 영혼은 그것에 진저리가 나 있소. 하지만 서머슨 양과 나는 곧장 이 서류를 그 소송을 맡은 내 사무 변호사에게 넘길 것이고, 그 존재는 지체 없이 이해관계가 있는 다른 모든 당사자에게 알릴 것이오.\"\n\n\"잔다이스 씨가 이보다 더 공정하게 말씀하실 수는 없지요, 아시겠소.\" 버킷 씨가 함께 온 사람에게 말했습니다. \"그리고 누구도 부당한 일을 당하지 않으리라는 게 이제 당신께 분명해졌으니—그건 당신 마음에 큰 위안이 될 게 틀림없습니다만—이제 당신을 다시 가마에 태워 댁으로 모셔 가는 의식으로 넘어가도 되겠지요.\"\n\n그는 빗장을 풀고 짐꾼들을 불러들여, 우리에게 아침 인사를 건네고는, 의미심장한 눈빛과 함께 헤어지면서 손가락을 한 번 까딱이고 제 갈 길을 갔습니다.\n\n우리도 우리 갈 길을 갔으니, 곧 링컨 법학원으로 되도록 빨리 향했습니다. 켄지 씨는 마침 한가했고, 우리는 그가 먼지 낀 방의 책상 앞에 앉아 있는 것을 보았습니다. 표정 없어 보이는 책들과 서류 더미에 둘러싸인 채였습니다. 거피 씨가 우리에게 의자를 놓아주자, 켄지 씨는 잔다이스 씨가 자기 사무실에 나타난 보기 드문 광경에 느낀 놀라움과 기쁨을 표했습니다. 그는 말하는 동안 이중 안경을 만지작거렸고, 그 어느 때보다 더 입심 좋은 켄지다웠습니다.\n\n\"바라건대,\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양의 다정한 감화가,\" 그가 내게 고개를 숙였습니다. \"잔다이스 씨로 하여금,\" 그가 그에게 고개를 숙였습니다. \"어떤 소송과 어떤 법정—말하자면, 우리 직업이라는 기둥들이 늘어선 그 위풍당당한 회랑에 한자리를 차지하는—에 대한 그 적의를 조금이나마 누그러뜨리도록 이끌었기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"내 생각에는,\" 후견인이 대답했습니다. \"서머슨 양이 그 법정과 그 소송의 폐해를 너무 많이 보아왔기에, 그것들에 유리한 어떤 영향도 끼치지 못할 것 같소. 그렇지만 그것들도 내가 여기 온 까닭의 한 부분이긴 하오. 켄지 씨, 내가 이 서류를 당신 책상에 내려놓고 이 일에서 손을 떼기 전에, 이것이 어떻게 내 손에 들어왔는지 말해두겠소.\"\n\n그는 그것을 짧고 또렷하게 이야기했습니다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"그게 소송 사건이었다 한들, 이보다 더 명확하고 요령 있게 진술될 수는 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"영국 법이든 형평법이든, 명확하고 요령 있던 적이 있던가요?\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"오, 저런!\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n처음에 그는 그 서류에 별 중요성을 두지 않는 듯했으나, 막상 그것을 보자 더 흥미를 느끼는 것 같았고, 안경 너머로 그것을 펼쳐 조금 읽고 나자 깜짝 놀랐습니다. \"잔다이스 씨,\" 그가 서류에서 눈을 떼며 말했습니다. \"이걸 읽어보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"천만에!\" 후견인이 대답했습니다.\n\n\"하지만, 선생님,\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"이건 그 소송에 걸린 어떤 것보다도 나중 날짜의 유언장입니다. 전부 유언자의 친필인 것으로 보이고요. 정식으로 작성되고 증인까지 갖추었습니다. 그리고 설령 이 불에 탄 흔적이 어쩌면 그렇게 추정될 만큼 폐기하려던 의도였다 하더라도, 폐기된 것은 아닙니다. 자, 여기 완벽한 증서가 있습니다!\"\n\n\"그래서!\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?\"\n\n\"거피 군!\" 켄지 씨가 목소리를 높여 외쳤습니다. \"실례했습니다, 잔다이스 씨.\"\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"사이먼드 법학원의 볼스 씨께. 안부 전하고. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스. 만나서 얘기하고 싶다고.\"\n\n거피 씨가 사라졌습니다.\n\n\"잔다이스 씨, 이게 당신과 무슨 상관이냐고 물으셨지요. 만약 이 문서를 읽어보셨다면, 이것이 당신의 지분을 상당히 줄이긴 하나, 그래도 여전히 아주 후한 몫을 남겨준다는 것을, 여전히 아주 후한 몫을 남겨준다는 것을 보셨을 겁니다.\" 켄지 씨가 손을 설득하듯 부드럽게 내저으며 말했습니다. \"더 나아가 이것이 리처드 카스톤 씨와 에이다 클레어 양, 이제는 리처드 카스톤 부인이 된 분의 지분을 아주 크게 높여준다는 것도 보셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"켄지 씨,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그 소송이 이 비열한 대법원으로 끌어들인 그 번지르르한 재산이 전부 내 두 어린 사촌에게 돌아갈 수 있다면, 나는 더없이 만족하겠소. 하지만 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서 무슨 좋은 것이 나올 거라고 나더러 믿으라는 거요?\"\n\n\"오, 정말이지, 잔다이스 씨! 편견이군요, 편견입니다. 선생님, 여긴 아주 위대한 나라입니다, 아주 위대한 나라예요. 그 형평법 제도는 아주 위대한 제도이고요, 아주 위대한 제도입니다. 정말로, 정말이라니까요!\"\n\n후견인은 더 말하지 않았고, 볼스 씨가 도착했습니다. 그는 켄지 씨의 직업적 명성에 겸손하게 감복한 듯했습니다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 볼스 씨? 여기 내 옆자리에 앉아서 이 서류를 좀 살펴봐 주시겠소?\"\n\n볼스 씨는 청한 대로 했고, 한 단어 한 단어 읽는 듯했습니다. 그는 그것에 흥분하지 않았는데, 사실 그는 무엇에도 흥분하는 법이 없었습니다. 충분히 살펴본 뒤, 그는 켄지 씨와 함께 창가로 물러나 검은 장갑을 낀 손으로 입을 가린 채 한참 동안 이야기를 나누었습니다. 켄지 씨가 그의 말이 채 끝나기도 전에 반박하려 드는 것을 보고도 나는 놀라지 않았으니, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서는 어떤 두 사람도 무엇 하나 의견이 일치한 적이 없음을 알기 때문이었습니다. 하지만 그는 켄지 씨까지도 이겨내는 듯했는데, 그 대화는 거의 \"재산관리관\", \"회계관\", \"보고서\", \"재산\", \"비용\"이라는 말들로만 이루어진 것처럼 들렸습니다. 이야기를 마치자 그들은 켄지 씨의 책상으로 돌아와 소리 내어 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래! 하지만 이건 아주 주목할 만한 문서입니다, 볼스 씨.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"정말 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 아주 중요한 문서이고요, 볼스 씨.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n볼스 씨가 다시 말했습니다. \"정말 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 볼스 씨 말씀대로, 다음 개정 기간에 그 소송이 사건 목록에 오르면, 이 문서는 거기서 뜻밖이면서도 흥미로운 대목이 되겠지요.\" 켄지 씨가 거만하게 후견인을 바라보며 말했습니다.\n\n볼스 씨는, 체면을 지키려 애쓰는 변변찮은 개업 변호사로서, 이런 권위자에게서 자기 견해를 어떤 식으로든 확인받자 흡족해했습니다.\n\n\"그런데,\" 후견인이 잠시 침묵한 끝에 일어서며 물었습니다. 그 침묵 동안 켄지 씨는 돈을 짤랑거렸고 볼스 씨는 여드름을 짜고 있었습니다. \"그 다음 개정 기간은 언제요?\"\n\n\"다음 개정 기간은, 잔다이스 씨, 다음 달입니다.\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"물론 우리는 곧장 이 문서로 필요한 절차를 밟고 그것에 관한 필요한 증거를 모을 것입니다. 그리고 물론 그 소송이 사건 목록에 올랐다는 통상의 통지를 받으시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 나는 물론, 그것에 늘 하던 대로 관심을 기울이겠소.\"\n\n\"여전히 고집하시는군요, 선생님,\" 켄지 씨가 우리를 바깥 사무실을 지나 문까지 배웅하며 말했습니다. \"그 넓은 식견을 지니시고도, 여전히 세간의 편견을 따라 메아리치기로 마음먹으셨군요? 우리는 번창하는 공동체입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 아주 번창하는 공동체예요. 우리는 위대한 나라입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 우리는 아주 위대한 나라입니다. 이것은 위대한 제도입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 그런데 위대한 나라가 자그마한 제도를 갖기를 바라십니까? 정말이지, 정말이라니까요!\"\n\n그는 계단 꼭대기에서 이렇게 말했는데, 오른손을 마치 은빛 흙손이라도 되는 양 부드럽게 놀려, 그 말의 회반죽을 제도라는 구조물에 펴 발라 천년만년 굳히려는 듯했습니다.\n\n조지의 사격장은 세놓이고, 재고는 처분되었으며, 조지 본인은 체스니 월드에서 레스터 경의 승마를 수행하며 말고삐를 잡은 손이 미덥지 못한 까닭에 그 곁에 바짝 붙어 말을 몬다. 하지만 오늘 조지는 그 일에 매여 있지 않다. 오늘 그는 좀 더 북쪽 철강 지대로 길을 나서, 두루 살펴보러 가는 길이다.\n\n좀 더 북쪽 철강 지대로 접어들면, 체스니 월드의 그 싱그러운 초록 숲 같은 것은 뒤로 멀어진다. 그리고 탄광과 잿더미, 높다란 굴뚝과 붉은 벽돌, 시들어버린 초목, 살을 태우는 불길, 결코 걷히지 않는 묵직한 연기 구름이 풍경의 특징이 된다. 그런 것들 사이로 기병이 말을 몰며 사방을 둘러보는데, 줄곧 자기가 찾으러 온 무언가를 찾고 있다.\n\n마침내, 쇳소리가 쩌렁쩌렁 울리고 지금껏 본 것보다 더 많은 불길과 더 많은 연기가 자욱한, 분주한 어느 읍내의 시커먼 운하 다리 위에서, 석탄길 먼지에 거뭇해진 기병이 말을 멈추고 한 일꾼에게 이 근방에서 라운스웰이라는 이름을 아느냐고 묻는다.\n\n\"아니, 나리,\" 일꾼이 대꾸한다. \"내 이름도 내가 모르겠소?\"\n\n\"여기서 그렇게 잘 알려진 이름인가, 친구?\" 기병이 묻는다.\n\n\"라운스웰 말이오? 아! 바로 맞혔수다.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금 어디로 가야 하나?\" 기병이 앞쪽을 흘끔 보며 묻는다.\n\n\"은행, 공장, 아니면 저택?\" 일꾼이 알고 싶어 한다.\n\n\"흠! 라운스웰이 그렇게나 대단한 모양이로군.\" 기병이 턱을 쓸며 중얼거린다. \"이거 그냥 발길을 돌릴까 하는 마음이 절반은 드는걸. 원, 어딜 찾아야 할지조차 모르겠으니. 라운스웰 씨를 공장에서 만날 수 있을 것 같소?\"\n\n\"어디서 만날지는 말하기가 쉽지 않수다—이 시간이면 그가 읍내에 있을 경우 그든 그 아들이든 거기서 만날 수 있겠지만, 계약 일로 자주 떠나 있으니.\"\n\n그럼 공장은 어디인가? 아니, 저 굴뚝들이 보이지 않소—제일 높은 것들 말이오! 그렇소, 저것들이 보이는군. 좋수다! 저 굴뚝들에 눈을 박고 곧장 갈 수 있는 만큼 곧장 가시오, 그러면 이내 왼쪽으로 꺾이는 길 아래로 거리 한쪽을 이루는 커다란 벽돌담에 둘러싸인 그것들이 보일 거요. 거기가 라운스웰이오.\n\n기병은 알려준 이에게 고마워하고 사방을 둘러보며 천천히 말을 몬다. 그는 발길을 돌리지 않고, 마부 말마따나 라운스웰의 일꾼 몇이 점심을 먹고 있는 어느 선술집에 말을 맡긴다(게다가 손수 빗질이라도 해주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같다). 라운스웰의 일꾼 몇이 마침 점심시간이라 일을 멈추었는데, 읍내 전체를 점령하다시피 한 듯하다. 힘줄이 단단하고 억세다, 라운스웰의 일꾼들은—더러 검댕도 묻었고.\n\n그는 벽돌담의 출입구에 이르러 안을 들여다보고, 온갖 단계와 갖가지 형태로 어지러이 널린 쇠붙이의 난장판을 본다—막대로, 쐐기로, 판으로, 물탱크로, 보일러로, 차축으로, 바퀴로, 톱니로, 크랭크로, 레일로. 기계의 따로따로인 부품으로서 기괴하고 비뚤어진 형태로 비틀리고 뒤틀린 채로. 산더미처럼 쌓인 부서진 쇠는 세월에 녹슬어 있고, 저 멀리 용광로 속 쇠는 젊은 시절을 누리며 이글이글 부글부글 끓는다. 증기 해머의 타격 아래 밝은 불꽃놀이처럼 쇠가 사방으로 흩날리고, 시뻘겋게 단 쇠, 새하얗게 단 쇠, 식어서 검은 쇠가 있다. 쇠 맛이 나고, 쇠 냄새가 나며, 쇠 소리가 바벨탑처럼 뒤엉킨다.\n\n\"여기도 사람 골치를 지끈거리게 하는 데로군!\" 기병이 사무실을 찾아 두리번거리며 말한다. \"여기 오는 게 누구지? 옛날, 내가 자리 잡기 전의 나와 어찌나 닮았는지. 닮은 게 집안 내력이라면, 저 사람은 내 조카가 분명한데. 안녕하시오, 선생.\"\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 선생. 누구를 찾으십니까?\"\n\n\"실례지만. 젊은 라운스웰 씨, 맞소?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"댁의 부친을 찾고 있었소, 선생. 그분과 한마디 나누고 싶어서.\"\n\n젊은이가, 마침 부친이 계시니 시간을 잘 골라 오셨다고 말하며, 부친을 만날 수 있는 사무실로 길을 안내한다. \"내가 자리 잡기 전의 나와 어찌나 닮았는지—기막히게 닮았어!\" 기병은 뒤따르며 생각한다. 두 사람은 마당에 있는, 위층에 사무실이 딸린 건물에 이른다. 사무실 안의 신사를 보는 순간, 조지 씨는 얼굴이 새빨개진다.\n\n\"부친께 누구라고 전할까요?\" 젊은이가 묻는다.\n\n쇠 생각으로 머릿속이 가득 찬 조지는 자포자기하여 \"스틸이라고\"라 답하고, 그렇게 소개된다. 그는 사무실 안의 신사와 단둘이 남겨지는데, 신사는 책상 앞에 앉아 회계 장부들을 펼쳐놓았고, 숱한 숫자와 정교한 도형 스케치로 얼룩진 종잇장 몇 장이 놓여 있다. 휑한 사무실이고, 창문도 휑하니 아래쪽 쇠붙이 풍경을 내다본다. 책상 위에는 쓰임에 따라 여러 시기에 시험하려고 일부러 부러뜨린 쇳조각 몇 개가 한데 뒹군다. 모든 것에 쇳가루가 앉아 있다. 창문 너머로는 높다란 굴뚝에서 묵직하게 굴러 나온 연기가 안개처럼 자욱한 다른 굴뚝들의 바빌론 같은 연기와 뒤섞이는 모습이 보인다.\n\n* 스틸: 영어로 '강철(steel)'을 뜻한다. 조지가 쇠붙이 풍경에 정신이 팔린 나머지 본명 대신 둘러댄 가명이다.\n\n\"무슨 용건이신지요, 스틸 씨.\" 손님이 녹슨 의자에 앉자 신사가 말한다.\n\"음, 라운스웰 씨.\" 조지가 왼팔을 무릎에 올리고 모자를 손에 든 채 몸을 앞으로 기울이며, 형의 눈을 마주치기를 한사코 꺼리며 대답한다. \"이번 방문이 환영받기보다 주제넘은 짓으로 드러날 수도 있겠다는 예감이 없지 않소이다. 나는 한때 용기병으로 복무했는데, 내가 꽤 마음을 주었던 전우 하나가, 내 착각이 아니라면, 댁의 형제였소. 내가 알기로 댁에게는 집안을 좀 골치 아프게 하고 달아나, 멀리 떨어져 있는 것 말고는 아무런 쓸모도 없었던 형제가 있었지요?\"\n\n\"정말 확실하십니까,\" 철강업자가 달라진 목소리로 되묻는다. \"댁의 성함이 스틸이라는 게 말입니다?\"\n\n기병이 머뭇거리며 그를 바라본다. 형이 벌떡 일어나 그의 이름을 부르며 두 손을 덥석 잡는다.\n\n\"형은 나보다 너무 빠르구려!\" 기병이 눈에서 눈물을 쏟으며 외친다. \"어떻게 지냈소, 그리운 형님? 형이 나를 보고 이렇게나 반가워할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐소. 어떻게 지냈소, 그리운 형님, 어떻게 지냈소!\"\n\n두 사람은 거듭거듭 악수하고 서로 끌어안는데, 기병은 여전히 \"어떻게 지냈소, 그리운 형님!\"이라는 말에다, 형이 나를 보고 이렇게나 반가워할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다는 다짐을 짝지어 붙인다!\n\n\"그렇기는커녕,\" 그는 그곳에 도착하기까지의 사연을 모조리 털어놓은 끝에 단언한다. \"나는 내 정체를 밝힐 생각이 거의 없었소. 형이 어떻게든 내 이름을 너그럽게 받아들인다면, 차츰 마음을 다잡아 편지 한 통 쓸 수 있는 데까지 가볼까 했지. 하지만 형, 형이 내 소식을 전혀 반갑잖은 것으로 여겼다 해도, 나는 놀라지 않았을 거요.\"\n\n\"우리가 그걸 어떤 소식으로 여기는지는 집에 가서 보여주마, 조지.\" 형이 대답한다. \"오늘은 집안의 큰 경삿날이니, 햇볕에 그을린 늙은 군인 양반아, 너는 이보다 더 좋은 날에 올 수가 없었어. 오늘 나는 내 아들 와트와 약조를 맺는데, 오늘로부터 일 년 뒤 이날, 네가 그 숱한 여행길에서 본 그 누구 못지않게 예쁘고 착한 아가씨와 결혼하기로 했단다. 그 애는 내일 네 조카딸 하나와 함께 독일로 가서, 교육의 마무리 손질을 좀 받을 거야. 우리는 이 일을 잔치로 치를 참인데, 너를 그 주인공으로 삼으마.\"\n\n조지 씨는 처음에 이 제안에 완전히 압도되어 그 영예를 한사코 마다한다. 그러나 형과 조카에게—이들에 관해서도 이렇게나 반가워할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다는 다짐을 거듭하는데—떠밀려, 우아한 저택으로 끌려간다. 그 저택의 온갖 살림에는 부모의 본디 소박한 습관과, 달라진 신분 및 자녀들의 높아진 형편에 어울리는 것들이 흐뭇하게 뒤섞여 있는 게 눈에 띈다. 여기서 조지 씨는 지금의 조카딸들이 지닌 우아함과 교양에, 그리고 장차 조카며느리가 될 로사의 아름다움에, 또 이 젊은 숙녀들의 다정한 인사에 적잖이 당황하여, 그 인사를 일종의 꿈결 속에서 받는다. 그는 조카의 깍듯한 처신에도 호되게 기가 꺾이고, 자기가 망나니라는 서글픈 자각에 사로잡힌다. 그래도 큰 즐거움과 더없이 화기애애한 자리와 끝없는 흥겨움이 펼쳐지고, 조지 씨는 그 모든 것을 무뚝뚝하고 군인답게 헤쳐나가며, 결혼식에 참석해 신부를 인도하겠다는 그의 약속은 만장일치의 호응을 얻는다. 그날 밤, 조지 씨가 형네 집의 가장 좋은 침대에 누워 이 모든 일을 떠올리며, 조카딸들의(저녁 내내 하늘하늘한 모슬린 차림으로 위풍당당하던) 모습이 독일식으로 왈츠를 추며 그의 침대보 위를 누비는 환영을 보노라니, 머릿속이 빙글빙글 돈다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 형제는 철강업자의 방에 틀어박힌다. 거기서 형이 특유의 또렷하고 분별 있는 어조로, 자기 사업 안에서 조지를 어떻게 가장 잘 자리 잡게 해줄 수 있을지 자기 생각을 펼치려는데, 조지가 그의 손을 꽉 쥐며 말을 막는다.\n\n\"형, 형제 이상으로 따뜻한 환대에 백만 번 고맙고, 형제 이상으로 깊은 그 뜻에는 또 백만 번 더 고맙소. 하지만 내 계획은 이미 정해져 있소. 그 얘기를 꺼내기 전에, 집안 문제 한 가지를 형과 상의하고 싶소. 어떻게 해야,\" 기병이 팔짱을 끼고 굽힐 줄 모르는 단호함으로 형을 바라보며 말한다. \"어떻게 해야 어머니가 나를 지워버리게 할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"네 말을 제대로 알아들었는지 모르겠구나, 조지.\" 철강업자가 대꾸한다.\n\n\"내 말은, 형, 어떻게 해야 어머니가 나를 지워버리게 할 수 있느냐는 거요. 어떻게든 그렇게 하시도록 해야 하오.\"\n\n\"어머니 유언장에서 너를 지운다는 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"당연히 그렇소. 한마디로,\" 기병이 한층 더 단호하게 팔짱을 끼며 말한다. \"내 말은—나를—지워버리라는 거요!\"\n\n\"이보게 조지,\" 형이 대꾸한다. \"네가 꼭 그런 절차를 겪어야만 하느냐?\"\n\n\"그렇소! 반드시! 그것 없이 돌아오는 비열한 짓은 저지를 수 없소. 그러지 않고는 다시 떠나지 않으리란 보장이 영영 안 서니까. 나는, 형이든 형의 자식들이든 그 권리를 빼앗으려고 슬그머니 집에 기어든 게 아니오. 내 권리야 오래전에 스스로 내던졌고! 내가 여기 남아 떳떳이 고개를 들려면, 지워져야만 하오. 자. 형은 명석한 통찰과 지혜로 이름난 사람이니, 어떻게 하면 그 일을 이룰 수 있는지 말해줄 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"내가 말해주마, 조지.\" 철강업자가 신중하게 대답한다. \"그 일을 어떻게 하면 이룰 수 없는지를. 그게 오히려 목적에 들어맞기를 바란다. 우리 어머니를 보아라, 어머니를 생각해라, 너를 되찾았을 때 어머니의 그 격정을 떠올려보아라. 어머니가 가장 아끼는 아들을 상대로 그런 짓을 하도록 이끌 만한 사정이 이 세상에 있다고 믿느냐? (그 사랑 많고 다정한 노부인께!) 그런 제안을 하는 것 자체가 어머니께 얼마나 모욕일지, 그것과 견주어 어머니가 동의할 가능성이 조금이라도 있다고 믿느냐? 그렇게 믿는다면 너는 틀렸다. 안 돼, 조지! 너는 지워지지 않은 채로 남기로 마음을 굳혀야 한다.\" 철강업자는 깊이 실망하여 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴 동생을 지켜보며, 얼굴에 흥겨운 미소를 띤다. \"하지만 그 일이 이루어진 것이나 거의 다름없이 처리할 방도는 있을 거다.\"\n\n\"어떻게요, 형?\"\n\n\"네가 정 그러기로 작정했다면, 불운하게도 무엇을 물려받든 그걸 유언으로 네가 원하는 대로 처분할 수 있잖느냐.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇구려!\" 기병이 다시 곰곰이 생각하며 말한다. 그러고는 형의 손에 자기 손을 얹고 아쉬운 듯 묻는다. \"형, 그 얘기를 형수와 식구들한테 좀 해주면 안 되겠소?\"\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐 있겠느냐.\"\n\"고맙소. 그리고 어쩌면, 내가 의심할 바 없는 떠돌이이긴 해도, 좀스러운 부류가 아니라 무모하고 덜렁대는 부류의 떠돌이라고 한마디 해줄 수도 있겠소?\"\n\n철강업자가 흥겨운 미소를 누르며 그러마고 한다.\n\n\"고맙소. 고맙소. 한시름 놓이는구려.\" 기병이 팔짱을 풀고 두 손을 각각 무릎에 얹으며 가슴을 한 번 들썩이고 말한다. \"지워지는 데에도 마음을 단단히 두긴 했지만!\"\n\n마주 앉은 형제는 서로 무척 닮았다. 다만 묵직한 소박함과 세상 물정에 닳지 않은 구석은 온전히 기병 쪽에만 있다.\n\n\"그럼,\" 그가 실망을 떨치며 말을 잇는다. \"다음이자 마지막으로, 그 내 계획 말이오. 형은 형제답게도 나더러 여기에 들어와, 형의 끈기와 분별이 일군 성과들 한가운데에서 한자리를 차지하라고 권했소. 진심으로 고맙소. 아까도 말했듯 형제 이상가는 일이고, 진심으로 고맙소.\" 그가 오랫동안 형의 손을 흔든다. \"하지만 사실은 말이오, 형, 나는—나는 말하자면 잡초 같은 놈이라, 제대로 된 정원에 옮겨 심기엔 이미 너무 늦었소.\"\n\n\"이보게 조지,\" 형이 그 강인하고 듬직한 이마를 동생에게로 모으며 미더운 미소를 띠고 대답한다. \"그건 내게 맡기고, 한번 해보게 해다오.\"\n\n조지가 고개를 젓는다. \"누군가 할 수 있다면 형이 해낼 수 있을 거요, 그건 의심치 않소. 하지만 그건 될 일이 아니오. 될 일이 아니라니까, 선생! 그런데 반대로 일이 이렇게 풀렸소. 나는 레스터 데드록 경께—집안의 비통한 일들로 병을 얻으신 그분께—얼마간 사소한 도움이라도 드릴 수 있게 되었고, 그분은 그 도움을 다른 누구보다 우리 어머니의 아들에게서 받고 싶어 하신다오.\"\n\n\"음, 이보게 조지,\" 형이 그 트인 얼굴에 아주 옅은 그늘을 드리우며 대답한다. \"네가 레스터 데드록 경 댁의 식솔 부대에서 봉직하기를 더 바란다면—\"\n\n\"바로 그거요, 형.\" 기병이 다시 손을 무릎에 얹으며 형의 말을 막고 외친다. \"바로 그거! 형은 그 생각이 영 내키지 않는 거요. 나는 개의치 않소. 형은 누군가의 지휘를 받는 데 익숙지 않지만, 나는 익숙하오. 형의 주변은 모든 것이 완벽한 질서와 규율 속에 있소. 내 주변은 모든 것을 그렇게 잡아둘 필요가 있고. 우리는 같은 손으로 일을 처리하거나 같은 자리에서 사물을 바라보는 데 익숙지 않소. 내 병영식 몸가짐에 대해선 길게 말하지 않겠소. 어젯밤엔 제법 마음 편히 지냈고, 또 어쩌다 한 번쯤이야 여기서도 눈에 띄지 않을 테니까. 하지만 나는 체스니 월드에서 가장 잘 지낼 거요. 거기엔 잡초가 자랄 자리가 여기보다 더 넓으니까. 게다가 그 다정한 노부인도 행복해지실 테고. 그러니 나는 레스터 데드록 경의 제안을 받아들이겠소. 내년에 신부를 인도하러 건너올 때, 아니 언제 오든, 나는 그 식솔 부대를 매복시켜둘 만큼의 분별은 있을 테니, 형의 땅에서 그걸 부려먹진 않으리다. 다시 한번 진심으로 고맙소. 그리고 형이 세워나갈 라운스웰 가문을 생각하면 자랑스럽소.\"\n\n\"너는 너 자신을 알지, 조지.\" 형이 동생의 손을 마주 쥐며 말한다. \"어쩌면 내가 나 자신을 아는 것보다 너를 더 잘 알지도 모르겠구나. 네 길을 가거라. 다만 우리가 다시 서로를 영영 잃지만 않도록, 네 길을 가거라.\"\n\n\"그럴 염려는 없소!\" 기병이 대답한다. \"자, 말머리를 집 쪽으로 돌리기 전에, 형, 한 가지 부탁이 있소—괜찮다면—편지 한 통을 좀 봐주시오. 이 근방에서 부치려고 가져왔는데, 체스니 월드라는 이름이 지금 이 편지를 받을 사람에겐 아픈 이름일 수도 있어서요. 나도 편지 쓰는 일엔 그리 익숙지 않은 데다, 이번 편지는 솔직하면서도 섬세하기를 바라는 까닭에 각별히 마음을 쓰고 있소.\"\n\n이러면서 그는 다소 흐린 잉크로 빽빽하되 단정하고 둥근 필체로 쓴 편지 한 통을 철강업자에게 건네고, 철강업자는 다음과 같이 읽는다.\n\n에스더 서머슨 양께,\n\n버킷 형사로부터, 어떤 사람의 서류 가운데 저에게 보낸 편지 한 통이 발견되었다는 전갈을 받고서, 저는 외람되이 양께 다음을 알려드립니다. 그것은 다만 외국에서 보내온 몇 줄의 지시문으로, 그 무렵 영국에 있던 젊고 아리따운 한 숙녀, 당시 미혼이었던 그분께 동봉된 편지를 언제, 어디서, 어떻게 전할지에 관한 것이었습니다. 저는 그대로 충실히 이행하였습니다.\n\n또한 저는 외람되이 양께 알려드립니다. 그것은 오로지 필적의 증거로서 저에게서 받아간 것이며, 그렇지 않았다면 저는 제 손에 있는 것 중 가장 해롭지 않아 보이는 그것을, 심장을 먼저 총으로 꿰뚫리기 전에는 결코 내주지 않았을 것입니다.\n\n또한 저는 외람되이 말씀드립니다. 어느 불운한 신사가 살아 있으리라고 짐작이라도 할 수 있었다면, 저는 그분이 숨은 곳을 찾아낼 때까지 결코 쉴 수 없었고 쉬지도 않았을 것이며, 제 마지막 한 푼까지 그분과 나누었을 것입니다. 그것이 저의 의무이자 또한 저의 바람이었을 테니까요. 그러나 그분은 (공식적으로) 익사한 것으로 보고되었고, 서인도제도에서 돌아온 지 몇 시간도 안 되어 아일랜드의 어느 항구에서 밤중에 수송선 뱃전 너머로 떨어진 것이 분명합니다. 이는 제가 그 배에 탔던 장교와 병사 양쪽에게서 직접 들은 바이며, (공식적으로) 확인된 일임을 저는 알고 있습니다.\n\n또한 저는 외람되이 밝힙니다. 졸병 가운데 하나라는 미천한 처지로나마, 저는 양의 철저히 헌신하고 흠모하는 종이며 또 영원히 그러할 것이고, 양께서 지니신 자질을 다른 무엇보다도, 이 짧은 글의 한계를 훨씬 넘어서도록 떠받든다는 것을.\n\n삼가 올립니다,\n조지\n\n\"좀 격식을 차렸구나.\" 형이 어리둥절한 얼굴로 편지를 다시 접으며 한마디 한다.\n\n\"하지만 모범적인 젊은 숙녀에게 보내선 안 될 만한 구석은 없겠소?\" 동생이 묻는다.\n\n\"전혀 없다.\"\n\n그리하여 편지는 봉인되어, 그날의 철강 관련 우편물 사이에 끼워 부쳐질 채비를 갖춘다. 이 일을 마치고 조지 씨는 가족 일행에게 진심 어린 작별을 고하고 안장을 얹어 말에 오르려 한다. 그러나 형은 그를 그리 빨리 떠나보내기가 아쉬워, 가벼운 무개마차로 그가 하룻밤 묵으며 말을 쉬게 할 곳까지 함께 가서, 아침까지 그와 머물자고 제안한다. 그 여정의 상당 부분 동안 하인 하나가 체스니 월드에서 데려온 순종 늙은 잿빛 말을 타고 따라온다. 이 제안은 기쁘게 받아들여지고, 이어 흐뭇한 승마와 흐뭇한 만찬과 흐뭇한 아침 식사가, 모두 형제간의 정담 속에 이어진다. 그런 다음 두 사람은 다시 한번 오래 진심으로 악수하고 헤어지는데, 철강업자는 연기와 불길 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 기병은 푸른 들판 쪽으로 향한다. 이른 오후, 그가 오래된 느릅나무 아래로 마구가 절그렁절그렁 짤랑짤랑 울리는 상상에 잠겨 말을 몰아가노라니, 가로수 길의 잔디 위로 그 묵직한 군대식 빠른 걸음의 잦아든 소리가 들려온다.\n\n후견인과 그런 대화를 나눈 지 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 아침, 후견인은 봉인한 종이 한 장을 내 손에 쥐여주며 말했습니다. \"이건 다음 달에 쓸 거란다, 얘야.\" 그 안에는 이백 파운드가 들어 있었습니다.\n\n나는 이제 아주 조용히 필요하다고 여겨지는 준비를 시작했습니다. 후견인의 취향에 맞추어 물건을 골랐는데, 그분 취향이야 물론 잘 알고 있었으므로, 그분 마음에 들도록 옷가지를 갖추었고 무척 잘 해내기를 바랐습니다. 이 모든 일을 그토록 조용히 한 까닭은, 에이다가 못내 서운해할지도 모른다는 예전 그 걱정에서 아직 완전히 벗어나지 못한 데다, 후견인 자신이 워낙 조용한 분이었기 때문입니다. 모든 사정을 헤아릴 때 우리는 가장 은밀하고 소박한 방식으로 결혼하게 되리라는 데 의심이 없었습니다. 어쩌면 나는 에이다에게 이렇게 말하기만 하면 될지도 몰랐습니다. \"내일 내가 결혼하는 걸 보러 올래, 얘야?\" 어쩌면 우리 결혼식도 에이다 자신의 것만큼 꾸밈없을 테고, 다 끝날 때까지 그 일에 대해 굳이 한마디도 할 필요가 없을지도 몰랐습니다. 내가 고를 수 있다면 이렇게 하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했습니다.\n\n내가 유일하게 예외로 둔 사람은 우드코트 부인이었습니다. 나는 부인에게 후견인과 결혼할 예정이며 우리가 약혼한 지 얼마 되었다고 말씀드렸습니다. 부인은 크게 찬성했습니다. 부인은 나에게 아무리 잘해줘도 모자라다고 여겼고, 우리가 처음 알았을 때에 견주면 지금은 놀라우리만치 누그러져 있었습니다. 나에게 도움이 되는 일이라면 부인이 마다할 수고가 없었지만, 굳이 말할 것도 없이 나는 그 친절을 시험에 들게 하지 않는 선에서, 부인의 호의를 흡족하게 할 만큼만 받아들였습니다.\n\n물론 지금은 후견인을 소홀히 할 때가 아니었고, 물론 내 사랑하는 친구를 소홀히 할 때도 아니었습니다. 그래서 나는 할 일이 잔뜩 있었는데, 그게 오히려 기뻤습니다. 찰리로 말하자면, 바느질에 파묻혀 도무지 보이지가 않을 지경이었습니다. 자기 둘레에 바느질감을 산더미처럼 쌓아 두고—바구니 가득, 탁자 가득—조금만 손을 대고, 그 동그란 눈으로 해야 할 일거리를 멀거니 바라보며 한참을 보내고, 곧 그 일을 하리라고 스스로를 다독이는 것이야말로 찰리의 크나큰 위엄이자 기쁨이었습니다.\n\n한편 나는 솔직히 유언장 문제를 두고는 후견인과 뜻을 같이할 수가 없었으니, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송을 두고 다소 낙관적인 기대를 품고 있었습니다. 우리 둘 가운데 누가 옳았는지는 곧 드러나겠지만, 나는 분명 기대를 부추기는 쪽이었습니다. 리처드에게는 그 발견이 한바탕 분주함과 들뜸을 불러일으켜 한동안 그를 떠받쳐주었으나, 이제 그는 희망의 탄력마저 잃은 채 내가 보기에 오직 그 열병 같은 불안만 붙들고 있는 듯했습니다. 어느 날 우리가 이 문제를 이야기할 때 후견인이 한 어떤 말로 미루어, 나는 내 결혼이 우리가 고대하라는 말을 들었던 그 개정 기간이 지난 다음에야 이루어지리라는 걸 알았습니다. 그래서 나는 더욱더, 리처드와 에이다가 조금이라도 더 형편이 펴졌을 때 결혼할 수 있다면 얼마나 기쁠까 생각했습니다.\n\n개정 기간이 정말 코앞에 다가왔을 무렵, 후견인은 시외로 불려가 우드코트 씨 일로 요크셔까지 내려갔습니다. 그분은 미리 자기가 그곳에 가 있어야 한다고 나에게 말해두었습니다. 어느 밤 사랑하는 친구네에서 막 돌아와, 내 새 옷가지를 한가운데 두고 앉아 사방에 둘러놓은 그것들을 바라보며 생각에 잠겨 있을 때, 후견인의 편지 한 통이 나에게 전해졌습니다. 편지는 시골로 자기에게 와달라고 청하면서, 어느 역마차에 내 자리를 잡아두었고 아침 몇 시에 시내를 떠나야 하는지를 일러주었습니다. 추신에는 에이다와 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있게 되리라는 말이 덧붙어 있었습니다.\n\n그때 나는 여행만큼 예상 밖인 일도 없었지만, 반 시간 만에 채비를 갖추고 이튿날 이른 아침 지시받은 대로 길을 나섰습니다. 나는 온종일 길을 달리며, 도대체 무슨 일로 이렇게 먼 곳까지 나를 부른 걸까 종일토록 궁금해했습니다. 이런 까닭이려나 했다가, 또 저런 까닭이려나 했다가 했지만, 나는 결코, 결코, 결코 진실 근처에도 가닿지 못했습니다.\n\n여정의 끝에 다다랐을 때는 밤이었고, 후견인이 나를 기다리고 있었습니다. 이건 큰 위안이었으니, 저녁 무렵부터 나는 (편지가 워낙 짧았던 탓에 더더욱) 그분이 편찮으신 게 아닐까 걱정하기 시작했던 것입니다. 그러나 그분은 그 자리에, 더없이 건강한 모습으로 있었습니다. 그 정겨운 얼굴이 가장 밝고 환한 빛을 띤 모습을 다시 보았을 때, 나는 속으로 말했습니다. 또 무슨 크나큰 선행을 베푸셨구나, 하고요. 그렇게 말하는 데 대단한 통찰이 필요한 건 아니었습니다. 그분이 거기 있다는 사실 자체가 친절을 베푸는 행위임을 알고 있었으니까요.\n\n호텔에 저녁상이 차려졌고, 우리 둘만 식탁에 앉았을 때 그분이 말했습니다. \"내가 너를 왜 여기로 데려왔는지 궁금해 죽겠지, 우리 꼬마 아가씨?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 후견인님.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"제가 저를 파티마라고, 후견인님을 푸른수염이라고 여기는 건 아니지만, 좀 궁금하긴 해요.\"\n\n* 파티마와 푸른수염: 페로의 동화 '푸른수염'에 나오는 인물들. 호기심에 못 이겨 들어가서는 안 될 방을 열어보는 아내가 파티마다.\n\"그렇다면 네가 밤에 편히 쉴 수 있도록, 얘야,\" 그분이 명랑하게 대꾸했습니다. \"내일까지 기다리지 않고 말해주마. 나는 어떻게든 우드코트에게, 가엾고 불운한 조에게 베푼 그의 인정과, 내 어린 사촌들에게 베푼 값을 매길 수 없는 봉사와, 우리 모두에게 그가 지니는 소중함에 대해 내 마음을 전하고 싶었단다. 그가 이곳에 정착하기로 정해졌을 때, 나는 그가 제 몸 누일 소박하고 적당한 작은 보금자리 하나를 받아주지 않겠느냐고 청해볼까 하는 생각이 떠올랐지. 그래서 그런 곳을 찾아보게 했더니, 아주 손쉬운 조건으로 그런 곳을 하나 찾아냈고, 나는 그를 위해 그곳을 손질해 사람이 살 만하게 만들어 왔단다. 그런데 그저께 그곳을 둘러보며 다 됐다는 보고를 받고 보니, 내가 살림을 아는 사람이 못 되어서 모든 게 마땅한 대로 되었는지 어떤지를 알 수가 없더구나. 그래서 어떻게든 구할 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 꼬마 살림꾼을 불러다 조언과 의견을 듣기로 한 거지. 그리고 여기 그 사람이 있구나.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"웃으면서 울고, 둘을 한꺼번에 하면서 말이야!\"\n\n그분이 너무나 다정하고, 너무나 선하고, 너무나 존경스러웠기 때문입니다. 나는 그분을 어떻게 생각하는지 말하려 했지만, 한마디도 또렷이 내뱉을 수가 없었습니다.\n\n\"쯧, 쯧!\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"별것도 아닌 일에 너무 호들갑이로구나, 꼬마 아가씨. 아니, 더든 아주머니, 어쩜 그리 흐느끼니, 그렇게 흐느껴서야!\"\n\n\"이건 더없는 기쁨에서 나오는 거예요, 후견인님—감사한 마음으로 가슴이 벅차서요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"네 마음에 들었다니 기쁘구나. 그럴 줄 알았지. 황폐한 집의 꼬마 안주인에게 즐거운 깜짝 선물이 되라고 한 거란다.\"\n\n나는 그분에게 입을 맞추고 눈물을 닦았습니다. \"이제 알겠어요!\" 내가 말했습니다. \"오래전부터 후견인님 얼굴에서 이걸 봐왔거든요.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말 그랬니, 얘야?\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"사람 얼굴을 읽어내는 더든 아주머니라니, 참!\"\n\n그분이 어찌나 별스럽게 쾌활했던지 나도 오래 그렇지 않을 수 없었고, 조금이라도 그렇지 않았던 것이 거의 부끄러울 지경이었습니다. 잠자리에 들었을 때 나는 울었습니다. 울었다고 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다. 다만 그것이 기쁨에서 나온 것이기를 바라지만, 기쁨에서 나온 것인지는 그리 확신할 수 없습니다. 나는 편지의 한마디 한마디를 두 번씩 거듭 외었습니다.\n\n더없이 아름다운 여름 아침이 뒤따랐고, 아침을 먹은 뒤 우리는 팔짱을 끼고 나가 내가 그 대단한 살림꾼의 의견을 내놓아야 할 그 집을 보러 갔습니다. 우리는 옆 담장에 난 문으로 화원에 들어섰는데, 그 열쇠를 그분이 갖고 있었습니다. 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은, 화단과 꽃들이 모두 우리 집 내 화단과 꽃들과 똑같은 방식으로 꾸며져 있다는 것이었습니다.\n\n\"보다시피, 얘야,\" 후견인이 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 내 표정을 살피려 가만히 서서 말했습니다. \"이보다 나은 본보기는 없을 줄 알기에 네 것을 빌렸단다.\"\n\n우리는 푸른 잎새 사이로 버찌가 옹기종기 깃들어 있고 사과나무 그림자가 풀밭에서 장난치는 어여쁜 과수원을 지나 집 자체로 나아갔습니다. 인형의 방들로 이루어진 오두막, 그야말로 시골티 나는 오두막이었지만, 그토록 사랑스러운 곳, 그토록 고요하고 그토록 아름다운 곳, 그 둘레로 그토록 풍요롭고 미소 짓는 들녘이 펼쳐진 곳이었습니다. 물줄기는 저 멀리로 반짝이며 흘러가는데, 한쪽은 온통 여름 초목에 뒤덮였고 다른 한쪽은 윙윙대는 물방앗간을 돌렸으며, 가장 가까운 지점에서는 명랑한 마을 곁 풀밭을 가로질러 어른거렸습니다. 그 마을에서는 크리켓 선수들이 화사한 무리를 이루어 모여들고, 향긋한 서풍에 나부끼는 하얀 천막에서 깃발 하나가 펄럭이고 있었습니다. 그리고 우리가 그 어여쁜 방들을 지나, 작은 시골풍 베란다 문을 빠져나가, 인동덩굴과 재스민과 인동꽃으로 화관처럼 엮인 자그마한 나무 기둥 회랑 아래를 걷는 동안에도, 나는 벽지 무늬에서, 가구 빛깔에서, 그 모든 어여쁜 물건의 배치에서, 그들이 칭찬하면서도 웃어넘기곤 했던 나의 자그마한 취향과 공상을, 나의 자그마한 방식과 궁리를, 사방 어디서나 나의 별난 버릇들을 보았습니다.\n\n이 모든 아름다운 것에 대해 아무리 감탄해도 모자랄 지경이었지만, 이것을 보았을 때 내 마음에는 남모를 의심 하나가 솟았습니다. 아, 이걸로 그분이 더 행복해질까, 하고 나는 생각했습니다. 내가 이렇게 그분 앞에 나서지 않는 편이 그분의 평안을 위해 더 낫지 않았을까? 내가 그분이 생각하던 그런 사람은 아니었어도, 그분은 여전히 나를 무척이나 깊이 사랑했으니, 이것이 그분이 잃었다고 믿는 것을 슬프게 일깨울지도 모르는 일이었습니다. 나는 그분이 나를 잊기를 바라지는 않았습니다—그분은 이런 기억의 보조 장치가 없었더라면 어쩌면 잊지 않았을지도 모릅니다—그러나 내 길이 그분의 길보다 수월했으니, 그분이 그걸로 더 행복해질 수만 있다면 나는 그 정도는 기꺼이 받아들일 수 있었을 것입니다.\n\n\"자, 이제, 꼬마 아가씨,\" 이런 것들을 나에게 보여주며 내가 그것들을 마음에 들어 하는 모습을 지켜보는 그 순간만큼 자랑스럽고 즐거워하는 후견인을 나는 본 적이 없었습니다. \"이제 마지막으로, 이 집 이름이란다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 부르나요, 후견인님?\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 그분이 말했습니다. \"와서 보렴.\"\n\n그분은 그때까지 피해 왔던 현관으로 나를 데려갔고, 우리가 나가기 전에 멈춰 서서 말했습니다. \"얘야, 이름을 짐작하지 못하겠니?\"\n\"모르겠어요!\" 내가 말했습니다.\n\n우리는 현관을 나섰고, 그분은 그 위에 적힌 글자를 나에게 보여주었습니다. 황폐한 집.\n\n그분은 바로 곁 잎새 사이의 자리로 나를 이끌어, 내 곁에 앉아 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 이렇게 말했습니다. \"사랑하는 내 딸아, 그동안 우리 사이에 있었던 일에서, 나는 진정으로 네 행복을 마음 깊이 바랐단다, 바라건대 말이지. 네가 답을 가져왔던 그 편지를 내가 썼을 때,\" 그분은 그 편지를 떠올리며 미소 지었습니다. \"나는 내 행복을 너무 많이 염두에 두었지. 하지만 네 행복도 마음에 두었단다. 다른 사정이었다면 내가 언젠가 너를 아내로 맞으리라는, 네가 아주 어렸을 적 이따금 꾸곤 했던 그 옛 꿈을 다시 꾸었을지 어떨지는 굳이 나 자신에게 물을 필요가 없겠지. 나는 그걸 다시 꾸었고, 편지를 썼고, 너는 답을 가져왔어. 내 말을 잘 따라오고 있니, 얘야?\"\n\n나는 몸이 차가워졌고 심하게 떨렸지만, 그분이 내뱉는 한마디도 놓치지 않았습니다. 내가 앉아 그분을 뚫어지게 바라보고 햇살이 잎새 사이로 부드럽게 비쳐 그분의 맨머리 위로 내려앉는 동안, 나는 그분 위에 어린 그 광채가 마치 천사들의 광채와도 같다고 느꼈습니다.\n\n\"내 말을 듣되, 말은 하지 말거라, 얘야. 이제 말할 차례는 나란다. 내가 한 일이 정말 너를 행복하게 해줄지를 의심하기 시작한 게 언제였는지는 중요치 않아. 우드코트가 돌아왔고, 나는 이내 아무 의심도 남지 않았단다.\"\n\n나는 두 팔로 그분의 목을 끌어안고 그분의 가슴에 고개를 묻고 울었습니다. \"여기 가볍게, 마음 놓고 기대거라, 얘야.\" 그분이 나를 부드럽게 끌어당기며 말했습니다. \"나는 이제 네 후견인이자 네 아버지란다. 마음 놓고 여기 쉬렴.\"\n\n잎새의 부드러운 살랑임처럼 다독이며, 무르익는 날씨처럼 정겹게, 햇살처럼 환하고 자애롭게, 그분은 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"내 말을 알아듣거라, 사랑하는 내 딸아. 너처럼 그토록 효성스럽고 그토록 헌신적인 사람이 나와 함께 만족하고 행복하리라는 데는 의심이 없었단다. 하지만 나는 네가 누구와 함께라면 더 행복할지를 보았지. 더든 아주머니가 그걸 까맣게 모르고 있을 때 내가 그의 비밀을 꿰뚫어 본 건 이상한 일이 아니야. 그 안에서 결코 변할 수 없는 선함을, 정작 그 자신보다 내가 훨씬 더 잘 알고 있었으니까. 그래! 나는 오래도록 앨런 우드코트의 속내를 들어왔지만, 정작 그는 어제, 네가 여기 오기 몇 시간 전에야 내 속내를 들었단다. 그러나 나는 내 에스더의 빛나는 본보기를 그냥 잃어버릴 수는 없었어. 사랑하는 내 딸의 미덕을 한 점이라도 눈에 띄지 않고 기림받지 못한 채로 둘 수는 없었지. 내 딸이 모건 앱 케리그의 혈통에 그저 너그러운 묵인으로 받아들여지게 둘 수는 없었단다. 아니, 웨일스의 모든 산을 황금으로 달아 준다 해도 그럴 수는 없었어!\"\n\n그분은 잠시 멈추고 내 이마에 입을 맞추었고, 나는 다시금 흐느끼며 울었습니다. 그분의 칭찬이 주는 고통스러운 기쁨을 차마 견딜 수 없을 것만 같았기 때문입니다.\n\n\"쉿, 꼬마 아가씨! 울지 말거라. 오늘은 기쁨의 날이 되어야 해. 나는 이날을 손꼽아 기다려왔단다,\" 그분이 의기양양하게 말했습니다. \"몇 달이고 몇 달이고 말이야! 몇 마디만 더 하면, 종종걸음 아주머니, 내 할 말은 끝난단다. 내 에스더의 가치를 티끌만큼도 헛되이 버리지 않겠다고 마음먹고, 나는 우드코트 부인을 따로 불러 속내를 털어놓았지. '자, 부인,' 내가 말했네. '나는 분명히 알아챘소—아니, 사실은 확실히 알고 있소—당신 아들이 내 피후견인을 사랑한다는 걸 말이오. 게다가 내 피후견인이 당신 아들을 사랑한다는 것도 아주 확신하오. 하지만 그 애는 의무감과 애정 때문에 제 사랑을 희생할 것이고, 어찌나 완전하게, 어찌나 철저하게, 어찌나 경건하게 희생할지, 당신이 밤낮으로 지켜본다 해도 결코 눈치채지 못할 것이오.' 그러고는 부인에게 우리 이야기를 다 들려주었단다—우리의—너와 나의 이야기 말이야. '자, 부인,' 내가 말했지. '이걸 다 알고서도, 우리와 함께 와서 사시오. 와서 내 아이를 시시각각 보시오. 당신이 본 것을, 그 애의 혈통—그건 이러이러하오—과 견주어 보고,'—나는 그걸 에둘러 말하기를 경멸했으니까—'그 문제에 대해 마음을 온전히 정한 다음에, 무엇이 참된 정통인지 나에게 말해 보시오.' 아니, 부인의 그 오랜 웨일스 핏줄에 영광 있으라, 얘야,\" 후견인이 열정에 차 외쳤습니다. \"내 믿건대, 그 핏줄이 고동치게 하는 그 가슴은, 더든 아주머니를 향해 내 가슴 못지않게 따뜻하게, 못지않게 감탄하며, 못지않게 사랑스럽게 뛰고 있단다!\"\n\n그분은 다정하게 내 고개를 들어 올렸고, 내가 그분에게 매달리자, 옛 아버지 같은 그 다정한 방식으로 거듭거듭 입을 맞추었습니다. 내가 곰곰이 생각하곤 했던 그 보호자다운 태도 위로, 이제 얼마나 환한 빛이 비추던지요!\n\n\"마지막으로 한마디만 더. 앨런 우드코트가 너에게 말을 건넸을 때, 얘야, 그는 내가 알고 동의한 가운데 그렇게 한 것이란다—하지만 나는 그에게 아무런 부추김도 주지 않았지, 그럼, 안 했고말고. 이 깜짝 선물들이야말로 나에게 크나큰 보답이었으니, 나는 그 한 조각이라도 내놓기에는 너무 인색했단 말이다. 그는 오가는 모든 이야기를 나에게 와서 전하기로 되어 있었고, 그렇게 했지. 더 할 말은 없구나. 사랑하는 내 딸아, 앨런 우드코트는 네 아버지가 숨을 거두고 누웠을 때 그 곁에 서 있었단다—네 어머니 곁에 서 있었지. 이곳이 황폐한 집이란다. 오늘 나는 이 집에 그 꼬마 안주인을 주노라. 그리고 하느님 앞에 맹세컨대, 이날은 내 평생에 가장 환한 날이로구나!\"\n\n그분이 일어서며 나를 함께 일으켜 세웠습니다. 우리는 더는 둘만이 아니었습니다. 내 남편이—나는 이제 꼬박 칠 년의 행복한 세월 동안 그 이름으로 그를 불러왔습니다—내 곁에 서 있었습니다.\n\n\"앨런,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"나로부터 기꺼운 선물을 받게나, 사내가 가질 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 아내일세. 자네가 이 사람을 받을 자격이 있음을 내가 안다는 것 말고 자네에게 무슨 말을 더 하겠나! 이 사람이 자네에게 가져다주는 이 작은 보금자리도 함께 받게. 이 사람이 이곳을 어떻게 만들지는 자네가 알겠지, 앨런. 같은 이름의 그 집을 어떻게 만들어 왔는지 자네가 아니까. 이따금 그 행복을 나에게도 나눠 주게나, 그런들 내가 무얼 희생하겠나? 아무것도, 아무것도 없네.\"\n\n그분은 한 번 더 나에게 입을 맞추었고, 이제 두 눈에 눈물이 어린 채 한결 부드럽게 말했습니다. \"에스더, 사랑하는 내 딸아, 이토록 오랜 세월이 지나, 여기에도 일종의 작별이 있구나. 내 잘못이 너에게 얼마간 괴로움을 안겼다는 걸 안다. 네 마음속 옛자리로 이 늙은 후견인을 다시 들여, 그를 용서해 주려무나. 그리고 그건 네 기억에서 지워 버리렴. 앨런, 내 소중한 사람을 받게나.\"\n\n그분은 푸른 잎의 지붕 아래에서 걸어 나와, 바깥 햇살 속에 멈춰 서서 우리 쪽으로 명랑하게 돌아서며 말했습니다. \"나는 이 근방 어딘가에 있을 테니. 서풍이로구나, 꼬마 아가씨, 정서풍이야! 이제 누구도 나에게 더 고마워하지 말거라, 나는 다시 홀아비 시절 버릇으로 돌아갈 참이니까. 누구든 이 경고를 무시한다면, 나는 달아나서 다시는 돌아오지 않을 테다!\"\n\n그날 우리의 행복은 얼마나 컸던지, 그 기쁨, 그 안식, 그 희망, 그 감사, 그 더없는 복락이라니! 우리는 그달이 가기 전에 결혼하기로 했지만, 우리 집으로 와서 거기 살림을 차릴 시기는 리처드와 에이다에게 달려 있었습니다.\n\n이튿날 우리 셋은 함께 집으로 돌아갔습니다. 시내에 닿자마자 앨런은 리처드를 보러, 그리고 우리의 기쁜 소식을 그와 내 사랑하는 친구에게 전하러 곧장 갔습니다. 늦은 시각이긴 했지만, 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 잠깐 에이다에게 가볼 작정이었습니다. 그래도 먼저 후견인과 함께 집으로 가서 그분의 차를 끓여 드리고 그분 곁 낡은 의자에 앉으려 했습니다. 그 의자가 이렇게 빨리 비게 될 거라고는 생각하고 싶지 않았기 때문입니다.\n\n집에 돌아와 보니, 어느 젊은이가 그 하루 동안에만 세 번이나 나를 찾아왔는데, 세 번째 방문 때 내가 밤 열 시 전에는 돌아오지 않을 거라는 말을 듣고는, 그쯤 다시 들르겠다는 말을 남겼다고 했습니다. 그는 세 번 명함을 두고 갔습니다. 거피 씨였습니다.\n\n그 방문의 목적이 궁금해진 데다, 나는 늘 그 방문객을 무언가 우스꽝스러운 것과 결부시켜 떠올리곤 했던 터라, 거피 씨를 두고 웃다가 그만 후견인에게 그의 옛 청혼과 그 뒤의 철회 이야기를 하게 되었습니다. \"그렇다면,\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"이 영웅을 꼭 맞아들여야겠구나.\" 그래서 거피 씨가 다시 오면 안으로 들이라는 지시가 내려졌는데, 그 지시가 떨어지기가 무섭게 그가 다시 왔습니다.\n\n그는 후견인이 나와 함께 있는 것을 보고 당황했지만, 이내 정신을 차리고 말했습니다. \"안녕하십니까, 선생님?\"\n\n\"안녕하시오, 선생?\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님, 그럭저럭 지냅니다.\" 거피 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"제 어머니, 올드스트리트로드의 거피 부인과, 제 각별한 벗 위블 씨를 소개해도 되겠습니까. 다시 말해, 제 벗이 위블이라는 이름으로 통해 왔습니다만, 그 이름은 사실 진짜로 조블링입니다.\"\n\n후견인이 그들에게 앉으라고 청했고, 그들은 모두 자리에 앉았습니다.\n\n\"토니,\" 어색한 침묵 끝에 거피 씨가 자기 벗에게 말했습니다. \"자네가 사건을 열어 주겠나?\"\n\n\"자네가 직접 하게나.\" 그 벗이 다소 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"그럼, 잔다이스 씨, 선생님,\" 잠시 생각한 끝에 거피 씨가 입을 떼었습니다. 그의 어머니는 그게 무척이나 즐거웠던지, 팔꿈치로 조블링 씨를 쿡쿡 찌르고 나에게 더없이 별난 방식으로 윙크하며 그 즐거움을 드러냈습니다. \"저는 서머슨 양을 단둘이 만나뵐 줄 알았던 터라, 존경하올 선생님이 자리하실 줄은 미처 예상하지 못했습니다. 그렇지만 서머슨 양이 어쩌면 선생님께 말씀드렸을지 모르겠습니다만, 예전에 저희 사이에 무슨 일이 좀 오갔다는 것을요?\"\n\n\"서머슨 양이,\" 후견인이 미소 지으며 대꾸했습니다. \"그런 취지의 이야기를 나에게 전한 바 있소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"일이 한결 수월해지는군요. 선생님, 저는 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에서 견습 계약을 마쳤습니다. 모든 당사자가 만족할 만하게 마무리되었다고 믿습니다. 저는 이제 (사람을 진이 빠지도록 들볶는 시험을, 알 필요도 없는 잡다한 헛소리에 관해 치른 끝에) 변호사 명부에 등재되어 자격증을 받았으니, 선생님께서 보시는 게 무슨 흡족함이 된다면 보여 드리지요.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 거피 씨.\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다. \"나는 기꺼이—내가 법률 용어를 쓰는 것 같소만—그 자격증을 인정하겠소.\"\n\n그리하여 거피 씨는 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내려던 것을 그만두고 그것 없이 말을 이었습니다.\n\n\"저 자신은 자본이 없습니다만, 제 어머니께서 연금 형태로 된 약간의 재산을 가지고 계십니다\"—여기서 거피 씨의 어머니는 그 말을 아무리 즐겨도 모자란다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들고, 손수건을 입에 갖다 대며 다시금 나에게 윙크했습니다—\"그러니 사업을 꾸리며 들어가는 자질구레한 경비로 몇 파운드쯤은 결코 모자랄 일이 없습니다. 게다가 무이자이니, 아시다시피 그게 이점이지요.\" 거피 씨가 감정을 실어 말했습니다.\n\n\"분명 이점이오.\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"저에게는 약간의 연줄이 있습니다,\" 거피 씨가 말을 이었습니다. \"그게 램버스의 월컷 광장 쪽으로 뻗어 있지요. 그래서 저는 그 동네에 집을 한 채 얻었는데, 제 벗들의 의견으로는 거저나 다름없는 거래입니다 (세금이 우습도록 적고, 붙박이 가구 사용료까지 집세에 포함되어 있거든요). 거기서 곧바로 제 사업을 차릴 작정입니다.\"\n\n여기서 거피 씨의 어머니는 또 한바탕 유난스럽게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고, 자기를 봐주는 사람이라면 누구에게나 짓궂게 미소 짓기 시작했습니다.\n\n\"부엌을 빼고 방이 여섯입니다,\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"제 벗들의 의견으로는 널찍한 집이지요. 제가 벗들이라고 말할 때, 그건 주로 제 벗 조블링을 가리키는데, 제가 믿기로 이 친구는 저를,\" 거피 씨가 감상적인 분위기로 그를 바라보았습니다. \"어린 시절부터 알아온 사이입니다.\"\n\n조블링 씨가 두 다리를 슬쩍 미끄러뜨리는 동작으로 이를 확인해 주었습니다.\n\n\"제 벗 조블링이 사무원 자격으로 저를 도울 것이며, 그 집에 함께 살 것입니다,\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"제 어머니께서도 올드스트리트로드의 현재 거처가 분기 임대를 마치고 만료되면 그 집에 함께 사실 것입니다. 그러니 어울릴 사람이 모자랄 일은 없겠지요. 제 벗 조블링은 천성이 취향상 귀족적인 데다, 상류 사회의 동향에도 밝아서, 제가 지금 펼쳐 보이는 이 계획을 전적으로 뒷받침해 줍니다.\"\n\n조블링 씨가 \"분명히 그렇습니다\"라고 말하며 거피 씨 어머니의 팔꿈치로부터 조금 물러났습니다.\n\n\"자, 선생님께서는 서머슨 양의 속내를 들으시는 분이니, 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠습니다만,\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"(어머니, 부디 좀 가만히 계셔 주셨으면 합니다만) 서머슨 양의 모습이 한때 제 가슴에 새겨져 있었고, 제가 그분께 청혼을 했더랬습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 들었소.\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다.\n\n\"제가 어찌할 수 없었던, 아니 오히려 정반대였던 사정들이,\" 거피 씨가 말을 이었습니다. \"한동안 그 모습의 인상을 흐려놓았지요. 그때 서머슨 양의 처신은 더없이 품위 있었습니다. 한술 더 떠 대범했다고까지 말할 수 있겠습니다.\"\n\n후견인이 내 어깨를 토닥였는데 무척이나 재미있어하는 눈치였습니다.\n\n\"자, 선생님,\" 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"이제 저 자신이 그런 마음가짐에 이르러서, 대범한 처신을 서로 주고받기를 바라게 되었습니다. 저는 서머슨 양께, 어쩌면 그분이 제게 그럴 능력이 있다고는 거의 여기지 않으셨을 그런 높은 경지로 제가 올라설 수 있음을 증명하고 싶습니다. 제 가슴에서 뽑혀 나갔으리라 짐작했던 그 모습이, 알고 보니 뽑혀 나가지 않았더군요. 그것이 제게 미치는 영향력은 여전히 어마어마합니다. 그래서 그것에 굴복하여, 저는 우리 가운데 누구도 어찌할 수 없었던 그 사정들을 못 본 척 넘기고, 예전에 영광스럽게도 제가 서머슨 양께 드렸던 그 청혼을 다시금 드리고자 합니다. 월컷 광장의 집과, 사업과, 저 자신을 서머슨 양 앞에 바치오니, 부디 받아 주시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 대범하구려, 선생.\" 후견인이 말했습니다.\n\n\"네, 선생님,\" 거피 씨가 솔직하게 대답했습니다. \"제 바람은 대범하게 처신하자는 것입니다. 저는 서머슨 양께 이런 제안을 드리는 것이 결코 제 몸값을 떨어뜨리는 일이라고 여기지 않으며, 제 벗들의 의견도 그렇지 않습니다. 그래도 제 자잘한 결점들을 상쇄할 만한 것으로 헤아려 주실 만한 사정들이 있으니, 그렇게 하여 공정하고 형평에 맞는 균형에 이를 수 있겠다는 점을 말씀드립니다.\"\n\n\"내가 직접,\" 후견인이 종을 울리며 웃으며 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양을 대신해 그대의 청혼에 답하리다. 그녀는 그대의 훌륭한 뜻을 매우 고맙게 여기며, 그대에게 좋은 밤 되기를 빌고, 그대가 잘되기를 빈다오.\"\n\n\"오!\" 거피 씨가 멍한 표정으로 말했습니다. \"그게, 선생님, 수락에 해당합니까, 거절입니까, 아니면 검토입니까?\"\n\n\"단호한 거절에 해당하오, 괜찮다면.\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다.\n\n거피 씨는 못 믿겠다는 눈으로 자기 벗을 보았다가, 갑자기 몹시 화가 난 자기 어머니를 보았다가, 바닥을 보았다가, 천장을 보았습니다.\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그렇다면, 조블링, 자네가 스스로 자처하는 그런 벗이라면, 어머니를 원치도 않는 곳에 그대로 두지 말고 통로 밖으로 모셔 내드릴 만도 한데 말이지.\"\n\n그러나 거피 부인은 통로 밖으로 나오기를 한사코 거부했습니다. 들으려고도 하지 않았습니다. \"아니, 썩 꺼지지 못해,\" 그녀가 후견인에게 말했습니다. \"무슨 뜻이야? 내 아들이 당신한테 모자란단 말이야? 부끄러운 줄 알아야지. 썩 나가!\"\n\n\"이보시오, 부인.\" 후견인이 대꾸했습니다. \"내 집 내 방에서 나가라고 청하는 건 도무지 사리에 맞지 않소.\"\n\n\"그딴 건 모르겠고,\" 거피 부인이 말했습니다. \"썩 나가. 우리가 당신한테 모자라거든, 가서 모자라지 않은 사람을 구해 와. 어서 가서 찾으라고.\"\n\n거피 부인의 그 익살 부리는 재간이 더없이 깊은 모욕감으로 돌변하는 그 잽싼 모양새에 나는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았습니다.\n\n\"가서 당신한테 모자라지 않을 사람을 찾으라니까,\" 거피 부인이 거듭 말했습니다. \"썩 꺼져!\" 거피 씨의 어머니를 그토록 놀라게 하고 그토록 분개하게 만드는 일은, 우리가 나가지 않는 것만 한 게 없는 듯했습니다. \"왜 안 나가는 거야?\" 거피 부인이 말했습니다. \"여기서 뭘 꾸물대고 있는 거냐고?\"\n\n\"어머니,\" 그녀가 후견인에게로 슬금슬금 다가갈 때마다 늘 앞을 막아서서 한쪽 어깨로 밀어내며 아들이 끼어들었습니다. \"제발 입 좀 다물어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"안 돼, 윌리엄,\" 그녀가 대꾸했습니다. \"안 다물 거야! 저 사람이 나가지 않는 한, 안 다물 거라고!\"\n\n그러나 거피 씨와 조블링 씨는 둘이 함께 (이제 막 험한 말을 퍼붓기 시작한) 거피 씨의 어머니를 에워싸, 그녀의 거센 뜻을 거슬러 가며 아래층으로 데려갔습니다. 그녀의 몸이 계단을 한 칸씩 내려갈 때마다 목소리는 한 칸씩 높아져 갔고, 우리더러 당장 우리한테 모자라지 않을 사람을 가서 찾으라고, 무엇보다도 우리더러 썩 나가라고 줄곧 우겨 댔습니다.\n\n개정 기간이 시작되었고, 후견인은 켄지 씨로부터 이틀 뒤면 소송이 진행된다는 통지를 받았습니다. 나는 그 유언장에 충분히 큰 희망을 품고 있어 마음이 들떠 있었기에, 앨런과 나는 그날 아침 법정에 가보기로 했습니다. 리처드는 몹시 심란해했고, 병이 아직 마음의 병이긴 했어도 어찌나 쇠약하고 기운이 없는지, 내 사랑하는 아이는 정말이지 그를 받쳐줘야 할 절박한 처지에 있었습니다. 그러나 그녀는 이제 머지않아—아주 가까이 다가온—자기에게 찾아올 도움을 내다보며, 결코 시들지 않았습니다.\n\n소송이 진행될 곳은 웨스트민스터였습니다. 장담하건대 그곳에서 이미 백 번도 넘게 진행되었겠지만, 나는 이번에는 어떤 결말로 이어질지도 모른다는 생각을 떨쳐낼 수 없었습니다. 우리는 시간을 넉넉히 잡아 웨스트민스터 홀에 닿으려고 아침 식사를 마치자마자 집을 나섰고, 활기 넘치는 거리를 지나 그곳으로 함께 걸어갔습니다—어찌나 행복하고 묘하게 느껴지던지!\n\n길을 걸으며 리처드와 에이다를 위해 무엇을 해줄 수 있을지 궁리하던 차에, 누군가 \"에스더! 사랑하는 에스더! 에스더!\" 하고 부르는 소리가 들렸습니다. 캐디 젤리비였습니다. 그녀가(워낙 학생이 많아서) 이제 학생들을 찾아다니느라 빌려 타고 다니는 작은 마차의 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고는, 백 야드나 떨어진 거리에서 나를 와락 끌어안기라도 할 것 같았습니다. 나는 후견인이 해주신 모든 일을 알리려고 그녀에게 짧은 편지를 써 보냈지만, 직접 찾아가 만날 틈이 도무지 없던 터였습니다. 우리는 당연히 발길을 돌렸고, 그 다정한 아이는 황홀경에 빠진 상태였습니다. 나에게 꽃을 가져다주던 그날 밤 이야기를 어찌나 신나서 떠들어대던지, 두 손으로 내 얼굴을(보닛까지 통째로) 감싸 쥐려고 어찌나 작정하던지, 온갖 사랑스러운 이름으로 나를 불러대며 앨런에게는 내가 자기한테 무슨 무슨 일을 해주었다고—나도 모를 일까지—늘어놓으며 그토록 마구잡이로 굴어대는 통에, 나는 그만 그 작은 마차에 올라타 그녀가 하고 싶은 말과 행동을 마음껏 하도록 내버려두며 진정시킬 수밖에 없었습니다. 창가에 선 앨런은 캐디만큼이나 기뻐했고, 나는 또 두 사람 못지않게 기뻤습니다. 그러니 내가 웃음을 터뜨리며, 얼굴이 붉어진 채, 차림새는 엉망진창이 되어, 보이는 한 끝까지 마차 창밖으로 우리를 지켜보던 캐디를 돌아보며 나왔다는 게 아니라, 그래도 그 자리를 빠져나왔다는 게 신기할 따름입니다.\n\n이 일로 우리는 십오 분쯤 늦어버렸고, 웨스트민스터 홀에 닿았을 때는 그날의 일정이 이미 시작된 뒤였습니다. 더 나쁜 것은, 대법정에 어찌나 유난스러운 인파가 몰려들었는지 문간까지 꽉 들어차서, 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보지도 듣지도 못했다는 사실이었습니다. 무언가 우스운 일인 듯했습니다. 이따금 웃음소리가 터지고 \"정숙!\" 하는 외침이 따랐으니까요. 무언가 흥미로운 일인 듯했습니다. 다들 더 가까이 가려고 떠밀고 안간힘을 썼으니까요. 무언가 법조계 신사들을 잔뜩 신나게 하는 일인 듯했습니다. 인파 바깥쪽에 가발에 구레나룻을 한 젊은 변호사가 여럿 있었는데, 그중 한 사람이 다른 이들에게 그 이야기를 들려주자 다들 주머니에 손을 찔러 넣고 허리가 꺾이도록 웃어대며 홀 바닥을 쿵쿵 구르며 돌아다녔으니까요.\n\n우리는 곁에 있던 신사에게 무슨 소송이 진행 중인지 아느냐고 물었습니다. 그는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스라고 일러주었습니다. 우리는 그 소송에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 아느냐고 물었습니다. 그는 정말이지, 아니, 모르겠다고, 아무도 안 적이 없다고 했지만, 자기가 알아낸 바로는 끝났다고 했습니다. 오늘치가 끝난 거냐고 우리는 물었습니다. 아니, 그가 말했습니다, 아주 끝났다고.\n\n아주 끝났다니!\n\n이 영문 모를 대답을 듣고서 우리는 어리둥절함에 사로잡혀 서로를 바라보았습니다. 유언장이 마침내 모든 걸 바로잡아 리처드와 에이다가 부자가 될 수 있다는 게 정말 가능한 일일까? 너무 좋아서 도무지 사실 같지 않았습니다. 아아, 정말 그랬습니다!\n\n우리의 마음 졸임은 짧게 끝났습니다. 이내 인파가 흩어지더니 사람들이 줄지어 쏟아져 나왔는데, 다들 상기되고 달아오른 얼굴에 탁한 공기까지 잔뜩 몰고 나왔습니다. 그래도 하나같이 더없이 즐거워하는 모습이라, 법정이 아니라 어떤 소극이나 요술 구경에서 나오는 사람들 같았습니다. 우리는 한쪽으로 비켜서서 아는 얼굴이 없나 살폈는데, 곧 큼직한 서류 뭉치들이 들려 나오기 시작했습니다—가방에 든 뭉치, 어떤 가방에도 들어가지 않을 만큼 큰 뭉치, 갖가지 모양에 아예 형체도 없는 어마어마한 종이 더미였습니다. 운반하는 사람들은 그 무게에 비틀거리며 우선 아무렇게나 홀 바닥에 내던져 놓고는, 더 가지고 나오려고 되돌아갔습니다. 그 서기들조차 웃고 있었습니다. 우리는 그 서류들을 흘끗 들여다보았고, 어디에나 잔다이스 대 잔다이스라고 적혀 있는 것을 보고는, 그 한복판에 서 있던 관리처럼 보이는 사람에게 소송이 끝났느냐고 물었습니다. 그렇다고, 마침내 다 끝장났다고 그가 말하고는, 그 역시 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n바로 그때 우리는 켄지 씨가 점잖고 상냥한 위엄을 두른 채 법정에서 나오는 것을 보았습니다. 그는 공손하게 제 가방을 직접 들고 따르는 볼스 씨의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었습니다. 우리를 먼저 알아본 건 볼스 씨였습니다. \"여기 서머슨 양이 있군요, 변호사님.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"그리고 우드코트 씨도요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇군! 그래. 정말이지!\" 켄지 씨가 세련된 정중함으로 나에게 모자를 들어 올리며 말했습니다. \"안녕하셨습니까? 만나서 반갑습니다. 잔다이스 씨는 여기 안 계시오?\"\n\n네. 그분은 결코 이런 곳에 오시지 않는다고 나는 그에게 일러주었습니다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 켄지 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"오늘 그분이 여기 안 계신 게 차라리 다행이오. 그분의—내 좋은 벗이 안 계신 자리이니 이렇게 말해도 될까요, 굽힐 줄 모르는 그 독특한 견해 말이오—어쩌면 더 굳어졌을지도 모를 테니까. 사리에 닿게는 아니지만, 더 굳어졌을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"실례지만 오늘 무슨 일이 처리된 겁니까?\" 앨런이 물었습니다.\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨소?\" 켄지 씨가 지나칠 만큼 점잖게 되물었습니다.\n\n\"오늘 무슨 일이 처리됐습니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 처리됐느냐,\" 켄지 씨가 되뇌었습니다. \"그렇지. 그래. 음, 처리된 건 별로 없소. 별로 없어요. 우리는 제동이 걸렸소—느닷없이 멈춰 섰다고 해야겠군요—그 무엇이라고 할까요, 문턱에서 말이오.\"\n\n\"이 유언장이 진짜 문서로 인정됩니까, 변호사님?\" 앨런이 물었습니다. \"그것 좀 말씀해 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"내가 할 수만 있다면야 더없이 기꺼이,\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 우리는 거기까지 들어가지 못했소. 거기까지 들어가지 못했어요.\"\n\n\"거기까지 들어가지 못했지요.\" 볼스 씨가 그 낮고 속에서 울리는 목소리를 메아리처럼 되뇌었습니다.\n\n\"잘 헤아려 보셔야 하오, 우드코트 씨.\" 켄지 씨가 그 은빛 흙손 같은 말솜씨를 설득조로 매끄럽게 놀리며 말했습니다. \"이것은 위대한 소송이었고, 길게 끌어온 소송이었으며, 복잡한 소송이었소. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 대법원 관행의 기념비라 일컬어져 왔는데, 그 표현이 영 틀린 것도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"게다가 인내심이 그 위에 오래도록 앉아 있었지요.\" 앨런이 말했습니다.\n\n* 인내심: 셰익스피어의 「십이야」에 나오는 \"기념비 위에 앉은 인내처럼\"이라는 구절을 빗댄 말이다.\n\"참으로 훌륭하시오, 우드코트 씨.\" 켄지 씨가 특유의 어딘가 거들먹거리는 웃음을 흘리며 대꾸했습니다. \"참 훌륭해요! 더 헤아려 보셔야 하오, 우드코트 씨.\" 거의 준엄해 보일 만큼 위엄을 갖추며 말을 이었습니다. \"이 위대한 소송에 얽힌 수많은 난관과 우발 사태와 빼어난 의제(擬制)와 절차 형식에는, 학식과 능력과 웅변과 지식과 지성이, 우드코트 씨, 고매한 지성이 쏟아부어졌소. 여러 해에 걸쳐, 그—에—말하자면 법조계의 정화(精華)와, 그—에—감히 덧붙이자면, 대법관석의 무르익은 가을 결실이—잔다이스 대 잔다이스에 아낌없이 베풀어졌단 말이오. 만약 대중이 이 위대한 손길의 혜택을 누리고, 나라가 그 장식을 얻는다면, 그것은 돈으로든 돈에 값하는 것으로든 치러져야 하는 법이오, 우드코트 씨.\"\n\n\"켄지 씨.\" 앨런이 순간 모든 것을 깨달은 표정으로 말했습니다. \"실례지만 우리는 시간이 촉박합니다. 그러니까 재산 전체가 소송 비용으로 다 삼켜진 것으로 드러났다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"흠! 그런 것 같소이다.\" 켄지 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"볼스 씨, 댁은 어찌 생각하오?\"\n\n\"그런 것 같습니다.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"그래서 소송이 그렇게 효력을 잃고 녹아 없어진다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 켄지 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"볼스 씨?\"\n\n\"아마 그렇겠지요.\" 볼스 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n\"내 가장 사랑하는 사람.\" 앨런이 속삭였습니다. \"이건 리처드의 가슴을 무너뜨릴 거요!\"\n\n그의 얼굴에는 그런 두려움의 충격이 어려 있었고, 그가 리처드를 워낙 속속들이 알았으며, 나 또한 리처드가 차츰 쇠해가는 모습을 그토록 많이 지켜본 터라, 내 사랑하는 아이가 불길한 사랑의 예감을 가득 담아 나에게 했던 말이 귓가에 조종(弔鐘)처럼 울렸습니다.\n\n\"혹 C씨를 찾으신다면, 변호사님.\" 볼스 씨가 우리 뒤를 따라오며 말했습니다. \"법정 안에 계실 겁니다. 좀 쉬게 그곳에 두고 왔어요. 안녕히 가십시오, 변호사님. 안녕히 가십시오, 서머슨 양.\" 그는 가방 끈을 비비 꼬아 매며 켄지 씨를 서둘러 뒤따라가려 했는데—그 상냥한 대화 상대의 그늘을 떠나기가 두려운 듯했습니다—나에게 그 느릿느릿 집어삼키는 듯한 눈길을 던지더니, 마치 의뢰인의 마지막 한 점을 삼켜버린 양 한 번 숨을 헐떡였습니다. 그러고는 단추를 목까지 채운 그 검고 음산한 형체가 홀 끝의 낮은 문 쪽으로 미끄러져 사라졌습니다.\n\n\"내 사랑.\" 앨런이 말했습니다. \"당신이 나에게 맡긴 임무를 잠시 동안만 내게 맡겨주오. 이 소식을 가지고 집으로 갔다가, 이따 에이다네로 와요!\"\n\n나는 그가 나를 마차에 태우게 두지 않고, 한순간도 지체 말고 리처드에게 가달라고, 그가 바라는 대로 하게 나를 내버려두라고 간청했습니다. 서둘러 집으로 와서 후견인을 찾아, 내가 어떤 소식을 안고 돌아왔는지 차근차근 말씀드렸습니다. \"꼬마 아주머니.\" 그가 자신을 위해서는 조금도 동요하지 않고 말했습니다. \"어떤 조건으로든 그 소송이 끝났다는 건 내가 바랐던 것보다 더 큰 축복이로구나. 하지만 내 가엾은 어린 조카들은!\"\n\n우리는 오전 내내 그들 이야기를 나누며 무엇을 할 수 있을지 의논했습니다. 오후에 후견인은 나와 함께 사이먼드 법학원까지 걸어와 문 앞에서 나를 내려주었습니다. 나는 위층으로 올라갔습니다. 사랑하는 그 아이는 내 발소리를 듣자 좁은 복도로 나와 내 목을 끌어안았지만, 곧 마음을 가다듬고는 리처드가 나를 여러 번 찾았다고 말했습니다. 앨런이 법정 한구석에 앉아 있는 그를 발견했는데, 마치 돌로 만든 석상 같았다고 그녀가 일러주었습니다. 깨워서 정신을 차리게 하자 그가 벌떡 일어나 사나운 목소리로 판사에게 무슨 말을 하려는 듯했답니다. 하지만 입안에 피가 가득 차오르는 바람에 멈췄고, 앨런이 그를 집으로 데려왔다고 했습니다.\n\n내가 들어갔을 때 그는 눈을 감은 채 소파에 누워 있었습니다. 탁자에는 강심제가 놓여 있었고, 방은 되도록 바람이 잘 통하게 하면서도 어둑하게 해두었으며, 더없이 정돈되고 조용했습니다. 앨런은 그의 뒤에 서서 무겁게 그를 지켜보고 있었습니다. 그의 얼굴은 내게 핏기라곤 전혀 없어 보였고, 그가 나를 보지 못하는 채로 내가 그를 바라보게 되자, 비로소 처음으로 그가 얼마나 야위었는지 똑똑히 보였습니다. 그래도 그는 오랜만에 보는 그 어느 때보다 잘생겨 보였습니다.\n\n나는 말없이 그의 곁에 앉았습니다. 이윽고 눈을 뜬 그가 기운 없는 목소리로, 그러나 예전의 그 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. \"더든 아주머니, 키스해 줘요, 그대여!\"\n\n이토록 쇠약한 상태에서도 그가 명랑하게 앞날을 내다보는 모습을 보니, 나에게는 큰 위안이자 놀라움이었습니다. 우리가 곧 결혼할 거라는 사실에, 말로는 다 표현할 수 없을 만큼 행복하다고 그가 말했습니다. 내 남편은 자기와 에이다에게 수호천사였다며, 우리 둘을 축복하고 인생이 우리에게 베풀 수 있는 모든 기쁨을 빌어주었습니다. 그가 내 남편의 손을 잡아 제 가슴에 가져다 대는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 내 심장마저 터져버릴 것만 같았습니다.\n\n우리는 되도록 앞날에 관해 이야기했고, 그는 발로 설 수만 있다면 우리 결혼식에 꼭 참석해야겠다고 여러 번 말했습니다. 에이다가 어떻게든 자기를 데려갈 방도를 마련해줄 거라고 그가 말했습니다. \"그럼요, 물론이에요, 사랑하는 리처드!\" 그러나 사랑하는 그 아이가 그토록 평온하고 아름답게, 그토록 희망에 차서—이제 자기에게 곧 찾아올 도움을 그토록 가까이 둔 채—그에게 대답하는 동안, 나는 알았습니다—나는 알고 있었습니다!\n\n그가 너무 많이 말하는 것은 좋지 않았기에, 그가 잠잠해지면 우리도 잠잠해졌습니다. 그의 곁에 앉아, 나는 사랑하는 사람을 위해 바느질하는 척했습니다. 그가 늘 내가 바쁜 걸 두고 농담을 하곤 했으니까요. 에이다는 그의 머리를 제 팔에 받쳐 안고 베개에 기대어 있었습니다. 그는 자주 깜빡 졸았고, 깨어날 때마다 그가 보이지 않으면 무엇보다 먼저 \"우드코트는 어디 있지?\" 하고 물었습니다.\n\n내가 눈을 들어 후견인이 작은 현관에 서 있는 것을 보았을 때는 어느덧 저녁이 찾아온 뒤였습니다. \"저기 누구죠, 더든 아주머니?\" 리처드가 나에게 물었습니다. 문은 그의 등 뒤에 있었지만, 그는 내 얼굴에서 누군가 와 있다는 것을 알아챘던 것입니다.\n\n나는 앨런에게 어찌하면 좋을지 눈짓으로 물었고, 그가 \"그래요\" 하고 고갯짓하자, 리처드에게 몸을 굽혀 일러주었습니다. 후견인은 무슨 일인지 알아채고는 곧장 내 곁으로 가만히 다가와 리처드의 손에 자기 손을 얹었습니다. \"오, 어르신.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"어르신은 좋은 분이세요, 좋은 분이에요!\" 그러고는 처음으로 울음을 터뜨렸습니다.\n\n좋은 사람의 표상 같은 후견인은 내 자리에 앉아, 리처드의 손을 자기 손으로 계속 잡았습니다.\n\n\"릭, 얘야.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"구름이 걷혔고 이제 환해졌구나. 이제 우리는 볼 수 있어. 우리는 다들 어리둥절했지, 릭, 너나 나나 더하고 덜할 뿐. 그게 무슨 대수겠니! 그래, 좀 어떠냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"몹시 기운이 없어요, 어르신. 하지만 더 튼튼해지길 바라요. 저는 이제 세상을 다시 시작해야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼! 옳은 말이다!\" 후견인이 외쳤습니다.\n\n\"이제 옛날 방식으로 시작하지는 않을 거예요.\" 리처드가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. \"이제 교훈을 하나 얻었어요, 어르신. 혹독한 교훈이었지만, 제가 정말로 그걸 배웠다는 건 믿으셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"됐다, 됐어.\" 후견인이 그를 다독이며 말했습니다. \"됐다, 됐어, 됐어, 얘야!\"\n\n\"제가 생각해 봤는데요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 말을 이었습니다. \"이 세상에서 제가 무엇보다도 보고 싶은 건 그들의 집이에요—더든 아주머니와 우드코트의 집 말이에요. 기운을 차리기 시작할 때 그리로 옮겨갈 수만 있다면, 어디서보다도 거기서 더 빨리 나을 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"실은 나도 그 생각을 하고 있었단다, 릭.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"우리 꼬마 아주머니도 마찬가지고. 그애와 나는 바로 오늘 그 이야기를 했지. 그애 남편이 반대하지 않을 게다. 어떠냐?\"\n\n리처드가 미소 지으며, 소파 머리맡에 서 있는 그에게 닿으려고 팔을 들어 올렸습니다.\n\n\"에이다 이야기는 하지 않겠어요.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"하지만 저는 그녀를 생각해요, 정말 많이 생각해 왔어요. 저 사람을 보세요! 여기 좀 보세요, 어르신. 정작 자기야말로 이 베개에 기대 쉬어야 할 처지인데도 이렇게 몸을 굽히고 있잖아요, 내 사랑, 내 가엾은 사람!\"\n\n그가 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 우리 가운데 누구도 입을 열지 않았습니다. 그는 천천히 그녀를 놓아주었고, 그녀는 우리를 바라보다가 하늘을 올려다보며 입술을 움직였습니다.\n\n\"제가 황폐한 집으로 내려가면요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"어르신께 들려드릴 이야기가 많을 거예요. 어르신도 제게 보여주실 게 많으실 테고요. 가실 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 가고말고, 릭.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다. 어르신답네요, 어르신다워요.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"하지만 다 어르신다운 일이죠. 어르신이 어떻게 그 집을 꾸미셨는지, 에스더가 늘 좋아하던 취향과 버릇을 어떻게 다 기억해 두셨는지 사람들이 제게 얘기해 줬어요. 마치 옛날 그 황폐한 집으로 다시 돌아가는 것 같겠어요.\"\n\n\"너도 거기 오면 좋겠구나, 릭. 알다시피 나는 이제 외로운 사람이니, 나를 찾아오는 건 자선을 베푸는 일이지. 나를 찾아와 주는 자선 말이다, 얘야!\" 그가 에이다의 황금빛 머리칼을 가만히 쓰다듬으며, 그 한 줌을 입술에 가져다 대고는 그녀에게 다시 말했습니다. (그녀가 홀로 남겨진다면 그녀를 보살피겠노라고 그는 마음속으로 다짐했던 것 같습니다.)\n\n\"그것은 괴로운 꿈이었나요?\" 리처드가 후견인의 두 손을 간절히 움켜쥐며 말했습니다.\n\n\"그뿐이란다, 릭. 그뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그러니 좋은 분이신 어르신은 그것을 한낱 꿈으로 흘려보내고, 그 꿈꾼 자를 용서하고 가엾이 여겨, 그가 깨어날 때 너그럽게 북돋아 주실 수 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"그럼, 할 수 있고말고. 나라고 또 다른 꿈꾸는 자가 아니고 무엇이겠니, 릭?\"\n\n\"저는 세상을 다시 시작할 거예요!\" 리처드가 눈에 빛을 띠며 말했습니다.\n\n내 남편이 에이다 쪽으로 조금 더 다가섰고, 나는 그가 후견인에게 경계하라는 듯 엄숙하게 손을 들어 올리는 것을 보았습니다.\n\n\"제가 언제쯤 이곳을 떠나 그 정겨운 고장으로, 옛 시절이 있는 그곳으로 갈 수 있을까요? 에이다가 제게 어떤 사람이었는지 말할 기운을 얻을 그곳으로, 제 숱한 잘못과 어리석음을 떠올릴 수 있을 그곳으로, 아직 태어나지 않은 제 아이를 이끌 사람이 되도록 저를 가다듬을 그곳으로요.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"언제쯤 갈 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"릭, 얘야, 네가 충분히 튼튼해지면 가는 거란다.\" 후견인이 대답했습니다.\n\n\"에이다, 내 사랑!\"\n\n그가 몸을 조금 일으키려 했습니다. 앨런이 그를 일으켜 그녀의 품에 안기게 해주었습니다. 그것이 그가 바라던 바였으니까요.\n\n\"내 사랑, 나는 당신에게 못할 짓을 많이 했소. 나는 당신이 가는 길에 가엾은 떠돌이 그림자처럼 드리웠고, 당신을 가난과 고생에 시집보냈으며, 당신의 재산을 바람결에 흩어버렸소. 내가 세상을 다시 시작하기 전에, 이 모든 걸 용서해 주겠소, 나의 에이다?\"\n\n그녀가 몸을 굽혀 그에게 입 맞추자 그의 얼굴에 미소가 환히 번졌습니다. 그는 천천히 얼굴을 그녀의 품에 묻고, 두 팔로 그녀의 목을 더 가까이 끌어안고는, 한 번 작별의 흐느낌과 함께 세상을 시작했습니다. 이 세상이 아니라, 오, 이 세상이 아니라! 이 세상을 바로잡는 그 세상을.\n\n깊은 밤, 모든 것이 고요해졌을 때, 가엾이 정신이 나간 플라이트 양이 울며 나에게 다가와, 자기 새들을 모두 풀어주었다고 일러주었습니다.\n\n링컨셔의 시골에는 이 달라진 나날에 정적이 감돈다. 가문의 내력 한 자락에 정적이 감돌듯이. 떠도는 이야기로는 레스터 경이 입을 열 만한 자들에게 돈을 쥐여 침묵을 사들였다고 하지만, 이건 어딘가 절뚝거리는 이야기로, 힘없이 속닥거리며 기어 다닐 뿐, 그나마 좀 더 또렷이 빛나는 생명의 불씨를 보일라치면 이내 사그라든다. 아름다운 데드록 마님이 공원의 영묘에 잠들어 있다는 것만은 분명하다. 그곳은 나무들이 머리 위로 어둑하게 가지를 드리우고, 밤이면 올빼미 소리가 숲을 울리는 곳이다. 그러나 그녀가 어디서 옮겨져 이 외딴 자리의 메아리 사이에 눕게 되었는지, 또 어떻게 죽었는지는 온통 수수께끼다. 그녀의 옛 벗 가운데 몇몇은—주로 해골 같은 목에 복숭앗빛 뺨을 한 요염한 부인들 가운데서 찾아볼 만한 이들로, 다른 사내들을 죄다 잃은 뒤 음산한 죽음을 상대로 추파를 던지는 요부 같은 꼴로 커다란 부채를 으스스하게 만지작거리며—세상 사람들이 한데 모일 때면 이따금 이런 말을 흘리곤 했다. 영묘에 묻힌 데드록 가문의 유골이 그녀가 끼어든 그 모독에 들고일어나지 않는 게 이상하다고. 그러나 죽어 사라진 데드록 가문 사람들은 그것을 아주 담담히 받아들이고, 항의했다는 소문은 한 번도 들린 적이 없다.\n\n움푹 팬 땅의 고사리 덤불 사이에서 올라와 나무들 사이로 난 승마길을 굽이쳐, 이따금 이 적막한 곳으로 말발굽 소리가 다가온다. 그러면 레스터 경의 모습이 보인다—병들어 등이 굽고 거의 앞을 못 보지만 여전히 위엄을 갖춘 자태로, 한결같이 고삐 곁을 지키는 건장한 사내를 옆에 거느린 채 말을 몬다. 영묘 문 앞 어느 자리에 이르면 레스터 경의 길든 말이 스스로 걸음을 멈추고, 레스터 경은 모자를 벗어 든 채 잠시 가만히 서 있다가 다시 말을 몰아 떠난다.\n\n대담한 보이손과의 전쟁은 아직도 한창인데, 다만 들쭉날쭉한 간격을 두고 때로는 뜨겁게, 때로는 차갑게, 흔들리는 불꽃처럼 깜박인다. 진상은 이렇다고 한다. 레스터 경이 아주 링컨셔로 내려오자, 보이손 씨가 통행권을 포기하고 레스터 경이 바라는 대로 무엇이든 하려는 기색을 뚜렷이 내비쳤다는 것이다. 그런데 레스터 경은 이것을 자신의 병이나 불운에 대한 동정쯤으로 여겨 어찌나 발끈하고 어찌나 거창하게 모욕감을 느꼈던지, 보이손 씨는 이웃을 본래 모습으로 되돌리려면 노골적인 침입 행위를 저지를 수밖에 없는 처지에 놓이고 말았다. 그리하여 보이손 씨는 여전히 분쟁의 통행로에 어마어마한 경고문을 내붙이고, (머리 위에 새를 올린 채) 제 집 안 성역에서 레스터 경을 격렬하게 성토한다. 또한 여전히 작은 교회에서는 옛날처럼 그의 존재를 천연덕스럽게 모르는 척하며 그에게 맞선다. 그러나 수군대는 말로는, 그가 옛 원수에게 가장 사납게 굴 때가 사실은 가장 깊이 배려하는 때이며, 레스터 경은 끝까지 화해하지 않는 위엄에 젖어 자신이 얼마나 너그러운 대접을 받고 있는지 거의 짐작도 못 한다고 한다. 그가 자신의 적수와 두 자매의 운명 속에서 얼마나 가까이 함께 아파했는지도 거의 모른다. 이제 그 사실을 아는 적수는 그에게 그것을 일러줄 위인이 못 된다. 그렇게 다툼은 양쪽 모두 만족스럽게 이어진다.\n\n공원의 문지기 오두막 가운데 하나—바로 그 옛날 링컨셔에 강물이 넘쳤을 때 마님이 문지기의 아이를 바라보곤 하던, 저택이 보이는 그 오두막에—건장한 사내, 옛날의 기병이 살고 있다. 그의 지난 직업의 유물 몇 가지가 벽에 걸려 있는데, 마구간 마당을 돌아다니는 절름발이 작은 사내가 그것들을 번쩍번쩍 닦아 두는 일을 더없는 낙으로 삼는다. 그는 늘 부지런한 작은 사내로, 마구간 문간에서 등자쇠며 재갈이며 굴레사슬이며 마구 장식이며, 마구간 마당에서 닦이는 것이라면 무엇이든 윤을 내며 문지르는 삶을 산다. 게다가 텁수룩하고 어딘가 망가진 작은 사내로, 어지간히 두들겨 맞은 잡종 늙은 개와 영락없이 닮았다. 그는 필이라는 이름에 대답한다.\n\n위풍당당한 늙은 가정부가(이제는 귀가 더 어두워졌다) 아들의 팔에 기대 교회에 가는 모습을 보는 것은 흐뭇한 광경이며, 또—요즘은 저택에 찾는 손님이 드물어 이를 지켜보는 이가 별로 없지만—두 사람이 레스터 경을 대하는 태도와 레스터 경이 그들을 대하는 태도를 살피는 것도 그러하다. 한여름이면 손님들이 찾아오는데, 다른 철에는 체스니 월드에 낯선 잿빛 외투와 우산이 나뭇잎 사이로 보이고, 두 젊은 아가씨가 외딴 톱질 구덩이며 공원의 그런 구석진 곳에서 뛰노는 모습이 이따금 눈에 띄며, 기병의 문간에서 파이프 두 대의 연기가 향긋한 저녁 공기 속으로 굽이굽이 피어오른다. 그럴 때면 오두막 안에서 \"영국 척탄병\"이라는 신명 나는 가락을 노래하는 피리 소리가 들리고, 저녁이 짙어지면 두 사내가 함께 이리저리 거니는 동안 무뚝뚝하고 완고한 목소리로 이렇게 말하는 게 들린다. \"하지만 난 절대 할멈 앞에선 인정 안 해. 규율은 지켜야 하는 법이니까.\"\n\n저택의 대부분은 닫혀 있고, 이제는 더 이상 구경거리 집이 아니다. 그래도 레스터 경은 긴 응접실에서 쪼그라든 위세나마 지키며, 마님의 초상화 앞 옛 자리에서 쉰다. 밤이면 넓은 가리개로 둘러막고 오직 그 한 부분만 밝혀 두니, 응접실의 불빛은 점점 오그라들고 줄어들다가 마침내 사라져 버릴 듯 보인다. 사실 조금만 더 지나면 레스터 경에게도 그 불빛은 모두 꺼질 것이고, 그토록 빈틈없이 닫혀 그토록 완고해 보이는 영묘의 축축한 문이 열려 그를 맞아들일 것이다.\n\n세월이 흐를수록 얼굴의 붉은 자리는 더 분홍빛으로, 흰 자리는 더 누렇게 물들어 가는 볼럼니아는 긴긴 저녁이면 레스터 경에게 책을 읽어 주며, 하품을 감추려 갖은 잔꾀를 부리는데, 그 가운데 으뜸가는 데다 가장 잘 듣는 수법이 진주 목걸이를 발그레한 입술 사이에 물어 두는 것이다. 버피 대 부들 문제를 다룬 장황한 논설들—버피가 얼마나 흠 하나 없이 깨끗하고 부들이 얼마나 악당인지, 나라가 죄다 부들이고 버피가 없어 어떻게 망하는지, 아니면 죄다 버피고 부들이 없어 어떻게 구원받는지(둘 중 하나여야 하며 그 밖에 다른 길은 있을 수 없다)를 늘어놓는 글들—이 그녀가 읽는 글의 주된 거리다. 레스터 경은 그것이 무슨 내용인지 따지지 않고, 그다지 새겨듣는 기색도 없다. 다만 볼럼니아가 슬쩍 읽기를 멈추려 들면 그 순간 번쩍 정신을 차려, 그녀가 마지막으로 읽은 말을 우렁우렁 되뇌고는 다소 못마땅한 투로 혹시 피곤하냐고 묻는다는 점만은 예외다. 그래도 볼럼니아는 새처럼 폴짝폴짝 뛰어다니며 서류들을 쪼아 대던 끝에, 친척에게 \"무슨 일이 생길\" 경우 자신에 관해 적어 둔 비망록 한 장에 내려앉았으니, 이것이야말로 방대한 독서에 대한 후한 보상이요, 권태라는 용마저 가까이 못 오게 막아 준다.\n\n친척들은 대체로 따분한 체스니 월드를 꺼리지만, 사냥철에는 조금 발길을 들인다. 그때면 농장에 총소리가 들리고, 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 몰이꾼들과 사냥터지기들이 옛 약속 장소에서 풀 죽은 친척들이 두셋씩 오기를 기다린다. 그 쇠약한 친척은 이곳의 음울함에 더욱 쇠약해져, 총을 들지 못하는 시간이면 참회하듯 소파 쿠션에 묻혀 끙끙 앓으며, 이런 빌어먹을 늙은 감옥은—사람을—꿰매—버려야—영영—그래야 한다고 하소연한다.\n\n링컨셔의 이렇게 달라진 풍경 속에서 볼럼니아에게 유일하게 큰 행사라면, 드물고 멀찍이 떨어진 채 가끔 찾아오는 그 순간들, 곧 공개 무도회를 빛내는 일로 주(州)나 나라를 위해 무언가 해야 할 때다. 그럴 때면 정말이지 주름 장식을 두른 요정 같은 그녀가 선녀 같은 자태로 나타나, 친척의 호위를 받으며 기쁘게 열네 마일이나 떨어진 낡고 지친 무도회장으로 향한다. 그곳은 평범한 한 해의 삼백예순나흘 밤낮 동안은 거꾸로 뒤집힌 낡은 의자와 탁자로 가득한, 지구 반대편의 헛간 같은 곳이다. 그럴 때면 정말이지 그녀는 겸손한 몸가짐과 소녀 같은 활기로, 그리고 이빨이 너무 빽빽이 들어찬 흉측한 늙은 장군이 한 개에 이 기니씩 받고 그 이빨들을 아직 한 개도 뽑지 않았던 그 시절처럼 폴짝폴짝 뛰노는 모습으로 뭇사람의 마음을 사로잡는다. 그럴 때면 그녀는 좋은 가문 출신의 목가적 요정처럼 춤의 미궁 속을 빙글빙글 돌고 휘감는다. 그럴 때면 시골 총각들이 차를, 레모네이드를, 샌드위치를, 경의를 바치며 나타난다. 그럴 때면 그녀는 다정하다가도 매정하고, 도도하다가도 수수하며, 변화무쌍하고, 곱게 제멋대로다. 그럴 때면 그녀와, 그 무도회장을 장식하는 옛 시대의 작은 유리 샹들리에들 사이에는 묘한 닮음이 생긴다. 그것들은 빈약한 줄기와, 듬성듬성한 작은 유리 방울들과, 방울이 매달려야 할 자리에 방울 없이 실망스럽게 박힌 마디들과, 마디도 방울도 죄다 떨어져 나간 앙상한 작은 가지들과, 가냘프게 프리즘처럼 반짝이는 작은 빛들로 하여, 하나같이 볼럼니아를 닮은 듯하다.\n\n그 밖에 볼럼니아에게 링컨셔의 삶이란, 나무들을 내다보는 우거진 저택의 광막한 공백이다. 그 나무들은 한숨짓고, 두 손을 비틀고, 고개를 떨구며, 단조로운 우울 속에 창유리에 눈물을 흩뿌린다. 웅장함의 미궁이지만, 이는 인간으로 이루어진 어느 옛 가문과 그 유령 같은 닮은꼴들의 것이라기보다, 메아리와 우렛소리로 이루어진 어느 옛 가문의 것이니, 그것들은 어떤 소리든 들릴 때마다 백 개의 무덤에서 뛰쳐나와 온 건물을 울리며 메아리쳐 간다. 쓰이지 않는 복도와 계단의 황량한 벌판, 그곳에서 밤중에 침실 바닥에 빗 하나만 떨어뜨려도 은밀한 발소리가 저택을 가로질러 심부름을 떠나는 셈이 된다. 사람들이 혼자 돌아다니기를 꺼리는 곳, 하녀가 불에서 재 한 점만 떨어져도 비명을 지르고, 때와 철을 가리지 않고 울음을 터뜨리며, 천한 신경 쇠약의 희생양이 되어 일을 그만두겠다 알리고 떠나 버리는 곳.\n\n이것이 체스니 월드다. 제 몸의 그토록 많은 부분이 어둠과 공허에 내맡겨진 채, 여름 햇살 아래서나 겨울 잿빛 하늘 아래서나 거의 달라지는 것 없이, 늘 그토록 음침하고 미동조차 없다—이제는 낮에 펄럭이는 깃발도 없고, 밤에 줄지어 반짝이는 불빛도 없으며, 오가는 가족도 없고, 차고 창백한 방의 형상에 혼을 불어넣을 손님도 없으며, 둘레에 생명의 기척이라곤 없다. 격정도 자긍심도, 낯선 이의 눈에까지도, 이 링컨셔의 시골에서는 사그라지고, 그곳을 흐릿한 안식에 내어 주고 말았다.\n\n꼬박 칠 년이라는 행복한 세월 동안 나는 황폐한 집의 안주인으로 지냈습니다. 지금까지 적은 것에 덧붙일 몇 마디는 금세 끝납니다. 그러고 나면 나와, 내가 이 글을 띄우는 알지 못하는 벗은 영영 헤어지겠지요. 내 쪽에서는 정겨운 추억을 잔뜩 품은 채로 말입니다. 그리고 그분 쪽에서도 얼마간은, 부디 그러하기를 바랍니다.\n\n사람들이 내 사랑하는 친구를 내 품에 안겨주었고, 나는 여러 주 동안 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않았습니다. 그토록 많은 일을 해내야 했던 그 어린아이는 제 아버지 무덤에 잔디가 깔리기도 전에 태어났습니다. 사내아이였습니다. 나와 내 남편과 후견인은 그 아이에게 제 아버지의 이름을 붙여주었습니다.\n\n내 사랑하는 친구가 믿고 기대했던 그 도움은 정말로 그녀에게 찾아왔습니다. 다만 그것은 영원하신 섭리 안에서 다른 목적을 위해 온 것이었습니다. 이 아기에게 맡겨진 일은 제 아버지가 아니라 제 어머니를 축복하고 다시 일으키는 것이었으나, 그 일을 해내는 힘만큼은 대단했습니다. 그 가냘프고 작은 손의 힘을, 그리고 그 손길이 어떻게 내 사랑하는 친구의 마음을 어루만져 낫게 하고 그 안에 희망을 일으키는지를 보았을 때, 나는 하느님의 선하심과 자애로우심을 새삼 새롭게 느꼈습니다.\n\n두 사람은 무럭무럭 자랐고, 나는 이윽고 사랑하는 그 아이가 내 시골 정원으로 들어와 아기를 품에 안고 그곳을 거니는 모습을 보게 되었습니다. 그 무렵 나는 이미 결혼한 몸이었습니다. 행복한 사람들 가운데서도 가장 행복했습니다.\n\n바로 이 무렵 후견인이 우리에게 와서 에이다에게 언제 집으로 돌아올 거냐고 물었습니다.\n\n\"두 집 다 네 집이란다, 얘야.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"그래도 더 오래된 황폐한 집이 우선이지. 너와 내 아이가 옮길 만큼 충분히 튼튼해지거든 와서 네 집을 차지하려무나.\"\n\n에이다는 그를 \"더없이 사랑하는 사촌 오라버니 존\"이라고 불렀습니다. 하지만 그는 안 된다고, 이제는 후견인이어야 한다고 했습니다. 이제부터 그는 에이다의 후견인이요 그 아이의 후견인이며, 그 이름과는 오래 묵은 인연이 있다는 것이었습니다. 그래서 에이다는 그를 후견인이라고 불렀고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 후견인이라고 부릅니다. 아이들은 그를 다른 이름으로는 알지 못합니다. 아이들이라고 했지요. 나에게는 어린 딸이 둘 있습니다.\n\n찰리가(여전히 눈이 동그랗고 문법은 도무지 맞지 않는 그 찰리가) 우리 이웃의 방앗간 주인과 결혼했다니 도무지 믿기지 않습니다. 그래도 사실이 그렇습니다. 지금도 이렇게 이른 아침 여름 창가의 책상에서 글을 쓰다가 고개를 들면, 바로 그 방앗간이 돌기 시작하는 게 보입니다. 그 방앗간 주인이 찰리의 버릇을 버려놓지나 않으면 좋으련만, 그는 찰리를 무척 아낍니다. 찰리는 이런 혼처에 좀 우쭐대기도 하는데, 그가 형편이 넉넉하고 청혼이 줄을 섰던 사람이기 때문입니다. 내 어린 하녀로 말하자면, 마치 시간이 칠 년 동안 반 시간 전 멈춰 있던 방앗간처럼 가만히 멈춰 있었던 게 아닌가 싶을 정도입니다. 찰리의 동생 어린 에마가 예전의 찰리와 어쩜 그리도 똑같은지요. 찰리의 남동생 톰으로 말하자면, 그 애가 학교에서 셈을 어떻게 했는지는 차마 입에 올리기 두렵지만, 아마 소수였던 것 같습니다. 어쨌든 그 애는 그 방앗간 주인에게 도제로 들어갔는데, 착하고 수줍음 많은 녀석이라 늘 누군가에게 반하고는 그걸 부끄러워합니다.\n\n캐디 젤리비는 가장 마지막 휴가를 우리와 함께 보냈는데, 어느 때보다 더 사랑스러운 사람이 되어 있었습니다. 마치 평생 무용 강습 한 번 해본 적 없다는 듯, 아이들과 어울려 끊임없이 집 안팎을 춤추며 드나들었습니다. 캐디는 이제 마차를 빌리는 대신 자기 작은 마차를 두고 살며, 뉴먼 거리보다 서쪽으로 꼬박 이 마일이나 더 떨어진 곳에 삽니다. 캐디는 정말 열심히 일합니다. 남편이(더없이 훌륭한 사람이지요) 다리를 절어 거의 아무것도 하지 못하기 때문입니다. 그래도 캐디는 더없이 만족스러워하며 자기가 해야 할 일을 온 마음을 다해 합니다. 젤리비 씨는 예전에 딸의 옛집에서 그랬던 것처럼, 새집에서도 벽에 머리를 기댄 채 저녁을 보냅니다. 듣자 하니 젤리비 부인은 딸의 변변찮은 결혼과 그 일거리 때문에 크게 자존심이 상한 모양이지만, 시간이 지나면 그 일을 잘 넘겼기를 바랍니다. 부인은 보리오불라가 일에 실망했는데, 그곳은 보리오불라 왕이(그 풍토에서 살아남은) 사람들을 모조리 럼주에 팔아넘기려 한 탓에 결국 실패로 돌아갔습니다. 그래서 부인은 여성의 의회 진출권 문제를 새로 붙잡았는데, 캐디 말로는 그게 예전 일보다 더 많은 서신 왕래가 필요한 사명이라고 합니다. 캐디의 가엾은 어린 딸을 하마터면 잊을 뻔했습니다. 이제 그 아이는 그렇게 조그맣지는 않지만, 듣지도 말하지도 못합니다. 나는 캐디만 한 어머니가 또 없으리라 믿습니다. 캐디는 얼마 안 되는 짬짬이 시간에 제 아이의 고통을 덜어주려고 농아를 위한 온갖 기술을 헤아릴 수 없이 익히고 있습니다.\n\n마치 캐디 이야기는 영영 끝나지 않을 것처럼, 여기서 나는 피피와 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 떠오릅니다. 피피는 세관에서 일하며 아주 잘해내고 있습니다. 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 뇌졸중 기운이 심해졌으면서도 여전히 거리에서 우아한 몸가짐을 뽐내고, 여전히 옛날 방식대로 즐기며, 여전히 옛날 방식대로 떠받들어집니다. 그는 한결같이 피피를 후원하는데, 자기 화장방에 있는 아끼는 프랑스 시계를 피피에게 물려주기로 했다고들 합니다. 그런데 그건 그의 것이 아닙니다.\n\n집에서 처음 모은 돈으로 우리는 오로지 후견인을 위해 작은 투덜방을 덧대어 우리의 아담한 집을 늘렸고, 다음번 그가 우리를 보러 내려왔을 때 성대하게 그곳을 처음으로 열어 보였습니다. 끝맺음에 다가가니 가슴이 벅차올라 나는 이 모든 것을 가볍게 쓰려고 애씁니다만, 그분에 대해 쓰자니 눈물이 제멋대로 흘러내립니다.\n\n나는 그를 바라볼 때마다 우리의 가엾고 사랑스러운 리처드가 그를 두고 좋은 사람이라 부르던 소리를 듣습니다. 에이다와 그 예쁜 사내아이에게 그는 더없이 다정한 아버지입니다. 나에게 그는 언제나 그랬던 그 모습 그대로인데, 그것에 무슨 이름을 붙일 수 있을까요? 그는 내 남편의 가장 좋고 가장 소중한 벗이며, 우리 아이들이 사랑하는 사람이며, 우리가 가장 깊이 사랑하고 우러르는 분입니다. 그러면서도 나는 그를 마치 더 높은 존재처럼 느끼면서도 그와 너무나 허물없고 편해서, 나 자신이 다 신기할 정도입니다. 나는 옛 이름들을 한 번도 잃은 적이 없고, 그도 제 이름을 잃은 적이 없습니다. 그가 우리와 함께 있을 때면 나는 늘 그의 곁 내 낡은 의자 말고 다른 자리에 앉는 법이 없습니다. 종종걸음 아주머니, 더든 아주머니, 꼬마 아낙—모두 예전 그대로입니다. 그리고 나는 \"네, 후견인!\" 하고 예전과 똑같이 대답합니다.\n\n그가 나를 현관으로 데려가 그 이름을 읽게 한 그날 이후로, 나는 단 한순간도 바람이 동쪽에서 부는 것을 알아챈 적이 없습니다. 언젠가 나는 그에게 요즘은 바람이 도무지 동쪽에서 부는 것 같지 않다고 말했고, 그는 그렇다고, 정말 그렇다고, 바람은 바로 그날 그 방향에서 영영 떠나버렸다고 했습니다.\n\n내 사랑하는 그 아이는 어느 때보다 더 아름다워진 것 같습니다. 그녀의 얼굴에 깃들어 있던 슬픔은—이제는 거기에 없습니다만—그 순결한 표정마저 정화하여 더욱 거룩한 기품을 부여한 듯합니다. 이따금 눈을 들어, 그녀가 아직도 입고 있는 검은 옷차림으로 내 리처드를 가르치는 모습을 보면, 나는 느낍니다—말로 표현하기 어렵지만—그녀가 기도 속에서 사랑하는 에스더를 기억해준다는 걸 안다는 게 얼마나 좋은 일인지를요.\n\n나는 그 아이를 내 리처드라고 부릅니다! 하지만 그 애는 자기에게 엄마가 둘 있다고, 그중 하나가 나라고 합니다.\n\n우리는 은행에 돈이 넉넉하지는 않지만 늘 형편이 나아져 왔고, 부족함 없이 살 만큼은 충분히 가졌습니다. 나는 남편과 함께 바깥을 거닐 때면 언제나 사람들이 그를 축복하는 소리를 듣습니다. 어느 신분의 집에 들어가든 그를 칭송하는 말을 듣거나 고마워하는 눈빛 속에서 그것을 봅니다. 밤에 잠자리에 누울 때면 언제나, 그날 하루 동안 그가 고통을 덜어주고 절박한 처지에 놓인 어느 동료 인간을 다독여주었음을 압니다. 회복할 가망이 없던 이들의 병상에서, 그 마지막 순간에 그의 끈기 있는 보살핌에 감사하는 마음이 자주, 또 자주 하늘로 올라갔음을 압니다. 이것이야말로 부유한 게 아니겠습니까?\n\n사람들은 의사의 아내라며 나마저 칭송합니다. 사람들은 내가 돌아다니면 나마저 좋아하고, 나를 어찌나 떠받드는지 도리어 무안할 지경입니다. 이 모든 것이 다 그 사람 덕입니다, 내 사랑, 내 자랑인 그 사람 말입니다! 사람들이 그 사람 때문에 나를 좋아하듯, 나도 내 삶에서 하는 모든 일을 그 사람을 위해 합니다.\n\n하루 이틀 전, 내일 내려오기로 한 내 사랑하는 친구와 후견인과 어린 리처드를 맞을 채비를 부산스레 마치고 나서, 나는 하필이면 그 현관에, 더없이 잊지 못할 그 현관에 나와 앉아 있었습니다. 그때 앨런이 집에 돌아왔습니다. 그래서 그가 말했습니다. \"내 소중한 꼬마 아낙, 여기서 뭘 하고 있소?\" 그래서 나는 말했습니다. \"앨런, 달빛이 어찌나 환하게 비치는지, 밤이 어찌나 감미로운지, 여기 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있었어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 생각을 하고 있었소, 여보?\" 그러자 앨런이 물었습니다.\n\n\"당신도 참 궁금한 것도 많으셔라!\" 내가 말했습니다. \"말씀드리기 좀 부끄럽지만, 그래도 할게요. 내 옛날 얼굴을—그게 어떠했든 간에 말이에요—생각하고 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그 얼굴에 대해 무슨 생각을 했소, 내 부지런한 꿀벌 아씨?\" 앨런이 물었습니다.\n\n\"이런 생각을 했어요. 설령 내가 그 얼굴을 그대로 간직했다 한들, 당신이 지금보다 나를 더 사랑할 수는 도저히 없었으리라고요.\"\n\n\"'그게 어떠했든 간에'라고?\" 앨런이 웃으며 말했습니다.\n\n\"물론, 어떠했든 간에 말이에요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 더든 아주머니.\" 앨런이 내 팔을 제 팔에 끼우며 말했습니다. \"당신은 거울을 들여다보기는 하오?\"\n\n\"당신도 알잖아요. 내가 그러는 걸 보면서요.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신이 그 어느 때보다 더 예뻐졌다는 걸 모른단 말이오?\"\n\n나는 그걸 알지 못했습니다. 지금도 그걸 알고 있는지 잘 모르겠습니다. 하지만 나는 압니다. 더없이 사랑스러운 내 아이들이 무척 예쁘고, 내 사랑하는 친구가 무척 아름답고, 내 남편이 무척 잘생겼고, 내 후견인이 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 환하고 자애로운 얼굴을 지녔다는 것을요. 그러니 그들은 내가 그리 아름답지 않아도 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있다는 것을—설령 정말 그렇다손 치더라도—.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-1023"]=[[["Preface I. In Chancery II. In Fashion III. A Progress IV. Telescopic Philanthropy V. A Morning Adventure VI. Quite at Home VII. The Ghost’s Walk VIII. Covering a Multitude of Sins IX. Signs and Tokens X. The Law-Writer XI. Our Dear Brother XII. On the Watch XIII. Esther’s Narrative XIV. Deportment XV. Bell Yard XVI. Tom-all-Alone’s XVII. Esther’s Narrative XVIII. Lady Dedlock XIX. Moving On XX. A New Lodger XXI. The Smallweed Family XXII. Mr. Bucket XXIII. Esther’s Narrative XXIV. An Appeal Case XXV. Mrs. Snagsby Sees It All XXVI. Sharpshooters XXVII. More Old Soldiers Than One XXVIII. The Ironmaster XXIX. The Young Man XXX. Esther’s Narrative XXXI. Nurse and Patient XXXII. The Appointed Time XXXIII. Interlopers XXXIV. A Turn of the Screw XXXV. Esther’s Narrative XXXVI. Chesney Wold XXXVII. Jarndyce and Jarndyce XXXVIII. A Struggle XXXIX. Attorney and Client XL. National and Domestic XLI. In Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Room XLII. In Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Chambers XLIII. Esther’s Narrative XLIV. The Letter and the Answer XLV. In Trust XLVI. Stop Him! XLVII. Jo’s Will XLVIII. Closing In XLIX. Dutiful Friendship L. Esther’s Narrative LI. Enlightened LII. Obstinacy LIII. The Track LIV. Springing a Mine LV. Flight LVI. Pursuit LVII. Esther’s Narrative LVIII. A Wintry Day and Night LIX. Esther’s Narrative LX. Perspective LXI. A Discovery LXII. Another Discovery LXIII. Steel and Iron LXIV. Esther’s Narrative LXV. Beginning the World LXVI. Down in Lincolnshire LXVII. The Close of Esther’s Narrative\nA Chancery judge once had the kindness to inform me, as one of a company of some hundred and fifty men and women not labouring under any suspicions of lunacy, that the Court of Chancery, though the shining subject of much popular prejudice (at which point I thought the judge’s eye had a cast in my direction), was almost immaculate. There had been, he admitted, a trivial blemish or so in its rate of progress, but this was exaggerated and had been entirely owing to the “parsimony of the public,” which guilty public, it appeared, had been until lately bent in the most determined manner on by no means enlarging the number of Chancery judges appointed—I believe by Richard the Second, but any other king will do as well.", "7. 유령의 길"]], [["This seemed to me too profound a joke to be inserted in the body of this book or I should have restored it to Conversation Kenge or to Mr.", "8. 숱한 죄악을 덮으며\n9."], ["Vholes, with one or other of whom I think it must have originated. In such mouths I might have coupled it with an apt quotation from one of Shakespeare’s sonnets:", "징조와 조짐"]], [["“My nature is subdued To what it works in, like the dyer’s hand: Pity me, then, and wish I were renewed!”", "10. 법률 필경사\n11. 우리 형제"]], [["But as it is wholesome that the parsimonious public should know what has been doing, and still is doing, in this connexion, I mention here that everything set forth in these pages concerning the Court of Chancery is substantially true, and within the truth.", "12."], ["The case of Gridley is in no essential altered from one of actual occurrence, made public by a disinterested person who was professionally acquainted with the whole of the monstrous wrong from beginning to end. At the present moment (August, 1853) there is a suit before the court which was commenced nearly twenty years ago, in which from thirty to forty counsel have been known to appear at one time, in which costs have been incurred to the amount of seventy thousand pounds, which is A FRIENDLY SUIT, and which is (I am assured) no nearer to its termination now than when it was begun.", "망보며\n13."], ["There is another well-known suit in Chancery, not yet decided, which was commenced before the close of the last century and in which more than double the amount of seventy thousand pounds has been swallowed up in costs. If I wanted other authorities for Jarndyce and Jarndyce, I could rain them on these pages, to the shame of—a parsimonious public.", "에스더의 이야기"]], [["There is only one other point on which I offer a word of remark. The possibility of what is called spontaneous combustion has been denied since the death of Mr. Krook; and my good friend Mr. Lewes (quite mistaken, as he soon found, in supposing the thing to have been abandoned by all authorities) published some ingenious letters to me at the time when that event was chronicled, arguing that spontaneous combustion could not possibly be. I have no need to observe that I do not wilfully or negligently mislead my readers and that before I wrote that description I took pains to investigate the subject. There are about thirty cases on record, of which the most famous, that of the Countess Cornelia de Baudi Cesenate, was minutely investigated and described by Giuseppe Bianchini, a prebendary of Verona, otherwise distinguished in letters, who published an account of it at Verona in 1731, which he afterwards republished at Rome. The appearances, beyond all rational doubt, observed in that case are the appearances observed in Mr. Krook’s case. The next most famous instance happened at Rheims six years earlier, and the historian in that case is Le Cat, one of the most renowned surgeons produced by France. The subject was a woman, whose husband was ignorantly convicted of having murdered her; but on solemn appeal to a higher court, he was acquitted because it was shown upon the evidence that she had died the death of which this name of spontaneous combustion is given. I do not think it necessary to add to these notable facts, and that general reference to the authorities which will be found at page 30, vol. ii.,* the recorded opinions and experiences of distinguished medical professors, French, English, and Scotch, in more modern days, contenting myself with observing that I shall not abandon the facts until there shall have been a considerable spontaneous combustion of the testimony on which human occurrences are usually received.**", "14. 몸가짐\n15. 벨 야드"]], [["In Bleak House I have purposely dwelt upon the romantic side of familiar things.", "16. 톰 혼자 사는 곳\n17. 에스더의 이야기"]], [["*Transcriber’s note. This referred to a specific page in the printed book.", "18."], ["In this Project Gutenberg edition the pertinent information is in Chapter XXX, paragraph 90.", "데드록 마님\n19. 비키시오"]], [["** Another case, very clearly described by a dentist, occurred at the town of Columbus, in the United States of America, quite recently.", "20. 새 하숙인\n21."], ["The subject was a German who kept a liquor-shop and was an inveterate drunkard.", "스몰위드 일가"]], [["In Chancery", "22. 버킷 씨"]], [["London. Michaelmas term lately over, and the Lord Chancellor sitting in Lincoln’s Inn Hall. Implacable November weather. As much mud in the streets as if the waters had but newly retired from the face of the earth, and it would not be wonderful to meet a Megalosaurus, forty feet long or so, waddling like an elephantine lizard up Holborn Hill. Smoke lowering down from chimney-pots, making a soft black drizzle, with flakes of soot in it as big as full-grown snowflakes—gone into mourning, one might imagine, for the death of the sun. Dogs, undistinguishable in mire. Horses, scarcely better; splashed to their very blinkers. Foot passengers, jostling one another’s umbrellas in a general infection of ill temper, and losing their foot-hold at street-corners, where tens of thousands of other foot passengers have been slipping and sliding since the day broke (if this day ever broke), adding new deposits to the crust upon crust of mud, sticking at those points tenaciously to the pavement, and accumulating at compound interest.", "23. 에스더의 이야기\n24. 항소 사건"]], [["Fog everywhere. Fog up the river, where it flows among green aits and meadows; fog down the river, where it rolls defiled among the tiers of shipping and the waterside pollutions of a great (and dirty) city.", "25."], ["Fog on the Essex marshes, fog on the Kentish heights. Fog creeping into the cabooses of collier-brigs; fog lying out on the yards and hovering in the rigging of great ships; fog drooping on the gunwales of barges and small boats.", "스내그스비 부인은 다 알아챈다\n26."], ["Fog in the eyes and throats of ancient Greenwich pensioners, wheezing by the firesides of their wards; fog in the stem and bowl of the afternoon pipe of the wrathful skipper, down in his close cabin; fog cruelly pinching the toes and fingers of his shivering little ’prentice boy on deck. Chance people on the bridges peeping over the parapets into a nether sky of fog, with fog all round them, as if they were up in a balloon and hanging in the misty clouds.", "명사수들"]], [["Gas looming through the fog in divers places in the streets, much as the sun may, from the spongey fields, be seen to loom by husbandman and ploughboy.", "27. 늙은 군인이 하나만은 아니다\n28."], ["Most of the shops lighted two hours before their time—as the gas seems to know, for it has a haggard and unwilling look.", "철물 제조업자"]], [["The raw afternoon is rawest, and the dense fog is densest, and the muddy streets are muddiest near that leaden-headed old obstruction, appropriate ornament for the threshold of a leaden-headed old corporation, Temple Bar.", "29. 그 젊은이\n30."], ["And hard by Temple Bar, in Lincoln’s Inn Hall, at the very heart of the fog, sits the Lord High Chancellor in his High Court of Chancery.", "에스더의 이야기"]], [["Never can there come fog too thick, never can there come mud and mire too deep, to assort with the groping and floundering condition which this High Court of Chancery, most pestilent of hoary sinners, holds this day in the sight of heaven and earth.", "31. 간병인과 환자\n32. 약속한 시각"]], [["On such an afternoon, if ever, the Lord High Chancellor ought to be sitting here—as here he is—with a foggy glory round his head, softly fenced in with crimson cloth and curtains, addressed by a large advocate with great whiskers, a little voice, and an interminable brief, and outwardly directing his contemplation to the lantern in the roof, where he can see nothing but fog. On such an afternoon some score of members of the High Court of Chancery bar ought to be—as here they are—mistily engaged in one of the ten thousand stages of an endless cause, tripping one another up on slippery precedents, groping knee-deep in technicalities, running their goat-hair and horsehair warded heads against walls of words and making a pretence of equity with serious faces, as players might. On such an afternoon the various solicitors in the cause, some two or three of whom have inherited it from their fathers, who made a fortune by it, ought to be—as are they not?—ranged in a line, in a long matted well (but you might look in vain for truth at the bottom of it) between the registrar’s red table and the silk gowns, with bills, cross-bills, answers, rejoinders, injunctions, affidavits, issues, references to masters, masters’ reports, mountains of costly nonsense, piled before them. Well may the court be dim, with wasting candles here and there; well may the fog hang heavy in it, as if it would never get out; well may the stained-glass windows lose their colour and admit no light of day into the place; well may the uninitiated from the streets, who peep in through the glass panes in the door, be deterred from entrance by its owlish aspect and by the drawl, languidly echoing to the roof from the padded dais where the Lord High Chancellor looks into the lantern that has no light in it and where the attendant wigs are all stuck in a fog-bank! This is the Court of Chancery, which has its decaying houses and its blighted lands in every shire, which has its worn-out lunatic in every madhouse and its dead in every churchyard, which has its ruined suitor with his slipshod heels and threadbare dress borrowing and begging through the round of every man’s acquaintance, which gives to monied might the means abundantly of wearying out the right, which so exhausts finances, patience, courage, hope, so overthrows the brain and breaks the heart, that there is not an honourable man among its practitioners who would not give—who does not often give—the warning, “Suffer any wrong that can be done you rather than come here!” Who happen to be in the Lord Chancellor’s court this murky afternoon besides the Lord Chancellor, the counsel in the cause, two or three counsel who are never in any cause, and the well of solicitors before mentioned? There is the registrar below the judge, in wig and gown; and there are two or three maces, or petty-bags, or privy purses, or whatever they may be, in legal court suits. These are all yawning, for no crumb of amusement ever falls from Jarndyce and Jarndyce (the cause in hand), which was squeezed dry years upon years ago. The short-hand writers, the reporters of the court, and the reporters of the newspapers invariably decamp with the rest of the regulars when Jarndyce and Jarndyce comes on. Their places are a blank. Standing on a seat at the side of the hall, the better to peer into the curtained sanctuary, is a little mad old woman in a squeezed bonnet who is always in court, from its sitting to its rising, and always expecting some incomprehensible judgment to be given in her favour. Some say she really is, or was, a party to a suit, but no one knows for certain because no one cares. She carries some small litter in a reticule which she calls her documents, principally consisting of paper matches and dry lavender. A sallow prisoner has come up, in custody, for the half-dozenth time to make a personal application “to purge himself of his contempt,” which, being a solitary surviving executor who has fallen into a state of conglomeration about accounts of which it is not pretended that he had ever any knowledge, he is not at all likely ever to do. In the meantime his prospects in life are ended. Another ruined suitor, who periodically appears from Shropshire and breaks out into efforts to address the Chancellor at the close of the day’s business and who can by no means be made to understand that the Chancellor is legally ignorant of his existence after making it desolate for a quarter of a century, plants himself in a good place and keeps an eye on the judge, ready to call out “My Lord!” in a voice of sonorous complaint on the instant of his rising. A few lawyers’ clerks and others who know this suitor by sight linger on the chance of his furnishing some fun and enlivening the dismal weather a little.", "33. 끼어든 자들"]], [["Jarndyce and Jarndyce drones on. This scarecrow of a suit has, in course of time, become so complicated that no man alive knows what it means. The parties to it understand it least, but it has been observed that no two Chancery lawyers can talk about it for five minutes without coming to a total disagreement as to all the premises. Innumerable children have been born into the cause; innumerable young people have married into it; innumerable old people have died out of it. Scores of persons have deliriously found themselves made parties in Jarndyce and Jarndyce without knowing how or why; whole families have inherited legendary hatreds with the suit. The little plaintiff or defendant who was promised a new rocking-horse when Jarndyce and Jarndyce should be settled has grown up, possessed himself of a real horse, and trotted away into the other world. Fair wards of court have faded into mothers and grandmothers; a long procession of Chancellors has come in and gone out; the legion of bills in the suit have been transformed into mere bills of mortality; there are not three Jarndyces left upon the earth perhaps since old Tom Jarndyce in despair blew his brains out at a coffee-house in Chancery Lane; but Jarndyce and Jarndyce still drags its dreary length before the court, perennially hopeless.", "34. 나사를 한 번 더 조이며\n35. 에스더의 이야기"]], [["Jarndyce and Jarndyce has passed into a joke. That is the only good that has ever come of it. It has been death to many, but it is a joke in the profession. Every master in Chancery has had a reference out of it. Every Chancellor was “in it,” for somebody or other, when he was counsel at the bar. Good things have been said about it by blue-nosed, bulbous-shoed old benchers in select port-wine committee after dinner in hall. Articled clerks have been in the habit of fleshing their legal wit upon it. The last Lord Chancellor handled it neatly, when, correcting Mr. Blowers, the eminent silk gown who said that such a thing might happen when the sky rained potatoes, he observed, “or when we get through Jarndyce and Jarndyce, Mr. Blowers”—a pleasantry that particularly tickled the maces, bags, and purses.", "36. 체스니 월드\n37. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스"]], [["How many people out of the suit Jarndyce and Jarndyce has stretched forth its unwholesome hand to spoil and corrupt would be a very wide question. From the master upon whose impaling files reams of dusty warrants in Jarndyce and Jarndyce have grimly writhed into many shapes, down to the copying-clerk in the Six Clerks’ Office who has copied his tens of thousands of Chancery folio-pages under that eternal heading, no man’s nature has been made better by it. In trickery, evasion, procrastination, spoliation, botheration, under false pretences of all sorts, there are influences that can never come to good. The very solicitors’ boys who have kept the wretched suitors at bay, by protesting time out of mind that Mr. Chizzle, Mizzle, or otherwise was particularly engaged and had appointments until dinner, may have got an extra moral twist and shuffle into themselves out of Jarndyce and Jarndyce. The receiver in the cause has acquired a goodly sum of money by it but has acquired too a distrust of his own mother and a contempt for his own kind. Chizzle, Mizzle, and otherwise have lapsed into a habit of vaguely promising themselves that they will look into that outstanding little matter and see what can be done for Drizzle—who was not well used—when Jarndyce and Jarndyce shall be got out of the office. Shirking and sharking in all their many varieties have been sown broadcast by the ill-fated cause; and even those who have contemplated its history from the outermost circle of such evil have been insensibly tempted into a loose way of letting bad things alone to take their own bad course, and a loose belief that if the world go wrong it was in some off-hand manner never meant to go right.", "38. 어떤 분투\n39. 변호사와 의뢰인"]], [["Thus, in the midst of the mud and at the heart of the fog, sits the Lord High Chancellor in his High Court of Chancery.", "40. 나라와 가정\n41. 토킹혼 씨의 방에서"]], [["“Mr. Tangle,”", "42."], ["says the Lord High Chancellor, latterly something restless under the eloquence of that learned gentleman.", "토킹혼 씨의 사무실에서\n43. 에스더의 이야기"]], [["“Mlud,” says Mr.", "44."], ["Tangle. Mr.", "편지와 답장\n45."], ["Tangle knows more of Jarndyce and Jarndyce than anybody. He is famous for it—supposed never to have read anything else since he left school.", "위임받아"]], [["“Have you nearly concluded your argument?”", "46. 저자를 잡아라! 47. 조의 유언"]], [["“Mlud, no—variety of points—feel it my duty tsubmit—ludship,”", "48. 좁혀 들며\n49."], ["is the reply that slides out of Mr. Tangle.", "충직한 우정"]], [["“Several members of the bar are still to be heard, I believe?”", "50. 에스더의 이야기\n51."], ["says the Chancellor with a slight smile.", "깨우침"]], [["Eighteen of Mr.", "52."], ["Tangle’s learned friends, each armed with a little summary of eighteen hundred sheets, bob up like eighteen hammers in a pianoforte, make eighteen bows, and drop into their eighteen places of obscurity.", "고집\n53. 자취"]], [["“We will proceed with the hearing on Wednesday fortnight,” says the Chancellor.", "54."], ["For the question at issue is only a question of costs, a mere bud on the forest tree of the parent suit, and really will come to a settlement one of these days.", "지뢰를 터뜨리며\n55. 도주"]], [["The Chancellor rises; the bar rises; the prisoner is brought forward in a hurry; the man from Shropshire cries, “My lord!”", "56. 추격\n57."], ["Maces, bags, and purses indignantly proclaim silence and frown at the man from Shropshire.", "에스더의 이야기"]], [["“In reference,”", "58."], ["proceeds the Chancellor, still on Jarndyce and Jarndyce, “to the young girl—”", "겨울 낮과 밤\n59. 에스더의 이야기"]], [["“Begludship’s pardon—boy,” says Mr.", "60."], ["Tangle prematurely. “In reference,”", "전망\n61."], ["proceeds the Chancellor with extra distinctness, “to the young girl and boy, the two young people”—Mr. Tangle crushed—“whom I directed to be in attendance to-day and who are now in my private room, I will see them and satisfy myself as to the expediency of making the order for their residing with their uncle.”", "어떤 발견"]], [["Mr. Tangle on his legs again.", "62."], ["“Begludship’s pardon—dead.”", "또 다른 발견\n63. 강철과 무쇠"]], [["“With their”—Chancellor looking through his double eye-glass at the papers on his desk—“grandfather.”", "64. 에스더의 이야기\n65. 세상을 시작하며"]], [["“Begludship’s pardon—victim of rash action—brains.”", "66. 링컨셔의 시골에서\n67. 에스더 이야기의 끝맺음"]], [["Suddenly a very little counsel with a terrific bass voice arises, fully inflated, in the back settlements of the fog, and says, “Will your lordship allow me?", "언젠가 어느 대법원 판사가 친절하게도, 광기를 의심받을 만한 구석이라곤 전혀 없는 백오십 명 남짓한 남녀 무리 가운데 한 사람인 나에게 이렇게 알려준 적이 있다. 대법원은 비록 세간의 편견이 화려하게 들끓는 대상이긴 하지만(이 대목에서 나는 판사의 눈길이 내 쪽으로 흘끔 향했다고 느꼈다), 거의 흠 하나 없이 깨끗하다는 것이다."], ["I appear for him.", "판사는 진행 속도에 사소한 결점이 한둘 있긴 했다고 인정했으나, 그것마저 과장된 데다 전적으로 \"대중의 인색함\""], ["He is a cousin, several times removed.", "탓이라고 했다."], ["I am not at the moment prepared to inform the court in what exact remove he is a cousin, but he IS a cousin.”", "그 죄 많은 대중이라는 것이, 알고 보니 최근까지도 더없이 단호한 태도로 대법원 판사의 정원을 결코 늘리지 않으려 작심해왔다는 것이었다. 그 정원이 정해진 건 내 생각에 리처드 이세 때이지만, 다른 어느 왕이라고 해도 상관없겠다."]], [["Leaving this address (delivered like a sepulchral message) ringing in the rafters of the roof, the very little counsel drops, and the fog knows him no more.", "이 농담은 책 본문에 끼워 넣기에는 너무 심오한 듯해서, 그러지 않았다면 나는 이 말을 입심 좋은 켄지 씨나 볼스 씨에게 되돌려주었을 것이다. 둘 중 한 사람 입에서 나온 말이 분명하다고 보기 때문이다."], ["Everybody looks for him.", "그런 입에 올렸다면 셰익스피어 소네트 한 구절을 적절히 짝지어 붙였을지도 모른다."], ["Nobody can see him.", "\"내 천성은 길들었네"]], [["“I will speak with both the young people,” says the Chancellor anew, “and satisfy myself on the subject of their residing with their cousin.", "염색공의 손처럼, 제 일하는 것에 물들어서."], ["I will mention the matter to-morrow morning when I take my seat.”", "그러니 나를 가엾이 여기고, 내가 새로워지기를 빌어주오!\""]], [["The Chancellor is about to bow to the bar when the prisoner is presented. Nothing can possibly come of the prisoner’s conglomeration but his being sent back to prison, which is soon done. The man from Shropshire ventures another remonstrative “My lord!” but the Chancellor, being aware of him, has dexterously vanished. Everybody else quickly vanishes too. A battery of blue bags is loaded with heavy charges of papers and carried off by clerks; the little mad old woman marches off with her documents; the empty court is locked up. If all the injustice it has committed and all the misery it has caused could only be locked up with it, and the whole burnt away in a great funeral pyre—why so much the better for other parties than the parties in Jarndyce and Jarndyce!", "그러나 인색한 대중이 이 문제와 관련해 그동안 어떤 일이 벌어졌고 지금도 벌어지고 있는지를 아는 것이 유익하기에, 나는 여기서 밝혀둔다. 이 책에 적힌 대법원에 관한 모든 내용은 실질적으로 진실이며, 진실의 범위 안에 있다. 그리들리의 사건은 본질에서 실제로 일어난 한 사건과 조금도 다르지 않으니, 그 끔찍한 부정을 처음부터 끝까지 직무상 속속들이 아는 어느 이해관계 없는 인사가 세상에 공개한 일이다. 바로 지금 이 순간(1853년 8월) 이 법정에는 거의 이십 년 전에 시작된 소송이 하나 걸려 있는데, 한자리에 변호인이 서른에서 마흔 명까지 나선 적이 있고, 발생한 소송 비용이 칠만 파운드에 이르며, 이것이 우호적 소송인데도, (장담하건대) 시작될 때나 지금이나 종결에 한 발짝도 가까워지지 않았다. 또 다른 유명한 대법원 소송이 하나 있는데 아직 판결이 나지 않았으니, 지난 세기가 저물기 전에 시작되어 칠만 파운드의 곱절이 넘는 금액이 소송 비용으로 삼켜졌다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에 다른 근거가 더 필요하다면, 나는 이 지면에 그것을 비처럼 쏟아부을 수 있으니, 그건 어느 인색한 대중의 수치다. 딱 한 가지만 더 한마디 덧붙이려 한다. 이른바 자연발화의 가능성은 크룩 씨가 죽은 뒤로 부정당해왔다. 나의 좋은 벗 루이스 씨는(그 일이 모든 권위자에게 폐기되었다고 여긴 것이 곧 알게 되듯 완전한 착각이었다) 그 사건이 기록되던 무렵 나에게 기발한 편지 몇 통을 공개하여, 자연발화란 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이라고 주장했다. 내가 독자를 고의로든 부주의로든 오도하지 않으며, 그 장면을 쓰기 전에 이 주제를 애써 조사했다는 점은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠다. 기록에 남은 사례가 서른 건쯤 되는데, 그 가운데 가장 유명한 코르넬리아 데 바우디 체세나테 백작 부인의 사건은 베로나의 참사회원 주세페 비안키니가 세밀하게 조사하고 기술했다. 그는 문필로도 이름난 인물로, 1731년 베로나에서 그 기록을 펴냈고 나중에 로마에서 다시 펴냈다. 그 사건에서 관찰된 정황은, 합리적인 의심을 모두 넘어, 크룩 씨의 사건에서 관찰된 정황과 똑같다. 그다음으로 유명한 사례는 그보다 육 년 앞서 랭스에서 일어났는데, 이 사건의 기록자는 프랑스가 배출한 가장 이름난 외과의 가운데 한 사람인 르 카다. 그 대상은 한 여인이었고, 그 남편은 아무것도 모르는 채 아내를 살해했다는 죄로 유죄 판결을 받았다. 그러나 상급 법원에 엄숙히 항소한 결과, 그녀가 자연발화라는 이름으로 불리는 바로 그 죽음을 맞았다는 사실이 증거로 드러나 무죄가 선고되었다. 나는 이 주목할 만한 사실들과, 제2권 30면에서 찾아볼 수 있는 권위자들에 대한 일반적 언급, 곧 더 근래에 활동한 프랑스, 영국, 스코틀랜드의 저명한 의학 교수들의 기록된 견해와 경험을 굳이 더 보탤 필요는 없다고 본다. 다만 한마디만 덧붙이자면, 인간의 사건이 으레 받아들여지는 그 증언이 상당량 자연발화로 사라지기 전까지는, 나는 이 사실들을 버리지 않으리라."]], [["It is but a glimpse of the world of fashion that we want on this same miry afternoon.", "* 옮긴이 주: 이 대목은 인쇄본의 특정 면을 가리킨다."], ["It is not so unlike the Court of Chancery but that we may pass from the one scene to the other, as the crow flies.", "이 프로젝트 구텐베르크 판본에서는 관련 내용이 제30장 90번째 문단에 있다."], ["Both the world of fashion and the Court of Chancery are things of precedent and usage: oversleeping Rip Van Winkles who have played at strange games through a deal of thundery weather; sleeping beauties whom the knight will wake one day, when all the stopped spits in the kitchen shall begin to turn prodigiously!", "* 또 한 가지 사례는 어느 치과의가 아주 또렷이 기술한 것으로, 비교적 최근 아메리카 합중국 콜럼버스라는 도시에서 일어났다. 그 대상은 술집을 운영하던 독일인이었으며, 구제 불능의 술꾼이었다."]], [["It is not a large world. Relatively even to this world of ours, which has its limits too (as your Highness shall find when you have made the tour of it and are come to the brink of the void beyond), it is a very little speck. There is much good in it; there are many good and true people in it; it has its appointed place. But the evil of it is that it is a world wrapped up in too much jeweller’s cotton and fine wool, and cannot hear the rushing of the larger worlds, and cannot see them as they circle round the sun. It is a deadened world, and its growth is sometimes unhealthy for want of air.", "「황폐한 집」에서 나는 친숙한 것들의 낭만적인 면을 일부러 곱씹어 다루었다. 1853년"]], [["My Lady Dedlock has returned to her house in town for a few days previous to her departure for Paris, where her ladyship intends to stay some weeks, after which her movements are uncertain.", "런던. 미카엘 축일 개정 기간이 막 끝났고, 대법원장은 링컨 법학원 회관에 자리 잡았다."], ["The fashionable intelligence says so for the comfort of the Parisians, and it knows all fashionable things.", "무자비한 십일월 날씨. 거리에는 진흙이 어찌나 두껍게 깔렸는지 마치 물이 이제 막 대지 표면에서 물러난 것만 같으니, 사십 척쯤 되는 거대공룡 한 마리가 코끼리만 한 도마뱀처럼 뒤뚱뒤뚱 홀본 언덕을 기어오르는 광경을 마주친대도 그리 놀랄 일은 아니겠다."], ["To know things otherwise were to be unfashionable. My Lady Dedlock has been down at what she calls, in familiar conversation, her “place”", "굴뚝 꼭대기에서 연기가 내려앉아 부드러운 검은 이슬비를 이루고, 그 안에는 다 자란 눈송이만큼 굵은 검댕 조각이 섞여 있다."], ["in Lincolnshire. The waters are out in Lincolnshire.", "마치 태양의 죽음을 애도해 상복을 차려입은 듯하다."], ["An arch of the bridge in the park has been sapped and sopped away.", "진창에 뒤섞여 분간조차 안 되는 개들. 그보다 나을 것도 없는 말들."], ["The adjacent low-lying ground for half a mile in breadth is a stagnant river with melancholy trees for islands in it and a surface punctured all over, all day long, with falling rain.", "눈가리개까지 흙탕물이 튀었다. 걸어 다니는 행인들은 너 나 할 것 없이 심술이라는 전염병에 옮은 듯 서로 우산을 부딪쳐대고, 동틀 녘부터(이날에 동이 트기는 했다면) 수만 명의 다른 행인이 미끄러지고 자빠진 길모퉁이에서 발을 헛디딘다."], ["My Lady Dedlock’s place has been extremely dreary. The weather for many a day and night has been so wet that the trees seem wet through, and the soft loppings and prunings of the woodman’s axe can make no crash or crackle as they fall.", "켜켜이 쌓인 진흙 더께 위에 새로운 침전물을 보태고, 그 지점마다 포도에 끈질기게 들러붙어, 복리로 불어난다."], ["The deer, looking soaked, leave quagmires where they pass.", "사방이 안개투성이다."], ["The shot of a rifle loses its sharpness in the moist air, and its smoke moves in a tardy little cloud towards the green rise, coppice-topped, that makes a background for the falling rain.", "강 위쪽도 안개, 푸른 모래톱과 풀밭 사이를 흐르는 그곳도 안개다. 강 아래쪽도 안개, 빽빽이 늘어선 선박들과 거대하고 (지저분한) 도시의 강변 오염물 사이를 더럽게 굴러다니는 그곳도 안개다."], ["The view from my Lady Dedlock’s own windows is alternately a lead-coloured view and a view in Indian ink.", "에식스 습지에도 안개, 켄트의 고지대에도 안개."], ["The vases on the stone terrace in the foreground catch the rain all day; and the heavy drops fall—drip, drip, drip—upon the broad flagged pavement, called from old time the Ghost’s Walk, all night.", "안개는 석탄 운반선의 취사간으로 기어들고, 큰 배의 활대 위에 드러눕고 삭구 사이를 맴돈다. 안개는 거룻배와 작은 배의 뱃전에 축 늘어진다."], ["On Sundays the little church in the park is mouldy; the oaken pulpit breaks out into a cold sweat; and there is a general smell and taste as of the ancient Dedlocks in their graves.", "안개는 병동 난롯가에서 쌕쌕거리는 늙은 그리니치 연금 수령자들의 눈과 목구멍에도, 비좁은 선실 아래서 성난 선장이 빨아 대는 오후 담뱃대의 대통과 통에도 깃들고, 갑판 위에서 덜덜 떠는 어린 견습생의 발가락과 손가락을 잔인하게 꼬집는다."], ["My Lady Dedlock (who is childless), looking out in the early twilight from her boudoir at a keeper’s lodge and seeing the light of a fire upon the latticed panes, and smoke rising from the chimney, and a child, chased by a woman, running out into the rain to meet the shining figure of a wrapped-up man coming through the gate, has been put quite out of temper. My Lady Dedlock says she has been “bored to death.”", "다리 위를 지나던 사람들은 난간 너머로 안개로 이루어진 발아래 하늘을 들여다보는데, 사방이 온통 안개여서 마치 자기가 기구를 타고 올라가 자욱한 구름 속에 매달린 것만 같다."]], [["Therefore my Lady Dedlock has come away from the place in Lincolnshire and has left it to the rain, and the crows, and the rabbits, and the deer, and the partridges and pheasants. The pictures of the Dedlocks past and gone have seemed to vanish into the damp walls in mere lowness of spirits, as the housekeeper has passed along the old rooms shutting up the shutters. And when they will next come forth again, the fashionable intelligence—which, like the fiend, is omniscient of the past and present, but not the future—cannot yet undertake to say.", "가스등이 거리 곳곳에서 안개를 뚫고 어렴풋이 빛난다. 마치 흠뻑 젖은 들판에서 농부와 쟁기질하는 소년이 어렴풋이 떠오르는 태양을 바라보듯이. 가게는 거의 다 평소보다 두 시간이나 일찍 불을 밝혔다. 가스등도 그 사실을 아는지, 야위고 마지못한 듯한 표정이다. 쌀쌀한 오후가 가장 쌀쌀하고, 짙은 안개가 가장 짙으며, 진흙투성이 거리가 가장 진흙투성이인 곳은, 저 둔하기 짝이 없는 늙은 장애물 근처다. 둔하기 짝이 없는 늙은 법인의 문턱에 딱 어울리는 장식물, 바로 템플 바다. 그리고 템플 바 바로 곁, 안개의 한복판에 있는 링컨 법학원 회관에, 대법원장이 자신의 대법원에 앉아 있다."]], [["Sir Leicester Dedlock is only a baronet, but there is no mightier baronet than he. His family is as old as the hills, and infinitely more respectable. He has a general opinion that the world might get on without hills but would be done up without Dedlocks. He would on the whole admit nature to be a good idea (a little low, perhaps, when not enclosed with a park-fence), but an idea dependent for its execution on your great county families. He is a gentleman of strict conscience, disdainful of all littleness and meanness and ready on the shortest notice to die any death you may please to mention rather than give occasion for the least impeachment of his integrity. He is an honourable, obstinate, truthful, high-spirited, intensely prejudiced, perfectly unreasonable man.", "이 대법원이, 케케묵은 죄인들 가운데 가장 해로운 그것이, 오늘 하늘과 땅이 지켜보는 가운데 더듬거리고 허우적대는 꼴과 맞아떨어지려면, 아무리 짙은 안개라도 모자라고 아무리 깊은 진흙과 진창이라도 모자랄 것이다. 이런 오후라면, 다른 어느 때보다도, 대법원장은 바로 여기에 앉아 있어야 마땅하다. 실제로 여기 앉아 있다. 머리 둘레에는 안개의 후광을 두르고, 진홍빛 천과 휘장으로 폭신하게 둘러싸인 채로, 큼직한 구레나룻에 자그마한 목소리, 끝도 없이 이어지는 변론서를 든 거구의 변호사가 그에게 말을 늘어놓고, 그는 겉으로는 지붕의 채광창 쪽으로 시선을 두지만 거기서 보이는 것이라곤 안개뿐이다. 이런 오후라면 대법원 법정 변호사단의 스무 명 남짓한 사람들이, 실제로 여기 그러고 있듯이, 끝없는 소송의 만 가지 단계 가운데 하나에 안개 속에서 매달려, 미끄러운 판례를 두고 서로 발을 걸어 넘어뜨리고, 무릎까지 빠지는 전문 용어 속을 더듬거리며, 염소 털과 말총으로 만든 가발 쓴 머리를 말의 벽에 들이받고, 마치 배우들이 그러듯 정색한 얼굴로 형평을 가장한다. 이런 오후라면 그 소송에 관여한 갖가지 사무 변호사들이, 그중 두셋은 그것으로 한밑천 잡은 아버지에게서 소송을 물려받았는데, 실제로 그러지 않은가, 한 줄로 늘어서 있을 것이다. 등기관의 붉은 탁자와 비단 법복 사이, 길게 깔개를 깐 우물 같은 자리에 (다만 그 바닥에서 진실을 찾으려 들면 헛수고일 테지만), 소장과 반소장, 답변서, 재항변서, 강제명령서, 진술서, 쟁점, 심리관에게 보내는 회부서, 심리관의 보고서, 값비싼 헛소리의 산더미를 앞에 쌓아 놓고서. 법정이 흐릿한 것도 당연하다, 여기저기서 양초가 닳아 가니. 안개가 거기 무겁게 걸려 영영 빠져나가지 못할 듯한 것도 당연하다. 스테인드글라스 창이 색을 잃고 한 줄기 햇빛도 들이지 못하는 것도 당연하다. 거리에서 문의 유리창으로 안을 들여다보는 문외한들이 그 부엉이 같은 음산한 분위기에, 그리고 폭신한 단상에서 빛이라곤 없는 채광창을 들여다보는 대법원장 쪽에서 지붕까지 나른하게 메아리치며 늘어지는 말소리에 들어설 엄두를 못 내는 것도 당연하다, 단상에서는 시중드는 가발들이 죄다 안개 둑에 처박혀 있으니! 이것이 바로 대법원이다. 모든 주마다 무너져 가는 집과 황폐해진 땅을 거느리고, 모든 정신병원마다 진이 빠진 미치광이를, 모든 교회 묘지마다 죽은 자를 거느린 곳. 닳아빠진 신발 뒤축에 해진 옷을 걸친 파산한 소송인이 아는 사람마다 찾아다니며 빌리고 구걸하게 만드는 곳. 돈의 위세에는 정당한 권리를 지치게 만들 수단을 차고 넘치게 쥐여 주는 곳. 재산과 인내와 용기와 희망을 어찌나 바닥내고, 머리를 무너뜨리고 가슴을 찢어 놓는지, 그 종사자 가운데 명예로운 사람이라면 누구나 이런 경고를 던지지 않을 수 없는, 실제로 자주 던지는 곳. \"여기 오느니 차라리 당할 수 있는 부당함은 다 당하시오!\""]], [["Sir Leicester is twenty years, full measure, older than my Lady. He will never see sixty-five again, nor perhaps sixty-six, nor yet sixty-seven. He has a twist of the gout now and then and walks a little stiffly. He is of a worthy presence, with his light-grey hair and whiskers, his fine shirt-frill, his pure-white waistcoat, and his blue coat with bright buttons always buttoned. He is ceremonious, stately, most polite on every occasion to my Lady, and holds her personal attractions in the highest estimation. His gallantry to my Lady, which has never changed since he courted her, is the one little touch of romantic fancy in him.", "이 음침한 오후, 대법원장의 법정에 자리한 이는, 대법원장과 그 소송의 변호인, 어느 소송에도 끼지 않는 변호인 두셋, 그리고 앞서 말한 사무 변호사들의 우물 말고 또 누가 있을까? 판사 아래쪽에는 가발에 법복을 갖춘 등기관이 있다. 그리고 법정용 정장을 차려입고 권표나 소송 문서 가방이나 옥새 주머니나 뭐 그런 것을 든 사람이 두셋 있다. 이들은 죄다 하품을 한다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송(지금 다루는 그 사건)에서는 재미 부스러기 하나 떨어지는 법이 없으니, 그것은 오래오래전에 진작 바싹 짜내어졌다. 속기사와 법정 기록원, 신문 기자들은 잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 시작되면 어김없이 다른 단골들과 함께 자리를 뜬다. 그들의 자리는 텅 비어 있다. 휘장을 친 성소를 더 잘 들여다보려고 회관 한쪽 좌석에 올라선 사람이 있으니, 찌그러진 보닛을 쓴 자그마한 미친 노파다. 그녀는 법정이 열릴 때부터 파할 때까지 늘 자리를 지키며, 도무지 알 수 없는 어떤 판결이 자기에게 유리하게 내려지기를 언제나 기다린다. 어떤 이는 그녀가 정말 어느 소송의 당사자이거나 그랬다고 하지만, 아무도 확실히는 모른다, 아무도 신경 쓰지 않으니까. 그녀는 망태기에 자질구레한 쓰레기를 넣고 다니며 그것을 자기 서류라고 부르는데, 주로 종이 성냥과 마른 라벤더로 이루어져 있다. 누렇게 뜬 죄수 하나가 구금 상태로 끌려 나왔으니, 벌써 여섯 번째로 \"자기 모욕죄를 씻겠다\"는 본인 신청을 하러 온 것이다. 그러나 그는 단 하나 살아남은 유언 집행인으로, 자기가 알았다고는 누구도 주장하지 않는 회계 장부 문제로 뒤죽박죽 빠져든 처지라, 그 일을 끝내기란 도무지 가망 없는 노릇이다. 그러는 사이 그의 인생 전망은 끝장나 버렸다. 또 다른 파산한 소송인이 있는데, 그는 슈롭셔에서 주기적으로 나타나 하루 업무가 끝날 무렵 대법원장에게 한마디 하려고 발버둥 치는 사람이다. 대법원장이 사반세기 동안 자기 삶을 황폐하게 만들어 놓고도 법적으로는 자기 존재를 모른다는 사실을 도무지 납득하지 못하는 그는, 좋은 자리를 차지하고 판사를 노려보다가, 판사가 일어서는 순간 낭랑하게 울리는 항의의 목소리로 \"각하!\" 하고 외칠 채비를 한다. 이 소송인의 얼굴을 아는 변호사 사무원 몇과 다른 이들은, 그가 무슨 볼거리를 제공해 이 음울한 날씨를 조금이나마 활기차게 해 줄까 싶어 어슬렁대며 남는다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 단조롭게 웅웅거리며 이어진다. 이 허수아비 같은 소송은 세월이 흐르면서 어찌나 복잡해졌는지 살아 있는 누구도 그 뜻을 모른다. 그 당사자들이 가장 모르는데, 대법원 변호사 둘이 그 소송을 두고 오 분만 이야기해도 모든 전제를 놓고 완전히 엇갈리지 않는 법이 없다는 것이 지금껏 관찰된 바다. 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 아이가 이 소송 속에서 태어났고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 젊은이가 이 소송 속에서 혼인했으며, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 노인이 이 소송에서 빠져나가듯 죽었다. 수십 명이 영문도 모른 채 어쩌다 자기가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 당사자가 되어 있는 것을 정신없이 발견했고, 온 집안이 이 소송과 함께 전설처럼 내려오는 증오를 물려받았다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 해결되면 새 흔들목마를 사 주겠다는 약속을 받았던 어린 원고나 피고는, 어느덧 자라 진짜 말을 손에 넣고는, 저세상으로 총총히 달려가 버렸다. 어여쁘던 법원의 피후견 처녀들은 시들어 어머니가 되고 할머니가 되었다. 대법원장의 긴 행렬이 들고 났다. 소송에 쌓인 군단 같은 소장들은 그저 사망자 명부로 둔갑했다. 늙은 톰 잔다이스가 절망 끝에 챈서리 거리의 어느 커피점에서 제 머리통을 날려 버린 뒤로, 어쩌면 이 땅에 잔다이스라는 사람은 셋도 남지 않았을 것이다. 그래도 잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 여전히 그 음울한 몸뚱이를 법정 앞에서 질질 끌고 다니니, 영원토록 가망 없는 채로."]], [["Indeed, he married her for love. A whisper still goes about that she had not even family; howbeit, Sir Leicester had so much family that perhaps he had enough and could dispense with any more. But she had beauty, pride, ambition, insolent resolve, and sense enough to portion out a legion of fine ladies. Wealth and station, added to these, soon floated her upward, and for years now my Lady Dedlock has been at the centre of the fashionable intelligence and at the top of the fashionable tree.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 웃음거리가 되어 버렸다. 그것이 이 소송에서 나온 단 하나의 좋은 결실이다. 그것은 많은 사람에게 죽음이었지만, 법조계에서는 농담거리다. 대법원의 심리관이라면 누구나 그것에서 회부를 하나쯤 받아 보았다. 대법원장이라면 누구나 변호사 시절 누군가를 위해 \"거기 끼어\" 있었다. 코가 시퍼렇고 구근처럼 부푼 신발을 신은 늙은 법학원 간부들이 회관에서 만찬을 마친 뒤 엄선된 포트와인 위원회 자리에서 그것을 두고 그럴듯한 우스개를 떨었다. 견습 변호사들은 그것에 대고 법률적 재치를 갈고닦는 버릇이 있었다. 지난번 대법원장은 그것을 솜씨 좋게 다루었으니, 저명한 비단 법복의 블로어스 씨가 하늘에서 감자가 비처럼 쏟아진다면 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있겠다고 하자, 그 말을 바로잡으며 \"아니면 우리가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 다 끝마쳤을 때라든가요, 블로어스 씨\" 하고 한마디 한 것인데, 이 익살에 권표며 가방이며 주머니를 든 자들이 유독 간지러워했다."]], [["How Alexander wept when he had no more worlds to conquer, everybody knows—or has some reason to know by this time, the matter having been rather frequently mentioned.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 소송 바깥의 얼마나 많은 사람에게 그 해로운 손을 뻗쳐 망치고 더럽혔는지는 참으로 막막한 질문이겠다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 먼지 쌓인 영장 다발이 무더기로 음산하게 온갖 모양으로 비틀려 꽂힌 꽂이를 둔 심리관부터, 그 영원한 표제 아래 수만 장의 대법원 이절판 면을 베껴 온 육서기과의 필사 서기에 이르기까지, 그것 때문에 천성이 나아진 사람은 하나도 없다."], ["My Lady Dedlock, having conquered HER world, fell not into the melting, but rather into the freezing, mood.", "온갖 종류의 거짓 구실 아래 자행되는 속임수, 회피, 미루기, 약탈, 골치 썩이기 속에는 결코 좋게 끝날 수 없는 영향력이 도사리고 있다. 가엾은 소송인들을 멀찍이 떼어 놓느라 치즐 씨든 미즐 씨든 누구든 특별히 바빠서 만찬 때까지 약속이 꽉 찼다고 까마득한 옛날부터 우겨 온 그 변호사 사무실 심부름꾼 소년들조차, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 덕에 도덕성이 한층 더 뒤틀리고 비뚤어졌을지 모른다."], ["An exhausted composure, a worn-out placidity, an equanimity of fatigue not to be ruffled by interest or satisfaction, are the trophies of her victory.", "이 소송의 재산 관리인은 그것으로 제법 짭짤한 돈을 챙겼지만, 그러면서 제 어머니마저 못 믿는 불신과 제 동족을 향한 경멸도 함께 챙겼다. 치즐과 미즐을 비롯한 무리는, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스가 사무실에서 치워지고 나면 저 미결로 남은 자질구레한 건을 들여다보고 부당한 대우를 받은 드리즐을 위해 무엇을 해 줄 수 있을지 알아보겠노라고 막연히 스스로 다짐하는 버릇에 빠져들었다."], ["She is perfectly well-bred.", "온갖 형태의 직무 태만과 사기 행각이 이 불운한 소송에서 사방으로 뿌려졌다."], ["If she could be translated to heaven to-morrow, she might be expected to ascend without any rapture.", "이런 악행의 가장 바깥 테두리에서 그 역사를 지켜본 사람들조차, 나쁜 일은 나쁜 길로 제멋대로 가게 내버려 두는 헐거운 태도에, 그리고 세상이 잘못 돌아간다면 그것은 어쩌다 그리된 것일 뿐 애초에 바로 돌아가게 되어 있지도 않았다는 헐거운 믿음에, 자기도 모르게 솔깃해지고 말았다. 이리하여, 진흙 한복판에, 안개의 심장부에, 대법원장이 자신의 대법원에 앉아 있다."]], [["She has beauty still, and if it be not in its heyday, it is not yet in its autumn.", "\"탱글 씨.\" 대법원장이 말한다."], ["She has a fine face—originally of a character that would be rather called very pretty than handsome, but improved into classicality by the acquired expression of her fashionable state.", "요즈음 들어 그는 그 박식한 신사의 달변에 다소 안절부절못하는 기색이다."], ["Her figure is elegant and has the effect of being tall.", "\"각하.\" 탱글 씨가 말한다."], ["Not that she is so, but that “the most is made,” as the Honourable Bob Stables has frequently asserted upon oath, “of all her points.”", "탱글 씨는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에 관해서라면 누구보다 많이 안다."], ["The same authority observes that she is perfectly got up and remarks in commendation of her hair especially that she is the best-groomed woman in the whole stud.", "그는 그것으로 유명한데, 학교를 떠난 뒤로는 다른 것은 아무것도 읽은 적이 없으리라고들 한다."]], [["With all her perfections on her head, my Lady Dedlock has come up from her place in Lincolnshire (hotly pursued by the fashionable intelligence) to pass a few days at her house in town previous to her departure for Paris, where her ladyship intends to stay some weeks, after which her movements are uncertain.", "\"변론을 거의 마쳤소?\""], ["And at her house in town, upon this muddy, murky afternoon, presents himself an old-fashioned old gentleman, attorney-at-law and eke solicitor of the High Court of Chancery, who has the honour of acting as legal adviser of the Dedlocks and has as many cast-iron boxes in his office with that name outside as if the present baronet were the coin of the conjuror’s trick and were constantly being juggled through the whole set.", "\"각하, 아닙니다, 여러 쟁점이, 소생의 본분이라 사료되어 개진하건대, 각하.\""], ["Across the hall, and up the stairs, and along the passages, and through the rooms, which are very brilliant in the season and very dismal out of it—fairy-land to visit, but a desert to live in—the old gentleman is conducted by a Mercury in powder to my Lady’s presence.", "탱글 씨에게서 미끄러져 나오는 대답이다."]], [["The old gentleman is rusty to look at, but is reputed to have made good thrift out of aristocratic marriage settlements and aristocratic wills, and to be very rich. He is surrounded by a mysterious halo of family confidences, of which he is known to be the silent depository. There are noble mausoleums rooted for centuries in retired glades of parks among the growing timber and the fern, which perhaps hold fewer noble secrets than walk abroad among men, shut up in the breast of Mr. Tulkinghorn. He is of what is called the old school—a phrase generally meaning any school that seems never to have been young—and wears knee-breeches tied with ribbons, and gaiters or stockings. One peculiarity of his black clothes and of his black stockings, be they silk or worsted, is that they never shine. Mute, close, irresponsive to any glancing light, his dress is like himself. He never converses when not professionally consulted. He is found sometimes, speechless but quite at home, at corners of dinner-tables in great country houses and near doors of drawing-rooms, concerning which the fashionable intelligence is eloquent, where everybody knows him and where half the Peerage stops to say “How do you do, Mr. Tulkinghorn?” He receives these salutations with gravity and buries them along with the rest of his knowledge.", "\"법정 변호사 몇 분의 진술이 아직 남은 듯한데, 그렇소?\" 대법원장이 옅은 미소를 띠며 말한다. 탱글 씨의 박식한 동료 열여덟 명이, 저마다 천팔백 장짜리 요약서로 무장하고서, 피아노 안의 망치 열여덟 개처럼 불쑥 솟구쳐 열여덟 번 절을 하고는, 자기네 열여덟 개의 흐릿한 자리로 도로 주저앉는다."]], [["Sir Leicester Dedlock is with my Lady and is happy to see Mr.", "\"심리는 이 주 뒤 수요일에 속행하겠소.\""], ["Tulkinghorn.", "대법원장이 말한다."], ["There is an air of prescription about him which is always agreeable to Sir Leicester; he receives it as a kind of tribute. He likes Mr.", "지금 다투는 문제란 그저 소송 비용 문제일 뿐이니, 본 소송이라는 큰 나무에 돋은 한낱 봉오리에 지나지 않으며, 언젠가는 정말로 결판이 나기는 할 것이다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s dress; there is a kind of tribute in that too.", "대법원장이 일어선다. 법정 변호사들이 일어선다."], ["It is eminently respectable, and likewise, in a general way, retainer-like.", "죄수가 황급히 앞으로 끌려 나온다. 슈롭셔에서 온 사내가 \"각하!\""], ["It expresses, as it were, the steward of the legal mysteries, the butler of the legal cellar, of the Dedlocks.", "하고 외친다. 권표며 가방이며 주머니를 든 자들이 분개하여 정숙을 외치며 슈롭셔에서 온 사내를 노려본다."]], [["Has Mr. Tulkinghorn any idea of this himself? It may be so, or it may not, but there is this remarkable circumstance to be noted in everything associated with my Lady Dedlock as one of a class—as one of the leaders and representatives of her little world. She supposes herself to be an inscrutable Being, quite out of the reach and ken of ordinary mortals—seeing herself in her glass, where indeed she looks so. Yet every dim little star revolving about her, from her maid to the manager of the Italian Opera, knows her weaknesses, prejudices, follies, haughtinesses, and caprices and lives upon as accurate a calculation and as nice a measure of her moral nature as her dressmaker takes of her physical proportions. Is a new dress, a new custom, a new singer, a new dancer, a new form of jewellery, a new dwarf or giant, a new chapel, a new anything, to be set up? There are deferential people in a dozen callings whom my Lady Dedlock suspects of nothing but prostration before her, who can tell you how to manage her as if she were a baby, who do nothing but nurse her all their lives, who, humbly affecting to follow with profound subservience, lead her and her whole troop after them; who, in hooking one, hook all and bear them off as Lemuel Gulliver bore away the stately fleet of the majestic Lilliput. “If you want to address our people, sir,” say Blaze and Sparkle, the jewellers—meaning by our people Lady Dedlock and the rest—“you must remember that you are not dealing with the general public; you must hit our people in their weakest place, and their weakest place is such a place.” “To make this article go down, gentlemen,” say Sheen and Gloss, the mercers, to their friends the manufacturers, “you must come to us, because we know where to have the fashionable people, and we can make it fashionable.” “If you want to get this print upon the tables of my high connexion, sir,” says Mr. Sladdery, the librarian, “or if you want to get this dwarf or giant into the houses of my high connexion, sir, or if you want to secure to this entertainment the patronage of my high connexion, sir, you must leave it, if you please, to me, for I have been accustomed to study the leaders of my high connexion, sir, and I may tell you without vanity that I can turn them round my finger”—in which Mr. Sladdery, who is an honest man, does not exaggerate at all.", "\"그 어린 처녀에 관해서는—\" 대법원장이 여전히 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 이어 간다. \"황송하오나 각하, 사내아이입니다.\" 탱글 씨가 성급히 끼어든다. \"그 어린 처녀와 사내아이, 그 두 젊은이에 관해서는—\" 대법원장이 한층 또렷하게 이어 가니 탱글 씨는 찍소리도 못 한다. \"오늘 출석하라고 내가 지시했고 지금 내 집무실에 와 있는 그들을, 내가 만나 보고 그들이 백부와 함께 거주하도록 명령을 내리는 것이 타당한지 직접 판단해 보겠소.\""]], [["Therefore, while Mr.", "탱글 씨가 다시 벌떡 일어선다."], ["Tulkinghorn may not know what is passing in the Dedlock mind at present, it is very possible that he may.", "\"황송하오나 각하, 작고하셨습니다.\""]], [["“My Lady’s cause has been again before the Chancellor, has it, Mr.", "\"그러면 그들의—\" 대법원장이 겹안경 너머로 책상 위 서류들을 훑어본다."], ["Tulkinghorn?”", "\"조부와 함께.\""], ["says Sir Leicester, giving him his hand.", "\"황송하오나 각하, 경솔한 행동의 희생자가, 머리통이.\""]], [["“Yes. It has been on again to-day,”", "별안간 아주 자그마한 변호인 하나가 무시무시한 저음으로, 잔뜩 부풀어, 안개의 뒷자리 변두리에서 일어서더니 말한다."], ["Mr.", "\"각하, 한 말씀 올려도 되겠습니까? 제가 그를 변호합니다."], ["Tulkinghorn replies, making one of his quiet bows to my Lady, who is on a sofa near the fire, shading her face with a hand-screen.", "그는 사촌으로, 몇 다리 건너간 사촌입니다. 정확히 몇 다리 건너간 사촌인지는 지금 당장 법정에 아뢸 채비가 되어 있지 않으나, 사촌인 것만은 분명합니다.\""]], [["“It would be useless to ask,”", "이 진술을(마치 무덤에서 보내온 전갈처럼 읊조린) 지붕 서까래에 쟁쟁히 울려 놓은 채, 그 아주 자그마한 변호인이 도로 주저앉으니, 안개는 더는 그를 알아보지 못한다."], ["says my Lady with the dreariness of the place in Lincolnshire still upon her, “whether anything has been done.”", "모두가 그를 찾는다. 아무도 그를 보지 못한다."]], [["“Nothing that YOU would call anything has been done to-day,”", "\"내가 두 젊은이 모두와 이야기해 보고,\" 대법원장이 다시 말한다."], ["replies Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "\"그들이 사촌과 함께 거주하는 문제에 관해 직접 판단해 보겠소."], ["“Nor ever will be,” says my Lady.", "이 일은 내일 아침 착석할 때 다시 거론하겠소.\""]], [["Sir Leicester has no objection to an interminable Chancery suit. It is a slow, expensive, British, constitutional kind of thing. To be sure, he has not a vital interest in the suit in question, her part in which was the only property my Lady brought him; and he has a shadowy impression that for his name—the name of Dedlock—to be in a cause, and not in the title of that cause, is a most ridiculous accident. But he regards the Court of Chancery, even if it should involve an occasional delay of justice and a trifling amount of confusion, as a something devised in conjunction with a variety of other somethings by the perfection of human wisdom for the eternal settlement (humanly speaking) of everything. And he is upon the whole of a fixed opinion that to give the sanction of his countenance to any complaints respecting it would be to encourage some person in the lower classes to rise up somewhere—like Wat Tyler.", "대법원장이 막 법정 변호사들에게 인사하려는데 죄수가 앞에 세워진다. 그 죄수의 뒤죽박죽 사정에서 나올 수 있는 결과라곤 도로 감옥에 보내지는 것뿐이고, 그 일은 이내 처리된다. 슈롭셔에서 온 사내가 또 한 번 항의 섞인 \"각하!\"를 용감히 외쳐 보지만, 대법원장은 그를 진작 알아챈 터라 잽싸게 자취를 감춘 뒤다. 다른 사람들도 모두 재빨리 사라진다. 파란 가방 부대에 묵직한 서류 탄약이 장전되어 사무원들 손에 실려 나간다. 자그마한 미친 노파가 자기 서류를 들고 행군하듯 떠난다. 텅 빈 법정에 자물쇠가 채워진다. 이 법정이 저지른 모든 부정과 야기한 모든 비참함까지 함께 잠가 버리고, 그 전부를 거대한 화장용 장작더미 위에서 불살라 없앨 수만 있다면, 어쩌면 그것은 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 당사자들을 뺀 다른 모든 이에게 그야말로 더없이 좋은 일이겠다! 이 진창 같은 오후에 우리가 들여다보고 싶은 것은 유행의 세계를 살짝 엿보는 정도다. 그 세계는 대법원과 그리 다르지 않아서, 까마귀가 곧장 날아가듯 한 장면에서 다른 장면으로 건너갈 수 있다. 유행의 세계도 대법원도 둘 다 전례와 관습으로 굴러가는 것들이다. 천둥 치는 궂은 날씨 속에서 기이한 놀이에 빠져 늘어지게 잠든 립 밴 윙클들이며, 어느 날 기사가 깨워줄 잠자는 미녀들이다. 그날이 오면 부엌에서 멈춰 섰던 꼬챙이가 죄다 어마어마하게 돌아가기 시작하리라!"]], [["“As a few fresh affidavits have been put upon the file,”", "* 립 밴 윙클: 워싱턴 어빙의 단편 주인공으로, 산에서 술을 마시고 이십 년 동안 잠들었다 깨어난 사내."], ["says Mr.", "크지 않은 세계다."], ["Tulkinghorn, “and as they are short, and as I proceed upon the troublesome principle of begging leave to possess my clients with any new proceedings in a cause”—cautious man Mr.", "그 나름의 한계가 있는 우리 세계에 견주어도(전하께서 그 세계를 한 바퀴 돌아 저편 허무의 벼랑에 다다르면 알게 되시리라) 아주 자그마한 티끌에 지나지 않는다. 그 안에도 좋은 것이 많고, 선하고 진실한 사람도 많으며, 정해진 자리가 있다."], ["Tulkinghorn, taking no more responsibility than necessary—“and further, as I see you are going to Paris, I have brought them in my pocket.”", "그러나 그 세계의 악은, 보석 세공사가 쓰는 솜과 고운 양털에 너무 겹겹이 싸여 더 큰 세계들이 휘몰아치는 소리를 듣지 못하고, 그것들이 태양을 돌며 도는 모습을 보지 못한다는 데 있다. 감각이 마비된 세계요, 공기가 모자라 그 자람이 이따금 병들곤 하는 세계다."]], [["(Sir Leicester was going to Paris too, by the by, but the delight of the fashionable intelligence was in his Lady.)\nMr. Tulkinghorn takes out his papers, asks permission to place them on a golden talisman of a table at my Lady’s elbow, puts on his spectacles, and begins to read by the light of a shaded lamp.", "데드록 마님은 파리로 떠나기 며칠 전에 시내 저택으로 돌아와 있다. 마님은 파리에서 몇 주 머물 작정이며, 그 뒤 행보는 아직 정해지지 않았다. 유행 소식통이 파리 사람들을 안심시키려고 그렇게 전하는데, 그 소식통은 유행에 관한 일이라면 죄다 꿰고 있다. 달리 안다는 것은 곧 유행에 뒤떨어진다는 뜻이다. 데드록 마님은 친근한 대화에서 자신의 \"별장\"이라 부르는 링컨셔의 영지에 내려가 있었다. 링컨셔에는 물이 넘쳐흐른다. 정원에 놓인 다리의 아치 하나가 무너져 물에 잠겼다. 너비 반 마일에 이르는 인근 저지대는 고인 강물이 되어, 음울한 나무들이 그 가운데 섬처럼 솟았고, 수면은 종일 떨어지는 빗방울로 온통 구멍이 뚫린다. 데드록 마님의 별장은 더없이 황량하기 그지없다. 여러 날 여러 밤 날씨가 어찌나 축축한지 나무들이 흠뻑 젖은 듯하고, 나무꾼의 도끼에 베이고 잘려나간 가지들이 떨어질 때조차 우지끈 소리 한 번 내지 못한다. 흠뻑 젖은 사슴들은 지나간 자리마다 진창을 남긴다. 소총 한 발의 날카로운 소리도 눅눅한 공기 속에 무뎌지고, 그 연기는 떨어지는 비를 배경으로 잡목이 우거진 푸른 언덕배기 쪽으로 굼뜬 작은 구름이 되어 흘러간다. 데드록 마님의 창에서 내다보이는 풍경은 어떤 때는 납빛이요 어떤 때는 먹빛이다. 앞뜰 돌 테라스에 놓인 화병들은 종일 비를 받고, 묵직한 빗방울이 뚝뚝뚝 옛날부터 유령의 길이라 불려온 너른 포석 위로 밤새도록 떨어진다. 일요일이면 정원 안 작은 교회에서는 곰팡내가 나고, 떡갈나무 설교단에서 식은땀이 배어 나오며, 무덤 속에 누운 옛 데드록 가문 사람들 같은 냄새와 맛이 사방에 감돈다. 자식이 없는 데드록 마님은 이른 황혼 녘 규방에서 사냥터지기 오두막을 내다보다가, 격자창에 어린 불빛과 굴뚝에서 피어오르는 연기, 그리고 한 여인에게 쫓기는 아이가 빗속으로 달려나가 대문을 들어서는, 옷을 잔뜩 껴입은 사내의 환한 모습을 맞이하는 광경을 보고는 잔뜩 심사가 뒤틀려버렸다. 데드록 마님은 자신이 \"지루해 죽을 뻔했다\"고 말한다."]], [["“‘In Chancery. Between John Jarndyce—’”", "그리하여 데드록 마님은 링컨셔의 별장을 떠나 비와 까마귀와 토끼와 사슴과 자고새와 꿩에게 그곳을 맡겨버렸다."], ["My Lady interrupts, requesting him to miss as many of the formal horrors as he can.", "가정부가 옛 방들을 차례로 지나며 덧문을 닫는 동안, 가고 없는 옛 데드록 가문 사람들의 초상화는 그저 풀이 죽은 채 축축한 벽 속으로 사라져가는 듯했다. 그 초상화들이 다음번 언제 다시 모습을 드러낼지는, 악마처럼 과거와 현재는 죄다 알아도 미래만은 알지 못하는 유행 소식통도 아직은 장담하지 못한다."]], [["Mr.", "레스터 데드록 경은 한낱 준남작에 지나지 않으나, 그보다 더 위세 당당한 준남작은 없다. 그의 가문은 언덕만큼이나 오래되었고, 그보다 한없이 더 존귀하다."], ["Tulkinghorn glances over his spectacles and begins again lower down.", "그는 세상이 언덕 없이도 그럭저럭 굴러갈 수는 있어도 데드록 가문 없이는 끝장이 나리라는 지론을 품고 있다. 그는 자연이라는 것이 대체로 괜찮은 발상이라고(정원 울타리로 둘러싸지 않았을 때는 좀 천박할지 몰라도) 인정은 하지만, 그 발상이 실현되려면 결국 자네 같은 대단한 지방 명문가들에 기대야 한다고 여긴다."], ["My Lady carelessly and scornfully abstracts her attention.", "그는 양심이 엄격한 신사로, 온갖 옹졸함과 비루함을 경멸하며, 제 고결함에 티끌만 한 흠집이라도 잡힐 빌미를 줄 바에는 당신이 어떤 죽음을 들먹이든 단 한 마디 통보만으로 기꺼이 그 죽음을 맞을 각오가 되어 있다. 그는 명예롭고, 고집스럽고, 정직하고, 기개가 드높으며, 지독하게 편견에 찌들었고, 더없이 비합리적인 사내다."], ["Sir Leicester in a great chair looks at the file and appears to have a stately liking for the legal repetitions and prolixities as ranging among the national bulwarks.", "레스터 경은 마님보다 꼬박 스무 살 위다. 그는 예순다섯을 다시는 보지 못할 것이요, 어쩌면 예순여섯도, 예순일곱도 그러할 것이다."], ["It happens that the fire is hot where my Lady sits and that the hand-screen is more beautiful than useful, being priceless but small.", "이따금 통풍이 도져서 걸음걸이가 조금 뻣뻣하다. 옅은 잿빛 머리칼과 구레나룻, 고운 셔츠 주름 장식, 새하얀 조끼, 그리고 늘 단단히 채워둔 번쩍이는 단추가 달린 푸른 외투를 갖춘 그는 풍채가 그럴듯하다."], ["My Lady, changing her position, sees the papers on the table—looks at them nearer—looks at them nearer still—asks impulsively, “Who copied that?”", "그는 격식을 차리고 위엄이 있으며, 어떤 자리에서든 마님에게 더없이 정중하고, 마님의 자태를 무엇보다 높이 친다. 구애하던 시절 이래 한 번도 변치 않은 마님을 향한 그의 헌신은, 그에게 남은 단 하나의 낭만적 정취다."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn stops short, surprised by my Lady’s animation and her unusual tone.", "사실 그는 사랑해서 마님과 결혼했다."], ["“Is it what you people call law-hand?”", "마님에게는 이렇다 할 가문조차 없었다는 수군거림이 아직도 떠돈다. 그러나 레스터 경은 가문이 차고 넘쳐서 어쩌면 더는 필요 없을 만큼, 얼마든지 그것을 양보할 수 있었던 것이다."], ["she asks, looking full at him in her careless way again and toying with her screen.", "그 대신 마님에게는 미모와 자존심과 야망, 오만한 결단, 그리고 명문가 귀부인 한 무리에게 나눠주고도 남을 만큼의 분별이 있었다. 여기에 재산과 지위가 더해지자 마님은 이내 떠올라, 이제 여러 해째 유행 소식통의 한복판이자 유행이라는 나무의 꼭대기에 자리해왔다."]], [["“Not quite. Probably”—Mr.", "정복할 세계가 더는 없자 알렉산드로스가 어떻게 울었는지는 누구나 안다."], ["Tulkinghorn examines it as he speaks—“the legal character which it has was acquired after the original hand was formed.", "아니, 이쯤 되면 다들 알 만한 까닭이 있는데, 그 일이 꽤 자주 입에 오르내려온 탓이다. 데드록 마님은 자신의 세계를 정복하고도 녹아내리는 기분이 아니라 차라리 얼어붙는 기분에 잠겨들었다."], ["Why do you ask?” “Anything to vary this detestable monotony.", "기진맥진한 평정, 닳고 닳은 차분함, 흥미나 만족 따위로는 흐트러지지 않는 권태로운 평온이 그녀가 거둔 승리의 전리품이다."], ["Oh, go on, do!”", "그녀는 더없이 잘 자란 사람이다. 내일 당장 천국으로 옮겨진다 해도, 그녀는 황홀경 하나 없이 올라가리라 짐작할 만하다."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn reads again.", "마님에게는 여전히 미모가 있고, 그 절정은 지났을지 몰라도 아직 가을에 접어들지는 않았다."], ["The heat is greater; my Lady screens her face. Sir Leicester dozes, starts up suddenly, and cries, “Eh?", "그녀는 고운 얼굴을 지녔는데, 본디 잘생겼다기보다는 아주 예쁘다고 할 만한 인상이었으나, 유행하는 신분에서 몸에 밴 표정 덕에 고전적인 기품으로 다듬어졌다."], ["What do you say?”", "자태는 우아하고 키가 커 보이는 효과를 낸다."], ["“I say I am afraid,” says Mr.", "실제로 큰 것은 아니고, 명예로운 밥 스테이블스가 여러 번 단언했다시피 \"그 모든 점이 한껏 살려진\""], ["Tulkinghorn, who had risen hastily, “that Lady Dedlock is ill.”", "까닭이다. 같은 권위자는 그녀가 빈틈없이 차려입었다고 평하며, 특히 머리 모양을 두고 온 마구간을 통틀어 가장 잘 손질된 암말이라고 칭찬한다."]], [["“Faint,” my Lady murmurs with white lips, “only that; but it is like the faintness of death.", "이 모든 완벽함을 머리에 인 채 데드록 마님은 (바짝 뒤쫓는 유행 소식통과 함께) 링컨셔의 별장에서 올라와 파리로 떠나기 전 시내 저택에서 며칠을 보낸다."], ["Don’t speak to me.", "마님은 파리에서 몇 주 머물 작정이며, 그 뒤 행보는 아직 정해지지 않았다."], ["Ring, and take me to my room!” Mr.", "그런데 이 질척하고 음침한 오후, 마님의 시내 저택에 한 구식 노신사가 찾아드는데, 그는 변호사이자 대법원 사무 변호사로, 데드록 가문의 법률 고문을 맡는 영예를 누리고 있다."], ["Tulkinghorn retires into another chamber; bells ring, feet shuffle and patter, silence ensues.", "그의 사무실에는 그 가문의 이름이 겉에 적힌 무쇠 상자가 어찌나 많은지, 마치 지금의 준남작이 요술쟁이의 동전이라도 되어 그 상자들 사이를 끊임없이 옮겨 다니며 묘기에 부려지는 것만 같다. 노신사는 분을 바른 머큐리의 안내를 받아, 현관을 가로질러 계단을 오르고 복도를 지나, 사교철에는 더없이 휘황찬란하지만 철 지나면 더없이 음산한 방들을 가로질러 마님 앞으로 인도된다."], ["Mercury at last begs Mr. Tulkinghorn to return.", "잠깐 구경하기에는 요정 나라요, 살기에는 사막인 방들이다."]], [["“Better now,” quoth Sir Leicester, motioning the lawyer to sit down and read to him alone. “I have been quite alarmed. I never knew my Lady swoon before. But the weather is extremely trying, and she really has been bored to death down at our place in Lincolnshire.”", "* 머큐리: 로마 신화의 전령신. 여기서는 심부름과 안내를 맡은 하인을 빗댄 표현. 노신사는 보기에 녹슨 듯 후줄근하지만, 귀족들의 혼인 재산 계약이며 유언장을 다루어 짭짤하게 재산을 모았고 무척 부유하다는 평판이 자자하다. 그를 둘러싸고는 가문의 비밀이라는 신비로운 후광이 감도는데, 그가 그 비밀을 말없이 간직하는 보관소라는 사실은 익히 알려져 있다. 정원의 외진 숲속, 자라나는 재목과 고사리 사이에 수백 년째 뿌리내린 고귀한 능묘들이 있지만, 그 안에 담긴 고귀한 비밀이 차라리 토킹혼 씨의 가슴속에 갇혀 세상을 돌아다니는 비밀보다 적을지도 모른다. 그는 이른바 옛 유파에 속하는 사람인데, 이 말은 대개 한 번도 젊었던 적이 없어 보이는 온갖 유파를 일컫는다. 그는 리본으로 묶은 반바지에 각반이나 긴 양말을 갖춰 입는다. 그의 검은 옷과 검은 양말은, 그것이 비단이든 모직이든, 결코 윤이 나지 않는다는 한 가지 특징이 있다. 말이 없고, 속을 닫아걸었으며, 어떤 스쳐 가는 빛에도 응답하지 않는 그의 옷차림은 꼭 그 사람을 닮았다. 그는 직무상 자문을 구하지 않으면 결코 입을 열지 않는다. 그는 이따금, 말 한마디 없으면서도 더없이 제집처럼 편안한 모습으로, 시골 대저택의 만찬 식탁 구석이나 응접실 문가에 자리해 있곤 하는데, 그런 응접실을 두고 유행 소식통은 입에 침이 마르도록 떠들어댄다. 그곳에서는 누구나 그를 알아보고, 귀족 명부에 오른 사람의 절반이 걸음을 멈추고 \"안녕하시오, 토킹혼 씨?\" 하고 인사를 건넨다. 그는 그 인사들을 진중하게 받아들여, 다른 모든 지식과 함께 깊이 묻어둔다."]], [["I have a great deal of difficulty in beginning to write my portion of these pages, for I know I am not clever. I always knew that. I can remember, when I was a very little girl indeed, I used to say to my doll when we were alone together, “Now, Dolly, I am not clever, you know very well, and you must be patient with me, like a dear!” And so she used to sit propped up in a great arm-chair, with her beautiful complexion and rosy lips, staring at me—or not so much at me, I think, as at nothing—while I busily stitched away and told her every one of my secrets.", "레스터 데드록 경은 마님과 함께 있다가 토킹혼 씨를 보고 반가워한다. 그에게는 오랜 관례가 몸에 밴 분위기가 있어, 레스터 경은 늘 그 점을 흡족하게 여기며 일종의 공물로 받아들인다. 그는 토킹혼 씨의 옷차림이 마음에 드는데, 거기에도 일종의 공물이 깃들어 있다. 더없이 점잖으면서, 또한 대체로 가신다운 분위기를 풍긴다. 말하자면 데드록 가문의 법률 비밀을 맡은 집사요, 법률 술 곳간을 지키는 시종 같은 인상을 자아낸다. 토킹혼 씨 자신은 이런 것을 짐작이나 할까? 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있다. 그러나 한 계급의 일원으로서, 자신의 작은 세계를 이끄는 우두머리이자 대표 가운데 하나로서, 데드록 마님과 관련된 온갖 것에는 주목할 만한 한 가지 사정이 있다. 그녀는 자신을 보통 인간의 손길과 분별이 도무지 닿지 못하는 헤아릴 수 없는 존재라 여긴다. 거울에 비친 자신을 보면 과연 그렇게 보이기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀를 둘러싸고 도는 희미한 작은 별들은, 하녀에서부터 이탈리아 오페라 극장의 지배인에 이르기까지, 하나같이 그녀의 약점과 편견과 어리석음과 오만함과 변덕을 꿰뚫고 있으며, 마치 양재사가 그녀의 몸 치수를 재듯 그녀의 됨됨이를 정확히 계산하고 곱게 가늠하며 살아간다. 새 옷이든, 새 관습이든, 새 가수든, 새 무용수든, 새 보석 양식이든, 새 난쟁이나 거인이든, 새 예배당이든, 그 무엇이든 하나 내세우려는가? 데드록 마님은 그저 자기 앞에 엎드릴 뿐이라 여기는 사람들이 열두 직종에 두루 있는데, 그 굽실대는 자들은 마님을 갓난아기 다루듯 주무르는 법을 일러줄 수 있고, 평생을 오로지 마님 시중드는 일로만 보내며, 한껏 겸손한 척 깊이 머리를 조아리며 따르는 시늉을 하면서 실은 마님과 그 무리 전체를 제 뒤로 줄줄이 끌고 다닌다. 하나를 낚으면 죄다 낚아채어, 레뮤얼 걸리버가 위풍당당한 릴리퍼트의 함대를 통째로 끌고 갔듯 몽땅 채어 가는 것이다. \"저희 손님들께 물건을 권하시려거든, 선생,\" 보석상 블레이즈와 스파클은 이렇게 말한다. 저희 손님이란 데드록 마님과 그 부류를 뜻한다. \"일반 대중을 상대하는 게 아니라는 걸 명심하셔야 합니다. 저희 손님들의 가장 약한 데를 찔러야 하는데, 그 약한 데가 바로 이런 곳이지요.\" \"이 물건을 잘 팔리게 하려면, 여러분,\" 포목상 신과 글로스는 제조업자 친구들에게 이렇게 말한다. \"저희한테 오셔야 합니다. 유행하는 분들을 어디서 잡아야 할지 저희가 알고, 저희가 그걸 유행으로 만들 수 있으니까요.\" \"이 책을 제 고귀한 단골들 탁자에 올리고 싶으시거든, 선생,\" 도서 대여업자 슬래더리 씨는 이렇게 말한다. \"아니면 이 난쟁이나 거인을 제 고귀한 단골들 댁에 들여보내고 싶으시거든, 선생, 또는 이 공연에 제 고귀한 단골들의 후원을 받게 하고 싶으시거든, 선생, 부디 그 일은 저한테 맡기십시오. 저는 제 고귀한 단골들의 우두머리를 연구하는 데 이골이 났으니, 자랑이 아니라 사실대로 말씀드리건대 그분들을 제 손가락 끝에서 마음대로 돌릴 수 있습니다.\" 정직한 사내인 슬래더리 씨의 이 말에는 조금도 과장이 없다."]], [["My dear old doll! I was such a shy little thing that I seldom dared to open my lips, and never dared to open my heart, to anybody else. It almost makes me cry to think what a relief it used to be to me when I came home from school of a day to run upstairs to my room and say, “Oh, you dear faithful Dolly, I knew you would be expecting me!” and then to sit down on the floor, leaning on the elbow of her great chair, and tell her all I had noticed since we parted. I had always rather a noticing way—not a quick way, oh, no!—a silent way of noticing what passed before me and thinking I should like to understand it better. I have not by any means a quick understanding. When I love a person very tenderly indeed, it seems to brighten. But even that may be my vanity.", "그러니 토킹혼 씨가 지금 데드록 마님의 마음속을 스쳐 가는 것이 무엇인지 모를 수도 있겠으나, 어쩌면 알고 있을 가능성도 다분하다. \"마님의 소송 건이 또 대법관 앞에 올랐다지요, 토킹혼 씨?\" 레스터 경이 손을 내밀며 말한다."]], [["I was brought up, from my earliest remembrance—like some of the princesses in the fairy stories, only I was not charming—by my godmother.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["At least, I only knew her as such. She was a good, good woman!", "오늘 또 다루어졌지요.\""], ["She went to church three times every Sunday, and to morning prayers on Wednesdays and Fridays, and to lectures whenever there were lectures; and never missed. She was handsome; and if she had ever smiled, would have been (I used to think) like an angel—but she never smiled.", "토킹혼 씨가 대답하며, 난롯가 소파에 앉아 손부채로 얼굴을 가리고 있는 마님에게 늘 하던 대로 조용히 고개를 숙인다."], ["She was always grave and strict. She was so very good herself, I thought, that the badness of other people made her frown all her life.", "\"무슨 진전이라도 있었느냐고 물어봐야 부질없겠지요.\""], ["I felt so different from her, even making every allowance for the differences between a child and a woman; I felt so poor, so trifling, and so far off that I never could be unrestrained with her—no, could never even love her as I wished. It made me very sorry to consider how good she was and how unworthy of her I was, and I used ardently to hope that I might have a better heart; and I talked it over very often with the dear old doll, but I never loved my godmother as I ought to have loved her and as I felt I must have loved her if I had been a better girl.", "마님이 링컨셔 별장의 그 황량함을 아직 떨치지 못한 채 말한다."]], [["This made me, I dare say, more timid and retiring than I naturally was and cast me upon Dolly as the only friend with whom I felt at ease.", "\"마님께서 진전이라 할 만한 일은 오늘 아무것도 없었습니다.\" 토킹혼 씨가 대답한다."], ["But something happened when I was still quite a little thing that helped it very much.", "\"앞으로도 영영 없겠지요.\" 마님이 말한다."]], [["I had never heard my mama spoken of.", "레스터 경은 끝도 없이 늘어지는 대법원 소송에 아무런 불만이 없다."], ["I had never heard of my papa either, but I felt more interested about my mama.", "그것은 느리고, 돈이 많이 들고, 영국적이며, 헌정 질서에 어울리는 그런 부류의 일이다. 물론 그가 문제의 소송에 절실한 이해관계가 있는 것은 아니다."], ["I had never worn a black frock, that I could recollect.", "마님이 그에게 가져온 유일한 재산이 바로 그 소송의 지분이었으니 말이다."], ["I had never been shown my mama’s grave. I had never been told where it was.", "게다가 그는 자기 이름, 곧 데드록이라는 이름이 어느 소송에 끼여 있으면서 정작 그 소송의 명칭에는 오르지 못한다는 것이 더없이 우스꽝스러운 사고라는 어렴풋한 느낌을 품고 있다."], ["Yet I had never been taught to pray for any relation but my godmother. I had more than once approached this subject of my thoughts with Mrs.", "그러나 그는 대법원이, 설령 이따금 정의를 지연시키고 약간의 혼란을 빚는다 하더라도, 인간의 지혜가 다다른 완벽함이 다른 온갖 것들과 더불어 고안해낸 것이라, (인간의 처지에서 말하자면) 만사를 영원히 매듭짓기 위한 무언가라고 여긴다."], ["Rachael, our only servant, who took my light away when I was in bed (another very good woman, but austere to me), and she had only said, “Esther, good night!”", "그리고 대체로 그는, 대법원을 두고 무슨 불평이든 거기에 자기 얼굴의 인준을 보태는 것은 곧 하층민 가운데 누군가가 어딘가에서 와트 타일러처럼 들고일어나도록 부추기는 일이라는 확고한 지론을 품고 있다."], ["and gone away and left me.", "* 와트 타일러: 1381년 잉글랜드 농민 봉기를 이끈 지도자."]], [["Although there were seven girls at the neighbouring school where I was a day boarder, and although they called me little Esther Summerson, I knew none of them at home.", "\"새로운 진술서가 몇 장 서류철에 더해진 데다,\" 토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["All of them were older than I, to be sure (I was the youngest there by a good deal), but there seemed to be some other separation between us besides that, and besides their being far more clever than I was and knowing much more than I did. One of them in the first week of my going to the school (I remember it very well) invited me home to a little party, to my great joy.", "\"그것이 짧고, 또 저는 소송에 새로운 진행이 생기면 송구하게도 의뢰인께 알려드린다는 번거로운 원칙을 따르는 데다\"—토킹혼 씨는 조심스러운 사람이라, 꼭 필요한 만큼만 책임을 진다—\"게다가 마님께서 파리로 가신다기에, 제가 주머니에 넣어 가져왔습니다.\""], ["But my godmother wrote a stiff letter declining for me, and I never went. I never went out at all.", "(그런데 레스터 경도 파리로 갈 참이었지만, 유행 소식통의 관심은 온통 마님에게 쏠려 있었다.)"]], [["It was my birthday. There were holidays at school on other birthdays—none on mine.", "토킹혼 씨가 서류를 꺼내 마님 팔꿈치 곁의 황금 부적 같은 탁자 위에 올려놓아도 되겠느냐고 청한 뒤, 안경을 쓰고 갓을 씌운 등불 빛에 의지해 읽기 시작한다."], ["There were rejoicings at home on other birthdays, as I knew from what I heard the girls relate to one another—there were none on mine.", "\"'대법원에서."], ["My birthday was the most melancholy day at home in the whole year.", "존 잔다이스와—'\""]], [["I have mentioned that unless my vanity should deceive me (as I know it may, for I may be very vain without suspecting it, though indeed I don’t), my comprehension is quickened when my affection is. My disposition is very affectionate, and perhaps I might still feel such a wound if such a wound could be received more than once with the quickness of that birthday.", "마님이 말을 가로막으며, 형식적인 끔찍한 대목은 되도록 많이 건너뛰어 달라고 청한다. 토킹혼 씨가 안경 너머로 흘끗 보더니, 한참 아랫부분부터 다시 읽기 시작한다. 마님은 무심하고 시큰둥하게 정신을 딴 데로 돌린다. 레스터 경은 커다란 안락의자에 앉아 서류철을 바라보며, 법률 특유의 되풀이와 장황함을 나라의 보루에 드는 것으로 여겨 위엄 있게 흐뭇해하는 듯하다. 마침 마님이 앉은 자리는 불기운이 뜨거운데, 손부채는 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼 귀하나 작아서 쓸모보다 아름다움이 앞선다. 마님이 자세를 바꾸다가 탁자 위의 서류를 본다. 조금 더 가까이 본다. 더 가까이 들여다본다. 그러고는 충동적으로 묻는다. \"이건 누가 베껴 쓴 거지요?\""]], [["Dinner was over, and my godmother and I were sitting at the table before the fire. The clock ticked, the fire clicked; not another sound had been heard in the room or in the house for I don’t know how long.", "토킹혼 씨가 마님의 활기와 평소답지 않은 어조에 놀라 말을 뚝 멈춘다."], ["I happened to look timidly up from my stitching, across the table at my godmother, and I saw in her face, looking gloomily at me, “It would have been far better, little Esther, that you had had no birthday, that you had never been born!”", "\"당신네가 법률체라고 부르는 글씨인가요?\" 마님이 다시 무심한 태도로 그를 정면으로 바라보며, 손부채를 만지작거리며 묻는다."]], [["I broke out crying and sobbing, and I said, “Oh, dear godmother, tell me, pray do tell me, did Mama die on my birthday?”", "\"꼭 그렇진 않습니다. 아마도\"—토킹혼 씨가 말하면서 그것을 살핀다—\"본래 글씨체가 잡힌 뒤에 법률체의 특징을 익힌 것 같군요. 왜 물으십니까?\" \"이 지긋지긋한 단조로움을 깨뜨릴 거라면 뭐든 좋아서요. 아, 어서 읽어요!\""]], [["“No,” she returned. “Ask me no more, child!”", "토킹혼 씨가 다시 읽는다. 열기는 한층 더해진다. 마님은 부채로 얼굴을 가린다. 레스터 경이 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가 별안간 화들짝 깨어 외친다. \"응? 뭐라고 했소?\" \"마님께서 편찮으신 듯하여 걱정입니다.\" 황급히 일어선 토킹혼 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Oh, do pray tell me something of her.", "\"현기증이에요.\" 마님이 핏기 없는 입술로 중얼거린다."], ["Do now, at last, dear godmother, if you please!", "\"그뿐이지만, 마치 죽음 같은 아찔함이군요. 말 걸지 마세요."], ["What did I do to her? How did I lose her?", "종을 울려서, 나를 방으로 데려다줘요!\""], ["Why am I so different from other children, and why is it my fault, dear godmother?", "토킹혼 씨가 다른 방으로 물러난다. 종이 울리고, 발소리가 이리저리 부산하게 오가더니, 이윽고 정적이 내려앉는다."], ["No, no, no, don’t go away. Oh, speak to me!”", "마침내 머큐리가 토킹혼 씨에게 돌아와 달라고 청한다."]], [["I was in a kind of fright beyond my grief, and I caught hold of her dress and was kneeling to her. She had been saying all the while, “Let me go!” But now she stood still.", "\"이제 좀 나아졌소.\" 레스터 경이 변호사에게 앉으라고 손짓하며, 자기 한 사람에게만 읽어달라고 이른다. \"내가 어찌나 놀랐는지. 마님이 정신을 잃는 건 처음 봤소. 허나 날씨가 워낙 사람을 못살게 구는 데다, 링컨셔 우리 별장에서 정말이지 지루해 죽을 뻔했으니 그럴 만도 하지.\" 이 책에서 내가 맡은 부분을 쓰기 시작하려니 여간 어려운 게 아닙니다. 나는 영리하지 못한 사람임을 알기 때문입니다. 그건 언제나 알고 있었습니다. 아주 어린 소녀였을 때, 나는 인형과 단둘이 있으면 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"있잖아, 인형아, 나는 영리하지 못해. 너도 잘 알지? 그러니까 착한 아이처럼 나를 너그럽게 참아주렴!\" 그러면 인형은 커다란 안락의자에 기대어 앉아, 그 고운 살결과 발그레한 입술로 나를 빤히 바라보았습니다. 아니, 나를 본다기보다는 허공을 보는 것 같았는데, 그동안 나는 부지런히 바느질하며 내 비밀을 하나도 빠짐없이 인형에게 털어놓았습니다."]], [["Her darkened face had such power over me that it stopped me in the midst of my vehemence. I put up my trembling little hand to clasp hers or to beg her pardon with what earnestness I might, but withdrew it as she looked at me, and laid it on my fluttering heart. She raised me, sat in her chair, and standing me before her, said slowly in a cold, low voice—I see her knitted brow and pointed finger—“Your mother, Esther, is your disgrace, and you were hers. The time will come—and soon enough—when you will understand this better and will feel it too, as no one save a woman can. I have forgiven her”—but her face did not relent—“the wrong she did to me, and I say no more of it, though it was greater than you will ever know—than any one will ever know but I, the sufferer. For yourself, unfortunate girl, orphaned and degraded from the first of these evil anniversaries, pray daily that the sins of others be not visited upon your head, according to what is written. Forget your mother and leave all other people to forget her who will do her unhappy child that greatest kindness. Now, go!”", "사랑하는 내 옛 인형! 나는 어찌나 수줍음이 많은 아이였던지, 다른 누구에게도 좀처럼 입을 열 엄두를 내지 못했고, 마음을 열 엄두는 더더욱 내지 못했습니다. 학교에서 집으로 돌아온 날, 내 방으로 한걸음에 뛰어 올라가 \"아, 사랑스럽고 믿음직한 인형아, 네가 나를 기다리고 있을 줄 알았어!\" 하고 말한 뒤, 인형의 커다란 의자 팔걸이에 기대어 바닥에 앉아, 헤어진 뒤로 내가 본 것을 죄다 들려주던 그 시간이 내게 얼마나 큰 위안이었는지 떠올리면 눈물이 날 지경입니다. 나는 늘 무언가를 눈여겨보는 편이었습니다. 빠릿빠릿한 게 아니라, 오, 천만에요! 그저 눈앞을 스쳐 가는 것을 말없이 지켜보며 그걸 더 잘 이해하면 좋겠다고 생각하는 식이었습니다. 나는 결코 이해가 빠른 사람이 아닙니다. 그래도 어떤 사람을 진심으로 다정하게 사랑할 때면 이해력이 환해지는 듯합니다. 하지만 그것마저 내 허영일지도 모릅니다. 내가 기억하는 가장 어린 시절부터, 나는 마치 동화 속 공주들처럼 대모님 손에 자랐습니다. 다만 나는 사랑스럽지 않았다는 점만 달랐지요. 적어도 나는 그분을 대모로만 알고 있었습니다. 정말, 정말 좋은 분이셨습니다! 일요일이면 세 번씩 교회에 가셨고, 수요일과 금요일에는 아침 기도회에, 설교가 있을 때면 언제든 설교를 들으러 가셨으며, 한 번도 거르지 않으셨습니다. 미인이셨고, 한 번이라도 미소 지으셨다면 (나는 늘 그렇게 생각했습니다) 천사 같으셨을 텐데, 그분은 결코 미소 짓지 않으셨습니다. 늘 엄숙하고 엄격하셨습니다. 그분 자신이 어찌나 선하신지, 다른 사람들의 악함 때문에 평생 미간을 찌푸리고 사신 거라고 나는 생각했습니다. 아이와 어른 사이의 차이를 아무리 너그럽게 감안해도, 나는 그분과 너무나 다르다고 느꼈습니다. 나는 너무나 보잘것없고, 너무나 하찮으며, 너무나 멀리 떨어진 존재 같아서 그분 앞에서는 도무지 마음을 놓을 수 없었습니다. 아니, 바라는 만큼 그분을 사랑할 수조차 없었습니다. 그분이 얼마나 선하신지, 내가 얼마나 그분께 어울리지 않는 존재인지를 생각하면 너무나 슬퍼서, 나는 내게 더 나은 마음씨가 깃들기를 간절히 바라곤 했습니다. 그 이야기를 사랑하는 옛 인형에게 자주자주 털어놓았지만, 나는 마땅히 사랑했어야 할 만큼, 더 나은 아이였다면 분명 사랑했을 만큼 대모님을 사랑하지 못했습니다."]], [["She checked me, however, as I was about to depart from her—so frozen as I was!—and added this, “Submission, self-denial, diligent work, are the preparations for a life begun with such a shadow on it. You are different from other children, Esther, because you were not born, like them, in common sinfulness and wrath. You are set apart.”", "이런 까닭에 나는 타고난 천성보다 더 소심하고 내성적인 아이가 되었던 듯하고, 마음 편히 지낼 수 있는 유일한 친구인 인형에게 더 매달리게 되었습니다. 그런데 아직 아주 어렸을 적, 그런 성향을 한층 더 부추긴 일이 하나 일어났습니다. 나는 우리 엄마에 관한 이야기를 들어본 적이 없었습니다. 우리 아빠에 관해서도 들어본 적이 없었지만, 엄마 쪽이 더 궁금했습니다. 내 기억으로 나는 검은 상복을 입어본 적이 없었습니다. 엄마 무덤을 본 적도 없었습니다. 그게 어디 있는지 들은 적도 없었습니다. 그런데도 나는 대모님 말고 다른 어떤 친척을 위해 기도하라고 배운 적이 없었습니다. 내가 잠자리에 들면 불을 꺼주던, 우리 집의 유일한 하녀인 레이철 부인에게 나는 마음속 이 문제를 한두 번 꺼낸 적이 있었습니다. 그분도 무척 좋은 분이었지만 내게는 엄격했는데, 그분은 그저 \"에스더, 잘 자거라!\" 하고는 자리를 떠나 나를 혼자 두고 가버렸습니다."]], [["I went up to my room, and crept to bed, and laid my doll’s cheek against mine wet with tears, and holding that solitary friend upon my bosom, cried myself to sleep. Imperfect as my understanding of my sorrow was, I knew that I had brought no joy at any time to anybody’s heart and that I was to no one upon earth what Dolly was to me.", "내가 통학생으로 다니던 이웃 학교에는 여자아이가 일곱 명 있었고, 그 애들은 나를 꼬마 에스더 서머슨이라고 불렀지만, 나는 그 애들 가운데 누구와도 집에서 어울리지 못했습니다. 물론 그 애들은 모두 나보다 나이가 많았고(나는 거기서 한참 어린 막내였습니다), 게다가 나보다 훨씬 영리하고 아는 것도 훨씬 많았지만, 그 차이 말고도 우리 사이에는 무언가 또 다른 거리가 있는 것 같았습니다. 그중 한 아이가 내가 학교에 다니기 시작한 첫 주에(아주 또렷이 기억합니다) 자기 집에서 여는 작은 모임에 나를 초대했는데, 나는 무척 기뻤습니다. 하지만 대모님이 나를 대신해 정중히 거절하는 딱딱한 편지를 써 보내셨고, 나는 끝내 가지 못했습니다. 나는 도무지 어디에도 나가지 않았습니다. 내 생일이었습니다. 다른 아이들의 생일에는 학교에 휴일이 있었지만, 내 생일에는 없었습니다. 다른 아이들의 생일에는 집에서 잔치가 벌어졌다고, 아이들이 서로 주고받는 이야기를 들어 알고 있었습니다. 하지만 내 생일에는 그런 게 없었습니다. 내 생일은 일 년 중 집에서 가장 우울한 날이었습니다."]], [["Dear, dear, to think how much time we passed alone together afterwards, and how often I repeated to the doll the story of my birthday and confided to her that I would try as hard as ever I could to repair the fault I had been born with (of which I confessedly felt guilty and yet innocent) and would strive as I grew up to be industrious, contented, and kind-hearted and to do some good to some one, and win some love to myself if I could. I hope it is not self-indulgent to shed these tears as I think of it. I am very thankful, I am very cheerful, but I cannot quite help their coming to my eyes.", "앞서 말했듯이, 내 허영이 나를 속이지 않는 한(나도 모르게 무척 허영심이 많을 수 있다는 건 알지만, 정말이지 그렇지는 않다고 믿습니다), 내 이해력은 애정이 깊어질 때 빨라집니다. 나는 천성이 무척 정이 많은 사람이라, 그 생일에 받았던 것 같은 상처를 한 번 이상 그렇게 빠르게 받을 수 있다면 지금도 그런 아픔을 느낄지 모릅니다. 저녁 식사가 끝나고, 대모님과 나는 난롯가 식탁에 앉아 있었습니다. 시계가 똑딱거리고, 불꽃이 탁탁 튀었습니다. 그밖에 다른 소리는 얼마나 오래도록인지 모르게 방 안에서도 집 안에서도 들리지 않았습니다. 나는 바느질하다 우연히 식탁 너머 대모님 쪽으로 머뭇머뭇 눈을 들었는데, 음울하게 나를 바라보는 그 얼굴에서 이런 말을 읽었습니다. \"꼬마 에스더, 너에게 생일 따위 없었더라면, 네가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면 훨씬 나았을 게다!\""]], [["There! I have wiped them away now and can go on again properly.", "나는 울음을 터뜨리며 흐느꼈고, 이렇게 말했습니다. \"오, 대모님, 말씀해 주세요, 제발 말씀해 주세요. 제 생일에 엄마가 돌아가셨나요?\" \"아니다.\" 대모님이 대답했습니다. \"더는 묻지 마라, 얘야!\""]], [["I felt the distance between my godmother and myself so much more after the birthday, and felt so sensible of filling a place in her house which ought to have been empty, that I found her more difficult of approach, though I was fervently grateful to her in my heart, than ever. I felt in the same way towards my school companions; I felt in the same way towards Mrs. Rachael, who was a widow; and oh, towards her daughter, of whom she was proud, who came to see her once a fortnight! I was very retired and quiet, and tried to be very diligent.", "\"오, 제발 엄마 이야기를 조금이라도 해주세요. 이제라도, 대모님, 부디 그래 주세요! 제가 엄마한테 무슨 짓을 했나요? 어쩌다 엄마를 잃었나요? 왜 저는 다른 아이들과 이렇게 다르고, 또 왜 그게 제 잘못인가요, 대모님? 아니에요, 아니에요, 가지 마세요. 오, 제게 말씀해 주세요!\" 나는 슬픔을 넘어선 어떤 두려움에 사로잡혀, 대모님의 옷자락을 붙잡고 그분 앞에 무릎을 꿇었습니다. 그러는 내내 대모님은 \"놓아라!\" 하고 말하던 참이었습니다. 하지만 이제 그분은 우뚝 멈춰 섰습니다."]], [["One sunny afternoon when I had come home from school with my books and portfolio, watching my long shadow at my side, and as I was gliding upstairs to my room as usual, my godmother looked out of the parlour-door and called me back. Sitting with her, I found—which was very unusual indeed—a stranger. A portly, important-looking gentleman, dressed all in black, with a white cravat, large gold watch seals, a pair of gold eye-glasses, and a large seal-ring upon his little finger.", "어두워진 그분의 얼굴에는 어찌나 큰 힘이 서려 있던지, 격해진 내 마음을 한복판에서 멈춰 세웠습니다. 나는 떨리는 작은 손을 들어 그분 손을 잡거나 있는 힘껏 간절히 용서를 빌려고 했지만, 그분이 나를 바라보자 손을 거두어 두근거리는 가슴에 갖다 댔습니다. 그분은 나를 일으켜 세우고 의자에 앉은 채 나를 자기 앞에 세워둔 뒤, 차갑고 낮은 목소리로 천천히 말했습니다. 찌푸린 미간과 곧추세운 손가락이 지금도 눈에 선합니다. \"에스더, 네 어머니는 네 수치이고, 너는 네 어머니의 수치였다. 머지않아, 충분히 빠른 때에, 너는 이걸 더 잘 깨닫게 되고, 또 여자만이 느낄 수 있는 방식으로 느끼게 될 게다. 나는 그 사람을 용서했다.\" 하지만 그분의 얼굴은 누그러지지 않았습니다. \"그 사람이 내게 저지른 잘못을 말이다. 그 잘못이 네가 영영 알 수 없을 만큼, 고통받은 나 말고는 그 누구도 영영 알 수 없을 만큼 컸지만, 나는 더는 그 일을 입에 담지 않으마. 너 자신으로 말하자면, 불행한 아이야, 이 사악한 기념일의 첫날부터 고아가 되고 천하게 떨어진 너는, 기록된 말씀대로 다른 이들의 죄가 네 머리 위에 임하지 않도록 날마다 기도하여라. 네 어머니를 잊고, 그 불행한 자식에게 가장 큰 친절을 베풀 사람들, 곧 네 어머니를 잊어줄 모든 이에게 그 사람을 잊도록 내버려 두어라. 자, 가거라!\" 그런데 그분은 내가 막 떠나려 할 때, 나는 그렇게나 얼어붙어 있었는데, 나를 멈춰 세우고 이 말을 덧붙였습니다. \"순종, 자기 부정, 부지런한 노동, 이것이 그런 그림자를 지고 시작된 삶을 위한 준비란다. 에스더, 너는 다른 아이들과 다르다. 너는 그 애들처럼 흔한 죄와 진노 가운데 태어나지 않았기 때문이다. 너는 따로 떼어 구별된 아이란다.\""]], [["“This,” said my godmother in an undertone, “is the child.”", "나는 내 방으로 올라가 살그머니 잠자리에 들어, 눈물에 젖은 인형의 뺨을 내 뺨에 갖다 댔고, 그 외로운 친구를 가슴에 꼭 안은 채 울다가 잠이 들었습니다."], ["Then she said in her naturally stern way of speaking, “This is Esther, sir.”", "내 슬픔을 온전히 이해하지는 못했지만, 나는 그동안 단 한 번도 누군가의 마음에 기쁨을 가져다준 적이 없으며, 인형이 내게 그런 존재인 것처럼 이 세상 누구에게도 그런 존재가 되어주지 못한다는 것만은 알았습니다."]], [["The gentleman put up his eye-glasses to look at me and said, “Come here, my dear!” He shook hands with me and asked me to take off my bonnet, looking at me all the while.", "아아, 그 뒤로 우리가 단둘이 얼마나 많은 시간을 함께 보냈는지, 내가 인형에게 내 생일 이야기를 얼마나 자주 되풀이했는지, 그리고 내가 타고난 잘못을(나는 분명 죄가 있으면서도 동시에 죄가 없다고 느꼈습니다) 있는 힘껏 바로잡으려 애쓰겠노라고, 자라면서 부지런하고 만족할 줄 알며 마음씨 고운 사람이 되어 누군가에게 작은 선을 베풀고 할 수만 있다면 내게도 사랑을 얻어내겠노라고 인형에게 얼마나 털어놓았는지를 떠올리면."], ["When I had complied, he said, “Ah!” and afterwards “Yes!”", "그 일을 생각하며 이렇게 눈물 흘리는 것이 자기 위안이 아니기를 바랍니다."], ["And then, taking off his eye-glasses and folding them in a red case, and leaning back in his arm-chair, turning the case about in his two hands, he gave my godmother a nod. Upon that, my godmother said, “You may go upstairs, Esther!”", "나는 무척 감사하고, 무척 즐겁지만, 눈가에 차오르는 눈물을 어쩔 수 없습니다."], ["And I made him my curtsy and left him.", "자! 이제 눈물을 다 닦았으니 다시 제대로 이어갈 수 있습니다."]], [["It must have been two years afterwards, and I was almost fourteen, when one dreadful night my godmother and I sat at the fireside.", "그 생일 이후 나는 대모님과 나 사이의 거리를 한층 더 절감했고, 비어 있어야 마땅했을 자리를 그분 집에서 차지하고 있다는 사실을 너무나 또렷이 의식해서, 마음속으로는 그분께 뜨겁게 감사하면서도 그분께 다가가기가 그 어느 때보다 더 어렵게 느껴졌습니다. 학교 친구들에게도 같은 마음이었고, 과부인 레이철 부인에게도 같은 마음이었습니다."], ["I was reading aloud, and she was listening.", "그리고 아, 그분이 자랑스러워하던, 보름에 한 번씩 그분을 보러 오던 그 딸에게도 그랬습니다!"], ["I had come down at nine o’clock as I always did to read the Bible to her, and was reading from St.", "나는 무척 내성적이고 조용했으며, 무척 부지런해지려고 애썼습니다. 어느 화창한 오후, 책과 화첩을 들고 학교에서 돌아와 옆으로 길게 드리운 내 그림자를 바라보며 늘 하던 대로 내 방으로 슬그머니 올라가던 참에, 대모님이 응접실 문밖으로 내다보며 나를 불러 세웠습니다."], ["John how our Saviour stooped down, writing with his finger in the dust, when they brought the sinful woman to him.", "그분 곁에는, 정말이지 매우 드문 일이었는데, 낯선 사람이 앉아 있었습니다. 풍채 좋고 위엄 있어 보이는 신사로, 온통 검은 옷차림에 흰 넥타이를 매고, 커다란 금시계 줄에 인장이 여럿 달려 있었으며, 금테 안경을 쓰고, 새끼손가락에는 커다란 인장 반지를 끼고 있었습니다."]], [["“So when they continued asking him, he lifted up himself and said unto them, ‘He that is without sin among you, let him first cast a stone at her!’”", "\"이 아이가.\" 대모님이 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다. \"그 아이입니다.\" 그러고는 본디 엄한 말투로 말했습니다. \"이 애가 에스더입니다, 선생님.\""]], [["I was stopped by my godmother’s rising, putting her hand to her head, and crying out in an awful voice from quite another part of the book, “‘Watch ye, therefore, lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping. And what I say unto you, I say unto all, Watch!’”", "신사는 안경을 들어 올려 나를 보더니 말했습니다. \"이리 오너라, 얘야!\" 그는 나와 악수하고는 보닛을 벗어보라고 했는데, 그러는 내내 나를 바라보았습니다. 내가 시키는 대로 하자 그는 \"아!\" 했고, 이어서 \"그래!\" 했습니다. 그러더니 안경을 벗어 빨간 안경집에 접어 넣고는 안락의자에 등을 기대고 두 손으로 안경집을 빙빙 돌리면서 대모님에게 고개를 까딱였습니다. 그러자 대모님이 말했습니다. \"위층으로 올라가도 좋다, 에스더!\" 나는 그에게 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 자리를 떴습니다. 그로부터 이 년쯤 지나 내가 거의 열네 살이 되었을 무렵, 어느 끔찍한 밤에 대모님과 나는 난롯가에 앉아 있었습니다. 나는 소리 내어 책을 읽었고 그분은 듣고 있었습니다. 나는 늘 그러듯 아홉 시에 내려와 그분께 성경을 읽어드리던 참이었는데, 마침 요한복음에서 사람들이 죄지은 여인을 우리 구주께 끌고 왔을 때, 구주께서 몸을 굽혀 손가락으로 흙바닥에 글을 쓰셨다는 대목을 읽고 있었습니다."]], [["In an instant, while she stood before me repeating these words, she fell down on the floor. I had no need to cry out; her voice had sounded through the house and been heard in the street.", "\"그들이 계속 묻거늘, 예수께서 일어나 그들에게 이르시되 '너희 중에 죄 없는 자가 먼저 그 여자에게 돌을 던지라!'\" 나는 읽기를 멈췄습니다. 대모님이 자리에서 일어나 손을 머리에 갖다 대더니, 책의 전혀 다른 대목을 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤기 때문입니다. \"'그러므로 너희는 깨어 있으라. 그가 홀연히 와서 너희가 자는 것을 보지 않게 하라. 내가 너희에게 하는 이 말을 모든 사람에게 하노니, 깨어 있으라!'\""]], [["She was laid upon her bed.", "그 말을 되뇌며 내 앞에 서 있던 그분이, 순식간에 바닥에 쓰러졌습니다. 내가 소리칠 필요도 없었습니다."], ["For more than a week she lay there, little altered outwardly, with her old handsome resolute frown that I so well knew carved upon her face.", "그분의 목소리는 온 집 안에 울려 퍼졌고 거리에서까지 들렸으니까요. 그분은 침대에 뉘어졌습니다."], ["Many and many a time, in the day and in the night, with my head upon the pillow by her that my whispers might be plainer to her, I kissed her, thanked her, prayed for her, asked her for her blessing and forgiveness, entreated her to give me the least sign that she knew or heard me.", "일주일이 넘도록 그분은 거기 누워 있었는데, 겉모습은 거의 그대로였고, 내가 그토록 잘 알던 그 옛 미인의 단호하게 찌푸린 표정이 얼굴에 새겨져 있었습니다. 낮에도 밤에도 몇 번이고, 나는 내 속삭임이 그분께 더 또렷이 들리도록 그분 곁 베개에 머리를 대고, 그분께 입 맞추고, 감사하고, 그분을 위해 기도하고, 축복과 용서를 빌고, 나를 알아보거나 들었다는 작은 표시라도 해달라고 간청했습니다."], ["No, no, no.", "아니, 아니, 아니었습니다."], ["Her face was immovable.", "그분의 얼굴은 미동도 없었습니다."], ["To the very last, and even afterwards, her frown remained unsoftened.", "마지막 순간까지, 아니 그 뒤에도, 찌푸린 표정은 끝내 풀리지 않았습니다."]], [["On the day after my poor good godmother was buried, the gentleman in black with the white neckcloth reappeared.", "가엾고 선하신 내 대모님이 묻힌 다음 날, 흰 목도리를 두른 검은 옷의 그 신사가 다시 나타났습니다."], ["I was sent for by Mrs. Rachael, and found him in the same place, as if he had never gone away.", "레이철 부인이 나를 부르러 보냈고, 나는 그가 전과 똑같은 자리에, 마치 한 번도 떠난 적이 없었던 듯 앉아 있는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["“My name is Kenge,”", "\"내 이름은 켄지란다.\" 그가 말했습니다."], ["he said; “you may remember it, my child; Kenge and Carboy, Lincoln’s Inn.”", "\"기억할지도 모르겠구나, 얘야. 링컨스 인의 켄지 앤드 카보이일세.\""]], [["I replied that I remembered to have seen him once before.", "나는 전에 한 번 그를 뵌 기억이 난다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“Pray be seated—here near me.", "\"자, 앉으렴, 내 곁에 가까이."], ["Don’t distress yourself; it’s of no use.", "너무 상심하지 마라. 그래봐야 소용없으니."], ["Mrs. Rachael, I needn’t inform you who were acquainted with the late Miss Barbary’s affairs, that her means die with her and that this young lady, now her aunt is dead—”", "레이철 부인, 작고하신 바버리 양의 사정을 잘 아셨으니 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없겠지만, 그분의 재산은 그분과 함께 소멸하였고, 이제 이모가 세상을 떠난 이 아가씨는—\""]], [["“My aunt, sir!”", "\"제 이모라고요, 선생님!\""]], [["“It is really of no use carrying on a deception when no object is to be gained by it,”", "\"아무 소득도 없는 마당에 거짓을 이어가봐야 정말 소용없는 일이지.\" 켄지 씨가 부드럽게 말했습니다."], ["said Mr. Kenge smoothly, “Aunt in fact, though not in law.", "\"법적으로는 아니지만, 사실상 이모란다."], ["Don’t distress yourself!", "너무 상심하지 마라!"], ["Don’t weep!", "울지 말고!"], ["Don’t tremble!", "떨지도 말고!"], ["Mrs. Rachael, our young friend has no doubt heard of—the—a—Jarndyce and Jarndyce.”", "레이철 부인, 우리 어린 친구는 분명—그—저—잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에 대해 들어본 적이 있겠지요.\""]], [["“Never,”", "\"전혀요.\""], ["said Mrs. Rachael.", "레이철 부인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Is it possible,” pursued Mr.", "\"그럴 수가 있나요.\""], ["Kenge, putting up his eye-glasses, “that our young friend—I BEG you won’t distress yourself!—never heard of Jarndyce and Jarndyce!”", "켄지 씨가 안경을 들어 올리며 말을 이었습니다. \"우리 어린 친구가—제발 상심하지 말라니까!—잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 한 번도 들어본 적이 없다니!\""]], [["I shook my head, wondering even what it was.", "나는 그게 대체 무엇인지 의아해하며 고개를 가로저었습니다."]], [["“Not of Jarndyce and Jarndyce?” said Mr.", "\"잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 모른다고?\""], ["Kenge, looking over his glasses at me and softly turning the case about and about as if he were petting something.", "켄지 씨가 안경 너머로 나를 바라보며, 마치 무언가를 어르듯 안경집을 부드럽게 빙빙 돌리면서 말했습니다."], ["“Not of one of the greatest Chancery suits known?", "\"알려진 가장 위대한 대법원 소송 가운데 하나를 모른다고?"], ["Not of Jarndyce and Jarndyce—the—a—in itself a monument of Chancery practice.", "그 자체로 대법원 관행의 기념비인—그—저—잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 말이다."], ["In which (I would say) every difficulty, every contingency, every masterly fiction, every form of procedure known in that court, is represented over and over again?", "그 안에는 (말하자면) 그 법정에 알려진 온갖 난제, 온갖 우연한 사정, 온갖 절묘한 의제(擬制), 온갖 절차 형식이 거듭거듭 펼쳐져 있단다."], ["It is a cause that could not exist out of this free and great country.", "이건 이 자유롭고 위대한 나라가 아니고서는 존재할 수 없는 소송이지."], ["I should say that the aggregate of costs in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, Mrs. Rachael”—I was afraid he addressed himself to her because I appeared inattentive—“amounts at the present hour to from SIX-ty to SEVEN-ty THOUSAND POUNDS!”", "내가 보기에 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에 들어간 비용을 다 합치면, 레이철 부인\"—내가 건성으로 듣는 듯 보여서 그분께 말을 돌린 모양이었습니다—\"지금 이 순간 육만에서 칠만 파운드에 이른답니다!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge, leaning back in his chair.", "켄지 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했습니다."]], [["I felt very ignorant, but what could I do?", "나는 내가 무척 무지하다고 느꼈지만, 어쩔 도리가 있었겠습니까?"], ["I was so entirely unacquainted with the subject that I understood nothing about it even then.", "나는 그 주제를 워낙 까맣게 몰라서 그때조차 아무것도 이해하지 못했습니다."]], [["“And she really never heard of the cause!”", "\"그런데 이 아이가 정말로 그 소송을 한 번도 들어본 적이 없다니!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Surprising!”", "\"놀랍군요!\""]], [["“Miss Barbary, sir,” returned Mrs.", "\"바버리 양은요, 선생님.\""], ["Rachael, “who is now among the Seraphim—”", "레이철 부인이 대답했습니다. \"지금은 천사들 가운데 계시지만—\""]], [["“I hope so, I am sure,”", "\"부디 그러기를 바랍니다, 진심으로.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge politely.", "켄지 씨가 정중히 말했습니다."]], [["“—Wished Esther only to know what would be serviceable to her.", "\"—에스더에게 그저 살아가는 데 도움 될 것만 알게 하기를 바라셨습니다."], ["And she knows, from any teaching she has had here, nothing more.”", "그래서 이 아이가 여기서 배운 것 말고는 더 아는 게 없습니다.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"음!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Upon the whole, very proper.", "\"전체적으로 매우 적절하군요."], ["Now to the point,”", "자, 이제 본론으로.\""], ["addressing me.", "그가 나를 향해 말했습니다."], ["“Miss Barbary, your sole relation (in fact that is, for I am bound to observe that in law you had none) being deceased and it naturally not being to be expected that Mrs. Rachael—”", "\"너의 유일한 친척인(사실 그렇다는 거다, 법적으로는 친척이 없었다는 점을 밝혀야 하니까) 바버리 양이 작고하셨고, 또 당연하게도 레이철 부인이—\""]], [["“Oh, dear no!”", "\"아, 천만에요!\""], ["said Mrs. Rachael quickly.", "레이철 부인이 얼른 말했습니다."]], [["“Quite so,” assented Mr.", "\"바로 그렇지요.\""], ["Kenge; “—that Mrs.", "켄지 씨가 맞장구쳤습니다."], ["Rachael should charge herself with your maintenance and support (I beg you won’t distress yourself), you are in a position to receive the renewal of an offer which I was instructed to make to Miss Barbary some two years ago and which, though rejected then, was understood to be renewable under the lamentable circumstances that have since occurred.", "\"—레이철 부인이 너를 부양하고 돌보는 일을 떠맡으리라고는 기대할 수 없으니(제발 상심하지 마라), 너는 이제 한 가지 제안을 새로이 받을 처지가 되었단다. 그건 한 이 년 전 내가 바버리 양께 전하라는 지시를 받았던 제안인데, 그때는 거절당했지만 그 뒤로 벌어진 가슴 아픈 사정 아래 다시 제안할 수 있는 것으로 양해되어 있었지."], ["Now, if I avow that I represent, in Jarndyce and Jarndyce and otherwise, a highly humane, but at the same time singular, man, shall I compromise myself by any stretch of my professional caution?” said Mr.", "자, 내가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에서, 또 그밖의 일에서, 더없이 인정 많으면서도 동시에 별난 한 분을 대리하고 있다고 밝힌다면, 직업상의 신중함을 어느 정도 늘여본들 내 입장을 곤란하게 만들 일이야 없겠지?\""], ["Kenge, leaning back in his chair again and looking calmly at us both.", "켄지 씨가 다시 의자에 등을 기대고 우리 둘을 차분히 바라보며 말했습니다."]], [["He appeared to enjoy beyond everything the sound of his own voice.", "그는 무엇보다도 자기 목소리를 듣는 일을 즐기는 듯했습니다."], ["I couldn’t wonder at that, for it was mellow and full and gave great importance to every word he uttered.", "그럴 만도 했던 것이, 그 목소리는 부드럽고 풍성해서 그가 내뱉는 한 마디 한 마디에 대단한 무게를 실어주었으니까요."], ["He listened to himself with obvious satisfaction and sometimes gently beat time to his own music with his head or rounded a sentence with his hand.", "그는 만족스러운 기색이 역력한 채로 제 목소리에 귀 기울였고, 때로는 고개를 까딱여 제 가락에 맞춰 부드럽게 박자를 맞추기도 하고, 손짓으로 문장을 둥글게 맺기도 했습니다."], ["I was very much impressed by him—even then, before I knew that he formed himself on the model of a great lord who was his client and that he was generally called Conversation Kenge.", "나는 그에게 깊은 인상을 받았습니다. 그가 제 의뢰인인 어느 위대한 귀족을 본떠 자기를 꾸민다는 것도, 그가 흔히 '입심꾼 켄지'라고 불린다는 것도 미처 알기 전인 그때조차 말입니다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"잔다이스 씨는.\""], ["Jarndyce,”", "그가 말을 이었습니다."], ["he pursued, “being aware of the—I would say, desolate—position of our young friend, offers to place her at a first-rate establishment where her education shall be completed, where her comfort shall be secured, where her reasonable wants shall be anticipated, where she shall be eminently qualified to discharge her duty in that station of life unto which it has pleased—shall I say Providence?—to call her.”", "\"우리 어린 친구의—말하자면, 외로운—처지를 알고서, 너를 일류 학교에 보내주겠다고 하신단다. 거기서 네 교육이 완성되고, 네 안락이 보장되며, 네 합당한 필요가 미리 채워지고, 또 거기서 너는—섭리라고 해야 할까?—그 부르심에 따라 마땅히 놓인 인생의 자리에서 네 의무를 훌륭히 다할 수 있는 자질을 갖추게 될 거란다.\""]], [["My heart was filled so full, both by what he said and by his affecting manner of saying it, that I was not able to speak, though I tried.", "그가 한 말과 그것을 말하는 감동적인 태도에 내 가슴이 어찌나 가득 차오르던지, 나는 애써 보았지만 입을 열 수가 없었습니다."]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 씨는.\""], ["he went on, “makes no condition beyond expressing his expectation that our young friend will not at any time remove herself from the establishment in question without his knowledge and concurrence.", "그가 계속 말했습니다. \"우리 어린 친구가 언제든 그분의 양해와 동의 없이는 그 학교를 떠나지 않으리라는 기대를 밝히는 것 말고는 아무 조건도 달지 않으신단다."], ["That she will faithfully apply herself to the acquisition of those accomplishments, upon the exercise of which she will be ultimately dependent. That she will tread in the paths of virtue and honour, and—the—a——so forth.”", "또 우리 친구가 종국에는 그것에 기대어 살아가야 할 여러 소양을 익히는 데 성실히 매진하리라는 것, 미덕과 명예의 길을 걸으리라는 것, 그리고—그—저—기타 등등 말이다.\""]], [["I was still less able to speak than before.", "나는 전보다 더더욱 입을 열 수가 없었습니다."]], [["“Now, what does our young friend say?”", "\"자, 우리 어린 친구는 뭐라고 하려나?\""], ["proceeded Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말을 이었습니다."], ["“Take time, take time!", "\"천천히, 천천히 하렴!"], ["I pause for her reply.", "네 대답을 기다리마."], ["But take time!”", "하지만 천천히 하렴!\""]], [["What the destitute subject of such an offer tried to say, I need not repeat.", "그런 제안을 받은 가엾고 빈털터리인 아이가 무슨 말을 하려 했는지는 굳이 되풀이할 필요가 없겠습니다."], ["What she did say, I could more easily tell, if it were worth the telling.", "그 아이가 실제로 무슨 말을 했는지는, 이야기할 가치가 있다면 더 쉽게 전할 수 있겠습니다."], ["What she felt, and will feel to her dying hour, I could never relate.", "그 아이가 무엇을 느꼈는지, 그리고 죽는 날까지 무엇을 느낄지는 도무지 풀어낼 수가 없습니다."]], [["This interview took place at Windsor, where I had passed (as far as I knew) my whole life.", "이 면담은 윈저에서 이루어졌는데, 내가 아는 한 나는 그곳에서 평생을 보낸 셈이었습니다."], ["On that day week, amply provided with all necessaries, I left it, inside the stagecoach, for Reading.", "그날부터 한 주 뒤, 필요한 것을 넉넉히 챙겨, 나는 역마차 안에 앉아 그곳을 떠나 레딩으로 향했습니다."]], [["Mrs. Rachael was too good to feel any emotion at parting, but I was not so good, and wept bitterly.", "레이철 부인은 너무나 선해서 이별에 아무런 감정도 느끼지 않았지만, 나는 그만큼 선하지 못해 서럽게 울었습니다."], ["I thought that I ought to have known her better after so many years and ought to have made myself enough of a favourite with her to make her sorry then.", "그 오랜 세월을 함께했으니 나는 그분을 더 잘 알았어야 했고, 그분이 나와 헤어지는 걸 못내 아쉬워할 만큼 그분께 사랑받는 아이가 되었어야 했다고 생각했습니다."], ["When she gave me one cold parting kiss upon my forehead, like a thaw-drop from the stone porch—it was a very frosty day—I felt so miserable and self-reproachful that I clung to her and told her it was my fault, I knew, that she could say good-bye so easily!", "그분이 차가운 작별의 입맞춤을 내 이마에 한 번 해주었을 때—마치 돌 현관에서 떨어지는 해빙의 물방울 같았는데, 몹시 서리가 내린 날이었습니다—나는 너무나 비참하고 자책감이 들어 그분께 매달리며, 이렇게 쉽게 작별 인사를 하실 수 있는 건 다 제 잘못인 줄 안다고 말했습니다!"]], [["“No, Esther!”", "\"아니다, 에스더!\""], ["she returned.", "그분이 대답했습니다."], ["“It is your misfortune!”", "\"그건 네 불행이지!\""]], [["The coach was at the little lawn-gate—we had not come out until we heard the wheels—and thus I left her, with a sorrowful heart.", "마차는 작은 잔디밭 문 앞에 와 있었습니다. 우리는 바퀴 소리가 들리고서야 나왔던 것입니다."], ["She went in before my boxes were lifted to the coach-roof and shut the door.", "그렇게 나는 슬픈 마음으로 그분을 떠났습니다. 그분은 내 짐가방이 마차 지붕에 실리기도 전에 안으로 들어가 문을 닫아버렸습니다."], ["As long as I could see the house, I looked back at it from the window through my tears. My godmother had left Mrs.", "나는 집이 보이는 동안 내내 눈물을 흘리며 창밖으로 그 집을 돌아보았습니다."], ["Rachael all the little property she possessed; and there was to be a sale; and an old hearth-rug with roses on it, which always seemed to me the first thing in the world I had ever seen, was hanging outside in the frost and snow.", "대모님은 자신이 가진 자그마한 재산을 모두 레이철 부인에게 남겼고, 곧 매각이 있을 예정이었습니다. 그리고 장미꽃 무늬가 있는, 내가 이 세상에서 가장 처음 본 물건이라고 늘 여기던 낡은 난로 깔개가, 서리와 눈을 맞으며 바깥에 걸려 있었습니다."], ["A day or two before, I had wrapped the dear old doll in her own shawl and quietly laid her—I am half ashamed to tell it—in the garden-earth under the tree that shaded my old window.", "하루이틀 전, 나는 사랑하는 옛 인형을 그 애의 숄로 감싸서, 말하기가 좀 부끄럽지만, 내 옛 창문에 그늘을 드리우던 나무 아래 정원 흙 속에 가만히 묻어주었습니다."], ["I had no companion left but my bird, and him I carried with me in his cage.", "이제 내게 남은 동무라곤 새 한 마리뿐이었고, 나는 새장에 든 그 새를 데리고 떠났습니다."]], [["When the house was out of sight, I sat, with my bird-cage in the straw at my feet, forward on the low seat to look out of the high window, watching the frosty trees, that were like beautiful pieces of spar, and the fields all smooth and white with last night’s snow, and the sun, so red but yielding so little heat, and the ice, dark like metal where the skaters and sliders had brushed the snow away.", "집이 시야에서 사라지자, 나는 발치 짚더미에 새장을 놓고, 높다란 창밖을 내다보려고 낮은 좌석 앞쪽으로 앉아, 아름다운 정동석 조각 같은 서리 맞은 나무들과, 간밤에 내린 눈으로 매끄럽고 하얗게 덮인 들판과, 새빨갛지만 온기는 거의 내주지 않는 해와, 스케이트 타는 사람과 미끄럼 타는 사람이 눈을 쓸어낸 자리마다 쇠붙이처럼 거뭇한 얼음을 바라보았습니다."], ["There was a gentleman in the coach who sat on the opposite seat and looked very large in a quantity of wrappings, but he sat gazing out of the other window and took no notice of me.", "마차 안에는 신사 한 명이 맞은편 좌석에 앉아 있었는데, 잔뜩 껴입은 옷가지 때문에 무척 커 보였습니다. 하지만 그는 반대쪽 창밖만 응시할 뿐 나에게는 눈길도 주지 않았습니다."]], [["I thought of my dead godmother, of the night when I read to her, of her frowning so fixedly and sternly in her bed, of the strange place I was going to, of the people I should find there, and what they would be like, and what they would say to me, when a voice in the coach gave me a terrible start.", "나는 돌아가신 대모님을, 그분께 책을 읽어드리던 그 밤을, 침상에서 그토록 굳게 또 엄하게 미간을 찌푸리던 그분을, 내가 가고 있는 그 낯선 곳을, 거기서 만날 사람들과 그들이 어떤 모습일지, 그들이 내게 뭐라고 할지를 생각하고 있었는데, 그때 마차 안에서 들려온 목소리가 나를 소스라치게 놀라게 했습니다."]], [["It said, “What the de-vil are you crying for?”", "그 목소리가 말했습니다. \"대체 뭣 때문에 우는 거냐?\""]], [["I was so frightened that I lost my voice and could only answer in a whisper, “Me, sir?”", "나는 어찌나 놀랐던지 목소리가 나오지 않아 그저 속삭이듯 대답할 수밖에 없었습니다. \"저 말인가요, 선생님?\""], ["For of course I knew it must have been the gentleman in the quantity of wrappings, though he was still looking out of his window.", "물론 잔뜩 껴입은 그 신사일 수밖에 없다는 건 알고 있었습니다. 그는 여전히 창밖을 내다보고 있었지만요."]], [["“Yes, you,”", "\"그래, 너 말이다.\""], ["he said, turning round.", "그가 몸을 돌리며 말했습니다."]], [["“I didn’t know I was crying, sir,”", "\"저는 울고 있는 줄 몰랐어요, 선생님.\""], ["I faltered.", "나는 더듬거렸습니다."]], [["“But you are!”", "\"하지만 울고 있잖니!\""], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했습니다."], ["“Look here!”", "\"이걸 보렴!\""], ["He came quite opposite to me from the other corner of the coach, brushed one of his large furry cuffs across my eyes (but without hurting me), and showed me that it was wet.", "그는 마차 다른 구석에서 내 바로 맞은편으로 다가오더니, 커다란 털 소맷부리 하나로 내 눈가를 (아프지 않게) 쓱 닦아내고는, 그게 젖었다는 걸 보여주었습니다."]], [["“There!", "\"자!"], ["Now you know you are,” he said.", "이제 알겠지, 네가 울고 있다는 걸.\""], ["“Don’t you?”", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“Yes, sir,” I said.", "\"안 그러냐?\""]], [["“And what are you crying for?” said the gentleman, “Don’t you want to go there?”", "\"네, 선생님.\" 내가 말했습니다. \"그래, 뭣 때문에 우는 거냐?\" 신사가 말했습니다. \"거기 가기 싫은 게냐?\""]], [["“Where, sir?”", "\"어디요, 선생님?\""]], [["“Where?", "\"어디라니?"], ["Why, wherever you are going,”", "아니, 네가 가는 데가 어디든 말이다.\""], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했습니다."]], [["“I am very glad to go there, sir,”", "\"저는 거기 가게 되어 무척 기뻐요, 선생님.\""], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했습니다."]], [["“Well, then!", "\"그럼 됐지!"], ["Look glad!”", "기쁜 표정을 지어 보렴!\""], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했습니다."]], [["I thought he was very strange, or at least that what I could see of him was very strange, for he was wrapped up to the chin, and his face was almost hidden in a fur cap with broad fur straps at the side of his head fastened under his chin; but I was composed again, and not afraid of him.", "나는 그가 무척 이상한 사람이라고, 적어도 내가 볼 수 있는 그의 모습이 무척 이상하다고 생각했습니다. 그는 턱까지 꽁꽁 싸매고 있었고, 얼굴은 양옆에 넓은 털 끈이 달려 턱밑에서 여민 털모자에 거의 가려져 있었으니까요."], ["So I told him that I thought I must have been crying because of my godmother’s death and because of Mrs.", "하지만 나는 다시 마음을 가라앉혔고, 그가 두렵지 않았습니다."], ["Rachael’s not being sorry to part with me.", "그래서 나는 대모님의 죽음 때문에, 또 레이철 부인이 나와 헤어지면서도 아쉬워하지 않으셨기 때문에 운 모양이라고 말했습니다."]], [["“Confound Mrs. Rachael!”", "\"레이철 부인 따위는 빌어먹을!\""], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했습니다."], ["“Let her fly away in a high wind on a broomstick!”", "\"거센 바람 부는 날에 빗자루나 타고 날아가버리라지!\""]], [["I began to be really afraid of him now and looked at him with the greatest astonishment.", "나는 이제 정말로 그가 무서워지기 시작해서, 한껏 놀란 눈으로 그를 바라보았습니다."], ["But I thought that he had pleasant eyes, although he kept on muttering to himself in an angry manner and calling Mrs. Rachael names.", "그래도 그가 화난 듯 혼자 중얼거리며 레이철 부인을 욕하긴 했지만, 눈매만은 다정해 보인다고 생각했습니다."]], [["After a little while he opened his outer wrapper, which appeared to me large enough to wrap up the whole coach, and put his arm down into a deep pocket in the side.", "잠시 뒤 그는 겉옷을 풀어 헤쳤는데, 내가 보기에 그건 마차 전체를 감싸고도 남을 만큼 큼지막했습니다. 그러고는 옆구리의 깊은 주머니에 손을 쑥 집어넣었습니다."]], [["“Now, look here!”", "\"자, 이걸 보렴!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“In this paper,”", "\"이 종이 안에는\""], ["which was nicely folded, “is a piece of the best plum-cake that can be got for money—sugar on the outside an inch thick, like fat on mutton chops.", "하고 가리킨 것은 깔끔하게 접힌 종이였습니다. \"돈으로 살 수 있는 가장 좋은 건포도 케이크 한 조각이 들어 있단다."], ["Here’s a little pie (a gem this is, both for size and quality), made in France.", "겉에 설탕이 손가락 한 마디나 두껍게 발렸는데, 꼭 양고기 토막에 붙은 비계 같지. 여기 작은 파이도 있다(이건 크기로 보나 질로 보나 보석 같은 물건이야)."], ["And what do you suppose it’s made of?", "프랑스에서 만든 거란다. 이게 뭘로 만든 건지 알겠니?"], ["Livers of fat geese.", "살진 거위 간이지."], ["There’s a pie!", "이런 파이라니!"], ["Now let’s see you eat ’em.”", "자, 어디 네가 먹는 걸 좀 보자꾸나.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님.\" 내가 대답했습니다."], ["I replied; “thank you very much indeed, but I hope you won’t be offended—they are too rich for me.”", "\"정말 무척 고맙지만, 부디 언짢게 여기지 말아 주세요. 저한테는 너무 기름진 음식이에요.\""]], [["“Floored again!”", "\"또 한 방 먹었군!\""], ["said the gentleman, which I didn’t at all understand, and threw them both out of window.", "신사가 말했는데, 나는 그 말을 도무지 알아들을 수 없었습니다. 그러고는 두 가지를 다 창밖으로 던져버렸습니다."]], [["He did not speak to me any more until he got out of the coach a little way short of Reading, when he advised me to be a good girl and to be studious, and shook hands with me.", "그는 레딩에 조금 못 미친 곳에서 마차를 내리기 전까지 더는 내게 말을 걸지 않았습니다. 내릴 때 그는 착한 아이가 되고 공부에 힘쓰라고 당부하며 나와 악수했습니다."], ["I must say I was relieved by his departure.", "솔직히 나는 그가 떠나서 마음이 놓였습니다."], ["We left him at a milestone.", "우리는 어느 이정표 앞에서 그를 내려주었습니다."], ["I often walked past it afterwards, and never for a long time without thinking of him and half expecting to meet him.", "그 뒤로 나는 자주 그 이정표 곁을 걸어 지났는데, 오랫동안 그곳을 지날 때마다 그를 떠올리며 혹시 그를 만나지 않을까 은근히 기대하곤 했습니다."], ["But I never did; and so, as time went on, he passed out of my mind.", "하지만 끝내 만나지 못했고, 그렇게 세월이 흐르면서 그는 내 기억에서 사라져갔습니다."]], [["When the coach stopped, a very neat lady looked up at the window and said, “Miss Donny.”", "마차가 멈추자, 무척 단정한 부인 한 분이 창문을 올려다보며 말했습니다. \"도니 양이지요.\""]], [["“No, ma’am, Esther Summerson.”", "\"아니요, 부인. 에스더 서머슨인데요.\""]], [["“That is quite right,”", "\"바로 맞아요.\""], ["said the lady, “Miss Donny.”", "부인이 말했습니다. \"도니 양 말이에요.\""]], [["I now understood that she introduced herself by that name, and begged Miss Donny’s pardon for my mistake, and pointed out my boxes at her request.", "나는 그제야 그분이 자기를 그 이름으로 소개한 것임을 알아차리고, 착각했던 것을 도니 양께 사과한 뒤, 부탁에 따라 내 짐가방을 가리켜 보였습니다."], ["Under the direction of a very neat maid, they were put outside a very small green carriage; and then Miss Donny, the maid, and I got inside and were driven away.", "무척 단정한 하녀의 지시에 따라 가방은 아주 자그마한 초록색 마차 바깥쪽에 실렸습니다. 그러고 나서 도니 양과 하녀와 나는 안에 올라타 마차를 타고 떠났습니다."]], [["“Everything is ready for you, Esther,”", "\"너를 위해 모든 게 준비되어 있단다, 에스더.\" 도니 양이 말했습니다."], ["said Miss Donny, “and the scheme of your pursuits has been arranged in exact accordance with the wishes of your guardian, Mr. Jarndyce.”", "\"네 학업 계획은 네 후견인이신 잔다이스 씨의 뜻에 꼭 맞게 짜여 있어.\""]], [["“Of—did you say, ma’am?”", "\"제—뭐라고 하셨어요, 부인?\""]], [["“Of your guardian, Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"네 후견인이신 잔다이스 씨 말이다.\""], ["said Miss Donny.", "도니 양이 말했습니다."]], [["I was so bewildered that Miss Donny thought the cold had been too severe for me and lent me her smelling-bottle.", "나는 어찌나 어리둥절했던지, 도니 양은 추위가 내게 너무 혹독했던 모양이라 여기고 자기 각성제 병을 빌려주었습니다."]], [["“Do you know my—guardian, Mr. Jarndyce, ma’am?”", "\"제—후견인이신 잔다이스 씨를 아세요, 부인?\""], ["I asked after a good deal of hesitation.", "나는 한참 머뭇거린 끝에 물었습니다."]], [["“Not personally, Esther,”", "\"직접 아는 건 아니란다, 에스더.\""], ["said Miss Donny; “merely through his solicitors, Messrs.", "도니 양이 말했습니다. \"그저 런던의 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소를 통해서지."], ["Kenge and Carboy, of London. A very superior gentleman, Mr.", "켄지 씨, 참으로 훌륭한 신사야."], ["Kenge. Truly eloquent indeed.", "정말이지 언변이 뛰어나지."], ["Some of his periods quite majestic!”", "그분의 어떤 문장들은 아주 장엄하다니까!\""]], [["I felt this to be very true but was too confused to attend to it.", "나는 그 말이 무척 옳다고 느꼈지만, 너무 혼란스러워 거기에 마음을 둘 수 없었습니다."], ["Our speedy arrival at our destination, before I had time to recover myself, increased my confusion, and I never shall forget the uncertain and the unreal air of everything at Greenleaf (Miss Donny’s house) that afternoon!", "미처 정신을 추스를 새도 없이 목적지에 빠르게 도착하는 바람에 나는 더욱 혼란스러워졌고, 그날 오후 그린리프(도니 양의 집)에서 본 모든 것의 그 불확실하고 비현실적인 분위기를 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다!"]], [["But I soon became used to it.", "하지만 나는 곧 거기에 익숙해졌습니다."], ["I was so adapted to the routine of Greenleaf before long that I seemed to have been there a great while and almost to have dreamed rather than really lived my old life at my godmother’s.", "오래지 않아 나는 그린리프의 일과에 어찌나 잘 적응했던지, 마치 거기서 아주 오래 지낸 것 같았고, 대모님 댁에서 보낸 지난 삶은 정말로 살았다기보다 차라리 꿈을 꾼 것만 같았습니다."], ["Nothing could be more precise, exact, and orderly than Greenleaf.", "그린리프만큼 정확하고 빈틈없고 질서정연한 곳은 어디에도 없었습니다."], ["There was a time for everything all round the dial of the clock, and everything was done at its appointed moment.", "시계 문자판을 따라 모든 일에는 정해진 때가 있었고, 모든 일이 정해진 순간에 행해졌습니다."]], [["We were twelve boarders, and there were two Miss Donnys, twins.", "우리는 기숙생이 열두 명이었고, 도니 양은 쌍둥이라 두 분이었습니다."], ["It was understood that I would have to depend, by and by, on my qualifications as a governess, and I was not only instructed in everything that was taught at Greenleaf, but was very soon engaged in helping to instruct others.", "나는 머지않아 가정교사로서의 자격에 기대어 살아가야 하리라는 점이 양해되어 있었기에, 그린리프에서 가르치는 모든 것을 배웠을 뿐 아니라 곧 다른 아이들을 가르치는 일도 돕게 되었습니다."], ["Although I was treated in every other respect like the rest of the school, this single difference was made in my case from the first.", "다른 모든 면에서는 학교의 나머지 아이들과 똑같이 대우받았지만, 이 한 가지 차이만은 처음부터 내게 적용되었습니다."], ["As I began to know more, I taught more, and so in course of time I had plenty to do, which I was very fond of doing because it made the dear girls fond of me.", "내가 더 많이 알게 될수록 나는 더 많이 가르쳤고, 그렇게 시간이 흐르면서 할 일이 잔뜩 생겼습니다. 그리고 나는 그 일을 무척 좋아했는데, 그 덕에 사랑스러운 아이들이 나를 따랐기 때문입니다."], ["At last, whenever a new pupil came who was a little downcast and unhappy, she was so sure—indeed I don’t know why—to make a friend of me that all new-comers were confided to my care.", "마침내는 풀이 죽고 시무룩한 새 학생이 들어올 때마다, 그 애는 어김없이, 정말이지 왜인지는 모르겠지만, 나를 친구로 삼는 통에 새로 오는 아이들은 모두 내게 맡겨졌습니다."], ["They said I was so gentle, but I am sure THEY were!", "다들 내가 무척 다정하다고 했지만, 정작 다정한 건 그 애들이었습니다!"], ["I often thought of the resolution I had made on my birthday to try to be industrious, contented, and true-hearted and to do some good to some one and win some love if I could; and indeed, indeed, I felt almost ashamed to have done so little and have won so much.", "나는 생일에 했던 결심을, 곧 부지런하고 만족할 줄 알며 진실한 마음을 지닌 사람이 되어 누군가에게 작은 선을 베풀고 할 수만 있다면 사랑을 얻겠노라던 그 결심을 자주 떠올렸습니다. 그러면서 정말, 정말이지, 나는 그토록 적게 해주고도 그토록 많이 받았다는 게 거의 부끄러울 지경이었습니다."]], [["I passed at Greenleaf six happy, quiet years.", "나는 그린리프에서 행복하고 평온한 여섯 해를 보냈습니다."], ["I never saw in any face there, thank heaven, on my birthday, that it would have been better if I had never been born.", "하늘에 감사하게도, 내 생일에 거기 어느 얼굴에서도 내가 차라리 태어나지 않았더라면 좋았으리라는 기색을 본 적이 없습니다."], ["When the day came round, it brought me so many tokens of affectionate remembrance that my room was beautiful with them from New Year’s Day to Christmas.", "그날이 돌아오면, 어찌나 많은 다정한 추억의 표시들이 안겨지던지, 내 방은 정월 초하루부터 성탄절까지 그것들로 아름다웠습니다."]], [["In those six years I had never been away except on visits at holiday time in the neighbourhood.", "그 여섯 해 동안 나는 방학 때 이웃을 방문하러 간 것 말고는 한 번도 멀리 떠난 적이 없습니다."], ["After the first six months or so I had taken Miss Donny’s advice in reference to the propriety of writing to Mr. Kenge to say that I was happy and grateful, and with her approval I had written such a letter.", "처음 여섯 달쯤 지났을 무렵, 나는 켄지 씨에게 행복하고 감사하다는 편지를 써 보내는 것이 도리에 맞을지 도니 양의 조언을 구했고, 그분의 동의를 얻어 그런 편지를 썼습니다."], ["I had received a formal answer acknowledging its receipt and saying, “We note the contents thereof, which shall be duly communicated to our client.”", "나는 그 편지를 잘 받았다는 격식 차린 답장을 받았는데, \"본 서신의 내용을 유념하였으며, 이를 당사 의뢰인에게 적절히 전달하겠습니다.\"라는 내용이었습니다."], ["After that I sometimes heard Miss Donny and her sister mention how regular my accounts were paid, and about twice a year I ventured to write a similar letter.", "그 뒤로 나는 이따금 도니 양과 그 자매가 내 비용이 얼마나 또박또박 치러지는지 이야기하는 걸 들었고, 한 해에 두 번쯤 비슷한 편지를 써 보내는 용기를 냈습니다."], ["I always received by return of post exactly the same answer in the same round hand, with the signature of Kenge and Carboy in another writing, which I supposed to be Mr. Kenge’s.", "나는 늘 답신 편으로 똑같은 둥근 필체로 쓴 토씨 하나 다르지 않은 답장을 받았는데, 켄지 앤드 카보이라는 서명만은 다른 필체로 되어 있어, 나는 그게 켄지 씨의 글씨려니 짐작했습니다."]], [["It seems so curious to me to be obliged to write all this about myself!", "나 자신에 관한 이 모든 걸 써야만 한다는 게 어찌나 묘하게 느껴지는지요!"], ["As if this narrative were the narrative of MY life!", "마치 이 이야기가 내 삶의 이야기이기라도 한 양 말입니다!"], ["But my little body will soon fall into the background now.", "하지만 이제 곧 내 자그마한 몸은 뒤편으로 물러날 것입니다."]], [["Six quiet years (I find I am saying it for the second time) I had passed at Greenleaf, seeing in those around me, as it might be in a looking-glass, every stage of my own growth and change there, when, one November morning, I received this letter.", "그린리프에서 평온한 여섯 해(보니 두 번째로 같은 말을 하고 있군요)를 보내며, 나는 거기서 자라고 변해가는 내 모습의 모든 단계를, 마치 거울에 비추듯 주위 사람들에게서 보아왔는데, 그러던 어느 십일월 아침에 이런 편지를 받았습니다."], ["I omit the date.", "날짜는 생략하겠습니다."]], [["Old Square, Lincoln’s Inn", "링컨스 인, 올드 스퀘어"]], [["Madam,", "아가씨께,"]], [["Jarndyce and Jarndyce", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스"]], [["Our clt Mr. Jarndyce being abt to rece into his house, under an Order of the Ct of Chy, a Ward of the Ct in this cause, for whom he wishes to secure an elgble compn, directs us to inform you that he will be glad of your serces in the afsd capacity.", "당사 의뢰인 잔다이스 씨께서 대법원의 명령에 따라 본 소송의 피후견인 한 분을 자택에 맞아들이려 하시며, 그 피후견인께 알맞은 동무를 마련해 드리고자 하시는바, 아가씨께서 상기한 자격으로 봉사해 주신다면 기쁘겠다는 뜻을 전하라 당사에 지시하셨습니다."]], [["We have arrngd for your being forded, carriage free, pr eight o’clock coach from Reading, on Monday morning next, to White Horse Cellar, Piccadilly, London, where one of our clks will be in waiting to convey you to our offe as above.", "당사는 다가오는 월요일 아침, 레딩에서 출발하는 여덟 시 마차 편으로 아가씨를 무료로 런던 피카딜리의 화이트 호스 셀러까지 모시도록 조처하였으며, 그곳에서 당사 직원 하나가 대기하다가 상기 주소의 당사 사무소로 아가씨를 안내할 것입니다."]], [["We are, Madam, Your obedt Servts,", "아가씨께, 충실한 당사 직원 올림,"]], [["Kenge and Carboy", "켄지 앤드 카보이"]], [["Miss Esther Summerson", "에스더 서머슨 양 귀하"]], [["Oh, never, never, never shall I forget the emotion this letter caused in the house!", "오, 이 편지가 그 집에 일으킨 감정을 나는 결코, 결코, 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다!"], ["It was so tender in them to care so much for me, it was so gracious in that father who had not forgotten me to have made my orphan way so smooth and easy and to have inclined so many youthful natures towards me, that I could hardly bear it.", "다들 나를 그토록 살뜰히 아껴주는 것이 너무나 따뜻했고, 나를 잊지 않은 그 아버지 같은 분이 고아인 내 앞길을 그토록 매끄럽고 수월하게 닦아주시고 그토록 많은 어린 마음을 내게로 기울게 해주신 것이 너무나 자애로워, 나는 도무지 견딜 수가 없었습니다. 그렇다고 그들이 덜 슬퍼했으면 좋았겠다는 건 아닙니다."], ["Not that I would have had them less sorry—I am afraid not; but the pleasure of it, and the pain of it, and the pride and joy of it, and the humble regret of it were so blended that my heart seemed almost breaking while it was full of rapture.", "안타깝게도 그건 아니지요. 다만 그 기쁨과 그 아픔과 그 긍지와 환희와 겸허한 아쉬움이 너무나 뒤섞여, 내 가슴은 환희로 가득 차 있으면서도 거의 터질 듯했습니다."]], [["The letter gave me only five days’ notice of my removal.", "편지는 떠나기까지 단 닷새의 말미만 주었습니다."], ["When every minute added to the proofs of love and kindness that were given me in those five days, and when at last the morning came and when they took me through all the rooms that I might see them for the last time, and when some cried, “Esther, dear, say good-bye to me here at my bedside, where you first spoke so kindly to me!”", "그 닷새 동안 시시각각 사랑과 다정함의 증거가 더해지고, 마침내 그날 아침이 밝아 그들이 마지막으로 둘러볼 수 있게 나를 모든 방으로 데려가고, 누구는 \"에스더, 얘, 네가 처음 내게 그토록 다정하게 말해주었던 여기 내 침대맡에서 작별 인사를 해줘!\""], ["and when others asked me only to write their names, “With Esther’s love,” and when they all surrounded me with their parting presents and clung to me weeping and cried, “What shall we do when dear, dear Esther’s gone!”", "하고 외치고, 또 누구는 그저 \"에스더의 사랑을 담아\"라고 자기 이름을 적어달라고 부탁하며, 다들 작별 선물을 들고 나를 에워싸 울며 매달려서는 \"사랑하는, 사랑하는 에스더가 가버리면 우리는 어쩌니!\""], ["and when I tried to tell them how forbearing and how good they had all been to me and how I blessed and thanked them every one, what a heart I had!", "하고 외치는데, 내가 그들이 내게 얼마나 너그럽고 선했는지, 또 내가 그들 하나하나를 얼마나 축복하고 고마워하는지 말해주려 애쓸 때, 내 가슴은 어떠했을까요!"]], [["And when the two Miss Donnys grieved as much to part with me as the least among them, and when the maids said, “Bless you, miss, wherever you go!”", "또 두 도니 양이 그 누구 못지않게 나와 헤어지는 걸 슬퍼하고, 하녀들이 \"어디를 가시든 복 받으세요, 아가씨!\""], ["and when the ugly lame old gardener, who I thought had hardly noticed me in all those years, came panting after the coach to give me a little nosegay of geraniums and told me I had been the light of his eyes—indeed the old man said so!—what a heart I had then!", "하고 말하며, 그 오랜 세월 나를 거의 눈여겨보지 않았다고 여겼던 못생기고 절름발이인 늙은 정원사가 제라늄 꽃다발을 건네려 헐떡이며 마차를 뒤쫓아 와서는 내가 자기 눈의 빛이었노라고—정말 그 노인이 그렇게 말했답니다!—할 때, 그때 내 가슴은 어떠했을까요!"]], [["And could I help it if with all this, and the coming to the little school, and the unexpected sight of the poor children outside waving their hats and bonnets to me, and of a grey-haired gentleman and lady whose daughter I had helped to teach and at whose house I had visited (who were said to be the proudest people in all that country), caring for nothing but calling out, “Good-bye, Esther.", "게다가 이 모든 일에 더해, 작은 학교에 들렀을 때 뜻밖에도 가엾은 아이들이 바깥에서 모자와 보닛을 흔들며 인사하는 모습과, 내가 가르치는 일을 돕고 또 그 집을 방문한 적이 있던 한 딸의 부모인, 흰머리의 신사와 부인이(그 일대에서 가장 콧대 높은 사람들이라고들 했지요) 아무것에도 아랑곳없이 \"잘 가요, 에스더. 부디 아주 행복하기를!\""], ["May you be very happy!”—could I help it if I was quite bowed down in the coach by myself and said “Oh, I am so thankful, I am so thankful!”", "하고 외치는 모습을 보았으니, 마차 안에 홀로 앉아 완전히 무너져내려 \"오, 너무나 감사해요, 너무나 감사해요!\""], ["many times over!", "하고 몇 번이고 되뇐 것을 어찌 어쩌겠습니까!"]], [["But of course I soon considered that I must not take tears where I was going after all that had been done for me.", "하지만 물론 나는 그토록 많은 것을 받고서 이제 가는 곳에 눈물을 가져가서는 안 된다고 곧 생각했습니다."], ["Therefore, of course, I made myself sob less and persuaded myself to be quiet by saying very often, “Esther, now you really must!", "그래서 당연히 흐느낌을 줄이려 애쓰며, \"에스더, 이제 정말 그래야 해! 이러면 안 돼!\""], ["This WILL NOT do!”", "하고 자꾸자꾸 되뇌어 마음을 다잡았습니다."], ["I cheered myself up pretty well at last, though I am afraid I was longer about it than I ought to have been; and when I had cooled my eyes with lavender water, it was time to watch for London.", "마침내 나는 제법 잘 기운을 차렸는데, 마땅한 것보다 시간이 더 걸린 듯하여 부끄럽습니다. 라벤더 물로 눈을 식히고 나니, 어느새 런던을 살필 때가 되어 있었습니다."]], [["I was quite persuaded that we were there when we were ten miles off, and when we really were there, that we should never get there.", "나는 십육 킬로미터나 떨어져 있을 때 이미 다 왔다고 철석같이 믿었고, 정작 다 왔을 때는 영영 도착하지 못할 것만 같았습니다."], ["However, when we began to jolt upon a stone pavement, and particularly when every other conveyance seemed to be running into us, and we seemed to be running into every other conveyance, I began to believe that we really were approaching the end of our journey.", "그러다 돌이 깔린 길에서 마차가 덜컹대기 시작하고, 특히 다른 탈것들이 죄다 우리 쪽으로 달려드는 듯하고 우리도 다른 탈것마다 들이받는 듯하자, 나는 우리가 정말로 여정의 끝에 다가가고 있음을 믿게 되었습니다."], ["Very soon afterwards we stopped.", "머지않아 우리는 멈춰 섰습니다."]], [["A young gentleman who had inked himself by accident addressed me from the pavement and said, “I am from Kenge and Carboy’s, miss, of Lincoln’s Inn.”", "뜻하지 않게 잉크를 묻힌 어느 젊은 신사가 길바닥에서 내게 말을 건넸습니다. \"저는 링컨스 인의 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 왔습니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["“If you please, sir,”", "\"잘 부탁드립니다, 선생님.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["He was very obliging, and as he handed me into a fly after superintending the removal of my boxes, I asked him whether there was a great fire anywhere?", "그는 무척 친절했고, 내 짐가방 옮기는 일을 감독한 뒤 나를 삯마차에 태워주었는데, 나는 그에게 어디에 큰불이라도 났느냐고 물었습니다."], ["For the streets were so full of dense brown smoke that scarcely anything was to be seen.", "거리가 짙은 갈색 연기로 가득 차 거의 아무것도 보이지 않았기 때문입니다."]], [["“Oh, dear no, miss,” he said.", "\"오, 천만에요, 아가씨.\""], ["“This is a London particular.”", "그가 말했습니다. \"이건 런던 명물이랍니다.\""]], [["I had never heard of such a thing.", "나는 그런 걸 들어본 적이 없었습니다."]], [["“A fog, miss,”", "\"안개예요, 아가씨.\""], ["said the young gentleman.", "젊은 신사가 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, indeed!”", "\"아, 그렇군요!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["We drove slowly through the dirtiest and darkest streets that ever were seen in the world (I thought) and in such a distracting state of confusion that I wondered how the people kept their senses, until we passed into sudden quietude under an old gateway and drove on through a silent square until we came to an odd nook in a corner, where there was an entrance up a steep, broad flight of stairs, like an entrance to a church.", "우리는 (내 생각에) 이 세상에서 본 적 없을 만큼 가장 더럽고 가장 어두운 거리들을, 사람들이 어떻게 제정신을 지키는지 의아할 만큼 정신 사납고 혼잡한 와중에 천천히 지나갔습니다. 그러다 어느 낡은 대문 아래로 들어서며 문득 고요해졌고, 적막한 광장을 가로질러 나아가다 어느 구석의 야릇한 모퉁이에 이르렀는데, 그곳에는 마치 교회 입구처럼 가파르고 너른 계단을 오르는 출입구가 있었습니다."], ["And there really was a churchyard outside under some cloisters, for I saw the gravestones from the staircase window.", "그리고 회랑 아래 바깥에는 정말로 교회 묘지가 있었으니, 계단 창문으로 묘비들이 보였기 때문입니다."]], [["This was Kenge and Carboy’s.", "이곳이 켄지 앤드 카보이였습니다."], ["The young gentleman showed me through an outer office into Mr. Kenge’s room—there was no one in it—and politely put an arm-chair for me by the fire.", "젊은 신사는 나를 바깥 사무실을 지나 켄지 씨의 방으로 안내했는데—거기엔 아무도 없었습니다—난롯가에 안락의자를 정중히 놓아주었습니다."], ["He then called my attention to a little looking-glass hanging from a nail on one side of the chimney-piece.", "그러고는 벽난로 선반 한쪽 못에 걸린 자그마한 거울로 내 주의를 끌었습니다."]], [["“In case you should wish to look at yourself, miss, after the journey, as you’re going before the Chancellor.", "\"여행 끝에 혹시 거울에 비춰보고 싶으실까 해서요, 아가씨. 대법관님 앞에 나가시는 거니까요."], ["Not that it’s requisite, I am sure,”", "꼭 그래야 한다는 건 아닙니다, 분명히요.\""], ["said the young gentleman civilly.", "젊은 신사가 공손히 말했습니다."]], [["“Going before the Chancellor?”", "\"대법관님 앞에 나간다고요?\""], ["I said, startled for a moment.", "나는 순간 흠칫 놀라 말했습니다."]], [["“Only a matter of form, miss,”", "\"그저 형식적인 절차랍니다, 아가씨.\""], ["returned the young gentleman.", "젊은 신사가 대답했습니다."], ["“Mr. Kenge is in court now.", "\"켄지 씨는 지금 법정에 계세요."], ["He left his compliments, and would you partake of some refreshment”—there were biscuits and a decanter of wine on a small table—“and look over the paper,”", "안부를 전하시며, 요기 좀 하시겠느냐고요\"—작은 탁자에 비스킷과 포도주가 든 유리병이 놓여 있었습니다—\"그리고 신문이라도 보고 계시랍니다.\""], ["which the young gentleman gave me as he spoke.", "젊은 신사는 말하면서 신문을 건넸습니다."], ["He then stirred the fire and left me.", "그러고는 불을 뒤적여 돋운 뒤 나를 두고 나갔습니다."]], [["Everything was so strange—the stranger from its being night in the day-time, the candles burning with a white flame, and looking raw and cold—that I read the words in the newspaper without knowing what they meant and found myself reading the same words repeatedly.", "모든 게 어찌나 낯설던지—대낮인데도 밤 같다는 점, 촛불이 하얀 불꽃을 내며 설익은 듯 차갑게 타오른다는 점에서 더더욱 낯설었습니다—나는 신문 속 글자들을 그 뜻도 모른 채 읽었고, 같은 글자를 거듭거듭 읽고 있는 나를 발견했습니다."], ["As it was of no use going on in that way, I put the paper down, took a peep at my bonnet in the glass to see if it was neat, and looked at the room, which was not half lighted, and at the shabby, dusty tables, and at the piles of writings, and at a bookcase full of the most inexpressive-looking books that ever had anything to say for themselves.", "그렇게 계속해봐야 소용없어서, 나는 신문을 내려놓고 거울에 보닛이 단정한지 흘끗 비춰본 뒤, 절반밖에 밝혀지지 않은 방과, 허름하고 먼지 낀 탁자들과, 수북이 쌓인 서류 더미와, 여태껏 무언가 할 말이 있다는 책들 가운데 가장 표정 없어 보이는 책들로 가득 찬 책장을 둘러보았습니다."], ["Then I went on, thinking, thinking, thinking; and the fire went on, burning, burning, burning; and the candles went on flickering and guttering, and there were no snuffers—until the young gentleman by and by brought a very dirty pair—for two hours.", "그러고는 계속 생각하고, 생각하고, 또 생각했습니다. 그러는 동안 불은 타고, 타고, 또 탔으며, 촛불은 일렁이며 촛농을 흘렸는데, 심지 가위가 없어서—이윽고 젊은 신사가 무척 더러운 가위 한 자루를 가져다줄 때까지—두 시간 동안이나 그러했습니다."]], [["At last Mr. Kenge came.", "마침내 켄지 씨가 왔습니다."], ["HE was not altered, but he was surprised to see how altered I was and appeared quite pleased.", "그분은 전혀 변하지 않았지만, 내가 얼마나 변했는지를 보고는 놀라며 무척 흐뭇해하는 듯했습니다."], ["“As you are going to be the companion of the young lady who is now in the Chancellor’s private room, Miss Summerson,” he said, “we thought it well that you should be in attendance also.", "\"지금 대법관님의 사실(私室)에 계신 아가씨의 동무가 되실 분이니, 서머슨 양, 우리는 아가씨도 함께 그 자리에 계시는 게 좋겠다고 여겼답니다.\""], ["You will not be discomposed by the Lord Chancellor, I dare say?”", "그분이 말했습니다. \"대법관님 앞이라고 당황하지는 않으시겠지요?\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"네, 선생님.\""], ["I said, “I don’t think I shall,”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그러지 않을 것 같아요.\""], ["really not seeing on consideration why I should be.", "곰곰 생각해봐도 정말 당황할 이유가 없어 보였습니다."]], [["So Mr. Kenge gave me his arm and we went round the corner, under a colonnade, and in at a side door.", "그래서 켄지 씨가 내게 팔을 내주었고, 우리는 모퉁이를 돌아 주랑(柱廊) 아래를 지나 옆문으로 들어섰습니다."], ["And so we came, along a passage, into a comfortable sort of room where a young lady and a young gentleman were standing near a great, loud-roaring fire.", "그렇게 우리는 복도를 따라 어느 아늑한 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기서는 젊은 아가씨와 젊은 신사가 크게 우르릉거리는 난롯가에 서 있었습니다."], ["A screen was interposed between them and it, and they were leaning on the screen, talking.", "그들과 불 사이에는 가리개가 놓여 있었고, 두 사람은 가리개에 기대어 이야기를 나누고 있었습니다."]], [["They both looked up when I came in, and I saw in the young lady, with the fire shining upon her, such a beautiful girl!", "내가 들어서자 둘 다 고개를 들었는데, 불빛이 비치는 그 젊은 아가씨를 보니 어찌나 아름다운 소녀이던지요!"], ["With such rich golden hair, such soft blue eyes, and such a bright, innocent, trusting face!", "그토록 풍성한 황금빛 머리칼에, 그토록 부드러운 파란 눈에, 그토록 환하고 순결하며 사람을 믿는 얼굴이라니!"]], [["“Miss Ada,” said Mr.", "\"에이다 양.\""], ["Kenge, “this is Miss Summerson.”", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"이쪽은 서머슨 양입니다.\""]], [["She came to meet me with a smile of welcome and her hand extended, but seemed to change her mind in a moment and kissed me.", "그녀는 환영의 미소를 띠고 손을 내밀며 나를 맞으러 다가오다가, 순간 마음을 바꾼 듯 내게 입을 맞추었습니다."], ["In short, she had such a natural, captivating, winning manner that in a few minutes we were sitting in the window-seat, with the light of the fire upon us, talking together as free and happy as could be.", "한마디로 그녀에게는 어찌나 자연스럽고 사람을 사로잡는 매력적인 태도가 있던지, 몇 분 만에 우리는 불빛을 받으며 창가 자리에 앉아 더없이 거리낌 없고 행복하게 이야기를 나누었습니다."]], [["What a load off my mind!", "내 마음이 어찌나 가뿐해지던지요!"], ["It was so delightful to know that she could confide in me and like me!", "그녀가 나를 믿어주고 좋아해줄 수 있다는 걸 아는 것이 너무나 기뻤습니다!"], ["It was so good of her, and so encouraging to me!", "그녀는 너무나 다정했고, 내게는 너무나 큰 힘이 되었습니다!"]], [["The young gentleman was her distant cousin, she told me, and his name Richard Carstone.", "그녀는 그 젊은 신사가 자기 먼 친척이라며, 이름은 리처드 카스턴이라고 일러주었습니다."], ["He was a handsome youth with an ingenuous face and a most engaging laugh; and after she had called him up to where we sat, he stood by us, in the light of the fire, talking gaily, like a light-hearted boy.", "그는 순박한 얼굴에 더없이 호감 가는 웃음을 지닌 잘생긴 청년이었습니다. 그녀가 우리가 앉은 곳으로 그를 부르자, 그는 불빛을 받으며 우리 곁에 서서, 근심 없는 소년처럼 명랑하게 이야기했습니다."], ["He was very young, not more than nineteen then, if quite so much, but nearly two years older than she was.", "그는 무척 어려서, 그때 기껏해야 열아홉 살, 그것도 채 안 되었을지 몰랐지만, 그녀보다는 거의 두 살 위였습니다."], ["They were both orphans and (what was very unexpected and curious to me) had never met before that day.", "둘 다 고아였고, (내게는 무척 뜻밖이고 신기하게도) 그날 이전에는 한 번도 만난 적이 없었습니다."], ["Our all three coming together for the first time in such an unusual place was a thing to talk about, and we talked about it; and the fire, which had left off roaring, winked its red eyes at us—as Richard said—like a drowsy old Chancery lion.", "우리 셋이 이런 별난 곳에서 처음으로 한자리에 모인 것은 이야깃거리가 될 만했고, 우리는 그 이야기를 나누었습니다. 그러자 우르릉거리기를 그친 불이, 리처드의 말마따나, 졸린 늙은 대법원 사자처럼 빨간 눈을 끔벅이며 우리에게 윙크했습니다."]], [["We conversed in a low tone because a full-dressed gentleman in a bag wig frequently came in and out, and when he did so, we could hear a drawling sound in the distance, which he said was one of the counsel in our case addressing the Lord Chancellor.", "우리는 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누었습니다. 정장 차림에 변호사 가발을 쓴 신사 한 명이 자주 들락거렸는데, 그가 드나들 때면 저 멀리서 느릿느릿 늘어지는 소리가 들렸기 때문입니다."], ["He told Mr. Kenge that the Chancellor would be up in five minutes; and presently we heard a bustle and a tread of feet, and Mr.", "그건 우리 소송의 변호인 가운데 한 명이 대법관님께 변론하는 소리라고 그가 말했습니다."], ["Kenge said that the Court had risen and his lordship was in the next room.", "그는 켄지 씨에게 대법관님이 오 분 안에 일어나실 거라고 했습니다. 이윽고 우리는 부산한 소리와 발걸음 소리를 들었고, 켄지 씨가 법정이 폐정했으며 대법관님이 옆방에 계신다고 말했습니다."]], [["The gentleman in the bag wig opened the door almost directly and requested Mr.", "가발을 쓴 신사가 거의 곧바로 문을 열고는 켄지 씨에게 들어오시라고 청했습니다."], ["Kenge to come in. Upon that, we all went into the next room, Mr.", "그러자 우리는 모두 옆방으로 들어갔는데, 켄지 씨가 내 사랑하는 그녀와 함께 맨 앞에 섰습니다—이제는 내게 너무나 자연스러워 이렇게 쓰지 않을 수가 없군요."], ["Kenge first, with my darling—it is so natural to me now that I can’t help writing it; and there, plainly dressed in black and sitting in an arm-chair at a table near the fire, was his lordship, whose robe, trimmed with beautiful gold lace, was thrown upon another chair.", "그리고 거기, 수수한 검은 옷차림으로 난롯가 탁자 옆 안락의자에 앉아 계신 분이 대법관님이었는데, 아름다운 금실 장식을 두른 그분의 법복은 다른 의자 위에 던져져 있었습니다."], ["He gave us a searching look as we entered, but his manner was both courtly and kind.", "우리가 들어서자 그분은 살피는 눈길을 보냈지만, 태도는 기품 있으면서도 다정했습니다."]], [["The gentleman in the bag wig laid bundles of papers on his lordship’s table, and his lordship silently selected one and turned over the leaves.", "가발을 쓴 신사가 대법관님 탁자에 서류 뭉치를 내려놓았고, 대법관님은 말없이 그중 하나를 골라 책장을 넘겼습니다."]], [["“Miss Clare,”", "\"클레어 양.\""], ["said the Lord Chancellor.", "대법관이 말했습니다."], ["“Miss Ada Clare?”", "\"에이다 클레어 양이지요?\""]], [["Mr. Kenge presented her, and his lordship begged her to sit down near him.", "켄지 씨가 그녀를 소개하자, 대법관님은 자기 곁에 앉으라고 청했습니다."], ["That he admired her and was interested by her even I could see in a moment.", "그분이 그녀를 흠모하고 그녀에게 마음을 두었다는 건 나조차 한눈에 알 수 있었습니다."], ["It touched me that the home of such a beautiful young creature should be represented by that dry, official place.", "그토록 아름다운 젊은 사람의 집이 저 메마르고 사무적인 곳으로 대신 채워진다는 게 가슴 아팠습니다."], ["The Lord High Chancellor, at his best, appeared so poor a substitute for the love and pride of parents.", "아무리 잘 봐줘도 대법관님은 부모의 사랑과 긍지를 대신하기엔 너무나 초라한 대용품 같았습니다."]], [["“The Jarndyce in question,”", "\"여기 거론된 잔다이스는,\""], ["said the Lord Chancellor, still turning over leaves, “is Jarndyce of Bleak House.”", "여전히 책장을 넘기며 대법관이 말했습니다. \"황폐한 집의 잔다이스로군.\""]], [["“Jarndyce of Bleak House, my lord,”", "\"황폐한 집의 잔다이스입니다, 대법관님.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“A dreary name,”", "\"음울한 이름이군.\""], ["said the Lord Chancellor.", "대법관이 말했습니다."]], [["“But not a dreary place at present, my lord,”", "\"하지만 지금은 음울한 곳이 아닙니다, 대법관님.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“And Bleak House,”", "\"그리고 황폐한 집은,\""], ["said his lordship, “is in—”", "대법관이 말했습니다. \"어디 있는—\""]], [["“Hertfordshire, my lord.”", "\"하트퍼드셔에 있습니다, 대법관님.\""]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House is not married?”", "\"황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨는 결혼하지 않았나?\""], ["said his lordship.", "대법관이 말했습니다."]], [["“He is not, my lord,” said Mr.", "\"하지 않았습니다, 대법관님.\""], ["Kenge. A pause.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Young Mr. Richard Carstone is present?”", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀습니다."], ["said the Lord Chancellor, glancing towards him.", "\"젊은 리처드 카스턴 군은 와 있는가?\" 대법관이 그를 흘끔 보며 말했습니다."]], [["Richard bowed and stepped forward.", "리처드는 고개 숙여 인사하고 앞으로 나섰습니다."]], [["“Hum!”", "\"흠!\""], ["said the Lord Chancellor, turning over more leaves.", "대법관이 책장을 더 넘기며 말했습니다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨는, 대법관님,\""], ["Jarndyce of Bleak House, my lord,”", "켄지 씨가 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다."], ["Mr. Kenge observed in a low voice, “if I may venture to remind your lordship, provides a suitable companion for—”", "\"황공하오나 다시금 상기시켜 드리자면, 알맞은 동무를 마련하셨는데, 그분은—\""]], [["“For Mr. Richard Carstone?”", "\"리처드 카스턴 군의 동무인가?\""], ["I thought (but I am not quite sure) I heard his lordship say in an equally low voice and with a smile.", "대법관이 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 미소를 띠며 그렇게 말한 것을 나는 들은 것 같았습니다(다만 확실하지는 않습니다)."]], [["“For Miss Ada Clare.", "\"에이다 클레어 양의 동무입니다."], ["This is the young lady.", "이쪽이 그 젊은 아가씨예요."], ["Miss Summerson.”", "서머슨 양입니다.\""]], [["His lordship gave me an indulgent look and acknowledged my curtsy very graciously.", "대법관님은 내게 너그러운 눈길을 보내고는 내 인사를 무척 자애롭게 받아주었습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson is not related to any party in the cause, I think?”", "\"서머슨 양은 이 소송의 어느 당사자와도 친척이 아닐 텐데, 그렇지?\""]], [["“No, my lord.”", "\"그렇습니다, 대법관님.\""]], [["Mr. Kenge leant over before it was quite said and whispered.", "켄지 씨가 그 말이 미처 끝나기도 전에 몸을 숙여 무언가를 속삭였습니다."], ["His lordship, with his eyes upon his papers, listened, nodded twice or thrice, turned over more leaves, and did not look towards me again until we were going away.", "대법관님은 서류에 눈을 둔 채 귀 기울이며 두세 번 고개를 끄덕이고 책장을 더 넘겼는데, 우리가 나갈 때까지 다시는 내 쪽을 보지 않았습니다."]], [["Mr. Kenge now retired, and Richard with him, to where I was, near the door, leaving my pet (it is so natural to me that again I can’t help it!) sitting near the Lord Chancellor, with whom his lordship spoke a little part, asking her, as she told me afterwards, whether she had well reflected on the proposed arrangement, and if she thought she would be happy under the roof of Mr.", "이제 켄지 씨가, 그리고 리처드도 함께, 문가에 있던 내 쪽으로 물러났는데, 내 귀염둥이(이게 너무나 자연스러워 또 어쩔 수가 없군요!)는 대법관님 곁에 남아 앉아 있었습니다."], ["Jarndyce of Bleak House, and why she thought so?", "대법관님은 그녀와 잠깐 이야기를 나누었는데, 나중에 그녀가 말해준 바로는, 제안된 그 합의를 잘 숙고했는지, 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨 지붕 아래서 행복할 것 같은지, 또 어째서 그렇게 생각하는지를 물으셨다고 했습니다. 이윽고 대법관님은 정중히 일어나 그녀를 놓아준 뒤, 일이 분쯤 리처드 카스턴과 이야기를 나누었습니다."], ["Presently he rose courteously and released her, and then he spoke for a minute or two with Richard Carstone, not seated, but standing, and altogether with more ease and less ceremony, as if he still knew, though he WAS Lord Chancellor, how to go straight to the candour of a boy.", "앉아서가 아니라 선 채로, 한결 편하고 격식을 덜 차린 태도로 말입니다. 마치 대법관님이면서도, 소년의 솔직함에 곧장 가닿는 법을 여전히 아는 것처럼요."]], [["“Very well!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said his lordship aloud.", "대법관이 소리 내어 말했습니다."], ["“I shall make the order.", "\"명령을 내리겠네."], ["Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House has chosen, so far as I may judge,”", "내가 판단하기로는, 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 씨는,\""], ["and this was when he looked at me, “a very good companion for the young lady, and the arrangement altogether seems the best of which the circumstances admit.”", "그러면서 그분은 나를 바라보았습니다. \"이 젊은 아가씨에게 무척 좋은 동무를 골랐고, 이 합의는 전체적으로 형편이 허락하는 한 가장 나은 것으로 보이네.\""]], [["He dismissed us pleasantly, and we all went out, very much obliged to him for being so affable and polite, by which he had certainly lost no dignity but seemed to us to have gained some.", "그분은 우리를 기분 좋게 물러가게 했고, 우리는 모두 그분이 그토록 상냥하고 정중했던 것에 무척 감사해하며 나왔습니다. 그렇게 함으로써 그분은 분명 위엄을 조금도 잃지 않았을뿐더러, 우리가 보기엔 오히려 위엄을 얻은 듯했습니다."]], [["When we got under the colonnade, Mr. Kenge remembered that he must go back for a moment to ask a question and left us in the fog, with the Lord Chancellor’s carriage and servants waiting for him to come out.", "주랑 아래로 나왔을 때, 켄지 씨는 잠깐 돌아가 물어볼 게 있다는 걸 떠올리고는, 대법관님의 마차와 하인들이 그분이 나오기를 기다리는 가운데 우리를 안개 속에 남겨두었습니다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["said Richard Carstone.", "리처드 카스턴이 말했습니다."], ["“THAT’S over!", "\"이제 끝났네요!"], ["And where do we go next, Miss Summerson?”", "그런데 다음엔 어디로 가는 거죠, 서머슨 양?\""]], [["“Don’t you know?”", "\"모르세요?\""], ["I said.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Not in the least,”", "\"전혀요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["“And don’t YOU know, my love?”", "\"그럼 너는 아니, 얘?\""], ["I asked Ada.", "나는 에이다에게 물었습니다."]], [["“No!” said she.", "\"몰라요!\""], ["“Don’t you?”", "그녀가 말했습니다."], ["“Not at all!” said I.", "\"언니는요?\""]], [["We looked at one another, half laughing at our being like the children in the wood, when a curious little old woman in a squeezed bonnet and carrying a reticule came curtsying and smiling up to us with an air of great ceremony.", "\"전혀 모르겠는걸!\" 내가 말했습니다. 우리는 서로 바라보며, 마치 숲속의 아이들 같은 우리 처지에 반쯤 웃고 있었는데, 그때 찌부러진 보닛을 쓰고 손가방을 든 별난 노파 하나가 무척 거창한 격식을 차린 태도로, 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지으며 우리에게 다가왔습니다."]], [["“Oh!” said she.", "\"오!\""], ["“The wards in Jarndyce!", "그녀가 말했습니다."], ["Ve-ry happy, I am sure, to have the honour!", "\"잔다이스의 피후견인들이군요!"], ["It is a good omen for youth, and hope, and beauty when they find themselves in this place, and don’t know what’s to come of it.”", "영광을 누리게 되어 참으로 기쁘답니다! 젊음과 희망과 아름다움이 이런 곳에 와서 앞날이 어찌 될지도 모르고 있다니, 좋은 징조예요.\""]], [["“Mad!”", "\"미쳤어.\""], ["whispered Richard, not thinking she could hear him.", "리처드가 노파에게 들리지 않으리라 여기고 속삭였습니다."]], [["“Right!", "\"맞아요!"], ["Mad, young gentleman,”", "미쳤지요, 젊은 신사 양반.\""], ["she returned so quickly that he was quite abashed.", "그녀가 어찌나 빨리 받아치던지 그는 무안해졌습니다."], ["“I was a ward myself.", "\"나도 한때 피후견인이었답니다."], ["I was not mad at that time,”", "그때는 미치지 않았었지요.\""], ["curtsying low and smiling between every little sentence.", "짧은 문장 하나하나 사이마다 낮게 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“I had youth and hope.", "\"내게도 젊음과 희망이 있었어요."], ["I believe, beauty.", "아름다움도 있었겠지요."], ["It matters very little now.", "이제는 다 부질없지만요."], ["Neither of the three served or saved me.", "그 셋 가운데 어느 하나도 나를 거들거나 구해주지 못했답니다."], ["I have the honour to attend court regularly.", "나는 영광스럽게도 꼬박꼬박 법정에 출석한답니다."], ["With my documents.", "내 서류들을 들고서요."], ["I expect a judgment.", "나는 판결을 기다리고 있어요."], ["Shortly.", "머지않아요."], ["On the Day of Judgment.", "심판의 날에 말이지요."], ["I have discovered that the sixth seal mentioned in the Revelations is the Great Seal.", "나는 요한계시록에 언급된 여섯째 봉인이 바로 국새(國璽)임을 밝혀냈답니다."], ["It has been open a long time!", "그게 열린 지 오래되었어요!"], ["Pray accept my blessing.”", "부디 내 축복을 받으세요.\""]], [["As Ada was a little frightened, I said, to humour the poor old lady, that we were much obliged to her.", "에이다가 조금 겁먹은 터라, 나는 가엾은 노파의 비위를 맞추려고 무척 고맙다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Ye-es!”", "\"그래—요!\""], ["she said mincingly.", "그녀가 점잔 빼며 말했습니다."], ["“I imagine so.", "\"그러리라 짐작해요."], ["And here is Conversation Kenge.", "그리고 여기 입심꾼 켄지가 오는군요."], ["With HIS documents!", "그의 서류들을 들고서요!"], ["How does your honourable worship do?”", "존경하는 나리, 안녕하신지요?\""]], [["“Quite well, quite well!", "\"아주 잘 지냅니다, 아주 잘요!"], ["Now don’t be troublesome, that’s a good soul!”", "자, 성가시게 굴지 마세요, 착한 양반이니까!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge, leading the way back.", "켄지 씨가 앞장서 돌아가며 말했습니다."]], [["“By no means,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["said the poor old lady, keeping up with Ada and me.", "가엾은 노파가 에이다와 나를 따라붙으며 말했습니다."], ["“Anything but troublesome.", "\"성가시게 굴기는커녕요."], ["I shall confer estates on both—which is not being troublesome, I trust?", "나는 두 사람 모두에게 영지를 하사할 거랍니다. 그건 성가신 일이 아니겠지요?"], ["I expect a judgment. Shortly.", "나는 판결을 기다리고 있어요."], ["On the Day of Judgment.", "머지않아요. 심판의 날에요."], ["This is a good omen for you.", "이건 두 사람에게 좋은 징조랍니다."], ["Accept my blessing!”", "내 축복을 받으세요!\""]], [["She stopped at the bottom of the steep, broad flight of stairs; but we looked back as we went up, and she was still there, saying, still with a curtsy and a smile between every little sentence, “Youth.", "그녀는 가파르고 너른 계단 아래서 걸음을 멈추었습니다. 하지만 우리가 계단을 오르며 돌아보니, 그녀는 여전히 거기 서서, 여전히 짧은 문장 하나하나 사이마다 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 지으며 이렇게 말하고 있었습니다."], ["And hope.", "\"젊음."], ["And beauty.", "그리고 희망."], ["And Chancery.", "그리고 아름다움."], ["And Conversation Kenge! Ha!", "그리고 대법원."], ["Pray accept my blessing!”", "그리고 입심꾼 켄지! 하!"], ["Telescopic Philanthropy", "부디 내 축복을 받으세요!\""]], [["We were to pass the night, Mr. Kenge told us when we arrived in his room, at Mrs.", "우리는 그날 밤을 젤리비 부인 댁에서 묵기로 했다고, 켄지 씨가 그의 방에 도착한 우리에게 일러주었습니다."], ["Jellyby’s; and then he turned to me and said he took it for granted I knew who Mrs. Jellyby was.", "그러더니 나를 돌아보며, 내가 젤리비 부인이 누구인지 당연히 알고 있으리라 여긴다고 말했습니다."]], [["“I really don’t, sir,”", "\"정말 모릅니다, 선생님.\""], ["I returned.", "내가 대답했습니다."], ["“Perhaps Mr. Carstone—or Miss Clare—”", "\"혹시 카스톤 씨나, 클레어 양은—\""]], [["But no, they knew nothing whatever about Mrs. Jellyby.", "하지만 두 사람도 젤리비 부인에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐습니다."], ["“In-deed! Mrs.", "\"저런!"], ["Jellyby,” said Mr.", "젤리비 부인은 말이지요.\""], ["Kenge, standing with his back to the fire and casting his eyes over the dusty hearth-rug as if it were Mrs.", "켄지 씨는 난로를 등지고 서서, 마치 먼지 낀 난로 깔개가 젤리비 부인의 전기라도 되는 양 거기에 눈길을 떨구며 말했습니다."], ["Jellyby’s biography, “is a lady of very remarkable strength of character who devotes herself entirely to the public.", "\"성품이 아주 비범하게 강인한 부인으로, 자신을 온전히 공공에 바치는 분입니다. 시기마다 광범위하고 다양한 공공 사안에 헌신해왔고, 지금은 (다른 무언가가 마음을 끌기 전까지는) 아프리카 사안에 헌신하고 있지요."], ["She has devoted herself to an extensive variety of public subjects at various times and is at present (until something else attracts her) devoted to the subject of Africa, with a view to the general cultivation of the coffee berry—AND the natives—and the happy settlement, on the banks of the African rivers, of our superabundant home population. Mr.", "커피 열매의 전반적인 재배를—그리고 원주민을—더불어 우리의 넘쳐나는 본국 인구를 아프리카 강기슭에 행복하게 정착시키는 것을 목표로 말입니다."], ["Jarndyce, who is desirous to aid any work that is considered likely to be a good work and who is much sought after by philanthropists, has, I believe, a very high opinion of Mrs. Jellyby.”", "좋은 일이라 여겨지는 모든 사업이라면 돕기를 바라며 자선가들에게 자주 부탁받는 잔다이스 씨도, 내가 알기로는, 젤리비 부인을 무척 높이 평가하고 있습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Kenge, adjusting his cravat, then looked at us.", "켄지 씨는 넥타이를 매만지고 나서 우리를 바라보았습니다."]], [["“And Mr. Jellyby, sir?”", "\"그러면 젤리비 씨는요, 선생님?\""], ["suggested Richard.", "리처드가 물었습니다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Mr. Jellyby,”", "젤리비 씨로 말하자면,\""], ["said Mr.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Kenge, “is—a—I don’t know that I can describe him to you better than by saying that he is the husband of Mrs. Jellyby.”", "\"그는—저—젤리비 부인의 남편이라고 말하는 것보다 더 잘 설명할 길이 있을지 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“A nonentity, sir?”", "\"존재감 없는 분이군요, 선생님?\""], ["said Richard with a droll look.", "리처드가 익살스러운 표정으로 말했습니다."]], [["“I don’t say that,”", "\"그렇다고는 안 했습니다.\""], ["returned Mr. Kenge gravely.", "켄지 씨가 정색하며 대답했습니다."], ["“I can’t say that, indeed, for I know nothing whatever OF Mr. Jellyby.", "\"사실 그렇게 말할 수 없지요, 젤리비 씨에 관해서는 도무지 아는 게 없으니까요."], ["I never, to my knowledge, had the pleasure of seeing Mr. Jellyby.", "내가 알기로는, 젤리비 씨를 뵙는 기쁨을 누린 적이 한 번도 없습니다."], ["He may be a very superior man, but he is, so to speak, merged—merged—in the more shining qualities of his wife.” Mr.", "어쩌면 아주 출중한 분일 수도 있지만, 말하자면, 부인의 더 빛나는 자질 속에 묻혀버린—묻혀버린 분이지요.\""], ["Kenge proceeded to tell us that as the road to Bleak House would have been very long, dark, and tedious on such an evening, and as we had been travelling already, Mr. Jarndyce had himself proposed this arrangement.", "켄지 씨는 이어서 우리에게, 이런 저녁에 황폐한 집까지 가는 길은 매우 길고 어둡고 지루할 터인 데다 우리가 이미 여행을 한 참이라, 잔다이스 씨가 손수 이런 조처를 제안했다고 말해주었습니다."], ["A carriage would be at Mrs. Jellyby’s to convey us out of town early in the forenoon of to-morrow.", "내일 오전 일찍 우리를 도시 밖으로 데려갈 마차가 젤리비 부인 댁에 와 있으리라고요."]], [["He then rang a little bell, and the young gentleman came in. Addressing him by the name of Guppy, Mr.", "그러고 나서 그가 작은 종을 울리자 그 젊은 신사가 들어왔습니다."], ["Kenge inquired whether Miss Summerson’s boxes and the rest of the baggage had been “sent round.” Mr.", "켄지 씨는 그를 거피라는 이름으로 부르며, 서머슨 양의 상자들과 나머지 짐이 \"보내졌는지\""], ["Guppy said yes, they had been sent round, and a coach was waiting to take us round too as soon as we pleased.", "물었습니다. 거피 씨는 그렇다고, 짐은 보내졌으며, 우리가 원하면 곧바로 우리도 데려갈 마차가 기다리고 있다고 답했습니다."]], [["“Then it only remains,” said Mr.", "\"그러면 이제 남은 일은,\""], ["Kenge, shaking hands with us, “for me to express my lively satisfaction in (good day, Miss Clare!) the arrangement this day concluded and my (GOOD-bye to you, Miss Summerson!) lively hope that it will conduce to the happiness, the (glad to have had the honour of making your acquaintance, Mr.", "켄지 씨가 우리와 악수하며 말했습니다. \"오늘 매듭지어진 이 조처에 (안녕히 가세요, 클레어 양!) 대한 나의 생생한 만족을 표하는 것과, 이 일이 관련된 모든 분의 행복과, (안녕히 가세요, 서머슨 양!) 안녕과, 모든 관점에서의 (알게 되어 영광이었습니다, 카스톤 씨!) 이익에 이바지하기를 바라는 나의 생생한 희망을 표하는 것뿐이로군요!"], ["Carstone!) welfare, the advantage in all points of view, of all concerned! Guppy, see the party safely there.”", "거피, 일행을 무사히 그곳까지 모셔다드리게.\""]], [["“Where IS ‘there,’ Mr. Guppy?”", "\"'그곳'이 어디입니까, 거피 씨?\""], ["said Richard as we went downstairs.", "우리가 계단을 내려가는데 리처드가 물었습니다."]], [["“No distance,” said Mr.", "\"멀지 않아요.\""], ["Guppy; “round in Thavies Inn, you know.”", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"아시다시피, 저쪽 세이비스 여관 근처지요.\""]], [["“I can’t say I know where it is, for I come from Winchester and am strange in London.”", "\"어디 있는지 안다고는 못 하겠군요, 나는 윈체스터에서 와서 런던이 낯설거든요.\""]], [["“Only round the corner,”", "\"바로 모퉁이만 돌면 됩니다.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“We just twist up Chancery Lane, and cut along Holborn, and there we are in four minutes’ time, as near as a toucher.", "\"대법원 골목을 휙 꺾어 올라가서 홀본을 따라 질러가면, 그야말로 눈 깜짝할 사이에, 사 분 만에 도착이지요."], ["This is about a London particular NOW, ain’t it, miss?”", "이만하면 이게 바로 런던 특유의 안개 아닙니까, 아가씨?\""], ["He seemed quite delighted with it on my account.", "그는 나를 봐서 그 안개를 무척 흐뭇해하는 듯했습니다."]], [["“The fog is very dense indeed!”", "\"안개가 정말 짙군요!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Not that it affects you, though, I’m sure,”", "\"하지만 아가씨께는 영향이 없는 게 분명합니다.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy, putting up the steps.", "발판을 올리며 거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“On the contrary, it seems to do you good, miss, judging from your appearance.”", "\"오히려, 안색으로 판단하건대, 아가씨께는 좋은 작용을 하는 것 같은데요.\""]], [["I knew he meant well in paying me this compliment, so I laughed at myself for blushing at it when he had shut the door and got upon the box; and we all three laughed and chatted about our inexperience and the strangeness of London until we turned up under an archway to our destination—a narrow street of high houses like an oblong cistern to hold the fog.", "나는 그가 좋은 뜻으로 이 칭찬을 건넨 줄 알았기에, 그가 문을 닫고 마부석에 올라탄 뒤에 거기에 얼굴을 붉힌 나 자신을 보고 웃었습니다. 그리고 우리 셋은 우리의 미숙함과 런던의 낯섦에 대해 웃으며 이야기를 나누다가, 어느 아치문 아래로 꺾어 들어 목적지에 이르렀습니다—안개를 가두어두는 길쭉한 물통 같은, 높은 집들이 늘어선 좁은 거리였습니다."], ["There was a confused little crowd of people, principally children, gathered about the house at which we stopped, which had a tarnished brass plate on the door with the inscription JELLYBY.", "우리가 멈춰 선 집 둘레에는 주로 아이들로 이루어진 작고 어수선한 무리가 모여 있었는데, 그 집 문에는 젤리비라는 글자가 새겨진, 빛바랜 놋쇠 명패가 붙어 있었습니다."]], [["“Don’t be frightened!”", "\"놀라지 마세요!\""], ["said Mr. Guppy, looking in at the coach-window.", "마차 창문을 들여다보며 거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“One of the young Jellybys been and got his head through the area railings!”", "\"젤리비네 어린것 하나가 지하실 난간 사이에 머리를 들이밀고 끼어버렸네요!\""]], [["“Oh, poor child,”", "\"어머, 가엾어라.\""], ["said I; “let me out, if you please!”", "내가 말했습니다. \"내려주세요, 부탁이에요!\""]], [["“Pray be careful of yourself, miss.", "\"부디 조심하세요, 아가씨."], ["The young Jellybys are always up to something,”", "젤리비네 어린것들은 늘 무슨 일을 벌이거든요.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["I made my way to the poor child, who was one of the dirtiest little unfortunates I ever saw, and found him very hot and frightened and crying loudly, fixed by the neck between two iron railings, while a milkman and a beadle, with the kindest intentions possible, were endeavouring to drag him back by the legs, under a general impression that his skull was compressible by those means.", "나는 그 가엾은 아이에게 다가갔는데, 내가 본 가장 꾀죄죄하고 가련한 어린것 가운데 하나였습니다. 아이는 잔뜩 달아오른 채 겁에 질려 큰 소리로 울며, 두 개의 쇠 난간 사이에 목이 끼어 옴짝달싹 못 하고 있었습니다."], ["As I found (after pacifying him) that he was a little boy with a naturally large head, I thought that perhaps where his head could go, his body could follow, and mentioned that the best mode of extrication might be to push him forward.", "우유 배달부와 교구지기가 더없이 친절한 마음으로 아이의 다리를 잡아 끄집어내려 애쓰고 있었는데, 그렇게 하면 두개골이 눌려 빠질 수 있으리라고들 믿는 모양이었습니다. 나는 (아이를 달랜 뒤에) 그 아이가 본디 머리가 큰 사내아이라는 것을 알아채고는, 머리가 들어간 곳이라면 어쩌면 몸도 따라갈 수 있겠다 싶어, 빼내는 가장 좋은 방법은 앞으로 밀어 넣는 것이라고 말했습니다."], ["This was so favourably received by the milkman and beadle that he would immediately have been pushed into the area if I had not held his pinafore while Richard and Mr. Guppy ran down through the kitchen to catch him when he should be released.", "우유 배달부와 교구지기가 이 말을 어찌나 흔쾌히 받아들였는지, 리처드와 거피 씨가 부엌을 통해 뛰어 내려가 아이가 풀려나면 받아 안으려 할 동안 내가 아이의 앞치마를 붙들지 않았더라면, 아이는 곧장 지하실로 밀려들어 갔을 것입니다."], ["At last he was happily got down without any accident, and then he began to beat Mr. Guppy with a hoop-stick in quite a frantic manner.", "마침내 아이는 다친 데 없이 무사히 내려졌고, 그러더니 아주 미친 듯이 굴렁쇠 막대로 거피 씨를 두드려 패기 시작했습니다."]], [["Nobody had appeared belonging to the house except a person in pattens, who had been poking at the child from below with a broom; I don’t know with what object, and I don’t think she did.", "그 집 식구로 보이는 사람은 아무도 나타나지 않았고, 다만 나막신을 신은 한 사람이 아래에서 빗자루로 아이를 쿡쿡 찌르고 있었을 뿐입니다. 무슨 까닭에서인지 나는 모르겠고, 그 사람도 모르는 것 같았습니다."], ["I therefore supposed that Mrs. Jellyby was not at home, and was quite surprised when the person appeared in the passage without the pattens, and going up to the back room on the first floor before Ada and me, announced us as, “Them two young ladies, Missis Jellyby!”", "그래서 나는 젤리비 부인이 집에 없으려니 짐작했는데, 그 사람이 나막신을 벗은 채로 복도에 나타나 에이다와 나보다 앞서 이 층 뒤쪽 방으로 올라가더니 \"젤리비 마님, 그 아가씨 두 분이에요!\"라며 우리를 알리는 바람에 적잖이 놀랐습니다."], ["We passed several more children on the way up, whom it was difficult to avoid treading on in the dark; and as we came into Mrs.", "올라가는 길에 우리는 어둠 속에서 밟지 않으려 애써야 하는 아이들 몇몇을 더 지나쳤습니다."], ["Jellyby’s presence, one of the poor little things fell downstairs—down a whole flight (as it sounded to me), with a great noise.", "그리고 우리가 젤리비 부인 앞에 들어서는데, 그 가엾은 어린것 하나가 계단을 굴러떨어졌습니다—한 층을 통째로 (적어도 내 귀에는 그렇게 들렸습니다), 요란한 소리를 내며 말입니다."]], [["Mrs. Jellyby, whose face reflected none of the uneasiness which we could not help showing in our own faces as the dear child’s head recorded its passage with a bump on every stair—Richard afterwards said he counted seven, besides one for the landing—received us with perfect equanimity.", "그 사랑스러운 아이의 머리가 계단마다 쿵쿵 부딪히며 떨어진 자취를 새기는데도—리처드가 나중에 말하기를 층계참 것 하나를 빼고도 일곱 번을 세었다고 했습니다—우리는 어쩔 수 없이 얼굴에 드러난 불안을 젤리비 부인의 얼굴에서는 조금도 찾아볼 수 없었습니다."], ["She was a pretty, very diminutive, plump woman of from forty to fifty, with handsome eyes, though they had a curious habit of seeming to look a long way off.", "부인은 더없이 차분하게 우리를 맞이했습니다. 그녀는 마흔에서 쉰 사이의, 곱고 아주 자그마하며 통통한 여인으로, 아름다운 눈을 지녔지만 그 눈에는 한참 먼 곳을 바라보는 듯한 묘한 버릇이 있었습니다."], ["As if—I am quoting Richard again—they could see nothing nearer than Africa!", "마치—또 리처드의 말을 빌리자면—아프리카보다 가까운 것은 아무것도 보이지 않는다는 듯이 말입니다!"]], [["“I am very glad indeed,” said Mrs.", "\"두 분을 맞이하는 기쁨을 누리게 되어 정말 기쁩니다.\""], ["Jellyby in an agreeable voice, “to have the pleasure of receiving you.", "젤리비 부인이 듣기 좋은 목소리로 말했습니다."], ["I have a great respect for Mr. Jarndyce, and no one in whom he is interested can be an object of indifference to me.”", "\"나는 잔다이스 씨를 무척 존경하고, 그분이 관심을 두는 사람이라면 누구도 내게 무심한 대상이 될 수 없으니까요.\""]], [["We expressed our acknowledgments and sat down behind the door, where there was a lame invalid of a sofa.", "우리는 감사의 뜻을 밝히고 문 뒤에 앉았는데, 거기에는 다리 하나가 망가진 환자 같은 소파가 놓여 있었습니다."], ["Mrs. Jellyby had very good hair but was too much occupied with her African duties to brush it.", "젤리비 부인은 머릿결이 무척 고왔지만 아프리카 일에 너무 바쁜 나머지 머리를 빗지 못했습니다."], ["The shawl in which she had been loosely muffled dropped onto her chair when she advanced to us; and as she turned to resume her seat, we could not help noticing that her dress didn’t nearly meet up the back and that the open space was railed across with a lattice-work of stay-lace—like a summer-house.", "우리에게 다가올 때 부인이 헐겁게 두르고 있던 숄이 의자 위로 흘러내렸고, 자리로 돌아가려고 몸을 돌리는데, 우리는 어쩔 수 없이 알아채고 말았습니다. 부인의 옷이 등 쪽에서 영 여며지지 않았고, 그 벌어진 자리는 코르셋 끈을 격자무늬로 가로질러 엮어두었다는 것을—마치 정자처럼 말입니다."]], [["The room, which was strewn with papers and nearly filled by a great writing-table covered with similar litter, was, I must say, not only very untidy but very dirty.", "종이가 흩어져 있고, 비슷한 잡동사니로 뒤덮인 커다란 책상이 거의 가득 차지하고 있는 그 방은, 솔직히 말해, 무척 어수선할 뿐 아니라 무척 더러웠습니다."], ["We were obliged to take notice of that with our sense of sight, even while, with our sense of hearing, we followed the poor child who had tumbled downstairs: I think into the back kitchen, where somebody seemed to stifle him.", "우리는 시각으로 그것을 알아챌 수밖에 없었는데, 그러는 동안에도 청각으로는 계단에서 굴러떨어진 그 가엾은 아이를 뒤쫓고 있었습니다. 아마 뒤편 부엌으로 간 듯했는데, 거기서 누군가가 아이를 입막음하는 것 같았습니다."]], [["But what principally struck us was a jaded and unhealthy-looking though by no means plain girl at the writing-table, who sat biting the feather of her pen and staring at us.", "하지만 무엇보다 우리 눈길을 끈 것은 책상에 앉아 있는, 지치고 병약해 보이지만 결코 못생기지는 않은 한 소녀였습니다. 소녀는 펜의 깃털을 물어뜯으며 우리를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었습니다."], ["I suppose nobody ever was in such a state of ink.", "그토록 잉크 범벅이 된 사람은 일찍이 없었으리라 싶었습니다."], ["And from her tumbled hair to her pretty feet, which were disfigured with frayed and broken satin slippers trodden down at heel, she really seemed to have no article of dress upon her, from a pin upwards, that was in its proper condition or its right place.", "헝클어진 머리부터, 굽이 닳아빠지고 해어져 터진 새틴 슬리퍼로 흉하게 망가진 고운 발끝까지, 핀에서 위로 단 하나도, 제대로 된 상태이거나 제자리에 있는 옷가지가 정말 없는 듯했습니다."]], [["“You find me, my dears,” said Mrs.", "\"보다시피, 얘들아,\""], ["Jellyby, snuffing the two great office candles in tin candlesticks, which made the room taste strongly of hot tallow (the fire had gone out, and there was nothing in the grate but ashes, a bundle of wood, and a poker), “you find me, my dears, as usual, very busy; but that you will excuse.", "젤리비 부인이 양철 촛대에 꽂힌 큼직한 사무용 양초 두 개의 심지를 잘랐는데, 그 바람에 방에서 뜨거운 짐승 기름 냄새가 진하게 풍겼습니다 (난로 불은 꺼져 있었고, 화격자 안에는 재와 장작 한 묶음, 부지깽이밖에 없었습니다), \"보다시피, 얘들아, 나는 늘 그렇듯 무척 바쁘단다. 하지만 너희가 이해해주리라 믿어."], ["The African project at present employs my whole time.", "지금은 아프리카 사업이 내 온 시간을 잡아먹고 있거든."], ["It involves me in correspondence with public bodies and with private individuals anxious for the welfare of their species all over the country.", "그 일로 나는 공공 단체들과, 또 전국 각지에서 자기 동족의 안녕을 간절히 바라는 개인들과 편지를 주고받느라 분주하단다."], ["I am happy to say it is advancing.", "일이 진척되고 있다고 말할 수 있어 기쁘구나."], ["We hope by this time next year to have from a hundred and fifty to two hundred healthy families cultivating coffee and educating the natives of Borrioboola-Gha, on the left bank of the Niger.”", "우리는 내년 이맘때까지 백오십에서 이백 가구의 건강한 가족이 니제르강 왼쪽 기슭의 보리오불라가에서 커피를 재배하고 원주민을 가르치게 되기를 바라고 있단다.\" * 보리오불라가: 디킨스가 지어낸 가상의 아프리카 지명으로, 헛된 박애주의의 표적을 풍자한다."]], [["As Ada said nothing, but looked at me, I said it must be very gratifying.", "에이다가 아무 말 없이 나를 바라보기에, 내가 무척 흐뭇한 일이겠다고 말했습니다."]], [["“It IS gratifying,”", "\"흐뭇한 일이지.\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby.", "젤리비 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“It involves the devotion of all my energies, such as they are; but that is nothing, so that it succeeds; and I am more confident of success every day.", "\"내 보잘것없는 기력이나마 온통 바쳐야 하는 일이지만, 성공하기만 한다면야 그쯤은 아무것도 아니지. 게다가 나는 날마다 성공을 더 확신하고 있단다."], ["Do you know, Miss Summerson, I almost wonder that YOU never turned your thoughts to Africa.”", "그런데 말이다, 서머슨 양, 나는 어쩐지 네가 한 번도 아프리카로 생각을 돌려본 적이 없다는 게 의아하구나.\""]], [["This application of the subject was really so unexpected to me that I was quite at a loss how to receive it.", "이렇게 화제를 내게 들이미는 것이 정말 너무 뜻밖이어서, 나는 그것을 어떻게 받아넘겨야 할지 도무지 몰랐습니다."], ["I hinted that the climate—", "나는 기후가—하고 넌지시 말을 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“The finest climate in the world!”", "\"세상에서 가장 좋은 기후란다!\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby.", "젤리비 부인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Indeed, ma’am?”", "\"정말요, 부인?\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["With precaution,”", "조심만 하면 말이다.\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby.", "젤리비 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“You may go into Holborn, without precaution, and be run over.", "\"조심하지 않고 홀본에 나갔다가는 마차에 치일 수도 있어."], ["You may go into Holborn, with precaution, and never be run over.", "조심해서 홀본에 나가면 결코 치이지 않지."], ["Just so with Africa.”", "아프리카도 꼭 그렇단다.\""]], [["I said, “No doubt.”", "나는 \"물론 그렇겠지요\"라고 말했습니다."], ["I meant as to Holborn.", "홀본을 두고 한 말이었습니다."]], [["“If you would like,” said Mrs.", "\"괜찮다면,\""], ["Jellyby, putting a number of papers towards us, “to look over some remarks on that head, and on the general subject, which have been extensively circulated, while I finish a letter I am now dictating to my eldest daughter, who is my amanuensis—”", "젤리비 부인이 종이 한 무더기를 우리 쪽으로 밀어주며 말했습니다. \"내가 맏딸—이 아이가 내 필경사거든—에게 지금 받아쓰게 하는 편지를 마저 끝내는 동안, 그 문제와 전반적인 사안에 관해 널리 배포된 소견들을 좀 훑어보려무나—\""]], [["The girl at the table left off biting her pen and made a return to our recognition, which was half bashful and half sulky.", "책상에 앉은 소녀는 펜 물어뜯기를 멈추고, 반은 수줍고 반은 뾰로통한 태도로 우리의 인사에 답례했습니다."]], [["“—I shall then have finished for the present,” proceeded Mrs.", "\"—그러면 지금으로서는 일이 끝나는 셈이지.\""], ["Jellyby with a sweet smile, “though my work is never done.", "젤리비 부인이 상냥한 미소를 띠며 말을 이었습니다. \"하기야 내 일이라는 게 끝이 없지만 말이다."], ["Where are you, Caddy?”", "어디까지 했지, 캐디?\""]], [["“‘Presents her compliments to Mr.", "\"'스왈로 씨께 안부를 전하며, 삼가—'\""], ["Swallow, and begs—’” said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."]], [["“‘And begs,’” said Mrs.", "\"'삼가,'\""], ["Jellyby, dictating, “‘to inform him, in reference to his letter of inquiry on the African project—’ No, Peepy!", "젤리비 부인이 받아쓰게 하며 말했습니다. \"'아프리카 사업에 관한 문의 편지와 관련하여 알려드리고자—'"], ["Not on my account!”", "안 돼, 피피! 나 때문이라면 됐어!\""]], [["Peepy (so self-named) was the unfortunate child who had fallen downstairs, who now interrupted the correspondence by presenting himself, with a strip of plaster on his forehead, to exhibit his wounded knees, in which Ada and I did not know which to pity most—the bruises or the dirt.", "피피는 (스스로 그렇게 부르는) 계단에서 굴러떨어진 그 가엾은 아이였는데, 이번에는 이마에 반창고 한 조각을 붙인 채로 다친 무릎을 내보이려고 나타나 편지 받아쓰기를 가로막았습니다. 그 무릎을 보고 에이다와 나는 멍 자국과 때 가운데 어느 쪽을 더 가엾어해야 할지 알 수 없었습니다."], ["Mrs. Jellyby merely added, with the serene composure with which she said everything, “Go along, you naughty Peepy!”", "젤리비 부인은 모든 말을 할 때와 똑같은 평온한 침착함으로 그저 한마디 덧붙였습니다."], ["and fixed her fine eyes on Africa again.", "\"저리 가, 이 말썽꾸러기 피피!\" 그러고는 다시 아름다운 눈을 아프리카에 고정했습니다."]], [["However, as she at once proceeded with her dictation, and as I interrupted nothing by doing it, I ventured quietly to stop poor Peepy as he was going out and to take him up to nurse.", "하지만 부인이 곧바로 받아쓰기를 이어갔고, 내가 그렇게 한다고 해서 무엇을 가로막는 것도 아니었기에, 나는 가엾은 피피가 나가려는 것을 조용히 붙들어 안아 올렸습니다."], ["He looked very much astonished at it and at Ada’s kissing him, but soon fell fast asleep in my arms, sobbing at longer and longer intervals, until he was quiet.", "아이는 그것과, 또 에이다가 입을 맞추는 것에 몹시 놀란 듯했지만, 이내 내 품에서 흐느낌의 간격이 점점 길어지더니 조용해질 때까지 곤히 잠들었습니다."], ["I was so occupied with Peepy that I lost the letter in detail, though I derived such a general impression from it of the momentous importance of Africa, and the utter insignificance of all other places and things, that I felt quite ashamed to have thought so little about it.", "나는 피피에게 정신이 팔려 편지 내용을 세세히 놓치고 말았지만, 그 편지에서 받은 전반적인 인상이라면, 아프리카가 더없이 중대하고 다른 모든 곳과 모든 것은 하나같이 하찮다는 것이어서, 그 일을 그토록 하찮게 여겨온 것이 정말 부끄러울 지경이었습니다."]], [["“Six o’clock!”", "\"여섯 시구나!\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby.", "젤리비 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“And our dinner hour is nominally (for we dine at all hours) five!", "\"우리 저녁 시간은 명목상 (우리는 아무 때나 먹으니까) 다섯 시인데 말이야!"], ["Caddy, show Miss Clare and Miss Summerson their rooms.", "캐디, 클레어 양과 서머슨 양께 방을 보여드리렴."], ["You will like to make some change, perhaps?", "아마 좀 갈아입고 싶을 테니까?"], ["You will excuse me, I know, being so much occupied.", "내가 이렇게 바쁜 걸, 알다시피, 두 분이 이해해주겠지."], ["Oh, that very bad child!", "어머, 저 못된 녀석!"], ["Pray put him down, Miss Summerson!”", "부디 그 아이를 내려놓으렴, 서머슨 양!\""]], [["I begged permission to retain him, truly saying that he was not at all troublesome, and carried him upstairs and laid him on my bed.", "나는 그 아이가 조금도 성가시지 않다고 진심으로 말하며 계속 안고 있게 해달라고 청한 뒤, 위층으로 데려가 내 침대에 눕혔습니다."], ["Ada and I had two upper rooms with a door of communication between.", "에이다와 나는 위층 방 두 개를 썼는데, 그 사이에는 통하는 문이 하나 있었습니다."], ["They were excessively bare and disorderly, and the curtain to my window was fastened up with a fork.", "방은 휑하기 짝이 없고 어수선했으며, 내 방 창문 커튼은 포크로 고정되어 있었습니다."]], [["“You would like some hot water, wouldn’t you?”", "\"뜨거운 물이 좀 필요하시죠?\""], ["said Miss Jellyby, looking round for a jug with a handle to it, but looking in vain.", "젤리비 양이 손잡이 달린 물주전자를 찾아 둘러보았지만 헛수고였습니다."]], [["“If it is not being troublesome,”", "\"성가시지 않다면요.\""], ["said we.", "우리가 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, it’s not the trouble,”", "\"아, 성가신 게 문제가 아니에요.\""], ["returned Miss Jellyby; “the question is, if there IS any.”", "젤리비 양이 대답했습니다. \"물이 있느냐 없느냐가 문제죠.\""]], [["The evening was so very cold and the rooms had such a marshy smell that I must confess it was a little miserable, and Ada was half crying.", "저녁 공기가 너무 차갑고 방에서는 늪 같은 냄새가 풍겨서, 솔직히 고백하건대 다소 비참한 기분이 들었고, 에이다는 반쯤 울먹였습니다."], ["We soon laughed, however, and were busily unpacking when Miss Jellyby came back to say that she was sorry there was no hot water, but they couldn’t find the kettle, and the boiler was out of order.", "그러나 우리는 곧 웃었고, 한창 짐을 풀고 있는데 젤리비 양이 돌아와 뜨거운 물이 없어 미안하다고, 주전자를 찾지 못한 데다 보일러도 고장 났다고 했습니다."]], [["We begged her not to mention it and made all the haste we could to get down to the fire again.", "우리는 그런 말 마시라며, 서둘러 다시 난롯가로 내려가려고 부랴부랴 채비를 했습니다."], ["But all the little children had come up to the landing outside to look at the phenomenon of Peepy lying on my bed, and our attention was distracted by the constant apparition of noses and fingers in situations of danger between the hinges of the doors.", "그런데 어린것들이 죄다 바깥 층계참으로 올라와 내 침대에 누운 피피라는 진귀한 광경을 구경하고 있어서, 문 경첩 사이 위태로운 자리에 코와 손가락이 쉴 새 없이 나타나는 통에 우리는 정신이 사나웠습니다."], ["It was impossible to shut the door of either room, for my lock, with no knob to it, looked as if it wanted to be wound up; and though the handle of Ada’s went round and round with the greatest smoothness, it was attended with no effect whatever on the door.", "어느 방이든 문을 닫는 것은 불가능했습니다. 내 방 자물쇠는 손잡이가 없어서 마치 태엽을 감아야 할 물건처럼 보였고, 에이다 방의 손잡이는 더없이 매끄럽게 빙빙 돌아갔지만 문에는 아무런 효력도 미치지 못했으니까요."], ["Therefore I proposed to the children that they should come in and be very good at my table, and I would tell them the story of Little Red Riding Hood while I dressed; which they did, and were as quiet as mice, including Peepy, who awoke opportunely before the appearance of the wolf.", "그래서 나는 아이들에게, 들어와서 내 탁자 앞에 얌전히 앉아 있으면 내가 옷을 갈아입는 동안 빨간 모자 소녀 이야기를 들려주겠다고 제안했습니다. 아이들은 그대로 따랐고, 늑대가 나타나기 직전에 마침맞게 잠에서 깬 피피까지 포함해서, 다들 생쥐처럼 조용했습니다."]], [["When we went downstairs we found a mug with “A Present from Tunbridge Wells” on it lighted up in the staircase window with a floating wick, and a young woman, with a swelled face bound up in a flannel bandage blowing the fire of the drawing-room (now connected by an open door with Mrs.", "아래층으로 내려가니 계단 창문에는 \"턴브리지웰스에서 온 선물\"이라고 적힌 머그잔이 둥둥 뜬 심지를 켜 밝히고 있었고, 한 젊은 여자가 부어오른 얼굴을 플란넬 붕대로 싸맨 채 응접실 (지금은 열린 문으로 젤리비 부인 방과 이어져 있었습니다) 난롯불을 후후 불며 무섭게 콜록거리고 있었습니다."], ["Jellyby’s room) and choking dreadfully. It smoked to that degree, in short, that we all sat coughing and crying with the windows open for half an hour, during which Mrs.", "한마디로 연기가 어찌나 자욱한지, 우리는 모두 창문을 열어둔 채 반 시간이나 기침하고 눈물을 흘리며 앉아 있었고, 그동안 젤리비 부인은 한결같이 온화한 성미로 아프리카에 관한 편지를 받아쓰게 하고 있었습니다."], ["Jellyby, with the same sweetness of temper, directed letters about Africa. Her being so employed was, I must say, a great relief to me, for Richard told us that he had washed his hands in a pie-dish and that they had found the kettle on his dressing-table, and he made Ada laugh so that they made me laugh in the most ridiculous manner.", "솔직히 부인이 그렇게 바쁜 것이 내게는 큰 다행이었는데, 리처드가 우리에게, 자기는 파이 접시에 손을 씻었고 화장대 위에서 주전자를 찾았다고 하면서 에이다를 어찌나 웃기던지, 둘이서 나까지 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없게 웃게 만들었기 때문입니다."]], [["Soon after seven o’clock we went down to dinner, carefully, by Mrs.", "일곱 시가 조금 지나 우리는 저녁을 먹으러 내려갔는데, 젤리비 부인의 충고에 따라 조심조심 내려갔습니다."], ["Jellyby’s advice, for the stair-carpets, besides being very deficient in stair-wires, were so torn as to be absolute traps.", "계단 양탄자는 고정용 철사가 한참 모자란 데다 어찌나 찢어졌는지 완전히 덫이나 다름없었으니까요."], ["We had a fine cod-fish, a piece of roast beef, a dish of cutlets, and a pudding; an excellent dinner, if it had had any cooking to speak of, but it was almost raw.", "우리는 좋은 대구 한 마리와 구운 쇠고기 한 덩이, 커틀릿 한 접시와 푸딩을 먹었습니다. 변변히 조리만 했더라면 훌륭한 저녁이었겠지만, 거의 날것이었습니다."], ["The young woman with the flannel bandage waited, and dropped everything on the table wherever it happened to go, and never moved it again until she put it on the stairs.", "플란넬 붕대를 한 젊은 여자가 시중을 들었는데, 닿는 대로 아무 데나 음식을 식탁에 툭 내려놓고는 그것을 계단에 갖다 둘 때까지 다시는 옮기지 않았습니다."], ["The person I had seen in pattens, who I suppose to have been the cook, frequently came and skirmished with her at the door, and there appeared to be ill will between them.", "내가 나막신을 신고 있는 모습을 본 그 사람은, 짐작건대 요리사였던 듯한데, 자주 문간으로 와서 그 여자와 옥신각신했고, 둘 사이에는 악감정이 있는 듯했습니다."]], [["All through dinner—which was long, in consequence of such accidents as the dish of potatoes being mislaid in the coal skuttle and the handle of the corkscrew coming off and striking the young woman in the chin—Mrs.", "저녁 식사 내내—감자 접시가 석탄통 속에 잘못 놓이고, 코르크 따개 손잡이가 빠져 젊은 여자의 턱을 친 따위의 사고 탓에 식사는 길어졌습니다—젤리비 부인은 한결같은 성미를 지켰습니다."], ["Jellyby preserved the evenness of her disposition. She told us a great deal that was interesting about Borrioboola-Gha and the natives, and received so many letters that Richard, who sat by her, saw four envelopes in the gravy at once.", "부인은 보리오불라가와 원주민에 관해 흥미로운 이야기를 잔뜩 들려주었고, 어찌나 많은 편지를 받았던지 그 곁에 앉은 리처드는 한꺼번에 봉투 네 개가 그레이비 소스에 빠져 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Some of the letters were proceedings of ladies’ committees or resolutions of ladies’ meetings, which she read to us; others were applications from people excited in various ways about the cultivation of coffee, and natives; others required answers, and these she sent her eldest daughter from the table three or four times to write.", "어떤 편지는 부인회 회의록이나 부인회 결의문이어서 우리에게 읽어주었고, 어떤 편지는 커피 재배와 원주민 문제로 이런저런 흥분에 빠진 사람들의 청원이었으며, 어떤 편지는 답장이 필요해서 부인은 그것을 쓰라고 식탁에서 맏딸을 서너 번이나 내보냈습니다."], ["She was full of business and undoubtedly was, as she had told us, devoted to the cause.", "부인은 일에 파묻혀 있었고, 우리에게 말한 대로, 분명 그 대의에 헌신하고 있었습니다."]], [["I was a little curious to know who a mild bald gentleman in spectacles was, who dropped into a vacant chair (there was no top or bottom in particular) after the fish was taken away and seemed passively to submit himself to Borrioboola-Gha but not to be actively interested in that settlement. As he never spoke a word, he might have been a native but for his complexion.", "생선이 치워진 뒤에 빈 의자 하나에 (특별히 상석이나 말석이랄 것도 없었습니다) 슬그머니 앉은, 안경을 쓴 온순하고 머리 벗어진 신사가 누구인지 나는 조금 궁금했습니다."], ["It was not until we left the table and he remained alone with Richard that the possibility of his being Mr. Jellyby ever entered my head.", "그는 보리오불라가에 수동적으로 자신을 내맡긴 듯했지만, 그 정착지에 능동적으로 관심을 두지는 않는 듯했습니다."], ["But he WAS Mr. Jellyby; and a loquacious young man called Mr.", "한마디도 입을 떼지 않아서, 피부색만 아니었다면 원주민이라 해도 믿었을 것입니다."], ["Quale, with large shining knobs for temples and his hair all brushed to the back of his head, who came in the evening, and told Ada he was a philanthropist, also informed her that he called the matrimonial alliance of Mrs.", "우리가 식탁을 떠나고 그가 리처드와 단둘이 남았을 때에야 비로소, 그가 젤리비 씨일지도 모른다는 생각이 내 머리에 떠올랐습니다. 그런데 그가 정말 젤리비 씨였습니다."], ["Jellyby with Mr. Jellyby the union of mind and matter.", "저녁에 찾아온, 관자놀이가 크고 번들거리는 혹처럼 솟고 머리카락을 모조리 뒤로 빗어 넘긴 퀘일 씨라는 수다스러운 젊은이가, 자신은 자선가라고 에이다에게 말하면서, 젤리비 부인과 젤리비 씨의 혼인 결합을 정신과 물질의 결합이라 부른다고 일러주었으니까요."]], [["This young man, besides having a great deal to say for himself about Africa and a project of his for teaching the coffee colonists to teach the natives to turn piano-forte legs and establish an export trade, delighted in drawing Mrs. Jellyby out by saying, “I believe now, Mrs.", "이 젊은이는 아프리카에 관해, 그리고 커피 식민지 개척자들에게 원주민이 피아노 다리를 깎는 법을 가르치게 하여 수출업을 일으키겠다는 자신의 사업 계획에 관해 할 말이 잔뜩 있을 뿐 아니라, 젤리비 부인의 말을 끌어내는 데 흥을 느꼈습니다."], ["Jellyby, you have received as many as from one hundred and fifty to two hundred letters respecting Africa in a single day, have you not?”", "\"젤리비 부인, 이제 보니 부인께서는 단 하루에 아프리카에 관한 편지를 백오십에서 이백 통까지 받으신 적이 있다지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""], ["or, “If my memory does not deceive me, Mrs. Jellyby, you once mentioned that you had sent off five thousand circulars from one post-office at one time?”—always repeating Mrs.", "라거나, \"제 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 젤리비 부인, 한번은 한 우체국에서 한꺼번에 회람 오천 부를 발송하셨다고 말씀하신 적이 있지요?\""], ["Jellyby’s answer to us like an interpreter. During the whole evening, Mr.", "같은 식으로 말입니다—그러고는 늘 통역사처럼 젤리비 부인의 대답을 우리에게 되풀이해주었습니다."], ["Jellyby sat in a corner with his head against the wall as if he were subject to low spirits.", "저녁 내내 젤리비 씨는 마치 의기소침에 시달리는 사람처럼 벽에 머리를 기댄 채 구석에 앉아 있었습니다."], ["It seemed that he had several times opened his mouth when alone with Richard after dinner, as if he had something on his mind, but had always shut it again, to Richard’s extreme confusion, without saying anything.", "저녁 식사 후 리처드와 단둘이 있을 때 그는, 마음에 무슨 사연이라도 있는 듯 여러 번 입을 열었다가, 늘 아무 말도 없이 다시 다물어버려서, 리처드를 몹시 당혹스럽게 했던 모양입니다."]], [["Mrs. Jellyby, sitting in quite a nest of waste paper, drank coffee all the evening and dictated at intervals to her eldest daughter.", "젤리비 부인은 폐지가 그득한 둥지 같은 곳에 앉아 저녁 내내 커피를 마시며 틈틈이 맏딸에게 받아쓰게 했습니다."], ["She also held a discussion with Mr. Quale, of which the subject seemed to be—if I understood it—the brotherhood of humanity, and gave utterance to some beautiful sentiments.", "부인은 또 퀘일 씨와 토론도 벌였는데, 그 주제는—내가 제대로 이해했다면—인류의 형제애인 듯했고, 아름다운 정서를 몇 마디 토로하기도 했습니다."], ["I was not so attentive an auditor as I might have wished to be, however, for Peepy and the other children came flocking about Ada and me in a corner of the drawing-room to ask for another story; so we sat down among them and told them in whispers “Puss in Boots”", "하지만 나는 바라던 만큼 귀 기울여 듣는 청자가 되지 못했는데, 피피와 다른 아이들이 응접실 구석에 있는 에이다와 내 곁으로 몰려들어 이야기를 하나 더 해달라고 졸랐기 때문입니다."], ["and I don’t know what else until Mrs. Jellyby, accidentally remembering them, sent them to bed.", "그래서 우리는 아이들 틈에 앉아 속삭이며 \"장화 신은 고양이\"며 그 밖에 무엇인지 모를 이야기들을 들려주다가, 젤리비 부인이 문득 아이들을 떠올리고는 잠자리로 보냈습니다."], ["As Peepy cried for me to take him to bed, I carried him upstairs, where the young woman with the flannel bandage charged into the midst of the little family like a dragon and overturned them into cribs.", "피피가 나더러 재워달라고 울어대기에 나는 아이를 위층으로 안고 올라갔는데, 거기서 플란넬 붕대를 한 젊은 여자가 용처럼 그 어린 식구들 한복판으로 달려들어 아이들을 아기 침대에 들이뒤집어 놓았습니다."]], [["After that I occupied myself in making our room a little tidy and in coaxing a very cross fire that had been lighted to burn, which at last it did, quite brightly. On my return downstairs, I felt that Mrs.", "그 후 나는 우리 방을 조금 치우고, 불을 붙여놓았지만 영 심술궂게 굴던 난로를 어르고 달래 타오르게 하는 일에 매달렸는데, 마침내 불이 아주 환하게 붙었습니다."], ["Jellyby looked down upon me rather for being so frivolous, and I was sorry for it, though at the same time I knew that I had no higher pretensions.", "다시 아래층으로 내려가면서, 나는 젤리비 부인이 내가 그토록 시시한 일에 매달린 것을 다소 깔보는 듯한 눈길로 본다는 것을 느꼈고, 그것이 안타까웠습니다. 하지만 동시에 나는 나 자신에게 그보다 더 거창한 자부심이 없다는 것도 알고 있었습니다."]], [["It was nearly midnight before we found an opportunity of going to bed, and even then we left Mrs. Jellyby among her papers drinking coffee and Miss Jellyby biting the feather of her pen.", "잠자리에 들 기회를 잡은 것은 거의 자정이 다 되어서였고, 그때조차 우리는 종이에 파묻혀 커피를 마시는 젤리비 부인과 펜의 깃털을 물어뜯는 젤리비 양을 그대로 두고 올라왔습니다."]], [["“What a strange house!”", "\"정말 이상한 집이야!\""], ["said Ada when we got upstairs.", "위층에 올라오자 에이다가 말했습니다."], ["“How curious of my cousin Jarndyce to send us here!”", "\"사촌 잔다이스 씨가 우리를 여기로 보내다니, 어쩜 이렇게 별나실까!\""]], [["“My love,”", "\"얘,\""], ["said I, “it quite confuses me.", "내가 말했습니다. \"정말 어리둥절해."], ["I want to understand it, and I can’t understand it at all.”", "이해하고 싶은데, 도무지 이해할 수가 없어.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐가?\""], ["asked Ada with her pretty smile.", "에이다가 고운 미소를 띠며 물었습니다."]], [["“All this, my dear,” said I.", "\"이 모든 게 말이야.\""], ["“It MUST be very good of Mrs.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["Jellyby to take such pains about a scheme for the benefit of natives—and yet—Peepy and the housekeeping!”", "\"젤리비 부인이 원주민을 위한 계획에 이토록 공을 들이는 건 분명 무척 갸륵한 일인데—그런데도—피피며 살림살이며!\""]], [["Ada laughed and put her arm about my neck as I stood looking at the fire, and told me I was a quiet, dear, good creature and had won her heart.", "에이다는 웃으며, 내가 난롯불을 바라보고 서 있는데 내 목에 팔을 두르고는, 나더러 조용하고 사랑스럽고 착한 사람이라 자기 마음을 사로잡았다고 말했습니다."], ["“You are so thoughtful, Esther,”", "\"넌 참 사려 깊어, 에스더.\""], ["she said, “and yet so cheerful!", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"그런데도 그렇게 명랑하잖아!"], ["And you do so much, so unpretendingly!", "게다가 그렇게나 많은 일을, 조금도 내세우지 않고 해내고!"], ["You would make a home out of even this house.”", "너라면 이런 집에서도 가정을 일굴 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["My simple darling!", "순진한 내 사랑스러운 친구!"], ["She was quite unconscious that she only praised herself and that it was in the goodness of her own heart that she made so much of me!", "그녀는 자기가 자신을 칭찬하고 있을 뿐이라는 것도, 자기 마음씨가 착하기에 나를 그토록 추켜세운다는 것도 전혀 깨닫지 못했습니다!"]], [["“May I ask you a question?”", "\"하나 물어봐도 될까?\""], ["said I when we had sat before the fire a little while.", "우리가 난롯가에 잠시 앉아 있다가 내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Five hundred,”", "\"오백 개라도.\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했습니다."]], [["“Your cousin, Mr. Jarndyce.", "\"네 사촌, 잔다이스 씨 말이야."], ["I owe so much to him.", "내가 그분께 신세를 너무 많이 졌잖아."], ["Would you mind describing him to me?”", "그분을 좀 설명해줄 수 있겠니?\""]], [["Shaking her golden hair, Ada turned her eyes upon me with such laughing wonder that I was full of wonder too, partly at her beauty, partly at her surprise.", "에이다는 황금빛 머리를 흔들며, 어찌나 웃음 어린 놀라움으로 내게 눈을 돌리는지, 나도 가슴 가득 놀라움이 차올랐습니다. 한편으로는 그녀의 아름다움에, 한편으로는 그녀의 놀라움에 말입니다."]], [["“Esther!”", "\"에스더!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤습니다."]], [["“My dear!”", "\"얘!\""]], [["“You want a description of my cousin Jarndyce?”", "\"내 사촌 잔다이스 씨를 설명해달라고?\""]], [["“My dear, I never saw him.”", "\"얘, 난 그분을 본 적이 없잖아.\""]], [["“And I never saw him!”", "\"나도 그분을 본 적이 없어!\""], ["returned Ada.", "에이다가 대답했습니다."]], [["Well, to be sure!", "세상에, 정말이지!"]], [["No, she had never seen him.", "그래요, 그녀는 그분을 한 번도 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["Young as she was when her mama died, she remembered how the tears would come into her eyes when she spoke of him and of the noble generosity of his character, which she had said was to be trusted above all earthly things; and Ada trusted it.", "어머니가 돌아가셨을 때 그녀는 어렸지만, 어머니가 그분 이야기와 그분의 고결하고 너그러운 성품—세상 그 무엇보다 믿을 만하다고 어머니가 말한 그 성품—을 말할 때면 눈에 눈물이 차오르던 것을 기억했습니다. 에이다는 그 말을 믿었습니다."], ["Her cousin Jarndyce had written to her a few months ago—“a plain, honest letter,”", "사촌 잔다이스 씨는 몇 달 전에 그녀에게 편지를 보냈는데—\"꾸밈없고 진솔한 편지였어.\""], ["Ada said—proposing the arrangement we were now to enter on and telling her that “in time it might heal some of the wounds made by the miserable Chancery suit.”", "라고 에이다가 말했습니다—우리가 이제 들어서게 된 이 조처를 제안하면서, \"머지않아 그 비참한 대법원 소송이 낸 상처를 얼마간 아물게 할 수도 있을 거라\"고 적었습니다."], ["She had replied, gratefully accepting his proposal.", "그녀는 고마운 마음으로 그 제안을 받아들이겠다는 답장을 보냈습니다."], ["Richard had received a similar letter and had made a similar response.", "리처드도 비슷한 편지를 받고 비슷한 답을 했습니다."], ["He HAD seen Mr. Jarndyce once, but only once, five years ago, at Winchester school.", "그는 오 년 전 윈체스터 학교에서 잔다이스 씨를 한 번, 딱 한 번 본 적이 있었습니다."], ["He had told Ada, when they were leaning on the screen before the fire where I found them, that he recollected him as “a bluff, rosy fellow.”", "내가 두 사람을 발견했을 때 난롯불 앞 가리개에 기대어 있던 리처드는, 에이다에게 그분을 \"활달하고 혈색 좋은 사람\"으로 기억한다고 말했습니다."], ["This was the utmost description Ada could give me.", "이것이 에이다가 내게 해줄 수 있는 설명의 전부였습니다."]], [["It set me thinking so that when Ada was asleep, I still remained before the fire, wondering and wondering about Bleak House, and wondering and wondering that yesterday morning should seem so long ago.", "그 일이 어찌나 나를 골똘하게 했는지, 에이다가 잠든 뒤에도 나는 여전히 난롯가에 남아 황폐한 집에 대해 자꾸자꾸 궁금해했고, 어제 아침이 어쩌면 이토록 까마득하게 느껴질까 하고 자꾸자꾸 의아해했습니다."], ["I don’t know where my thoughts had wandered when they were recalled by a tap at the door.", "문 두드리는 소리에 정신이 돌아왔을 때, 내 생각이 어디를 헤매고 있었는지 모르겠습니다."]], [["I opened it softly and found Miss Jellyby shivering there with a broken candle in a broken candlestick in one hand and an egg-cup in the other.", "나는 살그머니 문을 열었고, 젤리비 양이 한 손에는 부러진 촛대에 꽂힌 동강 난 양초를, 다른 손에는 달걀 잔을 들고 거기서 떨고 있는 것을 발견했습니다."]], [["“Good night!”", "\"안녕히 주무세요!\""], ["she said very sulkily.", "그녀가 무척 뾰로통하게 말했습니다."]], [["“Good night!”", "\"잘 자요!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“May I come in?”", "\"들어가도 돼요?\""], ["she shortly and unexpectedly asked me in the same sulky way.", "그녀가 똑같이 뾰로통한 태도로 짧고도 뜻밖에 내게 물었습니다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"그럼요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“Don’t wake Miss Clare.”", "\"클레어 양은 깨우지 말고요.\""]], [["She would not sit down, but stood by the fire dipping her inky middle finger in the egg-cup, which contained vinegar, and smearing it over the ink stains on her face, frowning the whole time and looking very gloomy.", "그녀는 앉으려 하지 않고 난롯가에 서서, 식초가 담긴 달걀 잔에 잉크 묻은 가운뎃손가락을 적셔 얼굴의 잉크 자국에 문질러대면서, 내내 얼굴을 찌푸리며 무척 침울한 표정을 지었습니다."]], [["“I wish Africa was dead!”", "\"아프리카 따위 없어졌으면 좋겠어요!\""], ["she said on a sudden.", "그녀가 불쑥 말했습니다."]], [["I was going to remonstrate.", "나는 타이르려 했습니다."]], [["“I do!”", "\"정말이에요!\""], ["she said “Don’t talk to me, Miss Summerson.", "그녀가 말했습니다. \"내게 아무 말 마세요, 서머슨 양."], ["I hate it and detest it.", "난 그게 밉고 끔찍해요."], ["It’s a beast!”", "그건 짐승이에요!\""]], [["I told her she was tired, and I was sorry.", "나는 그녀에게 피곤한 거라고, 안됐다고 말했습니다."], ["I put my hand upon her head, and touched her forehead, and said it was hot now but would be cool to-morrow.", "나는 그녀의 머리에 손을 얹고 이마를 짚으며, 지금은 뜨겁지만 내일이면 식을 거라고 말했습니다."], ["She still stood pouting and frowning at me, but presently put down her egg-cup and turned softly towards the bed where Ada lay.", "그녀는 여전히 입을 삐죽이며 나를 노려보고 서 있었지만, 이내 달걀 잔을 내려놓고 에이다가 누운 침대 쪽으로 살며시 몸을 돌렸습니다."]], [["“She is very pretty!”", "\"참 예뻐요!\""], ["she said with the same knitted brow and in the same uncivil manner.", "똑같이 찌푸린 이마로, 똑같이 무뚝뚝한 태도로 그녀가 말했습니다."]], [["I assented with a smile.", "나는 미소로 동의했습니다."]], [["“An orphan.", "\"고아죠."], ["Ain’t she?”", "그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"맞아요.\""]], [["“But knows a quantity, I suppose?", "\"그래도 아는 게 많겠죠?"], ["Can dance, and play music, and sing?", "춤출 줄도 알고, 악기도 다루고, 노래도 부르고?"], ["She can talk French, I suppose, and do geography, and globes, and needlework, and everything?”", "프랑스어도 할 줄 알고, 지리며 지구의며 바느질이며 뭐든 다 하겠죠?\""]], [["“No doubt,”", "\"틀림없이요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“I can’t,”", "\"난 못 해요.\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대답했습니다."], ["“I can’t do anything hardly, except write.", "\"쓰는 것 말고는 거의 아무것도 못 해요."], ["I’m always writing for Ma.", "난 늘 엄마 대신 글을 써요."], ["I wonder you two were not ashamed of yourselves to come in this afternoon and see me able to do nothing else.", "두 분은 오늘 오후에 들어와서 내가 그것밖에 못 하는 걸 보고도 부끄러운 줄 모르더군요."], ["It was like your ill nature.", "꼭 두 분 못된 성미 같았어요."], ["Yet you think yourselves very fine, I dare say!”", "그래도 두 분은 자기가 아주 잘난 줄 알겠죠, 분명히!\""]], [["I could see that the poor girl was near crying, and I resumed my chair without speaking and looked at her (I hope) as mildly as I felt towards her.", "나는 그 가엾은 소녀가 금방이라도 울 듯한 것을 알아챘고, 아무 말 없이 다시 의자에 앉아 그녀를 (바라건대) 내 마음만큼이나 부드럽게 바라보았습니다."]], [["“It’s disgraceful,”", "\"창피한 일이에요.\""], ["she said. “You know it is.", "그녀가 말했습니다."], ["The whole house is disgraceful.", "\"당신도 알잖아요. 이 집 전체가 창피해요."], ["The children are disgraceful.", "아이들도 창피하고요."], ["I’M disgraceful.", "나도 창피해요."], ["Pa’s miserable, and no wonder!", "아빠는 비참하고, 그럴 만도 하죠!"], ["Priscilla drinks—she’s always drinking.", "프리실라는 술을 마셔요—늘 마셔대요."], ["It’s a great shame and a great story of you if you say you didn’t smell her to-day.", "오늘 그 여자한테서 술 냄새를 못 맡았다고 한다면 그건 큰 흉이고 새빨간 거짓말이에요."], ["It was as bad as a public-house, waiting at dinner; you know it was!”", "저녁 시중을 들 때 술집이나 다름없었어요. 당신도 알잖아요!\""]], [["“My dear, I don’t know it,”", "\"얘, 난 몰라요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“You do,”", "\"알아요.\""], ["she said very shortly.", "그녀가 아주 매몰차게 말했습니다."], ["“You shan’t say you don’t.", "\"모른다는 말은 하지 말아요."], ["You do!”", "당신은 알아요!\""]], [["“Oh, my dear!” said I.", "\"어머, 얘!\""], ["“If you won’t let me speak—”", "내가 말했습니다. \"말도 못 하게 하면—\""]], [["“You’re speaking now.", "\"지금 말하고 있잖아요."], ["You know you are.", "당신도 알잖아요."], ["Don’t tell stories, Miss Summerson.”", "거짓말하지 마세요, 서머슨 양.\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘,\""], ["said I, “as long as you won’t hear me out—”", "내가 말했습니다. \"네가 내 말을 끝까지 들어주지 않는 한—\""]], [["“I don’t want to hear you out.”", "\"끝까지 듣고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, I think you do,”", "\"아니, 듣고 싶을 거예요.\""], ["said I, “because that would be so very unreasonable.", "내가 말했습니다. \"안 그러면 너무 억지스러우니까."], ["I did not know what you tell me because the servant did not come near me at dinner; but I don’t doubt what you tell me, and I am sorry to hear it.”", "저녁 식사 때 하인이 내 곁에 오지 않아서 네가 하는 말을 몰랐던 거예요. 하지만 네 말을 의심하지 않고, 그런 얘기를 들으니 안타까워요.\""]], [["“You needn’t make a merit of that,”", "\"그걸 가지고 잘난 척할 건 없어요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했습니다."]], [["“No, my dear,”", "\"그래, 얘.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“That would be very foolish.”", "\"그러면 아주 어리석은 짓이겠지.\""]], [["She was still standing by the bed, and now stooped down (but still with the same discontented face) and kissed Ada.", "그녀는 여전히 침대 곁에 서 있다가, 이제 (그러나 여전히 똑같이 못마땅한 얼굴로) 몸을 굽혀 에이다에게 입을 맞추었습니다."], ["That done, she came softly back and stood by the side of my chair.", "그러고는 살며시 돌아와 내 의자 옆에 섰습니다."], ["Her bosom was heaving in a distressful manner that I greatly pitied, but I thought it better not to speak.", "그녀의 가슴은 보기 안타까울 만큼 괴롭게 들썩였는데, 나는 그것이 무척 가엾었지만 말하지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다."]], [["“I wish I was dead!”", "\"차라리 죽어버렸으면 좋겠어요!\""], ["she broke out.", "그녀가 터뜨렸습니다."], ["“I wish we were all dead.", "\"우리 모두 죽어버렸으면 좋겠어요."], ["It would be a great deal better for us.”", "그게 우리한테 훨씬 나을 거예요.\""]], [["In a moment afterwards, she knelt on the ground at my side, hid her face in my dress, passionately begged my pardon, and wept.", "잠시 뒤에 그녀는 내 곁 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 내 옷자락에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 격하게 용서를 빌며 울었습니다."], ["I comforted her and would have raised her, but she cried no, no; she wanted to stay there!", "나는 그녀를 달래며 일으키려 했지만, 그녀는 싫다고, 싫다고, 거기 그대로 있고 싶다고 울먹였습니다!"]], [["“You used to teach girls,”", "\"당신은 여자아이들을 가르쳤다죠.\""], ["she said, “If you could only have taught me, I could have learnt from you!", "그녀가 말했습니다. \"당신이 나를 가르쳐주기만 했다면, 나도 당신한테 배울 수 있었을 텐데!"], ["I am so very miserable, and I like you so much!”", "난 너무나 비참한데, 당신이 너무 좋아요!\""]], [["I could not persuade her to sit by me or to do anything but move a ragged stool to where she was kneeling, and take that, and still hold my dress in the same manner.", "나는 그녀를 내 곁에 앉게 하거나 그 무엇이라도 하게끔 달랠 수 없었습니다. 그녀는 그저 너덜너덜한 발판 하나를 자기가 무릎 꿇은 자리로 끌어와 그 위에 앉고는, 여전히 똑같이 내 옷자락을 붙들고 있을 뿐이었습니다."], ["By degrees the poor tired girl fell asleep, and then I contrived to raise her head so that it should rest on my lap, and to cover us both with shawls.", "그러다 그 가엾고 지친 소녀는 차츰 잠이 들었고, 그제야 나는 그녀의 머리가 내 무릎에 놓이도록 들어 올려 우리 둘을 숄로 덮었습니다."], ["The fire went out, and all night long she slumbered thus before the ashy grate.", "난롯불은 꺼졌고, 그녀는 밤새도록 잿빛으로 식은 화격자 앞에서 그렇게 잠을 잤습니다."], ["At first I was painfully awake and vainly tried to lose myself, with my eyes closed, among the scenes of the day.", "처음에 나는 괴롭도록 말똥말똥해서, 눈을 감은 채 그날의 장면들 속으로 빠져들어 보려 애썼지만 헛수고였습니다."], ["At length, by slow degrees, they became indistinct and mingled.", "마침내 그 장면들이 더디고 더디게 흐릿해지더니 뒤섞였습니다."], ["I began to lose the identity of the sleeper resting on me.", "나는 내게 기댄 잠든 사람이 누구인지 알아볼 수 없게 되었습니다."], ["Now it was Ada, now one of my old Reading friends from whom I could not believe I had so recently parted.", "어떤 때는 에이다였다가, 어떤 때는 그토록 최근에 헤어졌다는 것이 믿기지 않는 리딩 시절의 옛 친구 가운데 하나였습니다."], ["Now it was the little mad woman worn out with curtsying and smiling, now some one in authority at Bleak House.", "어떤 때는 무릎 굽혀 인사하고 미소 짓느라 기진맥진한 그 가엾은 미친 여인이었다가, 어떤 때는 황폐한 집의 권위 있는 누군가였습니다."], ["Lastly, it was no one, and I was no one.", "마지막에는 아무도 아니었고, 나 또한 아무도 아니었습니다."]], [["The purblind day was feebly struggling with the fog when I opened my eyes to encounter those of a dirty-faced little spectre fixed upon me.", "눈을 떠 보니, 흐릿한 낮이 안개와 힘없이 씨름하고 있었고, 더러운 얼굴의 작은 유령이 나를 빤히 응시하는 눈과 마주쳤습니다."], ["Peepy had scaled his crib, and crept down in his bed-gown and cap, and was so cold that his teeth were chattering as if he had cut them all.", "피피가 아기 침대를 기어 넘어, 잠옷과 모자 차림으로 내려와 있었던 것입니다. 아이는 어찌나 추웠던지, 마치 이가 죄다 새로 난 것처럼 딱딱 부딪치고 있었습니다."]], [["A Morning Adventure\nAlthough the morning was raw, and although the fog still seemed heavy—I say seemed, for the windows were so encrusted with dirt that they would have made midsummer sunshine dim—I was sufficiently forewarned of the discomfort within doors at that early hour and sufficiently curious about London to think it a good idea on the part of Miss Jellyby when she proposed that we should go out for a walk.", "아침은 쌀쌀했고, 안개도 여전히 자욱한 듯했지만 — 굳이 \"듯했다\"고 적는 까닭은, 창문이 온통 때로 뒤덮여 한여름 햇살조차 침침하게 만들 지경이었기 때문입니다 — 그 이른 시각의 집 안이 얼마나 불편할지 충분히 짐작했고 런던도 무척 궁금했던 터라, 젤리비 양이 함께 산책을 나가자고 했을 때 저는 참 좋은 생각이라고 여겼습니다."]], [["“Ma won’t be down for ever so long,”", "\"엄마는 한참 동안 안 내려오실 거예요.\""], ["she said, “and then it’s a chance if breakfast’s ready for an hour afterwards, they dawdle so.", "젤리비 양이 말했습니다. \"그러고 나서도 한 시간은 지나야 아침이 차려질까 말까 하고요."], ["As to Pa, he gets what he can and goes to the office.", "다들 어찌나 꾸물대는지. 아빠로 말하면, 손에 잡히는 대로 챙겨 드시고 사무실로 가요."], ["He never has what you would call a regular breakfast.", "제대로 된 아침 식사라고 부를 만한 건 한 번도 못 드세요."], ["Priscilla leaves him out the loaf and some milk, when there is any, overnight.", "프리실라가 전날 밤에 빵 한 덩이랑, 있으면 우유도 좀 내놓아 두거든요."], ["Sometimes there isn’t any milk, and sometimes the cat drinks it.", "그런데 우유가 없을 때도 있고, 어떤 때는 고양이가 마셔버려요."], ["But I’m afraid you must be tired, Miss Summerson, and perhaps you would rather go to bed.”", "그래도 서머슨 양, 피곤하실 텐데요. 차라리 주무시는 게 낫지 않을까요?\""]], [["“I am not at all tired, my dear,”", "\"전혀 피곤하지 않아요.\""], ["said I, “and would much prefer to go out.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"오히려 나가고 싶어요.\""]], [["“If you’re sure you would,”", "\"정말 그러시다면,\""], ["returned Miss Jellyby, “I’ll get my things on.”", "젤리비 양이 대꾸했습니다. \"제 옷가지를 챙겨 입을게요.\""]], [["Ada said she would go too, and was soon astir.", "에이다도 같이 가겠다고 하더니 곧 움직이기 시작했습니다."], ["I made a proposal to Peepy, in default of being able to do anything better for him, that he should let me wash him and afterwards lay him down on my bed again.", "저는 피피에게 더 나은 무언가를 해줄 수가 없어서, 일단 씻겨주고 다시 제 침대에 눕혀주겠다고 제안했습니다."], ["To this he submitted with the best grace possible, staring at me during the whole operation as if he never had been, and never could again be, so astonished in his life—looking very miserable also, certainly, but making no complaint, and going snugly to sleep as soon as it was over.", "피피는 더없이 얌전하게 응했는데, 씻는 내내 저를 빤히 쳐다보는 것이, 마치 평생 그토록 놀라본 적이 없고 앞으로도 다시는 없을 것 같은 표정이었습니다 — 분명 무척 처량해 보이기도 했지만 불평 한마디 없이, 다 끝나자마자 새근새근 잠들었습니다."], ["At first I was in two minds about taking such a liberty, but I soon reflected that nobody in the house was likely to notice it.", "처음에는 그렇게 멋대로 굴어도 될지 마음이 두 갈래로 나뉘었지만, 이 집에서는 그런 일을 눈여겨볼 사람이 아무도 없으리라는 생각이 이내 들었습니다."]], [["What with the bustle of dispatching Peepy and the bustle of getting myself ready and helping Ada, I was soon quite in a glow.", "피피를 재워 보내느라 부산을 떨고, 또 제 채비를 하고 에이다까지 거드느라 부산을 떠는 사이에 저는 어느새 온몸이 후끈 달아올랐습니다."], ["We found Miss Jellyby trying to warm herself at the fire in the writing-room, which Priscilla was then lighting with a smutty parlour candlestick, throwing the candle in to make it burn better.", "우리가 보니 젤리비 양은 글방 난롯불에 몸을 녹이려 애쓰고 있었는데, 그때 프리실라가 그을음투성이 응접실 촛대로 불을 붙이느라 양초를 통째로 던져 넣어 더 잘 타게 하고 있었습니다."], ["Everything was just as we had left it last night and was evidently intended to remain so.", "모든 것이 어젯밤 우리가 두고 간 그대로였고, 앞으로도 그대로 둘 작정인 게 분명했습니다."], ["Below-stairs the dinner-cloth had not been taken away, but had been left ready for breakfast.", "아래층에는 만찬 식탁보가 그대로 깔린 채 아침상으로 쓰려고 남겨져 있었습니다."], ["Crumbs, dust, and waste-paper were all over the house.", "빵 부스러기며 먼지며 휴지가 온 집 안에 널려 있었습니다."], ["Some pewter pots and a milk-can hung on the area railings; the door stood open; and we met the cook round the corner coming out of a public-house, wiping her mouth.", "백랍 단지 몇 개와 우유통 하나가 지하층 난간에 걸려 있었고, 문은 활짝 열려 있었습니다. 우리는 모퉁이를 돌다가 술집에서 나오며 입을 닦는 요리사와 마주쳤습니다."], ["She mentioned, as she passed us, that she had been to see what o’clock it was.", "요리사는 우리를 지나치며, 지금 몇 시인지 보러 갔다 오는 길이라고 일러주었습니다."]], [["But before we met the cook, we met Richard, who was dancing up and down Thavies Inn to warm his feet.", "그러나 요리사와 마주치기 전에, 우리는 발을 녹이려고 세이비스 인 앞을 깡충깡충 오르내리는 리처드와 마주쳤습니다."], ["He was agreeably surprised to see us stirring so soon and said he would gladly share our walk.", "리처드는 우리가 이렇게 일찍 움직이는 걸 보고 반갑게 놀라며, 기꺼이 산책에 함께하겠다고 했습니다."], ["So he took care of Ada, and Miss Jellyby and I went first.", "그래서 리처드는 에이다를 맡았고, 젤리비 양과 저는 앞장섰습니다."], ["I may mention that Miss Jellyby had relapsed into her sulky manner and that I really should not have thought she liked me much unless she had told me so.", "한마디 덧붙이자면, 젤리비 양은 다시 그 뾰로통한 태도로 되돌아가 있었고, 본인이 직접 말해주지 않았다면 저를 그리 좋아하는 줄은 정말 몰랐을 겁니다."]], [["“Where would you wish to go?”", "\"어디로 가고 싶으세요?\""], ["she asked.", "젤리비 양이 물었습니다."]], [["“Anywhere, my dear,”", "\"아무 데나요.\""], ["I replied.", "제가 대답했습니다."]], [["“Anywhere’s nowhere,”", "\"아무 데나는 아무 데도 아니에요.\""], ["said Miss Jellyby, stopping perversely.", "젤리비 양이 심술궂게 걸음을 멈추며 말했습니다."]], [["“Let us go somewhere at any rate,”", "\"어쨌든 어디로든 가보자고요.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["She then walked me on very fast.", "그러자 젤리비 양은 저를 데리고 아주 빠르게 걸었습니다."]], [["“I don’t care!”", "\"난 상관없어요!\""], ["she said.", "젤리비 양이 말했습니다."], ["“Now, you are my witness, Miss Summerson, I say I don’t care—but if he was to come to our house with his great, shining, lumpy forehead night after night till he was as old as Methuselah, I wouldn’t have anything to say to him.", "\"자, 서머슨 양이 증인이에요. 난 상관없다고요 — 하지만 그 번들번들하고 울퉁불퉁한 커다란 이마를 들이밀고 밤마다 밤마다 우리 집에 찾아온대도, 므두셀라만큼 늙을 때까지 그런대도, 난 그 사람한테 할 말이 한마디도 없어요."], ["Such ASSES as he and Ma make of themselves!”", "그 사람이랑 엄마가 무슨 멍청이짓을 하는지 좀 보라고요!\" * 므두셀라: 구약성서에 나오는 인물로, 969세까지 살았다는 가장 장수한 사람."]], [["“My dear!”", "\"얘, 이러면 못써요!\""], ["I remonstrated, in allusion to the epithet and the vigorous emphasis Miss Jellyby set upon it.", "젤리비 양이 내뱉은 그 말과, 거기에 실은 거센 강조를 두고 저는 타일렀습니다."], ["“Your duty as a child—”", "\"자식 된 도리로서—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Don’t talk of duty as a child, Miss Summerson; where’s Ma’s duty as a parent?", "자식 된 도리 얘기는 꺼내지도 마세요, 서머슨 양. 부모 된 엄마의 도리는 어디 있는데요?"], ["All made over to the public and Africa, I suppose!", "죄다 대중이며 아프리카한테 넘겨버렸겠죠, 뭐!"], ["Then let the public and Africa show duty as a child; it’s much more their affair than mine.", "그럼 대중이랑 아프리카더러 자식 된 도리를 보이라고 하세요. 저보다 그쪽 일이 훨씬 더 크니까요."], ["You are shocked, I dare say!", "충격받으셨겠죠, 짐작해요!"], ["Very well, so am I shocked too; so we are both shocked, and there’s an end of it!”", "좋아요, 저도 충격받았어요. 그러니 우리 둘 다 충격받은 거고, 그걸로 끝이에요!\""]], [["She walked me on faster yet.", "젤리비 양은 저를 데리고 더더욱 빠르게 걸었습니다."]], [["“But for all that, I say again, he may come, and come, and come, and I won’t have anything to say to him.", "\"하지만 그래도 다시 말하는데, 그 사람이 오고 또 오고 또 와도, 난 그 사람한테 할 말이 한마디도 없어요."], ["I can’t bear him.", "그 사람을 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["If there’s any stuff in the world that I hate and detest, it’s the stuff he and Ma talk.", "이 세상에 내가 미워하고 진저리치는 게 하나 있다면, 그건 그 사람이랑 엄마가 떠드는 그 소리예요."], ["I wonder the very paving-stones opposite our house can have the patience to stay there and be a witness of such inconsistencies and contradictions as all that sounding nonsense, and Ma’s management!”", "우리 집 맞은편 깔린 돌들은 어쩜 그렇게 참을성 있게 거기 버티고 서서, 그 요란한 헛소리며 엄마의 살림 솜씨 같은, 앞뒤도 안 맞고 모순투성이인 꼴을 다 지켜볼 수 있는지 신기할 따름이에요!\""]], [["I could not but understand her to refer to Mr. Quale, the young gentleman who had appeared after dinner yesterday.", "저는 젤리비 양이 어제 만찬 뒤에 나타난 그 젊은 신사 퀘일 씨를 두고 하는 말이라고 짐작할 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["I was saved the disagreeable necessity of pursuing the subject by Richard and Ada coming up at a round pace, laughing and asking us if we meant to run a race.", "그런데 리처드와 에이다가 깔깔 웃으며 빠른 걸음으로 다가와 우리에게 경주라도 할 셈이냐고 묻는 바람에, 저는 그 거북한 화제를 더 좇아야 하는 곤란을 면했습니다."], ["Thus interrupted, Miss Jellyby became silent and walked moodily on at my side while I admired the long successions and varieties of streets, the quantity of people already going to and fro, the number of vehicles passing and repassing, the busy preparations in the setting forth of shop windows and the sweeping out of shops, and the extraordinary creatures in rags secretly groping among the swept-out rubbish for pins and other refuse.", "이렇게 말이 끊기자 젤리비 양은 입을 다물고 시무룩하게 제 옆을 걸었습니다. 그동안 저는 길게 이어지며 갖가지로 변하는 거리들이며, 벌써 오가는 수많은 사람들이며, 끊임없이 지나가고 다시 지나가는 숱한 탈것들이며, 진열창을 꾸미고 가게 안을 쓸어내는 분주한 채비며, 쓸어낸 쓰레기 더미를 헤집으며 핀이며 다른 부스러기를 슬쩍슬쩍 뒤지는 누더기 차림의 별난 생명체들을 신기하게 바라보았습니다."]], [["“So, cousin,”", "\"자, 사촌.\""], ["said the cheerful voice of Richard to Ada behind me.", "제 뒤에서 에이다를 향한 리처드의 명랑한 목소리가 들렸습니다."], ["“We are never to get out of Chancery!", "\"우리는 도무지 대법원에서 벗어날 수가 없나 봐!"], ["We have come by another way to our place of meeting yesterday, and—by the Great Seal, here’s the old lady again!”", "어제 만난 그 자리를 다른 길로 돌아왔는데도 — 옥새에 두고 맹세하건대, 여기 또 그 노부인이 계셔!\""]], [["Truly, there she was, immediately in front of us, curtsying, and smiling, and saying with her yesterday’s air of patronage, “The wards in Jarndyce!", "정말로, 노부인이 우리 바로 앞에 있었습니다. 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 미소 짓고는, 어제와 똑같은 후원자 같은 태도로 말했습니다."], ["Ve-ry happy, I am sure!”", "\"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이로군요! 정말, 정말 기뻐요!\""]], [["“You are out early, ma’am,”", "\"이른 시각에 나오셨네요, 부인.\""], ["said I as she curtsied to me.", "노부인이 제게 인사를 건네기에 제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Ye-es!", "\"그-럼요!"], ["I usually walk here early.", "저는 보통 이 시각에 여길 산책한답니다."], ["Before the court sits.", "법정이 열리기 전에요."], ["It’s retired.", "한적하거든요."], ["I collect my thoughts here for the business of the day,”", "저는 여기서 하루 일과를 위해 생각을 가다듬어요.\""], ["said the old lady mincingly.", "노부인이 점잔을 빼며 말했습니다."], ["“The business of the day requires a great deal of thought.", "\"하루 일과에는 생각이 아주 많이 필요하답니다."], ["Chancery justice is so ve-ry difficult to follow.”", "대법원의 정의란 따라가기가 정말, 정말 어렵거든요.\""]], [["“Who’s this, Miss Summerson?”", "\"이분은 누구세요, 서머슨 양?\""], ["whispered Miss Jellyby, drawing my arm tighter through her own.", "젤리비 양이 제 팔을 자기 팔에 더 바짝 끼며 속삭였습니다."]], [["The little old lady’s hearing was remarkably quick.", "작은 노부인의 귀는 놀랄 만큼 밝았습니다."], ["She answered for herself directly.", "부인은 곧장 직접 대답했습니다."]], [["“A suitor, my child.", "\"소송 당사자랍니다, 얘야."], ["At your service.", "잘 부탁해요."], ["I have the honour to attend court regularly.", "저는 영광스럽게도 법정에 꼬박꼬박 나간답니다."], ["With my documents.", "제 서류들을 들고서요."], ["Have I the pleasure of addressing another of the youthful parties in Jarndyce?”", "또 다른 잔다이스 사건의 젊은 당사자분과 인사를 나누는 영광을 누리는 건가요?\""], ["said the old lady, recovering herself, with her head on one side, from a very low curtsy.", "노부인은 아주 낮게 무릎을 굽혔다가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 몸을 추스르고 말했습니다."]], [["Richard, anxious to atone for his thoughtlessness of yesterday, good-naturedly explained that Miss Jellyby was not connected with the suit.", "어제의 경솔함을 만회하려 마음 졸이던 리처드가 싹싹하게, 젤리비 양은 그 소송과 관련이 없다고 설명했습니다."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했습니다."], ["“She does not expect a judgment?", "\"이 아가씨는 판결을 기다리지 않는군요?"], ["She will still grow old.", "그래도 늙어가긴 할 테죠."], ["But not so old. Oh, dear, no!", "하지만 그렇게까지 늙지는 않을 거예요."], ["This is the garden of Lincoln’s Inn.", "오, 저런, 아니고말고요! 여기는 링컨스 인의 정원이랍니다."], ["I call it my garden.", "저는 제 정원이라고 불러요."], ["It is quite a bower in the summer-time.", "여름철이면 아주 정자 같답니다."], ["Where the birds sing melodiously.", "새들이 곱게 노래하는 곳이지요."], ["I pass the greater part of the long vacation here.", "저는 긴 휴정기의 대부분을 여기서 보내요."], ["In contemplation.", "사색하면서요."], ["You find the long vacation exceedingly long, don’t you?”", "긴 휴정기가 정말 길게 느껴지지요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["We said yes, as she seemed to expect us to say so.", "노부인이 그렇게 대답하기를 바라는 듯해서, 우리는 그렇다고 했습니다."]], [["“When the leaves are falling from the trees and there are no more flowers in bloom to make up into nosegays for the Lord Chancellor’s court,”", "\"나무에서 잎이 떨어지고, 대법원장님 법정에 바칠 꽃다발로 엮을 꽃이 더는 피지 않으면,\" 노부인이 말했습니다."], ["said the old lady, “the vacation is fulfilled and the sixth seal, mentioned in the Revelations, again prevails.", "\"휴정기가 채워지고, 요한계시록에 나오는 여섯 번째 봉인이 다시 위세를 떨치지요. 부디 와서 제 거처를 봐주세요."], ["Pray come and see my lodging.", "제게 좋은 징조가 될 테니까요."], ["It will be a good omen for me.", "젊음과 희망과 아름다움이 거기 깃드는 일은 정말 드물거든요."], ["Youth, and hope, and beauty are very seldom there.", "그 어느 것이라도 저를 찾아온 지 오래, 정말 오래되었답니다.\""], ["It is a long, long time since I had a visit from either.”", "* 여섯 번째 봉인: 요한계시록에서 어린 양이 일곱 봉인을 차례로 떼는데, 여섯 번째 봉인이 열리자 큰 지진과 함께 해가 검어지고 달이 핏빛으로 변한다."]], [["She had taken my hand, and leading me and Miss Jellyby away, beckoned Richard and Ada to come too.", "노부인은 제 손을 잡고서 저와 젤리비 양을 이끌었고, 리처드와 에이다에게도 따라오라고 손짓했습니다."], ["I did not know how to excuse myself and looked to Richard for aid.", "저는 어떻게 사양해야 할지 몰라 리처드에게 도움을 청하는 눈길을 보냈습니다."], ["As he was half amused and half curious and all in doubt how to get rid of the old lady without offence, she continued to lead us away, and he and Ada continued to follow, our strange conductress informing us all the time, with much smiling condescension, that she lived close by.", "리처드는 반쯤 재미있어하고 반쯤 호기심에 차 있었으며, 노부인의 기분을 상하게 하지 않고 어떻게 떼어낼지 도무지 갈피를 못 잡고 있었기에, 노부인은 계속 우리를 이끌었고 리처드와 에이다도 계속 따라왔습니다. 그동안 이 기묘한 안내자는 줄곧 환하게 겸양 떨며, 자기가 바로 가까이에 산다고 알려주었습니다."]], [["It was quite true, as it soon appeared.", "곧 드러났듯이 그 말은 정말이었습니다."], ["She lived so close by that we had not time to have done humouring her for a few moments before she was at home.", "노부인이 어찌나 가까이에 사는지, 우리가 잠시 더 비위를 맞춰줄 겨를도 없이 부인은 벌써 집에 다다랐습니다."], ["Slipping us out at a little side gate, the old lady stopped most unexpectedly in a narrow back street, part of some courts and lanes immediately outside the wall of the inn, and said, “This is my lodging.", "작은 옆문으로 우리를 빠져나가게 한 노부인은 아주 뜻밖에도 좁은 뒷골목에 멈춰 섰는데, 그곳은 여관 담장 바로 바깥으로 이어진 몇몇 마당과 골목 가운데 한 자락이었습니다. 그러고는 말했습니다."], ["Pray walk up!”", "\"여기가 제 거처랍니다. 부디 올라오세요!\""]], [["She had stopped at a shop over which was written KROOK, RAG AND BOTTLE WAREHOUSE.", "노부인이 멈춰 선 곳은 한 가게 앞이었는데, 그 위에는 이렇게 적혀 있었습니다. 크룩, 넝마와 병 도매상."], ["Also, in long thin letters, KROOK, DEALER IN MARINE STORES.", "또 길고 가느다란 글씨로, 크룩, 선박용품 판매상이라고도 쓰여 있었습니다."], ["In one part of the window was a picture of a red paper mill at which a cart was unloading a quantity of sacks of old rags.", "진열창 한쪽에는 빨간 제지 공장 그림이 있었는데, 거기서 짐수레 한 대가 헌 넝마 자루를 잔뜩 부리고 있었습니다."], ["In another was the inscription BONES BOUGHT.", "다른 쪽에는 뼈 삽니다라는 글귀가 있었습니다."], ["In another, KITCHEN-STUFF BOUGHT.", "또 다른 쪽에는 부엌 부산물 삽니다."], ["In another, OLD IRON BOUGHT.", "또 다른 쪽에는 고철 삽니다."], ["In another, WASTE-PAPER BOUGHT.", "또 다른 쪽에는 휴지 삽니다."], ["In another, LADIES’ AND GENTLEMEN’S WARDROBES BOUGHT.", "또 다른 쪽에는 신사 숙녀용 의복 삽니다."], ["Everything seemed to be bought and nothing to be sold there.", "거기서는 모든 것을 사들이기만 할 뿐 파는 것은 아무것도 없는 듯했습니다."], ["In all parts of the window were quantities of dirty bottles—blacking bottles, medicine bottles, ginger-beer and soda-water bottles, pickle bottles, wine bottles, ink bottles; I am reminded by mentioning the latter that the shop had in several little particulars the air of being in a legal neighbourhood and of being, as it were, a dirty hanger-on and disowned relation of the law.", "진열창 곳곳에는 더러운 병이 잔뜩 있었습니다 — 구두약 병, 약병, 생강 맥주와 소다수 병, 절임 병, 포도주 병, 잉크병. 마지막 것을 언급하다 보니 떠오르는데, 그 가게는 몇 가지 자잘한 점에서 법조 동네에 자리 잡은 듯한, 이를테면 법률이 거느린 더러운 식객이자 의절한 친척 같은 분위기를 풍겼습니다."], ["There were a great many ink bottles.", "잉크병이 아주 많았습니다."], ["There was a little tottering bench of shabby old volumes outside the door, labelled “Law Books, all at 9d.”", "문밖에는 낡고 초라한 책들을 얹은 채 기우뚱거리는 작은 좌판이 있었는데, \"법률 서적, 모두 9펜스\"라고 붙어 있었습니다."], ["Some of the inscriptions I have enumerated were written in law-hand, like the papers I had seen in Kenge and Carboy’s office and the letters I had so long received from the firm.", "제가 늘어놓은 글귀 가운데 몇몇은 법조 글씨체로 쓰여 있었는데, 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에서 본 서류며, 제가 그 회사로부터 그토록 오래 받아온 편지들과 똑같았습니다."], ["Among them was one, in the same writing, having nothing to do with the business of the shop, but announcing that a respectable man aged forty-five wanted engrossing or copying to execute with neatness and dispatch: Address to Nemo, care of Mr.", "그 가운데 하나는 같은 글씨체였지만 가게 일과는 아무 상관이 없었으니, 마흔다섯 살의 점잖은 남자가 정서나 필사 일을 깔끔하고 신속하게 해드린다는 내용이었습니다. 연락처는 내부의 크룩 씨를 통해 니모에게."], ["Krook, within. There were several second-hand bags, blue and red, hanging up.", "파랗고 빨간 중고 가방 몇 개도 걸려 있었습니다."], ["A little way within the shop-door lay heaps of old crackled parchment scrolls and discoloured and dog’s-eared law-papers.", "가게 문 안쪽으로 조금 들어간 곳에는 낡고 쩍쩍 갈라진 양피지 두루마리와, 빛바래고 귀퉁이가 접힌 법률 서류가 수북이 쌓여 있었습니다."], ["I could have fancied that all the rusty keys, of which there must have been hundreds huddled together as old iron, had once belonged to doors of rooms or strong chests in lawyers’ offices.", "고철처럼 한데 뒤엉킨 그 녹슨 열쇠들이 — 분명 수백 개는 되었을 텐데 — 한때 변호사 사무소의 방문이나 튼튼한 금고에 달려 있던 것이리라고 상상해도 좋을 듯했습니다."], ["The litter of rags tumbled partly into and partly out of a one-legged wooden scale, hanging without any counterpoise from a beam, might have been counsellors’ bands and gowns torn up.", "받침대도 없이 들보에 매달려, 외다리 나무 저울 한쪽 접시에 얹혔다가 절반쯤 안팎으로 흘러넘친 그 너저분한 넝마 더미는, 갈가리 찢긴 법정 변호사의 띠와 법복일지도 모른다고 여길 만했습니다."], ["One had only to fancy, as Richard whispered to Ada and me while we all stood looking in, that yonder bones in a corner, piled together and picked very clean, were the bones of clients, to make the picture complete.", "우리 모두가 안을 들여다보며 서 있을 때 리처드가 에이다와 제게 속삭였듯이, 저 구석에 한데 쌓인 채 아주 말끔하게 발라진 뼈들이 의뢰인들의 뼈라고 상상하기만 하면, 그림은 완성되었습니다."]], [["As it was still foggy and dark, and as the shop was blinded besides by the wall of Lincoln’s Inn, intercepting the light within a couple of yards, we should not have seen so much but for a lighted lantern that an old man in spectacles and a hairy cap was carrying about in the shop.", "아직 안개가 끼고 어두운 데다, 게다가 가게는 링컨스 인 담장에 가려 빛이 채 두어 야드도 못 들어와 막혀 있었기에, 안경을 쓰고 털모자를 쓴 한 노인이 가게 안에서 들고 다니는 불 켜진 등불이 아니었다면 우리는 그토록 많은 것을 보지 못했을 겁니다."], ["Turning towards the door, he now caught sight of us.", "문 쪽으로 돌아서던 노인이 그제야 우리를 알아보았습니다."], ["He was short, cadaverous, and withered, with his head sunk sideways between his shoulders and the breath issuing in visible smoke from his mouth as if he were on fire within.", "키가 작고 송장처럼 핼쑥하며 쪼그라든 그 노인은, 어깨 사이로 머리를 한쪽으로 푹 파묻은 채, 마치 속에서 불이라도 붙은 듯 입에서 눈에 보이는 연기 같은 숨을 뿜어내고 있었습니다."], ["His throat, chin, and eyebrows were so frosted with white hairs and so gnarled with veins and puckered skin that he looked from his breast upward like some old root in a fall of snow.", "목과 턱과 눈썹은 흰 털로 어찌나 서리처럼 뒤덮였고 핏줄과 쪼글쪼글한 살로 어찌나 울툭불툭한지, 가슴께부터 위로는 마치 눈 속에 파묻힌 늙은 나무뿌리 같았습니다."]], [["“Hi, hi!”", "\"이봐요, 이봐요!\""], ["said the old man, coming to the door.", "노인이 문으로 다가오며 말했습니다."], ["“Have you anything to sell?”", "\"뭐 팔 거라도 있소?\""]], [["We naturally drew back and glanced at our conductress, who had been trying to open the house-door with a key she had taken from her pocket, and to whom Richard now said that as we had had the pleasure of seeing where she lived, we would leave her, being pressed for time.", "우리는 자연스레 뒤로 물러나 안내자를 흘끔 보았습니다. 그동안 노부인은 주머니에서 꺼낸 열쇠로 집 문을 열려고 애쓰고 있었는데, 리처드가 부인에게 사는 곳을 보았으니 기쁘고, 시간에 쫓기는 터라 이만 가보겠다고 말했습니다."], ["But she was not to be so easily left.", "그러나 부인은 그리 쉽게 떨쳐낼 상대가 아니었습니다."], ["She became so fantastically and pressingly earnest in her entreaties that we would walk up and see her apartment for an instant, and was so bent, in her harmless way, on leading me in, as part of the good omen she desired, that I (whatever the others might do) saw nothing for it but to comply.", "부인은 잠깐만 올라와 자기 방을 봐달라고 기상천외하게 매달리며 간절히 졸랐고, 자기가 바라는 좋은 징조의 일부라면서, 해될 것 없는 그 방식으로 저를 기어이 안으로 데려가려고 했기에, 저는(다른 사람들이야 어쩌든) 응하는 것 말고는 도리가 없다고 보았습니다."], ["I suppose we were all more or less curious; at any rate, when the old man added his persuasions to hers and said, “Aye, aye!", "아마 우리 모두 다소간 호기심이 일었던 모양입니다. 어쨌든 노인까지 부인의 설득에 가세해, \"그래요, 그래!"], ["Please her!", "비위를 맞춰줘요!"], ["It won’t take a minute!", "일 분도 안 걸려요!"], ["Come in, come in!", "들어와요, 들어와!"], ["Come in through the shop if t’other door’s out of order!”", "저쪽 문이 고장 났으면 가게로 들어와요!\""], ["we all went in, stimulated by Richard’s laughing encouragement and relying on his protection.", "하고 거들자, 우리는 다 같이 안으로 들어갔습니다. 리처드의 웃음 섞인 격려에 부추겨졌고, 그의 보호를 믿었기 때문입니다."]], [["“My landlord, Krook,”", "\"제 집주인, 크룩이에요.\""], ["said the little old lady, condescending to him from her lofty station as she presented him to us.", "작은 노부인이 그 드높은 자리에서 노인을 굽어보듯 우리에게 그를 소개하며 말했습니다."], ["“He is called among the neighbours the Lord Chancellor.", "\"이웃들 사이에서는 대법원장이라고 불린답니다."], ["His shop is called the Court of Chancery.", "이 가게는 대법원이라고 불리고요."], ["He is a very eccentric person.", "아주 별난 사람이에요."], ["He is very odd.", "정말 괴짜죠."], ["Oh, I assure you he is very odd!”", "오, 단언컨대 정말 괴짜라니까요!\""]], [["She shook her head a great many times and tapped her forehead with her finger to express to us that we must have the goodness to excuse him, “For he is a little—you know—M!”", "노부인은 고개를 여러 번 절레절레 흔들고 손가락으로 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드려, 부디 너그러이 봐줘야 한다는 뜻을 우리에게 전했습니다. \"이 사람이 좀—그러니까—머리가 좀!\""], ["said the old lady with great stateliness.", "노부인이 자못 위엄을 차리며 말했습니다."], ["The old man overheard, and laughed.", "노인이 그 말을 엿듣고 웃었습니다."]], [["“It’s true enough,”", "\"맞는 말이오.\""], ["he said, going before us with the lantern, “that they call me the Lord Chancellor and call my shop Chancery.", "노인이 등불을 들고 앞장서며 말했습니다. \"사람들이 날 대법원장이라 부르고 내 가게를 대법원이라 부르는 게 말이오."], ["And why do you think they call me the Lord Chancellor and my shop Chancery?”", "그런데 왜 날 대법원장이라 부르고 내 가게를 대법원이라 부르는지, 짐작이 가시오?\""]], [["“I don’t know, I am sure!”", "\"전혀 모르겠는데요.\""], ["said Richard rather carelessly.", "리처드가 다소 무심하게 말했습니다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보다시피,\""], ["said the old man, stopping and turning round, “they—Hi!", "노인이 멈춰 서서 돌아보며 말했습니다. \"사람들이 말이오—이봐!"], ["Here’s lovely hair!", "여기 곱디고운 머리카락 좀 보게!"], ["I have got three sacks of ladies’ hair below, but none so beautiful and fine as this.", "아래층에 숙녀들 머리카락이 세 자루나 있지만, 이렇게 곱고 고운 건 하나도 없소."], ["What colour, and what texture!”", "이 빛깔이며, 이 결이라니!\""]], [["“That’ll do, my good friend!”", "\"이제 됐어요, 영감님!\""], ["said Richard, strongly disapproving of his having drawn one of Ada’s tresses through his yellow hand.", "에이다의 머리타래 한 가닥을 자기 누런 손으로 끌어당기는 것을 강하게 못마땅해하며 리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“You can admire as the rest of us do without taking that liberty.”", "\"그런 무례를 범하지 않고도 우리 모두처럼 그저 감탄하시면 되잖아요.\""]], [["The old man darted at him a sudden look which even called my attention from Ada, who, startled and blushing, was so remarkably beautiful that she seemed to fix the wandering attention of the little old lady herself.", "노인이 리처드를 향해 갑작스러운 눈길을 쏘아붙였는데, 그 바람에 에이다에게 쏠려 있던 제 주의마저 그쪽으로 돌아갔습니다. 깜짝 놀라 얼굴을 붉힌 에이다가 어찌나 놀랍도록 아름다웠던지, 그 작은 노부인 자신의 산만한 주의마저 붙들어 매는 듯했습니다."], ["But as Ada interposed and laughingly said she could only feel proud of such genuine admiration, Mr. Krook shrunk into his former self as suddenly as he had leaped out of it.", "그러나 에이다가 끼어들어 웃으며 그토록 진심 어린 감탄이라면 오히려 자랑스러울 따름이라고 말하자, 크룩 씨는 펄쩍 튀어나왔을 때만큼이나 별안간 본래의 자기 모습으로 움츠러들었습니다."]], [["“You see, I have so many things here,”", "\"보다시피, 난 여기 온갖 것을 이렇게나 많이 가지고 있소.\" 노인이 등불을 치켜들며 다시 말을 이었습니다."], ["he resumed, holding up the lantern, “of so many kinds, and all as the neighbours think (but THEY know nothing), wasting away and going to rack and ruin, that that’s why they have given me and my place a christening.", "\"온갖 종류로 말이오. 이웃들 생각엔(하지만 그자들은 아무것도 몰라) 죄다 썩어가고 망가지고 폐허가 되어간다지."], ["And I have so many old parchmentses and papers in my stock.", "그래서 나하고 내 가게한테 그런 세례명을 붙여준 거요."], ["And I have a liking for rust and must and cobwebs.", "게다가 난 묵은 양피지며 서류를 재고로 어찌나 많이 쌓아뒀는지."], ["And all’s fish that comes to my net.", "또 녹이며 곰팡이며 거미줄을 좋아하지."], ["And I can’t abear to part with anything I once lay hold of (or so my neighbours think, but what do THEY know?) or to alter anything, or to have any sweeping, nor scouring, nor cleaning, nor repairing going on about me.", "내 그물에 걸리는 건 죄다 물고기란 말이오. 게다가 난 한번 손에 쥔 건 무엇이든 내놓지를 못하고(이웃들 생각엔 그렇다지만, 그자들이 뭘 알겠소?), 무엇 하나 바꾸지도 못하고, 내 주위에서 쓸거나 닦거나 청소하거나 수리하는 꼴은 도무지 못 견디지."], ["That’s the way I’ve got the ill name of Chancery.", "그래서 대법원이라는 못된 이름이 붙은 거요."], ["I don’t mind.", "난 상관없소."], ["I go to see my noble and learned brother pretty well every day, when he sits in the Inn.", "그분이 여관에서 자리에 앉아 계실 때면, 거의 매일 우리 고귀하고 박식하신 형제분을 뵈러 간다오."], ["He don’t notice me, but I notice him.", "그분은 나를 알은체하지 않지만, 난 그분을 알은체하지."], ["There’s no great odds betwixt us.", "우리 둘 사이엔 별 차이가 없소."], ["We both grub on in a muddle.", "둘 다 뒤죽박죽 속에서 아등바등 살아가니까."], ["Hi, Lady Jane!”", "이봐, 레이디 제인!\""]], [["A large grey cat leaped from some neighbouring shelf on his shoulder and startled us all.", "커다란 잿빛 고양이 한 마리가 근처 선반에서 노인의 어깨로 펄쩍 뛰어오르는 바람에 우리 모두 깜짝 놀랐습니다."]], [["“Hi!", "\"이봐!"], ["Show ’em how you scratch.", "어떻게 할퀴는지 보여줘 봐. 이봐!"], ["Hi! Tear, my lady!”", "찢어버려, 우리 아가씨!\""], ["said her master.", "주인이 말했습니다."]], [["The cat leaped down and ripped at a bundle of rags with her tigerish claws, with a sound that it set my teeth on edge to hear.", "고양이가 펄쩍 뛰어내리더니 호랑이 같은 발톱으로 넝마 뭉치를 갈가리 찢었는데, 그 소리가 어찌나 듣기에 소름이 돋던지 이가 다 시릴 지경이었습니다."]], [["“She’d do as much for any one I was to set her on,”", "\"내가 누구한테든 부추기면 이놈은 그만큼 해치울 거요.\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했습니다."], ["“I deal in cat-skins among other general matters, and hers was offered to me.", "\"난 여러 잡다한 것들 중에 고양이 가죽도 다루는데, 이놈 가죽도 내게 들어온 거였소."], ["It’s a very fine skin, as you may see, but I didn’t have it stripped off!", "보다시피 아주 좋은 가죽이지만, 난 벗겨내진 않았다오!"], ["THAT warn’t like Chancery practice though, says you!”", "하긴 그건 대법원 방식하곤 다르다고, 당신들은 말하겠지만!\""]], [["He had by this time led us across the shop, and now opened a door in the back part of it, leading to the house-entry.", "노인은 그동안 우리를 데리고 가게를 가로질러 왔고, 이제 가게 뒤편의 문을 열었는데, 그 문은 집 현관으로 통했습니다."], ["As he stood with his hand upon the lock, the little old lady graciously observed to him before passing out, “That will do, Krook.", "노인이 손을 자물쇠에 얹은 채 서 있자, 작은 노부인이 나가기 전에 점잖게 한마디 건넸습니다. \"그만하면 됐어요, 크룩."], ["You mean well, but are tiresome.", "마음은 고맙지만 사람 피곤하게 하는군요."], ["My young friends are pressed for time.", "제 젊은 친구들은 시간에 쫓기는 몸이랍니다."], ["I have none to spare myself, having to attend court very soon.", "저도 내드릴 시간이 없고요. 곧 법정에 나가야 하니까요."], ["My young friends are the wards in Jarndyce.”", "제 젊은 친구들은 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이에요.\""]], [["“Jarndyce!”", "\"잔다이스!\""], ["said the old man with a start.", "노인이 흠칫 놀라며 말했습니다."]], [["“Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "\"잔다이스 대 잔다이스."], ["The great suit, Krook,”", "그 대단한 소송 말이에요, 크룩.\""], ["returned his lodger.", "세입자가 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“Hi!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["exclaimed the old man in a tone of thoughtful amazement and with a wider stare than before.", "노인이 골똘히 놀란 어조로, 아까보다 더 휘둥그레진 눈으로 외쳤습니다."], ["“Think of it!”", "\"이거 참!\""]], [["He seemed so rapt all in a moment and looked so curiously at us that Richard said, “Why, you appear to trouble yourself a good deal about the causes before your noble and learned brother, the other Chancellor!”", "노인이 별안간 넋을 잃은 듯 우리를 어찌나 신기하게 바라보던지, 리처드가 말했습니다. \"아니, 영감님은 그 고귀하고 박식하신 형제분, 그러니까 또 다른 대법원장 앞에 걸린 사건들에 꽤나 마음을 쓰시나 보군요!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said the old man abstractedly.", "노인이 멍하니 말했습니다. \"그렇고말고!"], ["“Sure! YOUR name now will be—”", "그래, 당신 이름은—\""]], [["“Richard Carstone.”", "\"리처드 카스톤입니다.\""]], [["“Carstone,”", "\"카스톤.\""], ["he repeated, slowly checking off that name upon his forefinger; and each of the others he went on to mention upon a separate finger.", "노인이 검지로 그 이름을 천천히 짚어가며 되뇌었고, 이어 다른 사람들의 이름도 손가락 하나씩에 짚어가며 읊었습니다. \"그래."], ["“Yes. There was the name of Barbary, and the name of Clare, and the name of Dedlock, too, I think.”", "바버리라는 이름이 있었고, 클레어라는 이름이 있었고, 데드록이라는 이름도 있었지, 아마.\""]], [["“He knows as much of the cause as the real salaried Chancellor!”", "\"이 사람은 진짜 봉급 받는 대법원장만큼이나 그 사건을 훤히 아는데!\""], ["said Richard, quite astonished, to Ada and me.", "리처드가 깜짝 놀라 에이다와 제게 말했습니다."]], [["“Aye!”", "\"그래!\" 노인이 천천히 넋을 차리며 말했습니다."], ["said the old man, coming slowly out of his abstraction.", "\"그래! 톰 잔다이스—친척이라 이렇게 불러도 양해해주시오."], ["“Yes!", "하지만 그분은 법정에서 다른 이름으론 한 번도 불린 적이 없었고, 거기선—지금 이분만큼이나 잘 알려져 있었소.\" 노인이 세입자를 향해 살짝 고갯짓했습니다."], ["Tom Jarndyce—you’ll excuse me, being related; but he was never known about court by any other name, and was as well known there as—she is now,”", "\"톰 잔다이스는 여기 자주 들렀소. 사건이 열렸거나 열릴 참이면 안절부절못하고 어슬렁거리는 버릇이 들어서, 자그마한 가게 주인들한테 말을 걸며 무슨 일이 있어도 대법원에는 발 들이지 말라고 일렀지."], ["nodding slightly at his lodger.", "'왜냐면,' 그분이 그러길, '그건 느린 방앗간에 갈려 산산조각 나는 거요."], ["“Tom Jarndyce was often in here.", "약한 불에 천천히 구워지는 거요. 벌 한 마리 한 마리한테 쏘여 죽는 거요."], ["He got into a restless habit of strolling about when the cause was on, or expected, talking to the little shopkeepers and telling ’em to keep out of Chancery, whatever they did.", "물방울에 빠져 죽는 거요. 낟알 하나하나에 미쳐가는 거요.'"], ["‘For,’ says he, ‘it’s being ground to bits in a slow mill; it’s being roasted at a slow fire; it’s being stung to death by single bees; it’s being drowned by drops; it’s going mad by grains.’ He was as near making away with himself, just where the young lady stands, as near could be.”", "그분은 바로 저 아가씨가 서 있는 자리에서, 거의 제 손으로 목숨을 끊을 뻔했다오. 정말 아슬아슬했지.\""]], [["We listened with horror.", "우리는 소름이 끼친 채 귀를 기울였습니다."]], [["“He come in at the door,”", "\"그분이 그 짓을 한 날, 저 문으로 들어왔소.\" 노인이 가게를 가로지르는 상상의 자취를 천천히 가리키며 말했습니다."], ["said the old man, slowly pointing an imaginary track along the shop, “on the day he did it—the whole neighbourhood had said for months before that he would do it, of a certainty sooner or later—he come in at the door that day, and walked along there, and sat himself on a bench that stood there, and asked me (you’ll judge I was a mortal sight younger then) to fetch him a pint of wine.", "\"—온 동네가 몇 달 전부터, 그분이 머잖아 분명 그럴 거라고 입을 모았지—그날 그분이 저 문으로 들어와 저쪽으로 걸어와선, 저기 놓인 의자에 앉더니, 내게 부탁하더군(그때 난 죽도록 더 젊었던 걸 짐작하실 거요), 포도주 한 파인트만 가져다 달라고. '왜냐면,'"], ["‘For,’ says he, ‘Krook, I am much depressed; my cause is on again, and I think I’m nearer judgment than I ever was.’ I hadn’t a mind to leave him alone; and I persuaded him to go to the tavern over the way there, t’other side my lane (I mean Chancery Lane); and I followed and looked in at the window, and saw him, comfortable as I thought, in the arm-chair by the fire, and company with him.", "그분이 그러길, '크룩, 난 무척 우울하오. 내 사건이 또 열렸는데, 그 어느 때보다 판결에 가까워진 것 같소.'"], ["I hadn’t hardly got back here when I heard a shot go echoing and rattling right away into the inn.", "난 그분을 혼자 두고 싶지 않아서, 길 건너편, 우리 골목(대법원 골목 말이오) 맞은편에 있는 술집으로 가자고 설득했소. 그러고 따라가서 창문으로 들여다보니, 그분이 난롯가 안락의자에 앉아 일행과 함께, 내 보기엔 편안하게 있더군."], ["I ran out—neighbours ran out—twenty of us cried at once, ‘Tom Jarndyce!’”", "그런데 여기 채 돌아오기도 전에, 총소리가 메아리치고 덜컹대며 여관 안쪽으로 곧장 울려 퍼지는 소리가 들렸소. 난 뛰쳐나갔고—이웃들도 뛰쳐나왔고—스무 명이 한꺼번에 외쳤지, '톰 잔다이스!'\""]], [["The old man stopped, looked hard at us, looked down into the lantern, blew the light out, and shut the lantern up.", "노인은 말을 멈추고 우리를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 등불 속을 들여다보더니 불을 훅 끄고 등불을 닫았습니다."]], [["“We were right, I needn’t tell the present hearers.", "\"우리 짐작이 맞았소. 여기 계신 분들께 굳이 말할 것도 없지."], ["Hi!", "이봐!"], ["To be sure, how the neighbourhood poured into court that afternoon while the cause was on!", "그날 오후, 사건이 열리는 동안 온 동네가 어찌나 법정으로 몰려들었던지!"], ["How my noble and learned brother, and all the rest of ’em, grubbed and muddled away as usual and tried to look as if they hadn’t heard a word of the last fact in the case or as if they had—Oh, dear me!—nothing at all to do with it if they had heard of it by any chance!”", "우리 고귀하고 박식하신 형제분이며 다른 양반들 모두가 여느 때처럼 아등바등 뒤죽박죽 일을 처리하면서, 사건의 마지막 사실에 대해선 한마디도 못 들은 척, 아니면 설령 어쩌다 들었더라도—오, 저런!—자기들과는 아무 상관 없다는 척하려고 애쓰던 꼴이라니!\""]], [["Ada’s colour had entirely left her, and Richard was scarcely less pale.", "에이다의 핏기는 완전히 가셨고, 리처드도 그에 못잖게 창백했습니다. 저 자신의 감정으로 미루어 봐도 그럴 만했습니다."], ["Nor could I wonder, judging even from my emotions, and I was no party in the suit, that to hearts so untried and fresh it was a shock to come into the inheritance of a protracted misery, attended in the minds of many people with such dreadful recollections.", "저는 그 소송의 당사자도 아니었건만, 그토록 풋풋하고 시련을 모르는 마음에, 많은 이의 머릿속에 그토록 끔찍한 기억으로 얽힌 그 길고 긴 비참함을 물려받는다는 것이 충격일 수밖에 없었습니다. 저에게는 또 다른 불편함이 있었으니, 우리를 그곳으로 데려온 그 가엾고 얼이 반쯤 나간 사람에게 이 고통스러운 이야기가 들어맞는다는 점이었습니다."], ["I had another uneasiness, in the application of the painful story to the poor half-witted creature who had brought us there; but, to my surprise, she seemed perfectly unconscious of that and only led the way upstairs again, informing us with the toleration of a superior creature for the infirmities of a common mortal that her landlord was “a little—M—, you know!”", "그러나 놀랍게도 부인은 그 점을 전혀 의식하지 못한 채, 그저 다시 위층으로 앞장설 뿐이었고, 평범한 인간의 허물을 너그러이 보는 우월한 존재 같은 태도로, 자기 집주인이 \"머리가 좀—아시잖아요!\" 하고 우리에게 일러주었습니다."]], [["She lived at the top of the house, in a pretty large room, from which she had a glimpse of Lincoln’s Inn Hall.", "노부인은 집 꼭대기 층, 제법 큰 방에 살았는데, 그곳에서는 링컨스 인 홀이 빠끔히 내다보였습니다."], ["This seemed to have been her principal inducement, originally, for taking up her residence there.", "애초에 부인이 그곳에 거처를 정한 주된 이유가 이것인 듯했습니다."], ["She could look at it, she said, in the night, especially in the moonshine.", "부인은 밤이면, 특히 달빛이 비치면 그것을 바라볼 수 있다고 했습니다."], ["Her room was clean, but very, very bare.", "방은 깨끗했지만, 정말이지 너무나 휑했습니다."], ["I noticed the scantiest necessaries in the way of furniture; a few old prints from books, of Chancellors and barristers, wafered against the wall; and some half-dozen reticles and work-bags, “containing documents,”", "저는 가구라고는 더없이 빈약한 필수품 몇 가지뿐인 것을 알아챘습니다. 벽에는 책에서 뜯어낸 대법원장들과 법정 변호사들의 낡은 판화 몇 장이 풀로 붙어 있었고, 부인의 말로는 \"서류가 들어 있다\"는 손가방과 작업 가방이 대여섯 개쯤 있었습니다."], ["as she informed us. There were neither coals nor ashes in the grate, and I saw no articles of clothing anywhere, nor any kind of food.", "벽난로 안에는 석탄도 재도 없었고, 어디에도 옷가지 한 점, 음식 한 조각 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Upon a shelf in an open cupboard were a plate or two, a cup or two, and so forth, but all dry and empty.", "열린 찬장 선반 위에는 접시 한두 개, 잔 한두 개 따위가 있었지만 죄다 바싹 마른 채 텅 비어 있었습니다."], ["There was a more affecting meaning in her pinched appearance, I thought as I looked round, than I had understood before.", "방 안을 둘러보던 저는, 부인의 그 야윈 행색에 제가 전에 헤아렸던 것보다 한층 더 가슴 아픈 의미가 담겨 있다고 생각했습니다."]], [["“Extremely honoured, I am sure,”", "\"정말이지 더없는 영광이에요.\""], ["said our poor hostess with the greatest suavity, “by this visit from the wards in Jarndyce.", "가엾은 안주인이 더없이 상냥하게 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이 이렇게 찾아와 주시다니요."], ["And very much indebted for the omen.", "또 이 징조에 정말 큰 빚을 졌어요."], ["It is a retired situation.", "한적한 곳이지요."], ["Considering.", "그러고 보면요."], ["I am limited as to situation.", "저는 거처에 제약이 있답니다."], ["In consequence of the necessity of attending on the Chancellor.", "대법원장님 곁을 지켜야 할 필요 때문에요."], ["I have lived here many years.", "여기서 여러 해를 살았어요."], ["I pass my days in court, my evenings and my nights here.", "낮은 법정에서, 저녁과 밤은 여기서 보내지요."], ["I find the nights long, for I sleep but little and think much.", "밤이 길게 느껴져요. 잠은 거의 못 자고 생각은 많이 하거든요."], ["That is, of course, unavoidable, being in Chancery.", "그야 물론, 대법원에 얽힌 몸이니 어쩔 수 없는 일이지만요."], ["I am sorry I cannot offer chocolate.", "초콜릿을 대접하지 못해 죄송해요."], ["I expect a judgment shortly and shall then place my establishment on a superior footing.", "머잖아 판결이 날 거라, 그러면 살림을 더 나은 수준으로 꾸릴 생각이랍니다."], ["At present, I don’t mind confessing to the wards in Jarndyce (in strict confidence) that I sometimes find it difficult to keep up a genteel appearance.", "지금으로선, 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들께(아주 은밀히) 털어놓아도 괜찮겠지요. 가끔은 품위 있는 행색을 유지하기가 어렵다는 걸요."], ["I have felt the cold here.", "여기서 추위를 느껴왔어요."], ["I have felt something sharper than cold.", "추위보다 더 매서운 무언가를 느낀 적도 있고요."], ["It matters very little.", "별일 아니랍니다."], ["Pray excuse the introduction of such mean topics.”", "이런 누추한 이야기를 꺼낸 걸 부디 용서하세요.\""]], [["She partly drew aside the curtain of the long, low garret window and called our attention to a number of bird-cages hanging there, some containing several birds.", "노부인은 길고 낮은 다락방 창문의 커튼을 반쯤 젖히고, 거기 걸린 수많은 새장으로 우리의 눈길을 돌렸는데, 몇몇 새장에는 여러 마리가 들어 있었습니다."], ["There were larks, linnets, and goldfinches—I should think at least twenty.", "종다리, 홍방울새, 황금방울새가 있었으니—적어도 스무 마리는 되어 보였습니다."]], [["“I began to keep the little creatures,”", "\"제가 이 작은 것들을 기르기 시작한 건요,\""], ["she said, “with an object that the wards will readily comprehend.", "부인이 말했습니다. \"피후견인들이라면 금방 알아들을 만한 목적에서랍니다."], ["With the intention of restoring them to liberty.", "이 아이들을 자유로이 풀어주려는 뜻에서요."], ["When my judgment should be given.", "제 판결이 내려지면요. 그-럼요!"], ["Ye-es! They die in prison, though.", "그래도 이 아이들은 갇혀서 죽어요."], ["Their lives, poor silly things, are so short in comparison with Chancery proceedings that, one by one, the whole collection has died over and over again.", "가엾고 어리석은 것들의 목숨은 대법원 절차에 비하면 어찌나 짧은지, 한 마리 한 마리, 모은 새들이 죄다 거듭거듭 죽어 나갔답니다. 그거 아세요, 저는 의심스러워요."], ["I doubt, do you know, whether one of these, though they are all young, will live to be free!", "이 아이들이 다 어리긴 해도, 그중 한 마리라도 자유로워질 때까지 살아 있을지 말이에요!"], ["Ve-ry mortifying, is it not?”", "정말, 정말 비통한 일 아닌가요?\""]], [["Although she sometimes asked a question, she never seemed to expect a reply, but rambled on as if she were in the habit of doing so when no one but herself was present.", "노부인은 가끔 질문을 던졌지만, 정작 대답을 기대하는 법은 없었고, 혼자 있을 때 으레 그러던 버릇대로 두서없이 말을 이어갔습니다."]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\" 부인이 말을 이었습니다."], ["she pursued, “I positively doubt sometimes, I do assure you, whether while matters are still unsettled, and the sixth or Great Seal still prevails, I may not one day be found lying stark and senseless here, as I have found so many birds!”", "\"단언컨대, 저는 가끔 진심으로 의심한답니다. 일이 아직 매듭지어지지 않고, 여섯 번째 봉인, 곧 큰 봉인이 여전히 위세를 떨치는 동안, 제가 그 숱한 새들을 발견했듯이, 언젠가 저 자신도 여기 뻣뻣하게 넋을 잃고 누운 채 발견되는 게 아닐까 하고요!\""]], [["Richard, answering what he saw in Ada’s compassionate eyes, took the opportunity of laying some money, softly and unobserved, on the chimney-piece.", "리처드는 에이다의 안쓰러운 눈빛에서 읽어낸 마음에 답하듯, 그 틈을 타 아무도 모르게 돈을 살그머니 벽난로 선반 위에 올려놓았습니다."], ["We all drew nearer to the cages, feigning to examine the birds.", "우리는 다 같이 새를 살펴보는 척하며 새장으로 더 다가갔습니다."]], [["“I can’t allow them to sing much,”", "\"이 아이들이 너무 많이 노래하도록 둘 수가 없어요.\""], ["said the little old lady, “for (you’ll think this curious) I find my mind confused by the idea that they are singing while I am following the arguments in court.", "작은 노부인이 말했습니다. \"왜냐면(이상하게 들리시겠지만), 제가 법정에서 변론을 따라가는 동안 이 아이들이 노래하고 있다고 생각하면 정신이 어지러워지거든요."], ["And my mind requires to be so very clear, you know!", "제 정신은 정말, 정말 또렷해야 하는데 말이죠!"], ["Another time, I’ll tell you their names.", "이름은 다음번에 알려드릴게요. 지금 말고요."], ["Not at present. On a day of such good omen, they shall sing as much as they like.", "이렇게 좋은 징조가 깃든 날에는, 이 아이들이 노래하고 싶은 만큼 노래해도 좋아요."], ["In honour of youth,”", "젊음을 기려서,\" 미소와 절."], ["a smile and curtsy, “hope,”", "\"희망을 기려서,\" 미소와 절."], ["a smile and curtsy, “and beauty,”", "\"그리고 아름다움을 기려서요,\""], ["a smile and curtsy.", "미소와 절."], ["“There!", "\"자!"], ["We’ll let in the full light.”", "빛을 듬뿍 들이도록 하지요.\""]], [["The birds began to stir and chirp.", "새들이 부스럭대며 지저귀기 시작했습니다."]], [["“I cannot admit the air freely,”", "\"바람은 마음껏 들이지 못해요.\""], ["said the little old lady—the room was close, and would have been the better for it—“because the cat you saw downstairs, called Lady Jane, is greedy for their lives.", "작은 노부인이 말했습니다—방 안이 답답해서 바람을 들이면 한결 나았을 텐데도요—\"아래층에서 보신 레이디 제인이라는 고양이가 이 아이들 목숨을 노리거든요."], ["She crouches on the parapet outside for hours and hours.", "그 녀석이 바깥 난간에 몇 시간이고 웅크리고 앉아 있어요."], ["I have discovered,”", "제가 알아냈는데요,\""], ["whispering mysteriously, “that her natural cruelty is sharpened by a jealous fear of their regaining their liberty.", "부인이 알쏭달쏭하게 속삭였습니다. \"그 녀석의 타고난 잔인함은, 이 아이들이 다시 자유를 얻을까 봐 질투 어린 두려움에 더 날카로워진답니다."], ["In consequence of the judgment I expect being shortly given.", "제가 기다리는 판결이 머잖아 내려질 테니까요."], ["She is sly and full of malice.", "그 녀석은 교활하고 악의가 가득해요."], ["I half believe, sometimes, that she is no cat, but the wolf of the old saying.", "가끔은 그 녀석이 고양이가 아니라 옛말에 나오는 늑대가 아닐까 반쯤 믿어진다니까요."], ["It is so very difficult to keep her from the door.”", "그 녀석을 문에서 떼어놓기가 정말 어렵거든요.\""]], [["Some neighbouring bells, reminding the poor soul that it was half-past nine, did more for us in the way of bringing our visit to an end than we could easily have done for ourselves.", "근처 어딘가의 종소리가 그 가엾은 영혼에게 아홉 시 반이 되었음을 일깨워주자, 우리가 스스로 했더라면 쉽지 않았을 일—방문을 끝맺는 일—을 종소리가 대신 해주었습니다."], ["She hurriedly took up her little bag of documents, which she had laid upon the table on coming in, and asked if we were also going into court.", "부인은 들어올 때 탁자에 올려놓았던 작은 서류 가방을 황급히 집어 들고, 우리도 법정에 가는 길이냐고 물었습니다."], ["On our answering no, and that we would on no account detain her, she opened the door to attend us downstairs.", "우리가 아니라고, 결코 부인을 붙잡지 않겠다고 답하자, 부인은 우리를 배웅하러 아래층까지 가려고 문을 열었습니다."]], [["“With such an omen, it is even more necessary than usual that I should be there before the Chancellor comes in,”", "\"이런 징조가 있는 날에는, 평소보다 더더욱 제가 대법원장님이 들어오시기 전에 그곳에 가 있어야 해요.\""], ["said she, “for he might mention my case the first thing.", "부인이 말했습니다. \"맨 먼저 제 사건을 언급하실지도 모르거든요."], ["I have a presentiment that he WILL mention it the first thing this morning.”", "오늘 아침엔 그분이 맨 먼저 제 사건을 언급하실 거라는 예감이 들어요.\""]], [["She stopped to tell us in a whisper as we were going down that the whole house was filled with strange lumber which her landlord had bought piecemeal and had no wish to sell, in consequence of being a little M.", "부인은 아래층으로 내려가면서, 온 집 안이 집주인이 한 점 한 점 사들였으나 팔 마음이 없는 기묘한 잡동사니로 가득 차 있다고 속삭여 일러주었는데, 그것도 집주인이 머리가 좀 모자란 탓이라고 했습니다."], ["This was on the first floor.", "이것은 이 층에서였습니다."], ["But she had made a previous stoppage on the second floor and had silently pointed at a dark door there.", "그런데 부인은 그보다 앞서 삼 층에서 한 번 멈춰 서서, 거기 있는 어두운 문 하나를 말없이 가리켰던 적이 있습니다."]], [["“The only other lodger,”", "\"단 하나뿐인 다른 세입자예요.\""], ["she now whispered in explanation, “a law-writer.", "이제 부인이 속삭이며 설명했습니다. \"법률 필경사죠."], ["The children in the lanes here say he has sold himself to the devil.", "이 동네 골목 아이들은 그 사람이 악마에게 자기를 팔았다고 한답니다."], ["I don’t know what he can have done with the money.", "그 돈으로 뭘 했는지는 모르겠어요."], ["Hush!”", "쉿!\""]], [["She appeared to mistrust that the lodger might hear her even there, and repeating “Hush!”", "* 삼 층, 이 층: 영국식 층수 표기로, 영국에서는 우리식 일 층을 ground floor, 이 층을 first floor라 부른다."], ["went before us on tiptoe as though even the sound of her footsteps might reveal to him what she had said.", "여기서는 우리식 이 층(원문 first floor), 삼 층(원문 second floor)에 해당한다."]], [["Passing through the shop on our way out, as we had passed through it on our way in, we found the old man storing a quantity of packets of waste-paper in a kind of well in the floor.", "들어올 때 가게를 지나왔듯이 나갈 때도 가게를 지나가는데, 우리가 보니 노인은 바닥에 난 우물 같은 구멍에 휴지 꾸러미를 한 무더기 쟁여 넣고 있었습니다."], ["He seemed to be working hard, with the perspiration standing on his forehead, and had a piece of chalk by him, with which, as he put each separate package or bundle down, he made a crooked mark on the panelling of the wall.", "이마에 땀이 송골송골 맺힌 채 열심히 일하는 듯했고, 곁에 분필을 한 자루 두고서, 꾸러미나 뭉치를 하나씩 내려놓을 때마다 벽 판자에 삐뚤빼뚤한 표시를 했습니다."]], [["Richard and Ada, and Miss Jellyby, and the little old lady had gone by him, and I was going when he touched me on the arm to stay me, and chalked the letter J upon the wall—in a very curious manner, beginning with the end of the letter and shaping it backward.", "리처드와 에이다, 젤리비 양, 그리고 작은 노부인이 그를 지나쳐 갔고, 저도 막 지나가려는데 노인이 제 팔을 건드려 멈춰 세우더니, 벽에 J라는 글자를 분필로 적었습니다—아주 기묘한 방식으로, 글자의 끝에서 시작해 거꾸로 모양을 잡아가면서요."], ["It was a capital letter, not a printed one, but just such a letter as any clerk in Messrs. Kenge and Carboy’s office would have made.", "그것은 대문자였고, 인쇄체가 아니라, 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소의 어느 사무원이라도 썼을 법한 그런 글자였습니다."]], [["“Can you read it?”", "\"이걸 읽을 수 있소?\""], ["he asked me with a keen glance.", "노인이 날카로운 눈길로 제게 물었습니다."]], [["“Surely,”", "\"물론이죠.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."], ["“It’s very plain.”", "\"아주 또렷한데요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭐요?\" \"J예요.\""]], [["“J.” With another glance at me, and a glance at the door, he rubbed it out and turned an “a”", "노인은 제게 다시 한 번 눈길을 던지고, 문 쪽으로도 눈길을 던지더니, 그것을 지우고 그 자리에 \"a\"를 만들어 넣고(이번엔 대문자가 아니었습니다), \"이건 뭐요?\""], ["in its place (not a capital letter this time), and said, “What’s that?”", "하고 물었습니다."]], [["I told him.", "저는 알려주었습니다."], ["He then rubbed that out and turned the letter “r,” and asked me the same question.", "그러자 노인은 그것을 지우고 \"r\"이라는 글자를 만들어 똑같은 질문을 던졌습니다."], ["He went on quickly until he had formed in the same curious manner, beginning at the ends and bottoms of the letters, the word Jarndyce, without once leaving two letters on the wall together.", "노인은 빠르게 이어가며, 똑같이 기묘한 방식으로, 글자의 끝과 아래쪽에서 시작해, 벽에 두 글자를 한꺼번에 남겨두는 일이 한 번도 없이, 잔다이스라는 단어를 완성했습니다."]], [["“What does that spell?”", "\"이건 무슨 글자가 되오?\""], ["he asked me.", "노인이 제게 물었습니다."]], [["When I told him, he laughed.", "제가 알려주자 노인은 웃었습니다."], ["In the same odd way, yet with the same rapidity, he then produced singly, and rubbed out singly, the letters forming the words Bleak House.", "그러고는 그 똑같이 별난 방식으로, 하지만 그 똑같은 빠르기로, 황폐한 집이라는 단어를 이루는 글자들을 하나씩 만들었다가 하나씩 지웠습니다."], ["These, in some astonishment, I also read; and he laughed again.", "저는 다소 놀라며 그것도 읽어냈고, 노인은 다시 웃었습니다."]], [["“Hi!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["said the old man, laying aside the chalk.", "노인이 분필을 내려놓으며 말했습니다."], ["“I have a turn for copying from memory, you see, miss, though I can neither read nor write.”", "\"보다시피 난 기억으로 베껴 쓰는 데 재주가 있다오, 아가씨. 읽지도 쓰지도 못하지만 말이오.\""]], [["He looked so disagreeable and his cat looked so wickedly at me, as if I were a blood-relation of the birds upstairs, that I was quite relieved by Richard’s appearing at the door and saying, “Miss Summerson, I hope you are not bargaining for the sale of your hair.", "노인이 어찌나 거슬려 보이고, 그 고양이가 마치 제가 위층 새들의 피붙이라도 되는 양 어찌나 사납게 저를 노려보던지, 리처드가 문간에 나타나 이렇게 말해주었을 때 저는 정말이지 마음이 놓였습니다. \"서머슨 양, 설마 머리카락을 팔려고 흥정하시는 건 아니겠죠."], ["Don’t be tempted.", "넘어가지 마세요."], ["Three sacks below are quite enough for Mr. Krook!”", "아래층에 세 자루면 크룩 씨한테는 차고 넘쳐요!\""]], [["I lost no time in wishing Mr.", "저는 지체 없이 크룩 씨에게 안녕히 계시라 인사하고 밖에서 친구들과 합류했습니다."], ["Krook good morning and joining my friends outside, where we parted with the little old lady, who gave us her blessing with great ceremony and renewed her assurance of yesterday in reference to her intention of settling estates on Ada and me.", "거기서 우리는 작은 노부인과 작별했는데, 부인은 자못 격식을 차려 우리에게 축복을 빌어주고, 에이다와 제게 재산을 물려주겠다던 어제의 다짐을 거듭 일러주었습니다."], ["Before we finally turned out of those lanes, we looked back and saw Mr. Krook standing at his shop-door, in his spectacles, looking after us, with his cat upon his shoulder, and her tail sticking up on one side of his hairy cap like a tall feather.", "마침내 그 골목들을 벗어나기 전에 우리가 뒤를 돌아보니, 크룩 씨가 안경을 쓴 채 어깨에 고양이를 얹고 가게 문간에 서서 우리 뒷모습을 바라보고 있었는데, 고양이 꼬리가 그 털모자 한쪽 옆으로 마치 길쭉한 깃털처럼 곧추서 있었습니다."]], [["“Quite an adventure for a morning in London!”", "\"런던에서 보낸 아침치고는 제법 모험이었어!\""], ["said Richard with a sigh.", "리처드가 한숨을 쉬며 말했습니다."], ["“Ah, cousin, cousin, it’s a weary word this Chancery!”", "\"아아, 사촌, 사촌, 대법원이란 정말 지긋지긋한 말이야!\""]], [["“It is to me, and has been ever since I can remember,”", "\"제게도 그래요. 기억하는 한 줄곧 그랬고요.\""], ["returned Ada.", "에이다가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I am grieved that I should be the enemy—as I suppose I am—of a great number of relations and others, and that they should be my enemies—as I suppose they are—and that we should all be ruining one another without knowing how or why and be in constant doubt and discord all our lives.", "\"수많은 친척이며 다른 사람들의 원수가—제가 그런 모양이듯이—되어야 하고, 그들 또한 제 원수가—그런 모양이듯이—되어야 하고, 어떻게, 왜인지도 모른 채 우리 모두가 서로를 파멸시키며, 평생 끊임없는 의심과 불화 속에 살아가야 한다니 슬퍼요."], ["It seems very strange, as there must be right somewhere, that an honest judge in real earnest has not been able to find out through all these years where it is.”", "어딘가엔 분명 옳은 게 있을 텐데, 정직한 판사가 진심으로 애써도 이 긴 세월 내내 그게 어디 있는지 찾아내지 못했다니, 정말 이상해요.\""]], [["“Ah, cousin!”", "\"아아, 사촌!\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“Strange, indeed!", "\"정말 이상하지!"], ["All this wasteful, wanton chess-playing IS very strange.", "이 모든 낭비투성이에 제멋대로인 장기 놀음이란 게 정말 이상해."], ["To see that composed court yesterday jogging on so serenely and to think of the wretchedness of the pieces on the board gave me the headache and the heartache both together.", "어제 그 차분한 법정이 그토록 태연하게 굴러가는 걸 보면서, 판 위에 놓인 말들의 비참함을 떠올리니, 머리도 아프고 가슴도 아프더라."], ["My head ached with wondering how it happened, if men were neither fools nor rascals; and my heart ached to think they could possibly be either.", "사람들이 바보도 악당도 아니라면 어떻게 이런 일이 벌어지는지 의아해서 머리가 아팠고, 그들이 바보거나 악당일 수도 있다고 생각하니 가슴이 아팠어."], ["But at all events, Ada—I may call you Ada?”", "하지만 어쨌든, 에이다—에이다라고 불러도 될까?\""]], [["“Of course you may, cousin Richard.”", "\"물론이죠, 리처드 사촌.\""]], [["“At all events, Chancery will work none of its bad influences on US.", "\"하지만 어쨌든, 대법원은 우리한테는 그 나쁜 영향을 조금도 못 미칠 거야."], ["We have happily been brought together, thanks to our good kinsman, and it can’t divide us now!”", "고마운 친척 어른 덕분에 우린 다행히 한자리에 모였고, 이제 대법원도 우릴 갈라놓진 못해!\""]], [["“Never, I hope, cousin Richard!”", "\"그러길 바라요, 리처드 사촌!\""], ["said Ada gently.", "에이다가 부드럽게 말했습니다."]], [["Miss Jellyby gave my arm a squeeze and me a very significant look.", "젤리비 양이 제 팔을 꼭 쥐며 제게 의미심장한 눈길을 보냈습니다."], ["I smiled in return, and we made the rest of the way back very pleasantly.", "저는 미소로 답했고, 우리는 돌아오는 나머지 길을 아주 즐겁게 걸었습니다."]], [["In half an hour after our arrival, Mrs. Jellyby appeared; and in the course of an hour the various things necessary for breakfast straggled one by one into the dining-room.", "우리가 도착하고 반 시간이 지나서야 젤리비 부인이 나타났고, 한 시간이 흐르는 동안 아침 식사에 필요한 갖가지 물건이 하나씩 하나씩 식당으로 어슬렁어슬렁 흘러들어 왔습니다."], ["I do not doubt that Mrs. Jellyby had gone to bed and got up in the usual manner, but she presented no appearance of having changed her dress.", "젤리비 부인이 여느 사람처럼 잠자리에 들었다가 일어났음을 저는 의심하지 않지만, 부인은 옷을 갈아입은 기색이라곤 전혀 없었습니다."], ["She was greatly occupied during breakfast, for the morning’s post brought a heavy correspondence relative to Borrioboola-Gha, which would occasion her (she said) to pass a busy day.", "부인은 아침 식사 내내 무척 바빴는데, 아침 우편으로 보리오불라가 관련 서신이 잔뜩 도착해, 그 탓에(부인 말로는) 분주한 하루를 보내게 생겼기 때문입니다."], ["The children tumbled about, and notched memoranda of their accidents in their legs, which were perfect little calendars of distress; and Peepy was lost for an hour and a half, and brought home from Newgate market by a policeman.", "아이들은 여기저기 나뒹굴며 다친 자국을 다리에 흠집처럼 새겼는데, 그 다리는 그야말로 작은 고난의 달력이었습니다. 피피는 한 시간 반 동안 사라졌다가 경관 손에 이끌려 뉴게이트 시장에서 돌아왔습니다."], ["The equable manner in which Mrs. Jellyby sustained both his absence and his restoration to the family circle surprised us all.", "젤리비 부인이 피피가 사라진 일과 가족의 품으로 돌아온 일을 다 같이 그토록 태연하게 받아들이는 데에는 우리 모두 놀랐습니다."]], [["She was by that time perseveringly dictating to Caddy, and Caddy was fast relapsing into the inky condition in which we had found her.", "그때쯤 부인은 끈질기게 캐디에게 받아쓰기를 시키고 있었고, 캐디는 우리가 처음 보았을 때처럼 빠르게 다시 잉크투성이 꼴로 되돌아가고 있었습니다. 한 시에 우리를 태울 무개 마차가 왔고, 짐을 실을 짐수레도 왔습니다."], ["At one o’clock an open carriage arrived for us, and a cart for our luggage.", "젤리비 부인은 좋은 친구 잔다이스 씨에게 안부를 잔뜩 전해달라고 우리에게 신신당부했습니다."], ["Mrs. Jellyby charged us with many remembrances to her good friend Mr.", "캐디는 우리가 떠나는 것을 보려고 책상을 떠나, 복도에서 제게 입을 맞추고는, 펜을 깨물며 계단에 서서 흐느꼈습니다."], ["Jarndyce; Caddy left her desk to see us depart, kissed me in the passage, and stood biting her pen and sobbing on the steps; Peepy, I am happy to say, was asleep and spared the pain of separation (I was not without misgivings that he had gone to Newgate market in search of me); and all the other children got up behind the barouche and fell off, and we saw them, with great concern, scattered over the surface of Thavies Inn as we rolled out of its precincts.", "다행히도 피피는 잠들어 있어 이별의 아픔을 면했습니다(피피가 저를 찾아 뉴게이트 시장에 갔던 게 아닐까 하는 께름칙한 마음이 들지 않은 건 아니었습니다). 그리고 다른 아이들은 죄다 사륜마차 뒤에 올라탔다가 떨어졌고, 우리가 세이비스 여관 구역을 빠져나가며 굴러갈 때, 아이들이 그 일대에 흩어져 있는 것을 우리는 무척 안쓰러운 마음으로 바라보았습니다."]], [["Quite at Home\nThe day had brightened very much, and still brightened as we went westward.", "날이 한결 환하게 갰고, 우리가 서쪽으로 갈수록 더욱 환해졌다. 우리는 햇살과 신선한 바람을 가르며 길을 갔는데, 거리가 끝없이 이어지는 광경에, 상점들이 휘황찬란하게 빛나는 모습에, 어마어마한 마차 행렬에, 그리고 더 좋아진 날씨가 빛깔도 가지가지인 꽃처럼 거리로 불러낸 듯한 사람들 무리에 갈수록 감탄했다."], ["We went our way through the sunshine and the fresh air, wondering more and more at the extent of the streets, the brilliancy of the shops, the great traffic, and the crowds of people whom the pleasanter weather seemed to have brought out like many-coloured flowers.", "이윽고 우리는 그 놀라운 도시를 벗어나, 그것만으로도 내 눈에는 제법 큰 도시로 보일 만한 변두리를 지나기 시작했고, 마침내 다시 진짜 시골길로 접어들었다. 풍차가 있고, 짚가리 마당이 있고, 이정표가 있고, 농부들의 짐마차가 있고, 묵은 건초 냄새가 나고, 흔들리는 간판이 있고, 말 물통이 있는 길."], ["By and by we began to leave the wonderful city and to proceed through suburbs which, of themselves, would have made a pretty large town in my eyes; and at last we got into a real country road again, with windmills, rick-yards, milestones, farmers’ waggons, scents of old hay, swinging signs, and horse troughs: trees, fields, and hedge-rows.", "나무와 들판과 산울타리가 있는 길. 눈앞에 펼쳐진 초록빛 풍경과 등 뒤에 자리한 거대한 대도시를 바라보는 것은 즐거웠다."], ["It was delightful to see the green landscape before us and the immense metropolis behind; and when a waggon with a train of beautiful horses, furnished with red trappings and clear-sounding bells, came by us with its music, I believe we could all three have sung to the bells, so cheerful were the influences around.", "그러다가 붉은 마구를 두르고 맑은 소리를 내는 방울을 단 아름다운 말들이 줄지어 끄는 짐마차가 그 음악과 함께 우리를 스쳐 지날 때, 나는 우리 셋이 다 그 방울 소리에 맞춰 노래라도 부를 수 있었으리라 믿는다. 주위의 분위기가 그토록 흥겨웠다."]], [["“The whole road has been reminding me of my namesake Whittington,”", "\"이 길 내내 내 이름의 주인공인 휘팅턴이 떠오르더라니.\""], ["said Richard, “and that waggon is the finishing touch.", "리처드가 말했다. \"그런데 저 짐마차가 마지막 화룡점정이군."], ["Halloa!", "어이!"], ["What’s the matter?”", "무슨 일이지?\""]], [["We had stopped, and the waggon had stopped too.", "우리가 멈춰 섰는데, 짐마차도 멈춰 섰다."], ["Its music changed as the horses came to a stand, and subsided to a gentle tinkling, except when a horse tossed his head or shook himself and sprinkled off a little shower of bell-ringing.", "말들이 서자 그 음악이 바뀌어 잔잔한 딸랑임으로 잦아들었다. 다만 말이 머리를 흔들거나 몸을 떨 때마다 방울 소리가 한차례 소나기처럼 흩뿌려졌다."]], [["“Our postilion is looking after the waggoner,”", "\"우리 마부가 짐마차꾼을 부르고 있어.\""], ["said Richard, “and the waggoner is coming back after us.", "리처드가 말했다. \"그리고 짐마차꾼은 우리 쪽으로 되돌아오는군."], ["Good day, friend!”", "안녕하시오, 친구!\""], ["The waggoner was at our coach-door.", "짐마차꾼이 우리 마차 문 앞에 와 있었다."], ["“Why, here’s an extraordinary thing!”", "\"아니, 이거 참 기막힌 일인데!\""], ["added Richard, looking closely at the man.", "리처드가 그 사람을 유심히 보며 덧붙였다."], ["“He has got your name, Ada, in his hat!”", "\"이 사람 모자에 네 이름이 적혀 있어, 에이다!\""]], [["He had all our names in his hat.", "그의 모자에는 우리 이름이 다 적혀 있었다."], ["Tucked within the band were three small notes—one addressed to Ada, one to Richard, one to me.", "모자 띠 안에 작은 쪽지 세 장이 끼워져 있었는데, 하나는 에이다에게, 하나는 리처드에게, 하나는 나에게 온 것이었다."], ["These the waggoner delivered to each of us respectively, reading the name aloud first.", "짐마당꾼은 먼저 이름을 큰 소리로 읽고 나서 우리 각자에게 그것을 건네주었다. 누가 보낸 거냐는 리처드의 물음에 그는 짤막하게 대답했다."], ["In answer to Richard’s inquiry from whom they came, he briefly answered, “Master, sir, if you please”; and putting on his hat again (which was like a soft bowl), cracked his whip, re-awakened his music, and went melodiously away.", "\"주인 어른께서요, 나리, 괜찮으시다면요.\" 그러고는 부드러운 사발 같은 모자를 다시 쓰고, 채찍을 철썩 울리고, 그 음악을 다시 깨우더니, 가락에 실려 멀어져 갔다."]], [["“Is that Mr. Jarndyce’s waggon?”", "\"저게 잔다이스 씨의 짐마차요?\""], ["said Richard, calling to our post-boy.", "리처드가 우리 마부 소년을 향해 외치며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"예, 나리.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Going to London.”", "\"런던으로 가는 길입니다.\""]], [["We opened the notes.", "우리는 쪽지를 펼쳤다."], ["Each was a counterpart of the other and contained these words in a solid, plain hand.", "모두 똑같은 내용으로, 또박또박 꾸밈없는 필체로 이런 말이 적혀 있었다."]], [["I look forward, my dear, to our meeting easily and without constraint on either side.", "나는, 사랑하는 이여, 우리가 어느 쪽에도 거리낌이나 부담 없이 편안하게 만나기를 고대한다."], ["I therefore have to propose that we meet as old friends and take the past for granted.", "그래서 우리가 오랜 벗처럼 만나, 지난 일은 당연한 것으로 묻어두자고 제안하는 바이다."], ["It will be a relief to you possibly, and to me certainly, and so my love to you.", "어쩌면 너에게도 홀가분할 것이고, 나에게는 분명히 그러할 것이니, 그럼 너에게 사랑을 전한다."]], [["John Jarndyce", "존 잔다이스"]], [["I had perhaps less reason to be surprised than either of my companions, having never yet enjoyed an opportunity of thanking one who had been my benefactor and sole earthly dependence through so many years. I had not considered how I could thank him, my gratitude lying too deep in my heart for that; but I now began to consider how I could meet him without thanking him, and felt it would be very difficult indeed.", "나는 두 동행보다 어쩌면 놀랄 이유가 덜했을 것이다. 그토록 오랜 세월 나의 은인이자 이 세상에서 유일하게 기댈 데였던 분에게 감사를 전할 기회를 아직 한 번도 누리지 못했기 때문이다. 어떻게 감사를 전할지 생각해 본 적도 없었다. 내 고마움이 그러기에는 가슴속에 너무 깊이 자리하고 있었으니까. 그러나 이제 나는 어떻게 하면 감사를 전하지 않으면서 그분을 만날 수 있을까 생각하기 시작했고, 그것이 실로 무척 어려우리라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["The notes revived in Richard and Ada a general impression that they both had, without quite knowing how they came by it, that their cousin Jarndyce could never bear acknowledgments for any kindness he performed and that sooner than receive any he would resort to the most singular expedients and evasions or would even run away.", "그 쪽지는 리처드와 에이다에게도, 어디서 비롯되었는지는 분명히 알지 못한 채 둘 다 막연히 품고 있던 인상을 되살려 놓았다. 그것은 사촌 잔다이스가 자기가 베푼 어떤 친절에 대해서도 감사 인사를 결코 견디지 못하며, 감사를 받느니 차라리 더없이 별난 술수와 회피를 동원하거나 심지어 도망쳐 버리기까지 하리라는 것이었다."], ["Ada dimly remembered to have heard her mother tell, when she was a very little child, that he had once done her an act of uncommon generosity and that on her going to his house to thank him, he happened to see her through a window coming to the door, and immediately escaped by the back gate, and was not heard of for three months.", "에이다는 아주 어린아이였을 적에 어머니가 들려준 이야기를 어렴풋이 기억했다. 그가 한번은 어머니에게 보기 드문 후한 일을 베풀었는데, 어머니가 감사를 전하러 그의 집에 갔더니, 그가 마침 창문 너머로 어머니가 문간으로 다가오는 것을 보고는 곧장 뒷문으로 달아나 석 달 동안 소식이 없었다는 것이다."], ["This discourse led to a great deal more on the same theme, and indeed it lasted us all day, and we talked of scarcely anything else.", "이 이야기는 같은 주제로 한참 더 이어졌고, 사실 하루 종일 이어져서, 우리는 거의 다른 이야기는 하지 않았다."], ["If we did by any chance diverge into another subject, we soon returned to this, and wondered what the house would be like, and when we should get there, and whether we should see Mr. Jarndyce as soon as we arrived or after a delay, and what he would say to us, and what we should say to him.", "어쩌다 다른 화제로 빠지더라도 이내 이 이야기로 돌아와, 그 집은 어떻게 생겼을지, 언제 도착할지, 도착하자마자 잔다이스 씨를 뵙게 될지 아니면 좀 기다린 뒤일지, 그분이 우리에게 무슨 말씀을 하실지, 우리는 그분께 무슨 말씀을 드려야 할지 궁금해했다."], ["All of which we wondered about, over and over again.", "이 모든 것을 우리는 거듭거듭 궁금해했다."]], [["The roads were very heavy for the horses, but the pathway was generally good, so we alighted and walked up all the hills, and liked it so well that we prolonged our walk on the level ground when we got to the top.", "길은 말들에게는 몹시 험했지만 보도는 대체로 좋았다. 그래서 우리는 마차에서 내려 언덕은 모조리 걸어 올라갔고, 그게 어찌나 좋았던지 꼭대기에 닿으면 평평한 땅에서도 걸음을 더 이어갔다."], ["At Barnet there were other horses waiting for us, but as they had only just been fed, we had to wait for them too, and got a long fresh walk over a common and an old battle-field before the carriage came up.", "바닛에는 우리를 위한 다른 말들이 기다리고 있었지만, 마침 먹이를 막 먹은 참이라 우리는 또 그 말들을 기다려야 했고, 마차가 따라오기 전에 공유지와 옛 전쟁터를 가로질러 한참 동안 상쾌하게 걸었다."], ["These delays so protracted the journey that the short day was spent and the long night had closed in before we came to St.", "이런 지체들로 여정이 길어져, 짧은 낮이 다 가고 긴 밤이 내려앉은 뒤에야 우리는 세인트올번스에 닿았다."], ["Albans, near to which town Bleak House was, we knew.", "우리가 알기로 황폐한 집은 그 마을 근처에 있었다."]], [["By that time we were so anxious and nervous that even Richard confessed, as we rattled over the stones of the old street, to feeling an irrational desire to drive back again.", "그때쯤 우리는 어찌나 마음을 졸이고 안절부절못했던지, 옛 거리의 돌바닥 위를 덜컹거리며 지날 무렵에는 리처드조차 도로 돌아가고 싶은 이치에 닿지 않는 충동을 느꼈노라고 털어놓았다."], ["As to Ada and me, whom he had wrapped up with great care, the night being sharp and frosty, we trembled from head to foot.", "그가 밤이 매섭고 서리가 내려 정성껏 몸을 감싸준 에이다와 나로 말하자면, 우리는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨었다."], ["When we turned out of the town, round a corner, and Richard told us that the post-boy, who had for a long time sympathized with our heightened expectation, was looking back and nodding, we both stood up in the carriage (Richard holding Ada lest she should be jolted down) and gazed round upon the open country and the starlight night for our destination.", "마을을 벗어나 모퉁이를 돌 때, 한참 전부터 우리의 고조된 기대에 동조해 오던 마부 소년이 뒤를 돌아보며 고개를 끄덕인다고 리처드가 일러주자, 우리는 둘 다 마차 안에서 일어서서(에이다가 흔들려 넘어지지 않도록 리처드가 붙잡아 주었다) 우리의 목적지를 찾아 탁 트인 들판과 별빛 어린 밤하늘을 둘러보았다."], ["There was a light sparkling on the top of a hill before us, and the driver, pointing to it with his whip and crying, “That’s Bleak House!” put his horses into a canter and took us forward at such a rate, uphill though it was, that the wheels sent the road drift flying about our heads like spray from a water-mill.", "우리 앞 언덕 꼭대기에 불빛 하나가 반짝이고 있었고, 마부가 채찍으로 그것을 가리키며 \"저게 황폐한 집입니다!\"라고 외치더니, 말들을 가벼운 구보로 몰아 비탈길인데도 어찌나 빠른 속도로 우리를 앞으로 데려갔던지, 바퀴가 물레방아에서 튀는 물보라처럼 길의 흙먼지를 우리 머리 위로 흩날려 보냈다."], ["Presently we lost the light, presently saw it, presently lost it, presently saw it, and turned into an avenue of trees and cantered up towards where it was beaming brightly.", "이내 우리는 그 불빛을 놓쳤고, 이내 다시 보았고, 이내 놓쳤다가, 이내 다시 보더니, 가로수 길로 접어들어 불빛이 환하게 비치는 쪽으로 구보로 올라갔다."], ["It was in a window of what seemed to be an old-fashioned house with three peaks in the roof in front and a circular sweep leading to the porch.", "그것은 앞쪽 지붕에 세 개의 뾰족한 봉우리가 솟고 현관까지 둥글게 이어지는 진입로가 딸린, 고풍스러워 보이는 집의 한 창문에서 새어 나오는 불빛이었다."], ["A bell was rung as we drew up, and amidst the sound of its deep voice in the still air, and the distant barking of some dogs, and a gush of light from the opened door, and the smoking and steaming of the heated horses, and the quickened beating of our own hearts, we alighted in no inconsiderable confusion.", "우리가 다다르자 종이 울렸고, 고요한 공기 속 그 깊은 종소리와, 어디선가 멀리서 개들이 짖는 소리와, 열린 문에서 쏟아지는 불빛과, 달아오른 말들이 내뿜는 김과 연기와, 우리 자신의 빨라진 심장 박동 한가운데서, 우리는 적잖은 혼란 속에 마차에서 내렸다."]], [["“Ada, my love, Esther, my dear, you are welcome.", "\"에이다, 사랑스러운 아이야, 에스더, 귀여운 아이야, 잘 왔다."], ["I rejoice to see you!", "너희를 보니 정말 기쁘구나!"], ["Rick, if I had a hand to spare at present, I would give it you!”", "릭, 지금 손이 하나라도 남아 있다면 너에게 내밀 텐데!\""]], [["The gentleman who said these words in a clear, bright, hospitable voice had one of his arms round Ada’s waist and the other round mine, and kissed us both in a fatherly way, and bore us across the hall into a ruddy little room, all in a glow with a blazing fire.", "맑고 밝고 정겨운 목소리로 이 말을 한 신사는 한쪽 팔로 에이다의 허리를, 다른 팔로 내 허리를 감싸 안고, 아버지처럼 우리 둘에게 입을 맞춘 뒤, 우리를 홀 너머로 데리고 가, 활활 타오르는 불로 온통 발그레하게 빛나는 자그마한 방으로 안내했다."], ["Here he kissed us again, and opening his arms, made us sit down side by side on a sofa ready drawn out near the hearth.", "여기서 그는 다시 우리에게 입을 맞추고, 두 팔을 벌려 우리를 벽난로 옆에 미리 끌어다 놓은 소파에 나란히 앉게 했다."], ["I felt that if we had been at all demonstrative, he would have run away in a moment.", "나는 우리가 조금이라도 감정을 드러냈다면 그가 당장 달아나 버렸으리라는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Now, Rick!”", "\"자, 릭!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I have a hand at liberty.", "\"손이 하나 비었구나."], ["A word in earnest is as good as a speech.", "진심 어린 한마디면 일장 연설만큼 값지지."], ["I am heartily glad to see you.", "너를 보니 진심으로 기쁘다."], ["You are at home.", "여기가 네 집이야."], ["Warm yourself!”", "몸 좀 녹여라!\""]], [["Richard shook him by both hands with an intuitive mixture of respect and frankness, and only saying (though with an earnestness that rather alarmed me, I was so afraid of Mr.", "리처드는 본능적으로 존경과 솔직함이 뒤섞인 태도로 그의 두 손을 맞잡고 흔들며, 다만 이렇게만 말했다(그런데 그 말투가 어찌나 진지했던지 나는 잔다이스 씨가 갑자기 사라져 버릴까 봐 너무 겁이 나서 오히려 놀랐다)."], ["Jarndyce’s suddenly disappearing), “You are very kind, sir!", "\"정말 친절하십니다, 어르신!"], ["We are very much obliged to you!”", "정말 큰 신세를 졌습니다!\""], ["laid aside his hat and coat and came up to the fire.", "그러고는 모자와 외투를 벗어 놓고 불가로 다가왔다."]], [["“And how did you like the ride?", "\"그래, 오는 길은 어땠느냐?"], ["And how did you like Mrs. Jellyby, my dear?”", "그리고 젤리비 부인은 어떻더냐, 얘야?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce to Ada.", "잔다이스 씨가 에이다에게 물었다."]], [["While Ada was speaking to him in reply, I glanced (I need not say with how much interest) at his face.", "에이다가 그에게 대답을 하는 동안, 나는 (얼마나 큰 관심을 가지고 그랬는지는 말할 것도 없으리라) 그의 얼굴을 흘끗 보았다."], ["It was a handsome, lively, quick face, full of change and motion; and his hair was a silvered iron-grey.", "그것은 잘생기고 생기 있고 민첩한 얼굴로, 변화와 움직임이 가득했다. 그리고 그의 머리카락은 은빛이 도는 쇳빛 회색이었다."], ["I took him to be nearer sixty than fifty, but he was upright, hearty, and robust.", "나는 그를 쉰보다는 예순에 가깝다고 보았으나, 그는 꼿꼿하고 정정하고 다부졌다."], ["From the moment of his first speaking to us his voice had connected itself with an association in my mind that I could not define; but now, all at once, a something sudden in his manner and a pleasant expression in his eyes recalled the gentleman in the stagecoach six years ago on the memorable day of my journey to Reading.", "그가 우리에게 처음 말을 건넨 순간부터 그의 목소리는 내 마음속에서 도무지 짚어낼 수 없는 어떤 기억과 이어졌다. 그런데 이제, 별안간, 그의 태도에서 느껴진 어떤 갑작스러움과 그의 눈에 어린 정다운 표정이, 육 년 전 내가 리딩으로 가던 그 잊지 못할 날에 역마차에서 본 그 신사를 떠올리게 했다."], ["I was certain it was he.", "나는 그가 바로 그 사람임을 확신했다."], ["I never was so frightened in my life as when I made the discovery, for he caught my glance, and appearing to read my thoughts, gave such a look at the door that I thought we had lost him.", "그 사실을 깨달았을 때처럼 평생 그토록 겁이 났던 적은 없었으니, 그가 내 눈길을 알아채고 내 생각을 읽기라도 한 듯 문 쪽을 어찌나 쳐다보던지, 나는 우리가 그를 놓쳐 버린 줄 알았다."]], [["However, I am happy to say he remained where he was, and asked me what I thought of Mrs. Jellyby.", "그러나 다행스럽게도 그는 그 자리에 그대로 있었고, 나에게 젤리비 부인을 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물었다."]], [["“She exerts herself very much for Africa, sir,”", "\"그분은 아프리카를 위해 무척 애쓰시더군요, 어르신.\""], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Nobly!”", "\"고결하지!\""], ["returned Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["“But you answer like Ada.”", "\"그런데 에이다처럼 대답하는구나.\""], ["Whom I had not heard.", "나는 에이다가 그렇게 말하는 걸 듣지 못했다."], ["“You all think something else, I see.”", "\"보아하니 너희 모두 다른 생각을 하고 있구나.\""]], [["“We rather thought,”", "\"저희는 차라리 이렇게 생각했어요.\""], ["said I, glancing at Richard and Ada, who entreated me with their eyes to speak, “that perhaps she was a little unmindful of her home.”", "내가 리처드와 에이다를 흘끗 보며 말했는데, 둘은 눈으로 어서 말하라고 나에게 간청하고 있었다. \"어쩌면 그분이 자기 가정에는 조금 소홀하신 게 아닌가 하고요.\""]], [["“Floored!”", "\"한 방 먹었군!\""], ["cried Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["I was rather alarmed again.", "나는 또다시 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["“Well!", "\"좋아!"], ["I want to know your real thoughts, my dear.", "나는 네 진짜 생각을 알고 싶구나, 얘야."], ["I may have sent you there on purpose.”", "내가 일부러 너희를 그곳에 보냈을 수도 있으니.\""]], [["“We thought that, perhaps,”", "\"저희가 생각하기에는, 어쩌면\" 내가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["said I, hesitating, “it is right to begin with the obligations of home, sir; and that, perhaps, while those are overlooked and neglected, no other duties can possibly be substituted for them.”", "\"가정에 대한 의무부터 시작하는 것이 옳고요, 어르신. 그리고 어쩌면, 그 의무가 외면당하고 등한시되는 동안에는 다른 어떤 의무로도 그것을 대신할 수 없는 게 아닌가 합니다.\""]], [["“The little Jellybys,”", "\"그 어린 젤리비 아이들은\""], ["said Richard, coming to my relief, “are really—I can’t help expressing myself strongly, sir—in a devil of a state.”", "리처드가 나를 거들어 나서며 말했다. \"정말이지 — 강하게 말하지 않을 수가 없네요, 어르신 — 끔찍한 꼴이더군요.\""]], [["“She means well,”", "\"그 사람은 뜻은 좋아.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce hastily.", "잔다이스 씨가 황급히 말했다."], ["“The wind’s in the east.”", "\"바람이 동쪽에서 부는구나.\""]], [["“It was in the north, sir, as we came down,”", "\"저희가 내려올 때는 북쪽에서 불던데요, 어르신.\""], ["observed Richard.", "리처드가 짚었다."]], [["“My dear Rick,” said Mr.", "\"이보게 릭.\""], ["Jarndyce, poking the fire, “I’ll take an oath it’s either in the east or going to be.", "잔다이스 씨가 불을 쑤석거리며 말했다. \"맹세컨대 동쪽이거나 곧 동쪽이 될 거다."], ["I am always conscious of an uncomfortable sensation now and then when the wind is blowing in the east.”", "나는 바람이 동쪽에서 불면 가끔씩 영 거북한 느낌이 들곤 하거든.\""]], [["“Rheumatism, sir?”", "\"신경통이신가요, 어르신?\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했다."]], [["“I dare say it is, Rick.", "\"아마 그럴 거다, 릭."], ["I believe it is.", "그렇겠지."], ["And so the little Jell—I had my doubts about ’em—are in a—oh, Lord, yes, it’s easterly!”", "그러니까 그 어린 젤 — 나도 그 애들이 걱정스럽긴 했다만 — 그 애들이 — 아, 이런, 그래, 동쪽이로구나!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."]], [["He had taken two or three undecided turns up and down while uttering these broken sentences, retaining the poker in one hand and rubbing his hair with the other, with a good-natured vexation at once so whimsical and so lovable that I am sure we were more delighted with him than we could possibly have expressed in any words.", "그는 이 끊어진 문장들을 내뱉는 동안 어정쩡하게 두세 번 방 안을 오락가락 거닐었는데, 한 손에는 부지깽이를 쥐고 다른 손으로는 머리를 문지르면서, 그 짜증마저 더없이 사람 좋은 데다 어찌나 엉뚱하고 사랑스러웠던지, 나는 우리가 어떤 말로도 다 표현하지 못할 만큼 그에게 흠뻑 빠졌음을 확신한다."], ["He gave an arm to Ada and an arm to me, and bidding Richard bring a candle, was leading the way out when he suddenly turned us all back again.", "그는 한쪽 팔은 에이다에게, 다른 팔은 나에게 내밀고, 리처드더러 촛불을 가져오라 이르며 앞장서 나가다가, 별안간 우리 모두를 도로 돌려세웠다."]], [["“Those little Jellybys.", "\"그 어린 젤리비 아이들 말이다."], ["Couldn’t you—didn’t you—now, if it had rained sugar-plums, or three-cornered raspberry tarts, or anything of that sort!”", "너희가 — 혹시 — 그러니까, 사탕이라도 비처럼 쏟아졌거나, 세모꼴 산딸기 파이라도, 뭐 그런 것이라도 쏟아졌더라면!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, cousin—”", "\"아, 사촌 오라버니 —\""], ["Ada hastily began.", "에이다가 황급히 입을 열었다."]], [["“Good, my pretty pet.", "\"좋아, 내 예쁜 아가."], ["I like cousin.", "사촌이라니 마음에 드는구나."], ["Cousin John, perhaps, is better.”", "사촌 존, 이라면 더 좋을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“Then, cousin John—”", "\"그럼, 사촌 존 오라버니 —\""], ["Ada laughingly began again.", "에이다가 웃으며 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Ha, ha!", "\"하, 하!"], ["Very good indeed!”", "정말 좋구나!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce with great enjoyment.", "잔다이스 씨가 무척 흐뭇해하며 말했다."], ["“Sounds uncommonly natural.", "\"유난히 자연스럽게 들리는구나."], ["Yes, my dear?”", "그래, 얘야?\""]], [["“It did better than that.", "\"그것보다 더 좋은 게 쏟아졌어요."], ["It rained Esther.”", "에스더가 비처럼 쏟아졌으니까요.\""]], [["“Aye?”", "\"오?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."], ["“What did Esther do?”", "\"에스더가 뭘 했는데?\""]], [["“Why, cousin John,”", "\"그게요, 사촌 존 오라버니.\""], ["said Ada, clasping her hands upon his arm and shaking her head at me across him—for I wanted her to be quiet—“Esther was their friend directly.", "에이다가 두 손을 그의 팔에 감고는 그를 사이에 두고 나를 향해 고개를 가로저으며 말했다 — 나는 그 애가 잠자코 있기를 바랐으니까. \"에스더는 단박에 그 애들의 친구가 됐어요."], ["Esther nursed them, coaxed them to sleep, washed and dressed them, told them stories, kept them quiet, bought them keepsakes”—My dear girl!", "에스더는 그 애들을 돌보고, 어르고 달래 재우고, 씻기고 옷을 입히고, 이야기를 들려주고, 조용히 시키고, 기념될 선물도 사 주었어요.\" — 사랑스러운 아이!"], ["I had only gone out with Peepy after he was found and given him a little, tiny horse!—“and, cousin John, she softened poor Caroline, the eldest one, so much and was so thoughtful for me and so amiable!", "나는 피피가 발견된 뒤에 그 애를 데리고 잠깐 나가 자그마한 장난감 말 하나를 사 주었을 뿐인데! — \"게다가, 사촌 존 오라버니, 에스더는 가엾은 만이 캐럴라인을 어찌나 부드럽게 다독였는지 몰라요, 그리고 저를 어찌나 살뜰히 챙겨 주고 또 얼마나 상냥했는지!"], ["No, no, I won’t be contradicted, Esther dear!", "아니, 아니, 반박하면 안 돼, 에스더야!"], ["You know, you know, it’s true!”", "알잖아, 너도 알잖아, 다 사실이라는 걸!\""]], [["The warm-hearted darling leaned across her cousin John and kissed me, and then looking up in his face, boldly said, “At all events, cousin John, I WILL thank you for the companion you have given me.”", "마음씨 따뜻한 그 사랑스러운 아이가 사촌 존을 사이에 두고 몸을 기울여 나에게 입을 맞추고는, 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 당돌하게 말했다. \"어쨌든, 사촌 존 오라버니, 저는 저에게 짝지어 주신 동무에 대해 꼭 감사드리겠어요.\""], ["I felt as if she challenged him to run away.", "나는 그 애가 마치 그에게 어디 한번 달아나 보라고 도전이라도 하는 것 같았다."], ["But he didn’t.", "그러나 그는 달아나지 않았다."]], [["“Where did you say the wind was, Rick?”", "\"바람이 어디서 분다고 했지, 릭?\""], ["asked Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 물었다."]], [["“In the north as we came down, sir.”", "\"저희가 내려올 때는 북쪽이었습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["“You are right.", "\"네 말이 맞다."], ["There’s no east in it.", "동쪽 기운은 없구나."], ["A mistake of mine.", "내가 잘못 짚었어."], ["Come, girls, come and see your home!”", "자, 아가씨들, 와서 너희 집을 둘러보거라!\""]], [["It was one of those delightfully irregular houses where you go up and down steps out of one room into another, and where you come upon more rooms when you think you have seen all there are, and where there is a bountiful provision of little halls and passages, and where you find still older cottage-rooms in unexpected places with lattice windows and green growth pressing through them.", "그것은 한 방에서 다른 방으로 가려면 계단을 오르내려야 하고, 방을 다 보았다 싶으면 또 다른 방이 나타나고, 자그마한 홀과 복도가 넉넉하게 갖춰져 있고, 뜻밖의 자리에서 격자창이 달리고 그 사이로 푸른 덩굴이 비집고 들어온, 더 오래된 시골집 같은 방들을 마주치게 되는, 그런 기분 좋게 들쭉날쭉한 집들 가운데 하나였다."], ["Mine, which we entered first, was of this kind, with an up-and-down roof that had more corners in it than I ever counted afterwards and a chimney (there was a wood fire on the hearth) paved all around with pure white tiles, in every one of which a bright miniature of the fire was blazing.", "우리가 맨 먼저 들어간 내 방이 바로 그런 곳이었는데, 모서리가 너무 많아 나중에 다 세어 보지도 못한 들쭉날쭉한 지붕에, (벽난로에는 장작불이 타고 있었고) 그 둘레가 온통 새하얀 타일로 깔린 굴뚝이 있어, 그 타일 하나하나마다 불꽃의 작고 환한 모형이 활활 타오르고 있었다."], ["Out of this room, you went down two steps into a charming little sitting-room looking down upon a flower-garden, which room was henceforth to belong to Ada and me.", "이 방에서 두 계단을 내려가면 꽃밭이 내려다보이는 사랑스러운 자그마한 거실이 나왔는데, 이 방은 이제부터 에이다와 내 차지가 될 곳이었다."], ["Out of this you went up three steps into Ada’s bedroom, which had a fine broad window commanding a beautiful view (we saw a great expanse of darkness lying underneath the stars), to which there was a hollow window-seat, in which, with a spring-lock, three dear Adas might have been lost at once.", "여기서 세 계단을 올라가면 에이다의 침실이 나왔는데, 아름다운 경치가 한눈에 들어오는 멋지고 널찍한 창문이 있었고(우리는 별빛 아래 펼쳐진 드넓은 어둠을 보았다), 그 아래 움푹한 창가 의자가 딸려 있어, 용수철 자물쇠를 달면 사랑스러운 에이다 셋쯤은 한꺼번에 숨겨 둘 만했다."], ["Out of this room you passed into a little gallery, with which the other best rooms (only two) communicated, and so, by a little staircase of shallow steps with a number of corner stairs in it, considering its length, down into the hall.", "이 방에서 빠져나오면 자그마한 회랑으로 통했는데, 거기로 다른 손님방들(둘뿐이었다)이 이어졌고, 그렇게 길이에 비해 모퉁이 계단이 여럿 끼인, 얕은 단의 자그마한 층계를 따라 홀로 내려갈 수 있었다."], ["But if instead of going out at Ada’s door you came back into my room, and went out at the door by which you had entered it, and turned up a few crooked steps that branched off in an unexpected manner from the stairs, you lost yourself in passages, with mangles in them, and three-cornered tables, and a native Hindu chair, which was also a sofa, a box, and a bedstead, and looked in every form something between a bamboo skeleton and a great bird-cage, and had been brought from India nobody knew by whom or when.", "그러나 에이다의 문으로 나가는 대신 내 방으로 되돌아와, 들어왔던 문으로 다시 나가서, 계단에서 뜻밖의 모양으로 갈라져 나간 비뚜름한 계단 몇 개를 따라 오르면, 복도 속에서 길을 잃게 되는데, 그 복도에는 빨래 짜는 기계며, 세모꼴 탁자며, 소파이면서 궤짝이면서 침상이기도 한 인도 원주민의 의자가 놓여 있었으니, 그것은 어느 모로 보나 대나무 해골과 커다란 새장 사이의 무언가처럼 생겼고, 누가 언제 인도에서 가져왔는지는 아무도 몰랐다."], ["From these you came on Richard’s room, which was part library, part sitting-room, part bedroom, and seemed indeed a comfortable compound of many rooms.", "거기서 리처드의 방으로 이어졌는데, 그 방은 서재이기도 하고 거실이기도 하고 침실이기도 해서, 정말이지 여러 방을 한데 아늑하게 버무려 놓은 것 같았다."], ["Out of that you went straight, with a little interval of passage, to the plain room where Mr. Jarndyce slept, all the year round, with his window open, his bedstead without any furniture standing in the middle of the floor for more air, and his cold bath gaping for him in a smaller room adjoining.", "거기서 곧장, 복도를 조금 지나면, 잔다이스 씨가 잠을 자는 수수한 방이 나왔는데, 그는 일 년 내내 창문을 열어 둔 채, 공기를 더 쐬려고 아무 가구도 딸리지 않은 침대를 방 한가운데에 놓고 잤으며, 옆에 붙은 더 작은 방에서는 찬물 목욕통이 그를 기다리며 입을 벌리고 있었다."], ["Out of that you came into another passage, where there were back-stairs and where you could hear the horses being rubbed down outside the stable and being told to “Hold up” and “Get over,”", "거기서 나오면 또 다른 복도가 나왔는데, 거기에는 뒷계단이 있었고, 마구간 밖에서 말들의 몸을 문질러 주는 소리와, 울퉁불퉁한 돌바닥에서 말들이 자꾸 미끄러지자 \"버텨\", \"넘어가\"라고 이르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["as they slipped about very much on the uneven stones. Or you might, if you came out at another door (every room had at least two doors), go straight down to the hall again by half-a-dozen steps and a low archway, wondering how you got back there or had ever got out of it.", "아니면, 다른 문으로 나가면(방마다 문이 적어도 둘은 있었다), 계단 대여섯 개와 낮은 아치 문을 지나 곧장 홀로 다시 내려갈 수 있었는데, 어떻게 거기로 되돌아왔는지, 아니 애초에 어떻게 거기서 나왔는지 어리둥절해하면서 말이다."]], [["The furniture, old-fashioned rather than old, like the house, was as pleasantly irregular.", "가구는, 집과 마찬가지로, 오래되었다기보다는 고풍스러운 것이었고, 기분 좋게 들쭉날쭉했다."], ["Ada’s sleeping-room was all flowers—in chintz and paper, in velvet, in needlework, in the brocade of two stiff courtly chairs which stood, each attended by a little page of a stool for greater state, on either side of the fire-place.", "에이다의 침실은 온통 꽃이었다 — 친츠와 벽지에도, 벨벳에도, 자수에도, 벽난로 양옆에 더 격식을 차리려는 듯 자그마한 발받침 시동을 하나씩 거느린 채 놓인 뻣뻣하고 궁정풍인 두 의자의 브로케이드 천에도."], ["Our sitting-room was green and had framed and glazed upon the walls numbers of surprising and surprised birds, staring out of pictures at a real trout in a case, as brown and shining as if it had been served with gravy; at the death of Captain Cook; and at the whole process of preparing tea in China, as depicted by Chinese artists.", "우리 거실은 초록빛이었고, 벽에는 액자에 끼워 유리를 덧댄 놀랍고도 놀란 표정의 새들이 잔뜩 걸려, 그림 밖으로 응시하고 있었다 — 마치 그레이비라도 끼얹은 듯 갈색으로 반들거리는, 유리 상자 속의 진짜 송어를 향해, 쿡 선장의 죽음을 향해, 그리고 중국 화가들이 그린 대로 중국에서 차를 우려내는 전 과정을 향해."], ["In my room there were oval engravings of the months—ladies haymaking in short waists and large hats tied under the chin, for June; smooth-legged noblemen pointing with cocked-hats to village steeples, for October.", "내 방에는 열두 달을 그린 타원형 판화들이 있었는데 — 짧은 허리선 옷에 턱 밑에서 묶는 커다란 모자를 쓴 채 건초를 만드는 여인들은 유월이었고, 매끈한 다리에 삼각모를 쓰고 마을 첨탑을 가리키는 귀족들은 시월이었다."], ["Half-length portraits in crayons abounded all through the house, but were so dispersed that I found the brother of a youthful officer of mine in the china-closet and the grey old age of my pretty young bride, with a flower in her bodice, in the breakfast-room.", "크레용으로 그린 반신 초상화가 집 안 곳곳에 넘쳐났는데, 어찌나 흩어져 걸렸던지, 나는 내 젊은 장교 초상화의 형제를 도자기 벽장에서 발견했고, 가슴팍에 꽃 한 송이를 꽂은 어여쁜 젊은 신부가 늙어 백발이 된 모습을 아침 식사 방에서 발견했다."], ["As substitutes, I had four angels, of Queen Anne’s reign, taking a complacent gentleman to heaven, in festoons, with some difficulty; and a composition in needlework representing fruit, a kettle, and an alphabet.", "그 대신 내 방에는, 앤 여왕 시절의 천사 넷이 꽃줄 장식에 매달려 흡족한 표정의 신사 하나를 제법 애를 먹어 가며 천국으로 데려가는 그림이 있었고, 과일과 주전자와 알파벳을 수놓은 자수 작품도 하나 있었다."], ["All the movables, from the wardrobes to the chairs and tables, hangings, glasses, even to the pincushions and scent-bottles on the dressing-tables, displayed the same quaint variety.", "옷장에서부터 의자와 탁자, 휘장, 거울, 심지어 화장대 위의 바늘꽂이와 향수병에 이르기까지, 옮길 수 있는 모든 물건이 한결같이 이 별스러운 다채로움을 드러냈다."], ["They agreed in nothing but their perfect neatness, their display of the whitest linen, and their storing-up, wheresoever the existence of a drawer, small or large, rendered it possible, of quantities of rose-leaves and sweet lavender.", "그것들이 일치하는 점이라곤 더없이 말끔하다는 것, 더없이 새하얀 아마포를 펼쳐 보인다는 것, 그리고 크든 작든 서랍이 있는 곳이라면 어디든 장미 꽃잎과 향긋한 라벤더를 잔뜩 갈무리해 두었다는 것뿐이었다."], ["Such, with its illuminated windows, softened here and there by shadows of curtains, shining out upon the starlight night; with its light, and warmth, and comfort; with its hospitable jingle, at a distance, of preparations for dinner; with the face of its generous master brightening everything we saw; and just wind enough without to sound a low accompaniment to everything we heard, were our first impressions of Bleak House.", "별빛 어린 밤하늘을 향해 환하게 빛나는, 군데군데 커튼 그림자로 부드러워진 불 밝은 창들과, 그 빛과 온기와 안락함과, 멀리서 들려오는 저녁 식사 준비의 정겨운 달그락거림과, 우리가 보는 모든 것을 환하게 밝혀 주는 너그러운 주인의 얼굴과, 우리가 듣는 모든 것에 나직한 반주를 곁들일 만큼만 바깥에서 부는 바람 — 이것이 황폐한 집에 대한 우리의 첫인상이었다."]], [["“I am glad you like it,” said Mr.", "\"너희 마음에 든다니 기쁘구나.\""], ["Jarndyce when he had brought us round again to Ada’s sitting-room.", "잔다이스 씨가 우리를 다시 에이다의 거실로 데려왔을 때 말했다."], ["“It makes no pretensions, but it is a comfortable little place, I hope, and will be more so with such bright young looks in it.", "\"내세울 건 없어도, 아늑하고 자그마한 곳이라고 믿는다. 그리고 이렇게 밝고 젊은 얼굴들이 깃들면 더 그렇게 되겠지."], ["You have barely half an hour before dinner.", "저녁 식사까지는 삼십 분이 채 안 남았구나."], ["There’s no one here but the finest creature upon earth—a child.”", "여기에는 이 세상에서 가장 멋진 사람 하나밖에 없단다 — 어린아이지.\""]], [["“More children, Esther!”", "\"아이가 또 있나 봐, 에스더!\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t mean literally a child,”", "\"말 그대로 어린아이라는 건 아니다.\""], ["pursued Mr. Jarndyce; “not a child in years.", "잔다이스 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["He is grown up—he is at least as old as I am—but in simplicity, and freshness, and enthusiasm, and a fine guileless inaptitude for all worldly affairs, he is a perfect child.”", "\"나이로 따져 아이는 아니야. 그는 다 컸지 — 적어도 나만큼은 나이를 먹었어 — 그러나 천진함과 신선함과 열정에서는, 그리고 온갖 세상사에는 도무지 서투른 그 곱고 순박한 무능함에서는, 그는 완벽한 어린아이란다.\""]], [["We felt that he must be very interesting.", "우리는 그가 무척 흥미로운 사람임에 틀림없다고 느꼈다."]], [["“He knows Mrs. Jellyby,”", "\"그는 젤리비 부인을 알지.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."], ["“He is a musical man, an amateur, but might have been a professional.", "\"음악을 하는 사람인데, 아마추어지만 직업으로 삼았을 수도 있었어."], ["He is an artist too, an amateur, but might have been a professional.", "화가이기도 한데, 역시 아마추어지만 직업으로 삼았을 수도 있었지."], ["He is a man of attainments and of captivating manners.", "재주가 많고 사람을 끄는 데가 있어."], ["He has been unfortunate in his affairs, and unfortunate in his pursuits, and unfortunate in his family; but he don’t care—he’s a child!”", "사업에서도 불운했고, 자기가 좇는 일에서도 불운했고, 집안에서도 불운했지. 그래도 개의치 않아 — 그는 어린아이니까!\""]], [["“Did you imply that he has children of his own, sir?”", "\"그분에게 자기 자식이 있다는 말씀이신가요, 어르신?\""], ["inquired Richard.", "리처드가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, Rick!", "\"그렇다, 릭!"], ["Half-a-dozen.", "여섯이야. 더 되지!"], ["More! Nearer a dozen, I should think.", "내 생각엔 열둘에 가까울 거다."], ["But he has never looked after them.", "그런데 그는 한 번도 그 애들을 돌본 적이 없어."], ["How could he?", "어떻게 돌보겠느냐?"], ["He wanted somebody to look after HIM.", "자기를 돌봐 줄 사람이 필요한 사람인걸."], ["He is a child, you know!”", "어린아이라니까!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And have the children looked after themselves at all, sir?”", "\"그럼 그 아이들은 그나마 제 앞가림은 했나요, 어르신?\""], ["inquired Richard.", "리처드가 물었다."]], [["“Why, just as you may suppose,”", "\"글쎄, 너도 짐작이 가겠지만.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce, his countenance suddenly falling.", "잔다이스 씨가 별안간 안색이 어두워지며 말했다."], ["“It is said that the children of the very poor are not brought up, but dragged up.", "\"더없이 가난한 집 아이들은 길러지는 게 아니라 끌어올려지는 거라는 말이 있지."], ["Harold Skimpole’s children have tumbled up somehow or other.", "해럴드 스킴폴의 아이들은 어찌어찌 굴러서 컸어."], ["The wind’s getting round again, I am afraid.", "바람이 또 동쪽으로 도는 모양이다."], ["I feel it rather!”", "좀 느껴지는걸!\""]], [["Richard observed that the situation was exposed on a sharp night.", "리처드는 이렇게 추운 밤에는 자리가 바람에 휑하니 노출되어 있다고 말했다."]], [["“It IS exposed,”", "\"노출되어 있고말고.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."], ["“No doubt that’s the cause.", "\"분명 그게 원인이겠지."], ["Bleak House has an exposed sound.", "황폐한 집이라는 이름부터가 휑한 느낌이 들지 않느냐."], ["But you are coming my way.", "그건 그렇고, 너도 내가 가는 쪽으로 가는구나."], ["Come along!”", "자, 가자!\""]], [["Our luggage having arrived and being all at hand, I was dressed in a few minutes and engaged in putting my worldly goods away when a maid (not the one in attendance upon Ada, but another, whom I had not seen) brought a basket into my room with two bunches of keys in it, all labelled.", "우리 짐이 도착해 모두 손 닿는 곳에 있었으므로, 나는 몇 분 만에 옷을 갈아입고 내 세간을 정리하고 있었는데, 그때 하녀 하나가(에이다를 시중드는 그 하녀가 아니라, 내가 본 적 없는 다른 하녀였다) 라벨이 죄 붙은 열쇠 꾸러미 두 개가 담긴 바구니를 들고 내 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["“For you, miss, if you please,” said she.", "\"아가씨 것이에요, 괜찮으시다면요.\""], ["“For me?” said I.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“The housekeeping keys, miss.”", "\"제 것이라고요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["I showed my surprise, for she added with some little surprise on her own part, “I was told to bring them as soon as you was alone, miss.", "\"살림 열쇠예요, 아가씨.\" 내가 놀라움을 드러내자, 그녀도 약간 놀란 기색으로 덧붙였다."], ["Miss Summerson, if I don’t deceive myself?”", "\"아가씨 혼자 계실 때 곧바로 갖다드리라는 분부였어요. 서머슨 아가씨, 맞으시죠?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“That is my name.”", "\"그게 제 이름이에요.\""]], [["“The large bunch is the housekeeping, and the little bunch is the cellars, miss.", "\"큰 꾸러미는 살림 열쇠고, 작은 꾸러미는 지하 저장실 열쇠예요, 아가씨."], ["Any time you was pleased to appoint to-morrow morning, I was to show you the presses and things they belong to.”", "내일 아침 정하시는 시각이면 언제든, 그 열쇠에 맞는 찬장이며 물건들을 보여 드리라고 하셨어요.\""]], [["I said I would be ready at half-past six, and after she was gone, stood looking at the basket, quite lost in the magnitude of my trust.", "나는 여섯 시 반에 준비하고 있겠다고 말했고, 그녀가 나간 뒤, 나에게 맡겨진 신임의 무게에 그만 넋이 나간 채 바구니를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Ada found me thus and had such a delightful confidence in me when I showed her the keys and told her about them that it would have been insensibility and ingratitude not to feel encouraged.", "에이다가 이런 나를 발견했는데, 내가 그 애에게 열쇠를 보여 주고 그 사연을 들려주자, 그 애가 어찌나 흐뭇하게 나를 믿어 주었던지, 거기서 힘을 얻지 않는다면 그건 무정하고 배은망덕한 짓이었으리라."], ["I knew, to be sure, that it was the dear girl’s kindness, but I liked to be so pleasantly cheated.", "물론 그게 사랑스러운 그 애의 다정함에서 나온 것임을 알았지만, 나는 그렇게 기분 좋게 속아 넘어가는 것이 좋았다."]], [["When we went downstairs, we were presented to Mr. Skimpole, who was standing before the fire telling Richard how fond he used to be, in his school-time, of football.", "우리가 아래층으로 내려가니, 스킴폴 씨에게 소개되었는데, 그는 불 앞에 서서 리처드에게 자기가 학창 시절에 축구를 얼마나 좋아했는지 이야기하고 있었다."], ["He was a little bright creature with a rather large head, but a delicate face and a sweet voice, and there was a perfect charm in him.", "그는 머리가 제법 크지만 얼굴이 섬세하고 목소리가 감미로운, 자그맣고 환한 사람이었고, 그에게는 더없는 매력이 있었다."], ["All he said was so free from effort and spontaneous and was said with such a captivating gaiety that it was fascinating to hear him talk.", "그가 하는 말은 죄다 애쓴 데가 없이 절로 흘러나왔고, 어찌나 사람을 끄는 쾌활함으로 말하던지, 그가 이야기하는 것을 듣고 있노라면 빠져들지 않을 수가 없었다."], ["Being of a more slender figure than Mr. Jarndyce and having a richer complexion, with browner hair, he looked younger.", "잔다이스 씨보다 호리호리한 체구에 안색이 더 불그레하고 머리카락도 더 갈색이어서, 그는 더 젊어 보였다."], ["Indeed, he had more the appearance in all respects of a damaged young man than a well-preserved elderly one.", "사실 그는 모든 면에서 잘 보존된 노년의 신사라기보다는 망가진 청년에 가까운 모습이었다."], ["There was an easy negligence in his manner and even in his dress (his hair carelessly disposed, and his neck-kerchief loose and flowing, as I have seen artists paint their own portraits) which I could not separate from the idea of a romantic youth who had undergone some unique process of depreciation.", "그의 태도에는, 그리고 옷차림에까지도(머리카락은 아무렇게나 헝클어져 있었고, 목수건은 화가들이 자기 초상화를 그릴 때 즐겨 그리는 모습 그대로 느슨하게 흘러내려 있었다), 무언가 거리낌 없는 무심함이 배어 있었는데, 나는 그것을 어떤 독특한 퇴락의 과정을 거친 낭만적인 청년이라는 인상과 도무지 떼어 놓을 수 없었다."], ["It struck me as being not at all like the manner or appearance of a man who had advanced in life by the usual road of years, cares, and experiences.", "그것은 세월과 근심과 경험이라는 흔한 길을 따라 인생을 거쳐 온 사람의 태도나 모습과는 전혀 같지 않다는 느낌을 주었다."]], [["I gathered from the conversation that Mr. Skimpole had been educated for the medical profession and had once lived, in his professional capacity, in the household of a German prince.", "대화에서 나는 스킴폴 씨가 의술을 배웠고, 한때 그 직분으로 어느 독일 군주의 집안에서 살았다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He told us, however, that as he had always been a mere child in point of weights and measures and had never known anything about them (except that they disgusted him), he had never been able to prescribe with the requisite accuracy of detail.", "그러나 그는, 자기가 무게와 분량을 따지는 일에는 늘 한낱 어린아이였고 그런 것에 대해서는(자기를 진저리 나게 했다는 것 말고는) 아무것도 모른 채 살았던 터라, 처방에 꼭 필요한 세밀한 정확함을 도무지 갖출 수가 없었다고 우리에게 말했다."], ["In fact, he said, he had no head for detail.", "사실 자기는 세부적인 것에는 도무지 머리가 돌아가지 않는다고 그는 말했다."], ["And he told us, with great humour, that when he was wanted to bleed the prince or physic any of his people, he was generally found lying on his back in bed, reading the newspapers or making fancy-sketches in pencil, and couldn’t come.", "그러고는 무척 익살스럽게, 군주의 피를 뽑거나 그 집안사람 누군가에게 약을 처방해야 할 때면, 자기는 으레 침대에 등을 대고 누워 신문을 읽거나 연필로 마음 가는 대로 그림이나 끼적이고 있어서 올 수가 없었다고 들려주었다."], ["The prince, at last, objecting to this, “in which,”", "마침내 군주가 이를 못마땅해하자 — \"그 점에서는,\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole, in the frankest manner, “he was perfectly right,”", "스킴폴 씨가 더없이 솔직한 태도로 말했다."], ["the engagement terminated, and Mr.", "\"그분이 전적으로 옳았지요.\""], ["Skimpole having (as he added with delightful gaiety) “nothing to live upon but love, fell in love, and married, and surrounded himself with rosy cheeks.”", "— 그 일자리가 끝나 버렸고, 스킴폴 씨는 (그가 흐뭇한 쾌활함으로 덧붙인 대로) \"사랑 말고는 먹고살 게 없었던지라, 사랑에 빠졌고, 결혼했고, 발그레한 볼들에 둘러싸이게 되었\"다."], ["His good friend Jarndyce and some other of his good friends then helped him, in quicker or slower succession, to several openings in life, but to no purpose, for he must confess to two of the oldest infirmities in the world: one was that he had no idea of time, the other that he had no idea of money.", "그러자 그의 좋은 벗 잔다이스와 그 밖의 좋은 벗 몇몇이 그에게, 빠르거나 느린 순서로, 인생의 여러 활로를 마련해 주었으나, 다 헛수고였다. 그도 그럴 것이 그는 세상에서 가장 오래된 두 가지 약점이 있음을 고백할 수밖에 없었으니, 하나는 시간 개념이 없다는 것이고, 다른 하나는 돈 개념이 없다는 것이었다."], ["In consequence of which he never kept an appointment, never could transact any business, and never knew the value of anything!", "그 탓에 그는 약속을 지킨 적이 없고, 어떤 사무도 처리할 수 없었으며, 무엇의 값어치도 알지 못했다!"], ["Well!", "어쨌든!"], ["So he had got on in life, and here he was!", "그렇게 그는 인생을 헤쳐 왔고, 이렇게 여기 있는 것이다!"], ["He was very fond of reading the papers, very fond of making fancy-sketches with a pencil, very fond of nature, very fond of art.", "그는 신문 읽기를 무척 좋아했고, 연필로 마음 가는 대로 그림 그리기를 무척 좋아했고, 자연을 무척 좋아했고, 예술을 무척 좋아했다. 그가 세상에 바라는 것이라곤 자기를 그저 살게 내버려 두라는 것뿐이었다."], ["All he asked of society was to let him live.", "그것은 대단한 게 아니었다."], ["THAT wasn’t much. His wants were few.", "그의 바람은 몇 가지 안 되었다."], ["Give him the papers, conversation, music, mutton, coffee, landscape, fruit in the season, a few sheets of Bristol-board, and a little claret, and he asked no more.", "신문과 대화와 음악과 양고기와 커피와 풍경과 철 따라 나는 과일과 브리스틀 판지 몇 장과 약간의 클라레만 주면, 그는 더 바라지 않았다. 그는 세상에서 한낱 어린아이일 뿐이었지만, 달을 따 달라고 떼쓰지는 않았다."], ["He was a mere child in the world, but he didn’t cry for the moon.", "그는 세상을 향해 이렇게 말했다. \"각자 평화로이 제 갈 길을 가시오!"], ["He said to the world, “Go your several ways in peace!", "붉은 옷을 입든, 푸른 옷을 입든, 성직자의 흰 소매를 달든 마음대로 하시오."], ["Wear red coats, blue coats, lawn sleeves; put pens behind your ears, wear aprons; go after glory, holiness, commerce, trade, any object you prefer; only—let Harold Skimpole live!”", "귀 뒤에 펜을 꽂든, 앞치마를 두르든, 영광이든 거룩함이든 상업이든 장사든 무엇이든 마음에 드는 것을 좇으시오. 다만 — 해럴드 스킴폴만은 살게 내버려 두시오!\""]], [["All this and a great deal more he told us, not only with the utmost brilliancy and enjoyment, but with a certain vivacious candour—speaking of himself as if he were not at all his own affair, as if Skimpole were a third person, as if he knew that Skimpole had his singularities but still had his claims too, which were the general business of the community and must not be slighted.", "이 모든 것을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을, 그는 더없이 빛나고 흐뭇한 태도로 들려주었을 뿐 아니라, 어딘가 생기 넘치는 솔직함으로 우리에게 이야기했다 — 마치 자기가 자기 자신과는 전혀 상관없는 사람인 양, 스킴폴이 제삼자인 양, 스킴폴에게는 별난 구석이 있긴 하지만 그래도 챙겨야 할 몫이 있어서 그것이 공동체 전체의 일이니 소홀히 해서는 안 된다는 것을 잘 안다는 듯이."], ["He was quite enchanting.", "그는 정말이지 사람을 홀렸다."], ["If I felt at all confused at that early time in endeavouring to reconcile anything he said with anything I had thought about the duties and accountabilities of life (which I am far from sure of), I was confused by not exactly understanding why he was free of them.", "그 이른 시점에 그가 한 어떤 말을 인생의 의무와 책임에 대해 내가 품어 온 어떤 생각과 화해시켜 보려 애쓰면서 내가 조금이라도 혼란스러웠다면(그랬는지는 전혀 확신할 수 없지만), 그것은 그가 어째서 그 의무에서 자유로운지를 정확히 이해하지 못한 데서 온 혼란이었다."], ["That he WAS free of them, I scarcely doubted; he was so very clear about it himself.", "그가 그 의무에서 자유롭다는 것은 거의 의심하지 않았으니, 그 자신이 그 점에 대해서는 너무도 분명했기 때문이다."]], [["“I covet nothing,” said Mr.", "\"나는 아무것도 탐내지 않아요.\""], ["Skimpole in the same light way.", "스킴폴 씨가 여전히 가벼운 투로 말했다."], ["“Possession is nothing to me.", "\"소유라는 건 나에게 아무것도 아니에요."], ["Here is my friend Jarndyce’s excellent house.", "여기 내 벗 잔다이스의 훌륭한 집이 있지요."], ["I feel obliged to him for possessing it.", "나는 그가 이 집을 소유한 것에 대해 그에게 고마움을 느낍니다."], ["I can sketch it and alter it. I can set it to music.", "나는 이 집을 스케치할 수도 있고, 고쳐 그릴 수도 있어요."], ["When I am here, I have sufficient possession of it and have neither trouble, cost, nor responsibility.", "음악으로 옮길 수도 있고요. 내가 여기 있는 동안에는 나도 이 집을 충분히 소유하고 있는 셈인 데다, 골칫거리도, 비용도, 책임도 없지요."], ["My steward’s name, in short, is Jarndyce, and he can’t cheat me.", "한마디로 내 집사의 이름이 잔다이스인데, 그가 나를 속일 리도 없고요."], ["We have been mentioning Mrs. Jellyby.", "우리가 젤리비 부인 이야기를 하고 있었지요."], ["There is a bright-eyed woman, of a strong will and immense power of business detail, who throws herself into objects with surprising ardour!", "눈이 초롱초롱하고, 의지가 강하고, 사무를 세세히 처리하는 능력이 어마어마한 여인, 놀라운 열의로 어떤 일에 몸을 던지는 여인이지요!"], ["I don’t regret that I have not a strong will and an immense power of business detail to throw myself into objects with surprising ardour.", "나에게 강한 의지와 사무를 세세히 처리하는 어마어마한 능력이 없어서 놀라운 열의로 어떤 일에 몸을 던지지 못한다고 해서 나는 아쉬워하지 않아요."], ["I can admire her without envy.", "나는 시샘 없이 그녀를 흠모할 수 있어요."], ["I can sympathize with the objects.", "그녀가 좇는 일에 공감할 수도 있고요."], ["I can dream of them.", "그것을 꿈꿀 수도 있어요."], ["I can lie down on the grass—in fine weather—and float along an African river, embracing all the natives I meet, as sensible of the deep silence and sketching the dense overhanging tropical growth as accurately as if I were there.", "나는 풀밭에 누워 — 날씨 좋은 날에 — 아프리카의 강을 따라 떠내려가며, 마주치는 원주민을 죄다 끌어안고, 그 깊은 정적을 또렷이 느끼고, 빽빽이 우거진 열대의 초목을 마치 그곳에 있는 것처럼 정확하게 스케치할 수 있어요."], ["I don’t know that it’s of any direct use my doing so, but it’s all I can do, and I do it thoroughly.", "내가 그렇게 한다고 해서 그게 직접적으로 무슨 쓸모가 있는지는 모르겠지만, 그게 내가 할 수 있는 전부고, 나는 그걸 철저히 해내지요."], ["Then, for heaven’s sake, having Harold Skimpole, a confiding child, petitioning you, the world, an agglomeration of practical people of business habits, to let him live and admire the human family, do it somehow or other, like good souls, and suffer him to ride his rocking-horse!”", "그러니 제발, 해럴드 스킴폴이라는, 사람을 믿어 의심치 않는 어린아이가, 당신들에게, 실무에 밝은 사람들이 모인 이 세상에, 자기를 살게 해 주고 인간 가족을 흠모하게 해 달라고 청원하니, 어떻게든, 마음씨 고운 분들답게, 그렇게 해 주시고, 그가 흔들목마를 타도록 내버려 두시오!\""]], [["It was plain enough that Mr. Jarndyce had not been neglectful of the adjuration.", "잔다이스 씨가 그 간청을 소홀히 하지 않았다는 것은 충분히 분명했다."], ["Mr. Skimpole’s general position there would have rendered it so without the addition of what he presently said.", "그가 이내 한 말을 보태지 않더라도, 거기서 스킴폴 씨가 차지한 전반적인 처지만으로도 그 점이 명백했다."]], [["“It’s only you, the generous creatures, whom I envy,” said Mr.", "\"내가 부러워하는 건 오직 당신들, 너그러운 분들뿐이에요.\""], ["Skimpole, addressing us, his new friends, in an impersonal manner.", "스킴폴 씨가 자기의 새 벗인 우리를 향해 마치 남 이야기하듯 말했다."], ["“I envy you your power of doing what you do.", "\"나는 당신들이 하는 그 일을 해내는 그 능력이 부럽소."], ["It is what I should revel in myself.", "그건 나 자신이 마음껏 누리고 싶은 거니까요."], ["I don’t feel any vulgar gratitude to you.", "나는 당신들에게 속된 고마움 따위는 조금도 느끼지 않아요."], ["I almost feel as if YOU ought to be grateful to ME for giving you the opportunity of enjoying the luxury of generosity. I know you like it.", "오히려 당신들이 나에게 고마워해야 할 것 같은 기분마저 든다오, 너그러움이라는 호사를 누릴 기회를 내가 당신들에게 주었으니 말이오."], ["For anything I can tell, I may have come into the world expressly for the purpose of increasing your stock of happiness.", "당신들이 그걸 좋아한다는 걸 나는 알아요. 어쩌면 나는 당신들의 행복의 곳간을 불려 주려는 바로 그 목적을 위해 이 세상에 왔는지도 모르지요."], ["I may have been born to be a benefactor to you by sometimes giving you an opportunity of assisting me in my little perplexities.", "어쩌면 나는, 내 자그마한 곤경에서 나를 거들 기회를 이따금 당신들에게 줌으로써 당신들의 은인이 되도록 태어났는지도 모르오."], ["Why should I regret my incapacity for details and worldly affairs when it leads to such pleasant consequences?", "그것이 이토록 즐거운 결과로 이어지는데, 내가 세부적인 일과 세상사에 무능하다고 해서 어찌 아쉬워하겠소?"], ["I don’t regret it therefore.”", "그러니 나는 그것을 아쉬워하지 않는다오.\""]], [["Of all his playful speeches (playful, yet always fully meaning what they expressed) none seemed to be more to the taste of Mr. Jarndyce than this.", "그의 모든 장난스러운 말 가운데(장난스럽지만, 그 표현하는 바를 늘 온전히 진심으로 담고 있었다) 이 말만큼 잔다이스 씨의 취향에 들어맞는 것은 없어 보였다."], ["I had often new temptations, afterwards, to wonder whether it was really singular, or only singular to me, that he, who was probably the most grateful of mankind upon the least occasion, should so desire to escape the gratitude of others.", "나는 나중에, 아주 사소한 일에도 인류 가운데 누구보다 고마워할 줄 알 그가, 어째서 남들의 고마움은 그토록 피하려 하는 것인지가 정말로 별난 일인지, 아니면 나에게만 별난 일로 보이는 것인지, 곱씹어 보고 싶은 유혹을 새삼 자주 느꼈다."]], [["We were all enchanted.", "우리는 모두 홀딱 빠졌다."], ["I felt it a merited tribute to the engaging qualities of Ada and Richard that Mr. Skimpole, seeing them for the first time, should be so unreserved and should lay himself out to be so exquisitely agreeable.", "스킴폴 씨가 에이다와 리처드를 처음 보면서도 그토록 격의 없이, 더없이 살갑게 굴려고 애쓰는 것은, 두 사람의 사람을 끄는 됨됨이에 마땅히 바칠 만한 찬사라고 나는 느꼈다."], ["They (and especially Richard) were naturally pleased, for similar reasons, and considered it no common privilege to be so freely confided in by such an attractive man.", "두 사람은(특히 리처드는) 같은 이유로 자연히 흐뭇해했고, 그토록 매력 있는 사람에게서 그토록 격의 없는 신뢰를 받는 것을 보통이 아닌 특권으로 여겼다."], ["The more we listened, the more gaily Mr. Skimpole talked.", "우리가 귀를 기울일수록 스킴폴 씨는 더욱 흥겹게 이야기했다."], ["And what with his fine hilarious manner and his engaging candour and his genial way of lightly tossing his own weaknesses about, as if he had said, “I am a child, you know! You are designing people compared with me”", "그리고 그 멋들어진 들뜬 태도와, 사람을 끄는 솔직함과, 자기 약점을 가볍게 툭툭 던지는 정겨운 방식이 어우러져 — 마치 \"나는 어린아이라오!"], ["(he really made me consider myself in that light) “but I am gay and innocent; forget your worldly arts and play with me!” the effect was absolutely dazzling.", "나에 비하면 당신들은 꿍꿍이가 있는 사람들이지요\"(그는 정말로 나로 하여금 나 자신을 그런 사람으로 여기게 만들었다) \"하지만 나는 명랑하고 순진하니, 당신들의 세속적인 잔재주는 잊어버리고 나와 함께 놀아요!\"라고 말하는 듯이 — 그 효과는 그야말로 눈부셨다."]], [["He was so full of feeling too and had such a delicate sentiment for what was beautiful or tender that he could have won a heart by that alone.", "게다가 그는 감정이 너무도 풍부했고, 아름답거나 다정한 것을 향한 섬세한 감수성이 어찌나 깊었던지, 그것 하나만으로도 사람의 마음을 사로잡을 만했다."], ["In the evening, when I was preparing to make tea and Ada was touching the piano in the adjoining room and softly humming a tune to her cousin Richard, which they had happened to mention, he came and sat down on the sofa near me and so spoke of Ada that I almost loved him.", "그날 저녁, 내가 차를 우릴 채비를 하고 있고 에이다가 옆방에서 피아노를 짚으며 사촌 리처드에게 — 두 사람이 우연히 이야기에 올린 — 어떤 곡조를 나직이 흥얼거리고 있을 때, 그는 다가와 내 곁 소파에 앉더니 에이다 이야기를 어찌나 하던지, 나는 그를 사랑할 뻔했다."]], [["“She is like the morning,” he said.", "\"그 아가씨는 아침 같아요.\""], ["“With that golden hair, those blue eyes, and that fresh bloom on her cheek, she is like the summer morning.", "그가 말했다. \"그 금빛 머리카락에, 그 파란 눈에, 두 볼의 그 싱그러운 홍조까지, 그녀는 여름날 아침 같지요."], ["The birds here will mistake her for it.", "여기 새들이 그녀를 아침으로 착각할 거예요."], ["We will not call such a lovely young creature as that, who is a joy to all mankind, an orphan.", "온 인류에게 기쁨인 저토록 사랑스러운 젊은 사람을, 우리는 고아라고 부르지 맙시다."], ["She is the child of the universe.”", "그녀는 우주의 아이예요.\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce, I found, was standing near us with his hands behind him and an attentive smile upon his face.", "문득 보니 잔다이스 씨가 두 손을 등 뒤로 모으고 얼굴에는 귀 기울이는 미소를 띤 채 우리 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["“The universe,”", "\"우주란,\""], ["he observed, “makes rather an indifferent parent, I am afraid.”", "그가 말했다. \"유감스럽지만 어지간히 무심한 부모일 텐데.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I don’t know!”", "그건 모르지요!\""], ["cried Mr. Skimpole buoyantly.", "스킴폴 씨가 들떠서 외쳤다."]], [["“I think I do know,”", "\"내 생각엔 내가 알 것 같은데.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"글쎄!\""], ["cried Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“You know the world (which in your sense is the universe), and I know nothing of it, so you shall have your way.", "\"당신은 세상을 알지요(당신이 말하는 의미에서는 그게 우주고요). 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모르니, 당신 뜻대로 하시구려."], ["But if I had mine,”", "그러나 내 뜻대로 한다면,\""], ["glancing at the cousins, “there should be no brambles of sordid realities in such a path as that.", "두 사촌을 흘끗 보며 그가 말했다. \"저런 길에는 속된 현실의 가시덤불 따위는 없어야 해요."], ["It should be strewn with roses; it should lie through bowers, where there was no spring, autumn, nor winter, but perpetual summer.", "장미가 흩뿌려져 있어야 하고요. 봄도, 가을도, 겨울도 없이 영원한 여름만 있는 나무 그늘 사이로 그 길이 뻗어 있어야 하지요."], ["Age or change should never wither it.", "세월도 변화도 그 길을 결코 시들게 해선 안 돼요."], ["The base word money should never be breathed near it!”", "돈이라는 천박한 말은 그 근처에서 결코 입에 올려서도 안 되고요!\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce patted him on the head with a smile, as if he had been really a child, and passing a step or two on, and stopping a moment, glanced at the young cousins.", "잔다이스 씨는 그가 정말 어린아이라도 되는 양 미소를 지으며 그의 머리를 토닥이고는, 한두 걸음 더 나아가 잠시 멈춰 서서 젊은 두 사촌을 바라보았다."], ["His look was thoughtful, but had a benignant expression in it which I often (how often!) saw again, which has long been engraven on my heart.", "그 눈길은 생각에 잠겨 있었으나, 거기에는 내가 훗날 자주(얼마나 자주였던가!) 다시 보게 될, 오래도록 내 가슴에 새겨진 인자한 표정이 어려 있었다."], ["The room in which they were, communicating with that in which he stood, was only lighted by the fire.", "두 사람이 있는 방은 그가 서 있는 방과 통해 있었는데, 오직 난롯불로만 밝혀져 있었다."], ["Ada sat at the piano; Richard stood beside her, bending down.", "에이다는 피아노 앞에 앉아 있고, 리처드는 그 곁에 서서 몸을 기울이고 있었다."], ["Upon the wall, their shadows blended together, surrounded by strange forms, not without a ghostly motion caught from the unsteady fire, though reflecting from motionless objects.", "벽에는 두 사람의 그림자가 한데 어우러져, 기이한 형상들에 둘러싸인 채, 움직이지 않는 물체에서 비친 것이건만 일렁이는 불길에서 옮아 온 어딘가 유령 같은 움직임마저 깃들어 있었다."], ["Ada touched the notes so softly and sang so low that the wind, sighing away to the distant hills, was as audible as the music.", "에이다가 어찌나 부드럽게 건반을 짚고 어찌나 나직이 노래하던지, 먼 언덕으로 한숨처럼 잦아드는 바람 소리가 그 음악만큼이나 또렷이 들렸다."], ["The mystery of the future and the little clue afforded to it by the voice of the present seemed expressed in the whole picture.", "미래의 신비와, 현재의 목소리가 그것에 던져 주는 작은 실마리가, 그 그림 전체에 표현되어 있는 듯했다."]], [["But it is not to recall this fancy, well as I remember it, that I recall the scene.", "그러나 이 장면을 내가 떠올리는 것은, 이 환상을 — 똑똑히 기억하기는 하지만 — 되살리기 위해서가 아니다."], ["First, I was not quite unconscious of the contrast in respect of meaning and intention between the silent look directed that way and the flow of words that had preceded it.", "첫째, 나는 그쪽을 향한 그 말없는 눈길과 그에 앞선 도도한 말의 흐름 사이에 의미와 의도의 면에서 어떤 대조가 있음을 어렴풋이나마 의식하고 있었다."], ["Secondly, though Mr. Jarndyce’s glance as he withdrew it rested for but a moment on me, I felt as if in that moment he confided to me—and knew that he confided to me and that I received the confidence—his hope that Ada and Richard might one day enter on a dearer relationship.", "둘째, 잔다이스 씨가 눈길을 거둘 때 그것이 내게 머문 것은 한순간뿐이었으나, 나는 그 순간 그가 나에게 — 에이다와 리처드가 언젠가 더 애틋한 사이로 맺어지기를 바라는 그 소망을 — 털어놓았고, 자기가 나에게 그것을 털어놓는다는 것을, 그리고 내가 그 속내를 받아들인다는 것을 알고 있다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["Mr. Skimpole could play on the piano and the violoncello, and he was a composer—had composed half an opera once, but got tired of it—and played what he composed with taste.", "스킴폴 씨는 피아노와 첼로를 연주할 줄 알았고, 작곡가이기도 해서 — 한번은 오페라를 반쯤 작곡했다가 싫증이 나 버렸다는데 — 자기가 작곡한 것을 운치 있게 연주했다."], ["After tea we had quite a little concert, in which Richard—who was enthralled by Ada’s singing and told me that she seemed to know all the songs that ever were written—and Mr.", "차를 마신 뒤 우리는 자그마한 음악회를 열었는데, 거기서 리처드는 — 에이다의 노래에 홀딱 빠져, 그 애가 세상에 나온 노래란 노래는 죄다 아는 것 같다고 나에게 말했다 — 잔다이스 씨와 나와 함께 청중이 되었다."], ["Jarndyce, and I were the audience. After a little while I missed first Mr.", "얼마쯤 지나 나는 먼저 스킴폴 씨가, 이어 리처드가 보이지 않는 것을 알아챘고, 리처드가 어떻게 이렇게 오래 자리를 비워 이 많은 것을 놓칠 수 있나 생각하고 있는데, 나에게 열쇠를 건네주었던 그 하녀가 문 안으로 얼굴을 들이밀며 말했다."], ["Skimpole and afterwards Richard, and while I was thinking how could Richard stay away so long and lose so much, the maid who had given me the keys looked in at the door, saying, “If you please, miss, could you spare a minute?”", "\"괜찮으시면, 아가씨, 잠깐 시간 좀 내주실 수 있으세요?\""]], [["When I was shut out with her in the hall, she said, holding up her hands, “Oh, if you please, miss, Mr.", "내가 그녀와 함께 홀로 나와 문이 닫히자, 그녀가 두 손을 치켜들며 말했다."], ["Carstone says would you come upstairs to Mr. Skimpole’s room.", "\"아, 죄송하지만요, 아가씨, 카스톤 씨가요, 스킴폴 씨 방으로 위층에 올라와 주시겠냐고요."], ["He has been took, miss!”", "그분이 잡혔어요, 아가씨!\""]], [["“Took?”", "\"잡혔다고요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Took, miss.", "\"잡혔어요, 아가씨."], ["Sudden,”", "갑자기요.\""], ["said the maid.", "하녀가 말했다."]], [["I was apprehensive that his illness might be of a dangerous kind, but of course I begged her to be quiet and not disturb any one and collected myself, as I followed her quickly upstairs, sufficiently to consider what were the best remedies to be applied if it should prove to be a fit. She threw open a door and I went into a chamber, where, to my unspeakable surprise, instead of finding Mr.", "나는 그의 병이 위중한 종류일까 봐 겁이 났으나, 물론 그녀에게 조용히 하고 누구도 놀라게 하지 말라고 당부하고는, 그녀를 따라 서둘러 위층으로 올라가면서, 만약 발작이라면 무슨 처치가 가장 좋을지 헤아릴 만큼은 마음을 가다듬었다."], ["Skimpole stretched upon the bed or prostrate on the floor, I found him standing before the fire smiling at Richard, while Richard, with a face of great embarrassment, looked at a person on the sofa, in a white great-coat, with smooth hair upon his head and not much of it, which he was wiping smoother and making less of with a pocket-handkerchief.", "그녀가 문을 활짝 열어젖혀 나는 어느 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기서 — 말로 다 할 수 없이 놀랍게도 — 침대에 뻗어 있거나 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 스킴폴 씨를 발견하는 대신, 나는 그가 불 앞에 서서 리처드를 향해 미소 짓고 있는 것을 발견했다. 한편 리처드는 몹시 난처한 얼굴로, 흰 외투를 걸치고 머리에 머리카락이 매끈하게, 그것도 얼마 없이 붙은 한 사람을 소파에서 바라보고 있었는데, 그 사람은 손수건으로 그 머리카락을 더 매끈하게 쓸어 넘기며 더 적어 보이게 만들고 있었다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["said Richard hurriedly, “I am glad you are come.", "리처드가 다급하게 말했다. \"와 주셔서 다행이에요."], ["You will be able to advise us. Our friend Mr.", "우리에게 조언을 해 주실 수 있겠군요."], ["Skimpole—don’t be alarmed!—is arrested for debt.”", "우리 벗 스킴폴 씨가 — 놀라지 마세요! — 빚 때문에 체포되었어요.\""]], [["“And really, my dear Miss Summerson,” said Mr.", "\"그리고 정말이지, 서머슨 양.\""], ["Skimpole with his agreeable candour, “I never was in a situation in which that excellent sense and quiet habit of method and usefulness, which anybody must observe in you who has the happiness of being a quarter of an hour in your society, was more needed.”", "스킴폴 씨가 그 사람 좋은 솔직함으로 말했다. \"나는 십오 분만 당신 곁에 있어 본 사람이라면 누구나 알아챌, 그 빼어난 분별력과 차분하고 빈틈없으며 쓸모 있는 습성이, 지금처럼 더 절실히 필요했던 적이 없는 처지에 놓인 적이 없다오.\""]], [["The person on the sofa, who appeared to have a cold in his head, gave such a very loud snort that he startled me.", "소파에 앉은 그 사람은 코감기가 든 듯, 어찌나 요란하게 콧김을 내뿜던지 나는 화들짝 놀랐다."]], [["“Are you arrested for much, sir?”", "\"많은 액수에 체포되신 건가요, 어르신?\""], ["I inquired of Mr. Skimpole.", "나는 스킴폴 씨에게 물었다."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson,”", "\"이보세요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said he, shaking his head pleasantly, “I don’t know.", "그가 정겹게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"나도 몰라요."], ["Some pounds, odd shillings, and halfpence, I think, were mentioned.”", "몇 파운드에다 잔돈 몇 실링하고 반 페니가 어쩌고 하던 것 같긴 한데.\""]], [["“It’s twenty-four pound, sixteen, and sevenpence ha’penny,”", "\"이십사 파운드 십육 실링하고 칠 펜스 반이에요.\""], ["observed the stranger.", "낯선 사람이 짚었다."], ["“That’s wot it is.”", "\"고게 액수요.\""]], [["“And it sounds—somehow it sounds,”", "\"그리고 그게 — 어쩐지 그게,\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Skimpole, “like a small sum?”", "\"적은 액수처럼 들리지 않나요?\""]], [["The strange man said nothing but made another snort.", "낯선 사람은 아무 말도 하지 않고 또 한차례 콧김을 내뿜었다."], ["It was such a powerful one that it seemed quite to lift him out of his seat.", "어찌나 힘찬 콧김이었던지 그가 제 자리에서 들썩 솟구치는 것만 같았다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"스킴폴 씨는,\""], ["Skimpole,”", "리처드가 나에게 말했다."], ["said Richard to me, “has a delicacy in applying to my cousin Jarndyce because he has lately—I think, sir, I understood you that you had lately—”", "\"내 사촌 잔다이스에게 부탁하기를 꺼리고 계세요. 요즘 들어 — 제가 알기로는, 어르신, 요즘 들어 — 라고 말씀하신 것 같은데 —\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"아, 그렇소!\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole, smiling.", "스킴폴 씨가 미소 지으며 받았다."], ["“Though I forgot how much it was and when it was.", "\"얼마였는지, 또 언제였는지는 잊어버렸지만요."], ["Jarndyce would readily do it again, but I have the epicure-like feeling that I would prefer a novelty in help, that I would rather,”", "잔다이스라면 기꺼이 또 해 주겠지만, 나에게는 미식가 같은 기분이 있어서, 도움이라면 좀 색다른 것이 좋겠다, 차라리,\" 그러고는 리처드와 나를 보며 말했다."], ["and he looked at Richard and me, “develop generosity in a new soil and in a new form of flower.”", "\"새로운 토양에서, 새로운 모양의 꽃으로 너그러움을 피워 내고 싶다는 마음이 든다오.\""]], [["“What do you think will be best, Miss Summerson?”", "\"어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을까요, 서머슨 양?\""], ["said Richard, aside.", "리처드가 옆에서 나직이 말했다."]], [["I ventured to inquire, generally, before replying, what would happen if the money were not produced.", "나는 대답하기에 앞서, 두루뭉술하게, 그 돈이 마련되지 않으면 어떻게 되는 것이냐고 감히 물어보았다."]], [["“Jail,”", "\"감옥이지.\""], ["said the strange man, coolly putting his handkerchief into his hat, which was on the floor at his feet.", "낯선 사람이 발치 바닥에 놓인 자기 모자에 손수건을 태연히 집어넣으며 말했다."], ["“Or Coavinses.”", "\"아니면 코빈시스든가.\""]], [["“May I ask, sir, what is—”", "\"실례지만, 어르신, 무엇이 —\""]], [["“Coavinses?”", "\"코빈시스 말이오?\""], ["said the strange man.", "낯선 사람이 말했다."], ["“A ’ouse.”", "\"집이오.\""]], [["Richard and I looked at one another again.", "리처드와 나는 다시 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["It was a most singular thing that the arrest was our embarrassment and not Mr. Skimpole’s.", "이 체포가 스킴폴 씨가 아니라 우리의 난처함이 되어 버렸다는 것은 더없이 별난 일이었다."], ["He observed us with a genial interest, but there seemed, if I may venture on such a contradiction, nothing selfish in it.", "그는 정겨운 관심을 가지고 우리를 지켜보았는데, 이런 모순된 말을 굳이 해도 된다면, 거기에는 이기적인 데라곤 조금도 없어 보였다."], ["He had entirely washed his hands of the difficulty, and it had become ours.", "그는 이 곤경에서 완전히 손을 씻어 버렸고, 그것은 우리의 것이 되어 있었다."]], [["“I thought,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["he suggested, as if good-naturedly to help us out, “that being parties in a Chancery suit concerning (as people say) a large amount of property, Mr.", "그가 마치 사람 좋게 우리를 거들어 주려는 듯 넌지시 말했다."], ["Richard or his beautiful cousin, or both, could sign something, or make over something, or give some sort of undertaking, or pledge, or bond?", "\"(사람들이 흔히 말하듯) 거액의 재산이 걸린 대법원 소송의 당사자들이니, 리처드 군이나 그 아름다운 사촌이나, 아니면 둘이서, 뭔가에 서명을 하거나, 뭔가를 양도하거나, 무슨 약정이나 담보나 증서 같은 걸 줄 수 있지 않겠소?"], ["I don’t know what the business name of it may be, but I suppose there is some instrument within their power that would settle this?”", "그게 법률 용어로 뭐라 불리는지는 모르겠지만, 이 일을 해결해 줄 무슨 증서 같은 게 두 사람의 권한 안에 있지 않을까 싶은데?\""]], [["“Not a bit on it,”", "\"택도 없소.\""], ["said the strange man.", "낯선 사람이 말했다."]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말이오?\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 받았다."], ["“That seems odd, now, to one who is no judge of these things!”", "\"이런 일은 도통 모르는 사람한테는, 그것참, 이상하게 들리는군요!\""]], [["“Odd or even,”", "\"이상하든 멀쩡하든,\""], ["said the stranger gruffly, “I tell you, not a bit on it!”", "낯선 사람이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"내 말하지 않았소, 택도 없다고!\""]], [["“Keep your temper, my good fellow, keep your temper!” Mr.", "\"성질 좀 누그러뜨려요, 이보시오, 좀 누그러뜨려요!\""], ["Skimpole gently reasoned with him as he made a little drawing of his head on the fly-leaf of a book.", "스킴폴 씨가 책의 속표지에 그 사람의 머리를 자그맣게 그려 넣으며 부드럽게 타일렀다."], ["“Don’t be ruffled by your occupation.", "\"당신 직업 때문에 부아를 내지는 말아요."], ["We can separate you from your office; we can separate the individual from the pursuit.", "우리는 당신을 당신 직책과 떼어 놓을 수 있으니까요. 우리는 개인을 그 생업과 떼어 놓을 수 있어요."], ["We are not so prejudiced as to suppose that in private life you are otherwise than a very estimable man, with a great deal of poetry in your nature, of which you may not be conscious.”", "우리는 사사로운 삶에서 당신이 더없이 훌륭한 사람, 본인은 미처 모를지라도 천성에 시정이 가득한 사람이 아니라고 여길 만큼 편견에 사로잡혀 있지 않다오.\""]], [["The stranger only answered with another violent snort, whether in acceptance of the poetry-tribute or in disdainful rejection of it, he did not express to me.", "낯선 사람은 또 한차례 거센 콧김으로만 대꾸했을 뿐, 그것이 그 시정의 헌사를 받아들이는 것인지 아니면 경멸하며 물리치는 것인지는 나에게 내비치지 않았다."]], [["“Now, my dear Miss Summerson, and my dear Mr. Richard,”", "\"자, 이보세요, 서머슨 양, 그리고 리처드 군.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole gaily, innocently, and confidingly as he looked at his drawing with his head on one side, “here you see me utterly incapable of helping myself, and entirely in your hands!", "스킴폴 씨가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 자기가 그린 그림을 들여다보며 명랑하고 천진하고 미덥게 말했다."], ["I only ask to be free.", "\"여기 보다시피 나는 나 자신을 도울 길이 도무지 없고, 온전히 당신들 손에 달려 있어요!"], ["The butterflies are free.", "나는 그저 자유로워지기를 바랄 뿐이오."], ["Mankind will surely not deny to Harold Skimpole what it concedes to the butterflies!”", "나비는 자유롭지요. 인류가 설마 나비에게 허락하는 것을 해럴드 스킴폴에게는 거절하지 않겠지요!\""]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson,”", "\"이보세요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said Richard in a whisper, “I have ten pounds that I received from Mr.", "리처드가 속삭이며 말했다. \"켄지 씨에게서 받은 십 파운드가 있어요."], ["Kenge. I must try what that will do.”", "그걸로 어떻게 되는지 해 봐야겠어요.\""]], [["I possessed fifteen pounds, odd shillings, which I had saved from my quarterly allowance during several years.", "나에게는 여러 해에 걸쳐 분기마다 받은 용돈에서 모은 십오 파운드 남짓이 있었다."], ["I had always thought that some accident might happen which would throw me suddenly, without any relation or any property, on the world and had always tried to keep some little money by me that I might not be quite penniless.", "나는 친척 하나 재산 하나 없이 별안간 세상에 내던져지는 무슨 일이 생길지도 모른다고 늘 생각해 왔고, 아주 빈털터리가 되지는 않도록 늘 얼마간 돈을 곁에 두려고 애써 왔다."], ["I told Richard of my having this little store and having no present need of it, and I asked him delicately to inform Mr. Skimpole, while I should be gone to fetch it, that we would have the pleasure of paying his debt.", "나는 리처드에게 이 작은 비축이 있고 지금 당장은 그것이 필요하지 않다고 말했고, 내가 그것을 가지러 간 사이에, 우리가 기꺼이 그의 빚을 갚아 드리겠다고 스킴폴 씨에게 넌지시 전해 달라고 그에게 조심스레 부탁했다."]], [["When I came back, Mr. Skimpole kissed my hand and seemed quite touched.", "내가 돌아오자, 스킴폴 씨는 내 손에 입을 맞추며 무척 감격한 듯했다."], ["Not on his own account (I was again aware of that perplexing and extraordinary contradiction), but on ours, as if personal considerations were impossible with him and the contemplation of our happiness alone affected him.", "자기 자신 때문이 아니라(나는 그 어리둥절하고 기이한 모순을 다시 한번 느꼈다), 우리 때문이었으니, 마치 그에게는 자기를 위한 헤아림이란 있을 수 없고 오직 우리의 행복을 떠올리는 것만이 그를 움직이는 것 같았다."], ["Richard, begging me, for the greater grace of the transaction, as he said, to settle with Coavinses (as Mr. Skimpole now jocularly called him), I counted out the money and received the necessary acknowledgment.", "리처드가, 그의 말마따나 거래에 더 멋을 더하려고, 코빈시스와(이제 스킴폴 씨는 그를 그렇게 익살스레 불렀다) 셈을 치러 달라고 나에게 청했으므로, 나는 돈을 세어 주고 필요한 영수증을 받았다."], ["This, too, delighted Mr. Skimpole.", "이것 역시 스킴폴 씨를 흐뭇하게 했다."]], [["His compliments were so delicately administered that I blushed less than I might have done and settled with the stranger in the white coat without making any mistakes.", "그의 찬사가 어찌나 섬세하게 베풀어졌던지, 나는 그러지 않았더라면 더 붉어졌을 얼굴을 덜 붉히며, 흰 외투를 입은 낯선 사람과 한 치의 실수도 없이 셈을 치렀다."], ["He put the money in his pocket and shortly said, “Well, then, I’ll wish you a good evening, miss.”", "그는 돈을 주머니에 넣고는 짤막하게 말했다. \"그럼, 좋은 저녁 보내십쇼, 아가씨.\""]], [["“My friend,” said Mr.", "\"이보시오.\""], ["Skimpole, standing with his back to the fire after giving up the sketch when it was half finished, “I should like to ask you something, without offence.”", "스킴폴 씨가 절반쯤 그린 스케치를 마저 포기하고 난로에 등을 댄 채 서서 말했다. \"기분 나빠하지 말고, 내 뭐 하나 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["I think the reply was, “Cut away, then!”", "대답은 아마 \"그럼 어디 까놓고 물어보쇼!\"였던 것 같다."]], [["“Did you know this morning, now, that you were coming out on this errand?”", "\"오늘 아침에 말이오, 당신 오늘 이 일로 나설 거라는 걸 알고 있었소?\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Know’d it yes’day aft’noon at tea-time,”", "\"어제 오후 차 마실 때 알았수다.\""], ["said Coavinses.", "코빈시스가 말했다."]], [["“It didn’t affect your appetite?", "\"그게 당신 입맛에는 영향을 안 줬소?"], ["Didn’t make you at all uneasy?”", "마음이 조금도 불편하지 않았고?\""]], [["“Not a bit,”", "\"눈곱만치도.\""], ["said Coavinses.", "코빈시스가 말했다."], ["“I know’d if you wos missed to-day, you wouldn’t be missed to-morrow.", "\"오늘 당신을 놓쳐도 내일 놓칠 일은 없을 거 아뉴."], ["A day makes no such odds.”", "하루쯤 차이야 별것도 아니지.\""]], [["“But when you came down here,” proceeded Mr.", "\"그러나 당신이 여기 내려올 때는,\""], ["Skimpole, “it was a fine day.", "스킴폴 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["The sun was shining, the wind was blowing, the lights and shadows were passing across the fields, the birds were singing.”", "\"날이 좋았소. 해가 빛나고, 바람이 불고, 빛과 그림자가 들판을 가로질러 지나가고, 새들이 노래하고 있었지요.\""]], [["“Nobody said they warn’t, in MY hearing,”", "\"안 그렇다고 한 사람은 내 귀엔 없었수다.\""], ["returned Coavinses.", "코빈시스가 받았다."]], [["“No,” observed Mr.", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했다."], ["“But what did you think upon the road?”", "\"그런데 오는 길에 무슨 생각을 했소?\""]], [["“Wot do you mean?”", "\"그게 뭔 소리요?\""], ["growled Coavinses with an appearance of strong resentment.", "코빈시스가 잔뜩 부아가 난 기색으로 으르렁거렸다. \"생각이라!"], ["“Think! I’ve got enough to do, and little enough to get for it without thinking.", "나는 할 일도 많고, 그래 봤자 손에 쥐는 것도 쥐꼬리만 한데 생각이라니."], ["Thinking!”", "생각!\""], ["(with profound contempt).", "(깊디깊은 경멸을 담아.)"]], [["“Then you didn’t think, at all events,” proceeded Mr.", "\"그렇다면 적어도 이런 생각은 하지 않았겠군요.\""], ["Skimpole, “to this effect: ‘Harold Skimpole loves to see the sun shine, loves to hear the wind blow, loves to watch the changing lights and shadows, loves to hear the birds, those choristers in Nature’s great cathedral.", "스킴폴 씨가 말을 이었다. \"'해럴드 스킴폴은 해가 빛나는 걸 보기 좋아하고, 바람이 부는 소리 듣기를 좋아하고, 변하는 빛과 그림자 지켜보기를 좋아하고, 새들, 저 자연의 대성당에서 노래하는 성가대원들의 소리 듣기를 좋아하지."], ["And does it seem to me that I am about to deprive Harold Skimpole of his share in such possessions, which are his only birthright!’ You thought nothing to that effect?”", "그런데 내가 지금 해럴드 스킴폴에게서 그런 소유물의, 그의 유일한 타고난 권리인 그것의 몫을 빼앗으려는 게 아닌가!' 이런 생각은 조금도 하지 않았겠지요?\""]], [["“I—certainly—did—NOT,”", "\"나는 — 정말로 — 안 — 했소.\""], ["said Coavinses, whose doggedness in utterly renouncing the idea was of that intense kind that he could only give adequate expression to it by putting a long interval between each word, and accompanying the last with a jerk that might have dislocated his neck.", "코빈시스가 말했는데, 그 생각을 깡그리 부인하는 그 옹고집이 어찌나 지독했던지, 그는 한 단어 한 단어 사이에 긴 틈을 두어야만, 게다가 마지막 단어에는 목이 삐끗할 만한 홱 젖힘까지 곁들여야만 그것을 제대로 표현할 수 있었다."]], [["“Very odd and very curious, the mental process is, in you men of business!”", "\"무척 별나고 무척 흥미롭군요, 당신네 사업가들의 머릿속 작용이라는 것이 말이오!\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole thoughtfully.", "스킴폴 씨가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["“Thank you, my friend.", "\"고맙소, 이보시오."], ["Good night.”", "잘 가시오.\""]], [["As our absence had been long enough already to seem strange downstairs, I returned at once and found Ada sitting at work by the fireside talking to her cousin John.", "우리가 자리를 비운 것이 이미 아래층에서 이상하게 보일 만큼 길어졌으므로, 나는 곧장 돌아왔고, 에이다가 난롯가에서 뜨개질을 하며 사촌 존과 이야기를 나누고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Mr. Skimpole presently appeared, and Richard shortly after him.", "이내 스킴폴 씨가 나타났고, 그 뒤를 이어 곧 리처드가 들어왔다."], ["I was sufficiently engaged during the remainder of the evening in taking my first lesson in backgammon from Mr. Jarndyce, who was very fond of the game and from whom I wished of course to learn it as quickly as I could in order that I might be of the very small use of being able to play when he had no better adversary.", "나는 그날 저녁 남은 동안 잔다이스 씨에게서 주사위놀이의 첫 수업을 받느라 충분히 바빴는데, 그는 그 놀이를 무척 좋아했고, 나로서는 물론 그가 더 나은 상대가 없을 때 그와 한판 둘 수 있는, 그 자그마한 쓸모라도 되고 싶어서 되도록 빨리 그것을 배우고 싶었다."], ["But I thought, occasionally, when Mr. Skimpole played some fragments of his own compositions or when, both at the piano and the violoncello, and at our table, he preserved with an absence of all effort his delightful spirits and his easy flow of conversation, that Richard and I seemed to retain the transferred impression of having been arrested since dinner and that it was very curious altogether.", "그러나 나는 이따금, 스킴폴 씨가 자기가 작곡한 곡의 단편을 연주할 때나, 피아노에서든 첼로에서든 또 우리 식탁에서든 그가 아무런 애쓴 기색 없이 그 유쾌한 흥과 막힘없는 입담을 이어 갈 때면, 리처드와 나는 저녁 식사 이후로 체포되어 있던 듯한 그 옮아 온 인상을 여전히 떨치지 못하고 있구나, 그리고 통틀어 보면 참으로 묘한 일이구나 하는 생각이 들었다."]], [["It was late before we separated, for when Ada was going at eleven o’clock, Mr. Skimpole went to the piano and rattled hilariously that the best of all ways to lengthen our days was to steal a few hours from night, my dear!", "우리가 자리를 파한 것은 밤이 깊어서였으니, 에이다가 열한 시에 일어서려 하자 스킴폴 씨가 피아노로 가서, 우리 나날을 늘리는 가장 좋은 길은 밤에서 몇 시간을 훔쳐 오는 것이라는 곡을 흥겹게 두드려 댔던 것이다, 사랑스러운 이여!"], ["It was past twelve before he took his candle and his radiant face out of the room, and I think he might have kept us there, if he had seen fit, until daybreak. Ada and Richard were lingering for a few moments by the fire, wondering whether Mrs.", "그가 촛불과 그 빛나는 얼굴을 들고 방에서 나간 것은 자정이 지나서였는데, 그가 마음만 먹었다면 우리를 동틀 녘까지 그 자리에 붙들어 두었을 성싶다."], ["Jellyby had yet finished her dictation for the day, when Mr. Jarndyce, who had been out of the room, returned.", "에이다와 리처드가 젤리비 부인이 그날 받아쓰기를 이제 끝마쳤을까 궁금해하며 난롯가에서 잠시 미적거리고 있는데, 방에서 나가 있던 잔다이스 씨가 돌아왔다."]], [["“Oh, dear me, what’s this, what’s this!”", "\"아이고, 저런, 이게 무슨, 이게 무슨 일이냐!\""], ["he said, rubbing his head and walking about with his good-humoured vexation.", "그가 머리를 문지르며, 그 사람 좋은 짜증과 함께 방 안을 거닐면서 말했다."], ["“What’s this they tell me?", "\"이게 무슨 소리들이냐?"], ["Rick, my boy, Esther, my dear, what have you been doing?", "릭, 얘야, 에스더, 귀여운 아이야, 너희가 무슨 짓을 한 거냐?"], ["Why did you do it?", "왜 그런 짓을 했어?"], ["How could you do it?", "어떻게 그럴 수가 있느냐?"], ["How much apiece was it?", "각자 얼마씩이었지?"], ["The wind’s round again.", "바람이 또 도는구나."], ["I feel it all over me!”", "온몸으로 느껴진다!\""]], [["We neither of us quite knew what to answer.", "우리 둘 다 무어라 대답해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Come, Rick, come!", "\"이리 오너라, 릭, 어서!"], ["I must settle this before I sleep.", "잠들기 전에 이걸 매듭지어야겠다."], ["How much are you out of pocket?", "너는 얼마나 손해를 봤느냐?"], ["You two made the money up, you know!", "너희 둘이 그 돈을 모았다며, 그렇지!"], ["Why did you?", "왜 그랬느냐?"], ["How could you?", "어떻게 그럴 수가 있어?"], ["Oh, Lord, yes, it’s due east—must be!”", "아, 이런, 그래, 정동풍이야 — 틀림없어!\""]], [["“Really, sir,”", "\"정말이지, 어르신.\""], ["said Richard, “I don’t think it would be honourable in me to tell you.", "리처드가 말했다. \"제가 그걸 말씀드리는 건 떳떳하지 못한 일인 것 같습니다."], ["Mr. Skimpole relied upon us—”", "스킴폴 씨가 저희를 믿고 —\""]], [["“Lord bless you, my dear boy!", "\"이런 딱한 녀석을 봤나!"], ["He relies upon everybody!”", "그 사람은 누구든 다 믿어!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce, giving his head a great rub and stopping short.", "잔다이스 씨가 머리를 박박 문지르며 우뚝 멈춰 서서 말했다."]], [["“Indeed, sir?”", "\"정말이십니까, 어르신?\""]], [["“Everybody!", "\"누구든 다지!"], ["And he’ll be in the same scrape again next week!” said Mr.", "그리고 다음 주에도 똑같은 곤경에 빠질 게다!\""], ["Jarndyce, walking again at a great pace, with a candle in his hand that had gone out.", "잔다이스 씨가 꺼져 버린 촛불을 손에 든 채 다시 큰 보폭으로 거닐며 말했다."], ["“He’s always in the same scrape.", "\"그는 늘 똑같은 곤경에 빠져 있어."], ["He was born in the same scrape.", "그 곤경 속에서 태어났지."], ["I verily believe that the announcement in the newspapers when his mother was confined was ‘On Tuesday last, at her residence in Botheration Buildings, Mrs.", "그의 어머니가 몸을 풀었을 때 신문에 난 공고가 분명 이랬을 거라고 나는 정말로 믿는다."], ["Skimpole of a son in difficulties.’”", "'지난 화요일, 골칫거리 빌딩의 자택에서, 스킴폴 부인이 곤경에 빠진 아들을 낳다.'\""]], [["Richard laughed heartily but added, “Still, sir, I don’t want to shake his confidence or to break his confidence, and if I submit to your better knowledge again, that I ought to keep his secret, I hope you will consider before you press me any more.", "리처드는 한바탕 시원하게 웃고는 덧붙였다. \"그래도, 어르신, 저는 그분의 신뢰를 흔들거나 저버리고 싶지는 않습니다."], ["Of course, if you do press me, sir, I shall know I am wrong and will tell you.”", "그래서 그분의 비밀을 지켜야 한다는, 어르신의 더 나은 판단에 다시 따른다 하더라도, 저를 더 다그치시기 전에 한 번 헤아려 주시길 바랍니다. 물론 어르신이 그래도 다그치신다면, 저는 제가 잘못하고 있음을 알고 말씀드리겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well!” cried Mr.", "\"좋다!\""], ["Jarndyce, stopping again, and making several absent endeavours to put his candlestick in his pocket.", "잔다이스 씨가 다시 걸음을 멈추며, 촛대를 주머니에 넣으려고 멍하니 몇 번 애쓰면서 외쳤다. \"내가 — 여기!"], ["“I—here! Take it away, my dear.", "이것 좀 치워 다오, 얘야."], ["I don’t know what I am about with it; it’s all the wind—invariably has that effect—I won’t press you, Rick; you may be right.", "내가 이걸로 뭘 하는지 모르겠구나. 다 바람 탓이야 — 어김없이 이런 식이지 — 너를 다그치지 않으마, 릭."], ["But really—to get hold of you and Esther—and to squeeze you like a couple of tender young Saint Michael’s oranges!", "네 말이 옳을 게다. 그러나 정말이지 — 너하고 에스더를 붙들어서 — 마치 한 쌍의 여린 풋 세인트마이클 오렌지처럼 쥐어짜다니!"], ["It’ll blow a gale in the course of the night!”", "밤사이에 강풍이 몰아치겠구나!\""]], [["He was now alternately putting his hands into his pockets as if he were going to keep them there a long time, and taking them out again and vehemently rubbing them all over his head.", "그는 이제 두 손을 오랫동안 거기 둘 작정인 양 주머니에 찔러 넣었다가, 다시 빼내어 머리를 온통 사납게 문지르기를 번갈아 했다."]], [["I ventured to take this opportunity of hinting that Mr. Skimpole, being in all such matters quite a child—", "나는 이 틈을 타, 스킴폴 씨가 그런 모든 일에는 정말이지 어린아이여서 —라고 넌지시 운을 떼 보았다."]], [["“Eh, my dear?”", "\"응, 얘야?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce, catching at the word.", "잔다이스 씨가 그 말을 덥석 붙들며 말했다."]], [["“Being quite a child, sir,”", "\"정말이지 어린아이여서요, 어르신.\""], ["said I, “and so different from other people—”", "내가 말했다. \"여느 사람들과는 너무도 다르니까요 —\""]], [["“You are right!”", "\"네 말이 옳다!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce, brightening.", "잔다이스 씨가 얼굴이 환해지며 말했다."], ["“Your woman’s wit hits the mark.", "\"네 여자다운 슬기가 핵심을 찌르는구나."], ["He is a child—an absolute child.", "그는 어린아이야 — 영락없는 어린아이지."], ["I told you he was a child, you know, when I first mentioned him.”", "내가 그를 처음 입에 올렸을 때, 그가 어린아이라고 너희에게 말했잖느냐.\""]], [["Certainly!", "물론이지요!"], ["Certainly!", "물론이고말고요!"], ["we said.", "우리는 말했다."]], [["“And he IS a child.", "\"그리고 그는 어린아이지."], ["Now, isn’t he?”", "자, 그렇지 않으냐?\""], ["asked Mr. Jarndyce, brightening more and more.", "잔다이스 씨가 점점 더 얼굴이 환해지며 물었다."]], [["He was indeed, we said.", "정말 그렇습니다, 우리는 말했다."]], [["“When you come to think of it, it’s the height of childishness in you—I mean me—” said Mr.", "\"가만 생각해 보면, 그를 한순간이라도 어른으로 여긴 너희가 — 아니, 내가 — 더없이 유치한 짓을 한 거다.\""], ["Jarndyce, “to regard him for a moment as a man.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."], ["You can’t make HIM responsible.", "\"그를 책임지게 할 수는 없어."], ["The idea of Harold Skimpole with designs or plans, or knowledge of consequences!", "해럴드 스킴폴이 꿍꿍이나 계략을 품다니, 아니면 결과를 헤아리다니, 가당키나 하냐!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!”", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["It was so delicious to see the clouds about his bright face clearing, and to see him so heartily pleased, and to know, as it was impossible not to know, that the source of his pleasure was the goodness which was tortured by condemning, or mistrusting, or secretly accusing any one, that I saw the tears in Ada’s eyes, while she echoed his laugh, and felt them in my own.", "그의 환한 얼굴을 둘러싼 먹구름이 걷히는 것을 보는 것이, 그리고 그가 그토록 진심으로 흐뭇해하는 것을 보는 것이, 그리고 그 기쁨의 근원이 누군가를 단죄하거나 의심하거나 남몰래 탓하는 일로 괴로워하는 그 선량함이라는 것을 — 모를 수가 없으니 — 아는 것이, 어찌나 더없이 흐뭇했던지, 나는 에이다가 그의 웃음을 따라 웃으면서 눈에 눈물이 어리는 것을 보았고, 내 눈에서도 그 눈물을 느꼈다."]], [["“Why, what a cod’s head and shoulders I am,” said Mr.", "\"이런, 내가 무슨 대구 대가리에 어깻죽지 같은 멍청이람.\""], ["Jarndyce, “to require reminding of it!", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"그런 걸 일깨워 줘야 하다니!"], ["The whole business shows the child from beginning to end.", "이 일 전체가 처음부터 끝까지 어린아이를 그대로 보여 주는구나."], ["Nobody but a child would have thought of singling YOU two out for parties in the affair!", "어린아이가 아니고서야 누가 이 일의 당사자로 하필 너희 둘을 콕 집을 생각을 했겠느냐!"], ["Nobody but a child would have thought of YOUR having the money!", "어린아이가 아니고서야 누가 너희한테 그 돈이 있을 거라고 생각했겠어!"], ["If it had been a thousand pounds, it would have been just the same!”", "그게 천 파운드였다 해도 매한가지였을 거다!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce with his whole face in a glow.", "잔다이스 씨가 온 얼굴을 환히 빛내며 말했다."]], [["We all confirmed it from our night’s experience.", "우리는 모두 그날 밤의 경험으로 그 말이 옳다고 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“To be sure, to be sure!”", "\"암, 그렇고말고, 그렇고말고!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."], ["“However, Rick, Esther, and you too, Ada, for I don’t know that even your little purse is safe from his inexperience—I must have a promise all round that nothing of this sort shall ever be done any more.", "\"그건 그렇고, 릭, 에스더, 그리고 너도, 에이다, 네 자그마한 지갑이라고 그의 철없음에서 안전한지 모르겠으니 — 나는 다 같이 약속을 받아 두어야겠다, 다시는 이런 일을 결코 하지 않겠다고."], ["No advances!", "선금은 안 된다!"], ["Not even sixpences.”", "단돈 육 펜스도 안 돼.\""]], [["We all promised faithfully, Richard with a merry glance at me touching his pocket as if to remind me that there was no danger of OUR transgressing.", "우리는 모두 굳게 약속했고, 리처드는 우리야 어길 위험이 없다는 것을 나에게 일깨우려는 듯 나를 향해 흥겨운 눈짓을 보내며 제 주머니를 톡톡 두드렸다."]], [["“As to Skimpole,” said Mr.", "\"스킴폴로 말하자면,\""], ["Jarndyce, “a habitable doll’s house with good board and a few tin people to get into debt with and borrow money of would set the boy up in life.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다. \"사람이 살 만한 인형의 집에다 끼니나 잘 챙겨 주고, 빚을 지거나 돈을 빌릴 양철 인형 몇이나 마련해 주면, 그 애한테는 인생살이로 더없이 그만일 게다."], ["He is in a child’s sleep by this time, I suppose; it’s time I should take my craftier head to my more worldly pillow.", "지금쯤 그는 아이처럼 잠들어 있겠지. 나도 이 더 약은 머리를 더 세속적인 베개에 누일 때가 되었구나."], ["Good night, my dears.", "잘들 자거라, 얘들아."], ["God bless you!”", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["He peeped in again, with a smiling face, before we had lighted our candles, and said, “Oh!", "우리가 촛불을 켜기도 전에 그가 미소 띤 얼굴로 다시 빼꼼 들여다보며 말했다."], ["I have been looking at the weather-cock.", "\"오! 풍향계를 보고 왔다."], ["I find it was a false alarm about the wind.", "바람에 대해서는 내가 괜한 호들갑을 떤 거더구나."], ["It’s in the south!”", "남쪽이야!\""], ["And went away singing to himself.", "그러고는 혼자 흥얼거리며 가 버렸다."]], [["Ada and I agreed, as we talked together for a little while upstairs, that this caprice about the wind was a fiction and that he used the pretence to account for any disappointment he could not conceal, rather than he would blame the real cause of it or disparage or depreciate any one.", "에이다와 나는 위층에서 잠시 이런저런 이야기를 나누다가, 바람에 대한 이 변덕이 꾸며 낸 것이며, 그가 그 핑계를 쓰는 것은, 차마 감추지 못하는 어떤 실망의 진짜 원인을 탓하거나 누군가를 깎아내리거나 낮잡아 보느니, 차라리 그것으로 둘러대기 위해서라는 데 뜻을 모았다."], ["We thought this very characteristic of his eccentric gentleness and of the difference between him and those petulant people who make the weather and the winds (particularly that unlucky wind which he had chosen for such a different purpose) the stalking-horses of their splenetic and gloomy humours.", "우리는 이것이 그의 별난 다정함을, 그리고 날씨와 바람을(특히 그가 그토록 다른 목적에 골라 쓴 그 운수 사나운 바람을) 자기네 골나고 침울한 심사의 핑곗거리로 삼는 그 짜증스러운 사람들과 그의 차이를, 참으로 잘 보여 준다고 생각했다."]], [["Indeed, so much affection for him had been added in this one evening to my gratitude that I hoped I already began to understand him through that mingled feeling. Any seeming inconsistencies in Mr.", "정말이지, 이 단 하루 저녁에 그에 대한 애정이 내 고마움에 어찌나 많이 더해졌던지, 나는 그 뒤섞인 감정을 통해 벌써 그를 이해하기 시작한 것이기를 바랐다."], ["Skimpole or in Mrs. Jellyby I could not expect to be able to reconcile, having so little experience or practical knowledge.", "스킴폴 씨에게서든 젤리비 부인에게서든 겉으로 드러나는 어떤 모순들을 내가 능히 화해시키리라고는 기대할 수 없었으니, 나에게는 경험도 실제적인 지식도 너무 적었기 때문이다."], ["Neither did I try, for my thoughts were busy when I was alone, with Ada and Richard and with the confidence I had seemed to receive concerning them.", "화해시키려고 애쓰지도 않았으니, 혼자 있을 때 내 생각은 에이다와 리처드로, 그리고 두 사람에 관해 내가 전해 받은 듯한 그 속내로 분주했기 때문이다."], ["My fancy, made a little wild by the wind perhaps, would not consent to be all unselfish, either, though I would have persuaded it to be so if I could.", "어쩌면 바람 탓에 조금 들떴는지, 내 공상은 끝까지 온전히 욕심 없는 것이 되기를 좀처럼 받아들이지 않았다, 할 수만 있다면 그러도록 타일렀을 텐데도."], ["It wandered back to my godmother’s house and came along the intervening track, raising up shadowy speculations which had sometimes trembled there in the dark as to what knowledge Mr. Jarndyce had of my earliest history—even as to the possibility of his being my father, though that idle dream was quite gone now.", "그것은 대모님의 집으로 거슬러 올라가, 그 사이의 자취를 따라 더듬어 오면서, 거기 어둠 속에서 이따금 떨리곤 했던 그 어렴풋한 억측들을 — 잔다이스 씨가 내 가장 어린 시절의 내력에 대해 무엇을 알고 있을까 하는, 심지어 그가 내 아버지일 수도 있지 않을까 하는 억측까지 — 불러일으켰다, 비록 그 부질없는 꿈은 이제 말끔히 사라지고 없었지만."]], [["It was all gone now, I remembered, getting up from the fire.", "이제 그것은 다 사라지고 없다고, 나는 난롯가에서 일어서며 떠올렸다."], ["It was not for me to muse over bygones, but to act with a cheerful spirit and a grateful heart.", "지나간 일을 곱씹는 것은 내가 할 바가 아니요, 명랑한 마음과 고마운 가슴으로 행동하는 것이 내가 할 바였다."], ["So I said to myself, “Esther, Esther, Esther!", "그래서 나는 스스로에게 말했다. \"에스더, 에스더, 에스더!"], ["Duty, my dear!”", "본분이야, 얘야!\""], ["and gave my little basket of housekeeping keys such a shake that they sounded like little bells and rang me hopefully to bed. The Ghost’s Walk", "그러고는 살림 열쇠가 담긴 자그마한 바구니를 흔들어, 작은 종소리처럼 짤랑이게 하니, 그 소리가 나를 희망차게 잠자리로 불러들였다."]], [["While Esther sleeps, and while Esther wakes, it is still wet weather down at the place in Lincolnshire.", "에스더가 잠들었을 때나, 에스더가 깨어 있을 때나, 링컨셔의 그 저택은 여전히 비에 젖어 있다."], ["The rain is ever falling—drip, drip, drip—by day and night upon the broad flagged terrace-pavement, the Ghost’s Walk.", "비는 끊임없이 내린다. 뚝, 뚝, 뚝, 밤낮없이 넓적한 판석을 깐 테라스 보도, 그러니까 유령의 길 위로 떨어진다."], ["The weather is so very bad down in Lincolnshire that the liveliest imagination can scarcely apprehend its ever being fine again.", "링컨셔 일대의 날씨가 어찌나 고약한지, 제아무리 생기 넘치는 상상력이라도 다시 맑게 갤 날을 떠올리기란 좀처럼 어렵다."], ["Not that there is any superabundant life of imagination on the spot, for Sir Leicester is not here (and, truly, even if he were, would not do much for it in that particular), but is in Paris with my Lady; and solitude, with dusky wings, sits brooding upon Chesney Wold.", "그렇다고 그곳에 상상력이 넘쳐흐를 일도 없으니, 레스터 경이 여기 없는 까닭이다(설령 있다 한들, 솔직히 말해 그 방면에서는 별 보탬이 못 되겠지만). 경은 마님과 함께 파리에 가 있고, 적막이 거무스름한 날개를 펼친 채 체스니 월드 위에 웅크리고 앉아 시름에 잠겨 있다."]], [["There may be some motions of fancy among the lower animals at Chesney Wold.", "체스니 월드의 미물들 사이에서는 어쩌면 상상의 움직임이 조금쯤 일렁이고 있을지 모른다."], ["The horses in the stables—the long stables in a barren, red-brick court-yard, where there is a great bell in a turret, and a clock with a large face, which the pigeons who live near it and who love to perch upon its shoulders seem to be always consulting—THEY may contemplate some mental pictures of fine weather on occasions, and may be better artists at them than the grooms.", "마구간의 말들 말이다. 휑뎅그렁한 붉은 벽돌 안마당에 늘어선 기다란 마구간, 작은 탑에 커다란 종이 달려 있고, 그 곁에 사는 데다 그 어깨에 즐겨 내려앉는 비둘기들이 늘 들여다보며 묻기라도 하는 듯한, 큼직한 문자판이 달린 시계가 있는 그곳, 그 말들이라면 이따금 맑은 날의 풍경을 머릿속에 그려볼 수도 있겠고, 어쩌면 마부들보다 솜씨 좋은 화가일지도 모른다."], ["The old roan, so famous for cross-country work, turning his large eyeball to the grated window near his rack, may remember the fresh leaves that glisten there at other times and the scents that stream in, and may have a fine run with the hounds, while the human helper, clearing out the next stall, never stirs beyond his pitchfork and birch-broom.", "거친 들판을 가로지르는 일로 그토록 이름난 늙은 적갈색 말은, 여물통 옆 쇠창살 창으로 커다란 눈알을 굴리며, 다른 철이면 그곳에서 반짝이던 싱싱한 잎사귀와 솔솔 풍기던 냄새를 떠올리고, 사냥개들과 함께 신나게 내달리고 있을지 모른다. 정작 사람 일꾼은 옆 칸을 치우면서 쇠스랑과 자작나무 빗자루를 손에서 놓지 못하는데도 말이다."], ["The grey, whose place is opposite the door and who with an impatient rattle of his halter pricks his ears and turns his head so wistfully when it is opened, and to whom the opener says, “Woa grey, then, steady!", "문 맞은편이 제자리인 잿빛 말은, 문이 열릴 때면 안달이 나서 고삐를 절그럭거리며 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 그토록 애타게 고개를 돌리는데, 문을 연 사람이 \"워, 워, 잿빛아, 가만! 오늘은 아무도 너 찾지 않아!\""], ["Noabody wants you to-day!” may know it quite as well as the man.", "하고 일러주는 그 말을, 어쩌면 사람만큼이나 잘 알아듣고 있을지 모른다."], ["The whole seemingly monotonous and uncompanionable half-dozen, stabled together, may pass the long wet hours when the door is shut in livelier communication than is held in the servants’ hall or at the Dedlock Arms, or may even beguile the time by improving (perhaps corrupting) the pony in the loose-box in the corner.", "함께 마구간에 매인, 겉보기엔 단조롭고 서로 데면데면한 듯한 여섯 마리는, 문이 닫혀 있는 비 오는 긴 시간 동안, 하인 방이나 데드록 암스에서 오가는 것보다 훨씬 활기찬 대화를 나누고 있을지도 모르고, 심지어 구석 빈 칸에 있는 조랑말을 가르치며(어쩌면 버릇을 망쳐가며) 시간을 달래고 있을지도 모른다."]], [["So the mastiff, dozing in his kennel in the court-yard with his large head on his paws, may think of the hot sunshine when the shadows of the stable-buildings tire his patience out by changing and leave him at one time of the day no broader refuge than the shadow of his own house, where he sits on end, panting and growling short, and very much wanting something to worry besides himself and his chain.", "마찬가지로, 안마당 개집에서 커다란 머리를 앞발 위에 얹고 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 마스티프도 뜨거운 햇볕을 떠올리고 있을지 모른다. 마구간 건물의 그림자가 시시각각 옮겨가며 녀석의 인내심을 바닥내고, 하루 중 어느 때면 제 집 그림자보다 더 너른 피난처도 남겨주지 않을 때, 녀석은 거기 꼿꼿이 앉아 헐떡이며 컹컹 짧게 으르렁대고는, 제 몸뚱이와 제 사슬 말고 물어뜯을 무언가가 몹시도 그리워진다."], ["So now, half-waking and all-winking, he may recall the house full of company, the coach-houses full of vehicles, the stables full of horses, and the out-buildings full of attendants upon horses, until he is undecided about the present and comes forth to see how it is.", "그래서 지금도, 반쯤 잠든 채 연신 눈을 끔벅이며, 손님으로 가득 찬 저택, 마차로 가득 찬 마차고, 말로 가득 찬 마구간, 말을 돌보는 일꾼들로 가득 찬 부속 건물을 떠올리다가, 지금이 어떤지 종잡지 못해 밖으로 나와 사정을 살핀다."], ["Then, with that impatient shake of himself, he may growl in the spirit, “Rain, rain, rain!", "그러고는 안달하듯 몸을 부르르 떨며 마음속으로 으르렁댄다. \"비, 비, 비!"], ["Nothing but rain—and no family here!”", "비밖에 없잖아, 식구라곤 하나도 없고!\""], ["as he goes in again and lies down with a gloomy yawn.", "그러고는 다시 들어가 침울하게 하품하며 드러눕는다."]], [["So with the dogs in the kennel-buildings across the park, who have their restless fits and whose doleful voices when the wind has been very obstinate have even made it known in the house itself—upstairs, downstairs, and in my Lady’s chamber.", "공원 건너편 사육장 건물의 개들도 그렇다. 녀석들도 안절부절못하는 발작이 도지고, 바람이 유난히 끈질긴 날이면 그 구슬픈 울음소리가 저택 안까지, 위층에도 아래층에도 마님의 침실에도 알려질 지경이다."], ["They may hunt the whole country-side, while the raindrops are pattering round their inactivity.", "빗방울이 그 게으른 몸뚱이들 둘레에 후드득거리는 동안, 녀석들은 마음속으로 온 동네를 누비며 사냥하고 있을지 모른다."], ["So the rabbits with their self-betraying tails, frisking in and out of holes at roots of trees, may be lively with ideas of the breezy days when their ears are blown about or of those seasons of interest when there are sweet young plants to gnaw.", "마찬가지로, 제 꼬리가 제 정체를 일러바치는 토끼들, 나무뿌리 굴을 들락날락 깡충대는 토끼들도, 귀가 바람에 나부끼던 산들바람 부는 날들이나, 갉아먹을 달콤하고 어린 새싹이 돋는 그 흥겨운 철을 떠올리며 생기가 돌지 모른다."], ["The turkey in the poultry-yard, always troubled with a class-grievance (probably Christmas), may be reminiscent of that summer morning wrongfully taken from him when he got into the lane among the felled trees, where there was a barn and barley.", "가금 마당의 칠면조는 늘 제 계급에 맺힌 한이 있는데(아마 크리스마스 탓이리라), 베어 넘긴 나무들 사이 오솔길로 빠져나가 헛간과 보리가 있던 곳에 이르렀던, 부당하게 빼앗긴 그 여름 아침을 떠올리고 있을지 모른다."], ["The discontented goose, who stoops to pass under the old gateway, twenty feet high, may gabble out, if we only knew it, a waddling preference for weather when the gateway casts its shadow on the ground.", "스무 자 높이 옛 대문 아래를 몸을 숙이고 지나는 불만 많은 거위는, 우리가 알아들을 수만 있다면, 대문이 땅에 그림자를 드리우는 날씨가 더 좋다고 뒤뚱뒤뚱 떠들어댈지도 모른다."]], [["Be this as it may, there is not much fancy otherwise stirring at Chesney Wold.", "어쨌든, 체스니 월드에서 그 밖에 별달리 일렁이는 상상이란 없다."], ["If there be a little at any odd moment, it goes, like a little noise in that old echoing place, a long way and usually leads off to ghosts and mystery.", "어쩌다 한 번 그런 것이 조금 일더라도, 그것은 메아리가 울리는 그 오래된 곳의 자그마한 소리처럼 멀리멀리 퍼져나가다가, 으레 유령과 수수께끼로 이어지고 만다."]], [["It has rained so hard and rained so long down in Lincolnshire that Mrs. Rouncewell, the old housekeeper at Chesney Wold, has several times taken off her spectacles and cleaned them to make certain that the drops were not upon the glasses.", "링컨셔에는 비가 어찌나 세차게, 어찌나 오래 쏟아졌는지, 체스니 월드의 늙은 가정부 라운스웰 부인은 몇 번이나 안경을 벗어 닦으며 빗방울이 안경알에 묻은 게 아닌지 확인했다."], ["Mrs. Rouncewell might have been sufficiently assured by hearing the rain, but that she is rather deaf, which nothing will induce her to believe.", "라운스웰 부인은 빗소리만 들어도 충분히 알 수 있었을 테지만, 귀가 좀 어두운 탓이다."], ["She is a fine old lady, handsome, stately, wonderfully neat, and has such a back and such a stomacher that if her stays should turn out when she dies to have been a broad old-fashioned family fire-grate, nobody who knows her would have cause to be surprised.", "본인은 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사실을 인정하려 들지 않는다. 부인은 훌륭한 노부인으로, 미모가 있고, 위엄이 있으며, 놀랍도록 단정한 데다, 등허리며 가슴받이가 어찌나 꼿꼿한지, 죽은 뒤 그 코르셋이 알고 보니 구식 집안에서 쓰던 넓적한 무쇠 벽난로 살이었다 한들, 부인을 아는 누구도 놀랄 까닭이 없을 정도다."], ["Weather affects Mrs. Rouncewell little.", "날씨는 라운스웰 부인에게 별 영향을 못 미친다."], ["The house is there in all weathers, and the house, as she expresses it, “is what she looks at.”", "저택은 어떤 날씨에도 그 자리에 있고, 부인 표현대로 저택이야말로 \"부인이 바라보는 것\"이다."], ["She sits in her room (in a side passage on the ground floor, with an arched window commanding a smooth quadrangle, adorned at regular intervals with smooth round trees and smooth round blocks of stone, as if the trees were going to play at bowls with the stones), and the whole house reposes on her mind.", "부인은 자기 방에 앉아 있는데(일층 옆 복도에 있는 그 방은 아치형 창이 매끈한 사각 안뜰을 내려다보고, 그 안뜰에는 매끈하고 둥근 나무들과 매끈하고 둥근 돌덩이들이 일정한 간격으로 늘어서 있어, 마치 나무들이 돌을 가지고 볼링이라도 할 참인 듯하다), 저택 전체가 부인의 마음 위에 고요히 깃들어 있다."], ["She can open it on occasion and be busy and fluttered, but it is shut up now and lies on the breadth of Mrs. Rouncewell’s iron-bound bosom in a majestic sleep.", "부인은 필요할 때 저택을 열어젖히고 분주히 호들갑을 떨 수도 있지만, 지금은 닫혀 있어, 라운스웰 부인의 무쇠로 두른 듯한 너른 가슴팍 위에서 장엄한 잠에 빠져 있다."]], [["It is the next difficult thing to an impossibility to imagine Chesney Wold without Mrs. Rouncewell, but she has only been here fifty years.", "라운스웰 부인 없는 체스니 월드를 떠올린다는 것은 거의 불가능에 가까운 일이지만, 부인이 이곳에 있은 지는 고작 오십 년이다."], ["Ask her how long, this rainy day, and she shall answer “fifty year, three months, and a fortnight, by the blessing of heaven, if I live till Tuesday.”", "비 내리는 오늘 그 세월을 물으면, 부인은 이렇게 답하리라. \"하늘의 가호로 화요일까지 살아 있다면, 오십 년 석 달 하고도 보름이지요.\""], ["Mr. Rouncewell died some time before the decease of the pretty fashion of pig-tails, and modestly hid his own (if he took it with him) in a corner of the churchyard in the park near the mouldy porch.", "라운스웰 씨는 변발이라는 예쁘장한 유행이 사라지기 얼마 전에 세상을 떠났고, 제 변발은(저세상까지 가지고 갔다면 모를까) 공원 안 묘지의, 이끼 낀 현관 곁 한구석에 다소곳이 묻어두었다."], ["He was born in the market-town, and so was his young widow.", "그는 장이 서는 읍내에서 태어났고, 그의 젊은 미망인도 그러했다."], ["Her progress in the family began in the time of the last Sir Leicester and originated in the still-room.", "부인이 이 집안에서 자리를 잡기 시작한 것은 선대 레스터 경 시절이었고, 그 시작은 식료품 저장실이었다."]], [["The present representative of the Dedlocks is an excellent master.", "지금의 데드록가 당주는 더없이 훌륭한 주인이다."], ["He supposes all his dependents to be utterly bereft of individual characters, intentions, or opinions, and is persuaded that he was born to supersede the necessity of their having any.", "그는 제게 딸린 모든 식솔이 개개의 인격이나 의향이나 의견이라곤 깡그리 없는 존재라 여기며, 자신이 바로 그것들을 가질 필요를 대신하려고 태어난 사람이라 굳게 믿는다."], ["If he were to make a discovery to the contrary, he would be simply stunned—would never recover himself, most likely, except to gasp and die.", "만에 하나 그와 반대되는 사실을 알게 된다면, 그는 그저 얼이 빠져버릴 것이다. 십중팔구 다시는 정신을 못 차리고, 헐떡이다 숨이나 거두는 것 말고는 회복할 길이 없으리라."], ["But he is an excellent master still, holding it a part of his state to be so.", "그래도 그는 여전히 훌륭한 주인이며, 그렇게 처신하는 것이 제 신분의 한 몫이라 여긴다."], ["He has a great liking for Mrs.", "그는 라운스웰 부인을 매우 아낀다."], ["Rouncewell; he says she is a most respectable, creditable woman.", "부인이야말로 더없이 존경할 만하고 믿음직한 여인이라고 말한다. 그는 체스니 월드에 내려올 때나 떠날 때나 늘 부인과 악수를 나눈다."], ["He always shakes hands with her when he comes down to Chesney Wold and when he goes away; and if he were very ill, or if he were knocked down by accident, or run over, or placed in any situation expressive of a Dedlock at a disadvantage, he would say if he could speak, “Leave me, and send Mrs.", "그리고 만일 그가 중병에 걸리거나, 사고로 쓰러지거나, 마차에 깔리거나, 데드록가 사람이 불리한 처지에 놓였음을 드러낼 어떤 상황에 빠진다면, 말을 할 수만 있다면 이렇게 말하리라. \"다들 물러가고, 여기 라운스웰 부인을 불러오게!\""], ["Rouncewell here!” feeling his dignity, at such a pass, safer with her than with anybody else.", "그런 고비에서는 제 위엄이 다른 누구보다 부인 곁에서 더 안전하다고 느끼는 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Rouncewell has known trouble.", "라운스웰 부인은 시름을 겪어보았다."], ["She has had two sons, of whom the younger ran wild, and went for a soldier, and never came back. Even to this hour, Mrs.", "부인에게는 아들이 둘 있었는데, 그중 작은아들은 빗나가서 군인이 되어 떠난 뒤 영영 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Rouncewell’s calm hands lose their composure when she speaks of him, and unfolding themselves from her stomacher, hover about her in an agitated manner as she says what a likely lad, what a fine lad, what a gay, good-humoured, clever lad he was!", "지금 이 순간까지도, 라운스웰 부인의 차분하던 두 손은 그 아들 이야기가 나오면 평정을 잃고, 가슴받이에서 풀려나 안절부절못하며 부인 둘레를 맴돈다. 그러면서 부인은 그 아이가 얼마나 싹수 있는 아이였는지, 얼마나 훤칠한 아이였는지, 얼마나 명랑하고 사람 좋고 영리한 아이였는지 늘어놓는다!"], ["Her second son would have been provided for at Chesney Wold and would have been made steward in due season, but he took, when he was a schoolboy, to constructing steam-engines out of saucepans and setting birds to draw their own water with the least possible amount of labour, so assisting them with artful contrivance of hydraulic pressure that a thirsty canary had only, in a literal sense, to put his shoulder to the wheel and the job was done.", "둘째 아들은 체스니 월드에서 앞날을 보장받아 때가 되면 집사가 되었을 터였다. 그러나 그는 학생 시절부터 냄비로 증기기관을 만들고, 새들이 되도록 힘 안 들이고 제가 마실 물을 길어 올리도록 길들이는 데 빠져들었으니, 교묘한 수압 장치로 도와준 덕에 목마른 카나리아는 말 그대로 어깨로 바퀴를 밀기만 하면 일이 끝나는 식이었다."], ["This propensity gave Mrs. Rouncewell great uneasiness.", "이런 성벽은 라운스웰 부인의 속을 몹시 태웠다."], ["She felt it with a mother’s anguish to be a move in the Wat Tyler direction, well knowing that Sir Leicester had that general impression of an aptitude for any art to which smoke and a tall chimney might be considered essential.", "부인은 그것이 와트 타일러 쪽으로 한 걸음 내딛는 일이라고 어머니다운 비통함으로 느꼈으니, 레스터 경이 연기와 높다란 굴뚝이 따라붙는 모든 기예에 능한 자들에 대해 그러한 막연한 인상을 품고 있음을 잘 알았던 것이다."], ["But the doomed young rebel (otherwise a mild youth, and very persevering), showing no sign of grace as he got older but, on the contrary, constructing a model of a power-loom, she was fain, with many tears, to mention his backslidings to the baronet.", "그러나 그 가엾은 어린 반역자는(그 밖으로는 온순한 데다 무척 끈기 있는 젊은이였다) 나이가 들어도 마음을 고쳐먹을 기미를 보이기는커녕, 도리어 역직기 모형을 만들어내자, 부인은 눈물깨나 흘리며 그 비뚤어진 행실을 준남작에게 아뢰지 않을 수 없었다. \"라운스웰 부인,\""], ["“Mrs. Rouncewell,”", "레스터 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir Leicester, “I can never consent to argue, as you know, with any one on any subject.", "\"알다시피 나는 어떤 주제로도 누구와 입씨름하는 데 도무지 응할 수 없는 사람이오."], ["You had better get rid of your boy; you had better get him into some Works.", "그 아이는 떼어내는 편이 낫겠소. 어디 공장에라도 들여보내는 게 좋겠소."], ["The iron country farther north is, I suppose, the congenial direction for a boy with these tendencies.”", "이런 기질을 타고난 아이한테는 북쪽으로 더 올라간 철공 지대가 맞춤한 방향일 듯하오.\" 그리하여 그는 더 북쪽으로 갔고, 더 북쪽에서 자라났다."], ["Farther north he went, and farther north he grew up; and if Sir Leicester Dedlock ever saw him when he came to Chesney Wold to visit his mother, or ever thought of him afterwards, it is certain that he only regarded him as one of a body of some odd thousand conspirators, swarthy and grim, who were in the habit of turning out by torchlight two or three nights in the week for unlawful purposes.", "레스터 데드록 경이 어머니를 뵈러 체스니 월드에 온 그를 행여 마주쳤다거나, 그 뒤로 한 번이라도 그를 떠올린 적이 있다면, 분명 그를 한 무리의 음험하고 험상궂은 음모자 수천 명 가운데 하나로만 여겼으리라. 한 주에 두세 밤씩 횃불을 들고 불법한 목적으로 떼 지어 나서는 무리 말이다."]], [["Nevertheless, Mrs. Rouncewell’s son has, in the course of nature and art, grown up, and established himself, and married, and called unto him Mrs.", "그래도 라운스웰 부인의 아들은 천성과 기예의 도움으로 무사히 자라나, 제 자리를 잡고, 장가를 들고, 라운스웰 부인의 손자를 낳았다."], ["Rouncewell’s grandson, who, being out of his apprenticeship, and home from a journey in far countries, whither he was sent to enlarge his knowledge and complete his preparations for the venture of this life, stands leaning against the chimney-piece this very day in Mrs. Rouncewell’s room at Chesney Wold.", "그 손자는 견습 과정을 마치고, 견문을 넓혀 세상살이라는 모험을 준비하는 일을 완성하라며 보내진 먼 나라들에서 막 여행을 마치고 돌아와, 바로 오늘 체스니 월드의 라운스웰 부인 방에서 벽난로 선반에 기대어 서 있다."]], [["“And, again and again, I am glad to see you, Watt!", "\"몇 번을 봐도, 와트, 널 보니 반갑구나!"], ["And, once again, I am glad to see you, Watt!”", "다시 봐도, 와트, 널 보니 반가워!\""], ["says Mrs. Rouncewell.", "라운스웰 부인이 말한다."], ["“You are a fine young fellow.", "\"참 훤칠한 젊은이가 됐어."], ["You are like your poor uncle George.", "가엾은 네 삼촌 조지를 빼닮았구나."], ["Ah!”", "아!\""], ["Mrs. Rouncewell’s hands unquiet, as usual, on this reference.", "라운스웰 부인의 손이, 늘 그렇듯 그 이름을 입에 올리자 안절부절못한다."]], [["“They say I am like my father, grandmother.”", "\"다들 제가 아버지를 닮았다고 하던데요, 할머니.\""]], [["“Like him, also, my dear—but most like your poor uncle George!", "\"아버지도 닮았지, 얘야. 하지만 가엾은 네 삼촌 조지를 가장 닮았어!"], ["And your dear father.”", "그리고 네 어진 아버지도.\""], ["Mrs. Rouncewell folds her hands again.", "라운스웰 부인이 다시 두 손을 모은다."], ["“He is well?”", "\"네 아버지는 잘 있느냐?\""]], [["“Thriving, grandmother, in every way.”", "\"무엇 하나 빠짐없이 번창하고 있어요, 할머니.\""]], [["“I am thankful!” Mrs.", "\"고마운 일이지!\""], ["Rouncewell is fond of her son but has a plaintive feeling towards him, much as if he were a very honourable soldier who had gone over to the enemy.", "라운스웰 부인은 아들을 사랑하지만, 마치 그가 적진으로 넘어가버린 더없이 명예로운 군인이기라도 한 양, 그를 향해 애잔한 마음을 품고 있다."]], [["“He is quite happy?”", "\"아주 행복하다더냐?\""], ["says she.", "부인이 묻는다."]], [["“Quite.”", "\"아주요.\""]], [["“I am thankful!", "\"고마운 일이야!"], ["So he has brought you up to follow in his ways and has sent you into foreign countries and the like?", "그래, 그래서 네 아버지가 너를 제 길을 따르도록 키워서, 외국이며 이런저런 곳으로 보낸 게로구나?"], ["Well, he knows best.", "그래, 그 사람이 제일 잘 알겠지."], ["There may be a world beyond Chesney Wold that I don’t understand.", "체스니 월드 너머에 내가 모르는 세상이 있을 수도 있지."], ["Though I am not young, either.", "나라고 젊은 것도 아니고."], ["And I have seen a quantity of good company too!”", "게다가 나도 훌륭한 분들을 어지간히 많이 겪어봤다만!\""]], [["“Grandmother,”", "\"할머니,\""], ["says the young man, changing the subject, “what a very pretty girl that was I found with you just now.", "젊은이가 화제를 돌리며 말한다. \"방금 할머니 곁에서 만난 그 아가씨, 참 어여쁘던데요."], ["You called her Rosa?”", "로자라고 부르시던가요?\""]], [["“Yes, child.", "\"그래, 얘야."], ["She is daughter of a widow in the village.", "마을 어느 미망인의 딸이란다."], ["Maids are so hard to teach, now-a-days, that I have put her about me young.", "요즘은 하녀 가르치기가 어찌나 힘든지, 어려서부터 내 곁에 두었지."], ["She’s an apt scholar and will do well.", "잘 배우는 아이라 앞날이 트일 게다."], ["She shows the house already, very pretty.", "벌써 저택 안내도 아주 곱게 해낸단다."], ["She lives with me at my table here.”", "여기 내 식탁에서 나랑 함께 지내지.\""]], [["“I hope I have not driven her away?”", "\"제가 그 아가씨를 쫓아버린 건 아닌가 모르겠네요?\""]], [["“She supposes we have family affairs to speak about, I dare say.", "\"우리가 집안일을 의논할 거라 여겼겠지, 아마."], ["She is very modest.", "무척 얌전한 아이야."], ["It is a fine quality in a young woman.", "젊은 여자한테는 좋은 자질이지."], ["And scarcer,” says Mrs.", "게다가 예전보다,\""], ["Rouncewell, expanding her stomacher to its utmost limits, “than it formerly was!”", "라운스웰 부인이 가슴받이를 한껏 부풀리며 말한다. \"더 귀해진 자질이고 말이다!\""]], [["The young man inclines his head in acknowledgment of the precepts of experience.", "젊은이는 경험에서 우러난 가르침을 받든다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙인다."], ["Mrs. Rouncewell listens.", "라운스웰 부인이 귀를 기울인다."]], [["“Wheels!” says she.", "\"바퀴 소리!\""], ["They have long been audible to the younger ears of her companion.", "부인이 말한다. 곁에 있는 젊은이의 더 젊은 귀에는 진작 들려오던 소리다."], ["“What wheels on such a day as this, for gracious sake?”", "\"이런 날에 웬 바퀴 소리람, 세상에?\""]], [["After a short interval, a tap at the door.", "잠깐 사이를 두고 문 두드리는 소리가 난다."], ["“Come in!”", "\"들어와라!\""], ["A dark-eyed, dark-haired, shy, village beauty comes in—so fresh in her rosy and yet delicate bloom that the drops of rain which have beaten on her hair look like the dew upon a flower fresh gathered.", "검은 눈에 검은 머리, 수줍은 시골 미인이 들어온다. 발그레하면서도 고운 그 생기가 어찌나 싱싱한지, 머리칼에 들이친 빗방울이 갓 꺾은 꽃에 맺힌 이슬 같다."]], [["“What company is this, Rosa?”", "\"무슨 손님이냐, 로자?\""], ["says Mrs. Rouncewell.", "라운스웰 부인이 묻는다."]], [["“It’s two young men in a gig, ma’am, who want to see the house—yes, and if you please, I told them so!”", "\"이륜마차에 탄 젊은 분 둘인데, 저택을 둘러보고 싶다고 해요, 마님. 네, 외람되지만, 제가 그렇게 일렀어요!\""], ["in quick reply to a gesture of dissent from the housekeeper.", "가정부가 안 된다는 손짓을 하자 로자가 얼른 덧붙인다."], ["“I went to the hall-door and told them it was the wrong day and the wrong hour, but the young man who was driving took off his hat in the wet and begged me to bring this card to you.”", "\"현관문으로 나가서, 날도 안 맞고 시간도 안 맞는다고 했어요. 그랬더니 마차를 몰던 젊은 분이 빗속에서 모자를 벗고는, 이 명함을 마님께 갖다드려 달라고 사정사정하지 뭐예요.\""]], [["“Read it, my dear Watt,”", "\"네가 읽어보렴, 와트야.\""], ["says the housekeeper.", "가정부가 말한다."]], [["Rosa is so shy as she gives it to him that they drop it between them and almost knock their foreheads together as they pick it up.", "로자가 어찌나 수줍어하며 명함을 건네는지, 둘 사이에서 그것을 떨어뜨려, 함께 주우려다 하마터면 이마를 부딪칠 뻔한다."], ["Rosa is shyer than before.", "로자는 아까보다 더 수줍어진다."]], [["“Mr. Guppy” is all the information the card yields.", "명함이 알려주는 정보라고는 \"거피 씨\"가 전부다."]], [["“Guppy!”", "\"거피!\""], ["repeats Mrs.", "라운스웰 부인이 되뇐다."], ["Rouncewell, “MR.", "\"거피 씨라!"], ["Guppy! Nonsense, I never heard of him!”", "별일이군, 들어본 적도 없는 사람인데!\""]], [["“If you please, he told ME that!” says Rosa.", "\"외람되지만, 그분도 저한테 그렇게 말했어요!\""], ["“But he said that he and the other young gentleman came from London only last night by the mail, on business at the magistrates’ meeting, ten miles off, this morning, and that as their business was soon over, and they had heard a great deal said of Chesney Wold, and really didn’t know what to do with themselves, they had come through the wet to see it.", "로자가 말한다. \"그런데 자기랑 다른 젊은 신사분이 어젯밤에야 우편마차로 런던에서 내려왔다고 했어요."], ["They are lawyers. He says he is not in Mr.", "오늘 아침 십 마일 떨어진 데서 열리는 치안판사 회합에 볼일이 있어서요."], ["Tulkinghorn’s office, but he is sure he may make use of Mr.", "그런데 그 볼일이 금세 끝났는데, 체스니 월드 이야기를 익히 들어온 데다 도무지 무얼 하며 시간을 보내야 할지 몰라서, 빗속을 뚫고 구경하러 왔다네요."], ["Tulkinghorn’s name if necessary.”", "변호사들이래요."], ["Finding, now she leaves off, that she has been making quite a long speech, Rosa is shyer than ever.", "자기는 토킹혼 씨 사무소 사람은 아니지만, 필요하다면 토킹혼 씨 이름을 대도 괜찮을 거라고 했어요.\" 말을 마치고 보니 자기가 꽤나 긴 이야기를 늘어놓았음을 깨닫고, 로자는 그 어느 때보다 수줍어진다."]], [["Now, Mr. Tulkinghorn is, in a manner, part and parcel of the place, and besides, is supposed to have made Mrs.", "그런데 토킹혼 씨로 말하자면, 어떤 의미에서는 이 저택과 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 사람이며, 게다가 라운스웰 부인의 유언장을 작성해준 사람으로 통한다."], ["Rouncewell’s will. The old lady relaxes, consents to the admission of the visitors as a favour, and dismisses Rosa.", "노부인은 마음을 누그러뜨려, 호의를 베푸는 셈치고 방문객들을 들이기로 하고, 로자를 내보낸다."], ["The grandson, however, being smitten by a sudden wish to see the house himself, proposes to join the party.", "그런데 손자가 문득 저도 저택을 둘러보고 싶은 마음이 동해, 일행에 끼겠다고 한다."], ["The grandmother, who is pleased that he should have that interest, accompanies him—though to do him justice, he is exceedingly unwilling to trouble her.", "손자가 그런 관심을 보이는 것이 기쁜 할머니는 그를 따라나선다. 정작 손자는, 공평하게 말하자면, 할머니를 번거롭게 하기를 몹시 꺼리는데도 말이다."]], [["“Much obliged to you, ma’am!”", "\"고맙습니다, 마님!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, divesting himself of his wet dreadnought in the hall.", "거피 씨가 현관에서 비에 젖은 두꺼운 외투를 벗으며 말한다."], ["“Us London lawyers don’t often get an out, and when we do, we like to make the most of it, you know.”", "\"저희 런던 변호사들이야 바깥나들이가 흔치 않으니, 어쩌다 나오면 본전을 톡톡히 뽑고 싶거든요, 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["The old housekeeper, with a gracious severity of deportment, waves her hand towards the great staircase.", "늙은 가정부는 점잖으면서도 근엄한 몸가짐으로 거대한 계단을 향해 손짓한다."], ["Mr. Guppy and his friend follow Rosa; Mrs.", "거피 씨와 그 친구가 로자를 따른다."], ["Rouncewell and her grandson follow them; a young gardener goes before to open the shutters.", "라운스웰 부인과 손자가 그들을 따른다. 젊은 정원사 하나가 앞장서서 덧문을 연다."]], [["As is usually the case with people who go over houses, Mr. Guppy and his friend are dead beat before they have well begun.", "저택을 둘러보러 다니는 이들이 으레 그렇듯, 거피 씨와 그 친구는 제대로 시작하기도 전에 녹초가 된다."], ["They straggle about in wrong places, look at wrong things, don’t care for the right things, gape when more rooms are opened, exhibit profound depression of spirits, and are clearly knocked up.", "둘은 엉뚱한 곳에서 어슬렁대고, 엉뚱한 것을 바라보며, 정작 봐야 할 것에는 무심하고, 새 방이 열릴 때마다 하품을 하고, 깊은 의기소침을 드러내며, 누가 봐도 진이 빠져 있다."], ["In each successive chamber that they enter, Mrs. Rouncewell, who is as upright as the house itself, rests apart in a window-seat or other such nook and listens with stately approval to Rosa’s exposition.", "그들이 차례로 들어서는 방마다, 저택 못지않게 꼿꼿한 라운스웰 부인은 창가 자리나 그런 구석에 따로 떨어져 앉아, 로자의 설명에 위엄 있는 만족의 빛을 띠고 귀를 기울인다."], ["Her grandson is so attentive to it that Rosa is shyer than ever—and prettier.", "손자가 어찌나 그 설명에 열중하는지, 로자는 그 어느 때보다 수줍어진다. 그리고 그 어느 때보다 어여뻐진다."], ["Thus they pass on from room to room, raising the pictured Dedlocks for a few brief minutes as the young gardener admits the light, and reconsigning them to their graves as he shuts it out again. It appears to the afflicted Mr.", "그렇게 그들은 방에서 방으로 옮겨 다니며, 젊은 정원사가 빛을 들일 때면 그림 속 데드록가 사람들을 잠깐씩 되살려냈다가, 그가 다시 빛을 가리면 그들을 무덤으로 돌려보낸다."], ["Guppy and his inconsolable friend that there is no end to the Dedlocks, whose family greatness seems to consist in their never having done anything to distinguish themselves for seven hundred years.", "시달릴 대로 시달린 거피 씨와 위로할 길 없는 그 친구에게는, 데드록가 사람들이 끝도 없이 이어지는 것만 같다. 그 집안의 위대함이란 칠백 년 동안 이렇다 할 일이라곤 도무지 한 적이 없다는 데 있는 듯하다."]], [["Even the long drawing-room of Chesney Wold cannot revive Mr. Guppy’s spirits.", "체스니 월드의 그 기다란 응접실조차 거피 씨의 기운을 되살리지 못한다."], ["He is so low that he droops on the threshold and has hardly strength of mind to enter.", "그는 어찌나 풀이 죽었는지 문턱에서 고개를 떨구고, 안으로 들어설 기력조차 거의 없다."], ["But a portrait over the chimney-piece, painted by the fashionable artist of the day, acts upon him like a charm.", "그런데 벽난로 선반 위에 걸린 초상화 한 점이, 당대의 유행 화가가 그린 그 그림이, 마법처럼 그에게 작용한다."], ["He recovers in a moment.", "그는 순식간에 기운을 차린다."], ["He stares at it with uncommon interest; he seems to be fixed and fascinated by it.", "그는 예사롭지 않은 관심으로 그것을 뚫어지게 본다. 그것에 못 박힌 듯, 홀린 듯하다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"이런!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“Who’s that?”", "\"저분은 누구죠?\""]], [["“The picture over the fire-place,”", "\"벽난로 위 그림은,\""], ["says Rosa, “is the portrait of the present Lady Dedlock.", "로자가 말한다. \"지금의 데드록 마님 초상화예요."], ["It is considered a perfect likeness, and the best work of the master.”", "완벽하게 닮았다는 평을 듣고, 화가의 최고 걸작으로 꼽힌답니다.\""]], [["“Blest,” says Mr.", "\"맙소사,\""], ["Guppy, staring in a kind of dismay at his friend, “if I can ever have seen her.", "거피 씨가 일종의 당혹감에 휩싸여 친구를 바라보며 말한다. \"내가 저분을 본 적이 있을 리가 없는데."], ["Yet I know her!", "그런데도 아는 얼굴이야!"], ["Has the picture been engraved, miss?”", "이 그림이 판화로 새겨진 적이 있나요, 아가씨?\""]], [["“The picture has never been engraved.", "\"그림이 판화로 새겨진 적은 없어요."], ["Sir Leicester has always refused permission.”", "레스터 경께서 늘 허락하지 않으셨거든요.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"이거 참!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy in a low voice.", "거피 씨가 나직한 목소리로 말한다."], ["“I’ll be shot if it ain’t very curious how well I know that picture!", "\"내가 저 그림을 이렇게 잘 아는 게 어찌나 묘한지, 정말이지 알다가도 모르겠네!"], ["So that’s Lady Dedlock, is it!”", "그래, 저분이 데드록 마님이라 이거지!\""]], [["“The picture on the right is the present Sir Leicester Dedlock.", "\"오른쪽 그림은 지금의 레스터 데드록 경이세요."], ["The picture on the left is his father, the late Sir Leicester.”", "왼쪽 그림은 그분의 아버님, 돌아가신 레스터 경이고요.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy has no eyes for either of these magnates.", "거피 씨에게는 이 두 대인의 모습이 눈에 들어오지도 않는다."], ["“It’s unaccountable to me,”", "\"도무지 영문을 모르겠어요,\""], ["he says, still staring at the portrait, “how well I know that picture!", "그는 여전히 초상화를 응시하며 말한다. \"내가 저 그림을 이토록 잘 알다니!"], ["I’m dashed,” adds Mr.", "정말이지,\""], ["Guppy, looking round, “if I don’t think I must have had a dream of that picture, you know!”", "거피 씨가 둘레를 둘러보며 덧붙인다. \"꿈에서 저 그림을 본 게 틀림없는 것 같단 말이죠, 아시겠어요!\""]], [["As no one present takes any especial interest in Mr.", "거피 씨의 꿈 따위에 특별히 관심을 두는 사람이 그 자리에 없으니, 그 추측은 더 캐묻지 않는다."], ["Guppy’s dreams, the probability is not pursued.", "그러나 그는 여전히 초상화에 어찌나 빠져 있는지, 젊은 정원사가 덧문을 닫을 때까지 그 앞에서 꼼짝도 않고 서 있다."], ["But he still remains so absorbed by the portrait that he stands immovable before it until the young gardener has closed the shutters, when he comes out of the room in a dazed state that is an odd though a sufficient substitute for interest and follows into the succeeding rooms with a confused stare, as if he were looking everywhere for Lady Dedlock again.", "그러다 멍한 상태로 방을 나서는데, 그 멍한 꼴이 관심이라기엔 묘하지만 그럭저럭 관심을 대신하기는 한다. 그러고는 어리둥절한 눈길로 다음 방들로 따라 들어가는데, 마치 사방에서 데드록 마님을 다시 찾으려는 듯하다."]], [["He sees no more of her.", "그는 더는 마님을 보지 못한다."], ["He sees her rooms, which are the last shown, as being very elegant, and he looks out of the windows from which she looked out, not long ago, upon the weather that bored her to death.", "그는 마지막으로 안내되는 마님의 방들이 무척 우아하다고 보고, 마님이 얼마 전 자신을 죽도록 따분하게 만든 그 날씨를 내다보던 바로 그 창으로 밖을 내다본다."], ["All things have an end, even houses that people take infinite pains to see and are tired of before they begin to see them.", "만물에는 끝이 있는 법, 사람들이 무진 애를 써가며 구경하러 왔다가 채 보기도 전에 싫증을 내는 저택조차 그러하다. 그는 구경의 끝에 다다랐고, 싱싱한 시골 미인은 설명의 끝에 다다랐다."], ["He has come to the end of the sight, and the fresh village beauty to the end of her description; which is always this: “The terrace below is much admired.", "그 끝맺음은 늘 이러하다. \"아래쪽 테라스는 많은 분이 감탄하시는 곳이에요."], ["It is called, from an old story in the family, the Ghost’s Walk.”", "집안에 전해 내려오는 옛이야기에서 따와, 유령의 길이라 부른답니다.\""]], [["“No?”", "\"설마?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, greedily curious.", "거피 씨가 게걸스러운 호기심으로 묻는다."], ["“What’s the story, miss?", "\"무슨 이야긴데요, 아가씨?"], ["Is it anything about a picture?”", "혹시 그림과 관련된 이야긴가요?\""]], [["“Pray tell us the story,”", "\"그 이야기 좀 들려주세요.\""], ["says Watt in a half whisper.", "와트가 반쯤 속삭이듯 말한다."]], [["“I don’t know it, sir.”", "\"저는 모르는걸요, 도련님.\""], ["Rosa is shyer than ever.", "로자는 그 어느 때보다 수줍어한다."]], [["“It is not related to visitors; it is almost forgotten,”", "\"방문객들에게 들려주는 이야기는 아니랍니다. 이젠 거의 잊혔지요.\""], ["says the housekeeper, advancing.", "가정부가 앞으로 나서며 말한다."], ["“It has never been more than a family anecdote.”", "\"그저 집안에 전해 내려오는 일화에 지나지 않아요.\""]], [["“You’ll excuse my asking again if it has anything to do with a picture, ma’am,”", "\"거듭 여쭙는 걸 너그러이 봐주십시오만, 혹시 그림과 무슨 관련이 있습니까, 마님?\""], ["observes Mr.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["Guppy, “because I do assure you that the more I think of that picture the better I know it, without knowing how I know it!”", "\"정말이지, 저 그림을 생각하면 할수록 더 잘 아는 것만 같은데, 어떻게 아는지는 도무지 모르겠으니 드리는 말씀입니다!\""]], [["The story has nothing to do with a picture; the housekeeper can guarantee that.", "그 이야기는 그림과 아무 관련이 없다. 가정부가 그것은 장담할 수 있다."], ["Mr. Guppy is obliged to her for the information and is, moreover, generally obliged.", "거피 씨는 알려주어 고맙다고 하고, 게다가 두루 신세를 졌다고 한다."], ["He retires with his friend, guided down another staircase by the young gardener, and presently is heard to drive away.", "그는 친구와 함께 물러나, 젊은 정원사의 안내를 받아 다른 계단으로 내려가고, 이윽고 마차를 몰아 떠나는 소리가 들린다."], ["It is now dusk. Mrs.", "어느덧 땅거미가 진다."], ["Rouncewell can trust to the discretion of her two young hearers and may tell THEM how the terrace came to have that ghostly name.", "라운스웰 부인은 자기 곁 두 젊은 청자의 입이 무겁다는 것을 믿을 수 있으니, 그 테라스가 어쩌다 그 유령 같은 이름을 얻게 되었는지 이들에게 들려줄 수 있다."]], [["She seats herself in a large chair by the fast-darkening window and tells them: “In the wicked days, my dears, of King Charles the First—I mean, of course, in the wicked days of the rebels who leagued themselves against that excellent king—Sir Morbury Dedlock was the owner of Chesney Wold.", "부인은 빠르게 어두워지는 창가의 커다란 의자에 자리를 잡고 이야기를 들려준다. \"찰스 일세 임금님의 그 사악한 시절에, 얘들아."], ["Whether there was any account of a ghost in the family before those days, I can’t say.", "물론 내 말은, 그 훌륭하신 임금님에 맞서 작당한 반역도들의 그 사악한 시절을 두고 하는 말이다만. 모버리 데드록 경이 체스니 월드의 주인이셨지."], ["I should think it very likely indeed.”", "그 시절 이전에 이 집안에 유령 이야기가 있었는지는 나도 모르겠구나. 십중팔구 있었을 성싶다만.\""]], [["Mrs. Rouncewell holds this opinion because she considers that a family of such antiquity and importance has a right to a ghost.", "라운스웰 부인이 이런 생각을 품는 까닭은, 그토록 유서 깊고 지체 높은 집안이라면 마땅히 유령 하나쯤 가질 권리가 있다고 여기기 때문이다."], ["She regards a ghost as one of the privileges of the upper classes, a genteel distinction to which the common people have no claim.", "부인은 유령을 상류층의 특권 가운데 하나로, 평민은 감히 넘볼 수 없는 고상한 표지로 여긴다."]], [["“Sir Morbury Dedlock,” says Mrs.", "\"모버리 데드록 경께서는,\""], ["Rouncewell, “was, I have no occasion to say, on the side of the blessed martyr.", "라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"말할 것도 없이 그 거룩한 순교자 편이셨다."], ["But it IS supposed that his Lady, who had none of the family blood in her veins, favoured the bad cause.", "그런데 핏줄에 집안의 피라곤 한 방울도 섞이지 않은 그분의 마님은, 그릇된 편을 두둔하셨다고들 해."], ["It is said that she had relations among King Charles’s enemies, that she was in correspondence with them, and that she gave them information.", "듣자 하니 마님은 찰스 임금님의 적들 가운데 친척이 있어, 그들과 편지를 주고받으며 정보를 넘겼다는 게야."], ["When any of the country gentlemen who followed his Majesty’s cause met here, it is said that my Lady was always nearer to the door of their council-room than they supposed.", "임금님의 대의를 따르는 이 고장 양반들이 이곳에 모일 때면, 마님이 늘 그분들 생각보다 회합 방문 가까이 다가가 계셨다더구나."], ["Do you hear a sound like a footstep passing along the terrace, Watt?”", "와트야, 테라스를 따라 지나가는 발소리 같은 게 들리지 않니?\""]], [["Rosa draws nearer to the housekeeper.", "로자가 가정부 곁으로 바짝 다가선다."]], [["“I hear the rain-drip on the stones,”", "\"빗방울이 돌 위에 떨어지는 소리는 들려요,\""], ["replies the young man, “and I hear a curious echo—I suppose an echo—which is very like a halting step.”", "젊은이가 답한다. \"그리고 묘한 메아리도 들리는데요, 메아리겠지요, 절뚝거리는 발소리랑 꼭 닮았어요.\""]], [["The housekeeper gravely nods and continues: “Partly on account of this division between them, and partly on other accounts, Sir Morbury and his Lady led a troubled life.", "가정부가 무겁게 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 잇는다. \"한편으로는 두 분 사이의 이런 갈라섬 때문에, 또 한편으로는 다른 까닭들로, 모버리 경과 마님은 풍파 많은 삶을 사셨다."], ["She was a lady of a haughty temper.", "마님은 거만한 성미를 지닌 분이셨지."], ["They were not well suited to each other in age or character, and they had no children to moderate between them.", "두 분은 나이로 보나 성품으로 보나 잘 어울리지 못했고, 사이를 누그러뜨려줄 자식도 없었어."], ["After her favourite brother, a young gentleman, was killed in the civil wars (by Sir Morbury’s near kinsman), her feeling was so violent that she hated the race into which she had married.", "마님이 끔찍이 아끼던 오라버니, 젊은 양반이 내전에서 목숨을 잃은 뒤로(모버리 경의 가까운 친척 손에 그리되었지), 마님의 원한이 어찌나 사무쳤던지 시집온 그 가문 자체를 미워하셨다."], ["When the Dedlocks were about to ride out from Chesney Wold in the king’s cause, she is supposed to have more than once stolen down into the stables in the dead of night and lamed their horses; and the story is that once at such an hour, her husband saw her gliding down the stairs and followed her into the stall where his own favourite horse stood.", "데드록가 사람들이 임금님의 대의를 받들어 체스니 월드를 떠나 말을 달리려 할 때면, 마님이 한 번 이상 한밤중에 마구간으로 몰래 내려가 말들의 다리를 절게 만들었다더구나. 전해 오는 이야기로는, 한번은 그런 시각에 남편 되시는 분이 마님이 계단을 미끄러지듯 내려가는 모습을 보고, 자기가 아끼는 말이 있는 칸까지 뒤따라 들어갔다는 게야."], ["There he seized her by the wrist, and in a struggle or in a fall or through the horse being frightened and lashing out, she was lamed in the hip and from that hour began to pine away.”", "거기서 그분이 마님의 손목을 붙잡았는데, 실랑이 끝이었는지, 넘어지면서였는지, 아니면 놀란 말이 발길질을 해댄 탓이었는지, 마님은 엉덩이를 다쳐 절게 되셨고, 그 시각부터 시름시름 야위어가기 시작하셨단다.\""]], [["The housekeeper has dropped her voice to a little more than a whisper.", "가정부가 목소리를 속삭임보다 조금 큰 정도로 낮춘다."]], [["“She had been a lady of a handsome figure and a noble carriage.", "\"마님은 자태가 고운 데다 거동도 기품 있는 분이셨다. 그런데 그렇게 변해버린 것을 한 번도 탓하지 않으셨어."], ["She never complained of the change; she never spoke to any one of being crippled or of being in pain, but day by day she tried to walk upon the terrace, and with the help of the stone balustrade, went up and down, up and down, up and down, in sun and shadow, with greater difficulty every day.", "절름발이가 됐다거나 아프다거나, 누구에게도 입에 올리지 않으셨지. 그저 날마다 테라스를 걸으려 애쓰셨다."], ["At last, one afternoon her husband (to whom she had never, on any persuasion, opened her lips since that night), standing at the great south window, saw her drop upon the pavement.", "돌난간에 의지해서, 오르락내리락, 오르락내리락, 오르락내리락, 볕에서도 그늘에서도, 날이 갈수록 더 힘겹게 걸으셨어. 그러던 어느 날 오후, 그날 밤 이후로 무슨 말로 구슬려도 마님이 한마디도 입을 열지 않으셨던 그 남편 되시는 분이, 커다란 남쪽 창가에 서 계시다가 마님이 보도 위로 쓰러지는 것을 보셨단다."], ["He hastened down to raise her, but she repulsed him as he bent over her, and looking at him fixedly and coldly, said, ‘I will die here where I have walked.", "그분이 황급히 내려가 부축하려 하셨지만, 마님은 몸을 굽히는 그분을 밀쳐내고는, 그분을 차갑게 똑바로 쏘아보며 말씀하셨지. '나는 내가 걷던 이곳에서 죽겠어요."], ["And I will walk here, though I am in my grave.", "그리고 무덤에 들어가서도 이곳을 걷겠어요."], ["I will walk here until the pride of this house is humbled.", "이 집안의 오만이 꺾일 때까지 나는 이곳을 걸을 거예요."], ["And when calamity or when disgrace is coming to it, let the Dedlocks listen for my step!’”", "그리고 이 집에 재앙이 닥치거나 치욕이 닥칠 때면, 데드록가 사람들은 내 발소리에 귀를 기울이게 될 거예요!'\""]], [["Watt looks at Rosa.", "와트가 로자를 바라본다."], ["Rosa in the deepening gloom looks down upon the ground, half frightened and half shy.", "짙어가는 어스름 속에서 로자는 반쯤은 겁먹고 반쯤은 수줍어, 바닥을 내려다본다."]], [["“There and then she died.", "\"마님은 그 자리에서, 그 순간에 숨을 거두셨다."], ["And from those days,” says Mrs.", "그리고 그날 이후로,\""], ["Rouncewell, “the name has come down—the Ghost’s Walk.", "라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"그 이름이 전해 내려온 게야."], ["If the tread is an echo, it is an echo that is only heard after dark, and is often unheard for a long while together.", "유령의 길이라고. 그 발소리가 메아리라면, 그것은 어두워진 뒤에야 들리는 메아리이고, 오랫동안 한 번도 안 들릴 때도 많아."], ["But it comes back from time to time; and so sure as there is sickness or death in the family, it will be heard then.”", "하지만 이따금 다시 돌아오지. 그리고 집안에 병이나 죽음이 있을 때면, 어김없이 그 소리가 들린단다.\""]], [["“And disgrace, grandmother—”", "\"치욕이 있을 때도요, 할머니.\""], ["says Watt.", "와트가 말한다."]], [["“Disgrace never comes to Chesney Wold,”", "\"치욕이란 체스니 월드에 닥치는 법이 없다.\""], ["returns the housekeeper.", "가정부가 받는다."]], [["Her grandson apologizes with “True.", "손자가 \"맞아요, 맞는 말씀이에요.\""], ["True.”", "하며 사과한다."]], [["“That is the story.", "\"그게 그 이야기란다."], ["Whatever the sound is, it is a worrying sound,” says Mrs.", "그 소리가 무엇이든, 사람 마음을 졸이게 하는 소리지.\""], ["Rouncewell, getting up from her chair; “and what is to be noticed in it is that it MUST BE HEARD.", "라운스웰 부인이 의자에서 일어서며 말한다. \"그런데 그 소리에서 눈여겨볼 점은, 그것이 반드시 들리고야 만다는 거야."], ["My Lady, who is afraid of nothing, admits that when it is there, it must be heard.", "아무것도 두려워하지 않으시는 마님조차, 그 소리가 났다 하면 반드시 들리고 만다고 인정하시지."], ["You cannot shut it out.", "막아낼 도리가 없어."], ["Watt, there is a tall French clock behind you (placed there, ’a purpose) that has a loud beat when it is in motion and can play music.", "와트야, 네 뒤에 키 큰 프랑스 시계가 있지(일부러 거기 놓아둔 게다), 움직일 때면 소리가 크게 울리고 음악도 연주하는 시계 말이다."], ["You understand how those things are managed?”", "그런 물건이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알겠니?\""]], [["“Pretty well, grandmother, I think.”", "\"제법 알아요, 할머니, 그런 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Set it a-going.”", "\"그걸 한번 돌려보렴.\""]], [["Watt sets it a-going—music and all.", "와트가 시계를 돌린다. 음악이며 무엇이며 다 함께."]], [["“Now, come hither,”", "\"자, 이리 오너라.\""], ["says the housekeeper.", "가정부가 말한다."], ["“Hither, child, towards my Lady’s pillow.", "\"이리, 얘야, 마님 베개 쪽으로."], ["I am not sure that it is dark enough yet, but listen!", "아직 충분히 어두워졌는지는 모르겠다만, 들어보렴!"], ["Can you hear the sound upon the terrace, through the music, and the beat, and everything?”", "이 음악이며, 박자며, 그 모든 것 너머로, 테라스에서 나는 소리가 들리니?\""]], [["“I certainly can!”", "\"틀림없이 들려요!\""]], [["“So my Lady says.”", "\"마님도 그러시지.\""]], [["Covering a Multitude of Sins\nIt was interesting when I dressed before daylight to peep out of window, where my candles were reflected in the black panes like two beacons, and finding all beyond still enshrouded in the indistinctness of last night, to watch how it turned out when the day came on.", "날이 밝기도 전에 옷을 차려입고 창밖을 내다보는 일은 흥미로웠습니다. 새카만 유리창에 촛불 두 개가 봉화처럼 비치고, 그 너머는 온통 간밤의 어슴푸레함에 싸여 있어 도무지 분간이 가지 않았지요."], ["As the prospect gradually revealed itself and disclosed the scene over which the wind had wandered in the dark, like my memory over my life, I had a pleasure in discovering the unknown objects that had been around me in my sleep.", "그렇게 날이 밝아 오면서 풍경이 어떻게 드러나는지 지켜보는 일 말입니다. 어둠 속을 헤매던 바람이 — 마치 내 기억이 내 삶 위를 헤매듯 — 휩쓸고 지나간 그 풍경이 차츰차츰 모습을 드러내자, 나는 잠든 동안 내 주위를 둘러싸고 있던 낯선 사물들을 하나하나 발견하는 즐거움을 누렸습니다."], ["At first they were faintly discernible in the mist, and above them the later stars still glimmered.", "처음에는 안개 속에 희미하게 어른거릴 뿐이었고, 그 위로는 새벽에 뒤처진 별들이 아직 가물가물 빛났습니다."], ["That pale interval over, the picture began to enlarge and fill up so fast that at every new peep I could have found enough to look at for an hour.", "그 창백한 막간이 지나자 그림은 빠르게 커지고 채워져, 새로 한 번 내다볼 때마다 한 시간은 족히 바라볼 거리가 생길 정도였습니다."], ["Imperceptibly my candles became the only incongruous part of the morning, the dark places in my room all melted away, and the day shone bright upon a cheerful landscape, prominent in which the old Abbey Church, with its massive tower, threw a softer train of shadow on the view than seemed compatible with its rugged character.", "어느새 내 촛불만이 아침과 어울리지 않는 유일한 부분이 되었고, 방 안의 어두운 구석들은 모두 녹아 없어졌으며, 햇살이 명랑한 풍경 위로 환하게 빛났습니다. 그 풍경에서 유독 도드라진 것은 묵직한 탑을 인 옛 수도원 교회였는데, 그 거칠고 투박한 성격과는 도무지 어울리지 않게 한결 부드러운 그림자 자락을 풍경 위에 드리우고 있었습니다."], ["But so from rough outsides (I hope I have learnt), serene and gentle influences often proceed.", "하지만 그렇게 거친 겉모습에서도 평온하고 온화한 기운이 흘러나오는 일이 흔하다는 것을 (부디 내가 깨우쳤기를 바랍니다) 그때 배웠습니다."]], [["Every part of the house was in such order, and every one was so attentive to me, that I had no trouble with my two bunches of keys, though what with trying to remember the contents of each little store-room drawer and cupboard; and what with making notes on a slate about jams, and pickles, and preserves, and bottles, and glass, and china, and a great many other things; and what with being generally a methodical, old-maidish sort of foolish little person, I was so busy that I could not believe it was breakfast-time when I heard the bell ring.", "집안 구석구석이 어찌나 잘 정돈되어 있고 모두가 어찌나 나를 살뜰히 챙겨 주는지, 열쇠 꾸러미 두 묶음을 들고도 아무런 어려움이 없었습니다. 그래도 작은 곳간의 서랍이며 찬장마다 무엇이 들었는지 기억해 내려 애쓰고, 잼이며 절임이며 설탕절임이며 병이며 유리잔이며 도자기며 그 밖에 수많은 것을 석판에 적어 두느라, 게다가 워낙 매사 꼼꼼하고 노처녀 같은 어수룩한 자그마한 사람이다 보니, 어찌나 바빴던지 종이 울리는 소리를 듣고도 벌써 아침 식사 시간이라는 게 믿기지 않을 정도였습니다."], ["Away I ran, however, and made tea, as I had already been installed into the responsibility of the tea-pot; and then, as they were all rather late and nobody was down yet, I thought I would take a peep at the garden and get some knowledge of that too.", "그래도 나는 부리나케 달려가 차를 우렸지요. 이미 찻주전자를 맡는 책임이 내게 주어졌으니까요."], ["I found it quite a delightful place—in front, the pretty avenue and drive by which we had approached (and where, by the by, we had cut up the gravel so terribly with our wheels that I asked the gardener to roll it); at the back, the flower-garden, with my darling at her window up there, throwing it open to smile out at me, as if she would have kissed me from that distance.", "그러고 나니 다들 좀 늦은 데다 아직 아무도 내려오지 않았기에, 나는 정원도 한번 들여다보고 그쪽도 좀 알아 두는 게 좋겠다 싶었습니다. 정원은 정말 기분 좋은 곳이었습니다."], ["Beyond the flower-garden was a kitchen-garden, and then a paddock, and then a snug little rick-yard, and then a dear little farm-yard.", "앞쪽으로는 우리가 들어올 때 지나온 예쁜 가로수 길과 마찻길이 있었는데 (그러고 보니 우리가 마차 바퀴로 자갈을 어찌나 험하게 파헤쳐 놓았던지, 나는 정원사에게 롤러로 좀 다져 달라고 부탁했습니다), 뒤쪽으로는 화원이 펼쳐져 있었고, 저 위 창가에는 내 사랑하는 사람이 창을 활짝 열어젖히고 나를 향해 미소 지으며, 그 먼 거리에서도 내게 입맞춤이라도 보내려는 듯했습니다. 화원 너머로는 채마밭이 있었고, 그다음엔 작은 방목장, 그다음엔 아늑한 낟가리 마당, 그다음엔 사랑스러운 작은 농장 마당이 있었습니다."], ["As to the house itself, with its three peaks in the roof; its various-shaped windows, some so large, some so small, and all so pretty; its trellis-work, against the south-front for roses and honey-suckle, and its homely, comfortable, welcoming look—it was, as Ada said when she came out to meet me with her arm through that of its master, worthy of her cousin John, a bold thing to say, though he only pinched her dear cheek for it.", "집 자체로 말하자면, 지붕에 솟은 세 개의 뾰족한 봉우리며, 어떤 건 그리도 크고 어떤 건 그리도 작은, 하지만 하나같이 어찌나 예쁜지 모를 갖가지 모양의 창문들이며, 남쪽 정면에 장미와 인동덩굴을 올리려고 친 격자 시렁이며, 그 정겹고 아늑하고 반겨 주는 듯한 모습이며 — 에이다가 집주인의 팔짱을 끼고 나를 맞으러 나오면서 한 말마따나, 그 사촌 오라버니 존에게 어울리는 집이었습니다. 대담한 말이긴 했지만, 그는 그 말에 그저 그녀의 어여쁜 뺨을 살짝 꼬집어 줄 뿐이었지요."]], [["Mr. Skimpole was as agreeable at breakfast as he had been overnight.", "스킴폴 씨는 아침 식사 자리에서도 간밤만큼이나 유쾌했습니다."], ["There was honey on the table, and it led him into a discourse about bees.", "식탁에 꿀이 놓여 있었는데, 그것이 그를 꿀벌에 관한 이야기로 이끌었지요."], ["He had no objection to honey, he said (and I should think he had not, for he seemed to like it), but he protested against the overweening assumptions of bees.", "그는 꿀에는 아무 이의가 없다고 했습니다(아마 그랬을 겁니다, 꿀을 좋아하는 듯했으니까요). 다만 꿀벌의 그 잘난 척하는 우쭐댐에는 항의한다는 거였습니다."], ["He didn’t at all see why the busy bee should be proposed as a model to him; he supposed the bee liked to make honey, or he wouldn’t do it—nobody asked him.", "그는 어째서 부지런한 꿀벌이 자기더러 본받으라는 모범으로 제시되어야 하는지 도무지 이해할 수 없다고 했습니다. 꿀벌은 꿀 만들기를 좋아하니까 만드는 것일 테고, 안 그러면 만들지 않을 텐데, 아무도 시키지도 않았다는 거지요."], ["It was not necessary for the bee to make such a merit of his tastes.", "꿀벌이 제 취향을 가지고 그렇게까지 공치사할 필요는 없다는 겁니다."], ["If every confectioner went buzzing about the world banging against everything that came in his way and egotistically calling upon everybody to take notice that he was going to his work and must not be interrupted, the world would be quite an unsupportable place.", "만약 제과업자가 죄다 세상을 윙윙거리며 돌아다니면서 앞길에 걸리는 것마다 쾅쾅 부딪고, 잘난 척하며 모두에게 \"내가 지금 일하러 가니 방해 말라\"고 떠들어 댄다면, 세상은 도무지 견딜 수 없는 곳이 되고 말 거라는 거였습니다."], ["Then, after all, it was a ridiculous position to be smoked out of your fortune with brimstone as soon as you had made it.", "게다가 결국, 애써 재산을 모으자마자 유황 연기에 그을려 쫓겨나는 신세라니, 이런 우스꽝스러운 처지가 어디 있느냐는 겁니다."], ["You would have a very mean opinion of a Manchester man if he spun cotton for no other purpose.", "맨체스터의 어떤 사람이 그런 목적밖에 없이 면사를 잣는다면 다들 그를 아주 한심하게 여길 거라고요."], ["He must say he thought a drone the embodiment of a pleasanter and wiser idea.", "그는 수벌이야말로 훨씬 유쾌하고 현명한 발상을 구현한 존재라고 말하지 않을 수 없다고 했습니다."], ["The drone said unaffectedly, “You will excuse me; I really cannot attend to the shop!", "수벌은 짐짓 꾸밈없이 이렇게 말한다는 거지요. \"실례지만, 나는 정말 가게 일에 신경 쓸 수가 없네!"], ["I find myself in a world in which there is so much to see and so short a time to see it in that I must take the liberty of looking about me and begging to be provided for by somebody who doesn’t want to look about him.”", "볼 것은 이리도 많은데 볼 시간은 이리도 짧은 세상에 와 있고 보니, 나는 마음껏 주위를 둘러보는 자유를 누리고, 둘러볼 마음이 없는 누군가가 나를 좀 먹여 살려 주십사 부탁드릴 수밖에 없네.\" 스킴폴 씨에게는 이것이 수벌의 철학으로 보였고, 그는 이를 아주 훌륭한 철학이라 여겼습니다."], ["This appeared to Mr.", "물론 수벌이 꿀벌과 사이좋게 지낼 마음만 있다는 전제하에서요."], ["Skimpole to be the drone philosophy, and he thought it a very good philosophy, always supposing the drone to be willing to be on good terms with the bee, which, so far as he knew, the easy fellow always was, if the consequential creature would only let him, and not be so conceited about his honey!", "그런데 그가 아는 한, 그 느긋한 친구는 늘 그럴 마음이 있다는 겁니다. 저 거들먹거리는 녀석이 가만 놔두기만 한다면, 제 꿀을 가지고 그렇게 우쭐대지만 않는다면 말이지요!"]], [["He pursued this fancy with the lightest foot over a variety of ground and made us all merry, though again he seemed to have as serious a meaning in what he said as he was capable of having.", "그는 이 공상을 더없이 가벼운 발걸음으로 온갖 화제를 넘나들며 펼쳐 우리 모두를 즐겁게 했습니다. 다만 이번에도 그가 하는 말에는 그가 품을 수 있는 한껏 진지한 뜻이 담겨 있는 듯했지요."], ["I left them still listening to him when I withdrew to attend to my new duties.", "나는 새 임무를 보러 자리를 뜰 때까지도 다들 그의 이야기에 귀 기울이고 있는 모습을 두고 나왔습니다."], ["They had occupied me for some time, and I was passing through the passages on my return with my basket of keys on my arm when Mr.", "한동안 일에 매달렸다가, 열쇠 바구니를 팔에 걸고 복도를 지나 돌아오는데, 잔다이스 씨가 자기 침실 옆 작은 방으로 나를 불렀습니다."], ["Jarndyce called me into a small room next his bed-chamber, which I found to be in part a little library of books and papers and in part quite a little museum of his boots and shoes and hat-boxes.", "가 보니 한쪽은 책과 서류가 쌓인 작은 서재였고, 다른 한쪽은 그의 장화며 구두며 모자 상자가 들어찬 그야말로 작은 박물관이었습니다."]], [["“Sit down, my dear,”", "\"앉으렴, 얘야.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“This, you must know, is the growlery.", "\"여기는 말이지, 투덜방이란다."], ["When I am out of humour, I come and growl here.”", "기분이 언짢을 때면 나는 여기 와서 투덜거리지.\""]], [["“You must be here very seldom, sir,”", "\"여기 오실 일이 아주 드물겠네요, 어르신.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, you don’t know me!”", "\"오, 넌 나를 모르는구나!\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“When I am deceived or disappointed in—the wind, and it’s easterly, I take refuge here.", "\"내가 무언가에 — 바람에 속거나 실망할 때, 그리고 바람이 동쪽에서 불어올 때, 나는 여기로 피신한단다."], ["The growlery is the best-used room in the house.", "투덜방은 이 집에서 가장 자주 쓰는 방이야."], ["You are not aware of half my humours yet.", "너는 아직 내 변덕의 절반도 모르지."], ["My dear, how you are trembling!”", "얘야, 너 어쩜 그렇게 떨고 있니!\""]], [["I could not help it; I tried very hard, but being alone with that benevolent presence, and meeting his kind eyes, and feeling so happy and so honoured there, and my heart so full—I kissed his hand.", "어쩔 수가 없었습니다. 무던히 애를 썼지만, 그 자비로운 분과 단둘이 있으면서, 그 다정한 눈길과 마주치고, 그곳에 있다는 것이 그토록 행복하고 영광스러워, 가슴이 벅차오르다 못해 — 나는 그의 손에 입을 맞추고 말았습니다."], ["I don’t know what I said, or even that I spoke.", "내가 무슨 말을 했는지, 아니 말을 하기는 했는지조차 모르겠습니다."], ["He was disconcerted and walked to the window; I almost believed with an intention of jumping out, until he turned and I was reassured by seeing in his eyes what he had gone there to hide.", "그는 당황하여 창가로 걸어갔습니다. 거의 창밖으로 뛰어내릴 작정인가 싶을 정도였는데, 그가 돌아서자 그 눈 속에서 숨기려고 그리로 갔던 것을 보고서야 나는 안심했습니다."], ["He gently patted me on the head, and I sat down.", "그는 내 머리를 가만히 토닥여 주었고, 나는 자리에 앉았습니다."]], [["“There!", "\"자! 자!\""], ["There!” he said.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“That’s over.", "\"다 끝났다. 흥!"], ["Pooh! Don’t be foolish.”", "어리석게 굴지 말려무나.\""]], [["“It shall not happen again, sir,”", "\"다시는 이러지 않겠습니다, 어르신.\""], ["I returned, “but at first it is difficult—”", "내가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 처음이라 어렵네요—\""]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"별소릴!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“It’s easy, easy. Why not?", "\"쉬운 일이야, 쉬워."], ["I hear of a good little orphan girl without a protector, and I take it into my head to be that protector.", "안 될 게 뭐냐? 보호자도 없는 착한 고아 소녀가 있다는 소식을 듣고, 내가 그 보호자가 되기로 마음먹은 것뿐이다."], ["She grows up, and more than justifies my good opinion, and I remain her guardian and her friend.", "그 아이가 자라나 내 좋은 평가를 넘치도록 입증해 주었고, 나는 그 아이의 후견인이자 벗으로 남았지."], ["What is there in all this?", "이 모든 일에 무슨 거창한 게 있단 말이냐?"], ["So, so!", "자, 자!"], ["Now, we have cleared off old scores, and I have before me thy pleasant, trusting, trusty face again.”", "이제 묵은 셈은 다 정리되었고, 내 앞에는 다시 너의 그 다정하고, 믿음직하고, 미더운 얼굴이 있구나.\""]], [["I said to myself, “Esther, my dear, you surprise me!", "나는 속으로 말했습니다. \"에스더, 얘야, 너 정말 놀랍구나!"], ["This really is not what I expected of you!”", "이건 정말 너답지 않잖니!\""], ["And it had such a good effect that I folded my hands upon my basket and quite recovered myself. Mr.", "그리고 그것이 어찌나 효과가 좋았던지, 나는 바구니 위에 두 손을 모으고 완전히 마음을 추슬렀습니다."], ["Jarndyce, expressing his approval in his face, began to talk to me as confidentially as if I had been in the habit of conversing with him every morning for I don’t know how long.", "잔다이스 씨는 얼굴 가득 흡족한 빛을 띠며, 마치 내가 언제부턴지도 모를 만큼 오랫동안 매일 아침 그와 이야기를 나눠 온 사이라도 되는 양 허물없이 말을 건네기 시작했습니다."], ["I almost felt as if I had.", "나도 거의 그래 온 듯한 기분이 들었지요."]], [["“Of course, Esther,”", "\"물론, 에스더,\""], ["he said, “you don’t understand this Chancery business?”", "그가 말했습니다. \"너는 이 대법원 일을 이해하지 못하겠지?\""]], [["And of course I shook my head.", "물론 나는 고개를 저었습니다."]], [["“I don’t know who does,” he returned.", "\"누가 이해하는지 나도 모르겠다.\""], ["“The lawyers have twisted it into such a state of bedevilment that the original merits of the case have long disappeared from the face of the earth.", "그가 대꾸했습니다. \"변호사들이 사건을 어찌나 비비 꼬아 마귀 들린 꼴로 만들어 놓았는지, 사건의 본래 시비는 진작에 이 땅 위에서 사라져 버렸지."], ["It’s about a will and the trusts under a will—or it was once.", "본디 어떤 유언과 그 유언에 딸린 신탁에 관한 거였단다 — 아니, 한때는 그랬지."], ["It’s about nothing but costs now.", "지금은 오로지 소송 비용에 관한 것뿐이야."], ["We are always appearing, and disappearing, and swearing, and interrogating, and filing, and cross-filing, and arguing, and sealing, and motioning, and referring, and reporting, and revolving about the Lord Chancellor and all his satellites, and equitably waltzing ourselves off to dusty death, about costs.", "우리는 끊임없이 출두하고, 퇴장하고, 선서하고, 심문하고, 서류를 제출하고, 또 맞제출하고, 변론하고, 봉인하고, 신청하고, 위탁하고, 보고하고, 대법관과 그의 위성들 주위를 빙글빙글 돌면서, 소송 비용을 두고 형평법답게 왈츠를 추며 먼지투성이 죽음 속으로 흘러간단다."], ["That’s the great question.", "그게 가장 큰 문제지."], ["All the rest, by some extraordinary means, has melted away.”", "나머지는 죄다, 어떤 기막힌 수작으로, 녹아 사라져 버렸어.\""]], [["“But it was, sir,”", "\"하지만 본디는, 어르신,\" 내가 그를 본론으로 되돌리려고 말했습니다."], ["said I, to bring him back, for he began to rub his head, “about a will?”", "그가 머리를 긁기 시작했거든요. \"어떤 유언에 관한 거였다고요?\""]], [["“Why, yes, it was about a will when it was about anything,”", "\"음, 그래, 무언가에 관한 것이었을 때는 유언에 관한 거였지.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“A certain Jarndyce, in an evil hour, made a great fortune, and made a great will.", "\"어떤 잔다이스라는 자가, 불운한 시각에, 막대한 재산을 모으고 막대한 유언장을 남겼단다. 그 유언에 딸린 신탁을 어떻게 운영하느냐는 문제로 다투는 사이에, 유언으로 남긴 그 재산은 흥청망청 탕진되고 있지."], ["In the question how the trusts under that will are to be administered, the fortune left by the will is squandered away; the legatees under the will are reduced to such a miserable condition that they would be sufficiently punished if they had committed an enormous crime in having money left them, and the will itself is made a dead letter.", "유언으로 유산을 받게 된 사람들은 어찌나 비참한 처지로 떨어졌는지, 돈을 물려받았다는 게 무슨 엄청난 죄라도 되어 그 벌을 받는다 친대도 충분할 지경이고, 유언장 자체는 사문서가 되어 버렸어. 이 통탄할 소송 내내, 그 안에 있는 사람이라면 한 사람만 빼고 누구나 이미 다 아는 일을, 하필 모르는 그 한 사람한테 알아내라고 떠넘기지."], ["All through the deplorable cause, everything that everybody in it, except one man, knows already is referred to that only one man who don’t know, it to find out—all through the deplorable cause, everybody must have copies, over and over again, of everything that has accumulated about it in the way of cartloads of papers (or must pay for them without having them, which is the usual course, for nobody wants them) and must go down the middle and up again through such an infernal country-dance of costs and fees and nonsense and corruption as was never dreamed of in the wildest visions of a witch’s Sabbath.", "이 통탄할 소송 내내, 모두가 그 사건을 둘러싸고 산더미처럼 쌓인 — 수레로 실어 나를 만한 — 서류 사본을 거듭거듭 받아 가야만 한단다(아니면 받지도 못한 채 값을 치러야 하는데, 이게 보통이지. 아무도 그걸 원하지 않으니까)."], ["Equity sends questions to law, law sends questions back to equity; law finds it can’t do this, equity finds it can’t do that; neither can so much as say it can’t do anything, without this solicitor instructing and this counsel appearing for A, and that solicitor instructing and that counsel appearing for B; and so on through the whole alphabet, like the history of the apple pie.", "그러고는 마녀의 안식일이라는 가장 광란에 찬 환상 속에서도 꿈꾼 적 없을 만큼 지독한, 소송 비용과 수수료와 헛소리와 부패로 짜인 지옥 같은 춤판을 가운데로 내려갔다가 다시 올라와야만 해. 형평법원은 보통법원에 질문을 보내고, 보통법원은 형평법원에 질문을 되돌려 보내지."], ["And thus, through years and years, and lives and lives, everything goes on, constantly beginning over and over again, and nothing ever ends.", "보통법원은 이걸 할 수 없다 하고, 형평법원은 저걸 할 수 없다 하고, 어느 쪽도 변호사 갑이 갑을 대리해 사무 처리를 맡기고 갑의 변호인이 출두하고, 또 다른 변호사 을이 을을 대리해 사무 처리를 맡기고 을의 변호인이 출두하지 않고서는 아무것도 할 수 없다는 말조차 못 하지. 그렇게 알파벳 전부를 거쳐 가는 게, 꼭 '사과 파이의 역사'라는 노래 같단다."], ["And we can’t get out of the suit on any terms, for we are made parties to it, and MUST BE parties to it, whether we like it or not.", "그리하여 여러 해를 거듭하고, 여러 생애를 거듭하면서, 모든 것이 끊임없이 처음부터 다시 시작될 뿐, 끝나는 것은 하나도 없어. 그리고 우리는 어떤 조건으로도 그 소송에서 빠져나올 수가 없단다."], ["But it won’t do to think of it!", "우리는 당사자로 엮였고, 좋든 싫든 당사자가 될 수밖에 없으니까."], ["When my great uncle, poor Tom Jarndyce, began to think of it, it was the beginning of the end!”", "하지만 그걸 자꾸 생각해선 안 돼! 내 종조부, 가엾은 톰 잔다이스가 그걸 곱씹기 시작했을 때, 그게 바로 종말의 시작이었거든!\""]], [["“The Mr. Jarndyce, sir, whose story I have heard?”", "\"제가 그 이야기를 들었던 그 잔다이스 씨 말씀인가요, 어르신?\""]], [["He nodded gravely.", "그는 무겁게 고개를 끄덕였습니다."], ["“I was his heir, and this was his house, Esther.", "\"내가 그분의 상속인이었고, 이 집이 바로 그분의 집이었단다, 에스더."], ["When I came here, it was bleak indeed.", "내가 여기 왔을 때, 정말이지 황량하기 그지없었지."], ["He had left the signs of his misery upon it.”", "그분은 자신의 비참함의 흔적을 이 집에 고스란히 남겨 놓으셨거든.\""]], [["“How changed it must be now!”", "\"지금은 얼마나 달라졌을까요!\""], ["I said.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“It had been called, before his time, the Peaks.", "\"그분 시절 전에는 이 집을 '봉우리집'이라 불렀단다."], ["He gave it its present name and lived here shut up, day and night poring over the wicked heaps of papers in the suit and hoping against hope to disentangle it from its mystification and bring it to a close.", "그분이 지금의 이름을 붙이고, 여기 틀어박혀, 밤낮없이 소송에 얽힌 그 사악한 서류 더미를 들여다보며, 그 미궁에서 사건을 풀어내 끝장을 보겠노라고 헛된 희망에 매달려 사셨지."], ["In the meantime, the place became dilapidated, the wind whistled through the cracked walls, the rain fell through the broken roof, the weeds choked the passage to the rotting door.", "그러는 동안 집은 황폐해져, 갈라진 벽 틈으로 바람이 휘파람을 불었고, 부서진 지붕으로 비가 새어 들었고, 잡초가 썩어 가는 문으로 통하는 길을 뒤덮었어."], ["When I brought what remained of him home here, the brains seemed to me to have been blown out of the house too, it was so shattered and ruined.”", "내가 그분의 남은 유해를 이 집으로 모셔 왔을 때, 이 집에서도 머릿골이 송두리째 날아가 버린 것만 같았단다. 그만큼 부서지고 무너져 있었지.\""]], [["He walked a little to and fro after saying this to himself with a shudder, and then looked at me, and brightened, and came and sat down again with his hands in his pockets.", "이렇게 혼잣말처럼 내뱉고 몸서리를 친 그는, 잠시 이리저리 거닐다가 나를 바라보더니, 얼굴이 환해지면서 다시 와 앉아 두 손을 호주머니에 넣었습니다."]], [["“I told you this was the growlery, my dear.", "\"여기가 투덜방이라고 했지, 얘야."], ["Where was I?”", "어디까지 얘기했더라?\""]], [["I reminded him, at the hopeful change he had made in Bleak House.", "나는 그가 황폐한 집에 가져온 그 희망찬 변화 대목을 일깨워 주었습니다."]], [["“Bleak House; true.", "\"황폐한 집, 그래."], ["There is, in that city of London there, some property of ours which is much at this day what Bleak House was then; I say property of ours, meaning of the suit’s, but I ought to call it the property of costs, for costs is the only power on earth that will ever get anything out of it now or will ever know it for anything but an eyesore and a heartsore.", "저기 런던이라는 도시에 우리 소유의 재산이 하나 있는데, 그게 오늘날 딱 그 시절의 황폐한 집 같은 꼴이란다. 우리 소유라고 했지만 소송의 소유라는 뜻이고, 사실은 소송 비용의 소유라고 불러야 옳을 거야."], ["It is a street of perishing blind houses, with their eyes stoned out, without a pane of glass, without so much as a window-frame, with the bare blank shutters tumbling from their hinges and falling asunder, the iron rails peeling away in flakes of rust, the chimneys sinking in, the stone steps to every door (and every door might be death’s door) turning stagnant green, the very crutches on which the ruins are propped decaying.", "이제 그 재산에서 무엇이든 뽑아낼 힘은, 또 그것을 흉물이자 골칫거리 말고 무엇으로든 알아줄 힘은, 이 세상에 소송 비용밖에 없으니까. 그건 죽어 가는 눈먼 집들이 늘어선 거리란다."], ["Although Bleak House was not in Chancery, its master was, and it was stamped with the same seal.", "눈은 돌멩이에 짓이겨졌고, 유리 한 장 남지 않았고, 창틀조차 없으며, 휑한 덧문은 경첩에서 떨어져 나가 산산이 부서지고, 쇠 난간은 녹이 슬어 비늘처럼 벗겨지고, 굴뚝은 주저앉고, 문마다 놓인 돌계단은(그 문 하나하나가 죽음의 문이라 해도 좋을 만큼) 시퍼런 이끼로 썩어 들고, 폐허를 떠받친 그 버팀목들마저 삭아 가고 있지. 황폐한 집은 대법원에 걸리지 않았지만, 그 주인은 걸려 있었고, 집에도 똑같은 봉인이 찍혔던 거야."], ["These are the Great Seal’s impressions, my dear, all over England—the children know them!”", "이게 바로 국새의 자국이란다, 얘야, 잉글랜드 방방곡곡에 찍혀 있지 — 아이들도 다 알아본다니까!\""]], [["“How changed it is!”", "\"정말 많이 달라졌네요!\""], ["I said again.", "나는 또 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, so it is,”", "\"음, 그렇지.\""], ["he answered much more cheerfully; “and it is wisdom in you to keep me to the bright side of the picture.”", "그가 한결 명랑하게 대답했습니다. \"그리고 나를 그림의 밝은 쪽으로만 붙들어 두다니, 너 참 현명하구나.\""], ["(The idea of my wisdom!) “These are things I never talk about or even think about, excepting in the growlery here.", "(내가 현명하다니요!) \"이런 것들은 내가 좀처럼 입에 올리지도, 떠올리지도 않는단다. 여기 투덜방에서가 아니면 말이지."], ["If you consider it right to mention them to Rick and Ada,”", "이걸 릭과 에이다에게 말하는 게 옳다고 여긴다면,\""], ["looking seriously at me, “you can.", "그가 나를 진지하게 바라보며 말했습니다."], ["I leave it to your discretion, Esther.”", "\"말해도 좋아. 네 판단에 맡기마, 에스더.\""]], [["“I hope, sir—”", "\"바라옵건대, 어르신—\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“I think you had better call me guardian, my dear.”", "\"너는 나를 후견인이라 부르는 게 좋겠다, 얘야.\""]], [["I felt that I was choking again—I taxed myself with it, “Esther, now, you know you are!”—when he feigned to say this slightly, as if it were a whim instead of a thoughtful tenderness.", "또다시 목이 메어 오는 것이 느껴졌습니다 — 나는 스스로를 다그쳤지요. \"에스더, 자, 너도 알잖니!\""], ["But I gave the housekeeping keys the least shake in the world as a reminder to myself, and folding my hands in a still more determined manner on the basket, looked at him quietly.", "그가 마치 사려 깊은 다정함이 아니라 한낱 변덕인 양 가볍게 던지는 말투로 그렇게 말했을 때 말입니다. 하지만 나는 스스로 다잡으려고 살림 열쇠를 세상에서 가장 작게 한 번 흔들고는, 두 손을 바구니 위에 더더욱 단단히 모은 채 그를 차분히 바라보았습니다."]], [["“I hope, guardian,”", "\"바라옵건대, 후견인님,\""], ["said I, “that you may not trust too much to my discretion.", "내가 말했습니다. \"제 판단을 너무 믿지 말아 주세요."], ["I hope you may not mistake me.", "저를 잘못 보지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["I am afraid it will be a disappointment to you to know that I am not clever, but it really is the truth, and you would soon find it out if I had not the honesty to confess it.”", "제가 영리하지 못하다는 걸 알면 실망하실까 봐 두렵지만, 그것이 정말 사실이며, 제가 정직하게 고백하지 않더라도 머지않아 알아차리실 거예요.\""]], [["He did not seem at all disappointed; quite the contrary.", "그는 조금도 실망한 기색이 아니었습니다. 오히려 그 반대였지요."], ["He told me, with a smile all over his face, that he knew me very well indeed and that I was quite clever enough for him.", "그는 얼굴 가득 미소를 지으며, 자기는 나를 아주 잘 알고 있으며, 나는 자기에게는 충분히 영리한 사람이라고 했습니다."]], [["“I hope I may turn out so,”", "\"그렇게 드러나면 좋겠습니다만,\""], ["said I, “but I am much afraid of it, guardian.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"정말 그럴까 두렵네요, 후견인님.\""]], [["“You are clever enough to be the good little woman of our lives here, my dear,”", "\"너는 여기 우리 삶의 착하고 자그마한 안주인이 될 만큼 충분히 영리하단다, 얘야.\""], ["he returned playfully; “the little old woman of the child’s (I don’t mean Skimpole’s) rhyme:", "그가 장난스레 대꾸했습니다. \"동요에 나오는 그 자그마한 할머니 말이다 (스킴폴 동요가 아니란다):"]], [["“‘Little old woman, and whither so high?’ ‘To sweep the cobwebs out of the sky.’”", "\"'자그마한 할머니, 그리 높이 어디로 가시오?' '하늘의 거미줄을 쓸어 내러 간다오.'\""]], [["“You will sweep them so neatly out of OUR sky in the course of your housekeeping, Esther, that one of these days we shall have to abandon the growlery and nail up the door.”", "\"네가 살림을 살아 나가는 동안 우리 하늘의 거미줄을 어찌나 말끔히 쓸어 낼지, 머지않아 우리는 투덜방을 버리고 그 문을 못질해 닫아야 할 거야, 에스더.\""]], [["This was the beginning of my being called Old Woman, and Little Old Woman, and Cobweb, and Mrs. Shipton, and Mother Hubbard, and Dame Durden, and so many names of that sort that my own name soon became quite lost among them.", "이것이 내가 노파니, 자그마한 노파니, 거미줄이니, 십턴 부인이니, 허버드 아주머니니, 더든 부인이니 하는 갖가지 이름으로 불리게 된 시작이었고, 그런 이름이 어찌나 많았던지 내 본래 이름이 곧 그 사이에 파묻혀 버릴 지경이었습니다."]], [["“However,” said Mr.", "\"그건 그렇고,\""], ["Jarndyce, “to return to our gossip.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"우리 잡담으로 돌아가 보자."], ["Here’s Rick, a fine young fellow full of promise.", "여기 릭이라는 녀석이 있지, 앞날이 창창한 멋진 젊은이 말이다."], ["What’s to be done with him?”", "저 아이를 어떻게 해야 할까?\""]], [["Oh, my goodness, the idea of asking my advice on such a point!", "아이고 맙소사, 그런 문제에 내 조언을 구하시다니요!"]], [["“Here he is, Esther,” said Mr.", "\"릭으로 말하자면, 에스더,\""], ["Jarndyce, comfortably putting his hands into his pockets and stretching out his legs.", "잔다이스 씨가 편안히 두 손을 호주머니에 찌르고 다리를 쭉 뻗으며 말했습니다."], ["“He must have a profession; he must make some choice for himself.", "\"직업을 가져야 해. 스스로 어떤 선택을 해야 하지."], ["There will be a world more wiglomeration about it, I suppose, but it must be done.”", "그 일에도 가발법석이 한바탕 더 벌어지겠지만, 아무튼 해야 할 일이야.\""]], [["“More what, guardian?”", "\"무슨 법석이라고요, 후견인님?\""], ["said I.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“More wiglomeration,”", "\"가발법석 말이다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“It’s the only name I know for the thing.", "\"그 물건을 부를 이름은 그것밖에 모르겠구나."], ["He is a ward in Chancery, my dear.", "그 아이는 대법원의 피후견인이란다, 얘야."], ["Kenge and Carboy will have something to say about it; Master Somebody—a sort of ridiculous sexton, digging graves for the merits of causes in a back room at the end of Quality Court, Chancery Lane—will have something to say about it; counsel will have something to say about it; the Chancellor will have something to say about it; the satellites will have something to say about it; they will all have to be handsomely fee’d, all round, about it; the whole thing will be vastly ceremonious, wordy, unsatisfactory, and expensive, and I call it, in general, wiglomeration.", "켄지와 카보이가 한마디 거들 테고, 누구누구 보좌관 나리 — 챈서리 레인 끝자락 퀄리티 코트 뒷방에서 사건의 시비를 위한 무덤이나 파는, 우스꽝스러운 묘지기 같은 작자 — 가 한마디 거들 테고, 변호인이 한마디 거들 테고, 대법관이 한마디 거들 테고, 그 위성들이 한마디 거들 테지. 그 일로 죄다 두루두루 두둑이 수임료를 챙길 테고, 모든 절차가 엄청나게 격식을 차리고, 말만 많고, 만족스럽지 못하고, 돈만 잡아먹을 테니, 나는 그걸 통틀어 가발법석이라 부른단다."], ["How mankind ever came to be afflicted with Wiglomeration, or for whose sins these young people ever fell into a pit of it, I don’t know; so it is.”", "인류가 어쩌다 가발법석이라는 재앙에 걸려들었는지, 또 이 젊은이들이 누구의 죄 때문에 그 구덩이에 빠지게 되었는지, 나는 도무지 모르겠다. 어쨌든 그런 거야.\""]], [["He began to rub his head again and to hint that he felt the wind.", "그는 다시 머리를 긁기 시작하더니 바람이 느껴진다고 넌지시 비쳤습니다."], ["But it was a delightful instance of his kindness towards me that whether he rubbed his head, or walked about, or did both, his face was sure to recover its benignant expression as it looked at mine; and he was sure to turn comfortable again and put his hands in his pockets and stretch out his legs.", "그래도 나를 향한 그의 다정함을 보여 주는 흐뭇한 본보기였던 것은, 그가 머리를 긁든, 이리저리 거닐든, 둘 다 하든 간에, 내 얼굴을 바라볼 때면 그 얼굴이 어김없이 자애로운 표정을 되찾았고, 어김없이 다시 편안해져서 두 손을 호주머니에 넣고 다리를 쭉 뻗는다는 점이었습니다."]], [["“Perhaps it would be best, first of all,”", "\"무엇보다 먼저,\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “to ask Mr. Richard what he inclines to himself.”", "\"리처드 씨한테 스스로 어느 쪽에 마음이 기우는지 물어보는 게 가장 좋겠네요.\""]], [["“Exactly so,”", "\"바로 그거야.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“That’s what I mean!", "\"내 말이 그 말이지!"], ["You know, just accustom yourself to talk it over, with your tact and in your quiet way, with him and Ada, and see what you all make of it.", "알겠니, 너의 그 눈치와 그 차분한 방식으로 그 아이며 에이다와 함께 이 일을 의논하는 데 익숙해지렴. 그러고 너희끼리 어떤 결론을 내는지 보자꾸나."], ["We are sure to come at the heart of the matter by your means, little woman.”", "네 손을 거치면 우리는 틀림없이 이 일의 핵심에 다다를 거야, 자그마한 아가씨.\""]], [["I really was frightened at the thought of the importance I was attaining and the number of things that were being confided to me.", "내가 떠맡게 된 비중과 내게 맡겨지는 일들의 수를 생각하니 정말이지 겁이 더럭 났습니다. 나는 결코 이런 걸 뜻한 게 아니었거든요."], ["I had not meant this at all; I had meant that he should speak to Richard.", "그가 리처드에게 직접 말해 주기를 바랐던 거였지요."], ["But of course I said nothing in reply except that I would do my best, though I feared (I really felt it necessary to repeat this) that he thought me much more sagacious than I was.", "하지만 물론 나는 최선을 다하겠노라는 말 외엔 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았습니다. 다만 그가 나를 실제보다 훨씬 더 슬기로운 사람으로 여기는 게 아닐까 두렵다고 (정말이지 이 말은 거듭해야 할 것 같았습니다) 덧붙였지요."], ["At which my guardian only laughed the pleasantest laugh I ever heard.", "그 말에 후견인은 그저 내가 들어 본 중에 가장 유쾌한 웃음을 터뜨릴 뿐이었습니다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["he said, rising and pushing back his chair.", "그가 일어서며 의자를 뒤로 밀고 말했습니다."], ["“I think we may have done with the growlery for one day!", "\"오늘은 투덜방을 이만 끝내도 될 것 같구나!"], ["Only a concluding word.", "마지막으로 한마디만."], ["Esther, my dear, do you wish to ask me anything?”", "에스더, 얘야, 나한테 뭐 물어보고 싶은 게 있니?\""]], [["He looked so attentively at me that I looked attentively at him and felt sure I understood him.", "그가 어찌나 골똘히 나를 바라보던지, 나도 그를 골똘히 바라보았고, 그분의 뜻을 알아차렸다는 확신이 들었습니다."]], [["“About myself, sir?”", "\"제 자신에 관해서요, 어르신?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Guardian,”", "\"후견인님,\""], ["said I, venturing to put my hand, which was suddenly colder than I could have wished, in his, “nothing!", "나는 어느새 바라던 것보다 차가워진 손을 그의 손에 살며시 얹어 보며 말했습니다. \"아무것도요!"], ["I am quite sure that if there were anything I ought to know or had any need to know, I should not have to ask you to tell it to me.", "만약 제가 마땅히 알아야 할 게 있거나 알 필요가 있는 게 있다면, 제가 알려 달라고 청할 필요도 없으리라는 걸 저는 확신해요."], ["If my whole reliance and confidence were not placed in you, I must have a hard heart indeed.", "제 모든 의지와 믿음을 어르신께 두지 않는다면, 저는 정말이지 모진 마음을 지닌 사람일 거예요."], ["I have nothing to ask you, nothing in the world.”", "저는 여쭤 볼 게 없어요, 세상에 아무것도 없답니다.\""]], [["He drew my hand through his arm and we went away to look for Ada.", "그는 내 손을 끌어다 자기 팔짱을 끼게 하고는, 우리는 함께 에이다를 찾으러 갔습니다."], ["From that hour I felt quite easy with him, quite unreserved, quite content to know no more, quite happy.", "그 순간부터 나는 그와 함께 있는 것이 더없이 편안했고, 더없이 거리낌 없었고, 더 알 것이 없다는 데 더없이 만족했고, 더없이 행복했습니다."]], [["We lived, at first, rather a busy life at Bleak House, for we had to become acquainted with many residents in and out of the neighbourhood who knew Mr.", "처음에 우리는 황폐한 집에서 제법 분주하게 지냈습니다. 잔다이스 씨를 아는, 동네 안팎의 수많은 주민들과 안면을 터야 했거든요."], ["Jarndyce. It seemed to Ada and me that everybody knew him who wanted to do anything with anybody else’s money.", "에이다와 내가 보기에는, 남의 돈으로 무슨 일이든 해 보려는 사람이라면 누구나 그를 아는 듯했습니다."], ["It amazed us when we began to sort his letters and to answer some of them for him in the growlery of a morning to find how the great object of the lives of nearly all his correspondents appeared to be to form themselves into committees for getting in and laying out money.", "아침에 투덜방에서 그의 편지를 분류하고 그 가운데 몇 통에 대신 답장을 쓰기 시작하면서, 그와 편지를 주고받는 거의 모든 이들의 삶에서 가장 큰 목적이라는 게, 돈을 거둬들이고 또 뿌리기 위한 위원회를 꾸리는 일인 듯하다는 것을 알게 되어 우리는 깜짝 놀랐습니다."], ["The ladies were as desperate as the gentlemen; indeed, I think they were even more so.", "부인들도 신사들 못지않게 필사적이었지요. 아니, 오히려 더 필사적이었던 것 같습니다."], ["They threw themselves into committees in the most impassioned manner and collected subscriptions with a vehemence quite extraordinary.", "그분들은 더없이 격정적인 태도로 위원회에 뛰어들었고, 그야말로 유별난 열의로 기부금을 거뒀습니다."], ["It appeared to us that some of them must pass their whole lives in dealing out subscription-cards to the whole post-office directory—shilling cards, half-crown cards, half-sovereign cards, penny cards.", "우리가 보기에 그들 가운데 어떤 이들은 우체국 주소록에 실린 모든 사람에게 기부 카드를 나눠 주느라 평생을 다 보내는 게 틀림없는 듯했습니다 — 1실링짜리 카드, 반 크라운짜리 카드, 반 소브린짜리 카드, 1페니짜리 카드 말이지요."], ["They wanted everything.", "그들은 모든 걸 원했습니다."], ["They wanted wearing apparel, they wanted linen rags, they wanted money, they wanted coals, they wanted soup, they wanted interest, they wanted autographs, they wanted flannel, they wanted whatever Mr. Jarndyce had—or had not.", "입을 옷가지를 원했고, 아마포 헝겊을 원했고, 돈을 원했고, 석탄을 원했고, 수프를 원했고, 이자를 원했고, 친필 서명을 원했고, 플란넬을 원했고, 잔다이스 씨가 가진 것은 — 또는 가지지 않은 것은 — 무엇이든 원했습니다."], ["Their objects were as various as their demands.", "그들의 목적도 그 요구만큼이나 가지가지였습니다."], ["They were going to raise new buildings, they were going to pay off debts on old buildings, they were going to establish in a picturesque building (engraving of proposed west elevation attached) the Sisterhood of Mediaeval Marys, they were going to give a testimonial to Mrs. Jellyby, they were going to have their secretary’s portrait painted and presented to his mother-in-law, whose deep devotion to him was well known, they were going to get up everything, I really believe, from five hundred thousand tracts to an annuity and from a marble monument to a silver tea-pot.", "새 건물을 세우려 했고, 옛 건물의 빚을 갚으려 했고, 그림처럼 아름다운 건물(서쪽 정면 예정도 동판화 첨부)에 '중세 마리아회'를 세우려 했고, 젤리비 부인에게 공로패를 바치려 했고, 자기네 간사의 초상화를 그려 그를 향한 깊은 헌신으로 잘 알려진 그의 장모에게 선사하려 했고, 정말이지 제 생각엔 오십만 부의 소책자에서부터 연금에 이르기까지, 또 대리석 기념비에서부터 은제 찻주전자에 이르기까지 온갖 것을 마련하려 했습니다."], ["They took a multitude of titles.", "그들은 수많은 명칭을 내걸었습니다."], ["They were the Women of England, the Daughters of Britain, the Sisters of all the cardinal virtues separately, the Females of America, the Ladies of a hundred denominations.", "잉글랜드의 여인회, 브리튼의 딸들회, 일곱 가지 추요 덕목을 저마다 따로 내건 자매회, 아메리카의 여성회, 백 가지 교파의 부인회였지요."], ["They appeared to be always excited about canvassing and electing.", "그들은 늘 유세를 벌이고 선출하는 일로 흥분에 차 있는 듯했습니다."], ["They seemed to our poor wits, and according to their own accounts, to be constantly polling people by tens of thousands, yet never bringing their candidates in for anything.", "우리의 변변찮은 머리로 보기엔, 또 그들 스스로 떠벌리는 바로도, 그들은 끊임없이 수만 명을 상대로 투표를 벌이면서도 정작 자기네 후보를 무엇 하나로도 당선시키는 일은 없는 듯했습니다."], ["It made our heads ache to think, on the whole, what feverish lives they must lead.", "통틀어 그들이 얼마나 열에 들뜬 삶을 살아가야 하는지를 생각하면 우리는 머리가 다 지끈거렸습니다."]], [["Among the ladies who were most distinguished for this rapacious benevolence (if I may use the expression) was a Mrs. Pardiggle, who seemed, as I judged from the number of her letters to Mr. Jarndyce, to be almost as powerful a correspondent as Mrs. Jellyby herself. We observed that the wind always changed when Mrs. Pardiggle became the subject of conversation and that it invariably interrupted Mr. Jarndyce and prevented his going any farther, when he had remarked that there were two classes of charitable people; one, the people who did a little and made a great deal of noise; the other, the people who did a great deal and made no noise at all. We were therefore curious to see Mrs. Pardiggle, suspecting her to be a type of the former class, and were glad when she called one day with her five young sons.", "이 탐욕스러운 자선(이런 표현을 써도 된다면)으로 가장 두드러진 부인들 가운데 파디글 부인이라는 분이 있었습니다. 잔다이스 씨에게 보낸 편지의 수로 미루어 짐작건대, 그분은 젤리비 부인 못지않게 강력한 편지 상대인 듯했지요. 우리는 파디글 부인이 대화의 화제로 오를 때면 어김없이 바람이 바뀐다는 것을, 그리고 자선하는 사람에는 두 부류가 있는데 — 하나는 하는 일은 보잘것없으면서 요란하게 떠드는 사람들이고, 다른 하나는 하는 일은 막대하면서 전혀 소리를 내지 않는 사람들이라는 — 그 말을 잔다이스 씨가 꺼내려 할 때면 그 바람이 어김없이 그의 말을 끊어 더 나아가지 못하게 한다는 것을 알아차렸습니다. 그래서 우리는 파디글 부인을 한번 보고 싶어졌고, 그분이 전자에 속하는 본보기이리라 짐작했는데, 어느 날 그분이 다섯 어린 아들들을 데리고 찾아왔을 때 반가웠습니다."]], [["She was a formidable style of lady with spectacles, a prominent nose, and a loud voice, who had the effect of wanting a great deal of room.", "그분은 안경을 끼고, 코가 우뚝하고, 목소리가 우렁찬, 어딘가 위압적인 유형의 부인이었는데, 자리를 한껏 많이 차지하고 싶어 하는 듯한 인상을 주었습니다."], ["And she really did, for she knocked down little chairs with her skirts that were quite a great way off.", "실제로도 그랬지요. 한참 멀찍이 떨어져 있는 작은 의자들까지 치맛자락으로 넘어뜨렸으니까요."], ["As only Ada and I were at home, we received her timidly, for she seemed to come in like cold weather and to make the little Pardiggles blue as they followed.", "마침 에이다와 나만 집에 있던 터라, 우리는 머뭇거리며 그분을 맞았습니다. 그분이 찬 날씨처럼 들어오는 듯했고, 뒤따르는 어린 파디글들을 추위에 새파랗게 질리게 만드는 듯했거든요."]], [["“These, young ladies,” said Mrs.", "\"이 아이들은요, 아가씨들,\""], ["Pardiggle with great volubility after the first salutations, “are my five boys.", "첫인사를 마친 뒤 파디글 부인이 청산유수로 말했습니다. \"제 다섯 아들입니다."], ["You may have seen their names in a printed subscription list (perhaps more than one) in the possession of our esteemed friend Mr. Jarndyce.", "우리의 존경하는 벗 잔다이스 씨가 가지고 계신 인쇄된 기부자 명단(아마 한 장이 아니라 여러 장일 거예요)에서 이 아이들 이름을 보셨을지도 모르겠네요."], ["Egbert, my eldest (twelve), is the boy who sent out his pocket-money, to the amount of five and threepence, to the Tockahoopo Indians.", "맏이 에그버트(열두 살)는 토커후포 인디언들에게 자기 용돈 5실링 3펜스를 죄다 부친 아이고요."], ["Oswald, my second (ten and a half), is the child who contributed two and nine-pence to the Great National Smithers Testimonial.", "둘째 오즈월드(열 살 반)는 위대한 국민 스미더스 기념기금에 2실링 9펜스를 보탠 아이예요."], ["Francis, my third (nine), one and sixpence halfpenny; Felix, my fourth (seven), eightpence to the Superannuated Widows; Alfred, my youngest (five), has voluntarily enrolled himself in the Infant Bonds of Joy, and is pledged never, through life, to use tobacco in any form.”", "셋째 프랜시스(아홉 살)는 1실링 6펜스 반페니를, 넷째 펠릭스(일곱 살)는 노령 과부회에 8펜스를 냈고요. 막내 앨프리드(다섯 살)는 제 발로 '유아 환희 서약단'에 가입해서, 평생토록 어떤 형태로든 담배를 입에 대지 않기로 서약했답니다.\""]], [["We had never seen such dissatisfied children.", "우리는 그렇게 불만스러운 아이들을 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["It was not merely that they were weazened and shrivelled—though they were certainly that too—but they looked absolutely ferocious with discontent.", "그저 비쩍 마르고 쪼그라들었다는 것만이 아니라 — 물론 그렇기도 했지만 — 아이들은 불만에 차서 그야말로 사납기 짝이 없어 보였습니다."], ["At the mention of the Tockahoopo Indians, I could really have supposed Egbert to be one of the most baleful members of that tribe, he gave me such a savage frown.", "토커후포 인디언 이야기가 나왔을 때, 에그버트가 어찌나 무지막지하게 인상을 찌푸리던지, 나는 정말로 그 아이가 그 부족 가운데 가장 흉포한 일원이라 여길 뻔했습니다."], ["The face of each child, as the amount of his contribution was mentioned, darkened in a peculiarly vindictive manner, but his was by far the worst.", "아이들은 저마다 자기 기부 금액이 언급될 때마다 유독 앙심에 찬 표정으로 얼굴이 어두워졌는데, 그중에서도 에그버트가 단연 제일 심했습니다."], ["I must except, however, the little recruit into the Infant Bonds of Joy, who was stolidly and evenly miserable.", "다만 '유아 환희 서약단'에 막 입단한 막내만은 예외로 둬야겠는데, 그 아이는 둔하게, 그러나 한결같이 비참한 표정이었습니다."]], [["“You have been visiting, I understand,” said Mrs.", "\"두 분이서 젤리비 부인 댁에 다녀오셨다고요?\""], ["Pardiggle, “at Mrs. Jellyby’s?”", "파디글 부인이 말했습니다."]], [["We said yes, we had passed one night there.", "우리는 그렇다고, 거기서 하룻밤을 묵었다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“Mrs. Jellyby,”", "\"젤리비 부인은요,\""], ["pursued the lady, always speaking in the same demonstrative, loud, hard tone, so that her voice impressed my fancy as if it had a sort of spectacles on too—and I may take the opportunity of remarking that her spectacles were made the less engaging by her eyes being what Ada called “choking eyes,”", "그 부인이 줄곧 똑같이 과시적이고 우렁차고 딱딱한 어조로 말을 이었는데, 어찌나 그러던지 그 목소리에마저 일종의 안경이 끼워져 있는 듯한 인상을 받았습니다. 그러고 보니 이 자리를 빌려 한마디 하자면, 그분의 안경이 한결 덜 매력적으로 보였던 까닭은 그 눈이 에이다가 '숨 막히는 눈'이라 부른, 그러니까 몹시 튀어나온 눈이었기 때문입니다."], ["meaning very prominent—“Mrs. Jellyby is a benefactor to society and deserves a helping hand.", "\"젤리비 부인은 사회의 은인이고 도와드릴 만한 분이지요."], ["My boys have contributed to the African project—Egbert, one and six, being the entire allowance of nine weeks; Oswald, one and a penny halfpenny, being the same; the rest, according to their little means.", "우리 아이들도 그 아프리카 사업에 기부를 했어요 — 에그버트가 1실링 6펜스, 이건 아홉 주치 용돈을 통째로 낸 거고요. 오즈월드가 1실링 1펜스 반페니, 역시 똑같고요."], ["Nevertheless, I do not go with Mrs.", "나머지 아이들도 제 형편껏 냈답니다."], ["Jellyby in all things. I do not go with Mrs.", "그렇긴 해도, 저는 젤리비 부인과 모든 점에서 뜻이 같진 않아요."], ["Jellyby in her treatment of her young family. It has been noticed.", "저는 젤리비 부인이 어린 자식들을 대하는 방식에는 동의하지 않습니다."], ["It has been observed that her young family are excluded from participation in the objects to which she is devoted.", "사람들이 지적했지요. 그 부인의 어린 자식들이 그분이 헌신하는 사업에 참여하지 못한다는 게 사람들 눈에 띄었던 거예요."], ["She may be right, she may be wrong; but, right or wrong, this is not my course with MY young family.", "그분이 옳을 수도, 그를 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 옳든 그르든, 그건 제 어린 자식들에 대한 제 방식은 아니랍니다."], ["I take them everywhere.”", "저는 이 아이들을 어디든 데리고 다녀요.\""]], [["I was afterwards convinced (and so was Ada) that from the ill-conditioned eldest child, these words extorted a sharp yell.", "그 말에 나는 (그리고 에이다도) 나중에 확신하게 되었지만, 저 성질 사나운 맏이에게서 날카로운 비명이 터져 나오려던 것을, 그 아이가 하품으로 둘러댄 거였습니다."], ["He turned it off into a yawn, but it began as a yell.", "하지만 시작은 분명 비명이었지요."]], [["“They attend matins with me (very prettily done) at half-past six o’clock in the morning all the year round, including of course the depth of winter,”", "\"이 아이들은 일 년 내내, 물론 한겨울에도, 아침 여섯 시 반에 저와 함께 조과에 참석한답니다(아주 예쁘게 한답니다).\""], ["said Mrs.", "파디글 부인이 빠르게 말했습니다."], ["Pardiggle rapidly, “and they are with me during the revolving duties of the day.", "\"그리고 하루 종일 돌아가며 이어지는 제 일과 내내 저와 함께하지요. 저는 학교 부인이고, 방문 부인이고, 낭독 부인이고, 배급 부인이에요."], ["I am a School lady, I am a Visiting lady, I am a Reading lady, I am a Distributing lady; I am on the local Linen Box Committee and many general committees; and my canvassing alone is very extensive—perhaps no one’s more so.", "지역 아마포 상자 위원회를 비롯해 여러 일반 위원회에도 들어 있고요. 제가 벌이는 유세만 해도 매우 광범위해서 — 아마 저만 한 사람이 없을 거예요."], ["But they are my companions everywhere; and by these means they acquire that knowledge of the poor, and that capacity of doing charitable business in general—in short, that taste for the sort of thing—which will render them in after life a service to their neighbours and a satisfaction to themselves.", "하지만 어디든 이 아이들이 저와 동무가 되어 주고, 그렇게 함으로써 가난한 이들에 대한 그 지식을, 또 자선 사업 전반을 해 나가는 그 역량을 — 한마디로 그런 일에 대한 취향을 — 익히는 거지요. 그게 훗날 이 아이들을 이웃에게 보탬이 되고 자기 자신에게 흡족함이 되는 사람으로 만들어 줄 거예요."], ["My young family are not frivolous; they expend the entire amount of their allowance in subscriptions, under my direction; and they have attended as many public meetings and listened to as many lectures, orations, and discussions as generally fall to the lot of few grown people.", "제 어린 자식들은 경박하지 않아요. 받은 용돈을 제 지도에 따라 죄다 기부에 쓰지요."], ["Alfred (five), who, as I mentioned, has of his own election joined the Infant Bonds of Joy, was one of the very few children who manifested consciousness on that occasion after a fervid address of two hours from the chairman of the evening.”", "그리고 이 아이들은 보통 어른도 몇 안 되는 사람만이 겪는 만큼 많은 공개 집회에 참석하고, 그만큼 많은 강연과 연설과 토론을 들어 왔답니다. 앞서 말씀드린, 제 발로 '유아 환희 서약단'에 가입한 앨프리드(다섯 살)는 그날 저녁 사회자가 두 시간에 걸쳐 열변을 토한 뒤에도 의식을 유지한 몇 안 되는 아이들 가운데 하나였어요.\""]], [["Alfred glowered at us as if he never could, or would, forgive the injury of that night.", "앨프리드는 그날 밤 입은 상처를 결코 용서할 수 없다는, 아니 용서하지 않겠다는 듯이 우리를 노려보았습니다."]], [["“You may have observed, Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양도 보셨을지 모르겠네요.\" 파디글 부인이 말했습니다."], ["said Mrs. Pardiggle, “in some of the lists to which I have referred, in the possession of our esteemed friend Mr.", "\"우리의 존경하는 벗 잔다이스 씨가 가지고 계신, 아까 말씀드린 그 명단들 가운데 어떤 것을 보면, 제 어린 자식들 이름 끝에 'O."], ["Jarndyce, that the names of my young family are concluded with the name of O.", "A. 파디글, 왕립학회 회원, 1파운드'라고 적혀 있을 거예요."], ["A. Pardiggle, F.R.S., one pound.", "이게 그 아이들 아버지랍니다."], ["That is their father. We usually observe the same routine.", "우리는 으레 똑같은 순서를 따르지요."], ["I put down my mite first; then my young family enrol their contributions, according to their ages and their little means; and then Mr.", "제가 먼저 푼돈을 적어 넣고, 그다음 제 어린 자식들이 나이와 형편껏 기부를 올리고, 그러고 나면 파디글 씨가 마지막을 장식해요."], ["Pardiggle brings up the rear. Mr.", "파디글 씨는 제 지도에 따라 자기 몫의 소소한 기부를 보태는 걸 기쁘게 여긴답니다."], ["Pardiggle is happy to throw in his limited donation, under my direction; and thus things are made not only pleasant to ourselves, but, we trust, improving to others.”", "이렇게 해서 우리 자신에게 유쾌할 뿐 아니라, 부디 남들에게도 보탬이 되리라 믿는 거지요.\""]], [["Suppose Mr. Pardiggle were to dine with Mr. Jellyby, and suppose Mr. Jellyby were to relieve his mind after dinner to Mr. Pardiggle, would Mr. Pardiggle, in return, make any confidential communication to Mr. Jellyby? I was quite confused to find myself thinking this, but it came into my head.", "만약 파디글 씨가 젤리비 씨와 함께 만찬을 든다면, 그리고 젤리비 씨가 만찬 뒤에 파디글 씨에게 속내를 털어놓는다면, 파디글 씨도 그 답례로 젤리비 씨에게 무슨 은밀한 이야기를 건넬까? 내가 이런 생각을 하고 있다는 걸 깨닫고 나는 무척 당황했지만, 그래도 그 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐습니다."]], [["“You are very pleasantly situated here!”", "\"여기는 참 기분 좋은 곳에 자리 잡으셨네요!\""], ["said Mrs. Pardiggle.", "파디글 부인이 말했습니다."]], [["We were glad to change the subject, and going to the window, pointed out the beauties of the prospect, on which the spectacles appeared to me to rest with curious indifference.", "우리는 화제를 바꾸게 되어 반가웠고, 창가로 가서 풍경의 아름다움을 가리켜 보였는데, 그 안경은 내가 보기에 묘하게 무심한 태도로 그 위에 머무는 듯했습니다."]], [["“You know Mr. Gusher?”", "\"거셔 씨를 아세요?\""], ["said our visitor.", "우리의 손님이 말했습니다."]], [["We were obliged to say that we had not the pleasure of Mr. Gusher’s acquaintance.", "우리는 거셔 씨를 알게 되는 기쁨은 누리지 못했다고 말할 수밖에 없었습니다."]], [["“The loss is yours, I assure you,”", "\"손해는 두 분 몫이에요, 정말이지.\""], ["said Mrs. Pardiggle with her commanding deportment.", "파디글 부인이 그 위압적인 거동으로 말했습니다."], ["“He is a very fervid, impassioned speaker—full of fire!", "\"그분은 아주 열정적이고 격정적인 연설가랍니다 — 불꽃이 넘쳐요!"], ["Stationed in a waggon on this lawn, now, which, from the shape of the land, is naturally adapted to a public meeting, he would improve almost any occasion you could mention for hours and hours!", "지금 이 잔디밭에 마차 한 대 세워 두면, 이 땅의 모양새가 자연스레 공개 집회에 딱 맞으니, 두 분이 떠올릴 수 있는 거의 어떤 자리든 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 일깨워 줄 거예요!"], ["By this time, young ladies,” said Mrs.", "자, 그런데 아가씨들,\""], ["Pardiggle, moving back to her chair and overturning, as if by invisible agency, a little round table at a considerable distance with my work-basket on it, “by this time you have found me out, I dare say?”", "파디글 부인이 자기 의자로 물러가면서, 마치 보이지 않는 손에라도 이끌린 듯 제법 멀찍이 놓인, 내 반짇고리가 얹힌 작고 둥근 탁자를 넘어뜨리며 말했습니다. \"이쯤 되면 두 분도 저를 간파하셨겠지요?\""]], [["This was really such a confusing question that Ada looked at me in perfect dismay.", "이건 정말이지 어찌나 헷갈리는 질문이던지, 에이다가 어쩔 줄 모르고 나를 쳐다보았습니다."], ["As to the guilty nature of my own consciousness after what I had been thinking, it must have been expressed in the colour of my cheeks.", "내가 방금 했던 생각 탓에 마음 한구석이 켕겼던 것으로 말하자면, 분명 내 뺨의 빛깔에 고스란히 드러났을 겁니다."]], [["“Found out, I mean,” said Mrs.", "\"간파하셨다는 건, 제 말은,\""], ["Pardiggle, “the prominent point in my character.", "파디글 부인이 말했습니다. \"제 성격의 두드러진 점 말이에요."], ["I am aware that it is so prominent as to be discoverable immediately.", "그게 워낙 두드러져서 단박에 알아차리실 수 있다는 걸 저도 압니다."], ["I lay myself open to detection, I know.", "제가 간파당하기 좋게 스스로를 드러내 놓고 있다는 걸 알지요."], ["Well!", "뭐!"], ["I freely admit, I am a woman of business.", "솔직히 인정하건대, 저는 일하는 여자예요."], ["I love hard work; I enjoy hard work.", "저는 고된 일을 사랑하고, 고된 일을 즐깁니다."], ["The excitement does me good.", "그 흥분이 제겐 보약이지요."], ["I am so accustomed and inured to hard work that I don’t know what fatigue is.”", "고된 일에 어찌나 길들고 단련되었는지, 저는 피로가 뭔지를 모른답니다.\""]], [["We murmured that it was very astonishing and very gratifying, or something to that effect.", "우리는 그것 참 놀랍고도 참 흐뭇한 일이라거나, 뭐 그런 비슷한 말을 웅얼거렸습니다."], ["I don’t think we knew what it was either, but this is what our politeness expressed.", "그게 무슨 말인지 우리도 잘 몰랐던 것 같지만, 우리의 예의가 표현한 것은 그런 거였지요."]], [["“I do not understand what it is to be tired; you cannot tire me if you try!”", "\"저는 피곤하다는 게 뭔지 모릅니다. 아무리 애를 써도 저를 지치게 할 순 없어요!\""], ["said Mrs. Pardiggle.", "파디글 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“The quantity of exertion (which is no exertion to me), the amount of business (which I regard as nothing), that I go through sometimes astonishes myself. I have seen my young family, and Mr.", "\"제가 때때로 치러 내는 그 노력의 양(저에게는 노력 축에도 못 들지만)과 그 일의 분량(저는 아무것도 아니라 여기는)을 보면 저 스스로도 깜짝 놀랄 정도랍니다."], ["Pardiggle, quite worn out with witnessing it, when I may truly say I have been as fresh as a lark!”", "제 어린 자식들과 파디글 씨가 그걸 지켜보는 것만으로 녹초가 되어 버린 걸 본 적도 있어요. 그때 제가 종달새처럼 팔팔했다고 한들 거짓이 아니지요!\""]], [["If that dark-visaged eldest boy could look more malicious than he had already looked, this was the time when he did it.", "그 거무튀튀한 얼굴의 맏이가 이미 보여 준 것보다 더 악의에 찬 표정을 지을 수 있었다면, 바로 그 순간이 그랬습니다."], ["I observed that he doubled his right fist and delivered a secret blow into the crown of his cap, which was under his left arm.", "나는 그 아이가 오른쪽 주먹을 불끈 쥐고, 왼쪽 겨드랑이에 끼고 있던 제 모자 꼭대기에 은밀히 한 방 먹이는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["“This gives me a great advantage when I am making my rounds,”", "\"이게 순회를 돌 때 제겐 큰 강점이 되지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Pardiggle.", "파디글 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“If I find a person unwilling to hear what I have to say, I tell that person directly, ‘I am incapable of fatigue, my good friend, I am never tired, and I mean to go on until I have done.’ It answers admirably!", "\"제 이야기를 듣기 싫어하는 사람을 만나면, 저는 그 사람한테 곧장 이렇게 말해요. '여보세요, 저는 피로를 모르는 사람이라, 절대 지치지 않고, 다 마칠 때까지 계속할 작정이랍니다.'"], ["Miss Summerson, I hope I shall have your assistance in my visiting rounds immediately, and Miss Clare’s very soon.”", "그러면 기막히게 통한다니까요! 서머슨 양, 제 방문 순회에 어서 양의 도움을 받을 수 있길 바라고요, 클레어 양의 도움도 머지않아 받았으면 해요.\""]], [["At first I tried to excuse myself for the present on the general ground of having occupations to attend to which I must not neglect.", "처음에 나는 소홀히 해선 안 될 일거리가 있다는 일반적인 핑계로 지금은 사양하려고 했습니다."], ["But as this was an ineffectual protest, I then said, more particularly, that I was not sure of my qualifications.", "하지만 그것이 먹히지 않는 항변이 되자, 그다음엔 좀 더 구체적으로, 내가 그 자질을 갖추었는지 확신이 서지 않는다고 말했습니다."], ["That I was inexperienced in the art of adapting my mind to minds very differently situated, and addressing them from suitable points of view.", "처지가 사뭇 다른 사람들의 마음에 내 마음을 맞추고, 적절한 관점에서 그들에게 말을 건네는 기술에는 내가 미숙하다고요."], ["That I had not that delicate knowledge of the heart which must be essential to such a work.", "그런 일에 반드시 갖추어야 할, 사람 마음에 대한 그 섬세한 통찰이 내겐 없다고요."], ["That I had much to learn, myself, before I could teach others, and that I could not confide in my good intentions alone.", "남을 가르치기에 앞서 나부터 배워야 할 것이 많고, 내 선한 의도만 믿고 나설 수는 없다고요."], ["For these reasons I thought it best to be as useful as I could, and to render what kind services I could to those immediately about me, and to try to let that circle of duty gradually and naturally expand itself.", "이런 까닭에, 나는 내가 할 수 있는 한 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고, 바로 곁에 있는 사람들에게 내가 할 수 있는 친절을 베풀며, 그 의무의 테두리가 차츰 자연스레 넓어지도록 두는 게 가장 좋겠다고 여긴다고요."], ["All this I said with anything but confidence, because Mrs.", "이 모든 말을, 나는 자신감이라곤 도무지 없이 했습니다."], ["Pardiggle was much older than I, and had great experience, and was so very military in her manners.", "파디글 부인이 나보다 한참 연상인 데다, 경험이 많고, 거동이 어찌나 군대식이던지요."]], [["“You are wrong, Miss Summerson,”", "\"그건 양이 틀렸어요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said she, “but perhaps you are not equal to hard work or the excitement of it, and that makes a vast difference.", "그분이 말했습니다. \"하지만 어쩌면 양은 고된 일이나 그 흥분을 감당할 그릇이 못 되나 보군요."], ["If you would like to see how I go through my work, I am now about—with my young family—to visit a brickmaker in the neighbourhood (a very bad character) and shall be glad to take you with me.", "그렇다면야 사정이 영 다르지요. 제가 어떻게 일을 해내는지 보고 싶다면, 마침 지금 제가 — 어린 자식들과 함께 — 이 동네의 어떤 벽돌공(아주 나쁜 위인이지요)을 방문하러 가는 길이니, 양을 데려가면 기쁘겠어요."], ["Miss Clare also, if she will do me the favour.”", "클레어 양도 청을 들어주신다면요.\""]], [["Ada and I interchanged looks, and as we were going out in any case, accepted the offer.", "에이다와 나는 눈빛을 주고받았고, 어차피 나가려던 참이었으므로 그 제안을 받아들였습니다."], ["When we hastily returned from putting on our bonnets, we found the young family languishing in a corner and Mrs. Pardiggle sweeping about the room, knocking down nearly all the light objects it contained.", "우리가 보닛을 챙겨 쓰고 부랴부랴 돌아와 보니, 어린 자식들은 한구석에서 풀이 죽어 늘어져 있었고, 파디글 부인은 방 안을 휩쓸고 다니며 그 안에 있던 가벼운 물건들을 거의 죄다 넘어뜨리고 있었습니다."], ["Mrs. Pardiggle took possession of Ada, and I followed with the family.", "파디글 부인이 에이다를 차지했고, 나는 그 아이들을 거느리고 뒤를 따랐습니다."]], [["Ada told me afterwards that Mrs.", "나중에 에이다가 들려준 바로는, 파디글 부인은 벽돌공의 집에 이르는 내내 똑같이 우렁찬 어조로(사실 나도 어쩌다 엿들었지요) 어떤 흥분되는 다툼 이야기를 늘어놓았다고 합니다."], ["Pardiggle talked in the same loud tone (that, indeed, I overheard) all the way to the brickmaker’s about an exciting contest which she had for two or three years waged against another lady relative to the bringing in of their rival candidates for a pension somewhere.", "어딘가의 연금을 두고 자기네 경쟁 후보를 내세우는 문제로 다른 부인과 이삼 년 동안 벌여 온 다툼이었지요."], ["There had been a quantity of printing, and promising, and proxying, and polling, and it appeared to have imparted great liveliness to all concerned, except the pensioners—who were not elected yet.", "그동안 어마어마한 양의 인쇄물과 약속과 위임장과 투표가 오갔고, 그 일은 관련된 모든 이에게 큰 활기를 불어넣은 듯했습니다 — 정작 그 연금 수급자들만 빼고요. 그들은 아직 선출되지도 않았으니까요."]], [["I am very fond of being confided in by children and am happy in being usually favoured in that respect, but on this occasion it gave me great uneasiness.", "나는 아이들이 내게 속내를 털어놓는 것을 무척 좋아하고, 대개 그런 호의를 누리는 것을 기쁘게 여깁니다만, 이번만은 그것이 나를 몹시 불편하게 했습니다."], ["As soon as we were out of doors, Egbert, with the manner of a little footpad, demanded a shilling of me on the ground that his pocket-money was “boned” from him.", "우리가 집 밖으로 나오자마자 에그버트가 꼬마 노상강도 같은 태도로 나한테 1실링을 내놓으라고 졸랐는데, 자기 용돈을 \"뺏겼다\"는 게 그 이유였습니다."], ["On my pointing out the great impropriety of the word, especially in connexion with his parent (for he added sulkily “By her!”), he pinched me and said, “Oh, then!", "내가 그 말이 몹시 부적절하다고, 더구나 제 부모와 관련해서는 더더욱(아이가 시무룩하게 \"엄마한테!\"라고 덧붙였거든요) 그렇다고 일러 주자, 그 아이는 나를 꼬집으며 말했습니다. \"오, 그럼!"], ["Now!", "어디!"], ["Who are you!", "누나가 뭔데!"], ["YOU wouldn’t like it, I think?", "누나라도 싫을걸, 안 그래?"], ["What does she make a sham for, and pretend to give me money, and take it away again?", "엄마는 뭣 하러 속임수를 써서 돈을 주는 척하고는 다시 빼앗아 가는 거야?"], ["Why do you call it my allowance, and never let me spend it?”", "왜 그걸 내 용돈이라고 부르면서 한 번도 못 쓰게 하느냐고?\""], ["These exasperating questions so inflamed his mind and the minds of Oswald and Francis that they all pinched me at once, and in a dreadfully expert way—screwing up such little pieces of my arms that I could hardly forbear crying out.", "이 약 올리는 질문들에 그 아이의 마음과 오즈월드, 프랜시스의 마음까지 어찌나 달아올랐던지, 셋이 한꺼번에 나를 꼬집었는데, 그 솜씨가 어찌나 능수능란하던지 — 내 팔의 살점을 어찌나 야무지게 비틀어 대던지, 나는 비명을 참기 힘들 지경이었습니다."], ["Felix, at the same time, stamped upon my toes.", "펠릭스는 그와 동시에 내 발가락을 짓밟았고요."], ["And the Bond of Joy, who on account of always having the whole of his little income anticipated stood in fact pledged to abstain from cakes as well as tobacco, so swelled with grief and rage when we passed a pastry-cook’s shop that he terrified me by becoming purple.", "그리고 늘 제 쥐꼬리만 한 수입을 죄다 미리 당겨 써 버린 탓에 사실상 담배뿐 아니라 과자까지 끊기로 서약한 신세인 그 환희의 서약단원은, 우리가 어느 제과점 앞을 지날 때 슬픔과 분노로 어찌나 부풀어 오르던지 얼굴이 시뻘게져서 나를 겁에 질리게 했습니다."], ["I never underwent so much, both in body and mind, in the course of a walk with young people as from these unnaturally constrained children when they paid me the compliment of being natural.", "나는 평생 젊은이들과 산책하면서, 이렇게 부자연스럽게 옥죄어진 아이들이 자연스러워졌답시고 내게 베푼 그 호의에서만큼 몸과 마음으로 그토록 시달려 본 적이 없습니다."]], [["I was glad when we came to the brickmaker’s house, though it was one of a cluster of wretched hovels in a brick-field, with pigsties close to the broken windows and miserable little gardens before the doors growing nothing but stagnant pools.", "벽돌공의 집에 다다랐을 때, 나는 반가웠습니다. 비록 그 집이 벽돌 밭에 다닥다닥 모여 선 비참한 오두막 무리 가운데 하나로, 깨진 창문 바로 옆에 돼지우리가 붙어 있고, 문 앞 초라한 텃밭에는 고인 물웅덩이밖에 자라는 게 없었지만요."], ["Here and there an old tub was put to catch the droppings of rain-water from a roof, or they were banked up with mud into a little pond like a large dirt-pie.", "여기저기 낡은 통을 놓아 지붕에서 떨어지는 빗물을 받기도 했고, 흙으로 둑을 쌓아 큼지막한 진흙 파이 같은 작은 웅덩이를 만들어 두기도 했습니다."], ["At the doors and windows some men and women lounged or prowled about, and took little notice of us except to laugh to one another or to say something as we passed about gentlefolks minding their own business and not troubling their heads and muddying their shoes with coming to look after other people’s.", "문가와 창가에서 남자며 여자 몇이 어슬렁거리거나 서성였는데, 우리에겐 별다른 관심을 두지 않은 채 그저 자기들끼리 웃거나, 우리가 지나가는 동안 점잖은 양반들이 제 일이나 챙길 것이지 쓸데없이 머리 싸매고 신발에 진흙 묻혀 가며 남의 일이나 들여다보러 온다는 둥 무어라 떠들 뿐이었습니다."]], [["Mrs. Pardiggle, leading the way with a great show of moral determination and talking with much volubility about the untidy habits of the people (though I doubted if the best of us could have been tidy in such a place), conducted us into a cottage at the farthest corner, the ground-floor room of which we nearly filled.", "파디글 부인은 도덕적 결의를 한껏 드러내며 앞장을 섰고, 사람들의 지저분한 생활 습관에 대해 청산유수로 떠들면서(하기야 그런 곳에서야 우리 가운데 누군들 깔끔할 수 있었을까 싶었지만), 우리를 가장 외진 구석의 오두막으로 이끌었습니다."], ["Besides ourselves, there were in this damp, offensive room a woman with a black eye, nursing a poor little gasping baby by the fire; a man, all stained with clay and mud and looking very dissipated, lying at full length on the ground, smoking a pipe; a powerful young man fastening a collar on a dog; and a bold girl doing some kind of washing in very dirty water.", "그 일층 방을 우리는 거의 가득 채우다시피 했지요. 우리 말고도 이 눅눅하고 역겨운 방 안에는, 눈에 멍이 든 여자가 불 곁에서 숨을 헐떡이는 가엾은 갓난아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었고, 진흙과 흙투성이에 몹시 방탕해 보이는 남자가 바닥에 큰대자로 누워 파이프를 빨고 있었으며, 우람한 젊은 사내가 개에게 목줄을 채우고 있었고, 당돌해 보이는 처녀가 몹시 더러운 물로 빨래 같은 걸 하고 있었습니다."], ["They all looked up at us as we came in, and the woman seemed to turn her face towards the fire as if to hide her bruised eye; nobody gave us any welcome.", "우리가 들어서자 그들은 모두 우리를 올려다보았고, 그 여자는 멍든 눈을 가리려는 듯 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 돌리는 듯했습니다. 아무도 우리를 반겨 주지 않았습니다."]], [["“Well, my friends,” said Mrs.", "\"자, 여러분.\""], ["Pardiggle, but her voice had not a friendly sound, I thought; it was much too business-like and systematic.", "파디글 부인이 말했는데, 그 목소리에는 다정한 구석이라곤 없는 듯했습니다. 내 생각엔 너무 사무적이고 체계적이었지요."], ["“How do you do, all of you?", "\"다들 어떻게 지내세요?"], ["I am here again.", "제가 또 왔답니다."], ["I told you, you couldn’t tire me, you know.", "저를 지치게 할 순 없다고 말씀드렸지요."], ["I am fond of hard work, and am true to my word.”", "저는 고된 일을 좋아하고, 한번 한 말은 꼭 지키는 사람이거든요.\""]], [["“There an’t,”", "\"더 들어올 사람은,\""], ["growled the man on the floor, whose head rested on his hand as he stared at us, “any more on you to come in, is there?”", "바닥에 누운 남자가 한 손으로 머리를 괴고 우리를 빤히 노려보며 으르렁거렸습니다. \"없는 거요, 응?\""]], [["“No, my friend,” said Mrs.", "\"없어요, 여보세요.\""], ["Pardiggle, seating herself on one stool and knocking down another.", "파디글 부인이 의자 하나에 앉으면서 다른 하나를 넘어뜨리며 말했습니다."], ["“We are all here.”", "\"우리가 다예요.\""]], [["“Because I thought there warn’t enough of you, perhaps?”", "\"하기야 당신네 머릿수가 좀 모자라지 않나 싶었거든.\""], ["said the man, with his pipe between his lips as he looked round upon us.", "남자가 입에 파이프를 문 채 우리를 둘러보며 말했습니다."]], [["The young man and the girl both laughed.", "젊은 사내와 처녀가 둘 다 웃었습니다."], ["Two friends of the young man, whom we had attracted to the doorway and who stood there with their hands in their pockets, echoed the laugh noisily.", "우리가 문간으로 끌어들였던, 호주머니에 손을 찌른 채 거기 서 있던 그 젊은 사내의 친구 둘도 요란하게 따라 웃었습니다."]], [["“You can’t tire me, good people,” said Mrs.", "\"여러분은 저를 지치게 할 수 없어요, 착한 분들.\""], ["Pardiggle to these latter.", "파디글 부인이 그 친구들에게 말했습니다."], ["“I enjoy hard work, and the harder you make mine, the better I like it.”", "\"저는 고된 일을 즐기고, 여러분이 제 일을 고되게 만들수록 저는 더 좋답니다.\""]], [["“Then make it easy for her!”", "\"그럼 부인 일이나 수월하게 해 주쇼!\""], ["growled the man upon the floor.", "바닥에 누운 남자가 으르렁거렸습니다."], ["“I wants it done, and over.", "\"난 그게 끝나길, 다 끝나길 바란단 말이오."], ["I wants a end of these liberties took with my place.", "내 집에서 이런 멋대로 굴기가 끝나길 바라요."], ["I wants an end of being drawed like a badger.", "오소리처럼 끌려 다니는 신세가 끝나길 바란단 말이오."], ["Now you’re a-going to poll-pry and question according to custom—I know what you’re a-going to be up to.", "자, 이제 당신은 늘 하던 대로 투표 캐고 꼬치꼬치 캐물을 작정이겠지 — 무슨 수작을 부릴지 다 안다고."], ["Well!", "흥!"], ["You haven’t got no occasion to be up to it.", "그럴 거 없어요. 내가 수고를 덜어 주지."], ["I’ll save you the trouble.", "내 딸이 빨래 중이냐고?"], ["Is my daughter a-washin?", "그래, 빨래 중이오."], ["Yes, she IS a-washin.", "저 물 좀 보쇼."], ["Look at the water.", "냄새도 맡아 보고!"], ["Smell it! That’s wot we drinks.", "저게 우리가 마시는 물이오."], ["How do you like it, and what do you think of gin instead!", "어떻소, 마음에 드쇼, 차라리 진을 마시는 게 어떨 것 같소!"], ["An’t my place dirty?", "내 집이 더럽지 않으냐고?"], ["Yes, it is dirty—it’s nat’rally dirty, and it’s nat’rally onwholesome; and we’ve had five dirty and onwholesome children, as is all dead infants, and so much the better for them, and for us besides.", "그래, 더럽소 — 본래부터 더럽고, 본래부터 몸에 안 좋아요. 그리고 우린 더럽고 몸에 안 좋은 애를 다섯이나 뒀는데, 죄다 갓난쟁이로 죽었지."], ["Have I read the little book wot you left?", "그게 그 애들한텐, 그리고 우리한테도 차라리 나아요."], ["No, I an’t read the little book wot you left.", "당신이 두고 간 그 작은 책 읽었느냐고?"], ["There an’t nobody here as knows how to read it; and if there wos, it wouldn’t be suitable to me.", "아니, 당신이 두고 간 작은 책 안 읽었소. 여긴 그걸 읽을 줄 아는 사람이 아무도 없고, 설령 있다 한들 나한텐 안 맞아요."], ["It’s a book fit for a babby, and I’m not a babby.", "그건 갓난쟁이한테나 맞는 책인데, 난 갓난쟁이가 아니거든."], ["If you was to leave me a doll, I shouldn’t nuss it.", "당신이 나한테 인형을 두고 간들, 난 그걸 어르지 않을 거요."], ["How have I been conducting of myself?", "내가 어떻게 처신해 왔느냐고?"], ["Why, I’ve been drunk for three days; and I’da been drunk four if I’da had the money.", "흥, 사흘 동안 곤드레만드레 취해 있었지. 돈만 있었으면 나흘은 취해 있었을 거요."], ["Don’t I never mean for to go to church?", "교회에 갈 생각은 도통 없느냐고?"], ["No, I don’t never mean for to go to church.", "그래, 교회에 갈 생각 따윈 도통 없소."], ["I shouldn’t be expected there, if I did; the beadle’s too gen-teel for me.", "간들 거기서 날 반기지도 않을 거고. 교회지기가 나한텐 너무 점잖으셔서 말이오."], ["And how did my wife get that black eye?", "그래, 내 마누라가 저 멍든 눈은 어쩌다 그리됐느냐고?"], ["Why, I give it her; and if she says I didn’t, she’s a Lie!”", "흥, 내가 그랬소. 마누라가 아니라고 한다면, 그건 거짓말이지!\""]], [["He had pulled his pipe out of his mouth to say all this, and he now turned over on his other side and smoked again. Mrs.", "이 말을 죄다 쏟아내려고 그는 입에서 파이프를 빼냈다가, 이제 반대쪽으로 돌아누우며 다시 파이프를 빨았습니다."], ["Pardiggle, who had been regarding him through her spectacles with a forcible composure, calculated, I could not help thinking, to increase his antagonism, pulled out a good book as if it were a constable’s staff and took the whole family into custody.", "파디글 부인은 안경 너머로 그를 바라보며, 내 생각엔 그의 적개심을 한층 부추기기에 딱 알맞은 강압적인 침착함을 짓고 있다가, 마치 순경의 곤봉이라도 되는 양 좋은 책 한 권을 꺼내 들고는 온 식구를 구금했습니다."], ["I mean into religious custody, of course; but she really did it as if she were an inexorable moral policeman carrying them all off to a station-house.", "물론 종교적 구금이라는 뜻이지만, 정말이지 그분의 거동은 마치 무자비한 도덕 경찰이 그들을 죄다 유치장으로 끌고 가는 것만 같았습니다."]], [["Ada and I were very uncomfortable.", "에이다와 나는 몹시 거북했습니다."], ["We both felt intrusive and out of place, and we both thought that Mrs. Pardiggle would have got on infinitely better if she had not had such a mechanical way of taking possession of people.", "우리는 둘 다 주제넘게 끼어든 데다 자리에 어울리지 않는다고 느꼈고, 둘 다 파디글 부인이 사람을 그렇게 기계적으로 휘어잡는 방식만 아니었다면 한없이 일이 더 잘 풀렸으리라 생각했습니다."], ["The children sulked and stared; the family took no notice of us whatever, except when the young man made the dog bark, which he usually did when Mrs.", "아이들은 시무룩하게 노려보았고, 그 식구들은 우리에겐 전혀 아랑곳하지 않았습니다."], ["Pardiggle was most emphatic.", "다만 그 젊은 사내가 개를 짖게 만들 때만 빼고요."], ["We both felt painfully sensible that between us and these people there was an iron barrier which could not be removed by our new friend.", "그는 파디글 부인이 가장 힘주어 말할 때면 으레 그렇게 하곤 했습니다. 우리는 둘 다 우리와 이 사람들 사이에 우리의 새 벗이 결코 치울 수 없는 쇠 장벽이 가로놓여 있음을 가슴 아프게 절감했습니다."], ["By whom or how it could be removed, we did not know, but we knew that.", "그것이 누구의 손으로 어떻게 치워질 수 있는지는 알 수 없었지만, 그것만은 알았지요."], ["Even what she read and said seemed to us to be ill-chosen for such auditors, if it had been imparted ever so modestly and with ever so much tact.", "그분이 읽고 말한 내용조차, 설령 아무리 겸손하게, 아무리 큰 눈치를 가지고 전했다 한들, 우리가 보기엔 그런 청중에게는 영 잘못 고른 것 같았습니다."], ["As to the little book to which the man on the floor had referred, we acquired a knowledge of it afterwards, and Mr. Jarndyce said he doubted if Robinson Crusoe could have read it, though he had had no other on his desolate island.", "바닥에 누운 남자가 말한 그 작은 책으로 말하자면, 우리는 나중에 그것에 대해 알게 되었는데, 잔다이스 씨는 로빈슨 크루소가 그 외딴섬에 다른 책이라곤 그것뿐이었다 한들 과연 그 책을 읽었을지 의심스럽다고 했습니다."]], [["We were much relieved, under these circumstances, when Mrs. Pardiggle left off.", "이런 형편에서, 파디글 부인이 입을 다물었을 때 우리는 무척 안도했습니다."]], [["The man on the floor, then turning his head round again, said morosely, “Well!", "그러자 바닥에 누운 남자가 다시 고개를 돌리고는 퉁명스럽게 말했습니다."], ["You’ve done, have you?”", "\"흥! 다 끝났소, 응?\""]], [["“For to-day, I have, my friend.", "\"오늘은 끝났어요, 여보세요."], ["But I am never fatigued.", "하지만 저는 결코 지치는 법이 없답니다."], ["I shall come to you again in your regular order,”", "정해진 순서에 따라 다시 찾아오지요.\""], ["returned Mrs. Pardiggle with demonstrative cheerfulness.", "파디글 부인이 과시적인 명랑함으로 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“So long as you goes now,”", "\"지금 가 주기만 한다면야,\""], ["said he, folding his arms and shutting his eyes with an oath, “you may do wot you like!”", "그가 팔짱을 끼고 욕설과 함께 눈을 감으며 말했습니다. \"당신 맘대로 하쇼!\""]], [["Mrs. Pardiggle accordingly rose and made a little vortex in the confined room from which the pipe itself very narrowly escaped.", "그러자 파디글 부인이 일어서며 그 비좁은 방 안에 작은 소용돌이를 일으켰는데, 그 통에 파이프조차 가까스로 화를 면했습니다."], ["Taking one of her young family in each hand, and telling the others to follow closely, and expressing her hope that the brickmaker and all his house would be improved when she saw them next, she then proceeded to another cottage.", "어린 자식들을 양손에 하나씩 잡고, 나머지에게는 바싹 따라오라 이르며, 다음에 찾아올 때는 벽돌공과 그 온 식구가 나아져 있기를 바란다는 말을 남기고, 그분은 또 다른 오두막으로 향했습니다."], ["I hope it is not unkind in me to say that she certainly did make, in this as in everything else, a show that was not conciliatory of doing charity by wholesale and of dealing in it to a large extent.", "내가 이런 말을 하는 게 박정한 짓이 아니길 바랍니다만, 그분은 이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 분명, 자선을 도매로 베풀고 그것을 대규모로 거래한다는, 결코 사람의 마음을 누그러뜨리지 못하는 그런 과시를 한 것은 사실입니다."]], [["She supposed that we were following her, but as soon as the space was left clear, we approached the woman sitting by the fire to ask if the baby were ill.", "그분은 우리가 따라오는 줄 알았지만, 공간이 비자마자 우리는 불 곁에 앉은 여자에게 다가가 아기가 아픈지 물어보았습니다."]], [["She only looked at it as it lay on her lap.", "그 여자는 무릎에 누운 아기를 그저 바라보기만 했습니다."], ["We had observed before that when she looked at it she covered her discoloured eye with her hand, as though she wished to separate any association with noise and violence and ill treatment from the poor little child.", "우리는 앞서, 그 여자가 아기를 바라볼 때면 멍든 눈을 손으로 가린다는 걸 눈여겨보았지요. 마치 그 가엾은 어린것에게서, 소란과 폭력과 학대에 얽힌 일체의 기억을 떼어 놓고 싶다는 듯이 말입니다."]], [["Ada, whose gentle heart was moved by its appearance, bent down to touch its little face.", "그 모습에 다정한 마음이 움직인 에이다가 허리를 굽혀 아기의 작은 얼굴을 만지려 했습니다."], ["As she did so, I saw what happened and drew her back.", "그러는 순간, 나는 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보고 그녀를 끌어당겼습니다."], ["The child died.", "아기가 숨을 거둔 것이었습니다."]], [["“Oh, Esther!”", "\"오, 에스더!\""], ["cried Ada, sinking on her knees beside it.", "에이다가 아기 곁에 무릎을 꿇으며 외쳤습니다."], ["“Look here!", "\"이것 좀 봐!"], ["Oh, Esther, my love, the little thing!", "오, 에스더, 내 사랑, 이 가엾은 것!"], ["The suffering, quiet, pretty little thing!", "이 고생하던, 얌전한, 어여쁜 작은 것!"], ["I am so sorry for it.", "너무 가여워."], ["I am so sorry for the mother.", "저 어머니도 너무 가여워."], ["I never saw a sight so pitiful as this before!", "나는 평생 이토록 애처로운 광경을 본 적이 없어!"], ["Oh, baby, baby!”", "오, 아기야, 아기야!\""]], [["Such compassion, such gentleness, as that with which she bent down weeping and put her hand upon the mother’s might have softened any mother’s heart that ever beat.", "그렇게 울며 허리를 굽혀 어머니의 손 위에 제 손을 얹는 그 연민, 그 다정함은, 일찍이 뛰어 본 그 어떤 어머니의 마음이라도 누그러뜨릴 만했습니다."], ["The woman at first gazed at her in astonishment and then burst into tears.", "그 여자는 처음엔 놀라 그녀를 바라보더니, 이내 울음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["Presently I took the light burden from her lap, did what I could to make the baby’s rest the prettier and gentler, laid it on a shelf, and covered it with my own handkerchief.", "이윽고 나는 그 여자의 무릎에서 가벼운 짐을 받아 들고, 아기의 안식을 한결 더 어여쁘고 평온하게 해 주려고 할 수 있는 일을 한 다음, 선반 위에 눕히고 내 손수건으로 덮어 주었습니다."], ["We tried to comfort the mother, and we whispered to her what Our Saviour said of children.", "우리는 어머니를 위로하려 애쓰며, 우리 구주께서 어린아이들에 대해 하신 말씀을 그 여자에게 속삭였습니다."], ["She answered nothing, but sat weeping—weeping very much.", "여자는 아무 대답도 없이 그저 앉아 울기만 했습니다 — 하염없이 울었습니다."]], [["When I turned, I found that the young man had taken out the dog and was standing at the door looking in upon us with dry eyes, but quiet.", "내가 돌아보니, 그 젊은 사내가 개를 데리고 나갔다가 문가에 서서 마른 눈으로, 그러나 차분히 우리를 들여다보고 있었습니다."], ["The girl was quiet too and sat in a corner looking on the ground.", "처녀도 차분히 한구석에 앉아 바닥을 내려다보고 있었습니다."], ["The man had risen.", "남자는 일어나 있었습니다."], ["He still smoked his pipe with an air of defiance, but he was silent.", "그는 여전히 반항하는 기색으로 파이프를 빨고 있었지만, 입은 다물고 있었지요."]], [["An ugly woman, very poorly clothed, hurried in while I was glancing at them, and coming straight up to the mother, said, “Jenny!", "내가 그들을 둘러보는 사이, 행색이 몹시 초라한 못생긴 여자 하나가 황급히 들어오더니, 곧장 그 어머니에게 다가가 말했습니다."], ["Jenny!”", "\"제니!"], ["The mother rose on being so addressed and fell upon the woman’s neck.", "제니!\" 그렇게 불린 어머니가 일어나 그 여자의 목에 매달려 안겼습니다."]], [["She also had upon her face and arms the marks of ill usage.", "이 여자 역시 얼굴과 팔에 학대당한 자국이 있었습니다."], ["She had no kind of grace about her, but the grace of sympathy; but when she condoled with the woman, and her own tears fell, she wanted no beauty.", "그녀에게는 동정심이라는 우아함 말고는 아무런 우아함도 없었습니다. 하지만 그녀가 그 어머니를 위로하며 제 눈에서도 눈물이 떨어질 때, 그녀에게는 더 이상 아름다움이 필요치 않았습니다."], ["I say condoled, but her only words were “Jenny! Jenny!”", "위로했다고는 했지만, 그녀가 한 말이라곤 \"제니!"], ["All the rest was in the tone in which she said them.", "제니!\"뿐이었습니다. 나머지는 모두 그 말을 내뱉는 어조에 담겨 있었지요."]], [["I thought it very touching to see these two women, coarse and shabby and beaten, so united; to see what they could be to one another; to see how they felt for one another, how the heart of each to each was softened by the hard trials of their lives.", "거칠고 남루하고 매 맞은 이 두 여자가 그토록 하나로 묶여 있는 모습을, 서로에게 어떤 존재가 될 수 있는지를, 서로를 어떻게 헤아리는지를, 모진 시련이 저마다의 마음을 어떻게 서로에게 누그러뜨려 놓았는지를 보는 일은, 나는 무척 가슴 뭉클하다고 생각했습니다."], ["I think the best side of such people is almost hidden from us.", "나는 이런 사람들의 가장 선한 면은 우리에게 거의 가려져 있다고 생각합니다."], ["What the poor are to the poor is little known, excepting to themselves and God.", "가난한 이가 가난한 이에게 어떤 존재인지는, 그들 자신과 하느님 말고는 거의 알려져 있지 않으니까요."]], [["We felt it better to withdraw and leave them uninterrupted.", "우리는 물러나 그들을 방해하지 않고 두는 게 낫겠다고 느꼈습니다."], ["We stole out quietly and without notice from any one except the man.", "우리는 그 남자 말고는 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않은 채 조용히 빠져나왔습니다."], ["He was leaning against the wall near the door, and finding that there was scarcely room for us to pass, went out before us.", "그는 문 가까운 벽에 기대어 있다가, 우리가 지나갈 자리가 거의 없는 걸 보더니 우리보다 먼저 밖으로 나갔습니다."], ["He seemed to want to hide that he did this on our account, but we perceived that he did, and thanked him.", "그는 자기가 우리를 위해 그런다는 걸 감추고 싶어 하는 듯했지만, 우리는 그가 그러는 걸 알아채고 고맙다고 했습니다."], ["He made no answer.", "그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["Ada was so full of grief all the way home, and Richard, whom we found at home, was so distressed to see her in tears (though he said to me, when she was not present, how beautiful it was too!), that we arranged to return at night with some little comforts and repeat our visit at the brick-maker’s house.", "에이다는 집으로 돌아오는 내내 슬픔에 잠겨 있었고, 집에서 우리를 맞은 리처드는 그녀가 눈물짓는 모습에 어찌나 마음 아파하던지(그러면서도 그녀가 없을 때 나한테는, 그 모습이 또 얼마나 아름답던지 모른다고 했지만요), 우리는 밤에 자그마한 위안거리들을 챙겨 다시 가서 벽돌공의 집을 한 번 더 찾아보기로 했습니다."], ["We said as little as we could to Mr. Jarndyce, but the wind changed directly.", "잔다이스 씨에게는 되도록 말을 아꼈지만, 그래도 바람은 곧장 방향을 바꿨습니다."]], [["Richard accompanied us at night to the scene of our morning expedition.", "리처드는 밤에 우리가 아침에 다녀온 그곳까지 동행했습니다."], ["On our way there, we had to pass a noisy drinking-house, where a number of men were flocking about the door.", "가는 길에 우리는 시끌벅적한 술집 하나를 지나야 했는데, 그 문 앞에 사내들이 잔뜩 몰려 있었습니다."], ["Among them, and prominent in some dispute, was the father of the little child.", "그들 사이에서, 무슨 다툼을 벌이며 두드러져 보이는 이가, 바로 그 어린아이의 아버지였습니다."], ["At a short distance, we passed the young man and the dog, in congenial company.", "조금 떨어진 곳에서 우리는 그 젓은 사내와 개가 죽이 맞는 패거리와 어울려 있는 것을 지나쳤습니다."], ["The sister was standing laughing and talking with some other young women at the corner of the row of cottages, but she seemed ashamed and turned away as we went by.", "누이는 오두막들이 늘어선 줄 모퉁이에서 다른 젊은 여자들과 웃고 떠들며 서 있었지만, 우리가 지나가자 부끄러운 듯 고개를 돌렸습니다."]], [["We left our escort within sight of the brickmaker’s dwelling and proceeded by ourselves.", "우리는 벽돌공의 집이 보이는 곳에 호위병을 남겨 두고 우리끼리 나아갔습니다."], ["When we came to the door, we found the woman who had brought such consolation with her standing there looking anxiously out.", "문 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그토록 큰 위안을 가져다주었던 그 여자가 거기 서서 불안하게 밖을 내다보고 있는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["“It’s you, young ladies, is it?”", "\"아가씨들이시군요?\""], ["she said in a whisper.", "그녀가 속삭이듯 말했습니다."], ["“I’m a-watching for my master.", "\"제 남편을 망보고 있는 중이에요."], ["My heart’s in my mouth.", "가슴이 입 밖으로 튀어나올 것만 같네요."], ["If he was to catch me away from home, he’d pretty near murder me.”", "제가 집을 비운 걸 들키기라도 하면, 절 거의 죽여 놓을 거예요.\""]], [["“Do you mean your husband?”", "\"남편이라고요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“Yes, miss, my master.", "\"네, 아가씨, 제 남편이요."], ["Jenny’s asleep, quite worn out.", "제니는 자고 있어요, 완전히 녹초가 됐거든요."], ["She’s scarcely had the child off her lap, poor thing, these seven days and nights, except when I’ve been able to take it for a minute or two.”", "가엾게도 이레 낮밤을, 제가 잠깐씩 안아 줄 수 있었을 때 말고는 그 애를 무릎에서 거의 내려놓지도 못했답니다.\""]], [["As she gave way for us, she went softly in and put what we had brought near the miserable bed on which the mother slept.", "그녀는 우리에게 자리를 내주면서 가만히 안으로 들어가, 우리가 가져온 것을 어머니가 잠든 그 초라한 침상 가까이에 놓았습니다. 방을 치우려는 노력은 전혀 없었습니다 — 본디 깨끗해지기를 바랄 수 없을 만큼 가망 없는 곳인 듯했지요."], ["No effort had been made to clean the room—it seemed in its nature almost hopeless of being clean; but the small waxen form from which so much solemnity diffused itself had been composed afresh, and washed, and neatly dressed in some fragments of white linen; and on my handkerchief, which still covered the poor baby, a little bunch of sweet herbs had been laid by the same rough, scarred hands, so lightly, so tenderly!", "하지만 그토록 많은 엄숙함을 풍기던 그 작은 밀랍 같은 형체는 다시 가지런히 뉘어졌고, 씻겨졌고, 흰 아마포 조각으로 단정하게 감싸여 있었습니다. 그리고 가엾은 아기를 여전히 덮고 있던 내 손수건 위에는, 바로 그 거칠고 흉터진 손이, 어찌나 가볍게, 어찌나 다정하게, 향긋한 풀 한 다발을 얹어 놓았던지요!"]], [["“May heaven reward you!”", "\"하늘이 당신에게 갚아 주시길!\""], ["we said to her.", "우리는 그녀에게 말했습니다."], ["“You are a good woman.”", "\"당신은 착한 여인이에요.\""]], [["“Me, young ladies?”", "\"제가요, 아가씨들?\""], ["she returned with surprise.", "그녀가 놀라며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Jenny, Jenny!”", "제니, 제니!\""]], [["The mother had moaned in her sleep and moved.", "어머니가 잠결에 신음하며 몸을 뒤척였던 것입니다."], ["The sound of the familiar voice seemed to calm her again.", "그 낯익은 목소리가 그녀를 다시 가라앉히는 듯했습니다."], ["She was quiet once more.", "그녀는 다시 잠잠해졌습니다."]], [["How little I thought, when I raised my handkerchief to look upon the tiny sleeper underneath and seemed to see a halo shine around the child through Ada’s drooping hair as her pity bent her head—how little I thought in whose unquiet bosom that handkerchief would come to lie after covering the motionless and peaceful breast!", "내가 손수건을 들어 그 아래 잠든 작은 이를 들여다보며, 가엾게 여겨 고개 숙인 에이다의 드리워진 머리카락 사이로 아기 둘레에 후광이 비치는 것을 본 듯했던 그때, 나는 얼마나 까맣게 몰랐던지요 — 미동도 없이 평온한 그 가슴을 덮었던 이 손수건이 훗날 누구의 편치 못한 가슴 위에 놓이게 될지를 말입니다! 다만 나는 그저 이렇게만 생각했습니다."], ["I only thought that perhaps the Angel of the child might not be all unconscious of the woman who replaced it with so compassionate a hand; not all unconscious of her presently, when we had taken leave, and left her at the door, by turns looking, and listening in terror for herself, and saying in her old soothing manner, “Jenny, Jenny!”", "어쩌면 그 아이의 천사가, 그토록 가여워하는 손길로 손수건을 다시 덮어 준 그 여자를 아주 모르지는 않으리라고. 우리가 작별을 고하고 그녀를 문가에 남겨 두었을 때, 번갈아 가며 제 처지가 두려워 밖을 살피고 귀 기울이면서도, 예의 그 다독이는 말투로 \"제니, 제니!\""], ["Signs and Tokens", "하고 되뇌던 그녀를, 그 천사가 그때에도 아주 모르지는 않으리라고요."]], [["I don’t know how it is I seem to be always writing about myself.", "어쩌다 보니 저는 늘 제 이야기만 쓰는 것 같습니다."], ["I mean all the time to write about other people, and I try to think about myself as little as possible, and I am sure, when I find myself coming into the story again, I am really vexed and say, “Dear, dear, you tiresome little creature, I wish you wouldn’t!”", "마음으로는 줄곧 다른 사람들 이야기를 쓰려고 하고, 저 자신은 되도록 적게 생각하려 애쓰며, 정말이지 이야기 속에 또 제가 끼어드는 걸 발견할 때마다 속이 상해서 \"어머, 어머, 이 성가신 것아, 제발 그러지 좀 마!\"라고 말합니다만, 다 소용없는 일입니다."], ["but it is all of no use. I hope any one who may read what I write will understand that if these pages contain a great deal about me, I can only suppose it must be because I have really something to do with them and can’t be kept out.", "제가 쓰는 글을 읽으실 분이 계시다면, 이 지면에 저에 관한 이야기가 잔뜩 들어 있더라도 그건 제가 정말로 그 일들과 얽혀 있어서 도저히 빠질 수 없기 때문이라고 짐작하실 수밖에 없다는 점을 이해해주셨으면 합니다."]], [["My darling and I read together, and worked, and practised, and found so much employment for our time that the winter days flew by us like bright-winged birds.", "사랑스러운 그 애와 저는 함께 책을 읽고, 일하고, 연습하며, 시간을 그토록 알차게 채운 덕분에 겨울날들은 밝은 날개를 단 새처럼 곁을 스쳐 날아갔습니다."], ["Generally in the afternoons, and always in the evenings, Richard gave us his company.", "대개 오후에, 그리고 저녁에는 언제나 리처드가 우리와 함께 있어주었습니다."], ["Although he was one of the most restless creatures in the world, he certainly was very fond of our society.", "그는 세상에서 가장 가만있지 못하는 사람 가운데 하나였지만, 우리와 어울리는 걸 무척이나 좋아한 게 분명했습니다."]], [["He was very, very, very fond of Ada.", "그는 에이다를 아주, 아주, 아주 좋아했습니다."], ["I mean it, and I had better say it at once.", "정말입니다, 그러니 곧바로 말해두는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["I had never seen any young people falling in love before, but I found them out quite soon.", "저는 그때까지 젊은 사람들이 사랑에 빠지는 모습을 본 적이 없었지만, 곧 두 사람의 마음을 알아챘습니다."], ["I could not say so, of course, or show that I knew anything about it.", "물론 입 밖에 낼 수도, 제가 뭔가 안다는 내색을 할 수도 없었습니다."], ["On the contrary, I was so demure and used to seem so unconscious that sometimes I considered within myself while I was sitting at work whether I was not growing quite deceitful.", "오히려 너무 얌전을 빼며 아무것도 모르는 척하다 보니, 일감을 들고 앉아 있다가 가끔은 내가 영 음흉한 사람이 되어가는 건 아닐까 하고 속으로 헤아려보기도 했습니다."]], [["But there was no help for it.", "그렇지만 어쩔 도리가 없었습니다."], ["All I had to do was to be quiet, and I was as quiet as a mouse.", "제가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 잠자코 있는 것뿐이었고, 저는 생쥐처럼 조용했습니다."], ["They were as quiet as mice too, so far as any words were concerned, but the innocent manner in which they relied more and more upon me as they took more and more to one another was so charming that I had great difficulty in not showing how it interested me.", "두 사람도 말로 따지자면 생쥐처럼 조용했지만, 서로에게 점점 더 빠져들수록 점점 더 저에게 기대오는 그 천진한 태도가 어찌나 사랑스러웠던지, 제가 얼마나 관심을 두고 있는지 내색하지 않기가 무척 힘들었습니다."]], [["“Our dear little old woman is such a capital old woman,”", "\"우리 귀여운 꼬마 할머니는 정말 둘도 없는 할머니라니까요.\""], ["Richard would say, coming up to meet me in the garden early, with his pleasant laugh and perhaps the least tinge of a blush, “that I can’t get on without her.", "리처드는 아침 일찍 정원으로 저를 맞으러 나오며, 기분 좋은 웃음과 어쩌면 아주 살짝 붉어진 뺨으로 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"이분 없이는 도무지 못 견디겠어요."], ["Before I begin my harum-scarum day—grinding away at those books and instruments and then galloping up hill and down dale, all the country round, like a highwayman—it does me so much good to come and have a steady walk with our comfortable friend, that here I am again!”", "정신없는 하루를 시작하기 전에—그 책들이며 도구들과 씨름하다가 노상강도처럼 온 동네를 언덕 위로 골짜기 아래로 말 달려 누비기 전에—나와서 우리의 마음 편한 친구와 차분하게 한 바퀴 걷는 게 얼마나 좋은지, 그래서 또 이렇게 나왔지 뭐예요!\""]], [["“You know, Dame Durden, dear,”", "\"있잖아요, 더든 아주머니.\""], ["Ada would say at night, with her head upon my shoulder and the firelight shining in her thoughtful eyes, “I don’t want to talk when we come upstairs here.", "에이다는 밤이면 제 어깨에 머리를 기대고, 사색에 잠긴 눈에 난롯불빛을 담은 채 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"여기 위층에 올라오면 말하고 싶지가 않아요."], ["Only to sit a little while thinking, with your dear face for company, and to hear the wind and remember the poor sailors at sea—”", "그저 잠깐 앉아서, 언니의 사랑스러운 얼굴을 벗 삼아 생각에 잠기고, 바람 소리를 들으며 바다에 나간 가엾은 뱃사람들을 떠올리고 싶을 뿐이에요—\""]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["Perhaps Richard was going to be a sailor.", "어쩌면 리처드는 뱃사람이 될 참이었습니다."], ["We had talked it over very often now, and there was some talk of gratifying the inclination of his childhood for the sea. Mr.", "우리는 이제 그 얘기를 자주 나누었고, 어릴 적부터 품어온 바다에 대한 그의 마음을 채워주자는 말도 오갔습니다."], ["Jarndyce had written to a relation of the family, a great Sir Leicester Dedlock, for his interest in Richard’s favour, generally; and Sir Leicester had replied in a gracious manner that he would be happy to advance the prospects of the young gentleman if it should ever prove to be within his power, which was not at all probable, and that my Lady sent her compliments to the young gentleman (to whom she perfectly remembered that she was allied by remote consanguinity) and trusted that he would ever do his duty in any honourable profession to which he might devote himself.", "잔다이스 씨는 집안의 친척인 위대한 레스터 데드록 경에게 편지를 써서, 리처드에게 두루 마음을 써달라고 부탁했습니다. 레스터 경은 정중하게 답장을 보내, 자기 힘이 닿는 일이라면—그럴 가능성은 도무지 없지만—그 젊은이의 앞날을 기꺼이 밀어주겠노라고, 그리고 마님께서도 그 젊은이(먼 핏줄로 인척이 된다는 사실을 또렷이 기억한다는)에게 안부를 전하며, 그가 어떤 명예로운 직업에 몸담든 늘 제 본분을 다하기를 바란다고 했습니다."]], [["“So I apprehend it’s pretty clear,”", "\"그러니 내가 보기엔 꽤 분명해요.\""], ["said Richard to me, “that I shall have to work my own way.", "리처드가 저에게 말했습니다. \"내 길은 내 힘으로 헤쳐가야 한다는 거죠."], ["Never mind!", "상관없어요!"], ["Plenty of people have had to do that before now, and have done it.", "지금까지도 그렇게 해야 했던 사람은 숱하게 많았고, 다들 해냈으니까."], ["I only wish I had the command of a clipping privateer to begin with and could carry off the Chancellor and keep him on short allowance until he gave judgment in our cause.", "나야 그저 처음부터 날쌘 사략선 한 척만 손에 쥘 수 있다면, 대법관을 납치해서 우리 소송에 판결을 내릴 때까지 굶기다시피 가둬두고 싶을 뿐이에요."], ["He’d find himself growing thin, if he didn’t look sharp!”", "정신 바짝 안 차리면 점점 야위어가는 자기 꼴을 보게 될걸요!\""]], [["With a buoyancy and hopefulness and a gaiety that hardly ever flagged, Richard had a carelessness in his character that quite perplexed me, principally because he mistook it, in such a very odd way, for prudence.", "좀처럼 사그라지지 않는 활기와 희망과 명랑함을 지녔으면서도, 리처드의 성격에는 저를 무척 당혹스럽게 하는 무심함이 깃들어 있었습니다. 무엇보다 그가 그 무심함을 참으로 묘한 방식으로 신중함이라 착각했기 때문입니다."], ["It entered into all his calculations about money in a singular manner which I don’t think I can better explain than by reverting for a moment to our loan to Mr. Skimpole.", "그것은 돈에 관한 그의 모든 셈에 유별난 방식으로 끼어들었는데, 잠시 스킴폴 씨에게 돈을 빌려준 일로 되돌아가는 것보다 더 잘 설명할 길은 없을 듯합니다."]], [["Mr. Jarndyce had ascertained the amount, either from Mr.", "잔다이스 씨는 스킴폴 씨 본인에게서든 코빈시즈에게서든 그 액수를 알아내, 제 손에 그 돈을 쥐여주며 제 몫은 챙기고 나머지는 리처드에게 건네라고 일렀습니다."], ["Skimpole himself or from Coavinses, and had placed the money in my hands with instructions to me to retain my own part of it and hand the rest to Richard. The number of little acts of thoughtless expenditure which Richard justified by the recovery of his ten pounds, and the number of times he talked to me as if he had saved or realized that amount, would form a sum in simple addition.", "리처드가 자기 십 파운드를 도로 찾았다는 명목으로 정당화한 자잘한 충동적 지출의 횟수와, 마치 그 액수를 아끼거나 벌어들이기라도 한 것처럼 저에게 말한 횟수를 더하면 그저 단순한 덧셈 문제가 될 것입니다."]], [["“My prudent Mother Hubbard, why not?”", "\"신중한 우리 허버드 아주머니, 안 될 게 뭐 있어요?\""], ["he said to me when he wanted, without the least consideration, to bestow five pounds on the brickmaker.", "그는 조금도 망설이지 않고 벽돌공에게 오 파운드를 주려 했을 때 저에게 이렇게 말했습니다."], ["“I made ten pounds, clear, out of Coavinses’ business.”", "\"코빈시즈 일로 십 파운드를 고스란히 벌었잖아요.\""]], [["“How was that?”", "\"그게 어떻게 그래요?\""], ["said I.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, I got rid of ten pounds which I was quite content to get rid of and never expected to see any more.", "\"아니, 나는 기꺼이 떠나보낼 셈이었고 다시는 못 볼 줄 알았던 십 파운드를 떼였잖아요."], ["You don’t deny that?”", "그건 아니라고 못 하겠죠?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Very well!", "\"좋아요!"], ["Then I came into possession of ten pounds—”", "그러고 나서 나는 십 파운드를 손에 넣었어요—\""]], [["“The same ten pounds,”", "\"같은 십 파운드잖아요.\""], ["I hinted.", "제가 넌지시 일렀습니다."]], [["“That has nothing to do with it!”", "\"그건 상관없어요!\""], ["returned Richard.", "리처드가 받아쳤습니다."], ["“I have got ten pounds more than I expected to have, and consequently I can afford to spend it without being particular.”", "\"나는 기대했던 것보다 십 파운드를 더 갖게 됐고, 따라서 까다롭게 굴 것 없이 그 돈을 쓸 여유가 있는 거예요.\""]], [["In exactly the same way, when he was persuaded out of the sacrifice of these five pounds by being convinced that it would do no good, he carried that sum to his credit and drew upon it.", "꼭 마찬가지로, 그 오 파운드를 내놓아봐야 아무 소용없다는 걸 깨닫고 그 지출을 단념했을 때도, 그는 그 액수를 자기 잔고에 적어 넣고 거기서 끌어다 썼습니다."]], [["“Let me see!” he would say.", "\"어디 보자!\""], ["“I saved five pounds out of the brickmaker’s affair, so if I have a good rattle to London and back in a post-chaise and put that down at four pounds, I shall have saved one.", "그는 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다. \"벽돌공 일에서 오 파운드를 아꼈으니까, 역마차로 런던을 신나게 한 번 다녀오면서 그 비용을 사 파운드로 잡으면, 일 파운드를 아낀 셈이지."], ["And it’s a very good thing to save one, let me tell you: a penny saved is a penny got!”", "일 파운드를 아끼는 건 아주 좋은 일이거든, 내 말 들어봐요. 한 푼 아끼면 한 푼 버는 거니까!\""]], [["I believe Richard’s was as frank and generous a nature as there possibly can be.", "리처드의 천성은 그보다 더할 수 없을 만큼 솔직하고 너그러웠다고 저는 믿습니다."], ["He was ardent and brave, and in the midst of all his wild restlessness, was so gentle that I knew him like a brother in a few weeks.", "그는 열정적이고 용감했으며, 그 거친 들뜸 한복판에서도 어찌나 부드러웠던지 몇 주 만에 저는 그를 친오빠처럼 여기게 되었습니다."], ["His gentleness was natural to him and would have shown itself abundantly even without Ada’s influence; but with it, he became one of the most winning of companions, always so ready to be interested and always so happy, sanguine, and light-hearted.", "그 부드러움은 그에게 타고난 것이어서 에이다의 영향이 없었더라도 넘치도록 드러났겠지만, 거기에 더해 그는 더없이 사람을 끄는 벗이 되었으니, 늘 무엇에든 흔쾌히 관심을 보였고 늘 그토록 행복하고, 낙천적이고, 마음이 가벼웠습니다."], ["I am sure that I, sitting with them, and walking with them, and talking with them, and noticing from day to day how they went on, falling deeper and deeper in love, and saying nothing about it, and each shyly thinking that this love was the greatest of secrets, perhaps not yet suspected even by the other—I am sure that I was scarcely less enchanted than they were and scarcely less pleased with the pretty dream.", "두 사람과 함께 앉아 있고, 함께 걷고, 함께 이야기하며, 날마다 두 사람이 어떻게 점점 더 깊이 사랑에 빠지면서도 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고, 저마다 이 사랑이야말로 더없이 큰 비밀이며 어쩌면 상대조차 아직 눈치채지 못했으리라 수줍게 여기는 모습을 지켜보던 저는—저 또한 두 사람 못지않게 마음을 빼앗겼고, 그 어여쁜 꿈에 두 사람 못지않게 기뻐했다고 확신합니다."]], [["We were going on in this way, when one morning at breakfast Mr.", "이렇게 지내던 어느 날 아침, 식사 자리에서 잔다이스 씨가 편지 한 통을 받았습니다."], ["Jarndyce received a letter, and looking at the superscription, said, “From Boythorn?", "겉봉을 보더니 그가 말했습니다. \"보이손한테서 온 건가?"], ["Aye, aye!”", "그래, 그렇군!\""], ["and opened and read it with evident pleasure, announcing to us in a parenthesis when he was about half-way through, that Boythorn was “coming down”", "그러고는 분명 흐뭇한 기색으로 편지를 뜯어 읽었는데, 절반쯤 읽었을 무렵 보이손이 \"내려와\" 방문할 거라고 우리에게 한마디 끼워 알렸습니다."], ["on a visit. Now who was Boythorn, we all thought.", "자, 보이손이 누굴까, 우리는 모두 그렇게 생각했습니다."], ["And I dare say we all thought too—I am sure I did, for one—would Boythorn at all interfere with what was going forward?", "그리고 우리 모두—적어도 저는 분명히 그랬습니다—이런 생각도 했을 것입니다. 보이손이 지금 한창 무르익어가는 일에 조금이라도 끼어들지는 않을까?"]], [["“I went to school with this fellow, Lawrence Boythorn,” said Mr.", "\"이 친구, 로런스 보이손하고는 같이 학교를 다녔지.\""], ["Jarndyce, tapping the letter as he laid it on the table, “more than five and forty years ago.", "잔다이스 씨가 편지를 탁자에 내려놓으며 그것을 톡톡 두드렸습니다. \"사십오 년도 더 전에 말이야."], ["He was then the most impetuous boy in the world, and he is now the most impetuous man.", "그땐 세상에서 가장 충동적인 소년이었는데, 지금은 세상에서 가장 충동적인 사내야."], ["He was then the loudest boy in the world, and he is now the loudest man.", "그땐 세상에서 가장 목소리 큰 소년이었는데, 지금은 세상에서 가장 목소리 큰 사내고."], ["He was then the heartiest and sturdiest boy in the world, and he is now the heartiest and sturdiest man.", "그땐 세상에서 가장 기운차고 다부진 소년이었는데, 지금은 세상에서 가장 기운차고 다부진 사내지."], ["He is a tremendous fellow.”", "엄청난 친구라네.\""]], [["“In stature, sir?”", "\"덩치가 말입니까, 어르신?\""], ["asked Richard.", "리처드가 물었습니다."]], [["“Pretty well, Rick, in that respect,” said Mr.", "\"그쪽으로도 꽤 그렇지, 릭.\""], ["Jarndyce; “being some ten years older than I and a couple of inches taller, with his head thrown back like an old soldier, his stalwart chest squared, his hands like a clean blacksmith’s, and his lungs!", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"나보다 열 살쯤 위에 키는 두어 인치 더 크고, 늙은 군인처럼 고개를 뒤로 젖히고, 떡 벌어진 가슴팍을 네모지게 펴고, 손은 깨끗한 대장장이 손 같고, 게다가 그 허파라니!"], ["There’s no simile for his lungs.", "그 허파에 견줄 만한 게 없어."], ["Talking, laughing, or snoring, they make the beams of the house shake.”", "말을 하든, 웃든, 코를 골든, 집 들보가 흔들릴 지경이거든.\""]], [["As Mr. Jarndyce sat enjoying the image of his friend Boythorn, we observed the favourable omen that there was not the least indication of any change in the wind.", "잔다이스 씨가 친구 보이손의 모습을 떠올리며 즐거워하는 동안, 우리는 바람 방향이 바뀔 기미가 조금도 없다는 길조를 알아챘습니다."]], [["“But it’s the inside of the man, the warm heart of the man, the passion of the man, the fresh blood of the man, Rick—and Ada, and little Cobweb too, for you are all interested in a visitor—that I speak of,”", "\"하지만 내가 말하려는 건 그 사람의 속, 그 사람의 따뜻한 마음, 그 사람의 열정, 그 사람의 펄펄 끓는 피야, 릭—그리고 에이다, 또 우리 꼬마 거미줄, 손님 얘기니 너희도 다 관심 있겠지.\" 그가 말을 이었습니다."], ["he pursued. “His language is as sounding as his voice.", "\"그 사람 말투는 목소리만큼이나 쩌렁쩌렁하지."], ["He is always in extremes, perpetually in the superlative degree.", "늘 극단으로 치닫고, 노상 최상급으로만 말한다네."], ["In his condemnation he is all ferocity.", "비난할 땐 온통 사납기 그지없어."], ["You might suppose him to be an ogre from what he says, and I believe he has the reputation of one with some people.", "그가 하는 말만 들으면 식인 괴물쯤으로 여길 만하고, 실제로 어떤 사람들 사이에선 그런 평판도 있는 모양이야."], ["There!", "자!"], ["I tell you no more of him beforehand.", "미리 더 일러주진 않겠네."], ["You must not be surprised to see him take me under his protection, for he has never forgotten that I was a low boy at school and that our friendship began in his knocking two of my head tyrant’s teeth out (he says six) before breakfast.", "그가 나를 제 보호 아래 두려 하더라도 놀라지들 말게. 그는 내가 학교에서 약한 아이였다는 것과, 우리 우정이 그가 나를 괴롭히던 우두머리의 이를 아침 식사 전에 두 대(본인은 여섯 대라고 하지만) 부러뜨린 데서 시작됐다는 걸 결코 잊지 않았거든."], ["Boythorn and his man,”", "보이손과 그 하인은,\""], ["to me, “will be here this afternoon, my dear.”", "그가 저를 향해 말했습니다. \"오늘 오후에 도착할 거야, 얘야.\""]], [["I took care that the necessary preparations were made for Mr. Boythorn’s reception, and we looked forward to his arrival with some curiosity.", "저는 보이손 씨를 맞을 채비를 빈틈없이 갖추도록 신경 썼고, 우리는 다소 궁금한 마음으로 그의 도착을 기다렸습니다."], ["The afternoon wore away, however, and he did not appear.", "그러나 오후가 다 지나도록 그는 나타나지 않았습니다."], ["The dinner-hour arrived, and still he did not appear.", "저녁 식사 시각이 되어도 여전히 나타나지 않았습니다."], ["The dinner was put back an hour, and we were sitting round the fire with no light but the blaze when the hall-door suddenly burst open and the hall resounded with these words, uttered with the greatest vehemence and in a stentorian tone: “We have been misdirected, Jarndyce, by a most abandoned ruffian, who told us to take the turning to the right instead of to the left.", "식사를 한 시간 미루고, 우리가 불빛이라곤 난롯불뿐인 가운데 둘러앉아 있을 때, 현관문이 느닷없이 벌컥 열리더니 더없이 격렬하게, 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 이런 말이 현관에 울려 퍼졌습니다. \"길을 잘못 들었지 뭔가, 잔다이스, 더없이 막돼먹은 망나니 놈 탓이야."], ["He is the most intolerable scoundrel on the face of the earth.", "왼쪽으로 꺾으랬어야 할 걸 오른쪽으로 꺾으라고 했단 말이지."], ["His father must have been a most consummate villain, ever to have such a son.", "지구상에 그놈보다 더 못 봐줄 악당은 없을 거야. 그런 자식을 두다니 그 아비도 분명 둘도 없는 악한이었을 게야."], ["I would have had that fellow shot without the least remorse!”", "그놈은 눈곱만큼의 후회도 없이 총살시켜버렸어야 했어!\""]], [["“Did he do it on purpose?”", "\"그자가 일부러 그랬답니까?\""], ["Mr. Jarndyce inquired.", "잔다이스 씨가 물었습니다."]], [["“I have not the slightest doubt that the scoundrel has passed his whole existence in misdirecting travellers!”", "\"그 악당이 평생 나그네들 길을 엉뚱하게 일러주며 살아왔다는 데 나는 한 점 의심도 없어!\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 받아쳤습니다."], ["“By my soul, I thought him the worst-looking dog I had ever beheld when he was telling me to take the turning to the right.", "\"맹세컨대, 오른쪽으로 꺾으라고 일러줄 때 보니 내 평생 본 것 중에 가장 흉악하게 생긴 개 같은 놈이더군."], ["And yet I stood before that fellow face to face and didn’t knock his brains out!”", "그런데도 그놈을 코앞에 마주 보고 서서 골통을 박살내지 않았다니!\""]], [["“Teeth, you mean?”", "\"이 말이겠지?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!” laughed Mr.", "\"하, 하, 하!\""], ["Lawrence Boythorn, really making the whole house vibrate.", "로런스 보이손 씨가 웃음을 터뜨리니, 정말로 온 집안이 다 울렸습니다."], ["“What, you have not forgotten it yet!", "\"아니, 아직도 그걸 안 잊었군!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["And that was another most consummate vagabond!", "그놈도 또 하나의 둘도 없는 부랑자였지!"], ["By my soul, the countenance of that fellow when he was a boy was the blackest image of perfidy, cowardice, and cruelty ever set up as a scarecrow in a field of scoundrels.", "맹세컨대, 그놈이 소년이었을 때의 그 낯짝은 악당들 밭에 세워둔 허수아비 중에서도 가장 시커먼 배신과 비겁과 잔인의 화신이었어."], ["If I were to meet that most unparalleled despot in the streets to-morrow, I would fell him like a rotten tree!”", "내일 길에서 그 비할 데 없는 폭군을 만난다면, 썩은 나무처럼 단번에 베어 넘어뜨려 줄 텐데!\""]], [["“I have no doubt of it,”", "\"그러시겠지요.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Now, will you come upstairs?”", "\"자, 이제 위층으로 올라가시겠소?\""]], [["“By my soul, Jarndyce,”", "\"맹세컨대, 잔다이스.\""], ["returned his guest, who seemed to refer to his watch, “if you had been married, I would have turned back at the garden-gate and gone away to the remotest summits of the Himalaya Mountains sooner than I would have presented myself at this unseasonable hour.”", "손님이 시계를 보는 듯하더니 받아 말했습니다. \"자네가 결혼한 몸이었다면, 이런 당치 않은 시각에 나타나느니 차라리 정원 문에서 발길을 돌려 히말라야산맥 가장 외진 봉우리까지라도 가버렸을 거야.\""]], [["“Not quite so far, I hope?”", "\"그렇게까지 멀리는 안 가셨겠지요?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“By my life and honour, yes!”", "\"내 목숨과 명예를 걸고, 갔을 거야!\""], ["cried the visitor.", "손님이 외쳤습니다."], ["“I wouldn’t be guilty of the audacious insolence of keeping a lady of the house waiting all this time for any earthly consideration.", "\"세상 그 무엇을 위해서라도, 안주인을 이토록 오래 기다리게 하는 무엄하고 발칙한 죄는 짓지 않았을 거야."], ["I would infinitely rather destroy myself—infinitely rather!”", "차라리 나 자신을 끝장내는 편이 한없이 낫지—한없이 낫고말고!\""]], [["Talking thus, they went upstairs, and presently we heard him in his bedroom thundering “Ha, ha, ha!”", "이렇게 이야기를 나누며 두 사람은 위층으로 올라갔고, 이윽고 우리는 그가 침실에서 \"하, 하, 하!\""], ["and again “Ha, ha, ha!”", "또 \"하, 하, 하!\""], ["until the flattest echo in the neighbourhood seemed to catch the contagion and to laugh as enjoyingly as he did or as we did when we heard him laugh.", "우레처럼 웃어대는 소리를 들었습니다. 마침내 동네에서 가장 무덤덤한 메아리마저 그 웃음에 전염된 듯, 그가 웃는 만큼이나, 또 우리가 그 웃음을 듣고 웃는 만큼이나 신나게 따라 웃는 것 같았습니다."]], [["We all conceived a prepossession in his favour, for there was a sterling quality in this laugh, and in his vigorous, healthy voice, and in the roundness and fullness with which he uttered every word he spoke, and in the very fury of his superlatives, which seemed to go off like blank cannons and hurt nothing.", "우리는 모두 그에게 호감을 품게 되었습니다. 그 웃음에도, 우렁차고 건강한 목소리에도, 한마디 한마디를 둥글고 꽉 차게 내뱉는 그 말투에도, 마치 공포탄처럼 펑펑 터지되 아무것도 다치게 하지 않는 그 최상급의 격렬함 자체에도 진국 같은 무언가가 있었기 때문입니다."], ["But we were hardly prepared to have it so confirmed by his appearance when Mr. Jarndyce presented him.", "그러나 잔다이스 씨가 그를 소개했을 때, 그 호감이 외모로 이토록 굳어질 줄은 우리도 미처 예상하지 못했습니다."], ["He was not only a very handsome old gentleman—upright and stalwart as he had been described to us—with a massive grey head, a fine composure of face when silent, a figure that might have become corpulent but for his being so continually in earnest that he gave it no rest, and a chin that might have subsided into a double chin but for the vehement emphasis in which it was constantly required to assist; but he was such a true gentleman in his manner, so chivalrously polite, his face was lighted by a smile of so much sweetness and tenderness, and it seemed so plain that he had nothing to hide, but showed himself exactly as he was—incapable, as Richard said, of anything on a limited scale, and firing away with those blank great guns because he carried no small arms whatever—that really I could not help looking at him with equal pleasure as he sat at dinner, whether he smilingly conversed with Ada and me, or was led by Mr.", "그는 단지 아주 잘생긴 노신사—듣던 대로 꼿꼿하고 다부진—였을 뿐 아니라, 묵직한 잿빛 머리에, 입을 다물면 더없이 차분한 얼굴에, 워낙 늘 진지함에 골몰해 한순간도 쉴 틈을 주지 않은 탓에 비대해질 법도 했으나 그러지 못한 체격에, 끊임없이 그 격렬한 강조를 거들어야 했던 까닭에 겹턱으로 처질 법도 했으나 그러지 못한 턱을 지녔습니다. 게다가 그는 태도가 어찌나 참된 신사인지, 기사처럼 정중했고, 그 얼굴은 더없이 다정하고 따뜻한 미소로 환했으며, 숨길 것이라곤 하나도 없이 있는 그대로의 자기를 고스란히 내보이는 게 너무도 분명해 보였습니다—리처드의 말마따나 어떤 일도 자잘한 규모로는 못 하는 사람, 자잘한 무기라곤 아예 지니지 않아 저 공포탄 같은 거포만 펑펑 쏘아대는 사람이었습니다—그래서 정말이지 저는 식사 자리에 앉은 그를 한결같은 즐거움으로 바라보지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["Jarndyce into some great volley of superlatives, or threw up his head like a bloodhound and gave out that tremendous “Ha, ha, ha!”", "그가 에이다와 저에게 미소 지으며 말을 건넬 때도, 잔다이스 씨에게 이끌려 최상급 어휘를 한바탕 쏟아낼 때도, 사냥개처럼 고개를 쳐들고 그 어마어마한 \"하, 하, 하!\"를 터뜨릴 때도 말입니다."]], [["“You have brought your bird with you, I suppose?”", "\"새도 데려오셨겠지요?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“By heaven, he is the most astonishing bird in Europe!”", "\"맹세코, 그놈은 유럽에서 가장 놀라운 새야!\""], ["replied the other.", "상대가 답했습니다."], ["“He IS the most wonderful creature!", "\"정말 둘도 없이 신통한 녀석이지!"], ["I wouldn’t take ten thousand guineas for that bird.", "만 기니를 준대도 그 새는 안 내놓을 거야."], ["I have left an annuity for his sole support in case he should outlive me.", "그놈이 나보다 오래 살 경우를 대비해 그놈 부양만을 위한 연금까지 따로 마련해뒀다네."], ["He is, in sense and attachment, a phenomenon.", "영리함으로 보나 정으로 보나 그놈은 경이 그 자체야."], ["And his father before him was one of the most astonishing birds that ever lived!”", "그리고 그 아비 새도 여태껏 살았던 새 중에 가장 놀라운 녀석이었지!\""]], [["The subject of this laudation was a very little canary, who was so tame that he was brought down by Mr. Boythorn’s man, on his forefinger, and after taking a gentle flight round the room, alighted on his master’s head.", "이 칭송의 대상은 아주 자그마한 카나리아였는데, 어찌나 길이 잘 들었던지 보이손 씨의 하인이 집게손가락에 앉혀 데리고 내려왔고, 그놈은 방 안을 한 바퀴 살랑살랑 날더니 주인의 머리 위에 내려앉았습니다."], ["To hear Mr. Boythorn presently expressing the most implacable and passionate sentiments, with this fragile mite of a creature quietly perched on his forehead, was to have a good illustration of his character, I thought.", "이 가냘프고 자그마한 미물이 제 이마에 가만히 앉아 있는 채로, 보이손 씨가 더없이 무자비하고 격정적인 소회를 토해내는 모습을 듣고 있자니, 그의 성격을 잘 보여주는 좋은 본보기라는 생각이 들었습니다."]], [["“By my soul, Jarndyce,”", "\"맹세컨대, 잔다이스.\""], ["he said, very gently holding up a bit of bread to the canary to peck at, “if I were in your place I would seize every master in Chancery by the throat to-morrow morning and shake him until his money rolled out of his pockets and his bones rattled in his skin.", "그가 카나리아가 쪼아 먹도록 빵 한 조각을 아주 부드럽게 들어 올리며 말했습니다. \"내가 자네 처지라면 내일 아침 당장 대법원의 사무관들 멱살을 하나하나 거머쥐고, 주머니에서 돈이 쏟아져 나오고 가죽 속에서 뼈다귀가 덜그럭거릴 때까지 흔들어대겠어."], ["I would have a settlement out of somebody, by fair means or by foul.", "정당한 수단으로든 부정한 수단으로든 누군가에게서 합의를 받아내고 말겠어."], ["If you would empower me to do it, I would do it for you with the greatest satisfaction!”", "자네가 나한테 그럴 권한만 준다면, 더없이 흡족한 마음으로 자네를 위해 해주겠네!\""], ["(All this time the very small canary was eating out of his hand.)", "(이러는 내내 그 아주 자그마한 카나리아는 그의 손에서 먹이를 쪼아 먹고 있었습니다.)"]], [["“I thank you, Lawrence, but the suit is hardly at such a point at present,”", "\"고맙네, 로런스. 하지만 그 소송은 지금 그런 단계라고 보긴 어렵다네.\""], ["returned Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 웃으며 받았습니다."], ["Jarndyce, laughing, “that it would be greatly advanced even by the legal process of shaking the bench and the whole bar.”", "\"판사석과 변호인단 전부를 흔들어대는 법적 절차를 거친다 한들 크게 진척될 만한 처지가 아니거든.\""]], [["“There never was such an infernal cauldron as that Chancery on the face of the earth!”", "\"이 지구상에 그 대법원만큼 지독한 가마솥은 또 없을 거야!\""], ["said Mr. Boythorn.", "보이손 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Nothing but a mine below it on a busy day in term time, with all its records, rules, and precedents collected in it and every functionary belonging to it also, high and low, upward and downward, from its son the Accountant-General to its father the Devil, and the whole blown to atoms with ten thousand hundredweight of gunpowder, would reform it in the least!”", "\"개정 기간 한창 바쁜 날 그 밑에 지뢰를 묻고, 거기 딸린 기록이며 규칙이며 판례를 죄다 그러모으고, 거기 매인 관리란 관리는 높든 낮든, 위든 아래든, 그 아들뻘인 회계감사관에서부터 그 아비뻘인 악마에 이르기까지 죄다 한데 모아, 화약 만 헌드레드웨이트로 통째 산산조각 내어 날려버리는 것 말고는, 그걸 눈곱만큼이라도 뜯어고칠 길이 없어!\""]], [["It was impossible not to laugh at the energetic gravity with which he recommended this strong measure of reform.", "이토록 기운찬 진지함으로 그가 권하는 이 강력한 개혁책에 웃지 않기란 불가능했습니다."], ["When we laughed, he threw up his head and shook his broad chest, and again the whole country seemed to echo to his “Ha, ha, ha!”", "우리가 웃자 그는 고개를 쳐들고 떡 벌어진 가슴팍을 들썩였으며, 또다시 온 동네가 그의 \"하, 하, 하!\"에 메아리치는 듯했습니다."], ["It had not the least effect in disturbing the bird, whose sense of security was complete and who hopped about the table with its quick head now on this side and now on that, turning its bright sudden eye on its master as if he were no more than another bird.", "그래도 그 새를 흩트리는 데에는 조금의 효과도 없었으니, 안전하다는 확신에 가득 찬 그놈은 탁자 위를 폴짝폴짝 돌아다니며 잰 고개를 이쪽저쪽으로 갸웃거리고, 마치 주인이 별것 아닌 또 한 마리 새이기라도 한 양 그 밝고 재빠른 눈을 그에게 돌리곤 했습니다."]], [["“But how do you and your neighbour get on about the disputed right of way?”", "\"그런데 통행권 다툼을 두고 이웃과는 어찌 지내시오?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“You are not free from the toils of the law yourself!”", "\"당신도 법의 굴레에서 자유롭지는 못하니 말이오!\""]], [["“The fellow has brought actions against ME for trespass, and I have brought actions against HIM for trespass,”", "\"그놈은 무단침입으로 나한테 소송을 걸었고, 나도 무단침입으로 그놈한테 소송을 걸었네.\""], ["returned Mr. Boythorn.", "보이손 씨가 받았습니다."], ["“By heaven, he is the proudest fellow breathing.", "\"맹세코, 그놈은 숨 쉬는 사람 중에 가장 오만한 작자야."], ["It is morally impossible that his name can be Sir Leicester.", "그 이름이 레스터 경일 리가 도덕적으로 도무지 없어."], ["It must be Sir Lucifer.”", "분명 루시퍼 경일 거야.\""]], [["“Complimentary to our distant relation!”", "\"우리 먼 친척에 대한 칭찬이로군요!\""], ["said my guardian laughingly to Ada and Richard.", "후견인이 에이다와 리처드에게 웃으며 말했습니다."]], [["“I would beg Miss Clare’s pardon and Mr. Carstone’s pardon,”", "\"클레어 양과 카스톤 씨에게 용서를 빌어야 마땅하겠지.\""], ["resumed our visitor, “if I were not reassured by seeing in the fair face of the lady and the smile of the gentleman that it is quite unnecessary and that they keep their distant relation at a comfortable distance.”", "손님이 다시 말을 이었습니다. \"다만 아가씨의 고운 얼굴과 신사분의 미소에서, 그럴 필요가 조금도 없으며 두 분이 그 먼 친척을 적당히 편안한 거리에 두고 계시다는 걸 읽고 안심하지 않았더라면 말이야.\""]], [["“Or he keeps us,”", "\"아니면 그쪽이 우리를 두는 거겠지요.\""], ["suggested Richard.", "리처드가 거들었습니다."]], [["“By my soul,” exclaimed Mr.", "\"맹세코,\""], ["Boythorn, suddenly firing another volley, “that fellow is, and his father was, and his grandfather was, the most stiff-necked, arrogant imbecile, pig-headed numskull, ever, by some inexplicable mistake of Nature, born in any station of life but a walking-stick’s!", "보이손 씨가 별안간 또 한바탕 포문을 열며 외쳤습니다. \"그놈도, 그 아비도, 그 할아비도, 자연이 도무지 설명할 수 없는 실수를 저질러 지팡이 신세 말고는 어떤 처지로도 태어나지 말았어야 할, 둘도 없이 목이 뻣뻣하고 거만한 멍청이, 고집불통 돌대가리들이었어!"], ["The whole of that family are the most solemnly conceited and consummate blockheads!", "그 집안 전체가 더없이 엄숙하게 자만에 찬, 둘도 없는 얼간이들이라고!"], ["But it’s no matter; he should not shut up my path if he were fifty baronets melted into one and living in a hundred Chesney Wolds, one within another, like the ivory balls in a Chinese carving.", "하지만 상관없네. 그놈이 쉰 명의 준남작을 하나로 녹여 빚은 자라 한들, 그래서 중국 조각의 상아 공처럼 하나가 다른 하나를 품은 체스니 월드 백 채에 산다 한들, 내 길은 막지 못해."], ["The fellow, by his agent, or secretary, or somebody, writes to me ‘Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, presents his compliments to Mr.", "그놈이 제 대리인인지, 비서인지, 누군가를 시켜 나한테 이렇게 써 보낸단 말이지."], ["Lawrence Boythorn, and has to call his attention to the fact that the green pathway by the old parsonage-house, now the property of Mr. Lawrence Boythorn, is Sir Leicester’s right of way, being in fact a portion of the park of Chesney Wold, and that Sir Leicester finds it convenient to close up the same.’ I write to the fellow, ‘Mr.", "'준남작 레스터 데드록 경이 로런스 보이손 씨에게 안부를 전하며, 현재 로런스 보이손 씨의 소유인 옛 사제관 옆 푸른 오솔길은 사실상 체스니 월드 정원의 일부로서 레스터 경의 통행권에 속하는바, 레스터 경이 이를 폐쇄함이 편리하다고 보는 사실에 주의를 환기하고자 한다.'"], ["Lawrence Boythorn presents his compliments to Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and has to call HIS attention to the fact that he totally denies the whole of Sir Leicester Dedlock’s positions on every possible subject and has to add, in reference to closing up the pathway, that he will be glad to see the man who may undertake to do it.’ The fellow sends a most abandoned villain with one eye to construct a gateway.", "그래서 나는 그놈한테 이렇게 써 보내지. '로런스 보이손 씨가 준남작 레스터 데드록 경에게 안부를 전하며, 가능한 모든 사안에 걸쳐 레스터 데드록 경의 입장을 통째로 전면 부정하는 사실에 그의 주의를 환기하고자 하며, 오솔길 폐쇄와 관련해서는 그 일을 맡아 해보겠다는 자를 한번 만나 뵙기를 고대한다고 덧붙이는 바이다.'"], ["I play upon that execrable scoundrel with a fire-engine until the breath is nearly driven out of his body.", "그러자 그놈이 애꾸눈에 더없이 막돼먹은 악당을 보내 문을 세우려 들더군."], ["The fellow erects a gate in the night. I chop it down and burn it in the morning.", "나는 그 천하의 악당한테 소방 펌프를 들이대서 숨이 거의 다 빠져나갈 지경으로 물을 퍼부어줬지."], ["He sends his myrmidons to come over the fence and pass and repass.", "그놈은 한밤중에 문을 세워. 나는 아침에 그걸 찍어 넘어뜨리고 불태워버려."], ["I catch them in humane man traps, fire split peas at their legs, play upon them with the engine—resolve to free mankind from the insupportable burden of the existence of those lurking ruffians.", "그놈이 제 졸개들을 보내 울타리를 넘어 들락날락하게 하면, 나는 사람 다치지 않는 덫으로 그놈들을 잡고, 다리에 콩알을 쏘아대고, 펌프 물을 퍼부어대지—그렇게 숨어 다니는 망나니들의 존재라는 견딜 수 없는 짐에서 인류를 해방하기로 작정한 거야."], ["He brings actions for trespass; I bring actions for trespass.", "그놈이 무단침입으로 소송을 걸면, 나도 무단침입으로 소송을 걸어."], ["He brings actions for assault and battery; I defend them and continue to assault and batter.", "그놈이 폭행과 구타로 소송을 걸면, 나는 그걸 방어하면서 계속 폭행하고 구타하지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!”", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["To hear him say all this with unimaginable energy, one might have thought him the angriest of mankind.", "상상도 못 할 기세로 이 모든 말을 쏟아내는 그를 듣고 있노라면, 인류 중에 가장 성난 사람이라 여길 만했습니다."], ["To see him at the very same time, looking at the bird now perched upon his thumb and softly smoothing its feathers with his forefinger, one might have thought him the gentlest.", "바로 그 순간 엄지에 앉은 새를 들여다보며 집게손가락으로 그 깃털을 살살 쓰다듬는 그를 보고 있노라면, 더없이 온순한 사람이라 여길 만했습니다."], ["To hear him laugh and see the broad good nature of his face then, one might have supposed that he had not a care in the world, or a dispute, or a dislike, but that his whole existence was a summer joke.", "그가 웃는 소리를 듣고 그때 그 얼굴에 드넓게 번지는 호인다움을 보고 있노라면, 세상에 근심이라곤, 다툼이라곤, 미워하는 것이라곤 하나도 없이, 그의 온 생애가 여름날의 농담 한 자락이라 짐작할 만했습니다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"안 돼, 안 돼.\""], ["he said, “no closing up of my paths by any Dedlock!", "그가 말했습니다. \"데드록 따위가 내 길을 막다니, 어림도 없지!"], ["Though I willingly confess,”", "물론 기꺼이 인정하건대,\""], ["here he softened in a moment, “that Lady Dedlock is the most accomplished lady in the world, to whom I would do any homage that a plain gentleman, and no baronet with a head seven hundred years thick, may.", "여기서 그는 한순간에 누그러졌습니다. \"데드록 마님은 세상에서 가장 기품 넘치는 부인이며, 일곱 세기나 묵은 골통을 인 준남작 따위가 아니라 한낱 평범한 신사가 바칠 수 있는 어떤 경의라도 나는 그분께 바치겠어."], ["A man who joined his regiment at twenty and within a week challenged the most imperious and presumptuous coxcomb of a commanding officer that ever drew the breath of life through a tight waist—and got broke for it—is not the man to be walked over by all the Sir Lucifers, dead or alive, locked or unlocked.", "스무 살에 연대에 들어가 일주일도 안 되어, 꽉 졸라맨 허리로 숨을 쉬는 인간 중에 가장 거만하고 건방진 멋쟁이 지휘관에게 결투를 신청하고—그 때문에 강등까지 당한—사내가, 죽었든 살았든, 잠겼든 열렸든, 루시퍼 경 따위 누구한테도 짓밟힐 사내는 아니지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!”", "하, 하, 하!\""]], [["“Nor the man to allow his junior to be walked over either?”", "\"제 후배가 짓밟히는 것 또한 두고 볼 사내가 아니고요?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Most assuredly not!” said Mr.", "\"두말하면 잔소리지!\""], ["Boythorn, clapping him on the shoulder with an air of protection that had something serious in it, though he laughed.", "보이손 씨가 그의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했습니다. 웃고는 있었지만 그 보호하는 듯한 기색에는 어딘가 진지한 데가 있었습니다."], ["“He will stand by the low boy, always.", "\"그는 늘 약한 아이 편에 설 거야."], ["Jarndyce, you may rely upon him!", "잔다이스, 자네는 그를 믿어도 좋아!"], ["But speaking of this trespass—with apologies to Miss Clare and Miss Summerson for the length at which I have pursued so dry a subject—is there nothing for me from your men Kenge and Carboy?”", "그런데 이 무단침입 얘기 말인데—이토록 메마른 주제를 길게도 늘어놓은 데 대해 클레어 양과 서머슨 양께 사과를 드리며—자네 사람들, 켄지와 카보이한테서 나한테 온 건 없나?\""]], [["“I think not, Esther?”", "\"없는 것 같지, 에스더?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Nothing, guardian.”", "\"없어요, 후견인님.\""]], [["“Much obliged!”", "\"고맙군요!\""], ["said Mr. Boythorn.", "보이손 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Had no need to ask, after even my slight experience of Miss Summerson’s forethought for every one about her.”", "\"서머슨 양께서 주변 모두를 얼마나 살뜰히 챙기시는지 잠깐 겪어본 것만으로도, 물어볼 필요조차 없었는데 말입니다.\""], ["(They all encouraged me; they were determined to do it.) “I inquired because, coming from Lincolnshire, I of course have not yet been in town, and I thought some letters might have been sent down here.", "(다들 저를 추켜세웠습니다. 작정하고 그러는 것이었습니다.) \"내가 물어본 건, 링컨셔에서 온 터라 아직 시내에 들르지 못해, 혹 여기로 편지가 내려왔을까 싶어서였어요."], ["I dare say they will report progress to-morrow morning.”", "내일 아침이면 진척 상황을 알려오겠지요.\""]], [["I saw him so often in the course of the evening, which passed very pleasantly, contemplate Richard and Ada with an interest and a satisfaction that made his fine face remarkably agreeable as he sat at a little distance from the piano listening to the music—and he had small occasion to tell us that he was passionately fond of music, for his face showed it—that I asked my guardian as we sat at the backgammon board whether Mr. Boythorn had ever been married.", "매우 즐겁게 흘러간 그 저녁 내내, 저는 그가 피아노에서 조금 떨어져 앉아 음악에 귀 기울이며 리처드와 에이다를 바라보는 모습을 자주 보았습니다. 그 관심과 흐뭇함이 어찌나 그 멋진 얼굴을 유난히 보기 좋게 했던지—음악을 열렬히 좋아한다는 말을 굳이 우리에게 할 필요도 없었으니, 얼굴에 그대로 드러났던 것입니다—저는 우리가 주사위 놀이판 앞에 앉았을 때 후견인에게 보이손 씨가 결혼한 적이 있느냐고 물었습니다. \"아니.\" 그가 말했습니다. \"없어.\""]], [["“No,” said he. “No.” “But he meant to be!” said I.", "\"하지만 하려고는 하셨죠!\" 제가 말했습니다."]], [["“How did you find out that?”", "\"그건 어떻게 알았지?\""], ["he returned with a smile.", "그가 미소 지으며 받았습니다."], ["“Why, guardian,”", "\"그게요, 후견인님.\""], ["I explained, not without reddening a little at hazarding what was in my thoughts, “there is something so tender in his manner, after all, and he is so very courtly and gentle to us, and—”", "저는 마음속에 떠오른 생각을 무릅쓰고 말하려니 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 설명했습니다. \"어쨌든 저분 태도에는 어딘가 무척 다정한 데가 있고, 우리에게 그토록 정중하고 부드럽게 대해주시고, 또—\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce directed his eyes to where he was sitting as I have just described him.", "잔다이스 씨는 제가 방금 묘사한 대로 그가 앉아 있는 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸습니다."]], [["I said no more.", "저는 더 말하지 않았습니다."]], [["“You are right, little woman,”", "\"네 말이 맞아, 꼬마 아가씨.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 답했습니다."], ["“He was all but married once.", "\"그는 한 번, 거의 결혼할 뻔했지."], ["Long ago.", "아주 오래전에."], ["And once.”", "딱 한 번.\""]], [["“Did the lady die?”", "\"그 여인이 세상을 떠났나요?\""]], [["“No—but she died to him.", "\"아니—다만 그에게는 죽은 거나 마찬가지였어."], ["That time has had its influence on all his later life.", "그때 일이 그 뒤로 그의 온 삶에 그늘을 드리웠지."], ["Would you suppose him to have a head and a heart full of romance yet?”", "그가 아직도 머리와 가슴에 낭만을 가득 품고 있으리라 짐작이나 했겠니?\""]], [["“I think, guardian, I might have supposed so.", "\"제 생각엔, 후견인님, 어쩌면 짐작했을지도 모르겠어요."], ["But it is easy to say that when you have told me so.”", "하지만 그렇게 말씀해주시고 나니 그렇게 말하기야 쉽지요.\""]], [["“He has never since been what he might have been,” said Mr.", "\"그는 그 뒤로 한 번도 그가 될 수 있었던 사람이 되지 못했어.\""], ["Jarndyce, “and now you see him in his age with no one near him but his servant and his little yellow friend.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그래서 이제 그 나이가 되도록, 곁에 둔 거라곤 하인과 저 자그마한 노란 친구뿐인 모습을 너도 보는 거지."], ["It’s your throw, my dear!”", "네 차례다, 얘야!\""]], [["I felt, from my guardian’s manner, that beyond this point I could not pursue the subject without changing the wind.", "저는 후견인의 태도로 미루어, 이 이상은 바람의 방향을 바꾸지 않고서는 이 이야기를 더 캐물을 수 없겠다고 느꼈습니다. 그래서 더는 질문을 삼갔습니다."], ["I therefore forbore to ask any further questions.", "마음이 가긴 했지만 캐고 싶지는 않았습니다."], ["I was interested, but not curious. I thought a little while about this old love story in the night, when I was awakened by Mr.", "밤중에 보이손 씨의 우렁찬 코 고는 소리에 잠이 깼을 때, 저는 이 오래된 사랑 이야기를 잠시 곱씹어보았습니다."], ["Boythorn’s lusty snoring; and I tried to do that very difficult thing, imagine old people young again and invested with the graces of youth.", "그리고 무척이나 어려운 일, 곧 노인들을 다시 젊게, 청춘의 우아함을 두른 모습으로 그려보려 애썼습니다."], ["But I fell asleep before I had succeeded, and dreamed of the days when I lived in my godmother’s house.", "그러나 그 일을 이루기도 전에 잠이 들어, 대모님 댁에 살던 시절의 꿈을 꾸었습니다."], ["I am not sufficiently acquainted with such subjects to know whether it is at all remarkable that I almost always dreamed of that period of my life.", "저는 이런 일에 대해 잘 알지 못해, 제가 거의 늘 제 삶의 그 시절을 꿈꾸는 것이 조금이라도 별스러운 일인지 어떤지는 모르겠습니다."]], [["With the morning there came a letter from Messrs. Kenge and Carboy to Mr. Boythorn informing him that one of their clerks would wait upon him at noon. As it was the day of the week on which I paid the bills, and added up my books, and made all the household affairs as compact as possible, I remained at home while Mr. Jarndyce, Ada, and Richard took advantage of a very fine day to make a little excursion, Mr. Boythorn was to wait for Kenge and Carboy’s clerk and then was to go on foot to meet them on their return.", "아침이 되자 켄지 카보이 법률사무소에서 보이손 씨에게 편지가 와서, 정오에 사무원 하나가 찾아뵐 거라고 알렸습니다. 그날은 제가 청구서를 치르고, 장부를 결산하고, 집안 살림을 되도록 빈틈없이 추스르는 요일이었던지라, 잔다이스 씨와 에이다와 리처드가 더없이 맑은 날을 틈타 잠깐 소풍을 떠나는 동안 저는 집에 남았고, 보이손 씨는 켄지 카보이의 사무원을 기다렸다가 걸어서 나가 돌아오는 그들을 마중하기로 했습니다."]], [["Well!", "자!"], ["I was full of business, examining tradesmen’s books, adding up columns, paying money, filing receipts, and I dare say making a great bustle about it when Mr.", "저는 상인들의 장부를 살피고, 숫자 칸을 합산하고, 돈을 치르고, 영수증을 철하느라 일에 파묻혀, 분명 부산을 떨고 있었습니다."], ["Guppy was announced and shown in.", "그때 거피 씨가 왔다는 전갈과 함께 그가 안으로 안내되어 들어왔습니다."], ["I had had some idea that the clerk who was to be sent down might be the young gentleman who had met me at the coach-office, and I was glad to see him, because he was associated with my present happiness.", "내려보낸다는 그 사무원이 마차 사무소에서 저를 맞아주었던 그 젊은이일지도 모른다는 생각이 어렴풋이 들던 참이라, 저는 그를 보고 반가웠습니다. 그는 지금의 제 행복과 얽혀 있는 사람이었으니까요."]], [["I scarcely knew him again, he was so uncommonly smart.", "그가 어찌나 유난히 말쑥하던지 저는 그를 한눈에 알아보지도 못했습니다."], ["He had an entirely new suit of glossy clothes on, a shining hat, lilac-kid gloves, a neckerchief of a variety of colours, a large hot-house flower in his button-hole, and a thick gold ring on his little finger.", "윤기 흐르는 새 옷을 한 벌 빼입고, 번쩍이는 모자에, 연보라색 새끼염소 가죽 장갑에, 갖가지 색이 어우러진 목도리에, 단춧구멍에는 커다란 온실 꽃을 꽂고, 새끼손가락에는 두툼한 금반지를 끼고 있었습니다. 게다가 곰 기름이며 다른 향수 냄새로 식당을 온통 진동시켰습니다."], ["Besides which, he quite scented the dining-room with bear’s-grease and other perfumery.", "하인이 돌아올 때까지 자리에 앉으시라 권하자, 그가 저를 어찌나 빤히 보는지 저는 무척 당황했습니다."], ["He looked at me with an attention that quite confused me when I begged him to take a seat until the servant should return; and as he sat there crossing and uncrossing his legs in a corner, and I asked him if he had had a pleasant ride, and hoped that Mr. Kenge was well, I never looked at him, but I found him looking at me in the same scrutinizing and curious way.", "그가 구석에 앉아 다리를 꼬았다 풀었다 하는 동안, 제가 오는 길이 편하셨느냐고, 켄지 씨는 안녕하시냐고 물었지만, 그를 볼 때마다 그 역시 똑같이 꼬치꼬치 캐는 듯 호기심 어린 눈으로 저를 바라보고 있는 것이었습니다."]], [["When the request was brought to him that he would go upstairs to Mr. Boythorn’s room, I mentioned that he would find lunch prepared for him when he came down, of which Mr.", "위층 보이손 씨 방으로 올라와달라는 청이 그에게 전해지자, 저는 내려오면 점심이 차려져 있을 테니 잔다이스 씨께서 들기를 바라신다고 일러두었습니다."], ["Jarndyce hoped he would partake.", "그는 다소 어색한 기색으로 문손잡이를 잡은 채 말했습니다."], ["He said with some embarrassment, holding the handle of the door, “Shall I have the honour of finding you here, miss?”", "\"내려오면 여기서 아가씨를 뵙는 영광을 누릴 수 있을까요?\""], ["I replied yes, I should be there; and he went out with a bow and another look.", "저는 네, 여기 있겠다고 답했고, 그는 인사를 꾸벅하고 한 번 더 저를 보고는 나갔습니다."]], [["I thought him only awkward and shy, for he was evidently much embarrassed; and I fancied that the best thing I could do would be to wait until I saw that he had everything he wanted and then to leave him to himself.", "저는 그가 그저 서투르고 수줍은 사람이라고만 여겼습니다. 무척 당황한 게 분명했으니까요."], ["The lunch was soon brought, but it remained for some time on the table.", "그래서 제가 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은 그에게 필요한 것이 다 갖춰졌는지 확인할 때까지 기다렸다가 혼자 두는 것이라고 생각했습니다."], ["The interview with Mr.", "점심은 곧 차려졌지만, 한동안 탁자 위에 그대로 놓여 있었습니다."], ["Boythorn was a long one, and a stormy one too, I should think, for although his room was at some distance I heard his loud voice rising every now and then like a high wind, and evidently blowing perfect broadsides of denunciation.", "보이손 씨와의 면담은 길었고, 또 제 짐작엔 험악하기까지 했던 모양입니다. 그 방이 좀 떨어져 있는데도 이따금 거센 바람처럼 치솟는 그의 우렁찬 목소리가 들렸고, 분명 비난의 일제포격을 완벽하게 퍼붓고 있었으니까요."]], [["At last Mr. Guppy came back, looking something the worse for the conference.", "마침내 거피 씨가 그 면담으로 다소 시달린 기색으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["“My eye, miss,”", "\"이런, 아가씨.\""], ["he said in a low voice, “he’s a Tartar!”", "그가 나직이 말했습니다. \"그분 정말 무서운 양반이네요!\""]], [["“Pray take some refreshment, sir,”", "\"좀 드시지요, 선생님.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["Mr. Guppy sat down at the table and began nervously sharpening the carving-knife on the carving-fork, still looking at me (as I felt quite sure without looking at him) in the same unusual manner.", "거피 씨는 탁자에 앉아 고기 써는 칼을 고기 포크에 대고 초조하게 갈기 시작하면서도, (제가 그를 보지 않고도 똑똑히 느꼈듯이) 여전히 그 예사롭지 않은 눈초리로 저를 바라보았습니다."], ["The sharpening lasted so long that at last I felt a kind of obligation on me to raise my eyes in order that I might break the spell under which he seemed to labour, of not being able to leave off.", "칼 가는 일이 어찌나 오래 이어지던지, 마침내 저는 멈추지 못하고 끌려가는 듯한 그의 그 주문을 깨주어야 할 것 같은 일종의 의무감에 눈을 들었습니다."]], [["He immediately looked at the dish and began to carve.", "그러자 그는 곧장 접시로 눈을 돌리고 고기를 썰기 시작했습니다."]], [["“What will you take yourself, miss?", "\"아가씨는 뭘 좀 드시겠어요?"], ["You’ll take a morsel of something?”", "한 점이라도 드시지요?\""]], [["“No, thank you,”", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Shan’t I give you a piece of anything at all, miss?”", "\"정말 아무것도 한 점 안 드릴까요, 아가씨?\""], ["said Mr. Guppy, hurriedly drinking off a glass of wine.", "거피 씨가 포도주 한 잔을 황급히 들이켜며 말했습니다."]], [["“Nothing, thank you,”", "\"아무것도요, 고맙습니다.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."], ["“I have only waited to see that you have everything you want.", "\"저는 그저 선생님께 필요한 것이 다 갖춰졌는지 확인하려고 기다린 것뿐입니다."], ["Is there anything I can order for you?”", "제가 시켜드릴 것이라도 있을까요?\""]], [["“No, I am much obliged to you, miss, I’m sure.", "\"아닙니다, 정말 감사합니다, 아가씨."], ["I’ve everything that I can require to make me comfortable—at least I—not comfortable—I’m never that.”", "편히 지내는 데 필요한 건 다 있습니다—적어도 저는—편하진 않습니다—저는 한 번도 편한 적이 없으니까요.\""], ["He drank off two more glasses of wine, one after another.", "그는 포도주를 잇따라 두 잔 더 들이켰습니다."]], [["I thought I had better go.", "저는 자리를 뜨는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, miss!”", "\"실례합니다, 아가씨!\""], ["said Mr. Guppy, rising when he saw me rise.", "제가 일어서는 걸 보고 거피 씨가 따라 일어서며 말했습니다."], ["“But would you allow me the favour of a minute’s private conversation?”", "\"그런데 잠깐 단둘이 말씀 나누는 호의를 베풀어주실 수 있을까요?\""]], [["Not knowing what to say, I sat down again.", "뭐라 해야 할지 몰라 저는 다시 앉았습니다."]], [["“What follows is without prejudice, miss?” said Mr.", "\"지금부터 드릴 말씀은 불리하게 작용하지 않는 거지요, 아가씨?\""], ["Guppy, anxiously bringing a chair towards my table.", "거피 씨가 의자를 제 탁자 쪽으로 끌어오며 조마조마하게 말했습니다."]], [["“I don’t understand what you mean,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said I, wondering.", "의아해하며 제가 말했습니다."]], [["“It’s one of our law terms, miss.", "\"저희 쪽 법률 용어 가운데 하나입니다, 아가씨."], ["You won’t make any use of it to my detriment at Kenge and Carboy’s or elsewhere.", "켄지 카보이에서든 다른 어디서든 이 말을 제게 불리하게 쓰지 않으신다는 거지요."], ["If our conversation shouldn’t lead to anything, I am to be as I was and am not to be prejudiced in my situation or worldly prospects.", "우리 대화가 아무것도 낳지 못하더라도, 저는 본래대로 남고, 제 처지나 세상 앞날에 불리함을 입지 않는다는 겁니다."], ["In short, it’s in total confidence.”", "한마디로, 완전한 비밀이라는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["“I am at a loss, sir,”", "\"저는 도무지 헤아리기 어렵습니다, 선생님.\" 제가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “to imagine what you can have to communicate in total confidence to me, whom you have never seen but once; but I should be very sorry to do you any injury.”", "\"딱 한 번 뵈었을 뿐인 저에게 무슨 완전한 비밀을 털어놓으실 게 있는지 말입니다. 다만 선생님께 어떤 해라도 끼치게 된다면 무척 안타까울 것입니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, miss.", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨."], ["I’m sure of it—that’s quite sufficient.” All this time Mr.", "그러시리라 믿습니다—그것으로 충분합니다.\""], ["Guppy was either planing his forehead with his handkerchief or tightly rubbing the palm of his left hand with the palm of his right.", "이러는 내내 거피 씨는 손수건으로 이마를 문지르거나, 오른 손바닥으로 왼 손바닥을 꽉꽉 비벼대고 있었습니다."], ["“If you would excuse my taking another glass of wine, miss, I think it might assist me in getting on without a continual choke that cannot fail to be mutually unpleasant.”", "\"포도주를 한 잔 더 드는 걸 너그러이 봐주신다면, 아가씨, 그게 서로에게 불쾌할 수밖에 없는 그 끊임없는 목멤 없이 말을 이어가는 데 도움이 될 것 같습니다.\""]], [["He did so, and came back again.", "그는 그렇게 하고는 다시 돌아왔습니다."], ["I took the opportunity of moving well behind my table.", "저는 그 틈을 타 탁자 뒤로 한참 물러섰습니다."]], [["“You wouldn’t allow me to offer you one, would you miss?”", "\"아가씨는 한 잔 권해도 안 받으시겠지요?\""], ["said Mr. Guppy, apparently refreshed.", "거피 씨가 한결 기운을 차린 듯 말했습니다."]], [["“Not any,”", "\"안 받겠습니다.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Not half a glass?”", "\"반 잔도요?\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Quarter? No!", "\"사분의 일은요?"], ["Then, to proceed.", "안 되겠군요!"], ["My present salary, Miss Summerson, at Kenge and Carboy’s, is two pound a week.", "그러면, 본론으로 들어가겠습니다. 켄지 카보이에서 제가 지금 받는 봉급은, 서머슨 양, 주급 이 파운드입니다."], ["When I first had the happiness of looking upon you, it was one fifteen, and had stood at that figure for a lengthened period.", "처음 아가씨를 뵙는 행복을 누렸을 때는 일 파운드 십오 실링이었고, 그 액수에 오래도록 머물러 있었습니다."], ["A rise of five has since taken place, and a further rise of five is guaranteed at the expiration of a term not exceeding twelve months from the present date.", "그 뒤로 오 실링이 올랐고, 지금부터 열두 달을 넘지 않는 기한이 끝날 때 다시 오 실링 인상이 보장되어 있습니다."], ["My mother has a little property, which takes the form of a small life annuity, upon which she lives in an independent though unassuming manner in the Old Street Road.", "제 어머니는 자그마한 재산이 있는데, 소액 종신 연금의 형태여서, 그것으로 올드 스트리트 로드에서 검소하나마 자립해 사십니다."], ["She is eminently calculated for a mother-in-law.", "어머니는 시어머니 자리에 더없이 어울리는 분입니다."], ["She never interferes, is all for peace, and her disposition easy.", "결코 참견하지 않으시고, 매사 평화를 좋아하시며, 성품이 무던하십니다."], ["She has her failings—as who has not?—but I never knew her do it when company was present, at which time you may freely trust her with wines, spirits, or malt liquors.", "흠이야 있지요—흠 없는 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?—하지만 손님이 계실 땐 그러시는 걸 한 번도 본 적이 없으니, 그럴 땐 포도주든 독주든 맥주든 마음 놓고 맡기셔도 됩니다."], ["My own abode is lodgings at Penton Place, Pentonville.", "저 자신의 거처는 펜턴빌의 펜턴 플레이스에 있는 셋방입니다."], ["It is lowly, but airy, open at the back, and considered one of the ’ealthiest outlets.", "누추하나 바람이 잘 통하고, 뒤가 트여 있어, 가장 '건강한' 외곽으로 꼽힙니다."], ["Miss Summerson!", "서머슨 양!"], ["In the mildest language, I adore you.", "더없이 점잖은 말로 표현하자면, 저는 아가씨를 흠모합니다."], ["Would you be so kind as to allow me (as I may say) to file a declaration—to make an offer!”", "부디 제가 (말하자면) 선언장을 제출하는—청혼을 올리는 호의를 허락해주시겠습니까!\""]], [["Mr. Guppy went down on his knees.", "거피 씨가 무릎을 꿇었습니다."], ["I was well behind my table and not much frightened.", "저는 탁자 뒤로 한참 물러나 있던 터라 그리 겁나지는 않았습니다. 저는 말했습니다."], ["I said, “Get up from that ridiculous position immediately, sir, or you will oblige me to break my implied promise and ring the bell!”", "\"그 우스꽝스러운 자세에서 당장 일어나세요, 선생님. 안 그러면 제가 한 암묵적인 약속을 깨고 종을 울릴 수밖에 없습니다!\""]], [["“Hear me out, miss!” said Mr.", "\"끝까지 들어주십시오, 아가씨!\""], ["Guppy, folding his hands.", "거피 씨가 두 손을 모으며 말했습니다."]], [["“I cannot consent to hear another word, sir,”", "\"한마디도 더 들을 수 없습니다, 선생님.\""], ["I returned, “Unless you get up from the carpet directly and go and sit down at the table as you ought to do if you have any sense at all.”", "제가 받았습니다. \"조금이라도 분별이 있으시다면 마땅히 그러셔야 하듯, 당장 양탄자에서 일어나 탁자로 가 앉지 않으시는 한 말입니다.\""]], [["He looked piteously, but slowly rose and did so.", "그는 가엾은 표정을 지으면서도 천천히 일어나 그렇게 했습니다."]], [["“Yet what a mockery it is, miss,”", "\"하지만 이 무슨 조롱입니까, 아가씨.\""], ["he said with his hand upon his heart and shaking his head at me in a melancholy manner over the tray, “to be stationed behind food at such a moment.", "그가 가슴에 손을 얹고 쟁반 너머로 우울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했습니다. \"이런 순간에 음식 뒤에 세워지다니요."], ["The soul recoils from food at such a moment, miss.”", "이런 순간에 영혼은 음식을 물리치는 법입니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["“I beg you to conclude,”", "\"부디 끝맺어주십시오.\""], ["said I; “you have asked me to hear you out, and I beg you to conclude.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"끝까지 들어달라 하셨으니, 부디 끝맺어주십시오.\""]], [["“I will, miss,”", "\"그러겠습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“As I love and honour, so likewise I obey.", "\"제가 사랑하고 공경하듯, 또한 그렇게 따르겠습니다."], ["Would that I could make thee the subject of that vow before the shrine!”", "제단 앞에서 그대를 그 서약의 대상으로 삼을 수만 있다면!\""]], [["“That is quite impossible,”", "\"그건 전혀 불가능합니다.\""], ["said I, “and entirely out of the question.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"전적으로 말도 안 되는 일이고요.\""]], [["“I am aware,” said Mr.", "\"저도 압니다.\""], ["Guppy, leaning forward over the tray and regarding me, as I again strangely felt, though my eyes were not directed to him, with his late intent look, “I am aware that in a worldly point of view, according to all appearances, my offer is a poor one.", "거피 씨가 쟁반 위로 몸을 기울이며, 비록 제 눈은 그에게 향하지 않았으나 또다시 묘하게 느껴지듯, 아까 그 골똘한 눈초리로 저를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"세상 잣대로 보자면, 겉으로 드러나는 모든 정황으로 보자면, 제 청혼이 보잘것없다는 걸 압니다."], ["But, Miss Summerson!", "하지만, 서머슨 양!"], ["Angel!", "천사여!"], ["No, don’t ring—I have been brought up in a sharp school and am accustomed to a variety of general practice.", "아니, 종은 울리지 마십시오—저는 모진 곳에서 자라 온갖 잡다한 실무에 익숙합니다."], ["Though a young man, I have ferreted out evidence, got up cases, and seen lots of life.", "젊은 사람이지만, 증거를 캐내고, 사건을 꾸미고, 세상사를 숱하게 보아왔습니다."], ["Blest with your hand, what means might I not find of advancing your interests and pushing your fortunes!", "그대의 손을 얻는 복을 누린다면, 그대의 이익을 키우고 그대의 운수를 밀어주는 데 무슨 수단인들 못 찾겠습니까!"], ["What might I not get to know, nearly concerning you?", "그대와 깊이 관련된 무슨 일인들 알아내지 못하겠습니까!"], ["I know nothing now, certainly; but what MIGHT I not if I had your confidence, and you set me on?”", "지금 당장은 분명 아무것도 모릅니다. 하지만 그대의 믿음을 얻고 그대가 저를 부추겨주신다면, 무슨 일인들 못 알아내겠습니까?\""]], [["I told him that he addressed my interest or what he supposed to be my interest quite as unsuccessfully as he addressed my inclination, and he would now understand that I requested him, if he pleased, to go away immediately.", "저는 그가 제 마음에 호소한 것 못지않게 제 이익이라는 것에—그가 제 이익이라 여기는 것에—호소한 일도 전혀 성공하지 못했다고, 그러니 괜찮으시다면 당장 떠나주십사 청한다는 걸 이제 알아들으셨으면 한다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Cruel miss,” said Mr.", "\"매정한 아가씨.\""], ["Guppy, “hear but another word!", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["I think you must have seen that I was struck with those charms on the day when I waited at the Whytorseller.", "\"다만 한마디만 더 들어주십시오! 제가 '하얀 말 셀러'에서 시중들던 날 그 매력에 마음을 빼앗긴 걸 분명 보셨을 줄 압니다."], ["I think you must have remarked that I could not forbear a tribute to those charms when I put up the steps of the ’ackney-coach.", "제가 삯마차 발판을 접어 올릴 때 그 매력에 경의를 표하지 않을 수 없었던 걸 분명 알아채셨을 줄 압니다."], ["It was a feeble tribute to thee, but it was well meant.", "그대에게 바친 변변찮은 경의였지만, 진심에서 우러난 것이었습니다."], ["Thy image has ever since been fixed in my breast.", "그날 이후로 그대의 모습이 줄곧 제 가슴에 박혀 있습니다."], ["I have walked up and down of an evening opposite Jellyby’s house only to look upon the bricks that once contained thee.", "저녁이면 젤리비 댁 맞은편을 오르내리며, 한때 그대를 품었던 벽돌이나마 바라보았습니다."], ["This out of to-day, quite an unnecessary out so far as the attendance, which was its pretended object, went, was planned by me alone for thee alone.", "오늘 이 나들이는, 핑계로 내세운 업무로 따지면 전혀 필요 없는 나들이였으나, 오로지 그대만을 위해 저 혼자 꾸민 것이었습니다."], ["If I speak of interest, it is only to recommend myself and my respectful wretchedness.", "제가 이익을 말한 것도, 그저 저 자신과 저의 공손한 비참함을 천거하려는 것일 따름입니다."], ["Love was before it, and is before it.”", "사랑이 그보다 앞섰고, 지금도 앞섭니다.\""]], [["“I should be pained, Mr. Guppy,”", "\"저는 마음이 아플 것입니다, 거피 씨.\""], ["said I, rising and putting my hand upon the bell-rope, “to do you or any one who was sincere the injustice of slighting any honest feeling, however disagreeably expressed.", "제가 일어나 종 줄에 손을 얹으며 말했습니다. \"선생님께든, 진심을 다하는 누구에게든, 아무리 거북하게 표현된 것일지라도 그 정직한 마음을 가벼이 여겨 부당하게 대하는 일은 말입니다."], ["If you have really meant to give me a proof of your good opinion, though ill-timed and misplaced, I feel that I ought to thank you.", "비록 때와 자리가 어긋났을지언정 정말로 저에게 호의의 증표를 보이려 하신 거라면, 저는 마땅히 감사를 드려야 한다고 느낍니다."], ["I have very little reason to be proud, and I am not proud.", "저는 우쭐할 까닭이 거의 없으며, 우쭐하지도 않습니다."], ["I hope,”", "바라건대,\""], ["I think I added, without very well knowing what I said, “that you will now go away as if you had never been so exceedingly foolish and attend to Messrs.", "제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 이렇게 덧붙인 듯합니다."], ["Kenge and Carboy’s business.”", "\"이제 마치 이토록 어리석은 짓은 한 적도 없는 양 떠나시어, 켄지 카보이의 업무에 마음 쓰셨으면 합니다.\""]], [["“Half a minute, miss!”", "\"잠깐만요, 아가씨!\""], ["cried Mr. Guppy, checking me as I was about to ring.", "제가 막 종을 울리려는데 거피 씨가 저를 멈춰 세우며 외쳤습니다."], ["“This has been without prejudice?”", "\"이건 불리하게 작용하지 않는 거지요?\""]], [["“I will never mention it,”", "\"결코 입에 올리지 않겠습니다.\""], ["said I, “unless you should give me future occasion to do so.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"선생님께서 앞으로 제게 그럴 빌미를 주지 않으시는 한 말입니다.\""]], [["“A quarter of a minute, miss! In case you should think better at any time, however distant—THAT’S no consequence, for my feelings can never alter—of anything I have said, particularly what might I not do, Mr.", "\"십오 초만요, 아가씨!"], ["William Guppy, eighty-seven, Penton Place, or if removed, or dead (of blighted hopes or anything of that sort), care of Mrs. Guppy, three hundred and two, Old Street Road, will be sufficient.”", "혹 언젠가, 아무리 먼 훗날이라도—그건 상관없습니다, 제 마음은 결코 변할 리 없으니까요—제가 드린 어떤 말씀에 대해, 특히 제가 무슨 일인들 못 하겠느냐던 그 대목에 대해 마음을 고쳐 잡으시거든, 펜턴 플레이스 팔십칠번지 윌리엄 거피, 혹 이사를 갔거나, (꺾인 희망이니 뭐니 그런 까닭으로) 죽었거든 올드 스트리트 로드 삼백이번지 거피 부인 앞으로, 그것이면 충분합니다.\""]], [["I rang the bell, the servant came, and Mr. Guppy, laying his written card upon the table and making a dejected bow, departed.", "저는 종을 울렸고, 하인이 들어왔으며, 거피 씨는 자기 명함을 탁자에 내려놓고 풀 죽은 인사를 꾸벅하고는 떠났습니다."], ["Raising my eyes as he went out, I once more saw him looking at me after he had passed the door.", "그가 나갈 때 눈을 들어보니, 문을 지난 뒤에도 다시 한번 저를 바라보고 있었습니다."]], [["I sat there for another hour or more, finishing my books and payments and getting through plenty of business.", "저는 그 자리에 한 시간 남짓 더 앉아, 장부와 지불을 마무리하고 일을 잔뜩 해치웠습니다."], ["Then I arranged my desk, and put everything away, and was so composed and cheerful that I thought I had quite dismissed this unexpected incident.", "그러고는 책상을 정리하고, 모든 걸 치워두었으며, 어찌나 마음이 가라앉고 명랑해졌던지 이 뜻밖의 일을 완전히 떨쳐버린 줄 알았습니다."], ["But, when I went upstairs to my own room, I surprised myself by beginning to laugh about it and then surprised myself still more by beginning to cry about it.", "하지만 제 방으로 올라가서는, 그 일을 두고 웃기 시작하는 저 자신에 깜짝 놀랐고, 이어 그 일을 두고 울기 시작하는 저 자신에 더욱 깜짝 놀랐습니다."], ["In short, I was in a flutter for a little while and felt as if an old chord had been more coarsely touched than it ever had been since the days of the dear old doll, long buried in the garden.", "한마디로, 저는 잠시 마음이 술렁였고, 오래전 정원에 묻힌 그 사랑스러운 낡은 인형 시절 이후로 한 번도 건드려진 적 없던 옛 가락의 줄이, 그때보다 더 거칠게 튕겨진 듯한 기분이 들었습니다."]], [["The Law-Writer\nOn the eastern borders of Chancery Lane, that is to say, more particularly in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, Mr. Snagsby, law-stationer, pursues his lawful calling.", "챈서리 레인 동쪽 변두리, 더 정확히 말하면 커시터 거리의 쿡스 코트에서, 법률 문구상 스내그스비 씨가 합법적인 생업에 종사한다."], ["In the shade of Cook’s Court, at most times a shady place, Mr. Snagsby has dealt in all sorts of blank forms of legal process; in skins and rolls of parchment; in paper—foolscap, brief, draft, brown, white, whitey-brown, and blotting; in stamps; in office-quills, pens, ink, India-rubber, pounce, pins, pencils, sealing-wax, and wafers; in red tape and green ferret; in pocket-books, almanacs, diaries, and law lists; in string boxes, rulers, inkstands—glass and leaden—pen-knives, scissors, bodkins, and other small office-cutlery; in short, in articles too numerous to mention, ever since he was out of his time and went into partnership with Peffer.", "대체로 그늘진 곳인 쿡스 코트의 그늘 아래에서, 스내그스비 씨는 온갖 종류의 법률 절차용 빈 서식이며, 양피지 가죽과 두루마리며, 종이—대판지, 약식 서류용지, 초안용지, 갈색지, 백색지, 누르스름한 백색지, 압지—며, 인지며, 사무용 깃펜, 펜, 잉크, 지우개, 가루 분말, 핀, 연필, 봉랍, 봉함지며, 빨간 끈과 초록 끈이며, 수첩, 달력, 일기장, 법률 인명록이며, 노끈 상자, 자, 잉크병—유리와 납으로 된 것—펜나이프, 가위, 송곳, 그 밖의 자잘한 사무용 칼붙이며, 한마디로 일일이 열거하기엔 너무 많은 물품을 다뤄왔으니, 그가 도제 생활을 마치고 페퍼와 동업에 들어선 이래로 줄곧 그래왔다."], ["On that occasion, Cook’s Court was in a manner revolutionized by the new inscription in fresh paint, PEFFER AND SNAGSBY, displacing the time-honoured and not easily to be deciphered legend PEFFER only.", "그 무렵 쿡스 코트는 갓 칠한 새 간판, 곧 오래되어 알아보기도 어려운 ‘페퍼’라는 글자만 적힌 옛 명패를 밀어내고 들어선 ‘페퍼 앤드 스내그스비’ 덕분에 어떤 의미에서 혁명을 맞은 셈이다."], ["For smoke, which is the London ivy, had so wreathed itself round Peffer’s name and clung to his dwelling-place that the affectionate parasite quite overpowered the parent tree.", "런던의 담쟁이덩굴이라 할 매연이 페퍼의 이름을 칭칭 휘감고 그의 거처에 들러붙은 나머지, 그 정 깊은 기생식물이 모체인 나무를 완전히 짓눌러버렸기 때문이다."]], [["Peffer is never seen in Cook’s Court now.", "페퍼는 이제 쿡스 코트에서 보이지 않는다."], ["He is not expected there, for he has been recumbent this quarter of a century in the churchyard of St. Andrews, Holborn, with the waggons and hackney-coaches roaring past him all the day and half the night like one great dragon.", "그가 이곳에 나타나리라 기대하는 사람도 없으니, 그는 지난 사반세기 동안 홀본의 세인트 앤드루스 묘지에 누워 있고, 짐마차와 삯마차가 하루 종일, 또 밤의 절반 동안 한 마리 거대한 용처럼 그의 곁을 우르릉거리며 지나다닌다."], ["If he ever steal forth when the dragon is at rest to air himself again in Cook’s Court until admonished to return by the crowing of the sanguine cock in the cellar at the little dairy in Cursitor Street, whose ideas of daylight it would be curious to ascertain, since he knows from his personal observation next to nothing about it—if Peffer ever do revisit the pale glimpses of Cook’s Court, which no law-stationer in the trade can positively deny, he comes invisibly, and no one is the worse or wiser.", "만약 그 용이 잠잠해진 틈에 그가 슬그머니 빠져나와 다시 쿡스 코트에서 바람을 쐰다면—커시터 거리의 작은 우유 가게 지하실에서 핏빛처럼 붉은 수탉이 울어 그만 돌아가라고 일러줄 때까지 말이다. 그 수탉이 낮이란 걸 어떻게 알고 있는지 알아내는 일도 흥미로울 텐데, 제 눈으로 직접 본 것이라곤 거의 없는 처지이니—만약 페퍼가 쿡스 코트의 희미한 빛 줄기를 정말로 다시 찾는다면, 이 바닥의 어느 법률 문구상도 단호히 부정하지 못할 일이거니와, 그는 보이지 않게 찾아오므로 누구도 더 손해 볼 일이 없고 더 알게 될 일도 없다."]], [["In his lifetime, and likewise in the period of Snagsby’s “time” of seven long years, there dwelt with Peffer in the same law-stationering premises a niece—a short, shrewd niece, something too violently compressed about the waist, and with a sharp nose like a sharp autumn evening, inclining to be frosty towards the end.", "페퍼가 살아 있을 적, 또한 스내그스비가 칠 년이라는 긴 도제 ‘기간’을 보내던 시절, 같은 법률 문구상 건물에서 페퍼와 함께 조카딸 하나가 살았다—키 작고 약삭빠른 조카딸로, 허리는 좀 지나치다 싶을 만큼 졸라매여 있었고, 가을 저녁처럼 뾰족한 코는 끝으로 갈수록 서리가 내릴 듯 쌀쌀맞았다."], ["The Cook’s Courtiers had a rumour flying among them that the mother of this niece did, in her daughter’s childhood, moved by too jealous a solicitude that her figure should approach perfection, lace her up every morning with her maternal foot against the bed-post for a stronger hold and purchase; and further, that she exhibited internally pints of vinegar and lemon-juice, which acids, they held, had mounted to the nose and temper of the patient.", "쿡스 코트 주민들 사이에는 소문 하나가 떠돌았는데, 이 조카딸의 어머니가 딸이 어릴 적, 딸의 몸매가 완벽에 가까워지기를 너무도 질투심 어린 정성으로 바란 나머지, 매일 아침 더 단단히 힘을 주려고 침대 기둥에 어머니의 발을 받친 채 딸을 동여맸다는 것이며, 게다가 식초와 레몬즙을 몇 파인트씩 속으로 들이부었는데, 그 산성이 환자의 코와 성미로 치솟아 올랐다는 것이다."], ["With whichsoever of the many tongues of Rumour this frothy report originated, it either never reached or never influenced the ears of young Snagsby, who, having wooed and won its fair subject on his arrival at man’s estate, entered into two partnerships at once. So now, in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, Mr.", "소문이라는 여러 혀 가운데 어느 것에서 이 거품 같은 이야기가 비롯되었든, 그것은 젊은 스내그스비의 귀에 끝내 닿지 않았거나 닿았어도 아무 영향을 미치지 못했으니, 그는 성년에 이르자마자 그 어여쁜 주인공에게 구애해 손에 넣었고, 단번에 두 가지 동업 관계에 발을 들였다."], ["Snagsby and the niece are one; and the niece still cherishes her figure, which, however tastes may differ, is unquestionably so far precious that there is mighty little of it.", "그리하여 이제 커시터 거리의 쿡스 코트에서는, 스내그스비 씨와 그 조카딸이 한 몸이다. 조카딸은 여전히 제 몸매를 애지중지하는데, 취향이야 제각각이겠으나, 그 몸매가 워낙 얼마 되지도 않는다는 점에서만큼은 의심할 여지 없이 귀하다."]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "스내그스비 부부는 한 뼈에 한 살일 뿐 아니라, 이웃들이 보기엔 한 목소리이기도 하다."], ["Snagsby are not only one bone and one flesh, but, to the neighbours’ thinking, one voice too.", "오직 스내그스비 부인에게서만 흘러나오는 듯한 그 목소리는 쿡스 코트에서 아주 자주 들린다."], ["That voice, appearing to proceed from Mrs. Snagsby alone, is heard in Cook’s Court very often.", "스내그스비 씨는 그 감미로운 가락을 통하지 않고서는 좀처럼 목소리를 내는 일이 없다."], ["Mr. Snagsby, otherwise than as he finds expression through these dulcet tones, is rarely heard.", "그는 온순하고 머리가 벗어진, 소심한 남자로, 번들거리는 머리통 뒤쪽에 짧고 뻣뻣한 검은 머리카락 한 줌이 삐죽 솟아 있다."], ["He is a mild, bald, timid man with a shining head and a scrubby clump of black hair sticking out at the back.", "그는 유순함과 비만으로 기우는 편이다."], ["He tends to meekness and obesity. As he stands at his door in Cook’s Court in his grey shop-coat and black calico sleeves, looking up at the clouds, or stands behind a desk in his dark shop with a heavy flat ruler, snipping and slicing at sheepskin in company with his two ’prentices, he is emphatically a retiring and unassuming man.", "회색 가게용 외투에 검은 옥양목 토시를 끼고 쿡스 코트의 문간에 서서 구름을 올려다보거나, 어두운 가게 안쪽 책상 뒤에 서서 묵직한 평자를 들고 두 도제와 함께 양피지를 자르고 베어낼 때면, 그는 두드러지게 내성적이고 겸손한 사람이다."], ["From beneath his feet, at such times, as from a shrill ghost unquiet in its grave, there frequently arise complainings and lamentations in the voice already mentioned; and haply, on some occasions when these reach a sharper pitch than usual, Mr.", "그럴 때면 그의 발밑에서, 마치 무덤 속에서 안식하지 못하는 날카로운 유령에게서 나오듯, 앞서 말한 그 목소리로 푸념과 탄식이 자주 솟아오른다. 그러다 어쩌다 그 소리가 평소보다 한층 날카로운 음으로 치달을 때면, 스내그스비 씨는 도제들에게 이렇게 말한다."], ["Snagsby mentions to the ’prentices, “I think my little woman is a-giving it to Guster!”", "“우리 마누라가 거스터를 닦달하는 모양이군!”"]], [["This proper name, so used by Mr. Snagsby, has before now sharpened the wit of the Cook’s Courtiers to remark that it ought to be the name of Mrs.", "스내그스비 씨가 이렇게 쓰는 이 고유명사는, 일찍부터 쿡스 코트 주민들의 재치를 자극해, 그것이야말로 스내그스비 부인의 이름이어야 마땅하다는 말을 끌어내곤 했다."], ["Snagsby, seeing that she might with great force and expression be termed a Guster, in compliment to her stormy character.", "부인의 폭풍 같은 성격에 빗대면 ‘거스터’라 불러도 더없이 힘차고 표현력 있게 들어맞으리라는 것이다. 그러나 그것은, 연봉 오십 실링과 옷가지가 어중간하게 채워진 아주 작은 상자 하나를 빼고는, 구빈원 출신의 비쩍 마른 젊은 여자가 가진 유일한 소유물이자 이름이다."], ["It is, however, the possession, and the only possession except fifty shillings per annum and a very small box indifferently filled with clothing, of a lean young woman from a workhouse (by some supposed to have been christened Augusta) who, although she was farmed or contracted for during her growing time by an amiable benefactor of his species resident at Tooting, and cannot fail to have been developed under the most favourable circumstances, “has fits,” which the parish can’t account for.", "(어떤 이들은 그 여자가 오거스타라는 이름으로 세례를 받았으리라 짐작한다.) 그 여자는 자라는 동안 투팅에 사는, 동족을 사랑하는 어느 자애로운 자선가에게 위탁되거나 계약되어 길러졌으니, 더없이 좋은 환경에서 자라났음에 틀림없을 텐데도, “발작을 일으키고”, 그 까닭은 교구로서도 도무지 설명할 길이 없다."]], [["Guster, really aged three or four and twenty, but looking a round ten years older, goes cheap with this unaccountable drawback of fits, and is so apprehensive of being returned on the hands of her patron saint that except when she is found with her head in the pail, or the sink, or the copper, or the dinner, or anything else that happens to be near her at the time of her seizure, she is always at work.", "거스터는 실제 나이가 스물서넛쯤이지만 족히 열 살은 더 들어 보이는데, 그 영문 모를 발작이라는 흠 때문에 헐값에 부려진다."], ["She is a satisfaction to the parents and guardians of the ’prentices, who feel that there is little danger of her inspiring tender emotions in the breast of youth; she is a satisfaction to Mrs. Snagsby, who can always find fault with her; she is a satisfaction to Mr.", "게다가 제 수호성인의 손에 도로 떠넘겨질까 너무도 겁을 내는 통에, 발작이 일어난 순간 마침 곁에 있던 물통이나 개수대, 구리솥, 저녁 음식, 그 밖의 무언가에 머리를 처박은 채 발견될 때를 빼고는, 늘 일하는 중이다."], ["Snagsby, who thinks it a charity to keep her. The law-stationer’s establishment is, in Guster’s eyes, a temple of plenty and splendour.", "그 여자는 도제들의 부모와 보호자들에게는 만족스러운 존재이니, 그 가슴에 풋풋한 연정을 불러일으킬 위험이 거의 없다고들 여기기 때문이다."], ["She believes the little drawing-room upstairs, always kept, as one may say, with its hair in papers and its pinafore on, to be the most elegant apartment in Christendom.", "또 늘 트집 잡을 거리를 찾을 수 있는 스내그스비 부인에게도 만족스러운 존재이고, 그 여자를 거두는 것이 자선이라고 여기는 스내그스비 씨에게도 만족스러운 존재다."], ["The view it commands of Cook’s Court at one end (not to mention a squint into Cursitor Street) and of Coavinses’ the sheriff’s officer’s backyard at the other she regards as a prospect of unequalled beauty.", "거스터의 눈에 이 법률 문구상의 가게는 풍요와 화려함의 전당이다. 그 여자는 위층의 작은 응접실—말하자면 늘 머리에 종이를 말고 앞치마를 두른 듯 단정히 지켜지는 그 방이 기독교 세계에서 가장 우아한 방이라 믿는다."], ["The portraits it displays in oil—and plenty of it too—of Mr. Snagsby looking at Mrs.", "한쪽 끝으로는 쿡스 코트가 (커시터 거리를 곁눈질하는 것은 말할 것도 없고) 내다보이고, 다른 쪽 끝으로는 집달리 코아빈스의 뒷마당이 내다보이는 그 전망을, 그 여자는 견줄 데 없는 아름다움의 풍경이라 여긴다."], ["Snagsby and of Mrs. Snagsby looking at Mr.", "그 방에 걸린 유화 초상화들—그것도 잔뜩 걸려 있다—곧 스내그스비 부인을 바라보는 스내그스비 씨의 초상과, 스내그스비 씨를 바라보는 스내그스비 부인의 초상은, 그 여자의 눈에는 라파엘이나 티치아노의 걸작이나 다름없다."], ["Snagsby are in her eyes as achievements of Raphael or Titian. Guster has some recompenses for her many privations.", "거스터는 그 숱한 결핍에 대해 나름의 보상을 누리는 셈이다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby refers everything not in the practical mysteries of the business to Mrs.", "스내그스비 씨는 가게 운영의 실무적 비법에 속하지 않는 모든 일을 스내그스비 부인에게 맡긴다."], ["Snagsby. She manages the money, reproaches the tax-gatherers, appoints the times and places of devotion on Sundays, licenses Mr.", "부인은 돈을 관리하고, 세금 징수원을 나무라고, 일요일의 예배 시간과 장소를 정하고, 스내그스비 씨의 여흥을 허가하며, 저녁 식탁에 무엇을 올릴지 자기 마음대로 정하는 일에 대해서는 아무런 책임도 인정하지 않는다."], ["Snagsby’s entertainments, and acknowledges no responsibility as to what she thinks fit to provide for dinner, insomuch that she is the high standard of comparison among the neighbouring wives a long way down Chancery Lane on both sides, and even out in Holborn, who in any domestic passages of arms habitually call upon their husbands to look at the difference between their (the wives’) position and Mrs. Snagsby’s, and their (the husbands’) behaviour and Mr.", "그리하여 부인은 챈서리 레인 양편으로 멀리까지, 심지어 홀본 바깥에 이르는 동네 아낙들 사이에서 비교의 드높은 기준이 되어, 집안싸움이 벌어질 때면 아낙들은 으레 남편들에게 자기들(아낙들)의 처지와 스내그스비 부인의 처지를 견주어보라, 또 자기들(남편들)의 행실과 스내그스비 씨의 행실이 얼마나 다른지 보라고 들이댄다."], ["Snagsby’s. Rumour, always flying bat-like about Cook’s Court and skimming in and out at everybody’s windows, does say that Mrs.", "늘 박쥐처럼 쿡스 코트를 누비며 누구네 창이든 들락날락 스치고 다니는 소문은 이렇게 말한다."], ["Snagsby is jealous and inquisitive and that Mr. Snagsby is sometimes worried out of house and home, and that if he had the spirit of a mouse he wouldn’t stand it.", "스내그스비 부인은 질투가 심하고 캐묻기 좋아하며, 스내그스비 씨는 때때로 제 집에서조차 시달려 쫓겨날 지경인데, 만약 그에게 쥐만 한 기개라도 있다면 그런 꼴을 참지 않으리라고."], ["It is even observed that the wives who quote him to their self-willed husbands as a shining example in reality look down upon him and that nobody does so with greater superciliousness than one particular lady whose lord is more than suspected of laying his umbrella on her as an instrument of correction. But these vague whisperings may arise from Mr.", "심지어 제 고집 센 남편들에게 그를 빛나는 본보기랍시고 들먹이는 아낙들이 실은 그를 깔본다는 말도 있으니, 그 누구보다 거만하게 그러는 이는 어느 부인 하나로, 그 부인의 남편으로 말할 것 같으면 우산을 교정 도구로 삼아 아내에게 휘두른다는 의심을 단단히 받고 있다."], ["Snagsby’s being in his way rather a meditative and poetical man, loving to walk in Staple Inn in the summer-time and to observe how countrified the sparrows and the leaves are, also to lounge about the Rolls Yard of a Sunday afternoon and to remark (if in good spirits) that there were old times once and that you’d find a stone coffin or two now under that chapel, he’ll be bound, if you was to dig for it.", "그러나 이런 어렴풋한 수군거림은 스내그스비 씨가 나름대로 사색을 즐기고 시심이 깊은 사람이라는 데서 비롯된 것인지도 모른다. 그는 여름철이면 스테이플 인을 거닐며 참새와 나뭇잎이 어쩌면 그리도 시골스러운지 살피기를 좋아하고, 일요일 오후면 롤스 야드를 어슬렁거리며 (기분이 좋을 때면) 옛 시절도 있었지 하면서, 저 예배당 밑을 파보면 분명 돌 관 한두 개쯤은 나올 거라고 장담하기를 좋아한다."], ["He solaces his imagination, too, by thinking of the many Chancellors and Vices, and Masters of the Rolls who are deceased; and he gets such a flavour of the country out of telling the two ’prentices how he HAS heard say that a brook “as clear as crystal”", "또 그는 세상을 떠난 그 많은 대법원장과 부원장과 기록보관장관을 떠올리며 상상에 위안을 얻는다. 그리고 두 도제에게, 턴스타일이 진짜 회전문이던 시절, “수정처럼 맑은”"], ["once ran right down the middle of Holborn, when Turnstile really was a turnstile, leading slap away into the meadows—gets such a flavour of the country out of this that he never wants to go there.", "시냇물이 홀본 한복판을 곧장 흘러 풀밭으로 곧바로 빠져나갔다더라는 이야기를 들려주며 어찌나 시골 정취를 듬뿍 우려내는지—그렇게 시골 정취를 듬뿍 우려내는 통에, 정작 그곳에 갈 마음은 영영 들지 않는다."]], [["The day is closing in and the gas is lighted, but is not yet fully effective, for it is not quite dark. Mr.", "날이 저물어가고 가스등이 켜졌지만, 아직 완전히 어둡지는 않은 터라 그 빛이 온전히 위력을 발휘하지는 못한다."], ["Snagsby standing at his shop-door looking up at the clouds sees a crow who is out late skim westward over the slice of sky belonging to Cook’s Court.", "가게 문간에 서서 구름을 올려다보던 스내그스비 씨는, 늦도록 나돌던 까마귀 한 마리가 쿡스 코트 몫의 하늘 조각 위를 서쪽으로 스쳐 가는 것을 본다."], ["The crow flies straight across Chancery Lane and Lincoln’s Inn Garden into Lincoln’s Inn Fields.", "까마귀는 챈서리 레인과 링컨스 인 가든을 곧장 가로질러 링컨스 인 필즈로 날아든다."]], [["Here, in a large house, formerly a house of state, lives Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "이곳, 한때 권세가의 저택이던 어느 큰 집에 토킹혼 씨가 산다."], ["It is let off in sets of chambers now, and in those shrunken fragments of its greatness, lawyers lie like maggots in nuts.", "지금은 여러 채의 셋방으로 쪼개져, 그 위세가 쪼그라든 조각들 속에서 변호사들이 호두 속 구더기처럼 들어앉아 있다."], ["But its roomy staircases, passages, and antechambers still remain; and even its painted ceilings, where Allegory, in Roman helmet and celestial linen, sprawls among balustrades and pillars, flowers, clouds, and big-legged boys, and makes the head ache—as would seem to be Allegory’s object always, more or less.", "그러나 그 널찍한 계단과 복도와 대기실은 여전히 남아 있고, 채색된 천장마저 그대로다. 그 천장에서는 로마식 투구를 쓰고 천상의 아마포를 두른 우의(寓意)가 난간과 기둥과 꽃과 구름과 다리 굵은 소년들 사이에 큰대자로 뻗어, 보는 이의 머리를 지끈거리게 한다—언제나 머리를 아프게 하는 것이야말로 우의의 목적인 듯싶다, 어느 정도는 말이다."], ["Here, among his many boxes labelled with transcendent names, lives Mr. Tulkinghorn, when not speechlessly at home in country-houses where the great ones of the earth are bored to death.", "이곳, 거창한 이름이 적힌 그 숱한 상자들 틈에 토킹혼 씨가 사는데, 세상의 권세가들이 따분해 죽을 지경에 빠져 있는 시골 저택에서 말없이 머무르지 않을 때면 그렇다."], ["Here he is to-day, quiet at his table.", "오늘도 그는 이곳에서 책상 앞에 조용히 앉아 있다."], ["An oyster of the old school whom nobody can open.", "누구도 까지 못하는 구식 굴 한 마리다."]], [["Like as he is to look at, so is his apartment in the dusk of the present afternoon.", "생김새가 그러하듯, 오늘 오후의 땅거미 속 그의 방도 그러하다."], ["Rusty, out of date, withdrawing from attention, able to afford it.", "녹슬고, 시대에 뒤떨어졌고, 시선에서 물러나 있으나, 그럴 만한 여유가 있다."], ["Heavy, broad-backed, old-fashioned, mahogany-and-horsehair chairs, not easily lifted; obsolete tables with spindle-legs and dusty baize covers; presentation prints of the holders of great titles in the last generation or the last but one, environ him.", "묵직하고 등판 넓은 구식의, 들기조차 쉽지 않은 마호가니와 말총 의자들이며, 가락처럼 가느다란 다리에 먼지 앉은 모직 천이 덮인 한물간 탁자들이며, 지난 세대나 그 전 세대의 거창한 작위를 지녔던 이들이 증정한 판화들이 그를 에워싼다."], ["A thick and dingy Turkey-carpet muffles the floor where he sits, attended by two candles in old-fashioned silver candlesticks that give a very insufficient light to his large room.", "두껍고 칙칙한 터키 양탄자가 그가 앉은 바닥의 소리를 잠재우고, 구식 은 촛대에 꽂힌 양초 두 자루가 그의 큰 방에 아주 모자란 빛을 던진다."], ["The titles on the backs of his books have retired into the binding; everything that can have a lock has got one; no key is visible.", "책등에 적힌 제목들은 제본 속으로 물러나 숨었고, 자물쇠를 채울 수 있는 것은 죄다 자물쇠가 채워져 있으며, 열쇠는 하나도 보이지 않는다."], ["Very few loose papers are about.", "낱장 서류는 거의 흩어져 있지 않다."], ["He has some manuscript near him, but is not referring to it.", "그의 곁에는 필사본이 하나 있지만, 그는 그것을 들여다보지 않는다."], ["With the round top of an inkstand and two broken bits of sealing-wax he is silently and slowly working out whatever train of indecision is in his mind.", "잉크병의 둥근 뚜껑과 봉랍 부스러기 두 조각으로, 그는 제 마음속에 자리한 망설임의 갈피를 잡으려 말없이 천천히 궁리해본다."], ["Now the inkstand top is in the middle, now the red bit of sealing-wax, now the black bit.", "이제 잉크병 뚜껑이 한가운데에, 이제 빨간 봉랍 조각이, 이제 검은 조각이."], ["That’s not it.", "그게 아니다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn must gather them all up and begin again.", "토킹혼 씨는 그것들을 죄다 그러모아 다시 시작해야 한다."]], [["Here, beneath the painted ceiling, with foreshortened Allegory staring down at his intrusion as if it meant to swoop upon him, and he cutting it dead, Mr. Tulkinghorn has at once his house and office.", "이곳, 채색된 천장 아래, 짧게 줄어든 우의가 마치 그에게 덮쳐들 작정인 듯 그의 침입을 내려다보고, 그는 그것을 본 척도 않은 채, 토킹혼 씨는 집과 사무실을 한데 두고 있다."], ["He keeps no staff, only one middle-aged man, usually a little out at elbows, who sits in a high pew in the hall and is rarely overburdened with business.", "그는 직원을 두지 않고, 다만 중년 남자 하나를 둘 뿐인데, 대개 팔꿈치가 좀 해진 차림으로 현관의 높은 칸막이 자리에 앉아 있고, 좀처럼 일에 치이는 법이 없다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn is not in a common way.", "토킹혼 씨는 흔한 방식의 사람이 아니다."], ["He wants no clerks.", "그에게 서기 따위는 필요 없다."], ["He is a great reservoir of confidences, not to be so tapped.", "그는 비밀이 고인 거대한 저수지이니, 그렇게 함부로 길어낼 수 없다."], ["His clients want HIM; he is all in all.", "그의 의뢰인들은 바로 그를 원한다."], ["Drafts that he requires to be drawn are drawn by special-pleaders in the temple on mysterious instructions; fair copies that he requires to be made are made at the stationers’, expense being no consideration.", "그가 전부인 것이다. 그가 작성을 원하는 초안은 신비로운 지시에 따라 템플의 특별 변론가들이 작성하고, 그가 원하는 정서본은 비용을 따지지 않고 문구상에서 만들어진다."], ["The middle-aged man in the pew knows scarcely more of the affairs of the peerage than any crossing-sweeper in Holborn.", "칸막이 자리의 그 중년 남자는 홀본의 어느 길거리 청소부보다도 귀족 사회의 일에 대해 거의 더 아는 바가 없다."]], [["The red bit, the black bit, the inkstand top, the other inkstand top, the little sand-box.", "빨간 조각, 검은 조각, 잉크병 뚜껑, 다른 잉크병 뚜껑, 작은 모래통."], ["So!", "자!"], ["You to the middle, you to the right, you to the left.", "너는 가운데로, 너는 오른쪽으로, 너는 왼쪽으로."], ["This train of indecision must surely be worked out now or never.", "이 망설임의 갈피는 이제 풀어내든지 영영 못 풀든지 해야 한다."], ["Now! Mr.", "지금이다!"], ["Tulkinghorn gets up, adjusts his spectacles, puts on his hat, puts the manuscript in his pocket, goes out, tells the middle-aged man out at elbows, “I shall be back presently.”", "토킹혼 씨가 일어나 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 모자를 쓰고 필사본을 주머니에 넣은 다음 밖으로 나가며, 팔꿈치 해진 중년 남자에게 “곧 돌아오겠네”라고 이른다."], ["Very rarely tells him anything more explicit.", "이보다 더 분명한 말을 그에게 건네는 일은 좀처럼 없다."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn goes, as the crow came—not quite so straight, but nearly—to Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street.", "토킹혼 씨는 까마귀가 왔던 길을 따라—그만큼 곧장은 아니지만 거의 비슷하게—커시터 거리의 쿡스 코트로 간다."], ["To Snagsby’s, Law-Stationer’s, Deeds engrossed and copied, Law-Writing executed in all its branches, &c., &c., &c.", "스내그스비 법률 문구상으로, 곧 ‘증서 정서 및 복사, 모든 분야의 법률 필사 시행, 기타 등등, 기타 등등, 기타 등등’이라 적힌 곳으로."]], [["It is somewhere about five or six o’clock in the afternoon, and a balmy fragrance of warm tea hovers in Cook’s Court.", "지금은 오후 다섯 시나 여섯 시쯤이고, 따뜻한 차의 향긋한 내음이 쿡스 코트에 감돈다."], ["It hovers about Snagsby’s door.", "그 내음은 스내그스비의 문간을 맴돈다."], ["The hours are early there: dinner at half-past one and supper at half-past nine. Mr.", "이곳의 시간표는 이른 편이라, 점심은 한 시 반, 저녁은 아홉 시 반이다."], ["Snagsby was about to descend into the subterranean regions to take tea when he looked out of his door just now and saw the crow who was out late.", "스내그스비 씨는 차를 마시려고 지하 영역으로 내려가려던 참이었는데, 방금 문밖을 내다보다가 늦도록 나돌던 그 까마귀를 본 것이다."]], [["“Master at home?”", "“주인장 계신가?”"]], [["Guster is minding the shop, for the ’prentices take tea in the kitchen with Mr. and Mrs.", "가게는 거스터가 지키고 있는데, 도제들이 스내그스비 부부와 함께 부엌에서 차를 마시는 까닭이다."], ["Snagsby; consequently, the robe-maker’s two daughters, combing their curls at the two glasses in the two second-floor windows of the opposite house, are not driving the two ’prentices to distraction as they fondly suppose, but are merely awakening the unprofitable admiration of Guster, whose hair won’t grow, and never would, and it is confidently thought, never will.", "따라서 맞은편 집 이층 두 창문에서 두 거울에 비추어 곱슬머리를 빗는 의상 제작자의 두 딸은, 저희가 흐뭇이 짐작하는 대로 두 도제를 애간장 녹게 하는 것이 아니라, 그저 거스터의 보람 없는 동경을 일깨우고 있을 뿐이다. 거스터의 머리카락은 자라지 않으며, 여태 한 번도 자란 적이 없고, 앞으로도 영영 자라지 않으리라고 다들 굳게 믿는다."]], [["“Master at home?”", "“주인장 계신가?”"], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."]], [["Master is at home, and Guster will fetch him.", "주인장은 안에 있고, 거스터가 그를 부르러 간다."], ["Guster disappears, glad to get out of the shop, which she regards with mingled dread and veneration as a storehouse of awful implements of the great torture of the law—a place not to be entered after the gas is turned off.", "거스터는 가게에서 벗어나게 된 것이 반가워 사라진다. 그 여자는 가게를, 법이라는 거대한 고문에 쓰일 무시무시한 도구들이 가득한 창고로, 두려움과 외경이 뒤섞인 마음으로 바라보니, 가스 불을 끈 뒤에는 결코 들어가서는 안 될 곳이다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby appears, greasy, warm, herbaceous, and chewing.", "스내그스비 씨가 번들거리고 따뜻하고 풀 냄새 풍기는 차림으로 음식을 씹으며 나타난다."], ["Bolts a bit of bread and butter.", "버터 바른 빵 한 조각을 꿀꺽 삼킨다."], ["Says, “Bless my soul, sir!", "이렇게 말한다. “아이고, 이런!"], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn!”", "토킹혼 씨 아니십니까!”"]], [["“I want half a word with you, Snagsby.”", "“자네에게 잠깐 한마디 하려고 왔네, 스내그스비.”"]], [["“Certainly, sir!", "“여부가 있겠습니까, 선생님!"], ["Dear me, sir, why didn’t you send your young man round for me?", "아이고 이런, 선생님, 어찌 사람을 시켜 저를 부르지 않으셨습니까?"], ["Pray walk into the back shop, sir.”", "부디 안쪽 가게로 드시지요.”"], ["Snagsby has brightened in a moment.", "스내그스비는 순식간에 환해진다."]], [["The confined room, strong of parchment-grease, is warehouse, counting-house, and copying-office.", "비좁은 그 방은 양피지 기름 냄새가 짙게 배어, 창고이자 회계실이자 필사실이다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn sits, facing round, on a stool at the desk.", "토킹혼 씨가 몸을 돌려 책상 앞 걸상에 앉는다."]], [["“Jarndyce and Jarndyce, Snagsby.”", "“잔다이스 대 잔다이스 건일세, 스내그스비.”"]], [["“Yes, sir.” Mr.", "“예, 선생님.”"], ["Snagsby turns up the gas and coughs behind his hand, modestly anticipating profit. Mr.", "스내그스비 씨가 가스 불을 돋우고 손으로 입을 가린 채 기침을 하며, 이문이 생길 것을 점잖게 미리 기대한다."], ["Snagsby, as a timid man, is accustomed to cough with a variety of expressions, and so to save words.", "소심한 사람인 스내그스비 씨는 갖가지 뜻을 담아 기침함으로써 말을 아끼는 데 익숙하다."]], [["“You copied some affidavits in that cause for me lately.”", "“요전에 그 사건의 진술서 몇 장을 자네가 베껴주었지.”"]], [["“Yes, sir, we did.”", "“예, 선생님, 그렇게 했지요.”"]], [["“There was one of them,” says Mr.", "“그중 하나가 말일세,”"], ["Tulkinghorn, carelessly feeling—tight, unopenable oyster of the old school!—in the wrong coat-pocket, “the handwriting of which is peculiar, and I rather like.", "토킹혼 씨가 무심히—굳게 닫혀 열리지 않는 구식 굴이여!—엉뚱한 외투 주머니를 더듬으며 말한다. “필체가 독특해서 내 마음에 제법 들었거든."], ["As I happened to be passing, and thought I had it about me, I looked in to ask you—but I haven’t got it.", "마침 지나는 길이고, 가지고 있는 줄 알아서, 들러 자네에게 물어보려던 참인데—없군."], ["No matter, any other time will do.", "상관없네, 다음에 아무 때나 하지."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["here it is!", "여기 있군!"], ["I looked in to ask you who copied this.”", "이걸 누가 베꼈는지 물어보려고 들렀네.”"]], [["“Who copied this, sir?” says Mr.", "“이걸 누가 베꼈느냐고요, 선생님?”"], ["Snagsby, taking it, laying it flat on the desk, and separating all the sheets at once with a twirl and a twist of the left hand peculiar to lawstationers. “We gave this out, sir.", "스내그스비 씨가 그것을 받아 책상 위에 평평히 펼쳐놓고, 법률 문구상 특유의 왼손 손놀림으로 휙 비틀어 단번에 모든 장을 갈라놓으며 말한다."], ["We were giving out rather a large quantity of work just at that time.", "“이건 저희가 하청을 주었습니다, 선생님. 그때 마침 일감을 제법 많이 내보내던 참이었지요."], ["I can tell you in a moment who copied it, sir, by referring to my book.”", "제 장부를 들춰보면 누가 베꼈는지 금세 말씀드릴 수 있습니다, 선생님.”"]], [["Mr.", "스내그스비 씨가 금고에서 장부를 꺼내 내리고, 어딘가에 걸린 듯하던 버터 바른 빵 조각을 다시 한번 꿀꺽 삼키더니, 진술서를 곁눈질하면서 오른쪽 집게손가락을 장부의 한 면을 따라 죽 미끄러뜨린다."], ["Snagsby takes his book down from the safe, makes another bolt of the bit of bread and butter which seemed to have stopped short, eyes the affidavit aside, and brings his right forefinger travelling down a page of the book, “Jewby—Packer—Jarndyce.”", "“주비—패커—잔다이스.”"]], [["“Jarndyce!", "“잔다이스!"], ["Here we are, sir,” says Mr.", "여기 있군요, 선생님,”"], ["Snagsby. “To be sure!", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."], ["I might have remembered it.", "“그렇고말고요!"], ["This was given out, sir, to a writer who lodges just over on the opposite side of the lane.”", "진작 떠올렸어야 했는데. 이건 바로 길 건너편에 하숙하는 필경사에게 맡긴 것입니다, 선생님.”"]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn has seen the entry, found it before the law-stationer, read it while the forefinger was coming down the hill.", "토킹혼 씨는 이미 그 기재 항목을 보았으니, 법률 문구상보다 먼저 찾아냈고, 그 집게손가락이 비탈을 내려오는 동안 다 읽어버렸다."]], [["“WHAT do you call him?", "“그자를 뭐라고 부르나?"], ["Nemo?”", "네모?”"], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“Nemo, sir. Here it is.", "“네모입니다, 선생님."], ["Forty-two folio.", "여기 있네요."], ["Given out on the Wednesday night at eight o’clock, brought in on the Thursday morning at half after nine.”", "사절판 마흔두 장. 수요일 밤 여덟 시에 내주어, 목요일 아침 아홉 시 반에 들여왔습니다.”"]], [["“Nemo!”", "“네모!”"], ["repeats Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 되뇐다."], ["Tulkinghorn. “Nemo is Latin for no one.”", "“네모는 라틴어로 ‘아무도 아닌 사람’이라는 뜻이지.”"]], [["“It must be English for some one, sir, I think,” Mr.", "“제 생각엔 그게 영어로는 ‘어떤 사람’이라는 뜻이지 싶습니다, 선생님,”"], ["Snagsby submits with his deferential cough.", "스내그스비 씨가 공손한 기침과 함께 의견을 내놓는다."], ["“It is a person’s name.", "“사람 이름이니까요."], ["Here it is, you see, sir!", "보십시오, 여기 있지 않습니까, 선생님!"], ["Forty-two folio.", "사절판 마흔두 장."], ["Given out Wednesday night, eight o’clock; brought in Thursday morning, half after nine.”", "수요일 밤 여덟 시에 내주고, 목요일 아침 아홉 시 반에 들여왔습니다.”"]], [["The tail of Mr. Snagsby’s eye becomes conscious of the head of Mrs. Snagsby looking in at the shop-door to know what he means by deserting his tea. Mr. Snagsby addresses an explanatory cough to Mrs. Snagsby, as who should say, “My dear, a customer!”", "스내그스비 씨의 곁눈에, 그가 어쩌자고 차를 내팽개쳤는지 알아보려고 가게 문간을 들여다보는 스내그스비 부인의 머리가 들어온다. 스내그스비 씨가 스내그스비 부인을 향해 설명조의 기침을 건네는데, 마치 “여보, 손님이라오!”라고 말하는 듯하다."]], [["“Half after nine, sir,”", "“아홉 시 반입니다, 선생님,”"], ["repeats Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 되뇐다."], ["“Our law-writers, who live by job-work, are a queer lot; and this may not be his name, but it’s the name he goes by.", "“삯일로 먹고사는 저희 법률 필경사들은 묘한 족속이라, 이게 그자의 본명이 아닐지도 모르지만, 어쨌든 그가 행세하는 이름입니다."], ["I remember now, sir, that he gives it in a written advertisement he sticks up down at the Rule Office, and the King’s Bench Office, and the Judges’ Chambers, and so forth.", "이제 생각났는데요, 선생님, 그자가 규칙 사무소며 왕좌 재판소 사무소며 판사 집무실이며 그런 데에 손수 써서 내붙이는 광고에 그 이름을 적습니다."], ["You know the kind of document, sir—wanting employ?”", "어떤 종류의 문서인지 아시지요, 선생님—일감을 구한다는 것 말입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn glances through the little window at the back of Coavinses’, the sheriff’s officer’s, where lights shine in Coavinses’ windows.", "토킹혼 씨가 집달리 코아빈스의 뒤편 작은 창을 통해 흘끔 내다보니, 코아빈스의 창문에 불빛이 비친다."], ["Coavinses’ coffee-room is at the back, and the shadows of several gentlemen under a cloud loom cloudily upon the blinds. Mr.", "코아빈스의 커피실은 뒤쪽에 있는데, 어두컴컴한 처지의 신사 여럿이 드리운 그림자가 차양막 위로 어렴풋이 어른거린다."], ["Snagsby takes the opportunity of slightly turning his head to glance over his shoulder at his little woman and to make apologetic motions with his mouth to this effect: “Tul-king-horn—rich—in-flu-en-tial!”", "스내그스비 씨는 그 틈을 타 살짝 고개를 돌려 어깨 너머로 제 마누라를 흘끗 보고는, 입을 놀려 이런 뜻으로 변명하는 시늉을 한다. “토킹—혼—부자—세도가!”"]], [["“Have you given this man work before?”", "“이 사람에게 전에도 일을 준 적이 있나?”"], ["asks Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 묻는다."]], [["“Oh, dear, yes, sir!", "“아이고, 그럼요, 선생님!"], ["Work of yours.”", "선생님 일감이었지요.”"]], [["“Thinking of more important matters, I forget where you said he lived?”", "“더 중요한 일을 생각하느라, 어디 산다고 했는지 잊었네만?”"]], [["“Across the lane, sir.", "“길 건너편입니다, 선생님."], ["In fact, he lodges at a—”", "실은 그자가 하숙하는 데가—”"], ["Mr. Snagsby makes another bolt, as if the bit of bread and butter were insurmountable “—at a rag and bottle shop.”", "스내그스비 씨가 버터 바른 빵 조각이 도무지 넘어가지 않는다는 듯 또 한 번 꿀꺽 삼킨다 “—넝마와 병을 다루는 가게입니다.”"]], [["“Can you show me the place as I go back?”", "“돌아가는 길에 그곳을 보여줄 수 있겠나?”"]], [["“With the greatest pleasure, sir!”", "“더없는 기쁨이지요, 선생님!”"]], [["Mr. Snagsby pulls off his sleeves and his grey coat, pulls on his black coat, takes his hat from its peg.", "스내그스비 씨가 토시를 벗고 회색 외투를 벗더니, 검은 외투를 걸치고 못에서 모자를 집어 든다."], ["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["Here is my little woman!”", "여기 우리 마누라가 있군요!”"], ["he says aloud.", "그가 큰 소리로 말한다."], ["“My dear, will you be so kind as to tell one of the lads to look after the shop while I step across the lane with Mr. Tulkinghorn?", "“여보, 내가 토킹혼 씨와 함께 잠깐 길 건너에 다녀오는 동안 누구 한 녀석더러 가게를 봐달라고 일러주겠소?"], ["Mrs. Snagsby, sir—I shan’t be two minutes, my love!”", "제 아내입니다, 선생님—이 분도 안 걸려요, 여보!”"]], [["Mrs. Snagsby bends to the lawyer, retires behind the counter, peeps at them through the window-blind, goes softly into the back office, refers to the entries in the book still lying open.", "스내그스비 부인이 변호사에게 허리를 굽히고는 계산대 뒤로 물러나, 차양막 너머로 두 사람을 엿보다가, 살그머니 안쪽 사무실로 들어가, 아직 펼쳐진 채 놓인 장부의 기재 항목을 들여다본다."], ["Is evidently curious.", "호기심이 일었음이 분명하다."]], [["“You will find that the place is rough, sir,” says Mr.", "“그곳이 거칠다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다, 선생님,”"], ["Snagsby, walking deferentially in the road and leaving the narrow pavement to the lawyer; “and the party is very rough.", "스내그스비 씨가 좁은 인도를 변호사에게 양보하고 자기는 공손히 차도로 걸으며 말한다. “그 작자도 아주 거칠고요."], ["But they’re a wild lot in general, sir.", "하기야 대개가 거친 족속들입니다, 선생님."], ["The advantage of this particular man is that he never wants sleep.", "이 사람의 좋은 점이라면, 도통 잠을 자려 들지 않는다는 겁니다."], ["He’ll go at it right on end if you want him to, as long as ever you like.”", "원하시면 끝장을 볼 때까지, 원하시는 만큼 얼마든지 곧장 일에 매달리지요.”"]], [["It is quite dark now, and the gas-lamps have acquired their full effect.", "이제는 완전히 어두워졌고, 가스등은 온전히 위력을 발휘하게 되었다."], ["Jostling against clerks going to post the day’s letters, and against counsel and attorneys going home to dinner, and against plaintiffs and defendants and suitors of all sorts, and against the general crowd, in whose way the forensic wisdom of ages has interposed a million of obstacles to the transaction of the commonest business of life; diving through law and equity, and through that kindred mystery, the street mud, which is made of nobody knows what and collects about us nobody knows whence or how—we only knowing in general that when there is too much of it we find it necessary to shovel it away—the lawyer and the law-stationer come to a rag and bottle shop and general emporium of much disregarded merchandise, lying and being in the shadow of the wall of Lincoln’s Inn, and kept, as is announced in paint, to all whom it may concern, by one Krook.", "그날의 편지를 부치러 가는 서기들이며, 저녁을 먹으러 집으로 향하는 변호사며 사무 변호사며, 원고와 피고와 온갖 종류의 소송 당사자들이며, 살아가며 마주하는 가장 흔한 일조차 처리하는 데 오랜 세월의 법조계 지혜가 백만 가지 장애물을 끼워 넣어둔 길 위의 일반 군중과 부딪치고 떠밀리며—보통법과 형평법을 헤치고, 또 그것과 한 핏줄인 신비, 곧 거리의 진창을 헤치며—그 진창은 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 아무도 모르고, 어디서 어떻게 우리 둘레에 쌓이는지도 아무도 모르며, 다만 그게 너무 많아지면 삽으로 퍼내야 한다는 것만 대충 알 뿐이다—변호사와 법률 문구상은 넝마와 병을 다루는 가게이자 푸대접받는 온갖 물건이 거래되는 잡화점에 이른다. 그 가게는 링컨스 인 담장의 그늘에 자리 잡고 있으며, 페인트로 적혀 알려진 대로, 관련된 모든 이를 위해 크룩이라는 자가 운영한다."]], [["“This is where he lives, sir,”", "“여기가 그자가 사는 곳입니다, 선생님,”"], ["says the law-stationer.", "법률 문구상이 말한다."]], [["“This is where he lives, is it?”", "“여기가 그자가 사는 곳이란 말이지?”"], ["says the lawyer unconcernedly.", "변호사가 무심히 말한다."], ["“Thank you.”", "“고맙네.”"]], [["“Are you not going in, sir?”", "“들어가지 않으십니까, 선생님?”"]], [["“No, thank you, no; I am going on to the Fields at present.", "“아니, 됐네, 고맙네. 지금은 필즈로 가는 길이라서 말이야."], ["Good evening.", "잘 가게."], ["Thank you!”", "고마웠네!”"], ["Mr. Snagsby lifts his hat and returns to his little woman and his tea.", "스내그스비 씨가 모자를 들어 보이고는 제 마누라와 차가 있는 곳으로 돌아간다."]], [["But Mr. Tulkinghorn does not go on to the Fields at present.", "그러나 토킹혼 씨는 지금 필즈로 가지 않는다."], ["He goes a short way, turns back, comes again to the shop of Mr. Krook, and enters it straight.", "그는 잠깐 걷다가 발길을 되돌려 다시 크룩 씨의 가게로 와서는 곧장 안으로 들어선다."], ["It is dim enough, with a blot-headed candle or so in the windows, and an old man and a cat sitting in the back part by a fire.", "가게 안은 창가에 심지 끝이 뭉툭한 양초가 한두 개 켜져 있을 뿐 어둑하고, 안쪽에 노인 하나와 고양이 한 마리가 불가에 앉아 있다."], ["The old man rises and comes forward, with another blot-headed candle in his hand.", "노인이 일어나 또 다른 심지 끝 뭉툭한 양초를 손에 들고 앞으로 나온다."]], [["“Pray is your lodger within?”", "“실례하오만, 하숙인이 안에 있소?”"]], [["“Male or female, sir?”", "“남자입니까, 여자입니까, 선생님?”"], ["says Mr. Krook.", "크룩 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Male.", "“남자요."], ["The person who does copying.”", "필사 일을 하는 사람 말이오.”"]], [["Mr. Krook has eyed his man narrowly.", "크룩 씨가 상대를 찬찬히 뜯어본다."], ["Knows him by sight.", "얼굴은 안다."], ["Has an indistinct impression of his aristocratic repute.", "그가 귀족 사회에서 명망 있다는 것도 어렴풋이 알고 있다."]], [["“Did you wish to see him, sir?”", "“그분을 만나보려고요, 선생님?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그렇소.”"]], [["“It’s what I seldom do myself,”", "“저야 좀처럼 안 하는 일입니다만,”"], ["says Mr. Krook with a grin.", "크룩 씨가 히죽 웃으며 말한다."], ["“Shall I call him down?", "“불러 내려오라 할까요?"], ["But it’s a weak chance if he’d come, sir!”", "하지만 내려올 가망은 별로 없습니다, 선생님!”"]], [["“I’ll go up to him, then,”", "“그럼 내가 올라가겠소,”"], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Second floor, sir.", "“삼층입니다, 선생님."], ["Take the candle.", "양초를 가져가시지요."], ["Up there!” Mr.", "저 위쪽입니다!”"], ["Krook, with his cat beside him, stands at the bottom of the staircase, looking after Mr.", "크룩 씨가 고양이를 곁에 둔 채 계단 아래에 서서 토킹혼 씨의 뒷모습을 지켜본다."], ["Tulkinghorn. “Hi-hi!”", "“이봐요, 이봐요!”"], ["he says when Mr. Tulkinghorn has nearly disappeared.", "토킹혼 씨가 거의 사라질 즈음 그가 말한다."], ["The lawyer looks down over the hand-rail.", "변호사가 난간 너머로 내려다본다."], ["The cat expands her wicked mouth and snarls at him.", "고양이가 사악한 입을 쩍 벌리고 그를 향해 으르렁거린다."]], [["“Order, Lady Jane!", "“조용히, 레이디 제인!"], ["Behave yourself to visitors, my lady!", "손님에게 예의를 지켜라, 아가씨!"], ["You know what they say of my lodger?”", "저 양반, 우리 하숙인을 두고 사람들이 뭐라 하는지 아시오?”"], ["whispers Krook, going up a step or two.", "크룩이 한두 계단 올라서며 속삭인다."]], [["“What do they say of him?”", "“그를 두고 뭐라 하던가?”"]], [["“They say he has sold himself to the enemy, but you and I know better—he don’t buy.", "“그자가 제 영혼을 원수에게 팔아넘겼다고들 합니다만, 당신이나 나는 그게 아니란 걸 알지요—그 원수는 사들이는 법이 없으니까요."], ["I’ll tell you what, though; my lodger is so black-humoured and gloomy that I believe he’d as soon make that bargain as any other.", "하지만 이건 말씀드리지요. 우리 하숙인은 어찌나 음울하고 침침한지, 그런 거래라면 어떤 거래든 못지않게 선뜻 응할 위인이라고 나는 믿습니다."], ["Don’t put him out, sir.", "그자를 자극하지 마십시오, 선생님."], ["That’s my advice!”", "그게 제 충곱니다!”"]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn with a nod goes on his way.", "토킹혼 씨가 고개를 끄덕이고는 가던 길을 간다."], ["He comes to the dark door on the second floor.", "그는 삼층의 어두운 문 앞에 이른다."], ["He knocks, receives no answer, opens it, and accidentally extinguishes his candle in doing so.", "문을 두드리지만 아무 대답이 없어, 문을 여는데, 그러는 사이에 그만 양초를 꺼뜨리고 만다."]], [["The air of the room is almost bad enough to have extinguished it if he had not.", "만약 그가 꺼뜨리지 않았더라도, 방의 공기가 거의 그것을 꺼뜨릴 만큼 고약하다."], ["It is a small room, nearly black with soot, and grease, and dirt.", "작은 방으로, 검댕과 기름과 때로 거의 새카맣다."], ["In the rusty skeleton of a grate, pinched at the middle as if poverty had gripped it, a red coke fire burns low.", "마치 가난이 움켜쥔 듯 한가운데가 잘록한, 녹슨 해골 같은 벽난로 틀 안에서 빨간 코크스 불이 사위어간다."], ["In the corner by the chimney stand a deal table and a broken desk, a wilderness marked with a rain of ink.", "굴뚝 옆 구석에는 전나무 탁자 하나와 부서진 책상 하나가 서 있는데, 잉크가 비처럼 쏟아진 자국으로 얼룩진 황무지 같다."], ["In another corner a ragged old portmanteau on one of the two chairs serves for cabinet or wardrobe; no larger one is needed, for it collapses like the cheeks of a starved man.", "다른 구석에는 두 개뿐인 의자 가운데 하나에 너덜너덜한 낡은 여행 가방이 놓여 옷장이나 의류함 노릇을 하는데, 더 큰 것은 필요치도 않으니, 그것은 굶주린 사람의 두 볼처럼 푹 꺼져 있다."], ["The floor is bare, except that one old mat, trodden to shreds of rope-yarn, lies perishing upon the hearth.", "바닥에는 아무것도 깔려 있지 않고, 다만 밟혀 노끈 가닥으로 너덜너덜해진 낡은 깔개 하나가 난로 앞에서 삭아가고 있을 뿐이다."], ["No curtain veils the darkness of the night, but the discoloured shutters are drawn together, and through the two gaunt holes pierced in them, famine might be staring in—the banshee of the man upon the bed.", "밤의 어둠을 가려줄 휘장은 없고, 다만 색 바랜 덧창이 닫혀 있는데, 거기 뚫린 앙상한 구멍 두 개를 통해 기근이 안을 노려보고 있는지도 모른다—침대에 누운 사내를 곡하는 밴시처럼."]], [["For, on a low bed opposite the fire, a confusion of dirty patchwork, lean-ribbed ticking, and coarse sacking, the lawyer, hesitating just within the doorway, sees a man.", "벽난로 맞은편의 낮은 침대, 곧 더러운 누비이불과 갈빗대처럼 앙상한 침대보와 거친 마대가 뒤엉킨 그 위에서, 문간 안쪽에 멈칫 선 변호사는 사내 하나를 본다."], ["He lies there, dressed in shirt and trousers, with bare feet.", "그는 셔츠와 바지 차림에 맨발로 거기 누워 있다."], ["He has a yellow look in the spectral darkness of a candle that has guttered down until the whole length of its wick (still burning) has doubled over and left a tower of winding-sheet above it.", "사위어 내려 심지 전체가 (아직 타고 있는 채로) 둘로 접히고 그 위에 수의 같은 탑이 솟은 양초의 유령 같은 어둠 속에서, 그의 얼굴은 누렇게 비친다."], ["His hair is ragged, mingling with his whiskers and his beard—the latter, ragged too, and grown, like the scum and mist around him, in neglect.", "머리카락은 헝클어져 구레나룻과 턱수염에 뒤섞이고—턱수염 역시 헝클어진 채, 그를 둘러싼 더께와 안개처럼 방치 속에 자라 있다."], ["Foul and filthy as the room is, foul and filthy as the air is, it is not easy to perceive what fumes those are which most oppress the senses in it; but through the general sickliness and faintness, and the odour of stale tobacco, there comes into the lawyer’s mouth the bitter, vapid taste of opium.", "방이 더럽고 추악한 만큼, 공기 또한 더럽고 추악하여, 그 안에서 어떤 연기가 감각을 가장 짓누르는지는 가려내기 쉽지 않다. 그러나 전반적인 메스꺼움과 아득함, 그리고 묵은 담배 냄새를 뚫고, 변호사의 입안으로 아편의 쓰고 들척지근한 맛이 스며든다."]], [["“Hallo, my friend!”", "“이보게, 친구!”"], ["he cries, and strikes his iron candlestick against the door.", "그가 외치며 쇠 촛대로 문을 친다."]], [["He thinks he has awakened his friend.", "그는 친구를 깨웠으리라 생각한다."], ["He lies a little turned away, but his eyes are surely open.", "사내는 약간 돌아누운 채이지만, 두 눈은 분명히 뜨고 있다."]], [["“Hallo, my friend!”", "“이보게, 친구!”"], ["he cries again.", "그가 다시 외친다."], ["“Hallo!", "“이봐!"], ["Hallo!”", "이봐!”"]], [["As he rattles on the door, the candle which has drooped so long goes out and leaves him in the dark, with the gaunt eyes in the shutters staring down upon the bed. Our Dear Brother", "그가 문을 두드려대는 사이, 그토록 오래 사위어가던 양초가 꺼져 그를 어둠 속에 남기고, 덧창의 앙상한 두 눈이 침대를 내려다보며 노려본다."]], [["A touch on the lawyer’s wrinkled hand as he stands in the dark room, irresolute, makes him start and say, “What’s that?”", "어두운 방에 서서 갈피를 못 잡고 머뭇거리던 변호사는 누군가 주름진 손을 건드리자 흠칫 놀라며 묻는다. \"뭐냐, 이게?\""]], [["“It’s me,”", "\"나요.\""], ["returns the old man of the house, whose breath is in his ear.", "집주인 노인이 대답하는데, 그 숨결이 변호사 귓전에 닿는다."], ["“Can’t you wake him?” “No.”", "\"저 사람 못 깨우겠소?\""]], [["“What have you done with your candle?”", "\"못 깨우겠소.\" \"촛불은 어떻게 했소?\""]], [["“It’s gone out.", "\"꺼졌소."], ["Here it is.”", "여기 있소.\""]], [["Krook takes it, goes to the fire, stoops over the red embers, and tries to get a light.", "크룩이 초를 받아 들고 난로로 가서, 벌건 잿불 위로 몸을 숙이고 불을 붙여보려 한다."], ["The dying ashes have no light to spare, and his endeavours are vain.", "사그라드는 재에는 나눠줄 불씨가 없어 그 애씀이 헛수고다."], ["Muttering, after an ineffectual call to his lodger, that he will go downstairs and bring a lighted candle from the shop, the old man departs. Mr.", "하숙인을 한 번 더 불러봐도 소용없자, 아래층으로 내려가 가게에서 불 켠 초를 가져오겠다고 중얼거리며 노인은 자리를 뜬다."], ["Tulkinghorn, for some new reason that he has, does not await his return in the room, but on the stairs outside.", "토킹혼 씨는 무슨 새로운 속셈이 생겼는지 방 안에서 노인을 기다리지 않고 바깥 계단에서 기다린다."]], [["The welcome light soon shines upon the wall, as Krook comes slowly up with his green-eyed cat following at his heels.", "크룩이 초록 눈의 고양이를 발치에 거느리고 천천히 올라오자, 반가운 불빛이 곧 벽을 비춘다."], ["“Does the man generally sleep like this?”", "\"저 사람은 늘 이렇게 자나?\""], ["inquired the lawyer in a low voice.", "변호사가 나직한 목소리로 묻는다. \"에이!"], ["“Hi! I don’t know,”", "나도 모르오.\""], ["says Krook, shaking his head and lifting his eyebrows.", "크룩이 고개를 가로젓고 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말한다."], ["“I know next to nothing of his habits except that he keeps himself very close.”", "\"저 사람 습관이라곤 워낙 자기를 꽁꽁 감추고 산다는 것 말고는 거의 아는 게 없소.\""]], [["Thus whispering, they both go in together.", "이렇게 소곤거리며 둘이 함께 들어선다."], ["As the light goes in, the great eyes in the shutters, darkening, seem to close.", "불빛이 들어가자, 덧문에 그려진 커다란 눈들이 어두워지며 감기는 듯하다."], ["Not so the eyes upon the bed.", "침대 위의 두 눈은 그렇지 않다."]], [["“God save us!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["exclaims Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 외친다."], ["“He is dead!”", "\"죽었어!\""], ["Krook drops the heavy hand he has taken up so suddenly that the arm swings over the bedside.", "크룩이 들어 올렸던 그 묵직한 손을 어찌나 갑자기 놓아버리는지, 팔이 침대 옆으로 휙 늘어진다."]], [["They look at one another for a moment.", "둘은 한순간 서로를 마주 본다."]], [["“Send for some doctor!", "\"의사를 불러요!"], ["Call for Miss Flite up the stairs, sir.", "계단 위로 가서 플라이트 양을 부르시오, 선생."], ["Here’s poison by the bed!", "침대 옆에 독약이 있소!"], ["Call out for Flite, will you?”", "플라이트를 좀 불러주겠소?\""], ["says Krook, with his lean hands spread out above the body like a vampire’s wings.", "크룩이 흡혈귀의 날개처럼 깡마른 두 손을 시신 위로 펼친 채 말한다."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn hurries to the landing and calls, “Miss Flite!", "토킹혼 씨가 층계참으로 서둘러 가서 외친다."], ["Flite!", "\"플라이트 양!"], ["Make haste, here, whoever you are!", "플라이트! 빨리 좀, 여보시오, 거기 누구든!"], ["Flite!”", "플라이트!\""], ["Krook follows him with his eyes, and while he is calling, finds opportunity to steal to the old portmanteau and steal back again.", "크룩은 그를 눈으로 좇다가, 그가 부르는 사이 낡은 여행 가방으로 슬그머니 다가갔다가 다시 슬그머니 돌아올 틈을 잡는다."]], [["“Run, Flite, run!", "\"달려요, 플라이트, 달려!"], ["The nearest doctor!", "제일 가까운 의사한테로!"], ["Run!” So Mr.", "달려요!\""], ["Krook addresses a crazy little woman who is his female lodger, who appears and vanishes in a breath, who soon returns accompanied by a testy medical man brought from his dinner, with a broad, snuffy upper lip and a broad Scotch tongue.", "크룩 씨가 이렇게 외친 상대는 그의 여자 하숙인인 정신 나간 자그마한 노파로, 그녀는 눈 깜짝할 사이에 나타났다 사라지더니, 저녁 식사 도중에 끌려 나온 성마른 의사를 데리고 곧 돌아온다. 그 의사는 윗입술이 넓적하고 코담배 자국이 묻었으며 스코틀랜드 사투리가 걸쭉하다."]], [["“Ey!", "\"어이!"], ["Bless the hearts o’ ye,”", "다들 복 받으시오.\""], ["says the medical man, looking up at them after a moment’s examination.", "의사가 잠깐 살펴보고 나서 고개를 들며 말한다."], ["“He’s just as dead as Phairy!”", "\"이 양반 영락없이 죽었구먼, 파라오처럼!\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn (standing by the old portmanteau) inquires if he has been dead any time.", "토킹혼 씨가(낡은 여행 가방 옆에 선 채) 죽은 지 얼마나 됐느냐고 묻는다."]], [["“Any time, sir?” says the medical gentleman.", "\"얼마나 됐냐고요, 선생?\""], ["“It’s probable he wull have been dead aboot three hours.”", "의사 양반이 말한다. \"죽은 지 한 세 시간쯤 됐을 게요.\""]], [["“About that time, I should say,”", "\"그쯤 됐을 겁니다.\""], ["observes a dark young man on the other side of the bed.", "침대 건너편에 선 거무스름한 젊은이가 한마디 보탠다."]], [["“Air you in the maydickle prayfession yourself, sir?”", "\"선생도 의술 하시는 분이오?\""], ["inquires the first.", "먼저 온 의사가 묻는다."]], [["The dark young man says yes.", "거무스름한 젊은이가 그렇다고 답한다."]], [["“Then I’ll just tak’ my depairture,”", "\"그럼 난 그냥 물러가겠소.\""], ["replies the other, “for I’m nae gude here!”", "의사가 대꾸한다. \"여기선 내가 쓸모가 없으니!\""], ["With which remark he finishes his brief attendance and returns to finish his dinner.", "이 말과 함께 그는 짧은 진료를 마치고 저녁 식사를 마저 끝내러 돌아간다."]], [["The dark young surgeon passes the candle across and across the face and carefully examines the law-writer, who has established his pretensions to his name by becoming indeed No one.", "거무스름한 젊은 외과의가 죽은 자의 얼굴 위로 촛불을 이리저리 옮겨가며 그 법률 필경사를 찬찬히 살핀다. 필경사는 정말로 '아무도 아닌 자'가 됨으로써 그 이름값을 입증한 셈이다. * 아무도 아닌 자: 죽은 필경사는 '네모(Nemo)'라는 이름을 썼는데, 라틴어로 '아무도 아닌 사람'이라는 뜻이다."]], [["“I knew this person by sight very well,”", "\"이 사람 얼굴은 아주 잘 압니다.\""], ["says he.", "그가 말한다."], ["“He has purchased opium of me for the last year and a half.", "\"지난 일 년 반 동안 나한테서 아편을 사 갔지요."], ["Was anybody present related to him?”", "여기 이 사람 친척 되는 분 계십니까?\""], ["glancing round upon the three bystanders.", "그가 둘러서 있는 세 사람을 휘둘러보며 말한다."]], [["“I was his landlord,”", "\"내가 이 사람 집주인이오.\""], ["grimly answers Krook, taking the candle from the surgeon’s outstretched hand.", "크룩이 외과의가 내민 손에서 초를 받아 들며 무뚝뚝하게 대답한다."], ["“He told me once I was the nearest relation he had.”", "\"이 사람이 한번은 나더러 제일 가까운 친척이라고 한 적이 있소.\""]], [["“He has died,” says the surgeon, “of an over-dose of opium, there is no doubt.", "\"이 사람은 아편 과다 복용으로 죽었습니다."], ["The room is strongly flavoured with it.", "의심할 여지가 없어요.\" 외과의가 말한다."], ["There is enough here now,”", "\"방 안에 냄새가 진동하는군요."], ["taking an old tea-pot from Mr.", "지금 여기 있는 것만 해도,\" 크룩 씨에게서 낡은 찻주전자를 받아 들며 말한다."], ["Krook, “to kill a dozen people.”", "\"열 사람은 너끈히 죽일 분량입니다.\""]], [["“Do you think he did it on purpose?”", "\"일부러 그랬다고 보시오?\""], ["asks Krook.", "크룩이 묻는다."]], [["“Took the over-dose?”", "\"과다 복용 말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"그렇소!\""], ["Krook almost smacks his lips with the unction of a horrible interest.", "크룩이 끔찍한 호기심에 입맛을 다실 듯한 기색이다."]], [["“I can’t say.", "\"뭐라 말씀 못 드리겠습니다."], ["I should think it unlikely, as he has been in the habit of taking so much.", "워낙 많이 복용해온 사람이니 그럴 것 같진 않습니다만."], ["But nobody can tell.", "하지만 누가 알겠습니까."], ["He was very poor, I suppose?”", "이 사람 무척 가난했지요, 아마?\""]], [["“I suppose he was.", "\"가난했을 거요."], ["His room—don’t look rich,”", "이 사람 방을 보면, 부유해 보이진 않으니까.\""], ["says Krook, who might have changed eyes with his cat, as he casts his sharp glance around.", "크룩이 날카로운 눈길을 둘러보며 말하는데, 그 고양이와 눈을 맞바꿨다 해도 믿길 만하다."], ["“But I have never been in it since he had it, and he was too close to name his circumstances to me.”", "\"그래도 이 사람이 든 뒤로 난 한 번도 들어와 본 적이 없소. 형편을 나한테 말할 만큼 속내를 트지도 않았고.\""]], [["“Did he owe you any rent?”", "\"집세는 밀린 게 있소?\""]], [["“Six weeks.”", "\"여섯 주치요.\""]], [["“He will never pay it!”", "\"이제 영영 못 받겠군요!\""], ["says the young man, resuming his examination.", "젊은이가 다시 살피기 시작하며 말한다."], ["“It is beyond a doubt that he is indeed as dead as Pharaoh; and to judge from his appearance and condition, I should think it a happy release.", "\"이 사람이 영락없이 파라오처럼 죽었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없습니다. 그리고 행색과 형편으로 미루어 보면, 차라리 잘 떠난 거라고 봐야겠지요."], ["Yet he must have been a good figure when a youth, and I dare say, good-looking.”", "그래도 젊었을 적엔 풍채가 좋았을 겁니다. 잘생겼을 거고요.\""], ["He says this, not unfeelingly, while sitting on the bedstead’s edge with his face towards that other face and his hand upon the region of the heart.", "그가 침대 모서리에 걸터앉아 저쪽 얼굴을 마주 보며 한 손을 심장께에 올린 채, 매정하지 않은 어조로 이렇게 말한다. \"한번은 이런 생각을 한 적이 있습니다."], ["“I recollect once thinking there was something in his manner, uncouth as it was, that denoted a fall in life.", "이 사람의 거동에, 비록 거칠긴 해도, 한때 영락한 신세를 짐작케 하는 무언가가 있다고 말입니다."], ["Was that so?”", "그랬습니까?\""], ["he continues, looking round.", "그가 둘러보며 말을 잇는다."]], [["Krook replies, “You might as well ask me to describe the ladies whose heads of hair I have got in sacks downstairs.", "크룩이 대꾸한다. \"그건 나더러 아래층 자루에 든 머리카락 임자였던 부인들을 묘사해보라는 것과 마찬가지요."], ["Than that he was my lodger for a year and a half and lived—or didn’t live—by law-writing, I know no more of him.”", "일 년 반 동안 내 하숙인이었고, 필경 일로 먹고살았다는 것 말고는, 아니 먹고살지 못했다는 것 말고는, 나도 더는 아는 게 없소.\""]], [["During this dialogue Mr. Tulkinghorn has stood aloof by the old portmanteau, with his hands behind him, equally removed, to all appearance, from all three kinds of interest exhibited near the bed—from the young surgeon’s professional interest in death, noticeable as being quite apart from his remarks on the deceased as an individual; from the old man’s unction; and the little crazy woman’s awe.", "이런 대화가 오가는 동안 토킹혼 씨는 낡은 여행 가방 옆에 뒷짐을 진 채 멀찍이 서서, 침대 가까이서 드러난 세 가지 관심 — 죽은 자를 한 개인으로 두고 한 말과는 사뭇 동떨어진, 죽음을 향한 젊은 외과의의 직업적 관심과, 노인의 게걸스러운 흥미와, 정신 나간 자그마한 노파의 두려움 — 그 어느 쪽과도 똑같이 동떨어져 보인다."], ["His imperturbable face has been as inexpressive as his rusty clothes.", "그의 태연한 얼굴은 낡은 옷만큼이나 아무 표정이 없다."], ["One could not even say he has been thinking all this while.", "그가 이러는 내내 무언가 생각하고 있었다고조차 말할 수 없을 정도다."], ["He has shown neither patience nor impatience, nor attention nor abstraction.", "그는 참을성도 조바심도, 주의도 무심함도 드러내지 않았다."], ["He has shown nothing but his shell.", "그가 드러낸 것이라곤 껍데기뿐이다."], ["As easily might the tone of a delicate musical instrument be inferred from its case, as the tone of Mr. Tulkinghorn from his case.", "섬세한 악기의 음색을 그 상자만 보고 짐작할 수 없듯, 토킹혼 씨의 음색도 그 껍데기만 보고는 짐작할 수 없다."]], [["He now interposes, addressing the young surgeon in his unmoved, professional way.", "이제 그가 흔들림 없는 직업적 태도로 젊은 외과의에게 말을 건네며 끼어든다."]], [["“I looked in here,”", "\"내가 이 방을 들여다본 건,\""], ["he observes, “just before you, with the intention of giving this deceased man, whom I never saw alive, some employment at his trade of copying.", "그가 말한다. \"바로 선생보다 조금 앞선 일인데, 살아 있는 모습은 한 번도 본 적 없는 이 죽은 사람에게 필사 일거리를 좀 맡기려는 생각에서였소."], ["I had heard of him from my stationer—Snagsby of Cook’s Court.", "쿡스 코트의 문구상 스내그스비한테서 이 사람 얘길 들었지요."], ["Since no one here knows anything about him, it might be as well to send for Snagsby.", "여기 아무도 이 사람에 관해 아는 게 없으니, 스내그스비를 부르는 게 좋겠소."], ["Ah!”", "아!\""], ["to the little crazy woman, who has often seen him in court, and whom he has often seen, and who proposes, in frightened dumb-show, to go for the law-stationer.", "법정에서 자기를 자주 보았고 자기도 자주 본 그 정신 나간 자그마한 노파를 향해, 그녀가 겁먹은 몸짓으로 법률 문구상을 데려오겠다는 시늉을 하자 하는 말이다."], ["“Suppose you do!”", "\"그래주시오!\""]], [["While she is gone, the surgeon abandons his hopeless investigation and covers its subject with the patchwork counterpane.", "그녀가 가 있는 동안 외과의는 가망 없는 조사를 그만두고, 조각보 침대보로 그 대상을 덮는다."], ["Mr. Krook and he interchange a word or two.", "크룩 씨와 외과의가 한두 마디 주고받는다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn says nothing, but stands, ever, near the old portmanteau.", "토킹혼 씨는 아무 말 없이, 여전히 낡은 여행 가방 곁에 서 있다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby arrives hastily in his grey coat and his black sleeves.", "스내그스비 씨가 잿빛 외투에 검은 토시 차림으로 황급히 도착한다."], ["“Dear me, dear me,”", "\"이런, 이런.\""], ["he says; “and it has come to this, has it!", "그가 말한다. \"결국 이 지경이 됐군요, 그래!"], ["Bless my soul!”", "맙소사!\""]], [["“Can you give the person of the house any information about this unfortunate creature, Snagsby?”", "\"이 가엾은 사람에 관해 집주인한테 뭐라도 알려줄 게 있소, 스내그스비?\""], ["inquires Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 묻는다."], ["“He was in arrears with his rent, it seems.", "\"집세를 밀렸던 모양인데."], ["And he must be buried, you know.”", "게다가 묻어주기는 해야 하지 않겠소.\""]], [["“Well, sir,” says Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 선생.\""], ["Snagsby, coughing his apologetic cough behind his hand, “I really don’t know what advice I could offer, except sending for the beadle.”", "스내그스비 씨가 손으로 입을 가리고 미안한 듯 기침을 하며 말한다. \"저로선 교구 직원을 부르는 것 말고는 무슨 조언을 드릴 수 있을지 정말 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t speak of advice,”", "\"조언을 말하는 게 아니오.\""], ["returns Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 대꾸한다."], ["“I could advise—”", "\"조언이라면 내가 할 수도—\""]], [["“No one better, sir, I am sure,” says Mr.", "\"선생만큼 잘하실 분이야 없지요, 암요.\""], ["Snagsby, with his deferential cough.", "스내그스비 씨가 공손한 기침과 함께 말한다."]], [["“I speak of affording some clue to his connexions, or to where he came from, or to anything concerning him.”", "\"이 사람의 연고지나, 어디서 왔는지나, 그에 관한 무엇이라도 실마리가 될 만한 걸 말하는 거요.\""]], [["“I assure you, sir,” says Mr.", "\"분명히 말씀드립니다만, 선생.\""], ["Snagsby after prefacing his reply with his cough of general propitiation, “that I no more know where he came from than I know—”", "스내그스비 씨가 만사를 무마하는 기침으로 대답을 떼며 말한다. \"저는 이 사람이 어디서 왔는지 도무지 모릅니다, 마치—\""]], [["“Where he has gone to, perhaps,”", "\"어디로 갔는지를 모르는 만큼이나, 아마.\""], ["suggests the surgeon to help him out.", "외과의가 거들어주려고 말을 보탠다."]], [["A pause. Mr.", "잠시 침묵."], ["Tulkinghorn looking at the law-stationer.", "토킹혼 씨가 법률 문구상을 바라본다."], ["Mr. Krook, with his mouth open, looking for somebody to speak next.", "크룩 씨는 입을 벌린 채 다음에 입을 뗄 사람을 찾는다."]], [["“As to his connexions, sir,” says Mr.", "\"이 사람 연고지로 말하자면, 선생.\""], ["Snagsby, “if a person was to say to me, ‘Snagsby, here’s twenty thousand pound down, ready for you in the Bank of England if you’ll only name one of ’em,’ I couldn’t do it, sir!", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"누가 저더러 '스내그스비, 여기 이만 파운드를 잉글랜드 은행에 당장 준비해뒀으니 그 연고자 한 사람만 대보시오'"], ["About a year and a half ago—to the best of my belief, at the time when he first came to lodge at the present rag and bottle shop—”", "한다 해도, 저는 못 댑니다, 선생! 한 일 년 반 전쯤에 — 제가 기억하기로는, 이 사람이 지금 이 넝마와 병 가게에 처음 하숙하러 들어왔을 무렵에—\""]], [["“That was the time!”", "\"바로 그때였소!\""], ["says Krook with a nod.", "크룩이 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다."]], [["“About a year and a half ago,” says Mr.", "\"한 일 년 반 전쯤에,\""], ["Snagsby, strengthened, “he came into our place one morning after breakfast, and finding my little woman (which I name Mrs.", "힘을 얻은 스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"이 사람이 어느 날 아침 식사가 끝난 뒤 저희 가게에 들어왔는데, 가게에 있던 제 안사람이(이렇게 부를 땐 스내그스비 부인이라고 합니다) 마침 거기 있었지요."], ["Snagsby when I use that appellation) in our shop, produced a specimen of his handwriting and gave her to understand that he was in want of copying work to do and was, not to put too fine a point upon it,”", "그러더니 자기 필체 견본을 내보이며, 필사 일거리를 구한다고, 그리고 너무 까놓고 말하긴 뭣합니다만,\" 이건 스내그스비 씨가 솔직하게 말할 때 즐겨 쓰는 변명으로, 그는 늘 따지듯 거리낌 없는 투로 이 말을 내놓는다."], ["a favourite apology for plain speaking with Mr.", "\"쪼들린다고 하더군요!"], ["Snagsby, which he always offers with a sort of argumentative frankness, “hard up!", "제 안사람이 원래 낯선 사람을, 더구나 — 너무 까놓고 말하긴 뭣합니다만 — 무얼 바라고 오는 사람을 그리 살갑게 대하는 편이 아닙니다. 그런데 이 사람한테는 어쩐지 마음이 좀 끌렸던 모양이에요."], ["My little woman is not in general partial to strangers, particular—not to put too fine a point upon it—when they want anything.", "수염을 안 깎아서였는지, 머리가 손질이 안 돼서였는지, 아니면 여자들만 아는 무슨 이유에서였는지는 선생 판단에 맡깁니다만. 어쨌든 안사람이 그 견본을 받았고, 주소도 받아뒀습니다."], ["But she was rather took by something about this person, whether by his being unshaved, or by his hair being in want of attention, or by what other ladies’ reasons, I leave you to judge; and she accepted of the specimen, and likewise of the address.", "제 안사람이 이름을 잘 못 알아듣거든요.\" 스내그스비 씨가 손으로 입을 가리고 신중을 기하는 기침을 한 뒤 말을 잇는다."], ["My little woman hasn’t a good ear for names,” proceeds Mr.", "\"그래서 네모를 님로드랑 똑같은 걸로 여겼지 뭡니까."], ["Snagsby after consulting his cough of consideration behind his hand, “and she considered Nemo equally the same as Nimrod.", "그 바람에 식사 때면 저한테 이러는 버릇이 들었어요. '스내그스비, 당신 아직 님로드한테 일거리 안 줬구려!'"], ["In consequence of which, she got into a habit of saying to me at meals, ‘Mr.", "아니면 '스내그스비, 잔다이스 건 그 서른여덟 장짜리 대법원 폴리오를 왜 님로드한테 안 줬어요?' 뭐 그런 식으로요."], ["Snagsby, you haven’t found Nimrod any work yet!’ or ‘Mr.", "그렇게 해서 이 사람이 차츰 저희 가게 삯일에 발을 들였습니다."], ["Snagsby, why didn’t you give that eight and thirty Chancery folio in Jarndyce to Nimrod?’ or such like.", "손이 빠르고 밤일도 마다하지 않는 사람이라는 것, 가령 수요일 밤에 폴리오 마흔다섯 장쯤 맡기면 목요일 아침엔 다 해서 들고 온다는 것 말고는, 제가 이 사람에 관해 아는 건 그게 전부입니다. 그 모든 게—\""], ["And that is the way he gradually fell into job-work at our place; and that is the most I know of him except that he was a quick hand, and a hand not sparing of night-work, and that if you gave him out, say, five and forty folio on the Wednesday night, you would have it brought in on the Thursday morning.", "스내그스비 씨가 침대 쪽으로 정중하게 모자를 까딱해 보이며 말을 맺는데, 마치 \"여기 제 점잖은 친구분도 그럴 형편만 됐다면 두말없이 확인해주셨을 겁니다\" 하고 덧붙이는 듯하다."], ["All of which—” Mr.", "* 폴리오: 법률 문서의 분량 단위로, 일정한 낱말 수를 한 장으로 세었다."], ["Snagsby concludes by politely motioning with his hat towards the bed, as much as to add, “I have no doubt my honourable friend would confirm if he were in a condition to do it.”", "* 님로드: 성경에 나오는 사냥꾼이자 용맹한 인물. 스내그스비 부인이 '네모'를 비슷한 어감의 '님로드'로 잘못 알아들었다는 뜻이다."]], [["“Hadn’t you better see,” says Mr.", "\"가서 살펴보는 게 낫지 않겠소,\""], ["Tulkinghorn to Krook, “whether he had any papers that may enlighten you?", "토킹혼 씨가 크룩에게 말한다. \"뭐든 단서가 될 만한 서류라도 있는지 말이오."], ["There will be an inquest, and you will be asked the question.", "검시가 있을 텐데, 그때 그런 걸 물을 거요."], ["You can read?”", "글은 읽을 줄 아시오?\""]], [["“No, I can’t,”", "\"아니, 못 읽소.\""], ["returns the old man with a sudden grin.", "노인이 불쑥 씩 웃으며 대꾸한다."]], [["“Snagsby,” says Mr.", "\"스내그스비.\""], ["Tulkinghorn, “look over the room for him.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"당신이 대신 방을 좀 살펴봐 주시오."], ["He will get into some trouble or difficulty otherwise.", "안 그러면 이 사람이 무슨 곤란이나 시비에 휘말릴 거요."], ["Being here, I’ll wait if you make haste, and then I can testify on his behalf, if it should ever be necessary, that all was fair and right.", "마침 내가 여기 있으니, 서둘러주면 기다리겠소. 그러면 혹시라도 필요할 때 모든 게 정당하고 떳떳했다고 내가 이 사람을 위해 증언해줄 수 있을 테니."], ["If you will hold the candle for Mr. Snagsby, my friend, he’ll soon see whether there is anything to help you.”", "여보시오, 당신이 스내그스비 씨한테 초를 들어주면, 단서가 될 만한 게 있는지 곧 알 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“In the first place, here’s an old portmanteau, sir,”", "\"우선, 여기 낡은 여행 가방이 있군요, 선생.\""], ["says Snagsby.", "스내그스비가 말한다."]], [["Ah, to be sure, so there is! Mr.", "아, 그렇지, 정말 거기 있구나!"], ["Tulkinghorn does not appear to have seen it before, though he is standing so close to it, and though there is very little else, heaven knows.", "토킹혼 씨는 그 가방 코앞에 서 있는데도, 또 하늘이 알다시피 그 방엔 그것 말고 별것도 없는데도, 전에 본 적이 없다는 기색이다."]], [["The marine-store merchant holds the light, and the law-stationer conducts the search.", "해묵은 잡동사니 장수가 불을 들고, 법률 문구상이 수색을 이끈다."], ["The surgeon leans against the corner of the chimney-piece; Miss Flite peeps and trembles just within the door.", "외과의는 벽난로 선반 모서리에 기대 있고, 플라이트 양은 문간 안쪽에서 빠끔히 들여다보며 떨고 있다."], ["The apt old scholar of the old school, with his dull black breeches tied with ribbons at the knees, his large black waistcoat, his long-sleeved black coat, and his wisp of limp white neckerchief tied in the bow the peerage knows so well, stands in exactly the same place and attitude.", "옛 학파의 노련한 노학자는 무릎께를 리본으로 묶은 칙칙한 검은 반바지에, 큼지막한 검은 조끼, 긴 소매 검은 외투, 그리고 귀족 사회가 익히 아는 그 매듭으로 묶은 한 가닥 흐물흐물한 흰 목수건 차림으로, 똑같은 자리에 똑같은 자세로 서 있다."]], [["There are some worthless articles of clothing in the old portmanteau; there is a bundle of pawnbrokers’ duplicates, those turnpike tickets on the road of poverty; there is a crumpled paper, smelling of opium, on which are scrawled rough memoranda—as, took, such a day, so many grains; took, such another day, so many more—begun some time ago, as if with the intention of being regularly continued, but soon left off.", "낡은 여행 가방 안에는 쓸모없는 옷가지 몇 점이 들어 있다. 전당포 전표 뭉치가 있는데, 이는 가난이라는 길 위의 통행료 영수증이다."], ["There are a few dirty scraps of newspapers, all referring to coroners’ inquests; there is nothing else.", "아편 냄새가 나는 구겨진 종이 한 장도 있는데, 거기엔 거친 메모가 휘갈겨져 있다 — 어느 날 몇 그레인을 복용했다, 또 어느 날 몇 그레인을 더 복용했다, 하는 식으로, 한동안 꼬박꼬박 이어 적을 작정으로 시작한 듯하나 이내 그만둔 모양이다."], ["They search the cupboard and the drawer of the ink-splashed table.", "죄다 검시 보도와 관련된 더러운 신문 조각이 몇 점 있고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 없다."], ["There is not a morsel of an old letter or of any other writing in either.", "그들은 찬장과 잉크 얼룩진 탁자 서랍을 뒤진다. 어느 쪽에도 묵은 편지 한 조각, 다른 어떤 글씨 한 조각 없다."], ["The young surgeon examines the dress on the law-writer.", "젊은 외과의가 법률 필경사가 입은 옷을 살핀다."], ["A knife and some odd halfpence are all he finds. Mr.", "찾아낸 것이라곤 칼 한 자루와 동전 몇 닢이 전부다."], ["Snagsby’s suggestion is the practical suggestion after all, and the beadle must be called in.", "결국 스내그스비 씨의 제안이 가장 실속 있는 제안이었던 셈이니, 교구 직원을 불러들여야 한다. * 그레인: 약 0.0648그램에 해당하는 무게 단위로, 약물의 분량을 잴 때 썼다."]], [["So the little crazy lodger goes for the beadle, and the rest come out of the room.", "그리하여 정신 나간 자그마한 하숙인이 교구 직원을 부르러 가고, 나머지는 방에서 나온다."], ["“Don’t leave the cat there!”", "\"고양이를 거기 두지 마시오!\" 외과의가 말한다."], ["says the surgeon; “that won’t do!” Mr.", "\"그건 안 될 일이오!\""], ["Krook therefore drives her out before him, and she goes furtively downstairs, winding her lithe tail and licking her lips.", "그래서 크룩 씨가 고양이를 앞세워 내몬다. 고양이는 날렵한 꼬리를 휘감고 입맛을 다시며 슬그머니 아래층으로 내려간다."]], [["“Good night!”", "\"안녕히!\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn, and goes home to Allegory and meditation.", "토킹혼 씨가 말하고는, '우의'와 명상이 있는 자기 집으로 돌아간다."]], [["By this time the news has got into the court.", "* 우의: 토킹혼 씨 방 천장에 그려진 알레고리 벽화를 가리킨다."], ["Groups of its inhabitants assemble to discuss the thing, and the outposts of the army of observation (principally boys) are pushed forward to Mr.", "손가락으로 무언가를 가리키는 형상으로, 작품 내내 상징적으로 등장한다."], ["Krook’s window, which they closely invest.", "이쯤 되자 그 소식이 골목 안에 퍼진다."], ["A policeman has already walked up to the room, and walked down again to the door, where he stands like a tower, only condescending to see the boys at his base occasionally; but whenever he does see them, they quail and fall back.", "동네 사람들이 떼 지어 모여 이 일을 떠들어대고, 관찰병 부대의 전초병들(주로 사내아이들)이 크룩 씨네 창문 앞으로 밀고 나가 그 창을 바짝 에워싼다. 경관 하나가 벌써 방으로 올라갔다가 다시 문간으로 내려와서는, 탑처럼 버티고 선 채 이따금 제 발치의 아이들을 흘끗 굽어볼 따름이다."], ["Mrs. Perkins, who has not been for some weeks on speaking terms with Mrs.", "하지만 그가 한 번이라도 눈길을 주면 아이들은 움찔하며 뒷걸음친다."], ["Piper in consequence for an unpleasantness originating in young Perkins’ having “fetched”", "퍼킨스 부인은 몇 주째 파이퍼 부인과 말도 안 섞고 지냈는데 — 어린 퍼킨스가 어린 파이퍼에게 \"한 대 먹인\""], ["young Piper “a crack,” renews her friendly intercourse on this auspicious occasion.", "데서 비롯된 불쾌한 일 탓이었다 — 이 경사스러운 기회에 다정한 왕래를 새로 튼다."], ["The potboy at the corner, who is a privileged amateur, as possessing official knowledge of life and having to deal with drunken men occasionally, exchanges confidential communications with the policeman and has the appearance of an impregnable youth, unassailable by truncheons and unconfinable in station-houses.", "길모퉁이 술집 사환 녀석은 인생을 공식적으로 안다 할 만큼 술 취한 자들을 더러 상대해본 처지라 특권을 누리는 풋내기인데, 경관과 은밀한 이야기를 주고받으며 곤봉으로도 못 건드리고 유치장에도 못 가둘 무적의 청년인 양 으스댄다."], ["People talk across the court out of window, and bare-headed scouts come hurrying in from Chancery Lane to know what’s the matter.", "사람들이 창 너머로 골목을 가로질러 떠들고, 맨머리 정찰병들이 무슨 일인가 알아보려고 챈서리 레인에서 부랴부랴 달려든다."], ["The general feeling seems to be that it’s a blessing Mr. Krook warn’t made away with first, mingled with a little natural disappointment that he was not.", "분위기로 보아 크룩 씨가 먼저 당하지 않은 게 천만다행이라는 기색인데, 그렇지 못한 데 대한 자연스러운 실망감도 조금 섞여 있는 듯하다."], ["In the midst of this sensation, the beadle arrives.", "이 소동 한복판으로 교구 직원이 당도한다."]], [["The beadle, though generally understood in the neighbourhood to be a ridiculous institution, is not without a certain popularity for the moment, if it were only as a man who is going to see the body.", "교구 직원이라는 자리는 동네에서 대체로 우스꽝스러운 직책으로 통하지만, 이 순간만큼은 시신을 보러 들어갈 사람이라는 이유 하나만으로도 어느 정도 인기가 없지 않다."], ["The policeman considers him an imbecile civilian, a remnant of the barbarous watchmen times, but gives him admission as something that must be borne with until government shall abolish him.", "경관은 그를 미개한 야경꾼 시절의 유물 같은 얼간이 민간인으로 여기지만, 정부가 이 자리를 없애기 전까지는 견뎌야 할 존재로 치고 들여보내 준다."], ["The sensation is heightened as the tidings spread from mouth to mouth that the beadle is on the ground and has gone in.", "교구 직원이 현장에 와서 안으로 들어갔다는 소식이 입에서 입으로 퍼지면서 분위기가 한층 달아오른다."]], [["By and by the beadle comes out, once more intensifying the sensation, which has rather languished in the interval. He is understood to be in want of witnesses for the inquest to-morrow who can tell the coroner and jury anything whatever respecting the deceased.", "이윽고 교구 직원이 나오자, 그새 좀 시들해졌던 분위기가 다시금 후끈 달아오른다."], ["Is immediately referred to innumerable people who can tell nothing whatever. Is made more imbecile by being constantly informed that Mrs.", "알고 보니 그는 내일 검시에 쓸 증인을, 그러니까 죽은 자에 관해 검시관과 배심에게 뭐라도 말해줄 수 있는 증인을 찾고 있다."], ["Green’s son “was a law-writer his-self and knowed him better than anybody,”", "그러자 대뜸 아무것도 모르는 사람들이 무수히 그에게 떠밀려 온다."], ["which son of Mrs. Green’s appears, on inquiry, to be at the present time aboard a vessel bound for China, three months out, but considered accessible by telegraph on application to the Lords of the Admiralty.", "게다가 그린 부인네 아들이 \"지가 직접 법률 필경사였고 누구보다 그 사람을 잘 알았다\"는 말을 거듭 듣게 되면서 한층 더 얼간이가 되고 마는데, 알아보니 그린 부인네 그 아들은 지금 중국행 배를 타고 떠난 지 석 달째라, 다만 해군성 경들에게 신청하면 전보로 연락이 닿으리라는 것이다."], ["Beadle goes into various shops and parlours, examining the inhabitants, always shutting the door first, and by exclusion, delay, and general idiotcy exasperating the public.", "교구 직원은 이 가게 저 응접실로 들어가 사람들을 캐묻는데, 늘 문부터 닫고는, 사람들을 따돌리고 늑장 부리고 전반적으로 멍청하게 굴어 대중의 약을 올린다."], ["Policeman seen to smile to potboy.", "경관이 술집 사환을 보며 빙긋 웃는 모습이 보인다."], ["Public loses interest and undergoes reaction.", "대중은 흥미를 잃고 반작용을 일으킨다."], ["Taunts the beadle in shrill youthful voices with having boiled a boy, choruses fragments of a popular song to that effect and importing that the boy was made into soup for the workhouse.", "아이들이 새된 목소리로, 교구 직원이 사내애 하나를 삶아 먹었다고 빈정대며 그 내용을 담은 유행가 가락을 한 토막씩 합창하는데, 그 애가 구빈원 수프 거리가 되었다는 노래다."], ["Policeman at last finds it necessary to support the law and seize a vocalist, who is released upon the flight of the rest on condition of his getting out of this then, come, and cutting it—a condition he immediately observes.", "마침내 경관은 법을 떠받칠 필요를 느껴 노래꾼 하나를 붙잡는데, 나머지가 줄행랑을 치자 그놈도 당장 여기서 꺼지겠다는, 자, 어서 사라지겠다는 조건으로 풀어주니 — 그놈은 즉시 그 조건을 지킨다."], ["So the sensation dies off for the time; and the unmoved policeman (to whom a little opium, more or less, is nothing), with his shining hat, stiff stock, inflexible great-coat, stout belt and bracelet, and all things fitting, pursues his lounging way with a heavy tread, beating the palms of his white gloves one against the other and stopping now and then at a street-corner to look casually about for anything between a lost child and a murder.", "그리하여 소동은 한동안 잦아든다. 그리고 (아편이 좀 많고 적고는 아무것도 아닌) 태연한 경관은, 번쩍이는 모자에 빳빳한 목깃, 뻣뻣한 외투, 튼튼한 허리띠와 팔찌, 그 밖에 빠짐없이 갖춘 차림으로, 흰 장갑 낀 손바닥을 마주 두드리며 무거운 발걸음으로 어슬렁어슬렁 제 길을 가다가, 이따금 길모퉁이에 멈춰 서서 미아부터 살인 사건에 이르기까지 아무거나 무심히 둘러본다."]], [["Under cover of the night, the feeble-minded beadle comes flitting about Chancery Lane with his summonses, in which every juror’s name is wrongly spelt, and nothing rightly spelt but the beadle’s own name, which nobody can read or wants to know.", "밤의 장막을 틈타, 모자란 머리의 교구 직원이 소환장을 들고 챈서리 레인 일대를 종종거리며 돌아다닌다. 그 소환장에는 배심원들 이름이 죄다 틀리게 적혀 있고, 제대로 적힌 것이라곤 교구 직원 자기 이름뿐인데, 그건 아무도 읽을 수 없고 알고 싶지도 않은 이름이다."], ["The summonses served and his witnesses forewarned, the beadle goes to Mr.", "소환장을 다 돌리고 증인들에게 미리 일러둔 뒤, 교구 직원은 크룩 씨네로 가서, 미리 약속해둔 빈민 몇과의 자그마한 약속을 지킨다."], ["Krook’s to keep a small appointment he has made with certain paupers, who, presently arriving, are conducted upstairs, where they leave the great eyes in the shutter something new to stare at, in that last shape which earthly lodgings take for No one—and for Every one.", "이내 도착한 빈민들은 위층으로 안내되는데, 거기서 그들은 덧문에 그려진 커다란 눈들에게 새로 노려볼 거리를 남긴다 — '아무도 아닌 자'에게, 또 '모든 사람'에게 이승의 거처가 마지막으로 취하는 바로 그 모습을."]], [["And all that night the coffin stands ready by the old portmanteau; and the lonely figure on the bed, whose path in life has lain through five and forty years, lies there with no more track behind him that any one can trace than a deserted infant.", "그리고 그날 밤 내내 관이 낡은 여행 가방 옆에 채비를 갖춘 채 놓여 있다. 마흔다섯 해 동안 인생길을 걸어온 침대 위의 외로운 형체는, 버려진 갓난아이만큼이나 누구도 더듬어볼 자취 하나 남기지 못한 채 거기 누워 있다."]], [["Next day the court is all alive—is like a fair, as Mrs. Perkins, more than reconciled to Mrs.", "이튿날, 골목 안은 온통 활기에 넘쳐 — 파이퍼 부인과 진작에 화해한 퍼킨스 부인이 그 훌륭한 여인과 다정한 대화를 나누며 말하듯 — 영락없는 장날 같다."], ["Piper, says in amicable conversation with that excellent woman. The coroner is to sit in the first-floor room at the Sol’s Arms, where the Harmonic Meetings take place twice a week and where the chair is filled by a gentleman of professional celebrity, faced by Little Swills, the comic vocalist, who hopes (according to the bill in the window) that his friends will rally round him and support first-rate talent.", "검시관은 솔스 암스 이층 방에서 자리를 펼 예정인데, 거기선 일주일에 두 번 화음 모임이 열리고, 그 모임의 좌장은 직업적으로 이름난 신사가 맡되, 그 맞은편엔 희극 노래꾼 리틀 스윌스가 앉아 (창문에 붙은 광고에 따르면) 친구들이 자기 곁에 모여 일류 재능을 응원해주기를 바란다고 적혀 있다."], ["The Sol’s Arms does a brisk stroke of business all the morning.", "솔스 암스는 아침 내내 장사가 신바람 난다."], ["Even children so require sustaining under the general excitement that a pieman who has established himself for the occasion at the corner of the court says his brandy-balls go off like smoke.", "아이들조차 이 들뜬 분위기에 기운을 차릴 게 필요한지, 이 일을 노리고 골목 모퉁이에 자리 잡은 파이 장수가 자기네 브랜디 사탕이 연기처럼 팔려나간다고 말할 정도다."], ["What time the beadle, hovering between the door of Mr. Krook’s establishment and the door of the Sol’s Arms, shows the curiosity in his keeping to a few discreet spirits and accepts the compliment of a glass of ale or so in return.", "그러는 사이 교구 직원은 크룩 씨네 가게 문과 솔스 암스 문 사이를 어슬렁거리며, 자기가 맡아둔 구경거리를 입 무거운 몇몇에게 보여주고는 그 대가로 맥주 한두 잔의 호의를 받아 마신다."]], [["At the appointed hour arrives the coroner, for whom the jurymen are waiting and who is received with a salute of skittles from the good dry skittle-ground attached to the Sol’s Arms.", "정해진 시각에 검시관이 도착하는데, 배심원들이 그를 기다리고 있고, 솔스 암스에 딸린 잘 마른 스키틀 마당에서 스키틀 소리가 그를 인사하듯 맞는다."], ["The coroner frequents more public-houses than any man alive.", "검시관은 살아 있는 그 누구보다도 술집을 많이 드나드는 사람이다."], ["The smell of sawdust, beer, tobacco-smoke, and spirits is inseparable in his vocation from death in its most awful shapes.", "그의 직업에서 톱밥과 맥주, 담배 연기와 독한 술 냄새는 가장 무시무시한 모습의 죽음과 떼려야 뗄 수 없다."], ["He is conducted by the beadle and the landlord to the Harmonic Meeting Room, where he puts his hat on the piano and takes a Windsor-chair at the head of a long table formed of several short tables put together and ornamented with glutinous rings in endless involutions, made by pots and glasses.", "그는 교구 직원과 술집 주인의 안내로 화음 모임방에 들어가, 모자를 피아노 위에 올려놓고, 짧은 탁자 여럿을 붙여 만든 긴 탁자 상석에 윈저 의자를 놓고 앉는다. 그 탁자엔 술잔과 단지가 남긴 끈적한 동그라미 자국이 끝없이 뒤엉켜 새겨져 있다."], ["As many of the jury as can crowd together at the table sit there. The rest get among the spittoons and pipes or lean against the piano.", "배심원들은 탁자에 다닥다닥 붙어 앉을 수 있는 만큼 거기 앉고, 나머지는 가래받이와 담뱃대 사이에 끼어 있거나 피아노에 기대선다."], ["Over the coroner’s head is a small iron garland, the pendant handle of a bell, which rather gives the majesty of the court the appearance of going to be hanged presently.", "검시관 머리 위엔 자그마한 무쇠 화환이, 곧 종을 매단 손잡이가 드리워져 있어, 법정의 위엄이 머잖아 교수형에라도 처해질 것 같은 꼴이다."]], [["Call over and swear the jury!", "* 스키틀: 나무 핀을 공으로 쓰러뜨리는 영국식 볼링 놀이. 배심원 호명하고 선서시켜라!"], ["While the ceremony is in progress, sensation is created by the entrance of a chubby little man in a large shirt-collar, with a moist eye and an inflamed nose, who modestly takes a position near the door as one of the general public, but seems familiar with the room too.", "그 절차가 진행되는 동안, 큼지막한 셔츠 깃에 눈가가 촉촉하고 코가 벌건 토실토실한 자그마한 사내가 들어와 한바탕 술렁임을 일으킨다. 그는 그저 일반 방청객인 양 점잖게 문간 가까이 자리를 잡지만, 이 방을 꽤 잘 아는 눈치다."], ["A whisper circulates that this is Little Swills.", "저 사람이 리틀 스윌스라는 소곤거림이 돈다."], ["It is considered not unlikely that he will get up an imitation of the coroner and make it the principal feature of the Harmonic Meeting in the evening.", "저녁 화음 모임에서 그가 검시관 흉내를 한바탕 꾸며 그날의 백미로 삼으리라는 게 다들 점치는 바다."]], [["“Well, gentlemen—”", "\"자, 여러분—\""], ["the coroner begins.", "검시관이 입을 뗀다."]], [["“Silence there, will you!”", "\"거기 조용히, 좀!\""], ["says the beadle.", "교구 직원이 말한다."], ["Not to the coroner, though it might appear so.", "그렇게 들릴 법도 하지만, 검시관더러 한 말은 아니다."]], [["“Well, gentlemen,”", "\"자, 여러분.\""], ["resumes the coroner.", "검시관이 말을 잇는다."], ["“You are impanelled here to inquire into the death of a certain man.", "\"여러분은 어떤 사람의 죽음을 조사하고자 여기 배심으로 소집되었습니다."], ["Evidence will be given before you as to the circumstances attending that death, and you will give your verdict according to the—skittles; they must be stopped, you know, beadle!—evidence, and not according to anything else.", "그 죽음에 얽힌 정황에 관한 증거가 여러분 앞에 제시될 것이고, 여러분은 그 — 스키틀 좀, 저거 멈추게 해야겠소, 교구 직원! — 증거에 따라 평결을 내리되, 그 밖에 다른 무엇에도 따르지 마십시오."], ["The first thing to be done is to view the body.”", "가장 먼저 할 일은 시신을 검안하는 것입니다.\""]], [["“Make way there!”", "\"길 좀 비키시오!\""], ["cries the beadle.", "교구 직원이 외친다."]], [["So they go out in a loose procession, something after the manner of a straggling funeral, and make their inspection in Mr.", "그리하여 그들은 흐트러진 행렬을 지어 나가는데, 어쩐지 뿔뿔이 흩어진 장례 행렬 같은 모양새다."], ["Krook’s back second floor, from which a few of the jurymen retire pale and precipitately.", "크룩 씨네 뒤편 삼층에서 검안을 하는데, 배심원 몇은 새파랗게 질려 허둥지둥 물러난다."], ["The beadle is very careful that two gentlemen not very neat about the cuffs and buttons (for whose accommodation he has provided a special little table near the coroner in the Harmonic Meeting Room) should see all that is to be seen.", "교구 직원은 소맷부리와 단추가 별로 단정치 못한 두 신사가(이들을 위해 그는 화음 모임방의 검시관 곁에 특별히 작은 탁자 하나를 마련해두었다) 볼 만한 건 죄다 보도록 무척 신경 쓴다. 이들이야말로 이런 종류의 조사를 줄줄이 기록해 세상에 알리는 공인된 연대기 작가들이기 때문이다."], ["For they are the public chroniclers of such inquiries by the line; and he is not superior to the universal human infirmity, but hopes to read in print what “Mooney, the active and intelligent beadle of the district,” said and did and even aspires to see the name of Mooney as familiarly and patronizingly mentioned as the name of the hangman is, according to the latest examples.", "게다가 그도 인간 보편의 약점에서 벗어난 사람은 아니라, \"이 지역의 민첩하고 영민한 교구 직원 무니\"가 무어라 말하고 무얼 했다는 내용을 활자로 읽기를 바라며, 더 나아가 요즘 사례에 비추어 사형 집행인의 이름만큼이나 무니라는 이름이 친근하고 너그러이 거론되는 걸 보기를 열망한다."]], [["Little Swills is waiting for the coroner and jury on their return. Mr.", "검시관과 배심이 돌아오기를 리틀 스윌스가 기다리고 있다."], ["Tulkinghorn, also.", "토킹혼 씨도."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn is received with distinction and seated near the coroner between that high judicial officer, a bagatelle-board, and the coal-box.", "토킹혼 씨는 정중히 영접을 받아, 그 높으신 사법관과 바가텔 판, 석탄통 사이에서 검시관 곁에 앉는다."], ["The inquiry proceeds.", "조사가 진행된다."], ["The jury learn how the subject of their inquiry died, and learn no more about him.", "배심은 조사 대상이 어떻게 죽었는지는 알게 되지만, 그에 관해서는 그 이상 아무것도 알지 못한다."], ["“A very eminent solicitor is in attendance, gentlemen,”", "\"아주 저명한 사무변호사 한 분이 자리해 계십니다, 여러분.\" 검시관이 말한다."], ["says the coroner, “who, I am informed, was accidentally present when discovery of the death was made, but he could only repeat the evidence you have already heard from the surgeon, the landlord, the lodger, and the law-stationer, and it is not necessary to trouble him.", "\"들으니 이분이 시신 발견 당시 우연히 그 자리에 계셨다 하나, 여러분이 외과의와 집주인, 하숙인과 법률 문구상한테서 이미 들으신 증언을 되풀이하실 따름이니, 굳이 번거롭게 해드릴 필요는 없겠습니다. 이보다 더 아는 게 있는 분, 자리에 계십니까?\""], ["Is anybody in attendance who knows anything more?”", "* 바가텔: 작은 공을 막대로 쳐서 구멍에 넣는 당구 비슷한 실내 놀이."]], [["Mrs. Piper pushed forward by Mrs. Perkins. Mrs. Piper sworn.", "퍼킨스 부인이 파이퍼 부인을 앞으로 떠민다. 파이퍼 부인 선서."]], [["Anastasia Piper, gentlemen. Married woman.", "배심원 여러분, 어내스테이저 파이퍼입니다."], ["Now, Mrs.", "유부녀이고요."], ["Piper, what have you got to say about this?", "자, 파이퍼 부인, 이 일에 관해 하실 말씀이 무엇입니까?"]], [["Why, Mrs. Piper has a good deal to say, chiefly in parentheses and without punctuation, but not much to tell.", "웬걸, 파이퍼 부인은 할 말이 잔뜩인데, 대개 군더더기 삽입구뿐이요 구두점도 없는 데다, 정작 들려줄 만한 알맹이는 별로 없다."], ["Mrs. Piper lives in the court (which her husband is a cabinet-maker), and it has long been well beknown among the neighbours (counting from the day next but one before the half-baptizing of Alexander James Piper aged eighteen months and four days old on accounts of not being expected to live such was the sufferings gentlemen of that child in his gums) as the plaintive—so Mrs.", "파이퍼 부인은 이 골목에 사는데(남편이 가구장이라) 동네에선 진작에 잘 알려진 일이라(알렉산더 제임스 파이퍼가 생후 십팔 개월 나흘 적에 잇몸 때문에 어찌나 고생을 했던지 살아남지 못할 줄 알고 약식으로 세례를 준 그 전전날부터 치자면) 그 원고가 — 파이퍼 부인은 죽은 자를 한사코 이렇게 부른다 — 자기 몸을 팔아넘겼다는 소문이 돌았다는 것이다."], ["Piper insists on calling the deceased—was reported to have sold himself.", "그 소문이 시작된 건 그 원고의 인상 때문인 것 같다고 한다."], ["Thinks it was the plaintive’s air in which that report originatinin. See the plaintive often and considered as his air was feariocious and not to be allowed to go about some children being timid (and if doubted hoping Mrs.", "그 원고를 자주 보았는데, 그 인상이 흉포해 보여서, 어떤 아이들은 겁이 많으니 함부로 돌아다니게 둬선 안 될 사람이라 여겼다고 한다(미덥지 않으시면 퍼킨스 부인을 불러주시기 바라는데 그분이 여기 와 있고 남편과 자신과 가족의 체면을 세워줄 것이라고)."], ["Perkins may be brought forard for she is here and will do credit to her husband and herself and family).", "그 원고가 아이들한테 약 오르고 시달리는 걸 본 적이 있다고 한다(애들은 어차피 애들이라, 더구나 장난기 많은 애들이라면, 무드셀라처럼 점잖기를 바랄 수 없잖아요, 그쪽도 그러지 못했으면서)."], ["Has seen the plaintive wexed and worrited by the children (for children they will ever be and you cannot expect them specially if of playful dispositions to be Methoozellers which you was not yourself).", "이 때문에 또 그 사람 어두운 낯빛 때문에, 그가 주머니에서 곡괭이를 꺼내 조니 머리를 쪼개는 꿈을 자주 꿨다고 한다(그 애가 겁이라곤 없어서 바짝 따라붙으며 자꾸 그 사람한테 소리를 질러댔거든요). 그렇지만 그 원고가 곡괭이는커녕 다른 어떤 흉기를 꺼내는 건 전혀 본 적이 없다고 한다."], ["On accounts of this and his dark looks has often dreamed as she see him take a pick-axe from his pocket and split Johnny’s head (which the child knows not fear and has repeatually called after him close at his eels). Never however see the plaintive take a pick-axe or any other wepping far from it.", "아이들이 뒤쫓으며 소리치면 그가 황급히 달아나는 건 봤는데, 마치 아이들을 좋아하지 않는 듯했다고, 그리고 그가 아이든 어른이든 누구한테 말 거는 건 한 번도 못 봤다고 한다(저 길 건너 모퉁이 돌아 골목 아래 횡단로를 쓰는 그 애만 빼고요, 그 애가 여기 있다면 그 사람이 자기한테 자주 말 거는 걸 봤다고 일러드릴 텐데요)."], ["Has seen him hurry away when run and called after as if not partial to children and never see him speak to neither child nor grown person at any time (excepting the boy that sweeps the crossing down the lane over the way round the corner which if he was here would tell you that he has been seen a-speaking to him frequent).", "* 무드셀라: 성경에서 969세까지 살았다는 인물 므두셀라. 여기선 '점잖고 늙수그레한 사람'을 비꼬는 비유다."]], [["Says the coroner, is that boy here?", "검시관이 묻는다, 그 애가 여기 있소?"], ["Says the beadle, no, sir, he is not here.", "교구 직원이 답한다, 아닙니다, 선생, 여기 없습니다."], ["Says the coroner, go and fetch him then.", "검시관이 말한다, 그럼 가서 데려오시오."], ["In the absence of the active and intelligent, the coroner converses with Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "민첩하고 영민한 직원이 자리를 비운 사이, 검시관은 토킹혼 씨와 담소를 나눈다."]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["Here’s the boy, gentlemen!", "그 애가 왔습니다, 여러분!"]], [["Here he is, very muddy, very hoarse, very ragged.", "여기 그 애가 왔다, 진흙투성이에, 목이 잔뜩 쉬고, 누더기 차림이다."], ["Now, boy!", "자, 얘야!"], ["But stop a minute.", "하지만 잠깐."], ["Caution.", "주의할 것."], ["This boy must be put through a few preliminary paces.", "이 아이는 먼저 몇 가지 예비 절차를 거쳐야 한다."]], [["Name, Jo.", "이름, 조."], ["Nothing else that he knows on.", "그 밖엔 아는 게 없다."], ["Don’t know that everybody has two names.", "사람마다 이름이 둘인 줄도 모른다."], ["Never heerd of sich a think.", "그런 건 들어본 적도 없다."], ["Don’t know that Jo is short for a longer name.", "조가 더 긴 이름의 줄임말인 줄도 모른다."], ["Thinks it long enough for HIM.", "자기한테는 그것만으로도 충분히 길다고 여긴다."], ["HE don’t find no fault with it.", "그걸로 트집 잡을 생각도 없다."], ["Spell it? No.", "철자를 대보라고?"], ["HE can’t spell it.", "아니. 철자는 못 댄다."], ["No father, no mother, no friends.", "아버지도 어머니도 친구도 없다."], ["Never been to school.", "학교라곤 가본 적이 없다."], ["What’s home?", "집이 뭔데?"], ["Knows a broom’s a broom, and knows it’s wicked to tell a lie.", "빗자루가 빗자루인 건 알고, 거짓말하는 게 나쁘다는 것도 안다."], ["Don’t recollect who told him about the broom or about the lie, but knows both.", "빗자루며 거짓말이며 누가 가르쳐줬는지는 기억 못 하지만, 둘 다 알기는 안다."], ["Can’t exactly say what’ll be done to him arter he’s dead if he tells a lie to the gentlemen here, but believes it’ll be something wery bad to punish him, and serve him right—and so he’ll tell the truth.", "여기 신사분들한테 거짓말을 하면 죽은 뒤에 자기한테 무슨 일이 벌어질지 콕 집어 말하진 못하겠는데, 벌을 받으려고 아주 고약한 일이 닥칠 거고 그래 싸다고 믿는다 — 그러니 사실대로 말하겠다."]], [["“This won’t do, gentlemen!”", "\"이래선 안 되겠습니다, 여러분!\""], ["says the coroner with a melancholy shake of the head.", "검시관이 침울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말한다."]], [["“Don’t you think you can receive his evidence, sir?”", "\"이 아이 증언을 받아들이실 수 없겠습니까, 선생?\""], ["asks an attentive juryman.", "주의 깊은 배심원 하나가 묻는다."]], [["“Out of the question,”", "\"어림없는 일입니다.\""], ["says the coroner.", "검시관이 말한다."], ["“You have heard the boy.", "\"방금 그 아이 말을 들으셨잖습니까."], ["‘Can’t exactly say’ won’t do, you know.", "'콕 집어 말하진 못하겠다'는 건 안 됩니다, 아시다시피."], ["We can’t take THAT in a court of justice, gentlemen.", "우리는 그런 걸 사법 법정에서 받아들일 수 없어요, 여러분."], ["It’s terrible depravity.", "끔찍한 타락이지요."], ["Put the boy aside.”", "저 아이는 물려두시오.\""]], [["Boy put aside, to the great edification of the audience, especially of Little Swills, the comic vocalist.", "아이는 한쪽으로 물려지고, 방청객들, 특히 희극 노래꾼 리틀 스윌스는 이를 더없이 유익한 구경거리로 여긴다."]], [["Now. Is there any other witness?", "자, 다른 증인이 또 있소?"], ["No other witness.", "다른 증인은 없다."]], [["Very well, gentlemen!", "좋습니다, 여러분!"], ["Here’s a man unknown, proved to have been in the habit of taking opium in large quantities for a year and a half, found dead of too much opium.", "여기 신원 미상의 남자가 있고, 일 년 반 동안 아편을 다량 복용해온 것으로 입증되었으며, 아편 과다로 숨진 채 발견되었습니다."], ["If you think you have any evidence to lead you to the conclusion that he committed suicide, you will come to that conclusion.", "그가 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 결론으로 이끌 만한 증거가 있다고 보시면, 그 결론에 이르시면 됩니다."], ["If you think it is a case of accidental death, you will find a verdict accordingly.", "사고사라고 보시면, 그에 맞춰 평결을 내리시면 됩니다."]], [["Verdict accordingly.", "그에 맞춰 평결. 사고사."], ["Accidental death. No doubt.", "의심할 여지 없음."], ["Gentlemen, you are discharged.", "여러분, 해산하셔도 됩니다."], ["Good afternoon.", "안녕히 가십시오."]], [["While the coroner buttons his great-coat, Mr. Tulkinghorn and he give private audience to the rejected witness in a corner.", "검시관이 외투 단추를 잠그는 동안, 토킹혼 씨와 그는 구석에서 퇴짜 맞은 증인을 따로 만난다."]], [["That graceless creature only knows that the dead man (whom he recognized just now by his yellow face and black hair) was sometimes hooted and pursued about the streets.", "그 보잘것없는 녀석이 아는 것이라곤, 죽은 사내가(방금 누런 얼굴과 검은 머리로 알아본 그 사람이) 이따금 길거리에서 야유받고 쫓겨 다녔다는 것뿐이다."], ["That one cold winter night when he, the boy, was shivering in a doorway near his crossing, the man turned to look at him, and came back, and having questioned him and found that he had not a friend in the world, said, “Neither have I.", "어느 추운 겨울밤, 그 애가, 그러니까 조가, 제 횡단로 근처 어느 문간에서 덜덜 떨고 있을 때, 그 사내가 돌아서서 자기를 보더니, 되돌아와서, 이것저것 묻고는 이 애한테 세상에 친구 하나 없다는 걸 알자, \"나도 없단다."], ["Not one!”", "하나도!\""], ["and gave him the price of a supper and a night’s lodging.", "하고는 저녁 한 끼와 하룻밤 잠자리 값을 쥐여주었다는 것이다."], ["That the man had often spoken to him since and asked him whether he slept sound at night, and how he bore cold and hunger, and whether he ever wished to die, and similar strange questions.", "그 뒤로도 사내가 자주 말을 걸어, 밤에 잠은 푹 자는지, 추위와 굶주림은 어떻게 견디는지, 죽고 싶은 적은 없는지, 그런 이상한 것들을 묻곤 했다는 것이다."], ["That when the man had no money, he would say in passing, “I am as poor as you to-day, Jo,”", "사내는 돈이 없을 땐 지나치며 \"나도 오늘은 너만큼이나 가난하구나, 조\""], ["but that when he had any, he had always (as the boy most heartily believes) been glad to give him some.", "하곤 했지만, 돈이 좀 있을 땐 늘(이 애가 진심으로 믿기로는) 기꺼이 얼마쯤 나눠주었다는 것이다."]], [["“He was wery good to me,”", "\"그분은 저한테 참 잘해주셨어요.\""], ["says the boy, wiping his eyes with his wretched sleeve.", "아이가 다 해진 소매로 눈을 훔치며 말한다."], ["“Wen I see him a-layin’ so stritched out just now, I wished he could have heerd me tell him so.", "\"방금 그분이 그렇게 쭉 뻗어 누운 걸 보니, 제가 이 말을 하는 걸 그분이 들으셨더라면 싶었어요."], ["He wos wery good to me, he wos!”", "그분은 저한테 참 잘해주셨어요, 정말요!\""]], [["As he shuffles downstairs, Mr. Snagsby, lying in wait for him, puts a half-crown in his hand.", "아이가 발을 끌며 아래층으로 내려갈 때, 그를 기다리고 있던 스내그스비 씨가 그 손에 반 크라운을 쥐여준다."], ["“If you ever see me coming past your crossing with my little woman—I mean a lady—” says Mr.", "\"혹시라도 내가 우리 안사람을 — 아니, 어느 부인을 데리고 네 횡단로 앞을 지나가는 걸 보거든,\""], ["Snagsby with his finger on his nose, “don’t allude to it!”", "스내그스비 씨가 코에 손가락을 갖다 대고 말한다. \"그 일은 모른 척해다오!\""]], [["For some little time the jurymen hang about the Sol’s Arms colloquially.", "한동안 배심원들이 솔스 암스 언저리에 모여 두런두런 이야기를 나눈다."], ["In the sequel, half-a-dozen are caught up in a cloud of pipe-smoke that pervades the parlour of the Sol’s Arms; two stroll to Hampstead; and four engage to go half-price to the play at night, and top up with oysters.", "그 끝에, 예닐곱은 솔스 암스 응접실에 자욱한 담배 연기 구름 속에 휩싸이고, 둘은 햄스테드로 어슬렁어슬렁 떠나며, 넷은 밤에 반값으로 연극을 보고 굴 안주로 마무리하기로 약속한다."], ["Little Swills is treated on several hands.", "리틀 스윌스는 여러 손에서 한턱을 얻어먹는다."], ["Being asked what he thinks of the proceedings, characterizes them (his strength lying in a slangular direction) as “a rummy start.”", "이 절차를 어떻게 보느냐는 물음에, 그는(그의 장기가 속어 쪽에 있는지라) 그것을 \"별난 한판\"이라 평한다."], ["The landlord of the Sol’s Arms, finding Little Swills so popular, commends him highly to the jurymen and public, observing that for a song in character he don’t know his equal and that that man’s character-wardrobe would fill a cart.", "솔스 암스 주인은 리틀 스윌스가 이리 인기 있는 걸 보고는, 배심원들과 손님들에게 그를 한껏 추켜세우며, 배역을 살린 노래로 말하자면 그에 견줄 자를 모르겠다고, 그자의 분장 의상만 해도 짐수레 하나를 가득 채우고도 남을 거라고 한다."]], [["Thus, gradually the Sol’s Arms melts into the shadowy night and then flares out of it strong in gas. The Harmonic Meeting hour arriving, the gentleman of professional celebrity takes the chair, is faced (red-faced) by Little Swills; their friends rally round them and support first-rate talent. In the zenith of the evening, Little Swills says, “Gentlemen, if you’ll permit me, I’ll attempt a short description of a scene of real life that came off here to-day.” Is much applauded and encouraged; goes out of the room as Swills; comes in as the coroner (not the least in the world like him); describes the inquest, with recreative intervals of piano-forte accompaniment, to the refrain: With his (the coroner’s) tippy tol li doll, tippy tol lo doll, tippy tol li doll, Dee!", "이렇게 솔스 암스는 차츰 어스름한 밤 속으로 녹아들었다가, 이내 가스 불빛으로 환히 타오르며 다시 솟아난다. 화음 모임 시각이 닥치자, 직업적으로 이름난 신사가 좌장을 맡고, 그 맞은편엔 (벌건 얼굴의) 리틀 스윌스가 앉는다. 친구들이 그들 곁에 모여들어 일류 재능을 응원한다. 저녁이 무르익을 무렵, 리틀 스윌스가 말한다. \"여러분, 허락해주신다면, 오늘 이곳에서 벌어진 실제 삶의 한 장면을 짧게 묘사해보겠습니다.\" 갈채와 격려가 쏟아진다. 그는 스윌스로 방을 나갔다가, 검시관이 되어 들어온다(실은 눈곱만큼도 닮지 않았다). 그러고는 피아노 반주를 곁들인 흥겨운 막간을 사이사이 끼워가며 검시를 묘사하는데, 후렴은 이렇다. 그분의(검시관의) 티피 톨 리 돌, 티피 톨 로 돌, 티피 톨 리 돌, 디!"]], [["The jingling piano at last is silent, and the Harmonic friends rally round their pillows.", "땡땡거리던 피아노가 마침내 잠잠해지고, 화음 모임 친구들은 저마다 제 베개로 모여든다."], ["Then there is rest around the lonely figure, now laid in its last earthly habitation; and it is watched by the gaunt eyes in the shutters through some quiet hours of night.", "그러자 이제 마지막 이승의 거처에 뉘인 그 외로운 형체 둘레에 고요가 깃들고, 밤의 적막한 몇 시간 동안 덧문에 그려진 앙상한 눈들이 그를 지켜본다."], ["If this forlorn man could have been prophetically seen lying here by the mother at whose breast he nestled, a little child, with eyes upraised to her loving face, and soft hand scarcely knowing how to close upon the neck to which it crept, what an impossibility the vision would have seemed!", "만일 이 가엾은 사내가 여기 이렇게 누워 있는 모습을, 한때 자기 품에 안겨 사랑 어린 얼굴을 우러러보던 갓난아이의 보드라운 손이 어찌 감싸 쥐어야 할지도 모른 채 그 목덜미로 기어오르던 — 그 젖을 물리던 어머니가 예언처럼 미리 보았더라면, 그 광경이 얼마나 있을 수 없는 일로 보였겠는가!"], ["Oh, if in brighter days the now-extinguished fire within him ever burned for one woman who held him in her heart, where is she, while these ashes are above the ground!", "오, 더 환했던 시절에, 이제는 꺼져버린 그의 가슴속 불길이 그를 마음에 품은 어느 여인을 향해 단 한 번이라도 타올랐다면, 이 잿더미가 땅 위에 놓인 지금 그녀는 어디 있는가!"]], [["It is anything but a night of rest at Mr. Snagsby’s, in Cook’s Court, where Guster murders sleep by going, as Mr.", "쿡스 코트 스내그스비 씨네는 도무지 안식의 밤이 아닌데, 거스터가 — 스내그스비 씨 본인이 인정하듯, 너무 까놓고 말하긴 뭣하지만 — 발작을 한 번에서 스무 번으로 줄줄이 일으키며 잠을 죽이고 있기 때문이다."], ["Snagsby himself allows—not to put too fine a point upon it—out of one fit into twenty.", "이 발작의 빌미는 거스터에게 여린 마음과 더불어 어쩌면 상상력이라 할 만한 어떤 예민한 기질이 있다는 데 있다 — 투팅과 그녀의 수호성인만 아니었더라면 말이다."], ["The occasion of this seizure is that Guster has a tender heart and a susceptible something that possibly might have been imagination, but for Tooting and her patron saint.", "그게 무엇이든, 어쨌든 그것이 차 마시는 시각에 스내그스비 씨가 들려준, 자기가 거든 그 조사 이야기에 어찌나 끔찍하게 사로잡혔던지, 저녁 식사 때 그녀는 둥근 네덜란드 치즈 하나를 앞세워 날리며 부엌으로 나동그라지더니 유난히 긴 발작에 빠졌고, 거기서 겨우 빠져나오자마자 또 다른 발작으로, 또 다른 발작으로, 그렇게 짧은 틈을 사이에 둔 발작의 사슬을 줄줄이 이어갔다."], ["Be it what it may, now, it was so direfully impressed at tea-time by Mr.", "그녀는 그 짧은 틈마저 가련하게도, 자기가 \"아주 정신이 들거든\""], ["Snagsby’s account of the inquiry at which he had assisted that at supper-time she projected herself into the kitchen, preceded by a flying Dutch cheese, and fell into a fit of unusual duration, which she only came out of to go into another, and another, and so on through a chain of fits, with short intervals between, of which she has pathetically availed herself by consuming them in entreaties to Mrs.", "내보내지 말아 달라고 스내그스비 부인에게 애원하는 데, 또 자기를 돌바닥에 뉘어놓고 다들 자러 가라고 온 식구에게 호소하는 데 써먹는다. 그리하여 마침내 커시터 스트리트 자그마한 낙농장의 수탉이 동틀 녘을 두고 그 사심 없는 황홀경에 빠져드는 소리를 듣고서, 스내그스비 씨는 더없이 참을성 많은 사람인데도 긴 한숨을 내쉬며 말한다."], ["Snagsby not to give her warning “when she quite comes to,”", "\"난 정말이지 네가 죽은 줄로만 알았다!\""], ["and also in appeals to the whole establishment to lay her down on the stones and go to bed.", "* 투팅: 런던 외곽의 빈민 위탁 양육 시설이 있던 곳."], ["Hence, Mr. Snagsby, at last hearing the cock at the little dairy in Cursitor Street go into that disinterested ecstasy of his on the subject of daylight, says, drawing a long breath, though the most patient of men, “I thought you was dead, I am sure!”", "거스터가 그곳 출신임을 암시하며, 열악한 환경 탓에 상상력이라 할 만한 것이 제대로 자라지 못했다는 풍자다."]], [["What question this enthusiastic fowl supposes he settles when he strains himself to such an extent, or why he should thus crow (so men crow on various triumphant public occasions, however) about what cannot be of any moment to him, is his affair.", "이 신바람 난 닭이 그토록 기를 쓸 때 대체 무슨 문제를 매듭짓는다고 여기는지, 아니면 제게 조금도 중할 게 없는 일을 두고 어찌 이리 울어대는지는(하기야 사람도 갖가지 의기양양한 공식 자리에서 이렇게 울어대긴 한다) 그놈 사정이다."], ["It is enough that daylight comes, morning comes, noon comes.", "어쨌든 동이 트고, 아침이 오고, 정오가 온다는 것으로 족하다."]], [["Then the active and intelligent, who has got into the morning papers as such, comes with his pauper company to Mr.", "그러자 그 민첩하고 영민한 자가 — 그렇게 조간신문에 실린 그자가 — 빈민 일행을 거느리고 크룩 씨네로 와서, 여기 떠나간 우리 형제의 시신을 둘러메고 사방이 꽉 막힌 어느 묘지로 향한다."], ["Krook’s and bears off the body of our dear brother here departed to a hemmed-in churchyard, pestiferous and obscene, whence malignant diseases are communicated to the bodies of our dear brothers and sisters who have not departed, while our dear brothers and sisters who hang about official back-stairs—would to heaven they HAD departed!—are very complacent and agreeable.", "역병이 들끓고 추악한 그곳에서는 떠나가지 않은 우리 형제자매의 몸으로 악성 질병이 옮겨 가는 한편, 관청 뒷계단을 어슬렁대는 우리 형제자매는 — 차라리 그들이 떠나갔더라면, 오, 그랬더라면! — 더없이 흡족하고 흐뭇해한다."], ["Into a beastly scrap of ground which a Turk would reject as a savage abomination and a Caffre would shudder at, they bring our dear brother here departed to receive Christian burial.", "터키인이라면 야만스러운 가증으로 여겨 마다하고 카프레인이라면 몸서리쳤을 짐승 우리 같은 한 뙈기 땅으로, 그들은 여기 떠나간 우리 형제를 데려와 기독교식 장례를 치러준다. * 카프레: 남아프리카의 한 부족을 일컫던 옛말로, 당시 영국인이 '미개인'의 본보기로 흔히 들먹이던 표현이다."]], [["With houses looking on, on every side, save where a reeking little tunnel of a court gives access to the iron gate—with every villainy of life in action close on death, and every poisonous element of death in action close on life—here they lower our dear brother down a foot or two, here sow him in corruption, to be raised in corruption: an avenging ghost at many a sick-bedside, a shameful testimony to future ages how civilization and barbarism walked this boastful island together.", "사방으로 집들이 내려다보는 가운데 — 다만 김이 무럭무럭 오르는 자그마한 굴 같은 골목 하나만이 무쇠 대문으로 통하는 곳에서 — 삶의 온갖 악행이 죽음에 바짝 붙어 벌어지고, 죽음의 온갖 독한 기운이 삶에 바짝 붙어 벌어지는 이곳에서, 그들은 여기 떠나간 우리 형제를 한두 자쯤 내려놓는다. 여기 그를 부패 속에 씨 뿌려, 부패 속에서 일으켜 세워지게 한다. 숱한 병상 머리맡을 떠도는 복수의 유령으로, 또 문명과 야만이 이 잘난 척하는 섬나라를 어떻게 나란히 걸어왔는지를 후세에 증언하는 부끄러운 증거로."]], [["Come night, come darkness, for you cannot come too soon or stay too long by such a place as this!", "오라, 밤이여, 오라, 어둠이여, 이런 곳에는 아무리 빨리 와도 이르지 않고 아무리 오래 머물러도 길지 않으니!"], ["Come, straggling lights into the windows of the ugly houses; and you who do iniquity therein, do it at least with this dread scene shut out!", "오라, 흉한 집들의 창마다 듬성듬성 깃드는 불빛이여. 그리고 그 안에서 못된 짓을 저지르는 그대들이여, 최소한 이 끔찍한 광경만은 가려둔 채 저지르라!"], ["Come, flame of gas, burning so sullenly above the iron gate, on which the poisoned air deposits its witch-ointment slimy to the touch!", "오라, 무쇠 대문 위에서 그토록 음울하게 타오르는 가스 불길이여, 그 대문에는 독한 공기가 손에 닿으면 끈적이는 마녀의 고약을 발라놓는구나!"], ["It is well that you should call to every passerby, “Look here!”", "그대가 지나는 행인마다 붙잡고 \"여기를 보시오!\" 하고 외쳐줌이 마땅하리라."]], [["With the night comes a slouching figure through the tunnel-court to the outside of the iron gate.", "밤과 더불어, 구부정한 형체 하나가 굴 같은 골목을 지나 무쇠 대문 바깥으로 다가온다."], ["It holds the gate with its hands and looks in between the bars, stands looking in for a little while.", "그것은 두 손으로 대문을 붙잡고 창살 사이로 안을 들여다보며, 한동안 그렇게 서서 들여다본다."]], [["It then, with an old broom it carries, softly sweeps the step and makes the archway clean.", "그러다가 들고 온 낡은 빗자루로, 그것은 가만가만 계단을 쓸어 아치 길을 깨끗이 한다."], ["It does so very busily and trimly, looks in again a little while, and so departs.", "그것은 더없이 부지런하고 야무지게 그렇게 하고는, 다시 한동안 안을 들여다보더니, 이내 자리를 뜬다."]], [["Jo, is it thou?", "조, 너로구나?"], ["Well, well!", "그래, 그래!"], ["Though a rejected witness, who “can’t exactly say” what will be done to him in greater hands than men’s, thou art not quite in outer darkness.", "비록 퇴짜 맞은 증인이요, 사람의 손보다 더 큰 손이 자기한테 무슨 일을 하실지 \"콕 집어 말하진 못하\"는 처지일망정, 너는 아주 캄캄한 바깥 어둠 속에 있는 건 아니로구나."], ["There is something like a distant ray of light in thy muttered reason for this: “He wos wery good to me, he wos!”", "네가 중얼거린 이 까닭 속에 어딘가 머나먼 빛줄기 같은 것이 깃들어 있으니."], ["On the Watch", "\"그분은 저한테 참 잘해주셨어요, 정말요!\""]], [["It has left off raining down in Lincolnshire at last, and Chesney Wold has taken heart.", "링컨셔에 마침내 비가 그쳤고, 체스니 월드는 다시 생기를 찾았다."], ["Mrs. Rouncewell is full of hospitable cares, for Sir Leicester and my Lady are coming home from Paris.", "라운스웰 부인은 손님맞이 채비로 분주하니, 레스터 경과 마님이 파리에서 돌아오기 때문이다."], ["The fashionable intelligence has found it out and communicates the glad tidings to benighted England.", "유행 소식통은 이 사실을 알아내어 어둠에 잠긴 잉글랜드에 그 기쁜 소식을 전한다."], ["It has also found out that they will entertain a brilliant and distinguished circle of the ELITE of the BEAU MONDE (the fashionable intelligence is weak in English, but a giant refreshed in French) at the ancient and hospitable family seat in Lincolnshire.", "또한 소식통은 그분들이 링컨셔의 유서 깊고 인심 후한 가문의 저택에서 사교계 최고 명사들로 이루어진 화려하고 고귀한 모임을 베풀 거라는 사실까지 알아냈다(유행 소식통은 영어에는 약하지만 프랑스어 앞에서는 새 기운을 얻은 거인이다)."]], [["For the greater honour of the brilliant and distinguished circle, and of Chesney Wold into the bargain, the broken arch of the bridge in the park is mended; and the water, now retired within its proper limits and again spanned gracefully, makes a figure in the prospect from the house.", "화려하고 고귀한 모임의 영예를 더하고, 덤으로 체스니 월드의 영예까지 더하기 위해, 정원에 놓인 다리의 부서진 아치가 손질되었다. 이제 제 경계 안으로 물러나 다시 우아하게 다리 밑을 흐르는 물줄기가 저택에서 바라보는 풍경에 한몫한다."], ["The clear, cold sunshine glances into the brittle woods and approvingly beholds the sharp wind scattering the leaves and drying the moss.", "맑고 차가운 햇살이 메마른 숲속으로 비스듬히 스며들어, 매서운 바람이 낙엽을 흩날리고 이끼를 말리는 광경을 흡족하게 굽어본다."], ["It glides over the park after the moving shadows of the clouds, and chases them, and never catches them, all day.", "햇살은 정원을 가로질러 구름의 움직이는 그림자를 뒤쫓다가, 끝내 붙잡지 못한 채 온종일 뒤만 쫓는다."], ["It looks in at the windows and touches the ancestral portraits with bars and patches of brightness never contemplated by the painters.", "창문을 들여다보고는 조상들의 초상화에 화가가 미처 생각지도 못한 빛줄기와 빛무리를 드리운다."], ["Athwart the picture of my Lady, over the great chimney-piece, it throws a broad bend-sinister of light that strikes down crookedly into the hearth and seems to rend it.", "큰 벽난로 위에 걸린 마님의 초상화를 비스듬히 가로질러, 햇살은 널찍한 사선 띠 같은 빛을 던지는데, 그 빛은 비뚤어진 채 난로 바닥으로 내리꽂혀 그것을 찢어발기는 듯하다."]], [["Through the same cold sunshine and the same sharp wind, my Lady and Sir Leicester, in their travelling chariot (my Lady’s woman and Sir Leicester’s man affectionate in the rumble), start for home.", "바로 그 차가운 햇살과 바로 그 매서운 바람을 뚫고, 마님과 레스터 경은 여행용 마차를 타고(마님의 하녀와 레스터 경의 하인은 뒷자리에서 다정하게 앉아) 집으로 향한다."], ["With a considerable amount of jingling and whip-cracking, and many plunging demonstrations on the part of two bare-backed horses and two centaurs with glazed hats, jack-boots, and flowing manes and tails, they rattle out of the yard of the Hôtel Bristol in the Place Vendôme and canter between the sun-and-shadow-chequered colonnade of the Rue de Rivoli and the garden of the ill-fated palace of a headless king and queen, off by the Place of Concord, and the Elysian Fields, and the Gate of the Star, out of Paris.", "방울 소리며 채찍 휘두르는 소리가 요란하게 울리고, 안장 없는 말 두 마리와 번쩍이는 모자에 장화를 신고 갈기와 꼬리를 휘날리는 켄타우로스 같은 마부 두 사람이 거세게 날뛰는 가운데, 마차는 방돔 광장에 자리한 호텔 브리스톨의 마당을 덜컹덜컹 빠져나가, 햇빛과 그늘이 바둑판처럼 얼룩진 리볼리 거리의 회랑과 머리 잘린 왕과 왕비의 불운한 궁전 정원 사이를 달려, 콩코르드 광장과 엘리제 들판과 별의 문을 지나, 파리를 벗어난다."]], [["Sooth to say, they cannot go away too fast, for even here my Lady Dedlock has been bored to death.", "사실대로 말하자면, 아무리 빨리 떠나도 모자랄 지경이니, 이곳에서마저 데드록 마님은 지루해 죽을 지경이었기 때문이다."], ["Concert, assembly, opera, theatre, drive, nothing is new to my Lady under the worn-out heavens.", "음악회며 모임이며 오페라며 연극이며 마차 산책이며, 닳고 닳은 하늘 아래 마님에게 새로운 것이라곤 하나도 없다."], ["Only last Sunday, when poor wretches were gay—within the walls playing with children among the clipped trees and the statues in the Palace Garden; walking, a score abreast, in the Elysian Fields, made more Elysian by performing dogs and wooden horses; between whiles filtering (a few) through the gloomy Cathedral of Our Lady to say a word or two at the base of a pillar within flare of a rusty little gridiron-full of gusty little tapers; without the walls encompassing Paris with dancing, love-making, wine-drinking, tobacco-smoking, tomb-visiting, billiard card and domino playing, quack-doctoring, and much murderous refuse, animate and inanimate—only last Sunday, my Lady, in the desolation of Boredom and the clutch of Giant Despair, almost hated her own maid for being in spirits.", "바로 지난 일요일만 해도, 가엾은 가난뱅이들이 흥겨워하던 그날—성벽 안에서는 궁전 정원의 다듬은 나무와 조각상들 사이에서 아이들과 놀았고, 묘기 부리는 개와 목마 덕분에 한층 더 낙원다워진 엘리제 들판을 스무 명씩 나란히 줄지어 거닐었으며, 틈틈이 (몇몇은) 침침한 노트르담 대성당으로 스며들어, 바람에 흔들리는 작은 양초가 가득 꽂힌 녹슨 자그마한 촛대의 불빛 아래 기둥 발치에서 두어 마디 기도를 올렸고, 성벽 밖에서는 춤추고 사랑을 나누고 술을 마시고 담배를 피우고 무덤을 찾고 당구와 카드와 도미노를 치고 돌팔이 의술을 펴는 등 살아 있는 것이든 죽은 것이든 살벌한 온갖 찌꺼기로 파리를 에워싸던 그날—바로 지난 일요일에도, 마님은 권태의 황량함 속에서 절망 거인의 손아귀에 붙들린 채, 자기 하녀가 활기차다는 이유만으로 하마터면 미워할 뻔했다."]], [["She cannot, therefore, go too fast from Paris.", "그러니 마님은 파리에서 아무리 빨리 떠나도 모자란다."], ["Weariness of soul lies before her, as it lies behind—her Ariel has put a girdle of it round the whole earth, and it cannot be unclasped—but the imperfect remedy is always to fly from the last place where it has been experienced.", "영혼의 권태가 뒤에 깔린 만큼 앞에도 깔려 있으니—마님의 요정 아리엘이 그 권태로 온 땅을 한 바퀴 띠처럼 둘러놓았고, 그 띠는 풀 길이 없다—그래도 한 가지 불완전한 처방이라면, 그것을 맛본 마지막 장소에서 언제나 달아나는 것이다."], ["Fling Paris back into the distance, then, exchanging it for endless avenues and cross-avenues of wintry trees!", "그러니 파리를 멀리 뒤로 내던지고, 겨울 나무가 끝없이 늘어선 가로수길과 교차로로 바꿔치자!"], ["And, when next beheld, let it be some leagues away, with the Gate of the Star a white speck glittering in the sun, and the city a mere mound in a plain—two dark square towers rising out of it, and light and shadow descending on it aslant, like the angels in Jacob’s dream!", "그리고 다음번에 파리를 바라볼 때는, 별의 문이 햇빛에 반짝이는 흰 점으로 보일 만큼, 도시가 들판에 솟은 한낱 흙무더기로 보일 만큼 몇 리는 떨어진 곳에서 보도록 하자—그 흙무더기에서 두 개의 검은 사각 탑이 솟아오르고, 빛과 그림자가 야곱의 꿈에 나온 천사들처럼 비스듬히 그 위로 내려앉도록!"]], [["Sir Leicester is generally in a complacent state, and rarely bored.", "레스터 경은 대체로 흡족한 상태이고, 좀처럼 지루해하는 법이 없다."], ["When he has nothing else to do, he can always contemplate his own greatness.", "달리 할 일이 없을 때면 언제든 자기 자신의 위대함을 곱씹어볼 수 있기 때문이다."], ["It is a considerable advantage to a man to have so inexhaustible a subject.", "그토록 무궁무진한 화제를 지녔다는 것은 사람에게 상당한 이점이다."], ["After reading his letters, he leans back in his corner of the carriage and generally reviews his importance to society.", "편지를 다 읽고 나면, 그는 마차 구석에 등을 기대고 대체로 사회에서 자기가 차지하는 비중을 되새겨본다."]], [["“You have an unusual amount of correspondence this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침엔 편지가 유난히 많이 왔나 봐요?\""], ["says my Lady after a long time.", "한참 만에 마님이 말한다."], ["She is fatigued with reading.", "책 읽기에 지친 참이다."], ["Has almost read a page in twenty miles.", "이십 마일을 오는 동안 겨우 한 쪽을 읽었다."]], [["“Nothing in it, though.", "\"아무것도 없는 거요, 그래도."], ["Nothing whatever.”", "정말 아무것도.\""]], [["“I saw one of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s long effusions, I think?”", "\"토킹혼 씨의 긴 편지가 한 장 있던 것 같던데요?\""]], [["“You see everything,”", "\"당신은 뭐든 다 보는군.\""], ["says Sir Leicester with admiration.", "레스터 경이 감탄하며 말한다."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["sighs my Lady.", "마님이 한숨짓는다."], ["“He is the most tiresome of men!”", "\"사람치고 제일 지긋지긋한 양반이지요!\""]], [["“He sends—I really beg your pardon—he sends,”", "\"그가—이거 정말 미안하구려—그가,\""], ["says Sir Leicester, selecting the letter and unfolding it, “a message to you.", "레스터 경이 편지를 골라 펼치며 말한다, \"당신에게 전갈을 보냈소."], ["Our stopping to change horses as I came to his postscript drove it out of my memory.", "추신에 이르렀을 때 마침 말을 바꾸느라 멈춰서 그만 까맣게 잊었구려."], ["I beg you’ll excuse me.", "부디 용서하시오."], ["He says—”", "그가 말하길—\""], ["Sir Leicester is so long in taking out his eye-glass and adjusting it that my Lady looks a little irritated.", "레스터 경이 안경을 꺼내 맞추는 데 어찌나 오래 걸리는지 마님이 살짝 짜증스러운 기색을 보인다."], ["“He says ‘In the matter of the right of way—’ I beg your pardon, that’s not the place.", "\"그가 말하길 '통행권 문제에 관하여—' 미안하오, 그 대목이 아니구려."], ["He says—yes!", "그가 말하길—그래!"], ["Here I have it!", "여기 있구려!"], ["He says, ‘I beg my respectful compliments to my Lady, who, I hope, has benefited by the change.", "그가 말하길, '마님께 정중히 안부를 전합니다. 부디 환경을 바꾸시어 효험을 보셨기를 바랍니다."], ["Will you do me the favour to mention (as it may interest her) that I have something to tell her on her return in reference to the person who copied the affidavit in the Chancery suit, which so powerfully stimulated her curiosity.", "마님께서 흥미를 가지실 만한 일이라 여쭙건대, 마님께서 그토록 강하게 호기심을 보이셨던 대법원 소송의 진술서를 베껴 쓴 그 사람과 관련하여, 돌아오시면 말씀드릴 것이 있다고 전해 주시겠습니까."], ["I have seen him.’”", "그자를 만났습니다.'\""]], [["My Lady, leaning forward, looks out of her window.", "마님이 몸을 앞으로 기울여 창밖을 내다본다."]], [["“That’s the message,”", "\"이게 그 전갈이오.\""], ["observes Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 말한다."]], [["“I should like to walk a little,”", "\"좀 걷고 싶어요.\""], ["says my Lady, still looking out of her window.", "여전히 창밖을 내다보며 마님이 말한다."]], [["“Walk?”", "\"걷겠다고?\""], ["repeats Sir Leicester in a tone of surprise.", "레스터 경이 놀란 어조로 되묻는다."]], [["“I should like to walk a little,”", "\"좀 걷고 싶어요.\""], ["says my Lady with unmistakable distinctness.", "마님이 또렷하기 그지없게 말한다."], ["“Please to stop the carriage.”", "\"마차를 세워 주세요.\""]], [["The carriage is stopped, the affectionate man alights from the rumble, opens the door, and lets down the steps, obedient to an impatient motion of my Lady’s hand.", "마차가 멈추고, 다정한 하인이 뒷자리에서 내려와 문을 열고 발판을 내리니, 마님이 손을 조급하게 움직여 보인 명령에 따른 것이다."], ["My Lady alights so quickly and walks away so quickly that Sir Leicester, for all his scrupulous politeness, is unable to assist her, and is left behind.", "마님이 어찌나 잽싸게 내려 어찌나 잽싸게 걸어가는지, 레스터 경은 그 깍듯한 예의에도 불구하고 그녀를 거들지 못한 채 뒤에 처진다."], ["A space of a minute or two has elapsed before he comes up with her.", "일이 분쯤 지나서야 그는 그녀를 따라잡는다."], ["She smiles, looks very handsome, takes his arm, lounges with him for a quarter of a mile, is very much bored, and resumes her seat in the carriage.", "마님은 미소 짓고, 무척 아름다워 보이고, 그의 팔을 잡고, 사분의 일 마일쯤 그와 함께 어슬렁거리다가, 몹시 지루해하더니, 다시 마차 자리로 돌아온다."]], [["The rattle and clatter continue through the greater part of three days, with more or less of bell-jingling and whip-cracking, and more or less plunging of centaurs and bare-backed horses.", "덜컹거림과 달그락거림은 사흘 가운데 거의 내내 이어지고, 방울 소리와 채찍 소리도 더하거나 덜한 채 따르며, 켄타우로스와 안장 없는 말이 날뛰는 것도 더하거나 덜한 채 이어진다."], ["Their courtly politeness to each other at the hotels where they tarry is the theme of general admiration.", "묵어가는 호텔마다 두 사람이 서로에게 보이는 궁정식 예의는 두루 감탄을 자아내는 화젯거리다."], ["Though my Lord IS a little aged for my Lady, says Madame, the hostess of the Golden Ape, and though he might be her amiable father, one can see at a glance that they love each other.", "황금 원숭이 여관의 안주인 마담 말로는, 나리께서 마님보다는 좀 연세가 있고 그 다정한 아버지뻘은 됨직하지만, 두 분이 서로 사랑한다는 건 한눈에 알아볼 수 있단다."], ["One observes my Lord with his white hair, standing, hat in hand, to help my Lady to and from the carriage.", "백발이 성성한 나리께서 모자를 손에 들고 서서 마님이 마차에 오르고 내리는 걸 거드는 모습을 보라."], ["One observes my Lady, how recognisant of my Lord’s politeness, with an inclination of her gracious head and the concession of her so-genteel fingers!", "나리의 예의에 그토록 고마워하며 우아한 고개를 살짝 숙이고 그토록 고상한 손가락을 내미는 마님을 보라!"], ["It is ravishing!", "황홀할 지경이다!"]], [["The sea has no appreciation of great men, but knocks them about like the small fry.", "바다는 위대한 인물을 알아보는 안목 따위는 없어서, 그들을 송사리처럼 함부로 흔들어댄다."], ["It is habitually hard upon Sir Leicester, whose countenance it greenly mottles in the manner of sage-cheese and in whose aristocratic system it effects a dismal revolution.", "바다는 레스터 경에게 늘 모질게 굴어, 그의 얼굴을 세이지 치즈처럼 푸르뎅뎅하게 얼룩지게 하고, 그의 귀족적 체질에 음울한 혁명을 일으킨다."], ["It is the Radical of Nature to him.", "바다는 그에게 자연이 낳은 급진파다."], ["Nevertheless, his dignity gets over it after stopping to refit, and he goes on with my Lady for Chesney Wold, lying only one night in London on the way to Lincolnshire.", "그래도 그의 위엄은 멈춰 추스른 뒤 그 고비를 넘기고, 그는 마님과 함께 체스니 월드로 향하는 길에 런던에서 단 하룻밤만 묵으며 링컨셔로 나아간다."]], [["Through the same cold sunlight, colder as the day declines, and through the same sharp wind, sharper as the separate shadows of bare trees gloom together in the woods, and as the Ghost’s Walk, touched at the western corner by a pile of fire in the sky, resigns itself to coming night, they drive into the park.", "바로 그 차가운 햇살을 뚫고—날이 저물수록 더 차가워지는 그 햇살을, 또 바로 그 매서운 바람을 뚫고—헐벗은 나무들의 따로따로 드리운 그림자가 숲속에서 한데 어우러져 어두워지고, 서쪽 모퉁이가 하늘에 쌓인 불더미에 물든 유령의 길이 다가오는 밤에 몸을 맡기는 가운데, 두 사람은 마차를 타고 정원으로 들어선다."], ["The rooks, swinging in their lofty houses in the elm-tree avenue, seem to discuss the question of the occupancy of the carriage as it passes underneath, some agreeing that Sir Leicester and my Lady are come down, some arguing with malcontents who won’t admit it, now all consenting to consider the question disposed of, now all breaking out again in violent debate, incited by one obstinate and drowsy bird who will persist in putting in a last contradictory croak.", "느릅나무 가로수길 높다란 둥지에서 흔들리던 떼까마귀들은 마차가 그 밑을 지날 때 마차에 누가 탔는지를 두고 갑론을박하는 듯하니, 어떤 놈들은 레스터 경과 마님이 내려오셨다고 동의하고, 어떤 놈들은 그걸 인정하지 않으려는 불평꾼들과 논쟁을 벌이다가, 이제 다들 그 문제를 다 매듭지었다고 여기는가 싶더니, 또다시 격렬한 논쟁으로 와르르 터져 나오는데, 끝까지 마지막으로 반박하는 까악 소리를 기어이 보태려 드는 고집스럽고 졸음에 겨운 한 마리가 부추긴 탓이다."], ["Leaving them to swing and caw, the travelling chariot rolls on to the house, where fires gleam warmly through some of the windows, though not through so many as to give an inhabited expression to the darkening mass of front.", "까마귀들이 흔들리고 까악거리도록 내버려둔 채, 여행용 마차는 저택으로 굴러가니, 그곳 창문 몇몇으로는 난롯불이 따뜻하게 비치지만, 그 수가 많지 않아 어둑해지는 저택 정면의 거대한 덩어리에 사람 사는 집다운 표정을 주기에는 모자란다."], ["But the brilliant and distinguished circle will soon do that.", "하지만 화려하고 고귀한 모임이 곧 그 일을 해줄 것이다."]], [["Mrs. Rouncewell is in attendance and receives Sir Leicester’s customary shake of the hand with a profound curtsy.", "라운스웰 부인이 시중을 들러 나와, 레스터 경이 늘 그러듯 내미는 악수를 깊이 무릎 굽혀 인사하며 받는다."]], [["“How do you do, Mrs. Rouncewell?", "\"잘 지냈소, 라운스웰 부인?"], ["I am glad to see you.”", "보니 반갑구려.\""]], [["“I hope I have the honour of welcoming you in good health, Sir Leicester?”", "\"건강한 모습으로 맞이하는 영예를 누리게 되었으면 합니다, 레스터 경?\""]], [["“In excellent health, Mrs. Rouncewell.”", "\"아주 건강하다오, 라운스웰 부인.\""]], [["“My Lady is looking charmingly well,”", "\"마님께서도 더없이 어여쁘고 건강해 보이십니다.\""], ["says Mrs. Rouncewell with another curtsy.", "라운스웰 부인이 또 한 번 무릎 굽혀 인사하며 말한다."]], [["My Lady signifies, without profuse expenditure of words, that she is as wearily well as she can hope to be.", "마님은 말을 헤프게 쓰지 않고, 바랄 수 있는 만큼은 지쳐도 건강하다는 뜻을 내비친다."]], [["But Rosa is in the distance, behind the housekeeper; and my Lady, who has not subdued the quickness of her observation, whatever else she may have conquered, asks, “Who is that girl?”", "그런데 가정부 뒤편 멀찍이 로사가 서 있다. 다른 건 다 정복했을지 몰라도 관찰의 민첩함만은 누그러뜨리지 못한 마님이 묻는다. \"저 아가씨는 누구지?\""]], [["“A young scholar of mine, my Lady.", "\"제가 가르치는 어린 제자입니다, 마님."], ["Rosa.”", "로사라고 합니다.\""]], [["“Come here, Rosa!”", "\"이리 오너라, 로사!\""], ["Lady Dedlock beckons her, with even an appearance of interest.", "데드록 마님은 흥미를 보이는 기색까지 띠며 손짓해 부른다."], ["“Why, do you know how pretty you are, child?”", "\"얘야, 네가 얼마나 예쁜지 알고 있느냐?\""], ["she says, touching her shoulder with her two forefingers.", "두 집게손가락으로 그녀의 어깨를 만지며 마님이 말한다."]], [["Rosa, very much abashed, says, “No, if you please, my Lady!”", "로사는 무척 부끄러워하며 말한다. \"아니요, 마님!\""], ["and glances up, and glances down, and don’t know where to look, but looks all the prettier.", "그러고는 눈길을 올렸다 내렸다 어디를 봐야 할지 모르는데, 그럴수록 더 예뻐 보인다."]], [["“How old are you?”", "\"몇 살이지?\""]], [["“Nineteen, my Lady.”", "\"열아홉입니다, 마님.\""]], [["“Nineteen,”", "\"열아홉이라.\""], ["repeats my Lady thoughtfully.", "마님이 생각에 잠겨 되뇐다."], ["“Take care they don’t spoil you by flattery.”", "\"아첨에 휘둘려 사람 버리지 않게 조심하거라.\""]], [["“Yes, my Lady.”", "\"네, 마님.\""]], [["My Lady taps her dimpled cheek with the same delicate gloved fingers and goes on to the foot of the oak staircase, where Sir Leicester pauses for her as her knightly escort.", "마님은 보조개 팬 그녀의 뺨을 그 섬세한 장갑 낀 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리고는 떡갈나무 계단 발치로 걸어가니, 그곳에서 레스터 경이 기사다운 호위 노릇을 하려고 그녀를 기다린다."], ["A staring old Dedlock in a panel, as large as life and as dull, looks as if he didn’t know what to make of it, which was probably his general state of mind in the days of Queen Elizabeth.", "벽판에 그려진, 실물 크기에 실물만큼이나 따분한 데드록 가문의 한 노인이 멀거니 내려다보는데, 이를 어찌 받아들여야 할지 모르겠다는 표정이니, 이는 아마 엘리자베스 여왕 시절 그의 평소 마음 상태였으리라."]], [["That evening, in the housekeeper’s room, Rosa can do nothing but murmur Lady Dedlock’s praises.", "그날 저녁, 가정부 방에서 로사는 데드록 마님을 칭송하는 말밖에 할 줄 모른다."], ["She is so affable, so graceful, so beautiful, so elegant; has such a sweet voice and such a thrilling touch that Rosa can feel it yet!", "그분은 어찌나 상냥하고, 어찌나 우아하고, 어찌나 아름답고, 어찌나 기품 있는지, 목소리가 어찌나 달콤하고 손길이 어찌나 짜릿한지, 로사는 아직도 그 감촉이 느껴질 정도다!"], ["Mrs. Rouncewell confirms all this, not without personal pride, reserving only the one point of affability.", "라운스웰 부인은 이 모든 말을 확인해 주는데, 거기에 개인적인 자부심도 없지 않으나, 단 한 가지 상냥함이라는 점만은 유보한다."], ["Mrs. Rouncewell is not quite sure as to that.", "라운스웰 부인은 그 점에 대해서는 썩 확신이 서지 않는다."], ["Heaven forbid that she should say a syllable in dispraise of any member of that excellent family, above all, of my Lady, whom the whole world admires; but if my Lady would only be “a little more free,”", "그 훌륭한 가문의 어느 누구를, 무엇보다 온 세상이 흠모하는 마님을 헐뜯는 말은 한마디도 하지 않으련다. 하지만 마님이 조금만 더 \"스스럼없이\""], ["not quite so cold and distant, Mrs. Rouncewell thinks she would be more affable.", "대해 주신다면, 그리 차갑고 거리를 두지만 않으신다면, 더 상냥해 보이실 거라고 라운스웰 부인은 생각한다."]], [["“’Tis almost a pity,” Mrs.", "\"거의 안타까울 지경이지요.\""], ["Rouncewell adds—only “almost” because it borders on impiety to suppose that anything could be better than it is, in such an express dispensation as the Dedlock affairs—“that my Lady has no family.", "라운스웰 부인이 덧붙인다—데드록 가문의 일이라는 명백한 섭리 안에서 무엇이 지금보다 더 나을 수 있으리라 여기는 것 자체가 불경에 가깝기에 \"거의\"라고만 한 것이다—\"마님께 가족이 없다는 게 말이다."], ["If she had had a daughter now, a grown young lady, to interest her, I think she would have had the only kind of excellence she wants.”", "만약 따님이 하나 있어서, 다 자란 아가씨가 있어서 마님 마음을 사로잡았더라면, 마님께 모자란 단 한 가지 미덕을 채우셨을 텐데.\""]], [["“Might not that have made her still more proud, grandmother?”", "\"그랬다간 마님이 더더욱 거만해지셨을지도 모르잖아요, 할머니?\""], ["says Watt, who has been home and come back again, he is such a good grandson.", "와트가 말하는데, 워낙 착한 손자라 집에 갔다가 다시 돌아온 참이다."]], [["“More and most, my dear,”", "\"더더욱이라느니 하는 말은, 얘야,\""], ["returns the housekeeper with dignity, “are words it’s not my place to use—nor so much as to hear—applied to any drawback on my Lady.”", "가정부가 위엄 있게 대꾸한다, \"마님을 두고 어떤 흠이든 갖다 붙이는 데 내가 입에 담을 말도 아니고—귀에 담을 말도 아니다.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, grandmother.", "\"죄송해요, 할머니."], ["But she is proud, is she not?”", "하지만 마님은 거만하시잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“If she is, she has reason to be.", "\"거만하시다면, 거만하실 만한 이유가 있어서다."], ["The Dedlock family have always reason to be.”", "데드록 가문은 늘 그럴 만한 이유가 있단다.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["says Watt, “it’s to be hoped they line out of their prayer-books a certain passage for the common people about pride and vainglory.", "와트가 말한다. \"그렇다면 평민들 보라고 기도서에 적힌, 거만과 헛된 영광에 관한 어떤 구절은 빼버리시는 게 좋겠네요."], ["Forgive me, grandmother!", "용서하세요, 할머니!"], ["Only a joke!”", "그냥 농담이에요!\""]], [["“Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, my dear, are not fit subjects for joking.”", "\"레스터 경과 데드록 마님은 농담 삼을 분들이 아니다, 얘야.\""]], [["“Sir Leicester is no joke by any means,”", "\"레스터 경은 결코 농담거리가 아니지요.\""], ["says Watt, “and I humbly ask his pardon.", "와트가 말한다. \"그분께는 삼가 용서를 빕니다."], ["I suppose, grandmother, that even with the family and their guests down here, there is no objection to my prolonging my stay at the Dedlock Arms for a day or two, as any other traveller might?”", "그런데 할머니, 가문 어른들과 손님들이 다 내려와 계셔도, 여느 나그네처럼 제가 데드록 암스 여관에 하루 이틀 더 머무는 데에는 별 거리낄 게 없겠지요?\""]], [["“Surely, none in the world, child.”", "\"그야 세상에 거리낄 게 뭐가 있겠니, 얘야.\""]], [["“I am glad of that,”", "\"그거 다행이네요.\""], ["says Watt, “because I have an inexpressible desire to extend my knowledge of this beautiful neighbourhood.”", "와트가 말한다. \"이 아름다운 동네를 더 알고 싶은 마음이 이루 말할 수 없거든요.\""]], [["He happens to glance at Rosa, who looks down and is very shy indeed.", "그는 마침 로사를 흘끗 보는데, 로사는 눈을 내리깔고 정말이지 무척 수줍어한다."], ["But according to the old superstition, it should be Rosa’s ears that burn, and not her fresh bright cheeks, for my Lady’s maid is holding forth about her at this moment with surpassing energy.", "하지만 옛 미신대로라면 화끈거려야 할 것은 로사의 싱그럽고 발그레한 뺨이 아니라 두 귀일 터이니, 지금 이 순간 마님의 하녀가 로사에 대해 유난한 기세로 떠들어대고 있기 때문이다."]], [["My Lady’s maid is a Frenchwoman of two and thirty, from somewhere in the southern country about Avignon and Marseilles, a large-eyed brown woman with black hair who would be handsome but for a certain feline mouth and general uncomfortable tightness of face, rendering the jaws too eager and the skull too prominent.", "마님의 하녀는 서른두 살 난 프랑스 여자로, 아비뇽과 마르세유 부근 남쪽 어디 출신이다. 검은 머리에 눈이 큰 갈색 피부의 여자인데, 고양이 같은 입매와 얼굴 전체에 어딘가 거북하게 팽팽한 긴장감만 아니라면 미인일 텐데, 그 탓에 턱이 너무 사납고 두개골이 너무 도드라진다."], ["There is something indefinably keen and wan about her anatomy, and she has a watchful way of looking out of the corners of her eyes without turning her head which could be pleasantly dispensed with, especially when she is in an ill humour and near knives.", "그 골격에는 어딘가 형언하기 어려운 예리함과 핼쑥함이 감돌고, 고개를 돌리지 않은 채 눈꼬리로 곁눈질하며 살피는 버릇이 있으니, 그 버릇은—특히 기분이 언짢고 칼붙이가 가까이 있을 때면—없는 편이 차라리 나을 것이다."], ["Through all the good taste of her dress and little adornments, these objections so express themselves that she seems to go about like a very neat she-wolf imperfectly tamed.", "옷차림과 자잘한 장신구가 취향 좋게 갖춰져 있어도, 이런 거슬리는 점들이 스스로 드러나서, 그녀는 어설프게 길든 아주 단정한 암늑대처럼 돌아다니는 듯하다."], ["Besides being accomplished in all the knowledge appertaining to her post, she is almost an Englishwoman in her acquaintance with the language; consequently, she is in no want of words to shower upon Rosa for having attracted my Lady’s attention, and she pours them out with such grim ridicule as she sits at dinner that her companion, the affectionate man, is rather relieved when she arrives at the spoon stage of that performance.", "제 직분에 걸맞은 온갖 지식을 두루 갖춘 데다, 영어 실력까지 거의 영국 여자에 맞먹어, 마님의 관심을 끌었다는 이유로 로사에게 퍼부을 말이 모자랄 리 없으니, 저녁 식탁에 앉아 그 말을 어찌나 음산한 조롱을 담아 쏟아내는지, 함께 앉은 그 다정한 하인은 그녀가 마침내 그 식사의 숟가락 단계에 이르자 차라리 안도할 정도다."]], [["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["She, Hortense, been in my Lady’s service since five years and always kept at the distance, and this doll, this puppet, caressed—absolutely caressed—by my Lady on the moment of her arriving at the house!", "그녀, 오르탕스는 마님을 모신 지 오 년인데도 늘 거리를 두고 부려졌는데, 이 인형 같은, 이 꼭두각시 같은 계집애가 집에 도착하자마자 마님의 귀여움을 받다니—그야말로 귀여움을 받다니!"], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["“And do you know how pretty you are, child?”", "\"얘야, 네가 얼마나 예쁜지 알고 있느냐?\""], ["“No, my Lady.”", "\"아니요, 마님.\""], ["You are right there!", "그건 옳은 대답이지!"], ["“And how old are you, child!", "\"그래 몇 살이냐, 얘야!"], ["And take care they do not spoil you by flattery, child!”", "아첨에 휘둘려 사람 버리지 않게 조심하거라, 얘야!\""], ["Oh, how droll!", "오, 어찌나 우스운지!"], ["It is the BEST thing altogether.", "무엇보다 최고의 구경거리다."]], [["In short, it is such an admirable thing that Mademoiselle Hortense can’t forget it; but at meals for days afterwards, even among her countrywomen and others attached in like capacity to the troop of visitors, relapses into silent enjoyment of the joke—an enjoyment expressed, in her own convivial manner, by an additional tightness of face, thin elongation of compressed lips, and sidewise look, which intense appreciation of humour is frequently reflected in my Lady’s mirrors when my Lady is not among them.", "요컨대 어찌나 대단한 구경거리인지 오르탕스 양은 그 일을 잊지 못하니, 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 식사 자리에서, 같은 처지로 손님 무리를 따라온 동향 여자들이나 다른 이들과 함께 있을 때조차도, 그 농담을 떠올리며 말없이 즐기는 데 빠져든다—그 즐거움은 그녀 특유의 흥겨운 방식으로, 곧 한층 더 팽팽해진 얼굴과, 꾹 다물어 가늘게 늘어난 입술과, 곁눈질로 드러나는데, 이 유머에 대한 강렬한 음미는, 마님이 곁에 없을 때면 마님의 거울들에 곧잘 비친다."]], [["All the mirrors in the house are brought into action now, many of them after a long blank.", "이제 집 안의 거울이란 거울은 죄다 동원되었으니, 그중 많은 것은 오랫동안 텅 비어 있던 끝이다."], ["They reflect handsome faces, simpering faces, youthful faces, faces of threescore and ten that will not submit to be old; the entire collection of faces that have come to pass a January week or two at Chesney Wold, and which the fashionable intelligence, a mighty hunter before the Lord, hunts with a keen scent, from their breaking cover at the Court of St.", "거울은 잘생긴 얼굴, 새치름하게 웃는 얼굴, 젊은 얼굴, 늙기를 마다하는 일흔 줄 얼굴을 비춘다. 체스니 월드에서 일이 주의 일월을 보내러 온 얼굴들의 총집합인데, 주님 앞의 막강한 사냥꾼 같은 유행 소식통은 그들이 세인트 제임스 궁정에서 은신처를 박차고 나오는 순간부터 끝내 사냥당해 죽는 순간까지 예리한 후각으로 뒤쫓는다."], ["James’s to their being run down to death.", "링컨셔의 이 저택은 온통 활기가 넘친다."], ["The place in Lincolnshire is all alive. By day guns and voices are heard ringing in the woods, horsemen and carriages enliven the park roads, servants and hangers-on pervade the village and the Dedlock Arms.", "낮이면 총소리와 사람 목소리가 숲속에 울려 퍼지고, 말 탄 이들과 마차들이 정원 길을 활기차게 만들며, 하인들과 빌붙어 사는 자들이 마을과 데드록 암스 여관을 누빈다."], ["Seen by night from distant openings in the trees, the row of windows in the long drawing-room, where my Lady’s picture hangs over the great chimney-piece, is like a row of jewels set in a black frame.", "밤에 멀리 떨어진 나무 사이 틈으로 바라보면, 큰 벽난로 위에 마님의 초상화가 걸린 기다란 응접실의 한 줄로 늘어선 창문은 검은 액자에 박힌 한 줄의 보석 같다."], ["On Sunday the chill little church is almost warmed by so much gallant company, and the general flavour of the Dedlock dust is quenched in delicate perfumes.", "일요일이면 으슬으슬 추운 작은 교회가 그 많은 화사한 손님들 덕에 거의 따뜻해질 지경이고, 데드록 가문에 감도는 곰팡내는 은은한 향수에 묻혀버린다."]], [["The brilliant and distinguished circle comprehends within it no contracted amount of education, sense, courage, honour, beauty, and virtue.", "화려하고 고귀한 모임은 그 안에 적잖은 양의 교양, 분별, 용기, 명예, 미모, 미덕을 품고 있다."], ["Yet there is something a little wrong about it in despite of its immense advantages.", "그런데도 그 엄청난 장점들에도 불구하고 어딘가 조금 잘못된 데가 있다."], ["What can it be?", "대체 무엇일까?"]], [["Dandyism?", "멋 부림일까?"], ["There is no King George the Fourth now (more the pity) to set the dandy fashion; there are no clear-starched jack-towel neckcloths, no short-waisted coats, no false calves, no stays.", "멋쟁이 유행을 퍼뜨릴 조지 사세는 이제 없다(더더욱 안타깝다). 빳빳이 풀 먹인 행주 같은 목수건도, 허리 짧은 외투도, 가짜 종아리도, 코르셋도 없다."], ["There are no caricatures, now, of effeminate exquisites so arrayed, swooning in opera boxes with excess of delight and being revived by other dainty creatures poking long-necked scent-bottles at their noses.", "그렇게 차려입고 오페라 좌석에서 기쁨에 겨워 까무러쳤다가 다른 곱상한 것들이 목 긴 향수병을 코에 들이대 깨어나는 여자 같은 멋쟁이들을 그린 풍자화도 이제 없다."], ["There is no beau whom it takes four men at once to shake into his buckskins, or who goes to see all the executions, or who is troubled with the self-reproach of having once consumed a pea.", "가죽 바지에 몸을 욱여넣는 데 장정 넷이 한꺼번에 달려들어 흔들어야 하는 멋쟁이도, 처형이란 처형은 죄다 구경 가는 멋쟁이도, 완두콩 한 알 먹어치웠다고 자책에 시달리는 멋쟁이도 없다."], ["But is there dandyism in the brilliant and distinguished circle notwithstanding, dandyism of a more mischievous sort, that has got below the surface and is doing less harmless things than jack-towelling itself and stopping its own digestion, to which no rational person need particularly object?", "그렇다면 화려하고 고귀한 모임에는 그럼에도 멋 부림이 있는 것일까, 그것도 좀 더 해로운 종류의, 겉으로 드러나지 않게 표면 아래로 파고들어, 행주처럼 빳빳이 굴거나 제 소화나 망치는 따위—분별 있는 이라면 굳이 트집 잡을 것도 없는 그런 짓보다 덜 무해한 일을 저지르는 멋 부림이?"]], [["Why, yes. It cannot be disguised.", "그렇다, 있다."], ["There ARE at Chesney Wold this January week some ladies and gentlemen of the newest fashion, who have set up a dandyism—in religion, for instance.", "감출 수 없다. 체스니 월드에는 이 일월 한 주 동안, 이를테면 종교에서 멋 부림을 차린 최신 유행의 신사 숙녀들이 있다."], ["Who in mere lackadaisical want of an emotion have agreed upon a little dandy talk about the vulgar wanting faith in things in general, meaning in the things that have been tried and found wanting, as though a low fellow should unaccountably lose faith in a bad shilling after finding it out!", "그저 감정거리가 권태롭게 그리워서, 평민들이 대체로 만물에 대한 믿음을 잃었다는—그러니까 이미 시험해 보았다가 모자란다고 판명 난 것들에 대한 믿음을 잃었다는 자그마한 멋쟁이식 잡담에 의기투합한 자들 말이다, 마치 천박한 작자가 가짜 실링을 알아보고 나서 영문 모를 이유로 그 가짜 실링에 대한 믿음을 잃기라도 한 양!"], ["Who would make the vulgar very picturesque and faithful by putting back the hands upon the clock of time and cancelling a few hundred years of history.", "시간의 시계 바늘을 거꾸로 돌려 수백 년의 역사를 지워버림으로써 평민들을 무척 그림 같고 신실하게 만들려는 자들 말이다."]], [["There are also ladies and gentlemen of another fashion, not so new, but very elegant, who have agreed to put a smooth glaze on the world and to keep down all its realities.", "또 그만큼 새롭지는 않으나 무척 우아한, 다른 유행의 신사 숙녀들도 있다. 세상에 매끄러운 광택을 입혀 온갖 현실을 짓눌러두기로 의기투합한 자들이다."], ["For whom everything must be languid and pretty.", "이들에게는 모든 것이 나른하고 예뻐야 한다."], ["Who have found out the perpetual stoppage.", "영원한 정지를 발견한 자들이다."], ["Who are to rejoice at nothing and be sorry for nothing.", "어떤 일에도 기뻐하지 않고 어떤 일에도 슬퍼하지 않으려는 자들이다."], ["Who are not to be disturbed by ideas.", "어떤 사상에도 흔들리지 않으려는 자들이다."], ["On whom even the fine arts, attending in powder and walking backward like the Lord Chamberlain, must array themselves in the milliners’ and tailors’ patterns of past generations and be particularly careful not to be in earnest or to receive any impress from the moving age.", "이들에게는 분을 바르고 시종장처럼 뒷걸음질 치며 시중드는 순수 예술마저도, 지난 세대 모자장이와 재단사의 본을 따라 차려입어야 하고, 무엇보다 진지해지지 않도록, 그리고 흘러가는 시대로부터 어떤 자취도 새겨 받지 않도록 각별히 조심해야 한다."]], [["Then there is my Lord Boodle, of considerable reputation with his party, who has known what office is and who tells Sir Leicester Dedlock with much gravity, after dinner, that he really does not see to what the present age is tending.", "그다음으로는 자기 당내에서 상당한 명망을 누리며 관직이 무엇인지 익히 아는 부들 경이 있는데, 만찬 뒤에 그는 레스터 데드록 경에게 자못 진지하게, 정말이지 요즘 세상이 어디로 가는지 도무지 모르겠다고 말한다."], ["A debate is not what a debate used to be; the House is not what the House used to be; even a Cabinet is not what it formerly was.", "토론은 예전의 그 토론이 아니고, 의회는 예전의 그 의회가 아니며, 심지어 내각조차도 예전의 그 내각이 아니다."], ["He perceives with astonishment that supposing the present government to be overthrown, the limited choice of the Crown, in the formation of a new ministry, would lie between Lord Coodle and Sir Thomas Doodle—supposing it to be impossible for the Duke of Foodle to act with Goodle, which may be assumed to be the case in consequence of the breach arising out of that affair with Hoodle.", "그는 놀랍게도, 현 정부가 무너진다고 가정할 때, 새 내각을 꾸리는 데 왕실의 제한된 선택지가 쿠들 경과 토머스 두들 경 사이에 놓일 거라고 본다—단, 푸들 공작이 구들과 손잡는 게 불가능하다고 가정한다면 말인데, 후들과의 그 사건에서 비롯된 불화 탓에 그게 사실이라고 봐도 무방하다."], ["Then, giving the Home Department and the leadership of the House of Commons to Joodle, the Exchequer to Koodle, the Colonies to Loodle, and the Foreign Office to Moodle, what are you to do with Noodle?", "그러고 나서 내무부와 하원 원내총무 자리는 주들에게, 재무부는 쿠들에게, 식민부는 루들에게, 외무부는 무들에게 준다면, 누들은 대체 어쩔 셈인가? 그에게 추밀원 의장 자리를 줄 수는 없다."], ["You can’t offer him the Presidency of the Council; that is reserved for Poodle.", "그건 푸들 몫으로 잡아두었으니까. 그를 산림부에 앉힐 수도 없다."], ["You can’t put him in the Woods and Forests; that is hardly good enough for Quoodle.", "그건 쿠들에게도 좀처럼 과분하니까. 그러면 어찌 되는가?"], ["What follows? That the country is shipwrecked, lost, and gone to pieces (as is made manifest to the patriotism of Sir Leicester Dedlock) because you can’t provide for Noodle!", "누들 자리를 마련해 주지 못하기 때문에 나라가 난파하고, 침몰하고, 산산조각 난다는 것이다(이는 레스터 데드록 경의 애국심에 또렷이 드러난 사실이다)!"]], [["On the other hand, the Right Honourable William Buffy, M.P., contends across the table with some one else that the shipwreck of the country—about which there is no doubt; it is only the manner of it that is in question—is attributable to Cuffy.", "다른 한편으로, 하원 의원이자 우경 윌리엄 버피는 식탁 너머로 다른 누군가와 맞서, 나라의 난파는—그건 의심할 여지가 없고, 다만 그 방식만이 문제다—커피 탓이라고 주장한다."], ["If you had done with Cuffy what you ought to have done when he first came into Parliament, and had prevented him from going over to Duffy, you would have got him into alliance with Fuffy, you would have had with you the weight attaching as a smart debater to Guffy, you would have brought to bear upon the elections the wealth of Huffy, you would have got in for three counties Juffy, Kuffy, and Luffy, and you would have strengthened your administration by the official knowledge and the business habits of Muffy.", "커피가 처음 의회에 들어왔을 때 마땅히 했어야 할 처치를 그에게 했더라면, 그래서 그가 더피에게 넘어가지 못하게 막았더라면, 그를 푸피와 동맹시킬 수 있었을 것이고, 노련한 토론가로서 구피에게 따라붙는 무게를 자기편에 둘 수 있었을 것이고, 후피의 재력을 선거에 활용할 수 있었을 것이고, 세 개 주에서 주피와 쿠피와 루피를 당선시킬 수 있었을 것이고, 무피의 실무 지식과 일머리로 정부를 강화할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["All this, instead of being as you now are, dependent on the mere caprice of Puffy!", "이 모든 게 지금처럼 한낱 푸피의 변덕에 매달리는 대신에 말이다!"]], [["As to this point, and as to some minor topics, there are differences of opinion; but it is perfectly clear to the brilliant and distinguished circle, all round, that nobody is in question but Boodle and his retinue, and Buffy and HIS retinue.", "이 점에 관해서, 그리고 몇몇 사소한 화제에 관해서는 의견이 갈린다. 하지만 화려하고 고귀한 모임에는 어느 모로 보나 부들과 그 무리, 그리고 버피와 그 무리 외에는 거론할 자가 없다는 것이 더없이 분명하다."], ["These are the great actors for whom the stage is reserved.", "이들이 무대를 독차지한 위대한 배우들이다."], ["A People there are, no doubt—a certain large number of supernumeraries, who are to be occasionally addressed, and relied upon for shouts and choruses, as on the theatrical stage; but Boodle and Buffy, their followers and families, their heirs, executors, administrators, and assigns, are the born first-actors, managers, and leaders, and no others can appear upon the scene for ever and ever.", "물론 민중이라는 것도 있다—이따금 말을 걸어주고, 연극 무대에서처럼 함성과 합창을 맡겨 의지할 어느 정도 큰 무리의 단역들 말이다. 하지만 부들과 버피, 그들의 추종자와 가족, 그들의 상속자, 유언 집행인, 유산 관리인, 양수인이야말로 타고난 주연이자 감독이자 지도자이며, 다른 누구도 그 무대에 영영 등장할 수 없다."]], [["In this, too, there is perhaps more dandyism at Chesney Wold than the brilliant and distinguished circle will find good for itself in the long run.", "이 점에서도 어쩌면 체스니 월드에는, 화려하고 고귀한 모임이 길게 보아 제게 이로우리라 여길 만한 양보다 더 많은 멋 부림이 깃들어 있는지 모른다."], ["For it is, even with the stillest and politest circles, as with the circle the necromancer draws around him—very strange appearances may be seen in active motion outside.", "왜냐하면 아무리 고요하고 점잖은 모임이라 해도, 마법사가 제 둘레에 그려놓은 원처럼—원 바깥에서는 무척 기이한 형상들이 활발히 움직이는 게 보이게 마련이기 때문이다."], ["With this difference, that being realities and not phantoms, there is the greater danger of their breaking in.", "다만 다른 점이라면, 그것들이 환영이 아니라 현실인 만큼, 안으로 쳐들어올 위험이 그만큼 더 크다는 것이다."]], [["Chesney Wold is quite full anyhow, so full that a burning sense of injury arises in the breasts of ill-lodged ladies’-maids, and is not to be extinguished.", "어쨌거나 체스니 월드는 발 디딜 틈 없이 꽉 차서, 변변찮은 방을 받은 하녀들 가슴에 활활 타오르는 억울함이 솟구치니, 도무지 꺼질 줄 모른다."], ["Only one room is empty.", "빈방이라곤 하나뿐이다."], ["It is a turret chamber of the third order of merit, plainly but comfortably furnished and having an old-fashioned business air.", "세 번째 등급에 드는 작은 탑 방인데, 소박하지만 안락하게 꾸며져 있고 어딘가 구식 사무실 같은 분위기를 풍긴다."], ["It is Mr. Tulkinghorn’s room, and is never bestowed on anybody else, for he may come at any time.", "토킹혼 씨의 방으로, 그가 언제든 들이닥칠 수 있어 다른 누구에게도 결코 내주지 않는 방이다."], ["He is not come yet.", "그는 아직 오지 않았다."], ["It is his quiet habit to walk across the park from the village in fine weather, to drop into this room as if he had never been out of it since he was last seen there, to request a servant to inform Sir Leicester that he is arrived in case he should be wanted, and to appear ten minutes before dinner in the shadow of the library-door.", "날씨가 좋을 때면 마을에서 정원을 가로질러 걸어와, 마치 지난번 본 이래 한 번도 자리를 비운 적 없었다는 듯 이 방으로 슬며시 들어와서는, 혹시 자기를 찾을지 모르니 레스터 경에게 자기가 도착했다고 알려달라고 하인에게 청하고, 만찬 십 분 전에 서재 문 그늘에 모습을 드러내는 것이 그의 조용한 버릇이다."], ["He sleeps in his turret with a complaining flag-staff over his head, and has some leads outside on which, any fine morning when he is down here, his black figure may be seen walking before breakfast like a larger species of rook.", "그는 머리 위에서 깃대가 끽끽 우는 탑 방에서 잠들고, 바깥에는 납지붕이 좀 있어서, 이 아래 내려와 있을 때면 날씨 좋은 아침마다 그의 검은 형체가 아침 식사 전에 그 위를 거니는 것을, 떼까마귀의 좀 더 큰 변종처럼 거니는 것을 볼 수 있다."]], [["Every day before dinner, my Lady looks for him in the dusk of the library, but he is not there.", "날마다 만찬 전이면 마님은 어둑한 서재에서 그를 찾지만, 그는 거기 없다."], ["Every day at dinner, my Lady glances down the table for the vacant place that would be waiting to receive him if he had just arrived, but there is no vacant place.", "날마다 만찬 자리에서 마님은 식탁을 죽 훑어 그가 막 도착했다면 맞이하려고 비워두었을 빈자리를 찾지만, 빈자리는 없다."], ["Every night my Lady casually asks her maid, “Is Mr.", "밤마다 마님은 무심한 척 하녀에게 묻는다."], ["Tulkinghorn come?”", "\"토킹혼 씨는 오셨느냐?\""]], [["Every night the answer is, “No, my Lady, not yet.”", "밤마다 대답은 한결같다. \"아니요, 마님, 아직입니다.\""]], [["One night, while having her hair undressed, my Lady loses herself in deep thought after this reply until she sees her own brooding face in the opposite glass, and a pair of black eyes curiously observing her.", "어느 밤, 머리를 풀어 내리던 마님은 이 대답을 듣고 깊은 생각에 잠겨 자신을 잊고 있다가, 맞은편 거울에 비친 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 제 얼굴을 발견하고, 또 자기를 호기심 어린 눈초리로 살피는 검은 두 눈을 발견한다."]], [["“Be so good as to attend,”", "\"이거 좀 신경 써 다오.\""], ["says my Lady then, addressing the reflection of Hortense, “to your business.", "그러자 마님이 거울에 비친 오르탕스를 향해 말한다. \"네 할 일에 말이다."], ["You can contemplate your beauty at another time.”", "네 미모는 다른 때에 감상하면 되잖느냐.\""]], [["“Pardon!", "\"송구합니다!"], ["It was your Ladyship’s beauty.”", "마님의 미모를 본 거였습니다.\""]], [["“That,”", "\"그건,\""], ["says my Lady, “you needn’t contemplate at all.”", "마님이 말한다, \"전혀 감상할 필요 없느니라.\""]], [["At length, one afternoon a little before sunset, when the bright groups of figures which have for the last hour or two enlivened the Ghost’s Walk are all dispersed and only Sir Leicester and my Lady remain upon the terrace, Mr.", "마침내 어느 오후, 해 지기 조금 전, 지난 한두 시간 유령의 길을 활기차게 만들던 화사한 무리들이 죄다 흩어지고 테라스에는 레스터 경과 마님만 남았을 때, 토킹혼 씨가 모습을 드러낸다."], ["Tulkinghorn appears. He comes towards them at his usual methodical pace, which is never quickened, never slackened.", "그는 늘 그러듯 한결같이 절도 있는 걸음으로 두 사람에게 다가오는데, 그 걸음은 빨라지는 법도 느려지는 법도 없다."], ["He wears his usual expressionless mask—if it be a mask—and carries family secrets in every limb of his body and every crease of his dress.", "그는 늘 그러듯 무표정한 가면을—그것이 가면이라면—쓰고, 몸의 마디마디와 옷의 주름마다 가문의 비밀을 지니고 다닌다."], ["Whether his whole soul is devoted to the great or whether he yields them nothing beyond the services he sells is his personal secret.", "그가 온 영혼을 그 귀하신 분들에게 바치는지, 아니면 자기가 파는 봉사 이상은 아무것도 내주지 않는지는 그만의 개인적 비밀이다."], ["He keeps it, as he keeps the secrets of his clients; he is his own client in that matter, and will never betray himself.", "그는 의뢰인들의 비밀을 지키듯 그 비밀을 지킨다. 그 일에서는 그 자신이 제 의뢰인이니, 결코 스스로를 저버리지 않는다."]], [["“How do you do, Mr. Tulkinghorn?”", "\"잘 지냈소, 토킹혼 씨?\""], ["says Sir Leicester, giving him his hand.", "레스터 경이 손을 내밀며 말한다."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn is quite well.", "토킹혼 씨는 아주 잘 지낸다."], ["Sir Leicester is quite well.", "레스터 경도 아주 잘 지낸다."], ["My Lady is quite well.", "마님도 아주 잘 지낸다."], ["All highly satisfactory.", "모두 더없이 흡족하다."], ["The lawyer, with his hands behind him, walks at Sir Leicester’s side along the terrace.", "변호사는 뒷짐을 진 채 레스터 경 옆에 서서 테라스를 따라 걷는다."], ["My Lady walks upon the other side.", "마님은 반대편에서 걷는다."]], [["“We expected you before,”", "\"좀 더 일찍 올 줄 알았소만.\""], ["says Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 말한다."], ["A gracious observation.", "너그러운 한마디다."], ["As much as to say, “Mr.", "이렇게 말하는 셈이다."], ["Tulkinghorn, we remember your existence when you are not here to remind us of it by your presence.", "\"토킹혼 씨, 당신이 여기 있어 그 존재를 일깨워주지 않을 때조차 우리는 당신의 존재를 기억하고 있다오."], ["We bestow a fragment of our minds upon you, sir, you see!”", "우리는 당신에게 우리 마음 한 조각을 베푸는 거요, 보시다시피!\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn, comprehending it, inclines his head and says he is much obliged.", "그 뜻을 알아챈 토킹혼 씨는 고개를 숙이며 무척 황송하다고 말한다."]], [["“I should have come down sooner,”", "\"좀 더 일찍 내려왔어야 했습니다만,\""], ["he explains, “but that I have been much engaged with those matters in the several suits between yourself and Boythorn.”", "그가 해명한다, \"어르신과 보이손 사이의 여러 소송 건에 워낙 매여 있었습니다.\""]], [["“A man of a very ill-regulated mind,”", "\"심성이 아주 고약하게 비뚤어진 작자요.\""], ["observes Sir Leicester with severity.", "레스터 경이 매섭게 평한다."], ["“An extremely dangerous person in any community.", "\"어느 사회에 있든 더없이 위험한 인물이지."], ["A man of a very low character of mind.”", "심성이 무척 천박한 작자란 말이오.\""]], [["“He is obstinate,”", "\"고집이 셉니다.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."]], [["“It is natural to such a man to be so,”", "\"그런 작자에겐 그게 천성이지.\""], ["says Sir Leicester, looking most profoundly obstinate himself.", "레스터 경 자신이 무척이나 고집스러워 보이는 표정으로 말한다."], ["“I am not at all surprised to hear it.”", "\"그런 말 들어도 조금도 놀랍지 않소.\""]], [["“The only question is,”", "\"문제는 단 하나입니다.\""], ["pursues the lawyer, “whether you will give up anything.”", "변호사가 말을 잇는다. \"어르신께서 뭐든 양보하실 의향이 있으신지요.\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"없소, 토킹혼 씨.\""], ["replies Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 대답한다."], ["“Nothing.", "\"아무것도."], ["I give up?”", "내가 양보를 한다고?\""]], [["“I don’t mean anything of importance.", "\"중요한 것을 두고 드리는 말씀은 아닙니다."], ["That, of course, I know you would not abandon.", "그야 물론 어르신께서 포기하지 않으시리라는 걸 압니다."], ["I mean any minor point.”", "사소한 점이라도 말입니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Tulkinghorn,”", "\"토킹혼 씨,\""], ["returns Sir Leicester, “there can be no minor point between myself and Mr.", "레스터 경이 받는다. \"나와 보이손 씨 사이에 사소한 점이란 있을 수 없소."], ["Boythorn. If I go farther, and observe that I cannot readily conceive how ANY right of mine can be a minor point, I speak not so much in reference to myself as an individual as in reference to the family position I have it in charge to maintain.”", "한 걸음 더 나아가, 내 권리 가운데 어떤 것이 어찌 사소한 점이 될 수 있을지 도무지 떠올릴 수 없다고 말한다면, 이는 한 개인으로서의 나 자신을 두고 하는 말이라기보다 내가 지킬 책임을 맡은 가문의 지위를 두고 하는 말이오.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn inclines his head again.", "토킹혼 씨가 다시 고개를 숙인다."], ["“I have now my instructions,”", "\"이제 지시를 받들었습니다.\""], ["he says. “Mr.", "그가 말한다."], ["Boythorn will give us a good deal of trouble—”", "\"보이손 씨가 우리를 적잖이 성가시게 할 텐데—\""]], [["“It is the character of such a mind, Mr. Tulkinghorn,”", "\"그런 심성을 지닌 자의 본성이 바로 그것이오, 토킹혼 씨,\""], ["Sir Leicester interrupts him, “TO give trouble.", "레스터 경이 그의 말을 가로막는다. \"성가시게 구는 것 말이오."], ["An exceedingly ill-conditioned, levelling person.", "더없이 심성 고약한, 평등 타령이나 하는 작자요."], ["A person who, fifty years ago, would probably have been tried at the Old Bailey for some demagogue proceeding, and severely punished—if not,”", "오십 년 전이었다면 십중팔구 무슨 선동가 짓을 벌인 죄로 올드 베일리 법정에 끌려가 호되게 벌을 받았을, 아니,\""], ["adds Sir Leicester after a moment’s pause, “if not hanged, drawn, and quartered.”", "레스터 경이 잠시 뜸을 들이고 덧붙인다, \"아니, 교수형에 처해져 내장이 끄집어내지고 사지가 갈가리 찢겼을 작자요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester appears to discharge his stately breast of a burden in passing this capital sentence, as if it were the next satisfactory thing to having the sentence executed.", "레스터 경은 이 사형 선고를 내리며 위풍당당한 가슴에서 짐을 덜어내는 듯하니, 마치 그 선고를 실제로 집행하는 것 다음으로 흡족한 일이라는 듯하다."]], [["“But night is coming on,”", "\"하지만 밤이 다가오는구려.\""], ["says he, “and my Lady will take cold.", "그가 말한다. \"마님이 감기 들겠소."], ["My dear, let us go in.”", "여보, 안으로 들어갑시다.\""]], [["As they turn towards the hall-door, Lady Dedlock addresses Mr. Tulkinghorn for the first time.", "두 사람이 현관 쪽으로 돌아설 때, 데드록 마님이 처음으로 토킹혼 씨에게 말을 건넨다."]], [["“You sent me a message respecting the person whose writing I happened to inquire about.", "\"제가 우연히 필체를 물어본 그 사람에 관해 전갈을 보내셨더군요."], ["It was like you to remember the circumstance; I had quite forgotten it.", "그 일을 기억하시다니 과연 당신답네요. 저는 까맣게 잊고 있었거든요."], ["Your message reminded me of it again.", "당신 전갈 덕에 다시 떠올렸어요."], ["I can’t imagine what association I had with a hand like that, but I surely had some.”", "제가 그런 필체를 두고 무슨 연상을 했던 건지 도무지 모르겠지만, 분명 뭔가 있긴 했어요.\""]], [["“You had some?” Mr.", "\"뭔가 있으셨다고요?\""], ["Tulkinghorn repeats.", "토킹혼 씨가 되뇐다."]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 그럼요!\""], ["returns my Lady carelessly.", "마님이 무심하게 대꾸한다."], ["“I think I must have had some.", "\"분명 뭔가 있었을 거예요."], ["And did you really take the trouble to find out the writer of that actual thing—what is it!—affidavit?”", "그런데 정말로 수고를 들여, 그 실제 물건을—그게 뭐였더라!—진술서를 베껴 쓴 사람을 찾아내셨다고요?\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “How very odd!”", "\"참 묘한 일이네요!\""]], [["They pass into a sombre breakfast-room on the ground floor, lighted in the day by two deep windows.", "두 사람은 일층의 음침한 아침 식사실로 들어서는데, 낮에는 깊숙한 두 개의 창으로 빛이 든다."], ["It is now twilight.", "지금은 황혼 무렵이다."], ["The fire glows brightly on the panelled wall and palely on the window-glass, where, through the cold reflection of the blaze, the colder landscape shudders in the wind and a grey mist creeps along, the only traveller besides the waste of clouds.", "난롯불이 벽판에는 밝게, 창유리에는 희미하게 일렁이는데, 그 창유리에는 불길의 차가운 반사 너머로 더 차가운 풍경이 바람에 떨고, 잿빛 안개가 기어가니, 떠도는 구름 떼 말고는 그 안개만이 유일한 나그네다."]], [["My Lady lounges in a great chair in the chimney-corner, and Sir Leicester takes another great chair opposite.", "마님은 벽난로 모퉁이의 커다란 의자에 나른히 기대앉고, 레스터 경은 맞은편의 또 다른 커다란 의자에 앉는다."], ["The lawyer stands before the fire with his hand out at arm’s length, shading his face.", "변호사는 난롯불 앞에 서서 한 손을 팔 길이만큼 쭉 뻗어 제 얼굴을 가린다."], ["He looks across his arm at my Lady.", "그는 그 팔 너머로 마님을 바라본다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["he says, “I inquired about the man, and found him.", "그가 말한다. \"그자에 대해 알아보았고, 찾아냈습니다."], ["And, what is very strange, I found him—”", "한데 참으로 기이하게도, 찾고 보니 그자가—\""]], [["“Not to be any out-of-the-way person, I am afraid!”", "\"별 특별할 것 없는 사람이었나 보군요!\""], ["Lady Dedlock languidly anticipates.", "데드록 마님이 나른하게 앞질러 말한다."]], [["“I found him dead.”", "\"죽어 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, dear me!”", "\"아이고, 저런!\""], ["remonstrated Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 못마땅한 듯 한마디 한다."], ["Not so much shocked by the fact as by the fact of the fact being mentioned.", "그 사실 자체에 충격받았다기보다 그 사실이 입에 오른다는 사실에 충격받은 투다."]], [["“I was directed to his lodging—a miserable, poverty-stricken place—and I found him dead.”", "\"그자의 하숙으로 안내받아 갔는데—비참하고 가난에 찌든 곳이었지요—찾고 보니 죽어 있었습니다.\""]], [["“You will excuse me, Mr. Tulkinghorn,”", "\"이거 양해해 주시오, 토킹혼 씨.\""], ["observes Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 한마디 한다."], ["“I think the less said—”", "\"내 생각엔 말을 아끼는 편이—\""]], [["“Pray, Sir Leicester, let me hear the story out” (it is my Lady speaking).", "\"부디, 레스터 경, 이야기를 끝까지 듣게 해줘요\"(마님이 하는 말이다)."], ["“It is quite a story for twilight.", "\"황혼 무렵에 딱 어울리는 이야기네요."], ["How very shocking!", "어찌나 충격적인지!"], ["Dead?”", "죽었다고요?\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn re-asserts it by another inclination of his head.", "토킹혼 씨가 다시 한번 고개를 숙여 그렇다고 거듭 못 박는다."], ["“Whether by his own hand—”", "\"스스로 목숨을 끊은 건지—\""]], [["“Upon my honour!”", "\"맹세코!\""], ["cries Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 외친다."], ["“Really!”", "\"원, 정말이지!\""]], [["“Do let me hear the story!”", "\"제발 그 이야기 좀 듣게 해줘요!\""], ["says my Lady.", "마님이 말한다."]], [["“Whatever you desire, my dear.", "\"당신이 원하는 거라면 무엇이든, 여보."], ["But, I must say—”", "하지만 한마디 해야겠는데—\""]], [["“No, you mustn’t say!", "\"아니요, 하지 마세요!"], ["Go on, Mr. Tulkinghorn.”", "계속하세요, 토킹혼 씨.\""]], [["Sir Leicester’s gallantry concedes the point, though he still feels that to bring this sort of squalor among the upper classes is really—really—", "레스터 경의 신사도가 그 점을 양보하지만, 그래도 그는 이런 종류의 누추함을 상류층 사이로 끌어들이는 게 정말이지—정말이지—싶다."]], [["“I was about to say,”", "\"제가 말씀드리려던 건,\""], ["resumes the lawyer with undisturbed calmness, “that whether he had died by his own hand or not, it was beyond my power to tell you.", "변호사가 흔들림 없는 침착함으로 말을 잇는다, \"그자가 스스로 목숨을 끊었는지 아닌지는 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 범위를 넘어선다는 거였습니다."], ["I should amend that phrase, however, by saying that he had unquestionably died of his own act, though whether by his own deliberate intention or by mischance can never certainly be known.", "다만 그 표현은 이렇게 고쳐야겠지요. 그자가 제 행위로 죽었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없으나, 그것이 스스로의 계획적인 의도에 의한 것인지 아니면 사고에 의한 것인지는 결코 확실히 알 수 없다고 말입니다."], ["The coroner’s jury found that he took the poison accidentally.”", "검시 배심은 그자가 우연히 독을 먹었다고 판정했습니다.\""]], [["“And what kind of man,”", "\"그래 어떤 부류의 사람이었나요,\""], ["my Lady asks, “was this deplorable creature?”", "마님이 묻는다, \"그 가엾은 자는?\""]], [["“Very difficult to say,”", "\"말씀드리기가 무척 어렵습니다.\""], ["returns the lawyer, shaking his head.", "변호사가 고개를 저으며 대답한다."], ["“He had lived so wretchedly and was so neglected, with his gipsy colour and his wild black hair and beard, that I should have considered him the commonest of the common.", "\"어찌나 비참하게 살았고 어찌나 방치되었던지, 집시처럼 거뭇한 낯빛에 헝클어진 검은 머리와 수염을 한 꼴이, 천한 자들 중에서도 가장 천한 자로 여겼을 정도입니다."], ["The surgeon had a notion that he had once been something better, both in appearance and condition.”", "외과의는 그자가 한때는 겉모습으로 보나 처지로 보나 좀 더 나은 사람이었으리라는 견해를 내놓더군요.\""]], [["“What did they call the wretched being?”", "\"그 가엾은 자를 뭐라고 불렀나요?\""]], [["“They called him what he had called himself, but no one knew his name.”", "\"그자가 스스로 부르던 이름으로 불렀습니다만, 아무도 그자의 이름을 알지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Not even any one who had attended on him?”", "\"그자를 돌보던 사람조차도요?\""]], [["“No one had attended on him.", "\"그자를 돌본 사람은 아무도 없었습니다."], ["He was found dead.", "그자는 죽은 채 발견되었지요."], ["In fact, I found him.”", "사실, 제가 그자를 발견했습니다.\""]], [["“Without any clue to anything more?”", "\"더 알아낼 단서라곤 전혀 없었고요?\""]], [["“Without any; there was,”", "\"전혀요. 다만,\""], ["says the lawyer meditatively, “an old portmanteau, but—No, there were no papers.”", "변호사가 생각에 잠겨 말한다, \"낡은 여행 가방이 하나 있긴 했습니다만—아니요, 서류 같은 건 없었습니다.\""]], [["During the utterance of every word of this short dialogue, Lady Dedlock and Mr. Tulkinghorn, without any other alteration in their customary deportment, have looked very steadily at one another—as was natural, perhaps, in the discussion of so unusual a subject.", "이 짧은 대화의 한마디 한마디가 오가는 동안, 데드록 마님과 토킹혼 씨는 평소의 몸가짐에 다른 변화 하나 없이 서로를 무척 빤히 바라보았다—하긴 이토록 별난 주제를 논하다 보면 자연스러운 일이었으리라."], ["Sir Leicester has looked at the fire, with the general expression of the Dedlock on the staircase.", "레스터 경은 계단에 걸린 데드록 가문 사람의 그 표정 그대로, 난롯불을 바라보았다."], ["The story being told, he renews his stately protest, saying that as it is quite clear that no association in my Lady’s mind can possibly be traceable to this poor wretch (unless he was a begging-letter writer), he trusts to hear no more about a subject so far removed from my Lady’s station.", "이야기가 끝나자 그는 위엄 어린 항변을 거듭하며 말하길, 마님 마음속의 어떤 연상도 이 가엾은 작자와는 도저히 이어질 수 없음이 명백하니(그자가 구걸 편지나 써대던 자가 아니라면 말이지만), 마님의 신분과는 그토록 동떨어진 주제에 관해 더는 듣지 않기를 바란다고 한다."]], [["“Certainly, a collection of horrors,”", "\"확실히, 끔찍한 일들을 한데 모아놓은 셈이네요.\""], ["says my Lady, gathering up her mantles and furs, “but they interest one for the moment!", "마님이 망토와 모피를 추슬러 모으며 말한다. \"하지만 그 순간만큼은 흥미롭긴 하지요!"], ["Have the kindness, Mr. Tulkinghorn, to open the door for me.”", "토킹혼 씨, 미안하지만 제게 문 좀 열어 주세요.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn does so with deference and holds it open while she passes out.", "토킹혼 씨가 정중하게 문을 열고는 그녀가 지나가도록 잡아준다."], ["She passes close to him, with her usual fatigued manner and insolent grace.", "그녀는 늘 그러듯 지친 듯한 태도와 오만한 우아함을 지닌 채 그의 곁을 바싹 스쳐 지나간다."], ["They meet again at dinner—again, next day—again, for many days in succession.", "두 사람은 만찬에서 다시 마주친다—다음 날 또—여러 날 잇따라 또."], ["Lady Dedlock is always the same exhausted deity, surrounded by worshippers, and terribly liable to be bored to death, even while presiding at her own shrine.", "데드록 마님은 언제나 한결같이 지친 여신이니, 숭배자들에게 둘러싸여, 제 신전을 손수 주관하는 와중에도 지루해 죽을 지경에 무섭도록 곧잘 빠진다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn is always the same speechless repository of noble confidences, so oddly out of place and yet so perfectly at home.", "토킹혼 씨는 언제나 한결같이 고귀한 비밀을 담아두는 말없는 보관소이니, 어찌나 묘하게 어울리지 않으면서도 어찌나 완벽하게 제집처럼 편안한지."], ["They appear to take as little note of one another as any two people enclosed within the same walls could.", "두 사람은 같은 벽 안에 갇힌 어느 두 사람만큼이나 서로에게 거의 눈길도 주지 않는 듯하다."], ["But whether each evermore watches and suspects the other, evermore mistrustful of some great reservation; whether each is evermore prepared at all points for the other, and never to be taken unawares; what each would give to know how much the other knows—all this is hidden, for the time, in their own hearts. Esther’s Narrative", "하지만 둘이 영영 서로를 살피고 의심하는지, 영영 무슨 커다란 감춤이 있지 않을까 서로를 못 미더워하는지, 둘이 영영 어느 면에서나 서로를 맞상대할 채비를 갖춘 채 결코 허를 잡히지 않으려 하는지, 상대가 얼마나 많이 아는지를 알아내기 위해 둘 다 무엇이든 내놓으려 하는지—이 모든 것은, 당분간, 각자의 가슴속에 감춰져 있다."]], [["We held many consultations about what Richard was to be, first without Mr.", "리처드를 무엇으로 키울지를 두고 우리는 여러 차례 상의했습니다."], ["Jarndyce, as he had requested, and afterwards with him, but it was a long time before we seemed to make progress.", "처음에는 잔다이스 씨가 그러길 바라서 그분 없이 의논했고, 나중에는 그분과 함께 의논했지만, 좀처럼 진전이 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Richard said he was ready for anything.", "리처드는 무엇이든 좋다고 했습니다."], ["When Mr. Jarndyce doubted whether he might not already be too old to enter the Navy, Richard said he had thought of that, and perhaps he was.", "잔다이스 씨가 해군에 들어가기엔 이미 나이가 너무 많지 않겠느냐고 의문을 던지자, 리처드는 그 생각도 해봤다며 아마 그럴지도 모르겠다고 했습니다."], ["When Mr. Jarndyce asked him what he thought of the Army, Richard said he had thought of that, too, and it wasn’t a bad idea.", "잔다이스 씨가 육군은 어떻게 생각하느냐고 묻자, 리처드는 그것도 생각해봤는데 나쁜 생각은 아니라고 했습니다."], ["When Mr. Jarndyce advised him to try and decide within himself whether his old preference for the sea was an ordinary boyish inclination or a strong impulse, Richard answered, Well he really HAD tried very often, and he couldn’t make out.", "잔다이스 씨가 바다를 좋아하던 그 오랜 마음이 그저 흔한 소년다운 끌림인지 아니면 강한 충동인지 스스로 결정해보라고 권하자, 리처드는 정말이지 여러 번 애써봤는데 도무지 알 수가 없다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“How much of this indecision of character,” Mr.", "\"이렇게 우유부단한 성격이,\""], ["Jarndyce said to me, “is chargeable on that incomprehensible heap of uncertainty and procrastination on which he has been thrown from his birth, I don’t pretend to say; but that Chancery, among its other sins, is responsible for some of it, I can plainly see.", "잔다이스 씨가 나에게 말했습니다. \"그 애가 태어나면서부터 떠밀려온, 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 불확실과 미루기의 더미 탓이 얼마나 되는지는 내가 함부로 말하지 않겠네."], ["It has engendered or confirmed in him a habit of putting off—and trusting to this, that, and the other chance, without knowing what chance—and dismissing everything as unsettled, uncertain, and confused.", "하지만 대법원이 다른 숱한 죄악과 더불어 그 일부에 책임이 있다는 건 똑똑히 알 수 있어. 대법원이 그 애한테 미루는 버릇을 심어주었거나 굳혀준 거지."], ["The character of much older and steadier people may be even changed by the circumstances surrounding them.", "무슨 가망인지도 모르면서 이런저런 가망에 기대고, 모든 걸 미결, 불확실, 혼란으로 치워버리는 버릇 말일세."], ["It would be too much to expect that a boy’s, in its formation, should be the subject of such influences and escape them.”", "훨씬 나이 들고 한결 진중한 사람의 성격도 둘러싼 환경에 따라 바뀔 수 있어. 한창 자라나는 소년의 성격이 그런 영향에 노출되고도 거기서 벗어나기를 바라는 건 지나친 욕심이지.\""]], [["I felt this to be true; though if I may venture to mention what I thought besides, I thought it much to be regretted that Richard’s education had not counteracted those influences or directed his character.", "나는 이 말이 옳다고 느꼈습니다. 다만 내 생각을 감히 보태도 된다면, 리처드의 교육이 그런 영향을 막아내거나 그의 성격을 바로잡아주지 못한 것이 무척 안타까웠습니다."], ["He had been eight years at a public school and had learnt, I understood, to make Latin verses of several sorts in the most admirable manner.", "그는 팔 년 동안 퍼블릭 스쿨에 다니며, 내가 듣기로는 여러 종류의 라틴어 시를 더없이 훌륭하게 짓는 법을 배웠다고 합니다."], ["But I never heard that it had been anybody’s business to find out what his natural bent was, or where his failings lay, or to adapt any kind of knowledge to HIM.", "그러나 그의 타고난 성향이 무엇인지, 약점이 어디 있는지를 알아내거나, 어떤 종류의 지식이든 그에게 맞추는 일이 누군가의 책무였다는 말은 들어본 적이 없습니다."], ["HE had been adapted to the verses and had learnt the art of making them to such perfection that if he had remained at school until he was of age, I suppose he could only have gone on making them over and over again unless he had enlarged his education by forgetting how to do it.", "정작 그가 시에 맞춰졌고, 시 짓는 솜씨를 어찌나 완벽하게 익혔던지, 성년이 될 때까지 학교에 남아 있었다면 짓는 법을 까먹어서 교육의 폭을 넓히기라도 하지 않는 한, 그저 같은 시를 짓고 또 짓는 일밖에 못 했으리라 싶습니다."], ["Still, although I had no doubt that they were very beautiful, and very improving, and very sufficient for a great many purposes of life, and always remembered all through life, I did doubt whether Richard would not have profited by some one studying him a little, instead of his studying them quite so much.", "그래도 그 시들이 무척 아름답고, 무척 사람을 키워주고, 인생의 숱한 목적에 무척 쓸모가 있으며, 평생토록 늘 기억에 남으리라는 점은 의심하지 않았지만, 리처드가 시를 그토록 열심히 공부하는 대신 누군가가 그를 조금 공부해주었더라면 더 득을 보지 않았을까 하는 의심은 들었습니다."]], [["To be sure, I knew nothing of the subject and do not even now know whether the young gentlemen of classic Rome or Greece made verses to the same extent—or whether the young gentlemen of any country ever did.", "물론 나는 이 분야를 전혀 몰랐고, 고전 시대 로마나 그리스의 젊은 신사들이 똑같은 정도로 시를 지었는지, 아니 어느 나라의 젊은 신사들이라도 그런 적이 있었는지조차 지금도 모릅니다."]], [["“I haven’t the least idea,”", "\"내가 뭐가 되는 게 좋을지,\""], ["said Richard, musing, “what I had better be.", "리처드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했습니다."], ["Except that I am quite sure I don’t want to go into the Church, it’s a toss-up.”", "\"도무지 모르겠어요. 성직자가 되긴 정말 싫다는 것만 분명하고, 나머지는 동전 던지기예요.\""]], [["“You have no inclination in Mr. Kenge’s way?”", "\"켄지 씨 쪽으로는 마음이 안 가니?\""], ["suggested Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 넌지시 물었습니다."]], [["“I don’t know that, sir!”", "\"그건 모르겠어요, 어르신!\""], ["replied Richard.", "리처드가 대답했습니다."], ["“I am fond of boating.", "\"전 보트 타기를 좋아하잖아요."], ["Articled clerks go a good deal on the water.", "견습 서기들은 물 위에서 꽤 많이 지내니까요."], ["It’s a capital profession!”", "굉장한 직업이에요!\""]], [["“Surgeon—”", "\"외과의는—\""], ["suggested Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 넌지시 권했습니다."]], [["“That’s the thing, sir!”", "\"바로 그거예요, 어르신!\""], ["cried Richard.", "리처드가 외쳤습니다."]], [["I doubt if he had ever once thought of it before.", "나는 그가 그 전에 한 번이라도 그 생각을 해본 적이 있는지 의심스러웠습니다."]], [["“That’s the thing, sir,”", "\"바로 그거예요, 어르신.\""], ["repeated Richard with the greatest enthusiasm.", "리처드가 더없이 열을 올리며 거듭 말했습니다. \"드디어 찾았어요."], ["“We have got it at last. M.R.C.S.!”", "왕립외과의사회 회원이요!\""]], [["He was not to be laughed out of it, though he laughed at it heartily.", "그는 진심으로 껄껄 웃었지만, 웃음으로 그 마음을 떨쳐낼 수는 없었습니다."], ["He said he had chosen his profession, and the more he thought of it, the more he felt that his destiny was clear; the art of healing was the art of all others for him.", "그는 직업을 정했다고 했고, 생각하면 생각할수록 자신의 운명이 분명하게 느껴진다고 했습니다. 치유하는 기술이야말로 다른 무엇보다 자기에게 딱 맞는 기술이라는 것이었습니다."], ["Mistrusting that he only came to this conclusion because, having never had much chance of finding out for himself what he was fitted for and having never been guided to the discovery, he was taken by the newest idea and was glad to get rid of the trouble of consideration, I wondered whether the Latin verses often ended in this or whether Richard’s was a solitary case.", "그가 무엇에 맞는지 스스로 알아낼 기회가 거의 없었고, 그것을 발견하도록 이끌어준 적도 없었기에, 그저 가장 새로운 생각에 사로잡혀 고민의 수고를 덜게 된 것이 기뻐서 이런 결론에 이른 것은 아닐까 미심쩍었던 나는, 라틴어 시가 흔히 이렇게 끝나는 것인지, 아니면 리처드만 유독 그런 경우인지 궁금했습니다."]], [["Mr. Jarndyce took great pains to talk with him seriously and to put it to his good sense not to deceive himself in so important a matter.", "잔다이스 씨는 그와 진지하게 이야기를 나누고, 이렇게 중요한 일에서 스스로를 속이지 말라고 그의 분별심에 호소하느라 무척 애를 썼습니다."], ["Richard was a little grave after these interviews, but invariably told Ada and me that it was all right, and then began to talk about something else.", "리처드는 이런 대화를 마치고 나면 조금 심각해졌지만, 언제나 에이다와 나에게는 다 괜찮다고 말한 다음 곧 다른 이야기를 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“By heaven!” cried Mr.", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Boythorn, who interested himself strongly in the subject—though I need not say that, for he could do nothing weakly; “I rejoice to find a young gentleman of spirit and gallantry devoting himself to that noble profession!", "보이손 씨가 외쳤습니다. 그는 이 문제에 강하게 관심을 보였는데—하긴 굳이 말할 필요도 없는 것이, 그는 무엇이든 미적지근하게 하는 법이 없었으니까요."], ["The more spirit there is in it, the better for mankind and the worse for those mercenary task-masters and low tricksters who delight in putting that illustrious art at a disadvantage in the world.", "\"기개와 패기가 넘치는 젊은 신사가 그 고귀한 직업에 몸을 바치려 한다니 정말 기쁘구먼! 거기 기개가 넘칠수록 인류에게는 더 좋고, 그 빛나는 기술을 세상에서 불리한 처지로 몰아넣고 즐거워하는 그 돈만 밝히는 부역꾼들과 비열한 사기꾼들에게는 더 나쁜 일이지."], ["By all that is base and despicable,” cried Mr.", "비열하고 천박한 모든 것에 걸고 말하건대,\""], ["Boythorn, “the treatment of surgeons aboard ship is such that I would submit the legs—both legs—of every member of the Admiralty Board to a compound fracture and render it a transportable offence in any qualified practitioner to set them if the system were not wholly changed in eight and forty hours!”", "보이손 씨가 외쳤습니다. \"배 위에서 외과의를 다루는 꼴이라니, 해군성 위원회 위원이란 위원은 죄다 두 다리를—두 다리를 다—복합 골절로 만들어버린 다음, 마흔여덟 시간 안에 그 제도를 통째로 바꾸지 않는 한, 자격 있는 의사가 그 다리를 맞추기만 해도 유배형에 처할 죄로 삼고 싶을 지경이라네!\""]], [["“Wouldn’t you give them a week?”", "\"일주일은 주지 않겠소?\""], ["asked Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 물었습니다."]], [["“No!” cried Mr.", "\"안 되네!\""], ["Boythorn firmly.", "보이손 씨가 단호하게 외쳤습니다. \"무슨 일이 있어도 안 돼!"], ["“Not on any consideration!", "마흔여덟 시간이야! 자치체니 교구니 교구 위원회니, 그 비슷하게 모여서 그런 연설이나 주고받는 멍청한 얼간이 무리로 말하자면, 맙소사, 그자들은 그 비참한 삶의 짧은 여생을 수은 광산에서 일하게 해야 마땅하지."], ["Eight and forty hours!", "그자들의 역겨운 영어가 해 아래서 쓰이는 언어를 오염시키지 못하게 하려고만 해도 그래야 하네. 그런 작자들로 말하자면, 지식을 좇는 신사들의 열정을 비열하게 이용해먹고는, 그 인생의 가장 좋은 시절과 오랜 학업과 값비싼 교육이 베푼 헤아릴 수 없는 봉사에 대한 보답으로, 서기조차 받아들이지 않을 만큼 쥐꼬리만 한 보수를 쥐여주는 자들이지."], ["As to corporations, parishes, vestry-boards, and similar gatherings of jolter-headed clods who assemble to exchange such speeches that, by heaven, they ought to be worked in quicksilver mines for the short remainder of their miserable existence, if it were only to prevent their detestable English from contaminating a language spoken in the presence of the sun—as to those fellows, who meanly take advantage of the ardour of gentlemen in the pursuit of knowledge to recompense the inestimable services of the best years of their lives, their long study, and their expensive education with pittances too small for the acceptance of clerks, I would have the necks of every one of them wrung and their skulls arranged in Surgeons’ Hall for the contemplation of the whole profession in order that its younger members might understand from actual measurement, in early life, HOW thick skulls may become!”", "그런 놈은 하나하나 목을 비틀어버리고, 그 두개골을 외과의사 회관에 진열해서 온 의료계가 들여다보게 하고 싶네. 젊은 회원들이 어려서부터 실제로 재어보고, 두개골이 얼마나 두꺼워질 수 있는지를 깨닫도록 말일세!\""]], [["He wound up this vehement declaration by looking round upon us with a most agreeable smile and suddenly thundering, “Ha, ha, ha!”", "그는 이 격렬한 선언을 마무리하면서, 더없이 유쾌한 미소로 우리를 둘러보더니, 느닷없이 \"하, 하, 하!\""], ["over and over again, until anybody else might have been expected to be quite subdued by the exertion.", "하고 천둥처럼 거듭 웃어젖혔습니다. 다른 사람 같았으면 그 힘 빼는 일에 완전히 기진맥진했을 만큼 말입니다."]], [["As Richard still continued to say that he was fixed in his choice after repeated periods for consideration had been recommended by Mr. Jarndyce and had expired, and he still continued to assure Ada and me in the same final manner that it was “all right,” it became advisable to take Mr. Kenge into council. Mr. Kenge, therefore, came down to dinner one day, and leaned back in his chair, and turned his eye-glasses over and over, and spoke in a sonorous voice, and did exactly what I remembered to have seen him do when I was a little girl.", "잔다이스 씨가 권한 거듭된 숙고의 기간이 지나고도 리처드가 여전히 선택을 굳혔다고 말하고, 여전히 에이다와 나에게 똑같이 단호한 태도로 \"다 괜찮다\"고 장담하자, 켄지 씨를 불러 의논하는 편이 좋겠다 싶었습니다. 그리하여 어느 날 켄지 씨가 만찬에 내려와, 의자에 기대앉아, 안경을 빙글빙글 돌리고, 낭랑한 목소리로 말했는데, 내가 어린 소녀였을 때 보았던 모습과 한 치도 다르지 않았습니다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Yes. Well!", "\"그렇지요."], ["A very good profession, Mr.", "그렇고말고요!"], ["Jarndyce, a very good profession.”", "아주 훌륭한 직업입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 아주 훌륭한 직업이지요.\""]], [["“The course of study and preparation requires to be diligently pursued,”", "\"공부와 준비 과정을 부지런히 밟아야 하지요.\""], ["observed my guardian with a glance at Richard.", "후견인이 리처드를 흘긋 보며 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, no doubt,”", "\"오, 그야 두말할 것 없지요.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Diligently.”", "\"부지런히요.\""]], [["“But that being the case, more or less, with all pursuits that are worth much,” said Mr.", "\"하지만 그건 정도의 차이는 있어도 제법 값어치 있는 모든 일에 다 해당하니,\""], ["Jarndyce, “it is not a special consideration which another choice would be likely to escape.”", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"다른 선택을 했다고 해서 피해 갈 만한 특별한 사정은 아니지요.\""]], [["“Truly,” said Mr.", "\"참으로 그렇습니다.\""], ["Kenge. “And Mr.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Richard Carstone, who has so meritoriously acquitted himself in the—shall I say the classic shades?—in which his youth had been passed, will, no doubt, apply the habits, if not the principles and practice, of versification in that tongue in which a poet was said (unless I mistake) to be born, not made, to the more eminently practical field of action on which he enters.”", "\"또 리처드 카스톤 군은—그 고전의 그늘이라고 할까요?—젊은 시절을 보낸 그곳에서 그토록 훌륭하게 처신했으니, 시인은 (내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면) 만들어지는 것이 아니라 태어나는 것이라고들 하는 그 언어로 시를 짓던 습관을, 원리와 실제까지는 아니더라도, 이제 발을 들이려는 그 한층 두드러지게 실용적인 행동의 장에 틀림없이 적용할 겁니다.\""]], [["“You may rely upon it,”", "\"믿으셔도 됩니다.\""], ["said Richard in his off-hand manner, “that I shall go at it and do my best.”", "리처드가 거침없는 태도로 말했습니다. \"제가 달려들어서 최선을 다할 거예요.\""]], [["“Very well, Mr. Jarndyce!”", "\"아주 좋습니다, 잔다이스 씨!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge, gently nodding his head.", "켄지 씨가 부드럽게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했습니다."], ["“Really, when we are assured by Mr. Richard that he means to go at it and to do his best,”", "\"정말이지, 리처드 군이 달려들어서 최선을 다하겠다고 우리에게 장담하니,\""], ["nodding feelingly and smoothly over those expressions, “I would submit to you that we have only to inquire into the best mode of carrying out the object of his ambition.", "그 표현들을 음미하듯 부드럽게 고개를 끄덕이면서. \"이제 우리는 그의 포부를 이룰 가장 좋은 방법만 알아보면 되겠다고 말씀드리고 싶습니다."], ["Now, with reference to placing Mr. Richard with some sufficiently eminent practitioner.", "자, 리처드 군을 충분히 저명한 어느 의사에게 맡기는 일에 관해서요."], ["Is there any one in view at present?”", "지금 염두에 둔 분이 있으신지요?\""]], [["“No one, Rick, I think?”", "\"없는 것 같은데, 릭?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“No one, sir,”", "\"없어요, 어르신.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."]], [["“Quite so!”", "\"바로 그렇군요!\""], ["observed Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“As to situation, now.", "\"그럼 장소에 관해서는 어떨까요."], ["Is there any particular feeling on that head?”", "그 점에 무슨 특별한 바람이 있으신지요?\""]], [["“N—no,”", "\"아—아니요.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."]], [["“Quite so!”", "\"바로 그렇군요!\""], ["observed Mr. Kenge again.", "켄지 씨가 다시 말했습니다."]], [["“I should like a little variety,”", "\"전 변화가 좀 있었으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Richard; “I mean a good range of experience.”", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"그러니까, 폭넓은 경험을 쌓고 싶다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Very requisite, no doubt,”", "\"두말할 것 없이 꼭 필요한 일이지요.\""], ["returned Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 응답했습니다."], ["“I think this may be easily arranged, Mr. Jarndyce?", "\"이건 쉽게 마련될 것 같습니다, 잔다이스 씨?"], ["We have only, in the first place, to discover a sufficiently eligible practitioner; and as soon as we make our want—and shall I add, our ability to pay a premium?—known, our only difficulty will be in the selection of one from a large number.", "우선 충분히 적합한 의사를 찾기만 하면 됩니다. 우리의 바람을—거기다 사례금을 낼 능력까지 있다고 덧붙일까요?—알리는 즉시, 우리의 유일한 어려움은 수많은 의사 가운데 하나를 고르는 일이 되겠지요."], ["We have only, in the second place, to observe those little formalities which are rendered necessary by our time of life and our being under the guardianship of the court.", "둘째로는, 우리의 나이와 법원의 후견을 받는 처지 때문에 필요해진 그 자질구레한 절차들만 지키면 됩니다."], ["We shall soon be—shall I say, in Mr. Richard’s own light-hearted manner, ‘going at it’—to our heart’s content.", "그러면 머잖아—리처드 군 특유의 명랑한 말투를 빌려 '달려든다'고 할까요—마음껏 일을 추진하게 될 겁니다."], ["It is a coincidence,” said Mr.", "묘한 우연이지만,\""], ["Kenge with a tinge of melancholy in his smile, “one of those coincidences which may or may not require an explanation beyond our present limited faculties, that I have a cousin in the medical profession.", "켄지 씨가 미소에 한 줄기 우수를 띠며 말했습니다. \"우리의 현재 한정된 능력으로는 설명이 필요할 수도, 필요 없을 수도 있는 그런 우연 가운데 하나인데요, 마침 제 사촌 하나가 의료계에 몸담고 있습니다."], ["He might be deemed eligible by you and might be disposed to respond to this proposal.", "그분이라면 보시기에 적합할 수도 있고, 이 제안에 응할 마음이 있을 수도 있겠지요."], ["I can answer for him as little as for you, but he MIGHT!”", "어르신을 두고 장담 못 하듯 그분을 두고도 장담은 못 하지만, 어쩌면 그럴 수도 있습니다!\""]], [["As this was an opening in the prospect, it was arranged that Mr. Kenge should see his cousin.", "이것이 앞날에 트인 길이었기에, 켄지 씨가 자기 사촌을 만나보기로 정해졌습니다."], ["And as Mr. Jarndyce had before proposed to take us to London for a few weeks, it was settled next day that we should make our visit at once and combine Richard’s business with it.", "그리고 잔다이스 씨가 이미 우리를 몇 주 동안 런던에 데려가겠다고 했던 터라, 이튿날 우리는 곧장 그곳을 방문하면서 리처드의 일도 함께 처리하기로 정했습니다."]], [["Mr. Boythorn leaving us within a week, we took up our abode at a cheerful lodging near Oxford Street over an upholsterer’s shop.", "보이손 씨가 일주일 안에 우리 곁을 떠났고, 우리는 옥스퍼드 거리 근처, 가구상 위층의 쾌적한 하숙에 거처를 잡았습니다."], ["London was a great wonder to us, and we were out for hours and hours at a time, seeing the sights, which appeared to be less capable of exhaustion than we were.", "런던은 우리에게 커다란 경이였습니다. 우리는 몇 시간이고 거리를 쏘다니며 구경거리를 보았는데, 그 구경거리는 우리보다 동이 날 줄을 모르는 듯했습니다."], ["We made the round of the principal theatres, too, with great delight, and saw all the plays that were worth seeing.", "우리는 큰 극장들도 더없이 즐겁게 두루 돌며 볼 만한 연극은 죄다 보았습니다."], ["I mention this because it was at the theatre that I began to be made uncomfortable again by Mr. Guppy.", "이 이야기를 꺼내는 까닭은, 바로 그 극장에서 다시금 거피 씨 때문에 거북해지기 시작했기 때문입니다."]], [["I was sitting in front of the box one night with Ada, and Richard was in the place he liked best, behind Ada’s chair, when, happening to look down into the pit, I saw Mr.", "어느 날 밤 나는 에이다와 함께 특별석 앞쪽에 앉아 있었고, 리처드는 자기가 가장 좋아하는 자리, 에이다의 의자 뒤에 있었는데, 마침 일층 평석을 내려다보다가 거피 씨를 발견했습니다."], ["Guppy, with his hair flattened down upon his head and woe depicted in his face, looking up at me.", "그는 머리카락을 머리에 납작하게 붙이고, 얼굴에는 비탄을 가득 담은 채 나를 올려다보고 있었습니다."], ["I felt all through the performance that he never looked at the actors but constantly looked at me, and always with a carefully prepared expression of the deepest misery and the profoundest dejection.", "공연 내내 나는 그가 배우들은 한 번도 보지 않고 줄곧 나만 바라본다는 것을 느꼈습니다. 그것도 언제나 더없이 깊은 비참과 가장 심오한 낙담을 정성껏 빚어낸 표정으로 말입니다."]], [["It quite spoiled my pleasure for that night because it was so very embarrassing and so very ridiculous.", "그날 밤 내 즐거움은 완전히 망가지고 말았습니다. 너무도 거북하고 너무도 우스꽝스러웠으니까요."], ["But from that time forth, we never went to the play without my seeing Mr.", "하지만 그때부터 우리는 연극을 보러 갈 때마다 반드시 일층 평석에서 거피 씨를 보았습니다."], ["Guppy in the pit, always with his hair straight and flat, his shirt-collar turned down, and a general feebleness about him.", "그는 언제나 머리카락을 곧게 펴 납작하게 붙이고, 셔츠 깃을 접어 내리고, 어딘가 전반적으로 기운 없는 모습이었습니다."], ["If he were not there when we went in, and I began to hope he would not come and yielded myself for a little while to the interest of the scene, I was certain to encounter his languishing eyes when I least expected it and, from that time, to be quite sure that they were fixed upon me all the evening.", "우리가 들어갈 때 그가 거기 없으면, 나는 그가 오지 않기를 바라기 시작해 잠시나마 무대의 재미에 빠져들었지만, 그러다가 가장 뜻밖의 순간에 영락없이 그 애처로운 눈길과 마주쳤고, 그때부터는 저녁 내내 그 눈길이 나에게 고정되어 있다는 것을 확신하게 되었습니다."]], [["I really cannot express how uneasy this made me.", "이것이 나를 얼마나 불편하게 했는지 정말이지 이루 말할 수 없습니다."], ["If he would only have brushed up his hair or turned up his collar, it would have been bad enough; but to know that that absurd figure was always gazing at me, and always in that demonstrative state of despondency, put such a constraint upon me that I did not like to laugh at the play, or to cry at it, or to move, or to speak.", "그가 머리카락이라도 빗어 세우거나 깃이라도 올렸다면 그것만으로도 충분히 끔찍했을 텐데, 그 우스꽝스러운 모습이 늘 나를 응시하고 있다는 것을, 그것도 언제나 그렇게 노골적인 낙담의 상태로 그러고 있다는 것을 안다는 사실이 어찌나 나를 옥죄던지, 나는 연극을 보며 웃지도, 울지도, 움직이지도, 말하지도 못했습니다."], ["I seemed able to do nothing naturally. As to escaping Mr.", "무엇 하나 자연스럽게 할 수가 없는 것 같았습니다."], ["Guppy by going to the back of the box, I could not bear to do that because I knew Richard and Ada relied on having me next them and that they could never have talked together so happily if anybody else had been in my place.", "특별석 뒤쪽으로 가서 거피 씨를 피한다는 것은, 도저히 그럴 수 없었습니다. 리처드와 에이다가 나를 자기들 옆에 두고 싶어 한다는 것을, 그리고 내 자리에 다른 누가 있었다면 그 둘이 그토록 즐겁게 이야기를 나누지 못했으리라는 것을 알았으니까요."], ["So there I sat, not knowing where to look—for wherever I looked, I knew Mr.", "그래서 나는 어디를 봐야 할지 모른 채 그 자리에 앉아 있었습니다."], ["Guppy’s eyes were following me—and thinking of the dreadful expense to which this young man was putting himself on my account.", "어디를 보든 거피 씨의 눈이 나를 좇고 있다는 것을 알았으니까요. 그러면서 이 젊은이가 나 때문에 치르고 있는 끔찍한 비용을 생각했습니다."]], [["Sometimes I thought of telling Mr. Jarndyce.", "이따금 잔다이스 씨에게 말할까 하는 생각도 들었습니다."], ["Then I feared that the young man would lose his situation and that I might ruin him.", "그러다가는 그 젊은이가 일자리를 잃을지도 모르고, 그러면 내가 그를 망치게 될까 봐 두려웠습니다."], ["Sometimes I thought of confiding in Richard, but was deterred by the possibility of his fighting Mr. Guppy and giving him black eyes.", "이따금 리처드에게 털어놓을까 하는 생각도 들었지만, 그가 거피 씨와 싸워 눈에 멍이라도 들게 할 가능성 때문에 그만두었습니다."], ["Sometimes I thought, should I frown at him or shake my head.", "이따금 그에게 눈살을 찌푸리거나 고개를 저어볼까 생각도 했습니다."], ["Then I felt I could not do it.", "그러다가는 그럴 수 없다는 것을 깨달았습니다."], ["Sometimes I considered whether I should write to his mother, but that ended in my being convinced that to open a correspondence would be to make the matter worse.", "이따금 그의 어머니에게 편지를 써볼까 따져보기도 했지만, 그것은 편지 왕래를 트는 일이 문제를 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이라는 확신으로 끝나고 말았습니다."], ["I always came to the conclusion, finally, that I could do nothing. Mr.", "나는 끝내 늘 아무것도 할 수 없다는 결론에 이르렀습니다."], ["Guppy’s perseverance, all this time, not only produced him regularly at any theatre to which we went, but caused him to appear in the crowd as we were coming out, and even to get up behind our fly—where I am sure I saw him, two or three times, struggling among the most dreadful spikes.", "이러는 내내 거피 씨의 끈기는 우리가 가는 어느 극장에든 그를 어김없이 나타나게 했을 뿐 아니라, 우리가 나올 때면 인파 속에 모습을 드러내게 했고, 심지어 우리 마차 뒤에 올라타게까지 했습니다. 거기서 나는 그가 그 무시무시한 쇠창살들 사이에서 두세 번 버둥거리는 것을 분명히 보았습니다."], ["After we got home, he haunted a post opposite our house.", "우리가 집에 돌아온 뒤에는 그가 우리 집 맞은편 기둥 곁을 맴돌았습니다."], ["The upholsterer’s where we lodged being at the corner of two streets, and my bedroom window being opposite the post, I was afraid to go near the window when I went upstairs, lest I should see him (as I did one moonlight night) leaning against the post and evidently catching cold.", "우리가 묵던 가구상이 두 거리가 만나는 모퉁이에 있었고, 내 침실 창문이 그 기둥 맞은편이었던 탓에, 나는 위층에 올라갈 때면 창가에 다가가기가 두려웠습니다. (어느 달밤에 실제로 보았듯이) 그가 기둥에 기댄 채 뻔히 감기에 걸려가는 모습을 보게 될까 봐서요."], ["If Mr. Guppy had not been, fortunately for me, engaged in the daytime, I really should have had no rest from him.", "다행히 거피 씨가 낮에는 일에 매여 있지 않았더라면, 정말이지 나는 그에게서 한순간도 쉴 틈이 없었을 겁니다."]], [["While we were making this round of gaieties, in which Mr. Guppy so extraordinarily participated, the business which had helped to bring us to town was not neglected. Mr. Kenge’s cousin was a Mr. Bayham Badger, who had a good practice at Chelsea and attended a large public institution besides. He was quite willing to receive Richard into his house and to superintend his studies, and as it seemed that those could be pursued advantageously under Mr. Badger’s roof, and Mr. Badger liked Richard, and as Richard said he liked Mr. Badger “well enough,” an agreement was made, the Lord Chancellor’s consent was obtained, and it was all settled.", "거피 씨가 그토록 유별나게 끼어든 이 흥겨운 나들이를 이어가는 동안에도, 우리를 런던으로 데려오는 데 한몫한 그 일은 소홀히 하지 않았습니다. 켄지 씨의 사촌은 베이엄 배저 씨로, 첼시에서 진료를 잘하고 있었고 그 밖에도 큰 공공 기관에 출근하고 있었습니다. 그는 리처드를 집에 받아들여 학업을 지도하는 데 기꺼이 응했고, 그 학업을 배저 씨의 집에서 유익하게 밟을 수 있을 듯했으며, 배저 씨는 리처드를 마음에 들어 했고, 리처드도 배저 씨가 \"그런대로 마음에 든다\"고 해서, 합의가 이루어지고 대법원장의 동의도 얻어, 모든 것이 정해졌습니다."]], [["On the day when matters were concluded between Richard and Mr. Badger, we were all under engagement to dine at Mr.", "리처드와 배저 씨 사이의 일이 마무리된 날, 우리는 모두 배저 씨 댁에서 만찬을 들기로 약속이 되어 있었습니다."], ["Badger’s house. We were to be “merely a family party,”", "배저 부인의 쪽지에 적힌 대로 우리는 \"그저 가족끼리의 모임\"이 될 참이었고, 거기 모인 부인이라곤 배저 부인 본인뿐이었습니다."], ["Mrs. Badger’s note said; and we found no lady there but Mrs.", "그녀는 응접실에서 갖가지 물건에 둘러싸여 있었는데, 그것들은 그녀가 그림을 조금 그리고, 피아노를 조금 치고, 기타를 조금 치고, 하프를 조금 타고, 노래를 조금 부르고, 바느질을 조금 하고, 책을 조금 읽고, 시를 조금 쓰고, 식물 채집을 조금 한다는 것을 말해주고 있었습니다."], ["Badger herself. She was surrounded in the drawing-room by various objects, indicative of her painting a little, playing the piano a little, playing the guitar a little, playing the harp a little, singing a little, working a little, reading a little, writing poetry a little, and botanizing a little.", "그녀는 내 짐작에 쉰 살쯤 된 부인으로, 젊게 차려입었고 안색이 무척 고왔습니다."], ["She was a lady of about fifty, I should think, youthfully dressed, and of a very fine complexion. If I add to the little list of her accomplishments that she rouged a little, I do not mean that there was any harm in it.", "그녀의 자질구레한 재주 목록에 연지를 조금 바른다는 것까지 보탠다 해도, 거기에 무슨 흠이 있다는 뜻은 아닙니다."]], [["Mr. Bayham Badger himself was a pink, fresh-faced, crisp-looking gentleman with a weak voice, white teeth, light hair, and surprised eyes, some years younger, I should say, than Mrs.", "베이엄 배저 씨 본인은 혈색이 발그레하고 얼굴이 싱싱하며 산뜻한 인상의 신사로, 목소리는 가냘프고, 치아는 희고, 머리카락은 옅고, 눈은 놀란 듯했는데, 내 짐작에 베이엄 배저 부인보다 몇 살은 아래였습니다."], ["Bayham Badger. He admired her exceedingly, but principally, and to begin with, on the curious ground (as it seemed to us) of her having had three husbands.", "그는 그녀를 대단히 흠모했지만, 그 주된 까닭은, 그리고 무엇보다 첫째 까닭은, (우리가 보기엔) 그녀가 남편을 셋이나 두었다는 묘한 점이었습니다."], ["We had barely taken our seats when he said to Mr.", "우리가 자리에 앉기가 무섭게 그는 자못 의기양양하게 잔다이스 씨에게 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce quite triumphantly, “You would hardly suppose that I am Mrs. Bayham Badger’s third!”", "\"제가 베이엄 배저 부인의 세 번째 남편이라곤 좀처럼 짐작 못 하실 겁니다!\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그렇습니까?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Her third!” said Mr.", "\"세 번째입니다!\""], ["Badger. “Mrs.", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Bayham Badger has not the appearance, Miss Summerson, of a lady who has had two former husbands?” I said “Not at all!”", "\"베이엄 배저 부인은 전남편을 둘이나 두었던 부인 같아 보이지 않지요, 서머슨 양?\""]], [["“And most remarkable men!”", "나는 \"전혀 그렇지 않네요!\" 하고 말했습니다."], ["said Mr. Badger in a tone of confidence.", "\"게다가 더없이 비범한 인물들이었습니다!\""], ["“Captain Swosser of the Royal Navy, who was Mrs.", "배저 씨가 은밀한 어조로 말했습니다."], ["Badger’s first husband, was a very distinguished officer indeed.", "\"배저 부인의 첫 남편이었던 왕립 해군의 스워서 함장은 참으로 걸출한 장교였지요."], ["The name of Professor Dingo, my immediate predecessor, is one of European reputation.”", "제 바로 전임자인 딩고 교수의 이름은 유럽 전역에 명성이 자자합니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Badger overheard him and smiled.", "배저 부인이 그 말을 듣고 미소 지었습니다."]], [["“Yes, my dear!” Mr.", "\"그래요, 여보!\""], ["Badger replied to the smile, “I was observing to Mr.", "배저 씨가 그 미소에 응답했습니다."], ["Jarndyce and Miss Summerson that you had had two former husbands—both very distinguished men.", "\"당신이 전남편을 둘이나—둘 다 더없이 걸출한 인물을—두었다고 잔다이스 씨와 서머슨 양께 말씀드리던 참이오."], ["And they found it, as people generally do, difficult to believe.”", "그런데 두 분도 사람들이 으레 그러듯 믿기 어려워하시더군.\""]], [["“I was barely twenty,” said Mrs.", "\"제가 왕립 해군의 스워서 함장과 결혼했을 때는 겨우 스무 살이었어요.\""], ["Badger, “when I married Captain Swosser of the Royal Navy.", "배저 부인이 말했습니다."], ["I was in the Mediterranean with him; I am quite a sailor.", "\"그이와 함께 지중해에 있었으니, 저는 영락없는 뱃사람이지요."], ["On the twelfth anniversary of my wedding-day, I became the wife of Professor Dingo.”", "결혼 열두 돌이 되던 날, 저는 딩고 교수의 아내가 되었어요.\""]], [["“Of European reputation,” added Mr.", "\"유럽 전역에 명성이 자자한 분이지요.\""], ["Badger in an undertone.", "배저 씨가 나직이 덧붙였습니다."]], [["“And when Mr. Badger and myself were married,”", "\"그리고 배저 씨와 제가 결혼했을 때도,\""], ["pursued Mrs.", "배저 부인이 말을 이었습니다."], ["Badger, “we were married on the same day of the year.", "\"한 해 중 같은 날에 했답니다."], ["I had become attached to the day.”", "제가 그 날짜에 정이 들었거든요.\""]], [["“So that Mrs. Badger has been married to three husbands—two of them highly distinguished men,”", "\"그러니까 배저 부인은 남편을 셋 두었고—그중 둘은 더없이 걸출한 인물이었으며,\""], ["said Mr.", "배저 씨가 사실들을 정리하며 말했습니다."], ["Badger, summing up the facts, “and each time upon the twenty-first of March at eleven in the forenoon!”", "\"그것도 매번 삼월 이십일일 오전 열한 시에 결혼한 셈이지요!\""]], [["We all expressed our admiration.", "우리는 모두 감탄을 표했습니다."]], [["“But for Mr.", "\"배저 씨의 겸손함만 아니라면,\""], ["Badger’s modesty,” said Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce, “I would take leave to correct him and say three distinguished men.”", "\"외람되지만 그분 말씀을 바로잡아 걸출한 인물이 셋이라고 하고 싶군요.\""]], [["“Thank you, Mr. Jarndyce!", "\"고맙습니다, 잔다이스 씨!"], ["What I always tell him!”", "제가 늘 그이한테 하는 말이에요!\""], ["observed Mrs. Badger.", "배저 부인이 말했습니다."]], [["“And, my dear,”", "\"그런데 여보,\""], ["said Mr. Badger, “what do I always tell you?", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["That without any affectation of disparaging such professional distinction as I may have attained (which our friend Mr. Carstone will have many opportunities of estimating), I am not so weak—no, really,”", "\"내가 당신한테 늘 하는 말이 뭐요?"], ["said Mr. Badger to us generally, “so unreasonable—as to put my reputation on the same footing with such first-rate men as Captain Swosser and Professor Dingo.", "내가 이룬 의사로서의 명성이 (이건 우리 카스톤 군이 앞으로 가늠해볼 기회가 숱하게 있을 테고) 어떻든, 그걸 깎아내리는 척하려는 게 아니라, 나는 내 명성을 스워서 함장이나 딩고 교수 같은 일류 인물들과 같은 반열에 올려놓을 만큼 그렇게 어리석지—아니, 정말이지,\""], ["Perhaps you may be interested, Mr.", "배저 씨가 우리 모두를 향해 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce,” continued Mr.", "\"그렇게 분별없는 사람은 아니라는 거지요."], ["Bayham Badger, leading the way into the next drawing-room, “in this portrait of Captain Swosser.", "어쩌면 흥미로우실지도 모르겠습니다, 잔다이스 씨.\" 베이엄 배저 씨가 옆 응접실로 앞장서 들어가며 말을 이었습니다."], ["It was taken on his return home from the African station, where he had suffered from the fever of the country.", "\"이게 스워서 함장의 초상화입니다. 아프리카 주둔지에서 그곳 열병에 시달리다 귀국했을 때 그린 것이지요."], ["Mrs. Badger considers it too yellow.", "배저 부인은 너무 누렇다고 합니다만, 참으로 훌륭한 두상이에요."], ["But it’s a very fine head. A very fine head!”", "참으로 훌륭한 두상이지요!\""]], [["We all echoed, “A very fine head!”", "우리는 모두 따라 외쳤습니다. \"참으로 훌륭한 두상이네요!\""]], [["“I feel when I look at it,” said Mr.", "\"이걸 볼 때마다 이런 느낌이 듭니다.\""], ["Badger, “‘that’s a man I should like to have seen!’ It strikingly bespeaks the first-class man that Captain Swosser pre-eminently was.", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["On the other side, Professor Dingo.", "\"'이 사람은 내가 직접 만나봤으면 싶은 인물이야!'"], ["I knew him well—attended him in his last illness—a speaking likeness!", "스워서 함장이 단연 일류 인물이었다는 것을 인상 깊게 말해주지요. 반대편은 딩고 교수입니다."], ["Over the piano, Mrs. Bayham Badger when Mrs.", "제가 잘 알았지요—그분 임종 때 진료도 했고요—꼭 닮았지요!"], ["Swosser. Over the sofa, Mrs.", "피아노 위쪽은 스워서 부인이었을 적의 베이엄 배저 부인입니다."], ["Bayham Badger when Mrs. Dingo.", "소파 위쪽은 딩고 부인이었을 적의 베이엄 배저 부인이고요."], ["Of Mrs. Bayham Badger IN ESSE, I possess the original and have no copy.”", "현재의 베이엄 배저 부인으로 말하면, 제가 원본을 소장하고 있으니 모사본은 없습니다.\""]], [["Dinner was now announced, and we went downstairs.", "이제 만찬이 차려졌다는 전갈이 와서 우리는 아래층으로 내려갔습니다."], ["It was a very genteel entertainment, very handsomely served.", "무척 품격 있는 자리였고, 차림새도 무척 훌륭했습니다."], ["But the captain and the professor still ran in Mr. Badger’s head, and as Ada and I had the honour of being under his particular care, we had the full benefit of them.", "그러나 함장과 교수는 여전히 배저 씨의 머릿속을 떠나지 않았고, 에이다와 나는 그의 각별한 보살핌을 받는 영광을 누린 터라, 그 두 사람의 덕을 톡톡히 보았습니다."]], [["“Water, Miss Summerson?", "\"물 드시겠습니까, 서머슨 양?"], ["Allow me!", "제가 따라드리지요!"], ["Not in that tumbler, pray.", "그 잔에는 말고요, 부디."], ["Bring me the professor’s goblet, James!”", "제임스, 교수의 술잔을 가져오게!\""]], [["Ada very much admired some artificial flowers under a glass.", "에이다가 유리갓 아래 놓인 조화 몇 송이를 무척 감탄하며 보았습니다."]], [["“Astonishing how they keep!” said Mr.", "\"이게 어찌나 잘 보존되었는지 놀랍지요!\""], ["Badger. “They were presented to Mrs.", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Bayham Badger when she was in the Mediterranean.”", "\"베이엄 배저 부인이 지중해에 있을 때 선물받은 거랍니다.\""]], [["He invited Mr. Jarndyce to take a glass of claret.", "그는 잔다이스 씨에게 클라레 한 잔을 들기를 권했습니다."]], [["“Not that claret!”", "\"그 클라레 말고요!\""], ["he said. “Excuse me!", "그가 말했습니다."], ["This is an occasion, and ON an occasion I produce some very special claret I happen to have.", "\"죄송합니다만! 오늘은 특별한 날이고, 특별한 날에는 제가 마침 가지고 있는 아주 특별한 클라레를 내놓지요."], ["(James, Captain Swosser’s wine!) Mr. Jarndyce, this is a wine that was imported by the captain, we will not say how many years ago.", "(제임스, 스워서 함장의 포도주를!) 잔다이스 씨, 이건 함장이 들여온 포도주인데, 몇 년 전인지는 굳이 말하지 않겠습니다."], ["You will find it very curious.", "무척 진귀하다 여기실 겁니다."], ["My dear, I shall be happy to take some of this wine with you.", "여보, 당신과 이 포도주를 함께 들 수 있다니 기쁘구려."], ["(Captain Swosser’s claret to your mistress, James!) My love, your health!”", "(제임스, 스워서 함장의 클라레를 마님께!) 여보, 당신의 건강을 위하여!\""]], [["After dinner, when we ladies retired, we took Mrs. Badger’s first and second husband with us.", "만찬이 끝나고 우리 부인네들이 물러날 때, 우리는 배저 부인의 첫 남편과 둘째 남편을 함께 데리고 갔습니다."], ["Mrs. Badger gave us in the drawing-room a biographical sketch of the life and services of Captain Swosser before his marriage and a more minute account of him dating from the time when he fell in love with her at a ball on board the Crippler, given to the officers of that ship when she lay in Plymouth Harbour.", "배저 부인은 응접실에서 스워서 함장의 결혼 전 생애와 복무에 관한 전기적 개관을, 이어서 그가 플리머스 항에 정박해 있던 크리플러호 장교들을 위해 그 배 위에서 열린 무도회에서 자기에게 반한 때부터의 한층 자세한 이야기를 우리에게 들려주었습니다."]], [["“The dear old Crippler!”", "\"그리운 옛 크리플러호!\""], ["said Mrs. Badger, shaking her head.", "배저 부인이 고개를 저으며 말했습니다."], ["“She was a noble vessel.", "\"고귀한 배였지요."], ["Trim, ship-shape, all a taunto, as Captain Swosser used to say.", "단정하고, 말끔하고, 스워서 함장 말마따나 더없이 빈틈없었어요."], ["You must excuse me if I occasionally introduce a nautical expression; I was quite a sailor once.", "제가 가끔 항해 용어를 끼워 넣더라도 양해해주세요. 한때는 영락없는 뱃사람이었으니까요."], ["Captain Swosser loved that craft for my sake.", "스워서 함장은 저 때문에 그 배를 사랑했답니다."], ["When she was no longer in commission, he frequently said that if he were rich enough to buy her old hulk, he would have an inscription let into the timbers of the quarter-deck where we stood as partners in the dance to mark the spot where he fell—raked fore and aft (Captain Swosser used to say) by the fire from my tops.", "그 배가 더는 현역으로 쓰이지 않게 되었을 때, 그이는 자기가 그 낡은 선체를 살 만큼 부자라면, 우리가 춤 짝으로 서 있던 후갑판 목재에 글귀를 새겨 넣어 자기가 쓰러진 자리를 표시하겠다고 자주 말하곤 했어요—제 돛대 끝에서 쏟아진 포화에 뱃머리부터 고물까지 휩쓸려서 말이지요(스워서 함장의 표현이었어요)."], ["It was his naval way of mentioning my eyes.”", "그건 제 두 눈을 두고 그이가 해군식으로 한 말이었답니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Badger shook her head, sighed, and looked in the glass.", "배저 부인은 고개를 젓고, 한숨을 쉬고, 거울을 들여다보았습니다."]], [["“It was a great change from Captain Swosser to Professor Dingo,”", "\"스워서 함장에서 딩고 교수로 바뀐 것은 엄청난 변화였어요.\""], ["she resumed with a plaintive smile.", "그녀가 애잔한 미소를 지으며 말을 이었습니다."], ["“I felt it a good deal at first.", "\"처음엔 무척 실감이 났지요."], ["Such an entire revolution in my mode of life!", "제 생활 방식이 통째로 뒤바뀌었으니까요!"], ["But custom, combined with science—particularly science—inured me to it.", "하지만 습관에 학문이—특히 학문이—더해지니 거기에 익숙해지더군요."], ["Being the professor’s sole companion in his botanical excursions, I almost forgot that I had ever been afloat, and became quite learned.", "교수님의 식물 채집 여행에 유일한 동반자로 따라다니다 보니, 제가 한때 배를 탔다는 사실조차 거의 잊어버리고, 제법 박식해졌어요."], ["It is singular that the professor was the antipodes of Captain Swosser and that Mr. Badger is not in the least like either!”", "묘하게도 그 교수님은 스워서 함장과 정반대였고, 배저 씨는 또 그 둘 중 누구와도 조금도 닮지 않았지요!\""]], [["We then passed into a narrative of the deaths of Captain Swosser and Professor Dingo, both of whom seem to have had very bad complaints.", "이어서 우리는 스워서 함장과 딩고 교수의 죽음에 관한 이야기로 넘어갔는데, 두 사람 다 아주 고약한 병을 앓았던 모양이었습니다."], ["In the course of it, Mrs. Badger signified to us that she had never madly loved but once and that the object of that wild affection, never to be recalled in its fresh enthusiasm, was Captain Swosser.", "그 이야기 도중 배저 부인은 자기가 미친 듯이 사랑한 것은 평생 단 한 번뿐이었으며, 그 싱그러운 열정으로는 결코 되돌릴 수 없는 그 격렬한 애정의 대상이 바로 스워서 함장이었다고 우리에게 일러주었습니다."], ["The professor was yet dying by inches in the most dismal manner, and Mrs. Badger was giving us imitations of his way of saying, with great difficulty, “Where is Laura?", "교수는 더없이 처량한 모습으로 한 치 한 치 죽어가고 있던 참이었고, 배저 부인이 그가 몹시 힘겹게 \"로라는 어디 있나?"], ["Let Laura give me my toast and water!”", "로라가 내 토스트와 물을 가져오게 해!\""], ["when the entrance of the gentlemen consigned him to the tomb.", "하고 말하던 흉내를 우리에게 내 보이고 있는데, 마침 신사들이 들어오는 바람에 그를 무덤으로 보내고 말았습니다."]], [["Now, I observed that evening, as I had observed for some days past, that Ada and Richard were more than ever attached to each other’s society, which was but natural, seeing that they were going to be separated so soon.", "그런데 나는 그날 저녁, 지난 며칠 동안 그래왔듯이, 에이다와 리처드가 그 어느 때보다 서로 함께 있기를 좋아한다는 것을 알아챘습니다. 머잖아 헤어질 처지였으니 그것은 당연한 일이었습니다."], ["I was therefore not very much surprised when we got home, and Ada and I retired upstairs, to find Ada more silent than usual, though I was not quite prepared for her coming into my arms and beginning to speak to me, with her face hidden.", "그래서 집에 돌아와 에이다와 내가 위층으로 물러났을 때, 에이다가 평소보다 말이 없는 것을 보고도 나는 그리 놀라지 않았습니다. 다만 그녀가 내 품에 안겨 얼굴을 묻은 채 말을 꺼내기 시작하리라고는 미처 예상하지 못했습니다."]], [["“My darling Esther!”", "\"사랑하는 에스더!\""], ["murmured Ada.", "에이다가 속삭였습니다."], ["“I have a great secret to tell you!”", "\"너한테 들려줄 굉장한 비밀이 있어!\""]], [["A mighty secret, my pretty one, no doubt!", "엄청난 비밀이겠지, 우리 귀여운 아이야, 두말할 것 없이!"]], [["“What is it, Ada?”", "\"그게 뭔데, 에이다?\""]], [["“Oh, Esther, you would never guess!”", "\"오, 에스더, 넌 절대 못 맞힐 거야!\""]], [["“Shall I try to guess?”", "\"내가 한번 맞혀볼까?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, no! Don’t!", "\"오, 안 돼!"], ["Pray don’t!”", "그러지 마!"], ["cried Ada, very much startled by the idea of my doing so.", "제발 그러지 마!\" 에이다가 내가 그러겠다는 생각에 몹시 놀라 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Now, I wonder who it can be about?”", "\"자, 누구에 관한 걸까?\""], ["said I, pretending to consider.", "내가 곰곰이 생각하는 척하며 말했습니다."]], [["“It’s about—”", "\"그건 바로—\""], ["said Ada in a whisper.", "에이다가 속삭였습니다."], ["“It’s about—my cousin Richard!”", "\"그건 바로—내 사촌 리처드에 관한 거야!\""]], [["“Well, my own!”", "\"그래, 우리 아가!\""], ["said I, kissing her bright hair, which was all I could see.", "나는 그녀의 빛나는 머리카락에 입을 맞추며 말했습니다. 그것이 내가 볼 수 있는 전부였으니까요."], ["“And what about him?”", "\"그 사람이 어쨌는데?\""]], [["“Oh, Esther, you would never guess!”", "\"오, 에스더, 넌 절대 못 맞힐 거야!\""]], [["It was so pretty to have her clinging to me in that way, hiding her face, and to know that she was not crying in sorrow but in a little glow of joy, and pride, and hope, that I would not help her just yet.", "그녀가 그렇게 나에게 매달려 얼굴을 묻고 있는 모습이 어찌나 사랑스럽던지, 또 그녀가 슬퍼서가 아니라 기쁨과 자랑과 희망의 작은 설렘으로 우는 줄을 알기에, 나는 아직 그녀를 도와주지 않았습니다."]], [["“He says—I know it’s very foolish, we are both so young—but he says,”", "\"그이가 그러는데—나도 알아, 너무 어리석다는 거, 우린 둘 다 너무 어리니까—하지만 그이가 그러는데,\""], ["with a burst of tears, “that he loves me dearly, Esther.”", "그녀가 눈물을 왈칵 쏟으며 말했습니다. \"날 무척 사랑한대, 에스더.\""]], [["“Does he indeed?” said I.", "\"정말 그래?\""], ["“I never heard of such a thing!", "내가 말했습니다. \"그런 얘긴 처음 듣는걸!"], ["Why, my pet of pets, I could have told you that weeks and weeks ago!”", "아니, 우리 아가 중의 아가야, 그건 내가 몇 주, 아니 몇 주도 더 전에 너한테 말해줄 수 있었어!\""]], [["To see Ada lift up her flushed face in joyful surprise, and hold me round the neck, and laugh, and cry, and blush, was so pleasant!", "에이다가 기쁜 놀라움에 발그레한 얼굴을 들고, 내 목을 끌어안고, 웃고, 울고, 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 보니 어찌나 흐뭇하던지요!"]], [["“Why, my darling,”", "\"아니, 우리 아가,\""], ["said I, “what a goose you must take me for!", "내가 말했습니다. \"넌 나를 어지간한 바보로 여겼나 보구나!"], ["Your cousin Richard has been loving you as plainly as he could for I don’t know how long!”", "네 사촌 리처드는 언제부터인지도 모를 만큼 오래도록 너를 더없이 빤히 사랑해왔는데!\""]], [["“And yet you never said a word about it!”", "\"그런데도 넌 한마디도 안 했잖아!\""], ["cried Ada, kissing me.", "에이다가 나에게 입을 맞추며 외쳤습니다."]], [["“No, my love,”", "\"그래, 우리 아가.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“I waited to be told.”", "\"난 네가 말해주기를 기다린 거야.\""]], [["“But now I have told you, you don’t think it wrong of me, do you?”", "\"하지만 이제 내가 말했으니, 내가 잘못했다곤 생각 안 하지, 그렇지?\""], ["returned Ada.", "에이다가 응답했습니다."], ["She might have coaxed me to say no if I had been the hardest-hearted duenna in the world.", "내가 세상에서 가장 모진 보호자였다 해도 그녀는 나를 어르고 달래어 아니라는 말을 끌어낼 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["Not being that yet, I said no very freely.", "아직 그런 사람은 아니었기에, 나는 거리낌 없이 아니라고 했습니다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"자 이제,\""], ["said I, “I know the worst of it.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"최악의 사정까지 다 알았네.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s not quite the worst of it, Esther dear!”", "\"오, 그게 아주 최악은 아니야, 에스더!\""], ["cried Ada, holding me tighter and laying down her face again upon my breast.", "에이다가 나를 더 꼭 끌어안고 다시 내 가슴에 얼굴을 묻으며 외쳤습니다."]], [["“No?”", "\"아니라고?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“Not even that?”", "\"그것마저도 아니란 말이야?\""]], [["“No, not even that!”", "\"응, 그것마저도 아니야!\""], ["said Ada, shaking her head.", "에이다가 고개를 저으며 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, you never mean to say—”", "\"아니, 설마 네 말은—\""], ["I was beginning in joke.", "내가 농담조로 말을 꺼냈습니다."]], [["But Ada, looking up and smiling through her tears, cried, “Yes, I do!", "그러나 에이다는 눈물 너머로 고개를 들어 미소 지으며 외쳤습니다. \"응, 맞아!"], ["You know, you know I do!”", "알잖아, 너도 알잖아!\""], ["And then sobbed out, “With all my heart I do!", "그러고는 흐느끼며 토해냈습니다. \"온 마음을 다해 그래!"], ["With all my whole heart, Esther!”", "내 온 마음을 다해서, 에스더!\""]], [["I told her, laughing, why I had known that, too, just as well as I had known the other!", "나는 웃으며 그녀에게 말해주었습니다. 그것 역시 다른 사실만큼이나 똑같이 진작에 알고 있었던 까닭을요!"], ["And we sat before the fire, and I had all the talking to myself for a little while (though there was not much of it); and Ada was soon quiet and happy.", "우리는 난롯가에 앉았고, 한동안 이야기는 나 혼자 도맡았습니다(그래봐야 많지도 않았지만). 에이다는 이내 차분해지고 행복해졌습니다."]], [["“Do you think my cousin John knows, dear Dame Durden?”", "\"내 사촌 존도 알고 계실까, 더든 부인?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었습니다."]], [["“Unless my cousin John is blind, my pet,”", "\"내 사촌 존이 눈먼 사람이 아닌 다음에야, 우리 아가.\""], ["said I, “I should think my cousin John knows pretty well as much as we know.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"내 사촌 존은 우리가 아는 만큼은 잘 알고 계실 거야.\""]], [["“We want to speak to him before Richard goes,”", "\"리처드가 떠나기 전에 그분께 말씀드리고 싶어.\""], ["said Ada timidly, “and we wanted you to advise us, and to tell him so.", "에이다가 머뭇거리며 말했습니다. \"그래서 네가 우리한테 조언도 해주고, 그분께 그렇게 전해주었으면 했어."], ["Perhaps you wouldn’t mind Richard’s coming in, Dame Durden?”", "혹시 리처드가 들어와도 괜찮을까, 더든 부인?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Richard is outside, is he, my dear?”", "리처드가 밖에 있구나, 그렇지, 우리 아가?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“I am not quite certain,”", "\"잘은 모르겠지만,\" 에이다가 수줍은 순진함으로 응답했습니다."], ["returned Ada with a bashful simplicity that would have won my heart if she had not won it long before, “but I think he’s waiting at the door.”", "진작에 내 마음을 사로잡지 않았더라면 바로 그 모습에 사로잡혔을 만한 순진함이었지요. \"문간에서 기다리고 있는 것 같아.\""]], [["There he was, of course.", "물론 그는 거기 있었습니다."], ["They brought a chair on either side of me, and put me between them, and really seemed to have fallen in love with me instead of one another, they were so confiding, and so trustful, and so fond of me.", "두 사람은 내 양옆에 의자를 가져다 놓고 나를 가운데 앉히더니, 정말이지 서로를 사랑하는 대신 나에게 반한 것처럼, 어찌나 나를 믿고 의지하며 좋아하던지요."], ["They went on in their own wild way for a little while—I never stopped them; I enjoyed it too much myself—and then we gradually fell to considering how young they were, and how there must be a lapse of several years before this early love could come to anything, and how it could come to happiness only if it were real and lasting and inspired them with a steady resolution to do their duty to each other, with constancy, fortitude, and perseverance, each always for the other’s sake.", "두 사람은 한동안 자기들 식으로 마음껏 들떠 떠들었고—나는 그들을 막지 않았습니다. 나도 그것이 너무도 즐거웠으니까요—그러다가 우리는 차츰 그들이 얼마나 어린지, 이 이른 사랑이 무언가로 결실을 맺으려면 몇 해는 흘러야 한다는 것, 그리고 그것이 참되고 변함없으며, 서로를 위해 변함없음과 꿋꿋함과 끈기로 각자의 도리를 다하려는 굳건한 결심을 그들에게 불어넣을 때에야 비로소 행복으로 이어질 수 있다는 것을 헤아리게 되었습니다."], ["Well!", "그래요!"], ["Richard said that he would work his fingers to the bone for Ada, and Ada said that she would work her fingers to the bone for Richard, and they called me all sorts of endearing and sensible names, and we sat there, advising and talking, half the night.", "리처드는 에이다를 위해 손가락이 뼈만 남도록 일하겠다고 했고, 에이다는 리처드를 위해 손가락이 뼈만 남도록 일하겠다고 했으며, 두 사람은 나를 온갖 다정하고 분별 있는 이름으로 불렀습니다. 우리는 거기 앉아 조언하고 이야기하며 밤의 절반을 보냈습니다."], ["Finally, before we parted, I gave them my promise to speak to their cousin John to-morrow.", "마침내 헤어지기 전에, 나는 내일 그들의 사촌 존에게 말씀드리겠다고 두 사람에게 약속했습니다."]], [["So, when to-morrow came, I went to my guardian after breakfast, in the room that was our town-substitute for the growlery, and told him that I had it in trust to tell him something.", "그리하여 이튿날이 되자, 나는 아침을 먹고 후견인에게 갔습니다. 우리의 시골 투덜방을 대신하는 방이었지요. 거기서 나는 그분께 전해드릴 일을 부탁받았다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Well, little woman,”", "\"그래, 우리 작은 부인,\""], ["said he, shutting up his book, “if you have accepted the trust, there can be no harm in it.”", "그분이 책을 덮으며 말했습니다. \"자네가 그 부탁을 맡았다면, 거기에 나쁜 일이 있을 리 없겠지.\""]], [["“I hope not, guardian,” said I.", "\"그러길 바라요, 후견인님.\""], ["“I can guarantee that there is no secrecy in it.", "내가 말했습니다. \"거기엔 아무 비밀도 없다고 장담할 수 있어요."], ["For it only happened yesterday.”", "어제서야 일어난 일이거든요.\""]], [["“Aye?", "\"그래?"], ["And what is it, Esther?”", "그래서 무슨 일인가, 에스더?\""]], [["“Guardian,”", "\"후견인님,\""], ["said I, “you remember the happy night when first we came down to Bleak House?", "내가 말했습니다. \"우리가 처음 황폐한 집으로 내려가던 그 행복한 밤을 기억하세요?"], ["When Ada was singing in the dark room?”", "에이다가 어두운 방에서 노래를 부르던 때요?\""]], [["I wished to call to his remembrance the look he had given me then.", "나는 그분이 그때 나에게 던졌던 그 눈빛을 떠올리게 하고 싶었습니다."], ["Unless I am much mistaken, I saw that I did so.", "내가 크게 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 그렇게 했다는 것을 알아챌 수 있었습니다."]], [["“Because—”", "\"그게요—\""], ["said I with a little hesitation.", "내가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, my dear!”", "\"그래, 우리 아가!\""], ["said he.", "그분이 말했습니다."], ["“Don’t hurry.”", "\"서두르지 말게.\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"그게요,\""], ["said I, “Ada and Richard have fallen in love.", "내가 말했습니다. \"에이다와 리처드가 서로 사랑에 빠졌어요."], ["And have told each other so.”", "그리고 서로 그렇다고 말했고요.\""]], [["“Already!”", "\"벌써!\""], ["cried my guardian, quite astonished.", "후견인이 깜짝 놀라 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“And to tell you the truth, guardian, I rather expected it.”", "\"그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 후견인님, 저는 이미 그럴 줄 짐작하고 있었어요.\""]], [["“The deuce you did!”", "\"세상에, 자네가 말인가!\""], ["said he.", "그분이 말했습니다."]], [["He sat considering for a minute or two, with his smile, at once so handsome and so kind, upon his changing face, and then requested me to let them know that he wished to see them.", "그분은 변해가는 얼굴에 그토록 잘생기고도 그토록 다정한 미소를 띤 채, 잠시 일이 분 동안 곰곰이 생각하며 앉아 있다가, 두 사람을 보고 싶다는 뜻을 전하라고 나에게 일렀습니다."], ["When they came, he encircled Ada with one arm in his fatherly way and addressed himself to Richard with a cheerful gravity.", "두 사람이 오자, 그분은 아버지처럼 한쪽 팔로 에이다를 감싸 안고는 명랑하면서도 진중한 태도로 리처드에게 말했습니다."]], [["“Rick,”", "\"릭,\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce, “I am glad to have won your confidence.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["I hope to preserve it.", "\"자네 신뢰를 얻었다니 기쁘네."], ["When I contemplated these relations between us four which have so brightened my life and so invested it with new interests and pleasures, I certainly did contemplate, afar off, the possibility of you and your pretty cousin here (don’t be shy, Ada, don’t be shy, my dear!) being in a mind to go through life together.", "그 신뢰를 계속 지켜가길 바라네. 내 삶을 그토록 환하게 밝히고, 새로운 흥미와 즐거움으로 채워준 우리 네 사람 사이의 이 관계를 떠올려볼 때, 나는 분명 저 멀리서나마, 자네와 여기 자네의 어여쁜 사촌이 (수줍어 말게, 에이다, 수줍어 말게, 우리 아가!) 평생을 함께 가려는 마음을 품을 가능성을 떠올렸다네."], ["I saw, and do see, many reasons to make it desirable.", "그게 바람직하다고 여길 만한 이유를 여럿 보았고, 지금도 보고 있어."], ["But that was afar off, Rick, afar off!”", "하지만 그건 저 멀리 있는 일이었네, 릭, 저 멀리!\""]], [["“We look afar off, sir,”", "\"저희도 저 멀리를 바라봅니다, 어르신.\""], ["returned Richard.", "리처드가 응답했습니다."]], [["“Well!” said Mr.", "\"좋아!\""], ["Jarndyce. “That’s rational.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Now, hear me, my dears!", "\"그건 이치에 맞는 말이지."], ["I might tell you that you don’t know your own minds yet, that a thousand things may happen to divert you from one another, that it is well this chain of flowers you have taken up is very easily broken, or it might become a chain of lead.", "자, 내 말을 들어보게, 우리 아이들! 자네들은 아직 제 마음을 모른다고, 자네들을 서로에게서 갈라놓을 숱한 일이 일어날 수 있다고, 자네들이 집어 든 이 꽃사슬이 아주 쉽게 끊어진다는 게 다행이라고, 안 그러면 그게 납사슬이 될 수도 있다고 말할 수도 있겠지."], ["But I will not do that.", "하지만 난 그러지 않겠네."], ["Such wisdom will come soon enough, I dare say, if it is to come at all.", "그런 지혜는, 정 올 것이라면 머잖아 충분히 오겠지."], ["I will assume that a few years hence you will be in your hearts to one another what you are to-day.", "나는 몇 해 뒤에도 자네들이 마음속으로 서로에게 오늘과 똑같으리라 여기겠네."], ["All I say before speaking to you according to that assumption is, if you DO change—if you DO come to find that you are more commonplace cousins to each other as man and woman than you were as boy and girl (your manhood will excuse me, Rick!)—don’t be ashamed still to confide in me, for there will be nothing monstrous or uncommon in it.", "그 가정에 따라 자네들에게 말하기 전에 한마디만 하자면, 만약 자네들이 정말로 변한다면—소년 소녀였을 때보다 어른 남녀로서 서로에게 더 평범한 사촌일 뿐임을 정말로 알게 된다면 (자네의 성년을 걸고 양해하게, 릭!)—그래도 부끄러워 말고 나에게 털어놓게. 거기엔 끔찍하거나 별난 구석이 전혀 없을 테니까."], ["I am only your friend and distant kinsman.", "나는 그저 자네들의 친구이자 먼 친척일 뿐이야."], ["I have no power over you whatever.", "자네들에게 아무런 권한도 없어."], ["But I wish and hope to retain your confidence if I do nothing to forfeit it.”", "다만 내가 그것을 잃을 짓을 하지 않는 한, 자네들의 신뢰를 계속 지니길 바라고 또 희망하네.\""]], [["“I am very sure, sir,”", "\"확신합니다, 어르신.\""], ["returned Richard, “that I speak for Ada too when I say that you have the strongest power over us both—rooted in respect, gratitude, and affection—strengthening every day.”", "리처드가 응답했습니다. \"어르신께서 저희 두 사람 모두에게 가장 강한 힘을 지니셨다고—존경과 감사와 애정에 뿌리내려 날마다 깊어가는 힘을 지니셨다고 말씀드릴 때, 에이다를 대신해서도 말씀드리는 거예요.\""]], [["“Dear cousin John,”", "\"사랑하는 사촌 존.\""], ["said Ada, on his shoulder, “my father’s place can never be empty again.", "에이다가 그분의 어깨에 기대어 말했습니다. \"아버지의 자리가 다시는 빈자리가 되지 않을 거예요."], ["All the love and duty I could ever have rendered to him is transferred to you.”", "제가 아버지께 드릴 수 있었던 그 모든 사랑과 도리를 이제 사촌께 옮겨드릴게요.\""]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Now for our assumption.", "\"이제 우리의 가정으로 돌아가지."], ["Now we lift our eyes up and look hopefully at the distance!", "자, 눈을 들어 희망을 품고 저 멀리를 바라보세!"], ["Rick, the world is before you; and it is most probable that as you enter it, so it will receive you.", "릭, 세상은 자네 앞에 펼쳐져 있어. 그리고 십중팔구 자네가 세상에 들어서는 대로 세상도 자네를 맞아줄 걸세."], ["Trust in nothing but in Providence and your own efforts.", "섭리와 자네 자신의 노력 말고는 아무것도 믿지 말게."], ["Never separate the two, like the heathen waggoner.", "그 둘을 결코 갈라놓지 말게나, 저 이교도 마부처럼."], ["Constancy in love is a good thing, but it means nothing, and is nothing, without constancy in every kind of effort.", "사랑에서의 변함없음은 좋은 것이지만, 온갖 노력에서의 변함없음이 없으면 그건 아무 의미도 없고 아무것도 아니야."], ["If you had the abilities of all the great men, past and present, you could do nothing well without sincerely meaning it and setting about it.", "자네가 고금의 위인들이 지닌 온갖 재능을 다 가졌다 해도, 진심으로 마음먹고 착수하지 않으면 무엇 하나 제대로 해낼 수 없네."], ["If you entertain the supposition that any real success, in great things or in small, ever was or could be, ever will or can be, wrested from Fortune by fits and starts, leave that wrong idea here or leave your cousin Ada here.”", "크든 작든 어떤 참된 성공이, 들쭉날쭉 변덕으로 운명에게서 비틀어 얻어진 적이 있었거나 있을 수 있다고, 또 앞으로 그러할 수 있거나 그럴 것이라고 여긴다면, 그 그릇된 생각을 여기 두고 가든지, 아니면 자네 사촌 에이다를 여기 두고 가게.\""]], [["“I will leave IT here, sir,”", "\"그건 여기 두고 가겠습니다, 어르신.\""], ["replied Richard smiling, “if I brought it here just now (but I hope I did not), and will work my way on to my cousin Ada in the hopeful distance.”", "리처드가 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"혹 제가 방금 그걸 여기 들여왔다면 말이지요 (그러지 않았기를 바랍니다만), 그리고 일해 나아가 저 희망에 찬 먼 곳의 사촌 에이다에게 이르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Right!”", "\"옳거니!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“If you are not to make her happy, why should you pursue her?”", "\"그녀를 행복하게 해줄 게 아니라면, 어찌 그녀를 좇겠는가?\""]], [["“I wouldn’t make her unhappy—no, not even for her love,”", "\"전 그녀를 불행하게 만들지 않겠어요—아니요, 그녀의 사랑을 얻기 위해서라도 그러지 않겠어요.\""], ["retorted Richard proudly.", "리처드가 당당하게 응수했습니다."]], [["“Well said!”", "\"잘 말했네!\""], ["cried Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 외쳤습니다."], ["“That’s well said!", "\"참 잘 말했어!"], ["She remains here, in her home with me.", "에이다는 여기, 나와 함께 제 집에 남을 거야."], ["Love her, Rick, in your active life, no less than in her home when you revisit it, and all will go well.", "그녀를 사랑하게, 릭, 자네가 돌아와 찾는 이 집에서만큼이나 자네의 분주한 삶 속에서도 사랑하게. 그러면 모든 게 잘될 거야."], ["Otherwise, all will go ill.", "그러지 않으면 모든 게 잘못될 테고."], ["That’s the end of my preaching.", "이걸로 내 설교는 끝일세."], ["I think you and Ada had better take a walk.”", "자네와 에이다는 산책이나 좀 다녀오는 게 좋겠어.\""]], [["Ada tenderly embraced him, and Richard heartily shook hands with him, and then the cousins went out of the room, looking back again directly, though, to say that they would wait for me.", "에이다가 그분을 다정하게 끌어안았고, 리처드는 그분과 진심으로 악수를 나누었습니다. 그러고 나서 두 사촌은 방을 나섰는데, 곧장 뒤돌아보며 나를 기다리겠다고 말했습니다."]], [["The door stood open, and we both followed them with our eyes as they passed down the adjoining room, on which the sun was shining, and out at its farther end.", "문이 열려 있어서, 우리 두 사람은 그들이 햇빛이 비쳐드는 옆방을 지나 그 저편 끝으로 나갈 때까지 눈으로 좇았습니다."], ["Richard with his head bent, and her hand drawn through his arm, was talking to her very earnestly; and she looked up in his face, listening, and seemed to see nothing else.", "리처드는 고개를 숙인 채, 그녀의 손을 자기 팔에 끼우고 그녀에게 무척 진지하게 이야기하고 있었습니다. 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 귀를 기울였고, 다른 것은 아무것도 보이지 않는 듯했습니다."], ["So young, so beautiful, so full of hope and promise, they went on lightly through the sunlight as their own happy thoughts might then be traversing the years to come and making them all years of brightness.", "그토록 젊고, 그토록 아름답고, 그토록 희망과 약속으로 가득 찬 두 사람은 햇살 속을 가뿐히 걸어갔습니다. 그들의 행복한 생각이 그때 다가올 세월을 가로질러 그 모든 세월을 환한 빛의 나날로 만들고 있는 것처럼 말이지요."], ["So they passed away into the shadow and were gone.", "그렇게 두 사람은 그늘 속으로 사라져 보이지 않게 되었습니다."], ["It was only a burst of light that had been so radiant.", "그토록 눈부셨던 것은 한 줄기 빛이 터져 나온 것뿐이었습니다."], ["The room darkened as they went out, and the sun was clouded over.", "그들이 나가자 방은 어두워졌고, 해는 구름에 가렸습니다."]], [["“Am I right, Esther?”", "\"내가 옳은가, 에스더?\""], ["said my guardian when they were gone.", "두 사람이 가버린 뒤 후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["He was so good and wise to ask ME whether he was right!", "그분은 어찌나 선하고 지혜로운지, 자기가 옳은지를 나에게 다 묻다니요!"]], [["“Rick may gain, out of this, the quality he wants.", "\"릭은 이번 일로 자기에게 모자란 자질을 얻을지도 몰라."], ["Wants, at the core of so much that is good!” said Mr.", "그토록 좋은 점이 많은데도, 그 핵심에 모자란 자질을 말일세!\""], ["Jarndyce, shaking his head.", "잔다이스 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했습니다."], ["“I have said nothing to Ada, Esther.", "\"나는 에이다에게는 아무 말도 하지 않았네, 에스더."], ["She has her friend and counsellor always near.”", "그 아이에겐 벗이자 조언자가 언제나 곁에 있으니.\""], ["And he laid his hand lovingly upon my head.", "그러고는 사랑스럽게 손을 내 머리에 얹었습니다."]], [["I could not help showing that I was a little moved, though I did all I could to conceal it.", "나는 감추려고 온갖 애를 썼지만, 조금 마음이 움직였다는 것을 드러내지 않을 수 없었습니다."]], [["“Tut tut!” said he.", "\"쯧쯧!\""], ["“But we must take care, too, that our little woman’s life is not all consumed in care for others.”", "그분이 말했습니다. \"하지만 우리 작은 부인의 삶이 온통 남을 보살피는 데만 쓰이지 않도록 우리도 조심해야 해.\""]], [["“Care?", "\"보살핀다니요?"], ["My dear guardian, I believe I am the happiest creature in the world!”", "사랑하는 후견인님, 저는 제가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람이라고 믿는걸요!\""]], [["“I believe so, too,”", "\"나도 그렇게 믿네.\""], ["said he.", "그분이 말했습니다."], ["“But some one may find out what Esther never will—that the little woman is to be held in remembrance above all other people!”", "\"하지만 누군가는 에스더가 결코 깨닫지 못할 것을 알아낼 걸세—이 작은 부인이야말로 다른 그 누구보다 더 깊이 기억할 사람이라는 것을 말이야!\""]], [["I have omitted to mention in its place that there was some one else at the family dinner party.", "마땅한 자리에서 언급하지 못한 것이 있는데, 그 가족 만찬 자리에 또 한 사람이 있었습니다."], ["It was not a lady.", "부인은 아니었습니다."], ["It was a gentleman.", "신사였지요."], ["It was a gentleman of a dark complexion—a young surgeon.", "피부가 가무잡잡한 신사로—젊은 외과의였습니다."], ["He was rather reserved, but I thought him very sensible and agreeable.", "그는 다소 말수가 적었지만, 나는 그가 무척 분별 있고 호감 가는 사람이라고 생각했습니다."], ["At least, Ada asked me if I did not, and I said yes.", "적어도 에이다가 나에게 그렇게 생각하지 않느냐고 물었고, 나는 그렇다고 했습니다."]], [["Deportment\nRichard left us on the very next evening to begin his new career, and committed Ada to my charge with great love for her and great trust in me.", "리처드는 바로 다음 날 저녁에 새로운 앞날을 시작하러 우리를 떠나면서, 에이다를 향한 깊은 사랑과 나를 향한 깊은 신뢰를 담아 그녀를 내게 맡겼습니다."], ["It touched me then to reflect, and it touches me now, more nearly, to remember (having what I have to tell) how they both thought of me, even at that engrossing time.", "그토록 정신없이 바쁜 와중에도 두 사람이 나를 그렇게 생각해 주었다는 것을, 그때도 떠올리면 가슴이 뭉클했지만 지금 (앞으로 들려드릴 이야기를 안고 있는 까닭에) 다시 떠올리면 더욱 절실히 가슴에 사무칩니다."], ["I was a part of all their plans, for the present and the future.", "나는 두 사람의 모든 계획에, 현재와 미래의 모든 계획에 한 자리를 차지하고 있었습니다."], ["I was to write Richard once a week, making my faithful report of Ada, who was to write to him every alternate day.", "나는 일주일에 한 번씩 리처드에게 편지를 써서 에이다의 소식을 충실히 알려 주기로 했고, 에이다는 하루걸러 한 번씩 그에게 편지를 쓰기로 했습니다."], ["I was to be informed, under his own hand, of all his labours and successes; I was to observe how resolute and persevering he would be; I was to be Ada’s bridesmaid when they were married; I was to live with them afterwards; I was to keep all the keys of their house; I was to be made happy for ever and a day.", "그가 직접 쓴 편지로 그의 온갖 노고와 성공을 전해 듣기로 했으며, 그가 얼마나 굳세고 끈기 있게 해 나가는지 지켜보기로 했고, 두 사람이 결혼하면 에이다의 신부 들러리가 되고, 그 후에는 둘과 함께 살며, 그들 집의 열쇠를 모두 맡고, 영원히, 그리고 하루 더, 행복하게 지내기로 했습니다."]], [["“And if the suit SHOULD make us rich, Esther—which it may, you know!” said Richard to crown all.", "\"게다가 만에 하나 그 소송이 우리를 부자로 만들어 주기라도 하면 말이야, 에스더 — 그럴 수도 있잖아, 안 그래!\""], ["A shade crossed Ada’s face.", "리처드가 모든 말을 마무리 짓듯 말했습니다."]], [["“My dearest Ada,” asked Richard, “why not?”", "에이다의 얼굴에 그늘이 스쳐 지나갔습니다."]], [["“It had better declare us poor at once,”", "\"내 사랑 에이다,\" 리처드가 물었습니다."], ["said Ada.", "\"왜 안 된다는 거야?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"차라리 당장 우리를 가난뱅이라고 선고해 주는 편이 나아요.\""], ["I don’t know about that,”", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"오!"], ["returned Richard, “but at all events, it won’t declare anything at once.", "그건 잘 모르겠지만,\" 리처드가 대꾸했습니다."], ["It hasn’t declared anything in heaven knows how many years.”", "\"어쨌든 당장 무엇 하나 선고해 주진 않을 거야. 도대체 몇 년째 아무것도 선고하지 못하고 있는지 하느님만 아실 노릇이니까.\""]], [["“Too true,”", "\"정말 그렇죠.\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, but,”", "\"그래, 하지만,\""], ["urged Richard, answering what her look suggested rather than her words, “the longer it goes on, dear cousin, the nearer it must be to a settlement one way or other.", "리처드는 그녀의 말보다 그녀의 표정이 암시하는 바에 대답하듯 다그쳤습니다. \"사촌, 오래 끌면 끌수록 이렇든 저렇든 어떻게든 결판에 가까워질 수밖에 없잖아."], ["Now, is not that reasonable?”", "자, 그게 이치에 맞지 않아?\""]], [["“You know best, Richard.", "\"당신이 제일 잘 알겠죠, 리처드."], ["But I am afraid if we trust to it, it will make us unhappy.”", "하지만 우리가 거기 기대를 걸면 그게 우릴 불행하게 만들까 봐 두려워요.\""]], [["“But, my Ada, we are not going to trust to it!”", "\"그렇지만, 내 에이다, 우린 거기 기대를 걸지 않을 거야!\""], ["cried Richard gaily.", "리처드가 명랑하게 외쳤습니다."], ["“We know it better than to trust to it.", "\"거기 기대를 걸 만큼 모르지 않으니까."], ["We only say that if it SHOULD make us rich, we have no constitutional objection to being rich.", "우린 그저 만에 하나 그게 우리를 부자로 만들어 준다면, 우리도 부자가 되는 데 천성적으로 반대할 까닭은 없다는 것뿐이야."], ["The court is, by solemn settlement of law, our grim old guardian, and we are to suppose that what it gives us (when it gives us anything) is our right.", "그 법정은 엄숙한 법의 결정으로 정해진 우리의 무뚝뚝한 늙은 후견인이고, 그것이 우리에게 주는 것(무엇이든 주기라도 한다면)은 당연히 우리 권리라고 봐야지."], ["It is not necessary to quarrel with our right.”", "우리 권리와 싸울 필요는 없잖아.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그렇죠,\""], ["said Ada, “but it may be better to forget all about it.”", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"하지만 그냥 다 잊어버리는 편이 나을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"좋아, 좋아,\""], ["cried Richard, “then we will forget all about it!", "리처드가 외쳤습니다. \"그럼 다 잊어버리자고!"], ["We consign the whole thing to oblivion.", "깡그리 망각 속에 처박아 버리는 거야."], ["Dame Durden puts on her approving face, and it’s done!”", "두든 아주머니가 흡족한 얼굴을 지으면, 그걸로 끝!\""]], [["“Dame Durden’s approving face,”", "\"두든 아주머니의 흡족한 얼굴은,\" 그의 책을 챙겨 넣던 상자 너머로 내가 내다보며 말했습니다."], ["said I, looking out of the box in which I was packing his books, “was not very visible when you called it by that name; but it does approve, and she thinks you can’t do better.”", "\"당신이 그렇게 부를 때는 그다지 보이지 않았지만, 그래도 흡족해하긴 해요. 그리고 이보다 더 잘하긴 어렵다고 생각하고요.\""]], [["So, Richard said there was an end of it, and immediately began, on no other foundation, to build as many castles in the air as would man the Great Wall of China.", "그러자 리처드는 이걸로 끝난 일이라 말하고는, 다른 토대라곤 아무것도 없이 곧장 만리장성에 다 늘어세울 만큼 많은 공중누각을 짓기 시작했습니다."], ["He went away in high spirits.", "그는 잔뜩 들뜬 기분으로 떠났습니다."], ["Ada and I, prepared to miss him very much, commenced our quieter career.", "에이다와 나는 그가 무척 그리울 것을 각오하고서, 한결 조용해진 앞날을 시작했습니다."]], [["On our arrival in London, we had called with Mr. Jarndyce at Mrs.", "런던에 도착했을 때 우리는 잔다이스 씨와 함께 젤리비 부인 댁을 찾았지만, 운이 닿지 않아 그녀를 집에서 만나지 못했습니다."], ["Jellyby’s but had not been so fortunate as to find her at home.", "어딘가 차 모임에 가면서 젤리비 양도 데려간 모양이었습니다."], ["It appeared that she had gone somewhere to a tea-drinking and had taken Miss Jellyby with her. Besides the tea-drinking, there was to be some considerable speech-making and letter-writing on the general merits of the cultivation of coffee, conjointly with natives, at the Settlement of Borrioboola-Gha.", "차 모임 외에도, 보리오불라가 정착지에서 원주민들과 더불어 커피를 재배하는 일이 두루 어떤 이로움을 지니는가를 두고 상당한 연설과 편지 쓰기가 예정되어 있다고 했습니다."], ["All this involved, no doubt, sufficient active exercise of pen and ink to make her daughter’s part in the proceedings anything but a holiday.", "이 모든 것이 의심할 바 없이 펜과 잉크의 충분한 노동을 요구했을 테니, 그 절차에서 딸이 맡은 몫이 도무지 휴일과는 거리가 멀었을 것입니다."]], [["It being now beyond the time appointed for Mrs. Jellyby’s return, we called again.", "이제 젤리비 부인이 돌아오기로 한 시각이 지났기에 우리는 다시 찾아갔습니다."], ["She was in town, but not at home, having gone to Mile End directly after breakfast on some Borrioboolan business, arising out of a society called the East London Branch Aid Ramification.", "그녀는 시내에 있었지만 집에는 없었으니, 아침 식사를 마치자마자 동런던 지부 보조 분파라는 단체에서 비롯된 어떤 보리오불라 관련 용무로 마일엔드에 갔다는 것이었습니다."], ["As I had not seen Peepy on the occasion of our last call (when he was not to be found anywhere, and when the cook rather thought he must have strolled away with the dustman’s cart), I now inquired for him again.", "지난번 방문 때 피피를 보지 못했던 터라(그때 그 아이는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었고, 요리사는 청소부 수레를 따라 어슬렁어슬렁 가버린 게 틀림없다고 했습니다) 나는 다시 그 아이의 안부를 물었습니다."], ["The oyster shells he had been building a house with were still in the passage, but he was nowhere discoverable, and the cook supposed that he had “gone after the sheep.”", "그가 집을 짓는다고 쌓아 올렸던 굴 껍데기는 여전히 복도에 있었지만, 아이는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었고, 요리사는 그가 \"양 떼를 쫓아갔나 보다\""], ["When we repeated, with some surprise, “The sheep?”", "했습니다. 우리가 적잖이 놀라 \"양 떼요?\""], ["she said, Oh, yes, on market days he sometimes followed them quite out of town and came back in such a state as never was!", "하고 되묻자, 요리사는 아, 네, 장날이면 가끔 양 떼를 따라 시 밖까지 나갔다가 말도 못 할 꼴이 되어 돌아온다고 했습니다!"]], [["I was sitting at the window with my guardian on the following morning, and Ada was busy writing—of course to Richard—when Miss Jellyby was announced, and entered, leading the identical Peepy, whom she had made some endeavours to render presentable by wiping the dirt into corners of his face and hands and making his hair very wet and then violently frizzling it with her fingers.", "이튿날 아침에 나는 후견인과 함께 창가에 앉아 있었고, 에이다는 편지를 쓰느라 바빴는데 — 물론 리처드에게 보내는 편지였습니다 — 그때 젤리비 양이 왔다는 전갈이 들어오더니, 바로 그 피피의 손을 잡고 들어왔습니다. 젤리비 양은 그 아이를 그럴듯하게 꾸며 보려고 얼마쯤 애를 썼는데, 얼굴과 손에 묻은 때를 한구석으로 문질러 몰아넣고, 머리카락을 흠뻑 적신 뒤 손가락으로 마구 비벼 곱슬곱슬하게 만든 식이었습니다."], ["Everything the dear child wore was either too large for him or too small.", "그 가엾은 아이가 걸친 것은 하나같이 너무 크거나 너무 작았습니다."], ["Among his other contradictory decorations he had the hat of a bishop and the little gloves of a baby.", "다른 모순된 장식들과 더불어, 그는 주교가 쓸 법한 모자를 쓰고 갓난아기용 작은 장갑을 끼고 있었습니다."], ["His boots were, on a small scale, the boots of a ploughman, while his legs, so crossed and recrossed with scratches that they looked like maps, were bare below a very short pair of plaid drawers finished off with two frills of perfectly different patterns.", "신발은 작은 치수이긴 했으나 영락없는 농사꾼의 장화였고, 긁힌 자국이 이리저리 엇갈려 마치 지도처럼 보이는 두 다리는, 서로 전혀 다른 무늬의 주름 장식 두 개로 끝을 마무리한 아주 짧은 격자무늬 속바지 아래로 맨살이 드러나 있었습니다."], ["The deficient buttons on his plaid frock had evidently been supplied from one of Mr. Jellyby’s coats, they were so extremely brazen and so much too large.", "격자무늬 윗도리에 부족한 단추는 젤리비 씨의 외투 가운데 하나에서 떼어다 단 것이 분명했으니, 어찌나 번쩍이고 또 어찌나 큼지막한지요."], ["Most extraordinary specimens of needlework appeared on several parts of his dress, where it had been hastily mended, and I recognized the same hand on Miss Jellyby’s.", "옷 곳곳에 황급히 기운 자리마다 보기 드물게 별난 바느질 표본이 나타났는데, 나는 젤리비 양의 옷에서도 같은 손길을 알아보았습니다."], ["She was, however, unaccountably improved in her appearance and looked very pretty.", "그렇지만 젤리비 양 자신은 어찌 된 영문인지 차림새가 부쩍 나아져 무척 예뻐 보였습니다."], ["She was conscious of poor little Peepy being but a failure after all her trouble, and she showed it as she came in by the way in which she glanced first at him and then at us.", "그녀는 그 모든 수고에도 불구하고 가엾은 어린 피피가 끝내 실패작에 그쳤다는 것을 알고 있었고, 들어오면서 먼저 그 아이를 흘끔 보고 다음에 우리를 보는 그 눈길로 그 마음을 내비쳤습니다."]], [["“Oh, dear me!”", "\"이런, 저런!\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“Due east!”", "\"정동풍일세!\""]], [["Ada and I gave her a cordial welcome and presented her to Mr.", "에이다와 나는 그녀를 다정하게 맞이하고 잔다이스 씨에게 소개했는데, 젤리비 양은 자리에 앉으면서 그에게 이렇게 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce, to whom she said as she sat down, “Ma’s compliments, and she hopes you’ll excuse her, because she’s correcting proofs of the plan.", "\"엄마가 안부 전해 달래요, 그리고 양해해 주십사 하고요. 지금 계획서 교정을 보고 계시거든요."], ["She’s going to put out five thousand new circulars, and she knows you’ll be interested to hear that.", "새 회람장 오천 장을 돌릴 거라는데, 선생님도 그 소식을 들으면 흥미로워하실 거라고 하셨어요. 한 장 가지고 왔어요."], ["I have brought one of them with me.", "엄마가 안부 전해 달래요.\""], ["Ma’s compliments.” With which she presented it sulkily enough.", "그러면서 그녀는 꽤나 뚱한 태도로 그것을 내밀었습니다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙군요,\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“I am much obliged to Mrs. Jellyby.", "\"젤리비 부인께 대단히 감사하다고 전해 주세요."], ["Oh, dear me!", "이런, 저런!"], ["This is a very trying wind!”", "견디기 참 힘든 바람이로군요!\""]], [["We were busy with Peepy, taking off his clerical hat, asking him if he remembered us, and so on.", "우리는 피피에게 매달려 그의 성직자 모자를 벗기고, 우리를 기억하느냐 묻고, 그런저런 일을 하고 있었습니다."], ["Peepy retired behind his elbow at first, but relented at the sight of sponge-cake and allowed me to take him on my lap, where he sat munching quietly. Mr.", "피피는 처음엔 제 팔꿈치 뒤로 숨었지만, 스펀지케이크를 보고는 마음이 풀려 내 무릎에 앉도록 허락했고, 거기서 조용히 우물우물 케이크를 먹으며 앉아 있었습니다."], ["Jarndyce then withdrawing into the temporary growlery, Miss Jellyby opened a conversation with her usual abruptness.", "그러고 나서 잔다이스 씨가 임시 투덜방으로 물러가자, 젤리비 양은 여느 때처럼 불쑥 이야기를 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“We are going on just as bad as ever in Thavies Inn,”", "\"세이비스 인은 여전히 형편없이 돌아가고 있어요,\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했습니다."], ["“I have no peace of my life.", "\"사는 게 도무지 편할 날이 없어요."], ["Talk of Africa!", "아프리카는 무슨 아프리카예요!"], ["I couldn’t be worse off if I was a what’s-his-name—man and a brother!”", "제가 그 뭐냐 — 사람이자 형제라는 그 흑인이었어도 이보다 더 처지가 나쁘진 않았을 거예요!\""]], [["I tried to say something soothing.", "나는 무언가 달래는 말을 하려고 애썼습니다."]], [["“Oh, it’s of no use, Miss Summerson,”", "\"오, 소용없어요, 서머슨 양,\""], ["exclaimed Miss Jellyby, “though I thank you for the kind intention all the same.", "젤리비 양이 외쳤습니다. \"그래도 그 다정한 뜻은 고마워요."], ["I know how I am used, and I am not to be talked over.", "제가 어떤 취급을 받는지 저도 다 알고, 말로 구워삶을 수 있는 사람이 아니에요."], ["YOU wouldn’t be talked over if you were used so.", "서머슨 양도 그런 취급을 받았다면 말에 넘어가지 않으셨을걸요."], ["Peepy, go and play at Wild Beasts under the piano!”", "피피, 피아노 밑에 가서 맹수 놀이나 해!\""]], [["“I shan’t!”", "\"싫어!\""], ["said Peepy.", "피피가 말했습니다."]], [["“Very well, you ungrateful, naughty, hard-hearted boy!”", "\"좋아, 이 은혜도 모르고 못된, 매정한 녀석!\""], ["returned Miss Jellyby with tears in her eyes.", "젤리비 양이 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I’ll never take pains to dress you any more.”", "\"다신 너 입혀 주느라 고생 안 할 거야.\""]], [["“Yes, I will go, Caddy!”", "\"갈게, 갈게, 캐디!\""], ["cried Peepy, who was really a good child and who was so moved by his sister’s vexation that he went at once.", "피피가 외쳤습니다. 그 아이는 사실 착한 아이여서 누나가 속상해하는 것에 그렇게 마음이 움직여 당장 가버렸습니다."]], [["“It seems a little thing to cry about,”", "\"이런 일로 우는 게 별것 아닌 것처럼 보이겠죠,\""], ["said poor Miss Jellyby apologetically, “but I am quite worn out.", "가엾은 젤리비 양이 변명하듯 말했습니다. \"하지만 저는 완전히 녹초가 됐어요."], ["I was directing the new circulars till two this morning.", "오늘 새벽 두 시까지 새 회람장 주소를 쓰고 있었거든요."], ["I detest the whole thing so that that alone makes my head ache till I can’t see out of my eyes.", "그 모든 게 어찌나 지긋지긋한지, 그것만으로도 머리가 지끈거려 눈앞이 다 안 보일 지경이에요."], ["And look at that poor unfortunate child!", "그리고 저 가엾은 불쌍한 아이 좀 보세요!"], ["Was there ever such a fright as he is!”", "세상에 저런 흉한 꼴이 또 어디 있겠어요!\""]], [["Peepy, happily unconscious of the defects in his appearance, sat on the carpet behind one of the legs of the piano, looking calmly out of his den at us while he ate his cake.", "피피는 다행히도 제 꼴이 어떤지 까맣게 모른 채, 피아노 다리 하나 뒤편 카펫에 앉아 케이크를 먹으면서 제 굴속에서 우리를 차분히 내다보고 있었습니다."]], [["“I have sent him to the other end of the room,”", "\"방 저쪽 끝으로 보낸 건,\""], ["observed Miss Jellyby, drawing her chair nearer ours, “because I don’t want him to hear the conversation.", "젤리비 양이 의자를 우리 쪽으로 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했습니다. \"이야기를 못 듣게 하려고 그런 거예요."], ["Those little things are so sharp!", "저런 어린것들은 어찌나 약삭빠른지!"], ["I was going to say, we really are going on worse than ever.", "아까 하려던 말은, 우리 집이 정말 어느 때보다 더 엉망으로 돌아가고 있다는 거예요."], ["Pa will be a bankrupt before long, and then I hope Ma will be satisfied.", "머잖아 아빠가 파산할 텐데, 그러면 엄마도 만족하시겠죠."], ["There’ll be nobody but Ma to thank for it.”", "그게 다 엄마 탓이라는 것 말고는 탓할 게 없을 거예요.\""]], [["We said we hoped Mr. Jellyby’s affairs were not in so bad a state as that.", "우리는 젤리비 씨의 형편이 그렇게까지 나쁜 지경은 아니길 바란다고 말했습니다."]], [["“It’s of no use hoping, though it’s very kind of you,”", "\"바라 봤자 소용없어요, 마음은 정말 고맙지만요,\""], ["returned Miss Jellyby, shaking her head.", "젤리비 양이 고개를 저으며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Pa told me only yesterday morning (and dreadfully unhappy he is) that he couldn’t weather the storm.", "\"아빠가 바로 어제 아침에 (무척 불행해하시면서) 이 풍랑을 헤쳐 나갈 수 없을 거라고 말씀하셨어요."], ["I should be surprised if he could.", "헤쳐 나가신다면 오히려 제가 놀랄 거예요."], ["When all our tradesmen send into our house any stuff they like, and the servants do what they like with it, and I have no time to improve things if I knew how, and Ma don’t care about anything, I should like to make out how Pa is to weather the storm.", "우리 거래처들이 죄다 제 마음대로 아무 물건이나 집에 들이밀고, 하인들은 그걸 또 제멋대로 써버리고, 저는 방법을 안다 해도 형편을 나아지게 할 시간이 없고, 엄마는 아무것에도 관심이 없는데, 도대체 아빠가 무슨 수로 이 풍랑을 헤쳐 나갈지 따져 보고 싶을 정도예요."], ["I declare if I was Pa, I’d run away.”", "정말이지 제가 아빠라면 달아나 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["“My dear!”", "\"얘야!\""], ["said I, smiling.", "내가 미소 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“Your papa, no doubt, considers his family.”", "\"네 아버지께서는 분명 가족을 생각하시는 거란다.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, his family is all very fine, Miss Summerson,”", "\"오, 네, 아빠의 가족이라는 게 참으로 훌륭하기도 하죠, 서머슨 양,\""], ["replied Miss Jellyby; “but what comfort is his family to him?", "젤리비 양이 대답했습니다. \"그런데 아빠한테 가족이 무슨 위안이 되겠어요?"], ["His family is nothing but bills, dirt, waste, noise, tumbles downstairs, confusion, and wretchedness.", "아빠의 가족이라곤 청구서, 더러움, 낭비, 소음, 계단에서 굴러떨어지는 일, 난장판, 그리고 비참함뿐인걸요."], ["His scrambling home, from week’s end to week’s end, is like one great washing-day—only nothing’s washed!”", "한 주가 시작될 때부터 끝날 때까지 아빠가 허둥지둥 돌아오는 그 집은 거대한 빨래하는 날 같아요 — 다만 빨리는 건 아무것도 없죠!\""]], [["Miss Jellyby tapped her foot upon the floor and wiped her eyes.", "젤리비 양은 바닥에 발을 톡톡 구르며 눈물을 훔쳤습니다."]], [["“I am sure I pity Pa to that degree,”", "\"정말이지 그 정도로 아빠가 가엾어요,\""], ["she said, “and am so angry with Ma that I can’t find words to express myself!", "그녀가 말했습니다. \"그리고 엄마한테는 어찌나 화가 나는지 말로 다 표현할 수가 없어요!"], ["However, I am not going to bear it, I am determined.", "어쨌든 저는 이걸 참고 견디지 않을 거예요, 단단히 결심했어요."], ["I won’t be a slave all my life, and I won’t submit to be proposed to by Mr. Quale.", "평생 노예로 살지 않을 거고, 퀘일 씨의 청혼을 받아들이지도 않을 거예요."], ["A pretty thing, indeed, to marry a philanthropist.", "박애주의자랑 결혼하는 게 참 대단한 일이겠네요, 정말."], ["As if I hadn’t had enough of THAT!”", "그런 건 이미 지긋지긋하도록 겪지 않았느냐 말이에요!\""], ["said poor Miss Jellyby.", "가엾은 젤리비 양이 말했습니다."]], [["I must confess that I could not help feeling rather angry with Mrs. Jellyby myself, seeing and hearing this neglected girl and knowing how much of bitterly satirical truth there was in what she said.", "고백하건대, 이 방치된 소녀를 보고 그 말을 들으며, 또 그녀가 한 말 속에 얼마나 쓰디쓴 풍자적 진실이 담겨 있는지 알기에, 나 자신도 젤리비 부인에게 적잖이 화가 나는 것을 어쩔 수가 없었습니다."]], [["“If it wasn’t that we had been intimate when you stopped at our house,”", "\"서머슨 양이 우리 집에 묵으셨을 때 친하게 지낸 사이가 아니었다면,\" 젤리비 양이 말을 이었습니다."], ["pursued Miss Jellyby, “I should have been ashamed to come here to-day, for I know what a figure I must seem to you two.", "\"오늘 여기 오는 것이 부끄러웠을 거예요. 제가 두 분께 어떤 꼴로 보일지 잘 아니까요."], ["But as it is, I made up my mind to call, especially as I am not likely to see you again the next time you come to town.”", "하지만 그래도 저는 꼭 찾아오기로 마음먹었어요, 더구나 다음에 두 분이 시내에 오실 때는 저를 다시 보기 어려울 테니까요.\""]], [["She said this with such great significance that Ada and I glanced at one another, foreseeing something more.", "그녀가 어찌나 의미심장하게 이 말을 했던지, 에이다와 나는 무언가 더 있겠구나 짐작하며 서로 눈길을 주고받았습니다."]], [["“No!”", "\"안 돼요!\""], ["said Miss Jellyby, shaking her head.", "젤리비 양이 고개를 저으며 말했습니다."], ["“Not at all likely!", "\"전혀 어려울 거예요!"], ["I know I may trust you two.", "두 분은 믿어도 된다는 걸 알아요."], ["I am sure you won’t betray me.", "두 분은 절 배신하지 않으실 거예요."], ["I am engaged.”", "저, 약혼했어요.\""]], [["“Without their knowledge at home?”", "\"집안 식구들 몰래?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, good gracious me, Miss Summerson,”", "\"아니, 어쩌면 좋아요, 서머슨 양,\""], ["she returned, justifying herself in a fretful but not angry manner, “how can it be otherwise?", "그녀가 짜증스럽지만 화내는 투는 아니게 스스로를 변호하며 대꾸했습니다. \"어떻게 달리할 수가 있겠어요?"], ["You know what Ma is—and I needn’t make poor Pa more miserable by telling HIM.”", "엄마가 어떤 사람인지 아시잖아요 — 그리고 불쌍한 아빠한테 말씀드려서 더 비참하게 만들 필요도 없고요.\""]], [["“But would it not be adding to his unhappiness to marry without his knowledge or consent, my dear?”", "\"하지만 얘야, 아버지께서 모르시거나 허락하지 않으신 채 결혼하는 것이 오히려 그분의 불행을 더하는 일이 되지 않겠니?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["said Miss Jellyby, softening.", "젤리비 양이 누그러지며 말했습니다."], ["“I hope not.", "\"그러지 않았으면 해요."], ["I should try to make him happy and comfortable when he came to see me, and Peepy and the others should take it in turns to come and stay with me, and they should have some care taken of them then.”", "아빠가 저를 보러 오시면 행복하고 편안하게 해 드리려고 애쓸 거예요. 그리고 피피랑 다른 동생들도 번갈아 와서 저랑 지내게 할 거고, 그때는 그 아이들도 돌봄을 좀 받을 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["There was a good deal of affection in poor Caddy.", "가엾은 캐디에게는 다정한 마음이 가득했습니다."], ["She softened more and more while saying this and cried so much over the unwonted little home-picture she had raised in her mind that Peepy, in his cave under the piano, was touched, and turned himself over on his back with loud lamentations.", "이 말을 하면서 그녀는 점점 더 누그러졌고, 마음속에 떠올린 그 낯선 작은 가정의 그림에 어찌나 펑펑 울었던지, 피아노 밑 동굴 속의 피피까지 마음이 움직여 큰 소리로 한탄하며 벌렁 등을 대고 나뒹굴었습니다."], ["It was not until I had brought him to kiss his sister, and had restored him to his place on my lap, and had shown him that Caddy was laughing (she laughed expressly for the purpose), that we could recall his peace of mind; even then it was for some time conditional on his taking us in turns by the chin and smoothing our faces all over with his hand.", "내가 그 아이를 데려와 누나에게 입을 맞추게 하고, 다시 내 무릎 위 제자리에 앉히고, 캐디가 웃고 있다고 보여 주고 나서야(그녀는 일부러 그러려고 웃어 주었습니다) 우리는 그 아이의 마음을 가라앉힐 수 있었습니다. 그러고도 한동안은, 그 아이가 우리 턱을 번갈아 붙들고 손으로 우리 얼굴을 죄다 쓰다듬게 해 준다는 조건에서만 마음이 풀렸습니다."], ["At last, as his spirits were not equal to the piano, we put him on a chair to look out of window; and Miss Jellyby, holding him by one leg, resumed her confidence.", "마침내 그의 기분이 피아노까지 갈 만큼은 되지 않았기에, 우리는 그를 의자에 앉혀 창밖을 내다보게 했습니다. 그리고 젤리비 양은 그의 한쪽 다리를 붙든 채 다시 속내를 털어놓기 시작했습니다."]], [["“It began in your coming to our house,”", "“그건 당신이 우리 집에 온 일에서 시작됐어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했습니다."]], [["We naturally asked how. “I felt I was so awkward,”", "\"제가 너무 어설프다는 걸 느꼈거든요,\""], ["she replied, “that I made up my mind to be improved in that respect at all events and to learn to dance.", "그녀가 대답했습니다. \"그래서 어쨌든 그 점만이라도 나아져야겠다, 춤을 배워야겠다고 마음먹었어요."], ["I told Ma I was ashamed of myself, and I must be taught to dance.", "엄마한테 제가 부끄럽다고, 춤을 꼭 배워야겠다고 말했죠."], ["Ma looked at me in that provoking way of hers as if I wasn’t in sight, but I was quite determined to be taught to dance, and so I went to Mr. Turveydrop’s Academy in Newman Street.”", "엄마는 늘 그러시는 그 사람 약 올리는 식으로, 마치 제가 눈에 들어오지도 않는다는 듯이 저를 보셨지만, 저는 춤을 배우기로 단단히 마음먹었고, 그래서 뉴먼 가에 있는 터비드롭 씨의 학원에 갔어요.\""]], [["“And was it there, my dear—”", "\"그래서 거기서, 얘야 —\""], ["I began.", "내가 말을 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“Yes, it was there,”", "\"네, 거기서였어요,\""], ["said Caddy, “and I am engaged to Mr.", "캐디가 말했습니다."], ["Turveydrop. There are two Mr.", "\"그리고 저는 터비드롭 씨와 약혼했어요."], ["Turveydrops, father and son. My Mr.", "터비드롭 씨는 두 분이세요, 아버지와 아들이요."], ["Turveydrop is the son, of course.", "제 터비드롭 씨는 물론 아들이죠."], ["I only wish I had been better brought up and was likely to make him a better wife, for I am very fond of him.”", "제가 더 잘 자라서 그 사람한테 더 나은 아내가 될 수 있다면 좋으련만, 저는 그 사람을 정말 좋아하거든요.\""]], [["“I am sorry to hear this,”", "\"고백하건대, 이 말을 들으니 안타깝구나.\""], ["said I, “I must confess.”", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“I don’t know why you should be sorry,”", "\"왜 안타까워하시는지 모르겠어요,\""], ["she retorted a little anxiously, “but I am engaged to Mr.", "그녀가 살짝 불안한 듯 받아쳤습니다."], ["Turveydrop, whether or no, and he is very fond of me.", "\"하지만 어쨌든 저는 터비드롭 씨와 약혼했고, 그 사람도 저를 무척 좋아해요."], ["It’s a secret as yet, even on his side, because old Mr.", "아직은 비밀이에요, 그 사람 쪽에서도요."], ["Turveydrop has a share in the connexion and it might break his heart or give him some other shock if he was told of it abruptly.", "늙은 터비드롭 씨가 그 관계에 한몫 끼어 있어서, 갑자기 말씀드리면 그분 가슴이 무너지거나 무슨 다른 충격을 받으실지도 모르거든요."], ["Old Mr. Turveydrop is a very gentlemanly man indeed—very gentlemanly.”", "늙은 터비드롭 씨는 정말 점잖은 신사분이에요 — 아주 점잖으시죠.\""]], [["“Does his wife know of it?”", "\"그분 부인은 그 일을 아시니?\""], ["asked Ada.", "에이다가 물었습니다."]], [["“Old Mr. Turveydrop’s wife, Miss Clare?”", "\"늙은 터비드롭 씨의 부인 말씀이세요, 클레어 양?\""], ["returned Miss Jellyby, opening her eyes.", "젤리비 양이 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“There’s no such person.", "\"그런 사람은 없어요."], ["He is a widower.”", "그분은 홀아비세요.\""]], [["We were here interrupted by Peepy, whose leg had undergone so much on account of his sister’s unconsciously jerking it like a bell-rope whenever she was emphatic that the afflicted child now bemoaned his sufferings with a very low-spirited noise.", "여기서 우리는 피피 때문에 말을 끊어야 했습니다. 누나가 무슨 말을 힘주어 할 때마다 종 끈을 잡아당기듯 무심코 그 다리를 홱홱 잡아당기는 통에 그 다리가 어찌나 시달렸던지, 가엾은 아이가 이제 아주 풀죽은 소리로 제 고초를 한탄했던 것입니다."], ["As he appealed to me for compassion, and as I was only a listener, I undertook to hold him.", "그가 내게 동정을 호소했고 나는 그저 듣는 사람일 뿐이었기에, 내가 그를 안고 있기로 했습니다."], ["Miss Jellyby proceeded, after begging Peepy’s pardon with a kiss and assuring him that she hadn’t meant to do it.", "젤리비 양은 입을 맞추며 피피에게 미안하다고 빌고 일부러 그런 게 아니라고 안심시킨 뒤 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“That’s the state of the case,”", "\"사정이 그래요,\" 캐디가 말했습니다."], ["said Caddy. “If I ever blame myself, I still think it’s Ma’s fault.", "\"제가 저 자신을 탓하게 될 때도, 그래도 그건 엄마 잘못이라고 생각해요."], ["We are to be married whenever we can, and then I shall go to Pa at the office and write to Ma.", "우리는 형편이 닿는 대로 결혼할 거고, 그러면 저는 아빠가 계신 사무실로 가서 엄마한테 편지를 쓸 거예요. 엄마는 별로 동요하지도 않으실걸요."], ["It won’t much agitate Ma; I am only pen and ink to HER.", "엄마한테 저는 펜과 잉크일 뿐이니까요. 한 가지 큰 위안은,\""], ["One great comfort is,”", "캐디가 흐느끼며 말했습니다."], ["said Caddy with a sob, “that I shall never hear of Africa after I am married. Young Mr.", "\"결혼하고 나면 다시는 아프리카 소리를 듣지 않아도 된다는 거예요."], ["Turveydrop hates it for my sake, and if old Mr. Turveydrop knows there is such a place, it’s as much as he does.”", "젊은 터비드롭 씨는 저를 위해서 그걸 끔찍이 싫어하고, 늙은 터비드롭 씨로 말하면 그런 곳이 있다는 것을 안다 해도 딱 거기까지가 전부예요.\""]], [["“It was he who was very gentlemanly, I think!”", "\"방금 점잖은 신사라고 했던 그분이 바로 그분이지!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Very gentlemanly indeed,”", "\"정말로 아주 점잖으세요,\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."], ["“He is celebrated almost everywhere for his deportment.”", "\"그분은 그 몸가짐으로 거의 어디서나 유명하세요.\""]], [["“Does he teach?”", "\"가르치시는 거니?\""], ["asked Ada.", "에이다가 물었습니다."]], [["“No, he don’t teach anything in particular,”", "\"아니요, 딱히 뭘 가르치시진 않아요,\""], ["replied Caddy.", "캐디가 대답했습니다."], ["“But his deportment is beautiful.”", "\"하지만 그분 몸가짐은 정말 멋져요.\""]], [["Caddy went on to say with considerable hesitation and reluctance that there was one thing more she wished us to know, and felt we ought to know, and which she hoped would not offend us.", "캐디는 적잖이 망설이고 머뭇거리면서 말을 이어, 우리가 알아 주었으면 하는, 또 우리가 마땅히 알아야 한다고 느끼는, 그러면서도 우리가 언짢아하지 않기를 바라는 일이 한 가지 더 있다고 했습니다."], ["It was that she had improved her acquaintance with Miss Flite, the little crazy old lady, and that she frequently went there early in the morning and met her lover for a few minutes before breakfast—only for a few minutes.", "그건 그녀가 그 작고 정신이 좀 이상한 노부인 플라이트 양과 알고 지내는 사이가 더 깊어졌다는 것, 그래서 이른 아침에 자주 그곳에 가서 아침 식사 전 잠깐 동안 — 정말 잠깐 동안만 — 연인을 만난다는 것이었습니다. \"다른 때에도 거기 가지만,\""], ["“I go there at other times,”", "캐디가 말했습니다."], ["said Caddy, “but Prince does not come then. Young Mr.", "\"그때는 프린스가 오지 않아요."], ["Turveydrop’s name is Prince; I wish it wasn’t, because it sounds like a dog, but of course he didn’t christen himself.", "젊은 터비드롭 씨 이름이 프린스예요. 개 이름 같아서 안 그랬으면 싶지만, 물론 본인이 자기한테 그 이름을 붙인 건 아니죠."], ["Old Mr. Turveydrop had him christened Prince in remembrance of the Prince Regent.", "늙은 터비드롭 씨가 섭정 왕세자를 기리며 프린스라는 세례명을 붙여 주셨대요."], ["Old Mr. Turveydrop adored the Prince Regent on account of his deportment.", "늙은 터비드롭 씨는 그 몸가짐 때문에 섭정 왕세자를 흠모하셨거든요."], ["I hope you won’t think the worse of me for having made these little appointments at Miss Flite’s, where I first went with you, because I like the poor thing for her own sake and I believe she likes me.", "제가 처음에 두 분과 함께 갔던 플라이트 양 댁에서 이렇게 몰래 약속을 잡았다고 해서 저를 나쁘게 보지는 말아 주세요. 저는 그 가엾은 분을 그분 자체로 좋아하고, 그분도 저를 좋아하신다고 믿어요."], ["If you could see young Mr. Turveydrop, I am sure you would think well of him—at least, I am sure you couldn’t possibly think any ill of him.", "젊은 터비드롭 씨를 보시면, 분명 좋게 생각하실 거예요 — 적어도 절대 나쁘게는 생각하실 수 없을 거예요."], ["I am going there now for my lesson.", "지금 그리로 수업을 받으러 가는 길이에요."], ["I couldn’t ask you to go with me, Miss Summerson; but if you would,”", "서머슨 양께 같이 가자고 청할 수는 없지만, 혹시 같이 가 주신다면,\""], ["said Caddy, who had said all this earnestly and tremblingly, “I should be very glad—very glad.”", "이 모든 말을 진지하게, 떨리는 목소리로 한 캐디가 말했습니다. \"정말 기쁠 거예요 — 정말 기쁠 거예요.\""]], [["It happened that we had arranged with my guardian to go to Miss Flite’s that day.", "마침 우리는 그날 플라이트 양 댁에 가기로 후견인과 약속해 두었던 참이었습니다."], ["We had told him of our former visit, and our account had interested him; but something had always happened to prevent our going there again.", "우리는 그분께 지난번 방문 이야기를 했고, 우리 이야기를 흥미로워하셨지만, 늘 무슨 일이 생겨 다시 가지 못했던 것입니다."], ["As I trusted that I might have sufficient influence with Miss Jellyby to prevent her taking any very rash step if I fully accepted the confidence she was so willing to place in me, poor girl, I proposed that she and I and Peepy should go to the academy and afterwards meet my guardian and Ada at Miss Flite’s, whose name I now learnt for the first time.", "이 가엾은 소녀가 내게 기꺼이 맡기려는 그 신뢰를 내가 온전히 받아들인다면, 그녀가 무모한 짓을 저지르지 않도록 막을 만큼의 영향력은 내게 있으리라 믿었기에, 나는 그녀와 나와 피피가 함께 학원에 갔다가 그 후에 플라이트 양 댁에서 후견인과 에이다를 만나자고 제안했습니다. 플라이트 양의 이름은 이때 처음 알게 되었습니다."], ["This was on condition that Miss Jellyby and Peepy should come back with us to dinner.", "단, 젤리비 양과 피피가 우리와 함께 돌아와 저녁을 먹는다는 조건이었습니다."], ["The last article of the agreement being joyfully acceded to by both, we smartened Peepy up a little with the assistance of a few pins, some soap and water, and a hair-brush, and went out, bending our steps towards Newman Street, which was very near.", "약속의 마지막 항목에 둘 다 기쁘게 응했으므로, 우리는 핀 몇 개와 비누와 물, 그리고 머리빗의 도움을 받아 피피를 조금 말끔하게 단장시킨 뒤 길을 나서, 아주 가까운 뉴먼 가로 발걸음을 옮겼습니다."]], [["I found the academy established in a sufficiently dingy house at the corner of an archway, with busts in all the staircase windows.", "학원은 아치형 통로 모퉁이의 꽤 칙칙한 집에 자리 잡고 있었고, 계단의 모든 창마다 흉상이 놓여 있었습니다."], ["In the same house there were also established, as I gathered from the plates on the door, a drawing-master, a coal-merchant (there was, certainly, no room for his coals), and a lithographic artist.", "문에 붙은 명패로 미루어 보건대, 같은 집에 데생 선생, 석탄 상인(석탄을 둘 자리는 분명 없어 보였습니다만), 그리고 석판 화가도 함께 들어 있었습니다."], ["On the plate which, in size and situation, took precedence of all the rest, I read, MR. TURVEYDROP.", "크기와 위치로 보아 나머지 모든 것보다 앞자리를 차지한 명패에는 \"터비드롭 씨\"라고 적혀 있었습니다."], ["The door was open, and the hall was blocked up by a grand piano, a harp, and several other musical instruments in cases, all in progress of removal, and all looking rakish in the daylight.", "문은 열려 있었고, 현관은 그랜드 피아노 한 대와 하프 한 대, 그리고 상자에 든 여러 악기로 가로막혀 있었는데, 모두 옮기는 중이었고 한낮의 빛 속에서 하나같이 방탕한 꼴로 보였습니다."], ["Miss Jellyby informed me that the academy had been lent, last night, for a concert.", "젤리비 양은 어젯밤 학원을 음악회에 빌려주었다고 알려 주었습니다."]], [["We went upstairs—it had been quite a fine house once, when it was anybody’s business to keep it clean and fresh, and nobody’s business to smoke in it all day—and into Mr. Turveydrop’s great room, which was built out into a mews at the back and was lighted by a skylight.", "우리는 위층으로 올라갔습니다 — 누군가 깨끗하고 산뜻하게 관리하는 것이 일이고, 온종일 거기서 담배를 피우는 것이 아무의 일도 아니던 시절에는 꽤 훌륭한 집이었습니다 — 그리고 터비드롭 씨의 큰 방으로 들어갔는데, 그 방은 뒤편 마구간 골목 쪽으로 튀어나오게 지어 천창으로 빛이 들어왔습니다."], ["It was a bare, resounding room smelling of stables, with cane forms along the walls, and the walls ornamented at regular intervals with painted lyres and little cut-glass branches for candles, which seemed to be shedding their old-fashioned drops as other branches might shed autumn leaves.", "마구간 냄새가 풍기는 휑하니 울리는 방이었고, 벽을 따라 등나무 의자가 놓여 있었으며, 벽에는 일정한 간격으로 칠한 리라와 양초를 꽂는 작은 컷글라스 가지들이 장식되어 있었는데, 그 가지들은 다른 나뭇가지가 가을 잎을 떨구듯 케케묵은 유리 방울을 흘리고 있는 듯 보였습니다."], ["Several young lady pupils, ranging from thirteen or fourteen years of age to two or three and twenty, were assembled; and I was looking among them for their instructor when Caddy, pinching my arm, repeated the ceremony of introduction.", "열서너 살에서 스물둘셋 살에 이르는 여러 젊은 아가씨 제자들이 모여 있었습니다. 그 사이에서 그들의 선생을 찾고 있을 때, 캐디가 내 팔을 꼬집으며 소개 의식을 되풀이했습니다."], ["“Miss Summerson, Mr. Prince Turveydrop!”", "\"서머슨 양, 프린스 터비드롭 씨예요!\""]], [["I curtsied to a little blue-eyed fair man of youthful appearance with flaxen hair parted in the middle and curling at the ends all round his head.", "나는 젊어 보이는, 파란 눈에 살결이 흰 자그마한 남자에게 인사를 했습니다. 그는 아마빛 머리카락을 가운데로 가르고 끝을 머리통 둘레로 둥글게 말아 올리고 있었습니다."], ["He had a little fiddle, which we used to call at school a kit, under his left arm, and its little bow in the same hand.", "왼팔 밑에는 학교에서 우리가 '키트'라고 부르던 작은 바이올린을 끼고, 같은 손에 그 작은 활을 쥐고 있었습니다."], ["His little dancing-shoes were particularly diminutive, and he had a little innocent, feminine manner which not only appealed to me in an amiable way, but made this singular effect upon me, that I received the impression that he was like his mother and that his mother had not been much considered or well used.", "그의 작은 무용화는 유난히 자그마했고, 그에게는 어딘가 순진하고 여성스러운 태도가 있어 호감 가는 방식으로 내 마음을 끌었을 뿐 아니라, 이런 묘한 효과까지 내게 미쳤으니, 그가 제 어머니를 닮았으며 그 어머니는 그다지 존중받지도 좋은 대접을 받지도 못했으리라는 인상을 받게 한 것입니다."]], [["“I am very happy to see Miss Jellyby’s friend,”", "\"젤리비 양의 친구분을 뵙게 되어 정말 기쁩니다,\""], ["he said, bowing low to me.", "그가 내게 깊이 허리를 숙이며 말했습니다."], ["“I began to fear,”", "\"여느 때보다 늦으셔서,\""], ["with timid tenderness, “as it was past the usual time, that Miss Jellyby was not coming.”", "수줍은 다정함을 담아 말을 이었습니다. \"젤리비 양이 오시지 않는 줄로 슬슬 걱정하던 참이었어요.\""]], [["“I beg you will have the goodness to attribute that to me, who have detained her, and to receive my excuses, sir,”", "\"그 점은 부디 그녀를 붙잡아 둔 저의 탓으로 돌려 주시고, 제 사과를 받아 주세요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, dear!”", "\"오, 저런!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["“And pray,”", "\"그러니 제발,\""], ["I entreated, “do not allow me to be the cause of any more delay.”", "내가 간청했습니다. \"제가 더 이상 지체의 원인이 되지 않도록 해 주세요.\""]], [["With that apology I withdrew to a seat between Peepy (who, being well used to it, had already climbed into a corner place) and an old lady of a censorious countenance whose two nieces were in the class and who was very indignant with Peepy’s boots.", "그렇게 사과하고서 나는 피피(이런 일에 익숙해 벌써 구석 자리로 기어 올라가 있었습니다)와, 두 조카딸이 그 반에 있는, 비판적인 표정의 한 노부인 사이의 자리로 물러났습니다. 그 노부인은 피피의 장화를 무척 못마땅해했습니다."], ["Prince Turveydrop then tinkled the strings of his kit with his fingers, and the young ladies stood up to dance.", "그러고 나서 프린스 터비드롭이 손가락으로 자기 키트의 줄을 튕기자, 젊은 아가씨들이 춤을 추려고 일어섰습니다."], ["Just then there appeared from a side-door old Mr. Turveydrop, in the full lustre of his deportment.", "바로 그때 옆문에서 늙은 터비드롭 씨가, 그 몸가짐의 광채를 한껏 뽐내며 나타났습니다."]], [["He was a fat old gentleman with a false complexion, false teeth, false whiskers, and a wig.", "그는 가짜 혈색에 가짜 이, 가짜 구레나룻, 그리고 가발을 한 뚱뚱한 늙은 신사였습니다."], ["He had a fur collar, and he had a padded breast to his coat, which only wanted a star or a broad blue ribbon to be complete.", "모피 깃을 둘렀고, 외투 가슴에는 솜을 넣어 부풀렸는데, 별 모양 훈장이나 넓은 청색 띠 하나만 더하면 완벽할 지경이었습니다."], ["He was pinched in, and swelled out, and got up, and strapped down, as much as he could possibly bear.", "그는 견딜 수 있는 한 최대로 조이고, 부풀리고, 차려입고, 졸라매고 있었습니다."], ["He had such a neckcloth on (puffing his very eyes out of their natural shape), and his chin and even his ears so sunk into it, that it seemed as though he must inevitably double up if it were cast loose.", "어찌나 큼지막한 목수건을 둘렀는지(눈알이 본래 모양에서 튀어나올 지경이었습니다), 턱은 물론 귀까지 거기 파묻혀, 그것을 풀어 놓으면 영락없이 푹 고꾸라질 것만 같았습니다."], ["He had under his arm a hat of great size and weight, shelving downward from the crown to the brim, and in his hand a pair of white gloves with which he flapped it as he stood poised on one leg in a high-shouldered, round-elbowed state of elegance not to be surpassed.", "그는 겨드랑이에 크고 무거운, 정수리에서 챙으로 비스듬히 내려간 모자를 끼고, 손에는 흰 장갑 한 켤레를 쥐고서, 한쪽 다리로 균형을 잡은 채 어깨를 치켜올리고 팔꿈치를 둥글린, 더 능가할 수 없는 우아한 자세로 그 모자를 톡톡 쳤습니다."], ["He had a cane, he had an eye-glass, he had a snuff-box, he had rings, he had wristbands, he had everything but any touch of nature; he was not like youth, he was not like age, he was not like anything in the world but a model of deportment.", "그는 지팡이가 있었고, 외알 안경이 있었고, 코담뱃갑이 있었고, 반지가 있었고, 소맷부리가 있었고, 무엇이든 다 있었으나 자연스러움이라곤 한 점도 없었습니다. 그는 젊음 같지도 않았고, 늙음 같지도 않았으며, 이 세상 무엇과도 닮지 않았으니, 오직 몸가짐의 본보기일 뿐이었습니다."]], [["“Father!", "\"아버지!"], ["A visitor.", "손님이 오셨어요."], ["Miss Jellyby’s friend, Miss Summerson.”", "젤리비 양의 친구분, 서머슨 양이세요.\""]], [["“Distinguished,” said Mr.", "\"서머슨 양께서 와 주시어,\""], ["Turveydrop, “by Miss Summerson’s presence.”", "터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"영광입니다.\""], ["As he bowed to me in that tight state, I almost believe I saw creases come into the whites of his eyes.", "그 빳빳한 차림으로 내게 절을 할 때, 나는 그의 흰자위에 주름이 잡히는 것을 거의 보았다고 믿습니다."]], [["“My father,”", "\"제 아버지는,\""], ["said the son, aside, to me with quite an affecting belief in him, “is a celebrated character.", "아들이 그에 대한 자못 가슴 뭉클한 믿음을 담아 내게 나직이 말했습니다. \"유명한 분이세요."], ["My father is greatly admired.”", "제 아버지는 크게 존경받으시죠.\""]], [["“Go on, Prince! Go on!”", "\"계속하렴, 프린스!"], ["said Mr.", "계속해!\""], ["Turveydrop, standing with his back to the fire and waving his gloves condescendingly.", "터비드롭 씨가 난로를 등지고 서서 장갑을 너그러이 흔들며 말했습니다."], ["“Go on, my son!”", "\"계속하거라, 내 아들아!\""]], [["At this command, or by this gracious permission, the lesson went on.", "이 명령에, 아니 이 자비로운 허락에, 수업이 계속되었습니다."], ["Prince Turveydrop sometimes played the kit, dancing; sometimes played the piano, standing; sometimes hummed the tune with what little breath he could spare, while he set a pupil right; always conscientiously moved with the least proficient through every step and every part of the figure; and never rested for an instant.", "프린스 터비드롭은 때로 키트를 켜며 춤추었고, 때로 선 채로 피아노를 쳤으며, 때로는 제자를 바로잡아 주는 동안 남은 숨을 짜내어 곡조를 흥얼거렸고, 가장 서툰 제자와 더불어 모든 스텝과 춤동작의 모든 부분을 양심껏 함께 움직였으며, 한순간도 쉬지 않았습니다."], ["His distinguished father did nothing whatever but stand before the fire, a model of deportment.", "그 유명한 그의 아버지는 난로 앞에 선 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았으니, 그저 몸가짐의 본보기였습니다."]], [["“And he never does anything else,”", "\"그리고 저 양반은 절대 다른 건 안 해요,\""], ["said the old lady of the censorious countenance.", "비판적인 표정의 노부인이 말했습니다."], ["“Yet would you believe that it’s HIS name on the door-plate?”", "\"그런데도 문패에 적힌 게 저 양반 이름이라니, 믿어지세요?\""]], [["“His son’s name is the same, you know,”", "\"아들 이름도 똑같잖아요, 아시다시피.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“He wouldn’t let his son have any name if he could take it from him,”", "\"빼앗을 수만 있다면 아들한테 이름 하나 갖게 두지도 않을 사람이에요,\""], ["returned the old lady.", "노부인이 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Look at the son’s dress!”", "\"아들 옷차림 좀 보세요!\""], ["It certainly was plain—threadbare—almost shabby.", "정말 그 옷차림은 수수하고 — 닳고 — 거의 초라할 지경이었습니다."], ["“Yet the father must be garnished and tricked out,”", "\"그런데 아비는 치장하고 멋을 부려야 한다는군요,\" 노부인이 말했습니다."], ["said the old lady, “because of his deportment.", "\"그 몸가짐 때문에요. 내가 그 작자를 추방해 버리고 싶다니까요!"], ["I’d deport him! Transport him would be better!”", "차라리 유배 보내는 게 낫겠어요!\""]], [["I felt curious to know more concerning this person.", "나는 이 사람에 대해 더 알고 싶은 호기심이 일었습니다."], ["I asked, “Does he give lessons in deportment now?”", "그래서 물었습니다. \"저분이 지금 몸가짐 수업을 하시나요?\""]], [["“Now!”", "\"지금이라뇨!\""], ["returned the old lady shortly.", "노부인이 짧게 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Never did.”", "\"한 적도 없어요.\""]], [["After a moment’s consideration, I suggested that perhaps fencing had been his accomplishment.", "잠시 생각한 끝에, 나는 어쩌면 펜싱이 그분의 재주였던 게 아니냐고 넌지시 말해 보았습니다."]], [["“I don’t believe he can fence at all, ma’am,”", "\"저 양반이 펜싱이라도 할 줄 안다고는 믿기지 않는걸요, 부인.\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했습니다."]], [["I looked surprised and inquisitive.", "나는 놀라며 캐묻는 표정을 지었습니다."], ["The old lady, becoming more and more incensed against the master of deportment as she dwelt upon the subject, gave me some particulars of his career, with strong assurances that they were mildly stated.", "노부인은 그 화제를 곱씹을수록 점점 더 그 몸가짐의 대가에게 분개하더니, 그의 이력을 몇 가지 들려주면서 이것도 아주 점잖게 말하는 것이라고 거듭 단언했습니다."]], [["He had married a meek little dancing-mistress, with a tolerable connexion (having never in his life before done anything but deport himself), and had worked her to death, or had, at the best, suffered her to work herself to death, to maintain him in those expenses which were indispensable to his position.", "그는 그럭저럭 손님이 붙은 온순하고 자그마한 무용 선생과 결혼했고(그는 평생 몸가짐을 차리는 것 말고는 아무 일도 한 적이 없었습니다), 그녀를 일을 시켜 죽음에 이르게 했거나, 잘 봐줘야 그녀가 스스로 일에 치여 죽도록 내버려 둔 것이었으니, 자기 처지에 없어서는 안 될 그 비용을 대게 하려는 것이었습니다."], ["At once to exhibit his deportment to the best models and to keep the best models constantly before himself, he had found it necessary to frequent all public places of fashionable and lounging resort, to be seen at Brighton and elsewhere at fashionable times, and to lead an idle life in the very best clothes.", "자기 몸가짐을 최고의 본보기들에게 선보이는 동시에 그 최고의 본보기들을 늘 눈앞에 두기 위해, 그는 유행과 한가로운 사교의 온갖 공공장소를 드나들어야 했고, 철철이 브라이턴이며 여러 곳에 모습을 드러내야 했으며, 더없이 좋은 옷을 차려입고 빈둥거리는 삶을 살아야 했습니다."], ["To enable him to do this, the affectionate little dancing-mistress had toiled and laboured and would have toiled and laboured to that hour if her strength had lasted so long.", "그가 이렇게 하도록, 그 다정하고 자그마한 무용 선생은 고되게 일하고 또 일했으니, 기력만 버텨 주었다면 마지막 순간까지도 고되게 일하고 또 일했을 것입니다."], ["For the mainspring of the story was that in spite of the man’s absorbing selfishness, his wife (overpowered by his deportment) had, to the last, believed in him and had, on her death-bed, in the most moving terms, confided him to their son as one who had an inextinguishable claim upon him and whom he could never regard with too much pride and deference.", "이 이야기의 핵심은, 그 남자의 사무치는 이기심에도 불구하고, 그의 아내가(그 몸가짐에 압도당하여) 끝까지 그를 믿었다는 것, 그리고 임종의 자리에서 더없이 감동적인 말로 그를 아들에게 맡기되, 꺼지지 않는 권리를 아들에게 지닌 분, 아무리 큰 자부심과 공경으로 떠받들어도 지나치지 않을 분으로 당부했다는 것이었습니다."], ["The son, inheriting his mother’s belief, and having the deportment always before him, had lived and grown in the same faith, and now, at thirty years of age, worked for his father twelve hours a day and looked up to him with veneration on the old imaginary pinnacle.", "어머니의 그 믿음을 물려받고, 늘 그 몸가짐을 눈앞에 두어 온 아들은, 똑같은 신앙 속에서 살며 자라났고, 이제 서른 살이 되어, 하루 열두 시간을 아버지를 위해 일하면서 그 케케묵은 가공의 정점에 올라앉은 아버지를 우러러보고 있었습니다."]], [["“The airs the fellow gives himself!”", "\"저 작자가 부리는 거드름이라니!\""], ["said my informant, shaking her head at old Mr. Turveydrop with speechless indignation as he drew on his tight gloves, of course unconscious of the homage she was rendering.", "내게 이야기를 들려주던 노부인이, 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 빳빳한 장갑을 끼는 모습을 보며 — 물론 그는 그녀가 바치는 그 경의를 까맣게 모르고 있었습니다 — 말없는 분노로 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했습니다."], ["“He fully believes he is one of the aristocracy!", "\"저 양반은 자기가 귀족 가운데 하나라고 철석같이 믿고 있어요!"], ["And he is so condescending to the son he so egregiously deludes that you might suppose him the most virtuous of parents.", "게다가 그렇게 어처구니없이 속여 먹는 아들한테는 어찌나 너그럽게 구는지, 보고 있노라면 세상에서 가장 덕 있는 아비라고 여길 정도라니까요."], ["Oh!”", "오!\""], ["said the old lady, apostrophizing him with infinite vehemence.", "노부인이 한없는 격정으로 그를 향해 부르짖었습니다."], ["“I could bite you!”", "\"콱 물어뜯어 주고 싶어요!\""]], [["I could not help being amused, though I heard the old lady out with feelings of real concern.", "나는 진심 어린 걱정으로 노부인의 말을 끝까지 들었지만, 그래도 웃음이 나는 것을 어쩔 수 없었습니다."], ["It was difficult to doubt her with the father and son before me.", "아버지와 아들을 눈앞에 두고 보니 그녀의 말을 의심하기란 어려웠습니다."], ["What I might have thought of them without the old lady’s account, or what I might have thought of the old lady’s account without them, I cannot say.", "노부인의 이야기 없이 두 사람을 보았다면 어떻게 생각했을지, 또 두 사람을 보지 않고 노부인의 이야기만 들었다면 어떻게 생각했을지는 알 수 없습니다."], ["There was a fitness of things in the whole that carried conviction with it.", "그 전체에는 사물의 들어맞음이 있어 절로 수긍이 가게 했습니다."]], [["My eyes were yet wandering, from young Mr. Turveydrop working so hard, to old Mr. Turveydrop deporting himself so beautifully, when the latter came ambling up to me and entered into conversation.", "젊은 터비드롭 씨가 그토록 고되게 일하는 모습에서, 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 그토록 멋들어지게 몸가짐을 차리는 모습으로 내 눈길이 아직 헤매고 있을 때, 후자가 어슬렁어슬렁 내게 다가와 말을 걸기 시작했습니다."]], [["He asked me, first of all, whether I conferred a charm and a distinction on London by residing in it?", "그는 무엇보다도 먼저, 내가 런던에 거주함으로써 이 도시에 매력과 품격을 더해 주는 것이냐고 물었습니다."], ["I did not think it necessary to reply that I was perfectly aware I should not do that, in any case, but merely told him where I did reside.", "나는 어떤 경우에도 내가 그러지 못하리라는 것을 잘 안다고 대답할 필요는 없다고 보아, 그저 내가 사는 곳이 어디인지만 알려 주었습니다."]], [["“A lady so graceful and accomplished,”", "\"그토록 우아하고 교양 있는 숙녀분께서는,\""], ["he said, kissing his right glove and afterwards extending it towards the pupils, “will look leniently on the deficiencies here.", "그가 오른쪽 장갑에 입을 맞추고 나서 그 손을 제자들 쪽으로 뻗으며 말했습니다. \"이곳의 부족함을 너그러이 보아 주시겠지요."], ["We do our best to polish—polish—polish!”", "우리는 갈고닦고 — 갈고닦고 — 갈고닦으려 최선을 다하고 있습니다!\""]], [["He sat down beside me, taking some pains to sit on the form, I thought, in imitation of the print of his illustrious model on the sofa.", "그는 내 옆에 앉았는데, 소파에 앉은 그 명망 높은 본보기의 판화를 흉내 내느라 의자에 꽤나 공들여 앉는 것 같았습니다."], ["And really he did look very like it.", "그리고 정말로 그는 그 판화와 무척 닮아 보였습니다."]], [["“To polish—polish—polish!”", "\"갈고닦고 — 갈고닦고 — 갈고닦는다!\""], ["he repeated, taking a pinch of snuff and gently fluttering his fingers.", "그가 코담배를 한 줌 집으며 손가락을 가볍게 나풀거리고는 거듭 말했습니다."], ["“But we are not, if I may say so to one formed to be graceful both by Nature and Art—”", "\"하지만, 천성과 기예로 두루 우아하도록 빚어진 분께 이런 말을 해도 된다면 —\""], ["with the high-shouldered bow, which it seemed impossible for him to make without lifting up his eyebrows and shutting his eyes “—we are not what we used to be in point of deportment.”", "눈썹을 치켜올리고 눈을 감지 않고서는 도무지 못 할 듯한 그 어깨를 치켜올린 절을 하며 말했습니다. \"— 몸가짐이라는 점에서 우리는 예전 같지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Are we not, sir?”", "\"그렇지 않은가요, 선생님?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“We have degenerated,”", "\"우리는 타락했습니다,\""], ["he returned, shaking his head, which he could do to a very limited extent in his cravat.", "목수건 속에서 아주 제한된 정도로나마 고개를 저으며 그가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“A levelling age is not favourable to deportment.", "\"평준화의 시대는 몸가짐에 호의적이지 않지요."], ["It develops vulgarity.", "그것은 천박함을 키웁니다."], ["Perhaps I speak with some little partiality.", "어쩌면 제가 다소 편파적으로 말하는지도 모르겠습니다."], ["It may not be for me to say that I have been called, for some years now, Gentleman Turveydrop, or that his Royal Highness the Prince Regent did me the honour to inquire, on my removing my hat as he drove out of the Pavilion at Brighton (that fine building), ‘Who is he?", "제가 몇 해 전부터 '신사 터비드롭'이라 불려 왔다거나, 섭정 왕세자 전하께서 브라이턴의 그 멋진 건물 파빌리온에서 마차를 타고 나오실 때 제가 모자를 벗자 황송하게도 '저 사람은 누구인가? 도대체 저자가 누구냔 말이다?"], ["Who the devil is he?", "어찌하여 내가 저 사람을 모른단 말인가?"], ["Why don’t I know him?", "어찌하여 저 사람에게 연 삼만 파운드가 없단 말인가?'"], ["Why hasn’t he thirty thousand a year?’ But these are little matters of anecdote—the general property, ma’am—still repeated occasionally among the upper classes.”", "하고 물으셨다는 것을, 제가 말씀드릴 자리는 아닐지도 모릅니다. 하지만 이런 것은 사소한 일화의 부스러기일 뿐 — 누구나 아는 이야기지요, 부인 — 지금도 상류층 사이에서 이따금 회자되고는 합니다.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["He replied with the high-shouldered bow.", "그는 어깨를 치켜올린 절로 대답했습니다."], ["“Where what is left among us of deportment,”", "\"우리에게 몸가짐이라 할 만한 것이 남아,\" 그가 덧붙였습니다."], ["he added, “still lingers.", "\"아직 명맥을 잇는 곳이지요."], ["England—alas, my country!—has degenerated very much, and is degenerating every day.", "영국은 — 아아, 내 조국이여! — 몹시 타락하였고, 나날이 타락해 가고 있습니다."], ["She has not many gentlemen left.", "이제 신사가 그리 많이 남지 않았습니다."], ["We are few.", "우리는 소수입니다."], ["I see nothing to succeed us but a race of weavers.”", "우리 뒤를 이을 것이라곤 직조공의 무리 말고는 보이질 않는군요.\""]], [["“One might hope that the race of gentlemen would be perpetuated here,”", "\"이곳에서 신사의 혈통이 길이 이어지기를 바랄 수도 있겠지요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“You are very good.”", "\"참으로 친절하십니다.\""], ["He smiled with a high-shouldered bow again.", "그는 또다시 어깨를 치켜올린 절과 함께 미소 지었습니다. \"과찬이십니다."], ["“You flatter me. But, no—no!", "하지만, 아니지요 — 아니에요!"], ["I have never been able to imbue my poor boy with that part of his art.", "저는 제 가엾은 아들에게 그 기예의 그 부분만큼은 끝내 불어넣어 줄 수가 없었습니다."], ["Heaven forbid that I should disparage my dear child, but he has—no deportment.”", "제 사랑하는 아이를 깎아내릴 생각은 결코 없습니다만, 그 아이에게는 — 몸가짐이 없습니다.\""]], [["“He appears to be an excellent master,”", "\"훌륭한 선생님이신 듯하던데요.\""], ["I observed.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Understand me, my dear madam, he IS an excellent master.", "\"오해는 마세요, 부인. 그 아이는 훌륭한 선생이 맞습니다."], ["All that can be acquired, he has acquired.", "익힐 수 있는 것은 모두 익혔지요."], ["All that can be imparted, he can impart.", "전수할 수 있는 것은 모두 전수할 수 있고요."], ["But there ARE things—”", "하지만 그것만으로는 안 되는 것이 — \""], ["He took another pinch of snuff and made the bow again, as if to add, “This kind of thing, for instance.”", "그는 코담배를 또 한 줌 집고는 다시 절을 했으니, 마치 \"이를테면 이런 것 말입니다\" 하고 덧붙이려는 듯했습니다."]], [["I glanced towards the centre of the room, where Miss Jellyby’s lover, now engaged with single pupils, was undergoing greater drudgery than ever.", "나는 방 한가운데로 눈길을 던졌습니다. 거기서 젤리비 양의 연인은 이제 제자들을 한 사람씩 맡아 어느 때보다 더한 고역을 치르고 있었습니다."]], [["“My amiable child,”", "\"내 사랑스러운 아이.\""], ["murmured Mr. Turveydrop, adjusting his cravat.", "터비드롭 씨가 목수건을 매만지며 중얼거렸습니다."]], [["“Your son is indefatigable,”", "\"아드님은 지칠 줄을 모르네요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“It is my reward,” said Mr.", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니,\""], ["Turveydrop, “to hear you say so.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"그것이 제 보답입니다."], ["In some respects, he treads in the footsteps of his sainted mother.", "어떤 점에서 그 아이는 성스러운 제 어미의 발자취를 따르고 있지요."], ["She was a devoted creature.", "그 사람은 헌신적인 사람이었습니다."], ["But wooman, lovely wooman,” said Mr.", "하지만 여인, 사랑스러운 여인이여,\""], ["Turveydrop with very disagreeable gallantry, “what a sex you are!”", "터비드롭 씨가 자못 거북한 정중함을 담아 말했습니다. \"그대들은 참으로 대단한 성(性)이로다!\""]], [["I rose and joined Miss Jellyby, who was by this time putting on her bonnet.", "나는 일어나 젤리비 양에게 갔는데, 그녀는 이미 보닛을 쓰고 있었습니다."], ["The time allotted to a lesson having fully elapsed, there was a general putting on of bonnets.", "수업에 할당된 시간이 다 지나, 다들 보닛을 쓰는 참이었습니다."], ["When Miss Jellyby and the unfortunate Prince found an opportunity to become betrothed I don’t know, but they certainly found none on this occasion to exchange a dozen words.", "젤리비 양과 그 불운한 프린스가 언제 약혼할 짬을 냈는지는 모르겠으나, 이날만큼은 분명 열 마디도 주고받을 짬을 내지 못했습니다."]], [["“My dear,” said Mr.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Turveydrop benignly to his son, “do you know the hour?”", "터비드롭 씨가 아들에게 인자하게 말했습니다. \"지금 몇 시인지 아느냐?\""]], [["“No, father.” The son had no watch.", "\"모르겠어요, 아버지.\""], ["The father had a handsome gold one, which he pulled out with an air that was an example to mankind.", "아들에게는 시계가 없었습니다. 아버지에게는 멋진 금시계가 있어, 그것을 인류의 본보기가 될 만한 태도로 꺼내 들었습니다."]], [["“My son,”", "\"아들아,\""], ["said he, “it’s two o’clock.", "그가 말했습니다. \"두 시구나."], ["Recollect your school at Kensington at three.”", "세 시에 켄징턴에서 수업이 있다는 걸 잊지 마라.\""]], [["“That’s time enough for me, father,”", "\"그 정도면 넉넉해요, 아버지,\""], ["said Prince.", "프린스가 말했습니다."], ["“I can take a morsel of dinner standing and be off.”", "\"선 채로 저녁을 한 술 뜨고 곧장 가면 돼요.\""]], [["“My dear boy,”", "\"내 사랑하는 아들아,\""], ["returned his father, “you must be very quick.", "아버지가 대꾸했습니다. \"아주 서둘러야 한다."], ["You will find the cold mutton on the table.”", "식탁에 식은 양고기가 있을 게다.\""]], [["“Thank you, father.", "\"고맙습니다, 아버지."], ["Are YOU off now, father?”", "이제 나가시는 거예요, 아버지?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear.", "\"그래, 얘야."], ["I suppose,” said Mr.", "아무래도,\""], ["Turveydrop, shutting his eyes and lifting up his shoulders with modest consciousness, “that I must show myself, as usual, about town.”", "터비드롭 씨가 눈을 감고 겸손한 자의식과 함께 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했습니다. \"여느 때처럼 시내에 한번 얼굴을 비춰야겠지.\""]], [["“You had better dine out comfortably somewhere,”", "\"어디 편한 데서 느긋하게 식사하고 오세요.\""], ["said his son.", "아들이 말했습니다."]], [["“My dear child, I intend to.", "\"내 사랑하는 아이야, 그럴 작정이다."], ["I shall take my little meal, I think, at the French house, in the Opera Colonnade.”", "오페라 콜로네이드에 있는 프랑스 식당에서 가볍게 한 끼 들까 한다.\""]], [["“That’s right.", "\"좋아요."], ["Good-bye, father!”", "다녀오세요, 아버지!\""], ["said Prince, shaking hands.", "프린스가 악수하며 말했습니다."]], [["“Good-bye, my son.", "\"잘 있거라, 내 아들아."], ["Bless you!”", "신의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["Mr. Turveydrop said this in quite a pious manner, and it seemed to do his son good, who, in parting from him, was so pleased with him, so dutiful to him, and so proud of him that I almost felt as if it were an unkindness to the younger man not to be able to believe implicitly in the elder.", "터비드롭 씨가 이 말을 자못 경건하게 했더니, 아들에게 좋은 효험을 주는 듯했습니다."], ["The few moments that were occupied by Prince in taking leave of us (and particularly of one of us, as I saw, being in the secret), enhanced my favourable impression of his almost childish character.", "아들은 아버지와 헤어지며 그를 어찌나 기뻐하고, 그에게 어찌나 효성스럽고, 그를 어찌나 자랑스러워하던지, 이 젊은이를 위해서라도 늙은 아버지를 곧이곧대로 믿어 주지 못하는 것이 그에게 매정한 짓처럼 느껴질 지경이었습니다."], ["I felt a liking for him and a compassion for him as he put his little kit in his pocket—and with it his desire to stay a little while with Caddy—and went away good-humouredly to his cold mutton and his school at Kensington, that made me scarcely less irate with his father than the censorious old lady.", "프린스가 우리에게 작별을 고하느라(그리고 내가 보기에 비밀을 아는 처지인 만큼, 특히 우리 중 한 사람에게 작별을 고하느라) 보낸 그 짧은 순간들은, 거의 아이 같은 그의 성품에 대한 내 호의적 인상을 한층 더 짙게 해 주었습니다. 그가 작은 키트를 주머니에 넣으면서 — 캐디와 잠시라도 더 있고 싶은 바람도 그와 함께 거두어 넣으면서 — 식은 양고기와 켄징턴 수업을 향해 싹싹하게 떠나가는 모습을 보니, 나는 그에게 호감과 더불어 연민을 느꼈고, 그 통에 비판적인 노부인 못지않게 그의 아버지에게 화가 났습니다."]], [["The father opened the room door for us and bowed us out in a manner, I must acknowledge, worthy of his shining original.", "아버지는 우리에게 방문을 열어 주고, 솔직히 인정하건대 그 빛나는 원본에 손색없는 태도로 우리를 배웅해 나갔습니다."], ["In the same style he presently passed us on the other side of the street, on his way to the aristocratic part of the town, where he was going to show himself among the few other gentlemen left.", "같은 태도로 그는 곧 길 건너편에서 우리를 지나쳐, 몇 안 남은 다른 신사들 사이에 모습을 드러내러 시내의 귀족 구역으로 향했습니다."], ["For some moments, I was so lost in reconsidering what I had heard and seen in Newman Street that I was quite unable to talk to Caddy or even to fix my attention on what she said to me, especially when I began to inquire in my mind whether there were, or ever had been, any other gentlemen, not in the dancing profession, who lived and founded a reputation entirely on their deportment.", "잠시 나는 뉴먼 가에서 듣고 본 것을 다시 곱씹느라 어찌나 정신이 팔렸던지, 캐디에게 말을 걸기는커녕 그녀가 내게 하는 말에 주의를 모을 수조차 없었습니다. 특히, 무용업에 종사하지 않으면서도 오로지 몸가짐 하나로 살아가며 명성을 쌓은 다른 신사들이 지금 있거나 일찍이 있었던 적이 있을까 마음속으로 헤아리기 시작하면서부터 더욱 그러했습니다."], ["This became so bewildering and suggested the possibility of so many Mr. Turveydrops that I said, “Esther, you must make up your mind to abandon this subject altogether and attend to Caddy.”", "이런 생각이 어찌나 어지럽고, 또 그토록 많은 터비드롭 씨가 있을 수 있다는 가능성을 떠올리게 했던지, 나는 \"에스더, 이 주제는 죄다 그만 떨쳐 버리고 캐디한테 마음을 쓰기로 단단히 마음먹어야겠다\"라고 다짐했습니다."], ["I accordingly did so, and we chatted all the rest of the way to Lincoln’s Inn.", "그래서 나는 그렇게 했고, 우리는 링컨스인까지 가는 내내 줄곧 이야기를 나누었습니다."]], [["Caddy told me that her lover’s education had been so neglected that it was not always easy to read his notes.", "캐디는 자기 연인이 받은 교육이 어찌나 소홀했던지 그의 쪽지를 읽어 내기가 늘 쉽지만은 않다고 말했습니다."], ["She said if he were not so anxious about his spelling and took less pains to make it clear, he would do better; but he put so many unnecessary letters into short words that they sometimes quite lost their English appearance.", "그녀는, 그가 철자에 그렇게 안달하지 않고 그걸 또렷하게 하려고 그토록 애쓰지만 않으면 오히려 더 나을 거라고 했습니다. 그런데 짧은 단어에 쓸데없는 글자를 어찌나 많이 끼워 넣는지, 그것들이 때로는 영어로 보이지도 않을 지경이라는 것이었습니다."], ["“He does it with the best intention,”", "\"최선의 뜻으로 그러는 거지만,\""], ["observed Caddy, “but it hasn’t the effect he means, poor fellow!”", "캐디가 말했습니다. \"그가 바라는 효과는 못 내요, 가엾은 사람!\""], ["Caddy then went on to reason, how could he be expected to be a scholar when he had passed his whole life in the dancing-school and had done nothing but teach and fag, fag and teach, morning, noon, and night!", "그러고 나서 캐디는 따져 물었습니다. 평생을 무용 학원에서 보내며 가르치고 죽도록 일하고, 죽도록 일하고 가르치는 것 말고는 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 한 게 없는데, 어떻게 그에게 학식을 바랄 수 있겠느냐고요!"], ["And what did it matter?", "그리고 그게 무슨 대수냐고요?"], ["She could write letters enough for both, as she knew to her cost, and it was far better for him to be amiable than learned.", "그녀는 — 호되게 겪어 잘 알듯이 — 둘 몫의 편지를 충분히 쓸 수 있고, 그가 학식 있는 것보다 다정한 편이 훨씬 낫다고 했습니다."], ["“Besides, it’s not as if I was an accomplished girl who had any right to give herself airs,”", "\"게다가 제가 무슨 교양 있는 처녀라서 거드름을 피울 자격이 있는 것도 아니잖아요,\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."], ["“I know little enough, I am sure, thanks to Ma!”", "\"엄마 덕분에 정말이지 아는 게 쥐뿔도 없는걸요!\""]], [["“There’s another thing I want to tell you, now we are alone,”", "\"이제 우리 둘뿐이니 한 가지 더 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있어요,\""], ["continued Caddy, “which I should not have liked to mention unless you had seen Prince, Miss Summerson.", "캐디가 말을 이었습니다. \"서머슨 양이 프린스를 보지 않으셨다면 꺼내고 싶지 않았을 얘기예요."], ["You know what a house ours is.", "우리 집이 어떤 집인지 아시잖아요."], ["It’s of no use my trying to learn anything that it would be useful for Prince’s wife to know in OUR house.", "우리 집에서는 프린스의 아내가 알아 두면 쓸모 있을 만한 걸 뭐 하나 배우려 해도 소용이 없어요."], ["We live in such a state of muddle that it’s impossible, and I have only been more disheartened whenever I have tried.", "우리는 그런 난장판 속에서 사니까 불가능하고, 시도할 때마다 더 의기소침해지기만 했어요."], ["So I get a little practice with—who do you think?", "그래서 저는 어디서 조금씩 연습을 하는데 — 어디겠어요?"], ["Poor Miss Flite!", "가엾은 플라이트 양 댁이에요!"], ["Early in the morning I help her to tidy her room and clean her birds, and I make her cup of coffee for her (of course she taught me), and I have learnt to make it so well that Prince says it’s the very best coffee he ever tasted, and would quite delight old Mr.", "이른 아침에 그분 방 정돈하고 새들 돌보는 걸 도와드리고, 커피도 타 드려요(물론 그분이 가르쳐 주셨죠). 그런데 어찌나 잘 타게 됐는지, 프린스는 자기가 마셔 본 커피 중에 단연 최고라면서, 커피에 정말 까다로운 늙은 터비드롭 씨도 아주 흡족해하실 거래요."], ["Turveydrop, who is very particular indeed about his coffee.", "저는 작은 푸딩도 만들 줄 알고, 양고기 목살이며 차며 설탕이며 버터며 살림에 필요한 여러 가지를 사는 법도 알아요."], ["I can make little puddings too; and I know how to buy neck of mutton, and tea, and sugar, and butter, and a good many housekeeping things.", "바느질은 아직 솜씨가 없지만요,\" 캐디가 피피의 윗도리에 기운 자리를 흘끔 보며 말했습니다."], ["I am not clever at my needle, yet,”", "\"그래도 차차 나아지겠죠."], ["said Caddy, glancing at the repairs on Peepy’s frock, “but perhaps I shall improve, and since I have been engaged to Prince and have been doing all this, I have felt better-tempered, I hope, and more forgiving to Ma.", "그리고 프린스와 약혼하고 이런 걸 죄다 하면서, 제 성미도 한결 누그러지고 엄마한테도 더 너그러워진 것 같아요, 그러기를 바라요."], ["It rather put me out at first this morning to see you and Miss Clare looking so neat and pretty and to feel ashamed of Peepy and myself too, but on the whole I hope I am better-tempered than I was and more forgiving to Ma.”", "오늘 아침 처음엔 서머슨 양과 클레어 양이 그렇게 단정하고 예쁜 모습이신 걸 보고, 피피와 제 꼴이 부끄러워 좀 속이 상했지만, 그래도 대체로는 제 성미가 예전보다 나아지고 엄마한테도 더 너그러워졌기를 바라요.\""]], [["The poor girl, trying so hard, said it from her heart, and touched mine.", "그토록 애쓰는 이 가엾은 소녀가 진심으로 한 말이라 내 마음을 울렸습니다. \"캐디, 얘야,\""], ["“Caddy, my love,”", "내가 대답했습니다."], ["I replied, “I begin to have a great affection for you, and I hope we shall become friends.”", "\"나는 네게 큰 정이 들기 시작했단다. 우리 친구가 되었으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["“Oh, do you?”", "\"오, 정말요?\""], ["cried Caddy.", "캐디가 외쳤습니다."], ["“How happy that would make me!”", "\"그렇게 되면 얼마나 행복할까요!\""]], [["“My dear Caddy,”", "\"내 사랑하는 캐디,\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “let us be friends from this time, and let us often have a chat about these matters and try to find the right way through them.”", "\"지금부터 우리 친구가 되자꾸나. 그리고 이런 일들을 자주 함께 이야기하면서 헤쳐 나갈 옳은 길을 찾아보자.\""], ["Caddy was overjoyed.", "캐디는 더없이 기뻐했습니다."], ["I said everything I could in my old-fashioned way to comfort and encourage her, and I would not have objected to old Mr. Turveydrop that day for any smaller consideration than a settlement on his daughter-in-law.", "나는 그녀를 위로하고 격려하려고 내 구식대로 할 수 있는 말은 다 했고, 그날만큼은 며느리 앞으로 재산을 떼어 준다는 것보다 못한 어떤 조건으로도 늙은 터비드롭 씨에게 이의를 달고 싶지 않았을 것입니다."]], [["By this time we were come to Mr. Krook’s, whose private door stood open.", "이 무렵 우리는 크룩 씨의 가게에 이르렀는데, 그 개인 출입문이 열려 있었습니다."], ["There was a bill, pasted on the door-post, announcing a room to let on the second floor.", "문기둥에 한 장의 광고가 붙어, 삼층에 세놓을 방이 있다고 알리고 있었습니다."], ["It reminded Caddy to tell me as we proceeded upstairs that there had been a sudden death there and an inquest and that our little friend had been ill of the fright.", "그것을 보고 캐디는, 위층으로 올라가면서 내게 들려주기를, 그곳에서 갑작스러운 죽음이 있었고 검시가 있었으며, 우리의 작은 친구가 그 놀란 충격으로 앓아누웠다고 했습니다."], ["The door and window of the vacant room being open, we looked in.", "빈방의 문과 창이 열려 있어, 우리는 안을 들여다보았습니다."], ["It was the room with the dark door to which Miss Flite had secretly directed my attention when I was last in the house.", "지난번 내가 이 집에 왔을 때 플라이트 양이 몰래 내 주의를 끌었던, 그 검은 문이 달린 방이었습니다."], ["A sad and desolate place it was, a gloomy, sorrowful place that gave me a strange sensation of mournfulness and even dread.", "그곳은 슬프고 황량한 곳, 음울하고 비통한 곳이어서, 내게 야릇한 애상감, 아니 두려움마저 안겨 주었습니다."], ["“You look pale,”", "\"안색이 창백해요,\""], ["said Caddy when we came out, “and cold!”", "우리가 밖으로 나오자 캐디가 말했습니다. \"게다가 추워 보이고요!\""], ["I felt as if the room had chilled me.", "나는 그 방이 나를 오싹하게 만든 것만 같았습니다."]], [["We had walked slowly while we were talking, and my guardian and Ada were here before us.", "이야기하느라 우리는 천천히 걸었던 터라, 후견인과 에이다가 우리보다 먼저 와 있었습니다."], ["We found them in Miss Flite’s garret.", "우리는 두 사람을 플라이트 양의 다락방에서 만났습니다."], ["They were looking at the birds, while a medical gentleman who was so good as to attend Miss Flite with much solicitude and compassion spoke with her cheerfully by the fire.", "그들은 새들을 들여다보고 있었고, 그동안 깊은 배려와 연민으로 친절히 플라이트 양을 돌보는 한 의사 양반이 난롯가에서 그녀와 명랑하게 이야기를 나누고 있었습니다."]], [["“I have finished my professional visit,”", "\"왕진을 마쳤습니다,\""], ["he said, coming forward.", "그가 앞으로 나서며 말했습니다."], ["“Miss Flite is much better and may appear in court (as her mind is set upon it) to-morrow.", "\"플라이트 양은 한결 좋아지셔서, (마음을 그리 굳히셨으니) 내일은 법정에 나가셔도 되겠습니다."], ["She has been greatly missed there, I understand.”", "듣자 하니 거기서 그분을 무척 그리워했다더군요.\""]], [["Miss Flite received the compliment with complacency and dropped a general curtsy to us.", "플라이트 양은 그 칭찬을 흐뭇하게 받으며 우리 모두에게 두루 인사를 했습니다."]], [["“Honoured, indeed,”", "\"참으로 영광이에요,\""], ["said she, “by another visit from the wards in Jarndyce!", "그녀가 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들이 또 찾아와 주시다니!"], ["Ve-ry happy to receive Jarndyce of Bleak House beneath my humble roof!”", "황폐한 집의 잔다이스 어른을 누추한 제 지붕 아래 모시게 되어 무 ─ 척 기쁩니다!\""], ["with a special curtsy.", "하고 특별히 한 번 더 인사했습니다."], ["“Fitz-Jarndyce, my dear”—she had bestowed that name on Caddy, it appeared, and always called her by it—“a double welcome!”", "\"피츠잔다이스, 얘야 ─\" 알고 보니 그녀는 캐디에게 그 이름을 붙여 주고 늘 그렇게 불렀습니다 ─ \"너에게는 두 곱절로 환영을!\""]], [["“Has she been very ill?” asked Mr.", "\"많이 편찮으셨던가요?\""], ["Jarndyce of the gentleman whom we had found in attendance on her.", "잔다이스 씨가, 그녀를 돌보고 있던 양반에게 물었습니다."], ["She answered for herself directly, though he had put the question in a whisper.", "그가 속삭이듯 물었는데도, 그녀가 곧장 자기 입으로 대답했습니다."]], [["“Oh, decidedly unwell!", "\"오, 확실히 편치 않았지요!"], ["Oh, very unwell indeed,”", "오, 정말 무척 편치 않았어요,\""], ["she said confidentially.", "그녀가 은밀히 말했습니다."], ["“Not pain, you know—trouble.", "\"아픈 게 아니라, 아시잖아요 ─ 심란한 거예요."], ["Not bodily so much as nervous, nervous!", "몸이라기보다 신경, 신경이 그래요!"], ["The truth is,”", "사실은,\""], ["in a subdued voice and trembling, “we have had death here.", "가라앉은 목소리로 떨면서 말했습니다. \"여기서 죽음이 있었답니다."], ["There was poison in the house.", "이 집에 독이 들었어요."], ["I am very susceptible to such horrid things.", "저는 그런 끔찍한 일에 아주 민감하답니다."], ["It frightened me. Only Mr.", "그게 저를 겁에 질리게 했어요."], ["Woodcourt knows how much.", "얼마나 그랬는지는 우드코트 선생님만 아세요."], ["My physician, Mr. Woodcourt!”", "제 주치의, 우드코트 선생님이세요!\""], ["with great stateliness.", "자못 위엄을 갖추어 말했습니다."], ["“The wards in Jarndyce—Jarndyce of Bleak House—Fitz-Jarndyce!”", "\"잔다이스 사건의 피후견인들 ─ 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 어른 ─ 피츠잔다이스!\""]], [["“Miss Flite,” said Mr.", "\"플라이트 양은,\""], ["Woodcourt in a grave kind of voice, as if he were appealing to her while speaking to us, and laying his hand gently on her arm, “Miss Flite describes her illness with her usual accuracy.", "우드코트 씨가, 우리에게 말하면서도 그녀에게 호소하는 듯한 진지한 목소리로, 그녀의 팔에 손을 살며시 얹으며 말했습니다. \"여느 때처럼 정확하게 당신의 병환을 설명하십니다."], ["She was alarmed by an occurrence in the house which might have alarmed a stronger person, and was made ill by the distress and agitation.", "더 강건한 사람도 놀랐을 법한 이 집의 한 사건에 놀라셨고, 그 비탄과 동요로 병이 나신 겁니다."], ["She brought me here in the first hurry of the discovery, though too late for me to be of any use to the unfortunate man.", "그분이 발견 직후의 그 황급한 와중에 저를 이리로 데려오셨는데, 그 가엾은 사내에게는 제가 아무 도움이 될 수 없을 만큼 이미 너무 늦었더군요."], ["I have compensated myself for that disappointment by coming here since and being of some small use to her.”", "그 실망에 대한 보상으로, 저는 그 후로 이곳을 찾아 그분께 조금이나마 도움이 되어 드리고 있습니다.\""]], [["“The kindest physician in the college,”", "\"의사회에서 가장 친절한 의사 선생님이세요,\""], ["whispered Miss Flite to me.", "플라이트 양이 내게 속삭였습니다."], ["“I expect a judgment.", "\"저는 판결을 기다린답니다."], ["On the day of judgment.", "최후의 심판 날에요."], ["And shall then confer estates.”", "그러면 영지를 나눠 드릴 거예요.\""]], [["“She will be as well in a day or two,” said Mr.", "\"하루 이틀이면 이분도,\""], ["Woodcourt, looking at her with an observant smile, “as she ever will be.", "우드코트 씨가 살피는 미소로 그녀를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"지금껏 늘 그러셨던 만큼은 좋아지실 겁니다."], ["In other words, quite well of course.", "다시 말해, 물론 아주 건강해지신다는 뜻이지요."], ["Have you heard of her good fortune?”", "이분의 횡재 소식은 들으셨나요?\""]], [["“Most extraordinary!”", "\"더없이 놀라운 일이지요!\""], ["said Miss Flite, smiling brightly.", "플라이트 양이 환하게 웃으며 말했습니다."], ["“You never heard of such a thing, my dear!", "\"그런 얘긴 들어 본 적도 없으실 거예요, 얘야!"], ["Every Saturday, Conversation Kenge or Guppy (clerk to Conversation K.) places in my hand a paper of shillings.", "토요일마다 '입심 좋은 켄지', 아니면 (입심 좋은 켄지의 서기인) 거피가 제 손에 실링이 든 종이 꾸러미를 쥐여 준답니다."], ["Shillings.", "실링이에요."], ["I assure you!", "정말이라니까요!"], ["Always the same number in the paper.", "늘 종이 안에 똑같은 개수가 들어 있어요."], ["Always one for every day in the week.", "늘 한 주의 매일에 하나씩요."], ["Now you know, really!", "자, 이거 정말이지!"], ["So well-timed, is it not? Ye-es!", "어쩜 그리 때맞춰 오는지, 안 그래요?"], ["From whence do these papers come, you say?", "그─럼요! 그 꾸러미가 어디서 오느냐고요?"], ["That is the great question.", "그게 바로 큰 의문이지요."], ["Naturally.", "당연하고말고요."], ["Shall I tell you what I think?", "제 생각을 말씀드릴까요?"], ["I think,”", "제 생각엔,\""], ["said Miss Flite, drawing herself back with a very shrewd look and shaking her right forefinger in a most significant manner, “that the Lord Chancellor, aware of the length of time during which the Great Seal has been open (for it has been open a long time!), forwards them.", "플라이트 양이 자못 영민한 눈빛으로 몸을 뒤로 빼며, 오른쪽 집게손가락을 더없이 의미심장하게 흔들면서 말했습니다. \"대법원장님이 옥새가 그토록 오래 열려 있었던 것을 아시고는(정말로 오래 열려 있었으니까요!), 그걸 보내 주시는 거예요."], ["Until the judgment I expect is given.", "제가 기다리는 그 판결이 내려질 때까지요."], ["Now that’s very creditable, you know.", "자, 이거 참으로 갸륵한 일이지요."], ["To confess in that way that he IS a little slow for human life.", "인간의 수명에 비해 자기가 좀 굼뜨다는 걸 그렇게 인정하시다니요."], ["So delicate!", "어쩜 그리 섬세하신지!"], ["Attending court the other day—I attend it regularly, with my documents—I taxed him with it, and he almost confessed.", "저번에 법정에 갔을 때 ─ 저는 제 서류들을 들고 꼬박꼬박 나간답니다 ─ 그 일로 그분을 추궁했더니, 거의 인정하시더라고요."], ["That is, I smiled at him from my bench, and HE smiled at me from his bench.", "그러니까, 제 자리에서 제가 그분께 미소 지었고, 그분도 자기 자리에서 제게 미소 지으셨어요."], ["But it’s great good fortune, is it not?", "하지만 이거 큰 횡재지요, 안 그래요?"], ["And Fitz-Jarndyce lays the money out for me to great advantage.", "게다가 피츠잔다이스가 그 돈을 저를 위해 아주 유리하게 굴려 준답니다."], ["Oh, I assure you to the greatest advantage!”", "오, 정말이지 더없이 유리하게요!\""]], [["I congratulated her (as she addressed herself to me) upon this fortunate addition to her income and wished her a long continuance of it.", "나는 (그녀가 내게 말을 건넸으므로) 수입에 이 다행스러운 보탬이 생긴 것을 축하하고, 그것이 오래도록 이어지기를 빌어 주었습니다."], ["I did not speculate upon the source from which it came or wonder whose humanity was so considerate.", "나는 그 돈이 어디서 오는지 짐작해 보지도, 누구의 인정이 그토록 사려 깊은지 궁금해하지도 않았습니다."], ["My guardian stood before me, contemplating the birds, and I had no need to look beyond him.", "후견인이 내 앞에서 새들을 바라보며 서 있었으니, 나는 그분 너머로 눈길을 둘 필요가 없었습니다."]], [["“And what do you call these little fellows, ma’am?”", "\"그런데 이 작은 녀석들을 뭐라고 부르시나요, 부인?\""], ["said he in his pleasant voice.", "그분이 그 다정한 목소리로 말했습니다."], ["“Have they any names?”", "\"이름이라도 있나요?\""]], [["“I can answer for Miss Flite that they have,”", "\"플라이트 양을 대신해서 제가 장담하건대 이름이 있어요,\""], ["said I, “for she promised to tell us what they were.", "내가 말했습니다. \"이름이 뭔지 말해 주겠다고 약속하셨거든요."], ["Ada remembers?”", "에이다도 기억하지?\""]], [["Ada remembered very well.", "에이다는 아주 잘 기억하고 있었습니다."]], [["“Did I?”", "\"제가 그랬던가요?\""], ["said Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 말했습니다."], ["“Who’s that at my door?", "\"내 문 앞에 있는 게 누구지?"], ["What are you listening at my door for, Krook?”", "무엇하러 내 문 앞에서 엿듣는 거예요, 크룩?\""]], [["The old man of the house, pushing it open before him, appeared there with his fur cap in his hand and his cat at his heels.", "집주인 노인이 문을 밀어 열며, 모피 모자를 손에 들고 고양이를 발치에 거느린 채 거기 나타났습니다."]], [["“I warn’t listening, Miss Flite,”", "\"엿들은 거 아니에요, 플라이트 양,\""], ["he said, “I was going to give a rap with my knuckles, only you’re so quick!”", "그가 말했습니다. \"주먹으로 문을 똑똑 두드리려던 참인데, 어찌나 잽싸신지!\""]], [["“Make your cat go down.", "\"그 고양이 내려보내요."], ["Drive her away!”", "쫓아 버려요!\""], ["the old lady angrily exclaimed.", "노부인이 화를 내며 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Bah, bah!", "\"쯧, 쯧!"], ["There ain’t no danger, gentlefolks,” said Mr.", "위험할 거 없습니다요, 손님들,\""], ["Krook, looking slowly and sharply from one to another until he had looked at all of us; “she’d never offer at the birds when I was here unless I told her to it.”", "크룩 씨가 우리 한 사람 한 사람을 다 둘러볼 때까지 천천히 날카롭게 눈길을 옮기며 말했습니다. \"제가 시키지 않는 한, 제가 여기 있을 땐 이놈이 절대 새들한테 덤비는 법이 없어요.\""]], [["“You will excuse my landlord,”", "\"제 집주인을 양해해 주세요,\""], ["said the old lady with a dignified air.", "노부인이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했습니다. \"엠(M)이에요, 완전히 엠이라고요!"], ["“M, quite M! What do you want, Krook, when I have company?”", "무슨 일이에요, 크룩, 내게 손님이 와 계신데?\""]], [["“Hi!”", "\"하이!\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했습니다."], ["“You know I am the Chancellor.”", "\"내가 대법원장인 거 아시잖수.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["returned Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 대꾸했습니다."], ["“What of that?”", "\"그게 어쨌다고요?\""]], [["“For the Chancellor,”", "\"대법원장이,\""], ["said the old man with a chuckle, “not to be acquainted with a Jarndyce is queer, ain’t it, Miss Flite?", "노인이 킬킬거리며 말했습니다. \"잔다이스 한 분을 모르고 지낸다는 게 우습잖수, 안 그래요, 플라이트 양?"], ["Mightn’t I take the liberty?", "제가 외람되이 한 말씀 드려도 될까요?"], ["Your servant, sir.", "안녕하십니까, 어른."], ["I know Jarndyce and Jarndyce a’most as well as you do, sir.", "저는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 사건을 어른 못지않게 훤히 알고 있습죠."], ["I knowed old Squire Tom, sir.", "옛날 톰 영주님도 알았고요."], ["I never to my knowledge see you afore though, not even in court.", "하지만 제가 알기로 어른을 전에 뵌 적은 없어요, 법정에서조차요."], ["Yet, I go there a mortal sight of times in the course of the year, taking one day with another.”", "그래도 저는 한 해 동안 어느 날 어느 날 합치면 정말 무던히도 자주 거길 간답니다.\""]], [["“I never go there,”", "\"나는 거기 가는 일이 없소,\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce (which he never did on any consideration).", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다(실제로 그분은 무슨 일이 있어도 결코 가지 않았습니다)."], ["“I would sooner go—somewhere else.”", "\"차라리 ─ 다른 어디라도 가지요.\""]], [["“Would you though?”", "\"그러시다고요?\""], ["returned Krook, grinning.", "크룩이 히죽거리며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“You’re bearing hard upon my noble and learned brother in your meaning, sir, though perhaps it is but nat’ral in a Jarndyce.", "\"어른께서는 그 뜻으로 제 고결하고 학식 높은 형제분을 호되게 나무라시는 셈이지만, 잔다이스 가문 분이시니 어쩌면 당연한 노릇이겠죠."], ["The burnt child, sir!", "불에 덴 아이라잖습니까요, 어른!"], ["What, you’re looking at my lodger’s birds, Mr. Jarndyce?”", "아니, 제 하숙인 새들을 보고 계셨네요, 잔다이스 어른?\""], ["The old man had come by little and little into the room until he now touched my guardian with his elbow and looked close up into his face with his spectacled eyes.", "노인은 조금씩 조금씩 방 안으로 들어와, 어느새 팔꿈치로 내 후견인을 건드리며 안경 낀 눈으로 그분 얼굴을 바싹 들여다보았습니다."], ["“It’s one of her strange ways that she’ll never tell the names of these birds if she can help it, though she named ’em all.”", "\"저 새들 이름은 자기가 다 지어 놓고도, 어지간하면 절대 안 가르쳐 주려고 하는 게 저분의 별난 버릇 가운데 하나죠.\""], ["This was in a whisper.", "이건 속삭임이었습니다."], ["“Shall I run ’em over, Flite?”", "\"내가 죽 읊어 볼까요, 플라이트?\""], ["he asked aloud, winking at us and pointing at her as she turned away, affecting to sweep the grate.", "그가 큰 소리로 물으며 우리에게 눈을 찡긋하고는, 모른 척 화덕을 쓰는 시늉을 하며 돌아서는 그녀를 손가락으로 가리켰습니다."]], [["“If you like,”", "\"좋으실 대로요.\""], ["she answered hurriedly.", "그녀가 황급히 대답했습니다."]], [["The old man, looking up at the cages after another look at us, went through the list.", "노인은 우리를 또 한 번 보고 나서 새장들을 올려다보며, 그 목록을 죽 읊었습니다."]], [["“Hope, Joy, Youth, Peace, Rest, Life, Dust, Ashes, Waste, Want, Ruin, Despair, Madness, Death, Cunning, Folly, Words, Wigs, Rags, Sheepskin, Plunder, Precedent, Jargon, Gammon, and Spinach.", "\"희망, 환희, 청춘, 평화, 안식, 생명, 먼지, 잿더미, 황폐, 결핍, 파멸, 절망, 광기, 죽음, 교활, 어리석음, 말, 가발, 누더기, 양피지, 노략질, 판례, 횡설수설, 헛소리, 그리고 시금치."], ["That’s the whole collection,”", "이게 컬렉션 전부예요,\""], ["said the old man, “all cooped up together, by my noble and learned brother.”", "노인이 말했습니다. \"제 고결하고 학식 높은 형제분 덕분에 한데 갇혀 있는 신세죠.\""]], [["“This is a bitter wind!”", "\"매서운 바람일세!\""], ["muttered my guardian.", "내 후견인이 중얼거렸습니다."]], [["“When my noble and learned brother gives his judgment, they’re to be let go free,”", "\"제 고결하고 학식 높은 형제분이 판결을 내려 주시면, 저것들을 풀어 줄 거예요,\""], ["said Krook, winking at us again.", "크룩이 다시 우리에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했습니다."], ["“And then,”", "\"그러고 나면,\""], ["he added, whispering and grinning, “if that ever was to happen—which it won’t—the birds that have never been caged would kill ’em.”", "그가 속삭이며 히죽거리며 덧붙였습니다. \"그런 일이 정말로 일어난다면 ─ 그럴 리야 없겠지만 ─ 한 번도 갇혀 본 적 없는 새들이 저것들을 물어 죽이겠죠.\""]], [["“If ever the wind was in the east,”", "\"바람이 정녕 동쪽에서 분 적이 있다면,\""], ["said my guardian, pretending to look out of the window for a weathercock, “I think it’s there to-day!”", "내 후견인이 풍향계를 찾는 척 창밖을 내다보며 말했습니다. \"오늘이 바로 그날인 것 같군!\""]], [["We found it very difficult to get away from the house.", "우리는 그 집에서 떠나기가 무척 어려웠습니다."], ["It was not Miss Flite who detained us; she was as reasonable a little creature in consulting the convenience of others as there possibly could be.", "우리를 붙든 건 플라이트 양이 아니었습니다. 그녀로 말하면 남의 편의를 헤아리는 데서 그보다 더 분별 있을 수 없을 만큼 사리 밝은 작은 사람이었습니다."], ["It was Mr. Krook.", "붙든 것은 크룩 씨였습니다."], ["He seemed unable to detach himself from Mr. Jarndyce.", "그는 잔다이스 씨에게서 떨어질 줄을 모르는 듯했습니다."], ["If he had been linked to him, he could hardly have attended him more closely.", "그에게 사슬로 묶여 있었다 한들 그보다 더 바싹 붙어 따라다닐 수는 없었을 것입니다."], ["He proposed to show us his Court of Chancery and all the strange medley it contained; during the whole of our inspection (prolonged by himself) he kept close to Mr.", "그는 자신의 대법원과 그 안에 든 온갖 기묘한 잡동사니를 보여 주겠다고 했습니다."], ["Jarndyce and sometimes detained him under one pretence or other until we had passed on, as if he were tormented by an inclination to enter upon some secret subject which he could not make up his mind to approach.", "(그 자신이 한껏 늘인) 둘러보는 내내, 그는 잔다이스 씨에게 바싹 붙어 다니며, 때로는 이런저런 핑계로 그분을 붙들어 두었다가 우리가 먼저 지나가게 했으니, 마치 차마 다가갈 결심을 못 하는 어떤 은밀한 화제를 꺼내고 싶은 충동에 시달리는 것만 같았습니다."], ["I cannot imagine a countenance and manner more singularly expressive of caution and indecision, and a perpetual impulse to do something he could not resolve to venture on, than Mr.", "그날 크룩 씨의 표정과 태도처럼 신중함과 우유부단함을, 또 무언가 하고 싶으면서도 차마 감행할 결심을 못 하는 끊임없는 충동을, 그토록 유난히 또렷하게 드러내는 얼굴과 몸짓을 나는 달리 떠올릴 수가 없습니다."], ["Krook’s was that day. His watchfulness of my guardian was incessant.", "내 후견인을 살피는 그의 눈길은 한시도 그치지 않았습니다."], ["He rarely removed his eyes from his face.", "그분 얼굴에서 좀처럼 눈을 떼지 않았던 것입니다."], ["If he went on beside him, he observed him with the slyness of an old white fox.", "그분 옆에서 걸을 때면 늙은 흰여우 같은 음흉함으로 그분을 관찰했습니다."], ["If he went before, he looked back.", "앞장서 갈 때면 뒤를 돌아보았습니다."], ["When we stood still, he got opposite to him, and drawing his hand across and across his open mouth with a curious expression of a sense of power, and turning up his eyes, and lowering his grey eyebrows until they appeared to be shut, seemed to scan every lineament of his face.", "우리가 멈춰 서면 그분 맞은편에 자리를 잡고는, 무언가 힘을 쥐었다는 야릇한 표정으로 벌린 입을 손으로 거듭거듭 쓸어내리며, 눈을 치뜨고 잿빛 눈썹을 내려 마치 감긴 듯 보일 때까지 내리누른 채, 그분 얼굴의 모든 윤곽을 샅샅이 뜯어보는 듯했습니다."]], [["At last, having been (always attended by the cat) all over the house and having seen the whole stock of miscellaneous lumber, which was certainly curious, we came into the back part of the shop.", "마침내, (늘 고양이를 거느리고) 집 안을 죄다 돌아보고, 그 잡다한 폐물 더미를 ─ 분명 신기하긴 했습니다 ─ 모조리 구경한 끝에, 우리는 가게 뒤편으로 들어섰습니다."], ["Here on the head of an empty barrel stood on end were an ink-bottle, some old stumps of pens, and some dirty playbills; and against the wall were pasted several large printed alphabets in several plain hands.", "거기 거꾸로 세워 둔 빈 통 머리 위에는 잉크병 하나와 닳아빠진 펜 토막 몇 개, 그리고 더러운 공연 전단 몇 장이 놓여 있었고, 벽에는 여러 종류의 단순한 필체로 인쇄한 큼지막한 알파벳 표가 여러 장 붙어 있었습니다."]], [["“What are you doing here?”", "\"여기서 뭘 하고 계신가요?\""], ["asked my guardian.", "내 후견인이 물었습니다."]], [["“Trying to learn myself to read and write,”", "\"혼자 읽고 쓰는 법을 익혀 보려고요.\""], ["said Krook.", "크룩이 말했습니다."]], [["“And how do you get on?”", "\"그래, 잘 되어 가나요?\""]], [["“Slow. Bad,”", "\"더디지요."], ["returned the old man impatiently.", "영 신통찮아요,\" 노인이 조급하게 대꾸했습니다."], ["“It’s hard at my time of life.”", "\"이 나이엔 힘든 일이에요.\""]], [["“It would be easier to be taught by some one,”", "\"누구한테 배우면 더 수월할 텐데요.\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Aye, but they might teach me wrong!”", "\"옳거니, 하지만 잘못 가르칠지도 모르잖수!\""], ["returned the old man with a wonderfully suspicious flash of his eye.", "노인이 놀랍도록 의심 어린 눈빛을 번득이며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I don’t know what I may have lost by not being learned afore.", "\"진작 못 배워서 제가 뭘 손해 봤는지는 모르겠어요."], ["I wouldn’t like to lose anything by being learned wrong now.”", "그렇다고 이제 와 잘못 배워서 뭐라도 손해 보긴 싫거든요.\""]], [["“Wrong?”", "\"잘못 가르친다니요?\""], ["said my guardian with his good-humoured smile.", "내 후견인이 그 너그러운 미소를 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“Who do you suppose would teach you wrong?”", "\"도대체 누가 당신을 잘못 가르치겠어요?\""]], [["“I don’t know, Mr. Jarndyce of Bleak House!”", "\"모르죠, 황폐한 집의 잔다이스 어른!\""], ["replied the old man, turning up his spectacles on his forehead and rubbing his hands.", "노인이 안경을 이마 위로 밀어 올리고 두 손을 비비며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I don’t suppose as anybody would, but I’d rather trust my own self than another!”", "\"그럴 사람이야 없겠지만, 저는 남보다 제 자신을 믿는 편이 낫겠어요!\""]], [["These answers and his manner were strange enough to cause my guardian to inquire of Mr. Woodcourt, as we all walked across Lincoln’s Inn together, whether Mr.", "이런 대답과 그의 태도가 어찌나 기묘했던지, 내 후견인은 우리 모두가 함께 링컨스인을 가로질러 걷는 동안 우드코트 씨에게, 크룩 씨가 정말로 그 하숙인이 말하듯 정신이 온전치 못한 것이냐고 물었습니다."], ["Krook were really, as his lodger represented him, deranged. The young surgeon replied, no, he had seen no reason to think so.", "젊은 외과의는, 아니라고, 그렇게 여길 만한 근거는 보지 못했다고 대답했습니다."], ["He was exceedingly distrustful, as ignorance usually was, and he was always more or less under the influence of raw gin, of which he drank great quantities and of which he and his back-shop, as we might have observed, smelt strongly; but he did not think him mad as yet.", "무지가 으레 그렇듯 그는 지독히도 의심이 많고, 늘 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 독한 진의 기운에 절어 있는데 ─ 그 술을 엄청나게 마셔 대는 통에, 우리도 알아챘다시피 그와 그 가게 뒤편에서 독한 술 냄새가 진동했습니다 ─ 그래도 아직 미쳤다고는 보지 않는다고 했습니다."]], [["On our way home, I so conciliated Peepy’s affections by buying him a windmill and two flour-sacks that he would suffer nobody else to take off his hat and gloves and would sit nowhere at dinner but at my side.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에, 나는 피피에게 풍차 하나와 밀가루 자루 두 개를 사 주어 그 아이의 호감을 어찌나 단단히 사 두었던지, 그는 다른 누구도 자기 모자와 장갑을 벗기지 못하게 했고, 저녁 식탁에서도 내 곁 말고는 어디에도 앉으려 하지 않았습니다."], ["Caddy sat upon the other side of me, next to Ada, to whom we imparted the whole history of the engagement as soon as we got back.", "캐디는 내 반대편, 에이다 옆에 앉았는데, 우리는 돌아오자마자 에이다에게 약혼의 전말을 죄다 들려주었습니다."], ["We made much of Caddy, and Peepy too; and Caddy brightened exceedingly; and my guardian was as merry as we were; and we were all very happy indeed until Caddy went home at night in a hackney-coach, with Peepy fast asleep, but holding tight to the windmill.", "우리는 캐디를, 그리고 피피까지도 한껏 떠받들었고, 캐디는 더없이 환해졌으며, 내 후견인도 우리만큼이나 즐거워했습니다. 밤에 캐디가 삯마차를 타고 집으로 돌아갈 때까지 ─ 피피는 곤히 잠들었지만 풍차만은 꼭 움켜쥔 채로였습니다 ─ 우리 모두는 정말이지 더없이 행복했습니다."]], [["I have forgotten to mention—at least I have not mentioned—that Mr. Woodcourt was the same dark young surgeon whom we had met at Mr.", "내가 미처 말하지 못한 것이 있는데 ─ 적어도 언급하지 않은 것이 있는데 ─ 우드코트 씨가 바로 우리가 배저 씨 댁에서 만났던 그 가무잡잡한 젊은 외과의였다는 것입니다."], ["Badger’s. Or that Mr.", "또 잔다이스 씨가 그날 그를 저녁 식사에 초대했다는 것, 그가 왔다는 것도요."], ["Jarndyce invited him to dinner that day. Or that he came.", "또 다들 돌아가고 나서 내가 에이다에게 \"자, 내 사랑, 우리 리처드 얘기나 좀 해 볼까!\""], ["Or that when they were all gone and I said to Ada, “Now, my darling, let us have a little talk about Richard!” Ada laughed and said—", "하고 말했더니, 에이다가 웃으며 말했다는 것도요 ─"]], [["But I don’t think it matters what my darling said.", "하지만 내 사랑이 무슨 말을 했는지는 중요치 않은 것 같습니다."], ["She was always merry. Bell Yard", "그 애는 언제나 명랑했으니까요."]], [["While we were in London Mr. Jarndyce was constantly beset by the crowd of excitable ladies and gentlemen whose proceedings had so much astonished us.", "우리가 런던에 머무는 동안 잔다이스 씨는 그 행동거지로 우리를 그토록 놀라게 한 흥분 잘하는 신사 숙녀 무리에게 끊임없이 시달렸습니다."], ["Mr. Quale, who presented himself soon after our arrival, was in all such excitements.", "우리가 도착하고 얼마 안 되어 모습을 드러낸 퀘일 씨도 그런 흥분에는 빠짐없이 끼어 있었습니다."], ["He seemed to project those two shining knobs of temples of his into everything that went on and to brush his hair farther and farther back, until the very roots were almost ready to fly out of his head in inappeasable philanthropy.", "그는 번들거리는 두 관자놀이 혹을 무슨 일이든 닥치는 대로 들이밀고, 머리카락을 점점 더 뒤로 빗어 넘기는 통에 마침내 그 뿌리가 달랠 길 없는 박애심에 못 이겨 머리에서 튀어나오기 직전처럼 보일 지경이었습니다."], ["All objects were alike to him, but he was always particularly ready for anything in the way of a testimonial to any one.", "그에게는 무엇이든 다 똑같았지만, 누구에게든 표창장을 바치는 일이라면 언제나 유난히 채비가 되어 있었습니다."], ["His great power seemed to be his power of indiscriminate admiration.", "그의 위대한 재능은 무엇이든 가리지 않고 찬탄하는 능력인 듯했습니다."], ["He would sit for any length of time, with the utmost enjoyment, bathing his temples in the light of any order of luminary.", "그는 어느 등급의 빛나는 인물이 발하는 광채든 그 빛에 관자놀이를 적시면서 더없는 즐거움에 잠겨 얼마든지 오래도록 앉아 있을 수 있었습니다."], ["Having first seen him perfectly swallowed up in admiration of Mrs. Jellyby, I had supposed her to be the absorbing object of his devotion.", "처음에 그가 젤리비 부인에 대한 찬탄에 완전히 빠져드는 모습을 본 나는, 그녀가 그의 헌신을 통째로 빨아들이는 대상인 줄로만 알았습니다."], ["I soon discovered my mistake and found him to be train-bearer and organ-blower to a whole procession of people.", "나는 곧 내 착각을 깨닫고, 그가 한 무리의 사람들이 줄지어 행진하는 그 모든 행렬에서 옷자락을 받쳐 들고 풍금에 바람을 넣는 사람이라는 사실을 알게 되었습니다."]], [["Mrs. Pardiggle came one day for a subscription to something, and with her, Mr. Quale. Whatever Mrs. Pardiggle said, Mr. Quale repeated to us; and just as he had drawn Mrs. Jellyby out, he drew Mrs. Pardiggle out. Mrs. Pardiggle wrote a letter of introduction to my guardian in behalf of her eloquent friend Mr. Gusher. With Mr. Gusher appeared Mr. Quale again. Mr. Gusher, being a flabby gentleman with a moist surface and eyes so much too small for his moon of a face that they seemed to have been originally made for somebody else, was not at first sight prepossessing; yet he was scarcely seated before Mr. Quale asked Ada and me, not inaudibly, whether he was not a great creature—which he certainly was, flabbily speaking, though Mr. Quale meant in intellectual beauty—and whether we were not struck by his massive configuration of brow. In short, we heard of a great many missions of various sorts among this set of people, but nothing respecting them was half so clear to us as that it was Mr. Quale’s mission to be in ecstasies with everybody else’s mission and that it was the most popular mission of all.", "어느 날 파디글 부인이 무언가에 기부금을 받으러 왔는데, 함께 온 사람이 퀘일 씨였습니다. 파디글 부인이 무슨 말을 하든 퀘일 씨가 우리에게 되풀이했고, 젤리비 부인의 말을 끌어냈듯이 파디글 부인의 말도 끌어냈습니다. 파디글 부인은 자기의 능변가 친구 거셔 씨를 위해 내 후견인에게 소개장을 한 통 써 주었습니다. 거셔 씨가 나타날 때도 퀘일 씨가 또 함께였습니다. 거셔 씨는 표면이 축축하게 늘어진 신사로, 그 둥근 보름달 같은 얼굴에 비해 눈이 턱없이 작아 본래 다른 누군가를 위해 만들어진 것을 가져다 박은 듯이 보였으니, 첫눈에 호감을 주는 인물은 아니었습니다. 그런데도 그가 채 자리에 앉기도 전에 퀘일 씨가 에이다와 나에게, 다 들릴 만한 목소리로, 저분이 위대한 인물이 아니냐고 물었습니다. 늘어진 외양으로 따지자면 분명 그러했지만, 퀘일 씨는 지적인 아름다움을 두고 한 말이었습니다. 그러고는 우리가 저 우람한 이마의 생김새에 감탄하지 않느냐고 물었습니다. 한마디로, 우리는 이 무리 사이에서 갖가지 사명에 관한 이야기를 무척 많이 들었지만, 그 어느 것도 우리에게 절반만큼도 분명하지 않았던 데 비해, 퀘일 씨의 사명이란 다른 모든 사람의 사명에 황홀해하는 것이며 그것이야말로 가장 인기 있는 사명이라는 사실만큼은 더없이 또렷했습니다."]], [["Mr. Jarndyce had fallen into this company in the tenderness of his heart and his earnest desire to do all the good in his power; but that he felt it to be too often an unsatisfactory company, where benevolence took spasmodic forms, where charity was assumed as a regular uniform by loud professors and speculators in cheap notoriety, vehement in profession, restless and vain in action, servile in the last degree of meanness to the great, adulatory of one another, and intolerable to those who were anxious quietly to help the weak from failing rather than with a great deal of bluster and self-laudation to raise them up a little way when they were down, he plainly told us.", "잔다이스 씨가 이 무리에 발을 들인 것은 마음이 여린 데다 자기 힘닿는 데까지 온갖 선을 베풀고자 하는 간절한 바람 때문이었습니다."], ["When a testimonial was originated to Mr. Quale by Mr.", "그러나 그것이 너무도 자주 만족스럽지 못한 무리라고 그는 우리에게 솔직히 말했습니다."], ["Gusher (who had already got one, originated by Mr. Quale), and when Mr.", "여기서는 자선이 발작하듯 터져 나오고, 값싼 악명을 노리는 떠벌이꾼과 투기꾼들이 박애를 무슨 제복인 양 걸치고 다니며, 입으로는 격렬하고 행동에서는 들떠 허영에 차 있으며, 권세 있는 자에게는 비굴함의 밑바닥까지 굽실거리고, 서로를 치켜세우면서, 약한 자가 쓰러지지 않도록 조용히 도우려는 이들에게는 견딜 수 없는 존재가 되니, 약한 자가 쓰러진 뒤에야 요란을 떨고 제 자랑을 늘어놓으며 겨우 조금 일으켜 세우는 자들이었습니다."], ["Gusher spoke for an hour and a half on the subject to a meeting, including two charity schools of small boys and girls, who were specially reminded of the widow’s mite, and requested to come forward with halfpence and be acceptable sacrifices, I think the wind was in the east for three whole weeks.", "거셔 씨가 퀘일 씨에게 표창장을 바치고(퀘일 씨가 먼저 거셔 씨에게 표창장을 바친 적이 있었지요), 거셔 씨가 그 일을 두고 한 시간 반 동안 모임에서 연설을 했는데, 그 자리에는 어린 소년 소녀들이 다니는 자선 학교 두 곳도 끼어 있어, 그 아이들에게 과부의 동전 이야기를 특별히 일깨우며 반 페니씩 들고 나와 가상한 제물이 되라고 청했을 때에는, 동풍이 꼬박 삼 주 동안 불었던 것 같습니다."]], [["I mention this because I am coming to Mr. Skimpole again.", "내가 이런 이야기를 꺼내는 까닭은 다시 스킴폴 씨 이야기로 넘어가기 위해서입니다."], ["It seemed to me that his off-hand professions of childishness and carelessness were a great relief to my guardian, by contrast with such things, and were the more readily believed in since to find one perfectly undesigning and candid man among many opposites could not fail to give him pleasure.", "자기는 어린애처럼 철없고 무심할 뿐이라는 그의 거리낌 없는 고백이, 이런 일들과 견주어 보면 내 후견인에게 큰 위안이 되었던 듯하고, 또 그만큼 더 쉽게 믿어졌던 것 같습니다. 정반대인 사람들이 우글거리는 가운데 아무 꿍꿍이 없이 솔직한 사람을 하나라도 발견한다는 것은 그에게 기쁨을 주지 않을 수 없었으니까요."], ["I should be sorry to imply that Mr.", "스킴폴 씨가 이를 알아채고 약삭빠르게 처신했다고 넌지시 말하려는 것은 아닙니다."], ["Skimpole divined this and was politic; I really never understood him well enough to know.", "나는 정말이지 그를 그만큼 잘 이해한 적이 없어 알 수가 없었습니다."], ["What he was to my guardian, he certainly was to the rest of the world.", "그가 내 후견인에게 어떤 사람이었든, 세상 나머지 사람들에게도 분명 똑같은 사람이었습니다."]], [["He had not been very well; and thus, though he lived in London, we had seen nothing of him until now.", "그는 몸이 그리 좋지 않았습니다. 그래서 런던에 살면서도 우리는 이제껏 그를 통 보지 못했습니다."], ["He appeared one morning in his usual agreeable way and as full of pleasant spirits as ever.", "그가 어느 날 아침, 늘 그렇듯 호감 가는 태도로, 여느 때처럼 유쾌한 기운을 잔뜩 머금고 나타났습니다."]], [["Well, he said, here he was!", "자, 그가 말하기를, 자기가 왔다는 것이었습니다!"], ["He had been bilious, but rich men were often bilious, and therefore he had been persuading himself that he was a man of property.", "담즙이 끓어 속이 좋지 않았는데, 부유한 사람들도 흔히 담즙이 끓는 법이니, 그래서 자기도 재산가라고 스스로 믿어 왔다는 것이었습니다."], ["So he was, in a certain point of view—in his expansive intentions.", "어떤 관점에서 보면 그는 정말로 재산가였습니다. 다시 말해 그 너그러운 마음씨에서요."], ["He had been enriching his medical attendant in the most lavish manner.", "그는 자기를 돌보는 의사를 더없이 후하게 부유하게 해 주었다고 했습니다."], ["He had always doubled, and sometimes quadrupled, his fees.", "진료비를 늘 곱절로, 때로는 네 곱절로 쳐 주었다는 것이었습니다."], ["He had said to the doctor, “Now, my dear doctor, it is quite a delusion on your part to suppose that you attend me for nothing.", "그는 의사에게 이렇게 말했다고 합니다. \"여보게, 친애하는 의사 선생, 자네가 나를 거저 봐준다고 여긴다면 그건 자네의 착각일세."], ["I am overwhelming you with money—in my expansive intentions—if you only knew it!”", "나는 자네에게 돈을 퍼붓고 있다네. 내 너그러운 마음속에서 말일세."], ["And really (he said) he meant it to that degree that he thought it much the same as doing it.", "자네가 그걸 알기만 한다면!\" 그리고 정말로(그가 말하기를) 그는 그렇게 마음먹은 정도가, 실제로 돈을 주는 것과 거의 다를 바 없다고 여길 만큼이었다는 것이었습니다."], ["If he had had those bits of metal or thin paper to which mankind attached so much importance to put in the doctor’s hand, he would have put them in the doctor’s hand. Not having them, he substituted the will for the deed.", "인류가 그토록 중하게 여기는 그 쇳조각이나 얇은 종잇장을 손에 쥐고 있었더라면 의사의 손에 쥐여 주었을 것이고, 그것이 없으니 행위 대신 마음을 내놓았다는 것이었습니다."], ["Very well!", "좋습니다!"], ["If he really meant it—if his will were genuine and real, which it was—it appeared to him that it was the same as coin, and cancelled the obligation.", "그가 정말로 그렇게 마음먹었다면, 그의 마음이 진짜이고 진실하다면, 사실 그러했으니, 그에게는 그것이 동전과 다름없어 빚을 갚은 셈이 된다는 것이었습니다."]], [["“It may be, partly, because I know nothing of the value of money,” said Mr.", "\"어쩌면 그건 부분적으로 내가 돈의 가치라는 걸 도통 모르기 때문일 수도 있겠지요.\""], ["Skimpole, “but I often feel this.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 나는 자주 이렇게 느낍니다."], ["It seems so reasonable!", "너무도 이치에 맞는 것 같거든요!"], ["My butcher says to me he wants that little bill.", "우리 정육점 주인이 나한테 그 자그마한 청구서를 받았으면 한다고 말합니다."], ["It’s a part of the pleasant unconscious poetry of the man’s nature that he always calls it a ‘little’ bill—to make the payment appear easy to both of us.", "그가 늘 그걸 '자그마한' 청구서라고 부르는 건 그 사람 천성에 깃든 흐뭇하고 무심한 시심의 한 자락인데, 우리 둘 다에게 지불을 쉬워 보이게 하려는 게지요."], ["I reply to the butcher, ‘My good friend, if you knew it, you are paid.", "나는 정육점 주인에게 이렇게 대답합니다. '여보게, 자네가 그걸 안다면, 자네는 이미 받은 셈일세."], ["You haven’t had the trouble of coming to ask for the little bill.", "자그마한 청구서를 받으러 오는 수고도 들지 않았고. 자네는 받은 거야."], ["You are paid. I mean it.’”", "진심으로 하는 말일세.'\""]], [["“But, suppose,”", "\"하지만 만약에 말이오,\""], ["said my guardian, laughing, “he had meant the meat in the bill, instead of providing it?”", "내 후견인이 웃으며 말했습니다. \"그자가 고기를 대주는 대신 청구서 속의 고기로 마음만 먹었다고 한다면 어떻겠소?\""]], [["“My dear Jarndyce,”", "\"이보시오, 친애하는 잔다이스,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다."], ["he returned, “you surprise me.", "\"당신은 나를 놀라게 하는구려. 정육점 주인의 입장에 서다니."], ["You take the butcher’s position.", "한번은 내가 거래하던 정육점 주인이 바로 그 자리에 섰지요. 그가 말합디다."], ["A butcher I once dealt with occupied that very ground.", "'나리, 어쩌자고 파운드당 십팔 펜스나 하는 봄철 새끼 양고기를 잡수셨습니까?' '내가 어쩌자고 파운드당 십팔 펜스짜리 봄철 새끼 양고기를 먹었느냐고, 여보게 정직한 친구?'"], ["Says he, ‘Sir, why did you eat spring lamb at eighteen pence a pound?’ ‘Why did I eat spring lamb at eighteen pence a pound, my honest friend?’ said I, naturally amazed by the question.", "나는 그 질문에 당연히 깜짝 놀라 말했지요. '봄철 새끼 양고기가 좋으니까!'"], ["‘I like spring lamb!’ This was so far convincing.", "여기까지는 그럴듯하게 들렸습니다. '그렇다면 나리,'"], ["‘Well, sir,’ says he, ‘I wish I had meant the lamb as you mean the money!’ ‘My good fellow,’ said I, ‘pray let us reason like intellectual beings.", "그가 말합디다. '나리께서 돈을 마음먹으시듯 나도 그 양고기를 마음만 먹었더라면 좋았겠습니다!'"], ["How could that be?", "'여보게,' 내가 말했지요."], ["It was impossible.", "'제발 지성을 가진 존재답게 따져 봅세."], ["You HAD got the lamb, and I have NOT got the money.", "어떻게 그럴 수가 있겠나? 그건 불가능하지."], ["You couldn’t really mean the lamb without sending it in, whereas I can, and do, really mean the money without paying it!’ He had not a word.", "자네는 양고기를 가지고 있었고, 나는 돈을 가지고 있지 않네. 자네는 양고기를 부쳐 주지 않고서야 정말로 마음먹을 수가 없지만, 나는 돈을 치르지 않고서도 정말로 마음먹을 수 있고, 실제로 그러고 있지 않은가!'"], ["There was an end of the subject.”", "그는 한마디도 못 합디다. 그것으로 그 이야기는 끝이었지요.\""]], [["“Did he take no legal proceedings?”", "\"그자가 법적 절차는 밟지 않았소?\""], ["inquired my guardian.", "내 후견인이 물었습니다."]], [["“Yes, he took legal proceedings,”", "\"네, 법적 절차를 밟았습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“But in that he was influenced by passion, not by reason.", "\"하지만 그 일에서 그는 이성이 아니라 격정에 휘둘린 거지요."], ["Passion reminds me of Boythorn.", "격정이라니 보이손이 떠오르는군요."], ["He writes me that you and the ladies have promised him a short visit at his bachelor-house in Lincolnshire.”", "그가 나한테 편지를 보냈는데, 당신과 두 아가씨가 링컨셔에 있는 자기 독신자 저택에 잠깐 들르기로 약속했다더군요.\""]], [["“He is a great favourite with my girls,” said Mr.", "\"그 친구는 우리 아가씨들이 무척 좋아하지요.\""], ["Jarndyce, “and I have promised for them.”", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그래서 내가 아이들 대신 약속을 했소.\""]], [["“Nature forgot to shade him off, I think,”", "\"내 생각엔 자연이 그 사람을 빚어낼 때 음영을 넣는 걸 깜빡한 것 같습니다.\""], ["observed Mr. Skimpole to Ada and me.", "스킴폴 씨가 에이다와 나를 돌아보며 말했습니다."], ["“A little too boisterous—like the sea.", "\"조금 지나치게 시끄럽거든요. 꼭 바다처럼요."], ["A little too vehement—like a bull who has made up his mind to consider every colour scarlet.", "조금 지나치게 격렬하고요. 무슨 색이든 다 진홍빛이라고 단단히 작정한 황소처럼 말이지요."], ["But I grant a sledge-hammering sort of merit in him!”", "하지만 그에게 무쇠 망치 같은 미덕이 있다는 건 인정합니다!\""]], [["I should have been surprised if those two could have thought very highly of one another, Mr.", "저 두 사람이 서로를 그리 높이 평가할 수 있다면 나는 오히려 놀랐을 것입니다."], ["Boythorn attaching so much importance to many things and Mr. Skimpole caring so little for anything.", "보이손 씨는 수많은 것에 그토록 큰 의미를 두는 사람이고 스킴폴 씨는 무엇에도 그토록 무심한 사람이니까요."], ["Besides which, I had noticed Mr. Boythorn more than once on the point of breaking out into some strong opinion when Mr.", "게다가 나는 스킴폴 씨 이야기만 나오면 보이손 씨가 무언가 강한 의견을 터뜨리기 직전까지 가는 모습을 한두 번 본 적이 있었습니다."], ["Skimpole was referred to. Of course I merely joined Ada in saying that we had been greatly pleased with him.", "물론 나는 그저 에이다와 더불어, 그분이 무척 좋았다고만 말했습니다."]], [["“He has invited me,” said Mr.", "\"그가 나를 초대했지요.\""], ["Skimpole; “and if a child may trust himself in such hands—which the present child is encouraged to do, with the united tenderness of two angels to guard him—I shall go.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그러니 한 아이가 그런 손에 자기 몸을 맡겨도 된다면, 지금 이 아이로 말하자면 두 천사의 다정함이 한데 모여 지켜 주고 있으니 마음 놓고 그럴 수 있겠지요, 나는 가렵니다."], ["He proposes to frank me down and back again.", "그가 갈 때나 올 때나 비용을 대 주겠다고 합디다."], ["I suppose it will cost money?", "그게 돈이 들겠지요? 실링쯤일까요?"], ["Shillings perhaps?", "아니면 파운드쯤?"], ["Or pounds? Or something of that sort?", "뭐 그런 거겠지요?"], ["By the by, Coavinses.", "그러고 보니, 코빈시스."], ["You remember our friend Coavinses, Miss Summerson?”", "우리 친구 코빈시스를 기억하시지요, 서머슨 양?\""]], [["He asked me as the subject arose in his mind, in his graceful, light-hearted manner and without the least embarrassment.", "그는 마음속에 그 화제가 떠오르는 대로, 우아하고 가벼운 태도로, 조금도 거북해하는 기색 없이 나에게 물었습니다."]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"아, 네!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Coavinses has been arrested by the Great Bailiff,”", "\"코빈시스가 위대한 집행관에게 체포되었답니다.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“He will never do violence to the sunshine any more.”", "\"그는 다시는 햇빛에 폭력을 휘두르지 못하게 됐지요.\""]], [["It quite shocked me to hear it, for I had already recalled with anything but a serious association the image of the man sitting on the sofa that night wiping his head.", "그 말을 들으니 나는 적잖이 충격을 받았습니다. 그날 밤 소파에 앉아 이마를 닦던 그 사람의 모습이 떠올랐는데, 결코 진지하달 수 없는 기분으로 떠올렸던 터였으니까요."]], [["“His successor informed me of it yesterday,”", "\"그의 후임이 어제 그 소식을 전해 주더군요.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“His successor is in my house now—in possession, I think he calls it.", "\"그 후임이 지금 우리 집에 와 있어요. 자기 말로는 점유 중이라던가요."], ["He came yesterday, on my blue-eyed daughter’s birthday.", "그가 어제 왔는데, 하필이면 내 푸른 눈의 딸 생일이었지요."], ["I put it to him, ‘This is unreasonable and inconvenient.", "내가 그에게 따졌습니다. '이건 도리에 맞지도 않고 곤란한 일이오."], ["If you had a blue-eyed daughter you wouldn’t like ME to come, uninvited, on HER birthday?’ But he stayed.”", "당신에게 푸른 눈의 딸이 있다면, 내가 초대도 받지 않고 그 애 생일에 찾아오는 걸 좋아하지 않을 게 아니오?' 하지만 그는 그대로 눌러앉았습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Skimpole laughed at the pleasant absurdity and lightly touched the piano by which he was seated.", "스킴폴 씨는 그 유쾌한 어처구니없음에 웃음을 터뜨리고는, 곁에 앉아 있던 피아노를 가볍게 짚었습니다."]], [["“And he told me,”", "\"그리고 그가 나한테 말하기를,\""], ["he said, playing little chords where I shall put full stops, “The Coavinses had left.", "그가 말했는데, 내가 마침표를 찍는 자리마다 작은 화음을 짚었습니다."], ["Three children.", "\"코빈시스가 떠났다고."], ["No mother.", "아이가 셋."], ["And that Coavinses’ profession.", "어머니는 없고. 게다가 코빈시스의 직업이."], ["Being unpopular.", "인기가 없어서."], ["The rising Coavinses.", "자라나는 코빈시스들이."], ["Were at a considerable disadvantage.”", "상당히 불리한 처지에 놓였다고.\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce got up, rubbing his head, and began to walk about.", "잔다이스 씨가 머리를 문지르며 일어나 이리저리 거닐기 시작했습니다."], ["Mr. Skimpole played the melody of one of Ada’s favourite songs.", "스킴폴 씨는 에이다가 좋아하는 노래 가운데 한 곡의 가락을 연주했습니다."], ["Ada and I both looked at Mr. Jarndyce, thinking that we knew what was passing in his mind.", "에이다와 나는 둘 다 잔다이스 씨를 바라보며, 그의 마음속에 무슨 생각이 오가는지 우리가 안다고 여겼습니다."]], [["After walking and stopping, and several times leaving off rubbing his head, and beginning again, my guardian put his hand upon the keys and stopped Mr. Skimpole’s playing.", "이리저리 거닐다가 멈추고, 몇 번이고 머리 문지르기를 그만두었다가 다시 시작하던 끝에, 내 후견인이 건반 위에 손을 얹어 스킴폴 씨의 연주를 멈추게 했습니다."], ["“I don’t like this, Skimpole,”", "\"이건 마음에 들지 않소, 스킴폴.\""], ["he said thoughtfully.", "그가 생각에 잠겨 말했습니다."]], [["Mr. Skimpole, who had quite forgotten the subject, looked up surprised.", "그 화제를 까맣게 잊고 있던 스킴폴 씨가 놀라며 고개를 들었습니다."]], [["“The man was necessary,”", "\"그 사람은 꼭 필요한 사람이었소.\""], ["pursued my guardian, walking backward and forward in the very short space between the piano and the end of the room and rubbing his hair up from the back of his head as if a high east wind had blown it into that form.", "내 후견인이 말을 이으며, 피아노와 방 끝 사이의 아주 좁은 공간을 앞뒤로 오가면서, 거센 동풍이 그렇게 불어 놓기라도 한 듯 뒤통수 쪽 머리카락을 위로 쓸어 올렸습니다."], ["“If we make such men necessary by our faults and follies, or by our want of worldly knowledge, or by our misfortunes, we must not revenge ourselves upon them.", "\"우리가 우리의 잘못과 어리석음으로, 혹은 세상 물정에 어두워서, 혹은 우리의 불행 탓에 그런 사람들을 필요로 만들어 놓고서는, 그들에게 앙갚음을 해서는 안 되오."], ["There was no harm in his trade.", "그의 직업에는 잘못이 없었소."], ["He maintained his children.", "그는 제 아이들을 먹여 살렸소."], ["One would like to know more about this.”", "이 일을 좀 더 알아보고 싶구려.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Coavinses?” cried Mr.", "코빈시스 말이오?\""], ["Skimpole, at length perceiving what he meant.", "스킴폴 씨가 마침내 그 뜻을 알아채고 소리쳤습니다."], ["“Nothing easier.", "\"그보다 쉬운 일이 없지요."], ["A walk to Coavinses’ headquarters, and you can know what you will.”", "코빈시스의 본거지까지 걸어가면, 당신이 알고 싶은 걸 다 알 수 있습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce nodded to us, who were only waiting for the signal.", "잔다이스 씨가 신호만 기다리던 우리에게 고갯짓을 했습니다."], ["“Come!", "\"자!"], ["We will walk that way, my dears.", "그쪽으로 걸어가 보자, 얘들아."], ["Why not that way as soon as another!”", "다른 데보다 그쪽으로 가지 못할 까닭이 어디 있겠느냐!\""], ["We were quickly ready and went out. Mr.", "우리는 얼른 채비를 하고 밖으로 나섰습니다."], ["Skimpole went with us and quite enjoyed the expedition.", "스킴폴 씨도 우리와 함께 가며 그 행차를 무척 즐거워했습니다."], ["It was so new and so refreshing, he said, for him to want Coavinses instead of Coavinses wanting him!", "코빈시스가 자기를 찾는 게 아니라 자기가 코빈시스를 찾으니, 너무도 새롭고 너무도 상쾌하다고 그가 말했습니다!"]], [["He took us, first, to Cursitor Street, Chancery Lane, where there was a house with barred windows, which he called Coavinses’ Castle.", "그는 우리를 먼저 챈서리 길의 커시터 거리로 데려갔는데, 거기에는 창살을 두른 창문이 달린 집 한 채가 있었습니다. 그는 그곳을 코빈시스의 성이라고 불렀습니다."], ["On our going into the entry and ringing a bell, a very hideous boy came out of a sort of office and looked at us over a spiked wicket.", "우리가 입구로 들어가 종을 울리자, 사무실 비슷한 곳에서 아주 흉하게 생긴 소년 하나가 나와 끝이 뾰족한 쪽문 너머로 우리를 쳐다보았습니다."]], [["“Who did you want?”", "\"누굴 찾으세요?\""], ["said the boy, fitting two of the spikes into his chin.", "소년이 뾰족한 살 두 개를 제 턱에 끼우며 말했습니다."]], [["“There was a follower, or an officer, or something, here,” said Mr.", "\"여기 추심꾼인지, 집행관인지, 뭐 그런 사람이 하나 있었는데,\""], ["Jarndyce, “who is dead.”", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"죽었다더구나.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"네?\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했습니다."], ["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“I want to know his name, if you please?”", "\"괜찮다면 그 사람 이름을 알고 싶구나.\""]], [["“Name of Neckett,”", "\"네킷이란 사람이에요.\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했습니다."]], [["“And his address?”", "\"주소는?\""]], [["“Bell Yard,”", "\"벨 야드요.\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했습니다."], ["“Chandler’s shop, left hand side, name of Blinder.”", "\"양초 가게, 왼쪽 편, 블라인더라는 사람.\""]], [["“Was he—I don’t know how to shape the question—”", "\"그 사람은, 어떻게 물어야 할지 모르겠다만,\""], ["murmured my guardian, “industrious?”", "내 후견인이 중얼거렸습니다. \"부지런했느냐?\""]], [["“Was Neckett?”", "\"네킷 말이에요?\""], ["said the boy.", "소년이 말했습니다."], ["“Yes, wery much so.", "\"네, 아주 그랬죠."], ["He was never tired of watching.", "망보는 데는 지칠 줄을 몰랐어요."], ["He’d set upon a post at a street corner eight or ten hours at a stretch if he undertook to do it.”", "한번 맡으면 길모퉁이 기둥에 여덟 시간이고 열 시간이고 내처 붙어 있었다니까요.\""]], [["“He might have done worse,”", "\"그보다 못할 수도 있었겠지.\""], ["I heard my guardian soliloquize.", "내 후견인이 혼잣말하는 것을 나는 들었습니다."], ["“He might have undertaken to do it and not done it.", "\"맡고도 안 할 수도 있었을 테니. 고맙구나."], ["Thank you. That’s all I want.”", "내가 알고 싶은 건 그게 다란다.\""]], [["We left the boy, with his head on one side and his arms on the gate, fondling and sucking the spikes, and went back to Lincoln’s Inn, where Mr.", "우리는 머리를 한쪽으로 갸웃하고 두 팔을 문에 걸친 채 뾰족한 살을 어루만지고 빨아 대는 소년을 남겨 두고, 링컨 법학원으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["Skimpole, who had not cared to remain nearer Coavinses, awaited us.", "코빈시스에 더 가까이 머물고 싶어 하지 않던 스킴폴 씨가 거기서 우리를 기다리고 있었습니다."], ["Then we all went to Bell Yard, a narrow alley at a very short distance.", "그러고 나서 우리는 다 같이 벨 야드로 갔는데, 아주 가까운 거리에 있는 좁은 골목이었습니다."], ["We soon found the chandler’s shop.", "우리는 곧 양초 가게를 찾아냈습니다."], ["In it was a good-natured-looking old woman with a dropsy, or an asthma, or perhaps both.", "그 안에는 사람 좋아 보이는 노파가 있었는데, 부종을 앓는지, 천식을 앓는지, 어쩌면 둘 다였습니다."]], [["“Neckett’s children?”", "\"네킷네 아이들이오?\""], ["said she in reply to my inquiry.", "노파가 내 물음에 대답했습니다."], ["“Yes, Surely, miss.", "\"네, 그럼요, 아가씨."], ["Three pair, if you please.", "괜찮으시다면 삼 층이오."], ["Door right opposite the stairs.”", "계단 바로 맞은편 문이지요.\""], ["And she handed me the key across the counter.", "그러고는 계산대 너머로 나에게 열쇠를 건넸습니다."]], [["I glanced at the key and glanced at her, but she took it for granted that I knew what to do with it.", "나는 열쇠를 한 번 보고 노파를 한 번 보았지만, 노파는 내가 그걸 어떻게 쓰는지 안다고 당연하게 여겼습니다."], ["As it could only be intended for the children’s door, I came out without asking any more questions and led the way up the dark stairs.", "그것이 아이들 방문에 쓰라는 것일 수밖에 없었으므로, 나는 더 묻지 않고 나와 어두운 계단으로 앞장서 올라갔습니다."], ["We went as quietly as we could, but four of us made some noise on the aged boards, and when we came to the second story we found we had disturbed a man who was standing there looking out of his room.", "우리는 되도록 조용히 올라갔지만, 넷이나 되다 보니 낡은 마룻널에서 소리가 좀 났고, 이 층에 이르렀을 때는 우리가 자기 방에서 밖을 내다보며 서 있던 한 남자를 방해한 것을 알았습니다."]], [["“Is it Gridley that’s wanted?”", "\"찾는 게 그리들리요?\""], ["he said, fixing his eyes on me with an angry stare.", "그가 나에게 성난 눈길을 박으며 말했습니다."]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아닙니다, 선생님.\""], ["said I; “I am going higher up.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"저는 더 위로 올라갑니다.\""]], [["He looked at Ada, and at Mr. Jarndyce, and at Mr.", "그는 에이다를, 잔다이스 씨를, 스킴폴 씨를 차례로 쳐다보았는데, 그들이 나를 지나 뒤따라 올라갈 때마다 똑같은 성난 눈길을 하나하나에게 박았습니다."], ["Skimpole, fixing the same angry stare on each in succession as they passed and followed me. Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 그에게 인사를 건넸습니다."], ["Jarndyce gave him good day.", "\"안녕하시오!\""], ["“Good day!” he said abruptly and fiercely.", "그가 퉁명스럽고 사납게 말했습니다."], ["He was a tall, sallow man with a careworn head on which but little hair remained, a deeply lined face, and prominent eyes.", "그는 키가 크고 누르스름한 남자로, 근심에 찌든 머리에는 머리카락이 얼마 남지 않았고, 얼굴에는 깊은 주름이 잡혀 있었으며, 눈이 툭 불거져 있었습니다."], ["He had a combative look and a chafing, irritable manner which, associated with his figure—still large and powerful, though evidently in its decline—rather alarmed me.", "호전적인 표정과 부글부글 끓어오르는 신경질적인 태도가, 비록 한물간 기색이 역력하지만 여전히 크고 우람한 그 몸집과 어우러져 나를 적잖이 겁먹게 했습니다."], ["He had a pen in his hand, and in the glimpse I caught of his room in passing, I saw that it was covered with a litter of papers.", "그는 손에 펜을 쥐고 있었고, 지나치며 흘끗 본 그의 방은 어지러이 흩어진 종이 더미로 뒤덮여 있었습니다."]], [["Leaving him standing there, we went up to the top room.", "우리는 그를 거기 세워 둔 채 맨 위층 방으로 올라갔습니다."], ["I tapped at the door, and a little shrill voice inside said, “We are locked in.", "내가 문을 두드리자, 안에서 작고 새된 목소리가 말했습니다. \"우린 갇혀 있어요."], ["Mrs. Blinder’s got the key!”", "블라인더 부인이 열쇠를 가지고 있어요!\""]], [["I applied the key on hearing this and opened the door.", "나는 이 말을 듣고 열쇠를 끼워 문을 열었습니다."], ["In a poor room with a sloping ceiling and containing very little furniture was a mite of a boy, some five or six years old, nursing and hushing a heavy child of eighteen months.", "천장이 비스듬하게 기울고 가구라곤 거의 없는 초라한 방 안에, 다섯이나 여섯 살쯤 된 자그마한 사내아이 하나가 열여덟 달쯤 된 묵직한 아기를 안고 어르며 달래고 있었습니다."], ["There was no fire, though the weather was cold; both children were wrapped in some poor shawls and tippets as a substitute.", "날씨가 추운데도 불은 피워져 있지 않았습니다. 두 아이는 그 대신 허름한 숄과 목도리를 둘둘 두르고 있었습니다."], ["Their clothing was not so warm, however, but that their noses looked red and pinched and their small figures shrunken as the boy walked up and down nursing and hushing the child with its head on his shoulder.", "그래도 옷이 그리 따뜻하지는 않은지, 사내아이가 아기를 어깨에 기대게 한 채 어르고 달래며 이리저리 걷는 동안, 두 아이의 코는 빨갛게 얼어붙어 보였고 작은 몸은 잔뜩 움츠러들어 있었습니다."]], [["“Who has locked you up here alone?”", "\"누가 너희끼리만 여기 가두어 놓았니?\""], ["we naturally asked.", "우리는 당연히 물었습니다."]], [["“Charley,”", "\"찰리요.\""], ["said the boy, standing still to gaze at us.", "사내아이가 멈춰 서서 우리를 빤히 쳐다보며 말했습니다."]], [["“Is Charley your brother?”", "\"찰리가 네 형이니?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["She’s my sister, Charlotte.", "누나예요, 샬럿이요."], ["Father called her Charley.”", "아버지가 찰리라고 불렀어요.\""]], [["“Are there any more of you besides Charley?”", "\"찰리 말고 너희가 또 있니?\""]], [["“Me,”", "\"저요.\""], ["said the boy, “and Emma,”", "사내아이가 말했습니다. \"그리고 엠마요.\""], ["patting the limp bonnet of the child he was nursing.", "안고 있던 아기의 축 늘어진 보닛을 토닥이며 말했습니다."], ["“And Charley.”", "\"그리고 찰리요.\""]], [["“Where is Charley now?”", "\"찰리는 지금 어디 있니?\""]], [["“Out a-washing,”", "\"빨래하러 나갔어요.\""], ["said the boy, beginning to walk up and down again and taking the nankeen bonnet much too near the bedstead by trying to gaze at us at the same time.", "사내아이가 다시 이리저리 걷기 시작하며 말했습니다. 동시에 우리를 빤히 보려다 보니 누런 무명 보닛을 침대 틀에 너무 가까이 들이밀었습니다."]], [["We were looking at one another and at these two children when there came into the room a very little girl, childish in figure but shrewd and older-looking in the face—pretty-faced too—wearing a womanly sort of bonnet much too large for her and drying her bare arms on a womanly sort of apron.", "우리가 서로를 보고 또 이 두 아이를 보고 있을 때, 아주 어린 여자아이 하나가 방으로 들어왔습니다. 몸집은 어린아이였지만 얼굴은 영악하고 나이 들어 보였습니다."], ["Her fingers were white and wrinkled with washing, and the soap-suds were yet smoking which she wiped off her arms.", "게다가 얼굴은 예뻤습니다. 자기에게는 너무 큰 어른 여자용 보닛을 쓰고, 어른 여자용 앞치마에다 맨팔을 닦으며 들어왔습니다."], ["But for this, she might have been a child playing at washing and imitating a poor working-woman with a quick observation of the truth.", "손가락은 빨래로 하얗게 쪼글쪼글했고, 팔에서 닦아 낸 비눗물 거품은 아직도 김이 나고 있었습니다. 그것만 아니었다면, 진실을 빠르게 관찰하여 가난한 일하는 여자를 흉내 내며 빨래 놀이를 하는 어린아이로 보였을 것입니다."]], [["She had come running from some place in the neighbourhood and had made all the haste she could.", "여자아이는 동네 어딘가에서 뛰어와 있는 힘껏 서두른 참이었습니다."], ["Consequently, though she was very light, she was out of breath and could not speak at first, as she stood panting, and wiping her arms, and looking quietly at us.", "그래서 몸이 무척 가벼운데도 숨이 차서, 처음에는 말도 하지 못한 채 헐떡이며 서서 팔을 닦으며 조용히 우리를 바라보았습니다."]], [["“Oh, here’s Charley!”", "\"아, 찰리가 왔다!\""], ["said the boy.", "사내아이가 말했습니다."]], [["The child he was nursing stretched forth its arms and cried out to be taken by Charley.", "사내아이가 안고 있던 아기가 두 팔을 내밀며 찰리에게 안기겠다고 울어 댔습니다."], ["The little girl took it, in a womanly sort of manner belonging to the apron and the bonnet, and stood looking at us over the burden that clung to her most affectionately.", "여자아이는 앞치마와 보닛에 어울리는 어른 여자 같은 몸짓으로 아기를 받아 안고, 더없이 다정하게 자기에게 매달리는 짐을 안은 채 우리를 바라보며 서 있었습니다."]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"이게 가능한 일이오,\""], ["whispered my guardian as we put a chair for the little creature and got her to sit down with her load, the boy keeping close to her, holding to her apron, “that this child works for the rest?", "우리가 그 작은 아이에게 의자를 갖다 주고 그 짐을 안은 채 앉게 했을 때, 사내아이가 누나 곁에 바짝 붙어 앞치마를 붙들고 있는 가운데, 내 후견인이 속삭였습니다. \"이 아이가 나머지를 위해 일을 한다는 게?"], ["Look at this!", "이것 좀 보시오!"], ["For God’s sake, look at this!”", "제발, 이것 좀 보란 말이오!\""]], [["It was a thing to look at.", "그것은 정말 볼 만한 광경이었습니다."], ["The three children close together, and two of them relying solely on the third, and the third so young and yet with an air of age and steadiness that sat so strangely on the childish figure.", "세 아이가 바짝 붙어 있고, 그중 둘은 오로지 셋째에게만 의지하며, 그 셋째는 그토록 어리면서도 어린 몸에 너무도 낯설게 어울리는, 어른스럽고 의젓한 분위기를 풍기고 있었습니다."]], [["“Charley, Charley!”", "\"찰리, 찰리야!\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“How old are you?”", "\"너 몇 살이니?\""]], [["“Over thirteen, sir,”", "\"열세 살 넘었어요, 선생님.\""], ["replied the child.", "아이가 대답했습니다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["What a great age,”", "정말 대단한 나이로구나.\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“What a great age, Charley!”", "\"정말 대단한 나이야, 찰리!\""]], [["I cannot describe the tenderness with which he spoke to her, half playfully yet all the more compassionately and mournfully.", "그가 아이에게 말하던 그 다정함을, 절반은 장난스러우면서도 그래서 더욱 가엾고 애틋했던 그 어조를 나는 이루 다 그려 낼 수가 없습니다."]], [["“And do you live alone here with these babies, Charley?”", "\"그래서 너는 이 아기들과 여기서 혼자 사는 거니, 찰리?\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 선생님.\""], ["returned the child, looking up into his face with perfect confidence, “since father died.”", "아이가 더없이 굳은 믿음으로 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 대답했습니다. \"아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로요.\""]], [["“And how do you live, Charley?", "\"그럼 어떻게 살아가니, 찰리?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Charley,”", "찰리야,\""], ["said my guardian, turning his face away for a moment, “how do you live?”", "내 후견인이 잠깐 얼굴을 돌리며 말했습니다. \"어떻게 살아가는 거니?\""]], [["“Since father died, sir, I’ve gone out to work.", "\"아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 일을 나가요, 선생님."], ["I’m out washing to-day.”", "오늘도 빨래하러 나갔어요.\""]], [["“God help you, Charley!”", "\"하느님이 너를 도우시기를, 찰리!\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“You’re not tall enough to reach the tub!”", "\"넌 키가 작아서 빨래통에도 손이 안 닿을 텐데!\""]], [["“In pattens I am, sir,”", "\"나막신을 신으면 닿아요, 선생님.\""], ["she said quickly.", "아이가 얼른 말했습니다."], ["“I’ve got a high pair as belonged to mother.”", "\"어머니가 신던 굽 높은 나막신이 있거든요.\""]], [["“And when did mother die?", "\"그럼 어머니는 언제 돌아가셨니?"], ["Poor mother!”", "가엾은 어머니!\""]], [["“Mother died just after Emma was born,”", "\"어머니는 엠마가 태어나고 얼마 안 돼서 돌아가셨어요.\""], ["said the child, glancing at the face upon her bosom.", "아이가 제 가슴에 안긴 얼굴을 흘끗 보며 말했습니다."], ["“Then father said I was to be as good a mother to her as I could.", "\"그러고 나서 아버지가, 내가 할 수 있는 만큼 좋은 엄마 노릇을 해 주라고 하셨어요."], ["And so I tried.", "그래서 그러려고 했어요."], ["And so I worked at home and did cleaning and nursing and washing for a long time before I began to go out.", "그래서 한참 동안 집에서 일하면서 청소하고 아기 보고 빨래하다가, 그러고 나서 밖에 일 나가기 시작했어요."], ["And that’s how I know how; don’t you see, sir?”", "그래서 할 줄 알아요. 그렇잖아요, 선생님?\""]], [["“And do you often go out?”", "\"그럼 너는 자주 나가니?\""]], [["“As often as I can,”", "\"할 수 있는 만큼 자주요.\""], ["said Charley, opening her eyes and smiling, “because of earning sixpences and shillings!”", "찰리가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 웃으며 말했습니다. \"육 펜스도 벌고 일 실링도 버니까요!\""]], [["“And do you always lock the babies up when you go out?”", "\"그럼 나갈 때는 늘 아기들을 가둬 두니?\""]], [["“To keep ’em safe, sir, don’t you see?” said Charley.", "\"안전하게 지키려고요, 그렇잖아요, 선생님?\""], ["“Mrs. Blinder comes up now and then, and Mr.", "찰리가 말했습니다."], ["Gridley comes up sometimes, and perhaps I can run in sometimes, and they can play you know, and Tom an’t afraid of being locked up, are you, Tom?”", "\"블라인더 부인이 가끔 올라와 보시고, 그리들리 아저씨도 가끔 올라오시고, 어쩌면 저도 가끔 잠깐 들를 수 있고, 또 얘들끼리 놀 수도 있고요. 그리고 톰은 갇혀 있는 걸 무서워 안 해요, 그렇지, 톰?\""]], [["“No-o!”", "\"으응!\""], ["said Tom stoutly.", "톰이 씩씩하게 말했습니다."]], [["“When it comes on dark, the lamps are lighted down in the court, and they show up here quite bright—almost quite bright.", "\"어두워지면 안마당 아래쪽에 등불이 켜지는데, 그게 여기까지 아주 환하게 비춰요. 거의 아주 환하게요."], ["Don’t they, Tom?”", "그렇지, 톰?\""]], [["“Yes, Charley,”", "\"응, 찰리.\""], ["said Tom, “almost quite bright.”", "톰이 말했습니다. \"거의 아주 환해.\""]], [["“Then he’s as good as gold,”", "\"그러면 얘는 정말 착하게 굴어요.\""], ["said the little creature—Oh, in such a motherly, womanly way!", "그 작은 아이가 말했습니다. 오, 어쩜 그토록 엄마같이, 어른 여자같이 말하던지요!"], ["“And when Emma’s tired, he puts her to bed.", "\"그리고 엠마가 졸리면 얘가 재워 줘요."], ["And when he’s tired he goes to bed himself.", "그리고 자기가 졸리면 스스로 잠자리에 들고요."], ["And when I come home and light the candle and has a bit of supper, he sits up again and has it with me.", "그리고 제가 집에 와서 촛불을 켜고 저녁을 좀 먹으면, 얘가 다시 일어나서 저랑 같이 먹어요."], ["Don’t you, Tom?”", "그렇지, 톰?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, Charley!”", "\"응, 찰리!\""], ["said Tom. “That I do!”", "톰이 말했습니다."], ["And either in this glimpse of the great pleasure of his life or in gratitude and love for Charley, who was all in all to him, he laid his face among the scanty folds of her frock and passed from laughing into crying.", "\"그렇게 해!\" 그러고는 자기 인생의 가장 큰 즐거움을 이렇게 언뜻 떠올린 탓인지, 아니면 자기에게 전부나 다름없는 찰리를 향한 고마움과 사랑 때문인지, 그는 누나 옷자락의 빈약한 주름 사이에 얼굴을 파묻고 웃다가 울음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["It was the first time since our entry that a tear had been shed among these children.", "우리가 들어온 뒤로 이 아이들 사이에서 눈물이 흘러내린 것은 그때가 처음이었습니다."], ["The little orphan girl had spoken of their father and their mother as if all that sorrow were subdued by the necessity of taking courage, and by her childish importance in being able to work, and by her bustling busy way.", "어린 고아 소녀는 마치 그 모든 슬픔이 용기를 내야 하는 절박함에, 일을 할 수 있다는 어린아이다운 자부심에, 그리고 부산스레 분주한 제 몸가짐에 가라앉기라도 한 듯 아버지와 어머니 이야기를 했습니다."], ["But now, when Tom cried, although she sat quite tranquil, looking quietly at us, and did not by any movement disturb a hair of the head of either of her little charges, I saw two silent tears fall down her face.", "그러나 이제 톰이 울자, 비록 그녀는 더없이 차분히 앉아 조용히 우리를 바라보며 작은 두 아이의 머리카락 한 올도 흐트러뜨리지 않았지만, 나는 소리 없는 두 줄기 눈물이 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["I stood at the window with Ada, pretending to look at the housetops, and the blackened stack of chimneys, and the poor plants, and the birds in little cages belonging to the neighbours, when I found that Mrs. Blinder, from the shop below, had come in (perhaps it had taken her all this time to get upstairs) and was talking to my guardian.", "나는 에이다와 함께 창가에 서서 지붕들과 시커멓게 그을린 굴뚝 무더기와 가엾은 화초들과 이웃집의 작은 새장에 든 새들을 보는 척하다가, 아래층 가게에서 블라인더 부인이 들어와(아마 계단을 올라오는 데만 이만큼 시간이 걸렸겠지요) 내 후견인과 이야기하고 있는 것을 알아챘습니다."]], [["“It’s not much to forgive ’em the rent, sir,”", "\"집세를 면해 주는 건 별것 아니에요, 선생님.\""], ["she said; “who could take it from them!”", "부인이 말했습니다. \"누가 저 애들한테 집세를 받겠어요!\""]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"그래요, 그래요!\""], ["said my guardian to us two.", "내 후견인이 우리 둘에게 말했습니다. \"언젠가 이 착한 부인이, 그것이 별것이었음을 깨닫게 될 때가 오리라는 것만으로 충분하오."], ["“It is enough that the time will come when this good woman will find that it WAS much, and that forasmuch as she did it unto the least of these—This child,”", "그리고 이 보잘것없는 사람 가운데 하나에게 베푼 것이니 곧... 이 아이가,\""], ["he added after a few moments, “could she possibly continue this?”", "그가 잠시 후 덧붙였습니다. \"정말로 이 일을 계속할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Really, sir, I think she might,” said Mrs.", "\"정말이지, 선생님, 제 생각엔 할 수 있을 것 같아요.\""], ["Blinder, getting her heavy breath by painful degrees.", "블라인더 부인이 가쁜 숨을 괴롭게 한 모금씩 몰아쉬며 말했습니다."], ["“She’s as handy as it’s possible to be.", "\"저렇게 야무진 애가 없어요."], ["Bless you, sir, the way she tended them two children after the mother died was the talk of the yard!", "아이고 선생님, 어머니가 죽은 뒤에 저 애가 두 아이를 보살핀 솜씨는 이 골목의 화젯거리였답니다! 그리고 아버지가 병들고 나서 저 애가 아버지를 돌보는 모습은 정말이지 보기에 놀라웠어요, 정말로요!"], ["And it was a wonder to see her with him after he was took ill, it really was! ‘Mrs.", "'블라인더 부인,'"], ["Blinder,’ he said to me the very last he spoke—he was lying there—‘Mrs.", "그이가 마지막으로 한 말이 이거였어요. 거기 누운 채로요."], ["Blinder, whatever my calling may have been, I see a angel sitting in this room last night along with my child, and I trust her to Our Father!’”", "'블라인더 부인, 내 직업이 무엇이었든, 어젯밤 이 방에 천사가 내 아이와 함께 앉아 있는 걸 보았소. 나는 저 애를 우리 아버지께 맡깁니다!'\""]], [["“He had no other calling?”", "\"다른 직업은 없었소?\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“No, sir,” returned Mrs.", "\"없었어요, 선생님.\""], ["Blinder, “he was nothing but a follerers.", "블라인더 부인이 대답했습니다. \"그이는 그저 추심꾼일 뿐이었지요."], ["When he first came to lodge here, I didn’t know what he was, and I confess that when I found out I gave him notice.", "처음 여기 세 들어 왔을 때는 그이가 뭐 하는 사람인지 몰랐는데, 솔직히 말씀드리면 알고 나서는 나가라고 통보했답니다. 골목에서 좋아하지들 않았거든요."], ["It wasn’t liked in the yard.", "다른 세입자들도 못마땅해했고요."], ["It wasn’t approved by the other lodgers.", "그건 점잖은 직업이 아니라서요.\""], ["It is NOT a genteel calling,” said Mrs.", "블라인더 부인이 말했습니다."], ["Blinder, “and most people do object to it. Mr.", "\"사람들 대부분이 싫어하지요."], ["Gridley objected to it very strong, and he is a good lodger, though his temper has been hard tried.”", "그리들리 씨가 아주 강하게 싫어했는데, 그래도 그분은 좋은 세입자예요. 성미가 모질게 단련되긴 했지만요.\""]], [["“So you gave him notice?”", "\"그래서 나가라고 통보했소?\""], ["said my guardian.", "내 후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“So I gave him notice,”", "\"그래서 나가라고 통보했지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Blinder.", "블라인더 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“But really when the time came, and I knew no other ill of him, I was in doubts.", "\"그런데 막상 때가 되고 보니, 그이한테 다른 나쁜 점이라곤 알지 못해서 망설여지더군요."], ["He was punctual and diligent; he did what he had to do, sir,” said Mrs.", "그이는 시간을 잘 지키고 부지런했어요."], ["Blinder, unconsciously fixing Mr.", "자기가 할 일을 했지요, 선생님.\""], ["Skimpole with her eye, “and it’s something in this world even to do that.”", "블라인더 부인이 자기도 모르게 스킴폴 씨에게 눈길을 박으며 말했습니다. \"이 세상에서 그것만 해도 어디예요.\""]], [["“So you kept him after all?”", "\"그래서 결국 그 사람을 데리고 있었소?\""]], [["“Why, I said that if he could arrange with Mr.", "\"그러니까요, 제가 그랬지요."], ["Gridley, I could arrange it with the other lodgers and should not so much mind its being liked or disliked in the yard.", "그이가 그리들리 씨하고 잘 풀 수 있다면, 다른 세입자들하고는 내가 잘 처리할 수 있고, 골목에서 좋아하든 싫어하든 그렇게까지 마음 쓰지는 않겠다고요."], ["Mr. Gridley gave his consent gruff—but gave it.", "그리들리 씨가 퉁명스럽게 허락하긴 했지만, 어쨌든 허락했어요."], ["He was always gruff with him, but he has been kind to the children since.", "그분은 늘 그이한테 퉁명스러웠지만, 그래도 아이들한테는 그 뒤로 다정하게 대해 주셨어요."], ["A person is never known till a person is proved.”", "사람은 겪어 보기 전에는 도무지 알 수가 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["“Have many people been kind to the children?”", "\"아이들한테 다정하게 대해 준 사람이 많았소?\""], ["asked Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 물었습니다."]], [["“Upon the whole, not so bad, sir,” said Mrs.", "\"대체로 그리 나쁘진 않았어요, 선생님.\""], ["Blinder; “but certainly not so many as would have been if their father’s calling had been different.", "블라인더 부인이 말했습니다. \"하지만 아버지 직업이 달랐다면 그랬을 만큼 많지는 분명 않았지요."], ["Mr. Coavins gave a guinea, and the follerers made up a little purse.", "코빈스 씨가 일 기니를 주었고, 추심꾼들이 푼돈을 모아 주었어요."], ["Some neighbours in the yard that had always joked and tapped their shoulders when he went by came forward with a little subscription, and—in general—not so bad.", "그이가 지나갈 때면 늘 농담을 던지고 어깨를 툭툭 치던 골목 이웃 몇몇이 나서서 자그마한 모금을 해 주었고요. 그래서 대체로 그리 나쁘진 않았어요."], ["Similarly with Charlotte.", "샬럿도 마찬가지예요."], ["Some people won’t employ her because she was a follerer’s child; some people that do employ her cast it at her; some make a merit of having her to work for them, with that and all her draw-backs upon her, and perhaps pay her less and put upon her more.", "어떤 사람들은 추심꾼의 자식이라고 저 애를 안 쓰고, 또 어떤 사람들은 부려 먹으면서도 그걸 들먹이며 면박을 주고, 어떤 사람들은 그 온갖 흠을 다 안은 저 애를 일 시켜 주는 걸 무슨 선심이라도 베푸는 양 굴면서, 어쩌면 품삯은 덜 주고 일은 더 시키지요."], ["But she’s patienter than others would be, and is clever too, and always willing, up to the full mark of her strength and over.", "그래도 저 애는 다른 애들보다 참을성이 많고, 영리하기도 하고, 늘 제 힘이 닿는 데까지, 그 이상으로 기꺼이 한답니다."], ["So I should say, in general, not so bad, sir, but might be better.”", "그러니 대체로 그리 나쁘진 않았다고 하겠지만, 더 나을 수도 있었겠지요, 선생님.\""]], [["Mrs. Blinder sat down to give herself a more favourable opportunity of recovering her breath, exhausted anew by so much talking before it was fully restored. Mr. Jarndyce was turning to speak to us when his attention was attracted by the abrupt entrance into the room of the Mr. Gridley who had been mentioned and whom we had seen on our way up.", "블라인더 부인은 채 가라앉지도 않은 숨을 그토록 많은 말로 다시 소진한 터라, 좀 더 편안히 숨을 고를 기회를 가지려 자리에 앉았습니다. 잔다이스 씨가 우리에게 말을 건네려고 돌아서는데, 앞서 이야기에 나왔고 우리가 올라오는 길에 보았던 바로 그 그리들리 씨가 불쑥 방으로 들어오는 바람에 그의 주의가 그쪽으로 쏠렸습니다."]], [["“I don’t know what you may be doing here, ladies and gentlemen,”", "\"여러분이 여기서 무얼 하시는지는 모르겠소만, 신사 숙녀 여러분,\""], ["he said, as if he resented our presence, “but you’ll excuse my coming in.", "그가 우리의 존재가 못마땅하다는 듯 말했습니다. \"내가 들어온 것을 양해해 주시오."], ["I don’t come in to stare about me.", "나는 두리번거리며 구경하러 온 게 아니오."], ["Well, Charley!", "자, 찰리!"], ["Well, Tom!", "자, 톰!"], ["Well, little one!", "자, 꼬맹아!"], ["How is it with us all to-day?”", "오늘 우리 모두 어떻게 지냈누?\""]], [["He bent over the group in a caressing way and clearly was regarded as a friend by the children, though his face retained its stern character and his manner to us was as rude as it could be.", "그는 어루만지듯 그 무리 위로 몸을 숙였고, 아이들에게는 분명 친구로 여겨지고 있었습니다. 비록 그의 얼굴은 여전히 험상궂은 빛을 띠고 우리를 대하는 태도는 더없이 무례했지만요."], ["My guardian noticed it and respected it.", "내 후견인은 그것을 알아채고 존중했습니다."]], [["“No one, surely, would come here to stare about him,”", "\"여기 두리번거리며 구경하러 올 사람은 분명 아무도 없을 게요.\""], ["he said mildly.", "그가 부드럽게 말했습니다."]], [["“May be so, sir, may be so,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지요, 선생, 그럴지도 모르지요.\""], ["returned the other, taking Tom upon his knee and waving him off impatiently.", "상대가 톰을 무릎에 앉혔다가 짜증스레 떨쳐 내며 대답했습니다."], ["“I don’t want to argue with ladies and gentlemen.", "\"나는 신사 숙녀들과 입씨름할 마음은 없소."], ["I have had enough of arguing to last one man his life.”", "입씨름이라면 한 사람 평생 몫으로 충분할 만큼 해 봤으니까.\""]], [["“You have sufficient reason, I dare say,”", "\"능히 그럴 만한 까닭이 있겠지요.\" 잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mr. Jarndyce, “for being chafed and irritated—”", "\"그렇게 부글부글 끓고 신경질이 날 만한—\""]], [["“There again!”", "\"또 그 소리!\""], ["exclaimed the man, becoming violently angry.", "남자가 사납게 화를 내며 외쳤습니다."], ["“I am of a quarrelsome temper.", "\"나는 싸움질하는 성미요."], ["I am irascible.", "화를 잘 내지."], ["I am not polite!”", "예의 같은 건 없소!\""]], [["“Not very, I think.”", "\"그리 예의 바르진 않으신 것 같소.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Gridley, putting down the child and going up to him as if he meant to strike him, “do you know anything of Courts of Equity?”", "그리들리가 아이를 내려놓고는 마치 후려치기라도 할 듯 그에게 다가서며 말했습니다. \"선생은 형평법 법정에 대해 뭐라도 아시오?\""]], [["“Perhaps I do, to my sorrow.”", "\"어쩌면 알지요, 가슴 아프게도.\""]], [["“To your sorrow?”", "\"가슴 아프게도?\""], ["said the man, pausing in his wrath, “if so, I beg your pardon.", "남자가 분노를 멈추며 말했습니다. \"그렇다면 미안하오."], ["I am not polite, I know.", "내가 예의가 없다는 건 나도 아오."], ["I beg your pardon!", "용서하시오!"], ["Sir,”", "선생,\""], ["with renewed violence, “I have been dragged for five and twenty years over burning iron, and I have lost the habit of treading upon velvet.", "다시 격렬해지며 말했습니다. \"나는 이십오 년을 불타는 쇠 위로 질질 끌려다녔고, 그래서 벨벳 위를 밟는 버릇은 잊어버렸소."], ["Go into the Court of Chancery yonder and ask what is one of the standing jokes that brighten up their business sometimes, and they will tell you that the best joke they have is the man from Shropshire.", "저쪽 대법원에 들어가서, 그들의 사무에 가끔 활기를 불어넣는 단골 농담이 무엇이냐고 물어보시오. 그러면 자기들이 가진 가장 좋은 농담은 슈롭셔에서 온 사람이라고 말해 줄 거요."], ["I,”", "내가,\""], ["he said, beating one hand on the other passionately, “am the man from Shropshire.”", "그가 한 손으로 다른 손을 격하게 내려치며 말했습니다. \"바로 그 슈롭셔에서 온 사람이오.\""]], [["“I believe I and my family have also had the honour of furnishing some entertainment in the same grave place,”", "\"나와 내 가족도 그 똑같은 엄숙한 곳에서 얼마간 웃음거리를 제공하는 영광을 누렸나 보오.\""], ["said my guardian composedly.", "내 후견인이 침착하게 말했습니다."], ["“You may have heard my name—Jarndyce.”", "\"내 이름을 들어 보셨을지도 모르겠소. 잔다이스요.\""]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 씨.\""], ["said Gridley with a rough sort of salutation, “you bear your wrongs more quietly than I can bear mine.", "그리들리가 거친 투로 인사를 건네며 말했습니다. \"당신은 나보다 자기 억울함을 더 조용히 견디시는구려."], ["More than that, I tell you—and I tell this gentleman, and these young ladies, if they are friends of yours—that if I took my wrongs in any other way, I should be driven mad!", "그뿐 아니라, 당신에게 말하고, 또 이 신사분과 이 젊은 아가씨들에게도, 당신의 벗이라면 말하리다. 내가 만약 내 억울함을 다른 식으로 받아들였다면 나는 미쳐 버렸을 거요!"], ["It is only by resenting them, and by revenging them in my mind, and by angrily demanding the justice I never get, that I am able to keep my wits together.", "그것에 분개하고, 마음속으로 거기에 앙갚음하고, 결코 얻지 못하는 정의를 노여워하며 요구하는 것, 오직 그것만으로 나는 제정신을 붙들어 둘 수 있소."], ["It is only that!”", "오직 그것뿐이오!\""], ["he said, speaking in a homely, rustic way and with great vehemence.", "그가 투박한 시골 사람 말투로, 무서운 격렬함을 띠고 말했습니다."], ["“You may tell me that I over-excite myself.", "\"당신은 내가 자신을 너무 흥분시킨다고 말할지 모르오."], ["I answer that it’s in my nature to do it, under wrong, and I must do it.", "나는 대답하리다. 억울함 앞에서 그렇게 하는 게 내 천성이고, 나는 그렇게 해야만 한다고."], ["There’s nothing between doing it, and sinking into the smiling state of the poor little mad woman that haunts the court.", "그렇게 하든가, 아니면 저 법정에 출몰하는 가엾은 미친 노파처럼 실실 웃는 상태로 가라앉든가, 둘 중 하나뿐이오."], ["If I was once to sit down under it, I should become imbecile.”", "내가 한 번이라도 그 밑에 주저앉는다면, 나는 백치가 되고 말 거요.\""]], [["The passion and heat in which he was, and the manner in which his face worked, and the violent gestures with which he accompanied what he said, were most painful to see.", "그가 휩싸인 격정과 열기, 일그러지던 얼굴, 말에 곁들이던 사나운 몸짓은 차마 보기가 더없이 고통스러웠습니다."]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 씨.\""], ["he said, “consider my case.", "그가 말했습니다. \"내 사정을 들어 보시오."], ["As true as there is a heaven above us, this is my case.", "우리 위에 하늘이 있다는 것이 진실이듯, 이것이 내 사정이오."], ["I am one of two brothers.", "나는 두 형제 가운데 하나요."], ["My father (a farmer) made a will and left his farm and stock and so forth to my mother for her life.", "우리 아버지는(농부였지요) 유언장을 만들어, 농장과 가축과 그 밖의 것들을 어머니에게 살아 계실 동안 남기셨소."], ["After my mother’s death, all was to come to me except a legacy of three hundred pounds that I was then to pay my brother.", "어머니가 돌아가신 뒤에는 모든 것이 나에게 오되, 다만 내가 그때 형에게 줄 삼백 파운드의 유산만은 빼고요."], ["My mother died.", "어머니가 돌아가셨소."], ["My brother some time afterwards claimed his legacy.", "형은 얼마 뒤에 자기 유산을 요구했소."], ["I and some of my relations said that he had had a part of it already in board and lodging and some other things.", "나와 친척 몇몇은, 형이 이미 식비와 숙박비, 그 밖의 몇 가지로 그 일부를 받았다고 말했소. 자, 잘 들으시오!"], ["Now mind! That was the question, and nothing else.", "그것이 쟁점이었고, 다른 건 아무것도 없었소."], ["No one disputed the will; no one disputed anything but whether part of that three hundred pounds had been already paid or not.", "누구도 유언장을 다투지 않았소. 그 삼백 파운드 가운데 일부가 이미 지불되었느냐 아니냐, 오직 그것만 다투었을 뿐이오."], ["To settle that question, my brother filing a bill, I was obliged to go into this accursed Chancery; I was forced there because the law forced me and would let me go nowhere else.", "그 문제를 가리려고, 형이 소장을 제출했고, 나는 이 저주받은 대법원으로 들어갈 수밖에 없었소. 법이 나를 거기로 몰아넣고 다른 어디로도 가지 못하게 했으니까."], ["Seventeen people were made defendants to that simple suit!", "그 단순한 소송에 피고로 세워진 사람이 열일곱이었소! 이 년 만에 처음 사건이 열렸소."], ["It first came on after two years.", "그러더니 또 이 년 동안 멈췄소."], ["It was then stopped for another two years while the master (may his head rot off!) inquired whether I was my father’s son, about which there was no dispute at all with any mortal creature.", "주심관이(그자의 머리가 썩어 떨어지기를!) 내가 우리 아버지의 아들이 맞는지 조사하는 동안 말이오. 그건 살아 있는 어떤 사람과도 전혀 다툴 여지가 없는 일인데도요."], ["He then found out that there were not defendants enough—remember, there were only seventeen as yet!—but that we must have another who had been left out and must begin all over again.", "그러더니 그자는 피고가 충분하지 않다는 걸 알아냈소. 잘 기억하시오, 아직 열일곱뿐이었는데도!"], ["The costs at that time—before the thing was begun!—were three times the legacy.", "빠진 사람이 하나 더 있으니 그를 마저 넣어야 하고, 모든 걸 처음부터 다시 시작해야 한다는 거였소. 그때까지 든 비용이, 일을 시작하기도 전에!"], ["My brother would have given up the legacy, and joyful, to escape more costs.", "그 유산의 세 배였소. 형은 더 드는 비용을 피하려고 유산을 기꺼이 포기하려 했소."], ["My whole estate, left to me in that will of my father’s, has gone in costs.", "우리 아버지의 그 유언장에 나에게 남겨진 내 전 재산이 비용으로 다 날아갔소."], ["The suit, still undecided, has fallen into rack, and ruin, and despair, with everything else—and here I stand, this day!", "아직도 판결이 나지 않은 그 소송은, 다른 모든 것과 함께 파멸과 폐허와 절망 속으로 빠져들었소. 그리고 나는, 오늘 이렇게 여기 서 있소!"], ["Now, Mr. Jarndyce, in your suit there are thousands and thousands involved, where in mine there are hundreds.", "자, 잔다이스 씨, 당신의 소송에는 수천 수만 파운드가 걸려 있지만, 내 소송에는 수백 파운드가 걸려 있을 뿐이오."], ["Is mine less hard to bear or is it harder to bear, when my whole living was in it and has been thus shamefully sucked away?”", "내 전 재산이 거기 걸려 있었고 이렇게 부끄럽게 빨려 나갔는데, 내 것은 견디기가 덜 힘들겠소, 아니면 더 힘들겠소?\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce said that he condoled with him with all his heart and that he set up no monopoly himself in being unjustly treated by this monstrous system.", "잔다이스 씨는 진심으로 그를 위로하며, 이 괴물 같은 제도에 부당하게 당하는 것을 자기 혼자 독차지하려는 게 아니라고 말했습니다."]], [["“There again!”", "\"또 그 소리!\""], ["said Mr. Gridley with no diminution of his rage.", "그리들리 씨가 노여움을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않고 말했습니다."], ["“The system!", "\"제도라니!"], ["I am told on all hands, it’s the system.", "사방에서들 나한테 그러는구려, 그게 제도라고."], ["I mustn’t look to individuals.", "개인들을 탓해선 안 된다고."], ["It’s the system.", "그게 제도라고."], ["I mustn’t go into court and say, ‘My Lord, I beg to know this from you—is this right or wrong?", "나는 법정에 들어가서 이렇게 말해선 안 된다는 거요. '판사님, 이것을 여쭙고자 합니다."], ["Have you the face to tell me I have received justice and therefore am dismissed?’ My Lord knows nothing of it.", "이게 옳습니까, 그릅니까? 제가 정의를 받았으니 그러므로 기각이라고 저한테 뻔뻔스레 말씀하실 수 있습니까?'"], ["He sits there to administer the system.", "판사님은 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르오."], ["I mustn’t go to Mr.", "그저 거기 앉아 제도를 집행할 뿐이지."], ["Tulkinghorn, the solicitor in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, and say to him when he makes me furious by being so cool and satisfied—as they all do, for I know they gain by it while I lose, don’t I?—I mustn’t say to him, ‘I will have something out of some one for my ruin, by fair means or foul!’ HE is not responsible.", "나는 링컨 법학원 광장의 변호사 토킹혼 씨에게 가서, 그자가 그토록 냉정하고 흡족한 꼴로 나를 미치게 만들 때—다들 그러는데, 내가 손해 보는 동안 그자들은 이득을 본다는 걸 내가 아니까, 그렇지 않소?—그자에게 이렇게 말해서도 안 된다는 거요. '나는 내 파멸의 대가를 누군가에게서 받아 내고야 말겠다, 정당한 수단으로든 부정한 수단으로든!'"], ["It’s the system.", "그자에게는 책임이 없다는 거지."], ["But, if I do no violence to any of them, here—I may!", "그게 제도라니까. 하지만 내가 여기서 그자들 누구에게도 폭력을 휘두르지 않는다면, 어쩌면 나도!"], ["I don’t know what may happen if I am carried beyond myself at last!", "마침내 내가 자제력을 잃고 미쳐 날뛰게 되면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 나도 모르겠소!"], ["I will accuse the individual workers of that system against me, face to face, before the great eternal bar!”", "나는 나에게 맞선 그 제도를 굴리는 개개의 일꾼들을, 얼굴을 맞대고, 위대하고 영원한 심판대 앞에서 고발하겠소!\""]], [["His passion was fearful.", "그의 격정은 무시무시했습니다."], ["I could not have believed in such rage without seeing it.", "직접 보지 않았다면 나는 그런 분노를 믿지 못했을 것입니다."]], [["“I have done!”", "\"다 했소!\""], ["he said, sitting down and wiping his face.", "그가 자리에 앉아 얼굴을 닦으며 말했습니다."], ["“Mr. Jarndyce, I have done!", "\"잔다이스 씨, 나는 다 했소!"], ["I am violent, I know.", "내가 사납다는 건 나도 아오."], ["I ought to know it.", "알아야 마땅하지."], ["I have been in prison for contempt of court.", "나는 법정 모욕죄로 감옥에 갔었소. 변호사를 협박한 죄로 감옥에 갔었소."], ["I have been in prison for threatening the solicitor.", "이런 곤경 저런 곤경을 겪었고, 또 겪을 거요."], ["I have been in this trouble, and that trouble, and shall be again.", "나는 슈롭셔에서 온 사람이오. 가끔은 그자들을 즐겁게 해 주는 정도를 넘어서기도 하지요."], ["I am the man from Shropshire, and I sometimes go beyond amusing them, though they have found it amusing, too, to see me committed into custody and brought up in custody and all that.", "비록 그자들은 내가 구금되고 구금된 채 끌려 나오고 하는 그 모든 꼴을 보는 게 즐겁다고들 여기지만요. 내가 자제하면 나한테 더 나을 거라고들 말합니다."], ["It would be better for me, they tell me, if I restrained myself.", "나는 그자들한테, 내가 만약 자제한다면 백치가 되고 말 거라고 대답하오."], ["I tell them that if I did restrain myself I should become imbecile.", "한때 나는 성미가 꽤 좋은 사람이었던 것 같소. 우리 고향 사람들은 그렇게 기억한다고들 하지만, 이제 나는 이 억울함 속에서 이렇게 분풀이를 하지 않으면 제정신을 붙들어 둘 수가 없소."], ["I was a good-enough-tempered man once, I believe.", "'그러느니 당신한테 훨씬 나을 게요, 그리들리 씨,'"], ["People in my part of the country say they remember me so, but now I must have this vent under my sense of injury or nothing could hold my wits together.", "지난주에 대법원장이 나한테 그럽디다. '여기서 시간을 허비하지 말고, 슈롭셔로 내려가 쓸모 있게 일하며 지내는 게.'"], ["It would be far better for you, Mr.", "'판사님, 판사님, 그러는 게 나으리란 걸 압니다,' 내가 그에게 말했소."], ["Gridley,’ the Lord Chancellor told me last week, ‘not to waste your time here, and to stay, usefully employed, down in Shropshire.’ ‘My Lord, my Lord, I know it would,’ said I to him, ‘and it would have been far better for me never to have heard the name of your high office, but unhappily for me, I can’t undo the past, and the past drives me here!’ Besides,”", "'그리고 판사님의 그 높은 직함을 애당초 듣지 않았더라면 나한테 훨씬 나았겠지요. 하지만 불행히도 나는 지난 일을 되돌릴 수가 없고, 그 지난 일이 나를 여기로 몰아대는 겁니다!'"], ["he added, breaking fiercely out, “I’ll shame them.", "게다가,\" 그가 사납게 터뜨리며 덧붙였습니다."], ["To the last, I’ll show myself in that court to its shame.", "\"나는 그자들에게 망신을 줄 거요. 끝까지, 나는 그 법정에 나타나 그 수치를 보여 주겠소."], ["If I knew when I was going to die, and could be carried there, and had a voice to speak with, I would die there, saying, ‘You have brought me here and sent me from here many and many a time.", "내가 언제 죽을지 알 수 있다면, 그리고 거기로 실려 갈 수 있고, 말할 목소리가 남아 있다면, 나는 거기서 죽으며 이렇게 말하겠소. '너희가 나를 몇 번이고 몇 번이고 여기로 끌고 왔다가 여기서 내보냈다."], ["Now send me out feet foremost!’”", "이제는 발부터 먼저 내보내거라!'\""]], [["His countenance had, perhaps for years, become so set in its contentious expression that it did not soften, even now when he was quiet.", "그의 얼굴은, 아마도 여러 해에 걸쳐, 그 싸우려 드는 표정으로 너무도 굳어 버린 탓에, 이렇게 잠잠해진 지금조차 부드러워지지 않았습니다."]], [["“I came to take these babies down to my room for an hour,”", "\"이 아기들을 한 시간쯤 내 방으로 데려가려고 왔소.\""], ["he said, going to them again, “and let them play about.", "그가 다시 아이들에게 다가가며 말했습니다. \"마음껏 놀게 하려고요."], ["I didn’t mean to say all this, but it don’t much signify.", "이런 말을 다 할 생각은 아니었소만, 뭐 그리 대수롭잖소."], ["You’re not afraid of me, Tom, are you?”", "너는 내가 무섭지 않지, 톰?\""]], [["“No!” said Tom.", "\"안 무서워요!\""], ["“You ain’t angry with ME.”", "톰이 말했습니다. \"아저씨는 나한테 화 안 내잖아요.\""]], [["“You are right, my child.", "\"옳거니, 우리 꼬맹이."], ["You’re going back, Charley?", "찰리 너는 돌아가는 거지?"], ["Aye?", "응?"], ["Come then, little one!”", "그럼 가자, 꼬맹아!\""], ["He took the youngest child on his arm, where she was willing enough to be carried.", "그는 가장 어린 아이를 팔에 안았는데, 아이도 안기는 것을 마다하지 않았습니다."], ["“I shouldn’t wonder if we found a ginger-bread soldier downstairs.", "\"아래층에 생강빵 병정이 있을지도 모르겠다."], ["Let’s go and look for him!”", "가서 찾아보자꾸나!\""]], [["He made his former rough salutation, which was not deficient in a certain respect, to Mr. Jarndyce, and bowing slightly to us, went downstairs to his room.", "그는 아까처럼 거친 인사를, 그래도 어떤 존중이 빠지지 않은 그 인사를 잔다이스 씨에게 건네고, 우리에게는 가볍게 고개 숙여 인사하고는, 아래층 자기 방으로 내려갔습니다."]], [["Upon that, Mr. Skimpole began to talk, for the first time since our arrival, in his usual gay strain.", "그러자 스킴폴 씨가, 우리가 도착한 뒤로 처음으로, 여느 때의 그 명랑한 가락으로 이야기하기 시작했습니다."], ["He said, Well, it was really very pleasant to see how things lazily adapted themselves to purposes.", "그는 말했습니다. 자, 세상일이 어찌 그리 게으르게 제 목적에 들어맞아 가는지 보는 것이 참으로 즐겁다고요."], ["Here was this Mr. Gridley, a man of a robust will and surprising energy—intellectually speaking, a sort of inharmonious blacksmith—and he could easily imagine that there Gridley was, years ago, wandering about in life for something to expend his superfluous combativeness upon—a sort of Young Love among the thorns—when the Court of Chancery came in his way and accommodated him with the exact thing he wanted.", "여기 이 그리들리 씨로 말하자면, 의지가 굳세고 정력이 놀라운 사람, 지적으로 말하자면 일종의 조화롭지 못한 대장장이인데, 그가 몇 해 전 인생을 떠돌며 남아도는 호전성을 쏟아부을 무언가를 찾아 헤매던 모습, 가시덤불 속의 어린 큐피드 같은 그 모습이 눈에 선하다는 것이었습니다."], ["There they were, matched, ever afterwards!", "그러다가 대법원이 그의 길에 나타나, 그가 원하던 바로 그것을 제공해 주었다는 것이지요. 그 후로 영영, 둘은 천생연분이었다!"], ["Otherwise he might have been a great general, blowing up all sorts of towns, or he might have been a great politician, dealing in all sorts of parliamentary rhetoric; but as it was, he and the Court of Chancery had fallen upon each other in the pleasantest way, and nobody was much the worse, and Gridley was, so to speak, from that hour provided for.", "그게 아니었다면 그는 온갖 도시를 폭파하는 위대한 장군이 되었거나, 아니면 온갖 의회식 수사를 늘어놓는 위대한 정치가가 되었을지도 모른다고요. 하지만 사정이 이러하여, 그와 대법원이 더없이 흐뭇한 방식으로 서로에게 맞닥뜨렸으니, 그리 손해 본 사람도 없고, 말하자면 그리들리는 그 시각부터 앞날이 마련된 셈이라는 것이었습니다."], ["Then look at Coavinses!", "그리고 코빈시스를 보라고요!"], ["How delightfully poor Coavinses (father of these charming children) illustrated the same principle! He, Mr.", "가엾은 코빈시스(이 사랑스러운 아이들의 아버지)가 그 똑같은 이치를 얼마나 멋지게 보여 주었는가!"], ["Skimpole, himself, had sometimes repined at the existence of Coavinses.", "자기, 곧 스킴폴 씨 본인도, 코빈시스의 존재를 가끔 원망했었다고요."], ["He had found Coavinses in his way.", "코빈시스가 자기 앞길을 가로막는다고 여겼다고요."], ["He could had dispensed with Coavinses.", "코빈시스 없이도 살 수 있었다고요."], ["There had been times when, if he had been a sultan, and his grand vizier had said one morning, “What does the Commander of the Faithful require at the hands of his slave?”", "만약 자기가 술탄이고, 어느 날 아침 대재상이 \"충성스러운 신도들의 지휘관께서는 노예의 손에서 무엇을 원하시나이까?\" 하고 물었다면, \"코빈시스의 목을!\""], ["he might have even gone so far as to reply, “The head of Coavinses!”", "하고 대답하기까지 했을 만한 때도 있었다고요."], ["But what turned out to be the case?", "하지만 알고 보니 어찌 된 일이었던가?"], ["That, all that time, he had been giving employment to a most deserving man, that he had been a benefactor to Coavinses, that he had actually been enabling Coavinses to bring up these charming children in this agreeable way, developing these social virtues!", "그 모든 세월 동안 자기가 더없이 마땅한 한 사람에게 일자리를 주고 있었다는 것, 자기가 코빈시스의 은인이었다는 것, 자기가 실은 코빈시스로 하여금 이 사랑스러운 아이들을 이렇게 흐뭇한 방식으로 길러 내고 이런 사회적 미덕을 키워 내게 해 주고 있었다는 것이었습니다!"], ["Insomuch that his heart had just now swelled and the tears had come into his eyes when he had looked round the room and thought, “I was the great patron of Coavinses, and his little comforts were MY work!”", "그리하여 방금 방 안을 둘러보며, \"내가 코빈시스의 위대한 후원자였고, 그가 누린 자그마한 안락이 내 작품이었구나!\" 하고 생각하니, 가슴이 벅차오르고 눈에 눈물이 고였다는 것이었습니다."]], [["There was something so captivating in his light way of touching these fantastic strings, and he was such a mirthful child by the side of the graver childhood we had seen, that he made my guardian smile even as he turned towards us from a little private talk with Mrs. Blinder.", "이 환상적인 현(絃)을 가볍게 튕기는 그의 태도에는 너무도 사람을 사로잡는 데가 있었고, 우리가 본 더 의젓한 어린아이 곁에서 그는 어찌나 명랑한 어린아이였던지, 블라인더 부인과 잠시 사사로운 이야기를 나누다 우리에게로 돌아서던 내 후견인마저 그 때문에 미소 짓게 만들었습니다."], ["We kissed Charley, and took her downstairs with us, and stopped outside the house to see her run away to her work.", "우리는 찰리에게 입을 맞추고, 그 애를 데리고 함께 아래층으로 내려가, 그 애가 일하러 뛰어가는 모습을 보려고 집 밖에 멈춰 섰습니다. 그 애가 어디로 가는지는 모르겠지만, 우리는 그 애가 뛰어가는 것을 보았습니다."], ["I don’t know where she was going, but we saw her run, such a little, little creature in her womanly bonnet and apron, through a covered way at the bottom of the court and melt into the city’s strife and sound like a dewdrop in an ocean. Tom-all-Alone’s", "어른 여자용 보닛과 앞치마를 두른 그토록 작고 작은 아이가, 안마당 끄트머리의 지붕 덮인 통로를 지나, 도시의 다툼과 소음 속으로, 마치 대양에 떨어진 이슬 한 방울처럼 녹아드는 것을요."]], [["My Lady Dedlock is restless, very restless.", "데드록 마님은 안절부절못한다, 도무지 가만있질 못한다."], ["The astonished fashionable intelligence hardly knows where to have her.", "깜짝 놀란 사교계 소식통은 마님을 어디서 붙잡아야 할지조차 가늠하지 못한다."], ["To-day she is at Chesney Wold; yesterday she was at her house in town; to-morrow she may be abroad, for anything the fashionable intelligence can with confidence predict.", "오늘은 체스니 월드에 있고, 어제는 시내 저택에 있었으며, 내일은 외국에 가 있을지도 모를 일이라, 사교계 소식통이 무엇 하나 자신 있게 점치지 못한다."], ["Even Sir Leicester’s gallantry has some trouble to keep pace with her.", "레스터 경의 헌신마저 마님을 뒤따르기가 적잖이 버겁다."], ["It would have more but that his other faithful ally, for better and for worse—the gout—darts into the old oak bed-chamber at Chesney Wold and grips him by both legs.", "더 버거울 뻔했으나, 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나 한결같은 그의 또 다른 충직한 동맹—통풍—이 체스니 월드의 늙은 떡갈나무 침실로 쏜살같이 달려들어 두 다리를 움켜쥐는 덕에 그나마 덜하다."]], [["Sir Leicester receives the gout as a troublesome demon, but still a demon of the patrician order.", "레스터 경은 통풍을 성가신 악마로 받아들이되, 그래도 명문가의 반열에 든 악마로 여긴다."], ["All the Dedlocks, in the direct male line, through a course of time during and beyond which the memory of man goeth not to the contrary, have had the gout.", "모든 데드록 가문 사람은, 직계 남계로, 사람의 기억이 거슬러 닿지 않는 아득한 세월 동안 그리고 그 너머로, 하나같이 통풍을 앓았다."], ["It can be proved, sir.", "증명할 수 있는 일이다, 경."], ["Other men’s fathers may have died of the rheumatism or may have taken base contagion from the tainted blood of the sick vulgar, but the Dedlock family have communicated something exclusive even to the levelling process of dying by dying of their own family gout.", "남의 집 아버지들은 류머티즘으로 죽었을지도 모르고, 병든 천것들의 오염된 피에서 천박한 전염을 옮았을지도 모르지만, 데드록 가문은 제 집안 통풍으로 죽음으로써 죽음이라는 만인 평등의 과정에마저 무언가 독점적인 것을 전했다."], ["It has come down through the illustrious line like the plate, or the pictures, or the place in Lincolnshire.", "그것은 은식기나, 그림이나, 링컨셔의 영지처럼 빛나는 가문의 혈통을 타고 내려왔다."], ["It is among their dignities.", "그것은 가문의 위엄 가운데 하나다."], ["Sir Leicester is perhaps not wholly without an impression, though he has never resolved it into words, that the angel of death in the discharge of his necessary duties may observe to the shades of the aristocracy, “My lords and gentlemen, I have the honour to present to you another Dedlock certified to have arrived per the family gout.”", "레스터 경은, 비록 말로 정리해본 적은 한 번도 없으나, 죽음의 천사가 그 마땅한 직무를 수행하면서 귀족의 망령들에게 이렇게 고하리라는 인상을 어쩌면 전혀 품지 않은 것은 아니다. \"경들과 신사 여러분, 가문 통풍을 타고 도착했음이 확인된 또 한 분의 데드록을 여러분께 소개하는 영광을 누립니다.\""]], [["Hence Sir Leicester yields up his family legs to the family disorder as if he held his name and fortune on that feudal tenure. He feels that for a Dedlock to be laid upon his back and spasmodically twitched and stabbed in his extremities is a liberty taken somewhere, but he thinks, “We have all yielded to this; it belongs to us; it has for some hundreds of years been understood that we are not to make the vaults in the park interesting on more ignoble terms; and I submit myself to the compromise.”", "그리하여 레스터 경은 마치 그 봉건적 보유 조건으로 제 이름과 재산을 지니기라도 한 듯, 가문의 다리를 가문의 질환에 바친다. 그는 데드록 사람이 등을 대고 누워 사지를 발작하듯 씰룩거리고 쑤시며 찔리는 것이 어딘가에서 자행된 무례라고 느끼지만, 이렇게 생각한다. \"우리는 모두 이것에 굴복해왔다. 이것은 우리에게 속한 것이다. 우리가 정원 묘소를 더 비천한 조건으로 흥미롭게 만들지 않으리라는 것은 수백 년 동안 이해되어온 바다. 그러니 나는 그 타협에 순순히 따른다.\""]], [["And a goodly show he makes, lying in a flush of crimson and gold in the midst of the great drawing-room before his favourite picture of my Lady, with broad strips of sunlight shining in, down the long perspective, through the long line of windows, and alternating with soft reliefs of shadow.", "그가 펼치는 광경은 자못 볼만하다. 진홍과 황금이 불그레하게 번지는 가운데, 큰 응접실 한복판 자신이 가장 아끼는 마님의 초상화 앞에 누워 있고, 긴 창들의 긴 행렬을 지나, 길게 트인 시야를 따라 햇살이 넓은 띠를 이루며 비쳐 들고, 그 햇살이 부드럽게 도드라진 그늘과 번갈아 어우러진다."], ["Outside, the stately oaks, rooted for ages in the green ground which has never known ploughshare, but was still a chase when kings rode to battle with sword and shield and rode a-hunting with bow and arrow, bear witness to his greatness.", "바깥에서는, 보습이라곤 한 번도 닿은 적 없는 푸른 땅에 오랜 세월 뿌리를 박은 위풍당당한 떡갈나무들이—왕들이 칼과 방패를 들고 전장으로 말을 달리고 활과 화살을 들고 사냥을 나서던 시절에도 여전히 사냥터였던 그 땅에서—그의 위대함을 증언한다."], ["Inside, his forefathers, looking on him from the walls, say, “Each of us was a passing reality here and left this coloured shadow of himself and melted into remembrance as dreamy as the distant voices of the rooks now lulling you to rest,”", "안에서는, 벽에서 그를 내려다보는 선조들이 이렇게 말한다. \"우리 하나하나도 여기서 한순간의 실체였고, 자신의 이 채색된 그림자를 남기고는, 지금 그대를 잠들게 하는 까마귀들의 먼 울음소리처럼 아련한 추억 속으로 녹아 사라졌다네.\""], ["and bear their testimony to his greatness too.", "그러며 그들 또한 그의 위대함을 증언한다."], ["And he is very great this day.", "그리고 그는 오늘 자못 위대하다."], ["And woe to Boythorn or other daring wight who shall presumptuously contest an inch with him!", "그러니 감히 한 치라도 그와 다투려 드는 보이손이나 그 밖의 무모한 자에게 화 있을진저!"]], [["My Lady is at present represented, near Sir Leicester, by her portrait.", "마님은 지금 레스터 경 곁에서 초상화로 대신 자리한다."], ["She has flitted away to town, with no intention of remaining there, and will soon flit hither again, to the confusion of the fashionable intelligence.", "마님은 그곳에 머물 뜻 없이 시내로 훌쩍 떠나버렸고, 머잖아 다시 이리로 훌쩍 돌아와 사교계 소식통을 혼란에 빠뜨릴 것이다."], ["The house in town is not prepared for her reception.", "시내 저택은 마님을 맞을 채비가 되어 있지 않다. 휘장에 덮여 음울하다."], ["It is muffled and dreary.", "분을 바른 머큐리 하나만이 현관 창가에서 처량하게 입을 헤벌리고 있다."], ["Only one Mercury in powder gapes disconsolate at the hall-window; and he mentioned last night to another Mercury of his acquaintance, also accustomed to good society, that if that sort of thing was to last—which it couldn’t, for a man of his spirits couldn’t bear it, and a man of his figure couldn’t be expected to bear it—there would be no resource for him, upon his honour, but to cut his throat!", "어젯밤 그는 자기와 안면 있는, 역시 상류 사교계에 익숙한 또 다른 머큐리에게 이렇게 토로했다. 이런 식의 생활이 계속될 거라면—그럴 리야 없겠지만, 자기 같은 기개를 지닌 사내가 견딜 수도 없고, 자기 같은 풍채를 지닌 사내가 견디리라 기대할 수도 없으니—명예를 걸고 말하건대 자기로서는 목을 그어버리는 것 말고는 다른 방도가 없을 거라고!"]], [["What connexion can there be between the place in Lincolnshire, the house in town, the Mercury in powder, and the whereabout of Jo the outlaw with the broom, who had that distant ray of light upon him when he swept the churchyard-step?", "링컨셔의 영지와, 시내 저택과, 분 바른 머큐리와, 빗자루를 든 무법자 조—교회 묘지 계단을 쓸 때 저 멀리서 한 줄기 빛이 그에게 닿았던 그 아이—의 행방 사이에 무슨 연관이 있을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["What connexion can there have been between many people in the innumerable histories of this world who from opposite sides of great gulfs have, nevertheless, been very curiously brought together!", "이 세상의 헤아릴 수 없는 사연 속에서, 거대한 심연의 양쪽 기슭에서 갈라져 살던 숱한 사람들이, 그럼에도 참으로 기묘하게 한자리에 엮인 일은 또 얼마나 많았던가!"]], [["Jo sweeps his crossing all day long, unconscious of the link, if any link there be.", "조는 무슨 연결 고리가 있다 한들 그 고리를 알지 못한 채, 온종일 자기 길목을 쓴다."], ["He sums up his mental condition when asked a question by replying that he “don’t know nothink.”", "무언가 물으면 그는 \"암것도 몰러유\"라고 답하는 것으로 제 정신 상태를 요약한다."], ["He knows that it’s hard to keep the mud off the crossing in dirty weather, and harder still to live by doing it.", "그는 궂은 날씨에 길목에서 진흙을 걷어내기가 힘들고, 그 일로 먹고살기는 더 힘들다는 것을 안다."], ["Nobody taught him even that much; he found it out.", "그것조차 누가 가르쳐주지 않았다. 스스로 깨쳤다."]], [["Jo lives—that is to say, Jo has not yet died—in a ruinous place known to the like of him by the name of Tom-all-Alone’s.", "조는 톰 혼자 사는 곳이라는 이름으로 그 비슷한 처지의 사람들에게 알려진, 허물어져가는 곳에서 산다—다시 말해, 조는 아직 죽지 않았다."], ["It is a black, dilapidated street, avoided by all decent people, where the crazy houses were seized upon, when their decay was far advanced, by some bold vagrants who after establishing their own possession took to letting them out in lodgings.", "그곳은 시커멓게 다 쓰러져가는 골목으로, 점잖은 사람이라면 누구나 피해 다니는데, 그 미친 듯이 낡은 집들은 한참 썩어 들어갈 무렵 어느 대담한 부랑자들에게 점거당했고, 그들은 제 소유를 확립한 뒤 그것을 셋방으로 내놓기 시작했다."], ["Now, these tumbling tenements contain, by night, a swarm of misery.", "이제 이 무너져가는 셋집들은 밤이면 떼 지어 모여드는 비참함을 품는다."], ["As on the ruined human wretch vermin parasites appear, so these ruined shelters have bred a crowd of foul existence that crawls in and out of gaps in walls and boards; and coils itself to sleep, in maggot numbers, where the rain drips in; and comes and goes, fetching and carrying fever and sowing more evil in its every footprint than Lord Coodle, and Sir Thomas Doodle, and the Duke of Foodle, and all the fine gentlemen in office, down to Zoodle, shall set right in five hundred years—though born expressly to do it.", "망가진 인간 군상에 기생충이 들끓듯, 이 망가진 거처들도 추잡한 무리를 길러내, 그것이 벽과 판자의 틈으로 기어들고 기어 나오고, 빗물이 새어 드는 곳에 구더기처럼 떼 지어 똬리를 틀고 잠들며, 발길마다 열병을 짊어지고 나르며, 쿠들 경과 토머스 두들 경과 푸들 공작과, 그 아래 주들에 이르기까지 관직에 앉은 그 모든 잘난 신사들이—바로 그러라고 작정하고 태어났음에도—오백 년 걸려도 바로잡지 못할 만큼 많은 악을 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 뿌리며 드나든다."]], [["Twice lately there has been a crash and a cloud of dust, like the springing of a mine, in Tom-all-Alone’s; and each time a house has fallen.", "최근 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서는 마치 지뢰가 터지듯 우지끈 무너지는 소리와 함께 먼지구름이 두 차례나 일었다."], ["These accidents have made a paragraph in the newspapers and have filled a bed or two in the nearest hospital.", "그때마다 집 한 채가 쓰러졌다. 이 사고들은 신문에 한 문단씩 실렸고, 가장 가까운 병원의 병상 한둘을 채웠다."], ["The gaps remain, and there are not unpopular lodgings among the rubbish.", "무너진 자리는 그대로 남았고, 잔해 틈에도 인기 없지 않은 셋방이 들어선다."], ["As several more houses are nearly ready to go, the next crash in Tom-all-Alone’s may be expected to be a good one.", "여러 채가 더 곧 무너질 듯하니, 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 다음번 무너짐은 제법 볼만하리라 예상해도 좋겠다."]], [["This desirable property is in Chancery, of course.", "이 탐나는 자산은 물론 대법원에 걸려 있다."], ["It would be an insult to the discernment of any man with half an eye to tell him so. Whether “Tom”", "눈치가 반쯤이라도 있는 사람에게 그걸 굳이 일러주는 것은 그 분별력에 대한 모욕이리라."], ["is the popular representative of the original plaintiff or defendant in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, or whether Tom lived here when the suit had laid the street waste, all alone, until other settlers came to join him, or whether the traditional title is a comprehensive name for a retreat cut off from honest company and put out of the pale of hope, perhaps nobody knows.", "\"톰\"이 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송의 본래 원고나 피고를 두루 일컫는 통칭인지, 아니면 그 소송이 골목을 폐허로 만든 뒤 다른 거주자들이 와서 합류하기 전까지 톰이 여기서 홀로 살았는지, 아니면 그 전해 내려오는 명칭이 정직한 무리에게서 끊겨 나오고 희망의 울타리 밖으로 내쳐진 은신처를 두루 가리키는 이름인지는 어쩌면 아무도 모른다."], ["Certainly Jo don’t know.", "조가 모르는 것만은 분명하다."]], [["“For I don’t,”", "\"왜냐믄 지는,\""], ["says Jo, “I don’t know nothink.”", "조가 말한다. \"지는 암것도 몰러유.\""]], [["It must be a strange state to be like Jo!", "조처럼 산다는 건 참으로 기이한 상태이리라!"], ["To shuffle through the streets, unfamiliar with the shapes, and in utter darkness as to the meaning, of those mysterious symbols, so abundant over the shops, and at the corners of streets, and on the doors, and in the windows!", "가게마다, 길모퉁이마다, 문짝마다, 창문마다 그토록 넘쳐나는 그 신비로운 기호들의 모양에도 낯설고 그 뜻에도 캄캄한 채 거리를 어슬렁어슬렁 헤맨다는 건!"], ["To see people read, and to see people write, and to see the postmen deliver letters, and not to have the least idea of all that language—to be, to every scrap of it, stone blind and dumb!", "사람들이 글 읽는 걸 보고, 사람들이 글 쓰는 걸 보고, 우체부가 편지를 배달하는 걸 보면서도, 그 모든 언어를 털끝만큼도 알지 못한다는 건—그 한 조각 한 조각에 돌처럼 눈멀고 벙어리가 된다는 건!"], ["It must be very puzzling to see the good company going to the churches on Sundays, with their books in their hands, and to think (for perhaps Jo DOES think at odd times) what does it all mean, and if it means anything to anybody, how comes it that it means nothing to me?", "일요일이면 점잖은 사람들이 책을 손에 들고 교회로 가는 걸 보면서, (어쩌면 조도 가끔은 정말 생각하니까) 저게 다 무슨 뜻일까, 누군가에게 무슨 뜻이 있다면 어째서 나에게는 아무 뜻도 없을까 하고 생각한다는 건 참으로 어리둥절하리라."], ["To be hustled, and jostled, and moved on; and really to feel that it would appear to be perfectly true that I have no business here, or there, or anywhere; and yet to be perplexed by the consideration that I AM here somehow, too, and everybody overlooked me until I became the creature that I am!", "떠밀리고, 부대끼고, 비키라는 소리를 듣고, 내가 여기도, 저기도, 그 어디에도 있을 자격이 없다는 게 더없이 진실인 듯 정말로 느끼면서도, 그래도 나 또한 어떻게든 여기 있고, 내가 지금 이 꼴이 되기까지 모두가 나를 못 본 척했다는 생각에 어쩔 줄 모른다는 건!"], ["It must be a strange state, not merely to be told that I am scarcely human (as in the case of my offering myself for a witness), but to feel it of my own knowledge all my life!", "내가 (증인으로 나서겠다 했을 때처럼) 사람이라 하기 어렵다는 말을 듣는 것뿐만 아니라, 평생토록 그것을 스스로 절감하며 산다는 건 참으로 기이한 상태이리라!"], ["To see the horses, dogs, and cattle go by me and to know that in ignorance I belong to them and not to the superior beings in my shape, whose delicacy I offend!", "말이며 개며 소가 내 곁을 지나가는 걸 보면서, 영문도 모른 채 내가 그것들에게 속할 뿐 나와 같은 모양을 한 우월한 존재들에게는 속하지 않으며, 그들의 고상함을 내가 거스른다는 것을 안다는 건!"], ["Jo’s ideas of a criminal trial, or a judge, or a bishop, or a government, or that inestimable jewel to him (if he only knew it) the Constitution, should be strange!", "형사 재판이나, 판사나, 주교나, 정부나, 혹은 (알기만 한다면) 그에게 더없이 값진 보석인 헌법에 대한 조의 관념이란 분명 기이하리라!"], ["His whole material and immaterial life is wonderfully strange; his death, the strangest thing of all.", "그의 물질적이고 비물질적인 삶 전체가 놀랍도록 기이하다. 그의 죽음은, 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 기이하다."]], [["Jo comes out of Tom-all-Alone’s, meeting the tardy morning which is always late in getting down there, and munches his dirty bit of bread as he comes along.", "조는 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 나와, 그곳까지 내려오는 데 늘 굼뜬 아침을 맞으며, 걸어오는 길에 더러운 빵 조각을 우물거린다."], ["His way lying through many streets, and the houses not yet being open, he sits down to breakfast on the door-step of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts and gives it a brush when he has finished as an acknowledgment of the accommodation.", "그가 가는 길은 숱한 거리를 지나고, 가게들이 아직 문을 열지 않았기에, 그는 해외선교복음전파협회 문간 계단에 앉아 아침을 먹고, 다 먹은 뒤에는 그 자리를 빌려준 데 대한 답례로 빗자루질을 한 번 해준다."], ["He admires the size of the edifice and wonders what it’s all about.", "그는 그 건물의 규모에 감탄하며, 대체 무얼 하는 곳일까 궁금해한다."], ["He has no idea, poor wretch, of the spiritual destitution of a coral reef in the Pacific or what it costs to look up the precious souls among the coco-nuts and bread-fruit.", "가엾은 녀석은 태평양 산호초의 영적 궁핍이 무엇인지, 야자나무와 빵나무 열매 사이에서 그 귀한 영혼들을 찾아내는 데 얼마나 큰 비용이 드는지 도무지 알 길이 없다."]], [["He goes to his crossing and begins to lay it out for the day.", "그는 제 길목으로 가 하루치 일을 벌일 채비를 한다. 도시가 깨어난다."], ["The town awakes; the great tee-totum is set up for its daily spin and whirl; all that unaccountable reading and writing, which has been suspended for a few hours, recommences.", "거대한 팽이가 날마다의 회전과 소용돌이를 위해 세워진다. 몇 시간 멈춰 있던 그 영문 모를 읽기와 쓰기가 다시 시작된다."], ["Jo and the other lower animals get on in the unintelligible mess as they can.", "조와 그 밖의 미천한 짐승들은 그 알아먹을 수 없는 난장판 속에서 그저 제 형편껏 살아간다."], ["It is market-day.", "장날이다."], ["The blinded oxen, over-goaded, over-driven, never guided, run into wrong places and are beaten out, and plunge red-eyed and foaming at stone walls, and often sorely hurt the innocent, and often sorely hurt themselves.", "눈먼 황소들이, 너무 들볶이고, 너무 내몰리고, 한 번도 제대로 이끌린 적 없이, 엉뚱한 데로 뛰어들었다가 매를 맞고 쫓겨나, 핏발 선 눈으로 거품을 물고 돌담에 들이박고, 종종 죄 없는 이를 호되게 다치게 하며, 종종 제 몸도 호되게 다친다."], ["Very like Jo and his order; very, very like!", "조와 그 족속과 꼭 닮았다. 어쩜 그리도, 그리도 닮았는지!"]], [["A band of music comes and plays.", "악대가 와서 연주한다."], ["Jo listens to it.", "조가 그것을 듣는다."], ["So does a dog—a drover’s dog, waiting for his master outside a butcher’s shop, and evidently thinking about those sheep he has had upon his mind for some hours and is happily rid of.", "개도 한 마리 듣는다—푸줏간 앞에서 주인을 기다리는 가축몰이꾼의 개로, 몇 시간 동안 마음에 짊어졌다가 다행히 벗어버린 그 양들을 분명 곱씹는 중이다."], ["He seems perplexed respecting three or four, can’t remember where he left them, looks up and down the street as half expecting to see them astray, suddenly pricks up his ears and remembers all about it.", "서너 마리 때문에 어리둥절한 듯, 어디다 두고 왔는지 떠올리지 못하고, 길을 위아래로 살피며 혹시 그것들이 어슬렁대지 않나 반쯤 기대하다가, 문득 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 모든 걸 기억해낸다."], ["A thoroughly vagabond dog, accustomed to low company and public-houses; a terrific dog to sheep, ready at a whistle to scamper over their backs and tear out mouthfuls of their wool; but an educated, improved, developed dog who has been taught his duties and knows how to discharge them.", "천것들 무리와 술집에 익숙한, 영락없는 떠돌이 개로, 양에게는 무시무시한 개이며, 휘파람 한 번이면 양 등을 타고 내달려 털을 한 움큼씩 물어뜯을 태세이지만, 제 임무를 배우고 그 임무를 어떻게 다하는지 아는, 교육받고 향상되고 발달한 개다."], ["He and Jo listen to the music, probably with much the same amount of animal satisfaction; likewise as to awakened association, aspiration, or regret, melancholy or joyful reference to things beyond the senses, they are probably upon a par.", "그와 조는 음악을 듣는데, 아마 거의 같은 정도의 짐승다운 만족을 누릴 것이다. 마찬가지로, 일깨워지는 연상이나, 동경이나, 후회나, 감각 너머의 것들에 대한 우울하거나 즐거운 떠올림에서도, 둘은 아마 막상막하일 것이다."], ["But, otherwise, how far above the human listener is the brute!", "그러나 그 밖의 점에서는, 이 짐승이 인간 청중보다 얼마나 까마득히 위에 있는가!"]], [["Turn that dog’s descendants wild, like Jo, and in a very few years they will so degenerate that they will lose even their bark—but not their bite.", "그 개의 후손들을 조처럼 야생으로 풀어놓아 보라. 그러면 몇 해 지나지 않아 어찌나 퇴화하는지 짖는 소리조차 잃어버릴 것이다—허나 무는 버릇만은 잃지 않으리라."]], [["The day changes as it wears itself away and becomes dark and drizzly.", "날은 저물어가며 변하여 어두워지고 부슬비가 내린다."], ["Jo fights it out at his crossing among the mud and wheels, the horses, whips, and umbrellas, and gets but a scanty sum to pay for the unsavoury shelter of Tom-all-Alone’s.", "조는 진흙과 바퀴들, 말들, 채찍들, 우산들 틈에서 제 길목을 지키며 싸우지만, 톰 혼자 사는 곳의 그 께름칙한 잠자리 값으로 치를 푼돈밖에 벌지 못한다. 땅거미가 깔린다."], ["Twilight comes on; gas begins to start up in the shops; the lamplighter, with his ladder, runs along the margin of the pavement.", "가게마다 가스등이 켜지기 시작한다. 사다리를 든 점등부가 보도 가장자리를 따라 달려간다."], ["A wretched evening is beginning to close in.", "비참한 저녁이 내려앉기 시작한다."]], [["In his chambers Mr. Tulkinghorn sits meditating an application to the nearest magistrate to-morrow morning for a warrant.", "토킹혼 씨는 자기 사무실에 앉아, 내일 아침 가장 가까운 치안판사에게 영장을 신청할 일을 곰곰 생각한다."], ["Gridley, a disappointed suitor, has been here to-day and has been alarming.", "소송에 좌절한 그리들리가 오늘 여기 다녀가며 위협을 가한 것이다."], ["We are not to be put in bodily fear, and that ill-conditioned fellow shall be held to bail again.", "우리가 신변의 위협을 받을 수는 없는 노릇이니, 그 못된 작자를 다시 보석에 묶어두어야 한다."], ["From the ceiling, foreshortened Allegory, in the person of one impossible Roman upside down, points with the arm of Samson (out of joint, and an odd one) obtrusively toward the window.", "천장에서는, 거꾸로 매달린 어느 있을 수 없는 로마인의 모습을 한, 단축법으로 그려진 우의화가 삼손의 팔로(관절이 어긋나고, 짝도 맞지 않는 팔로) 창문 쪽을 노골적으로 가리킨다."], ["Why should Mr. Tulkinghorn, for such no reason, look out of window?", "토킹혼 씨가 무슨 까닭에, 그런 아무 까닭도 없이 창밖을 내다보겠는가?"], ["Is the hand not always pointing there?", "저 손은 늘 그쪽을 가리키고 있지 않은가?"], ["So he does not look out of window.", "그러니 그는 창밖을 내다보지 않는다."]], [["And if he did, what would it be to see a woman going by? There are women enough in the world, Mr.", "그가 내다본들, 여자 하나가 지나가는 걸 본다 한들 그게 무어란 말인가?"], ["Tulkinghorn thinks—too many; they are at the bottom of all that goes wrong in it, though, for the matter of that, they create business for lawyers.", "세상에 여자는 넘치도록 많다고 토킹혼 씨는 생각한다—너무 많다. 세상에 잘못되는 모든 일의 밑바닥에는 여자가 있다, 하기야 그 덕에 변호사에게 일거리가 생기기는 하지만."], ["What would it be to see a woman going by, even though she were going secretly?", "여자 하나가 지나가는 걸 본다 한들, 설령 그 여자가 몰래 가는 길이라 한들 그게 무어란 말인가?"], ["They are all secret. Mr.", "여자들은 죄다 비밀스럽다."], ["Tulkinghorn knows that very well.", "토킹혼 씨는 그것을 아주 잘 안다."]], [["But they are not all like the woman who now leaves him and his house behind, between whose plain dress and her refined manner there is something exceedingly inconsistent.", "그러나 여자들이 모두, 지금 그와 그의 집을 뒤로하고 떠나는 저 여자 같지는 않다. 저 여자는 수수한 옷차림과 세련된 몸가짐 사이에 어딘가 몹시 어긋나는 데가 있다."], ["She should be an upper servant by her attire, yet in her air and step, though both are hurried and assumed—as far as she can assume in the muddy streets, which she treads with an unaccustomed foot—she is a lady.", "차림새로 보면 상급 하녀쯤 되어야 마땅한데, 그 분위기와 걸음걸이로 보면—비록 둘 다 서두르며 꾸민 것이고, 익숙지 않은 발로 디디는 진창길에서 꾸밀 수 있는 한껏 꾸민 것이긴 해도—분명 귀부인이다."], ["Her face is veiled, and still she sufficiently betrays herself to make more than one of those who pass her look round sharply.", "얼굴은 베일로 가렸으나, 그래도 충분히 제 본색을 드러내어, 곁을 지나는 이들 가운데 한둘 이상이 날카롭게 돌아보게 한다."]], [["She never turns her head.", "여자는 결코 고개를 돌리지 않는다."], ["Lady or servant, she has a purpose in her and can follow it.", "귀부인이든 하녀든, 그녀는 마음에 목적을 품었고 그것을 좇을 줄 안다."], ["She never turns her head until she comes to the crossing where Jo plies with his broom.", "조가 빗자루를 부리는 길목에 이를 때까지 그녀는 결코 고개를 돌리지 않는다."], ["He crosses with her and begs.", "조가 그녀와 함께 건너며 동냥을 한다."], ["Still, she does not turn her head until she has landed on the other side.", "그래도 그녀는 건너편에 다다를 때까지 고개를 돌리지 않는다."], ["Then she slightly beckons to him and says, “Come here!”", "그러고는 그에게 살짝 손짓하며 말한다. \"이리 와봐!\""]], [["Jo follows her a pace or two into a quiet court.", "조는 그녀를 따라 한두 걸음, 한적한 안뜰로 들어선다."]], [["“Are you the boy I’ve read of in the papers?”", "\"네가 신문에서 읽은 그 아이니?\""], ["she asked behind her veil.", "그녀가 베일 너머에서 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"몰러유,\""], ["says Jo, staring moodily at the veil, “nothink about no papers.", "조가 시무룩하게 베일을 쳐다보며 말한다. \"신문이고 뭐고 암것도."], ["I don’t know nothink about nothink at all.”", "지는 암것도, 워떤 것도 통 몰러유.\""]], [["“Were you examined at an inquest?”", "\"검시 심문에서 조사받았니?\""]], [["“I don’t know nothink about no—where I was took by the beadle, do you mean?”", "\"지는 암것도 몰러유—교구 관리한테 끌려갔던 거 말씀이세유?\" 조가 말한다."], ["says Jo. “Was the boy’s name at the inkwhich Jo?”", "\"그 검신가 뭔가 하던 데 나온 아이 이름이 조였남유?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“That’s me!”", "\"그게 저예유!\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“Come farther up.”", "\"좀 더 위로 와봐.\""]], [["“You mean about the man?”", "\"그 사람 얘기세유?\""], ["says Jo, following.", "조가 따라오며 말한다."], ["“Him as wos dead?”", "\"죽은 그 사람유?\""]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Speak in a whisper!", "속삭여서 말해! 그래."], ["Yes. Did he look, when he was living, so very ill and poor?”", "그 사람은, 살아 있을 때, 그렇게나 아프고 가난해 보였니?\""]], [["“Oh, jist!”", "\"아유, 똑같았지유!\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“Did he look like—not like YOU?”", "\"혹시—너랑은 안 닮아 보였니?\""], ["says the woman with abhorrence.", "그 여자가 진저리치며 말한다."]], [["“Oh, not so bad as me,”", "\"아유, 저만큼 형편없진 않았지유,\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."], ["“I’m a reg’lar one I am!", "\"지야 영락없는 거지구만유!"], ["You didn’t know him, did you?”", "그 사람을 아셨던 건 아니지유, 그쵸?\""]], [["“How dare you ask me if I knew him?”", "\"감히 네가 그 사람을 내가 알았느냐 묻다니?\""]], [["“No offence, my lady,”", "\"기분 상하셨다면 죄송혀유, 마님,\""], ["says Jo with much humility, for even he has got at the suspicion of her being a lady.", "조가 한껏 굽실대며 말한다. 그조차 그녀가 귀부인이 아닐까 의심하기에 이른 것이다."]], [["“I am not a lady.", "\"난 귀부인이 아냐."], ["I am a servant.”", "하녀야.\""]], [["“You are a jolly servant!”", "\"참말 좋은 하녀시구먼유!\""], ["says Jo without the least idea of saying anything offensive, merely as a tribute of admiration.", "조가 무슨 거슬리는 말을 할 뜻이라곤 조금도 없이, 그저 감탄의 표시로 말한다."]], [["“Listen and be silent.", "\"잘 듣고 입 다물어."], ["Don’t talk to me, and stand farther from me!", "나한테 말 걸지 말고, 좀 더 떨어져 서!"], ["Can you show me all those places that were spoken of in the account I read?", "내가 읽은 그 기사에 나온 곳들을 죄다 보여줄 수 있겠니?"], ["The place he wrote for, the place he died at, the place where you were taken to, and the place where he was buried?", "그 사람이 글을 써서 받았던 곳, 그 사람이 죽은 곳, 네가 끌려간 곳, 그리고 그 사람이 묻힌 곳을?"], ["Do you know the place where he was buried?”", "그 사람이 묻힌 곳은 아니?\""]], [["Jo answers with a nod, having also nodded as each other place was mentioned.", "조는 고개를 끄덕여 답하는데, 앞서 다른 곳들이 언급될 때마다도 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Go before me and show me all those dreadful places.", "\"내 앞장서서 그 끔찍한 곳들을 죄다 보여줘."], ["Stop opposite to each, and don’t speak to me unless I speak to you.", "각 장소 맞은편에 멈춰 서고, 내가 말 걸기 전엔 나한테 말하지 마."], ["Don’t look back.", "뒤돌아보지 마."], ["Do what I want, and I will pay you well.”", "내가 시키는 대로 하면 후하게 값을 쳐주마.\""]], [["Jo attends closely while the words are being spoken; tells them off on his broom-handle, finding them rather hard; pauses to consider their meaning; considers it satisfactory; and nods his ragged head.", "조는 말이 이어지는 동안 바짝 귀 기울여 듣는다. 빗자루 자루에 그 말들을 하나하나 짚어가며 헤아리는데, 제법 어려운 모양이다. 잠시 멈춰 그 뜻을 곱씹고, 흡족하다 여기며, 헝클어진 머리를 끄덕인다."]], [["“I’m fly,”", "\"알어들었구먼유,\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."], ["“But fen larks, you know.", "\"근디 장난질은 안 되유, 아시쥬."], ["Stow hooking it!”", "내빼기 없기유!\""]], [["“What does the horrible creature mean?”", "\"이 끔찍한 것이 대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거지?\""], ["exclaims the servant, recoiling from him.", "하녀가 그에게서 움찔 물러나며 외친다."]], [["“Stow cutting away, you know!”", "\"내빼기 없기라구유, 아시쥬!\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“I don’t understand you.", "\"무슨 소린지 모르겠다."], ["Go on before!", "어서 앞장서!"], ["I will give you more money than you ever had in your life.”", "네가 평생 만져본 적 없는 큰돈을 줄 테니.\""]], [["Jo screws up his mouth into a whistle, gives his ragged head a rub, takes his broom under his arm, and leads the way, passing deftly with his bare feet over the hard stones and through the mud and mire.", "조는 입을 오므려 휘파람 모양을 짓고, 헝클어진 머리를 한 번 긁고, 빗자루를 옆구리에 끼고는, 맨발로 딱딱한 돌과 진흙과 진창을 날렵하게 밟으며 앞장서 길을 안내한다."]], [["Cook’s Court.", "쿡 골목."], ["Jo stops.", "조가 멈춘다."], ["A pause.", "침묵."]], [["“Who lives here?”", "\"여기는 누가 살지?\""]], [["“Him wot give him his writing and give me half a bull,”", "\"그 사람한테 글 쓸 거 주고, 지한테는 반 크라운 줬던 양반이 살어유,\""], ["says Jo in a whisper without looking over his shoulder.", "조가 어깨너머로 돌아보지도 않고 속삭이며 말한다."]], [["“Go on to the next.”", "\"다음 데로 가.\""]], [["Krook’s house.", "크룩의 집."], ["Jo stops again.", "조가 다시 멈춘다."], ["A longer pause.", "좀 더 긴 침묵."]], [["“Who lives here?”", "\"여기는 누가 살지?\""]], [["“HE lived here,”", "\"그 사람이 여기 살었어유,\""], ["Jo answers as before.", "조가 앞서처럼 답한다."]], [["After a silence he is asked, “In which room?”", "한참 침묵하다가 그녀가 묻는다. \"어느 방에서?\""]], [["“In the back room up there.", "\"저 위 뒷방이유."], ["You can see the winder from this corner.", "이 모퉁이에서 창문이 보여유."], ["Up there!", "저기 위!"], ["That’s where I see him stritched out.", "거기서 지는 그 사람이 뻗어 있는 걸 봤어유."], ["This is the public-ouse where I was took to.”", "이건 지가 끌려갔던 그 술집이구유.\""]], [["“Go on to the next!”", "\"다음 데로 가!\""]], [["It is a longer walk to the next, but Jo, relieved of his first suspicions, sticks to the forms imposed upon him and does not look round.", "다음 장소까지는 좀 더 먼 길이지만, 처음의 의심을 떨쳐버린 조는 자기에게 지워진 절차를 충실히 지키며 돌아보지 않는다."], ["By many devious ways, reeking with offence of many kinds, they come to the little tunnel of a court, and to the gas-lamp (lighted now), and to the iron gate.", "온갖 종류의 역겨운 냄새가 진동하는 구불구불한 길을 숱하게 지나, 그들은 터널 같은 작은 골목에 이르고, (지금은 켜진) 가스등에 이르고, 쇠문에 이른다."]], [["“He was put there,”", "\"그 사람은 저기 들어갔어유,\""], ["says Jo, holding to the bars and looking in.", "조가 쇠창살을 붙들고 안을 들여다보며 말한다."]], [["“Where?", "\"어디?"], ["Oh, what a scene of horror!”", "아, 이 무슨 끔찍한 광경이람!\""]], [["“There!”", "\"저기유!\""], ["says Jo, pointing.", "조가 가리키며 말한다."], ["“Over yinder.", "\"저짝에유."], ["Among them piles of bones, and close to that there kitchin winder!", "저 뼈 무더기들 사이, 저기 부엌 창문 바싹 옆에!"], ["They put him wery nigh the top.", "그 사람을 맨 꼭대기 근처에 묻었어유."], ["They was obliged to stamp upon it to git it in.", "집어넣으려고 발로 꾹꾹 밟아 다져야 했어유."], ["I could unkiver it for you with my broom if the gate was open.", "문이 열려 있으면 지가 빗자루로 들춰서 보여드릴 수도 있는디."], ["That’s why they locks it, I s’pose,”", "그래서 잠가두는 거겄지유, 아마,\""], ["giving it a shake.", "그가 문을 흔들며 말한다."], ["“It’s always locked.", "\"늘 잠겨 있어유."], ["Look at the rat!”", "쥐 좀 봐유!\""], ["cries Jo, excited. “Hi!", "조가 들떠서 소리친다."], ["Look!", "\"어!"], ["There he goes!", "봐유! 저기 가유!"], ["Ho!", "야!"], ["Into the ground!”", "땅속으로!\""]], [["The servant shrinks into a corner, into a corner of that hideous archway, with its deadly stains contaminating her dress; and putting out her two hands and passionately telling him to keep away from her, for he is loathsome to her, so remains for some moments.", "하녀는 한 구석으로 움츠러든다, 죽음의 얼룩이 옷을 더럽히는 그 흉측한 아치 아래 한 구석으로. 그러고는 두 손을 내밀어, 그가 자기에게 역겹기 짝이 없으니 가까이 오지 말라고 격하게 이르며, 한동안 그렇게 머문다."], ["Jo stands staring and is still staring when she recovers herself.", "조는 멀거니 쳐다보며 서 있고, 그녀가 정신을 추스를 때까지도 여전히 쳐다본다."]], [["“Is this place of abomination consecrated ground?”", "\"이 끔찍한 곳이 축성된 땅이냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know nothink of consequential ground,”", "\"공쩡인지 뭔지 하는 땅은 지가 몰러유,\""], ["says Jo, still staring.", "조가 여전히 쳐다보며 말한다."]], [["“Is it blessed?”", "\"여기가 축복받은 곳이냐?\""]], [["“Which?”", "\"뭐가유?\""], ["says Jo, in the last degree amazed.", "조가 더없이 어리둥절해하며 말한다."]], [["“Is it blessed?”", "\"축복받은 곳이냐고?\""]], [["“I’m blest if I know,”", "\"낸들 알 게 뭐유,\""], ["says Jo, staring more than ever; “but I shouldn’t think it warn’t.", "조가 그 어느 때보다 멀뚱히 쳐다보며 말한다. \"근디 그렇진 않을 성싶은디유."], ["Blest?”", "축복이라구유?\""], ["repeats Jo, something troubled in his mind.", "조가 마음에 무언가 걸리는 듯 되뇐다."], ["“It an’t done it much good if it is.", "\"그게 그렇다면 별 덕은 못 봤지유. 축복이라구유?"], ["Blest? I should think it was t’othered myself.", "지가 보기엔 외려 저주받은 거 같은디유."], ["But I don’t know nothink!”", "근디 지는 암것도 몰러유!\""]], [["The servant takes as little heed of what he says as she seems to take of what she has said herself.", "하녀는 그가 하는 말에도, 자기가 한 말에 그러는 것만큼이나 거의 귀를 기울이지 않는다."], ["She draws off her glove to get some money from her purse.", "그녀가 지갑에서 돈을 꺼내려 장갑을 벗는다."], ["Jo silently notices how white and small her hand is and what a jolly servant she must be to wear such sparkling rings.", "조는 그녀의 손이 얼마나 희고 작은지, 저렇게 반짝이는 반지를 끼다니 참말 좋은 하녀일 거라고 말없이 눈여겨본다."]], [["She drops a piece of money in his hand without touching it, and shuddering as their hands approach.", "그녀는 손이 닿지 않게, 둘의 손이 가까워지자 진저리치며, 동전 한 닢을 그의 손에 떨어뜨린다."], ["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["she adds, “show me the spot again!”", "그녀가 덧붙인다. \"그 자리를 다시 보여줘!\""]], [["Jo thrusts the handle of his broom between the bars of the gate, and with his utmost power of elaboration, points it out.", "조는 빗자루 자루를 쇠문 창살 사이로 밀어 넣고, 온 힘을 다해 정성껏 그곳을 가리킨다."], ["At length, looking aside to see if he has made himself intelligible, he finds that he is alone.", "마침내, 제 말이 통했는지 보려고 곁눈질하다가, 그는 자기가 혼자라는 것을 깨닫는다."]], [["His first proceeding is to hold the piece of money to the gas-light and to be overpowered at finding that it is yellow—gold.", "그가 맨 먼저 하는 일은 동전을 가스등 불빛에 비춰 보는 것이고, 그것이 노란 빛깔—금이라는 것을 알고는 압도당하는 것이다."], ["His next is to give it a one-sided bite at the edge as a test of its quality.", "그다음 하는 일은 품질을 확인하려고 동전 가장자리를 한쪽으로 깨물어보는 것이다."], ["His next, to put it in his mouth for safety and to sweep the step and passage with great care.", "그다음에는 잃어버리지 않으려고 동전을 입에 넣고, 계단과 통로를 아주 정성껏 쓰는 것이다."], ["His job done, he sets off for Tom-all-Alone’s, stopping in the light of innumerable gas-lamps to produce the piece of gold and give it another one-sided bite as a reassurance of its being genuine.", "일을 마치자, 그는 톰 혼자 사는 곳으로 출발하는데, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 가스등 불빛마다 멈춰 서서 금화를 꺼내, 진짜라는 것을 거듭 확인하려고 또 한 번 한쪽으로 깨물어본다."]], [["The Mercury in powder is in no want of society to-night, for my Lady goes to a grand dinner and three or four balls.", "분 바른 머큐리는 오늘 밤 말동무가 아쉽지 않으니, 마님이 성대한 만찬에 가고 무도회에도 서너 군데 가기 때문이다."], ["Sir Leicester is fidgety down at Chesney Wold, with no better company than the gout; he complains to Mrs.", "레스터 경은 통풍 말고는 더 나은 벗도 없이 체스니 월드에서 안절부절못한다."], ["Rouncewell that the rain makes such a monotonous pattering on the terrace that he can’t read the paper even by the fireside in his own snug dressing-room.", "그는 라운스웰 부인에게, 빗물이 테라스에 어찌나 단조롭게 후두두 떨어지는지 자기 아늑한 옷방 난롯가에서조차 신문을 읽을 수가 없다고 투덜댄다."]], [["“Sir Leicester would have done better to try the other side of the house, my dear,”", "\"경께서 차라리 저택 반대편을 써보셨으면 좋았을 텐데, 얘야,\""], ["says Mrs. Rouncewell to Rosa.", "라운스웰 부인이 로사에게 말한다."], ["“His dressing-room is on my Lady’s side.", "\"경의 옷방이 마님 쪽에 있잖니."], ["And in all these years I never heard the step upon the Ghost’s Walk more distinct than it is to-night!”", "그런데 이 오랜 세월 동안 유령의 길 위로 들리는 그 발소리를 오늘 밤보다 또렷하게 들어본 적이 없구나!\""]], [["Esther’s Narrative\nRichard very often came to see us while we remained in London (though he soon failed in his letter-writing), and with his quick abilities, his good spirits, his good temper, his gaiety and freshness, was always delightful.", "리처드는 우리가 런던에 머무는 동안 자주 찾아왔습니다(편지 쓰는 일은 곧 시들해졌지만요). 그리고 그 빠른 재주와 밝은 기운, 너그러운 성품, 명랑함과 신선함으로 언제나 즐거움을 주었습니다."], ["But though I liked him more and more the better I knew him, I still felt more and more how much it was to be regretted that he had been educated in no habits of application and concentration.", "하지만 알면 알수록 그를 더 좋아하게 되면서도, 끈기 있게 한곳에 집중하는 습관을 그가 전혀 길러오지 못했다는 점이 못내 안타깝게 느껴졌습니다."], ["The system which had addressed him in exactly the same manner as it had addressed hundreds of other boys, all varying in character and capacity, had enabled him to dash through his tasks, always with fair credit and often with distinction, but in a fitful, dazzling way that had confirmed his reliance on those very qualities in himself which it had been most desirable to direct and train.", "성격도 능력도 제각각인 수백 명의 다른 소년에게 똑같은 방식으로 들이댔던 그 교육 제도는, 리처드가 자기 과제들을 늘 그럭저럭 좋은 평가를 받으며, 더러는 두드러진 성적으로 후딱 해치우게 해주긴 했습니다. 그러나 그 방식은 변덕스럽고 번쩍이는 식이어서, 정작 이끌어주고 다듬어주어야 마땅했던 바로 그 자질에 그가 더욱 기대게 만들 뿐이었습니다."], ["They were good qualities, without which no high place can be meritoriously won, but like fire and water, though excellent servants, they were very bad masters.", "그것들은 좋은 자질이고, 그것 없이는 높은 자리를 제대로 얻을 수 없는 자질이지만, 불과 물처럼 훌륭한 하인이긴 해도 아주 고약한 주인이었습니다."], ["If they had been under Richard’s direction, they would have been his friends; but Richard being under their direction, they became his enemies.", "만약 그 자질들이 리처드의 다스림 아래 있었다면 그의 벗이 되었을 테지만, 리처드가 그 자질들의 다스림 아래 있었기에 그것들은 그의 적이 되고 말았습니다."]], [["I write down these opinions not because I believe that this or any other thing was so because I thought so, but only because I did think so and I want to be quite candid about all I thought and did.", "내가 이런 생각을 적어두는 건, 내가 그렇게 여겼다고 해서 이것이나 다른 무엇이 실제로 그러했다고 믿어서가 아니라, 다만 내가 정말 그렇게 생각했고, 내가 생각하고 행한 모든 것을 솔직하게 밝히고 싶어서입니다. 이것이 리처드에 관한 내 생각이었습니다."], ["These were my thoughts about Richard.", "게다가 나는 후견인이 했던 말이 얼마나 옳았는지, 또 대법원 소송의 불확실함과 지체가 그의 천성에 어떤 기운을 불어넣었는지를 자주 보는 것 같았습니다. 자기가 거대한 도박판의 일부라고 느끼는 노름꾼의 무심한 마음 같은 것을 말입니다."], ["I thought I often observed besides how right my guardian was in what he had said, and that the uncertainties and delays of the Chancery suit had imparted to his nature something of the careless spirit of a gamester who felt that he was part of a great gaming system.", "* 대법원: 영국 형평법을 다루던 챈서리 법원. 끝없이 늘어지는 소송으로 악명 높았다."]], [["Mr. and Mrs. Bayham Badger coming one afternoon when my guardian was not at home, in the course of conversation I naturally inquired after Richard.", "어느 날 오후 후견인이 집에 없을 때 베이엄 배저 부부가 찾아왔는데, 이야기를 나누다 나는 자연스레 리처드의 안부를 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, Mr. Carstone,”", "\"아, 카스턴 씨 말이군요.\""], ["said Mrs.", "배저 부인이 말했습니다."], ["Badger, “is very well and is, I assure you, a great acquisition to our society.", "\"아주 잘 지내고 있고, 장담하건대 우리 모임에 큰 보탬이 되는 분이에요."], ["Captain Swosser used to say of me that I was always better than land a-head and a breeze a-starn to the midshipmen’s mess when the purser’s junk had become as tough as the fore-topsel weather earrings.", "스워서 함장은 늘 나를 두고 이렇게 말하곤 했죠. 사관후보생 식당에서 사무장이 내놓는 절인 고기가 앞돛 위 풍향삭처럼 질겨졌을 때, 뱃머리 앞의 육지와 고물 쪽에서 불어오는 산들바람보다도 내가 더 낫다고요."], ["It was his naval way of mentioning generally that I was an acquisition to any society.", "그건 어떤 모임에든 내가 보탬이 된다는 걸 두루 일컫는 그분의 해군식 표현이었어요."], ["I may render the same tribute, I am sure, to Mr.", "나는 카스턴 씨에게도 똑같은 찬사를 바칠 수 있다고 확신합니다."], ["Carstone.", "그런데 나는…"], ["But I—you won’t think me premature if I mention it?”", "내가 이런 말을 꺼내는 게 너무 성급하다고 여기진 않으시겠죠?\""]], [["I said no, as Mrs.", "나는 아니라고 했습니다."], ["Badger’s insinuating tone seemed to require such an answer.", "배저 부인의 은근한 말투가 그런 대답을 요구하는 듯했으니까요."]], [["“Nor Miss Clare?”", "\"클레어 양도 그렇지 않죠?\""], ["said Mrs. Bayham Badger sweetly.", "베이엄 배저 부인이 상냥하게 물었습니다."]], [["Ada said no, too, and looked uneasy.", "에이다 역시 아니라고 하면서 불편한 기색을 보였습니다."]], [["“Why, you see, my dears,” said Mrs.", "\"그러니까 말이에요, 얘들아.\""], ["Badger, “—you’ll excuse me calling you my dears?”", "배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"─내가 너희를 얘들아 하고 불러도 괜찮겠지?\""]], [["We entreated Mrs. Badger not to mention it.", "우리는 배저 부인에게 마음 쓰지 마시라고 간곡히 청했습니다."]], [["“Because you really are, if I may take the liberty of saying so,”", "\"왜냐하면 너희가 정말로, 이런 말 함부로 해도 된다면 말이지,\" 배저 부인이 말을 이었습니다."], ["pursued Mrs. Badger, “so perfectly charming.", "\"어쩜 그렇게 더할 나위 없이 사랑스러운지."], ["You see, my dears, that although I am still young—or Mr. Bayham Badger pays me the compliment of saying so—”", "그래서 말인데, 얘들아, 내가 아직 젊긴 하지만─베이엄 배저 씨가 그렇게 말해주는 영광을 베풀어주긴 하지만─\""]], [["“No,” Mr.", "\"아니오.\""], ["Badger called out like some one contradicting at a public meeting.", "배저 씨가 공개 집회에서 누군가의 말에 반박하듯 큰 소리로 외쳤습니다."], ["“Not at all!”", "\"전혀 아니지요!\""]], [["“Very well,” smiled Mrs.", "\"좋아요.\""], ["Badger, “we will say still young.”", "배저 부인이 미소 지었습니다. \"아직 젊다고 해두죠.\""]], [["“Undoubtedly,”", "\"두말할 것 없이요.\""], ["said Mr. Badger.", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“My dears, though still young, I have had many opportunities of observing young men.", "\"얘들아, 아직 젊긴 해도 나는 젊은 남자들을 관찰할 기회가 많았단다."], ["There were many such on board the dear old Crippler, I assure you.", "그리운 옛 크리플러호에는 그런 젊은이가 많았지, 정말이야."], ["After that, when I was with Captain Swosser in the Mediterranean, I embraced every opportunity of knowing and befriending the midshipmen under Captain Swosser’s command.", "그 후에 스워서 함장과 함께 지중해에 있을 때는, 함장 휘하의 사관후보생들을 알고 가까이할 기회를 하나도 놓치지 않았어."], ["YOU never heard them called the young gentlemen, my dears, and probably would not understand allusions to their pipe-claying their weekly accounts, but it is otherwise with me, for blue water has been a second home to me, and I have been quite a sailor.", "너희는 그들이 '젊은 신사분들'이라 불리는 걸 들어본 적도 없을 테고, 그들이 주간 회계 장부를 백토로 닦았다는 식의 비유도 아마 이해하지 못하겠지만, 나는 다르단다. 푸른 바다가 내겐 제2의 고향이었고, 나는 거의 뱃사람이나 다름없었으니까."], ["Again, with Professor Dingo.”", "그리고 또 딩고 교수와 함께 지낼 때도 그랬지.\""]], [["“A man of European reputation,”", "\"유럽 전역에 명성을 떨친 분이지요.\""], ["murmured Mr. Badger.", "배저 씨가 나직이 덧붙였습니다."]], [["“When I lost my dear first and became the wife of my dear second,” said Mrs.", "\"내가 사랑하는 첫째를 잃고 사랑하는 둘째의 아내가 되었을 때도,\""], ["Badger, speaking of her former husbands as if they were parts of a charade, “I still enjoyed opportunities of observing youth.", "배저 부인은 먼저 떠난 남편들을 마치 수수께끼 놀이의 한 토막처럼 말했습니다. \"나는 여전히 젊은이들을 관찰할 기회를 누렸어."], ["The class attendant on Professor Dingo’s lectures was a large one, and it became my pride, as the wife of an eminent scientific man seeking herself in science the utmost consolation it could impart, to throw our house open to the students as a kind of Scientific Exchange.", "딩고 교수의 강의를 듣는 수강생이 아주 많았는데, 학문 속에서 학문이 줄 수 있는 최고의 위안을 스스로 구하던 저명한 과학자의 아내로서, 우리 집을 학생들에게 일종의 '과학 교류소'처럼 활짝 열어두는 게 내 자랑거리가 되었지."], ["Every Tuesday evening there was lemonade and a mixed biscuit for all who chose to partake of those refreshments.", "화요일 저녁마다 그 다과를 들고 싶어 하는 모든 이를 위해 레모네이드와 갖가지 비스킷이 차려졌어."], ["And there was science to an unlimited extent.”", "그리고 과학이 끝도 없이 펼쳐졌단다.\""]], [["“Remarkable assemblies those, Miss Summerson,”", "\"참으로 놀라운 모임이었겠습니다, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said Mr. Badger reverentially.", "배저 씨가 경건하게 말했습니다."], ["“There must have been great intellectual friction going on there under the auspices of such a man!”", "\"그런 분의 후원 아래 거기서 얼마나 대단한 지적 마찰이 일어났겠습니까!\""]], [["“And now,” pursued Mrs.", "\"그리고 이제는,\""], ["Badger, “now that I am the wife of my dear third, Mr.", "배저 부인이 말을 이었습니다."], ["Badger, I still pursue those habits of observation which were formed during the lifetime of Captain Swosser and adapted to new and unexpected purposes during the lifetime of Professor Dingo.", "\"이제는 사랑하는 셋째 배저 씨의 아내가 되어서도, 나는 스워서 함장 생전에 몸에 밴, 그리고 딩고 교수 생전에 새롭고 뜻밖의 목적에 맞게 다듬어진 그 관찰 습관을 여전히 이어가고 있어."], ["I therefore have not come to the consideration of Mr. Carstone as a neophyte.", "그러니 나는 풋내기로서 카스턴 씨를 살피게 된 게 아니란다."], ["And yet I am very much of the opinion, my dears, that he has not chosen his profession advisedly.”", "그런데도 나는, 얘들아, 그분이 자기 직업을 신중하게 고른 게 아니라는 쪽으로 생각이 많이 기운단다.\""]], [["Ada looked so very anxious now that I asked Mrs. Badger on what she founded her supposition.", "이제 에이다가 너무도 근심스러운 얼굴을 하기에, 나는 배저 부인에게 그 짐작의 근거가 무엇인지 물었습니다."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양,\""], ["she replied, “on Mr.", "그녀가 대답했습니다."], ["Carstone’s character and conduct.", "\"카스턴 씨의 성격과 행동을 보고 하는 말이에요."], ["He is of such a very easy disposition that probably he would never think it worth while to mention how he really feels, but he feels languid about the profession.", "그분은 워낙 느긋한 성미라 자기 진심을 굳이 입에 올릴 가치가 있다고 여기지 않겠지만, 그 직업에 대해서는 흥이 나질 않아요."], ["He has not that positive interest in it which makes it his vocation.", "그것을 천직으로 삼게 할 만한 적극적인 관심이 없는 거죠."], ["If he has any decided impression in reference to it, I should say it was that it is a tiresome pursuit.", "그것과 관련해 무언가 또렷한 인상을 갖고 있다면, 내가 보기엔 지겨운 일이라는 인상일 거예요."], ["Now, this is not promising.", "자, 이건 가망이 없어요."], ["Young men like Mr. Allan Woodcourt who take it from a strong interest in all that it can do will find some reward in it through a great deal of work for a very little money and through years of considerable endurance and disappointment.", "앨런 우드코트 씨 같은 젊은이는 그 일이 해낼 수 있는 모든 것에 강한 관심을 품고 시작했기에, 쥐꼬리만 한 돈을 받고 엄청난 일을 하면서, 또 여러 해 동안 적잖은 인내와 실망을 겪으면서도 거기서 어떤 보람을 찾아낼 거예요."], ["But I am quite convinced that this would never be the case with Mr. Carstone.”", "하지만 카스턴 씨에게는 결코 그런 일이 없으리라고 나는 확신해요.\""]], [["“Does Mr. Badger think so too?”", "\"배저 씨도 그렇게 생각하시나요?\""], ["asked Ada timidly.", "에이다가 머뭇거리며 물었습니다."]], [["“Why,” said Mr.", "\"음,\""], ["Badger, “to tell the truth, Miss Clare, this view of the matter had not occurred to me until Mrs. Badger mentioned it.", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["But when Mrs. Badger put it in that light, I naturally gave great consideration to it, knowing that Mrs.", "\"사실대로 말하자면, 클레어 양, 배저 부인이 말을 꺼내기 전까지 나는 이 일을 그런 식으로 보지 못했습니다."], ["Badger’s mind, in addition to its natural advantages, has had the rare advantage of being formed by two such very distinguished (I will even say illustrious) public men as Captain Swosser of the Royal Navy and Professor Dingo.", "하지만 배저 부인이 그렇게 비춰주자, 나는 당연히 그것을 깊이 헤아렸지요. 배저 부인의 정신은 타고난 장점에 더해, 영국 해군의 스워서 함장과 딩고 교수라는 두 분의 매우 저명한(아니, 빛나는 분이라고까지 말하겠습니다) 공인에 의해 다듬어지는 드문 이점을 누렸다는 걸 알기 때문입니다."], ["The conclusion at which I have arrived is—in short, is Mrs. Badger’s conclusion.”", "내가 다다른 결론은─한마디로, 배저 부인의 결론입니다.\""]], [["“It was a maxim of Captain Swosser’s,” said Mrs.", "\"스워서 함장의 좌우명이 하나 있었지.\""], ["Badger, “speaking in his figurative naval manner, that when you make pitch hot, you cannot make it too hot; and that if you only have to swab a plank, you should swab it as if Davy Jones were after you.", "배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"그분 특유의 비유적인 해군식으로 말하자면, 역청을 뜨겁게 데울 때는 아무리 뜨겁게 해도 지나치지 않다, 그리고 갑판 한 장을 닦더라도 데비 존스가 뒤쫓아 오는 양 닦아야 한다는 거였어."], ["It appears to me that this maxim is applicable to the medical as well as to the nautical profession.”", "내 보기엔 이 좌우명이 항해뿐 아니라 의료라는 직업에도 똑같이 들어맞는 것 같구나.\""]], [["“To all professions,”", "“어느 직업에나 통하는 말이지요.”"], ["observed Mr. Badger.", "배저 씨가 말했다."], ["“It was admirably said by Captain Swosser.", "“스워서 함장이 참으로 훌륭하게 말씀하셨어요."], ["Beautifully said.”", "멋진 말이었죠.”"]], [["“People objected to Professor Dingo when we were staying in the north of Devon after our marriage,”", "\"모든 직업에 그렇지요.\" 배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mrs.", "\"스워서 함장께서 참으로 훌륭하게 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Badger, “that he disfigured some of the houses and other buildings by chipping off fragments of those edifices with his little geological hammer.", "멋진 말씀이지요.\" \"우리가 결혼하고 데번 북부에 머물 때 사람들이 딩고 교수를 못마땅해했어.\""], ["But the professor replied that he knew of no building save the Temple of Science.", "배저 부인이 말했습니다. \"교수가 그 자그마한 지질 망치로 집이며 다른 건물들에서 조각을 떼어내 흉하게 만든다고 말이지."], ["The principle is the same, I think?”", "하지만 교수는 자기가 아는 건물이라곤 오직 '과학의 신전'뿐이라고 대꾸했단다. 이치는 같다고 보는데, 그렇지 않니?\""]], [["“Precisely the same,”", "\"정확히 같습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Badger.", "배저 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Finely expressed!", "\"기막힌 표현이지요!"], ["The professor made the same remark, Miss Summerson, in his last illness, when (his mind wandering) he insisted on keeping his little hammer under the pillow and chipping at the countenances of the attendants.", "교수께서는 마지막 병석에서도 똑같은 말씀을 하셨습니다, 서머슨 양. (정신이 흐려진 채로) 그 자그마한 망치를 베개 밑에 두겠다고 고집하시며 곁에서 시중드는 사람들의 얼굴을 쪼아대셨지요."], ["The ruling passion!”", "천성으로 굳은 그 열정이란!\""]], [["Although we could have dispensed with the length at which Mr. and Mrs. Badger pursued the conversation, we both felt that it was disinterested in them to express the opinion they had communicated to us and that there was a great probability of its being sound. We agreed to say nothing to Mr. Jarndyce until we had spoken to Richard; and as he was coming next evening, we resolved to have a very serious talk with him.", "배저 부부가 그토록 길게 늘어놓은 대화는 굳이 없어도 좋았겠지만, 우리 둘 다 그들이 사심 없이 우리에게 그 의견을 전해주었으며, 그것이 옳을 가능성이 크다고 느꼈습니다. 우리는 리처드와 이야기하기 전까지는 잔다이스 씨에게 아무 말도 하지 않기로 했고, 그가 다음 날 저녁에 오기로 했기에 그와 아주 진지하게 의논하기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["So after he had been a little while with Ada, I went in and found my darling (as I knew she would be) prepared to consider him thoroughly right in whatever he said.", "그래서 그가 에이다와 잠시 함께 있은 뒤 내가 들어가 보니, 내 사랑하는 동무는(내 예상대로) 그가 무슨 말을 하든 옳다고 받아들일 채비가 되어 있었습니다."]], [["“And how do you get on, Richard?” said I.", "\"리처드, 일은 잘돼 가요?\""], ["I always sat down on the other side of him.", "내가 말했습니다. 나는 늘 그의 반대편에 앉았습니다."], ["He made quite a sister of me.", "그는 나를 누이처럼 여겼으니까요."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Well enough!”", "그럭저럭요!\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."]], [["“He can’t say better than that, Esther, can he?”", "\"이보다 더 좋게 말할 순 없잖아, 에스더, 그렇지?\""], ["cried my pet triumphantly.", "내 귀염둥이가 의기양양하게 외쳤습니다."]], [["I tried to look at my pet in the wisest manner, but of course I couldn’t.", "나는 더없이 현명한 표정으로 내 귀염둥이를 바라보려 했지만, 당연히 그럴 수 없었습니다."]], [["“Well enough?”", "\"그럭저럭이라고요?\""], ["I repeated.", "내가 되물었습니다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Richard, “well enough.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["It’s rather jog-trotty and humdrum.", "\"그럭저럭이요. 좀 단조롭고 따분해요."], ["But it’ll do as well as anything else!”", "하지만 다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지일 테니!\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["My dear Richard!”", "리처드도 참!\""], ["I remonstrated.", "내가 나무랐습니다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"왜 그래요?\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."]], [["“Do as well as anything else!”", "\"다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지라니요!\""]], [["“I don’t think there’s any harm in that, Dame Durden,”", "\"그게 잘못된 말은 아니지 않아, 더든 아주머니.\""], ["said Ada, looking so confidingly at me across him; “because if it will do as well as anything else, it will do very well, I hope.”", "에이다가 그를 사이에 두고 나를 그토록 믿음직스럽게 바라보며 말했습니다. \"다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지라면, 잘되리라는 거잖아, 그러길 바라.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, I hope so,”", "\"아, 그래, 나도 그러길 바라.\""], ["returned Richard, carelessly tossing his hair from his forehead.", "리처드가 무심하게 이마에서 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“After all, it may be only a kind of probation till our suit is—I forgot though.", "\"결국 우리 소송이 끝날 때까지의 일종의 수습 기간일지도 몰라─아 참, 깜빡했네."], ["I am not to mention the suit.", "소송 얘긴 하면 안 되지."], ["Forbidden ground!", "금지된 구역이니까!"], ["Oh, yes, it’s all right enough.", "아, 그래, 다 괜찮아."], ["Let us talk about something else.”", "다른 얘기나 하자.\""]], [["Ada would have done so willingly, and with a full persuasion that we had brought the question to a most satisfactory state.", "에이다는 기꺼이 그러려 했고, 우리가 이 문제를 더없이 만족스러운 상태로 매듭지었다고 굳게 믿었습니다."], ["But I thought it would be useless to stop there, so I began again.", "하지만 나는 거기서 멈추는 게 소용없으리라 여겨 다시 말을 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“No, but Richard,”", "\"안 돼요, 리처드,\""], ["said I, “and my dear Ada!", "내가 말했습니다. \"그리고 사랑하는 에이다!"], ["Consider how important it is to you both, and what a point of honour it is towards your cousin, that you, Richard, should be quite in earnest without any reservation.", "이게 너희 둘 모두에게 얼마나 중요한 일인지, 또 사촌 오라버니에 대한 명예가 걸린 문제인지 잘 생각해 봐. 리처드, 너는 아무 망설임 없이 정말로 진지해야 해."], ["I think we had better talk about this, really, Ada.", "정말이지, 이 얘기를 제대로 하는 게 좋겠어, 에이다."], ["It will be too late very soon.”", "곧 너무 늦어버릴 거야.\""]], [["“Oh, yes!", "\"아, 그래!"], ["We must talk about it!”", "우린 이 얘길 해야 해!\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했습니다."], ["“But I think Richard is right.”", "\"하지만 리처드 말이 옳다고 봐.\""]], [["What was the use of my trying to look wise when she was so pretty, and so engaging, and so fond of him!", "그 애가 그토록 예쁘고, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 그토록 그를 좋아하는데, 내가 현명한 표정을 지어본들 무슨 소용이 있었겠어요!"]], [["“Mr. and Mrs.", "\"어제 배저 부부가 다녀갔어요, 리처드,\""], ["Badger were here yesterday, Richard,”", "내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “and they seemed disposed to think that you had no great liking for the profession.”", "\"그분들은 리처드가 그 직업을 별로 좋아하지 않는다고 여기는 눈치였어요.\""]], [["“Did they though?”", "\"그분들이 그랬어요?\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Well, that rather alters the case, because I had no idea that they thought so, and I should not have liked to disappoint or inconvenience them.", "그럼, 얘기가 좀 달라지는데요. 그분들이 그렇게 생각할 줄은 몰랐고, 그분들을 실망시키거나 불편하게 하고 싶진 않으니까."], ["The fact is, I don’t care much about it.", "사실은요, 나도 그 일에 별 관심이 없어요."], ["But, oh, it don’t matter!", "하지만, 뭐, 상관없어요!"], ["It’ll do as well as anything else!”", "다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지일 테니까!\""]], [["“You hear him, Ada!”", "\"들었지, 에이다!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“The fact is,”", "\"사실은,\""], ["Richard proceeded, half thoughtfully and half jocosely, “it is not quite in my way.", "리처드가 반쯤은 생각에 잠긴 채 반쯤은 농담조로 말을 이었습니다. \"내 적성에 딱 맞지가 않아."], ["I don’t take to it.", "흥미가 안 생겨."], ["And I get too much of Mrs. Bayham Badger’s first and second.”", "그리고 베이엄 배저 부인의 첫째 남편이며 둘째 남편 얘기를 너무 많이 들어야 하고.\""]], [["“I am sure THAT’S very natural!”", "\"그야 정말 당연하지!\""], ["cried Ada, quite delighted.", "에이다가 무척 기뻐하며 외쳤습니다."], ["“The very thing we both said yesterday, Esther!”", "\"어제 우리 둘 다 했던 바로 그 말이잖아, 에스더!\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["pursued Richard, “it’s monotonous, and to-day is too like yesterday, and to-morrow is too like to-day.”", "리처드가 말을 이었습니다. \"단조롭고, 오늘은 어제와 너무 똑같고, 내일은 오늘과 너무 똑같아.\""]], [["“But I am afraid,”", "\"하지만 그건,\""], ["said I, “this is an objection to all kinds of application—to life itself, except under some very uncommon circumstances.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"온갖 끈기 있는 일에, 아니 아주 별난 사정이 아니고서야 삶 자체에 다 해당하는 불만이지 않을까 싶어.\""]], [["“Do you think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각해요?\""], ["returned Richard, still considering.", "리처드가 여전히 곰곰이 생각하며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Perhaps!", "\"아마도요!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Why, then, you know,”", "그렇다면, 그것 봐요,\""], ["he added, suddenly becoming gay again, “we travel outside a circle to what I said just now.", "그가 갑자기 다시 명랑해지며 덧붙였습니다. \"결국 방금 내가 한 말로 빙 돌아오는 셈이네요."], ["It’ll do as well as anything else.", "다른 무슨 일이든 매한가지라고요."], ["Oh, it’s all right enough!", "아, 다 괜찮아요!"], ["Let us talk about something else.”", "다른 얘기나 하자고요.\""]], [["But even Ada, with her loving face—and if it had seemed innocent and trusting when I first saw it in that memorable November fog, how much more did it seem now when I knew her innocent and trusting heart—even Ada shook her head at this and looked serious.", "하지만 그 다정한 얼굴의 에이다조차도─그 잊지 못할 11월 안개 속에서 처음 보았을 때 그 얼굴이 순진하고 미더워 보였다면, 이제 그 애의 순진하고 미더운 마음을 알게 된 지금은 얼마나 더 그래 보였는지요─에이다조차도 이 말에는 고개를 가로저으며 심각한 표정을 지었습니다."], ["So I thought it a good opportunity to hint to Richard that if he were sometimes a little careless of himself, I was very sure he never meant to be careless of Ada, and that it was a part of his affectionate consideration for her not to slight the importance of a step that might influence both their lives.", "그래서 나는 좋은 기회다 싶어 리처드에게 넌지시 일렀습니다. 가끔 그가 자기 자신에게 좀 무심할 수는 있어도, 에이다에게 무심하려는 마음은 결코 없으리라 내가 확신한다고, 또 두 사람의 인생을 좌우할 수 있는 결정의 중요성을 가볍게 여기지 않는 것이 그 애를 위하는 그의 애정 어린 배려의 한 부분이라고요."], ["This made him almost grave.", "이 말에 그는 거의 진지해졌습니다."]], [["“My dear Mother Hubbard,”", "\"사랑하는 허버드 할머니,\""], ["he said, “that’s the very thing!", "그가 말했습니다. \"바로 그거예요!"], ["I have thought of that several times and have been quite angry with myself for meaning to be so much in earnest and—somehow—not exactly being so.", "나도 그 생각을 여러 번 했고, 그토록 진지해지려고 마음먹고도─어쩐 일인지─딱히 그러지 못하는 나 자신에게 단단히 화가 났어요. 왜 그런지 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t know how it is; I seem to want something or other to stand by.", "뭔가 의지할 게 필요한 것 같아요. 내가 에이다를 얼마나 좋아하는지 누나도 짐작 못 할 거예요(사랑하는 사촌, 정말 많이 사랑해!)."], ["Even you have no idea how fond I am of Ada (my darling cousin, I love you, so much!), but I don’t settle down to constancy in other things.", "하지만 다른 일에는 한결같이 자리를 잡지 못하겠어요. 너무 힘겨운 일이고, 시간도 너무 오래 걸리고!\""], ["It’s such uphill work, and it takes such a time!”", "리처드가 짜증스러운 기색으로 말했습니다. * 허버드 할머니: 영국 전래 동요 속 인물."], ["said Richard with an air of vexation.", "살림을 도맡는 다정한 노파를 빗댄 애칭으로, 리처드가 에스더를 놀리듯 부르는 별명이다."]], [["“That may be,”", "\"아마도,\""], ["I suggested, “because you don’t like what you have chosen.”", "내가 넌지시 말했습니다. \"네가 고른 일을 좋아하지 않아서일 거야.\""]], [["“Poor fellow!” said Ada.", "\"가엾어라!\""], ["“I am sure I don’t wonder at it!”", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"정말 그럴 만도 해!\""]], [["No. It was not of the least use my trying to look wise.", "소용없었습니다."], ["I tried again, but how could I do it, or how could it have any effect if I could, while Ada rested her clasped hands upon his shoulder and while he looked at her tender blue eyes, and while they looked at him!", "내가 현명한 표정을 지어보려는 건 전혀 소용이 없었어요. 다시 애를 써봤지만, 에이다가 두 손을 맞잡아 그의 어깨에 얹고, 그가 그 부드러운 푸른 눈을 바라보고, 그 눈이 다시 그를 바라보는데, 내가 어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠으며, 설령 그런들 무슨 효과가 있었겠어요!"]], [["“You see, my precious girl,”", "\"있잖아, 내 소중한 사촌,\""], ["said Richard, passing her golden curls through and through his hand, “I was a little hasty perhaps; or I misunderstood my own inclinations perhaps.", "리처드가 그 애의 금빛 곱슬머리를 손가락 사이로 거듭 흘려보내며 말했습니다. \"내가 좀 성급했는지도 몰라."], ["They don’t seem to lie in that direction.", "아니면 내 성향을 내가 잘못 알았는지도 모르고."], ["I couldn’t tell till I tried.", "내 마음은 그쪽으로 기우는 것 같지 않아."], ["Now the question is whether it’s worth-while to undo all that has been done.", "해보기 전엔 알 수가 없었지. 이제 문제는 이미 해놓은 일을 죄다 되돌릴 만한 가치가 있느냐는 거야."], ["It seems like making a great disturbance about nothing particular.”", "별것도 아닌 일로 큰 소동을 벌이는 것 같단 말이지.\""]], [["“My dear Richard,”", "\"리처드,\""], ["said I, “how CAN you say about nothing particular?”", "내가 말했습니다. \"어떻게 별것도 아니라고 말할 수 있어요?\""]], [["“I don’t mean absolutely that,”", "\"꼭 그런 뜻은 아니에요.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“I mean that it MAY be nothing particular because I may never want it.”", "\"내 말은, 어쩌면 영영 그게 필요 없을지도 모르니까 별것 아닐 수도 있다는 거예요.\""]], [["Both Ada and I urged, in reply, not only that it was decidedly worth-while to undo what had been done, but that it must be undone. I then asked Richard whether he had thought of any more congenial pursuit.", "이에 에이다와 나는 한목소리로, 이미 해놓은 일을 되돌리는 게 분명히 가치 있을 뿐 아니라 반드시 되돌려야 한다고 거듭 다그쳤습니다. 그러고서 나는 리처드에게 더 마음에 맞는 일을 생각해 본 적이 있는지 물었습니다. \"이런, 우리 십턴 부인,\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"정곡을 찌르시네요. 네, 있어요. 법률이야말로 내게 딱 맞는 일이라고 생각해 왔어요.\""]], [["“There, my dear Mrs. Shipton,” said Richard, “you touch me home. Yes, I have. I have been thinking that the law is the boy for me.”", "* 십턴 부인: 16세기 영국의 전설적인 예언가. 리처드가 에스더의 통찰력을 빗대 붙인 또 다른 장난스러운 별명이다."]], [["“The law!”", "\"법률!\""], ["repeated Ada as if she were afraid of the name.", "에이다가 그 이름이 두려운 듯 되뇌었습니다."]], [["“If I went into Kenge’s office,”", "\"내가 켄지 씨 사무소에 들어가서,\" 리처드가 말했습니다."], ["said Richard, “and if I were placed under articles to Kenge, I should have my eye on the—hum!—the forbidden ground—and should be able to study it, and master it, and to satisfy myself that it was not neglected and was being properly conducted.", "\"켄지 밑에서 견습 계약을 맺게 되면, 그─음!─금지된 구역에 눈을 둘 수 있고, 그것을 공부하고, 통달하고, 그게 소홀히 다뤄지지 않고 제대로 진행되고 있는지 내 눈으로 확인할 수 있어. 에이다의 이익과 내 이익(같은 거지!)을 챙길 수 있고, 블랙스톤이며 그 모든 법학자를 더없이 맹렬한 열정으로 파고들 수 있을 거야.\""], ["I should be able to look after Ada’s interests and my own interests (the same thing!); and I should peg away at Blackstone and all those fellows with the most tremendous ardour.”", "* 블랙스톤: 윌리엄 블랙스톤. 그의 『영국법 주해』는 당시 법학도의 필독서였다."]], [["I was not by any means so sure of that, and I saw how his hankering after the vague things yet to come of those long-deferred hopes cast a shade on Ada’s face.", "나는 그 점에 도무지 확신이 서질 않았고, 오래 미뤄온 그 희망에서 아직 다가오지 않은 막연한 것들을 향한 그의 갈망이 에이다의 얼굴에 그늘을 드리우는 것을 보았습니다."], ["But I thought it best to encourage him in any project of continuous exertion, and only advised him to be quite sure that his mind was made up now.", "하지만 꾸준한 노력이 따르는 계획이라면 무엇이든 북돋아 주는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 다만 이번에는 정말 마음을 굳혔는지 확실히 하라고만 일렀습니다."]], [["“My dear Minerva,”", "\"사랑하는 미네르바,\""], ["said Richard, “I am as steady as you are.", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는 누나만큼이나 굳건해요."], ["I made a mistake; we are all liable to mistakes; I won’t do so any more, and I’ll become such a lawyer as is not often seen.", "실수를 했지만, 누구나 실수는 하는 법이고, 다시는 그러지 않을 거예요. 좀처럼 보기 드문 그런 법률가가 될 거예요."], ["That is, you know,”", "그러니까, 알다시피,\""], ["said Richard, relapsing into doubt, “if it really is worth-while, after all, to make such a disturbance about nothing particular!”", "리처드가 다시 의심에 빠져들며 말했습니다. \"별것도 아닌 일로 그렇게 큰 소동을 벌일 만한 가치가 정말로 있다면 말이에요!\""]], [["This led to our saying again, with a great deal of gravity, all that we had said already and to our coming to much the same conclusion afterwards.", "* 미네르바: 로마 신화 속 지혜의 여신. 역시 에스더의 분별력을 추켜세우는 별명이다."], ["But we so strongly advised Richard to be frank and open with Mr.", "이 때문에 우리는 이미 했던 말을 자못 진지하게 다시 되풀이했고, 결국 거의 같은 결론에 이르렀습니다."], ["Jarndyce, without a moment’s delay, and his disposition was naturally so opposed to concealment that he sought him out at once (taking us with him) and made a full avowal.", "하지만 우리가 리처드에게 한순간도 미루지 말고 잔다이스 씨에게 솔직하고 숨김없이 털어놓으라고 어찌나 강하게 권했던지, 또 그의 천성이 본디 감추는 것을 싫어했던지라, 그는 당장 (우리를 데리고) 후견인을 찾아가 모든 것을 다 털어놓았습니다."], ["“Rick,”", "\"릭,\""], ["said my guardian, after hearing him attentively, “we can retreat with honour, and we will.", "후견인이 그의 말을 주의 깊게 듣고 나서 말했습니다. \"우리는 명예롭게 물러설 수 있고, 또 그렇게 할 거다."], ["But we must be careful—for our cousin’s sake, Rick, for our cousin’s sake—that we make no more such mistakes.", "하지만 조심해야 해─우리 사촌을 위해서, 릭, 우리 사촌을 위해서─다시는 이런 실수를 하지 않도록 말이야."], ["Therefore, in the matter of the law, we will have a good trial before we decide.", "그러니 법률에 관해서는, 결정하기 전에 충분히 시험해 보기로 하자."], ["We will look before we leap, and take plenty of time about it.”", "뛰어들기 전에 잘 살피고, 시간을 넉넉히 들이는 거다.\""]], [["Richard’s energy was of such an impatient and fitful kind that he would have liked nothing better than to have gone to Mr. Kenge’s office in that hour and to have entered into articles with him on the spot.", "리처드의 열정은 어찌나 조급하고 변덕스러운 종류였던지, 그는 그 시각에 당장 켄지 씨 사무소로 달려가 그 자리에서 견습 계약을 맺는 것보다 더 바라는 게 없었을 겁니다."], ["Submitting, however, with a good grace to the caution that we had shown to be so necessary, he contented himself with sitting down among us in his lightest spirits and talking as if his one unvarying purpose in life from childhood had been that one which now held possession of him.", "그래도 그토록 필요하다고 우리가 일러둔 신중함을 흔쾌히 받아들이고는, 더없이 가벼운 기분으로 우리 사이에 앉아, 마치 어릴 적부터 인생의 한결같은 단 하나의 목표가 지금 그를 사로잡은 바로 그것이었다는 듯이 이야기하는 데 만족했습니다. 후견인은 그에게 무척 다정하고 따뜻하게 대했지만, 다소 진지했습니다."], ["My guardian was very kind and cordial with him, but rather grave, enough so to cause Ada, when he had departed and we were going upstairs to bed, to say, “Cousin John, I hope you don’t think the worse of Richard?”", "그가 떠나고 우리가 잠자리에 들려고 이층으로 올라갈 때 에이다가 이렇게 말할 만큼은요. \"존 사촌 오라버니, 리처드를 나쁘게 보시는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“No, my love,”", "\"아니란다, 얘야.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["“Because it was very natural that Richard should be mistaken in such a difficult case.", "\"이렇게 어려운 일에서 리처드가 갈피를 잡지 못한 건 아주 당연한 일이잖아요."], ["It is not uncommon.”", "흔히 있는 일이고요.\""]], [["“No, no, my love,”", "\"그래, 그래, 얘야.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“Don’t look unhappy.”", "\"그렇게 시무룩한 얼굴 하지 마라.\""]], [["“Oh, I am not unhappy, cousin John!”", "\"아, 저 시무룩하지 않아요, 존 사촌 오라버니!\""], ["said Ada, smiling cheerfully, with her hand upon his shoulder, where she had put it in bidding him good night.", "에이다가 잘 자라는 인사를 하며 얹어두었던 손을 그의 어깨에 그대로 둔 채, 명랑하게 미소 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“But I should be a little so if you thought at all the worse of Richard.”", "\"하지만 오라버니가 리처드를 조금이라도 나쁘게 여기신다면 좀 그럴 것 같아요.\""]], [["“My dear,” said Mr.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Jarndyce, “I should think the worse of him only if you were ever in the least unhappy through his means.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"네가 그 애 때문에 조금이라도 불행해지는 일이 생긴다면, 그때서야 나는 그 애를 나쁘게 볼 거다."], ["I should be more disposed to quarrel with myself even then, than with poor Rick, for I brought you together.", "하지만 그때조차도 나는 가엾은 릭보다 차라리 나 자신과 다투려 들겠지. 내가 너희를 맺어주었으니 말이야."], ["But, tut, all this is nothing!", "하지만, 쯧, 이런 건 다 아무것도 아니다!"], ["He has time before him, and the race to run.", "그 앤 아직 시간이 있고, 달려야 할 경주가 남았어."], ["I think the worse of him?", "내가 그 애를 나쁘게 본다고?"], ["Not I, my loving cousin!", "천만에, 사랑스러운 내 사촌!"], ["And not you, I swear!”", "그리고 너도 아니야, 내 장담하지!\""]], [["“No, indeed, cousin John,”", "\"네, 정말이에요, 존 사촌 오라버니,\""], ["said Ada, “I am sure I could not—I am sure I would not—think any ill of Richard if the whole world did.", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"온 세상이 다 그런다 해도 저는 리처드를 나쁘게─결코 나쁘게 생각할 수 없을 거예요."], ["I could, and I would, think better of him then than at any other time!”", "그땐 오히려 다른 어느 때보다도 그를 더 좋게 생각할 거고, 또 그러고 싶어요!\""]], [["So quietly and honestly she said it, with her hands upon his shoulders—both hands now—and looking up into his face, like the picture of truth!", "그 애가 두 손을─이제는 두 손 다─그의 어깨에 얹고 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 마치 진실 그 자체를 그린 그림처럼, 어찌나 차분하고 진솔하게 그 말을 하던지요!"]], [["“I think,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["said my guardian, thoughtfully regarding her, “I think it must be somewhere written that the virtues of the mothers shall occasionally be visited on the children, as well as the sins of the father.", "후견인이 그 애를 생각에 잠겨 바라보며 말했습니다. \"어딘가에 이렇게 적혀 있을 것 같구나."], ["Good night, my rosebud.", "아비의 죄뿐 아니라 어미의 미덕도 이따금 자식에게 내린다고 말이야."], ["Good night, little woman.", "잘 자거라, 내 장미꽃 봉오리야."], ["Pleasant slumbers!", "잘 자라, 작은 아가씨. 편히 자거라!"], ["Happy dreams!”", "좋은 꿈 꾸고!\""]], [["This was the first time I ever saw him follow Ada with his eyes with something of a shadow on their benevolent expression.", "후견인이 그 인자한 표정에 얼마간 그늘을 드리운 채 에이다를 눈으로 좇는 것을 본 건 그때가 처음이었습니다. 그 애가 난롯불 빛 속에서 노래할 때 그가 그 애와 리처드를 바라보던 그 눈길을 나는 똑똑히 기억하고 있었습니다."], ["I well remembered the look with which he had contemplated her and Richard when she was singing in the firelight; it was but a very little while since he had watched them passing down the room in which the sun was shining, and away into the shade; but his glance was changed, and even the silent look of confidence in me which now followed it once more was not quite so hopeful and untroubled as it had originally been.", "햇살이 비쳐 드는 방을 가로질러 두 사람이 걸어 나가 그늘 속으로 사라지는 모습을 그가 지켜본 것도 아주 잠깐 전의 일이었고요. 하지만 그의 눈빛은 변해 있었고, 이제 다시 나를 향한 그 말없는 신뢰의 눈길조차도 처음만큼 그렇게 희망차고 근심 없어 보이지는 않았습니다."]], [["Ada praised Richard more to me that night than ever she had praised him yet.", "그날 밤 에이다는 그 어느 때보다도 더 리처드를 칭찬했습니다."], ["She went to sleep with a little bracelet he had given her clasped upon her arm.", "그 애는 그가 준 작은 팔찌를 팔에 찬 채 잠들었습니다."], ["I fancied she was dreaming of him when I kissed her cheek after she had slept an hour and saw how tranquil and happy she looked.", "한 시간쯤 자고 난 그 애의 뺨에 입을 맞추며 그 평온하고 행복해 보이는 얼굴을 들여다보았을 때, 나는 그 애가 그의 꿈을 꾸고 있구나 싶었습니다."]], [["For I was so little inclined to sleep myself that night that I sat up working.", "그날 밤 나는 도무지 잠이 오질 않아 일어나 앉아 일을 했으니까요."], ["It would not be worth mentioning for its own sake, but I was wakeful and rather low-spirited.", "그것 자체로야 말할 거리도 못 되지만, 나는 잠이 오지 않았고 마음이 좀 가라앉아 있었습니다."], ["I don’t know why.", "왜인지는 모르겠습니다."], ["At least I don’t think I know why.", "적어도 안다고는 생각하지 않습니다."], ["At least, perhaps I do, but I don’t think it matters.", "어쩌면 알지도 모르지만, 그게 중요한 것 같지는 않습니다."]], [["At any rate, I made up my mind to be so dreadfully industrious that I would leave myself not a moment’s leisure to be low-spirited.", "아무튼 나는 가라앉은 기분에 빠질 한순간의 틈도 남기지 않도록 지독하게 부지런을 떨기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["For I naturally said, “Esther!", "나도 모르게 이렇게 말했으니까요. \"에스더!"], ["You to be low-spirited.", "네가 기분이 가라앉다니."], ["YOU!”", "네가!\""], ["And it really was time to say so, for I—yes, I really did see myself in the glass, almost crying.", "그렇게 말할 때가 정말 되긴 했습니다. 나는─그래요, 거울 속에서 거의 울고 있는 나 자신을 정말로 보았으니까요."], ["“As if you had anything to make you unhappy, instead of everything to make you happy, you ungrateful heart!”", "\"너를 불행하게 할 거라곤 하나도 없고 행복하게 할 것투성이인데도 그러다니, 이 배은망덕한 마음아!\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["If I could have made myself go to sleep, I would have done it directly, but not being able to do that, I took out of my basket some ornamental work for our house (I mean Bleak House) that I was busy with at that time and sat down to it with great determination.", "스스로 잠들 수만 있었다면 당장 그렇게 했겠지만, 그럴 수가 없어서, 나는 그 무렵 한창 매달려 있던 우리 집(황폐한 집 말이에요)을 위한 장식용 바느질감을 바구니에서 꺼내 굳은 각오로 앉아 일을 시작했습니다."], ["It was necessary to count all the stitches in that work, and I resolved to go on with it until I couldn’t keep my eyes open, and then to go to bed.", "그 작업은 코를 하나하나 다 세어야 했기에, 나는 눈을 뜨고 있을 수 없을 때까지 계속하다가 그제야 잠자리에 들기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["I soon found myself very busy.", "곧 나는 일에 푹 빠졌습니다."], ["But I had left some silk downstairs in a work-table drawer in the temporary growlery, and coming to a stop for want of it, I took my candle and went softly down to get it.", "그런데 임시 투덜방의 작업대 서랍에 명주실을 두고 내려와 일을 멈춘 터라, 나는 촛불을 들고 그걸 가지러 살그머니 아래층으로 내려갔습니다."], ["To my great surprise, on going in I found my guardian still there, and sitting looking at the ashes.", "그런데 들어가 보니 놀랍게도 후견인이 아직 거기 앉아 잿더미를 들여다보고 있는 게 아니겠어요."], ["He was lost in thought, his book lay unheeded by his side, his silvered iron-grey hair was scattered confusedly upon his forehead as though his hand had been wandering among it while his thoughts were elsewhere, and his face looked worn.", "그는 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 펴놓은 책은 옆에 팽개쳐진 채였으며, 은빛이 도는 잿빛 머리카락은 마치 다른 데 정신이 팔린 동안 손이 그 사이를 헤매고 다닌 양 이마 위로 어지럽게 흩어져 있었고, 얼굴은 지쳐 보였습니다."], ["Almost frightened by coming upon him so unexpectedly, I stood still for a moment and should have retired without speaking had he not, in again passing his hand abstractedly through his hair, seen me and started.", "그렇게 느닷없이 마주친 게 거의 무섭기까지 해서, 나는 잠시 가만히 서 있었습니다. 그가 다시 머리카락 사이로 멍하니 손을 쓸어 넘기다 나를 보고 흠칫하지 않았다면 아무 말 없이 물러났을 겁니다."]], [["“Esther!”", "\"에스더!\""]], [["I told him what I had come for.", "나는 무엇을 가지러 왔는지 말했습니다."]], [["“At work so late, my dear?”", "\"이렇게 늦게까지 일을 하고 있어, 얘야?\""]], [["“I am working late to-night,”", "\"오늘 밤은 늦게까지 일하고 있어요,\""], ["said I, “because I couldn’t sleep and wished to tire myself.", "내가 말했습니다. \"잠이 오질 않아서 몸을 좀 지치게 하고 싶었거든요."], ["But, dear guardian, you are late too, and look weary.", "그런데 후견인님, 후견인님도 늦으셨고, 또 피곤해 보이세요."], ["You have no trouble, I hope, to keep you waking?”", "잠 못 이루게 하는 걱정거리가 있으신 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“None, little woman, that YOU would readily understand,”", "\"없단다, 작은 아가씨야. 적어도 네가 쉽게 이해할 만한 건 아니야.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["He spoke in a regretful tone so new to me that I inwardly repeated, as if that would help me to his meaning, “That I could readily understand!”", "그가 내게는 너무도 낯선, 회한 어린 어조로 말했기에, 나는 그 뜻을 헤아리는 데 도움이라도 될 양 속으로 되뇌었습니다. \"내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 거라니!\""]], [["“Remain a moment, Esther,”", "\"잠깐 있어 보렴, 에스더.\""], ["said he, “You were in my thoughts.”", "그가 말했습니다. \"마침 네 생각을 하고 있었거든.\""]], [["“I hope I was not the trouble, guardian?”", "\"제가 걱정거리는 아니었겠죠, 후견인님?\""]], [["He slightly waved his hand and fell into his usual manner.", "그가 손을 살짝 내저으며 평소의 모습으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["The change was so remarkable, and he appeared to make it by dint of so much self-command, that I found myself again inwardly repeating, “None that I could understand!”", "그 변화가 어찌나 두드러지던지, 또 그토록 큰 자제력을 들여서야 그렇게 하는 듯 보이던지, 나는 또다시 속으로 되뇌고 있었습니다. \"내가 이해할 만한 건 아니라니!\""]], [["“Little woman,”", "\"작은 아가씨야,\""], ["said my guardian, “I was thinking—that is, I have been thinking since I have been sitting here—that you ought to know of your own history all I know.", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"나는 생각하고 있었단다─그러니까, 여기 앉아 있는 동안 줄곧 생각해 온 건데─네 신상에 관해 내가 아는 모든 걸 너도 알아야 마땅하다고 말이야."], ["It is very little.", "아주 적단다."], ["Next to nothing.”", "거의 없다시피 해.\""]], [["“Dear guardian,”", "\"후견인님,\""], ["I replied, “when you spoke to me before on that subject—”", "내가 대답했습니다. \"전에 그 일로 제게 말씀하셨을 때─\""]], [["“But since then,”", "\"하지만 그 후로,\""], ["he gravely interposed, anticipating what I meant to say, “I have reflected that your having anything to ask me, and my having anything to tell you, are different considerations, Esther.", "그가 내가 하려던 말을 앞질러 진지하게 끼어들었습니다. \"곰곰이 생각해 봤다."], ["It is perhaps my duty to impart to you the little I know.”", "네가 내게 물을 게 있느냐 하는 것과, 내가 네게 들려줄 게 있느냐 하는 건 별개의 문제더구나, 에스더. 내가 아는 그 적은 것이나마 네게 전하는 게 어쩌면 내 도리일 거야.\""]], [["“If you think so, guardian, it is right.”", "\"그렇게 생각하신다면, 후견인님, 그게 옳아요.\""]], [["“I think so,”", "\"그렇게 생각한다.\""], ["he returned very gently, and kindly, and very distinctly.", "그가 아주 부드럽고, 다정하게, 또렷이 대답했습니다."], ["“My dear, I think so now.", "\"얘야, 이제 그렇게 생각해."], ["If any real disadvantage can attach to your position in the mind of any man or woman worth a thought, it is right that you at least of all the world should not magnify it to yourself by having vague impressions of its nature.”", "생각할 가치가 있는 어떤 남자나 여자의 마음속에서든 네 처지에 어떤 진짜 불리함이 따라붙을 수 있다면, 적어도 너만은, 온 세상 누구보다도 너만은, 그 본질에 대해 막연한 인상을 품은 채 스스로 그것을 부풀려선 안 되니까.\""]], [["I sat down and said after a little effort to be as calm as I ought to be, “One of my earliest remembrances, guardian, is of these words: ‘Your mother, Esther, is your disgrace, and you were hers.", "나는 자리에 앉아, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 침착해지려 잠시 애쓴 뒤에 말했습니다. \"제 가장 어린 기억 가운데 하나가 이 말이에요, 후견인님."], ["The time will come, and soon enough, when you will understand this better, and will feel it too, as no one save a woman can.’”", "'네 어미는, 에스더, 네 수치고, 너는 네 어미의 수치였다. 머지않아 때가 올 거다."], ["I had covered my face with my hands in repeating the words, but I took them away now with a better kind of shame, I hope, and told him that to him I owed the blessing that I had from my childhood to that hour never, never, never felt it.", "네가 이걸 더 잘 이해하고, 또 여자만이 느낄 수 있는 식으로 그것을 절감하게 될 때가.'\" 그 말을 되뇌면서 나는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 있었지만, 이제는, 부디 더 나은 종류의 부끄러움이었기를 바라며, 그 손을 내리고 그에게 말했습니다."], ["He put up his hand as if to stop me.", "내가 어린 시절부터 이 순간까지 결코, 결코, 결코 그것을 느끼지 않았던 그 축복이 다 그분 덕이라고요."], ["I well knew that he was never to be thanked, and said no more.", "그가 나를 막으려는 듯 손을 들어 올렸습니다. 그에게 고마움을 표해서는 안 된다는 걸 나는 잘 알고 있었기에 더는 말하지 않았습니다."]], [["“Nine years, my dear,”", "\"구 년 전이란다, 얘야.\" 그가 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했습니다."], ["he said after thinking for a little while, “have passed since I received a letter from a lady living in seclusion, written with a stern passion and power that rendered it unlike all other letters I have ever read.", "\"어느 외딴곳에 은거하던 한 부인에게서 편지 한 통을 받은 게. 내가 이제껏 읽어본 그 어떤 편지와도 다르게, 준엄한 격정과 힘으로 쓰인 편지였지."], ["It was written to me (as it told me in so many words), perhaps because it was the writer’s idiosyncrasy to put that trust in me, perhaps because it was mine to justify it.", "그 편지는(글에 그렇게 또렷이 쓰여 있었듯) 내게 보내진 거였어. 어쩌면 나를 그렇게 믿는 게 글쓴이의 별난 성정이었기 때문일 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그 믿음을 증명하는 게 내 성정이었기 때문일 수도 있겠지."], ["It told me of a child, an orphan girl then twelve years old, in some such cruel words as those which live in your remembrance.", "그 편지는 한 아이에 대해 들려주었어. 그때 열두 살 난 고아 소녀였지."], ["It told me that the writer had bred her in secrecy from her birth, had blotted out all trace of her existence, and that if the writer were to die before the child became a woman, she would be left entirely friendless, nameless, and unknown.", "네 기억 속에 살아 있는 것과 비슷한, 그런 모진 말들로 말이야. 글쓴이는 그 아이를 태어날 때부터 비밀 속에서 길렀고, 그 아이가 존재했다는 흔적을 모조리 지워버렸으며, 만약 그 아이가 여자가 되기 전에 글쓴이가 죽는다면 그 아이는 벗도, 이름도, 신원도 전혀 없이 홀로 남겨질 거라고 했어."], ["It asked me to consider if I would, in that case, finish what the writer had begun.”", "그런 경우 글쓴이가 시작한 일을 내가 마무리해 줄 의향이 있는지 헤아려달라고 청했지.\""]], [["I listened in silence and looked attentively at him.", "나는 잠자코 들으며 그를 주의 깊게 바라보았습니다."]], [["“Your early recollection, my dear, will supply the gloomy medium through which all this was seen and expressed by the writer, and the distorted religion which clouded her mind with impressions of the need there was for the child to expiate an offence of which she was quite innocent.", "\"네 어린 시절의 기억이, 글쓴이가 이 모든 것을 보고 표현하던 그 음울한 매개와, 그 아이가 아무 죄도 없는 잘못을 속죄해야 한다는 인상으로 그 마음을 흐려놓던 그 뒤틀린 신앙을 짐작하게 해줄 거다."], ["I felt concerned for the little creature, in her darkened life, and replied to the letter.”", "나는 어둠에 갇힌 삶을 사는 그 작은 아이가 가여워 그 편지에 답장을 썼단다.\""]], [["I took his hand and kissed it.", "나는 그의 손을 잡아 입을 맞추었습니다."]], [["“It laid the injunction on me that I should never propose to see the writer, who had long been estranged from all intercourse with the world, but who would see a confidential agent if I would appoint one.", "\"그 편지는 내게 하나의 단서를 달았어."], ["I accredited Mr. Kenge.", "글쓴이를 만나자고 결코 청하지 말라는 거였지."], ["The lady said, of her own accord and not of his seeking, that her name was an assumed one.", "오래도록 세상과의 모든 왕래를 끊고 지내온 사람이지만, 내가 믿을 만한 대리인을 한 사람 정하면 그 사람은 만나겠다고 했어."], ["That she was, if there were any ties of blood in such a case, the child’s aunt.", "나는 켄지 씨를 보냈단다. 부인은, 그가 묻지도 않았는데 스스로, 자기 이름이 가명이라고 했어."], ["That more than this she would never (and he was well persuaded of the steadfastness of her resolution) for any human consideration disclose.", "그리고 만약 그런 경우에 혈연이라는 게 있다면, 자기가 그 아이의 이모라고. 그 이상은 어떤 인간적인 사정을 들어서도 결코 (그리고 그는 그 결심이 얼마나 굳센지 충분히 확신했지) 밝히지 않겠다고 했어."], ["My dear, I have told you all.”", "얘야, 이게 내가 아는 전부란다.\""]], [["I held his hand for a little while in mine.", "나는 그의 손을 잠시 내 두 손에 쥐고 있었습니다."]], [["“I saw my ward oftener than she saw me,”", "\"나는 내 피후견인이 나를 보는 것보다 더 자주 그 아이를 보았고,\""], ["he added, cheerily making light of it, “and I always knew she was beloved, useful, and happy.", "그가 별일 아닌 듯 명랑하게 덧붙였습니다. \"그 아이가 사랑받고, 쓸모 있고, 행복하다는 걸 늘 알고 있었지."], ["She repays me twenty-thousandfold, and twenty more to that, every hour in every day!”", "그 아이는 하루하루, 매시간 나에게 이만 배로, 아니 거기에 또 이만 배를 더해 갚아준단다!\""]], [["“And oftener still,”", "\"그리고 그보다 더 자주,\""], ["said I, “she blesses the guardian who is a father to her!”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그 아이는 자기에게 아버지나 다름없는 후견인에게 축복을 빌어요!\""]], [["At the word father, I saw his former trouble come into his face.", "아버지라는 말에, 나는 그 얼굴에 아까의 그 근심이 떠오르는 것을 보았습니다."], ["He subdued it as before, and it was gone in an instant; but it had been there and it had come so swiftly upon my words that I felt as if they had given him a shock.", "그는 앞서처럼 그것을 가라앉혔고, 그것은 순식간에 사라졌습니다. 하지만 분명히 거기 있었고, 내 말이 떨어지자마자 어찌나 빠르게 떠올랐던지, 마치 내 말이 그에게 충격을 준 것만 같았습니다."], ["I again inwardly repeated, wondering, “That I could readily understand.", "나는 또다시 의아해하며 속으로 되뇌었습니다. \"내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 거라니."], ["None that I could readily understand!”", "내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 건 아니라니!\""], ["No, it was true.", "그래요, 사실이었습니다."], ["I did not understand it.", "나는 그것을 이해하지 못했습니다."], ["Not for many and many a day.", "그 후로도 오래오래 이해하지 못했습니다."]], [["“Take a fatherly good night, my dear,”", "\"아버지가 하듯 잘 자라는 인사를 받으렴, 얘야.\""], ["said he, kissing me on the forehead, “and so to rest.", "그가 내 이마에 입을 맞추며 말했습니다. \"그리고 가서 쉬어."], ["These are late hours for working and thinking.", "일하고 생각하기엔 너무 늦은 시각이구나."], ["You do that for all of us, all day long, little housekeeper!”", "너는 하루 종일 우리 모두를 위해 그러고 있잖니, 작은 살림꾼아!\""]], [["I neither worked nor thought any more that night.", "그날 밤 나는 더는 일도 생각도 하지 않았습니다."], ["I opened my grateful heart to heaven in thankfulness for its providence to me and its care of me, and fell asleep.", "나는 나를 향한 하늘의 섭리와 보살핌에 감사하며 고마운 마음을 하늘에 활짝 열어 보이고는 잠이 들었습니다."]], [["We had a visitor next day.", "다음 날 우리에게 손님이 찾아왔습니다."], ["Mr. Allan Woodcourt came.", "앨런 우드코트 씨가 온 것입니다."], ["He came to take leave of us; he had settled to do so beforehand.", "그는 우리에게 작별을 고하러 왔는데, 미리 그렇게 하기로 정해둔 터였습니다."], ["He was going to China and to India as a surgeon on board ship.", "그는 선상 군의관으로 중국과 인도로 떠날 예정이었습니다."], ["He was to be away a long, long time.", "아주, 아주 오랫동안 떠나 있을 참이었습니다."]], [["I believe—at least I know—that he was not rich.", "나는 그가 부유하지 않다고 믿습니다─아니, 알고 있습니다."], ["All his widowed mother could spare had been spent in qualifying him for his profession.", "홀로 된 어머니가 마련할 수 있는 모든 것이 그의 직업 자격을 갖추는 데 들어갔던 것이지요."], ["It was not lucrative to a young practitioner, with very little influence in London; and although he was, night and day, at the service of numbers of poor people and did wonders of gentleness and skill for them, he gained very little by it in money.", "런던에서 별다른 연줄도 없는 젊은 개업의에게 그 일은 벌이가 좋지 않았습니다. 그는 밤낮으로 수많은 가난한 사람들을 위해 헌신하며 그들에게 놀랍도록 다정하고 능숙한 치료를 베풀었지만, 그것으로 돈은 거의 벌지 못했습니다."], ["He was seven years older than I.", "그는 나보다 일곱 살 위였습니다."], ["Not that I need mention it, for it hardly seems to belong to anything.", "굳이 말할 필요는 없습니다. 무엇과도 별 상관이 없어 보이니까요."]], [["I think—I mean, he told us—that he had been in practice three or four years and that if he could have hoped to contend through three or four more, he would not have made the voyage on which he was bound.", "내 생각에는─그러니까, 그가 우리에게 말하기로는─그가 삼사 년 정도 개업해 있었고, 만약 앞으로 삼사 년을 더 버텨낼 수 있으리라는 희망이 있었다면 그는 이번에 떠나는 항해에 나서지 않았으리라는 것이었습니다."], ["But he had no fortune or private means, and so he was going away.", "하지만 그에게는 재산도 사사로운 수입도 없었기에 떠나는 것이었습니다."], ["He had been to see us several times altogether.", "그는 그동안 우리를 여러 번 찾아왔었습니다."], ["We thought it a pity he should go away.", "우리는 그가 떠나는 게 안타까웠습니다."], ["Because he was distinguished in his art among those who knew it best, and some of the greatest men belonging to it had a high opinion of him.", "그는 그 분야를 가장 잘 아는 이들 사이에서 두각을 나타냈고, 거기에 속한 가장 위대한 인물들 가운데 더러는 그를 높이 평가했으니까요."]], [["When he came to bid us good-bye, he brought his mother with him for the first time.", "작별 인사를 하러 왔을 때, 그는 처음으로 어머니를 모시고 왔습니다."], ["She was a pretty old lady, with bright black eyes, but she seemed proud.", "그녀는 빛나는 검은 눈을 가진 어여쁜 노부인이었지만, 자존심이 강해 보였습니다."], ["She came from Wales and had had, a long time ago, an eminent person for an ancestor, of the name of Morgan ap-Kerrig—of some place that sounded like Gimlet—who was the most illustrious person that ever was known and all of whose relations were a sort of royal family.", "그녀는 웨일스 출신으로, 아주 오래전에 모건 앱케리그라는 이름의 저명한 조상을 두었다는데─김릿 비슷하게 들리는 어느 고장 사람이라고 했습니다─이제껏 알려진 가장 빛나는 인물이었으며 그의 친척이라면 죄다 일종의 왕족 같은 집안이었다고 했습니다."], ["He appeared to have passed his life in always getting up into mountains and fighting somebody; and a bard whose name sounded like Crumlinwallinwer had sung his praises in a piece which was called, as nearly as I could catch it, Mewlinnwillinwodd.", "그분은 평생토록 늘 산에 올라 누군가와 싸우며 보낸 듯했고, 크럼린왈린워라고 들리는 이름의 음유시인이 그의 공적을 기려, 내가 알아들은 바로는 뮬린윌린워드라는 제목의 노래를 지어 불렀다고 했습니다."]], [["Mrs. Woodcourt, after expatiating to us on the fame of her great kinsman, said that no doubt wherever her son Allan went he would remember his pedigree and would on no account form an alliance below it.", "우드코트 부인은 그 위대한 친척의 명성을 우리에게 한껏 늘어놓고 나서, 자기 아들 앨런이 어디를 가든 틀림없이 그 가문의 내력을 기억하여 결코 그보다 낮은 혼인을 맺지 않으리라고 말했습니다."], ["She told him that there were many handsome English ladies in India who went out on speculation, and that there were some to be picked up with property, but that neither charms nor wealth would suffice for the descendant from such a line without birth, which must ever be the first consideration.", "그녀는 아들에게 일렀습니다. 인도에는 한밑천 잡으러 건너간 잘생긴 영국 숙녀가 많고, 그중에는 재산까지 갖춘 이도 더러 줍듯이 얻을 수 있지만, 그런 가문의 후손에게는 미모도 재산도 가문의 혈통 없이는 충분치 않으며, 혈통이야말로 언제나 으뜸으로 따져야 할 것이라고요."], ["She talked so much about birth that for a moment I half fancied, and with pain—But what an idle fancy to suppose that she could think or care what MINE was!", "그녀가 어찌나 혈통 얘기를 많이 하던지 한순간 나는 어렴풋이, 그것도 아픈 마음으로 생각했습니다─하지만 그녀가 내 혈통이 어떤지를 생각하거나 마음 쓸 거라고 짐작하다니, 얼마나 부질없는 공상인지요!"]], [["Mr. Woodcourt seemed a little distressed by her prolixity, but he was too considerate to let her see it and contrived delicately to bring the conversation round to making his acknowledgments to my guardian for his hospitality and for the very happy hours—he called them the very happy hours—he had passed with us.", "우드코트 씨는 어머니의 장황함에 다소 난처해하는 듯했지만, 그것을 그녀에게 내비치기에는 너무도 사려 깊은 사람이어서, 슬며시 화제를 돌려 후견인에게 그 환대와, 우리와 함께 보낸 더없이 행복한 시간─그는 더없이 행복한 시간이라고 했습니다─에 대한 감사의 말을 전하게끔 했습니다."], ["The recollection of them, he said, would go with him wherever he went and would be always treasured.", "그 시간들의 기억이 어디를 가든 자기와 함께할 것이며 언제까지나 소중히 간직되리라고 그는 말했습니다. 그래서 우리는 한 사람씩 차례로 그에게 손을 내밀었고─적어도, 그들이 그랬고, 나도 그랬습니다."], ["And so we gave him our hands, one after another—at least, they did—and I did; and so he put his lips to Ada’s hand—and to mine; and so he went away upon his long, long voyage!", "그래서 그는 에이다의 손에─그리고 내 손에 입술을 댔습니다. 그러고는 그 아주, 아주 긴 항해를 떠났습니다!"]], [["I was very busy indeed all day and wrote directions home to the servants, and wrote notes for my guardian, and dusted his books and papers, and jingled my housekeeping keys a good deal, one way and another.", "나는 그날 하루 종일 정말 분주하게, 집의 하인들에게 보낼 지시를 적고, 후견인에게 전할 쪽지를 쓰고, 그의 책과 서류를 털고, 이런저런 일로 살림 열쇠 꾸러미를 꽤 짤랑거렸습니다."], ["I was still busy between the lights, singing and working by the window, when who should come in but Caddy, whom I had no expectation of seeing!", "해 질 녘 어스름 속에서 창가에서 노래하고 바느질하며 여전히 분주하던 차에, 뜻밖에도 누가 들어왔나 했더니 글쎄 캐디가 아니겠어요. 올 줄은 전혀 몰랐는데요!"]], [["“Why, Caddy, my dear,”", "\"어머, 캐디,\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “what beautiful flowers!”", "\"이 아름다운 꽃들 좀 봐!\""]], [["She had such an exquisite little nosegay in her hand.", "그 애 손에는 더없이 어여쁜 작은 꽃다발이 들려 있었습니다."]], [["“Indeed, I think so, Esther,”", "\"정말 그래, 에스더.\""], ["replied Caddy.", "캐디가 대답했습니다."], ["“They are the loveliest I ever saw.”", "\"내가 본 것 중에 제일 사랑스러운 꽃이야.\""]], [["“Prince, my dear?”", "\"프린스가 준 거지, 응?\""], ["said I in a whisper.", "내가 속삭이듯 물었습니다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["answered Caddy, shaking her head and holding them to me to smell.", "캐디가 고개를 가로저으며 꽃을 내게 내밀어 향기를 맡게 해주었습니다."], ["“Not Prince.”", "\"프린스 아니야.\""]], [["“Well, to be sure, Caddy!”", "\"어머나, 캐디!\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I. “You must have two lovers!”", "\"너 애인이 둘이구나!\""]], [["“What?", "\"왜?"], ["Do they look like that sort of thing?”", "이게 그런 것처럼 보여?\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."]], [["“Do they look like that sort of thing?”", "\"이게 그런 것처럼 보이냐고?\""], ["I repeated, pinching her cheek.", "나는 그 애의 뺨을 꼬집으며 되물었습니다."]], [["Caddy only laughed in return, and telling me that she had come for half an hour, at the expiration of which time Prince would be waiting for her at the corner, sat chatting with me and Ada in the window, every now and then handing me the flowers again or trying how they looked against my hair.", "캐디는 대답 대신 웃기만 하면서, 삼십 분 정도 있다 가려고 왔고 그 시간이 지나면 프린스가 모퉁이에서 기다리고 있을 거라고 했습니다. 그러고는 나와 에이다와 함께 창가에 앉아 두런두런 이야기를 나누며, 이따금 그 꽃을 내게 다시 건네기도 하고 내 머리에 대보며 어울리는지 살펴보기도 했습니다."], ["At last, when she was going, she took me into my room and put them in my dress.", "마침내 가려고 할 때, 그 애는 나를 내 방으로 데려가 그 꽃을 내 옷에 꽂아주었습니다."]], [["“For me?”", "\"나한테 주는 거야?\""], ["said I, surprised.", "내가 놀라서 물었습니다."]], [["“For you,” said Caddy with a kiss.", "\"너한테 주는 거야.\""], ["“They were left behind by somebody.”", "캐디가 입을 맞추며 말했습니다. \"누가 두고 간 거거든.\""]], [["“Left behind?”", "\"두고 갔다고?\""]], [["“At poor Miss Flite’s,” said Caddy.", "\"가엾은 플라이트 양 집에서.\""], ["“Somebody who has been very good to her was hurrying away an hour ago to join a ship and left these flowers behind.", "캐디가 말했습니다. \"그분에게 무척 잘해주던 누군가가 한 시간쯤 전에 배를 타러 부랴부랴 떠나면서 이 꽃을 두고 갔어."], ["No, no! Don’t take them out.", "안 돼, 안 돼!"], ["Let the pretty little things lie here,”", "빼지 마. 이 예쁜 것들 그냥 여기 두렴.\""], ["said Caddy, adjusting them with a careful hand, “because I was present myself, and I shouldn’t wonder if somebody left them on purpose!”", "캐디가 조심스러운 손길로 꽃을 매만지며 말했습니다. \"내가 직접 그 자리에 있었는데, 누군가 일부러 두고 갔다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 것 같으니까!\""]], [["“Do they look like that sort of thing?”", "\"이게 그런 것처럼 보이냐고?\""], ["said Ada, coming laughingly behind me and clasping me merrily round the waist.", "에이다가 웃으며 내 뒤로 다가와 즐겁게 내 허리를 감싸 안으며 말했습니다."], ["“Oh, yes, indeed they do, Dame Durden!", "\"아, 그래, 정말 그래 보여, 더든 아주머니!"], ["They look very, very like that sort of thing.", "정말, 정말 그런 것처럼 보인다고."], ["Oh, very like it indeed, my dear!”", "아, 정말이지 똑 그래 보여, 얘!\""]], [["Lady Dedlock\nIt was not so easy as it had appeared at first to arrange for Richard’s making a trial of Mr. Kenge’s office.", "리처드가 켄지 씨 사무실에서 시험 삼아 일하도록 주선하는 일은 처음 보였던 것만큼 쉽지 않았습니다."], ["Richard himself was the chief impediment.", "가장 큰 걸림돌은 리처드 자신이었습니다."], ["As soon as he had it in his power to leave Mr. Badger at any moment, he began to doubt whether he wanted to leave him at all.", "언제든 마음만 먹으면 배저 씨 곁을 떠날 수 있게 되자, 그는 도리어 정말 떠나고 싶은 건지 의심하기 시작했습니다."], ["He didn’t know, he said, really.", "솔직히 잘 모르겠다고 했습니다."], ["It wasn’t a bad profession; he couldn’t assert that he disliked it; perhaps he liked it as well as he liked any other—suppose he gave it one more chance!", "나쁜 직업도 아니고, 싫다고 단언할 수도 없으며, 어쩌면 다른 어떤 일만큼이나 좋아하는지도 모르니, 한 번만 더 기회를 줘보면 어떻겠느냐는 것이었습니다."], ["Upon that, he shut himself up for a few weeks with some books and some bones and seemed to acquire a considerable fund of information with great rapidity.", "그러고는 몇 주 동안 책 몇 권과 뼈 몇 개를 끼고 틀어박혀, 놀라운 속도로 상당한 지식을 쌓아가는 듯했습니다."], ["His fervour, after lasting about a month, began to cool, and when it was quite cooled, began to grow warm again.", "그러나 한 달쯤 이어지던 열정은 식어가기 시작했고, 완전히 식고 나자 다시 뜨거워지기 시작했습니다."], ["His vacillations between law and medicine lasted so long that midsummer arrived before he finally separated from Mr.", "법학과 의학 사이를 오가는 그의 갈팡질팡은 어찌나 오래 이어졌던지, 결국 배저 씨와 갈라서서 켄지-카보이 법률사무소의 시험 과정에 들어가기까지 한여름이 와버렸습니다."], ["Badger and entered on an experimental course of Messrs.", "그렇게 변덕스러우면서도 리처드는 \"이번에는\""], ["Kenge and Carboy. For all his waywardness, he took great credit to himself as being determined to be in earnest “this time.”", "반드시 진지하게 임하겠노라 결심했다며 스스로를 무척 대견해했습니다."], ["And he was so good-natured throughout, and in such high spirits, and so fond of Ada, that it was very difficult indeed to be otherwise than pleased with him.", "게다가 줄곧 어찌나 사람 좋고, 기분이 들떠 있고, 에이다를 끔찍이 아꼈던지, 그를 기쁘게 여기지 않기란 정말이지 어려웠습니다."]], [["“As to Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 아저씨 말이야,\""], ["who, I may mention, found the wind much given, during this period, to stick in the east; “As to Mr. Jarndyce,”", "그 무렵 잔다이스 아저씨는 바람이 자꾸 동쪽으로 분다고 느끼셨다는 점을 여기서 짚어두겠습니다."], ["Richard would say to me, “he is the finest fellow in the world, Esther!", "\"잔다이스 아저씨 말이야,\" 리처드는 내게 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다."], ["I must be particularly careful, if it were only for his satisfaction, to take myself well to task and have a regular wind-up of this business now.”", "\"이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 분이야, 에스더! 다른 건 다 제쳐두고 아저씨를 만족시켜드리기 위해서라도, 나는 각별히 조심해서 나 자신을 단단히 다잡고 이번 일을 깔끔하게 마무리지어야 해.\""]], [["The idea of his taking himself well to task, with that laughing face and heedless manner and with a fancy that everything could catch and nothing could hold, was ludicrously anomalous.", "그토록 잘 웃는 얼굴과 무심한 태도로, 무엇이든 손에 잡되 무엇도 붙들지 못하는 성미로 자기 자신을 단단히 다잡겠다는 발상은, 우습도록 앞뒤가 맞지 않았습니다."], ["However, he told us between-whiles that he was doing it to such an extent that he wondered his hair didn’t turn grey.", "그런데도 그는 이따금, 어찌나 단단히 다잡고 있는지 머리가 세지 않는 게 신기할 지경이라고 우리에게 말하곤 했습니다."], ["His regular wind-up of the business was (as I have said) that he went to Mr. Kenge’s about midsummer to try how he liked it.", "그가 일을 깔끔하게 마무리지은 방식이란 (앞서 말했듯이) 한여름쯤 켄지 씨 사무실로 가서 그 일이 마음에 드는지 시험해보는 것이었습니다."]], [["All this time he was, in money affairs, what I have described him in a former illustration—generous, profuse, wildly careless, but fully persuaded that he was rather calculating and prudent.", "이 무렵 내내 돈 문제에서 그는 앞서 한 차례 묘사했던 그대로였습니다. 너그럽고, 헤프고, 어처구니없이 무신경하면서도, 자신이 제법 계산적이고 신중하다고 철석같이 믿었습니다."], ["I happened to say to Ada, in his presence, half jestingly, half seriously, about the time of his going to Mr.", "그가 켄지 씨 사무실로 갈 무렵, 나는 그 앞에서 에이다에게 반쯤 농담조로 반쯤 진담조로, 돈을 저렇게 우습게 아니 포르투나투스의 지갑이라도 가져야겠다고 말한 적이 있습니다."], ["Kenge’s, that he needed to have Fortunatus’ purse, he made so light of money, which he answered in this way, “My jewel of a dear cousin, you hear this old woman!", "그러자 그는 이렇게 대꾸했습니다. \"내 보배 같은 사촌 누이야, 이 늙은 할멈 소리 좀 들어봐!"], ["Why does she say that?", "왜 저런 소리를 할까?"], ["Because I gave eight pounds odd (or whatever it was) for a certain neat waistcoat and buttons a few days ago.", "내가 며칠 전에 멋진 조끼와 단추를 사느라 팔 파운드 남짓(얼마였든)을 썼다고 저러는 거야."], ["Now, if I had stayed at Badger’s I should have been obliged to spend twelve pounds at a blow for some heart-breaking lecture-fees.", "그런데 말이지, 내가 배저 씨네 그냥 있었다면 가슴 찢어지는 강의료로 단번에 십이 파운드를 써야 했을 거야. 그러니 이 거래로 나는 사 파운드를—한꺼번에—번 셈이지!\""], ["So I make four pounds—in a lump—by the transaction!”", "* 포르투나투스의 지갑: 중세 설화에 나오는 마법의 지갑으로, 꺼내도 꺼내도 돈이 마르지 않는다."]], [["It was a question much discussed between him and my guardian what arrangements should be made for his living in London while he experimented on the law, for we had long since gone back to Bleak House, and it was too far off to admit of his coming there oftener than once a week.", "그가 법학을 시험해보는 동안 런던에서 어떻게 지내게 할지를 두고, 그와 후견인 사이에 의논이 많았습니다. 우리는 진작에 황폐한 집으로 돌아온 터라, 거리가 너무 멀어서 그가 그곳으로 일주일에 한 번 이상 오기란 무리였기 때문입니다."], ["My guardian told me that if Richard were to settle down at Mr. Kenge’s he would take some apartments or chambers where we too could occasionally stay for a few days at a time; “but, little woman,”", "후견인은 만약 리처드가 켄지 씨 사무실에 자리를 잡는다면 우리도 가끔 며칠씩 묵을 수 있는 셋집이나 사무실을 얻겠다고 하셨습니다."], ["he added, rubbing his head very significantly, “he hasn’t settled down there yet!”", "\"하지만 말이다, 꼬마 아가씨,\" 후견인은 의미심장하게 머리를 문지르며 덧붙이셨습니다."], ["The discussions ended in our hiring for him, by the month, a neat little furnished lodging in a quiet old house near Queen Square.", "\"그 녀석은 아직 거기 자리를 잡지 못했어!\" 의논 끝에 우리는 퀸 광장 근처의 한적한 옛집에 가구가 갖춰진 아담한 셋방을 달치기로 빌려주었습니다."], ["He immediately began to spend all the money he had in buying the oddest little ornaments and luxuries for this lodging; and so often as Ada and I dissuaded him from making any purchase that he had in contemplation which was particularly unnecessary and expensive, he took credit for what it would have cost and made out that to spend anything less on something else was to save the difference.", "그는 곧장 가진 돈을 죄다 쏟아부어 이 셋방을 꾸밀 더없이 별난 자잘한 장식품과 사치품을 사들였습니다. 그래서 에이다와 내가, 그가 사려고 마음먹은 물건 가운데 유난히 불필요하고 값비싼 것을 사지 말라고 만류할 때마다, 그는 그 물건에 들었을 비용을 자기 공으로 돌리고는, 다른 데 그보다 적게 쓰면 그 차액을 아끼는 셈이라고 우겼습니다."]], [["While these affairs were in abeyance, our visit to Mr. Boythorn’s was postponed.", "이런 일들이 마무리되지 않은 채 미뤄지는 동안, 보이손 씨 댁을 찾는 방문도 늦춰졌습니다."], ["At length, Richard having taken possession of his lodging, there was nothing to prevent our departure.", "마침내 리처드가 셋방에 들어가면서, 우리의 출발을 막을 일은 아무것도 없게 되었습니다."], ["He could have gone with us at that time of the year very well, but he was in the full novelty of his new position and was making most energetic attempts to unravel the mysteries of the fatal suit.", "그 계절이면 그도 우리와 함께 충분히 갈 수 있었지만, 새 자리의 신선함에 푹 빠져 그 운명적인 소송의 수수께끼를 풀어보려고 무척 열성을 내던 참이었습니다."], ["Consequently we went without him, and my darling was delighted to praise him for being so busy.", "그래서 우리는 그 없이 떠났고, 내 사랑하는 동무는 그가 그렇게 바쁜 것이 기특하다며 기뻐서 칭찬했습니다."]], [["We made a pleasant journey down into Lincolnshire by the coach and had an entertaining companion in Mr. Skimpole.", "우리는 역마차로 즐거운 여정을 따라 링컨셔로 내려갔고, 스킴폴 씨라는 재미난 길동무도 함께였습니다."], ["His furniture had been all cleared off, it appeared, by the person who took possession of it on his blue-eyed daughter’s birthday, but he seemed quite relieved to think that it was gone.", "듣자 하니 그의 가구는 그의 파란 눈 딸 생일날 압류해 간 사람이 죄다 실어 가버렸다는데, 그는 가구가 사라졌다고 생각하니 오히려 후련해하는 눈치였습니다."], ["Chairs and table, he said, were wearisome objects; they were monotonous ideas, they had no variety of expression, they looked you out of countenance, and you looked them out of countenance.", "의자며 탁자며 따분한 물건이라고 그는 말했습니다. 단조로운 관념이고, 표정에 변화라곤 없으며, 사람을 멋쩍게 노려보면 사람도 그것들을 멋쩍게 노려본다는 것이었습니다."], ["How pleasant, then, to be bound to no particular chairs and tables, but to sport like a butterfly among all the furniture on hire, and to flit from rosewood to mahogany, and from mahogany to walnut, and from this shape to that, as the humour took one!", "그러니 어떤 특정한 의자와 탁자에 매이지 않고, 빌린 가구들 사이를 나비처럼 날아다니며, 자단목에서 마호가니로, 마호가니에서 호두나무로, 이 모양에서 저 모양으로, 기분 내키는 대로 옮겨 다니니 얼마나 즐거운가!"]], [["“The oddity of the thing is,” said Mr.", "\"이 일의 우스운 점은 말이지,\""], ["Skimpole with a quickened sense of the ludicrous, “that my chairs and tables were not paid for, and yet my landlord walks off with them as composedly as possible.", "스킴폴 씨가 더욱 예민해진 익살스러운 감각으로 말했습니다. \"내 의자와 탁자 값은 치르지도 않았는데, 집주인이 그걸 더없이 태연하게 들고 가버린다는 거야."], ["Now, that seems droll!", "이거 참 익살맞지 않은가!"], ["There is something grotesque in it.", "어딘가 괴상한 데가 있어."], ["The chair and table merchant never engaged to pay my landlord my rent.", "의자와 탁자 상인은 내 집세를 집주인에게 대신 내주겠다고 약속한 적이 없거든."], ["Why should my landlord quarrel with HIM?", "그런데 집주인이 어째서 그 사람과 다투는 거지?"], ["If I have a pimple on my nose which is disagreeable to my landlord’s peculiar ideas of beauty, my landlord has no business to scratch my chair and table merchant’s nose, which has no pimple on it.", "만약 내 코에 뾰루지가 났는데 그게 집주인 특유의 미의식에 거슬린다면, 집주인이 뾰루지 하나 없는 의자와 탁자 상인의 코를 긁어댈 까닭이 없잖나."], ["His reasoning seems defective!”", "그 사람 논리는 결함이 있는 것 같아!\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["said my guardian good-humouredly, “it’s pretty clear that whoever became security for those chairs and tables will have to pay for them.”", "후견인이 너그럽게 말씀하셨습니다. \"그 의자와 탁자에 보증을 선 사람이 누구든 결국 그 값을 치러야 한다는 건 꽤 명백하지.\""]], [["“Exactly!”", "\"바로 그거예요!\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“That’s the crowning point of unreason in the business!", "\"이 일에서 가장 어처구니없는 대목이 바로 그겁니다!"], ["I said to my landlord, ‘My good man, you are not aware that my excellent friend Jarndyce will have to pay for those things that you are sweeping off in that indelicate manner.", "내가 집주인에게 말했지요. '여보게, 자네 그 물건들을 그렇게 무례하게 쓸어 가지만, 결국 그 값은 내 훌륭한 친구 잔다이스가 치러야 한다는 걸 모르나?"], ["Have you no consideration for HIS property?’ He hadn’t the least.”", "자네는 그분 재산은 안중에도 없는가?' 그런데 손톱만큼도 없더군요.\""]], [["“And refused all proposals,”", "\"그러고는 모든 제안을 거절했고,\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["“Refused all proposals,” returned Mr. Skimpole. “I made him business proposals. I had him into my room. I said, ‘You are a man of business, I believe?’ He replied, ‘I am.’ ‘Very well,’ said I, ‘now let us be business-like. Here is an inkstand, here are pens and paper, here are wafers. What do you want? I have occupied your house for a considerable period, I believe to our mutual satisfaction until this unpleasant misunderstanding arose; let us be at once friendly and business-like. What do you want?’ In reply to this, he made use of the figurative expression—which has something Eastern about it—that he had never seen the colour of my money. ‘My amiable friend,’ said I, ‘I never have any money. I never know anything about money.’ ‘Well, sir,’ said he, ‘what do you offer if I give you time?’ ‘My good fellow,’ said I, ‘I have no idea of time; but you say you are a man of business, and whatever you can suggest to be done in a business-like way with pen, and ink, and paper—and wafers—I am ready to do. Don’t pay yourself at another man’s expense (which is foolish), but be business-like!’ However, he wouldn’t be, and there was an end of it.”", "\"모든 제안을 거절했어요,\" 스킴폴 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"내가 사무적인 제안을 했거든요. 그자를 내 방으로 불러들였지요. 내가 말했어요. '자네 사업가지, 그렇지?' 그자가 대답하더군요. '그렇소.' '아주 좋아,' 내가 말했지요. '그럼 우리 사업가답게 해보세. 여기 잉크병이 있고, 펜과 종이가 있고, 봉인지가 있네. 자네는 무얼 원하나? 나는 자네 집에 꽤 오랫동안 살았고, 이 불쾌한 오해가 생기기 전까지는 서로 만족스러웠다고 믿네. 그러니 다정하면서도 사업가답게 굴어보세. 자네는 무얼 원하나?' 이에 그자는—어딘가 동방풍이 묻어나는—비유적인 표현을 쓰더군요. 내 돈은 색깔조차 본 적이 없다고요. '나의 정겨운 친구여,' 내가 말했지요. '나는 돈이라곤 한 푼도 없다네. 돈에 관해서는 아무것도 모르고.' '그럼, 선생,' 그자가 말하더군요. '시간을 드리면 무얼 내놓으시겠소?' '여보게,' 내가 말했지요. '나는 시간이란 게 뭔지 도무지 모른다네. 하지만 자네는 사업가라고 하니, 펜과 잉크와 종이로—그리고 봉인지로—사업가답게 처리할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 나는 기꺼이 하겠네. 남의 돈으로 자네 잇속을 챙기지는 말게(그건 어리석은 짓이니), 사업가답게 굴라고!' 그런데도 그자는 그러질 못하더군요. 그걸로 끝이었어요.\""]], [["If these were some of the inconveniences of Mr. Skimpole’s childhood, it assuredly possessed its advantages too.", "이런 것들이 스킴폴 씨의 어린아이 같은 천성이 안긴 불편 가운데 하나였다면, 그 천성에는 분명 이점도 있었습니다."], ["On the journey he had a very good appetite for such refreshment as came in our way (including a basket of choice hothouse peaches), but never thought of paying for anything.", "여정 내내 그는 우리 손에 들어온 먹을거리(고급 온실 복숭아 한 바구니까지 포함해서)를 아주 맛있게 즐겼지만, 무엇 하나 값을 치를 생각은 하지 않았습니다."], ["So when the coachman came round for his fee, he pleasantly asked him what he considered a very good fee indeed, now—a liberal one—and on his replying half a crown for a single passenger, said it was little enough too, all things considered, and left Mr. Jarndyce to give it him.", "그래서 마부가 삯을 받으러 다가오자, 그는 마부에게 정말 후한 삯이란, 그러니까 넉넉한 삯이란 얼마라고 생각하느냐고 다정하게 물었고, 승객 한 명당 반 크라운이라는 대답을 듣자, 이것저것 따져보면 그것도 적은 편이라며, 잔다이스 씨가 대신 치르도록 맡겨버렸습니다."]], [["It was delightful weather.", "날씨는 더없이 화창했습니다."], ["The green corn waved so beautifully, the larks sang so joyfully, the hedges were so full of wild flowers, the trees were so thickly out in leaf, the bean-fields, with a light wind blowing over them, filled the air with such a delicious fragrance!", "푸른 곡식이 어찌나 아름답게 물결치는지, 종다리가 어찌나 즐겁게 노래하는지, 산울타리는 들꽃으로 가득하고, 나무들은 잎이 빽빽하게 돋았으며, 콩밭 위로 산들바람이 불어와 공기를 그토록 향긋한 내음으로 채웠습니다!"], ["Late in the afternoon we came to the market-town where we were to alight from the coach—a dull little town with a church-spire, and a marketplace, and a market-cross, and one intensely sunny street, and a pond with an old horse cooling his legs in it, and a very few men sleepily lying and standing about in narrow little bits of shade.", "오후 늦게 우리는 역마차에서 내려야 할 장날 마을에 다다랐습니다. 교회 첨탑이 있고, 장터가 있고, 장터 십자가가 있고, 햇살이 쨍쨍 내리쬐는 거리 하나가 있고, 늙은 말 한 마리가 다리를 식히는 연못이 있고, 비좁은 그늘 자투리에 졸린 듯 눕거나 서 있는 남자 몇몇이 있는 따분한 작은 마을이었습니다."], ["After the rustling of the leaves and the waving of the corn all along the road, it looked as still, as hot, as motionless a little town as England could produce.", "길을 따라 줄곧 나뭇잎이 살랑거리고 곡식이 물결치던 풍경을 보다가 오니, 잉글랜드가 빚어낼 수 있는 가장 고요하고, 가장 무덥고, 가장 미동도 없는 작은 마을처럼 보였습니다."]], [["At the inn we found Mr. Boythorn on horseback, waiting with an open carriage to take us to his house, which was a few miles off.", "여관에서 우리는 말을 탄 보이손 씨를 만났는데, 그는 몇 마일 떨어진 자기 집으로 우리를 데려가려고 덮개 없는 마차를 대기시켜놓고 기다리던 중이었습니다."], ["He was overjoyed to see us and dismounted with great alacrity.", "그는 우리를 보고 몹시 기뻐하며 잽싸게 말에서 내렸습니다."]], [["“By heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["said he after giving us a courteous greeting.", "정중하게 인사를 건넨 뒤 그가 말했습니다."], ["“This a most infamous coach.", "\"이건 더없이 형편없는 역마차로군요."], ["It is the most flagrant example of an abominable public vehicle that ever encumbered the face of the earth.", "지구상에 짐스럽게 굴러다닌 가증스러운 대중교통수단 가운데서도 가장 노골적인 본보기입니다."], ["It is twenty-five minutes after its time this afternoon.", "오늘 오후엔 정각보다 이십오 분이나 늦었어요."], ["The coachman ought to be put to death!”", "저 마부는 사형감이오!\""]], [["“IS he after his time?”", "\"정각보다 늦었나요?\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole, to whom he happened to address himself.", "마침 보이손 씨가 말을 건넨 스킴폴 씨가 물었습니다."], ["“You know my infirmity.”", "\"내 약점은 잘 아시잖소.\""]], [["“Twenty-five minutes!", "\"이십오 분!"], ["Twenty-six minutes!”", "이십육 분!\""], ["replied Mr. Boythorn, referring to his watch.", "보이손 씨가 회중시계를 보며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“With two ladies in the coach, this scoundrel has deliberately delayed his arrival six and twenty minutes.", "\"숙녀 두 분을 태우고서도 저 악당은 일부러 이십육 분이나 늦게 도착한 거요."], ["Deliberately!", "일부러!"], ["It is impossible that it can be accidental!", "우연일 리가 없소!"], ["But his father—and his uncle—were the most profligate coachmen that ever sat upon a box.”", "게다가 저자의 아비도—그 삼촌도—마부석에 앉았던 자들 가운데 가장 방탕한 작자들이었지.\""]], [["While he said this in tones of the greatest indignation, he handed us into the little phaeton with the utmost gentleness and was all smiles and pleasure.", "이렇게 더없이 분개한 어조로 말하면서도, 그는 더없이 부드럽게 우리를 작은 사륜마차에 태웠고, 얼굴엔 온통 웃음과 기쁨이 가득했습니다."]], [["“I am sorry, ladies,”", "\"미안합니다, 숙녀분들,\""], ["he said, standing bare-headed at the carriage-door when all was ready, “that I am obliged to conduct you nearly two miles out of the way.", "채비가 다 끝나고 그가 마차 문 옆에 모자를 벗은 채 서서 말했습니다. \"어쩔 수 없이 두 마일 가까이 둘러 가야 하는군요."], ["But our direct road lies through Sir Leicester Dedlock’s park, and in that fellow’s property I have sworn never to set foot of mine, or horse’s foot of mine, pending the present relations between us, while I breathe the breath of life!”", "하지만 곧장 가는 길은 레스터 데드록 경의 영지를 가로지른답니다. 그자의 땅에는, 지금 우리 사이가 이런 관계로 있는 한, 내 발도 내 말 발도 결코 들이지 않겠다고 살아 숨 쉬는 동안 맹세했지요!\""], ["And here, catching my guardian’s eye, he broke into one of his tremendous laughs, which seemed to shake even the motionless little market-town.", "그러더니 후견인의 눈길과 마주치자, 그는 그 어마어마한 웃음을 한바탕 터뜨렸는데, 그 웃음은 미동도 없던 작은 장날 마을마저 뒤흔드는 듯했습니다."]], [["“Are the Dedlocks down here, Lawrence?”", "\"데드록 부부가 여기 내려와 있나, 로런스?\""], ["said my guardian as we drove along and Mr. Boythorn trotted on the green turf by the roadside.", "우리가 마차를 달리고 보이손 씨가 길가 푸른 잔디밭을 따라 말을 몰며 나아갈 때 후견인이 물으셨습니다."]], [["“Sir Arrogant Numskull is here,”", "\"거만한 멍청이 경께서 여기 와 계시지,\""], ["replied Mr. Boythorn.", "보이손 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Ha ha ha!", "\"하하하!"], ["Sir Arrogant is here, and I am glad to say, has been laid by the heels here.", "거만한 경께서 여기 와 계시고, 게다가 다행히도 여기 발이 묶여버리셨어."], ["My Lady,”", "마님은,\""], ["in naming whom he always made a courtly gesture as if particularly to exclude her from any part in the quarrel, “is expected, I believe, daily.", "그는 마님이라는 호칭을 부를 때면 늘, 마치 그 다툼에서 그녀만은 각별히 빼놓겠다는 듯 정중한 몸짓을 했습니다. \"내 짐작에 날마다 도착할 거라고 기다리는 모양이더군."], ["I am not in the least surprised that she postpones her appearance as long as possible.", "가능한 한 오래 모습을 미루는 게 나로선 조금도 놀랍지 않아."], ["Whatever can have induced that transcendent woman to marry that effigy and figure-head of a baronet is one of the most impenetrable mysteries that ever baffled human inquiry.", "도대체 무엇이 그 빼어난 여인을 저런 허울뿐인 준남작 인형에게 시집가게 했는지는, 인간의 탐구를 좌절시킨 가장 풀 수 없는 수수께끼 가운데 하나라네."], ["Ha ha ha ha!”", "하하하하!\""]], [["“I suppose,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said my guardian, laughing, “WE may set foot in the park while we are here?", "후견인이 웃으며 말씀하셨습니다. \"우리는 여기 있는 동안 영지에 발을 들여도 되겠지?"], ["The prohibition does not extend to us, does it?”", "그 금지령이 우리한테까지 미치진 않겠지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“I can lay no prohibition on my guests,”", "\"내 손님들에게는 어떤 금지령도 내릴 수 없습니다,\""], ["he said, bending his head to Ada and me with the smiling politeness which sat so gracefully upon him, “except in the matter of their departure.", "그가 에이다와 내게 고개를 숙이며 그토록 우아하게 어울리는 미소 띤 정중함으로 말했습니다. \"떠나는 일에 관해서만 빼고요."], ["I am only sorry that I cannot have the happiness of being their escort about Chesney Wold, which is a very fine place!", "다만 두 분을 모시고 체스니 월드를 안내하는 기쁨을 누릴 수 없다는 게 안타까울 따름입니다. 정말 멋진 곳이거든요!"], ["But by the light of this summer day, Jarndyce, if you call upon the owner while you stay with me, you are likely to have but a cool reception.", "하지만 잔다이스, 이 여름날의 햇빛에 두고 맹세하건대, 나와 머무는 동안 그 영지 주인을 찾아간다면 자네는 냉랭한 대접밖에 못 받을 걸세."], ["He carries himself like an eight-day clock at all times, like one of a race of eight-day clocks in gorgeous cases that never go and never went—Ha ha ha!—but he will have some extra stiffness, I can promise you, for the friends of his friend and neighbour Boythorn!”", "그자는 언제나 팔일 태엽 시계처럼 거드름을 피우거든. 한 번도 가지 않았고 앞으로도 가지 않을, 화려한 함에 든 팔일 태엽 시계 가문의 일원처럼 말이야—하하하!—하지만 장담컨대, 자기 친구이자 이웃인 보이손의 벗들에게는 한층 더 뻣뻣하게 굴 거야!\""]], [["“I shall not put him to the proof,”", "\"굳이 그걸 시험해보진 않겠네,\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“He is as indifferent to the honour of knowing me, I dare say, as I am to the honour of knowing him.", "\"그자가 나를 알게 되는 영광에 무심한 만큼, 나도 그자를 알게 되는 영광에 무심하다네."], ["The air of the grounds and perhaps such a view of the house as any other sightseer might get are quite enough for me.”", "영지의 공기와, 다른 구경꾼이라면 누구나 얻을 법한 저택 풍경 정도면 나로선 충분해.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said Mr. Boythorn.", "보이손 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I am glad of it on the whole.", "\"전체로 보면 그게 다행이지."], ["It’s in better keeping.", "그게 더 어울려."], ["I am looked upon about here as a second Ajax defying the lightning.", "이 근방에서 나는 번개에 맞서는 또 다른 아약스쯤으로 여겨진다네."], ["Ha ha ha ha!", "하하하하!"], ["When I go into our little church on a Sunday, a considerable part of the inconsiderable congregation expect to see me drop, scorched and withered, on the pavement under the Dedlock displeasure.", "일요일에 우리 작은 교회에 들어서면, 그 보잘것없는 신도들 가운데 상당수가, 내가 데드록의 노여움을 사서 포석 위에 그슬리고 시들어 털썩 쓰러지는 꼴을 볼 거라고 기대하지."], ["Ha ha ha ha!", "하하하하!"], ["I have no doubt he is surprised that I don’t.", "내가 그러지 않으니 그자가 놀라는 것도 무리는 아닐 거야."], ["For he is, by heaven, the most self-satisfied, and the shallowest, and the most coxcombical and utterly brainless ass!”", "그자는, 맹세코, 가장 자기만족에 빠지고, 가장 천박하고, 가장 멋이나 부리고, 골이라곤 텅텅 빈 얼간이거든!\""]], [["Our coming to the ridge of a hill we had been ascending enabled our friend to point out Chesney Wold itself to us and diverted his attention from its master.", "우리가 오르던 언덕 마루에 다다르자, 우리 벗은 체스니 월드 자체를 가리켜 보일 수 있게 되었고, 그 덕에 그의 관심도 저택 주인에게서 다른 데로 돌아갔습니다."]], [["It was a picturesque old house in a fine park richly wooded.", "* 아약스: 그리스 신화의 영웅."], ["Among the trees and not far from the residence he pointed out the spire of the little church of which he had spoken.", "폭풍 속에서 신들의 분노에 맞서 \"신들이 막아도 살아남겠다\"고 외치다 벼락을 맞아 죽었다는 이야기로 전해진다. 울창한 숲이 우거진 멋진 영지 안에 자리 잡은, 그림 같은 옛 저택이었습니다."], ["Oh, the solemn woods over which the light and shadow travelled swiftly, as if heavenly wings were sweeping on benignant errands through the summer air; the smooth green slopes, the glittering water, the garden where the flowers were so symmetrically arranged in clusters of the richest colours, how beautiful they looked!", "그는 나무들 사이로, 저택에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있는, 앞서 말했던 작은 교회의 첨탑을 가리켜 보였습니다. 아, 빛과 그림자가 빠르게 스쳐 가는 그 엄숙한 숲, 마치 천상의 날개가 여름 공기를 가르며 자비로운 심부름을 하는 듯했습니다."], ["The house, with gable and chimney, and tower, and turret, and dark doorway, and broad terrace-walk, twining among the balustrades of which, and lying heaped upon the vases, there was one great flush of roses, seemed scarcely real in its light solidity and in the serene and peaceful hush that rested on all around it.", "매끄러운 초록빛 비탈, 반짝이는 물, 더없이 짙은 빛깔의 꽃들이 무리지어 그토록 가지런히 배치된 정원, 그 모든 것이 얼마나 아름다워 보였는지요! 박공과 굴뚝과 탑과 작은 망루와 어두운 출입구와 널찍한 테라스 산책로를 갖추고, 그 난간 사이로 휘감기며 화병 위에 수북이 쌓인 장미가 한바탕 붉게 타오르는 그 저택은, 가벼운 듯 단단한 그 자태와 사방에 깃든 고요하고 평온한 정적 속에서, 도무지 실재하는 것 같지 않았습니다."], ["To Ada and to me, that above all appeared the pervading influence.", "에이다와 나에게는, 무엇보다 그 정적이 사방에 스며든 기운으로 다가왔습니다."], ["On everything, house, garden, terrace, green slopes, water, old oaks, fern, moss, woods again, and far away across the openings in the prospect to the distance lying wide before us with a purple bloom upon it, there seemed to be such undisturbed repose.", "모든 것에—저택, 정원, 테라스, 초록빛 비탈, 물, 늙은 떡갈나무, 고사리, 이끼, 다시 숲, 그리고 저 멀리 시야가 트인 사이로 자줏빛 노을이 어린 채 우리 앞에 광활하게 펼쳐진 먼 곳에 이르기까지—흐트러짐 없는 안식이 깃들어 있는 듯했습니다."]], [["When we came into the little village and passed a small inn with the sign of the Dedlock Arms swinging over the road in front, Mr.", "작은 마을로 들어서서, 데드록 암스라는 간판이 길 앞에서 흔들리는 작은 여관을 지날 때, 보이손 씨는 여관 문밖 의자에 앉아 있던 한 젊은 신사와 인사를 주고받았습니다."], ["Boythorn interchanged greetings with a young gentleman sitting on a bench outside the inn-door who had some fishing-tackle lying beside him.", "그 신사 곁에는 낚시 도구가 놓여 있었습니다."]], [["“That’s the housekeeper’s grandson, Mr.", "\"저 친구가 가정부의 손자인데, 라운스웰이라는 사람이라네,\""], ["Rouncewell by name,”", "그가 말했습니다."], ["said, he, “and he is in love with a pretty girl up at the house.", "\"그리고 저 친구는 저택에 사는 예쁜 처녀와 사랑에 빠졌지."], ["Lady Dedlock has taken a fancy to the pretty girl and is going to keep her about her own fair person—an honour which my young friend himself does not at all appreciate.", "데드록 마님이 그 예쁜 처녀를 마음에 들어 해서 자기 곁에 두려 하는데—내 젊은 친구는 그 영광을 도무지 달가워하지 않는다네."], ["However, he can’t marry just yet, even if his Rosebud were willing; so he is fain to make the best of it.", "하지만 설령 자기 장미 봉오리가 마음이 있다 해도, 저 친구는 아직 결혼할 처지가 못 되거든. 그러니 그저 형편에 맞춰 지낼 수밖에."], ["In the meanwhile, he comes here pretty often for a day or two at a time to—fish.", "그러는 사이 저 친구는 여기 꽤 자주 와서 하루 이틀씩 머물며—낚시를 하는 거지."], ["Ha ha ha ha!”", "하하하하!\""]], [["“Are he and the pretty girl engaged, Mr.", "\"저 사람과 그 예쁜 처녀는 약혼한 사인가요, 보이손 씨?\""], ["Boythorn?” asked Ada.", "에이다가 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, my dear Miss Clare,”", "\"이런, 클레어 양,\" 그가 대꾸했습니다."], ["he returned, “I think they may perhaps understand each other; but you will see them soon, I dare say, and I must learn from you on such a point—not you from me.”", "\"아마 둘이 서로 마음을 통하고 있는지도 모르지요. 하지만 곧 직접 보게 될 테니, 그런 일에 관해서는 내가 아가씨에게 배워야겠지요—아가씨가 나한테 배울 게 아니라.\""]], [["Ada blushed, and Mr. Boythorn, trotting forward on his comely grey horse, dismounted at his own door and stood ready with extended arm and uncovered head to welcome us when we arrived.", "에이다는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 보이손 씨는 잘생긴 회색 말을 몰아 앞으로 나아가 자기 집 문 앞에서 내린 뒤, 우리가 도착하자 팔을 벌리고 모자를 벗은 채 맞이할 채비를 하고 서 있었습니다."]], [["He lived in a pretty house, formerly the parsonage house, with a lawn in front, a bright flower-garden at the side, and a well-stocked orchard and kitchen-garden in the rear, enclosed with a venerable wall that had of itself a ripened ruddy look.", "그는 예전에 목사관이었던 아담한 집에 살았습니다. 앞에는 잔디밭이, 옆에는 화사한 꽃밭이, 뒤에는 잘 가꾸어진 과수원과 채소밭이 있었는데, 그 자체로 잘 익은 듯 불그스레한 빛이 도는 유서 깊은 담장으로 둘러싸여 있었습니다."], ["But, indeed, everything about the place wore an aspect of maturity and abundance.", "사실 그곳의 모든 것에는 무르익은 풍요로움이 깃들어 있었습니다."], ["The old lime-tree walk was like green cloisters, the very shadows of the cherry-trees and apple-trees were heavy with fruit, the gooseberry-bushes were so laden that their branches arched and rested on the earth, the strawberries and raspberries grew in like profusion, and the peaches basked by the hundred on the wall.", "오래된 보리수 산책로는 초록빛 회랑 같았고, 벚나무와 사과나무의 그림자마저 열매로 묵직했으며, 까치밥나무 덤불은 가지가 휘어져 땅에 닿을 만큼 열매를 가득 매달았고, 딸기와 산딸기도 그에 못지않게 무성하게 자랐으며, 복숭아는 담장가에서 수백 개씩 햇볕을 쬐고 있었습니다."], ["Tumbled about among the spread nets and the glass frames sparkling and winking in the sun there were such heaps of drooping pods, and marrows, and cucumbers, that every foot of ground appeared a vegetable treasury, while the smell of sweet herbs and all kinds of wholesome growth (to say nothing of the neighbouring meadows where the hay was carrying) made the whole air a great nosegay.", "펼쳐진 그물과 햇살에 반짝거리며 깜박이는 유리 온상틀 사이로, 축 처진 꼬투리며 호박이며 오이가 어찌나 수북이 뒹굴던지, 땅 한 자 한 자가 채소 보물창고 같았고, 향긋한 풀과 온갖 싱싱한 작물의 내음은(건초를 거두어들이는 이웃 풀밭은 말할 것도 없고) 온 공기를 거대한 꽃다발로 만들어주었습니다."], ["Such stillness and composure reigned within the orderly precincts of the old red wall that even the feathers hung in garlands to scare the birds hardly stirred; and the wall had such a ripening influence that where, here and there high up, a disused nail and scrap of list still clung to it, it was easy to fancy that they had mellowed with the changing seasons and that they had rusted and decayed according to the common fate.", "오래된 붉은 담장의 정연한 경내에는 어찌나 고요한 평온이 감돌던지, 새를 쫓으려고 꽃줄처럼 매달아둔 깃털조차 거의 흔들리지 않았습니다. 게다가 담장에는 무르익게 하는 기운이 어찌나 강한지, 군데군데 높이 박힌 쓸모를 잃은 못과 끈 자투리가 아직 매달려 있는 것을 보면, 그것들마저 바뀌는 계절을 따라 무르익었고, 만물의 공통된 운명을 따라 녹슬고 삭아갔다고 상상하기 쉬웠습니다."]], [["The house, though a little disorderly in comparison with the garden, was a real old house with settles in the chimney of the brick-floored kitchen and great beams across the ceilings. On one side of it was the terrible piece of ground in dispute, where Mr.", "집은 정원에 비하면 다소 어수선했지만, 벽돌 깐 부엌의 굴뚝가에 등받이 긴 의자가 놓이고 천장을 가로지르는 큼직한 들보가 있는, 진짜 오래된 집이었습니다."], ["Boythorn maintained a sentry in a smock-frock day and night, whose duty was supposed to be, in cases of aggression, immediately to ring a large bell hung up there for the purpose, to unchain a great bull-dog established in a kennel as his ally, and generally to deal destruction on the enemy. Not content with these precautions, Mr.", "그 한쪽에는 그 무시무시한 분쟁의 땅이 있었는데, 보이손 씨는 밤낮으로 작업복 차림의 보초를 세워두었고, 그 보초의 임무는 침범이 일어나면 즉시 그러려고 매달아둔 커다란 종을 울리고, 동맹군으로 개집에 둔 큰 불도그의 사슬을 풀고, 대체로 적에게 파멸을 안기는 것이라 했습니다."], ["Boythorn had himself composed and posted there, on painted boards to which his name was attached in large letters, the following solemn warnings: “Beware of the bull-dog.", "이런 대비로도 만족하지 못한 보이손 씨는, 자기 이름을 큼직한 글씨로 박은 칠한 나무판에 다음과 같은 엄숙한 경고문을 손수 지어 그곳에 내걸었습니다. \"불도그를 조심하라."], ["He is most ferocious.", "매우 사나움."], ["Lawrence Boythorn.”", "로런스 보이손.\""], ["“The blunderbus is loaded with slugs.", "\"나팔총에 산탄을 장전해두었음."], ["Lawrence Boythorn.”", "로런스 보이손.\""], ["“Man-traps and spring-guns are set here at all times of the day and night.", "\"이곳에는 사람 잡는 덫과 용수철 총이 밤낮을 가리지 않고 설치되어 있음."], ["Lawrence Boythorn.”", "로런스 보이손.\""], ["“Take notice.", "\"유의하라."], ["That any person or persons audaciously presuming to trespass on this property will be punished with the utmost severity of private chastisement and prosecuted with the utmost rigour of the law.", "누구든 뻔뻔스럽게 이 영지에 무단 침입하는 자는 더없이 가혹한 사적 응징을 받고, 더없이 엄정한 법의 심판을 받을 것임."], ["Lawrence Boythorn.”", "로런스 보이손.\""], ["These he showed us from the drawing-room window, while his bird was hopping about his head, and he laughed, “Ha ha ha ha!", "그는 객실 창문에서 이것들을 우리에게 보여주었는데, 그 사이 그의 새가 그의 머리 위에서 통통 뛰어다녔고, 그는 그것들을 가리키며 \"하하하하!"], ["Ha ha ha ha!”", "하하하하!\""], ["to that extent as he pointed them out that I really thought he would have hurt himself.", "어찌나 웃어대던지, 나는 정말이지 그가 제 몸을 상하게 할까 봐 걱정했습니다."]], [["“But this is taking a good deal of trouble,” said Mr.", "\"하지만 이건 꽤 수고스러운 일이로군요,\""], ["Skimpole in his light way, “when you are not in earnest after all.”", "스킴폴 씨가 그 특유의 가벼운 투로 말했습니다. \"어차피 진지하게 그러는 것도 아니면서요.\""]], [["“Not in earnest!”", "\"진지하지 않다니!\""], ["returned Mr. Boythorn with unspeakable warmth.", "보이손 씨가 말로 다 못 할 열기로 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Not in earnest!", "\"진지하지 않다니!"], ["If I could have hoped to train him, I would have bought a lion instead of that dog and would have turned him loose upon the first intolerable robber who should dare to make an encroachment on my rights.", "저 개를 길들일 수 있다고 기대했다면, 나는 저 개 대신 사자를 사들여서, 감히 내 권리를 침범하는 첫 번째 참을 수 없는 도둑놈에게 풀어놓았을 거요."], ["Let Sir Leicester Dedlock consent to come out and decide this question by single combat, and I will meet him with any weapon known to mankind in any age or country.", "레스터 데드록 경이 나와서 이 문제를 일대일 결투로 판가름하겠다고만 한다면, 나는 어느 시대 어느 나라의 무기든 인류에게 알려진 무기로 그를 상대하겠소. 나는 그 정도로 진지하오."], ["I am that much in earnest. Not more!”", "그 이상은 아니지만!\""]], [["We arrived at his house on a Saturday.", "우리는 토요일에 그의 집에 도착했습니다."], ["On the Sunday morning we all set forth to walk to the little church in the park.", "일요일 아침 우리는 모두 영지 안의 작은 교회로 걸어가려고 길을 나섰습니다."], ["Entering the park, almost immediately by the disputed ground, we pursued a pleasant footpath winding among the verdant turf and the beautiful trees until it brought us to the church-porch.", "거의 곧장 그 분쟁의 땅을 지나 영지로 들어선 우리는, 푸릇한 잔디와 아름다운 나무들 사이로 굽이도는 기분 좋은 오솔길을 따라가 마침내 교회 현관에 다다랐습니다."]], [["The congregation was extremely small and quite a rustic one with the exception of a large muster of servants from the house, some of whom were already in their seats, while others were yet dropping in.", "신도들은 무척 적었고, 저택에서 온 하인들이 한 무리 크게 모여 있는 것을 빼면 영락없는 시골 사람들이었습니다. 하인들 가운데 일부는 이미 자리에 앉아 있었고, 더러는 아직 하나둘 들어오고 있었습니다."], ["There were some stately footmen, and there was a perfect picture of an old coachman, who looked as if he were the official representative of all the pomps and vanities that had ever been put into his coach.", "위풍당당한 종복들이 몇 있었고, 그림 같은 늙은 마부가 하나 있었는데, 마치 자기 마차에 실린 적이 있는 온갖 허세와 허영의 공식 대표라도 되는 듯한 모습이었습니다."], ["There was a very pretty show of young women, and above them, the handsome old face and fine responsible portly figure of the housekeeper towered pre-eminent.", "젊은 여자들도 무척 어여쁘게 늘어서 있었고, 그들 위로 가정부의 잘생긴 늙은 얼굴과 듬직하고 위엄 있는 풍채가 단연 도드라졌습니다."], ["The pretty girl of whom Mr. Boythorn had told us was close by her.", "보이손 씨가 우리에게 말했던 그 예쁜 처녀가 가정부 바로 곁에 있었습니다."], ["She was so very pretty that I might have known her by her beauty even if I had not seen how blushingly conscious she was of the eyes of the young fisherman, whom I discovered not far off.", "그녀는 어찌나 예쁘던지, 멀지 않은 곳에 있던 젊은 낚시꾼의 시선을 그녀가 얼마나 부끄럽게 의식하는지 보지 못했더라도 그 아름다움만으로 알아볼 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["One face, and not an agreeable one, though it was handsome, seemed maliciously watchful of this pretty girl, and indeed of every one and everything there.", "한 얼굴이, 잘생기긴 했지만 그리 호감 가지 않는 한 얼굴이, 이 예쁜 처녀를, 아니 사실은 그곳의 모든 사람과 모든 것을 심술궂게 감시하는 듯했습니다."], ["It was a Frenchwoman’s.", "어느 프랑스 여자의 얼굴이었습니다."]], [["As the bell was yet ringing and the great people were not yet come, I had leisure to glance over the church, which smelt as earthy as a grave, and to think what a shady, ancient, solemn little church it was.", "종이 아직 울리고 있고 그 귀하신 분들이 아직 오지 않았기에, 나는 한가로이 교회를 둘러보았습니다. 무덤처럼 흙냄새가 풍기는 교회였는데, 얼마나 그늘지고 오래되고 엄숙한 작은 교회인지 새삼 느꼈습니다."], ["The windows, heavily shaded by trees, admitted a subdued light that made the faces around me pale, and darkened the old brasses in the pavement and the time and damp-worn monuments, and rendered the sunshine in the little porch, where a monotonous ringer was working at the bell, inestimably bright.", "나무에 짙게 가려진 창문은 은은한 빛만 들이는데, 그 빛이 내 둘레의 얼굴들을 창백하게 만들고, 포석에 박힌 오래된 놋쇠 명패와 세월과 습기에 닳은 기념비를 어둑하게 가렸으며, 단조롭게 종을 치는 사람이 일하고 있는 작은 현관의 햇살을 더없이 눈부시게 만들었습니다."], ["But a stir in that direction, a gathering of reverential awe in the rustic faces, and a blandly ferocious assumption on the part of Mr. Boythorn of being resolutely unconscious of somebody’s existence forewarned me that the great people were come and that the service was going to begin.", "그러나 그쪽에서 술렁임이 일고, 시골 사람들의 얼굴에 경건한 외경이 차오르고, 보이손 씨가 누군가의 존재를 단호히 모르는 척하겠다는 듯 점잖으면서도 사나운 태도를 짓는 것을 보고, 나는 그 귀하신 분들이 도착했으며 예배가 곧 시작되리라는 것을 미리 알아챘습니다."]], [["“‘Enter not into judgment with thy servant, O Lord, for in thy sight—’”", "\"'주여, 당신의 종을 심판하지 마옵소서, 당신 앞에서는—'\""]], [["Shall I ever forget the rapid beating at my heart, occasioned by the look I met as I stood up!", "자리에서 일어섰을 때 마주친 그 눈길이 내 가슴을 얼마나 빠르게 뛰게 했는지, 내가 과연 잊을 수 있을까요!"], ["Shall I ever forget the manner in which those handsome proud eyes seemed to spring out of their languor and to hold mine!", "그 잘생기고 오만한 두 눈이 나른함을 떨치고 솟구쳐 나와 내 눈을 붙드는 듯하던 그 모양을, 내가 과연 잊을 수 있을까요!"], ["It was only a moment before I cast mine down—released again, if I may say so—on my book; but I knew the beautiful face quite well in that short space of time.", "내가 다시 눈을 떨구어—말하자면 다시 풀려나서—책으로 시선을 돌리기까지는 한순간뿐이었지만, 그 짧은 시간에 나는 그 아름다운 얼굴을 아주 또렷이 알아보았습니다."]], [["And, very strangely, there was something quickened within me, associated with the lonely days at my godmother’s; yes, away even to the days when I had stood on tiptoe to dress myself at my little glass after dressing my doll.", "그리고 무척 이상하게도, 내 안에서 무언가가 깨어났는데, 그것은 대모님 집에서 보낸 외로운 나날과 이어져 있었습니다. 그렇습니다, 인형에게 옷을 입힌 다음 작은 거울 앞에서 발끝으로 서서 내 옷을 입던 그 시절까지 거슬러 올라가는 것이었습니다."], ["And this, although I had never seen this lady’s face before in all my life—I was quite sure of it—absolutely certain.", "그런데도 나는 이 마님의 얼굴을 평생 단 한 번도 본 적이 없었습니다—나는 그것을 확신했고—완전히 확신했습니다."]], [["It was easy to know that the ceremonious, gouty, grey-haired gentleman, the only other occupant of the great pew, was Sir Leicester Dedlock, and that the lady was Lady Dedlock.", "격식 차린 그 통풍 걸린 백발의 신사가, 그 커다란 신도석의 또 다른 단 한 사람이, 레스터 데드록 경이고, 그 마님이 데드록 마님이라는 것은 쉽게 알 수 있었습니다."], ["But why her face should be, in a confused way, like a broken glass to me, in which I saw scraps of old remembrances, and why I should be so fluttered and troubled (for I was still) by having casually met her eyes, I could not think.", "그런데 어째서 그 얼굴이 내게 깨진 거울처럼, 옛 기억의 조각들이 비치는 그런 것으로 어렴풋이 다가오는지, 또 어째서 우연히 그 눈과 마주친 일만으로 내가 (여전히 그러했듯이) 그토록 가슴이 두근거리고 마음이 어지러운지, 나는 도무지 알 수가 없었습니다."]], [["I felt it to be an unmeaning weakness in me and tried to overcome it by attending to the words I heard.", "나는 그것을 까닭 모를 내 약점으로 여기고, 내가 듣고 있는 말씀에 마음을 기울여 그 약점을 이겨내려 했습니다."], ["Then, very strangely, I seemed to hear them, not in the reader’s voice, but in the well-remembered voice of my godmother.", "그러자, 무척 이상하게도, 그 말씀이 낭독하는 이의 목소리가 아니라 또렷이 기억나는 대모님의 목소리로 들리는 듯했습니다."], ["This made me think, did Lady Dedlock’s face accidentally resemble my godmother’s?", "그래서 나는 생각했습니다, 데드록 마님의 얼굴이 우연히 대모님의 얼굴과 닮았던 걸까?"], ["It might be that it did, a little; but the expression was so different, and the stern decision which had worn into my godmother’s face, like weather into rocks, was so completely wanting in the face before me that it could not be that resemblance which had struck me.", "어쩌면 조금은 닮았는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 그 표정이 너무나 달랐고, 비바람이 바위에 새겨지듯 대모님의 얼굴에 새겨졌던 그 준엄한 단호함이 내 앞의 얼굴에는 깡그리 없었으니, 나를 사로잡은 것이 그런 닮음일 리는 없었습니다."], ["Neither did I know the loftiness and haughtiness of Lady Dedlock’s face, at all, in any one.", "게다가 데드록 마님 얼굴의 그 도도함과 거만함은, 내가 아는 그 누구에게서도 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["And yet I—I, little Esther Summerson, the child who lived a life apart and on whose birthday there was no rejoicing—seemed to arise before my own eyes, evoked out of the past by some power in this fashionable lady, whom I not only entertained no fancy that I had ever seen, but whom I perfectly well knew I had never seen until that hour.", "그런데도 나는—나, 외따로 떨어진 삶을 살았고 생일이 와도 누구 하나 기뻐해주지 않던 아이, 어린 에스더 서머슨이—이 멋쟁이 마님에게 깃든 어떤 힘에 이끌려 과거에서 불려 나와, 내 눈앞에 떠오르는 듯했습니다. 그 마님을 예전에 본 적이 있다는 환상은 조금도 품지 않았을뿐더러, 그 시각까지 결단코 본 적이 없다는 것을 나는 완벽하게 알고 있었는데도 말입니다."]], [["It made me tremble so to be thrown into this unaccountable agitation that I was conscious of being distressed even by the observation of the French maid, though I knew she had been looking watchfully here, and there, and everywhere, from the moment of her coming into the church.", "이 까닭 모를 동요에 빠져 어찌나 떨렸던지, 나는 그 프랑스 하녀의 시선에마저 괴로움을 느꼈습니다. 그녀가 교회에 들어선 순간부터 여기저기, 사방을 경계하듯 살피고 있었다는 것을 알면서도 말입니다."], ["By degrees, though very slowly, I at last overcame my strange emotion.", "무척 더디기는 했지만, 차츰차츰 나는 마침내 그 이상한 감정을 이겨냈습니다."], ["After a long time, I looked towards Lady Dedlock again.", "한참이 지나서야 나는 다시 데드록 마님 쪽을 바라보았습니다."], ["It was while they were preparing to sing, before the sermon.", "설교 전, 찬송을 부르려고 준비하던 무렵이었습니다."], ["She took no heed of me, and the beating at my heart was gone.", "마님은 내게 아무 관심도 두지 않았고, 내 가슴의 두근거림도 사라졌습니다."], ["Neither did it revive for more than a few moments when she once or twice afterwards glanced at Ada or at me through her glass.", "그 뒤로 마님이 한두 번 오페라글라스로 에이다나 나를 흘끗 보았을 때도, 그 두근거림은 잠깐을 넘어 되살아나지 않았습니다."]], [["The service being concluded, Sir Leicester gave his arm with much taste and gallantry to Lady Dedlock—though he was obliged to walk by the help of a thick stick—and escorted her out of church to the pony carriage in which they had come.", "예배가 끝나자, 레스터 경은 짙은 지팡이에 의지해 걸어야 했는데도, 무척 멋스럽고 정중하게 데드록 마님에게 팔을 내주어 그녀를 교회 밖, 그들이 타고 온 조랑말 마차까지 모셨습니다."], ["The servants then dispersed, and so did the congregation, whom Sir Leicester had contemplated all along (Mr.", "그러자 하인들이 흩어졌고, 신도들도 흩어졌습니다."], ["Skimpole said to Mr. Boythorn’s infinite delight) as if he were a considerable landed proprietor in heaven.", "레스터 경은 (스킴폴 씨가 보이손 씨가 더없이 좋아하라고 한 말처럼) 마치 자신이 천국의 상당한 토지를 소유한 지주라도 되는 양 줄곧 그 신도들을 굽어보고 있었습니다."]], [["“He believes he is!” said Mr.", "\"저 사람은 정말 그런 줄 안다니까!\""], ["Boythorn. “He firmly believes it.", "보이손 씨가 말했습니다."], ["So did his father, and his grandfather, and his great-grandfather!”", "\"철석같이 믿어. 그 아비도 그랬고, 할아비도, 증조할아비도 그랬지!\""]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"그거 아십니까,\""], ["pursued Mr. Skimpole very unexpectedly to Mr.", "스킴폴 씨가 보이손 씨에게 무척 뜻밖의 말을 꺼냈습니다."], ["Boythorn, “it’s agreeable to me to see a man of that sort.”", "\"나는 저런 부류의 사람을 보는 게 흐뭇하답니다.\""]], [["“IS it!”", "\"흐뭇하다고요!\""], ["said Mr. Boythorn.", "보이손 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Say that he wants to patronize me,”", "\"저자가 나를 너그러이 후원하려 든다고 칩시다,\""], ["pursued Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말을 이었습니다."], ["“Very well!", "\"아주 좋아요!"], ["I don’t object.”", "나는 마다하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“I do,” said Mr.", "\"나는 마다하겠소,\""], ["Boythorn with great vigour.", "보이손 씨가 무척 기세 좋게 말했습니다."]], [["“Do you really?”", "\"정말로요?\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole in his easy light vein.", "스킴폴 씨가 그 느긋하고 가벼운 투로 대꾸했습니다."], ["“But that’s taking trouble, surely.", "\"하지만 그건 분명 수고스러운 일입니다."], ["And why should you take trouble?", "한데 왜 굳이 수고를 사서 합니까?"], ["Here am I, content to receive things childishly as they fall out, and I never take trouble!", "보세요, 나는 일이 닥치는 대로 어린아이처럼 받아들이는 데 만족하고, 결코 수고를 사서 하지 않습니다!"], ["I come down here, for instance, and I find a mighty potentate exacting homage.", "이를테면 나는 여기 내려와서, 한 위대한 권세가가 경의를 요구하는 것을 봅니다."], ["Very well!", "아주 좋아요!"], ["I say ‘Mighty potentate, here IS my homage!", "나는 말하지요. '위대한 권세가여, 여기 내 경의를 바치오!"], ["It’s easier to give it than to withhold it.", "바치는 게 거두는 것보다 쉬우니까요. 자, 여기 있소."], ["Here it is.", "내게 보여줄 흐뭇한 것이 있다면, 나는 기꺼이 보겠소."], ["If you have anything of an agreeable nature to show me, I shall be happy to see it; if you have anything of an agreeable nature to give me, I shall be happy to accept it.’ Mighty potentate replies in effect, ‘This is a sensible fellow.", "내게 줄 흐뭇한 것이 있다면, 나는 기꺼이 받겠소.' 그러면 위대한 권세가는 사실상 이렇게 답하지요."], ["I find him accord with my digestion and my bilious system.", "'이자는 분별 있는 친구로군. 내 소화와 담즙 체질에 잘 맞는단 말이야."], ["He doesn’t impose upon me the necessity of rolling myself up like a hedgehog with my points outward.", "이자는 나로 하여금 고슴도치처럼 가시를 밖으로 곤두세워 몸을 둘둘 말 필요를 안기지 않는군. 나는 활짝 펴고, 마음을 열고, 밀턴의 구름처럼 은빛 안감을 밖으로 뒤집어 보이니, 둘 다에게 더 흐뭇하지 않은가.'"], ["I expand, I open, I turn my silver lining outward like Milton’s cloud, and it’s more agreeable to both of us.’ That’s my view of such things, speaking as a child!”", "그게 그런 일들에 대한 내 견해입니다. 어린아이로서 하는 말이지요!\""]], [["“But suppose you went down somewhere else to-morrow,” said Mr.", "\"하지만 자네가 내일 어디 다른 데로 내려갔는데,\""], ["Boythorn, “where there was the opposite of that fellow—or of this fellow.", "보이손 씨가 말했습니다. \"거기엔 저자와—혹은 이 사람과—정반대인 자가 있다고 칩시다."], ["How then?”", "그럼 어쩌겠나?\""]], [["“How then?” said Mr.", "\"그럼 어쩌겠느냐고요?\""], ["Skimpole with an appearance of the utmost simplicity and candour.", "스킴폴 씨가 더없이 천진하고 솔직한 얼굴로 말했습니다."], ["“Just the same then!", "\"그때도 똑같지요!"], ["I should say, ‘My esteemed Boythorn’—to make you the personification of our imaginary friend—‘my esteemed Boythorn, you object to the mighty potentate?", "나는 이렇게 말할 겁니다. '나의 존경하는 보이손'—자네를 우리의 가상 친구로 삼아서—'나의 존경하는 보이손, 자네는 그 위대한 권세가가 못마땅한가?"], ["Very good.", "아주 좋아."], ["So do I.", "나도 그렇다네."], ["I take it that my business in the social system is to be agreeable; I take it that everybody’s business in the social system is to be agreeable.", "사회 체계 안에서 내 할 일은 흐뭇한 사람이 되는 것이라고 나는 생각하네. 사회 체계 안에서 누구나 할 일은 흐뭇한 사람이 되는 것이라고 나는 생각하네."], ["It’s a system of harmony, in short.", "요컨대 조화의 체계지."], ["Therefore if you object, I object.", "그러니 자네가 못마땅하면, 나도 못마땅하다네."], ["Now, excellent Boythorn, let us go to dinner!’”", "자, 훌륭한 보이손, 우리 저녁 먹으러 가세!'\""]], [["“But excellent Boythorn might say,”", "\"하지만 훌륭한 보이손은 이렇게 말할지도 모르지,\""], ["returned our host, swelling and growing very red, “I’ll be—”", "우리 주인은 부풀어 오르며 얼굴이 새빨개져서 대꾸했습니다. \"내가 절대—\""]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠습니다,\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Very likely he would.”", "\"그럴 법하군요.\""]], [["“—if I WILL go to dinner!” cried Mr.", "\"—저녁을 먹으러 갈 줄 아느냐고!\""], ["Boythorn in a violent burst and stopping to strike his stick upon the ground.", "보이손 씨가 격하게 터뜨리며 외쳤고, 걸음을 멈추고 지팡이로 땅을 내리쳤습니다. \"그러고는 아마 이렇게 덧붙이겠지."], ["“And he would probably add, ‘Is there such a thing as principle, Mr. Harold Skimpole?’”", "'원칙이라는 게 있긴 한가, 해럴드 스킴폴 씨?'\""]], [["“To which Harold Skimpole would reply, you know,”", "\"거기에 해럴드 스킴폴은 이렇게 대답하겠지요,\""], ["he returned in his gayest manner and with his most ingenuous smile, “‘Upon my life I have not the least idea!", "그는 더없이 명랑한 태도로, 더없이 천연덕스러운 미소를 지으며 대꾸했습니다. \"'맹세코 나는 도무지 모르겠소이다!"], ["I don’t know what it is you call by that name, or where it is, or who possesses it.", "당신이 그 이름으로 부르는 것이 무엇인지, 어디 있는지, 누가 가졌는지 나는 모릅니다."], ["If you possess it and find it comfortable, I am quite delighted and congratulate you heartily.", "당신이 그것을 가졌고 편안하다면, 나는 더없이 기쁘고 진심으로 축하드립니다. 하지만 장담컨대 나는 그것에 관해 아무것도 모릅니다."], ["But I know nothing about it, I assure you; for I am a mere child, and I lay no claim to it, and I don’t want it!’ So, you see, excellent Boythorn and I would go to dinner after all!”", "나는 한낱 어린아이이고, 그것을 주장하지도 않으며, 원하지도 않으니까요!' 그러니 보시다시피, 결국 훌륭한 보이손과 나는 저녁을 먹으러 갈 겁니다!\""]], [["This was one of many little dialogues between them which I always expected to end, and which I dare say would have ended under other circumstances, in some violent explosion on the part of our host. But he had so high a sense of his hospitable and responsible position as our entertainer, and my guardian laughed so sincerely at and with Mr.", "이것은 두 사람 사이의 자잘한 대화 가운데 하나였는데, 나는 그 대화가 늘 우리 주인 쪽에서 격렬하게 폭발하는 것으로 끝나리라 예상했고, 다른 상황이었다면 정말 그렇게 끝났으리라 봅니다."], ["Skimpole, as a child who blew bubbles and broke them all day long, that matters never went beyond this point. Mr.", "하지만 그는 우리를 대접하는 주인으로서 자신의 후하고 책임 있는 자리를 무척 소중히 여겼고, 후견인은 온종일 거품을 불었다 터뜨리는 어린아이 같은 스킴폴 씨를 두고, 또 그와 함께 어찌나 진심으로 웃었던지, 사태는 결코 이 지점을 넘어서지 않았습니다."], ["Skimpole, who always seemed quite unconscious of having been on delicate ground, then betook himself to beginning some sketch in the park which he never finished, or to playing fragments of airs on the piano, or to singing scraps of songs, or to lying down on his back under a tree and looking at the sky—which he couldn’t help thinking, he said, was what he was meant for; it suited him so exactly.", "자신이 위태로운 땅을 밟고 있었다는 것을 전혀 의식하지 못하는 듯한 스킴폴 씨는, 그러고 나면 영지에서 끝내 완성하지 못할 어떤 스케치를 시작하거나, 피아노로 곡조의 단편들을 치거나, 노래 조각을 흥얼거리거나, 나무 아래 등을 대고 누워 하늘을 바라보았습니다. 그는 자기가 바로 이러려고 태어난 모양이라고, 이게 자기한테 너무도 꼭 맞는다며 그런 생각을 떨칠 수 없다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Enterprise and effort,”", "\"모험과 노력이란,\""], ["he would say to us (on his back), “are delightful to me.", "그는 (드러누운 채) 우리에게 말하곤 했습니다. \"내게는 흐뭇한 것들이지요."], ["I believe I am truly cosmopolitan.", "나는 내가 진정한 세계주의자라고 믿습니다."], ["I have the deepest sympathy with them.", "나는 그런 것들에 더없이 깊은 공감을 품고 있어요."], ["I lie in a shady place like this and think of adventurous spirits going to the North Pole or penetrating to the heart of the Torrid Zone with admiration.", "나는 이런 그늘진 곳에 누워서, 북극으로 가거나 열대의 한복판을 파고드는 모험심 넘치는 영혼들을 흠모하며 떠올립니다. 돈벌레 같은 작자들은 묻지요."], ["Mercenary creatures ask, ‘What is the use of a man’s going to the North Pole?", "'사람이 북극에 가는 게 무슨 소용이람? 그게 무슨 득이 돼?'"], ["What good does it do?’ I can’t say; but, for anything I CAN say, he may go for the purpose—though he don’t know it—of employing my thoughts as I lie here.", "나는 모릅니다. 하지만 내가 말할 수 있는 한, 그 사람은 어쩌면—본인은 모르겠지만—내가 여기 누워 떠올릴 거리를 마련해주려고 가는 것인지도 모르지요."], ["Take an extreme case.", "극단적인 경우를 들어봅시다."], ["Take the case of the slaves on American plantations.", "아메리카 농장의 노예들을 예로 들지요."], ["I dare say they are worked hard, I dare say they don’t altogether like it.", "분명 그들은 고되게 부려질 테고, 분명 그걸 썩 좋아하진 않을 겁니다."], ["I dare say theirs is an unpleasant experience on the whole; but they people the landscape for me, they give it a poetry for me, and perhaps that is one of the pleasanter objects of their existence.", "분명 그들의 처지는 전체로 보면 유쾌하지 못한 경험이겠지요. 하지만 그들은 내게 풍경을 채워주고, 내게 시정을 안겨주니, 어쩌면 그것이 그들 존재의 더 즐거운 목적 가운데 하나인지도 모릅니다."], ["I am very sensible of it, if it be, and I shouldn’t wonder if it were!”", "만약 그렇다면 나는 그것을 무척 잘 느끼는 사람이고, 정말 그렇다 해도 놀랍지 않을 겁니다!\""]], [["I always wondered on these occasions whether he ever thought of Mrs.", "이런 자리에서 나는 늘 궁금했습니다."], ["Skimpole and the children, and in what point of view they presented themselves to his cosmopolitan mind.", "그가 스킴폴 부인과 아이들을 한 번이라도 떠올린 적이 있을까, 그들이 그의 세계주의적인 머릿속에 어떤 모습으로 나타날까."], ["So far as I could understand, they rarely presented themselves at all.", "내가 이해한 바로는, 그들은 거의 떠오르지 않는 듯했습니다."]], [["The week had gone round to the Saturday following that beating of my heart in the church; and every day had been so bright and blue that to ramble in the woods, and to see the light striking down among the transparent leaves and sparkling in the beautiful interlacings of the shadows of the trees, while the birds poured out their songs and the air was drowsy with the hum of insects, had been most delightful.", "교회에서 내 가슴이 그렇게 두근거린 날로부터, 한 주가 돌아 그다음 토요일에 이르렀습니다. 날마다 어찌나 화창하고 푸르렀던지, 숲속을 거닐며 투명한 잎새들 사이로 빛이 내리꽂혀 나무 그림자가 아름답게 얽힌 틈에서 반짝이는 것을 보고, 새들이 노래를 쏟아내고 공기가 벌레들의 윙윙거림으로 나른해지는 것을 느끼는 일은, 더없이 흐뭇했습니다."], ["We had one favourite spot, deep in moss and last year’s leaves, where there were some felled trees from which the bark was all stripped off.", "우리에게는 즐겨 찾는 한 곳이 있었습니다. 이끼와 지난해 낙엽이 깊이 깔린 곳으로, 껍질이 죄다 벗겨진 채 베어진 나무 몇 그루가 있었습니다."], ["Seated among these, we looked through a green vista supported by thousands of natural columns, the whitened stems of trees, upon a distant prospect made so radiant by its contrast with the shade in which we sat and made so precious by the arched perspective through which we saw it that it was like a glimpse of the better land.", "그 사이에 앉아서, 우리는 수천 개의 자연 기둥, 곧 허옇게 바랜 나무줄기들이 떠받친 초록빛 회랑 너머로, 멀리 펼쳐진 풍경을 바라보았습니다. 그 풍경은 우리가 앉은 그늘과 대비되어 더없이 눈부셨고, 우리가 그것을 바라보는 아치형 원근 너머의 정경이 너무도 귀해서, 마치 더 나은 세상을 언뜻 엿보는 것 같았습니다."], ["Upon the Saturday we sat here, Mr. Jarndyce, Ada, and I, until we heard thunder muttering in the distance and felt the large raindrops rattle through the leaves.", "그 토요일에 우리는—잔다이스 씨와 에이다와 나는—멀리서 천둥이 우르릉대는 소리가 들리고 큼직한 빗방울이 잎새를 후드득 때리는 것을 느낄 때까지 그곳에 앉아 있었습니다."]], [["The weather had been all the week extremely sultry, but the storm broke so suddenly—upon us, at least, in that sheltered spot—that before we reached the outskirts of the wood the thunder and lightning were frequent and the rain came plunging through the leaves as if every drop were a great leaden bead.", "그 한 주 내내 날씨가 무척 후텁지근했는데, 폭풍은 어찌나 갑자기 들이닥쳤던지—적어도 그 아늑한 자리에 있던 우리에게는—숲 가장자리에 채 이르기도 전에 천둥과 번개가 잦아지고, 비가 마치 한 방울 한 방울이 커다란 납구슬이라도 되는 듯 잎새를 뚫고 내리꽂혔습니다."], ["As it was not a time for standing among trees, we ran out of the wood, and up and down the moss-grown steps which crossed the plantation-fence like two broad-staved ladders placed back to back, and made for a keeper’s lodge which was close at hand.", "나무 사이에 서 있을 때가 아니었기에, 우리는 숲에서 뛰쳐나와, 마치 등을 맞댄 살이 넓은 사다리 두 개처럼 농장 울타리를 가로지른 이끼 낀 계단을 오르내려, 바로 가까이에 있던 사냥터지기의 오두막으로 향했습니다."], ["We had often noticed the dark beauty of this lodge standing in a deep twilight of trees, and how the ivy clustered over it, and how there was a steep hollow near, where we had once seen the keeper’s dog dive down into the fern as if it were water.", "우리는 나무들의 짙은 어스름 속에 서 있는 이 오두막의 어두운 아름다움을, 담쟁이가 그 위로 휘감긴 모습을, 그리고 근처의 가파른 우묵한 골짜기—언젠가 사냥터지기의 개가 마치 물속으로 뛰어들 듯 고사리 속으로 곤두박질치는 것을 본 적 있는 그곳을—종종 눈여겨보았습니다."]], [["The lodge was so dark within, now the sky was overcast, that we only clearly saw the man who came to the door when we took shelter there and put two chairs for Ada and me.", "하늘이 잔뜩 흐려진 지금, 오두막 안은 어찌나 어둡던지, 우리가 그곳에 피신하자 문간으로 나와 에이다와 나에게 의자 두 개를 놓아준 남자만이 또렷이 보였습니다. 격자창은 죄다 활짝 열려 있었고, 우리는 문간 바로 안쪽에 앉아 폭풍을 지켜보았습니다."], ["The lattice-windows were all thrown open, and we sat just within the doorway watching the storm.", "바람이 깨어나 나무들을 휘고 빗줄기를 연기 구름처럼 몰아가는 모습을 보고, 엄숙한 천둥소리를 듣고 번개를 보는 일은 장엄했습니다."], ["It was grand to see how the wind awoke, and bent the trees, and drove the rain before it like a cloud of smoke; and to hear the solemn thunder and to see the lightning; and while thinking with awe of the tremendous powers by which our little lives are encompassed, to consider how beneficent they are and how upon the smallest flower and leaf there was already a freshness poured from all this seeming rage which seemed to make creation new again.", "그리고 우리의 작은 목숨을 둘러싼 그 어마어마한 힘들을 외경에 차 떠올리면서도, 그 힘들이 얼마나 자비로운지를, 또 이 겉보기 광포함에서 쏟아져 나온 싱그러움이 어떻게 가장 작은 꽃과 잎새에마저 벌써 어려, 만물을 다시 새롭게 만드는 듯한지를 헤아리는 일도 장엄했습니다. \"이렇게 트인 곳에 앉아 있으면 위험하지 않나요?\""]], [["“Is it not dangerous to sit in so exposed a place?”", "\"어머, 아니야, 에스더, 우리 에스더!\" 에이다가 조용히 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, no, Esther dear!” said Ada quietly. Ada said it to me, but I had not spoken.", "에이다는 내게 그 말을 건넸지만, 정작 말을 한 건 내가 아니었습니다."]], [["The beating of my heart came back again.", "내 가슴의 두근거림이 다시 찾아왔습니다."], ["I had never heard the voice, as I had never seen the face, but it affected me in the same strange way.", "그 얼굴을 본 적이 없듯 나는 그 목소리도 들은 적이 없었지만, 그것은 똑같이 이상한 방식으로 나를 흔들었습니다."], ["Again, in a moment, there arose before my mind innumerable pictures of myself.", "또다시, 한순간에, 내 마음속에 나 자신의 무수한 모습이 떠올랐습니다."]], [["Lady Dedlock had taken shelter in the lodge before our arrival there and had come out of the gloom within.", "데드록 마님은 우리가 도착하기 전에 그 오두막에 피신해 있다가, 안쪽의 어둠 속에서 걸어 나온 것이었습니다."], ["She stood behind my chair with her hand upon it.", "그녀는 내 의자 뒤에 서서 의자에 손을 얹고 있었습니다."], ["I saw her with her hand close to my shoulder when I turned my head.", "고개를 돌리자, 그녀의 손이 내 어깨 가까이에 있는 것이 보였습니다."]], [["“I have frightened you?”", "\"내가 놀라게 했나요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했습니다."]], [["No. It was not fright.", "아니었습니다."], ["Why should I be frightened!", "놀란 것이 아니었습니다. 내가 왜 놀라겠습니까!"]], [["“I believe,”", "\"제가 지금,\" 데드록 마님이 후견인에게 말했습니다."], ["said Lady Dedlock to my guardian, “I have the pleasure of speaking to Mr. Jarndyce.”", "\"잔다이스 씨와 이야기하는 기쁨을 누리고 있는 듯하군요.\""]], [["“Your remembrance does me more honour than I had supposed it would, Lady Dedlock,”", "\"저를 기억해주시다니, 생각했던 것보다 더한 영광입니다, 데드록 마님,\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“I recognized you in church on Sunday.", "\"일요일에 교회에서 당신을 알아보았어요."], ["I am sorry that any local disputes of Sir Leicester’s—they are not of his seeking, however, I believe—should render it a matter of some absurd difficulty to show you any attention here.”", "레스터 경의 지역 분쟁이—하긴 그분이 일부러 벌인 일은 아니라고 알고 있습니다만—이곳에서 당신께 조금이라도 예를 갖추는 일을 그토록 우스꽝스럽게 어렵게 만든다니 유감입니다.\""]], [["“I am aware of the circumstances,”", "\"그 사정은 잘 알고 있습니다,\""], ["returned my guardian with a smile, “and am sufficiently obliged.”", "후견인이 미소 지으며 대꾸했습니다. \"그리고 그것만으로 충분히 황송합니다.\""]], [["She had given him her hand in an indifferent way that seemed habitual to her and spoke in a correspondingly indifferent manner, though in a very pleasant voice.", "그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀었는데, 그녀에게 몸에 밴 듯한 무심한 태도였고, 말투도 그에 어울리게 무심했지만, 목소리는 무척 듣기 좋았습니다."], ["She was as graceful as she was beautiful, perfectly self-possessed, and had the air, I thought, of being able to attract and interest any one if she had thought it worth her while.", "그녀는 아름다운 만큼 우아했고, 완벽하게 침착했으며, 마음만 먹는다면 누구든 끌어당기고 흥미를 불러일으킬 수 있는 분위기를 지녔다고 나는 생각했습니다."], ["The keeper had brought her a chair on which she sat in the middle of the porch between us.", "사냥터지기가 의자를 가져다주자, 그녀는 우리 사이, 현관 한가운데에 앉았습니다."]], [["“Is the young gentleman disposed of whom you wrote to Sir Leicester about and whose wishes Sir Leicester was sorry not to have it in his power to advance in any way?”", "\"당신이 레스터 경에게 편지로 부탁했던, 그리고 레스터 경이 어떤 식으로도 그 바람을 돕지 못해 안타까워했던 그 젊은이는 자리를 잡았나요?\""], ["she said over her shoulder to my guardian.", "그녀가 어깨너머로 후견인에게 물었습니다."]], [["“I hope so,”", "\"그러길 바랍니다,\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["She seemed to respect him and even to wish to conciliate him.", "그녀는 그를 존중하는 듯했고, 심지어 그의 마음을 누그러뜨리고 싶어 하는 것 같았습니다."], ["There was something very winning in her haughty manner, and it became more familiar—I was going to say more easy, but that could hardly be—as she spoke to him over her shoulder.", "그녀의 도도한 태도에는 무척 매혹적인 데가 있었고, 어깨너머로 그에게 말을 건넬수록 그 태도는 한층 친근해졌습니다—한층 편안해졌다고 말하려 했지만, 그건 도무지 어울리지 않는 표현이겠지요."]], [["“I presume this is your other ward, Miss Clare?”", "\"이쪽은 당신의 또 다른 피후견인, 클레어 양인가요?\""]], [["He presented Ada, in form.", "그는 격식을 갖춰 에이다를 소개했습니다."]], [["“You will lose the disinterested part of your Don Quixote character,” said Lady Dedlock to Mr.", "\"이렇게 아름다운 이의 억울함만 바로잡아주신다면, 당신의 돈키호테다운 면모에서 사심 없는 부분은 잃게 되실 거예요,\""], ["Jarndyce over her shoulder again, “if you only redress the wrongs of beauty like this.", "데드록 마님이 다시 어깨너머로 잔다이스 씨에게 말했습니다."], ["But present me,”", "\"하지만 이 아가씨도,\""], ["and she turned full upon me, “to this young lady too!”", "그녀가 내게로 온전히 몸을 돌렸습니다. \"제게 소개해주세요!\""]], [["“Miss Summerson really is my ward,”", "\"서머슨 양은 진짜로 제 피후견인입니다,\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I am responsible to no Lord Chancellor in her case.”", "\"이 아이에 관해서라면 저는 어떤 대법관에게도 책임질 일이 없지요.\""]], [["“Has Miss Summerson lost both her parents?”", "\"서머슨 양은 부모를 모두 잃었나요?\""], ["said my Lady. “Yes.”", "마님이 말했습니다."]], [["“She is very fortunate in her guardian.”", "\"그렇습니다.\" \"후견인을 참 잘 만났군요.\""]], [["Lady Dedlock looked at me, and I looked at her and said I was indeed.", "데드록 마님이 나를 바라보았고, 나도 그녀를 바라보며 정말 그렇다고 말했습니다."], ["All at once she turned from me with a hasty air, almost expressive of displeasure or dislike, and spoke to him over her shoulder again.", "그러자 그녀는 거의 못마땅함이나 싫증을 드러내듯 황급한 태도로 내게서 홱 돌아서더니, 다시 어깨너머로 그에게 말을 건넸습니다."]], [["“Ages have passed since we were in the habit of meeting, Mr. Jarndyce.”", "\"우리가 만나곤 하던 때로부터 여러 세월이 흘렀군요, 잔다이스 씨.\""]], [["“A long time.", "\"오랜 세월이지요."], ["At least I thought it was a long time, until I saw you last Sunday,”", "적어도 지난 일요일에 당신을 뵙기 전까지는 오랜 세월이라고 여겼습니다,\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["Even you are a courtier, or think it necessary to become one to me!”", "당신마저 아첨꾼이로군요, 아니면 제게 그래야 한다고 여기시거나!\""], ["she said with some disdain.", "그녀가 다소 경멸하듯 말했습니다."], ["“I have achieved that reputation, I suppose.”", "\"제가 그런 평판을 얻은 모양이지요, 아마.\""]], [["“You have achieved so much, Lady Dedlock,”", "\"당신은 너무도 많은 것을 이루셨으니, 데드록 마님,\""], ["said my guardian, “that you pay some little penalty, I dare say.", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"그 대가를 얼마쯤 치르시는 거겠지요, 아마."], ["But none to me.”", "하지만 제게 치르실 건 없습니다.\""]], [["“So much!”", "\"너무도 많은 것을!\""], ["she repeated, slightly laughing.", "그녀가 가볍게 웃으며 되뇌었습니다."], ["“Yes!”", "\"그렇죠!\""]], [["With her air of superiority, and power, and fascination, and I know not what, she seemed to regard Ada and me as little more than children.", "우월함과 권력과 매혹과, 그밖에 무어라 할 수 없는 분위기로, 그녀는 에이다와 나를 어린아이쯤으로밖에 여기지 않는 듯했습니다."], ["So, as she slightly laughed and afterwards sat looking at the rain, she was as self-possessed and as free to occupy herself with her own thoughts as if she had been alone.", "그래서 그녀는 가볍게 웃은 다음 앉아서 비를 바라보면서, 마치 혼자 있는 양 침착하게, 거리낌 없이 제 생각에 잠겼습니다."]], [["“I think you knew my sister when we were abroad together better than you know me?”", "\"우리가 함께 외국에 있을 때, 당신은 저보다 제 언니를 더 잘 알았던 것 같은데요?\""], ["she said, looking at him again.", "그녀가 다시 그를 바라보며 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, we happened to meet oftener,”", "\"그렇습니다, 그쪽과는 더 자주 만나게 되었지요,\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“We went our several ways,”", "\"우리는 각자의 길을 갔어요,\""], ["said Lady Dedlock, “and had little in common even before we agreed to differ.", "데드록 마님이 말했습니다. \"달리 살기로 마음먹기 전부터도 공통점이 거의 없었죠."], ["It is to be regretted, I suppose, but it could not be helped.”", "유감스러운 일이겠지만, 어쩔 수 없는 일이었어요.\""]], [["Lady Dedlock again sat looking at the rain.", "데드록 마님은 다시 앉아 비를 바라보았습니다."], ["The storm soon began to pass upon its way.", "폭풍은 곧 제 갈 길로 지나가기 시작했습니다."], ["The shower greatly abated, the lightning ceased, the thunder rolled among the distant hills, and the sun began to glisten on the wet leaves and the falling rain.", "빗줄기가 크게 잦아들고, 번개가 멎고, 천둥이 먼 언덕들 사이로 굴러갔으며, 햇살이 젖은 잎새와 떨어지는 빗방울 위에서 반짝이기 시작했습니다."], ["As we sat there, silently, we saw a little pony phaeton coming towards us at a merry pace.", "우리가 그곳에 말없이 앉아 있는데, 작은 조랑말 마차 한 대가 경쾌한 속도로 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 것이 보였습니다."]], [["“The messenger is coming back, my Lady,”", "\"심부름꾼이 마차를 가지고 돌아오는군요, 마님,\""], ["said the keeper, “with the carriage.”", "사냥터지기가 말했습니다."]], [["As it drove up, we saw that there were two people inside.", "마차가 다가오자, 그 안에 두 사람이 타고 있는 것이 보였습니다."], ["There alighted from it, with some cloaks and wrappers, first the Frenchwoman whom I had seen in church, and secondly the pretty girl, the Frenchwoman with a defiant confidence, the pretty girl confused and hesitating.", "망토며 덮개를 들고 마차에서 내린 것은, 먼저 교회에서 보았던 그 프랑스 여자였고, 다음으로 그 예쁜 처녀였습니다. 프랑스 여자는 도전하듯 당당했고, 예쁜 처녀는 어쩔 줄 몰라 머뭇거렸습니다."]], [["“What now?”", "\"이게 무슨 일이지?\""], ["said Lady Dedlock.", "데드록 마님이 말했습니다."], ["“Two!”", "\"둘이나!\""]], [["“I am your maid, my Lady, at the present,”", "\"지금은 제가 마님의 시중을 들고 있습니다, 마님,\""], ["said the Frenchwoman.", "프랑스 여자가 말했습니다."], ["“The message was for the attendant.”", "\"전갈은 시중드는 사람에게 온 것이었어요.\""]], [["“I was afraid you might mean me, my Lady,”", "\"마님께서 저를 부르시는 줄 알고 걱정했어요, 마님,\""], ["said the pretty girl.", "예쁜 처녀가 말했습니다."]], [["“I did mean you, child,”", "\"내가 부른 게 맞다, 얘야,\""], ["replied her mistress calmly.", "그녀의 주인이 차분히 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Put that shawl on me.”", "\"이 숄을 둘러다오.\""]], [["She slightly stooped her shoulders to receive it, and the pretty girl lightly dropped it in its place.", "그녀는 그것을 받으려고 어깨를 살짝 숙였고, 예쁜 처녀가 그것을 가뜬히 제자리에 걸쳐주었습니다."], ["The Frenchwoman stood unnoticed, looking on with her lips very tightly set.", "프랑스 여자는 본체만체 무시당한 채, 입술을 아주 앙다물고 지켜보고 서 있었습니다."]], [["“I am sorry,” said Lady Dedlock to Mr.", "\"우리가 옛 친분을 다시 잇기는 어려울 듯하여 유감입니다,\""], ["Jarndyce, “that we are not likely to renew our former acquaintance.", "데드록 마님이 잔다이스 씨에게 말했습니다."], ["You will allow me to send the carriage back for your two wards.", "\"두 피후견인을 위해 마차를 다시 돌려보내드리도록 허락해주세요."], ["It shall be here directly.”", "곧장 이리로 보내겠습니다.\""]], [["But as he would on no account accept this offer, she took a graceful leave of Ada—none of me—and put her hand upon his proffered arm, and got into the carriage, which was a little, low, park carriage with a hood.", "하지만 그가 결단코 그 제안을 받아들이지 않았기에, 그녀는 에이다에게 우아하게 작별을 고하고—내게는 한마디도 없이—그가 내민 팔에 손을 얹고서, 덮개가 달린 자그마하고 나지막한 영지용 마차에 올라탔습니다."]], [["“Come in, child,”", "\"타거라, 얘야,\""], ["she said to the pretty girl; “I shall want you.", "그녀가 예쁜 처녀에게 말했습니다. \"네가 필요할 테니."], ["Go on!”", "출발!\""]], [["The carriage rolled away, and the Frenchwoman, with the wrappers she had brought hanging over her arm, remained standing where she had alighted.", "마차가 굴러가 버리자, 프랑스 여자는 가져왔던 덮개를 팔에 걸친 채, 내렸던 자리에 그대로 서 있었습니다."]], [["I suppose there is nothing pride can so little bear with as pride itself, and that she was punished for her imperious manner.", "자존심이 가장 견디지 못하는 것은 다름 아닌 자존심 자체인가 봅니다. 그녀는 그 오만한 태도에 대한 벌을 받은 셈이었습니다."], ["Her retaliation was the most singular I could have imagined.", "그녀의 보복은 내가 상상할 수 있었던 가장 기이한 것이었습니다."], ["She remained perfectly still until the carriage had turned into the drive, and then, without the least discomposure of countenance, slipped off her shoes, left them on the ground, and walked deliberately in the same direction through the wettest of the wet grass.", "그녀는 마차가 진입로로 돌아 들어갈 때까지 완벽하게 미동도 없이 서 있다가, 이윽고 얼굴빛 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않은 채, 신발을 벗어 땅에 버려두고는, 흠뻑 젖은 풀밭 가운데서도 가장 젖은 곳을 골라 같은 방향으로 또박또박 걸어갔습니다."]], [["“Is that young woman mad?”", "\"저 젊은 여자가 미쳤소?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, no, sir!”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 나리!\""], ["said the keeper, who, with his wife, was looking after her.", "아내와 함께 그녀를 지켜보던 사냥터지기가 말했습니다."], ["“Hortense is not one of that sort.", "\"오르탕스는 그런 부류가 아니에요."], ["She has as good a head-piece as the best.", "머리는 누구 못지않게 좋답니다."], ["But she’s mortal high and passionate—powerful high and passionate; and what with having notice to leave, and having others put above her, she don’t take kindly to it.”", "하지만 무섭게 콧대 높고 격정적이지요—지독하게 콧대 높고 격정적이에요. 게다가 나가라는 통보를 받은 데다 남들이 자기 위로 올라서니, 그걸 곱게 받아들이지 못하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“But why should she walk shoeless through all that water?”", "\"하지만 어째서 저 물을 죄다 맨발로 걸어간단 말이오?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, indeed, sir, unless it is to cool her down!”", "\"글쎄요, 나리, 머리라도 식히려는 게 아니라면 모르겠습니다요!\""], ["said the man.", "남자가 말했습니다."]], [["“Or unless she fancies it’s blood,”", "\"아니면 저게 피라고 여기는 게지요,\""], ["said the woman.", "아내가 말했습니다."], ["“She’d as soon walk through that as anything else, I think, when her own’s up!”", "\"성질이 돋으면, 저런 물쯤은 다른 무엇이라도 그렇듯 거침없이 밟고 갈 사람이에요!\""]], [["We passed not far from the house a few minutes afterwards.", "몇 분 뒤 우리는 저택에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳을 지났습니다."], ["Peaceful as it had looked when we first saw it, it looked even more so now, with a diamond spray glittering all about it, a light wind blowing, the birds no longer hushed but singing strongly, everything refreshed by the late rain, and the little carriage shining at the doorway like a fairy carriage made of silver.", "처음 보았을 때도 평화로웠지만, 지금은 한층 더 평화로워 보였습니다. 다이아몬드 같은 물방울이 사방에서 반짝이고, 산들바람이 불고, 새들은 더는 잠잠하지 않고 힘차게 노래했으며, 모든 것이 막 지나간 비에 싱그러워졌고, 문간의 작은 마차는 은으로 빚은 요정의 마차처럼 빛나고 있었습니다."], ["Still, very steadfastly and quietly walking towards it, a peaceful figure too in the landscape, went Mademoiselle Hortense, shoeless, through the wet grass.", "그래도, 더없이 한결같고 조용하게 그 마차를 향해 걸어가는, 풍경 속의 또 하나의 평화로운 형상—마드무아젤 오르탕스가, 신발을 벗은 채, 젖은 풀밭을 걸어가고 있었습니다."]], [["Moving On\nIt is the long vacation in the regions of Chancery Lane.", "챈서리 레인 일대는 장기 휴정에 들어섰다."], ["The good ships Law and Equity, those teak-built, copper-bottomed, iron-fastened, brazen-faced, and not by any means fast-sailing clippers are laid up in ordinary.", "보통법과 형평법이라는 두 척의 멋진 배, 티크로 짜고 밑바닥에 구리를 대고 쇠못으로 단단히 박고 뱃머리에 놋쇠를 둘렀으나 결코 빠른 항해와는 거리가 먼 그 쾌속 범선들은 닻을 내린 채 묶여 있다."], ["The Flying Dutchman, with a crew of ghostly clients imploring all whom they may encounter to peruse their papers, has drifted, for the time being, heaven knows where.", "마주치는 사람마다 붙들고 제발 자기네 서류 좀 읽어봐 달라고 애원하는 유령 같은 의뢰인들을 태운 방황하는 네덜란드인 유령선은, 당분간 어디로 떠내려갔는지 하늘만이 안다."], ["The courts are all shut up; the public offices lie in a hot sleep.", "법정은 죄다 문을 닫았고, 관청들은 후텁지근한 잠에 빠져 있다."], ["Westminster Hall itself is a shady solitude where nightingales might sing, and a tenderer class of suitors than is usually found there, walk.", "웨스트민스터 홀마저 나이팅게일이 노래해도 좋을 그늘진 적막이 되어, 그곳에서 흔히 보던 부류보다 한결 부드러운 소송인들이 거니는 곳이 되었다."]], [["The Temple, Chancery Lane, Serjeants’ Inn, and Lincoln’s Inn even unto the Fields are like tidal harbours at low water, where stranded proceedings, offices at anchor, idle clerks lounging on lop-sided stools that will not recover their perpendicular until the current of Term sets in, lie high and dry upon the ooze of the long vacation.", "템플과 챈서리 레인, 변호사 회관, 링컨 법학원과 그 너머 들판까지, 모두 썰물 때의 항구를 닮았다. 좌초한 소송들과 닻을 내린 사무실들, 개정기의 물살이 밀려들기 전까지는 결코 똑바로 서지 못할 한쪽으로 기우뚱한 의자에 늘어진 채 빈둥대는 서기들이, 장기 휴정의 진창 위에 높직이 마른 채로 뒹군다."], ["Outer doors of chambers are shut up by the score, messages and parcels are to be left at the Porter’s Lodge by the bushel.", "사무실 바깥문은 수십 개씩 잠겨 있고, 전갈과 소포는 수위실에 한 무더기씩 맡겨진다."], ["A crop of grass would grow in the chinks of the stone pavement outside Lincoln’s Inn Hall, but that the ticket-porters, who have nothing to do beyond sitting in the shade there, with their white aprons over their heads to keep the flies off, grub it up and eat it thoughtfully.", "링컨 법학원 회관 밖 돌바닥 틈새에서 풀이 한 무더기 자라날 법도 하건만, 그늘에 앉아 파리를 쫓느라 하얀 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓴 것 말고는 할 일이 없는 짐꾼들이 그 풀을 뽑아 골똘히 씹어먹는 통에 그러지도 못한다."]], [["There is only one judge in town.", "읍내에 판사라곤 단 한 사람뿐이다."], ["Even he only comes twice a week to sit in chambers.", "그마저도 일주일에 두 번 사실에 나와 앉을 뿐이다."], ["If the country folks of those assize towns on his circuit could see him now!", "그가 순회하는 순회재판 읍의 시골 사람들이 지금 그 모습을 본다면 어떨까!"], ["No full-bottomed wig, no red petticoats, no fur, no javelin-men, no white wands.", "머리 전체를 덮는 가발도 없고, 붉은 법복도, 모피도, 창을 든 호위병도, 흰 지팡이도 없다."], ["Merely a close-shaved gentleman in white trousers and a white hat, with sea-bronze on the judicial countenance, and a strip of bark peeled by the solar rays from the judicial nose, who calls in at the shell-fish shop as he comes along and drinks iced ginger-beer!", "그저 흰 바지에 흰 모자를 쓴, 말끔히 면도한 신사일 뿐이다. 판사다운 얼굴은 바닷바람에 그을렸고, 판사다운 코에서는 햇볕에 익은 살갗이 한 꺼풀 벗겨졌으며, 오는 길에 조개구이 가게에 들러 얼음 넣은 진저비어를 들이켜는 사람이다!"]], [["The bar of England is scattered over the face of the earth.", "잉글랜드의 법조계는 온 지상에 흩어졌다."], ["How England can get on through four long summer months without its bar—which is its acknowledged refuge in adversity and its only legitimate triumph in prosperity—is beside the question; assuredly that shield and buckler of Britannia are not in present wear.", "역경에 처했을 때 공인된 피난처요 번영할 때 유일하게 정당한 승리인 그 법조계 없이, 잉글랜드가 긴 여름철 넉 달을 어떻게 견디는가 하는 것은 따로 따져볼 문제다. 다만 브리타니아의 그 방패와 작은 방패가 지금은 쓰이지 않고 있는 것만은 분명하다."], ["The learned gentleman who is always so tremendously indignant at the unprecedented outrage committed on the feelings of his client by the opposite party that he never seems likely to recover it is doing infinitely better than might be expected in Switzerland.", "상대편이 의뢰인의 감정을 전례 없이 짓밟았다며 늘 어마어마하게 분개하여 도무지 회복할 가망이 없어 보이던 그 학식 높은 신사는, 스위스에서 기대 이상으로 훨씬 잘 지내고 있다."], ["The learned gentleman who does the withering business and who blights all opponents with his gloomy sarcasm is as merry as a grig at a French watering-place.", "음울한 빈정거림으로 모든 적수를 시들게 하는 일을 도맡던 학식 높은 신사는, 프랑스의 어느 온천 휴양지에서 귀뚜라미처럼 흥겹다."], ["The learned gentleman who weeps by the pint on the smallest provocation has not shed a tear these six weeks.", "사소한 자극에도 한 통씩 눈물을 쏟던 학식 높은 신사는, 이 여섯 주 동안 눈물 한 방울 흘리지 않았다."], ["The very learned gentleman who has cooled the natural heat of his gingery complexion in pools and fountains of law until he has become great in knotty arguments for term-time, when he poses the drowsy bench with legal “chaff,” inexplicable to the uninitiated and to most of the initiated too, is roaming, with a characteristic delight in aridity and dust, about Constantinople.", "생강빛으로 불그레한 안색의 타고난 열기를 법의 웅덩이와 샘물에 식혀, 개정기에 쓸 까다로운 논변의 대가가 된 그 무척 학식 높은 신사는—문외한은 물론이고 그 길에 들어선 사람조차 대부분 알아듣지 못할 법률 헛소리로 졸음에 겨운 판사석을 곤혹스럽게 만드는 그 사람은—메마름과 먼지에 대한 특유의 취향에 흡족해하며 콘스탄티노플 일대를 떠돈다."], ["Other dispersed fragments of the same great palladium are to be found on the canals of Venice, at the second cataract of the Nile, in the baths of Germany, and sprinkled on the sea-sand all over the English coast.", "같은 위대한 수호상의 나머지 흩어진 조각들은 베네치아의 운하에서, 나일강의 둘째 폭포에서, 독일의 온천에서, 그리고 잉글랜드 해안 곳곳의 모래밭에 흩뿌려진 채 찾아볼 수 있다."], ["Scarcely one is to be encountered in the deserted region of Chancery Lane.", "인적 끊긴 챈서리 레인 지대에서는 그런 사람 하나 마주치기도 어렵다."], ["If such a lonely member of the bar do flit across the waste, and come upon a prowling suitor who is unable to leave off haunting the scenes of his anxiety, they frighten one another and retreat into opposite shades.", "그렇게 외로운 법조인 하나가 그 황무지를 가로질러 어른거리다가, 제 불안의 현장을 떠나지 못해 어슬렁대는 소송인과 맞닥뜨리기라도 하면, 둘은 서로 화들짝 놀라 반대편 그늘로 물러나고 만다."]], [["It is the hottest long vacation known for many years.", "여러 해 만에 가장 무더운 장기 휴정이다."], ["All the young clerks are madly in love, and according to their various degrees, pine for bliss with the beloved object, at Margate, Ramsgate, or Gravesend.", "젊은 서기들은 죄다 미친 듯이 사랑에 빠져, 저마다 정도는 다르나 마게이트에서, 램스게이트에서, 그레이브젠드에서 연인과 누릴 행복을 애타게 그린다."], ["All the middle-aged clerks think their families too large.", "중년의 서기들은 하나같이 제 식구가 너무 많다고 여긴다."], ["All the unowned dogs who stray into the Inns of Court and pant about staircases and other dry places seeking water give short howls of aggravation.", "법학원 안으로 흘러들어 계단이며 물기 없는 구석을 헐떡이며 물을 찾아 헤매는 주인 없는 개들은 죄다 짤막하게 짜증 섞인 울음을 토한다."], ["All the blind men’s dogs in the streets draw their masters against pumps or trip them over buckets.", "거리의 맹인 안내견들은 죄다 주인을 펌프 쪽으로 끌고 가거나 양동이에 걸려 넘어지게 한다."], ["A shop with a sun-blind, and a watered pavement, and a bowl of gold and silver fish in the window, is a sanctuary.", "차양을 치고 보도에 물을 뿌리고 진열창에 금붕어 은붕어 담은 어항을 놓아둔 가게는 하나의 성소다."], ["Temple Bar gets so hot that it is, to the adjacent Strand and Fleet Street, what a heater is in an urn, and keeps them simmering all night.", "템플 바는 어찌나 뜨거운지, 인접한 스트랜드와 플리트 거리에는 마치 끓이는 항아리 속 데우개 같아서, 밤새도록 그곳을 보글보글 끓게 만든다."]], [["There are offices about the Inns of Court in which a man might be cool, if any coolness were worth purchasing at such a price in dullness; but the little thoroughfares immediately outside those retirements seem to blaze.", "법학원 일대에는 둔하기 짝이 없는 그런 대가를 치르고서라도 시원함을 살 만하다면 시원하게 지낼 수 있는 사무실도 더러 있다. 하지만 그 은둔처 바로 바깥의 좁은 골목길들은 활활 타오르는 듯하다."], ["In Mr. Krook’s court, it is so hot that the people turn their houses inside out and sit in chairs upon the pavement—Mr.", "크룩 씨네 마당은 어찌나 더운지 사람들이 집 안을 통째로 밖으로 뒤집어내고는 보도에 의자를 놓고 앉아 있다—크룩 씨도 그 가운데 하나로, 거기서 (더위라곤 모르는) 고양이를 곁에 두고 연구에 몰두한다."], ["Krook included, who there pursues his studies, with his cat (who never is too hot) by his side. The Sol’s Arms has discontinued the Harmonic Meetings for the season, and Little Swills is engaged at the Pastoral Gardens down the river, where he comes out in quite an innocent manner and sings comic ditties of a juvenile complexion calculated (as the bill says) not to wound the feelings of the most fastidious mind.", "솔스 암스는 이번 철의 화음 모임을 중단했고, 리틀 스윌스는 강 하류의 목가 정원에 출연 계약을 맺어, 거기서 자못 천진한 태도로 나와서는 (전단지에 적힌 대로) 아무리 까다로운 사람의 감정도 상하지 않게끔 어린아이 같은 우스개 노래를 부른다."]], [["Over all the legal neighbourhood there hangs, like some great veil of rust or gigantic cobweb, the idleness and pensiveness of the long vacation.", "법조계 동네 전체에는, 거대한 녹의 장막이나 어마어마한 거미줄처럼, 장기 휴정의 나른함과 시름이 드리워 있다."], ["Mr. Snagsby, law-stationer of Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, is sensible of the influence not only in his mind as a sympathetic and contemplative man, but also in his business as a law-stationer aforesaid.", "커시터 거리 쿡스 코트의 법률 문방구 주인 스내그스비 씨는, 다정하고 사색을 즐기는 사람으로서의 마음으로도 그렇거니와, 앞서 말한 법률 문방구 주인으로서의 장사로도 그 영향을 절감한다."], ["He has more leisure for musing in Staple Inn and in the Rolls Yard during the long vacation than at other seasons, and he says to the two ’prentices, what a thing it is in such hot weather to think that you live in an island with the sea a-rolling and a-bowling right round you.", "그는 장기 휴정 동안 스테이플 법학원과 롤스 야드에서 다른 철보다 한가로이 사색에 잠길 여유가 더 많아져서, 두 도제에게 이런 무더운 날씨에 사방으로 바다가 출렁이며 굽이도는 섬에 살고 있다고 생각하면 참 묘한 일이라고 말한다."]], [["Guster is busy in the little drawing-room on this present afternoon in the long vacation, when Mr.", "장기 휴정의 어느 오후, 스내그스비 부부가 손님을 맞을 작정인 오늘, 거스터는 자그마한 응접실에서 분주하다."], ["and Mrs. Snagsby have it in contemplation to receive company.", "초대받은 손님은 수가 많다기보다는 자못 엄선된 분들로, 채드밴드 부부 두 사람뿐이다."], ["The expected guests are rather select than numerous, being Mr. and Mrs.", "채드밴드 씨는 말로도 글로도 자기 자신을 곧잘 그릇이라 칭하는 까닭에, 이따금 낯선 사람들에게 항해와 관련된 신사로 오해받기도 한다."], ["Chadband and no more. From Mr.", "하지만 그는 자기 표현을 빌리자면 \"성직에 몸담은\""], ["Chadband’s being much given to describe himself, both verbally and in writing, as a vessel, he is occasionally mistaken by strangers for a gentleman connected with navigation, but he is, as he expresses it, “in the ministry.” Mr.", "사람이다."], ["Chadband is attached to no particular denomination and is considered by his persecutors to have nothing so very remarkable to say on the greatest of subjects as to render his volunteering, on his own account, at all incumbent on his conscience; but he has his followers, and Mrs.", "채드밴드 씨는 어느 특정 교파에도 속해 있지 않으며, 그를 헐뜯는 자들은 그가 자청해서 나설 만큼 양심상 마땅히 그래야 할 정도로 가장 위대한 주제에 관해 그리 대단할 것 없는 이야기를 하는 사람이라고 여긴다."], ["Snagsby is of the number. Mrs.", "그래도 그를 따르는 무리가 있으니, 스내그스비 부인도 그 가운데 하나다."], ["Snagsby has but recently taken a passage upward by the vessel, Chadband; and her attention was attracted to that Bark A 1, when she was something flushed by the hot weather.", "스내그스비 부인은 최근에야 채드밴드라는 배에 올라 위로 향하는 항로에 몸을 실었다. 무더운 날씨에 얼마간 얼굴이 달아올랐을 무렵, 그녀의 주의가 그 일등급 범선에 끌렸던 것이다."]], [["“My little woman,” says Mr.", "\"우리 마누라는 말이지,\""], ["Snagsby to the sparrows in Staple Inn, “likes to have her religion rather sharp, you see!”", "스내그스비 씨가 스테이플 법학원의 참새들에게 말한다. \"종교를 좀 칼칼한 걸로 좋아한단 말씀이야, 그렇다니까!\""]], [["So Guster, much impressed by regarding herself for the time as the handmaid of Chadband, whom she knows to be endowed with the gift of holding forth for four hours at a stretch, prepares the little drawing-room for tea.", "그리하여 거스터는, 한 번에 네 시간씩 내리 설교를 늘어놓는 재주를 타고난 줄 익히 아는 채드밴드의 하녀로 잠시 자신을 여기는 일에 자못 감격하여, 차 마실 자그마한 응접실을 채비한다."], ["All the furniture is shaken and dusted, the portraits of Mr. and Mrs.", "가구는 죄다 털고 닦았고, 스내그스비 부부의 초상화는 젖은 천으로 매만졌으며, 가장 좋은 다기 한 벌이 차려졌다."], ["Snagsby are touched up with a wet cloth, the best tea-service is set forth, and there is excellent provision made of dainty new bread, crusty twists, cool fresh butter, thin slices of ham, tongue, and German sausage, and delicate little rows of anchovies nestling in parsley, not to mention new-laid eggs, to be brought up warm in a napkin, and hot buttered toast.", "그리고 보드라운 새 빵, 바삭한 꽈배기 빵, 시원하고 신선한 버터, 얇게 저민 햄과 우설과 독일 소시지, 그리고 파슬리에 폭 파묻힌 멸치젓을 작고 앙증맞게 줄지어 놓은 것까지 푸짐하게 마련해놓았으니, 따뜻한 행주에 싸서 김이 모락모락 올라오게 내올 갓 낳은 달걀과 뜨거운 버터 토스트는 말할 것도 없다."], ["For Chadband is rather a consuming vessel—the persecutors say a gorging vessel—and can wield such weapons of the flesh as a knife and fork remarkably well.", "채드밴드는 어지간히 먹어치우는 그릇이기 때문이다—헐뜯는 자들 말로는 게걸스레 처넣는 그릇이라 한다—나이프와 포크 같은 육신의 무기를 더없이 능란하게 휘두를 줄 안다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby in his best coat, looking at all the preparations when they are completed and coughing his cough of deference behind his hand, says to Mrs. Snagsby, “At what time did you expect Mr. and Mrs. Chadband, my love?”", "가장 좋은 외투를 차려입은 스내그스비 씨는 채비가 다 끝난 것을 둘러보며 손으로 입을 가리고 공손하게 기침을 한 번 하고는 스내그스비 부인에게 말한다. \"여보, 채드밴드 부부는 몇 시에 오시기로 했소?\""]], [["“At six,”", "\"여섯 시예요.\""], ["says Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인이 말한다."]], [["Mr.", "스내그스비 씨는 점잖고 무심한 투로 \"그 시간이 지났는데\""], ["Snagsby observes in a mild and casual way that “it’s gone that.”", "하고 한마디 한다."]], [["“Perhaps you’d like to begin without them,”", "\"그분들 없이 먼저 들고 싶으신가 보죠.\""], ["is Mrs. Snagsby’s reproachful remark.", "스내그스비 부인이 나무라듯 한마디 한다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby does look as if he would like it very much, but he says, with his cough of mildness, “No, my dear, no.", "스내그스비 씨는 정말이지 무척 그러고 싶은 표정이지만, 점잖은 기침을 곁들여 이렇게 말한다."], ["I merely named the time.”", "\"아니오, 여보, 아니오. 그냥 시간을 말한 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“What’s time,”", "\"시간이라는 게,\""], ["says Mrs.", "스내그스비 부인이 말한다."], ["Snagsby, “to eternity?”", "\"영원에 견주면 뭐예요?\""]], [["“Very true, my dear,”", "\"지당한 말씀이오, 여보.\" 스내그스비 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr.", "\"다만 누가 차 마시려고 먹을 것을 장만할 때는—아무래도—시간을 좀 더 염두에 두고 하는 것 아니겠소."], ["Snagsby.", "그리고 차 마실 시간을 정해놓았으면, 거기에 맞춰 오는 편이 낫지 않겠소.\""], ["“Only when a person lays in victuals for tea, a person does it with a view—perhaps—more to time.", "\"맞춰 오다니요!\" 스내그스비 부인이 매섭게 되뇐다."], ["And when a time is named for having tea, it’s better to come up to it.”", "\"맞춰요! 채드밴드 씨가 무슨 권투 선수라도 되는 줄 아세요!\""]], [["“To come up to it!” Mrs. Snagsby repeats with severity. “Up to it! As if Mr. Chadband was a fighter!”", "* 맞춰 오다: 원문은 'come up to it'으로, 권투에서 시합 시작 전 선수가 가운데 선까지 나와 맞붙는 'come up to scratch'를 떠올리게 하여, 스내그스비 부인이 권투 운운하며 트집 잡는 말장난이다."]], [["“Not at all, my dear,”", "\"천만에, 여보.\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["Here, Guster, who had been looking out of the bedroom window, comes rustling and scratching down the little staircase like a popular ghost, and falling flushed into the drawing-room, announces that Mr. and Mrs. Chadband have appeared in the court. The bell at the inner door in the passage immediately thereafter tinkling, she is admonished by Mrs. Snagsby, on pain of instant reconsignment to her patron saint, not to omit the ceremony of announcement. Much discomposed in her nerves (which were previously in the best order) by this threat, she so fearfully mutilates that point of state as to announce “Mr. and Mrs. Cheeseming, least which, Imeantersay, whatsername!” and retires conscience-stricken from the presence.", "이때 침실 창밖을 내다보고 있던 거스터가, 흔해빠진 유령처럼 바스락거리고 긁어대며 좁은 계단을 내려와 얼굴이 벌게진 채 응접실로 굴러들어와서는, 채드밴드 부부가 마당에 나타났다고 알린다. 그 직후 복도 안쪽 문에 달린 종이 딸랑거리자, 스내그스비 부인은 당장 수호성인에게로 도로 보내버리겠다고 으름장을 놓으며 손님 알리는 격식을 빠뜨리지 말라고 단단히 일러둔다. (앞서까지는 더없이 멀쩡하던) 신경이 이 위협에 크게 흐트러진 거스터는, 그 격식의 요점을 어찌나 끔찍이 망가뜨려놓는지 \"치즈밍 씨 부부, 아니 그게, 제 말씀은, 거시기예요!\"라고 알리고는 양심에 찔린 채 그 앞에서 물러난다."]], [["Mr. Chadband is a large yellow man with a fat smile and a general appearance of having a good deal of train oil in his system.", "채드밴드 씨는 누런 빛이 도는 덩치 큰 남자로, 기름진 미소를 띠고 있으며, 온몸에 고래기름이 잔뜩 차 있는 듯한 분위기를 풍긴다."], ["Mrs. Chadband is a stern, severe-looking, silent woman.", "채드밴드 부인은 근엄하고 매서운 인상에 말이 없는 여자다."], ["Mr. Chadband moves softly and cumbrously, not unlike a bear who has been taught to walk upright.", "채드밴드 씨는 부드러우면서도 둔중하게 움직이는데, 똑바로 걷도록 훈련받은 곰과 그리 다르지 않다."], ["He is very much embarrassed about the arms, as if they were inconvenient to him and he wanted to grovel, is very much in a perspiration about the head, and never speaks without first putting up his great hand, as delivering a token to his hearers that he is going to edify them.", "그는 팔을 무척 거북해하는데, 마치 팔이 불편하기라도 한 양, 차라리 엎드려 기고 싶어 하는 듯하다. 머리에는 땀이 흥건하고, 말을 꺼낼 때마다 먼저 그 커다란 손을 치켜드는데, 이제 듣는 이들을 교화하려 한다는 신호를 보내는 셈이다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["says Mr. Chadband, “peace be on this house!", "채드밴드 씨가 말한다."], ["On the master thereof, on the mistress thereof, on the young maidens, and on the young men!", "\"이 집에 평화가 깃들기를! 이 집 주인에게, 이 집 안주인에게, 젊은 처녀들에게, 그리고 젊은이들에게!"], ["My friends, why do I wish for peace?", "여러분, 나는 어찌하여 평화를 바라는가? 평화란 무엇인가?"], ["What is peace?", "그것이 전쟁인가?"], ["Is it war? No.", "아니다."], ["Is it strife? No.", "그것이 다툼인가?"], ["Is it lovely, and gentle, and beautiful, and pleasant, and serene, and joyful?", "아니다. 그것이 사랑스럽고, 온화하고, 아름답고, 즐겁고, 평온하고, 기쁜 것인가?"], ["Oh, yes!", "오, 그렇다!"], ["Therefore, my friends, I wish for peace, upon you and upon yours.”", "그러므로 여러분, 나는 여러분에게, 그리고 여러분의 식구에게 평화가 깃들기를 바라노라.\""]], [["In consequence of Mrs. Snagsby looking deeply edified, Mr. Snagsby thinks it expedient on the whole to say amen, which is well received.", "스내그스비 부인이 깊이 교화된 표정을 짓는 까닭에, 스내그스비 씨는 통틀어 아멘 하고 말하는 편이 좋겠다고 판단하는데, 이 말은 좋은 반응을 얻는다."]], [["“Now, my friends,”", "\"자, 여러분,\""], ["proceeds Mr.", "채드밴드 씨가 말을 잇는다."], ["Chadband, “since I am upon this theme—”", "\"내가 이 주제를 다루는 김에—\""]], [["Guster presents herself. Mrs.", "거스터가 모습을 드러낸다."], ["Snagsby, in a spectral bass voice and without removing her eyes from Chadband, says with dreadful distinctness, “Go away!”", "스내그스비 부인은 채드밴드에게서 눈길을 떼지 않은 채 유령 같은 저음으로 무시무시할 만큼 또렷이 말한다. \"물러가!\""]], [["“Now, my friends,”", "\"자, 여러분,\""], ["says Chadband, “since I am upon this theme, and in my lowly path improving it—”", "채드밴드가 말한다. \"내가 이 주제를 다루며, 나의 비천한 길에서 이를 깊이 새기는 김에—\""]], [["Guster is heard unaccountably to murmur “one thousing seven hundred and eighty-two.”", "거스터가 영문 모를 소리로 \"천칠백팔십이\"라고 중얼거리는 소리가 들린다."], ["The spectral voice repeats more solemnly, “Go away!”", "유령 같은 목소리가 한층 엄숙하게 되풀이한다. \"물러가!\""]], [["“Now, my friends,” says Mr.", "\"자, 여러분,\""], ["Chadband, “we will inquire in a spirit of love—”", "채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"우리 사랑의 정신으로 물어보기로 하자—\""]], [["Still Guster reiterates “one thousing seven hundred and eighty-two.”", "그래도 거스터는 \"천칠백팔십이\"를 거듭 읊는다."]], [["Mr. Chadband, pausing with the resignation of a man accustomed to be persecuted and languidly folding up his chin into his fat smile, says, “Let us hear the maiden!", "박해받는 데 이골이 난 사람의 체념을 보이며 말을 멈춘 채드밴드 씨는, 턱을 나른하게 기름진 미소 속으로 접어 넣으며 말한다."], ["Speak, maiden!”", "\"처녀의 말을 들어봅시다! 말해보거라, 처녀야!\""]], [["“One thousing seven hundred and eighty-two, if you please, sir.", "\"천칠백팔십이라는데요, 죄송해요, 나리."], ["Which he wish to know what the shilling ware for,”", "그 일 실링이 뭐 하는 돈인지 알고 싶다는데요.\""], ["says Guster, breathless.", "거스터가 숨을 헐떡이며 말한다."]], [["“For?” returns Mrs.", "\"뭐 하는 거냐고?\""], ["Chadband.", "채드밴드 부인이 받는다."], ["“For his fare!”", "\"찻삯 말이지!\""]], [["Guster replied that “he insistes on one and eightpence or on summonsizzing the party.” Mrs. Snagsby and Mrs. Chadband are proceeding to grow shrill in indignation when Mr. Chadband quiets the tumult by lifting up his hand.", "거스터는 \"그 사람이 일 실링 팔 펜스를 내든지, 안 그러면 사람을 소환하겠다고 우긴다\"고 대답한다. 스내그스비 부인과 채드밴드 부인이 분개하여 목소리를 날카롭게 높이려는 참에, 채드밴드 씨가 손을 들어 소란을 가라앉힌다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["says he, “I remember a duty unfulfilled yesterday.", "그가 말한다. \"어제 이행하지 못한 의무 하나가 떠오르는구려."], ["It is right that I should be chastened in some penalty.", "내가 얼마간 벌을 받아 마땅하오."], ["I ought not to murmur.", "투덜대서는 아니 되지요."], ["Rachael, pay the eightpence!”", "레이철, 그 팔 펜스를 치르시오!\""]], [["While Mrs. Snagsby, drawing her breath, looks hard at Mr. Snagsby, as who should say, “You hear this apostle!” and while Mr. Chadband glows with humility and train oil, Mrs. Chadband pays the money. It is Mr. Chadband’s habit—it is the head and front of his pretensions indeed—to keep this sort of debtor and creditor account in the smallest items and to post it publicly on the most trivial occasions.", "스내그스비 부인이 숨을 들이쉬며 마치 \"이 사도님 좀 보세요!\" 하는 듯한 표정으로 스내그스비 씨를 빤히 바라보는 동안, 그리고 채드밴드 씨가 겸허함과 고래기름으로 번들거리는 동안, 채드밴드 부인이 돈을 치른다. 이런 식의 채권 채무 셈을 아무리 사소한 항목까지 따지고, 더없이 하찮은 일에까지 공공연히 드러내는 것이 채드밴드 씨의 버릇이다—사실 그것이 그가 내세우는 허세의 으뜸가는 골자다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"여러분,\" 채드밴드가 말한다."], ["says Chadband, “eightpence is not much; it might justly have been one and fourpence; it might justly have been half a crown.", "\"팔 펜스는 큰돈이 아니오. 일 실링 사 펜스가 될 수도 있었고, 응당 반 크라운이 될 수도 있었소."], ["O let us be joyful, joyful!", "오, 기뻐합시다, 기뻐합시다!"], ["O let us be joyful!”", "오, 기뻐합시다!\""]], [["With which remark, which appears from its sound to be an extract in verse, Mr. Chadband stalks to the table, and before taking a chair, lifts up his admonitory hand.", "이 말은—그 가락으로 보아 시구의 한 토막인 듯한데—그 말과 함께 채드밴드 씨는 식탁으로 거드름을 피우며 다가가, 의자에 앉기에 앞서 훈계하는 손을 치켜든다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["says he, “what is this which we now behold as being spread before us?", "그가 말한다. \"지금 우리 앞에 차려진 이것은 무엇이오?"], ["Refreshment.", "음식이오."], ["Do we need refreshment then, my friends?", "그렇다면 우리에게 음식이 필요하오, 여러분?"], ["We do.", "그렇소."], ["And why do we need refreshment, my friends?", "그런데 어찌하여 우리에게 음식이 필요하오, 여러분? 우리가 한낱 죽을 목숨인 까닭이오."], ["Because we are but mortal, because we are but sinful, because we are but of the earth, because we are not of the air.", "우리가 한낱 죄지은 자인 까닭이오. 우리가 한낱 흙에서 난 자요, 공기에서 난 자가 아닌 까닭이오."], ["Can we fly, my friends?", "우리가 날 수 있소, 여러분?"], ["We cannot.", "우리는 날 수 없소."], ["Why can we not fly, my friends?”", "어찌하여 우리는 날 수 없소, 여러분?\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby, presuming on the success of his last point, ventures to observe in a cheerful and rather knowing tone, “No wings.”", "스내그스비 씨는 조금 전의 성공에 우쭐하여 명랑하고 자못 아는 체하는 투로 \"날개가 없으니까요\"라고 한마디 거든다."], ["But is immediately frowned down by Mrs. Snagsby.", "하지만 곧장 스내그스비 부인에게 눈총을 받아 입을 다문다."]], [["“I say, my friends,”", "\"내 말하노니, 여러분,\""], ["pursues Mr. Chadband, utterly rejecting and obliterating Mr.", "채드밴드 씨는 스내그스비 씨의 의견을 깡그리 묵살하고 지워버리며 말을 잇는다."], ["Snagsby’s suggestion, “why can we not fly?", "\"어찌하여 우리는 날 수 없소?"], ["Is it because we are calculated to walk?", "우리가 걷도록 지어진 까닭이오?"], ["It is.", "그렇소."], ["Could we walk, my friends, without strength?", "우리가 힘 없이 걸을 수 있소, 여러분?"], ["We could not.", "그럴 수 없소."], ["What should we do without strength, my friends?", "힘이 없으면 우리가 어찌 되겠소, 여러분?"], ["Our legs would refuse to bear us, our knees would double up, our ankles would turn over, and we should come to the ground.", "다리는 우리를 떠받치기를 마다할 것이고, 무릎은 꺾일 것이며, 발목은 접질리고, 우리는 땅바닥에 쓰러질 것이오."], ["Then from whence, my friends, in a human point of view, do we derive the strength that is necessary to our limbs?", "그렇다면 여러분, 인간의 관점에서 우리 사지에 꼭 필요한 그 힘을 우리는 어디서 얻는 것이오?"], ["Is it,”", "그것은,\""], ["says Chadband, glancing over the table, “from bread in various forms, from butter which is churned from the milk which is yielded unto us by the cow, from the eggs which are laid by the fowl, from ham, from tongue, from sausage, and from such like?", "채드밴드가 식탁을 한 번 훑어보며 말한다. \"갖가지 모양의 빵에서, 암소가 우리에게 내어주는 젖을 휘저어 만든 버터에서, 닭이 낳은 달걀에서, 햄에서, 우설에서, 소시지에서, 그리고 그와 같은 것들에서 얻는 것 아니오?"], ["It is.", "그렇소."], ["Then let us partake of the good things which are set before us!”", "그러니 우리 앞에 차려진 이 좋은 것들을 들기로 합시다!\""]], [["The persecutors denied that there was any particular gift in Mr. Chadband’s piling verbose flights of stairs, one upon another, after this fashion.", "채드밴드 씨가 이런 식으로 장황한 말의 계단을 한 단 한 단 쌓아 올리는 데 무슨 특별한 재능 따위는 없다고 헐뜯는 자들은 부인했다."], ["But this can only be received as a proof of their determination to persecute, since it must be within everybody’s experience that the Chadband style of oratory is widely received and much admired.", "하지만 채드밴드식 웅변이 널리 환영받고 크게 칭송받는다는 것은 누구나 겪어봤을 일이 분명하므로, 이는 그저 그들이 기어이 헐뜯고 말겠다는 결심의 증거로 받아들일 수밖에 없다."]], [["Mr. Chadband, however, having concluded for the present, sits down at Mr.", "어쨌거나 채드밴드 씨는 지금으로서는 말을 맺고, 스내그스비 씨의 식탁에 앉아 어마어마하게 먹어치운다."], ["Snagsby’s table and lays about him prodigiously. The conversion of nutriment of any sort into oil of the quality already mentioned appears to be a process so inseparable from the constitution of this exemplary vessel that in beginning to eat and drink, he may be described as always becoming a kind of considerable oil mills or other large factory for the production of that article on a wholesale scale.", "어떤 종류의 양분이든 앞서 말한 그 질의 기름으로 바꾸는 일이, 이 본보기 같은 그릇의 체질과 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 과정인 듯하니, 먹고 마시기 시작하면 그는 늘 그 물건을 도매 규모로 만들어내는 일종의 거대한 기름 방앗간이나 다른 큰 공장이 된다고 묘사해도 좋을 정도다."], ["On the present evening of the long vacation, in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, he does such a powerful stroke of business that the warehouse appears to be quite full when the works cease.", "장기 휴정의 이날 저녁, 커시터 거리 쿡스 코트에서 그가 어찌나 강력하게 한 건 해치우는지, 작업이 끝날 무렵에는 창고가 가득 찬 듯이 보인다."]], [["At this period of the entertainment, Guster, who has never recovered her first failure, but has neglected no possible or impossible means of bringing the establishment and herself into contempt—among which may be briefly enumerated her unexpectedly performing clashing military music on Mr. Chadband’s head with plates, and afterwards crowning that gentleman with muffins—at which period of the entertainment, Guster whispers Mr. Snagsby that he is wanted.", "이 향응이 무르익은 무렵, 첫 실수에서 끝내 헤어나지 못한 거스터는 이 집과 자신을 웃음거리로 만들 가능성이 있는 수단이든 없는 수단이든 하나도 빼놓지 않고 동원했으니—그 가운데 몇 가지만 간추리자면, 느닷없이 접시로 채드밴드 씨의 머리에 요란한 군악을 연주하더니 이어서 그 신사의 머리에 머핀을 씌워 왕관을 만들어준 일이 있다—바로 그 무렵 거스터가 스내그스비 씨에게 손님이 찾는다고 귀띔한다."]], [["“And being wanted in the—not to put too fine a point upon it—in the shop,” says Mr.", "\"그러니까 손님이—이렇게 말하면 좀 거시기하지만—가게에서 찾는다 하니,\""], ["Snagsby, rising, “perhaps this good company will excuse me for half a minute.”", "스내그스비 씨가 일어서며 말한다. \"이 좋은 분들께서 잠깐만 양해해주시겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby descends and finds the two ’prentices intently contemplating a police constable, who holds a ragged boy by the arm.", "스내그스비 씨가 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 두 도제가 누더기 차림의 소년의 팔을 붙들고 있는 순경 하나를 골똘히 바라보고 있다."]], [["“Why, bless my heart,” says Mr.", "\"아니, 이런 세상에,\""], ["Snagsby, “what’s the matter!”", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"무슨 일이오!\""]], [["“This boy,”", "\"이 녀석이 말입니다,\""], ["says the constable, “although he’s repeatedly told to, won’t move on—”", "순경이 말한다. \"거듭 일렀는데도 비키질 않아서요—\""]], [["“I’m always a-moving on, sar,”", "\"전 늘 비키고 있다구요, 나리.\""], ["cries the boy, wiping away his grimy tears with his arm.", "소년이 팔로 꾀죄죄한 눈물을 닦으며 외친다."], ["“I’ve always been a-moving and a-moving on, ever since I was born.", "\"태어난 뒤로 줄곧 비키고 또 비키고 있다니까요."], ["Where can I possibly move to, sir, more nor I do move!”", "지금보다 더 어디로 비키란 말이에요, 나리, 대체 어디로요!\""]], [["“He won’t move on,”", "\"이 녀석이 비키질 않아서요,\""], ["says the constable calmly, with a slight professional hitch of his neck involving its better settlement in his stiff stock, “although he has been repeatedly cautioned, and therefore I am obliged to take him into custody.", "순경이 침착하게 말하며, 빳빳한 깃 속으로 목을 더 잘 자리 잡게 하려는 듯 직업적으로 목을 슬쩍 움직인다. \"거듭 주의를 줬는데도 말입니다."], ["He’s as obstinate a young gonoph as I know.", "그래서 부득이 잡아넣을 수밖에 없습죠. 제가 아는 놈팡이 중에 이만큼 고집 센 어린 좀도둑도 없습니다."], ["He WON’T move on.”", "이 녀석이 도무지 비키질 않아요.\""]], [["“Oh, my eye!", "\"아이고, 어쩌라구요!"], ["Where can I move to!”", "대체 어디로 비키냐구요!\""], ["cries the boy, clutching quite desperately at his hair and beating his bare feet upon the floor of Mr. Snagsby’s passage.", "소년이 머리카락을 잡아 쥐어뜯고 맨발로 스내그스비 씨네 복도 바닥을 쾅쾅 구르며 자못 필사적으로 외친다."]], [["“Don’t you come none of that or I shall make blessed short work of you!”", "\"그딴 짓 했다간 내가 아주 짧게 끝장을 내줄 테다!\""], ["says the constable, giving him a passionless shake.", "순경이 무덤덤하게 소년을 한 번 흔들며 말한다."], ["“My instructions are that you are to move on.", "\"내가 받은 명령은 네놈더러 비키라는 거다."], ["I have told you so five hundred times.”", "오백 번도 더 일렀잖느냐.\""]], [["“But where?”", "\"근데 어디로요?\""], ["cries the boy.", "소년이 외친다."]], [["“Well! Really, constable, you know,”", "\"글쎄, 순경 양반, 그게 말이오,\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby wistfully, and coughing behind his hand his cough of great perplexity and doubt, “really, that does seem a question.", "스내그스비 씨가 안쓰러운 듯 말하며, 손으로 입을 가리고 몹시 당혹스럽고 미심쩍은 기침을 한다."], ["Where, you know?”", "\"정말이지 그게 좀 문제 같소만. 어디로, 그러게 말이오?\""]], [["“My instructions don’t go to that,”", "\"제 명령은 거기까지는 아닙니다.\""], ["replies the constable.", "순경이 대답한다."], ["“My instructions are that this boy is to move on.”", "\"제가 받은 명령은 이 녀석더러 비키라는 것뿐입죠.\""]], [["Do you hear, Jo?", "알아듣겠느냐, 조?"], ["It is nothing to you or to any one else that the great lights of the parliamentary sky have failed for some few years in this business to set you the example of moving on.", "의회라는 하늘의 거대한 빛들이 이 일에서 몇 해 동안 네게 비키는 본보기를 보여주지 못했다는 것은, 너에게도 다른 누구에게도 아무것도 아니다."], ["The one grand recipe remains for you—the profound philosophical prescription—the be-all and the end-all of your strange existence upon earth.", "네게는 단 하나의 위대한 처방이 남아 있으니—심오한 철학적 처방—이 땅 위 네 기묘한 존재의 전부이자 궁극이다."], ["Move on!", "비켜라!"], ["You are by no means to move off, Jo, for the great lights can’t at all agree about that.", "그렇다고 아주 떠나버려서는 결코 아니 된다, 조. 그 점에 대해서는 그 거대한 빛들이 도무지 의견 일치를 보지 못하니까."], ["Move on!", "비켜라!"]], [["Mr. Snagsby says nothing to this effect, says nothing at all indeed, but coughs his forlornest cough, expressive of no thoroughfare in any direction.", "스내그스비 씨는 이런 취지로는 아무 말도 하지 않는다."], ["By this time Mr. and Mrs.", "사실 아무 말도 하지 않고, 어느 방향으로도 길이 없다는 뜻을 담아 더없이 처량한 기침만 할 뿐이다."], ["Chadband and Mrs. Snagsby, hearing the altercation, have appeared upon the stairs.", "이때쯤 말다툼 소리를 들은 채드밴드 부부와 스내그스비 부인이 계단에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Guster having never left the end of the passage, the whole household are assembled.", "거스터는 복도 끝을 한 번도 떠난 적이 없으니, 온 식구가 다 모인 셈이다."]], [["“The simple question is, sir,”", "\"간단히 여쭙겠습니다, 나리,\""], ["says the constable, “whether you know this boy.", "순경이 말한다. \"이 녀석을 아십니까?"], ["He says you do.”", "이 녀석이 안다고 합니다만.\""]], [["Mrs.", "스내그스비 부인이 높은 곳에서 대뜸 외친다."], ["Snagsby, from her elevation, instantly cries out, “No he don’t!”", "\"아니, 모르세요!\""]], [["“My lit-tle woman!”", "\"여보, 마누라!\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby, looking up the staircase.", "스내그스비 씨가 계단 위를 올려다보며 말한다."], ["“My love, permit me!", "\"여보, 잠깐만!"], ["Pray have a moment’s patience, my dear.", "제발 잠깐만 참아주시오, 여보. 이 아이를 좀 알기는 아오."], ["I do know something of this lad, and in what I know of him, I can’t say that there’s any harm; perhaps on the contrary, constable.”", "그런데 내가 아는 한, 무슨 나쁜 구석이 있다고는 못 하겠소. 순경 양반, 어쩌면 오히려 그 반대일지도 모르오.\""], ["To whom the law-stationer relates his Joful and woeful experience, suppressing the half-crown fact.", "그러고는 법률 문방구 주인이 순경에게 조와 얽힌 딱하고도 서글픈 사연을 들려주는데, 반 크라운 이야기만은 빼놓는다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"글쎄요!\""], ["says the constable, “so far, it seems, he had grounds for what he said.", "순경이 말한다. \"그렇다면 여태까지는 이 녀석이 한 말에 근거가 있었던 모양입니다."], ["When I took him into custody up in Holborn, he said you knew him.", "제가 홀본 위쪽에서 이 녀석을 잡았을 때, 나리를 안다고 하더군요."], ["Upon that, a young man who was in the crowd said he was acquainted with you, and you were a respectable housekeeper, and if I’d call and make the inquiry, he’d appear.", "그러자 군중 속에 있던 어떤 젊은이가 나리와 아는 사이라면서, 나리가 점잖은 가장이시고, 제가 가서 알아보면 자기가 나타나겠다고 했지요."], ["The young man don’t seem inclined to keep his word, but—Oh!", "그 젊은이는 약속을 지킬 마음이 없는 듯합니다만—오!"], ["Here IS the young man!”", "마침 그 젊은이가 왔군요!\""]], [["Enter Mr. Guppy, who nods to Mr. Snagsby and touches his hat with the chivalry of clerkship to the ladies on the stairs.", "거피 씨가 들어와, 스내그스비 씨에게 고갯짓을 하고는 서기다운 정중함으로 계단 위 부인들에게 모자에 손을 갖다 댄다."]], [["“I was strolling away from the office just now when I found this row going on,” says Mr.", "\"방금 사무실에서 어슬렁어슬렁 나오다가 이 소동이 벌어지는 걸 봤지 뭡니까,\""], ["Guppy to the law-stationer, “and as your name was mentioned, I thought it was right the thing should be looked into.”", "거피 씨가 법률 문방구 주인에게 말한다. \"그런데 선생 성함이 나오기에, 이 일을 좀 알아보는 게 옳겠다 싶었지요.\""]], [["“It was very good-natured of you, sir,” says Mr.", "\"참 친절도 하시오, 선생,\""], ["Snagsby, “and I am obliged to you.”", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다. \"고맙소이다.\""], ["And Mr. Snagsby again relates his experience, again suppressing the half-crown fact.", "그러고는 스내그스비 씨가 다시 자기 사연을 들려주는데, 이번에도 반 크라운 이야기는 빼놓는다."]], [["“Now, I know where you live,”", "\"자, 난 네놈이 어디 사는지 안다,\""], ["says the constable, then, to Jo.", "순경이 그제야 조에게 말한다."], ["“You live down in Tom-all-Alone’s.", "\"톰 혼자 사는 곳에 살지."], ["That’s a nice innocent place to live in, ain’t it?”", "살기에 참 좋고 깨끗한 데로구나, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“I can’t go and live in no nicer place, sir,” replies Jo.", "\"전 더 좋고 깨끗한 데 가서 살 수가 없어요, 나리.\""], ["“They wouldn’t have nothink to say to me if I wos to go to a nice innocent place fur to live.", "조가 대답한다. \"제가 좋고 깨끗한 데 가서 살려고 해봤자 거기선 거들떠도 안 본다구요."], ["Who ud go and let a nice innocent lodging to such a reg’lar one as me!”", "누가 저 같은 진짜배기한테 좋고 깨끗한 셋방을 내주겠어요!\""]], [["“You are very poor, ain’t you?”", "\"넌 무척 가난하지, 안 그러냐?\""], ["says the constable.", "순경이 말한다."]], [["“Yes, I am indeed, sir, wery poor in gin’ral,”", "\"네, 정말 그래요, 나리, 대체로 무척 가난하죠.\""], ["replies Jo.", "조가 대답한다."], ["“I leave you to judge now!", "\"자, 그럼 직접 판단해보십쇼!"], ["I shook these two half-crowns out of him,”", "이 녀석한테 손만 한 번 댔는데,\""], ["says the constable, producing them to the company, “in only putting my hand upon him!”", "순경이 사람들에게 반 크라운 두 닢을 꺼내 보이며 말한다. \"이 반 크라운 두 닢이 떨어져 나왔지 뭡니까!\""]], [["“They’re wot’s left, Mr.", "\"그건 남은 거예요, 스내그스비 나리,\" 조가 말한다."], ["Snagsby,”", "\"어떤 마님이 저한테 준 일 파운드 금화에서 남은 거라구요."], ["says Jo, “out of a sov-ring as wos give me by a lady in a wale as sed she wos a servant and as come to my crossin one night and asked to be showd this ’ere ouse and the ouse wot him as you giv the writin to died at, and the berrin-ground wot he’s berrid in.", "너울 쓴 마님이 자기가 하녀라면서 어느 날 밤에 제 길목으로 와서는, 이 집이랑, 나리가 글 써준 그 사람이 죽은 집이랑, 그 사람이 묻힌 묘지를 좀 보여달라고 했어요. 마님이 저한테 그러는 거예요, '네가 그 심리에 나온 애냐?'"], ["She ses to me she ses ‘are you the boy at the inkwhich?’ she ses.", "하구요. 그래서 제가 '네'"], ["I ses ‘yes’ I ses.", "그랬죠. 마님이 또 저한테 그러는 거예요, '그 데를 다 보여줄 수 있겠냐?'"], ["She ses to me she ses ‘can you show me all them places?’ I ses ‘yes I can’ I ses.", "하구요. 그래서 제가 '네, 그럼요'"], ["And she ses to me ‘do it’ and I dun it and she giv me a sov’ring and hooked it.", "그랬어요. 그랬더니 마님이 저한테 '그럼 해다오'"], ["And I an’t had much of the sov’ring neither,”", "하길래, 제가 해드렸더니, 마님이 일 파운드 금화를 주고 휙 가버렸다구요. 근데 그 금화도 그닥 못 챙겼어요,\""], ["says Jo, with dirty tears, “fur I had to pay five bob, down in Tom-all-Alone’s, afore they’d square it fur to give me change, and then a young man he thieved another five while I was asleep and another boy he thieved ninepence and the landlord he stood drains round with a lot more on it.”", "조가 꾀죄죄한 눈물을 흘리며 말한다. \"톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 잔돈으로 바꿔주는 값으로 오 실링을 먼저 줘야 했고, 그담엔 제가 자는 새 어떤 젊은 놈이 또 오 실링을 슬쩍했고, 또 딴 애가 구 펜스를 슬쩍했고, 주인장은 거기에 더 보태서 술을 쫙 돌렸으니까요.\""]], [["“You don’t expect anybody to believe this, about the lady and the sovereign, do you?”", "\"이 마님이랑 금화 이야기를 누가 곧이듣기라도 할 것 같으냐, 응?\""], ["says the constable, eyeing him aside with ineffable disdain.", "순경이 이루 말할 수 없는 경멸을 담아 곁눈질로 소년을 흘겨보며 말한다."]], [["“I don’t know as I do, sir,”", "\"믿으실 거라곤 생각 안 해요, 나리.\""], ["replies Jo.", "조가 대답한다."], ["“I don’t expect nothink at all, sir, much, but that’s the true hist’ry on it.”", "\"전 아무것도, 별로 기대 안 해요, 나리. 근데 그게 진짜 있었던 일이에요.\""]], [["“You see what he is!”", "\"이 녀석 꼴 좀 보십쇼!\""], ["the constable observes to the audience.", "순경이 청중에게 한마디 한다."], ["“Well, Mr. Snagsby, if I don’t lock him up this time, will you engage for his moving on?”", "\"자, 스내그스비 나리, 제가 이번엔 이 녀석을 가두지 않는다면, 나리께서 이 녀석이 비키게 책임지시겠습니까?\""]], [["“No!” cries Mrs.", "\"안 돼요!\""], ["Snagsby from the stairs.", "계단에서 스내그스비 부인이 외친다."]], [["“My little woman!”", "\"여보, 마누라!\""], ["pleads her husband.", "남편이 사정한다."], ["“Constable, I have no doubt he’ll move on.", "\"순경 양반, 이 아이가 비킬 거라고 난 믿어 의심치 않소."], ["You know you really must do it,”", "알다시피 자넨 정말 그래야만 하네,\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I’m everyways agreeable, sir,”", "\"전 어떻게든 다 좋아요, 나리.\""], ["says the hapless Jo.", "가엾은 조가 말한다."]], [["“Do it, then,”", "\"그럼 그렇게 해라,\""], ["observes the constable.", "순경이 한마디 한다."], ["“You know what you have got to do.", "\"네가 뭘 해야 하는지 알지. 그렇게 해!"], ["Do it! And recollect you won’t get off so easy next time.", "그리고 명심해라, 다음번엔 이렇게 쉽게 넘어가진 못할 거다."], ["Catch hold of your money.", "네 돈이나 챙겨라."], ["Now, the sooner you’re five mile off, the better for all parties.”", "자, 어서 오 마일 떨어진 데로 갈수록 모두한테 좋을 거다.\""]], [["With this farewell hint and pointing generally to the setting sun as a likely place to move on to, the constable bids his auditors good afternoon and makes the echoes of Cook’s Court perform slow music for him as he walks away on the shady side, carrying his iron-bound hat in his hand for a little ventilation.", "이렇게 작별의 충고를 던지고, 비켜갈 만한 곳이라며 지는 해 쪽을 대충 가리킨 순경은, 듣고 있던 사람들에게 작별 인사를 하고는, 약간의 통풍을 위해 쇠 두른 모자를 손에 들고 그늘진 쪽으로 걸어가며, 쿡스 코트의 메아리가 자신을 위해 느린 음악을 연주하게 만든다."]], [["Now, Jo’s improbable story concerning the lady and the sovereign has awakened more or less the curiosity of all the company. Mr.", "자, 마님과 금화에 관한 조의 믿기 힘든 이야기는 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람의 호기심을 얼마간 일깨워놓았다."], ["Guppy, who has an inquiring mind in matters of evidence and who has been suffering severely from the lassitude of the long vacation, takes that interest in the case that he enters on a regular cross-examination of the witness, which is found so interesting by the ladies that Mrs.", "증거 문제에 관해서라면 캐묻기 좋아하는 두뇌를 지녔고 장기 휴정의 권태에 시달려온 거피 씨는, 이 사건에 흥미가 동한 나머지 정식으로 증인을 반대신문하기 시작하는데, 부인들이 어찌나 흥미진진해하는지 스내그스비 부인은 정중하게 거피 씨에게 위층으로 올라와 차 한 잔 들기를 청한다."], ["Snagsby politely invites him to step upstairs and drink a cup of tea, if he will excuse the disarranged state of the tea-table, consequent on their previous exertions. Mr.", "앞선 소동 탓에 차상이 어수선해진 것은 양해해달라면서 말이다."], ["Guppy yielding his assent to this proposal, Jo is requested to follow into the drawing-room doorway, where Mr.", "거피 씨가 이 제안에 동의를 표하자, 조는 응접실 문간으로 따라 들어오라는 청을 받는다."], ["Guppy takes him in hand as a witness, patting him into this shape, that shape, and the other shape like a butterman dealing with so much butter, and worrying him according to the best models.", "거기서 거피 씨는 마치 버터 장수가 버터 한 덩이를 다루듯, 조를 이 모양 저 모양 또 다른 모양으로 주물럭거리며 증인으로 붙잡고는, 더없이 훌륭한 본보기를 좇아 그를 들들 볶는다."], ["Nor is the examination unlike many such model displays, both in respect of its eliciting nothing and of its being lengthy, for Mr.", "또한 이 신문은, 아무것도 끄집어내지 못한다는 점에서나 길고 지루하다는 점에서나, 그런 본보기다운 연출과 다를 바 없다."], ["Guppy is sensible of his talent, and Mrs. Snagsby feels not only that it gratifies her inquisitive disposition, but that it lifts her husband’s establishment higher up in the law.", "거피 씨는 제 재능을 절감하고, 스내그스비 부인은 이 일이 자신의 캐묻기 좋아하는 성미를 만족시킬 뿐 아니라 남편의 가게를 법조계에서 한층 더 높이 올려준다고 느끼는 까닭이다."], ["During the progress of this keen encounter, the vessel Chadband, being merely engaged in the oil trade, gets aground and waits to be floated off.", "이 치열한 격돌이 진행되는 동안, 한낱 기름 장사에만 매여 있던 채드밴드라는 배는, 좌초한 채 다시 띄워지기를 기다린다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"음!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“Either this boy sticks to it like cobbler’s-wax or there is something out of the common here that beats anything that ever came into my way at Kenge and Carboy’s.”", "\"이 녀석이 구두장이 밀랍처럼 끝까지 고집을 부리는 거거나, 아니면 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 내가 겪어본 그 어떤 일도 능가하는 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 게 여기 있는 거로군.\""]], [["Mrs. Chadband whispers Mrs.", "채드밴드 부인이 스내그스비 부인에게 귀엣말을 하자, 스내그스비 부인이 외친다."], ["Snagsby, who exclaims, “You don’t say so!”", "\"어머, 그게 정말이에요!\""]], [["“For years!”", "\"여러 해 됐어요!\""], ["replied Mrs. Chadband.", "채드밴드 부인이 대답했다."]], [["“Has known Kenge and Carboy’s office for years,” Mrs. Snagsby triumphantly explains to Mr. Guppy. “Mrs. Chadband—this gentleman’s wife—Reverend Mr. Chadband.”", "\"켄지 앤드 카보이 사무실을 여러 해 전부터 아셨대요.\" 스내그스비 부인이 의기양양하게 거피 씨에게 설명한다. \"채드밴드 부인이세요—이분 부인이시죠—채드밴드 목사님이요.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed!”", "\"오, 그렇습니까!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Before I married my present husband,”", "\"지금 남편과 결혼하기 전 일이에요.\""], ["says Mrs. Chadband.", "채드밴드 부인이 말한다."]], [["“Was you a party in anything, ma’am?” says Mr.", "\"부인께서 무슨 사건의 당사자이셨습니까?\""], ["Guppy, transferring his cross-examination.", "거피 씨가 반대신문의 화살을 돌리며 말한다. \"아니요.\""]], [["“No.” “NOT a party in anything, ma’am?”", "\"무슨 사건의 당사자가 아니셨다고요?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."]], [["Mrs. Chadband shakes her head.", "채드밴드 부인이 고개를 젓는다."]], [["“Perhaps you were acquainted with somebody who was a party in something, ma’am?”", "\"그렇다면 혹시 무슨 사건의 당사자인 누군가를 아셨습니까?\" 거피 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr. Guppy, who likes nothing better than to model his conversation on forensic principles.", "그는 자기 대화를 법정의 원칙에 따라 본뜨는 것보다 더 좋아하는 일이 없다."]], [["“Not exactly that, either,”", "\"꼭 그런 것도 아니에요.\""], ["replies Mrs. Chadband, humouring the joke with a hard-favoured smile.", "채드밴드 부인이 무뚝뚝한 미소로 농에 장단을 맞추며 대답한다."]], [["“Not exactly that, either!”", "\"꼭 그런 것도 아니다!\""], ["repeats Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 되뇐다."], ["“Very good.", "\"아주 좋습니다."], ["Pray, ma’am, was it a lady of your acquaintance who had some transactions (we will not at present say what transactions) with Kenge and Carboy’s office, or was it a gentleman of your acquaintance?", "그럼 부인, 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무실과 어떤 거래(지금 무슨 거래인지는 말하지 않기로 합시다)를 한 사람이 부인이 아시는 어떤 여인이었습니까, 아니면 부인이 아시는 어떤 신사였습니까? 천천히 생각하십시오, 부인."], ["Take time, ma’am.", "차차 알게 될 테니까요."], ["We shall come to it presently. Man or woman, ma’am?”", "남자입니까, 여자입니까, 부인?\""]], [["“Neither,” says Mrs.", "\"둘 다 아니에요.\""], ["Chadband as before.", "채드밴드 부인이 아까처럼 말한다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["A child!” says Mr.", "아이로군요!\""], ["Guppy, throwing on the admiring Mrs. Snagsby the regular acute professional eye which is thrown on British jurymen.", "거피 씨가 영국 배심원에게 던지곤 하는 그 날카롭고 직업적인 눈길을 감탄해 마지않는 스내그스비 부인에게 던지며 말한다."], ["“Now, ma’am, perhaps you’ll have the kindness to tell us WHAT child.”", "\"자, 부인, 그럼 그게 어떤 아이였는지 친절히 일러주시겠습니까.\""]], [["“You have got it at last, sir,”", "\"드디어 맞히셨네요, 나리.\""], ["says Mrs. Chadband with another hard-favoured smile.", "채드밴드 부인이 또 한 번 무뚝뚝한 미소를 지으며 말한다."], ["“Well, sir, it was before your time, most likely, judging from your appearance.", "\"글쎄요, 나리, 보아하니 나리가 태어나기도 전 일일 거예요."], ["I was left in charge of a child named Esther Summerson, who was put out in life by Messrs. Kenge and Carboy.”", "저는 에스더 서머슨이라는 아이를 맡아 길렀는데, 그 아이를 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에서 세상에 내보냈지요.\""]], [["“Miss Summerson, ma’am!”", "\"서머슨 아가씨요, 부인!\""], ["cries Mr. Guppy, excited.", "거피 씨가 흥분하여 외친다."]], [["“I call her Esther Summerson,” says Mrs.", "\"저는 그 애를 에스더 서머슨이라 부릅니다.\""], ["Chadband with austerity.", "채드밴드 부인이 엄숙하게 말한다."], ["“There was no Miss-ing of the girl in my time.", "\"내가 데리고 있을 적엔 그 계집애한테 '아가씨'라곤 없었어요."], ["It was Esther.", "그냥 에스더였죠."], ["‘Esther, do this!", "'에스더, 이거 해라!"], ["Esther, do that!’ and she was made to do it.”", "에스더, 저거 해라!' 하면 그 애는 시키는 대로 해야 했지요.\""]], [["“My dear ma’am,” returns Mr.", "\"부인,\""], ["Guppy, moving across the small apartment, “the humble individual who now addresses you received that young lady in London when she first came here from the establishment to which you have alluded.", "거피 씨가 그 좁은 방을 가로질러 다가가며 말한다. \"지금 부인께 말씀드리는 이 보잘것없는 인간이, 방금 부인께서 언급하신 그곳에서 그 젊은 아가씨가 런던에 처음 왔을 때 그분을 맞이한 사람입니다."], ["Allow me to have the pleasure of taking you by the hand.”", "부디 제가 부인의 손을 잡는 기쁨을 누리게 해주십시오.\""]], [["Mr. Chadband, at last seeing his opportunity, makes his accustomed signal and rises with a smoking head, which he dabs with his pocket-handkerchief.", "마침내 기회를 엿본 채드밴드 씨가 늘 하던 신호를 보내며 김이 모락모락 나는 머리를 손수건으로 톡톡 두드리며 일어선다."], ["Mrs.", "스내그스비 부인이 \"쉿!\""], ["Snagsby whispers “Hush!”", "하고 속삭인다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["says Chadband, “we have partaken in moderation”", "채드밴드가 말한다."], ["(which was certainly not the case so far as he was concerned) “of the comforts which have been provided for us.", "\"우리는 우리를 위해 마련된 위안을 절제하여 들었소\"(적어도 그로 말하자면 결코 그렇지 않았지만) \"이 집이 이 땅의 기름진 것을 누리며 살기를."], ["May this house live upon the fatness of the land; may corn and wine be plentiful therein; may it grow, may it thrive, may it prosper, may it advance, may it proceed, may it press forward!", "이 집에 곡식과 포도주가 넘쳐나기를. 이 집이 자라고, 번창하고, 번영하고, 나아가고, 전진하고, 앞으로 밀고 나아가기를!"], ["But, my friends, have we partaken of anything else?", "그러나 여러분, 우리가 그 밖에 또 무엇을 들었소?"], ["We have.", "들었소이다."], ["My friends, of what else have we partaken?", "여러분, 우리가 그 밖에 또 무엇을 들었소?"], ["Of spiritual profit? Yes.", "영적인 유익이오?"], ["From whence have we derived that spiritual profit?", "그렇소. 우리는 그 영적인 유익을 어디서 얻었소?"], ["My young friend, stand forth!”", "나의 젊은 벗이여, 앞으로 나오라!\""]], [["Jo, thus apostrophized, gives a slouch backward, and another slouch forward, and another slouch to each side, and confronts the eloquent Chadband with evident doubts of his intentions.", "이렇게 불려 나온 조는 뒤로 한 번 구부정하고, 앞으로 또 한 번 구부정하고, 양옆으로 한 번씩 더 구부정하더니, 그 의도를 영 미심쩍어하는 기색으로 달변의 채드밴드와 마주 선다."]], [["“My young friend,”", "\"나의 젊은 벗이여,\""], ["says Chadband, “you are to us a pearl, you are to us a diamond, you are to us a gem, you are to us a jewel.", "채드밴드가 말한다. \"그대는 우리에게 진주요, 그대는 우리에게 다이아몬드요, 그대는 우리에게 보석이요, 그대는 우리에게 보배로다."], ["And why, my young friend?”", "그런데 어찌하여 그러한가, 나의 젊은 벗이여?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"몰라요.\""], ["replies Jo.", "조가 대답한다."], ["“I don’t know nothink.”", "\"전 아무것도 몰라요.\""]], [["“My young friend,”", "\"나의 젊은 벗이여,\""], ["says Chadband, “it is because you know nothing that you are to us a gem and jewel.", "채드밴드가 말한다. \"그대가 아무것도 모르는 까닭에 그대가 우리에게 보석이요 보배인 것이오."], ["For what are you, my young friend?", "그대는 무엇인가, 나의 젊은 벗이여?"], ["Are you a beast of the field?", "그대는 들의 짐승인가? 아니다."], ["No. A bird of the air?", "공중의 새인가?"], ["No.", "아니다."], ["A fish of the sea or river?", "바다나 강의 물고기인가? 아니다."], ["No. You are a human boy, my young friend.", "그대는 사람의 아이로다, 나의 젊은 벗이여."], ["A human boy.", "사람의 아이."], ["O glorious to be a human boy!", "오, 사람의 아이라는 것은 영광스럽도다!"], ["And why glorious, my young friend?", "그런데 어찌하여 영광스러운가, 나의 젊은 벗이여?"], ["Because you are capable of receiving the lessons of wisdom, because you are capable of profiting by this discourse which I now deliver for your good, because you are not a stick, or a staff, or a stock, or a stone, or a post, or a pillar.", "그대가 지혜의 가르침을 받을 수 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 그대의 유익을 위해 내가 지금 베푸는 이 말씀으로 이로움을 얻을 수 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 막대기도, 지팡이도, 그루터기도, 돌멩이도, 말뚝도, 기둥도 아닌 까닭이오."]], [["O running stream of sparkling joy To be a soaring human boy!", "오 반짝이는 기쁨의 흐르는 시냇물이여 드높이 솟구치는 사람의 아이가 된다는 것은!"]], [["And do you cool yourself in that stream now, my young friend?", "그래, 그대는 지금 그 시냇물에서 몸을 식히고 있는가, 나의 젊은 벗이여? 아니다."], ["No. Why do you not cool yourself in that stream now?", "어찌하여 그대는 지금 그 시냇물에서 몸을 식히지 않는가?"], ["Because you are in a state of darkness, because you are in a state of obscurity, because you are in a state of sinfulness, because you are in a state of bondage.", "그대가 어둠 속에 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 캄캄함 속에 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 죄악 속에 있는 까닭이요, 그대가 속박 속에 있는 까닭이오."], ["My young friend, what is bondage?", "나의 젊은 벗이여, 속박이란 무엇인가?"], ["Let us, in a spirit of love, inquire.”", "우리 사랑의 정신으로 물어보기로 하자.\""]], [["At this threatening stage of the discourse, Jo, who seems to have been gradually going out of his mind, smears his right arm over his face and gives a terrible yawn.", "설교가 이런 위협적인 단계에 이르자, 점점 정신이 나가는 듯하던 조가 오른팔로 얼굴을 쓱 문지르며 끔찍하게 하품을 한다."], ["Mrs. Snagsby indignantly expresses her belief that he is a limb of the arch-fiend.", "스내그스비 부인은 저 녀석이 마왕의 수족이라 믿는다고 분개하여 표명한다."]], [["“My friends,” says Mr.", "\"여러분,\""], ["Chadband with his persecuted chin folding itself into its fat smile again as he looks round, “it is right that I should be humbled, it is right that I should be tried, it is right that I should be mortified, it is right that I should be corrected.", "채드밴드 씨가 박해받는 턱을 다시 기름진 미소 속으로 접어 넣으며 둘러보고 말한다. \"내가 낮추어지는 것이 마땅하오."], ["I stumbled, on Sabbath last, when I thought with pride of my three hours’ improving.", "내가 시험받는 것이 마땅하오. 내가 욕을 보는 것이 마땅하오."], ["The account is now favourably balanced: my creditor has accepted a composition.", "내가 바로잡히는 것이 마땅하오. 나는 지난 안식일에 내 세 시간의 교화를 두고 우쭐한 마음을 품었다가 걸려 넘어졌소."], ["O let us be joyful, joyful!", "이제 그 셈은 좋게 균형이 맞춰졌으니, 내 채권자가 화해를 받아들였소. 오, 기뻐합시다, 기뻐합시다!"], ["O let us be joyful!”", "오, 기뻐합시다!\""]], [["Great sensation on the part of Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인 쪽에서 커다란 감동이 인다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["says Chadband, looking round him in conclusion, “I will not proceed with my young friend now.", "채드밴드가 마무리하며 둘러보고 말한다. \"나는 지금 나의 젊은 벗과는 더 나아가지 않으렵니다."], ["Will you come to-morrow, my young friend, and inquire of this good lady where I am to be found to deliver a discourse unto you, and will you come like the thirsty swallow upon the next day, and upon the day after that, and upon the day after that, and upon many pleasant days, to hear discourses?”", "나의 젊은 벗이여, 그대는 내일 와서 이 착한 부인께 내가 그대에게 말씀을 베풀려면 어디로 가야 하는지 여쭤보겠는가?"], ["(This with a cow-like lightness.)", "그리고 그 이튿날에도, 그다음 날에도, 또 그다음 날에도, 그리고 숱한 즐거운 날에, 목마른 제비처럼 말씀을 들으러 오겠는가?\"(이 말은 암소처럼 가뿐하게 한다.)"]], [["Jo, whose immediate object seems to be to get away on any terms, gives a shuffling nod. Mr.", "당장 어떻게든 빠져나가는 것이 목적인 듯한 조는 어물어물 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["Guppy then throws him a penny, and Mrs. Snagsby calls to Guster to see him safely out of the house.", "그러자 거피 씨가 그에게 일 페니를 던져주고, 스내그스비 부인은 거스터를 불러 이 녀석을 무사히 집 밖으로 내보내라 한다."], ["But before he goes downstairs, Mr. Snagsby loads him with some broken meats from the table, which he carries away, hugging in his arms.", "하지만 그가 아래층으로 내려가기 전에 스내그스비 씨가 식탁에서 남은 고기 부스러기를 잔뜩 안겨주고, 조는 그것을 두 팔로 끌어안고 들고 나간다."]], [["So, Mr. Chadband—of whom the persecutors say that it is no wonder he should go on for any length of time uttering such abominable nonsense, but that the wonder rather is that he should ever leave off, having once the audacity to begin—retires into private life until he invests a little capital of supper in the oil-trade.", "이리하여 채드밴드 씨는—헐뜯는 자들 말로는, 그가 그토록 가증스러운 헛소리를 한도 끝도 없이 늘어놓는 것이야 놀랄 일이 아니지만, 일단 뻔뻔하게 시작해놓고 어쩌다 그치기라도 하는 것이 오히려 놀랄 일이라는데—저녁이라는 약간의 밑천을 기름 장사에 투자할 때까지 사생활 속으로 물러난다."], ["Jo moves on, through the long vacation, down to Blackfriars Bridge, where he finds a baking stony corner wherein to settle to his repast.", "조는 장기 휴정을 가로질러 비키고 또 비켜, 블랙프라이어스 다리까지 내려가서는, 자리 잡고 한 끼 들 만한 뜨끈하게 달궈진 돌 모퉁이를 찾아낸다."]], [["And there he sits, munching and gnawing, and looking up at the great cross on the summit of St. Paul’s Cathedral, glittering above a red-and-violet-tinted cloud of smoke.", "그리고 거기 앉아, 우물우물 씹고 잘근잘근 뜯으며, 붉고 보랏빛으로 물든 연기 구름 위로 반짝이는 세인트 폴 대성당 꼭대기의 거대한 십자가를 올려다본다."], ["From the boy’s face one might suppose that sacred emblem to be, in his eyes, the crowning confusion of the great, confused city—so golden, so high up, so far out of his reach.", "소년의 얼굴을 보면 그 거룩한 상징이 그의 눈에는, 이 거대하고 어수선한 도시에서 으뜸가는 어수선함—그토록 금빛 찬란하고, 그토록 높이 솟았고, 그토록 손이 닿지 않는 것—으로 비치는가 보다. 거기 그가 앉아 있다."], ["There he sits, the sun going down, the river running fast, the crowd flowing by him in two streams—everything moving on to some purpose and to one end—until he is stirred up and told to “move on” too.", "해는 저물고, 강물은 빠르게 흐르고, 군중은 두 갈래 물줄기로 그의 곁을 흘러간다—저마다 어떤 목적을 향해, 하나의 끝을 향해 모두가 비켜 나아간다—마침내 그도 누군가에게 떠밀려 \"비키라\"는 말을 들을 때까지."]], [["A New Lodger\nThe long vacation saunters on towards term-time like an idle river very leisurely strolling down a flat country to the sea.", "장기 휴정기가 개정기를 향해 어슬렁어슬렁 흘러간다. 마치 한가로운 강물이 평평한 들판을 느릿느릿 거닐며 바다로 흘러가듯이."], ["Mr. Guppy saunters along with it congenially.", "구피 씨는 그 흐름과 마음 맞게 어슬렁거린다."], ["He has blunted the blade of his penknife and broken the point off by sticking that instrument into his desk in every direction.", "그는 주머니칼 날을 무디게 만들었고, 그 칼을 책상에 사방으로 푹푹 찔러대다 끝을 부러뜨렸다."], ["Not that he bears the desk any ill will, but he must do something, and it must be something of an unexciting nature, which will lay neither his physical nor his intellectual energies under too heavy contribution.", "책상에 무슨 원한이 있어서가 아니라, 무엇이든 해야 하기 때문이고, 그 무엇이라는 것이 육체의 기력도 지적인 기력도 너무 무겁게 끌어다 쓰지 않는, 시시한 종류여야 하기 때문이다."], ["He finds that nothing agrees with him so well as to make little gyrations on one leg of his stool, and stab his desk, and gape.", "그는 등받이 없는 의자의 다리 하나로 빙글빙글 도는 것, 책상을 푹푹 찌르는 것, 그리고 하품하는 것만큼 자기에게 잘 맞는 일이 없다고 깨닫는다."]], [["Kenge and Carboy are out of town, and the articled clerk has taken out a shooting license and gone down to his father’s, and Mr.", "켄지와 카보이는 시내를 떠나 있고, 견습 사무원은 수렵 면허를 따서 아버지 댁으로 내려갔으며, 구피 씨의 동료 봉급생활자 두 명도 휴가를 떠나 부재중이다."], ["Guppy’s two fellow-stipendiaries are away on leave. Mr.", "구피 씨와 리처드 카스톤 씨가 사무실의 위엄을 나눠 가진다."], ["Guppy and Mr. Richard Carstone divide the dignity of the office.", "그런데 카스톤 씨가 당분간 켄지의 방에 자리를 잡는 바람에 구피 씨는 속이 부글부글 끓는다."], ["But Mr. Carstone is for the time being established in Kenge’s room, whereat Mr.", "어찌나 끓는지, 올드 스트리트 로드에서 어머니와 바닷가재와 상추로 저녁을 먹는 은밀한 시간이면 그는 신랄한 빈정거림으로 어머니에게 알린다."], ["Guppy chafes. So exceedingly that he with biting sarcasm informs his mother, in the confidential moments when he sups with her off a lobster and lettuce in the Old Street Road, that he is afraid the office is hardly good enough for swells, and that if he had known there was a swell coming, he would have got it painted.", "사무실이 멋쟁이 나리한테 어울리기엔 영 변변찮은 게 걱정이라느니, 멋쟁이가 온다는 걸 미리 알았더라면 페인트칠이라도 해뒀을 거라느니 하고."]], [["Mr. Guppy suspects everybody who enters on the occupation of a stool in Kenge and Carboy’s office of entertaining, as a matter of course, sinister designs upon him.", "구피 씨는 켄지와 카보이 사무실에서 의자 하나를 차지하고 들어앉는 사람이라면 누구든, 당연한 일이라는 듯 자기에게 음흉한 속셈을 품고 있으리라고 의심한다."], ["He is clear that every such person wants to depose him.", "그런 자들은 하나같이 자기를 끌어내리려 든다고 굳게 믿는다."], ["If he be ever asked how, why, when, or wherefore, he shuts up one eye and shakes his head.", "어떻게, 왜, 언제, 무슨 까닭으로 그러느냐고 물으면 그는 한쪽 눈을 감고 고개를 절레절레 흔든다."], ["On the strength of these profound views, he in the most ingenious manner takes infinite pains to counterplot when there is no plot, and plays the deepest games of chess without any adversary.", "이 심오한 견해를 밑천 삼아, 그는 음모가 없는데도 더없이 교묘한 방식으로 무한한 공을 들여 맞불 음모를 꾸미고, 상대도 없는 가장 심오한 장기판을 펼친다."]], [["It is a source of much gratification to Mr. Guppy, therefore, to find the new-comer constantly poring over the papers in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, for he well knows that nothing but confusion and failure can come of that.", "그러니 새로 온 사람이 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 서류를 끊임없이 들여다보는 모습은 구피 씨에게 크나큰 만족의 원천이 된다."], ["His satisfaction communicates itself to a third saunterer through the long vacation in Kenge and Carboy’s office, to wit, Young Smallweed.", "그런 짓에서 나올 것이라곤 혼란과 실패뿐임을 그는 잘 알기 때문이다. 그의 만족은 켄지와 카보이 사무실에서 장기 휴정기를 어슬렁대는 또 한 사람, 즉 어린 스몰위드에게로 옮겨간다."]], [["Whether Young Smallweed (metaphorically called Small and eke Chick Weed, as it were jocularly to express a fledgling) was ever a boy is much doubted in Lincoln’s Inn.", "어린 스몰위드가(은유적으로 스몰이라고 불리고, 또 햇병아리를 익살스레 표현하듯 칙위드라고도 불린다) 과연 소년이었던 적이 있는지는 링컨 법학원에서 크게 의심받는 바다."], ["He is now something under fifteen and an old limb of the law.", "그는 지금 열다섯 살이 채 안 되었으나 법률계의 노련한 고참이다."], ["He is facetiously understood to entertain a passion for a lady at a cigar-shop in the neighbourhood of Chancery Lane and for her sake to have broken off a contract with another lady, to whom he had been engaged some years.", "그가 챈서리 레인 근처 시가 가게의 어느 아가씨에게 연정을 품고 있고, 그녀를 위해 몇 해 전부터 약혼한 사이였던 다른 아가씨와의 약속을 깨버렸다는 것이 우스개처럼 통용된다."], ["He is a town-made article, of small stature and weazen features, but may be perceived from a considerable distance by means of his very tall hat.", "그는 도시에서 빚어진 물건으로, 키가 작고 이목구비가 쪼글쪼글하지만, 유난히 키 큰 모자 덕분에 상당히 먼 거리에서도 알아볼 수 있다."], ["To become a Guppy is the object of his ambition.", "구피가 되는 것이 그의 야망의 목표다."], ["He dresses at that gentleman (by whom he is patronized), talks at him, walks at him, founds himself entirely on him. He is honoured with Mr.", "그는 그 신사(자기를 후원해주는)를 본떠 옷을 입고, 그를 본떠 말하고, 그를 본떠 걷고, 자기 전부를 온전히 그 사람 위에 세운다."], ["Guppy’s particular confidence and occasionally advises him, from the deep wells of his experience, on difficult points in private life.", "그는 구피 씨의 각별한 신임을 받는 영광을 누리며, 이따금 자기 경험의 깊은 샘에서 길어 올린 지혜로 사생활의 까다로운 대목에 관해 그에게 조언한다."]], [["Mr. Guppy has been lolling out of window all the morning after trying all the stools in succession and finding none of them easy, and after several times putting his head into the iron safe with a notion of cooling it. Mr. Smallweed has been twice dispatched for effervescent drinks, and has twice mixed them in the two official tumblers and stirred them up with the ruler. Mr. Guppy propounds for Mr. Smallweed’s consideration the paradox that the more you drink the thirstier you are and reclines his head upon the window-sill in a state of hopeless languor.", "구피 씨는 의자란 의자를 차례로 다 앉아보고도 편한 것이 하나도 없자, 또 머리를 식혀볼 생각으로 몇 번이나 철제 금고에 머리를 들이밀어보고도, 아침 내내 창밖으로 몸을 늘어뜨린 채 빈둥거렸다. 스몰위드 씨는 거품 나는 음료를 가지러 두 번 심부름을 다녀왔고, 두 번 다 사무실용 텀블러 두 잔에 그것을 타서 자로 휘저었다. 구피 씨는 마실수록 더 목이 마르다는 역설을 스몰위드 씨에게 깊이 생각해보라고 내놓고는, 가망 없는 나른함에 빠져 창턱에 머리를 기댄다."]], [["While thus looking out into the shade of Old Square, Lincoln’s Inn, surveying the intolerable bricks and mortar, Mr. Guppy becomes conscious of a manly whisker emerging from the cloistered walk below and turning itself up in the direction of his face.", "그렇게 링컨 법학원 올드 스퀘어의 그늘을 내다보며 견딜 수 없이 지긋지긋한 벽돌과 회반죽을 둘러보다가, 구피 씨는 아래쪽 회랑 보도에서 사내다운 구레나룻 한 쌍이 불쑥 솟아올라 자기 얼굴 쪽으로 치켜드는 것을 알아챈다."], ["At the same time, a low whistle is wafted through the Inn and a suppressed voice cries, “Hip!", "그와 동시에 나직한 휘파람이 법학원 안으로 흘러들고, 억눌린 목소리가 외친다. \"어이!"], ["Gup-py!”", "구-피!\""]], [["“Why, you don’t mean it!”", "\"이런, 설마!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, aroused.", "구피 씨가 정신이 번쩍 들어 말한다."], ["“Small!", "\"스몰!"], ["Here’s Jobling!”", "조블링이 왔어!\""], ["Small’s head looks out of window too and nods to Jobling.", "스몰의 머리도 창밖으로 나와 조블링에게 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“Where have you sprung up from?”", "\"자넨 어디서 불쑥 솟아난 거야?\""], ["inquires Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 묻는다."]], [["“From the market-gardens down by Deptford.", "\"뎁퍼드 아래쪽 채소밭에서."], ["I can’t stand it any longer.", "더는 못 견디겠어."], ["I must enlist.", "입대해야겠어."], ["I say!", "이봐!"], ["I wish you’d lend me half a crown.", "반 크라운만 좀 빌려주면 좋겠는데."], ["Upon my soul, I’m hungry.”", "정말이지, 배가 고파.\""]], [["Jobling looks hungry and also has the appearance of having run to seed in the market-gardens down by Deptford.", "조블링은 굶주린 행색이고, 게다가 뎁퍼드 아래쪽 채소밭에서 시들어 씨가 다 빠져버린 듯한 모습이다."]], [["“I say!", "\"이봐!"], ["Just throw out half a crown if you have got one to spare.", "여유가 있으면 반 크라운만 던져줘."], ["I want to get some dinner.”", "저녁이라도 좀 먹게.\""]], [["“Will you come and dine with me?” says Mr.", "\"나랑 같이 저녁 먹지 않을래?\""], ["Guppy, throwing out the coin, which Mr. Jobling catches neatly.", "구피 씨가 동전을 던지며 말하고, 조블링 씨는 그것을 깔끔하게 받아챈다."]], [["“How long should I have to hold out?”", "\"얼마나 버텨야 하는데?\""], ["says Jobling.", "조블링이 말한다."]], [["“Not half an hour.", "\"삼십 분도 안 걸려."], ["I am only waiting here till the enemy goes,”", "적이 떠날 때까지 여기서 기다리는 중이거든.\""], ["returns Mr. Guppy, butting inward with his head.", "구피 씨가 머리로 안쪽을 가리키며 대답한다."]], [["“What enemy?”", "\"무슨 적?\""]], [["“A new one.", "\"새로 온 적."], ["Going to be articled.", "견습 들어올 작자."], ["Will you wait?”", "기다릴 텐가?\""]], [["“Can you give a fellow anything to read in the meantime?”", "\"그동안 읽을 거라도 좀 줄 수 있어?\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."]], [["Smallweed suggests the law list.", "스몰위드가 법률 명부를 권한다."], ["But Mr. Jobling declares with much earnestness that he “can’t stand it.”", "그러나 조블링 씨는 자못 진지하게 그건 \"도저히 못 견디겠다\"고 단언한다."]], [["“You shall have the paper,”", "\"신문을 줄게.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 말한다."], ["“He shall bring it down.", "\"얘가 내려다 줄 거야."], ["But you had better not be seen about here.", "그래도 여기서 어슬렁대는 걸 들키지 않는 게 좋겠지."], ["Sit on our staircase and read.", "우리 계단에 앉아서 읽어."], ["It’s a quiet place.”", "조용한 데니까.\""]], [["Jobling nods intelligence and acquiescence.", "조블링은 알아들었다는 듯, 또 그러겠다는 듯 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["The sagacious Smallweed supplies him with the newspaper and occasionally drops his eye upon him from the landing as a precaution against his becoming disgusted with waiting and making an untimely departure.", "영리한 스몰위드는 그에게 신문을 갖다주고, 그가 기다리다 진력이 나서 때 이른 발걸음을 옮기지 않도록 미리 막느라 이따금 층계참에서 그에게 눈길을 떨군다."], ["At last the enemy retreats, and then Smallweed fetches Mr. Jobling up.", "마침내 적이 물러가자, 스몰위드는 조블링 씨를 위층으로 데려온다."]], [["“Well, and how are you?”", "\"그래, 어떻게 지냈나?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, shaking hands with him.", "구피 씨가 그와 악수하며 말한다."]], [["“So, so.", "\"그저 그렇지."], ["How are you?”", "자넨 어때?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy replying that he is not much to boast of, Mr.", "구피 씨가 자랑할 만한 형편은 못 된다고 대답하자, 조블링 씨가 슬쩍 한마디를 던진다."], ["Jobling ventures on the question, “How is SHE?”", "\"그분은 어떠셔?\" 이를 구피 씨는 무례한 참견으로 여겨 받아친다."], ["This Mr. Guppy resents as a liberty, retorting, “Jobling, there ARE chords in the human mind—”", "\"조블링, 인간의 마음에는 건드려선 안 될 현이라는 게 있는 법이야―\""], ["Jobling begs pardon.", "조블링이 사과한다."]], [["“Any subject but that!”", "\"그 얘기만 빼고 뭐든!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy with a gloomy enjoyment of his injury.", "구피 씨가 자기 상처를 음울하게 즐기며 말한다."], ["“For there ARE chords, Jobling—”", "\"현이라는 게 있단 말일세, 조블링―\""]], [["Mr. Jobling begs pardon again.", "조블링 씨가 다시 사과한다."]], [["During this short colloquy, the active Smallweed, who is of the dinner party, has written in legal characters on a slip of paper, “Return immediately.”", "이 짧은 입씨름이 오가는 동안, 저녁 자리에 끼어든 부지런한 스몰위드는 종잇조각에 법률 문서체로 \"즉시 돌아옴\"이라고 적어두었다."], ["This notification to all whom it may concern, he inserts in the letter-box, and then putting on the tall hat at the angle of inclination at which Mr. Guppy wears his, informs his patron that they may now make themselves scarce.", "그는 관계자 모두에게 알리는 이 통지문을 우편함에 끼워넣고는, 구피 씨가 쓰는 각도 그대로 키 큰 모자를 비스듬히 눌러쓰고 후원자에게 이제 슬그머니 자리를 떠도 좋다고 알린다."]], [["Accordingly they betake themselves to a neighbouring dining-house, of the class known among its frequenters by the denomination slap-bang, where the waitress, a bouncing young female of forty, is supposed to have made some impression on the susceptible Smallweed, of whom it may be remarked that he is a weird changeling to whom years are nothing. He stands precociously possessed of centuries of owlish wisdom. If he ever lay in a cradle, it seems as if he must have lain there in a tail-coat. He has an old, old eye, has Smallweed; and he drinks and smokes in a monkeyish way; and his neck is stiff in his collar; and he is never to be taken in; and he knows all about it, whatever it is. In short, in his bringing up he has been so nursed by Law and Equity that he has become a kind of fossil imp, to account for whose terrestrial existence it is reported at the public offices that his father was John Doe and his mother the only female member of the Roe family, also that his first long-clothes were made from a blue bag.", "그리하여 그들은 근처의 식당으로 향한다. 단골들 사이에서 후딱식당이라는 이름으로 통하는 부류의 가게로, 거기서 통통하게 잘 자란 마흔 살 먹은 젊은 여종업원이 감수성 예민한 스몰위드에게 모종의 인상을 남긴 것으로 짐작되는데, 이 스몰위드로 말하자면 세월이라는 것이 아무 의미도 없는 기이한 바꿔친 아이라 할 만하다. 그는 조숙하게도 수백 년에 걸친 부엉이 같은 지혜를 갖춘 채 서 있다. 요람에 누운 적이 있다 한들, 거기 누울 때도 연미복 차림이었을 것만 같다. 스몰위드는 늙고 늙은 눈을 가졌다. 그리고 원숭이처럼 마시고 피운다. 목은 옷깃 속에서 빳빳하다. 결코 속아 넘어가는 법이 없다. 그것이 무엇이든 간에, 그 모든 것을 다 안다. 한마디로, 그는 자라는 동안 법과 형평법의 손에 어찌나 길러졌는지, 일종의 화석이 된 도깨비가 되어버렸다. 그가 이 땅에 존재하게 된 내력을 설명하느라 관청가에서는 그의 아버지가 존 도이고 어머니는 로 일가의 유일한 여성 식구였다는 소문이 돌고, 또 그의 첫 배냇저고리가 파란 서류 가방으로 지어졌다는 소문도 돈다. * 존 도와 로: 영국 법률에서 신원 미상의 가상 당사자를 가리키는 통칭. 도깨비 같은 출생 내력을 법률 용어로 빗댄 농담이다."]], [["Into the dining-house, unaffected by the seductive show in the window of artificially whitened cauliflowers and poultry, verdant baskets of peas, coolly blooming cucumbers, and joints ready for the spit, Mr. Smallweed leads the way.", "진열창에 인위적으로 하얗게 만든 콜리플라워와 가금류, 푸릇푸릇한 완두콩 바구니, 시원하게 윤기 도는 오이, 꼬챙이에 꿸 채비를 마친 고깃덩이가 사람을 홀리듯 늘어서 있지만, 스몰위드 씨는 거기에 조금도 흔들리지 않고 식당 안으로 앞장서 들어간다."], ["They know him there and defer to him.", "거기 사람들은 그를 알아보고 떠받든다."], ["He has his favourite box, he bespeaks all the papers, he is down upon bald patriarchs, who keep them more than ten minutes afterwards.", "그에게는 단골 칸막이 자리가 있고, 그는 신문이란 신문을 다 차지하며, 그것을 십 분 넘게 붙들고 있는 대머리 노인네들에게 매섭게 군다. 제대로 부푼 \"빵\""], ["It is of no use trying him with anything less than a full-sized “bread” or proposing to him any joint in cut unless it is in the very best cut.", "한 덩이가 아니고서는 그에게 무엇을 내밀어도 소용없고, 부위 또한 최상품이 아니고서는 어떤 고기를 권해도 받지 않는다."], ["In the matter of gravy he is adamant.", "그레이비에 관해서는 그는 한 치도 양보가 없다."]], [["Conscious of his elfin power and submitting to his dread experience, Mr.", "구피 씨는 그 요정 같은 위력을 의식하고 그 무시무시한 경륜에 머리를 조아리며 그날의 만찬을 고르는 일에 그의 의견을 구한다."], ["Guppy consults him in the choice of that day’s banquet, turning an appealing look towards him as the waitress repeats the catalogue of viands and saying “What do YOU take, Chick?”", "여종업원이 음식 목록을 줄줄이 읊는 동안 그에게 매달리는 눈길을 돌리며 묻는다."], ["Chick, out of the profundity of his artfulness, preferring “veal and ham and French beans—and don’t you forget the stuffing, Polly”", "\"자넨 뭘 들겠나, 칙?\" 칙은 능청맞은 깊은 속에서 \"송아지 고기에 햄에 강낭콩―그리고 속도 잊으면 안 돼, 폴리\""], ["(with an unearthly cock of his venerable eye), Mr. Guppy and Mr.", "하고(점잖은 눈을 으스스하게 한쪽 치뜨며) 정하니, 구피 씨와 조블링 씨도 똑같이 주문한다."], ["Jobling give the like order. Three pint pots of half-and-half are superadded.", "거기에 반반 섞은 맥주 한 파인트짜리 단지 셋이 더 보태진다."], ["Quickly the waitress returns bearing what is apparently a model of the Tower of Babel but what is really a pile of plates and flat tin dish-covers.", "곧 여종업원이 겉보기엔 바벨탑 모형 같지만 실은 접시와 납작한 양철 뚜껑을 잔뜩 쌓아 올린 것을 들고 돌아온다."], ["Mr. Smallweed, approving of what is set before him, conveys intelligent benignity into his ancient eye and winks upon her.", "스몰위드 씨는 자기 앞에 놓인 것을 흡족해하며 그 고색창연한 눈에 영민한 자애로움을 담아 그녀에게 윙크한다."], ["Then, amid a constant coming in, and going out, and running about, and a clatter of crockery, and a rumbling up and down of the machine which brings the nice cuts from the kitchen, and a shrill crying for more nice cuts down the speaking-pipe, and a shrill reckoning of the cost of nice cuts that have been disposed of, and a general flush and steam of hot joints, cut and uncut, and a considerably heated atmosphere in which the soiled knives and tablecloths seem to break out spontaneously into eruptions of grease and blotches of beer, the legal triumvirate appease their appetites.", "그러자, 끊임없이 들어오고 나가고 이리저리 뛰어다니는 가운데, 그릇 부딪치는 소리가 요란하고, 부엌에서 좋은 부위를 실어 나르는 기계가 위아래로 덜컹대며 오르내리고, 전성관 아래로 좋은 부위를 더 달라고 날카롭게 외치는 소리와 이미 처분된 좋은 부위의 값을 날카롭게 셈하는 소리가 들리며, 자른 것과 자르지 않은 뜨거운 고깃덩이의 김과 열기가 사방에 자욱하고, 상당히 후끈 달아오른 공기 속에서 더러워진 칼과 식탁보가 절로 기름덩어리와 맥주 얼룩을 발진처럼 터뜨려내는 듯한, 그런 한복판에서 이 법률계의 삼두정치는 식욕을 달랜다."]], [["Mr. Jobling is buttoned up closer than mere adornment might require.", "조블링 씨는 단순한 멋내기에 필요한 정도보다 더 단단히 단추를 채우고 있다."], ["His hat presents at the rims a peculiar appearance of a glistening nature, as if it had been a favourite snail-promenade.", "그의 모자는 챙 가장자리에 윤기 나는 묘한 빛을 띠는데, 마치 거기가 달팽이들의 단골 산책로라도 되었던 것 같다."], ["The same phenomenon is visible on some parts of his coat, and particularly at the seams.", "같은 현상이 그의 외투 군데군데에서도, 특히 솔기에서 두드러지게 보인다."], ["He has the faded appearance of a gentleman in embarrassed circumstances; even his light whiskers droop with something of a shabby air.", "그는 곤궁한 처지에 빠진 신사처럼 빛바랜 행색이다. 옅은 빛깔의 구레나룻마저 어딘가 초라한 기운을 띤 채 축 늘어져 있다."]], [["His appetite is so vigorous that it suggests spare living for some little time back.", "그의 식욕이 어찌나 왕성한지, 한동안 굶주린 살림을 해왔음을 짐작케 한다."], ["He makes such a speedy end of his plate of veal and ham, bringing it to a close while his companions are yet midway in theirs, that Mr.", "그는 일행이 아직 자기 접시의 절반밖에 못 비웠을 때 송아지 고기와 햄 접시를 후딱 해치워버려, 구피 씨가 한 접시 더 들라고 권한다."], ["Guppy proposes another.", "\"고맙네, 구피.\""], ["“Thank you, Guppy,” says Mr.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."], ["Jobling, “I really don’t know but what I WILL take another.”", "\"정말이지 한 접시 더 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\""]], [["Another being brought, he falls to with great goodwill.", "한 접시가 더 나오자 그는 매우 흔쾌히 달려든다."]], [["Mr. Guppy takes silent notice of him at intervals until he is half way through this second plate and stops to take an enjoying pull at his pint pot of half-and-half (also renewed) and stretches out his legs and rubs his hands.", "구피 씨는 이따금 그를 말없이 지켜본다."], ["Beholding him in which glow of contentment, Mr.", "마침내 그가 이 두 번째 접시를 절반쯤 비우고는 잠시 멈춰 반반 섞은 맥주(이것도 새로 채운 것이다) 단지를 흐뭇하게 들이켜고 다리를 쭉 뻗으며 두 손을 비빌 때까지."], ["Guppy says, “You are a man again, Tony!”", "그렇게 흡족함으로 발그레해진 그를 바라보며 구피 씨가 말한다. \"다시 사람이 됐군, 토니!\""]], [["“Well, not quite yet,”", "\"글쎄, 아직 완전히는 아냐.\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."], ["“Say, just born.”", "\"이제 막 태어난 셈이지.\""]], [["“Will you take any other vegetables?", "\"다른 채소도 좀 들겠나? 풀?"], ["Grass? Peas?", "완두콩?"], ["Summer cabbage?”", "여름배추?\""]], [["“Thank you, Guppy,”", "\"고맙네, 구피.\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."], ["“I really don’t know but what I WILL take summer cabbage.”", "\"정말이지 여름배추를 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\""]], [["Order given; with the sarcastic addition (from Mr.", "주문이 들어간다. 거기에 스몰위드 씨의 빈정대는 한마디가 덧붙는다."], ["Smallweed) of “Without slugs, Polly!”", "\"민달팽이는 빼고, 폴리!\""], ["And cabbage produced.", "그러자 배추가 나온다."]], [["“I am growing up, Guppy,” says Mr.", "\"점점 자라는 중이야, 구피.\""], ["Jobling, plying his knife and fork with a relishing steadiness.", "조블링 씨가 맛깔스럽게 한결같이 칼과 포크를 놀리며 말한다."]], [["“Glad to hear it.”", "\"그거 반갑군.\""]], [["“In fact, I have just turned into my teens,”", "\"실은 이제 막 십 대로 접어들었거든.\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."]], [["He says no more until he has performed his task, which he achieves as Messrs.", "그는 자기 몫을 다 해치울 때까지 더는 말이 없다."], ["Guppy and Smallweed finish theirs, thus getting over the ground in excellent style and beating those two gentlemen easily by a veal and ham and a cabbage.", "그 일을 구피 씨와 스몰위드 씨가 끝마칠 무렵에 완수하니, 멋들어진 솜씨로 거리를 따라잡아 송아지 고기와 햄 한 접시에 배추 한 접시 차이로 두 신사를 거뜬히 앞지른다."]], [["“Now, Small,” says Mr.", "\"자, 스몰.\""], ["Guppy, “what would you recommend about pastry?”", "구피 씨가 말한다. \"후식은 뭘 권하겠나?\""]], [["“Marrow puddings,”", "\"골수 푸딩이죠.\""], ["says Mr. Smallweed instantly.", "스몰위드 씨가 즉각 말한다."]], [["“Aye, aye!” cries Mr.", "\"옳거니, 옳거니!\""], ["Jobling with an arch look.", "조블링 씨가 짓궂은 표정으로 외친다."], ["“You’re there, are you?", "\"거기 있었군, 그래?"], ["Thank you, Mr. Guppy, I don’t know but what I WILL take a marrow pudding.”", "고맙네, 구피 씨, 골수 푸딩을 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\""]], [["Three marrow puddings being produced, Mr. Jobling adds in a pleasant humour that he is coming of age fast.", "골수 푸딩 셋이 나오자, 조블링 씨는 유쾌한 기분으로 자기가 빠르게 성년이 되어간다고 덧붙인다."], ["To these succeed, by command of Mr. Smallweed, “three Cheshires,”", "거기에 이어 스몰위드 씨의 분부로 \"체셔 치즈 셋\"이, 또 거기에 이어 \"작은 럼주 셋\"이 나온다."], ["and to those “three small rums.” This apex of the entertainment happily reached, Mr.", "이 만찬의 정점에 흐뭇하게 다다르자, 조블링 씨는(칸막이 자리 한쪽을 혼자 독차지하고 있던 터라) 양탄자 깔린 좌석에 다리를 올려놓고 벽에 기대어 말한다."], ["Jobling puts up his legs on the carpeted seat (having his own side of the box to himself), leans against the wall, and says, “I am grown up now, Guppy.", "\"이제 다 컸어, 구피."], ["I have arrived at maturity.”", "무르익은 나이에 이르렀지.\""]], [["“What do you think, now,” says Mr.", "\"이제 어떻게 생각하나,\""], ["Guppy, “about—you don’t mind Smallweed?”", "구피 씨가 말한다. \"그러니까―스몰위드는 괜찮지?\""]], [["“Not the least in the world.", "\"전혀 상관없어."], ["I have the pleasure of drinking his good health.”", "이 친구의 건강을 위해 기꺼이 한잔 들지.\""]], [["“Sir, to you!”", "\"감사합니다, 선생님!\""], ["says Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I was saying, what do you think NOW,”", "\"내 말은, 이제 어떻게 생각하느냐는 거야,\""], ["pursues Mr.", "구피 씨가 잇는다."], ["Guppy, “of enlisting?”", "\"입대 말일세?\""]], [["“Why, what I may think after dinner,”", "\"글쎄, 저녁을 먹고 나서 드는 생각하고,\""], ["returns Mr.", "조블링 씨가 대답한다."], ["Jobling, “is one thing, my dear Guppy, and what I may think before dinner is another thing.", "\"저녁을 먹기 전에 드는 생각은 또 다른 법이지, 친애하는 구피. 그래도 저녁을 먹고 나서까지도 나는 자문하게 돼."], ["Still, even after dinner, I ask myself the question, What am I to do?", "난 뭘 해야 하나? 어떻게 살아야 하나?"], ["How am I to live?", "일 포 망제, 알잖아.\""], ["Ill fo manger, you know,”", "조블링 씨는 그 말을 마치 영국 마구간에 꼭 필요한 비품이라도 가리키는 양 발음한다."], ["says Mr.", "\"일 포 망제."], ["Jobling, pronouncing that word as if he meant a necessary fixture in an English stable.", "그게 프랑스 속담이거든. 그리고 망제하는 건 프랑스 사람한테나 나한테나 똑같이 꼭 필요한 일이야."], ["“Ill fo manger.", "어쩌면 더하지.\""], ["That’s the French saying, and mangering is as necessary to me as it is to a Frenchman.", "* 일 포 망제: 프랑스어 \"Il faut manger(먹어야 한다)\"를 멋대로 영어식으로 발음한 것."], ["Or more so.”", "'manger'를 영국 마구간의 여물통(manger)으로 빗댄 말장난이다."]], [["Mr. Smallweed is decidedly of opinion “much more so.”", "스몰위드 씨는 단연 \"훨씬 더하다\"는 의견이다."]], [["“If any man had told me,”", "\"누가 나한테 이런 말을 했더라면,\""], ["pursues Jobling, “even so lately as when you and I had the frisk down in Lincolnshire, Guppy, and drove over to see that house at Castle Wold—”", "조블링이 잇는다. \"자네하고 내가 링컨셔로 신나게 놀러 가서, 마차를 몰고 캐슬 월드에 있는 그 저택을 보러 갔을 때만 해도 말이야, 구피―\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed corrects him—Chesney Wold.", "스몰위드 씨가 바로잡는다―체스니 월드."]], [["“Chesney Wold.", "\"체스니 월드."], ["(I thank my honourable friend for that cheer.) If any man had told me then that I should be as hard up at the present time as I literally find myself, I should have—well, I should have pitched into him,” says Mr.", "(그렇게 고쳐준 내 명예로운 벗에게 감사하네.) 그때 누가 나한테, 내가 지금 처한 이 형편처럼 말 그대로 쪼들리게 되리라고 말했더라면, 나는―글쎄, 나는 그자한테 달려들었을 거야.\""], ["Jobling, taking a little rum-and-water with an air of desperate resignation; “I should have let fly at his head.”", "조블링 씨가 자포자기한 듯 체념한 기색으로 럼주에 물을 탄 것을 조금 들이켜며 말한다. \"그자 머리통을 향해 한 방 날렸을 거라고.\""]], [["“Still, Tony, you were on the wrong side of the post then,”", "\"그래도 그때 자넨 잘못된 쪽에 있었어, 토니.\""], ["remonstrates Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 타이른다."], ["“You were talking about nothing else in the gig.”", "\"마차에서 내내 그 얘기만 했잖나.\""]], [["“Guppy,”", "\"구피,\""], ["says Mr. Jobling, “I will not deny it.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."], ["I was on the wrong side of the post.", "\"부정하진 않겠네. 나는 잘못된 쪽에 있었어."], ["But I trusted to things coming round.”", "하지만 일이 잘 풀리리라고 믿었거든.\""]], [["That very popular trust in flat things coming round!", "만사가 절로 잘 풀리리라는 그 흔해 빠진 믿음이라니!"], ["Not in their being beaten round, or worked round, but in their “coming”", "두들겨서 풀리거나 애써서 풀리는 게 아니라, 저절로 \"풀려\" 온다는 믿음이라니!"], ["round! As though a lunatic should trust in the world’s “coming”", "마치 미치광이가 세상이 저절로 \"삼각형이 되어\""], ["triangular!", "오리라고 믿기라도 하듯이!"]], [["“I had confident expectations that things would come round and be all square,” says Mr.", "\"일이 잘 풀려서 다 맞아떨어지리라고 자신만만하게 기대했지.\""], ["Jobling with some vagueness of expression and perhaps of meaning too.", "조블링 씨가 표현이, 어쩌면 의미까지도 다소 흐릿하게 말한다."], ["“But I was disappointed.", "\"그런데 실망하고 말았어."], ["They never did.", "영영 풀리지 않았거든."], ["And when it came to creditors making rows at the office and to people that the office dealt with making complaints about dirty trifles of borrowed money, why there was an end of that connexion.", "그러다 채권자들이 사무실에서 소란을 피우고, 사무실이 거래하던 사람들이 빌린 돈이니 하는 지저분한 자질구레한 일로 항의해대기에 이르자, 뭐, 그 인연도 그걸로 끝장이었지."], ["And of any new professional connexion too, for if I was to give a reference to-morrow, it would be mentioned and would sew me up.", "게다가 새로운 직업상의 인연도 마찬가지야. 내일 누가 신원보증을 부탁해온다 해도 그 일이 거론될 테고, 그러면 난 끝장이거든."], ["Then what’s a fellow to do?", "그러니 사내가 뭘 어쩌겠나?"], ["I have been keeping out of the way and living cheap down about the market-gardens, but what’s the use of living cheap when you have got no money?", "그동안 채소밭 근처에 숨어서 싸게 먹고 살았지만, 돈이 한 푼도 없는데 싸게 사는 게 무슨 소용이야?"], ["You might as well live dear.”", "차라리 비싸게 사는 거나 마찬가지지.\""]], [["“Better,” Mr.", "\"그게 낫지.\""], ["Smallweed thinks.", "스몰위드 씨가 생각한다."]], [["“Certainly.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["It’s the fashionable way; and fashion and whiskers have been my weaknesses, and I don’t care who knows it,”", "그게 유행하는 방식이거든. 그런데 유행하고 구레나룻이 내 약점이라네."], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "누가 알든 난 상관 안 해.\""], ["“They are great weaknesses—Damme, sir, they are great.", "조블링 씨가 말한다. \"그것들은 대단한 약점이야―제기랄, 선생, 정말 대단하다고."], ["Well,” proceeds Mr.", "자,\""], ["Jobling after a defiant visit to his rum-and-water, “what can a fellow do, I ask you, BUT enlist?”", "조블링 씨가 럼주에 물을 탄 것을 도전적으로 한 번 더 들이켜고 나서 잇는다. \"묻겠는데, 사내가 입대 말고 뭘 할 수 있겠나?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy comes more fully into the conversation to state what, in his opinion, a fellow can do.", "구피 씨가 대화에 더 깊이 끼어들어, 자기 생각에 사내가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 밝힌다."], ["His manner is the gravely impressive manner of a man who has not committed himself in life otherwise than as he has become the victim of a tender sorrow of the heart.", "그의 태도는, 마음에 깃든 애틋한 슬픔의 희생양이 되었다는 것 말고는 인생에서 달리 자기 발목을 잡힐 짓을 한 적 없는 사내의, 진중하고 인상적인 태도다."]], [["“Jobling,” says Mr.", "\"조블링,\""], ["Guppy, “myself and our mutual friend Smallweed—”", "구피 씨가 말한다. \"나하고 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드는―\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed modestly observes, “Gentlemen both!”", "스몰위드 씨가 겸손하게 한마디 한다."], ["and drinks.", "\"두 분 다 신사시죠!\" 그러고는 마신다."]], [["“—Have had a little conversation on this matter more than once since you—”", "\"―자네가 그렇게 된 뒤로 이 문제를 두고 한두 번 짧게 이야기를 나눴거든―\""]], [["“Say, got the sack!”", "\"잘렸다고 말해!\""], ["cries Mr. Jobling bitterly.", "조블링 씨가 쓰라리게 외친다."], ["“Say it, Guppy.", "\"그렇게 말하라고, 구피."], ["You mean it.”", "그 뜻이잖나.\""]], [["“No-o-o!", "\"아니, 아냐!"], ["Left the Inn,” Mr.", "법학원을 떠났다고요.\""], ["Smallweed delicately suggests.", "스몰위드 씨가 점잖게 거든다."]], [["“Since you left the Inn, Jobling,” says Mr.", "\"자네가 법학원을 떠난 뒤로, 조블링,\""], ["Guppy; “and I have mentioned to our mutual friend Smallweed a plan I have lately thought of proposing.", "구피 씨가 말한다. \"나는 최근에 한번 제안해볼까 생각해둔 계획을 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드에게 말해뒀네."], ["You know Snagsby the stationer?”", "문방구상 스내그스비를 알지?\""]], [["“I know there is such a stationer,”", "\"그런 문방구상이 있다는 건 알지.\""], ["returns Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 대답한다."], ["“He was not ours, and I am not acquainted with him.”", "\"우리 거래처는 아니었어. 나랑 안면은 없네.\""]], [["“He IS ours, Jobling, and I AM acquainted with him,” Mr.", "\"우리 거래처가 맞고, 나는 그 사람하고 안면이 있다네, 조블링.\""], ["Guppy retorts. “Well, sir!", "구피 씨가 받아친다."], ["I have lately become better acquainted with him through some accidental circumstances that have made me a visitor of his in private life.", "\"그런데 말일세! 어쩌다 우연한 일들로 그 사람 사생활에까지 드나드는 손님이 되는 바람에, 최근에 더 잘 알게 됐거든."], ["Those circumstances it is not necessary to offer in argument.", "그 정황은 굳이 늘어놓을 필요 없어."], ["They may—or they may not—have some reference to a subject which may—or may not—have cast its shadow on my existence.”", "어쩌면 내 인생에 그림자를 드리웠을지도―아닐지도―모르는 어떤 문제와 관련이 있을지도―아닐지도―모르지만 말일세.\""]], [["As it is Mr. Guppy’s perplexing way with boastful misery to tempt his particular friends into this subject, and the moment they touch it, to turn on them with that trenchant severity about the chords in the human mind, both Mr. Jobling and Mr. Smallweed decline the pitfall by remaining silent.", "자랑스러운 비애에 잠겨 자기 절친한 벗들을 이 화제로 끌어들였다가, 그들이 그 화제를 건드리는 순간 인간의 마음에 깃든 현이 어쩌고 하는 신랄한 준엄함으로 돌변해 그들에게 들이대는 것이 구피 씨의 곤혹스러운 버릇인지라, 조블링 씨도 스몰위드 씨도 입을 다문 채 그 함정을 피한다."]], [["“Such things may be,” repeats Mr.", "\"그런 일들이 있을 수도,\""], ["Guppy, “or they may not be.", "구피 씨가 되뇐다."], ["They are no part of the case.", "\"없을 수도 있지."], ["It is enough to mention that both Mr.", "그건 이 일과 아무 상관 없어."], ["and Mrs. Snagsby are very willing to oblige me and that Snagsby has, in busy times, a good deal of copying work to give out.", "스내그스비 씨도 그 부인도 기꺼이 나를 도와주려 한다는 점, 그리고 스내그스비가 바쁜 철이면 내줄 필사 일거리가 꽤 많다는 점만 말해두면 충분해."], ["He has all Tulkinghorn’s, and an excellent business besides.", "토킹혼 일을 죄다 맡고 있는 데다, 그 밖에도 장사가 아주 잘되거든."], ["I believe if our mutual friend Smallweed were put into the box, he could prove this?”", "우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드를 증인석에 세우면, 이걸 입증해줄 수 있을 거야. 안 그래?\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed nods and appears greedy to be sworn.", "스몰위드 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 어서 선서하고 싶어 안달하는 듯 보인다."]], [["“Now, gentlemen of the jury,”", "\"자, 배심원 여러분,\" 구피 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr. Guppy, “—I mean, now, Jobling—you may say this is a poor prospect of a living.", "\"―아니, 그러니까, 조블링―자넨 이게 변변찮은 밥벌이 전망이라고 할지도 모르지."], ["Granted.", "인정해."], ["But it’s better than nothing, and better than enlistment.", "하지만 아무것도 안 하는 것보단 낫고, 입대보다도 낫네."], ["You want time.", "자넨 시간이 필요해."], ["There must be time for these late affairs to blow over.", "이 최근의 일들이 가라앉을 시간이 있어야 한단 말이야."], ["You might live through it on much worse terms than by writing for Snagsby.”", "스내그스비를 위해 필사하는 것보다 훨씬 못한 조건으로도 그 시간을 버텨낼 수 있을 텐데.\""]], [["Mr. Jobling is about to interrupt when the sagacious Smallweed checks him with a dry cough and the words, “Hem!", "조블링 씨가 막 말을 끊으려 하는데, 영리한 스몰위드가 마른기침과 함께 한마디로 그를 제지한다."], ["Shakspeare!”", "\"흠! 셰익스피어!\""]], [["“There are two branches to this subject, Jobling,”", "\"이 문제엔 두 가지 갈래가 있어, 조블링.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 말한다."], ["“That is the first.", "\"그게 첫째지."], ["I come to the second.", "둘째로 넘어가겠네."], ["You know Krook, the Chancellor, across the lane.", "골목 건너편의 챈슬러, 크룩을 알지."], ["Come, Jobling,” says Mr.", "자, 조블링,\""], ["Guppy in his encouraging cross-examination-tone, “I think you know Krook, the Chancellor, across the lane?”", "구피 씨가 격려하는 반대신문 어조로 말한다. \"골목 건너편의 챈슬러, 크룩을 알 텐데?\""]], [["“I know him by sight,”", "\"얼굴은 알지.\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."]], [["“You know him by sight.", "\"얼굴은 안다."], ["Very well.", "아주 좋아."], ["And you know little Flite?”", "그럼 꼬마 플라이트도 알지?\""]], [["“Everybody knows her,”", "\"그 여자야 다들 알지.\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Everybody knows her.", "\"다들 안다."], ["VERY well.", "아주 좋아."], ["Now it has been one of my duties of late to pay Flite a certain weekly allowance, deducting from it the amount of her weekly rent, which I have paid (in consequence of instructions I have received) to Krook himself, regularly in her presence.", "그런데 요즘 내 임무 중 하나가 플라이트에게 매주 일정액의 수당을 주는 일이거든. 거기서 매주 집세를 떼는데, 그 집세는(내가 받은 지시에 따라) 어김없이 그녀가 보는 앞에서 크룩 본인에게 직접 치르지."], ["This has brought me into communication with Krook and into a knowledge of his house and his habits.", "그러다 보니 크룩과 왕래하게 됐고, 그 집과 그자의 습성도 알게 됐어."], ["I know he has a room to let.", "그가 세놓을 방이 하나 있다는 걸 알아."], ["You may live there at a very low charge under any name you like, as quietly as if you were a hundred miles off.", "거기서라면 자넨 아주 싼 값에, 무슨 이름을 대든, 백 마일은 떨어진 데 있는 것처럼 조용히 지낼 수 있어."], ["He’ll ask no questions and would accept you as a tenant at a word from me—before the clock strikes, if you chose.", "그자는 아무것도 캐묻지 않을 테고, 내 말 한마디면 자넬 세입자로 받아줄 거야―자네가 원하면 시계가 종을 치기도 전에 말이지."], ["And I tell you another thing, Jobling,” says Mr.", "그리고 한 가지 더 말해두겠는데, 조블링,\""], ["Guppy, who has suddenly lowered his voice and become familiar again, “he’s an extraordinary old chap—always rummaging among a litter of papers and grubbing away at teaching himself to read and write, without getting on a bit, as it seems to me.", "구피 씨가 갑자기 목소리를 낮추고 다시 허물없는 투가 되어 말한다. \"그자는 별난 늙은이야―늘 어수선한 종이 더미를 뒤지고, 읽고 쓰는 법을 혼자 익히겠다고 죽어라 파고드는데, 내가 보기엔 영 진척이 없거든."], ["He is a most extraordinary old chap, sir.", "정말이지 별난 늙은이야, 선생."], ["I don’t know but what it might be worth a fellow’s while to look him up a bit.”", "사내라면 그자를 좀 파고들어볼 만하지 않을까 싶기도 하다고.\""]], [["“You don’t mean—”", "\"설마 자네 말은―\""], ["Mr. Jobling begins.", "조블링 씨가 운을 뗀다."]], [["“I mean,” returns Mr.", "\"내 말은,\""], ["Guppy, shrugging his shoulders with becoming modesty, “that I can’t make him out.", "구피 씨가 어울리는 겸손으로 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답한다. \"그자를 도무지 알아낼 수가 없다는 거야."], ["I appeal to our mutual friend Smallweed whether he has or has not heard me remark that I can’t make him out.”", "내가 그자를 알아낼 수 없다고 말하는 걸 들었는지 못 들었는지, 우리 공동의 벗 스몰위드에게 물어보겠네.\""]], [["Mr.", "스몰위드 씨가 간결한 증언을 내놓는다."], ["Smallweed bears the concise testimony, “A few!”", "\"몇 번!\""]], [["“I have seen something of the profession and something of life, Tony,”", "\"나도 이 직업을 좀 겪었고 인생도 좀 겪어봤어, 토니.\" 구피 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr. Guppy, “and it’s seldom I can’t make a man out, more or less.", "\"그래서 사람을 어느 정도 못 알아보는 일이 좀처럼 없거든."], ["But such an old card as this, so deep, so sly, and secret (though I don’t believe he is ever sober), I never came across.", "그런데 이런 늙은 능구렁이는, 이렇게 속이 깊고, 음흉하고, 비밀스러운(하긴 그자가 멀쩡하게 깨어 있던 적이 있을 것 같진 않지만) 작자는, 여태 만나본 적이 없어. 자, 그자는 분명 어마어마하게 늙었어, 알겠나."], ["Now, he must be precious old, you know, and he has not a soul about him, and he is reported to be immensely rich; and whether he is a smuggler, or a receiver, or an unlicensed pawnbroker, or a money-lender—all of which I have thought likely at different times—it might pay you to knock up a sort of knowledge of him.", "곁에 사람 하나 없고, 엄청난 부자라는 소문도 있지. 그자가 밀수꾼인지, 장물아비인지, 무허가 전당포 주인인지, 고리대금업자인지―시기마다 다 그럴싸하다 싶었네만―여하튼 그자에 관한 지식을 쌓아두면 자네한테 득이 될지도 몰라."], ["I don’t see why you shouldn’t go in for it, when everything else suits.”", "다른 게 다 들어맞는 마당에, 한번 해보지 않을 이유가 없잖아.\""]], [["Mr. Jobling, Mr. Guppy, and Mr. Smallweed all lean their elbows on the table and their chins upon their hands, and look at the ceiling. After a time, they all drink, slowly lean back, put their hands in their pockets, and look at one another.", "조블링 씨, 구피 씨, 스몰위드 씨가 모두 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 턱을 받친 채 천장을 올려다본다. 잠시 후 그들은 모두 천천히 술을 마시고, 등을 뒤로 기대고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣고는 서로를 바라본다."]], [["“If I had the energy I once possessed, Tony!”", "\"한때 가졌던 그 기력이 나한테 남아 있다면 말이야, 토니!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy with a sigh.", "구피 씨가 한숨을 쉬며 말한다."], ["“But there are chords in the human mind—”", "\"하지만 인간의 마음에는 건드려선 안 될 현이라는 게―\""]], [["Expressing the remainder of the desolate sentiment in rum-and-water, Mr.", "그 처량한 심정의 나머지를 럼주에 물 탄 것으로 토로하고 나서, 구피 씨는 이 모험을 토니 조블링에게 넘기는 것으로 결말을 짓는다."], ["Guppy concludes by resigning the adventure to Tony Jobling and informing him that during the vacation and while things are slack, his purse, “as far as three or four or even five pound goes,”", "그러면서 휴정기 동안 일이 한가할 때는 자기 지갑이 \"삼 파운드든 사 파운드든, 심지어 오 파운드까지\" 그가 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 열려 있으리라고 알린다."], ["will be at his disposal.", "\"그도 그럴 것이, 윌리엄 구피가 제 벗에게 등을 돌렸다는 말이,\""], ["“For never shall it be said,”", "구피 씨가 힘주어 덧붙인다."], ["Mr. Guppy adds with emphasis, “that William Guppy turned his back upon his friend!”", "\"결코 나와선 안 되니까!\""]], [["The latter part of the proposal is so directly to the purpose that Mr.", "제안의 뒷부분이 어찌나 곧장 핵심을 찌르는지, 조블링 씨가 감격에 겨워 말한다."], ["Jobling says with emotion, “Guppy, my trump, your fist!” Mr.", "\"구피, 이 멋진 친구야, 주먹 한번 내밀어보게!\""], ["Guppy presents it, saying, “Jobling, my boy, there it is!”", "구피 씨가 주먹을 내밀며 말한다. \"조블링, 이 친구야, 여기 있네!\""], ["Mr.", "조블링 씨가 화답한다."], ["Jobling returns, “Guppy, we have been pals now for some years!” Mr.", "\"구피, 우리가 이제 알고 지낸 지도 여러 해네!\""], ["Guppy replies, “Jobling, we have.”", "구피 씨가 대꾸한다. \"조블링, 그렇지.\""]], [["They then shake hands, and Mr.", "그러고 나서 둘은 악수하고, 조블링 씨가 감개무량한 투로 덧붙인다."], ["Jobling adds in a feeling manner, “Thank you, Guppy, I don’t know but what I WILL take another glass for old acquaintance sake.”", "\"고맙네, 구피, 옛정을 봐서 한 잔 더 안 들고 못 배기겠는걸.\""]], [["“Krook’s last lodger died there,”", "\"크룩의 지난번 하숙인은 거기서 죽었지.\""], ["observes Mr. Guppy in an incidental way.", "구피 씨가 지나가는 말처럼 일러둔다."]], [["“Did he though!”", "\"정말 그랬다고!\""], ["says Mr. Jobling.", "조블링 씨가 말한다."]], [["“There was a verdict.", "\"평결이 났어."], ["Accidental death.", "사고사라고."], ["You don’t mind that?”", "자넨 그런 거 안 꺼리지?\""]], [["“No,” says Mr.", "\"안 꺼려.\""], ["Jobling, “I don’t mind it; but he might as well have died somewhere else.", "조블링 씨가 말한다. \"꺼리진 않네만, 그자도 다른 데 가서 죽었으면 좋았잖아."], ["It’s devilish odd that he need go and die at MY place!”", "굳이 내 방에 와서 죽어야 한다니 지독하게 께름칙하군!\""], ["Mr. Jobling quite resents this liberty, several times returning to it with such remarks as, “There are places enough to die in, I should think!”", "조블링 씨는 이 무례를 자못 못마땅해하며, 여러 번 그 얘기로 되돌아가 이런 말을 던진다."], ["or, “He wouldn’t have liked my dying at HIS place, I dare say!”", "\"죽을 데야 차고 넘칠 텐데, 내 참!\"이라느니, \"내가 자기 방에서 죽으면 그자도 안 좋아했을걸, 장담하지!\"라느니 하고."]], [["However, the compact being virtually made, Mr. Guppy proposes to dispatch the trusty Smallweed to ascertain if Mr.", "그래도 약정이 사실상 맺어진 터라, 구피 씨는 미덥은 스몰위드를 보내 크룩 씨가 집에 있는지 알아보자고 제안한다."], ["Krook is at home, as in that case they may complete the negotiation without delay. Mr.", "그렇다면 지체 없이 흥정을 마무리 지을 수 있을 테니까."], ["Jobling approving, Smallweed puts himself under the tall hat and conveys it out of the dining-rooms in the Guppy manner. He soon returns with the intelligence that Mr.", "조블링 씨가 찬성하자, 스몰위드는 키 큰 모자 아래에 자기를 욱여넣고 구피식으로 그것을 식당 밖으로 모셔 나간다."], ["Krook is at home and that he has seen him through the shop-door, sitting in the back premises, sleeping “like one o’clock.”", "그는 곧 크룩 씨가 집에 있다는 소식과 함께, 가게 문 너머로 안쪽 방에 앉아 \"세상모르고\" 자고 있는 그를 봤다고 알리며 돌아온다."]], [["“Then I’ll pay,” says Mr.", "\"그럼 내가 셈을 치르지.\""], ["Guppy, “and we’ll go and see him.", "구피 씨가 말한다. \"가서 그 사람을 만나보세."], ["Small, what will it be?”", "스몰, 얼마지?\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed, compelling the attendance of the waitress with one hitch of his eyelash, instantly replies as follows: “Four veals and hams is three, and four potatoes is three and four, and one summer cabbage is three and six, and three marrows is four and six, and six breads is five, and three Cheshires is five and three, and four half-pints of half-and-half is six and three, and four small rums is eight and three, and three Pollys is eight and six.", "스몰위드 씨가 속눈썹을 한 번 까딱여 여종업원을 불러 세우고는 즉시 이렇게 대답한다."], ["Eight and six in half a sovereign, Polly, and eighteenpence out!”", "\"송아지 고기와 햄 넷이 삼 실링, 감자 넷이 삼 실링 사 펜스, 여름배추 하나가 삼 실링 육 펜스, 골수 푸딩 셋이 사 실링 육 펜스, 빵 여섯이 오 실링, 체셔 치즈 셋이 오 실링 삼 펜스, 반반 섞은 맥주 반 파인트 넷이 육 실링 삼 펜스, 작은 럼주 넷이 팔 실링 삼 펜스, 폴리 셋이 팔 실링 육 펜스. 반 소버린에서 팔 실링 육 펜스 제하면, 폴리, 일 실링 육 펜스 거슬러주면 돼!\""]], [["Not at all excited by these stupendous calculations, Smallweed dismisses his friends with a cool nod and remains behind to take a little admiring notice of Polly, as opportunity may serve, and to read the daily papers, which are so very large in proportion to himself, shorn of his hat, that when he holds up the Times to run his eye over the columns, he seems to have retired for the night and to have disappeared under the bedclothes.", "이 어마어마한 계산에도 조금도 들뜨지 않은 채, 스몰위드는 쌀쌀맞게 고개를 끄덕여 벗들을 배웅하고는, 기회가 닿는 대로 폴리를 흠모하며 지켜보고 일간지를 읽으려고 뒤에 남는다. 모자를 벗고 나니 신문이 그의 몸집에 비해 어찌나 큰지, 그가 타임스를 치켜들고 지면을 훑을 때면 마치 밤을 맞아 잠자리에 들어 이불 밑으로 사라져버린 사람처럼 보인다."]], [["Mr. Guppy and Mr.", "구피 씨와 조블링 씨는 넝마와 병을 파는 가게로 향하는데, 거기서 그들은 크룩이 여전히 세상모르고 자고 있는 것을 발견한다."], ["Jobling repair to the rag and bottle shop, where they find Krook still sleeping like one o’clock, that is to say, breathing stertorously with his chin upon his breast and quite insensible to any external sounds or even to gentle shaking.", "다시 말해, 턱을 가슴에 묻은 채 드르렁드르렁 코를 골며, 바깥소리는 물론 살살 흔들어도 도무지 깨어나지 않는 상태로 말이다."], ["On the table beside him, among the usual lumber, stand an empty gin-bottle and a glass.", "그의 곁 탁자 위에는 늘 그렇듯 잡동사니 틈에 빈 진 병과 잔 하나가 놓여 있다."], ["The unwholesome air is so stained with this liquor that even the green eyes of the cat upon her shelf, as they open and shut and glimmer on the visitors, look drunk.", "건강에 해로운 공기가 그 술 냄새에 어찌나 절어 있는지, 선반 위 고양이의 초록 눈마저 떴다 감았다 하며 방문객들을 향해 번뜩일 때 술에 취한 듯 보인다."]], [["“Hold up here!” says Mr.", "\"여보쇼, 정신 차려요!\""], ["Guppy, giving the relaxed figure of the old man another shake.", "구피 씨가 늘어진 노인의 몸을 다시 한번 흔들며 말한다."], ["“Mr. Krook!", "\"크룩 씨!"], ["Halloa, sir!”", "이봐요, 선생!\""]], [["But it would seem as easy to wake a bundle of old clothes with a spirituous heat smouldering in it.", "그러나 술기운이 안에서 후끈 타오르는 헌 옷 뭉치를 깨우는 편이 차라리 쉬워 보인다."], ["“Did you ever see such a stupor as he falls into, between drink and sleep?”", "\"술이랑 잠 사이에서 이렇게 곯아떨어지는 작자를 본 적 있나?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 말한다."]], [["“If this is his regular sleep,”", "\"이게 이 사람 평소 잠버릇이라면,\""], ["returns Jobling, rather alarmed, “it’ll last a long time one of these days, I am thinking.”", "조블링이 다소 겁먹은 채 대답한다. \"조만간 어느 날엔 영영 안 깨고 오래도록 잘 것 같은데.\""]], [["“It’s always more like a fit than a nap,” says Mr.", "\"매번 한숨 자는 것보다는 발작에 가까워.\""], ["Guppy, shaking him again. “Halloa, your lordship!", "구피 씨가 그를 다시 흔들며 말한다."], ["Why, he might be robbed fifty times over!", "\"이봐요, 각하! 이러다 쉰 번은 털리겠소!"], ["Open your eyes!”", "눈 좀 떠봐요!\""]], [["After much ado, he opens them, but without appearing to see his visitors or any other objects.", "한참 애를 쓴 끝에 그가 눈을 뜨지만, 방문객들도 다른 어떤 것도 보는 기색이 없다."], ["Though he crosses one leg on another, and folds his hands, and several times closes and opens his parched lips, he seems to all intents and purposes as insensible as before.", "그는 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽에 포개고, 두 손을 맞잡고, 메마른 입술을 여러 번 다물었다 벌렸다 하면서도, 누가 봐도 아까나 다름없이 의식이 없어 보인다."]], [["“He is alive, at any rate,”", "\"어쨌든 살아는 있군.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 말한다."], ["“How are you, my Lord Chancellor.", "\"어떻게 지내십니까, 우리 대법관 각하."], ["I have brought a friend of mine, sir, on a little matter of business.”", "작은 볼일이 있어 제 친구를 하나 데려왔습니다, 선생.\""]], [["The old man still sits, often smacking his dry lips without the least consciousness.", "노인은 여전히 앉은 채, 아무 의식도 없이 마른 입술을 자주 쩝쩝거린다."], ["After some minutes he makes an attempt to rise.", "몇 분이 지나자 그는 일어서보려 시도한다."], ["They help him up, and he staggers against the wall and stares at them.", "그들이 부축해 일으키니, 그는 벽에 휘청 기대며 그들을 멀거니 바라본다."]], [["“How do you do, Mr. Krook?”", "\"안녕하십니까, 크룩 씨?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy in some discomfiture.", "구피 씨가 다소 당황하며 말한다."], ["“How do you do, sir?", "\"안녕하세요, 선생?"], ["You are looking charming, Mr. Krook.", "안색이 아주 좋아 보이십니다, 크룩 씨."], ["I hope you are pretty well?”", "그럭저럭 평안하시기를 바랍니다만?\""]], [["The old man, in aiming a purposeless blow at Mr. Guppy, or at nothing, feebly swings himself round and comes with his face against the wall.", "노인은 구피 씨를, 아니 허공을 향해 목적 없는 주먹을 휘두르려다, 맥없이 제 몸을 빙 돌려 얼굴부터 벽에 부딪친다."], ["So he remains for a minute or two, heaped up against it, and then staggers down the shop to the front door.", "그렇게 벽에 쌓이듯 기댄 채로 한두 분 가만히 있다가, 비틀비틀 가게를 가로질러 정문으로 걸어 내려간다."], ["The air, the movement in the court, the lapse of time, or the combination of these things recovers him.", "바깥 공기, 마당의 움직임, 흐르는 시간, 아니면 이것들이 한데 어우러진 덕에 그가 정신을 차린다."], ["He comes back pretty steadily, adjusting his fur cap on his head and looking keenly at them.", "그는 머리에 모피 모자를 고쳐 쓰고 그들을 날카롭게 쳐다보며 제법 차분하게 돌아온다."]], [["“Your servant, gentlemen; I’ve been dozing.", "\"잘들 오셨소, 신사 양반들. 깜빡 졸았구먼."], ["Hi!", "어허!"], ["I am hard to wake, odd times.”", "가끔 어찌나 깨어나기 힘든지.\""]], [["“Rather so, indeed, sir,”", "\"정말 그러신가 봅니다, 선생.\""], ["responds Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 응한다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐요?"], ["You’ve been a-trying to do it, have you?”", "댁들이 날 깨우려고 애를 썼다, 그거요?\""], ["says the suspicious Krook.", "의심 많은 크룩이 말한다."]], [["“Only a little,”", "\"조금요.\""], ["Mr. Guppy explains.", "구피 씨가 둘러댄다."]], [["The old man’s eye resting on the empty bottle, he takes it up, examines it, and slowly tilts it upside down.", "노인의 눈이 빈 병에 가닿자, 그는 그것을 집어 들어 살펴보고는 천천히 거꾸로 기울인다."]], [["“I say!”", "\"아니!\""], ["he cries like the hobgoblin in the story.", "그가 옛이야기에 나오는 도깨비처럼 외친다."], ["“Somebody’s been making free here!”", "\"누가 여기 손을 댔구먼!\""]], [["“I assure you we found it so,”", "\"맹세컨대 저희가 왔을 때 이미 그랬습니다.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 말한다."], ["“Would you allow me to get it filled for you?”", "\"제가 채워다 드려도 될까요?\""]], [["“Yes, certainly I would!”", "\"그럼, 물론 좋고말고!\""], ["cries Krook in high glee.", "크룩이 크게 기뻐하며 외친다."], ["“Certainly I would!", "\"물론이지!"], ["Don’t mention it!", "사양할 거 없네!"], ["Get it filled next door—Sol’s Arms—the Lord Chancellor’s fourteenpenny.", "옆집에서 채워 와―솔스 암스 말이야―대법관표 십사 펜스짜리로."], ["Bless you, they know ME!”", "어이쿠, 거긴 날 다 안다고!\""]], [["He so presses the empty bottle upon Mr. Guppy that that gentleman, with a nod to his friend, accepts the trust and hurries out and hurries in again with the bottle filled.", "그가 어찌나 빈 병을 구피 씨에게 떠안기는지, 그 신사는 친구에게 고개를 한 번 끄덕여 보이고는 그 임무를 받아들여, 서둘러 나갔다가 병을 채워서 서둘러 다시 들어온다."], ["The old man receives it in his arms like a beloved grandchild and pats it tenderly.", "노인은 그것을 마치 사랑하는 손주라도 되는 양 두 팔로 받아 안고 다정하게 토닥인다."]], [["“But, I say,”", "\"그런데 말이야,\""], ["he whispers, with his eyes screwed up, after tasting it, “this ain’t the Lord Chancellor’s fourteenpenny.", "그가 한 모금 맛을 보고 나서, 눈을 잔뜩 찡그린 채 속삭인다. \"이건 대법관표 십사 펜스짜리가 아니구먼."], ["This is eighteenpenny!”", "십팔 펜스짜리야!\""]], [["“I thought you might like that better,”", "\"그게 더 입에 맞으실 것 같아서요.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "구피 씨가 말한다."]], [["“You’re a nobleman, sir,”", "\"댁은 귀족이구먼, 선생.\""], ["returns Krook with another taste, and his hot breath seems to come towards them like a flame.", "크룩이 한 모금 더 맛보며 대답하자, 그의 뜨거운 입김이 불꽃처럼 그들 쪽으로 끼쳐오는 듯하다."], ["“You’re a baron of the land.”", "\"댁은 이 땅의 남작이야.\""]], [["Taking advantage of this auspicious moment, Mr. Guppy presents his friend under the impromptu name of Mr.", "이 상서로운 순간을 틈타, 구피 씨는 즉석에서 위블 씨라는 이름으로 친구를 소개하고 방문 목적을 밝힌다."], ["Weevle and states the object of their visit. Krook, with his bottle under his arm (he never gets beyond a certain point of either drunkenness or sobriety), takes time to survey his proposed lodger and seems to approve of him.", "크룩은 병을 겨드랑이에 낀 채(그는 취하는 정도든 멀쩡한 정도든 결코 일정한 선을 넘지 못한다) 시간을 들여 세입자가 될 사람을 살펴보고는 그가 마음에 드는 모양이다."], ["“You’d like to see the room, young man?”", "\"방을 보고 싶나, 젊은이?\""], ["he says. “Ah!", "그가 말한다."], ["It’s a good room!", "\"아! 좋은 방이지!"], ["Been whitewashed.", "회칠도 했어."], ["Been cleaned down with soft soap and soda.", "물비누에 소다까지 써서 말끔히 닦아냈고."], ["Hi!", "어허!"], ["It’s worth twice the rent, letting alone my company when you want it and such a cat to keep the mice away.”", "집세의 곱절은 값어치를 하지. 게다가 필요할 때 말동무가 되어줄 나도 있고, 쥐를 쫓아줄 저런 고양이도 있는데 말이야.\""]], [["Commending the room after this manner, the old man takes them upstairs, where indeed they do find it cleaner than it used to be and also containing some old articles of furniture which he has dug up from his inexhaustible stores. The terms are easily concluded—for the Lord Chancellor cannot be hard on Mr. Guppy, associated as he is with Kenge and Carboy, Jarndyce and Jarndyce, and other famous claims on his professional consideration—and it is agreed that Mr. Weevle shall take possession on the morrow. Mr. Weevle and Mr. Guppy then repair to Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, where the personal introduction of the former to Mr. Snagsby is effected and (more important) the vote and interest of Mrs. Snagsby are secured. They then report progress to the eminent Smallweed, waiting at the office in his tall hat for that purpose, and separate, Mr. Guppy explaining that he would terminate his little entertainment by standing treat at the play but that there are chords in the human mind which would render it a hollow mockery.", "이런 식으로 방을 추켜세우며 노인은 그들을 위층으로 데려가는데, 과연 거기서 그들은 방이 예전보다 깨끗해진 것은 물론, 그가 무궁무진한 창고에서 캐낸 낡은 가구 몇 점이 들어차 있는 것을 발견한다. 조건은 수월하게 합의된다―대법관으로서도 켄지와 카보이, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스, 그 밖에 제 직업적 고려를 요하는 숱한 유명한 청구들과 얽힌 구피 씨에게 모질게 굴 수는 없는 노릇이니―그리하여 위블 씨가 이튿날 입주하기로 정해진다. 그러고 나서 위블 씨와 구피 씨는 커시터 스트리트의 쿡스 코트로 향하는데, 거기서 전자가 스내그스비 씨에게 직접 소개되고, (더 중요하게는) 스내그스비 부인의 찬동과 호의를 확보한다. 이어 그들은 이 일을 위해 사무실에서 키 큰 모자를 쓴 채 기다리던 거물 스몰위드에게 진행 상황을 보고하고 헤어진다. 구피 씨는 이 작은 잔치를 연극 한 편 한턱냄으로써 마무리하고 싶지만 인간의 마음에 깃든 현이 그것을 공허한 흉내로 만들어버릴 것이라고 설명하면서."]], [["On the morrow, in the dusk of evening, Mr. Weevle modestly appears at Krook’s, by no means incommoded with luggage, and establishes himself in his new lodging, where the two eyes in the shutters stare at him in his sleep, as if they were full of wonder.", "이튿날 저물녘 어스름 속에, 위블 씨는 짐이라곤 거의 거추장스러울 것 없이 단출하게 크룩의 집에 점잖이 모습을 드러내고는 새 하숙에 자리를 잡는데, 거기서는 덧창에 난 두 개의 눈이 마치 경이로 가득 찬 양 잠든 그를 빤히 쳐다본다."], ["On the following day Mr.", "그 이튿날, 손재주 좋고 변변찮은 젊은이인 위블 씨는 플라이트 양에게 바늘과 실을, 집주인에게 망치를 빌려와 일에 착수한다."], ["Weevle, who is a handy good-for-nothing kind of young fellow, borrows a needle and thread of Miss Flite and a hammer of his landlord and goes to work devising apologies for window-curtains, and knocking up apologies for shelves, and hanging up his two teacups, milkpot, and crockery sundries on a pennyworth of little hooks, like a shipwrecked sailor making the best of it.", "창 커튼인 척하는 것을 궁리해 만들고, 선반인 척하는 것을 뚝딱 두들겨 세우고, 찻잔 두 개와 우유 단지, 자질구레한 그릇붙이를 한 푼어치 작은 고리에 걸어두는 품이, 난파한 선원이 형편껏 살림을 꾸리는 모양새다."]], [["But what Mr.", "그러나 위블 씨가 몇 안 되는 소유물 가운데 그 무엇보다 애지중지하는 것은(옅은 빛깔의 구레나룻 다음으로, 그것에 대해서만큼은 사내의 가슴에 오직 구레나룻만이 불러일으킬 수 있는 애착을 품고 있다) 진정 국민적인 작품인 『앨비언의 여신들, 또는 영국 미인 은하 화랑』에서 골라 모은 동판화 인쇄물이다."], ["Weevle prizes most of all his few possessions (next after his light whiskers, for which he has an attachment that only whiskers can awaken in the breast of man) is a choice collection of copper-plate impressions from that truly national work The Divinities of Albion, or Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty, representing ladies of title and fashion in every variety of smirk that art, combined with capital, is capable of producing.", "자본과 결합한 기예가 빚어낼 수 있는 온갖 갖가지 알랑거리는 미소를 띤, 작위와 유행을 갖춘 귀부인들을 담아낸 작품이다."], ["With these magnificent portraits, unworthily confined in a band-box during his seclusion among the market-gardens, he decorates his apartment; and as the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty wears every variety of fancy dress, plays every variety of musical instrument, fondles every variety of dog, ogles every variety of prospect, and is backed up by every variety of flower-pot and balustrade, the result is very imposing.", "채소밭에 칩거하는 동안 종이 상자에 누추하게 갇혀 있던 이 으리으리한 초상화들로 그는 제 방을 장식한다. 그리고 영국 미인 은하 화랑은 온갖 갖가지 화려한 드레스를 걸치고, 온갖 갖가지 악기를 연주하며, 온갖 갖가지 개를 쓰다듬고, 온갖 갖가지 경치에 추파를 던지며, 온갖 갖가지 화분과 난간을 배경으로 두르고 있는 터라, 그 효과는 자못 위풍당당하다."]], [["But fashion is Mr. Weevle’s, as it was Tony Jobling’s, weakness.", "그러나 유행은 토니 조블링이 그러했듯 위블 씨의 약점이다."], ["To borrow yesterday’s paper from the Sol’s Arms of an evening and read about the brilliant and distinguished meteors that are shooting across the fashionable sky in every direction is unspeakable consolation to him.", "저녁이면 솔스 암스에서 어제 신문을 빌려와, 유행의 하늘을 사방으로 가로지르며 쏟아지는 찬란하고 고귀한 유성들에 관해 읽는 것이 그에게는 이루 말할 수 없는 위안이다."], ["To know what member of what brilliant and distinguished circle accomplished the brilliant and distinguished feat of joining it yesterday or contemplates the no less brilliant and distinguished feat of leaving it to-morrow gives him a thrill of joy.", "어느 찬란하고 고귀한 사교계의 어느 일원이 어제 그 사교계에 합류하는 찬란하고 고귀한 위업을 이뤘는지, 또 내일 그것을 떠나는 그에 못지않게 찬란하고 고귀한 위업을 궁리하고 있는지를 아는 것은 그에게 기쁨의 전율을 안긴다."], ["To be informed what the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty is about, and means to be about, and what Galaxy marriages are on the tapis, and what Galaxy rumours are in circulation, is to become acquainted with the most glorious destinies of mankind.", "영국 미인 은하 화랑이 지금 무엇을 하고 있고 무엇을 하려는지, 어떤 은하의 혼사가 도마에 올라 있는지, 어떤 은하의 풍문이 떠도는지를 전해 듣는 것은 인류의 더없이 영광스러운 운명과 친해지는 일이다."], ["Mr. Weevle reverts from this intelligence to the Galaxy portraits implicated, and seems to know the originals, and to be known of them.", "위블 씨는 이 소식에서 거기 얽힌 은하의 초상화로 눈길을 되돌리는데, 마치 그 실물들을 알고 있고 또 그들에게 알려져 있기라도 한 듯하다."]], [["For the rest he is a quiet lodger, full of handy shifts and devices as before mentioned, able to cook and clean for himself as well as to carpenter, and developing social inclinations after the shades of evening have fallen on the court. At those times, when he is not visited by Mr.", "그 밖에 그는 조용한 하숙인으로, 앞서 말했듯 손쉬운 임시변통과 궁리가 넘쳐, 목수 일은 물론 제 손으로 요리하고 청소까지 해낼 줄 알며, 마당에 저녁 어스름이 내려앉은 뒤로는 사교적인 성향을 드러낸다."], ["Guppy or by a small light in his likeness quenched in a dark hat, he comes out of his dull room—where he has inherited the deal wilderness of desk bespattered with a rain of ink—and talks to Krook or is “very free,” as they call it in the court, commendingly, with any one disposed for conversation.", "그런 때면, 구피 씨가 찾아오거나 검은 모자 속에 꺼져버린 그의 작은 분신 같은 불빛이 찾아오지 않을 적이면, 그는 칙칙한 제 방에서―잉크가 비처럼 흩뿌려진 전나무 책상의 황무지를 물려받은 그 방에서―나와 크룩과 이야기를 나누거나, 마당 사람들이 칭찬조로 부르는 말마따나 대화에 끌리는 누구하고든 \"아주 허물없이\""], ["Wherefore, Mrs.", "군다."], ["Piper, who leads the court, is impelled to offer two remarks to Mrs.", "그런 까닭에 마당을 주름잡는 파이퍼 부인은 퍼킨스 부인에게 두 가지 소견을 내놓지 않고는 못 배긴다."], ["Perkins: firstly, that if her Johnny was to have whiskers, she could wish ’em to be identically like that young man’s; and secondly, “Mark my words, Mrs.", "첫째, 만약 자기네 조니가 구레나룻을 기른다면 꼭 저 젊은이 것하고 똑같았으면 좋겠다는 것."], ["Perkins, ma’am, and don’t you be surprised, Lord bless you, if that young man comes in at last for old Krook’s money!” The Smallweed Family", "둘째, \"내 말 명심해요, 퍼킨스 부인, 그리고 놀라지 마요, 어이구 맙소사, 저 젊은이가 끝내 크룩 영감의 재산을 차지하게 되더라도 말이에요!\""]], [["In a rather ill-favoured and ill-savoured neighbourhood, though one of its rising grounds bears the name of Mount Pleasant, the Elfin Smallweed, christened Bartholomew and known on the domestic hearth as Bart, passes that limited portion of his time on which the office and its contingencies have no claim.", "생김새도 별로요 풍기는 냄새도 별로인 동네이긴 하지만, 그 가운데 비탈진 언덕 하나가 마운트 플레전트라는 이름을 달고 있는 그곳에서, 요정 같은 스몰위드 — 세례명은 바살러뮤요 집안 화롯가에서는 바트라 불리는 — 는 사무소와 그 잡다한 일거리가 침범할 수 없는 자기만의 한정된 시간을 보낸다."], ["He dwells in a little narrow street, always solitary, shady, and sad, closely bricked in on all sides like a tomb, but where there yet lingers the stump of an old forest tree whose flavour is about as fresh and natural as the Smallweed smack of youth.", "그는 좁다란 골목길에 사는데, 늘 적막하고 그늘지고 쓸쓸하며, 무덤처럼 사방이 벽돌로 빈틈없이 둘러싸여 있다. 그래도 그곳엔 옛 숲의 고목 그루터기 하나가 아직 남아 있으니, 그 풍취가 신선하고 자연스럽기로는 스몰위드 일가에서 풍기는 젊음의 맛과 엇비슷하다."]], [["There has been only one child in the Smallweed family for several generations.", "스몰위드 집안에는 여러 대에 걸쳐 아이라곤 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["Little old men and women there have been, but no child, until Mr. Smallweed’s grandmother, now living, became weak in her intellect and fell (for the first time) into a childish state.", "조그만 늙은이 남녀는 있어도 아이는 없었으니, 스몰위드 씨의 할머니 — 지금도 살아 있는 — 가 정신이 흐려져서 (난생처음) 어린아이 같은 상태로 떨어지기 전까지는 그랬다."], ["With such infantine graces as a total want of observation, memory, understanding, and interest, and an eternal disposition to fall asleep over the fire and into it, Mr. Smallweed’s grandmother has undoubtedly brightened the family.", "관찰력도 기억력도 이해력도 흥미도 전혀 없는 데다 불 앞에서, 그것도 불 속으로 졸아떨어지는 영원한 성향이라는, 그런 유아적 우아함을 갖춘 덕분에 스몰위드 씨의 할머니는 의심할 바 없이 집안을 환하게 밝혀놓았다."]], [["Mr. Smallweed’s grandfather is likewise of the party.", "스몰위드 씨의 할아버지 역시 이 일행에 든다."], ["He is in a helpless condition as to his lower, and nearly so as to his upper, limbs, but his mind is unimpaired.", "그는 하반신은 영 가망 없는 상태이고 상반신도 거의 그러하지만, 정신만은 멀쩡하다."], ["It holds, as well as it ever held, the first four rules of arithmetic and a certain small collection of the hardest facts.", "그 정신은 예나 지금이나 똑같이 산수의 사칙연산과 가장 딱딱한 사실 몇 가지를 그러쥐고 있다."], ["In respect of ideality, reverence, wonder, and other such phrenological attributes, it is no worse off than it used to be.", "이상이니 경외니 경탄이니 하는 그 밖의 골상학적 자질로 말하자면, 옛날에 비해 조금도 못해진 데가 없다."], ["Everything that Mr. Smallweed’s grandfather ever put away in his mind was a grub at first, and is a grub at last.", "스몰위드 씨의 할아버지가 평생 머릿속에 챙겨 넣은 것은 무엇이든 처음에도 애벌레요 끝에도 애벌레다."], ["In all his life he has never bred a single butterfly.", "그는 평생토록 나비 한 마리 길러낸 적이 없다."]], [["The father of this pleasant grandfather, of the neighbourhood of Mount Pleasant, was a horny-skinned, two-legged, money-getting species of spider who spun webs to catch unwary flies and retired into holes until they were entrapped.", "마운트 플레전트 동네에 사는 이 유쾌한 할아버지의 아버지는, 가죽 같은 살갗에 두 다리로 서서 돈을 긁어모으는 거미 종류였으니, 방심한 파리를 잡으려 거미줄을 치고는 먹이가 걸려들 때까지 구멍 속에 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["The name of this old pagan’s god was Compound Interest.", "이 늙은 이교도가 섬긴 신의 이름은 복리(複利)였다."], ["He lived for it, married it, died of it.", "그는 복리를 위해 살았고, 복리와 혼인했으며, 복리 때문에 죽었다."], ["Meeting with a heavy loss in an honest little enterprise in which all the loss was intended to have been on the other side, he broke something—something necessary to his existence, therefore it couldn’t have been his heart—and made an end of his career.", "손실이란 손실은 죄다 상대편이 떠안도록 꾸민, 정직하기 짝이 없는 어떤 자그마한 사업에서 큰 손실을 보게 되자, 그는 무언가가 — 자기 존재에 꼭 필요한 무언가가, 그러니 그게 심장일 리는 없는 무언가가 — 부러져버려 그렇게 생을 마감했다."], ["As his character was not good, and he had been bred at a charity school in a complete course, according to question and answer, of those ancient people the Amorites and Hittites, he was frequently quoted as an example of the failure of education.", "그는 품행이 좋지 못한 데다, 자선 학교에서 문답 형식으로 저 옛 백성 아모리 족속과 헷 족속에 관한 과정을 통째로 배우며 자랐던 탓에, 흔히 교육이 실패한 본보기로 인용되곤 했다."]], [["His spirit shone through his son, to whom he had always preached of “going out” early in life and whom he made a clerk in a sharp scrivener’s office at twelve years old.", "그의 정신은 아들에게로 스며들어 빛을 냈으니, 그는 늘 아들에게 인생 일찍부터 \"세상에 나가라\"고 설교했고 열두 살 적에 약삭빠른 어느 필경사 사무소의 서기로 박아 넣었다."], ["There the young gentleman improved his mind, which was of a lean and anxious character, and developing the family gifts, gradually elevated himself into the discounting profession.", "거기서 그 젊은 양반은 야위고 근심에 찬 성품의 머리를 갈고닦았으며, 집안 내림의 재능을 키워 점차 어음 할인업으로 신분을 끌어올렸다."], ["Going out early in life and marrying late, as his father had done before him, he too begat a lean and anxious-minded son, who in his turn, going out early in life and marrying late, became the father of Bartholomew and Judith Smallweed, twins.", "아버지가 그랬듯 인생 일찍 세상에 나가 늦게 혼인한 그 역시 야위고 근심에 찬 마음을 가진 아들을 낳았고, 그 아들 또한 인생 일찍 세상에 나가 늦게 혼인하여 쌍둥이 바살러뮤와 주디스 스몰위드의 아비가 되었다."], ["During the whole time consumed in the slow growth of this family tree, the house of Smallweed, always early to go out and late to marry, has strengthened itself in its practical character, has discarded all amusements, discountenanced all story-books, fairy-tales, fictions, and fables, and banished all levities whatsoever.", "이 집안 나무가 더디게 자라는 동안 줄곧, 늘 일찍 나가고 늦게 혼인한 스몰위드 가문은 실용적인 기질로 제 몸을 단단히 다져왔으니, 온갖 오락을 내던지고, 온갖 이야기책과 동화와 소설과 우화를 백안시했으며, 어떤 종류의 경박함도 죄다 추방해버렸다."], ["Hence the gratifying fact that it has had no child born to it and that the complete little men and women whom it has produced have been observed to bear a likeness to old monkeys with something depressing on their minds.", "그리하여, 이 집안에 아이라곤 태어난 적이 없으며 이 집안이 빚어낸 완성된 꼬마 남녀들이 하나같이 마음에 무언가 우울한 걸 얹은 늙은 원숭이를 닮았더라는, 그 흐뭇한 사실이 생겨난 것이다."]], [["At the present time, in the dark little parlour certain feet below the level of the street—a grim, hard, uncouth parlour, only ornamented with the coarsest of baize table-covers, and the hardest of sheet-iron tea-trays, and offering in its decorative character no bad allegorical representation of Grandfather Smallweed’s mind—seated in two black horsehair porter’s chairs, one on each side of the fire-place, the superannuated Mr. and Mrs.", "지금 이 시각, 길바닥보다 몇 자쯤 아래에 있는 어둑한 작은 거실 — 험상궂고 딱딱하고 볼품없는 거실, 더없이 거친 천으로 만든 식탁보와 더없이 딱딱한 양철 찻쟁반 말고는 아무 장식도 없는, 그 장식적 성격으로 보자면 스몰위드 할아버지의 정신을 우의적으로 표현하기에 손색이 없는 그 거실 — 에서, 벽난로 양쪽에 하나씩 놓인 검은 말총 안락의자에 늙어빠진 스몰위드 노부부가 앉아 장밋빛 시간을 흘려보낸다."], ["Smallweed while away the rosy hours. On the stove are a couple of trivets for the pots and kettles which it is Grandfather Smallweed’s usual occupation to watch, and projecting from the chimney-piece between them is a sort of brass gallows for roasting, which he also superintends when it is in action.", "난로 위에는 냄비와 주전자를 얹는 삼발이가 한 쌍 놓여 있는데 그걸 지켜보는 게 스몰위드 할아버지의 일상 소일거리요, 그 사이로 굴뚝 선반에서 튀어나온 것은 고기 굽는 일종의 놋쇠 교수대이니 그것이 작동할 때면 그가 역시 감독한다."], ["Under the venerable Mr. Smallweed’s seat and guarded by his spindle legs is a drawer in his chair, reported to contain property to a fabulous amount.", "노쇠하신 스몰위드 씨의 자리 밑, 막대기 같은 그의 두 다리가 지키는 곳에는 의자 속 서랍이 하나 있는데, 거기엔 엄청난 액수의 재산이 들었다는 소문이 돈다."], ["Beside him is a spare cushion with which he is always provided in order that he may have something to throw at the venerable partner of his respected age whenever she makes an allusion to money—a subject on which he is particularly sensitive.", "그의 곁에는 여분의 방석이 하나 있어 늘 채워져 있으니, 존경할 만한 나이를 함께 먹은 동반자가 돈 이야기 — 그가 유난히 민감해하는 화제 — 를 들먹일 때마다 던질 무언가가 있어야 하기 때문이다."]], [["“And where’s Bart?”", "\"바트는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["Grandfather Smallweed inquires of Judy, Bart’s twin sister.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 바트의 쌍둥이 누이 주디에게 묻는다."]], [["“He an’t come in yet,”", "\"아직 안 들어왔어요.\""], ["says Judy.", "주디가 말한다."]], [["“It’s his tea-time, isn’t it?” “No.”", "\"걔 차 마실 시간 아니냐?\""]], [["“How much do you mean to say it wants then?”", "\"아니에요.\" \"그럼 얼마나 남았다는 게냐?\""]], [["“Ten minutes.”", "\"십 분이요.\""]], [["“Hey?”", "\"응?\""]], [["“Ten minutes.”", "\"십 분이라고요.\""], ["(Loud on the part of Judy.)", "(주디가 큰 소리로 말한다.)"]], [["“Ho!”", "\"호!\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다."], ["“Ten minutes.”", "\"십 분이라.\""]], [["Grandmother Smallweed, who has been mumbling and shaking her head at the trivets, hearing figures mentioned, connects them with money and screeches like a horrible old parrot without any plumage, “Ten ten-pound notes!”", "삼발이를 향해 우물거리며 고개를 내젓던 스몰위드 할머니가 숫자 이야기를 듣고는 그걸 돈에 갖다 붙여, 깃털이라곤 하나도 없는 끔찍한 늙은 앵무새처럼 꽥꽥거린다. \"십 파운드짜리 지폐 열 장!\""]], [["Grandfather Smallweed immediately throws the cushion at her.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 즉각 그녀에게 방석을 내던진다."]], [["“Drat you, be quiet!”", "\"빌어먹을, 조용히 좀 못 해!\""], ["says the good old man.", "그 착하신 노인이 말한다."]], [["The effect of this act of jaculation is twofold. It not only doubles up Mrs.", "이 투척 행위의 효과는 두 갈래다."], ["Smallweed’s head against the side of her porter’s chair and causes her to present, when extricated by her granddaughter, a highly unbecoming state of cap, but the necessary exertion recoils on Mr. Smallweed himself, whom it throws back into HIS porter’s chair like a broken puppet.", "그것은 스몰위드 부인의 머리를 안락의자 옆구리에 처박아 접어버려, 손녀가 끌어내고 보면 그녀의 모자가 영 꼴사납게 흐트러져 있게 만들 뿐 아니라, 거기 드는 힘이 도로 스몰위드 씨 자신에게 튕겨와 그를 부러진 꼭두각시처럼 제 안락의자에 처박는다."], ["The excellent old gentleman being at these times a mere clothes-bag with a black skull-cap on the top of it, does not present a very animated appearance until he has undergone the two operations at the hands of his granddaughter of being shaken up like a great bottle and poked and punched like a great bolster.", "이런 때면 그 훌륭한 노신사는 검은 두건을 꼭대기에 얹은 한낱 옷보따리에 지나지 않으니, 손녀의 손으로 두 차례 시술 — 큰 병처럼 흔들리고, 큰 베개처럼 쿡쿡 찔리고 두들겨 맞는 — 을 거치기 전에는 그다지 생기 있는 모습을 띠지 못한다."], ["Some indication of a neck being developed in him by these means, he and the sharer of his life’s evening again sit fronting one another in their two porter’s chairs, like a couple of sentinels long forgotten on their post by the Black Serjeant, Death.", "이런 수단으로 목 비슷한 게 그에게서 얼마쯤 돋아나면, 그와 그의 인생 황혼을 함께하는 동반자는 다시 두 안락의자에 마주 앉으니, 마치 검은 하사관 죽음이 초소에 세워놓고 오래도록 잊어버린 두 보초병 같다."]], [["Judy the twin is worthy company for these associates.", "쌍둥이 주디는 이 패거리와 어울리기에 알맞은 동무다."], ["She is so indubitably sister to Mr. Smallweed the younger that the two kneaded into one would hardly make a young person of average proportions, while she so happily exemplifies the before-mentioned family likeness to the monkey tribe that attired in a spangled robe and cap she might walk about the table-land on the top of a barrel-organ without exciting much remark as an unusual specimen.", "그녀가 젊은 스몰위드 씨의 누이라는 건 도무지 의심할 여지가 없으니, 둘을 한데 반죽해 하나로 빚어도 평균 체격의 젊은이 하나가 채 되지 않을 지경이요, 게다가 그녀는 앞서 말한 원숭이 족속을 닮은 집안 내림을 어찌나 잘 본보이는지, 번쩍이는 옷과 모자를 차려입혀 손풍금 꼭대기의 평평한 자리를 거닐게 해도 별난 표본이라고 입길에 오르내릴 일이 없을 정도다."], ["Under existing circumstances, however, she is dressed in a plain, spare gown of brown stuff.", "그러나 지금 형편에서는 갈색 천으로 지은 수수하고 빈약한 가운을 걸치고 있다."]], [["Judy never owned a doll, never heard of Cinderella, never played at any game.", "주디는 인형을 가져본 적도, 신데렐라 이야기를 들어본 적도, 어떤 놀이를 해본 적도 없다."], ["She once or twice fell into children’s company when she was about ten years old, but the children couldn’t get on with Judy, and Judy couldn’t get on with them.", "열 살 무렵 한두 번 아이들 무리에 끼어든 적은 있으나, 아이들은 주디와 어울리지 못했고 주디도 아이들과 어울리지 못했다."], ["She seemed like an animal of another species, and there was instinctive repugnance on both sides.", "그녀는 다른 종류의 짐승 같았고, 양쪽 모두 본능적인 거부감을 느꼈다."], ["It is very doubtful whether Judy knows how to laugh.", "주디가 웃을 줄이나 아는지는 자못 의심스럽다."], ["She has so rarely seen the thing done that the probabilities are strong the other way.", "그녀는 그런 행위를 워낙 본 적이 드물어서, 오히려 모를 가능성이 농후하다."], ["Of anything like a youthful laugh, she certainly can have no conception.", "젊은이다운 웃음 비슷한 것이라면 분명 머릿속에 떠올릴 수조차 없을 터이다."], ["If she were to try one, she would find her teeth in her way, modelling that action of her face, as she has unconsciously modelled all its other expressions, on her pattern of sordid age.", "만일 한번 웃어보려 한다면, 제 이가 거치적거리는 걸 발견하리니, 그 얼굴 동작을, 다른 모든 표정을 무의식중에 본떠왔듯이, 추레한 노년이라는 제 본보기를 따라 빚어낼 테니 말이다."], ["Such is Judy.", "그런 게 주디다."]], [["And her twin brother couldn’t wind up a top for his life.", "그리고 그녀의 쌍둥이 오라비는 죽었다 깨도 팽이 하나 감을 줄 모른다."], ["He knows no more of Jack the Giant Killer or of Sinbad the Sailor than he knows of the people in the stars.", "그는 거인 잡는 잭이나 뱃사람 신드바드에 대해, 별자리 속 사람들만큼이나 아는 게 없다."], ["He could as soon play at leap-frog or at cricket as change into a cricket or a frog himself.", "그가 개구리뜀이나 크리켓을 할 줄 아는 정도는, 제 몸이 크리켓 벌레나 개구리로 둔갑할 줄 아는 정도와 매한가지다."], ["But he is so much the better off than his sister that on his narrow world of fact an opening has dawned into such broader regions as lie within the ken of Mr. Guppy.", "그래도 누이보다는 한결 형편이 나으니, 사실밖에 모르는 그의 좁은 세계에 거피 씨의 식견이 미치는 그런 한층 너른 영역으로 통하는 틈새 하나가 동터왔기 때문이다."], ["Hence his admiration and his emulation of that shining enchanter.", "그리하여 그는 그 빛나는 마법사를 흠모하고 본받으려 든다."]], [["Judy, with a gong-like clash and clatter, sets one of the sheet-iron tea-trays on the table and arranges cups and saucers.", "주디는 양철 찻쟁반 하나를 징을 치듯 쨍그랑 요란하게 식탁에 내려놓고 찻잔과 받침을 늘어놓는다."], ["The bread she puts on in an iron basket, and the butter (and not much of it) in a small pewter plate.", "빵은 무쇠 바구니에 담고, 버터는 (그나마 얼마 안 되는) 작은 백랍 접시에 담아 올린다."], ["Grandfather Smallweed looks hard after the tea as it is served out and asks Judy where the girl is.", "스몰위드 할아버지는 차가 따라지는 모양을 매섭게 살피더니 주디에게 그 계집애는 어디 있느냐고 묻는다."]], [["“Charley, do you mean?”", "\"찰리 말이에요?\""], ["says Judy.", "주디가 말한다."]], [["“Hey?”", "\"응?\""], ["from Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 되묻는다."]], [["“Charley, do you mean?”", "\"찰리 말이냐고요?\""]], [["This touches a spring in Grandmother Smallweed, who, chuckling as usual at the trivets, cries, “Over the water!", "이 말이 스몰위드 할머니의 어떤 태엽을 건드리니, 여느 때처럼 삼발이를 향해 킬킬거리던 그녀가 외친다."], ["Charley over the water, Charley over the water, over the water to Charley, Charley over the water, over the water to Charley!”", "\"물 건너! 물 건너 찰리, 물 건너 찰리, 물 건너 찰리에게로, 물 건너 찰리, 물 건너 찰리에게로!\""], ["and becomes quite energetic about it.", "그러고는 그 일에 자못 기운을 낸다."], ["Grandfather looks at the cushion but has not sufficiently recovered his late exertion.", "할아버지가 방석을 쳐다보지만 아까 쓴 기력을 아직 추스르지 못한 터다."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["he says when there is silence.", "잠잠해지자 그가 말한다."], ["“If that’s her name. She eats a deal.", "\"그게 그 애 이름이라면 말이지."], ["It would be better to allow her for her keep.”", "걘 너무 많이 처먹어. 식비를 따로 쳐주는 게 낫겠어.\""]], [["Judy, with her brother’s wink, shakes her head and purses up her mouth into no without saying it.", "주디는 오라비와 똑같이 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하며 고개를 젓고는, 입으로 소리는 내지 않은 채 아니라는 모양으로 입술을 오므린다."]], [["“No?”", "\"안 돼?\""], ["returns the old man.", "노인이 되묻는다."], ["“Why not?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“She’d want sixpence a day, and we can do it for less,”", "\"걘 하루에 육 펜스를 달랄 텐데, 우리는 그보다 적게 들이고도 해낼 수 있거든요.\""], ["says Judy.", "주디가 말한다."]], [["“Sure?”", "\"틀림없냐?\""]], [["Judy answers with a nod of deepest meaning and calls, as she scrapes the butter on the loaf with every precaution against waste and cuts it into slices, “You, Charley, where are you?”", "주디는 더없이 깊은 뜻을 담아 고개를 끄덕여 답하고는, 낭비될세라 갖은 조심을 다해 빵덩이에 버터를 긁어 펴 바르고 조각으로 썰면서 외친다. \"찰리, 너, 어디 있어?\""], ["Timidly obedient to the summons, a little girl in a rough apron and a large bonnet, with her hands covered with soap and water and a scrubbing brush in one of them, appears, and curtsys.", "부름에 머뭇머뭇 따르며, 거친 앞치마에 큼직한 보닛을 쓴 어린 계집아이가 두 손에 비눗물을 묻히고 한 손엔 솔을 든 채 나타나 무릎을 굽혀 인사한다."]], [["“What work are you about now?”", "\"지금 무슨 일 하고 있어?\""], ["says Judy, making an ancient snap at her like a very sharp old beldame.", "주디가 아주 매서운 늙은 노파처럼 옛 솜씨로 딱 채뜨리듯 묻는다."]], [["“I’m a-cleaning the upstairs back room, miss,”", "\"위층 뒷방 청소하고 있어요, 아가씨.\""], ["replies Charley.", "찰리가 답한다."]], [["“Mind you do it thoroughly, and don’t loiter.", "\"꼼꼼히 해, 꾸물대지 말고."], ["Shirking won’t do for me.", "농땡이는 나한테 안 통해."], ["Make haste!", "서둘러!"], ["Go along!”", "가봐!\""], ["cries Judy with a stamp upon the ground.", "주디가 발을 쾅 구르며 외친다."], ["“You girls are more trouble than you’re worth, by half.”", "\"너희 계집애들은 쓸모보다 골칫거리가 두 배는 더하다니까.\""]], [["On this severe matron, as she returns to her task of scraping the butter and cutting the bread, falls the shadow of her brother, looking in at the window.", "이 엄한 마님이 버터를 긁어 펴 바르고 빵을 써는 일로 되돌아갈 때, 창문으로 들여다보는 오라비의 그림자가 그녀 위로 드리운다."], ["For whom, knife and loaf in hand, she opens the street-door.", "그 사람을 들이려고 그녀는 칼과 빵덩이를 손에 든 채 거리로 난 문을 연다."]], [["“Aye, aye, Bart!”", "\"오냐, 오냐, 바트!\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다."], ["“Here you are, hey?”", "\"왔구나, 응?\""]], [["“Here I am,”", "\"왔어요.\""], ["says Bart.", "바트가 말한다."]], [["“Been along with your friend again, Bart?”", "\"또 네 친구랑 같이 있었던 게냐, 바트?\""]], [["Small nods.", "스몰위드가 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“Dining at his expense, Bart?”", "\"걔 돈으로 저녁 얻어먹고, 바트?\""]], [["Small nods again.", "스몰위드가 또 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“That’s right.", "\"잘했다."], ["Live at his expense as much as you can, and take warning by his foolish example.", "할 수 있는 한 그 녀석 돈으로 살고, 그 어리석은 본보기에서 교훈을 얻어라."], ["That’s the use of such a friend.", "그런 친구가 쓸모 있는 데가 그거지."], ["The only use you can put him to,”", "네가 그 녀석을 써먹을 수 있는 유일한 데가 그거야.\""], ["says the venerable sage.", "노현인이 말한다."]], [["His grandson, without receiving this good counsel as dutifully as he might, honours it with all such acceptance as may lie in a slight wink and a nod and takes a chair at the tea-table. The four old faces then hover over teacups like a company of ghastly cherubim, Mrs.", "손자는 이 좋은 충고를 마땅히 받들 만큼 곱게 받들지는 않으나, 한쪽 눈을 살짝 찡긋하고 고개를 끄덕여 보일 만큼의 수긍으로나마 그 충고에 경의를 표하고는 찻상에 의자를 끌어다 앉는다."], ["Smallweed perpetually twitching her head and chattering at the trivets and Mr. Smallweed requiring to be repeatedly shaken up like a large black draught.", "그러자 네 늙은 얼굴이 찻잔 위로 모여드니, 마치 섬뜩한 아기 천사 무리 같은데, 스몰위드 부인은 끊임없이 머리를 씰룩이며 삼발이를 향해 종알대고 스몰위드 씨는 큼직한 검은 물약처럼 거듭 흔들어줘야 한다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["says the good old gentleman, reverting to his lesson of wisdom.", "그 착하신 노신사가 자신의 지혜로운 교훈으로 되돌아가며 말한다."], ["“That’s such advice as your father would have given you, Bart.", "\"그게 바로 네 아비가 너한테 해주었을 충고다, 바트. 넌 네 아비를 본 적이 없지."], ["You never saw your father.", "더없이 안타까운 일이야."], ["More’s the pity. He was my true son.”", "그 녀석은 내 진짜배기 아들이었어.\""], ["Whether it is intended to be conveyed that he was particularly pleasant to look at, on that account, does not appear.", "그렇기에 그 아비가 보기에 특별히 흐뭇한 인물이었다는 뜻을 전하려는 것인지는 드러나지 않는다."]], [["“He was my true son,”", "\"그 녀석은 내 진짜배기 아들이었지.\""], ["repeats the old gentleman, folding his bread and butter on his knee, “a good accountant, and died fifteen years ago.”", "노신사가 버터 바른 빵을 무릎 위에서 접으며 되뇐다. \"솜씨 좋은 회계사였고, 십오 년 전에 죽었어.\""]], [["Mrs. Smallweed, following her usual instinct, breaks out with “Fifteen hundred pound.", "스몰위드 부인은 여느 때 본능을 좇아 불쑥 터뜨린다."], ["Fifteen hundred pound in a black box, fifteen hundred pound locked up, fifteen hundred pound put away and hid!”", "\"천오백 파운드. 검은 상자 속에 천오백 파운드, 자물쇠 채운 천오백 파운드, 따로 챙겨 숨겨둔 천오백 파운드!\""], ["Her worthy husband, setting aside his bread and butter, immediately discharges the cushion at her, crushes her against the side of her chair, and falls back in his own, overpowered. His appearance, after visiting Mrs.", "그녀의 훌륭한 남편은 버터 바른 빵을 옆으로 치우고 즉각 그녀에게 방석을 발사해 그녀를 의자 옆구리에 짓뭉개버리고는, 제 의자에 기진하여 나자빠진다."], ["Smallweed with one of these admonitions, is particularly impressive and not wholly prepossessing, firstly because the exertion generally twists his black skull-cap over one eye and gives him an air of goblin rakishness, secondly because he mutters violent imprecations against Mrs. Smallweed, and thirdly because the contrast between those powerful expressions and his powerless figure is suggestive of a baleful old malignant who would be very wicked if he could.", "스몰위드 부인에게 이런 훈계 한 차례를 베풀고 난 그의 몰골은 유난히 인상적이되 그리 호감 가는 꼴은 아니니, 첫째로 그 힘쓰는 통에 검은 두건이 한쪽 눈 위로 비뚜로 돌아가 도깨비 같은 난봉꾼 분위기를 풍기게 만들고, 둘째로 그가 스몰위드 부인을 향해 사나운 저주를 중얼대며, 셋째로 그 우렁찬 표현과 무력한 몸뚱이의 대비가, 할 수만 있다면 한껏 못되게 굴었을 음험하고 늙은 악당을 떠올리게 하기 때문이다."], ["All this, however, is so common in the Smallweed family circle that it produces no impression.", "그러나 이 모든 게 스몰위드 집안에서는 워낙 흔한 일이라 아무런 인상도 남기지 못한다."], ["The old gentleman is merely shaken and has his internal feathers beaten up, the cushion is restored to its usual place beside him, and the old lady, perhaps with her cap adjusted and perhaps not, is planted in her chair again, ready to be bowled down like a ninepin.", "노신사는 그저 흔들려 속 깃털을 두들겨 펴질 뿐이요, 방석은 그의 곁 제자리로 돌아가며, 노부인은 모자를 바로 했을 수도 안 했을 수도 있는 채로 다시 의자에 심기니, 구주희 핀처럼 또 한 번 쓰러질 채비를 갖추고서다."]], [["Some time elapses in the present instance before the old gentleman is sufficiently cool to resume his discourse, and even then he mixes it up with several edifying expletives addressed to the unconscious partner of his bosom, who holds communication with nothing on earth but the trivets.", "이번 경우엔 노신사가 다시 이야기를 이을 만큼 충분히 식기까지 한참이 걸리고, 그러고도 그는 이야기에 몇 마디 교화적인 욕설을 섞어 넣으니, 그 욕설의 대상은 이 세상 그 무엇과도 — 삼발이 말고는 — 교류하지 않는, 의식 없는 품속 동반자다. 이를테면 이런 식이다."], ["As thus: “If your father, Bart, had lived longer, he might have been worth a deal of money—you brimstone chatterer!—but just as he was beginning to build up the house that he had been making the foundations for, through many a year—you jade of a magpie, jackdaw, and poll-parrot, what do you mean!—he took ill and died of a low fever, always being a sparing and a spare man, full of business care—I should like to throw a cat at you instead of a cushion, and I will too if you make such a confounded fool of yourself!—and your mother, who was a prudent woman as dry as a chip, just dwindled away like touchwood after you and Judy were born—you are an old pig.", "\"네 아비가, 바트, 좀 더 오래 살았더라면 큰돈을 모았을지도 모른다 — 이 유황불 같은 수다쟁이야! — 헌데 그토록 여러 해 기초를 다져온 집을 막 쌓아 올리려던 참에 — 이 까치, 갈까마귀, 앵무새 같은 화냥년아, 대체 무슨 짓이냐!"], ["You are a brimstone pig.", "— 병이 들어 미열로 죽고 말았으니, 늘 아끼고 아쉬워하는 사람인 데다 사업 걱정으로 꽉 차 있었거든 — 방석 대신 고양이를 던지고 싶구나, 네가 그렇게 빌어먹을 바보짓을 또 하면 정말로 그러마! — 그리고 네 어미는, 대팻밥처럼 메마른 신중한 여자였는데, 너랑 주디가 태어난 뒤로 부싯깃처럼 그냥 시들어버렸지 — 너는 늙은 돼지야."], ["You’re a head of swine!”", "유황불 같은 돼지라고. 너는 돼지 우두머리야!\""]], [["Judy, not interested in what she has often heard, begins to collect in a basin various tributary streams of tea, from the bottoms of cups and saucers and from the bottom of the tea-pot for the little charwoman’s evening meal.", "주디는 숱하게 들어온 이야기엔 관심이 없는지라, 어린 잡역부 계집아이의 저녁거리로 쓸 차의 갖가지 잔여 줄기를 찻잔과 받침 바닥, 그리고 찻주전자 바닥에서 그러모아 대접 하나에 담기 시작한다."], ["In like manner she gets together, in the iron bread-basket, as many outside fragments and worn-down heels of loaves as the rigid economy of the house has left in existence.", "같은 식으로 그녀는 이 집의 빡빡한 절약이 남겨놓은, 빵 바깥 부스러기와 닳아빠진 빵 꽁다리를 닥치는 대로 무쇠 빵 바구니에 모아 담는다."]], [["“But your father and me were partners, Bart,”", "\"하지만 네 아비와 나는 동업자였다, 바트.\""], ["says the old gentleman, “and when I am gone, you and Judy will have all there is.", "노신사가 말한다. \"그러니 내가 가고 나면 너와 주디가 있는 거 다 차지할 거야."], ["It’s rare for you both that you went out early in life—Judy to the flower business, and you to the law.", "너희 둘 다 인생 일찍 세상에 나간 게 보기 드물게 잘된 일이지 — 주디는 꽃 장사로, 너는 법 쪽으로."], ["You won’t want to spend it.", "너희는 그걸 쓰고 싶지 않을 게야."], ["You’ll get your living without it, and put more to it.", "그거 없이도 벌어먹고, 거기다 더 보탤 테니까."], ["When I am gone, Judy will go back to the flower business and you’ll still stick to the law.”", "내가 가고 나면 주디는 꽃 장사로 돌아가고 너는 여전히 법에 들러붙어 있겠지.\""]], [["One might infer from Judy’s appearance that her business rather lay with the thorns than the flowers, but she has in her time been apprenticed to the art and mystery of artificial flower-making.", "주디의 생김새로 보자면 그녀가 다루는 건 꽃보다는 가시 쪽이라고 짐작할 법하나, 그래도 한때 그녀는 조화(造花) 만드는 기술과 비법을 도제로 배운 적이 있다."], ["A close observer might perhaps detect both in her eye and her brother’s, when their venerable grandsire anticipates his being gone, some little impatience to know when he may be going, and some resentful opinion that it is time he went.", "눈썰미 있는 이라면 어쩌면, 노쇠하신 조부가 자신이 가고 없을 때를 미리 그릴 때 그녀와 그 오라비의 눈에서 다 같이, 그가 대체 언제 가려나 알고 싶어하는 약간의 조바심과, 이제 갈 때도 됐다는 약간의 원망 어린 속내를 읽어낼지도 모른다."]], [["“Now, if everybody has done,”", "\"자, 다들 먹었으면,\""], ["says Judy, completing her preparations, “I’ll have that girl in to her tea.", "주디가 채비를 마무리하며 말한다. \"그 계집애를 들여서 차를 줘야겠어."], ["She would never leave off if she took it by herself in the kitchen.”", "부엌에서 혼자 먹게 두면 도무지 끝낼 줄을 모르거든.\""]], [["Charley is accordingly introduced, and under a heavy fire of eyes, sits down to her basin and a Druidical ruin of bread and butter.", "그리하여 찰리가 안으로 불려 들어와, 쏟아지는 눈길의 맹포화 아래, 제 대접과 드루이드 유적처럼 폐허가 된 버터 바른 빵을 앞에 놓고 앉는다."], ["In the active superintendence of this young person, Judy Smallweed appears to attain a perfectly geological age and to date from the remotest periods.", "이 어린아이를 부산히 감독하는 데서, 주디 스몰위드는 완벽하게 지질학적인 연령에 다다라 까마득한 태곳적부터 비롯된 존재처럼 보인다."], ["Her systematic manner of flying at her and pouncing on her, with or without pretence, whether or no, is wonderful, evincing an accomplishment in the art of girl-driving seldom reached by the oldest practitioners.", "빌미가 있든 없든, 어쩌든 저쩌든, 체계적으로 그 아이에게 달려들어 덮치는 그녀의 솜씨는 놀랍기 그지없으니, 가장 노련한 명인들조차 좀처럼 이르지 못하는 계집애 부리기 기예의 경지를 드러낸다."]], [["“Now, don’t stare about you all the afternoon,”", "\"자, 오후 내내 두리번거리지 말고,\""], ["cries Judy, shaking her head and stamping her foot as she happens to catch the glance which has been previously sounding the basin of tea, “but take your victuals and get back to your work.”", "차 대접을 미리부터 가늠하고 있던 그 아이의 시선을 마침 붙들고서 주디가 고개를 흔들고 발을 구르며 외친다. \"음식이나 먹고 일하러 돌아가.\""]], [["“Yes, miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨.\""], ["says Charley.", "찰리가 말한다."]], [["“Don’t say yes,”", "\"네라고 하지 마.\""], ["returns Miss Smallweed, “for I know what you girls are.", "스몰위드 아가씨가 받는다. \"너희 계집애들이 어떤지 내가 다 아니까."], ["Do it without saying it, and then I may begin to believe you.”", "입으로 떠들지 말고 그냥 해, 그래야 내가 너를 믿기 시작할 테니.\""]], [["Charley swallows a great gulp of tea in token of submission and so disperses the Druidical ruins that Miss Smallweed charges her not to gormandize, which “in you girls,” she observes, is disgusting.", "찰리는 순종의 표시로 차를 한 모금 크게 들이켜고는 드루이드 유적을 어찌나 해치우는지, 스몰위드 아가씨가 게걸스레 처먹지 말라고 다그치며 \"너희 계집애들\"의 그런 짓은 역겹다고 한마디한다."], ["Charley might find some more difficulty in meeting her views on the general subject of girls but for a knock at the door.", "계집애라는 전반적 화제에 관한 그녀의 견해를 맞추는 일은 찰리에게 한층 어려웠을지도 모르나, 마침 문 두드리는 소리가 난다."]], [["“See who it is, and don’t chew when you open it!”", "\"누군지 보고 와, 문 열 때 우물우물 씹지 말고!\""], ["cries Judy.", "주디가 외친다."]], [["The object of her attentions withdrawing for the purpose, Miss Smallweed takes that opportunity of jumbling the remainder of the bread and butter together and launching two or three dirty tea-cups into the ebb-tide of the basin of tea as a hint that she considers the eating and drinking terminated.", "그녀의 관심을 받는 아이가 그러려고 물러가자, 스몰위드 아가씨는 그 틈을 타 남은 버터 바른 빵을 죄다 한데 뭉뚱그리고 더러운 찻잔 두세 개를 차 대접의 썰물 속으로 던져 넣으니, 먹고 마시는 일은 끝난 걸로 친다는 암시다."]], [["“Now!", "\"자!"], ["Who is it, and what’s wanted?”", "누구야, 무슨 일이래?\""], ["says the snappish Judy.", "까칠한 주디가 묻는다."]], [["It is one Mr. George, it appears.", "조지 씨라는 사람인 듯하다."], ["Without other announcement or ceremony, Mr. George walks in.", "다른 기별이나 격식도 없이 조지 씨가 성큼 걸어 들어온다."]], [["“Whew!”", "\"휴!\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“You are hot here.", "\"여기 덥군요."], ["Always a fire, eh?", "늘 불을 피우는군, 응?"], ["Well!", "글쎄!"], ["Perhaps you do right to get used to one.” Mr.", "하긴 불에 익숙해지는 게 옳을지도 모르겠소.\""], ["George makes the latter remark to himself as he nods to Grandfather Smallweed.", "조지 씨는 스몰위드 할아버지에게 고개를 까딱하며 뒷말은 혼잣말로 한다."]], [["“Ho!", "\"호!"], ["It’s you!”", "자네구먼!\""], ["cries the old gentleman.", "노신사가 외친다."], ["“How de do?", "\"잘 지냈나?"], ["How de do?”", "잘 지냈어?\""]], [["“Middling,”", "\"그럭저럭요.\""], ["replies Mr. George, taking a chair.", "조지 씨가 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 답한다."], ["“Your granddaughter I have had the honour of seeing before; my service to you, miss.”", "\"손녀분은 전에 뵌 영광이 있고요. 그럼 안녕하시오, 아가씨.\""]], [["“This is my grandson,”", "\"이쪽은 내 손자라네.\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다."], ["“You ha’n’t seen him before.", "\"전에 본 적 없겠지."], ["He is in the law and not much at home.”", "법 쪽에 있어서 집에 붙어 있는 일이 드물거든.\""]], [["“My service to him, too!", "\"이 사람한테도 안부 전하오!"], ["He is like his sister.", "누이를 빼닮았군."], ["He is very like his sister.", "누이랑 어찌나 닮았는지."], ["He is devilish like his sister,”", "누이랑 지독하게 닮았소.\""], ["says Mr. George, laying a great and not altogether complimentary stress on his last adjective.", "조지 씨가 마지막 형용사에 크고도 그다지 칭찬은 아닌 힘을 실어 말한다."]], [["“And how does the world use you, Mr. George?”", "\"그래, 세상살이는 자넬 어떻게 대접하던가, 조지 씨?\""], ["Grandfather Smallweed inquires, slowly rubbing his legs.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 다리를 천천히 문지르며 묻는다."]], [["“Pretty much as usual.", "\"여느 때랑 거의 똑같죠."], ["Like a football.”", "축구공처럼요.\""]], [["He is a swarthy brown man of fifty, well made, and good looking, with crisp dark hair, bright eyes, and a broad chest.", "그는 쉰 살의 가무잡잡한 갈색 피부 사내로, 체격이 다부지고 잘생겼으며, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리에 빛나는 눈, 떡 벌어진 가슴을 지녔다."], ["His sinewy and powerful hands, as sunburnt as his face, have evidently been used to a pretty rough life.", "얼굴만큼이나 햇볕에 그을린 그의 힘줄 불거진 억센 두 손은, 한눈에 봐도 꽤나 험한 삶에 익은 손이다."], ["What is curious about him is that he sits forward on his chair as if he were, from long habit, allowing space for some dress or accoutrements that he has altogether laid aside.", "그에게서 묘한 점은, 마치 오랜 습관 때문에, 이젠 죄 벗어던진 어떤 옷가지나 장구(裝具)를 위해 자리를 비워두기라도 하듯 의자 앞쪽에 걸터앉아 있다는 것이다."], ["His step too is measured and heavy and would go well with a weighty clash and jingle of spurs.", "걸음걸이 또한 절도 있고 묵직해서, 박차가 묵직하게 쟁그랑 부딪치는 소리와 잘 어울릴 법하다."], ["He is close-shaved now, but his mouth is set as if his upper lip had been for years familiar with a great moustache; and his manner of occasionally laying the open palm of his broad brown hand upon it is to the same effect.", "지금은 말끔히 면도했으나, 그의 입매는 마치 윗입술이 여러 해 큼직한 콧수염과 친했던 듯 굳어 있고, 이따금 넓적한 갈색 손바닥을 펴서 그 위에 갖다 대는 버릇도 같은 인상을 준다."], ["Altogether one might guess Mr. George to have been a trooper once upon a time.", "통틀어 보면 조지 씨가 한때 기병이었으리라 짐작할 만하다."]], [["A special contrast Mr. George makes to the Smallweed family.", "조지 씨는 스몰위드 집안과 유독 대조를 이룬다."], ["Trooper was never yet billeted upon a household more unlike him.", "이토록 자기와 안 닮은 가정에 배치된 기병은 일찍이 없었다."], ["It is a broadsword to an oyster-knife.", "굴 까는 칼에 견준 양손검 격이다."], ["His developed figure and their stunted forms, his large manner filling any amount of room and their little narrow pinched ways, his sounding voice and their sharp spare tones, are in the strongest and the strangest opposition.", "그의 발달한 체구와 그들의 발육 멈춘 형상, 어떤 공간이든 가득 채우는 그의 큼직한 거동과 그들의 좁다랗고 옹색한 처신, 우렁찬 그의 목소리와 날카롭고 빈약한 그들의 음색은 더없이 강하고도 더없이 기묘한 대립을 이룬다."], ["As he sits in the middle of the grim parlour, leaning a little forward, with his hands upon his thighs and his elbows squared, he looks as though, if he remained there long, he would absorb into himself the whole family and the whole four-roomed house, extra little back-kitchen and all.", "험상궂은 거실 한가운데, 약간 앞으로 몸을 숙이고 두 손을 허벅지에 얹고 팔꿈치를 모로 벌린 채 앉아 있는 그를 보면, 저기 오래 앉아 있다가는 온 식구와 방 네 칸짜리 집 전체를, 별채인 자그마한 뒷부엌까지 통째로 제 몸 안에 빨아들일 듯싶다."]], [["“Do you rub your legs to rub life into ’em?”", "\"다리를 문지르는 건 거기 생기를 돌게 하려는 겁니까?\""], ["he asks of Grandfather Smallweed after looking round the room.", "그가 방 안을 둘러본 뒤 스몰위드 할아버지에게 묻는다."]], [["“Why, it’s partly a habit, Mr. George, and—yes—it partly helps the circulation,”", "\"글쎄, 절반은 버릇이고, 또 — 그래 — 절반은 혈액 순환을 돕는다네.\""], ["he replies.", "그가 답한다."]], [["“The cir-cu-la-tion!”", "\"혈-액-순-환이라!\""], ["repeats Mr. George, folding his arms upon his chest and seeming to become two sizes larger.", "조지 씨가 두 팔을 가슴에 포개며, 몸집이 두 치수쯤 더 커지는 듯 보이며 되뇐다."], ["“Not much of that, I should think.”", "\"그런 거 별로 없을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Truly I’m old, Mr. George,”", "\"내가 늙긴 늙었지, 조지 씨.\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다."], ["“But I can carry my years.", "\"그래도 내 나이는 짊어질 만해."], ["I’m older than HER,”", "난 저 사람보다 늙었어.\""], ["nodding at his wife, “and see what she is?", "아내를 향해 고갯짓하며. \"그런데 저 꼴 좀 보게?"], ["You’re a brimstone chatterer!”", "이 유황불 같은 수다쟁이야!\""], ["with a sudden revival of his late hostility.", "아까의 적의를 별안간 되살리며."]], [["“Unlucky old soul!”", "\"불쌍한 노인네!\""], ["says Mr. George, turning his head in that direction.", "조지 씨가 그쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 말한다."], ["“Don’t scold the old lady.", "\"할머니를 나무라지 마쇼."], ["Look at her here, with her poor cap half off her head and her poor hair all in a muddle.", "여기 이 꼴을 보쇼, 가엾게 모자가 머리에서 반쯤 벗겨지고 머리칼이 죄다 헝클어졌잖소."], ["Hold up, ma’am. That’s better.", "자, 똑바로 앉으세요, 마님."], ["There we are!", "그래야 낫죠."], ["Think of your mother, Mr.", "됐다!"], ["Smallweed,” says Mr.", "어머니를 생각하쇼, 스몰위드 씨.\""], ["George, coming back to his seat from assisting her, “if your wife an’t enough.”", "조지 씨가 할머니를 거든 뒤 제자리로 돌아오며 말한다. \"아내로 모자라거든 말이오.\""]], [["“I suppose you were an excellent son, Mr.", "\"자넨 효자였겠지, 조지 씨?\""], ["George?” the old man hints with a leer.", "노인이 곁눈질하며 넌지시 묻는다."]], [["The colour of Mr.", "조지 씨의 얼굴빛이 다소 짙어지며 답한다."], ["George’s face rather deepens as he replies, “Why no.", "\"글쎄, 아니오."], ["I wasn’t.”", "난 안 그랬소.\""]], [["“I am astonished at it.”", "\"그것참 놀랍군.\""]], [["“So am I.", "\"나도 놀랍소."], ["I ought to have been a good son, and I think I meant to have been one.", "좋은 아들이었어야 했고, 그러려고 마음먹기도 했던 것 같소."], ["But I wasn’t.", "헌데 안 그랬소."], ["I was a thundering bad son, that’s the long and the short of it, and never was a credit to anybody.”", "난 천하에 못된 아들이었어, 그게 한마디로 전부요, 그 누구한테도 자랑거리였던 적이 없소.\""]], [["“Surprising!”", "\"놀랍구먼!\""], ["cries the old man.", "노인이 외친다."]], [["“However,” Mr.", "\"하지만,\""], ["George resumes, “the less said about it, the better now.", "조지 씨가 다시 입을 연다. \"이젠 그 얘긴 안 할수록 좋겠소."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["You know the agreement.", "약속은 아시잖소."], ["Always a pipe out of the two months’ interest! (Bosh!", "두 달 이자에서 늘 파이프 담배 하나!"], ["It’s all correct.", "(헛소리 마쇼!"], ["You needn’t be afraid to order the pipe.", "다 맞소. 파이프 시키는 거 겁낼 거 없소."], ["Here’s the new bill, and here’s the two months’ interest-money, and a devil-and-all of a scrape it is to get it together in my business.)”", "여기 새 어음 있고, 여기 두 달 치 이자 돈 있소, 이걸 내 장사로 긁어모으느라 죽을 똥을 쌌소이다.)\""]], [["Mr. George sits, with his arms folded, consuming the family and the parlour while Grandfather Smallweed is assisted by Judy to two black leathern cases out of a locked bureau, in one of which he secures the document he has just received, and from the other takes another similar document which he hands to Mr.", "조지 씨가 두 팔을 포갠 채 앉아 식구와 거실을 집어삼키는 동안, 스몰위드 할아버지는 주디의 도움으로 자물쇠 채운 책상에서 검은 가죽 상자 두 개를 꺼내, 그중 한 상자엔 방금 받은 서류를 챙겨 넣고, 다른 상자에서는 비슷한 서류 하나를 꺼내 조지 씨에게 건네니, 조지 씨는 그걸 비틀어 말아 파이프 불쏘시개로 삼는다."], ["George, who twists it up for a pipelight. As the old man inspects, through his glasses, every up-stroke and down-stroke of both documents before he releases them from their leathern prison, and as he counts the money three times over and requires Judy to say every word she utters at least twice, and is as tremulously slow of speech and action as it is possible to be, this business is a long time in progress.", "노인이 두 서류를 가죽 감옥에서 풀어주기 전에 안경 너머로 획 하나하나, 올려 긋고 내려 그은 자국 하나하나를 다 살피고, 돈을 세 번이나 거듭 세고, 주디에게는 무슨 말이든 적어도 두 번씩 하라고 요구하며, 말과 행동이 더할 나위 없이 떨리고 굼뜬 까닭에, 이 일은 한참이나 더디게 진행된다."], ["When it is quite concluded, and not before, he disengages his ravenous eyes and fingers from it and answers Mr.", "일이 완전히 끝나고 나서야, 그 전에는 결코 아니고, 그는 게걸스러운 눈과 손가락을 거기서 떼고 조지 씨의 아까 그 말에 답한다."], ["George’s last remark by saying, “Afraid to order the pipe?", "\"파이프 시키는 걸 겁낸다고?"], ["We are not so mercenary as that, sir.", "우리가 그렇게 인색하진 않다네, 이 사람아."], ["Judy, see directly to the pipe and the glass of cold brandy-and-water for Mr. George.”", "주디야, 당장 파이프하고 조지 씨 드릴 차가운 브랜디 물 한 잔 챙겨라.\""]], [["The sportive twins, who have been looking straight before them all this time except when they have been engrossed by the black leathern cases, retire together, generally disdainful of the visitor, but leaving him to the old man as two young cubs might leave a traveller to the parental bear.", "이 장난기 어린 쌍둥이는 그동안 줄곧 — 검은 가죽 상자에 정신이 팔렸을 때만 빼고 — 똑바로 앞만 바라보고 있었는데, 함께 물러가며 대체로 손님을 업신여기되, 마치 새끼 곰 두 마리가 나그네를 어미 곰에게 떠넘기듯 그를 노인 손에 맡겨둔다."]], [["“And there you sit, I suppose, all the day long, eh?”", "\"그래서 영감님은 여기 이렇게 온종일 앉아 있는 거요, 응?\""], ["says Mr. George with folded arms.", "조지 씨가 두 팔을 포갠 채 말한다."]], [["“Just so, just so,”", "\"바로 그렇지, 바로 그래.\""], ["the old man nods.", "노인이 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“And don’t you occupy yourself at all?”", "\"그러고는 아무 일도 안 하고요?\""]], [["“I watch the fire—and the boiling and the roasting—”", "\"불을 지켜본다네 — 끓는 것도, 굽는 것도 —\""]], [["“When there is any,”", "\"있을 때 말이죠.\""], ["says Mr. George with great expression.", "조지 씨가 의미심장하게 말한다."]], [["“Just so.", "\"바로 그렇지."], ["When there is any.”", "있을 때.\""]], [["“Don’t you read or get read to?”", "\"책을 읽거나 누가 읽어주거나 하진 않고요?\""]], [["The old man shakes his head with sharp sly triumph.", "노인이 매섭고 능청맞은 승리감으로 고개를 젓는다."], ["“No, no.", "\"아니, 아니."], ["We have never been readers in our family.", "우리 집안은 책 읽는 사람이 없었어. 그건 남는 게 없거든."], ["It don’t pay.", "부질없는 짓."], ["Stuff.", "게으름."], ["Idleness.", "어리석음이지."], ["Folly. No, no!”", "아니, 아니!\""]], [["“There’s not much to choose between your two states,”", "\"그럼 영감 처지나 할멈 처지나 별로 가릴 게 없네.\""], ["says the visitor in a key too low for the old man’s dull hearing as he looks from him to the old woman and back again.", "손님이 노인의 둔한 귀에는 안 들릴 만큼 낮은 목소리로, 노인에게서 노부인에게로, 다시 노인에게로 시선을 옮기며 말한다."], ["“I say!”", "\"이봐요!\""], ["in a louder voice.", "좀 더 큰 목소리로."]], [["“I hear you.”", "\"듣고 있네.\""]], [["“You’ll sell me up at last, I suppose, when I am a day in arrear.”", "\"내가 하루라도 밀리면 결국 다 팔아넘기겠죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“My dear friend!”", "\"이 친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["cries Grandfather Smallweed, stretching out both hands to embrace him.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 그를 끌어안으려고 두 손을 뻗으며 외친다."], ["“Never!", "\"절대!"], ["Never, my dear friend!", "절대 안 그러지, 친애하는 벗이여!"], ["But my friend in the city that I got to lend you the money—HE might!”", "헌데 자네한테 돈을 빌려주게 해준 시내의 내 친구 — 그 사람은 그럴지도 모르지!\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You can’t answer for him?” says Mr.", "그 사람은 영감님이 보증 못 한다?"], ["George, finishing the inquiry in his lower key with the words “You lying old rascal!”", "'이 거짓말쟁이 늙은 악당아!'\" 조지 씨가 질문을 마저 던지고는 낮은 목소리로 끝맺는다."]], [["“My dear friend, he is not to be depended on.", "\"친애하는 벗이여, 그 사람은 믿을 수가 없어."], ["I wouldn’t trust him.", "나라도 안 믿을 거야."], ["He will have his bond, my dear friend.”", "그 사람은 제 증서대로 받아낼 게야, 친애하는 벗이여.\""]], [["“Devil doubt him,”", "\"그놈이야 의심할 것도 없겠지.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["Charley appearing with a tray, on which are the pipe, a small paper of tobacco, and the brandy-and-water, he asks her, “How do you come here!", "찰리가 파이프와 자그마한 종이 봉지 담배, 브랜디 물을 담은 쟁반을 들고 나타나자, 그가 아이에게 묻는다. \"넌 어쩌다 여기 와 있냐!"], ["You haven’t got the family face.”", "이 집안 얼굴은 아닌데.\""]], [["“I goes out to work, sir,”", "\"전 일하러 나와 있는 거예요, 아저씨.\""], ["returns Charley.", "찰리가 답한다."]], [["The trooper (if trooper he be or have been) takes her bonnet off, with a light touch for so strong a hand, and pats her on the head.", "기병은 (그가 기병이거나 기병이었다면) 그토록 힘센 손치고는 가볍게 아이의 보닛을 벗겨주고 머리를 토닥인다."], ["“You give the house almost a wholesome look.", "\"네가 이 집에 그나마 건강한 기색을 좀 보태는구나."], ["It wants a bit of youth as much as it wants fresh air.”", "이 집은 신선한 공기만큼이나 젊음이 좀 필요하니까.\""], ["Then he dismisses her, lights his pipe, and drinks to Mr. Smallweed’s friend in the city—the one solitary flight of that esteemed old gentleman’s imagination.", "그러고는 아이를 내보내고, 파이프에 불을 붙이고, 시내의 스몰위드 씨 친구를 위해 — 그 점잖은 노신사 상상력이 펼친 단 하나의 외로운 비상(飛翔)을 위해 — 잔을 든다."]], [["“So you think he might be hard upon me, eh?”", "\"그래, 영감님은 그 사람이 나한테 모질게 굴 거라고 보는 거요, 응?\""]], [["“I think he might—I am afraid he would.", "\"그럴 것 같아 — 그럴까 봐 걱정이야."], ["I have known him do it,”", "그 사람이 그러는 걸 내가 봤거든.\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed incautiously, “twenty times.”", "스몰위드 할아버지가 부주의하게 말한다. \"스무 번은.\""]], [["Incautiously, because his stricken better-half, who has been dozing over the fire for some time, is instantly aroused and jabbers “Twenty thousand pounds, twenty twenty-pound notes in a money-box, twenty guineas, twenty million twenty per cent, twenty—”", "부주의하다 함은, 한동안 불 앞에서 졸던 망가진 그의 반쪽이 즉각 깨어나 지껄여대기 때문이니, \"이만 파운드, 돈궤 속 이십 파운드짜리 지폐 스무 장, 이십 기니, 이천만의 이십 퍼센트, 스물—\""], ["and is then cut short by the flying cushion, which the visitor, to whom this singular experiment appears to be a novelty, snatches from her face as it crushes her in the usual manner.", "하다가 날아드는 방석에 말이 뚝 끊긴다. 이 진기한 실험이 처음인 듯싶은 손님이, 여느 때처럼 그녀를 짓뭉개는 그 방석을 그녀 얼굴에서 낚아챈다."]], [["“You’re a brimstone idiot.", "\"이 유황불 같은 멍청이."], ["You’re a scorpion—a brimstone scorpion!", "넌 전갈이야 — 유황불 같은 전갈!"], ["You’re a sweltering toad.", "푹푹 삶기는 두꺼비라고."], ["You’re a chattering clattering broomstick witch that ought to be burnt!”", "화형당해 마땅한, 종알대고 덜그럭대는 빗자루 마녀야!\""], ["gasps the old man, prostrate in his chair.", "노인이 의자에 널브러진 채 헐떡인다."], ["“My dear friend, will you shake me up a little?”", "\"친애하는 벗이여, 날 좀 흔들어 주겠나?\""]], [["Mr. George, who has been looking first at one of them and then at the other, as if he were demented, takes his venerable acquaintance by the throat on receiving this request, and dragging him upright in his chair as easily as if he were a doll, appears in two minds whether or no to shake all future power of cushioning out of him and shake him into his grave.", "조지 씨는, 마치 미친 사람 구경하듯 한 사람을 봤다 또 한 사람을 봤다 하던 차에, 이 부탁을 받자 노쇠한 지인의 멱살을 잡고는, 인형이라도 되는 양 손쉽게 의자에 똑바로 끌어 일으키더니, 다시는 방석 던질 힘이 안 남도록 그를 마저 흔들어 무덤으로 보내버릴까 말까 두 마음을 품은 듯하다."], ["Resisting the temptation, but agitating him violently enough to make his head roll like a harlequin’s, he puts him smartly down in his chair again and adjusts his skull-cap with such a rub that the old man winks with both eyes for a minute afterwards.", "유혹은 물리치되, 그의 머리가 어릿광대 머리처럼 까딱이도록 사납게 흔들고 나서, 다시 보기 좋게 의자에 앉히고는 두건을 어찌나 박박 문질러 바로잡는지, 노인이 그 뒤로 한참 두 눈을 끔뻑인다."]], [["“O Lord!”", "\"아이고!\""], ["gasps Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 헐떡인다."], ["“That’ll do.", "\"그만하면 됐네."], ["Thank you, my dear friend, that’ll do.", "고맙네, 친애하는 벗이여, 그만하면 돼."], ["Oh, dear me, I’m out of breath.", "아이고, 숨이 다 차는구먼."], ["O Lord!”", "아이고!\""], ["And Mr. Smallweed says it not without evident apprehensions of his dear friend, who still stands over him looming larger than ever.", "그러고 스몰위드 씨가 이 말을 하는 데는, 여전히 어느 때보다 거대하게 자기 위로 우뚝 솟아 있는 친애하는 벗에 대한 두려움이 역력히 묻어 있다."]], [["The alarming presence, however, gradually subsides into its chair and falls to smoking in long puffs, consoling itself with the philosophical reflection, “The name of your friend in the city begins with a D, comrade, and you’re about right respecting the bond.”", "그러나 그 위협적인 존재는 점차 제 의자로 가라앉아 길게 뻐끔뻐끔 담배를 피우기 시작하더니, 다음과 같은 철학적 성찰로 스스로를 달랜다. \"시내의 자네 친구 이름이 D로 시작하는구려, 동지, 그리고 증서에 관해선 자네 말이 어지간히 맞소.\""]], [["“Did you speak, Mr. George?”", "\"무슨 말 했나, 조지 씨?\""], ["inquires the old man.", "노인이 묻는다."]], [["The trooper shakes his head, and leaning forward with his right elbow on his right knee and his pipe supported in that hand, while his other hand, resting on his left leg, squares his left elbow in a martial manner, continues to smoke.", "기병은 고개를 젓고, 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 오른 무릎에 얹고 그 손으로 파이프를 받친 채 몸을 앞으로 숙이는 한편, 왼다리에 얹은 다른 손으로 왼쪽 팔꿈치를 군인답게 모로 세우고서 계속 담배를 피운다."], ["Meanwhile he looks at Mr. Smallweed with grave attention and now and then fans the cloud of smoke away in order that he may see him the more clearly.", "그러는 동안 그는 진지한 주의를 기울여 스몰위드 씨를 바라보고, 이따금 그를 더 또렷이 보려고 담배 연기 구름을 부채질해 흩는다."]], [["“I take it,”", "\"내 생각엔 말이오,\""], ["he says, making just as much and as little change in his position as will enable him to reach the glass to his lips with a round, full action, “that I am the only man alive (or dead either) that gets the value of a pipe out of YOU?”", "그가 자세를 딱 그만큼만 바꿔 둥글고 묵직한 동작으로 잔을 입에 가져갈 수 있게 하며 말한다. \"영감님한테서 파이프 한 대만큼의 값이라도 뽑아내는 건 산 사람 (죽은 사람 통틀어도) 가운데 나 하나뿐인 것 같소만?\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["returns the old man, “it’s true that I don’t see company, Mr.", "노인이 받는다. \"내가 손님을 안 들이고 대접을 안 하는 건 사실이지, 조지 씨."], ["George, and that I don’t treat.", "그럴 형편이 못 되거든."], ["I can’t afford to it. But as you, in your pleasant way, made your pipe a condition—”", "헌데 자네가, 그 유쾌한 식으로, 파이프를 조건으로 걸었으니—\""]], [["“Why, it’s not for the value of it; that’s no great thing.", "\"뭐, 그 값 때문은 아니오. 그거야 대단한 것도 아니지."], ["It was a fancy to get it out of you.", "영감님한테서 뭐라도 뜯어내고 싶은 마음이었소."], ["To have something in for my money.”", "내 돈값으로 뭐라도 받아두자고.\""]], [["“Ha!", "\"하!"], ["You’re prudent, prudent, sir!”", "자넨 신중하구먼, 신중해, 이 사람아!\""], ["cries Grandfather Smallweed, rubbing his legs.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 다리를 문지르며 외친다."]], [["“Very.", "\"무척요."], ["I always was.”", "난 늘 그랬소.\""], ["Puff.", "뻐끔."], ["“It’s a sure sign of my prudence that I ever found the way here.”", "\"내가 여기까지 길을 찾아 들어온 것부터가 내 신중함의 확실한 증거지.\""], ["Puff.", "뻐끔."], ["“Also, that I am what I am.”", "\"또, 지금 이 모양 이 꼴인 것도.\""], ["Puff.", "뻐끔."], ["“I am well known to be prudent,”", "\"난 신중하기로 소문이 자자하오.\""], ["says Mr. George, composedly smoking.", "조지 씨가 태연히 담배를 피우며 말한다."], ["“I rose in life that way.”", "\"그래서 그렇게 출세했지.\""]], [["“Don’t be down-hearted, sir.", "\"기죽지 말게, 이 사람아."], ["You may rise yet.”", "자넨 아직 출세할 수 있어.\""]], [["Mr. George laughs and drinks.", "조지 씨가 웃고는 술을 마신다."]], [["“Ha’n’t you no relations, now,”", "\"이제, 친척 없나,\""], ["asks Grandfather Smallweed with a twinkle in his eyes, “who would pay off this little principal or who would lend you a good name or two that I could persuade my friend in the city to make you a further advance upon?", "스몰위드 할아버지가 눈을 반짝이며 묻는다. \"이 자그마한 원금을 갚아주거나, 보증인 이름 한둘을 빌려주면 내가 시내 친구를 설득해 자네한테 돈을 더 빌려주게 할 수 있는 그런 친척 말이야?"], ["Two good names would be sufficient for my friend in the city.", "시내 친구한텐 좋은 보증인 이름 둘이면 충분하거든."], ["Ha’n’t you no such relations, Mr. George?”", "그런 친척 없나, 조지 씨?\""]], [["Mr. George, still composedly smoking, replies, “If I had, I shouldn’t trouble them.", "조지 씨는 여전히 태연히 담배를 피우며 답한다."], ["I have been trouble enough to my belongings in my day.", "\"있어도, 그 사람들을 귀찮게 하진 않겠소."], ["It MAY be a very good sort of penitence in a vagabond, who has wasted the best time of his life, to go back then to decent people that he never was a credit to and live upon them, but it’s not my sort.", "내 한창때 내 식구들한테 끼친 폐만으로도 차고 넘치니까. 인생의 가장 좋은 시절을 허투루 흘려보낸 부랑자가, 한 번도 자랑거리였던 적 없는 점잖은 사람들한테 돌아가 빌붙어 사는 게 아주 좋은 종류의 참회일지는 모르겠소만, 그건 내 식이 아니오."], ["The best kind of amends then for having gone away is to keep away, in my opinion.”", "떠나버린 데 대한 최선의 보상은 계속 떨어져 있는 거다, 그게 내 생각이오.\""]], [["“But natural affection, Mr. George,”", "\"하지만 천륜의 정이란 게 있잖나, 조지 씨.\""], ["hints Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 넌지시 떠본다."]], [["“For two good names, hey?” says Mr.", "\"좋은 보증인 이름 둘 때문에, 응?\""], ["George, shaking his head and still composedly smoking. “No.", "조지 씨가 고개를 저으며 여전히 태연히 담배를 피운다."], ["That’s not my sort either.”", "\"아니오. 그것도 내 식이 아니오.\""]], [["Grandfather Smallweed has been gradually sliding down in his chair since his last adjustment and is now a bundle of clothes with a voice in it calling for Judy.", "스몰위드 할아버지는 아까 바로잡힌 뒤로 차츰 의자에서 미끄러져 내려와, 이제는 주디를 부르는 목소리가 담긴 옷보따리 신세다."], ["That houri, appearing, shakes him up in the usual manner and is charged by the old gentleman to remain near him.", "그 천녀(天女)가 나타나 여느 때처럼 그를 흔들어 일으키니, 노신사는 그녀에게 제 곁에 머물러 있으라 이른다."], ["For he seems chary of putting his visitor to the trouble of repeating his late attentions.", "손님에게 아까 그 수고를 또 끼치기는 꺼려지는 모양이다."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["he observes when he is in trim again.", "그가 다시 매무새를 추스르고 말한다."], ["“If you could have traced out the captain, Mr. George, it would have been the making of you.", "\"자네가 그 대위를 찾아냈더라면, 조지 씨, 그게 자네 팔자를 폈을 텐데."], ["If when you first came here, in consequence of our advertisement in the newspapers—when I say ‘our,’ I’m alluding to the advertisements of my friend in the city, and one or two others who embark their capital in the same way, and are so friendly towards me as sometimes to give me a lift with my little pittance—if at that time you could have helped us, Mr. George, it would have been the making of you.”", "자네가 처음 여기 왔을 때, 우리가 신문에 낸 광고를 보고 말이야 — '우리'라고 할 때 나는 시내 내 친구와, 같은 식으로 제 자본을 굴리며 가끔 내 쥐꼬리만 한 밑천에 보탬을 줄 만큼 나한테 우호적인 한두 사람의 광고를 가리키는 거네만 — 그때 자네가 우릴 도울 수 있었더라면, 조지 씨, 그게 자네 팔자를 폈을 거란 말이야.\""]], [["“I was willing enough to be ‘made,’ as you call it,”", "\"영감님 말대로 '팔자 펴는' 거라면 나야 얼마든지 응할 마음이었소.\""], ["says Mr.", "조지 씨가 아까만큼 평온하게 담배를 피우지는 못하며 말한다."], ["George, smoking not quite so placidly as before, for since the entrance of Judy he has been in some measure disturbed by a fascination, not of the admiring kind, which obliges him to look at her as she stands by her grandfather’s chair, “but on the whole, I am glad I wasn’t now.”", "주디가 들어온 뒤로 어느 정도 마음이 어지러워졌으니, 흠모와는 거리가 먼 어떤 끌림 때문에 그녀가 조부의 의자 곁에 서 있는 모습을 자꾸 바라보지 않을 수 없는 탓이다. \"하지만 다 따지고 보면, 이젠 안 그랬던 게 기쁘오.\""]], [["“Why, Mr. George?", "\"왜, 조지 씨?"], ["In the name of—of brimstone, why?”", "대체 — 유황불 같은 무슨 까닭으로, 왜?\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed with a plain appearance of exasperation.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 드러내놓고 분통을 터뜨리며 말한다."], ["(Brimstone apparently suggested by his eye lighting on Mrs. Smallweed in her slumber.)", "(유황불은 아무래도 잠든 스몰위드 부인에게 눈길이 닿은 데서 떠오른 모양이다.)"]], [["“For two reasons, comrade.”", "\"두 가지 이유 때문이오, 동지.\""]], [["“And what two reasons, Mr. George?", "\"그래, 무슨 두 가지 이유야, 조지 씨?"], ["In the name of the—”", "대체 그—\""]], [["“Of our friend in the city?”", "\"시내 우리 친구의 이름으로?\""], ["suggests Mr. George, composedly drinking.", "조지 씨가 태연히 술을 마시며 거든다."]], [["“Aye, if you like.", "\"오냐, 그렇게 하든지."], ["What two reasons?”", "무슨 두 가지 이유?\""]], [["“In the first place,” returns Mr.", "\"첫째로는,\""], ["George, but still looking at Judy as if she being so old and so like her grandfather it is indifferent which of the two he addresses, “you gentlemen took me in.", "조지 씨가 받지만, 여전히 주디를 바라보면서다. 그녀가 워낙 늙어 조부를 빼닮은 터라 둘 중 누구한테 말을 거는지 아무래도 상관없다는 듯이."], ["You advertised that Mr.", "\"당신네 양반들이 날 속였소."], ["Hawdon (Captain Hawdon, if you hold to the saying ‘Once a captain, always a captain’) was to hear of something to his advantage.”", "당신네가 광고하기를 호든 씨가 — '한 번 대위면 영원히 대위'라는 말을 고집한다면 호든 대위가 — 자기한테 득 될 소식을 듣게 될 거라고 했잖소.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""], ["returns the old man shrilly and sharply.", "노인이 새되고 날카롭게 받는다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래서!\""], ["says Mr. George, smoking on.", "조지 씨가 계속 담배를 피우며 말한다."], ["“It wouldn’t have been much to his advantage to have been clapped into prison by the whole bill and judgment trade of London.”", "\"런던의 어음과 판결 장사치 떼한테 몽땅 감옥에 처박히는 게 그 사람한테 그리 득 될 일은 아니었을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["“How do you know that?", "\"그걸 자네가 어찌 아나?"], ["Some of his rich relations might have paid his debts or compounded for ’em.", "그 사람 부유한 친척 누군가가 빚을 갚아줬거나 화의(和議)로 처리했을지도 모르지."], ["Besides, he had taken US in.", "게다가 그 사람은 우릴 속였어."], ["He owed us immense sums all round.", "사방에 우리한테 어마어마한 빚을 졌단 말이야."], ["I would sooner have strangled him than had no return.", "한 푼도 못 받느니 차라리 내가 목을 졸랐을 거야."], ["If I sit here thinking of him,”", "여기 앉아 그 사람 생각을 하노라면,\""], ["snarls the old man, holding up his impotent ten fingers, “I want to strangle him now.”", "노인이 무력한 열 손가락을 들어 올리며 으르렁댄다. \"지금이라도 목을 조르고 싶구먼.\""], ["And in a sudden access of fury, he throws the cushion at the unoffending Mrs. Smallweed, but it passes harmlessly on one side of her chair.", "그러고는 별안간 치솟는 분노에 죄 없는 스몰위드 부인에게 방석을 던지지만, 그것은 그녀 의자 옆으로 무사히 비껴간다."]], [["“I don’t need to be told,”", "\"그 사람이 무겁게 살다 파멸로 치달았다는 거야 굳이 들을 필요도 없소.\""], ["returns the trooper, taking his pipe from his lips for a moment and carrying his eyes back from following the progress of the cushion to the pipe-bowl which is burning low, “that he carried on heavily and went to ruin.", "기병이 잠시 입에서 파이프를 떼고, 방석의 행로를 좇던 눈길을 다 타들어 사그라지는 담뱃대 통으로 도로 거두며 받는다."], ["I have been at his right hand many a day when he was charging upon ruin full-gallop.", "\"그 사람이 파멸을 향해 전속력으로 돌격할 때 난 여러 날 그 사람 오른편에 있었소."], ["I was with him when he was sick and well, rich and poor.", "아플 때나 성할 때나, 부유할 때나 가난할 때나 함께였지."], ["I laid this hand upon him after he had run through everything and broken down everything beneath him—when he held a pistol to his head.”", "그 사람이 모든 걸 탕진하고 발밑의 모든 걸 무너뜨린 뒤 — 머리에 권총을 들이댔을 때 — 내가 이 손을 그 사람한테 얹었소.\""]], [["“I wish he had let it off,”", "\"그때 방아쇠를 당겼더라면 좋았을걸.\""], ["says the benevolent old man, “and blown his head into as many pieces as he owed pounds!”", "그 자비로운 노인이 말한다. \"지가 진 빚 파운드 수만큼 머리통이 산산조각 나도록 말이야!\""]], [["“That would have been a smash indeed,”", "\"그랬으면 정말 박살이 났겠지.\" 기병이 냉정하게 받는다."], ["returns the trooper coolly; “any way, he had been young, hopeful, and handsome in the days gone by, and I am glad I never found him, when he was neither, to lead to a result so much to his advantage.", "\"어쨌거나 그 사람도 지난날엔 젊고 희망차고 잘생긴 사람이었소, 그러니 그 사람이 그 무엇도 아니게 됐을 때 그렇게나 '득 될' 결말로 끌고 갈 만큼 끝내 못 찾아낸 게 다행이오."], ["That’s reason number one.”", "그게 첫째 이유요.\""]], [["“I hope number two’s as good?”", "\"둘째 이유도 그만큼 잘난 거겠지?\""], ["snarls the old man.", "노인이 으르렁댄다."]], [["“Why, no.", "\"뭐, 아니오."], ["It’s more of a selfish reason.", "그건 좀 더 이기적인 이유요."], ["If I had found him, I must have gone to the other world to look.", "내가 그 사람을 찾았으려면 저세상까지 찾으러 가야 했을 테니까."], ["He was there.”", "그 사람은 거기 있었소.\""]], [["“How do you know he was there?”", "\"그 사람이 거기 있는 줄 자네가 어찌 아나?\""]], [["“He wasn’t here.”", "\"여기 없었으니까.\""]], [["“How do you know he wasn’t here?”", "\"여기 없는 줄을 자네가 어찌 알아?\""]], [["“Don’t lose your temper as well as your money,” says Mr.", "\"돈에다 성질까지 같이 잃지는 마쇼.\""], ["George, calmly knocking the ashes out of his pipe.", "조지 씨가 침착하게 파이프 재를 털며 말한다."], ["“He was drowned long before.", "\"그 사람은 한참 전에 물에 빠져 죽었소."], ["I am convinced of it.", "난 그렇게 확신하오."], ["He went over a ship’s side.", "뱃전 너머로 넘어갔거든."], ["Whether intentionally or accidentally, I don’t know.", "일부러였는지 사고였는지는 모르오."], ["Perhaps your friend in the city does.", "어쩌면 시내의 영감님 친구는 알지도 모르고."], ["Do you know what that tune is, Mr. Smallweed?”", "이 가락이 무슨 가락인지 아시오, 스몰위드 씨?\""], ["he adds after breaking off to whistle one, accompanied on the table with the empty pipe.", "그가 말을 끊고 빈 파이프로 식탁을 두드려 가락 하나를 휘파람으로 분 뒤 덧붙인다."]], [["“Tune!”", "\"가락!\""], ["replied the old man.", "노인이 답한다. \"아니."], ["“No. We never have tunes here.”", "우리 집엔 가락이라곤 없어.\""]], [["“That’s the Dead March in Saul.", "\"이게 「사울」에 나오는 장송곡이오."], ["They bury soldiers to it, so it’s the natural end of the subject.", "군인들을 이 가락에 맞춰 묻지요, 그러니 이 화제의 자연스러운 마무리겠소."], ["Now, if your pretty granddaughter—excuse me, miss—will condescend to take care of this pipe for two months, we shall save the cost of one next time.", "자, 영감님의 예쁜 손녀가 — 미안하오, 아가씨 — 황송하게도 이 파이프를 두 달간 보관해준다면, 다음번엔 하나 값을 아끼겠소."], ["Good evening, Mr. Smallweed!”", "그럼 안녕히 계시오, 스몰위드 씨!\""]], [["“My dear friend!”", "\"친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["the old man gives him both his hands.", "노인이 그에게 두 손을 다 내민다."]], [["“So you think your friend in the city will be hard upon me if I fall in a payment?”", "\"그래서 내가 한 번 갚을 걸 못 내면 시내 영감님 친구가 나한테 모질게 굴 거다, 이거요?\""], ["says the trooper, looking down upon him like a giant.", "기병이 거인처럼 그를 내려다보며 말한다."]], [["“My dear friend, I am afraid he will,”", "\"친애하는 벗이여, 그럴까 봐 걱정이야.\""], ["returns the old man, looking up at him like a pygmy.", "노인이 난쟁이처럼 그를 올려다보며 받는다."]], [["Mr. George laughs, and with a glance at Mr. Smallweed and a parting salutation to the scornful Judy, strides out of the parlour, clashing imaginary sabres and other metallic appurtenances as he goes.", "조지 씨가 웃더니, 스몰위드 씨를 한 번 흘끗 보고 멸시하는 주디에게 작별 인사를 던지고는, 거실 밖으로 성큼성큼 걸어 나가며 가는 길에 상상의 군도와 그 밖의 쇠붙이 장구들을 쟁그랑쟁그랑 부딪친다."]], [["“You’re a damned rogue,”", "\"넌 빌어먹을 악당이야.\""], ["says the old gentleman, making a hideous grimace at the door as he shuts it.", "노신사가 문을 닫으며 문을 향해 흉측한 표정을 짓고 말한다."], ["“But I’ll lime you, you dog, I’ll lime you!”", "\"하지만 끈끈이로 너를 잡아주마, 이 개자식, 끈끈이로 잡아주고말고!\""]], [["After this amiable remark, his spirit soars into those enchanting regions of reflection which its education and pursuits have opened to it, and again he and Mrs. Smallweed while away the rosy hours, two unrelieved sentinels forgotten as aforesaid by the Black Serjeant.", "이 다정한 한마디 뒤로 그의 정신은, 그것의 교육과 추구가 열어준 저 황홀한 사색의 영역으로 솟아오르고, 또다시 그와 스몰위드 부인은 장밋빛 시간을 흘려보내니, 앞서 말한 대로 검은 하사관이 잊어버린 채 내버려둔, 누구의 교대도 받지 못한 두 보초병이다."]], [["While the twain are faithful to their post, Mr. George strides through the streets with a massive kind of swagger and a grave-enough face.", "이 둘이 제 초소를 충실히 지키는 동안, 조지 씨는 묵직한 호기를 부리며 자못 심각한 얼굴로 거리를 성큼성큼 누빈다."], ["It is eight o’clock now, and the day is fast drawing in.", "이제 여덟 시요 날이 빠르게 저문다."], ["He stops hard by Waterloo Bridge and reads a playbill, decides to go to Astley’s Theatre.", "그는 워털루 다리 바로 옆에 멈춰 공연 전단을 읽고 애스틀리 극장에 가기로 마음먹는다."], ["Being there, is much delighted with the horses and the feats of strength; looks at the weapons with a critical eye; disapproves of the combats as giving evidences of unskilful swordsmanship; but is touched home by the sentiments.", "거기 가서는 말들과 묘기에 무척 즐거워하고, 무기들은 비평가의 눈으로 뜯어보며, 결투 장면은 서툰 검술 솜씨를 드러낸다고 못마땅해하되, 그 정서에는 가슴이 뭉클해진다."], ["In the last scene, when the Emperor of Tartary gets up into a cart and condescends to bless the united lovers by hovering over them with the Union Jack, his eyelashes are moistened with emotion.", "마지막 장면에서, 타타르의 황제가 수레에 올라타 황송하게도 유니언 잭을 든 채 두 연인 위를 맴돌며 그들을 축복하자, 그의 속눈썹이 감격으로 촉촉이 젖는다."]], [["The theatre over, Mr. George comes across the water again and makes his way to that curious region lying about the Haymarket and Leicester Square which is a centre of attraction to indifferent foreign hotels and indifferent foreigners, racket-courts, fighting-men, swordsmen, footguards, old china, gaming-houses, exhibitions, and a large medley of shabbiness and shrinking out of sight.", "극이 끝나자 조지 씨는 다시 강을 건너, 헤이마켓과 레스터 광장 일대에 펼쳐진 저 기묘한 구역으로 향하니, 거기는 변변찮은 외국인 호텔과 변변찮은 외국인들, 라켓 코트, 격투가, 검객, 보병 근위병, 골동 도자기, 도박장, 흥행물, 그리고 후줄근함과 남의 눈을 피해 움츠러든 것들이 잔뜩 뒤섞여 끌려드는 중심지다."], ["Penetrating to the heart of this region, he arrives by a court and a long whitewashed passage at a great brick building composed of bare walls, floors, roof-rafters, and skylights, on the front of which, if it can be said to have any front, is painted GEORGE’S SHOOTING GALLERY, &c.", "이 구역의 심장부까지 파고들어, 그는 안마당 하나와 회칠한 긴 통로를 지나 큼직한 벽돌 건물에 다다르니, 건물은 휑한 벽과 마룻바닥, 지붕 서까래, 채광창으로 이루어져 있고, 그 정면에는 — 정면이랄 게 있다 치면 — 「조지의 사격장 외(外)」라고 적혀 있다."]], [["Into George’s Shooting Gallery, &c., he goes; and in it there are gaslights (partly turned off now), and two whitened targets for rifle-shooting, and archery accommodation, and fencing appliances, and all necessaries for the British art of boxing.", "「조지의 사격장 외」 안으로 그가 들어선다."], ["None of these sports or exercises being pursued in George’s Shooting Gallery to-night, which is so devoid of company that a little grotesque man with a large head has it all to himself and lies asleep upon the floor.", "그 안에는 가스등이 (지금은 일부 꺼져 있다) 켜져 있고, 소총 사격용으로 희게 칠한 표적 두 개와 양궁 시설, 펜싱 기구, 그리고 권투라는 영국 기예에 필요한 일체가 갖춰져 있다. 오늘 밤 「조지의 사격장」에서는 이런 운동이나 연습이 하나도 행해지지 않으니, 워낙 손님이 없어 큰 머리를 한 자그맣고 괴상한 사내 하나가 그곳을 통째로 독차지하고 바닥에 누워 잠들어 있다."]], [["The little man is dressed something like a gunsmith, in a green-baize apron and cap; and his face and hands are dirty with gunpowder and begrimed with the loading of guns.", "그 자그마한 사내는 총포 만드는 장인처럼 녹색 천 앞치마와 모자를 걸쳤고, 얼굴과 손은 화약으로 더럽고 총에 화약 재는 일로 새카맣게 그을렸다."], ["As he lies in the light before a glaring white target, the black upon him shines again.", "눈부시게 흰 표적 앞 불빛 속에 누워 있으니, 그에게 묻은 검댕이 다시 번들거린다."], ["Not far off is the strong, rough, primitive table with a vice upon it at which he has been working.", "멀지 않은 곳엔 그가 일하던, 바이스가 물려 있는 튼튼하고 거칠고 투박한 작업대가 있다."], ["He is a little man with a face all crushed together, who appears, from a certain blue and speckled appearance that one of his cheeks presents, to have been blown up, in the way of business, at some odd time or times.", "그는 얼굴이 온통 한데 짜부라진 자그마한 사내인데, 한쪽 뺨에 보이는 어떤 푸르스름하고 얼룩덜룩한 모양새로 미루어, 일하다 어쩌다 한두 차례 폭발 사고를 당한 듯하다."]], [["“Phil!”", "\"필!\""], ["says the trooper in a quiet voice.", "기병이 조용한 목소리로 부른다."]], [["“All right!”", "\"네!\""], ["cries Phil, scrambling to his feet.", "필이 허둥지둥 일어서며 외친다."]], [["“Anything been doing?”", "\"무슨 일이라도 있었나?\""]], [["“Flat as ever so much swipes,”", "\"맥주처럼 김 빠진 하루였죠.\""], ["says Phil.", "필이 말한다."], ["“Five dozen rifle and a dozen pistol.", "\"소총 다섯 다스에 권총 한 다스."], ["As to aim!”", "한데 그 조준 솜씨라니!\""], ["Phil gives a howl at the recollection.", "필이 그걸 떠올리며 한 차례 울부짖는다."]], [["“Shut up shop, Phil!”", "\"가게 닫아라, 필!\""]], [["As Phil moves about to execute this order, it appears that he is lame, though able to move very quickly.", "필이 이 명령을 따르려고 움직이는 걸 보면, 그가 절름발이임을 알 수 있다."], ["On the speckled side of his face he has no eyebrow, and on the other side he has a bushy black one, which want of uniformity gives him a very singular and rather sinister appearance.", "그래도 아주 잽싸게 움직이긴 한다. 얼룩진 쪽 얼굴엔 눈썹이 없고, 다른 쪽엔 무성한 검은 눈썹이 있어, 이 좌우의 불균형이 그에게 자못 기묘하고 다소 음흉한 인상을 준다."], ["Everything seems to have happened to his hands that could possibly take place consistently with the retention of all the fingers, for they are notched, and seamed, and crumpled all over.", "그의 두 손엔 손가락을 모조리 보전하는 한도 안에서 일어날 수 있는 일은 죄다 일어난 듯하니, 온통 베이고 갈라지고 쭈글쭈글하다."], ["He appears to be very strong and lifts heavy benches about as if he had no idea what weight was.", "그는 무척 힘이 센 듯, 무게가 무언지조차 모르는 양 무거운 의자들을 번쩍번쩍 들어 옮긴다."], ["He has a curious way of limping round the gallery with his shoulder against the wall and tacking off at objects he wants to lay hold of instead of going straight to them, which has left a smear all round the four walls, conventionally called “Phil’s mark.”", "그는 사격장을 절뚝거리며 도는 묘한 버릇이 있어, 어깨를 벽에 댄 채 돌다가 붙잡고 싶은 물건이 있으면 곧장 그리로 가지 않고 빗겨 다가가니, 그 때문에 네 벽을 빙 둘러 자국이 하나 남았는데, 흔히들 \"필의 자국\"이라 부른다."]], [["This custodian of George’s Gallery in George’s absence concludes his proceedings, when he has locked the great doors and turned out all the lights but one, which he leaves to glimmer, by dragging out from a wooden cabin in a corner two mattresses and bedding.", "조지가 없는 동안 「조지의 사격장」을 지키는 이 파수꾼은, 큰 문을 잠그고 등불 하나만 남겨 깜박이게 두고 나머지를 모두 끈 다음, 구석의 나무 칸막이 방에서 매트리스 두 장과 침구를 끌어내는 것으로 제 일과를 마무리한다."], ["These being drawn to opposite ends of the gallery, the trooper makes his own bed and Phil makes his.", "이것들을 사격장 양 끝으로 끌어다 놓고, 기병은 제 잠자리를 깔고 필은 제 잠자리를 깐다."]], [["“Phil!”", "\"필!\""], ["says the master, walking towards him without his coat and waistcoat, and looking more soldierly than ever in his braces.", "주인이 외투와 조끼를 벗고 그에게 걸어오며 부르니, 멜빵 차림이라 어느 때보다 군인다워 보인다."], ["“You were found in a doorway, weren’t you?”", "\"넌 출입구에 버려져 있었다고 했지?\""]], [["“Gutter,”", "\"개천에요.\""], ["says Phil.", "필이 말한다."], ["“Watchman tumbled over me.”", "\"야경꾼이 절 보고 자빠졌죠.\""]], [["“Then vagabondizing came natural to YOU from the beginning.”", "\"그럼 부랑 생활이야 너한텐 처음부터 타고난 거였구나.\""]], [["“As nat’ral as possible,”", "\"더없이 타고난 거였죠.\""], ["says Phil.", "필이 말한다."]], [["“Good night!”", "\"잘 자라!\""]], [["“Good night, guv’ner.”", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 주인님.\""]], [["Phil cannot even go straight to bed, but finds it necessary to shoulder round two sides of the gallery and then tack off at his mattress.", "필은 잠자리에도 곧장 들지 못하고, 사격장 두 면을 어깨로 휘돌고 나서야 제 매트리스로 빗겨 들어야 직성이 풀린다."], ["The trooper, after taking a turn or two in the rifle-distance and looking up at the moon now shining through the skylights, strides to his own mattress by a shorter route and goes to bed too.", "기병은 사격 거리를 한두 차례 거닐고 이제 채광창으로 비쳐드는 달을 올려다본 뒤, 더 짧은 길로 제 매트리스로 성큼 다가가 역시 잠자리에 든다."]], [["Mr. Bucket\nAllegory looks pretty cool in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, though the evening is hot, for both Mr.", "링컨 법학원 광장에서는 우의화 인물이 제법 시원해 보인다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s windows are wide open, and the room is lofty, gusty, and gloomy.", "저녁 공기는 후텁지근하건만, 토킹혼 씨의 창문 두 짝이 활짝 열려 있는 데다 방 자체가 높다랗고 바람이 잘 통하며 음침하기 때문이다."], ["These may not be desirable characteristics when November comes with fog and sleet or January with ice and snow, but they have their merits in the sultry long vacation weather.", "안개와 진눈깨비가 몰려드는 십일월이나 얼음과 눈이 쌓이는 일월이 닥치면 이런 특성이 그리 달갑지 않겠지만, 무덥고 긴 휴정기 날씨에는 나름의 미덕이 있다."], ["They enable Allegory, though it has cheeks like peaches, and knees like bunches of blossoms, and rosy swellings for calves to its legs and muscles to its arms, to look tolerably cool to-night.", "그 덕분에, 복숭아 같은 뺨에 꽃송이 다발 같은 무릎, 종아리에는 발그레한 살집이 오르고 팔에는 근육이 붙은 우의화 인물도 오늘 밤은 그런대로 시원해 보이는 것이다."]], [["Plenty of dust comes in at Mr. Tulkinghorn’s windows, and plenty more has generated among his furniture and papers.", "토킹혼 씨의 창문으로는 먼지가 듬뿍 들어오고, 그의 가구와 서류 틈에서도 먼지가 그득그득 생겨난다."], ["It lies thick everywhere.", "어디나 먼지가 두텁게 쌓여 있다."], ["When a breeze from the country that has lost its way takes fright and makes a blind hurry to rush out again, it flings as much dust in the eyes of Allegory as the law—or Mr. Tulkinghorn, one of its trustiest representatives—may scatter, on occasion, in the eyes of the laity.", "길을 잃은 시골 미풍 한 줄기가 놀라 허둥지둥 다시 빠져나가려 할 때면, 우의화 인물의 두 눈에 먼지를 잔뜩 끼얹는데, 그건 법이—아니 그 법의 가장 믿음직한 대리인 가운데 하나인 토킹혼 씨가—이따금 일반 백성의 눈에 먼지를 뿌리는 양과 어금지금하다."]], [["In his lowering magazine of dust, the universal article into which his papers and himself, and all his clients, and all things of earth, animate and inanimate, are resolving, Mr.", "서류도, 그 자신도, 그의 모든 의뢰인도, 산 것이든 죽은 것이든 세상 만물이 다 같이 녹아들어 가는 그 보편의 물질, 곧 음침하게 쌓인 먼지 창고 한가운데서, 토킹혼 씨는 열린 창문 가에 앉아 묵은 포트와인 한 병을 즐긴다. 깐깐하고 빈틈없고 메마르고 과묵한 사람이지만, 그도 묵은 포도주를 누구 못지않게 즐길 줄 안다."], ["Tulkinghorn sits at one of the open windows enjoying a bottle of old port.", "그에게는 광장 아래 어느 은밀한 지하실에 값을 매길 수 없는 포트와인 저장고가 있는데, 이것이 그의 숱한 비밀 가운데 하나다."], ["Though a hard-grained man, close, dry, and silent, he can enjoy old wine with the best. He has a priceless bin of port in some artful cellar under the Fields, which is one of his many secrets.", "오늘처럼 사무실에서 혼자 식사하며 커피하우스에서 생선 한 토막과 스테이크나 닭고기를 들여올 때면, 그는 촛불을 들고 인적 끊긴 저택 아래 메아리치는 영역으로 내려가, 멀리서 천둥치듯 문 닫히는 소리를 앞세우고는, 흙냄새 풍기는 기운에 둘러싸여 점잖게 돌아온다."], ["When he dines alone in chambers, as he has dined to-day, and has his bit of fish and his steak or chicken brought in from the coffee-house, he descends with a candle to the echoing regions below the deserted mansion, and heralded by a remote reverberation of thundering doors, comes gravely back encircled by an earthy atmosphere and carrying a bottle from which he pours a radiant nectar, two score and ten years old, that blushes in the glass to find itself so famous and fills the whole room with the fragrance of southern grapes.", "손에 든 병에서 그는 빛나는 신주(神酒)를 따른다. 오십 년 묵은 그 술은 제 명성에 부끄러운 듯 잔 속에서 발그레 물들며 온 방 안을 남쪽 포도 향기로 가득 채운다."]], [["Mr.", "토킹혼 씨는 황혼녘 열린 창가에 앉아 술을 즐긴다."], ["Tulkinghorn, sitting in the twilight by the open window, enjoys his wine.", "술이 그 오십 년 침묵과 은둔의 세월을 그에게 속삭이기라도 하는 듯, 그를 한층 더 단단히 닫아 건다. 그 어느 때보다 속을 알 수 없는 사람이 되어, 그는 앉아 마시며, 비밀스레 무르익어 간다고 할까, 그 황혼의 시각에 자기가 아는 온갖 비밀을 곱씹는다."], ["As if it whispered to him of its fifty years of silence and seclusion, it shuts him up the closer.", "시골의 어두워 가는 숲과, 도회의 거대하고 텅 빈 채 닫혀 버린 저택들에 얽힌 비밀을. 그러다 어쩌면 자기 자신과, 자기 가문의 내력과, 자기 돈과, 자기 유언을—누구에게나 다 비밀인 그것을—한두 번 떠올릴지도 모른다."], ["More impenetrable than ever, he sits, and drinks, and mellows as it were in secrecy, pondering at that twilight hour on all the mysteries he knows, associated with darkening woods in the country, and vast blank shut-up houses in town, and perhaps sparing a thought or two for himself, and his family history, and his money, and his will—all a mystery to every one—and that one bachelor friend of his, a man of the same mould and a lawyer too, who lived the same kind of life until he was seventy-five years old, and then suddenly conceiving (as it is supposed) an impression that it was too monotonous, gave his gold watch to his hair-dresser one summer evening and walked leisurely home to the Temple and hanged himself.", "또 자기와 한 부류인, 역시 변호사였던 그 독신 친구 하나를. 그 친구는 일흔다섯이 되도록 똑같은 삶을 살다가, 어느 날 문득 (사람들 짐작으로는) 그 삶이 너무 단조롭다는 생각이 들었던지, 어느 여름 저녁 자기 이발사에게 금시계를 주고는 느긋이 템플의 집으로 걸어와 목을 매달았다."]], [["But Mr. Tulkinghorn is not alone to-night to ponder at his usual length.", "그러나 오늘 밤 토킹혼 씨는 평소처럼 길게 곱씹을 처지가 못 된다."], ["Seated at the same table, though with his chair modestly and uncomfortably drawn a little way from it, sits a bald, mild, shining man who coughs respectfully behind his hand when the lawyer bids him fill his glass.", "같은 탁자에 함께 앉은 사람이 있으니, 의자를 탁자에서 겸손하게 또 어색하게 조금 물려 놓은 채로다. 변호사가 잔을 채우라고 권하면 손으로 입을 가리고 공손하게 기침하는, 대머리에 온순하고 번들거리는 사내다."]], [["“Now, Snagsby,” says Mr.", "\"자, 스내그스비,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, “to go over this odd story again.”", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"그 묘한 얘기를 다시 한번 짚어 봅시다.\""]], [["“If you please, sir.”", "\"예, 그러십시오, 나리.\""]], [["“You told me when you were so good as to step round here last night—”", "\"어젯밤 친절하게도 이리 들러 주셨을 때 말이오—\""]], [["“For which I must ask you to excuse me if it was a liberty, sir; but I remember that you had taken a sort of an interest in that person, and I thought it possible that you might—just—wish—to—”", "\"외람된 일이었다면 부디 너그러이 보아 주십사 청해야겠습니다, 나리. 다만 나리께서 그 사람한테 어떤 관심 같은 걸 가지고 계셨던 게 기억나서요, 어쩌면 나리께서—그저—원하실지도—하는 생각이 들어서—\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn is not the man to help him to any conclusion or to admit anything as to any possibility concerning himself.", "토킹혼 씨는 상대의 말을 마무리해 줄 사람도, 자기와 관련해 무슨 가능성이든 인정해 줄 사람도 아니다."], ["So Mr.", "그래서 스내그스비 씨는 어색하게 기침하며 말끝을 흐린다."], ["Snagsby trails off into saying, with an awkward cough, “I must ask you to excuse the liberty, sir, I am sure.”", "\"외람된 짓을 너그러이 보아 주십사 청해야겠습니다, 정말이지요, 나리.\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“You told me, Snagsby, that you put on your hat and came round without mentioning your intention to your wife.", "\"스내그스비, 당신은 모자를 쓰고 부인한테는 어디 간다는 말도 없이 이리 왔다고 했지요."], ["That was prudent I think, because it’s not a matter of such importance that it requires to be mentioned.”", "잘하셨소. 굳이 입에 올릴 만큼 중대한 일은 아니니까.\""]], [["“Well, sir,” returns Mr.", "\"저, 그게 말입니다, 나리,\""], ["Snagsby, “you see, my little woman is—not to put too fine a point upon it—inquisitive.", "스내그스비 씨가 대답한다. \"솔직히 말씀드리자면 제 마누라가—호기심이 많습니다."], ["She’s inquisitive.", "호기심이 많아요."], ["Poor little thing, she’s liable to spasms, and it’s good for her to have her mind employed.", "가엾게도 그 사람은 발작을 잘 일으키는데, 머리를 어디다 쓰게 하면 좋답니다."], ["In consequence of which she employs it—I should say upon every individual thing she can lay hold of, whether it concerns her or not—especially not.", "그래서 머리를 굴리는데—말하자면 손에 잡히는 건 무엇이든, 자기와 상관이 있든 없든—특히 상관없는 일에다 굴린다고 해야겠지요."], ["My little woman has a very active mind, sir.”", "제 마누라는 머리가 아주 잘 돌아갑니다, 나리.\""]], [["Mr.", "스내그스비 씨는 술을 마시며 손으로 입을 가리고 감탄 어린 기침을 하고는 중얼거린다."], ["Snagsby drinks and murmurs with an admiring cough behind his hand, “Dear me, very fine wine indeed!”", "\"이런, 정말 훌륭한 술입니다요!\""]], [["“Therefore you kept your visit to yourself last night?”", "\"그래서 어젯밤 다녀온 일을 혼자만 알고 있었다는 거요?\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“And to-night too?”", "\"오늘 밤도 그렇고?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, and to-night, too.", "\"예, 나리, 오늘 밤도 그렇습니다."], ["My little woman is at present in—not to put too fine a point on it—in a pious state, or in what she considers such, and attends the Evening Exertions (which is the name they go by) of a reverend party of the name of Chadband.", "제 마누라가 요즘—솔직히 말씀드려—신앙심에 빠진 상태라, 아니 본인이 그렇게 여기는 상태라서, 채드밴드라는 목사 양반의 '저녁 정진'이라는 데(그게 그 모임 이름입니다요)에 나갑니다."], ["He has a great deal of eloquence at his command, undoubtedly, but I am not quite favourable to his style myself.", "그 양반 말솜씨가 굉장한 건 두말할 나위 없지만, 저는 그 양반 투가 그리 마음에 들지는 않습니다."], ["That’s neither here nor there.", "뭐 이건 여기서 할 얘기는 아니고요."], ["My little woman being engaged in that way made it easier for me to step round in a quiet manner.”", "마누라가 그 일에 매여 있어서 제가 조용히 다녀오기가 한결 수월했지요.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn assents.", "토킹혼 씨가 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["“Fill your glass, Snagsby.”", "\"잔 채우시오, 스내그스비.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir, I am sure,”", "\"고맙습니다, 정말이지요, 나리.\""], ["returns the stationer with his cough of deference.", "문방구상이 공손한 기침과 함께 대답한다."], ["“This is wonderfully fine wine, sir!”", "\"이거 참 기막히게 좋은 술입니다요, 나리!\""]], [["“It is a rare wine now,”", "\"이제는 귀한 술이지요.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“It is fifty years old.”", "\"오십 년 묵은 거요.\""]], [["“Is it indeed, sir?", "\"정말입니까요, 나리?"], ["But I am not surprised to hear it, I am sure.", "하지만 그 말씀을 듣고도 놀랍지 않습니다요, 정말이지요."], ["It might be—any age almost.”", "이거야—거의 무슨 햇수든 다 될 만하니까요.\""], ["After rendering this general tribute to the port, Mr. Snagsby in his modesty coughs an apology behind his hand for drinking anything so precious.", "포트와인에 이렇게 두루뭉술 찬사를 바친 뒤, 스내그스비 씨는 겸손하게도 이토록 귀한 걸 마시는 데 대해 손으로 입을 가리고 사죄하듯 기침한다."]], [["“Will you run over, once again, what the boy said?” asks Mr.", "\"그 아이가 한 말을 한 번 더 짚어 주시겠소?\""], ["Tulkinghorn, putting his hands into the pockets of his rusty smallclothes and leaning quietly back in his chair.", "토킹혼 씨가 낡은 반바지 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣고 의자에 조용히 기대며 묻는다."]], [["“With pleasure, sir.”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 나리.\""]], [["Then, with fidelity, though with some prolixity, the law-stationer repeats Jo’s statement made to the assembled guests at his house.", "그러자 법률 문방구상은 충실하게, 다만 좀 장황하게, 조가 자기 집에 모인 손님들 앞에서 했던 진술을 되풀이한다."], ["On coming to the end of his narrative, he gives a great start and breaks off with, “Dear me, sir, I wasn’t aware there was any other gentleman present!”", "이야기 끝에 다다랐을 때, 그는 화들짝 놀라며 말을 뚝 끊는다. \"아이고, 나리, 여기 다른 신사분이 계신 줄은 미처 몰랐습니다요!\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby is dismayed to see, standing with an attentive face between himself and the lawyer at a little distance from the table, a person with a hat and stick in his hand who was not there when he himself came in and has not since entered by the door or by either of the windows.", "스내그스비 씨가 질겁한 것은, 탁자에서 조금 떨어진 곳, 자기와 변호사 사이에 모자와 지팡이를 손에 든 사람 하나가 주의 깊은 얼굴로 서 있는 걸 보았기 때문이다."], ["There is a press in the room, but its hinges have not creaked, nor has a step been audible upon the floor.", "자기가 들어왔을 때는 거기 없던 사람이고, 그 뒤로 문으로든 두 창문 어느 쪽으로든 들어온 적도 없는 사람이다."], ["Yet this third person stands there with his attentive face, and his hat and stick in his hands, and his hands behind him, a composed and quiet listener.", "방 안에 장롱이 하나 있긴 하나 경첩이 삐걱대지도 않았고, 마룻바닥에 발소리 하나 들리지 않았다. 그런데도 이 세 번째 인물은 주의 깊은 얼굴로, 모자와 지팡이를 손에 들고 그 손을 뒤로 한 채, 차분하고 조용한 청자로 거기 서 있다."], ["He is a stoutly built, steady-looking, sharp-eyed man in black, of about the middle-age.", "검은 옷차림에 다부진 체격, 차분해 보이는 인상, 날카로운 눈매를 한 중년 사내다."], ["Except that he looks at Mr. Snagsby as if he were going to take his portrait, there is nothing remarkable about him at first sight but his ghostly manner of appearing.", "스내그스비 씨를 마치 초상화라도 그릴 듯이 바라본다는 것 말고는, 첫눈에 눈에 띄는 점이라곤 유령처럼 나타난 그 태도뿐이다."]], [["“Don’t mind this gentleman,”", "\"이분은 신경 쓰지 마시오.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn in his quiet way.", "토킹혼 씨가 조용한 투로 말한다."], ["“This is only Mr. Bucket.”", "\"그냥 버킷 씨일 뿐이니까.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed, sir?”", "\"아, 그렇습니까요, 나리?\""], ["returns the stationer, expressing by a cough that he is quite in the dark as to who Mr. Bucket may be.", "문방구상이 대답하면서, 버킷 씨가 대체 누구인지 도무지 알 길이 없다는 뜻을 기침으로 내비친다."]], [["“I wanted him to hear this story,”", "\"이 사람더러 그 얘기를 들으라 했소.\""], ["says the lawyer, “because I have half a mind (for a reason) to know more of it, and he is very intelligent in such things.", "변호사가 말한다. \"내가 (그럴 만한 까닭이 있어서) 그걸 좀 더 알고 싶은 마음이 반쯤 있는데, 이 사람이 그런 일에 아주 밝거든."], ["What do you say to this, Bucket?”", "어떻게 보나, 버킷?\""]], [["“It’s very plain, sir.", "\"아주 간단합니다요, 나리."], ["Since our people have moved this boy on, and he’s not to be found on his old lay, if Mr. Snagsby don’t object to go down with me to Tom-all-Alone’s and point him out, we can have him here in less than a couple of hours’ time.", "우리 쪽 사람들이 그 아이를 쫓아내서 늘 있던 자리에 없으니, 스내그스비 씨가 저랑 톰 혼자 사는 곳까지 같이 내려가 그 아이를 짚어 주기만 한다면, 두 시간도 안 돼서 여기 데려올 수 있습니다요."], ["I can do it without Mr. Snagsby, of course, but this is the shortest way.”", "물론 스내그스비 씨 없이도 할 수 있지만, 이게 제일 빠른 길이지요.\""]], [["“Mr. Bucket is a detective officer, Snagsby,”", "\"버킷 씨는 형사요, 스내그스비.\""], ["says the lawyer in explanation.", "변호사가 설명을 덧붙인다."]], [["“Is he indeed, sir?”", "\"정말입니까요, 나리?\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby with a strong tendency in his clump of hair to stand on end.", "스내그스비 씨가 말하는데, 그 텁수룩한 머리칼이 곤두서려는 기색이 역력하다."]], [["“And if you have no real objection to accompany Mr. Bucket to the place in question,”", "\"버킷 씨와 함께 그곳에 가는 데 정말로 거리낄 게 없으시다면,\""], ["pursues the lawyer, “I shall feel obliged to you if you will do so.”", "변호사가 말을 잇는다. \"그렇게 해 주시면 신세를 진 걸로 알겠소.\""]], [["In a moment’s hesitation on the part of Mr. Snagsby, Bucket dips down to the bottom of his mind.", "스내그스비 씨가 한순간 머뭇거리자, 버킷은 그의 속마음 밑바닥까지 꿰뚫는다."]], [["“Don’t you be afraid of hurting the boy,”", "\"그 아이를 해치게 될까 봐 걱정 마시오.\""], ["he says.", "그가 말한다."], ["“You won’t do that.", "\"그럴 일 없습니다."], ["It’s all right as far as the boy’s concerned.", "그 아이한테는 아무 탈 없는 일이에요."], ["We shall only bring him here to ask him a question or so I want to put to him, and he’ll be paid for his trouble and sent away again.", "우리는 그저 그 아이를 여기 데려와 내가 묻고 싶은 걸 한두 가지 물어볼 뿐이고, 수고비를 줘서 다시 돌려보낼 겁니다."], ["It’ll be a good job for him.", "그 아이한테는 좋은 일이죠."], ["I promise you, as a man, that you shall see the boy sent away all right.", "사내대장부로서 약속하리다. 그 아이가 무사히 돌아가는 걸 당신 눈으로 보게 될 거요."], ["Don’t you be afraid of hurting him; you an’t going to do that.”", "해치게 될까 봐 걱정 마시오. 그럴 일 없으니까.\""]], [["“Very well, Mr. Tulkinghorn!”", "\"좋습니다, 토킹혼 씨!\""], ["cries Mr. Snagsby cheerfully.", "스내그스비 씨가 쾌활하게 외친다."], ["And reassured, “Since that’s the case—”", "그러고는 안심한 듯, \"사정이 그렇다면—\""]], [["“Yes!", "\"그렇소!"], ["And lookee here, Mr. Snagsby,”", "그리고 이것 보시오, 스내그스비 씨.\""], ["resumes Bucket, taking him aside by the arm, tapping him familiarly on the breast, and speaking in a confidential tone.", "버킷이 그의 팔을 잡아 한쪽으로 데려가며 가슴을 친근하게 톡톡 두드리고는 은밀한 투로 말을 잇는다."], ["“You’re a man of the world, you know, and a man of business, and a man of sense.", "\"당신은 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이고, 사업하는 사람이고, 분별 있는 사람이오."], ["That’s what YOU are.”", "당신이 그런 사람이란 말이지.\""]], [["“I am sure I am much obliged to you for your good opinion,”", "\"좋게 봐 주셔서 정말 감사합니다요.\""], ["returns the stationer with his cough of modesty, “but—”", "문방구상이 겸손한 기침과 함께 대답한다. \"하지만—\""]], [["“That’s what YOU are, you know,”", "\"당신이 그런 사람이라니까.\""], ["says Bucket.", "버킷이 말한다."], ["“Now, it an’t necessary to say to a man like you, engaged in your business, which is a business of trust and requires a person to be wide awake and have his senses about him and his head screwed on tight (I had an uncle in your business once)—it an’t necessary to say to a man like you that it’s the best and wisest way to keep little matters like this quiet.", "\"자, 당신처럼 사업하는 사람한테 굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 그게 신용으로 하는 사업이라 사람이 정신을 바짝 차리고 감각을 곤두세우고 머리를 단단히 박고 있어야 하는 일 아니오 (한때 내 삼촌도 당신과 같은 장사를 했지요)—당신 같은 사람한테 굳이 말할 것도 없지만, 이런 자잘한 일은 조용히 묻어 두는 게 가장 현명하고 좋은 길이오."], ["Don’t you see?", "알겠소?"], ["Quiet!”", "조용히!\""]], [["“Certainly, certainly,”", "\"물론이지요, 물론입니다.\""], ["returns the other.", "상대가 대답한다."]], [["“I don’t mind telling YOU,”", "\"당신한테는 말해도 괜찮겠지.\""], ["says Bucket with an engaging appearance of frankness, “that as far as I can understand it, there seems to be a doubt whether this dead person wasn’t entitled to a little property, and whether this female hasn’t been up to some games respecting that property, don’t you see?”", "버킷이 솔직한 척 사람 끄는 태도로 말한다. \"내가 알기로는, 이 죽은 사람이 작은 재산을 물려받을 권리가 있었던 게 아닌가 하는 의심이 있고, 또 어떤 여자가 그 재산을 두고 무슨 수작을 부린 게 아닌가 싶단 말이오, 알겠소?\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby, but not appearing to see quite distinctly.", "스내그스비 씨가 말하지만, 썩 또렷이 알아들은 기색은 아니다."]], [["“Now, what YOU want,” pursues Bucket, again tapping Mr.", "\"자, 당신이 바라는 건,\""], ["Snagsby on the breast in a comfortable and soothing manner, “is that every person should have their rights according to justice.", "버킷이 다시 스내그스비 씨의 가슴을 편안하고 달래는 투로 톡톡 두드리며 말을 잇는다. \"정의에 따라 누구나 제 권리를 누리는 것 아니겠소."], ["That’s what YOU want.”", "당신이 바라는 게 그거지.\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"암요, 그렇고말고요.\""], ["returns Mr. Snagsby with a nod.", "스내그스비 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 대답한다."]], [["“On account of which, and at the same time to oblige a—do you call it, in your business, customer or client?", "\"그러니까, 그것 때문에, 그리고 동시에—당신네 장사에서는 손님이라 부르오, 고객이라 부르오?"], ["I forget how my uncle used to call it.”", "우리 삼촌이 뭐라 불렀는지 깜빡했네.\""]], [["“Why, I generally say customer myself,”", "\"뭐, 저는 보통 손님이라고 합니다요.\""], ["replies Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 대답한다."]], [["“You’re right!”", "\"맞아요!\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket, shaking hands with him quite affectionately.", "버킷 씨가 그와 아주 다정하게 악수하며 대꾸한다."], ["“—On account of which, and at the same time to oblige a real good customer, you mean to go down with me, in confidence, to Tom-all-Alone’s and to keep the whole thing quiet ever afterwards and never mention it to any one.", "\"—그것 때문에, 그리고 동시에 정말 좋은 손님 하나 봐주려고, 당신은 나랑 같이 비밀리에 톰 혼자 사는 곳에 내려가서, 그다음엔 이 일을 두고두고 조용히 묻어 두고 누구한테도 말하지 않을 작정이오."], ["That’s about your intentions, if I understand you?”", "그게 당신 뜻이지요, 내가 제대로 알아들었다면?\""]], [["“You are right, sir.", "\"맞습니다요, 나리."], ["You are right,”", "맞습니다요.\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Then here’s your hat,”", "\"그럼 여기 모자요.\""], ["returns his new friend, quite as intimate with it as if he had made it; “and if you’re ready, I am.”", "새로 사귄 친구가 마치 제가 만든 모자라도 되는 양 격의 없이 다루며 대꾸한다. \"당신이 준비됐으면, 나도 됐소.\""]], [["They leave Mr. Tulkinghorn, without a ruffle on the surface of his unfathomable depths, drinking his old wine, and go down into the streets.", "두 사람은 가늠할 길 없는 깊은 속에 물결 하나 일지 않은 채 묵은 술을 마시는 토킹혼 씨를 남겨 두고, 거리로 내려간다."]], [["“You don’t happen to know a very good sort of person of the name of Gridley, do you?”", "\"그리들리라는, 아주 괜찮은 양반 하나 혹시 알지 않소?\""], ["says Bucket in friendly converse as they descend the stairs.", "계단을 내려가며 버킷이 다정하게 말을 건넨다."]], [["“No,” says Mr.", "\"아니요.\""], ["Snagsby, considering, “I don’t know anybody of that name.", "스내그스비 씨가 생각해 보고는 대답한다. \"그런 이름은 모르겠는데요."], ["Why?”", "왜 그러십니까?\""]], [["“Nothing particular,”", "\"별것 아니오.\""], ["says Bucket; “only having allowed his temper to get a little the better of him and having been threatening some respectable people, he is keeping out of the way of a warrant I have got against him—which it’s a pity that a man of sense should do.”", "버킷이 말한다. \"그저 그 양반이 성질을 못 이겨 점잖은 사람들 몇을 위협한 일이 있어서, 내가 받아 둔 영장을 피해 숨어 다니고 있거든—분별 있는 사람이 그러다니 딱한 노릇이지요.\""]], [["As they walk along, Mr.", "길을 걸어가며 스내그스비 씨는 새삼스러운 일을 하나 알아챈다."], ["Snagsby observes, as a novelty, that however quick their pace may be, his companion still seems in some undefinable manner to lurk and lounge; also, that whenever he is going to turn to the right or left, he pretends to have a fixed purpose in his mind of going straight ahead, and wheels off, sharply, at the very last moment.", "둘의 걸음이 아무리 빨라도, 동행은 여전히 무어라 형용하기 어려운 식으로 어슬렁대며 빈둥거리는 듯 보인다는 것이다. 또 오른쪽이든 왼쪽이든 돌려고 할 때면, 마음속에 곧장 앞으로 갈 작정이 정해진 척하다가 마지막 순간에 홱 방향을 튼다는 것도."], ["Now and then, when they pass a police-constable on his beat, Mr. Snagsby notices that both the constable and his guide fall into a deep abstraction as they come towards each other, and appear entirely to overlook each other, and to gaze into space.", "이따금 순찰 중인 순경을 지나칠 때면, 순경도 안내자도 서로 마주 다가오면서 깊은 상념에 잠겨 상대를 까맣게 못 본 척하며 허공을 응시한다는 것을, 스내그스비 씨는 알아챈다."], ["In a few instances, Mr. Bucket, coming behind some under-sized young man with a shining hat on, and his sleek hair twisted into one flat curl on each side of his head, almost without glancing at him touches him with his stick, upon which the young man, looking round, instantly evaporates.", "몇 번인가는, 번들거리는 모자를 쓰고 매끈한 머리칼을 양옆으로 한 가닥씩 납작하게 말아 붙인 작달막한 젊은이 뒤로 다가간 버킷 씨가, 거의 쳐다보지도 않고 지팡이로 툭 건드리자, 젊은이가 돌아보더니 그 자리에서 증발하듯 사라진다."], ["For the most part Mr. Bucket notices things in general, with a face as unchanging as the great mourning ring on his little finger or the brooch, composed of not much diamond and a good deal of setting, which he wears in his shirt.", "대체로 버킷 씨는 새끼손가락에 낀 큼직한 추모 반지나, 다이아몬드는 얼마 안 되고 받침 장식만 잔뜩인 셔츠 브로치만큼이나 변함없는 얼굴로 세상사 전반을 둘러본다."]], [["When they come at last to Tom-all-Alone’s, Mr. Bucket stops for a moment at the corner and takes a lighted bull’s-eye from the constable on duty there, who then accompanies him with his own particular bull’s-eye at his waist.", "마침내 톰 혼자 사는 곳에 이르자, 버킷 씨는 모퉁이에서 잠시 멈춰 그곳 당직 순경에게 불 켠 등롱을 받아 든다."], ["Between his two conductors, Mr.", "순경은 자기 등롱을 허리에 차고 그를 따라나선다."], ["Snagsby passes along the middle of a villainous street, undrained, unventilated, deep in black mud and corrupt water—though the roads are dry elsewhere—and reeking with such smells and sights that he, who has lived in London all his life, can scarce believe his senses.", "두 안내자 사이에 끼어, 스내그스비 씨는 흉악한 거리 한가운데를 지나간다. 다른 길은 다 말라 있는데도 이곳은 배수도 안 되고 통풍도 안 되어 시커먼 진흙과 썩은 물이 발목까지 잠기고, 평생 런던에서 살아온 그조차 제 감각을 믿기 어려울 만큼 지독한 냄새와 광경이 진동한다."], ["Branching from this street and its heaps of ruins are other streets and courts so infamous that Mr. Snagsby sickens in body and mind and feels as if he were going every moment deeper down into the infernal gulf.", "이 거리와 무더기진 폐허에서 뻗어 나간 또 다른 거리와 골목들이 어찌나 추악한지, 스내그스비 씨는 몸도 마음도 메스꺼워져 마치 순간순간 지옥의 구렁텅이 속으로 더 깊이 빠져드는 것만 같다."]], [["“Draw off a bit here, Mr. Snagsby,”", "\"이쪽으로 좀 비키시오, 스내그스비 씨.\""], ["says Bucket as a kind of shabby palanquin is borne towards them, surrounded by a noisy crowd.", "시끄러운 무리에 둘러싸인 채 허름한 가마 같은 것이 그들 쪽으로 실려 오자 버킷이 말한다."], ["“Here’s the fever coming up the street!”", "\"열병이 거리를 올라오고 있소!\""]], [["As the unseen wretch goes by, the crowd, leaving that object of attraction, hovers round the three visitors like a dream of horrible faces and fades away up alleys and into ruins and behind walls, and with occasional cries and shrill whistles of warning, thenceforth flits about them until they leave the place.", "보이지 않는 가엾은 자가 지나가자, 그 구경거리를 버려둔 무리는 끔찍한 얼굴들로 이루어진 한바탕 꿈처럼 세 방문객 주위를 맴돌다가, 골목으로, 폐허 속으로, 담벼락 뒤로 스러져 간다. 그러고는 이따금 고함과 날카로운 경고의 휘파람을 내지르며, 그들이 이곳을 떠날 때까지 줄곧 주변을 어른거린다."]], [["“Are those the fever-houses, Darby?”", "\"저것들이 열병 집이냐, 더비?\""], ["Mr. Bucket coolly asks as he turns his bull’s-eye on a line of stinking ruins.", "버킷 씨가 악취 풍기는 폐허의 한 줄로 등롱을 돌리며 태연히 묻는다."]], [["Darby replies that “all them are,” and further that in all, for months and months, the people “have been down by dozens”", "더비는 \"다 그렇습니다\"라고 대답하고, 덧붙여 모든 집에서 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 사람들이 \"수십 명씩 쓰러졌고\""], ["and have been carried out dead and dying “like sheep with the rot.”", "죽거나 죽어 가는 채로 \"썩어 가는 양들처럼\""], ["Bucket observing to Mr.", "실려 나갔다고 한다."], ["Snagsby as they go on again that he looks a little poorly, Mr. Snagsby answers that he feels as if he couldn’t breathe the dreadful air.", "다시 걸음을 옮기며 버킷이 스내그스비 씨더러 안색이 좀 안 좋아 보인다고 하자, 스내그스비 씨는 이 끔찍한 공기를 도무지 들이쉴 수가 없을 것 같다고 대답한다."]], [["There is inquiry made at various houses for a boy named Jo.", "여러 집을 돌며 조라는 아이를 찾아 묻는다."], ["As few people are known in Tom-all-Alone’s by any Christian sign, there is much reference to Mr. Snagsby whether he means Carrots, or the Colonel, or Gallows, or Young Chisel, or Terrier Tip, or Lanky, or the Brick.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳에서는 제대로 된 세례명으로 알려진 사람이 거의 없는 터라, 스내그스비 씨가 말하는 게 당근인지, 대령인지, 교수대인지, 어린 끌인지, 테리어 팁인지, 키다리인지, 벽돌인지를 두고 자꾸 되묻는다."], ["Mr. Snagsby describes over and over again.", "스내그스비 씨는 몇 번이고 다시 설명한다."], ["There are conflicting opinions respecting the original of his picture.", "그가 그린 그림의 원본이 누구인지를 두고 의견이 엇갈린다."], ["Some think it must be Carrots, some say the Brick.", "더러는 당근이 분명하다 하고, 더러는 벽돌이라 한다."], ["The Colonel is produced, but is not at all near the thing.", "대령이 끌려 나오지만 도무지 닮은 데가 없다."], ["Whenever Mr. Snagsby and his conductors are stationary, the crowd flows round, and from its squalid depths obsequious advice heaves up to Mr.", "스내그스비 씨와 두 안내자가 멈춰 설 때마다 무리가 주위로 몰려들어, 그 추레한 밑바닥에서 굽실거리는 조언이 버킷 씨에게 솟아오른다."], ["Bucket. Whenever they move, and the angry bull’s-eyes glare, it fades away and flits about them up the alleys, and in the ruins, and behind the walls, as before.", "그들이 움직이고 성난 등롱불이 번득일 때마다, 무리는 아까처럼 골목으로, 폐허 속으로, 담벼락 뒤로 스러졌다가 다시 어른거린다."]], [["At last there is a lair found out where Toughy, or the Tough Subject, lays him down at night; and it is thought that the Tough Subject may be Jo.", "마침내 텁수룩이, 곧 텁수룩 양반이 밤에 누워 자는 굴 하나가 밝혀지고, 그 텁수룩 양반이 조일지도 모른다는 생각이 든다."], ["Comparison of notes between Mr. Snagsby and the proprietress of the house—a drunken face tied up in a black bundle, and flaring out of a heap of rags on the floor of a dog-hutch which is her private apartment—leads to the establishment of this conclusion.", "스내그스비 씨와 그 집 안주인—검은 보따리에 싸인 술 취한 얼굴로, 개집이나 다름없는 제 거처 마룻바닥의 누더기 더미 속에서 번득이며 내다보는 여자—이 서로 정보를 맞춰 본 끝에 이 결론에 다다른다."], ["Toughy has gone to the doctor’s to get a bottle of stuff for a sick woman but will be here anon.", "텁수룩이는 병든 여자에게 줄 약 한 병을 받으러 의사한테 갔지만 곧 돌아올 거란다."]], [["“And who have we got here to-night?”", "\"오늘 밤 여기 있는 게 누구누구냐?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, opening another door and glaring in with his bull’s-eye.", "버킷 씨가 또 다른 문을 열고 등롱으로 안을 비추며 말한다."], ["“Two drunken men, eh?", "\"술 취한 사내 둘이라, 응?"], ["And two women?", "그리고 여자 둘이고?"], ["The men are sound enough,”", "사내들은 잠은 잘 자는구먼.\""], ["turning back each sleeper’s arm from his face to look at him.", "자고 있는 사내들의 팔을 하나씩 얼굴에서 젖혀 보면서."], ["“Are these your good men, my dears?”", "\"이 사람들이 댁들 서방인가, 아주머니들?\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"예, 나리.\""], ["returns one of the women.", "여자 하나가 대답한다."], ["“They are our husbands.”", "\"우리 남편들이오.\""]], [["“Brickmakers, eh?”", "\"벽돌공이고?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["“What are you doing here?", "\"여기서 뭣들 하고 있어?"], ["You don’t belong to London.”", "런던 사람들이 아닐 텐데.\""]], [["“No, sir.", "\"예, 나리."], ["We belong to Hertfordshire.”", "우리는 허트퍼드셔 사람들이오.\""]], [["“Whereabouts in Hertfordshire?”", "\"허트퍼드셔 어디쯤?\""]], [["“Saint Albans.”", "\"세인트 올번스요.\""]], [["“Come up on the tramp?”", "\"걸어서 올라왔나?\""]], [["“We walked up yesterday.", "\"어제 걸어 올라왔소."], ["There’s no work down with us at present, but we have done no good by coming here, and shall do none, I expect.”", "지금 우리 동네에는 일이 없는데, 여기 와 봐야 별수 없었고 앞으로도 별수 없을 듯싶소.\""]], [["“That’s not the way to do much good,” says Mr.", "\"그런다고 별수가 나는 건 아니지.\""], ["Bucket, turning his head in the direction of the unconscious figures on the ground.", "버킷 씨가 바닥에 정신없이 뻗은 사내들 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 말한다."]], [["“It an’t indeed,”", "\"정말 그렇소.\""], ["replies the woman with a sigh.", "여자가 한숨을 쉬며 대답한다."], ["“Jenny and me knows it full well.”", "\"제니랑 나는 그걸 뼈저리게 알지요.\""]], [["The room, though two or three feet higher than the door, is so low that the head of the tallest of the visitors would touch the blackened ceiling if he stood upright.", "방은 문보다 두세 자쯤 높긴 하나 천장이 어찌나 낮은지, 방문객 중 가장 키 큰 이가 똑바로 선다면 머리가 시커먼 천장에 닿을 지경이다."], ["It is offensive to every sense; even the gross candle burns pale and sickly in the polluted air.", "어느 감각에나 역겹기 그지없다. 굵직한 양초마저 더럽혀진 공기 속에서 파리하고 병약하게 타오른다."], ["There are a couple of benches and a higher bench by way of table.", "긴 의자 두 개와, 탁자 노릇을 하는 좀 더 높은 의자 하나가 있다."], ["The men lie asleep where they stumbled down, but the women sit by the candle.", "사내들은 비틀거리다 쓰러진 그 자리에 잠들어 있고, 여자들은 촛불 곁에 앉아 있다."], ["Lying in the arms of the woman who has spoken is a very young child.", "아까 말한 여자의 팔에는 아주 어린 아기가 안겨 있다."]], [["“Why, what age do you call that little creature?”", "\"아니, 저 작은 것이 몇 살이오?\""], ["says Bucket.", "버킷이 말한다."], ["“It looks as if it was born yesterday.”", "\"어제 태어난 것 같구먼.\""], ["He is not at all rough about it; and as he turns his light gently on the infant, Mr.", "그 태도가 조금도 거칠지 않다."], ["Snagsby is strangely reminded of another infant, encircled with light, that he has seen in pictures.", "그가 등불을 부드럽게 아기 쪽으로 돌리자, 스내그스비 씨는 그림 속에서 본 적 있는, 빛으로 둘러싸인 또 다른 아기가 묘하게 떠오른다."]], [["“He is not three weeks old yet, sir,”", "\"아직 세 주도 안 됐어요, 나리.\""], ["says the woman.", "여자가 말한다."]], [["“Is he your child?”", "\"댁의 아기요?\""]], [["The other woman, who was bending over it when they came in, stoops down again and kisses it as it lies asleep.", "\"제 아기지요.\" 들어왔을 때 아기 위로 몸을 숙이고 있던 다른 여자가, 다시 몸을 굽혀 잠든 아기에게 입을 맞춘다."]], [["“You seem as fond of it as if you were the mother yourself,”", "\"꼭 댁이 친어머니라도 되는 것처럼 아끼는구먼.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I was the mother of one like it, master, and it died.”", "\"저도 이만한 아기의 어미였는데, 나리, 그 애가 죽었지요.\""]], [["“Ah, Jenny, Jenny!”", "\"아아, 제니, 제니!\""], ["says the other woman to her.", "다른 여자가 그녀에게 말한다."], ["“Better so.", "\"차라리 그게 나아."], ["Much better to think of dead than alive, Jenny!", "산 것보다 죽은 걸 생각하는 게 훨씬 나아, 제니!"], ["Much better!”", "훨씬 나아!\""]], [["“Why, you an’t such an unnatural woman, I hope,”", "\"아니, 설마 제 자식이 죽기를 바랄 만큼,\""], ["returns Bucket sternly, “as to wish your own child dead?”", "버킷이 매섭게 대꾸한다. \"그렇게 모진 여자는 아니겠지?\""]], [["“God knows you are right, master,”", "\"하느님은 아시지요, 나리 말씀이 옳다는 걸.\""], ["she returns.", "그녀가 대답한다."], ["“I am not.", "\"그런 건 아니에요."], ["I’d stand between it and death with my own life if I could, as true as any pretty lady.”", "할 수만 있다면 제 목숨을 던져서라도 그 애와 죽음 사이를 막아설 거예요, 어느 고운 마님 못지않게 진심으로요.\""]], [["“Then don’t talk in that wrong manner,”", "\"그럼 그런 그릇된 소리는 마시오.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, mollified again.", "다시 누그러진 버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Why do you do it?”", "\"왜 그런 소리를 하나?\""]], [["“It’s brought into my head, master,”", "\"머릿속에 그런 생각이 떠오르는걸요, 나리.\""], ["returns the woman, her eyes filling with tears, “when I look down at the child lying so.", "여자가 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해서 대답한다. \"저렇게 누운 아기를 내려다보면요."], ["If it was never to wake no more, you’d think me mad, I should take on so.", "만약 다시는 깨어나지 못한다면, 제가 어찌나 미쳐 날뛸지 나리도 절 미쳤다 하실 거예요."], ["I know that very well.", "저도 잘 알아요."], ["I was with Jenny when she lost hers—warn’t I, Jenny?—and I know how she grieved.", "제니가 자기 애를 잃었을 때 제가 곁에 있었거든요—그렇지, 제니?—그 애가 얼마나 슬퍼했는지 저는 알아요."], ["But look around you at this place.", "하지만 이곳을 둘러보세요."], ["Look at them,”", "저들을 보세요.\""], ["glancing at the sleepers on the ground.", "바닥에 잠든 이들을 흘끔 보며."], ["“Look at the boy you’re waiting for, who’s gone out to do me a good turn.", "\"나리가 기다리는 저 아이를 보세요, 저를 도와주려고 나간 그 아이를요."], ["Think of the children that your business lays with often and often, and that YOU see grow up!”", "나리네 일이 흔하디흔하게 엮이고, 또 자라는 걸 나리도 보시는 그 아이들을 생각해 보세요!\""]], [["“Well, well,” says Mr.", "\"자, 자.\""], ["Bucket, “you train him respectable, and he’ll be a comfort to you, and look after you in your old age, you know.”", "버킷 씨가 말한다. \"이 애를 번듯하게 키우면, 그 애가 댁한테 위안이 되고 늙어서 댁을 돌봐 줄 거 아니오.\""]], [["“I mean to try hard,”", "\"열심히 애써 볼 작정이에요.\""], ["she answers, wiping her eyes.", "그녀가 눈을 훔치며 대답한다."], ["“But I have been a-thinking, being over-tired to-night and not well with the ague, of all the many things that’ll come in his way.", "\"하지만 오늘 밤 너무 지치고 학질로 몸도 성치 않다 보니, 이 애 앞길에 닥칠 그 많은 일을 자꾸 생각하게 돼요."], ["My master will be against it, and he’ll be beat, and see me beat, and made to fear his home, and perhaps to stray wild.", "제 남편은 그걸 반대할 테고, 그러면 이 앤 매를 맞고, 제가 맞는 것도 보고, 집을 무서워하게 되고, 어쩌면 거칠게 떠돌게 될 거예요."], ["If I work for him ever so much, and ever so hard, there’s no one to help me; and if he should be turned bad ‘spite of all I could do, and the time should come when I should sit by him in his sleep, made hard and changed, an’t it likely I should think of him as he lies in my lap now and wish he had died as Jenny’s child died!”", "제가 이 애를 위해 아무리 일하고 아무리 애써도, 도와줄 사람 하나 없고요. 제가 할 수 있는 걸 다 해도 끝내 이 애가 나쁘게 빗나가서, 모질어지고 딴사람이 되어 버린 이 애 곁에 제가 앉아 그 잠든 모습을 지켜보는 날이 온다면, 지금 제 무릎에 누운 이 애를 떠올리며 차라리 제니의 애처럼 죽어 버렸으면 했더라면 싶지 않겠어요!\""]], [["“There, there!”", "\"자, 자!\""], ["says Jenny.", "제니가 말한다."], ["“Liz, you’re tired and ill.", "\"리즈, 넌 지치고 아프잖아."], ["Let me take him.”", "내가 안을게.\""]], [["In doing so, she displaces the mother’s dress, but quickly readjusts it over the wounded and bruised bosom where the baby has been lying.", "그러면서 제니가 어미의 옷을 헤치는데, 아기가 누워 있던 그 상처 나고 멍든 가슴 위로 얼른 옷을 다시 여며 준다."]], [["“It’s my dead child,”", "\"바로 내 죽은 애 때문에,\""], ["says Jenny, walking up and down as she nurses, “that makes me love this child so dear, and it’s my dead child that makes her love it so dear too, as even to think of its being taken away from her now.", "제니가 아기를 안고 어르며 이리저리 거니는데, \"내가 이 애를 이토록 끔찍이 사랑하는 거예요. 그리고 내 죽은 애 때문에, 저 사람도 이 애를 이토록 끔찍이 사랑해서, 지금 이 애를 빼앗긴다는 생각조차 못 견디는 거고요."], ["While she thinks that, I think what fortune would I give to have my darling back.", "저 사람이 그렇게 생각할 때면, 나는 내 귀한 애를 되찾을 수만 있다면 무슨 복이라도 내놓겠다고 생각해요."], ["But we mean the same thing, if we knew how to say it, us two mothers does in our poor hearts!”", "하지만 우리 둘은, 말로 옮길 줄만 안다면, 가엾은 마음속으로 똑같은 걸 생각하는 거예요, 어미 된 우리 둘이 말이에요!\""]], [["As Mr. Snagsby blows his nose and coughs his cough of sympathy, a step is heard without.", "스내그스비 씨가 코를 풀고 동정의 기침을 하는데, 밖에서 발소리가 들린다."], ["Mr. Bucket throws his light into the doorway and says to Mr.", "버킷 씨가 등불을 문간으로 비추며 스내그스비 씨에게 말한다."], ["Snagsby, “Now, what do you say to Toughy?", "\"자, 텁수룩이는 어떻소?"], ["Will HE do?”", "저 애면 되겠소?\""]], [["“That’s Jo,”", "\"저게 조올시다.\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["Jo stands amazed in the disk of light, like a ragged figure in a magic-lantern, trembling to think that he has offended against the law in not having moved on far enough.", "조는 등불 동그라미 속에서, 마술 환등기 속 누더기 형상처럼 어리둥절 서 있다. 충분히 멀리 가지 않아 법을 어긴 게 아닐까 싶어 벌벌 떤다."], ["Mr. Snagsby, however, giving him the consolatory assurance, “It’s only a job you will be paid for, Jo,”", "그러나 스내그스비 씨가 \"수고하면 돈 받을 일일 뿐이다, 조\""], ["he recovers; and on being taken outside by Mr.", "하고 다독여 안심시키자, 조는 기운을 차린다."], ["Bucket for a little private confabulation, tells his tale satisfactorily, though out of breath.", "버킷 씨가 조용히 의논하려고 조를 밖으로 데려가자, 조는 숨을 헐떡이면서도 제 이야기를 흡족하게 늘어놓는다."]], [["“I have squared it with the lad,” says Mr.", "\"그 애랑 얘기 다 맞췄으니,\""], ["Bucket, returning, “and it’s all right.", "버킷 씨가 돌아오며 말한다. \"다 됐소."], ["Now, Mr. Snagsby, we’re ready for you.”", "자, 스내그스비 씨, 당신 차례요.\""]], [["First, Jo has to complete his errand of good nature by handing over the physic he has been to get, which he delivers with the laconic verbal direction that “it’s to be all took d’rectly.”", "먼저 조는 받아 온 약을 넘겨주어 마음씨 좋은 심부름을 마무리하는데, \"지금 당장 다 먹어야 한다\"는 짤막한 당부와 함께 건넨다."], ["Secondly, Mr. Snagsby has to lay upon the table half a crown, his usual panacea for an immense variety of afflictions.", "다음으로 스내그스비 씨가 탁자 위에 반 크라운을 올려놓으니, 이는 그가 온갖 종류의 곤경에 두루 쓰는 만병통치약이다."], ["Thirdly, Mr. Bucket has to take Jo by the arm a little above the elbow and walk him on before him, without which observance neither the Tough Subject nor any other Subject could be professionally conducted to Lincoln’s Inn Fields.", "셋째로 버킷 씨가 조의 팔을 팔꿈치 조금 위로 잡고 자기 앞에 세워 걷게 하니, 이 절차가 없으면 텁수룩 양반이든 그 어떤 양반이든 격식대로 링컨 법학원 광장까지 인도될 수가 없다."], ["These arrangements completed, they give the women good night and come out once more into black and foul Tom-all-Alone’s.", "이 절차가 모두 끝나자, 그들은 여자들에게 잘 자라 인사하고 다시금 시커멓고 더러운 톰 혼자 사는 곳으로 나온다."]], [["By the noisome ways through which they descended into that pit, they gradually emerge from it, the crowd flitting, and whistling, and skulking about them until they come to the verge, where restoration of the bull’s-eyes is made to Darby.", "그 구렁텅이로 내려갔던 그 역겨운 길을 따라, 그들은 차츰 그곳을 빠져나온다. 무리가 어른거리고 휘파람 불고 슬금슬금 주위를 맴돌다가, 마침내 가장자리에 이르러 더비에게 등롱을 돌려준다."], ["Here the crowd, like a concourse of imprisoned demons, turns back, yelling, and is seen no more. Through the clearer and fresher streets, never so clear and fresh to Mr.", "여기서 무리는 갇혀 있던 악귀 떼처럼 악을 쓰며 돌아서고, 다시는 보이지 않는다."], ["Snagsby’s mind as now, they walk and ride until they come to Mr. Tulkinghorn’s gate.", "스내그스비 씨의 마음에 지금처럼 맑고 상쾌해 보인 적 없는 한결 맑고 산뜻한 거리들을 지나, 그들은 걷고 또 타고 하여 토킹혼 씨네 대문에 이른다."]], [["As they ascend the dim stairs (Mr. Tulkinghorn’s chambers being on the first floor), Mr.", "어둑한 계단을 오르며(토킹혼 씨의 사무실은 이 층에 있다), 버킷 씨는 바깥문 열쇠가 주머니에 있으니 초인종을 울릴 필요가 없다고 일러 준다."], ["Bucket mentions that he has the key of the outer door in his pocket and that there is no need to ring.", "그런 일에는 누구보다 능숙한 버킷이건만, 문을 여는 데 한참 시간을 들이고 소리도 제법 낸다."], ["For a man so expert in most things of that kind, Bucket takes time to open the door and makes some noise too. It may be that he sounds a note of preparation.", "어쩌면 준비하라는 신호음을 울리는 것인지도 모른다."]], [["Howbeit, they come at last into the hall, where a lamp is burning, and so into Mr. Tulkinghorn’s usual room—the room where he drank his old wine to-night.", "아무튼 그들은 마침내 등불 하나 켜진 현관에 들어서고, 이어 토킹혼 씨의 평소 방으로—오늘 밤 그가 묵은 술을 마시던 그 방으로—들어선다."], ["He is not there, but his two old-fashioned candlesticks are, and the room is tolerably light.", "그는 거기 없지만 그의 구식 촛대 두 개가 놓여 있어, 방은 그런대로 환하다."]], [["Mr. Bucket, still having his professional hold of Jo and appearing to Mr. Snagsby to possess an unlimited number of eyes, makes a little way into this room, when Jo starts and stops.", "여전히 직업적인 솜씨로 조를 붙든 채, 스내그스비 씨가 보기엔 눈이 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 듯한 버킷 씨가 이 방으로 조금 들어서는데, 조가 흠칫 멈춰 선다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"왜 그래?\""], ["says Bucket in a whisper.", "버킷이 속삭인다."]], [["“There she is!”", "\"저기 그 여자가 있어요!\""], ["cries Jo.", "조가 외친다."]], [["“Who!”", "\"누구?\""]], [["“The lady!”", "\"그 마님이요!\""]], [["A female figure, closely veiled, stands in the middle of the room, where the light falls upon it.", "베일을 촘촘히 두른 여자 형상 하나가 방 한가운데, 빛이 떨어지는 자리에 서 있다."], ["It is quite still and silent.", "미동도 없고 소리도 없다."], ["The front of the figure is towards them, but it takes no notice of their entrance and remains like a statue.", "형상의 앞쪽이 그들을 향해 있으나, 그들이 들어선 것에 아랑곳없이 조각상처럼 가만히 있다."]], [["“Now, tell me,”", "\"자, 말해 봐라.\""], ["says Bucket aloud, “how you know that to be the lady.”", "버킷이 큰 소리로 말한다. \"저게 그 마님인 걸 어떻게 아느냐.\""]], [["“I know the wale,”", "\"베일을 알아요.\""], ["replies Jo, staring, “and the bonnet, and the gownd.”", "조가 빤히 쳐다보며 대답한다. \"그리고 보닛이랑, 가운도요.\""]], [["“Be quite sure of what you say, Tough,”", "\"네 말을 똑똑히 확인해라, 텁수룩이.\""], ["returns Bucket, narrowly observant of him.", "버킷이 그를 면밀히 살피며 대꾸한다."], ["“Look again.”", "\"다시 봐라.\""]], [["“I am a-looking as hard as ever I can look,”", "\"있는 힘껏 보고 있는걸요.\""], ["says Jo with starting eyes, “and that there’s the wale, the bonnet, and the gownd.”", "조가 눈을 부릅뜨며 말한다. \"저게 바로 그 베일이고, 보닛이고, 가운이에요.\""]], [["“What about those rings you told me of?”", "\"네가 말한 그 반지들은?\""], ["asks Bucket.", "버킷이 묻는다."]], [["“A-sparkling all over here,”", "\"여기 온통 반짝거렸어요.\""], ["says Jo, rubbing the fingers of his left hand on the knuckles of his right without taking his eyes from the figure.", "조가 형상에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 왼손 손가락으로 오른손 손마디를 문지르며 말한다."]], [["The figure removes the right-hand glove and shows the hand.", "형상이 오른손 장갑을 벗고 손을 보인다."]], [["“Now, what do you say to that?”", "\"자, 저건 어떠냐?\""], ["asks Bucket.", "버킷이 묻는다."]], [["Jo shakes his head.", "조가 고개를 젓는다."], ["“Not rings a bit like them.", "\"그것들이랑 조금도 안 닮은 반지예요."], ["Not a hand like that.”", "그런 손도 아니고요.\""]], [["“What are you talking of?”", "\"무슨 소리야?\""], ["says Bucket, evidently pleased though, and well pleased too.", "버킷이 말하는데, 분명 흡족한, 그것도 아주 흡족한 기색이다."]], [["“Hand was a deal whiter, a deal delicater, and a deal smaller,”", "\"손이 훨씬 더 희고, 훨씬 더 곱고, 훨씬 더 작았어요.\""], ["returns Jo.", "조가 대답한다."]], [["“Why, you’ll tell me I’m my own mother next,”", "\"허, 이러다 내가 내 친어미라고 하겠구나.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Do you recollect the lady’s voice?”", "\"그 마님 목소리는 기억하느냐?\""]], [["“I think I does,”", "\"기억하는 것 같아요.\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["The figure speaks.", "형상이 입을 연다."], ["“Was it at all like this?", "\"혹시 이런 목소리였니?"], ["I will speak as long as you like if you are not sure.", "확실치 않으면 네가 원하는 만큼 얼마든 말해 주마."], ["Was it this voice, or at all like this voice?”", "이 목소리였니, 아니면 이 목소리와 비슷하기라도 하니?\""]], [["Jo looks aghast at Mr.", "조가 버킷 씨를 질겁한 얼굴로 본다."], ["Bucket. “Not a bit!”", "\"조금도 안 닮았어요!\""]], [["“Then, what,”", "\"그럼 어째서,\""], ["retorts that worthy, pointing to the figure, “did you say it was the lady for?”", "그 훌륭한 사내가 형상을 가리키며 되묻는다. \"저게 그 마님이라고 했느냐?\""]], [["“Cos,”", "\"그건요,\""], ["says Jo with a perplexed stare but without being at all shaken in his certainty, “cos that there’s the wale, the bonnet, and the gownd.", "조가 어리둥절 빤히 보면서도 확신은 조금도 흔들리지 않은 채 말한다. \"그건 저게 바로 그 베일이고, 보닛이고, 가운이라서요."], ["It is her and it an’t her.", "저건 그 여자이면서도 그 여자가 아니에요."], ["It an’t her hand, nor yet her rings, nor yet her woice.", "그 여자 손도 아니고, 반지도 아니고, 목소리도 아니에요."], ["But that there’s the wale, the bonnet, and the gownd, and they’re wore the same way wot she wore ’em, and it’s her height wot she wos, and she giv me a sov’ring and hooked it.”", "그치만 저게 바로 그 베일이고, 보닛이고, 가운이고, 그 여자가 두르던 그대로 둘렀고, 키도 그 여자만 하고, 그 여자는 나한테 일 파운드 금화를 주고 내뺐다고요.\""]], [["“Well!” says Mr.", "\"좋아!\""], ["Bucket slightly, “we haven’t got much good out of YOU.", "버킷 씨가 대수롭잖게 말한다. \"너한테서는 별로 건진 게 없구나."], ["But, however, here’s five shillings for you.", "그래도, 자, 여기 오 실링이다."], ["Take care how you spend it, and don’t get yourself into trouble.”", "어떻게 쓰는지 조심하고, 말썽에 휘말리지 마라.\""], ["Bucket stealthily tells the coins from one hand into the other like counters—which is a way he has, his principal use of them being in these games of skill—and then puts them, in a little pile, into the boy’s hand and takes him out to the door, leaving Mr.", "버킷은 동전을 한 손에서 다른 손으로 패 세듯 슬쩍 헤아리는데—이건 그의 버릇으로, 동전을 주로 이런 손재간 놀이에 쓴다—그러고는 작은 무더기로 모아 아이 손에 쥐여 주고 문 밖으로 데리고 나간다."], ["Snagsby, not by any means comfortable under these mysterious circumstances, alone with the veiled figure. But on Mr.", "그 바람에 이 알 수 없는 상황에 도무지 마음이 편치 않은 스내그스비 씨는 베일 두른 형상과 단둘이 남겨진다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s coming into the room, the veil is raised and a sufficiently good-looking Frenchwoman is revealed, though her expression is something of the intensest.", "그러나 토킹혼 씨가 방으로 들어오자 베일이 걷히고, 제법 반반한 프랑스 여자가 드러나는데, 그 표정만은 어딘가 더없이 사나운 데가 있다."]], [["“Thank you, Mademoiselle Hortense,”", "\"고맙소, 오르탕스 양.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn with his usual equanimity.", "토킹혼 씨가 평소의 침착함으로 말한다."], ["“I will give you no further trouble about this little wager.”", "\"이 자그마한 내기 건으로 더는 폐를 끼치지 않으리다.\""]], [["“You will do me the kindness to remember, sir, that I am not at present placed?”", "\"제가 지금은 자리가 없는 처지인 걸 부디 잊지 말아 주시겠지요, 나리?\""], ["says mademoiselle.", "마드무아젤이 말한다."]], [["“Certainly, certainly!”", "\"물론이오, 물론.\""]], [["“And to confer upon me the favour of your distinguished recommendation?”", "\"그리고 나리의 그 빛나는 추천이라는 호의를 제게 베풀어 주시겠지요?\""]], [["“By all means, Mademoiselle Hortense.”", "\"여부가 있겠소, 오르탕스 양.\""]], [["“A word from Mr. Tulkinghorn is so powerful.”", "\"토킹혼 씨의 한마디는 그토록 막강하니까요.\""]], [["“It shall not be wanting, mademoiselle.”", "\"빠짐없이 해 드리리다, 마드무아젤.\""]], [["“Receive the assurance of my devoted gratitude, dear sir.”", "\"제 한결같은 감사를 받아 주십시오, 친애하는 나리.\""]], [["“Good night.”", "\"잘 가시오.\""]], [["Mademoiselle goes out with an air of native gentility; and Mr.", "마드무아젤은 타고난 기품 어린 자태로 나간다."], ["Bucket, to whom it is, on an emergency, as natural to be groom of the ceremonies as it is to be anything else, shows her downstairs, not without gallantry.", "그리고 버킷 씨는, 비상시에는 의전관 노릇 하는 것이 다른 무엇 하는 것 못지않게 자연스러운 사람인지라, 자못 정중하게 그녀를 아래층까지 배웅한다."]], [["“Well, Bucket?”", "\"그래, 버킷?\""], ["quoth Mr. Tulkinghorn on his return.", "돌아온 그에게 토킹혼 씨가 묻는다."]], [["“It’s all squared, you see, as I squared it myself, sir.", "\"보시다시피, 제가 직접 맞춰 둔 대로 다 정리됐습니다요, 나리."], ["There an’t a doubt that it was the other one with this one’s dress on.", "그게 이 여자 옷을 입은 그 다른 여자였다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없습니다."], ["The boy was exact respecting colours and everything. Mr.", "그 애가 빛깔이며 뭐며 다 정확했지요."], ["Snagsby, I promised you as a man that he should be sent away all right. Don’t say it wasn’t done!”", "스내그스비 씨, 그 애가 무사히 돌아갈 거라고 사내대장부로서 약속드렸는데, 그대로 되지 않았다곤 못 하실 겁니다!\""]], [["“You have kept your word, sir,”", "\"약속을 지키셨소, 나리.\""], ["returns the stationer; “and if I can be of no further use, Mr.", "문방구상이 대답한다."], ["Tulkinghorn, I think, as my little woman will be getting anxious—”", "\"더 도울 일이 없다면, 토킹혼 씨, 제 마누라가 애를 태우고 있을 듯해서—\""]], [["“Thank you, Snagsby, no further use,”", "\"고맙소, 스내그스비, 더 도울 일은 없소.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“I am quite indebted to you for the trouble you have taken already.”", "\"이미 수고해 주신 것만으로도 신세를 톡톡히 졌소.\""]], [["“Not at all, sir.", "\"천만에요, 나리."], ["I wish you good night.”", "그럼 안녕히 주무십시오.\""]], [["“You see, Mr.", "\"있잖소, 스내그스비 씨.\""], ["Snagsby,” says Mr.", "버킷 씨가 그를 문까지 배웅하며 거듭거듭 악수하면서 말한다."], ["Bucket, accompanying him to the door and shaking hands with him over and over again, “what I like in you is that you’re a man it’s of no use pumping; that’s what YOU are.", "\"내가 당신한테서 마음에 드는 점은, 당신이 캐물어 봐야 아무 소용없는 사람이란 거요. 당신이 그런 사람이지."], ["When you know you have done a right thing, you put it away, and it’s done with and gone, and there’s an end of it.", "옳은 일을 했다 싶으면 그걸 딱 치워 버리고, 그걸로 끝, 사라져 버리고, 그게 끝이오."], ["That’s what YOU do.”", "당신이 그러는 사람이지.\""]], [["“That is certainly what I endeavour to do, sir,”", "\"그러려고 애쓰는 건 분명합니다요, 나리.\""], ["returns Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 대답한다."]], [["“No, you don’t do yourself justice.", "\"아니, 당신은 자기를 너무 박하게 보는구먼."], ["It an’t what you endeavour to do,” says Mr.", "애쓰는 정도가 아니라.\""], ["Bucket, shaking hands with him and blessing him in the tenderest manner, “it’s what you DO.", "버킷 씨가 그와 악수하며 더없이 다정하게 축복을 빌어 주듯 말한다. \"당신은 실제로 그렇게 하는 사람이오."], ["That’s what I estimate in a man in your way of business.”", "그게 바로 내가 당신 같은 장사하는 사람한테서 높이 사는 점이지.\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby makes a suitable response and goes homeward so confused by the events of the evening that he is doubtful of his being awake and out—doubtful of the reality of the streets through which he goes—doubtful of the reality of the moon that shines above him.", "스내그스비 씨는 알맞은 대꾸를 하고 집으로 향하는데, 저녁의 일들에 어찌나 얼떨떨한지 자기가 깨어 밖에 나와 있는 건지조차 미덥지 않고—지나는 거리가 진짜인지도 미덥지 않고—머리 위에 빛나는 달이 진짜인지도 미덥지 않다."], ["He is presently reassured on these subjects by the unchallengeable reality of Mrs.", "그러나 이 문제들에 대해 그는 곧 의심할 여지 없는 진짜인 스내그스비 부인 덕에 안심하게 되니, 그녀는 머리에 종이 말이 개와 잠자리 모자를 완벽한 벌집처럼 두르고 안 자고 기다리는 중이다."], ["Snagsby, sitting up with her head in a perfect beehive of curl-papers and night-cap, who has dispatched Guster to the police-station with official intelligence of her husband’s being made away with, and who within the last two hours has passed through every stage of swooning with the greatest decorum.", "그녀는 남편이 해코지를 당했다는 공식 정보를 들려 거스터를 경찰서로 보낸 데다, 지난 두 시간 동안 더없이 점잖은 품위를 갖추고 기절의 온갖 단계를 두루 거쳤더랬다."], ["But as the little woman feelingly says, many thanks she gets for it! Esther’s Narrative", "그러나 이 자그마한 부인이 절절하게 말하듯, 그렇게 해 준 보답이 참으로 융숭하기도 하지!"]], [["We came home from Mr. Boythorn’s after six pleasant weeks.", "여섯 주에 걸친 즐거운 시간을 보낸 뒤, 우리는 보이손 씨 댁에서 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["We were often in the park and in the woods and seldom passed the lodge where we had taken shelter without looking in to speak to the keeper’s wife; but we saw no more of Lady Dedlock, except at church on Sundays.", "우리는 공원과 숲을 자주 거닐었고, 비를 피했던 그 오두막을 지날 때면 으레 안을 들여다보며 관리인의 아내와 말을 나누곤 했다. 하지만 데드록 마님은 일요일 교회에서 말고는 더 보지 못했다."], ["There was company at Chesney Wold; and although several beautiful faces surrounded her, her face retained the same influence on me as at first.", "체스니 월드에는 손님이 모여 있었고, 아름다운 얼굴들이 마님을 에워쌌지만, 마님의 얼굴은 처음과 똑같은 영향을 내게 미쳤다."], ["I do not quite know even now whether it was painful or pleasurable, whether it drew me towards her or made me shrink from her.", "그것이 괴로움이었는지 즐거움이었는지, 나를 마님에게로 끌어당겼는지 아니면 움츠러들게 했는지, 나는 지금도 잘 모르겠다."], ["I think I admired her with a kind of fear, and I know that in her presence my thoughts always wandered back, as they had done at first, to that old time of my life.", "나는 일종의 두려움을 품고 마님을 흠모했던 것 같고, 마님 앞에 서면 내 생각은 처음 그랬듯이 늘 내 삶의 그 옛 시절로 떠돌아갔다."]], [["I had a fancy, on more than one of these Sundays, that what this lady so curiously was to me, I was to her—I mean that I disturbed her thoughts as she influenced mine, though in some different way.", "이런 일요일이 거듭되는 동안, 나는 한 번 이상 이런 공상에 잠겼다. 이 부인이 그토록 묘하게 나에게 어떤 존재인 것처럼, 나 또한 그분에게 그런 존재가 아닐까 하는 것이었다."], ["But when I stole a glance at her and saw her so composed and distant and unapproachable, I felt this to be a foolish weakness.", "그러니까, 마님이 내 생각을 흔들어 놓듯이, 비록 어딘가 다른 방식이긴 해도 나도 마님의 생각을 어지럽히는 게 아닐까 싶었다."], ["Indeed, I felt the whole state of my mind in reference to her to be weak and unreasonable, and I remonstrated with myself about it as much as I could.", "하지만 슬쩍 곁눈질로 마님을 보면, 그토록 침착하고 멀고 다가갈 수 없는 모습이어서, 나는 이것이 어리석은 나약함이라고 느꼈다. 정말이지, 마님과 관련한 내 마음 전체가 나약하고 분별없다고 느껴졌고, 나는 그 점을 두고 할 수 있는 한 스스로를 타일렀다."]], [["One incident that occurred before we quitted Mr. Boythorn’s house, I had better mention in this place.", "보이손 씨 댁을 떠나기 전에 일어난 한 가지 일을, 여기서 짚고 넘어가는 편이 낫겠다."]], [["I was walking in the garden with Ada when I was told that some one wished to see me.", "내가 에이다와 함께 정원을 거닐고 있을 때, 누군가 나를 만나고 싶어 한다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["Going into the breakfast-room where this person was waiting, I found it to be the French maid who had cast off her shoes and walked through the wet grass on the day when it thundered and lightened.", "그 사람이 기다리고 있는 아침 식당으로 들어가 보니, 천둥과 번개가 치던 날 신발을 벗어 던지고 젖은 풀밭을 걸어가던 그 프랑스인 하녀였다."]], [["“Mademoiselle,”", "\"마드무아젤,\" 그녀는 너무 간절한 눈빛으로 나를 뚫어지게 바라보며 입을 열었다."], ["she began, looking fixedly at me with her too-eager eyes, though otherwise presenting an agreeable appearance and speaking neither with boldness nor servility, “I have taken a great liberty in coming here, but you know how to excuse it, being so amiable, mademoiselle.”", "그래도 다른 면에서는 호감 가는 모습이었고, 말투에 당돌함도 비굴함도 없었다. \"이렇게 찾아온 게 큰 결례인 줄 압니다만, 마드무아젤, 워낙 다정하신 분이니 너그러이 봐주실 줄로 알아요.\""]], [["“No excuse is necessary,”", "\"용서를 구할 일은 없어요.\""], ["I returned, “if you wish to speak to me.”", "내가 대답했다. \"내게 할 말이 있다면 말이에요.\""]], [["“That is my desire, mademoiselle.", "\"바로 그게 제 바람입니다, 마드무아젤."], ["A thousand thanks for the permission.", "허락해 주셔서 천 번 감사드려요."], ["I have your leave to speak.", "말씀드려도 되는 거지요."], ["Is it not?”", "그렇지요?\""], ["she said in a quick, natural way.", "그녀가 빠르고 자연스러운 말씨로 물었다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이에요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["“Mademoiselle, you are so amiable!", "\"마드무아젤, 정말 다정하세요!"], ["Listen then, if you please.", "그럼 들어 주세요, 부탁드려요."], ["I have left my Lady.", "저는 마님 곁을 떠났습니다."], ["We could not agree.", "우리는 도무지 맞질 않았어요."], ["My Lady is so high, so very high.", "마님은 어찌나 도도하신지, 정말이지 너무나 도도하세요."], ["Pardon!", "죄송해요!"], ["Mademoiselle, you are right!”", "마드무아젤, 옳으신 말씀이에요!\""], ["Her quickness anticipated what I might have said presently but as yet had only thought.", "그녀의 재빠름은 내가 곧 입에 올렸을 법한, 그러나 아직 생각만 하고 있던 말을 앞질렀다."], ["“It is not for me to come here to complain of my Lady.", "\"여기까지 와서 마님을 험담할 처지는 못 됩니다."], ["But I say she is so high, so very high.", "하지만 마님이 너무나 도도하시다는 것만은 말씀드려요."], ["I will not say a word more.", "정말 너무나 도도하세요. 더는 한마디도 하지 않겠어요."], ["All the world knows that.”", "세상이 다 아는 사실이니까요.\""]], [["“Go on, if you please,”", "\"계속하세요, 부탁드려요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Assuredly; mademoiselle, I am thankful for your politeness.", "\"그럼요. 마드무아젤, 친절하게 대해 주셔서 감사합니다."], ["Mademoiselle, I have an inexpressible desire to find service with a young lady who is good, accomplished, beautiful.", "마드무아젤, 저는 착하고, 교양 있고, 아름다운 젊은 아가씨를 모시고 싶은 마음이 이루 말할 수 없습니다."], ["You are good, accomplished, and beautiful as an angel.", "마드무아젤께서는 천사처럼 착하고, 교양 있고, 아름다우세요."], ["Ah, could I have the honour of being your domestic!”", "아, 제가 마드무아젤의 하녀가 되는 영광을 누릴 수만 있다면!\""]], [["“I am sorry—”", "\"미안하지만—\""], ["I began.", "내가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Do not dismiss me so soon, mademoiselle!”", "\"그렇게 빨리 물리치지 말아 주세요, 마드무아젤!\""], ["she said with an involuntary contraction of her fine black eyebrows.", "그녀는 고운 검은 눈썹을 저도 모르게 찌푸리며 말했다. \"잠깐만 희망을 품게 해 주세요!"], ["“Let me hope a moment!", "마드무아젤, 저도 압니다."], ["Mademoiselle, I know this service would be more retired than that which I have quitted.", "이 자리가 제가 떠나온 그 자리보다 훨씬 한적하리라는 걸요. 좋아요!"], ["Well! I wish that.", "저는 그걸 바라요."], ["I know this service would be less distinguished than that which I have quitted.", "이 자리가 제가 떠나온 그 자리보다 덜 화려하리라는 것도 압니다."], ["Well!", "좋아요!"], ["I wish that, I know that I should win less, as to wages here.", "저는 그걸 바라요. 여기서는 받는 삯이 더 적으리라는 것도 알아요."], ["Good.", "좋습니다."], ["I am content.”", "저는 만족해요.\""]], [["“I assure you,”", "\"분명히 말하지만,\""], ["said I, quite embarrassed by the mere idea of having such an attendant, “that I keep no maid—”", "그런 시중꾼을 둔다는 생각만으로도 무척 당황하여 내가 말했다. \"나는 하녀를 두지 않아요—\""]], [["“Ah, mademoiselle, but why not?", "\"아, 마드무아젤, 그렇지만 왜 안 되나요?"], ["Why not, when you can have one so devoted to you!", "이토록 헌신할 사람을 둘 수 있는데 왜 안 되겠어요!"], ["Who would be enchanted to serve you; who would be so true, so zealous, and so faithful every day!", "마드무아젤을 모시는 데 황홀해할 사람을요. 날마다 그토록 진실하고, 그토록 열성적이고, 그토록 충직할 사람을요!"], ["Mademoiselle, I wish with all my heart to serve you.", "마드무아젤, 저는 진심으로 마드무아젤을 모시고 싶습니다."], ["Do not speak of money at present.", "지금은 돈 이야기는 하지 마세요."], ["Take me as I am.", "있는 그대로 저를 받아 주세요."], ["For nothing!”", "거저요!\""]], [["She was so singularly earnest that I drew back, almost afraid of her.", "그녀가 어찌나 유별나게 간절한지 나는 거의 겁이 나 뒷걸음질을 쳤다."], ["Without appearing to notice it, in her ardour she still pressed herself upon me, speaking in a rapid subdued voice, though always with a certain grace and propriety.", "그런 기색을 알아챈 내색도 없이, 그녀는 열정에 휩싸여 여전히 빠르고 낮은 목소리로 나에게 자꾸만 매달렸다. 그러면서도 늘 어떤 우아함과 단정함을 잃지 않았다."]], [["“Mademoiselle, I come from the South country where we are quick and where we like and dislike very strong.", "\"마드무아젤, 저는 남쪽 지방 출신이에요. 그곳 사람들은 성미가 급하고, 좋아하고 싫어하는 게 아주 분명하지요."], ["My Lady was too high for me; I was too high for her.", "마님은 저에게 너무 도도하셨고, 저도 마님에게 너무 도도했어요."], ["It is done—past—finished!", "끝난 일이에요—지나간—마무리된 일입니다!"], ["Receive me as your domestic, and I will serve you well.", "저를 하녀로 받아 주세요. 그럼 잘 모실게요."], ["I will do more for you than you figure to yourself now.", "마드무아젤이 지금 상상하시는 것보다 더 잘해 드릴 거예요."], ["Chut!", "쉿!"], ["Mademoiselle, I will—no matter, I will do my utmost possible in all things.", "마드무아젤, 저는—무슨 일이든, 무슨 일이든 제가 할 수 있는 모든 걸 다할 거예요."], ["If you accept my service, you will not repent it.", "제 시중을 받아 주시면 후회하지 않으실 거예요."], ["Mademoiselle, you will not repent it, and I will serve you well.", "마드무아젤, 후회하지 않으실 거예요. 그리고 저는 잘 모실게요."], ["You don’t know how well!”", "얼마나 잘 모실지 모르실 거예요!\""]], [["There was a lowering energy in her face as she stood looking at me while I explained the impossibility of my engaging her (without thinking it necessary to say how very little I desired to do so), which seemed to bring visibly before me some woman from the streets of Paris in the reign of terror.", "내가 그녀를 들일 수 없는 까닭을 설명하는 동안(들이고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 없는지까지 굳이 말할 필요는 없다고 여겼다), 서서 나를 바라보는 그녀의 얼굴에는 음울하게 도사린 기운이 어려 있었는데, 그것이 공포 정치 시절 파리 거리를 누비던 어떤 여인의 모습을 눈앞에 선연히 떠올리게 했다."]], [["She heard me out without interruption and then said with her pretty accent and in her mildest voice, “Hey, mademoiselle, I have received my answer!", "그녀는 내 말을 끊지 않고 끝까지 듣고는, 고운 억양과 더없이 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"어머, 마드무아젤, 답을 들었네요!"], ["I am sorry of it.", "유감입니다."], ["But I must go elsewhere and seek what I have not found here.", "그래도 다른 데로 가서 여기서 못 찾은 것을 찾아봐야지요."], ["Will you graciously let me kiss your hand?”", "황송하오나 손등에 입을 맞추게 해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["She looked at me more intently as she took it, and seemed to take note, with her momentary touch, of every vein in it.", "그녀는 내 손을 잡으며 더욱 골똘히 나를 바라보았고, 잠깐 닿는 동안 그 손의 핏줄 하나하나까지 새겨 두려는 듯했다."], ["“I fear I surprised you, mademoiselle, on the day of the storm?”", "\"폭풍우가 치던 날, 제가 마드무아젤을 놀라게 했나 봐요?\""], ["she said with a parting curtsy.", "그녀는 작별의 절을 하며 말했다."]], [["I confessed that she had surprised us all.", "나는 우리 모두를 놀라게 했다고 인정했다."]], [["“I took an oath, mademoiselle,”", "\"저는 맹세를 했답니다, 마드무아젤.\""], ["she said, smiling, “and I wanted to stamp it on my mind so that I might keep it faithfully.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그래서 그 맹세를 충실히 지키려고 마음에 새겨 두고 싶었어요."], ["And I will!", "꼭 지킬 거예요!"], ["Adieu, mademoiselle!”", "안녕히 계세요, 마드무아젤!\""]], [["So ended our conference, which I was very glad to bring to a close.", "이렇게 우리의 면담은 끝났고, 나는 이 자리를 마무리하게 되어 무척 기뻤다. 짐작하건대 그녀는 마을을 떠난 듯했다."], ["I supposed she went away from the village, for I saw her no more; and nothing else occurred to disturb our tranquil summer pleasures until six weeks were out and we returned home as I began just now by saying.", "그 뒤로 다시는 보지 못했으니까. 그리고 여섯 주가 다 차서 방금 말한 대로 집으로 돌아올 때까지, 우리의 고요한 여름날의 즐거움을 흩뜨릴 일은 더는 일어나지 않았다."]], [["At that time, and for a good many weeks after that time, Richard was constant in his visits.", "그 무렵, 그리고 그 뒤로도 여러 주에 걸쳐 리처드는 꾸준히 우리를 찾아왔다."], ["Besides coming every Saturday or Sunday and remaining with us until Monday morning, he sometimes rode out on horseback unexpectedly and passed the evening with us and rode back again early next day.", "토요일이나 일요일마다 와서 월요일 아침까지 우리와 함께 지내는 것은 물론, 이따금 불쑥 말을 타고 나와서 저녁을 함께 보낸 뒤 이튿날 일찍 다시 말을 타고 돌아가기도 했다."], ["He was as vivacious as ever and told us he was very industrious, but I was not easy in my mind about him.", "그는 여전히 활기가 넘쳤고 자기가 무척 부지런히 일한다고 말했지만, 나는 그가 마음에 걸려 편치 않았다."], ["It appeared to me that his industry was all misdirected.", "내가 보기에 그의 부지런함은 죄다 엉뚱한 데로 쏠려 있었다."], ["I could not find that it led to anything but the formation of delusive hopes in connexion with the suit already the pernicious cause of so much sorrow and ruin.", "그 부지런함이 이미 그토록 많은 슬픔과 파멸을 불러온 그 해로운 소송과 얽혀 헛된 희망을 키우는 것 말고 무엇으로도 이어지지 않는 듯했다."], ["He had got at the core of that mystery now, he told us, and nothing could be plainer than that the will under which he and Ada were to take I don’t know how many thousands of pounds must be finally established if there were any sense or justice in the Court of Chancery—but oh, what a great IF that sounded in my ears—and that this happy conclusion could not be much longer delayed.", "그는 이제 그 수수께끼의 핵심을 파고들었다며, 대법원에 조금이라도 분별이나 정의가 남아 있다면 자기와 에이다가 몇천 파운드인지 모를 돈을 받게 될 그 유언장이 끝내 인정받으리라는 것보다 더 명백한 일은 없다고 말했다. 하지만, 아, 그 \"만약\"이라는 말이 내 귀에는 얼마나 거대하게 울렸던가."], ["He proved this to himself by all the weary arguments on that side he had read, and every one of them sunk him deeper in the infatuation.", "그리고 이 행복한 결말이 그리 오래 미뤄지지는 않으리라고 했다. 그는 그쪽 편에 서서 읽은 온갖 지긋지긋한 논거를 들어 이를 스스로에게 입증해 보였는데, 그 하나하나가 그를 망상 속으로 더 깊이 빠뜨렸다."], ["He had even begun to haunt the court.", "그는 심지어 법정을 들락거리기 시작했다."], ["He told us how he saw Miss Flite there daily, how they talked together, and how he did her little kindnesses, and how, while he laughed at her, he pitied her from his heart.", "그는 거기서 날마다 플라이트 양을 본다고, 둘이 함께 이야기를 나눈다고, 그녀에게 자잘한 친절을 베푼다고, 그러면서도 그녀를 비웃으면서 한편으로는 진심으로 가엾이 여긴다고 들려주었다."], ["But he never thought—never, my poor, dear, sanguine Richard, capable of so much happiness then, and with such better things before him—what a fatal link was riveting between his fresh youth and her faded age, between his free hopes and her caged birds, and her hungry garret, and her wandering mind.", "하지만 그는 결코 생각하지 못했다—결코, 가엾고 다정하고 낙천적인 내 리처드, 그때만 해도 그토록 큰 행복을 누릴 수 있었고 그 앞에 그토록 더 나은 것들이 펼쳐져 있던 그 사람은—그의 싱그러운 젊음과 그녀의 시든 노년 사이에, 그의 자유로운 희망과 그녀의 새장에 갇힌 새들과 그녀의 굶주린 다락방과 그녀의 헤매는 정신 사이에 얼마나 운명적인 사슬이 단단히 채워지고 있는지를."]], [["Ada loved him too well to mistrust him much in anything he said or did, and my guardian, though he frequently complained of the east wind and read more than usual in the growlery, preserved a strict silence on the subject.", "에이다는 그를 너무나 사랑한지라 그가 하는 말이나 행동을 그다지 의심하지 못했고, 후견인은 동풍이 분다고 자주 투덜대며 으르렁 방에서 평소보다 책을 많이 읽긴 했지만 이 일에 대해서는 굳게 입을 다물었다."], ["So I thought one day when I went to London to meet Caddy Jellyby, at her solicitation, I would ask Richard to be in waiting for me at the coach-office, that we might have a little talk together.", "그래서 어느 날 캐디 젤리비의 청을 받고 그녀를 만나러 런던에 가게 되었을 때, 나는 리처드에게 역마차 사무소에서 나를 기다려 달라고 부탁하여 둘이서 잠깐 이야기를 나누어 보아야겠다고 생각했다."], ["I found him there when I arrived, and we walked away arm in arm.", "내가 도착하니 그가 거기 있었고, 우리는 팔짱을 끼고 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“Well, Richard,”", "\"리처드,\""], ["said I as soon as I could begin to be grave with him, “are you beginning to feel more settled now?”", "그를 두고 진지해질 만하다 싶자마자 내가 말했다. \"이제 좀 자리가 잡히는 것 같니?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, my dear!”", "\"오, 그럼, 에스더!\""], ["returned Richard.", "리처드가 대답했다."], ["“I’m all right enough.”", "\"난 충분히 괜찮아.\""]], [["“But settled?”", "\"하지만 자리가 잡혔냐고?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean, settled?”", "\"자리가 잡힌다는 게 무슨 뜻이야?\""], ["returned Richard with his gay laugh.", "리처드가 명랑하게 웃으며 되물었다."]], [["“Settled in the law,”", "\"법조계에 자리가 잡혔냐고.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, aye,”", "\"아, 그래,\""], ["replied Richard, “I’m all right enough.”", "리처드가 대답했다. \"난 충분히 괜찮아.\""]], [["“You said that before, my dear Richard.”", "\"방금도 그렇게 말했잖아, 리처드.\""]], [["“And you don’t think it’s an answer, eh?", "\"그게 대답이 안 된다고 생각하는 거지, 응?"], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["Perhaps it’s not.", "아마 아닐지도 모르지."], ["Settled?", "자리가 잡혔냐고?"], ["You mean, do I feel as if I were settling down?”", "그러니까, 내가 자리를 잡아 가는 것 같으냐는 거지?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Why, no, I can’t say I am settling down,” said Richard, strongly emphasizing “down,”", "\"글쎄, 아니지, 자리를 잡아 간다고는 못 하겠어.\""], ["as if that expressed the difficulty, “because one can’t settle down while this business remains in such an unsettled state.", "리처드가 마치 그 말에 어려움이 담겨 있다는 듯 \"잡아\"에 잔뜩 힘을 주며 말했다. \"이 일이 이렇게 안 잡힌 채로 있는 한 누구도 자리를 잡을 수가 없으니까."], ["When I say this business, of course I mean the—forbidden subject.”", "이 일이라고 하면, 물론 그—입에 올리면 안 되는 그 일을 말하는 거야.\""]], [["“Do you think it will ever be in a settled state?”", "\"그게 언젠가 매듭이 지어지긴 할 것 같니?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Not the least doubt of it,”", "\"한 치도 의심하지 않아.\""], ["answered Richard.", "리처드가 대답했다."]], [["We walked a little way without speaking, and presently Richard addressed me in his frankest and most feeling manner, thus: “My dear Esther, I understand you, and I wish to heaven I were a more constant sort of fellow.", "우리는 말없이 조금 걸었고, 이윽고 리처드가 더없이 솔직하고 진실한 태도로 나에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다. \"에스더, 무슨 말인지 알아."], ["I don’t mean constant to Ada, for I love her dearly—better and better every day—but constant to myself.", "그리고 내가 좀 더 한결같은 사람이었으면 하고 진심으로 바라. 에이다에게 한결같다는 뜻이 아니야."], ["(Somehow, I mean something that I can’t very well express, but you’ll make it out.) If I were a more constant sort of fellow, I should have held on either to Badger or to Kenge and Carboy like grim death, and should have begun to be steady and systematic by this time, and shouldn’t be in debt, and—”", "난 그 애를 끔찍이 사랑하니까—날마다 점점 더—그게 아니라 나 자신에게 한결같다는 뜻이야. (어쩐지, 잘 표현은 못 하겠지만, 너라면 알아들을 거야.) 내가 좀 더 한결같은 사람이었다면, 배저 선생이든 켄지 앤 카보이든 죽기 살기로 매달렸을 테고, 지금쯤은 차분하고 체계적으로 굴기 시작했을 테고, 빚도 안 졌을 테고, 그리고—\""]], [["“ARE you in debt, Richard?”", "\"빚을 졌단 말이니, 리처드?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"응,\""], ["said Richard, “I am a little so, my dear.", "리처드가 말했다. \"조금 졌어, 에스더."], ["Also, I have taken rather too much to billiards and that sort of thing.", "게다가 당구나 그런 것에도 좀 지나치게 손을 댔어. 자, 이제 죄다 털어놓았다."], ["Now the murder’s out; you despise me, Esther, don’t you?”", "너 나를 경멸하는 거지, 에스더, 그렇지?\""]], [["“You know I don’t,”", "\"내가 그러지 않는 거 알잖아.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“You are kinder to me than I often am to myself,”", "\"넌 내가 나 자신을 대하는 것보다 훨씬 다정하게 나를 대해 주는구나.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he returned.", "\"에스더, 나는 자리를 못 잡는 참 불운한 놈이야. 그렇지만 내가 어떻게 자리를 잡을 수 있겠어?"], ["“My dear Esther, I am a very unfortunate dog not to be more settled, but how CAN I be more settled?", "완공되지 않은 집에서 산다면 거기에 정착할 수 없을 거야. 손대는 일마다 끝맺지 못한 채 내버려 두라는 선고를 받는다면, 무슨 일에든 마음 붙이기가 힘들 거야."], ["If you lived in an unfinished house, you couldn’t settle down in it; if you were condemned to leave everything you undertook unfinished, you would find it hard to apply yourself to anything; and yet that’s my unhappy case.", "그런데 그게 바로 내 불행한 처지야. 나는 온갖 우여곡절을 안은 이 끝나지 않는 분쟁 속으로 태어났고, 그것은 내가 소송과 양복 한 벌의 차이도 채 알기 전부터 나를 들뜨게 흔들어 놓기 시작했어."], ["I was born into this unfinished contention with all its chances and changes, and it began to unsettle me before I quite knew the difference between a suit at law and a suit of clothes; and it has gone on unsettling me ever since; and here I am now, conscious sometimes that I am but a worthless fellow to love my confiding cousin Ada.”", "그 뒤로 줄곧 나를 흔들어 놓았고, 그래서 지금 나는 이러고 있는 거야. 나를 믿어 주는 사촌 에이다를 사랑하기엔 나야말로 보잘것없는 놈이라고 이따금 절감하면서 말이야.\""]], [["We were in a solitary place, and he put his hands before his eyes and sobbed as he said the words.", "우리는 한적한 곳에 있었고, 그는 이 말을 하면서 두 손으로 눈을 가리고 흐느꼈다."]], [["“Oh, Richard!”", "\"오, 리처드!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Do not be so moved.", "\"그렇게 마음 아파하지 마."], ["You have a noble nature, and Ada’s love may make you worthier every day.”", "너는 고결한 천성을 지녔고, 에이다의 사랑이 날마다 너를 더 훌륭하게 만들어 줄 거야.\""]], [["“I know, my dear,”", "\"알아, 에스더.\""], ["he replied, pressing my arm, “I know all that.", "그가 내 팔을 꼭 쥐며 대답했다. \"다 알아."], ["You mustn’t mind my being a little soft now, for I have had all this upon my mind for a long time, and have often meant to speak to you, and have sometimes wanted opportunity and sometimes courage.", "내가 지금 조금 무른 꼴을 보여도 흉보지 마. 이걸 오랫동안 마음에 담아 두고 너한테 말하려고 몇 번이나 마음먹었는데, 어떨 땐 기회가 없고 어떨 땐 용기가 없었어."], ["I know what the thought of Ada ought to do for me, but it doesn’t do it.", "에이다를 생각하면 마땅히 내가 어떻게 되어야 하는지 알지만, 그게 그렇게 되질 않아."], ["I am too unsettled even for that.", "나는 그러기에도 너무 들떠 있어."], ["I love her most devotedly, and yet I do her wrong, in doing myself wrong, every day and hour.", "나는 그 애를 더없이 헌신적으로 사랑하면서도, 날이면 날마다 시간마다 나 자신을 그르치며 그 애에게도 못할 짓을 하고 있어."], ["But it can’t last for ever.", "하지만 이게 영원히 갈 수는 없어."], ["We shall come on for a final hearing and get judgment in our favour, and then you and Ada shall see what I can really be!”", "우리는 최종 심리에 회부되어 우리에게 유리한 판결을 받을 거야. 그러면 너랑 에이다도 내가 정말 어떤 사람이 될 수 있는지 보게 될 거야!\""]], [["It had given me a pang to hear him sob and see the tears start out between his fingers, but that was infinitely less affecting to me than the hopeful animation with which he said these words.", "그가 흐느끼는 소리를 듣고 손가락 사이로 눈물이 솟는 것을 보니 가슴이 미어졌지만, 그가 이 말을 하며 보인 희망에 찬 활기는 그보다 비할 데 없이 더 나를 아프게 했다."]], [["“I have looked well into the papers, Esther.", "\"그 서류들을 잘 들여다봤어, 에스더."], ["I have been deep in them for months,”", "몇 달째 깊이 파고들었지.\""], ["he continued, recovering his cheerfulness in a moment, “and you may rely upon it that we shall come out triumphant.", "그는 순식간에 명랑함을 되찾으며 말을 이었다. \"우리가 승리를 거두리라는 걸 믿어도 돼."], ["As to years of delay, there has been no want of them, heaven knows!", "세월이 끌린 거로 말하자면, 하늘이 아시듯이 모자란 적이 없었지!"], ["And there is the greater probability of our bringing the matter to a speedy close; in fact, it’s on the paper now.", "그러니 이 일을 빨리 끝낼 가능성이 그만큼 더 크다는 거야. 사실 지금 심리 일정에 올라 있어."], ["It will be all right at last, and then you shall see!”", "끝내 다 잘될 거고, 그러면 너도 보게 될 거야!\""]], [["Recalling how he had just now placed Messrs. Kenge and Carboy in the same category with Mr. Badger, I asked him when he intended to be articled in Lincoln’s Inn.", "그가 방금 켄지 앤 카보이 변호사 사무소를 배저 선생과 같은 부류로 묶어 말한 것이 떠올라, 나는 언제 링컨 법학원에서 견습 계약을 맺을 생각이냐고 물었다."]], [["“There again!", "\"또 그 얘기로구나!"], ["I think not at all, Esther,”", "아예 안 할 것 같아, 에스더.\""], ["he returned with an effort.", "그가 애써 대답했다."], ["“I fancy I have had enough of it.", "\"그건 이제 진절머리가 난 것 같아."], ["Having worked at Jarndyce and Jarndyce like a galley slave, I have slaked my thirst for the law and satisfied myself that I shouldn’t like it.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송에 노예처럼 매달려 일하다 보니, 법에 대한 갈증은 풀렸고 내가 그걸 좋아하지 않으리라는 걸 알게 됐어."], ["Besides, I find it unsettles me more and more to be so constantly upon the scene of action.", "게다가 이렇게 늘 사건 현장에 붙어 있으니 점점 더 마음이 들뜨더라고."], ["So what,”", "그러니,\""], ["continued Richard, confident again by this time, “do I naturally turn my thoughts to?”", "리처드는 이때쯤 다시 자신감을 되찾고 말을 이었다. \"내가 자연스레 마음을 두는 게 뭐겠어?\""]], [["“I can’t imagine,”", "\"짐작이 안 가는걸.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Don’t look so serious,”", "\"그렇게 심각한 표정 짓지 마.\""], ["returned Richard, “because it’s the best thing I can do, my dear Esther, I am certain.", "리처드가 대답했다. \"이게 내가 할 수 있는 최선이거든, 에스더, 확실해."], ["It’s not as if I wanted a profession for life.", "평생 직업이 필요한 것도 아니잖아."], ["These proceedings will come to a termination, and then I am provided for. No.", "이 소송 절차는 끝날 테고, 그러면 나는 살길이 마련되는 거니까."], ["I look upon it as a pursuit which is in its nature more or less unsettled, and therefore suited to my temporary condition—I may say, precisely suited.", "아니야. 나는 그것을 본디 어느 정도 정착되지 않은 일, 그래서 내 임시 처지에 딱 맞는—그래, 그야말로 딱 맞는 일이라고 봐."], ["What is it that I naturally turn my thoughts to?”", "내가 자연스레 마음을 두는 게 뭐겠어?\""]], [["I looked at him and shook my head.", "나는 그를 바라보며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“What,”", "\"군대 말고,\""], ["said Richard, in a tone of perfect conviction, “but the army!”", "리처드가 더없이 확신에 찬 어조로 말했다. \"뭐가 있겠어!\""]], [["“The army?”", "\"군대?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“The army, of course.", "\"군대지, 당연히."], ["What I have to do is to get a commission; and—there I am, you know!”", "내가 할 일은 장교 임관장을 따는 거야. 그러면—나는 그걸로 된 거지, 알겠어!\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했다."]], [["And then he showed me, proved by elaborate calculations in his pocket-book, that supposing he had contracted, say, two hundred pounds of debt in six months out of the army; and that he contracted no debt at all within a corresponding period in the army—as to which he had quite made up his mind; this step must involve a saving of four hundred pounds in a year, or two thousand pounds in five years, which was a considerable sum.", "그러고 나서 그는 수첩에 적은 정교한 계산을 들이대며 나에게 입증해 보였다. 가령 군대 밖에서 여섯 달 동안 이백 파운드쯤 빚을 졌다 치고, 군대 안에서는 같은 기간에 빚을 전혀 안 진다 하면—이 점은 그가 단단히 마음을 정한 바였다—이 한 걸음으로 한 해에 사백 파운드, 또는 오 년에 이천 파운드를 아끼게 되는 셈이고, 이는 상당한 금액이라는 것이었다."], ["And then he spoke so ingenuously and sincerely of the sacrifice he made in withdrawing himself for a time from Ada, and of the earnestness with which he aspired—as in thought he always did, I know full well—to repay her love, and to ensure her happiness, and to conquer what was amiss in himself, and to acquire the very soul of decision, that he made my heart ache keenly, sorely.", "그러고는 한동안 에이다 곁을 떠나며 치르는 희생에 대해, 또 에이다의 사랑에 보답하고 그녀의 행복을 지켜 주고 자기 안의 잘못된 점을 이겨 내고 결단의 정수 그 자체를 갖추기를—내가 잘 알듯이 그가 늘 마음속으로 갈망하던 대로—얼마나 간절히 바라는지에 대해 어찌나 천진하고 진실하게 말하던지, 내 가슴은 날카롭고 쓰라리게 저며 왔다. 나는 생각했기 때문이다."], ["For, I thought, how would this end, how could this end, when so soon and so surely all his manly qualities were touched by the fatal blight that ruined everything it rested on!", "이게 어떻게 끝날까, 어떻게 끝날 수 있을까. 이토록 빨리 또 이토록 틀림없이 그의 사내다운 자질이 죄다, 깃드는 것마다 망가뜨리는 그 운명의 마름병에 닿아 버리는데."]], [["I spoke to Richard with all the earnestness I felt, and all the hope I could not quite feel then, and implored him for Ada’s sake not to put any trust in Chancery.", "나는 내가 느끼는 온갖 간절함을 담아, 그리고 그때는 도무지 품을 수 없던 온갖 희망을 담아 리처드에게 말했고, 에이다를 위해서라도 대법원에 아무 믿음도 걸지 말라고 애원했다."], ["To all I said, Richard readily assented, riding over the court and everything else in his easy way and drawing the brightest pictures of the character he was to settle into—alas, when the grievous suit should loose its hold upon him!", "내가 한 모든 말에 리처드는 선뜻 동의하면서도, 그 특유의 태평한 투로 법정이며 무엇이며 죄다 가뿐히 넘겨 버리고는, 자기가 장차 정착하게 될 인물상을 더없이 밝게 그려 보였다—아, 그 비통한 소송이 그를 옭아맨 손아귀를 풀어 줄 때 말이다!"], ["We had a long talk, but it always came back to that, in substance.", "우리는 오래 이야기를 나누었지만, 결국 알맹이는 늘 그 이야기로 되돌아왔다."]], [["At last we came to Soho Square, where Caddy Jellyby had appointed to wait for me, as a quiet place in the neighbourhood of Newman Street.", "마침내 우리는 소호 광장에 이르렀다. 캐디 젤리비가 뉴먼 거리 근처의 조용한 곳이라며 나를 기다리겠다고 정해 둔 데였다."], ["Caddy was in the garden in the centre and hurried out as soon as I appeared.", "캐디는 한가운데 정원에 있다가, 내가 모습을 보이자마자 서둘러 나왔다."], ["After a few cheerful words, Richard left us together.", "명랑한 인사를 몇 마디 나눈 뒤, 리처드는 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 자리를 떴다."]], [["“Prince has a pupil over the way, Esther,”", "\"프린스가 길 건너에서 제자를 가르치고 있어, 에스더.\""], ["said Caddy, “and got the key for us.", "캐디가 말했다. \"우리한테 열쇠를 구해 줬어."], ["So if you will walk round and round here with me, we can lock ourselves in and I can tell you comfortably what I wanted to see your dear good face about.”", "그러니까 나랑 여기를 빙빙 거닐면, 안에 우리끼리만 잠그고 들어앉아서 네 다정하고 착한 얼굴을 보고 싶었던 그 이야기를 마음 편히 들려줄 수 있어.\""]], [["“Very well, my dear,”", "\"좋아, 캐디.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Nothing could be better.”", "\"그보다 좋을 순 없지.\""], ["So Caddy, after affectionately squeezing the dear good face as she called it, locked the gate, and took my arm, and we began to walk round the garden very cosily.", "그러자 캐디는 그 다정하고 착한 얼굴이라고 부르던 내 얼굴을 애정 어린 손길로 한번 매만지고는 문을 잠그고 내 팔짱을 끼었고, 우리는 아늑하게 정원을 빙빙 돌기 시작했다."]], [["“You see, Esther,”", "\"있잖아, 에스더.\""], ["said Caddy, who thoroughly enjoyed a little confidence, “after you spoke to me about its being wrong to marry without Ma’s knowledge, or even to keep Ma long in the dark respecting our engagement—though I don’t believe Ma cares much for me, I must say—I thought it right to mention your opinions to Prince.", "비밀 이야기를 한껏 즐기던 캐디가 말했다. \"네가 엄마 모르게 결혼하는 건 잘못이고, 우리 약혼을 엄마한테 오래 숨겨 두는 것도 잘못이라고 말해 준 뒤로—솔직히 엄마가 날 그리 마음에 두는 것 같진 않지만—나는 네 의견을 프린스한테 전해 주는 게 옳겠다 싶었어."], ["In the first place because I want to profit by everything you tell me, and in the second place because I have no secrets from Prince.”", "첫째로는 네가 해 주는 말은 뭐든 새겨 듣고 싶어서고, 둘째로는 프린스한테는 숨기는 게 하나도 없어서야.\""]], [["“I hope he approved, Caddy?”", "\"그가 찬성하던, 캐디?\""]], [["“Oh, my dear!", "\"오, 얘!"], ["I assure you he would approve of anything you could say.", "장담하는데 네가 한 말이라면 뭐든 찬성할걸."], ["You have no idea what an opinion he has of you!”", "그 사람이 너를 얼마나 떠받드는지 넌 모를 거야!\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"정말!\""]], [["“Esther, it’s enough to make anybody but me jealous,”", "\"에스더, 나 말고 다른 사람 같으면 누구라도 질투할 만하다니까.\" 캐디가 웃으며 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다."], ["said Caddy, laughing and shaking her head; “but it only makes me joyful, for you are the first friend I ever had, and the best friend I ever can have, and nobody can respect and love you too much to please me.”", "\"그런데 난 그저 기쁘기만 해. 너는 내가 처음으로 사귄 친구이자 앞으로도 사귈 수 있는 가장 좋은 친구니까, 누가 너를 아무리 떠받들고 사랑한대도 난 좋기만 해.\""]], [["“Upon my word, Caddy,”", "\"정말이지, 캐디.\""], ["said I, “you are in the general conspiracy to keep me in a good humour.", "내가 말했다. \"너도 나를 기분 좋게 해 주려는 그 합동 음모에 끼어 있구나."], ["Well, my dear?”", "그래서, 얘?\""]], [["“Well!", "\"그래서!"], ["I am going to tell you,”", "이제 말해 줄게.\""], ["replied Caddy, crossing her hands confidentially upon my arm.", "캐디가 다정하게 두 손을 내 팔에 포개며 대답했다."], ["“So we talked a good deal about it, and so I said to Prince, ‘Prince, as Miss Summerson—’”", "\"그래서 우리는 그 일을 두고 한참 이야기했고, 그래서 내가 프린스한테 말했어. '프린스, 서머슨 양이—'\""]], [["“I hope you didn’t say ‘Miss Summerson’?”", "\"'서머슨 양'이라고 하진 않았겠지?\""]], [["“No.", "\"응."], ["I didn’t!”", "안 그랬어!\""], ["cried Caddy, greatly pleased and with the brightest of faces.", "캐디가 더없이 환한 얼굴로 무척 기뻐하며 외쳤다. \"'에스더'라고 했어."], ["“I said, ‘Esther.’ I said to Prince, ‘As Esther is decidedly of that opinion, Prince, and has expressed it to me, and always hints it when she writes those kind notes, which you are so fond of hearing me read to you, I am prepared to disclose the truth to Ma whenever you think proper.", "프린스한테 그랬지. '에스더가 분명히 그렇게 생각하고, 그걸 나한테 말해 줬고, 당신이 그렇게 들려 달라고 좋아하는 그 다정한 편지를 쓸 때마다 늘 그 얘기를 넌지시 비추니까, 프린스, 당신이 적당하다고 여길 때면 언제든 엄마한테 사실을 털어놓을 마음의 준비가 돼 있어."], ["And I think, Prince,’ said I, ‘that Esther thinks that I should be in a better, and truer, and more honourable position altogether if you did the same to your papa.’”", "그리고 프린스,' 내가 말했어, '당신도 당신 아버님께 똑같이 한다면, 에스더 생각엔 내 처지가 모든 면에서 더 떳떳하고, 더 진실하고, 더 명예로워질 거래.'\""]], [["“Yes, my dear,”", "\"그래, 캐디.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Esther certainly does think so.”", "\"에스더는 분명히 그렇게 생각해.\""]], [["“So I was right, you see!”", "\"그러니까 내가 옳았던 거지!\""], ["exclaimed Caddy.", "캐디가 외쳤다."], ["“Well!", "\"그런데!"], ["This troubled Prince a good deal, not because he had the least doubt about it, but because he is so considerate of the feelings of old Mr.", "이게 프린스를 꽤나 곤란하게 했어. 그가 거기에 조금이라도 의심을 품어서가 아니라, 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 심정을 워낙 헤아리는 사람이라서야."], ["Turveydrop; and he had his apprehensions that old Mr. Turveydrop might break his heart, or faint away, or be very much overcome in some affecting manner or other if he made such an announcement.", "그런 말을 꺼내면 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 상심해서 가슴이 미어지거나, 정신을 잃거나, 이런저런 가슴 아픈 모양으로 크게 무너져 내리지 않을까 염려한 거야."], ["He feared old Mr. Turveydrop might consider it undutiful and might receive too great a shock.", "늙은 터비드롭 씨가 그걸 불효라고 여기고 너무 큰 충격을 받지 않을까 걱정한 거지."], ["For old Mr. Turveydrop’s deportment is very beautiful, you know, Esther,”", "알다시피, 에스더, 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 몸가짐은 아주 우아하잖아.\""], ["said Caddy, “and his feelings are extremely sensitive.”", "캐디가 말했다. \"그리고 감정이 무척 여리고.\""]], [["“Are they, my dear?”", "\"그래, 얘?\""]], [["“Oh, extremely sensitive.", "\"오, 무척 여려."], ["Prince says so.", "프린스가 그렇게 말해."], ["Now, this has caused my darling child—I didn’t mean to use the expression to you, Esther,”", "그래서 이게 내 소중한 아가를—너한테 그런 표현을 쓰려던 건 아니었는데, 에스더.\""], ["Caddy apologized, her face suffused with blushes, “but I generally call Prince my darling child.”", "캐디가 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올라 사과했다. \"그런데 난 프린스를 으레 내 소중한 아가라고 불러.\""]], [["I laughed; and Caddy laughed and blushed, and went on.", "내가 웃자 캐디도 웃으며 얼굴을 붉혔고, 말을 이었다."]], [["“This has caused him, Esther—”", "\"이게 그 사람을, 에스더—\""]], [["“Caused whom, my dear?”", "\"누구를, 얘?\""]], [["“Oh, you tiresome thing!”", "\"오, 이 짓궂은 것아!\""], ["said Caddy, laughing, with her pretty face on fire.", "캐디가 고운 얼굴이 불타오르듯 발개져서 웃으며 말했다."], ["“My darling child, if you insist upon it!", "\"정 그렇게 우긴다면, 내 소중한 아가를!"], ["This has caused him weeks of uneasiness and has made him delay, from day to day, in a very anxious manner.", "이게 그 사람을 몇 주째 마음 졸이게 했고, 날이면 날마다 무척 애를 태우며 미루게 했어. 결국 그가 나한테 말하더라."], ["At last he said to me, ‘Caddy, if Miss Summerson, who is a great favourite with my father, could be prevailed upon to be present when I broke the subject, I think I could do it.’ So I promised I would ask you.", "'캐디, 우리 아버지가 무척 아끼시는 서머슨 양이 내가 그 이야기를 꺼낼 때 함께 있어 주신다면, 내가 해낼 수 있을 것 같아.' 그래서 내가 너한테 부탁해 보겠다고 약속했어."], ["And I made up my mind, besides,”", "그리고 게다가 마음먹은 게 있는데,\""], ["said Caddy, looking at me hopefully but timidly, “that if you consented, I would ask you afterwards to come with me to Ma.", "캐디가 희망에 차 있으면서도 머뭇머뭇 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"네가 응해 주면, 그다음엔 나랑 같이 엄마한테 가 달라고 부탁하려고 했어."], ["This is what I meant when I said in my note that I had a great favour and a great assistance to beg of you.", "편지에 너한테 큰 부탁이자 큰 도움을 청할 일이 있다고 한 게 바로 이거야."], ["And if you thought you could grant it, Esther, we should both be very grateful.”", "그러니 에스더, 네가 들어줄 수 있겠다 싶으면, 우리 둘 다 정말 고마워할 거야.\""]], [["“Let me see, Caddy,”", "\"어디 보자, 캐디.\""], ["said I, pretending to consider.", "내가 곰곰 생각하는 척하며 말했다."], ["“Really, I think I could do a greater thing than that if the need were pressing.", "\"정말이지, 사정이 급하다면 그보다 더한 일도 해 줄 수 있을 것 같은데."], ["I am at your service and the darling child’s, my dear, whenever you like.”", "나는 너와 그 소중한 아가를 위해 언제든 대기하고 있을게, 얘, 네가 원할 때면.\""]], [["Caddy was quite transported by this reply of mine, being, I believe, as susceptible to the least kindness or encouragement as any tender heart that ever beat in this world; and after another turn or two round the garden, during which she put on an entirely new pair of gloves and made herself as resplendent as possible that she might do no avoidable discredit to the Master of Deportment, we went to Newman Street direct.", "캐디는 내 이 대답에 그만 황홀해했다. 이 세상에서 뛰어 본 그 어떤 여린 가슴에도 뒤지지 않을 만큼, 아주 작은 친절이나 격려에도 마음이 움직이는 아이였으니까. 그러고서 정원을 한두 바퀴 더 도는 동안, 그녀는 완전히 새 장갑을 끼고 한껏 눈부시게 꾸몄다. 몸가짐의 대가에게 어쩔 수 없는 망신만은 끼치지 않으려는 마음에서였다. 그런 뒤 우리는 곧장 뉴먼 거리로 갔다."]], [["Prince was teaching, of course.", "프린스는 물론 가르치는 중이었다."], ["We found him engaged with a not very hopeful pupil—a stubborn little girl with a sulky forehead, a deep voice, and an inanimate, dissatisfied mama—whose case was certainly not rendered more hopeful by the confusion into which we threw her preceptor.", "우리가 가 보니 그는 그리 가망 없어 보이는 제자—이마를 잔뜩 찌푸린 고집스러운 어린 여자아이로, 목소리가 굵고, 활기 없이 불만스러운 엄마를 둔 아이였다—를 데리고 씨름하고 있었는데, 우리가 끼어들어 선생을 당황하게 만든 통에 그 아이의 가망이 더 밝아졌을 리는 없었다."], ["The lesson at last came to an end, after proceeding as discordantly as possible; and when the little girl had changed her shoes and had had her white muslin extinguished in shawls, she was taken away.", "수업은 더없이 삐걱대며 이어지다가 마침내 끝났다. 어린 여자아이가 신발을 갈아 신고 흰 모슬린 옷을 숄로 덮어 가린 뒤, 아이는 끌려 나갔다."], ["After a few words of preparation, we then went in search of Mr.", "우리는 준비할 말을 몇 마디 나눈 다음, 터비드롭 씨를 찾아 나섰다."], ["Turveydrop, whom we found, grouped with his hat and gloves, as a model of deportment, on the sofa in his private apartment—the only comfortable room in the house.", "그는 집 안에서 유일하게 안락한 방인 자기 거처의 소파에 모자와 장갑을 곁에 갖춘 채, 몸가짐의 본보기처럼 자리하고 있었다."], ["He appeared to have dressed at his leisure in the intervals of a light collation, and his dressing-case, brushes, and so forth, all of quite an elegant kind, lay about.", "가벼운 식사 짬짬이 느긋하게 차림을 갖춘 듯했고, 죄다 제법 우아한 화장 도구며 솔 따위가 여기저기 놓여 있었다."]], [["“Father, Miss Summerson; Miss Jellyby.”", "\"아버지, 서머슨 양입니다. 젤리비 양이고요.\""]], [["“Charmed!", "\"황홀하옵니다!"], ["Enchanted!” said Mr.", "더없이 기쁩니다!\""], ["Turveydrop, rising with his high-shouldered bow.", "터비드롭 씨가 어깨를 한껏 치켜올린 절을 하며 일어섰다."], ["“Permit me!”", "\"잠깐만요!\""], ["Handing chairs.", "의자를 내밀며."], ["“Be seated!”", "\"앉으시지요!\""], ["Kissing the tips of his left fingers.", "왼손 손가락 끝에 입을 맞추며."], ["“Overjoyed!”", "\"더없이 기쁩니다!\""], ["Shutting his eyes and rolling.", "눈을 감고 몸을 굴리듯 흔들며."], ["“My little retreat is made a paradise.”", "\"저의 자그마한 안식처가 낙원이 되었군요.\""], ["Recomposing himself on the sofa like the second gentleman in Europe.", "유럽에서 둘째가는 신사라도 되는 양 소파에 다시 자세를 가다듬으며."]], [["“Again you find us, Miss Summerson,”", "\"또 이렇게 저희를 보시는군요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said he, “using our little arts to polish, polish!", "그가 말했다. \"갈고닦고 또 갈고닦는 저희의 작은 기예를 펼치는 중에 말입니다!"], ["Again the sex stimulates us and rewards us by the condescension of its lovely presence.", "또 한 번 여성이 저희를 북돋우고, 그 사랑스러운 자태로 황송히 곁을 내어 주어 저희에게 보답을 주는군요."], ["It is much in these times (and we have made an awfully degenerating business of it since the days of his Royal Highness the Prince Regent—my patron, if I may presume to say so) to experience that deportment is not wholly trodden under foot by mechanics.", "이런 시절에(섭정 왕세자 전하—외람되오나 저의 후원자라 일컬어도 좋다면—의 시대 이래로 저희는 이 일을 참담하게 타락시키고 말았습니다만), 몸가짐이 기능공들의 발밑에 온통 짓밟히지만은 않았음을 실감하는 것은 큰 일입니다."], ["That it can yet bask in the smile of beauty, my dear madam.”", "그것이 아직도 아름다움의 미소 속에서 볕을 쬘 수 있다는 것이, 부인.\""]], [["I said nothing, which I thought a suitable reply; and he took a pinch of snuff.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않았는데, 그게 알맞은 대답이라고 여겼다. 그러자 그는 코담배를 한 줌 집었다."]], [["“My dear son,” said Mr.", "\"내 아들아,\""], ["Turveydrop, “you have four schools this afternoon.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"너는 오늘 오후에 학교가 네 군데 있구나."], ["I would recommend a hasty sandwich.”", "후딱 샌드위치라도 먹어 두기를 권한다.\""]], [["“Thank you, father,”", "\"고맙습니다, 아버지.\""], ["returned Prince, “I will be sure to be punctual.", "프린스가 대답했다. \"꼭 시간을 어기지 않겠습니다."], ["My dear father, may I beg you to prepare your mind for what I am going to say?”", "아버지, 제가 드릴 말씀에 마음을 단단히 다잡아 주시기를 부탁드려도 될까요?\""]], [["“Good heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed the model, pale and aghast as Prince and Caddy, hand in hand, bent down before him.", "본보기는 프린스와 캐디가 손을 맞잡고 자기 앞에 몸을 굽히자 새파랗게 질려 아연실색하며 외쳤다."], ["“What is this?", "\"이게 무슨 일이냐?"], ["Is this lunacy!", "미친 짓이냐!"], ["Or what is this?”", "아니면 이게 대체 뭐냐?\""]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["returned Prince with great submission, “I love this young lady, and we are engaged.”", "프린스가 더없이 공손하게 대답했다. \"저는 이 아가씨를 사랑하고, 우리는 약혼했습니다.\""]], [["“Engaged!” cried Mr.", "\"약혼이라니!\""], ["Turveydrop, reclining on the sofa and shutting out the sight with his hand.", "터비드롭 씨가 소파에 몸을 기대며 손으로 그 광경을 가려 막고 외쳤다."], ["“An arrow launched at my brain by my own child!”", "\"제 자식이 내 머리에 쏘아 보낸 화살이로구나!\""]], [["“We have been engaged for some time, father,”", "\"우리는 얼마 전부터 약혼한 사이입니다, 아버지.\""], ["faltered Prince, “and Miss Summerson, hearing of it, advised that we should declare the fact to you and was so very kind as to attend on the present occasion.", "프린스가 더듬더듬 말했다. \"그리고 그 사실을 듣고 서머슨 양께서 아버지께 이를 알려 드리라고 조언해 주셨고, 더없이 친절하게도 오늘 이 자리에 함께해 주셨습니다."], ["Miss Jellyby is a young lady who deeply respects you, father.”", "젤리비 양은 아버지를 깊이 존경하는 아가씨입니다, 아버지.\""]], [["Mr. Turveydrop uttered a groan.", "터비드롭 씨가 신음을 토했다."]], [["“No, pray don’t!", "\"안 됩니다, 제발 그러지 마세요!"], ["Pray don’t, father,”", "제발 그러지 마세요, 아버지.\""], ["urged his son.", "아들이 간곡히 청했다."], ["“Miss Jellyby is a young lady who deeply respects you, and our first desire is to consider your comfort.”", "\"젤리비 양은 아버지를 깊이 존경하는 아가씨이고, 우리의 첫째가는 바람은 아버지의 안락을 헤아리는 것입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Turveydrop sobbed.", "터비드롭 씨가 흐느꼈다."]], [["“No, pray don’t, father!”", "\"안 됩니다, 제발 그러지 마세요, 아버지!\""], ["cried his son.", "아들이 외쳤다."]], [["“Boy,” said Mr.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Turveydrop, “it is well that your sainted mother is spared this pang.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"성스러운 네 어미가 이 아픔을 겪지 않게 된 것이 다행이로구나."], ["Strike deep, and spare not.", "깊이 찔러라, 사정 두지 말고."], ["Strike home, sir, strike home!”", "정통으로 찔러라, 얘야, 정통으로 찔러!\""]], [["“Pray don’t say so, father,”", "\"제발 그런 말씀 마세요, 아버지.\""], ["implored Prince, in tears.", "프린스가 눈물을 흘리며 애원했다."], ["“It goes to my heart.", "\"가슴이 미어집니다."], ["I do assure you, father, that our first wish and intention is to consider your comfort.", "정말이지, 아버지, 우리의 첫째가는 소망이자 작정은 아버지의 안락을 헤아리는 것입니다."], ["Caroline and I do not forget our duty—what is my duty is Caroline’s, as we have often said together—and with your approval and consent, father, we will devote ourselves to making your life agreeable.”", "캐롤라인과 저는 우리의 도리를 잊지 않습니다—우리가 늘 함께 이야기했듯이, 제 도리가 곧 캐롤라인의 도리입니다—그러니 아버지께서 승낙하고 허락해 주신다면, 아버지, 우리는 아버지의 삶을 즐겁게 해 드리는 데 온 힘을 바치겠습니다.\""]], [["“Strike home,”", "\"정통으로 찔러라.\""], ["murmured Mr. Turveydrop.", "터비드롭 씨가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Strike home!”", "\"정통으로 찔러!\""], ["But he seemed to listen, I thought, too.", "그러면서도 내가 보기엔, 귀를 기울이고 있기도 했다."]], [["“My dear father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["returned Prince, “we well know what little comforts you are accustomed to and have a right to, and it will always be our study and our pride to provide those before anything.", "프린스가 대답했다. \"우리는 아버지께서 익숙하시고 또 누리실 자격이 있는 자잘한 안락이 무엇인지 잘 알고, 무엇보다 먼저 그것을 마련해 드리는 것이 언제나 우리의 마음 씀이자 자랑이 될 것입니다."], ["If you will bless us with your approval and consent, father, we shall not think of being married until it is quite agreeable to you; and when we ARE married, we shall always make you—of course—our first consideration.", "아버지, 아버지께서 승낙과 허락으로 우리를 축복해 주신다면, 우리는 아버지께서 흡족하실 때가 되기 전에는 결혼은 생각조차 하지 않겠습니다. 그리고 결혼한 뒤에도 우리는 늘 아버지를—당연히—맨 먼저 생각하겠습니다."], ["You must ever be the head and master here, father; and we feel how truly unnatural it would be in us if we failed to know it or if we failed to exert ourselves in every possible way to please you.”", "아버지, 아버지께서는 언제까지나 이곳의 가장이자 주인이셔야 합니다. 만약 우리가 그것을 모르거나, 아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 갖은 애를 다 쏟지 못한다면, 그것이 얼마나 천륜에 어긋나는 일일지 우리는 절감하고 있습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Turveydrop underwent a severe internal struggle and came upright on the sofa again with his cheeks puffing over his stiff cravat, a perfect model of parental deportment.", "터비드롭 씨는 심한 내적 갈등을 치른 끝에 다시 소파에 꼿꼿이 몸을 세웠고, 두 뺨을 빳빳한 크라바트 위로 불룩 부풀린 채, 더없이 완벽한 어버이 몸가짐의 본보기가 되었다."]], [["“My son!”", "\"내 아들아!\""], ["said Mr. Turveydrop.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했다."], ["“My children!", "\"내 아이들아!"], ["I cannot resist your prayer.", "너희의 간청을 뿌리칠 수가 없구나."], ["Be happy!”", "행복하거라!\""]], [["His benignity as he raised his future daughter-in-law and stretched out his hand to his son (who kissed it with affectionate respect and gratitude) was the most confusing sight I ever saw.", "장차 며느리가 될 아이를 일으켜 세우고 아들에게 손을 내미는(아들은 그 손에 애정 어린 존경과 감사를 담아 입을 맞추었다) 그의 너그러운 모습은, 내가 본 가장 어리둥절한 광경이었다."]], [["“My children,”", "\"내 아이들아,\""], ["said Mr. Turveydrop, paternally encircling Caddy with his left arm as she sat beside him, and putting his right hand gracefully on his hip.", "터비드롭 씨가 자기 곁에 앉은 캐디를 왼팔로 어버이답게 감싸 안고 오른손을 우아하게 허리에 얹으며 말했다."], ["“My son and daughter, your happiness shall be my care.", "\"내 아들과 딸아, 너희의 행복을 내가 보살피마."], ["I will watch over you.", "내가 너희를 지켜보마."], ["You shall always live with me”—meaning, of course, I will always live with you—“this house is henceforth as much yours as mine; consider it your home.", "너희는 언제까지나 나와 함께 살아라\"—물론 그 뜻은, 내가 언제까지나 너희와 함께 살겠다는 것이었다—\"이 집은 이제부터 내 것인 만큼이나 너희 것이기도 하니, 너희 집으로 여기거라."], ["May you long live to share it with me!”", "오래도록 살아 나와 함께 이 집을 누리기를!\""]], [["The power of his deportment was such that they really were as much overcome with thankfulness as if, instead of quartering himself upon them for the rest of his life, he were making some munificent sacrifice in their favour.", "그의 몸가짐의 위력이 어찌나 대단하던지, 그가 남은 평생 그들에게 얹혀살려는 것이 아니라 마치 그들을 위해 무슨 대단한 희생이라도 베푸는 양, 두 사람은 정말로 감사함에 북받쳐 있었다."]], [["“For myself, my children,” said Mr.", "\"나로 말하자면, 내 아이들아,\""], ["Turveydrop, “I am falling into the sear and yellow leaf, and it is impossible to say how long the last feeble traces of gentlemanly deportment may linger in this weaving and spinning age.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"나는 시들어 누렇게 마른 잎으로 접어들고 있고, 신사다운 몸가짐의 마지막 희미한 자취가 이 베 짜고 실 잣는 시대에 얼마나 더 머물러 줄지는 가늠할 길이 없구나."], ["But, so long, I will do my duty to society and will show myself, as usual, about town.", "하지만 그동안만은 나는 사회에 대한 내 도리를 다할 것이고, 늘 그래 왔듯 시내를 거닐며 내 모습을 보일 것이다."], ["My wants are few and simple.", "내가 바라는 바는 적고 소박하다."], ["My little apartment here, my few essentials for the toilet, my frugal morning meal, and my little dinner will suffice.", "여기 이 자그마한 거처, 단장에 쓸 몇 가지 필수품, 검소한 아침 끼니, 그리고 자그마한 저녁 식사면 족하다."], ["I charge your dutiful affection with the supply of these requirements, and I charge myself with all the rest.”", "이 자잘한 필요를 채우는 일은 너희의 효심에 맡기고, 나머지는 모두 내가 떠맡으마.\""]], [["They were overpowered afresh by his uncommon generosity.", "두 사람은 그의 남다른 너그러움에 다시금 압도되었다."]], [["“My son,” said Mr.", "\"내 아들아,\""], ["Turveydrop, “for those little points in which you are deficient—points of deportment, which are born with a man, which may be improved by cultivation, but can never be originated—you may still rely on me.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했다. \"네게 모자란 그 자잘한 점들—타고나는 것이요, 갈고닦아 나아질 수는 있어도 결코 없던 데서 만들어 낼 수는 없는 몸가짐의 점들로 말하자면—그것은 여전히 나를 믿어도 된다."], ["I have been faithful to my post since the days of his Royal Highness the Prince Regent, and I will not desert it now.", "나는 섭정 왕세자 전하의 시대 이래로 내 자리를 충실히 지켜 왔고, 이제 와서 그것을 저버리지 않으마. 그래, 내 아들아."], ["No, my son. If you have ever contemplated your father’s poor position with a feeling of pride, you may rest assured that he will do nothing to tarnish it.", "네가 일찍이 네 아비의 가난한 처지를 자랑스러운 마음으로 헤아려 본 적이 있다면, 그가 그것을 더럽힐 일은 결코 하지 않으리라 믿어도 좋다."], ["For yourself, Prince, whose character is different (we cannot be all alike, nor is it advisable that we should), work, be industrious, earn money, and extend the connexion as much as possible.”", "너로 말하자면, 프린스, 너는 성품이 다르니(우리가 다 같을 수는 없고, 그래야 마땅한 것도 아니지), 일하고, 부지런히 힘쓰고, 돈을 벌고, 인맥을 되도록 넓혀라.\""]], [["“That you may depend I will do, dear father, with all my heart,”", "\"틀림없이 그렇게 하겠습니다, 아버지, 온 마음을 다해서요.\""], ["replied Prince.", "프린스가 대답했다."]], [["“I have no doubt of it,”", "\"의심치 않는다.\" 터비드롭 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Turveydrop.", "\"네 자질은 빛나지는 않아도, 내 사랑하는 아이야, 한결같고 쓸모가 있지."], ["“Your qualities are not shining, my dear child, but they are steady and useful.", "그리고 내 아이들아, 너희 둘에게는 그저 한마디만 일러 두마. 내가 그 길에 한 줄기 빛을 비추는 행복을 누렸다고 믿는, 어느 성스러운 여인의 마음을 담아서 말이다."], ["And to both of you, my children, I would merely observe, in the spirit of a sainted wooman on whose path I had the happiness of casting, I believe, SOME ray of light, take care of the establishment, take care of my simple wants, and bless you both!”", "이 살림을 잘 건사하고, 나의 소박한 필요를 잘 챙겨 다오. 그리고 너희 둘에게 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["Old Mr. Turveydrop then became so very gallant, in honour of the occasion, that I told Caddy we must really go to Thavies Inn at once if we were to go at all that day.", "이윽고 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 이 경사를 기리느라 어찌나 한껏 정중하게 굴던지, 나는 캐디에게 오늘 안에 떠브스 법학원에 갈 작정이라면 정말이지 당장 떠나야 한다고 말했다."], ["So we took our departure after a very loving farewell between Caddy and her betrothed, and during our walk she was so happy and so full of old Mr.", "그래서 우리는 캐디와 그녀의 약혼자가 더없이 다정하게 작별 인사를 나눈 뒤 길을 나섰다."], ["Turveydrop’s praises that I would not have said a word in his disparagement for any consideration.", "걸어가는 내내 그녀는 어찌나 행복해하며 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 입이 닳도록 칭찬하던지, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 그를 깎아내리는 말은 한마디도 하지 않으리라 마음먹었다."]], [["The house in Thavies Inn had bills in the windows announcing that it was to let, and it looked dirtier and gloomier and ghastlier than ever.", "떠브스 법학원의 그 집은 창문마다 세놓는다는 광고가 붙어 있었고, 그 어느 때보다 더 더럽고 더 음울하고 더 으스스해 보였다."], ["The name of poor Mr. Jellyby had appeared in the list of bankrupts but a day or two before, and he was shut up in the dining-room with two gentlemen and a heap of blue bags, account-books, and papers, making the most desperate endeavours to understand his affairs.", "가엾은 젤리비 씨의 이름이 바로 하루 이틀 전에 파산자 명단에 올랐고, 그는 두 신사와 푸른 서류 가방 더미와 장부와 서류에 둘러싸여 식당에 틀어박혀, 자기 사정을 이해해 보려고 갖은 필사적인 애를 다 쓰고 있었다."], ["They appeared to me to be quite beyond his comprehension, for when Caddy took me into the dining-room by mistake and we came upon Mr.", "내가 보기에 그것은 그의 이해를 한참 벗어나 있는 듯했다."], ["Jellyby in his spectacles, forlornly fenced into a corner by the great dining-table and the two gentlemen, he seemed to have given up the whole thing and to be speechless and insensible.", "캐디가 나를 식당으로 잘못 데리고 들어가는 바람에 우리가 안경을 쓴 젤리비 씨와 맞닥뜨렸을 때, 그는 커다란 식탁과 두 신사에게 구석으로 처량하게 둘러막힌 채, 모든 걸 단념하고 말도 못 하고 넋이 나간 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["Going upstairs to Mrs. Jellyby’s room (the children were all screaming in the kitchen, and there was no servant to be seen), we found that lady in the midst of a voluminous correspondence, opening, reading, and sorting letters, with a great accumulation of torn covers on the floor.", "젤리비 부인의 방으로 올라가 보니(아이들은 죄다 부엌에서 악을 쓰며 울어 댔고, 하인은 그림자도 보이지 않았다), 그 부인은 잔뜩 쌓인 편지더미 한복판에서 편지를 뜯고, 읽고, 분류하고 있었으며, 바닥에는 뜯어낸 봉투가 수북이 쌓여 있었다."], ["She was so preoccupied that at first she did not know me, though she sat looking at me with that curious, bright-eyed, far-off look of hers.", "그녀는 어찌나 정신이 팔려 있던지 처음엔 나를 알아보지도 못했다. 그 특유의 묘하고, 눈이 반짝이며, 아득히 먼 곳을 보는 눈길로 나를 바라보며 앉아 있으면서도 말이다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Miss Summerson!”", "서머슨 양!\""], ["she said at last.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I was thinking of something so different!", "\"내가 전혀 다른 걸 생각하고 있었네!"], ["I hope you are well.", "잘 지내지?"], ["I am happy to see you.", "만나서 반가워요."], ["Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Clare quite well?”", "잔다이스 씨와 클레어 양은 아주 잘 지내고?\""]], [["I hoped in return that Mr. Jellyby was quite well.", "나는 그 답례로 젤리비 씨도 아주 잘 지내시기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["“Why, not quite, my dear,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄, 그리 잘 지내진 못해, 얘.\""], ["Jellyby in the calmest manner.", "젤리비 부인이 더없이 태연하게 말했다."], ["“He has been unfortunate in his affairs and is a little out of spirits.", "\"사업이 잘 안 풀려서 기운이 좀 빠져 있어."], ["Happily for me, I am so much engaged that I have no time to think about it.", "나로서는 다행스럽게도 내가 워낙 바빠서 그걸 생각할 겨를이 없어."], ["We have, at the present moment, one hundred and seventy families, Miss Summerson, averaging five persons in each, either gone or going to the left bank of the Niger.”", "지금 이 순간에도, 서머슨 양, 우리한테는 한 가구당 평균 다섯 명꼴인 백일흔 가구가 니제르 강 왼편으로 떠났거나 떠나려 하고 있어.\""]], [["I thought of the one family so near us who were neither gone nor going to the left bank of the Niger, and wondered how she could be so placid.", "나는 니제르 강 왼편으로 떠나지도 떠나려 하지도 않은, 우리 바로 곁에 있는 그 한 가구를 떠올리며, 그녀가 어떻게 저토록 평온할 수 있는지 의아했다."]], [["“You have brought Caddy back, I see,”", "\"캐디를 데려왔구나.\""], ["observed Mrs. Jellyby with a glance at her daughter.", "젤리비 부인이 딸을 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["“It has become quite a novelty to see her here.", "\"이 애가 여기 보이는 게 영 새삼스럽네."], ["She has almost deserted her old employment and in fact obliges me to employ a boy.”", "옛 일거리는 거의 내팽개치다시피 해서, 사실 나는 사내아이를 하나 써야 할 지경이야.\""]], [["“I am sure, Ma—”", "\"정말이지, 엄마—\""], ["began Caddy.", "캐디가 입을 떼었다."]], [["“Now you know, Caddy,”", "\"이제 알겠지, 캐디.\""], ["her mother mildly interposed, “that I DO employ a boy, who is now at his dinner.", "어머니가 부드럽게 말을 가로막았다. \"내가 사내아이를 하나 쓰고 있고, 그 애는 지금 밥 먹으러 갔어."], ["What is the use of your contradicting?”", "네가 말대꾸해 봐야 무슨 소용이니?\""]], [["“I was not going to contradict, Ma,”", "\"말대꾸하려던 게 아니에요, 엄마.\""], ["returned Caddy.", "캐디가 대꾸했다."], ["“I was only going to say that surely you wouldn’t have me be a mere drudge all my life.”", "\"그저, 설마 저더러 평생 한낱 종처럼 부려 먹히라고는 안 하시겠죠 하고 말하려던 거예요.\""]], [["“I believe, my dear,” said Mrs.", "\"내 생각엔, 얘,\""], ["Jellyby, still opening her letters, casting her bright eyes smilingly over them, and sorting them as she spoke, “that you have a business example before you in your mother.", "여전히 편지를 뜯고, 반짝이는 눈으로 미소 지으며 훑어 내리고, 말하면서 분류하는 와중에 젤리비 부인이 말했다. \"네 눈앞에 바로 네 어미라는 사업의 본보기가 있잖니."], ["Besides. A mere drudge?", "게다가, 한낱 종이라니?"], ["If you had any sympathy with the destinies of the human race, it would raise you high above any such idea.", "네게 인류의 운명에 대한 공감이 조금이라도 있다면, 그런 생각 따위는 훌쩍 넘어설 텐데."], ["But you have none.", "하지만 넌 그게 없어."], ["I have often told you, Caddy, you have no such sympathy.”", "내가 누누이 말했잖니, 캐디, 넌 그런 공감이 없다고.\""]], [["“Not if it’s Africa, Ma, I have not.”", "\"그게 아프리카 일이라면, 엄마, 저는 없어요.\""]], [["“Of course you have not.", "\"당연히 없겠지."], ["Now, if I were not happily so much engaged, Miss Summerson,” said Mrs.", "자, 내가 다행히 이렇게 바쁘지 않았다면, 서머슨 양,\""], ["Jellyby, sweetly casting her eyes for a moment on me and considering where to put the particular letter she had just opened, “this would distress and disappoint me.", "젤리비 부인이 잠깐 나에게 다정하게 눈길을 던지고는 방금 뜯은 그 편지를 어디에 둘지 가늠하며 말했다. \"이런 일에 속이 상하고 실망했을 거야."], ["But I have so much to think of, in connexion with Borrioboola-Gha and it is so necessary I should concentrate myself that there is my remedy, you see.”", "하지만 보리오불라가 일과 관련해 생각할 게 워낙 많고, 거기에 정신을 집중해야 하니, 보다시피 그게 곧 내 처방인 셈이지.\""]], [["As Caddy gave me a glance of entreaty, and as Mrs. Jellyby was looking far away into Africa straight through my bonnet and head, I thought it a good opportunity to come to the subject of my visit and to attract Mrs. Jellyby’s attention.", "캐디가 나에게 애원하는 눈길을 던졌고, 젤리비 부인은 내 보닛과 머리를 그대로 뚫고 아프리카 저 멀리를 바라보고 있었으므로, 나는 지금이야말로 내가 찾아온 용건을 꺼내어 젤리비 부인의 주의를 끌기 좋은 기회라고 생각했다."]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"아마,\""], ["I began, “you will wonder what has brought me here to interrupt you.”", "내가 말을 꺼냈다. \"제가 여기 와서 일을 방해하니 무슨 일인가 궁금하시겠지요.\""]], [["“I am always delighted to see Miss Summerson,”", "\"나는 서머슨 양을 보면 언제나 반가워요.\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby, pursuing her employment with a placid smile.", "젤리비 부인이 평온한 미소를 띤 채 하던 일을 이어 가며 말했다."], ["“Though I wish,”", "\"다만 바라는 건,\""], ["and she shook her head, “she was more interested in the Borrioboolan project.”", "그러면서 그녀가 고개를 가로저었다. \"이 애가 보리오불라 사업에 좀 더 관심을 가져 주는 거지.\""]], [["“I have come with Caddy,”", "\"제가 캐디와 함께 온 건,\""], ["said I, “because Caddy justly thinks she ought not to have a secret from her mother and fancies I shall encourage and aid her (though I am sure I don’t know how) in imparting one.”", "내가 말했다. \"캐디가 어머니께 비밀을 두어서는 안 된다고 옳게 생각하면서도, 그걸 털어놓는 데 제가 힘을 보태고 거들어 주리라고(어떻게 할 수 있을지는 저도 정말 모르겠지만) 여겨서예요.\""]], [["“Caddy,” said Mrs.", "\"캐디,\""], ["Jellyby, pausing for a moment in her occupation and then serenely pursuing it after shaking her head, “you are going to tell me some nonsense.”", "젤리비 부인이 하던 일을 잠깐 멈췄다가 고개를 가로저은 뒤 태연히 다시 이어 가며 말했다. \"너 또 무슨 허튼소리를 하려는 게로구나.\""]], [["Caddy untied the strings of her bonnet, took her bonnet off, and letting it dangle on the floor by the strings, and crying heartily, said, “Ma, I am engaged.”", "캐디는 보닛 끈을 풀고 보닛을 벗어, 끈을 잡은 채 바닥에 대롱대롱 늘어뜨리고는, 서럽게 울며 말했다. \"엄마, 저 약혼했어요.\""]], [["“Oh, you ridiculous child!” observed Mrs.", "\"오, 이 우스꽝스러운 것아!\""], ["Jellyby with an abstracted air as she looked over the dispatch last opened; “what a goose you are!”", "젤리비 부인이 가장 나중에 뜯은 편지를 훑어보며 넋이 나간 듯이 말했다. \"넌 어쩜 이리 멍청하니!\""]], [["“I am engaged, Ma,”", "\"저 약혼했어요, 엄마.\""], ["sobbed Caddy, “to young Mr.", "캐디가 흐느꼈다."], ["Turveydrop, at the academy; and old Mr.", "\"학원의 젊은 터비드롭 씨와요."], ["Turveydrop (who is a very gentlemanly man indeed) has given his consent, and I beg and pray you’ll give us yours, Ma, because I never could be happy without it.", "그리고 늙은 터비드롭 씨가(정말이지 아주 신사다운 분이세요) 승낙해 주셨어요. 그러니 엄마, 엄마도 우리한테 승낙해 주시기를 빌고 또 빌어요."], ["I never, never could!”", "저는 그게 없으면 도무지 행복할 수 없을 테니까요."], ["sobbed Caddy, quite forgetful of her general complainings and of everything but her natural affection.", "절대로, 절대로 못 할 거예요!\" 캐디는 평소의 온갖 불평이며 다른 모든 것을 까맣게 잊고 오로지 타고난 정에만 사로잡혀 흐느꼈다."]], [["“You see again, Miss Summerson,” observed Mrs.", "\"또 보이지요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["Jellyby serenely, “what a happiness it is to be so much occupied as I am and to have this necessity for self-concentration that I have.", "젤리비 부인이 태연히 말했다. \"나처럼 이렇게 바쁘게 지내며, 또 이렇게 정신을 한데 모아야 할 필요를 지닌다는 게 얼마나 다행인지를요."], ["Here is Caddy engaged to a dancing-master’s son—mixed up with people who have no more sympathy with the destinies of the human race than she has herself!", "보세요, 캐디는 무용 선생의 아들과 약혼했어요—저 자신처럼 인류의 운명에 도통 공감이라곤 없는 사람들과 한데 얽혀서요!"], ["This, too, when Mr. Quale, one of the first philanthropists of our time, has mentioned to me that he was really disposed to be interested in her!”", "게다가 이건, 우리 시대 으뜸가는 박애주의자 가운데 한 분인 퀘일 씨가 이 애한테 정말로 관심을 두고 싶다고 나한테 말씀해 주셨을 때 벌어진 일이라니까요!\""]], [["“Ma, I always hated and detested Mr. Quale!”", "\"엄마, 저는 퀘일 씨를 늘 미워하고 진저리쳤어요!\""], ["sobbed Caddy.", "캐디가 흐느꼈다."]], [["“Caddy, Caddy!”", "\"캐디, 캐디!\""], ["returned Mrs. Jellyby, opening another letter with the greatest complacency.", "젤리비 부인이 더없이 흡족한 얼굴로 또 다른 편지를 뜯으며 대꾸했다."], ["“I have no doubt you did.", "\"그랬을 테지, 의심치 않아."], ["How could you do otherwise, being totally destitute of the sympathies with which he overflows!", "그분이 넘치도록 지닌 그 공감이 네겐 도무지 없으니, 어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠니!"], ["Now, if my public duties were not a favourite child to me, if I were not occupied with large measures on a vast scale, these petty details might grieve me very much, Miss Summerson.", "자, 만약 내 공적인 사명이 내게 애지중지하는 자식 같은 것이 아니었다면, 내가 어마어마한 규모의 큰 사업에 몰두하고 있지 않았다면, 이런 자잘한 일들이 나를 무척 슬프게 했을지도 몰라요, 서머슨 양."], ["But can I permit the film of a silly proceeding on the part of Caddy (from whom I expect nothing else) to interpose between me and the great African continent?", "하지만 캐디 따위가 벌인 어리석은 짓의 막 같은 것이(이 애한테는 그 이상 바랄 것도 없지만) 나와 저 위대한 아프리카 대륙 사이를 가로막게 둘 수 있을까요?"], ["No. No,”", "아니죠."], ["repeated Mrs.", "아니에요,\""], ["Jellyby in a calm clear voice, and with an agreeable smile, as she opened more letters and sorted them.", "젤리비 부인이 더 많은 편지를 뜯어 분류하며, 차분하고 또렷한 목소리로 흐뭇한 미소를 띤 채 되풀이했다."], ["“No, indeed.”", "\"정말 아니에요.\""]], [["I was so unprepared for the perfect coolness of this reception, though I might have expected it, that I did not know what to say.", "나는 충분히 예상할 수도 있었건만, 이 더없이 냉랭한 응대에 어찌나 마음의 준비가 안 되어 있던지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["Caddy seemed equally at a loss. Mrs.", "캐디도 마찬가지로 어쩔 줄 모르는 듯했다."], ["Jellyby continued to open and sort letters and to repeat occasionally in quite a charming tone of voice and with a smile of perfect composure, “No, indeed.”", "젤리비 부인은 계속해서 편지를 뜯고 분류하면서, 이따금 더없이 매력적인 목소리로, 더없이 평온한 미소를 띤 채 \"정말 아니에요\"를 되뇌었다."]], [["“I hope, Ma,”", "\"엄마, 바라건대,\""], ["sobbed poor Caddy at last, “you are not angry?”", "가엾은 캐디가 마침내 흐느끼며 말했다. \"화나신 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“Oh, Caddy, you really are an absurd girl,” returned Mrs.", "\"오, 캐디, 넌 정말 어처구니없는 애로구나.\""], ["Jellyby, “to ask such questions after what I have said of the preoccupation of my mind.”", "젤리비 부인이 대꾸했다. \"내 마음이 얼마나 딴 데 가 있는지 그토록 말했는데도 그런 걸 묻다니.\""]], [["“And I hope, Ma, you give us your consent and wish us well?”", "\"그리고 엄마, 바라건대, 우리한테 승낙해 주시고 잘되기를 빌어 주시는 거지요?\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했다."]], [["“You are a nonsensical child to have done anything of this kind,”", "\"넌 이런 짓을 저지르다니 분별없는 애야.\" 젤리비 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Jellyby; “and a degenerate child, when you might have devoted yourself to the great public measure.", "\"또 위대한 공적 사업에 헌신할 수도 있었을 텐데 그런 걸 보면 못난 애지."], ["But the step is taken, and I have engaged a boy, and there is no more to be said.", "하지만 일은 벌어졌고, 나는 사내아이를 하나 들였으니, 더 할 말도 없어."], ["Now, pray, Caddy,” said Mrs.", "자, 제발, 캐디,\""], ["Jellyby, for Caddy was kissing her, “don’t delay me in my work, but let me clear off this heavy batch of papers before the afternoon post comes in!”", "캐디가 자기에게 입을 맞추고 있었으므로 젤리비 부인이 말했다. \"오후 우편물이 오기 전에 이 묵직한 서류 뭉치를 마저 처리해야 하니, 내 일을 더 붙들고 늘어지지 마라!\""]], [["I thought I could not do better than take my leave; I was detained for a moment by Caddy’s saying, “You won’t object to my bringing him to see you, Ma?”", "나는 이만 작별하는 것이 상책이겠다 싶었다. 캐디가 이렇게 말하는 통에 잠깐 발이 묶이긴 했지만. \"엄마, 제가 그분을 데려와서 인사드리게 해도 괜찮죠?\""]], [["“Oh, dear me, Caddy,” cried Mrs.", "\"오, 저런, 캐디.\""], ["Jellyby, who had relapsed into that distant contemplation, “have you begun again?", "어느새 다시 그 아득한 사색에 잠겨 있던 젤리비 부인이 외쳤다. \"또 시작이니?"], ["Bring whom?”", "누굴 데려온다고?\""]], [["“Him, Ma.”", "\"그분요, 엄마.\""]], [["“Caddy, Caddy!”", "\"캐디, 캐디!\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby, quite weary of such little matters.", "젤리비 부인이 그런 자잘한 일에 진력이 난 듯 말했다."], ["“Then you must bring him some evening which is not a Parent Society night, or a Branch night, or a Ramification night.", "\"그럼 본부 모임이 있는 밤도, 지부 모임이 있는 밤도, 분회 모임이 있는 밤도 아닌 어느 저녁에 데려오렴."], ["You must accommodate the visit to the demands upon my time.", "내 시간이 요구하는 바에 그 방문을 맞춰야 해."], ["My dear Miss Summerson, it was very kind of you to come here to help out this silly chit.", "서머슨 양, 이렇게 와서 이 어리석은 계집애를 도와주다니 정말 친절했어요."], ["Good-bye!", "잘 가요!"], ["When I tell you that I have fifty-eight new letters from manufacturing families anxious to understand the details of the native and coffee-cultivation question this morning, I need not apologize for having very little leisure.”", "오늘 아침에 원주민과 커피 재배 문제의 세부 사항을 알고 싶어 하는 제조업 가문들한테서 새로 쉰여덟 통이나 편지가 왔다고 하면, 내가 여유가 거의 없는 것도 굳이 변명할 필요가 없겠지요.\""]], [["I was not surprised by Caddy’s being in low spirits when we went downstairs, or by her sobbing afresh on my neck, or by her saying she would far rather have been scolded than treated with such indifference, or by her confiding to me that she was so poor in clothes that how she was ever to be married creditably she didn’t know.", "우리가 아래층으로 내려갈 때 캐디가 풀이 죽어 있어도, 내 목에 매달려 다시 흐느껴도, 차라리 야단을 맞았더라면 이런 무관심을 받느니 훨씬 나았을 거라고 말해도, 옷가지가 워낙 변변찮아서 어떻게 남부끄럽지 않게 결혼할 수 있을지 모르겠다고 나에게 털어놓아도, 나는 놀라지 않았다."], ["I gradually cheered her up by dwelling on the many things she would do for her unfortunate father and for Peepy when she had a home of her own; and finally we went downstairs into the damp dark kitchen, where Peepy and his little brothers and sisters were grovelling on the stone floor and where we had such a game of play with them that to prevent myself from being quite torn to pieces I was obliged to fall back on my fairy-tales.", "나는 그녀가 자기 집을 갖게 되면 불운한 아버지와 피피를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 숱한 일들을 들려주며 차츰 그녀의 기운을 북돋웠다. 그러고서 마침내 우리는 눅눅하고 어두컴컴한 부엌으로 내려갔는데, 거기서는 피피와 그 어린 동생들이 돌바닥에 엎드려 기어 다니고 있었고, 우리가 그 아이들과 어찌나 신나게 놀았던지, 갈가리 찢기는 꼴을 면하려고 나는 동화 이야기를 꺼내 들 수밖에 없었다."], ["From time to time I heard loud voices in the parlour overhead, and occasionally a violent tumbling about of the furniture.", "이따금 위층 거실에서 큰 목소리가 들려왔고, 가끔은 가구가 우당탕 거칠게 나뒹구는 소리도 들렸다."], ["The last effect I am afraid was caused by poor Mr. Jellyby’s breaking away from the dining-table and making rushes at the window with the intention of throwing himself into the area whenever he made any new attempt to understand his affairs.", "마지막 소리는, 짐작건대 가엾은 젤리비 씨가 자기 사정을 이해해 보려고 새로 한 번 시도할 때마다 식탁을 박차고 뛰쳐나와 채광창 밖 지하 통로로 몸을 던지려고 창문으로 돌진하는 통에 난 것 같아 걱정스러웠다."]], [["As I rode quietly home at night after the day’s bustle, I thought a good deal of Caddy’s engagement and felt confirmed in my hopes (in spite of the elder Mr. Turveydrop) that she would be the happier and better for it.", "그날의 부산함을 뒤로하고 밤에 조용히 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오면서, 나는 캐디의 약혼을 곰곰 생각하며, (늙은 터비드롭 씨가 마음에 걸리긴 했지만) 그녀가 이 일로 더 행복해지고 더 나아지리라는 내 바람에 확신을 굳혔다."], ["And if there seemed to be but a slender chance of her and her husband ever finding out what the model of deportment really was, why that was all for the best too, and who would wish them to be wiser?", "그리고 그녀와 그녀의 남편이 그 몸가짐의 본보기가 실은 어떤 사람인지 끝내 알아챌 가망이 거의 없어 보인다 한들, 그것 또한 다 잘된 일이었으니, 누가 그들이 더 영리해지기를 바라겠는가?"], ["I did not wish them to be any wiser and indeed was half ashamed of not entirely believing in him myself.", "나는 그들이 조금이라도 더 영리해지기를 바라지 않았고, 사실 나 자신도 그를 온전히 믿지 않는 것이 반쯤 부끄러웠다."], ["And I looked up at the stars, and thought about travellers in distant countries and the stars THEY saw, and hoped I might always be so blest and happy as to be useful to some one in my small way.", "그리고 나는 별들을 올려다보며, 먼 나라의 여행자들과 그들이 바라보는 별들을 생각하면서, 나도 늘 내 작은 방식으로나마 누군가에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되는 복과 행복을 누릴 수 있기를 바랐다."]], [["They were so glad to see me when I got home, as they always were, that I could have sat down and cried for joy if that had not been a method of making myself disagreeable.", "집에 도착하니 식구들은 늘 그렇듯 나를 보고 어찌나 반가워하던지, 그게 나 자신을 밉살스럽게 만드는 짓만 아니었다면 나는 주저앉아 기쁨의 눈물을 흘릴 뻔했다."], ["Everybody in the house, from the lowest to the highest, showed me such a bright face of welcome, and spoke so cheerily, and was so happy to do anything for me, that I suppose there never was such a fortunate little creature in the world.", "집안의 위아래 모든 사람이 더없이 환한 얼굴로 나를 반기고, 더없이 명랑하게 말을 건네고, 나를 위해서라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 해 주려고 하니, 세상에 나처럼 복 받은 자그마한 것은 일찍이 없었으리라 싶었다."]], [["We got into such a chatty state that night, through Ada and my guardian drawing me out to tell them all about Caddy, that I went on prose, prose, prosing for a length of time.", "그날 밤 우리는 어찌나 수다스러운 분위기에 빠져들었던지—에이다와 후견인이 캐디 이야기를 죄다 들려 달라고 나를 꾀어낸 통에—나는 한참 동안 주절주절, 주절주절, 주절주절 늘어놓고 말았다."], ["At last I got up to my own room, quite red to think how I had been holding forth, and then I heard a soft tap at my door.", "마침내 내 방으로 올라가면서, 내가 그토록 떠벌렸다는 생각에 얼굴이 새빨개졌는데, 그때 문을 부드럽게 두드리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["So I said, “Come in!”", "그래서 내가 \"들어와요!\""], ["and there came in a pretty little girl, neatly dressed in mourning, who dropped a curtsy.", "하자, 상복을 단정하게 차려입은 예쁘장한 어린 여자아이가 들어와 무릎을 굽혀 인사했다."]], [["“If you please, miss,”", "\"실례합니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said the little girl in a soft voice, “I am Charley.”", "어린 여자아이가 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"저는 찰리예요.\""]], [["“Why, so you are,”", "\"어머나, 정말 그렇구나.\""], ["said I, stooping down in astonishment and giving her a kiss.", "나는 깜짝 놀라 몸을 숙이며 그 아이에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["“How glad am I to see you, Charley!”", "\"찰리, 만나서 어찌나 반가운지!\""]], [["“If you please, miss,”", "\"실례합니다, 아가씨.\""], ["pursued Charley in the same soft voice, “I’m your maid.”", "찰리가 여전히 부드러운 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"저는 아가씨의 하녀예요.\""]], [["“Charley?”", "\"찰리가?\""]], [["“If you please, miss, I’m a present to you, with Mr. Jarndyce’s love.”", "\"실례합니다, 아가씨, 저는 잔다이스 씨가 사랑을 담아 아가씨께 드리는 선물이에요.\""]], [["I sat down with my hand on Charley’s neck and looked at Charley.", "나는 찰리의 목덜미에 손을 얹고 앉아 찰리를 바라보았다."]], [["“And oh, miss,”", "\"그리고요, 아가씨,\""], ["says Charley, clapping her hands, with the tears starting down her dimpled cheeks, “Tom’s at school, if you please, and learning so good!", "찰리가 보조개 팬 뺨으로 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 두 손을 마주치며 말했다. \"톰은 학교에 다녀요, 그리고 어찌나 잘 배우는지요!"], ["And little Emma, she’s with Mrs. Blinder, miss, a-being took such care of!", "그리고 어린 에마는요, 블라인더 부인 댁에 있는데, 어찌나 살뜰히 보살핌을 받는지요!"], ["And Tom, he would have been at school—and Emma, she would have been left with Mrs. Blinder—and me, I should have been here—all a deal sooner, miss; only Mr.", "그리고 톰은 진작 학교에 갔을 거고요—에마도 진작 블라인더 부인 댁에 맡겨졌을 거고요—저도 진작 여기 와 있었을 거예요, 아가씨."], ["Jarndyce thought that Tom and Emma and me had better get a little used to parting first, we was so small.", "다만 잔다이스 씨가, 톰이랑 에마랑 저랑 헤어지는 일에 먼저 좀 익숙해지는 게 낫겠다고 여기셨어요. 우리가 워낙 어렸으니까요."], ["Don’t cry, if you please, miss!”", "울지 마세요, 아가씨, 제발요!\""]], [["“I can’t help it, Charley.”", "\"어쩔 수가 없구나, 찰리.\""]], [["“No, miss, nor I can’t help it,”", "\"네, 아가씨, 저도 어쩔 수가 없어요.\""], ["says Charley.", "찰리가 말했다."], ["“And if you please, miss, Mr. Jarndyce’s love, and he thinks you’ll like to teach me now and then.", "\"그리고요, 아가씨, 잔다이스 씨가 사랑을 전하시며, 아가씨가 이따금 저를 가르쳐 주시면 좋아하실 것 같다고 하셨어요."], ["And if you please, Tom and Emma and me is to see each other once a month.", "그리고요, 톰이랑 에마랑 저는 한 달에 한 번씩 서로 만나기로 했어요."], ["And I’m so happy and so thankful, miss,”", "그래서 저는 너무 행복하고 너무 고마워요, 아가씨.\""], ["cried Charley with a heaving heart, “and I’ll try to be such a good maid!”", "찰리가 벅차오르는 가슴으로 외쳤다. \"그리고 저는 정말 좋은 하녀가 되도록 애쓸게요!\""]], [["“Oh, Charley dear, never forget who did all this!”", "\"오, 찰리야, 누가 이 모든 걸 해 주었는지 절대 잊지 마라!\""]], [["“No, miss, I never will.", "\"네, 아가씨, 절대 안 잊을 거예요."], ["Nor Tom won’t.", "톰도 안 잊을 거고요."], ["Nor yet Emma.", "에마도요."], ["It was all you, miss.”", "다 아가씨가 해 주신 거예요.\""]], [["“I have known nothing of it.", "\"나는 아무것도 몰랐단다."], ["It was Mr. Jarndyce, Charley.”", "잔다이스 씨가 하신 거야, 찰리.\""]], [["“Yes, miss, but it was all done for the love of you and that you might be my mistress.", "\"네, 아가씨, 그렇지만 다 아가씨를 사랑하셔서, 또 아가씨가 제 주인님이 되시라고 하신 거예요."], ["If you please, miss, I am a little present with his love, and it was all done for the love of you.", "실례지만, 아가씨, 저는 그분이 사랑을 담아 보내신 자그마한 선물이고, 다 아가씨를 사랑하셔서 하신 일이에요."], ["Me and Tom was to be sure to remember it.”", "저랑 톰은 꼭 그걸 기억하기로 했어요.\""]], [["Charley dried her eyes and entered on her functions, going in her matronly little way about and about the room and folding up everything she could lay her hands upon.", "찰리는 눈물을 닦고 자기 소임에 나섰다. 그 의젓한 작은 몸가짐으로 방안을 이리저리 돌며 손에 닿는 것은 죄다 개켜 정리했다."], ["Presently Charley came creeping back to my side and said, “Oh, don’t cry, if you please, miss.”", "이윽고 찰리가 슬그머니 내 곁으로 다시 다가와 말했다. \"오, 울지 마세요, 아가씨, 제발요.\""]], [["And I said again, “I can’t help it, Charley.”", "그래서 나는 다시 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없구나, 찰리.\""]], [["And Charley said again, “No, miss, nor I can’t help it.”", "그러자 찰리가 다시 말했다. \"네, 아가씨, 저도 어쩔 수가 없어요.\""], ["And so, after all, I did cry for joy indeed, and so did she. An Appeal Case", "그리하여 결국 나는 정말로 기쁨에 겨워 울고 말았고, 찰리도 그러했다."]], [["As soon as Richard and I had held the conversation of which I have given an account, Richard communicated the state of his mind to Mr.", "리처드와 내가 앞서 적은 그 대화를 나눈 직후, 리처드는 자기 마음 상태를 잔다이스 씨에게 털어놓았습니다."], ["Jarndyce. I doubt if my guardian were altogether taken by surprise when he received the representation, though it caused him much uneasiness and disappointment.", "후견인이 그 이야기를 들었을 때 아주 크게 놀라셨는지는 잘 모르겠지만, 그것이 후견인께 적잖은 불안과 실망을 안긴 것만은 분명합니다."], ["He and Richard were often closeted together, late at night and early in the morning, and passed whole days in London, and had innumerable appointments with Mr. Kenge, and laboured through a quantity of disagreeable business.", "후견인과 리처드는 밤늦게도, 이른 아침에도 자주 둘이서만 방에 틀어박혔고, 런던에서 온종일 시간을 보냈으며, 켄지 씨와 수없이 약속을 잡고, 골치 아픈 일을 잔뜩 헤치고 나갔습니다."], ["While they were thus employed, my guardian, though he underwent considerable inconvenience from the state of the wind and rubbed his head so constantly that not a single hair upon it ever rested in its right place, was as genial with Ada and me as at any other time, but maintained a steady reserve on these matters.", "그렇게 바쁘게 지내는 동안, 후견인은 바람의 상태 때문에 적잖은 불편을 겪으면서 머리를 어찌나 쉴 새 없이 문질러대시던지 머리카락 한 올도 제자리에 가만히 있질 못했지만, 그래도 에이다와 나에게는 여느 때와 다름없이 다정하셨고, 다만 그 일들에 관해서는 한결같이 입을 다무셨습니다."], ["And as our utmost endeavours could only elicit from Richard himself sweeping assurances that everything was going on capitally and that it really was all right at last, our anxiety was not much relieved by him.", "그리고 우리가 아무리 애를 써봐야 리처드 본인에게서 끌어낼 수 있는 거라곤 모든 일이 더없이 잘 풀리고 있으며 마침내 정말 다 괜찮아졌다는 막연한 장담뿐이었으니, 그를 통해서는 우리 불안이 그다지 가시지 않았습니다."]], [["We learnt, however, as the time went on, that a new application was made to the Lord Chancellor on Richard’s behalf as an infant and a ward, and I don’t know what, and that there was a quantity of talking, and that the Lord Chancellor described him in open court as a vexatious and capricious infant, and that the matter was adjourned and readjourned, and referred, and reported on, and petitioned about until Richard began to doubt (as he told us) whether, if he entered the army at all, it would not be as a veteran of seventy or eighty years of age.", "그러나 시간이 흐르면서 우리도 차츰 알게 되었습니다. 리처드가 미성년자이자 피후견인이라는 둥, 뭐라는 둥의 자격으로 리처드를 위해 대법원장에게 새 신청서가 올라갔다는 것, 말이 무성하게 오갔다는 것, 대법원장이 공개 법정에서 리처드를 두고 성가시고 변덕스러운 미성년자라고 했다는 것, 그 문제가 연기되고 또 연기되고, 다른 데로 회부되고, 보고서가 올라오고, 청원이 거듭된 끝에, 리처드가 (그가 우리에게 말한 대로) 도대체 자기가 군대에 들어가긴 들어가게 된다면 그게 일흔이나 여든 먹은 노병으로서가 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다는 것까지요."], ["At last an appointment was made for him to see the Lord Chancellor again in his private room, and there the Lord Chancellor very seriously reproved him for trifling with time and not knowing his mind—“a pretty good joke, I think,”", "마침내 그가 대법원장을 다시 그 집무실에서 만날 약속이 잡혔고, 거기서 대법원장은 시간을 헛되이 흘려보내고 제 마음 하나 모른다며 그를 아주 엄숙하게 꾸짖었습니다. \"그쪽에서 그런 말이 나오다니, 꽤 그럴듯한 농담 아닌가요.\""], ["said Richard, “from that quarter!”—and at last it was settled that his application should be granted.", "리처드가 말했습니다. 그리고 마침내 그의 신청을 받아들이는 것으로 정해졌습니다."], ["His name was entered at the Horse Guards as an applicant for an ensign’s commission; the purchase-money was deposited at an agent’s; and Richard, in his usual characteristic way, plunged into a violent course of military study and got up at five o’clock every morning to practise the broadsword exercise.", "그의 이름이 기병사령부에 소위 임관 신청자로 등록되었고, 임관에 드는 돈은 대리인에게 예치되었으며, 리처드는 늘 그렇듯 그다운 방식으로 군사 학습이라는 격렬한 과정에 곤두박질치듯 뛰어들어, 날마다 새벽 다섯 시에 일어나 대검 훈련을 익혔습니다."]], [["Thus, vacation succeeded term, and term succeeded vacation.", "이렇게 휴정기가 개정기에 이어지고, 개정기가 다시 휴정기에 이어졌습니다."], ["We sometimes heard of Jarndyce and Jarndyce as being in the paper or out of the paper, or as being to be mentioned, or as being to be spoken to; and it came on, and it went off.", "우리는 이따금 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 사건 목록에 올랐다느니 빠졌다느니, 언급될 예정이라느니, 논의될 거라느니 하는 소식을 들었습니다. 그러다 사건이 다가왔다가는 또 흐지부지 물러가곤 했습니다."], ["Richard, who was now in a professor’s house in London, was able to be with us less frequently than before; my guardian still maintained the same reserve; and so time passed until the commission was obtained and Richard received directions with it to join a regiment in Ireland.", "이제 런던의 어느 교관 집에 머물던 리처드는 예전보다 우리와 함께할 시간이 줄었고, 후견인은 여전히 같은 침묵을 지켰습니다. 그렇게 시간이 흘러 마침내 임관이 이루어졌고, 리처드는 그와 함께 아일랜드의 한 연대로 부임하라는 지시를 받았습니다."]], [["He arrived post-haste with the intelligence one evening, and had a long conference with my guardian.", "어느 날 저녁, 그가 그 소식을 안고 한달음에 달려와서는 후견인과 오랫동안 이야기를 나누었습니다."], ["Upwards of an hour elapsed before my guardian put his head into the room where Ada and I were sitting and said, “Come in, my dears!”", "한 시간 넘게 지나고서야 후견인이 에이다와 내가 앉아 있는 방으로 머리를 들이밀고 \"얘들아, 들어오너라!\" 하고 부르셨습니다."], ["We went in and found Richard, whom we had last seen in high spirits, leaning on the chimney-piece looking mortified and angry.", "우리가 들어가 보니, 우리가 마지막으로 봤을 때만 해도 기운이 펄펄하던 리처드가 벽난로 선반에 기대선 채 굴욕스럽고 분한 얼굴을 하고 있었습니다."]], [["“Rick and I, Ada,” said Mr.", "\"에이다야, 릭과 나는,\""], ["Jarndyce, “are not quite of one mind.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"마음이 영 하나로 모이질 않는구나."], ["Come, come, Rick, put a brighter face upon it!”", "자, 자, 릭, 좀 더 밝은 얼굴을 해보게!\""]], [["“You are very hard with me, sir,”", "\"어르신은 저한테 너무 모지십니다.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“The harder because you have been so considerate to me in all other respects and have done me kindnesses that I can never acknowledge.", "\"다른 모든 면에서 저를 그토록 살뜰히 헤아려주시고, 제가 도저히 다 갚지 못할 친절을 베풀어주셨기에 더더욱 모질게 느껴집니다."], ["I never could have been set right without you, sir.”", "어르신이 안 계셨다면 저는 결코 바로 설 수 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"좋아, 좋아!\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I want to set you more right yet.", "\"나는 자네를 더 바로 세우고 싶다네."], ["I want to set you more right with yourself.”", "자네가 자네 자신과의 관계에서 더 바로 서기를 바라는 거야.\""]], [["“I hope you will excuse my saying, sir,”", "\"외람되지만 한 말씀 드려도 괜찮을지요, 어르신.\""], ["returned Richard in a fiery way, but yet respectfully, “that I think I am the best judge about myself.”", "리처드가 발끈하면서도 공손하게 대꾸했습니다. \"제 일은 제가 가장 잘 판단한다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“I hope you will excuse my saying, my dear Rick,”", "\"외람되지만 나도 한마디 하지, 릭.\""], ["observed Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 더없이 다정한 쾌활함과 좋은 기분으로 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce with the sweetest cheerfulness and good humour, “that it’s quite natural in you to think so, but I don’t think so.", "\"자네가 그렇게 생각하는 건 지극히 당연한 일이지만, 나는 그렇게 보지 않는다네. 릭, 나는 내 도리를 다해야 하네."], ["I must do my duty, Rick, or you could never care for me in cool blood; and I hope you will always care for me, cool and hot.”", "그러지 않으면 자네가 냉정해진 뒤에 나를 아낄 수가 없을 테니까. 그리고 나는 자네가 늘 나를 아껴주기를 바라네, 냉정할 때나 뜨거울 때나 말일세.\""]], [["Ada had turned so pale that he made her sit down in his reading-chair and sat beside her.", "에이다의 얼굴이 어찌나 새파래졌던지, 후견인은 그녀를 자신의 독서용 의자에 앉히고 그 곁에 자리를 잡았습니다."]], [["“It’s nothing, my dear,”", "\"아무것도 아니란다, 얘야.\""], ["he said, “it’s nothing.", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"정말 아무것도 아니야."], ["Rick and I have only had a friendly difference, which we must state to you, for you are the theme.", "릭과 나는 그저 다정한 의견 차이가 있었을 뿐인데, 너에게 말해두어야겠구나. 그 주제가 바로 너거든."], ["Now you are afraid of what’s coming.”", "자, 이제 무슨 말이 나올지 겁이 나는 모양이구나.\""]], [["“I am not indeed, cousin John,”", "\"그런 거 아니에요, 존 오라버니.\""], ["replied Ada with a smile, “if it is to come from you.”", "에이다가 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"그게 오라버니에게서 나오는 말이라면요.\""]], [["“Thank you, my dear.", "\"고맙다, 얘야."], ["Do you give me a minute’s calm attention, without looking at Rick.", "잠깐만 차분히 내 말에 귀를 기울여주렴, 릭은 보지 말고. 그리고 우리 꼬마 아주머니, 너도 마찬가지다."], ["And, little woman, do you likewise.", "얘야,\" 안락의자 팔걸이에 놓인 에이다의 손 위에 자신의 손을 얹으며 후견인이 말을 이었습니다."], ["My dear girl,”", "\"우리 넷이서 나눈 이야기를 기억하지?"], ["putting his hand on hers as it lay on the side of the easy-chair, “you recollect the talk we had, we four when the little woman told me of a little love affair?”", "이 꼬마 아주머니가 어떤 자그마한 사랑 이야기를 나에게 들려주었을 때 말이다.\""]], [["“It is not likely that either Richard or I can ever forget your kindness that day, cousin John.”", "\"그날 오라버니가 베풀어주신 친절을 리처드도 저도 결코 잊을 리 없어요, 존 오라버니.\""]], [["“I can never forget it,”", "\"저는 결코 잊지 못합니다.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."]], [["“And I can never forget it,”", "\"저도 결코 잊지 못해요.\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했습니다."]], [["“So much the easier what I have to say, and so much the easier for us to agree,”", "\"그렇다면 내가 할 말도 한결 쉬워지고, 우리가 마음을 모으기도 한결 수월해지겠구나.\""], ["returned my guardian, his face irradiated by the gentleness and honour of his heart.", "후견인이 대답했습니다. 그 마음의 온화함과 명예로움이 얼굴에 환하게 비쳤습니다."], ["“Ada, my bird, you should know that Rick has now chosen his profession for the last time.", "\"에이다, 우리 새야, 너도 알아야 한다. 릭은 이제 마지막으로 제 직업을 정한 거야."], ["All that he has of certainty will be expended when he is fully equipped.", "완전히 채비를 갖추고 나면, 그가 확실히 가진 것은 모조리 거기에 들어가게 된다."], ["He has exhausted his resources and is bound henceforward to the tree he has planted.”", "그는 자기 재원을 다 써버렸고, 이제부터는 자기가 심은 그 나무에 매여 있게 되는 거야.\""]], [["“Quite true that I have exhausted my present resources, and I am quite content to know it.", "\"제가 지금 가진 재원을 다 써버린 건 사실이고, 저는 그걸 아는 데 아무 불만 없습니다."], ["But what I have of certainty, sir,”", "하지만 어르신, 제가 확실히 가진 것이,\""], ["said Richard, “is not all I have.”", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"제가 가진 전부는 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Rick, Rick!”", "\"릭, 릭!\""], ["cried my guardian with a sudden terror in his manner, and in an altered voice, and putting up his hands as if he would have stopped his ears.", "후견인이 갑자기 겁에 질린 듯한 몸짓으로, 달라진 목소리로, 마치 귀라도 틀어막으려는 듯 두 손을 치켜들며 외쳤습니다."], ["“For the love of God, don’t found a hope or expectation on the family curse!", "\"제발 부탁이니, 그 집안의 저주 위에 희망이나 기대를 세우지 말게!"], ["Whatever you do on this side the grave, never give one lingering glance towards the horrible phantom that has haunted us so many years.", "무덤 이쪽 세상에서 무얼 하든, 그 끔찍한 유령 쪽으로는 한 번도 미련 어린 눈길을 던지지 말게. 그놈은 그토록 오랜 세월 우리를 따라다니며 괴롭혀온 놈이야."], ["Better to borrow, better to beg, better to die!”", "차라리 꾸어 쓰는 게 낫고, 차라리 빌어먹는 게 낫고, 차라리 죽는 게 낫네!\""]], [["We were all startled by the fervour of this warning.", "이 경고의 격렬함에 우리는 모두 흠칫 놀랐습니다."], ["Richard bit his lip and held his breath, and glanced at me as if he felt, and knew that I felt too, how much he needed it.", "리처드는 입술을 깨물고 숨을 죽인 채, 자기가 그 말을 얼마나 절실히 필요로 하는지 스스로 느끼고 있으며 나도 그걸 느끼고 있음을 안다는 듯 나를 흘끗 보았습니다."]], [["“Ada, my dear,” said Mr.", "\"에이다, 얘야.\""], ["Jarndyce, recovering his cheerfulness, “these are strong words of advice, but I live in Bleak House and have seen a sight here.", "잔다이스 씨가 쾌활함을 되찾으며 말했습니다. \"이건 너무 센 충고의 말이긴 하다만, 나는 황폐한 집에 살면서 여기서 그런 광경을 직접 보았단다."], ["Enough of that.", "그 얘긴 그만하자."], ["All Richard had to start him in the race of life is ventured.", "릭이 인생이라는 경주에서 출발하는 데 쓸 밑천은 모조리 걸렸다."], ["I recommend to him and you, for his sake and your own, that he should depart from us with the understanding that there is no sort of contract between you.", "나는 그와 너에게, 그를 위해서나 너 자신을 위해서나 이렇게 권한다. 그가 우리 곁을 떠날 때, 너희 둘 사이에는 그 어떤 약속도 없다는 양해 아래 떠나야 한다고 말이다."], ["I must go further.", "한 걸음 더 나아가야겠구나."], ["I will be plain with you both.", "너희 둘 모두에게 솔직히 말하마."], ["You were to confide freely in me, and I will confide freely in you.", "너희는 나를 거리낌 없이 믿어주기로 했으니, 나도 너희를 거리낌 없이 믿고 말하마."], ["I ask you wholly to relinquish, for the present, any tie but your relationship.”", "나는 너희에게, 당분간은 혈연이라는 인연을 빼고 다른 모든 끈을 온전히 내려놓으라고 부탁한다.\""]], [["“Better to say at once, sir,”", "\"차라리 단번에 말씀하시지요, 어르신.\""], ["returned Richard, “that you renounce all confidence in me and that you advise Ada to do the same.”", "리처드가 대꾸했습니다. \"저에 대한 모든 믿음을 거두시고, 에이다에게도 그렇게 하라고 권하시는 거라고 말입니다.\""]], [["“Better to say nothing of the sort, Rick, because I don’t mean it.”", "\"그런 말은 입에도 올리지 않는 게 낫겠네, 릭. 나는 그런 뜻이 아니니까.\""]], [["“You think I have begun ill, sir,”", "\"제가 첫발을 잘못 뗐다고 생각하시는 거지요, 어르신.\""], ["retorted Richard.", "리처드가 받아쳤습니다."], ["“I HAVE, I know.”", "\"그건 맞습니다, 저도 압니다.\""]], [["“How I hoped you would begin, and how go on, I told you when we spoke of these things last,” said Mr.", "\"내가 자네에게 어떻게 시작하고 어떻게 이어가기를 바랐는지는, 지난번에 이런 일들을 이야기할 때 다 말했네.\""], ["Jarndyce in a cordial and encouraging manner.", "잔다이스 씨가 진심 어린, 북돋우는 태도로 말했습니다."], ["“You have not made that beginning yet, but there is a time for all things, and yours is not gone by; rather, it is just now fully come.", "\"자네는 아직 그 시작을 떼지 못했지만, 모든 일에는 때가 있는 법이고, 자네의 때는 지나간 게 아니라네. 오히려 바로 지금 완전히 무르익은 거지."], ["Make a clear beginning altogether.", "모든 걸 깨끗하게 처음부터 시작하게."], ["You two (very young, my dears) are cousins.", "너희 둘은 (얘들아, 아직 한참 어리지) 사촌이다."], ["As yet, you are nothing more.", "지금으로선 그 이상은 아무것도 아니야."], ["What more may come must come of being worked out, Rick, and no sooner.”", "그 이상의 무언가가 생긴다면, 그건 노력으로 일궈낸 결과로 와야 하는 것이지, 릭, 그보다 먼저 와서는 안 돼.\""]], [["“You are very hard with me, sir,”", "\"어르신은 저한테 너무 모지십니다.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“Harder than I could have supposed you would be.”", "\"제가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 모지셔요.\""]], [["“My dear boy,” said Mr.", "\"얘야,\""], ["Jarndyce, “I am harder with myself when I do anything that gives you pain.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다. \"자네에게 고통을 주는 일을 할 때면, 나는 나 자신에게 더 모질다네."], ["You have your remedy in your own hands.", "처방은 자네 손안에 쥐여 있어."], ["Ada, it is better for him that he should be free and that there should be no youthful engagement between you.", "에이다야, 릭이 자유로운 게, 그리고 너희 사이에 어린 나이의 약혼 따위가 없는 게 그에게 더 낫단다."], ["Rick, it is better for her, much better; you owe it to her.", "릭, 그게 에이다에게도 더 나아, 훨씬 더. 자네는 에이다에게 그걸 빚지고 있는 거야."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Each of you will do what is best for the other, if not what is best for yourselves.”", "너희 각자가 서로를 위해 가장 좋은 길을 택하게 될 게다, 설령 자기 자신을 위한 가장 좋은 길은 못 되더라도 말이다.\""]], [["“Why is it best, sir?”", "\"어째서 그게 가장 좋다는 겁니까, 어르신?\""], ["returned Richard hastily.", "리처드가 황급히 대꾸했습니다."], ["“It was not when we opened our hearts to you.", "\"저희가 어르신께 마음을 털어놓았을 때는 그게 가장 좋은 길이 아니었잖습니까."], ["You did not say so then.”", "그때는 그런 말씀 안 하셨어요.\""]], [["“I have had experience since.", "\"그 뒤로 내가 겪은 게 있어서다."], ["I don’t blame you, Rick, but I have had experience since.”", "자네를 탓하는 게 아니야, 릭. 하지만 그 뒤로 내가 겪은 게 있어.\""]], [["“You mean of me, sir.”", "\"저를 두고 겪으셨다는 말씀이군요, 어르신.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"음!"], ["Yes, of both of you,”", "그래, 너희 둘 다를 두고 겪었지.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce kindly.", "잔다이스 씨가 다정하게 말했습니다."], ["“The time is not come for your standing pledged to one another.", "\"너희가 서로에게 매여 약속을 지킬 때는 아직 오지 않았다."], ["It is not right, and I must not recognize it.", "그건 옳지 않고, 나는 그걸 인정해서는 안 돼."], ["Come, come, my young cousins, begin afresh!", "자, 자, 우리 어린 사촌들아, 새로 시작하렴!"], ["Bygones shall be bygones, and a new page turned for you to write your lives in.”", "지나간 일은 지나간 일로 묻고, 너희가 너희 삶을 써내려갈 새 페이지를 펼치는 거야.\""]], [["Richard gave an anxious glance at Ada but said nothing.", "리처드는 근심 어린 눈길로 에이다를 흘끗 보았지만 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["“I have avoided saying one word to either of you or to Esther,”", "\"나는 이제껏 너희 둘에게도, 에스더에게도 한마디 입에 올리지 않으려 애써왔다.\""], ["said Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce, “until now, in order that we might be open as the day, and all on equal terms.", "\"지금 이 순간까지 말이다. 그래야 우리가 대낮처럼 훤히 트인 채로, 모두 똑같은 처지에서 이야기할 수 있을 테니까."], ["I now affectionately advise, I now most earnestly entreat, you two to part as you came here.", "이제 나는 다정한 마음으로 권하고, 더없이 간절히 청한다. 너희 둘, 여기 왔던 그 모습 그대로 헤어지렴."], ["Leave all else to time, truth, and steadfastness.", "나머지는 모두 시간과 진실과 한결같음에 맡기고."], ["If you do otherwise, you will do wrong, and you will have made me do wrong in ever bringing you together.”", "만일 그러지 않는다면, 너희는 그릇된 일을 하는 것이고, 너희를 애초에 한자리에 모은 나까지 그릇된 일을 한 사람으로 만드는 것이다.\""]], [["A long silence succeeded.", "긴 침묵이 뒤따랐습니다."]], [["“Cousin Richard,” said Ada then, raising her blue eyes tenderly to his face, “after what our cousin John has said, I think no choice is left us. Your mind may be quite at ease about me, for you will leave me here under his care and will be sure that I can have nothing to wish for—quite sure if I guide myself by his advice. I—I don’t doubt, cousin Richard,” said Ada, a little confused, “that you are very fond of me, and I—I don’t think you will fall in love with anybody else. But I should like you to consider well about it too, as I should like you to be in all things very happy. You may trust in me, cousin Richard. I am not at all changeable; but I am not unreasonable, and should never blame you. Even cousins may be sorry to part; and in truth I am very, very sorry, Richard, though I know it’s for your welfare. I shall always think of you affectionately, and often talk of you with Esther, and—and perhaps you will sometimes think a little of me, cousin Richard. So now,” said Ada, going up to him and giving him her trembling hand, “we are only cousins again, Richard—for the time perhaps—and I pray for a blessing on my dear cousin, wherever he goes!”", "\"리처드 사촌,\" 이윽고 에이다가 푸른 눈을 다정하게 들어 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했습니다. \"존 오라버니가 하신 말씀을 듣고 나니, 우리에게 다른 선택의 여지는 없는 것 같아요. 나에 대해서는 마음 푹 놓으세요. 사촌은 나를 여기 오라버니의 보살핌 아래 두고 떠나는 거고, 내가 바랄 게 아무것도 없으리라는 걸 분명히 믿어도 돼요. 오라버니의 조언대로 나를 다스린다면 정말 분명히 그럴 테니까요. 나는… 나는 의심하지 않아요, 리처드 사촌.\" 에이다가 조금 당황한 듯 말했습니다. \"사촌이 나를 무척 아낀다는 걸요. 그리고 나는… 나는 사촌이 다른 누군가와 사랑에 빠질 거라고는 생각하지 않아요. 하지만 사촌도 이 일을 잘 헤아려봤으면 좋겠어요. 나는 사촌이 모든 일에서 아주 행복하기를 바라니까요. 나를 믿어도 돼요, 리처드 사촌. 나는 조금도 변덕스럽지 않아요. 그렇다고 막무가내인 사람도 아니어서, 사촌을 결코 탓하지 않을 거예요. 사촌끼리도 헤어지는 게 슬플 수 있잖아요. 그리고 정말이지 나는 너무, 너무 슬퍼요, 리처드. 이게 사촌의 앞날을 위한 일인 줄 알면서도요. 나는 늘 사촌을 다정하게 떠올릴 거고, 에스더와 자주 사촌 이야기를 나눌 거예요. 그리고… 그리고 어쩌면 사촌도 가끔은 나를 조금쯤 떠올려줄지 모르죠, 리처드 사촌. 자, 이제는요,\" 에이다가 그에게 다가가 떨리는 손을 내밀며 말했습니다. \"우리는 다시 사촌일 뿐이에요, 리처드. 어쩌면 한동안만요. 그리고 나는 어디로 가든 내 소중한 사촌에게 축복이 내리기를 기도할게요!\""]], [["It was strange to me that Richard should not be able to forgive my guardian for entertaining the very same opinion of him which he himself had expressed of himself in much stronger terms to me.", "리처드가 자기 자신에 대해 나에게 훨씬 더 거센 표현으로 직접 했던 바로 그 평가를, 후견인이 똑같이 품었다는 이유로 그를 용서하지 못한다는 게 나에게는 이상하게 느껴졌습니다."], ["But it was certainly the case.", "하지만 분명 그랬습니다."], ["I observed with great regret that from this hour he never was as free and open with Mr. Jarndyce as he had been before.", "나는 이 시각 이후로 리처드가 잔다이스 씨에게 예전만큼 거리낌 없이 마음을 열지 못하는 것을 무척 안타까운 마음으로 지켜보았습니다."], ["He had every reason given him to be so, but he was not; and solely on his side, an estrangement began to arise between them.", "그렇게 해도 좋을 온갖 이유가 그에게 주어졌건만, 그는 그러지 않았습니다. 그리고 오로지 그의 쪽에서만, 두 사람 사이에 서먹함이 싹트기 시작했습니다."]], [["In the business of preparation and equipment he soon lost himself, and even his grief at parting from Ada, who remained in Hertfordshire while he, Mr.", "리처드는 곧 준비와 채비라는 분주함 속에 빠져들어, 에이다와 헤어지는 슬픔마저 잊어버렸습니다. 에이다는 허트퍼드셔에 남았고, 리처드와 잔다이스 씨와 나는 일주일간 런던으로 올라갔습니다."], ["Jarndyce, and I went up to London for a week. He remembered her by fits and starts, even with bursts of tears, and at such times would confide to me the heaviest self-reproaches.", "그는 발작하듯 불쑥불쑥 에이다를 떠올렸는데, 때로는 울음을 터뜨리기까지 했고, 그럴 때면 더없이 무거운 자책의 말을 나에게 털어놓았습니다."], ["But in a few minutes he would recklessly conjure up some undefinable means by which they were both to be made rich and happy for ever, and would become as gay as possible.", "하지만 몇 분만 지나면 두 사람이 영원히 부유하고 행복하게 될 무슨 막연한 방도를 마구잡이로 꾸며내고는, 더할 나위 없이 들떠 명랑해지곤 했습니다."]], [["It was a busy time, and I trotted about with him all day long, buying a variety of things of which he stood in need.", "분주한 시기였고, 나는 그가 필요로 하는 갖가지 물건을 사들이며 온종일 그와 함께 종종걸음을 쳤습니다."], ["Of the things he would have bought if he had been left to his own ways I say nothing.", "그가 제멋대로 두었더라면 샀을 법한 물건들에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["He was perfectly confidential with me, and often talked so sensibly and feelingly about his faults and his vigorous resolutions, and dwelt so much upon the encouragement he derived from these conversations that I could never have been tired if I had tried.", "그는 나에게 모든 걸 거리낌 없이 털어놓았고, 자기 결점과 굳센 다짐에 관해 자주 그토록 사리 분별 있게, 그토록 절절하게 이야기했으며, 이런 대화에서 자기가 얻는 격려를 어찌나 곱씹던지, 나는 아무리 애를 써도 그와 함께 있는 게 지겨워질 수가 없었습니다."]], [["There used, in that week, to come backward and forward to our lodging to fence with Richard a person who had formerly been a cavalry soldier; he was a fine bluff-looking man, of a frank free bearing, with whom Richard had practised for some months.", "그 일주일 동안, 리처드와 검술을 겨루러 우리 숙소를 오가던 사람이 하나 있었습니다. 예전에 기병이었던 사람으로, 풍채가 훤하고 우락부락한 외모에, 솔직하고 거침없는 태도를 지닌 남자였습니다."], ["I heard so much about him, not only from Richard, but from my guardian too, that I was purposely in the room with my work one morning after breakfast when he came.", "리처드는 그와 몇 달째 함께 연습해온 터였습니다. 나는 리처드뿐 아니라 후견인에게서도 그에 관한 이야기를 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 어느 날 아침 식사를 마친 뒤 그가 왔을 때, 나는 일부러 일감을 들고 방에 자리를 잡고 있었습니다."]], [["“Good morning, Mr. George,”", "\"좋은 아침이오, 조지 씨.\""], ["said my guardian, who happened to be alone with me. “Mr.", "마침 나와 단둘이 있던 후견인이 말했습니다."], ["Carstone will be here directly.", "\"카스턴 군이 곧 올 거요."], ["Meanwhile, Miss Summerson is very happy to see you, I know.", "그동안, 서머슨 양이 당신을 만나서 무척 반가워한다는 걸 내가 잘 알지요."], ["Sit down.”", "앉으시오.\""]], [["He sat down, a little disconcerted by my presence, I thought, and without looking at me, drew his heavy sunburnt hand across and across his upper lip.", "그는 자리에 앉았는데, 내가 보기에 내가 있는 게 좀 거북한 듯했고, 나는 쳐다보지도 않은 채 햇볕에 그을린 투박한 손으로 윗입술을 자꾸자꾸 쓸어내렸습니다."]], [["“You are as punctual as the sun,”", "\"당신은 해처럼 시간을 어김없이 지키시는군.\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Military time, sir,”", "\"군대식 시간입니다, 어르신.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“Force of habit.", "\"몸에 밴 버릇이지요."], ["A mere habit in me, sir.", "그저 버릇일 뿐입니다, 어르신."], ["I am not at all business-like.”", "저는 사업가 기질이라곤 도무지 없습니다.\""]], [["“Yet you have a large establishment, too, I am told?”", "\"그래도 제법 큰 사업체를 가지고 있다고 들었는데?\""], ["said Mr. Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Not much of a one, sir.", "\"대단할 것도 없습니다, 어르신."], ["I keep a shooting gallery, but not much of a one.”", "사격장을 하나 하긴 합니다만, 변변치 못합니다.\""]], [["“And what kind of a shot and what kind of a swordsman do you make of Mr. Carstone?”", "\"그래, 카스턴 군은 당신 밑에서 어느 정도 사수가 되고, 어느 정도 검객이 되었소?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Pretty good, sir,”", "\"제법 괜찮습니다, 어르신.\""], ["he replied, folding his arms upon his broad chest and looking very large. “If Mr.", "그가 넓은 가슴 위로 팔짱을 끼고 아주 듬직하게 보이는 자세로 대답했습니다."], ["Carstone was to give his full mind to it, he would come out very good.”", "\"카스턴 군이 온 마음을 쏟기만 한다면, 아주 훌륭해질 겁니다.\""]], [["“But he don’t, I suppose?”", "\"하지만 그러질 않는단 말이지요?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“He did at first, sir, but not afterwards.", "\"처음엔 그랬습니다, 어르신. 그런데 나중엔 안 그러더군요."], ["Not his full mind.", "온 마음을 쏟지 않아요."], ["Perhaps he has something else upon it—some young lady, perhaps.”", "어쩌면 마음에 다른 게 얹혀 있는지도 모르죠. 어떤 아가씨 같은 거요.\""], ["His bright dark eyes glanced at me for the first time.", "그의 빛나는 검은 눈이 처음으로 나를 흘끗 보았습니다."]], [["“He has not me upon his mind, I assure you, Mr. George,”", "\"단언컨대 그 마음에 얹혀 있는 게 저는 아닙니다, 조지 씨.\""], ["said I, laughing, “though you seem to suspect me.”", "내가 웃으며 말했습니다. \"저를 의심하시는 것 같지만요.\""]], [["He reddened a little through his brown and made me a trooper’s bow.", "그는 그을린 얼굴이 살짝 붉어지며 나에게 기병식 인사를 꾸벅했습니다."], ["“No offence, I hope, miss.", "\"기분 상하시지 않았으면 좋겠습니다, 아가씨."], ["I am one of the roughs.”", "저는 거칠게 자란 사람이라서요.\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“I take it as a compliment.”", "\"칭찬으로 받아들이는걸요.\""]], [["If he had not looked at me before, he looked at me now in three or four quick successive glances.", "전에는 나를 쳐다보지 않았더라도, 이제 그는 빠르게 서너 차례 연거푸 나를 쳐다보았습니다."], ["“I beg your pardon, sir,”", "\"죄송합니다, 어르신.\""], ["he said to my guardian with a manly kind of diffidence, “but you did me the honour to mention the young lady’s name—”", "그가 후견인에게 사내다운 머뭇거림을 담아 말했습니다. \"방금 황송하게도 아가씨의 성함을 말씀해주셨는데요—\""]], [["“Miss Summerson.”", "\"서머슨 양이오.\""]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["he repeated, and looked at me again.", "그가 되뇌고는 다시 나를 보았습니다."]], [["“Do you know the name?”", "\"그 이름을 아시나요?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“No, miss.", "\"아닙니다, 아가씨."], ["To my knowledge I never heard it.", "제가 알기로는 한 번도 들어본 적이 없습니다."], ["I thought I had seen you somewhere.”", "어디선가 뵌 적이 있는 것 같아서요.\""]], [["“I think not,”", "\"그건 아닐 거예요.\""], ["I returned, raising my head from my work to look at him; and there was something so genuine in his speech and manner that I was glad of the opportunity.", "나는 그를 보려고 일감에서 고개를 들며 대답했습니다. 그의 말투와 태도에 어딘가 진솔한 데가 있어서 나는 이 기회가 반가웠습니다."], ["“I remember faces very well.”", "\"저는 얼굴을 아주 잘 기억하거든요.\""]], [["“So do I, miss!”", "\"저도 그렇습니다, 아가씨!\""], ["he returned, meeting my look with the fullness of his dark eyes and broad forehead.", "그가 검은 눈과 넓은 이마를 가득 들어 내 눈길을 맞받으며 대답했습니다."], ["“Humph!", "\"흠!"], ["What set me off, now, upon that!”", "대체 뭐가 자꾸 나를 이러게 만드는 거지!\""]], [["His once more reddening through his brown and being disconcerted by his efforts to remember the association brought my guardian to his relief.", "그가 다시 한번 그을린 얼굴이 붉어지면서, 그 연상을 떠올리려 애쓰다가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하자, 후견인이 그를 구해주려고 나섰습니다."]], [["“Have you many pupils, Mr. George?”", "\"제자가 많소, 조지 씨?\""]], [["“They vary in their number, sir.", "\"그날그날 수가 다릅니다, 어르신."], ["Mostly they’re but a small lot to live by.”", "대개는 그걸로 먹고살기엔 빠듯한 수입니다.\""]], [["“And what classes of chance people come to practise at your gallery?”", "\"당신 사격장에는 어떤 부류의 손님들이 연습하러 오나요?\""]], [["“All sorts, sir. Natives and foreigners.", "\"별별 사람이 다 옵니다, 어르신."], ["From gentlemen to ’prentices.", "내국인도 외국인도요. 신사부터 도제까지요."], ["I have had Frenchwomen come, before now, and show themselves dabs at pistol-shooting.", "전에는 프랑스 여자들이 와서 권총 솜씨가 보통이 아닌 걸 뽐낸 적도 있습니다."], ["Mad people out of number, of course, but THEY go everywhere where the doors stand open.”", "미친 사람들이야 셀 수도 없죠. 하긴 그런 사람들은 문이 열려 있는 데라면 어디든 가니까요.\""]], [["“People don’t come with grudges and schemes of finishing their practice with live targets, I hope?”", "\"앙심을 품고 와서, 산 표적으로 연습을 마무리하겠다는 속셈을 가진 사람은 없겠지요?\""], ["said my guardian, smiling.", "후견인이 미소 지으며 말했습니다."]], [["“Not much of that, sir, though that HAS happened.", "\"그런 일은 흔치 않습니다, 어르신."], ["Mostly they come for skill—or idleness.", "그런 일이 있긴 했지만요."], ["Six of one, and half-a-dozen of the other.", "대개는 솜씨를 키우러 오거나, 빈둥거리러 옵니다."], ["I beg your pardon,”", "이거나 저거나 거기서 거기죠."], ["said Mr.", "외람되지만,\""], ["George, sitting stiffly upright and squaring an elbow on each knee, “but I believe you’re a Chancery suitor, if I have heard correct?”", "조지 씨가 뻣뻣하게 허리를 곧추세우고 무릎마다 팔꿈치를 짚어 모으며 말했습니다. \"제가 잘못 들은 게 아니라면, 어르신께서 대법원 소송 당사자라고 하시던데요?\""]], [["“I am sorry to say I am.”", "\"유감스럽게도 그렇소.\""]], [["“I have had one of YOUR compatriots in my time, sir.”", "\"제가 한창때 어르신과 같은 처지의 사람을 하나 알았습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["“A Chancery suitor?”", "\"대법원 소송 당사자를?\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다."], ["“How was that?”", "\"어떻게 된 일이오?\""]], [["“Why, the man was so badgered and worried and tortured by being knocked about from post to pillar, and from pillar to post,”", "\"글쎄요, 그 사람이 이리저리 떠밀리고 또 떠밀려서, 이 기둥에서 저 기둥으로 두들겨 맞듯 시달리고 들볶이고 고문당하다시피 하다 보니,\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mr.", "\"성미가 비뚤어졌습니다."], ["George, “that he got out of sorts.", "저는 그가 누구를 겨눌 생각이 있었다고는 보지 않습니다만, 어찌나 분하고 사나운 상태였던지, 와서 오십 발 쏠 돈을 치르고는 시뻘게질 때까지 쏘아대곤 했죠."], ["I don’t believe he had any idea of taking aim at anybody, but he was in that condition of resentment and violence that he would come and pay for fifty shots and fire away till he was red hot.", "하루는 곁에 아무도 없고 그가 저한테 제 억울함을 화내며 늘어놓길래 제가 그랬습니다. '이 연습이 자네한테 안전판이 되어준다면야, 그거 좋네."], ["One day I said to him when there was nobody by and he had been talking to me angrily about his wrongs, ‘If this practice is a safety-valve, comrade, well and good; but I don’t altogether like your being so bent upon it in your present state of mind; I’d rather you took to something else.’ I was on my guard for a blow, he was that passionate; but he received it in very good part and left off directly.", "하지만 지금 그런 마음 상태로 여기에 이렇게 매달리는 건 나도 영 마뜩잖아. 자네가 다른 데 마음을 붙이는 게 좋겠어.'"], ["We shook hands and struck up a sort of friendship.”", "어찌나 격해 있던지 저는 한 대 맞을 각오를 했는데, 그가 아주 순순히 받아들이고 곧장 그만두더군요. 우리는 악수를 나누고 일종의 우정을 맺었습니다.\""]], [["“What was that man?”", "\"그 사람이 누구였소?\""], ["asked my guardian in a new tone of interest.", "후견인이 새삼 흥미가 묻어나는 어조로 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, he began by being a small Shropshire farmer before they made a baited bull of him,”", "\"글쎄요, 그가 미끼로 던져진 황소 꼴이 되기 전엔, 슈롭셔의 자그마한 농부로 시작한 사람이었습니다.\""], ["said Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Was his name Gridley?”", "\"이름이 그리들리였소?\""]], [["“It was, sir.”", "\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["Mr. George directed another succession of quick bright glances at me as my guardian and I exchanged a word or two of surprise at the coincidence, and I therefore explained to him how we knew the name.", "후견인과 내가 그 우연에 놀라 몇 마디 주고받는 동안 조지 씨는 또 한 차례 나에게 빠르고 빛나는 눈길을 연거푸 던졌고, 그래서 나는 우리가 어떻게 그 이름을 알게 되었는지 그에게 설명해주었습니다."], ["He made me another of his soldierly bows in acknowledgment of what he called my condescension.", "그는 자기가 황송하다고 부른 나의 친절에 답례하듯 또 한 번 군인다운 인사를 꾸벅했습니다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠습니다,\""], ["he said as he looked at me, “what it is that sets me off again—but—bosh!", "그가 나를 보며 말했습니다. \"대체 뭐가 또 나를 이러게 만드는 건지—그런데—제기랄!"], ["What’s my head running against!”", "내 머릿속이 도대체 뭐랑 부딪히고 있는 거람!\""], ["He passed one of his heavy hands over his crisp dark hair as if to sweep the broken thoughts out of his mind and sat a little forward, with one arm akimbo and the other resting on his leg, looking in a brown study at the ground.", "그는 투박한 손 하나로 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 한 번 쓸어 마치 깨진 생각들을 머릿속에서 쓸어내려는 듯하더니, 한쪽 팔은 허리에 짚고 다른 팔은 다리에 얹은 채 몸을 살짝 앞으로 숙이고 앉아, 깊은 생각에 잠겨 바닥을 들여다보았습니다."]], [["“I am sorry to learn that the same state of mind has got this Gridley into new troubles and that he is in hiding,”", "\"그 똑같은 마음 상태가 이 그리들리를 새로운 곤경으로 몰아넣어 지금 몸을 숨기고 있다니, 듣기에 안타깝구려.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“So I am told, sir,” returned Mr.", "\"그렇게 들었습니다, 어르신.\""], ["George, still musing and looking on the ground.", "조지 씨가 여전히 생각에 잠겨 바닥을 내려다보며 대답했습니다."], ["“So I am told.”", "\"그렇게 들었습니다.\""]], [["“You don’t know where?”", "\"어디 있는지는 모르시오?\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"모릅니다, 어르신.\""], ["returned the trooper, lifting up his eyes and coming out of his reverie.", "기병이 눈을 들어 몽상에서 깨어나며 대답했습니다."], ["“I can’t say anything about him.", "\"그에 대해서는 아무 말씀도 드릴 수가 없습니다."], ["He will be worn out soon, I expect.", "곧 다 닳아 없어질 겁니다, 제 짐작에는요."], ["You may file a strong man’s heart away for a good many years, but it will tell all of a sudden at last.”", "굳센 사람의 심장도 여러 해 동안 갈아내다 보면, 결국 어느 순간 단번에 무너지는 법이니까요.\""]], [["Richard’s entrance stopped the conversation.", "리처드가 들어오는 바람에 대화가 끊겼습니다."], ["Mr. George rose, made me another of his soldierly bows, wished my guardian a good day, and strode heavily out of the room.", "조지 씨는 자리에서 일어나 나에게 또 한 번 군인다운 인사를 꾸벅하고, 후견인께 좋은 하루 보내시라 인사한 뒤, 묵직한 걸음으로 성큼성큼 방을 나갔습니다."]], [["This was the morning of the day appointed for Richard’s departure.", "이날 아침이 바로 리처드의 출발이 예정된 날이었습니다."], ["We had no more purchases to make now; I had completed all his packing early in the afternoon; and our time was disengaged until night, when he was to go to Liverpool for Holyhead.", "이제 더 살 물건도 없었고, 나는 그날 오후 일찍 그의 짐을 모두 꾸려놓았으며, 밤이 될 때까지 우리 시간은 비어 있었습니다. 그는 밤에 홀리헤드로 가려고 리버풀로 떠날 예정이었습니다."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce being again expected to come on that day, Richard proposed to me that we should go down to the court and hear what passed.", "그날 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 또 거론될 것으로 예상되었기에, 리처드는 우리가 법정에 내려가 무슨 일이 오가는지 들어보자고 제안했습니다."], ["As it was his last day, and he was eager to go, and I had never been there, I gave my consent and we walked down to Westminster, where the court was then sitting.", "마지막 날인 데다 그가 가고 싶어 안달이었고, 나는 거기 한 번도 가본 적이 없었기에, 나는 그러자고 했고, 우리는 그때 개정 중이던 웨스트민스터로 걸어 내려갔습니다."], ["We beguiled the way with arrangements concerning the letters that Richard was to write to me and the letters that I was to write to him and with a great many hopeful projects.", "우리는 리처드가 나에게 쓸 편지와 내가 그에게 쓸 편지에 관한 약속을 하고, 또 희망에 찬 온갖 계획을 세우며 길을 즐겁게 보냈습니다."], ["My guardian knew where we were going and therefore was not with us.", "후견인은 우리가 어디로 가는지 알고 있었기에 함께하지 않았습니다."]], [["When we came to the court, there was the Lord Chancellor—the same whom I had seen in his private room in Lincoln’s Inn—sitting in great state and gravity on the bench, with the mace and seals on a red table below him and an immense flat nosegay, like a little garden, which scented the whole court. Below the table, again, was a long row of solicitors, with bundles of papers on the matting at their feet; and then there were the gentlemen of the bar in wigs and gowns—some awake and some asleep, and one talking, and nobody paying much attention to what he said. The Lord Chancellor leaned back in his very easy chair with his elbow on the cushioned arm and his forehead resting on his hand; some of those who were present dozed; some read the newspapers; some walked about or whispered in groups: all seemed perfectly at their ease, by no means in a hurry, very unconcerned, and extremely comfortable.", "우리가 법정에 이르렀을 때, 대법원장이 거기 있었습니다. 내가 링컨스인의 집무실에서 본 바로 그 사람이었는데, 자못 위엄 있고 근엄하게 법대에 앉아 있었고, 그 아래 붉은 탁자에는 권표와 옥새가 놓여 있었으며, 작은 정원 같은 거대하고 납작한 꽃다발이 법정 전체에 향기를 풍기고 있었습니다. 그 탁자 아래로는 또 사무변호사들이 길게 한 줄로 늘어앉아, 발치 깔개 위에 서류 뭉치를 쌓아두고 있었습니다. 그리고 가발과 법복 차림의 법정변호사들이 있었는데, 깨어 있는 이도 있고 잠든 이도 있었으며, 한 사람은 말을 하고 있었지만 그가 하는 말에 별로 귀를 기울이는 사람이 없었습니다. 대법원장은 더없이 편안한 의자에 등을 기대고, 쿠션 댄 팔걸이에 팔꿈치를 올린 채 이마를 손에 받치고 있었습니다. 그 자리에 있는 사람들 가운데 더러는 졸고, 더러는 신문을 읽고, 더러는 이리저리 거닐거나 무리 지어 소곤거렸습니다. 모두가 더없이 느긋해 보였고, 조금도 서두르는 기색 없이, 너무도 태평하고, 지극히 안락했습니다."]], [["To see everything going on so smoothly and to think of the roughness of the suitors’ lives and deaths; to see all that full dress and ceremony and to think of the waste, and want, and beggared misery it represented; to consider that while the sickness of hope deferred was raging in so many hearts this polite show went calmly on from day to day, and year to year, in such good order and composure; to behold the Lord Chancellor and the whole array of practitioners under him looking at one another and at the spectators as if nobody had ever heard that all over England the name in which they were assembled was a bitter jest, was held in universal horror, contempt, and indignation, was known for something so flagrant and bad that little short of a miracle could bring any good out of it to any one—this was so curious and self-contradictory to me, who had no experience of it, that it was at first incredible, and I could not comprehend it.", "모든 일이 그토록 매끄럽게 굴러가는 것을 보면서 소송 당사자들의 거친 삶과 죽음을 떠올리는 일, 그 화려한 정장과 격식을 다 보면서 그것이 표상하는 낭비와 궁핍과 거지꼴이 된 비참을 떠올리는 일, 그토록 많은 가슴속에서 미뤄진 희망의 병이 사납게 들끓는 동안에도 이 점잖은 구경거리가 날마다, 해마다 그토록 질서정연하고 침착하게 태연히 굴러간다는 것을 헤아리는 일, 대법원장과 그 아래 늘어선 모든 법조인이 서로를, 또 방청객들을 바라보면서, 마치 그들이 모여든 그 이름이 잉글랜드 방방곡곡에서 쓰디쓴 농담거리가 되었고, 누구나 진저리치며 경멸하고 분개하는 대상이 되었으며, 너무도 노골적이고 악독해서 거기서 누군가에게 좋은 것이 나오려면 기적이라도 일어나야 할 무언가로 알려져 있다는 것을 일찍이 들어본 적도 없다는 듯 굴고 있는 것을 바라보는 일—이 모든 것이, 그런 경험이라곤 없던 나에게는 너무도 기이하고 자기모순적이어서, 처음에는 도무지 믿기지 않았고, 나는 그것을 이해할 수가 없었습니다. 나는 리처드가 앉혀준 자리에 앉아 들으려고 애쓰며 주위를 둘러보았습니다."], ["I sat where Richard put me, and tried to listen, and looked about me; but there seemed to be no reality in the whole scene except poor little Miss Flite, the madwoman, standing on a bench and nodding at it.", "하지만 그 광경 전체에서 현실감이라곤 어디에도 없는 듯했고, 다만 가엾고 자그마한 플라이트 양만은 예외였습니다. 그 미친 노부인은 긴 의자 위에 올라서서 법정을 향해 고개를 끄덕이고 있었습니다."]], [["Miss Flite soon espied us and came to where we sat.", "플라이트 양은 곧 우리를 알아보고 우리가 앉은 곳으로 다가왔습니다."], ["She gave me a gracious welcome to her domain and indicated, with much gratification and pride, its principal attractions. Mr.", "그녀는 자기 영토에 온 나를 정중히 환영하고는, 한껏 흐뭇해하며 자랑스럽게 그곳의 주요 볼거리를 가리켜 보였습니다."], ["Kenge also came to speak to us and did the honours of the place in much the same way, with the bland modesty of a proprietor.", "켄지 씨도 우리에게 말을 건네러 와서, 거의 비슷한 방식으로, 주인다운 부드러운 겸손함을 풍기며 이곳을 안내해주었습니다. 방문하기에 그리 좋은 날은 아니라고 그가 말했습니다."], ["It was not a very good day for a visit, he said; he would have preferred the first day of term; but it was imposing, it was imposing.", "개정 첫날이었으면 더 좋았을 거라고요. 하지만 그래도 위풍당당하다, 위풍당당하다고 했습니다."]], [["When we had been there half an hour or so, the case in progress—if I may use a phrase so ridiculous in such a connexion—seemed to die out of its own vapidity, without coming, or being by anybody expected to come, to any result.", "우리가 거기 머문 지 반 시간쯤 지나자, 진행 중이던 사건은—이런 자리에 어울리지도 않는 이 우스꽝스러운 표현을 써도 된다면—아무 결론에도 이르지 않은 채, 누구도 결론에 이르리라 기대하지도 않은 채, 그저 제풀에 김이 빠져 시들어 죽어버리는 듯했습니다."], ["The Lord Chancellor then threw down a bundle of papers from his desk to the gentlemen below him, and somebody said, “Jarndyce and Jarndyce.”", "그러자 대법원장이 자기 책상에서 서류 뭉치 하나를 아래쪽 신사들에게 던졌고, 누군가가 \"잔다이스 대 잔다이스\" 하고 말했습니다."], ["Upon this there was a buzz, and a laugh, and a general withdrawal of the bystanders, and a bringing in of great heaps, and piles, and bags and bags full of papers.", "그러자 웅성거림과 웃음소리가 일고, 구경꾼들이 우르르 빠져나갔으며, 산더미 같은, 첩첩이 쌓인, 자루마다 그득한 서류들이 들어왔습니다."]], [["I think it came on “for further directions”—about some bill of costs, to the best of my understanding, which was confused enough.", "내가 이해한 바로는—이것 또한 어지간히 뒤죽박죽이었지만—어떤 소송 비용 명세서에 관해 \"추가 지시를 받기 위해\" 사건이 거론된 듯했습니다."], ["But I counted twenty-three gentlemen in wigs who said they were “in it,” and none of them appeared to understand it much better than I.", "그런데 자기가 \"이 사건에 관여하고 있다\"고 말하는 가발 쓴 신사를 세어보니 스물세 명이었고, 그중 누구도 나보다 이 일을 더 잘 이해하는 것 같지 않았습니다."], ["They chatted about it with the Lord Chancellor, and contradicted and explained among themselves, and some of them said it was this way, and some of them said it was that way, and some of them jocosely proposed to read huge volumes of affidavits, and there was more buzzing and laughing, and everybody concerned was in a state of idle entertainment, and nothing could be made of it by anybody. After an hour or so of this, and a good many speeches being begun and cut short, it was “referred back for the present,”", "그들은 대법원장과 그 일을 두고 잡담을 나누고, 자기들끼리 서로 반박하고 설명하며, 더러는 이쪽이라 하고, 더러는 저쪽이라 했으며, 더러는 진술서를 산더미처럼 읽어보자고 익살스레 제안했고, 또 웅성거림과 웃음소리가 일었으며, 관련된 모든 사람이 한가한 여흥을 즐기는 상태였고, 누구도 그 일로 아무것도 매듭짓지 못했습니다."], ["as Mr. Kenge said, and the papers were bundled up again before the clerks had finished bringing them in.", "한 시간쯤 이러고, 여러 발언이 시작되었다가 도중에 끊긴 끝에, 켄지 씨의 말마따나 그것은 \"당분간 다시 회부\"되었고, 서기들이 미처 서류를 다 들여오기도 전에 서류는 다시 묶여버렸습니다."]], [["I glanced at Richard on the termination of these hopeless proceedings and was shocked to see the worn look of his handsome young face.", "이 가망 없는 절차가 끝났을 때 나는 리처드를 흘끗 보았다가, 그 잘생기고 젊은 얼굴에 떠오른 지친 기색을 보고 충격을 받았습니다."], ["“It can’t last for ever, Dame Durden.", "\"이게 영원히 갈 리야 없겠지, 더든 아주머니."], ["Better luck next time!”", "다음번엔 운이 더 좋을 거야!\""], ["was all he said.", "그가 한 말은 그게 다였습니다."]], [["I had seen Mr. Guppy bringing in papers and arranging them for Mr.", "나는 거피 씨가 켄지 씨를 위해 서류를 들여와 정리하는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Kenge; and he had seen me and made me a forlorn bow, which rendered me desirous to get out of the court.", "그도 나를 보고는 처량한 인사를 꾸벅했는데, 그 바람에 나는 어서 법정 밖으로 나가고 싶어졌습니다."], ["Richard had given me his arm and was taking me away when Mr. Guppy came up.", "리처드가 나에게 팔을 내주고 나를 데리고 나가려는데 거피 씨가 다가왔습니다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr.", "\"실례합니다, 카스턴 씨.\""], ["Carstone,”", "그가 속삭이듯 말했습니다."], ["said he in a whisper, “and Miss Summerson’s also, but there’s a lady here, a friend of mine, who knows her and wishes to have the pleasure of shaking hands.”", "\"그리고 서머슨 양께도요. 여기 제 친구인 어떤 부인이 계신데, 서머슨 양을 아는 사이라서 악수의 영광을 누리고 싶어 하십니다.\""], ["As he spoke, I saw before me, as if she had started into bodily shape from my remembrance, Mrs.", "그가 그렇게 말하는 사이, 내 앞에 마치 내 기억 속에서 불쑥 몸을 갖추고 튀어나온 듯한 사람이 보였습니다."], ["Rachael of my godmother’s house.", "대모님 댁에 있던 레이철 부인이었습니다."]], [["“How do you do, Esther?”", "\"잘 지냈니, 에스더?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했습니다."], ["“Do you recollect me?”", "\"나를 알아보겠니?\""]], [["I gave her my hand and told her yes and that she was very little altered.", "나는 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 알아본다고, 그녀가 거의 변하지 않았다고 말했습니다."]], [["“I wonder you remember those times, Esther,”", "\"네가 그 시절을 기억하다니 신기하구나, 에스더.\""], ["she returned with her old asperity.", "그녀가 예전 그대로의 모진 말투로 대꾸했습니다."], ["“They are changed now.", "\"이젠 다 달라졌지."], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["I am glad to see you, and glad you are not too proud to know me.”", "너를 보니 반갑고, 네가 너무 거만해져서 나를 모른 체하지 않으니 그것도 반갑구나.\""], ["But indeed she seemed disappointed that I was not.", "하지만 사실 그녀는 내가 거만하지 않다는 걸 오히려 실망스러워하는 눈치였습니다."]], [["“Proud, Mrs. Rachael!”", "\"거만하다니요, 레이철 부인!\""], ["I remonstrated.", "내가 항변했습니다."]], [["“I am married, Esther,”", "\"나는 결혼했단다, 에스더.\""], ["she returned, coldly correcting me, “and am Mrs.", "그녀가 쌀쌀맞게 나를 바로잡으며 대꾸했습니다."], ["Chadband. Well!", "\"이제 채드밴드 부인이야."], ["I wish you good day, and I hope you’ll do well.”", "그래! 그럼 잘 있거라, 부디 잘 지내기를 바란다.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy, who had been attentive to this short dialogue, heaved a sigh in my ear and elbowed his own and Mrs.", "이 짧은 대화에 귀를 기울이고 있던 거피 씨가 내 귓가에 한숨을 한 번 내쉬더니, 우리가 한복판에 끼어 있던, 들고 나는 사람들의 어수선한 작은 무리—사건이 바뀌면서 한데 모인 무리—를 팔꿈치로 헤치며 자기와 레이철 부인의 길을 텄습니다."], ["Rachael’s way through the confused little crowd of people coming in and going out, which we were in the midst of and which the change in the business had brought together. Richard and I were making our way through it, and I was yet in the first chill of the late unexpected recognition when I saw, coming towards us, but not seeing us, no less a person than Mr.", "리처드와 나는 그 사이를 비집고 나아가는 중이었고, 나는 방금 뜻밖에 누군가를 알아본 첫 한기에서 채 벗어나지도 못한 참이었는데, 우리 쪽으로 다가오는—다만 우리를 보지는 못한 채로—사람을 보았으니, 다름 아닌 조지 씨였습니다."], ["George. He made nothing of the people about him as he tramped on, staring over their heads into the body of the court.", "그는 사람들 머리 너머로 법정 안쪽을 노려보며 성큼성큼 걸어오느라 주위 사람들에는 아랑곳도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["“George!”", "\"조지!\""], ["said Richard as I called his attention to him.", "내가 그를 가리키자 리처드가 불렀습니다."]], [["“You are well met, sir,”", "\"잘 만났습니다, 카스턴 씨.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“And you, miss.", "\"아가씨도요."], ["Could you point a person out for me, I want?", "제가 찾는 사람을 좀 짚어주실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I don’t understand these places.”", "저는 이런 데가 도무지 익숙지가 않아서요.\""]], [["Turning as he spoke and making an easy way for us, he stopped when we were out of the press in a corner behind a great red curtain.", "그렇게 말하며 몸을 돌려 우리에게 길을 터주던 그는, 우리가 인파에서 빠져나와 커다란 붉은 휘장 뒤 한구석에 이르자 걸음을 멈췄습니다."]], [["“There’s a little cracked old woman,”", "\"머리가 좀 돈 자그마한 노부인이 하나 있는데요,\""], ["he began, “that—”", "그가 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"그분이—\""]], [["I put up my finger, for Miss Flite was close by me, having kept beside me all the time and having called the attention of several of her legal acquaintance to me (as I had overheard to my confusion) by whispering in their ears, “Hush!", "나는 손가락을 세워 보였습니다. 플라이트 양이 줄곧 내 곁을 떠나지 않고 내내 붙어 있다가, (내가 듣고 당황했듯이) 자기와 안면 있는 법조인 몇몇의 귀에 대고 \"쉿!"], ["Fitz Jarndyce on my left!”", "내 왼쪽에 잔다이스 후예예요!\" 하고 속삭이며 그들에게 나를 가리켜 보이고 있었기 때문입니다."]], [["“Hem!”", "\"흠!\""], ["said Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“You remember, miss, that we passed some conversation on a certain man this morning?", "\"아가씨, 오늘 아침에 우리가 어떤 사람에 관해 얘기를 좀 나눈 거 기억하시죠?"], ["Gridley,”", "그리들리요.\""], ["in a low whisper behind his hand.", "손으로 입을 가리고 낮게 속삭였습니다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“He is hiding at my place.", "\"그가 제 집에 숨어 있습니다."], ["I couldn’t mention it.", "그땐 말씀드릴 수가 없었어요."], ["Hadn’t his authority.", "그의 허락을 못 받았거든요."], ["He is on his last march, miss, and has a whim to see her.", "그가 마지막 행군 길에 들어섰는데, 아가씨, 저 부인을 보고 싶다고 합니다."], ["He says they can feel for one another, and she has been almost as good as a friend to him here.", "둘이 서로 마음을 헤아려줄 수 있다더군요. 저 부인이 여기서 그에게 거의 친구나 다름없이 잘해주었답니다."], ["I came down to look for her, for when I sat by Gridley this afternoon, I seemed to hear the roll of the muffled drums.”", "제가 저 부인을 찾으러 내려온 겁니다. 오늘 오후에 그리들리 곁에 앉아 있자니, 천에 싸인 북소리가 울리는 것만 같았거든요.\""]], [["“Shall I tell her?”", "\"제가 부인께 말씀드릴까요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Would you be so good?”", "\"그래 주시겠습니까?\""], ["he returned with a glance of something like apprehension at Miss Flite.", "그가 플라이트 양 쪽을 보며 무언가 겁먹은 듯한 눈길로 대답했습니다."], ["“It’s a providence I met you, miss; I doubt if I should have known how to get on with that lady.”", "\"아가씨를 만난 게 천운입니다. 저 부인을 어떻게 상대해야 할지 도무지 몰랐을 텐데요.\""], ["And he put one hand in his breast and stood upright in a martial attitude as I informed little Miss Flite, in her ear, of the purport of his kind errand.", "그러고는 한 손을 가슴에 얹고 군인다운 자세로 꼿꼿이 섰습니다. 나는 자그마한 플라이트 양의 귀에 대고 그의 친절한 심부름의 취지를 일러주었습니다."]], [["“My angry friend from Shropshire!", "\"슈롭셔에서 온 내 성난 친구!"], ["Almost as celebrated as myself!”", "나만큼이나 유명한 사람이지!\""], ["she exclaimed.", "그녀가 외쳤습니다."], ["“Now really!", "\"어머, 정말이지!"], ["My dear, I will wait upon him with the greatest pleasure.”", "얘야, 더없이 기쁜 마음으로 그분을 찾아뵙겠어.\""]], [["“He is living concealed at Mr.", "\"그분은 조지 씨 댁에 몸을 숨기고 사세요.\""], ["George’s,” said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["This is Mr. George.”", "이분이 조지 씨예요.\""]], [["“In—deed!”", "\"어머나! 정말이지!\""], ["returned Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Very proud to have the honour!", "\"이런 영광을 누리다니 무척 자랑스럽군요!"], ["A military man, my dear.", "군인이란다, 얘야."], ["You know, a perfect general!”", "그러니까, 완벽한 장군이지!\""], ["she whispered to me.", "그녀가 나에게 속삭였습니다."]], [["Poor Miss Flite deemed it necessary to be so courtly and polite, as a mark of her respect for the army, and to curtsy so very often that it was no easy matter to get her out of the court.", "가엾은 플라이트 양은 군대에 대한 존경의 표시로 이토록 깍듯하고 정중해야 한다고 여겼고, 또 얼마나 자주 무릎 굽혀 인사를 하던지 그녀를 법정 밖으로 데리고 나오는 게 여간 어려운 일이 아니었습니다."], ["When this was at last done, and addressing Mr. George as “General,”", "가까스로 그 일이 끝나고, 조지 씨를 \"장군님\"이라 부르며 그에게 팔을 맡기자, 구경하던 몇몇 한량이 무척 재미있어했습니다."], ["she gave him her arm, to the great entertainment of some idlers who were looking on, he was so discomposed and begged me so respectfully “not to desert him”", "그는 어찌나 어쩔 줄 몰라 하던지, 나에게 \"제발 저를 버리지 말아 달라\"고 어찌나 공손히 청하던지, 나는 차마 그러지 못하겠다는 마음이 들었습니다."], ["that I could not make up my mind to do it, especially as Miss Flite was always tractable with me and as she too said, “Fitz Jarndyce, my dear, you will accompany us, of course.”", "더구나 플라이트 양은 나에게는 늘 고분고분했고, 그녀 또한 \"잔다이스 후예야, 얘야, 너도 당연히 우리와 함께 가야지\" 하고 말했으니까요."], ["As Richard seemed quite willing, and even anxious, that we should see them safely to their destination, we agreed to do so. And as Mr.", "리처드도 우리가 그들을 목적지까지 안전하게 데려다주는 일을 기꺼이, 아니 오히려 바라기까지 하는 눈치여서, 우리는 그러기로 했습니다."], ["George informed us that Gridley’s mind had run on Mr. Jarndyce all the afternoon after hearing of their interview in the morning, I wrote a hasty note in pencil to my guardian to say where we were gone and why.", "그리고 조지 씨가 알려준 바로는, 그리들리가 오늘 아침 두 사람의 면담 이야기를 들은 뒤 오후 내내 잔다이스 씨를 마음에 두고 있었다고 하기에, 나는 우리가 어디로 가는지, 왜 가는지를 연필로 후견인께 급히 적어 보냈습니다."], ["Mr. George sealed it at a coffee-house, that it might lead to no discovery, and we sent it off by a ticket-porter.", "조지 씨는 그게 무슨 단서가 되지 않도록 어느 커피점에서 봉인했고, 우리는 그것을 일당 짐꾼 편에 부쳤습니다."]], [["We then took a hackney-coach and drove away to the neighbourhood of Leicester Square.", "그런 다음 우리는 삯마차를 잡아타고 레스터 광장 근처로 달려갔습니다."], ["We walked through some narrow courts, for which Mr. George apologized, and soon came to the shooting gallery, the door of which was closed.", "우리는 비좁은 골목 몇 군데를 지나갔는데, 조지 씨가 그 골목에 대해 미안해했고, 이윽고 사격장에 이르렀습니다."], ["As he pulled a bell-handle which hung by a chain to the door-post, a very respectable old gentleman with grey hair, wearing spectacles, and dressed in a black spencer and gaiters and a broad-brimmed hat, and carrying a large gold-beaded cane, addressed him.", "문은 닫혀 있었습니다. 그가 문기둥에 사슬로 매달린 종 손잡이를 잡아당기자, 백발에 안경을 쓰고, 검은 모직 짧은 외투에 각반을 차고, 챙 넓은 모자를 쓴, 큼직한 황금 머리 지팡이를 든 매우 점잖은 노신사가 그에게 말을 걸었습니다."]], [["“I ask your pardon, my good friend,”", "\"실례하오, 친구.\""], ["said he, “but is this George’s Shooting Gallery?”", "그가 말했습니다. \"여기가 조지 사격장이오?\""]], [["“It is, sir,” returned Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\""], ["George, glancing up at the great letters in which that inscription was painted on the whitewashed wall.", "조지 씨가 회칠한 벽에 그 글귀가 적힌 큼직한 글자들을 올려다보며 대답했습니다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["To be sure!”", "그렇군요!\""], ["said the old gentleman, following his eyes.", "노신사가 그의 눈길을 따라가며 말했습니다."], ["“Thank you.", "\"고맙소."], ["Have you rung the bell?”", "종을 울리셨소?\""]], [["“My name is George, sir, and I have rung the bell.”", "\"제 이름이 조지입니다, 어르신. 그리고 종은 제가 울렸습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed?”", "\"오, 그래요?\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했습니다."], ["“Your name is George?", "\"당신 이름이 조지라고?"], ["Then I am here as soon as you, you see.", "그렇다면 보다시피 내가 당신만큼 빨리 온 셈이군."], ["You came for me, no doubt?”", "분명 당신이 나를 부르러 왔겠지요?\""]], [["“No, sir.", "\"아닙니다, 어르신."], ["You have the advantage of me.”", "무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed?”", "\"오, 그래요?\""], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했습니다."], ["“Then it was your young man who came for me.", "\"그렇다면 당신의 젊은이가 나를 부르러 온 게로군."], ["I am a physician and was requested—five minutes ago—to come and visit a sick man at George’s Shooting Gallery.”", "나는 의사인데, 오 분 전에 조지 사격장으로 와서 아픈 사람을 봐달라는 청을 받았소.\""]], [["“The muffled drums,” said Mr.", "\"천에 싸인 북소리로군요.\""], ["George, turning to Richard and me and gravely shaking his head.", "조지 씨가 리처드와 나를 돌아보며 무겁게 고개를 가로젓고 말했습니다."], ["“It’s quite correct, sir.", "\"맞습니다, 어르신."], ["Will you please to walk in.”", "안으로 드시지요.\""]], [["The door being at that moment opened by a very singular-looking little man in a green-baize cap and apron, whose face and hands and dress were blackened all over, we passed along a dreary passage into a large building with bare brick walls where there were targets, and guns, and swords, and other things of that kind.", "그 순간 초록색 거친 모직 모자에 앞치마를 두른, 얼굴과 손과 옷이 온통 새카맣게 그을린, 아주 별스러운 외모의 자그마한 사내가 문을 열어주었습니다. 우리는 음산한 복도를 지나, 벽돌이 그대로 드러난 커다란 건물 안으로 들어갔는데, 거기에는 표적과 총과 칼과 그런 종류의 물건들이 있었습니다."], ["When we had all arrived here, the physician stopped, and taking off his hat, appeared to vanish by magic and to leave another and quite a different man in his place.", "우리가 모두 여기 다다르자 의사가 걸음을 멈추고 모자를 벗었는데, 그러자 그는 마치 마법처럼 사라지고 그 자리에 전혀 다른 사람이 들어선 듯했습니다."]], [["“Now lookee here, George,”", "\"자, 여기 좀 보게, 조지.\""], ["said the man, turning quickly round upon him and tapping him on the breast with a large forefinger.", "그 사내가 그를 향해 휙 돌아서며 굵은 집게손가락으로 그의 가슴을 톡톡 두드리고 말했습니다."], ["“You know me, and I know you.", "\"자네는 나를 알고, 나도 자네를 알지."], ["You’re a man of the world, and I’m a man of the world.", "자네는 세상 물정 아는 사람이고, 나도 세상 물정 아는 사람이야."], ["My name’s Bucket, as you are aware, and I have got a peace-warrant against Gridley.", "자네도 알다시피 내 이름은 버킷이고, 나는 그리들리에 대한 평화유지 영장을 가지고 있네."], ["You have kept him out of the way a long time, and you have been artful in it, and it does you credit.”", "자네는 그를 오랫동안 빼돌려놓았고, 그 솜씨가 제법 능청스러웠어. 칭찬받을 만하지.\""]], [["Mr. George, looking hard at him, bit his lip and shook his head.", "조지 씨는 그를 매섭게 노려보며 입술을 깨물고 고개를 가로저었습니다."]], [["“Now, George,”", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["said the other, keeping close to him, “you’re a sensible man and a well-conducted man; that’s what YOU are, beyond a doubt.", "상대가 그에게 바싹 붙어서 말했습니다. \"자네는 분별 있는 사람이고, 처신이 반듯한 사람이지."], ["And mind you, I don’t talk to you as a common character, because you have served your country and you know that when duty calls we must obey.", "의심할 여지 없이 자네가 바로 그런 사람이야. 그리고 이건 알아두게."], ["Consequently you’re very far from wanting to give trouble.", "내가 자네를 흔해빠진 작자 대하듯 말하는 게 아니라네. 자네는 나라에 봉사한 사람이고, 의무가 부르면 우리가 따라야 한다는 걸 아는 사람이니까."], ["If I required assistance, you’d assist me; that’s what YOU’D do.", "그러니 자네가 일을 어렵게 만들 생각이 없다는 건 명백하지. 내가 도움이 필요하면 자네가 나를 도울 거야."], ["Phil Squod, don’t you go a-sidling round the gallery like that”—the dirty little man was shuffling about with his shoulder against the wall, and his eyes on the intruder, in a manner that looked threatening—“because I know you and won’t have it.”", "그게 바로 자네라는 사람이지. 필 스쿼드, 그런 식으로 사격장을 슬금슬금 돌아다니지 마라\"—그 꾀죄죄한 자그마한 사내가 어깨를 벽에 붙인 채, 눈은 침입자에게 둔 채로 위협적으로 보이는 태도로 발을 끌며 돌아다니고 있었습니다—\"내가 너를 잘 아니까, 그런 짓은 못 봐주겠다.\""]], [["“Phil!”", "\"필!\""], ["said Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, guv’ner.”", "\"네, 주인님.\""]], [["“Be quiet.”", "\"가만있어.\""]], [["The little man, with a low growl, stood still.", "그 자그마한 사내는 나직이 으르렁대며 그 자리에 멈춰 섰습니다."]], [["“Ladies and gentlemen,”", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분,\" 버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mr.", "\"이 일에서 거슬리게 보일 만한 게 있더라도 양해해주시오."], ["Bucket, “you’ll excuse anything that may appear to be disagreeable in this, for my name’s Inspector Bucket of the Detective, and I have a duty to perform.", "내 이름은 형사부의 버킷 경위이고, 나는 수행할 임무가 있소이다. 조지, 나는 내 사람이 어디 있는지 안다네."], ["George, I know where my man is because I was on the roof last night and saw him through the skylight, and you along with him.", "어젯밤 지붕 위에 올라가 채광창으로 그를 봤거든. 자네가 함께 있는 것도 봤고."], ["He is in there, you know,”", "그는 저 안에 있어, 알겠나,\" 손으로 가리키며 말했습니다."], ["pointing; “that’s where HE is—on a sofy.", "\"바로 저기 있지. 긴 의자 위에 말이야."], ["Now I must see my man, and I must tell my man to consider himself in custody; but you know me, and you know I don’t want to take any uncomfortable measures.", "자, 나는 내 사람을 봐야 하고, 내 사람한테 스스로 구금된 몸이라 여기라고 일러줘야 하네. 하지만 자네는 나를 알지."], ["You give me your word, as from one man to another (and an old soldier, mind you, likewise), that it’s honourable between us two, and I’ll accommodate you to the utmost of my power.”", "내가 거북한 수를 쓰고 싶어 하지 않는다는 걸 알 거야. 자네가 나한테 사내 대 사내로서(게다가 명심하게, 자네는 노병이기도 하지) 우리 둘 사이가 떳떳하다고 약속해준다면, 나도 내 힘닿는 데까지 자네 편의를 봐주겠네.\""]], [["“I give it,”", "\"약속하겠소.\""], ["was the reply.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“But it wasn’t handsome in you, Mr. Bucket.”", "\"하지만 당신은 점잖지 못했소, 버킷 씨.\""]], [["“Gammon, George!", "\"허튼소리, 조지!"], ["Not handsome?” said Mr.", "점잖지 못했다고?\""], ["Bucket, tapping him on his broad breast again and shaking hands with him.", "버킷 씨가 다시 그의 넓은 가슴을 톡톡 두드리고 악수를 나누며 말했습니다."], ["“I don’t say it wasn’t handsome in you to keep my man so close, do I?", "\"내가 자네더러, 내 사람을 그렇게 꽁꽁 숨겨둔 게 점잖지 못했다고 했나?"], ["Be equally good-tempered to me, old boy!", "나한테도 똑같이 너그럽게 굴게, 친구!"], ["Old William Tell, Old Shaw, the Life Guardsman!", "우리 윌리엄 텔 영감, 쇼 영감, 근위 기병 같으니라고!"], ["Why, he’s a model of the whole British army in himself, ladies and gentlemen.", "아 글쎄, 이 사람 하나가 영국 군대 전체의 본보기라니까요, 신사 숙녀 여러분."], ["I’d give a fifty-pun’ note to be such a figure of a man!”", "이런 사내대장부 풍채라면 나는 오십 파운드 지폐라도 내놓겠소!\""]], [["The affair being brought to this head, Mr.", "일이 이렇게 마무리되자, 조지 씨는 잠시 생각하더니, 자기가 먼저 들어가서 자기 전우(그는 그렇게 불렀습니다)를 만나되 플라이트 양을 데리고 가겠다고 제안했습니다."], ["George, after a little consideration, proposed to go in first to his comrade (as he called him), taking Miss Flite with him. Mr.", "버킷 씨가 동의하자, 그들은 사격장 저 안쪽 끝으로 가버렸고, 우리는 총이 잔뜩 놓인 탁자 옆에 앉거나 선 채로 남았습니다."], ["Bucket agreeing, they went away to the further end of the gallery, leaving us sitting and standing by a table covered with guns.", "버킷 씨는 이 틈을 타 가벼운 잡담을 나누었습니다."], ["Mr. Bucket took this opportunity of entering into a little light conversation, asking me if I were afraid of fire-arms, as most young ladies were; asking Richard if he were a good shot; asking Phil Squod which he considered the best of those rifles and what it might be worth first-hand, telling him in return that it was a pity he ever gave way to his temper, for he was naturally so amiable that he might have been a young woman, and making himself generally agreeable.", "나에게는 대개의 젊은 아가씨들처럼 총기가 무섭지 않으냐 물었고, 리처드에게는 사격 솜씨가 좋으냐 물었으며, 필 스쿼드에게는 저 소총들 가운데 어느 게 제일 좋은 것 같으냐, 새것으로 사면 얼마쯤 나가겠느냐 물었고, 그 답례로, 너는 천성이 어찌나 상냥한지 여자로 태어났어도 좋았을 텐데 성질을 못 이겨 그 모양이 됐으니 안타깝다고 말하며, 두루 좋은 분위기를 만들었습니다."]], [["After a time he followed us to the further end of the gallery, and Richard and I were going quietly away when Mr. George came after us.", "얼마 뒤 그가 우리를 따라 사격장 저 안쪽 끝으로 왔고, 리처드와 내가 조용히 나가려는데 조지 씨가 우리를 뒤따라왔습니다."], ["He said that if we had no objection to see his comrade, he would take a visit from us very kindly.", "그는 우리가 자기 전우를 만나는 데 거리낌이 없다면, 우리가 와주는 걸 무척 고맙게 여기겠다고 말했습니다."], ["The words had hardly passed his lips when the bell was rung and my guardian appeared, “on the chance,”", "그 말이 그의 입에서 채 떨어지기도 전에 종이 울렸고 후견인이 나타났습니다. \"혹시나,\""], ["he slightly observed, “of being able to do any little thing for a poor fellow involved in the same misfortune as himself.”", "그가 무심한 듯 한마디 했습니다. \"자기와 똑같은 불운에 휘말린 가엾은 사람을 위해 사소한 거라도 도울 수 있을까 해서\""], ["We all four went back together and went into the place where Gridley was.", "왔다는 거였습니다. 우리 넷은 함께 되돌아가 그리들리가 있는 곳으로 들어갔습니다."]], [["It was a bare room, partitioned off from the gallery with unpainted wood.", "그곳은 칠하지 않은 나무로 사격장에서 칸막이를 친 텅 빈 방이었습니다."], ["As the screening was not more than eight or ten feet high and only enclosed the sides, not the top, the rafters of the high gallery roof were overhead, and the skylight through which Mr. Bucket had looked down.", "칸막이가 높이 여덟이나 열 자쯤밖에 안 되고 옆면만 두르고 위는 막지 않았기에, 높다란 사격장 지붕의 서까래가 머리 위로 드러나 있었고, 버킷 씨가 내려다본 그 채광창도 있었습니다."], ["The sun was low—near setting—and its light came redly in above, without descending to the ground.", "해가 낮게 걸려 곧 질 무렵이라, 햇빛이 위쪽으로 붉게 들어왔지만 바닥까지는 내려오지 않았습니다."], ["Upon a plain canvas-covered sofa lay the man from Shropshire, dressed much as we had seen him last, but so changed that at first I recognized no likeness in his colourless face to what I recollected.", "밋밋한 천을 씌운 긴 의자 위에 슈롭셔에서 온 그 사내가 누워 있었는데, 우리가 지난번 봤을 때와 거의 같은 옷차림이었으나 어찌나 변했던지, 처음에는 그 핏기 없는 얼굴에서 내가 기억하던 모습과 닮은 구석을 알아볼 수가 없었습니다."]], [["He had been still writing in his hiding-place, and still dwelling on his grievances, hour after hour.", "그는 그 은신처에서도 여전히 글을 쓰고 있었고, 시간이 흐르고 또 흘러도 여전히 자기 억울함을 곱씹고 있었습니다."], ["A table and some shelves were covered with manuscript papers and with worn pens and a medley of such tokens.", "탁자 하나와 선반 몇 개가 원고와 닳은 펜, 그리고 그런 잡다한 흔적들로 뒤덮여 있었습니다."], ["Touchingly and awfully drawn together, he and the little mad woman were side by side and, as it were, alone.", "가슴 아프게도, 또 두렵도록, 한데 모여, 그와 그 자그마한 미친 부인은 나란히, 말하자면 단둘이 있었습니다."], ["She sat on a chair holding his hand, and none of us went close to them.", "그녀는 의자에 앉아 그의 손을 잡고 있었고, 우리 중 누구도 그들 가까이 다가가지 않았습니다."]], [["His voice had faded, with the old expression of his face, with his strength, with his anger, with his resistance to the wrongs that had at last subdued him.", "그의 목소리는 사그라들어 있었습니다. 예전의 그 얼굴 표정과 함께, 그 힘과 함께, 그 분노와 함께, 마침내 그를 무릎 꿇린 그 부당함에 맞서던 저항과 함께 말입니다."], ["The faintest shadow of an object full of form and colour is such a picture of it as he was of the man from Shropshire whom we had spoken with before.", "형태와 색을 가득 머금은 어떤 사물의 가장 희미한 그림자—그것이 그 사물을 보여주는 그림이라고 한다면, 지금의 그는 우리가 전에 이야기를 나눈 그 슈롭셔 사내의 그런 희미한 그림자였습니다."]], [["He inclined his head to Richard and me and spoke to my guardian.", "그는 리처드와 나에게 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 후견인에게 말을 건넸습니다."]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce, it is very kind of you to come to see me.", "\"잔다이스 씨, 저를 보러 와주시다니 참 친절하십니다."], ["I am not long to be seen, I think.", "제가 보일 날도 이제 얼마 남지 않은 듯합니다."], ["I am very glad to take your hand, sir.", "어르신 손을 잡게 되어 무척 기쁩니다."], ["You are a good man, superior to injustice, and God knows I honour you.”", "어르신은 좋은 분이고, 부당함보다 높이 계신 분이니, 하느님은 아시겠지만 저는 어르신을 존경합니다.\""]], [["They shook hands earnestly, and my guardian said some words of comfort to him.", "그들은 진심을 담아 악수를 나누었고, 후견인은 그에게 위로의 말을 몇 마디 건넸습니다."]], [["“It may seem strange to you, sir,” returned Gridley; “I should not have liked to see you if this had been the first time of our meeting. But you know I made a fight for it, you know I stood up with my single hand against them all, you know I told them the truth to the last, and told them what they were, and what they had done to me; so I don’t mind your seeing me, this wreck.”", "\"어르신께는 이상하게 들릴지도 모르겠습니다.\" 그리들리가 대꾸했습니다. \"만일 이번이 우리가 처음 만나는 자리였다면, 저는 이런 모습을 어르신께 보이고 싶지 않았을 겁니다. 하지만 어르신도 아시다시피 저는 끝까지 싸웠습니다. 제 한 손으로 그들 전부에 맞서 일어섰지요. 끝까지 그들에게 진실을 말하고, 그들이 어떤 자들인지, 그들이 저에게 무슨 짓을 했는지 말해주었습니다. 그러니 이 폐허가 된 꼴을 어르신이 보셔도 저는 개의치 않습니다.\""]], [["“You have been courageous with them many and many a time,”", "\"당신은 그들에게 수없이, 수없이 용감하게 맞섰소.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대답했습니다."]], [["“Sir, I have been,”", "\"어르신, 그랬지요.\""], ["with a faint smile.", "그가 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“I told you what would come of it when I ceased to be so, and see here!", "\"제가 더는 그러지 못하게 되면 어떻게 될지 어르신께 말씀드렸지요. 그런데 보십시오!"], ["Look at us—look at us!”", "우리를 좀 보세요, 우리를!\""], ["He drew the hand Miss Flite held through her arm and brought her something nearer to him.", "그는 플라이트 양이 잡고 있던 손을 그녀의 팔에 꿰어 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 조금 더 끌어당겼습니다."]], [["“This ends it.", "\"이걸로 끝입니다."], ["Of all my old associations, of all my old pursuits and hopes, of all the living and the dead world, this one poor soul alone comes natural to me, and I am fit for.", "제 모든 옛 인연 가운데, 제 모든 옛 추구와 희망 가운데, 산 자와 죽은 자의 온 세상 가운데, 이 가엾은 영혼 하나만이 저에게 자연스럽게 다가오고, 또 제가 어울릴 만한 사람입니다."], ["There is a tie of many suffering years between us two, and it is the only tie I ever had on earth that Chancery has not broken.”", "우리 둘 사이에는 여러 해에 걸친 고난의 끈이 있고, 그것이야말로 대법원이 끊어놓지 못한, 제가 이 땅에서 가졌던 유일한 끈입니다.\""]], [["“Accept my blessing, Gridley,”", "\"내 축복을 받으세요, 그리들리.\""], ["said Miss Flite in tears.", "플라이트 양이 눈물을 흘리며 말했습니다."], ["“Accept my blessing!”", "\"내 축복을 받으세요!\""]], [["“I thought, boastfully, that they never could break my heart, Mr.", "\"저는 우쭐대며 생각했습니다. 그들이 결코 제 마음을 꺾지는 못하리라고요, 잔다이스 씨."], ["Jarndyce. I was resolved that they should not.", "그들이 그러지 못하게 하겠노라 굳게 다짐했지요."], ["I did believe that I could, and would, charge them with being the mockery they were until I died of some bodily disorder.", "저는 정말로 믿었습니다. 제가 무슨 몸의 병으로 죽을 때까지, 그들이 얼마나 우스꽝스러운 자들인지 끝까지 따져 물을 수 있고, 또 그러겠노라고요."], ["But I am worn out.", "하지만 저는 다 닳아버렸습니다."], ["How long I have been wearing out, I don’t know; I seemed to break down in an hour.", "얼마나 오래 닳아왔는지는 모르겠습니다. 한 시간 만에 무너져버린 것만 같으니까요."], ["I hope they may never come to hear of it.", "부디 그들이 이걸 듣게 되지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["I hope everybody here will lead them to believe that I died defying them, consistently and perseveringly, as I did through so many years.”", "여기 계신 모두가, 제가 그 숱한 세월 그래왔듯 한결같이, 끈질기게 그들에게 맞서다 죽었다고 그들이 믿게끔 해주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Here Mr. Bucket, who was sitting in a corner by the door, good-naturedly offered such consolation as he could administer.", "이때 문가 한구석에 앉아 있던 버킷 씨가 인심 좋게도, 자기가 해줄 수 있는 위로를 건넸습니다."]], [["“Come, come!”", "\"이보시오, 이보시오!\""], ["he said from his corner.", "그가 자기 구석에서 말했습니다."], ["“Don’t go on in that way, Mr. Gridley.", "\"그렇게 굴지 마시오, 그리들리 씨."], ["You are only a little low.", "그저 좀 기운이 빠진 것뿐이오."], ["We are all of us a little low sometimes. I am.", "우리 모두 때로는 좀 기운이 빠지기 마련이지."], ["Hold up, hold up!", "나도 그렇소."], ["You’ll lose your temper with the whole round of ’em, again and again; and I shall take you on a score of warrants yet, if I have luck.”", "기운 내요, 기운 내! 당신은 또 그자들 전부에게 거듭거듭 성을 낼 거고, 나는 운만 따라준다면 앞으로도 영장을 한 다발은 들고 당신을 잡으러 올 테니까.\""]], [["He only shook his head.", "그는 그저 고개를 가로저을 뿐이었습니다."]], [["“Don’t shake your head,”", "\"고개 젓지 마시오.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Nod it; that’s what I want to see you do.", "\"끄덕여요. 내가 보고 싶은 게 바로 그거요."], ["Why, Lord bless your soul, what times we have had together!", "아니, 맙소사, 우리가 함께 보낸 시절이 얼마나 대단했소!"], ["Haven’t I seen you in the Fleet over and over again for contempt?", "내가 당신을 법정모독죄로 플리트 감옥에서 보고 또 보지 않았소?"], ["Haven’t I come into court, twenty afternoons for no other purpose than to see you pin the Chancellor like a bull-dog?", "당신이 불도그처럼 대법원장에게 달려드는 걸 보겠다는 그 하나의 목적으로, 내가 법정에 스무 번이나 오후마다 나오지 않았소?"], ["Don’t you remember when you first began to threaten the lawyers, and the peace was sworn against you two or three times a week?", "당신이 처음 변호사들을 위협하기 시작했을 때, 일주일에 두세 번씩 당신을 상대로 평화유지 선서가 청구되던 일 기억 안 나오?"], ["Ask the little old lady there; she has been always present.", "저기 자그마한 노부인께 여쭤보시오. 늘 그 자리에 계셨으니까."], ["Hold up, Mr. Gridley, hold up, sir!”", "기운 내요, 그리들리 씨, 기운 내요, 양반!\""]], [["“What are you going to do about him?”", "\"이 사람을 어쩌실 작정입니까?\""], ["asked George in a low voice.", "조지가 낮은 목소리로 물었습니다."]], [["“I don’t know yet,”", "\"아직 모르겠네.\""], ["said Bucket in the same tone.", "버킷이 같은 어조로 말했습니다."], ["Then resuming his encouragement, he pursued aloud: “Worn out, Mr.", "그러고는 다시 격려를 이어, 큰 소리로 말했습니다. \"다 닳았다고요, 그리들리 씨?"], ["Gridley? After dodging me for all these weeks and forcing me to climb the roof here like a tom cat and to come to see you as a doctor?", "이 몇 주 내내 나를 따돌리고, 나로 하여금 이 지붕을 수고양이처럼 기어오르게 만들고, 의사인 척하고 당신을 보러 오게 만들어놓고서?"], ["That ain’t like being worn out.", "그게 어디 다 닳은 사람 같소. 천만에!"], ["I should think not! Now I tell you what you want.", "자, 내가 당신한테 필요한 게 뭔지 말해주리다."], ["You want excitement, you know, to keep YOU up; that’s what YOU want.", "당신은 자극이 필요해, 알겠소, 기운을 차리려면 말이오. 그게 바로 당신한테 필요한 거요."], ["You’re used to it, and you can’t do without it.", "당신은 거기 길든 사람이라, 그게 없으면 못 견디는 거지."], ["I couldn’t myself.", "나라도 그럴 거요."], ["Very well, then; here’s this warrant got by Mr. Tulkinghorn of Lincoln’s Inn Fields, and backed into half-a-dozen counties since.", "자, 그렇다면 여기 링컨스인필즈의 토킹혼 씨가 받아낸 이 영장이 있고, 그 뒤로 대여섯 개 주에 효력이 미치게 되었소."], ["What do you say to coming along with me, upon this warrant, and having a good angry argument before the magistrates?", "이 영장으로 나와 함께 가서, 치안판사 앞에서 한바탕 성난 입씨름을 벌이는 게 어떻겠소?"], ["It’ll do you good; it’ll freshen you up and get you into training for another turn at the Chancellor.", "그게 당신한테 약이 될 거요. 당신을 생생하게 해주고, 대법원장과 또 한판 붙을 훈련을 시켜줄 거란 말이오."], ["Give in?", "항복한다고?"], ["Why, I am surprised to hear a man of your energy talk of giving in.", "아니, 당신처럼 기운찬 사람이 항복을 입에 올리다니 놀랍구려."], ["You mustn’t do that.", "그러면 안 되지."], ["You’re half the fun of the fair in the Court of Chancery.", "당신은 대법정에서 벌어지는 구경거리의 절반은 차지하는 사람이오."], ["George, you lend Mr. Gridley a hand, and let’s see now whether he won’t be better up than down.”", "조지, 자네가 그리들리 씨를 좀 부축하게, 어디 이분이 누워 있는 것보다 일어나 있는 게 낫지 않은지 봅시다.\""]], [["“He is very weak,”", "\"이 사람은 무척 약해졌습니다.\""], ["said the trooper in a low voice.", "기병이 낮은 목소리로 말했습니다."]], [["“Is he?”", "\"그런가?\""], ["returned Bucket anxiously.", "버킷이 근심스레 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I only want to rouse him.", "\"나는 그저 이 사람 기운을 돋우려는 것뿐이네."], ["I don’t like to see an old acquaintance giving in like this.", "옛 지인이 이렇게 손드는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않아서야."], ["It would cheer him up more than anything if I could make him a little waxy with me.", "나한테 좀 발끈하게 만들 수만 있다면, 그게 무엇보다 이 사람 기운을 북돋울 텐데."], ["He’s welcome to drop into me, right and left, if he likes.", "좋다면 나한테 닥치는 대로 한 방씩 날려도 좋소."], ["I shall never take advantage of it.”", "절대 그걸 빌미 삼지 않을 테니.\""]], [["The roof rang with a scream from Miss Flite, which still rings in my ears.", "플라이트 양의 비명이 지붕에 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸고, 그 소리는 지금도 내 귓가에 울립니다."]], [["“Oh, no, Gridley!”", "\"오, 안 돼요, 그리들리!\""], ["she cried as he fell heavily and calmly back from before her.", "그가 그녀 앞에서 무겁게, 또 고요히 뒤로 쓰러지자 그녀가 울부짖었습니다."], ["“Not without my blessing.", "\"내 축복도 받지 않고는 안 돼요."], ["After so many years!”", "그 숱한 세월이 지났는데!\""]], [["The sun was down, the light had gradually stolen from the roof, and the shadow had crept upward.", "해가 졌고, 빛은 지붕에서 차츰 빠져나갔으며, 그림자는 위쪽으로 슬금슬금 기어올라 갔습니다."], ["But to me the shadow of that pair, one living and one dead, fell heavier on Richard’s departure than the darkness of the darkest night.", "하지만 나에게는, 하나는 산 자요 하나는 죽은 자인 그 한 쌍의 그림자가, 리처드의 출발 위에 가장 캄캄한 밤의 어둠보다도 더 무겁게 드리워졌습니다."], ["And through Richard’s farewell words I heard it echoed: “Of all my old associations, of all my old pursuits and hopes, of all the living and the dead world, this one poor soul alone comes natural to me, and I am fit for.", "그리고 리처드의 작별 인사 사이로 나는 그 말이 메아리치는 것을 들었습니다."], ["There is a tie of many suffering years between us two, and it is the only tie I ever had on earth that Chancery has not broken!”", "\"제 모든 옛 인연 가운데, 제 모든 옛 추구와 희망 가운데, 산 자와 죽은 자의 온 세상 가운데, 이 가엾은 영혼 하나만이 저에게 자연스럽게 다가오고, 또 제가 어울릴 만한 사람입니다."], ["Mrs. Snagsby Sees It All", "우리 둘 사이에는 여러 해에 걸친 고난의 끈이 있고, 그것이야말로 대법원이 끊어놓지 못한, 제가 이 땅에서 가졌던 유일한 끈입니다!\""]], [["There is disquietude in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street.", "커서터가의 쿡스코트가 술렁인다."], ["Black suspicion hides in that peaceful region.", "시커먼 의혹이 그 평화로운 지역에 도사린다."], ["The mass of Cook’s Courtiers are in their usual state of mind, no better and no worse; but Mr.", "쿡스코트 사람들 대부분은 늘 그렇듯 평소 마음 상태 그대로다, 더 낫지도 더 못하지도 않다."], ["Snagsby is changed, and his little woman knows it.", "그러나 스내그스비 씨는 달라졌고, 그의 작은 아내는 그것을 안다."]], [["For Tom-all-Alone’s and Lincoln’s Inn Fields persist in harnessing themselves, a pair of ungovernable coursers, to the chariot of Mr. Snagsby’s imagination; and Mr.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳과 링컨스인 광장이 한사코 한 쌍의 길들지 않은 준마가 되어 스내그스비 씨 상상의 마차에 멍에를 메기 때문이다."], ["Bucket drives; and the passengers are Jo and Mr. Tulkinghorn; and the complete equipage whirls though the law-stationery business at wild speed all round the clock.", "마부석에는 버킷 씨가 앉고, 승객은 조와 토킹혼 씨이며, 완벽하게 채비를 갖춘 그 마차는 미친 듯한 속도로 법률 문구점 사업을 가로질러 시계가 한 바퀴 도는 내내 빙글빙글 돈다."], ["Even in the little front kitchen where the family meals are taken, it rattles away at a smoking pace from the dinner-table, when Mr. Snagsby pauses in carving the first slice of the leg of mutton baked with potatoes and stares at the kitchen wall.", "식구들이 끼니를 먹는 작은 앞부엌에서도 마차는 식탁에서 김을 뿜으며 달려 나가는데, 스내그스비 씨가 감자를 곁들여 구운 양고기 다리의 첫 점을 썰다 말고 손을 멈춘 채 부엌 벽을 멍하니 노려보는 바로 그 순간이다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby cannot make out what it is that he has had to do with.", "스내그스비 씨는 자기가 대체 무슨 일에 엮였는지 도무지 종잡지 못한다."], ["Something is wrong somewhere, but what something, what may come of it, to whom, when, and from which unthought of and unheard of quarter is the puzzle of his life. His remote impressions of the robes and coronets, the stars and garters, that sparkle through the surface-dust of Mr.", "어딘가 뭔가 잘못됐다, 그러나 그 뭔가가 무엇인지, 거기서 무엇이 나올지, 누구에게, 언제, 또 생각지도 듣지도 못한 어느 방향에서 닥칠지가 그의 인생을 사로잡은 수수께끼다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s chambers; his veneration for the mysteries presided over by that best and closest of his customers, whom all the Inns of Court, all Chancery Lane, and all the legal neighbourhood agree to hold in awe; his remembrance of Detective Mr. Bucket with his forefinger and his confidential manner, impossible to be evaded or declined, persuade him that he is a party to some dangerous secret without knowing what it is.", "토킹혼 씨 사무실의 먼지 덮인 표면 너머로 반짝이는 법복과 관모, 훈장과 가터 띠에 대한 그의 어렴풋한 인상, 모든 법학원과 챈서리 레인과 법조계 일대가 입을 모아 경외하는, 가장 훌륭하고 가장 가까운 그 단골 손님이 주관하는 신비에 대한 그의 숭배, 집게손가락을 곧추세우고 은근한 태도를 짓던, 피하거나 거절하기란 도무지 불가능했던 형사 버킷 씨에 대한 그의 기억, 이 모든 것이 그로 하여금 자신이 무언가 위험한 비밀의 당사자이면서도 그게 뭔지조차 모른다고 믿게 만든다."], ["And it is the fearful peculiarity of this condition that, at any hour of his daily life, at any opening of the shop-door, at any pull of the bell, at any entrance of a messenger, or any delivery of a letter, the secret may take air and fire, explode, and blow up—Mr.", "그리고 이 상태의 무서운 특징은 이것이니, 일상의 어느 시각이든, 가게 문이 열리는 어느 순간이든, 종이 당겨지는 어느 때든, 심부름꾼이 들어오는 어느 때든, 편지가 배달되는 어느 때든, 그 비밀이 바람을 타고 불이 붙어 폭발하고 터져버릴지도 모른다는 것이다."], ["Bucket only knows whom.", "누구를 날려버릴지는 오직 버킷 씨만 안다."]], [["For which reason, whenever a man unknown comes into the shop (as many men unknown do) and says, “Is Mr. Snagsby in?”", "그런 까닭에 모르는 남자가 가게에 들어와(모르는 남자는 숱하게 들어온다) \"스내그스비 씨 계시오?\"라거나 그와 비슷하게 악의 없는 말을 던질 때마다, 스내그스비 씨의 심장은 죄지은 가슴팍을 쿵쿵 두드린다."], ["or words to that innocent effect, Mr. Snagsby’s heart knocks hard at his guilty breast.", "그는 그런 물음에 어찌나 시달리는지, 사내아이가 물어오기라도 하면 계산대 너머로 그 녀석의 귀를 톡 튀기며 분풀이를 하고, 그게 무슨 수작이냐, 어째서 단번에 똑바로 말하지 못하느냐고 따져 묻는다."], ["He undergoes so much from such inquiries that when they are made by boys he revenges himself by flipping at their ears over the counter and asking the young dogs what they mean by it and why they can’t speak out at once? More impracticable men and boys persist in walking into Mr.", "더 다루기 힘든 남자들과 아이들이 한사코 스내그스비 씨의 잠 속으로 걸어 들어와 영문 모를 질문으로 그를 공포에 떨게 하는지라, 커서터가의 작은 우유 가게 수탉이 아침이랍시고 늘 하던 그 우스꽝스러운 식으로 홰를 치며 울 무렵이면, 스내그스비 씨는 종종 악몽의 한복판에서 발버둥치고 있고, 그의 작은 아내가 그를 흔들며 \"이 양반이 대체 왜 이래!\""], ["Snagsby’s sleep and terrifying him with unaccountable questions, so that often when the cock at the little dairy in Cursitor Street breaks out in his usual absurd way about the morning, Mr. Snagsby finds himself in a crisis of nightmare, with his little woman shaking him and saying “What’s the matter with the man!”", "하고 있다."]], [["The little woman herself is not the least item in his difficulty.", "그 작은 아내 자신이 그가 겪는 어려움에서 결코 작은 항목이 아니다."], ["To know that he is always keeping a secret from her, that he has under all circumstances to conceal and hold fast a tender double tooth, which her sharpness is ever ready to twist out of his head, gives Mr. Snagsby, in her dentistical presence, much of the air of a dog who has a reservation from his master and will look anywhere rather than meet his eye.", "자신이 늘 아내에게 비밀을 숨기고 있다는 것, 어떤 상황에서도 흔들리는 어금니 하나를 감추고 단단히 붙들고 있어야 한다는 것, 그런데 아내의 날카로움은 언제든 그 어금니를 그의 머리에서 비틀어 뽑아낼 채비가 되어 있다는 것, 이런 사정이 스내그스비 씨로 하여금 그 치과의사 같은 아내 앞에서, 주인에게 켕기는 구석이 있어 그 눈길을 마주하느니 차라리 아무 데나 둘러보는 개 같은 꼴을 하게 만든다."]], [["These various signs and tokens, marked by the little woman, are not lost upon her.", "작은 아내가 포착한 이런저런 징표와 조짐은 그녀에게서 흘려보내지지 않는다."], ["They impel her to say, “Snagsby has something on his mind!” And thus suspicion gets into Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street.", "그것들이 그녀를 부추겨 \"스내그스비, 저 양반 마음에 뭔가 있어!\""], ["From suspicion to jealousy, Mrs.", "하고 말하게 한다."], ["Snagsby finds the road as natural and short as from Cook’s Court to Chancery Lane.", "그리하여 의혹이 커서터가의 쿡스코트에 스며든다."], ["And thus jealousy gets into Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street. Once there (and it was always lurking thereabout), it is very active and nimble in Mrs.", "의혹에서 질투로 가는 길을, 스내그스비 부인은 쿡스코트에서 챈서리 레인으로 가는 길만큼이나 자연스럽고도 가깝게 여긴다."], ["Snagsby’s breast, prompting her to nocturnal examinations of Mr.", "그리하여 질투가 커서터가의 쿡스코트에 스며든다."], ["Snagsby’s pockets; to secret perusals of Mr. Snagsby’s letters; to private researches in the day book and ledger, till, cash-box, and iron safe; to watchings at windows, listenings behind doors, and a general putting of this and that together by the wrong end.", "일단 거기 들어서면(사실 늘 그 언저리에 도사리고 있었지만), 그것은 스내그스비 부인의 가슴속에서 무척이나 활발하고 잰싸게 움직여, 그녀로 하여금 밤이면 스내그스비 씨의 주머니를 뒤지게 하고, 스내그스비 씨의 편지를 몰래 정독하게 하고, 낮이면 매상 장부와 원장과 돈서랍과 무쇠 금고를 은밀히 조사하게 하고, 창가에서 망보고 문 뒤에서 엿듣고, 이것저것을 죄다 엉뚱한 쪽으로 끼워 맞추게 한다."]], [["Mrs. Snagsby is so perpetually on the alert that the house becomes ghostly with creaking boards and rustling garments.", "스내그스비 부인이 어찌나 줄곧 촉을 곤두세우고 있는지, 집안은 삐걱대는 마룻장과 바스락대는 옷자락으로 유령이 나올 것만 같다."], ["The ’prentices think somebody may have been murdered there in bygone times.", "견습공들은 옛날 옛적에 누가 여기서 살해당한 건 아닐까 생각한다."], ["Guster holds certain loose atoms of an idea (picked up at Tooting, where they were found floating among the orphans) that there is buried money underneath the cellar, guarded by an old man with a white beard, who cannot get out for seven thousand years because he said the Lord’s Prayer backwards.", "거스터는 한 가지 막연한 생각의 흩어진 조각들을 품고 있는데(투팅에서 주워들은 것으로, 그곳 고아들 사이에 떠돌던 이야기다), 지하실 밑에 돈이 묻혀 있고 흰 수염을 기른 노인이 그것을 지키는데, 주기도문을 거꾸로 외운 탓에 칠천 년 동안 밖으로 나오지 못한다는 것이다."]], [["“Who was Nimrod?” Mrs.", "\"님로드가 누구였더라?\""], ["Snagsby repeatedly inquires of herself.", "스내그스비 부인이 거듭 자문한다."], ["“Who was that lady—that creature?", "\"그 여자가 누구였지, 그 계집이?"], ["And who is that boy?”", "그리고 저 사내애는 또 누구야?\""], ["Now, Nimrod being as dead as the mighty hunter whose name Mrs. Snagsby has appropriated, and the lady being unproducible, she directs her mental eye, for the present, with redoubled vigilance to the boy.", "그런데 님로드는 스내그스비 부인이 그 이름을 빌려온 저 위대한 사냥꾼만큼이나 깡그리 죽어버렸고, 그 여자는 끄집어낼 길이 없으니, 그녀는 지금으로서는 갑절로 경계심을 곤두세워 마음의 눈길을 사내애에게 돌린다."], ["“And who,” quoth Mrs.", "\"그리고 저 애는,\""], ["Snagsby for the thousand and first time, “is that boy?", "스내그스비 부인이 천 번하고도 한 번째로 되뇐다. \"저 애는 누구야?"], ["Who is that—!”", "저자가 누구냐고!\""], ["And there Mrs. Snagsby is seized with an inspiration.", "그리고 바로 거기서 스내그스비 부인은 한 줄기 영감에 사로잡힌다."]], [["He has no respect for Mr. Chadband.", "저 애는 채드밴드 씨를 존경하지 않는다."], ["No, to be sure, and he wouldn’t have, of course.", "그래, 정말 그렇다, 물론 존경할 리가 없다."], ["Naturally he wouldn’t, under those contagious circumstances.", "당연히 그렇지, 그 전염되기 쉬운 사정을 생각하면."], ["He was invited and appointed by Mr. Chadband—why, Mrs.", "저 애는 채드밴드 씨한테 초대받고 지시받았다, 어머, 스내그스비 부인이 제 두 귀로 똑똑히 들었다고!"], ["Snagsby heard it herself with her own ears!—to come back, and be told where he was to go, to be addressed by Mr.", "다시 돌아오라고, 그러면 어디로 가야 할지 일러주겠노라고, 채드밴드 씨가 말씀을 베풀어주겠노라고."], ["Chadband; and he never came!", "그런데 저 애는 결코 오지 않았다!"], ["Why did he never come?", "어째서 결코 오지 않았을까?"], ["Because he was told not to come.", "오지 말라는 말을 들었으니까."], ["Who told him not to come?", "누가 오지 말라고 했을까?"], ["Who?", "누가?"], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["Mrs. Snagsby sees it all.", "스내그스비 부인은 이제 죄다 알아챘다."]], [["But happily (and Mrs. Snagsby tightly shakes her head and tightly smiles) that boy was met by Mr. Chadband yesterday in the streets; and that boy, as affording a subject which Mr. Chadband desires to improve for the spiritual delight of a select congregation, was seized by Mr. Chadband and threatened with being delivered over to the police unless he showed the reverend gentleman where he lived and unless he entered into, and fulfilled, an undertaking to appear in Cook’s Court to-morrow night, “to—mor—row—night,” Mrs. Snagsby repeats for mere emphasis with another tight smile and another tight shake of her head; and to-morrow night that boy will be here, and to-morrow night Mrs. Snagsby will have her eye upon him and upon some one else; and oh, you may walk a long while in your secret ways (says Mrs. Snagsby with haughtiness and scorn), but you can’t blind ME!", "하지만 다행히도(스내그스비 부인은 야무지게 고개를 흔들고 야무지게 미소 짓는다) 그 애를 채드밴드 씨가 어제 거리에서 마주쳤다. 그리고 그 애는, 가려 뽑은 신도들의 영적 기쁨을 위해 채드밴드 씨가 갈고닦아 활용하고자 하는 소재를 제공하는지라, 채드밴드 씨에게 붙들렸고, 자기가 어디 사는지 그 목사님께 보여주지 않으면, 또 내일 밤 쿡스코트에 나타나겠다는 약속을 맺고 이행하지 않으면 경찰에 넘기겠다는 위협을 받았으니, \"내—일—밤,\" 스내그스비 부인이 그저 강조하느라 또 한 번 야무지게 미소 짓고 또 한 번 야무지게 고개를 흔들며 되풀이한다. 그리고 내일 밤 그 애가 이리로 올 것이고, 내일 밤 스내그스비 부인은 그 애에게서, 그리고 또 다른 누군가에게서 눈을 떼지 않을 것이며, 오, 너희야 너희 은밀한 길을 오래도록 걷고 다닐 수 있겠지(스내그스비 부인이 거만과 경멸을 담아 말한다), 하지만 이 몸의 눈은 못 가려!"]], [["Mrs. Snagsby sounds no timbrel in anybody’s ears, but holds her purpose quietly, and keeps her counsel.", "스내그스비 부인은 누구의 귀에도 작은북을 울리지 않고, 제 속셈을 조용히 간직하며 입을 다문다."], ["To-morrow comes, the savoury preparations for the Oil Trade come, the evening comes. Comes Mr.", "내일이 온다, 유지업을 위한 구수한 채비도 차려진다, 저녁이 온다."], ["Snagsby in his black coat; come the Chadbands; come (when the gorging vessel is replete) the ’prentices and Guster, to be edified; comes at last, with his slouching head, and his shuffle backward, and his shuffle forward, and his shuffle to the right, and his shuffle to the left, and his bit of fur cap in his muddy hand, which he picks as if it were some mangy bird he had caught and was plucking before eating raw, Jo, the very, very tough subject Mr. Chadband is to improve.", "검은 외투를 걸친 스내그스비 씨가 온다, 채드밴드 부부가 온다, (게걸스러운 그릇이 가득 차고 나면) 가르침을 받으러 견습공들과 거스터가 온다, 마침내 그가 온다, 푹 수그린 머리에, 뒤로 질질, 앞으로 질질, 오른쪽으로 질질, 왼쪽으로 질질 걸으며, 진흙투성이 손에 털모자 조각을 쥔 채, 마치 자기가 잡아온 옴 오른 새라도 되는 양 날로 먹기 전에 깃털을 쥐어뜯듯 그것을 쥐어뜯으며, 조가 온다, 채드밴드 씨가 갈고닦아야 할 그 지독하게도 뻣뻣한 소재가."]], [["Mrs. Snagsby screws a watchful glance on Jo as he is brought into the little drawing-room by Guster.", "스내그스비 부인은 거스터에게 이끌려 작은 응접실로 들어오는 조에게 감시의 눈초리를 날카롭게 고정한다."], ["He looks at Mr. Snagsby the moment he comes in.", "그는 들어오자마자 스내그스비 씨를 쳐다본다."], ["Aha!", "아하!"], ["Why does he look at Mr. Snagsby?", "어째서 저 애가 스내그스비 씨를 쳐다보지?"], ["Mr. Snagsby looks at him.", "스내그스비 씨도 그를 쳐다본다."], ["Why should he do that, but that Mrs. Snagsby sees it all?", "스내그스비 부인이 이미 죄다 알아챈 까닭이 아니라면 어째서 저 양반이 저럴까?"], ["Why else should that look pass between them, why else should Mr. Snagsby be confused and cough a signal cough behind his hand?", "그렇지 않다면 어째서 저런 눈길이 둘 사이를 오갈까, 어째서 스내그스비 씨가 당황해서 손으로 입을 가리고 신호 같은 헛기침을 할까?"], ["It is as clear as crystal that Mr. Snagsby is that boy’s father.", "스내그스비 씨가 저 애의 아비라는 건 수정처럼 명백하다."]], [["“Peace, my friends,”", "\"평안하시기를, 친애하는 벗들이여,\""], ["says Chadband, rising and wiping the oily exudations from his reverend visage.", "채드밴드 씨가 일어서며 거룩한 얼굴에서 배어 나온 기름기를 닦으며 말한다. \"우리에게 평안이 있기를!"], ["“Peace be with us! My friends, why with us?", "친애하는 벗들이여, 어찌하여 우리에게입니까?"], ["Because,”", "무릇,\""], ["with his fat smile, “it cannot be against us, because it must be for us; because it is not hardening, because it is softening; because it does not make war like the hawk, but comes home unto us like the dove.", "그는 기름진 미소를 띤다. \"평안은 우리에게 맞서지 못하기에, 무릇 평안은 우리를 위한 것이어야 하기에, 무릇 평안은 굳히지 않고 무르게 하기에, 무릇 평안은 매처럼 전쟁을 일으키지 않고 비둘기처럼 우리에게로 돌아오기에."], ["Therefore, my friends, peace be with us!", "그러므로 친애하는 벗들이여, 우리에게 평안이 있기를!"], ["My human boy, come forward!”", "인간 소년아, 앞으로 나오너라!\""]], [["Stretching forth his flabby paw, Mr. Chadband lays the same on Jo’s arm and considers where to station him.", "채드밴드 씨가 물렁한 손바닥을 쭉 내밀어 그것을 조의 팔에 얹고 어디다 세워둘지 궁리한다."], ["Jo, very doubtful of his reverend friend’s intentions and not at all clear but that something practical and painful is going to be done to him, mutters, “You let me alone.", "조는 그 거룩하신 벗의 의도를 무척 의심스러워하며, 무언가 실질적이고 고통스러운 일이 자기에게 가해지려는 게 아닌가 영 확신이 안 서서 중얼거린다. \"냅둬요."], ["I never said nothink to you.", "난 아저씨한테 아무 말도 안 했어요."], ["You let me alone.”", "냅두라구요.\""]], [["“No, my young friend,”", "\"아니다, 젊은 벗이여,\""], ["says Chadband smoothly, “I will not let you alone.", "채드밴드 씨가 매끄럽게 말한다. \"나는 너를 내버려두지 않으련다."], ["And why?", "어찌하여?"], ["Because I am a harvest-labourer, because I am a toiler and a moiler, because you are delivered over unto me and are become as a precious instrument in my hands.", "무릇 나는 추수하는 일꾼이기에, 무릇 나는 애쓰고 땀 흘리는 자이기에, 무릇 너는 내게 맡겨져 내 손안의 귀중한 도구가 되었기에."], ["My friends, may I so employ this instrument as to use it to your advantage, to your profit, to your gain, to your welfare, to your enrichment!", "친애하는 벗들이여, 부디 내가 이 도구를 그대들의 유익을 위하여, 그대들의 이득을 위하여, 그대들의 소득을 위하여, 그대들의 복리를 위하여, 그대들의 풍요를 위하여 부려쓰게 하소서!"], ["My young friend, sit upon this stool.”", "젊은 벗이여, 이 걸상에 앉아라.\""]], [["Jo, apparently possessed by an impression that the reverend gentleman wants to cut his hair, shields his head with both arms and is got into the required position with great difficulty and every possible manifestation of reluctance.", "조는 그 거룩하신 양반이 자기 머리카락을 자르려는 줄로 단단히 믿은 모양인지 두 팔로 머리를 감싸고, 온갖 거부의 몸짓을 다 보이며 갖은 애를 먹인 끝에야 요구받은 자세로 앉혀진다."]], [["When he is at last adjusted like a lay-figure, Mr. Chadband, retiring behind the table, holds up his bear’s-paw and says, “My friends!”", "마침내 마네킹처럼 자리가 잡히자, 채드밴드 씨는 탁자 뒤로 물러나 곰 발바닥 같은 손을 치켜들고 말한다."], ["This is the signal for a general settlement of the audience.", "\"친애하는 벗들이여!\" 이것이 청중이 두루 자리를 잡으라는 신호다."], ["The ’prentices giggle internally and nudge each other.", "견습공들은 속으로 낄낄대며 서로 팔꿈치로 쿡쿡 찌른다."], ["Guster falls into a staring and vacant state, compounded of a stunned admiration of Mr. Chadband and pity for the friendless outcast whose condition touches her nearly.", "거스터는 멍하니 넋을 놓은 상태에 빠지는데, 채드밴드 씨에 대한 얼떨떨한 경탄과, 제 처지가 절절히 와닿는 저 의지가지없는 버림받은 아이에 대한 연민이 뒤섞인 것이다."], ["Mrs. Snagsby silently lays trains of gunpowder.", "스내그스비 부인은 말없이 화약 도화선을 깐다."], ["Mrs. Chadband composes herself grimly by the fire and warms her knees, finding that sensation favourable to the reception of eloquence.", "채드밴드 부인은 난롯가에서 험상궂게 몸을 추스르고 무릎을 덥히는데, 그 감각이 웅변을 받아들이기에 알맞다고 여겨서다."]], [["It happens that Mr. Chadband has a pulpit habit of fixing some member of his congregation with his eye and fatly arguing his points with that particular person, who is understood to be expected to be moved to an occasional grunt, groan, gasp, or other audible expression of inward working, which expression of inward working, being echoed by some elderly lady in the next pew and so communicated like a game of forfeits through a circle of the more fermentable sinners present, serves the purpose of parliamentary cheering and gets Mr.", "마침 채드밴드 씨에게는 설교단의 버릇이 하나 있으니, 신도 가운데 한 사람을 눈으로 콕 찍어 붙들고 그 특정인을 상대로 제 논점을 기름지게 펼치는 것이다."], ["Chadband’s steam up. From mere force of habit, Mr.", "그러면 그 사람은 이따금 끙, 끄응, 헉 하는 소리나, 속이 움직이는 그밖의 들릴 만한 표현으로 화답하리라 기대되며, 속이 움직이는 그 표현은 옆 좌석의 어느 나이 든 부인에게 메아리쳐, 거기 모인 더 발효되기 쉬운 죄인들의 무리로 벌금 놀이처럼 번져나가, 의회의 환호 같은 구실을 하며 채드밴드 씨의 증기를 끌어올린다."], ["Chadband in saying “My friends!” has rested his eye on Mr.", "그저 버릇의 힘으로, 채드밴드 씨는 \"친애하는 벗들이여!\""], ["Snagsby and proceeds to make that ill-starred stationer, already sufficiently confused, the immediate recipient of his discourse.", "하고 말하면서 눈길을 스내그스비 씨에게 얹어두었고, 이제 그 불운한 문구상을, 이미 충분히 당황한 사람을, 제 설교의 직통 수신자로 삼는다."]], [["“We have here among us, my friends,”", "\"우리 가운데 여기, 친애하는 벗들이여,\""], ["says Chadband, “a Gentile and a heathen, a dweller in the tents of Tom-all-Alone’s and a mover-on upon the surface of the earth.", "채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"한 이방인이자 한 이교도가 있으니, 톰 혼자 사는 곳의 천막에 깃든 자요, 이 땅 거죽 위를 떠도는 자라."], ["We have here among us, my friends,” and Mr.", "우리 가운데 여기, 친애하는 벗들이여,\""], ["Chadband, untwisting the point with his dirty thumb-nail, bestows an oily smile on Mr. Snagsby, signifying that he will throw him an argumentative back-fall presently if he be not already down, “a brother and a boy.", "그러면서 채드밴드 씨는 더러운 엄지손톱으로 논점을 비틀어 풀어내며, 스내그스비 씨에게 기름진 미소를 베푸는데, 이는 그가 아직 쓰러지지 않았다면 곧 논변의 뒤집기 한판으로 메다꽂아주겠다는 뜻이다."], ["Devoid of parents, devoid of relations, devoid of flocks and herds, devoid of gold and silver and of precious stones.", "\"한 형제이자 한 소년이 있으니. 부모도 없고, 친척도 없고, 양 떼와 소 떼도 없고, 금도 은도 보석도 없는 자라."], ["Now, my friends, why do I say he is devoid of these possessions?", "자, 친애하는 벗들이여, 어찌하여 내가 그에게 이런 소유가 없다고 말하는가?"], ["Why?", "어찌하여?"], ["Why is he?” Mr.", "어찌하여 그러한가?\""], ["Chadband states the question as if he were propounding an entirely new riddle of much ingenuity and merit to Mr. Snagsby and entreating him not to give it up.", "채드밴드 씨는 마치 무척 기발하고 훌륭한, 아주 새로운 수수께끼를 스내그스비 씨에게 내놓으며 부디 포기하지 말라고 간청하기라도 하듯 그 물음을 던진다."]], [["Mr. Snagsby, greatly perplexed by the mysterious look he received just now from his little woman—at about the period when Mr.", "스내그스비 씨는 방금 작은 아내에게서 받은 그 알 수 없는 눈길에 크게 당황한 터라—채드밴드 씨가 부모라는 단어를 입에 올린 바로 그 무렵의 일이었다—조심스레 한마디 던지고 만다."], ["Chadband mentioned the word parents—is tempted into modestly remarking, “I don’t know, I’m sure, sir.”", "\"글쎄요, 정말 모르겠습니다, 목사님.\""], ["On which interruption Mrs. Chadband glares and Mrs.", "이렇게 끼어들자 채드밴드 부인은 눈을 부라리고 스내그스비 부인은 \"창피한 줄 아세요!\""], ["Snagsby says, “For shame!”", "하고 말한다."]], [["“I hear a voice,”", "\"내게 한 음성이 들리는구나,\""], ["says Chadband; “is it a still small voice, my friends?", "채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"그것이 고요하고 작은 음성인가, 친애하는 벗들이여?"], ["I fear not, though I fain would hope so—”", "그러기를 간절히 바라나, 아무래도 아닌 듯하구나—\""]], [["“Ah—h!”", "\"아—!\""], ["from Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인에게서 새어 나온다."]], [["“Which says, ‘I don’t know.’ Then I will tell you why.", "\"그 음성이 이르기를, '나는 모르겠다' 하는구나."], ["I say this brother present here among us is devoid of parents, devoid of relations, devoid of flocks and herds, devoid of gold, of silver, and of precious stones because he is devoid of the light that shines in upon some of us.", "그러면 내가 그대들에게 어찌하여인지 일러주리라. 내 말하노니, 지금 우리 가운데 있는 이 형제는 부모도 없고, 친척도 없고, 양 떼와 소 떼도 없고, 금도 은도 보석도 없으니, 이는 우리 가운데 어떤 이들에게 비쳐드는 빛이 그에게는 없는 까닭이라."], ["What is that light?", "그 빛이 무엇인가?"], ["What is it?", "그것이 무엇인가?"], ["I ask you, what is that light?”", "내 그대들에게 묻노니, 그 빛이 무엇인가?\""]], [["Mr. Chadband draws back his head and pauses, but Mr. Snagsby is not to be lured on to his destruction again. Mr. Chadband, leaning forward over the table, pierces what he has got to follow directly into Mr. Snagsby with the thumb-nail already mentioned.", "채드밴드 씨는 고개를 뒤로 빼고 잠시 멈추나, 스내그스비 씨는 다시 제 파멸로 꾀어들지 않는다. 채드밴드 씨는 탁자 위로 몸을 기울여, 앞서 말한 그 엄지손톱으로 곧바로 이어질 말을 스내그스비 씨 속으로 콕 찔러넣는다."]], [["“It is,”", "\"그것은,\""], ["says Chadband, “the ray of rays, the sun of suns, the moon of moons, the star of stars.", "채드밴드 씨가 말한다. \"빛 중의 빛이요, 해 중의 해요, 달 중의 달이요, 별 중의 별이라."], ["It is the light of Terewth.”", "그것은 진리의 빛이라.\""]], [["Mr. Chadband draws himself up again and looks triumphantly at Mr. Snagsby as if he would be glad to know how he feels after that.", "채드밴드 씨는 다시 몸을 곧추세우고는, 마치 그 말을 듣고 어떤 심정일지 알고 싶어 못 견디겠다는 듯 의기양양하게 스내그스비 씨를 바라본다."]], [["“Of Terewth,”", "\"진리의,\""], ["says Mr. Chadband, hitting him again.", "채드밴드 씨가 또 한 방 먹인다."], ["“Say not to me that it is NOT the lamp of lamps.", "\"그것이 등불 중의 등불이 아니라고 내게 말하지 말라."], ["I say to you it is.", "내 그대에게 이르노니 그것이 바로 그것이라."], ["I say to you, a million of times over, it is. It is!", "내 그대에게 백만 번이고 거듭 이르노니 그것이 바로 그것이라."], ["I say to you that I will proclaim it to you, whether you like it or not; nay, that the less you like it, the more I will proclaim it to you.", "그것이라! 내 그대에게 이르노니, 그대가 좋든 싫든 내가 그대에게 선포하리라, 아니, 그대가 싫어할수록 더욱더 그대에게 선포하리라."], ["With a speaking-trumpet!", "나팔을 불어가며!"], ["I say to you that if you rear yourself against it, you shall fall, you shall be bruised, you shall be battered, you shall be flawed, you shall be smashed.”", "내 그대에게 이르노니, 그대가 그것에 맞서 일어선다면, 그대는 쓰러지리라, 그대는 짓이겨지리라, 그대는 두들겨 맞으리라, 그대는 금이 가리라, 그대는 박살나리라.\""]], [["The present effect of this flight of oratory—much admired for its general power by Mr. Chadband’s followers—being not only to make Mr. Chadband unpleasantly warm, but to represent the innocent Mr. Snagsby in the light of a determined enemy to virtue, with a forehead of brass and a heart of adamant, that unfortunate tradesman becomes yet more disconcerted and is in a very advanced state of low spirits and false position when Mr. Chadband accidentally finishes him.", "이 웅변의 비상이—채드밴드 씨의 추종자들이 그 두루 막강한 위력으로 크게 떠받드는 솜씨다—지금 빚어낸 효과는, 채드밴드 씨를 불쾌하리만치 후끈 달아오르게 했을 뿐 아니라, 죄 없는 스내그스비 씨를 놋쇠 같은 이마와 금강석 같은 심장을 지닌 미덕의 단호한 원수처럼 보이게 만든 것이라, 그 불운한 장사꾼은 더욱더 낭패스러워져, 채드밴드 씨가 어쩌다 그에게 마지막 일격을 가할 무렵엔 풀 죽고 처지가 거북해진 상태가 한참 깊어진다."]], [["“My friends,”", "\"친애하는 벗들이여,\""], ["he resumes after dabbing his fat head for some time—and it smokes to such an extent that he seems to light his pocket-handkerchief at it, which smokes, too, after every dab—“to pursue the subject we are endeavouring with our lowly gifts to improve, let us in a spirit of love inquire what is that Terewth to which I have alluded.", "그는 한동안 기름진 머리를 톡톡 두드린 뒤에 다시 이어간다—그런데 그 머리에서 어찌나 김이 모락모락 솟는지 거기다 손수건에 불을 붙이는 것만 같고, 두드릴 때마다 손수건마저 김을 뿜는 듯하다—\"우리가 보잘것없는 재능으로나마 갈고닦아 보려 애쓰는 이 주제를 좇아, 사랑의 마음으로 물어보자, 내가 언급한 그 진리가 무엇인가를."], ["For, my young friends,”", "무릇, 젊은 벗들이여,\""], ["suddenly addressing the ’prentices and Guster, to their consternation, “if I am told by the doctor that calomel or castor-oil is good for me, I may naturally ask what is calomel, and what is castor-oil.", "그가 견습공들과 거스터를 별안간 지목하니 그들이 화들짝 놀란다. \"의사가 내게 감홍이나 피마자유가 몸에 좋다고 일러준다면, 나는 당연히 감홍이 무엇이고 피마자유가 무엇인지 물을 수 있으리라."], ["I may wish to be informed of that before I dose myself with either or with both.", "그 둘 중 하나로, 혹은 둘 다로 내 몸을 다스리기 전에 그것을 알고 싶을 수 있으리라."], ["Now, my young friends, what is this Terewth then?", "자, 젊은 벗들이여, 그렇다면 이 진리란 무엇인가?"], ["Firstly (in a spirit of love), what is the common sort of Terewth—the working clothes—the every-day wear, my young friends?", "첫째로(사랑의 마음으로), 흔히 말하는 종류의 진리—일하는 옷—날마다 걸치는 그 진리란 무엇인가, 젊은 벗들이여?"], ["Is it deception?”", "그것은 속임인가?\""]], [["“Ah—h!”", "\"아—!\""], ["from Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인에게서."]], [["“Is it suppression?”", "\"그것은 숨김인가?\""]], [["A shiver in the negative from Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인에게서 아니라는 떨림."]], [["“Is it reservation?”", "\"그것은 감춤인가?\""]], [["A shake of the head from Mrs. Snagsby—very long and very tight.", "스내그스비 부인에게서 고개를 가로젓는 몸짓—아주 길고 아주 야무진."]], [["“No, my friends, it is neither of these.", "\"아니다, 친애하는 벗들이여, 그 어느 것도 아니다."], ["Neither of these names belongs to it.", "그 이름 중 어느 것도 그것에 어울리지 않는다."], ["When this young heathen now among us—who is now, my friends, asleep, the seal of indifference and perdition being set upon his eyelids; but do not wake him, for it is right that I should have to wrestle, and to combat and to struggle, and to conquer, for his sake—when this young hardened heathen told us a story of a cock, and of a bull, and of a lady, and of a sovereign, was THAT the Terewth?", "지금 우리 가운데 있는 이 젊은 이교도가—그는 지금, 친애하는 벗들이여, 잠들어 있고, 그 눈꺼풀에는 무관심과 멸망의 봉인이 찍혀 있다, 그러나 그를 깨우지 말라, 그를 위하여 내가 씨름하고, 맞붙어 싸우고, 분투하고, 이겨내야 마땅하기 때문이라—이 젊고 완고한 이교도가 우리에게 수탉이며, 황소며, 한 부인이며, 한 군주에 관한 허튼소리를 늘어놓았을 때, 그것이 진리였는가? 아니다."], ["No. Or if it was partly, was it wholly and entirely?", "혹 일부라도 그러했다 한들, 그것이 온전히 전부 그러했는가?"], ["No, my friends, no!”", "아니다, 친애하는 벗들이여, 아니다!\""]], [["If Mr. Snagsby could withstand his little woman’s look as it enters at his eyes, the windows of his soul, and searches the whole tenement, he were other than the man he is.", "스내그스비 씨가 제 영혼의 창인 두 눈으로 들어와 온 집안을 샅샅이 뒤지는 작은 아내의 눈길을 견뎌낼 수 있다면, 그는 지금의 그가 아닌 딴사람일 것이다."], ["He cowers and droops.", "그는 움츠러들고 시들어든다."]], [["“Or, my juvenile friends,”", "\"혹은, 어린 벗들이여,\" 채드밴드 씨가 그들의 이해 수준으로 내려서며 말하는데, 그 기름지고 온순한 미소에는 일부러 이를 위해 계단을 한참 내려왔노라는 매우 노골적인 과시가 어려 있다."], ["says Chadband, descending to the level of their comprehension with a very obtrusive demonstration in his greasily meek smile of coming a long way downstairs for the purpose, “if the master of this house was to go forth into the city and there see an eel, and was to come back, and was to call unto him the mistress of this house, and was to say, ‘Sarah, rejoice with me, for I have seen an elephant!’ would THAT be Terewth?”", "\"이 집의 주인이 도성으로 나아가 거기서 뱀장어 한 마리를 보고는, 돌아와, 이 집의 안주인을 제게로 불러, '세라여, 나와 더불어 기뻐하라, 내가 코끼리를 보았노라!' 하고 말한다면, 그것이 진리이겠는가?\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby in tears.", "스내그스비 부인은 눈물짓는다."]], [["“Or put it, my juvenile friends, that he saw an elephant, and returning said ‘Lo, the city is barren, I have seen but an eel,’ would THAT be Terewth?”", "\"혹은 이렇게 해보자, 어린 벗들이여, 그가 코끼리를 보고도, 돌아와 '보라, 도성은 황량하도다, 나는 뱀장어 한 마리밖에 보지 못하였노라' 하고 말한다면, 그것이 진리이겠는가?\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby sobbing loudly.", "스내그스비 부인이 큰 소리로 흐느낀다."]], [["“Or put it, my juvenile friends,”", "\"혹은 이렇게 해보자, 어린 벗들이여,\""], ["said Chadband, stimulated by the sound, “that the unnatural parents of this slumbering heathen—for parents he had, my juvenile friends, beyond a doubt—after casting him forth to the wolves and the vultures, and the wild dogs and the young gazelles, and the serpents, went back to their dwellings and had their pipes, and their pots, and their flutings and their dancings, and their malt liquors, and their butcher’s meat and poultry, would THAT be Terewth?”", "채드밴드 씨가 그 소리에 자극받아 말한다. \"이 잠든 이교도의 인륜을 저버린 부모가—그에게도 부모가 있었다, 어린 벗들이여, 의심할 여지없이—그를 이리 떼와 독수리 떼와, 들개 떼와 어린 영양 떼와, 뱀들에게 내던져버린 뒤, 제 거처로 돌아가 곰방대를 빨고, 술단지를 기울이고, 피리를 불고 춤을 추고, 맥주를 들이켜고, 푸줏간 고기와 가금을 먹었다면, 그것이 진리이겠는가?\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby replies by delivering herself a prey to spasms, not an unresisting prey, but a crying and a tearing one, so that Cook’s Court re-echoes with her shrieks.", "스내그스비 부인은 발작의 먹이로 제 몸을 내던지는 것으로 화답하는데, 순순한 먹이가 아니라 울부짖고 쥐어뜯는 먹이라, 쿡스코트가 그녀의 비명으로 메아리친다."], ["Finally, becoming cataleptic, she has to be carried up the narrow staircase like a grand piano.", "마침내 강경증에 빠진 그녀를 그랜드피아노 옮기듯 좁은 계단 위로 떠메고 올라가야 한다."], ["After unspeakable suffering, productive of the utmost consternation, she is pronounced, by expresses from the bedroom, free from pain, though much exhausted, in which state of affairs Mr. Snagsby, trampled and crushed in the piano-forte removal, and extremely timid and feeble, ventures to come out from behind the door in the drawing-room.", "이루 말할 수 없는 고초 끝에, 더없는 낭패를 빚어낸 끝에, 침실에서 보내온 급보로 그녀는 통증은 가셨으나 몹시 지쳤노라고 선언되는데, 그런 와중에 피아노포르테 이송 작업에서 짓밟히고 깔린 스내그스비 씨가, 몹시 겁먹고 기진한 채로, 응접실 문 뒤에서 빠끔히 나와 본다."]], [["All this time Jo has been standing on the spot where he woke up, ever picking his cap and putting bits of fur in his mouth.", "이 모든 동안 조는 자기가 잠에서 깬 그 자리에 줄곧 서서, 내내 모자를 쥐어뜯어 털 부스러기를 입에 넣고 있었다."], ["He spits them out with a remorseful air, for he feels that it is in his nature to be an unimprovable reprobate and that it’s no good HIS trying to keep awake, for HE won’t never know nothink.", "그는 뉘우치는 기색으로 그것을 뱉어낸다, 자기는 천성이 도무지 개선될 수 없는 죄인이며, 자기가 깨어 있으려 애써봐야 헛일이라고, 자기는 결코 아무것도 모르고 말 거라고 느끼기 때문이다."], ["Though it may be, Jo, that there is a history so interesting and affecting even to minds as near the brutes as thine, recording deeds done on this earth for common men, that if the Chadbands, removing their own persons from the light, would but show it thee in simple reverence, would but leave it unimproved, would but regard it as being eloquent enough without their modest aid—it might hold thee awake, and thou might learn from it yet!", "그러나 조여, 어쩌면 너의 마음처럼 짐승에 가까운 마음에게마저 너무도 흥미롭고 가슴 저민, 이 땅에서 평범한 사람들을 위해 행해진 행적을 기록한 이야기가 하나 있을지도 모른다, 만일 채드밴드 무리가 제 몸뚱이를 그 빛에서 비켜주고, 그저 소박한 경외로 그것을 네게 보여주기만 한다면, 그저 그것을 갈고닦지 않은 채로 내버려두기만 한다면, 그저 그것이 그들의 가당찮은 도움 없이도 충분히 웅변적임을 알아주기만 한다면—그 이야기가 너를 깨어 있게 붙들고, 너는 거기서 아직 무언가를 배울 수 있을지도 모른다!"]], [["Jo never heard of any such book.", "조는 그런 책에 대해 들어본 적이 없다."], ["Its compilers and the Reverend Chadband are all one to him, except that he knows the Reverend Chadband and would rather run away from him for an hour than hear him talk for five minutes.", "그것을 엮은 이들이나 채드밴드 목사나 그에게는 다 한가지인데, 다만 채드밴드 목사는 알고 있고, 그가 오 분 떠드는 걸 듣느니 차라리 한 시간 동안 그한테서 달아나는 편이 낫겠다고 여길 뿐이다."], ["“It an’t no good my waiting here no longer,”", "\"여기서 더 기다려봐야 소용없겠어,\""], ["thinks Jo. “Mr.", "조는 생각한다."], ["Snagsby an’t a-going to say nothink to me to-night.”", "\"스내그스비 아저씨는 오늘 밤 나한테 아무 말도 안 할 거야.\""], ["And downstairs he shuffles.", "그리고 그는 질질 끌며 아래층으로 내려간다."]], [["But downstairs is the charitable Guster, holding by the handrail of the kitchen stairs and warding off a fit, as yet doubtfully, the same having been induced by Mrs. Snagsby’s screaming.", "그런데 아래층에는 마음씨 고운 거스터가, 부엌 계단 난간을 붙들고 발작을 막아내고 있는데, 아직은 막아낼지 어떨지 미덥지가 않다, 스내그스비 부인의 비명에 유발된 발작이기 때문이다."], ["She has her own supper of bread and cheese to hand to Jo, with whom she ventures to interchange a word or so for the first time.", "그녀는 제 몫의 빵과 치즈 야참을 조에게 건네줄 참이고, 처음으로 그와 한두 마디 말을 주고받아 본다."]], [["“Here’s something to eat, poor boy,”", "\"이거 먹어, 가엾은 애야,\""], ["says Guster.", "거스터가 말한다."]], [["“Thank’ee, mum,”", "\"고맙습니다, 누나,\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“Are you hungry?”", "\"배고프니?\""]], [["“Jist!”", "\"딱 그래요!\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“What’s gone of your father and your mother, eh?”", "\"네 아빠랑 엄마는 어떻게 됐어, 응?\""]], [["Jo stops in the middle of a bite and looks petrified.", "조는 한 입 베어 물다 말고 돌처럼 굳어버린다."], ["For this orphan charge of the Christian saint whose shrine was at Tooting has patted him on the shoulder, and it is the first time in his life that any decent hand has been so laid upon him.", "투팅에 사당을 둔 그 기독교 성인이 거두는 고아 가운데 하나인 이 처녀가 조의 어깨를 토닥였는데, 그의 평생에 어떤 점잖은 손길이 그에게 그렇게 얹힌 것은 이번이 처음이기 때문이다."]], [["“I never know’d nothink about ’em,”", "\"난 그런 거 하나도 몰라요,\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“No more didn’t I of mine,” cries Guster.", "\"나도 우리 엄마 아빠는 그래,\""], ["She is repressing symptoms favourable to the fit when she seems to take alarm at something and vanishes down the stairs.", "거스터가 외친다. 그녀는 발작에 유리한 증세를 억누르고 있다가, 무언가에 놀란 듯하더니 계단 아래로 사라진다."]], [["“Jo,”", "\"조,\""], ["whispers the law-stationer softly as the boy lingers on the step.", "그 아이가 계단에서 머뭇거리자 법률 문구상이 부드럽게 속삭인다."]], [["“Here I am, Mr. Snagsby!”", "\"여기 있어요, 스내그스비 아저씨!\""]], [["“I didn’t know you were gone—there’s another half-crown, Jo.", "\"난 네가 간 줄 몰랐다—여기 반 크라운 더 있다, 조."], ["It was quite right of you to say nothing about the lady the other night when we were out together.", "일전에 우리 둘이 함께 나갔던 그날 밤, 그 부인에 대해 아무 말 안 한 건 정말 잘한 일이었어."], ["It would breed trouble.", "괜한 말썽을 부를 테니까."], ["You can’t be too quiet, Jo.”", "너는 아무리 조용히 굴어도 지나치지 않다, 조.\""]], [["“I am fly, master!”", "\"잘 알아요, 아저씨!\""]], [["And so, good night.", "그러고서, 잘 자거라."]], [["A ghostly shade, frilled and night-capped, follows the law-stationer to the room he came from and glides higher up.", "주름 장식을 두르고 나이트캡을 쓴 유령 같은 그림자 하나가 법률 문구상을 따라 그가 나온 방으로 가더니 더 위층으로 스르르 올라간다."], ["And henceforth he begins, go where he will, to be attended by another shadow than his own, hardly less constant than his own, hardly less quiet than his own.", "그리고 이제부터 그는, 어디를 가든, 제 그림자가 아닌 또 다른 그림자의 시중을 받기 시작하는데, 제 그림자에 못지않게 한결같고, 제 그림자에 못지않게 조용한 그림자다."], ["And into whatsoever atmosphere of secrecy his own shadow may pass, let all concerned in the secrecy beware!", "그리고 그의 그림자가 어떤 비밀의 기운 속으로 들어가든, 그 비밀에 얽힌 모든 이들은 조심할지어다!"], ["For the watchful Mrs. Snagsby is there too—bone of his bone, flesh of his flesh, shadow of his shadow.", "경계를 늦추지 않는 스내그스비 부인 또한 거기 있을 테니—그의 뼈 중의 뼈요, 그의 살 중의 살이요, 그의 그림자 중의 그림자가."]], [["Sharpshooters\nWintry morning, looking with dull eyes and sallow face upon the neighbourhood of Leicester Square, finds its inhabitants unwilling to get out of bed.", "겨울 아침이 흐릿한 눈과 누르스름한 얼굴로 레스터 광장 일대를 굽어보는데, 그곳 주민들은 좀체 잠자리에서 빠져나오려 하지 않는다."], ["Many of them are not early risers at the brightest of times, being birds of night who roost when the sun is high and are wide awake and keen for prey when the stars shine out.", "그들 가운데 상당수는 아무리 환한 시각에도 일찍 일어나는 법이 없으니, 해가 높이 뜨면 둥지에 깃들고 별이 반짝일 때면 두 눈 부릅뜨고 먹잇감을 노리는 밤의 새들인 까닭이다."], ["Behind dingy blind and curtain, in upper story and garret, skulking more or less under false names, false hair, false titles, false jewellery, and false histories, a colony of brigands lie in their first sleep.", "거무튀튀한 차양과 커튼 뒤, 위층과 다락방에서, 가짜 이름과 가짜 머리카락과 가짜 작위와 가짜 보석과 가짜 내력 아래 어지간히 몸을 숨긴 채, 한 무리의 산적 떼가 첫잠에 빠져 있다."], ["Gentlemen of the green-baize road who could discourse from personal experience of foreign galleys and home treadmills; spies of strong governments that eternally quake with weakness and miserable fear, broken traitors, cowards, bullies, gamesters, shufflers, swindlers, and false witnesses; some not unmarked by the branding-iron beneath their dirty braid; all with more cruelty in them than was in Nero, and more crime than is in Newgate.", "외국의 갤리선과 본국의 디딜방아를 몸소 겪은 경험담을 풀어놓을 만한 노름판의 신사들, 허약함과 비참한 두려움에 영영 떠는 강성 정부의 첩자들, 망가진 반역자와 겁쟁이와 깡패와 노름꾼과 사기꾼과 협잡꾼과 위증꾼, 그중 더러는 더러운 장식끈 밑에 낙인의 자국이 또렷하다. 모두가 네로보다 더한 잔혹을 품었고, 뉴게이트 감옥보다 더한 죄악을 품었다."], ["For howsoever bad the devil can be in fustian or smock-frock (and he can be very bad in both), he is a more designing, callous, and intolerable devil when he sticks a pin in his shirt-front, calls himself a gentleman, backs a card or colour, plays a game or so of billiards, and knows a little about bills and promissory notes, than in any other form he wears.", "무명베나 작업복 차림의 악마가 제아무리 악할 수 있다 해도(둘 다 입어도 무척 악할 수 있다), 셔츠 앞섶에 핀을 꽂고 자신을 신사라 일컫고 카드나 패에 돈을 걸고 당구 한두 판을 치고 어음과 약속어음을 조금 아는 그런 악마야말로, 다른 어떤 모습으로 나타날 때보다 더 음흉하고 더 냉혹하고 더 견딜 수 없는 악마다."], ["And in such form Mr. Bucket shall find him, when he will, still pervading the tributary channels of Leicester Square.", "버킷 씨는 그런 모습으로, 마음만 먹으면 언제든, 레스터 광장으로 흘러드는 곁가지 물길에 여전히 스며 있는 그를 찾아낼 것이다."]], [["But the wintry morning wants him not and wakes him not. It wakes Mr.", "그러나 겨울 아침은 그를 원하지 않고 그를 깨우지도 않는다."], ["George of the shooting gallery and his familiar.", "겨울 아침은 사격장의 조지 씨와 그의 단짝을 깨운다."], ["They arise, roll up and stow away their mattresses.", "둘은 일어나 매트리스를 둘둘 말아 치워 둔다."], ["Mr. George, having shaved himself before a looking-glass of minute proportions, then marches out, bare-headed and bare-chested, to the pump in the little yard and anon comes back shining with yellow soap, friction, drifting rain, and exceedingly cold water.", "조지 씨는 자그마한 거울 앞에서 면도를 마친 뒤, 맨머리에 맨가슴으로 작은 마당의 펌프로 성큼성큼 걸어 나가고, 이윽고 노란 비누와 마찰과 흩날리는 빗방울과 더없이 차가운 물로 번들거리며 돌아온다."], ["As he rubs himself upon a large jack-towel, blowing like a military sort of diver just come up, his hair curling tighter and tighter on his sunburnt temples the more he rubs it so that it looks as if it never could be loosened by any less coercive instrument than an iron rake or a curry-comb—as he rubs, and puffs, and polishes, and blows, turning his head from side to side the more conveniently to excoriate his throat, and standing with his body well bent forward to keep the wet from his martial legs, Phil, on his knees lighting a fire, looks round as if it were enough washing for him to see all that done, and sufficient renovation for one day to take in the superfluous health his master throws off.", "큼직한 거친 수건에 몸을 문지르며, 방금 물 위로 올라온 군대식 잠수부처럼 푸푸 숨을 내뿜으며, 문지를수록 햇볕에 그을린 관자놀이의 머리칼이 점점 더 단단히 곱슬거려서 쇠갈퀴나 빗솔 같은 억센 도구가 아니고서는 도무지 풀어질 성싶지 않은 그 머리칼을 문지르며 — 문지르고, 헐떡이고, 닦고, 숨을 내뿜으며, 목을 더 편하게 박박 문지르려고 고개를 이쪽저쪽으로 돌리며, 군인다운 다리에 물이 튀지 않게 몸을 잔뜩 앞으로 굽힌 채 서 있는 동안, 무릎을 꿇고 불을 붙이던 필은 마치 그 광경을 보는 것만으로 제 몸 씻기는 충분하다는 듯, 그리고 제 주인이 내던지는 남아도는 건강을 받아들이는 것만으로 하루치 활력 회복은 넉넉하다는 듯 둘레를 돌아본다."]], [["When Mr. George is dry, he goes to work to brush his head with two hard brushes at once, to that unmerciful degree that Phil, shouldering his way round the gallery in the act of sweeping it, winks with sympathy.", "조지 씨는 몸이 마르면 단단한 솔 두 개로 한꺼번에 머리를 빗어 대는데, 그 모습이 어찌나 가차 없는지, 사격장을 쓸며 어깨로 비집고 돌아다니던 필이 동정 어린 눈을 찡긋한다."], ["This chafing over, the ornamental part of Mr. George’s toilet is soon performed.", "이 박박 비비기가 끝나면 조지 씨의 몸단장에서 멋 내는 부분은 금세 마무리된다."], ["He fills his pipe, lights it, and marches up and down smoking, as his custom is, while Phil, raising a powerful odour of hot rolls and coffee, prepares breakfast.", "그는 파이프에 담배를 채워 불을 붙이고, 늘 그러듯 담배를 피우며 위아래로 성큼성큼 거니는데, 그동안 필은 뜨거운 빵과 커피의 짙은 냄새를 풍기며 아침을 준비한다."], ["He smokes gravely and marches in slow time.", "그는 진지하게 담배를 피우며 느린 박자로 걷는다."], ["Perhaps this morning’s pipe is devoted to the memory of Gridley in his grave.", "어쩌면 오늘 아침의 파이프는 무덤 속 그리들리를 추모하는 데 바쳐졌는지도 모른다."]], [["“And so, Phil,”", "\"그래서 말이지, 필,\""], ["says George of the shooting gallery after several turns in silence, “you were dreaming of the country last night?”", "한참을 말없이 거닐던 사격장의 조지가 입을 연다. \"어젯밤에 시골 꿈을 꿨다고?\""]], [["Phil, by the by, said as much in a tone of surprise as he scrambled out of bed.", "그러고 보니 필은 잠자리에서 허둥지둥 기어 나오면서 놀란 어조로 그렇게 말했었다."]], [["“Yes, guv’ner.”", "\"네, 두목님.\""]], [["“What was it like?”", "\"어떤 모습이었나?\""]], [["“I hardly know what it was like, guv’ner,”", "\"어떤 모습이었는지는 저도 잘 모르겠습니다, 두목님.\""], ["said Phil, considering.", "필이 곰곰이 생각하며 말한다."]], [["“How did you know it was the country?”", "\"그럼 시골인 줄은 어떻게 알았어?\""]], [["“On account of the grass, I think.", "\"풀밭 때문인 것 같습니다."], ["And the swans upon it,”", "그리고 그 위에 있던 백조들 때문이고요.\""], ["says Phil after further consideration.", "한참 더 생각한 끝에 필이 말한다."]], [["“What were the swans doing on the grass?”", "\"백조들이 풀밭에서 뭘 하고 있었는데?\""]], [["“They was a-eating of it, I expect,”", "\"풀을 뜯어 먹고 있었던 것 같습니다.\""], ["says Phil.", "필이 말한다."]], [["The master resumes his march, and the man resumes his preparation of breakfast.", "주인은 다시 걷기 시작하고, 부하는 다시 아침 준비에 매달린다."], ["It is not necessarily a lengthened preparation, being limited to the setting forth of very simple breakfast requisites for two and the broiling of a rasher of bacon at the fire in the rusty grate; but as Phil has to sidle round a considerable part of the gallery for every object he wants, and never brings two objects at once, it takes time under the circumstances.", "그것이 꼭 긴 준비라야 하는 건 아니니, 두 사람 몫의 아주 단출한 아침 식사 도구를 차려 놓고 녹슨 화격자의 불에 베이컨 한 조각을 굽는 일로 그치기 때문이다. 하지만 필은 필요한 물건 하나하나를 가지러 사격장을 거의 한 바퀴씩 옆걸음으로 돌아야 하고, 한 번에 두 가지를 결코 가져오는 법이 없으니, 사정이 이런지라 시간이 걸린다."], ["At length the breakfast is ready.", "마침내 아침이 차려진다."], ["Phil announcing it, Mr. George knocks the ashes out of his pipe on the hob, stands his pipe itself in the chimney corner, and sits down to the meal.", "필이 그것을 알리자, 조지 씨는 화로 선반에 파이프를 두드려 재를 털고, 파이프 자체는 굴뚝 모퉁이에 세워 둔 다음, 식사 자리에 앉는다."], ["When he has helped himself, Phil follows suit, sitting at the extreme end of the little oblong table and taking his plate on his knees.", "그가 제 몫을 덜고 나면, 필도 그를 따라 작은 직사각형 식탁의 맨 끝에 앉아 접시를 무릎 위에 올려놓는다."], ["Either in humility, or to hide his blackened hands, or because it is his natural manner of eating.", "겸손해서인지, 아니면 새까매진 손을 감추려는 것인지, 아니면 그것이 그가 밥 먹는 타고난 방식이어서인지."]], [["“The country,” says Mr.", "\"시골이라.\""], ["George, plying his knife and fork; “why, I suppose you never clapped your eyes on the country, Phil?”", "나이프와 포크를 놀리며 조지 씨가 말한다. \"이봐, 자넨 시골이라곤 한 번도 구경한 적이 없을 것 같은데, 필?\""]], [["“I see the marshes once,”", "\"습지는 한 번 봤습니다.\""], ["says Phil, contentedly eating his breakfast.", "필이 흡족하게 아침을 먹으며 말한다."]], [["“What marshes?”", "\"무슨 습지?\""]], [["“THE marshes, commander,”", "\"그 습지요, 사령관님.\""], ["returns Phil.", "필이 대답한다."]], [["“Where are they?”", "\"그게 어디 있는데?\""]], [["“I don’t know where they are,”", "\"어디 있는지는 모릅니다.\" 필이 말한다."], ["says Phil; “but I see ’em, guv’ner.", "\"하지만 봤습니다, 두목님. 평평했어요."], ["They was flat. And miste.”", "그리고 안개가 자욱했고요.\""]], [["Governor and commander are interchangeable terms with Phil, expressive of the same respect and deference and applicable to nobody but Mr. George.", "두목이니 사령관이니 하는 말은 필에게 서로 바꿔 쓸 수 있는 호칭으로, 똑같은 존경과 공경을 나타내며 조지 씨 말고는 그 누구에게도 쓰이지 않는다."]], [["“I was born in the country, Phil.”", "\"난 시골에서 태어났어, 필.\""]], [["“Was you indeed, commander?”", "\"정말이십니까, 사령관님?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["And bred there.”", "거기서 자랐지.\""]], [["Phil elevates his one eyebrow, and after respectfully staring at his master to express interest, swallows a great gulp of coffee, still staring at him.", "필은 한쪽 눈썹을 치켜올리고, 관심을 드러내려 공손히 주인을 빤히 쳐다본 다음, 여전히 그를 응시하며 커피를 크게 한 모금 꿀꺽 들이켠다."]], [["“There’s not a bird’s note that I don’t know,”", "\"내가 모르는 새소리란 없지.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“Not many an English leaf or berry that I couldn’t name.", "\"이름을 못 댈 영국 나뭇잎이나 열매도 별로 없고."], ["Not many a tree that I couldn’t climb yet if I was put to it.", "마음만 먹으면 지금도 못 오를 나무가 별로 없어."], ["I was a real country boy, once.", "한때 난 진짜 시골 아이였어."], ["My good mother lived in the country.”", "우리 착한 어머니가 시골에 사셨거든.\""]], [["“She must have been a fine old lady, guv’ner,”", "\"훌륭한 노부인이셨겠습니다, 두목님.\""], ["Phil observes.", "필이 한마디 한다."]], [["“Aye!", "\"암!"], ["And not so old either, five and thirty years ago,”", "그것도 삼십오 년 전엔 그리 늙으시지도 않았고.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“But I’ll wager that at ninety she would be near as upright as me, and near as broad across the shoulders.”", "\"하지만 장담컨대 아흔이 되셔도 나만큼이나 꼿꼿하고, 나만큼이나 어깨가 떡 벌어지셨을 거야.\""]], [["“Did she die at ninety, guv’ner?”", "\"아흔에 돌아가셨습니까, 두목님?\""], ["inquires Phil.", "필이 묻는다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["Bosh!", "헛소리!"], ["Let her rest in peace, God bless her!”", "부디 편히 잠드시기를, 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\""], ["says the trooper.", "기병이 말한다."], ["“What set me on about country boys, and runaways, and good-for-nothings?", "\"대체 뭣 때문에 시골 아이니 가출이니 쓸모없는 놈들이니 하는 얘기를 꺼냈을까?"], ["You, to be sure!", "바로 자네 때문이지, 암!"], ["So you never clapped your eyes upon the country—marshes and dreams excepted.", "그래서 자넨 습지하고 꿈 빼고는 시골이라곤 한 번도 구경 못 했단 말이지."], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["Phil shakes his head.", "필이 고개를 가로젓는다."]], [["“Do you want to see it?”", "\"보고 싶나?\""]], [["“N-no, I don’t know as I do, particular,”", "\"아-아니요, 딱히 그런 건 모르겠습니다.\""], ["says Phil.", "필이 말한다."]], [["“The town’s enough for you, eh?”", "\"도시면 충분하다 이거지, 응?\""]], [["“Why, you see, commander,”", "\"그게, 사령관님,\""], ["says Phil, “I ain’t acquainted with anythink else, and I doubt if I ain’t a-getting too old to take to novelties.”", "필이 말한다. \"저는 다른 건 아는 게 없고, 게다가 새로운 걸 익히기엔 너무 늙어 가는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“How old ARE you, Phil?”", "\"자넨 대체 몇 살인가, 필?\""], ["asks the trooper, pausing as he conveys his smoking saucer to his lips.", "김이 나는 받침 접시를 입으로 가져가다 말고 기병이 묻는다."]], [["“I’m something with a eight in it,”", "\"여덟이 들어가는 어떤 숫자입니다.\""], ["says Phil.", "필이 말한다."], ["“It can’t be eighty.", "\"여든은 아니고요."], ["Nor yet eighteen.", "그렇다고 열여덟도 아니고."], ["It’s betwixt ’em, somewheres.”", "그 사이 어디쯤입니다.\""]], [["Mr. George, slowly putting down his saucer without tasting its contents, is laughingly beginning, “Why, what the deuce, Phil—”", "조지 씨는 받침 접시를 맛도 보지 않은 채 천천히 내려놓으며 웃음 섞인 목소리로 \"이런, 대체 무슨 소리야, 필—\""], ["when he stops, seeing that Phil is counting on his dirty fingers.", "하고 입을 떼다가, 필이 더러운 손가락을 꼽으며 셈하는 것을 보고 멈춘다."]], [["“I was just eight,” says Phil, “agreeable to the parish calculation, when I went with the tinker. I was sent on a errand, and I see him a-sittin under a old buildin with a fire all to himself wery comfortable, and he says, ‘Would you like to come along a me, my man?’ I says ‘Yes,’ and him and me and the fire goes home to Clerkenwell together. That was April Fool Day. I was able to count up to ten; and when April Fool Day come round again, I says to myself, ‘Now, old chap, you’re one and a eight in it.’ April Fool Day after that, I says, ‘Now, old chap, you’re two and a eight in it.’ In course of time, I come to ten and a eight in it; two tens and a eight in it. When it got so high, it got the upper hand of me, but this is how I always know there’s a eight in it.”", "\"제가 땜장이를 따라갔을 때, 교구 셈법으로 딱 여덟이었습니다.\" 필이 말한다. \"심부름을 가던 길에, 그 사람이 낡은 건물 밑에서 혼자 불을 쬐며 아주 편안하게 앉아 있는 걸 봤죠. 그가 '이봐 젊은이, 나하고 같이 갈 텐가?' 하길래 제가 '예' 했고, 그 사람하고 저하고 그 불하고 함께 클러큰웰의 집으로 갔습니다. 그게 만우절이었어요. 저는 열까지 셀 줄 알았는데, 만우절이 다시 돌아왔을 때 속으로 그랬죠. '자, 이 친구야, 넌 이제 하나에 여덟이 들어가는 나이다.' 그다음 만우절엔 그랬고요. '자, 이 친구야, 넌 이제 둘에 여덟이 들어가는 나이다.' 그러다 보니 열에 여덟이 들어가는 나이가 됐고, 두 번의 열에 여덟이 들어가는 나이가 됐죠. 그게 너무 높이 올라가니까 제 머리로 감당이 안 됐는데, 어쨌든 그래서 여덟이 들어간다는 건 늘 압니다.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["says Mr. George, resuming his breakfast.", "조지 씨가 아침 식사를 다시 시작하며 말한다."], ["“And where’s the tinker?”", "\"그래, 그 땜장이는 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Drink put him in the hospital, guv’ner, and the hospital put him—in a glass-case, I HAVE heerd,”", "\"술 때문에 병원에 들어갔고, 병원이 그 사람을—유리 상자에 넣었다고, 그렇게 들었습니다.\""], ["Phil replies mysteriously.", "필이 알쏭달쏭하게 대답한다."]], [["“By that means you got promotion?", "\"그래서 자넨 승진했군?"], ["Took the business, Phil?”", "사업을 물려받았나, 필?\""]], [["“Yes, commander, I took the business.", "\"네, 사령관님, 사업을 물려받았죠."], ["Such as it was.", "뭐 그런 게 사업이라면요."], ["It wasn’t much of a beat—round Saffron Hill, Hatton Garden, Clerkenwell, Smiffeld, and there—poor neighbourhood, where they uses up the kettles till they’re past mending.", "그리 대단한 구역도 아니었습니다—새프런 힐, 해튼 가든, 클러큰웰, 스미스필드 그 일대로, 가난한 동네라 다 떨어질 때까지 주전자를 쓰는 곳이었죠."], ["Most of the tramping tinkers used to come and lodge at our place; that was the best part of my master’s earnings.", "떠돌이 땜장이 대부분이 우리 집에 와서 묵었는데, 그게 우리 주인 벌이의 가장 좋은 부분이었습니다."], ["But they didn’t come to me. I warn’t like him.", "그런데 그 사람들이 저한테는 안 왔어요."], ["He could sing ’em a good song.", "저는 그 사람 같지가 않았거든요."], ["I couldn’t! He could play ’em a tune on any sort of pot you please, so as it was iron or block tin.", "그 사람은 그들한테 노래를 멋들어지게 불러 줄 수 있었지만, 저는 못 했죠!"], ["I never could do nothing with a pot but mend it or bile it—never had a note of music in me.", "그 사람은 쇠나 양철로 된 거라면 무슨 냄비든 곡조를 뽑아냈지만, 저는 냄비로는 때우거나 물 끓이는 것 말고는 아무것도 못 했어요—음악적 재주라곤 타고난 게 없었으니까요."], ["Besides, I was too ill-looking, and their wives complained of me.”", "게다가 저는 너무 못생겨서, 그 사람들 아내들이 저를 두고 불평했습니다.\""]], [["“They were mighty particular.", "\"그 사람들 참 까다롭군."], ["You would pass muster in a crowd, Phil!”", "군중 속에선 자네도 충분히 무난히 통할 텐데, 필!\""], ["says the trooper with a pleasant smile.", "기병이 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 말한다."]], [["“No, guv’ner,”", "\"아닙니다, 두목님.\""], ["returns Phil, shaking his head.", "필이 고개를 저으며 대답한다."], ["“No, I shouldn’t.", "\"아닙니다, 그렇지 않을 겁니다."], ["I was passable enough when I went with the tinker, though nothing to boast of then; but what with blowing the fire with my mouth when I was young, and spileing my complexion, and singeing my hair off, and swallering the smoke, and what with being nat’rally unfort’nate in the way of running against hot metal and marking myself by sich means, and what with having turn-ups with the tinker as I got older, almost whenever he was too far gone in drink—which was almost always—my beauty was queer, wery queer, even at that time.", "땜장이를 따라갔을 땐 그럭저럭 봐줄 만했죠, 뭐 그때도 자랑할 정도는 아니었지만요. 그런데 어릴 적에 입으로 불을 불어 대다 얼굴빛을 망치고, 머리카락을 그슬리고, 연기를 들이마시고, 게다가 뜨거운 쇠붙이에 부딪쳐 그 자국을 내고 다니는 게 타고난 불운이었고, 또 나이가 좀 들면서 땜장이가 술에 너무 곯아떨어졌을 때마다—거의 늘 그랬지만—주먹다짐을 벌이고 한 탓에, 그때부터 이미 제 미모가 묘했죠, 아주 묘했어요."], ["As to since, what with a dozen years in a dark forge where the men was given to larking, and what with being scorched in a accident at a gas-works, and what with being blowed out of winder case-filling at the firework business, I am ugly enough to be made a show on!”", "그 뒤로는 또 어떻고요. 사내들이 장난질을 일삼던 어두운 대장간에서 십수 년을 보낸 데다, 가스 공장 사고에서 그슬리고, 또 불꽃놀이 사업에서 화약 채우다 창밖으로 나가떨어진 통에, 저는 구경거리로 내세워도 될 만큼 추하게 됐습니다!\""]], [["Resigning himself to which condition with a perfectly satisfied manner, Phil begs the favour of another cup of coffee.", "그런 처지에 더없이 만족한 태도로 체념하며, 필은 커피 한 잔을 더 청한다. 그것을 마시며 그가 말한다."], ["While drinking it, he says, “It was after the case-filling blow-up when I first see you, commander.", "\"화약 채우다 터진 사고가 있고 나서, 제가 사령관님을 처음 봤죠."], ["You remember?”", "기억하십니까?\""]], [["“I remember, Phil.", "\"기억하지, 필."], ["You were walking along in the sun.”", "자넨 햇빛 속을 걸어가고 있었어.\""]], [["“Crawling, guv’ner, again a wall—”", "\"기어가고 있었습니다, 두목님, 벽에 붙어서—\""]], [["“True, Phil—shouldering your way on—”", "\"맞아, 필—어깨로 비집으며 나아가고 있었지—\""]], [["“In a night-cap!”", "\"잠자리 모자를 쓰고요!\""], ["exclaims Phil, excited.", "필이 흥분하여 외친다."]], [["“In a night-cap—”", "\"잠자리 모자를 쓰고—\""]], [["“And hobbling with a couple of sticks!”", "\"그리고 지팡이 두 개로 절뚝거리며요!\""], ["cries Phil, still more excited.", "필이 더욱 흥분하여 소리친다."]], [["“With a couple of sticks.", "\"지팡이 두 개로."], ["When—”", "그때—\""]], [["“When you stops, you know,”", "\"그때 사령관님이 멈춰 서시더니,\""], ["cries Phil, putting down his cup and saucer and hastily removing his plate from his knees, “and says to me, ‘What, comrade!", "필이 컵과 받침 접시를 내려놓고 무릎의 접시를 황급히 치우며 외친다. \"저한테 이러셨죠."], ["You have been in the wars!’ I didn’t say much to you, commander, then, for I was took by surprise that a person so strong and healthy and bold as you was should stop to speak to such a limping bag of bones as I was.", "'이런, 전우여! 자네 전쟁터에 다녀왔구먼!'"], ["But you says to me, says you, delivering it out of your chest as hearty as possible, so that it was like a glass of something hot, ‘What accident have you met with?", "저는 그때 사령관님한테 별말 못 했습니다, 사령관님처럼 그렇게 튼튼하고 건강하고 대담한 분이 저 같은 절뚝거리는 뼈다귀 자루한테 말을 걸려고 멈춰 서다니 깜짝 놀랐거든요. 그런데 사령관님은 가슴에서 우러나오듯 더없이 다정하게, 마치 뜨거운 무언가 한 잔처럼 이렇게 말씀하셨죠."], ["You have been badly hurt.", "'무슨 사고를 당한 건가? 심하게 다쳤구먼."], ["What’s amiss, old boy?", "어찌 된 일이야, 이 친구야?"], ["Cheer up, and tell us about it!’ Cheer up!", "기운 내고 어디 얘기해 보게!' 기운 내라니요!"], ["I was cheered already!", "저는 이미 기운이 났습니다!"], ["I says as much to you, you says more to me, I says more to you, you says more to me, and here I am, commander! Here I am, commander!”", "제가 사령관님한테 그만큼 말했고, 사령관님이 저한테 더 말씀하셨고, 제가 사령관님한테 더 말했고, 사령관님이 저한테 더 말씀하셨고, 그래서 제가 여기 있습니다, 사령관님!"], ["cries Phil, who has started from his chair and unaccountably begun to sidle away.", "제가 여기 있습니다, 사령관님!\" 필은 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 영문 모를 옆걸음질을 치며 외친다."], ["“If a mark’s wanted, or if it will improve the business, let the customers take aim at me.", "\"과녁이 필요하다면, 아니면 그게 사업에 도움이 된다면, 손님들더러 저를 겨냥하라고 하십쇼."], ["They can’t spoil MY beauty.", "그들이 제 미모는 망치지 못합니다."], ["I’M all right.", "저는 끄떡없어요."], ["Come on!", "자, 덤벼라!"], ["If they want a man to box at, let ’em box at me.", "권투 상대가 필요하다면, 저한테 권투하라고 하십쇼."], ["Let ’em knock me well about the head.", "제 머리통을 실컷 갈겨도 됩니다."], ["I don’t mind.", "전 상관없어요."], ["If they want a light-weight to be throwed for practice, Cornwall, Devonshire, or Lancashire, let ’em throw me.", "콘월식이든 데번셔식이든 랭커셔식이든, 연습용으로 메다꽂을 가벼운 상대가 필요하다면, 저를 던지라고 하십쇼."], ["They won’t hurt ME.", "저는 안 다칩니다."], ["I have been throwed, all sorts of styles, all my life!”", "저는 평생토록 온갖 방식으로 메다꽂혀 봤거든요!\""]], [["With this unexpected speech, energetically delivered and accompanied by action illustrative of the various exercises referred to, Phil Squod shoulders his way round three sides of the gallery, and abruptly tacking off at his commander, makes a butt at him with his head, intended to express devotion to his service.", "이 뜻밖의 연설을 기운차게 늘어놓으며, 언급한 갖가지 운동을 흉내 내는 동작까지 곁들이며, 필 스쿼드는 사격장의 세 면을 어깨로 비집고 돌더니, 느닷없이 사령관 쪽으로 방향을 틀어 머리로 그를 들이받는 시늉을 하는데, 그건 충성을 표하려는 것이다."], ["He then begins to clear away the breakfast.", "그러고는 아침상을 치우기 시작한다."]], [["Mr. George, after laughing cheerfully and clapping him on the shoulder, assists in these arrangements and helps to get the gallery into business order.", "조지 씨는 유쾌하게 웃으며 그의 어깨를 두드려 준 다음, 정리를 거들고 사격장을 영업할 수 있게 갖추도록 돕는다."], ["That done, he takes a turn at the dumb-bells, and afterwards weighing himself and opining that he is getting “too fleshy,” engages with great gravity in solitary broadsword practice.", "그것이 끝나면 그는 아령을 한 차례 들고, 이어 체중을 재 보고는 자신이 \"너무 살이 찌고 있다\"고 진단하며 더없이 진지하게 혼자 검술 연습에 몰두한다."], ["Meanwhile Phil has fallen to work at his usual table, where he screws and unscrews, and cleans, and files, and whistles into small apertures, and blackens himself more and more, and seems to do and undo everything that can be done and undone about a gun.", "그동안 필은 늘 쓰는 작업대에 들러붙어 나사를 조였다 풀었다 하고, 닦고, 줄질하고, 작은 구멍에 휘파람을 불어 넣고, 점점 더 새까매지며, 총에 관해 할 수 있는 일과 되돌릴 수 있는 일이라면 죄다 하고 또 되돌리는 듯하다."]], [["Master and man are at length disturbed by footsteps in the passage, where they make an unusual sound, denoting the arrival of unusual company.", "마침내 주인과 부하는 복도에서 들려오는 발소리에 방해받는데, 그 소리는 예사롭지 않게 들려 예사롭지 않은 손님의 도착을 알린다. 점점 사격장에 가까워지는 그 발소리는, 첫눈에 보기에 일 년 중 십일월 오일* 말고는 도무지 어울리지 않을 한 무리를 끌고 들어온다."], ["These steps, advancing nearer and nearer to the gallery, bring into it a group at first sight scarcely reconcilable with any day in the year but the fifth of November.", "* 십일월 오일: '가이 포크스의 날'. 1605년 의회 폭파 음모(화약 음모 사건)가 발각된 것을 기념해 영국에서 모닥불을 피우고 불꽃놀이를 하며 가이 포크스의 인형을 태우는 날."]], [["It consists of a limp and ugly figure carried in a chair by two bearers and attended by a lean female with a face like a pinched mask, who might be expected immediately to recite the popular verses commemorative of the time when they did contrive to blow Old England up alive but for her keeping her lips tightly and defiantly closed as the chair is put down.", "그 무리는 가마꾼 둘이 의자에 메고 온 흐물흐물하고 추한 형체 하나와, 그를 시중드는, 쪼그라든 가면 같은 얼굴의 깡마른 여자 하나로 이루어져 있다. 그 여자는 이제라도 당장, 그들이 늙은 잉글랜드를 산 채로 날려 버리려 꾸몄던 그 시절을 기념하는 그 유명한 시구를 읊을 듯하건만, 의자가 내려놓이는 순간까지 입술을 굳게 다물고 도전적으로 다물고 있어 그러질 않는다."], ["At which point the figure in it gasping, “O Lord!", "그 대목에서 의자에 앉은 형체가 헐떡인다."], ["Oh, dear me!", "\"오, 맙소사!"], ["I am shaken!”", "아이고, 이런!"], ["adds, “How de do, my dear friend, how de do?”", "흔들려서 죽겠네!\" 그러고는 덧붙인다."], ["Mr. George then descries, in the procession, the venerable Mr.", "\"잘 지냈나, 친애하는 벗이여, 잘 지냈어?\""], ["Smallweed out for an airing, attended by his granddaughter Judy as body-guard.", "그제야 조지 씨는 그 행렬 속에서, 바람 쐬러 나온 그 존귀하신 스몰위드 씨와, 그의 호위병 격으로 시중드는 손녀 주디를 알아본다."]], [["“Mr. George, my dear friend,”", "\"조지 씨, 친애하는 벗이여,\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, removing his right arm from the neck of one of his bearers, whom he has nearly throttled coming along, “how de do?", "오는 길에 거의 목을 졸라 댄 가마꾼 하나의 목에서 오른팔을 떼며 스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"잘 지냈나?"], ["You’re surprised to see me, my dear friend.”", "나를 보니 놀랐지, 친애하는 벗이여.\""]], [["“I should hardly have been more surprised to have seen your friend in the city,”", "\"댁의 그 친구를 도심에서 만났대도 이보다 더 놀라진 않았겠습니다.\""], ["returns Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 대답한다."]], [["“I am very seldom out,”", "\"나는 좀처럼 밖에 안 나오지.\""], ["pants Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 헐떡인다."], ["“I haven’t been out for many months.", "\"몇 달째 나오질 않았어."], ["It’s inconvenient—and it comes expensive.", "불편하기도 하고—비용도 들고."], ["But I longed so much to see you, my dear Mr. George.", "하지만 자네가 어찌나 보고 싶던지, 친애하는 조지 씨."], ["How de do, sir?”", "잘 지냈나, 선생?\""]], [["“I am well enough,”", "\"그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“I hope you are the same.”", "\"당신도 마찬가지이길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“You can’t be too well, my dear friend.” Mr.", "\"자넨 아무리 잘 지내도 모자라지, 친애하는 벗이여.\""], ["Smallweed takes him by both hands.", "스몰위드 씨가 그의 두 손을 맞잡는다."], ["“I have brought my granddaughter Judy.", "\"내 손녀 주디를 데려왔네."], ["I couldn’t keep her away.", "도저히 떼어 놓을 수가 있어야지."], ["She longed so much to see you.”", "자네가 어찌나 보고 싶다던지.\""]], [["“Hum!", "\"흠!"], ["She bears it calmly!”", "무던하게도 잘 견디는군!\""], ["mutters Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 중얼거린다."]], [["“So we got a hackney-cab, and put a chair in it, and just round the corner they lifted me out of the cab and into the chair, and carried me here that I might see my dear friend in his own establishment! This,”", "\"그래서 우리는 삯마차를 한 대 잡아 거기에 의자를 싣고, 모퉁이를 막 돌아서 나를 마차에서 들어내 의자에 앉히고, 친애하는 벗을 그의 일터에서 볼 수 있도록 여기까지 메고 왔지!"], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, alluding to the bearer, who has been in danger of strangulation and who withdraws adjusting his windpipe, “is the driver of the cab.", "이 사람은,\" 목이 졸릴 뻔하여 숨통을 가다듬으며 물러나는 가마꾼을 가리키며 스몰위드 영감이 말한다."], ["He has nothing extra.", "\"마차 마부야."], ["It is by agreement included in his fare.", "따로 줄 건 없어. 약속대로 삯에 포함된 거니까."], ["This person,”", "이 사람은,\""], ["the other bearer, “we engaged in the street outside for a pint of beer.", "다른 가마꾼이다. \"맥주 한 파인트 값으로 길거리에서 고용했네."], ["Which is twopence.", "그게 이 펜스지."], ["Judy, give the person twopence.", "주디, 이 사람한테 이 펜스 주거라."], ["I was not sure you had a workman of your own here, my dear friend, or we needn’t have employed this person.”", "친애하는 벗이여, 여기 자네 일꾼이 있는지 몰라서 그랬어, 알았더라면 이 사람을 쓸 필요도 없었을 텐데.\""]], [["Grandfather Smallweed refers to Phil with a glance of considerable terror and a half-subdued “O Lord!", "스몰위드 영감은 상당히 겁에 질린 눈길과 반쯤 억눌린 \"오, 맙소사!"], ["Oh, dear me!”", "아이고, 이런!\"을 곁들여 필을 가리킨다."], ["Nor in his apprehension, on the surface of things, without some reason, for Phil, who has never beheld the apparition in the black-velvet cap before, has stopped short with a gun in his hand with much of the air of a dead shot intent on picking Mr. Smallweed off as an ugly old bird of the crow species.", "겉으로 보면 그가 겁먹은 데에 까닭이 없는 것도 아니니, 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 이 유령 같은 형체를 난생처음 본 필이, 손에 총을 든 채 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 까마귀 종류의 추한 늙은 새 한 마리 잡아 떨어뜨리겠다고 작정한 명사수의 분위기를 한껏 풍기고 있는 까닭이다."]], [["“Judy, my child,”", "\"주디, 얘야,\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, “give the person his twopence.", "스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"이 사람한테 이 펜스 주거라."], ["It’s a great deal for what he has done.”", "그가 한 일에 비하면 후한 거다.\""]], [["The person, who is one of those extraordinary specimens of human fungus that spring up spontaneously in the western streets of London, ready dressed in an old red jacket, with a “mission” for holding horses and calling coaches, received his twopence with anything but transport, tosses the money into the air, catches it over-handed, and retires.", "그 사람은 런던 서쪽 거리에서 절로 솟아나는 인간 곰팡이의 그 유별난 표본 가운데 하나로, 낡은 빨간 윗도리를 차려입고 말을 잡아 주거나 마차를 불러 주는 \"사명\"을 띤 자인데, 이 펜스를 도무지 기쁜 기색이라곤 없이 받아 들더니, 동전을 공중에 휙 던졌다가 손등으로 낚아챈 다음 물러난다."]], [["“My dear Mr. George,”", "\"친애하는 조지 씨,\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, “would you be so kind as help to carry me to the fire?", "스몰위드 영감이 말한다. \"나를 불 가까이로 메고 가도록 좀 도와주겠나?"], ["I am accustomed to a fire, and I am an old man, and I soon chill.", "나는 불에 익숙하고, 이렇게 늙은 몸이라 금세 으슬으슬해지거든."], ["Oh, dear me!”", "아이고, 이런!\""]], [["His closing exclamation is jerked out of the venerable gentleman by the suddenness with which Mr. Squod, like a genie, catches him up, chair and all, and deposits him on the hearth-stone.", "존귀하신 신사의 이 끝맺음 감탄사는, 스쿼드 씨가 마치 마신처럼 별안간 그를 의자째 번쩍 들어 화롯돌 위에 내려놓는 바람에 그의 입에서 튀어나온 것이다."]], [["“O Lord!” says Mr.", "\"오, 맙소사!\""], ["Smallweed, panting. “Oh, dear me!", "스몰위드 씨가 헐떡이며 말한다."], ["Oh, my stars!", "\"아이고, 이런!"], ["My dear friend, your workman is very strong—and very prompt.", "아이고, 별들이여! 친애하는 벗이여, 자네 일꾼은 참 힘이 세군—게다가 아주 잽싸고."], ["O Lord, he is very prompt!", "오, 맙소사, 정말 잽싸구먼!"], ["Judy, draw me back a little.", "주디, 나를 좀 뒤로 빼다오."], ["I’m being scorched in the legs,”", "다리가 그슬리고 있어.\""], ["which indeed is testified to the noses of all present by the smell of his worsted stockings.", "과연 그의 털양말 타는 냄새가 그 자리에 있는 모두의 코에 그것을 입증한다."]], [["The gentle Judy, having backed her grandfather a little way from the fire, and having shaken him up as usual, and having released his overshadowed eye from its black-velvet extinguisher, Mr.", "상냥한 주디가 할아버지를 불에서 조금 뒤로 빼고, 늘 그러듯 그를 흔들어 추슬러 주고, 검은 벨벳 모자라는 끄개에 가려 그늘진 눈을 풀어 주자, 스몰위드 씨는 다시 \"아이고, 이런!"], ["Smallweed again says, “Oh, dear me! O Lord!”", "오, 맙소사!\""], ["and looking about and meeting Mr. George’s glance, again stretches out both hands.", "하더니 둘레를 둘러보다 조지 씨의 눈길과 마주치자 다시 두 손을 내민다."]], [["“My dear friend!", "\"친애하는 벗이여!"], ["So happy in this meeting!", "이렇게 만나다니 정말 기쁘군!"], ["And this is your establishment?", "그래, 이게 자네 일터인가?"], ["It’s a delightful place.", "멋진 곳이야."], ["It’s a picture!", "그림 같구먼."], ["You never find that anything goes off here accidentally, do you, my dear friend?”", "여기선 뭐가 우발적으로 발사되는 일은 결코 없겠지, 안 그런가, 친애하는 벗이여?\""], ["adds Grandfather Smallweed, very ill at ease.", "스몰위드 영감이 몹시 불안해하며 덧붙인다."]], [["“No, no.", "\"없죠, 없습니다."], ["No fear of that.”", "그럴 염려는 없습니다.\""]], [["“And your workman.", "\"그리고 자네 일꾼 말이야."], ["He—Oh, dear me!—he never lets anything off without meaning it, does he, my dear friend?”", "그가—아이고, 이런!—마음먹지 않고서 뭘 발사하는 일은 결코 없겠지, 안 그런가, 친애하는 벗이여?\""]], [["“He has never hurt anybody but himself,”", "\"그는 자기 자신 말고는 누굴 다치게 한 적이 없습니다.\""], ["says Mr. George, smiling.", "조지 씨가 미소 지으며 말한다."]], [["“But he might, you know.", "\"하지만 다치게 할 수도 있지 않나."], ["He seems to have hurt himself a good deal, and he might hurt somebody else,”", "자기 몸은 꽤나 많이 다친 것 같으니, 남도 다치게 할 수 있겠어.\""], ["the old gentleman returns.", "노신사가 대꾸한다."], ["“He mightn’t mean it—or he even might. Mr.", "\"마음먹지 않고서도—아니, 마음먹고서라도 말이야."], ["George, will you order him to leave his infernal fire-arms alone and go away?”", "조지 씨, 그더러 그 망할 총기는 좀 내버려 두고 저리 가 있으라고 명령해 주겠나?\""]], [["Obedient to a nod from the trooper, Phil retires, empty-handed, to the other end of the gallery.", "기병의 고갯짓에 따라, 필은 빈손으로 사격장 저쪽 끝으로 물러난다."], ["Mr. Smallweed, reassured, falls to rubbing his legs.", "안심한 스몰위드 씨는 다리를 문지르기 시작한다."]], [["“And you’re doing well, Mr. George?”", "\"그래, 조지 씨, 일은 잘되고 있나?\""], ["he says to the trooper, squarely standing faced about towards him with his broadsword in his hand.", "그가 손에 폭넓은 검을 든 채 자기 쪽으로 똑바로 돌아서 있는 기병에게 말한다."], ["“You are prospering, please the Powers?”", "\"신의 가호로, 번창하고 있나?\""]], [["Mr. George answers with a cool nod, adding, “Go on.", "조지 씨는 냉랭하게 고갯짓으로 대답하며 덧붙인다."], ["You have not come to say that, I know.”", "\"어서 말하시죠. 그 말 하러 오신 게 아닌 줄은 압니다.\""]], [["“You are so sprightly, Mr. George,”", "\"자넨 참 활기가 넘쳐, 조지 씨.\""], ["returns the venerable grandfather.", "존귀하신 할아버지가 대답한다."], ["“You are such good company.”", "\"자넨 정말 함께 있기 좋은 사람이야.\""]], [["“Ha ha!", "\"하하!"], ["Go on!”", "어서 말하시죠!\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."]], [["“My dear friend!", "\"친애하는 벗이여!"], ["But that sword looks awful gleaming and sharp.", "그런데 그 검은 무섭도록 번쩍이고 날카로워 보이는군."], ["It might cut somebody, by accident.", "우발적으로 누굴 벨 수도 있겠어."], ["It makes me shiver, Mr.", "오싹해지는구먼, 조지 씨."], ["George. Curse him!”", "빌어먹을 놈!\""], ["says the excellent old gentleman apart to Judy as the trooper takes a step or two away to lay it aside.", "기병이 검을 치워 두려고 한두 걸음 물러서자, 이 훌륭하신 노신사가 주디에게 따로 속삭인다."], ["“He owes me money, and might think of paying off old scores in this murdering place.", "\"저놈은 나한테 빚이 있어서, 이 살벌한 데서 묵은 셈을 갚을 생각을 할지도 모른다."], ["I wish your brimstone grandmother was here, and he’d shave her head off.”", "네 그 망할 할미가 여기 있었으면, 저놈이 그 할미 머리를 밀어 버릴 텐데.\""]], [["Mr.", "조지 씨는 돌아와 팔짱을 끼고, 의자에서 시시각각 점점 더 아래로 미끄러져 내리는 노인을 내려다보며 조용히 말한다."], ["George, returning, folds his arms, and looking down at the old man, sliding every moment lower and lower in his chair, says quietly, “Now for it!”", "\"자, 시작해 보시죠!\""]], [["“Ho!”", "\"호!\""], ["cries Mr. Smallweed, rubbing his hands with an artful chuckle.", "스몰위드 씨가 교활하게 킥킥거리며 두 손을 비빈다."], ["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Now for it.", "시작해 보자고."], ["Now for what, my dear friend?”", "뭘 시작해 보자는 거지, 친애하는 벗이여?\""]], [["“For a pipe,”", "\"파이프를요.\""], ["says Mr. George, who with great composure sets his chair in the chimney-corner, takes his pipe from the grate, fills it and lights it, and falls to smoking peacefully.", "조지 씨가 말하더니, 더없이 침착하게 의자를 굴뚝 모퉁이에 놓고, 화격자에서 파이프를 집어 담배를 채워 불을 붙이고는, 평온하게 담배를 피우기 시작한다."]], [["This tends to the discomfiture of Mr. Smallweed, who finds it so difficult to resume his object, whatever it may be, that he becomes exasperated and secretly claws the air with an impotent vindictiveness expressive of an intense desire to tear and rend the visage of Mr.", "이것은 스몰위드 씨를 당혹스럽게 만드는 데 보탬이 되는데, 그는 제 목적이 무엇이든 그것을 다시 꺼내기가 어찌나 어려운지, 약이 바짝 올라, 조지 씨의 얼굴을 갈가리 찢어발기고 싶은 강렬한 욕구를 드러내며 무력한 앙심으로 몰래 허공을 할퀸다."], ["George. As the excellent old gentleman’s nails are long and leaden, and his hands lean and veinous, and his eyes green and watery; and, over and above this, as he continues, while he claws, to slide down in his chair and to collapse into a shapeless bundle, he becomes such a ghastly spectacle, even in the accustomed eyes of Judy, that that young virgin pounces at him with something more than the ardour of affection and so shakes him up and pats and pokes him in divers parts of his body, but particularly in that part which the science of self-defence would call his wind, that in his grievous distress he utters enforced sounds like a paviour’s rammer.", "이 훌륭하신 노신사의 손톱은 길고 납빛이며, 손은 비쩍 마르고 핏줄이 불거졌고, 눈은 푸르스름하고 축축한 데다, 그 위에 더해 그가 할퀴는 동안에도 의자에서 계속 미끄러져 내려 형체 없는 보따리로 폭삭 주저앉는 통에, 늘 보아 온 주디의 눈에도 어찌나 소름 끼치는 광경이 되는지, 그 젊은 처녀는 애정 이상의 무언가를 담은 열정으로 그에게 달려들어 그를 흔들어 추스르고, 몸 여기저기를, 특히 호신술에서 명치라 부를 그 부위를 두드리고 쑤셔 대니, 그가 비참한 고통 속에 도로 다지는 기계 같은 소리를 억지로 토해 낸다."]], [["When Judy has by these means set him up again in his chair, with a white face and a frosty nose (but still clawing), she stretches out her weazen forefinger and gives Mr. George one poke in the back.", "이런 방법으로 주디가 그를 의자에 다시 똑바로 앉혀 놓자, 얼굴은 하얗게 코는 서리 맞은 듯한(그러면서도 여전히 할퀴는) 그가 보이고, 그녀는 시든 집게손가락을 내밀어 조지 씨의 등을 한 번 쿡 찌른다."], ["The trooper raising his head, she makes another poke at her esteemed grandfather, and having thus brought them together, stares rigidly at the fire.", "기병이 고개를 들자, 그녀는 존경하는 할아버지를 또 한 번 쿡 찌르더니, 이렇게 둘을 마주 보게 해 놓고는, 불을 뻣뻣하게 응시한다."]], [["“Aye, aye!", "\"그래, 그래!"], ["Ho, ho!", "호, 호!"], ["U—u—u—ugh!”", "으—으—으—윽!\""], ["chatters Grandfather Smallweed, swallowing his rage.", "스몰위드 영감이 분을 삼키며 이를 딱딱거린다."], ["“My dear friend!”", "\"친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["(still clawing).", "(여전히 할퀴며.)"]], [["“I tell you what,”", "\"내 말해 두겠는데,\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“If you want to converse with me, you must speak out.", "\"나하고 대화하고 싶으면 똑바로 말해야 합니다."], ["I am one of the roughs, and I can’t go about and about.", "나는 거친 사람이라 빙빙 둘러서 말할 줄을 몰라요."], ["I haven’t the art to do it.", "그럴 재주가 없습니다."], ["I am not clever enough.", "그만큼 영리하지가 못해요."], ["It don’t suit me.", "나한텐 안 맞습니다."], ["When you go winding round and round me,”", "당신이 나를 빙글빙글 둘러쌀 때면,\""], ["says the trooper, putting his pipe between his lips again, “damme, if I don’t feel as if I was being smothered!”", "기병이 다시 파이프를 입에 물며 말한다. \"젠장, 마치 숨이 막혀 죽을 것 같단 말입니다!\""]], [["And he inflates his broad chest to its utmost extent as if to assure himself that he is not smothered yet.", "그러고는 자신이 아직 숨 막혀 죽지 않았음을 확인하려는 듯, 넓은 가슴을 한껏 부풀린다."]], [["“If you have come to give me a friendly call,” continues Mr.", "\"친근하게 한번 들러 보려고 오신 거라면,\""], ["George, “I am obliged to you; how are you?", "조지 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"감사합니다."], ["If you have come to see whether there’s any property on the premises, look about you; you are welcome.", "잘 지내십니까? 이 안에 무슨 재산거리가 있나 보러 오신 거라면, 둘러보시죠."], ["If you want to out with something, out with it!”", "환영입니다. 무언가 털어놓을 게 있으시면, 그냥 털어놓으시죠!\""]], [["The blooming Judy, without removing her gaze from the fire, gives her grandfather one ghostly poke.", "활짝 핀 주디는 불에서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 할아버지를 유령처럼 한 번 쿡 찌른다."]], [["“You see!", "\"보십쇼!"], ["It’s her opinion too.", "저 애 생각도 같지 않습니까."], ["And why the devil that young woman won’t sit down like a Christian,” says Mr.", "그런데 대체 저 처녀가 왜 사람답게 앉질 않는지,\""], ["George with his eyes musingly fixed on Judy, “I can’t comprehend.”", "조지 씨가 생각에 잠긴 눈을 주디에게 고정한 채 말한다. \"당최 알 수가 없군요.\""]], [["“She keeps at my side to attend to me, sir,”", "\"저 애는 나를 시중들려고 내 곁을 지키는 거야, 선생.\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 영감이 말한다."], ["“I am an old man, my dear Mr. George, and I need some attention.", "\"나는 늙은이라네, 친애하는 조지 씨, 그래서 시중이 좀 필요해."], ["I can carry my years; I am not a brimstone poll-parrot”", "나이는 견딜 만하지. 나는 망할 앵무새가 아니거든\""], ["(snarling and looking unconsciously for the cushion), “but I need attention, my dear friend.”", "(으르렁대며 무의식중에 방석을 찾으며) \"하지만 시중이 필요하다네, 친애하는 벗이여.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"좋습니다!\""], ["returns the trooper, wheeling his chair to face the old man.", "기병이 의자를 돌려 노인을 마주하며 대답한다."], ["“Now then?”", "\"자, 그래서요?\""]], [["“My friend in the city, Mr. George, has done a little business with a pupil of yours.”", "\"도심에 있는 내 친구가 말이야, 조지 씨, 자네 제자 하나하고 작은 거래를 했어.\""]], [["“Has he?”", "\"그랬습니까?\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“I am sorry to hear it.”", "\"유감이군요.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"그렇다네, 선생.\""], ["Grandfather Smallweed rubs his legs.", "스몰위드 영감이 다리를 문지른다."], ["“He is a fine young soldier now, Mr. George, by the name of Carstone.", "\"이제 어엿한 젊은 군인이지, 조지 씨, 카스톤이라는 이름의."], ["Friends came forward and paid it all up, honourable.”", "친구들이 나서서 명예롭게 죄다 갚아 줬어.\""]], [["“Did they?”", "\"그랬습니까?\""], ["returns Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 대꾸한다."], ["“Do you think your friend in the city would like a piece of advice?”", "\"도심에 계신 그 친구분께서 조언 한마디 들으실 마음이 있으실까요?\""]], [["“I think he would, my dear friend.", "\"있고말고, 친애하는 벗이여."], ["From you.”", "자네한테서라면.\""]], [["“I advise him, then, to do no more business in that quarter.", "\"그럼 그분께 조언드리죠. 그쪽으로는 더 이상 거래하지 마시라고요."], ["There’s no more to be got by it.", "거기선 더 뜯어낼 게 없습니다."], ["The young gentleman, to my knowledge, is brought to a dead halt.”", "내가 아는 한, 그 젊은 신사는 완전히 멈춰 섰어요.\""]], [["“No, no, my dear friend. No, no, Mr.", "\"아니, 아니, 친애하는 벗이여."], ["George. No, no, no, sir,”", "아니야, 아니, 조지 씨."], ["remonstrates Grandfather Smallweed, cunningly rubbing his spare legs.", "아니, 아니, 아니야, 선생.\" 스몰위드 영감이 비쩍 마른 다리를 교활하게 문지르며 항변한다."], ["“Not quite a dead halt, I think.", "\"완전히 멈춰 선 건 아니라고 봐."], ["He has good friends, and he is good for his pay, and he is good for the selling price of his commission, and he is good for his chance in a lawsuit, and he is good for his chance in a wife, and—oh, do you know, Mr. George, I think my friend would consider the young gentleman good for something yet?”", "그 사람한텐 좋은 친구들이 있고, 봉급도 받을 만하고, 임관 자리를 팔면 받을 값도 있고, 소송에서 한몫 잡을 가망도 있고, 아내 덕 볼 가망도 있고—아, 그러고 보니, 조지 씨, 내 친구는 그 젊은 신사가 아직 뭔가 건질 만하다고 여길 것 같은데?\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, turning up his velvet cap and scratching his ear like a monkey.", "스몰위드 영감이 벨벳 모자를 추켜올리고 원숭이처럼 귀를 긁으며 말한다."]], [["Mr. George, who has put aside his pipe and sits with an arm on his chair-back, beats a tattoo on the ground with his right foot as if he were not particularly pleased with the turn the conversation has taken.", "파이프를 치워 두고 한 팔을 의자 등받이에 걸친 채 앉아 있던 조지 씨가, 대화가 흘러가는 방향이 영 마뜩잖다는 듯 오른발로 바닥을 통통 두드린다."]], [["“But to pass from one subject to another,”", "\"한데 한 화제에서 다른 화제로 넘어가 보세.\""], ["resumes Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 다시 말한다."], ["“‘To promote the conversation,’ as a joker might say.", "\"'대화에 활기를 더하기 위해' 라고, 익살꾼이라면 그러겠지."], ["To pass, Mr. George, from the ensign to the captain.”", "넘어가 보자고, 조지 씨, 그 소위에서 그 대위로 말이야.\""]], [["“What are you up to, now?” asks Mr.", "\"이제 또 무슨 꿍꿍이입니까?\""], ["George, pausing with a frown in stroking the recollection of his moustache.", "조지 씨가 콧수염을 기억하듯 쓰다듬다 말고 눈살을 찌푸리며 묻는다."], ["“What captain?”", "\"무슨 대위 말입니까?\""]], [["“Our captain.", "\"우리 대위 말이야."], ["The captain we know of.", "우리가 아는 그 대위."], ["Captain Hawdon.”", "호던 대위.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["That’s it, is it?”", "그거였군요?\""], ["says Mr. George with a low whistle as he sees both grandfather and granddaughter looking hard at him.", "조지 씨가 할아버지와 손녀가 둘 다 자신을 뚫어지게 바라보는 것을 보며 나직이 휘파람을 불며 말한다."], ["“You are there!", "\"거기 있었군요!"], ["Well?", "그래서요?"], ["What about it?", "그게 뭐 어떻다는 겁니까?"], ["Come, I won’t be smothered any more.", "자, 나는 더 이상 숨 막히긴 싫습니다."], ["Speak!”", "말씀하시죠!\""]], [["“My dear friend,”", "\"친애하는 벗이여,\""], ["returns the old man, “I was applied—Judy, shake me up a little!—I was applied to yesterday about the captain, and my opinion still is that the captain is not dead.”", "노인이 대답한다. \"어제 누가 나한테 의뢰를 했어—주디, 나를 좀 흔들어 다오!—어제 누가 그 대위에 관해 나한테 의뢰를 했는데, 내 생각엔 여전히 그 대위가 죽지 않았다는 거야.\""]], [["“Bosh!”", "\"헛소리.\""], ["observes Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 한마디 한다."]], [["“What was your remark, my dear friend?”", "\"방금 뭐라고 했나, 친애하는 벗이여?\""], ["inquires the old man with his hand to his ear.", "노인이 귀에 손을 갖다 대며 묻는다. \"헛소리라고요!\""]], [["“Bosh!” “Ho!”", "\"호!\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 영감이 말한다."], ["“Mr. George, of my opinion you can judge for yourself according to the questions asked of me and the reasons given for asking ’em.", "\"조지 씨, 내 생각이 어떤지는, 나한테 던져진 질문들과 그걸 묻는 이유들로 미루어 자네 스스로 판단할 수 있을 걸세."], ["Now, what do you think the lawyer making the inquiries wants?”", "자, 그 조회하는 변호사가 뭘 원할 것 같나?\""]], [["“A job,”", "\"일감이겠죠.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Nothing of the kind!”", "\"천만에!\""]], [["“Can’t be a lawyer, then,” says Mr.", "\"그럼 변호사일 리가 없죠.\""], ["George, folding his arms with an air of confirmed resolution.", "조지 씨가 확고한 결심의 분위기로 팔짱을 끼며 말한다."]], [["“My dear friend, he is a lawyer, and a famous one.", "\"친애하는 벗이여, 그는 변호사야, 그것도 유명한."], ["He wants to see some fragment in Captain Hawdon’s writing.", "그는 호던 대위 필체의 글 조각을 좀 보고 싶어 해."], ["He don’t want to keep it.", "그걸 가지려는 게 아니야."], ["He only wants to see it and compare it with a writing in his possession.”", "그저 보고서, 자기가 가진 어떤 글씨와 대조해 보려는 거지.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“Well, Mr. George.", "\"그래서, 조지 씨."], ["Happening to remember the advertisement concerning Captain Hawdon and any information that could be given respecting him, he looked it up and came to me—just as you did, my dear friend.", "그가 마침 호던 대위에 관한, 그리고 그에 관해 줄 수 있는 어떤 정보에 관한 그 광고를 기억해 내고, 그걸 찾아본 다음 나를 찾아왔어—꼭 자네가 그랬듯이 말이야, 친애하는 벗이여."], ["WILL you shake hands?", "악수 좀 하겠나?"], ["So glad you came that day!", "그날 자네가 와 줘서 어찌나 기쁘던지!"], ["I should have missed forming such a friendship if you hadn’t come!”", "자네가 안 왔더라면 이런 우정을 맺을 기회를 놓쳤을 거야!\""]], [["“Well, Mr. Smallweed?”", "\"그래서요, 스몰위드 씨?\""], ["says Mr. George again after going through the ceremony with some stiffness.", "다소 뻣뻣하게 그 의식을 치르고 나서 조지 씨가 다시 말한다."]], [["“I had no such thing.", "\"나한테는 그런 게 없었어."], ["I have nothing but his signature.", "그 사람 서명 말고는 가진 게 없거든."], ["Plague pestilence and famine, battle murder and sudden death upon him,”", "그놈한테 역병과 기근, 전쟁과 살인과 급사가 떨어지기를,\""], ["says the old man, making a curse out of one of his few remembrances of a prayer and squeezing up his velvet cap between his angry hands, “I have half a million of his signatures, I think!", "노인은 몇 안 되는 기도 기억 가운데 하나를 저주로 둔갑시키며, 성난 두 손으로 벨벳 모자를 우그러뜨리며 말한다. \"그놈 서명이라면 오십만 장은 될 거야, 아마!"], ["But you,”", "그런데 자네는,\""], ["breathlessly recovering his mildness of speech as Judy re-adjusts the cap on his skittle-ball of a head, “you, my dear Mr.", "주디가 그의 그 볼링공 같은 머리에 모자를 다시 바로 씌워 주자, 그는 숨 가쁘게 부드러운 말투를 되찾으며 말한다."], ["George, are likely to have some letter or paper that would suit the purpose.", "\"자네는, 친애하는 조지 씨, 그 목적에 맞을 편지나 서류를 갖고 있을 법하단 말이지."], ["Anything would suit the purpose, written in the hand.”", "그 필체로 쓰인 거라면 뭐든 그 목적에 맞을 거야.\""]], [["“Some writing in that hand,”", "\"그 필체로 쓰인 글이라.\""], ["says the trooper, pondering; “may be, I have.”", "기병이 곰곰이 생각하며 말한다. \"어쩌면, 있을지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“My dearest friend!”", "\"더없이 친애하는 벗이여!\""]], [["“May be, I have not.”", "\"어쩌면, 없을지도 모르고요.\""]], [["“Ho!”", "\"호!\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, crest-fallen.", "스몰위드 영감이 풀이 죽어 말한다."]], [["“But if I had bushels of it, I would not show as much as would make a cartridge without knowing why.”", "\"하지만 그게 한 말이나 있다 해도, 영문을 모르고서야 탄약 하나 만들 만큼도 내보이지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Sir, I have told you why.", "\"선생, 영문은 말해 줬잖나."], ["My dear Mr. George, I have told you why.”", "친애하는 조지 씨, 영문은 말해 줬다고.\""]], [["“Not enough,”", "\"충분치 않습니다.\""], ["says the trooper, shaking his head.", "기병이 고개를 저으며 말한다."], ["“I must know more, and approve it.”", "\"나는 더 알아야겠고, 그게 납득이 가야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Then, will you come to the lawyer?", "\"그럼 변호사한테 가 보겠나?"], ["My dear friend, will you come and see the gentleman?”", "친애하는 벗이여, 가서 그 신사를 만나 보겠나?\""], ["urges Grandfather Smallweed, pulling out a lean old silver watch with hands like the leg of a skeleton.", "스몰위드 영감이 해골 다리 같은 바늘이 달린 비쩍 마른 낡은 은시계를 꺼내며 재촉한다."], ["“I told him it was probable I might call upon him between ten and eleven this forenoon, and it’s now half after ten.", "\"내가 그 사람한테, 오늘 오전 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 들를 것 같다고 말해 뒀는데, 지금이 열 시 반이야."], ["Will you come and see the gentleman, Mr. George?”", "가서 그 신사를 만나 보겠나, 조지 씨?\""]], [["“Hum!”", "\"흠!\""], ["says he gravely.", "그가 진지하게 말한다."], ["“I don’t mind that.", "\"그건 상관없습니다."], ["Though why this should concern you so much, I don’t know.”", "다만 이게 어째서 당신한테 그토록 중대한 일인지는 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“Everything concerns me that has a chance in it of bringing anything to light about him.", "\"그에 관해 뭔가를 밝혀낼 가망이 있는 일이라면 죄다 나한테 중대하지."], ["Didn’t he take us all in?", "그자가 우리 모두를 속이지 않았나?"], ["Didn’t he owe us immense sums, all round?", "우리 모두한테 어마어마한 돈을 빚지지 않았어?"], ["Concern me?", "나한테 중대하냐고?"], ["Who can anything about him concern more than me?", "그자에 관한 일이 나보다 누구한테 더 중대할 수 있겠나?"], ["Not, my dear friend,”", "그렇다고,\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, lowering his tone, “that I want YOU to betray anything.", "스몰위드 영감이 목소리를 낮추며 말한다. \"친애하는 벗이여, 자네더러 뭘 누설하라는 건 아니야."], ["Far from it.", "천만에."], ["Are you ready to come, my dear friend?”", "갈 채비가 됐나, 친애하는 벗이여?\""]], [["“Aye!", "\"예!"], ["I’ll come in a moment.", "곧 가죠."], ["I promise nothing, you know.”", "아무것도 약속하진 않습니다, 아시겠지만.\""]], [["“No, my dear Mr. George; no.”", "\"그럼, 친애하는 조지 씨, 그럼.\""]], [["“And you mean to say you’re going to give me a lift to this place, wherever it is, without charging for it?”", "\"그리고 그게 어디든 그 장소까지 나를 공짜로 태워 주겠다는 말씀입니까?\""], ["Mr. George inquires, getting his hat and thick wash-leather gloves.", "조지 씨가 모자와 두툼한 무두질 가죽 장갑을 챙기며 묻는다."]], [["This pleasantry so tickles Mr. Smallweed that he laughs, long and low, before the fire. But ever while he laughs, he glances over his paralytic shoulder at Mr. George and eagerly watches him as he unlocks the padlock of a homely cupboard at the distant end of the gallery, looks here and there upon the higher shelves, and ultimately takes something out with a rustling of paper, folds it, and puts it in his breast. Then Judy pokes Mr. Smallweed once, and Mr. Smallweed pokes Judy once.", "이 농담이 어찌나 스몰위드 씨의 비위를 간질이는지, 그는 불 앞에서 길고 나직하게 웃는다. 그러면서도 웃는 내내, 그는 마비된 어깨 너머로 조지 씨를 흘끔거리며, 그가 사격장 저쪽 끝의 수수한 찬장 자물쇠를 열고, 위쪽 선반들을 여기저기 둘러보다가, 마침내 종이 부스럭거리는 소리와 함께 무언가를 꺼내 접어서 품에 넣는 것을 열심히 지켜본다. 그러자 주디가 스몰위드 씨를 한 번 쿡 찌르고, 스몰위드 씨가 주디를 한 번 쿡 찌른다."]], [["“I am ready,”", "\"준비됐습니다.\""], ["says the trooper, coming back.", "기병이 돌아오며 말한다."], ["“Phil, you can carry this old gentleman to his coach, and make nothing of him.”", "\"필, 이 노신사를 마차까지 메다 드려라, 가뿐히 다루고.\""]], [["“Oh, dear me!", "\"오, 이런!"], ["O Lord!", "오, 맙소사!"], ["Stop a moment!”", "잠깐 멈추게!\""], ["says Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 말한다."], ["“He’s so very prompt!", "\"이 사람 너무 잽싸단 말이야!"], ["Are you sure you can do it carefully, my worthy man?”", "조심해서 할 수 있겠나, 친애하는 양반?\""]], [["Phil makes no reply, but seizing the chair and its load, sidles away, tightly hugged by the now speechless Mr.", "필은 대꾸 없이 의자와 그 짐을 움켜잡고 옆걸음으로 가는데, 이제 말문이 막힌 스몰위드 씨를 꽉 끌어안고서, 마치 이 노신사를 가장 가까운 화산까지 메고 가라는 흡족한 임무라도 받은 양 복도를 따라 내달린다."], ["Smallweed, and bolts along the passage as if he had an acceptable commission to carry the old gentleman to the nearest volcano.", "하지만 그 짧은 위탁이 마차에서 끝나니, 그는 거기에 영감을 내려놓는다."], ["His shorter trust, however, terminating at the cab, he deposits him there; and the fair Judy takes her place beside him, and the chair embellishes the roof, and Mr. George takes the vacant place upon the box.", "그러자 어여쁜 주디가 그 곁에 자리를 잡고, 의자는 지붕을 장식하며, 조지 씨는 마부석의 빈자리에 앉는다."]], [["Mr. George is quite confounded by the spectacle he beholds from time to time as he peeps into the cab through the window behind him, where the grim Judy is always motionless, and the old gentleman with his cap over one eye is always sliding off the seat into the straw and looking upward at him out of his other eye with a helpless expression of being jolted in the back. More Old Soldiers Than One", "조지 씨는 이따금 뒤쪽 창문으로 마차 안을 들여다볼 때 보이는 광경에 어안이 벙벙해지는데, 그 안에서는 험상궂은 주디가 늘 미동도 없고, 모자가 한쪽 눈을 덮은 노신사가 늘 자리에서 짚 더미 위로 미끄러져 내리며, 나머지 한쪽 눈으로 등이 덜컹거려 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 표정으로 그를 올려다보고 있다."]], [["Mr. George has not far to ride with folded arms upon the box, for their destination is Lincoln’s Inn Fields.", "조지 씨가 팔짱을 끼고 마부석에 앉아 가야 할 거리는 멀지 않다."], ["When the driver stops his horses, Mr.", "목적지가 링컨스인필즈이기 때문이다."], ["George alights, and looking in at the window, says, “What, Mr.", "마부가 말을 세우자 조지 씨는 내려서 창 안을 들여다보며 말한다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s your man, is he?”", "\"아니, 토킹혼 씨가 당신 사람이라는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear friend.", "\"그렇다네, 친애하는 벗이여."], ["Do you know him, Mr. George?”", "그 사람을 아시나, 조지 씨?\""]], [["“Why, I have heard of him—seen him too, I think.", "\"글쎄, 소문은 들었소—본 적도 있는 것 같고."], ["But I don’t know him, and he don’t know me.”", "하지만 난 그 사람을 모르고, 그 사람도 나를 모르오.\""]], [["There ensues the carrying of Mr. Smallweed upstairs, which is done to perfection with the trooper’s help.", "이제 스몰위드 씨를 위층으로 옮기는 일이 이어지는데, 기병의 도움으로 완벽하게 처리된다."], ["He is borne into Mr. Tulkinghorn’s great room and deposited on the Turkey rug before the fire.", "그는 토킹혼 씨의 널찍한 방으로 옮겨져 벽난로 앞 터키 양탄자 위에 내려진다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn is not within at the present moment but will be back directly.", "토킹혼 씨는 지금 자리에 없지만 곧 돌아올 것이다."], ["The occupant of the pew in the hall, having said thus much, stirs the fire and leaves the triumvirate to warm themselves.", "현관 칸막이 자리를 지키는 사내가 이 정도 말을 전한 다음 불을 휘저어 돋우고는, 세 사람이 몸을 녹이도록 내버려두고 떠난다."]], [["Mr. George is mightily curious in respect of the room.", "조지 씨는 그 방에 대해 자못 호기심이 인다."], ["He looks up at the painted ceiling, looks round at the old law-books, contemplates the portraits of the great clients, reads aloud the names on the boxes.", "그는 그림이 그려진 천장을 올려다보고, 낡은 법률 서적들을 둘러보고, 거물 의뢰인들의 초상화를 찬찬히 바라보고, 상자에 적힌 이름들을 소리 내어 읽는다."]], [["“‘Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,’” Mr.", "\"'레스터 데드록 경, 준남작.'\""], ["George reads thoughtfully.", "조지 씨가 골똘히 읽는다."], ["“Ha!", "\"허!"], ["‘Manor of Chesney Wold.’ Humph!”", "'체스니 월드 영지.'"], ["Mr.", "흠!\""], ["George stands looking at these boxes a long while—as if they were pictures—and comes back to the fire repeating, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and Manor of Chesney Wold, hey?”", "조지 씨는 마치 그것들이 그림이라도 되는 양 한참 동안 그 상자들을 바라보고 서 있다가, 벽난로 쪽으로 돌아오며 거듭 중얼거린다. \"레스터 데드록 경, 준남작에다 체스니 월드 영지라, 응?\""]], [["“Worth a mint of money, Mr. George!”", "\"엄청난 돈줄이지요, 조지 씨!\""], ["whispers Grandfather Smallweed, rubbing his legs.", "스몰위드 영감이 다리를 문지르며 속삭인다."], ["“Powerfully rich!”", "\"어마어마한 부자랍니다!\""]], [["“Who do you mean?", "\"누구 말이오?"], ["This old gentleman, or the Baronet?”", "이 늙은 양반이오, 아니면 그 준남작이오?\""]], [["“This gentleman, this gentleman.”", "\"이 양반 말입니다, 이 양반.\""]], [["“So I have heard; and knows a thing or two, I’ll hold a wager.", "\"나도 그렇게 들었소. 게다가 한두 가지쯤은 아는 사람이지, 내기를 걸어도 좋소."], ["Not bad quarters, either,”", "거처도 나쁘지 않고.\""], ["says Mr. George, looking round again.", "조지 씨가 다시 둘러보며 말한다."], ["“See the strong-box yonder!”", "\"저기 저 금고 좀 보시오!\""]], [["This reply is cut short by Mr. Tulkinghorn’s arrival.", "이 대답은 토킹혼 씨가 들어서면서 끊긴다."], ["There is no change in him, of course.", "물론 그에게는 아무런 변화가 없다."], ["Rustily drest, with his spectacles in his hand, and their very case worn threadbare.", "낡아빠진 옷차림에, 안경을 손에 들었는데 그 안경집조차 닳고 닳아 올이 다 드러났다."], ["In manner, close and dry.", "태도는 입이 무겁고 메마르다."], ["In voice, husky and low.", "목소리는 쉰 듯 낮다."], ["In face, watchful behind a blind; habitually not uncensorious and contemptuous perhaps.", "얼굴은 가림막 뒤에서 살피는 듯한데, 평소에 비난과 경멸의 기색이 없다고는 할 수 없을 것이다."], ["The peerage may have warmer worshippers and faithfuller believers than Mr. Tulkinghorn, after all, if everything were known.", "모든 것이 다 알려진다면, 토킹혼 씨보다 귀족 계급을 더 뜨겁게 숭배하고 더 충실히 믿는 자들이 어쩌면 따로 있을지도 모른다."]], [["“Good morning, Mr. Smallweed, good morning!”", "\"안녕하시오, 스몰위드 씨, 안녕하시오!\""], ["he says as he comes in.", "그가 들어오며 말한다."], ["“You have brought the sergeant, I see.", "\"하사관을 데려오셨군."], ["Sit down, sergeant.”", "앉으시오, 하사관.\""]], [["As Mr.", "토킹혼 씨는 장갑을 벗어 모자 안에 넣으면서, 반쯤 감은 눈으로 방 건너편에 서 있는 기병을 바라보며 어쩌면 속으로 이렇게 말하는지도 모른다."], ["Tulkinghorn takes off his gloves and puts them in his hat, he looks with half-closed eyes across the room to where the trooper stands and says within himself perchance, “You’ll do, my friend!”", "\"자네라면 쓸 만하겠어, 친구!\""]], [["“Sit down, sergeant,”", "\"앉으시오, 하사관.\""], ["he repeats as he comes to his table, which is set on one side of the fire, and takes his easy-chair.", "그는 벽난로 한쪽에 놓인 탁자로 다가가 안락의자에 앉으며 다시 말한다."], ["“Cold and raw this morning, cold and raw!” Mr.", "\"오늘 아침은 춥고 을씨년스럽구먼, 춥고 을씨년스러워!\""], ["Tulkinghorn warms before the bars, alternately, the palms and knuckles of his hands and looks (from behind that blind which is always down) at the trio sitting in a little semicircle before him.", "토킹혼 씨는 난로 살창 앞에서 두 손바닥과 손등을 번갈아 녹이며, (언제나 내려져 있는 그 가림막 뒤에서) 자기 앞에 작은 반원을 그리며 앉은 세 사람을 바라본다."]], [["“Now, I can feel what I am about”", "\"자, 이제 내가 무슨 일을 하는지 느낄 수 있겠소\""], ["(as perhaps he can in two senses), “Mr. Smallweed.”", "(어쩌면 두 가지 의미에서 그럴지도 모른다), \"스몰위드 씨.\""], ["The old gentleman is newly shaken up by Judy to bear his part in the conversation.", "노인은 대화에 한몫 끼도록 주디에게 다시 한 번 흔들려 추슬러진다."], ["“You have brought our good friend the sergeant, I see.”", "\"우리 좋은 벗 하사관을 데려오셨군그래.\""]], [["“Yes, sir,” returns Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\""], ["Smallweed, very servile to the lawyer’s wealth and influence.", "스몰위드 씨가 변호사의 재산과 권세에 한껏 굽실거리며 대답한다."]], [["“And what does the sergeant say about this business?”", "\"그래, 하사관은 이 일에 대해 뭐라고 하오?\""]], [["“Mr. George,”", "\"조지 씨라는데,\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed with a tremulous wave of his shrivelled hand, “this is the gentleman, sir.”", "스몰위드 영감이 쪼그라든 손을 떨며 흔들어 보이며 말한다. \"이 양반이 그 사람입니다, 어르신.\""]], [["Mr. George salutes the gentleman but otherwise sits bolt upright and profoundly silent—very forward in his chair, as if the full complement of regulation appendages for a field-day hung about him.", "조지 씨는 그 신사에게 경례를 붙이지만 그 밖에는 꼿꼿이 똑바로 앉아 깊이 침묵을 지킨다—마치 사열식에 필요한 규정 장비 일습을 온통 몸에 두르고 있기라도 한 듯, 의자에 잔뜩 당겨 앉은 채로."]], [["Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 말을 잇는다."], ["Tulkinghorn proceeds, “Well, George—I believe your name is George?”", "\"자, 조지—이름이 조지가 맞지?\""]], [["“It is so, Sir.”", "\"그렇습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["“What do you say, George?”", "\"어떻게 하겠소, 조지?\""]], [["“I ask your pardon, sir,”", "\"실례지만, 어르신,\""], ["returns the trooper, “but I should wish to know what YOU say?”", "기병이 대꾸한다. \"저야말로 어르신께서 어떻게 하실 작정인지 알고 싶습니다만?\""]], [["“Do you mean in point of reward?”", "\"보상 문제를 말하는 거요?\""]], [["“I mean in point of everything, sir.”", "\"모든 문제를 말하는 겁니다, 어르신.\""]], [["This is so very trying to Mr.", "이 말이 스몰위드 씨의 비위를 어찌나 거스르는지, 그는 별안간 \"이 유황 같은 짐승 놈아!\""], ["Smallweed’s temper that he suddenly breaks out with “You’re a brimstone beast!” and as suddenly asks pardon of Mr.", "하고 터뜨리고는 또 별안간 토킹혼 씨에게 용서를 구하더니, 이 말실수를 변명한답시고 주디에게 이렇게 말한다."], ["Tulkinghorn, excusing himself for this slip of the tongue by saying to Judy, “I was thinking of your grandmother, my dear.”", "\"네 할미를 생각하고 있었느니라, 얘야.\""]], [["“I supposed, sergeant,” Mr.", "\"내 생각에는, 하사관,\""], ["Tulkinghorn resumes as he leans on one side of his chair and crosses his legs, “that Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 의자 한쪽에 기대어 다리를 꼬며 말을 잇는다."], ["Smallweed might have sufficiently explained the matter.", "\"스몰위드 씨가 그 일을 충분히 설명해 두었을 듯한데."], ["It lies in the smallest compass, however.", "어쨌든 아주 간단한 일이오."], ["You served under Captain Hawdon at one time, and were his attendant in illness, and rendered him many little services, and were rather in his confidence, I am told.", "자네는 한때 호든 대위 밑에서 복무했고, 그가 병들었을 때 시중을 들었고, 자잘한 일을 여러 가지 도와주었고, 그의 신임을 꽤 받았다고 들었소."], ["That is so, is it not?”", "그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, that is so,” says Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 어르신, 그렇습니다.\""], ["George with military brevity.", "조지 씨가 군인답게 짤막하게 말한다."]], [["“Therefore you may happen to have in your possession something—anything, no matter what; accounts, instructions, orders, a letter, anything—in Captain Hawdon’s writing.", "\"그렇다면 자네가 무언가를 가지고 있을 법한데—무엇이든, 뭐든 상관없소. 장부든, 지시서든, 명령서든, 편지든, 무엇이든—호든 대위가 직접 쓴 것 말이오."], ["I wish to compare his writing with some that I have.", "나는 그의 필적을 내가 가진 어떤 것과 대조해 보고 싶소."], ["If you can give me the opportunity, you shall be rewarded for your trouble.", "그 기회를 줄 수 있다면, 수고에 대해 보상하리다."], ["Three, four, five, guineas, you would consider handsome, I dare say.”", "삼, 사, 오 기니쯤이면 후하다고 여기겠지, 아마.\""]], [["“Noble, my dear friend!”", "\"통이 크시군요, 친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["cries Grandfather Smallweed, screwing up his eyes.", "스몰위드 영감이 두 눈을 가늘게 모으며 외친다."]], [["“If not, say how much more, in your conscience as a soldier, you can demand.", "\"그게 아니라면, 군인의 양심을 걸고 얼마를 더 요구할 수 있는지 말해 보시오."], ["There is no need for you to part with the writing, against your inclination—though I should prefer to have it.”", "내키지 않는다면 그 필적을 굳이 넘기지 않아도 좋소—나로서야 받는 편이 더 좋겠지만.\""]], [["Mr. George sits squared in exactly the same attitude, looks at the painted ceiling, and says never a word.", "조지 씨는 똑같은 자세 그대로 네모반듯하게 앉아 그림 그려진 천장을 바라보며 한마디도 하지 않는다."], ["The irascible Mr. Smallweed scratches the air.", "성마른 스몰위드 씨가 허공을 긁어댄다."]], [["“The question is,” says Mr.", "\"문제는 말이오,\""], ["Tulkinghorn in his methodical, subdued, uninterested way, “first, whether you have any of Captain Hawdon’s writing?”", "토킹혼 씨가 그 조리 있고 가라앉은, 무심한 투로 말한다. \"첫째, 자네가 호든 대위의 필적을 조금이라도 가지고 있느냐 하는 거요.\""]], [["“First, whether I have any of Captain Hawdon’s writing, sir,”", "\"첫째, 제가 호든 대위의 필적을 조금이라도 가지고 있느냐, 어르신.\""], ["repeats Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 따라 말한다."]], [["“Secondly, what will satisfy you for the trouble of producing it?”", "\"둘째, 그것을 내놓는 수고의 대가로 무엇이면 만족하겠소?\""]], [["“Secondly, what will satisfy me for the trouble of producing it, sir,”", "\"둘째, 그것을 내놓는 수고의 대가로 무엇이면 만족하겠느냐, 어르신.\""], ["repeats Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 따라 말한다."]], [["“Thirdly, you can judge for yourself whether it is at all like that,” says Mr.", "\"셋째, 이게 그것과 조금이라도 비슷한지는 자네 스스로 판단할 수 있을 거요.\""], ["Tulkinghorn, suddenly handing him some sheets of written paper tied together.", "토킹혼 씨가 별안간 한데 묶은 종이 몇 장을 그에게 건네며 말한다."]], [["“Whether it is at all like that, sir.", "\"이게 그것과 조금이라도 비슷한지, 어르신."], ["Just so,”", "바로 그렇군요.\""], ["repeats Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 따라 말한다."]], [["All three repetitions Mr. George pronounces in a mechanical manner, looking straight at Mr.", "이 세 번의 따라 말하기를 조지 씨는 토킹혼 씨를 똑바로 쳐다보며 기계적인 투로 내뱉는다."], ["Tulkinghorn; nor does he so much as glance at the affidavit in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, that has been given to him for his inspection (though he still holds it in his hand), but continues to look at the lawyer with an air of troubled meditation.", "검토하라고 그에게 건네진 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송의 진술서를 (여전히 손에 들고 있으면서도) 흘끗 보지도 않은 채, 그저 곤혹스럽게 골똘한 기색으로 변호사를 계속 바라보기만 한다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“What do you say?”", "\"어떻게 하겠소?\""]], [["“Well, sir,” replies Mr.", "\"글쎄요, 어르신,\""], ["George, rising erect and looking immense, “I would rather, if you’ll excuse me, have nothing to do with this.”", "조지 씨가 똑바로 일어서서 거대해 보이는 모습으로 대답한다. \"외람되지만, 저는 차라리 이 일에 손대지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 겉으로는 조금도 흔들림 없이 묻는다."], ["Tulkinghorn, outwardly quite undisturbed, demands, “Why not?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Why, sir,” returns the trooper.", "\"그게 말입니다, 어르신,\""], ["“Except on military compulsion, I am not a man of business.", "기병이 대꾸한다. \"군의 강제 명령이라면 모를까, 저는 사무에 밝은 사람이 아닙니다."], ["Among civilians I am what they call in Scotland a ne’er-do-weel.", "민간인들 사이에서 저는 스코틀랜드 말로 이른바 건달이올시다."], ["I have no head for papers, sir.", "저는 서류라면 머리가 안 돌아갑니다, 어르신."], ["I can stand any fire better than a fire of cross questions. I mentioned to Mr.", "추궁의 포화보다는 그 어떤 포화 속이라도 더 잘 견딜 수 있습니다."], ["Smallweed, only an hour or so ago, that when I come into things of this kind I feel as if I was being smothered.", "한 시간쯤 전에 스몰위드 씨에게도 말씀드렸는데, 저는 이런 일에 끼어들면 숨이 막히는 것만 같습니다."], ["And that is my sensation,” says Mr.", "그리고 그게 바로 지금 이 순간 제 심정입니다.\""], ["George, looking round upon the company, “at the present moment.”", "조지 씨가 좌중을 둘러보며 말한다."]], [["With that, he takes three strides forward to replace the papers on the lawyer’s table and three strides backward to resume his former station, where he stands perfectly upright, now looking at the ground and now at the painted ceiling, with his hands behind him as if to prevent himself from accepting any other document whatever.", "그러고는 성큼성큼 세 걸음 앞으로 나아가 그 서류를 변호사의 탁자에 도로 놓고, 다시 성큼성큼 세 걸음 뒤로 물러나 원래 자리로 돌아가서, 거기에 완벽하게 똑바로 서서, 다른 어떤 서류든 받아들이지 못하게 막으려는 듯 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채, 한 번은 바닥을, 한 번은 그림 그려진 천장을 번갈아 바라본다."]], [["Under this provocation, Mr. Smallweed’s favourite adjective of disparagement is so close to his tongue that he begins the words “my dear friend”", "이런 약 올림에 스몰위드 씨가 즐겨 쓰는 멸시의 형용사가 혀끝까지 어찌나 바싹 차올랐는지, 그는 \"친애하는 벗이여\"라는 말을 \"유\""], ["with the monosyllable “brim,” thus converting the possessive pronoun into brimmy and appearing to have an impediment in his speech.", "한 음절로 시작하는 바람에 소유의 호칭을 \"유애하는\"으로 바꿔 버려, 말을 더듬는 것처럼 보인다."], ["Once past this difficulty, however, he exhorts his dear friend in the tenderest manner not to be rash, but to do what so eminent a gentleman requires, and to do it with a good grace, confident that it must be unobjectionable as well as profitable. Mr.", "그러나 이 난관을 넘기고 나자, 그는 더없이 다정한 투로 친애하는 벗에게 경솔하게 굴지 말고, 저토록 훌륭한 양반이 요구하는 바를, 그것도 흔쾌한 태도로 들어주라고 권하면서, 그것이 분명 흠잡을 데 없는 데다 돈벌이도 될 것이라고 호언한다."], ["Tulkinghorn merely utters an occasional sentence, as, “You are the best judge of your own interest, sergeant.”", "토킹혼 씨는 이따금 한 마디씩만 던질 뿐이다. 이를테면, \"자기 이익이야 자네가 제일 잘 판단할 테지, 하사관.\""], ["“Take care you do no harm by this.”", "\"이 일로 해를 입지 않도록 조심하시오.\""], ["“Please yourself, please yourself.”", "\"마음대로 하시오, 마음대로.\""], ["“If you know what you mean, that’s quite enough.”", "\"자네 말뜻을 자네가 안다면, 그걸로 충분하오.\""], ["These he utters with an appearance of perfect indifference as he looks over the papers on his table and prepares to write a letter.", "이런 말들을, 그는 탁자 위의 서류를 들여다보며 편지를 쓸 채비를 하면서, 더없이 무심한 기색으로 내뱉는다."]], [["Mr. George looks distrustfully from the painted ceiling to the ground, from the ground to Mr. Smallweed, from Mr. Smallweed to Mr. Tulkinghorn, and from Mr. Tulkinghorn to the painted ceiling again, often in his perplexity changing the leg on which he rests.", "조지 씨는 그림 그려진 천장에서 바닥으로, 바닥에서 스몰위드 씨에게로, 스몰위드 씨에게서 토킹혼 씨에게로, 토킹혼 씨에게서 다시 그림 그려진 천장으로 미심쩍은 눈길을 옮기며, 곤혹스러운 나머지 몸을 지탱하는 다리를 자주 바꾼다."]], [["“I do assure you, sir,”", "\"정말이지 말씀드리는데, 어르신,\""], ["says Mr.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["George, “not to say it offensively, that between you and Mr. Smallweed here, I really am being smothered fifty times over.", "\"기분 상하시라고 드리는 말은 아닙니다만, 어르신과 여기 이 스몰위드 씨 사이에서 저는 정말 쉰 번도 더 숨이 막히는 것 같습니다."], ["I really am, sir.", "정말입니다, 어르신."], ["I am not a match for you gentlemen.", "저는 두 분 같은 양반들의 상대가 못 됩니다."], ["Will you allow me to ask why you want to see the captain’s hand, in the case that I could find any specimen of it?”", "한 가지 여쭤도 될까요? 제가 그 대위의 필적을 무어라도 찾아낸다 치고, 대체 그걸 어디에 쓰시려는 겁니까?\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn quietly shakes his head.", "토킹혼 씨가 조용히 고개를 가로젓는다."], ["“No.", "\"안 되오."], ["If you were a man of business, sergeant, you would not need to be informed that there are confidential reasons, very harmless in themselves, for many such wants in the profession to which I belong.", "자네가 사무에 밝은 사람이라면, 내가 속한 직업에서는 그 자체로는 지극히 무해하지만 비밀로 해야 하는 사정이 그런 요구에 흔히 따른다는 걸 굳이 일러줄 필요도 없을 거요."], ["But if you are afraid of doing any injury to Captain Hawdon, you may set your mind at rest about that.”", "하지만 호든 대위에게 무슨 해라도 끼칠까 두려운 거라면, 그 점은 마음 놓아도 좋소.\""]], [["“Aye!", "\"아!"], ["He is dead, sir.”", "그분은 돌아가셨습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["“IS he?” Mr.", "\"그래요?\""], ["Tulkinghorn quietly sits down to write.", "토킹혼 씨가 조용히 자리에 앉아 글을 쓴다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"그런데 말입니다, 어르신,\""], ["says the trooper, looking into his hat after another disconcerted pause, “I am sorry not to have given you more satisfaction.", "기병이 또 한 번 당황스러운 침묵 끝에 자기 모자 속을 들여다보며 말한다. \"더 흡족하게 해 드리지 못해 송구합니다."], ["If it would be any satisfaction to any one that I should be confirmed in my judgment that I would rather have nothing to do with this by a friend of mine who has a better head for business than I have, and who is an old soldier, I am willing to consult with him.", "만약 누구라도, 제가 이 일에 손대지 않는 편이 낫다는 제 판단을—저보다 사무에 밝고 게다가 늙은 군인인 제 벗 하나에게서—확인받는 것이 흡족하다면야, 기꺼이 그와 의논해 보겠습니다."], ["I—I really am so completely smothered myself at present,” says Mr.", "저는—저는 정말이지 지금 이렇게 완전히 숨이 막혀서,\""], ["George, passing his hand hopelessly across his brow, “that I don’t know but what it might be a satisfaction to me.”", "조지 씨가 절망스럽게 이마를 손으로 쓸어내리며 말한다. \"그게 저로서도 흡족할지 어떨지조차 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed, hearing that this authority is an old soldier, so strongly inculcates the expediency of the trooper’s taking counsel with him, and particularly informing him of its being a question of five guineas or more, that Mr. George engages to go and see him. Mr. Tulkinghorn says nothing either way.", "이 권위자라는 사람이 늙은 군인이라는 말을 듣자, 스몰위드 씨는 기병이 그와 의논하는 것이 마땅하다고 어찌나 강하게 주입하는지, 더구나 이것이 오 기니, 아니 그 이상이 걸린 문제라는 점을 특별히 일러주는 통에, 조지 씨는 그를 만나러 가겠노라 약속한다. 토킹혼 씨는 이러쿵저러쿵 한마디도 하지 않는다."]], [["“I’ll consult my friend, then, by your leave, sir,”", "\"그럼 어르신의 허락을 얻어 제 벗과 의논해 보겠습니다.\""], ["says the trooper, “and I’ll take the liberty of looking in again with the final answer in the course of the day.", "기병이 말한다. \"그리고 오늘 중으로 최종 답변을 가지고 외람되나마 다시 들르겠습니다."], ["Mr. Smallweed, if you wish to be carried downstairs—”", "스몰위드 씨, 아래층으로 옮겨 드릴까요—\""]], [["“In a moment, my dear friend, in a moment.", "\"잠깐만, 친애하는 벗이여, 잠깐만."], ["Will you first let me speak half a word with this gentleman in private?”", "우선 이 양반과 단둘이 반 마디만 나누게 해 주겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly, sir.", "\"물론입니다, 어르신."], ["Don’t hurry yourself on my account.”", "저 때문에 서두르지 마시지요.\""], ["The trooper retires to a distant part of the room and resumes his curious inspection of the boxes, strong and otherwise.", "기병은 방 저편 멀찍한 곳으로 물러나 튼튼한 것이든 아니든 그 상자들을 다시 호기심 어린 눈으로 살펴본다."]], [["“If I wasn’t as weak as a brimstone baby, sir,”", "\"제가 유황 갓난애처럼 이렇게 기운이 없지만 않다면 말입니다, 어르신,\""], ["whispers Grandfather Smallweed, drawing the lawyer down to his level by the lapel of his coat and flashing some half-quenched green fire out of his angry eyes, “I’d tear the writing away from him.", "스몰위드 영감이 변호사의 외투 깃을 잡고 제 눈높이까지 끌어내리며, 성난 두 눈에서 반쯤 꺼진 녹색 불꽃을 번득이며 속삭인다. \"그자한테서 그 필적을 뜯어내고 말 텐데."], ["He’s got it buttoned in his breast.", "그자는 그걸 가슴팍에 단추로 채워 넣어 두었어요."], ["I saw him put it there.", "내가 거기 넣는 걸 봤습니다."], ["Judy saw him put it there.", "주디도 거기 넣는 걸 봤고요."], ["Speak up, you crabbed image for the sign of a walking-stick shop, and say you saw him put it there!”", "어디 말해 보아라, 이 지팡이 가게 간판으로나 쓸 심술궂은 형상아, 그자가 거기 넣는 걸 봤다고 말이다!\""], ["This vehement conjuration the old gentleman accompanies with such a thrust at his granddaughter that it is too much for his strength, and he slips away out of his chair, drawing Mr. Tulkinghorn with him, until he is arrested by Judy, and well shaken.", "노인은 이 격렬한 호소에 손녀를 어찌나 세게 떠미는지, 그 힘이 제 몸에 부쳐 의자에서 미끄러져 내리며 토킹혼 씨까지 함께 끌고 내려가다가, 주디에게 붙들려 호되게 흔들리고서야 멈춘다."]], [["“Violence will not do for me, my friend,”", "\"폭력은 나한테 안 통하오, 벗이여.\""], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn then remarks coolly.", "그러자 토킹혼 씨가 차갑게 한마디 한다."]], [["“No, no, I know, I know, sir.", "\"네, 네, 압니다, 알지요, 어르신."], ["But it’s chafing and galling—it’s—it’s worse than your smattering chattering magpie of a grandmother,”", "하지만 속이 타고 부아가 치밀어서—그러니까—그자가 필요한 걸 가지고도 안 내놓는다는 걸 아는 게, 떠벌이며 지껄여대는 까치 같은 당신네 할미보다도 더 고약하단 말이오,\""], ["to the imperturbable Judy, who only looks at the fire, “to know he has got what’s wanted and won’t give it up.", "그는 그저 불만 바라보고 있는 태연한 주디에게 이렇게 쏘아붙이고는 말을 잇는다. \"그걸 안 내놔!"], ["He, not to give it up!", "그자가! 그놈이!"], ["HE! A vagabond!", "부랑자 주제에!"], ["But never mind, sir, never mind.", "하지만 괜찮습니다, 어르신, 괜찮아요."], ["At the most, he has only his own way for a little while.", "길어 봤자 그자는 잠깐 제멋대로 굴 뿐이지."], ["I have him periodically in a vice. I’ll twist him, sir.", "내가 주기적으로 그놈을 죔쇠에 물려 둔 셈이니까."], ["I’ll screw him, sir.", "비틀어 버리겠소, 어르신."], ["If he won’t do it with a good grace, I’ll make him do it with a bad one, sir!", "나사처럼 조여 버리겠소, 어르신. 흔쾌히 안 하겠다면, 마지못해서라도 하게 만들고 말겠소, 어르신!"], ["Now, my dear Mr. George,”", "자, 친애하는 조지 씨,\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed, winking at the lawyer hideously as he releases him, “I am ready for your kind assistance, my excellent friend!”", "스몰위드 영감이 변호사를 놓아주며 그에게 흉측하게 윙크를 보내고 말한다. \"이제 자네의 친절한 도움을 받을 준비가 됐네, 나의 훌륭한 벗이여!\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn, with some shadowy sign of amusement manifesting itself through his self-possession, stands on the hearth-rug with his back to the fire, watching the disappearance of Mr. Smallweed and acknowledging the trooper’s parting salute with one slight nod.", "토킹혼 씨는 침착함 사이로 어렴풋한 즐거움의 기색을 비치며, 벽난로 깔개 위에 불을 등지고 서서, 스몰위드 씨가 사라지는 것을 지켜보며, 기병이 작별하며 붙이는 경례에 가벼운 목례 한 번으로 답한다."]], [["It is more difficult to get rid of the old gentleman, Mr.", "조지 씨가 보니, 이 노인을 떼어내는 일이 그를 아래층으로 옮기는 일을 거드는 것보다 더 까다롭다."], ["George finds, than to bear a hand in carrying him downstairs, for when he is replaced in his conveyance, he is so loquacious on the subject of the guineas and retains such an affectionate hold of his button—having, in truth, a secret longing to rip his coat open and rob him—that some degree of force is necessary on the trooper’s part to effect a separation.", "운반 도구에 도로 앉혀 놓자 노인은 기니 이야기를 어찌나 수다스럽게 늘어놓고 또 그의 단추를 어찌나 애틋하게 붙잡고 늘어지는지—사실은 그의 외투를 찢어 열고 털어내고픈 은밀한 갈망을 품은 채—기병으로서는 어느 정도 힘을 써야만 떼어낼 수 있을 지경이다."], ["It is accomplished at last, and he proceeds alone in quest of his adviser.", "마침내 그 일을 해내고, 그는 홀로 자기 조언자를 찾아 나선다."]], [["By the cloisterly Temple, and by Whitefriars (there, not without a glance at Hanging-Sword Alley, which would seem to be something in his way), and by Blackfriars Bridge, and Blackfriars Road, Mr.", "회랑이 늘어선 템플을 지나고, 화이트프라이어스를 지나(거기서 행잉소드 골목을 한번 흘끗 보는데, 그 골목이 그의 진로상 무언가 의미가 있는 듯하다), 블랙프라이어스 다리와 블랙프라이어스 길을 지나, 조지 씨는 묵직한 발걸음으로 차분히 행진해 자그마한 가게들이 늘어선 어느 거리로 간다."], ["George sedately marches to a street of little shops lying somewhere in that ganglion of roads from Kent and Surrey, and of streets from the bridges of London, centring in the far-famed elephant who has lost his castle formed of a thousand four-horse coaches to a stronger iron monster than he, ready to chop him into mince-meat any day he dares.", "그 거리는 켄트와 서리에서 오는 길들과 런던의 다리들에서 오는 거리들이 한데 얽혀드는 그 길목 어딘가에 있는데, 그 한복판에는 그 유명한 코끼리가 버티고 있다. 한때 사두마차 천 대로 이루어진 제 성채를 자랑하던 그 코끼리는, 이제 저보다 더 힘센 무쇠 괴물에게 그 성채를 빼앗기고 말았으니, 그 괴물은 코끼리가 감히 덤비기만 하면 언제든 잘게 다져 버릴 태세다."], ["To one of the little shops in this street, which is a musician’s shop, having a few fiddles in the window, and some Pan’s pipes and a tambourine, and a triangle, and certain elongated scraps of music, Mr. George directs his massive tread.", "이 거리에 늘어선 작은 가게들 가운데 하나, 곧 진열창에 바이올린 몇 대와 팬파이프 몇 개, 탬버린 하나, 트라이앵글 하나, 그리고 길쭉길쭉한 악보 조각 몇 점을 늘어놓은 어느 악기점으로, 조지 씨는 그 묵직한 발걸음을 옮긴다."], ["And halting at a few paces from it, as he sees a soldierly looking woman, with her outer skirts tucked up, come forth with a small wooden tub, and in that tub commence a-whisking and a-splashing on the margin of the pavement, Mr.", "그러고는 거기서 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에 멈춰 서는데, 군인다워 보이는 한 여인이 겉치마를 걷어 올린 채 작은 나무통을 들고 나와, 보도 가장자리에서 그 통에 든 것을 휘젓고 첨벙거리기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 조지 씨가 혼잣말을 한다."], ["George says to himself, “She’s as usual, washing greens.", "\"여전하군, 푸성귀를 씻고 있어."], ["I never saw her, except upon a baggage-waggon, when she wasn’t washing greens!”", "짐마차 위에 있을 때 빼고는, 저 사람이 푸성귀 안 씻는 꼴을 본 적이 없다니까!\""]], [["The subject of this reflection is at all events so occupied in washing greens at present that she remains unsuspicious of Mr. George’s approach until, lifting up herself and her tub together when she has poured the water off into the gutter, she finds him standing near her.", "이 상념의 주인공은 어쨌거나 지금 푸성귀 씻는 일에 어찌나 골몰해 있는지, 조지 씨가 다가오는 것을 전혀 눈치채지 못하다가, 물을 도랑에 따라 버리고 통과 함께 몸을 일으키면서야 그가 가까이 서 있는 것을 알아챈다."], ["Her reception of him is not flattering.", "그를 맞이하는 태도는 살가운 구석이 없다."]], [["“George, I never see you but I wish you was a hundred mile away!”", "\"조지, 난 당신을 볼 때마다 차라리 백 마일은 멀리 떨어져 있었으면 싶다니까!\""]], [["The trooper, without remarking on this welcome, follows into the musical-instrument shop, where the lady places her tub of greens upon the counter, and having shaken hands with him, rests her arms upon it.", "기병은 이 인사에 대해 아무런 토를 달지 않고 그녀를 따라 악기점 안으로 들어가는데, 부인은 푸성귀 통을 계산대 위에 올려놓고 그와 악수를 나눈 다음 그 위에 두 팔을 괸다."]], [["“I never,”", "\"난 말이야, 조지,\""], ["she says, “George, consider Matthew Bagnet safe a minute when you’re near him.", "그녀가 말한다. \"당신이 곁에 있으면 단 일 분도 매슈 배그닛이 안전하다고 여겨지질 않아."], ["You are that restless and that roving—”", "당신이 그렇게나 좀이 쑤시고 떠돌이 기질이라서—\""]], [["“Yes!", "\"맞소!"], ["I know I am, Mrs. Bagnet.", "나도 알아요, 배그닛 부인."], ["I know I am.”", "나도 알지요.\""]], [["“You know you are!”", "\"안다면서!\""], ["says Mrs. Bagnet.", "배그닛 부인이 말한다."], ["“What’s the use of that?", "\"그게 무슨 소용이람?"], ["WHY are you?”", "대체 왜 그런 거야?\""]], [["“The nature of the animal, I suppose,”", "\"타고난 짐승 본성이겠지요, 아마.\""], ["returns the trooper good-humouredly.", "기병이 너스레 떨며 대꾸한다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"흥!\""], ["cries Mrs. Bagnet, something shrilly.", "배그닛 부인이 다소 날카롭게 외친다."], ["“But what satisfaction will the nature of the animal be to me when the animal shall have tempted my Mat away from the musical business to New Zealand or Australey?”", "\"그 짐승이 내 매트를 꾀어내서 악기 장사를 때려치우고 뉴질랜드니 오스트레일리아니로 데려가 버린다면, 그 짐승 본성이라는 게 나한테 무슨 위안이 되겠어?\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet is not at all an ill-looking woman.", "배그닛 부인은 결코 못생긴 여자가 아니다."], ["Rather large-boned, a little coarse in the grain, and freckled by the sun and wind which have tanned her hair upon the forehead, but healthy, wholesome, and bright-eyed.", "골격이 다소 굵고, 살결이 조금 거칠고, 햇볕과 바람에 그을려 주근깨가 돋고 이마께 머리칼이 누렇게 탔지만, 건강하고 정정하고 눈매가 또랑또랑하다."], ["A strong, busy, active, honest-faced woman of from forty-five to fifty.", "마흔다섯에서 쉰쯤 된, 야무지고 부지런하고 활기차고 정직한 얼굴의 여인이다."], ["Clean, hardy, and so economically dressed (though substantially) that the only article of ornament of which she stands possessed appear’s to be her wedding-ring, around which her finger has grown to be so large since it was put on that it will never come off again until it shall mingle with Mrs. Bagnet’s dust.", "깔끔하고 다부지고, 또 어찌나 알뜰하게(그러면서도 야무지게) 차려입었는지, 그녀가 지닌 장신구라곤 결혼반지 하나뿐인 듯한데, 그것을 끼운 뒤로 손가락이 어찌나 굵어졌는지 이제 그 반지는 배그닛 부인의 유골과 뒤섞일 때까지 결코 빠지지 않을 것이다."]], [["“Mrs. Bagnet,”", "\"배그닛 부인,\""], ["says the trooper, “I am on my parole with you.", "기병이 말한다. \"나는 당신에게 맹세코 약속하리다."], ["Mat will get no harm from me.", "매트는 나 때문에 아무 해도 입지 않을 거요."], ["You may trust me so far.”", "그 점만은 나를 믿어도 좋소.\""]], [["“Well, I think I may.", "\"글쎄, 믿어도 될 것 같긴 해."], ["But the very looks of you are unsettling,”", "하지만 당신은 보고만 있어도 마음이 들뜬다니까.\""], ["Mrs. Bagnet rejoins.", "배그닛 부인이 받아친다."], ["“Ah, George, George!", "\"아, 조지, 조지!"], ["If you had only settled down and married Joe Pouch’s widow when he died in North America, SHE’D have combed your hair for you.”", "당신이 북아메리카에서 조 파우치가 죽었을 때 그 과부랑 결혼해서 진작 자리나 잡았더라면, 그 여자가 당신 머리를 곱게 빗겨 줬을 텐데.\""]], [["“It was a chance for me, certainly,”", "\"확실히 나한테 기회이긴 했지요.\""], ["returns the trooper half laughingly, half seriously, “but I shall never settle down into a respectable man now.", "기병이 반은 웃으며 반은 진지하게 대꾸한다. \"하지만 난 이제 점잖은 사내로 자리 잡을 일은 영영 없을 거요."], ["Joe Pouch’s widow might have done me good—there was something in her, and something of her—but I couldn’t make up my mind to it.", "조 파우치의 과부가 나한테 잘해 줬을지도 모르지요—그 여자한테 뭔가가 있었고, 또 그 여자다운 무언가도 있었으니까—하지만 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없었소."], ["If I had had the luck to meet with such a wife as Mat found!”", "내가 매트가 만난 것 같은 아내를 만나는 행운이라도 누렸더라면 모를까!\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet, who seems in a virtuous way to be under little reserve with a good sort of fellow, but to be another good sort of fellow herself for that matter, receives this compliment by flicking Mr. George in the face with a head of greens and taking her tub into the little room behind the shop.", "배그닛 부인은 사람 좋은 사내한테는 순박한 식으로 별로 격을 차리지 않는 듯하지만, 그 점에서는 자기야말로 또 한 명의 사람 좋은 사내인지라, 이 칭찬에 답한답시고 푸성귀 한 포기로 조지 씨의 얼굴을 탁 치고는 통을 들고 가게 뒤편의 작은 방으로 들어간다."]], [["“Why, Quebec, my poppet,”", "\"이런, 퀘벡, 우리 귀염둥이,\""], ["says George, following, on invitation, into that department.", "조지가 청을 받고 그 방으로 따라 들어가며 말한다."], ["“And little Malta, too!", "\"그리고 우리 꼬마 몰타도!"], ["Come and kiss your Bluffy!”", "이리 와서 너희 블러피한테 뽀뽀해 주렴!\""]], [["These young ladies—not supposed to have been actually christened by the names applied to them, though always so called in the family from the places of their birth in barracks—are respectively employed on three-legged stools, the younger (some five or six years old) in learning her letters out of a penny primer, the elder (eight or nine perhaps) in teaching her and sewing with great assiduity.", "이 어린 아가씨들은—실제로 그렇게 세례명을 받은 건 아닌 듯하지만, 막사에서 태어난 곳을 따 집안에서는 늘 그렇게 불린다—각각 다리 셋 달린 걸상에 앉아 있는데, 동생은(대여섯 살쯤) 한 푼짜리 입문서로 글자를 배우는 중이고, 언니는(아마 여덟이나 아홉 살) 동생을 가르치며 부지런히 바느질을 하는 중이다."], ["Both hail Mr. George with acclamations as an old friend and after some kissing and romping plant their stools beside him.", "둘 다 조지 씨를 오랜 벗으로 반기며 환호하고, 한바탕 뽀뽀하고 뒹굴며 놀고 나서는 걸상을 그의 곁에 갖다 놓는다."]], [["“And how’s young Woolwich?”", "\"그래, 어린 울위치는 어떻게 지내요?\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["There now!” cries Mrs.", "그러게 말이야!\""], ["Bagnet, turning about from her saucepans (for she is cooking dinner) with a bright flush on her face.", "배그닛 부인이 (저녁을 짓고 있던 터라) 냄비들에서 몸을 돌리며, 얼굴에 환한 홍조를 띠고 외친다."], ["“Would you believe it?", "\"믿어지겠어?"], ["Got an engagement at the theayter, with his father, to play the fife in a military piece.”", "글쎄 극장에서 제 아비랑 함께 군악극에서 횡적을 부는 일자리를 얻었지 뭐야.\""]], [["“Well done, my godson!”", "\"잘했구나, 내 대자!\""], ["cries Mr. George, slapping his thigh.", "조지 씨가 제 허벅지를 탁 치며 외친다."]], [["“I believe you!”", "\"내 말이 그 말이라니까!\""], ["says Mrs. Bagnet.", "배그닛 부인이 말한다."], ["“He’s a Briton.", "\"걘 영국인이야."], ["That’s what Woolwich is.", "울위치가 바로 그런 애지."], ["A Briton!”", "영국인!\""]], [["“And Mat blows away at his bassoon, and you’re respectable civilians one and all,”", "\"그리고 매트는 바순을 불어대고, 당신네는 너나없이 점잖은 민간인들이고,\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“Family people.", "\"어엿한 가정을 이루고."], ["Children growing up.", "아이들은 무럭무럭 자라고."], ["Mat’s old mother in Scotland, and your old father somewhere else, corresponded with, and helped a little, and—well, well!", "스코틀랜드에 있는 매트의 노모와, 어딘가에 있는 당신네 노부와도 편지를 주고받고 조금씩 도와드리고, 그리고—그래, 그래!"], ["To be sure, I don’t know why I shouldn’t be wished a hundred mile away, for I have not much to do with all this!”", "정말이지, 내가 백 마일 멀리 떨어져 있었으면 좋겠다는 소리를 들어도 할 말이 없지, 난 이 모든 것과는 별 인연이 없으니까!\""]], [["Mr. George is becoming thoughtful, sitting before the fire in the whitewashed room, which has a sanded floor and a barrack smell and contains nothing superfluous and has not a visible speck of dirt or dust in it, from the faces of Quebec and Malta to the bright tin pots and pannikins upon the dresser shelves—Mr.", "조지 씨는 점점 생각에 잠겨든다."], ["George is becoming thoughtful, sitting here while Mrs. Bagnet is busy, when Mr.", "모래를 깔아 놓은 바닥에 막사 냄새가 풍기고, 군더더기라곤 하나도 없으며, 퀘벡과 몰타의 얼굴부터 찬장 선반 위의 반들거리는 양철 냄비며 작은 통에 이르기까지 눈에 띄는 먼지나 티끌 한 점 없는, 회칠한 그 방에서 벽난로 앞에 앉아—조지 씨는 점점 생각에 잠겨든다."], ["Bagnet and young Woolwich opportunely come home. Mr.", "배그닛 부인이 바쁘게 일하는 동안 그가 여기 앉아 있을 때, 배그닛 씨와 어린 울위치가 마침맞게 집으로 돌아온다."], ["Bagnet is an ex-artilleryman, tall and upright, with shaggy eyebrows and whiskers like the fibres of a coco-nut, not a hair upon his head, and a torrid complexion.", "배그닛 씨는 퇴역 포병으로, 키가 크고 꼿꼿하며, 텁수룩한 눈썹에 야자열매 섬유 같은 구레나룻을 길렀고, 머리에는 머리카락 한 올 없으며, 얼굴빛이 이글거리듯 검붉다."], ["His voice, short, deep, and resonant, is not at all unlike the tones of the instrument to which he is devoted.", "그의 목소리는 짧고 굵고 우렁찬데, 그가 애지중지하는 악기 소리와 조금도 다르지 않다."], ["Indeed there may be generally observed in him an unbending, unyielding, brass-bound air, as if he were himself the bassoon of the human orchestra.", "실로 그에게서는 대체로 강직하고 굽힐 줄 모르는, 놋쇠로 둘러싼 듯한 분위기가 풍기는데, 마치 그 자신이 인간 관현악단의 바순이기라도 한 것 같다."], ["Young Woolwich is the type and model of a young drummer.", "어린 울위치는 어린 고수의 전형이요 본보기다."]], [["Both father and son salute the trooper heartily. He saying, in due season, that he has come to advise with Mr.", "아비와 아들 둘 다 기병을 진심으로 반긴다."], ["Bagnet, Mr. Bagnet hospitably declares that he will hear of no business until after dinner and that his friend shall not partake of his counsel without first partaking of boiled pork and greens.", "그가 때맞춰 배그닛 씨와 의논하러 왔다고 말하자, 배그닛 씨는 저녁 식사 전에는 무슨 일이든 듣지 않겠노라, 또 자기 벗이 삶은 돼지고기와 푸성귀부터 먹지 않고서는 자기 조언도 들을 수 없노라 손님 대접하듯 선언한다."], ["The trooper yielding to this invitation, he and Mr. Bagnet, not to embarrass the domestic preparations, go forth to take a turn up and down the little street, which they promenade with measured tread and folded arms, as if it were a rampart.", "기병이 이 청에 응하자, 그와 배그닛 씨는 살림 채비에 방해되지 않도록 그 자그마한 거리를 한 바퀴 오르내리러 나가서, 마치 거기가 성벽이라도 되는 양 팔짱을 끼고 절도 있는 걸음걸이로 거닌다."]], [["“George,”", "\"조지,\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그닛 씨가 말한다."], ["“You know me.", "\"자네는 날 알지."], ["It’s my old girl that advises.", "조언은 내 마누라가 하는 거야."], ["She has the head.", "머리는 그 사람이 돌아가."], ["But I never own to it before her.", "하지만 난 그 사람 앞에서는 절대 그걸 인정 안 해."], ["Discipline must be maintained.", "규율은 지켜야 하니까."], ["Wait till the greens is off her mind.", "푸성귀가 그 사람 머릿속에서 떠날 때까지 기다리게."], ["Then we’ll consult.", "그런 다음에 의논하지."], ["Whatever the old girl says, do—do it!”", "마누라가 뭐라고 하든, 따르게—그대로 따라!\""]], [["“I intend to, Mat,”", "\"그럴 작정이야, 매트.\""], ["replies the other.", "상대가 대답한다."], ["“I would sooner take her opinion than that of a college.”", "\"난 대학교 의견보다 그 사람 의견을 더 따르고 싶으니까.\""]], [["“College,”", "\"대학교라.\""], ["returns Mr. Bagnet in short sentences, bassoon-like.", "배그닛 씨가 바순처럼 짧은 문장으로 대꾸한다."], ["“What college could you leave—in another quarter of the world—with nothing but a grey cloak and an umbrella—to make its way home to Europe?", "\"어떤 대학교가—세상 반대편 어느 구석에서—회색 외투 한 벌과 우산 하나만 달랑 들고—제 힘으로 유럽 집까지 찾아올 수 있겠나?"], ["The old girl would do it to-morrow.", "우리 마누라라면 당장 내일이라도 해낼걸."], ["Did it once!”", "실제로 한 번 했고!\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"자네 말이 맞아.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."]], [["“What college,”", "\"어떤 대학교가,\""], ["pursues Bagnet, “could you set up in life—with two penn’orth of white lime—a penn’orth of fuller’s earth—a ha’porth of sand—and the rest of the change out of sixpence in money?", "배그닛이 말을 잇는다. \"사람 인생을 일으켜 세울 수 있겠나—두 푼어치 백회 가루랑—한 푼어치 풀러스 흙이랑—반 푼어치 모래랑—그러고도 육 펜스에서 남은 잔돈만 가지고?"], ["That’s what the old girl started on.", "우리 마누라가 그걸로 시작했어."], ["In the present business.”", "지금 이 장사를.\""]], [["“I am rejoiced to hear it’s thriving, Mat.”", "\"잘되고 있다니 기쁜걸, 매트.\""]], [["“The old girl,” says Mr.", "\"우리 마누라는,\""], ["Bagnet, acquiescing, “saves.", "배그닛 씨가 수긍하며 말한다. \"저축을 해."], ["Has a stocking somewhere.", "어딘가에 양말짝을 하나 두고 있어."], ["With money in it.", "그 안에 돈이 들었지."], ["I never saw it.", "난 본 적 없어."], ["But I know she’s got it.", "하지만 그게 있다는 건 알지."], ["Wait till the greens is off her mind.", "푸성귀가 그 사람 머릿속에서 떠날 때까지 기다리게."], ["Then she’ll set you up.”", "그런 다음에 자넬 일으켜 줄 테니.\""]], [["“She is a treasure!”", "\"보물 같은 분이로군!\""], ["exclaims Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 외친다."]], [["“She’s more.", "\"그 이상이지."], ["But I never own to it before her.", "하지만 난 그 사람 앞에서는 절대 인정 안 해."], ["Discipline must be maintained.", "규율은 지켜야 하니까."], ["It was the old girl that brought out my musical abilities.", "내 음악 재주를 끌어내 준 게 우리 마누라야."], ["I should have been in the artillery now but for the old girl.", "마누라가 아니었으면 난 지금도 포병대에 있었을걸."], ["Six years I hammered at the fiddle.", "육 년을 바이올린에 매달렸어."], ["Ten at the flute.", "십 년을 플루트에."], ["The old girl said it wouldn’t do; intention good, but want of flexibility; try the bassoon.", "마누라가 그건 안 되겠다더군. 마음은 좋은데 융통성이 부족하다고."], ["The old girl borrowed a bassoon from the bandmaster of the Rifle Regiment.", "바순을 해 보라더라고. 마누라가 소총 연대 군악대장한테서 바순을 빌려 왔어."], ["I practised in the trenches.", "난 참호 속에서 연습했지."], ["Got on, got another, get a living by it!”", "늘었고, 또 한 대 더 구하고, 그걸로 먹고살게 됐어!\""]], [["George remarks that she looks as fresh as a rose and as sound as an apple.", "조지가 부인은 장미처럼 싱싱하고 사과처럼 탱탱해 보인다고 한마디 한다."]], [["“The old girl,” says Mr.", "\"우리 마누라는,\""], ["Bagnet in reply, “is a thoroughly fine woman.", "배그닛 씨가 답으로 말한다. \"철두철미 훌륭한 여자야."], ["Consequently she is like a thoroughly fine day.", "그러니 철두철미 화창한 날 같지."], ["Gets finer as she gets on.", "나이 들수록 더 화창해져."], ["I never saw the old girl’s equal.", "우리 마누라만 한 사람은 본 적이 없어."], ["But I never own to it before her.", "하지만 난 그 사람 앞에서는 절대 인정 안 해."], ["Discipline must be maintained!”", "규율은 지켜야 하니까!\""]], [["Proceeding to converse on indifferent matters, they walk up and down the little street, keeping step and time, until summoned by Quebec and Malta to do justice to the pork and greens, over which Mrs. Bagnet, like a military chaplain, says a short grace.", "이런저런 시답잖은 일들을 이야기하며 그들은 보조와 박자를 맞춰 그 자그마한 거리를 오르내리다가, 마침내 퀘벡과 몰타에게 불려 돼지고기와 푸성귀를 정당하게 대접받으러 가는데, 그 음식을 앞에 두고 배그닛 부인은 군목처럼 짤막한 식전 기도를 올린다."], ["In the distribution of these comestibles, as in every other household duty, Mrs. Bagnet develops an exact system, sitting with every dish before her, allotting to every portion of pork its own portion of pot-liquor, greens, potatoes, and even mustard, and serving it out complete.", "이 음식을 나누어 줄 때도, 다른 모든 집안일에서와 마찬가지로, 배그닛 부인은 빈틈없는 체계를 발휘하여, 모든 접시를 제 앞에 늘어놓고, 돼지고기 한 토막 한 토막에 각자 몫의 육수와 푸성귀와 감자, 심지어 겨자까지 배정하여 완전하게 차려서 내놓는다."], ["Having likewise served out the beer from a can and thus supplied the mess with all things necessary, Mrs. Bagnet proceeds to satisfy her own hunger, which is in a healthy state.", "마찬가지로 깡통에서 맥주까지 따라 돌려 식구들에게 필요한 모든 것을 갖추어 준 다음에야, 배그닛 부인은 비로소 제 허기를 채우는데, 그 허기는 건강한 상태다."], ["The kit of the mess, if the table furniture may be so denominated, is chiefly composed of utensils of horn and tin that have done duty in several parts of the world.", "식탁 살림이라 부를 만한 것들은—식탁에 차린 살림이 그렇게 불릴 수 있다면—주로 뿔과 양철로 만든 도구들로, 세계 여러 곳에서 한몫씩 해 온 물건들이다."], ["Young Woolwich’s knife, in particular, which is of the oyster kind, with the additional feature of a strong shutting-up movement which frequently balks the appetite of that young musician, is mentioned as having gone in various hands the complete round of foreign service.", "특히 어린 울위치의 칼은 굴 까는 종류에다 한 가지 특징이 더해진 것인데, 강하게 접히는 동작 때문에 그 어린 악사의 식욕을 자주 좌절시키는 그 칼은, 여러 손을 거쳐 외국 복무지를 빠짐없이 돌고 돌아온 물건이라는 이야기가 나온다."]], [["The dinner done, Mrs. Bagnet, assisted by the younger branches (who polish their own cups and platters, knives and forks), makes all the dinner garniture shine as brightly as before and puts it all away, first sweeping the hearth, to the end that Mr. Bagnet and the visitor may not be retarded in the smoking of their pipes. These household cares involve much pattening and counter-pattening in the backyard and considerable use of a pail, which is finally so happy as to assist in the ablutions of Mrs. Bagnet herself. That old girl reappearing by and by, quite fresh, and sitting down to her needlework, then and only then—the greens being only then to be considered as entirely off her mind—Mr. Bagnet requests the trooper to state his case.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자, 배그닛 부인은 어린 가지들의 (제 잔과 접시며 칼과 포크를 직접 닦는) 도움을 받아, 식탁의 모든 장식거리를 전과 다름없이 환하게 반짝이게 닦아 죄다 치워 놓는데, 배그닛 씨와 손님이 파이프 피우는 일에 지장이 없도록 먼저 벽난로 앞부터 쓸어낸다. 이런 집안일에는 뒷마당에서 나막신을 신고 오가는 일이 잔뜩 따르고 물통도 상당히 쓰이는데, 그 물통은 마침내 배그닛 부인 자신의 몸단장을 거드는 영광까지 누린다. 그 마누라가 이윽고 아주 산뜻한 모습으로 다시 나타나 바느질거리를 들고 자리에 앉고서야, 바로 그제야—그제야 비로소 푸성귀가 그녀의 머릿속에서 완전히 떠났다고 여길 수 있으므로—배그닛 씨는 기병에게 사정을 말해 보라고 청한다."]], [["This Mr. George does with great discretion, appearing to address himself to Mr.", "이 일을 조지 씨는 무척 신중하게 해내는데, 겉으로는 배그닛 씨에게 말하는 척하면서도, 배그닛 씨 자신이 그러하듯, 내내 오직 그 마누라에게만 눈을 두고 있다."], ["Bagnet, but having an eye solely on the old girl all the time, as Bagnet has himself. She, equally discreet, busies herself with her needlework.", "그녀 역시 똑같이 신중하게 제 바느질거리에 골몰한다."], ["The case fully stated, Mr. Bagnet resorts to his standard artifice for the maintenance of discipline.", "사정을 다 말하자, 배그닛 씨는 규율 유지를 위한 자기 특유의 술책에 의지한다."]], [["“That’s the whole of it, is it, George?”", "\"그게 전부인가, 조지?\""], ["says he.", "그가 말한다."]], [["“That’s the whole of it.”", "\"그게 전부일세.\""]], [["“You act according to my opinion?”", "\"내 의견대로 하겠다는 거지?\""]], [["“I shall be guided,”", "\"전적으로 그것에 따르겠네.\""], ["replies George, “entirely by it.”", "조지가 대답한다."]], [["“Old girl,”", "\"마누라,\""], ["says Mr.", "배그닛 씨가 말한다."], ["Bagnet, “give him my opinion.", "\"내 의견을 이 친구한테 말해 줘."], ["You know it.", "당신도 알잖아."], ["Tell him what it is.”", "그게 뭔지 말해 주라고.\""]], [["It is that he cannot have too little to do with people who are too deep for him and cannot be too careful of interference with matters he does not understand—that the plain rule is to do nothing in the dark, to be a party to nothing underhanded or mysterious, and never to put his foot where he cannot see the ground. This, in effect, is Mr.", "그 의견인즉, 자기로서는 도무지 속을 헤아릴 수 없는 사람들과는 되도록 엮이지 않을수록 좋고, 자기가 이해하지 못하는 일에 끼어드는 데는 아무리 조심해도 지나치지 않다는 것—요컨대 어둠 속에서는 아무 일도 하지 말고, 음흉하거나 알 수 없는 일에는 한 패가 되지 말며, 땅이 보이지 않는 곳에는 결코 발을 들이지 말라는 것이 명백한 원칙이라는 것이다."], ["Bagnet’s opinion, as delivered through the old girl, and it so relieves Mr. George’s mind by confirming his own opinion and banishing his doubts that he composes himself to smoke another pipe on that exceptional occasion and to have a talk over old times with the whole Bagnet family, according to their various ranges of experience.", "이것이, 그 마누라의 입을 통해 전해진 배그닛 씨의 의견의 골자인데, 이 의견은 조지 씨 자신의 생각을 뒷받침하고 그의 의구심을 몰아냄으로써 그의 마음을 어찌나 놓이게 하는지, 그는 이 특별한 자리를 빌려 파이프를 한 대 더 피우며 마음을 가라앉히고, 배그닛 가족 전원과 저마다의 경험 폭에 따라 옛이야기를 나눈다."]], [["Through these means it comes to pass that Mr. George does not again rise to his full height in that parlour until the time is drawing on when the bassoon and fife are expected by a British public at the theatre; and as it takes time even then for Mr.", "이런 까닭에, 조지 씨가 그 거실에서 다시 한껏 키를 펴고 일어서는 것은, 영국 대중이 극장에서 바순과 횡적을 기다릴 시각이 다가올 무렵에 이르러서다."], ["George, in his domestic character of Bluffy, to take leave of Quebec and Malta and insinuate a sponsorial shilling into the pocket of his godson with felicitations on his success in life, it is dark when Mr. George again turns his face towards Lincoln’s Inn Fields.", "더구나 그렇게 되어서도, 조지 씨가 블러피라는 가정 안의 인물로서 퀘벡과 몰타에게 작별을 고하고, 인생의 성공을 축하하며 대자의 주머니에 대부로서 일 실링을 슬쩍 찔러 넣는 데에도 시간이 걸리는 터라, 조지 씨가 다시 링컨스인필즈로 발길을 돌릴 때에는 이미 날이 저물어 있다."]], [["“A family home,”", "\"가정이라는 건,\""], ["he ruminates as he marches along, “however small it is, makes a man like me look lonely.", "그가 걸어가며 곰곰이 생각한다. \"아무리 자그마해도, 나 같은 사람을 외로워 보이게 만든단 말이지."], ["But it’s well I never made that evolution of matrimony.", "하지만 내가 그 결혼이라는 전술 변환을 끝내 안 한 건 잘한 일이야."], ["I shouldn’t have been fit for it.", "난 그런 데 어울리는 위인이 못 됐을 테니."], ["I am such a vagabond still, even at my present time of life, that I couldn’t hold to the gallery a month together if it was a regular pursuit or if I didn’t camp there, gipsy fashion.", "이 나이가 되어서도 여전히 이렇게나 떠돌이 신세라, 사격 연습장 일이 정규 생업이거나, 거기서 집시처럼 천막을 치고 살지 않는다면야, 한 달도 붙어 있질 못할 거야."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["I disgrace nobody and cumber nobody; that’s something.", "난 누구한테 망신을 주지도 않고 누구한테 짐이 되지도 않아."], ["I have not done that for many a long year!”", "그것만 해도 어디냐. 오랜 세월 그것조차 못 해 봤으니!\""]], [["So he whistles it off and marches on.", "그래서 그는 그것을 휘파람으로 떨쳐 버리고 계속 행진한다."]], [["Arrived in Lincoln’s Inn Fields and mounting Mr.", "링컨스인필즈에 다다라 토킹혼 씨의 계단을 오르니, 바깥문이 닫혀 있고 사무실도 잠겨 있다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s stair, he finds the outer door closed and the chambers shut, but the trooper not knowing much about outer doors, and the staircase being dark besides, he is yet fumbling and groping about, hoping to discover a bell-handle or to open the door for himself, when Mr.", "그러나 기병은 바깥문에 대해 잘 모르는 데다 계단마저 어두운 터라, 초인종 손잡이라도 찾아내거나 제 손으로 문을 열어 볼까 싶어 여태 더듬거리며 헤매고 있는데, 토킹혼 씨가 (물론 조용히) 계단을 올라와 성난 목소리로 묻는다."], ["Tulkinghorn comes up the stairs (quietly, of course) and angrily asks, “Who is that?", "\"거 누구요?"], ["What are you doing there?”", "거기서 뭐 하는 거요?\""]], [["“I ask your pardon, sir.", "\"실례합니다, 어르신."], ["It’s George.", "조지입니다."], ["The sergeant.”", "하사관 말입니다.\""]], [["“And couldn’t George, the sergeant, see that my door was locked?”", "\"그래, 하사관 조지는 내 문이 잠긴 것도 못 봤단 말이오?\""]], [["“Why, no, sir, I couldn’t.", "\"그게, 네, 못 봤습니다, 어르신."], ["At any rate, I didn’t,”", "어쨌든, 못 봤습니다.\""], ["says the trooper, rather nettled.", "기병이 다소 부아가 나서 말한다."]], [["“Have you changed your mind?", "\"마음을 바꿨소?"], ["Or are you in the same mind?”", "아니면 그대로요?\""], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn demands.", "토킹혼 씨가 묻는다."], ["But he knows well enough at a glance.", "하지만 그는 한눈에 충분히 알고 있다."]], [["“In the same mind, sir.”", "\"그대롭니다, 어르신.\""]], [["“I thought so.", "\"그럴 줄 알았소."], ["That’s sufficient.", "그걸로 됐소."], ["You can go.", "가도 좋소."], ["So you are the man,” says Mr.", "그래, 자네가 바로 그 사람이로군,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, opening his door with the key, “in whose hiding-place Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 열쇠로 문을 열며 말한다."], ["Gridley was found?”", "\"자네 은신처에서 그리들리가 발견됐다지?\""]], [["“Yes, I AM the man,”", "\"네, 제가 바로 그 사람입니다.\""], ["says the trooper, stopping two or three stairs down.", "기병이 두세 계단 아래에서 멈춰 서며 말한다."], ["“What then, sir?”", "\"그래서 어쨌다는 겁니까, 어르신?\""]], [["“What then?", "\"어쨌다고?"], ["I don’t like your associates.", "난 자네 패거리가 마음에 안 드네."], ["You should not have seen the inside of my door this morning if I had thought of your being that man.", "자네가 그자라는 걸 진작 알았더라면, 오늘 아침 내 문 안쪽은 구경도 못 했을 거요. 그리들리라니?"], ["Gridley? A threatening, murderous, dangerous fellow.”", "위협이나 일삼는, 살기등등한, 위험한 작자.\""]], [["With these words, spoken in an unusually high tone for him, the lawyer goes into his rooms and shuts the door with a thundering noise.", "평소답지 않게 한껏 높인 목소리로 이 말을 내뱉고, 변호사는 자기 방으로 들어가 천둥 같은 소리를 내며 문을 닫는다."]], [["Mr. George takes his dismissal in great dudgeon, the greater because a clerk coming up the stairs has heard the last words of all and evidently applies them to him.", "조지 씨는 몹시 언짢게 이 축객령을 받아들이는데, 계단을 올라오던 서기 하나가 그 마지막 말을 죄다 듣고 그것을 분명히 자기한테 적용하는 통에 더욱 언짢다."], ["“A pretty character to bear,”", "\"참 듣기 좋은 평판일세.\""], ["the trooper growls with a hasty oath as he strides downstairs.", "기병이 황급히 욕을 한마디 내뱉으며 성큼성큼 계단을 내려가며 으르렁댄다."], ["“A threatening, murderous, dangerous fellow!”", "\"위협이나 일삼는, 살기등등한, 위험한 작자라니!\""], ["And looking up, he sees the clerk looking down at him and marking him as he passes a lamp.", "그러고는 고개를 들어 보니, 그 서기가 그를 내려다보며, 그가 등불을 지나칠 때 그 모습을 눈여겨 새겨 두고 있다."], ["This so intensifies his dudgeon that for five minutes he is in an ill humour.", "이 때문에 그의 언짢음이 어찌나 심해지는지, 그는 오 분쯤 기분이 잡친 채로 있다."], ["But he whistles that off like the rest of it and marches home to the shooting gallery.", "그러나 그는 나머지 것들처럼 그것도 휘파람으로 떨쳐 버리고 사격 연습장 집으로 행진해 간다."]], [["The Ironmaster\nSir Leicester Dedlock has got the better, for the time being, of the family gout and is once more, in a literal no less than in a figurative point of view, upon his legs.", "레스터 데드록 경은 가문의 통풍을 이번만큼은 이겨내고, 비유적으로뿐 아니라 글자 그대로의 의미에서도 다시금 제 두 다리로 일어섰다. 그는 링컨셔의 영지에 머물고 있다."], ["He is at his place in Lincolnshire; but the waters are out again on the low-lying grounds, and the cold and damp steal into Chesney Wold, though well defended, and eke into Sir Leicester’s bones.", "그러나 저지대 땅에는 또다시 물이 차오르고, 추위와 습기가 야무지게 방비한 체스니 월드에까지 스며들며, 덤으로 레스터 경의 뼛속까지 파고든다."], ["The blazing fires of faggot and coal—Dedlock timber and antediluvian forest—that blaze upon the broad wide hearths and wink in the twilight on the frowning woods, sullen to see how trees are sacrificed, do not exclude the enemy.", "데드록 가문의 목재와 태곳적 숲에서 나온 장작과 석탄이 넓디넓은 난로에서 활활 타오르며, 나무가 어떻게 희생되는지 못마땅하게 지켜보는 음울한 숲을 향해 황혼녘에 깜박이지만, 그래도 적을 막아내지는 못한다."], ["The hot-water pipes that trail themselves all over the house, the cushioned doors and windows, and the screens and curtains fail to supply the fires’ deficiencies and to satisfy Sir Leicester’s need.", "집안 구석구석을 휘감으며 뻗어 나간 온수관도, 쿠션을 댄 문과 창문도, 칸막이와 커튼도, 난롯불의 부족분을 채우거나 레스터 경의 필요를 만족시키지는 못한다."], ["Hence the fashionable intelligence proclaims one morning to the listening earth that Lady Dedlock is expected shortly to return to town for a few weeks.", "그리하여 어느 날 아침 상류층 소식통은 귀 기울이는 온 세상에 데드록 마님이 몇 주 묵을 요량으로 곧 도시로 돌아올 예정이라고 알린다."]], [["It is a melancholy truth that even great men have their poor relations.", "위대한 인물에게도 가난한 친척이 있다는 것은 서글픈 진실이다."], ["Indeed great men have often more than their fair share of poor relations, inasmuch as very red blood of the superior quality, like inferior blood unlawfully shed, WILL cry aloud and WILL be heard.", "사실 위대한 인물은 가난한 친척을 분에 넘치게 많이 거느린 경우가 흔하다. 상등품의 새빨간 피라는 것이, 불법으로 흘린 하등의 피와 마찬가지로, 기어이 큰소리로 울부짖고 기어이 누군가의 귀에 들리고야 마는 까닭이다."], ["Sir Leicester’s cousins, in the remotest degree, are so many Murders in the respect that they “will out.”", "레스터 경의 친척은, 아무리 먼 촌수라도, \"들통나고야 마는\" 살인 사건처럼 수도 없이 많다."], ["Among whom there are cousins who are so poor that one might almost dare to think it would have been the happier for them never to have been plated links upon the Dedlock chain of gold, but to have been made of common iron at first and done base service.", "그 가운데에는 너무도 가난한 친척들이 있어서, 차라리 데드록 가문의 황금 사슬에 끼워진 도금 고리가 되지 말고, 애당초 흔해 빠진 무쇠로 만들어져 천한 일이나 하며 살았더라면 더 행복했으리라고 거의 단언하고 싶을 지경이다."]], [["Service, however (with a few limited reservations, genteel but not profitable), they may not do, being of the Dedlock dignity.", "그러나 일이라는 것은(점잖되 돈벌이는 되지 않는 몇 가지 제한된 예외를 빼면) 데드록 가문의 품위 때문에 그들이 손댈 수 없는 노릇이다."], ["So they visit their richer cousins, and get into debt when they can, and live but shabbily when they can’t, and find—the women no husbands, and the men no wives—and ride in borrowed carriages, and sit at feasts that are never of their own making, and so go through high life.", "그래서 그들은 더 부유한 친척을 찾아다니고, 가능할 때는 빚을 지며, 그러지 못할 때는 초라하게 산다. 그리하여 여자들은 남편을 못 찾고 남자들은 아내를 못 찾으며, 빌린 마차를 타고, 제 손으로는 결코 차린 적 없는 잔칫상에 앉아, 그렇게 상류 생활을 헤쳐 나간다."], ["The rich family sum has been divided by so many figures, and they are the something over that nobody knows what to do with.", "부유한 가문의 합계가 너무 많은 숫자로 나뉜 탓에, 그들은 아무도 처치할 줄 모르는 나머지 우수리다."]], [["Everybody on Sir Leicester Dedlock’s side of the question and of his way of thinking would appear to be his cousin more or less.", "레스터 데드록 경 편에 서서 그와 같은 방식으로 생각하는 사람이라면 누구나 다소간 그의 친척인 듯하다."], ["From my Lord Boodle, through the Duke of Foodle, down to Noodle, Sir Leicester, like a glorious spider, stretches his threads of relationship.", "부들 경부터 푸들 공작을 거쳐 누들에 이르기까지, 레스터 경은 찬란한 거미처럼 친척 관계의 실을 사방으로 뻗는다."], ["But while he is stately in the cousinship of the Everybodys, he is a kind and generous man, according to his dignified way, in the cousinship of the Nobodys; and at the present time, in despite of the damp, he stays out the visit of several such cousins at Chesney Wold with the constancy of a martyr.", "그러나 그는 누구나 다 아는 거물 친척들 앞에서는 위엄을 차리면서도, 아무도 모르는 보잘것없는 친척들에게는 자기 나름의 점잖은 방식으로 친절하고 너그러운 사람이다. 그래서 지금도 그는 습기에도 아랑곳없이, 체스니 월드에 찾아온 그런 친척 몇몇의 방문을 순교자 같은 인내심으로 끝까지 견뎌낸다."]], [["Of these, foremost in the front rank stands Volumnia Dedlock, a young lady (of sixty) who is doubly highly related, having the honour to be a poor relation, by the mother’s side, to another great family.", "그 가운데 맨 앞줄 선두에 서는 인물이 볼룸니아 데드록이다. 또 다른 명문가에 외가 쪽으로 가난한 친척이 되는 영예를 누리니, 이중으로 고귀한 혈통을 지닌 (예순 살의) 젊은 아가씨다."], ["Miss Volumnia, displaying in early life a pretty talent for cutting ornaments out of coloured paper, and also for singing to the guitar in the Spanish tongue, and propounding French conundrums in country houses, passed the twenty years of her existence between twenty and forty in a sufficiently agreeable manner.", "볼룸니아 양은 어린 시절부터 색종이를 오려 장식품 만드는 데 제법 솜씨를 보였고, 기타에 맞춰 스페인 말로 노래를 부르거나 시골 저택에서 프랑스어 수수께끼를 내는 재주도 있어서, 스무 살에서 마흔 살 사이의 이십 년 세월을 그런대로 즐겁게 보냈다."], ["Lapsing then out of date and being considered to bore mankind by her vocal performances in the Spanish language, she retired to Bath, where she lives slenderly on an annual present from Sir Leicester and whence she makes occasional resurrections in the country houses of her cousins.", "그러다 한물가 버리고, 스페인 말 노래 솜씨로 사람들을 지겹게 한다는 평을 듣게 되자, 그녀는 바스로 물러나 레스터 경이 해마다 보내주는 선물에 의지해 빠듯하게 살며, 이따금 친척들의 시골 저택에서 부활하곤 한다."], ["She has an extensive acquaintance at Bath among appalling old gentlemen with thin legs and nankeen trousers, and is of high standing in that dreary city.", "그녀는 바스에서 다리가 가늘고 무명 바지를 입은 소름 끼치는 노신사들 사이에 폭넓은 친분을 쌓아, 그 음산한 도시에서 높은 지위를 누린다."], ["But she is a little dreaded elsewhere in consequence of an indiscreet profusion in the article of rouge and persistency in an obsolete pearl necklace like a rosary of little bird’s-eggs.", "그러나 다른 곳에서는 다소 꺼림칙한 존재인데, 연지를 분별없이 잔뜩 처바르는 데다, 작은 새알을 꿴 묵주 같은 한물간 진주 목걸이를 고집스레 걸고 다니는 탓이다."]], [["In any country in a wholesome state, Volumnia would be a clear case for the pension list.", "건전한 상태에 놓인 나라라면 어디서든 볼룸니아는 두말없이 연금 대상자가 되었을 것이다."], ["Efforts have been made to get her on it, and when William Buffy came in, it was fully expected that her name would be put down for a couple of hundred a year.", "그녀를 연금 명단에 올리려는 노력이 있었고, 윌리엄 버피가 입각했을 때는 그녀의 이름이 연 이백 파운드짜리로 적힐 거라고 다들 기대했다."], ["But William Buffy somehow discovered, contrary to all expectation, that these were not the times when it could be done, and this was the first clear indication Sir Leicester Dedlock had conveyed to him that the country was going to pieces.", "그러나 윌리엄 버피는 어쩐 일인지, 모두의 기대와 달리, 지금이 그런 일을 할 수 있는 시절이 아님을 알아냈으니, 이것이 레스터 데드록 경에게 나라가 망조에 들었다고 전한 첫 번째 명백한 징조였다."]], [["There is likewise the Honourable Bob Stables, who can make warm mashes with the skill of a veterinary surgeon and is a better shot than most gamekeepers.", "마찬가지로 명예로운 밥 스테이블스라는 인물도 있는데, 수의사 못지않은 솜씨로 따뜻한 사료죽을 쑬 줄 알고, 웬만한 사냥터지기보다 명사수다."], ["He has been for some time particularly desirous to serve his country in a post of good emoluments, unaccompanied by any trouble or responsibility.", "그는 한동안 골치 아픈 일이나 책임이 따르지 않으면서도 보수가 두둑한 자리에서 나라에 봉사하기를 유독 갈망해왔다."], ["In a well-regulated body politic this natural desire on the part of a spirited young gentleman so highly connected would be speedily recognized, but somehow William Buffy found when he came in that these were not times in which he could manage that little matter either, and this was the second indication Sir Leicester Dedlock had conveyed to him that the country was going to pieces.", "잘 굴러가는 정치 조직이라면, 이토록 고귀한 연줄을 지닌 패기만만한 젊은 신사의 이 자연스러운 소망이 곧장 인정받았을 텐데, 어쩐 일인지 윌리엄 버피는 입각하고서 지금이 그 사소한 일조차 처리할 수 있는 시절이 아님을 알아냈으니, 이것이 레스터 데드록 경에게 나라가 망조에 들었다고 전한 두 번째 징조였다."]], [["The rest of the cousins are ladies and gentlemen of various ages and capacities, the major part amiable and sensible and likely to have done well enough in life if they could have overcome their cousinship; as it is, they are almost all a little worsted by it, and lounge in purposeless and listless paths, and seem to be quite as much at a loss how to dispose of themselves as anybody else can be how to dispose of them.", "나머지 친척들은 나이도 능력도 제각각인 신사 숙녀로, 대부분은 상냥하고 분별 있으며, 친척이라는 굴레만 극복할 수 있었다면 인생을 그럭저럭 잘 꾸려나갔을 사람들이다. 사정이 그렇다 보니 그들은 거의 다 그 굴레에 조금씩 휘둘려, 목적도 의욕도 없는 길에서 빈둥대며, 남들이 그들을 어떻게 처치해야 할지 몰라 난감해하는 만큼이나 자기 자신을 어찌해야 할지 몰라 갈피를 못 잡는 듯하다."]], [["In this society, and where not, my Lady Dedlock reigns supreme.", "이런 무리 가운데서, 아니 어디서든, 데드록 마님은 최고로 군림한다."], ["Beautiful, elegant, accomplished, and powerful in her little world (for the world of fashion does not stretch ALL the way from pole to pole), her influence in Sir Leicester’s house, however haughty and indifferent her manner, is greatly to improve it and refine it.", "아름답고 우아하고 교양 있고, 자기만의 작은 세계에서 막강한 그녀이고 보니(유행의 세계라는 것이 극에서 극까지 죄다 뻗어 있는 건 아니니까), 비록 그 태도는 도도하고 무심할지언정, 레스터 경의 저택에서 그녀가 끼치는 영향은 그 집을 크게 끌어올리고 세련되게 한다."], ["The cousins, even those older cousins who were paralysed when Sir Leicester married her, do her feudal homage; and the Honourable Bob Stables daily repeats to some chosen person between breakfast and lunch his favourite original remark, that she is the best-groomed woman in the whole stud.", "친척들은, 심지어 레스터 경이 그녀와 결혼했을 때 충격으로 몸이 마비될 지경이던 손위 친척들마저, 그녀에게 봉건시대의 신하처럼 예를 갖춘다. 그리고 명예로운 밥 스테이블스는 날마다 아침과 점심 사이에 누군가 골라잡은 사람에게 자기가 가장 아끼는 독창적인 말을 되풀이하니, 그녀가 온 마구간을 통틀어 가장 잘 손질된 암말이라는 것이다."]], [["Such the guests in the long drawing-room at Chesney Wold this dismal night when the step on the Ghost’s Walk (inaudible here, however) might be the step of a deceased cousin shut out in the cold.", "유령의 길에 울리는 발소리가(하기야 여기서는 들리지도 않지만) 추위에 쫓겨난 고인 친척의 발소리일지도 모를 이 음울한 밤, 체스니 월드의 기다란 응접실에 모인 손님들이 바로 그런 무리다."], ["It is near bed-time.", "잠자리에 들 시각이 가깝다."], ["Bedroom fires blaze brightly all over the house, raising ghosts of grim furniture on wall and ceiling.", "침실마다 난롯불이 환하게 타오르며, 벽과 천장에 음산한 가구의 유령을 일으켜 세운다."], ["Bedroom candlesticks bristle on the distant table by the door, and cousins yawn on ottomans.", "문가의 먼 탁자에는 침실용 촛대들이 빽빽이 늘어서 있고, 친척들은 오토만에 앉아 하품을 한다."], ["Cousins at the piano, cousins at the soda-water tray, cousins rising from the card-table, cousins gathered round the fire.", "피아노 앞에 친척, 탄산수 쟁반 곁에 친척, 카드 탁자에서 일어서는 친척, 난롯가에 둘러앉은 친척."], ["Standing on one side of his own peculiar fire (for there are two), Sir Leicester.", "자기 전용 난로(난로가 둘이 있다) 한쪽에 서 있는 레스터 경."], ["On the opposite side of the broad hearth, my Lady at her table.", "넓은 난로의 맞은편에 자기 탁자 앞에 앉은 마님."], ["Volumnia, as one of the more privileged cousins, in a luxurious chair between them.", "더 특권을 누리는 친척의 하나로서 두 사람 사이의 호화로운 의자에 앉은 볼룸니아."], ["Sir Leicester glancing, with magnificent displeasure, at the rouge and the pearl necklace.", "그 연지와 진주 목걸이를 더없이 못마땅한 눈길로 흘끔거리는 레스터 경."]], [["“I occasionally meet on my staircase here,”", "\"이 집 층계에서 가끔 마주치는데,\""], ["drawls Volumnia, whose thoughts perhaps are already hopping up it to bed, after a long evening of very desultory talk, “one of the prettiest girls, I think, that I ever saw in my life.”", "매우 두서없는 이야기로 긴 저녁을 보낸 터라 생각이 벌써 층계를 콩콩 뛰어올라 잠자리로 향하는 듯한 볼룸니아가 느릿느릿 말한다. \"내 평생 본 가운데 가장 예쁜 축에 드는 아가씨를 말이에요.\""]], [["“A _protégée_ of my Lady’s,”", "\"마님께서 거두신 아이지요.\""], ["observes Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 한마디 한다."]], [["“I thought so.", "\"그럴 줄 알았어요."], ["I felt sure that some uncommon eye must have picked that girl out.", "그 아이를 골라낸 건 분명 보통 안목이 아니라고 확신했지요. 정말이지 경이로운 아이예요."], ["She really is a marvel.", "인형 같은 미모랄까,\""], ["A dolly sort of beauty perhaps,”", "볼룸니아 양은 자기 취향은 따로 남겨두며 말한다."], ["says Miss Volumnia, reserving her own sort, “but in its way, perfect; such bloom I never saw!”", "\"하지만 그 나름으로는 완벽하죠. 그렇게 싱그러운 혈색은 정말 본 적이 없어요!\""]], [["Sir Leicester, with his magnificent glance of displeasure at the rouge, appears to say so too.", "연지를 더없이 못마땅한 눈길로 흘끔거리는 레스터 경도 그렇다고 말하는 듯하다."]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"실은,\""], ["remarks my Lady languidly, “if there is any uncommon eye in the case, it is Mrs.", "마님이 나른하게 말한다. \"이 일에 보통 안목이 끼어 있다면, 그건 내가 아니라 라운스웰 부인의 안목이에요."], ["Rouncewell’s, and not mine. Rosa is her discovery.”", "로자는 그이가 찾아낸 아이지요.\""]], [["“Your maid, I suppose?”", "\"마님의 하녀인가 보군요?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니에요."], ["My anything; pet—secretary—messenger—I don’t know what.”", "뭐든 다 해주는 아이죠. 애완동물이자, 비서이자, 심부름꾼이자, 글쎄 뭐라 해야 할지.\""]], [["“You like to have her about you, as you would like to have a flower, or a bird, or a picture, or a poodle—no, not a poodle, though—or anything else that was equally pretty?”", "\"마치 꽃이나 새나 그림이나 푸들을 곁에 두고 싶은 것처럼, 아니, 푸들은 아니고, 아무튼 그만큼 예쁜 무언가를 곁에 두고 싶어 하시는 거군요?\""], ["says Volumnia, sympathizing.", "볼룸니아가 맞장구친다."], ["“Yes, how charming now!", "\"그래요, 정말 사랑스럽잖아요!"], ["And how well that delightful old soul Mrs. Rouncewell is looking.", "그리고 그 사랑스러운 노인네 라운스웰 부인은 어쩜 그리 정정한지요."], ["She must be an immense age, and yet she is as active and handsome!", "어마어마한 연세일 텐데도, 그렇게 활기차고 멋지다니!"], ["She is the dearest friend I have, positively!”", "정말이지 내겐 둘도 없이 소중한 벗이에요!\""]], [["Sir Leicester feels it to be right and fitting that the housekeeper of Chesney Wold should be a remarkable person.", "레스터 경은 체스니 월드의 가정부라면 마땅히 비범한 인물이어야 한다고 여긴다."], ["Apart from that, he has a real regard for Mrs. Rouncewell and likes to hear her praised.", "그것과 별개로, 그는 라운스웰 부인을 진심으로 존중하며 그녀를 칭찬하는 말을 듣기 좋아한다."], ["So he says, “You are right, Volumnia,”", "그래서 그는 말한다. \"옳은 말이오, 볼룸니아.\""], ["which Volumnia is extremely glad to hear.", "볼룸니아는 그 말을 듣고 무척 기뻐한다."]], [["“She has no daughter of her own, has she?”", "\"그이에게 딸은 없지요?\""]], [["“Mrs. Rouncewell?", "\"라운스웰 부인 말이오?"], ["No, Volumnia.", "없소, 볼룸니아."], ["She has a son.", "아들이 있지."], ["Indeed, she had two.”", "사실 둘이 있었소.\""]], [["My Lady, whose chronic malady of boredom has been sadly aggravated by Volumnia this evening, glances wearily towards the candlesticks and heaves a noiseless sigh.", "오늘 저녁 볼룸니아 때문에 만성적인 권태가 한심하게 더 도진 마님은, 촛대 쪽으로 지친 눈길을 던지며 소리 없는 한숨을 내쉰다."]], [["“And it is a remarkable example of the confusion into which the present age has fallen; of the obliteration of landmarks, the opening of floodgates, and the uprooting of distinctions,”", "\"라운스웰 부인의 아들이 의회에 들어오라는 권유를 받았다는 이야기를 토킹혼 씨에게서 들었는데, 이건 지금 시대가 빠져든 혼란의 주목할 만한 본보기요."], ["says Sir Leicester with stately gloom, “that I have been informed by Mr. Tulkinghorn that Mrs.", "경계표가 지워지고, 수문이 열리고, 신분의 구별이 뿌리째 뽑혀 나간 본보기란 말이오.\""], ["Rouncewell’s son has been invited to go into Parliament.”", "레스터 경이 위엄 있는 침울함으로 말한다."]], [["Miss Volumnia utters a little sharp scream.", "볼룸니아 양이 작게 날카로운 비명을 지른다."]], [["“Yes, indeed,”", "\"그렇소, 정말이지.\""], ["repeats Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 거듭 말한다."], ["“Into Parliament.”", "\"의회로 말이오.\""]], [["“I never heard of such a thing!", "\"그런 일은 들어본 적도 없어요!"], ["Good gracious, what is the man?”", "세상에, 그 사람은 대체 뭐 하는 사람인데요?\""], ["exclaims Volumnia.", "볼룸니아가 소리친다."]], [["“He is called, I believe—an—ironmaster.”", "\"내 알기로 그자를, 음, 철물 제조업자라고 부르는 모양이오.\""], ["Sir Leicester says it slowly and with gravity and doubt, as not being sure but that he is called a lead-mistress or that the right word may be some other word expressive of some other relationship to some other metal.", "레스터 경은 그것을 천천히, 무겁게, 미심쩍어하며 말한다. 혹시 납 제조업자라고 부르는 게 아닌지, 아니면 다른 어떤 금속과 다른 어떤 관계를 나타내는 다른 어떤 말이 맞는 게 아닌지 확신이 서지 않는다는 투다."]], [["Volumnia utters another little scream.", "볼룸니아가 또 한 번 작게 비명을 지른다."]], [["“He has declined the proposal, if my information from Mr.", "\"토킹혼 씨에게서 들은 내 정보가 정확하다면, 분명 그럴 텐데, 토킹혼 씨야 늘 정확하고 빈틈없으니 말이오, 그자가 그 제안을 거절했다고 하오."], ["Tulkinghorn be correct, as I have no doubt it is.", "그렇다고 해도,\" 레스터 경이 말한다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn being always correct and exact; still that does not,”", "\"그렇다고 해도 이 변칙이 줄어드는 건 아니오."], ["says Sir Leicester, “that does not lessen the anomaly, which is fraught with strange considerations—startling considerations, as it appears to me.”", "그 안에는 기묘한 고려거리가, 내가 보기에 소스라치게 놀라운 고려거리가 가득 차 있단 말이오.\""]], [["Miss Volumnia rising with a look candlestick-wards, Sir Leicester politely performs the grand tour of the drawing-room, brings one, and lights it at my Lady’s shaded lamp.", "볼룸니아 양이 촛대 쪽으로 눈길을 던지며 일어서자, 레스터 경은 정중하게 응접실을 한 바퀴 거창하게 돌아 촛대를 하나 가져와서는 마님의 갓 씌운 등불에 불을 붙인다."]], [["“I must beg you, my Lady,” he says while doing so, “to remain a few moments, for this individual of whom I speak arrived this evening shortly before dinner and requested in a very becoming note”—Sir Leicester, with his habitual regard to truth, dwells upon it—“I am bound to say, in a very becoming and well-expressed note, the favour of a short interview with yourself and MYself on the subject of this young girl. As it appeared that he wished to depart to-night, I replied that we would see him before retiring.”", "\"부탁이오, 마님.\" 그가 불을 붙이며 말한다. \"잠깐만 더 있어 주시오. 내가 말하는 이 작자가 오늘 저녁 식사 직전에 도착해, 아주 격식 있는 쪽지로 청해왔소.\" 진실을 향한 평소의 존중에 따라 레스터 경은 이 대목을 곱씹는다. \"이건 인정해야겠소만, 아주 격식 있고 잘 표현된 쪽지로, 그 어린 처녀의 일을 두고 당신과 나를 잠깐 만나 뵙는 호의를 베풀어달라고 청해왔단 말이오. 오늘 밤 떠나기를 바라는 듯하기에, 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 만나주겠다고 답했소.\""]], [["Miss Volumnia with a third little scream takes flight, wishing her hosts—O Lud!—well rid of the—what is it?—ironmaster!", "볼룸니아 양은 세 번째로 작게 비명을 지르며 줄행랑을 치는데, 부디 주인 부부가, 아이고 맙소사, 그 뭐라더라, 철물 제조업자인가 하는 작자를 무사히 떨쳐내기를 바란다면서다."]], [["The other cousins soon disperse, to the last cousin there.", "다른 친척들도 곧 흩어져, 마지막 한 사람까지 자리를 뜬다."], ["Sir Leicester rings the bell, “Make my compliments to Mr.", "레스터 경이 종을 울린다."], ["Rouncewell, in the housekeeper’s apartments, and say I can receive him now.”", "\"라운스웰 씨가 가정부 거처에 있으니 내 안부를 전하고, 지금 만나주겠다고 일러라.\""]], [["My Lady, who has heard all this with slight attention outwardly, looks towards Mr. Rouncewell as he comes in.", "이 모든 이야기를 겉으로는 건성으로 듣고 있던 마님이, 라운스웰 씨가 들어오자 그쪽을 바라본다."], ["He is a little over fifty perhaps, of a good figure, like his mother, and has a clear voice, a broad forehead from which his dark hair has retired, and a shrewd though open face.", "그는 쉰을 조금 넘긴 듯하고, 어머니를 닮아 풍채가 좋으며, 또랑또랑한 목소리에, 검은 머리가 물러난 넓은 이마를 지녔고, 빈틈없으면서도 솔직한 얼굴이다."], ["He is a responsible-looking gentleman dressed in black, portly enough, but strong and active.", "검은 옷을 입은 믿음직스러운 인상의 신사로, 몸집이 제법 듬직하면서도 다부지고 활기차다."], ["Has a perfectly natural and easy air and is not in the least embarrassed by the great presence into which he comes.", "더없이 자연스럽고 편안한 태도이며, 자기가 들어선 위풍당당한 자리에 조금도 주눅 들지 않는다."]], [["“Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, as I have already apologized for intruding on you, I cannot do better than be very brief.", "\"레스터 경과 데드록 마님, 폐를 끼쳐 죄송하다는 사과를 이미 드린 마당이니, 최대한 짧게 말씀드리는 것이 상책이겠지요."], ["I thank you, Sir Leicester.”", "감사합니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["The head of the Dedlocks has motioned towards a sofa between himself and my Lady.", "데드록 가문의 수장이 자기와 마님 사이의 소파를 손짓으로 가리킨다."], ["Mr. Rouncewell quietly takes his seat there.", "라운스웰 씨가 조용히 거기에 앉는다."]], [["“In these busy times, when so many great undertakings are in progress, people like myself have so many workmen in so many places that we are always on the flight.”", "\"이렇게 바쁜 시절에, 큰 사업이 한꺼번에 그렇게 많이 벌어지다 보니, 저 같은 사람은 곳곳에 일꾼이 많아서 늘 이리저리 날아다니듯 분주합니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester is content enough that the ironmaster should feel that there is no hurry there; there, in that ancient house, rooted in that quiet park, where the ivy and the moss have had time to mature, and the gnarled and warted elms and the umbrageous oaks stand deep in the fern and leaves of a hundred years; and where the sun-dial on the terrace has dumbly recorded for centuries that time which was as much the property of every Dedlock—while he lasted—as the house and lands.", "레스터 경은 철물 제조업자가 이곳에서는 서두를 필요가 없음을 느낀다면 그것으로 흡족하다. 담쟁이와 이끼가 무르익을 만큼 세월이 흐른 이 고요한 정원에 뿌리내린 이 유서 깊은 저택에서는, 옹이지고 혹투성이인 느릅나무와 그늘 짙은 떡갈나무가 백 년 묵은 양치류와 낙엽 속에 깊이 잠겨 서 있고, 테라스의 해시계는 수백 년 동안 묵묵히 시간을 기록해왔으니, 그 시간이란 살아 있는 동안 데드록 가문 사람마다 저택이며 영지만큼이나 자기 소유였던 것이다."], ["Sir Leicester sits down in an easy-chair, opposing his repose and that of Chesney Wold to the restless flights of ironmasters.", "레스터 경은 안락의자에 앉아, 자기와 체스니 월드의 안식을 철물 제조업자들의 쉴 새 없는 비행에 맞세운다."]], [["“Lady Dedlock has been so kind,” proceeds Mr.", "\"데드록 마님께서,\""], ["Rouncewell with a respectful glance and a bow that way, “as to place near her a young beauty of the name of Rosa.", "라운스웰 씨가 그쪽으로 공손한 눈길을 던지며 고개를 숙이고 말을 잇는다. \"친절하게도 로자라는 이름의 어린 미인을 곁에 두셨지요."], ["Now, my son has fallen in love with Rosa and has asked my consent to his proposing marriage to her and to their becoming engaged if she will take him—which I suppose she will.", "그런데 제 아들이 로자에게 반해서, 그 아이에게 청혼하고 만일 그 아이가 받아준다면, 받아주리라 봅니다만, 약혼하고 싶다며 제 허락을 구했습니다."], ["I have never seen Rosa until to-day, but I have some confidence in my son’s good sense—even in love.", "저는 오늘에야 로자를 처음 봤습니다만, 제 아들의 분별력은, 사랑에 빠진 와중이라 해도 어느 정도 믿습니다."], ["I find her what he represents her, to the best of my judgment; and my mother speaks of her with great commendation.”", "제 판단으로는 그 아이가 아들이 말한 그대로이고, 제 어머니도 그 아이를 매우 칭찬하시더군요.\""]], [["“She in all respects deserves it,”", "\"그 아이는 모든 면에서 칭찬받을 만하지요.\""], ["says my Lady.", "마님이 말한다."]], [["“I am happy, Lady Dedlock, that you say so, and I need not comment on the value to me of your kind opinion of her.”", "\"그리 말씀해주시니 기쁩니다, 데드록 마님. 그 아이에 대한 마님의 호의 어린 평가가 제게 얼마나 값진지는 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요.\""]], [["“That,”", "\"그건,\" 레스터 경이 이루 말할 수 없이 위엄을 차리며 한마디 한다."], ["observes Sir Leicester with unspeakable grandeur, for he thinks the ironmaster a little too glib, “must be quite unnecessary.”", "그는 철물 제조업자가 입심이 좀 지나치다고 여기는 터다. \"전혀 필요 없는 말일 것이오.\""]], [["“Quite unnecessary, Sir Leicester.", "\"전혀 필요 없는 말이지요, 레스터 경."], ["Now, my son is a very young man, and Rosa is a very young woman.", "그런데 제 아들은 아주 젊은 청년이고, 로자도 아주 젊은 처녀입니다."], ["As I made my way, so my son must make his; and his being married at present is out of the question.", "제가 제 길을 헤쳐 왔듯, 제 아들도 제 길을 헤쳐 나가야 하니, 지금 결혼시키는 것은 어림없는 일입니다."], ["But supposing I gave my consent to his engaging himself to this pretty girl, if this pretty girl will engage herself to him, I think it a piece of candour to say at once—I am sure, Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, you will understand and excuse me—I should make it a condition that she did not remain at Chesney Wold.", "다만 이 예쁜 처녀가 제 아들과 약혼하겠다면, 제 아들이 이 예쁜 처녀와 약혼하도록 허락한다고 칠 때, 이건 솔직히 당장 말씀드리는 게 도리겠지요. 레스터 경과 데드록 마님, 분명 이해하고 너그러이 봐주시리라 믿습니다만, 저는 그 아이가 체스니 월드에 남지 않는다는 것을 조건으로 내걸겠습니다."], ["Therefore, before communicating further with my son, I take the liberty of saying that if her removal would be in any way inconvenient or objectionable, I will hold the matter over with him for any reasonable time and leave it precisely where it is.”", "그러니 제 아들과 더 이야기를 나누기 전에, 외람되게 말씀드리자면, 만일 그 아이를 데려가는 일이 어떤 식으로든 불편하거나 마뜩잖으시다면, 합당한 기간만큼 아들과 이 문제를 보류하고 지금 이대로 그냥 놔두겠습니다.\""]], [["Not remain at Chesney Wold!", "체스니 월드에 남지 않는다니!"], ["Make it a condition!", "그것을 조건으로 걸다니!"], ["All Sir Leicester’s old misgivings relative to Wat Tyler and the people in the iron districts who do nothing but turn out by torchlight come in a shower upon his head, the fine grey hair of which, as well as of his whiskers, actually stirs with indignation.", "와트 타일러며, 횃불을 밝히고 죄다 들고일어나는 짓이나 일삼는 철공 지대 사람들에 대해 레스터 경이 품어온 해묵은 불안이 그의 머리 위로 우수수 쏟아져, 그 고운 잿빛 머리칼은 물론 구레나룻까지 정말로 분노에 곤두선다."]], [["“Am I to understand, sir,”", "\"내가 이렇게 이해해도 되겠소, 선생,\" 레스터 경이 말한다."], ["says Sir Leicester, “and is my Lady to understand”—he brings her in thus specially, first as a point of gallantry, and next as a point of prudence, having great reliance on her sense—“am I to understand, Mr.", "\"그리고 마님도 이렇게 이해해도 되겠소,\" 이렇게 마님을 특별히 끌어들이는데, 첫째로는 정중함의 표시이고, 둘째로는 그녀의 분별력을 크게 믿는 터라 신중함의 표시다."], ["Rouncewell, and is my Lady to understand, sir, that you consider this young woman too good for Chesney Wold or likely to be injured by remaining here?”", "\"내가 이렇게 이해해도 되겠소, 라운스웰 씨, 그리고 마님도, 선생, 그대가 이 어린 처녀를 체스니 월드에 두기엔 너무 아깝다고 여기거나, 이곳에 남으면 그 아이가 해를 입으리라고 여긴다는 뜻이오?\""]], [["“Certainly not, Sir Leicester,”", "\"결코 그렇지 않습니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear it.”", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘구려.\""], ["Sir Leicester very lofty indeed.", "레스터 경, 더없이 거만하다."]], [["“Pray, Mr. Rouncewell,”", "\"라운스웰 씨,\""], ["says my Lady, warning Sir Leicester off with the slightest gesture of her pretty hand, as if he were a fly, “explain to me what you mean.”", "마님이 레스터 경을 파리라도 되는 양 예쁜 손으로 살짝 손짓해 물러나게 하며 말한다. \"무슨 뜻인지 내게 설명해주세요.\""]], [["“Willingly, Lady Dedlock.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 데드록 마님."], ["There is nothing I could desire more.”", "저로서는 더 바랄 게 없습니다.\""]], [["Addressing her composed face, whose intelligence, however, is too quick and active to be concealed by any studied impassiveness, however habitual, to the strong Saxon face of the visitor, a picture of resolution and perseverance, my Lady listens with attention, occasionally slightly bending her head.", "침착한 마님의 얼굴, 하지만 그 안의 총기는 아무리 몸에 밴 무표정으로도 감출 수 없을 만큼 빠르고 활달한 그 얼굴을, 결의와 끈기의 표상인 손님의 강인한 색슨족 얼굴을 향하고서, 마님은 이따금 고개를 살짝 숙이며 주의 깊게 듣는다."]], [["“I am the son of your housekeeper, Lady Dedlock, and passed my childhood about this house. My mother has lived here half a century and will die here I have no doubt. She is one of those examples—perhaps as good a one as there is—of love, and attachment, and fidelity in such a nation, which England may well be proud of, but of which no order can appropriate the whole pride or the whole merit, because such an instance bespeaks high worth on two sides—on the great side assuredly, on the small one no less assuredly.”", "\"저는 마님의 가정부의 아들이고, 이 집 언저리에서 어린 시절을 보냈습니다, 데드록 마님. 제 어머니는 이곳에서 반세기를 사셨고, 틀림없이 이곳에서 돌아가시겠지요. 어머니는 그런 본보기, 아마 둘도 없는 본보기 가운데 하나입니다. 영국이 자랑스러워해도 좋을 사랑과 헌신과 충성의 본보기 말이지요. 하지만 어느 계급도 그 자랑이나 그 공로를 통째로 차지할 수는 없습니다. 그런 본보기란 양편 모두의 높은 인품을 증언하니까요. 큰 쪽의 인품을 분명히 증언하지만, 작은 쪽의 인품도 그에 못지않게 분명히 증언하지요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester snorts a little to hear the law laid down in this way, but in his honour and his love of truth, he freely, though silently, admits the justice of the ironmaster’s proposition.", "레스터 경은 이런 식으로 펼쳐지는 논리에 콧방귀를 살짝 뀌지만, 명예심과 진실에 대한 사랑에서, 비록 말은 없으나, 철물 제조업자의 명제가 옳음을 선선히 인정한다."]], [["“Pardon me for saying what is so obvious, but I wouldn’t have it hastily supposed,”", "\"이렇게 뻔한 말씀을 드려 송구합니다만, 제가 이곳에서 어머니가 처한 처지를 부끄러워한다거나, 체스니 월드와 가문에 마땅히 표해야 할 존경이 모자란다고,\""], ["with the least turn of his eyes towards Sir Leicester, “that I am ashamed of my mother’s position here, or wanting in all just respect for Chesney Wold and the family.", "레스터 경 쪽으로 눈길을 아주 살짝 돌리며, \"성급히 넘겨짚으시는 건 원치 않습니다."], ["I certainly may have desired—I certainly have desired, Lady Dedlock—that my mother should retire after so many years and end her days with me.", "저는 분명 바랐을 겁니다, 정말이지 바랐습니다, 데드록 마님, 그 오랜 세월이 지났으니 제 어머니가 물러나 저와 함께 여생을 보내시기를요."], ["But as I have found that to sever this strong bond would be to break her heart, I have long abandoned that idea.”", "하지만 이 굳건한 인연을 끊는 것이 곧 어머니의 가슴을 무너뜨리는 일임을 깨닫고는, 진작에 그 생각을 접었습니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester very magnificent again at the notion of Mrs. Rouncewell being spirited off from her natural home to end her days with an ironmaster.", "라운스웰 부인이 타고난 보금자리에서 채여 나가 철물 제조업자와 여생을 보낸다는 발상에, 레스터 경은 다시금 더없이 위엄을 차린다."]], [["“I have been,”", "\"저는,\""], ["proceeds the visitor in a modest, clear way, “an apprentice and a workman.", "손님이 겸손하고 또렷한 어조로 말을 잇는다. \"도제였고 일꾼이었습니다."], ["I have lived on workman’s wages, years and years, and beyond a certain point have had to educate myself.", "여러 해, 여러 해 동안 일꾼의 품삯으로 살았고, 어느 선까지는 독학으로 저를 가르쳐야 했지요."], ["My wife was a foreman’s daughter, and plainly brought up.", "제 아내는 작업반장의 딸로, 수수하게 자랐습니다."], ["We have three daughters besides this son of whom I have spoken, and being fortunately able to give them greater advantages than we have had ourselves, we have educated them well, very well.", "방금 말씀드린 아들 말고도 우리에겐 딸이 셋 있는데, 다행히 우리가 누리지 못한 더 큰 혜택을 그 아이들에게 줄 수 있었던 터라, 잘, 아주 잘 가르쳤습니다."], ["It has been one of our great cares and pleasures to make them worthy of any station.”", "그 아이들을 어떤 지위에도 어울릴 만한 사람으로 키우는 것이 우리의 크나큰 근심거리이자 기쁨의 하나였지요.\""]], [["A little boastfulness in his fatherly tone here, as if he added in his heart, “even of the Chesney Wold station.”", "여기 그 아버지다운 어조에는 자랑기가 좀 묻어나니, 마치 마음속으로 \"체스니 월드의 지위에마저 어울리도록\"이라고 덧붙이는 것만 같다."], ["Not a little more magnificence, therefore, on the part of Sir Leicester.", "그러니 레스터 경 쪽에서는 위엄이 한층 더할밖에."]], [["“All this is so frequent, Lady Dedlock, where I live, and among the class to which I belong, that what would be generally called unequal marriages are not of such rare occurrence with us as elsewhere.", "\"이런 일은 제가 사는 곳에서, 또 제가 속한 계급 안에서 워낙 흔해서, 흔히들 격이 안 맞는 결혼이라 부를 만한 일이 우리에게는 다른 데만큼 드물지 않습니다, 데드록 마님. 아들이 가끔 아버지에게 털어놓지요, 이를테면 공장의 어떤 처녀에게 반했다고요."], ["A son will sometimes make it known to his father that he has fallen in love, say, with a young woman in the factory.", "한때 자기도 공장에서 일했던 아버지는 처음엔 십중팔구 좀 실망할 겁니다. 아들에게 다른 뜻을 품고 있었을 수도 있으니까요."], ["The father, who once worked in a factory himself, will be a little disappointed at first very possibly.", "그래도 십중팔구는, 그 처녀가 흠 없는 품성이라는 것을 확인하고 나면, 아들에게 이렇게 말하겠지요."], ["It may be that he had other views for his son.", "'네가 정말 진심인지 확실히 해둬야겠다."], ["However, the chances are that having ascertained the young woman to be of unblemished character, he will say to his son, ‘I must be quite sure you are in earnest here.", "이건 너희 둘 다에게 중대한 일이니까. 그러니 내가 이 아가씨를 두 해 동안 가르치게 하마.'"], ["This is a serious matter for both of you.", "아니면 이렇게도 말하겠지요."], ["Therefore I shall have this girl educated for two years,’ or it may be, ‘I shall place this girl at the same school with your sisters for such a time, during which you will give me your word and honour to see her only so often.", "'내가 이 아가씨를 네 누이들과 같은 학교에 얼마간 보낼 테니, 그동안 너는 명예를 걸고 가끔씩만 그 아이를 만나겠다고 내게 약속해라. 그 기간이 끝났을 때, 그 아이가 혜택을 누려 너와 어지간히 대등해지고, 너희 둘의 마음이 그대로라면, 내가 너희를 행복하게 해주는 데 내 몫을 다하마.'"], ["If at the expiration of that time, when she has so far profited by her advantages as that you may be upon a fair equality, you are both in the same mind, I will do my part to make you happy.’ I know of several cases such as I describe, my Lady, and I think they indicate to me my own course now.”", "제가 말씀드린 것 같은 사례를 여럿 알고 있습니다, 마님. 그리고 그것이 지금 제가 가야 할 길을 일러주는 듯합니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester’s magnificence explodes.", "레스터 경의 위엄이 터져 나온다."], ["Calmly, but terribly.", "차분하게, 그러나 무시무시하게."]], [["“Mr. Rouncewell,”", "\"라운스웰 씨,\""], ["says Sir Leicester with his right hand in the breast of his blue coat, the attitude of state in which he is painted in the gallery, “do you draw a parallel between Chesney Wold and a—”", "레스터 경이 푸른 외투 가슴팍에 오른손을 찔러 넣은 채 말한다. 회랑에 걸린 초상화 속 그 위풍당당한 자세 그대로다."], ["Here he resists a disposition to choke, “a factory?”", "\"그대는 체스니 월드와 어떤, \" 여기서 그는 목이 멜 것 같은 충동을 억누르며, \"공장을 나란히 견준단 말이오?\""]], [["“I need not reply, Sir Leicester, that the two places are very different; but for the purposes of this case, I think a parallel may be justly drawn between them.”", "\"두 곳이 매우 다르다는 것은 굳이 답할 필요도 없겠지요, 레스터 경. 그러나 이번 경우에 한해서는, 두 곳을 나란히 견주는 것이 온당하다고 봅니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester directs his majestic glance down one side of the long drawing-room and up the other before he can believe that he is awake.", "레스터 경은 자기가 정녕 깨어 있는 것인지 믿어지지 않아, 위엄 있는 눈길을 기다란 응접실의 한쪽 끝으로 내리뜨렸다가 다른 쪽 끝으로 올려뜬다."]], [["“Are you aware, sir, that this young woman whom my Lady—my Lady—has placed near her person was brought up at the village school outside the gates?”", "\"선생, 마님이, 마님께서 곁에 두신 이 어린 처녀가 성문 밖 마을 학교에서 자랐다는 것을 알고 있소?\""]], [["“Sir Leicester, I am quite aware of it.", "\"레스터 경, 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["A very good school it is, and handsomely supported by this family.”", "아주 훌륭한 학교이고, 이 가문이 후하게 지원하고 있지요.\""]], [["“Then, Mr. Rouncewell,”", "\"그렇다면, 라운스웰 씨,\""], ["returns Sir Leicester, “the application of what you have said is, to me, incomprehensible.”", "레스터 경이 받아친다. \"그대가 한 말을 어디에 빗대는 건지 나로서는 도무지 모르겠소.\""]], [["“Will it be more comprehensible, Sir Leicester, if I say,”", "\"이렇게 말씀드리면 좀 더 이해가 되시겠습니까, 레스터 경,\""], ["the ironmaster is reddening a little, “that I do not regard the village school as teaching everything desirable to be known by my son’s wife?”", "철물 제조업자가 얼굴을 살짝 붉히며 말한다. \"저는 마을 학교가 제 며느릿감이 마땅히 알아야 할 모든 것을 가르친다고 보지는 않습니다.\""]], [["From the village school of Chesney Wold, intact as it is this minute, to the whole framework of society; from the whole framework of society, to the aforesaid framework receiving tremendous cracks in consequence of people (iron-masters, lead-mistresses, and what not) not minding their catechism, and getting out of the station unto which they are called—necessarily and for ever, according to Sir Leicester’s rapid logic, the first station in which they happen to find themselves; and from that, to their educating other people out of THEIR stations, and so obliterating the landmarks, and opening the floodgates, and all the rest of it; this is the swift progress of the Dedlock mind.", "체스니 월드의 마을 학교에서, 이 순간에도 멀쩡한 그 학교에서, 사회 전체의 골격으로. 사회 전체의 골격에서, 앞서 말한 그 골격이 사람들이(철물 제조업자며, 납 제조업자며, 뭐며 하는 자들이) 교리문답은 거들떠보지도 않고, 자기가 부름받은 신분을 벗어나려는 탓에 무시무시하게 쩍쩍 금이 가는 데로. 그 신분이란, 레스터 경의 잽싼 논리에 따르면, 반드시 그리고 영원히, 그들이 어쩌다 처음 몸담은 바로 그 신분이다. 그리고 거기서 다시, 그들이 다른 사람들마저 그 사람들 본래의 신분에서 벗어나도록 가르쳐, 그리하여 경계표를 지우고, 수문을 열어젖히고, 운운하는 데로. 이것이 데드록 정신의 잽싼 진행이다."]], [["“My Lady, I beg your pardon.", "\"마님, 죄송합니다."], ["Permit me, for one moment!”", "잠깐만 양해해주시오!\""], ["She has given a faint indication of intending to speak.", "마님이 말하려는 기색을 희미하게 내비쳤던 것이다."], ["“Mr. Rouncewell, our views of duty, and our views of station, and our views of education, and our views of—in short, ALL our views—are so diametrically opposed, that to prolong this discussion must be repellent to your feelings and repellent to my own.", "\"라운스웰 씨, 의무에 대한 우리의 견해도, 신분에 대한 우리의 견해도, 교육에 대한 우리의 견해도, 그리고 한마디로 우리의 모든 견해가 너무도 정반대라, 이 논의를 더 끄는 것은 그대의 감정에도 거슬리고 나 자신의 감정에도 거슬릴 것이오."], ["This young woman is honoured with my Lady’s notice and favour.", "이 어린 처녀는 마님의 관심과 총애를 받는 영예를 누리고 있소."], ["If she wishes to withdraw herself from that notice and favour or if she chooses to place herself under the influence of any one who may in his peculiar opinions—you will allow me to say, in his peculiar opinions, though I readily admit that he is not accountable for them to me—who may, in his peculiar opinions, withdraw her from that notice and favour, she is at any time at liberty to do so.", "만일 그 아이가 그 관심과 총애에서 스스로 물러나기를 바라거나, 자기 나름의 유별난 견해로, 유별난 견해라고 말하는 걸 양해하시오, 비록 그가 그 견해를 내게 해명할 책임이 없다는 건 선뜻 인정하오만, 아무튼 자기 나름의 유별난 견해로 그 아이를 그 관심과 총애에서 떼어내려는 누군가의 영향 아래 스스로 들어가기로 한다면, 언제든 그렇게 할 자유가 있소."], ["We are obliged to you for the plainness with which you have spoken.", "그대가 솔직하게 말해준 데 대해서는 고맙게 생각하오."], ["It will have no effect of itself, one way or other, on the young woman’s position here.", "그것만으로는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 이곳에서의 그 처녀의 처지에 아무런 영향도 미치지 못할 것이오."], ["Beyond this, we can make no terms; and here we beg—if you will be so good—to leave the subject.”", "그 이상으로 우리가 어떤 조건도 맺을 수는 없소. 그러니 부디 그대가 너그러이, 이 문제는 여기서 접어주기를 청하오.\""]], [["The visitor pauses a moment to give my Lady an opportunity, but she says nothing.", "손님은 마님에게 기회를 주려고 잠시 멈추지만, 그녀는 아무 말이 없다. 그러자 그는 일어서서 대답한다."], ["He then rises and replies, “Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock, allow me to thank you for your attention and only to observe that I shall very seriously recommend my son to conquer his present inclinations.", "\"레스터 경과 데드록 마님, 경청해주셔서 감사드립니다. 그리고 한 말씀만 드리자면, 제 아들에게 지금의 마음을 누르라고 아주 진지하게 권하겠습니다."], ["Good night!”", "안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["“Mr. Rouncewell,”", "\"라운스웰 씨,\""], ["says Sir Leicester with all the nature of a gentleman shining in him, “it is late, and the roads are dark.", "레스터 경이 신사의 천성을 온전히 빛내며 말한다. \"시각도 늦었고 길도 어둡소."], ["I hope your time is not so precious but that you will allow my Lady and myself to offer you the hospitality of Chesney Wold, for to-night at least.”", "그대의 시간이 그토록 귀하지만은 않아, 적어도 오늘 밤만은 마님과 내가 체스니 월드의 후의를 베풀도록 허락해주기를 바라오.\""]], [["“I hope so,”", "\"그랬으면 합니다.\""], ["adds my Lady.", "마님이 거든다."]], [["“I am much obliged to you, but I have to travel all night in order to reach a distant part of the country punctually at an appointed time in the morning.”", "\"매우 감사합니다만, 아침 정해진 시각에 맞춰 먼 고장에 닿으려면 밤새 길을 가야 합니다.\""]], [["Therewith the ironmaster takes his departure, Sir Leicester ringing the bell and my Lady rising as he leaves the room.", "그 말과 함께 철물 제조업자는 떠나고, 레스터 경은 종을 울리며, 마님은 그가 방을 나설 때 일어선다."]], [["When my Lady goes to her boudoir, she sits down thoughtfully by the fire, and inattentive to the Ghost’s Walk, looks at Rosa, writing in an inner room.", "마님은 자기 내실로 가서 난롯가에 깊은 생각에 잠겨 앉아, 유령의 길에는 마음을 두지 않은 채, 안쪽 방에서 글을 쓰고 있는 로자를 바라본다."], ["Presently my Lady calls her.", "이윽고 마님이 그 아이를 부른다."]], [["“Come to me, child.", "\"이리 오너라, 얘야."], ["Tell me the truth.", "솔직히 말해보렴."], ["Are you in love?”", "너 사랑에 빠졌니?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["My Lady!”", "마님!\""]], [["My Lady, looking at the downcast and blushing face, says smiling, “Who is it?", "마님은 수줍게 고개 숙여 발그레해진 얼굴을 보며 미소 띠고 말한다."], ["Is it Mrs.", "\"그게 누구냐?"], ["Rouncewell’s grandson?”", "라운스웰 부인의 손자니?\""]], [["“Yes, if you please, my Lady.", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 마님."], ["But I don’t know that I am in love with him—yet.”", "하지만 제가 그분을 사랑하는지는, 아직 잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Yet, you silly little thing!", "\"아직이라니, 이 어리석은 것아!"], ["Do you know that he loves YOU, yet?”", "그럼 그가 너를 사랑한다는 건 아직도 모르니?\""]], [["“I think he likes me a little, my Lady.”", "\"저를 조금 좋아하시는 것 같아요, 마님.\""], ["And Rosa bursts into tears.", "그러고는 로자가 울음을 터뜨린다."]], [["Is this Lady Dedlock standing beside the village beauty, smoothing her dark hair with that motherly touch, and watching her with eyes so full of musing interest?", "마을의 미인 곁에 서서, 어머니 같은 손길로 그 아이의 검은 머리를 쓸어내리며, 사색에 잠긴 관심이 가득 어린 눈으로 그 아이를 지켜보는 이 사람이 정녕 데드록 마님인가?"], ["Aye, indeed it is!", "그렇다, 정말이지 그렇다!"]], [["“Listen to me, child.", "\"내 말을 듣거라, 얘야."], ["You are young and true, and I believe you are attached to me.”", "너는 젊고 진실하지. 게다가 너는 나를 따른다고 나는 믿는다.\""]], [["“Indeed I am, my Lady.", "\"정말 그렇습니다, 마님."], ["Indeed there is nothing in the world I wouldn’t do to show how much.”", "정말이지 제가 얼마나 마님을 따르는지 보여드릴 수만 있다면 이 세상에 못 할 일이 없어요.\""]], [["“And I don’t think you would wish to leave me just yet, Rosa, even for a lover?”", "\"그럼 너는, 로자, 사랑하는 사람을 위해서라도, 아직은 나를 떠나고 싶지 않겠지?\""]], [["“No, my Lady! Oh, no!”", "\"안 떠나요, 마님!"], ["Rosa looks up for the first time, quite frightened at the thought.", "아, 안 떠나요!\" 로자는 그 생각에 잔뜩 놀라 처음으로 고개를 든다."]], [["“Confide in me, my child.", "\"내게 털어놓으렴, 얘야."], ["Don’t fear me.", "나를 두려워 말고."], ["I wish you to be happy, and will make you so—if I can make anybody happy on this earth.”", "나는 네가 행복하기를 바라고, 너를 행복하게 해주마. 내가 이 세상에서 누군가를 행복하게 해줄 수 있다면 말이다.\""]], [["Rosa, with fresh tears, kneels at her feet and kisses her hand.", "로자는 새로 눈물을 쏟으며 그녀의 발치에 무릎 꿇고 손에 입을 맞춘다."], ["My Lady takes the hand with which she has caught it, and standing with her eyes fixed on the fire, puts it about and about between her own two hands, and gradually lets it fall.", "마님은 그 아이가 붙든 손을 거두어, 난롯불에 눈길을 박은 채 서서, 그것을 두 손 사이에 이리저리 넣고 굴리다가, 차츰 떨어뜨린다."], ["Seeing her so absorbed, Rosa softly withdraws; but still my Lady’s eyes are on the fire.", "마님이 그토록 골똘히 잠긴 것을 보고 로자는 살며시 물러나지만, 마님의 눈길은 여전히 난롯불에 머문다."]], [["In search of what?", "무엇을 찾는 걸까?"], ["Of any hand that is no more, of any hand that never was, of any touch that might have magically changed her life?", "이제는 없는 어떤 손을, 애당초 없었던 어떤 손을, 마법처럼 그녀의 삶을 바꿔놓았을지도 모를 어떤 손길을?"], ["Or does she listen to the Ghost’s Walk and think what step does it most resemble?", "아니면 유령의 길에 귀 기울이며 그것이 어떤 발소리를 가장 닮았는지 헤아리는 걸까? 사내의 발소리?"], ["A man’s? A woman’s?", "여인의 발소리?"], ["The pattering of a little child’s feet, ever coming on—on—on?", "자꾸만 다가오고 또 다가오고 또 다가오는 어린아이의 자그마한 발자국 소리?"], ["Some melancholy influence is upon her, or why should so proud a lady close the doors and sit alone upon the hearth so desolate?", "어떤 우울한 기운이 그녀를 짓누르는 게 아니라면, 어찌하여 이토록 도도한 마님이 문을 닫아걸고 그토록 쓸쓸하게 홀로 난롯가에 앉아 있는 걸까?"]], [["Volumnia is away next day, and all the cousins are scattered before dinner.", "이튿날 볼룸니아는 떠나고, 친척들도 모두 저녁 식사 전에 흩어진다."], ["Not a cousin of the batch but is amazed to hear from Sir Leicester at breakfast-time of the obliteration of landmarks, and opening of floodgates, and cracking of the framework of society, manifested through Mrs.", "라운스웰 부인의 아들을 통해 드러난 경계표의 지워짐과, 수문의 열림과, 사회 골격의 쩍쩍 갈라짐에 대해, 아침 식사 자리에서 레스터 경에게 듣고 기겁하지 않는 친척이 그 무리 가운데 하나도 없다."], ["Rouncewell’s son. Not a cousin of the batch but is really indignant, and connects it with the feebleness of William Buffy when in office, and really does feel deprived of a stake in the country—or the pension list—or something—by fraud and wrong.", "정말로 분개하지 않는 친척, 그것을 윌리엄 버피의 재임 시절 나약함과 결부 짓지 않는 친척, 사기와 부정 탓에 자기가 나라의, 아니 연금 명단의, 아니 무언가의 한몫을 빼앗긴 기분을 정말로 느끼지 않는 친척이 그 무리 가운데 하나도 없다."], ["As to Volumnia, she is handed down the great staircase by Sir Leicester, as eloquent upon the theme as if there were a general rising in the north of England to obtain her rouge-pot and pearl necklace.", "볼룸니아로 말하자면, 레스터 경이 큰 층계를 따라 그녀를 손수 부축해 내려가는데, 마치 그녀의 연지 단지와 진주 목걸이를 차지하려고 잉글랜드 북부에서 대대적인 봉기라도 일어난 양 그 주제를 두고 열변을 토한다. 그리하여 하녀들과 시종들이 우당탕거리는 가운데, 제 한 몸 건사하기가 아무리 빠듯해도 하녀와 시종만은 반드시 두어야 하는 것이 그들 친척 됨의 한 가지 부속물인지라, 친척들은 하늘의 사방 바람을 향해 뿔뿔이 흩어진다."], ["And thus, with a clatter of maids and valets—for it is one appurtenance of their cousinship that however difficult they may find it to keep themselves, they MUST keep maids and valets—the cousins disperse to the four winds of heaven; and the one wintry wind that blows to-day shakes a shower from the trees near the deserted house, as if all the cousins had been changed into leaves. The Young Man", "그리고 오늘 부는 겨울바람 한 줄기가 인적 끊긴 저택 근처의 나무들에서 우수수 잎을 떨구니, 마치 친척들이 죄다 나뭇잎으로 변해버린 것만 같다."]], [["Chesney Wold is shut up, carpets are rolled into great scrolls in corners of comfortless rooms, bright damask does penance in brown holland, carving and gilding puts on mortification, and the Dedlock ancestors retire from the light of day again.", "체스니 월드는 굳게 닫혔다. 양탄자는 살풍경한 방구석에 둘둘 말려 거대한 두루마리가 되었고, 화사한 다마스크 천은 칙칙한 갈색 마포를 뒤집어쓴 채 속죄하고, 조각과 금박은 고행하듯 빛을 죽였으며, 데드록 가문의 조상들은 다시 한번 햇빛에서 물러난다."], ["Around and around the house the leaves fall thick, but never fast, for they come circling down with a dead lightness that is sombre and slow.", "집을 빙빙 에워싸며 나뭇잎이 두툼하게 떨어지는데, 결코 빠르지는 않다. 음울하고 느릿하게, 죽은 듯한 가벼움으로 빙글빙글 맴돌아 내려오기 때문이다."], ["Let the gardener sweep and sweep the turf as he will, and press the leaves into full barrows, and wheel them off, still they lie ankle-deep.", "정원사가 아무리 잔디를 쓸고 또 쓸어 나뭇잎을 손수레 가득 눌러 담아 실어 날라도, 나뭇잎은 여전히 발목까지 쌓여 있다."], ["Howls the shrill wind round Chesney Wold; the sharp rain beats, the windows rattle, and the chimneys growl.", "날카로운 바람이 체스니 월드를 휘돌며 울부짖고, 매서운 빗줄기가 들이치고, 창문은 덜컹대고, 굴뚝은 으르렁댄다."], ["Mists hide in the avenues, veil the points of view, and move in funeral-wise across the rising grounds.", "안개는 가로수 길에 몸을 숨기고, 전망을 가리며, 장례 행렬처럼 비탈진 땅을 가로질러 흐른다."], ["On all the house there is a cold, blank smell like the smell of a little church, though something dryer, suggesting that the dead and buried Dedlocks walk there in the long nights and leave the flavour of their graves behind them.", "집 전체에 작은 교회에서 나는 냄새 같은 차갑고 공허한 냄새가, 다만 그보다 조금 더 메마른 냄새가 감돈다. 마치 죽어 묻힌 데드록 조상들이 긴긴밤마다 그곳을 거닐며 자기 무덤의 향취를 남겨두고 가는 듯하다."]], [["But the house in town, which is rarely in the same mind as Chesney Wold at the same time, seldom rejoicing when it rejoices or mourning when it mourns, excepting when a Dedlock dies—the house in town shines out awakened.", "그러나 도시의 저택은, 좀처럼 체스니 월드와 같은 시기에 같은 기분이 되는 법이 없어, 한쪽이 기뻐할 때 함께 기뻐하거나 한쪽이 슬퍼할 때 함께 슬퍼하는 일이 드물고, 다만 데드록 가문 사람이 죽었을 때만 예외인데, 그 도시의 저택은 잠에서 깨어나 환히 빛난다."], ["As warm and bright as so much state may be, as delicately redolent of pleasant scents that bear no trace of winter as hothouse flowers can make it, soft and hushed so that the ticking of the clocks and the crisp burning of the fires alone disturb the stillness in the rooms, it seems to wrap those chilled bones of Sir Leicester’s in rainbow-coloured wool.", "위풍당당함이 자아낼 수 있는 한껏 따스하고 환하게, 온실 꽃이 빚어낼 수 있는 한껏 겨울 기색이라곤 한 점 없는 향긋한 내음을 섬세하게 풍기며, 시계의 똑딱임과 벽난로 불의 바삭한 타는 소리만이 방 안의 고요를 깨뜨릴 만큼 부드럽고 잔잔하게, 그 저택은 레스터 경의 그 시린 뼈마디를 무지갯빛 양모로 감싸주는 듯하다."], ["And Sir Leicester is glad to repose in dignified contentment before the great fire in the library, condescendingly perusing the backs of his books or honouring the fine arts with a glance of approbation.", "레스터 경은 서재의 커다란 난롯불 앞에서 위엄 있는 만족감에 잠겨 쉬는 것을 흐뭇해하며, 황송하다는 듯 책등을 훑어보거나 미술품에 흡족한 눈길을 보내 영예를 베푼다."], ["For he has his pictures, ancient and modern.", "그에게는 고금의 그림들이 있으니 말이다."], ["Some of the Fancy Ball School in which art occasionally condescends to become a master, which would be best catalogued like the miscellaneous articles in a sale.", "더러는 가장무도회파에 속하는 것들로, 미술이 이따금 황송하게도 거장 노릇을 해주는 부류인데, 이런 것들은 경매 잡화 목록처럼 정리하는 편이 제일 어울린다."], ["As “Three high-backed chairs, a table and cover, long-necked bottle (containing wine), one flask, one Spanish female’s costume, three-quarter face portrait of Miss Jogg the model, and a suit of armour containing Don Quixote.”", "이를테면 \"등받이 높은 의자 셋, 탁자 하나와 덮개 하나, 목 긴 병 하나(포도주 들었음), 플라스크 하나, 스페인 여인 의상 하나, 모델 조그 양의 사분의 삼 측면 초상, 그리고 돈키호테가 든 갑옷 한 벌.\""], ["Or “One stone terrace (cracked), one gondola in distance, one Venetian senator’s dress complete, richly embroidered white satin costume with profile portrait of Miss Jogg the model, one Scimitar superbly mounted in gold with jewelled handle, elaborate Moorish dress (very rare), and Othello.”", "또는 \"돌 테라스 하나(금 갔음), 멀리 보이는 곤돌라 하나, 베네치아 원로원 의원 의상 한 벌 완비, 모델 조그 양의 옆얼굴 초상이 곁든 화려한 자수 흰 새틴 의상 하나, 보석 박은 손잡이에 금으로 호화롭게 장식한 신월도 하나, 정교한 무어인 의상(아주 희귀함) 하나, 그리고 오셀로.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn comes and goes pretty often, there being estate business to do, leases to be renewed, and so on.", "토킹혼 씨는 영지 업무라든가 임대차 계약 갱신 따위가 있어 꽤 자주 드나든다."], ["He sees my Lady pretty often, too; and he and she are as composed, and as indifferent, and take as little heed of one another, as ever.", "그는 마님도 꽤 자주 본다. 그리고 그와 그녀는 여전히 차분하고, 여전히 무심하며, 여전히 서로에게 거의 마음을 쓰지 않는다."], ["Yet it may be that my Lady fears this Mr. Tulkinghorn and that he knows it.", "그러나 어쩌면 마님은 이 토킹혼 씨를 두려워하고, 그는 그것을 알고 있는지도 모른다."], ["It may be that he pursues her doggedly and steadily, with no touch of compunction, remorse, or pity.", "어쩌면 그는 가책도, 후회도, 연민도 한 점 없이 끈질기고 꾸준하게 그녀를 뒤쫓는지도 모른다."], ["It may be that her beauty and all the state and brilliancy surrounding her only gives him the greater zest for what he is set upon and makes him the more inflexible in it.", "어쩌면 그녀의 아름다움과 그녀를 둘러싼 온갖 위풍과 광채가 도리어 그가 작정한 일에 더 큰 흥취를 돋우고, 그 일에 한결 더 완강해지게 만드는지도 모른다."], ["Whether he be cold and cruel, whether immovable in what he has made his duty, whether absorbed in love of power, whether determined to have nothing hidden from him in ground where he has burrowed among secrets all his life, whether he in his heart despises the splendour of which he is a distant beam, whether he is always treasuring up slights and offences in the affability of his gorgeous clients—whether he be any of this, or all of this, it may be that my Lady had better have five thousand pairs of fashionable eyes upon her, in distrustful vigilance, than the two eyes of this rusty lawyer with his wisp of neckcloth and his dull black breeches tied with ribbons at the knees.", "그가 차갑고 잔인하든, 자기 의무로 삼은 일에 흔들림이 없든, 권력욕에 빠져 있든, 평생 비밀 사이를 파고들어 온 영역에서 어느 것 하나 자기 모르게 숨겨지는 것을 용납지 않으려 작정했든, 자기가 한낱 머나먼 빛줄기에 지나지 않는 그 화려함을 마음속으로 멸시하든, 화려한 의뢰인들의 살가운 태도 속에 받은 멸시와 모욕을 늘 차곡차곡 쌓아두든, 이 가운데 어느 하나이든 혹은 전부이든, 어쩌면 마님으로서는 목에 헝겊 조각 같은 넥타이를 두르고 무릎에 리본을 묶은 칙칙한 검정 반바지를 입은 이 녹슨 변호사의 두 눈보다, 차라리 유행을 좇는 눈동자 오천 쌍이 미덥지 못한 경계심으로 자기를 지켜보는 편이 나을지도 모른다."]], [["Sir Leicester sits in my Lady’s room—that room in which Mr.", "레스터 경이 마님의 방에 앉아 있다."], ["Tulkinghorn read the affidavit in Jarndyce and Jarndyce—particularly complacent.", "토킹혼 씨가 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송의 진술서를 읽었던 바로 그 방이다. 유난히 흡족한 표정이다."], ["My Lady, as on that day, sits before the fire with her screen in her hand.", "마님은 그날과 마찬가지로 손에 가리개를 든 채 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있다."], ["Sir Leicester is particularly complacent because he has found in his newspaper some congenial remarks bearing directly on the floodgates and the framework of society.", "레스터 경이 유난히 흡족한 까닭은 신문에서 사회의 둑과 골격을 곧장 겨냥한 마음에 쏙 드는 논평을 찾아냈기 때문이다."], ["They apply so happily to the late case that Sir Leicester has come from the library to my Lady’s room expressly to read them aloud.", "그 논평이 요전번 사건에 어찌나 절묘하게 들어맞는지, 레스터 경은 일부러 그것을 소리 내어 읽어주려고 서재에서 마님의 방까지 건너왔다."], ["“The man who wrote this article,”", "\"이 기사를 쓴 사람은 말이오,\""], ["he observes by way of preface, nodding at the fire as if he were nodding down at the man from a mount, “has a well-balanced mind.”", "그는 서두를 떼며, 마치 높은 산봉우리에서 그 사람을 내려다보며 고개를 끄덕이듯 난롯불을 향해 끄덕이며 말한다. \"균형 잡힌 정신을 지녔어.\""]], [["The man’s mind is not so well balanced but that he bores my Lady, who, after a languid effort to listen, or rather a languid resignation of herself to a show of listening, becomes distraught and falls into a contemplation of the fire as if it were her fire at Chesney Wold, and she had never left it.", "그 사람의 정신이 그리 균형 잡혀 있다고 보기는 어렵지만, 어쨌든 그 사람은 마님을 지루하게 만든다. 마님은 나른하게 귀 기울여 보려 애쓰다가, 아니 차라리 귀 기울이는 시늉이나마 나른하게 받아들여 보려다가, 어느새 정신이 딴 데로 흩어져 난롯불을 골똘히 바라본다."], ["Sir Leicester, quite unconscious, reads on through his double eye-glass, occasionally stopping to remove his glass and express approval, as “Very true indeed,”", "마치 그것이 체스니 월드의 자기 난롯불이고, 한 번도 그곳을 떠난 적이 없는 듯이. 레스터 경은 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 코안경 너머로 계속 읽어 내려가다가, 이따금 멈춰 안경을 벗고는 \"참으로 옳은 말이야,\""], ["“Very properly put,”", "\"참 적절하게 짚었어,\""], ["“I have frequently made the same remark myself,”", "\"나도 종종 똑같은 말을 하곤 했지\""], ["invariably losing his place after each observation, and going up and down the column to find it again.", "하고 찬동을 표하는데, 매번 그렇게 한마디 하고 나면 어김없이 읽던 자리를 잃어버려 다시 찾으려 단을 위아래로 더듬는다."]], [["Sir Leicester is reading with infinite gravity and state when the door opens, and the Mercury in powder makes this strange announcement, “The young man, my Lady, of the name of Guppy.”", "레스터 경이 더없이 진지하고 위엄 있게 읽고 있는데 문이 열리더니, 분을 뒤집어쓴 머큐리가 이 기묘한 통고를 한다. \"마님, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이가 왔습니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester pauses, stares, repeats in a killing voice, “The young man of the name of Guppy?”", "레스터 경이 멈칫하고, 빤히 쳐다보다가, 사람 잡을 듯한 목소리로 되뇐다. \"거피라는 이름의 젊은이라고?\""]], [["Looking round, he beholds the young man of the name of Guppy, much discomfited and not presenting a very impressive letter of introduction in his manner and appearance.", "둘러보니, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이가, 잔뜩 주눅 든 채, 태도와 행색으로 보아 그리 인상적인 소개장이 못 되는 모습으로 서 있다. * 머큐리: 로마 신화의 전령신. 디킨스가 분 바른 가발을 쓰고 시중드는 하인을 빈정대어 부르는 별칭이다."]], [["“Pray,”", "\"그래,\""], ["says Sir Leicester to Mercury, “what do you mean by announcing with this abruptness a young man of the name of Guppy?”", "레스터 경이 머큐리에게 말한다. \"거피라는 이름의 젊은이가 왔다고 이렇게 불쑥 알리다니, 도대체 무슨 뜻이냐?\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir Leicester, but my Lady said she would see the young man whenever he called.", "\"송구합니다, 레스터 경. 다만 마님께서 그 젊은이가 찾아오면 언제든 만나겠다고 말씀하셨기에."], ["I was not aware that you were here, Sir Leicester.”", "레스터 경께서 여기 계신 줄은 미처 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["With this apology, Mercury directs a scornful and indignant look at the young man of the name of Guppy which plainly says, “What do you come calling here for and getting ME into a row?”", "이렇게 변명하고 나서 머큐리는 거피라는 이름의 젊은이에게 경멸과 분노가 어린 눈길을 던지는데, 그 눈빛은 분명 이렇게 말하고 있다. \"대체 뭐 하러 여기 찾아와서 나를 곤경에 빠뜨리는 거야?\""]], [["“It’s quite right.", "\"잘했다."], ["I gave him those directions,”", "내가 그 사람한테 그렇게 일러두었어,\""], ["says my Lady.", "마님이 말한다."], ["“Let the young man wait.”", "\"젊은이를 기다리게 하렴.\""]], [["“By no means, my Lady.", "\"천만에요, 부인."], ["Since he has your orders to come, I will not interrupt you.”", "당신이 오라고 분부했다니 내가 방해하지 않으리다.\""], ["Sir Leicester in his gallantry retires, rather declining to accept a bow from the young man as he goes out and majestically supposing him to be some shoemaker of intrusive appearance.", "레스터 경은 정중하게 물러가는데, 나가는 길에 젊은이의 인사를 받아주기를 사양하다시피 하며, 위풍당당하게도 그를 주제넘게 찾아온 구두장이쯤으로 짐작한다."]], [["Lady Dedlock looks imperiously at her visitor when the servant has left the room, casting her eyes over him from head to foot.", "하인이 방을 나가자, 데드록 마님은 손님을 도도하게 바라보며 머리끝에서 발끝까지 훑는다."], ["She suffers him to stand by the door and asks him what he wants.", "그를 문가에 세워둔 채, 무슨 용건이냐고 묻는다."]], [["“That your ladyship would have the kindness to oblige me with a little conversation,”", "\"마님께서 친절을 베푸시어 잠시 대화를 허락해주셨으면 합니다,\""], ["returns Mr. Guppy, embarrassed.", "거피 씨가 쩔쩔매며 대꾸한다."]], [["“You are, of course, the person who has written me so many letters?”", "\"네가 그렇게나 숱한 편지를 보낸 그 사람이렷다?\""]], [["“Several, your ladyship.", "\"몇 통이었습니다, 마님."], ["Several before your ladyship condescended to favour me with an answer.”", "마님께서 황송하게도 답장을 내려주시기 전까지 몇 통 보냈지요.\""]], [["“And could you not take the same means of rendering a Conversation unnecessary?", "\"그럼 같은 방식으로, 그러니까 편지로 이 대화를 불필요하게 만들 수는 없었느냐?"], ["Can you not still?”", "지금이라도 그럴 수 없느냐?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy screws his mouth into a silent “No!”", "거피 씨는 입을 오므려 소리 없이 \"안 됩니다!\""], ["and shakes his head.", "하는 모양을 짓고는 고개를 젓는다."]], [["“You have been strangely importunate.", "\"넌 기이하리만치 끈질겼다."], ["If it should appear, after all, that what you have to say does not concern me—and I don’t know how it can, and don’t expect that it will—you will allow me to cut you short with but little ceremony.", "결국 네가 할 말이 나와는 아무 상관도 없는 것으로 드러난다면, 어떻게 상관이 있을지도 모르겠고 그러리라 기대도 않지만, 그럴 때는 별 예의 차리지 않고 네 말을 끊어버릴 테니 그리 알아라."], ["Say what you have to say, if you please.”", "할 말이 있거든 어서 하렴.\""]], [["My Lady, with a careless toss of her screen, turns herself towards the fire again, sitting almost with her back to the young man of the name of Guppy.", "마님은 가리개를 무심히 한 번 까딱하고는 다시 난롯불 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이에게 거의 등을 돌리다시피 하고 앉는다."]], [["“With your ladyship’s permission, then,”", "\"그렇다면 마님의 허락을 얻어,\""], ["says the young man, “I will now enter on my business.", "젊은이가 말한다. \"이제 용건으로 들어가겠습니다."], ["Hem!", "흠!"], ["I am, as I told your ladyship in my first letter, in the law.", "저는 첫 편지에서 마님께 말씀드린 대로 법조계에 몸담고 있습니다."], ["Being in the law, I have learnt the habit of not committing myself in writing, and therefore I did not mention to your ladyship the name of the firm with which I am connected and in which my standing—and I may add income—is tolerably good.", "법조계에 있다 보니 글로는 함부로 발을 들이지 않는 습관을 익혔습지요. 그래서 제가 몸담은 회사 이름, 거기서 제 지위가, 덧붙이자면 수입도 제법 괜찮은 그 회사의 이름은 마님께 적어 보내지 않았습니다."], ["I may now state to your ladyship, in confidence, that the name of that firm is Kenge and Carboy, of Lincoln’s Inn, which may not be altogether unknown to your ladyship in connexion with the case in Chancery of Jarndyce and Jarndyce.”", "이제 마님께 은밀히 아뢰자면, 그 회사 이름은 링컨 법학원의 켄지 앤드 카보이로, 어쩌면 마님께서도 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 대법원 소송과 관련하여 영 모르시지는 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["My Lady’s figure begins to be expressive of some attention.", "마님의 자태에 얼마간 주의를 기울이는 기색이 비치기 시작한다."], ["She has ceased to toss the screen and holds it as if she were listening.", "마님은 가리개를 까딱이던 손길을 멈추고, 귀를 기울이는 양 그것을 든 채로 있다."]], [["“Now, I may say to your ladyship at once,”", "\"자, 마님께 곧바로 말씀드리겠습니다만,\""], ["says Mr.", "거피 씨가 조금 대담해져서 말한다."], ["Guppy, a little emboldened, “it is no matter arising out of Jarndyce and Jarndyce that made me so desirous to speak to your ladyship, which conduct I have no doubt did appear, and does appear, obtrusive—in fact, almost blackguardly.”", "\"제가 이토록 마님과 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한 까닭은 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서 비롯된 일 때문이 아닙니다. 물론 이런 행동이 주제넘어 보였고 또 지금도 그렇게 보이리라는 건, 사실 거의 불한당 같다는 것도 의심치 않습니다만.\""]], [["After waiting for a moment to receive some assurance to the contrary, and not receiving any, Mr.", "그렇지 않다고 안심시켜 주는 말을 잠시 기다렸으나 아무 말도 듣지 못하자, 거피 씨가 말을 잇는다."], ["Guppy proceeds, “If it had been Jarndyce and Jarndyce, I should have gone at once to your ladyship’s solicitor, Mr.", "\"만일 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 건이었다면, 저는 곧장 마님의 변호사인 더 필즈의 토킹혼 씨께 갔을 겁니다."], ["Tulkinghorn, of the Fields. I have the pleasure of being acquainted with Mr.", "저는 토킹혼 씨와 안면이 있는 영광을 누리고 있습니다."], ["Tulkinghorn—at least we move when we meet one another—and if it had been any business of that sort, I should have gone to him.”", "적어도 서로 마주치면 목례 정도는 나누지요. 그런 종류의 일이었다면 저는 그분께 갔을 겁니다.\""]], [["My Lady turns a little round and says, “You had better sit down.”", "마님이 몸을 조금 돌리고 말한다. \"앉는 게 좋겠다.\""]], [["“Thank your ladyship.” Mr.", "\"감사합니다, 마님.\""], ["Guppy does so. “Now, your ladyship”—Mr.", "거피 씨가 자리에 앉는다."], ["Guppy refers to a little slip of paper on which he has made small notes of his line of argument and which seems to involve him in the densest obscurity whenever he looks at it—“I—Oh, yes!—I place myself entirely in your ladyship’s hands.", "\"자, 마님,\" 거피 씨는 자기 논지를 작게 메모해둔 쪽지를 들여다보는데, 그것을 볼 때마다 외려 더없이 짙은 안갯속으로 빠져드는 듯하다."], ["If your ladyship was to make any complaint to Kenge and Carboy or to Mr.", "\"저는, 아 그렇지! 저는 전적으로 마님 손에 저 자신을 맡기겠습니다."], ["Tulkinghorn of the present visit, I should be placed in a very disagreeable situation.", "만일 마님께서 오늘 이 방문을 두고 켄지 앤드 카보이나 토킹혼 씨께 무어라 항의하신다면, 저는 아주 난처한 처지에 놓이게 됩니다."], ["That, I openly admit.", "그건 솔직히 인정합니다."], ["Consequently, I rely upon your ladyship’s honour.”", "그러니 저는 마님의 명예를 믿겠습니다.\""]], [["My Lady, with a disdainful gesture of the hand that holds the screen, assures him of his being worth no complaint from her.", "마님은 가리개를 든 손으로 경멸하는 몸짓을 지어, 그가 자기 항의를 받을 만한 가치도 없는 자임을 일러준다."]], [["“Thank your ladyship,” says Mr.", "\"감사합니다, 마님,\""], ["Guppy; “quite satisfactory.", "거피 씨가 말한다. \"더없이 만족스럽습니다."], ["Now—I—dash it!—The fact is that I put down a head or two here of the order of the points I thought of touching upon, and they’re written short, and I can’t quite make out what they mean.", "자, 저는, 에라 모르겠다! 실은 제가 짚으려던 요점의 순서를 한두 가지 여기 적어두었는데, 짧게 줄여 쓴 데다 무슨 뜻인지 도통 알아먹질 못하겠습니다."], ["If your ladyship will excuse me taking it to the window half a moment, I—”", "마님께서 잠깐만 창가로 가져가 보는 걸 양해해주신다면, 저는…\""]], [["Mr. Guppy, going to the window, tumbles into a pair of love-birds, to whom he says in his confusion, “I beg your pardon, I am sure.”", "거피 씨는 창가로 가다가 잉꼬 한 쌍에게 부딪치고는, 당황하여 그들에게 말한다."], ["This does not tend to the greater legibility of his notes.", "\"아이고 죄송합니다, 정말로.\" 이래서야 메모가 더 잘 읽힐 리 없다."], ["He murmurs, growing warm and red and holding the slip of paper now close to his eyes, now a long way off, “C.S.", "그는 얼굴이 달아올라 벌게진 채, 쪽지를 눈에 바싹 댔다가 다시 멀찍이 떼었다가 하며 중얼거린다. \"씨에스."], ["What’s C.S.", "씨에스가 뭐였더라?"], ["for?", "아!"], ["Oh! C.S.!", "씨에스!"], ["Oh, I know!", "아, 알겠다!"], ["Yes, to be sure!”", "그래, 그렇고말고!\""], ["And comes back enlightened.", "그러고는 깨달음을 얻어 돌아온다."]], [["“I am not aware,” says Mr.", "\"마님께서,\""], ["Guppy, standing midway between my Lady and his chair, “whether your ladyship ever happened to hear of, or to see, a young lady of the name of Miss Esther Summerson.”", "거피 씨가 마님과 자기 의자 중간쯤에 서서 말한다. \"혹시라도 에스더 서머슨 양이라는 이름의 젊은 아가씨에 대해 들어보셨거나 만나보신 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["My Lady’s eyes look at him full.", "마님의 두 눈이 그를 똑바로 바라본다."], ["“I saw a young lady of that name not long ago.", "\"그런 이름의 젊은 아가씨를 얼마 전에 보았다."], ["This past autumn.”", "지난 가을이었지.\""]], [["“Now, did it strike your ladyship that she was like anybody?” asks Mr.", "\"그런데 마님께서 보시기에 그 아가씨가 누군가와 닮았다는 생각이 들지 않으셨습니까?\""], ["Guppy, crossing his arms, holding his head on one side, and scratching the corner of his mouth with his memoranda.", "거피 씨가 팔짱을 끼고 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃하며 메모지로 입가를 긁적이며 묻는다."]], [["My Lady removes her eyes from him no more.", "마님은 그에게서 더는 눈을 떼지 않는다."]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“Not like your ladyship’s family?”", "\"마님의 가문 사람들과 닮지 않았던가요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“I think your ladyship,” says Mr.", "\"제 생각에 마님께서는,\""], ["Guppy, “can hardly remember Miss Summerson’s face?”", "거피 씨가 말한다. \"서머슨 양의 얼굴을 잘 기억하지 못하시겠지요?\""]], [["“I remember the young lady very well.", "\"그 젊은 아가씨를 아주 잘 기억한다."], ["What has this to do with me?”", "한데 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐?\""]], [["“Your ladyship, I do assure you that having Miss Summerson’s image imprinted on my ’eart—which I mention in confidence—I found, when I had the honour of going over your ladyship’s mansion of Chesney Wold while on a short out in the county of Lincolnshire with a friend, such a resemblance between Miss Esther Summerson and your ladyship’s own portrait that it completely knocked me over, so much so that I didn’t at the moment even know what it WAS that knocked me over.", "\"마님, 정말로 장담컨대 저는 서머슨 양의 모습을 제 심장에 아로새겨 두고 있는데, 이건 은밀히 말씀드리는 겁니다만, 어느 벗과 함께 링컨셔 지방에 잠깐 나들이 갔을 때 마님의 저택 체스니 월드를 둘러보는 영광을 누리다가, 에스더 서머슨 양과 마님의 초상화가 어찌나 닮았는지 그만 완전히 나가떨어지고 말았습니다. 어찌나 심하게 나가떨어졌던지 그 순간에는 도대체 무엇이 저를 그렇게 나가떨어지게 했는지조차 몰랐을 정도였습니다."], ["And now I have the honour of beholding your ladyship near (I have often, since that, taken the liberty of looking at your ladyship in your carriage in the park, when I dare say you was not aware of me, but I never saw your ladyship so near), it’s really more surprising than I thought it.”", "그리고 이제 마님을 가까이서 뵙는 영광을 누리고 보니, 그 뒤로도 공원에서 마차에 타신 마님을 종종 외람되게 훔쳐보긴 했습니다만, 그땐 아마 마님께서 저를 알아채지 못하셨겠지요, 어쨌든 마님을 이렇게 가까이서 뵌 적은 없었는데, 정말이지 제가 생각했던 것보다 한층 더 놀랍습니다.\""]], [["Young man of the name of Guppy!", "거피라는 이름의 젊은이여!"], ["There have been times, when ladies lived in strongholds and had unscrupulous attendants within call, when that poor life of yours would NOT have been worth a minute’s purchase, with those beautiful eyes looking at you as they look at this moment.", "귀부인들이 견고한 성채에 살며 부르면 달려올 거리에 양심이라곤 없는 시종들을 거느리던 시절이 있었으니, 그런 때라면 지금 이 순간 그대를 바라보는 저 아름다운 두 눈 앞에서, 그대의 가련한 목숨은 단 일 분의 값어치도 못 되었으리라."]], [["My Lady, slowly using her little hand-screen as a fan, asks him again what he supposes that his taste for likenesses has to do with her.", "마님은 작은 손가리개를 부채 삼아 느릿느릿 부치며, 닮은꼴을 향한 그의 취미가 도대체 자기와 무슨 상관이 있다고 여기느냐고 다시 묻는다."]], [["“Your ladyship,” replies Mr.", "\"마님,\""], ["Guppy, again referring to his paper, “I am coming to that.", "거피 씨가 다시 자기 쪽지를 들여다보며 대답한다. \"이제 그 얘기로 들어갑니다."], ["Dash these notes! Oh!", "에라, 이놈의 메모!"], ["‘Mrs.", "아!"], ["Chadband.’ Yes.” Mr.", "'채드밴드 부인.'"], ["Guppy draws his chair a little forward and seats himself again.", "그렇지.\" 거피 씨는 의자를 조금 앞으로 당겨 다시 자리에 앉는다."], ["My Lady reclines in her chair composedly, though with a trifle less of graceful ease than usual perhaps, and never falters in her steady gaze.", "마님은 의자에 차분히 기대 있는데, 어쩌면 평소보다 우아한 여유는 약간 덜할지 몰라도, 그 흔들림 없는 응시만은 조금도 흐트러지지 않는다. \"아, 잠깐만요!\""], ["“A—stop a minute, though!” Mr.", "거피 씨가 또 들여다본다."], ["Guppy refers again.", "\"이에스가 두 번?"], ["“E.S. twice?", "아, 그렇지!"], ["Oh, yes! Yes, I see my way now, right on.”", "그래, 이제 길이 보입니다, 곧장 가면 돼요.\""]], [["Rolling up the slip of paper as an instrument to point his speech with, Mr. Guppy proceeds.", "거피 씨는 자기 말을 강조하는 도구로 삼으려 쪽지를 둘둘 말아 들고는 말을 잇는다."]], [["“Your ladyship, there is a mystery about Miss Esther Summerson’s birth and bringing up.", "\"마님, 에스더 서머슨 양의 출생과 양육에는 수수께끼가 있습니다."], ["I am informed of that fact because—which I mention in confidence—I know it in the way of my profession at Kenge and Carboy’s.", "제가 그 사실을 아는 건, 이것도 은밀히 말씀드리는 겁니다만, 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 직업상 알게 되었기 때문입니다."], ["Now, as I have already mentioned to your ladyship, Miss Summerson’s image is imprinted on my ’eart.", "자, 이미 마님께 말씀드린 대로 서머슨 양의 모습은 제 심장에 아로새겨져 있습니다."], ["If I could clear this mystery for her, or prove her to be well related, or find that having the honour to be a remote branch of your ladyship’s family she had a right to be made a party in Jarndyce and Jarndyce, why, I might make a sort of a claim upon Miss Summerson to look with an eye of more dedicated favour on my proposals than she has exactly done as yet.", "만일 제가 그녀를 위해 이 수수께끼를 풀어준다든가, 그녀가 좋은 가문 출신임을 입증한다든가, 혹은 그녀가 마님 가문의 먼 갈래에 드는 영광을 지녀 잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 당사자로 들어갈 권리가 있음을 알아낸다든가 한다면, 저는 서머슨 양에게 그동안 정확히 보여주지 않았던 것보다 더 헌신적인 호의의 눈길로 제 청혼을 봐달라고 일종의 요구를 할 수 있을 겁니다."], ["In fact, as yet she hasn’t favoured them at all.”", "사실 그녀는 지금껏 제 청혼에 도통 호의를 보인 적이 없거든요.\""]], [["A kind of angry smile just dawns upon my Lady’s face.", "마님의 얼굴에 일종의 노여운 미소가 막 어린다."]], [["“Now, it’s a very singular circumstance, your ladyship,”", "\"자, 이건 참으로 기묘한 정황입니다, 마님,\""], ["says Mr.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["Guppy, “though one of those circumstances that do fall in the way of us professional men—which I may call myself, for though not admitted, yet I have had a present of my articles made to me by Kenge and Carboy, on my mother’s advancing from the principal of her little income the money for the stamp, which comes heavy—that I have encountered the person who lived as servant with the lady who brought Miss Summerson up before Mr.", "\"그래도 저희 법조인의 길에 곧잘 끼어드는 정황 가운데 하나이긴 합니다만, 저도 법조인이라 부를 만한 게, 정식 자격은 못 얻었어도 켄지 앤드 카보이에서 견습 계약서를 선물로 받았거든요. 인지대가 만만치 않은데 그걸 어머니가 얼마 안 되는 수입의 원금에서 떼어 대주셨지요."], ["Jarndyce took charge of her.", "어쨌든 그 기묘한 정황이란, 제가 잔다이스 씨가 서머슨 양을 맡기 전에 그녀를 길러준 부인 곁에서 하녀로 살았던 사람을 만났다는 겁니다."], ["That lady was a Miss Barbary, your ladyship.”", "그 부인은 바버리 양이라는 분이었습니다, 마님.\""]], [["Is the dead colour on my Lady’s face reflected from the screen which has a green silk ground and which she holds in her raised hand as if she had forgotten it, or is it a dreadful paleness that has fallen on her?", "마님의 얼굴에 어린 송장 같은 빛깔은, 초록 비단 바탕의 가리개에서 비친 것인가, 그녀가 까맣게 잊은 듯 치켜든 손에 든 그 가리개에서 비친 것인가, 아니면 그녀에게 내려앉은 끔찍한 창백함인가?"]], [["“Did your ladyship,”", "\"마님께서는,\" 거피 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr. Guppy, “ever happen to hear of Miss Barbary?”", "\"혹시 바버리 양에 대해 들어보신 적이 있으신지요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠다. 그런 것도 같고."], ["I think so. Yes.”", "그래, 들어봤다.\""]], [["“Was Miss Barbary at all connected with your ladyship’s family?”", "\"바버리 양이 마님 가문과 무슨 관련이라도 있었습니까?\""]], [["My Lady’s lips move, but they utter nothing.", "마님의 입술이 움직이지만, 아무 말도 나오지 않는다."], ["She shakes her head.", "그녀가 고개를 젓는다."]], [["“NOT connected?”", "\"관련이 없다고요?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Not to your ladyship’s knowledge, perhaps?", "어쩌면 마님께서 아시는 한에서는 없다는 거겠지요?"], ["Ah!", "아하!"], ["But might be? Yes.”", "그래도 있을 수는 있겠지요?"], ["After each of these interrogatories, she has inclined her head.", "그렇군요.\" 이렇게 물을 때마다 그녀는 고개를 숙여 보였다."], ["“Very good!", "\"아주 좋습니다!"], ["Now, this Miss Barbary was extremely close—seems to have been extraordinarily close for a female, females being generally (in common life at least) rather given to conversation—and my witness never had an idea whether she possessed a single relative.", "자, 이 바버리 양은 입이 무척 무거운 분이었습니다. 여자치고는 유난히 입이 무거웠던 모양인데, 여자란 대개 적어도 일상에서는 말하기를 꽤 즐기는 법이잖습니까."], ["On one occasion, and only one, she seems to have been confidential to my witness on a single point, and she then told her that the little girl’s real name was not Esther Summerson, but Esther Hawdon.”", "어쨌든 제 증인은 그분에게 친척이 한 명이라도 있는지조차 도무지 짐작할 수 없었답니다. 한번, 딱 한 번, 그분이 단 한 가지 점에 대해서만 제 증인에게 속을 털어놓은 적이 있는데, 그때 그분은 그 어린 여자아이의 진짜 이름이 에스더 서머슨이 아니라 에스더 혼이라고 말했답니다.\""]], [["“My God!”", "\"세상에!\""]], [["Mr. Guppy stares.", "거피 씨가 빤히 쳐다본다."], ["Lady Dedlock sits before him looking him through, with the same dark shade upon her face, in the same attitude even to the holding of the screen, with her lips a little apart, her brow a little contracted, but for the moment dead.", "데드록 마님은 그의 앞에 앉아, 얼굴에 그 똑같은 검은 그늘을 드리운 채, 가리개 든 손짓까지 그대로인 자세로, 입술을 조금 벌리고, 이마를 조금 찌푸린 채, 그를 꿰뚫어 보는데, 순간 송장처럼 굳어 있다."], ["He sees her consciousness return, sees a tremor pass across her frame like a ripple over water, sees her lips shake, sees her compose them by a great effort, sees her force herself back to the knowledge of his presence and of what he has said.", "그는 그녀에게 의식이 돌아오는 것을, 수면에 이는 잔물결처럼 그녀의 온몸을 떨림이 스쳐 지나가는 것을, 그녀의 입술이 떨리는 것을, 그녀가 안간힘을 다해 그것을 다잡는 것을, 그의 존재와 그가 한 말에 대한 인식으로 자신을 억지로 되돌리는 것을 본다."], ["All this, so quickly, that her exclamation and her dead condition seem to have passed away like the features of those long-preserved dead bodies sometimes opened up in tombs, which, struck by the air like lightning, vanish in a breath.", "이 모든 것이 어찌나 빠르게 지나갔던지, 그녀의 탄성과 그 송장 같던 상태는 마치 무덤에서 이따금 파헤쳐지는 오래 보존된 시신들의 모습처럼, 바깥 공기가 번개처럼 들이치자 한순간 숨결에 사라져버린 듯하다."]], [["“Your ladyship is acquainted with the name of Hawdon?”", "\"마님께서는 혼이라는 이름을 아십니까?\""]], [["“I have heard it before.”", "\"전에 들어본 적 있다.\""]], [["“Name of any collateral or remote branch of your ladyship’s family?”", "\"마님 가문의 방계나 먼 갈래에 드는 누군가의 이름인가요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“Now, your ladyship,”", "\"자, 마님,\" 거피 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr. Guppy, “I come to the last point of the case, so far as I have got it up.", "\"이제 제가 알아낸 한도 안에서, 이 사건의 마지막 대목으로 들어갑니다."], ["It’s going on, and I shall gather it up closer and closer as it goes on.", "사건은 지금도 진행 중이고, 진행될수록 저는 더 바싹바싹 추려 모을 작정입니다."], ["Your ladyship must know—if your ladyship don’t happen, by any chance, to know already—that there was found dead at the house of a person named Krook, near Chancery Lane, some time ago, a law-writer in great distress.", "마님께서도 아셔야 할 게, 혹시라도 이미 알고 계시지 않다면 말입니다만, 얼마 전 챈서리 레인 부근의 크룩이라는 사람의 집에서 한 법률 필경사가 몹시 곤궁한 처지로 죽은 채 발견되었습니다."], ["Upon which law-writer there was an inquest, and which law-writer was an anonymous character, his name being unknown.", "그 법률 필경사를 두고 검시가 열렸는데, 이 필경사는 이름도 알려지지 않은 무명의 인물이었습니다."], ["But, your ladyship, I have discovered very lately that that law-writer’s name was Hawdon.”", "그런데 마님, 제가 아주 최근에 알아낸 바로는, 그 법률 필경사의 이름이 혼이었습니다.\""]], [["“And what is THAT to me?”", "\"그래서 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐?\""]], [["“Aye, your ladyship, that’s the question!", "\"아, 마님, 바로 그게 문제입니다!"], ["Now, your ladyship, a queer thing happened after that man’s death.", "자, 마님, 그 사람이 죽은 뒤에 기묘한 일이 벌어졌습니다."], ["A lady started up, a disguised lady, your ladyship, who went to look at the scene of action and went to look at his grave.", "한 부인이 나타났는데, 변장한 부인이었습니다, 마님, 그 부인이 사건 현장을 보러 가고 그의 무덤도 보러 갔지요."], ["She hired a crossing-sweeping boy to show it her.", "그 부인은 길거리 청소부 소년을 사서 무덤을 안내받았습니다."], ["If your ladyship would wish to have the boy produced in corroboration of this statement, I can lay my hand upon him at any time.”", "마님께서 이 진술을 뒷받침할 증거로 그 소년을 데려오기를 바라신다면, 저는 언제든 그 아이를 잡아올 수 있습니다.\""]], [["The wretched boy is nothing to my Lady, and she does NOT wish to have him produced.", "그 가련한 소년은 마님에게 아무것도 아니며, 마님은 그를 데려오기를 바라지 않는다."]], [["“Oh, I assure your ladyship it’s a very queer start indeed,”", "\"아, 정말이지 마님, 그건 참으로 기묘한 일이었습니다,\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“If you was to hear him tell about the rings that sparkled on her fingers when she took her glove off, you’d think it quite romantic.”", "\"그 부인이 장갑을 벗었을 때 손가락에 반짝이던 반지들 이야기를 그 아이한테서 들으시면, 마님께서도 어지간히 낭만적이라 여기실 겁니다.\""]], [["There are diamonds glittering on the hand that holds the screen.", "가리개를 든 손에 다이아몬드가 반짝이고 있다."], ["My Lady trifles with the screen and makes them glitter more, again with that expression which in other times might have been so dangerous to the young man of the name of Guppy.", "마님은 가리개를 만지작거려 그것을 더 반짝이게 하는데, 또다시 그 표정을 짓는다. 다른 시절이었다면 거피라는 이름의 젊은이에게 자못 위험했을 그 표정을."]], [["“It was supposed, your ladyship, that he left no rag or scrap behind him by which he could be possibly identified.", "\"마님, 그 사람이 신원을 가려낼 만한 천 조각 하나, 부스러기 하나 남기지 않았으리라 여겨졌습니다. 하지만 남겼지요."], ["But he did. He left a bundle of old letters.”", "그는 오래된 편지 한 뭉치를 남겼습니다.\""]], [["The screen still goes, as before.", "가리개는 여전히 아까처럼 움직인다."], ["All this time her eyes never once release him.", "이 내내 그녀의 두 눈은 단 한 번도 그를 놓아주지 않는다."]], [["“They were taken and secreted.", "\"그 편지들은 누군가 가져가 감춰두었습니다."], ["And to-morrow night, your ladyship, they will come into my possession.”", "그런데 내일 밤이면, 마님, 그것이 제 손에 들어옵니다.\""]], [["“Still I ask you, what is this to me?”", "\"그래도 다시 묻겠다, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐?\""]], [["“Your ladyship, I conclude with that.”", "\"마님, 그 말씀으로 마무리하겠습니다.\""], ["Mr. Guppy rises.", "거피 씨가 일어선다."], ["“If you think there’s enough in this chain of circumstances put together—in the undoubted strong likeness of this young lady to your ladyship, which is a positive fact for a jury; in her having been brought up by Miss Barbary; in Miss Barbary stating Miss Summerson’s real name to be Hawdon; in your ladyship’s knowing both these names VERY WELL; and in Hawdon’s dying as he did—to give your ladyship a family interest in going further into the case, I will bring these papers here.", "\"한데 짜 맞춰진 이 정황의 사슬에, 그러니까 이 젊은 아가씨가 마님과 의심할 바 없이 강하게 닮았다는 점, 이건 배심원 앞에서는 확실한 사실입니다만, 그 아가씨가 바버리 양 손에 자랐다는 점, 바버리 양이 서머슨 양의 진짜 이름을 혼이라 했다는 점, 마님께서 이 두 이름을 다 아주 잘 아신다는 점, 그리고 혼이 그렇게 죽었다는 점, 이 모든 것에 마님께서 이 사건을 더 깊이 파고들 만한 가문의 이해관계를 부여하는 무언가가 충분히 들어 있다고 여기신다면, 저는 그 서류들을 이리로 가져오겠습니다."], ["I don’t know what they are, except that they are old letters: I have never had them in my possession yet.", "그게 무엇인지는, 오래된 편지라는 것 말고는 저도 모릅니다. 아직 제 손에 넣어본 적이 없으니까요."], ["I will bring those papers here as soon as I get them and go over them for the first time with your ladyship.", "그것을 손에 넣는 대로 이리로 가져와, 마님과 함께 처음으로 펼쳐 보겠습니다."], ["I have told your ladyship my object.", "제 목적은 마님께 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I have told your ladyship that I should be placed in a very disagreeable situation if any complaint was made, and all is in strict confidence.”", "어떤 항의라도 들어오면 제가 아주 난처한 처지에 놓인다는 것도 말씀드렸고요. 모든 게 철저히 비밀입니다.\""]], [["Is this the full purpose of the young man of the name of Guppy, or has he any other?", "이것이 거피라는 이름의 젊은이의 온전한 속셈일까, 아니면 다른 무엇이 더 있는 걸까?"], ["Do his words disclose the length, breadth, depth, of his object and suspicion in coming here; or if not, what do they hide?", "그의 말은 여기 찾아온 그의 목적과 의심의 길이며 너비며 깊이를 다 드러내고 있는 걸까, 아니라면 무엇을 감추고 있는 걸까?"], ["He is a match for my Lady there.", "그 점에서는 그도 마님에게 만만찮은 상대다."], ["She may look at him, but he can look at the table and keep that witness-box face of his from telling anything.", "마님은 그를 바라볼 수 있어도, 그는 탁자를 바라보며 그 증인석 같은 얼굴이 아무것도 발설하지 않도록 지킬 수 있으니 말이다."]], [["“You may bring the letters,”", "\"편지를 가져와도 좋다,\""], ["says my Lady, “if you choose.”", "마님이 말한다. \"네가 원한다면.\""]], [["“Your ladyship is not very encouraging, upon my word and honour,”", "\"마님께서는 정말이지 별로 격려가 되어주지 않으시는군요, 맹세코,\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, a little injured.", "거피 씨가 조금 서운한 듯 말한다."]], [["“You may bring the letters,”", "\"편지를 가져와도 좋다,\""], ["she repeats in the same tone, “if you—please.”", "그녀가 같은 어조로 되풀이한다. \"네가, 원한다면.\""]], [["“It shall be done.", "\"그리하겠습니다."], ["I wish your ladyship good day.”", "마님, 안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["On a table near her is a rich bauble of a casket, barred and clasped like an old strong-chest.", "그녀 곁의 탁자에는 오래된 금고처럼 빗장과 잠금쇠가 채워진 호화로운 보석함 하나가 놓여 있다."], ["She, looking at him still, takes it to her and unlocks it.", "그녀는 여전히 그를 바라보면서 그것을 끌어당겨 자물쇠를 연다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I assure your ladyship I am not actuated by any motives of that sort,” says Mr.", "마님, 단언컨대 저는 그런 따위 동기로 움직이는 게 아닙니다,\""], ["Guppy, “and I couldn’t accept anything of the kind.", "거피 씨가 말한다. \"그런 것은 도저히 받을 수 없습니다."], ["I wish your ladyship good day, and am much obliged to you all the same.”", "마님, 안녕히 계십시오. 그래도 정말 감사합니다.\""]], [["So the young man makes his bow and goes downstairs, where the supercilious Mercury does not consider himself called upon to leave his Olympus by the hall-fire to let the young man out.", "이렇게 젊은이는 인사를 하고 아래층으로 내려가는데, 거기서 거만한 머큐리는 젊은이를 내보내주려고 현관 난롯불 옆 자기 올림포스를 떠날 필요는 없다고 여긴다."]], [["As Sir Leicester basks in his library and dozes over his newspaper, is there no influence in the house to startle him, not to say to make the very trees at Chesney Wold fling up their knotted arms, the very portraits frown, the very armour stir?", "레스터 경이 서재에서 느긋이 쉬며 신문 위로 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있는 동안, 이 집 안에는 그를 소스라치게 할 기운이라곤 없는 걸까? 하물며 체스니 월드의 나무들이 옹이진 팔을 치켜들게 하고, 초상화들이 눈살을 찌푸리게 하고, 갑옷들이 들썩이게 할 기운이 없는 걸까?"]], [["No.", "없다."], ["Words, sobs, and cries are but air, and air is so shut in and shut out throughout the house in town that sounds need be uttered trumpet-tongued indeed by my Lady in her chamber to carry any faint vibration to Sir Leicester’s ears; and yet this cry is in the house, going upward from a wild figure on its knees.", "말도, 흐느낌도, 울부짖음도 한낱 공기일 뿐이요, 이 도시의 저택은 어디고 공기가 어찌나 꽁꽁 갇히고 막혀 있는지, 마님이 자기 방에서 나팔 같은 목청으로 외쳐야 비로소 어렴풋한 떨림이나마 레스터 경의 귀에 가닿을 만하다. 그런데도 이 울부짖음은 집 안에 있어, 무릎 꿇은 어떤 광란의 형상에게서 위로 솟구쳐 오른다."]], [["“O my child, my child!", "\"오 내 아이, 내 아이!"], ["Not dead in the first hours of her life, as my cruel sister told me, but sternly nurtured by her, after she had renounced me and my name!", "잔인한 언니가 내게 말했듯 태어나 첫 시간에 죽은 게 아니라, 언니가 나와 내 이름을 저버린 뒤로 그 손에 모질게 길러졌구나!"], ["O my child, O my child!”", "오 내 아이, 오 내 아이!\""]], [["Esther’s Narrative\nRichard had been gone away some time when a visitor came to pass a few days with us.", "리처드가 떠난 지 얼마쯤 지났을 때, 며칠 묵으러 손님이 찾아왔습니다."], ["It was an elderly lady. It was Mrs.", "나이 지긋한 부인이었지요."], ["Woodcourt, who, having come from Wales to stay with Mrs. Bayham Badger and having written to my guardian, “by her son Allan’s desire,”", "바로 우드코트 부인으로, 웨일스에서 와 베이엄 배저 부인 댁에 머물던 차에, \"아들 앨런의 부탁으로\""], ["to report that she had heard from him and that he was well “and sent his kind remembrances to all of us,” had been invited by my guardian to make a visit to Bleak House.", "후견인에게 편지를 보내 아들 소식을 들었으며 아들이 잘 지내고 \"우리 모두에게 다정한 안부를 전한다\"라고 알렸더니, 후견인이 황폐한 집을 한번 방문하라고 청한 것이었습니다."], ["She stayed with us nearly three weeks.", "부인은 우리와 거의 삼 주를 함께 지냈습니다."], ["She took very kindly to me and was extremely confidential, so much so that sometimes she almost made me uncomfortable.", "나를 무척 다정하게 대했고 흉허물 없이 속내를 털어놓았는데, 어찌나 그러던지 때로는 나를 거의 불편하게 만들 정도였습니다."], ["I had no right, I knew very well, to be uncomfortable because she confided in me, and I felt it was unreasonable; still, with all I could do, I could not quite help it.", "부인이 나를 믿고 속을 털어놓는다 해서 내가 불편해할 권리가 없다는 건 나도 잘 알았고, 그게 이치에 맞지 않는다고도 느꼈습니다. 그래도 아무리 애를 써도 그 마음을 영 떨치지 못했습니다."]], [["She was such a sharp little lady and used to sit with her hands folded in each other looking so very watchful while she talked to me that perhaps I found that rather irksome.", "부인은 어찌나 영민한 작은 분인지, 나에게 이야기하는 동안 두 손을 맞잡은 채 앉아 무척이나 살피는 눈빛으로 바라보곤 했는데, 어쩌면 나는 그게 다소 거북했던 것 같습니다."], ["Or perhaps it was her being so upright and trim, though I don’t think it was that, because I thought that quaintly pleasant.", "아니면 그토록 꼿꼿하고 단정한 자세 탓이었을지도 모르지만, 그건 아닌 듯합니다. 그건 어딘가 예스럽게 정겹다고 여겼으니까요."], ["Nor can it have been the general expression of her face, which was very sparkling and pretty for an old lady.", "노부인치고는 무척 반짝이고 고운, 그 얼굴 전체에서 풍기는 표정 때문도 아닐 겁니다."], ["I don’t know what it was.", "무엇 때문이었는지 모르겠습니다."], ["Or at least if I do now, I thought I did not then.", "아니면 적어도 지금은 안다 해도, 그때는 몰랐다고 여겼습니다."], ["Or at least—but it don’t matter.", "아니면 적어도 — 하지만 그건 중요치 않습니다."]], [["Of a night when I was going upstairs to bed, she would invite me into her room, where she sat before the fire in a great chair; and, dear me, she would tell me about Morgan ap-Kerrig until I was quite low-spirited!", "밤에 내가 잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갈 때면, 부인은 나를 자기 방으로 청해 들였는데, 그 방에서 부인은 커다란 의자에 앉아 난롯불 앞을 지키고 있었습니다. 그러고는, 세상에, 내가 아주 풀이 죽을 때까지 모건 앱케리그 이야기를 늘어놓는 것이었습니다!"], ["Sometimes she recited a few verses from Crumlinwallinwer and the Mewlinnwillinwodd (if those are the right names, which I dare say they are not), and would become quite fiery with the sentiments they expressed.", "가끔은 크럼린왈린워와 뮬린윌린워드(이게 맞는 이름이라면 말이지만, 분명 아닐 겁니다)에서 시구를 몇 줄 읊었고, 거기 담긴 정취에 자못 열을 올리곤 했습니다."], ["Though I never knew what they were (being in Welsh), further than that they were highly eulogistic of the lineage of Morgan ap-Kerrig.", "그것들이 (웨일스 말이라) 무슨 뜻인지 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없었지만, 모건 앱케리그 가문을 더없이 드높이 기리는 내용이라는 것만은 알았습니다."]], [["“So, Miss Summerson,”", "\"그러니까, 서머슨 양,\""], ["she would say to me with stately triumph, “this, you see, is the fortune inherited by my son.", "부인은 위엄에 찬 의기양양한 투로 나에게 말하곤 했습니다. \"이게 바로 내 아들이 물려받은 재산이라우."], ["Wherever my son goes, he can claim kindred with Ap-Kerrig.", "내 아들이 어딜 가든 앱케리그와 한 핏줄이라고 내세울 수 있지요."], ["He may not have money, but he always has what is much better—family, my dear.”", "돈은 없을지언정, 그보다 훨씬 나은 걸 늘 지니고 있으니 — 바로 가문이에요, 그래요.\""]], [["I had my doubts of their caring so very much for Morgan ap-Kerrig in India and China, but of course I never expressed them.", "인도며 중국에서 사람들이 모건 앱케리그를 그토록 끔찍이 여길까 싶어 나는 의구심이 들었지만, 물론 그런 말은 한 번도 입 밖에 내지 않았습니다."], ["I used to say it was a great thing to be so highly connected.", "그렇게 명문가와 이어져 있다는 건 대단한 일이라고 말하곤 했지요."]], [["“It IS, my dear, a great thing,”", "\"대단한 일이라우, 그래요,\" 우드코트 부인은 대답하곤 했습니다."], ["Mrs. Woodcourt would reply.", "\"불리한 점도 있긴 하지요."], ["“It has its disadvantages; my son’s choice of a wife, for instance, is limited by it, but the matrimonial choice of the royal family is limited in much the same manner.”", "이를테면 내 아들이 아내를 고르는 폭이 그 때문에 좁아진다든가. 하지만 왕가의 혼인 상대 선택도 꼭 그런 식으로 좁혀지는 법이라우.\""]], [["Then she would pat me on the arm and smooth my dress, as much as to assure me that she had a good opinion of me, the distance between us notwithstanding.", "그러고 나면 내 팔을 토닥이고 옷매무새를 매만지곤 했는데, 우리 사이에 놓인 거리에도 불구하고 나를 좋게 본다는 걸 새삼 확인시켜 주려는 듯했습니다."]], [["“Poor Mr. Woodcourt, my dear,”", "\"가엾은 우드코트 씨는 말이에요,\""], ["she would say, and always with some emotion, for with her lofty pedigree she had a very affectionate heart, “was descended from a great Highland family, the MacCoorts of MacCoort.", "부인은 늘 어떤 감회에 젖어 말하곤 했는데, 그토록 드높은 혈통을 지녔으면서도 무척 다정한 마음을 지녔으니까요. \"하일랜드의 대단한 가문, 매쿠어트 가의 매쿠어트 출신이었답니다."], ["He served his king and country as an officer in the Royal Highlanders, and he died on the field.", "왕립 하일랜더 연대 장교로 임금과 나라를 섬기다가 전장에서 숨을 거두었지요."], ["My son is one of the last representatives of two old families.", "내 아들은 두 오래된 가문의 마지막 후손 가운데 하나라우."], ["With the blessing of heaven he will set them up again and unite them with another old family.”", "하늘의 축복으로 그 가문을 다시 일으켜 또 다른 오래된 가문과 맺어줄 거예요.\""]], [["It was in vain for me to try to change the subject, as I used to try, only for the sake of novelty or perhaps because—but I need not be so particular.", "화제를 바꿔보려고 애써도 소용없었습니다. 그저 새로운 이야기를 해보고 싶어서, 아니면 어쩌면 — 하지만 그렇게 시시콜콜 따질 필요는 없겠지요."], ["Mrs. Woodcourt never would let me change it.", "우드코트 부인은 결코 화제를 바꾸도록 내버려 두지 않았습니다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"이봐요,\""], ["she said one night, “you have so much sense and you look at the world in a quiet manner so superior to your time of life that it is a comfort to me to talk to you about these family matters of mine.", "어느 날 밤 부인이 말했습니다. \"당신은 분별이 무척 깊고, 나이에 비해 한참 높은 데서 세상을 차분히 바라보니, 내 집안 일을 이렇게 당신에게 이야기하는 게 나로선 위안이 된다우."], ["You don’t know much of my son, my dear; but you know enough of him, I dare say, to recollect him?”", "당신은 내 아들을 잘 모르지요. 하지만 그래도, 분명, 떠올릴 만큼은 알지요?\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am.", "\"네, 부인."], ["I recollect him.”", "그분을 기억합니다.\""]], [["“Yes, my dear.", "\"그래요."], ["Now, my dear, I think you are a judge of character, and I should like to have your opinion of him.”", "자, 이봐요, 당신은 사람 됨됨이를 볼 줄 안다고 생각해요. 그러니 그 애에 대한 당신 의견을 듣고 싶구려.\""]], [["“Oh, Mrs. Woodcourt,”", "\"어머, 우드코트 부인,\""], ["said I, “that is so difficult!”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그건 너무 어려운데요!\""]], [["“Why is it so difficult, my dear?”", "\"왜 그게 그리 어렵다는 거예요?\""], ["she returned.", "부인이 되물었습니다."], ["“I don’t see it myself.”", "\"나는 잘 모르겠는데.\""]], [["“To give an opinion—”", "\"의견을 말씀드린다는 게 —\""]], [["“On so slight an acquaintance, my dear.", "\"이렇게 친분이 얕은데 말이지요."], ["THAT’S true.”", "그래요, 맞아요.\""]], [["I didn’t mean that, because Mr.", "내 말은 그런 뜻이 아니었습니다."], ["Woodcourt had been at our house a good deal altogether and had become quite intimate with my guardian.", "우드코트 씨는 우리 집에 두루 자주 드나들었고 후견인과도 꽤 가까운 사이가 되었으니까요."], ["I said so, and added that he seemed to be very clever in his profession—we thought—and that his kindness and gentleness to Miss Flite were above all praise.", "나는 그렇게 말하고, 그분이 자기 직업에서 무척 유능해 보인다고 — 우리 생각에 그렇다고 — 덧붙였으며, 플라이트 양에게 베푸는 친절과 다정함은 무어라 칭찬해도 모자란다고 했습니다."]], [["“You do him justice!”", "\"제대로 보았네요!\""], ["said Mrs. Woodcourt, pressing my hand.", "우드코트 부인이 내 손을 꼭 누르며 말했습니다."], ["“You define him exactly.", "\"그 애를 정확히 짚어냈어요."], ["Allan is a dear fellow, and in his profession faultless. I say it, though I am his mother.", "앨런은 사랑스러운 녀석이고, 제 일에서는 나무랄 데가 없지요."], ["Still, I must confess he is not without faults, love.”", "어미인 내가 하는 말이지만요."], ["“None of us are,” said I.", "그래도 솔직히 말하자면, 그 애에게 흠이 없는 건 아니라우.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"우리 가운데 누구도 흠이 없는 사람은 없지요,\""], ["But his really are faults that he might correct, and ought to correct,”", "내가 말했습니다. \"아!"], ["returned the sharp old lady, sharply shaking her head.", "하지만 그 애 흠은 정말이지 고칠 수 있고, 마땅히 고쳐야 할 흠이라우,\" 영민한 노부인이 매섭게 고개를 저으며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I am so much attached to you that I may confide in you, my dear, as a third party wholly disinterested, that he is fickleness itself.”", "\"당신에게 무척 정이 들었으니, 아무 이해관계 없는 제삼자에게 하듯 당신에게 털어놓을 수 있겠네요. 그 애는 변덕 그 자체예요.\""]], [["I said I should have thought it hardly possible that he could have been otherwise than constant to his profession and zealous in the pursuit of it, judging from the reputation he had earned.", "그분이 쌓은 명성으로 미루어 보면, 자기 직업에 한결같고 그 길을 좇는 데 열성을 다하지 않았으리라고는 도무지 생각하기 어렵겠다고 나는 말했습니다."]], [["“You are right again, my dear,”", "\"그것도 옳은 말이에요,\""], ["the old lady retorted, “but I don’t refer to his profession, look you.”", "노부인이 받아쳤습니다. \"하지만 내가 말하는 건 그 애 직업이 아니라우, 봐요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“No,” said she.", "\"그래요,\""], ["“I refer, my dear, to his social conduct.", "부인이 말했습니다. \"내가 말하는 건, 이봐요, 그 애의 사교 처신이라우."], ["He is always paying trivial attentions to young ladies, and always has been, ever since he was eighteen.", "그 애는 늘 젊은 아가씨들에게 시시한 관심을 기울이고, 열여덟 살 적부터 줄곧 그래왔지요."], ["Now, my dear, he has never really cared for any one of them and has never meant in doing this to do any harm or to express anything but politeness and good nature.", "그런데 말이에요, 그 애는 그중 누구도 진심으로 마음에 둔 적이 없고, 그러면서 무슨 해를 끼치거나 예의와 호의 말고 다른 무엇을 드러내려던 적도 없어요."], ["Still, it’s not right, you know; is it?”", "그래도 옳지 않은 일이지요. 안 그래요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네,\""], ["said I, as she seemed to wait for me.", "부인이 내 대답을 기다리는 듯해 나는 그렇게 말했습니다."]], [["“And it might lead to mistaken notions, you see, my dear.”", "\"그러다 보면 오해를 살 수도 있잖아요, 봐요, 이봐요.\""]], [["I supposed it might.", "그럴 수도 있겠다고 나는 짐작했습니다."]], [["“Therefore, I have told him many times that he really should be more careful, both in justice to himself and in justice to others. And he has always said, ‘Mother, I will be; but you know me better than anybody else does, and you know I mean no harm—in short, mean nothing.’ All of which is very true, my dear, but is no justification. However, as he is now gone so far away and for an indefinite time, and as he will have good opportunities and introductions, we may consider this past and gone. And you, my dear,” said the old lady, who was now all nods and smiles, “regarding your dear self, my love?”", "\"그래서 나는 그 애한테 여러 번 일렀어요. 정말 좀 더 조심해야 한다고, 자기를 위해서나 남을 위해서나 말이지요. 그러면 그 애는 늘 이러더군요. '어머니, 그러겠어요. 하지만 어머니는 누구보다 저를 잘 아시잖아요. 제가 아무 해도 끼칠 뜻이 없다는 걸 — 한마디로, 아무 뜻도 없다는 걸 아시잖아요.' 다 옳은 말이지요, 이봐요. 하지만 변명은 못 되지요. 하지만 이제 그 애가 그렇게 멀리, 기약 없이 떠났고, 좋은 기회와 소개도 많이 받을 테니, 이건 다 지나간 일로 칠 수 있겠어요. 그런데 당신은 말이에요, 이봐요,\" 어느새 온통 고갯짓과 미소뿐인 노부인이 말했습니다. \"당신 자신은 어떻게 할 거예요?\""]], [["“Me, Mrs. Woodcourt?”", "\"저 말씀인가요, 우드코트 부인?\""]], [["“Not to be always selfish, talking of my son, who has gone to seek his fortune and to find a wife—when do you mean to seek YOUR fortune and to find a husband, Miss Summerson?", "\"제 운을 찾아 떠나고 아내를 구하러 간 아들 이야기만 늘어놓으며 늘 그렇게 제 식구 일만 챙길 순 없지요 — 당신은 언제 당신 운을 찾고 남편을 구할 생각이에요, 서머슨 양? 어머나, 봐요!"], ["Hey, look you! Now you blush!”", "이제 얼굴이 다 붉어지네요!\""]], [["I don’t think I did blush—at all events, it was not important if I did—and I said my present fortune perfectly contented me and I had no wish to change it.", "나는 얼굴을 붉히지 않은 것 같습니다 — 설령 붉혔다 해도 그건 중요한 일이 아니었고요 — 그래서 나는 지금의 내 처지가 더없이 만족스러우며 그것을 바꿀 마음이 없다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Shall I tell you what I always think of you and the fortune yet to come for you, my love?”", "\"내가 당신과, 당신에게 앞으로 다가올 운에 대해 늘 어떻게 생각하는지 말해줄까요?\""], ["said Mrs. Woodcourt.", "우드코트 부인이 말했습니다."]], [["“If you believe you are a good prophet,”", "\"부인께서 스스로 예언을 잘하신다고 믿으신다면요,\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, then, it is that you will marry some one very rich and very worthy, much older—five and twenty years, perhaps—than yourself.", "\"그렇다면, 당신은 무척 부유하고 무척 훌륭하며 당신보다 한참 — 어쩌면 스물다섯 살쯤 — 나이 많은 누군가와 결혼할 거예요."], ["And you will be an excellent wife, and much beloved, and very happy.”", "그리고 더없이 훌륭한 아내가 되어, 많은 사랑을 받고, 무척 행복할 거예요.\""]], [["“That is a good fortune,”", "\"좋은 운이네요,\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I. “But why is it to be mine?”", "\"하지만 왜 그게 제 운이어야 하나요?\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"이봐요,\""], ["she returned, “there’s suitability in it—you are so busy, and so neat, and so peculiarly situated altogether that there’s suitability in it, and it will come to pass.", "부인이 대꾸했습니다. \"거기엔 어울림이 있어요 — 당신은 그토록 부지런하고, 그토록 야무지며, 처지가 그토록 유달리 남다르니, 거기엔 어울림이 있고, 그러니 그렇게 될 거예요."], ["And nobody, my love, will congratulate you more sincerely on such a marriage than I shall.”", "그리고 당신이 그런 혼인을 한다면, 이봐요, 나만큼 진심으로 축하해줄 사람도 없을 거예요.\""]], [["It was curious that this should make me uncomfortable, but I think it did.", "이런 말이 나를 불편하게 만들다니 묘한 일이었지만, 정말 그랬던 것 같습니다."], ["I know it did.", "분명 그랬습니다."], ["It made me for some part of that night uncomfortable.", "그 때문에 나는 그날 밤 한동안 불편했습니다."], ["I was so ashamed of my folly that I did not like to confess it even to Ada, and that made me more uncomfortable still.", "내 어리석음이 어찌나 부끄럽던지 에이다에게조차 털어놓고 싶지 않았고, 그게 나를 더더욱 불편하게 만들었습니다."], ["I would have given anything not to have been so much in the bright old lady’s confidence if I could have possibly declined it.", "어떻게든 거절할 수만 있었다면, 그 명랑한 노부인의 속내 이야기를 그토록 깊이 듣지 않기 위해 무엇이든 다 내놓았을 겁니다."], ["It gave me the most inconsistent opinions of her.", "그 때문에 나는 부인에 대해 더없이 앞뒤가 안 맞는 생각을 품게 되었습니다."], ["At one time I thought she was a story-teller, and at another time that she was the pink of truth.", "어느 순간에는 부인이 이야기를 꾸며대는 사람이라 여겼고, 또 어느 순간에는 진실의 본보기라 여겼습니다."], ["Now I suspected that she was very cunning, next moment I believed her honest Welsh heart to be perfectly innocent and simple.", "지금은 부인이 무척 교활하다고 의심하다가, 다음 순간에는 그 정직한 웨일스 마음이 더없이 천진하고 단순하다고 믿었습니다."], ["And after all, what did it matter to me, and why did it matter to me?", "그리고 따지고 보면, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이며, 왜 나에게 상관이 되었을까요?"], ["Why could not I, going up to bed with my basket of keys, stop to sit down by her fire and accommodate myself for a little while to her, at least as well as to anybody else, and not trouble myself about the harmless things she said to me?", "왜 나는 열쇠 바구니를 들고 잠자리에 들러 올라가다가, 부인의 난롯불 곁에 잠시 앉아 적어도 다른 누구에게 그러듯 부인에게 잠깐 맞춰주며, 부인이 나에게 건넨 해롭지 않은 말들로 마음을 졸이지 않을 수 없었을까요?"], ["Impelled towards her, as I certainly was, for I was very anxious that she should like me and was very glad indeed that she did, why should I harp afterwards, with actual distress and pain, on every word she said and weigh it over and over again in twenty scales?", "분명 나는 부인에게 마음이 끌렸으니 — 부인이 나를 좋아해 주길 무척 바랐고 정말로 그래주어 더없이 기뻤으니 — 왜 그러고 나서 그 말 한마디 한마디를, 진짜 괴로움과 아픔에 사로잡혀 곱씹고, 스무 개 저울에 올려 거듭거듭 달아보았을까요?"], ["Why was it so worrying to me to have her in our house, and confidential to me every night, when I yet felt that it was better and safer somehow that she should be there than anywhere else?", "부인이 우리 집에 머물며 밤마다 나에게 속을 터놓는 게 왜 나에게 그토록 마음 쓰이는 일이었을까요? 그러면서도 부인이 다른 어느 곳보다 여기 있는 편이 어쩐지 더 낫고 더 안전하다고 느꼈으면서요."], ["These were perplexities and contradictions that I could not account for.", "이것들은 내가 설명할 수 없는 곤혹스러움이요 모순이었습니다."], ["At least, if I could—but I shall come to all that by and by, and it is mere idleness to go on about it now.", "적어도, 설명할 수 있다 해도 — 하지만 그 모든 건 차차 이야기할 테니, 지금 늘어놓는 건 그저 부질없는 짓일 뿐입니다."]], [["So when Mrs. Woodcourt went away, I was sorry to lose her but was relieved too.", "그래서 우드코트 부인이 떠났을 때, 나는 부인을 잃는 게 서운했지만 한편으론 마음이 놓이기도 했습니다."], ["And then Caddy Jellyby came down, and Caddy brought such a packet of domestic news that it gave us abundant occupation.", "그러고 나서 캐디 젤리비가 내려왔는데, 캐디가 어찌나 집안 소식 보따리를 잔뜩 가져왔던지 우리는 할 일이 넘쳐났습니다."]], [["First Caddy declared (and would at first declare nothing else) that I was the best adviser that ever was known.", "먼저 캐디는 (처음에는 다른 말은 한사코 입에 올리지 않으며) 내가 세상에 둘도 없는 더없이 훌륭한 조언자라고 단언했습니다."], ["This, my pet said, was no news at all; and this, I said, of course, was nonsense.", "내 귀염둥이는 그건 전혀 새삼스러운 소식이 아니라고 했고, 나는 물론 그건 터무니없는 소리라고 했습니다."], ["Then Caddy told us that she was going to be married in a month and that if Ada and I would be her bridesmaids, she was the happiest girl in the world.", "그러고 나서 캐디는 한 달 뒤에 결혼하게 되었다고, 에이다와 내가 들러리를 서준다면 자기는 세상에서 가장 행복한 처녀가 되겠다고 말했습니다."], ["To be sure, this was news indeed; and I thought we never should have done talking about it, we had so much to say to Caddy, and Caddy had so much to say to us.", "정말이지 이건 그야말로 소식이었지요. 우리는 캐디에게 할 말이 너무 많고 캐디도 우리에게 할 말이 너무 많아, 그 이야기는 도무지 끝날 줄 몰랐습니다."]], [["It seemed that Caddy’s unfortunate papa had got over his bankruptcy—“gone through the Gazette,” was the expression Caddy used, as if it were a tunnel—with the general clemency and commiseration of his creditors, and had got rid of his affairs in some blessed manner without succeeding in understanding them, and had given up everything he possessed (which was not worth much, I should think, to judge from the state of the furniture), and had satisfied every one concerned that he could do no more, poor man.", "알고 보니 캐디의 불운한 아빠는 파산을 헤쳐 나온 모양이었습니다 — 캐디가 쓴 말로는 \"관보를 통과했다\"라고 했는데, 마치 무슨 터널이라도 되는 양 — 채권자들의 너그러운 자비와 동정 덕에 어찌어찌 복받은 방식으로 일을 정리했지만 정작 그 내막은 끝내 이해하지 못했고, 가진 것을 죄다 내놓았으며(가구 상태로 미루어 보건대 그리 값나가는 것도 아니었을 듯합니다), 더는 어쩔 도리가 없다는 걸 관계자 모두에게 납득시켰으니, 가엾은 양반이었지요."], ["So, he had been honourably dismissed to “the office” to begin the world again.", "그렇게 해서 명예롭게 풀려나 \"사무실\"로 가 다시 처음부터 세상을 시작하게 되었습니다."], ["What he did at the office, I never knew; Caddy said he was a “custom-house and general agent,”", "그 사무실에서 무슨 일을 하는지는 나도 끝내 알지 못했습니다."], ["and the only thing I ever understood about that business was that when he wanted money more than usual he went to the docks to look for it, and hardly ever found it.", "캐디는 아빠가 \"세관 겸 일반 대리업자\"라고 했는데, 그 일에 대해 내가 알아들은 건 단 하나, 평소보다 돈이 더 궁할 때면 아빠가 부두로 그것을 찾으러 가지만 좀처럼 찾지 못한다는 것뿐이었습니다."]], [["As soon as her papa had tranquillized his mind by becoming this shorn lamb, and they had removed to a furnished lodging in Hatton Garden (where I found the children, when I afterwards went there, cutting the horse hair out of the seats of the chairs and choking themselves with it), Caddy had brought about a meeting between him and old Mr. Turveydrop; and poor Mr.", "이렇게 털 깎인 어린양 신세가 되어 아빠가 마음을 추스르고, 식구들이 해턴 가든의 가구 딸린 셋방으로 옮겨 가자(나중에 그곳에 가보니 아이들이 의자 방석에서 말총을 뽑아내 그걸로 목이 메도록 입에 물고 있었습니다), 캐디는 아빠와 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 만나는 자리를 마련했습니다."], ["Jellyby, being very humble and meek, had deferred to Mr. Turveydrop’s deportment so submissively that they had become excellent friends.", "그러자 가엾은 젤리비 씨가 워낙 겸손하고 온순한 분이라 터비드롭 씨의 풍채에 어찌나 고분고분 굽실거렸던지, 둘은 더없이 좋은 벗이 되었습니다."], ["By degrees, old Mr. Turveydrop, thus familiarized with the idea of his son’s marriage, had worked up his parental feelings to the height of contemplating that event as being near at hand and had given his gracious consent to the young couple commencing housekeeping at the academy in Newman Street when they would.", "차츰 늙은 터비드롭 씨도 그렇게 아들의 결혼이라는 생각에 익숙해지면서, 그 일이 코앞에 닥쳤음을 헤아릴 만큼 부정을 한껏 끌어올렸고, 젊은 부부가 원할 때 뉴먼 거리의 학원에서 살림을 시작해도 좋다고 너그러이 승낙했습니다."]], [["“And your papa, Caddy.", "\"그래서 너희 아빠는, 캐디."], ["What did he say?”", "뭐라고 하셨어?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Poor Pa,”", "가엾은 아빠,\""], ["said Caddy, “only cried and said he hoped we might get on better than he and Ma had got on.", "캐디가 말했습니다. \"그저 우시기만 하면서, 우리가 아빠랑 엄마보다는 더 잘 지내길 바란다고만 하셨어."], ["He didn’t say so before Prince, he only said so to me.", "프린스 앞에서는 그러지 않으시고, 나한테만 그러셨지. 그러고는 이러셨어."], ["And he said, ‘My poor girl, you have not been very well taught how to make a home for your husband, but unless you mean with all your heart to strive to do it, you had better murder him than marry him—if you really love him.’”", "'가엾은 내 딸아, 너는 남편을 위해 가정을 꾸리는 법을 제대로 배우지 못했다. 하지만 진심으로 온 마음을 다해 그러려고 애쓸 작정이 아니라면 — 그 사람을 정말로 사랑한다면 — 결혼하느니 차라리 그를 죽이는 게 낫다.'\""]], [["“And how did you reassure him, Caddy?”", "\"그래서 너는 아빠를 어떻게 안심시켰어, 캐디?\""]], [["“Why, it was very distressing, you know, to see poor Pa so low and hear him say such terrible things, and I couldn’t help crying myself.", "\"그게, 너도 알겠지만, 가엾은 아빠가 그렇게 풀이 죽어 그런 끔찍한 말씀을 하시는 걸 보니 무척 마음이 아파서, 나도 울지 않을 수 없었어."], ["But I told him that I DID mean it with all my heart and that I hoped our house would be a place for him to come and find some comfort in of an evening and that I hoped and thought I could be a better daughter to him there than at home.", "하지만 나는 정말 온 마음을 다할 작정이라고, 우리 집이 아빠가 저녁이면 찾아와 위안을 얻을 수 있는 곳이 되길 바란다고, 거기서는 집에 있을 때보다 더 나은 딸이 될 수 있길 바라고 또 그러리라 생각한다고 말씀드렸어."], ["Then I mentioned Peepy’s coming to stay with me, and then Pa began to cry again and said the children were Indians.”", "그러고 나서 피피가 나랑 같이 와서 지내게 될 거라고 했더니, 아빠가 또 우시면서 그 애들은 인디언이라고 하셨어.\""]], [["“Indians, Caddy?”", "\"인디언이라고, 캐디?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"응,\""], ["said Caddy, “wild Indians.", "캐디가 말했습니다. \"사나운 인디언이래."], ["And Pa said”—here she began to sob, poor girl, not at all like the happiest girl in the world—“that he was sensible the best thing that could happen to them was their being all tomahawked together.”", "그러고는 아빠가 이러셨어\" — 여기서 그 애는 흐느끼기 시작했는데, 가엾게도 세상에서 가장 행복한 처녀와는 영 딴판이었지요 — \"그 애들한테 일어날 수 있는 가장 좋은 일은 다 같이 토마호크에 찍히는 거라는 걸 자기도 잘 안다고 하셨어.\""]], [["Ada suggested that it was comfortable to know that Mr. Jellyby did not mean these destructive sentiments.", "에이다는 젤리비 씨가 그런 파괴적인 말을 진심으로 한 게 아니라는 걸 아니 다행이라고 했습니다."]], [["“No, of course I know Pa wouldn’t like his family to be weltering in their blood,”", "\"응, 물론 아빠가 식구들이 피투성이가 되어 나뒹구는 걸 바라지 않으신다는 건 나도 알아,\" 캐디가 말했습니다."], ["said Caddy, “but he means that they are very unfortunate in being Ma’s children and that he is very unfortunate in being Ma’s husband; and I am sure that’s true, though it seems unnatural to say so.”", "\"하지만 그 애들이 엄마의 자식인 게 무척 불운하고, 아빠가 엄마의 남편인 게 무척 불운하다는 뜻이지. 이런 말 하는 게 도리에 어긋나는 것 같긴 하지만, 그게 사실인 건 분명해.\""]], [["I asked Caddy if Mrs. Jellyby knew that her wedding-day was fixed.", "나는 캐디에게 젤리비 부인이 결혼 날짜가 잡힌 걸 아느냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["You know what Ma is, Esther,”", "너도 엄마가 어떤지 알잖아, 에스더,\""], ["she returned.", "그 애가 대답했습니다."], ["“It’s impossible to say whether she knows it or not.", "\"아시는지 모르시는지 도무지 말할 수가 없어. 여러 번 말씀드렸지."], ["She has been told it often enough; and when she IS told it, she only gives me a placid look, as if I was I don’t know what—a steeple in the distance,”", "그런데 말씀드려도 그저 나를 차분한 눈빛으로 바라보실 뿐인데, 마치 내가 뭐랄까 — 저 멀리 있는 첨탑쯤 되는 것처럼 말이야,\" 캐디가 문득 떠오른 생각을 말했습니다."], ["said Caddy with a sudden idea; “and then she shakes her head and says ‘Oh, Caddy, Caddy, what a tease you are!’ and goes on with the Borrioboola letters.”", "\"그러고는 고개를 저으면서 '아, 캐디, 캐디, 넌 어쩜 그렇게 사람을 못살게 구니!' 하시고는 보리오불라 편지를 계속 쓰셔.\""]], [["“And about your wardrobe, Caddy?”", "\"그럼 네 혼수는, 캐디?\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I. For she was under no restraint with us.", "그 애는 우리 앞에서 조금도 거리낌이 없었으니까요."]], [["“Well, my dear Esther,”", "\"글쎄, 에스더,\""], ["she returned, drying her eyes, “I must do the best I can and trust to my dear Prince never to have an unkind remembrance of my coming so shabbily to him.", "그 애가 눈물을 닦으며 대답했습니다. \"할 수 있는 데까지 최선을 다하고, 내가 이렇게 초라한 차림으로 가더라도 사랑하는 프린스가 결코 서운하게 여기지 않으리라 믿는 수밖에."], ["If the question concerned an outfit for Borrioboola, Ma would know all about it and would be quite excited.", "보리오불라로 보낼 채비에 관한 문제라면 엄마가 죄다 알고 한껏 들떠 하시겠지."], ["Being what it is, she neither knows nor cares.”", "그게 아니니, 알지도 못하고 신경도 안 쓰셔.\""]], [["Caddy was not at all deficient in natural affection for her mother, but mentioned this with tears as an undeniable fact, which I am afraid it was.", "캐디는 어머니에 대한 자연스러운 애정이 조금도 모자라지 않았지만, 부정할 수 없는 사실로서 이 말을 눈물과 함께 꺼냈고, 안타깝게도 그건 사실이었습니다."], ["We were sorry for the poor dear girl and found so much to admire in the good disposition which had survived under such discouragement that we both at once (I mean Ada and I) proposed a little scheme that made her perfectly joyful.", "우리는 가엾은 그 애가 딱했고, 그토록 풀 죽을 만한 처지에서도 살아남은 그 착한 천성에 탄복할 점이 어찌나 많던지, 우리 둘은(에이다와 나 말입니다) 그 자리에서 그 애를 더없이 기쁘게 할 작은 계획을 내놓았습니다."], ["This was her staying with us for three weeks, my staying with her for one, and our all three contriving and cutting out, and repairing, and sewing, and saving, and doing the very best we could think of to make the most of her stock.", "그건 캐디가 우리 집에서 삼 주를 지내고, 내가 캐디네서 일주일을 지내며, 우리 셋이 머리를 짜내 마름질하고, 손보고, 바느질하고, 아껴서, 그 애가 가진 것을 최대한 활용하도록 우리가 생각해낼 수 있는 최선을 다하는 것이었습니다."], ["My guardian being as pleased with the idea as Caddy was, we took her home next day to arrange the matter and brought her out again in triumph with her boxes and all the purchases that could be squeezed out of a ten-pound note, which Mr.", "후견인도 캐디만큼이나 그 생각을 반겨서, 우리는 이튿날 캐디를 집에 데려가 일을 마련하고, 다시 의기양양하게 데리고 나왔습니다. 상자들과, 십 파운드짜리 지폐 한 장에서 짜낼 수 있는 모든 물건까지 함께 말입니다."], ["Jellyby had found in the docks I suppose, but which he at all events gave her.", "그 지폐는 젤리비 씨가 부두에서 찾아낸 것이지 싶은데, 어쨌든 그가 그 애에게 준 것이었습니다."], ["What my guardian would not have given her if we had encouraged him, it would be difficult to say, but we thought it right to compound for no more than her wedding-dress and bonnet.", "우리가 부추겼다면 후견인이 그 애에게 무엇을 더 주지 않았을지 가늠하기 어렵겠지만, 우리는 결혼 예복과 보닛 이상은 받지 않기로 매듭짓는 게 옳다고 여겼습니다."], ["He agreed to this compromise, and if Caddy had ever been happy in her life, she was happy when we sat down to work.", "후견인도 이 타협에 동의했고, 캐디가 평생 한 번이라도 행복했던 적이 있다면, 우리가 일감을 펼쳐놓고 앉았을 때 행복했습니다."]], [["She was clumsy enough with her needle, poor girl, and pricked her fingers as much as she had been used to ink them.", "가엾게도 그 애는 바늘 솜씨가 영 서툴러서, 예전에 손에 잉크를 묻히던 만큼이나 손가락을 찔러댔습니다."], ["She could not help reddening a little now and then, partly with the smart and partly with vexation at being able to do no better, but she soon got over that and began to improve rapidly.", "이따금 얼굴이 약간 붉어지는 걸 어쩌지 못했는데, 따끔거려서 그렇기도 하고 더 잘 못하는 게 분해서 그렇기도 했지요. 하지만 곧 그것을 이겨내고 부쩍 솜씨가 늘기 시작했습니다."], ["So day after day she, and my darling, and my little maid Charley, and a milliner out of the town, and I, sat hard at work, as pleasantly as possible.", "그렇게 날마다 그 애와, 내 귀염둥이와, 내 어린 하녀 찰리와, 시내에서 온 모자 만드는 여자와, 내가 더없이 즐겁게 일에 몰두했습니다."]], [["Over and above this, Caddy was very anxious “to learn housekeeping,”", "이뿐 아니라 캐디는 자기 말대로 \"살림을 배우고\" 싶어 무척 안달이었습니다."], ["as she said.", "그런데, 세상에!"], ["Now, mercy upon us! The idea of her learning housekeeping of a person of my vast experience was such a joke that I laughed, and coloured up, and fell into a comical confusion when she proposed it.", "어마어마한 경륜을 지닌 내게서 살림을 배운다는 발상이 어찌나 우스웠던지, 그 애가 그러자고 했을 때 나는 웃음을 터뜨리고, 얼굴이 붉어지고, 우스꽝스러운 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 꼴에 빠지고 말았습니다."], ["However, I said, “Caddy, I am sure you are very welcome to learn anything that you can learn of ME, my dear,”", "그래도 나는 \"캐디, 나한테서 배울 수 있는 거라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 가르쳐줄게\""], ["and I showed her all my books and methods and all my fidgety ways.", "하고는, 내 장부며 방식이며 내 까탈스러운 버릇을 죄다 보여주었습니다."], ["You would have supposed that I was showing her some wonderful inventions, by her study of them; and if you had seen her, whenever I jingled my housekeeping keys, get up and attend me, certainly you might have thought that there never was a greater imposter than I with a blinder follower than Caddy Jellyby.", "그 애가 그것들을 들여다보는 모습을 보면, 내가 무슨 놀라운 발명품이라도 보여주는 줄 알았을 겁니다. 그리고 내가 살림 열쇠를 짤랑거릴 때마다 그 애가 벌떡 일어나 시중을 드는 걸 보았다면, 캐디 젤리비처럼 눈먼 추종자를 거느린 나처럼 대단한 사기꾼은 세상에 없으리라 여겼을 게 분명합니다."]], [["So what with working and housekeeping, and lessons to Charley, and backgammon in the evening with my guardian, and duets with Ada, the three weeks slipped fast away.", "그렇게 일하고 살림하고, 찰리에게 글을 가르치고, 저녁이면 후견인과 주사위 놀이를 하고, 에이다와 이중창을 부르는 사이 삼 주가 후딱 지나갔습니다."], ["Then I went home with Caddy to see what could be done there, and Ada and Charley remained behind to take care of my guardian.", "그러고 나서 나는 캐디와 함께 그 애 집으로 가 거기서 무엇을 할 수 있을지 보았고, 에이다와 찰리는 후견인을 보살피러 남았습니다."]], [["When I say I went home with Caddy, I mean to the furnished lodging in Hatton Garden. We went to Newman Street two or three times, where preparations were in progress too—a good many, I observed, for enhancing the comforts of old Mr.", "캐디와 함께 그 애 집으로 갔다고 했지만, 해턴 가든의 가구 딸린 셋방을 말하는 겁니다."], ["Turveydrop, and a few for putting the newly married couple away cheaply at the top of the house—but our great point was to make the furnished lodging decent for the wedding-breakfast and to imbue Mrs. Jellyby beforehand with some faint sense of the occasion.", "우리는 뉴먼 거리에도 두세 번 갔는데, 거기서도 채비가 진행 중이었습니다 — 보아하니 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 안락을 더하기 위한 것이 꽤 많았고, 갓 결혼한 부부를 집 맨 위층에 값싸게 들이기 위한 것은 몇 가지뿐이었습니다 — 하지만 우리의 큰 목표는 가구 딸린 셋방을 결혼식 아침 식사에 어울리게 그럴듯하게 꾸미고, 젤리비 부인에게 미리 그날의 행사에 대한 어렴풋한 감각이나마 불어넣는 것이었습니다."]], [["The latter was the more difficult thing of the two because Mrs.", "둘 중에 후자가 더 어려운 일이었습니다."], ["Jellyby and an unwholesome boy occupied the front sitting-room (the back one was a mere closet), and it was littered down with waste-paper and Borrioboolan documents, as an untidy stable might be littered with straw.", "젤리비 부인과 병약한 사내아이가 앞쪽 거실을 차지하고 있었는데(뒤쪽 방은 그저 벽장이나 다름없었지요), 그곳은 어수선한 마구간에 짚이 깔리듯 폐지와 보리오불라 서류들이 어지러이 널려 있었습니다."], ["Mrs. Jellyby sat there all day drinking strong coffee, dictating, and holding Borrioboolan interviews by appointment.", "젤리비 부인은 온종일 거기 앉아 진한 커피를 들이켜고, 받아쓰기를 시키고, 약속을 잡아 보리오불라 면담을 했습니다."], ["The unwholesome boy, who seemed to me to be going into a decline, took his meals out of the house.", "병약한 사내아이는 내가 보기에 시름시름 야위어가는 듯했는데, 끼니는 집 밖에서 해결했습니다."], ["When Mr. Jellyby came home, he usually groaned and went down into the kitchen.", "젤리비 씨는 집에 돌아오면 으레 끙 앓는 소리를 내며 부엌으로 내려갔습니다."], ["There he got something to eat if the servant would give him anything, and then, feeling that he was in the way, went out and walked about Hatton Garden in the wet.", "거기서 하녀가 무어라도 내주면 요기를 했고, 그러고 나면 자기가 거치적거린다는 생각에 밖으로 나가 비를 맞으며 해턴 가든을 거닐었습니다."], ["The poor children scrambled up and tumbled down the house as they had always been accustomed to do.", "가엾은 아이들은 늘 하던 대로 집을 기어오르다 굴러떨어지곤 했지요."]], [["The production of these devoted little sacrifices in any presentable condition being quite out of the question at a week’s notice, I proposed to Caddy that we should make them as happy as we could on her marriage morning in the attic where they all slept, and should confine our greatest efforts to her mama and her mama’s room, and a clean breakfast. In truth Mrs.", "일주일이라는 짧은 말미에 이 가엾은 어린 희생양들을 봐줄 만한 꼴로 꾸며낸다는 건 도무지 어림없는 일이었으므로, 나는 캐디에게 제안하기를, 결혼식 날 아침 그 애들이 다 같이 자는 다락방에서 할 수 있는 만큼 즐겁게 해주고, 우리의 가장 큰 노력은 그 애 엄마와 엄마 방, 그리고 깔끔한 아침 식사에 쏟자고 했습니다."], ["Jellyby required a good deal of attention, the lattice-work up her back having widened considerably since I first knew her and her hair looking like the mane of a dustman’s horse.", "사실 젤리비 부인은 손이 꽤 많이 가는 분이었습니다. 등의 격자 모양 끈은 내가 처음 알았을 적보다 한참 벌어져 있었고, 머리카락은 청소부 말의 갈기 같았으니까요."]], [["Thinking that the display of Caddy’s wardrobe would be the best means of approaching the subject, I invited Mrs. Jellyby to come and look at it spread out on Caddy’s bed in the evening after the unwholesome boy was gone.", "캐디의 혼수를 펼쳐 보이는 것이 이 화제로 다가서는 가장 좋은 방편이겠다 싶어, 나는 병약한 사내아이가 돌아간 뒤 저녁에 젤리비 부인을 청해, 침대에 펼쳐놓은 그것을 보러 오게 했습니다."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양,\""], ["said she, rising from her desk with her usual sweetness of temper, “these are really ridiculous preparations, though your assisting them is a proof of your kindness.", "부인이 평소의 더없이 온화한 기색으로 책상에서 일어나며 말했습니다. \"이건 정말이지 우스꽝스러운 채비예요."], ["There is something so inexpressibly absurd to me in the idea of Caddy being married!", "그래도 당신이 거드는 건 당신의 친절을 보여주는 증표지요. 캐디가 결혼한다는 생각만 하면 어쩐지 말로 다 못 할 만큼 우습기 짝이 없다우!"], ["Oh, Caddy, you silly, silly, silly puss!”", "아, 캐디, 이 바보, 바보, 바보 같은 것아!\""]], [["She came upstairs with us notwithstanding and looked at the clothes in her customary far-off manner.", "그래도 부인은 우리와 함께 위층으로 올라와 늘 그러듯 동떨어진 태도로 옷가지를 들여다보았습니다. 그것들은 부인에게 한 가지 또렷한 생각을 일깨운 모양이었습니다."], ["They suggested one distinct idea to her, for she said with her placid smile, and shaking her head, “My good Miss Summerson, at half the cost, this weak child might have been equipped for Africa!”", "부인이 차분한 미소를 띤 채 고개를 저으며 말했으니까요. \"착한 서머슨 양, 이 반값이면 이 가냘픈 아이를 아프리카로 보낼 채비를 갖출 수 있었을 텐데!\""]], [["On our going downstairs again, Mrs. Jellyby asked me whether this troublesome business was really to take place next Wednesday.", "다시 아래층으로 내려가자, 젤리비 부인은 이 성가신 일이 정말로 다음 수요일에 벌어지는 게 맞느냐고 나에게 물었습니다."], ["And on my replying yes, she said, “Will my room be required, my dear Miss Summerson?", "그렇다고 대답하자 부인은 이렇게 말했습니다. \"내 방이 필요한가요, 서머슨 양?"], ["For it’s quite impossible that I can put my papers away.”", "내 서류를 치운다는 건 도무지 불가능한 일이라서요.\""]], [["I took the liberty of saying that the room would certainly be wanted and that I thought we must put the papers away somewhere.", "나는 외람되게도 그 방이 분명 필요할 거라고, 그러니 서류를 어딘가에 치워두어야 할 것 같다고 말했습니다. \"글쎄요, 서머슨 양,\""], ["“Well, my dear Miss Summerson,”", "젤리비 부인이 말했습니다."], ["said Mrs. Jellyby, “you know best, I dare say.", "\"당신이 제일 잘 알겠지요."], ["But by obliging me to employ a boy, Caddy has embarrassed me to that extent, overwhelmed as I am with public business, that I don’t know which way to turn.", "하지만 캐디가 나를 사내아이까지 부리도록 떠밀어, 가뜩이나 공무에 파묻혀 사는 나를 어찌나 곤란하게 만드는지, 도무지 어느 쪽으로 돌아서야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["We have a Ramification meeting, too, on Wednesday afternoon, and the inconvenience is very serious.”", "게다가 수요일 오후에는 지부 회의도 있어서, 여간 불편한 게 아니랍니다.\""]], [["“It is not likely to occur again,”", "\"이런 일이 또 일어나진 않겠지요,\""], ["said I, smiling.", "나는 미소 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“Caddy will be married but once, probably.”", "\"캐디는 아마 결혼을 딱 한 번만 할 테니까요.\""]], [["“That’s true,” Mrs.", "\"그건 맞아요,\""], ["Jellyby replied; “that’s true, my dear.", "젤리비 부인이 대답했습니다."], ["I suppose we must make the best of it!”", "\"맞는 말이에요. 어떻게든 잘 넘겨야겠지요!\""]], [["The next question was how Mrs. Jellyby should be dressed on the occasion.", "다음 문제는 그날 젤리비 부인이 무엇을 입을 것인가였습니다."], ["I thought it very curious to see her looking on serenely from her writing-table while Caddy and I discussed it, occasionally shaking her head at us with a half-reproachful smile like a superior spirit who could just bear with our trifling.", "캐디와 내가 그 일을 의논하는 동안 부인이 글쓰는 탁자에서 태연히 지켜보다가, 우리의 하찮은 짓을 겨우 견뎌주는 우월한 영혼처럼 절반쯤 나무라는 미소를 띠고 이따금 고개를 젓는 걸 보니 무척 묘했습니다."]], [["The state in which her dresses were, and the extraordinary confusion in which she kept them, added not a little to our difficulty; but at length we devised something not very unlike what a common-place mother might wear on such an occasion.", "부인의 옷가지가 어떤 상태인지, 또 그것을 얼마나 유별나게 어질러놓고 사는지가 우리의 어려움에 적잖이 보탬이 되었습니다. 하지만 마침내 우리는 그런 자리에 평범한 어머니가 입을 법한 것과 그리 다르지 않은 무언가를 마련해냈습니다."], ["The abstracted manner in which Mrs. Jellyby would deliver herself up to having this attire tried on by the dressmaker, and the sweetness with which she would then observe to me how sorry she was that I had not turned my thoughts to Africa, were consistent with the rest of her behaviour.", "옷 만드는 여자가 그 차림을 입혀보는 동안 젤리비 부인이 넋을 놓고 몸을 내맡기던 모습이며, 그러고 나서 내가 아프리카로 생각을 돌리지 않은 게 못내 안타깝다고 더없이 온화하게 나에게 말하던 모습은, 부인의 다른 처신과 한결같았습니다."]], [["The lodging was rather confined as to space, but I fancied that if Mrs.", "그 셋방은 공간이 다소 비좁았지만, 나는 이런 상상을 했습니다."], ["Jellyby’s household had been the only lodgers in Saint Paul’s or Saint Peter’s, the sole advantage they would have found in the size of the building would have been its affording a great deal of room to be dirty in.", "설령 젤리비 부인의 식솔이 세인트폴 대성당이나 세인트피터 대성당의 유일한 세입자였다 한들, 그 건물의 크기에서 찾아낼 수 있는 단 하나의 이점은 더럽힐 자리가 넉넉하다는 것뿐이었으리라고요."], ["I believe that nothing belonging to the family which it had been possible to break was unbroken at the time of those preparations for Caddy’s marriage, that nothing which it had been possible to spoil in any way was unspoilt, and that no domestic object which was capable of collecting dirt, from a dear child’s knee to the door-plate, was without as much dirt as could well accumulate upon it.", "캐디의 결혼을 위한 그 채비가 한창일 무렵, 그 집안에 속한 것 가운데 깨뜨릴 수 있었던 것치고 깨지지 않은 게 없었고, 어떤 식으로든 망가뜨릴 수 있었던 것치고 망가지지 않은 게 없었으며, 사랑스러운 아이의 무릎부터 문패에 이르기까지 먼지가 쌓일 만한 살림살이치고 쌓일 수 있는 만큼 먼지가 잔뜩 끼지 않은 게 없었다고 나는 믿습니다."]], [["Poor Mr. Jellyby, who very seldom spoke and almost always sat when he was at home with his head against the wall, became interested when he saw that Caddy and I were attempting to establish some order among all this waste and ruin and took off his coat to help.", "좀처럼 말이 없고 집에 있을 때면 거의 늘 머리를 벽에 기댄 채 앉아 있던 가엾은 젤리비 씨는, 캐디와 내가 이 온갖 폐물과 잔해 속에서 얼마간 질서를 세워보려 애쓰는 걸 보더니 관심이 동했는지, 윗옷을 벗고 거들었습니다."], ["But such wonderful things came tumbling out of the closets when they were opened—bits of mouldy pie, sour bottles, Mrs. Jellyby’s caps, letters, tea, forks, odd boots and shoes of children, firewood, wafers, saucepan-lids, damp sugar in odds and ends of paper bags, footstools, blacklead brushes, bread, Mrs.", "그런데 벽장을 열자 어찌나 놀라운 것들이 우르르 쏟아져 나오던지 — 곰팡이 슨 파이 조각, 시어버린 병들, 젤리비 부인의 모자들, 편지들, 차, 포크들, 짝 안 맞는 아이들 장화와 신발, 땔감, 봉인지, 냄비 뚜껑들, 여기저기 종이봉지 자투리에 든 눅눅한 설탕, 발받침들, 흑연 솔들, 빵, 젤리비 부인의 보닛들, 제본에 버터가 들러붙은 책들, 부러진 촛대에 거꾸로 처박혀 꺼진 흘러내린 양초 토막들, 호두 껍데기들, 새우 대가리와 꼬리들, 식탁 깔개들, 장갑들, 커피 찌꺼기, 우산들 — 그는 겁먹은 얼굴이 되어 다시 손을 놓았습니다."], ["Jellyby’s bonnets, books with butter sticking to the binding, guttered candle ends put out by being turned upside down in broken candlesticks, nutshells, heads and tails of shrimps, dinner-mats, gloves, coffee-grounds, umbrellas—that he looked frightened, and left off again. But he came regularly every evening and sat without his coat, with his head against the wall, as though he would have helped us if he had known how.", "그래도 그는 저녁마다 어김없이 와서, 윗옷도 벗어둔 채 머리를 벽에 기대고 앉아, 마치 방법만 안다면 우리를 거들기라도 할 것처럼 있었습니다."]], [["“Poor Pa!”", "\"가엾은 아빠!\""], ["said Caddy to me on the night before the great day, when we really had got things a little to rights.", "큰 날을 하루 앞둔 밤, 정말로 어지간히 정돈이 되었을 무렵 캐디가 나에게 말했습니다."], ["“It seems unkind to leave him, Esther.", "\"아빠를 두고 떠나는 게 매정한 일 같아, 에스더."], ["But what could I do if I stayed!", "하지만 내가 남는다고 무얼 할 수 있겠어!"], ["Since I first knew you, I have tidied and tidied over and over again, but it’s useless.", "너를 처음 알게 된 뒤로 치우고 또 치우고 거듭 치워봤지만, 다 소용없는 일이야."], ["Ma and Africa, together, upset the whole house directly.", "엄마랑 아프리카가 합세하면 온 집을 대번에 뒤집어놓거든."], ["We never have a servant who don’t drink.", "우리 집엔 술 안 마시는 하녀라곤 한 명도 없어."], ["Ma’s ruinous to everything.”", "엄마는 모든 걸 망쳐놓고 마셔.\""]], [["Mr. Jellyby could not hear what she said, but he seemed very low indeed and shed tears, I thought.", "젤리비 씨는 그 애가 한 말을 들을 수 없었지만, 무척이나 풀이 죽어 보였고, 내 보기에 눈물을 흘렸습니다."]], [["“My heart aches for him; that it does!”", "\"아빠 생각만 하면 가슴이 아파. 정말이야!\""], ["sobbed Caddy.", "캐디가 흐느꼈습니다."], ["“I can’t help thinking to-night, Esther, how dearly I hope to be happy with Prince, and how dearly Pa hoped, I dare say, to be happy with Ma.", "\"오늘 밤 자꾸만 이런 생각이 들어, 에스더. 내가 얼마나 간절히 프린스와 행복하길 바라는지, 그리고 아빠도 분명 얼마나 간절히 엄마와 행복하길 바라셨을지."], ["What a disappointed life!”", "얼마나 좌절뿐인 인생인지!\""]], [["“My dear Caddy!” said Mr.", "\"내 사랑하는 캐디!\""], ["Jellyby, looking slowly round from the wail.", "젤리비 씨가 벽에서 천천히 고개를 돌리며 말했습니다."], ["It was the first time, I think, I ever heard him say three words together.", "그가 단어 세 개를 잇달아 말하는 걸 들은 건 그때가 처음이었지 싶습니다."]], [["“Yes, Pa!”", "\"네, 아빠!\""], ["cried Caddy, going to him and embracing him affectionately.", "캐디가 외치며 다가가 다정하게 그를 끌어안았습니다."]], [["“My dear Caddy,”", "\"사랑하는 캐디,\""], ["said Mr. Jellyby.", "젤리비 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Never have—”", "\"절대 갖지 마—\""]], [["“Not Prince, Pa?”", "\"프린스 말고요, 아빠?\""], ["faltered Caddy.", "캐디가 더듬거렸습니다."], ["“Not have Prince?”", "\"프린스를 갖지 말라고요?\""]], [["“Yes, my dear,”", "\"아니다, 얘야,\""], ["said Mr. Jellyby.", "젤리비 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Have him, certainly.", "\"그 애는 당연히 가져야지."], ["But, never have—”", "하지만, 절대 갖지 마—\""]], [["I mentioned in my account of our first visit in Thavies Inn that Richard described Mr. Jellyby as frequently opening his mouth after dinner without saying anything.", "타비스 인에서의 첫 방문을 이야기할 때, 리처드가 젤리비 씨를 두고 저녁 식사 뒤에 아무 말도 없이 입만 자주 벌리던 사람으로 묘사했다고 적었습니다."], ["It was a habit of his.", "그건 그분의 버릇이었지요."], ["He opened his mouth now a great many times and shook his head in a melancholy manner.", "그는 지금도 여러 번 입을 벌리고는 서글픈 기색으로 고개를 저었습니다."]], [["“What do you wish me not to have?", "\"제가 무얼 갖지 말기를 바라세요?"], ["Don’t have what, dear Pa?”", "무얼 갖지 말라는 거예요, 사랑하는 아빠?\""], ["asked Caddy, coaxing him, with her arms round his neck.", "캐디가 그의 목에 팔을 두른 채 어르며 물었습니다."]], [["“Never have a mission, my dear child.”", "\"절대 사명을 갖지 마라, 사랑하는 내 딸아.\""]], [["Mr. Jellyby groaned and laid his head against the wall again, and this was the only time I ever heard him make any approach to expressing his sentiments on the Borrioboolan question.", "젤리비 씨는 끙 앓는 소리를 내며 다시 벽에 머리를 기댔는데, 그가 보리오불라 문제에 대한 심경을 표현하려는 기색을 조금이라도 내비치는 걸 내가 들은 건 그때가 유일했습니다."], ["I suppose he had been more talkative and lively once, but he seemed to have been completely exhausted long before I knew him.", "한때는 그도 더 말이 많고 활기찼으리라 짐작하지만, 내가 알기 한참 전에 이미 완전히 기진맥진해버린 듯했습니다."]], [["I thought Mrs. Jellyby never would have left off serenely looking over her papers and drinking coffee that night.", "그날 밤 젤리비 부인은 태연히 서류를 들여다보고 커피를 마시는 일을 도무지 그만둘 줄 모를 것 같았습니다."], ["It was twelve o’clock before we could obtain possession of the room, and the clearance it required then was so discouraging that Caddy, who was almost tired out, sat down in the middle of the dust and cried.", "우리가 그 방을 차지하게 된 건 열두 시가 되어서였는데, 그때 치워야 할 것이 어찌나 막막하던지 거의 녹초가 된 캐디는 먼지 한가운데 주저앉아 울었습니다."], ["But she soon cheered up, and we did wonders with it before we went to bed.", "하지만 곧 기운을 차렸고, 우리는 잠자리에 들기 전에 그 방을 놀랍도록 바꿔놓았습니다."]], [["In the morning it looked, by the aid of a few flowers and a quantity of soap and water and a little arrangement, quite gay.", "아침이 되자 그 방은 꽃 몇 송이와 넉넉한 비누 물, 약간의 정리 덕에 제법 화사해 보였습니다."], ["The plain breakfast made a cheerful show, and Caddy was perfectly charming.", "소박한 아침 식탁은 흥겨운 광경을 자아냈고, 캐디는 더없이 사랑스러웠습니다."], ["But when my darling came, I thought—and I think now—that I never had seen such a dear face as my beautiful pet’s.", "하지만 내 귀염둥이가 왔을 때, 나는 생각했습니다 — 지금도 생각합니다 — 내 아름다운 귀염둥이만큼 사랑스러운 얼굴은 한 번도 본 적이 없다고요."]], [["We made a little feast for the children upstairs, and we put Peepy at the head of the table, and we showed them Caddy in her bridal dress, and they clapped their hands and hurrahed, and Caddy cried to think that she was going away from them and hugged them over and over again until we brought Prince up to fetch her away—when, I am sorry to say, Peepy bit him.", "우리는 위층에서 아이들에게 작은 잔치를 베풀어주고, 피피를 식탁 상석에 앉히고, 신부 차림을 한 캐디를 보여주었더니, 아이들은 손뼉을 치며 만세를 불렀습니다. 캐디는 그 애들을 떠난다는 생각에 울며 몇 번이고 끌어안았고, 마침내 우리가 프린스를 데려와 그 애를 데려가게 했을 때 — 안타깝게도, 피피가 그를 깨물었습니다."], ["Then there was old Mr. Turveydrop downstairs, in a state of deportment not to be expressed, benignly blessing Caddy and giving my guardian to understand that his son’s happiness was his own parental work and that he sacrificed personal considerations to ensure it.", "그러고 나서 아래층에는 이루 말할 수 없는 풍채를 갖춘 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 있었는데, 캐디에게 자애로이 축복을 내리고, 아들의 행복은 다름 아닌 자기가 부모로서 이룬 일이며 그것을 보장하려고 개인적인 사정을 희생했다고 후견인에게 넌지시 알렸습니다."], ["“My dear sir,” said Mr.", "\"이보십시오,\""], ["Turveydrop, “these young people will live with me; my house is large enough for their accommodation, and they shall not want the shelter of my roof.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 젊은이들은 저와 함께 살 겁니다."], ["I could have wished—you will understand the allusion, Mr.", "제 집은 그들을 들이기에 넉넉하니, 제 지붕의 보살핌을 마다하게 하지 않을 겁니다."], ["Jarndyce, for you remember my illustrious patron the Prince Regent—I could have wished that my son had married into a family where there was more deportment, but the will of heaven be done!”", "저는 바랐습니다만 — 빗댄 뜻을 아시겠지요, 잔다이스 씨, 제 고귀한 후원자이신 섭정 왕세자 전하를 기억하실 테니 — 제 아들이 풍채가 더 갖춰진 가문과 혼인하기를 바랐습니다만, 하늘의 뜻대로 되었지요!\""]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "파디글 부부도 한자리에 있었습니다 — 파디글 씨는 고집스러운 인상에 커다란 조끼를 걸치고 뻣뻣한 머리를 한 사내로, 늘 굵직한 저음으로 자기 헌금이며, 파디글 부인의 헌금이며, 다섯 아들의 헌금에 대해 떠들어댔습니다."], ["Pardiggle were of the party—Mr. Pardiggle, an obstinate-looking man with a large waistcoat and stubbly hair, who was always talking in a loud bass voice about his mite, or Mrs.", "퀘일 씨도 와 있었는데, 늘 그렇듯 머리를 뒤로 빗어 넘기고 관자놀이 혹이 번들번들 빛나는 채였습니다."], ["Pardiggle’s mite, or their five boys’ mites. Mr.", "그는 실연당한 연인이 아니라, 그 자리에 함께 온 어느 젊은 — 적어도 미혼인 — 숙녀, 위스크 양에게 받아들여진 사람으로 와 있었습니다."], ["Quale, with his hair brushed back as usual and his knobs of temples shining very much, was also there, not in the character of a disappointed lover, but as the accepted of a young—at least, an unmarried—lady, a Miss Wisk, who was also there. Miss Wisk’s mission, my guardian said, was to show the world that woman’s mission was man’s mission and that the only genuine mission of both man and woman was to be always moving declaratory resolutions about things in general at public meetings.", "위스크 양의 사명은, 후견인 말로는, 여자의 사명이 곧 남자의 사명임을, 그리고 남자와 여자 모두의 유일하게 참된 사명이란 공개 모임에서 온갖 일에 대해 늘 선언적 결의안을 발의하는 것임을 세상에 보여주는 것이었습니다."], ["The guests were few, but were, as one might expect at Mrs. Jellyby’s, all devoted to public objects only.", "손님은 적었지만, 젤리비 부인 댁에서 으레 그러하듯, 하나같이 오로지 공적인 대의에만 헌신하는 이들이었습니다."], ["Besides those I have mentioned, there was an extremely dirty lady with her bonnet all awry and the ticketed price of her dress still sticking on it, whose neglected home, Caddy told me, was like a filthy wilderness, but whose church was like a fancy fair.", "앞서 말한 이들 말고도, 보닛을 온통 비뚜로 쓰고 옷에 가격표가 그대로 달려 있는 무척 지저분한 부인이 있었는데, 캐디 말로는 그 부인이 방치한 집은 더러운 황야 같지만 그 부인의 교회는 자선 바자회 같다고 했습니다."], ["A very contentious gentleman, who said it was his mission to be everybody’s brother but who appeared to be on terms of coolness with the whole of his large family, completed the party.", "자기 사명은 모든 사람의 형제가 되는 것이라면서도 정작 자기 대가족 전체와는 냉랭한 사이로 보이는, 무척 시비조인 신사 한 명이 그 무리를 완성했습니다."]], [["A party, having less in common with such an occasion, could hardly have been got together by any ingenuity.", "이런 행사와 이토록 어울리지 않는 무리를 어떤 재간을 부려도 좀처럼 그러모으기 어려웠을 겁니다."], ["Such a mean mission as the domestic mission was the very last thing to be endured among them; indeed, Miss Wisk informed us, with great indignation, before we sat down to breakfast, that the idea of woman’s mission lying chiefly in the narrow sphere of home was an outrageous slander on the part of her tyrant, man.", "가정의 사명처럼 하찮은 사명은 그들 사이에서 도무지 참아낼 수 없는 것이었습니다. 사실, 위스크 양은 아침 식사 자리에 앉기도 전에 우리에게 잔뜩 분개하며 알려주기를, 여자의 사명이 주로 가정이라는 좁은 영역에 있다는 발상은 폭군인 남자가 저지른 터무니없는 비방이라고 했습니다."], ["One other singularity was that nobody with a mission—except Mr. Quale, whose mission, as I think I have formerly said, was to be in ecstasies with everybody’s mission—cared at all for anybody’s mission.", "또 한 가지 별난 점은, 사명을 지닌 이들 가운데 누구도 — 앞서 말한 것 같은데, 모든 사람의 사명에 황홀해하는 게 사명인 퀘일 씨만 빼고 — 남의 사명에는 도통 관심이 없다는 것이었습니다."], ["Mrs. Pardiggle being as clear that the only one infallible course was her course of pouncing upon the poor and applying benevolence to them like a strait-waistcoat; as Miss Wisk was that the only practical thing for the world was the emancipation of woman from the thraldom of her tyrant, man.", "파디글 부인은 유일하게 그르치지 않는 길이란 가난한 이들을 덮쳐 구속복을 입히듯 자선을 들이붓는 자기 방식뿐이라고 확신했고, 위스크 양은 세상에 유일하게 실질적인 일이란 폭군 남자의 굴레에서 여자를 해방하는 것이라고 확신했습니다."], ["Mrs. Jellyby, all the while, sat smiling at the limited vision that could see anything but Borrioboola-Gha.", "그러는 내내 젤리비 부인은 보리오불라가 말고는 무엇이든 눈에 담을 수 있는 그 좁은 시야를 향해 미소를 지으며 앉아 있었습니다."]], [["But I am anticipating now the purport of our conversation on the ride home instead of first marrying Caddy.", "하지만 지금 나는 캐디부터 결혼시키지 않고 집으로 돌아오는 마차 안에서 나눈 대화의 요지를 앞당겨 말하고 있군요."], ["We all went to church, and Mr. Jellyby gave her away.", "우리는 모두 교회로 갔고, 젤리비 씨가 그 애를 신랑에게 건넸습니다."], ["Of the air with which old Mr. Turveydrop, with his hat under his left arm (the inside presented at the clergyman like a cannon) and his eyes creasing themselves up into his wig, stood stiff and high-shouldered behind us bridesmaids during the ceremony, and afterwards saluted us, I could never say enough to do it justice.", "식이 진행되는 동안 늙은 터비드롭 씨가, 왼팔 아래에 모자를 끼고(그 안쪽이 마치 대포처럼 목사를 향했지요) 두 눈을 가발 속으로 잔뜩 찡그린 채, 빳빳하게 높은 어깨를 하고 우리 들러리들 뒤에 서 있다가 식이 끝난 뒤 우리에게 인사하던 그 태도란, 아무리 말해도 제대로 옮기기 어려웠습니다."], ["Miss Wisk, whom I cannot report as prepossessing in appearance, and whose manner was grim, listened to the proceedings, as part of woman’s wrongs, with a disdainful face. Mrs.", "외모가 호감을 준다고는 도무지 전할 수 없고 태도도 험상궂은 위스크 양은, 그 절차를 여자가 당하는 부당함의 한 대목으로 여기며 경멸 어린 얼굴로 지켜보았습니다."], ["Jellyby, with her calm smile and her bright eyes, looked the least concerned of all the company.", "젤리비 부인은 차분한 미소와 초롱초롱한 눈으로, 그 자리에서 가장 무심한 사람처럼 보였습니다."]], [["We duly came back to breakfast, and Mrs. Jellyby sat at the head of the table and Mr.", "우리는 어김없이 아침 식사를 하러 돌아왔고, 젤리비 부인이 식탁 상석에, 젤리비 씨가 말석에 앉았습니다."], ["Jellyby at the foot. Caddy had previously stolen upstairs to hug the children again and tell them that her name was Turveydrop.", "캐디는 앞서 위층으로 살그머니 올라가 아이들을 다시 한번 끌어안고는 이제 자기 이름이 터비드롭이라고 일러주었습니다."], ["But this piece of information, instead of being an agreeable surprise to Peepy, threw him on his back in such transports of kicking grief that I could do nothing on being sent for but accede to the proposal that he should be admitted to the breakfast table.", "그런데 이 소식은 피피에게 반가운 놀라움이 되기는커녕, 그 애를 발버둥 치는 슬픔의 발작에 빠뜨려 벌렁 자빠뜨렸으니, 불려간 나로서는 그 애를 아침 식탁에 끼워주자는 제안에 응하는 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었습니다."], ["So he came down and sat in my lap; and Mrs. Jellyby, after saying, in reference to the state of his pinafore, “Oh, you naughty Peepy, what a shocking little pig you are!”", "그래서 그 애가 내려와 내 무릎에 앉았고, 젤리비 부인은 그 애 턱받이 꼴을 두고 \"어머, 이 못된 피피야, 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍한 새끼돼지람!\""], ["was not at all discomposed.", "하고는 조금도 마음이 흐트러지지 않았습니다."], ["He was very good except that he brought down Noah with him (out of an ark I had given him before we went to church) and WOULD dip him head first into the wine-glasses and then put him in his mouth.", "그 애는 무척 얌전했는데, 다만 노아를 들고 내려와(교회에 가기 전에 내가 준 방주에서 꺼낸 것이었지요) 기어이 노아를 거꾸로 포도주 잔에 담갔다가 입에 넣곤 했습니다."]], [["My guardian, with his sweet temper and his quick perception and his amiable face, made something agreeable even out of the ungenial company.", "후견인은 그 다정한 성품과 빠른 눈치, 그리고 상냥한 얼굴로, 이 살가울 데 없는 무리에서마저 무언가 흥겨운 것을 빚어냈습니다."], ["None of them seemed able to talk about anything but his, or her, own one subject, and none of them seemed able to talk about even that as part of a world in which there was anything else; but my guardian turned it all to the merry encouragement of Caddy and the honour of the occasion, and brought us through the breakfast nobly.", "그들 가운데 누구도 자기만의 단 하나의 주제 말고는 도무지 이야기할 줄 모르는 듯했고, 더구나 그 주제마저도 다른 무언가가 있는 세상의 일부로서 이야기할 줄은 도무지 모르는 듯했습니다. 하지만 후견인은 그 모든 것을 캐디를 흥겹게 북돋우고 그날의 영예를 기리는 쪽으로 돌려, 우리를 훌륭하게 아침 식사 자리에서 이끌어냈습니다."], ["What we should have done without him, I am afraid to think, for all the company despising the bride and bridegroom and old Mr.", "그가 없었다면 우리가 어떻게 했을지 생각만 해도 두렵습니다."], ["Turveydrop—and old Mr. Thurveydrop, in virtue of his deportment, considering himself vastly superior to all the company—it was a very unpromising case.", "손님 모두가 신랑신부와 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 깔보고 — 게다가 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 자기 풍채에 힘입어 자기가 손님 모두보다 한참 우월하다고 여겼으니 — 여간 막막한 형편이 아니었으니까요."]], [["At last the time came when poor Caddy was to go and when all her property was packed on the hired coach and pair that was to take her and her husband to Gravesend.", "마침내 가엾은 캐디가 떠나야 할 때가 왔고, 그 애와 남편을 그레이브젠드로 데려갈 세낸 마차와 두 마리 말에 그 애 짐이 죄다 실렸습니다."], ["It affected us to see Caddy clinging, then, to her deplorable home and hanging on her mother’s neck with the greatest tenderness.", "그때 캐디가 그 한심한 집에 매달려, 더없는 애틋함으로 어머니 목에 안기는 걸 보니 우리는 마음이 뭉클했습니다."]], [["“I am very sorry I couldn’t go on writing from dictation, Ma,”", "\"받아쓰기를 계속 못 해드려서 정말 죄송해요, 엄마,\" 캐디가 흐느꼈습니다."], ["sobbed Caddy. “I hope you forgive me now.”", "\"이제 저를 용서해주셨으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Oh, Caddy, Caddy!”", "\"아, 캐디, 캐디!\""], ["said Mrs. Jellyby.", "젤리비 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“I have told you over and over again that I have engaged a boy, and there’s an end of it.”", "\"내가 너한테 거듭거듭 말하지 않았니, 사내아이를 들였다고. 그걸로 끝난 일이야.\""]], [["“You are sure you are not the least angry with me, Ma?", "\"엄마, 정말 저한테 조금도 화 안 나신 거 맞죠?"], ["Say you are sure before I go away, Ma?”", "가기 전에 화 안 났다고 말씀해주세요, 엄마?\""]], [["“You foolish Caddy,” returned Mrs.", "\"이 어리석은 캐디야,\""], ["Jellyby, “do I look angry, or have I inclination to be angry, or time to be angry?", "젤리비 부인이 대답했습니다. \"내가 화난 얼굴이니, 화낼 마음이라도 있니, 화낼 짬이라도 있니?"], ["How CAN you?”", "어쩜 그러니?\""]], [["“Take a little care of Pa while I am gone, Mama!”", "\"제가 없는 동안 아빠를 좀 챙겨주세요, 엄마!\""]], [["Mrs. Jellyby positively laughed at the fancy.", "젤리비 부인은 그 발상에 영락없이 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다."], ["“You romantic child,”", "\"이 공상에 빠진 아이야,\""], ["said she, lightly patting Caddy’s back.", "부인이 캐디의 등을 가볍게 토닥이며 말했습니다."], ["“Go along.", "\"어서 가렴."], ["I am excellent friends with you.", "나는 너랑 더없이 좋은 사이란다."], ["Now, good-bye, Caddy, and be very happy!”", "자, 잘 가, 캐디, 부디 행복하렴!\""]], [["Then Caddy hung upon her father and nursed his cheek against hers as if he were some poor dull child in pain.", "그러고 나서 캐디는 아버지에게 매달려, 마치 그가 아픔에 잠긴 가엾고 멍한 아이라도 되는 양 그의 뺨을 제 뺨에 어루만졌습니다."], ["All this took place in the hall.", "이 모든 일이 현관에서 벌어졌습니다."], ["Her father released her, took out his pocket handkerchief, and sat down on the stairs with his head against the wall.", "그 애 아버지는 그 애를 놓아주고, 손수건을 꺼내, 머리를 벽에 기댄 채 계단에 주저앉았습니다."], ["I hope he found some consolation in walls.", "그가 벽에서 얼마간의 위안을 얻었기를 바랍니다."], ["I almost think he did.", "정말 그랬을 것 같기도 합니다."]], [["And then Prince took her arm in his and turned with great emotion and respect to his father, whose deportment at that moment was overwhelming.", "그러고 나서 프린스가 그 애 팔짱을 끼고는, 더없는 감동과 존경을 담아 아버지에게로 돌아섰는데, 그 순간 아버지의 풍채는 압도적이었습니다."]], [["“Thank you over and over again, father!”", "\"거듭거듭 감사드립니다, 아버지!\""], ["said Prince, kissing his hand.", "프린스가 아버지의 손에 입 맞추며 말했습니다."], ["“I am very grateful for all your kindness and consideration regarding our marriage, and so, I can assure you, is Caddy.”", "\"우리 결혼에 베풀어주신 온갖 친절과 배려에 정말 깊이 감사드립니다. 분명 말씀드리지만, 캐디도 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Very,”", "\"정말로요,\""], ["sobbed Caddy.", "캐디가 흐느꼈습니다."], ["“Ve-ry!”", "\"정-말로요!\""]], [["“My dear son,” said Mr.", "\"사랑하는 내 아들아,\""], ["Turveydrop, “and dear daughter, I have done my duty.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"그리고 사랑하는 내 딸아, 나는 내 도리를 다했다."], ["If the spirit of a sainted wooman hovers above us and looks down on the occasion, that, and your constant affection, will be my recompense.", "만일 성스러운 여인의 영혼이 우리 머리 위를 맴돌며 이 자리를 굽어본다면, 그것과 너희의 한결같은 애정이 나의 보답이 되리라."], ["You will not fail in YOUR duty, my son and daughter, I believe?”", "너희도 너희 도리를 저버리지 않으리라 믿는다, 내 아들아, 내 딸아?\""]], [["“Dear father, never!”", "\"사랑하는 아버지, 결코 그러지 않겠습니다!\""], ["cried Prince.", "프린스가 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Never, never, dear Mr. Turveydrop!”", "\"결코, 결코 그러지 않겠어요, 사랑하는 터비드롭 씨!\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."]], [["“This,” returned Mr.", "\"그래야 마땅하지,\""], ["Turveydrop, “is as it should be.", "터비드롭 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["My children, my home is yours, my heart is yours, my all is yours.", "\"내 아이들아, 내 집은 너희 것이고, 내 마음도 너희 것이며, 내 모든 것이 너희 것이다."], ["I will never leave you; nothing but death shall part us.", "나는 결코 너희를 떠나지 않으리라. 오직 죽음만이 우리를 갈라놓으리라."], ["My dear son, you contemplate an absence of a week, I think?”", "사랑하는 내 아들아, 일주일쯤 자리를 비울 작정인 게지?\""]], [["“A week, dear father.", "\"일주일이요, 사랑하는 아버지."], ["We shall return home this day week.”", "오늘로부터 일주일째 되는 날 집으로 돌아오겠습니다.\""]], [["“My dear child,” said Mr.", "\"사랑하는 내 아이야,\""], ["Turveydrop, “let me, even under the present exceptional circumstances, recommend strict punctuality.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다. \"지금 이 예외적인 상황에서라도, 엄정하게 시간을 지키기를 당부하마."], ["It is highly important to keep the connexion together; and schools, if at all neglected, are apt to take offence.”", "거래처를 한데 묶어두는 게 무척 중요하니라. 학원이란 조금이라도 소홀히 하면 토라지기 십상이거든.\""]], [["“This day week, father, we shall be sure to be home to dinner.”", "\"오늘로부터 일주일째 되는 날, 아버지, 저희는 꼭 저녁 식사에 맞춰 집에 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said Mr. Turveydrop.", "터비드롭 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“You will find fires, my dear Caroline, in your own room, and dinner prepared in my apartment.", "\"사랑하는 캐럴라인, 네 방에는 난롯불이 피워져 있을 것이고, 저녁 식사는 내 거처에 차려져 있을 게다."], ["Yes, yes, Prince!”", "그래, 그래, 프린스!\""], ["anticipating some self-denying objection on his son’s part with a great air.", "무언가 사양하는 반론을 펴려는 아들을 거창한 기색으로 앞질러 막으며 말했습니다."], ["“You and our Caroline will be strange in the upper part of the premises and will, therefore, dine that day in my apartment.", "\"너와 우리 캐럴라인은 위층 살림이 낯설 테니, 그날은 내 거처에서 저녁을 들도록 해라."], ["Now, bless ye!”", "자, 너희에게 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["They drove away, and whether I wondered most at Mrs. Jellyby or at Mr.", "그들은 마차를 타고 떠났고, 내가 젤리비 부인에게 더 놀랐는지 터비드롭 씨에게 더 놀랐는지 나로서는 알 수 없었습니다."], ["Turveydrop, I did not know. Ada and my guardian were in the same condition when we came to talk it over.", "우리가 그 일을 두고 이야기를 나눌 때 에이다와 후견인도 같은 처지였습니다."], ["But before we drove away too, I received a most unexpected and eloquent compliment from Mr. Jellyby.", "하지만 우리도 마차를 타고 떠나기 전에, 나는 젤리비 씨에게서 더없이 뜻밖이면서도 절절한 찬사를 받았습니다."], ["He came up to me in the hall, took both my hands, pressed them earnestly, and opened his mouth twice.", "그는 현관에서 나에게 다가와 내 두 손을 잡고는, 간절하게 꼭 누르며, 입을 두 번 벌렸습니다."], ["I was so sure of his meaning that I said, quite flurried, “You are very welcome, sir.", "나는 그분의 뜻을 어찌나 확신했던지, 무척 허둥대며 이렇게 말했습니다. \"천만에요, 선생님."], ["Pray don’t mention it!”", "부디 마음 쓰지 마세요!\""]], [["“I hope this marriage is for the best, guardian,”", "\"이 결혼이 잘되는 거였으면 좋겠어요, 후견인,\""], ["said I when we three were on our road home.", "우리 셋이 집으로 가는 길에 내가 말했습니다."]], [["“I hope it is, little woman.", "\"그러길 바라야지, 꼬마 아가씨."], ["Patience.", "인내심을 갖자꾸나."], ["We shall see.”", "두고 보면 알겠지.\""]], [["“Is the wind in the east to-day?”", "\"오늘은 바람이 동쪽에서 부나요?\""], ["I ventured to ask him.", "나는 외람되이 그에게 물어보았습니다."]], [["He laughed heartily and answered, “No.”", "그는 호탕하게 웃으며 대답했습니다. \"아니.\""]], [["“But it must have been this morning, I think,”", "\"하지만 오늘 아침엔 분명 그랬던 것 같아요,\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["He answered “No” again, and this time my dear girl confidently answered “No”", "그는 다시 \"아니\"라고 대답했고, 이번에는 내 사랑하는 아이도 자신만만하게 \"아니\"라고 대답하며, 금빛 머리카락에 활짝 핀 꽃을 단, 영락없는 봄날 같은 그 어여쁜 머리를 흔들었습니다."], ["too and shook the lovely head which, with its blooming flowers against the golden hair, was like the very spring.", "\"동풍에 대해 참 많이도 아는구나, 못난 내 귀염둥이야,\""], ["“Much YOU know of east winds, my ugly darling,” said I, kissing her in my admiration—I couldn’t help it.", "나는 탄복한 나머지 그 애에게 입을 맞추며 말했습니다 — 도무지 그러지 않을 수 없었으니까요."]], [["Well!", "그래요!"], ["It was only their love for me, I know very well, and it is a long time ago.", "그건 그저 나를 향한 그들의 사랑이었을 뿐임을 나는 잘 알며, 한참 전 일입니다."], ["I must write it even if I rub it out again, because it gives me so much pleasure.", "다시 지워버릴지언정 나는 이것을 적어두어야겠습니다. 적어두면 나에게 큰 기쁨이 되니까요."], ["They said there could be no east wind where Somebody was; they said that wherever Dame Durden went, there was sunshine and summer air.", "그들은 누군가가 있는 곳에는 동풍이 불 리 없다고 했습니다. 더든 아주머니가 가는 곳에는 어디든 햇살과 여름 공기가 있다고 했습니다."]], [["Nurse and Patient\nI had not been at home again many days when one evening I went upstairs into my own room to take a peep over Charley’s shoulder and see how she was getting on with her copy-book.", "집에 다시 돌아온 지 며칠 지나지 않은 어느 날 저녁, 나는 위층 내 방으로 올라가 찰리의 어깨너머로 글씨 연습장을 들여다보며 얼마나 늘었는지 살펴보았습니다."], ["Writing was a trying business to Charley, who seemed to have no natural power over a pen, but in whose hand every pen appeared to become perversely animated, and to go wrong and crooked, and to stop, and splash, and sidle into corners like a saddle-donkey.", "찰리에게 글씨 쓰기는 여간 힘겨운 일이 아니었습니다. 펜을 다루는 타고난 재주가 도무지 없는 듯해서, 찰리 손에 들리기만 하면 펜이라는 펜은 죄다 심술궂게 살아나 비뚤배뚤 엇나가고, 멈췄다가 잉크를 튀기고, 안장 얹은 당나귀처럼 모퉁이로 슬금슬금 비켜갔습니다."], ["It was very odd to see what old letters Charley’s young hand had made, they so wrinkled, and shrivelled, and tottering, it so plump and round.", "찰리의 어린 손이 어찌나 늙은 글자를 만들어내는지 보고 있으면 참 이상했습니다. 글자는 주름지고 쪼그라들고 비틀거리는데, 손은 통통하고 동그스름했으니 말입니다."], ["Yet Charley was uncommonly expert at other things and had as nimble little fingers as I ever watched.", "그래도 찰리는 다른 일에는 유난히 재주가 빼어났고, 내가 본 어느 누구보다 손놀림이 야무지고 잽쌌습니다."]], [["“Well, Charley,”", "\"자, 찰리.\""], ["said I, looking over a copy of the letter O in which it was represented as square, triangular, pear-shaped, and collapsed in all kinds of ways, “we are improving.", "나는 네모나기도 하고 세모나기도 하고 배 모양으로 늘어지기도 하고 갖가지로 찌그러진 'O' 자가 늘어선 연습장을 들여다보며 말했습니다."], ["If we only get to make it round, we shall be perfect, Charley.”", "\"점점 나아지는구나. 이걸 동그랗게만 쓸 수 있으면 완벽해지겠다, 찰리.\""]], [["Then I made one, and Charley made one, and the pen wouldn’t join Charley’s neatly, but twisted it up into a knot.", "그러고서 내가 하나 써 보이고 찰리도 하나 써 보았는데, 펜은 찰리의 글자를 말끔하게 잇지 못하고 매듭처럼 배배 꼬아놓고 말았습니다."]], [["“Never mind, Charley.", "\"괜찮아, 찰리."], ["We shall do it in time.”", "시간이 지나면 되게 마련이야.\""]], [["Charley laid down her pen, the copy being finished, opened and shut her cramped little hand, looked gravely at the page, half in pride and half in doubt, and got up, and dropped me a curtsy.", "연습이 끝나자 찰리는 펜을 내려놓고, 뻣뻣하게 굳은 작은 손을 폈다 쥐었다 하더니, 절반은 자랑스럽고 절반은 미덥지 못한 표정으로 연습장을 진지하게 들여다본 뒤, 일어나서 나에게 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 인사했습니다."]], [["“Thank you, miss.", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨."], ["If you please, miss, did you know a poor person of the name of Jenny?”", "그런데 아가씨, 혹시 제니라는 가엾은 여자분을 아세요?\""]], [["“A brickmaker’s wife, Charley?", "\"벽돌공 아내 말이니, 찰리?"], ["Yes.”", "그래, 알지.\""]], [["“She came and spoke to me when I was out a little while ago, and said you knew her, miss.", "\"조금 전에 제가 바깥에 나갔을 때 와서 말을 걸었어요. 아가씨가 자기를 안다고 하던데요."], ["She asked me if I wasn’t the young lady’s little maid—meaning you for the young lady, miss—and I said yes, miss.”", "제가 그 아가씨네 어린 하녀가 아니냐고 묻기에—그 아가씨가 바로 아가씨를 두고 한 말이에요, 아가씨—제가 그렇다고 했어요.\""]], [["“I thought she had left this neighbourhood altogether, Charley.”", "\"난 그 여자가 이 동네를 아예 떠난 줄 알았는데, 찰리.\""]], [["“So she had, miss, but she’s come back again to where she used to live—she and Liz.", "\"그랬대요, 아가씨. 그런데 예전에 살던 데로 다시 돌아왔대요—리즈하고 같이요."], ["Did you know another poor person of the name of Liz, miss?”", "아가씨, 혹시 리즈라는 다른 가엾은 여자분도 아세요?\""]], [["“I think I do, Charley, though not by name.”", "\"알 것 같구나, 찰리. 이름은 몰라도 말이야.\""]], [["“That’s what she said!”", "\"그 여자분도 바로 그렇게 말했어요!\""], ["returned Charley.", "찰리가 받았습니다."], ["“They have both come back, miss, and have been tramping high and low.”", "\"둘 다 돌아왔는데, 아가씨, 여기저기 정처 없이 떠돌아다녔대요.\""]], [["“Tramping high and low, have they, Charley?”", "\"여기저기 떠돌아다녔다고, 찰리?\""]], [["“Yes, miss.”", "\"네, 아가씨.\""], ["If Charley could only have made the letters in her copy as round as the eyes with which she looked into my face, they would have been excellent.", "찰리가 내 얼굴을 들여다보는 그 동그란 눈만큼 연습장 글자를 동그랗게 쓸 수 있었다면 더없이 훌륭했을 것입니다."], ["“And this poor person came about the house three or four days, hoping to get a glimpse of you, miss—all she wanted, she said—but you were away.", "\"그 가엾은 여자분이 아가씨를 한 번이라도 뵙고 싶어서 사나흘 동안 집 근처를 서성였대요—그것만 바랐다고요—그런데 아가씨가 안 계셨던 거예요."], ["That was when she saw me.", "그때 저를 본 거고요."], ["She saw me a-going about, miss,”", "제가 돌아다니는 걸 봤대요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Charley with a short laugh of the greatest delight and pride, “and she thought I looked like your maid!”", "찰리가 더없이 기쁘고 자랑스러운 듯 짧게 웃으며 말했습니다. \"그러고는 제가 아가씨 하녀처럼 보인다고 생각했대요!\""]], [["“Did she though, really, Charley?”", "\"정말 그랬다니, 찰리?\""]], [["“Yes, miss!”", "\"네, 아가씨!\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했습니다."], ["“Really and truly.”", "\"정말로, 진짜로요.\""], ["And Charley, with another short laugh of the purest glee, made her eyes very round again and looked as serious as became my maid.", "그러고서 찰리는 더없이 순수한 환희로 다시 짧게 웃고는, 눈을 또 동그랗게 뜨고 내 하녀답게 자못 진지한 표정을 지었습니다."], ["I was never tired of seeing Charley in the full enjoyment of that great dignity, standing before me with her youthful face and figure, and her steady manner, and her childish exultation breaking through it now and then in the pleasantest way.", "나는 찰리가 그 대단한 위엄을 한껏 누리며 내 앞에 서 있는 모습을, 그 앳된 얼굴과 자태와 의젓한 몸가짐, 그리고 그 사이로 이따금 더없이 사랑스럽게 터져 나오는 어린아이 같은 환희를 보고 또 보아도 도무지 싫증이 나지 않았습니다."]], [["“And where did you see her, Charley?”", "\"그래서 그 여자를 어디서 봤니, 찰리?\""], ["said I.", "나는 물었습니다."]], [["My little maid’s countenance fell as she replied, “By the doctor’s shop, miss.”", "내 작은 하녀의 얼굴빛이 어두워지며 대답했습니다. \"약방 앞에서요, 아가씨.\""], ["For Charley wore her black frock yet.", "찰리는 아직 검은 옷을 입고 있었으니까요."]], [["I asked if the brickmaker’s wife were ill, but Charley said no.", "나는 벽돌공 아내가 아픈 거냐고 물었지만, 찰리는 아니라고 했습니다."], ["It was some one else.", "다른 사람이라는 거였습니다."], ["Some one in her cottage who had tramped down to Saint Albans and was tramping he didn’t know where.", "그 오두막에 머무는 누군가가 세인트올번스까지 걸어 내려왔는데, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 떠돌고 있다고 했습니다."], ["A poor boy, Charley said.", "가엾은 사내아이라고, 찰리가 말했습니다."], ["No father, no mother, no any one.", "아버지도 어머니도, 아무도 없다고요."], ["“Like as Tom might have been, miss, if Emma and me had died after father,”", "\"톰도, 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤에 엠마하고 제가 죽었더라면 저렇게 됐을지도 몰라요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Charley, her round eyes filling with tears.", "찰리가 말하는데 동그란 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐습니다."]], [["“And she was getting medicine for him, Charley?”", "\"그래서 그 아이한테 줄 약을 사고 있었니, 찰리?\""]], [["“She said, miss,”", "\"그 아이가 예전에 자기한테 그만큼 해준 적이 있다고 했어요, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Charley, “how that he had once done as much for her.”", "찰리가 대답했습니다."]], [["My little maid’s face was so eager and her quiet hands were folded so closely in one another as she stood looking at me that I had no great difficulty in reading her thoughts.", "내 작은 하녀의 얼굴은 어찌나 간절한지, 또 나를 바라보며 서 있는 그 얌전한 두 손을 어찌나 꼭 맞잡고 있는지, 나는 그 마음을 읽어내기가 그리 어렵지 않았습니다."], ["“Well, Charley,”", "\"그래, 찰리.\""], ["said I, “it appears to me that you and I can do no better than go round to Jenny’s and see what’s the matter.”", "나는 말했습니다. \"내가 보기엔 너와 내가 제니네 집에 가서 무슨 일인지 알아보는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없겠구나.\""]], [["The alacrity with which Charley brought my bonnet and veil, and having dressed me, quaintly pinned herself into her warm shawl and made herself look like a little old woman, sufficiently expressed her readiness.", "찰리가 내 모자와 베일을 가져와 나에게 씌워주고는, 자기도 따뜻한 숄을 옛스럽게 핀으로 여며 작은 노파처럼 차려입는 그 날렵함만으로도 채비가 다 됐음을 충분히 알 수 있었습니다."], ["So Charley and I, without saying anything to any one, went out.", "그렇게 찰리와 나는 아무에게도 말하지 않고 집을 나섰습니다."]], [["It was a cold, wild night, and the trees shuddered in the wind.", "춥고 사나운 밤이었고, 나무들이 바람에 부르르 떨었습니다."], ["The rain had been thick and heavy all day, and with little intermission for many days.", "비가 하루 종일 굵고 세차게 내렸고, 여러 날 동안 거의 그치지 않고 쏟아졌습니다."], ["None was falling just then, however.", "그래도 그 순간만큼은 비가 내리지 않았습니다."], ["The sky had partly cleared, but was very gloomy—even above us, where a few stars were shining.", "하늘은 어느 정도 개었지만 무척 음울했습니다—몇 개의 별이 반짝이는 머리 위쪽조차 그러했습니다."], ["In the north and north-west, where the sun had set three hours before, there was a pale dead light both beautiful and awful; and into it long sullen lines of cloud waved up like a sea stricken immovable as it was heaving.", "세 시간 전에 해가 진 북쪽과 북서쪽에는 아름다우면서도 무시무시한 창백한 죽은 빛이 깔려 있었고, 그 속으로 길게 뻗은 음울한 구름 줄기가 일렁이다 그대로 굳어버린 바다처럼 너울거리며 솟구쳐 올랐습니다."], ["Towards London a lurid glare overhung the whole dark waste, and the contrast between these two lights, and the fancy which the redder light engendered of an unearthly fire, gleaming on all the unseen buildings of the city and on all the faces of its many thousands of wondering inhabitants, was as solemn as might be.", "런던 쪽으로는 시뻘건 불빛이 그 어두운 황야 전체를 뒤덮었는데, 이 두 빛이 빚어내는 대조와, 그 붉은 빛이 불러일으키는 환상—이 세상 것이 아닌 불길이 도시의 보이지 않는 온갖 건물과 경탄에 잠긴 수천수만 주민의 얼굴 위에서 번득이는 듯한 환상은—이루 말할 수 없이 장엄했습니다."]], [["I had no thought that night—none, I am quite sure—of what was soon to happen to me.", "그날 밤 나에게는 곧 닥쳐올 일에 대한 어떤 예감도 없었습니다—전혀, 정말 단언하건대 없었습니다."], ["But I have always remembered since that when we had stopped at the garden-gate to look up at the sky, and when we went upon our way, I had for a moment an undefinable impression of myself as being something different from what I then was.", "그러나 그 뒤로 늘 기억합니다. 정원 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 하늘을 올려다보았을 때, 그리고 다시 길을 나섰을 때, 나는 한순간 내가 그때의 나와는 무언가 다른 존재라는, 뭐라 규정할 수 없는 느낌에 사로잡혔습니다."], ["I know it was then and there that I had it.", "바로 그때 그 자리에서 그 느낌을 받았다는 것을 압니다."], ["I have ever since connected the feeling with that spot and time and with everything associated with that spot and time, to the distant voices in the town, the barking of a dog, and the sound of wheels coming down the miry hill.", "그 뒤로 나는 그 느낌을 그 장소와 그 시간에, 그리고 그 장소와 그 시간에 얽힌 모든 것—마을에서 들려오던 아득한 목소리들, 개 짖는 소리, 진창 언덕을 내려오던 수레바퀴 소리—에 늘 결부시키게 되었습니다."]], [["It was Saturday night, and most of the people belonging to the place where we were going were drinking elsewhere.", "토요일 밤이었고, 우리가 가려는 곳에 사는 사람들은 대부분 다른 데서 술을 마시고 있었습니다."], ["We found it quieter than I had previously seen it, though quite as miserable.", "그곳은 전에 봤을 때보다 조용했지만, 여전히 참담하기는 마찬가지였습니다."], ["The kilns were burning, and a stifling vapour set towards us with a pale-blue glare.", "가마들이 불타오르며 숨 막히는 수증기가 창백한 푸른빛을 띠고 우리 쪽으로 밀려왔습니다."]], [["We came to the cottage, where there was a feeble candle in the patched window.", "우리는 그 오두막에 이르렀습니다. 누덕누덕 기운 창문에 희미한 촛불 하나가 켜져 있었습니다."], ["We tapped at the door and went in.", "우리는 문을 두드리고 안으로 들어갔습니다."], ["The mother of the little child who had died was sitting in a chair on one side of the poor fire by the bed; and opposite to her, a wretched boy, supported by the chimney-piece, was cowering on the floor.", "죽은 어린아이의 어머니가 침대 옆 변변찮은 난롯가 한쪽에 의자를 놓고 앉아 있었고, 그 맞은편에는 비참한 행색의 사내아이 하나가 벽난로 선반에 몸을 기댄 채 바닥에 웅크리고 있었습니다."], ["He held under his arm, like a little bundle, a fragment of a fur cap; and as he tried to warm himself, he shook until the crazy door and window shook.", "아이는 작은 보따리라도 되는 양 모피 모자 조각 하나를 팔 밑에 끼고 있었습니다. 몸을 녹이려 애쓰면서도 어찌나 떨어대는지 다 망가진 문과 창문까지 덜컹덜컹 흔들렸습니다."], ["The place was closer than before and had an unhealthy and a very peculiar smell.", "그곳은 전보다 더 후텁지근했고, 건강에 해로운 아주 묘한 냄새가 났습니다."]], [["I had not lifted my veil when I first spoke to the woman, which was at the moment of our going in.", "처음 그 여자에게 말을 걸었을 때, 그러니까 우리가 막 들어선 그 순간에, 나는 아직 베일을 걷지 않았습니다."], ["The boy staggered up instantly and stared at me with a remarkable expression of surprise and terror.", "사내아이는 곧장 비틀거리며 일어나더니, 놀라움과 공포가 뒤섞인 야릇한 표정으로 나를 빤히 쳐다보았습니다."]], [["His action was so quick and my being the cause of it was so evident that I stood still instead of advancing nearer.", "아이의 동작이 어찌나 빠르고, 또 내가 그 원인이라는 게 어찌나 분명했던지, 나는 가까이 다가가는 대신 그 자리에 가만히 멈춰 섰습니다."]], [["“I won’t go no more to the berryin ground,”", "\"무덤 구덩이엔 다신 안 가.\""], ["muttered the boy; “I ain’t a-going there, so I tell you!”", "아이가 중얼거렸습니다. \"거긴 안 갈 거야, 그러니까 말해두는데!\""]], [["I lifted my veil and spoke to the woman.", "나는 베일을 걷고 여자에게 말을 건넸습니다."], ["She said to me in a low voice, “Don’t mind him, ma’am.", "여자는 낮은 목소리로 나에게 말했습니다. \"신경 쓰지 마세요, 마님."], ["He’ll soon come back to his head,”", "곧 제정신이 돌아올 거예요.\""], ["and said to him, “Jo, Jo, what’s the matter?”", "그러고는 아이에게 말했습니다. \"조, 조, 왜 그래?\""]], [["“I know wot she’s come for!”", "\"저 여자가 뭐 하러 왔는지 다 안다고!\""], ["cried the boy.", "아이가 소리쳤습니다."]], [["“Who?”", "\"누가?\""]], [["“The lady there.", "\"저기 저 마님 말이야."], ["She’s come to get me to go along with her to the berryin ground.", "나를 데리고 무덤 구덩이로 가려고 온 거야."], ["I won’t go to the berryin ground.", "난 무덤 구덩이엔 안 가."], ["I don’t like the name on it.", "그 이름부터가 싫어."], ["She might go a-berryin ME.”", "나까지 파묻어버릴지도 몰라.\""], ["His shivering came on again, and as he leaned against the wall, he shook the hovel.", "다시 오한이 도지자, 아이는 벽에 기댄 채 그 누추한 오두막을 흔들어댔습니다."]], [["“He has been talking off and on about such like all day, ma’am,”", "\"하루 종일 저런 소리를 띄엄띄엄 해댔어요, 마님.\""], ["said Jenny softly.", "제니가 부드럽게 말했습니다."], ["“Why, how you stare!", "\"아니, 왜 그렇게 빤히 봐!"], ["This is MY lady, Jo.”", "이분이 우리 마님이야, 조.\""]], [["“Is it?”", "\"그래?\""], ["returned the boy doubtfully, and surveying me with his arm held out above his burning eyes.", "아이가 미심쩍은 듯 받으며, 이글거리는 두 눈 위로 팔을 들어 가리고 나를 살폈습니다."], ["“She looks to me the t’other one.", "\"내 눈엔 딴 사람으로 보이는데."], ["It ain’t the bonnet, nor yet it ain’t the gownd, but she looks to me the t’other one.”", "모자도 아니고 옷도 아닌데, 내 눈엔 딴 사람으로 보여.\""]], [["My little Charley, with her premature experience of illness and trouble, had pulled off her bonnet and shawl and now went quietly up to him with a chair and sat him down in it like an old sick nurse.", "병과 고생을 일찍부터 겪어온 나의 어린 찰리는 모자와 숄을 벗어놓고, 이제 의자 하나를 들고 조용히 아이에게 다가가, 늙은 간병인처럼 아이를 의자에 앉혔습니다."], ["Except that no such attendant could have shown him Charley’s youthful face, which seemed to engage his confidence.", "다만 그런 간병인이라면 아이에게 보여줄 수 없었을 찰리의 앳된 얼굴이, 아이의 마음을 사로잡는 듯했습니다."]], [["“I say!”", "\"저기!\""], ["said the boy.", "아이가 말했습니다."], ["“YOU tell me.", "\"네가 말해봐."], ["Ain’t the lady the t’other lady?”", "저 마님이 딴 마님 아니야?\""]], [["Charley shook her head as she methodically drew his rags about him and made him as warm as she could.", "찰리는 고개를 가로저으며 아이의 누더기를 차근차근 여며주고 할 수 있는 한 따뜻하게 해주었습니다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["the boy muttered.", "아이가 중얼거렸습니다."], ["“Then I s’pose she ain’t.”", "\"그럼 딴 사람이 아닌가 보네.\""]], [["“I came to see if I could do you any good,” said I.", "\"네가 좀 나아지게 도울 수 있을까 해서 왔단다.\""], ["“What is the matter with you?”", "나는 말했습니다. \"어디가 아프니?\""]], [["“I’m a-being froze,”", "\"꽁꽁 얼었다가.\""], ["returned the boy hoarsely, with his haggard gaze wandering about me, “and then burnt up, and then froze, and then burnt up, ever so many times in a hour.", "아이가 쉰 목소리로 받으며, 초췌한 눈길을 내 주위로 헤매게 했습니다. \"그다음엔 활활 타고, 또 얼었다가 또 타고, 한 시간에도 몇 번씩 그래."], ["And my head’s all sleepy, and all a-going mad-like—and I’m so dry—and my bones isn’t half so much bones as pain.”", "머리는 온통 졸리고, 미쳐 돌아가는 것 같고—목은 바싹바싹 마르고—뼈마디는 뼈라기보다 통증 덩어리야.\""]], [["“When did he come here?”", "\"이 아이가 언제 여기 왔지요?\""], ["I asked the woman.", "나는 여자에게 물었습니다."]], [["“This morning, ma’am, I found him at the corner of the town.", "\"오늘 아침에 마을 모퉁이에서 발견했어요, 마님."], ["I had known him up in London yonder.", "저 위 런던에서 알던 아이거든요."], ["Hadn’t I, Jo?”", "그렇지, 조?\""]], [["“Tom-all-Alone’s,”", "\"톰 혼자 사는 곳.\""], ["the boy replied.", "아이가 대답했습니다."]], [["Whenever he fixed his attention or his eyes, it was only for a very little while.", "아이는 주의를 모으거나 눈을 한곳에 두어도, 그건 아주 잠깐뿐이었습니다."], ["He soon began to droop his head again, and roll it heavily, and speak as if he were half awake.", "곧 다시 고개를 떨구고는 무겁게 가누다가, 반쯤 잠든 사람처럼 말하기 시작했습니다."]], [["“When did he come from London?”", "\"이 아이가 런던에서 언제 왔지요?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었습니다."]], [["“I come from London yes’day,”", "\"난 어제 런던에서 왔어.\""], ["said the boy himself, now flushed and hot.", "이제 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 아이가 직접 말했습니다."], ["“I’m a-going somewheres.”", "\"어디론가 가는 길이야.\""]], [["“Where is he going?”", "\"이 아이가 어디로 가는데요?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었습니다."]], [["“Somewheres,”", "\"어디론가.\""], ["repeated the boy in a louder tone.", "아이가 더 큰 소리로 되풀이했습니다."], ["“I have been moved on, and moved on, more nor ever I was afore, since the t’other one give me the sov’ring.", "\"그 딴 사람이 나한테 금화를 준 뒤로, 전보다 더 쫓겨 다니고 또 쫓겨 다녔어."], ["Mrs. Snagsby, she’s always a-watching, and a-driving of me—what have I done to her?—and they’re all a-watching and a-driving of me.", "스내그스비 부인은 늘 나를 감시하면서 쫓아내—내가 그 여자한테 뭘 어쨌다고?—다들 나를 감시하고 쫓아내."], ["Every one of ’em’s doing of it, from the time when I don’t get up, to the time when I don’t go to bed.", "하나같이 그래, 내가 일어나지 못하는 때부터 잠자리에 들지 못하는 때까지."], ["And I’m a-going somewheres.", "그래서 난 어디론가 가는 거야."], ["That’s where I’m a-going.", "그게 내가 가는 데야."], ["She told me, down in Tom-all-Alone’s, as she came from Stolbuns, and so I took the Stolbuns Road.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 그 사람이 나한테 그랬어, 스톨번스에서 왔다고. 그래서 나도 스톨번스 길로 잡았어."], ["It’s as good as another.”", "다른 데나 매한가지니까.\""]], [["He always concluded by addressing Charley.", "아이는 늘 마지막엔 찰리에게 말을 건넸습니다."]], [["“What is to be done with him?”", "\"이 아이를 어떻게 하면 좋지?\""], ["said I, taking the woman aside.", "나는 여자를 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했습니다."], ["“He could not travel in this state even if he had a purpose and knew where he was going!”", "\"설령 목적지가 있고 어디로 가는지 안다 해도 이런 상태로는 길을 갈 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“I know no more, ma’am, than the dead,”", "\"저도 도무지 모르겠어요, 마님. 죽은 사람이나 다름없죠.\""], ["she replied, glancing compassionately at him.", "여자가 가엾다는 듯 아이를 흘끔 보며 대답했습니다. \"차라리 죽은 사람들이 더 잘 알지도 모르죠, 말만 해줄 수 있다면요."], ["“Perhaps the dead know better, if they could only tell us.", "가엾어서 하루 종일 여기 데리고 있으면서 죽도 먹이고 약도 줬어요. 리즈는 누가 받아줄지 알아보러 나갔고요(여기 이 침대에 누운 내 예쁜이는—저이 아이지만 내 아이라고 부른답니다)."], ["I’ve kept him here all day for pity’s sake, and I’ve given him broth and physic, and Liz has gone to try if any one will take him in (here’s my pretty in the bed—her child, but I call it mine); but I can’t keep him long, for if my husband was to come home and find him here, he’d be rough in putting him out and might do him a hurt.", "그래도 오래는 못 데리고 있어요. 우리 남편이 집에 와서 이 애가 있는 걸 보면 거칠게 내쫓을 테고, 그러다 다치게 할지도 모르거든요."], ["Hark!", "들어보세요!"], ["Here comes Liz back!”", "리즈가 돌아오네요!\""]], [["The other woman came hurriedly in as she spoke, and the boy got up with a half-obscured sense that he was expected to be going.", "여자가 말하는 사이 다른 여자가 황급히 들어왔고, 이제 가야 하나 보다 하는 어렴풋한 느낌으로 아이가 일어섰습니다."], ["When the little child awoke, and when and how Charley got at it, took it out of bed, and began to walk about hushing it, I don’t know.", "어린아이가 언제 깨어났는지, 또 찰리가 언제 어떻게 다가가 아이를 침대에서 안아 올려 어르면서 거닐기 시작했는지, 나는 모릅니다."], ["There she was, doing all this in a quiet motherly manner as if she were living in Mrs.", "어느새 찰리는 그 모든 일을 조용하고 자애로운 손길로 해내고 있었습니다."], ["Blinder’s attic with Tom and Emma again.", "마치 블라인더 부인네 다락방에서 톰과 엠마를 다시 돌보고 있기라도 한 듯이요."]], [["The friend had been here and there, and had been played about from hand to hand, and had come back as she went.", "그 친구는 이리저리 돌아다니며 이 손 저 손으로 떠밀리다, 떠날 때와 똑같은 빈손으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["At first it was too early for the boy to be received into the proper refuge, and at last it was too late.", "처음엔 너무 일러서 아이를 제대로 된 보호소에 받아줄 수 없었고, 나중엔 너무 늦었던 것입니다."], ["One official sent her to another, and the other sent her back again to the first, and so backward and forward, until it appeared to me as if both must have been appointed for their skill in evading their duties instead of performing them.", "한 관리가 그 여자를 다른 관리에게 보내고, 그 관리가 다시 처음 관리에게 돌려보내고, 그렇게 앞으로 뒤로 떠밀려 다닌 끝에, 나에게는 두 관리 모두 직무를 수행하라고가 아니라 회피하는 솜씨가 뛰어나다고 임명받은 게 아닌가 싶을 지경이었습니다."], ["And now, after all, she said, breathing quickly, for she had been running and was frightened too, “Jenny, your master’s on the road home, and mine’s not far behind, and the Lord help the boy, for we can do no more for him!”", "그러고도 결국, 여자는 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했습니다—내내 뛰어다닌 데다 겁까지 먹은 탓이었습니다—\"제니, 자네 남편이 집에 오는 길이고, 우리 집 양반도 멀지 않았어. 하느님이 이 애를 도우시길, 우린 더는 해줄 게 없으니!\""], ["They put a few halfpence together and hurried them into his hand, and so, in an oblivious, half-thankful, half-insensible way, he shuffled out of the house.", "두 사람은 반 페니짜리 동전 몇 닢을 모아 아이 손에 황급히 쥐여주었고, 그렇게 아이는 멍하니, 절반은 고마워하고 절반은 아무 감각도 없는 채로, 발을 질질 끌며 집을 나섰습니다."]], [["“Give me the child, my dear,”", "\"아이를 이리 줘요, 새댁.\""], ["said its mother to Charley, “and thank you kindly too!", "어린아이의 어머니가 찰리에게 말했습니다. \"고마워요, 정말로!"], ["Jenny, woman dear, good night!", "제니, 잘 자요, 우리 어진 아주머니!"], ["Young lady, if my master don’t fall out with me, I’ll look down by the kiln by and by, where the boy will be most like, and again in the morning!”", "아가씨, 우리 집 양반이랑 틀어지지만 않으면, 조금 있다 가마 쪽으로 내려가 볼게요. 그 애가 거기 있을 가능성이 제일 크니까요."], ["She hurried off, and presently we passed her hushing and singing to her child at her own door and looking anxiously along the road for her drunken husband.", "아침에 또 가보고요!\" 여자는 황급히 떠났고, 이내 우리는 그 여자가 제 집 문간에서 아이를 어르고 노래를 불러주며, 술 취한 남편이 오는지 길을 따라 애타게 살피는 곁을 지나쳤습니다."]], [["I was afraid of staying then to speak to either woman, lest I should bring her into trouble.", "그때 나는 두 여자 중 누구와도 말을 나누려고 머물렀다가는 곤경에 빠뜨릴까 봐 두려웠습니다."], ["But I said to Charley that we must not leave the boy to die.", "하지만 나는 찰리에게, 그 아이를 그냥 죽게 내버려둘 수는 없다고 말했습니다."], ["Charley, who knew what to do much better than I did, and whose quickness equalled her presence of mind, glided on before me, and presently we came up with Jo, just short of the brick-kiln.", "나보다 무엇을 해야 할지 훨씬 잘 알고, 침착함 못지않게 행동도 잰 찰리가 내 앞으로 미끄러지듯 나아갔고, 우리는 이내 벽돌 가마 바로 못 미친 곳에서 조를 따라잡았습니다."]], [["I think he must have begun his journey with some small bundle under his arm and must have had it stolen or lost it.", "생각건대 아이는 작은 보따리를 팔 밑에 끼고 길을 나섰다가 그걸 도둑맞았거나 잃어버렸던 모양입니다."], ["For he still carried his wretched fragment of fur cap like a bundle, though he went bare-headed through the rain, which now fell fast.", "이제 빠르게 쏟아지는 빗속을 맨머리로 걸으면서도, 그 비참한 모피 모자 조각을 여전히 보따리처럼 들고 있었으니까요."], ["He stopped when we called to him and again showed a dread of me when I came up, standing with his lustrous eyes fixed upon me, and even arrested in his shivering fit.", "우리가 부르자 아이는 멈춰 섰고, 내가 다가가자 다시 나를 두려워하며, 빛나는 두 눈을 나에게 고정한 채, 떨리던 발작까지 멎은 듯 우뚝 섰습니다."]], [["I asked him to come with us, and we would take care that he had some shelter for the night.", "나는 아이에게 우리와 함께 가자고, 그러면 오늘 밤 묵을 곳을 마련해 주겠다고 했습니다."]], [["“I don’t want no shelter,”", "\"잘 데 따위 필요 없어.\""], ["he said; “I can lay amongst the warm bricks.”", "아이가 말했습니다. \"따뜻한 벽돌 틈에 누우면 돼.\""]], [["“But don’t you know that people die there?”", "\"하지만 거기서 사람들이 죽는 거 몰라?\""], ["replied Charley.", "찰리가 받았습니다."]], [["“They dies everywheres,”", "\"사람들은 어디서나 죽어.\""], ["said the boy.", "아이가 말했습니다."], ["“They dies in their lodgings—she knows where; I showed her—and they dies down in Tom-all-Alone’s in heaps.", "\"셋방에서도 죽고—이 여자가 알아, 내가 보여줬으니까—톰 혼자 사는 곳에선 무더기로 죽어."], ["They dies more than they lives, according to what I see.”", "내가 보기엔 사는 사람보다 죽는 사람이 더 많아.\""], ["Then he hoarsely whispered Charley, “If she ain’t the t’other one, she ain’t the forrenner.", "그러고는 쉰 목소리로 찰리에게 속삭였습니다. \"저 마님이 딴 마님이 아니면, 그 외국 여자도 아니네."], ["Is there THREE of ’em then?”", "그럼 셋이나 되는 거야?\""]], [["Charley looked at me a little frightened.", "찰리가 약간 겁먹은 얼굴로 나를 바라보았습니다."], ["I felt half frightened at myself when the boy glared on me so.", "아이가 나를 그렇게 노려보니 나 역시 내 자신이 조금 두려워졌습니다."]], [["But he turned and followed when I beckoned to him, and finding that he acknowledged that influence in me, I led the way straight home.", "그러나 내가 손짓하자 아이는 돌아서서 따라왔고, 아이가 나에게 그런 힘이 있음을 인정하는 것을 보고, 나는 곧장 집으로 앞장섰습니다."], ["It was not far, only at the summit of the hill.", "멀지 않았습니다, 언덕 꼭대기였으니까요."], ["We passed but one man.", "우리는 사람을 딱 한 명 마주쳤습니다."], ["I doubted if we should have got home without assistance, the boy’s steps were so uncertain and tremulous.", "아이의 발걸음이 어찌나 불안하고 후들거리는지, 도움 없이 집에 닿을 수 있을지 의문이었습니다."], ["He made no complaint, however, and was strangely unconcerned about himself, if I may say so strange a thing.", "그래도 아이는 아무 불평이 없었고, 이렇게 이상한 표현을 써도 된다면, 제 몸에 대해 이상하리만치 무심했습니다."]], [["Leaving him in the hall for a moment, shrunk into the corner of the window-seat and staring with an indifference that scarcely could be called wonder at the comfort and brightness about him, I went into the drawing-room to speak to my guardian.", "나는 잠시 아이를 현관에 남겨두었는데, 아이는 창가 자리 구석에 잔뜩 웅크린 채, 주위의 안락함과 환함을 보면서도 경이로움이라고도 할 수 없는 무관심한 눈으로 빤히 바라보았습니다."], ["There I found Mr.", "나는 후견인에게 말씀드리러 응접실로 들어갔습니다."], ["Skimpole, who had come down by the coach, as he frequently did without notice, and never bringing any clothes with him, but always borrowing everything he wanted.", "거기에는 스킴폴 씨가 와 있었는데, 자주 그러듯 기별도 없이 역마차를 타고 내려온 것이었습니다. 옷가지 하나 가져오는 법 없이, 필요한 건 무엇이든 늘 빌려 쓰는 사람이었습니다."]], [["They came out with me directly to look at the boy.", "두 사람은 곧장 나와 함께 아이를 보러 나갔습니다."], ["The servants had gathered in the hall too, and he shivered in the window-seat with Charley standing by him, like some wounded animal that had been found in a ditch.", "하인들도 현관에 모여 있었고, 아이는 도랑에서 발견된 상처 입은 짐승처럼 창가 자리에서 떨고 있었으며, 찰리가 그 곁에 서 있었습니다."]], [["“This is a sorrowful case,”", "\"딱한 경우로구나.\""], ["said my guardian after asking him a question or two and touching him and examining his eyes.", "후견인이 아이에게 한두 마디 물어보고, 손을 짚어보고, 눈을 살펴본 뒤에 말했습니다."], ["“What do you say, Harold?”", "\"자넨 어떻게 생각하나, 해럴드?\""]], [["“You had better turn him out,”", "\"내쫓는 게 좋겠소.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말인가?\""], ["inquired my guardian, almost sternly.", "후견인이 거의 준엄하다 싶게 물었습니다."]], [["“My dear Jarndyce,”", "\"이보게 잔다이스.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole, “you know what I am: I am a child.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Be cross to me if I deserve it.", "\"내가 어떤 사람인지 자네도 알지 않나."], ["But I have a constitutional objection to this sort of thing.", "나는 어린애일세. 내가 그럴 만하면 나한테 화를 내게."], ["I always had, when I was a medical man.", "하지만 나는 이런 일에는 천성적으로 거부감이 있어."], ["He’s not safe, you know.", "의사 노릇을 할 때부터 늘 그랬지."], ["There’s a very bad sort of fever about him.”", "저 아이는 위험해, 알겠나. 몹시 고약한 열병에 걸려 있거든.\""]], [["Mr. Skimpole had retreated from the hall to the drawing-room again and said this in his airy way, seated on the music-stool as we stood by.", "스킴폴 씨는 현관에서 다시 응접실로 물러나, 우리가 곁에 선 가운데 피아노 의자에 앉아 그 특유의 가벼운 투로 이렇게 말했습니다."]], [["“You’ll say it’s childish,”", "\"유치한 소리라고 하겠지.\""], ["observed Mr. Skimpole, looking gaily at us.", "스킴폴 씨가 우리를 명랑하게 바라보며 말했습니다."], ["“Well, I dare say it may be; but I AM a child, and I never pretend to be anything else.", "\"글쎄, 그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 나는 어린애고, 다른 무엇인 척한 적도 없네."], ["If you put him out in the road, you only put him where he was before.", "저 아이를 길바닥에 내놓는다 해도, 결국 원래 있던 자리에 도로 갖다 놓는 것뿐이야."], ["He will be no worse off than he was, you know.", "전보다 더 나빠질 것도 없어, 알겠나."], ["Even make him better off, if you like.", "자네가 원한다면 더 나아지게 할 수도 있지."], ["Give him sixpence, or five shillings, or five pound ten—you are arithmeticians, and I am not—and get rid of him!”", "저 애한테 6펜스를 주든, 5실링을 주든, 5파운드 10실링을 주든—자네들은 셈에 밝고 나는 그렇지 않으니—그렇게 떼어내 버리게!\""]], [["“And what is he to do then?”", "\"그럼 그러고 나서 저 아인 어쩌란 말인가?\""], ["asked my guardian.", "후견인이 물었습니다."]], [["“Upon my life,” said Mr.", "\"맹세코.\""], ["Skimpole, shrugging his shoulders with his engaging smile, “I have not the least idea what he is to do then.", "스킴폴 씨가 그 매력적인 미소를 띠고 어깨를 으쓱이며 말했습니다. \"그러고 나서 저 애가 어쩔지는 나도 도무지 모르겠네."], ["But I have no doubt he’ll do it.”", "하지만 어떻게든 하리란 건 의심치 않아.\""]], [["“Now, is it not a horrible reflection,”", "\"그런데 말일세, 이거 끔찍한 노릇 아닌가.\""], ["said my guardian, to whom I had hastily explained the unavailing efforts of the two women, “is it not a horrible reflection,”", "내가 후견인에게 두 여자의 헛수고를 황급히 설명해 드리자 후견인이 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리며 방 안을 오가며 말했습니다."], ["walking up and down and rumpling his hair, “that if this wretched creature were a convicted prisoner, his hospital would be wide open to him, and he would be as well taken care of as any sick boy in the kingdom?”", "\"이거 끔찍한 노릇 아니냐고. 만약 이 가엾은 아이가 형이 확정된 죄수라면, 병원 문이 활짝 열려 있을 테고, 이 나라 어느 병든 아이 못지않게 잘 보살핌을 받을 게 아닌가?\""]], [["“My dear Jarndyce,”", "\"이보게 잔다이스.\""], ["returned Mr.", "스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다."], ["Skimpole, “you’ll pardon the simplicity of the question, coming as it does from a creature who is perfectly simple in worldly matters, but why ISN’T he a prisoner then?”", "\"세상일에는 더없이 단순한 인간이 던지는 단순한 질문이니 너그러이 봐주게. 그렇다면 저 애는 왜 죄수가 아닌가?\""]], [["My guardian stopped and looked at him with a whimsical mixture of amusement and indignation in his face.", "후견인은 걸음을 멈추고, 즐거움과 분노가 묘하게 뒤섞인 얼굴로 그를 바라보았습니다."]], [["“Our young friend is not to be suspected of any delicacy, I should imagine,”", "\"우리 어린 친구한테 무슨 섬세한 감정 따위가 있다고 의심할 순 없을 것 같네만.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole, unabashed and candid.", "스킴폴 씨가 주눅 들지 않고 솔직하게 말했습니다."], ["“It seems to me that it would be wiser, as well as in a certain kind of way more respectable, if he showed some misdirected energy that got him into prison.", "\"내가 보기엔, 저 애가 감옥에 가게 할 만한 엉뚱한 기운이라도 좀 부려주는 편이 더 현명하고, 또 어떤 면에선 더 떳떳하지 않겠나."], ["There would be more of an adventurous spirit in it, and consequently more of a certain sort of poetry.”", "그러면 모험심이라도 좀 깃들 테고, 따라서 어떤 종류의 시정(詩情)도 더 생길 거란 말이지.\""]], [["“I believe,”", "\"내가 보기엔 말일세.\""], ["returned my guardian, resuming his uneasy walk, “that there is not such another child on earth as yourself.”", "후견인이 다시 불안한 걸음을 옮기며 받았습니다. \"이 땅 위에 자네 같은 어린애는 또 없을 거야.\""]], [["“Do you really?”", "\"정말 그런가?\" 스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mr.", "\"그럴 만도 하지!"], ["Skimpole.", "하지만 솔직히 말해, 우리 어린 친구가 제 처지에 맞게나마, 제게 열린 시정을 제 몸에 두르려 들지 않을 이유를 모르겠어."], ["“I dare say!", "저 애도 분명 식욕을 타고났을 거야—건강이 좀 더 멀쩡할 땐, 아마 식욕이 아주 왕성하겠지. 좋아."], ["But I confess I don’t see why our young friend, in his degree, should not seek to invest himself with such poetry as is open to him.", "우리 어린 친구의 자연스러운 저녁 식사 시간, 그러니까 한낮쯤에, 우리 어린 친구가 사회에 대고 사실상 이렇게 말하는 거야. '나는 배가 고픕니다."], ["He is no doubt born with an appetite—probably, when he is in a safer state of health, he has an excellent appetite.", "부디 그 숟가락을 꺼내 나를 먹여주시겠습니까?' 그런데 숟가락 체계 전체를 도맡아 관리하노라 자처하고, 우리 어린 친구 몫의 숟가락도 있노라 공언하는 사회가, 정작 그 숟가락을 내놓지 않는단 말이지."], ["Very well.", "그래서 우리 어린 친구가 이렇게 말하는 거야. '그렇다면 내가 그걸 낚아채더라도 양해해 주셔야겠습니다.'"], ["At our young friend’s natural dinner hour, most likely about noon, our young friend says in effect to society, ‘I am hungry; will you have the goodness to produce your spoon and feed me?’ Society, which has taken upon itself the general arrangement of the whole system of spoons and professes to have a spoon for our young friend, does NOT produce that spoon; and our young friend, therefore, says ‘You really must excuse me if I seize it.’ Now, this appears to me a case of misdirected energy, which has a certain amount of reason in it and a certain amount of romance; and I don’t know but what I should be more interested in our young friend, as an illustration of such a case, than merely as a poor vagabond—which any one can be.”", "자, 이건 내가 보기에 엉뚱한 데로 쏠린 기운의 한 사례인데, 거기엔 어느 정도 이치도 있고 어느 정도 낭만도 있어. 그래서 나로선 우리 어린 친구를, 그저 떠돌이 거지로—그거야 누구나 될 수 있는 거고—볼 때보다, 그런 사례의 본보기로 볼 때 더 흥미를 느낄 것 같단 말일세.\""]], [["“In the meantime,”", "\"그러는 사이에.\""], ["I ventured to observe, “he is getting worse.”", "나는 감히 한마디 했습니다. \"저 아이는 점점 나빠지고 있어요.\""]], [["“In the meantime,” said Mr.", "\"그러는 사이에.\""], ["Skimpole cheerfully, “as Miss Summerson, with her practical good sense, observes, he is getting worse.", "스킴폴 씨가 쾌활하게 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양이 그 실용적인 양식으로 짚었듯, 저 애는 점점 나빠지고 있지."], ["Therefore I recommend your turning him out before he gets still worse.”", "그러니 더 나빠지기 전에 내쫓으라고 권하는 걸세.\""]], [["The amiable face with which he said it, I think I shall never forget.", "그 말을 하면서 짓던 그 상냥한 얼굴을, 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것 같습니다."]], [["“Of course, little woman,”", "\"물론이지, 우리 꼬마 아주머니.\""], ["observed my guardian, turning to me, “I can ensure his admission into the proper place by merely going there to enforce it, though it’s a bad state of things when, in his condition, that is necessary.", "후견인이 나를 돌아보며 말했습니다. \"내가 그곳에 직접 가서 강제로 밀어붙이면 저 애를 마땅한 데 들여보낼 수야 있지."], ["But it’s growing late, and is a very bad night, and the boy is worn out already.", "저 애 상태에 그런 짓까지 해야 한다는 게 한심한 노릇이긴 하다만. 하지만 밤이 깊어가고, 날씨도 몹시 사납고, 저 애는 이미 기진맥진했어."], ["There is a bed in the wholesome loft-room by the stable; we had better keep him there till morning, when he can be wrapped up and removed.", "마구간 옆 위생적인 다락방에 침대가 하나 있으니, 아침까지 거기 재우는 게 낫겠다. 아침이 되면 잘 싸매서 옮기면 되고."], ["We’ll do that.”", "그렇게 하자꾸나.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole, with his hands upon the keys of the piano as we moved away.", "우리가 자리를 뜨려는데 스킴폴 씨가 피아노 건반에 손을 얹으며 말했습니다."], ["“Are you going back to our young friend?”", "\"우리 어린 친구한테 돌아가는 건가?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“How I envy you your constitution, Jarndyce!”", "\"자네 그 체질이 얼마나 부러운지 모르겠네, 잔다이스!\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole with playful admiration.", "스킴폴 씨가 장난스러운 감탄을 담아 받았습니다."], ["“You don’t mind these things; neither does Miss Summerson.", "\"자넨 이런 일에 개의치 않잖아. 서머슨 양도 마찬가지고."], ["You are ready at all times to go anywhere, and do anything.", "자네들은 언제든 어디든 가고 무슨 일이든 할 채비가 돼 있어."], ["Such is will!", "그게 바로 의지란 거지!"], ["I have no will at all—and no won’t—simply can’t.”", "나는 의지라곤 통 없어—'싫다'는 것도 없어—그저 못 할 뿐이야.\""]], [["“You can’t recommend anything for the boy, I suppose?”", "\"저 아이한테 뭐 권할 만한 게 없겠나?\" 후견인이 어깨너머로 반쯤 화난 듯 돌아보며 말했습니다."], ["said my guardian, looking back over his shoulder half angrily; only half angrily, for he never seemed to consider Mr. Skimpole an accountable being.", "반쯤만 화난 것이었는데, 그는 스킴폴 씨를 책임을 물을 만한 존재로 여기는 법이 결코 없었기 때문입니다."]], [["“My dear Jarndyce, I observed a bottle of cooling medicine in his pocket, and it’s impossible for him to do better than take it.", "\"이보게 잔다이스, 저 애 주머니에 열을 식히는 약병이 하나 있는 걸 봤네. 그걸 먹는 것보다 더 나은 방도는 없을 거야."], ["You can tell them to sprinkle a little vinegar about the place where he sleeps and to keep it moderately cool and him moderately warm.", "저 애가 자는 자리 둘레에 식초를 좀 뿌리고, 자리는 적당히 시원하게, 애는 적당히 따뜻하게 해주라고 일러두게."], ["But it is mere impertinence in me to offer any recommendation.", "하지만 내가 이런 권고를 늘어놓는다는 게 그저 주제넘은 짓이지."], ["Miss Summerson has such a knowledge of detail and such a capacity for the administration of detail that she knows all about it.”", "서머슨 양이 워낙 세세한 일을 잘 알고 또 세세한 일을 척척 처리하는 능력이 있으니, 다 알아서 할 걸세.\""]], [["We went back into the hall and explained to Jo what we proposed to do, which Charley explained to him again and which he received with the languid unconcern I had already noticed, wearily looking on at what was done as if it were for somebody else.", "우리는 다시 현관으로 돌아가 조에게 우리가 하려는 바를 설명했고, 찰리가 다시 한번 일러주었습니다. 아이는 내가 이미 눈여겨본 그 나른한 무관심으로 받아들이며, 마치 남의 일이기라도 한 양 진행되는 일을 지친 눈으로 바라보았습니다."], ["The servants compassionating his miserable state and being very anxious to help, we soon got the loft-room ready; and some of the men about the house carried him across the wet yard, well wrapped up.", "하인들이 그 비참한 처지를 가엾이 여겨 무척 거들고 싶어 했으므로, 우리는 이내 다락방을 준비했고, 집안 남자들 몇이 아이를 잘 싸매어 젖은 마당을 가로질러 옮겼습니다."], ["It was pleasant to observe how kind they were to him and how there appeared to be a general impression among them that frequently calling him “Old Chap”", "그들이 아이에게 얼마나 친절한지, 또 그를 자꾸 \"어이, 친구\" 하고 부르면 기운이 살아나리라는 생각이 그들 사이에 두루 퍼져 있는 듯한 것이 보기 좋았습니다."], ["was likely to revive his spirits. Charley directed the operations and went to and fro between the loft-room and the house with such little stimulants and comforts as we thought it safe to give him.", "찰리가 일을 지휘하며, 안전하다 싶은 약간의 자양분과 위안거리를 들고 다락방과 본채 사이를 오갔습니다."], ["My guardian himself saw him before he was left for the night and reported to me when he returned to the growlery to write a letter on the boy’s behalf, which a messenger was charged to deliver at day-light in the morning, that he seemed easier and inclined to sleep.", "후견인도 밤새 아이만 남겨두기 전에 직접 살펴보고, 아침 동틀 무렵 전령을 시켜 전하기로 한 편지를 아이를 위해 쓰러 으르렁 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 아이가 한결 편안해진 듯하고 잠들 기색이라고 나에게 일러주었습니다."], ["They had fastened his door on the outside, he said, in case of his being delirious, but had so arranged that he could not make any noise without being heard.", "혹시라도 헛소리를 할까 봐 문은 바깥에서 잠가두었지만, 아이가 무슨 소리라도 내면 들리도록 해두었다고도 했습니다."]], [["Ada being in our room with a cold, Mr. Skimpole was left alone all this time and entertained himself by playing snatches of pathetic airs and sometimes singing to them (as we heard at a distance) with great expression and feeling.", "에이다는 감기에 걸려 우리 방에 있었으므로, 스킴폴 씨는 그동안 내내 혼자 남아, 애처로운 가락을 토막토막 치고 이따금 거기에 맞춰 (멀리서 우리에게 들려오듯) 풍부한 감정과 표현을 담아 노래를 부르며 시간을 보냈습니다."], ["When we rejoined him in the drawing-room he said he would give us a little ballad which had come into his head “apropos of our young friend,”", "우리가 응접실에서 다시 그와 합류하자, 그는 \"우리 어린 친구에게 어울리는\""], ["and he sang one about a peasant boy,", "짤막한 발라드 하나가 떠올랐다며, 어느 시골 소년에 관한 노래를 불러주었습니다."]], [["“Thrown on the wide world, doomed to wander and roam, Bereft of his parents, bereft of a home.”", "\"넓은 세상에 내던져져, 정처 없이 떠돌 운명, 부모도 여의고, 집도 여의었네.\""]], [["quite exquisitely.", "참으로 절묘하게 불렀습니다."], ["It was a song that always made him cry, he told us.", "그는 이 노래를 부르면 늘 눈물이 난다고 우리에게 말했습니다."]], [["He was extremely gay all the rest of the evening, for he absolutely chirped—those were his delighted words—when he thought by what a happy talent for business he was surrounded.", "그는 그날 저녁 내내 더없이 흥겨워했습니다. 자기가 일에 그토록 행복한 재능을 가진 이들에게 둘러싸여 있다고 생각하니, 그야말로 \"재잘재잘 흥이 났다\"—그 자신의 들뜬 표현이었습니다."], ["He gave us, in his glass of negus, “Better health to our young friend!”", "그는 니거스 술잔을 들고 \"우리 어린 친구의 쾌차를 위하여!\"라고 우리에게 건배하며, 그 아이가 휘팅턴처럼 훗날 런던 시장이 되도록 점지된 게 아니겠느냐며 그 가상의 경우를 흥겹게 펼쳐 보였습니다."], ["and supposed and gaily pursued the case of his being reserved like Whittington to become Lord Mayor of London.", "그렇게 되면 분명 그 애는 잔다이스 재단과 서머슨 양로원을 세우고, 해마다 세인트올번스로 향하는 소규모 시 순례단도 꾸리겠지요."], ["In that event, no doubt, he would establish the Jarndyce Institution and the Summerson Almshouses, and a little annual Corporation Pilgrimage to St. Albans.", "우리 어린 친구가 제 나름으로는 훌륭한 아이임을 의심치 않지만, 그 나름이라는 게 해럴드 스킴폴식은 아니라고 그는 말했습니다."], ["He had no doubt, he said, that our young friend was an excellent boy in his way, but his way was not the Harold Skimpole way; what Harold Skimpole was, Harold Skimpole had found himself, to his considerable surprise, when he first made his own acquaintance; he had accepted himself with all his failings and had thought it sound philosophy to make the best of the bargain; and he hoped we would do the same.", "해럴드 스킴폴이 어떤 사람인지는, 해럴드 스킴폴이 처음 제 자신과 알게 되었을 때 적잖이 놀라며 스스로 깨달았다고요. 그는 온갖 결점까지 다 안고 제 자신을 받아들였으며, 그 거래에서 최선을 끌어내는 것이 건전한 철학이라 여겨왔다고, 그리고 우리도 그렇게 하기를 바란다고 했습니다."]], [["Charley’s last report was that the boy was quiet.", "찰리의 마지막 보고는 아이가 잠잠하다는 것이었습니다."], ["I could see, from my window, the lantern they had left him burning quietly; and I went to bed very happy to think that he was sheltered.", "나는 내 방 창문에서, 그들이 아이에게 남겨준 등불이 조용히 타오르는 것을 볼 수 있었습니다. 그래서 아이가 비바람을 피해 보호받고 있다는 생각에 무척 행복한 마음으로 잠자리에 들었습니다."]], [["There was more movement and more talking than usual a little before daybreak, and it awoke me.", "동트기 조금 전, 여느 때보다 부산한 움직임과 말소리가 일어 나를 깨웠습니다."], ["As I was dressing, I looked out of my window and asked one of our men who had been among the active sympathizers last night whether there was anything wrong about the house.", "옷을 입으며 창밖을 내다보다, 간밤에 적극적으로 거들던 우리 집 남자 하나에게 집에 무슨 일이라도 있느냐고 물었습니다."], ["The lantern was still burning in the loft-window.", "다락방 창에는 아직 등불이 타고 있었습니다."]], [["“It’s the boy, miss,”", "\"그 아이 때문입니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["“Is he worse?”", "\"더 나빠졌나요?\""], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Gone, miss.”", "\"사라졌어요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Dead!” “Dead, miss?", "\"죽었느냐고요, 아가씨?"], ["No.", "아닙니다."], ["Gone clean off.”", "감쪽같이 사라졌어요.\""]], [["At what time of the night he had gone, or how, or why, it seemed hopeless ever to divine.", "밤 몇 시에 사라졌는지, 어떻게, 왜 사라졌는지는 아무리 해도 끝내 알아낼 길이 없을 듯했습니다."], ["The door remaining as it had been left, and the lantern standing in the window, it could only be supposed that he had got out by a trap in the floor which communicated with an empty cart-house below.", "문은 잠가둔 그대로였고 등불은 창에 그대로 놓여 있었으니, 아래쪽 빈 수레 창고로 통하는 바닥의 들창으로 빠져나갔다고밖에는 짐작할 수 없었습니다."], ["But he had shut it down again, if that were so; and it looked as if it had not been raised.", "하지만 그렇다 해도 아이는 그걸 도로 닫아두었고, 들어 올린 흔적조차 없어 보였습니다."], ["Nothing of any kind was missing.", "없어진 물건은 아무것도 없었습니다."], ["On this fact being clearly ascertained, we all yielded to the painful belief that delirium had come upon him in the night and that, allured by some imaginary object or pursued by some imaginary horror, he had strayed away in that worse than helpless state; all of us, that is to say, but Mr.", "이 사실이 분명히 밝혀지자, 우리는 모두 가슴 아픈 결론에 이를 수밖에 없었습니다. 밤사이 아이에게 섬망이 찾아왔고, 무슨 환영에 홀렸거나 무슨 환상의 공포에 쫓겨, 무력하다는 말로도 모자랄 그 상태로 어디론가 헤매 나갔으리라는 것이었습니다."], ["Skimpole, who repeatedly suggested, in his usual easy light style, that it had occurred to our young friend that he was not a safe inmate, having a bad kind of fever upon him, and that he had with great natural politeness taken himself off.", "다만 스킴폴 씨만은 예외였는데, 그는 평소의 그 느긋하고 가벼운 투로 거듭 이렇게 넌지시 말했습니다. 우리 어린 친구가 고약한 열병에 걸린 자기는 묵어도 안전치 못한 손님이라는 생각이 들어, 타고난 예의를 한껏 발휘해 제 발로 떠났을 거라고요."]], [["Every possible inquiry was made, and every place was searched.", "할 수 있는 모든 수소문을 했고, 곳곳을 다 뒤졌습니다."], ["The brick-kilns were examined, the cottages were visited, the two women were particularly questioned, but they knew nothing of him, and nobody could doubt that their wonder was genuine.", "벽돌 가마를 살피고, 오두막들을 찾아가고, 특히 두 여자에게 캐물었지만, 그들은 아이에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그 놀라움이 진심임을 누구도 의심할 수 없었습니다."], ["The weather had for some time been too wet and the night itself had been too wet to admit of any tracing by footsteps.", "한동안 날씨가 너무 궂었고 그날 밤 역시 너무 궂어서 발자국으로 행방을 좇을 수는 없었습니다."], ["Hedge and ditch, and wall, and rick and stack, were examined by our men for a long distance round, lest the boy should be lying in such a place insensible or dead; but nothing was seen to indicate that he had ever been near.", "혹시 아이가 어딘가에 의식을 잃거나 죽어 쓰러져 있을까 봐, 우리 집 남자들은 멀리까지 산울타리와 도랑과 담장, 짚단과 낟가리를 다 뒤졌지만, 아이가 근처에라도 왔었다는 흔적은 하나도 보이지 않았습니다."], ["From the time when he was left in the loft-room, he vanished.", "다락방에 남겨진 그때부터, 아이는 자취도 없이 사라진 것이었습니다."]], [["The search continued for five days.", "수색은 닷새 동안 이어졌습니다."], ["I do not mean that it ceased even then, but that my attention was then diverted into a current very memorable to me.", "그때 수색이 그쳤다는 말은 아니고, 다만 그때 내 주의가 나로서는 무척이나 잊지 못할 다른 흐름으로 쏠렸다는 뜻입니다."]], [["As Charley was at her writing again in my room in the evening, and as I sat opposite to her at work, I felt the table tremble.", "저녁에 찰리가 내 방에서 또 글씨 연습을 하고, 나는 그 맞은편에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었는데, 탁자가 흔들리는 것이 느껴졌습니다."], ["Looking up, I saw my little maid shivering from head to foot.", "고개를 들어보니 내 어린 하녀가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있었습니다."]], [["“Charley,”", "\"찰리.\""], ["said I, “are you so cold?”", "나는 말했습니다. \"그렇게 춥니?\""]], [["“I think I am, miss,” she replied.", "\"그런가 봐요, 아가씨.\""], ["“I don’t know what it is.", "찰리가 대답했습니다."], ["I can’t hold myself still.", "\"왜 이러는지 모르겠어요."], ["I felt so yesterday at about this same time, miss.", "가만히 있을 수가 없어요. 어제도 이맘때쯤 이랬어요, 아가씨."], ["Don’t be uneasy, I think I’m ill.”", "걱정 마세요, 제가 아픈가 봐요.\""]], [["I heard Ada’s voice outside, and I hurried to the door of communication between my room and our pretty sitting-room, and locked it.", "밖에서 에이다의 목소리가 들리기에, 나는 서둘러 내 방과 우리의 예쁜 거실 사이의 연결문으로 가서 잠갔습니다. 아슬아슬하게 때맞춰서요."], ["Just in time, for she tapped at it while my hand was yet upon the key.", "내 손이 아직 열쇠에 닿아 있을 때 에이다가 문을 두드렸으니까요."]], [["Ada called to me to let her in, but I said, “Not now, my dearest.", "에이다가 들여보내 달라고 불렀지만, 나는 말했습니다. \"지금은 안 돼, 내 사랑."], ["Go away.", "저리 가 있어."], ["There’s nothing the matter; I will come to you presently.”", "별일 아니야. 이따 너한테 갈게.\""], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["It was a long, long time before my darling girl and I were companions again.", "사랑하는 그 아이와 내가 다시 벗이 되기까지는 참으로, 참으로 오랜 시간이 걸렸습니다."]], [["Charley fell ill.", "찰리가 병이 났습니다."], ["In twelve hours she was very ill.", "열두 시간 만에 매우 위중해졌습니다."], ["I moved her to my room, and laid her in my bed, and sat down quietly to nurse her.", "나는 찰리를 내 방으로 옮겨 내 침대에 눕히고, 조용히 곁에 앉아 간호했습니다."], ["I told my guardian all about it, and why I felt it was necessary that I should seclude myself, and my reason for not seeing my darling above all.", "나는 후견인에게 자초지종을 다 말씀드렸습니다. 내가 왜 스스로를 가두어야 한다고 여기는지, 무엇보다 사랑하는 그 아이를 만나지 않으려는 까닭이 무엇인지를요."], ["At first she came very often to the door, and called to me, and even reproached me with sobs and tears; but I wrote her a long letter saying that she made me anxious and unhappy and imploring her, as she loved me and wished my mind to be at peace, to come no nearer than the garden.", "처음에 에이다는 자주 문 앞에 와서 나를 부르고, 흐느끼며 눈물로 나를 원망하기까지 했습니다. 하지만 나는 긴 편지를 써서, 네가 나를 불안하고 괴롭게 만든다고, 그러니 나를 사랑하고 내 마음이 평온하기를 바란다면 정원보다 더 가까이는 오지 말아달라고 간곡히 빌었습니다."], ["After that she came beneath the window even oftener than she had come to the door, and if I had learnt to love her dear sweet voice before when we were hardly ever apart, how did I learn to love it then, when I stood behind the window-curtain listening and replying, but not so much as looking out!", "그 뒤로 에이다는 문 앞에 오던 것보다 더 자주 창 아래로 왔는데, 우리가 거의 떨어져 지낸 적이 없던 전에도 그 다정하고 고운 목소리를 사랑하는 법을 배웠다면, 창 커튼 뒤에 서서 귀 기울여 대답만 할 뿐 내다보지조차 못하던 그때, 나는 그 목소리를 사랑하는 법을 얼마나 더 깊이 배웠던지요!"], ["How did I learn to love it afterwards, when the harder time came!", "그 뒤 더 힘겨운 때가 닥쳤을 때, 또 나는 그 목소리를 사랑하는 법을 얼마나 더 깊이 배웠던지요!"]], [["They put a bed for me in our sitting-room; and by keeping the door wide open, I turned the two rooms into one, now that Ada had vacated that part of the house, and kept them always fresh and airy.", "사람들이 우리 거실에 내 침대를 놓아주었습니다. 이제 에이다가 그쪽을 비웠으니, 나는 문을 활짝 열어둔 채 두 방을 하나로 트고, 늘 신선하고 통풍이 잘되게 해두었습니다."], ["There was not a servant in or about the house but was so good that they would all most gladly have come to me at any hour of the day or night without the least fear or unwillingness, but I thought it best to choose one worthy woman who was never to see Ada and whom I could trust to come and go with all precaution.", "집 안팎의 하인이라면 누구 하나 빠짐없이 어찌나 착한지, 낮이든 밤이든 어느 때라도 조금의 두려움이나 꺼리는 기색 없이 더없이 기꺼이 나에게 와주었을 것입니다. 하지만 나는 에이다와는 결코 마주치지 않을, 그리고 빈틈없이 조심해서 드나들 거라 믿을 수 있는 점잖은 여인 하나를 고르는 것이 가장 낫겠다고 생각했습니다."], ["Through her means I got out to take the air with my guardian when there was no fear of meeting Ada, and wanted for nothing in the way of attendance, any more than in any other respect.", "그 여인 덕분에 나는 에이다와 마주칠 염려가 없을 때 후견인과 함께 나가 바람을 쐴 수 있었고, 다른 어떤 면에서와 마찬가지로 시중드는 일에서도 아쉬울 것이 없었습니다."]], [["And thus poor Charley sickened and grew worse, and fell into heavy danger of death, and lay severely ill for many a long round of day and night.", "그렇게 가엾은 찰리는 점점 더 앓고 더 나빠져, 죽음의 위험에 깊이 빠져들었고, 길고 긴 낮과 밤이 거듭되는 동안 위중하게 누워 있었습니다."], ["So patient she was, so uncomplaining, and inspired by such a gentle fortitude that very often as I sat by Charley holding her head in my arms—repose would come to her, so, when it would come to her in no other attitude—I silently prayed to our Father in heaven that I might not forget the lesson which this little sister taught me.", "그토록 잘 참고, 그토록 불평이 없고, 그토록 온화한 꿋꿋함으로 버티는 모습에, 나는 자주 찰리 곁에 앉아 그 머리를 두 팔로 안고서—다른 어떤 자세로도 쉬지 못하던 아이가 그렇게 하면 쉬곤 했습니다—이 어린 누이가 나에게 가르쳐준 교훈을 잊지 않게 해달라고 하늘에 계신 우리 아버지께 말없이 기도했습니다."]], [["I was very sorrowful to think that Charley’s pretty looks would change and be disfigured, even if she recovered—she was such a child with her dimpled face—but that thought was, for the greater part, lost in her greater peril.", "설령 회복한다 해도 찰리의 예쁜 얼굴이 흉하게 망가질 거라는 생각에 나는 무척 슬펐습니다—보조개 진 얼굴이 아직 그토록 어린아이였으니까요—하지만 그 생각은 대체로 그 아이의 더 큰 위험 앞에 묻혀버렸습니다."], ["When she was at the worst, and her mind rambled again to the cares of her father’s sick bed and the little children, she still knew me so far as that she would be quiet in my arms when she could lie quiet nowhere else, and murmur out the wanderings of her mind less restlessly.", "가장 위중하던 때, 정신이 다시 아버지의 병상과 어린 동생들 걱정으로 헤맬 때조차, 그 아이는 다른 어디서도 가만히 누워 있지 못하면서 내 품에서는 잠잠해질 만큼은 나를 알아보았고, 헤매는 정신의 헛소리도 덜 불안하게 흘려냈습니다. 그럴 때면 나는 이런 생각을 하곤 했습니다."], ["At those times I used to think, how should I ever tell the two remaining babies that the baby who had learned of her faithful heart to be a mother to them in their need was dead!", "남은 두 어린 동생에게, 그 충실한 마음으로 어려울 때 그들에게 어머니 노릇 하는 법을 깨우친 그 아이가 죽었다는 걸 내가 어떻게 말한단 말인가!"]], [["There were other times when Charley knew me well and talked to me, telling me that she sent her love to Tom and Emma and that she was sure Tom would grow up to be a good man. At those times Charley would speak to me of what she had read to her father as well as she could to comfort him, of that young man carried out to be buried who was the only son of his mother and she was a widow, of the ruler’s daughter raised up by the gracious hand upon her bed of death. And Charley told me that when her father died she had kneeled down and prayed in her first sorrow that he likewise might be raised up and given back to his poor children, and that if she should never get better and should die too, she thought it likely that it might come into Tom’s mind to offer the same prayer for her. Then would I show Tom how these people of old days had been brought back to life on earth, only that we might know our hope to be restored to heaven!", "찰리가 나를 또렷이 알아보고 나에게 말을 거는 다른 때도 있었습니다. 톰과 엠마에게 사랑을 전한다고, 톰은 분명 자라서 훌륭한 사람이 될 거라고 말했습니다. 그럴 때면 찰리는 아버지를 위로하려고 자기 딴에는 최선을 다해 읽어드렸던 이야기들을 들려주었습니다. 죽어 묻히러 실려 나가던 청년 이야기, 그 청년은 어느 어머니의 외아들이었고 그 어머니는 과부였다는 이야기, 그리고 죽음의 자리에 누운 회당장의 딸이 자비로운 손길로 일으켜진 이야기를요. 그러고서 찰리는, 아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 첫 슬픔에 무릎 꿇고 기도했다고 했습니다. 아버지도 그처럼 일으켜져 가엾은 자식들에게 돌려보내지기를요. 그리고 자기가 끝내 낫지 못하고 죽게 된다면, 톰의 마음에도 자기를 위해 똑같은 기도를 올릴 생각이 떠오를 것 같다고 했습니다. 그러면 나는 톰에게 보여주리라고 생각했습니다. 옛 시절의 이 사람들이 이 땅에서 되살아난 것은, 오로지 우리가 하늘로 회복되리라는 우리의 희망을 알게 하기 위함이었다고 말입니다!"]], [["But of all the various times there were in Charley’s illness, there was not one when she lost the gentle qualities I have spoken of.", "그러나 찰리가 앓는 그 온갖 때를 통틀어, 내가 말한 그 온화한 품성을 그 아이가 잃은 적은 한 번도 없었습니다."], ["And there were many, many when I thought in the night of the last high belief in the watching angel, and the last higher trust in God, on the part of her poor despised father.", "그리고 밤이면 나는 여러 번, 참으로 여러 번, 그 가엾고 멸시받던 아버지가 마지막으로 지녔던 그 드높은 믿음—지켜보는 천사에 대한 믿음, 그리고 하느님에 대한 한층 더 드높은 신뢰—을 떠올렸습니다."]], [["And Charley did not die.", "그리고 찰리는 죽지 않았습니다."], ["She flutteringly and slowly turned the dangerous point, after long lingering there, and then began to mend.", "위험한 고비에서 오래도록 머뭇거린 끝에, 가물가물 천천히 그 고비를 넘어섰고, 그러고 나서 차도를 보이기 시작했습니다."], ["The hope that never had been given, from the first, of Charley being in outward appearance Charley any more soon began to be encouraged; and even that prospered, and I saw her growing into her old childish likeness again.", "처음부터 도무지 가질 수 없던 희망—찰리가 겉모습에서나마 예전의 찰리로 남으리라는 희망—이 이내 살아나기 시작했습니다. 그것마저 잘 풀려, 나는 그 아이가 옛 어린아이 모습으로 다시 자라나는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["It was a great morning when I could tell Ada all this as she stood out in the garden; and it was a great evening when Charley and I at last took tea together in the next room.", "에이다가 정원에 서 있을 때 이 모든 이야기를 전해줄 수 있었던 그날 아침은 참으로 벅찬 아침이었습니다. 그리고 찰리와 내가 마침내 옆방에서 함께 차를 마신 그날 저녁은 참으로 벅찬 저녁이었습니다."], ["But on that same evening, I felt that I was stricken cold.", "그러나 바로 그날 저녁, 나는 한기가 온몸을 덮치는 것을 느꼈습니다."]], [["Happily for both of us, it was not until Charley was safe in bed again and placidly asleep that I began to think the contagion of her illness was upon me.", "우리 둘에게는 다행스럽게도, 찰리가 다시 무사히 침대에 누워 평온히 잠든 뒤에야 비로소 나는 그 아이의 병이 나에게 옮았다는 생각이 들기 시작했습니다."], ["I had been able easily to hide what I felt at tea-time, but I was past that already now, and I knew that I was rapidly following in Charley’s steps.", "차를 마실 때만 해도 느끼는 바를 쉽게 감출 수 있었지만, 이제는 이미 그 단계를 지나 있었고, 나는 내가 빠르게 찰리의 전철을 밟고 있음을 알았습니다."]], [["I was well enough, however, to be up early in the morning, and to return my darling’s cheerful blessing from the garden, and to talk with her as long as usual.", "그래도 나는 아침 일찍 일어나, 정원에서 들려오는 사랑하는 아이의 명랑한 축복에 화답하고, 여느 때처럼 오래 이야기를 나눌 만큼은 괜찮았습니다."], ["But I was not free from an impression that I had been walking about the two rooms in the night, a little beside myself, though knowing where I was; and I felt confused at times—with a curious sense of fullness, as if I were becoming too large altogether.", "하지만 간밤에 내가 어디 있는지는 알면서도 약간 정신이 나간 채 두 방을 서성였다는 느낌만은 떨칠 수가 없었습니다. 그리고 이따금 정신이 어지러웠는데—몸이 온통 너무 커지기라도 하는 듯, 묘하게 부풀어 오르는 느낌이 들었습니다."]], [["In the evening I was so much worse that I resolved to prepare Charley, with which view I said, “You’re getting quite strong, Charley, are you not?”", "저녁이 되자 훨씬 더 나빠져서, 나는 찰리에게 마음의 준비를 시키기로 마음먹고 이렇게 말했습니다. \"이제 아주 튼튼해졌지, 찰리, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Oh, quite!”", "\"네, 아주요!\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했습니다."]], [["“Strong enough to be told a secret, I think, Charley?”", "\"비밀 하나 들어도 될 만큼 튼튼한 것 같니, 찰리?\""]], [["“Quite strong enough for that, miss!”", "\"그러기엔 충분히 튼튼해요, 아가씨!\" 찰리가 외쳤습니다."], ["cried Charley.", "그러나 찰리의 얼굴은 그 기쁨이 한껏 차오른 순간에 무너지고 말았습니다."], ["But Charley’s face fell in the height of her delight, for she saw the secret in MY face; and she came out of the great chair, and fell upon my bosom, and said “Oh, miss, it’s my doing!", "내 얼굴에서 그 비밀을 읽었으니까요. 찰리는 그 큰 의자에서 빠져나와 내 가슴에 와락 안기며 말했습니다."], ["It’s my doing!”", "\"오, 아가씨, 다 제 탓이에요!"], ["and a great deal more out of the fullness of her grateful heart.", "다 제 탓이에요!\" 그러고는 고마움으로 가득 찬 마음에서 우러난 말을 한참이나 더 쏟아냈습니다."]], [["“Now, Charley,”", "\"자, 찰리.\""], ["said I after letting her go on for a little while, “if I am to be ill, my great trust, humanly speaking, is in you.", "나는 한동안 그렇게 하도록 내버려둔 뒤에 말했습니다. \"내가 앓게 된다면, 사람의 힘으로 말하건대, 내가 가장 크게 믿는 건 너야."], ["And unless you are as quiet and composed for me as you always were for yourself, you can never fulfil it, Charley.”", "그런데 네가 늘 너 자신을 위해 그랬던 것처럼 나를 위해서도 차분하고 침착하지 않으면, 그 일을 결코 해낼 수 없어, 찰리.\""]], [["“If you’ll let me cry a little longer, miss,”", "\"조금만 더 울게 해주세요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했습니다."], ["“Oh, my dear, my dear!", "\"오, 아가씨, 아가씨!"], ["If you’ll only let me cry a little longer.", "조금만 더 울게 해주세요."], ["Oh, my dear!”—how affectionately and devotedly she poured this out as she clung to my neck, I never can remember without tears—“I’ll be good.”", "오, 아가씨!\"—내 목에 매달려 이 말을 얼마나 다정하고 헌신적으로 쏟아냈던지, 나는 지금도 눈물 없이는 떠올릴 수가 없습니다—\"착하게 굴게요.\""]], [["So I let Charley cry a little longer, and it did us both good.", "그래서 나는 찰리가 조금 더 울도록 두었고, 그것은 우리 둘 모두에게 좋았습니다."]], [["“Trust in me now, if you please, miss,”", "\"이제 저를 믿어주세요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Charley quietly.", "찰리가 차분하게 말했습니다."], ["“I am listening to everything you say.”", "\"아가씨가 하시는 말씀 하나하나 다 듣고 있어요.\""]], [["“It’s very little at present, Charley.", "\"지금은 아주 사소한 거야, 찰리."], ["I shall tell your doctor to-night that I don’t think I am well and that you are going to nurse me.”", "오늘 밤 네 의사 선생님께 내가 몸이 좋지 않은 것 같다고, 그리고 네가 나를 간호할 거라고 말씀드릴 거야.\""]], [["For that the poor child thanked me with her whole heart.", "그 말에 가엾은 아이는 진심을 다해 나에게 고마워했습니다."], ["“And in the morning, when you hear Miss Ada in the garden, if I should not be quite able to go to the window-curtain as usual, do you go, Charley, and say I am asleep—that I have rather tired myself, and am asleep.", "\"그리고 아침에 에이다 아가씨가 정원에 온 소리가 들릴 때, 내가 여느 때처럼 창 커튼으로 갈 수 없거든, 찰리야, 네가 가서 내가 자고 있다고 말해다오—내가 좀 피곤해서 자고 있다고 말이야."], ["At all times keep the room as I have kept it, Charley, and let no one come.”", "언제든 방은 내가 해온 그대로 두고, 찰리, 아무도 들이지 마라.\""]], [["Charley promised, and I lay down, for I was very heavy.", "찰리는 약속했고, 나는 자리에 누웠습니다."], ["I saw the doctor that night and asked the favour of him that I wished to ask relative to his saying nothing of my illness in the house as yet.", "몸이 무척 무거웠으니까요. 그날 밤 의사를 만나, 내 병에 대해 아직은 집안에 아무 말도 하지 말아달라고 청하고 싶던 부탁을 그에게 드렸습니다."], ["I have a very indistinct remembrance of that night melting into day, and of day melting into night again; but I was just able on the first morning to get to the window and speak to my darling.", "그날 밤이 낮으로 녹아들고, 낮이 다시 밤으로 녹아들던 일은 무척 흐릿하게만 기억납니다. 다만 첫날 아침에는 가까스로 창가로 가서 사랑하는 아이에게 말을 건넬 수 있었습니다."]], [["On the second morning I heard her dear voice—Oh, how dear now!—outside; and I asked Charley, with some difficulty (speech being painful to me), to go and say I was asleep.", "이튿날 아침, 나는 그 다정한 목소리를—오, 이제 얼마나 다정한지요!—밖에서 들었고, 찰리에게 (말하는 것이 고통스러워) 어렵사리, 가서 내가 자고 있다고 전해달라고 했습니다."], ["I heard her answer softly, “Don’t disturb her, Charley, for the world!”", "찰리가 나직이 대답하는 소리가 들렸습니다. \"아가씨를 깨우지 마세요, 찰리, 무슨 일이 있어도요!\""]], [["“How does my own Pride look, Charley?”", "\"내 자랑둥이는 어떻게 보이니, 찰리?\""], ["I inquired.", "나는 물었습니다."]], [["“Disappointed, miss,”", "\"실망하셨어요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Charley, peeping through the curtain.", "찰리가 커튼 사이로 엿보며 말했습니다."]], [["“But I know she is very beautiful this morning.”", "\"하지만 오늘 아침 무척 아름다울 거라는 건 알아.\""]], [["“She is indeed, miss,”", "\"정말 그러세요, 아가씨.\""], ["answered Charley, peeping.", "찰리가 엿보며 대답했습니다."], ["“Still looking up at the window.”", "\"아직도 창을 올려다보고 계세요.\""]], [["With her blue clear eyes, God bless them, always loveliest when raised like that!", "그 맑고 푸른 두 눈, 하느님의 축복이 깃들기를, 그렇게 들어 올려질 때 늘 가장 사랑스러운 그 눈이여!"]], [["I called Charley to me and gave her her last charge.", "나는 찰리를 곁으로 불러 마지막 당부를 했습니다."]], [["“Now, Charley, when she knows I am ill, she will try to make her way into the room.", "\"자, 찰리, 내가 아프다는 걸 에이다가 알면, 어떻게든 이 방으로 들어오려 할 거야."], ["Keep her out, Charley, if you love me truly, to the last!", "막아다오, 찰리, 나를 진정 사랑한다면, 끝까지 막아다오!"], ["Charley, if you let her in but once, only to look upon me for one moment as I lie here, I shall die.”", "찰리, 단 한 번이라도 에이다를 들여보내, 내가 여기 누운 모습을 한순간이라도 보게 하면, 나는 죽을 거야.\""]], [["“I never will!", "\"절대 안 그럴게요!"], ["I never will!”", "절대로요!\""], ["she promised me.", "찰리가 나에게 약속했습니다."]], [["“I believe it, my dear Charley.", "\"믿는다, 사랑하는 찰리."], ["And now come and sit beside me for a little while, and touch me with your hand.", "자, 이제 잠시 내 곁에 와 앉아서, 네 손으로 나를 만져다오."], ["For I cannot see you, Charley; I am blind.”", "너를 볼 수가 없구나, 찰리. 나는 앞이 보이지 않아.\""]], [["The Appointed Time\nIt is night in Lincoln’s Inn—perplexed and troublous valley of the shadow of the law, where suitors generally find but little day—and fat candles are snuffed out in offices, and clerks have rattled down the crazy wooden stairs and dispersed.", "링컨법학원에 밤이 내린다. 법의 그림자가 드리운 골짜기, 미궁처럼 얽혀 시름만 가득하고, 소송 당사자들이 햇빛이라곤 좀처럼 보기 어려운 곳이다."], ["The bell that rings at nine o’clock has ceased its doleful clangour about nothing; the gates are shut; and the night-porter, a solemn warder with a mighty power of sleep, keeps guard in his lodge.", "사무실마다 굵은 양초가 꺼지고, 서기들은 삐걱대는 나무 계단을 우당탕 내려와 뿔뿔이 흩어졌다. 아홉 시에 울리는 종은 아무 의미도 없이 구슬프게 떨던 소리를 멈추었고, 출입문은 닫혔으며, 야간 수위는 무시무시한 잠 솜씨를 자랑하는 근엄한 파수꾼답게 수위실에서 보초를 선다."], ["From tiers of staircase windows clogged lamps like the eyes of Equity, bleared Argus with a fathomless pocket for every eye and an eye upon it, dimly blink at the stars.", "층층이 늘어선 계단 창문에서, 형평법의 눈동자처럼 때 낀 등불이, 눈마다 깊이를 알 수 없는 호주머니를 달고 그 위에 또 눈 하나를 얹은 흐릿한 아르고스처럼, 별을 향해 가물가물 깜박인다."], ["In dirty upper casements, here and there, hazy little patches of candlelight reveal where some wise draughtsman and conveyancer yet toils for the entanglement of real estate in meshes of sheep-skin, in the average ratio of about a dozen of sheep to an acre of land.", "여기저기 지저분한 위층 여닫이창에서는, 자그마한 촛불 얼룩이 부옇게 어른거리며, 어느 영리한 서류 작성인과 부동산 양도 전문가가 아직도 부동산을 양피지 그물에 옭아매느라 골몰하는 곳을 드러낸다. 그 비율은 한 에이커당 양 열두 마리꼴이다."], ["Over which bee-like industry these benefactors of their species linger yet, though office-hours be past, that they may give, for every day, some good account at last.", "이 꿀벌 같은 근면함 위에서, 자기 종족의 은인이라 할 이들은 근무 시간이 지났는데도 여전히 미적거리니, 그것은 하루하루 마침내 무언가 좋은 셈을 치르기 위함이다."]], [["In the neighbouring court, where the Lord Chancellor of the rag and bottle shop dwells, there is a general tendency towards beer and supper. Mrs.", "이웃한 마당, 헌 옷과 빈 병을 사고파는 가게의 대법관이 사는 그곳에는, 맥주와 야식 쪽으로 기우는 전반적인 분위기가 감돈다."], ["Piper and Mrs. Perkins, whose respective sons, engaged with a circle of acquaintance in the game of hide and seek, have been lying in ambush about the by-ways of Chancery Lane for some hours and scouring the plain of the same thoroughfare to the confusion of passengers—Mrs.", "파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인, 두 사람의 아들들은 한 무리의 동무들과 어울려 숨바꼭질을 하느라 몇 시간째 챈서리 거리 샛길마다 매복하고, 같은 큰길의 평원을 누비며 행인들을 어수선하게 만들었는데, 그 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인이 이제 막 아이들이 잠자리에 든 것을 두고 서로 축하를 나누고는, 아직도 어느 문간에서 작별 인사 몇 마디를 주고받으며 미적거린다."], ["Piper and Mrs. Perkins have but now exchanged congratulations on the children being abed, and they still linger on a door-step over a few parting words.", "크룩 씨와 그의 하숙인, 그리고 크룩 씨가 \"노상 술에 절어 있다\"는 사실, 또 그 젊은이의 유산 상속 전망이, 여느 때처럼 두 사람 대화의 주된 재료다."], ["Mr. Krook and his lodger, and the fact of Mr.", "그러나 두 사람은 솔즈 암스에서 열리는 화음 모임에 대해서도 할 말이 있으니, 반쯤 열린 창문 너머로 피아노 소리가 짤랑짤랑 마당까지 흘러나오고, 거기서 꼬마 스윌스가 영락없는 요릭처럼 화음 애호가들을 웃음바다에 빠뜨린 끝에, 이제는 합창곡에서 굵직한 저음을 맡아 친구와 후원자들을 향해 애절하게 호소하는 소리가 들린다."], ["Krook’s being “continually in liquor,” and the testamentary prospects of the young man are, as usual, the staple of their conversation.", "\"들어요, 들어요, 들어요, 저 푸-울 떨어지는 소리를!\""], ["But they have something to say, likewise, of the Harmonic Meeting at the Sol’s Arms, where the sound of the piano through the partly opened windows jingles out into the court, and where Little Swills, after keeping the lovers of harmony in a roar like a very Yorick, may now be heard taking the gruff line in a concerted piece and sentimentally adjuring his friends and patrons to “Listen, listen, listen, tew the wa-ter fall!” Mrs.", "퍼킨스 부인과 파이퍼 부인은 화음 모임을 거드는, 직업적으로 이름난 젊은 아가씨를 두고 의견을 견준다."], ["Perkins and Mrs. Piper compare opinions on the subject of the young lady of professional celebrity who assists at the Harmonic Meetings and who has a space to herself in the manuscript announcement in the window, Mrs.", "그 아가씨는 창문에 붙은 손글씨 안내문에 자기만의 자리를 차지하고 있는데, 퍼킨스 부인이 알아낸 바로는, 이름난 사이렌 'M."], ["Perkins possessing information that she has been married a year and a half, though announced as Miss M. Melvilleson, the noted siren, and that her baby is clandestinely conveyed to the Sol’s Arms every night to receive its natural nourishment during the entertainments.", "멜빌슨 양'으로 광고되긴 하지만 결혼한 지 일 년 반이나 되었고, 그 아기는 매일 밤 공연이 펼쳐지는 동안 자연스러운 영양을 공급받으려고 몰래 솔즈 암스로 옮겨진다는 것이다."], ["“Sooner than which, myself,” says Mrs.", "\"그렇게 사느니 차라리 나라면,\""], ["Perkins, “I would get my living by selling lucifers.” Mrs.", "퍼킨스 부인이 말한다, \"성냥팔이로 벌어먹고 살겠어요.\""], ["Piper, as in duty bound, is of the same opinion, holding that a private station is better than public applause, and thanking heaven for her own (and, by implication, Mrs. Perkins’) respectability.", "파이퍼 부인도 마땅히 그래야 한다는 듯 같은 의견이라, 사사로운 처지가 대중의 갈채보다 낫다고 여기며, 자신의(그리고 은연중에 퍼킨스 부인의) 떳떳함을 하늘에 감사한다."], ["By this time the pot-boy of the Sol’s Arms appearing with her supper-pint well frothed, Mrs. Piper accepts that tankard and retires indoors, first giving a fair good night to Mrs.", "그쯤 되자 솔즈 암스의 술집 심부름꾼이 거품이 잘 인 야식용 한 파인트를 들고 나타나니, 파이퍼 부인은 그 잔을 받아 들고는, 같은 술집에서 어린 퍼킨스가 잠자리에 들기 전에 받아 온 이래 줄곧 자기 한 파인트를 손에 쥐고 있던 퍼킨스 부인에게 정답게 작별 인사부터 건넨 뒤 안으로 물러난다."], ["Perkins, who has had her own pint in her hand ever since it was fetched from the same hostelry by young Perkins before he was sent to bed.", "이제 마당에는 가게 덧문을 다는 소리가 들리고, 담배 연기 같은 냄새가 풍긴다."], ["Now there is a sound of putting up shop-shutters in the court and a smell as of the smoking of pipes; and shooting stars are seen in upper windows, further indicating retirement to rest.", "위층 창문에서는 별똥별이 보이니, 다들 잠자리에 드는 참임을 한층 또렷이 알려준다."], ["Now, too, the policeman begins to push at doors; to try fastenings; to be suspicious of bundles; and to administer his beat, on the hypothesis that every one is either robbing or being robbed.", "이제는 또, 순경이 문마다 밀어보고, 빗장을 살피고, 보따리를 수상쩍어하고, 누구나 도둑질을 하거나 도둑맞는 중이라는 가정 아래 자기 순찰 구역을 돌기 시작한다."]], [["It is a close night, though the damp cold is searching too, and there is a laggard mist a little way up in the air.", "후텁지근한 밤이지만, 축축한 냉기 또한 살갗을 파고들고, 공기 중에는 한 발쯤 위로 굼뜬 안개가 떠 있다."], ["It is a fine steaming night to turn the slaughter-houses, the unwholesome trades, the sewerage, bad water, and burial-grounds to account, and give the registrar of deaths some extra business.", "도축장과 해로운 업종, 하수와 더러운 물, 그리고 매장지를 한껏 부려먹어 사망 등록관에게 일거리를 좀 더 안겨주기에 딱 좋은, 김이 무럭무럭 오르는 밤이다."], ["It may be something in the air—there is plenty in it—or it may be something in himself that is in fault; but Mr. Weevle, otherwise Jobling, is very ill at ease.", "공기 중에 무언가 있는 탓일 수도 있고—거기엔 별의별 게 다 있으니—아니면 그 자신 속에 무언가 잘못된 게 있는 탓일 수도 있겠지만, 위블 씨, 다른 이름으로는 조블링은, 영 마음이 편치 않다."], ["He comes and goes between his own room and the open street door twenty times an hour.", "그는 자기 방과 활짝 열린 거리 쪽 문 사이를 한 시간에 스무 번씩 오간다."], ["He has been doing so ever since it fell dark.", "날이 어두워진 뒤로 줄곧 그러고 있다."], ["Since the Chancellor shut up his shop, which he did very early to-night, Mr. Weevle has been down and up, and down and up (with a cheap tight velvet skull-cap on his head, making his whiskers look out of all proportion), oftener than before.", "대법관이 가게 문을 닫은 뒤로—오늘 밤은 아주 일찌감치 닫았다—위블 씨는 내려갔다 올라가고, 또 내려갔다 올라가기를(머리에 싸구려 벨벳 챙모자를 바짝 눌러쓴 탓에 구레나룻이 도무지 어울리지 않게 삐죽 튀어나온 채로) 전보다 잦게 되풀이한다."]], [["It is no phenomenon that Mr. Snagsby should be ill at ease too, for he always is so, more or less, under the oppressive influence of the secret that is upon him.", "스내그스비 씨 역시 마음이 편치 않은 것은 별스러운 일이 아니니, 그는 자기에게 얹힌 비밀의 압박 아래 늘 어느 정도는 그러하기 때문이다."], ["Impelled by the mystery of which he is a partaker and yet in which he is not a sharer, Mr. Snagsby haunts what seems to be its fountain-head—the rag and bottle shop in the court.", "자기도 한몫 끼었으면서 정작 그 속내는 나눠 받지 못한 그 수수께끼에 떠밀려, 스내그스비 씨는 그 발원지로 보이는 곳—마당의 헌 옷과 빈 병 가게—주위를 맴돈다."], ["It has an irresistible attraction for him.", "그곳은 그에게 거부할 수 없는 끌림을 지녔다."], ["Even now, coming round by the Sol’s Arms with the intention of passing down the court, and out at the Chancery Lane end, and so terminating his unpremeditated after-supper stroll of ten minutes’ long from his own door and back again, Mr. Snagsby approaches.", "지금도, 솔즈 암스를 돌아 마당을 지나 챈서리 거리 쪽 끝으로 빠져나가, 제 집 문에서 나왔다가 다시 돌아오는, 미처 작정도 없이 시작한 십 분짜리 야식 후 산책을 마무리할 작정으로, 스내그스비 씨가 다가온다."]], [["“What, Mr. Weevle?”", "\"아니, 위블 씨?\""], ["says the stationer, stopping to speak.", "문구상이 발걸음을 멈추고 말을 건넨다."], ["“Are YOU there?”", "\"거기 계셨소?\""]], [["“Aye!”", "\"예!\""], ["says Weevle, “Here I am, Mr.", "위블이 말한다."], ["Snagsby.”", "\"여기 있습니다, 스내그스비 씨.\""]], [["“Airing yourself, as I am doing, before you go to bed?”", "\"나처럼 잠자리에 들기 전에 바람 좀 쐬는 거요?\""], ["the stationer inquires.", "문구상이 묻는다."]], [["“Why, there’s not much air to be got here; and what there is, is not very freshening,”", "\"글쎄요, 여기선 쐴 바람이랄 게 별로 없고, 있는 것도 그리 상쾌하진 않네요.\""], ["Weevle answers, glancing up and down the court.", "위블이 마당을 위아래로 훑어보며 대답한다."]], [["“Very true, sir.", "\"지당한 말이오."], ["Don’t you observe,” says Mr.", "못 느끼셨소,\""], ["Snagsby, pausing to sniff and taste the air a little, “don’t you observe, Mr. Weevle, that you’re—not to put too fine a point upon it—that you’re rather greasy here, sir?”", "스내그스비 씨가 잠시 멈춰 공기를 좀 킁킁대며 맛보더니 말한다, \"위블 씨, 못 느끼셨소, 여기가—에둘러 말할 것도 없이—여기가 좀 느끼하다는 걸 말이오?\""]], [["“Why, I have noticed myself that there is a queer kind of flavour in the place to-night,”", "\"실은 저도 오늘 밤 이곳에 묘한 냄새가 떠도는 걸 눈치챘습니다,\""], ["Mr. Weevle rejoins.", "위블 씨가 대꾸한다."], ["“I suppose it’s chops at the Sol’s Arms.”", "\"솔즈 암스에서 굽는 고기 토막인가 보죠.\""]], [["“Chops, do you think?", "\"고기 토막이라, 그렇게 생각하시오?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Chops, eh?” Mr.", "고기 토막, 그렇소?\""], ["Snagsby sniffs and tastes again.", "스내그스비 씨가 다시 킁킁대며 맛본다."], ["“Well, sir, I suppose it is.", "\"글쎄, 그렇겠죠."], ["But I should say their cook at the Sol wanted a little looking after.", "한데 내 보기에 솔의 그 요리사는 좀 단속이 필요하겠소."], ["She has been burning ’em, sir!", "고기를 태우고 있구먼!"], ["And I don’t think”—Mr. Snagsby sniffs and tastes again and then spits and wipes his mouth—“I don’t think—not to put too fine a point upon it—that they were quite fresh when they were shown the gridiron.”", "게다가 내 생각엔\"—스내그스비 씨가 또다시 킁킁대며 맛보더니 침을 뱉고 입을 훔친다—\"내 생각엔—에둘러 말할 것도 없이—그것들이 석쇠에 올려질 때 그리 싱싱하지도 않았던 것 같소.\""]], [["“That’s very likely.", "\"그럴 법하군요."], ["It’s a tainting sort of weather.”", "뭐든 상하기 좋은 날씨니까요.\""]], [["“It IS a tainting sort of weather,”", "\"상하기 좋은 날씨고말고,\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby, “and I find it sinking to the spirits.”", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다, \"게다가 마음까지 가라앉히는 것 같소.\""]], [["“By George!", "\"맙소사!"], ["I find it gives me the horrors,”", "저는 소름이 다 끼치는걸요.\""], ["returns Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 대답한다."]], [["“Then, you see, you live in a lonesome way, and in a lonesome room, with a black circumstance hanging over it,”", "\"하긴, 보시오, 댁은 외딴 식으로, 외딴 방에서, 시커먼 사연이 머리 위에 드리운 채 사니까요.\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby, looking in past the other’s shoulder along the dark passage and then falling back a step to look up at the house.", "스내그스비 씨가 상대의 어깨 너머로 어두운 통로 안쪽을 들여다보더니 한 발짝 물러서서 집을 올려다보며 말한다."], ["“I couldn’t live in that room alone, as you do, sir.", "\"나라면 댁처럼 저 방에서 혼자는 못 살겠소."], ["I should get so fidgety and worried of an evening, sometimes, that I should be driven to come to the door and stand here sooner than sit there.", "어떤 저녁이면 어찌나 안절부절못하고 마음이 졸아드는지, 거기 앉아 있느니 차라리 문간에 나와 이렇게 서 있고 싶어질 거요."], ["But then it’s very true that you didn’t see, in your room, what I saw there.", "하긴, 댁은 그 방에서 내가 거기서 본 걸 못 봤으니, 그건 사실이오."], ["That makes a difference.”", "그게 차이를 만들지요.\""]], [["“I know quite enough about it,”", "\"그것에 대해선 저도 알 만큼 압니다.\""], ["returns Tony.", "토니가 대답한다."]], [["“It’s not agreeable, is it?”", "\"기분 좋은 일은 아니지요, 그렇소?\""], ["pursues Mr. Snagsby, coughing his cough of mild persuasion behind his hand.", "스내그스비 씨가 손바닥으로 입을 가린 채 부드럽게 구슬리는 기침을 하며 말을 잇는다."], ["“Mr. Krook ought to consider it in the rent.", "\"크룩 씨가 집세에서 그걸 좀 헤아려줘야 마땅하오."], ["I hope he does, I am sure.”", "부디 그러길 바라오, 정말로.\""]], [["“I hope he does,”", "\"저도 그러길 바랍니다,\""], ["says Tony.", "토니가 말한다."], ["“But I doubt it.”", "\"하지만 글쎄요.\""]], [["“You find the rent too high, do you, sir?”", "\"집세가 너무 비싸다, 그 말이오?\""], ["returns the stationer.", "문구상이 되묻는다."], ["“Rents ARE high about here.", "\"이 근방 집세가 비싸긴 하죠."], ["I don’t know how it is exactly, but the law seems to put things up in price.", "어찌 된 영문인지 정확히는 모르지만, 법이라는 게 물건값을 올려놓는 모양이오."], ["Not,” adds Mr.", "그렇다고,\""], ["Snagsby with his apologetic cough, “that I mean to say a word against the profession I get my living by.”", "스내그스비 씨가 변명하는 기침을 덧붙이며 말한다, \"내가 벌어먹고 사는 그 업종을 험담하려는 건 아니오만.\""]], [["Mr. Weevle again glances up and down the court and then looks at the stationer.", "위블 씨가 다시 마당을 위아래로 훑어보더니 문구상을 바라본다."], ["Mr. Snagsby, blankly catching his eye, looks upward for a star or so and coughs a cough expressive of not exactly seeing his way out of this conversation.", "스내그스비 씨는 멍하니 그 눈길과 마주치자, 별이라도 하나 찾는 양 위를 올려다보고는, 이 대화에서 어떻게 빠져나갈지 도무지 길이 안 보인다는 투의 기침을 한다."]], [["“It’s a curious fact, sir,”", "\"참 묘한 일이오, 선생,\""], ["he observes, slowly rubbing his hands, “that he should have been—”", "그가 천천히 두 손을 비비며 말한다, \"그 사람이 하필—\""]], [["“Who’s he?”", "\"그 사람이라니 누구요?\""], ["interrupts Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 말을 자른다."]], [["“The deceased, you know,” says Mr.", "\"왜, 그 죽은 사람 말이오.\""], ["Snagsby, twitching his head and right eyebrow towards the staircase and tapping his acquaintance on the button.", "스내그스비 씨가 계단 쪽으로 고개와 오른쪽 눈썹을 씰룩이며, 아는 이의 단추를 톡톡 건드린다."]], [["“Ah, to be sure!”", "\"아, 그렇지!\""], ["returns the other as if he were not over-fond of the subject.", "상대가 그 화제를 그리 달가워하지 않는 양 대꾸한다."], ["“I thought we had done with him.”", "\"그 얘긴 끝낸 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["“I was only going to say it’s a curious fact, sir, that he should have come and lived here, and been one of my writers, and then that you should come and live here, and be one of my writers too.", "\"내 말은 그저, 참 묘한 일이라는 거요, 선생. 그 사람이 여기 와서 살았고 내 필경사 가운데 하나였는데, 이번엔 또 댁이 와서 살면서 역시 내 필경사가 되었으니 말이오."], ["Which there is nothing derogatory, but far from it in the appellation,” says Mr.", "그렇다고 그 호칭에 얕잡는 뜻이 있다는 건 아니오, 천만의 말이오,\""], ["Snagsby, breaking off with a mistrust that he may have unpolitely asserted a kind of proprietorship in Mr. Weevle, “because I have known writers that have gone into brewers’ houses and done really very respectable indeed.", "스내그스비 씨가 자기가 위블 씨에게 어떤 소유주 행세를 무례하게 내세운 건 아닐까 하는 미심쩍음에 말을 끊더니 잇는다, \"왜냐하면 양조업자 집에 들어가서 정말 아주 점잖게 잘 지낸 필경사들도 내가 알기 때문이오."], ["Eminently respectable, sir,”", "더없이 점잖게 말이오, 선생,\""], ["adds Mr. Snagsby with a misgiving that he has not improved the matter.", "스내그스비 씨가 외려 일을 그르친 게 아닐까 하는 께름칙함에 덧붙인다."]], [["“It’s a curious coincidence, as you say,”", "\"말씀대로 묘한 우연이군요.\""], ["answers Weevle, once more glancing up and down the court.", "위블이 또 한 번 마당을 위아래로 훑어보며 대답한다."]], [["“Seems a fate in it, don’t there?”", "\"무슨 운명 같은 게 깃든 것 같지 않소?\""], ["suggests the stationer.", "문구상이 넌지시 말한다."]], [["“There does.”", "\"그러네요.\""]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그거요.\""], ["observes the stationer with his confirmatory cough.", "문구상이 자기 말을 굳히는 기침과 함께 말한다."], ["“Quite a fate in it.", "\"아주 운명적이오."], ["Quite a fate. Well, Mr.", "영락없는 운명."], ["Weevle, I am afraid I must bid you good night”—Mr. Snagsby speaks as if it made him desolate to go, though he has been casting about for any means of escape ever since he stopped to speak—“my little woman will be looking for me else.", "자, 위블 씨, 미안하지만 이만 작별 인사를 드려야겠소\"—스내그스비 씨는 말을 멈추고 입을 연 그 순간부터 줄곧 빠져나갈 핑계를 찾고 있었으면서도, 마치 떠나기가 못내 서운하다는 투로 말한다—\"안 그러면 우리 마누라가 날 찾아 나설 테니."], ["Good night, sir!”", "안녕히 계시오, 선생!\""]], [["If Mr. Snagsby hastens home to save his little woman the trouble of looking for him, he might set his mind at rest on that score.", "스내그스비 씨가 마누라더러 자기를 찾아 나서는 수고를 덜어주려고 부랴부랴 집으로 향하는 것이라면, 그 점은 마음을 푹 놓아도 좋다."], ["His little woman has had her eye upon him round the Sol’s Arms all this time and now glides after him with a pocket handkerchief wrapped over her head, honouring Mr. Weevle and his doorway with a searching glance as she goes past.", "그의 마누라는 줄곧 솔즈 암스 언저리에서 그를 눈여겨보고 있었으니, 이제 손수건을 머리에 둘러쓴 채 그를 뒤따라 미끄러지듯 다가오며, 지나는 길에 위블 씨와 그의 문간에 캐묻는 듯한 눈길을 베푼다."]], [["“You’ll know me again, ma’am, at all events,” says Mr.", "\"여하튼 부인은 날 다시 알아보겠구먼,\""], ["Weevle to himself; “and I can’t compliment you on your appearance, whoever you are, with your head tied up in a bundle.", "위블 씨가 혼잣말을 한다. \"한데 머리를 보따리처럼 동여맨 그 꼴을 보니, 당신이 누구든 간에 그 행색에 대해선 칭찬할 말이 없네요."], ["Is this fellow NEVER coming!”", "이 친구는 대체 왜 안 오는 거야!\""]], [["This fellow approaches as he speaks. Mr.", "그가 말하는 사이 그 친구가 다가온다."], ["Weevle softly holds up his finger, and draws him into the passage, and closes the street door. Then they go upstairs, Mr.", "위블 씨는 살그머니 손가락을 들어 보이며 그를 통로 안으로 끌어들이고는 거리 쪽 문을 닫는다."], ["Weevle heavily, and Mr. Guppy (for it is he) very lightly indeed.", "그러고 나서 두 사람이 위층으로 올라가는데, 위블 씨는 묵직하게, 거피 씨는(그가 바로 그 친구다) 정말이지 아주 가뿐하게 올라간다."], ["When they are shut into the back room, they speak low.", "안쪽 방에 들어가 문을 닫자, 두 사람은 낮은 목소리로 말한다."]], [["“I thought you had gone to Jericho at least instead of coming here,”", "\"여기 오는 대신 적어도 머나먼 예리코까지 간 줄 알았네.\""], ["says Tony.", "토니가 말한다."]], [["“Why, I said about ten.”", "\"아니, 열 시쯤이라고 했잖아.\""]], [["“You said about ten,”", "\"열 시쯤이라고 했지,\""], ["Tony repeats.", "토니가 되받는다."], ["“Yes, so you did say about ten.", "\"그래, 열 시쯤이라고 하긴 했어."], ["But according to my count, it’s ten times ten—it’s a hundred o’clock.", "한데 내 셈으론 열 곱하기 열이야—백 시쯤이라고."], ["I never had such a night in my life!”", "내 평생 이런 밤은 처음일세!\""]], [["“What has been the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이 있었는데?\""]], [["“That’s it!” says Tony.", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["“Nothing has been the matter.", "토니가 말한다. \"아무 일도 없었어."], ["But here have I been stewing and fuming in this jolly old crib till I have had the horrors falling on me as thick as hail.", "한데 이 멋들어진 옛날 굴속에서 끓고 부글대고 하다 보니, 소름이 우박처럼 마구 쏟아져 내리더라고."], ["THERE’S a blessed-looking candle!”", "저기 저 복받은 꼴의 촛불 좀 보게!\""], ["says Tony, pointing to the heavily burning taper on his table with a great cabbage head and a long winding-sheet.", "토니가 탁자 위에서 묵직하게 타들어가는, 양배추통만 한 심지에 길게 늘어진 수의 같은 촛농을 늘어뜨린 양초를 가리키며 말한다."]], [["“That’s easily improved,” Mr.", "\"그건 쉽게 고칠 수 있지.\""], ["Guppy observes as he takes the snuffers in hand.", "거피 씨가 심지 가위를 집어 들며 말한다."]], [["“IS it?”", "\"그래?\""], ["returns his friend.", "친구가 대꾸한다."], ["“Not so easily as you think.", "\"자네 생각만큼 쉽지 않아."], ["It has been smouldering like that ever since it was lighted.”", "불을 붙인 뒤로 줄곧 저렇게 거무튀튀하게 타고 있다고.\""]], [["“Why, what’s the matter with you, Tony?”", "\"아니, 자네 대체 무슨 일이야, 토니?\""], ["inquires Mr. Guppy, looking at him, snuffers in hand, as he sits down with his elbow on the table.", "거피 씨가 심지 가위를 손에 든 채, 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉은 그를 바라보며 묻는다."]], [["“William Guppy,”", "\"윌리엄 거피,\""], ["replies the other, “I am in the downs.", "상대가 대답한다, \"나 영 가라앉았어."], ["It’s this unbearably dull, suicidal room—and old Boguey downstairs, I suppose.” Mr.", "이 견딜 수 없이 음침하고, 자살이라도 부를 것 같은 방에다—아래층의 그 늙은 도깨비 영감 탓이겠지.\""], ["Weevle moodily pushes the snuffers-tray from him with his elbow, leans his head on his hand, puts his feet on the fender, and looks at the fire. Mr.", "위블 씨가 시무룩하게 팔꿈치로 심지 가위 받침을 밀쳐내고, 손에 머리를 기대고, 두 발을 난로 가리개에 얹은 채 불을 바라본다."], ["Guppy, observing him, slightly tosses his head and sits down on the other side of the table in an easy attitude.", "거피 씨는 그를 살피며 가볍게 고개를 까딱이고는, 탁자 맞은편에 느긋한 자세로 앉는다."]], [["“Wasn’t that Snagsby talking to you, Tony?”", "\"방금 자네한테 말 걸던 게 스내그스비 아니었나, 토니?\""]], [["“Yes, and he—yes, it was Snagsby,” said Mr.", "\"맞아, 그리고 그가—그래, 스내그스비였어.\""], ["Weevle, altering the construction of his sentence.", "위블 씨가 문장의 짜임새를 바꿔가며 말한다."]], [["“On business?”", "\"무슨 볼일로?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["No business.", "볼일은 없어."], ["He was only sauntering by and stopped to prose.”", "그냥 어슬렁대며 지나가다 멈춰서 주절댄 거지.\""]], [["“I thought it was Snagsby,”", "\"스내그스비인 줄 알았어,\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, “and thought it as well that he shouldn’t see me, so I waited till he was gone.”", "거피 씨가 말한다, \"그래서 그가 날 못 보는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 그가 갈 때까지 기다린 거야.\""]], [["“There we go again, William G.!”", "\"또 시작이군, 윌리엄 G.!\""], ["cried Tony, looking up for an instant.", "토니가 잠깐 고개를 들고 소리친다."], ["“So mysterious and secret!", "\"어찌나 수상쩍고 은밀한지!"], ["By George, if we were going to commit a murder, we couldn’t have more mystery about it!”", "맙소사, 우리가 살인이라도 저지르려는 참이라면 이보다 더 음험하게 굴 수도 없겠다!\""]], [["Mr.", "거피 씨는 짐짓 미소 짓는 척하며, 화제를 돌릴 요량으로, 방 안에 걸린 '영국 미인 은하 화랑'을 진짜든 가짜든 감탄 어린 눈길로 둘러보다가, 벽난로 선반 위에 걸린 데드록 마님의 초상화에서 그 둘러봄을 끝맺는다."], ["Guppy affects to smile, and with the view of changing the conversation, looks with an admiration, real or pretended, round the room at the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty, terminating his survey with the portrait of Lady Dedlock over the mantelshelf, in which she is represented on a terrace, with a pedestal upon the terrace, and a vase upon the pedestal, and her shawl upon the vase, and a prodigious piece of fur upon the shawl, and her arm on the prodigious piece of fur, and a bracelet on her arm.", "그림 속 그녀는 테라스에 서 있고, 테라스 위에는 받침대가, 받침대 위에는 항아리가, 항아리 위에는 그녀의 숄이, 숄 위에는 엄청난 모피 한 덩이가, 그 엄청난 모피 위에는 그녀의 팔이, 그 팔에는 팔찌가 얹혀 있다."]], [["“That’s very like Lady Dedlock,”", "\"저거 데드록 마님이랑 꼭 닮았네.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“It’s a speaking likeness.”", "\"말이라도 할 것 같은 닮은꼴이야.\""]], [["“I wish it was,”", "\"닮은꼴이면 좋으련만.\""], ["growls Tony, without changing his position.", "토니가 자세도 바꾸지 않은 채 으르렁대듯 말한다."], ["“I should have some fashionable conversation, here, then.”", "\"그럼 나도 여기서 상류층 대화나 좀 나눌 텐데.\""]], [["Finding by this time that his friend is not to be wheedled into a more sociable humour, Mr. Guppy puts about upon the ill-used tack and remonstrates with him.", "이쯤 되자 친구를 어르고 달랜다고 해서 좀 더 붙임성 있는 기분으로 끌어낼 수 없음을 알아채고, 거피 씨는 박대당한 자의 방향으로 뱃머리를 돌려 그를 나무란다."]], [["“Tony,”", "\"토니,\""], ["says he, “I can make allowances for lowness of spirits, for no man knows what it is when it does come upon a man better than I do, and no man perhaps has a better right to know it than a man who has an unrequited image imprinted on his ’eart.", "그가 말한다, \"기분이 가라앉는 건 내가 봐줄 수 있어. 그게 사람한테 닥쳤을 때 어떤 건지, 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람도 없고, 가슴에 짝사랑의 형상을 새긴 사람보다 그걸 더 잘 알 권리가 있는 사람도 아마 없을 테니까."], ["But there are bounds to these things when an unoffending party is in question, and I will acknowledge to you, Tony, that I don’t think your manner on the present occasion is hospitable or quite gentlemanly.”", "하지만 죄 없는 사람이 끼어 있을 땐, 이런 일에도 한도가 있는 법이야. 그래서 자네한테 인정하건대, 토니, 이번 경우에 자네 태도는 손님을 대접하는 것도, 신사다운 것도 아니라고 봐.\""]], [["“This is strong language, William Guppy,”", "\"말이 좀 세군, 윌리엄 거피.\""], ["returns Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 받아친다."]], [["“Sir, it may be,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지,\""], ["retorts Mr. William Guppy, “but I feel strongly when I use it.”", "윌리엄 거피 씨가 되받는다, \"하지만 이 말을 할 땐 마음이 진짜 거센걸.\""]], [["Mr. Weevle admits that he has been wrong and begs Mr. William Guppy to think no more about it. Mr. William Guppy, however, having got the advantage, cannot quite release it without a little more injured remonstrance.", "위블 씨는 자기가 잘못했음을 인정하고, 윌리엄 거피 씨에게 더는 마음에 두지 말아 달라고 청한다. 그러나 윌리엄 거피 씨는 이미 우위를 잡은 터라, 상처받은 자의 책망을 조금 더 늘어놓지 않고는 도무지 그 우위를 놓아주지 못한다."]], [["“No!", "\"아니!"], ["Dash it, Tony,”", "제기랄, 토니,\""], ["says that gentleman, “you really ought to be careful how you wound the feelings of a man who has an unrequited image imprinted on his ’eart and who is NOT altogether happy in those chords which vibrate to the tenderest emotions.", "그분이 말한다, \"자네 정말이지 조심해야 해. 가슴에 짝사랑의 형상을 새긴 데다, 가장 여린 감정에 떨리는 그 심금이 영 온전치 못한 사람의 마음을 어떻게 상하게 하는지 말이야."], ["You, Tony, possess in yourself all that is calculated to charm the eye and allure the taste.", "자네는, 토니, 눈을 호리고 취향을 꾈 만한 모든 걸 다 갖췄어."], ["It is not—happily for you, perhaps, and I may wish that I could say the same—it is not your character to hover around one flower.", "자네는—아마 자네한텐 다행스럽게도, 나도 똑같이 말할 수 있다면 좋겠다만—한 송이 꽃 둘레만 맴도는 그런 성격이 아니지."], ["The ole garden is open to you, and your airy pinions carry you through it.", "정원 전체가 자네한테 활짝 열려 있고, 자네의 가뿐한 날개가 자넬 그 정원 곳곳으로 실어 나르거든."], ["Still, Tony, far be it from me, I am sure, to wound even your feelings without a cause!”", "그래도 토니, 정말이지, 까닭도 없이 자네 마음마저 상하게 할 마음은 내게 결코 없다네!\""]], [["Tony again entreats that the subject may be no longer pursued, saying emphatically, “William Guppy, drop it!” Mr.", "토니는 다시금 그 얘긴 더 이어가지 말아 달라 간청하며, 힘주어 말한다, \"윌리엄 거피, 그만 좀 해!\""], ["Guppy acquiesces, with the reply, “I never should have taken it up, Tony, of my own accord.”", "거피 씨가 수긍하며 대답한다, \"내 발로 먼저 꺼낼 얘긴 절대 아니었어, 토니.\""]], [["“And now,”", "\"자, 이제,\""], ["says Tony, stirring the fire, “touching this same bundle of letters.", "토니가 불을 쑤시며 말한다, \"그 편지 꾸러미 얘긴데."], ["Isn’t it an extraordinary thing of Krook to have appointed twelve o’clock to-night to hand ’em over to me?”", "크룩이 그걸 나한테 넘기겠다고 오늘 밤 열두 시로 시각을 정한 거, 별난 일 아닌가?\""]], [["“Very.", "\"별나지."], ["What did he do it for?”", "왜 그런 거래?\""]], [["“What does he do anything for?", "\"그 양반이 뭘 하든 무슨 까닭이 있겠나?"], ["HE don’t know.", "자기도 몰라."], ["Said to-day was his birthday and he’d hand ’em over to-night at twelve o’clock.", "오늘이 자기 생일이라면서, 오늘 밤 열두 시에 넘겨주겠다더군."], ["He’ll have drunk himself blind by that time.", "그때쯤이면 곤드레만드레가 돼 있겠지."], ["He has been at it all day.”", "온종일 퍼마셨으니까.\""]], [["“He hasn’t forgotten the appointment, I hope?”", "\"약속을 잊진 않았겠지?\""]], [["“Forgotten?", "\"잊어?"], ["Trust him for that.", "그 양반은 절대 안 그래."], ["He never forgets anything.", "뭐든 잊는 법이 없거든."], ["I saw him to-night, about eight—helped him to shut up his shop—and he had got the letters then in his hairy cap.", "오늘 밤 여덟 시쯤 그 양반을 봤어—가게 문 닫는 걸 거들었지—그때 이미 그 편지들을 털북숭이 모자 속에 넣어두고 있더라고."], ["He pulled it off and showed ’em me.", "모자를 벗어서 나한테 보여줬어."], ["When the shop was closed, he took them out of his cap, hung his cap on the chair-back, and stood turning them over before the fire.", "가게 문을 닫고 나선, 모자에서 그걸 꺼내, 모자는 의자 등받이에 걸어두고, 불 앞에 서서 그걸 뒤적였어."], ["I heard him a little while afterwards, through the floor here, humming like the wind, the only song he knows—about Bibo, and old Charon, and Bibo being drunk when he died, or something or other.", "조금 있다가 여기 마룻바닥 너머로 들리던데, 바람처럼 흥얼대더라고. 그 양반이 아는 유일한 노래—비보랑 늙은 카론에 관한, 비보가 죽을 때 취해 있었다나 뭐라나 하는 노래 말이야."], ["He has been as quiet since as an old rat asleep in his hole.”", "그 뒤론 제 굴속에서 잠든 늙은 쥐처럼 쥐 죽은 듯 조용해.\""]], [["“And you are to go down at twelve?”", "\"그래서 자넨 열두 시에 내려가는 거고?\""]], [["“At twelve.", "\"열두 시에."], ["And as I tell you, when you came it seemed to me a hundred.”", "그러니 말했듯이, 자네가 왔을 땐 나한텐 백 시쯤으로 느껴졌다고.\""]], [["“Tony,”", "\"토니,\""], ["says Mr. Guppy after considering a little with his legs crossed, “he can’t read yet, can he?”", "거피 씨가 다리를 꼰 채 잠깐 곰곰이 생각하더니 말한다, \"그 양반 아직 글을 못 읽지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Read!", "\"읽어!"], ["He’ll never read.", "평생 못 읽어."], ["He can make all the letters separately, and he knows most of them separately when he sees them; he has got on that much, under me; but he can’t put them together.", "글자를 하나하나 따로 쓸 줄은 알고, 대부분은 보면 따로따로 알아보긴 해. 거기까진 내 밑에서 익혔지."], ["He’s too old to acquire the knack of it now—and too drunk.”", "하지만 그걸 한데 엮질 못해. 이제 그 요령을 익히기엔 너무 늙었어—게다가 너무 취해 있고.\""]], [["“Tony,”", "\"토니,\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, uncrossing and recrossing his legs, “how do you suppose he spelt out that name of Hawdon?”", "거피 씨가 다리를 풀었다가 다시 꼬며 말한다, \"그 양반이 호든이라는 그 이름을 어떻게 떠듬떠듬 읽어냈을 것 같아?\""]], [["“He never spelt it out.", "\"떠듬떠듬 읽은 게 아니야."], ["You know what a curious power of eye he has and how he has been used to employ himself in copying things by eye alone.", "그 양반 눈썰미가 얼마나 유별난지, 또 오로지 눈으로만 베껴 그리는 일에 얼마나 이골이 났는지 자네도 알잖아."], ["He imitated it, evidently from the direction of a letter, and asked me what it meant.”", "분명 어떤 편지 겉봉을 보고 그걸 그대로 본떠 그린 다음, 나한테 무슨 뜻이냐고 물은 거지.\""]], [["“Tony,” says Mr.", "\"토니,\""], ["Guppy, uncrossing and recrossing his legs again, “should you say that the original was a man’s writing or a woman’s?”", "거피 씨가 다시 다리를 풀었다가 또 꼬며 말한다, \"그 원본이 남자 글씨라고 보나, 여자 글씨라고 보나?\""]], [["“A woman’s.", "\"여자 글씨야."], ["Fifty to one a lady’s—slopes a good deal, and the end of the letter ‘n,’ long and hasty.”", "십중팔구 귀부인 거지—꽤나 비스듬히 기울었고, 글자 'n'의 끝이 길고 황급하거든.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy has been biting his thumb-nail during this dialogue, generally changing the thumb when he has changed the cross leg.", "거피 씨는 이 대화가 오가는 내내 엄지손톱을 물어뜯고 있었으니, 으레 꼰 다리를 바꿀 때마다 엄지도 바꿔 물었다."], ["As he is going to do so again, he happens to look at his coat-sleeve.", "또 그러려는 참에, 우연히 자기 외투 소맷부리를 보게 된다."], ["It takes his attention.", "그게 그의 눈길을 사로잡는다."], ["He stares at it, aghast.", "그가 기겁하여 그것을 빤히 본다."]], [["“Why, Tony, what on earth is going on in this house to-night?", "\"아니, 토니, 오늘 밤 이 집에서 대체 무슨 일이 벌어지는 거야?"], ["Is there a chimney on fire?”", "굴뚝에 불이라도 났나?\""]], [["“Chimney on fire!”", "\"굴뚝에 불이라니!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"이거 봐!\""], ["returns Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 대꾸한다."], ["“See how the soot’s falling.", "\"그을음이 어떻게 떨어지는지 좀 봐."], ["See here, on my arm!", "여기, 내 팔 위에!"], ["See again, on the table here!", "또 여기, 이 탁자 위에도!"], ["Confound the stuff, it won’t blow off—smears like black fat!”", "이 망할 것이, 불어도 안 떨어지네—시커먼 기름처럼 번져!\""]], [["They look at one another, and Tony goes listening to the door, and a little way upstairs, and a little way downstairs.", "두 사람은 서로를 바라보고, 토니는 문가에 가서 귀를 기울이고, 위층으로 조금, 아래층으로 조금 가본다."], ["Comes back and says it’s all right and all quiet, and quotes the remark he lately made to Mr. Snagsby about their cooking chops at the Sol’s Arms.", "돌아와서는 다 멀쩡하고 다 조용하다 말하며, 조금 전 솔즈 암스에서 고기 토막을 굽는다고 스내그스비 씨에게 했던 말을 다시 읊는다."]], [["“And it was then,” resumes Mr.", "\"그러니까 그때였단 말이지,\""], ["Guppy, still glancing with remarkable aversion at the coat-sleeve, as they pursue their conversation before the fire, leaning on opposite sides of the table, with their heads very near together, “that he told you of his having taken the bundle of letters from his lodger’s portmanteau?”", "거피 씨가 외투 소맷부리를 여전히 몹시 꺼림칙한 눈길로 흘끔거리며 다시 말을 잇는다. 두 사람은 불 앞에서 탁자를 사이에 두고 양쪽에 기대 머리를 바싹 맞댄 채 대화를 이어간다, \"그 양반이 자기 하숙인의 여행 가방에서 편지 꾸러미를 꺼냈다고 자네한테 말한 게?\""]], [["“That was the time, sir,”", "\"바로 그때였지, 친구.\""], ["answers Tony, faintly adjusting his whiskers.", "토니가 구레나룻을 힘없이 매만지며 대답한다."], ["“Whereupon I wrote a line to my dear boy, the Honourable William Guppy, informing him of the appointment for to-night and advising him not to call before, Boguey being a slyboots.”", "\"그래서 내가 친애하는 친구, 명예로우신 윌리엄 거피 군에게 한 줄 적어 보냈잖아. 오늘 밤 약속을 알리면서, 그 도깨비 영감이 워낙 음흉한 자라 그전엔 들르지 말라고 일러준 거지.\""]], [["The light vivacious tone of fashionable life which is usually assumed by Mr. Weevle sits so ill upon him to-night that he abandons that and his whiskers together, and after looking over his shoulder, appears to yield himself up a prey to the horrors again.", "위블 씨가 으레 꾸며 보이는 상류 사교계의 가볍고 활기찬 말투가 오늘 밤은 어찌나 그에게 어울리지 않는지, 그는 그 말투를 구레나룻과 함께 내던지고는, 어깨 너머를 돌아본 뒤, 다시금 그 소름의 먹잇감으로 자신을 내맡긴 듯 보인다."]], [["“You are to bring the letters to your room to read and compare, and to get yourself into a position to tell him all about them.", "\"자넨 그 편지들을 자네 방으로 가져와 읽고 견주어, 그 양반한테 그것들에 대해 죄다 일러줄 수 있는 처지가 되어야 하고."], ["That’s the arrangement, isn’t it, Tony?”", "그게 약속이지, 안 그래, 토니?\""], ["asks Mr. Guppy, anxiously biting his thumb-nail.", "거피 씨가 초조하게 엄지손톱을 물어뜯으며 묻는다."]], [["“You can’t speak too low.", "\"아무리 낮춰 말해도 지나치지 않아."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["That’s what he and I agreed.”", "그 양반이랑 내가 그렇게 합의했어.\""]], [["“I tell you what, Tony—”", "\"내 말 좀 들어봐, 토니—\""]], [["“You can’t speak too low,”", "\"아무리 낮춰 말해도 지나치지 않다니까.\""], ["says Tony once more.", "토니가 한 번 더 말한다."], ["Mr. Guppy nods his sagacious head, advances it yet closer, and drops into a whisper.", "거피 씨가 영민한 고개를 끄덕이고는, 그것을 한층 더 가까이 들이밀더니, 속삭임으로 잦아든다."]], [["“I tell you what.", "\"내 말 들어봐."], ["The first thing to be done is to make another packet like the real one so that if he should ask to see the real one while it’s in my possession, you can show him the dummy.”", "제일 먼저 할 일은 진짜랑 똑같은 꾸러미를 하나 더 만드는 거야. 그래야 진짜가 내 손에 있는 동안 그 양반이 진짜를 보자고 하면, 자네가 가짜를 보여줄 수 있잖아.\""]], [["“And suppose he detects the dummy as soon as he sees it, which with his biting screw of an eye is about five hundred times more likely than not,”", "\"그 양반이 보자마자 가짜인 걸 알아채면 어쩌고. 그 깨물 듯 파고드는 눈초리로 봐선, 못 알아챌 가능성보다 알아챌 가능성이 오백 배쯤 더 높을 텐데.\""], ["suggests Tony.", "토니가 넌지시 말한다."]], [["“Then we’ll face it out.", "\"그럼 우린 정면으로 맞서는 거야."], ["They don’t belong to him, and they never did.", "그건 그 양반 것이 아니고, 애당초 그 양반 것이었던 적도 없어."], ["You found that, and you placed them in my hands—a legal friend of yours—for security.", "자네가 그걸 발견했고, 안전하게 보관하라고—자네의 법률가 친구인—내 손에 맡긴 거지."], ["If he forces us to it, they’ll be producible, won’t they?”", "그 양반이 우릴 거기까지 몰아붙이면, 내놓으면 될 거 아냐, 안 그래?\""]], [["“Ye-es,” is Mr.", "\"그-렇지.\""], ["Weevle’s reluctant admission.", "위블 씨가 마지못해 인정한다."]], [["“Why, Tony,”", "\"아니, 토니,\""], ["remonstrates his friend, “how you look!", "친구가 나무란다, \"그 표정은 또 뭐야!"], ["You don’t doubt William Guppy?", "자네 이 윌리엄 거피를 의심하는 거야?"], ["You don’t suspect any harm?”", "무슨 나쁜 일이라도 있을까 봐 그래?\""]], [["“I don’t suspect anything more than I know, William,”", "\"난 내가 아는 것 이상은 의심하지 않아, 윌리엄.\""], ["returns the other gravely.", "상대가 진지하게 대답한다."]], [["“And what do you know?” urges Mr.", "\"그래서 자네가 아는 게 뭔데?\""], ["Guppy, raising his voice a little; but on his friend’s once more warning him, “I tell you, you can’t speak too low,”", "거피 씨가 목소리를 조금 높이며 다그치지만, 친구가 또 한 번 \"낮춰 말해도 지나치지 않다니까\""], ["he repeats his question without any sound at all, forming with his lips only the words, “What do you know?”", "하고 주의를 주자, 아무런 소리도 없이 입술 모양만으로 \"자네가 아는 게 뭔데?\"라는 말을 지어 보이며 그 물음을 되풀이한다."]], [["“I know three things.", "\"난 세 가지를 알아."], ["First, I know that here we are whispering in secrecy, a pair of conspirators.”", "첫째, 지금 우리 둘이 음모꾼 한 쌍처럼 은밀하게 속삭이고 있다는 거.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래서!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“And we had better be that than a pair of noodles, which we should be if we were doing anything else, for it’s the only way of doing what we want to do.", "\"그래도 멍청이 한 쌍보다는 그 편이 낫지. 다른 짓을 하고 있다면 우린 영락없는 멍청이 꼴일 테니까."], ["Secondly?”", "우리가 하려는 일을 해내는 길이 이것뿐이거든. 둘째는?\""]], [["“Secondly, it’s not made out to me how it’s likely to be profitable, after all.”", "\"둘째, 이게 결국 어떻게 돈벌이가 될지가 나한텐 도무지 안 잡혀.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy casts up his eyes at the portrait of Lady Dedlock over the mantelshelf and replies, “Tony, you are asked to leave that to the honour of your friend.", "거피 씨는 벽난로 선반 위 데드록 마님의 초상화로 눈을 치켜뜨고는 대답한다, \"토니, 그건 자네 친구의 명예에 맡겨두라고 부탁받는 거야."], ["Besides its being calculated to serve that friend in those chords of the human mind which—which need not be called into agonizing vibration on the present occasion—your friend is no fool.", "게다가 그게 그 친구한테, 인간 마음의 그 심금에서—이번 경우에 굳이 고통스럽게 떨리도록 불러낼 것까진 없는 그 심금에서—도움이 되도록 계산된 일이라는 점은 차치하더라도, 자네 친구는 바보가 아니거든."], ["What’s that?”", "저게 무슨 소리지?\""]], [["“It’s eleven o’clock striking by the bell of Saint Paul’s.", "\"성 바울 성당 종이 열한 시를 치는 거야. 귀 기울여 봐."], ["Listen and you’ll hear all the bells in the city jangling.”", "도시의 온 종이 어수선하게 뒤섞여 울리는 게 들릴 테니.\""]], [["Both sit silent, listening to the metal voices, near and distant, resounding from towers of various heights, in tones more various than their situations.", "두 사람 다 말없이 앉아, 가까이서 또 멀리서, 높이가 제각각인 종탑에서 울려 퍼지는 그 쇠붙이 목소리들에 귀 기울인다. 그 음색은 종탑들의 위치보다도 한결 제각각이다."], ["When these at length cease, all seems more mysterious and quiet than before.", "마침내 그 소리들이 그치자, 모든 것이 전보다 더 으스스하고 고요해 보인다."], ["One disagreeable result of whispering is that it seems to evoke an atmosphere of silence, haunted by the ghosts of sound—strange cracks and tickings, the rustling of garments that have no substance in them, and the tread of dreadful feet that would leave no mark on the sea-sand or the winter snow.", "속삭임의 한 가지 고약한 결과는, 그것이 침묵의 분위기를 불러내는 듯하다는 것이니, 그 침묵에는 소리의 유령들—기이한 삐걱임과 똑딱임, 알맹이라곤 없는 옷자락이 스치는 소리, 바닷가 모래나 겨울 눈 위에 아무 자국도 남기지 않을 무시무시한 발걸음 소리—이 깃든다."], ["So sensitive the two friends happen to be that the air is full of these phantoms, and the two look over their shoulders by one consent to see that the door is shut.", "마침 두 친구가 어찌나 예민해진 상태인지, 공기가 이런 허깨비들로 가득 차고, 두 사람은 누가 먼저랄 것도 없이 어깨 너머를 돌아보며 문이 닫혀 있는지 확인한다."]], [["“Yes, Tony?” says Mr.", "\"그래서, 토니?\""], ["Guppy, drawing nearer to the fire and biting his unsteady thumb-nail.", "거피 씨가 불 쪽으로 더 가까이 다가앉으며, 떨리는 엄지손톱을 물어뜯는다."], ["“You were going to say, thirdly?”", "\"셋째를 말하려던 참이었지?\""]], [["“It’s far from a pleasant thing to be plotting about a dead man in the room where he died, especially when you happen to live in it.”", "\"죽은 사람을 두고, 그 사람이 죽은 방에서 음모를 꾸미는 건 결코 유쾌한 일이 아니야. 더구나 자네가 하필 그 방에 살고 있다면 더더욱.\""]], [["“But we are plotting nothing against him, Tony.”", "\"하지만 우리가 그 양반한테 못된 짓을 꾸미는 건 아니잖아, 토니.\""]], [["“May be not, still I don’t like it.", "\"아닐지도 모르지. 그래도 난 영 싫어."], ["Live here by yourself and see how YOU like it.”", "자네가 여기서 혼자 살아보고 자넨 얼마나 좋은지 한번 봐.\""]], [["“As to dead men, Tony,” proceeds Mr.", "\"죽은 사람들 얘기라면, 토니,\""], ["Guppy, evading this proposal, “there have been dead men in most rooms.”", "거피 씨가 그 제안을 슬쩍 피하며 말을 잇는다, \"대개의 방마다 죽은 사람이 있었어.\""]], [["“I know there have, but in most rooms you let them alone, and—and they let you alone,”", "\"그건 나도 알아. 하지만 대개의 방에선 그들을 가만 내버려두고, 그러면—그러면 그들도 자넬 가만 내버려두지.\""], ["Tony answers.", "토니가 대답한다."]], [["The two look at each other again. Mr.", "두 사람은 다시 서로를 바라본다."], ["Guppy makes a hurried remark to the effect that they may be doing the deceased a service, that he hopes so. There is an oppressive blank until Mr.", "거피 씨가 어쩌면 우리가 죽은 이에게 도움을 주는 셈일지도 모른다고, 부디 그러길 바란다고 황급히 한마디 던진다."], ["Weevle, by stirring the fire suddenly, makes Mr. Guppy start as if his heart had been stirred instead.", "짓누르는 듯한 적막이 흐르다가, 위블 씨가 느닷없이 불을 쑤시는 바람에, 거피 씨는 마치 자기 심장이 쑤셔진 양 흠칫 놀란다."]], [["“Fah!", "\"우엑!"], ["Here’s more of this hateful soot hanging about,”", "이 지긋지긋한 그을음이 또 떠다니네.\" 그가 말한다."], ["says he. “Let us open the window a bit and get a mouthful of air.", "\"창문 좀 열어서 바람이라도 한 모금 들이마시자."], ["It’s too close.”", "너무 후텁지근해.\""]], [["He raises the sash, and they both rest on the window-sill, half in and half out of the room.", "그가 창틀을 올리자, 두 사람은 방 안에 반쯤, 밖에 반쯤 걸친 채 창턱에 기댄다."], ["The neighbouring houses are too near to admit of their seeing any sky without craning their necks and looking up, but lights in frowsy windows here and there, and the rolling of distant carriages, and the new expression that there is of the stir of men, they find to be comfortable.", "이웃한 집들이 너무 가까워서 목을 길게 빼고 올려다보지 않고는 하늘 한 조각 볼 수 없지만, 여기저기 꾀죄죄한 창문의 불빛이며, 멀리서 굴러가는 마차 소리며, 사람들의 부산함이 자아내는 그 새로운 기색이, 두 사람에겐 위안이 된다."], ["Mr. Guppy, noiselessly tapping on the window-sill, resumes his whispering in quite a light-comedy tone.", "거피 씨가 소리 없이 창턱을 톡톡 두드리며, 자못 가벼운 희극조로 다시 속삭이기 시작한다."]], [["“By the by, Tony, don’t forget old Smallweed,”", "\"참, 그건 그렇고, 토니, 그 늙은 스몰위드 잊지 말게.\""], ["meaning the younger of that name.", "그 집안의 젊은 쪽을 가리키는 말이다."], ["“I have not let him into this, you know.", "\"내가 그 친군 이 일에 안 끼워줬거든, 알지."], ["That grandfather of his is too keen by half.", "그 할아비라는 작자가 도가 지나치게 빈틈없어서 말이야."], ["It runs in the family.”", "그게 집안 내력이거든.\""]], [["“I remember,”", "\"기억하고 있어,\""], ["says Tony.", "토니가 말한다."], ["“I am up to all that.”", "\"그건 죄다 꿰고 있다고.\""]], [["“And as to Krook,”", "\"그리고 크룩 얘긴데,\""], ["resumes Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 다시 말을 잇는다."], ["“Now, do you suppose he really has got hold of any other papers of importance, as he has boasted to you, since you have been such allies?”", "\"자, 그 양반이 자네한테 큰소리친 대로, 자네들이 그렇게 동맹을 맺은 뒤로 정말 다른 중요한 서류를 손에 넣은 것 같아?\""]], [["Tony shakes his head.", "토니가 고개를 젓는다."], ["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠어."], ["Can’t imagine.", "짐작도 안 가."], ["If we get through this business without rousing his suspicions, I shall be better informed, no doubt.", "그 양반 의심을 사지 않고 이 일을 잘 마치면, 틀림없이 더 알게 되겠지."], ["How can I know without seeing them, when he don’t know himself?", "그 양반 자신도 모르는데, 내가 그걸 보지도 않고 어찌 알겠나?"], ["He is always spelling out words from them, and chalking them over the table and the shop-wall, and asking what this is and what that is; but his whole stock from beginning to end may easily be the waste-paper he bought it as, for anything I can say.", "노상 거기서 단어들을 떠듬떠듬 읽어내선, 탁자랑 가게 벽에다 분필로 끄적이고, 이건 뭐고 저건 뭐냐고 물어대지. 하지만 내가 보기엔, 처음부터 끝까지 그 양반 재고라는 게, 애당초 그 양반이 사들일 때 그랬던 그대로 그냥 휴지 뭉치일 수도 있어."], ["It’s a monomania with him to think he is possessed of documents.", "자기가 문서를 잔뜩 지녔다고 여기는 게 그 양반의 외곬 망상이거든."], ["He has been going to learn to read them this last quarter of a century, I should judge, from what he tells me.”", "그 양반이 나한테 한 말로 미루어 보건대, 지난 사반세기 동안 줄곧 그걸 읽는 법을 배우려고 들었던 것 같아.\""]], [["“How did he first come by that idea, though?", "\"그래도 애초에 그 생각을 어떻게 품게 된 걸까?"], ["That’s the question,”", "그게 문제야.\""], ["Mr. Guppy suggests with one eye shut, after a little forensic meditation.", "거피 씨가 한쪽 눈을 감고 잠시 법정에 선 듯 골똘히 궁리하더니 넌지시 말한다."], ["“He may have found papers in something he bought, where papers were not supposed to be, and may have got it into his shrewd head from the manner and place of their concealment that they are worth something.”", "\"어쩌면 뭘 사들였는데 그 안에서, 서류가 있으리라곤 생각지도 못한 데서 서류를 발견했을 수도 있어. 그래서 그걸 감춰둔 방식이며 장소로 미루어, 그게 뭔가 값나가는 거라고 그 약삭빠른 머리에 박아 넣었을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“Or he may have been taken in, in some pretended bargain.", "\"아니면 무슨 사기 흥정에 속아 넘어갔을 수도 있고."], ["Or he may have been muddled altogether by long staring at whatever he HAS got, and by drink, and by hanging about the Lord Chancellor’s Court and hearing of documents for ever,”", "아니면 자기가 가진 게 뭐든 간에 하도 오래 노려보고, 술에 절고, 대법관 법정 언저리를 어슬렁대며 노상 문서 얘길 들어대다가 죄다 머리가 뒤죽박죽되었는지도 모르지.\""], ["returns Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 대답한다."]], [["Mr. Guppy sitting on the window-sill, nodding his head and balancing all these possibilities in his mind, continues thoughtfully to tap it, and clasp it, and measure it with his hand, until he hastily draws his hand away.", "거피 씨는 창턱에 걸터앉아, 고개를 끄덕이며 이 모든 가능성을 머릿속에서 저울질하면서, 골똘히 그 창턱을 톡톡 두드리고, 손으로 움켜쥐고, 가늠해보기를 이어가다가, 화들짝 손을 떼어낸다."]], [["“What, in the devil’s name,”", "\"이게 대체,\""], ["he says, “is this!", "그가 말한다, \"이게 뭐야!"], ["Look at my fingers!”", "내 손가락 좀 봐!\""]], [["A thick, yellow liquor defiles them, which is offensive to the touch and sight and more offensive to the smell.", "걸쭉하고 누런 진액이 그 손가락을 더럽히는데, 손에도 눈에도 거슬리고, 냄새는 한층 더 역하다."], ["A stagnant, sickening oil with some natural repulsion in it that makes them both shudder.", "무언가 본능적인 거부감이 깃든, 고여서 메스꺼운 기름이라 두 사람 다 진저리를 친다."]], [["“What have you been doing here?", "\"자네 여기서 뭘 한 거야?"], ["What have you been pouring out of window?”", "창밖으로 뭘 쏟아부은 거냐고?\""]], [["“I pouring out of window!", "\"내가 창밖으로 쏟아붓다니!"], ["Nothing, I swear!", "아무것도 안 했어, 맹세코!"], ["Never, since I have been here!”", "여기 온 뒤로 단 한 번도!\""], ["cries the lodger.", "하숙인이 소리친다."]], [["And yet look here—and look here!", "그런데도 여기를 봐라—또 여기를!"], ["When he brings the candle here, from the corner of the window-sill, it slowly drips and creeps away down the bricks, here lies in a little thick nauseous pool.", "그가 창턱 모퉁이에서 촛불을 가져오자, 그것이 벽돌을 타고 천천히 똑똑 떨어지며 기어 내려가, 여기 걸쭉하고 메스꺼운 작은 웅덩이를 이루고 있다."]], [["“This is a horrible house,”", "\"끔찍한 집이야.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, shutting down the window.", "거피 씨가 창문을 쾅 내리며 말한다."], ["“Give me some water or I shall cut my hand off.”", "\"물 좀 줘, 안 그러면 이 손을 잘라버리겠어.\""]], [["He so washes, and rubs, and scrubs, and smells, and washes, that he has not long restored himself with a glass of brandy and stood silently before the fire when Saint Paul’s bell strikes twelve and all those other bells strike twelve from their towers of various heights in the dark air, and in their many tones.", "그가 어찌나 씻고, 문지르고, 벅벅 닦고, 냄새 맡고, 또 씻어대는지, 브랜디 한 잔으로 겨우 정신을 추스르고 불 앞에 말없이 선 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 성 바울 성당 종이 열두 시를 치고, 다른 그 모든 종도 높이가 제각각인 종탑에서, 어두운 공기 속으로, 저마다의 음색으로 열두 시를 친다."], ["When all is quiet again, the lodger says, “It’s the appointed time at last.", "다시 모든 것이 고요해지자, 하숙인이 말한다. \"드디어 약속한 시각이군."], ["Shall I go?”", "내려가 볼까?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy nods and gives him a “lucky touch” on the back, but not with the washed hand, though it is his right hand.", "거피 씨가 고개를 끄덕이고는 그의 등에 \"행운의 손길\"을 얹어주는데, 오른손이긴 해도 그 씻은 손은 아니다."]], [["He goes downstairs, and Mr. Guppy tries to compose himself before the fire for waiting a long time.", "그가 아래층으로 내려가고, 거피 씨는 오래 기다릴 마음으로 불 앞에서 마음을 가다듬어 보려 한다."], ["But in no more than a minute or two the stairs creak and Tony comes swiftly back.", "그러나 일이 분도 채 못 되어 계단이 삐걱대더니 토니가 부랴부랴 돌아온다."]], [["“Have you got them?”", "\"받아 왔어?\""]], [["“Got them! No.", "\"받아 와!"], ["The old man’s not there.”", "아니. 영감이 거기 없어.\""]], [["He has been so horribly frightened in the short interval that his terror seizes the other, who makes a rush at him and asks loudly, “What’s the matter?”", "그가 그 짧은 사이에 어찌나 무섭게 질겁했는지, 그 공포가 상대마저 사로잡는다. 상대가 그에게 와락 달려들며 큰 소리로 묻는다, \"무슨 일이야?\""]], [["“I couldn’t make him hear, and I softly opened the door and looked in.", "\"아무리 불러도 못 듣더라고. 그래서 살그머니 문을 열고 안을 들여다봤어."], ["And the burning smell is there—and the soot is there, and the oil is there—and he is not there!”", "거기 그 타는 냄새가 있고—그을음이 있고, 기름이 있는데—정작 그 양반은 없어!\""], ["Tony ends this with a groan.", "토니가 신음과 함께 말을 맺는다."]], [["Mr. Guppy takes the light.", "거피 씨가 촛불을 집어 든다."], ["They go down, more dead than alive, and holding one another, push open the door of the back shop.", "두 사람은 죽은 거나 진배없는 몰골로 서로를 부둥켜안은 채 아래로 내려가, 안쪽 가게 문을 밀어 연다."], ["The cat has retreated close to it and stands snarling, not at them, at something on the ground before the fire.", "고양이가 그 문에 바싹 붙어 물러나 있다가, 두 사람을 향해서가 아니라 불 앞 바닥에 놓인 무언가를 향해 으르렁대며 서 있다."], ["There is a very little fire left in the grate, but there is a smouldering, suffocating vapour in the room and a dark, greasy coating on the walls and ceiling.", "화로에는 불기가 거의 남아 있지 않은데, 방 안에는 자욱하게 숨 막히는 김이 떠돌고, 벽과 천장에는 거무튀튀한 기름때가 끼어 있다."], ["The chairs and table, and the bottle so rarely absent from the table, all stand as usual.", "의자와 탁자, 그리고 탁자에서 좀처럼 사라지는 법이 없는 그 술병이, 모두 여느 때처럼 놓여 있다."], ["On one chair-back hang the old man’s hairy cap and coat.", "의자 등받이 하나에는 영감의 털북숭이 모자와 외투가 걸려 있다."]], [["“Look!”", "\"보게!\""], ["whispers the lodger, pointing his friend’s attention to these objects with a trembling finger.", "하숙인이 떨리는 손가락으로 친구의 주의를 그 물건들로 이끌며 속삭인다."], ["“I told you so.", "\"내가 말했잖아."], ["When I saw him last, he took his cap off, took out the little bundle of old letters, hung his cap on the back of the chair—his coat was there already, for he had pulled that off before he went to put the shutters up—and I left him turning the letters over in his hand, standing just where that crumbled black thing is upon the floor.”", "마지막으로 그 양반을 봤을 때, 모자를 벗어선, 그 오래된 편지 꾸러미를 꺼내고, 모자를 의자 등받이에 걸었어—외투는 이미 거기 걸려 있었지, 덧문 달러 가기 전에 벗어뒀으니까—그리고 그 양반이 손에 든 편지를 뒤적이는 걸 남겨두고 내가 나왔거든. 바로 저기 마룻바닥에 부스러진 시커먼 게 놓인 그 자리에 서서 말이야.\""]], [["Is he hanging somewhere?", "어딘가에 목을 매단 걸까?"], ["They look up.", "두 사람이 위를 올려다본다."], ["No.", "아니다."]], [["“See!”", "\"봐!\""], ["whispers Tony.", "토니가 속삭인다."], ["“At the foot of the same chair there lies a dirty bit of thin red cord that they tie up pens with.", "\"그 똑같은 의자 발치에, 펜을 묶을 때 쓰는 가느다란 붉은 끈 한 토막이 더럽게 떨어져 있어."], ["That went round the letters.", "그게 편지들을 둘러 묶고 있던 거야."], ["He undid it slowly, leering and laughing at me, before he began to turn them over, and threw it there.", "그 양반이 뒤적이기 전에, 날 보고 히죽거리며 비웃으면서 그걸 천천히 풀어선, 저기로 던졌어."], ["I saw it fall.”", "떨어지는 걸 내가 봤다고.\""]], [["“What’s the matter with the cat?”", "\"고양이가 왜 저래?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“Look at her!”", "\"저것 좀 봐!\""]], [["“Mad, I think.", "\"미친 것 같아."], ["And no wonder in this evil place.”", "이 사악한 곳에서 그럴 만도 하지.\""]], [["They advance slowly, looking at all these things.", "두 사람은 이 모든 것을 살피며 천천히 다가간다."], ["The cat remains where they found her, still snarling at the something on the ground before the fire and between the two chairs.", "고양이는 처음 본 그 자리에 그대로 있으면서, 여전히 불 앞, 두 의자 사이 바닥에 놓인 그 무언가를 향해 으르렁댄다."], ["What is it?", "그게 뭘까?"], ["Hold up the light.", "촛불을 들어 올려라."]], [["Here is a small burnt patch of flooring; here is the tinder from a little bundle of burnt paper, but not so light as usual, seeming to be steeped in something; and here is—is it the cinder of a small charred and broken log of wood sprinkled with white ashes, or is it coal?", "여기 마룻바닥이 자그맣게 그슬린 자국이 있고, 여기 한 줌의 불탄 종이 꾸러미에서 나온 부싯깃 같은 것이 있는데, 여느 것처럼 가볍지가 않고 무언가에 흠뻑 젖은 듯하다. 그리고 여기 있는 이것은—희뿌연 재가 뿌려진, 작게 까맣게 타서 부러진 장작개비의 깜부기인가, 아니면 석탄인가?"], ["Oh, horror, he IS here!", "오, 끔찍하여라, 그가 여기 있다!"], ["And this from which we run away, striking out the light and overturning one another into the street, is all that represents him.", "그리고 우리가 촛불을 내동댕이치고 서로를 거리로 떠밀어 넘어뜨리며 줄행랑치게 만드는 이것이, 그를 대신하는 전부다."]], [["Help, help, help! Come into this house for heaven’s sake!", "사람 살려, 사람 살려, 사람 살려!"], ["Plenty will come in, but none can help.", "제발 이 집으로 와 다오!"], ["The Lord Chancellor of that court, true to his title in his last act, has died the death of all lord chancellors in all courts and of all authorities in all places under all names soever, where false pretences are made, and where injustice is done.", "숱한 사람이 들어오겠지만, 누구도 도울 수 없다. 그 법정의 대법관은, 그 마지막 행위에서마저 제 칭호에 충실하게도, 모든 법정의 모든 대법관이, 또 어떤 이름으로 불리든 거짓된 명분이 꾸며지고 부정이 저질러지는 모든 곳의 모든 권위자가 맞이하는 그 죽음을 맞이했다."], ["Call the death by any name your Highness will, attribute it to whom you will, or say it might have been prevented how you will, it is the same death eternally—inborn, inbred, engendered in the corrupted humours of the vicious body itself, and that only—spontaneous combustion, and none other of all the deaths that can be died.", "그 죽음을 전하께서 어떤 이름으로 부르시든, 누구의 탓으로 돌리시든, 또 어떻게 막을 수 있었을 거라 말씀하시든, 그것은 영원토록 똑같은 죽음이다—그 사악한 몸뚱이 자체의 썩어빠진 체액 속에서 타고나고, 길러지고, 잉태된 죽음, 오직 그뿐인 죽음—자연발화, 인간이 죽을 수 있는 그 모든 죽음 가운데 다른 어떤 것도 아닌 바로 그 죽음이다."]], [["Interlopers\nNow do those two gentlemen not very neat about the cuffs and buttons who attended the last coroner’s inquest at the Sol’s Arms reappear in the precincts with surprising swiftness (being, in fact, breathlessly fetched by the active and intelligent beadle), and institute perquisitions through the court, and dive into the Sol’s parlour, and write with ravenous little pens on tissue-paper.", "이번에는 솔즈 암스에서 열린 지난번 검시 심리에 참석했던, 소맷부리와 단추가 그리 단정치 못한 두 신사가 놀라우리만치 잽싸게 그 일대에 다시 나타나(사실 활동적이고 영민한 교구 직원이 숨 가쁘게 데려온 것이다), 골목을 샅샅이 뒤지고 다니며 솔즈 암스의 응접실로 잠수하듯 들어가더니, 게걸스러운 작은 펜으로 박엽지에 갈겨쓴다."], ["Now do they note down, in the watches of the night, how the neighbourhood of Chancery Lane was yesterday, at about midnight, thrown into a state of the most intense agitation and excitement by the following alarming and horrible discovery.", "이제 그들은 한밤의 야경 시각에, 어제 자정 무렵 챈서리 거리 일대가 다음과 같은 경악스럽고 끔찍한 발견으로 극도의 흥분과 동요에 휩싸였다고 받아 적는다. 이제 그들은 이렇게 늘어놓는다."], ["Now do they set forth how it will doubtless be remembered that some time back a painful sensation was created in the public mind by a case of mysterious death from opium occurring in the first floor of the house occupied as a rag, bottle, and general marine store shop, by an eccentric individual of intemperate habits, far advanced in life, named Krook; and how, by a remarkable coincidence, Krook was examined at the inquest, which it may be recollected was held on that occasion at the Sol’s Arms, a well-conducted tavern immediately adjoining the premises in question on the west side and licensed to a highly respectable landlord, Mr.", "얼마 전 어느 별난 개인—나이가 한참 든 데다 무절제한 습성을 지닌, 크룩이라는 자—이 넝마, 병, 잡다한 선구를 취급하는 가게로 쓰던 집의 이층에서 아편으로 의문스럽게 죽은 사건으로 세간에 고통스러운 충격이 일었던 것은 누구나 틀림없이 기억할 것이다. 그리고 놀라운 우연으로, 크룩이 바로 그 검시 심리에서 신문을 받았다는 사실도 기억날 것이다."], ["James George Bogsby. Now do they show (in as many words as possible) how during some hours of yesterday evening a very peculiar smell was observed by the inhabitants of the court, in which the tragical occurrence which forms the subject of that present account transpired; and which odour was at one time so powerful that Mr.", "그 심리는 당시 솔즈 암스에서 열렸는데, 그곳은 문제의 건물에 바로 서쪽으로 붙어 있는, 운영이 잘되는 술집이며, 더없이 점잖은 주인 제임스 조지 보그스비 씨가 허가를 받아 운영하는 곳이다."], ["Swills, a comic vocalist professionally engaged by Mr. J.", "이제 그들은 (될 수 있는 한 많은 말을 동원해) 보여준다."], ["G. Bogsby, has himself stated to our reporter that he mentioned to Miss M.", "어제 저녁 몇 시간 동안 이 골목 주민들이 아주 특이한 냄새를 맡았으며, 그 골목에서 지금 이 기사의 주제가 된 비극적 사건이 일어났다는 것을."], ["Melvilleson, a lady of some pretensions to musical ability, likewise engaged by Mr. J.", "그 악취가 한때 어찌나 지독했던지, 보그스비 씨에게 직업적으로 고용된 희극 가수 스윌스 씨는 본지 기자에게 직접 진술하기를, 자신이 멜빌슨 양에게—음악적 재능을 다소 자처하는 숙녀로, 역시 보그스비 씨에게 고용되어 ‘화음 모임’ 혹은 ‘집회’라 불리는 일련의 음악회에서 노래를 부르는데, 이 모임은 보그스비 씨의 지휘 아래 조지 이세의 법령에 따라 솔즈 암스에서 열리는 듯하다—그 탁한 공기 탓에 자기(스윌스 씨) 목이 심각하게 망가졌다고 말했다는 것이다."], ["G. Bogsby to sing at a series of concerts called Harmonic Assemblies, or Meetings, which it would appear are held at the Sol’s Arms under Mr.", "그때 그가 농담조로 한 말이, 자기는 텅 빈 우체국 같다는 것이었으니, 안에 음표라곤 한 장도 없기 때문이라 했다."], ["Bogsby’s direction pursuant to the Act of George the Second, that he (Mr. Swills) found his voice seriously affected by the impure state of the atmosphere, his jocose expression at the time being that he was like an empty post-office, for he hadn’t a single note in him.", "스윌스 씨의 이 증언은 같은 골목에 사는 영민한 기혼 부인 둘이 온전히 뒷받침하니, 각각 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인이라는 이름으로 알려진 이들이다."], ["How this account of Mr. Swills is entirely corroborated by two intelligent married females residing in the same court and known respectively by the names of Mrs.", "두 사람 모두 그 고약한 악취를 맡았고, 그것이 불운하게 죽은 크룩이 쓰던 건물에서 흘러나온다고 여겼다."], ["Piper and Mrs. Perkins, both of whom observed the foetid effluvia and regarded them as being emitted from the premises in the occupation of Krook, the unfortunate deceased.", "이 모든 것과 그 밖에 훨씬 더 많은 것을, 이 우울한 참사를 두고 화기애애한 동업 관계를 맺은 두 신사가 현장에서 받아 적는다."], ["All this and a great deal more the two gentlemen who have formed an amicable partnership in the melancholy catastrophe write down on the spot; and the boy population of the court (out of bed in a moment) swarm up the shutters of the Sol’s Arms parlour, to behold the tops of their heads while they are about it.", "그러는 동안 골목의 사내아이들은(순식간에 잠자리에서 빠져나와) 솔즈 암스 응접실의 덧문 위로 떼 지어 기어올라, 그들이 받아 적는 모습 위로 보이는 두 사람 정수리를 구경한다."]], [["The whole court, adult as well as boy, is sleepless for that night, and can do nothing but wrap up its many heads, and talk of the ill-fated house, and look at it.", "골목 전체가, 사내아이는 물론 어른까지, 그날 밤은 한숨도 못 자고, 그저 여러 머리를 싸매고서 그 박복한 집 이야기만 하며 그 집을 바라볼 뿐이다."], ["Miss Flite has been bravely rescued from her chamber, as if it were in flames, and accommodated with a bed at the Sol’s Arms.", "플라이트 양은 마치 집에 불이라도 난 것처럼 용감히 자기 방에서 구출되어 솔즈 암스에 잠자리를 얻었다."], ["The Sol neither turns off its gas nor shuts its door all night, for any kind of public excitement makes good for the Sol and causes the court to stand in need of comfort.", "솔즈는 밤새 가스등도 끄지 않고 문도 닫지 않으니, 어떤 종류의 대중적 흥분이든 솔즈에는 이득이 되고 골목은 위안을 필요로 하게 만들기 때문이다."], ["The house has not done so much in the stomachic article of cloves or in brandy-and-water warm since the inquest.", "이 집은 검시 심리 이후로 위장을 달래는 정향이나 따뜻한 브랜디 물에서 이만한 매상을 올린 적이 없다."], ["The moment the pot-boy heard what had happened, he rolled up his shirt-sleeves tight to his shoulders and said, “There’ll be a run upon us!”", "무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 듣자마자 술집 사환은 셔츠 소매를 어깨까지 단단히 걷어붙이며 말했다. \"우리한테 손님이 몰려들겠는걸!\""], ["In the first outcry, young Piper dashed off for the fire-engines and returned in triumph at a jolting gallop perched up aloft on the Phoenix and holding on to that fabulous creature with all his might in the midst of helmets and torches.", "처음 소동이 일었을 때 어린 파이퍼는 소방차를 부르러 달려갔다가, 흔들리는 질주 속에 피닉스호 꼭대기에 높이 걸터앉아, 그 전설 속 괴물을 죽어라 붙들고서, 투구와 횃불 한가운데서 의기양양하게 돌아왔다."], ["One helmet remains behind after careful investigation of all chinks and crannies and slowly paces up and down before the house in company with one of the two policemen who have likewise been left in charge thereof.", "모든 틈새와 구석을 꼼꼼히 조사한 끝에 투구 하나가 뒤에 남아, 마찬가지로 그곳 경비를 맡긴 경관 둘 중 한 사람과 짝을 이루어 집 앞을 천천히 오간다."], ["To this trio everybody in the court possessed of sixpence has an insatiate desire to exhibit hospitality in a liquid form.", "이 셋에게, 골목에서 육 펜스라도 지닌 자라면 누구나 술이라는 형태로 환대를 베풀고 싶어 안달이다."]], [["Mr. Weevle and his friend Mr.", "위블 씨와 그의 벗 거피 씨는 솔즈의 술집 안에 있으며, 그들이 그 자리에 머물러주기만 한다면 술집에 있는 무엇과도 바꿀 수 없을 만큼 솔즈에 값진 손님이다."], ["Guppy are within the bar at the Sol and are worth anything to the Sol that the bar contains if they will only stay there.", "\"지금은,\" 보그스비 씨가 말한다."], ["“This is not a time,” says Mr.", "\"돈을 두고 흥정할 때가 아니지요.\""], ["Bogsby, “to haggle about money,”", "그러면서도 그는 계산대 너머로 그것을 다소 날카롭게 챙긴다."], ["though he looks something sharply after it, over the counter; “give your orders, you two gentlemen, and you’re welcome to whatever you put a name to.”", "\"주문하세요, 두 신사분. 이름만 대시면 무엇이든 환영입니다.\""]], [["Thus entreated, the two gentlemen (Mr. Weevle especially) put names to so many things that in course of time they find it difficult to put a name to anything quite distinctly, though they still relate to all new-comers some version of the night they have had of it, and of what they said, and what they thought, and what they saw.", "이렇게 권유받은 두 신사는(특히 위블 씨가) 너무 많은 것에 이름을 대다 보니, 시간이 흐르자 무엇 하나 또렷하게 이름 대기가 어려운 지경에 이른다."], ["Meanwhile, one or other of the policemen often flits about the door, and pushing it open a little way at the full length of his arm, looks in from outer gloom.", "그래도 그들은 새로 오는 사람마다 붙잡고 자기들이 보낸 그 밤 이야기를, 무슨 말을 했고 무슨 생각을 했고 무엇을 보았는지를 이런저런 식으로 들려준다. 그러는 사이 경관 한둘이 곧잘 문가를 어른거리며, 팔을 한껏 뻗어 문을 조금 밀어젖히고는, 바깥 어둠 속에서 안을 들여다본다."], ["Not that he has any suspicions, but that he may as well know what they are up to in there.", "무슨 의심이 있어서가 아니라, 저 안에서 무슨 짓을 하는지 알아두는 편이 낫겠다 싶어서다."]], [["Thus night pursues its leaden course, finding the court still out of bed through the unwonted hours, still treating and being treated, still conducting itself similarly to a court that has had a little money left it unexpectedly.", "이렇게 밤은 납덩이 같은 걸음으로 흘러가, 골목이 여전히 이례적인 시각까지 잠자리에 들지 않고, 여전히 술을 사고 또 얻어 마시며, 뜻밖에 약간의 돈이 굴러든 골목처럼 굴고 있는 것을 본다."], ["Thus night at length with slow-retreating steps departs, and the lamp-lighter going his rounds, like an executioner to a despotic king, strikes off the little heads of fire that have aspired to lessen the darkness.", "이렇게 밤은 마침내 천천히 물러가는 발걸음으로 떠나고, 가로등 점등부는 순찰을 돌며, 마치 폭군 왕의 사형 집행인처럼, 어둠을 덜어보겠다고 솟아오른 작은 불꽃 머리들을 쳐낸다."], ["Thus the day cometh, whether or no.", "이렇게 날이 밝아온다, 그러든 말든."]], [["And the day may discern, even with its dim London eye, that the court has been up all night.", "그리고 날은, 흐릿한 런던의 눈으로나마, 이 골목이 밤새 깨어 있었음을 알아본다."], ["Over and above the faces that have fallen drowsily on tables and the heels that lie prone on hard floors instead of beds, the brick and mortar physiognomy of the very court itself looks worn and jaded.", "졸음에 겨워 탁자에 엎어진 얼굴들과, 침대 대신 딱딱한 바닥에 큰대자로 뻗은 발뒤꿈치들은 말할 것도 없고, 골목 자체의 벽돌과 회반죽으로 된 인상마저 지치고 핼쑥해 보인다."], ["And now the neighbourhood, waking up and beginning to hear of what has happened, comes streaming in, half dressed, to ask questions; and the two policemen and the helmet (who are far less impressible externally than the court) have enough to do to keep the door.", "이제 동네가 잠에서 깨어 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 듣기 시작하면서, 옷을 반쯤 걸친 채 줄줄이 몰려와 이것저것 캐묻는다. 그리고 두 경관과 투구는(겉보기로는 골목보다 훨씬 덜 동요하는 자들이라) 문을 지키느라 진땀을 뺀다."]], [["“Good gracious, gentlemen!”", "\"이런 세상에, 여보게들!\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby, coming up.", "스내그스비 씨가 다가오며 말한다."], ["“What’s this I hear!”", "\"이게 무슨 소린가!\""]], [["“Why, it’s true,”", "\"뭐, 사실이오.\""], ["returns one of the policemen.", "경관 하나가 대꾸한다."], ["“That’s what it is.", "\"그게 다요."], ["Now move on here, come!”", "자, 여기서 비키시오, 어서!\""]], [["“Why, good gracious, gentlemen,” says Mr.", "\"아니, 이런 세상에, 여보게들,\""], ["Snagsby, somewhat promptly backed away, “I was at this door last night betwixt ten and eleven o’clock in conversation with the young man who lodges here.”", "스내그스비 씨는 다소 잽싸게 물러서면서 말한다. \"내가 어젯밤 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 바로 이 문 앞에서 여기 하숙하는 젊은이와 이야기를 나눴소.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["returns the policeman.", "경관이 대꾸한다."], ["“You will find the young man next door then.", "\"그럼 그 젊은이는 옆집에서 찾을 수 있겠소."], ["Now move on here, some of you.”", "자, 여기 좀 비키시오, 다들.\""]], [["“Not hurt, I hope?”", "\"다치진 않았겠지요?\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Hurt? No.", "\"다쳐요?"], ["What’s to hurt him!”", "아니오. 그 사람이 뭘 다쳐!\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby, wholly unable to answer this or any question in his troubled mind, repairs to the Sol’s Arms and finds Mr.", "스내그스비 씨는 어수선한 마음으로 이 질문도 다른 어떤 질문도 도무지 답할 수 없어, 솔즈 암스로 발걸음을 옮긴다."], ["Weevle languishing over tea and toast with a considerable expression on him of exhausted excitement and exhausted tobacco-smoke.", "그곳에서 그는 위블 씨가 차와 토스트를 앞에 두고 늘어져 있는 것을 보니, 그 얼굴에는 흥분에 지치고 담배 연기에 지친 기색이 짙게 어려 있다."]], [["“And Mr. Guppy likewise!”", "\"게다가 거피 씨도 있군!\""], ["quoth Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."], ["“Dear, dear, dear!", "\"저런, 저런, 저런!"], ["What a fate there seems in all this!", "이 모든 일에 무슨 운명 같은 게 깃든 모양이야!"], ["And my lit—”", "그리고 우리 마—\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby’s power of speech deserts him in the formation of the words “my little woman.”", "스내그스비 씨는 \"우리 마누라\"라는 말을 채 빚어내지 못하고 말문이 막힌다."], ["For to see that injured female walk into the Sol’s Arms at that hour of the morning and stand before the beer-engine, with her eyes fixed upon him like an accusing spirit, strikes him dumb.", "그 시간, 그 이른 아침에 자기 마누라가, 그 학대받은 여인이 솔즈 암스로 걸어 들어와 맥주 따르는 기계 앞에 서서, 마치 고발하는 망령처럼 두 눈을 자기에게 못 박고 있는 것을 보니, 그는 그만 입이 떨어지지 않는 것이다."]], [["“My dear,” says Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Snagsby when his tongue is loosened, “will you take anything?", "스내그스비 씨가 혀가 풀리자 말한다. \"뭐라도 한잔하겠소?"], ["A little—not to put too fine a point upon it—drop of shrub?”", "조금—이렇게 너무 까다롭게 굴 건 없지만—과실주 한 모금이라도?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"싫어요.\""], ["says Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인이 말한다."]], [["“My love, you know these two gentlemen?”", "\"여보, 이 두 신사분을 알지요?\""]], [["“Yes!” says Mrs.", "\"네!\""], ["Snagsby, and in a rigid manner acknowledges their presence, still fixing Mr. Snagsby with her eye.", "스내그스비 부인은 그렇게 말하며 뻣뻣한 태도로 두 사람의 존재를 인정하되, 여전히 스내그스비 씨에게서 눈을 떼지 않는다."]], [["The devoted Mr. Snagsby cannot bear this treatment.", "헌신적인 스내그스비 씨는 이런 대접을 견딜 수 없다."], ["He takes Mrs. Snagsby by the hand and leads her aside to an adjacent cask.", "그는 스내그스비 부인의 손을 잡고 옆쪽 가까운 술통 곁으로 이끈다."]], [["“My little woman, why do you look at me in that way?", "\"여보, 왜 그런 눈으로 날 보는 거요?"], ["Pray don’t do it.”", "제발 그러지 마시오.\""]], [["“I can’t help my looks,” says Mrs.", "\"내 눈은 내 맘대로 안 돼요.\""], ["Snagsby, “and if I could I wouldn’t.”", "스내그스비 부인이 말한다. \"되더라도 안 그럴 거예요.\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby, with his cough of meekness, rejoins, “Wouldn’t you really, my dear?”", "스내그스비 씨는 온순한 기침을 하며 다시 말한다."], ["and meditates.", "\"정말 안 그러겠소, 여보?\" 그러고는 곰곰 생각한다."], ["Then coughs his cough of trouble and says, “This is a dreadful mystery, my love!”", "그러더니 곤혹스러운 기침을 하고서 말한다. \"이건 끔찍한 수수께끼야, 여보!\""], ["still fearfully disconcerted by Mrs. Snagsby’s eye.", "그러면서도 여전히 스내그스비 부인의 눈빛에 무섭도록 당황한다."]], [["“It IS,” returns Mrs.", "\"그래요,\""], ["Snagsby, shaking her head, “a dreadful mystery.”", "스내그스비 부인이 고개를 저으며 대꾸한다. \"끔찍한 수수께끼지요.\""]], [["“My little woman,” urges Mr.", "\"여보,\""], ["Snagsby in a piteous manner, “don’t for goodness’ sake speak to me with that bitter expression and look at me in that searching way!", "스내그스비 씨가 애처롭게 매달리듯 말한다. \"제발 부탁이니 그런 모진 표정으로 내게 말하지 말고, 그렇게 꿰뚫어 보는 눈으로 날 보지도 마시오!"], ["I beg and entreat of you not to do it.", "빌고 또 비니 제발 그러지 마시오."], ["Good Lord, you don’t suppose that I would go spontaneously combusting any person, my dear?”", "맙소사, 설마 내가 어떤 사람을 자연발화로 태워 죽이고 다닌다고 여기는 건 아니겠지, 여보?\""]], [["“I can’t say,”", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["returns Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인이 대꾸한다."]], [["On a hasty review of his unfortunate position, Mr.", "자기 처지가 얼마나 딱한지 황급히 되짚어 본 스내그스비 씨도 \"글쎄요\""], ["Snagsby “can’t say”", "할 수밖에 없다."], ["either. He is not prepared positively to deny that he may have had something to do with it.", "그는 자기가 그 일과 어떤 관련이 있었을지도 모른다는 것을 딱 잘라 부인할 처지가 못 된다."], ["He has had something—he don’t know what—to do with so much in this connexion that is mysterious that it is possible he may even be implicated, without knowing it, in the present transaction.", "그는 이 일과 관련된 그 숱한 수수께끼 같은 일들에—무엇인지도 모를 무언가에—관여한 바가 있어서, 자기도 모르는 새 이번 사건에마저 연루되었을 가능성이 있다."], ["He faintly wipes his forehead with his handkerchief and gasps.", "그는 손수건으로 이마를 힘없이 훔치며 숨을 헐떡인다."]], [["“My life,”", "\"여보,\""], ["says the unhappy stationer, “would you have any objections to mention why, being in general so delicately circumspect in your conduct, you come into a wine-vaults before breakfast?”", "불행한 문방구상이 말한다. \"당신은 평소 행실이 그토록 섬세하고 신중한 사람인데, 어째서 아침 식사 전에 술 창고에 들어왔는지 그 까닭을 말해주면 안 되겠소?\""]], [["“Why do YOU come here?”", "\"당신은 왜 여기 왔는데요?\""], ["inquires Mrs. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 부인이 묻는다."]], [["“My dear, merely to know the rights of the fatal accident which has happened to the venerable party who has been—combusted.”", "\"여보, 그저 그—태워진—그 점잖은 양반에게 닥친 끔찍한 사고의 자초지종을 알아보려고.\""], ["Mr. Snagsby has made a pause to suppress a groan.", "스내그스비 씨는 신음을 억누르느라 잠시 말을 멈췄다."], ["“I should then have related them to you, my love, over your French roll.”", "\"그래서 당신에게 프랑스식 빵을 곁들여 그 이야길 들려주려 했지, 여보.\""]], [["“I dare say you would!", "\"그러시겠지요!"], ["You relate everything to me, Mr. Snagsby.”", "당신은 내게 뭐든 다 얘기하니까요, 스내그스비 씨.\""]], [["“Every—my lit—”", "\"뭐든—우리 마—\""]], [["“I should be glad,” says Mrs.", "\"기쁘겠어요,\""], ["Snagsby after contemplating his increased confusion with a severe and sinister smile, “if you would come home with me; I think you may be safer there, Mr.", "스내그스비 부인이 그의 한층 깊어진 당혹을 매섭고 음흉한 미소로 바라보며 말한다. \"나와 함께 집에 가준다면 말이에요."], ["Snagsby, than anywhere else.”", "스내그스비 씨, 당신은 다른 어디보다 거기 있는 편이 더 안전할 거예요.\""]], [["“My love, I don’t know but what I may be, I am sure.", "\"여보, 정말 그럴지도 모르겠소, 정말로."], ["I am ready to go.”", "갈 채비가 됐소.\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby casts his eye forlornly round the bar, gives Messrs. Weevle and Guppy good morning, assures them of the satisfaction with which he sees them uninjured, and accompanies Mrs. Snagsby from the Sol’s Arms. Before night his doubt whether he may not be responsible for some inconceivable part in the catastrophe which is the talk of the whole neighbourhood is almost resolved into certainty by Mrs. Snagsby’s pertinacity in that fixed gaze. His mental sufferings are so great that he entertains wandering ideas of delivering himself up to justice and requiring to be cleared if innocent and punished with the utmost rigour of the law if guilty.", "스내그스비 씨는 술집 안을 처량하게 둘러보고, 위블 씨와 거피 씨에게 좋은 아침이라 인사하며 두 사람이 다치지 않은 것을 보니 흡족하다고 다짐하고는, 스내그스비 부인을 따라 솔즈 암스를 나선다. 밤이 되기 전에, 온 동네의 입길에 오르내리는 그 참사에서 자기가 도무지 상상할 수 없는 어떤 역할을 맡지나 않았을까 하는 그의 의혹은, 스내그스비 부인이 그 한결같은 응시를 집요하게 거두지 않는 통에 거의 확신으로 굳어진다. 그의 정신적 고통이 어찌나 큰지, 그는 차라리 자수하여, 무죄라면 풀어달라고, 유죄라면 법의 지엄한 형벌로 처벌해달라고 요구할까 하는 종잡을 수 없는 생각마저 품는다."]], [["Mr. Weevle and Mr. Guppy, having taken their breakfast, step into Lincoln’s Inn to take a little walk about the square and clear as many of the dark cobwebs out of their brains as a little walk may.", "위블 씨와 거피 씨는 아침을 먹고 나서, 잠깐 산책하며 머릿속의 거뭇한 거미줄을 산책으로 걷어낼 수 있는 만큼이라도 걷어내려고, 링컨스 인 광장으로 발을 들여놓는다."]], [["“There can be no more favourable time than the present, Tony,” says Mr.", "\"지금보다 더 좋은 때는 없을 거야, 토니,\""], ["Guppy after they have broodingly made out the four sides of the square, “for a word or two between us upon a point on which we must, with very little delay, come to an understanding.”", "둘이 침울하게 광장 네 변을 다 돌고 나자 거피 씨가 말한다. \"우리가 머잖아 합의를 봐야 할 한 가지 문제를 두고 둘이서 몇 마디 나누기에 말이야.\""]], [["“Now, I tell you what, William G.!”", "\"이봐, 내가 한마디 하겠는데, 윌리엄 G.!\""], ["returns the other, eyeing his companion with a bloodshot eye.", "상대가 충혈된 눈으로 동행을 노려보며 대꾸한다."], ["“If it’s a point of conspiracy, you needn’t take the trouble to mention it.", "\"그게 무슨 음모 따위 문제라면 굳이 꺼낼 것도 없어."], ["I have had enough of that, and I ain’t going to have any more.", "난 그런 거 진절머리가 나, 더는 사양이야."], ["We shall have YOU taking fire next or blowing up with a bang.”", "다음엔 자네마저 불붙거나 펑 터지는 꼴을 보게 될 판이라고.\""]], [["This supposititious phenomenon is so very disagreeable to Mr.", "이 가상의 현상이 거피 씨에게는 어찌나 거북한지, 그는 목소리를 떨며 점잔 빼는 투로 말한다."], ["Guppy that his voice quakes as he says in a moral way, “Tony, I should have thought that what we went through last night would have been a lesson to you never to be personal any more as long as you lived.” To which Mr.", "\"토니, 어젯밤 우리가 겪은 일이라면, 자넨 살아생전 다시는 인신공격 따윈 하지 말아야 한다는 교훈으로 삼았어야 했어.\""], ["Weevle returns, “William, I should have thought it would have been a lesson to YOU never to conspire any more as long as you lived.”", "이에 위블 씨가 대꾸한다. \"윌리엄, 난 그게 자네한테 살아생전 다시는 음모 따윈 꾸미지 말라는 교훈이 됐어야 한다고 생각했는데.\""], ["To which Mr.", "이에 거피 씨가 말한다."], ["Guppy says, “Who’s conspiring?”", "\"누가 음모를 꾸민다는 거야?\""], ["To which Mr.", "이에 조블링 씨가 받는다."], ["Jobling replies, “Why, YOU are!” To which Mr.", "\"아니, 자네가 꾸미잖아!\""], ["Guppy retorts, “No, I am not.”", "이에 거피 씨가 받아친다."], ["To which Mr.", "\"아니, 난 안 그래.\""], ["Jobling retorts again, “Yes, you are!”", "이에 조블링 씨가 또 받아친다."], ["To which Mr.", "\"아니, 자넨 그래!\""], ["Guppy retorts, “Who says so?”", "이에 거피 씨가 받아친다."], ["To which Mr.", "\"누가 그래?\""], ["Jobling retorts, “I say so!”", "이에 조블링 씨가 받아친다."], ["To which Mr.", "\"내가 그래!\""], ["Guppy retorts, “Oh, indeed?”", "이에 거피 씨가 받아친다."], ["To which Mr.", "\"오, 그래?\""], ["Jobling retorts, “Yes, indeed!”", "이에 조블링 씨가 받아친다."], ["And both being now in a heated state, they walk on silently for a while to cool down again.", "\"그래, 그렇다고!\" 그러고는 둘 다 이제 열이 오를 대로 올라, 한동안 말없이 걸으며 다시 열을 식힌다."]], [["“Tony,” says Mr.", "\"토니,\""], ["Guppy then, “if you heard your friend out instead of flying at him, you wouldn’t fall into mistakes.", "그러더니 거피 씨가 말한다. \"벗에게 달려들 게 아니라 끝까지 들어줬다면, 자네도 오해에 빠지진 않았을 거야."], ["But your temper is hasty and you are not considerate.", "하지만 자넨 성미가 급하고 사려가 부족해."], ["Possessing in yourself, Tony, all that is calculated to charm the eye—”", "토니, 자네는 보는 이의 눈을 사로잡을 만한 것을 죄다 갖춘 사람이면서—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Blow the eye!”", "눈은 꺼지라 그래!\""], ["cries Mr. Weevle, cutting him short.", "위블 씨가 그의 말을 잘라먹으며 외친다."], ["“Say what you have got to say!”", "\"할 말이나 하라고!\""]], [["Finding his friend in this morose and material condition, Mr. Guppy only expresses the finer feelings of his soul through the tone of injury in which he recommences, “Tony, when I say there is a point on which we must come to an understanding pretty soon, I say so quite apart from any kind of conspiring, however innocent.", "벗이 이렇게 시무룩하고 살벌한 상태인 것을 보고, 거피 씨는 자기 영혼의 그 섬세한 감정을 오로지 상처받은 어조로만 드러내며 다시 입을 연다."], ["You know it is professionally arranged beforehand in all cases that are tried what facts the witnesses are to prove.", "\"토니, 우리가 머잖아 합의를 봐야 할 문제가 하나 있다고 할 때, 난 그걸 어떤 종류의 음모와도, 아무리 결백한 음모일지언정, 전혀 별개로 말하는 거야."], ["Is it or is it not desirable that we should know what facts we are to prove on the inquiry into the death of this unfortunate old mo—gentleman?” (Mr.", "자네도 알다시피, 재판에 부쳐지는 모든 사건에서는 증인들이 어떤 사실을 입증할 것인지를 직업적으로 미리 정해둬."], ["Guppy was going to say “mogul,” but thinks “gentleman”", "이 불운한 노인—신사 양반의 사망에 관한 심리에서 우리가 어떤 사실을 입증해야 하는지, 우리가 알아두는 게 바람직하지 않겠어, 바람직하지 않겠어?\""], ["better suited to the circumstances.)", "(거피 씨는 \"노친네\"라고 할 참이었으나, 상황에는 \"신사 양반\"이 더 어울린다고 생각한다.)"]], [["“What facts?", "\"무슨 사실?"], ["THE facts.”", "그 사실 말이지.\""]], [["“The facts bearing on that inquiry.", "\"그 심리와 관련된 사실들 말이야."], ["Those are”—Mr.", "그건—\""], ["Guppy tells them off on his fingers—“what we knew of his habits, when you saw him last, what his condition was then, the discovery that we made, and how we made it.”", "거피 씨가 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 늘어놓는다. \"우리가 그의 습성에 대해 알던 것, 자네가 그를 마지막으로 본 게 언제인지, 그때 그의 상태가 어땠는지, 우리가 한 발견과 그걸 어떻게 했는지야.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["says Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 말한다."], ["“Those are about the facts.”", "\"그게 대충 그 사실들이지.\""]], [["“We made the discovery in consequence of his having, in his eccentric way, an appointment with you at twelve o’clock at night, when you were to explain some writing to him as you had often done before on account of his not being able to read.", "\"우리가 그 발견을 하게 된 건, 그가 그 별난 식대로 자네와 자정에 약속을 잡았기 때문이야. 그가 글을 못 읽으니, 전에도 자네가 종종 그랬듯이 그에게 어떤 글을 설명해주기로 했지."], ["I, spending the evening with you, was called down—and so forth.", "나는 자네와 저녁을 함께 보내다가 아래층으로 불려 내려갔고—뭐 그런 식이지."], ["The inquiry being only into the circumstances touching the death of the deceased, it’s not necessary to go beyond these facts, I suppose you’ll agree?”", "심리는 망자의 사망 정황에 관한 것일 뿐이니, 이 사실들 너머로 갈 필요는 없을 거야, 자네도 동의하겠지?\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아니!\""], ["returns Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 대꾸한다."], ["“I suppose not.”", "\"그렇겠지.\""]], [["“And this is not a conspiracy, perhaps?”", "\"그럼 이건 음모가 아니지, 아마?\""], ["says the injured Guppy.", "상처받은 거피가 말한다."]], [["“No,”", "\"그래,\""], ["returns his friend; “if it’s nothing worse than this, I withdraw the observation.”", "그의 벗이 대꾸한다. \"이것보다 더 나쁜 게 아니라면, 그 말은 거두지.\""]], [["“Now, Tony,” says Mr.", "\"자, 토니,\""], ["Guppy, taking his arm again and walking him slowly on, “I should like to know, in a friendly way, whether you have yet thought over the many advantages of your continuing to live at that place?”", "거피 씨가 다시 그의 팔을 잡고 천천히 걸음을 옮기며 말한다. \"벗으로서 묻고 싶은데, 자네가 그곳에 계속 사는 게 얼마나 이로운지 곰곰 생각해봤나?\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소리야?\""], ["says Tony, stopping.", "토니가 걸음을 멈추며 말한다."]], [["“Whether you have yet thought over the many advantages of your continuing to live at that place?”", "\"자네가 그곳에 계속 사는 게 얼마나 이로운지 곰곰 생각해봤느냐고?\""], ["repeats Mr. Guppy, walking him on again.", "거피 씨가 그를 다시 걷게 하며 되풀이한다."]], [["“At what place?", "\"어느 곳에?"], ["THAT place?”", "그곳에?\""], ["pointing in the direction of the rag and bottle shop.", "위블 씨가 넝마와 병 가게 쪽을 가리킨다."]], [["Mr. Guppy nods.", "거피 씨가 고개를 끄덕인다."]], [["“Why, I wouldn’t pass another night there for any consideration that you could offer me,”", "\"흥, 자네가 무슨 대가를 내건다 해도 거기서 하룻밤을 더 보내진 않을 거야.\""], ["says Mr. Weevle, haggardly staring.", "위블 씨가 핼쑥하게 노려보며 말한다."]], [["“Do you mean it though, Tony?”", "\"하지만 진심이야, 토니?\""]], [["“Mean it!", "\"진심이냐고!"], ["Do I look as if I mean it?", "내가 진심 아닌 것처럼 보여?"], ["I feel as if I do; I know that,”", "진심인 것 같다고. 그건 내가 알지.\""], ["says Mr. Weevle with a very genuine shudder.", "위블 씨가 정말로 진저리를 치며 말한다."]], [["“Then the possibility or probability—for such it must be considered—of your never being disturbed in possession of those effects lately belonging to a lone old man who seemed to have no relation in the world, and the certainty of your being able to find out what he really had got stored up there, don’t weigh with you at all against last night, Tony, if I understand you?”", "\"그렇다면, 세상천지에 일가친척 하나 없어 보이던 외로운 노인의 그 유품을, 자네가 손에 넣고도 아무에게도 방해받지 않을 가능성이나 개연성—그렇게 봐야 마땅하지—그리고 그가 거기에 정말로 무엇을 쟁여두었는지 자네가 알아낼 수 있다는 확실성이, 어젯밤 일과 견주면 자네한텐 아무 무게도 없다는 거지, 내가 자네 말을 제대로 이해한 거라면?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, biting his thumb with the appetite of vexation.", "거피 씨가 분한 식욕으로 엄지손가락을 깨물며 말한다."]], [["“Certainly not.", "\"당연히 그렇지."], ["Talk in that cool way of a fellow’s living there?”", "사람이 거기 산다는 걸 그렇게 태평하게 말해?\""], ["cries Mr. Weevle indignantly.", "위블 씨가 분개하여 외친다."], ["“Go and live there yourself.”", "\"자네가 가서 살든가.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I, Tony!”", "나야, 토니!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, soothing him.", "거피 씨가 그를 달래며 말한다."], ["“I have never lived there and couldn’t get a lodging there now, whereas you have got one.”", "\"난 거기 살아본 적도 없고 지금 거기서 하숙을 구할 수도 없어. 반면에 자넨 이미 하나 갖고 있잖아.\""]], [["“You are welcome to it,”", "\"자네 가지라고.\""], ["rejoins his friend, “and—ugh!—you may make yourself at home in it.”", "그의 벗이 대꾸한다. \"그리고—으윽!—거기서 마음껏 편히 지내든가.\""]], [["“Then you really and truly at this point,”", "\"그럼 자넨 정말이지 이 시점에,\" 거피 씨가 말한다."], ["says Mr. Guppy, “give up the whole thing, if I understand you, Tony?”", "\"이 일을 통째로 접겠다는 거군, 내가 자네 말을 제대로 이해한 거라면, 토니?\""]], [["“You never,”", "\"자넨,\""], ["returns Tony with a most convincing steadfastness, “said a truer word in all your life.", "토니가 더없이 확고한 단호함으로 대꾸한다. \"평생 그보다 더 옳은 말을 한 적이 없어."], ["I do!”", "정말 접을 거야!\""]], [["While they are so conversing, a hackney-coach drives into the square, on the box of which vehicle a very tall hat makes itself manifest to the public. Inside the coach, and consequently not so manifest to the multitude, though sufficiently so to the two friends, for the coach stops almost at their feet, are the venerable Mr.", "그들이 그렇게 이야기를 나누는 사이, 삯마차 한 대가 광장으로 들어오는데, 그 마차 마부석 위로 아주 키 큰 모자 하나가 대중 앞에 모습을 드러낸다."], ["Smallweed and Mrs. Smallweed, accompanied by their granddaughter Judy.", "마차 안에는—따라서 군중에게는 그만큼 드러나지 않으나, 마차가 거의 두 벗의 발치에 멈춰 서니 그들에게는 충분히 드러나는데—점잖은 스몰위드 씨와 스몰위드 부인이, 손녀 주디와 함께 타고 있다."]], [["An air of haste and excitement pervades the party, and as the tall hat (surmounting Mr.", "황급하고 들뜬 기색이 일행에 감돈다."], ["Smallweed the younger) alights, Mr. Smallweed the elder pokes his head out of window and bawls to Mr.", "그리고 키 큰 모자가(젊은 스몰위드 씨 머리에 얹혀) 마차에서 내리자, 늙은 스몰위드 씨가 창밖으로 머리를 쑥 내밀고 거피 씨에게 고함친다."], ["Guppy, “How de do, sir!", "\"안녕하시오, 선생!"], ["How de do!”", "안녕하시오!\""]], [["“What do Chick and his family want here at this time of the morning, I wonder!”", "\"이런 아침 시각에 칙 영감과 그 식솔이 여기 무슨 볼일이람!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy, nodding to his familiar.", "거피 씨가 자기 지인에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다."]], [["“My dear sir,”", "\"여보게, 선생,\""], ["cries Grandfather Smallweed, “would you do me a favour?", "스몰위드 할아버지가 외친다. \"부탁 하나 들어주겠나?"], ["Would you and your friend be so very obleeging as to carry me into the public-house in the court, while Bart and his sister bring their grandmother along?", "자네와 자네 벗이, 바트와 그 누이가 제 할머니를 모셔 오는 동안, 나를 저 골목 안 술집까지 좀 옮겨주는 너그러움을 베풀어줄 수 있겠나?"], ["Would you do an old man that good turn, sir?”", "늙은이한테 그 정도 호의는 베풀어주겠나, 선생?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy looks at his friend, repeating inquiringly, “The public-house in the court?”", "거피 씨가 벗을 바라보며 캐묻듯 되뇐다."], ["And they prepare to bear the venerable burden to the Sol’s Arms.", "\"골목 안 술집?\" 그러고는 둘이서 그 점잖은 짐을 솔즈 암스로 옮길 채비를 한다."]], [["“There’s your fare!”", "\"이게 자네 삯이야!\""], ["says the patriarch to the coachman with a fierce grin and shaking his incapable fist at him.", "노인이 마부에게 사납게 이를 드러내며, 말 안 듣는 주먹을 그에게 흔들면서 말한다."], ["“Ask me for a penny more, and I’ll have my lawful revenge upon you.", "\"여기서 한 푼이라도 더 달라 했단, 법대로 복수해주마."], ["My dear young men, be easy with me, if you please.", "친애하는 젊은이들, 제발 살살 다뤄주게."], ["Allow me to catch you round the neck.", "자네들 목을 끌어안게 해주게."], ["I won’t squeeze you tighter than I can help.", "어쩔 수 없는 만큼만 꽉 잡을 테니."], ["Oh, Lord!", "오, 맙소사!"], ["Oh, dear me!", "오, 이런!"], ["Oh, my bones!”", "오, 내 뼈마디!\""]], [["It is well that the Sol is not far off, for Mr. Weevle presents an apoplectic appearance before half the distance is accomplished.", "솔즈가 멀지 않은 것이 다행이니, 위블 씨는 거리의 절반도 가기 전에 졸도할 듯한 몰골이 되기 때문이다."], ["With no worse aggravation of his symptoms, however, than the utterance of divers croaking sounds expressive of obstructed respiration, he fulfils his share of the porterage and the benevolent old gentleman is deposited by his own desire in the parlour of the Sol’s Arms.", "그래도 막힌 호흡을 드러내는 갖가지 갈라진 소리를 토해내는 것 말고는 증세가 더 악화되진 않은 채, 그는 자기 몫의 운반을 완수하고, 그 인자한 노신사는 자기 뜻대로 솔즈 암스의 응접실에 부려진다."]], [["“Oh, Lord!” gasps Mr.", "\"오, 맙소사!\""], ["Smallweed, looking about him, breathless, from an arm-chair.", "스몰위드 씨가 안락의자에 앉아 숨을 헐떡이며 주위를 둘러보고 말한다."], ["“Oh, dear me!", "\"오, 이런!"], ["Oh, my bones and back!", "오, 내 뼈와 등짝!"], ["Oh, my aches and pains!", "오, 내 쑤시고 결리는 데!"], ["Sit down, you dancing, prancing, shambling, scrambling poll-parrot!", "좀 앉아, 이 춤추고 뛰고 비틀거리고 허둥대는 앵무새 같은 년아!"], ["Sit down!”", "앉으라고!\""]], [["This little apostrophe to Mrs. Smallweed is occasioned by a propensity on the part of that unlucky old lady whenever she finds herself on her feet to amble about and “set”", "스몰위드 부인에게 던진 이 짤막한 호통은, 그 불운한 노부인이 발을 디디고 설 때마다 어슬렁거리며 돌아다니다 무생물을 향해 \"절\"을 하고, 마치 마녀의 춤이라도 추듯 재잘대는 소리를 곁들이는 버릇 탓에 나온 것이다."], ["to inanimate objects, accompanying herself with a chattering noise, as in a witch dance. A nervous affection has probably as much to do with these demonstrations as any imbecile intention in the poor old woman, but on the present occasion they are so particularly lively in connexion with the Windsor arm-chair, fellow to that in which Mr.", "이 가엾은 노파의 이런 거동에는 어떤 얼빠진 의도 못지않게 신경 질환이 작용한 면도 있겠으나, 이번에는 스몰위드 씨가 앉아 있는 것과 짝을 이루는 윈저식 안락의자를 두고 유난히 활발하게 그 짓을 벌이는 통에, 손주들이 그녀를 그 의자에 눌러 앉히고 나서야 비로소 멈춘다."], ["Smallweed is seated, that she only quite desists when her grandchildren have held her down in it, her lord in the meanwhile bestowing upon her, with great volubility, the endearing epithet of “a pig-headed jackdaw,” repeated a surprising number of times.", "그러는 동안 그녀의 부군은 그녀에게 \"고집통 갈까마귀\"라는 정겨운 별칭을 어찌나 술술 퍼붓는지, 놀라울 만큼 여러 번 되풀이한다."]], [["“My dear sir,”", "\"여보게, 선생,\""], ["Grandfather Smallweed then proceeds, addressing Mr.", "그러더니 스몰위드 할아버지가 거피 씨에게 말을 건넨다."], ["Guppy, “there has been a calamity here.", "\"여기서 변고가 있었네."], ["Have you heard of it, either of you?”", "자네들 둘 중 누구라도 그 소식 들었나?\""]], [["“Heard of it, sir!", "\"들었느냐고요, 선생!"], ["Why, we discovered it.”", "아니, 우리가 발견했는걸요.\""]], [["“You discovered it.", "\"자네들이 발견했다."], ["You two discovered it!", "자네 둘이 발견했어!"], ["Bart, THEY discovered it!”", "바트, 얘들이 발견했다는구나!\""]], [["The two discoverers stare at the Smallweeds, who return the compliment.", "두 발견자가 스몰위드 일가를 빤히 쳐다보고, 일가도 그 응시를 똑같이 돌려준다."]], [["“My dear friends,”", "\"친애하는 벗들,\" 스몰위드 할아버지가 두 손을 내밀며 칭얼대듯 말한다."], ["whines Grandfather Smallweed, putting out both his hands, “I owe you a thousand thanks for discharging the melancholy office of discovering the ashes of Mrs. Smallweed’s brother.”", "\"스몰위드 부인의 오라비 유골을 발견하는 그 우울한 소임을 다해주었으니, 자네들에게 천 번의 감사를 빚졌네.\""]], [["“Eh?”", "\"네?\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Mrs. Smallweed’s brother, my dear friend—her only relation.", "\"스몰위드 부인의 오라비 말이야, 친애하는 벗—그 사람 유일한 혈육이지."], ["We were not on terms, which is to be deplored now, but he never WOULD be on terms.", "우리는 사이가 좋질 못했어, 이제 와선 한탄스러운 일이지만, 그자가 도무지 사이좋게 지내려 하질 않았어."], ["He was not fond of us.", "우리를 좋아하질 않았지."], ["He was eccentric—he was very eccentric.", "별난 사람이었어—아주 별난 사람이었지."], ["Unless he has left a will (which is not at all likely) I shall take out letters of administration.", "그가 유언장을 남기지 않았다면(전혀 그럴 성싶지 않지만) 내가 재산 관리 인가장을 받아낼 걸세."], ["I have come down to look after the property; it must be sealed up, it must be protected.", "재산을 살피러 내려온 거야. 봉인해둬야 해, 보호해둬야 해."], ["I have come down,”", "내려온 거야,\""], ["repeats Grandfather Smallweed, hooking the air towards him with all his ten fingers at once, “to look after the property.”", "스몰위드 할아버지가 열 손가락을 한꺼번에 갈고리처럼 굽혀 허공을 제 쪽으로 그러쥐며 되풀이한다. \"재산을 살피러 말이지.\""]], [["“I think, Small,”", "\"내 생각에, 스몰,\""], ["says the disconsolate Mr.", "풀이 죽은 거피 씨가 말한다."], ["Guppy, “you might have mentioned that the old man was your uncle.”", "\"그 노인이 당신 삼촌이라고 진작 말해줬어도 좋았을 텐데요.\""]], [["“You two were so close about him that I thought you would like me to be the same,”", "\"자네들 둘이 그 양반에 대해 어찌나 입을 다물던지, 나도 똑같이 입을 다물어주길 바라겠거니 했지.\""], ["returns that old bird with a secretly glistening eye.", "그 노물이 몰래 번뜩이는 눈으로 대꾸한다."], ["“Besides, I wasn’t proud of him.”", "\"게다가 난 그자가 자랑스럽지도 않았고.\""]], [["“Besides which, it was nothing to you, you know, whether he was or not,”", "\"게다가 그 사람이 그렇든 말든 당신과는 상관없는 일이었으니까요.\""], ["says Judy.", "주디가 말한다."], ["Also with a secretly glistening eye.", "역시 몰래 번뜩이는 눈으로."]], [["“He never saw me in his life to know me,”", "\"그자는 살아생전 날 알아볼 만큼 본 적도 없어.\""], ["observed Small; “I don’t know why I should introduce HIM, I am sure!”", "스몰이 한마디 거든다. \"내가 왜 그자를 끌어들여야 하느냐고, 정말!\""]], [["“No, he never communicated with us, which is to be deplored,”", "\"그래, 그자는 우리와 연락한 적이 없어, 한탄스러운 일이지,\""], ["the old gentleman strikes in, “but I have come to look after the property—to look over the papers, and to look after the property.", "노신사가 끼어든다. \"하지만 난 재산을 살피러 온 거야—서류를 검토하고, 재산을 살피러."], ["We shall make good our title.", "우리는 권리를 확립할 거야."], ["It is in the hands of my solicitor. Mr.", "그건 내 변호사 손에 있어."], ["Tulkinghorn, of Lincoln’s Inn Fields, over the way there, is so good as to act as my solicitor; and grass don’t grow under HIS feet, I can tell ye.", "저기 길 건너 링컨스 인 필즈의 토킹혼 씨가 친절하게도 내 변호사로 나서주었지. 장담하건대, 그 양반 발밑에선 풀도 안 자라."], ["Krook was Mrs.", "크룩은 스몰위드 부인의 유일한 오라비였어."], ["Smallweed’s only brother; she had no relation but Krook, and Krook had no relation but Mrs.", "부인한텐 크룩 말곤 혈육이 없었고, 크룩한텐 스몰위드 부인 말곤 혈육이 없었지."], ["Smallweed. I am speaking of your brother, you brimstone black-beetle, that was seventy-six years of age.”", "네 오라비 얘길 하는 거다, 이 유황불 검정 딱정벌레야, 일흔여섯 먹은 그 양반 말이야.\""]], [["Mrs. Smallweed instantly begins to shake her head and pipe up, “Seventy-six pound seven and sevenpence!", "스몰위드 부인이 즉시 고개를 흔들며 새된 소리로 외기 시작한다."], ["Seventy-six thousand bags of money!", "\"일흔여섯 파운드 칠 실링 칠 펜스!"], ["Seventy-six hundred thousand million of parcels of bank-notes!”", "일흔여섯 천 자루의 돈! 일흔여섯 억조 꾸러미의 지폐!\""]], [["“Will somebody give me a quart pot?”", "\"누가 나한테 일 쿼트짜리 술잔 좀 주겠나?\""], ["exclaims her exasperated husband, looking helplessly about him and finding no missile within his reach.", "격분한 그녀의 남편이 손닿는 곳에 던질 만한 것을 못 찾고 어쩔 줄 몰라 주위를 둘러보며 외친다."], ["“Will somebody obleege me with a spittoon?", "\"누가 나한테 가래통 좀 갖다주는 호의를 베풀겠나?"], ["Will somebody hand me anything hard and bruising to pelt at her?", "누가 단단하고 멍이 들 만한 걸 손에 쥐여줘서 저년한테 내던지게 해주겠나?"], ["You hag, you cat, you dog, you brimstone barker!” Here Mr.", "이 마귀할멈, 이 고양이년, 이 개년, 이 유황불 짖는 개야!\""], ["Smallweed, wrought up to the highest pitch by his own eloquence, actually throws Judy at her grandmother in default of anything else, by butting that young virgin at the old lady with such force as he can muster and then dropping into his chair in a heap.", "이 대목에서 스몰위드 씨는 제 입심에 한껏 격앙된 나머지, 달리 던질 게 없자 정말로 주디를 할머니에게 집어 던지듯, 이 젊은 처녀를 모을 수 있는 온 힘을 다해 노부인 쪽으로 들이받고는, 의자에 푹석 무너져 내린다."]], [["“Shake me up, somebody, if you’ll be so good,”", "\"누가 좀, 괜찮다면, 나를 일으켜 흔들어주게.\""], ["says the voice from within the faintly struggling bundle into which he has collapsed.", "그가 폭삭 주저앉아 만든, 힘없이 버둥대는 보따리 속에서 목소리가 들려온다."], ["“I have come to look after the property.", "\"재산을 살피러 온 거야."], ["Shake me up, and call in the police on duty at the next house to be explained to about the property.", "나를 흔들어 일으키고, 옆집에 근무하는 경관을 불러서 재산에 대해 설명을 해줘."], ["My solicitor will be here presently to protect the property.", "내 변호사가 곧 와서 재산을 지켜줄 거야."], ["Transportation or the gallows for anybody who shall touch the property!”", "재산에 손대는 놈은 누구든 유배형이나 교수형이다!\""], ["As his dutiful grandchildren set him up, panting, and putting him through the usual restorative process of shaking and punching, he still repeats like an echo, “The—the property!", "효성 깊은 손주들이 그를 일으켜 세우자, 그는 헐떡이며, 흔들고 두드리는 늘 하던 회복 절차를 받으면서도, 메아리처럼 여전히 되뇐다. \"그—그 재산!"], ["The property!", "그 재산!"], ["Property!”", "재산!\""]], [["Mr. Weevle and Mr.", "위블 씨와 거피 씨가 서로를 바라본다."], ["Guppy look at each other, the former as having relinquished the whole affair, the latter with a discomfited countenance as having entertained some lingering expectations yet.", "전자는 그 일을 통째로 단념한 자로서, 후자는 아직 어떤 미련 같은 기대를 품었던 자로서 낭패한 얼굴로."], ["But there is nothing to be done in opposition to the Smallweed interest. Mr.", "그러나 스몰위드 측 이해관계에 맞서 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없다."], ["Tulkinghorn’s clerk comes down from his official pew in the chambers to mention to the police that Mr. Tulkinghorn is answerable for its being all correct about the next of kin and that the papers and effects will be formally taken possession of in due time and course.", "토킹혼 씨의 서기가 법률 사무소의 제 공식 자리에서 내려와 경관에게 일러두기를, 가장 가까운 혈육에 관한 일은 모두 토킹혼 씨가 책임지고 정확히 처리할 것이며, 서류와 유품은 때가 되면 절차에 따라 정식으로 점유될 것이라 한다."], ["Mr. Smallweed is at once permitted so far to assert his supremacy as to be carried on a visit of sentiment into the next house and upstairs into Miss Flite’s deserted room, where he looks like a hideous bird of prey newly added to her aviary.", "스몰위드 씨는 이내 자기 권위를 그만큼은 내세우도록 허락받아, 감회에 젖은 방문 길에 옆집으로 옮겨져 위층 플라이트 양의 텅 빈 방으로 들어가는데, 그 모습이 마치 그녀의 새장에 새로 들여놓은 흉측한 맹금처럼 보인다."]], [["The arrival of this unexpected heir soon taking wind in the court still makes good for the Sol and keeps the court upon its mettle. Mrs. Piper and Mrs. Perkins think it hard upon the young man if there really is no will, and consider that a handsome present ought to be made him out of the estate. Young Piper and young Perkins, as members of that restless juvenile circle which is the terror of the foot-passengers in Chancery Lane, crumble into ashes behind the pump and under the archway all day long, where wild yells and hootings take place over their remains. Little Swills and Miss M. Melvilleson enter into affable conversation with their patrons, feeling that these unusual occurrences level the barriers between professionals and non-professionals. Mr. Bogsby puts up “The popular song of King Death, with chorus by the whole strength of the company,” as the great Harmonic feature of the week and announces in the bill that “J. G. B. is induced to do so at a considerable extra expense in consequence of a wish which has been very generally expressed at the bar by a large body of respectable individuals and in homage to a late melancholy event which has aroused so much sensation.” There is one point connected with the deceased upon which the court is particularly anxious, namely, that the fiction of a full-sized coffin should be preserved, though there is so little to put in it. Upon the undertaker’s stating in the Sol’s bar in the course of the day that he has received orders to construct “a six-footer,” the general solicitude is much relieved, and it is considered that Mr. Smallweed’s conduct does him great honour.", "이 뜻밖의 상속인이 들이닥쳤다는 소문이 골목에 금세 퍼지면서, 여전히 솔즈에는 이득이 되고 골목은 활기를 잃지 않는다. 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인은 정말로 유언장이 없다면 그 젊은이가 딱하다고 여기며, 유산에서 한몫 두둑이 떼어 그에게 선물로 줘야 마땅하다고 본다. 어린 파이퍼와 어린 퍼킨스는, 챈서리 거리 행인들의 공포의 대상인 그 들썩이는 아이 무리의 일원으로서, 하루 종일 펌프 뒤와 아치 길 아래에서 재가 되어 흩어지고, 그 잔해를 두고 거친 비명과 야유가 벌어진다. 꼬마 스윌스와 멜빌슨 양은 단골들과 싹싹하게 담소를 나누는데, 이 이례적인 사태가 직업인과 비직업인 사이의 장벽을 허문다고 느끼기 때문이다. 보그스비 씨는 \"온 출연진의 총력을 다한 합창이 곁들여진 인기곡 ‘죽음의 왕’\"을 그 주의 위대한 화음 모임 주제로 내걸고, 광고지에 이렇게 알린다. \"제이 지 비는 이번에 상당한 추가 비용을 무릅쓰고 이를 마련하니, 이는 술집에서 다수의 점잖은 분들이 두루 표한 바람에 따른 것이며, 또한 그토록 많은 이의 심금을 울린 근래의 어느 우울한 사건에 경의를 표하고자 함입니다.\" 망자와 관련하여 골목이 유난히 마음 졸이는 점이 한 가지 있으니, 곧 안에 채워 넣을 것이 그토록 적은데도 어른 크기 관이라는 허구만은 지켜져야 한다는 것이다. 그날 중에 장의사가 솔즈 술집에서 \"육 척짜리\"를 짜라는 주문을 받았다고 밝히자, 모두의 노심초사가 크게 누그러지고, 스몰위드 씨의 처사가 그에게 큰 명예가 된다고들 여긴다."]], [["Out of the court, and a long way out of it, there is considerable excitement too, for men of science and philosophy come to look, and carriages set down doctors at the corner who arrive with the same intent, and there is more learned talk about inflammable gases and phosphuretted hydrogen than the court has ever imagined.", "골목 밖, 그것도 한참 밖에서도 상당한 흥분이 인다. 과학자와 철학자 들이 구경하러 오고, 마차들이 같은 뜻으로 찾아온 의사들을 모퉁이에 내려놓으며, 가연성 기체니 인화수소니 하는, 골목이 일찍이 상상도 못 한 유식한 이야기가 오간다."], ["Some of these authorities (of course the wisest) hold with indignation that the deceased had no business to die in the alleged manner; and being reminded by other authorities of a certain inquiry into the evidence for such deaths reprinted in the sixth volume of the Philosophical Transactions; and also of a book not quite unknown on English medical jurisprudence; and likewise of the Italian case of the Countess Cornelia Baudi as set forth in detail by one Bianchini, prebendary of Verona, who wrote a scholarly work or so and was occasionally heard of in his time as having gleams of reason in him; and also of the testimony of Messrs.", "이 권위자들 가운데 일부는(물론 가장 현명한 자들이다) 망자가 그런 식으로 죽었을 리 없다고 분개하며 우긴다. 다른 권위자들이 그들에게 일깨우기를, 그런 죽음의 증거에 관한 어떤 조사가 「철학회보」"], ["Fodere and Mere, two pestilent Frenchmen who WOULD investigate the subject; and further, of the corroborative testimony of Monsieur Le Cat, a rather celebrated French surgeon once upon a time, who had the unpoliteness to live in a house where such a case occurred and even to write an account of it—still they regard the late Mr. Krook’s obstinacy in going out of the world by any such by-way as wholly unjustifiable and personally offensive.", "제육권에 재수록되어 있고, 또 영국 의학 법리학에 관한 제법 알려진 책 한 권도 있으며, 마찬가지로 베로나의 참사회원 비안키니가—학술서 한두 권을 써냈고 당대에 더러 이성의 번득임이 엿보이던 인물로—세밀히 기술한 이탈리아의 코르넬리아 바우디 백작 부인 사건도 있고, 또 그 주제를 기어이 파고들겠다고 한 골치 아픈 프랑스인 둘, 포데레 씨와 메르 씨의 증언도 있으며, 더 나아가 한때 제법 유명했던 프랑스 외과의 르 카 씨—그런 사건이 일어난 집에 살았던 데다 그에 대한 기록까지 써내는 무례함을 보인 자다—의 뒷받침하는 증언도 있다고 하는데도, 그들은 여전히 고 크룩 씨가 그런 옆길로 세상을 떠나겠다고 고집부린 것을 도무지 정당화할 수 없으며 개인적으로 불쾌하기까지 하다고 여긴다."], ["The less the court understands of all this, the more the court likes it, and the greater enjoyment it has in the stock in trade of the Sol’s Arms.", "골목은 이 모든 것을 알아듣지 못할수록 더 좋아하고, 솔즈 암스에 쟁여둔 상품을 한층 즐겁게 누린다."], ["Then there comes the artist of a picture newspaper, with a foreground and figures ready drawn for anything from a wreck on the Cornish coast to a review in Hyde Park or a meeting in Manchester, and in Mrs.", "이윽고 그림 신문의 화가가, 콘월 해안의 난파선에서부터 하이드 파크의 열병식이나 맨체스터의 집회까지 무엇에든 갖다 붙일 수 있는 배경과 인물을 미리 그려둔 채 찾아온다."], ["Perkins’ own room, memorable evermore, he then and there throws in upon the block Mr. Krook’s house, as large as life; in fact, considerably larger, making a very temple of it.", "그러고는 영원히 잊지 못할 퍼킨스 부인의 방에서, 거기 그 자리에서 판 위에 크룩 씨의 집을 실물 크기로 그려 넣으니, 사실은 실물보다 한참 크게, 아예 신전 하나로 만들어버린다."], ["Similarly, being permitted to look in at the door of the fatal chamber, he depicts that apartment as three-quarters of a mile long by fifty yards high, at which the court is particularly charmed.", "마찬가지로 그 운명의 방 문 안을 들여다보도록 허락받자, 그는 그 방을 길이는 사분의 삼 마일에 높이는 오십 야드로 묘사하는데, 골목은 이를 유난히 마음에 들어 한다."], ["All this time the two gentlemen before mentioned pop in and out of every house and assist at the philosophical disputations—go everywhere and listen to everybody—and yet are always diving into the Sol’s parlour and writing with the ravenous little pens on the tissue-paper.", "이 내내 앞서 말한 두 신사는 집집마다 들락거리며 철학 논쟁을 거들고—사방을 쏘다니며 누구의 말이든 귀담아듣고—그러면서도 늘 솔즈의 응접실로 잠수해 들어가 게걸스러운 작은 펜으로 박엽지에 갈겨쓴다."]], [["At last come the coroner and his inquiry, like as before, except that the coroner cherishes this case as being out of the common way and tells the gentlemen of the jury, in his private capacity, that “that would seem to be an unlucky house next door, gentlemen, a destined house; but so we sometimes find it, and these are mysteries we can’t account for!”", "마침내 검시관과 그의 심리가 전과 똑같이 찾아드는데, 다만 검시관이 이번 사건을 흔치 않은 일로 여겨 애지중지하며, 배심원 신사들에게 사사로이 이르기를, \"여러분, 저 옆집은 어째 불운한 집인 듯합니다, 운명 지어진 집 말입니다. 그래도 우리가 더러 그런 일을 마주치곤 하는데, 이런 건 우리가 설명할 수 없는 수수께끼지요!\""], ["After which the six-footer comes into action and is much admired.", "그러고 나서 육 척짜리가 등장하여 큰 찬사를 받는다."]], [["In all these proceedings Mr. Guppy has so slight a part, except when he gives his evidence, that he is moved on like a private individual and can only haunt the secret house on the outside, where he has the mortification of seeing Mr.", "이 모든 절차에서 거피 씨가 맡은 몫은, 증언할 때를 빼면 어찌나 미미한지, 그는 일개 사인처럼 떠밀려 다니며 그 비밀스러운 집을 바깥에서나 맴돌 따름이다."], ["Smallweed padlocking the door, and of bitterly knowing himself to be shut out.", "거기서 그는 스몰위드 씨가 문에 자물쇠를 채우는 것을 보는 굴욕을 겪고, 자기가 그 집에서 따돌려졌음을 쓰라리게 깨닫는다."], ["But before these proceedings draw to a close, that is to say, on the night next after the catastrophe, Mr. Guppy has a thing to say that must be said to Lady Dedlock.", "그러나 이 절차들이 마무리되기 전에, 다시 말해 그 참사 이튿날 밤, 거피 씨에게는 반드시 데드록 마님에게 해야 할 말이 한 가지 있다."]], [["For which reason, with a sinking heart and with that hang-dog sense of guilt upon him which dread and watching enfolded in the Sol’s Arms have produced, the young man of the name of Guppy presents himself at the town mansion at about seven o’clock in the evening and requests to see her ladyship.", "그런 까닭에, 가라앉는 가슴과, 솔즈 암스에 갇혀 두려움과 망보기로 빚어진 그 풀 죽은 죄책감을 안고서, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이는 저녁 일곱 시쯤 시내 저택에 모습을 드러내고 마님을 뵙기를 청한다. 머큐리가 마님은 만찬에 나가실 참이라고 답한다."], ["Mercury replies that she is going out to dinner; don’t he see the carriage at the door?", "문 앞에 마차가 있는 게 안 보이느냐는 것이다. 그렇다, 그도 문 앞에 마차가 있는 것을 본다."], ["Yes, he does see the carriage at the door; but he wants to see my Lady too.", "하지만 그는 마님도 뵙고 싶다는 것이다."]], [["Mercury is disposed, as he will presently declare to a fellow-gentleman in waiting, “to pitch into the young man”; but his instructions are positive.", "머큐리는, 잠시 후 대기 중인 동료 신사에게 떠벌리겠지만, \"저 젊은 놈을 한 대 갈겨주고\" 싶은 마음이다."], ["Therefore he sulkily supposes that the young man must come up into the library.", "그러나 그가 받은 지시는 명확하다. 그래서 그는 시무룩하게, 이 젊은이를 서재로 올려보내야겠거니 한다."], ["There he leaves the young man in a large room, not over-light, while he makes report of him.", "그곳, 그리 밝지 않은 큰 방에 젊은이를 남겨두고 그는 보고하러 간다."]], [["Mr. Guppy looks into the shade in all directions, discovering everywhere a certain charred and whitened little heap of coal or wood.", "거피 씨는 사방의 어둠 속을 들여다보며, 어디서나 거뭇하게 그을리고 희끄무레해진 작은 석탄인지 나무인지의 무더기를 발견한다."], ["Presently he hears a rustling.", "이윽고 그는 사각거리는 소리를 듣는다."], ["Is it—?", "그건—?"], ["No, it’s no ghost, but fair flesh and blood, most brilliantly dressed.", "아니, 유령이 아니라, 더없이 눈부시게 차려입은, 멀쩡한 살과 피다."]], [["“I have to beg your ladyship’s pardon,”", "\"마님께 용서를 빌어야겠습니다.\""], ["Mr. Guppy stammers, very downcast.", "거피 씨가 잔뜩 풀이 죽어 더듬거린다."], ["“This is an inconvenient time—”", "\"불편한 시각에—\""]], [["“I told you, you could come at any time.”", "\"아무 때나 와도 좋다고 했을 텐데.\""], ["She takes a chair, looking straight at him as on the last occasion.", "그녀가 의자를 잡으며, 지난번처럼 그를 똑바로 바라본다."]], [["“Thank your ladyship.", "\"감사합니다, 마님."], ["Your ladyship is very affable.”", "마님께선 참으로 너그러우십니다.\""]], [["“You can sit down.”", "\"앉아도 좋네.\""], ["There is not much affability in her tone.", "그 어조에는 너그러움이 그리 묻어 있지 않다."]], [["“I don’t know, your ladyship, that it’s worth while my sitting down and detaining you, for I—I have not got the letters that I mentioned when I had the honour of waiting on your ladyship.”", "\"마님, 제가 앉아서 마님을 붙들어둘 만한 일인지 모르겠습니다. 저는—제가 마님을 뵙는 영광을 누렸을 때 말씀드린 그 편지들을 가져오지 못했으니까요.\""]], [["“Have you come merely to say so?”", "\"그저 그 말을 하러 온 것인가?\""]], [["“Merely to say so, your ladyship.” Mr.", "\"그저 그 말씀을 드리러 왔습니다, 마님.\""], ["Guppy besides being depressed, disappointed, and uneasy, is put at a further disadvantage by the splendour and beauty of her appearance.", "거피 씨는 풀 죽고 실망하고 마음이 불편한 데다, 그녀의 자태가 뿜는 광채와 미모에 한층 더 기가 꺾인다."]], [["She knows its influence perfectly, has studied it too well to miss a grain of its effect on any one.", "그녀는 그 영향력을 완벽히 알고 있으니, 누구에게든 그 효과가 한 톨도 빗나가지 않도록 너무도 잘 연구해두었다."], ["As she looks at him so steadily and coldly, he not only feels conscious that he has no guide in the least perception of what is really the complexion of her thoughts, but also that he is being every moment, as it were, removed further and further from her.", "그녀가 그토록 한결같고 차갑게 그를 바라보자, 그는 그녀 생각의 빛깔이 정녕 어떠한지를 조금도 가늠할 길잡이가 없음을 의식할 뿐 아니라, 자신이 매 순간 그녀로부터, 말하자면, 점점 더 멀리 밀려나고 있음마저 느낀다."]], [["She will not speak, it is plain.", "그녀가 입을 열지 않으리란 건 분명하다."], ["So he must.", "그러니 그가 입을 열 수밖에."]], [["“In short, your ladyship,” says Mr.", "\"한마디로, 마님,\""], ["Guppy like a meanly penitent thief, “the person I was to have had the letters of, has come to a sudden end, and—”", "거피 씨가 비굴하게 뉘우치는 도둑처럼 말한다. \"제가 그 편지들을 받기로 했던 사람이 갑작스레 최후를 맞아서—\""], ["He stops.", "그가 말을 멈춘다."], ["Lady Dedlock calmly finishes the sentence.", "데드록 마님이 차분히 그 문장을 마무리 짓는다."]], [["“And the letters are destroyed with the person?”", "\"그래서 편지들도 그 사람과 함께 없어졌다는 건가?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy would say no if he could—as he is unable to hide.", "거피 씨는 할 수만 있다면 아니라고 하고 싶지만—숨기지 못한다."]], [["“I believe so, your ladyship.”", "\"그런 것 같습니다, 마님.\""]], [["If he could see the least sparkle of relief in her face now?", "지금 그녀의 얼굴에서 안도의 빛이 조금이라도 번뜩이는 것을 그가 볼 수 있다면?"], ["No, he could see no such thing, even if that brave outside did not utterly put him away, and he were not looking beyond it and about it.", "아니, 그는 그런 것을 볼 수 없으니, 설령 그 의연한 겉모습이 그를 아주 밀쳐내지 않고, 그가 그 너머와 그 언저리를 살피고 있었다 한들 마찬가지다."]], [["He falters an awkward excuse or two for his failure.", "그는 자기 실패에 대한 어색한 변명을 한두 마디 더듬거린다."]], [["“Is this all you have to say?”", "\"이게 자네가 할 말의 전부인가?\""], ["inquires Lady Dedlock, having heard him out—or as nearly out as he can stumble.", "데드록 마님이, 그의 말을 끝까지—혹은 그가 더듬거리며 마칠 수 있는 데까지—다 듣고 나서 묻는다."]], [["Mr. Guppy thinks that’s all.", "거피 씨는 그게 전부라고 생각한다."]], [["“You had better be sure that you wish to say nothing more to me, this being the last time you will have the opportunity.”", "\"내게 더 할 말이 정말 없는지 잘 헤아려보는 게 좋을 거네. 이번이 자네가 그럴 기회를 갖는 마지막일 테니.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy is quite sure.", "거피 씨는 확실하다."], ["And indeed he has no such wish at present, by any means.", "그리고 정녕 지금은 그런 바람이 도무지 없다."]], [["“That is enough.", "\"그걸로 됐네."], ["I will dispense with excuses.", "변명은 됐어. 잘 가게!\""], ["Good evening to you!” And she rings for Mercury to show the young man of the name of Guppy out.", "그러고는 그녀가 종을 울려, 거피라는 이름의 젊은이를 배웅하라고 머큐리를 부른다."]], [["But in that house, in that same moment, there happens to be an old man of the name of Tulkinghorn.", "그러나 그 집에는, 바로 그 순간, 토킹혼이라는 이름의 노인이 마침 있다."], ["And that old man, coming with his quiet footstep to the library, has his hand at that moment on the handle of the door—comes in—and comes face to face with the young man as he is leaving the room.", "그리고 그 노인이 조용한 발걸음으로 서재로 다가와, 바로 그 순간 문손잡이에 손을 얹고—들어와서—방을 나서던 젊은이와 정면으로 마주친다."]], [["One glance between the old man and the lady, and for an instant the blind that is always down flies up.", "노인과 마님 사이에 단 한 번 눈길이 오가고, 한순간, 늘 내려져 있던 차양이 휙 올라간다."], ["Suspicion, eager and sharp, looks out.", "의심이, 간절하고도 날카로운 의심이 내다본다."], ["Another instant, close again.", "또 한순간, 다시 닫힌다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Lady Dedlock.", "\"실례했습니다, 데드록 마님."], ["I beg your pardon a thousand times.", "천 번 만 번 실례했습니다."], ["It is so very unusual to find you here at this hour.", "이 시각에 여기 계시다니 참으로 드문 일이라서요."], ["I supposed the room was empty.", "방이 비었으려니 했습니다."], ["I beg your pardon!”", "실례했습니다!\""]], [["“Stay!”", "\"잠깐!\""], ["She negligently calls him back.", "그녀가 무심하게 그를 불러 세운다."], ["“Remain here, I beg.", "\"여기 계세요, 부탁드려요."], ["I am going out to dinner.", "나는 만찬에 나갑니다."], ["I have nothing more to say to this young man!”", "이 젊은이에게 더 할 말은 없어요!\""]], [["The disconcerted young man bows, as he goes out, and cringingly hopes that Mr. Tulkinghorn of the Fields is well.", "당황한 젊은이는 나가면서 인사를 굽실하고, 필즈의 토킹혼 씨가 안녕하시기를 비굴하게 바란다."]], [["“Aye, aye?”", "\"어, 어?\""], ["says the lawyer, looking at him from under his bent brows, though he has no need to look again—not he.", "변호사가 구부정한 눈썹 아래로 그를 바라보며 말한다. 다시 볼 필요도 없으면서—그는 그런 사람이 아니지만."], ["“From Kenge and Carboy’s, surely?”", "\"켄지 앤드 카보이 사람이 분명하지?\""]], [["“Kenge and Carboy’s, Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "\"켄지 앤드 카보이입니다, 토킹혼 씨."], ["Name of Guppy, sir.”", "이름은 거피입니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“To be sure.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["Why, thank you, Mr. Guppy, I am very well!”", "어허, 고맙네, 거피 군, 난 아주 잘 있네!\""]], [["“Happy to hear it, sir.", "\"그 말씀 들으니 기쁩니다, 선생님."], ["You can’t be too well, sir, for the credit of the profession.”", "우리 직업의 명예를 위해서라도 선생님은 아무리 건강하셔도 지나치지 않지요, 선생님.\""]], [["“Thank you, Mr. Guppy!”", "\"고맙네, 거피 군!\""]], [["Mr. Guppy sneaks away.", "거피 씨가 슬그머니 물러간다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn, such a foil in his old-fashioned rusty black to Lady Dedlock’s brightness, hands her down the staircase to her carriage.", "토킹혼 씨는, 그 구식의 빛바랜 검은 옷차림이 데드록 마님의 광채와 어찌나 대조되는지, 그녀를 부축해 계단 아래 마차까지 모신다."], ["He returns rubbing his chin, and rubs it a good deal in the course of the evening. A Turn of the Screw", "그는 턱을 문지르며 돌아오고, 그날 저녁 내내 턱을 꽤나 문지른다."]], [["“Now, what,” says Mr.", "“그래, 이게,”"], ["George, “may this be?", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["Is it blank cartridge or ball?", "“뭘까? 공포탄인가, 실탄인가?"], ["A flash in the pan or a shot?”", "헛불인가, 진짜 발포인가?”"]], [["An open letter is the subject of the trooper’s speculations, and it seems to perplex him mightily.", "부대원이 골똘히 살피는 대상은 펼쳐진 편지 한 통인데, 그를 단단히 헷갈리게 만드는 모양이다."], ["He looks at it at arm’s length, brings it close to him, holds it in his right hand, holds it in his left hand, reads it with his head on this side, with his head on that side, contracts his eyebrows, elevates them, still cannot satisfy himself.", "그는 편지를 팔 길이만큼 멀찍이 떼어 들여다보다가, 바짝 끌어당겨 보고, 오른손에 쥐었다가 왼손에 쥐었다가, 고개를 이쪽으로 기울여 읽고 저쪽으로 기울여 읽고, 눈썹을 찌푸렸다가 치켜올렸다가 해도 좀처럼 마음이 놓이지 않는다."], ["He smooths it out upon the table with his heavy palm, and thoughtfully walking up and down the gallery, makes a halt before it every now and then to come upon it with a fresh eye.", "그는 두툼한 손바닥으로 편지를 탁자에 펴 누른 다음, 생각에 잠겨 사격장을 오르락내리락하며 이따금 그 앞에 멈춰 서서 새로운 눈길로 다시 들여다본다."], ["Even that won’t do.", "그래도 소용이 없다."], ["“Is it,” Mr.", "“이게,”"], ["George still muses, “blank cartridge or ball?”", "조지 씨는 여전히 곱씹는다. “공포탄인가, 실탄인가?”"]], [["Phil Squod, with the aid of a brush and paint-pot, is employed in the distance whitening the targets, softly whistling in quick-march time and in drum-and-fife manner that he must and will go back again to the girl he left behind him.", "필 스쿼드는 저만치서 솔과 페인트 통을 들고 표적에 흰 칠을 하느라 바쁜데, 빠른 행진곡 박자로, 북과 피리 가락처럼, 자기는 두고 온 그 처녀에게 반드시 돌아가야 하고 또 돌아가고 말겠다는 노래를 나직이 휘파람으로 분다."]], [["“Phil!”", "“필!”"], ["The trooper beckons as he calls him.", "부대원이 부르며 손짓한다."]], [["Phil approaches in his usual way, sidling off at first as if he were going anywhere else and then bearing down upon his commander like a bayonet-charge.", "필은 늘 하던 식으로 다가오는데, 처음에는 마치 딴 데로 가려는 양 비스듬히 빠지다가 이내 총검 돌격하듯 상관에게 들이닥친다."], ["Certain splashes of white show in high relief upon his dirty face, and he scrapes his one eyebrow with the handle of the brush.", "더러운 얼굴에는 흰 페인트 자국 몇 점이 도드라지게 박혀 있고, 그는 솔 자루로 하나뿐인 눈썹을 긁적인다."]], [["“Attention, Phil!", "“차렷, 필!"], ["Listen to this.”", "이것 좀 들어봐.”"]], [["“Steady, commander, steady.”", "“각오됐습니다, 대장님, 각오됐습니다.”"]], [["“‘Sir. Allow me to remind you (though there is no legal necessity for my doing so, as you are aware) that the bill at two months’ date drawn on yourself by Mr.", "“‘귀하."], ["Matthew Bagnet, and by you accepted, for the sum of ninety-seven pounds four shillings and ninepence, will become due to-morrow, when you will please be prepared to take up the same on presentation.", "굳이 상기시켜 드릴 법적 의무는 없습니다만(귀하도 아시다시피), 매슈 배그닛 씨가 귀하 앞으로 발행하고 귀하가 인수한, 금액 구십칠 파운드 사 실링 구 펜스, 만기 두 달짜리 어음이 내일 만기가 됩니다. 제시되는 즉시 결제하실 수 있도록 준비해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["Yours, Joshua Smallweed.’ What do you make of that, Phil?”", "조슈아 스몰위드 올림.’ 어떻게 생각해, 필?”"]], [["“Mischief, guv’ner.”", "“말썽입니다, 두목.”"]], [["“Why?”", "“왜?”"]], [["“I think,”", "“제 생각엔요,”"], ["replies Phil after pensively tracing out a cross-wrinkle in his forehead with the brush-handle, “that mischeevious consequences is always meant when money’s asked for.”", "필이 솔 자루로 이마에 가로 주름을 골똘히 그어 보고는 대답한다. “돈을 내놓으라고 할 땐 늘 말썽스러운 일이 벌어지게 마련이거든요.”"]], [["“Lookye, Phil,”", "“이봐, 필,”"], ["says the trooper, sitting on the table.", "부대원이 탁자에 걸터앉으며 말한다."], ["“First and last, I have paid, I may say, half as much again as this principal in interest and one thing and another.”", "“처음부터 끝까지 따져보면, 나는 이 원금에다가 이자며 이것저것 해서 그 절반은 더 갚았다고 할 수 있어.”"]], [["Phil intimates by sidling back a pace or two, with a very unaccountable wrench of his wry face, that he does not regard the transaction as being made more promising by this incident.", "필은 한두 걸음 비스듬히 물러서며 비뚤어진 얼굴을 도무지 알 수 없게 일그러뜨리는데, 이 일로 거래의 앞날이 한층 밝아졌다고는 보지 않는다는 뜻이다."]], [["“And lookye further, Phil,”", "“그리고 또 이봐, 필,”"], ["says the trooper, staying his premature conclusions with a wave of his hand.", "부대원이 손짓으로 그의 성급한 결론을 막으며 말한다."], ["“There has always been an understanding that this bill was to be what they call renewed.", "“이 어음은 늘 그쪽 말로 갱신하기로 양해가 돼 있었어."], ["And it has been renewed no end of times.", "그리고 셀 수 없이 갱신돼 왔단 말이지."], ["What do you say now?”", "이제 뭐라고 할 텐가?”"]], [["“I say that I think the times is come to a end at last.”", "“이번엔 그 셀 수 없는 게 결국 끝장났구나, 그렇게 말하겠습니다.”"]], [["“You do?", "“그래?"], ["Humph!", "흠!"], ["I am much of the same mind myself.”", "나도 거의 같은 생각이야.”"]], [["“Joshua Smallweed is him that was brought here in a chair?”", "“조슈아 스몰위드라면, 의자에 실려서 여기 왔던 그 양반입니까?”"]], [["“The same.”", "“바로 그 사람이지.”"]], [["“Guv’ner,”", "“두목,”"], ["says Phil with exceeding gravity, “he’s a leech in his dispositions, he’s a screw and a wice in his actions, a snake in his twistings, and a lobster in his claws.”", "필이 더없이 엄숙하게 말한다. “그자는 성미로 보면 거머리고, 하는 짓으로 보면 나사에 죔쇠고, 몸 비트는 걸로 보면 뱀이고, 집게발로 보면 가재입니다.”"]], [["Having thus expressively uttered his sentiments, Mr. Squod, after waiting a little to ascertain if any further remark be expected of him, gets back by his usual series of movements to the target he has in hand and vigorously signifies through his former musical medium that he must and he will return to that ideal young lady.", "이렇게 자기 소견을 절묘하게 토해낸 스쿼드 씨는, 더 할 말을 기다리는지 잠시 살피다가, 늘 하던 일련의 동작으로 손에 잡고 있던 표적으로 돌아가, 아까 그 음악적 수단을 통해 자기는 반드시 그 가상의 아가씨에게 돌아가고 말겠다고 힘차게 알린다."], ["George, having folded the letter, walks in that direction.", "편지를 접은 조지는 그쪽으로 걸어간다."]], [["“There IS a way, commander,”", "“방법이 하나 있긴 합니다, 대장님,”"], ["says Phil, looking cunningly at him, “of settling this.”", "필이 그를 능청스레 쳐다보며 말한다. “이걸 해결할 방법이요.”"]], [["“Paying the money, I suppose?", "“돈을 갚는 거겠지?"], ["I wish I could.”", "나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠다.”"]], [["Phil shakes his head.", "필은 고개를 젓는다."], ["“No, guv’ner, no; not so bad as that.", "“아뇨, 두목, 아뇨, 그렇게 고약한 건 아니고요."], ["There IS a way,”", "방법이 하나 있습니다,”"], ["says Phil with a highly artistic turn of his brush; “what I’m a-doing at present.”", "필이 솔을 아주 예술적으로 한 번 휘두르며 말한다. “지금 제가 하고 있는 거죠.”"]], [["“Whitewashing.”", "“흰 칠 말이냐.”"]], [["“A pretty way that would be!", "필이 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["Do you know what would become of the Bagnets in that case?", "“그것참 근사한 방법이겠다! 그러면 배그닛네가 어떻게 되는지 알기나 해?"], ["Do you know they would be ruined to pay off my old scores?", "내 묵은 빚을 갚느라 그 사람들이 거덜 나는 거 몰라?"], ["YOU’RE a moral character,”", "너 참 도덕적인 인간이다,”"], ["says the trooper, eyeing him in his large way with no small indignation; “upon my life you are, Phil!”", "부대원이 적잖은 분노를 담아 그 특유의 너그러운 눈길로 그를 쳐다보며 말한다. “정말이지, 그래, 필!”"]], [["Phil, on one knee at the target, is in course of protesting earnestly, though not without many allegorical scoops of his brush and smoothings of the white surface round the rim with his thumb, that he had forgotten the Bagnet responsibility and would not so much as injure a hair of the head of any member of that worthy family when steps are audible in the long passage without, and a cheerful voice is heard to wonder whether George is at home.", "표적 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇은 필은, 솔로 비유적인 곡선을 잔뜩 그어대고 엄지로 가장자리 둘레의 흰 면을 매끈하게 다듬으면서도, 배그닛네에 대한 책임을 깜박했을 뿐 그 훌륭한 식구 누구의 머리카락 하나라도 다칠 마음은 없었노라고 진지하게 항변하는 참인데, 그때 바깥 긴 복도에서 발소리가 들리고, 조지가 집에 있는지 궁금해하는 쾌활한 목소리가 들린다."], ["Phil, with a look at his master, hobbles up, saying, “Here’s the guv’ner, Mrs.", "필은 주인을 한 번 쳐다보고는 절뚝거리며 일어서서 “두목 여기 계십니다, 배그닛 부인!"], ["Bagnet! Here he is!”", "여기 계세요!”"], ["and the old girl herself, accompanied by Mr. Bagnet, appears.", "하고 외치고, 늙은 아낙 본인이 배그닛 씨를 거느리고 나타난다."]], [["The old girl never appears in walking trim, in any season of the year, without a grey cloth cloak, coarse and much worn but very clean, which is, undoubtedly, the identical garment rendered so interesting to Mr. Bagnet by having made its way home to Europe from another quarter of the globe in company with Mrs.", "이 늙은 아낙은 한 해의 어느 철에든 외출 차림으로 나설 때 회색 천 망토를 두르지 않는 법이 없는데, 그 망토는 거칠고 몹시 닳았으되 아주 깨끗하니, 의심할 여지 없이 배그닛 부인과 우산 한 자루와 함께 지구 반대편에서 유럽 본국까지 흘러들어온 덕분에 배그닛 씨에게 그토록 흥미로운 물건이 된 바로 그 옷이다."], ["Bagnet and an umbrella. The latter faithful appendage is also invariably a part of the old girl’s presence out of doors.", "후자인 충실한 부속품 또한 늙은 아낙이 바깥나들이를 할 때면 어김없이 한 부분을 차지한다."], ["It is of no colour known in this life and has a corrugated wooden crook for a handle, with a metallic object let into its prow, or beak, resembling a little model of a fanlight over a street door or one of the oval glasses out of a pair of spectacles, which ornamental object has not that tenacious capacity of sticking to its post that might be desired in an article long associated with the British army.", "그것은 이승에서 알려진 그 어떤 색도 아니고, 손잡이는 골이 진 나무 갈고리이며, 뱃머리, 곧 부리에 해당하는 부분에는 금속 물건 하나가 박혀 있는데, 길가 문 위에 달린 부채꼴 채광창의 작은 모형이나 안경 한 짝에서 빼낸 타원형 알 하나를 닮았다. 다만 이 장식물은 영국군과 오래 함께한 물건에 마땅히 바랄 법한, 제자리에 끈질기게 붙어 있는 성질은 갖추지 못했다."], ["The old girl’s umbrella is of a flabby habit of waist and seems to be in need of stays—an appearance that is possibly referable to its having served through a series of years at home as a cupboard and on journeys as a carpet bag.", "늙은 아낙의 우산은 허리께가 흐물흐물한 품새라 코르셋이라도 받쳐줘야 할 형편인데, 이런 꼴이 된 까닭은 아마 여러 해 동안 집에서는 찬장으로, 여행 길에는 손가방으로 봉사해온 탓일 게다."], ["She never puts it up, having the greatest reliance on her well-proved cloak with its capacious hood, but generally uses the instrument as a wand with which to point out joints of meat or bunches of greens in marketing or to arrest the attention of tradesmen by a friendly poke.", "그녀는 우산을 결코 펴는 법이 없으니, 넉넉한 두건이 달린 자기 믿음직한 망토를 더없이 신뢰하기 때문이라, 대개는 그 도구를 지팡이처럼 써서 장을 볼 때 고깃덩이나 푸성귀 단을 가리키거나 상인들의 주의를 정답게 쿡 찔러 끄는 데 쓴다."], ["Without her market-basket, which is a sort of wicker well with two flapping lids, she never stirs abroad.", "펄럭이는 뚜껑이 둘 달린 일종의 버들 우물 같은 장바구니가 없으면 그녀는 결코 바깥나들이를 하지 않는다."], ["Attended by these her trusty companions, therefore, her honest sunburnt face looking cheerily out of a rough straw bonnet, Mrs. Bagnet now arrives, fresh-coloured and bright, in George’s Shooting Gallery.", "그리하여 이 믿음직한 길벗들을 거느리고, 거친 밀짚 보닛 밖으로 정직하고 햇볕에 그은 얼굴을 쾌활하게 내민 채, 배그닛 부인은 이제 혈색 좋고 환한 모습으로 조지의 사격장에 들어선다."]], [["“Well, George, old fellow,”", "“그래, 조지, 이 친구야,”"], ["says she, “and how do YOU do, this sunshiny morning?”", "그녀가 말한다. “이 햇살 좋은 아침에 자네 어떻게 지내?”"]], [["Giving him a friendly shake of the hand, Mrs. Bagnet draws a long breath after her walk and sits down to enjoy a rest.", "배그닛 부인은 그에게 정답게 악수를 건네고는, 걸어온 끝에 긴 숨을 내쉬며 한숨 돌리려고 자리에 앉는다."], ["Having a faculty, matured on the tops of baggage-waggons and in other such positions, of resting easily anywhere, she perches on a rough bench, unties her bonnet-strings, pushes back her bonnet, crosses her arms, and looks perfectly comfortable.", "짐수레 위며 그런 자리에서 단련된, 어디서든 손쉽게 쉬는 재주가 있는 터라, 그녀는 거친 의자에 걸터앉아 보닛 끈을 풀고 보닛을 뒤로 젖히고 팔짱을 끼고 더없이 편안한 표정을 짓는다."]], [["Mr. Bagnet in the meantime has shaken hands with his old comrade and with Phil, on whom Mrs. Bagnet likewise bestows a good-humoured nod and smile.", "그사이 배그닛 씨는 오랜 전우와, 또 필과 악수를 나누었는데, 필에게도 배그닛 부인이 마찬가지로 너그러운 고갯짓과 미소를 보낸다."]], [["“Now, George,” said Mrs.", "“자, 조지,”"], ["Bagnet briskly, “here we are, Lignum and myself”—she often speaks of her husband by this appellation, on account, as it is supposed, of Lignum Vitae having been his old regimental nickname when they first became acquainted, in compliment to the extreme hardness and toughness of his physiognomy—“just looked in, we have, to make it all correct as usual about that security.", "배그닛 부인이 활기차게 말한다. “우리가 왔다네, 리그넘하고 나하고”—그녀는 남편을 흔히 이 호칭으로 부르는데, 둘이 처음 알게 되었을 무렵 그의 인상이 워낙 단단하고 질긴 데 경의를 표해 ‘리그넘 비테’가 그의 옛 연대 별명이었던 까닭이라고들 한다—“늘 하던 대로 그 담보 건을 깔끔하게 처리하려고 잠깐 들렀어."], ["Give him the new bill to sign, George, and he’ll sign it like a man.”", "새 어음을 그이한테 줘서 서명하게 해, 조지. 그럼 사내답게 척 서명할 테니.”"]], [["“I was coming to you this morning,”", "“오늘 아침 내가 댁으로 찾아가려던 참이었어요.”"], ["observes the trooper reluctantly.", "부대원이 마지못해 말한다."]], [["“Yes, we thought you’d come to us this morning, but we turned out early and left Woolwich, the best of boys, to mind his sisters and came to you instead—as you see!", "“그래, 자네가 오늘 아침 우리한테 올 줄 알았지. 그래도 우리가 일찍 나서서, 그 착한 우리 울리치한테 누이들 보라고 맡기고 울위치를 떠나, 대신 자네한테 온 거야—보다시피!"], ["For Lignum, he’s tied so close now, and gets so little exercise, that a walk does him good.", "리그넘이 요즘 워낙 매여 지내고 운동을 못 하니, 걷는 게 몸에 좋거든."], ["But what’s the matter, George?”", "그런데 왜 그래, 조지?”"], ["asks Mrs. Bagnet, stopping in her cheerful talk.", "배그닛 부인이 쾌활한 말끝을 멈추며 묻는다."], ["“You don’t look yourself.”", "“자네 평소 같지가 않아.”"]], [["“I am not quite myself,”", "“좀 평소 같지가 않습니다,” 부대원이 대답한다."], ["returns the trooper; “I have been a little put out, Mrs. Bagnet.”", "“좀 마음이 어수선해서요, 배그닛 부인.”"]], [["Her bright quick eye catches the truth directly.", "그녀의 밝고 재빠른 눈이 당장 진실을 알아챈다."], ["“George!”", "“조지!”"], ["holding up her forefinger.", "그녀가 집게손가락을 치켜든다."], ["“Don’t tell me there’s anything wrong about that security of Lignum’s!", "“리그넘의 그 담보에 무슨 문제가 생겼다는 말은 하지 마!"], ["Don’t do it, George, on account of the children!”", "그러지 마, 조지, 애들을 봐서라도!”"]], [["The trooper looks at her with a troubled visage.", "부대원은 괴로운 낯빛으로 그녀를 쳐다본다."]], [["“George,”", "“조지,”"], ["says Mrs. Bagnet, using both her arms for emphasis and occasionally bringing down her open hands upon her knees.", "배그닛 부인이 강조하려고 두 팔을 다 쓰고 이따금 펼친 손을 무릎에 탁탁 내리치며 말한다."], ["“If you have allowed anything wrong to come to that security of Lignum’s, and if you have let him in for it, and if you have put us in danger of being sold up—and I see sold up in your face, George, as plain as print—you have done a shameful action and have deceived us cruelly.", "“자네가 리그넘의 그 담보에 무슨 문제가 닥치게 내버려뒀고, 그 사람을 그 일에 엮어 넣었고, 우리를 다 차압당할 위험에 빠뜨렸다면—자네 얼굴에 차압당한다고 활자처럼 또렷이 쓰여 있어, 조지—자네는 부끄러운 짓을 한 거고 우릴 모질게 속인 거야."], ["I tell you, cruelly, George.", "정말이지, 모질게, 조지."], ["There!”", "자!”"]], [["Mr. Bagnet, otherwise as immovable as a pump or a lamp-post, puts his large right hand on the top of his bald head as if to defend it from a shower-bath and looks with great uneasiness at Mrs. Bagnet.", "배그닛 씨는 평소 펌프나 가로등 기둥처럼 꿈쩍도 않는 사람이건만, 큼직한 오른손을 벗어진 머리 위에 얹어 마치 물벼락이라도 막으려는 양 하고는 몹시 불안한 눈으로 배그닛 부인을 쳐다본다."]], [["“George,”", "“조지,”"], ["says that old girl, “I wonder at you!", "그 늙은 아낙이 말한다. “난 자네가 어이없어!"], ["George, I am ashamed of you!", "조지, 난 자네가 부끄러워!"], ["George, I couldn’t have believed you would have done it!", "조지, 자네가 이런 짓을 했을 줄은 정말 믿을 수가 없어!"], ["I always knew you to be a rolling stone that gathered no moss, but I never thought you would have taken away what little moss there was for Bagnet and the children to lie upon.", "자네가 이끼 한 점 못 모으고 굴러다니는 돌멩이라는 건 늘 알았지만, 배그닛하고 애들이 깔고 누울 그 알량한 이끼마저 가져가 버릴 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어."], ["You know what a hard-working, steady-going chap he is.", "그이가 얼마나 부지런하고 착실한 사람인지 자네도 알잖아."], ["You know what Quebec and Malta and Woolwich are, and I never did think you would, or could, have had the heart to serve us so.", "퀘벡하고 몰타하고 울리치가 뭔지 자네도 알면서, 자네가 우리를 이렇게 만들 마음을 먹었거나 먹을 수 있었으리라곤 정말 생각도 못 했어."], ["Oh, George!” Mrs.", "아, 조지!”"], ["Bagnet gathers up her cloak to wipe her eyes on in a very genuine manner, “How could you do it?”", "배그닛 부인이 더없이 진심 어린 몸짓으로 망토 자락을 끌어모아 눈물을 훔친다. “어떻게 그럴 수가 있어?”"]], [["Mrs. Bagnet ceasing, Mr. Bagnet removes his hand from his head as if the shower-bath were over and looks disconsolately at Mr. George, who has turned quite white and looks distressfully at the grey cloak and straw bonnet.", "배그닛 부인이 말을 그치자, 배그닛 씨는 물벼락이 끝나기라도 한 양 머리에서 손을 떼고 조지 씨를 시무룩하게 바라보는데, 조지 씨는 새하얗게 질려 회색 망토와 밀짚 보닛을 괴로운 눈으로 쳐다본다."]], [["“Mat,”", "“맷,”"], ["says the trooper in a subdued voice, addressing him but still looking at his wife, “I am sorry you take it so much to heart, because I do hope it’s not so bad as that comes to.", "부대원이 가라앉은 목소리로, 그에게 말을 건네면서도 여전히 그의 아내를 바라보며 말한다. “자네가 이렇게까지 마음 아파해서 미안하네."], ["I certainly have, this morning, received this letter”—which he reads aloud—“but I hope it may be set right yet.", "사실은 그렇게까지 나쁘지는 않기를 바라거든. 분명히 오늘 아침 이 편지를 받긴 했지만”—그는 그것을 소리 내어 읽는다—“그래도 아직 바로잡을 수 있길 바라네."], ["As to a rolling stone, why, what you say is true.", "굴러다니는 돌멩이 얘기로 말하자면, 그래, 자네 말이 옳아."], ["I AM a rolling stone, and I never rolled in anybody’s way, I fully believe, that I rolled the least good to.", "난 굴러다니는 돌멩이가 맞아. 게다가 정말이지, 난 누구의 길에서도 굴러서 털끝만 한 득이라도 끼친 적이 없는 것 같아."], ["But it’s impossible for an old vagabond comrade to like your wife and family better than I like ’em, Mat, and I trust you’ll look upon me as forgivingly as you can.", "하지만 이 늙은 떠돌이 전우가 자네 아내며 식구를 내가 좋아하는 것보다 더 좋아할 사람은 없을 거야, 맷. 그러니 부디 최대한 너그럽게 봐주게."], ["Don’t think I’ve kept anything from you.", "내가 자네한테 뭘 숨겼다곤 생각 말고."], ["I haven’t had the letter more than a quarter of an hour.”", "이 편지를 받은 지 십오 분도 채 안 됐으니까.”"]], [["“Old girl,” murmurs Mr.", "“여보,”"], ["Bagnet after a short silence, “will you tell him my opinion?”", "배그닛 씨가 잠깐 침묵 끝에 중얼거린다. “저 친구한테 내 생각 좀 전해주겠어?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["Why didn’t he marry,” Mrs.", "저 사람은 왜,”"], ["Bagnet answers, half laughing and half crying, “Joe Pouch’s widder in North America?", "배그닛 부인이 반은 웃고 반은 울며 대답한다. “북아메리카에서 조 파우치네 과부랑 결혼하지 않았을까?"], ["Then he wouldn’t have got himself into these troubles.”", "그랬으면 이런 고생은 안 했을 텐데.”"]], [["“The old girl,”", "“여기 이 사람이,”"], ["says Mr.", "배그닛 씨가 말한다."], ["Bagnet, “puts it correct—why didn’t you?”", "“정확히 짚었네—자넨 왜 그렇게 안 했나?”"]], [["“Well, she has a better husband by this time, I hope,”", "“글쎄, 지금쯤 그 과부는 더 나은 남편을 만났겠지, 그러길 바라네,”"], ["returns the trooper.", "부대원이 대답한다."], ["“Anyhow, here I stand, this present day, NOT married to Joe Pouch’s widder.", "“어쨌든, 오늘 이 자리에 나는 조 파우치네 과부랑 결혼하지 않은 채로 서 있단 말이지."], ["What shall I do?", "어떡하면 좋겠나?"], ["You see all I have got about me.", "내가 가진 건 자네도 다 봐서 알 거야."], ["It’s not mine; it’s yours.", "내 것이 아니라 자네 거지."], ["Give the word, and I’ll sell off every morsel.", "한마디만 하게, 그럼 한 조각 남김없이 다 팔아 치울 테니."], ["If I could have hoped it would have brought in nearly the sum wanted, I’d have sold all long ago.", "필요한 금액 비슷하게라도 받을 수 있으리라 기대했으면 진작에 다 팔아 버렸을 거야."], ["Don’t believe that I’ll leave you or yours in the lurch, Mat.", "자네나 자네 식구를 곤경에 내버려 둘 거라곤 생각 말게, 맷."], ["I’d sell myself first.", "차라리 나 자신을 먼저 팔겠어."], ["I only wish,”", "다만,”"], ["says the trooper, giving himself a disparaging blow in the chest, “that I knew of any one who’d buy such a second-hand piece of old stores.”", "부대원이 제 가슴을 자조하듯 한 대 치며 말한다. “이런 중고 헌 물건 따위를 사 줄 사람을 누구든 알기만 한다면 좋겠네.”"]], [["“Old girl,” murmurs Mr.", "“여보,”"], ["Bagnet, “give him another bit of my mind.”", "배그닛 씨가 중얼거린다. “내 생각 한 토막 더 전해줘.”"]], [["“George,”", "“조지,” 늙은 아낙이 말한다."], ["says the old girl, “you are not so much to be blamed, on full consideration, except for ever taking this business without the means.”", "“곰곰이 따져보면 자넬 그렇게 탓할 일은 아니야. 다만 밑천도 없이 이 일을 떠맡은 건 빼고.”"]], [["“And that was like me!”", "“그게 딱 나다웠지!”"], ["observes the penitent trooper, shaking his head.", "뉘우치는 부대원이 고개를 저으며 말한다."], ["“Like me, I know.”", "“나다워, 나도 알아.”"]], [["“Silence!", "“조용히!"], ["The old girl,” says Mr.", "여기 이 사람은,”"], ["Bagnet, “is correct—in her way of giving my opinions—hear me out!”", "배그닛 씨가 말한다. “정확해—내 생각을 전하는 방식이 말이야—끝까지 들어!”"]], [["“That was when you never ought to have asked for the security, George, and when you never ought to have got it, all things considered.", "“바로 그때, 자네는 애당초 담보를 부탁하지 말았어야 했고, 이것저것 다 따져보면 받지도 말았어야 했어, 조지."], ["But what’s done can’t be undone.", "하지만 이미 엎질러진 물은 어쩔 수 없지."], ["You are always an honourable and straightforward fellow, as far as lays in your power, though a little flighty.", "자넨 좀 들떠 있긴 해도, 할 수 있는 한 늘 명예롭고 솔직한 사람이야."], ["On the other hand, you can’t admit but what it’s natural in us to be anxious with such a thing hanging over our heads.", "그렇지만 이런 일이 머리 위에 매달려 있으면 우리가 불안해하는 게 당연하다는 건 자네도 인정 못 할 리 없겠지."], ["So forget and forgive all round, George.", "그러니 다 같이 잊고 용서하자, 조지."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Forget and forgive all round!”", "다 같이 잊고 용서하자고!”"]], [["Mrs. Bagnet, giving him one of her honest hands and giving her husband the other, Mr. George gives each of them one of his and holds them while he speaks.", "배그닛 부인이 한 손은 그에게, 다른 한 손은 남편에게 정직한 손을 내미니, 조지 씨도 각각에게 한 손씩 내밀어 잡고는 말을 이어간다."]], [["“I do assure you both, there’s nothing I wouldn’t do to discharge this obligation.", "“두 분께 정말 장담하는데, 이 빚을 갚을 수만 있다면 못 할 일이 없어요."], ["But whatever I have been able to scrape together has gone every two months in keeping it up.", "하지만 내가 그러모을 수 있었던 건 두 달마다 이걸 유지하는 데 죄다 들어갔단 말입니다."], ["We have lived plainly enough here, Phil and I.", "여기서 필이랑 나는 충분히 검소하게 살았어요."], ["But the gallery don’t quite do what was expected of it, and it’s not—in short, it’s not the mint.", "그래도 이 사격장이 기대한 만큼 벌이가 되질 않아서요, 한마디로—한마디로 돈 찍어내는 조폐국은 아니거든요."], ["It was wrong in me to take it?", "내가 이걸 맡은 게 잘못이었냐고요?"], ["Well, so it was.", "그래요, 잘못이었죠."], ["But I was in a manner drawn into that step, and I thought it might steady me, and set me up, and you’ll try to overlook my having such expectations, and upon my soul, I am very much obliged to you, and very much ashamed of myself.”", "하지만 어떻게 보면 그 길로 끌려 들어간 셈이고, 이게 날 안정시키고 자리 잡게 해줄 거라 여겼던 거예요."], ["With these concluding words, Mr.", "내가 그런 기대를 품었던 건 부디 눈감아 주세요."], ["George gives a shake to each of the hands he holds, and relinquishing them, backs a pace or two in a broad-chested, upright attitude, as if he had made a final confession and were immediately going to be shot with all military honours.", "그리고 정말이지, 두 분께 정말 고맙고, 또 내 자신이 정말 부끄럽습니다.” 이 끝맺는 말과 함께 조지 씨는 잡고 있던 두 손을 각각 한 번씩 흔들고는 놓아주고, 떡 벌어진 가슴을 펴고 꼿꼿이 선 자세로 한두 걸음 물러서는데, 마치 마지막 고백을 마치고 당장 정식 군례를 받으며 총살당할 사람 같다."]], [["“George, hear me out!”", "“조지, 끝까지 들어!”"], ["says Mr. Bagnet, glancing at his wife.", "배그닛 씨가 아내를 흘끗 보며 말한다."], ["“Old girl, go on!”", "“여보, 계속해!”"]], [["Mr. Bagnet, being in this singular manner heard out, has merely to observe that the letter must be attended to without any delay, that it is advisable that George and he should immediately wait on Mr. Smallweed in person, and that the primary object is to save and hold harmless Mr. Bagnet, who had none of the money. Mr. George, entirely assenting, puts on his hat and prepares to march with Mr. Bagnet to the enemy’s camp.", "이 묘한 방식으로 끝까지 진술하게 된 배그닛 씨가 한 말이라곤, 이 편지는 한시도 지체 없이 처리해야 하고, 조지와 자기가 곧장 스몰위드 씨를 직접 찾아가는 게 좋겠으며, 무엇보다 우선할 일은 돈을 한 푼도 만지지 않은 배그닛 씨를 구제해 손해를 면하게 하는 것이라는 게 전부다. 조지 씨는 전적으로 동의하고는 모자를 쓰고 배그닛 씨와 함께 적진을 향해 행군할 채비를 한다."]], [["“Don’t you mind a woman’s hasty word, George,”", "“여자가 성급하게 한 말일랑 마음에 두지 마, 조지,”"], ["says Mrs. Bagnet, patting him on the shoulder.", "배그닛 부인이 그의 어깨를 토닥이며 말한다."], ["“I trust my old Lignum to you, and I am sure you’ll bring him through it.”", "“내 늙은 리그넘을 자네한테 맡길게. 자네가 이 일을 무사히 넘기게 해주리라 믿어.”"]], [["The trooper returns that this is kindly said and that he WILL bring Lignum through it somehow. Upon which Mrs.", "부대원은 참 다정한 말이라며, 어떻게든 리그넘이 이 일을 무사히 넘기게 하겠노라고 대답한다."], ["Bagnet, with her cloak, basket, and umbrella, goes home, bright-eyed again, to the rest of her family, and the comrades sally forth on the hopeful errand of mollifying Mr. Smallweed.", "그러자 배그닛 부인은 망토와 바구니와 우산을 챙겨, 다시 눈을 반짝이며 나머지 식구에게로 집으로 돌아가고, 두 전우는 스몰위드 씨를 누그러뜨리겠다는 희망찬 심부름 길에 나선다."]], [["Whether there are two people in England less likely to come satisfactorily out of any negotiation with Mr. Smallweed than Mr. George and Mr. Matthew Bagnet may be very reasonably questioned. Also, notwithstanding their martial appearance, broad square shoulders, and heavy tread, whether there are within the same limits two more simple and unaccustomed children in all the Smallweedy affairs of life. As they proceed with great gravity through the streets towards the region of Mount Pleasant, Mr. Bagnet, observing his companion to be thoughtful, considers it a friendly part to refer to Mrs. Bagnet’s late sally.", "스몰위드 씨와의 어떤 협상에서든 조지 씨와 매슈 배그닛 씨보다 만족스럽게 빠져나올 가능성이 더 낮은 두 사람이 잉글랜드에 또 있을지는 매우 합리적으로 의심해 볼 만하다. 또한 군인다운 풍채와 떡 벌어진 네모진 어깨와 묵직한 발걸음에도 불구하고, 스몰위드식 세상사 전반에 관한 한 이 두 사람보다 더 순진하고 서툰 어린애가 그 안에 또 있을지도. 두 사람이 더없이 엄숙한 얼굴로 거리를 지나 마운트 플레전트 쪽으로 나아가는데, 배그닛 씨는 동행이 생각에 잠긴 것을 보고 배그닛 부인이 아까 터뜨린 말에 대해 한마디 하는 것이 벗으로서의 도리라 여긴다."]], [["“George, you know the old girl—she’s as sweet and as mild as milk.", "“조지, 자네도 우리 마누라 알지—우유처럼 곱고 순한 사람이야."], ["But touch her on the children—or myself—and she’s off like gunpowder.”", "그런데 애들 얘기를—아니면 내 얘기를—건드리면, 화약처럼 터진다네.”"]], [["“It does her credit, Mat!”", "“그게 다 그분이 훌륭한 증거지, 맷!”"]], [["“George,” says Mr.", "“조지,”"], ["Bagnet, looking straight before him, “the old girl—can’t do anything—that don’t do her credit.", "배그닛 씨가 똑바로 앞을 보며 말한다. “우리 마누라가—하는 일이라면—훌륭한 증거 아닌 게 없어."], ["More or less.", "어느 정도든."], ["I never say so.", "난 절대 그렇게 말 안 하지만."], ["Discipline must be maintained.”", "군기는 지켜야 하거든.”"]], [["“She’s worth her weight in gold,”", "“그분은 황금만큼 값진 분이야.”"], ["says the trooper.", "부대원이 말한다."]], [["“In gold?”", "“황금이라?”"], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그닛 씨가 말한다."], ["“I’ll tell you what.", "“내 말 들어봐."], ["The old girl’s weight—is twelve stone six.", "우리 마누라 몸무게가—십이 스톤 육 파운드야."], ["Would I take that weight—in any metal—for the old girl? No.", "그 무게만큼을—어떤 금속으로 준다 해도—내가 우리 마누라랑 바꾸겠나?"], ["Why not?", "천만에."], ["Because the old girl’s metal is far more precious—than the preciousest metal.", "왜냐고? 우리 마누라라는 금속은—제일 값진 금속보다—훨씬 더 값지거든."], ["And she’s ALL metal!”", "게다가 그 사람은 통째로 금속이야!”"]], [["“You are right, Mat!”", "“자네 말이 맞아, 맷!”"]], [["“When she took me—and accepted of the ring—she ’listed under me and the children—heart and head; for life.", "“그 사람이 날 맞아들이고—반지를 받았을 때—나하고 애들 밑에—마음과 머리를 다해, 평생토록 입대한 거야."], ["She’s that earnest,” says Mr.", "그 사람은 어찌나 진지한지,”"], ["Bagnet, “and true to her colours—that, touch us with a finger—and she turns out—and stands to her arms.", "배그닛 씨가 말한다. “또 어찌나 제 깃발에 충실한지—누가 손가락 하나로 우릴 건드리면—그 사람이 떨쳐나와—무기를 들고 버틴다네."], ["If the old girl fires wide—once in a way—at the call of duty—look over it, George.", "우리 마누라가 어쩌다 한 번—임무에 충실하느라—허공에 대고 발포하더라도—못 본 척 넘겨주게, 조지."], ["For she’s loyal!”", "그 사람은 충성스러우니까!”"]], [["“Why, bless her, Mat,”", "“아니, 그분께 복이 있길, 맷,”"], ["returns the trooper, “I think the higher of her for it!”", "부대원이 대답한다. “난 그 일로 그분을 더 높이 보는걸!”"]], [["“You are right!” says Mr.", "“자네 말이 맞아!”"], ["Bagnet with the warmest enthusiasm, though without relaxing the rigidity of a single muscle.", "배그닛 씨가 더없이 뜨거운 열의로 말하지만, 근육 하나 풀어지는 일 없이 그대로다."], ["“Think as high of the old girl—as the rock of Gibraltar—and still you’ll be thinking low—of such merits.", "“우리 마누라를 지브롤터 바위만큼—높이 본다 해도—그 사람 덕에 비하면 여전히 낮게 보는 거야."], ["But I never own to it before her.", "하지만 그 사람 앞에선 절대 그렇다고 인정 안 해."], ["Discipline must be maintained.”", "군기는 지켜야 하거든.”"]], [["These encomiums bring them to Mount Pleasant and to Grandfather Smallweed’s house.", "이런 찬사를 늘어놓는 사이 두 사람은 마운트 플레전트에, 또 스몰위드 영감의 집에 다다른다."], ["The door is opened by the perennial Judy, who, having surveyed them from top to toe with no particular favour, but indeed with a malignant sneer, leaves them standing there while she consults the oracle as to their admission.", "문은 변함없는 주디가 열어주는데, 그녀는 두 사람을 머리끝부터 발끝까지 별 호감 없이, 아니 사실은 악의 가득한 비웃음으로 훑어보고는, 들여보낼지를 신탁에게 묻겠다며 그들을 그 자리에 세워둔 채 들어간다."], ["The oracle may be inferred to give consent from the circumstance of her returning with the words on her honey lips that they can come in if they want to it. Thus privileged, they come in and find Mr.", "그녀가 꿀 같은 입술에 들어오고 싶으면 들어와도 좋다는 말을 달고 돌아오는 정황으로 미루어 신탁이 허락을 내린 모양이다."], ["Smallweed with his feet in the drawer of his chair as if it were a paper foot-bath and Mrs. Smallweed obscured with the cushion like a bird that is not to sing.", "이리하여 특권을 얻은 두 사람은 안으로 들어가, 종이 족욕통이라도 되는 양 의자 서랍에 발을 담그고 있는 스몰위드 씨와, 노래하지 못하게 한 새처럼 방석에 가려진 스몰위드 부인을 본다."]], [["“My dear friend,”", "“이보게, 친애하는 벗,”"], ["says Grandfather Smallweed with those two lean affectionate arms of his stretched forth.", "스몰위드 영감이 그 비쩍 마른 다정한 두 팔을 앞으로 뻗으며 말한다."], ["“How de do?", "“잘 지냈나?"], ["How de do?", "잘 지냈어?"], ["Who is our friend, my dear friend?”", "이 친애하는 벗 양반, 이쪽 우리 벗은 누구신가?”"]], [["“Why this,”", "“그러니까 이쪽은,” 조지가 처음부터 살갑게 굴 수는 없는 터라 대답한다."], ["returns George, not able to be very conciliatory at first, “is Matthew Bagnet, who has obliged me in that matter of ours, you know.”", "“매슈 배그닛입니다. 아시다시피 우리 그 건에서 나를 도와준 사람이지요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["Mr. Bagnet?", "배그닛 씨?"], ["Surely!”", "그렇고말고!”"], ["The old man looks at him under his hand.", "영감은 손차양을 하고 그를 쳐다본다."]], [["“Hope you’re well, Mr.", "“안녕하신가요, 배그닛 씨?"], ["Bagnet? Fine man, Mr.", "멋진 분이로군요, 조지 씨!"], ["George! Military air, sir!”", "군인다운 풍채야, 선생!”"]], [["No chairs being offered, Mr. George brings one forward for Bagnet and one for himself.", "의자를 권하는 사람이 없으니 조지 씨는 배그닛에게 하나, 자기에게 하나 의자를 끌어다 놓는다."], ["They sit down, Mr. Bagnet as if he had no power of bending himself, except at the hips, for that purpose.", "두 사람은 앉는데, 배그닛 씨는 그러려고 엉덩이 외에는 몸을 구부릴 줄 모르는 사람 같다."]], [["“Judy,” says Mr.", "“주디,”"], ["Smallweed, “bring the pipe.”", "스몰위드 씨가 말한다. “담뱃대 가져와라.”"]], [["“Why, I don’t know,”", "“아니, 그럴 것 없습니다,”"], ["Mr.", "조지 씨가 끼어든다."], ["George interposes, “that the young woman need give herself that trouble, for to tell you the truth, I am not inclined to smoke it to-day.”", "“그 아가씨가 그런 수고까지 할 것 없어요. 사실 오늘은 담배를 피우고 싶은 생각이 없거든요.”"]], [["“Ain’t you?”", "“그래?”"], ["returns the old man.", "영감이 대꾸한다."], ["“Judy, bring the pipe.”", "“주디, 담뱃대 가져와.”"]], [["“The fact is, Mr. Smallweed,”", "“실은요, 스몰위드 씨,”"], ["proceeds George, “that I find myself in rather an unpleasant state of mind.", "조지가 말을 잇는다. “제가 좀 불쾌한 심정입니다."], ["It appears to me, sir, that your friend in the city has been playing tricks.”", "제가 보기엔 말이죠, 선생, 시내에 있는 당신 친구가 농간을 부린 것 같아서요.”"]], [["“Oh, dear no!”", "“오, 천만에!”"], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 영감이 말한다."], ["“He never does that!”", "“그 친구는 절대 그런 짓 안 해!”"]], [["“Don’t he?", "“안 한다고요?"], ["Well, I am glad to hear it, because I thought it might be HIS doing.", "그렇다니 다행이군요. 그 친구 짓일지도 모른다고 생각했거든요."], ["This, you know, I am speaking of.", "이거 말입니다."], ["This letter.”", "이 편지요.”"]], [["Grandfather Smallweed smiles in a very ugly way in recognition of the letter.", "스몰위드 영감은 편지를 알아보고는 아주 흉하게 웃는다."]], [["“What does it mean?”", "“이게 무슨 뜻입니까?”"], ["asks Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 묻는다."]], [["“Judy,”", "“주디,”"], ["says the old man.", "영감이 말한다."], ["“Have you got the pipe?", "“담뱃대 챙겼느냐?"], ["Give it to me.", "이리 다오."], ["Did you say what does it mean, my good friend?”", "이게 무슨 뜻이냐고 했나, 친애하는 벗?”"]], [["“Aye!", "“그래요!"], ["Now, come, come, you know, Mr. Smallweed,”", "자, 이거 보세요, 스몰위드 씨,”"], ["urges the trooper, constraining himself to speak as smoothly and confidentially as he can, holding the open letter in one hand and resting the broad knuckles of the other on his thigh, “a good lot of money has passed between us, and we are face to face at the present moment, and are both well aware of the understanding there has always been.", "부대원이 최대한 부드럽고 다정하게 말하려 애쓰며, 한 손에 펼친 편지를 쥐고 다른 손의 떡 벌어진 주먹을 허벅지에 얹은 채 다그친다. “우리 사이에 적잖은 돈이 오갔고, 지금 이렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 있고, 늘 양해가 있었다는 건 둘 다 잘 알잖아요."], ["I am prepared to do the usual thing which I have done regularly and to keep this matter going.", "나야 늘 꼬박꼬박 해온 그 통상적인 처리를 할 준비가 돼 있고, 이 건을 계속 이어갈 생각입니다."], ["I never got a letter like this from you before, and I have been a little put about by it this morning, because here’s my friend Matthew Bagnet, who, you know, had none of the money—”", "당신한테 이런 편지를 받은 건 처음이라, 오늘 아침 좀 어수선했어요. 여기 내 친구 매슈 배그닛은, 아시다시피, 그 돈을 한 푼도—”"]], [["“I DON’T know it, you know,”", "“난 그건 모르는데, 알다시피.”"], ["says the old man quietly.", "영감이 조용히 말한다."]], [["“Why, con-found you—it, I mean—I tell you so, don’t I?”", "“아니, 제기—이거 말이죠—내가 그렇다고 말하잖아요, 안 그래요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes, you tell me so,”", "“오, 그래, 자네가 그렇다고 말하지,”"], ["returns Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 영감이 대꾸한다."], ["“But I don’t know it.”", "“그래도 난 모른다네.”"]], [["“Well!”", "“좋아요!”"], ["says the trooper, swallowing his fire.", "부대원이 치미는 불을 삼키며 말한다."], ["“I know it.”", "“난 압니다.”"]], [["Mr. Smallweed replies with excellent temper, “Ah!", "스몰위드 씨가 더없이 좋은 기분으로 대답한다."], ["That’s quite another thing!”", "“아! 그건 또 전혀 다른 얘기지!”"], ["And adds, “But it don’t matter.", "그리고 덧붙인다. “하지만 그건 상관없어."], ["Mr. Bagnet’s situation is all one, whether or no.”", "그렇든 아니든 배그닛 씨 처지는 매한가지니까.”"]], [["The unfortunate George makes a great effort to arrange the affair comfortably and to propitiate Mr. Smallweed by taking him upon his own terms.", "불운한 조지는 이 일을 원만히 매듭짓고, 스몰위드 씨를 그쪽 조건에 맞춰주어 비위를 맞춰보려 무던히 애쓴다."]], [["“That’s just what I mean.", "“바로 그게 제 말입니다."], ["As you say, Mr. Smallweed, here’s Matthew Bagnet liable to be fixed whether or no.", "당신 말대로요, 스몰위드 씨, 여기 매슈 배그닛은 그렇든 아니든 옭매일 처지지요."], ["Now, you see, that makes his good lady very uneasy in her mind, and me too, for whereas I’m a harum-scarum sort of a good-for-nought that more kicks than halfpence come natural to, why he’s a steady family man, don’t you see?", "그런데 그게 그이의 착한 아내를 마음 무척 불안하게 하고, 나도 그렇거든요. 나야 발길질이나 받아 마땅한 칠칠치 못한 건달이지만, 이 사람은 착실한 가장이잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["Now, Mr. Smallweed,”", "자, 스몰위드 씨,”"], ["says the trooper, gaining confidence as he proceeds in his soldierly mode of doing business, “although you and I are good friends enough in a certain sort of a way, I am well aware that I can’t ask you to let my friend Bagnet off entirely.”", "부대원이 자기식 군인다운 일 처리 방식으로 말을 이으며 점점 자신감을 얻는다. “당신하고 나는 어떤 식으로든 꽤 좋은 사이지만, 내 친구 배그닛을 완전히 빼달라고 부탁할 수는 없다는 건 나도 잘 압니다.”"]], [["“Oh, dear, you are too modest.", "“아이고, 너무 겸손하시네."], ["You can ASK me anything, Mr. George.”", "자넨 뭐든 부탁해도 돼, 조지 씨.”"], ["(There is an ogreish kind of jocularity in Grandfather Smallweed to-day.)", "(오늘 스몰위드 영감에게는 식인귀 같은 익살이 어려 있다.)"]], [["“And you can refuse, you mean, eh?", "“그리고 당신은 거절할 수 있다, 그 말이죠, 응?"], ["Or not you so much, perhaps, as your friend in the city?", "아니면 당신보다는, 어쩌면 시내에 있는 당신 친구가?"], ["Ha ha ha!”", "하하하!”"]], [["“Ha ha ha!”", "“하하하!”"], ["echoes Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 영감이 따라 웃는다."], ["In such a very hard manner and with eyes so particularly green that Mr. Bagnet’s natural gravity is much deepened by the contemplation of that venerable man.", "그게 어찌나 모진지, 또 눈이 어찌나 유난히 시퍼런지, 배그닛 씨는 그 존경스러운 노인을 바라보는 통에 타고난 엄숙함이 한층 깊어진다."]], [["“Come!”", "“자!”"], ["says the sanguine George.", "낙천적인 조지가 말한다."], ["“I am glad to find we can be pleasant, because I want to arrange this pleasantly.", "“우리가 유쾌하게 굴 수 있어서 다행입니다. 이걸 유쾌하게 매듭짓고 싶었거든요."], ["Here’s my friend Bagnet, and here am I.", "여기 내 친구 배그닛이 있고, 여기 내가 있어요."], ["We’ll settle the matter on the spot, if you please, Mr. Smallweed, in the usual way.", "괜찮으시면 통상적인 방식으로 그 자리에서 이 건을 해결합시다, 스몰위드 씨."], ["And you’ll ease my friend Bagnet’s mind, and his family’s mind, a good deal if you’ll just mention to him what our understanding is.”", "그리고 우리 양해가 뭔지 이 사람한테 한마디만 해주시면, 내 친구 배그닛의 마음도, 그 식구들 마음도 한결 놓이겠네요.”"]], [["Here some shrill spectre cries out in a mocking manner, “Oh, good gracious!", "이때 어떤 새된 유령이 조롱하는 투로 외친다. “아이고, 맙소사!"], ["Oh!”", "아!”"], ["Unless, indeed, it be the sportive Judy, who is found to be silent when the startled visitors look round, but whose chin has received a recent toss, expressive of derision and contempt.", "정작 그게 장난기 어린 주디가 아니라면 말이지만, 놀란 손님들이 둘러보니 그녀는 잠자코 있으나, 그 턱이 방금 한 번 치켜올려져 비웃음과 경멸을 드러내고 있다."], ["Mr. Bagnet’s gravity becomes yet more profound.", "배그닛 씨의 엄숙함은 한층 더 깊어진다."]], [["“But I think you asked me, Mr. George”—old Smallweed, who all this time has had the pipe in his hand, is the speaker now—“I think you asked me, what did the letter mean?”", "“그런데 자네가 나한테 묻지 않았나, 조지 씨”—이때껏 줄곧 담뱃대를 손에 쥐고 있던 스몰위드 영감이 이번엔 화자다—“이 편지가 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었던 것 같은데?”"]], [["“Why, yes, I did,”", "“그야, 그랬죠,”"], ["returns the trooper in his off-hand way, “but I don’t care to know particularly, if it’s all correct and pleasant.”", "부대원이 무심한 투로 대답한다. “하지만 다 제대로 되고 유쾌하기만 하면, 굳이 자세히 알고 싶진 않아요.”"]], [["Mr. Smallweed, purposely balking himself in an aim at the trooper’s head, throws the pipe on the ground and breaks it to pieces.", "스몰위드 씨는 부대원의 머리를 겨누다가 일부러 빗맞히는 시늉으로 담뱃대를 바닥에 내던져 산산조각 낸다."]], [["“That’s what it means, my dear friend.", "“이게 그 뜻이야, 친애하는 벗."], ["I’ll smash you.", "내가 자넬 박살 낼 거야."], ["I’ll crumble you.", "가루로 만들어 줄 테야."], ["I’ll powder you.", "잘게 빻아 줄 테야."], ["Go to the devil!”", "지옥에나 가!”"]], [["The two friends rise and look at one another.", "두 벗은 일어나 서로를 쳐다본다."], ["Mr. Bagnet’s gravity has now attained its profoundest point.", "배그닛 씨의 엄숙함은 이제 더없이 깊은 경지에 이르렀다."]], [["“Go to the devil!”", "“지옥에나 가!”"], ["repeats the old man.", "영감이 거듭 말한다."], ["“I’ll have no more of your pipe-smokings and swaggerings.", "“자네 담뱃대질이며 으스대는 꼴은 이제 더 못 봐주겠어."], ["What?", "뭐?"], ["You’re an independent dragoon, too!", "자넨 독립적인 용기병이기도 하지!"], ["Go to my lawyer (you remember where; you have been there before) and show your independence now, will you?", "내 변호사한테 가(어딘지 기억하지, 전에도 가봤잖아). 거기서 그 독립을 한번 보여봐, 어때?"], ["Come, my dear friend, there’s a chance for you.", "자, 친애하는 벗, 자네한테도 기회가 있다네."], ["Open the street door, Judy; put these blusterers out!", "길가 문을 열어라, 주디. 이 허풍쟁이들을 내쫓아!"], ["Call in help if they don’t go.", "안 나가면 사람을 불러."], ["Put ’em out!”", "내쫓아 버려!”"]], [["He vociferates this so loudly that Mr. Bagnet, laying his hands on the shoulders of his comrade before the latter can recover from his amazement, gets him on the outside of the street door, which is instantly slammed by the triumphant Judy.", "영감이 어찌나 큰 소리로 악을 쓰는지, 배그닛 씨는 전우가 어안이 벙벙한 데서 채 헤어나기도 전에 그 어깨에 손을 얹어 길가 문 밖으로 끌어내고, 의기양양한 주디가 곧장 그 문을 쾅 닫는다."], ["Utterly confounded, Mr. George awhile stands looking at the knocker.", "완전히 얼이 빠진 조지 씨는 한동안 문고리를 멍하니 바라보고 서 있다."], ["Mr. Bagnet, in a perfect abyss of gravity, walks up and down before the little parlour window like a sentry and looks in every time he passes, apparently revolving something in his mind.", "더없는 엄숙의 심연에 빠진 배그닛 씨는 보초처럼 작은 거실 창문 앞을 오락가락하며 지날 때마다 안을 들여다보는데, 무언가를 머릿속에서 곱씹는 기색이 역력하다."]], [["“Come, Mat,” says Mr.", "“가세, 맷,”"], ["George when he has recovered himself, “we must try the lawyer.", "조지 씨가 정신을 차리고 말한다. “변호사한테 가봐야겠어."], ["Now, what do you think of this rascal?”", "그래, 저 악당을 어떻게 생각하나?”"]], [["Mr. Bagnet, stopping to take a farewell look into the parlour, replies with one shake of his head directed at the interior, “If my old girl had been here—I’d have told him!”", "배그닛 씨는 거실을 마지막으로 한 번 들여다보려고 멈춰 서서, 안쪽을 향해 고개를 한 번 젓고 대답한다."], ["Having so discharged himself of the subject of his cogitations, he falls into step and marches off with the trooper, shoulder to shoulder.", "“우리 마누라가 여기 있었더라면—내가 저자한테 한마디 했을 텐데!” 이렇게 골똘히 곱씹던 주제를 털어놓고 나서, 그는 보조를 맞춰 부대원과 어깨를 나란히 하고 행군해 나간다."]], [["When they present themselves in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, Mr. Tulkinghorn is engaged and not to be seen.", "두 사람이 링컨스 인 필즈에 모습을 드러내자, 토킹혼 씨는 일이 있어 만날 수 없다고 한다."], ["He is not at all willing to see them, for when they have waited a full hour, and the clerk, on his bell being rung, takes the opportunity of mentioning as much, he brings forth no more encouraging message than that Mr. Tulkinghorn has nothing to say to them and they had better not wait.", "그는 두 사람을 만날 마음이 전혀 없으니, 둘이 꼬박 한 시간을 기다린 끝에 서기가 종이 울리자 그 김에 그들 얘기를 꺼냈을 때도, 토킹혼 씨가 그들에게 할 말이 없으니 더 기다리지 않는 편이 좋겠다는 별로 달갑잖은 전갈밖에 가져오지 않는다."], ["They do wait, however, with the perseverance of military tactics, and at last the bell rings again and the client in possession comes out of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s room.", "그래도 두 사람은 군대식 전술의 끈기로 기다리고, 마침내 다시 종이 울리며 안에 있던 의뢰인이 토킹혼 씨의 방에서 나온다."]], [["The client is a handsome old lady, no other than Mrs. Rouncewell, housekeeper at Chesney Wold.", "그 의뢰인은 풍채 고운 노부인으로, 다름 아닌 체스니 월드의 가정부 라운스웰 부인이다."], ["She comes out of the sanctuary with a fair old-fashioned curtsy and softly shuts the door.", "그녀는 그 성소에서 우아한 옛날식 무릎 인사를 하며 나와 가만히 문을 닫는다."], ["She is treated with some distinction there, for the clerk steps out of his pew to show her through the outer office and to let her out.", "그녀는 이곳에서 어느 정도 대접을 받으니, 서기가 자기 자리에서 나와 바깥 사무실을 지나 그녀를 배웅하고 문밖으로 내보내 준다."], ["The old lady is thanking him for his attention when she observes the comrades in waiting.", "노부인이 그의 친절에 감사를 표하다가 기다리고 있는 두 전우를 알아본다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, sir, but I think those gentlemen are military?”", "“실례합니다만, 선생님, 저 신사분들은 군인이신 듯한데요?”"]], [["The clerk referring the question to them with his eye, and Mr.", "서기가 눈짓으로 그 물음을 두 사람에게 넘기는데, 조지 씨는 벽난로 위 달력에서 고개를 돌리지 않는다."], ["George not turning round from the almanac over the fire-place.", "그래서 배그닛 씨가 나서서 대답한다."], ["Mr. Bagnet takes upon himself to reply, “Yes, ma’am.", "“네, 부인."], ["Formerly.”", "예전에요.”"]], [["“I thought so.", "“그럴 줄 알았어요."], ["I was sure of it.", "틀림없을 거라 여겼지요."], ["My heart warms, gentlemen, at the sight of you.", "두 분을 뵈니 마음이 따뜻해지네요, 신사분들. 그런 분들을 뵐 때면 늘 그렇답니다."], ["It always does at the sight of such.", "신의 가호가 있기를, 신사분들!"], ["God bless you, gentlemen!", "늙은이를 너그러이 봐주세요."], ["You’ll excuse an old woman, but I had a son once who went for a soldier.", "저도 한때 군인이 된 아들이 하나 있었거든요. 잘생기고 훌륭한 청년이었어요."], ["A fine handsome youth he was, and good in his bold way, though some people did disparage him to his poor mother.", "제 방식대로 대담하니 착했지요. 더러 그 가엾은 어미 앞에서 아이를 헐뜯는 사람도 있었지만요."], ["I ask your pardon for troubling you, sir.", "폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다, 선생님."], ["God bless you, gentlemen!”", "신의 가호가 있기를, 신사분들!”"]], [["“Same to you, ma’am!”", "“부인께도요!”"], ["returns Mr. Bagnet with right good will.", "배그닛 씨가 진심 어린 호의로 대답한다."]], [["There is something very touching in the earnestness of the old lady’s voice and in the tremble that goes through her quaint old figure.", "노부인의 목소리에 어린 간절함과, 그 예스러운 자그마한 몸을 타고 흐르는 떨림에는 사람을 몹시 뭉클하게 하는 무언가가 있다."], ["But Mr. George is so occupied with the almanac over the fire-place (calculating the coming months by it perhaps) that he does not look round until she has gone away and the door is closed upon her.", "그러나 조지 씨는 벽난로 위 달력에 어찌나 골몰해 있는지(아마 그것으로 앞으로 다가올 달들을 헤아리고 있는지도) 그녀가 가버리고 등 뒤로 문이 닫힐 때까지 고개를 돌리지 않는다."]], [["“George,”", "“조지,”"], ["Mr. Bagnet gruffly whispers when he does turn from the almanac at last.", "배그닛 씨가 마침내 달력에서 돌아서며 무뚝뚝하게 속삭인다."], ["“Don’t be cast down!", "“기죽지 마!"], ["‘Why, soldiers, why—should we be melancholy, boys?’ Cheer up, my hearty!”", "‘아, 병사들이여, 어찌하여—우리가 우울해야 하나, 친구들?’ 기운 내, 이 사람아!”"]], [["The clerk having now again gone in to say that they are still there and Mr. Tulkinghorn being heard to return with some irascibility, “Let ’em come in then!”", "이제 서기가 다시 들어가 두 사람이 아직 있다고 전하고, 토킹혼 씨가 다소 짜증스럽게 “그럼 들여보내!”"], ["they pass into the great room with the painted ceiling and find him standing before the fire.", "하고 대꾸하는 소리가 들리자, 두 사람은 천장에 그림이 그려진 그 커다란 방으로 들어가, 벽난로 앞에 서 있는 그를 본다."]], [["“Now, you men, what do you want?", "“자, 자네들, 무슨 일인가?"], ["Sergeant, I told you the last time I saw you that I don’t desire your company here.”", "병장, 지난번 만났을 때 말했지. 여기서 자네 일행과 어울릴 생각이 없다고.”"]], [["Sergeant replies—dashed within the last few minutes as to his usual manner of speech, and even as to his usual carriage—that he has received this letter, has been to Mr. Smallweed about it, and has been referred there.", "병장은—지난 몇 분 사이에 평소의 말투며 평소의 몸가짐마저 풀이 죽은 채로—이 편지를 받고 스몰위드 씨에게 갔더니 이리로 가보라 했다고 대답한다."]], [["“I have nothing to say to you,”", "“난 자네한테 할 말이 없네,”"], ["rejoins Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 응수한다."], ["“If you get into debt, you must pay your debts or take the consequences.", "“빚을 졌으면 빚을 갚든지 그 결과를 감수하든지 해야지."], ["You have no occasion to come here to learn that, I suppose?”", "그걸 배우려고 여기까지 올 일은 없을 텐데, 안 그런가?”"]], [["Sergeant is sorry to say that he is not prepared with the money.", "병장은 유감스럽게도 돈을 마련하지 못했다고 말한다."]], [["“Very well!", "“좋아!"], ["Then the other man—this man, if this is he—must pay it for you.”", "그럼 다른 사람이—이 사람이 그 사람이라면—대신 갚아야지.”"]], [["Sergeant is sorry to add that the other man is not prepared with the money either.", "병장은 유감스럽게도 다른 사람 역시 돈을 마련하지 못했다고 덧붙인다."]], [["“Very well!", "“좋아!"], ["Then you must pay it between you or you must both be sued for it and both suffer.", "그럼 둘이 갚든지, 아니면 둘 다 고소당해 둘 다 곤욕을 치르든지 해야지."], ["You have had the money and must refund it.", "돈을 받았으면 도로 내놔야지."], ["You are not to pocket other people’s pounds, shillings, and pence and escape scot-free.”", "남의 파운드, 실링, 펜스를 챙겨놓고 무사히 빠져나갈 순 없네.”"]], [["The lawyer sits down in his easy-chair and stirs the fire.", "변호사는 안락의자에 앉아 불을 휘젓는다."], ["Mr. George hopes he will have the goodness to—“I tell you, sergeant, I have nothing to say to you.", "조지 씨는 부디 그가 선처를 베풀어—“말했잖나, 병장, 자네한테 할 말이 없네."], ["I don’t like your associates and don’t want you here.", "난 자네 일행이 마음에 안 들고, 자네가 여기 오는 것도 싫어."], ["This matter is not at all in my course of practice and is not in my office. Mr.", "이 일은 내 업무 영역에 전혀 들지 않고, 내 사무소 소관도 아니야."], ["Smallweed is good enough to offer these affairs to me, but they are not in my way.", "스몰위드 씨가 친절히도 이런 일을 나한테 가져오긴 하지만, 내 일이 아닐세."], ["You must go to Melchisedech’s in Clifford’s Inn.”", "클리퍼즈 인의 멜키세덱한테 가보게.”"]], [["“I must make an apology to you, sir,” says Mr.", "“선생님께 사과드려야겠습니다,”"], ["George, “for pressing myself upon you with so little encouragement—which is almost as unpleasant to me as it can be to you—but would you let me say a private word to you?”", "조지 씨가 말한다. “이렇게 달가워하지도 않으시는데 들이닥쳐서요—저로서도 선생님 못지않게 거북한 일이지만—단둘이 한마디 드려도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn rises with his hands in his pockets and walks into one of the window recesses.", "토킹혼 씨는 주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 일어나 창가 우묵한 곳 한 군데로 걸어간다."], ["“Now!", "“자!"], ["I have no time to waste.”", "낭비할 시간 없네.”"], ["In the midst of his perfect assumption of indifference, he directs a sharp look at the trooper, taking care to stand with his own back to the light and to have the other with his face towards it.", "완벽하게 꾸며낸 무관심 속에서도 그는 부대원에게 날카로운 눈길을 던지는데, 자기는 빛을 등지고 서고 상대는 빛을 마주 보게 하려고 빈틈없이 신경 쓴다."]], [["“Well, sir,” says Mr.", "“그러니까, 선생님,”"], ["George, “this man with me is the other party implicated in this unfortunate affair—nominally, only nominally—and my sole object is to prevent his getting into trouble on my account.", "조지 씨가 말한다. “저와 함께 온 이 사람이 이 딱한 일에 엮인 또 한 당사자입니다—명목상으로요, 그저 명목상으로만요—제 유일한 목적은 저 때문에 이 사람이 곤경에 빠지지 않게 하는 겁니다."], ["He is a most respectable man with a wife and family, formerly in the Royal Artillery—”", "이 사람은 아내와 식구가 있는 더없이 점잖은 사람이고, 예전에 왕립포병대에 있었—”"]], [["“My friend, I don’t care a pinch of snuff for the whole Royal Artillery establishment—officers, men, tumbrils, waggons, horses, guns, and ammunition.”", "“이보게, 난 왕립포병대 전체에 코담배 한 자밤만큼의 관심도 없어—장교든, 병사든, 탄약 수레든, 짐마차든, 말이든, 대포든, 탄약이든.”"]], [["“’Tis likely, sir.", "“그러시겠지요, 선생님."], ["But I care a good deal for Bagnet and his wife and family being injured on my account.", "하지만 저는 저 때문에 배그닛이며 그 아내와 식구가 해를 입는 데는 관심이 아주 큽니다."], ["And if I could bring them through this matter, I should have no help for it but to give up without any other consideration what you wanted of me the other day.”", "그래서 이 일에서 그들을 무사히 빼낼 수만 있다면, 다른 도리 없이, 며칠 전 선생님이 저한테 원하셨던 그것을 아무 조건 없이 내놓겠습니다.”"]], [["“Have you got it here?”", "“그걸 여기 가져왔나?”"]], [["“I have got it here, sir.”", "“여기 가져왔습니다, 선생님.”"]], [["“Sergeant,”", "“병장,”"], ["the lawyer proceeds in his dry passionless manner, far more hopeless in the dealing with than any amount of vehemence, “make up your mind while I speak to you, for this is final.", "변호사가 그 메마르고 무덤덤한 투로 말을 잇는데, 어떤 격렬함보다도 상대하기에 훨씬 더 가망 없는 어조다. “내가 말하는 동안 마음을 정하게."], ["After I have finished speaking I have closed the subject, and I won’t re-open it.", "이건 최종이니까. 내가 말을 마치고 나면 이 건은 닫힌 거고, 다시 열지 않을 거야."], ["Understand that.", "그것만 명심해."], ["You can leave here, for a few days, what you say you have brought here if you choose; you can take it away at once if you choose.", "가져왔다는 그것을 원하면 며칠 여기 맡겨둬도 좋고, 원하면 당장 도로 가져가도 좋아."], ["In case you choose to leave it here, I can do this for you—I can replace this matter on its old footing, and I can go so far besides as to give you a written undertaking that this man Bagnet shall never be troubled in any way until you have been proceeded against to the utmost, that your means shall be exhausted before the creditor looks to his.", "여기 맡겨두기로 한다면, 내가 자네를 위해 이렇게 해줄 수 있네—이 건을 예전 상태로 되돌려놓고, 거기에 더해, 자네가 최대한 추궁당하기 전까지는, 즉 채권자가 이 사람한테 손대기 전에 자네 재산이 먼저 바닥나기 전까지는 이 배그닛이라는 사람이 어떤 식으로도 시달리지 않으리라는 각서를 써줄 수 있어."], ["This is in fact all but freeing him.", "이건 사실상 이 사람을 풀어주는 거나 다름없네."], ["Have you decided?”", "결정했나?”"]], [["The trooper puts his hand into his breast and answers with a long breath, “I must do it, sir.”", "부대원이 가슴팍에 손을 넣고 긴 숨과 함께 대답한다. “그렇게 해야겠지요, 선생님.”"]], [["So Mr. Tulkinghorn, putting on his spectacles, sits down and writes the undertaking, which he slowly reads and explains to Bagnet, who has all this time been staring at the ceiling and who puts his hand on his bald head again, under this new verbal shower-bath, and seems exceedingly in need of the old girl through whom to express his sentiments.", "그리하여 토킹혼 씨는 안경을 쓰고 앉아 각서를 작성하고는, 천천히 읽으며 배그닛에게 설명해 주는데, 배그닛은 이때껏 줄곧 천장만 노려보고 있다가 이 새로운 말의 물벼락 아래 또다시 벗어진 머리에 손을 얹고는, 자기 심정을 대신 표현해 줄 늙은 아낙이 몹시도 절실해 보인다."], ["The trooper then takes from his breast-pocket a folded paper, which he lays with an unwilling hand at the lawyer’s elbow.", "이윽고 부대원이 가슴 주머니에서 접은 종이 한 장을 꺼내 내키지 않는 손길로 변호사 팔꿈치 곁에 내려놓는다."], ["“’Tis only a letter of instructions, sir.", "“그저 지시가 적힌 편지일 뿐입니다, 선생님."], ["The last I ever had from him.”", "그 사람한테 받은 마지막 편지죠.”"]], [["Look at a millstone, Mr.", "조지 씨여, 맷돌이 표정을 바꾸나 들여다보시오."], ["George, for some change in its expression, and you will find it quite as soon as in the face of Mr. Tulkinghorn when he opens and reads the letter!", "토킹혼 씨가 그 편지를 펴서 읽을 때 그 얼굴에서 변화를 찾느니 차라리 맷돌에서 찾는 편이 빠를 테니!"], ["He refolds it and lays it in his desk with a countenance as unperturbable as death.", "그는 편지를 도로 접어 책상에 넣어두는데, 그 낯빛이 죽음처럼 흔들림이 없다."]], [["Nor has he anything more to say or do but to nod once in the same frigid and discourteous manner and to say briefly, “You can go.", "그가 더 할 말도, 할 일도 없으니, 다만 그 똑같이 싸늘하고 무례한 투로 한 번 고개를 까딱하고는 짧게 말할 뿐이다. “가도 좋네."], ["Show these men out, there!”", "거기, 이 사람들 내보내!”"], ["Being shown out, they repair to Mr. Bagnet’s residence to dine.", "밖으로 안내되어 나온 두 사람은 저녁을 먹으러 배그닛 씨 댁으로 향한다."]], [["Boiled beef and greens constitute the day’s variety on the former repast of boiled pork and greens, and Mrs.", "전번의 삶은 돼지고기와 푸성귀에서 이날은 삶은 쇠고기와 푸성귀로 변화를 주었고, 배그닛 부인은 똑같은 방식으로 음식을 떠서 나눠주며 더없이 좋은 기분으로 양념을 친다."], ["Bagnet serves out the meal in the same way and seasons it with the best of temper, being that rare sort of old girl that she receives Good to her arms without a hint that it might be Better and catches light from any little spot of darkness near her.", "그녀는 더 나을 수도 있다는 내색 없이 ‘좋음’을 두 팔로 끌어안고, 곁에 어떤 자그마한 어둠의 얼룩이 있어도 거기서 빛을 길어 올리는 보기 드문 부류의 늙은 아낙인 것이다."], ["The spot on this occasion is the darkened brow of Mr. George; he is unusually thoughtful and depressed.", "이번의 그 얼룩은 조지 씨의 어두워진 이마인데, 그는 유난히 생각에 잠기고 풀이 죽어 있다."], ["At first Mrs. Bagnet trusts to the combined endearments of Quebec and Malta to restore him, but finding those young ladies sensible that their existing Bluffy is not the Bluffy of their usual frolicsome acquaintance, she winks off the light infantry and leaves him to deploy at leisure on the open ground of the domestic hearth.", "처음에 배그닛 부인은 퀘벡과 몰타의 합동 애교에 기대 그를 풀어보려 하지만, 그 어린 아가씨들이 지금 자기들 ‘블러피’가 평소 함께 까불며 놀던 그 블러피가 아님을 눈치챈 것을 보고는, 그 경보병들에게 눈짓으로 물러나라 이르고, 가정의 화로라는 트인 들판에서 그가 마음껏 펼쳐지도록 내버려 둔다."]], [["But he does not.", "그러나 그는 그러지 않는다."], ["He remains in close order, clouded and depressed.", "그는 밀집 대형 그대로, 흐리고 침울하게 머물러 있다."], ["During the lengthy cleaning up and pattening process, when he and Mr. Bagnet are supplied with their pipes, he is no better than he was at dinner.", "길게 이어지는 설거지와 나막신 신는 절차가 진행되는 동안, 그와 배그닛 씨에게 담뱃대가 주어졌을 때도, 그는 저녁 식사 때나 다를 바 없다."], ["He forgets to smoke, looks at the fire and ponders, lets his pipe out, fills the breast of Mr. Bagnet with perturbation and dismay by showing that he has no enjoyment of tobacco.", "그는 담배 피우는 것을 잊고, 불을 바라보며 골똘히 생각에 잠기고, 담뱃대를 꺼뜨리고, 담배를 도무지 즐기지 못하는 모습을 보여 배그닛 씨의 가슴을 불안과 당혹으로 가득 채운다."]], [["Therefore when Mrs. Bagnet at last appears, rosy from the invigorating pail, and sits down to her work, Mr.", "그리하여 배그닛 부인이 마침내 기운을 돋우는 물통에서 발갛게 달아올라 나타나 자기 일감을 들고 자리에 앉자, 배그닛 씨가 으르렁거린다."], ["Bagnet growls, “Old girl!”", "“여보!”"], ["and winks monitions to her to find out what’s the matter.", "그러고는 무슨 일인지 알아보라고 눈짓으로 신호를 보낸다."]], [["“Why, George!”", "“아니, 조지!”"], ["says Mrs. Bagnet, quietly threading her needle.", "배그닛 부인이 바늘에 조용히 실을 꿰며 말한다."], ["“How low you are!”", "“자네 어쩜 그리 기가 죽었어!”"]], [["“Am I?", "“그런가요?"], ["Not good company?", "같이 있기 별로지요?"], ["Well, I am afraid I am not.”", "그래요, 그런 것 같네요.”"]], [["“He ain’t at all like Bluffy, mother!”", "“이분 블러피 같지가 않아, 엄마!”"], ["cries little Malta.", "어린 몰타가 외친다."]], [["“Because he ain’t well, I think, mother,”", "“몸이 안 좋으셔서 그런 거야, 엄마.”"], ["adds Quebec.", "퀘벡이 덧붙인다."]], [["“Sure that’s a bad sign not to be like Bluffy, too!”", "“블러피 같지 않다는 건 분명 안 좋은 징조지!”"], ["returns the trooper, kissing the young damsels.", "부대원이 어린 아가씨들에게 입을 맞추며 대꾸한다."], ["“But it’s true,”", "“하지만 그게 맞아,”"], ["with a sigh, “true, I am afraid.", "한숨과 함께. “맞아, 안타깝게도."], ["These little ones are always right!”", "이 꼬마들은 늘 옳거든!”"]], [["“George,” says Mrs.", "“조지,”"], ["Bagnet, working busily, “if I thought you cross enough to think of anything that a shrill old soldier’s wife—who could have bitten her tongue off afterwards and ought to have done it almost—said this morning, I don’t know what I shouldn’t say to you now.”", "배그닛 부인이 바삐 일하며 말한다. “자네가, 오늘 아침 새된 늙은 군인의 아내가—나중에 제 혀를 깨물어 버리고 싶었고 진작에 그랬어야 마땅한—했던 그런 말을 마음에 담아둘 만큼 꼬여 있다고 내가 생각한다면, 지금 자네한테 무슨 말을 못 할지 모르겠어.”"]], [["“My kind soul of a darling,”", "“내 다정한 친구 같은 분,”"], ["returns the trooper.", "부대원이 대답한다."], ["“Not a morsel of it.”", "“그런 건 털끝만큼도 없어요.”"]], [["“Because really and truly, George, what I said and meant to say was that I trusted Lignum to you and was sure you’d bring him through it.", "“정말이지 진심으로 말하는데, 조지, 내가 한 말이며 하려던 말은 리그넘을 자네한테 맡기고 자네가 그이를 무사히 이 일에서 빼내주리라 믿는다는 거였어."], ["And you HAVE brought him through it, noble!”", "그리고 자네는 그이를 무사히 빼냈잖아, 훌륭하게!”"]], [["“Thankee, my dear!”", "“고마워요, 부인!”"], ["says George.", "조지가 말한다."], ["“I am glad of your good opinion.”", "“좋게 봐주셔서 기쁩니다.”"]], [["In giving Mrs. Bagnet’s hand, with her work in it, a friendly shake—for she took her seat beside him—the trooper’s attention is attracted to her face.", "배그닛 부인의 손을, 일감을 쥔 그 손을 정답게 한 번 흔드는데—그녀가 그의 곁에 앉았으므로—그 순간 부대원의 시선이 그녀의 얼굴로 쏠린다."], ["After looking at it for a little while as she plies her needle, he looks to young Woolwich, sitting on his stool in the corner, and beckons that fifer to him.", "그녀가 바늘을 놀리는 동안 잠시 그 얼굴을 바라보던 그는, 구석 걸상에 앉아 있는 어린 울리치 쪽으로 눈길을 돌려 그 피리 부는 아이를 손짓해 부른다."]], [["“See there, my boy,”", "“저기 좀 봐라, 얘야,”"], ["says George, very gently smoothing the mother’s hair with his hand, “there’s a good loving forehead for you!", "조지가 손으로 어머니의 머리카락을 아주 부드럽게 쓰다듬으며 말한다. “저기 착하고 사랑이 넘치는 이마가 있구나!"], ["All bright with love of you, my boy.", "온통 너에 대한 사랑으로 환하지, 얘야."], ["A little touched by the sun and the weather through following your father about and taking care of you, but as fresh and wholesome as a ripe apple on a tree.”", "네 아버지를 따라다니고 너를 돌보느라 햇볕과 비바람에 조금 그을리긴 했지만, 나무에 달린 잘 익은 사과처럼 싱싱하고 건강하단다.”"]], [["Mr. Bagnet’s face expresses, so far as in its wooden material lies, the highest approbation and acquiescence.", "배그닛 씨의 얼굴은 그 나무토막 같은 재질이 허락하는 한도 내에서 더없는 찬동과 동의를 드러낸다."]], [["“The time will come, my boy,”", "“언젠가 그런 날이 올 거야, 얘야,” 부대원이 말을 잇는다."], ["pursues the trooper, “when this hair of your mother’s will be grey, and this forehead all crossed and re-crossed with wrinkles, and a fine old lady she’ll be then.", "“네 어머니의 이 머리카락이 백발이 되고, 이 이마가 온통 주름으로 가로세로 얽히고, 그러면 멋진 노부인이 되시겠지. 젊을 때 마음을 단단히 먹어두렴."], ["Take care, while you are young, that you can think in those days, ‘I never whitened a hair of her dear head—I never marked a sorrowful line in her face!’ For of all the many things that you can think of when you are a man, you had better have THAT by you, Woolwich!”", "그날이 오면 ‘난 어머니의 그 소중한 머리카락 한 올도 세게 한 적 없고, 어머니 얼굴에 서글픈 주름 한 줄 새긴 적 없다’고 생각할 수 있도록. 어른이 되어 떠올릴 수 있는 그 숱한 것들 가운데, 그걸 곁에 지니는 편이 가장 나으니까, 울리치!”"]], [["Mr. George concludes by rising from his chair, seating the boy beside his mother in it, and saying, with something of a hurry about him, that he’ll smoke his pipe in the street a bit.", "조지 씨는 의자에서 일어나 그 아이를 어머니 곁에 앉히고, 어딘가 황급한 기색으로 거리에 나가 담배나 한 대 피우고 오겠다고 말하며 마무리한다."]], [["Esther’s Narrative\nI lay ill through several weeks, and the usual tenor of my life became like an old remembrance.", "저는 몇 주 동안이나 앓아누웠고, 그동안 제 삶의 평범한 흐름은 아득한 옛 기억처럼 멀어졌습니다."], ["But this was not the effect of time so much as of the change in all my habits made by the helplessness and inaction of a sick-room.", "그러나 이것은 세월이 흘러서라기보다, 병실의 무력함과 무위(無爲)가 제 모든 습관을 송두리째 바꿔놓은 탓이었습니다."], ["Before I had been confined to it many days, everything else seemed to have retired into a remote distance where there was little or no separation between the various stages of my life which had been really divided by years.", "병실에 갇힌 지 며칠 지나기도 전에, 다른 모든 것이 아득히 먼 곳으로 물러나버린 듯했고, 실제로는 여러 해로 나뉘어 있던 제 인생의 여러 단계들이 그 먼 곳에서는 거의, 아니 전혀 구분되지 않았습니다."], ["In falling ill, I seemed to have crossed a dark lake and to have left all my experiences, mingled together by the great distance, on the healthy shore.", "병에 걸리면서 저는 어두운 호수를 건너온 듯했고, 제 모든 경험은 그 까마득한 거리 때문에 한데 뒤섞인 채 건강한 쪽 기슭에 남겨두고 온 듯했습니다."]], [["My housekeeping duties, though at first it caused me great anxiety to think that they were unperformed, were soon as far off as the oldest of the old duties at Greenleaf or the summer afternoons when I went home from school with my portfolio under my arm, and my childish shadow at my side, to my godmother’s house.", "처음에는 제 살림 일이 그대로 방치되어 있다는 생각에 몹시 마음을 졸였지만, 그 일조차 곧 그린리프 시절의 가장 오래된 옛 임무들만큼이나, 아니면 화첩을 옆구리에 끼고 어린 그림자를 곁에 거느린 채 학교에서 대모님 댁으로 돌아가던 여름날 오후만큼이나 아득히 멀어졌습니다."], ["I had never known before how short life really was and into how small a space the mind could put it.", "저는 그전까지 삶이 정말로 얼마나 짧은지, 그리고 그 삶을 마음이 얼마나 작은 공간 안에 담아둘 수 있는지 미처 몰랐습니다."]], [["While I was very ill, the way in which these divisions of time became confused with one another distressed my mind exceedingly.", "몹시 앓는 동안, 이렇게 나뉜 시간의 토막들이 서로 뒤엉키는 그 방식이 제 마음을 한없이 괴롭혔습니다."], ["At once a child, an elder girl, and the little woman I had been so happy as, I was not only oppressed by cares and difficulties adapted to each station, but by the great perplexity of endlessly trying to reconcile them.", "어린아이이면서 동시에 좀 더 자란 소녀였고, 또 그토록 행복했던 작은 여인이기도 했던 저는, 각각의 처지에 어울리는 근심과 어려움에 짓눌렸을 뿐 아니라, 그것들을 끝없이 화해시키려 애쓰는 거대한 혼란에까지 시달렸습니다."], ["I suppose that few who have not been in such a condition can quite understand what I mean or what painful unrest arose from this source.", "그런 상태를 겪어보지 않은 분이라면 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지, 혹은 그것이 어떤 고통스러운 불안을 불러일으켰는지 온전히 이해하기는 어려우리라 생각합니다."]], [["For the same reason I am almost afraid to hint at that time in my disorder—it seemed one long night, but I believe there were both nights and days in it—when I laboured up colossal staircases, ever striving to reach the top, and ever turned, as I have seen a worm in a garden path, by some obstruction, and labouring again. I knew perfectly at intervals, and I think vaguely at most times, that I was in my bed; and I talked with Charley, and felt her touch, and knew her very well; yet I would find myself complaining, “Oh, more of these never-ending stairs, Charley—more and more—piled up to the sky’, I think!” and labouring on again.", "같은 이유로 저는 제 병중의 그 시간을 입에 올리기조차 두렵습니다. 그것은 하나의 긴 밤처럼 느껴졌지만, 실은 그 안에 밤도 낮도 다 들어 있었으리라 믿습니다. 그때 저는 거대한 계단을 힘겹게 기어올랐고, 늘 꼭대기에 닿으려 안간힘을 썼으나, 정원 길에서 본 벌레가 무언가에 가로막히듯 번번이 되돌려져 다시 기어오르기를 거듭했습니다. 이따금 저는 제가 침대에 누워 있다는 사실을 또렷이 알았고, 대체로는 어렴풋이나마 알았다고 생각합니다. 찰리와 이야기를 나누고, 그 아이의 손길을 느꼈으며, 그 아이를 아주 잘 알아보았습니다. 그런데도 어느새 저는 이렇게 하소연하고 있었습니다. \"아, 또 이 끝없는 계단이야, 찰리. 자꾸자꾸 — 하늘까지 쌓아 올린 것 같아!\" 그러고는 다시 힘겹게 기어올랐습니다."]], [["Dare I hint at that worse time when, strung together somewhere in great black space, there was a flaming necklace, or ring, or starry circle of some kind, of which I was one of the beads!", "더 끔찍했던 그 시간을 감히 입에 올려도 될까요. 거대하고 새까만 공간 어딘가에 활활 타오르는 목걸이인지, 고리인지, 별처럼 둥근 어떤 원이 꿰여 있었고, 저는 그 구슬 가운데 하나였습니다!"], ["And when my only prayer was to be taken off from the rest and when it was such inexplicable agony and misery to be a part of the dreadful thing?", "그리고 제 유일한 기도는 그 나머지로부터 떼어내어 달라는 것이었고, 그 무시무시한 것의 일부가 되어 있다는 사실이 이루 말할 수 없는 고통이요 비참이었던 그 시간 말입니다."]], [["Perhaps the less I say of these sick experiences, the less tedious and the more intelligible I shall be.", "이런 병중의 경험은 적게 말할수록 덜 지루하고 더 잘 전해지리라 생각합니다."], ["I do not recall them to make others unhappy or because I am now the least unhappy in remembering them.", "제가 이것을 다시 끄집어내는 까닭은 남을 불행하게 만들려는 것도, 지금 그것을 떠올리는 일이 조금이라도 불행해서도 아닙니다."], ["It may be that if we knew more of such strange afflictions we might be the better able to alleviate their intensity.", "어쩌면 우리가 이런 기이한 고통을 더 잘 안다면, 그 격렬함을 누그러뜨릴 길도 더 잘 찾을 수 있을지 모르기 때문입니다."]], [["The repose that succeeded, the long delicious sleep, the blissful rest, when in my weakness I was too calm to have any care for myself and could have heard (or so I think now) that I was dying, with no other emotion than with a pitying love for those I left behind—this state can be perhaps more widely understood.", "그 뒤에 찾아온 안식, 길고도 달콤한 잠, 더없이 행복한 휴식 — 쇠약해진 제가 너무도 고요하여 제 자신은 조금도 염려하지 않았고, 제가 죽어간다는 말을 들었다 해도(지금 돌이켜보면 그러했으리라 싶습니다) 뒤에 남겨두고 갈 이들을 향한 애틋한 사랑 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 없었으리라 싶은 그 상태 — 이것은 어쩌면 더 널리 이해받을 수 있을 것입니다."], ["I was in this state when I first shrunk from the light as it twinkled on me once more, and knew with a boundless joy for which no words are rapturous enough that I should see again.", "빛이 다시 한번 제 위에서 반짝일 때 제가 처음으로 그 빛을 피해 움츠러들었던 것도 바로 이 상태에서였고, 어떤 말로도 다 표현할 수 없는 끝없는 기쁨 속에서 제가 다시 볼 수 있게 되리라는 것을 알았던 것도 바로 그때였습니다."]], [["I had heard my Ada crying at the door, day and night; I had heard her calling to me that I was cruel and did not love her; I had heard her praying and imploring to be let in to nurse and comfort me and to leave my bedside no more; but I had only said, when I could speak, “Never, my sweet girl, never!” and I had over and over again reminded Charley that she was to keep my darling from the room whether I lived or died. Charley had been true to me in that time of need, and with her little hand and her great heart had kept the door fast.", "저는 에이더가 밤낮으로 문 앞에서 우는 소리를 들었습니다. 제가 모질다고, 저를 사랑하지 않는다고 외치는 소리도 들었습니다. 안으로 들어와 저를 간호하고 위로하게 해달라고, 제 머리맡을 다시는 떠나지 않게 해달라고 기도하고 애원하는 소리도 들었습니다. 그러나 저는 말을 할 수 있을 때면 그저 이렇게만 말했습니다. \"안 돼, 내 사랑하는 아가, 절대 안 돼!\" 그리고 제가 살든 죽든 그 아이를 이 방에 들이지 말라고 찰리에게 거듭거듭 당부했습니다. 찰리는 그 절박한 시간 내내 제게 진실했고, 그 작은 손과 큰 마음으로 문을 굳게 지켰습니다."]], [["But now, my sight strengthening and the glorious light coming every day more fully and brightly on me, I could read the letters that my dear wrote to me every morning and evening and could put them to my lips and lay my cheek upon them with no fear of hurting her.", "그러나 이제 시력이 차츰 돌아오고 그 영광스러운 빛이 날마다 더 충만하고 환하게 제게 비쳐들면서, 저는 사랑하는 그 아이가 아침저녁으로 써 보내는 편지를 읽을 수 있었고, 그것을 입술에 갖다 대고 뺨을 묻으면서도 그 아이에게 해를 끼칠까 두려워하지 않게 되었습니다."], ["I could see my little maid, so tender and so careful, going about the two rooms setting everything in order and speaking cheerfully to Ada from the open window again.", "저의 작은 하녀가 어찌나 다정하고 어찌나 세심하게 두 방을 오가며 모든 것을 정돈하고, 다시 열린 창문 너머로 에이더에게 명랑하게 말을 거는지를 볼 수 있었습니다."], ["I could understand the stillness in the house and the thoughtfulness it expressed on the part of all those who had always been so good to me.", "집 안의 그 고요함과, 늘 제게 그토록 잘해주던 모든 이들이 그 고요함으로 드러내는 그 깊은 배려를 이해할 수 있었습니다."], ["I could weep in the exquisite felicity of my heart and be as happy in my weakness as ever I had been in my strength.", "더없이 절절한 마음의 행복 속에서 눈물 흘릴 수 있었고, 건강할 때 그러했던 것 못지않게 쇠약한 가운데서도 행복할 수 있었습니다."]], [["By and by my strength began to be restored.", "머지않아 제 기력이 회복되기 시작했습니다."], ["Instead of lying, with so strange a calmness, watching what was done for me, as if it were done for some one else whom I was quietly sorry for, I helped it a little, and so on to a little more and much more, until I became useful to myself, and interested, and attached to life again.", "저를 위해 행해지는 일들을 마치 제가 조용히 가엾이 여기는 다른 누군가를 위한 일인 양 그렇게 이상한 평온함 속에서 누워 바라보기만 하던 대신, 저는 조금씩 거들기 시작했고, 그러다 좀 더, 또 훨씬 더 거들게 되어, 마침내 제 자신에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 되었고, 삶에 다시 관심을 갖고 애착을 느끼게 되었습니다."]], [["How well I remember the pleasant afternoon when I was raised in bed with pillows for the first time to enjoy a great tea-drinking with Charley!", "처음으로 베개를 받치고 일어나 앉아 찰리와 함께 거나하게 차를 마시던 그 즐거운 오후를 저는 얼마나 또렷이 기억하는지요! 그 작은 아이는 — 분명 약하고 병든 이들을 보살피라고 이 세상에 보내진 것이겠지요 — 어찌나 행복하고 어찌나 분주한지, 차 준비를 하다 말고 자꾸자꾸 멈춰서 제 가슴에 머리를 기대고 저를 어루만지며 기쁨의 눈물을 흘렸습니다."], ["The little creature—sent into the world, surely, to minister to the weak and sick—was so happy, and so busy, and stopped so often in her preparations to lay her head upon my bosom, and fondle me, and cry with joyful tears she was so glad, she was so glad, that I was obliged to say, “Charley, if you go on in this way, I must lie down again, my darling, for I am weaker than I thought I was!”", "너무 기뻐서, 너무 기뻐서요. 그래서 저는 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["So Charley became as quiet as a mouse and took her bright face here and there across and across the two rooms, out of the shade into the divine sunshine, and out of the sunshine into the shade, while I watched her peacefully.", "\"찰리, 네가 이렇게 하면 나는 다시 누워야 해, 아가. 내가 생각했던 것보다 더 약하거든!\""], ["When all her preparations were concluded and the pretty tea-table with its little delicacies to tempt me, and its white cloth, and its flowers, and everything so lovingly and beautifully arranged for me by Ada downstairs, was ready at the bedside, I felt sure I was steady enough to say something to Charley that was not new to my thoughts.", "그러자 찰리는 생쥐처럼 조용해져서, 그 환한 얼굴을 두 방을 가로질러 이리저리, 그늘에서 눈부신 햇살 속으로, 다시 햇살에서 그늘 속으로 옮겨 다녔고, 저는 평온히 그 모습을 지켜보았습니다. 마침내 모든 준비가 끝나, 저를 꾀어낼 자그마한 별미들과 하얀 보, 꽃들, 그리고 아래층의 에이더가 저를 위해 그토록 사랑스럽고 아름답게 차려놓은 그 모든 것이 놓인 예쁜 찻상이 침대 곁에 준비되자, 저는 마음속에 오래 품어온, 새삼스럽지 않은 이야기를 찰리에게 꺼낼 만큼 충분히 안정되어 있다는 확신이 들었습니다."]], [["First I complimented Charley on the room, and indeed it was so fresh and airy, so spotless and neat, that I could scarce believe I had been lying there so long.", "먼저 저는 방에 대해 찰리를 칭찬했는데, 정말이지 방은 어찌나 산뜻하고 바람이 잘 통하며, 어찌나 티 하나 없이 깔끔한지, 제가 그토록 오래 거기 누워 있었다는 것이 믿기지 않을 정도였습니다."], ["This delighted Charley, and her face was brighter than before.", "이 말에 찰리는 무척 기뻐했고, 그 얼굴은 전보다 더 환해졌습니다."]], [["“Yet, Charley,”", "\"그런데 찰리,\""], ["said I, looking round, “I miss something, surely, that I am accustomed to?”", "저는 방을 둘러보며 말했습니다. \"내가 익숙하던 뭔가가 분명 없는 것 같은데?\""]], [["Poor little Charley looked round too and pretended to shake her head as if there were nothing absent.", "가엾은 작은 찰리도 방을 둘러보더니, 없어진 것이라곤 아무것도 없다는 듯 고개를 가로젓는 시늉을 했습니다."]], [["“Are the pictures all as they used to be?”", "\"그림은 다 예전 그대로니?\""], ["I asked her.", "제가 그 아이에게 물었습니다."]], [["“Every one of them, miss,”", "\"하나도 빠짐없이 그대로예요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했습니다."]], [["“And the furniture, Charley?”", "\"가구는, 찰리?\""]], [["“Except where I have moved it about to make more room, miss.”", "\"공간을 더 내려고 제가 옮겨놓은 것만 빼고요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“And yet,”", "\"그런데도,\""], ["said I, “I miss some familiar object.", "제가 말했습니다. \"낯익은 물건 하나가 없는 것 같아."], ["Ah, I know what it is, Charley!", "아, 뭔지 알겠다, 찰리!"], ["It’s the looking-glass.”", "거울이야.\""]], [["Charley got up from the table, making as if she had forgotten something, and went into the next room; and I heard her sob there.", "찰리는 무언가를 깜빡 잊은 척하며 식탁에서 일어나 옆방으로 들어갔고, 저는 그 아이가 거기서 흐느끼는 소리를 들었습니다."]], [["I had thought of this very often.", "저는 이것을 아주 여러 번 생각해왔습니다."], ["I was now certain of it.", "이제 확신이 섰습니다."], ["I could thank God that it was not a shock to me now.", "그것이 지금 제게 충격이 아니라는 사실을 하느님께 감사할 수 있었습니다."], ["I called Charley back, and when she came—at first pretending to smile, but as she drew nearer to me, looking grieved—I took her in my arms and said, “It matters very little, Charley.", "저는 찰리를 다시 불렀고, 그 아이가 왔을 때 — 처음에는 웃는 척했지만 제게 다가올수록 슬픈 표정이 되더군요 — 그 아이를 두 팔로 안고 말했습니다. \"별일 아니야, 찰리."], ["I hope I can do without my old face very well.”", "옛날 내 얼굴 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있을 것 같아.\""]], [["I was presently so far advanced as to be able to sit up in a great chair and even giddily to walk into the adjoining room, leaning on Charley.", "저는 곧 큰 의자에 앉아 있을 수 있을 만큼, 심지어 찰리에게 기대어 어질어질하나마 옆방으로 걸어 들어갈 수 있을 만큼 회복되었습니다."], ["The mirror was gone from its usual place in that room too, but what I had to bear was none the harder to bear for that.", "그 방에서도 거울이 늘 있던 자리에서 사라지고 없었지만, 그렇다고 제가 견뎌야 할 것이 더 견디기 어려워지지는 않았습니다."]], [["My guardian had throughout been earnest to visit me, and there was now no good reason why I should deny myself that happiness.", "후견인은 줄곧 저를 찾아오고 싶어 안달이었고, 이제 제가 그 기쁨을 마다할 까닭은 조금도 없었습니다. 그분은 어느 날 아침에 오셨는데, 처음 들어섰을 때는 그저 저를 품에 안고 \"내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아이야!\"라고만 하실 수 있을 뿐이었습니다."], ["He came one morning, and when he first came in, could only hold me in his embrace and say, “My dear, dear girl!”", "그분의 마음이 얼마나 깊은 애정과 너그러움의 샘인지 저는 오래전부터 알고 있었습니다. 누가 저보다 더 잘 알겠습니까?"], ["I had long known—who could know better?—what a deep fountain of affection and generosity his heart was; and was it not worth my trivial suffering and change to fill such a place in it?", "그런 마음속에 그런 자리를 채우는 일이라면, 제 하찮은 고통과 변화쯤이야 값어치가 있지 않겠습니까? \"아, 그래!\""], ["“Oh, yes!”", "저는 생각했습니다."], ["I thought.", "\"그분은 나를 보셨고, 전보다 더 나를 사랑하셔."], ["“He has seen me, and he loves me better than he did; he has seen me and is even fonder of me than he was before; and what have I to mourn for!”", "나를 보시고도 예전보다 더 나를 아끼셔. 그러니 내가 슬퍼할 게 무엇이 있담!\""]], [["He sat down by me on the sofa, supporting me with his arm.", "그분은 소파에서 제 곁에 앉아 팔로 저를 받쳐주셨습니다."], ["For a little while he sat with his hand over his face, but when he removed it, fell into his usual manner.", "잠시 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 앉아 계셨지만, 손을 떼었을 때는 평소의 모습으로 돌아오셨습니다."], ["There never can have been, there never can be, a pleasanter manner.", "그보다 더 유쾌한 태도는 일찍이 없었고, 앞으로도 결코 없을 것입니다."]], [["“My little woman,”", "\"내 작은 여인,\""], ["said he, “what a sad time this has been.", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다. \"참으로 슬픈 시간이었구나."], ["Such an inflexible little woman, too, through all!”", "그 와중에도 어찌나 고집 센 작은 여인이던지!\""]], [["“Only for the best, guardian,”", "\"다 잘되자고 그런 거예요, 후견인 어른.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“For the best?”", "\"잘되자고?\""], ["he repeated tenderly.", "그분이 다정하게 되뇌셨습니다. \"그럼, 다 잘되자고 그랬지."], ["“Of course, for the best.", "하지만 그동안 에이더와 나는 완전히 외롭고 비참했단다. 네 친구 캐디는 아침저녁으로 드나들었고, 이 집 사람들은 하나같이 넋이 나가 풀이 죽어 있었지."], ["But here have Ada and I been perfectly forlorn and miserable; here has your friend Caddy been coming and going late and early; here has every one about the house been utterly lost and dejected; here has even poor Rick been writing—to ME too—in his anxiety for you!”", "심지어 가엾은 릭마저도 너를 걱정해서 편지를 — 그것도 내게! — 보냈단다!\""]], [["I had read of Caddy in Ada’s letters, but not of Richard.", "저는 에이더의 편지에서 캐디 이야기는 읽었으나 리처드 이야기는 읽지 못했습니다."], ["I told him so.", "저는 그 사실을 그분께 말씀드렸습니다."]], [["“Why, no, my dear,”", "\"음, 그래, 얘야.\""], ["he replied.", "그분이 대답하셨습니다."], ["“I have thought it better not to mention it to her.”", "\"그 애한테는 말하지 않는 게 낫겠다 싶었단다.\""]], [["“And you speak of his writing to YOU,”", "\"게다가 그 애가 어른께 편지했다고 말씀하시는데,\""], ["said I, repeating his emphasis.", "저는 그분이 강조한 말투를 따라 하며 말했습니다."], ["“As if it were not natural for him to do so, guardian; as if he could write to a better friend!”", "\"마치 그 애가 그러는 게 당연하지 않다는 듯이요, 후견인 어른. 마치 그 애가 더 좋은 친구에게 편지할 수 있기라도 한 것처럼요!\""]], [["“He thinks he could, my love,”", "\"그 애는 그럴 수 있다고 생각한단다, 얘야.\""], ["returned my guardian, “and to many a better.", "후견인이 대답하셨습니다. \"그것도 훨씬 더 좋은 여러 사람한테 말이지."], ["The truth is, he wrote to me under a sort of protest while unable to write to you with any hope of an answer—wrote coldly, haughtily, distantly, resentfully.", "실은 그 애가 내게 편지한 건 일종의 마지못함에서였어. 너한테는 답장을 기대할 수 없어 쓰지 못하던 차에 말이다."], ["Well, dearest little woman, we must look forbearingly on it.", "차갑게, 거만하게, 서먹하게, 원망 섞어 썼더구나. 그래, 가장 사랑하는 작은 여인아, 우리는 그것을 너그러이 보아야 한다."], ["He is not to blame.", "그 애 잘못이 아니야."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce has warped him out of himself and perverted me in his eyes.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 그 애를 제 본모습에서 비틀어놓았고, 그 애 눈에 나를 비뚤어진 사람으로 만들어놓은 게지."], ["I have known it do as bad deeds, and worse, many a time.", "그 소송이 그만큼, 아니 그보다 더 못된 짓을 저지르는 걸 나는 여러 번 보아왔단다."], ["If two angels could be concerned in it, I believe it would change their nature.”", "천사 둘이 거기 얽혀든다 해도, 그 본성이 바뀌고 말 거라고 나는 믿는다.\""]], [["“It has not changed yours, guardian.”", "\"하지만 어른의 본성은 바꾸지 못했잖아요, 후견인 어른.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, it has, my dear,” he said laughingly.", "\"오, 그렇고말고, 바꿔놓았지, 얘야.\""], ["“It has made the south wind easterly, I don’t know how often.", "그분이 웃으며 말씀하셨습니다. \"남풍을 동풍으로 둔갑시킨 게 몇 번인지 모른단다."], ["Rick mistrusts and suspects me—goes to lawyers, and is taught to mistrust and suspect me.", "릭은 나를 불신하고 의심해. 변호사들을 찾아가서는 나를 불신하고 의심하라고 배워 오지."], ["Hears I have conflicting interests, claims clashing against his and what not.", "내가 이해관계가 충돌한다느니, 제 권리와 부딪치는 청구권이 있다느니 하는 따위의 말을 듣는 게야."], ["Whereas, heaven knows that if I could get out of the mountains of Wiglomeration on which my unfortunate name has been so long bestowed (which I can’t) or could level them by the extinction of my own original right (which I can’t either, and no human power ever can, anyhow, I believe, to such a pass have we got), I would do it this hour.", "하지만 하늘이 알다시피, 내가 만일 내 불운한 이름이 그토록 오래 얹혀온 그 법률 잡동사니의 산더미에서 빠져나올 수만 있다면(빠져나올 수 없지만), 혹은 내 본래의 권리를 소멸시켜 그 산을 평평하게 만들 수만 있다면(이것 역시 할 수 없고, 어떤 인간의 힘으로도 결코 못 하지. 우리가 이미 그 지경에까지 와버렸다고 나는 믿는다), 나는 당장 이 순간에라도 그렇게 하겠다."], ["I would rather restore to poor Rick his proper nature than be endowed with all the money that dead suitors, broken, heart and soul, upon the wheel of Chancery, have left unclaimed with the Accountant-General—and that’s money enough, my dear, to be cast into a pyramid, in memory of Chancery’s transcendent wickedness.”", "나는 죽은 소송인들이 — 대법원이라는 수레바퀴에 마음도 영혼도 짓이겨진 채로 — 회계감사원장에게 청구하지 못하고 남겨둔 그 모든 돈을 받느니, 차라리 가엾은 릭에게 제 본래의 천성을 되돌려주는 편이 낫다. 얘야, 그 돈은 대법원의 어마어마한 사악함을 기리는 피라미드를 쌓아 올릴 만큼 충분한 액수란다.\""]], [["“IS it possible, guardian,”", "\"어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요, 후견인 어른,\""], ["I asked, amazed, “that Richard can be suspicious of you?”", "저는 깜짝 놀라 물었습니다. \"리처드가 어른을 의심한다는 게 가능한 일인가요?\""]], [["“Ah, my love, my love,”", "\"아, 얘야, 얘야.\""], ["he said, “it is in the subtle poison of such abuses to breed such diseases.", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다. \"그런 폐단의 교묘한 독은 바로 그런 병을 길러내는 법이란다."], ["His blood is infected, and objects lose their natural aspects in his sight.", "그 애 피는 감염되었고, 그 애 눈에는 사물이 본래 모습을 잃어버린 게지."], ["It is not HIS fault.”", "그 애 잘못이 아니야.\""]], [["“But it is a terrible misfortune, guardian.”", "\"하지만 그건 끔찍한 불행이에요, 후견인 어른.\""]], [["“It is a terrible misfortune, little woman, to be ever drawn within the influences of Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "\"잔다이스 대 잔다이스의 영향권 안에 끌려드는 것은 끔찍한 불행이지, 작은 여인아. 그보다 더한 불행을 나는 알지 못한다."], ["I know none greater. By little and little he has been induced to trust in that rotten reed, and it communicates some portion of its rottenness to everything around him.", "그 애는 야금야금 그 썩은 갈대에 의지하도록 꾀임을 당했고, 그 갈대는 제 썩은 기운을 그 애 주위 모든 것에 옮겨 넣는 게야."], ["But again I say with all my soul, we must be patient with poor Rick and not blame him.", "하지만 다시 한번 진심으로 말하건대, 우리는 가엾은 릭에게 인내심을 갖고, 그 애를 탓하지 말아야 한다."], ["What a troop of fine fresh hearts like his have I seen in my time turned by the same means!”", "내가 살아오는 동안 그 애처럼 싱싱하고 멋진 마음들이 똑같은 수법에 무너지는 걸 얼마나 많이 보았던지!\""]], [["I could not help expressing something of my wonder and regret that his benevolent, disinterested intentions had prospered so little.", "저는 그분의 자애롭고 사심 없는 뜻이 그토록 보람을 거두지 못한 데 대한 놀라움과 안타까움을 어느 정도 드러내지 않을 수 없었습니다."]], [["“We must not say so, Dame Durden,”", "\"그런 말은 하면 못쓴다, 더든 부인.\""], ["he cheerfully replied; “Ada is the happier, I hope, and that is much.", "그분이 명랑하게 대답하셨습니다. \"에이더는 더 행복할 게다."], ["I did think that I and both these young creatures might be friends instead of distrustful foes and that we might so far counter-act the suit and prove too strong for it.", "그러길 바라고, 그것만으로도 큰 거란다. 나는 나와 이 두 젊은이가 서로 불신하는 적이 아니라 벗이 될 수 있고, 그리하여 어느 정도는 소송에 맞서 그것을 이겨낼 만큼 강해질 수 있으리라 정말로 생각했지."], ["But it was too much to expect.", "하지만 그건 지나친 기대였어."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce was the curtain of Rick’s cradle.”", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 릭이 누운 요람의 장막이었으니까.\""]], [["“But, guardian, may we not hope that a little experience will teach him what a false and wretched thing it is?”", "\"하지만 후견인 어른, 약간의 경험만으로도 그 소송이 얼마나 거짓되고 비참한 것인지를 그 애가 깨닫게 되리라 기대할 수는 없을까요?\""]], [["“We WILL hope so, my Esther,” said Mr.", "\"우리는 그러길 기대해야지, 내 에스더.\""], ["Jarndyce, “and that it may not teach him so too late.", "잔다이스 씨가 말씀하셨습니다. \"그리고 그 깨달음이 너무 늦게 오지 않기를 바라야지."], ["In any case we must not be hard on him.", "어쨌든 우리는 그 애에게 모질게 굴어선 안 된다."], ["There are not many grown and matured men living while we speak, good men too, who if they were thrown into this same court as suitors would not be vitally changed and depreciated within three years—within two—within one.", "지금 이렇게 말하는 순간에도, 다 자라 성숙한 사내들 중에, 그것도 선량한 사람들 중에, 이 같은 법정에 소송인으로 던져진다면 삼 년 안에 — 이 년 안에 — 아니 일 년 안에 본질이 바뀌고 망가지지 않을 사람이 그리 많지 않단다."], ["How can we stand amazed at poor Rick?", "그러니 가엾은 릭을 두고 어찌 놀라겠느냐?"], ["A young man so unfortunate,”", "그토록 불운한 젊은이는,\""], ["here he fell into a lower tone, as if he were thinking aloud, “cannot at first believe (who could?) that Chancery is what it is.", "여기서 그분은 마치 혼잣말을 하듯 목소리를 낮추셨습니다. \"처음에는 대법원이 지금 모습 그대로의 그것이라는 사실을 믿지 못해(누군들 믿겠느냐?)."], ["He looks to it, flushed and fitfully, to do something with his interests and bring them to some settlement.", "얼굴이 붉어진 채 들떠서 그 법정을 바라보며, 제 이권에 무언가 결말을 내고 어떤 해결을 가져다주기를 기대하지. 그런데 법정은 미루고, 실망시키고, 시험하고, 고문하지."], ["It procrastinates, disappoints, tries, tortures him; wears out his sanguine hopes and patience, thread by thread; but he still looks to it, and hankers after it, and finds his whole world treacherous and hollow.", "그 애의 들뜬 희망과 인내심을 한 올 한 올 닳아 없애. 그래도 그 애는 여전히 그 법정을 바라보고, 그것을 갈망하며, 제 온 세상이 배신으로 가득하고 텅 비어 있음을 깨닫는단다."], ["Well, well, well!", "자, 자, 자!"], ["Enough of this, my dear!”", "이 이야기는 이만하면 됐다, 얘야!\""]], [["He had supported me, as at first, all this time, and his tenderness was so precious to me that I leaned my head upon his shoulder and loved him as if he had been my father.", "그분은 처음과 마찬가지로 이 모든 시간 내내 저를 받쳐주셨고, 그분의 다정함이 제게 어찌나 소중한지 저는 그분의 어깨에 머리를 기대고 마치 그분이 제 아버지라도 되는 양 그분을 사랑했습니다."], ["I resolved in my own mind in this little pause, by some means, to see Richard when I grew strong and try to set him right.", "이 잠깐의 멈춤 동안 저는 마음속으로, 어떻게든 기운을 차리면 리처드를 만나 그를 바로잡아주리라 다짐했습니다."]], [["“There are better subjects than these,”", "\"이런 것들보다는 더 나은 이야깃거리가 있단다,\""], ["said my guardian, “for such a joyful time as the time of our dear girl’s recovery.", "후견인이 말씀하셨습니다. \"우리 사랑하는 아이가 회복되는 이 기쁜 때에 어울리는 이야기 말이다."], ["And I had a commission to broach one of them as soon as I should begin to talk.", "그리고 나는 이야기를 시작하면 곧 꺼내야 할 부탁 한 가지를 맡아 왔지."], ["When shall Ada come to see you, my love?”", "에이더가 언제 너를 보러 오면 좋겠느냐, 얘야?\""]], [["I had been thinking of that too.", "저 역시 그 일을 생각하고 있었습니다."], ["A little in connexion with the absent mirrors, but not much, for I knew my loving girl would be changed by no change in my looks.", "사라진 거울과 약간 관련이 있긴 했지만, 그리 큰 관련은 아니었습니다. 제 사랑하는 그 아이가 제 외모의 어떤 변화에도 흔들리지 않으리라는 것을 저는 알고 있었으니까요."]], [["“Dear guardian,”", "\"사랑하는 후견인 어른,\""], ["said I, “as I have shut her out so long—though indeed, indeed, she is like the light to me—”", "제가 말했습니다. \"제가 그 애를 그토록 오래 들이지 않았으니 — 정말이지, 정말이지 그 애는 제게 빛과 같은 존재인데도요 —\""]], [["“I know it well, Dame Durden, well.”", "\"잘 안다, 더든 부인, 잘 알아.\""]], [["He was so good, his touch expressed such endearing compassion and affection, and the tone of his voice carried such comfort into my heart that I stopped for a little while, quite unable to go on.", "그분은 어찌나 자상하신지, 그 손길에 그토록 살뜰한 연민과 애정이 배어 있고, 그 음성에 그토록 깊은 위안이 실려 제 마음에 스며들어, 저는 잠시 말을 잇지 못하고 멈추었습니다. \"그래, 그래, 피곤하구나.\""], ["“Yes, yes, you are tired,” said he.", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“Rest a little.”", "\"조금 쉬렴.\""]], [["“As I have kept Ada out so long,”", "\"제가 에이더를 그토록 오래 들이지 않았으니,\""], ["I began afresh after a short while, “I think I should like to have my own way a little longer, guardian.", "잠시 뒤 저는 다시 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"제 뜻대로 조금만 더 하게 해주셨으면 좋겠어요, 후견인 어른."], ["It would be best to be away from here before I see her.", "그 애를 보기 전에 여기를 떠나 있는 편이 가장 좋을 것 같아요."], ["If Charley and I were to go to some country lodging as soon as I can move, and if I had a week there in which to grow stronger and to be revived by the sweet air and to look forward to the happiness of having Ada with me again, I think it would be better for us.”", "제가 움직일 수 있게 되는 대로 찰리와 함께 어디 시골 하숙으로 가서, 거기서 일주일쯤 묵으며 기력을 차리고 맑은 공기로 되살아나서, 에이더와 다시 함께 지내는 행복을 기다릴 수 있다면, 그게 우리 두 사람한테 더 나을 것 같아요.\""]], [["I hope it was not a poor thing in me to wish to be a little more used to my altered self before I met the eyes of the dear girl I longed so ardently to see, but it is the truth.", "그토록 간절히 보고 싶었던 사랑하는 그 아이의 눈앞에 서기 전에, 달라진 제 모습에 조금 더 익숙해지고 싶다고 바란 것이 제게 못난 마음이었다고는 생각하고 싶지 않지만, 그것이 진실입니다. 저는 그러기를 바랐습니다."], ["I did. He understood me, I was sure; but I was not afraid of that.", "그분은 저를 이해하셨으리라 확신했지만, 저는 그 점이 두렵지 않았습니다."], ["If it were a poor thing, I knew he would pass it over.", "설령 그것이 못난 마음이라 해도, 그분이 그것을 너그러이 넘겨주시리라는 것을 알았기 때문입니다."]], [["“Our spoilt little woman,”", "\"우리 응석받이 작은 여인은,\" 후견인이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["said my guardian, “shall have her own way even in her inflexibility, though at the price, I know, of tears downstairs.", "\"그 고집까지도 제 뜻대로 하게 해주마. 비록 아래층에서 누군가 눈물을 쏟는 값을 치르겠지만 말이다."], ["And see here!", "그리고 이걸 좀 보렴!"], ["Here is Boythorn, heart of chivalry, breathing such ferocious vows as never were breathed on paper before, that if you don’t go and occupy his whole house, he having already turned out of it expressly for that purpose, by heaven and by earth he’ll pull it down and not leave one brick standing on another!”", "여기 기사도의 화신인 보이손이 일찍이 종이에 적힌 적 없는 무시무시한 맹세를 토해놓았구나. 네가 내려가 자기 집을 통째로 차지하지 않으면 — 그자는 벌써 그러려고 일부러 집을 비웠단다 — 하늘과 땅에 맹세코 그 집을 헐어버려 벽돌 한 장도 다른 벽돌 위에 남겨두지 않겠다는 거다!\""]], [["And my guardian put a letter in my hand, without any ordinary beginning such as “My dear Jarndyce,”", "그러고서 후견인은 제 손에 편지 한 통을 쥐여주셨는데, \"친애하는 잔다이스 군에게\""], ["but rushing at once into the words, “I swear if Miss Summerson do not come down and take possession of my house, which I vacate for her this day at one o’clock, P.M.,” and then with the utmost seriousness, and in the most emphatic terms, going on to make the extraordinary declaration he had quoted.", "같은 평범한 첫인사도 없이 곧장 본론으로 돌진하여, \"맹세컨대 서머슨 양이 내려와 내 집을 차지하지 않으면 — 나는 오늘 오후 한 시 정각에 그녀를 위해 집을 비운다 —\"로 시작하더니, 이어서 더없이 진지하게, 더없이 강조하는 어투로 그분이 인용했던 그 기상천외한 선언을 늘어놓았습니다."], ["We did not appreciate the writer the less for laughing heartily over it, and we settled that I should send him a letter of thanks on the morrow and accept his offer.", "우리는 그것을 읽으며 마음껏 웃었지만, 그렇다고 그 편지를 쓴 이를 덜 높이 사지는 않았고, 이튿날 제가 감사 편지를 보내 그의 제안을 받아들이기로 정했습니다."], ["It was a most agreeable one to me, for all the places I could have thought of, I should have liked to go to none so well as Chesney Wold.", "그것은 제게 더없이 반가운 제안이었으니, 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 어느 곳보다도 저는 체스니 월드만큼 가고 싶은 데가 없었기 때문입니다."]], [["“Now, little housewife,”", "\"자, 작은 살림꾼아,\" 후견인이 시계를 보며 말씀하셨습니다."], ["said my guardian, looking at his watch, “I was strictly timed before I came upstairs, for you must not be tired too soon; and my time has waned away to the last minute.", "\"나는 위층으로 올라오기 전에 엄격하게 시간을 정해두었단다. 너를 너무 일찍 지치게 해선 안 되니까."], ["I have one other petition.", "그런데 그 시간이 이제 마지막 일분까지 다 닳아버렸구나."], ["Little Miss Flite, hearing a rumour that you were ill, made nothing of walking down here—twenty miles, poor soul, in a pair of dancing shoes—to inquire.", "부탁이 하나 더 있단다. 작은 플라이트 양이 네가 아프다는 소문을 듣고는, 가엾게도 무도화를 신은 채 — 이십 마일을 — 여기까지 걸어 내려오는 것쯤 아무것도 아니라는 듯 안부를 물으러 왔단다."], ["It was heaven’s mercy we were at home, or she would have walked back again.”", "우리가 마침 집에 있었던 게 천만다행이지, 안 그랬으면 그이는 또 걸어서 돌아갔을 게다.\""]], [["The old conspiracy to make me happy!", "저를 행복하게 만들려는 그 오랜 음모!"], ["Everybody seemed to be in it!", "모두가 거기 가담한 듯했습니다!"]], [["“Now, pet,”", "\"자, 귀염둥이야,\""], ["said my guardian, “if it would not be irksome to you to admit the harmless little creature one afternoon before you save Boythorn’s otherwise devoted house from demolition, I believe you would make her prouder and better pleased with herself than I—though my eminent name is Jarndyce—could do in a lifetime.”", "후견인이 말씀하셨습니다. \"네가 보이손의, 안 그러면 헐릴 운명인 그 헌신적인 집을 철거에서 구해주기 전에 어느 날 오후 그 해롭잖은 작은 이를 들여보내는 게 성가시지 않다면, 너는 그이를 — 비록 내 이름이 명망 높은 잔다이스라 해도 내가 평생을 들여 해줄 수 있는 것보다 — 더 자랑스럽고 더 흐뭇하게 만들어줄 수 있으리라 믿는다.\""]], [["I have no doubt he knew there would be something in the simple image of the poor afflicted creature that would fall like a gentle lesson on my mind at that time.", "저는 그분이, 그 가엾고 고통받는 이의 소박한 모습 속에 그 무렵 제 마음에 부드러운 가르침처럼 와닿을 무언가가 있으리라는 것을 알고 계셨으리라 확신합니다."], ["I felt it as he spoke to me.", "그분이 제게 말씀하시는 동안 저는 그것을 느꼈습니다."], ["I could not tell him heartily enough how ready I was to receive her.", "저는 그이를 맞이할 마음이 얼마나 간절한지 말로는 도무지 다 표현할 수가 없었습니다."], ["I had always pitied her, never so much as now.", "저는 늘 그이를 가엾이 여겨왔지만, 그때만큼 가엾이 여긴 적은 없었습니다."], ["I had always been glad of my little power to soothe her under her calamity, but never, never, half so glad before.", "그이를 그 불행 속에서 달래줄 작은 힘이나마 제게 있다는 것을 늘 기뻐해왔지만, 그때만큼, 그 절반만큼이라도 기뻤던 적은 일찍이 없었습니다."]], [["We arranged a time for Miss Flite to come out by the coach and share my early dinner.", "우리는 플라이트 양이 마차를 타고 나와 제 이른 저녁을 함께 들 시간을 정했습니다."], ["When my guardian left me, I turned my face away upon my couch and prayed to be forgiven if I, surrounded by such blessings, had magnified to myself the little trial that I had to undergo.", "후견인이 떠나신 뒤, 저는 긴 의자에 얼굴을 돌리고 누워, 만일 제가 이토록 많은 축복에 둘러싸여 있으면서 제가 겪어야 할 그 자그마한 시련을 스스로 부풀렸다면 용서해달라고 기도했습니다."], ["The childish prayer of that old birthday when I had aspired to be industrious, contented, and true-hearted and to do good to some one and win some love to myself if I could came back into my mind with a reproachful sense of all the happiness I had since enjoyed and all the affectionate hearts that had been turned towards me.", "부지런하고 만족할 줄 알며 진실한 사람이 되어, 누군가에게 선을 베풀고 할 수만 있다면 약간의 사랑이라도 얻고 싶다고 빌던 그 옛 생일날의 어린아이 같은 기도가, 그 뒤로 제가 누려온 그 모든 행복과 제게로 향해온 그 모든 다정한 마음들을 떠올리게 하는 자책감과 함께 다시 마음속에 되살아났습니다."], ["If I were weak now, what had I profited by those mercies?", "만일 지금 제가 약해진다면, 그 은혜들로부터 제가 얻은 것이 무엇이겠습니까?"], ["I repeated the old childish prayer in its old childish words and found that its old peace had not departed from it.", "저는 그 옛 어린아이의 기도를 그 옛 어린아이의 말 그대로 되뇌었고, 그 옛 평안이 그 기도에서 떠나지 않았음을 깨달았습니다."]], [["My guardian now came every day.", "이제 후견인은 날마다 오셨습니다."], ["In a week or so more I could walk about our rooms and hold long talks with Ada from behind the window-curtain.", "한 주쯤 더 지나자 저는 우리 방들을 거닐 수 있었고, 창문 커튼 뒤에서 에이더와 긴 이야기를 나눌 수 있었습니다."], ["Yet I never saw her, for I had not as yet the courage to look at the dear face, though I could have done so easily without her seeing me.", "그래도 저는 그 아이를 한 번도 보지 않았는데, 그 아이가 알아채지 못하게 쉽게 볼 수 있었는데도, 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 마주할 용기가 아직 나지 않았기 때문입니다."]], [["On the appointed day Miss Flite arrived.", "정해진 날 플라이트 양이 도착했습니다."], ["The poor little creature ran into my room quite forgetful of her usual dignity, and crying from her very heart of hearts, “My dear Fitz Jarndyce!” fell upon my neck and kissed me twenty times.", "그 가엾은 작은 이는 평소의 위엄도 까맣게 잊은 채 제 방으로 달려 들어와, 진심을 다해 마음속 깊은 곳에서부터 울며 \"내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스!\"라고 외치고는 제 목에 매달려 스무 번이나 입을 맞추었습니다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"어머나!\" 그이는 손가방에 손을 넣으며 말했습니다."], ["said she, putting her hand into her reticule, “I have nothing here but documents, my dear Fitz Jarndyce; I must borrow a pocket handkerchief.”", "\"여긴 서류밖에 없네요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스. 손수건을 좀 빌려야겠어요.\""]], [["Charley gave her one, and the good creature certainly made use of it, for she held it to her eyes with both hands and sat so, shedding tears for the next ten minutes.", "찰리가 손수건 하나를 건네주었고, 이 선량한 이는 분명 그것을 요긴하게 썼으니, 두 손으로 그것을 눈에 갖다 댄 채 그렇게 앉아 그다음 십 분 동안 내내 눈물을 흘렸습니다."]], [["“With pleasure, my dear Fitz Jarndyce,”", "\"기뻐서 그래요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스.\""], ["she was careful to explain.", "그이는 굳이 설명하려 애썼습니다."], ["“Not the least pain.", "\"조금도 괴로워서가 아니에요."], ["Pleasure to see you well again.", "당신이 다시 건강해진 걸 보니 기뻐서요."], ["Pleasure at having the honour of being admitted to see you.", "당신을 뵙는 영광을 누리게 되어 기뻐서요."], ["I am so much fonder of you, my love, than of the Chancellor.", "나는 대법관님보다 당신이 훨씬 더 좋답니다, 내 사랑."], ["Though I DO attend court regularly.", "비록 내가 법정에는 꼬박꼬박 나가지만요."], ["By the by, my dear, mentioning pocket handkerchiefs—”", "그런데 말이 나온 김에, 얘야, 손수건 이야기를 하다 보니 —\""]], [["Miss Flite here looked at Charley, who had been to meet her at the place where the coach stopped.", "여기서 플라이트 양은 찰리를 보았는데, 찰리는 마차가 서는 곳까지 그이를 마중 나갔던 참이었습니다."], ["Charley glanced at me and looked unwilling to pursue the suggestion.", "찰리는 저를 흘끔 보더니 그 말을 더 이어가기를 꺼리는 눈치였습니다."]], [["“Ve-ry right!”", "\"옳지!\""], ["said Miss Flite, “Ve-ry correct.", "플라이트 양이 말했습니다. \"옳고말고."], ["Truly!", "정말이지!"], ["Highly indiscreet of me to mention it; but my dear Miss Fitz Jarndyce, I am afraid I am at times (between ourselves, you wouldn’t think it) a little—rambling you know,”", "내가 그걸 입에 올리다니 참으로 경솔했네요. 하지만 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스 양, 솔직히 나는 가끔 (우리끼리니까 하는 말인데, 당신은 짐작도 못 하겠지만) 조금 — 횡설수설하거든요.\""], ["said Miss Flite, touching her forehead.", "플라이트 양은 제 이마를 가리키며 말했습니다."], ["“Nothing more.”", "\"그것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“What were you going to tell me?”", "\"무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거예요?\""], ["said I, smiling, for I saw she wanted to go on.", "저는 미소 지으며 말했습니다. 그이가 말을 잇고 싶어 하는 것을 보았기 때문입니다."], ["“You have roused my curiosity, and now you must gratify it.”", "\"제 호기심을 돋우어놓으셨으니, 이제 풀어주셔야지요.\""]], [["Miss Flite looked at Charley for advice in this important crisis, who said, “If you please, ma’am, you had better tell then,” and therein gratified Miss Flite beyond measure.", "플라이트 양은 이 중대한 고비에서 조언을 구하듯 찰리를 보았고, 찰리는 \"괜찮으시면, 아주머니, 말씀하시는 게 낫겠어요\"라고 말하여, 그로써 플라이트 양을 더없이 흐뭇하게 했습니다."]], [["“So sagacious, our young friend,”", "\"참 영리하지, 우리 어린 친구는.\""], ["said she to me in her mysterious way.", "그이는 그 특유의 알 듯 모를 듯한 태도로 제게 말했습니다."], ["“Diminutive.", "\"조그맣지만."], ["But ve-ry sagacious!", "아주아주 영리해요!"], ["Well, my dear, it’s a pretty anecdote.", "자, 얘야, 이건 예쁜 일화랍니다."], ["Nothing more.", "그것뿐이에요."], ["Still I think it charming.", "그래도 나는 참 매력적이라고 생각해요."], ["Who should follow us down the road from the coach, my dear, but a poor person in a very ungenteel bonnet—”", "마차에서 내려 우리를 따라 길을 내려온 게 누구겠어요, 얘야. 아주 촌스러운 보닛을 쓴 가엾은 사람이었답니다 —\""]], [["“Jenny, if you please, miss,”", "\"제니예요, 아주머니.\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했습니다."]], [["“Just so!”", "\"바로 그렇지!\""], ["Miss Flite acquiesced with the greatest suavity.", "플라이트 양은 더없이 상냥하게 수긍했습니다."], ["“Jenny.", "\"제니."], ["Ye-es!", "그래요!"], ["And what does she tell our young friend but that there has been a lady with a veil inquiring at her cottage after my dear Fitz Jarndyce’s health and taking a handkerchief away with her as a little keepsake merely because it was my amiable Fitz Jarndyce’s!", "그런데 그 사람이 우리 어린 친구에게 뭐라고 했느냐면, 베일을 쓴 어떤 부인이 자기 오두막에 찾아와 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스의 안부를 묻고는, 손수건 하나를 자그마한 기념으로 가져갔다지 뭐예요. 그저 그게 내 사랑스러운 피츠 잔다이스의 것이라는 이유만으로요!"], ["Now, you know, so very prepossessing in the lady with the veil!”", "자, 보세요, 그 베일 쓴 부인이 어찌나 마음을 끄는지!\""]], [["“If you please, miss,”", "\"괜찮으시면, 아가씨,\""], ["said Charley, to whom I looked in some astonishment, “Jenny says that when her baby died, you left a handkerchief there, and that she put it away and kept it with the baby’s little things.", "제가 다소 놀라 바라보자 찰리가 말했습니다. \"제니 말로는, 아기가 죽었을 때 아가씨가 거기 손수건 하나를 두고 가셨고, 자기가 그걸 챙겨서 아기의 자그마한 물건들과 함께 간직해두었대요."], ["I think, if you please, partly because it was yours, miss, and partly because it had covered the baby.”", "제 생각엔, 괜찮으시면, 한편으로는 그게 아가씨 것이어서고, 한편으로는 그게 아기를 덮어준 것이어서 그랬나 봐요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Diminutive,”", "\"조그맣지만,\""], ["whispered Miss Flite, making a variety of motions about her own forehead to express intellect in Charley.", "플라이트 양은 찰리의 총명함을 표현하느라 제 이마 언저리에 갖가지 손짓을 해 보이며 속삭였습니다. \"엄청나게 영리해요!"], ["“But exceedingly sagacious!", "게다가 참 사랑스럽고!"], ["And so dear! My love, she’s clearer than any counsel I ever heard!”", "얘야, 저 아이는 내가 들어본 그 어떤 변호사보다 더 또렷하답니다!\""]], [["“Yes, Charley,”", "\"그래, 찰리.\""], ["I returned.", "제가 대답했습니다."], ["“I remember it.", "\"기억나."], ["Well?”", "그래서?\""]], [["“Well, miss,”", "\"네, 아가씨,\""], ["said Charley, “and that’s the handkerchief the lady took.", "찰리가 말했습니다. \"그게 바로 그 부인이 가져간 손수건이에요."], ["And Jenny wants you to know that she wouldn’t have made away with it herself for a heap of money but that the lady took it and left some money instead.", "그리고 제니는 아가씨가 이걸 알아주셨으면 한대요. 자기는 돈을 산더미처럼 준대도 그걸 절대 내놓지 않았을 텐데, 그 부인이 가져가면서 대신 돈을 좀 두고 갔다고요."], ["Jenny don’t know her at all, if you please, miss!”", "제니는 그 부인을 전혀 모른대요, 괜찮으시면, 아가씨!\""]], [["“Why, who can she be?”", "\"그런데 그 부인이 누구일까?\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“My love,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["Miss Flite suggested, advancing her lips to my ear with her most mysterious look, “in MY opinion—don’t mention this to our diminutive friend—she’s the Lord Chancellor’s wife.", "플라이트 양이 더없이 알 듯 모를 듯한 표정으로 입술을 제 귀에 가까이 대며 넌지시 말했습니다. \"내 생각에는 — 이건 우리 조그만 친구한테는 말하지 말아요 — 그 부인은 대법관님의 부인이에요."], ["He’s married, you know.", "그분이 결혼했다는 거 알지요."], ["And I understand she leads him a terrible life.", "그런데 듣자 하니 그 부인이 그분을 못살게 군다더군요."], ["Throws his lordship’s papers into the fire, my dear, if he won’t pay the jeweller!”", "보석상한테 돈을 안 갚으면 각하의 서류를 불 속에 던져버린대요, 얘야!\""]], [["I did not think very much about this lady then, for I had an impression that it might be Caddy.", "저는 그때 이 부인에 대해 그리 깊이 생각하지 않았습니다. 그게 캐디일지도 모른다는 느낌이 들었기 때문입니다."], ["Besides, my attention was diverted by my visitor, who was cold after her ride and looked hungry and who, our dinner being brought in, required some little assistance in arraying herself with great satisfaction in a pitiable old scarf and a much-worn and often-mended pair of gloves, which she had brought down in a paper parcel.", "게다가 제 손님이 제 주의를 흩뜨려놓았는데, 그이는 마차를 타고 오느라 몸이 차가워진 데다 시장해 보였고, 저녁이 들어오자 종이 꾸러미에 싸 들고 온 가엾으리만치 낡은 숄과 닳고 닳아 여러 번 기운 장갑을 더없이 만족스럽게 차려입는 데에 약간의 도움을 필요로 했습니다."], ["I had to preside, too, over the entertainment, consisting of a dish of fish, a roast fowl, a sweetbread, vegetables, pudding, and Madeira; and it was so pleasant to see how she enjoyed it, and with what state and ceremony she did honour to it, that I was soon thinking of nothing else.", "게다가 저는 생선 요리와 닭구이, 췌장 요리, 채소, 푸딩, 마데이라주로 이루어진 그 잔치를 주관해야 했는데, 그이가 그것을 얼마나 즐기는지, 또 얼마나 위엄과 격식을 차려 그 음식에 경의를 표하는지를 지켜보는 일이 어찌나 즐거운지, 저는 곧 다른 아무것도 생각하지 않게 되었습니다."]], [["When we had finished and had our little dessert before us, embellished by the hands of my dear, who would yield the superintendence of everything prepared for me to no one, Miss Flite was so very chatty and happy that I thought I would lead her to her own history, as she was always pleased to talk about herself.", "식사를 마치고 우리 앞에 자그마한 후식이 놓였을 때 — 저를 위해 준비된 그 무엇도 다른 누구에게 감독을 맡기려 하지 않는 사랑하는 그 아이의 손으로 곱게 꾸며진 것이었지요 — 플라이트 양은 어찌나 수다스럽고 행복해 보이는지, 저는 그이를 그이 자신의 내력으로 이끌어보아야겠다고 생각했습니다. 그이는 늘 자기 이야기를 하는 걸 좋아했으니까요."], ["I began by saying “You have attended on the Lord Chancellor many years, Miss Flite?”", "저는 이렇게 운을 뗐습니다. \"대법관님 곁을 여러 해 동안 지키셨지요, 플라이트 양?\""]], [["“Oh, many, many, many years, my dear.", "\"오, 여러, 여러, 여러 해째예요, 얘야."], ["But I expect a judgment.", "하지만 나는 판결을 기다리고 있어요."], ["Shortly.”", "곧 나올 거예요.\""]], [["There was an anxiety even in her hopefulness that made me doubtful if I had done right in approaching the subject.", "그이의 그 희망 속에도 어떤 불안이 깃들어 있어서, 제가 이 화제를 꺼낸 게 잘한 일인지 의심스러워졌습니다."], ["I thought I would say no more about it.", "저는 이에 대해서는 더 말하지 않으려고 생각했습니다."]], [["“My father expected a judgment,”", "\"우리 아버지도 판결을 기다리셨어요.\""], ["said Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 말했습니다."], ["“My brother.", "\"우리 오라버니도."], ["My sister.", "우리 언니도."], ["They all expected a judgment.", "다들 판결을 기다렸지요."], ["The same that I expect.”", "내가 기다리는 것과 똑같은 판결을요.\""]], [["“They are all—”", "\"그분들은 모두 —\""]], [["“Ye-es.", "\"그래요."], ["Dead of course, my dear,”", "물론 돌아가셨지요, 얘야.\""], ["said she.", "그이가 말했습니다."]], [["As I saw she would go on, I thought it best to try to be serviceable to her by meeting the theme rather than avoiding it.", "저는 그이가 말을 계속하리라는 것을 알았기에, 이 화제를 피하기보다 차라리 마주함으로써 그이에게 도움이 되어보는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다."]], [["“Would it not be wiser,”", "\"이 판결을 더는 기다리지 않는 편이,\""], ["said I, “to expect this judgment no more?”", "제가 말했습니다. \"더 현명하지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Why, my dear,”", "\"아니, 얘야,\""], ["she answered promptly, “of course it would!”", "그이가 선뜻 대답했습니다. \"물론 그게 현명하겠지요!\""]], [["“And to attend the court no more?”", "\"그리고 법정에도 더는 나가지 않는 게요?\""]], [["“Equally of course,”", "\"그것도 물론이고요.\""], ["said she.", "그이가 말했습니다."], ["“Very wearing to be always in expectation of what never comes, my dear Fitz Jarndyce!", "\"결코 오지 않는 것을 늘 기다린다는 건 참으로 사람을 지치게 해요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스!"], ["Wearing, I assure you, to the bone!”", "정말이지, 뼛속까지 닳게 한다니까요!\""]], [["She slightly showed me her arm, and it was fearfully thin indeed.", "그이는 제게 자기 팔을 살짝 보여주었는데, 정말이지 무섭도록 야위어 있었습니다."]], [["“But, my dear,”", "\"하지만, 얘야,\""], ["she went on in her mysterious way, “there’s a dreadful attraction in the place.", "그이가 그 알 듯 모를 듯한 태도로 말을 이었습니다. \"그곳에는 무시무시한 끌어당김이 있어요."], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["Don’t mention it to our diminutive friend when she comes in.", "우리 조그만 친구가 들어오면 이 얘긴 하지 말아요."], ["Or it may frighten her.", "안 그러면 그 애가 겁먹을지 몰라요."], ["With good reason.", "그럴 만도 하지요."], ["There’s a cruel attraction in the place.", "그곳에는 잔인한 끌어당김이 있답니다."], ["You CAN’T leave it.", "거길 떠날 수가 없어요."], ["And you MUST expect.”", "그리고 기다릴 수밖에 없어요.\""]], [["I tried to assure her that this was not so.", "저는 그렇지 않다고 그이를 안심시켜보려 했습니다."], ["She heard me patiently and smilingly, but was ready with her own answer.", "그이는 참을성 있게 미소 지으며 제 말을 들었지만, 자기 나름의 대답을 준비하고 있었습니다."]], [["“Aye, aye, aye!", "\"그래요, 그래, 그래!"], ["You think so because I am a little rambling.", "내가 조금 횡설수설하니까 그렇게 생각하는 거예요."], ["Ve-ry absurd, to be a little rambling, is it not?", "조금 횡설수설하다니 참 우습지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["Ve-ry confusing, too. To the head.", "머릿속이 아주 어지럽기도 하고요."], ["I find it so.", "나도 그렇게 느껴요."], ["But, my dear, I have been there many years, and I have noticed.", "하지만, 얘야, 나는 거기 여러 해 있었고, 그래서 눈여겨봐 왔어요."], ["It’s the mace and seal upon the table.”", "그건 탁자 위의 권표와 옥새 때문이에요.\""]], [["What could they do, did she think? I mildly asked her.", "그것들이 무얼 할 수 있다는 거냐고, 그이는 어떻게 생각하느냐고 저는 부드럽게 물었습니다."]], [["“Draw,”", "\"끌어당기지요.\""], ["returned Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 대답했습니다."], ["“Draw people on, my dear.", "\"사람들을 끌어들여요, 얘야."], ["Draw peace out of them.", "그들에게서 평안을 빨아내요."], ["Sense out of them.", "분별을 빨아내요."], ["Good looks out of them.", "고운 외모를 빨아내요."], ["Good qualities out of them.", "좋은 성품을 빨아내요."], ["I have felt them even drawing my rest away in the night.", "나는 그것들이 밤에 내 잠마저 빨아내는 걸 느꼈답니다."], ["Cold and glittering devils!”", "차갑고 번쩍이는 악마들이에요!\""]], [["She tapped me several times upon the arm and nodded good-humouredly as if she were anxious I should understand that I had no cause to fear her, though she spoke so gloomily, and confided these awful secrets to me.", "그이는 제 팔을 여러 번 톡톡 두드리며 호의 어린 표정으로 고개를 끄덕였는데, 마치 그토록 음울하게 말하며 이 무시무시한 비밀들을 제게 털어놓으면서도, 제가 그이를 두려워할 까닭이 없음을 알아주기를 바라는 듯했습니다."]], [["“Let me see,”", "\"가만, 보자.\""], ["said she.", "그이가 말했습니다."], ["“I’ll tell you my own case.", "\"내 경우를 들려드리지요."], ["Before they ever drew me—before I had ever seen them—what was it I used to do?", "그것들이 나를 끌어당기기 전에 — 내가 그것들을 보기도 전에 — 내가 뭘 하던 사람이었더라?"], ["Tambourine playing?", "탬버린 연주? 아니."], ["No. Tambour work.", "자수 일이에요."], ["I and my sister worked at tambour work.", "나와 우리 언니는 자수 일을 했어요."], ["Our father and our brother had a builder’s business.", "우리 아버지와 오라버니는 건축업을 했고요."], ["We all lived together.", "우리는 다 같이 살았답니다."], ["Ve-ry respectably, my dear!", "아주아주 점잖게요, 얘야!"], ["First, our father was drawn—slowly.", "처음에 우리 아버지가 끌려갔어요 — 천천히."], ["Home was drawn with him.", "집안도 아버지와 함께 끌려갔지요."], ["In a few years he was a fierce, sour, angry bankrupt without a kind word or a kind look for any one.", "몇 해 만에 아버지는, 누구에게도 다정한 말 한마디 다정한 눈길 한 번 줄 줄 모르는 사납고 신경질적이고 성난 파산자가 되어버렸어요."], ["He had been so different, Fitz Jarndyce.", "전에는 그렇게도 다른 분이셨는데, 피츠 잔다이스."], ["He was drawn to a debtors’ prison.", "아버지는 채무자 감옥으로 끌려갔어요."], ["There he died.", "거기서 돌아가셨지요."], ["Then our brother was drawn—swiftly—to drunkenness.", "그다음엔 우리 오라버니가 끌려갔어요 — 순식간에 — 술독으로."], ["And rags.", "그리고 누더기로."], ["And death.", "그리고 죽음으로."], ["Then my sister was drawn.", "그다음엔 우리 언니가 끌려갔지요. 쉿!"], ["Hush! Never ask to what!", "어디로 끌려갔는지는 절대 묻지 말아요!"], ["Then I was ill and in misery, and heard, as I had often heard before, that this was all the work of Chancery.", "그러고 나서 나는 병들고 비참해졌고, 전에도 자주 들었듯이, 이 모든 게 대법원의 짓이라는 말을 들었어요."], ["When I got better, I went to look at the monster.", "몸이 나아지자 나는 그 괴물을 보러 갔지요."], ["And then I found out how it was, and I was drawn to stay there.”", "그러고서 나는 그게 어찌 된 일인지 알아냈고, 거기 머물도록 끌려들고 말았답니다.\""]], [["Having got over her own short narrative, in the delivery of which she had spoken in a low, strained voice, as if the shock were fresh upon her, she gradually resumed her usual air of amiable importance.", "자기 자신의 그 짤막한 이야기를 — 마치 그 충격이 아직도 생생하다는 듯 낮고 긴장된 목소리로 풀어놓던 그 이야기를 — 다 끝내고 나자, 그이는 차츰 평소의 상냥하고도 의젓한 태도를 되찾았습니다."]], [["“You don’t quite credit me, my dear!", "\"내 말을 영 못 믿겠지요, 얘야!"], ["Well, well!", "자, 자!"], ["You will, some day.", "언젠가는 믿게 될 거예요."], ["I am a little rambling.", "내가 조금 횡설수설하긴 해요."], ["But I have noticed.", "하지만 나는 눈여겨봐 왔답니다."], ["I have seen many new faces come, unsuspicious, within the influence of the mace and seal in these many years.", "이 여러 해 동안 의심 없이 권표와 옥새의 영향권 안으로 새로운 얼굴들이 들어오는 걸 많이 봤어요. 우리 아버지가 거기 들어오셨듯이."], ["As my father’s came there.", "우리 오라버니가 그랬듯이."], ["As my brother’s.", "우리 언니가 그랬듯이."], ["As my sister’s. As my own.", "나 자신이 그랬듯이."], ["I hear Conversation Kenge and the rest of them say to the new faces, ‘Here’s little Miss Flite.", "나는 입심 좋은 켄지며 그 밖의 사람들이 새 얼굴들에게 이렇게 말하는 걸 들어요. '여기 작은 플라이트 양이 계시오."], ["Oh, you are new here; and you must come and be presented to little Miss Flite!’ Ve-ry good.", "아, 당신은 여기 처음이군요. 그러니 이리 와서 작은 플라이트 양에게 인사드려야지요!'"], ["Proud I am sure to have the honour!", "아주 좋아요. 그 영광을 누리게 되어 영광이고말고요!"], ["And we all laugh.", "그러면 우리는 다 함께 웃지요."], ["But, Fitz Jarndyce, I know what will happen.", "하지만, 피츠 잔다이스, 나는 앞으로 무슨 일이 벌어질지 알아요."], ["I know, far better than they do, when the attraction has begun.", "끌어당김이 시작되면, 나는 그들보다 훨씬 더 잘 안답니다. 나는 그 징조를 알아요, 얘야."], ["I know the signs, my dear.", "그리들리에게서 그게 시작되는 걸 봤어요."], ["I saw them begin in Gridley.", "그리고 그게 끝나는 것도 봤지요."], ["And I saw them end.", "피츠 잔다이스, 내 사랑,\""], ["Fitz Jarndyce, my love,”", "다시 목소리를 낮추며 말했습니다."], ["speaking low again, “I saw them beginning in our friend the ward in Jarndyce.", "\"나는 그게 우리 친구, 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인에게서 시작되는 걸 봤어요."], ["Let some one hold him back.", "누군가 그를 붙들어줘야 해요."], ["Or he’ll be drawn to ruin.”", "안 그러면 그는 파멸로 끌려들고 말 거예요.\""]], [["She looked at me in silence for some moments, with her face gradually softening into a smile.", "그이는 잠시 말없이 저를 바라보았는데, 그 얼굴이 차츰 누그러지며 미소로 번졌습니다."], ["Seeming to fear that she had been too gloomy, and seeming also to lose the connexion in her mind, she said politely as she sipped her glass of wine, “Yes, my dear, as I was saying, I expect a judgment shortly.", "자기가 너무 음울했나 싶어 두려워하는 듯, 또한 머릿속에서 이야기의 갈피를 놓쳐버린 듯, 그이는 포도주잔을 홀짝이며 정중하게 말했습니다. \"그래요, 얘야, 아까 말했듯이, 나는 곧 판결을 기다리고 있어요."], ["Then I shall release my birds, you know, and confer estates.”", "그러면 알다시피 나는 내 새들을 풀어주고, 영지를 나누어줄 거랍니다.\""]], [["I was much impressed by her allusion to Richard and by the sad meaning, so sadly illustrated in her poor pinched form, that made its way through all her incoherence.", "저는 그이가 리처드를 두고 한 말에, 그리고 그이의 가엾고 야윈 모습으로 그토록 서글프게 그려진 그 슬픈 의미가 그 모든 두서없음을 뚫고 드러나는 데에 깊은 인상을 받았습니다."], ["But happily for her, she was quite complacent again now and beamed with nods and smiles.", "그러나 다행히도 그이는 이제 다시 아주 흡족해져서 끄덕임과 미소로 환히 빛났습니다."]], [["“But, my dear,”", "\"그런데, 얘야,\""], ["she said, gaily, reaching another hand to put it upon mine.", "그이는 다른 손을 마저 뻗어 제 손 위에 얹으며 명랑하게 말했습니다."], ["“You have not congratulated me on my physician.", "\"당신은 내 주치의 일로 나를 축하해주지 않았어요."], ["Positively not once, yet!”", "정말이지 단 한 번도요!\""]], [["I was obliged to confess that I did not quite know what she meant.", "저는 그이가 무슨 말을 하는지 잘 모르겠다고 고백할 수밖에 없었습니다."]], [["“My physician, Mr. Woodcourt, my dear, who was so exceedingly attentive to me.", "\"내 주치의, 우드코트 선생 말이에요, 얘야."], ["Though his services were rendered quite gratuitously.", "나한테 그렇게도 극진했던 분이지요. 그것도 보수라곤 한 푼 받지 않고서요."], ["Until the Day of Judgment.", "심판의 날까지요."], ["I mean THE judgment that will dissolve the spell upon me of the mace and seal.”", "내가 말하는 건 권표와 옥새가 내게 건 마법을 풀어줄 바로 그 판결이에요.\""]], [["“Mr. Woodcourt is so far away, now,”", "\"우드코트 씨는 지금 그토록 멀리 계셔서,\""], ["said I, “that I thought the time for such congratulation was past, Miss Flite.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"그런 축하를 드릴 때는 이미 지난 줄 알았어요, 플라이트 양.\""]], [["“But, my child,”", "\"하지만, 얘야,\""], ["she returned, “is it possible that you don’t know what has happened?”", "그이가 대답했습니다. \"무슨 일이 있었는지 모른다는 게 말이 되나요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"몰라요.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Not what everybody has been talking of, my beloved Fitz Jarndyce!”", "\"모두가 입에 올리던 그 일을 말이에요, 내 사랑하는 피츠 잔다이스!\""]], [["“No,” said I.", "\"몰라요.\""], ["“You forget how long I have been here.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"제가 여기 얼마나 오래 있었는지 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["“True!", "\"맞아요!"], ["My dear, for the moment—true.", "얘야, 잠깐 — 맞아요."], ["I blame myself.", "내 탓이네요."], ["But my memory has been drawn out of me, with everything else, by what I mentioned.", "하지만 내 기억력은, 다른 모든 것과 함께, 내가 말한 그것에 빨려나가 버렸거든요."], ["Ve-ry strong influence, is it not?", "아주 강한 영향력이지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["Well, my dear, there has been a terrible shipwreck over in those East Indian seas.”", "자, 얘야, 저 동인도 바다 건너에서 끔찍한 난파 사고가 있었답니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Woodcourt shipwrecked!”", "\"우드코트 씨가 난파를 당하셨어요!\""]], [["“Don’t be agitated, my dear.", "\"동요하지 말아요, 얘야."], ["He is safe.", "그분은 무사해요."], ["An awful scene.", "무시무시한 광경이었지요."], ["Death in all shapes.", "온갖 모습의 죽음이."], ["Hundreds of dead and dying.", "수백 명의 죽은 자와 죽어가는 자들이."], ["Fire, storm, and darkness.", "불길과 폭풍과 어둠이."], ["Numbers of the drowning thrown upon a rock.", "숱한 익사자들이 바위에 내던져졌지요."], ["There, and through it all, my dear physician was a hero.", "거기서, 그 모든 것 한가운데서 내 소중한 주치의는 영웅이었답니다."], ["Calm and brave through everything.", "모든 일을 헤쳐가며 침착하고 용감했어요."], ["Saved many lives, never complained in hunger and thirst, wrapped naked people in his spare clothes, took the lead, showed them what to do, governed them, tended the sick, buried the dead, and brought the poor survivors safely off at last!", "숱한 목숨을 구하고, 굶주리고 목마른 가운데서도 결코 불평하지 않고, 헐벗은 사람들을 자기 여벌 옷으로 감싸주고, 앞장서서 사람들에게 어찌해야 할지 일러주고, 그들을 통솔하고, 병든 자를 돌보고, 죽은 자를 묻어주고, 마침내 그 가엾은 생존자들을 무사히 데리고 빠져나왔지요!"], ["My dear, the poor emaciated creatures all but worshipped him.", "얘야, 그 가엾고 야윈 사람들이 그분을 거의 떠받들다시피 했답니다."], ["They fell down at his feet when they got to the land and blessed him.", "뭍에 닿자 그들은 그분 발치에 엎드려 그분을 축복했어요."], ["The whole country rings with it.", "온 나라가 그 이야기로 떠들썩하지요."], ["Stay!", "가만!"], ["Where’s my bag of documents?", "내 서류 가방이 어디 있더라?"], ["I have got it there, and you shall read it, you shall read it!”", "거기 가지고 왔으니, 당신이 읽어봐요, 꼭 읽어봐요!\""]], [["And I DID read all the noble history, though very slowly and imperfectly then, for my eyes were so dimmed that I could not see the words, and I cried so much that I was many times obliged to lay down the long account she had cut out of the newspaper.", "그리고 저는 그 고귀한 이야기를 빠짐없이 읽었습니다. 다만 그때는 아주 더디게, 또 온전치 못하게 읽었지요."], ["I felt so triumphant ever to have known the man who had done such generous and gallant deeds, I felt such glowing exultation in his renown, I so admired and loved what he had done, that I envied the storm-worn people who had fallen at his feet and blessed him as their preserver.", "제 눈이 어찌나 흐려졌는지 글자가 보이지 않았고, 어찌나 많이 울었는지 그이가 신문에서 오려낸 그 긴 기사를 몇 번이나 내려놓아야 했으니까요. 그토록 너그럽고 용맹한 일을 해낸 그 사람을 알고 지냈다는 사실에 저는 어찌나 의기양양했는지, 그의 명성에 어찌나 뜨거운 환희를 느꼈는지, 그가 한 일을 어찌나 흠모하고 사랑했는지, 그의 발치에 엎드려 그를 자기네 구원자라며 축복한 그 폭풍에 시달린 사람들이 부럽기까지 했습니다."], ["I could myself have kneeled down then, so far away, and blessed him in my rapture that he should be so truly good and brave.", "저 자신도 그때, 그토록 멀리 떨어진 곳에서나마 무릎 꿇고, 그가 그토록 진실로 선하고 용감하다는 사실에 황홀한 마음으로 그를 축복할 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["I felt that no one—mother, sister, wife—could honour him more than I.", "어머니든, 누이든, 아내든 — 그 누구도 저보다 더 그를 높일 수는 없으리라 느꼈습니다."], ["I did, indeed!", "정말로 그러했습니다!"]], [["My poor little visitor made me a present of the account, and when as the evening began to close in she rose to take her leave, lest she should miss the coach by which she was to return, she was still full of the shipwreck, which I had not yet sufficiently composed myself to understand in all its details.", "저의 가엾은 작은 손님은 그 기사를 제게 선물로 주었고, 저녁이 저물어들기 시작하여 자기가 타고 돌아갈 마차를 놓칠세라 일어나 작별을 고할 때에도, 여전히 그 난파 이야기로 가득 차 있었습니다. 저는 아직 그 이야기를 세세한 데까지 다 이해할 만큼 마음을 추스르지 못한 채였지요."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said she as she carefully folded up her scarf and gloves, “my brave physician ought to have a title bestowed upon him.", "그이는 자기 숄과 장갑을 정성껏 개키며 말했습니다. \"내 용감한 주치의는 작위를 받아야 마땅해요."], ["And no doubt he will.", "그리고 틀림없이 받을 거예요."], ["You are of that opinion?”", "당신도 그렇게 생각하지요?\""]], [["That he well deserved one, yes.", "작위를 받을 만한 분이냐, 그렇습니다."], ["That he would ever have one, no.", "작위를 받게 되겠느냐, 아닙니다."]], [["“Why not, Fitz Jarndyce?”", "\"왜 안 되나요, 피츠 잔다이스?\""], ["she asked rather sharply.", "그이가 다소 날카롭게 물었습니다."]], [["I said it was not the custom in England to confer titles on men distinguished by peaceful services, however good and great, unless occasionally when they consisted of the accumulation of some very large amount of money.", "저는, 영국에서는 평화로운 공로로 이름을 떨친 사람에게는 그것이 아무리 훌륭하고 위대할지라도, 이따금 그 공로가 어마어마한 거액의 돈을 쌓아 올린 것일 때를 빼고는, 작위를 내리는 관습이 없다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, good gracious,”", "\"아니, 세상에.\""], ["said Miss Flite, “how can you say that?", "플라이트 양이 말했습니다. \"어떻게 그런 말을 할 수 있어요?"], ["Surely you know, my dear, that all the greatest ornaments of England in knowledge, imagination, active humanity, and improvement of every sort are added to its nobility!", "얘야, 분명 당신도 알지 않나요? 지식, 상상력, 적극적인 인류애, 온갖 종류의 개선에서 영국의 가장 위대한 광채들이 모두 그 귀족 계급에 보태진다는 걸요!"], ["Look round you, my dear, and consider.", "둘러보고 잘 생각해봐요, 얘야."], ["YOU must be rambling a little now, I think, if you don’t know that this is the great reason why titles will always last in the land!”", "이걸 모른다면 지금 당신이 조금 횡설수설하는 거예요. 바로 이것이야말로 이 나라에서 작위가 영원히 이어지는 위대한 까닭이라고요!\""]], [["I am afraid she believed what she said, for there were moments when she was very mad indeed.", "그이가 자기 말을 정말로 믿는 게 아닐까 저는 두렵습니다. 정말로 정신이 온전치 못한 순간들이 있었으니까요."]], [["And now I must part with the little secret I have thus far tried to keep.", "이제 저는 지금까지 지켜오려 애쓴 그 작은 비밀을 털어놓아야겠습니다."], ["I had thought, sometimes, that Mr. Woodcourt loved me and that if he had been richer he would perhaps have told me that he loved me before he went away.", "저는 가끔, 우드코트 씨가 저를 사랑하시고, 만일 그분이 좀 더 부유했더라면 떠나기 전에 어쩌면 저를 사랑한다고 말해주셨을지도 모른다고 생각하곤 했습니다."], ["I had thought, sometimes, that if he had done so, I should have been glad of it.", "또 가끔은, 만일 그분이 그러셨더라면 제가 기뻐했으리라고 생각하기도 했습니다."], ["But how much better it was now that this had never happened!", "그러나 그런 일이 일어나지 않은 것이 지금 얼마나 다행인지요!"], ["What should I have suffered if I had had to write to him and tell him that the poor face he had known as mine was quite gone from me and that I freely released him from his bondage to one whom he had never seen!", "만일 제가 그분께 편지를 써서, 그분이 저의 것으로 알고 계셨던 그 가엾은 얼굴이 이제 제게서 완전히 사라졌으며, 한 번도 본 적 없는 한 여인에게 매인 그 속박에서 그분을 기꺼이 풀어드린다고 말해야 했더라면, 저는 얼마나 괴로웠을까요!"]], [["Oh, it was so much better as it was! With a great pang mercifully spared me, I could take back to my heart my childish prayer to be all he had so brightly shown himself; and there was nothing to be undone: no chain for me to break or for him to drag; and I could go, please God, my lowly way along the path of duty, and he could go his nobler way upon its broader road; and though we were apart upon the journey, I might aspire to meet him, unselfishly, innocently, better far than he had thought me when I found some favour in his eyes, at the journey’s end. Chesney Wold", "오, 이렇게 된 것이 얼마나 다행인지요! 자비롭게도 큰 고통 하나를 면한 채, 저는 그분이 그토록 환히 보여주신 그 모든 것이 되고 싶다는 어린아이 같은 기도를 다시 제 마음으로 거두어들일 수 있었습니다. 그리고 돌이킬 것이라곤 아무것도 없었습니다. 제가 끊어야 할 사슬도, 그분이 끌어야 할 사슬도 없었습니다. 그리하여 저는, 하느님께서 허락하신다면, 의무의 길을 따라 저의 낮은 걸음을 걸어갈 수 있고, 그분은 그분의 더 고귀한 길을 따라 그 더 넓은 길 위를 걸어가실 수 있었습니다. 비록 우리는 그 여정에서 서로 떨어져 있을지라도, 저는 그 여정의 끝에서 그분을 만나기를 바랄 수 있었습니다. 사심 없이, 순결하게, 그분이 제게서 어떤 호의를 발견하셨던 그때 저를 여기셨던 것보다 훨씬 더 나은 사람이 되어서요."]], [["Charley and I did not set off alone upon our expedition into Lincolnshire.", "찰리와 나는 링컨셔로 떠나는 여정에 단둘이서만 나선 게 아니었습니다."], ["My guardian had made up his mind not to lose sight of me until I was safe in Mr. Boythorn’s house, so he accompanied us, and we were two days upon the road.", "후견인은 내가 보이손 씨의 집에 무사히 도착할 때까지는 나에게서 눈을 떼지 않기로 마음을 굳혔던 터라 우리와 동행했고, 우리는 이틀 동안 길 위에 있었습니다."], ["I found every breath of air, and every scent, and every flower and leaf and blade of grass, and every passing cloud, and everything in nature, more beautiful and wonderful to me than I had ever found it yet.", "나는 한 줄기 바람도, 향기 하나하나도, 꽃과 잎과 풀잎 하나하나도, 흘러가는 구름 한 점도, 자연 속의 모든 것이 지금껏 느꼈던 그 어느 때보다 더 아름답고 경이롭게 느껴졌습니다."], ["This was my first gain from my illness.", "이것이 병을 앓고 얻은 나의 첫 수확이었습니다."], ["How little I had lost, when the wide world was so full of delight for me.", "넓은 세상이 나에게 이토록 기쁨으로 가득 차 있는데, 내가 잃은 것이 얼마나 사소한지요."]], [["My guardian intending to go back immediately, we appointed, on our way down, a day when my dear girl should come.", "후견인은 곧바로 돌아갈 작정이었으므로, 우리는 내려가는 길에 사랑하는 그 애가 와야 할 날을 정했습니다."], ["I wrote her a letter, of which he took charge, and he left us within half an hour of our arrival at our destination, on a delightful evening in the early summer-time.", "나는 그 애에게 편지를 한 통 썼고, 후견인이 그것을 맡았으며, 초여름의 어느 화창한 저녁, 목적지에 도착한 지 반 시간도 안 되어 그는 우리를 떠났습니다."]], [["If a good fairy had built the house for me with a wave of her wand, and I had been a princess and her favoured god-child, I could not have been more considered in it.", "어느 착한 요정이 지팡이를 한 번 휘둘러 나를 위해 그 집을 지어주었고 내가 공주이며 요정이 총애하는 대녀였다 한들, 그 집 안에서 이보다 더 극진한 배려를 받을 수는 없었을 것입니다."], ["So many preparations were made for me and such an endearing remembrance was shown of all my little tastes and likings that I could have sat down, overcome, a dozen times before I had revisited half the rooms.", "나를 위해 어찌나 많은 준비를 해두었던지, 그리고 나의 자질구레한 취향과 기호 하나하나를 어찌나 다정하게 기억해두었던지, 나는 방의 절반도 채 다시 둘러보기 전에 열두 번은 감격에 겨워 주저앉을 뻔했습니다."], ["I did better than that, however, by showing them all to Charley instead.", "하지만 그러는 대신 나는 그 방들을 죄다 찰리에게 보여주는 더 나은 길을 택했습니다."], ["Charley’s delight calmed mine; and after we had had a walk in the garden, and Charley had exhausted her whole vocabulary of admiring expressions, I was as tranquilly happy as I ought to have been.", "찰리가 기뻐하니 내 마음도 가라앉았고, 정원을 한 바퀴 거닐며 찰리가 감탄하는 표현이란 표현을 모조리 쏟아내고 나자, 나는 마땅히 그러해야 할 만큼 잔잔하게 행복해졌습니다."], ["It was a great comfort to be able to say to myself after tea, “Esther, my dear, I think you are quite sensible enough to sit down now and write a note of thanks to your host.”", "차를 마신 뒤 스스로에게 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것이 큰 위안이었습니다. \"에스더, 얘야, 이제 자리에 앉아 집주인에게 고맙다는 글을 쓸 만큼은 충분히 분별이 든 것 같구나.\""], ["He had left a note of welcome for me, as sunny as his own face, and had confided his bird to my care, which I knew to be his highest mark of confidence.", "그는 자기 얼굴만큼이나 환한 환영의 글을 나에게 남겨두었고, 자기 새를 내 손에 맡겼는데, 나는 그것이 그가 베풀 수 있는 가장 높은 신뢰의 표시임을 알았습니다."], ["Accordingly I wrote a little note to him in London, telling him how all his favourite plants and trees were looking, and how the most astonishing of birds had chirped the honours of the house to me in the most hospitable manner, and how, after singing on my shoulder, to the inconceivable rapture of my little maid, he was then at roost in the usual corner of his cage, but whether dreaming or no I could not report.", "그래서 나는 런던에 있는 그에게 짧은 글을 한 통 써서, 그가 아끼는 식물과 나무가 다들 어떤 모습인지, 더없이 놀라운 그 새가 어찌나 손님 대접 잘하는 태도로 나에게 짹짹거리며 이 집의 영예를 노래해주었는지, 또 어린 하녀가 도무지 형언할 수 없을 만큼 황홀해하는 가운데 내 어깨 위에서 노래를 부르고 나서는 새장 한구석 늘 앉던 자리에 자리를 잡았지만 꿈을 꾸는지 어떤지까지는 알릴 수가 없다고 적었습니다."], ["My note finished and sent off to the post, I made myself very busy in unpacking and arranging; and I sent Charley to bed in good time and told her I should want her no more that night.", "글을 마무리해 우체국으로 부치고 나서, 나는 짐을 풀고 정리하느라 무척 분주하게 움직였습니다. 그리고 찰리를 일찌감치 잠자리에 들도록 보내며 그날 밤은 더 이상 그 애가 필요하지 않을 거라고 일러두었습니다."]], [["For I had not yet looked in the glass and had never asked to have my own restored to me.", "나는 아직 거울을 들여다보지 않았고, 내 거울을 돌려달라고 청한 적도 없었기 때문입니다."], ["I knew this to be a weakness which must be overcome, but I had always said to myself that I would begin afresh when I got to where I now was.", "이것이 반드시 극복해야 할 약함임을 알면서도, 나는 지금 이곳에 도착하면 새로 시작하겠다고 늘 스스로에게 다짐해왔습니다."], ["Therefore I had wanted to be alone, and therefore I said, now alone, in my own room, “Esther, if you are to be happy, if you are to have any right to pray to be true-hearted, you must keep your word, my dear.”", "그래서 혼자 있고 싶었고, 그래서 이제 내 방에 홀로 남아 이렇게 말했습니다. \"에스더, 행복해지려거든, 진실한 마음을 갖게 해달라고 기도할 자격을 조금이라도 가지려거든, 약속을 지켜야 한단다, 얘야.\""], ["I was quite resolved to keep it, but I sat down for a little while first to reflect upon all my blessings.", "나는 그 약속을 꼭 지키리라 단단히 마음먹었지만, 우선은 잠깐 자리에 앉아 내가 받은 모든 축복을 곱씹어보았습니다."], ["And then I said my prayers and thought a little more.", "그러고 나서 기도를 올리고 조금 더 생각에 잠겼습니다."]], [["My hair had not been cut off, though it had been in danger more than once.", "내 머리카락은 잘리지 않았으니, 한두 번 위태로운 고비를 넘기긴 했지만 말입니다."], ["It was long and thick.", "머리카락은 길고 숱이 많았습니다."], ["I let it down, and shook it out, and went up to the glass upon the dressing-table.", "나는 그것을 풀어 내려 흔들어 헤치고는 화장대 위 거울 앞으로 다가갔습니다."], ["There was a little muslin curtain drawn across it.", "거울에는 얇은 모슬린 천 한 자락이 가려져 있었습니다."], ["I drew it back and stood for a moment looking through such a veil of my own hair that I could see nothing else.", "나는 그것을 젖히고 잠시 서서, 다른 것은 아무것도 보이지 않을 만큼 내 머리카락의 베일을 통해 들여다보았습니다."], ["Then I put my hair aside and looked at the reflection in the mirror, encouraged by seeing how placidly it looked at me.", "그러고 나서 머리카락을 옆으로 넘기고는, 거울이 어찌나 차분하게 나를 바라보는지에 용기를 얻어 비친 모습을 들여다보았습니다."], ["I was very much changed—oh, very, very much.", "나는 무척 많이 변해 있었습니다."], ["At first my face was so strange to me that I think I should have put my hands before it and started back but for the encouragement I have mentioned.", "아, 정말, 정말 많이요. 처음에는 내 얼굴이 어찌나 낯설던지, 앞서 말한 그 용기가 없었다면 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 뒷걸음질 쳤으리라 싶습니다."], ["Very soon it became more familiar, and then I knew the extent of the alteration in it better than I had done at first.", "이내 얼굴이 한결 익숙해졌고, 그제야 그 변화의 정도를 처음보다 더 또렷이 알게 되었습니다."], ["It was not like what I had expected, but I had expected nothing definite, and I dare say anything definite would have surprised me.", "내가 예상했던 것과는 달랐지만, 사실 나는 무엇 하나 뚜렷하게 예상한 것이 없었고, 어쩌면 뚜렷한 무엇이 있었더라면 그것대로 나를 놀라게 했을 것입니다."]], [["I had never been a beauty and had never thought myself one, but I had been very different from this.", "나는 한 번도 미인이었던 적이 없고 스스로 미인이라 여겨본 적도 없지만, 이런 모습과는 무척 달랐습니다."], ["It was all gone now.", "그것이 이제 죄다 사라진 것입니다."], ["Heaven was so good to me that I could let it go with a few not bitter tears and could stand there arranging my hair for the night quite thankfully.", "하늘이 나에게 어찌나 자비로우셨던지, 나는 쓰라리다 할 수 없는 눈물 몇 방울과 함께 그것을 떠나보낼 수 있었고, 그 자리에 서서 무척 감사한 마음으로 밤을 위해 머리를 매만질 수 있었습니다."]], [["One thing troubled me, and I considered it for a long time before I went to sleep.", "한 가지가 나를 괴롭혔고, 나는 잠들기 전 오랫동안 그것을 곰곰이 생각했습니다."], ["I had kept Mr. Woodcourt’s flowers.", "나는 우드코트 씨의 꽃을 간직해두었던 것입니다."], ["When they were withered I had dried them and put them in a book that I was fond of.", "그 꽃이 시들자 나는 말려서 내가 아끼는 책 갈피에 끼워두었습니다."], ["Nobody knew this, not even Ada.", "이것은 아무도 몰랐으니, 에이다조차 몰랐습니다."], ["I was doubtful whether I had a right to preserve what he had sent to one so different—whether it was generous towards him to do it.", "나는 그가 이토록 달라진 사람에게 보낸 것을 간직할 권리가 나에게 있는지, 그렇게 하는 것이 그에게 너그러운 일인지 의구심이 들었습니다. 나는 그에게 너그럽고 싶었습니다."], ["I wished to be generous to him, even in the secret depths of my heart, which he would never know, because I could have loved him—could have been devoted to him.", "그가 결코 알 리 없는 내 마음 깊은 은밀한 곳에서마저도요. 나는 그를 사랑할 수도 있었고, 그에게 헌신할 수도 있었기 때문입니다."], ["At last I came to the conclusion that I might keep them if I treasured them only as a remembrance of what was irrevocably past and gone, never to be looked back on any more, in any other light.", "마침내 나는 이런 결론에 이르렀습니다. 돌이킬 수 없이 지나가버려 다시는 다른 어떤 빛으로도 되돌아볼 일이 없는 것에 대한 추억으로만 소중히 간직한다면 그 꽃을 지녀도 되겠다고요."], ["I hope this may not seem trivial.", "이것이 하찮게 보이지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["I was very much in earnest.", "나는 무척 진지했습니다."]], [["I took care to be up early in the morning and to be before the glass when Charley came in on tiptoe.", "나는 아침 일찍 일어나, 찰리가 발끝으로 살그머니 들어올 때 거울 앞에 있도록 신경을 썼습니다."]], [["“Dear, dear, miss!”", "\"어머, 어머, 아가씨!\""], ["cried Charley, starting.", "찰리가 흠칫 놀라며 소리쳤습니다."], ["“Is that you?”", "\"그게 아가씨예요?\""]], [["“Yes, Charley,”", "\"그래, 찰리.\""], ["said I, quietly putting up my hair.", "나는 조용히 머리를 매만지며 대답했습니다."], ["“And I am very well indeed, and very happy.”", "\"그리고 나는 정말 건강하고, 정말 행복하단다.\""]], [["I saw it was a weight off Charley’s mind, but it was a greater weight off mine.", "나는 그것이 찰리의 마음에서 짐 하나를 덜어주었음을 알았지만, 내 마음에서는 더 큰 짐이 내려갔습니다."], ["I knew the worst now and was composed to it.", "이제 나는 최악을 알았고 거기에 마음을 가다듬었습니다."], ["I shall not conceal, as I go on, the weaknesses I could not quite conquer, but they always passed from me soon and the happier frame of mind stayed by me faithfully.", "앞으로 이야기를 이어가면서, 끝내 완전히 이겨내지 못한 약함들을 숨기지는 않겠습니다. 하지만 그것들은 언제나 곧 나에게서 사라졌고, 한결 행복한 마음가짐이 충실하게 내 곁에 머물러주었습니다."]], [["Wishing to be fully re-established in my strength and my good spirits before Ada came, I now laid down a little series of plans with Charley for being in the fresh air all day long.", "에이다가 오기 전에 기운과 명랑함을 온전히 되찾고 싶었던 나는, 이제 찰리와 함께 온종일 신선한 공기를 쐬며 지낼 자잘한 계획을 줄줄이 세웠습니다."], ["We were to be out before breakfast, and were to dine early, and were to be out again before and after dinner, and were to talk in the garden after tea, and were to go to rest betimes, and were to climb every hill and explore every road, lane, and field in the neighbourhood. As to restoratives and strengthening delicacies, Mr.", "우리는 아침 식사 전에 나가야 하고, 일찍 점심을 먹어야 하고, 점심 전후로 다시 나가야 하고, 차를 마신 뒤에는 정원에서 이야기를 나눠야 하고, 일찌감치 잠자리에 들어야 하며, 동네의 언덕이란 언덕은 죄다 올라야 하고 길과 오솔길과 들판이란 들판은 죄다 누벼야 했습니다."], ["Boythorn’s good housekeeper was for ever trotting about with something to eat or drink in her hand; I could not even be heard of as resting in the park but she would come trotting after me with a basket, her cheerful face shining with a lecture on the importance of frequent nourishment.", "보양식과 기운을 북돋우는 별미로 말하자면, 보이손 씨네 마음씨 좋은 가정부가 늘 손에 먹을 것이나 마실 것을 들고 종종걸음 치며 돌아다녔습니다. 내가 공원에서 쉬고 있다는 말만 들려도 그녀는 어김없이 바구니를 들고 종종걸음으로 쫓아왔고, 그 명랑한 얼굴에는 자주 영양을 보충하는 일이 얼마나 중요한지 늘어놓는 잔소리가 환하게 빛났습니다."], ["Then there was a pony expressly for my riding, a chubby pony with a short neck and a mane all over his eyes who could canter—when he would—so easily and quietly that he was a treasure.", "그리고 오로지 내가 타라고 마련해둔 조랑말이 한 마리 있었으니, 목이 짧고 갈기가 두 눈을 온통 덮은 통통한 조랑말로, 마음만 내키면 어찌나 수월하고 잔잔하게 가볍게 달리던지 그야말로 보물이었습니다."], ["In a very few days he would come to me in the paddock when I called him, and eat out of my hand, and follow me about.", "며칠 지나지 않아 그 녀석은 내가 부르면 방목장에서 나에게 다가와 내 손에서 받아먹고 나를 졸졸 따라다녔습니다."], ["We arrived at such a capital understanding that when he was jogging with me lazily, and rather obstinately, down some shady lane, if I patted his neck and said, “Stubbs, I am surprised you don’t canter when you know how much I like it; and I think you might oblige me, for you are only getting stupid and going to sleep,”", "우리는 더없이 훌륭하게 마음이 통해서, 녀석이 어느 그늘진 오솔길을 나를 태운 채 게으르고 다소 고집스럽게 터덜터덜 가고 있을 때, 내가 목을 토닥이며 \"스텁스, 내가 가볍게 달리는 걸 얼마나 좋아하는지 알면서 안 달리다니 놀랍구나. 좀 들어주면 좋으련만, 너는 점점 멍청해져서 졸기만 하니\""], ["he would give his head a comical shake or two and set off directly, while Charley would stand still and laugh with such enjoyment that her laughter was like music.", "하고 말하면, 녀석은 머리를 한두 번 익살스럽게 흔들고는 곧장 달려나갔고, 그러면 찰리는 가만히 서서 어찌나 신나게 웃던지 그 웃음소리가 음악 같았습니다."], ["I don’t know who had given Stubbs his name, but it seemed to belong to him as naturally as his rough coat.", "스텁스라는 이름을 누가 지어줬는지는 모르지만, 그 거친 털만큼이나 자연스럽게 녀석에게 어울리는 이름 같았습니다."], ["Once we put him in a little chaise and drove him triumphantly through the green lanes for five miles; but all at once, as we were extolling him to the skies, he seemed to take it ill that he should have been accompanied so far by the circle of tantalizing little gnats that had been hovering round and round his ears the whole way without appearing to advance an inch, and stopped to think about it.", "한번은 녀석을 작은 이륜마차에 매어 푸른 오솔길을 따라 오 마일을 의기양양하게 몰고 갔습니다. 그런데 우리가 녀석을 하늘 끝까지 치켜세우던 바로 그때, 가는 내내 한 치도 앞으로 나아가는 기색 없이 귓가를 빙글빙글 맴돌며 약을 올리던 그 작은 각다귀 떼를 여기까지 데려와야 했다는 게 영 못마땅한 듯, 녀석이 멈춰 서서 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하는 것이었습니다."], ["I suppose he came to the decision that it was not to be borne, for he steadily refused to move until I gave the reins to Charley and got out and walked, when he followed me with a sturdy sort of good humour, putting his head under my arm and rubbing his ear against my sleeve.", "도저히 참을 수 없다는 결론에 이른 모양인지, 녀석은 내가 찰리에게 고삐를 넘기고 내려서 걷기 시작할 때까지 한사코 움직이려 들지 않았습니다. 그러자 녀석은 듬직하게 좋은 기분으로 나를 따라오며, 내 팔 밑에 머리를 들이밀고 내 소매에 귀를 비벼댔습니다."], ["It was in vain for me to say, “Now, Stubbs, I feel quite sure from what I know of you that you will go on if I ride a little while,”", "\"자, 스텁스, 내가 너에 대해 아는 바로 미루어 내가 잠깐만 타면 네가 다시 갈 거라고 확신한단다\""], ["for the moment I left him, he stood stock still again.", "하고 말해봤자 소용없었으니, 내가 녀석에게서 떨어지는 순간 다시 못 박힌 듯 우뚝 서버렸기 때문입니다."], ["Consequently I was obliged to lead the way, as before; and in this order we returned home, to the great delight of the village.", "그래서 나는 별수 없이 아까처럼 앞장서야 했고, 이런 행렬로 마을 사람들이 무척 즐거워하는 가운데 집으로 돌아왔습니다."]], [["Charley and I had reason to call it the most friendly of villages, I am sure, for in a week’s time the people were so glad to see us go by, though ever so frequently in the course of a day, that there were faces of greeting in every cottage.", "찰리와 나는 그곳을 더없이 정다운 마을이라 부를 만했으니, 분명히 그랬습니다. 일주일이 지나자 사람들은 우리가 지나가는 걸 어찌나 반가워하던지, 하루에도 수없이 오갔는데도 집집마다 반기는 얼굴이 내다보였습니다."], ["I had known many of the grown people before and almost all the children, but now the very steeple began to wear a familiar and affectionate look.", "나는 어른들 가운데 많은 이를, 아이들은 거의 다 전부터 알고 지냈지만, 이제는 교회 첨탑마저 친숙하고 다정한 표정을 띠기 시작했습니다."], ["Among my new friends was an old old woman who lived in such a little thatched and whitewashed dwelling that when the outside shutter was turned up on its hinges, it shut up the whole house-front.", "새로 사귄 벗들 가운데에는 아주아주 나이 많은 노부인이 한 분 있었는데, 어찌나 자그마한 초가에 회칠한 집에 사는지 바깥 덧문을 돌쩌귀에 걸어 올리면 집 앞면 전체가 닫혀버릴 정도였습니다."], ["This old lady had a grandson who was a sailor, and I wrote a letter to him for her and drew at the top of it the chimney-corner in which she had brought him up and where his old stool yet occupied its old place.", "이 노부인에게는 뱃사람인 손자가 하나 있었는데, 나는 그녀를 대신해 손자에게 편지를 써주고 그 맨 위에 그녀가 손자를 키운 벽난로 구석을 그려 넣었습니다. 거기에는 손자의 옛 걸상이 여전히 옛 자리를 차지하고 있었지요."], ["This was considered by the whole village the most wonderful achievement in the world, but when an answer came back all the way from Plymouth, in which he mentioned that he was going to take the picture all the way to America, and from America would write again, I got all the credit that ought to have been given to the post-office and was invested with the merit of the whole system.", "온 마을은 이것을 세상에서 가장 놀라운 솜씨라 여겼는데, 머나먼 플리머스에서 답장이 와서 손자가 그 그림을 멀리 아메리카까지 가져갈 것이며 아메리카에서 다시 편지하겠다고 적은 것을 보고는, 마땅히 우체국에 돌아가야 할 공로가 죄다 나에게 돌아왔고, 나는 우편 제도 전체를 떠받든 공로까지 떠안게 되었습니다."]], [["Thus, what with being so much in the air, playing with so many children, gossiping with so many people, sitting on invitation in so many cottages, going on with Charley’s education, and writing long letters to Ada every day, I had scarcely any time to think about that little loss of mine and was almost always cheerful.", "이렇게 바깥 공기를 한껏 쐬고, 그토록 많은 아이와 놀고, 그토록 많은 사람과 수다를 떨고, 초대를 받아 그토록 많은 집에 앉아 있고, 찰리의 공부를 계속 봐주고, 날마다 에이다에게 긴 편지를 쓰다 보니, 그 자그마한 상실을 생각할 겨를이 거의 없었고 나는 거의 언제나 명랑했습니다."], ["If I did think of it at odd moments now and then, I had only to be busy and forget it.", "어쩌다 틈틈이 그것을 떠올리더라도, 그저 분주히 움직이면 잊혔습니다."], ["I felt it more than I had hoped I should once when a child said, “Mother, why is the lady not a pretty lady now like she used to be?”", "한번은 한 아이가 \"엄마, 저 아가씨는 왜 예전처럼 예쁜 아가씨가 아니에요?\" 하고 말했을 때 내가 바랐던 것보다 더 사무치게 그것을 느꼈습니다."], ["But when I found the child was not less fond of me, and drew its soft hand over my face with a kind of pitying protection in its touch, that soon set me up again.", "하지만 그 아이가 나를 조금도 덜 좋아하지 않는다는 걸, 그리고 그 손길에 가엾이 여기며 감싸주는 듯한 마음을 담아 보드라운 손으로 내 얼굴을 쓰다듬는다는 걸 알고는 곧 다시 기운을 차렸습니다."], ["There were many little occurrences which suggested to me, with great consolation, how natural it is to gentle hearts to be considerate and delicate towards any inferiority.", "어떤 열등함에 대해서든 사려 깊고 섬세하게 대하는 것이 다정한 마음에게 얼마나 자연스러운 일인지를, 큰 위로와 함께 일깨워준 자잘한 일이 많았습니다."], ["One of these particularly touched me.", "그 가운데 하나가 유독 나를 뭉클하게 했습니다."], ["I happened to stroll into the little church when a marriage was just concluded, and the young couple had to sign the register.", "어느 결혼식이 막 끝나고 젊은 신랑 신부가 혼인 등록부에 서명을 해야 할 때, 나는 마침 그 작은 교회 안으로 거닐어 들어갔습니다."]], [["The bridegroom, to whom the pen was handed first, made a rude cross for his mark; the bride, who came next, did the same.", "먼저 펜을 건네받은 신랑은 자기 표시로 서툰 십자가를 그렸고, 다음 차례인 신부도 똑같이 했습니다. 그런데 나는 지난번 그곳에 왔을 때 그 신부를 알았으니, 마을에서 가장 예쁜 처녀일 뿐 아니라 학교에서 꽤 두각을 나타낸 아이였던지라, 나도 모르게 다소 놀란 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았습니다."], ["Now, I had known the bride when I was last there, not only as the prettiest girl in the place, but as having quite distinguished herself in the school, and I could not help looking at her with some surprise.", "그녀는 옆으로 다가와, 진실한 사랑과 흠모의 눈물을 그 빛나는 눈에 가득 담은 채 나에게 속삭였습니다. \"저이는 더없이 착하고 좋은 사람이에요, 아가씨."], ["She came aside and whispered to me, while tears of honest love and admiration stood in her bright eyes, “He’s a dear good fellow, miss; but he can’t write yet—he’s going to learn of me—and I wouldn’t shame him for the world!”", "하지만 아직 글을 못 써요. 이제 저한테 배울 거예요."], ["Why, what had I to fear, I thought, when there was this nobility in the soul of a labouring man’s daughter!", "저는 무슨 일이 있어도 저이를 부끄럽게 하고 싶지 않아요!\" 노동자의 딸 영혼 속에 이런 고결함이 깃들어 있는데, 내가 무엇을 두려워하랴, 하고 나는 생각했습니다."]], [["The air blew as freshly and revivingly upon me as it had ever blown, and the healthy colour came into my new face as it had come into my old one.", "바람은 예나 다름없이 신선하고 생기를 북돋우며 나에게 불어왔고, 건강한 핏기가 옛 얼굴에 돌았던 것처럼 새 얼굴에도 돌았습니다."], ["Charley was wonderful to see, she was so radiant and so rosy; and we both enjoyed the whole day and slept soundly the whole night.", "찰리는 보기에 경이로울 만큼 환하고 발그레했습니다. 우리 둘은 하루를 온전히 즐겼고 밤새 곤히 잤습니다."]], [["There was a favourite spot of mine in the park-woods of Chesney Wold where a seat had been erected commanding a lovely view.", "체스니 월드의 공원 숲에는 내가 좋아하는 곳이 한 군데 있었는데, 사랑스러운 경치가 내려다보이는 자리에 벤치 하나가 놓여 있었습니다."], ["The wood had been cleared and opened to improve this point of sight, and the bright sunny landscape beyond was so beautiful that I rested there at least once every day.", "이 조망 지점을 살리려고 숲을 베어내 탁 트이게 해두었고, 그 너머로 펼쳐진 환하고 햇살 가득한 풍경이 어찌나 아름다운지 나는 하루에 적어도 한 번은 그곳에서 쉬었습니다."], ["A picturesque part of the Hall, called the Ghost’s Walk, was seen to advantage from this higher ground; and the startling name, and the old legend in the Dedlock family which I had heard from Mr.", "유령의 길이라 불리는, 그림처럼 아름다운 저택의 한 부분이 이 높은 곳에서 한눈에 잘 보였습니다."], ["Boythorn accounting for it, mingled with the view and gave it something of a mysterious interest in addition to its real charms.", "그 섬뜩한 이름과, 보이손 씨에게서 들은 그 유래를 설명하는 데드록 가문의 오랜 전설이 그 풍경과 뒤섞여, 실제의 매력에 더해 어딘가 신비로운 흥취를 자아냈습니다."], ["There was a bank here, too, which was a famous one for violets; and as it was a daily delight of Charley’s to gather wild flowers, she took as much to the spot as I did.", "이곳에는 또 제비꽃이 만발하기로 이름난 비탈이 하나 있었습니다. 찰리는 날마다 들꽃을 따는 것을 낙으로 삼았기에 나만큼이나 그 자리를 좋아했습니다."]], [["It would be idle to inquire now why I never went close to the house or never went inside it.", "내가 어째서 그 저택 가까이는 한 번도 가지 않았고 안으로는 한 번도 들어가지 않았는지를 이제 와 따져봐야 부질없는 일입니다."], ["The family were not there, I had heard on my arrival, and were not expected.", "도착했을 때 들으니 가족은 그곳에 없었고 올 예정도 없다고 했습니다."], ["I was far from being incurious or uninterested about the building; on the contrary, I often sat in this place wondering how the rooms ranged and whether any echo like a footstep really did resound at times, as the story said, upon the lonely Ghost’s Walk.", "나는 그 건물에 대해 호기심이나 관심이 없기는커녕, 오히려 이 자리에 앉아 방들이 어떻게 늘어서 있을지, 또 그 이야기처럼 정말로 이따금 발소리 같은 메아리가 그 쓸쓸한 유령의 길 위로 울려 퍼지는지 궁금해하곤 했습니다."], ["The indefinable feeling with which Lady Dedlock had impressed me may have had some influence in keeping me from the house even when she was absent.", "데드록 마님이 나에게 남긴 그 형언할 수 없는 느낌이, 그녀가 없을 때조차 나를 그 저택에서 멀어지게 한 데 어느 정도 영향을 주었는지도 모릅니다."], ["I am not sure.", "잘은 모르겠습니다."], ["Her face and figure were associated with it, naturally; but I cannot say that they repelled me from it, though something did.", "당연히 그녀의 얼굴과 자태가 그 저택과 결부되어 있었습니다. 하지만 그것이 나를 거기서 밀어냈다고는 할 수 없으니, 무언가가 밀어내긴 했지만요."], ["For whatever reason or no reason, I had never once gone near it, down to the day at which my story now arrives.", "무슨 이유에선지, 아니면 아무 이유도 없이, 나는 내 이야기가 이제 다다른 그날에 이르기까지 한 번도 그 저택 가까이 가지 않았습니다."]], [["I was resting at my favourite point after a long ramble, and Charley was gathering violets at a little distance from me.", "나는 오래 거닌 뒤 내가 좋아하는 그 자리에서 쉬고 있었고, 찰리는 나에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에서 제비꽃을 따고 있었습니다."], ["I had been looking at the Ghost’s Walk lying in a deep shade of masonry afar off and picturing to myself the female shape that was said to haunt it when I became aware of a figure approaching through the wood.", "나는 저 멀리 깊은 석조 그늘 속에 누운 유령의 길을 바라보며 그곳을 떠돈다는 그 여인의 모습을 마음속으로 그려보고 있었는데, 그때 숲을 지나 다가오는 한 형체를 알아차렸습니다."], ["The perspective was so long and so darkened by leaves, and the shadows of the branches on the ground made it so much more intricate to the eye, that at first I could not discern what figure it was.", "멀어지는 풍경이 어찌나 길고 나뭇잎으로 어둑하던지, 또 땅 위에 드리운 가지 그림자가 눈에 어찌나 어지럽게 얽혀 보이던지, 처음에는 그것이 어떤 형체인지 분간할 수가 없었습니다."], ["By little and little it revealed itself to be a woman’s—a lady’s—Lady Dedlock’s.", "조금씩 조금씩 그것은 여인의 형체임을 드러냈으니, 어느 귀부인의, 데드록 마님의 모습이었습니다."], ["She was alone and coming to where I sat with a much quicker step, I observed to my surprise, than was usual with her.", "그녀는 혼자였고, 내가 보기에 놀랍게도 평소의 그녀답지 않은 훨씬 빠른 걸음으로 내가 앉은 곳을 향해 다가왔습니다."]], [["I was fluttered by her being unexpectedly so near (she was almost within speaking distance before I knew her) and would have risen to continue my walk.", "뜻밖에 그녀가 이토록 가까이 와 있는 데 가슴이 두근거려(그녀를 알아보았을 때는 거의 말이 닿을 만한 거리였습니다) 나는 일어나 산책을 계속하려 했습니다. 하지만 그럴 수가 없었습니다."], ["But I could not. I was rendered motionless.", "나는 옴짝달싹할 수 없게 되었습니다."], ["Not so much by her hurried gesture of entreaty, not so much by her quick advance and outstretched hands, not so much by the great change in her manner and the absence of her haughty self-restraint, as by a something in her face that I had pined for and dreamed of when I was a little child, something I had never seen in any face, something I had never seen in hers before.", "애원하듯 다급한 그녀의 몸짓 때문도, 빠른 걸음과 내민 두 손 때문도, 태도의 큰 변화와 그 오만한 자제심이 사라진 것 때문도 아니었으니, 그보다는 어린아이 시절 내가 그토록 사무치게 그리워하고 꿈꾸었던 무언가가 그녀의 얼굴에 어려 있었기 때문이었습니다. 내가 그 어떤 얼굴에서도 본 적 없는 무언가, 그녀의 얼굴에서는 일찍이 본 적 없는 무언가였습니다."]], [["A dread and faintness fell upon me, and I called to Charley.", "두려움과 아찔함이 나를 덮쳤고, 나는 찰리를 불렀습니다."], ["Lady Dedlock stopped upon the instant and changed back almost to what I had known her.", "데드록 마님은 그 순간 멈춰 서서 내가 알던 그 모습으로 거의 되돌아갔습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson, I am afraid I have startled you,”", "\"서머슨 양, 내가 놀라게 한 모양이군요.\""], ["she said, now advancing slowly.", "그녀가 이제 천천히 다가오며 말했습니다."], ["“You can scarcely be strong yet.", "\"아직 기력이 온전치 않을 텐데요."], ["You have been very ill, I know.", "무척 앓았다고 들었어요."], ["I have been much concerned to hear it.”", "그 소식을 듣고 몹시 마음이 쓰였답니다.\""]], [["I could no more have removed my eyes from her pale face than I could have stirred from the bench on which I sat.", "내가 앉은 벤치에서 몸을 움직일 수 없었던 것만큼이나, 나는 그녀의 창백한 얼굴에서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었습니다."], ["She gave me her hand, and its deadly coldness, so at variance with the enforced composure of her features, deepened the fascination that overpowered me.", "그녀가 손을 내밀었는데, 억지로 가다듬은 표정과는 너무도 어긋나는 그 죽음 같은 차가움이, 나를 사로잡은 그 황홀한 두려움을 한층 깊게 만들었습니다."], ["I cannot say what was in my whirling thoughts.", "어지러이 소용돌이치던 내 머릿속에 무엇이 들어 있었는지 말로는 다 할 수 없습니다."]], [["“You are recovering again?”", "\"이제 다시 회복되어가나요?\""], ["she asked kindly.", "그녀가 다정하게 물었습니다."]], [["“I was quite well but a moment ago, Lady Dedlock.”", "\"방금 전까지만 해도 멀쩡했습니다, 데드록 마님.\""]], [["“Is this your young attendant?”", "\"이 아이가 시중드는 아이인가요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Will you send her on before and walk towards your house with me?”", "\"먼저 보내고 나와 함께 댁 쪽으로 걸어가겠어요?\""]], [["“Charley,”", "\"찰리.\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “take your flowers home, and I will follow you directly.”", "\"꽃을 집에 가져다 두렴. 곧 따라가마.\""]], [["Charley, with her best curtsy, blushingly tied on her bonnet and went her way.", "찰리는 더없이 공손하게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 발그레 얼굴을 붉히며 보닛 끈을 매고 제 갈 길을 갔습니다."], ["When she was gone, Lady Dedlock sat down on the seat beside me.", "그 애가 떠나자 데드록 마님은 내 곁 벤치에 앉았습니다."]], [["I cannot tell in any words what the state of my mind was when I saw in her hand my handkerchief with which I had covered the dead baby.", "죽은 아기를 덮어주었던 내 손수건이 그녀의 손에 들린 것을 보았을 때, 내 마음이 어떤 상태였는지는 어떤 말로도 이를 수 없습니다."]], [["I looked at her, but I could not see her, I could not hear her, I could not draw my breath.", "나는 그녀를 바라보았지만 그녀가 보이지 않았고, 그녀의 말이 들리지 않았으며, 숨을 쉴 수도 없었습니다."], ["The beating of my heart was so violent and wild that I felt as if my life were breaking from me.", "심장이 어찌나 격렬하고 사납게 뛰던지 내 목숨이 몸에서 떨어져 나가는 것만 같았습니다."], ["But when she caught me to her breast, kissed me, wept over me, compassionated me, and called me back to myself; when she fell down on her knees and cried to me, “Oh, my child, my child, I am your wicked and unhappy mother!", "하지만 그녀가 나를 가슴에 끌어안고, 입맞추고, 나를 굽어보며 울고, 나를 가엾이 여기며, 나를 제정신으로 불러냈을 때, 그녀가 무릎을 꿇고 나에게 부르짖었을 때, \"오, 내 아이, 내 아이, 내가 너의 사악하고 불행한 어미란다! 오, 부디 나를 용서해다오!\""], ["Oh, try to forgive me!”—when I saw her at my feet on the bare earth in her great agony of mind, I felt, through all my tumult of emotion, a burst of gratitude to the providence of God that I was so changed as that I never could disgrace her by any trace of likeness, as that nobody could ever now look at me and look at her and remotely think of any near tie between us.", "하고 외쳤을 때, 그녀가 그 큰 마음의 고통 속에 맨땅 위 내 발치에 엎드린 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 격동하는 온갖 감정 속에서도 하느님의 섭리에 대한 한 줄기 감사가 솟구치는 것을 느꼈습니다. 내가 이토록 변해버려서, 닮은 흔적으로 그녀를 욕보일 일이 결코 없으리라는 것, 이제는 누구도 나를 보고 그녀를 보며 우리 사이에 가까운 핏줄이 있으리라고는 어림조차 못 하리라는 것에 대한 감사였습니다."]], [["I raised my mother up, praying and beseeching her not to stoop before me in such affliction and humiliation.", "나는 어머니를 일으키며, 그토록 비통과 굴욕에 잠겨 내 앞에 엎드리지 말아달라고 기도하듯 간청했습니다."], ["I did so in broken, incoherent words, for besides the trouble I was in, it frightened me to see her at MY feet.", "끊기고 두서없는 말로 그렇게 했으니, 내가 겪던 괴로움에 더해, 어머니가 나의 발치에 엎드린 모습을 보는 것이 나를 두렵게 했기 때문입니다. 나는 어머니에게 말했습니다."], ["I told her—or I tried to tell her—that if it were for me, her child, under any circumstances to take upon me to forgive her, I did it, and had done it, many, many years.", "아니, 말하려 애썼습니다. 만약 그녀의 아이인 내가 어떤 상황에서든 그녀를 용서하는 일을 떠맡아야 한다면, 나는 그렇게 했고, 또 오래도록 그렇게 해왔노라고요."], ["I told her that my heart overflowed with love for her, that it was natural love which nothing in the past had changed or could change.", "나는 내 마음이 그녀를 향한 사랑으로 흘러넘친다고, 그것은 과거의 그 무엇도 바꾸지 않았고 바꿀 수도 없는 천성의 사랑이라고 말했습니다."], ["That it was not for me, then resting for the first time on my mother’s bosom, to take her to account for having given me life, but that my duty was to bless her and receive her, though the whole world turned from her, and that I only asked her leave to do it.", "그제야 처음으로 어머니의 품에 기댄 내가 나를 낳아주신 것을 두고 그녀를 책망할 일이 아니라, 온 세상이 그녀에게 등을 돌릴지라도 그녀를 축복하고 받아들이는 것이 나의 도리라고, 다만 그렇게 하도록 허락해주기만을 청한다고 말했습니다."], ["I held my mother in my embrace, and she held me in hers, and among the still woods in the silence of the summer day there seemed to be nothing but our two troubled minds that was not at peace.", "나는 어머니를 품에 안았고 어머니도 나를 품에 안았으니, 고요한 숲속, 여름날의 정적 속에서, 평화롭지 못한 것은 오직 마음을 졸이는 우리 두 사람뿐인 듯했습니다."]], [["“To bless and receive me,”", "\"나를 축복하고 받아들인다니,\""], ["groaned my mother, “it is far too late.", "어머니가 신음하듯 말했습니다. \"너무도 늦었구나."], ["I must travel my dark road alone, and it will lead me where it will.", "나는 내 어두운 길을 혼자 가야 하고, 그 길은 이끄는 대로 나를 데려갈 것이다."], ["From day to day, sometimes from hour to hour, I do not see the way before my guilty feet.", "하루하루, 때로는 시시각각, 나는 내 죄 많은 발 앞에 놓인 길이 보이지 않는다."], ["This is the earthly punishment I have brought upon myself.", "이것이 내가 스스로 불러온 이승의 벌이다."], ["I bear it, and I hide it.”", "나는 그것을 견디고, 또 숨긴다.\""]], [["Even in the thinking of her endurance, she drew her habitual air of proud indifference about her like a veil, though she soon cast it off again.", "자신의 인내를 생각하는 그 순간에조차, 어머니는 익숙한 오만한 무관심의 분위기를 베일처럼 다시 끌어다 둘렀지만, 이내 그것을 다시 벗어던졌습니다."]], [["“I must keep this secret, if by any means it can be kept, not wholly for myself.", "\"무슨 수로든 지킬 수 있다면 나는 이 비밀을 지켜야 한다. 온전히 나만을 위해서가 아니란다."], ["I have a husband, wretched and dishonouring creature that I am!”", "나에게는 남편이 있으니, 비참하고 명예를 더럽히는 내가 말이다!\""]], [["These words she uttered with a suppressed cry of despair, more terrible in its sound than any shriek.", "이 말을 어머니는 어떤 비명보다도 그 소리가 더 끔찍한, 짓눌린 절망의 외침으로 토해냈습니다."], ["Covering her face with her hands, she shrank down in my embrace as if she were unwilling that I should touch her; nor could I, by my utmost persuasions or by any endearments I could use, prevail upon her to rise.", "두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 마치 내가 자기 몸에 손대는 것을 꺼리듯 내 품 안으로 움츠러들었습니다. 나는 온갖 말로 설득하고 내가 쓸 수 있는 온갖 다정한 말을 써봐도 어머니를 일으켜 세울 수 없었습니다."], ["She said, no, no, no, she could only speak to me so; she must be proud and disdainful everywhere else; she would be humbled and ashamed there, in the only natural moments of her life.", "어머니는 안 된다고, 안 된다고, 안 된다고 했습니다. 자기는 나에게만 이렇게 말할 수 있고, 다른 모든 곳에서는 오만하고 도도해야 하며, 평생 단 한 번 자연스러운 이 순간, 이곳에서만 자신을 낮추고 부끄러워하겠다고요."]], [["My unhappy mother told me that in my illness she had been nearly frantic.", "불행한 어머니는, 내가 앓는 동안 거의 미칠 지경이었다고 말했습니다."], ["She had but then known that her child was living.", "그제야 비로소 자기 아이가 살아 있음을 알았던 것입니다."], ["She could not have suspected me to be that child before.", "그전에는 내가 그 아이일 줄 의심조차 할 수 없었습니다."], ["She had followed me down here to speak to me but once in all her life.", "어머니는 평생 단 한 번 나에게 말을 건네려고 이곳까지 나를 따라왔습니다."], ["We never could associate, never could communicate, never probably from that time forth could interchange another word on earth.", "우리는 결코 어울릴 수도, 연락을 주고받을 수도 없을 것이며, 아마 그때 이후로는 이 세상에서 다시는 단 한마디도 나눌 수 없으리라 했습니다."], ["She put into my hands a letter she had written for my reading only and said when I had read it and destroyed it—but not so much for her sake, since she asked nothing, as for her husband’s and my own—I must evermore consider her as dead.", "어머니는 오직 나만 읽으라고 쓴 편지 한 통을 내 손에 쥐여주며, 그것을 읽고 없애버린 뒤에는, 자기를 위해서라기보다는, 자기는 아무것도 청하지 않으니, 남편과 나 자신을 위해서, 앞으로 영원히 자기를 죽은 사람으로 여겨야 한다고 말했습니다."], ["If I could believe that she loved me, in this agony in which I saw her, with a mother’s love, she asked me to do that, for then I might think of her with a greater pity, imagining what she suffered.", "내가 본 그 고뇌 속에서 어머니가 어머니의 사랑으로 나를 사랑한다고 믿어줄 수 있다면, 부디 그렇게 해달라고 청했습니다. 그러면 어머니가 무엇을 견디는지 헤아리며 더 큰 연민으로 자기를 생각할 수 있을 테니까요."], ["She had put herself beyond all hope and beyond all help.", "어머니는 자신을 모든 희망 너머, 모든 도움 너머에 두었습니다."], ["Whether she preserved her secret until death or it came to be discovered and she brought dishonour and disgrace upon the name she had taken, it was her solitary struggle always; and no affection could come near her, and no human creature could render her any aid.", "죽을 때까지 비밀을 지키든, 아니면 그것이 발각되어 자기가 취한 이름에 불명예와 치욕을 가져오든, 그것은 언제나 어머니 홀로의 싸움이었습니다. 어떤 애정도 그녀에게 가까이 갈 수 없었고, 어떤 인간도 그녀에게 아무런 도움을 줄 수 없었습니다."]], [["“But is the secret safe so far?”", "\"하지만 그 비밀이 지금까지는 안전한가요?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었습니다."], ["“Is it safe now, dearest mother?”", "\"지금은 안전한가요, 사랑하는 어머니?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니다.\""], ["replied my mother.", "어머니가 대답했습니다."], ["“It has been very near discovery.", "\"발각될 뻔한 적이 있었단다."], ["It was saved by an accident.", "우연 덕에 모면했지."], ["It may be lost by another accident—to-morrow, any day.”", "또 다른 우연으로 들통날 수도 있다. 내일이라도, 언제라도.\""]], [["“Do you dread a particular person?”", "\"특별히 두려운 사람이 있으세요?\""]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Do not tremble and cry so much for me.", "나 때문에 그렇게 떨고 그렇게 울지 마라."], ["I am not worthy of these tears,”", "나는 그런 눈물을 받을 자격이 없단다.\""], ["said my mother, kissing my hands.", "어머니가 내 손에 입맞추며 말했습니다."], ["“I dread one person very much.”", "\"나는 한 사람을 몹시 두려워한단다.\""]], [["“An enemy?”", "\"적인가요?\""]], [["“Not a friend.", "\"벗은 아니다."], ["One who is too passionless to be either.", "둘 중 어느 쪽이라기엔 너무 무정한 사람이지."], ["He is Sir Leicester Dedlock’s lawyer, mechanically faithful without attachment, and very jealous of the profit, privilege, and reputation of being master of the mysteries of great houses.”", "그 사람은 레스터 데드록 경의 변호사로, 애정 없이 기계적으로 충직하며, 명문가의 비밀을 쥔 주인 노릇에서 오는 이익과 특권과 명성에 대해 무척 시샘이 많은 자란다.\""]], [["“Has he any suspicions?”", "\"의심하는 게 있나요?\""]], [["“Many.”", "\"많지.\""]], [["“Not of you?”", "\"어머니를요?\""], ["I said alarmed.", "내가 놀라 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes!", "\"그래!"], ["He is always vigilant and always near me.", "그자는 늘 빈틈없이 살피며 늘 내 곁에 있단다."], ["I may keep him at a standstill, but I can never shake him off.”", "멈춰 세워둘 수는 있어도, 결코 떨쳐낼 수는 없지.\""]], [["“Has he so little pity or compunction?”", "\"그자에게는 연민이나 거리낌이 그토록 없나요?\""]], [["“He has none, and no anger.", "\"전혀 없단다. 분노도 없지."], ["He is indifferent to everything but his calling.", "자기 직분 말고는 모든 것에 무관심한 자야."], ["His calling is the acquisition of secrets and the holding possession of such power as they give him, with no sharer or opponent in it.”", "그자의 직분이란 비밀을 캐내어, 그것이 안겨주는 권력을, 나눌 자도 맞설 자도 없이 거머쥐는 것이지.\""]], [["“Could you trust in him?”", "\"그자를 믿어볼 수는 없을까요?\""]], [["“I shall never try.", "\"결코 시도하지 않을 거다."], ["The dark road I have trodden for so many years will end where it will.", "내가 그토록 오랜 세월 밟아온 그 어두운 길은 이끄는 곳에서 끝나겠지."], ["I follow it alone to the end, whatever the end be.", "그 끝이 무엇이든 나는 끝까지 혼자 그 길을 간다."], ["It may be near, it may be distant; while the road lasts, nothing turns me.”", "가까울 수도, 멀 수도 있겠지. 길이 이어지는 한, 그 무엇도 나를 돌려세우지 못한다.\""]], [["“Dear mother, are you so resolved?”", "\"사랑하는 어머니, 그토록 마음을 굳히셨나요?\""]], [["“I AM resolved.", "\"굳혔단다."], ["I have long outbidden folly with folly, pride with pride, scorn with scorn, insolence with insolence, and have outlived many vanities with many more.", "나는 오랜 세월 어리석음을 어리석음으로, 오만을 오만으로, 멸시를 멸시로, 무례를 무례로 받아쳐왔고, 그보다 더 많은 허영으로 숱한 허영을 묻고 살아왔다."], ["I will outlive this danger, and outdie it, if I can.", "할 수만 있다면 이 위험도 견뎌 넘기고, 견뎌 죽어 넘기겠다."], ["It has closed around me almost as awfully as if these woods of Chesney Wold had closed around the house, but my course through it is the same.", "마치 이 체스니 월드의 숲이 저 저택을 에워싸듯, 그것이 거의 무시무시하게 나를 옭아왔지만, 그 속을 헤쳐가는 내 길은 한결같다."], ["I have but one; I can have but one.”", "나에게는 길이 하나뿐이고, 하나밖에 있을 수 없으니.\""]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce—”", "\"잔다이스 씨가—\""], ["I was beginning when my mother hurriedly inquired, “Does HE suspect?”", "내가 입을 떼려는데 어머니가 다급히 물었습니다. \"그 사람이 의심하느냐?\""]], [["“No,” said I.", "\"아니에요.\""], ["“No, indeed!", "내가 말했습니다."], ["Be assured that he does not!”", "\"아니요, 정말로요! 결코 의심하지 않는다고 안심하셔도 돼요!\""], ["And I told her what he had related to me as his knowledge of my story.", "그러고 나서 나는, 내 내력에 관해 그가 안다며 나에게 들려준 이야기를 어머니에게 전했습니다."], ["“But he is so good and sensible,”", "\"하지만 그분은 워낙 선량하고 분별 있는 분이라,\""], ["said I, “that perhaps if he knew—”", "내가 말했습니다. \"어쩌면 그분이 아신다면—\""]], [["My mother, who until this time had made no change in her position, raised her hand up to my lips and stopped me.", "그때까지 자세를 조금도 바꾸지 않았던 어머니가, 손을 들어 내 입술에 대고 나를 멈췄습니다."]], [["“Confide fully in him,”", "\"그 사람을 온전히 믿거라.\""], ["she said after a little while.", "잠시 뒤 어머니가 말했습니다. \"내가 기꺼이 허락하마."], ["“You have my free consent—a small gift from such a mother to her injured child!—but do not tell me of it.", "이런 어미가 상처 입힌 제 아이에게 주는 작은 선물이지! 하지만 그 일을 나에게는 말하지 마라."], ["Some pride is left in me even yet.”", "나에게도 아직은 얼마간 자존심이 남아 있단다.\""]], [["I explained, as nearly as I could then, or can recall now—for my agitation and distress throughout were so great that I scarcely understood myself, though every word that was uttered in the mother’s voice, so unfamiliar and so melancholy to me, which in my childhood I had never learned to love and recognize, had never been sung to sleep with, had never heard a blessing from, had never had a hope inspired by, made an enduring impression on my memory—I say I explained, or tried to do it, how I had only hoped that Mr.", "나는 그때 할 수 있는 한, 그리고 지금 떠올릴 수 있는 한 가까이 설명했습니다. 그 내내 나의 동요와 비통이 어찌나 컸던지 나 자신조차 거의 알아차리지 못했지만, 그토록 낯설고 그토록 구슬프던 어머니의 목소리로 발음된 한마디 한마디가, 어린 시절 나는 한 번도 사랑하고 알아보도록 배운 적 없고, 그 목소리에 잠들도록 노래를 들은 적도, 그 목소리에서 축복을 들은 적도, 그 목소리가 희망을 불어넣어준 적도 없던 그 목소리가, 내 기억에 오래도록 새겨졌습니다."], ["Jarndyce, who had been the best of fathers to me, might be able to afford some counsel and support to her.", "나는 설명했습니다. 아니, 설명하려 했습니다."], ["But my mother answered no, it was impossible; no one could help her.", "나에게 더없이 좋은 아버지가 되어주신 잔다이스 씨가 어머니에게 얼마간의 조언과 의지가 되어줄 수 있기를 바랐을 뿐이라고요. 하지만 어머니는 아니라고, 그건 불가능하다고, 아무도 자기를 도울 수 없다고 대답했습니다."], ["Through the desert that lay before her, she must go alone.", "자기 앞에 놓인 황야를, 어머니는 홀로 지나가야 한다고요."]], [["“My child, my child!” she said.", "\"내 아이, 내 아이!\""], ["“For the last time!", "어머니가 말했습니다."], ["These kisses for the last time!", "\"마지막으로! 이 입맞춤을 마지막으로!"], ["These arms upon my neck for the last time!", "내 목을 감은 이 두 팔도 마지막으로!"], ["We shall meet no more.", "우리는 다시 만나지 못한다."], ["To hope to do what I seek to do, I must be what I have been so long.", "내가 하려는 일을 이루기를 바란다면, 나는 그토록 오래 그래온 그대로의 모습이어야 한다."], ["Such is my reward and doom.", "그것이 나의 보답이요 운명이지."], ["If you hear of Lady Dedlock, brilliant, prosperous, and flattered, think of your wretched mother, conscience-stricken, underneath that mask!", "화려하고 번영하며 떠받들리는 데드록 마님 소식을 듣거든, 그 가면 아래 양심의 가책에 시달리는 너의 비참한 어미를 생각해다오!"], ["Think that the reality is in her suffering, in her useless remorse, in her murdering within her breast the only love and truth of which it is capable!", "진실은 어미의 고통 속에, 부질없는 회한 속에, 제 가슴이 품을 수 있는 단 하나의 사랑과 진실을 제 가슴속에서 죽이는 그 일 속에 있음을 생각해다오!"], ["And then forgive her if you can, and cry to heaven to forgive her, which it never can!”", "그러고는, 할 수 있거든 어미를 용서하고, 결코 용서할 수 없을 하늘을 향해 어미를 용서해달라고 부르짖어다오!\""]], [["We held one another for a little space yet, but she was so firm that she took my hands away, and put them back against my breast, and with a last kiss as she held them there, released them, and went from me into the wood.", "우리는 잠시 더 서로를 끌어안고 있었지만, 어머니는 어찌나 단호하던지 내 두 손을 떼어내 도로 내 가슴에 갖다 대고는, 거기 그 손을 쥔 채 마지막으로 입맞추며 놓아주고서, 나를 떠나 숲속으로 들어갔습니다."], ["I was alone, and calm and quiet below me in the sun and shade lay the old house, with its terraces and turrets, on which there had seemed to me to be such complete repose when I first saw it, but which now looked like the obdurate and unpitying watcher of my mother’s misery.", "나는 홀로 남았고, 햇빛과 그늘 속 저 아래에는 그 오랜 저택이 고요하고 잔잔하게 누워 있었습니다. 테라스와 작은 탑을 거느린 그 저택은, 내가 처음 보았을 때는 더없이 완전한 안식이 깃든 듯 보였건만, 이제는 어머니의 비참을 굽어보는 모질고 무자비한 감시자처럼 보였습니다."]], [["Stunned as I was, as weak and helpless at first as I had ever been in my sick chamber, the necessity of guarding against the danger of discovery, or even of the remotest suspicion, did me service.", "처음에는 멍하니 넋이 나가 병상에 누웠을 때만큼이나 약하고 속수무책이었지만, 발각의 위험을, 아니 가장 아득한 의심마저도 막아야 한다는 절박함이 나에게 도움이 되었습니다."], ["I took such precautions as I could to hide from Charley that I had been crying, and I constrained myself to think of every sacred obligation that there was upon me to be careful and collected.", "나는 내가 울었다는 것을 찰리에게 숨기려 할 수 있는 만큼 조심했고, 내가 신중하고 침착해야 할 온갖 거룩한 책무를 떠올리도록 스스로를 다잡았습니다."], ["It was not a little while before I could succeed or could even restrain bursts of grief, but after an hour or so I was better and felt that I might return.", "그렇게 해내기까지, 아니 북받치는 슬픔을 억누르기까지도 적잖은 시간이 걸렸지만, 한 시간쯤 지나자 한결 나아졌고 이제 돌아가도 되겠다 싶었습니다."], ["I went home very slowly and told Charley, whom I found at the gate looking for me, that I had been tempted to extend my walk after Lady Dedlock had left me and that I was over-tired and would lie down.", "나는 무척 천천히 집으로 갔고, 대문에서 나를 기다리던 찰리에게, 데드록 마님이 떠난 뒤 산책을 좀 더 늘리고 싶은 마음이 들었는데 너무 지쳐 좀 누워야겠다고 말했습니다."], ["Safe in my own room, I read the letter.", "내 방에 안전히 들어와서 나는 그 편지를 읽었습니다."], ["I clearly derived from it—and that was much then—that I had not been abandoned by my mother.", "그것에서 나는 분명히 알아냈으니, 그것이 그때로서는 큰 것이었습니다."], ["Her elder and only sister, the godmother of my childhood, discovering signs of life in me when I had been laid aside as dead, had in her stern sense of duty, with no desire or willingness that I should live, reared me in rigid secrecy and had never again beheld my mother’s face from within a few hours of my birth.", "내가 어머니에게 버림받은 것이 아니었다는 사실이었습니다. 내가 죽은 줄로 알고 한쪽에 치워두었을 때 내 몸에서 생명의 기색을 발견한 어머니의 손위이자 하나뿐인 언니, 내 어린 시절의 대모는, 내가 살기를 바라거나 그러길 원치도 않으면서, 엄격한 의무감에서 나를 철저한 비밀 속에 길렀고, 내가 태어난 지 몇 시간 만에 어머니의 얼굴을 다시는 보지 않았습니다."], ["So strangely did I hold my place in this world that until within a short time back I had never, to my own mother’s knowledge, breathed—had been buried—had never been endowed with life—had never borne a name.", "나는 이 세상에서 어찌나 기이하게 자리를 잡고 있었던지, 얼마 전까지도 나 자신의 어머니가 알기로는 한 번도 숨 쉰 적 없고, 묻혀버렸으며, 생명을 부여받은 적도, 이름을 가진 적도 없었던 것입니다."], ["When she had first seen me in the church she had been startled and had thought of what would have been like me if it had ever lived, and had lived on, but that was all then.", "어머니가 교회에서 나를 처음 보았을 때 흠칫 놀라며, 만약 그 아이가 살았더라면, 그리고 계속 살았더라면 나와 같았으리라고 생각했지만, 그때는 그것이 전부였습니다."]], [["What more the letter told me needs not to be repeated here.", "그 편지가 나에게 더 무엇을 말해주었는지는 여기서 되풀이할 필요가 없습니다."], ["It has its own times and places in my story.", "그것에는 내 이야기 속에서 제 나름의 때와 자리가 있습니다."]], [["My first care was to burn what my mother had written and to consume even its ashes.", "내가 가장 먼저 한 일은 어머니가 쓴 것을 태우고 그 재마저 없애버리는 것이었습니다."], ["I hope it may not appear very unnatural or bad in me that I then became heavily sorrowful to think I had ever been reared.", "그러고 나서 내가 이 세상에 길러진 것을 생각하며 마음이 무겁게 슬퍼졌다 한들, 그것이 내 안의 매우 부자연스럽거나 못된 일로 비치지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["That I felt as if I knew it would have been better and happier for many people if indeed I had never breathed.", "마치 내가 정말 한 번도 숨 쉬지 않았더라면 많은 사람에게 더 낫고 더 행복했으리라는 것을 아는 듯한 심정이었던 것을요."], ["That I had a terror of myself as the danger and the possible disgrace of my own mother and of a proud family name.", "나 자신을 두려워하여, 내가 나 자신의 어머니와 어느 자랑스러운 가문의 이름에 닥칠 위험이요 가능한 치욕이라 여겼던 것을요."], ["That I was so confused and shaken as to be possessed by a belief that it was right and had been intended that I should die in my birth, and that it was wrong and not intended that I should be then alive.", "내가 어찌나 혼란스럽고 흔들렸던지, 내가 태어나며 죽는 것이 옳고 또 그렇게 정해진 일이었으며, 그때 살아 있는 것은 그른 일이고 정해진 바가 아니었다는 믿음에 사로잡혔던 것을요."]], [["These are the real feelings that I had.", "이것이 내가 품었던 진짜 감정입니다."], ["I fell asleep worn out, and when I awoke I cried afresh to think that I was back in the world with my load of trouble for others.", "나는 기진하여 잠들었고, 깨어나서는 남들을 위한 근심의 짐을 진 채 이 세상에 돌아와 있다는 생각에 다시 울었습니다."], ["I was more than ever frightened of myself, thinking anew of her against whom I was a witness, of the owner of Chesney Wold, of the new and terrible meaning of the old words now moaning in my ear like a surge upon the shore, “Your mother, Esther, was your disgrace, and you are hers.", "나는 그 어느 때보다 나 자신이 두려웠으니, 내가 그에 맞서는 증인이 된 그녀를, 체스니 월드의 안주인을, 그리고 이제 바닷가에 밀려드는 파도처럼 내 귓가에 신음하는 그 오랜 말의 새롭고도 끔찍한 뜻을 새삼 떠올렸기 때문입니다. \"에스더, 네 어미는 너의 치욕이었고, 너는 그 어미의 치욕이다."], ["The time will come—and soon enough—when you will understand this better, and will feel it too, as no one save a woman can.”", "머지않아, 정말 머지않아 너는 이것을 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이며, 또한 여자만이 느낄 수 있는 방식으로 그것을 느끼게 될 것이다.\""], ["With them, those other words returned, “Pray daily that the sins of others be not visited upon your head.”", "그 말과 더불어, 그 다른 말도 되돌아왔습니다. \"남들의 죄가 네 머리 위에 떨어지지 않도록 날마다 기도하여라.\""], ["I could not disentangle all that was about me, and I felt as if the blame and the shame were all in me, and the visitation had come down.", "나는 나를 둘러싼 그 모든 것을 풀어 헤칠 수 없었고, 마치 그 비난과 수치가 죄다 내 안에 있고, 그 응징이 내려온 것만 같았습니다."]], [["The day waned into a gloomy evening, overcast and sad, and I still contended with the same distress.", "낮은 우중충하게 흐려 슬픈 저녁으로 기울었고, 나는 여전히 같은 고뇌와 씨름했습니다."], ["I went out alone, and after walking a little in the park, watching the dark shades falling on the trees and the fitful flight of the bats, which sometimes almost touched me, was attracted to the house for the first time.", "나는 홀로 밖으로 나가, 공원을 조금 거닐며 나무 위로 떨어지는 짙은 그늘과, 이따금 거의 나에게 닿을 듯 변덕스레 날아다니는 박쥐들을 바라보다가, 난생처음 그 저택에 이끌렸습니다."], ["Perhaps I might not have gone near it if I had been in a stronger frame of mind.", "마음이 좀 더 굳건했더라면 그 가까이 가지 않았을지도 모릅니다."], ["As it was, I took the path that led close by it.", "하지만 그때의 나는, 그 저택을 바짝 끼고 도는 오솔길로 접어들었습니다."]], [["I did not dare to linger or to look up, but I passed before the terrace garden with its fragrant odours, and its broad walks, and its well-kept beds and smooth turf; and I saw how beautiful and grave it was, and how the old stone balustrades and parapets, and wide flights of shallow steps, were seamed by time and weather; and how the trained moss and ivy grew about them, and around the old stone pedestal of the sun-dial; and I heard the fountain falling.", "나는 감히 머뭇거리거나 올려다보지 못했지만, 향긋한 내음과 널찍한 산책길과 잘 가꾼 화단과 매끄러운 잔디를 갖춘 테라스 정원 앞을 지나며, 그것이 얼마나 아름답고 장중한지, 그리고 오래된 돌난간과 흉벽과 얕은 계단의 넓은 층층대가 세월과 풍상에 어떻게 금이 가 있는지, 또 손질된 이끼와 담쟁이가 그 둘레와 해시계의 오래된 돌받침 둘레로 어떻게 자라 있는지를 보았습니다. 그리고 분수가 떨어지는 소리를 들었습니다."], ["Then the way went by long lines of dark windows diversified by turreted towers and porches of eccentric shapes, where old stone lions and grotesque monsters bristled outside dens of shadow and snarled at the evening gloom over the escutcheons they held in their grip.", "이윽고 길은 작은 탑을 인 망루와 별난 모양의 현관들로 다채로운, 어두운 창들이 길게 늘어선 곳을 지났으니, 그곳에는 오래된 돌사자와 기괴한 괴물들이 그늘진 굴 밖으로 곤두서서, 발톱에 움켜쥔 가문 문장 너머로 저녁 어스름을 향해 으르렁대고 있었습니다."], ["Thence the path wound underneath a gateway, and through a court-yard where the principal entrance was (I hurried quickly on), and by the stables where none but deep voices seemed to be, whether in the murmuring of the wind through the strong mass of ivy holding to a high red wall, or in the low complaining of the weathercock, or in the barking of the dogs, or in the slow striking of a clock.", "거기서 길은 한 대문 아래로 굽어들어, 정문이 있는 안마당을 지나(나는 얼른 서둘러 지나쳤습니다), 마구간 옆을 지났는데, 그곳에는 오직 묵직한 소리들만 깃든 듯했습니다. 높다란 붉은 담을 붙들고 우거진 담쟁이의 두꺼운 덩어리 사이로 바람이 속삭이는 소리에서든, 풍향계가 나직이 투덜대는 소리에서든, 개 짖는 소리에서든, 느릿느릿 울리는 시계 종소리에서든 말입니다."], ["So, encountering presently a sweet smell of limes, whose rustling I could hear, I turned with the turning of the path to the south front, and there above me were the balustrades of the Ghost’s Walk and one lighted window that might be my mother’s.", "그러다 이내 나뭇잎 바스락거리는 소리가 들리는 보리수의 달콤한 향내를 맞닥뜨리며, 나는 굽은 길을 따라 남쪽 정면으로 돌아섰고, 거기 내 위로 유령의 길 난간과, 어쩌면 어머니의 방일지도 모를 불 켜진 창 하나가 있었습니다."]], [["The way was paved here, like the terrace overhead, and my footsteps from being noiseless made an echoing sound upon the flags.", "이곳도 머리 위 테라스처럼 포석이 깔려 있어, 소리 없던 내 발걸음이 돌바닥 위에서 메아리치는 소리를 냈습니다."], ["Stopping to look at nothing, but seeing all I did see as I went, I was passing quickly on, and in a few moments should have passed the lighted window, when my echoing footsteps brought it suddenly into my mind that there was a dreadful truth in the legend of the Ghost’s Walk, that it was I who was to bring calamity upon the stately house and that my warning feet were haunting it even then.", "무엇 하나 멈춰 서서 바라보지 않으면서도 지나치며 보이는 것은 죄다 보면서, 나는 빠르게 지나가고 있었고 몇 발짝만 더 가면 불 켜진 창을 지나쳤을 터였습니다. 그때 메아리치는 내 발소리가 문득 마음속에 한 생각을 불러일으켰으니, 유령의 길 전설에는 무시무시한 진실이 깃들어 있다는 것, 이 위풍당당한 저택에 재앙을 불러올 자가 바로 나라는 것, 그리고 불길함을 알리는 내 발이 바로 그 순간 그곳을 떠돌고 있다는 것이었습니다."], ["Seized with an augmented terror of myself which turned me cold, I ran from myself and everything, retraced the way by which I had come, and never paused until I had gained the lodge-gate, and the park lay sullen and black behind me.", "나는 나 자신에 대한 한층 커진 두려움에 사로잡혀 온몸이 싸늘해졌고, 나 자신과 모든 것에서 달아나, 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔으며, 정문 수위실에 다다라 공원이 내 뒤로 시무룩하게 시커멓게 누울 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았습니다."]], [["Not before I was alone in my own room for the night and had again been dejected and unhappy there did I begin to know how wrong and thankless this state was.", "그 밤 내 방에 홀로 들어와 다시 거기서 풀이 죽고 불행해진 뒤에야, 비로소 나는 이런 마음 상태가 얼마나 그르고 배은망덕한지 깨닫기 시작했습니다."], ["But from my darling who was coming on the morrow, I found a joyful letter, full of such loving anticipation that I must have been of marble if it had not moved me; from my guardian, too, I found another letter, asking me to tell Dame Durden, if I should see that little woman anywhere, that they had moped most pitiably without her, that the housekeeping was going to rack and ruin, that nobody else could manage the keys, and that everybody in and about the house declared it was not the same house and was becoming rebellious for her return.", "그러나 이튿날 올 사랑하는 그 애에게서 기쁨에 찬 편지가 와 있었으니, 다정한 기대로 가득한 그 편지는 내가 대리석이 아닌 다음에야 마음이 움직일 수밖에 없는 것이었습니다. 후견인에게서도 또 한 통의 편지가 와 있었는데, 만약 어디서든 그 자그마한 사람을 보거든 더든 아주머니에게 전해달라며, 그녀가 없으니 다들 가엾을 만큼 풀이 죽었고, 살림은 엉망진창이 되어가고, 그 누구도 열쇠를 건사하지 못하며, 집 안팎의 모든 이가 이 집은 예전 그 집이 아니라고 입을 모으며 그녀가 돌아오기를 바라며 반란을 일으킬 지경이라고 적혀 있었습니다."], ["Two such letters together made me think how far beyond my deserts I was beloved and how happy I ought to be.", "이런 편지 두 통이 한꺼번에 오니, 내가 받을 자격을 한참 넘어 사랑받고 있다는 것과, 내가 얼마나 행복해야 마땅한지를 생각하게 되었습니다."], ["That made me think of all my past life; and that brought me, as it ought to have done before, into a better condition.", "그것은 나의 지난 삶을 죄다 떠올리게 했고, 마땅히 진작 그랬어야 하듯, 나를 한결 나은 상태로 이끌어주었습니다."]], [["For I saw very well that I could not have been intended to die, or I should never have lived; not to say should never have been reserved for such a happy life.", "나는 내가 죽도록 정해진 게 아니었음을 똑똑히 보았으니, 그랬다면 애초에 살지도 못했을 것이고, 더구나 이토록 행복한 삶을 위해 남겨지지도 않았을 테니까요."], ["I saw very well how many things had worked together for my welfare, and that if the sins of the fathers were sometimes visited upon the children, the phrase did not mean what I had in the morning feared it meant.", "얼마나 많은 일들이 함께 어우러져 나의 안녕을 위해 작용해왔는지를, 또 설령 아비들의 죄가 때로 자식에게 떨어진다 한들 그 말이 내가 아침에 두려워했던 그런 뜻은 아니라는 것을 똑똑히 보았습니다."], ["I knew I was as innocent of my birth as a queen of hers and that before my Heavenly Father I should not be punished for birth nor a queen rewarded for it.", "나는 내 출생에 대해 여왕이 자신의 출생에 결백한 것만큼이나 결백하며, 하늘에 계신 아버지 앞에서 출생을 두고 벌받지도, 여왕이 출생을 두고 상받지도 않으리라는 것을 알았습니다."], ["I had had experience, in the shock of that very day, that I could, even thus soon, find comforting reconcilements to the change that had fallen on me.", "나는 바로 그날의 충격 속에서, 이토록 빠르게도, 나에게 닥친 변화에 대해 위안이 되는 화해를 찾아낼 수 있다는 것을 경험했습니다."], ["I renewed my resolutions and prayed to be strengthened in them, pouring out my heart for myself and for my unhappy mother and feeling that the darkness of the morning was passing away.", "나는 결심을 새로이 다지며 그 결심 안에서 굳건해지기를 기도했고, 나 자신을 위해, 또 나의 불행한 어머니를 위해 마음을 쏟아내며, 아침의 어둠이 걷혀가고 있음을 느꼈습니다."], ["It was not upon my sleep; and when the next day’s light awoke me, it was gone.", "그 어둠은 잠 속까지는 따라오지 않았고, 이튿날의 빛이 나를 깨웠을 때 그것은 사라지고 없었습니다."]], [["My dear girl was to arrive at five o’clock in the afternoon.", "사랑하는 그 애는 오후 다섯 시에 도착할 예정이었습니다."], ["How to help myself through the intermediate time better than by taking a long walk along the road by which she was to come, I did not know; so Charley and I and Stubbs—Stubbs saddled, for we never drove him after the one great occasion—made a long expedition along that road and back.", "그 애가 올 길을 따라 멀리 산책을 다녀오는 것 말고는 그 사이의 시간을 어떻게 더 잘 보낼지 알 수 없었습니다. 그래서 찰리와 나와 스텁스가—그 한 번의 큰 일이 있은 뒤로는 다시 마차를 메우지 않았으므로 스텁스에는 안장을 얹어—그 길을 따라 멀리 다녀왔습니다."], ["On our return, we held a great review of the house and garden and saw that everything was in its prettiest condition, and had the bird out ready as an important part of the establishment.", "돌아와서 우리는 집과 정원을 거창하게 점검하며 모든 것이 더없이 예쁜 상태인지 살폈고, 이 살림살이의 중요한 일원으로 새도 꺼내 채비를 갖춰두었습니다."]], [["There were more than two full hours yet to elapse before she could come, and in that interval, which seemed a long one, I must confess I was nervously anxious about my altered looks.", "그 애가 올 때까지는 아직 꼬박 두 시간이 넘게 남아 있었는데, 길게만 느껴지던 그 사이에, 고백하건대 나는 달라진 내 모습 때문에 안절부절못하며 마음을 졸였습니다."], ["I loved my darling so well that I was more concerned for their effect on her than on any one.", "나는 사랑하는 그 애를 어찌나 끔찍이 사랑했던지, 내 모습이 누구보다도 그 애에게 어떤 영향을 줄지가 가장 마음 쓰였습니다."], ["I was not in this slight distress because I at all repined—I am quite certain I did not, that day—but, I thought, would she be wholly prepared?", "내가 이런 자잘한 괴로움에 빠진 것은 결코 한탄해서가 아니었으니—그날만큼은 정말 한탄하지 않았다고 확신합니다—다만 나는 생각했습니다."], ["When she first saw me, might she not be a little shocked and disappointed?", "그 애가 온전히 마음의 준비가 되어 있을까? 나를 처음 보았을 때, 좀 충격을 받고 실망하지 않을까?"], ["Might it not prove a little worse than she expected?", "예상보다 조금 더 나쁘게 드러나지 않을까?"], ["Might she not look for her old Esther and not find her?", "옛 에스더를 찾다가 못 찾는 게 아닐까?"], ["Might she not have to grow used to me and to begin all over again?", "나에게 다시 익숙해지느라 처음부터 모든 걸 새로 시작해야 하지 않을까?"]], [["I knew the various expressions of my sweet girl’s face so well, and it was such an honest face in its loveliness, that I was sure beforehand she could not hide that first look from me.", "나는 사랑스러운 그 애의 얼굴에 어리는 갖가지 표정을 너무도 잘 알았고, 그 사랑스러움 속의 얼굴이 어찌나 정직한지, 그 애가 그 첫 표정을 나에게 숨기지 못하리라는 것을 미리부터 확신했습니다. 그래서 나는 곰곰이 헤아려보았습니다."], ["And I considered whether, if it should signify any one of these meanings, which was so very likely, could I quite answer for myself?", "만약 그 표정이 이런 뜻들 가운데 어느 하나라도 담는다면, 그럴 가능성이 무척 높은데, 과연 내가 나 자신을 온전히 책임질 수 있을까?"]], [["Well, I thought I could.", "뭐, 할 수 있으리라 생각했습니다."], ["After last night, I thought I could.", "지난밤을 겪고 나니 할 수 있으리라 생각했습니다."], ["But to wait and wait, and expect and expect, and think and think, was such bad preparation that I resolved to go along the road again and meet her.", "하지만 기다리고 또 기다리고, 고대하고 또 고대하고, 생각하고 또 생각하는 것은 어찌나 나쁜 준비인지, 나는 그 길을 다시 따라가 그 애를 맞으러 가기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["So I said to Charley, “Charley, I will go by myself and walk along the road until she comes.”", "그래서 나는 찰리에게 말했습니다. \"찰리, 나 혼자 나가서 그 애가 올 때까지 길을 따라 걸을게.\""], ["Charley highly approving of anything that pleased me, I went and left her at home.", "찰리는 나를 기쁘게 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 크게 찬성했으므로, 나는 그 애를 집에 남겨두고 나섰습니다."]], [["But before I got to the second milestone, I had been in so many palpitations from seeing dust in the distance (though I knew it was not, and could not, be the coach yet) that I resolved to turn back and go home again.", "하지만 두 번째 이정표에 닿기도 전에, 나는 저 멀리 흙먼지가 이는 것을 보고(아직 마차일 리도 없고 그럴 수도 없다는 걸 알면서도) 어찌나 가슴이 두근거렸던지, 발길을 돌려 다시 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["And when I had turned, I was in such fear of the coach coming up behind me (though I still knew that it neither would, nor could, do any such thing) that I ran the greater part of the way to avoid being overtaken.", "그리고 발길을 돌리고 나니, 이번에는 마차가 내 뒤로 따라붙을까 봐 어찌나 두렵던지(그런 일이 일어나지도, 일어날 수도 없다는 걸 여전히 알면서도) 따라잡히지 않으려고 그 길의 거의 절반을 뛰었습니다."]], [["Then, I considered, when I had got safe back again, this was a nice thing to have done!", "그러고 나서, 무사히 다시 돌아와서 생각하니, 이거 참 잘한 짓이로구나 싶었습니다!"], ["Now I was hot and had made the worst of it instead of the best.", "이제 나는 몸이 후끈 달아올랐고, 최선이 아니라 최악으로 만들어버린 것이었습니다."]], [["At last, when I believed there was at least a quarter of an hour more yet, Charley all at once cried out to me as I was trembling in the garden, “Here she comes, miss!", "마침내, 적어도 십오 분은 더 남았으리라 여기고 있을 때, 정원에서 떨고 있던 나에게 찰리가 느닷없이 소리쳤습니다. \"저기 오세요, 아가씨!"], ["Here she is!”", "오셨어요!\""]], [["I did not mean to do it, but I ran upstairs into my room and hid myself behind the door.", "그럴 마음은 아니었는데, 나는 위층 내 방으로 뛰어 올라가 문 뒤에 숨었습니다."], ["There I stood trembling, even when I heard my darling calling as she came upstairs, “Esther, my dear, my love, where are you?", "거기서 나는, 사랑하는 그 애가 계단을 올라오며 \"에스더, 얘, 내 사랑, 어디 있어? 자그마한 사람아, 사랑하는 더든 아주머니!\""], ["Little woman, dear Dame Durden!”", "하고 부르는 소리가 들려올 때조차, 부들부들 떨며 서 있었습니다."]], [["She ran in, and was running out again when she saw me.", "그 애는 뛰어 들어왔다가, 나를 보고는 다시 뛰어나가려던 참이었습니다."], ["Ah, my angel girl!", "아, 나의 천사 같은 그 애!"], ["The old dear look, all love, all fondness, all affection.", "그 옛 다정한 눈빛, 온통 사랑이요, 온통 애틋함이요, 온통 정뿐인 눈빛."], ["Nothing else in it—no, nothing, nothing!", "그 밖에는 아무것도—아니, 아무것도, 아무것도 없었습니다!"]], [["Oh, how happy I was, down upon the floor, with my sweet beautiful girl down upon the floor too, holding my scarred face to her lovely cheek, bathing it with tears and kisses, rocking me to and fro like a child, calling me by every tender name that she could think of, and pressing me to her faithful heart.", "오, 내가 얼마나 행복했던지요. 마룻바닥에 주저앉아, 사랑스럽고 아름다운 그 애도 마룻바닥에 함께 주저앉아, 흉터 진 내 얼굴을 그 사랑스러운 뺨에 갖다 대고, 눈물과 입맞춤으로 그것을 적시고, 어린아이를 어르듯 나를 앞뒤로 흔들며, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 이름으로 나를 부르고, 그 변함없는 가슴에 나를 꼭 끌어안았습니다."]], [["Jarndyce and Jarndyce\nIf the secret I had to keep had been mine, I must have confided it to Ada before we had been long together.", "내가 지켜야 할 비밀이 내 것이었다면, 에이다와 함께 지낸 지 얼마 되지 않아 틀림없이 그녀에게 털어놓고 말았을 것입니다."], ["But it was not mine, and I did not feel that I had a right to tell it, even to my guardian, unless some great emergency arose.", "그러나 그건 내 것이 아니었고, 나는 어떤 중대한 위기가 닥치지 않는 한, 후견인에게조차 그 비밀을 말할 권리가 내게 있다고 느끼지 않았습니다. 혼자 짊어지기에는 무거운 짐이었습니다."], ["It was a weight to bear alone; still my present duty appeared to be plain, and blest in the attachment of my dear, I did not want an impulse and encouragement to do it.", "그래도 지금 내가 해야 할 도리는 분명해 보였고, 사랑하는 사람의 애정으로 복을 누리던 나에게는 그 일을 해낼 충동이나 격려가 따로 필요하지 않았습니다. 그녀가 잠들고 사방이 고요해질 때면 어머니에 대한 기억이 나를 깨워두고 밤을 슬픔으로 물들이곤 했지만, 다른 때에는 거기에 굴복하지 않았습니다."], ["Though often when she was asleep and all was quiet, the remembrance of my mother kept me waking and made the night sorrowful, I did not yield to it at another time; and Ada found me what I used to be—except, of course, in that particular of which I have said enough and which I have no intention of mentioning any more just now, if I can help it.", "그래서 에이다는 예전 그대로의 나를 보았습니다. 물론, 내가 이미 충분히 말했고 지금으로서는 가능한 한 더는 입에 올리지 않으려는 그 한 가지만은 빼고 말입니다."]], [["The difficulty that I felt in being quite composed that first evening when Ada asked me, over our work, if the family were at the house, and when I was obliged to answer yes, I believed so, for Lady Dedlock had spoken to me in the woods the day before yesterday, was great.", "에이다가 첫날 저녁 우리가 함께 바느질을 하다가, 그 집안 사람들이 저택에 와 있느냐고 물었을 때, 그리고 그저께 숲에서 데드록 마님이 내게 말을 걸었던 일이 있었기에 그런 것 같다, 그렇게 알고 있다고 대답할 수밖에 없었을 때, 침착함을 유지하기란 무척 힘들었습니다."], ["Greater still when Ada asked me what she had said, and when I replied that she had been kind and interested, and when Ada, while admitting her beauty and elegance, remarked upon her proud manner and her imperious chilling air.", "에이다가 마님이 뭐라고 했느냐고 물었을 때, 그리고 친절하고 관심을 보였다고 내가 대답했을 때, 또 에이다가 그 미모와 우아함은 인정하면서도 거만한 태도와 사람을 얼어붙게 하는 도도한 분위기를 두고 한마디 했을 때는 더더욱 힘들었습니다. 그러나 찰리가 자기도 모르게 나를 거들어 주었습니다."], ["But Charley helped me through, unconsciously, by telling us that Lady Dedlock had only stayed at the house two nights on her way from London to visit at some other great house in the next county and that she had left early on the morning after we had seen her at our view, as we called it.", "데드록 마님은 런던에서 이웃 주의 다른 큰 저택을 방문하러 가는 길에 이 집에서 겨우 이틀 밤만 묵었고, 우리가 이른바 우리의 전망대에서 마님을 본 그 이튿날 아침 일찍 떠났다고 일러준 것입니다. 찰리는 작은 주전자에도 큰 귀가 달렸다는 옛말을 톡톡히 증명해 주었습니다."], ["Charley verified the adage about little pitchers, I am sure, for she heard of more sayings and doings in a day than would have come to my ears in a month.", "내가 한 달 동안 귀에 담을 이야기보다 더 많은 말과 사연을 그 아이는 하루에 주워들었으니까요. * 작은 주전자에도 큰 귀가 달렸다: 아이들도 어른 못지않게 잘 듣고 알아챈다는 뜻의 영국 속담."]], [["We were to stay a month at Mr. Boythorn’s.", "우리는 보이손 씨 댁에서 한 달을 머물 예정이었습니다."], ["My pet had scarcely been there a bright week, as I recollect the time, when one evening after we had finished helping the gardener in watering his flowers, and just as the candles were lighted, Charley, appearing with a very important air behind Ada’s chair, beckoned me mysteriously out of the room.", "내 기억으로는, 사랑스러운 에이다가 그곳에서 화창한 한 주를 채 보내기도 전이었습니다. 어느 날 저녁, 정원사가 꽃에 물 주는 일을 거들고 막 촛불이 켜질 무렵, 찰리가 자못 중대한 표정으로 에이다의 의자 뒤에 나타나더니 알 수 없는 눈짓으로 나를 방 밖으로 불러냈습니다."]], [["“Oh! If you please, miss,”", "\"저, 아가씨, 괜찮으시면요.\""], ["said Charley in a whisper, with her eyes at their roundest and largest.", "찰리가 눈을 더없이 동그랗고 크게 뜬 채 소곤거렸습니다."], ["“You’re wanted at the Dedlock Arms.”", "\"데드록 암스에서 아가씨를 찾으세요.\""]], [["“Why, Charley,”", "\"어머, 찰리.\""], ["said I, “who can possibly want me at the public-house?”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그 술집에서 도대체 누가 나를 찾는다는 거니?\""]], [["“I don’t know, miss,” returned Charley, putting her head forward and folding her hands tight upon the band of her little apron, which she always did in the enjoyment of anything mysterious or confidential, “but it’s a gentleman, miss, and his compliments, and will you please to come without saying anything about it.”", "\"모르겠어요, 아가씨.\" 찰리가 고개를 앞으로 내밀고는 작은 앞치마 끈 위에 두 손을 꼭 모으며 대답했습니다. 무언가 비밀스럽거나 은밀한 일을 즐길 때면 늘 하는 동작이었습니다. \"그런데 어떤 신사분이세요, 아가씨. 안부를 전하면서, 아무한테도 말하지 마시고 좀 와 주십사 하셨어요.\""]], [["“Whose compliments, Charley?”", "\"누구의 안부라는 거니, 찰리?\""]], [["“His’n, miss,”", "\"그분의 거요, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Charley, whose grammatical education was advancing, but not very rapidly.", "찰리가 대답했습니다. 그 아이의 문법 실력은 늘고는 있었지만, 그리 빠르지는 않았습니다."]], [["“And how do you come to be the messenger, Charley?”", "\"그런데 어쩌다 네가 심부름꾼이 된 거니, 찰리?\""]], [["“I am not the messenger, if you please, miss,”", "\"제가 심부름꾼은 아니에요, 아가씨, 괜찮으시다면요.\""], ["returned my little maid.", "어린 하녀가 대답했습니다."], ["“It was W. Grubble, miss.”", "\"더블유 그러블 씨예요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“And who is W. Grubble, Charley?”", "\"더블유 그러블이 누구니, 찰리?\""]], [["“Mister Grubble, miss,”", "\"그러블 씨요, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Charley.", "찰리가 대답했습니다."], ["“Don’t you know, miss?", "\"모르세요, 아가씨?"], ["The Dedlock Arms, by W. Grubble,”", "데드록 암스, 더블유 그러블.\""], ["which Charley delivered as if she were slowly spelling out the sign.", "찰리는 마치 간판을 한 글자씩 천천히 읽는 것처럼 말했습니다."]], [["“Aye?", "\"아?"], ["The landlord, Charley?”", "그 집 주인 말이니, 찰리?\""]], [["“Yes, miss.", "\"네, 아가씨."], ["If you please, miss, his wife is a beautiful woman, but she broke her ankle, and it never joined.", "저, 괜찮으시면요, 아가씨, 그분 부인이 미인이신데요, 발목이 부러진 게 영영 안 붙었대요."], ["And her brother’s the sawyer that was put in the cage, miss, and they expect he’ll drink himself to death entirely on beer,”", "그리고 그 부인 오빠는 가둬 두는 우리에 들어갔던 톱장이인데요, 다들 그 사람이 맥주만 마시다가 아주 죽고 말 거라고 한대요.\""], ["said Charley.", "찰리가 말했습니다."]], [["Not knowing what might be the matter, and being easily apprehensive now, I thought it best to go to this place by myself.", "무슨 일인지 알 수 없는 데다 요즘은 쉽게 불안해지던 터라, 나는 혼자 그곳에 가는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다. 나는 찰리에게 보닛과 베일과 숄을 어서 가져오라 일렀고, 그것들을 걸친 뒤 비탈진 작은 거리를 따라 내려갔습니다."], ["I bade Charley be quick with my bonnet and veil and my shawl, and having put them on, went away down the little hilly street, where I was as much at home as in Mr. Boythorn’s garden.", "보이손 씨의 정원만큼이나 이제는 내 집처럼 익숙한 길이었습니다."]], [["Mr. Grubble was standing in his shirt-sleeves at the door of his very clean little tavern waiting for me.", "그러블 씨는 셔츠 바람으로 자기 가게의 더없이 깨끗한 작은 선술집 문 앞에 서서 나를 기다리고 있었습니다."], ["He lifted off his hat with both hands when he saw me coming, and carrying it so, as if it were an iron vessel (it looked as heavy), preceded me along the sanded passage to his best parlour, a neat carpeted room with more plants in it than were quite convenient, a coloured print of Queen Caroline, several shells, a good many tea-trays, two stuffed and dried fish in glass cases, and either a curious egg or a curious pumpkin (but I don’t know which, and I doubt if many people did) hanging from his ceiling.", "내가 오는 것을 보더니 두 손으로 모자를 벗어 들었는데, 마치 무쇠 그릇이라도 다루듯(실제로 그만큼 무거워 보였습니다) 그렇게 들고서, 모래를 뿌린 통로를 앞장서 걸어 가장 좋은 응접실로 안내했습니다. 카펫이 깔린 말끔한 방이었는데, 화초가 알맞다 싶은 정도보다 많이 놓여 있었고, 캐롤라인 왕비의 색판화 한 점, 조개껍데기 몇 개, 찻쟁반이 꽤 여러 개, 유리 상자에 든 박제하여 말린 물고기 두 마리, 그리고 천장에 매달린 기묘한 달걀인지 기묘한 호박인지 모를 것 하나가 있었습니다(어느 쪽인지 나는 모르겠고, 아는 사람도 별로 없었으리라 싶습니다)."], ["I knew Mr. Grubble very well by sight, from his often standing at his door.", "나는 그러블 씨가 자주 문 앞에 서 있던 덕분에 그의 얼굴을 잘 알고 있었습니다."], ["A pleasant-looking, stoutish, middle-aged man who never seemed to consider himself cozily dressed for his own fire-side without his hat and top-boots, but who never wore a coat except at church.", "보기 좋게 통통한, 사람 좋아 보이는 중년 사내였는데, 모자와 무릎까지 오는 장화 없이는 제 집 난롯가에서도 도무지 편히 차려입었다는 기분이 들지 않는 듯했지만, 교회에 갈 때 말고는 결코 외투를 걸치지 않는 사람이었습니다."]], [["He snuffed the candle, and backing away a little to see how it looked, backed out of the room—unexpectedly to me, for I was going to ask him by whom he had been sent.", "그는 촛불 심지를 다듬고는 그것이 어떻게 보이는지 살피려고 조금 물러섰다가, 그대로 방 밖으로 물러나 버렸습니다. 나로서는 뜻밖이었으니, 막 그에게 누가 보냈느냐고 물으려던 참이었기 때문입니다."], ["The door of the opposite parlour being then opened, I heard some voices, familiar in my ears I thought, which stopped.", "그때 맞은편 응접실 문이 열리며 어쩐지 귀에 익은 목소리들이 들리다가 뚝 멎었습니다."], ["A quick light step approached the room in which I was, and who should stand before me but Richard!", "빠르고 가벼운 발걸음이 내가 있는 방으로 다가오더니, 내 앞에 선 사람은 다름 아닌 리처드였습니다!"]], [["“My dear Esther!” he said.", "\"사랑하는 에스더!\""], ["“My best friend!”", "그가 말했습니다."], ["And he really was so warm-hearted and earnest that in the first surprise and pleasure of his brotherly greeting I could scarcely find breath to tell him that Ada was well.", "\"내 가장 좋은 벗!\" 그 형제 같은 인사가 어찌나 따뜻하고 진실하던지, 처음의 놀라움과 기쁨에 나는 에이다가 잘 지낸다는 말을 전할 숨조차 겨우 찾았습니다."]], [["“Answering my very thoughts—always the same dear girl!”", "\"내가 생각하던 바로 그걸 답해 주는구나. 언제나 변함없는 그 사랑스러운 아가씨야!\""], ["said Richard, leading me to a chair and seating himself beside me.", "리처드가 나를 의자로 이끌어 앉히고 제 옆에 앉으며 말했습니다."]], [["I put my veil up, but not quite.", "나는 베일을 올렸지만, 다 올리지는 않았습니다."]], [["“Always the same dear girl!”", "\"언제나 변함없는 그 사랑스러운 아가씨!\""], ["said Richard just as heartily as before.", "리처드가 아까처럼 진심을 담아 말했습니다."]], [["I put up my veil altogether, and laying my hand on Richard’s sleeve and looking in his face, told him how much I thanked him for his kind welcome and how greatly I rejoiced to see him, the more so because of the determination I had made in my illness, which I now conveyed to him.", "나는 베일을 완전히 올린 뒤, 리처드의 소매에 손을 얹고 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며, 따뜻하게 맞아 줘서 얼마나 고마운지, 그를 보게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지 말했습니다. 앓는 동안 내가 굳혔던 결심 때문에 더욱 그러했고, 나는 이제 그 결심을 그에게 전했습니다."]], [["“My love,”", "\"에스더.\" 리처드가 말했습니다."], ["said Richard, “there is no one with whom I have a greater wish to talk than you, for I want you to understand me.”", "\"내가 너보다 더 이야기를 나누고 싶은 사람은 없어. 네가 나를 이해해 주었으면 하니까.\""]], [["“And I want you, Richard,”", "\"그리고 나는, 리처드.\""], ["said I, shaking my head, “to understand some one else.”", "내가 고개를 가로저으며 말했습니다. \"네가 다른 어떤 분을 이해해 주었으면 해.\""]], [["“Since you refer so immediately to John Jarndyce,”", "\"네가 그렇게 곧장 존 잔다이스 이야기를 꺼내니,\""], ["said Richard, “—I suppose you mean him?”", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"그분을 말하는 거겠지?\""]], [["“Of course I do.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["“Then I may say at once that I am glad of it, because it is on that subject that I am anxious to be understood.", "\"그렇다면 곧바로 말하지만, 나는 그게 다행이라고 생각해. 바로 그 문제에서 내가 이해받기를 간절히 바라거든."], ["By you, mind—you, my dear! I am not accountable to Mr.", "명심해, 다른 누구도 아닌 너에게 말이야, 에스더!"], ["Jarndyce or Mr. Anybody.”", "나는 잔다이스 씨에게도, 그 누구에게도 해명할 책임이 없어.\""]], [["I was pained to find him taking this tone, and he observed it.", "그가 이런 어조를 쓰는 것을 보니 마음이 아팠고, 그도 그것을 알아챘습니다."]], [["“Well, well, my dear,”", "\"자, 자, 에스더.\""], ["said Richard, “we won’t go into that now.", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"그 이야기는 지금 들어가지 말자."], ["I want to appear quietly in your country-house here, with you under my arm, and give my charming cousin a surprise.", "나는 너를 팔짱에 끼고 여기 네 시골집에 조용히 나타나서, 내 사랑스러운 사촌을 놀라게 해 주고 싶어."], ["I suppose your loyalty to John Jarndyce will allow that?”", "존 잔다이스에 대한 너의 충성심도 그 정도는 허락하겠지?\""]], [["“My dear Richard,” I returned, “you know you would be heartily welcome at his house—your home, if you will but consider it so; and you are as heartily welcome here!”", "\"리처드.\" 내가 대답했습니다. \"그분 댁에서 네가 진심으로 환영받으리라는 걸 너도 알잖아. 네가 그렇게 여겨 주기만 한다면 그곳이 바로 네 집이고. 그리고 여기서도 너는 진심으로 환영받아!\""]], [["“Spoken like the best of little women!”", "\"세상에서 가장 좋은 꼬마 아주머니다운 말이로구나!\""], ["cried Richard gaily.", "리처드가 명랑하게 외쳤습니다."]], [["I asked him how he liked his profession.", "나는 그에게 직업이 마음에 드느냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Oh, I like it well enough!”", "\"아, 그럭저럭 마음에 들어!\""], ["said Richard. “It’s all right.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["It does as well as anything else, for a time.", "\"괜찮아. 한동안은 다른 어떤 일 못지않게 잘 맞고."], ["I don’t know that I shall care about it when I come to be settled, but I can sell out then and—however, never mind all that botheration at present.”", "자리를 잡고 나면 거기에 마음을 쓰게 될지는 모르겠지만, 그때 가서 자리를 팔고 나오면 되니까. 어쨌든, 지금은 그런 골치 아픈 이야기는 다 접어 두자.\""]], [["So young and handsome, and in all respects so perfectly the opposite of Miss Flite!", "그토록 젊고 잘생기고, 모든 면에서 플라이트 양과는 완벽히 정반대인데!"], ["And yet, in the clouded, eager, seeking look that passed over him, so dreadfully like her!", "그런데도 그를 스쳐 지나가는 그 흐릿하고 조급하고 무언가를 좇는 듯한 눈빛만은, 끔찍하게도 그녀를 빼닮았습니다!"]], [["“I am in town on leave just now,”", "\"나는 지금 휴가를 받아 시내에 와 있어.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"응."], ["I have run over to look after my—my Chancery interests before the long vacation,”", "긴 휴정기에 앞서 내, 내 대법원 사건을 좀 살피려고 잠깐 건너왔어.\""], ["said Richard, forcing a careless laugh.", "리처드가 애써 무심한 웃음을 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“We are beginning to spin along with that old suit at last, I promise you.”", "\"정말이지, 우리도 드디어 그 해묵은 소송에 속도를 내기 시작했거든.\""]], [["No wonder that I shook my head!", "내가 고개를 가로저은 것도 무리는 아니었습니다!"]], [["“As you say, it’s not a pleasant subject.”", "\"네 말마따나, 그게 유쾌한 화제는 아니지.\""], ["Richard spoke with the same shade crossing his face as before.", "리처드가 아까와 같은 그늘을 얼굴에 드리우며 말했습니다."], ["“Let it go to the four winds for to-night.", "\"오늘 밤만은 사방으로 날려 보내자. 후!"], ["Puff! Gone!", "사라졌다!"], ["Who do you suppose is with me?”", "누가 나랑 같이 왔게?\""]], [["“Was it Mr. Skimpole’s voice I heard?”", "\"내가 들은 게 스킴폴 씨 목소리였니?\""]], [["“That’s the man!", "\"바로 그 사람이야!"], ["He does me more good than anybody.", "그분만큼 나한테 도움이 되는 사람도 없어."], ["What a fascinating child it is!”", "어찌나 매혹적인 아이 같은지!\""]], [["I asked Richard if any one knew of their coming down together.", "나는 리처드에게 두 사람이 함께 내려온 걸 누가 아느냐고 물었습니다."], ["He answered, no, nobody.", "그는 아니, 아무도 모른다고 대답했습니다."], ["He had been to call upon the dear old infant—so he called Mr. Skimpole—and the dear old infant had told him where we were, and he had told the dear old infant he was bent on coming to see us, and the dear old infant had directly wanted to come too; and so he had brought him.", "그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기—그는 스킴폴 씨를 그렇게 불렀습니다—를 찾아갔더니 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기가 우리가 어디 있는지 일러 주었고, 자기는 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기에게 우리를 보러 갈 작정이라고 말했더니, 그 사랑스러운 늙은 아기가 곧장 자기도 따라가고 싶어 해서, 그래서 데려왔다는 것이었습니다."], ["“And he is worth—not to say his sordid expenses—but thrice his weight in gold,”", "\"그분은 충분히 그럴 만한 값어치가 있어. 그분이 쓰는 푼돈 같은 비용은 말할 것도 없고, 제 몸무게의 세 곱절만큼 금값을 해.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“He is such a cheery fellow.", "\"그렇게 유쾌한 사람이라니까."], ["No worldliness about him.", "속물근성이라곤 없어."], ["Fresh and green-hearted!”", "싱그럽고 풋풋한 마음을 가졌지!\""]], [["I certainly did not see the proof of Mr. Skimpole’s worldliness in his having his expenses paid by Richard, but I made no remark about that.", "스킴폴 씨가 리처드에게 비용을 대게 한다는 데서 그의 속물근성 없음이 증명된다고는 도무지 보이지 않았지만, 나는 그에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다."], ["Indeed, he came in and turned our conversation.", "마침 그가 들어와 우리 대화를 다른 데로 돌렸으니까요."], ["He was charmed to see me, said he had been shedding delicious tears of joy and sympathy at intervals for six weeks on my account, had never been so happy as in hearing of my progress, began to understand the mixture of good and evil in the world now, felt that he appreciated health the more when somebody else was ill, didn’t know but what it might be in the scheme of things that A should squint to make B happier in looking straight or that C should carry a wooden leg to make D better satisfied with his flesh and blood in a silk stocking.", "그는 나를 보고 더없이 반가워하며, 나 때문에 여섯 주 동안 틈틈이 기쁨과 공감의 달콤한 눈물을 흘렸노라고, 내 회복 소식을 들었을 때만큼 행복한 적이 없었노라고 말했습니다. 그리고 이제 세상에 선과 악이 뒤섞여 있는 까닭을 알 것 같다고, 누군가 아플 때 건강을 더 고맙게 여기게 된다고 느낀다고, 어쩌면 갑이 사팔뜨기가 되어야 을이 똑바로 보는 제 눈을 더 행복하게 여기게 되고, 병이 나무다리를 짚어야 정이 비단 양말 속의 제 살과 피에 더 만족하게 되는 것이 만물의 섭리인지도 모르겠다고 했습니다."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson, here is our friend Richard,” said Mr.", "\"서머슨 양, 여기 우리 친구 리처드를 보세요.\""], ["Skimpole, “full of the brightest visions of the future, which he evokes out of the darkness of Chancery.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"미래에 대한 더없이 밝은 환상으로 가득 차서, 그걸 대법원의 어둠 속에서 불러내고 있지요."], ["Now that’s delightful, that’s inspiriting, that’s full of poetry!", "자, 이거야말로 유쾌하고, 가슴 뛰고, 시정이 넘치는 일 아닙니까!"], ["In old times the woods and solitudes were made joyous to the shepherd by the imaginary piping and dancing of Pan and the nymphs.", "옛날에는 판과 님프들이 상상 속에서 피리를 불고 춤을 추어 숲과 외딴 들판을 양치기에게 즐거운 곳으로 만들어 주었지요. 우리의 이 목가적인 양치기 리처드는, 법정 판결의 가락에 맞추어 운명의 여신과 그 시종들을 법학원의 따분한 복도 사이로 뛰놀게 함으로써 그 우중충한 곳을 환하게 밝히고 있습니다."], ["This present shepherd, our pastoral Richard, brightens the dull Inns of Court by making Fortune and her train sport through them to the melodious notes of a judgment from the bench.", "정말 즐거운 일이지요! 성미 고약한 어떤 투덜이가 나에게 이렇게 말할 수도 있겠지요."], ["That’s very pleasant, you know!", "'이런 법률과 형평법의 폐단이 무슨 소용이오?"], ["Some ill-conditioned growling fellow may say to me, ‘What’s the use of these legal and equitable abuses?", "그걸 어떻게 옹호하겠소?' 그러면 나는 이렇게 답합니다."], ["How do you defend them?’ I reply, ‘My growling friend, I DON’T defend them, but they are very agreeable to me.", "'투덜이 친구, 나는 그걸 옹호하지 않소. 다만 나에게는 그게 무척 흐뭇하단 말이오."], ["There is a shepherd—youth, a friend of mine, who transmutes them into something highly fascinating to my simplicity.", "내 친구인 어느 양치기 청년이 그걸 내 단순한 마음에 더없이 매혹적인 무언가로 바꿔 주니까. 그것이 존재하는 까닭이 이것이라고는 말하지 않겠소."], ["I don’t say it is for this that they exist—for I am a child among you worldly grumblers, and not called upon to account to you or myself for anything—but it may be so.’”", "나는 당신네 속세의 투덜이들 사이에 끼인 어린아이일 뿐이고, 당신에게나 나 자신에게나 무엇 하나 해명할 의무가 없으니 말이오. 하지만 그럴지도 모르지 않소.'\""]], [["I began seriously to think that Richard could scarcely have found a worse friend than this.", "나는 리처드가 이보다 더 나쁜 친구를 만나기란 어려웠으리라고 진지하게 생각하기 시작했습니다."], ["It made me uneasy that at such a time when he most required some right principle and purpose he should have this captivating looseness and putting-off of everything, this airy dispensing with all principle and purpose, at his elbow.", "바른 원칙과 목적이 그에게 가장 절실한 바로 이런 때에, 매사를 흘려 보내고 미뤄 버리는 이 매혹적인 헐거움, 모든 원칙과 목적을 가뿐히 면제해 버리는 이 가벼움이 그의 곁에 있다는 것이 나를 불안하게 했습니다."], ["I thought I could understand how such a nature as my guardian’s, experienced in the world and forced to contemplate the miserable evasions and contentions of the family misfortune, found an immense relief in Mr.", "세상 물정을 겪을 만큼 겪었고 집안의 불운이 빚어낸 비참한 회피와 다툼을 늘 들여다볼 수밖에 없는 내 후견인 같은 천성이라면, 스킴폴 씨가 제 약점을 스스럼없이 고백하고 천진한 솔직함을 내보이는 데서 엄청난 위안을 얻는다는 것은 나도 이해할 수 있을 것 같았습니다."], ["Skimpole’s avowal of his weaknesses and display of guileless candour; but I could not satisfy myself that it was as artless as it seemed or that it did not serve Mr. Skimpole’s idle turn quite as well as any other part, and with less trouble.", "그러나 그것이 보이는 만큼 정말로 꾸밈이 없는 것인지, 혹은 그것이 다른 어떤 역할 못지않게, 그것도 더 적은 수고로, 스킴폴 씨의 안일한 목적에 들어맞는 것은 아닌지, 나는 도무지 확신할 수 없었습니다."]], [["They both walked back with me, and Mr. Skimpole leaving us at the gate, I walked softly in with Richard and said, “Ada, my love, I have brought a gentleman to visit you.”", "두 사람 다 나와 함께 걸어서 돌아왔고, 스킴폴 씨가 대문에서 우리와 헤어진 뒤, 나는 리처드와 함께 조용히 들어가 말했습니다."], ["It was not difficult to read the blushing, startled face.", "\"에이다, 손님 한 분을 모셔 왔어.\" 붉어진 채 깜짝 놀란 그 얼굴을 읽기란 어렵지 않았습니다."], ["She loved him dearly, and he knew it, and I knew it.", "그녀는 그를 깊이 사랑했고, 그도 그것을 알았으며, 나도 알았습니다."], ["It was a very transparent business, that meeting as cousins only.", "그저 사촌으로서의 만남이라니, 참으로 속이 빤히 들여다보이는 일이었습니다."]], [["I almost mistrusted myself as growing quite wicked in my suspicions, but I was not so sure that Richard loved her dearly.", "나는 내 의심이 어지간히 고약해지고 있는 게 아닌가 하여 나 자신마저 미덥지 않을 지경이었지만, 리처드가 그녀를 깊이 사랑한다는 것만은 그리 확신할 수 없었습니다."], ["He admired her very much—any one must have done that—and I dare say would have renewed their youthful engagement with great pride and ardour but that he knew how she would respect her promise to my guardian.", "그가 그녀를 무척 흠모한다는 것은—누구라도 그러지 않을 수 없었을 테니까요—분명했고, 후견인에게 한 약속을 그녀가 어떻게 지킬지 그가 알지 못했더라면 큰 자부심과 열정으로 어린 시절의 약혼을 다시 맺었으리라는 것도 분명했습니다."], ["Still I had a tormenting idea that the influence upon him extended even here, that he was postponing his best truth and earnestness in this as in all things until Jarndyce and Jarndyce should be off his mind.", "그래도 나에게는 그를 사로잡은 그 영향력이 여기까지 미쳤다는 괴로운 생각이 들었습니다. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 그의 마음에서 떨쳐지기 전까지는, 그가 이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서처럼 자신의 가장 참되고 진실한 마음을 뒤로 미루고 있다는 생각 말입니다."], ["Ah me!", "아아!"], ["What Richard would have been without that blight, I never shall know now!", "그 마름병만 없었더라면 리처드가 어떤 사람이 되었을지, 나는 이제 영영 알 수 없겠지요!"]], [["He told Ada, in his most ingenuous way, that he had not come to make any secret inroad on the terms she had accepted (rather too implicitly and confidingly, he thought) from Mr.", "그는 더없이 솔직한 태도로 에이다에게 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce, that he had come openly to see her and to see me and to justify himself for the present terms on which he stood with Mr. Jarndyce.", "그녀가 잔다이스 씨로부터 받아들인 조건(그가 보기에는 너무 고분고분하고 미덥게 받아들였다고 했습니다)에 무슨 은밀한 침범을 하러 온 것이 아니라, 떳떳하게 그녀를 보고 나를 보러 왔으며, 잔다이스 씨와 지금 자기가 맺고 있는 관계의 정당함을 밝히러 왔다고 말입니다."], ["As the dear old infant would be with us directly, he begged that I would make an appointment for the morning, when he might set himself right through the means of an unreserved conversation with me.", "사랑스러운 늙은 아기가 곧 우리에게 올 테니, 자기가 나와 거리낌 없는 대화를 통해 입장을 바로잡을 수 있도록 내일 아침에 약속을 잡아 달라고 청했습니다."], ["I proposed to walk with him in the park at seven o’clock, and this was arranged.", "나는 일곱 시에 그와 공원을 거닐자고 제안했고, 그렇게 정해졌습니다."], ["Mr. Skimpole soon afterwards appeared and made us merry for an hour.", "곧이어 스킴폴 씨가 나타나 한 시간 동안 우리를 즐겁게 해 주었습니다."], ["He particularly requested to see little Coavinses (meaning Charley) and told her, with a patriarchal air, that he had given her late father all the business in his power and that if one of her little brothers would make haste to get set up in the same profession, he hoped he should still be able to put a good deal of employment in his way.", "그는 특히 어린 코빈시스(찰리를 가리키는 말이었습니다)를 보고 싶다고 하더니, 가부장 같은 태도로 그 아이에게, 자기가 그 아이의 돌아가신 아버지에게 할 수 있는 한 모든 일거리를 주었노라고, 그리고 그 아이의 어린 남동생 하나가 어서 같은 직업에 발을 들인다면 자기가 여전히 일감을 꽤 많이 돌려줄 수 있기를 바란다고 말했습니다."]], [["“For I am constantly being taken in these nets,” said Mr.", "\"나는 늘 이런 그물에 걸리거든요.\""], ["Skimpole, looking beamingly at us over a glass of wine-and-water, “and am constantly being bailed out—like a boat.", "스킴폴 씨가 와인을 탄 물 한 잔을 앞에 두고 환하게 웃으며 우리를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"그리고 늘 보석으로 풀려나지요."], ["Or paid off—like a ship’s company.", "마치 배처럼 말입니다. 아니면 정산을 받지요."], ["Somebody always does it for me.", "배의 선원들처럼요. 늘 누군가가 나를 대신해 해 줘요."], ["I can’t do it, you know, for I never have any money.", "나는 못 하니까요, 알다시피 돈이 통 없거든요."], ["But somebody does it.", "하지만 누군가가 해 준답니다."], ["I get out by somebody’s means; I am not like the starling; I get out.", "나는 누군가의 손을 빌려 빠져나오지요. 나는 찌르레기 같지 않아요."], ["If you were to ask me who somebody is, upon my word I couldn’t tell you.", "나는 빠져나옵니다. 그 누군가가 누구냐고 물으신다면, 정말이지 나도 말씀드릴 수가 없어요."], ["Let us drink to somebody.", "자, 그 누군가를 위해 건배합시다."], ["God bless him!”", "신의 가호가 그에게 있기를!\""]], [["Richard was a little late in the morning, but I had not to wait for him long, and we turned into the park.", "* 찌르레기: 로런스 스턴의 「감상적 여행」에 나오는, 새장에 갇혀 \"나갈 수 없어\"라고 우는 찌르레기를 빗댄 말. 리처드는 아침에 조금 늦었지만, 그를 오래 기다릴 필요는 없었고, 우리는 공원으로 들어섰습니다."], ["The air was bright and dewy and the sky without a cloud.", "공기는 맑고 이슬을 머금었으며 하늘에는 구름 한 점 없었습니다. 새들이 즐겁게 지저귀고, 고사리와 풀과 나무에 맺힌 물방울이 눈부시게 반짝였습니다."], ["The birds sang delightfully; the sparkles in the fern, the grass, and trees, were exquisite to see; the richness of the woods seemed to have increased twenty-fold since yesterday, as if, in the still night when they had looked so massively hushed in sleep, Nature, through all the minute details of every wonderful leaf, had been more wakeful than usual for the glory of that day.", "숲의 풍요로움은 어제보다 스무 곱절은 짙어진 듯했습니다. 마치 만물이, 그토록 묵직하게 잠에 잠겨 고요하던 지난밤에, 그날의 영광을 위해 온갖 경이로운 잎사귀의 미세한 마디마디까지 평소보다 더 깨어 있기라도 했던 것처럼 말입니다."]], [["“This is a lovely place,”", "\"여긴 참 아름다운 곳이야.\""], ["said Richard, looking round.", "리처드가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했습니다."], ["“None of the jar and discord of law-suits here!”", "\"여기엔 소송의 그 삐걱거림과 불협화음이 하나도 없어!\""]], [["But there was other trouble.", "그러나 다른 근심거리가 있었습니다."]], [["“I tell you what, my dear girl,”", "\"있잖아, 에스더.\""], ["said Richard, “when I get affairs in general settled, I shall come down here, I think, and rest.”", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"전반적인 일들이 다 정리되고 나면, 나는 여기 내려와서 좀 쉬어야겠어.\""]], [["“Would it not be better to rest now?”", "\"지금 쉬는 편이 더 낫지 않을까?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“Oh, as to resting NOW,”", "\"아, 지금 쉬는 거라든가,\""], ["said Richard, “or as to doing anything very definite NOW, that’s not easy.", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"지금 뭔가 아주 확실한 걸 한다든가, 그건 쉽지 않아."], ["In short, it can’t be done; I can’t do it at least.”", "한마디로, 그건 안 돼. 적어도 나는 못 하지.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"어째서?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“You know why not, Esther.", "\"왜 안 되는지 너도 알잖아, 에스더."], ["If you were living in an unfinished house, liable to have the roof put on or taken off—to be from top to bottom pulled down or built up—to-morrow, next day, next week, next month, next year—you would find it hard to rest or settle.", "만약 네가 미완성인 집에 살고 있는데, 그 지붕이 내일이나 모레, 다음 주, 다음 달, 내년에 올려지거나 걷히거나, 위에서 아래까지 헐리거나 다시 세워질 판이라면, 너도 쉬거나 자리 잡기가 어려울 거야. 나도 그래."], ["So do I.", "지금이라고?"], ["Now? There’s no now for us suitors.”", "우리 같은 소송 당사자들에게 지금이란 건 없어.\""]], [["I could almost have believed in the attraction on which my poor little wandering friend had expatiated when I saw again the darkened look of last night.", "지난밤의 그 어두워진 표정을 다시 보았을 때, 나는 가엾은 작은 떠돌이 친구가 늘어놓던 그 끌림이라는 것을 거의 믿을 뻔했습니다."], ["Terrible to think it had in it also a shade of that unfortunate man who had died.", "거기에 또한 죽어 버린 그 불운한 사람의 그림자까지 어려 있다고 생각하니 끔찍했습니다."]], [["“My dear Richard,”", "\"리처드.\""], ["said I, “this is a bad beginning of our conversation.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"우리 대화의 시작이 좋지 못하구나.\""]], [["“I knew you would tell me so, Dame Durden.”", "\"네가 그렇게 말할 줄 알았어, 더든 아주머니.\""]], [["“And not I alone, dear Richard.", "\"나뿐만이 아니야, 리처드."], ["It was not I who cautioned you once never to found a hope or expectation on the family curse.”", "집안의 저주에 희망이나 기대를 절대로 걸지 말라고 언젠가 너에게 일러 준 사람이 나는 아니었잖아.\""]], [["“There you come back to John Jarndyce!”", "\"또 존 잔다이스 이야기로 돌아오는구나!\""], ["said Richard impatiently.", "리처드가 조바심을 내며 말했습니다."], ["“Well!", "\"좋아!"], ["We must approach him sooner or later, for he is the staple of what I have to say, and it’s as well at once.", "우린 늦든 빠르든 그분 이야기를 해야만 해. 그분이 내가 하려는 말의 핵심이니까."], ["My dear Esther, how can you be so blind?", "차라리 지금 하는 게 낫겠지. 에스더, 어떻게 그렇게 눈이 멀 수가 있어?"], ["Don’t you see that he is an interested party and that it may be very well for him to wish me to know nothing of the suit, and care nothing about it, but that it may not be quite so well for me?”", "그분이 이해관계가 걸린 당사자라는 게 안 보여? 내가 그 소송에 대해 아무것도 모르고 아무 관심도 없기를 그분이 바라는 게 그분으로서는 아주 좋은 일일지 몰라도, 나한테는 영 좋지 않을 수도 있다는 게 안 보이느냐고?\""]], [["“Oh, Richard,”", "\"아, 리처드.\""], ["I remonstrated, “is it possible that you can ever have seen him and heard him, that you can ever have lived under his roof and known him, and can yet breathe, even to me in this solitary place where there is no one to hear us, such unworthy suspicions?”", "내가 항의했습니다. \"네가 그분을 보았고 그분 말을 들었으면서, 그분 지붕 아래서 살며 그분을 알았으면서, 어떻게 아직도, 그것도 우리 말을 들을 사람 하나 없는 이 외딴곳에서 나에게까지, 그런 가당찮은 의심을 입에 담을 수가 있니?\""]], [["He reddened deeply, as if his natural generosity felt a pang of reproach.", "그는 타고난 너그러움이 책망의 아픔을 느끼기라도 한 듯 얼굴을 붉혔습니다."], ["He was silent for a little while before he replied in a subdued voice, “Esther, I am sure you know that I am not a mean fellow and that I have some sense of suspicion and distrust being poor qualities in one of my years.”", "그는 한참 동안 잠잠히 있다가 가라앉은 목소리로 대답했습니다. \"에스더, 내가 비열한 인간이 아니라는 거, 그리고 의심이나 불신 따위가 내 나이대 사람에게 형편없는 자질이라는 걸 내가 안다는 거, 너도 잘 알 거야.\""]], [["“I know it very well,”", "\"아주 잘 알지.\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I. “I am not more sure of anything.”", "\"이보다 더 확실히 아는 건 없어.\""]], [["“That’s a dear girl,” retorted Richard, “and like you, because it gives me comfort. I had need to get some scrap of comfort out of all this business, for it’s a bad one at the best, as I have no occasion to tell you.”", "\"그게 바로 사랑스러운 아가씨다운 말이야.\" 리처드가 받았습니다. \"그리고 너답고. 그 말이 나에게 위안이 되거든. 이 모든 일에서 위안 한 조각이라도 건져야겠어. 어차피 잘해 봐야 고약한 일이니까. 굳이 너한테 말할 것도 없지만.\""]], [["“I know perfectly,” said I.", "\"잘 알아.\""], ["“I know as well, Richard—what shall I say?", "내가 말했습니다. \"리처드, 나는 잘 알아."], ["as well as you do—that such misconstructions are foreign to your nature.", "뭐라고 해야 할까, 너만큼이나 잘 알아, 그런 곡해는 너의 천성과 어긋난다는 걸."], ["And I know, as well as you know, what so changes it.”", "그리고 무엇이 너를 그토록 바꿔 놓는지도, 너만큼이나 잘 알고.\""]], [["“Come, sister, come,”", "\"자, 누이야, 자.\""], ["said Richard a little more gaily, “you will be fair with me at all events.", "리처드가 조금 더 명랑하게 말했습니다. \"어쨌든 너는 나에게 공정하게 굴겠지."], ["If I have the misfortune to be under that influence, so has he.", "내가 불운하게도 그 영향력 아래 놓여 있다면, 그분 역시 그래."], ["If it has a little twisted me, it may have a little twisted him too.", "그게 나를 조금 비틀어 놓았다면, 그분도 조금 비틀어 놓았을지 몰라."], ["I don’t say that he is not an honourable man, out of all this complication and uncertainty; I am sure he is.", "이 모든 얽히고설킨 불확실함을 떼어 놓고 보면 그분이 명예로운 사람이 아니라는 게 아니야. 분명 그렇지."], ["But it taints everybody.", "하지만 그건 누구든 다 오염시켜."], ["You know it taints everybody.", "누구든 다 오염시킨다는 거, 너도 알잖아."], ["You have heard him say so fifty times.", "그분이 그렇게 말하는 걸 너도 쉰 번은 들었을걸."], ["Then why should HE escape?”", "그런데 어째서 그분만 빠져나가야 하지?\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"그건,\""], ["said I, “his is an uncommon character, and he has resolutely kept himself outside the circle, Richard.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그분은 보기 드문 인품을 지녔고, 단호하게 그 굴레 바깥에 자신을 두어 왔기 때문이야, 리처드.\""]], [["“Oh, because and because!”", "\"아, 때문이고 때문이고!\""], ["replied Richard in his vivacious way.", "리처드가 특유의 활기찬 투로 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I am not sure, my dear girl, but that it may be wise and specious to preserve that outward indifference.", "\"에스더, 나는 잘 모르겠어. 그렇게 겉으로 무관심한 척하는 게 어쩌면 영리하고 그럴듯한 처신일 수도 있지 않을까."], ["It may cause other parties interested to become lax about their interests; and people may die off, and points may drag themselves out of memory, and many things may smoothly happen that are convenient enough.”", "그러면 이해관계가 걸린 다른 당사자들이 제 몫에 느슨해질 수도 있고. 사람들이 하나둘 죽어 나가고, 쟁점들이 기억에서 흐려지고, 입맛에 딱 맞는 여러 일들이 슬그머니 벌어질 수도 있는 거야.\""]], [["I was so touched with pity for Richard that I could not reproach him any more, even by a look.", "나는 리처드가 어찌나 가여운지 더는, 눈빛으로조차도 그를 책망할 수 없었습니다."], ["I remembered my guardian’s gentleness towards his errors and with what perfect freedom from resentment he had spoken of them.", "나는 후견인이 그의 잘못을 두고 보이던 너그러움을, 그리고 조금의 원망도 없이 그것을 이야기하던 모습을 떠올렸습니다."]], [["“Esther,”", "\"에스더.\""], ["Richard resumed, “you are not to suppose that I have come here to make underhanded charges against John Jarndyce.", "리처드가 다시 말을 이었습니다. \"내가 여기 존 잔다이스를 음흉하게 헐뜯으러 온 거라고는 생각하지 마."], ["I have only come to justify myself.", "나는 그저 내 입장을 밝히러 왔을 뿐이야. 내 말은 이거야."], ["What I say is, it was all very well and we got on very well while I was a boy, utterly regardless of this same suit; but as soon as I began to take an interest in it and to look into it, then it was quite another thing.", "내가 이 소송에는 통 아랑곳하지 않던 어린 시절에는 만사가 다 좋았고 우리도 사이좋게 지냈어. 하지만 내가 거기에 관심을 갖고 들여다보기 시작하자마자, 사정이 완전히 달라졌지."], ["Then John Jarndyce discovers that Ada and I must break off and that if I don’t amend that very objectionable course, I am not fit for her.", "그러자 존 잔다이스는 에이다와 내가 헤어져야 한다는 둥, 내가 그 몹시 못마땅한 길을 고치지 않으면 나는 그녀에게 어울리지 않는다는 둥 하기 시작했어."], ["Now, Esther, I don’t mean to amend that very objectionable course: I will not hold John Jarndyce’s favour on those unfair terms of compromise, which he has no right to dictate.", "자, 에스더, 나는 그 몹시 못마땅한 길을 고칠 생각이 없어. 나는 그분이 명령할 권리도 없는 그런 불공정한 타협의 조건으로 존 잔다이스의 호의를 붙들지는 않겠어."], ["Whether it pleases him or displeases him, I must maintain my rights and Ada’s.", "그분 마음에 들든 안 들든, 나는 내 권리와 에이다의 권리를 지켜야 해."], ["I have been thinking about it a good deal, and this is the conclusion I have come to.”", "나는 이걸 꽤 오래 생각해 봤고, 이게 내가 내린 결론이야.\""]], [["Poor dear Richard!", "가엾은 리처드!"], ["He had indeed been thinking about it a good deal.", "그는 정말로 이 문제를 오래 생각해 온 것이었습니다."], ["His face, his voice, his manner, all showed that too plainly.", "그의 얼굴이, 목소리가, 태도가, 하나같이 그것을 너무도 또렷이 보여 주었습니다."]], [["“So I tell him honourably (you are to know I have written to him about all this) that we are at issue and that we had better be at issue openly than covertly.", "\"그래서 나는 그분에게 떳떳이 말하는 거야(내가 이 모든 걸 편지로 써 보냈다는 걸 너도 알아야 해). 우리는 입장이 갈리니, 차라리 은밀히 갈리느니 대놓고 갈리는 편이 낫다고."], ["I thank him for his goodwill and his protection, and he goes his road, and I go mine.", "나는 그분의 호의와 보호에 감사를 표하고, 그분은 그분 길을 가고, 나는 내 길을 가는 거야."], ["The fact is, our roads are not the same.", "사실 우리 길은 같지가 않아."], ["Under one of the wills in dispute, I should take much more than he.", "지금 다투고 있는 유언장 가운데 하나에 따르면, 나는 그분보다 훨씬 더 많이 받게 돼."], ["I don’t mean to say that it is the one to be established, but there it is, and it has its chance.”", "그게 인정될 유언장이라는 말은 아니지만, 어쨌든 그게 있고, 그것도 가망이 있는 거야.\""]], [["“I have not to learn from you, my dear Richard,”", "\"네 편지에 대해서라면, 리처드,\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “of your letter.", "\"너에게 처음 듣는 게 아니야."], ["I had heard of it already without an offended or angry word.”", "나는 이미 그 이야기를 들었어. 기분 상했다거나 화난 말은 한마디도 없이 말이야.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래?\""], ["replied Richard, softening.", "리처드가 누그러지며 대답했습니다."], ["“I am glad I said he was an honourable man, out of all this wretched affair.", "\"이 비참한 일을 떼어 놓고 보면 그분이 명예로운 사람이라고 내가 말해 두길 잘했네."], ["But I always say that and have never doubted it.", "하지만 나는 늘 그렇게 말하고, 한 번도 의심한 적이 없어."], ["Now, my dear Esther, I know these views of mine appear extremely harsh to you, and will to Ada when you tell her what has passed between us.", "자, 에스더, 이런 내 생각이 너한테는 몹시 모질게 보인다는 거 나도 알아. 우리 사이에 오간 이야기를 네가 에이다에게 전하면 그 애한테도 그렇게 보이겠지."], ["But if you had gone into the case as I have, if you had only applied yourself to the papers as I did when I was at Kenge’s, if you only knew what an accumulation of charges and counter-charges, and suspicions and cross-suspicions, they involve, you would think me moderate in comparison.”", "하지만 네가 나처럼 이 사건을 파고들었다면, 내가 켄지 씨 사무소에 있을 때처럼 서류에만 매달려 봤다면, 거기에 얼마나 많은 고발과 맞고발, 의심과 맞의심이 켜켜이 쌓여 있는지 알기만 했다면, 너는 도리어 나를 점잖은 편이라고 여길 거야.\""]], [["“Perhaps so,” said I.", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\""], ["“But do you think that, among those many papers, there is much truth and justice, Richard?”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그런데 리처드, 그 많은 서류들 가운데에 진실과 정의가 많이 들어 있다고 생각하니?\""]], [["“There is truth and justice somewhere in the case, Esther—”", "\"이 사건 어딘가에는 진실과 정의가 있어, 에스더—\""]], [["“Or was once, long ago,”", "\"아니면, 오래전 한때 있었거나.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Is—is—must be somewhere,”", "\"있어, 있어, 어딘가에 분명히 있어야 해.\""], ["pursued Richard impetuously, “and must be brought out.", "리처드가 격하게 말을 이었습니다. \"그리고 그걸 끄집어내야 해."], ["To allow Ada to be made a bribe and hush-money of is not the way to bring it out.", "에이다가 뇌물이나 입막음 돈으로 쓰이게 내버려 두는 건 그걸 끄집어내는 길이 아니야."], ["You say the suit is changing me; John Jarndyce says it changes, has changed, and will change everybody who has any share in it.", "너는 그 소송이 나를 바꾸어 놓는다고 하지. 존 잔다이스는 거기에 조금이라도 발을 담근 사람은 누구든 바꾸어 놓고, 바꾸어 놓았고, 또 바꾸어 놓을 거라고 하고."], ["Then the greater right I have on my side when I resolve to do all I can to bring it to an end.”", "그렇다면 그걸 끝장내려고 내가 할 수 있는 모든 걸 하기로 마음먹은 데에는 더더욱 정당한 명분이 내 편에 있는 거지.\""]], [["“All you can, Richard!", "\"네가 할 수 있는 모든 걸, 리처드!"], ["Do you think that in these many years no others have done all they could?", "그 오랜 세월 동안 다른 사람들은 할 수 있는 모든 걸 하지 않았다고 생각해?"], ["Has the difficulty grown easier because of so many failures?”", "그 많은 실패 때문에 이 일이 더 쉬워지기라도 했어?\""]], [["“It can’t last for ever,”", "\"이게 영원히 갈 수는 없어.\""], ["returned Richard with a fierceness kindling in him which again presented to me that last sad reminder.", "리처드가 다시금 나에게 그 마지막 슬픈 징후를 떠올리게 하는 사나운 기운을 그 안에 지핀 채 대답했습니다."], ["“I am young and earnest, and energy and determination have done wonders many a time.", "\"나는 젊고 진지해. 그리고 열정과 결단은 숱하게 기적을 일으켜 왔어."], ["Others have only half thrown themselves into it.", "다른 사람들은 거기에 절반밖에 몸을 던지지 않았어."], ["I devote myself to it.", "나는 거기에 나를 바칠 거야."], ["I make it the object of my life.”", "그걸 내 삶의 목표로 삼을 거야.\""]], [["“Oh, Richard, my dear, so much the worse, so much the worse!”", "\"아, 리처드, 그럴수록 더 나쁜 거야, 더 나쁜 거라고!\""]], [["“No, no, no, don’t you be afraid for me,”", "\"아냐, 아냐, 아냐, 나 때문에 겁먹지 마.\""], ["he returned affectionately.", "그가 다정하게 대답했습니다."], ["“You’re a dear, good, wise, quiet, blessed girl; but you have your prepossessions.", "\"너는 사랑스럽고, 착하고, 지혜롭고, 차분하고, 복된 아가씨야. 하지만 너한테는 너만의 선입견이 있어."], ["So I come round to John Jarndyce.", "그래서 다시 존 잔다이스 이야기로 돌아오는 거야."], ["I tell you, my good Esther, when he and I were on those terms which he found so convenient, we were not on natural terms.”", "에스더, 너한테 말하지만, 그분이 그토록 편하게 여기던 그 조건으로 그분과 내가 지내던 때, 우린 자연스러운 사이가 아니었어.\""]], [["“Are division and animosity your natural terms, Richard?”", "\"분열과 적의가 너에게는 자연스러운 사이니, 리처드?\""]], [["“No, I don’t say that.", "\"아니, 그런 말이 아니야."], ["I mean that all this business puts us on unnatural terms, with which natural relations are incompatible.", "내 말은, 이 모든 일이 우리를 부자연스러운 사이로 몰아넣었고, 그래서는 자연스러운 관계가 양립할 수 없다는 거야."], ["See another reason for urging it on!", "그러니 이 일을 어서 밀어붙여야 할 또 하나의 이유가 있는 거지!"], ["I may find out when it’s over that I have been mistaken in John Jarndyce.", "이게 다 끝나고 나면, 내가 존 잔다이스를 잘못 봤다는 걸 알게 될지도 몰라."], ["My head may be clearer when I am free of it, and I may then agree with what you say to-day.", "이 일에서 벗어나면 내 머리가 더 맑아질 수도 있고, 그러면 오늘 네가 한 말에 동의하게 될지도 모르지."], ["Very well.", "좋아."], ["Then I shall acknowledge it and make him reparation.”", "그러면 나는 그걸 인정하고 그분에게 보상할 거야.\""]], [["Everything postponed to that imaginary time!", "모든 것이 그 상상 속의 때로 미뤄지다니!"], ["Everything held in confusion and indecision until then!", "그때까지 모든 것이 혼란과 망설임 속에 붙들려 있다니!"]], [["“Now, my best of confidantes,” said Richard, “I want my cousin Ada to understand that I am not captious, fickle, and wilful about John Jarndyce, but that I have this purpose and reason at my back. I wish to represent myself to her through you, because she has a great esteem and respect for her cousin John; and I know you will soften the course I take, even though you disapprove of it; and—and in short,” said Richard, who had been hesitating through these words, “I—I don’t like to represent myself in this litigious, contentious, doubting character to a confiding girl like Ada.”", "\"자, 내 가장 좋은 속내 친구야.\" 리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는 사촌 에이다가 이렇게 이해해 줬으면 해. 내가 존 잔다이스를 두고 트집이나 잡고, 변덕스럽고, 제멋대로 구는 게 아니라, 이런 목적과 이유를 등에 업고 있다는 걸 말이야. 나는 너를 통해 그 애한테 내 모습을 전하고 싶어. 그 애는 사촌 존을 무척 존경하고 따르니까. 그리고 네가 비록 내 길을 못마땅해하더라도 그걸 부드럽게 전해 주리라는 걸 나는 아니까. 그리고, 한마디로,\" 이 말들을 더듬더듬 이어 가던 리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는, 나는 에이다처럼 사람을 믿는 아가씨에게 소송이나 일삼고, 다투기나 하고, 의심이나 하는 이런 인간으로 내 모습을 보이고 싶지가 않아.\""]], [["I told him that he was more like himself in those latter words than in anything he had said yet.", "나는 그에게, 지금껏 한 그 어떤 말보다도 그 마지막 말들에서 그가 더 그다워 보인다고 했습니다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["acknowledged Richard, “that may be true enough, my love.", "리처드가 인정했습니다. \"그 말이 어지간히 맞을지도 몰라, 에스더."], ["I rather feel it to be so.", "나도 그런 것 같다는 생각이 들긴 해."], ["But I shall be able to give myself fair-play by and by.", "하지만 머지않아 나는 나 자신에게 공정한 기회를 줄 수 있을 거야."], ["I shall come all right again, then, don’t you be afraid.”", "그때 가면 다시 멀쩡해질 거야. 그러니 겁먹지 마.\""]], [["I asked him if this were all he wished me to tell Ada.", "나는 그에게, 에이다에게 전해 달라는 게 이것이 전부냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Not quite,”", "\"꼭 그렇지는 않아.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"나는 그 애에게 이것만은 숨기지 않아야 해."], ["“I am bound not to withhold from her that John Jarndyce answered my letter in his usual manner, addressing me as ‘My dear Rick,’ trying to argue me out of my opinions, and telling me that they should make no difference in him.", "존 잔다이스가 늘 하던 식으로 내 편지에 답을 보내왔다는 거 말이야. 나를 '사랑하는 리크'라고 부르면서, 내 생각을 돌려놓으려고 설득하고, 자기는 조금도 달라지지 않을 거라고 하더군.(물론 다 좋은 말이지, 하지만 사정이 달라지는 건 아니야.) 그리고 나는 에이다가 이것도 알아 줬으면 해."], ["(All very well of course, but not altering the case.) I also want Ada to know that if I see her seldom just now, I am looking after her interests as well as my own—we two being in the same boat exactly—and that I hope she will not suppose from any flying rumours she may hear that I am at all light-headed or imprudent; on the contrary, I am always looking forward to the termination of the suit, and always planning in that direction.", "내가 지금 그 애를 좀처럼 만나지 못하더라도, 나는 내 이익뿐 아니라 그 애의 이익도 살피고 있다는 거 말이야. 우리 둘은 정확히 한배를 탄 처지니까."], ["Being of age now and having taken the step I have taken, I consider myself free from any accountability to John Jarndyce; but Ada being still a ward of the court, I don’t yet ask her to renew our engagement.", "그리고 그 애가 무슨 떠도는 소문을 듣더라도, 내가 들뜨거나 경솔하게 군다고 짐작하지 않았으면 해. 오히려 나는 늘 소송이 끝날 날을 내다보고, 늘 그 방향으로 궁리하고 있으니까."], ["When she is free to act for herself, I shall be myself once more and we shall both be in very different worldly circumstances, I believe.", "이제 나는 성년이 되었고 내가 내디딘 걸음을 내디뎠으니, 존 잔다이스에게 무엇 하나 해명할 책임이 없다고 여겨. 하지만 에이다는 아직 법정의 피후견인이니, 나는 아직 그 애에게 우리 약혼을 다시 맺자고는 청하지 않아."], ["If you tell her all this with the advantage of your considerate way, you will do me a very great and a very kind service, my dear Esther; and I shall knock Jarndyce and Jarndyce on the head with greater vigour.", "그 애가 제 뜻대로 행동할 수 있게 되면, 나는 다시 예전의 내가 될 거고, 우리 둘 다 형편이 아주 달라져 있을 거야, 분명히. 네가 그 사려 깊은 방식으로 이 모든 걸 그 애한테 전해 준다면, 너는 나에게 더없이 크고 더없이 고마운 일을 해 주는 거야, 에스더."], ["Of course I ask for no secrecy at Bleak House.”", "그러면 나는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 더욱 힘차게 때려눕힐 수 있을 거고. 물론 황폐한 집에서는 비밀로 해 달라는 게 아니야.\""]], [["“Richard,”", "\"리처드.\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “you place great confidence in me, but I fear you will not take advice from me?”", "\"너는 나를 무척 믿어 주는구나. 하지만 너는 내 충고는 듣지 않으려 하겠지?\""]], [["“It’s impossible that I can on this subject, my dear girl.", "\"이 문제에서만은 그럴 수가 없어, 에스더."], ["On any other, readily.”", "다른 어떤 일이라면 기꺼이 듣겠지만.\""]], [["As if there were any other in his life!", "마치 그의 삶에 다른 어떤 일이 있기라도 한 듯이!"], ["As if his whole career and character were not being dyed one colour!", "마치 그의 인생 전부와 인품 전체가 한 가지 색으로 물들고 있지 않기라도 한 듯이!"]], [["“But I may ask you a question, Richard?”", "\"그래도 한 가지는 물어봐도 되지, 리처드?\""]], [["“I think so,”", "\"되고말고.\""], ["said he, laughing.", "그가 웃으며 말했습니다."], ["“I don’t know who may not, if you may not.”", "\"네가 안 된다면 누가 되겠어.\""]], [["“You say, yourself, you are not leading a very settled life.”", "\"너 스스로 말했잖아, 그리 안정된 생활을 하고 있지 않다고.\""]], [["“How can I, my dear Esther, with nothing settled!”", "\"어떻게 그럴 수 있겠어, 에스더, 정해진 게 하나도 없는데!\""]], [["“Are you in debt again?”", "\"또 빚을 졌니?\""]], [["“Why, of course I am,”", "\"당연히 졌지.\""], ["said Richard, astonished at my simplicity.", "리처드가 내 순진함에 놀라며 말했습니다."]], [["“Is it of course?”", "\"그게 당연한 거니?\""]], [["“My dear child, certainly.", "\"에스더, 그럼."], ["I can’t throw myself into an object so completely without expense.", "비용을 들이지 않고서야 한 가지 목표에 이렇게 송두리째 몸을 던질 수가 없잖아."], ["You forget, or perhaps you don’t know, that under either of the wills Ada and I take something.", "잊었나 본데, 아니면 모르나 본데, 어느 쪽 유언장에 따르든 에이다와 나는 얼마간은 받게 돼."], ["It’s only a question between the larger sum and the smaller.", "그저 더 많은 액수냐 더 적은 액수냐의 문제일 뿐이지."], ["I shall be within the mark any way.", "어느 쪽이든 나는 그 한도 안에 있어."], ["Bless your heart, my excellent girl,”", "아이고, 내 착한 아가씨야.\""], ["said Richard, quite amused with me, “I shall be all right!", "리처드가 나를 보며 무척 재미있어하며 말했습니다. \"나는 괜찮을 거야!"], ["I shall pull through, my dear!”", "잘 헤쳐 나갈 거라고!\""]], [["I felt so deeply sensible of the danger in which he stood that I tried, in Ada’s name, in my guardian’s, in my own, by every fervent means that I could think of, to warn him of it and to show him some of his mistakes.", "나는 그가 처한 위험이 어찌나 절실히 느껴지던지, 에이다의 이름으로, 후견인의 이름으로, 나 자신의 이름으로, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 간곡한 방법을 다 써서 그에게 그 위험을 일러 주고 그의 잘못 몇 가지를 보여 주려 애썼습니다."], ["He received everything I said with patience and gentleness, but it all rebounded from him without taking the least effect.", "그는 내가 하는 말을 모두 참을성 있게, 부드럽게 받아들였지만, 그 모든 것이 조금의 효력도 없이 그에게서 튕겨 나가 버렸습니다."], ["I could not wonder at this after the reception his preoccupied mind had given to my guardian’s letter, but I determined to try Ada’s influence yet.", "후견인의 편지조차 그 골몰한 마음이 그렇게 받아들였던 터라 이것이 놀랍지는 않았지만, 나는 그래도 에이다의 영향력을 시험해 보기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["So when our walk brought us round to the village again, and I went home to breakfast, I prepared Ada for the account I was going to give her and told her exactly what reason we had to dread that Richard was losing himself and scattering his whole life to the winds. It made her very unhappy, of course, though she had a far, far greater reliance on his correcting his errors than I could have—which was so natural and loving in my dear!—and she presently wrote him this little letter:", "그래서 산책길이 마을로 다시 돌아왔을 때, 나는 집으로 돌아가 아침을 먹으며 에이다에게 들려줄 이야기를 준비시켰고, 우리에게 리처드가 자신을 잃고 자기 인생 전부를 바람에 흩날려 버릴까 봐 두려워할 만한 까닭이 무엇인지 그대로 일러 주었습니다. 그것은 물론 그녀를 무척 슬프게 했습니다. 그래도 그녀는 그가 자기 잘못을 바로잡으리라는 믿음을 나로서는 도저히 가질 수 없을 만큼 굳게, 훨씬 더 굳게 품고 있었습니다. 그것이 내 사랑하는 사람다운 너무도 자연스럽고 사랑이 넘치는 모습이었습니다. 그리고 그녀는 곧 그에게 이런 짤막한 편지를 썼습니다."]], [["My dearest cousin,", "사랑하는 사촌에게,"]], [["Esther has told me all you said to her this morning.", "오늘 아침 당신이 에스더에게 한 말을 그 애가 전부 들려주었어요."], ["I write this to repeat most earnestly for myself all that she said to you and to let you know how sure I am that you will sooner or later find our cousin John a pattern of truth, sincerity, and goodness, when you will deeply, deeply grieve to have done him (without intending it) so much wrong.", "그 애가 당신에게 한 모든 말을 나 자신을 위해 더없이 간절히 되풀이하고자, 그리고 머지않아 당신이 우리 사촌 존이 진실과 성실과 선함의 본보기임을 알게 되리라고 내가 얼마나 확신하는지 알려 드리고자 이 글을 씁니다. 그때 당신은 (그럴 뜻이 없으셨건만) 그분에게 그토록 큰 잘못을 저질렀음을 깊이, 깊이 가슴 아파하게 될 거예요."]], [["I do not quite know how to write what I wish to say next, but I trust you will understand it as I mean it.", "다음에 하려는 말을 어떻게 써야 할지 잘 모르겠지만, 내 뜻 그대로 헤아려 주시리라 믿어요. 사랑하는 사촌, 나는 한 가지가 두려워요."], ["I have some fears, my dearest cousin, that it may be partly for my sake you are now laying up so much unhappiness for yourself—and if for yourself, for me.", "당신이 지금 스스로에게 이토록 많은 불행을 쌓고 있는 게 어쩌면 얼마쯤은 나를 위해서가 아닐까, 그래서 만약 당신 자신을 위한 것이라면 곧 나를 위한 것이기도 하지 않을까 하는 거예요."], ["In case this should be so, or in case you should entertain much thought of me in what you are doing, I most earnestly entreat and beg you to desist.", "만약 그렇다면, 혹은 당신이 그 일을 하면서 나를 마음에 많이 두고 있다면, 나는 더없이 간절히 당신에게 그만두기를 빌고 또 청합니다."], ["You can do nothing for my sake that will make me half so happy as for ever turning your back upon the shadow in which we both were born.", "우리 둘이 함께 태어난 그 그림자에 영영 등을 돌리는 것, 그것 말고는 당신이 나를 위해 무엇을 한들 나를 그 절반만큼도 행복하게 해 줄 수 없을 거예요."], ["Do not be angry with me for saying this.", "이런 말을 한다고 나에게 노여워하지 마세요."], ["Pray, pray, dear Richard, for my sake, and for your own, and in a natural repugnance for that source of trouble which had its share in making us both orphans when we were very young, pray, pray, let it go for ever.", "부디, 부디, 사랑하는 리처드, 나를 위해, 또 당신 자신을 위해, 그리고 우리가 아주 어렸을 때 우리 둘을 고아로 만드는 데 한몫했던 그 괴로움의 근원을 마땅히 미워하는 마음으로, 부디, 부디, 그것을 영원히 놓아 버리세요."], ["We have reason to know by this time that there is no good in it and no hope, that there is nothing to be got from it but sorrow.", "우리는 이제껏 충분히 알게 되었잖아요. 거기엔 아무런 좋은 것도, 아무런 희망도 없고, 슬픔 말고는 얻을 게 아무것도 없다는 걸요."]], [["My dearest cousin, it is needless for me to say that you are quite free and that it is very likely you may find some one whom you will love much better than your first fancy.", "사랑하는 사촌, 당신이 전혀 매이지 않은 자유의 몸이라는 것, 그리고 당신이 첫사랑보다 훨씬 더 사랑하게 될 사람을 만날 가능성이 무척 크다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요."], ["I am quite sure, if you will let me say so, that the object of your choice would greatly prefer to follow your fortunes far and wide, however moderate or poor, and see you happy, doing your duty and pursuing your chosen way, than to have the hope of being, or even to be, very rich with you (if such a thing were possible) at the cost of dragging years of procrastination and anxiety and of your indifference to other aims.", "그리고 이런 말을 하도록 허락해 주신다면, 나는 확신해요. 당신이 택한 그 사람은, 미루고 또 미루는 세월과 불안을 견디고 당신이 다른 목표에 무심해지는 것을 대가로 치르면서 당신과 함께 (그런 일이 가능하다 한들) 큰 부자가 되기를 바라거나 정말로 부자가 되기보다는, 아무리 변변찮고 가난하더라도 어디든 멀리까지 당신의 운명을 따르며, 당신이 행복하게, 당신의 본분을 다하며, 당신이 택한 길을 걷는 모습을 보기를 훨씬 더 바라리라는 걸요."], ["You may wonder at my saying this so confidently with so little knowledge or experience, but I know it for a certainty from my own heart.", "아는 것도 겪은 것도 이리 적은 내가 이걸 이토록 자신 있게 말하는 게 의아하실지 모르지만, 나는 내 마음으로 그것을 확실히 알아요."]], [["Ever, my dearest cousin, your most affectionate", "언제까지나, 사랑하는 사촌, 당신을 가장 깊이 사랑하는"]], [["Ada", "에이다"]], [["This note brought Richard to us very soon, but it made little change in him if any.", "이 편지에 리처드는 곧 우리에게 왔지만, 그를 바꿔 놓은 것은 있다 한들 미미했습니다."], ["We would fairly try, he said, who was right and who was wrong—he would show us—we should see!", "누가 옳고 누가 그른지 공정하게 가려 보자고, 자기가 보여 주겠다고, 우리가 두고 보게 되리라고 그는 말했습니다."], ["He was animated and glowing, as if Ada’s tenderness had gratified him; but I could only hope, with a sigh, that the letter might have some stronger effect upon his mind on re-perusal than it assuredly had then.", "그는 에이다의 다정함이 흐뭇하기라도 한 듯 생기 넘치고 환했지만, 나는 그저 한숨을 내쉬며, 그 편지가 그때 분명히 발휘한 것보다는 다시 읽을 때 그의 마음에 좀 더 강한 효력을 미치기를 바랄 따름이었습니다."]], [["As they were to remain with us that day and had taken their places to return by the coach next morning, I sought an opportunity of speaking to Mr.", "그들은 그날 우리와 함께 머물다가 이튿날 아침 마차로 돌아가려고 자리를 잡아 둔 터라, 나는 스킴폴 씨와 이야기할 기회를 노렸습니다."], ["Skimpole. Our out-of-door life easily threw one in my way, and I delicately said that there was a responsibility in encouraging Richard.", "야외에서 지내는 생활 덕에 그런 기회는 쉽게 찾아왔고, 나는 리처드를 부추기는 데에는 책임이 따른다고 조심스레 말했습니다."]], [["“Responsibility, my dear Miss Summerson?”", "\"책임이라니요, 서머슨 양?\""], ["he repeated, catching at the word with the pleasantest smile.", "그가 더없이 유쾌한 미소를 지으며 그 말을 덥석 받았습니다."], ["“I am the last man in the world for such a thing.", "\"나야말로 세상에서 그런 것과 가장 거리가 먼 사람이지요."], ["I never was responsible in my life—I can’t be.”", "나는 평생 책임이라는 걸 져 본 적이 없어요. 질 수가 없거든요.\""]], [["“I am afraid everybody is obliged to be,”", "\"누구나 책임을 져야 하지 않을까요.\""], ["said I timidly enough, he being so much older and more clever than I.", "내가 꽤 머뭇거리며 말했습니다. 그가 나보다 훨씬 나이도 많고 영리했으니까요."]], [["“No, really?” said Mr.", "\"그래요, 정말로요?\""], ["Skimpole, receiving this new light with a most agreeable jocularity of surprise.", "스킴폴 씨가 그 새로운 깨달음을 더없이 흐뭇한 놀라움의 농담조로 받아들이며 말했습니다."], ["“But every man’s not obliged to be solvent? I am not.", "\"하지만 누구나 빚을 다 갚아야만 하는 건 아니잖아요?"], ["I never was.", "나는 안 그래요."], ["See, my dear Miss Summerson,”", "한 번도 그런 적이 없고요. 보세요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he took a handful of loose silver and halfpence from his pocket, “there’s so much money.", "그가 주머니에서 은화와 반 페니짜리 동전을 한 줌 꺼냈습니다. \"여기 이만큼 돈이 있어요."], ["I have not an idea how much.", "얼마인지는 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["I have not the power of counting.", "나는 셈하는 능력이 없거든요."], ["Call it four and ninepence—call it four pound nine.", "사 실링 구 펜스라 칩시다. 아니, 사 파운드 구 실링이라 해도 좋고요."], ["They tell me I owe more than that. I dare say I do.", "사람들은 내가 그보다 더 많은 빚을 졌다고들 합디다."], ["I dare say I owe as much as good-natured people will let me owe.", "그렇겠지요. 사람 좋은 이들이 지게 해 주는 만큼 빚을 졌겠지요."], ["If they don’t stop, why should I?", "그들이 멈추지 않는데 내가 왜 멈춰야 합니까?"], ["There you have Harold Skimpole in little.", "자, 여기 해럴드 스킴폴이라는 사람이 고스란히 있습니다."], ["If that’s responsibility, I am responsible.”", "그게 책임이라면, 나도 책임을 지는 셈이지요.\""]], [["The perfect ease of manner with which he put the money up again and looked at me with a smile on his refined face, as if he had been mentioning a curious little fact about somebody else, almost made me feel as if he really had nothing to do with it.", "그가 더없이 편안한 태도로 돈을 도로 집어넣고는, 마치 다른 누군가에 관한 묘한 사실 한 토막을 이야기하기라도 한 듯, 그 세련된 얼굴에 미소를 띤 채 나를 바라보는 통에, 나는 그가 정말로 이 일과는 아무 상관도 없는 사람처럼 느껴질 지경이었습니다."]], [["“Now, when you mention responsibility,”", "\"그런데 책임 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데요.\" 그가 다시 입을 열었습니다."], ["he resumed, “I am disposed to say that I never had the happiness of knowing any one whom I should consider so refreshingly responsible as yourself.", "\"나는 이렇게 말하고 싶군요. 내가 알고 지낸 사람 가운데 당신만큼 보기만 해도 산뜻하게 책임감이 넘치는 이를 알게 된 행복은 없었다고 말입니다."], ["You appear to me to be the very touchstone of responsibility.", "당신은 내게 책임감의 시금석 그 자체로 보여요. 서머슨 양, 당신이 자기를 중심으로 돌아가는 그 작고 질서정연한 체계 전체가 완벽히 돌아가도록 골몰하는 모습을 보면, 나는 이렇게 혼잣말을 하고 싶어진답니다."], ["When I see you, my dear Miss Summerson, intent upon the perfect working of the whole little orderly system of which you are the centre, I feel inclined to say to myself—in fact I do say to myself very often—THAT’S responsibility!”", "사실 자주 혼잣말로 그러지요. 저게 바로 책임감이다, 하고요!\""]], [["It was difficult, after this, to explain what I meant; but I persisted so far as to say that we all hoped he would check and not confirm Richard in the sanguine views he entertained just then.", "이쯤 되니 내 뜻을 설명하기란 어려웠지만, 나는 그래도, 그가 그때 리처드가 품고 있던 그 낙관적인 생각을 부추기지 말고 막아 주기를 우리 모두 바란다는 말만은 굽히지 않았습니다."]], [["“Most willingly,”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요.\""], ["he retorted, “if I could.", "그가 받아쳤습니다."], ["But, my dear Miss Summerson, I have no art, no disguise.", "\"내가 할 수만 있다면요. 하지만 서머슨 양, 나에게는 잔재주도, 가식도 없어요."], ["If he takes me by the hand and leads me through Westminster Hall in an airy procession after fortune, I must go.", "그가 내 손을 잡고 운명을 좇는 경쾌한 행렬을 따라 웨스트민스터 홀을 지나가자고 하면, 나는 가야만 합니다. 그가 '스킴폴, 이 춤에 끼어들게!'"], ["If he says, ‘Skimpole, join the dance!’ I must join it.", "하면, 나는 끼어들어야 해요. 상식이 있는 사람이라면 안 그러겠지요, 나도 압니다."], ["Common sense wouldn’t, I know, but I have NO common sense.”", "하지만 나에게는 상식이라는 게 도무지 없거든요.\""]], [["It was very unfortunate for Richard, I said.", "리처드에게는 그게 참 안된 일이라고 나는 말했습니다."]], [["“Do you think so!”", "\"그렇게 생각해요!\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Don’t say that, don’t say that.", "\"그런 말 마세요, 그런 말 마요."], ["Let us suppose him keeping company with Common Sense—an excellent man—a good deal wrinkled—dreadfully practical—change for a ten-pound note in every pocket—ruled account-book in his hand—say, upon the whole, resembling a tax-gatherer.", "그가 상식—훌륭한 사람이지요, 주름이 잔뜩 잡히고, 끔찍이도 실용적이며, 주머니마다 십 파운드 지폐를 바꿀 잔돈을 넣고, 손에는 칸 그어진 장부를 든, 한마디로 세금 징수원을 빼닮은 그런 사람—과 함께 다닌다고 칩시다. 우리 사랑하는 리처드는 낙관적이고 열정적이며, 장애물을 훌쩍훌쩍 뛰어넘고, 갓 돋은 새싹처럼 시정이 터질 듯 넘쳐서, 이 점잖기 그지없는 길동무에게 말하지요."], ["Our dear Richard, sanguine, ardent, overleaping obstacles, bursting with poetry like a young bud, says to this highly respectable companion, ‘I see a golden prospect before me; it’s very bright, it’s very beautiful, it’s very joyous; here I go, bounding over the landscape to come at it!’ The respectable companion instantly knocks him down with the ruled account-book; tells him in a literal, prosaic way that he sees no such thing; shows him it’s nothing but fees, fraud, horsehair wigs, and black gowns.", "'내 앞에 황금빛 전망이 보여. 너무도 환하고, 너무도 아름답고, 너무도 즐거워."], ["Now you know that’s a painful change—sensible in the last degree, I have no doubt, but disagreeable.", "자, 저걸 잡으려고 들판을 껑충껑충 뛰어 달려간다!' 그러면 그 점잖은 길동무는 대뜸 칸 그어진 장부로 그를 후려쳐 쓰러뜨리고는, 딱딱하고 멋대가리 없는 투로, 자기 눈엔 그런 게 안 보인다고 말하지요."], ["I can’t do it.", "그건 수임료와 사기와 말총 가발과 검은 법복일 뿐이라고 보여 주고요. 자, 그건 참 괴로운 변화 아닙니까."], ["I haven’t got the ruled account-book, I have none of the tax-gathering elements in my composition, I am not at all respectable, and I don’t want to be.", "더없이 분별 있는 일이긴 하겠지요, 그건 의심할 바 없어요, 하지만 영 못마땅하지요. 나는 그렇게 못 합니다."], ["Odd perhaps, but so it is!”", "나에게는 칸 그어진 장부도 없고, 세금 징수원 같은 구석이라곤 내 안에 하나도 없으며, 나는 도무지 점잖지가 못하고, 점잖아지고 싶지도 않아요. 이상할지 몰라도, 그게 그래요!\""]], [["It was idle to say more, so I proposed that we should join Ada and Richard, who were a little in advance, and I gave up Mr. Skimpole in despair.", "더 말해 봐야 부질없는 일이라, 나는 조금 앞서 걷고 있던 에이다와 리처드에게 가자고 제안하고는, 절망 속에 스킴폴 씨를 단념했습니다."], ["He had been over the Hall in the course of the morning and whimsically described the family pictures as we walked.", "그는 그날 아침 저택을 둘러보았던 터라, 우리가 걷는 동안 익살스럽게 가족 초상화들을 묘사했습니다."], ["There were such portentous shepherdesses among the Ladies Dedlock dead and gone, he told us, that peaceful crooks became weapons of assault in their hands.", "그가 들려준 바로는, 세상을 떠난 역대 데드록 마님들 가운데에는 어찌나 무시무시한 양치기 처녀들이 있는지, 그들 손에 들리면 평화로운 양치기 지팡이마저 공격용 무기가 되더라는 것이었습니다."], ["They tended their flocks severely in buckram and powder and put their sticking-plaster patches on to terrify commoners as the chiefs of some other tribes put on their war-paint.", "그들은 빳빳한 아마포 옷에 분을 바른 채 엄하게 양 떼를 치고, 다른 어떤 부족의 추장들이 전투용 칠을 하듯 평민들을 겁주려고 얼굴에 반창고 같은 애교점을 붙였더랍니다."], ["There was a Sir Somebody Dedlock, with a battle, a sprung-mine, volumes of smoke, flashes of lightning, a town on fire, and a stormed fort, all in full action between his horse’s two hind legs, showing, he supposed, how little a Dedlock made of such trifles.", "또 어느 데드록 경의 초상은 전투며, 폭파된 지뢰며, 자욱한 연기며, 번쩍이는 번개며, 불타는 도시며, 함락된 요새가 죄다 그의 말 두 뒷다리 사이에서 한창 벌어지고 있는데, 그가 보기엔 데드록 가문 사람이 그런 사소한 일쯤은 얼마나 대수롭잖게 여겼는지를 보여 주는 것이라고 했습니다."], ["The whole race he represented as having evidently been, in life, what he called “stuffed people”—a large collection, glassy eyed, set up in the most approved manner on their various twigs and perches, very correct, perfectly free from animation, and always in glass cases.", "그는 그 가문 전체가 살아생전에도 분명 그가 이른바 \"박제된 사람들\"이었다고 평했습니다. 유리알 같은 눈으로, 더없이 인정받는 방식으로 저마다의 잔가지와 횃대에 올려 세워진, 더없이 반듯하고, 생기라곤 조금도 없으며, 늘 유리 상자에 갇혀 있는, 한 무더기의 커다란 수집품이라고 말입니다."]], [["I was not so easy now during any reference to the name but that I felt it a relief when Richard, with an exclamation of surprise, hurried away to meet a stranger whom he first descried coming slowly towards us.", "이제는 그 이름이 입에 오를 때마다 마음이 편치 않았던 터라, 리처드가 놀라 소리치며 우리 쪽으로 천천히 다가오는 낯선 사람을 맨 먼저 알아보고 그를 맞으러 황급히 달려갔을 때, 나는 도리어 마음이 놓였습니다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"아니!\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Vholes!”", "\"볼스 아닌가!\""]], [["We asked if that were a friend of Richard’s.", "우리는 그가 리처드의 친구냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Friend and legal adviser,”", "\"친구이자 법률 고문이지요.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Now, my dear Miss Summerson, if you want common sense, responsibility, and respectability, all united—if you want an exemplary man—Vholes is THE man.”", "\"서머슨 양, 만약 당신이 상식과 책임감과 점잖음을 한 몸에 갖춘 사람을 찾는다면, 본받을 만한 사람을 찾는다면, 볼스야말로 바로 그 사람입니다.\""]], [["We had not known, we said, that Richard was assisted by any gentleman of that name.", "우리는 리처드가 그런 이름의 신사에게 도움을 받고 있는 줄 미처 몰랐다고 말했습니다."]], [["“When he emerged from legal infancy,” returned Mr.", "\"그가 법률상의 미성년기에서 벗어났을 때,\""], ["Skimpole, “he parted from our conversational friend Kenge and took up, I believe, with Vholes.", "스킴폴 씨가 대답했습니다. \"우리의 말솜씨 좋은 친구 켄지와 갈라서고는, 내 알기로 볼스를 택했지요."], ["Indeed, I know he did, because I introduced him to Vholes.”", "정말이지 그가 그랬다는 걸 나는 압니다. 내가 그를 볼스에게 소개해 줬으니까요.\""]], [["“Had you known him long?”", "\"그분을 오래 알고 지내셨나요?\""], ["asked Ada.", "에이다가 물었습니다."]], [["“Vholes?", "\"볼스 말이오?"], ["My dear Miss Clare, I had had that kind of acquaintance with him which I have had with several gentlemen of his profession.", "클레어 양, 나는 그와, 그쪽 직업을 가진 여러 신사들과 맺어 본 그런 종류의 친분을 맺고 있었지요. 그가 무언가를 아주 상냥하고 정중한 태도로 했는데, 절차를 밟았다, 라고 하는 게 그 표현일 겁니다."], ["He had done something or other in a very agreeable, civil manner—taken proceedings, I think, is the expression—which ended in the proceeding of his taking ME.", "그게 결국 나를 잡아들이는 절차로 끝났지요. 어떤 분이 친절히도 끼어들어 그 돈을 갚아 줬는데, 얼마얼마에 사 펜스가 그 액수였습니다."], ["Somebody was so good as to step in and pay the money—something and fourpence was the amount; I forget the pounds and shillings, but I know it ended with fourpence, because it struck me at the time as being so odd that I could owe anybody fourpence—and after that I brought them together.", "파운드와 실링은 잊어버렸지만, 사 펜스로 끝났다는 건 알아요. 내가 누구에게 사 펜스를 빚질 수 있다는 게 그때 어찌나 묘하게 여겨지던지요."], ["Vholes asked me for the introduction, and I gave it.", "그 뒤에 내가 그 둘을 맺어 준 겁니다. 볼스가 나에게 소개를 청했고, 나는 해 주었지요."], ["Now I come to think of it,”", "그러고 보니 말입니다,\""], ["he looked inquiringly at us with his frankest smile as he made the discovery, “Vholes bribed me, perhaps?", "그가 그 사실을 발견하고는 더없이 솔직한 미소를 띤 채 미심쩍은 눈으로 우리를 보며 말했습니다. \"볼스가 나에게 뇌물을 줬는지도 모르겠군요?"], ["He gave me something and called it commission.", "그가 나에게 무언가를 주면서 그걸 수수료라고 불렀거든요."], ["Was it a five-pound note?", "그게 오 파운드짜리 지폐였던가?"], ["Do you know, I think it MUST have been a five-pound note!”", "글쎄요, 아무래도 오 파운드짜리 지폐였던 게 분명한 것 같은데요!\""]], [["His further consideration of the point was prevented by Richard’s coming back to us in an excited state and hastily representing Mr.", "이 점을 더 곱씹어 보려던 그의 생각은, 리처드가 흥분한 채 우리에게 돌아와 황급히 볼스 씨를 소개하는 바람에 끊겼습니다."], ["Vholes—a sallow man with pinched lips that looked as if they were cold, a red eruption here and there upon his face, tall and thin, about fifty years of age, high-shouldered, and stooping.", "볼스 씨는 추워서 그런 듯한 얇은 입술을 가진 누르께한 사내로, 얼굴 여기저기에 붉은 발진이 돋아 있었고, 키가 크고 마른 데다, 나이는 쉰쯤 되어 보였으며, 어깨가 솟고 등이 굽어 있었습니다."], ["Dressed in black, black-gloved, and buttoned to the chin, there was nothing so remarkable in him as a lifeless manner and a slow, fixed way he had of looking at Richard.", "검은 옷을 입고 검은 장갑을 끼고 턱까지 단추를 채운 그에게서, 생기 없는 태도와 리처드를 바라보는 느릿하고 고정된 그 눈길만큼 두드러진 것은 없었습니다."]], [["“I hope I don’t disturb you, ladies,” said Mr.", "\"숙녀분들, 방해가 되지 않았으면 합니다.\""], ["Vholes, and now I observed that he was further remarkable for an inward manner of speaking. “I arranged with Mr.", "볼스 씨가 말했고, 이제 나는 그가 또 한 가지, 안으로 삼키듯 말하는 화법이 두드러진다는 것을 알아챘습니다."], ["Carstone that he should always know when his cause was in the Chancellor’s paper, and being informed by one of my clerks last night after post time that it stood, rather unexpectedly, in the paper for to-morrow, I put myself into the coach early this morning and came down to confer with him.”", "\"나는 카스톤 씨와, 그분 사건이 대법관의 처리 목록에 오를 때마다 늘 그분이 알도록 하기로 약속해 두었습니다. 그런데 어젯밤 우편 마감 시각이 지난 뒤 서기 하나에게서 그 사건이 뜻밖에도 내일 목록에 올랐다는 보고를 받고는, 오늘 아침 일찍 마차에 몸을 싣고 그분과 의논하러 내려온 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Richard, flushed, and looking triumphantly at Ada and me, “we don’t do these things in the old slow way now.", "리처드가 상기된 얼굴로 에이다와 나를 의기양양하게 바라보며 말했습니다. \"이제 우리는 예전의 그 굼뜬 방식으로 이런 일을 하지 않아."], ["We spin along now! Mr.", "이제 우린 속도를 낸다고!"], ["Vholes, we must hire something to get over to the post town in, and catch the mail to-night, and go up by it!”", "볼스 씨, 우리는 무얼 좀 빌려서 우편 마차가 다니는 읍내까지 건너가, 오늘 밤 우편 마차를 잡아타고 올라가야겠습니다!\""]], [["“Anything you please, sir,”", "\"무엇이든 분부대로 하지요, 선생.\""], ["returned Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 대답했습니다."], ["“I am quite at your service.”", "\"저는 전적으로 선생을 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Let me see,”", "\"어디 보자.\""], ["said Richard, looking at his watch.", "리처드가 시계를 보며 말했습니다."], ["“If I run down to the Dedlock, and get my portmanteau fastened up, and order a gig, or a chaise, or whatever’s to be got, we shall have an hour then before starting.", "\"내가 데드록 암스로 달려 내려가서 여행 가방을 단단히 꾸리고, 이륜마차든 사륜마차든 구할 수 있는 걸 부르고 나면, 출발하기까지 한 시간쯤 여유가 있겠군."], ["I’ll come back to tea.", "차 마실 때 맞춰 돌아올게."], ["Cousin Ada, will you and Esther take care of Mr. Vholes when I am gone?”", "사촌 에이다, 내가 없는 동안 너랑 에스더가 볼스 씨를 잘 보살펴 주겠니?\""]], [["He was away directly, in his heat and hurry, and was soon lost in the dusk of evening.", "그는 열기와 조바심에 휩싸여 곧장 떠났고, 이내 저물어 가는 어스름 속으로 사라졌습니다."], ["We who were left walked on towards the house.", "남은 우리는 집 쪽으로 걸어갔습니다."]], [["“Is Mr. Carstone’s presence necessary to-morrow, Sir?”", "\"내일 카스톤 씨가 꼭 가야 하나요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“Can it do any good?”", "\"그게 무슨 도움이 될까요?\""]], [["“No, miss,” Mr.", "\"아니요, 아가씨.\""], ["Vholes replied.", "볼스 씨가 대답했습니다."], ["“I am not aware that it can.”", "\"그게 도움이 되리라고는 보지 않습니다.\""]], [["Both Ada and I expressed our regret that he should go, then, only to be disappointed.", "그렇다면 그가 가 봐야 실망만 하게 될 텐데 가는 것이 아쉽다고, 에이다도 나도 안타까운 마음을 내비쳤습니다."]], [["“Mr. Carstone has laid down the principle of watching his own interests,”", "\"카스톤 씨가 자기 이익은 자기가 지킨다는 원칙을 세우셨지요.\""], ["said Mr.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Vholes, “and when a client lays down his own principle, and it is not immoral, it devolves upon me to carry it out.", "\"의뢰인이 자기 원칙을 세우고, 그게 부도덕한 것이 아니라면, 그것을 실행에 옮기는 것이 제 본분입니다."], ["I wish in business to be exact and open.", "저는 일에서 정확하고 떳떳하기를 바랍니다."], ["I am a widower with three daughters—Emma, Jane, and Caroline—and my desire is so to discharge the duties of life as to leave them a good name.", "저는 홀아비이고 딸이 셋—에마, 제인, 캐럴라인—있는데, 제 바람은 이 세상의 도리를 다하여 그 아이들에게 좋은 이름을 남겨 주는 것입니다."], ["This appears to be a pleasant spot, miss.”", "여긴 기분 좋은 곳이로군요, 아가씨.\""]], [["The remark being made to me in consequence of my being next him as we walked, I assented and enumerated its chief attractions.", "걷는 동안 내가 그의 옆에 있던 까닭에 그 말이 나에게 건네진 것이라, 나는 맞장구를 치며 이곳의 주요한 볼거리들을 꼽아 주었습니다."]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그렇군요?\""], ["said Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I have the privilege of supporting an aged father in the Vale of Taunton—his native place—and I admire that country very much.", "\"저는 토턴 골짜기에서—그분이 나고 자란 곳이지요—연로하신 아버지를 모시는 영광을 누리고 있는데, 그 고장을 무척 좋아한답니다."], ["I had no idea there was anything so attractive here.”", "이곳에 이토록 매력적인 데가 있는 줄은 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["To keep up the conversation, I asked Mr. Vholes if he would like to live altogether in the country.", "대화를 이어 가려고, 나는 볼스 씨에게 아예 시골에서 살고 싶으냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“There, miss,”", "\"그게 말입니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said he, “you touch me on a tender string.", "그가 말했습니다. \"아가씨가 제 아픈 데를 건드리시는군요."], ["My health is not good (my digestion being much impaired), and if I had only myself to consider, I should take refuge in rural habits, especially as the cares of business have prevented me from ever coming much into contact with general society, and particularly with ladies’ society, which I have most wished to mix in.", "제 건강이 좋지 못하고(소화가 많이 상해서요), 만약 저 하나만 생각하면 되는 처지라면 시골살이에 몸을 맡길 겁니다. 더구나 일에 매인 탓에 여태껏 일반적인 사교계에, 그중에서도 제가 가장 어울리고 싶었던 숙녀분들의 모임에 좀처럼 끼어 보지 못했으니 더욱 그렇지요."], ["But with my three daughters, Emma, Jane, and Caroline—and my aged father—I cannot afford to be selfish.", "하지만 제 세 딸 에마, 제인, 캐럴라인이 있고, 또 연로하신 아버지가 계시니, 저는 이기적으로 굴 형편이 못 됩니다."], ["It is true I have no longer to maintain a dear grandmother who died in her hundred and second year, but enough remains to render it indispensable that the mill should be always going.”", "사실 백둘에 돌아가신 사랑하는 할머니를 이제 더는 모시지 않아도 되긴 하지만, 그래도 방앗간을 늘 돌려야 할 만큼은 식구가 남아 있지요.\""]], [["It required some attention to hear him on account of his inward speaking and his lifeless manner.", "안으로 삼키듯 말하는 화법과 생기 없는 태도 탓에 그의 말을 알아들으려면 어지간히 신경을 써야 했습니다."]], [["“You will excuse my having mentioned my daughters,”", "\"제 딸들 이야기를 꺼낸 걸 너그러이 봐주십시오.\" 그가 말했습니다."], ["he said. “They are my weak point.", "\"그 아이들이 제 약점이거든요."], ["I wish to leave the poor girls some little independence, as well as a good name.”", "그 가엾은 것들에게 좋은 이름뿐 아니라 자그마한 독립의 밑천도 남겨 주고 싶답니다.\""]], [["We now arrived at Mr. Boythorn’s house, where the tea-table, all prepared, was awaiting us.", "이윽고 우리는 보이손 씨의 집에 다다랐는데, 차상이 죄다 차려진 채 우리를 기다리고 있었습니다."], ["Richard came in restless and hurried shortly afterwards, and leaning over Mr. Vholes’s chair, whispered something in his ear.", "리처드가 안절부절못하며 서둘러 들어온 것은 그 직후였고, 그는 볼스 씨의 의자 위로 몸을 숙여 그의 귀에 무어라 속삭였습니다."], ["Mr. Vholes replied aloud—or as nearly aloud I suppose as he had ever replied to anything—“You will drive me, will you, sir?", "볼스 씨는 소리 내어—아마 그가 무엇에든 답할 때 낼 수 있는 가장 큰 소리에 가깝게—대답했습니다."], ["It is all the same to me, sir.", "\"선생이 저를 태워다 주시겠다는 겁니까? 저야 아무래도 좋습니다, 선생."], ["Anything you please.", "무엇이든 분부대로 하지요."], ["I am quite at your service.”", "저는 전적으로 선생을 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["We understood from what followed that Mr. Skimpole was to be left until the morning to occupy the two places which had been already paid for.", "이어진 말로 보아, 우리는 스킴폴 씨가 이미 값을 치러 둔 두 자리를 차지하도록 아침까지 남겨 두기로 했다는 것을 알았습니다."], ["As Ada and I were both in low spirits concerning Richard and very sorry so to part with him, we made it as plain as we politely could that we should leave Mr. Skimpole to the Dedlock Arms and retire when the night-travellers were gone.", "에이다도 나도 리처드 때문에 마음이 가라앉아 있었고 그와 그렇게 헤어지는 것이 무척 안타까웠던 터라, 우리는 정중하게나마 분명히, 스킴폴 씨는 데드록 암스에 맡겨 두고 밤길을 떠나는 이들이 가고 나면 우리는 물러나겠다는 뜻을 밝혔습니다."]], [["Richard’s high spirits carrying everything before them, we all went out together to the top of the hill above the village, where he had ordered a gig to wait and where we found a man with a lantern standing at the head of the gaunt pale horse that had been harnessed to it.", "리처드의 들뜬 기분이 만사를 휩쓸어 버리는 통에, 우리는 모두 함께 마을 위쪽 언덕 꼭대기로 나갔습니다. 그가 이륜마차를 대기시켜 두라고 일러 둔 곳이었는데, 거기서 우리는 거기 매인 비쩍 마른 창백한 말의 머리맡에 등불을 든 사내 하나가 서 있는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["I never shall forget those two seated side by side in the lantern’s light, Richard all flush and fire and laughter, with the reins in his hand; Mr.", "등불 빛 속에 나란히 앉은 그 두 사람을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것입니다."], ["Vholes quite still, black-gloved, and buttoned up, looking at him as if he were looking at his prey and charming it.", "리처드는 온통 상기되고, 불타오르고, 웃음을 머금은 채 고삐를 손에 쥐고 있었고, 볼스 씨는 검은 장갑을 끼고 단추를 꼭 채운 채 미동도 없이, 마치 제 먹잇감을 바라보며 홀리기라도 하듯 그를 바라보고 있었습니다."], ["I have before me the whole picture of the warm dark night, the summer lightning, the dusty track of road closed in by hedgerows and high trees, the gaunt pale horse with his ears pricked up, and the driving away at speed to Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "나는 지금도 그 풍경을 통째로 눈앞에 그릴 수 있습니다. 따뜻하고 어두운 밤, 여름 번개, 산울타리와 키 큰 나무들에 에워싸인 먼지투성이 길, 귀를 쫑긋 세운 비쩍 마른 창백한 말, 그리고 잔다이스 대 잔다이스를 향해 쏜살같이 내달려 가던 그 모습을 말입니다."]], [["My dear girl told me that night how Richard’s being thereafter prosperous or ruined, befriended or deserted, could only make this difference to her, that the more he needed love from one unchanging heart, the more love that unchanging heart would have to give him; how he thought of her through his present errors, and she would think of him at all times—never of herself if she could devote herself to him, never of her own delights if she could minister to his.", "내 사랑하는 사람은 그날 밤 나에게 이렇게 말했습니다. 앞으로 리처드가 번창하든 파멸하든, 따르는 이가 있든 버림받든, 자기에게는 단 한 가지만 달라질 뿐이라고요. 그가 변치 않는 한 마음의 사랑을 더 필요로 할수록, 그 변치 않는 한 마음이 그에게 줄 사랑은 더욱 커지리라는 것이었습니다. 그는 지금의 잘못 속에서도 그녀를 생각해 주었으니, 그녀는 언제나 그를 생각하겠다고, 그에게 자신을 바칠 수만 있다면 결코 자기 자신은 생각하지 않겠다고, 그를 보살필 수만 있다면 결코 자기만의 기쁨은 생각하지 않겠다고 말입니다."]], [["And she kept her word?", "그리고 그녀는 그 약속을 지켰을까요?"]], [["I look along the road before me, where the distance already shortens and the journey’s end is growing visible; and true and good above the dead sea of the Chancery suit and all the ashy fruit it cast ashore, I think I see my darling.", "나는 내 앞에 놓인 길을 따라 멀리 바라봅니다. 그 길에서 이미 거리가 좁혀지고 여정의 끝이 보이기 시작합니다."], ["A Struggle", "그리고 대법원 소송이라는 죽음의 바다와 그것이 물가로 밀어낸 온갖 잿빛 열매들 위로, 참되고 선하게 떠오른 내 사랑하는 사람의 모습이 보이는 듯합니다."]], [["When our time came for returning to Bleak House again, we were punctual to the day and were received with an overpowering welcome.", "우리가 다시 황폐한 집으로 돌아갈 때가 되자, 우리는 정한 날짜를 어김없이 지켰고 압도적인 환영을 받았습니다."], ["I was perfectly restored to health and strength, and finding my housekeeping keys laid ready for me in my room, rang myself in as if I had been a new year, with a merry little peal.", "저는 건강과 기운을 완전히 되찾았고, 제 방에 살림 열쇠가 준비되어 놓인 것을 발견하고는, 마치 제가 새로 시작되는 한 해이기라도 한 양 명랑하게 작은 종소리를 울리며 저 자신을 종처럼 울렸습니다."], ["“Once more, duty, duty, Esther,”", "\"다시 한번, 의무, 의무야, 에스더.\""], ["said I; “and if you are not overjoyed to do it, more than cheerfully and contentedly, through anything and everything, you ought to be.", "저는 말했습니다. \"그리고 네가 그 일을 하면서 무슨 일이 있어도 더없이 기쁘고 만족스럽게, 더할 나위 없이 기뻐하며 하지 않는다면, 너는 마땅히 그래야 해."], ["That’s all I have to say to you, my dear!”", "내가 너에게 할 말은 그게 다란다, 얘야!\""]], [["The first few mornings were mornings of so much bustle and business, devoted to such settlements of accounts, such repeated journeys to and fro between the growlery and all other parts of the house, so many rearrangements of drawers and presses, and such a general new beginning altogether, that I had not a moment’s leisure.", "처음 며칠 아침은 어찌나 부산하고 분주한 아침이었던지, 회계 정산이며, 으르렁 방과 집 안 곳곳을 오가는 거듭된 발걸음이며, 서랍과 장롱을 수없이 다시 정리하는 일이며, 모든 것을 새로 시작하는 전반적인 일에 매달린 나머지 저는 한순간도 한가할 틈이 없었습니다."], ["But when these arrangements were completed and everything was in order, I paid a visit of a few hours to London, which something in the letter I had destroyed at Chesney Wold had induced me to decide upon in my own mind.", "그러나 이 정리가 다 끝나고 모든 것이 제자리를 잡자, 저는 몇 시간 동안 런던을 다녀왔는데, 체스니 월드에서 없애버린 그 편지 속의 무언가가 제 마음속에서 그렇게 하기로 결심하게 만들었던 것입니다."]], [["I made Caddy Jellyby—her maiden name was so natural to me that I always called her by it—the pretext for this visit and wrote her a note previously asking the favour of her company on a little business expedition.", "저는 캐디 젤리비를—그녀의 처녀 적 이름이 저에게는 어찌나 자연스럽던지 저는 늘 그 이름으로 그녀를 불렀습니다—이 방문의 구실로 삼아, 미리 그녀에게 쪽지를 써서 작은 볼일 나들이에 동행해주는 호의를 청했습니다."], ["Leaving home very early in the morning, I got to London by stage-coach in such good time that I got to Newman Street with the day before me.", "아침 아주 일찍 집을 떠나 역마차로 런던에 닿았는데, 어찌나 일찍 도착했던지 하루를 통째로 앞에 둔 채 뉴먼 거리에 이르렀습니다."]], [["Caddy, who had not seen me since her wedding-day, was so glad and so affectionate that I was half inclined to fear I should make her husband jealous.", "결혼식 날 이후로 저를 보지 못한 캐디는 어찌나 반가워하고 어찌나 다정하던지, 저는 그녀의 남편을 질투 나게 만드는 게 아닐까 슬그머니 걱정될 지경이었습니다."], ["But he was, in his way, just as bad—I mean as good; and in short it was the old story, and nobody would leave me any possibility of doing anything meritorious.", "그러나 그도 제 나름으로는 꼭 그만큼이나 나빴습니다—제 말은, 그만큼이나 좋았다는 뜻입니다. 한마디로 늘 똑같은 이야기였고, 누구도 제가 무언가 칭찬받을 만한 일을 할 가능성을 남겨주지 않았습니다."]], [["The elder Mr. Turveydrop was in bed, I found, and Caddy was milling his chocolate, which a melancholy little boy who was an apprentice—it seemed such a curious thing to be apprenticed to the trade of dancing—was waiting to carry upstairs.", "알고 보니 늙은 터비드롭 씨는 침대에 누워 있었고, 캐디는 그의 초콜릿을 휘젓고 있었으며, 견습생인 우울한 어린 소년이—춤을 추는 직업에 견습으로 들어간다는 게 어찌나 묘한 일로 여겨지던지—그것을 위층으로 나르려고 기다리고 있었습니다."], ["Her father-in-law was extremely kind and considerate, Caddy told me, and they lived most happily together.", "시아버지는 더없이 친절하고 사려 깊으며, 그들은 더할 나위 없이 행복하게 함께 산다고 캐디가 저에게 말했습니다."], ["(When she spoke of their living together, she meant that the old gentleman had all the good things and all the good lodging, while she and her husband had what they could get, and were poked into two corner rooms over the Mews.)", "(그들이 함께 산다고 말할 때, 그녀가 뜻한 바는, 그 노신사가 온갖 좋은 것과 온갖 좋은 거처를 다 차지하고, 그녀와 그녀의 남편은 얻을 수 있는 것만 가지고 마구간 위 구석방 두 칸에 처박혀 지낸다는 것이었습니다.)"]], [["“And how is your mama, Caddy?”", "\"그래, 어머님은 어떻게 지내시니, 캐디?\""], ["said I.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, I hear of her, Esther,”", "\"글쎄, 아버지를 통해 소식은 듣지, 에스더.\""], ["replied Caddy, “through Pa, but I see very little of her.", "캐디가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 직접 뵙는 일은 거의 없어."], ["We are good friends, I am glad to say, but Ma thinks there is something absurd in my having married a dancing-master, and she is rather afraid of its extending to her.”", "우리는 사이좋게 지내, 다행스럽게도 말이야. 그래도 엄마는 내가 무용 선생과 결혼한 게 좀 어이없다고 생각하시고, 그게 당신한테까지 옮을까 봐 꽤 두려워하셔.\""]], [["It struck me that if Mrs.", "저는 이런 생각이 들었습니다."], ["Jellyby had discharged her own natural duties and obligations before she swept the horizon with a telescope in search of others, she would have taken the best precautions against becoming absurd, but I need scarcely observe that I kept this to myself.", "만약 젤리비 부인이 망원경으로 지평선을 훑으며 남의 일을 찾아 나서기 전에 자기 본연의 의무와 책임부터 다했더라면, 어이없는 사람이 되는 것에 대한 최선의 예방책을 마련했으리라고 말입니다. 하지만 제가 이 생각을 마음속에만 담아두었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요."]], [["“And your papa, Caddy?”", "\"그럼 아버님은, 캐디?\""]], [["“He comes here every evening,”", "\"아버지는 저녁마다 여기 오셔.\""], ["returned Caddy, “and is so fond of sitting in the corner there that it’s a treat to see him.”", "캐디가 대답했습니다. \"저기 저 구석에 앉아 계시는 걸 어찌나 좋아하시는지, 그 모습을 보는 것만으로도 즐거워.\""]], [["Looking at the corner, I plainly perceived the mark of Mr. Jellyby’s head against the wall.", "구석을 바라보니, 벽에 젤리비 씨의 머리 자국이 또렷하게 박혀 있는 것이 보였습니다."], ["It was consolatory to know that he had found such a resting-place for it.", "그가 머리를 기댈 그런 안식처를 찾아냈다는 것을 알게 되어 마음이 위로가 되었습니다."]], [["“And you, Caddy,”", "\"그럼 너는, 캐디.\""], ["said I, “you are always busy, I’ll be bound?”", "제가 말했습니다. \"분명히 늘 바쁘겠지?\""]], [["“Well, my dear,”", "\"글쎄, 얘야.\" 캐디가 대답했습니다."], ["returned Caddy, “I am indeed, for to tell you a grand secret, I am qualifying myself to give lessons.", "\"정말 그래. 너한테 굉장한 비밀 하나를 털어놓자면, 나는 직접 수업을 가르칠 자격을 갖추려고 준비하는 중이거든."], ["Prince’s health is not strong, and I want to be able to assist him.", "프린스의 건강이 튼튼하질 못해서, 나는 그를 도울 수 있으면 좋겠어."], ["What with schools, and classes here, and private pupils, AND the apprentices, he really has too much to do, poor fellow!”", "학교들이며, 여기서 하는 수업이며, 개인 교습이며, 게다가 견습생들까지, 그이는 정말이지 할 일이 너무 많아, 가엾은 사람!\""]], [["The notion of the apprentices was still so odd to me that I asked Caddy if there were many of them.", "견습생이라는 개념이 저에게는 여전히 너무 묘해서, 저는 캐디에게 견습생이 많으냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Four,”", "\"넷이야.\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."], ["“One in-door, and three out.", "\"한 명은 집 안에, 셋은 바깥에."], ["They are very good children; only when they get together they WILL play—children-like—instead of attending to their work.", "다들 아주 착한 아이들이야. 다만 함께 모이기만 하면—아이들이 다 그렇듯이—일에 집중하지 않고 놀려고만 들지."], ["So the little boy you saw just now waltzes by himself in the empty kitchen, and we distribute the others over the house as well as we can.”", "그래서 방금 네가 본 그 어린 소년은 텅 빈 부엌에서 혼자 왈츠를 추고, 나머지는 우리가 할 수 있는 한 집 안 여기저기에 흩어놓아.\""]], [["“That is only for their steps, of course?”", "\"물론 스텝 연습만 하려는 거겠지?\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Only for their steps,”", "\"스텝 연습만.\""], ["said Caddy.", "캐디가 말했습니다."], ["“In that way they practise, so many hours at a time, whatever steps they happen to be upon.", "\"그런 식으로 그 애들은 한 번에 몇 시간씩, 그때그때 배우는 스텝이 무엇이든 연습해."], ["They dance in the academy, and at this time of year we do figures at five every morning.”", "무용 교습소에서 춤을 추고, 이맘때면 우리는 매일 아침 다섯 시에 군무 동작을 연습하지.\""]], [["“Why, what a laborious life!”", "\"세상에, 어찌나 고된 삶인지!\""], ["I exclaimed.", "저는 탄성을 질렀습니다."]], [["“I assure you, my dear,”", "\"정말이야, 얘야.\""], ["returned Caddy, smiling, “when the out-door apprentices ring us up in the morning (the bell rings into our room, not to disturb old Mr.", "캐디가 미소 지으며 대답했습니다."], ["Turveydrop), and when I put up the window and see them standing on the door-step with their little pumps under their arms, I am actually reminded of the Sweeps.”", "\"아침에 바깥 견습생들이 우리를 깨우려고 벨을 울리면(벨은 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 방해하지 않도록 우리 방으로 울리게 되어 있어), 내가 창문을 올리고 그 애들이 작은 무용화를 겨드랑이에 끼고 현관 계단에 서 있는 걸 보면, 정말이지 굴뚝 청소부들이 떠올라.\""]], [["All this presented the art to me in a singular light, to be sure.", "이 모든 것이 분명 그 예술을 저에게 묘한 빛으로 비추어 보였습니다."], ["Caddy enjoyed the effect of her communication and cheerfully recounted the particulars of her own studies.", "캐디는 자기 이야기가 일으킨 효과를 즐기며, 자기 공부에 관한 세세한 내용을 명랑하게 늘어놓았습니다."]], [["“You see, my dear, to save expense I ought to know something of the piano, and I ought to know something of the kit too, and consequently I have to practise those two instruments as well as the details of our profession.", "\"있잖아, 얘야, 비용을 아끼려면 나는 피아노도 좀 알아야 하고, 키트도 좀 알아야 해. 그래서 우리 일의 세세한 부분뿐 아니라 그 두 악기도 연습해야 하지."], ["If Ma had been like anybody else, I might have had some little musical knowledge to begin upon.", "엄마가 다른 사람들처럼 평범했더라면, 시작할 때 작은 음악 지식이라도 갖추고 있었을 텐데."], ["However, I hadn’t any; and that part of the work is, at first, a little discouraging, I must allow.", "하지만 나는 아무것도 없었어. 그래서 그 부분이 처음에는 좀 맥이 빠진다는 걸 인정해야겠어."], ["But I have a very good ear, and I am used to drudgery—I have to thank Ma for that, at all events—and where there’s a will there’s a way, you know, Esther, the world over.”", "그래도 나는 귀가 아주 좋고, 고된 일에는 익숙하거든—어쨌든 그건 엄마한테 고마워해야 할 일이지—그리고 너도 알다시피, 에스더, 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있는 법이잖아, 온 세상 어디서나.\""], ["Saying these words, Caddy laughingly sat down at a little jingling square piano and really rattled off a quadrille with great spirit.", "이렇게 말하며 캐디는 웃으면서 작고 딸랑거리는 사각 피아노 앞에 앉더니, 정말로 카드리유 한 곡을 아주 활기차게 우르르 쏟아냈습니다."], ["Then she good-humouredly and blushingly got up again, and while she still laughed herself, said, “Don’t laugh at me, please; that’s a dear girl!”", "그러고는 기분 좋게 얼굴을 붉히며 다시 일어나, 여전히 스스로 웃으면서 말했습니다. \"제발 나를 비웃지 마, 착하지!\""]], [["I would sooner have cried, but I did neither.", "저는 차라리 울고 싶었지만, 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않았습니다."], ["I encouraged her and praised her with all my heart.", "저는 진심을 다해 그녀를 격려하고 칭찬했습니다."], ["For I conscientiously believed, dancing-master’s wife though she was, and dancing-mistress though in her limited ambition she aspired to be, she had struck out a natural, wholesome, loving course of industry and perseverance that was quite as good as a mission.", "그녀가 무용 선생의 아내이고, 또 소박한 야심 속에서 무용 여선생이 되기를 꿈꾼다 하더라도, 그녀는 자연스럽고 건전하며 사랑이 깃든 근면과 끈기의 길을 스스로 개척해냈으며, 그것이 어떤 사명 못지않게 훌륭하다고 저는 양심에 비추어 믿었기 때문입니다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘야.\""], ["said Caddy, delighted, “you can’t think how you cheer me.", "캐디가 기뻐하며 말했습니다. \"네가 나를 얼마나 북돋아주는지 너는 모를 거야."], ["I shall owe you, you don’t know how much.", "너한테 얼마나 신세를 지는지, 너는 짐작도 못 할걸."], ["What changes, Esther, even in my small world!", "어쩜 이렇게 변했을까, 에스더, 내 작은 세상 안에서조차도 말이야!"], ["You recollect that first night, when I was so unpolite and inky?", "그 첫날 밤, 내가 그렇게 무례하고 잉크투성이였던 거 기억하지?"], ["Who would have thought, then, of my ever teaching people to dance, of all other possibilities and impossibilities!”", "그때 누가 생각이나 했겠어, 다른 온갖 가능한 일이며 불가능한 일을 다 제쳐두고 하필이면 내가 사람들에게 춤을 가르치게 될 줄을!\""]], [["Her husband, who had left us while we had this chat, now coming back, preparatory to exercising the apprentices in the ball-room, Caddy informed me she was quite at my disposal.", "우리가 이렇게 담소를 나누는 동안 자리를 떴던 그녀의 남편이, 무도실에서 견습생들을 가르칠 준비를 하러 이제 막 돌아오자, 캐디는 자기가 온전히 제 뜻에 따르겠다고 알렸습니다."], ["But it was not my time yet, I was glad to tell her, for I should have been vexed to take her away then.", "그러나 아직 제 시간은 아니었기에, 저는 그녀에게 그렇게 말할 수 있어 다행이었습니다. 지금 그녀를 데려간다면 마음이 편치 않았을 테니까요."], ["Therefore we three adjourned to the apprentices together, and I made one in the dance.", "그래서 우리 셋은 함께 견습생들에게로 옮겨 갔고, 저도 춤에 한몫 끼었습니다."]], [["The apprentices were the queerest little people.", "견습생들은 더없이 기묘한 꼬마들이었습니다."], ["Besides the melancholy boy, who, I hoped, had not been made so by waltzing alone in the empty kitchen, there were two other boys and one dirty little limp girl in a gauzy dress.", "그 우울한 소년 말고도—텅 빈 부엌에서 혼자 왈츠를 추다가 그렇게 된 건 아니기를 저는 바랐습니다—다른 두 소년과, 얇은 망사 드레스를 입은 지저분하고 축 늘어진 어린 소녀가 하나 있었습니다."], ["Such a precocious little girl, with such a dowdy bonnet on (that, too, of a gauzy texture), who brought her sandalled shoes in an old threadbare velvet reticule.", "어찌나 조숙한 어린 소녀였던지, 어찌나 촌스러운 보닛을(그것 역시 얇은 망사 천이었습니다) 쓰고 있었던지, 게다가 닳아빠진 낡은 벨벳 손가방에 샌들 신발을 넣어 들고 다녔습니다."], ["Such mean little boys, when they were not dancing, with string, and marbles, and cramp-bones in their pockets, and the most untidy legs and feet—and heels particularly.", "어찌나 보잘것없는 어린 소년들이었던지, 춤을 추지 않을 때는 주머니에 끈이며, 구슬이며, 공기놀이 뼈를 넣어두고, 다리와 발이—특히 발뒤꿈치가—더없이 지저분했습니다."]], [["I asked Caddy what had made their parents choose this profession for them.", "저는 캐디에게 무엇 때문에 그 아이들의 부모가 이 직업을 골라주었는지 물었습니다."], ["Caddy said she didn’t know; perhaps they were designed for teachers, perhaps for the stage.", "캐디는 모르겠다고 했습니다. 어쩌면 선생이 되게 하려는 것일 수도, 어쩌면 무대에 세우려는 것일 수도 있다고요."], ["They were all people in humble circumstances, and the melancholy boy’s mother kept a ginger-beer shop.", "다들 형편이 변변치 못한 사람들이었고, 그 우울한 소년의 어머니는 생강맥주 가게를 운영했습니다."]], [["We danced for an hour with great gravity, the melancholy child doing wonders with his lower extremities, in which there appeared to be some sense of enjoyment though it never rose above his waist.", "우리는 한 시간 동안 자못 엄숙하게 춤을 추었는데, 그 우울한 아이는 제 하반신으로 놀라운 솜씨를 부렸고, 거기에는 어떤 즐거움의 감각이 깃든 듯했으나 그 즐거움이 결코 허리 위로는 올라오지 않았습니다."], ["Caddy, while she was observant of her husband and was evidently founded upon him, had acquired a grace and self-possession of her own, which, united to her pretty face and figure, was uncommonly agreeable.", "캐디는 남편을 눈여겨 살피며 분명히 그를 본보기 삼고 있었지만, 그러면서도 자기만의 우아함과 침착함을 익혀냈고, 그것이 그녀의 예쁜 얼굴과 자태와 어우러져 보기 드물게 흐뭇했습니다."], ["She already relieved him of much of the instruction of these young people, and he seldom interfered except to walk his part in the figure if he had anything to do in it.", "그녀는 이미 이 어린 사람들을 가르치는 일의 상당 부분을 남편 대신 맡고 있었고, 남편은 군무에서 자기가 할 일이 있을 때 자기 자리를 거니는 것 말고는 좀처럼 끼어들지 않았습니다."], ["He always played the tune.", "곡은 늘 그가 연주했습니다."], ["The affectation of the gauzy child, and her condescension to the boys, was a sight.", "망사 옷을 입은 아이의 거드름과, 소년들을 깔보는 태도란 정말 볼만했습니다."], ["And thus we danced an hour by the clock.", "그렇게 우리는 시계로 한 시간 동안 춤을 추었습니다."]], [["When the practice was concluded, Caddy’s husband made himself ready to go out of town to a school, and Caddy ran away to get ready to go out with me.", "연습이 끝나자, 캐디의 남편은 어느 학교로 나갈 채비를 했고, 캐디는 저와 함께 나갈 준비를 하러 뛰어갔습니다."], ["I sat in the ball-room in the interval, contemplating the apprentices.", "저는 그동안 무도실에 앉아 견습생들을 바라보았습니다."], ["The two out-door boys went upon the staircase to put on their half-boots and pull the in-door boy’s hair, as I judged from the nature of his objections.", "바깥의 두 소년은 계단으로 가서 반장화를 신고는, 그 항의의 성격으로 미루어 짐작하건대 집 안 소년의 머리카락을 잡아당겼습니다."], ["Returning with their jackets buttoned and their pumps stuck in them, they then produced packets of cold bread and meat and bivouacked under a painted lyre on the wall.", "재킷 단추를 채우고 무용화를 그 안에 찔러 넣은 채 돌아온 그들은, 차가운 빵과 고기 꾸러미를 꺼내 벽에 그려진 리라 그림 아래에 진을 쳤습니다."], ["The little gauzy child, having whisked her sandals into the reticule and put on a trodden-down pair of shoes, shook her head into the dowdy bonnet at one shake, and answering my inquiry whether she liked dancing by replying, “Not with boys,” tied it across her chin, and went home contemptuous.", "망사 옷을 입은 어린 소녀는 샌들을 후딱 손가방 안에 던져 넣고 뒤축이 닳은 신발 한 켤레를 신더니, 머리를 한 번 흔들어 촌스러운 보닛 속으로 집어넣고는, 춤추는 걸 좋아하느냐는 제 물음에 \"남자애들이랑은 싫어요\"라고 대답하면서 보닛을 턱 아래로 매고, 멸시하는 태도로 집에 갔습니다."]], [["“Old Mr. Turveydrop is so sorry,”", "\"늙은 터비드롭 씨가 무척 안타까워하셔.\""], ["said Caddy, “that he has not finished dressing yet and cannot have the pleasure of seeing you before you go.", "캐디가 말했습니다. \"아직 옷 단장을 다 못 마치셔서, 네가 가기 전에 뵙는 기쁨을 누리지 못하신다고 말이야."], ["You are such a favourite of his, Esther.”", "너는 그분이 그렇게 아끼는 사람이거든, 에스더.\""]], [["I expressed myself much obliged to him, but did not think it necessary to add that I readily dispensed with this attention.", "저는 그분께 무척 감사하다는 뜻을 표했지만, 이런 배려쯤은 기꺼이 없어도 된다는 말까지 덧붙일 필요는 없다고 생각했습니다."]], [["“It takes him a long time to dress,”", "\"그분은 옷 단장에 오래 걸려.\" 캐디가 말했습니다."], ["said Caddy, “because he is very much looked up to in such things, you know, and has a reputation to support.", "\"왜냐하면, 너도 알다시피, 그런 일에서는 사람들이 그분을 무척 우러러보고, 그분도 지켜야 할 명성이 있으니까."], ["You can’t think how kind he is to Pa.", "그분이 아버지한테 얼마나 친절하신지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야."], ["He talks to Pa of an evening about the Prince Regent, and I never saw Pa so interested.”", "저녁이면 아버지한테 섭정 왕세자 이야기를 들려주시는데, 나는 아버지가 그렇게 흥미로워하시는 걸 본 적이 없어.\""]], [["There was something in the picture of Mr. Turveydrop bestowing his deportment on Mr.", "터비드롭 씨가 젤리비 씨에게 자신의 몸가짐을 베푸는 그림에는, 제 마음을 사로잡는 무언가가 있었습니다."], ["Jellyby that quite took my fancy. I asked Caddy if he brought her papa out much.", "저는 캐디에게 그분이 아버지를 자주 데리고 나가시느냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["said Caddy, “I don’t know that he does that, but he talks to Pa, and Pa greatly admires him, and listens, and likes it.", "캐디가 말했습니다. \"그러시는 것 같지는 않아."], ["Of course I am aware that Pa has hardly any claims to deportment, but they get on together delightfully.", "하지만 아버지한테 이야기를 들려주시고, 아버지는 그분을 무척 우러러보며 귀 기울여 들으시고, 또 그걸 좋아하셔."], ["You can’t think what good companions they make.", "물론 아버지가 몸가짐에 대해선 거의 자격이 없다는 건 나도 알지만, 두 분은 즐겁게 잘 어울리셔."], ["I never saw Pa take snuff before in my life, but he takes one pinch out of Mr.", "두 분이 얼마나 좋은 길동무가 되시는지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야."], ["Turveydrop’s box regularly and keeps putting it to his nose and taking it away again all the evening.”", "나는 평생 아버지가 코담배 피우시는 걸 본 적이 없는데, 이젠 터비드롭 씨의 담뱃갑에서 규칙적으로 한 줌씩 집어 들고는, 저녁 내내 코에 갖다 댔다 뗐다 하셔.\""]], [["That old Mr. Turveydrop should ever, in the chances and changes of life, have come to the rescue of Mr. Jellyby from Borrioboola-Gha appeared to me to be one of the pleasantest of oddities.", "늙은 터비드롭 씨가 인생의 온갖 우연과 변천 속에서 보리오불라가에서 온 젤리비 씨를 구해주기에 이르렀다는 것은, 저에게는 더없이 유쾌한 별난 일 중 하나로 여겨졌습니다."]], [["“As to Peepy,”", "\"피피로 말하자면,\""], ["said Caddy with a little hesitation, “whom I was most afraid of—next to having any family of my own, Esther—as an inconvenience to Mr.", "캐디가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했습니다. \"내가 제일 걱정했던 게—내 자식을 두는 것 다음으로 말이야, 에스더—그 애가 터비드롭 씨한테 폐가 될까 하는 거였는데, 그 노신사가 그 아이한테 베푸시는 친절은 이루 말할 수 없을 정도야."], ["Turveydrop, the kindness of the old gentleman to that child is beyond everything.", "그분이 그 애를 보고 싶다고 청하셔, 얘야!"], ["He asks to see him, my dear! He lets him take the newspaper up to him in bed; he gives him the crusts of his toast to eat; he sends him on little errands about the house; he tells him to come to me for sixpences.", "그 애한테 침대로 신문을 가져오게 하시고, 토스트 가장자리 빵 조각을 떼어 먹게 주시고, 집 안 잔심부름을 시키시고, 6펜스를 받으려면 나한테 오라고 일러주셔."], ["In short,”", "한마디로,\""], ["said Caddy cheerily, “and not to prose, I am a very fortunate girl and ought to be very grateful.", "캐디가 명랑하게 말했습니다. \"장황하게 굴지 않고 말하자면, 나는 아주 운 좋은 여자고, 무척 고마워해야 마땅하지."], ["Where are we going, Esther?”", "우리 어디로 가는 거야, 에스더?\""]], [["“To the Old Street Road,”", "\"올드 스트리트 로드로.\" 제가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “where I have a few words to say to the solicitor’s clerk who was sent to meet me at the coach-office on the very day when I came to London and first saw you, my dear.", "\"거기 사는 변호사 사무원에게 몇 마디 할 말이 있어서. 바로 내가 런던에 와서 처음 너를 만나던 날, 마차 사무소로 나를 마중 나왔던 그 사람 말이야."], ["Now I think of it, the gentleman who brought us to your house.”", "지금 생각해 보니, 우리를 너희 집까지 데려다준 그 신사였지.\""]], [["“Then, indeed, I seem to be naturally the person to go with you,”", "\"그렇다면, 정말이지, 내가 너랑 같이 가는 게 자연스러운 사람인 셈이네.\""], ["returned Caddy.", "캐디가 대답했습니다."]], [["To the Old Street Road we went and there inquired at Mrs.", "우리는 올드 스트리트 로드로 가서, 거기 거피 부인의 거처에서 거피 부인을 찾았습니다."], ["Guppy’s residence for Mrs. Guppy.", "거피 부인은 응접실들을 차지하고 있었는데, 사실 누가 자기를 찾기도 전에 앞쪽 응접실 문틈으로 내다보다가 호두처럼 제 몸을 깨뜨릴 뻔할 정도였고, 곧장 모습을 드러내며 안으로 들어오라고 청했습니다."], ["Mrs. Guppy, occupying the parlours and having indeed been visibly in danger of cracking herself like a nut in the front-parlour door by peeping out before she was asked for, immediately presented herself and requested us to walk in.", "그녀는 커다란 모자를 쓴 노부인으로, 코가 꽤 붉고 눈빛이 꽤 불안정했으나, 온통 웃음을 띠고 있었습니다."], ["She was an old lady in a large cap, with rather a red nose and rather an unsteady eye, but smiling all over.", "그녀의 비좁은 작은 거실은 손님을 맞이할 채비가 되어 있었고, 그 안에는 그녀 아들의 초상화가 한 점 걸려 있었는데, 저는 하마터면 여기에 이렇게 적을 뻔했습니다."], ["Her close little sitting-room was prepared for a visit, and there was a portrait of her son in it which, I had almost written here, was more like than life: it insisted upon him with such obstinacy, and was so determined not to let him off.", "그것이 실물보다 더 닮았다고 말입니다. 어찌나 고집스럽게 그를 우겨댔던지, 그를 결코 놓아주지 않겠다는 듯 단단히 작정한 모습이었습니다."]], [["Not only was the portrait there, but we found the original there too.", "초상화만 거기 있는 게 아니라, 그 실물도 거기 있었습니다."], ["He was dressed in a great many colours and was discovered at a table reading law-papers with his forefinger to his forehead.", "그는 갖가지 색깔로 차려입은 채, 탁자 앞에서 집게손가락을 이마에 댄 채 법률 서류를 읽고 있는 것이 눈에 띄었습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy, rising, “this is indeed an oasis.", "거피 씨가 일어서며 말했습니다."], ["Mother, will you be so good as to put a chair for the other lady and get out of the gangway.”", "\"이거야말로 정말 오아시스로군요. 어머니, 미안하지만 이쪽 숙녀분께 의자를 놓아드리고 통로에서 좀 비켜주시겠어요.\""]], [["Mrs. Guppy, whose incessant smiling gave her quite a waggish appearance, did as her son requested and then sat down in a corner, holding her pocket handkerchief to her chest, like a fomentation, with both hands.", "거피 부인은, 끊임없이 짓는 미소 탓에 자못 익살스러운 풍모를 띠고 있었는데, 아들이 청한 대로 하고는 구석에 앉아, 마치 찜질이라도 하듯 두 손으로 주머니 손수건을 가슴에 꼭 댔습니다."]], [["I presented Caddy, and Mr. Guppy said that any friend of mine was more than welcome.", "저는 캐디를 소개했고, 거피 씨는 제 친구라면 누구든 더없이 환영한다고 말했습니다."], ["I then proceeded to the object of my visit.", "그러고 나서 저는 방문의 목적으로 들어갔습니다."]], [["“I took the liberty of sending you a note, sir,”", "\"제가 외람되게 쪽지를 보내드렸지요, 선생님.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["Mr. Guppy acknowledged the receipt by taking it out of his breast-pocket, putting it to his lips, and returning it to his pocket with a bow.", "거피 씨는 가슴 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내어 입술에 갖다 댄 다음, 절을 하며 다시 주머니에 넣어둠으로써 그것을 받았음을 알렸습니다."], ["Mr. Guppy’s mother was so diverted that she rolled her head as she smiled and made a silent appeal to Caddy with her elbow.", "거피 씨의 어머니는 어찌나 재미있어하던지, 미소 지으며 고개를 이리저리 굴리고는 팔꿈치로 캐디에게 말없이 호소했습니다."]], [["“Could I speak to you alone for a moment?”", "\"잠깐 단둘이서 말씀 좀 나눌 수 있을까요?\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["Anything like the jocoseness of Mr. Guppy’s mother just now, I think I never saw.", "방금 거피 씨 어머니의 그 익살스러움 같은 것을, 저는 평생 본 적이 없는 것 같습니다."], ["She made no sound of laughter, but she rolled her head, and shook it, and put her handkerchief to her mouth, and appealed to Caddy with her elbow, and her hand, and her shoulder, and was so unspeakably entertained altogether that it was with some difficulty she could marshal Caddy through the little folding-door into her bedroom adjoining.", "그녀는 웃음소리는 내지 않았지만, 고개를 굴리고, 흔들고, 손수건을 입에 갖다 대고, 팔꿈치며 손이며 어깨로 캐디에게 호소했고, 도무지 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 재미있어한 나머지, 옆에 붙은 자기 침실로 통하는 작은 접이문 안으로 캐디를 데려가는 데 적잖이 애를 먹었습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,” said Mr.", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["Guppy, “you will excuse the waywardness of a parent ever mindful of a son’s appiness.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"아들의 행복을 늘 마음에 두는 어버이의 변덕을 양해해 주십시오."], ["My mother, though highly exasperating to the feelings, is actuated by maternal dictates.”", "제 어머니는 비록 감정적으로는 무척 사람을 화나게 하지만, 모성의 명령에 따라 움직이는 것입니다.\""]], [["I could hardly have believed that anybody could in a moment have turned so red or changed so much as Mr. Guppy did when I now put up my veil.", "제가 이제 베일을 들어 올리자, 거피 씨가 보인 만큼 한순간에 그토록 붉어지거나 그토록 변할 수 있는 사람이 있으리라고는, 저는 도저히 믿기 어려웠습니다."]], [["“I asked the favour of seeing you for a few moments here,”", "\"제가 켄지 씨 사무실로 찾아가는 대신 여기서 잠깐 뵙는 호의를 청한 것은,\""], ["said I, “in preference to calling at Mr.", "제가 말했습니다."], ["Kenge’s because, remembering what you said on an occasion when you spoke to me in confidence, I feared I might otherwise cause you some embarrassment, Mr. Guppy.”", "\"전에 선생님께서 저에게 비밀을 털어놓으며 말씀하셨던 그 일을 떠올리니, 그러지 않으면 제가 선생님께 어떤 곤란을 끼칠지도 모른다는 염려가 들었기 때문입니다, 거피 씨.\""]], [["I caused him embarrassment enough as it was, I am sure.", "이미 그것만으로도 제가 그분께 충분히 곤란을 끼쳤다는 것은 분명합니다."], ["I never saw such faltering, such confusion, such amazement and apprehension.", "저는 그토록 더듬거리고, 그토록 당황하고, 그토록 놀라고 두려워하는 모습을 본 적이 없습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,” stammered Mr.", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["Guppy, “I—I—beg your pardon, but in our profession—we—we—find it necessary to be explicit.", "거피 씨가 말을 더듬었습니다. \"저—저—실례합니다만, 우리 직업에서는—우리—우리는—명확히 말하는 것이 필요하다고 봅니다."], ["You have referred to an occasion, miss, when I—when I did myself the honour of making a declaration which—”", "양께서는 어떤 일을 언급하셨는데, 그때 제가—제가 영광스럽게도 어떤 선언을 했던—\""]], [["Something seemed to rise in his throat that he could not possibly swallow.", "무언가가 그의 목구멍에서 치밀어 올라 도무지 삼킬 수 없는 듯했습니다."], ["He put his hand there, coughed, made faces, tried again to swallow it, coughed again, made faces again, looked all round the room, and fluttered his papers.", "그는 거기에 손을 갖다 대고, 기침을 하고, 얼굴을 찡그리고, 다시 그것을 삼켜보려 하고, 또 기침을 하고, 또 얼굴을 찡그리고, 방 안을 빙 둘러보고, 자기 서류들을 팔랑거렸습니다."]], [["“A kind of giddy sensation has come upon me, miss,”", "\"일종의 어지러운 느낌이 저를 덮쳐서요, 양.\""], ["he explained, “which rather knocks me over.", "그가 설명했습니다. \"그게 저를 좀 쓰러뜨립니다."], ["I—er—a little subject to this sort of thing—er—by George!”", "저는—어—이런 종류의 일에 좀 약해서요—어—맙소사!\""]], [["I gave him a little time to recover.", "저는 그가 추스를 시간을 잠시 주었습니다."], ["He consumed it in putting his hand to his forehead and taking it away again, and in backing his chair into the corner behind him.", "그는 그 시간을 손을 이마에 댔다가 다시 떼는 것과, 자기 의자를 뒤쪽 구석으로 물리는 데 다 써버렸습니다."]], [["“My intention was to remark, miss,” said Mr.", "\"제가 말하려던 것은요, 양.\""], ["Guppy, “dear me—something bronchial, I think—hem!—to remark that you was so good on that occasion as to repel and repudiate that declaration.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"이런—기관지 쪽인 것 같습니다—에헴!—그러니까 그때 양께서 친절하게도 그 선언을 물리치고 거부하셨다는 것 말입니다."], ["You—you wouldn’t perhaps object to admit that?", "양께서—양께서 혹시 그것을 인정하시는 데 반대하지는 않으시겠지요?"], ["Though no witnesses are present, it might be a satisfaction to—to your mind—if you was to put in that admission.”", "증인이 한 사람도 없긴 하지만, 그 인정을 해두신다면—양의—양의 마음에—흡족한 일이 될지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["“There can be no doubt,”", "\"의심할 여지가 없습니다.\" 제가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “that I declined your proposal without any reservation or qualification whatever, Mr. Guppy.”", "\"저는 아무런 유보나 단서도 없이 선생님의 청혼을 거절했지요, 거피 씨.\""]], [["“Thank you, miss,”", "\"고맙습니다, 양.\""], ["he returned, measuring the table with his troubled hands.", "그가 어쩔 줄 모르는 두 손으로 탁자를 재며 대답했습니다."], ["“So far that’s satisfactory, and it does you credit.", "\"거기까지는 흡족하고, 양께 명예가 되는 일입니다."], ["Er—this is certainly bronchial!—must be in the tubes—er—you wouldn’t perhaps be offended if I was to mention—not that it’s necessary, for your own good sense or any person’s sense must show ’em that—if I was to mention that such declaration on my part was final, and there terminated?”", "어—이건 확실히 기관지예요!—틀림없이 기관에 있는 거예요—어—혹시 제가 한 가지 말씀드린다 해도 기분 나빠하지는 않으시겠지요—꼭 필요해서가 아니라, 양 본인의 분별이나 어느 누구의 분별로 보더라도 그건 자명할 테니까요—제 쪽에서 한 그 선언이 최종적인 것이었고, 거기서 끝났다는 것을 말씀드린다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“I quite understand that,”", "\"잘 알겠습니다.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Perhaps—er—it may not be worth the form, but it might be a satisfaction to your mind—perhaps you wouldn’t object to admit that, miss?”", "\"혹시—어—그런 형식까지 갖출 가치는 없을지 모르지만, 양의 마음에 흡족한 일이 될지도 모르니까요—혹시 그것을 인정하시는 데 반대하지는 않으시겠지요, 양?\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“I admit it most fully and freely,”", "\"더없이 충분히, 또 거리낌 없이 인정합니다.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["returned Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 대답했습니다."], ["“Very honourable, I am sure.", "\"정말 매우 명예로운 일입니다."], ["I regret that my arrangements in life, combined with circumstances over which I have no control, will put it out of my power ever to fall back upon that offer or to renew it in any shape or form whatever, but it will ever be a retrospect entwined—er—with friendship’s bowers.”", "제 인생의 처지가, 제가 어쩔 수 없는 여러 사정과 맞물려서, 그 청혼으로 다시 돌아가거나 그것을 어떤 모습이나 형태로든 새로이 하는 것을 영영 제 능력 밖의 일로 만들어버린 것이 유감스럽습니다만, 그것은 언제까지나—어—우정의 정자(亭子)와 얽힌 추억으로 남을 것입니다.\""], ["Mr. Guppy’s bronchitis came to his relief and stopped his measurement of the table.", "거피 씨의 기관지염이 그를 구원하러 와서, 그가 탁자를 재는 것을 멈추게 했습니다."]], [["“I may now perhaps mention what I wished to say to you?”", "\"이제 제가 선생님께 말씀드리고 싶었던 것을 말씀드려도 될까요?\""], ["I began.", "제가 입을 열었습니다."]], [["“I shall be honoured, I am sure,”", "\"영광이지요, 정말로.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I am so persuaded that your own good sense and right feeling, miss, will—will keep you as square as possible—that I can have nothing but pleasure, I am sure, in hearing any observations you may wish to offer.”", "\"양 본인의 훌륭한 분별과 올바른 감정이—양께서 되도록 공정하게 처신하시도록 지켜주리라고 저는 굳게 믿고 있으니—양께서 어떤 말씀을 하시든 그것을 듣는 것이 저에게는 기쁨일 따름입니다, 정말로.\""]], [["“You were so good as to imply, on that occasion—”", "\"선생님께서는 그때 친절하게도 이런 뜻을 비치셨지요—\""]], [["“Excuse me, miss,” said Mr.", "\"실례합니다, 양.\""], ["Guppy, “but we had better not travel out of the record into implication.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 우리는 기록을 벗어나 암시로 흘러가지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["I cannot admit that I implied anything.”", "제가 무언가를 암시했다는 것은 인정할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“You said on that occasion,”", "\"선생님께서는 그때 말씀하셨지요,\""], ["I recommenced, “that you might possibly have the means of advancing my interests and promoting my fortunes by making discoveries of which I should be the subject.", "제가 다시 시작했습니다. \"저를 대상으로 어떤 사실들을 밝혀냄으로써, 제 이익을 증진하고 제 처지를 나아지게 할 방도를 어쩌면 갖고 계실지도 모른다고요."], ["I presume that you founded that belief upon your general knowledge of my being an orphan girl, indebted for everything to the benevolence of Mr. Jarndyce.", "짐작건대 선생님께서는 제가 고아 처녀이며, 모든 것을 잔다이스 씨의 선의에 빚지고 있다는 것을 두루 아셨기에 그런 믿음을 품으셨겠지요."], ["Now, the beginning and the end of what I have come to beg of you is, Mr. Guppy, that you will have the kindness to relinquish all idea of so serving me.", "그런데, 제가 선생님께 청하러 온 일의 처음이자 끝은, 거피 씨, 부디 친절을 베푸셔서 그런 식으로 저를 도우시려는 생각을 깨끗이 단념해 주십사 하는 것입니다."], ["I have thought of this sometimes, and I have thought of it most lately—since I have been ill.", "저는 이따금 이 일을 생각해 왔고, 가장 최근에—제가 앓고 난 뒤로—이 일을 생각했습니다."], ["At length I have decided, in case you should at any time recall that purpose and act upon it in any way, to come to you and assure you that you are altogether mistaken.", "마침내 저는, 선생님께서 언제고 그 목적을 다시 떠올리시어 어떤 식으로든 실행에 옮기실 경우를 대비해, 선생님을 찾아뵙고 선생님께서 완전히 잘못 짚으셨음을 분명히 말씀드리기로 결심했습니다."], ["You could make no discovery in reference to me that would do me the least service or give me the least pleasure.", "선생님께서는 저와 관련하여, 저에게 조금이라도 도움이 되거나 조금이라도 기쁨을 줄 만한 사실을 결코 밝혀내실 수 없습니다."], ["I am acquainted with my personal history, and I have it in my power to assure you that you never can advance my welfare by such means.", "저는 제 개인사를 알고 있으며, 선생님께서 그런 수단으로는 결코 제 안녕을 증진하실 수 없다는 것을 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다."], ["You may, perhaps, have abandoned this project a long time.", "어쩌면 선생님께서는 이 계획을 오래전에 접으셨을지도 모릅니다."], ["If so, excuse my giving you unnecessary trouble.", "만일 그렇다면, 공연한 수고를 끼친 것을 용서해 주십시오."], ["If not, I entreat you, on the assurance I have given you, henceforth to lay it aside.", "그렇지 않다면, 제가 드린 이 다짐을 믿고, 이제부터는 그 생각을 내려놓아 주십사 간청드립니다."], ["I beg you to do this, for my peace.”", "부디 그렇게 해주시기를 청합니다, 제 마음의 평안을 위해서요.\""]], [["“I am bound to confess,” said Mr.", "\"고백하지 않을 수 없군요.\""], ["Guppy, “that you express yourself, miss, with that good sense and right feeling for which I gave you credit.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"양께서는, 제가 양께 인정해 드렸던 바로 그 훌륭한 분별과 올바른 감정으로 자신을 표현하고 계십니다."], ["Nothing can be more satisfactory than such right feeling, and if I mistook any intentions on your part just now, I am prepared to tender a full apology.", "그런 올바른 감정만큼 흡족한 것은 없지요. 그리고 방금 제가 양 쪽의 어떤 의도를 잘못 짚었다면, 저는 충분히 사과를 드릴 준비가 되어 있습니다."], ["I should wish to be understood, miss, as hereby offering that apology—limiting it, as your own good sense and right feeling will point out the necessity of, to the present proceedings.”", "양, 저는 이로써 그 사과를 드리는 것으로 이해되기를 바랍니다—다만, 양 본인의 훌륭한 분별과 올바른 감정이 그 필요성을 짚어주시는 대로, 그것을 지금의 이 일에 한정하면서 말입니다.\""]], [["I must say for Mr. Guppy that the snuffling manner he had had upon him improved very much.", "거피 씨를 위해 한마디 해두어야겠는데, 그가 띠고 있던 그 코맹맹이 같은 태도가 무척 나아졌다는 것입니다."], ["He seemed truly glad to be able to do something I asked, and he looked ashamed.", "그는 제가 청한 무언가를 할 수 있게 된 것을 진심으로 기뻐하는 듯했고, 부끄러워하는 기색이었습니다."]], [["“If you will allow me to finish what I have to say at once so that I may have no occasion to resume,”", "\"제가 다시 말을 꺼낼 일이 없도록, 제가 할 말을 지금 한꺼번에 마치게 허락해 주신다면,\" 그가 막 말을 하려는 것을 보고 저는 말을 이었습니다."], ["I went on, seeing him about to speak, “you will do me a kindness, sir.", "\"선생님은 저에게 친절을 베푸시는 셈이 됩니다, 선생님."], ["I come to you as privately as possible because you announced this impression of yours to me in a confidence which I have really wished to respect—and which I always have respected, as you remember.", "제가 되도록 은밀하게 선생님을 찾아온 것은, 선생님께서 이 생각을 저에게 알리실 때 비밀로 하셨고, 저는 그 비밀을 진정으로 존중하고 싶었기 때문입니다—그리고 기억하시겠지만, 저는 늘 그것을 존중해 왔습니다."], ["I have mentioned my illness.", "제가 앓았던 일을 말씀드렸지요."], ["There really is no reason why I should hesitate to say that I know very well that any little delicacy I might have had in making a request to you is quite removed.", "선생님께 어떤 청을 드리는 데 제가 느꼈을 작은 거리낌이 이제 완전히 사라졌다는 것을 제가 아주 잘 안다고 말하는 데, 사실 망설일 이유가 전혀 없습니다."], ["Therefore I make the entreaty I have now preferred, and I hope you will have sufficient consideration for me to accede to it.”", "그래서 저는 방금 드린 이 간청을 드리는 것이고, 부디 선생님께서 저를 충분히 배려하시어 이를 들어주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["I must do Mr. Guppy the further justice of saying that he had looked more and more ashamed and that he looked most ashamed and very earnest when he now replied with a burning face, “Upon my word and honour, upon my life, upon my soul, Miss Summerson, as I am a living man, I’ll act according to your wish!", "거피 씨에게 한 가지 더 공정하게 말해두어야겠는데, 그가 점점 더 부끄러워하는 기색이었고, 이제 발갛게 달아오른 얼굴로 대답할 때는 더없이 부끄러워하면서도 무척 진지했다는 것입니다."], ["I’ll never go another step in opposition to it.", "\"맹세코, 명예를 걸고, 제 목숨을 걸고, 제 영혼을 걸고, 서머슨 양, 제가 살아 있는 사람인 한, 저는 양의 뜻에 따라 행동하겠습니다!"], ["I’ll take my oath to it if it will be any satisfaction to you.", "그것에 반하는 짓은 한 발짝도 다시 하지 않겠습니다. 양의 마음에 조금이라도 흡족한 일이 된다면, 그것을 두고 맹세라도 하겠습니다."], ["In what I promise at this present time touching the matters now in question,” continued Mr.", "지금 이 자리에서, 지금 문제가 되고 있는 사안과 관련하여 제가 약속하는 바는,\""], ["Guppy rapidly, as if he were repeating a familiar form of words, “I speak the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth, so—”", "거피 씨가, 마치 익숙한 정형구를 외기라도 하듯 빠르게 이어 말했습니다. \"진실, 오로지 진실, 진실 외에는 아무것도 아닌 것을 말한다는 것이니, 그러므로—\""]], [["“I am quite satisfied,”", "\"아주 흡족합니다.\""], ["said I, rising at this point, “and I thank you very much.", "이 대목에서 일어서며 제가 말했습니다. \"정말 감사합니다."], ["Caddy, my dear, I am ready!”", "캐디, 얘야, 나는 준비됐어!\""]], [["Mr. Guppy’s mother returned with Caddy (now making me the recipient of her silent laughter and her nudges), and we took our leave.", "거피 씨의 어머니가 캐디와 함께 돌아왔는데(이제는 자기의 말없는 웃음과 팔꿈치질의 대상으로 저를 삼았습니다), 우리는 작별을 고했습니다."], ["Mr. Guppy saw us to the door with the air of one who was either imperfectly awake or walking in his sleep; and we left him there, staring.", "거피 씨는 잠이 덜 깼거나 잠결에 걷는 사람 같은 모습으로 우리를 문까지 배웅했고, 우리는 멍하니 바라보는 그를 거기 남겨둔 채 떠났습니다."]], [["But in a minute he came after us down the street without any hat, and with his long hair all blown about, and stopped us, saying fervently, “Miss Summerson, upon my honour and soul, you may depend upon me!”", "그러나 잠시 후 그는 모자도 쓰지 않고, 긴 머리카락을 온통 바람에 휘날리며 거리로 우리를 쫓아 내려와, 우리를 멈춰 세우고는 간절하게 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양, 명예와 영혼을 걸고, 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다!\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"믿습니다.\""], ["said I, “quite confidently.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"아주 굳게요.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, miss,” said Mr.", "\"실례합니다, 양.\""], ["Guppy, going with one leg and staying with the other, “but this lady being present—your own witness—it might be a satisfaction to your mind (which I should wish to set at rest) if you was to repeat those admissions.”", "한 다리는 떼고 다른 다리는 멈춘 채로 거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"하지만 이 숙녀분이 여기 계시니—양 본인의 증인이니—혹시 그 인정의 말을 다시 한번 해주신다면 양의 마음에(제가 안심시켜 드리고 싶은 그 마음에) 흡족한 일이 될지도 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, Caddy,”", "\"그럼, 캐디.\" 그녀를 돌아보며 제가 말했습니다."], ["said I, turning to her, “perhaps you will not be surprised when I tell you, my dear, that there never has been any engagement—”", "\"얘야, 내가 이렇게 말하더라도 너는 놀라지 않을 거야. 여태껏 그 어떤 약혼도 없었다고—\""]], [["“No proposal or promise of marriage whatsoever,”", "\"그 어떤 청혼이나 결혼 약속도 일절 없었다고요.\""], ["suggested Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 거들었습니다."]], [["“No proposal or promise of marriage whatsoever,”", "\"그 어떤 청혼이나 결혼 약속도 일절 없었다고.\""], ["said I, “between this gentleman—”", "제가 말했습니다. \"이 신사분과—\""]], [["“William Guppy, of Penton Place, Pentonville, in the county of Middlesex,”", "\"미들섹스 주 펜턴빌, 펜턴 플레이스에 사는 윌리엄 거피와요.\""], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸습니다."]], [["“Between this gentleman, Mr. William Guppy, of Penton Place, Pentonville, in the county of Middlesex, and myself.”", "\"이 신사분, 미들섹스 주 펜턴빌, 펜턴 플레이스에 사는 윌리엄 거피 씨와 저 사이에 말이야.\""]], [["“Thank you, miss,”", "\"고맙습니다, 양.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Very full—er—excuse me—lady’s name, Christian and surname both?” I gave them.", "\"아주 빠짐없군요—어—실례합니다만—이 숙녀분 성함은, 이름과 성을 다 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Married woman, I believe?”", "저는 그것을 말해주었습니다."], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "\"기혼 여성이시지요?\""], ["“Married woman.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Thank you.", "\"기혼 여성."], ["Formerly Caroline Jellyby, spinster, then of Thavies Inn, within the city of London, but extra-parochial; now of Newman Street, Oxford Street.", "고맙습니다. 예전에는 미혼인 캐럴라인 젤리비, 당시 런던 시 안에 있되 어느 교구에도 속하지 않는 데이비스 인에 거주, 현재는 옥스퍼드 거리의 뉴먼 거리에 거주."], ["Much obliged.”", "대단히 감사합니다.\""]], [["He ran home and came running back again.", "그는 집으로 뛰어갔다가 다시 뛰어 돌아왔습니다."]], [["“Touching that matter, you know, I really and truly am very sorry that my arrangements in life, combined with circumstances over which I have no control, should prevent a renewal of what was wholly terminated some time back,” said Mr.", "\"그 일로 말하자면, 아시다시피, 제 인생의 처지가, 제가 어쩔 수 없는 여러 사정과 맞물려서, 얼마 전에 완전히 끝나버린 그 일을 새로이 하는 것을 막아버리는 것이 정말이지 진심으로 무척 유감스럽습니다.\""], ["Guppy to me forlornly and despondently, “but it couldn’t be.", "거피 씨가 저에게 처량하고 풀 죽은 어조로 말했습니다. \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없었어요."], ["Now COULD it, you know!", "정말 어쩔 수가 없었지요, 아시잖습니까!"], ["I only put it to you.”", "그저 양께 여쭙는 겁니다.\""]], [["I replied it certainly could not.", "저는 확실히 어쩔 수 없었다고 대답했습니다."], ["The subject did not admit of a doubt.", "그 문제는 의심의 여지가 없었으니까요."], ["He thanked me and ran to his mother’s again—and back again.", "그는 저에게 고맙다고 하고는 다시 어머니에게로 뛰어갔다가—또다시 뛰어 돌아왔습니다."]], [["“It’s very honourable of you, miss, I am sure,”", "\"정말 매우 명예로운 일입니다, 양.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“If an altar could be erected in the bowers of friendship—but, upon my soul, you may rely upon me in every respect save and except the tender passion only!”", "\"우정의 정자 안에 제단을 세울 수 있다면—하지만, 맹세코, 그 애틋한 연정 하나만 빼고는 모든 면에서 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다!\""]], [["The struggle in Mr. Guppy’s breast and the numerous oscillations it occasioned him between his mother’s door and us were sufficiently conspicuous in the windy street (particularly as his hair wanted cutting) to make us hurry away.", "거피 씨의 가슴속에서 벌어지는 갈등과, 그 때문에 그가 어머니의 문과 우리 사이를 수없이 오가는 그 진동은, 바람 부는 거리에서(특히 그의 머리카락이 깎을 때가 되었던 만큼) 충분히 눈에 띄어서, 우리를 서둘러 떠나게 만들었습니다."], ["I did so with a lightened heart; but when we last looked back, Mr.", "저는 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 그렇게 했습니다."], ["Guppy was still oscillating in the same troubled state of mind.", "그러나 우리가 마지막으로 뒤돌아보았을 때, 거피 씨는 여전히 똑같이 심란한 마음 상태로 진동하고 있었습니다."]], [["Attorney and Client\nThe name of Mr.", "볼스 씨의 이름은 그 위에 \"일층\"이라는 표시를 달고서 챈서리 레인의 시먼즈 인에 있는 어느 문기둥에 새겨져 있다."], ["Vholes, preceded by the legend Ground-Floor, is inscribed upon a door-post in Symond’s Inn, Chancery Lane—a little, pale, wall-eyed, woebegone inn like a large dust-binn of two compartments and a sifter.", "작고, 핏기 없고, 사팔뜨기에, 비탄에 잠긴 듯한 그 법학원은, 칸막이 둘에 체 하나를 갖춘 커다란 쓰레기통 같다."], ["It looks as if Symond were a sparing man in his way and constructed his inn of old building materials which took kindly to the dry rot and to dirt and all things decaying and dismal, and perpetuated Symond’s memory with congenial shabbiness.", "마치 시먼드라는 사람이 제 나름으로 인색해서, 마른 썩음병과 먼지, 그리고 썩어가고 음울한 온갖 것들과 죽이 잘 맞는 낡은 건축 자재로 이 법학원을 지어, 어울리는 누추함으로 시먼드의 기억을 영원토록 남긴 듯하다. 시먼드를 기리는 이 칙칙한 문장(紋章) 안에, 볼스 씨의 법조계 문양이 한 칸 차지하고 있다."], ["Quartered in this dingy hatchment commemorative of Symond are the legal bearings of Mr. Vholes.", "* 문장(紋章): 본디 가문의 표지를 새긴 장식으로, 여기서는 음울한 건물 자체를 빗댄 표현이다."]], [["Mr. Vholes’s office, in disposition retiring and in situation retired, is squeezed up in a corner and blinks at a dead wall.", "볼스 씨의 사무실은 기질로 보나 위치로 보나 외진 곳으로, 한쪽 구석에 처박혀 막다른 벽을 멀거니 바라본다."], ["Three feet of knotty-floored dark passage bring the client to Mr. Vholes’s jet-black door, in an angle profoundly dark on the brightest midsummer morning and encumbered by a black bulk-head of cellarage staircase against which belated civilians generally strike their brows.", "옹이투성이 바닥에 어두운 통로 삼 피트를 지나면 의뢰인은 볼스 씨의 칠흑 같은 문에 이르는데, 그 모퉁이는 한여름 가장 환한 아침에도 깊디깊은 어둠에 잠겨 있고, 지하실 계단의 시커먼 칸막이벽이 가로막고 있어 늑장 부린 민사 변호사들이 으레 거기에 이마를 부딪친다."], ["Mr. Vholes’s chambers are on so small a scale that one clerk can open the door without getting off his stool, while the other who elbows him at the same desk has equal facilities for poking the fire.", "볼스 씨의 사무실은 규모가 어찌나 작은지, 서기 하나가 걸상에서 일어나지도 않고 문을 열 수 있고, 같은 책상에서 그와 팔꿈치를 부딪치는 다른 서기는 그만큼 손쉽게 불을 쑤석거릴 수 있다."], ["A smell as of unwholesome sheep blending with the smell of must and dust is referable to the nightly (and often daily) consumption of mutton fat in candles and to the fretting of parchment forms and skins in greasy drawers.", "곰팡내와 먼지내에 뒤섞인, 어딘가 건강에 해로운 양고기 같은 냄새가 풍기는 것은, 밤마다(흔히는 낮에도) 양 기름으로 만든 양초를 태운 탓이요, 기름때 묻은 서랍 속에서 양피지 서식과 가죽이 부스러지는 탓이다."], ["The atmosphere is otherwise stale and close.", "그 밖의 공기는 케케묵고 답답하다."], ["The place was last painted or whitewashed beyond the memory of man, and the two chimneys smoke, and there is a loose outer surface of soot everywhere, and the dull cracked windows in their heavy frames have but one piece of character in them, which is a determination to be always dirty and always shut unless coerced.", "이곳에 마지막으로 페인트칠이나 회칠을 한 것은 사람들 기억 너머의 일이고, 굴뚝 두 개는 연기를 내뿜으며, 사방에는 그을음이 헐겁게 표면을 덮고 있고, 묵직한 틀에 끼인 흐리고 금 간 창문들에는 단 하나의 개성이 있으니, 곧 강제로 시키지 않는 한 늘 더럽고 늘 닫혀 있겠다는 결의다."], ["This accounts for the phenomenon of the weaker of the two usually having a bundle of firewood thrust between its jaws in hot weather.", "그래서 둘 중 더 약한 창문이 무더운 날씨에도 그 아가리에 장작 한 묶음을 물고 있는 진풍경이 벌어지는 것이다."]], [["Mr. Vholes is a very respectable man.", "볼스 씨는 매우 존경할 만한 사람이다."], ["He has not a large business, but he is a very respectable man.", "사업 규모는 크지 않지만, 그래도 매우 존경할 만한 사람이다."], ["He is allowed by the greater attorneys who have made good fortunes or are making them to be a most respectable man.", "큰 재산을 모았거나 모으는 중인 큰 변호사들도 그를 더없이 존경할 만한 사람으로 인정한다. 그는 업무에서 단 한 번의 기회도 놓치는 법이 없는데, 이것이 존경받을 만한 표시다."], ["He never misses a chance in his practice, which is a mark of respectability.", "그는 어떤 즐거움도 누리지 않는데, 이것이 또 하나의 존경받을 만한 표시다."], ["He never takes any pleasure, which is another mark of respectability.", "그는 과묵하고 진지한데, 이것이 또 하나의 존경받을 만한 표시다. 소화 기능이 망가져 있는데, 이것이 대단히 존경받을 만하다."], ["He is reserved and serious, which is another mark of respectability.", "그리고 그는 풀, 곧 살덩이가 마르기 전에 베어 거두느라 여념이 없으니, 세 딸을 위해서다."], ["His digestion is impaired, which is highly respectable.", "게다가 그의 아버지는 톤턴 골짜기에서 그에게 기대어 산다."], ["And he is making hay of the grass which is flesh, for his three daughters. And his father is dependent on him in the Vale of Taunton.", "* 풀, 곧 살덩이: 성경 이사야서의 \"모든 육체는 풀이요\"를 빗댄 표현으로, 의뢰인을 풀이자 살덩이로 보고 거두어들인다는 풍자다."]], [["The one great principle of the English law is to make business for itself.", "영국 법의 단 하나 위대한 원리는, 제 자신을 위해 일거리를 만드는 것이다."], ["There is no other principle distinctly, certainly, and consistently maintained through all its narrow turnings.", "그 비좁은 모퉁이들을 하나도 빠짐없이 관통하여 또렷하고 확실하게 한결같이 유지되는 다른 원리란 없다."], ["Viewed by this light it becomes a coherent scheme and not the monstrous maze the laity are apt to think it.", "이 빛에 비추어 보면 법은 일관된 설계가 되니, 보통 사람들이 으레 여기듯 괴물 같은 미로가 아니다."], ["Let them but once clearly perceive that its grand principle is to make business for itself at their expense, and surely they will cease to grumble.", "보통 사람들이 단 한 번만이라도, 법의 그 웅대한 원리란 곧 자기들의 비용으로 법 제 자신의 일거리를 만드는 것임을 똑똑히 깨닫기만 한다면, 분명 투덜대기를 그치리라."]], [["But not perceiving this quite plainly—only seeing it by halves in a confused way—the laity sometimes suffer in peace and pocket, with a bad grace, and DO grumble very much.", "그러나 이것을 아주 또렷이 깨닫지 못한 채—혼란스럽게 절반쯤만 보고 있는 탓에—보통 사람들은 때로 마음 편치 않게 평온과 주머니를 동시에 축내며, 사실 무척 투덜댄다."], ["Then this respectability of Mr. Vholes is brought into powerful play against them.", "그러면 볼스 씨의 이 존경받을 만함이 그들을 향해 강력하게 발휘된다."], ["“Repeal this statute, my good sir?”", "\"이 법령을 폐지하라고요, 선생?\""], ["says Mr. Kenge to a smarting client.", "켄지 씨가 속 쓰린 의뢰인에게 말한다."], ["“Repeal it, my dear sir?", "\"그걸 폐지하라고요, 선생?"], ["Never, with my consent.", "절대로요, 내 동의로는 안 됩니다."], ["Alter this law, sir, and what will be the effect of your rash proceeding on a class of practitioners very worthily represented, allow me to say to you, by the opposite attorney in the case, Mr. Vholes?", "이 법을 고쳐 보시오, 선생, 그러면 선생의 그 경솔한 처사가, 이 사건에서 상대편 변호사인 볼스 씨가 더없이 훌륭히 대표하는—이렇게 말씀드려도 될지 모르나—한 부류의 법조 실무가들에게 어떤 영향을 끼치겠습니까?"], ["Sir, that class of practitioners would be swept from the face of the earth.", "선생, 그 부류의 실무가들은 지상에서 싹 쓸려 사라질 겁니다."], ["Now you cannot afford—I will say, the social system cannot afford—to lose an order of men like Mr.", "자, 선생은 볼스 씨 같은 인물의 부류를 잃을 여유가 없습니다—아니, 우리 사회 제도가 그럴 여유가 없다고 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Vholes. Diligent, persevering, steady, acute in business.", "부지런하고, 끈기 있고, 한결같고, 일에 빈틈없는 사람들 말입니다."], ["My dear sir, I understand your present feelings against the existing state of things, which I grant to be a little hard in your case; but I can never raise my voice for the demolition of a class of men like Mr. Vholes.”", "선생, 현 상태에 대한 지금의 심정을 이해합니다, 선생의 경우엔 조금 가혹한 면이 있다는 것도 인정하지요."], ["The respectability of Mr. Vholes has even been cited with crushing effect before Parliamentary committees, as in the following blue minutes of a distinguished attorney’s evidence.", "하지만 나는 볼스 씨 같은 부류의 사람들을 무너뜨리자고 결코 목소리를 높일 수 없습니다.\""], ["“Question (number five hundred and seventeen thousand eight hundred and sixty-nine): If I understand you, these forms of practice indisputably occasion delay?", "볼스 씨의 존경받을 만함은 의회 위원회 앞에서도 압도적인 효과를 내며 인용된 바 있으니, 어느 저명한 변호사의 증언을 담은 다음의 청서(靑書) 회의록이 그것이다. \"질문(제51만 7869번): 제가 이해하기로, 이 실무 절차들이 의문의 여지 없이 지연을 일으킵니까?"], ["Answer: Yes, some delay.", "답변: 그렇습니다, 다소 지연이 있지요."], ["Question: And great expense?", "질문: 그리고 막대한 비용도?"], ["Answer: Most assuredly they cannot be gone through for nothing.", "답변: 분명히 공짜로는 거칠 수 없는 일입니다."], ["Question: And unspeakable vexation?", "질문: 그리고 말로 다 못 할 골칫거리도?"], ["Answer: I am not prepared to say that.", "답변: 그건 말씀드릴 수 없습니다."], ["They have never given ME any vexation; quite the contrary.", "제게는 한 번도 골칫거리가 된 적이 없으니까요. 오히려 정반대지요."], ["Question: But you think that their abolition would damage a class of practitioners?", "질문: 그러나 그 절차들을 폐지하면 한 부류의 실무가들이 타격을 입으리라고 보십니까?"], ["Answer: I have no doubt of it.", "답변: 의심할 여지가 없습니다."], ["Question: Can you instance any type of that class?", "질문: 그 부류의 본보기를 하나 들어 주시겠습니까?"], ["Answer: Yes.", "답변: 그러지요."], ["I would unhesitatingly mention Mr. Vholes.", "주저 없이 볼스 씨를 들겠습니다."], ["He would be ruined.", "그는 파산할 겁니다."], ["Question: Mr. Vholes is considered, in the profession, a respectable man?", "질문: 볼스 씨는 업계에서 존경받을 만한 사람으로 여겨집니까?"], ["Answer:”—which proved fatal to the inquiry for ten years—“Mr.", "답변:\"—이것이 십 년 동안 그 조사를 결딴낸 한마디였다—\"볼스 씨는 업계에서 더없이 존경받을 만한 사람으로 여겨집니다.\""], ["Vholes is considered, in the profession, a MOST respectable man.”", "* 청서: 영국 의회나 추밀원이 펴낸 공식 보고서로, 표지가 푸른빛이라 그렇게 불렀다."]], [["So in familiar conversation, private authorities no less disinterested will remark that they don’t know what this age is coming to, that we are plunging down precipices, that now here is something else gone, that these changes are death to people like Vholes—a man of undoubted respectability, with a father in the Vale of Taunton, and three daughters at home.", "이리하여 격의 없는 잡담 속에서도, 그에 못지않게 사심 없는 사사로운 권위자들이 이렇게 말하곤 한다. 요즘 세상이 어찌 되려는지 모르겠다, 우리는 벼랑 아래로 곤두박질치고 있다, 자 보라 이번에 또 무엇이 사라졌다, 이런 변화는 볼스 같은 이들에게 죽음이나 다름없다—톤턴 골짜기에 아버지를 두고 집에 세 딸을 둔, 의심할 바 없이 존경받을 만한 사람에게 말이다."], ["Take a few steps more in this direction, say they, and what is to become of Vholes’s father?", "이 방향으로 몇 발짝만 더 나아가 보라, 그러면 볼스의 아버지는 어찌 되겠는가?"], ["Is he to perish?", "굶어 죽으란 말인가?"], ["And of Vholes’s daughters?", "그리고 볼스의 딸들은?"], ["Are they to be shirt-makers, or governesses? As though, Mr.", "셔츠 짓는 여공이나 가정교사가 되란 말인가?"], ["Vholes and his relations being minor cannibal chiefs and it being proposed to abolish cannibalism, indignant champions were to put the case thus: Make man-eating unlawful, and you starve the Vholeses!", "마치 볼스 씨와 그의 친족이 식인종 소추장들인데 식인 풍습을 폐지하자는 제안이 나오자, 분개한 옹호자들이 이렇게 항변하기라도 하듯이. 사람 잡아먹기를 불법으로 만들어 보라, 그러면 볼스 일가를 굶겨 죽이는 셈이다!"]], [["In a word, Mr. Vholes, with his three daughters and his father in the Vale of Taunton, is continually doing duty, like a piece of timber, to shore up some decayed foundation that has become a pitfall and a nuisance.", "한마디로, 톤턴 골짜기에 둔 세 딸과 아버지를 거느린 볼스 씨는, 함정이자 골칫거리가 되어버린 어느 썩은 토대를 떠받치는 한 토막 들보처럼 끊임없이 제 소임을 다하고 있다."], ["And with a great many people in a great many instances, the question is never one of a change from wrong to right (which is quite an extraneous consideration), but is always one of injury or advantage to that eminently respectable legion, Vholes.", "그리고 수많은 사람들, 수많은 경우에 있어 문제는 결코 그릇됨에서 옳음으로의 변화(이건 전혀 동떨어진 고려 사항이다)가 아니라, 언제나 그 지극히 존경받을 만한 군단, 곧 볼스 일족에게 손해냐 이익이냐 하는 것이다."]], [["The Chancellor is, within these ten minutes, “up” for the long vacation.", "대법원장은 십 분 전쯤 장기 휴정을 위해 \"퇴정\"했다."], ["Mr. Vholes, and his young client, and several blue bags hastily stuffed out of all regularity of form, as the larger sort of serpents are in their first gorged state, have returned to the official den.", "볼스 씨와 그의 젊은 의뢰인, 그리고 형체가 흐트러질 만큼 황급히 쑤셔 넣은 푸른 서류 가방 몇 개가—마치 큰 뱀들이 처음 잔뜩 삼킨 상태처럼 불룩하게—그 관청의 소굴로 돌아왔다."], ["Mr. Vholes, quiet and unmoved, as a man of so much respectability ought to be, takes off his close black gloves as if he were skinning his hands, lifts off his tight hat as if he were scalping himself, and sits down at his desk.", "볼스 씨는 그만한 존경받을 만한 사람이라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 조용하고 흔들림 없이, 마치 제 손의 가죽을 벗기듯 꽉 끼는 검은 장갑을 벗고, 마치 제 머리 가죽을 벗기듯 꽉 끼는 모자를 들어 올린 뒤, 책상 앞에 앉는다."], ["The client throws his hat and gloves upon the ground—tosses them anywhere, without looking after them or caring where they go; flings himself into a chair, half sighing and half groaning; rests his aching head upon his hand and looks the portrait of young despair.", "의뢰인은 모자와 장갑을 바닥에 내던진다—어디로 가는지 살피지도 신경 쓰지도 않고 아무 데나 내팽개친다. 반쯤 한숨 쉬고 반쯤 신음하며 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지고, 욱신거리는 머리를 손에 괴고서, 젊은 절망의 초상화 같은 모습을 보인다."]], [["“Again nothing done!”", "\"또 아무것도 못 했어!\""], ["says Richard.", "리처드가 말한다."], ["“Nothing, nothing done!”", "\"아무것도, 아무것도 못 했다고!\""]], [["“Don’t say nothing done, sir,”", "\"아무것도 못 했다고는 하지 마십시오, 선생.\""], ["returns the placid Vholes.", "차분한 볼스가 대꾸한다."], ["“That is scarcely fair, sir, scarcely fair!”", "\"그건 거의 공정치 못한 말입니다, 선생, 거의 공정치 못해요!\""]], [["“Why, what IS done?”", "\"아니, 도대체 뭘 했는데요?\""], ["says Richard, turning gloomily upon him.", "리처드가 침울하게 그를 돌아보며 말한다."]], [["“That may not be the whole question,”", "\"그게 문제의 전부는 아닐 겁니다.\""], ["returns Vholes, “The question may branch off into what is doing, what is doing?”", "볼스가 대꾸한다. \"문제는 무엇이 진행 중인가, 무엇이 진행 중인가로 갈라질 수 있지요.\""]], [["“And what is doing?”", "\"그래, 뭐가 진행 중인데요?\""], ["asks the moody client.", "시무룩한 의뢰인이 묻는다."]], [["Vholes, sitting with his arms on the desk, quietly bringing the tips of his five right fingers to meet the tips of his five left fingers, and quietly separating them again, and fixedly and slowly looking at his client, replies, “A good deal is doing, sir.", "볼스는 두 팔을 책상에 올린 채, 오른손 다섯 손가락 끝을 왼손 다섯 손가락 끝에 가만히 맞붙였다가 다시 가만히 떼며, 뚫어지게 천천히 의뢰인을 바라보며 대답한다. \"상당히 많은 게 진행 중입니다, 선생."], ["We have put our shoulders to the wheel, Mr. Carstone, and the wheel is going round.”", "우리는 수레바퀴에 어깨를 들이댔고, 카스톤 씨, 바퀴는 돌고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, with Ixion on it.", "\"그래요, 익시온을 태우고서 말이죠."], ["How am I to get through the next four or five accursed months?”", "앞으로 닥칠 이 저주받을 너덧 달을 나는 어떻게 견디란 말입니까?\" 젊은이는 의자에서 일어나 방 안을 거닐며 외친다."], ["exclaims the young man, rising from his chair and walking about the room.", "* 익시온: 그리스 신화 속 인물로, 영원히 돌아가는 불수레에 묶이는 벌을 받았다."]], [["“Mr. C.,”", "\"카스톤 씨.\""], ["returns Vholes, following him close with his eyes wherever he goes, “your spirits are hasty, and I am sorry for it on your account.", "볼스가 그가 가는 곳마다 두 눈으로 바싹 좇으며 대꾸한다. \"기분이 성급하시군요, 선생을 위해서도 안타깝습니다."], ["Excuse me if I recommend you not to chafe so much, not to be so impetuous, not to wear yourself out so.", "외람되나, 그렇게 안달하지 마시고, 그렇게 조급해하지 마시고, 그렇게 스스로를 닳게 하지 마시라고 권하겠습니다."], ["You should have more patience.", "좀 더 인내하셔야 합니다."], ["You should sustain yourself better.”", "자신을 좀 더 잘 지탱하셔야지요.\""]], [["“I ought to imitate you, in fact, Mr. Vholes?”", "\"사실은 당신을 본받으란 말이지요, 볼스 씨?\""], ["says Richard, sitting down again with an impatient laugh and beating the devil’s tattoo with his boot on the patternless carpet.", "리처드가 짜증 섞인 웃음을 흘리며 다시 앉아, 무늬 없는 양탄자 위에서 장화로 안달스레 발장단을 치며 말한다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생.\""], ["returns Vholes, always looking at the client as if he were making a lingering meal of him with his eyes as well as with his professional appetite.", "볼스가 대꾸하는데, 그는 늘 직업적 식욕뿐 아니라 두 눈으로도 의뢰인을 천천히 한 끼 식사처럼 음미하듯 바라본다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생.\""], ["returns Vholes with his inward manner of speech and his bloodless quietude, “I should not have had the presumption to propose myself as a model for your imitation or any man’s.", "볼스가 속으로 삼키는 듯한 말투와 핏기 없는 평온함으로 대꾸한다. \"내가 감히 나 자신을 선생이나 그 누구의 본보기로 내세울 만큼 주제넘지는 못합니다."], ["Let me but leave the good name to my three daughters, and that is enough for me; I am not a self-seeker.", "그저 내 세 딸에게 좋은 이름이나 남길 수 있다면 그것으로 족하지요. 나는 사욕을 채우는 사람이 아닙니다."], ["But since you mention me so pointedly, I will acknowledge that I should like to impart to you a little of my—come, sir, you are disposed to call it insensibility, and I am sure I have no objection—say insensibility—a little of my insensibility.”", "하지만 선생이 나를 그렇게 꼭 집어 말씀하시니, 인정하겠습니다. 내 것을 조금 나눠 드리고 싶긴 합니다, 내—자, 선생, 그걸 무감각이라 부르고 싶으시겠지요, 나도 이의 없습니다—무감각이라 합시다—내 무감각을 조금 말입니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Vholes,”", "\"볼스 씨.\""], ["explains the client, somewhat abashed, “I had no intention to accuse you of insensibility.”", "의뢰인이 다소 무안해하며 해명한다. \"당신을 무감각하다고 비난할 뜻은 없었습니다.\""]], [["“I think you had, sir, without knowing it,”", "\"있으셨다고 봅니다, 선생, 본인도 모르게요.\""], ["returns the equable Vholes.", "침착한 볼스가 대꾸한다."], ["“Very naturally.", "\"아주 자연스러운 일이지요."], ["It is my duty to attend to your interests with a cool head, and I can quite understand that to your excited feelings I may appear, at such times as the present, insensible.", "냉철한 머리로 선생의 이익을 챙기는 게 내 의무이고, 흥분한 선생의 감정에는 지금 같은 때에 내가 무감각하게 보일 수 있다는 걸 충분히 이해합니다. 내 딸들은 나를 더 잘 알지도 모르지요."], ["My daughters may know me better; my aged father may know me better.", "늙으신 내 아버지도 나를 더 잘 알지도 모르고요."], ["But they have known me much longer than you have, and the confiding eye of affection is not the distrustful eye of business.", "하지만 그들은 선생보다 나를 훨씬 더 오래 알아 왔고, 애정의 믿음 어린 눈은 사업의 의심 어린 눈과는 다릅니다. 그렇다고 사업의 눈이 의심 어린 것을 탓하는 건 아닙니다, 선생."], ["Not that I complain, sir, of the eye of business being distrustful; quite the contrary.", "오히려 정반대지요. 선생의 이익을 챙기면서, 나는 나에게 가능한 모든 견제가 있기를 바랍니다."], ["In attending to your interests, I wish to have all possible checks upon me; it is right that I should have them; I court inquiry.", "그런 게 있는 것이 마땅하지요. 나는 오히려 조사를 청합니다."], ["But your interests demand that I should be cool and methodical, Mr. Carstone; and I cannot be otherwise—no, sir, not even to please you.”", "하지만 선생의 이익을 위해서는 내가 냉철하고 빈틈없어야 하며, 카스톤 씨, 나는 달리 될 수가 없습니다—아니요, 선생, 선생을 기쁘게 하기 위해서라도요.\""]], [["Mr. Vholes, after glancing at the official cat who is patiently watching a mouse’s hole, fixes his charmed gaze again on his young client and proceeds in his buttoned-up, half-audible voice as if there were an unclean spirit in him that will neither come out nor speak out, “What are you to do, sir, you inquire, during the vacation.", "볼스 씨는, 생쥐 구멍을 끈기 있게 지켜보는 관청의 고양이를 한번 흘끔 본 뒤, 다시 그 홀린 듯한 시선을 젊은 의뢰인에게 고정하고서, 마치 그 안에 나오지도 못하고 말도 못 하는 부정한 영(靈)이 들어앉은 듯, 단추를 꼭 채운 듯 반쯤만 들리는 목소리로 말을 잇는다."], ["I should hope you gentlemen of the army may find many means of amusing yourselves if you give your minds to it.", "\"선생, 휴정 기간에 무엇을 해야 하느냐고 물으셨지요. 군대에 계신 신사분들이라면 마음만 먹으면 즐길 거리야 얼마든지 찾으실 수 있으리라 봅니다."], ["If you had asked me what I was to do during the vacation, I could have answered you more readily.", "만일 휴정 기간에 내가 무엇을 할 거냐고 물으셨다면, 더 선뜻 대답할 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["I am to attend to your interests.", "나는 선생의 이익을 챙길 겁니다."], ["I am to be found here, day by day, attending to your interests.", "날마다 여기서, 선생의 이익을 챙기고 있을 겁니다."], ["That is my duty, Mr. C., and term-time or vacation makes no difference to me.", "그게 내 의무입니다, 카스톤 씨, 개정 기간이든 휴정 기간이든 내겐 아무 차이가 없습니다."], ["If you wish to consult me as to your interests, you will find me here at all times alike.", "선생의 이익에 관해 나와 상의하고 싶으시면, 언제든 한결같이 여기서 나를 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다."], ["Other professional men go out of town.", "다른 법조인들은 시외로 나가지요."], ["I don’t.", "나는 안 나갑니다."], ["Not that I blame them for going; I merely say I don’t go.", "그들이 나가는 걸 탓하는 건 아닙니다. 그저 나는 안 나간다고 할 뿐이지요."], ["This desk is your rock, sir!”", "이 책상이 선생의 반석입니다, 선생!\""]], [["Mr. Vholes gives it a rap, and it sounds as hollow as a coffin.", "볼스 씨가 책상을 똑똑 두드리니, 관처럼 텅 빈 소리가 난다."], ["Not to Richard, though.", "그렇지만 리처드에게는 그렇지 않다."], ["There is encouragement in the sound to him.", "그 소리에는 그를 북돋우는 무언가가 있다."], ["Perhaps Mr. Vholes knows there is.", "어쩌면 볼스 씨는 거기에 그런 게 있다는 걸 아는지도 모른다."]], [["“I am perfectly aware, Mr.", "\"잘 알고 있습니다, 볼스 씨.\""], ["Vholes,”", "리처드가 좀 더 허물없이, 좀 더 너그러운 기분으로 말한다."], ["says Richard, more familiarly and good-humouredly, “that you are the most reliable fellow in the world and that to have to do with you is to have to do with a man of business who is not to be hoodwinked.", "\"당신이 세상에서 가장 믿을 만한 사람이고, 당신과 함께 일한다는 건 곧 결코 속아 넘어가지 않는 사업가와 함께 일하는 거라는 걸요. 하지만 내 처지에 서 보세요."], ["But put yourself in my case, dragging on this dislocated life, sinking deeper and deeper into difficulty every day, continually hoping and continually disappointed, conscious of change upon change for the worse in myself, and of no change for the better in anything else, and you will find it a dark-looking case sometimes, as I do.”", "이 어그러진 삶을 질질 끌면서, 날마다 점점 더 깊은 곤경으로 가라앉으면서, 끊임없이 바라고 끊임없이 실망하면서, 나 자신은 더 나쁜 쪽으로 거듭거듭 변하는데 다른 어떤 것도 더 나은 쪽으로는 바뀌지 않는 걸 절감하면서요. 그러면 당신도 때로는, 나처럼, 이게 어두컴컴한 사건이라는 걸 알게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“You know,” says Mr.", "\"아시다시피,\""], ["Vholes, “that I never give hopes, sir.", "볼스 씨가 말한다. \"나는 결코 희망을 주지 않습니다, 선생."], ["I told you from the first, Mr. C., that I never give hopes.", "처음부터 말씀드렸지요, 카스톤 씨, 나는 결코 희망을 주지 않는다고요."], ["Particularly in a case like this, where the greater part of the costs comes out of the estate, I should not be considerate of my good name if I gave hopes.", "특히 비용의 대부분이 그 재산에서 나오는 이런 사건에서는, 내가 희망을 준다면 내 좋은 이름을 소중히 여기지 않는 셈이 됩니다."], ["It might seem as if costs were my object.", "비용이 내 목적인 것처럼 보일 수 있으니까요."], ["Still, when you say there is no change for the better, I must, as a bare matter of fact, deny that.”", "그래도 더 나은 쪽으로 바뀐 게 전혀 없다고 하시니, 나는 순전한 사실로서 그건 부인해야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Aye?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["returns Richard, brightening.", "리처드가 밝아지며 대꾸한다."], ["“But how do you make it out?”", "\"그런데 그걸 어떻게 입증하시려고요?\""]], [["“Mr. Carstone, you are represented by—”", "\"카스톤 씨, 선생은 이제 대리되고 있습니다—\""]], [["“You said just now—a rock.”", "\"방금 말씀하셨지요—반석이라고요.\""]], [["“Yes, sir,” says Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생.\""], ["Vholes, gently shaking his head and rapping the hollow desk, with a sound as if ashes were falling on ashes, and dust on dust, “a rock.", "볼스 씨가 머리를 가만히 가로저으며, 마치 재 위에 재가, 먼지 위에 먼지가 떨어지는 듯한 소리가 나도록 텅 빈 책상을 똑똑 두드리며 말한다. \"반석이지요."], ["That’s something.", "그건 대단한 겁니다."], ["You are separately represented, and no longer hidden and lost in the interests of others.", "선생은 따로 대리되고 있고, 더는 남들의 이해 속에 숨겨지거나 묻혀 있지 않습니다."], ["THAT’S something.", "그건 대단한 겁니다."], ["The suit does not sleep; we wake it up, we air it, we walk it about.", "소송은 잠들어 있지 않습니다. 우리가 그걸 깨우고, 바람을 쐬어 주고, 데리고 거닙니다."], ["THAT’S something.", "그건 대단한 겁니다."], ["It’s not all Jarndyce, in fact as well as in name.", "그건 이름으로 보나 실제로 보나 더는 죄다 잔다이스만의 것이 아닙니다."], ["THAT’S something.", "그건 대단한 겁니다."], ["Nobody has it all his own way now, sir.", "이제는 아무도 제 멋대로만 할 수 없습니다, 선생."], ["And THAT’S something, surely.”", "그리고 그것도 분명 대단한 겁니다.\""]], [["Richard, his face flushing suddenly, strikes the desk with his clenched hand.", "리처드는 얼굴이 갑자기 확 달아오르며 움켜쥔 주먹으로 책상을 내리친다."]], [["“Mr. Vholes!", "\"볼스 씨!"], ["If any man had told me when I first went to John Jarndyce’s house that he was anything but the disinterested friend he seemed—that he was what he has gradually turned out to be—I could have found no words strong enough to repel the slander; I could not have defended him too ardently.", "내가 처음 존 잔다이스 씨의 집에 갔을 때 누군가 나에게, 그가 겉보기와 달리 사심 없는 벗이 아니라고—그가 차츰 드러난 그런 사람이라고—말했다면, 나는 그 중상을 물리칠 만큼 강한 말을 찾지 못했을 겁니다. 아무리 열렬히 그를 변호해도 모자랐을 겁니다."], ["So little did I know of the world!", "그만큼 나는 세상을 몰랐던 거지요! 그런데 지금은 당신에게 분명히 선언하건대, 그가 나에게는 이 소송의 화신이 되었습니다."], ["Whereas now I do declare to you that he becomes to me the embodiment of the suit; that in place of its being an abstraction, it is John Jarndyce; that the more I suffer, the more indignant I am with him; that every new delay and every new disappointment is only a new injury from John Jarndyce’s hand.”", "소송이 하나의 추상이 아니라, 곧 존 잔다이스라는 겁니다. 내가 괴로울수록 그에게 더 분개하게 되고, 새로운 지연이 닥칠 때마다, 새로운 실망이 닥칠 때마다 그건 그저 존 잔다이스의 손에서 나온 새로운 해코지일 뿐이라는 겁니다.\""]], [["“No, no,” says Vholes.", "\"아니, 아니지요.\""], ["“Don’t say so.", "볼스가 말한다."], ["We ought to have patience, all of us.", "\"그렇게는 말씀하지 마십시오. 우리 모두 인내해야 합니다."], ["Besides, I never disparage, sir.", "게다가 나는 결코 남을 헐뜯지 않습니다, 선생."], ["I never disparage.”", "나는 결코 남을 헐뜯지 않아요.\""]], [["“Mr. Vholes,”", "\"볼스 씨.\""], ["returns the angry client.", "성난 의뢰인이 대꾸한다."], ["“You know as well as I that he would have strangled the suit if he could.”", "\"그가 할 수만 있었다면 이 소송을 목 졸라 죽였으리라는 걸 당신도 나만큼 잘 알지 않습니까.\""]], [["“He was not active in it,” Mr.", "\"그분이 소송에 적극적이지 않았던 건 맞습니다.\""], ["Vholes admits with an appearance of reluctance.", "볼스 씨가 마지못한 기색으로 인정한다."], ["“He certainly was not active in it.", "\"분명 적극적이지 않았지요."], ["But however, but however, he might have had amiable intentions.", "하지만 그래도, 그래도, 그분에게 선량한 의도가 있었을지도 모릅니다."], ["Who can read the heart, Mr. C.!”", "누가 사람의 마음을 읽을 수 있겠습니까, 카스톤 씨!\""]], [["“You can,”", "\"당신은 할 수 있지요.\""], ["returns Richard.", "리처드가 대꾸한다."]], [["“Well enough to know what his intentions were.", "\"그의 의도가 무엇이었는지 알 만큼은요."], ["Are or are not our interests conflicting?", "우리 이익이 충돌하는 겁니까, 아닙니까?"], ["Tell—me—that!”", "그걸—나에게—말해 보세요!\""], ["says Richard, accompanying his last three words with three raps on his rock of trust.", "리처드는 마지막 세 마디를 그 믿음의 반석에 세 번 똑똑 두드리는 것과 함께 내뱉는다."]], [["“Mr. C.,”", "\"카스톤 씨.\""], ["returns Vholes, immovable in attitude and never winking his hungry eyes, “I should be wanting in my duty as your professional adviser, I should be departing from my fidelity to your interests, if I represented those interests as identical with the interests of Mr.", "볼스가 자세 하나 흐트러지지 않고, 굶주린 두 눈을 한 번도 깜박이지 않은 채 대꾸한다. \"선생의 직업적 조언자로서 내 의무를 저버리는 것이요, 선생의 이익에 대한 내 충실함에서 벗어나는 것이겠지요, 만일 내가 그 이익을 잔다이스 씨의 이익과 동일한 것으로 내세운다면 말입니다."], ["Jarndyce. They are no such thing, sir.", "그것들은 결코 같은 게 아닙니다, 선생."], ["I never impute motives; I both have and am a father, and I never impute motives.", "나는 결코 남의 동기를 함부로 단정하지 않습니다. 나에게는 아버지가 있고 나 또한 아버지인지라, 결코 남의 동기를 함부로 단정하지 않지요."], ["But I must not shrink from a professional duty, even if it sows dissensions in families.", "하지만 그것이 가문에 불화의 씨를 뿌리는 한이 있더라도, 나는 직업적 의무에서 움츠러들어서는 안 됩니다."], ["I understand you to be now consulting me professionally as to your interests?", "선생은 지금 선생의 이익에 관해 직업적으로 나와 상의하고 계신 걸로 압니다만?"], ["You are so?", "그러신 거지요?"], ["I reply, then, they are not identical with those of Mr. Jarndyce.”", "그러면 답하겠습니다, 그것들은 잔다이스 씨의 이익과 동일하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Of course they are not!”", "\"당연히 같지 않지요!\""], ["cries Richard.", "리처드가 외친다."], ["“You found that out long ago.”", "\"당신은 그걸 오래전에 알아냈잖아요.\""]], [["“Mr. C.,”", "\"카스톤 씨.\""], ["returns Vholes, “I wish to say no more of any third party than is necessary.", "볼스가 대꾸한다. \"어떤 제삼자에 관해서도 나는 필요 이상으로는 말하고 싶지 않습니다."], ["I wish to leave my good name unsullied, together with any little property of which I may become possessed through industry and perseverance, to my daughters Emma, Jane, and Caroline.", "나는 내 좋은 이름을, 그리고 근면과 끈기로 손에 넣게 될지 모를 보잘것없는 재산을 더럽히지 않고, 내 딸 에마와 제인과 캐롤라인에게 물려주고 싶습니다."], ["I also desire to live in amity with my professional brethren. When Mr.", "또한 나는 내 직업 형제들과 화목하게 지내고 싶고요."], ["Skimpole did me the honour, sir—I will not say the very high honour, for I never stoop to flattery—of bringing us together in this room, I mentioned to you that I could offer no opinion or advice as to your interests while those interests were entrusted to another member of the profession.", "스킴폴 씨가 나에게 영광을—아주 대단한 영광이라고는 하지 않겠습니다, 나는 결코 아첨에 굽실대지 않으니까요—우리 둘을 이 방에서 만나게 해 주는 영광을 베푸셨을 때, 선생, 나는 선생의 이익이 우리 업계의 다른 인사에게 맡겨져 있는 동안에는 그 이익에 관해 아무 의견도 조언도 드릴 수 없다고 말씀드렸습니다. 그리고 나는 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에 관해 마땅히 해야 할 말을 했지요, 그 사무소는 명망이 높으니까요."], ["And I spoke in such terms as I was bound to speak of Kenge and Carboy’s office, which stands high.", "그런데도 선생은 그 보호에서 선생의 이익을 거두어 나에게 맡기는 것이 옳다고 여기셨습니다."], ["You, sir, thought fit to withdraw your interests from that keeping nevertheless and to offer them to me.", "선생은 깨끗한 손으로 그것을 가져오셨고, 선생, 나도 깨끗한 손으로 받았습니다. 그 이익은 이제 이 사무소에서 최우선입니다."], ["You brought them with clean hands, sir, and I accepted them with clean hands.", "내 소화 기능은, 내가 말씀드린 걸 들으셨을지 모르나, 상태가 좋지 않아서 쉬면 좀 나아지겠지만, 선생, 내가 선생의 대리인인 한 나는 쉬지 않을 겁니다."], ["Those interests are now paramount in this office.", "선생이 나를 원하실 때면 언제든 여기서 나를 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다."], ["My digestive functions, as you may have heard me mention, are not in a good state, and rest might improve them; but I shall not rest, sir, while I am your representative.", "어디로든 나를 부르시면, 가겠습니다. 장기 휴정 동안, 선생, 나는 짬을 내어 선생의 이익을 점점 더 면밀히 연구하고, 미카엘 축일 개정 기간 이후 천지를(물론 대법원장까지 포함해서) 뒤흔들 채비를 마련하는 데 바치겠습니다."], ["Whenever you want me, you will find me here.", "그리고 내가 마침내 선생을 축하드릴 때면, 선생,\""], ["Summon me anywhere, and I will come. During the long vacation, sir, I shall devote my leisure to studying your interests more and more closely and to making arrangements for moving heaven and earth (including, of course, the Chancellor) after Michaelmas term; and when I ultimately congratulate you, sir,”", "볼스 씨가 단단히 결심한 사람다운 엄숙함으로 말한다."], ["says Mr. Vholes with the severity of a determined man, “when I ultimately congratulate you, sir, with all my heart, on your accession to fortune—which, but that I never give hopes, I might say something further about—you will owe me nothing beyond whatever little balance may be then outstanding of the costs as between solicitor and client not included in the taxed costs allowed out of the estate.", "\"내가 마침내 진심을 다해 선생의 재산 상속을 축하드릴 때면—그에 관해서는, 내가 결코 희망을 주지 않으니 그렇지, 안 그랬다면 한마디 더 보탤 수도 있겠습니다만—선생은 그때 미결로 남은 약간의 잔액 말고는 나에게 빚진 게 없을 겁니다, 곧 그 재산에서 인정된 사정(査定) 비용에 포함되지 않은, 변호사와 의뢰인 사이의 비용 말입니다."], ["I pretend to no claim upon you, Mr. C., but for the zealous and active discharge—not the languid and routine discharge, sir: that much credit I stipulate for—of my professional duty.", "나는 선생에게 아무 청구권도 주장하지 않습니다, 카스톤 씨, 다만 내 직업적 의무를 열의 있고 능동적으로 이행한 것에 대해서만은—나른하고 판에 박힌 이행이 아니라요, 선생, 그만큼의 공로는 분명히 해 두겠습니다."], ["My duty prosperously ended, all between us is ended.”", "내 의무가 순조롭게 끝나면, 우리 사이의 모든 것도 끝납니다.\""]], [["Vholes finally adds, by way of rider to this declaration of his principles, that as Mr. Carstone is about to rejoin his regiment, perhaps Mr.", "볼스는 마침내 이 신조 선언에 덧붙임처럼 한마디를 더한다."], ["C. will favour him with an order on his agent for twenty pounds on account.", "곧 카스톤 씨가 곧 연대로 복귀할 참이니, 어쩌면 카스톤 씨가 그의 대리인 앞으로 우선 이십 파운드를 지급하라는 지시서를 호의로 써 주시면 어떻겠냐고."]], [["“For there have been many little consultations and attendances of late, sir,”", "\"근래에 자잘한 상담과 출석이 많았던 데다, 선생,\""], ["observes Vholes, turning over the leaves of his diary, “and these things mount up, and I don’t profess to be a man of capital.", "볼스가 자기 일지를 한 장 한 장 넘기며 말한다. \"이런 것들이 쌓이고, 나는 자본이 넉넉한 사람이라고는 자처하지 않으니까요."], ["When we first entered on our present relations I stated to you openly—it is a principle of mine that there never can be too much openness between solicitor and client—that I was not a man of capital and that if capital was your object you had better leave your papers in Kenge’s office.", "우리가 처음 지금의 관계를 맺었을 때 나는 선생에게 솔직히 말씀드렸지요—변호사와 의뢰인 사이에는 아무리 솔직해도 지나치지 않다는 게 내 신조입니다—나는 자본이 넉넉한 사람이 아니며, 만일 자본이 선생의 목적이라면 차라리 서류를 켄지 사무소에 두는 편이 낫다고요. 그렇습니다, 카스톤 씨, 여기서는 자본의 이점도 단점도 찾으실 수 없을 겁니다, 선생."], ["No, Mr.", "이것이,\""], ["C., you will find none of the advantages or disadvantages of capital here, sir.", "볼스가 책상을 다시 한 번 텅 비게 친다. \"선생의 반석입니다."], ["This,” Vholes gives the desk one hollow blow again, “is your rock; it pretends to be nothing more.”", "그것은 그 이상인 척하지 않습니다.\""]], [["The client, with his dejection insensibly relieved and his vague hopes rekindled, takes pen and ink and writes the draft, not without perplexed consideration and calculation of the date it may bear, implying scant effects in the agent’s hands.", "의뢰인은, 풀죽었던 마음이 자기도 모르게 누그러지고 막연한 희망이 다시 지펴져, 펜과 잉크를 들어 지시서를 작성하는데, 거기 적을 날짜를 두고 적잖이 골머리를 앓으며 헤아린다, 대리인의 손에 든 자금이 변변치 못함을 넌지시 드러내면서."], ["All the while, Vholes, buttoned up in body and mind, looks at him attentively.", "그러는 내내 볼스는 몸도 마음도 단추를 꼭 채운 채 그를 유심히 바라본다."], ["All the while, Vholes’s official cat watches the mouse’s hole.", "그러는 내내 볼스의 관청 고양이는 생쥐 구멍을 지켜본다."]], [["Lastly, the client, shaking hands, beseeches Mr. Vholes, for heaven’s sake and earth’s sake, to do his utmost to “pull him through”", "마지막으로 의뢰인은 악수를 하며, 제발 하늘과 땅을 두고서, 자기를 대법원에서 \"끌어내 구해 달라고\""], ["the Court of Chancery. Mr.", "볼스 씨에게 온 힘을 다해 달라고 애원한다."], ["Vholes, who never gives hopes, lays his palm upon the client’s shoulder and answers with a smile, “Always here, sir.", "결코 희망을 주지 않는 볼스 씨는 의뢰인의 어깨에 손바닥을 얹고 미소 지으며 대답한다. \"늘 여기 있겠습니다, 선생."], ["Personally, or by letter, you will always find me here, sir, with my shoulder to the wheel.”", "직접이든 편지로든, 늘 여기서 수레바퀴에 어깨를 들이대고 있는 나를 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다, 선생.\""], ["Thus they part, and Vholes, left alone, employs himself in carrying sundry little matters out of his diary into his draft bill book for the ultimate behoof of his three daughters.", "이렇게 그들은 헤어지고, 혼자 남은 볼스는 자기 일지에서 자질구레한 사항 몇 가지를 청구서 초안 장부로 옮겨 적는 일에 몰두하니, 마침내 세 딸을 이롭게 할 요량에서다."], ["So might an industrious fox or bear make up his account of chickens or stray travellers with an eye to his cubs, not to disparage by that word the three raw-visaged, lank, and buttoned-up maidens who dwell with the parent Vholes in an earthy cottage situated in a damp garden at Kennington.", "부지런한 여우나 곰도 제 새끼들을 염두에 두고 잡은 병아리나 길 잃은 나그네의 셈을 그렇게 매기리라—그렇다고 그 말로 케닝턴의 축축한 정원에 들어선 흙냄새 나는 오두막에서 아비 볼스와 함께 사는, 풋낯에 비쩍 마르고 단추를 꼭 채운 세 처녀를 헐뜯으려는 건 아니다."]], [["Richard, emerging from the heavy shade of Symond’s Inn into the sunshine of Chancery Lane—for there happens to be sunshine there to-day—walks thoughtfully on, and turns into Lincoln’s Inn, and passes under the shadow of the Lincoln’s Inn trees.", "리처드는 시먼즈 인의 짙은 그늘에서 챈서리 레인의 햇살 속으로—마침 오늘은 그곳에 햇살이 비치고 있다—걸어 나와, 생각에 잠긴 채 걸음을 옮기다가 링컨즈 인으로 접어들어, 링컨즈 인의 나무 그늘 밑을 지난다."], ["On many such loungers have the speckled shadows of those trees often fallen; on the like bent head, the bitten nail, the lowering eye, the lingering step, the purposeless and dreamy air, the good consuming and consumed, the life turned sour.", "그 나무들의 얼룩진 그림자는 이런 부류의 어슬렁대는 자들 위로 숱하게 드리워졌으리라. 똑같이 수그린 머리, 물어뜯은 손톱, 침울한 눈빛, 머뭇대는 발걸음, 목적도 없이 몽롱한 분위기, 좋은 것을 갉아먹다 갉아먹힌 모습, 시어 버린 인생 위로."], ["This lounger is not shabby yet, but that may come.", "이 어슬렁대는 자는 아직 추레하지는 않으나, 그것도 닥쳐올 수 있다."], ["Chancery, which knows no wisdom but in precedent, is very rich in such precedents; and why should one be different from ten thousand?", "선례 말고는 아무 지혜도 모르는 대법원은 그런 선례라면 차고도 넘치게 풍부하니, 어찌 한 사람이 만 명과 다르겠는가?"]], [["Yet the time is so short since his depreciation began that as he saunters away, reluctant to leave the spot for some long months together, though he hates it, Richard himself may feel his own case as if it were a startling one.", "그러나 그의 몰락이 시작된 지는 너무도 짧은 시간이라, 비록 그곳을 미워하면서도 여러 달 동안 통째로 떠나기를 못내 아쉬워하며 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나가는 동안, 리처드 자신은 제 사정이 무슨 깜짝 놀랄 일이라도 되는 듯 느낄지도 모른다."], ["While his heart is heavy with corroding care, suspense, distrust, and doubt, it may have room for some sorrowful wonder when he recalls how different his first visit there, how different he, how different all the colours of his mind.", "그의 마음이 갉아먹는 근심과 초조와 불신과 의혹으로 무거우면서도, 그 안에는 서글픈 의아함이 들어설 자리가 있을지 모른다, 그가 그곳에 처음 찾아갔을 때가 얼마나 달랐던가, 자기 자신이 얼마나 달랐던가, 제 마음의 온갖 빛깔이 얼마나 달랐던가를 떠올릴 때면 말이다."], ["But injustice breeds injustice; the fighting with shadows and being defeated by them necessitates the setting up of substances to combat; from the impalpable suit which no man alive can understand, the time for that being long gone by, it has become a gloomy relief to turn to the palpable figure of the friend who would have saved him from this ruin and make HIM his enemy.", "그러나 부당함은 부당함을 낳으니, 그림자와 싸우다가 그림자에게 패하다 보면 맞서 싸울 실체를 세워야만 한다. 살아 있는 그 누구도 이해할 수 없는—이미 이해할 시기는 한참 지나 버린—만질 수 없는 소송에서, 만질 수 있는 친구의 모습으로, 곧 자기를 이 파멸에서 구해 주려 했을 친구의 모습으로 눈을 돌려 그를 적으로 삼는 것이 음울한 위안이 되어 버린 것이다."], ["Richard has told Vholes the truth.", "리처드는 볼스에게 진실을 말했다."], ["Is he in a hardened or a softened mood, he still lays his injuries equally at that door; he was thwarted, in that quarter, of a set purpose, and that purpose could only originate in the one subject that is resolving his existence into itself; besides, it is a justification to him in his own eyes to have an embodied antagonist and oppressor.", "마음이 모질어진 상태든 누그러진 상태든, 그는 여전히 자기가 입은 상처를 한결같이 그 문 앞에 내려놓는다. 그는 그쪽에서 어떤 정해진 목적에 의해 좌절당했고, 그 목적이란 그의 존재를 통째로 제 안으로 녹여 들이고 있는 그 단 하나의 주제에서밖에 비롯될 수 없으며, 게다가 형체를 갖춘 적수와 압제자를 두는 것은 그의 눈에 스스로를 정당화하는 일이기도 하다."]], [["Is Richard a monster in all this, or would Chancery be found rich in such precedents too if they could be got for citation from the Recording Angel?", "이 모든 일에서 리처드는 괴물인가, 아니면 기록 천사로부터 인용해 올 수만 있다면 대법원에 그런 선례들 또한 차고도 넘치게 풍부한 것으로 드러날까? * 기록 천사: 사람들의 행적을 빠짐없이 적어 두는 천사로, 최후 심판 때 그 기록이 증거가 된다고 한다."]], [["Two pairs of eyes not unused to such people look after him, as, biting his nails and brooding, he crosses the square and is swallowed up by the shadow of the southern gateway. Mr.", "이런 부류의 사람들을 보는 데 낯설지 않은 두 쌍의 눈이, 그가 손톱을 물어뜯으며 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 채 광장을 가로질러 남쪽 문의 그림자 속으로 삼켜질 때까지 그의 뒷모습을 좇는다."], ["Guppy and Mr. Weevle are the possessors of those eyes, and they have been leaning in conversation against the low stone parapet under the trees.", "그 눈의 임자는 거피 씨와 위블 씨로, 그들은 나무 밑 야트막한 돌 난간에 기대어 이야기를 나누고 있던 참이다."], ["He passes close by them, seeing nothing but the ground.", "그는 그들 바로 곁을 지나가지만, 땅바닥 말고는 아무것도 보지 못한다."]], [["“William,” says Mr.", "\"윌리엄,\""], ["Weevle, adjusting his whiskers, “there’s combustion going on there!", "위블 씨가 구레나룻을 매만지며 말한다. \"저기 안에서 연소(燃燒)가 일어나고 있어!"], ["It’s not a case of spontaneous, but it’s smouldering combustion it is.”", "자연발화는 아니지만, 그래도 속으로 부글부글 타오르는 연소라고.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."], ["“He wouldn’t keep out of Jarndyce, and I suppose he’s over head and ears in debt.", "\"그 친구는 잔다이스 소송에서 손을 떼려 하지 않았으니, 빚더미에 머리끝까지 잠겨 있겠지."], ["I never knew much of him.", "나는 그 친구를 잘 알지도 못했어."], ["He was as high as the monument when he was on trial at our place.", "우리 사무소에서 수습을 받을 때 보면 기념탑처럼 콧대가 높았거든."], ["A good riddance to me, whether as clerk or client!", "서기로든 의뢰인으로든 내겐 시원하게 떠나 준 셈이지!"], ["Well, Tony, that as I was mentioning is what they’re up to.”", "자, 토니, 내가 말한 게 바로 저들이 꾸미는 일이야.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy, refolding his arms, resettles himself against the parapet, as resuming a conversation of interest.", "거피 씨는 다시 팔짱을 끼고, 흥미로운 대화를 이어 가려는 듯 난간에 기대어 자세를 고쳐 잡는다."]], [["“They are still up to it, sir,” says Mr.", "\"저들은 아직도 그 일에 매달려 있어, 선생,\""], ["Guppy, “still taking stock, still examining papers, still going over the heaps and heaps of rubbish.", "거피 씨가 말한다. \"아직도 재고를 조사하고, 아직도 서류를 살피고, 아직도 산더미 같은 쓰레기를 뒤지고 있지."], ["At this rate they’ll be at it these seven years.”", "이런 속도라면 앞으로 칠 년은 거기 붙어 있겠어.\""]], [["“And Small is helping?”", "\"그리고 스몰이 거들고 있다고?\""]], [["“Small left us at a week’s notice.", "\"스몰은 일주일 전에 통보하고 우리를 떠났어."], ["Told Kenge his grandfather’s business was too much for the old gentleman and he could better himself by undertaking it.", "켄지에게 말하기를, 자기 할아버지 사업이 그 노인장에게는 벅차고, 자기가 그걸 맡으면 형편이 나아지겠다고 했다더군."], ["There had been a coolness between myself and Small on account of his being so close.", "스몰이 워낙 속을 안 터놓는 통에 나하고는 사이가 좀 서먹했었지."], ["But he said you and I began it, and as he had me there—for we did—I put our acquaintance on the old footing.", "그런데 그 친구 말이, 그건 너하고 내가 먼저 시작한 일 아니냐는 거야—사실 그렇긴 했으니까—그래서 거기서 내가 한 방 먹은 셈이라, 우리 사이를 예전대로 돌려놨지."], ["That’s how I come to know what they’re up to.”", "그렇게 해서 내가 저들이 꾸미는 일을 알게 된 거야.\""]], [["“You haven’t looked in at all?”", "\"넌 안에 한 번도 들여다본 적이 없고?\""]], [["“Tony,” says Mr.", "\"토니,\""], ["Guppy, a little disconcerted, “to be unreserved with you, I don’t greatly relish the house, except in your company, and therefore I have not; and therefore I proposed this little appointment for our fetching away your things.", "거피 씨가 조금 멋쩍어하며 말한다. \"너한테 숨김없이 말하자면, 나는 너랑 같이 있을 때 말고는 그 집이 영 마음에 안 들어서, 그래서 안 들여다봤어."], ["There goes the hour by the clock! Tony”—Mr.", "그래서 네 물건을 가지러 가자고 이 작은 약속을 잡은 거고."], ["Guppy becomes mysteriously and tenderly eloquent—“it is necessary that I should impress upon your mind once more that circumstances over which I have no control have made a melancholy alteration in my most cherished plans and in that unrequited image which I formerly mentioned to you as a friend.", "자, 시계가 그 시각을 알리는군! 토니\"—거피 씨가 알 수 없게, 또 애틋하게 능변을 늘어놓기 시작한다—\"내가 네 마음에 다시 한 번 새겨 둬야겠어, 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정들이 내 가장 소중한 계획에, 그리고 전에 친구로서 너에게 말했던 그 보답 없는 형상에 서글픈 변화를 일으켰다는 걸 말이야."], ["That image is shattered, and that idol is laid low.", "그 형상은 산산조각 났고, 그 우상은 무너져 내렸어."], ["My only wish now in connexion with the objects which I had an idea of carrying out in the court with your aid as a friend is to let ’em alone and bury ’em in oblivion.", "친구로서 네 도움을 받아 그 법정에서 이루어 볼까 생각했던 목적들과 관련해 지금 내 유일한 바람은, 그것들을 그냥 내버려 두고 망각 속에 묻어 버리는 거야."], ["Do you think it possible, do you think it at all likely (I put it to you, Tony, as a friend), from your knowledge of that capricious and deep old character who fell a prey to the—spontaneous element, do you, Tony, think it at all likely that on second thoughts he put those letters away anywhere, after you saw him alive, and that they were not destroyed that night?”", "너는 가능하다고 보냐, 조금이라도 그럴 법하다고 보냐(친구로서 너한테 묻는 거다, 토니), 그—자연발화의—기운에 먹잇감이 된 그 변덕스럽고 속 깊은 늙은이의 성격을 네가 아는 한에서 말이야, 토니, 너는 그자가 다시 생각해서, 네가 살아 있는 그자를 본 뒤에 그 편지들을 어디 다른 데 치워 두고, 그래서 그것들이 그날 밤에 불타 없어지지 않았을 가능성이 조금이라도 있다고 보냐?\""]], [["Mr. Weevle reflects for some time.", "위블 씨는 한동안 곰곰이 생각한다."], ["Shakes his head.", "고개를 가로젓는다."], ["Decidedly thinks not.", "단연코 아니라고 본다."]], [["“Tony,” says Mr.", "\"토니,\""], ["Guppy as they walk towards the court, “once again understand me, as a friend.", "거피 씨가 두 사람이 그 법정 쪽으로 걸어가며 말한다. \"다시 한 번 친구로서 나를 이해해 줘."], ["Without entering into further explanations, I may repeat that the idol is down.", "더 자세히 설명하지는 않더라도, 우상이 무너졌다는 건 거듭 말할 수 있어."], ["I have no purpose to serve now but burial in oblivion.", "이제 내가 이루려는 목적은 망각 속에 묻어 버리는 것밖에 없어."], ["To that I have pledged myself.", "거기에 나는 나 자신을 걸었어."], ["I owe it to myself, and I owe it to the shattered image, as also to the circumstances over which I have no control.", "나 자신을 위해서도, 산산조각 난 그 형상을 위해서도, 또 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정들을 위해서도 마땅히 그래야 하는 일이지."], ["If you was to express to me by a gesture, by a wink, that you saw lying anywhere in your late lodgings any papers that so much as looked like the papers in question, I would pitch them into the fire, sir, on my own responsibility.”", "만일 네가 몸짓으로든 눈짓으로든 나에게, 네가 전에 머물던 그 하숙에 문제의 그 편지들과 조금이라도 비슷해 보이는 서류가 어디 놓여 있는 걸 봤다고 표시해 준다면, 선생, 나는 내 책임으로 그것들을 불 속에 던져 넣겠어.\""]], [["Mr. Weevle nods.", "위블 씨가 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["Mr. Guppy, much elevated in his own opinion by having delivered these observations, with an air in part forensic and in part romantic—this gentleman having a passion for conducting anything in the form of an examination, or delivering anything in the form of a summing up or a speech—accompanies his friend with dignity to the court.", "거피 씨는, 일부는 법정 변론조로 일부는 낭만조로 이 견해들을 늘어놓은 데 스스로 크게 우쭐해져서—이 신사는 무엇이든 심문하는 형식으로 진행하거나, 무엇이든 논고나 변론 형식으로 늘어놓는 데 열정을 품고 있다—위엄 있게 친구를 데리고 그 법정으로 향한다."]], [["Never since it has been a court has it had such a Fortunatus’ purse of gossip as in the proceedings at the rag and bottle shop. Regularly, every morning at eight, is the elder Mr.", "그 법정이 법정이 된 이래로, 넝마와 병을 파는 가게에서 벌어지는 일만큼 화수분 같은 잡담거리를 가진 적은 없었다."], ["Smallweed brought down to the corner and carried in, accompanied by Mrs. Smallweed, Judy, and Bart; and regularly, all day, do they all remain there until nine at night, solaced by gipsy dinners, not abundant in quantity, from the cook’s shop, rummaging and searching, digging, delving, and diving among the treasures of the late lamented.", "어김없이 아침 여덟 시면 노인 스몰위드 씨가 그 모퉁이로 실려 내려와, 스몰위드 부인과 주디, 바트를 거느리고 안으로 옮겨진다."], ["What those treasures are they keep so secret that the court is maddened. In its delirium it imagines guineas pouring out of tea-pots, crown-pieces overflowing punch-bowls, old chairs and mattresses stuffed with Bank of England notes.", "그리고 어김없이 온종일 그들 모두는 밤 아홉 시까지 거기 머물며, 요릿집에서 가져온, 양으로는 넉넉지 않은 집시 식사로 요기를 달래면서, 고인이 된 그 안타까운 분의 보물들을 뒤지고 캐고, 파헤치고 파고들고 잠긴다."], ["It possesses itself of the sixpenny history (with highly coloured folding frontispiece) of Mr.", "그 보물들이 무엇인지 그들이 어찌나 꽁꽁 숨기는지 법정 골목 전체가 미칠 지경이다."], ["Daniel Dancer and his sister, and also of Mr. Elwes, of Suffolk, and transfers all the facts from those authentic narratives to Mr.", "그 골목은 정신착란에 빠져, 찻주전자에서 기니 금화가 쏟아지고, 펀치 사발에서 크라운 은화가 넘쳐흐르며, 낡은 의자와 매트리스마다 잉글랜드 은행권이 가득 채워져 있는 광경을 상상한다."], ["Krook. Twice when the dustman is called in to carry off a cartload of old paper, ashes, and broken bottles, the whole court assembles and pries into the baskets as they come forth.", "골목은 대니얼 댄서 씨와 그 누이의 6페니짜리 전기(요란하게 채색된 접이식 권두 삽화가 딸린)와, 서퍽의 엘위스 씨의 전기까지 손에 넣고서, 그 믿을 만한 이야기들에 담긴 온갖 사실을 죄다 크룩 씨에게 옮겨 붙인다."], ["Many times the two gentlemen who write with the ravenous little pens on the tissue-paper are seen prowling in the neighbourhood—shy of each other, their late partnership being dissolved. The Sol skilfully carries a vein of the prevailing interest through the Harmonic nights.", "청소부가 헌 종이와 재와 깨진 병을 한 수레 실어 나르러 불려 올 때면 두 번이나 온 골목이 모여들어, 광주리가 나오는 족족 그 안을 들여다본다."], ["Little Swills, in what are professionally known as “patter” allusions to the subject, is received with loud applause; and the same vocalist “gags”", "박엽지 위에 게걸스러운 작은 펜으로 글을 쓰던 두 신사가 그 근방을 어슬렁대는 모습이 여러 번 목격되니—동업 관계가 깨진 뒤로 서로를 멀리하는 사이다."], ["in the regular business like a man inspired. Even Miss M.", "솔 술집은 화음의 밤마다 그 만연한 관심의 광맥을 솜씨 좋게 한 줄기 꿰어 흐르게 한다."], ["Melvilleson, in the revived Caledonian melody of “We’re a-Nodding,” points the sentiment that “the dogs love broo”", "어린 스윌스가, 업계 용어로 \"주절거리기\"라 불리는 방식으로 그 화제를 빗대면 우레 같은 박수를 받고, 같은 가수가 정규 공연에서 영감에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 \"즉흥 익살\"을 부린다."], ["(whatever the nature of that refreshment may be) with such archness and such a turn of the head towards next door that she is immediately understood to mean Mr. Smallweed loves to find money, and is nightly honoured with a double encore.", "멜빌슨 양마저, 다시 유행한 칼레도니아 가락 \"우리는 끄덕이고 있어요\"에서 \"개들은 국물을 좋아해\"라는(그 음식의 정체가 무엇이든 간에) 대목을 어찌나 능청맞게, 또 옆집 쪽으로 고개를 어찌나 잘 틀며 강조하는지, 곧바로 스몰위드 씨가 돈 찾기를 좋아한다는 뜻으로 알아들어, 밤마다 두 차례 앙코르의 영예를 받는다."], ["For all this, the court discovers nothing; and as Mrs.", "이 모든 일에도 불구하고 골목은 아무것도 알아내지 못한다."], ["Piper and Mrs. Perkins now communicate to the late lodger whose appearance is the signal for a general rally, it is in one continual ferment to discover everything, and more.", "그리고 이제 파이퍼 부인과 퍼킨스 부인이, 모습을 드러내기만 하면 사람들이 죄다 우르르 모여드는 그 옛 하숙인에게 알려 주는 바, 골목은 모든 것을, 아니 그 이상을 알아내려 끊임없이 들끓고 있다."]], [["Mr. Weevle and Mr.", "위블 씨와 거피 씨는, 골목의 모든 눈이 자기들에게 쏠린 가운데, 한껏 인기 높은 채로, 고인이 된 그 안타까운 분의 집 닫힌 문을 두드린다."], ["Guppy, with every eye in the court’s head upon them, knock at the closed door of the late lamented’s house, in a high state of popularity. But being contrary to the court’s expectation admitted, they immediately become unpopular and are considered to mean no good.", "그러나 골목의 예상과 달리 안으로 들여보내지자, 그들은 곧장 인기를 잃고, 좋은 일을 꾸미는 게 아니라고 여겨진다."]], [["The shutters are more or less closed all over the house, and the ground-floor is sufficiently dark to require candles.", "집 안 곳곳의 덧창은 어지간히 다 닫혀 있고, 일층은 촛불이 필요할 만큼 어둡다."], ["Introduced into the back shop by Mr. Smallweed the younger, they, fresh from the sunlight, can at first see nothing save darkness and shadows; but they gradually discern the elder Mr.", "젊은 스몰위드 씨에게 이끌려 뒷가게로 들어선 두 사람은, 햇빛 속에 있다가 막 들어온지라 처음에는 어둠과 그림자밖에 아무것도 보지 못한다."], ["Smallweed seated in his chair upon the brink of a well or grave of waste-paper, the virtuous Judy groping therein like a female sexton, and Mrs. Smallweed on the level ground in the vicinity snowed up in a heap of paper fragments, print, and manuscript which would appear to be the accumulated compliments that have been sent flying at her in the course of the day.", "그러나 차츰 분간하게 되니, 노인 스몰위드 씨는 폐지(廢紙)의 우물인지 무덤인지 그 가장자리에 놓인 의자에 앉아 있고, 갸륵한 주디는 그 안을 여자 묘지기처럼 더듬고 있으며, 스몰위드 부인은 그 옆 평지에서, 하루 동안 그녀에게 날아든 누적된 찬사인 듯한 종잇조각과 인쇄물과 필사본 더미에 눈처럼 파묻혀 있다."], ["The whole party, Small included, are blackened with dust and dirt and present a fiendish appearance not relieved by the general aspect of the room.", "스몰을 포함한 일행 전체가 먼지와 흙으로 시커멓게 그을려, 방의 전반적인 꼴로도 누그러지지 않는 악귀 같은 행색을 하고 있다."], ["There is more litter and lumber in it than of old, and it is dirtier if possible; likewise, it is ghostly with traces of its dead inhabitant and even with his chalked writing on the wall.", "방 안에는 예전보다 잡동사니와 폐물이 더 많고, 가능하다면 더 더러워졌으며, 또한 죽은 거주자의 자취로, 심지어 벽에 분필로 휘갈긴 그의 글씨로까지, 으스스하다."]], [["On the entrance of visitors, Mr. Smallweed and Judy simultaneously fold their arms and stop in their researches.", "방문객이 들어서자 스몰위드 씨와 주디는 동시에 팔짱을 끼고 뒤지던 일을 멈춘다."]], [["“Aha!”", "\"아하!\""], ["croaks the old gentleman.", "노인이 쉰 목소리로 까옥거린다."], ["“How de do, gentlemen, how de do!", "\"안녕들 하신가, 신사 양반들, 안녕들 하신가!"], ["Come to fetch your property, Mr. Weevle?", "자네 물건을 가지러 왔구먼, 위블 씨?"], ["That’s well, that’s well.", "잘됐어, 잘됐어."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["We should have been forced to sell you up, sir, to pay your warehouse room if you had left it here much longer.", "자네가 그걸 여기 더 오래 놔뒀더라면, 선생, 우리는 창고 사용료를 받느라 자네 물건을 경매에 부쳐야 했을 거야."], ["You feel quite at home here again, I dare say?", "또 이렇게 오니 아주 제집 같지, 아무렴?"], ["Glad to see you, glad to see you!”", "반갑네, 반가워!\""]], [["Mr. Weevle, thanking him, casts an eye about.", "위블 씨는 그에게 고맙다고 하며 방 안을 한번 둘러본다."], ["Mr. Guppy’s eye follows Mr.", "거피 씨의 눈이 위블 씨의 눈을 좇는다."], ["Weevle’s eye. Mr.", "위블 씨의 눈은 아무 새 정보도 담지 못하고 되돌아온다."], ["Weevle’s eye comes back without any new intelligence in it. Mr.", "거피 씨의 눈은 되돌아오다가 스몰위드 씨의 눈과 마주친다."], ["Guppy’s eye comes back and meets Mr. Smallweed’s eye.", "그 살가운 노인은 여전히, 태엽이 풀려 가는 어떤 악기처럼 중얼거린다."], ["That engaging old gentleman is still murmuring, like some wound-up instrument running down, “How de do, sir—how de—how—”", "\"안녕하신가, 선생—안녕—안—\""], ["And then having run down, he lapses into grinning silence, as Mr.", "그러다 태엽이 다 풀리자, 씩 웃는 침묵에 잠긴다."], ["Guppy starts at seeing Mr. Tulkinghorn standing in the darkness opposite with his hands behind him.", "그 순간 거피 씨는 맞은편 어둠 속에 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 서 있는 토킹혼 씨를 보고 흠칫 놀란다."]], [["“Gentleman so kind as to act as my solicitor,”", "\"이 신사분이 친절하게도 내 변호사 노릇을 해 주신다네.\""], ["says Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 할아버지가 말한다."], ["“I am not the sort of client for a gentleman of such note, but he is so good!”", "\"나는 이만한 명망가에게 어울리는 의뢰인이 못 되는데도, 이분이 참으로 좋은 분이라!\""]], [["Mr. Guppy, slightly nudging his friend to take another look, makes a shuffling bow to Mr.", "거피 씨는 친구를 슬쩍 쿡 찔러 한 번 더 보게 하고는, 토킹혼 씨에게 어정쩡하게 허리 굽혀 인사한다."], ["Tulkinghorn, who returns it with an easy nod.", "토킹혼 씨는 가벼운 끄덕임으로 답한다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn is looking on as if he had nothing else to do and were rather amused by the novelty.", "토킹혼 씨는 마치 달리 할 일이 없어, 이 새로운 구경거리가 오히려 재미있다는 듯 바라보고 있다."]], [["“A good deal of property here, sir, I should say,” Mr.", "\"여기 재산이 꽤 많은 모양입니다, 선생.\""], ["Guppy observes to Mr. Smallweed.", "거피 씨가 스몰위드 씨에게 한마디 한다."]], [["“Principally rags and rubbish, my dear friend!", "\"주로 넝마와 쓰레기일세, 이 사람아!"], ["Rags and rubbish!", "넝마와 쓰레기!"], ["Me and Bart and my granddaughter Judy are endeavouring to make out an inventory of what’s worth anything to sell.", "나하고 바트하고 손녀딸 주디가 팔아서 한 푼이라도 값나갈 만한 것들의 목록을 만들어 보려는 참이지."], ["But we haven’t come to much as yet; we—haven’t—come—to—hah!”", "하지만 아직 별로 못 추렸어. 우리는—아직—추리지—못—하—\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed has run down again, while Mr. Weevle’s eye, attended by Mr. Guppy’s eye, has again gone round the room and come back.", "스몰위드 씨가 또 태엽이 풀렸는데, 그동안 위블 씨의 눈은 거피 씨의 눈을 거느리고 또 한 번 방을 한 바퀴 돌고 되돌아온다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"자, 선생.\""], ["says Mr. Weevle.", "위블 씨가 말한다."], ["“We won’t intrude any longer if you’ll allow us to go upstairs.”", "\"위층으로 올라가도록 허락해 주신다면, 더는 폐 끼치지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Anywhere, my dear sir, anywhere!", "\"어디든지, 이 사람아, 어디든지!"], ["You’re at home.", "자네 집이나 다름없으니."], ["Make yourself so, pray!”", "부디 편히 굴게!\""]], [["As they go upstairs, Mr. Guppy lifts his eyebrows inquiringly and looks at Tony.", "두 사람이 위층으로 올라가며 거피 씨는 의아한 듯 눈썹을 치켜올리고 토니를 바라본다."], ["Tony shakes his head.", "토니는 고개를 가로젓는다."], ["They find the old room very dull and dismal, with the ashes of the fire that was burning on that memorable night yet in the discoloured grate.", "그 옛 방은 음울하고 침침하기 짝이 없고, 그 잊지 못할 밤에 타오르던 불의 재가 변색된 벽난로 안에 아직 남아 있다."], ["They have a great disinclination to touch any object, and carefully blow the dust from it first.", "그들은 어떤 물건에도 손대기를 몹시 꺼려, 무엇이든 먼저 조심스레 먼지를 불어 낸다."], ["Nor are they desirous to prolong their visit, packing the few movables with all possible speed and never speaking above a whisper.", "또한 머무는 시간을 늘리고 싶지도 않아, 몇 안 되는 가져갈 물건들을 한껏 서둘러 챙기며, 결코 속삭임보다 높게 말하지 않는다."]], [["“Look here,”", "\"이것 봐,\""], ["says Tony, recoiling.", "토니가 흠칫 물러나며 말한다."], ["“Here’s that horrible cat coming in!”", "\"저 끔찍한 고양이가 들어오잖아!\""]], [["Mr. Guppy retreats behind a chair.", "거피 씨가 의자 뒤로 물러선다."], ["“Small told me of her.", "\"스몰이 저 녀석 얘기를 해 줬어."], ["She went leaping and bounding and tearing about that night like a dragon, and got out on the house-top, and roamed about up there for a fortnight, and then came tumbling down the chimney very thin.", "그날 밤 용처럼 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰고 날뛰며 사방을 할퀴고 다니다가 지붕 위로 빠져나가, 거기서 보름 동안 어슬렁대다가, 그러고는 굴뚝으로 비쩍 마른 꼴로 굴러떨어졌대."], ["Did you ever see such a brute?", "너 저런 짐승 본 적 있냐?"], ["Looks as if she knew all about it, don’t she?", "모든 걸 다 안다는 표정이잖아, 안 그래?"], ["Almost looks as if she was Krook.", "거의 자기가 크룩인 것 같은 꼴이라니까."], ["Shoohoo!", "쉿쉿!"], ["Get out, you goblin!”", "저리 가, 이 도깨비야!\""]], [["Lady Jane, in the doorway, with her tiger snarl from ear to ear and her club of a tail, shows no intention of obeying; but Mr.", "레이디 제인은 문간에서 귀에서 귀까지 호랑이처럼 으르렁대고 곤봉 같은 꼬리를 세운 채, 따를 기색을 전혀 보이지 않는다."], ["Tulkinghorn stumbling over her, she spits at his rusty legs, and swearing wrathfully, takes her arched back upstairs.", "그러나 토킹혼 씨가 그 녀석에 걸려 비틀거리자, 녀석은 그의 녹슨 듯한 다리를 향해 침을 칵 뱉고, 노여워 욕을 하듯 으르렁대며, 등을 활처럼 곤두세우고 위층으로 올라간다."], ["Possibly to roam the house-tops again and return by the chimney.", "어쩌면 또 지붕 위를 어슬렁대다가 굴뚝으로 돌아오려는 모양이다."]], [["“Mr. Guppy,”", "\"거피 씨.\""], ["says Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["Tulkinghorn, “could I have a word with you?”", "\"잠깐 한마디 나눌 수 있겠소?\""]], [["Mr. Guppy is engaged in collecting the Galaxy Gallery of British Beauty from the wall and depositing those works of art in their old ignoble band-box.", "거피 씨는 벽에서 영국 미인 화랑 전집을 거두어, 그 예술품들을 예의 볼품없는 모자 상자에 담느라 여념이 없다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["he returns, reddening, “I wish to act with courtesy towards every member of the profession, and especially, I am sure, towards a member of it so well known as yourself—I will truly add, sir, so distinguished as yourself.", "그가 얼굴을 붉히며 대꾸한다. \"나는 같은 업계의 어느 분에게나 예의를 지키고 싶고, 특히 선생처럼 잘 알려진 분에게라면 더더욱 그렇습니다—진심으로 덧붙이건대, 선생, 선생처럼 저명한 분에게라면요."], ["Still, Mr. Tulkinghorn, sir, I must stipulate that if you have any word with me, that word is spoken in the presence of my friend.”", "그렇지만 토킹혼 씨, 선생, 만일 나에게 하실 말씀이 있다면, 그 말씀은 내 친구가 있는 자리에서 하시기를 요구해야겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed?”", "\"아, 그렇소?\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"그렇습니다, 선생."], ["My reasons are not of a personal nature at all, but they are amply sufficient for myself.”", "내 이유는 전혀 사사로운 것이 아니지만, 나로서는 충분하고도 남습니다.\""]], [["“No doubt, no doubt.” Mr.", "\"그렇겠지, 그렇겠지.\""], ["Tulkinghorn is as imperturbable as the hearthstone to which he has quietly walked.", "토킹혼 씨는, 그가 조용히 걸어가 선 벽난로 앞 돌처럼 태연하다."], ["“The matter is not of that consequence that I need put you to the trouble of making any conditions, Mr.", "\"이 일은 내가 자네에게 어떤 조건을 내거는 수고를 끼칠 만큼 중대하지도 않네, 거피 씨.\""], ["Guppy.” He pauses here to smile, and his smile is as dull and rusty as his pantaloons.", "여기서 그는 잠시 멈추어 미소 짓는데, 그 미소는 그의 바지만큼이나 칙칙하고 녹슬어 있다."], ["“You are to be congratulated, Mr.", "\"축하받을 일이야, 거피 씨."], ["Guppy; you are a fortunate young man, sir.”", "자네는 운 좋은 젊은이일세, 선생.\""]], [["“Pretty well so, Mr.", "\"그럭저럭 그렇지요, 토킹혼 씨."], ["Tulkinghorn; I don’t complain.”", "불평하진 않습니다.\""]], [["“Complain?", "\"불평이라니?"], ["High friends, free admission to great houses, and access to elegant ladies!", "지체 높은 친구들에, 대저택에 자유로이 드나들고, 우아한 귀부인들과 만날 수 있는데!"], ["Why, Mr. Guppy, there are people in London who would give their ears to be you.”", "아니, 거피 씨, 런던에는 자네가 될 수만 있다면 두 귀라도 내놓을 사람들이 있다네.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy, looking as if he would give his own reddening and still reddening ears to be one of those people at present instead of himself, replies, “Sir, if I attend to my profession and do what is right by Kenge and Carboy, my friends and acquaintances are of no consequence to them nor to any member of the profession, not excepting Mr.", "거피 씨는, 지금 자기 대신 그런 사람들 중 하나가 될 수만 있다면 점점 더 붉어지는 제 두 귀라도 기꺼이 내놓겠다는 듯한 표정으로 대꾸한다."], ["Tulkinghorn of the Fields.", "\"선생, 내가 내 직무에 충실하고 켄지 앤드 카보이에 옳게 처신하는 한, 내 친구와 지인이 누구든 그 회사에도, 업계의 어느 분에게도, 그 필즈의 토킹혼 씨에게조차도 아무 상관 없는 일입니다."], ["I am not under any obligation to explain myself further; and with all respect for you, sir, and without offence—I repeat, without offence—”", "나는 더 이상 나를 해명할 어떤 의무도 없습니다. 그리고 선생을 온전히 존경하면서, 또 결례 없이—거듭 말하건대, 결례 없이—\""]], [["“Oh, certainly!”", "\"아, 물론이지!\""]], [["“—I don’t intend to do it.”", "\"—나는 그럴 생각이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Quite so,”", "\"그렇고말고.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn with a calm nod.", "토킹혼 씨가 차분히 끄덕이며 말한다."], ["“Very good; I see by these portraits that you take a strong interest in the fashionable great, sir?”", "\"아주 좋아. 이 초상들을 보니 자네는 유행을 선도하는 명사들에게 큰 관심이 있는 모양이군, 선생?\""]], [["He addresses this to the astounded Tony, who admits the soft impeachment.", "그는 이 말을 어리둥절해진 토니에게 건네는데, 토니는 그 부드러운 책망을 인정한다."]], [["“A virtue in which few Englishmen are deficient,”", "\"영국인이라면 누구나 갖춘 미덕이지.\""], ["observes Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 한마디 한다."], ["He has been standing on the hearthstone with his back to the smoked chimney-piece, and now turns round with his glasses to his eyes.", "그는 그을린 벽난로 선반을 등지고 벽난로 앞 돌 위에 서 있다가, 이제 안경을 눈에 갖다 대고 돌아선다."], ["“Who is this?", "\"이건 누구지?"], ["‘Lady Dedlock.’ Ha!", "'데드록 마님'이라."], ["A very good likeness in its way, but it wants force of character.", "하! 나름대로 아주 잘 그린 초상이지만, 개성의 힘이 부족하군."], ["Good day to you, gentlemen; good day!”", "그럼 잘들 있게, 신사 양반들. 잘들 있어!\""]], [["When he has walked out, Mr. Guppy, in a great perspiration, nerves himself to the hasty completion of the taking down of the Galaxy Gallery, concluding with Lady Dedlock.", "그가 걸어 나가자, 거피 씨는 식은땀을 뻘뻘 흘리며 마음을 다잡고, 미인 화랑을 마저 황급히 떼어 내려, 데드록 마님으로 끝맺는다."]], [["“Tony,”", "\"토니,\""], ["he says hurriedly to his astonished companion, “let us be quick in putting the things together and in getting out of this place.", "그가 어리둥절해하는 동료에게 황급히 말한다. \"어서 물건들을 챙겨서 이 집에서 나가자."], ["It were in vain longer to conceal from you, Tony, that between myself and one of the members of a swan-like aristocracy whom I now hold in my hand, there has been undivulged communication and association.", "토니, 너에게 더는 숨겨 봐야 소용없겠다, 지금 내 손에 쥔, 백조처럼 우아한 귀족의 한 분과 나 사이에, 밝혀지지 않은 교신과 교제가 있었다는 걸 말이야. 너에게 그걸 털어놓을 때가 있었을지도 몰라."], ["The time might have been when I might have revealed it to you.", "하지만 이제 다시는 그럴 일이 없을 거야."], ["It never will be more. It is due alike to the oath I have taken, alike to the shattered idol, and alike to circumstances over which I have no control, that the whole should be buried in oblivion.", "내가 한 맹세를 봐서도, 산산조각 난 그 우상을 봐서도, 또 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정들을 봐서도, 그 모든 게 망각 속에 묻혀야 마땅해."], ["I charge you as a friend, by the interest you have ever testified in the fashionable intelligence, and by any little advances with which I may have been able to accommodate you, so to bury it without a word of inquiry!”", "친구로서 너에게 당부한다, 네가 늘 유행계 소식에 보여 온 관심을 봐서도, 또 내가 너에게 변통해 줄 수 있었던 자잘한 가불을 봐서도, 한마디 캐묻지 말고 그걸 그렇게 묻어 다오!\""]], [["This charge Mr. Guppy delivers in a state little short of forensic lunacy, while his friend shows a dazed mind in his whole head of hair and even in his cultivated whiskers.", "이 당부를 거피 씨는 거의 법정 변론의 광기에 가까운 상태로 늘어놓고, 그러는 동안 그의 친구는 온통 헝클어진 머리칼로도, 심지어 공들여 가꾼 구레나룻으로까지도 얼떨떨한 심정을 내비친다."]], [["National and Domestic\nEngland has been in a dreadful state for some weeks.", "잉글랜드는 몇 주째 끔찍한 상태에 빠져 있다."], ["Lord Coodle would go out, Sir Thomas Doodle wouldn’t come in, and there being nobody in Great Britain (to speak of) except Coodle and Doodle, there has been no government.", "쿠들 경은 물러나려 하고, 토머스 두들 경은 들어오려 하지 않으니, 쿠들과 두들 말고는 그레이트브리튼에 (말할 만한 사람이라곤) 아무도 없는 터라 정부가 없는 셈이다. 한때 불가피해 보이던 그 두 위대한 인물의 결투가 끝내 벌어지지 않은 건 그나마 다행한 일이다."], ["It is a mercy that the hostile meeting between those two great men, which at one time seemed inevitable, did not come off, because if both pistols had taken effect, and Coodle and Doodle had killed each other, it is to be presumed that England must have waited to be governed until young Coodle and young Doodle, now in frocks and long stockings, were grown up.", "만약 두 발의 권총이 모두 명중해 쿠들과 두들이 서로를 죽였더라면, 짐작건대 잉글랜드는 지금 짧은 윗옷에 긴 양말 차림인 어린 쿠들과 어린 두들이 다 자랄 때까지 통치받기를 기다려야 했을 테니 말이다. 그러나 이 엄청난 국가적 재난은 쿠들 경의 시의적절한 깨달음 덕에 모면되었다."], ["This stupendous national calamity, however, was averted by Lord Coodle’s making the timely discovery that if in the heat of debate he had said that he scorned and despised the whole ignoble career of Sir Thomas Doodle, he had merely meant to say that party differences should never induce him to withhold from it the tribute of his warmest admiration; while it as opportunely turned out, on the other hand, that Sir Thomas Doodle had in his own bosom expressly booked Lord Coodle to go down to posterity as the mirror of virtue and honour.", "토론이 달아오른 와중에 자신이 토머스 두들 경의 그 보잘것없는 평생 행적 전부를 경멸하고 멸시한다고 말하긴 했으나, 그것은 그저 당파의 차이가 결코 자신으로 하여금 그 행적에 대한 가장 따뜻한 찬탄의 헌사를 거두게 하지는 못하리라는 뜻이었을 뿐이라는 깨달음이었다. 한편 그와 똑같이 절묘하게도, 토머스 두들 경 역시 자기 가슴속에 쿠들 경을 미덕과 명예의 거울로 후세에 명백히 등재해두었다는 사실이 밝혀졌다."], ["Still England has been some weeks in the dismal strait of having no pilot (as was well observed by Sir Leicester Dedlock) to weather the storm; and the marvellous part of the matter is that England has not appeared to care very much about it, but has gone on eating and drinking and marrying and giving in marriage as the old world did in the days before the flood.", "그래도 잉글랜드는 (레스터 데드록 경이 잘 짚었듯이) 폭풍을 헤쳐 나갈 키잡이가 없는 음울한 곤경에 몇 주째 처해 있다. 게다가 놀라운 점은, 잉글랜드가 이를 그리 심하게 걱정하는 기색이 없이, 대홍수 이전 옛 세상이 그러했듯 먹고 마시고 장가들고 시집보내기를 그대로 이어간다는 것이다."], ["But Coodle knew the danger, and Doodle knew the danger, and all their followers and hangers-on had the clearest possible perception of the danger.", "하지만 쿠들도 위험을 알았고, 두들도 위험을 알았으며, 그들의 추종자와 빌붙은 무리 모두 더없이 또렷하게 위험을 알아차렸다."], ["At last Sir Thomas Doodle has not only condescended to come in, but has done it handsomely, bringing in with him all his nephews, all his male cousins, and all his brothers-in-law.", "마침내 토머스 두들 경은 들어오기로 응낙했을 뿐 아니라 그 일을 멋들어지게 해냈으니, 자기 조카들 전부와 사촌 형제들 전부와 처남 매부들 전부를 데리고 들어온 것이다."], ["So there is hope for the old ship yet.", "그러니 이 낡은 배에도 아직 희망이 있다."]], [["Doodle has found that he must throw himself upon the country, chiefly in the form of sovereigns and beer.", "두들은 자신을 나라에 내던져야 한다는 것을, 그것도 주로 금화와 맥주의 형태로 그래야 한다는 것을 알아냈다."], ["In this metamorphosed state he is available in a good many places simultaneously and can throw himself upon a considerable portion of the country at one time.", "이렇게 변신한 상태로 그는 여러 곳에 동시에 출현할 수 있고, 한 번에 나라의 상당한 부분에 자기를 내던질 수 있다."], ["Britannia being much occupied in pocketing Doodle in the form of sovereigns, and swallowing Doodle in the form of beer, and in swearing herself black in the face that she does neither—plainly to the advancement of her glory and morality—the London season comes to a sudden end, through all the Doodleites and Coodleites dispersing to assist Britannia in those religious exercises.", "브리타니아는 금화 형태의 두들을 호주머니에 챙기고, 맥주 형태의 두들을 들이켜며, 자신은 그런 짓을 하지 않는다고 얼굴이 시퍼레지도록 맹세하느라 — 두말할 것 없이 그 영광과 도덕을 드높이기 위해 — 무척 분주한 까닭에, 모든 두들파와 쿠들파가 그 종교적 수행을 거들러 사방으로 흩어지면서 런던 사교철은 별안간 막을 내린다."]], [["Hence Mrs.", "그리하여 체스니 월드의 가정부 라운스웰 부인은, 아직 어떠한 지시도 내려오지 않았으나, 머잖아 가족이 돌아오리라 내다본다."], ["Rouncewell, housekeeper at Chesney Wold, foresees, though no instructions have yet come down, that the family may shortly be expected, together with a pretty large accession of cousins and others who can in any way assist the great Constitutional work.", "그것도 사촌들과 그 밖에 저 위대한 헌정 사업에 무엇으로든 손을 보탤 수 있는 사람들이 꽤 큼직하게 늘어 함께 오리라고. 그리하여 이 위풍당당한 노부인은 시간의 앞머리채를 움켜쥐고서, 그를 이끌고 층계를 오르내리고 화랑과 복도를 따라 거닐며 방마다 두루 다닌다."], ["And hence the stately old dame, taking Time by the forelock, leads him up and down the staircases, and along the galleries and passages, and through the rooms, to witness before he grows any older that everything is ready, that floors are rubbed bright, carpets spread, curtains shaken out, beds puffed and patted, still-room and kitchen cleared for action—all things prepared as beseems the Dedlock dignity.", "시간이 더 늙기 전에 모든 게 준비되었음을, 마룻바닥은 반들거리게 닦였고 양탄자는 깔렸으며 커튼은 털어 펼쳐졌고 침대는 부풀려 두드려졌으며 식기실과 부엌은 출동 태세를 갖추었음을 — 데드록 가문의 위엄에 걸맞게 만사가 채비되었음을 — 똑똑히 목격하게 하려는 것이다."]], [["This present summer evening, as the sun goes down, the preparations are complete.", "지금 이 여름 저녁, 해가 저물 무렵 준비는 끝났다."], ["Dreary and solemn the old house looks, with so many appliances of habitation and with no inhabitants except the pictured forms upon the walls.", "거주에 필요한 온갖 도구를 다 갖추었으면서도 벽에 그려진 형상들 말고는 거주자가 하나도 없으니, 이 오래된 저택은 황량하고 엄숙해 보인다."], ["So did these come and go, a Dedlock in possession might have ruminated passing along; so did they see this gallery hushed and quiet, as I see it now; so think, as I think, of the gap that they would make in this domain when they were gone; so find it, as I find it, difficult to believe that it could be without them; so pass from my world, as I pass from theirs, now closing the reverberating door; so leave no blank to miss them, and so die.", "이 자리에 사는 어느 데드록이 화랑을 거닐며 이렇게 곱씹었을지도 모른다. 저들도 이렇게 왔다 갔구나, 저들도 지금 내가 보는 것처럼 이 화랑이 고요히 잠든 모습을 보았구나, 저들도 지금 내가 생각하듯 자신들이 떠나면 이 영지에 생길 빈자리를 생각했구나, 저들도 지금 내가 그러하듯 이곳이 자기들 없이 존재할 수 있으리라곤 믿기 어려워했구나, 저들도 저 울리는 문을 이제 닫으며 내가 그들의 세상에서 떠나듯 내 세상에서 떠나갔구나, 저들도 그리워할 빈자리 하나 남기지 못한 채 그렇게 죽어갔구나, 라고."]], [["Through some of the fiery windows beautiful from without, and set, at this sunset hour, not in dull-grey stone but in a glorious house of gold, the light excluded at other windows pours in rich, lavish, overflowing like the summer plenty in the land.", "바깥에서 보면 아름다운, 그래서 이 노을 진 시각에는 칙칙한 잿빛 돌이 아니라 찬란한 황금의 집에 박힌 듯한 그 불타는 창문 몇몇으로, 다른 창에서는 막혔던 빛이 풍성하고 아낌없이, 이 땅의 여름 풍요처럼 넘쳐흐르며 쏟아져 든다."], ["Then do the frozen Dedlocks thaw.", "그러면 얼어붙었던 데드록들이 녹는다."], ["Strange movements come upon their features as the shadows of leaves play there.", "잎새의 그림자가 그 위에서 어른거리자 그들의 이목구비에 기이한 움직임이 인다."], ["A dense justice in a corner is beguiled into a wink.", "한구석의 근엄한 판사는 어느새 한쪽 눈을 찡긋한다."], ["A staring baronet, with a truncheon, gets a dimple in his chin.", "곤봉을 든 채 노려보는 어느 준남작은 턱에 보조개가 팬다."], ["Down into the bosom of a stony shepherdess there steals a fleck of light and warmth that would have done it good a hundred years ago.", "돌로 된 어느 양치기 처녀의 가슴께로 빛과 온기의 얼룩 한 점이 스며드는데, 백 년 전이었다면 그녀에게 좋았을 그런 온기다."], ["One ancestress of Volumnia, in high-heeled shoes, very like her—casting the shadow of that virgin event before her full two centuries—shoots out into a halo and becomes a saint.", "굽 높은 신을 신은, 그녀와 무척 닮은 볼루미니아의 한 여조상은 — 그 처녀의 사건이 드리울 그림자를 꼬박 두 세기나 앞질러 던지며 — 후광을 내쏘며 성녀가 된다."], ["A maid of honour of the court of Charles the Second, with large round eyes (and other charms to correspond), seems to bathe in glowing water, and it ripples as it glows.", "커다랗고 둥근 눈을 (그리고 거기 어울리는 다른 매력들도) 지닌 찰스 이세 궁정의 한 시녀는 빛나는 물속에 멱을 감는 듯하고, 그 물은 빛나면서 잔물결을 인다."]], [["But the fire of the sun is dying.", "그러나 태양의 불길은 사위어간다."], ["Even now the floor is dusky, and shadow slowly mounts the walls, bringing the Dedlocks down like age and death.", "어느덧 마룻바닥은 어둑하고, 그림자는 벽을 천천히 기어오르며 데드록들을 늙음과 죽음처럼 끌어내린다."], ["And now, upon my Lady’s picture over the great chimney-piece, a weird shade falls from some old tree, that turns it pale, and flutters it, and looks as if a great arm held a veil or hood, watching an opportunity to draw it over her.", "그리고 이제, 커다란 벽난로 위에 걸린 마님의 초상화 위로 어느 늙은 나무에서 기괴한 그늘이 드리워, 그것을 창백하게 만들고 펄럭이게 하니, 마치 거대한 팔 하나가 베일이나 두건을 든 채 그녀에게 그것을 씌울 기회를 노리는 것만 같다."], ["Higher and darker rises shadow on the wall—now a red gloom on the ceiling—now the fire is out.", "벽 위로 그림자는 점점 높고 어둡게 솟아오르고 — 이제 천장에는 붉은 음영이 — 이제 불이 꺼진다."]], [["All that prospect, which from the terrace looked so near, has moved solemnly away and changed—not the first nor the last of beautiful things that look so near and will so change—into a distant phantom.", "테라스에서 보면 그토록 가까워 보이던 그 모든 전망이, 엄숙하게 멀어지며 — 그토록 가까워 보이다 그렇게 변해가는 아름다운 것들 중 처음도 마지막도 아닌 채 — 아득한 환영으로 바뀐다."], ["Light mists arise, and the dew falls, and all the sweet scents in the garden are heavy in the air.", "옅은 안개가 피어오르고 이슬이 내리며 정원의 온갖 향긋한 내음이 공기 속에 무겁게 잠긴다."], ["Now the woods settle into great masses as if they were each one profound tree.", "이제 숲은 마치 저마다 한 그루 깊디깊은 나무이기라도 한 듯 거대한 덩어리로 가라앉는다."], ["And now the moon rises to separate them, and to glimmer here and there in horizontal lines behind their stems, and to make the avenue a pavement of light among high cathedral arches fantastically broken.", "그리고 이제 달이 떠올라 그 덩어리들을 갈라놓고, 줄기 뒤로 여기저기 가로 선을 그리며 어른거리고, 환상적으로 부서진 높은 대성당 아치 사이로 가로수길을 빛의 포석으로 만든다."]], [["Now the moon is high; and the great house, needing habitation more than ever, is like a body without life.", "이제 달은 높이 떴고, 그 어느 때보다 사람의 거처가 필요한 그 큰 저택은 생명 없는 몸뚱이 같다."], ["Now it is even awful, stealing through it, to think of the live people who have slept in the solitary bedrooms, to say nothing of the dead.", "이제 그 안을 살그머니 지나며, 외로운 침실들에서 잠들었던 산 사람들을 떠올리기만 해도 — 죽은 자들은 말할 것도 없거니와 — 으스스하다."], ["Now is the time for shadow, when every corner is a cavern and every downward step a pit, when the stained glass is reflected in pale and faded hues upon the floors, when anything and everything can be made of the heavy staircase beams excepting their own proper shapes, when the armour has dull lights upon it not easily to be distinguished from stealthy movement, and when barred helmets are frightfully suggestive of heads inside.", "지금이야말로 그림자의 시간, 구석마다 동굴이 되고 아래로 내딛는 걸음마다 구덩이가 되며, 색유리가 마룻바닥에 창백하게 바랜 빛깔로 비치고, 묵직한 계단 들보가 제 본래 모습만 빼고 무엇이든 다 될 수 있으며, 갑옷에는 슬그머니 움직이는 것과 좀처럼 분간하기 어려운 흐릿한 빛이 어리고, 창살 달린 투구가 그 안에 든 머리를 끔찍하게 떠올리게 하는 시간이다."], ["But of all the shadows in Chesney Wold, the shadow in the long drawing-room upon my Lady’s picture is the first to come, the last to be disturbed.", "그러나 체스니 월드의 온갖 그림자 가운데, 긴 응접실에서 마님의 초상화에 드리우는 그림자야말로 가장 먼저 찾아오고 가장 나중에야 흩어진다."], ["At this hour and by this light it changes into threatening hands raised up and menacing the handsome face with every breath that stirs.", "이 시각 이 빛 아래에서 그것은, 흔들리는 숨결마다 그 아름다운 얼굴을 향해 위협하듯 치켜든 손으로 변한다."]], [["“She is not well, ma’am,”", "\"마님께서 편찮으십니다, 부인.\""], ["says a groom in Mrs. Rouncewell’s audience-chamber.", "라운스웰 부인의 접견실에서 한 마부가 말한다."]], [["“My Lady not well!", "\"마님이 편찮으시다니!"], ["What’s the matter?”", "무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["“Why, my Lady has been but poorly, ma’am, since she was last here—I don’t mean with the family, ma’am, but when she was here as a bird of passage like.", "\"저, 마님께서 지난번에 여기 계셨을 때부터 영 좋질 못하셨습니다, 부인 — 가족분들과 함께 계셨을 때 말씀이 아니라, 그러니까 잠깐 머무는 철새처럼 다녀가셨을 때 말입니다."], ["My Lady has not been out much, for her, and has kept her room a good deal.”", "마님께서 평소답지 않게 거의 바깥출입을 안 하시고 방에만 꽤 오래 들어앉아 계셨습니다.\""]], [["“Chesney Wold, Thomas,”", "\"체스니 월드는 말이다, 토머스.\""], ["rejoins the housekeeper with proud complacency, “will set my Lady up!", "가정부가 자랑스러운 흡족함을 담아 받아친다. \"마님을 일으켜 세울 게다!"], ["There is no finer air and no healthier soil in the world!”", "세상에 이보다 더 좋은 공기도, 이보다 더 건강한 땅도 없느니라!\""]], [["Thomas may have his own personal opinions on this subject, probably hints them in his manner of smoothing his sleek head from the nape of his neck to his temples, but he forbears to express them further and retires to the servants’ hall to regale on cold meat-pie and ale.", "토머스는 이 문제에 나름의 개인적 견해가 있을지도 모르고, 목덜미에서 관자놀이까지 매끈한 머리를 쓸어 넘기는 그 몸짓에 그 견해를 슬쩍 내비치는 듯도 하나, 그 이상 말로 드러내기는 삼간 채 하인 방으로 물러나 차가운 고기 파이와 에일로 배를 채운다."]], [["This groom is the pilot-fish before the nobler shark.", "이 마부는 한결 고귀한 상어에 앞선 길잡이 물고기다."], ["Next evening, down come Sir Leicester and my Lady with their largest retinue, and down come the cousins and others from all the points of the compass.", "이튿날 저녁, 레스터 경과 마님이 가장 큰 수행단을 거느리고 내려오고, 사촌들과 그 밖의 사람들이 사방 방위에서 내려온다."], ["Thenceforth for some weeks backward and forward rush mysterious men with no names, who fly about all those particular parts of the country on which Doodle is at present throwing himself in an auriferous and malty shower, but who are merely persons of a restless disposition and never do anything anywhere.", "그때부터 몇 주 동안, 이름도 없는 수상쩍은 사내들이 앞으로 뒤로 분주히 오간다. 두들이 지금 황금과 맥아의 소나기를 퍼붓고 있는 바로 그 지역들을 죄다 날아다니지만, 실은 안절부절못하는 기질의 인간들일 뿐 어디서도 아무 일도 하지 않는 자들이다."]], [["On these national occasions Sir Leicester finds the cousins useful.", "이런 국가적 행사에서 레스터 경은 사촌들이 쓸모 있다고 여긴다."], ["A better man than the Honourable Bob Stables to meet the Hunt at dinner, there could not possibly be.", "사냥 모임과 만찬을 함께할 사람으로 명예로운 밥 스테이블스보다 나은 자는 도무지 있을 수 없다."], ["Better got up gentlemen than the other cousins to ride over to polling-booths and hustings here and there, and show themselves on the side of England, it would be hard to find.", "여기저기 투표소며 유세장으로 말을 몰고 가 잉글랜드 편에 선 모습을 보여줄 사람으로, 잘 차려입은 다른 사촌들보다 나은 자를 찾기란 어렵다."], ["Volumnia is a little dim, but she is of the true descent; and there are many who appreciate her sprightly conversation, her French conundrums so old as to have become in the cycles of time almost new again, the honour of taking the fair Dedlock in to dinner, or even the privilege of her hand in the dance.", "볼루미니아는 약간 흐리멍덩하나 혈통만은 진짜다. 그녀의 발랄한 대화, 너무 낡아서 세월의 순환 속에 도리어 거의 새것이 되어버린 그 프랑스식 수수께끼, 그 어여쁜 데드록을 만찬장에 데리고 들어가는 영광, 심지어 춤에서 그녀의 손을 잡는 특권을 높이 사는 이들이 많다."], ["On these national occasions dancing may be a patriotic service, and Volumnia is constantly seen hopping about for the good of an ungrateful and unpensioning country.", "이런 국가적 행사에서 춤은 애국적 봉사일 수 있고, 그리하여 볼루미니아는 은혜도 모르고 연금도 주지 않는 조국을 위해 줄곧 폴짝거리는 모습을 보인다."]], [["My Lady takes no great pains to entertain the numerous guests, and being still unwell, rarely appears until late in the day.", "마님은 그 숱한 손님들을 접대하느라 별로 애쓰지 않으며, 여전히 몸이 좋지 않아 좀처럼 낮 늦게야 모습을 드러낸다."], ["But at all the dismal dinners, leaden lunches, basilisk balls, and other melancholy pageants, her mere appearance is a relief.", "그러나 음울한 만찬, 납덩이 같은 오찬, 바실리스크처럼 사람을 굳히는 무도회, 그 밖의 우울한 행사 어디서든 그녀가 그저 나타나기만 해도 분위기가 한결 풀린다."], ["As to Sir Leicester, he conceives it utterly impossible that anything can be wanting, in any direction, by any one who has the good fortune to be received under that roof; and in a state of sublime satisfaction, he moves among the company, a magnificent refrigerator.", "레스터 경으로 말하자면, 그 지붕 아래 영접받는 행운을 누리는 자라면 누구든, 어느 모로 보나 부족할 게 도무지 있을 수 없다고 굳게 믿는다. 그러고는 숭고한 만족에 잠긴 채, 으리으리한 냉장고 한 대처럼 손님들 사이를 누빈다."]], [["Daily the cousins trot through dust and canter over roadside turf, away to hustings and polling-booths (with leather gloves and hunting-whips for the counties and kid gloves and riding-canes for the boroughs), and daily bring back reports on which Sir Leicester holds forth after dinner.", "날마다 사촌들은 먼지 속을 종종걸음 치고 길가 잔디 위를 가볍게 달려, 유세장과 투표소로 향한다 (주(州)에는 가죽 장갑과 사냥 채찍을 끼고 들고, 자치도시에는 새끼염소 가죽 장갑과 승마 지팡이를 끼고 들고서). 그러고는 날마다 보고를 가지고 돌아오면 레스터 경이 만찬 뒤에 그것을 두고 일장 연설을 늘어놓는다."], ["Daily the restless men who have no occupation in life present the appearance of being rather busy.", "날마다 평생 할 일이라곤 없는 그 안절부절못하는 사내들은 자못 바쁜 듯한 행색을 차린다."], ["Daily Volumnia has a little cousinly talk with Sir Leicester on the state of the nation, from which Sir Leicester is disposed to conclude that Volumnia is a more reflecting woman than he had thought her.", "날마다 볼루미니아는 나라의 형편을 두고 레스터 경과 사촌끼리의 소소한 담소를 나누는데, 그 덕에 레스터 경은 볼루미니아가 자신이 생각했던 것보다 사려 깊은 여인이라고 결론짓고 싶어진다."]], [["“How are we getting on?”", "\"우리, 어떻게 돼가나요?\""], ["says Miss Volumnia, clasping her hands.", "볼루미니아 양이 두 손을 맞잡으며 말한다."], ["“ARE we safe?”", "\"우리, 안전한가요?\""]], [["The mighty business is nearly over by this time, and Doodle will throw himself off the country in a few days more.", "이 무렵 그 거대한 사업은 거의 끝나가고, 두들은 며칠만 더 지나면 나라에서 자기를 거두어들일 참이다."], ["Sir Leicester has just appeared in the long drawing-room after dinner, a bright particular star surrounded by clouds of cousins.", "레스터 경은 만찬 뒤 막 긴 응접실에 모습을 드러낸 참인데, 사촌들의 구름에 둘러싸인 한 점 찬란한 별이다."]], [["“Volumnia,”", "\"볼루미니아.\""], ["replies Sir Leicester, who has a list in his hand, “we are doing tolerably.”", "손에 명단을 든 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"그런대로 해나가고 있다네.\""]], [["“Only tolerably!”", "\"그런대로라니요!\""]], [["Although it is summer weather, Sir Leicester always has his own particular fire in the evening. He takes his usual screened seat near it and repeats with much firmness and a little displeasure, as who should say, I am not a common man, and when I say tolerably, it must not be understood as a common expression, “Volumnia, we are doing tolerably.”", "비록 여름철이긴 하나, 레스터 경은 저녁이면 늘 자기만의 벽난로를 피운다. 그는 평소처럼 그 곁의 가림막 친 자리에 앉아, 자못 단호하면서도 약간은 언짢은 기색으로 이렇게 되뇐다. 마치 이렇게 말하려는 듯이. 나는 보통 사람이 아니니, 내가 그런대로라고 할 때 그것을 보통의 표현으로 알아들어서는 안 되네. \"볼루미니아, 우리는 그런대로 해나가고 있다네.\""]], [["“At least there is no opposition to YOU,”", "\"적어도 백부님께는 맞서는 자가 없잖아요.\""], ["Volumnia asserts with confidence.", "볼루미니아가 자신만만하게 단언한다."]], [["“No, Volumnia.", "\"없지, 볼루미니아."], ["This distracted country has lost its senses in many respects, I grieve to say, but—”", "이 정신 나간 나라가 여러 면에서 분별을 잃었다는 건, 말하기 가슴 아프지만, 그래도—\""]], [["“It is not so mad as that.", "\"그 정도로 미치지는 않았군요."], ["I am glad to hear it!”", "듣던 중 반가운 소리예요!\""]], [["Volumnia’s finishing the sentence restores her to favour.", "볼루미니아가 문장을 마무리해준 덕에 다시 총애를 회복한다."], ["Sir Leicester, with a gracious inclination of his head, seems to say to himself, “A sensible woman this, on the whole, though occasionally precipitate.”", "레스터 경은 우아하게 고개를 끄덕이며 속으로 이렇게 말하는 듯하다. \"전체로 보면 분별 있는 여인이로군, 이따금 성급하긴 해도.\""]], [["In fact, as to this question of opposition, the fair Dedlock’s observation was superfluous, Sir Leicester on these occasions always delivering in his own candidateship, as a kind of handsome wholesale order to be promptly executed.", "사실 이 반대 세력 문제로 말하자면, 그 어여쁜 데드록의 언급은 군더더기였다. 이런 경우 레스터 경은 늘 자기 출마를 일종의 멋들어진 대량 주문처럼 척척 내놓아 즉각 처리되도록 하기 때문이다."], ["Two other little seats that belong to him he treats as retail orders of less importance, merely sending down the men and signifying to the tradespeople, “You will have the goodness to make these materials into two members of Parliament and to send them home when done.”", "자기 소유인 다른 두 개의 작은 의석은 그보다 덜 중요한 소매 주문쯤으로 취급해, 그저 사람들을 내려보내고는 거래상에게 이렇게 통고할 따름이다. \"이 재료들을 두 명의 국회의원으로 만들어, 다 되거든 집으로 보내주는 친절을 베풀어주시오.\""]], [["“I regret to say, Volumnia, that in many places the people have shown a bad spirit, and that this opposition to the government has been of a most determined and most implacable description.”", "\"유감스럽게도 말일세, 볼루미니아, 여러 곳에서 민중이 못된 기질을 드러냈고, 정부에 대한 이 반대가 더없이 결연하고 더없이 가차 없는 양상을 띠었다네.\""]], [["“W-r-retches!”", "\"고-약-한 것들!\""], ["says Volumnia.", "볼루미니아가 말한다."]], [["“Even,”", "\"심지어 말이지,\""], ["proceeds Sir Leicester, glancing at the circumjacent cousins on sofas and ottomans, “even in many—in fact, in most—of those places in which the government has carried it against a faction—”", "레스터 경이 소파며 오토만에 둘러앉은 사촌들을 흘끔 보며 말을 잇는다. \"심지어 정부가 한 파벌을 누르고 이긴 그런 곳들 가운데 많은 데서 — 아니, 사실은 대부분에서—\""]], [["(Note, by the way, that the Coodleites are always a faction with the Doodleites, and that the Doodleites occupy exactly the same position towards the Coodleites.)", "(여담이지만 일러두자면, 쿠들파에게 두들파는 늘 한낱 파벌이고, 두들파에게 쿠들파 역시 똑같은 위치에 놓인다는 점을 유념하시라.)"]], [["“—Even in them I am shocked, for the credit of Englishmen, to be constrained to inform you that the party has not triumphed without being put to an enormous expense.", "\"—심지어 그런 곳들에서도, 잉글랜드인의 명예를 위해 차마 말하기 충격스러우나, 우리 당이 어마어마한 비용을 치르지 않고는 승리하지 못했다고 자네에게 알리지 않을 수 없네."], ["Hundreds,”", "수십만,\""], ["says Sir Leicester, eyeing the cousins with increasing dignity and swelling indignation, “hundreds of thousands of pounds!”", "레스터 경이 점점 더한 위엄과 부풀어 오르는 분노로 사촌들을 노려보며 말한다. \"수십만 파운드라네!\""]], [["If Volumnia have a fault, it is the fault of being a trifle too innocent, seeing that the innocence which would go extremely well with a sash and tucker is a little out of keeping with the rouge and pearl necklace.", "볼루미니아에게 흠이 하나 있다면, 그것은 약간 지나치게 순진한 게 흠인데, 어깨띠와 가슴 장식에는 더없이 잘 어울릴 그 순진함이 연지와 진주 목걸이에는 다소 어울리지 않는 까닭이다."], ["Howbeit, impelled by innocence, she asks, “What for?”", "어쨌든 그 순진함에 떠밀려 그녀가 묻는다. \"뭐 하느라요?\""]], [["“Volumnia,”", "\"볼루미니아.\""], ["remonstrates Sir Leicester with his utmost severity.", "레스터 경이 더없이 준엄하게 나무란다."], ["“Volumnia!”", "\"볼루미니아!\""]], [["“No, no, I don’t mean what for,”", "\"아뇨, 아뇨, 뭐 하느라냐는 게 아니에요.\""], ["cries Volumnia with her favourite little scream.", "볼루미니아가 평소 즐기는 그 작은 비명을 지르며 외친다."], ["“How stupid I am!", "\"내가 이렇게 멍청하다니!"], ["I mean what a pity!”", "어쩜 그리 딱한 일이냐는 뜻이었어요!\""]], [["“I am glad,”", "\"다행이군.\""], ["returns Sir Leicester, “that you do mean what a pity.”", "레스터 경이 대꾸한다. \"어쩜 그리 딱한 일이냐는 뜻이었다니 말일세.\""]], [["Volumnia hastens to express her opinion that the shocking people ought to be tried as traitors and made to support the party.", "볼루미니아는 서둘러 자기 의견을 밝히는데, 그 충격적인 자들은 반역자로 재판에 부쳐 당을 떠받치게 만들어야 마땅하다는 것이다."]], [["“I am glad, Volumnia,”", "\"다행이군, 볼루미니아.\""], ["repeats Sir Leicester, unmindful of these mollifying sentiments, “that you do mean what a pity.", "레스터 경이 이 누그러뜨리는 소감 따위는 아랑곳없이 되풀이한다. \"어쩜 그리 딱한 일이냐는 뜻이었다니."], ["It is disgraceful to the electors.", "유권자들에게는 망신스러운 일이지."], ["But as you, though inadvertently and without intending so unreasonable a question, asked me ‘what for?’ let me reply to you.", "하지만 자네가 무심결에, 그토록 당치 않은 질문을 할 뜻은 없이 내게 '뭐 하느라'고 물었으니, 답해주겠네."], ["For necessary expenses.", "필요한 경비라네."], ["And I trust to your good sense, Volumnia, not to pursue the subject, here or elsewhere.”", "그리고 자네의 분별을 믿으니, 볼루미니아, 여기서든 다른 데서든 이 화제를 더는 캐묻지 말게나.\""]], [["Sir Leicester feels it incumbent on him to observe a crushing aspect towards Volumnia because it is whispered abroad that these necessary expenses will, in some two hundred election petitions, be unpleasantly connected with the word bribery, and because some graceless jokers have consequently suggested the omission from the Church service of the ordinary supplication in behalf of the High Court of Parliament and have recommended instead that the prayers of the congregation be requested for six hundred and fifty-eight gentlemen in a very unhealthy state.", "레스터 경은 볼루미니아를 향해 짓누르는 듯한 표정을 짓는 것이 자기 본분이라 여긴다. 그 필요한 경비라는 것이 어림잡아 이백 건의 선거 청원에서 뇌물이라는 낱말과 불쾌하게 엮이리라는 소문이 바깥에 수군수군 돌고 있고, 그 탓에 짓궂은 익살꾼 몇몇이 예배 의식에서 고등 국회를 위한 통상의 기원을 빼버리고, 그 대신 매우 불건전한 상태에 놓인 신사 육백오십팔 명을 위해 회중의 기도를 청하자고 권한 까닭이다."]], [["“I suppose,”", "\"제 생각엔 말이에요,\""], ["observes Volumnia, having taken a little time to recover her spirits after her late castigation, “I suppose Mr.", "좀 전의 꾸지람에서 기운을 추스를 시간을 잠시 가진 뒤 볼루미니아가 말한다."], ["Tulkinghorn has been worked to death.”", "\"제 생각엔 토킹혼 씨가 죽도록 일에 시달렸을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"내가 모르겠는 건,\""], ["says Sir Leicester, opening his eyes, “why Mr.", "레스터 경이 눈을 동그랗게 뜨며 말한다."], ["Tulkinghorn should be worked to death. I don’t know what Mr.", "\"어찌하여 토킹혼 씨가 죽도록 일에 시달렸어야 하느냐 하는 점일세."], ["Tulkinghorn’s engagements may be.", "토킹혼 씨가 무슨 일을 맡고 있는지 나는 모르네."], ["He is not a candidate.”", "그는 후보자가 아니지 않은가.\""]], [["Volumnia had thought he might have been employed.", "볼루미니아는 그가 어디 고용되었을지도 모른다고 생각했던 것이다."], ["Sir Leicester could desire to know by whom, and what for.", "레스터 경은 누구한테, 무엇 하러 그랬다는 건지 알고 싶어 했다."], ["Volumnia, abashed again, suggests, by somebody—to advise and make arrangements.", "다시 머쓱해진 볼루미니아는, 누군가한테 — 조언을 해주고 일을 처리하라고 그랬을지 모른다고 더듬더듬 내놓는다."], ["Sir Leicester is not aware that any client of Mr. Tulkinghorn has been in need of his assistance.", "레스터 경은 토킹혼 씨의 어느 의뢰인이 그의 도움을 필요로 했다는 이야기는 들은 바 없다."]], [["Lady Dedlock, seated at an open window with her arm upon its cushioned ledge and looking out at the evening shadows falling on the park, has seemed to attend since the lawyer’s name was mentioned.", "방석 깔린 창턱에 팔을 얹고 열린 창가에 앉아 정원에 내리는 저녁 그림자를 내다보던 데드록 마님이, 변호사의 이름이 입에 오른 뒤로 귀를 기울이는 듯하다."]], [["A languid cousin with a moustache in a state of extreme debility now observes from his couch that man told him ya’as’dy that Tulkinghorn had gone down t’ that iron place t’ give legal ’pinion ’bout something, and that contest being over t’ day, ’twould be highly jawlly thing if Tulkinghorn should ’pear with news that Coodle man was floored.", "극도로 기운이 없어 보이는 콧수염 난 어느 나른한 사촌이 이제 자기 긴 의자에서 한마디 흘린다. 어떤 사람이 어저께 그에게 말하기를, 토킹혼이 무슨 일에 법률 의견을 내주러 그 철공소 동네로 내려갔다고 했다는 것이다. 그러니 오늘로 그 선거전이 끝났는데, 만약 토킹혼이 쿠들네 사람이 나가떨어졌다는 소식을 들고 나타나기라도 한다면 그거 참 거나하게 즐거운 일일 거라고 한다."]], [["Mercury in attendance with coffee informs Sir Leicester, hereupon, that Mr. Tulkinghorn has arrived and is taking dinner.", "커피를 들고 시중들던 머큐리가 이때 레스터 경에게 토킹혼 씨가 도착해 만찬을 들고 있다고 알린다."], ["My Lady turns her head inward for the moment, then looks out again as before.", "마님은 잠깐 고개를 안쪽으로 돌렸다가, 다시 아까처럼 바깥을 내다본다."]], [["Volumnia is charmed to hear that her delight is come.", "볼루미니아는 자기가 그토록 좋아하는 분이 왔다는 말에 황홀해한다."], ["He is so original, such a stolid creature, such an immense being for knowing all sorts of things and never telling them!", "그는 어찌나 독창적인지, 어찌나 무뚝뚝한 인간인지, 온갖 것을 다 알면서도 결코 입 밖에 내지 않는 어찌나 대단한 존재인지!"], ["Volumnia is persuaded that he must be a Freemason.", "볼루미니아는 그가 틀림없이 프리메이슨일 거라고 믿어 의심치 않는다."], ["Is sure he is at the head of a lodge, and wears short aprons, and is made a perfect idol of with candlesticks and trowels.", "분명 어느 지부의 우두머리여서 짧은 앞치마를 두르고, 촛대와 흙손으로 떠받들어지는 완벽한 우상이 되어 있을 거라고 확신한다."], ["These lively remarks the fair Dedlock delivers in her youthful manner, while making a purse.", "그 어여쁜 데드록은 지갑을 짜면서 이 활기찬 소감들을 그 앳된 태도로 늘어놓는다."]], [["“He has not been here once,”", "\"제가 온 뒤로,\""], ["she adds, “since I came.", "그녀가 덧붙인다."], ["I really had some thoughts of breaking my heart for the inconstant creature.", "\"그분이 여기 한 번도 안 오셨어요. 그 변덕쟁이 때문에 정말이지 가슴이 미어질 뻔했다니까요."], ["I had almost made up my mind that he was dead.”", "그분이 죽었나 보다고 거의 마음을 굳혔을 정도예요.\""]], [["It may be the gathering gloom of evening, or it may be the darker gloom within herself, but a shade is on my Lady’s face, as if she thought, “I would he were!”", "저녁의 짙어가는 어스름 탓일 수도 있고, 그녀 안의 더 어두운 어스름 탓일 수도 있으나, 마님의 얼굴에는 마치 \"차라리 그랬으면!\" 하고 생각하는 듯한 그늘이 깃든다."]], [["“Mr. Tulkinghorn,”", "\"토킹혼 씨는,\""], ["says Sir Leicester, “is always welcome here and always discreet wheresoever he is.", "레스터 경이 말한다. \"이곳에 늘 환영받는 사람이고, 어디에 있든 늘 신중한 사람일세."], ["A very valuable person, and deservedly respected.”", "무척 귀중한 인물이지, 마땅히 존경받을 만하고.\""]], [["The debilitated cousin supposes he is “’normously rich fler.”", "기진맥진한 사촌은 그가 \"어-엄청 부자인 친구\"인 모양이라고 짐작한다."]], [["“He has a stake in the country,”", "\"그는 이 나라에 이해관계가 걸려 있는 사람이야,\""], ["says Sir Leicester, “I have no doubt.", "레스터 경이 말한다."], ["He is, of course, handsomely paid, and he associates almost on a footing of equality with the highest society.”", "\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지. 물론 보수도 후하게 받고, 거의 대등한 처지로 최고 상류사회와 어울리지.\""]], [["Everybody starts.", "다들 흠칫한다."], ["For a gun is fired close by.", "가까이서 총성이 한 발 울린 까닭이다."]], [["“Good gracious, what’s that?”", "\"세상에, 저게 뭐죠?\""], ["cries Volumnia with her little withered scream.", "볼루미니아가 그 시들시들한 작은 비명을 지르며 외친다."]], [["“A rat,”", "\"쥐예요.\""], ["says my Lady.", "마님이 말한다."], ["“And they have shot him.”", "\"쥐를 쏜 거고요.\""]], [["Enter Mr. Tulkinghorn, followed by Mercuries with lamps and candles.", "토킹혼 씨가 들어오고, 등불과 양초를 든 머큐리들이 그 뒤를 따른다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["says Sir Leicester, “I think not.", "레스터 경이 말한다. \"내 생각엔 아닐세."], ["My Lady, do you object to the twilight?”", "마님, 이 어스름이 거슬리시오?\""]], [["On the contrary, my Lady prefers it.", "오히려 마님은 이 어스름이 더 좋다고 한다."]], [["“Volumnia?”", "\"볼루미니아?\""]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["Nothing is so delicious to Volumnia as to sit and talk in the dark.", "어둠 속에 앉아 두런두런 이야기 나누는 것만큼 볼루미니아에게 달콤한 일은 없다."]], [["“Then take them away,”", "\"그럼 그것들을 도로 가져가게.\""], ["says Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 말한다."], ["“Tulkinghorn, I beg your pardon.", "\"토킹혼, 실례했네."], ["How do you do?”", "그래, 잘 지내셨는가?\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn with his usual leisurely ease advances, renders his passing homage to my Lady, shakes Sir Leicester’s hand, and subsides into the chair proper to him when he has anything to communicate, on the opposite side of the Baronet’s little newspaper-table.", "토킹혼 씨는 여느 때처럼 느긋한 여유로 다가와, 마님에게 지나가는 경의를 표하고, 레스터 경과 악수한 뒤, 무언가 전할 말이 있을 때 으레 차지하는 자기 의자에 — 준남작의 자그마한 신문 탁자 맞은편 자리에 — 내려앉는다."], ["Sir Leicester is apprehensive that my Lady, not being very well, will take cold at that open window.", "레스터 경은 몸이 그리 좋지 않은 마님이 저 열린 창가에서 감기라도 들까 봐 염려스럽다."], ["My Lady is obliged to him, but would rather sit there for the air.", "마님은 그의 마음 씀은 고맙지만, 바람을 쐬려니 차라리 거기 앉아 있겠다고 한다."], ["Sir Leicester rises, adjusts her scarf about her, and returns to his seat. Mr.", "레스터 경은 일어나 그녀의 숄을 매무새 고쳐 둘러주고는 제자리로 돌아온다."], ["Tulkinghorn in the meanwhile takes a pinch of snuff.", "그사이 토킹혼 씨는 코담배를 한 줌 집는다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["says Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 말한다."], ["“How has that contest gone?”", "\"그 선거전은 어찌 됐나?\""]], [["“Oh, hollow from the beginning.", "\"오, 처음부터 허망했지요."], ["Not a chance.", "가망이 없었습니다."], ["They have brought in both their people.", "저쪽에서 자기네 사람을 둘 다 당선시켰어요."], ["You are beaten out of all reason.", "말도 안 되게 참패하셨습니다."], ["Three to one.”", "삼 대 일이었으니까요.\""]], [["It is a part of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s policy and mastery to have no political opinions; indeed, NO opinions.", "정치적 견해를 일절 갖지 않는 것이, 아니 사실은 어떤 견해도 일절 갖지 않는 것이 토킹혼 씨의 처세요 비결이다."], ["Therefore he says “you”", "그래서 그는 \"우리가\""], ["are beaten, and not “we.”", "졌다고 하지 않고 \"당신들이\" 졌다고 말한다."]], [["Sir Leicester is majestically wroth.", "레스터 경은 위엄 있게 격노한다."], ["Volumnia never heard of such a thing.", "볼루미니아는 그런 얘기는 난생처음 들어본다고 한다."], ["‘The debilitated cousin holds that it’s sort of thing that’s sure tapn slongs votes—giv’n—Mob.", "기진맥진한 사촌은 그게 바로 표가 — 주어지는 — 군중한테 — 표를 — 막 뿌려대면 으레 그리되는 일이라고 본다."]], [["“It’s the place, you know,”", "\"아시다시피 그곳은,\""], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn goes on to say in the fast-increasing darkness when there is silence again, “where they wanted to put up Mrs.", "토킹혼 씨가 다시 침묵이 깔린 가운데, 빠르게 짙어가는 어둠 속에서 말을 잇는다."], ["Rouncewell’s son.”", "\"저들이 라운스웰 부인의 아들을 내세우려던 바로 그곳입니다.\""]], [["“A proposal which, as you correctly informed me at the time, he had the becoming taste and perception,”", "\"그 제안은, 당시 자네가 내게 정확히 알려주었듯이, 그가 그것을 사양할 만한 합당한 안목과 분별을 지녔던 게지,\""], ["observes Sir Leicester, “to decline.", "레스터 경이 말한다."], ["I cannot say that I by any means approve of the sentiments expressed by Mr. Rouncewell when he was here for some half-hour in this room, but there was a sense of propriety in his decision which I am glad to acknowledge.”", "\"그가 여기 이 방에서 삼십 분쯤 머물렀을 때 내비친 그 생각들을 내가 무슨 수로든 옳다고는 도무지 말 못 하겠으나, 그래도 그 결정에는 분수를 아는 마음이 있었으니, 그 점은 기꺼이 인정하는 바일세.\""]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“It did not prevent him from being very active in this election, though.”", "\"그렇다고 그가 이번 선거에서 무척 열성으로 뛰지 않았던 건 아니지만 말입니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester is distinctly heard to gasp before speaking.", "레스터 경이 말을 꺼내기에 앞서 숨을 헉 들이켜는 소리가 또렷이 들린다."], ["“Did I understand you?", "\"내가 제대로 들었는가?"], ["Did you say that Mr. Rouncewell had been very active in this election?”", "자네 지금 라운스웰 씨가 이번 선거에서 무척 열성으로 뛰었다고 말한 겐가?\""]], [["“Uncommonly active.”", "\"유난스러울 만큼 열성이었지요.\""], ["“Against—”", "\"누구에게 맞서서—\""]], [["“Oh, dear yes, against you.", "\"오, 그렇고말고요, 어르신께 맞서서지요."], ["He is a very good speaker.", "그는 무척 훌륭한 연설가입니다."], ["Plain and emphatic.", "꾸밈없고 힘이 있지요."], ["He made a damaging effect, and has great influence.", "타격을 입힐 만한 영향을 끼쳤고, 세력도 막강합니다."], ["In the business part of the proceedings he carried all before him.”", "일의 실무적인 대목에서는 모든 걸 자기 뜻대로 휘어잡았어요.\""]], [["It is evident to the whole company, though nobody can see him, that Sir Leicester is staring majestically.", "비록 아무도 그를 볼 수는 없으나, 레스터 경이 위엄 있게 노려보고 있다는 것은 좌중 전체에 역력하다."]], [["“And he was much assisted,”", "\"게다가 그는,\" 토킹혼 씨가 마무리 짓듯 말한다."], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn as a wind-up, “by his son.”", "\"자기 아들에게서 큰 도움을 받았지요.\""]], [["“By his son, sir?”", "\"아들에게서라니, 여보게?\""], ["repeats Sir Leicester with awful politeness.", "레스터 경이 소름 끼치는 정중함으로 되묻는다."]], [["“By his son.”", "\"아들에게서요.\""]], [["“The son who wished to marry the young woman in my Lady’s service?”", "\"우리 마님 시중을 드는 그 젊은 여자와 혼인하고 싶어 하던 그 아들 말인가?\""]], [["“That son.", "\"바로 그 아들입니다."], ["He has but one.”", "그에게는 아들이 하나뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Then upon my honour,”", "\"그렇다면 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대,\""], ["says Sir Leicester after a terrific pause during which he has been heard to snort and felt to stare, “then upon my honour, upon my life, upon my reputation and principles, the floodgates of society are burst open, and the waters have—a—obliterated the landmarks of the framework of the cohesion by which things are held together!”", "레스터 경이 콧김을 내뿜는 소리가 들리고 노려보는 기운이 느껴지던 그 무시무시한 침묵 끝에 입을 연다. \"그렇다면 내 명예를 걸고, 내 목숨을 걸고, 내 명망과 신조를 걸고 말하건대, 사회의 수문이 활짝 터져버렸고, 그 물이 — 어 — 만물을 한데 붙들어주는 그 결속의 틀, 그 경계표를 죄다 지워버렸도다!\""]], [["General burst of cousinly indignation.", "사촌들 사이에서 일제히 분노가 터져 나온다."], ["Volumnia thinks it is really high time, you know, for somebody in power to step in and do something strong.", "볼루미니아는, 그러니까 말이에요, 권력 있는 누군가가 나서서 무언가 강력한 조치를 취할 때가 정말로 무르익었다고 생각한다."], ["Debilitated cousin thinks—country’s going—Dayvle—steeple-chase pace.", "기진맥진한 사촌은 — 나라가 — 망조가 — 장애물 경마 속도로 — 라고 생각한다."]], [["“I beg,”", "\"청컨대,\""], ["says Sir Leicester in a breathless condition, “that we may not comment further on this circumstance.", "레스터 경이 숨이 가쁜 상태로 말한다. \"이 일에 관해 더는 왈가왈부하지 말도록 하세."], ["Comment is superfluous.", "왈가왈부는 군더더기일 따름이지."], ["My Lady, let me suggest in reference to that young woman—”", "마님, 그 젊은 여자와 관련하여 한 가지 제안을 드리고 싶은데—\""]], [["“I have no intention,”", "\"저는 그 아이를 내보낼 뜻이,\""], ["observes my Lady from her window in a low but decided tone, “of parting with her.”", "마님이 창가에서 낮으나 단호한 어조로 말한다. \"없습니다.\""]], [["“That was not my meaning,”", "\"내 말은 그게 아니었소,\""], ["returns Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 대꾸한다."], ["“I am glad to hear you say so.", "\"그리 말해주니 반갑소."], ["I would suggest that as you think her worthy of your patronage, you should exert your influence to keep her from these dangerous hands.", "내가 제안하고 싶은 건, 당신이 그 아이를 후원할 만하다고 여기니, 그 위험한 손아귀에서 그 아이를 지키도록 당신의 영향력을 발휘하라는 것이오."], ["You might show her what violence would be done in such association to her duties and principles, and you might preserve her for a better fate.", "그런 자들과 어울리는 일이 그 아이의 본분과 신조에 어떤 횡포가 될지 일러주고, 더 나은 운명을 위해 그 아이를 지켜낼 수 있을 게요."], ["You might point out to her that she probably would, in good time, find a husband at Chesney Wold by whom she would not be—”", "머잖아 좋은 때가 오면 체스니 월드에서 남편감을 만날 수 있을 거라고, 그 사람이라면 그 아이를—\" 레스터 경은 잠시 생각하다가 덧붙인다."], ["Sir Leicester adds, after a moment’s consideration, “dragged from the altars of her forefathers.”", "\"그 아이를 제 조상의 제단에서 끌어내지는 않을 거라고 일러줄 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["These remarks he offers with his unvarying politeness and deference when he addresses himself to his wife.", "이런 말들을 그는 아내에게 건넬 때면 늘 한결같은 정중함과 공손함으로 내놓는다."], ["She merely moves her head in reply.", "그녀는 그저 고개만 살짝 끄덕여 답할 따름이다."], ["The moon is rising, and where she sits there is a little stream of cold pale light, in which her head is seen.", "달이 떠오르고, 그녀가 앉은 자리에는 차갑고 창백한 빛이 가느다란 줄기로 흘러들어, 그 속에 그녀의 머리가 비친다."]], [["“It is worthy of remark,” says Mr.", "\"그래도 주목할 만한 점은,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, “however, that these people are, in their way, very proud.”", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"그런 자들이 나름대로 무척 자존심이 세다는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Proud?”", "\"자존심이?\""], ["Sir Leicester doubts his hearing.", "레스터 경이 제 귀를 의심한다."]], [["“I should not be surprised if they all voluntarily abandoned the girl—yes, lover and all—instead of her abandoning them, supposing she remained at Chesney Wold under such circumstances.”", "\"제 짐작에는, 그런 처지에서 그 처녀가 체스니 월드에 그대로 남는다면, 그 처녀가 그들을 버리는 게 아니라 도리어 그들이 죄다 제 발로 그 처녀를 — 그래요, 연인까지 통째로 — 버린다 해도 저는 놀라지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"허!\""], ["says Sir Leicester tremulously.", "레스터 경이 떨리는 목소리로 말한다."], ["“Well!", "\"허!"], ["You should know, Mr.", "자네야 잘 알겠지, 토킹혼 씨."], ["Tulkinghorn. You have been among them.”", "자네가 그자들 틈에 있어봤으니 말일세.\""]], [["“Really, Sir Leicester,”", "\"정말이지, 레스터 경,\""], ["returns the lawyer, “I state the fact.", "변호사가 대꾸한다. \"저는 그저 사실을 말씀드리는 겁니다."], ["Why, I could tell you a story—with Lady Dedlock’s permission.”", "아니, 한 가지 이야기를 들려드릴 수도 있는데요 — 데드록 마님께서 허락해주신다면 말이지요.\""]], [["Her head concedes it, and Volumnia is enchanted.", "마님의 고갯짓이 그것을 허락하고, 볼루미니아는 넋을 잃는다."], ["A story!", "이야기라니!"], ["Oh, he is going to tell something at last!", "오, 그가 드디어 무언가를 들려주려나 보다!"], ["A ghost in it, Volumnia hopes?", "그 속에 유령이 나오기를, 볼루미니아는 바란다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니지요."], ["Real flesh and blood.” Mr.", "진짜 살과 피를 가진 사람입니다.\""], ["Tulkinghorn stops for an instant and repeats with some little emphasis grafted upon his usual monotony, “Real flesh and blood, Miss Dedlock.", "토킹혼 씨가 잠깐 멈췄다가, 평소의 단조로움에 약간의 강세를 덧붙여 되뇐다. \"진짜 살과 피를 가진 사람이지요, 데드록 양."], ["Sir Leicester, these particulars have only lately become known to me.", "레스터 경, 이 자초지종은 제가 최근에야 알게 된 것입니다."], ["They are very brief.", "아주 간략하지요."], ["They exemplify what I have said.", "제가 앞서 한 말을 잘 보여주는 사례입니다."], ["I suppress names for the present.", "이름은 당분간 밝히지 않겠습니다."], ["Lady Dedlock will not think me ill-bred, I hope?”", "데드록 마님께서 저를 무례하다 여기지 않으시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["By the light of the fire, which is low, he can be seen looking towards the moonlight.", "낮게 사위어가는 벽난로의 불빛으로, 그가 달빛 쪽을 바라보고 있는 모습이 보인다."], ["By the light of the moon Lady Dedlock can be seen, perfectly still.", "달빛으로, 데드록 마님이 미동도 없이 앉아 있는 모습이 보인다."]], [["“A townsman of this Mr. Rouncewell, a man in exactly parallel circumstances as I am told, had the good fortune to have a daughter who attracted the notice of a great lady.", "\"이 라운스웰 씨와 같은 고장 사람이, 듣자 하니 꼭 같은 처지에 놓인 어떤 사내가, 어느 귀부인의 눈길을 끈 딸 하나를 둔 행운을 누렸습니다."], ["I speak of really a great lady, not merely great to him, but married to a gentleman of your condition, Sir Leicester.”", "정말로 대단한 귀부인을 두고 하는 말입니다. 그저 그 사내 보기에 대단한 게 아니라, 레스터 경처럼 그런 지체 높은 신사와 혼인한 분이지요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester condescendingly says, “Yes, Mr.", "레스터 경이 거들먹거리듯 \"그래, 토킹혼 씨\""], ["Tulkinghorn,” implying that then she must have appeared of very considerable moral dimensions indeed in the eyes of an iron-master.", "하고 말하는데, 그렇다면 그 부인은 어느 철공소 주인의 눈에는 정말이지 어마어마한 도덕적 크기로 비쳤을 게 틀림없다는 뜻이 담겨 있다."]], [["“The lady was wealthy and beautiful, and had a liking for the girl, and treated her with great kindness, and kept her always near her.", "\"그 부인은 부유하고 아름다웠으며, 그 처녀를 마음에 들어 해 무척 다정하게 대했고, 늘 곁에 두었습니다."], ["Now this lady preserved a secret under all her greatness, which she had preserved for many years.", "그런데 이 부인은 그 온갖 고귀함 아래에 한 가지 비밀을 간직하고 있었으니, 여러 해 동안 지켜온 비밀이었지요."], ["In fact, she had in early life been engaged to marry a young rake—he was a captain in the army—nothing connected with whom came to any good.", "사실인즉, 그녀는 젊은 시절 어느 젊은 난봉꾼과 — 그자는 군대의 대위였습니다 — 혼인하기로 약혼한 적이 있었는데, 그자와 얽힌 일치고 좋게 끝난 게 하나도 없었습니다."], ["She never did marry him, but she gave birth to a child of which he was the father.”", "그녀는 끝내 그자와 혼인하지 않았으나, 그자를 아비로 둔 아이를 하나 낳았지요.\""]], [["By the light of the fire he can be seen looking towards the moonlight.", "벽난로 불빛으로, 그가 달빛 쪽을 바라보고 있는 모습이 보인다."], ["By the moonlight, Lady Dedlock can be seen in profile, perfectly still.", "달빛으로, 데드록 마님이 옆얼굴을 보인 채 미동도 없이 앉아 있는 모습이 보인다."]], [["“The captain in the army being dead, she believed herself safe; but a train of circumstances with which I need not trouble you led to discovery.", "\"그 군대의 대위가 죽었으니, 그녀는 자신이 안전하다고 믿었습니다. 하지만 굳이 여러분을 번거롭게 하지 않아도 될 일련의 정황이 발각으로 이어졌지요."], ["As I received the story, they began in an imprudence on her own part one day when she was taken by surprise, which shows how difficult it is for the firmest of us (she was very firm) to be always guarded.", "제가 전해 들은 바로는, 그것은 어느 날 그녀가 불시에 허를 찔려 스스로 저지른 한순간의 경솔함에서 비롯되었는데, 이는 우리 가운데 가장 굳센 사람이라도 (그녀는 무척 굳센 사람이었습니다) 언제나 빈틈없이 경계하기란 얼마나 어려운 일인지를 잘 보여줍니다."], ["There was great domestic trouble and amazement, you may suppose; I leave you to imagine, Sir Leicester, the husband’s grief.", "짐작하시겠지만 집안에 큰 풍파와 경악이 일었지요. 레스터 경, 그 남편의 비통함은 어떠했을지 상상에 맡기겠습니다."], ["But that is not the present point.", "하지만 그게 지금 요점은 아닙니다."], ["When Mr. Rouncewell’s townsman heard of the disclosure, he no more allowed the girl to be patronized and honoured than he would have suffered her to be trodden underfoot before his eyes.", "라운스웰 씨와 같은 고장 사람은 그 사실이 드러났다는 소식을 듣고는, 제 딸이 제 눈앞에서 발에 짓밟히도록 내버려두지 않을 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 제 딸이 후원받고 떠받들어지도록 그냥 두지 않았습니다."], ["Such was his pride, that he indignantly took her away, as if from reproach and disgrace.", "그 자존심이 어찌나 대단했던지, 그는 마치 치욕과 망신에서 끄집어내듯 분연히 딸을 데려가 버렸지요."], ["He had no sense of the honour done him and his daughter by the lady’s condescension; not the least.", "부인이 베푼 그 황송한 호의가 자기와 제 딸에게 얼마나 영예로운 일인지는 조금도 헤아리지 못했습니다. 손톱만큼도요."], ["He resented the girl’s position, as if the lady had been the commonest of commoners.", "그는 그 딸의 처지에 분개했으니, 마치 그 부인이 평민 중에서도 가장 천한 평민이기라도 한 것처럼 굴었지요."], ["That is the story.", "이게 그 이야기입니다."], ["I hope Lady Dedlock will excuse its painful nature.”", "데드록 마님께서 그 고통스러운 내용을 너그러이 봐주시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["There are various opinions on the merits, more or less conflicting with Volumnia’s.", "그 이야기의 됨됨이를 두고 갖가지 의견이 나오는데, 볼루미니아의 의견과는 다소간 어긋난다."], ["That fair young creature cannot believe there ever was any such lady and rejects the whole history on the threshold.", "그 어여쁜 젊은 처녀는 그런 부인이 세상에 있었으리라고는 도무지 믿지 못해, 그 사연 전부를 문턱에서부터 물리쳐버린다. 다수는 기진맥진한 사촌의 소감 쪽으로 기우는데, 그것을 몇 마디로 줄이면 — \"남의 일이지 — 라운스웰네 그 빌어먹을 고장 사람\""], ["The majority incline to the debilitated cousin’s sentiment, which is in few words—“no business—Rouncewell’s fernal townsman.”", "이다. 레스터 경은 대체로 마음속으로 와트 타일러까지 거슬러 올라가서는, 자기 나름의 구상에 따라 일련의 사건을 배열해본다."], ["Sir Leicester generally refers back in his mind to Wat Tyler and arranges a sequence of events on a plan of his own.", "* 와트 타일러: 1381년 영국 농민 반란을 이끈 지도자. 레스터 경은 모든 하층민의 반항을 이 옛 반란과 한통속으로 본다."]], [["There is not much conversation in all, for late hours have been kept at Chesney Wold since the necessary expenses elsewhere began, and this is the first night in many on which the family have been alone.", "도통 대화랄 게 별로 없다. 다른 데서 그 필요한 경비가 들기 시작한 뒤로 체스니 월드에서는 늦은 시각까지 깨어 지내왔고, 가족만 단출하게 있는 밤은 여러 날 만에 오늘이 처음인 까닭이다."], ["It is past ten when Sir Leicester begs Mr. Tulkinghorn to ring for candles.", "레스터 경이 토킹혼 씨에게 양초를 가져오라고 종을 울려달라 청할 무렵이면 열 시가 지난 뒤다."], ["Then the stream of moonlight has swelled into a lake, and then Lady Dedlock for the first time moves, and rises, and comes forward to a table for a glass of water.", "그제야 달빛 줄기는 부풀어 호수를 이루었고, 그제야 데드록 마님이 처음으로 몸을 움직여 일어서더니, 물 한 잔을 마시려 탁자 쪽으로 다가온다."], ["Winking cousins, bat-like in the candle glare, crowd round to give it; Volumnia (always ready for something better if procurable) takes another, a very mild sip of which contents her; Lady Dedlock, graceful, self-possessed, looked after by admiring eyes, passes away slowly down the long perspective by the side of that nymph, not at all improving her as a question of contrast.", "촛불 빛에 박쥐처럼 눈을 깜빡이는 사촌들이 물을 건네려 우르르 몰려든다. 볼루미니아는 (더 나은 게 있다면 늘 받아들일 채비가 되어 있는지라) 또 한 잔을 받아 아주 조심스레 한 모금 홀짝이는 것으로 흡족해한다."], ["In Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Room", "데드록 마님은, 우아하고 침착하게, 흠모하는 눈길의 배웅을 받으며, 그 요정 같은 처녀 곁을 지나 긴 회랑을 따라 천천히 멀어져 가는데, 대조라는 측면에서 보면 그 처녀를 조금도 돋보이게 해주지 않는다."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn arrives in his turret-room a little breathed by the journey up, though leisurely performed.", "토킹혼 씨는 천천히 올라왔는데도 계단을 오르느라 숨이 약간 가빠진 채 탑실에 들어선다."], ["There is an expression on his face as if he had discharged his mind of some grave matter and were, in his close way, satisfied.", "무언가 중대한 일을 마음에서 털어내고, 자기 특유의 속내 깊은 방식으로 흡족해하는 듯한 기색이 얼굴에 떠올라 있다."], ["To say of a man so severely and strictly self-repressed that he is triumphant would be to do him as great an injustice as to suppose him troubled with love or sentiment or any romantic weakness.", "이토록 엄격하고 철저하게 자신을 억누르는 사람을 두고 의기양양하다고 한다면, 그가 사랑이나 감상이나 어떤 낭만적 약점에 시달린다고 짐작하는 것만큼이나 큰 모욕이 되리라."], ["He is sedately satisfied.", "그는 차분하게 만족스러워한다."], ["Perhaps there is a rather increased sense of power upon him as he loosely grasps one of his veinous wrists with his other hand and holding it behind his back walks noiselessly up and down.", "한 손으로 핏줄 불거진 손목을 느슨하게 쥐어 등 뒤로 돌리고 소리 없이 오르내리는 그의 몸짓에는, 어쩌면 평소보다 한층 강해진 권력의 감각이 어려 있는지도 모른다."]], [["There is a capacious writing-table in the room on which is a pretty large accumulation of papers.", "방 안에는 널찍한 책상이 놓여 있고, 그 위에는 서류가 꽤 수북이 쌓여 있다."], ["The green lamp is lighted, his reading-glasses lie upon the desk, the easy-chair is wheeled up to it, and it would seem as though he had intended to bestow an hour or so upon these claims on his attention before going to bed.", "초록 등이 켜져 있고, 독서용 안경은 책상에 놓여 있으며, 안락의자는 그 앞으로 바싹 당겨져 있다. 잠자리에 들기 전에 한 시간 남짓 이 산적한 일거리에 시간을 쏟을 작정이었던 것처럼 보인다."], ["But he happens not to be in a business mind.", "그러나 마침 그는 일할 기분이 아니다."], ["After a glance at the documents awaiting his notice—with his head bent low over the table, the old man’s sight for print or writing being defective at night—he opens the French window and steps out upon the leads.", "살펴봐 달라고 기다리는 서류를 한 번 흘끗 보고는—책상 위로 머리를 잔뜩 숙인 채, 노인의 눈은 밤이 되면 인쇄물이나 글씨를 보는 데 영 시원찮으니—그는 프랑스식 창문을 열고 옥상 납판 위로 걸어 나간다."], ["There he again walks slowly up and down in the same attitude, subsiding, if a man so cool may have any need to subside, from the story he has related downstairs.", "거기서 그는 다시 똑같은 자세로 천천히 오르내리며, 이토록 냉정한 사람에게도 가라앉힐 것이 있다면, 아래층에서 들려준 그 이야기에서 마음을 가라앉힌다."]], [["The time was once when men as knowing as Mr. Tulkinghorn would walk on turret-tops in the starlight and look up into the sky to read their fortunes there.", "토킹혼 씨처럼 식견 있는 사람들이 별빛 아래 탑 꼭대기를 거닐며 하늘을 올려다보고 거기서 제 운명을 읽던 시절이 한때 있었다."], ["Hosts of stars are visible to-night, though their brilliancy is eclipsed by the splendour of the moon.", "오늘 밤 무수한 별이 보이지만, 그 광채는 달의 찬란함에 가려 빛을 잃는다."], ["If he be seeking his own star as he methodically turns and turns upon the leads, it should be but a pale one to be so rustily represented below.", "그가 옥상 납판 위를 규칙적으로 돌고 또 돌며 제 별을 찾고 있다면, 이렇게 빛바랜 모습으로 지상에 비친 것을 보건대 그건 어지간히 창백한 별이리라."], ["If he be tracing out his destiny, that may be written in other characters nearer to his hand.", "그가 제 운명을 더듬어 짚고 있다면, 그건 어쩌면 그의 손에 더 가까운 다른 글자로 적혀 있을지도 모른다."]], [["As he paces the leads with his eyes most probably as high above his thoughts as they are high above the earth, he is suddenly stopped in passing the window by two eyes that meet his own.", "그가 두 눈을 십중팔구 제 생각보다 한참 높은 곳에—땅 위로 높이 솟은 만큼이나 높은 곳에—두고 옥상 납판을 거니는데, 창문 앞을 지나다 제 눈과 마주친 두 눈에 문득 발길이 멎는다."], ["The ceiling of his room is rather low; and the upper part of the door, which is opposite the window, is of glass.", "그의 방 천장은 다소 낮고, 창문 맞은편에 있는 문의 윗부분은 유리로 되어 있다."], ["There is an inner baize door, too, but the night being warm he did not close it when he came upstairs.", "안쪽에 나사천을 댄 문도 하나 있지만, 밤 공기가 따뜻해서 그는 위층에 올라올 때 그 문을 닫지 않았다."], ["These eyes that meet his own are looking in through the glass from the corridor outside.", "그의 눈과 마주친 그 두 눈은 바깥 복도에서 유리 너머로 안을 들여다보고 있다."], ["He knows them well.", "그는 그 눈을 잘 안다."], ["The blood has not flushed into his face so suddenly and redly for many a long year as when he recognizes Lady Dedlock.", "데드록 마님을 알아본 순간만큼, 그의 얼굴에 피가 이토록 갑작스럽고 붉게 솟구친 적은 오랜 세월 없었다."]], [["He steps into the room, and she comes in too, closing both the doors behind her.", "그가 방 안으로 들어서자 그녀도 따라 들어와, 등 뒤로 두 문을 모두 닫는다."], ["There is a wild disturbance—is it fear or anger?—in her eyes.", "그녀의 두 눈에는 거친 동요가—두려움인가, 분노인가?—서려 있다."], ["In her carriage and all else she looks as she looked downstairs two hours ago.", "몸가짐도 그 밖의 모든 것도, 두 시간 전 아래층에서 보던 그 모습 그대로다."]], [["Is it fear or is it anger now?", "이제 그것은 두려움인가, 분노인가?"], ["He cannot be sure.", "그는 확신할 수 없다."], ["Both might be as pale, both as intent.", "둘 다 그만큼 창백할 수도, 둘 다 그만큼 골똘할 수도 있으니."]], [["“Lady Dedlock?”", "\"데드록 마님?\""]], [["She does not speak at first, nor even when she has slowly dropped into the easy-chair by the table.", "그녀는 처음에 입을 열지 않고, 책상 옆 안락의자에 천천히 주저앉고 나서도 말이 없다."], ["They look at each other, like two pictures.", "두 사람은 두 폭의 그림처럼 서로를 바라본다."]], [["“Why have you told my story to so many persons?”", "\"왜 그토록 많은 사람에게 제 이야기를 하셨죠?\""]], [["“Lady Dedlock, it was necessary for me to inform you that I knew it.”", "\"데드록 마님, 제가 그 일을 안다는 것을 마님께 알려드려야 했습니다.\""]], [["“How long have you known it?”", "\"언제부터 알고 계셨나요?\""]], [["“I have suspected it a long while—fully known it a little while.”", "\"오래전부터 의심했고, 확실히 안 것은 얼마 되지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Months?” “Days.”", "\"몇 달 전부터?\""]], [["He stands before her with one hand on a chair-back and the other in his old-fashioned waistcoat and shirt-frill, exactly as he has stood before her at any time since her marriage. The same formal politeness, the same composed deference that might as well be defiance; the whole man the same dark, cold object, at the same distance, which nothing has ever diminished.", "\"며칠 전부터입니다.\" 그는 한 손은 의자 등받이에, 다른 손은 구식 조끼와 셔츠 주름 장식에 얹고 그녀 앞에 서 있다. 그녀가 결혼한 뒤로 어느 때든 그녀 앞에 서던 바로 그 모습 그대로다. 똑같은 격식 차린 정중함, 차라리 반항이라 해도 좋을 똑같은 차분한 공손함. 사람 전체가 똑같이 어둡고 차가운 물체로, 똑같은 거리에 있으며, 그 무엇으로도 결코 줄어든 적이 없다."]], [["“Is this true concerning the poor girl?”", "\"그 가엾은 아가씨에 관한 이야기는 사실인가요?\""]], [["He slightly inclines and advances his head as not quite understanding the question.", "그는 질문을 잘 알아듣지 못한 듯 머리를 살짝 기울여 앞으로 내민다."]], [["“You know what you related.", "\"당신이 들려준 이야기 말이에요."], ["Is it true?", "그게 사실인가요?"], ["Do her friends know my story also?", "그 아이의 친지들도 제 이야기를 아나요?"], ["Is it the town-talk yet?", "벌써 온 동네에 소문이 났나요?"], ["Is it chalked upon the walls and cried in the streets?”", "담벼락에 분필로 휘갈겨지고 거리에서 떠들어대고 있나요?\""]], [["So!", "이런!"], ["Anger, and fear, and shame.", "분노와 두려움과 수치심."], ["All three contending.", "셋이 한꺼번에 다투고 있다."], ["What power this woman has to keep these raging passions down! Mr.", "이 여인은 이 끓어오르는 격정을 억누르는 데 얼마나 대단한 힘을 지녔는가!"], ["Tulkinghorn’s thoughts take such form as he looks at her, with his ragged grey eyebrows a hair’s breadth more contracted than usual under her gaze.", "토킹혼 씨는 그녀를 바라보며 이런 생각에 잠기는데, 그녀의 눈길 아래 그의 헝클어진 잿빛 눈썹이 평소보다 머리카락 한 올만큼 더 좁혀진다."]], [["“No, Lady Dedlock.", "\"아닙니다, 데드록 마님."], ["That was a hypothetical case, arising out of Sir Leicester’s unconsciously carrying the matter with so high a hand.", "그건 가정에서 꾸민 이야기로, 레스터 경께서 무심코 그 문제를 워낙 거만하게 다루신 데서 비롯된 겁니다."], ["But it would be a real case if they knew—what we know.”", "하지만 그들이—우리가 아는 것을—알게 된다면, 그건 실제 일이 되겠지요.\""]], [["“Then they do not know it yet?”", "\"그럼 그들은 아직 모르나요?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“Can I save the poor girl from injury before they know it?”", "\"그들이 알기 전에 그 가엾은 아이가 다치지 않도록 제가 구할 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Really, Lady Dedlock,” Mr.", "\"솔직히 말씀드려, 데드록 마님,\""], ["Tulkinghorn replies, “I cannot give a satisfactory opinion on that point.”", "토킹혼 씨가 대답한다. \"그 점에 관해서는 만족스러운 의견을 드릴 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["And he thinks, with the interest of attentive curiosity, as he watches the struggle in her breast, “The power and force of this woman are astonishing!”", "그러면서 그는 그녀의 가슴속에서 벌어지는 갈등을 지켜보며 골똘한 호기심에 가득 차 생각한다. \"이 여인의 힘과 기개는 참으로 놀랍구나!\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보세요,\""], ["she says, for the moment obliged to set her lips with all the energy she has, that she may speak distinctly, “I will make it plainer.", "그녀가 말한다. 또렷하게 말하려고 가진 기력을 다해 잠시 입술을 다물어야 했다."], ["I do not dispute your hypothetical case.", "\"더 분명히 말씀드리죠. 당신의 가정 자체를 따지려는 게 아니에요."], ["I anticipated it, and felt its truth as strongly as you can do, when I saw Mr. Rouncewell here.", "라운스웰 씨를 이곳에서 보았을 때, 저는 이미 그걸 예감했고 당신만큼이나 그 진실을 절절히 느꼈으니까요."], ["I knew very well that if he could have had the power of seeing me as I was, he would consider the poor girl tarnished by having for a moment been, although most innocently, the subject of my great and distinguished patronage.", "만약 그가 제 본모습을 꿰뚫어 볼 수만 있었다면, 그 가엾은 아이가 한순간이라도—더없이 순결한 처지였는데도—저의 대단하고 고귀한 후원의 대상이 되었던 것만으로 더럽혀졌다고 여기리라는 걸, 저는 너무도 잘 알았어요. 하지만 저는 그 아이에게 마음을 쓰고 있어요."], ["But I have an interest in her, or I should rather say—no longer belonging to this place—I had, and if you can find so much consideration for the woman under your foot as to remember that, she will be very sensible of your mercy.”", "아니, 차라리 이렇게 말해야겠군요—이제 이곳에 속한 사람이 아니니—쓰고 있었어요. 당신이 발밑에 짓밟힌 여인에게 그 정도의 배려라도 베풀어 그것을 기억해 주신다면, 그 아이는 당신의 자비를 깊이 새길 거예요.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn, profoundly attentive, throws this off with a shrug of self-depreciation and contracts his eyebrows a little more.", "토킹혼 씨는 깊이 귀를 기울이면서도, 자신을 낮추는 듯 어깨를 한 번 으쓱하며 이 말을 흘려보내고는 눈썹을 조금 더 좁힌다."]], [["“You have prepared me for my exposure, and I thank you for that too.", "\"당신은 제 폭로에 대비하게 해 주셨고, 그것에 대해서도 감사드려요."], ["Is there anything that you require of me?", "제게 요구하실 게 있나요?"], ["Is there any claim that I can release or any charge or trouble that I can spare my husband in obtaining HIS release by certifying to the exactness of your discovery?", "당신의 발견이 정확하다고 제가 증명함으로써, 제 남편이 풀려나는 데—그분의 자유를 되찾는 데—제가 풀어드릴 수 있는 권리나, 덜어드릴 수 있는 어떤 책임이나 수고가 있나요?"], ["I will write anything, here and now, that you will dictate.", "당신이 불러주는 대로, 지금 이 자리에서 무엇이든 쓰겠어요."], ["I am ready to do it.”", "그럴 준비가 되어 있어요.\""]], [["And she would do it, thinks the lawyer, watchful of the firm hand with which she takes the pen!", "그러고도 남을 여자다, 변호사는 그녀가 펜을 쥔 단호한 손을 지켜보며 생각한다!"]], [["“I will not trouble you, Lady Dedlock.", "\"수고를 끼치지 않겠습니다, 데드록 마님."], ["Pray spare yourself.”", "부디 그러지 마십시오.\""]], [["“I have long expected this, as you know.", "\"아시다시피 저는 오래전부터 이걸 예상해 왔어요."], ["I neither wish to spare myself nor to be spared.", "저 자신을 아끼고 싶지도, 누가 아껴주길 바라지도 않아요."], ["You can do nothing worse to me than you have done.", "당신이 이미 한 것보다 더 나쁜 짓을 제게 할 수는 없어요."], ["Do what remains now.”", "이제 남은 걸 하세요.\""]], [["“Lady Dedlock, there is nothing to be done.", "\"데드록 마님, 할 일은 아무것도 없습니다."], ["I will take leave to say a few words when you have finished.”", "말씀이 끝나시면 몇 마디 여쭙겠습니다.\""]], [["Their need for watching one another should be over now, but they do it all this time, and the stars watch them both through the opened window.", "이제 서로를 살필 필요는 끝났어야 마땅하건만, 두 사람은 그러는 내내 서로를 살피고 있고, 별들은 열린 창문 너머로 두 사람을 모두 지켜본다."], ["Away in the moonlight lie the woodland fields at rest, and the wide house is as quiet as the narrow one.", "저 멀리 달빛 속에 숲의 들판이 잠잠히 누워 있고, 너른 저택은 비좁은 그곳만큼이나 고요하다."], ["The narrow one!", "그 비좁은 곳!"], ["Where are the digger and the spade, this peaceful night, destined to add the last great secret to the many secrets of the Tulkinghorn existence?", "토킹혼이라는 존재의 숱한 비밀에 마지막 큰 비밀 하나를 보탤 운명을 짊어진 무덤 파는 자와 삽은, 이 평화로운 밤 어디에 있는가?"], ["Is the man born yet, is the spade wrought yet?", "그 사람은 이미 태어났는가, 그 삽은 이미 벼려졌는가?"], ["Curious questions to consider, more curious perhaps not to consider, under the watching stars upon a summer night.", "여름밤 지켜보는 별들 아래서 곱씹어 볼 만한 묘한 질문이요, 어쩌면 곱씹어 보지 않는 편이 더 묘한 질문이다."]], [["“Of repentance or remorse or any feeling of mine,”", "\"후회나 가책, 혹은 제 어떤 감정에 관해서는,\""], ["Lady Dedlock presently proceeds, “I say not a word.", "데드록 마님이 이윽고 말을 잇는다. \"한마디도 하지 않겠어요."], ["If I were not dumb, you would be deaf.", "제가 벙어리가 아니라면, 당신은 귀머거리겠죠."], ["Let that go by.", "그건 그냥 흘려보내요."], ["It is not for your ears.”", "당신의 귀에 들려줄 게 아니니까요.\""]], [["He makes a feint of offering a protest, but she sweeps it away with her disdainful hand.", "그가 항변하는 시늉을 하지만, 그녀는 경멸하듯 손을 휘저어 그것을 쓸어내 버린다."]], [["“Of other and very different things I come to speak to you.", "\"그것과는 전혀 다른 일을 말씀드리러 왔어요."], ["My jewels are all in their proper places of keeping.", "제 보석은 모두 제자리에 보관되어 있어요."], ["They will be found there.", "거기서 찾으실 거예요."], ["So, my dresses.", "제 드레스도 그래요."], ["So, all the valuables I have.", "제가 가진 귀중품도 죄다 그래요."], ["Some ready money I had with me, please to say, but no large amount.", "현금을 조금 몸에 지녔다고 전해 주세요. 그렇지만 큰 금액은 아니에요."], ["I did not wear my own dress, in order that I might avoid observation.", "남의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 제 옷은 입지 않았어요."], ["I went to be henceforward lost.", "이제부터 영영 자취를 감추려고 떠났다고요."], ["Make this known.", "이걸 알려 주세요."], ["I leave no other charge with you.”", "당신께 다른 책임은 남기지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, Lady Dedlock,”", "\"실례지만, 데드록 마님,\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn, quite unmoved.", "토킹혼 씨가 조금도 동요하지 않고 말한다."], ["“I am not sure that I understand you.", "\"마님 말씀을 제가 제대로 이해했는지 모르겠습니다."], ["You want—”", "마님께서 원하시는 건—\""]], [["“To be lost to all here.", "\"이곳 모든 사람에게서 자취를 감추는 거예요."], ["I leave Chesney Wold to-night.", "저는 오늘 밤 체스니 월드를 떠나요."], ["I go this hour.”", "지금 당장 떠나요.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn shakes his head.", "토킹혼 씨가 고개를 가로젓는다."], ["She rises, but he, without moving hand from chair-back or from old-fashioned waistcoat and shirt-frill, shakes his head.", "그녀가 일어선다. 그러나 그는 의자 등받이에서도, 구식 조끼와 셔츠 주름 장식에서도 손을 떼지 않은 채 고개를 가로젓는다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐죠?"], ["Not go as I have said?”", "제가 말한 대로 떠나지 못한다고요?\""]], [["“No, Lady Dedlock,”", "\"네, 데드록 마님,\""], ["he very calmly replies.", "그가 아주 차분하게 대답한다."]], [["“Do you know the relief that my disappearance will be?", "\"제가 사라지면 얼마나 후련해질지 모르세요?"], ["Have you forgotten the stain and blot upon this place, and where it is, and who it is?”", "이곳에 드리운 오점과 얼룩을, 그게 어디에 있고 누구인지를 잊으셨나요?\""]], [["“No, Lady Dedlock, not by any means.”", "\"아닙니다, 데드록 마님. 결코 그렇지 않습니다.\""]], [["Without deigning to rejoin, she moves to the inner door and has it in her hand when he says to her, without himself stirring hand or foot or raising his voice, “Lady Dedlock, have the goodness to stop and hear me, or before you reach the staircase I shall ring the alarm-bell and rouse the house.", "그녀는 대꾸할 가치도 없다는 듯 안쪽 문으로 다가가 손잡이를 잡는데, 그가 손이나 발 하나 움직이지 않고 목소리도 높이지 않은 채 말한다. \"데드록 마님, 부디 멈추시고 제 말을 들으십시오."], ["And then I must speak out before every guest and servant, every man and woman, in it.”", "그러지 않으면 마님이 계단에 닿기도 전에 제가 비상종을 울려 온 집안을 깨우겠습니다. 그러면 저는 이 집안의 모든 손님과 하인 앞에서, 모든 남자와 여자 앞에서 다 털어놓아야 합니다.\""]], [["He has conquered her.", "그가 그녀를 굴복시켰다."], ["She falters, trembles, and puts her hand confusedly to her head.", "그녀는 비틀거리고, 떨면서, 어쩔 줄 모르고 손을 머리에 가져다 댄다."], ["Slight tokens these in any one else, but when so practised an eye as Mr. Tulkinghorn’s sees indecision for a moment in such a subject, he thoroughly knows its value.", "다른 누구라면 사소한 기색이겠으나, 토킹혼 씨처럼 노련한 눈이 이런 인물에게서 한순간의 망설임을 읽어낼 때, 그는 그 가치를 속속들이 안다."]], [["He promptly says again, “Have the goodness to hear me, Lady Dedlock,”", "그가 곧바로 다시 말한다. \"부디 제 말을 들으십시오, 데드록 마님.\""], ["and motions to the chair from which she has risen.", "그러면서 그녀가 일어났던 의자를 가리킨다."], ["She hesitates, but he motions again, and she sits down.", "그녀가 머뭇거리자 그가 다시 가리키고, 그녀는 자리에 앉는다."]], [["“The relations between us are of an unfortunate description, Lady Dedlock; but as they are not of my making, I will not apologize for them.", "\"우리 사이의 관계가 딱한 사정이라는 건 압니다, 데드록 마님. 하지만 제가 만든 것이 아니니, 그에 대해 사과하지는 않겠습니다."], ["The position I hold in reference to Sir Leicester is so well known to you that I can hardly imagine but that I must long have appeared in your eyes the natural person to make this discovery.”", "제가 레스터 경에 대해 어떤 위치에 있는지는 마님께서 워낙 잘 아시니, 진작부터 제가 마님 눈에 이 발견을 할 만한 당연한 사람으로 비쳤으리라고 어렵잖이 짐작합니다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"이보세요,\""], ["she returns without looking up from the ground on which her eyes are now fixed, “I had better have gone.", "이제 두 눈을 바닥에 고정한 채 시선을 들지 않고 그녀가 대꾸한다. \"그냥 떠나게 두는 편이 나았을 거예요."], ["It would have been far better not to have detained me.", "저를 붙잡지 않는 게 훨씬 나았을 거라고요."], ["I have no more to say.”", "더 드릴 말씀은 없어요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, Lady Dedlock, if I add a little more to hear.”", "\"용서하십시오, 데드록 마님. 들어 주실 말씀을 조금만 더 보태겠습니다.\""]], [["“I wish to hear it at the window, then.", "\"그럼 창가에서 듣겠어요."], ["I can’t breathe where I am.”", "여기서는 숨이 막혀서요.\""]], [["His jealous glance as she walks that way betrays an instant’s misgiving that she may have it in her thoughts to leap over, and dashing against ledge and cornice, strike her life out upon the terrace below.", "그녀가 그쪽으로 걸어가자, 그가 던지는 시샘 어린 눈길에 한순간의 불안이 드러난다. 그녀가 창밖으로 뛰어내려 난간과 처마 돌림띠에 부딪히며 아래쪽 테라스에서 목숨을 끊으려는 생각을 품었을지도 모른다는 불안이다."], ["But a moment’s observation of her figure as she stands in the window without any support, looking out at the stars—not up—gloomily out at those stars which are low in the heavens, reassures him.", "그러나 그녀가 아무것에도 기대지 않고 창가에 서서 별을 바라보는—올려다보지 않고, 하늘 낮게 걸린 그 별들을 음울하게 내다보는—모습을 잠시 살피고는 그는 안심한다."], ["By facing round as she has moved, he stands a little behind her.", "그녀가 움직인 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 그는 그녀의 약간 뒤쪽에 선다."]], [["“Lady Dedlock, I have not yet been able to come to a decision satisfactory to myself on the course before me.", "\"데드록 마님, 저는 제 앞에 놓인 길에 관해 아직 스스로 만족할 만한 결정에 이르지 못했습니다."], ["I am not clear what to do or how to act next.", "무엇을 해야 할지, 다음에 어떻게 처신해야 할지 분명치 않습니다."], ["I must request you, in the meantime, to keep your secret as you have kept it so long and not to wonder that I keep it too.”", "그동안에는 부디 마님께서 오랫동안 지켜오신 그 비밀을 그대로 지키시고, 제가 그것을 함께 지키더라도 의아해하지 마시기를 청합니다.\""]], [["He pauses, but she makes no reply.", "그가 말을 멈추지만, 그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않는다."]], [["“Pardon me, Lady Dedlock.", "\"송구합니다, 데드록 마님."], ["This is an important subject.", "이건 중요한 문제입니다."], ["You are honouring me with your attention?”", "제 말에 귀 기울여 주고 계신지요?\""]], [["“I am.”", "\"그래요.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"감사합니다."], ["I might have known it from what I have seen of your strength of character.", "제가 본 마님의 강인한 성품으로 미루어 진작 알았어야 했습니다."], ["I ought not to have asked the question, but I have the habit of making sure of my ground, step by step, as I go on.", "그 질문은 드리지 말았어야 했는데, 저는 한 걸음 한 걸음 나아가면서 제 발판을 확실히 다지는 버릇이 있어서요."], ["The sole consideration in this unhappy case is Sir Leicester.”", "이 불행한 사건에서 유일하게 고려할 분은 레스터 경입니다.\""]], [["“Then why,”", "\"그럼 어째서,\""], ["she asks in a low voice and without removing her gloomy look from those distant stars, “do you detain me in his house?”", "저 먼 별들에서 음울한 시선을 거두지 않은 채 그녀가 나직이 묻는다. \"그분의 집에 저를 붙잡아 두는 거죠?\""]], [["“Because he IS the consideration.", "\"바로 그분이 고려할 대상이기 때문입니다."], ["Lady Dedlock, I have no occasion to tell you that Sir Leicester is a very proud man, that his reliance upon you is implicit, that the fall of that moon out of the sky would not amaze him more than your fall from your high position as his wife.”", "데드록 마님, 레스터 경께서 대단히 자존심 강한 분이고, 마님께 대한 의지가 절대적이며, 마님이 그분의 아내라는 높은 자리에서 추락하는 것을 보느니 차라리 저 달이 하늘에서 떨어지는 편이 그분을 덜 놀라게 하리라는 것은, 굳이 제가 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요.\""]], [["She breathes quickly and heavily, but she stands as unflinchingly as ever he has seen her in the midst of her grandest company.", "그녀는 가쁘고 무겁게 숨을 쉬지만, 그가 일찍이 그녀의 가장 성대한 손님들 한복판에서 보았던 그 모습 그대로 한 치도 움츠러들지 않고 서 있다."]], [["“I declare to you, Lady Dedlock, that with anything short of this case that I have, I would as soon have hoped to root up by means of my own strength and my own hands the oldest tree on this estate as to shake your hold upon Sir Leicester and Sir Leicester’s trust and confidence in you.", "\"분명히 말씀드리는데, 데드록 마님, 지금 제가 쥔 이 사건만 한 무언가가 없었다면, 저는 차라리 제 힘과 제 두 손으로 이 영지에서 가장 오래된 나무를 뿌리째 뽑아내기를 바랐을 겁니다. 마님이 레스터 경에게 쥔 영향력과, 마님에 대한 레스터 경의 신뢰와 믿음을 흔드는 것보다는요."], ["And even now, with this case, I hesitate.", "그리고 지금조차도, 이 사건을 손에 쥐고도 저는 망설이고 있습니다."], ["Not that he could doubt (that, even with him, is impossible), but that nothing can prepare him for the blow.”", "그분이 의심하실까 봐가 아닙니다(그건 그분에게조차 불가능한 일이지요). 그 어떤 것으로도 그분에게 그 충격을 대비시킬 수 없기 때문입니다.\""]], [["“Not my flight?”", "\"제가 달아나는 것으로도 안 되나요?\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대꾸한다."], ["“Think of it again.”", "\"다시 생각해 보세요.\""]], [["“Your flight, Lady Dedlock, would spread the whole truth, and a hundred times the whole truth, far and wide.", "\"마님이 달아나신다면, 데드록 마님, 그 진실 전체가, 아니 진실의 백 배가 사방팔방으로 퍼질 겁니다."], ["It would be impossible to save the family credit for a day.", "단 하루도 가문의 명예를 지키는 게 불가능할 겁니다."], ["It is not to be thought of.”", "생각조차 할 수 없는 일입니다.\""]], [["There is a quiet decision in his reply which admits of no remonstrance.", "그의 대답에는 어떤 항변도 허용하지 않는 조용한 단호함이 깃들어 있다."]], [["“When I speak of Sir Leicester being the sole consideration, he and the family credit are one.", "\"제가 레스터 경을 유일한 고려 대상이라고 말씀드릴 때, 그분과 가문의 명예는 하나입니다."], ["Sir Leicester and the baronetcy, Sir Leicester and Chesney Wold, Sir Leicester and his ancestors and his patrimony”—Mr. Tulkinghorn very dry here—“are, I need not say to you, Lady Dedlock, inseparable.”", "레스터 경과 준남작 작위, 레스터 경과 체스니 월드, 레스터 경과 그분의 조상들과 세습 재산은\"—여기서 토킹혼 씨는 자못 무미건조하게—\"굳이 마님께 말씀드릴 것도 없이, 데드록 마님, 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 것이지요.\""]], [["“Go on!”", "\"계속하세요!\""]], [["“Therefore,” says Mr.", "\"그러니,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, pursuing his case in his jog-trot style, “I have much to consider.", "토킹혼 씨가 느릿느릿한 특유의 어조로 사건을 짚어가며 말한다. \"고려할 게 많습니다."], ["This is to be hushed up if it can be.", "가능하다면 이 일은 덮어야 합니다."], ["How can it be, if Sir Leicester is driven out of his wits or laid upon a death-bed?", "그런데 레스터 경이 정신이 나가거나 임종의 자리에 누우신다면, 어떻게 덮을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["If I inflicted this shock upon him to-morrow morning, how could the immediate change in him be accounted for?", "제가 내일 아침 그분께 이 충격을 안긴다면, 그분의 그 갑작스러운 변화를 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["What could have caused it?", "무엇이 그것을 불러왔다 하겠습니까?"], ["What could have divided you?", "무엇이 두 분을 갈라놓았다 하겠습니까?"], ["Lady Dedlock, the wall-chalking and the street-crying would come on directly, and you are to remember that it would not affect you merely (whom I cannot at all consider in this business) but your husband, Lady Dedlock, your husband.”", "데드록 마님, 담벼락의 낙서와 거리의 외침이 곧장 들이닥칠 것이고, 마님께서 기억하셔야 할 것은, 그것이 마님 한 분께만(이 일에서 저는 마님을 도무지 고려할 수 없습니다만) 영향을 미치는 게 아니라 마님의 남편께, 데드록 마님, 마님의 남편께 영향을 미친다는 점입니다.\""]], [["He gets plainer as he gets on, but not an atom more emphatic or animated.", "말을 이어갈수록 그는 더 분명해지지만, 한 톨만큼도 더 격앙되거나 활기를 띠지는 않는다."]], [["“There is another point of view,”", "\"이 사건을 바라볼 또 다른 관점이 있습니다.\""], ["he continues, “in which the case presents itself.", "그가 말을 잇는다."], ["Sir Leicester is devoted to you almost to infatuation.", "\"레스터 경은 마님께 거의 미혹에 가까울 만큼 헌신적입니다."], ["He might not be able to overcome that infatuation, even knowing what we know.", "우리가 아는 것을 알게 되더라도, 그분은 그 미혹을 떨쳐내지 못하실 수도 있습니다."], ["I am putting an extreme case, but it might be so.", "극단적인 경우를 들고 있습니다만, 그럴 수도 있다는 겁니다."], ["If so, it were better that he knew nothing.", "만약 그렇다면, 그분은 아무것도 모르시는 편이 낫습니다."], ["Better for common sense, better for him, better for me.", "상식적으로도 낫고, 그분께도 낫고, 저에게도 낫지요."], ["I must take all this into account, and it combines to render a decision very difficult.”", "저는 이 모든 것을 헤아려야 하고, 그것들이 한데 얽혀 결정을 무척 어렵게 만듭니다.\""]], [["She stands looking out at the same stars without a word.", "그녀는 한마디 말도 없이 똑같은 별들을 내다보며 서 있다."], ["They are beginning to pale, and she looks as if their coldness froze her.", "별들이 빛을 잃기 시작하고, 그녀는 마치 그 차가움이 자기를 얼리기라도 하는 듯한 모습이다."]], [["“My experience teaches me,” says Mr.", "\"제 경험이 가르쳐 주는바,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, who has by this time got his hands in his pockets and is going on in his business consideration of the matter like a machine.", "이제 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 기계처럼 사무적으로 그 문제를 따져가며 토킹혼 씨가 말한다."], ["“My experience teaches me, Lady Dedlock, that most of the people I know would do far better to leave marriage alone.", "\"제 경험이 가르쳐 주는바, 데드록 마님, 제가 아는 대부분의 사람들은 결혼 따위 하지 않는 편이 훨씬 나았을 겁니다."], ["It is at the bottom of three fourths of their troubles.", "그게 그들이 겪는 고민의 사분의 삼의 뿌리지요."], ["So I thought when Sir Leicester married, and so I always have thought since.", "레스터 경이 결혼하실 때도 저는 그렇게 생각했고, 그 뒤로도 늘 그렇게 생각해 왔습니다."], ["No more about that.", "그 이야기는 그만하지요."], ["I must now be guided by circumstances.", "이제 저는 정황에 따라 처신해야 합니다."], ["In the meanwhile I must beg you to keep your own counsel, and I will keep mine.”", "그동안 마님께서는 부디 입을 다물어 주시고, 저도 그리하겠습니다.\""]], [["“I am to drag my present life on, holding its pains at your pleasure, day by day?”", "\"제 지금의 삶을 그대로 끌고 가면서, 당신 마음 내키는 대로 하루하루 그 고통을 견디라는 건가요?\""], ["she asks, still looking at the distant sky.", "여전히 먼 하늘을 바라보며 그녀가 묻는다."]], [["“Yes, I am afraid so, Lady Dedlock.”", "\"네, 유감스럽지만 그렇습니다, 데드록 마님.\""]], [["“It is necessary, you think, that I should be so tied to the stake?”", "\"제가 그렇게 형틀에 묶여 있어야 한다고, 그게 필요하다고 보시나요?\""]], [["“I am sure that what I recommend is necessary.”", "\"제가 권하는 것이 필요하다는 건 확신합니다.\""]], [["“I am to remain on this gaudy platform on which my miserable deception has been so long acted, and it is to fall beneath me when you give the signal?”", "\"제가 그 비참한 기만을 그토록 오래 연기해 온 이 화려한 무대 위에 그대로 남아 있다가, 당신이 신호를 보내면 그 무대가 제 발밑에서 무너져 내린다는 거군요?\""], ["she said slowly.", "그녀가 천천히 말한다."]], [["“Not without notice, Lady Dedlock.", "\"미리 알려드리지 않고 그러지는 않겠습니다, 데드록 마님."], ["I shall take no step without forewarning you.”", "저는 마님께 먼저 경고하지 않고는 어떤 조치도 취하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["She asks all her questions as if she were repeating them from memory or calling them over in her sleep.", "그녀는 마치 외운 것을 되뇌듯, 혹은 잠결에 읊조리듯 모든 질문을 던진다."]], [["“We are to meet as usual?”", "\"우리는 여느 때처럼 만나는 건가요?\""]], [["“Precisely as usual, if you please.”", "\"여느 때와 똑같이요, 그래 주신다면요.\""]], [["“And I am to hide my guilt, as I have done so many years?”", "\"그리고 저는 그토록 숱한 세월 그래왔듯 제 죄를 숨기는 거고요?\""]], [["“As you have done so many years.", "\"그토록 숱한 세월 그래오셨듯이요."], ["I should not have made that reference myself, Lady Dedlock, but I may now remind you that your secret can be no heavier to you than it was, and is no worse and no better than it was.", "제가 먼저 그런 말은 꺼내지 말았어야 했습니다, 데드록 마님. 하지만 이제 한 가지 일러둘 것은, 마님의 비밀이 예전보다 더 무거울 것도 없고, 예전보다 더 나빠지거나 더 나아진 것도 없다는 점입니다."], ["I know it certainly, but I believe we have never wholly trusted each other.”", "저는 그것을 확실히 압니다만, 우리는 한 번도 서로를 온전히 믿은 적이 없는 것 같군요.\""]], [["She stands absorbed in the same frozen way for some little time before asking, “Is there anything more to be said to-night?”", "그녀는 얼마간 똑같이 얼어붙은 자세로 골똘히 서 있다가 묻는다. \"오늘 밤 더 하실 말씀이 있나요?\""]], [["“Why,” Mr.", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["Tulkinghorn returns methodically as he softly rubs his hands, “I should like to be assured of your acquiescence in my arrangements, Lady Dedlock.”", "토킹혼 씨가 두 손을 가만히 비비며 차근차근 대답한다. \"제 조치에 마님께서 동의하신다는 걸 확인하고 싶습니다, 데드록 마님.\""]], [["“You may be assured of it.”", "\"확인하셔도 좋아요.\""]], [["“Good.", "\"좋습니다."], ["And I would wish in conclusion to remind you, as a business precaution, in case it should be necessary to recall the fact in any communication with Sir Leicester, that throughout our interview I have expressly stated my sole consideration to be Sir Leicester’s feelings and honour and the family reputation.", "그리고 끝으로, 업무상 신중을 기하는 차원에서 한 가지 일러둘 것은—혹여 레스터 경과의 어떤 대화에서 이 사실을 되짚을 필요가 생길 경우를 대비해서요—이번 면담 내내 제가 유일하게 고려하는 것은 레스터 경의 심정과 명예, 그리고 가문의 평판이라고 분명히 밝혔다는 점입니다."], ["I should have been happy to have made Lady Dedlock a prominent consideration, too, if the case had admitted of it; but unfortunately it does not.”", "사정이 허락했다면 저는 데드록 마님 역시 비중 있게 고려하게 되어 기뻤겠습니다만, 안타깝게도 사정이 그러지 못하는군요.\""]], [["“I can attest your fidelity, sir.”", "\"당신의 충직함은 제가 증언해 드리지요, 이보세요.\""]], [["Both before and after saying it she remains absorbed, but at length moves, and turns, unshaken in her natural and acquired presence, towards the door. Mr.", "그 말을 하기 전에도 후에도 그녀는 골똘히 잠겨 있다가, 마침내 몸을 움직여, 타고난 위엄과 몸에 밴 기품 어느 것도 흔들림 없이 문 쪽으로 돌아선다."], ["Tulkinghorn opens both the doors exactly as he would have done yesterday, or as he would have done ten years ago, and makes his old-fashioned bow as she passes out.", "토킹혼 씨는 어제 그랬을 법한, 혹은 십 년 전에 그랬을 법한 그대로 두 문을 열고, 그녀가 나갈 때 구식으로 허리 굽혀 인사한다."], ["It is not an ordinary look that he receives from the handsome face as it goes into the darkness, and it is not an ordinary movement, though a very slight one, that acknowledges his courtesy.", "어둠 속으로 사라지는 그 아름다운 얼굴에서 그가 받은 눈길은 예사로운 눈길이 아니요, 그의 정중함에 답하는 그 몸짓은 비록 아주 미미할망정 예사로운 몸짓이 아니다."], ["But as he reflects when he is left alone, the woman has been putting no common constraint upon herself.", "그러나 혼자 남아 곰곰이 헤아려 보건대, 그 여인은 예사롭지 않은 자제를 자기 자신에게 강요하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["He would know it all the better if he saw the woman pacing her own rooms with her hair wildly thrown from her flung-back face, her hands clasped behind her head, her figure twisted as if by pain.", "만약 그 여인이 자기 방을 거니는 모습을 보았다면, 그는 그것을 한층 더 잘 알았으리라. 뒤로 젖힌 얼굴에서 머리카락이 거칠게 흩날리고, 두 손은 머리 뒤로 깍지를 끼었으며, 몸은 고통에 비틀린 듯한 그 모습을."], ["He would think so all the more if he saw the woman thus hurrying up and down for hours, without fatigue, without intermission, followed by the faithful step upon the Ghost’s Walk.", "그 여인이 이렇게 몇 시간을, 지치지도 쉬지도 않고 오르내리는 모습을, 그리고 유령의 길 위로 그 충실한 발소리가 뒤따르는 모습을 보았다면, 그는 더더욱 그렇게 생각했으리라."], ["But he shuts out the now chilled air, draws the window-curtain, goes to bed, and falls asleep.", "그러나 그는 이제 싸늘해진 공기를 가두어 막고, 창문 커튼을 치고, 잠자리에 들어 잠든다."], ["And truly when the stars go out and the wan day peeps into the turret-chamber, finding him at his oldest, he looks as if the digger and the spade were both commissioned and would soon be digging.", "그리고 정녕 별들이 스러지고 핏기 없는 낮이 탑실 안을 들여다보며 더없이 늙어 보이는 그를 발견할 때, 그는 마치 무덤 파는 자와 삽이 둘 다 이미 부름을 받아 곧 흙을 파헤치기라도 할 것 같은 모습이다."]], [["The same wan day peeps in at Sir Leicester pardoning the repentant country in a majestically condescending dream; and at the cousins entering on various public employments, principally receipt of salary; and at the chaste Volumnia, bestowing a dower of fifty thousand pounds upon a hideous old general with a mouth of false teeth like a pianoforte too full of keys, long the admiration of Bath and the terror of every other community.", "그 똑같은 핏기 없는 낮이, 회개한 백성을 위풍당당하게 거들먹대며 사면하는 꿈에 잠긴 레스터 경을 들여다본다. 또 여러 공직에—주로 봉급을 받는 자리에—올라선 사촌들을 들여다본다."], ["Also into rooms high in the roof, and into offices in court-yards, and over stables, where humbler ambition dreams of bliss, in keepers’ lodges, and in holy matrimony with Will or Sally.", "또 정숙한 볼럼니아가, 건반이 너무 빽빽이 들어찬 피아노 같은 의치를 한 입에 단 흉측한 늙은 장군에게—오랫동안 바스의 찬탄이자 다른 모든 사교계의 공포였던 그 사내에게—오만 파운드의 지참금을 안기는 광경을 들여다본다."], ["Up comes the bright sun, drawing everything up with it—the Wills and Sallys, the latent vapour in the earth, the drooping leaves and flowers, the birds and beasts and creeping things, the gardeners to sweep the dewy turf and unfold emerald velvet where the roller passes, the smoke of the great kitchen fire wreathing itself straight and high into the lightsome air.", "또한 지붕 높이 자리한 방들과, 안마당의 사무실들과, 마구간 위쪽 방들도 들여다본다. 그곳에서는 더 소박한 야심이 행복을 꿈꾸고, 사냥터지기의 오두막에서, 윌이나 샐리와의 거룩한 혼인을 꿈꾼다."], ["Lastly, up comes the flag over Mr. Tulkinghorn’s unconscious head cheerfully proclaiming that Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock are in their happy home and that there is hospitality at the place in Lincolnshire.", "환한 해가 솟아올라 만물을 함께 끌어 올린다—윌들과 샐리들을, 땅속에 숨은 수증기를, 늘어진 잎과 꽃을, 새와 짐승과 기어 다니는 것들을, 이슬 맺힌 잔디를 쓸고 롤러가 지나간 자리마다 에메랄드빛 우단을 펼치는 정원사들을, 그리고 환한 대기 속으로 곧고 높이 똬리 틀며 피어오르는 대형 부엌 화덕의 연기를."], ["In Mr. Tulkinghorn’s Chambers", "마지막으로, 아무것도 모르고 잠든 토킹혼 씨의 머리 위로 깃발이 솟아올라, 레스터 경과 데드록 마님이 행복한 보금자리에 계시며 이 링컨셔의 저택에 환대가 베풀어진다는 것을 명랑하게 알린다."]], [["From the verdant undulations and the spreading oaks of the Dedlock property, Mr. Tulkinghorn transfers himself to the stale heat and dust of London.", "데드록 영지의 푸르른 굽이와 넓게 가지를 펼친 떡갈나무 숲에서 토킹혼 씨는 런던의 케케묵은 열기와 먼지 속으로 자리를 옮긴다."], ["His manner of coming and going between the two places is one of his impenetrabilities.", "그가 두 곳 사이를 오가는 방식은 그의 헤아릴 수 없는 면모 가운데 하나다."], ["He walks into Chesney Wold as if it were next door to his chambers and returns to his chambers as if he had never been out of Lincoln’s Inn Fields.", "그는 마치 체스니 월드가 자기 사무실 바로 옆집이라도 되는 양 그곳으로 걸어 들어가고, 마치 링컨스 인 필즈를 한 번도 떠난 적 없다는 듯 사무실로 돌아온다."], ["He neither changes his dress before the journey nor talks of it afterwards.", "떠나기 전에 옷을 갈아입는 법도 없고, 다녀온 뒤에 그 이야기를 입에 올리는 법도 없다."], ["He melted out of his turret-room this morning, just as now, in the late twilight, he melts into his own square.", "오늘 아침 탑방에서 슬며시 녹아 사라졌듯, 지금 저무는 어스름 속에서 그는 자기 광장으로 슬며시 녹아든다."]], [["Like a dingy London bird among the birds at roost in these pleasant fields, where the sheep are all made into parchment, the goats into wigs, and the pasture into chaff, the lawyer, smoke-dried and faded, dwelling among mankind but not consorting with them, aged without experience of genial youth, and so long used to make his cramped nest in holes and corners of human nature that he has forgotten its broader and better range, comes sauntering home.", "양은 모조리 양피지가 되고, 염소는 가발이 되고, 목초는 여물이 되어버린 이 쾌적한 들판에 깃들인 새들 틈에 끼어든 한 마리 거무튀튀한 런던 새처럼, 연기에 그을려 빛바랜 이 변호사는, 인간들 사이에 살되 어울리지는 않으며, 인정 넘치는 젊음을 겪어본 적도 없이 늙었고, 인간 본성의 구멍과 구석에 비좁은 둥지를 트는 데 너무 오래 익숙해져 그 본성의 더 넓고 더 나은 영역을 잊어버린 채, 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 돌아온다."], ["In the oven made by the hot pavements and hot buildings, he has baked himself dryer than usual; and he has in his thirsty mind his mellowed port-wine half a century old.", "뜨거운 포장도로와 뜨거운 건물이 만들어낸 화덕 속에서 그는 평소보다 더 바싹 구워졌고, 갈증 어린 마음속에는 반세기 묵어 그윽하게 익은 포트와인이 들어 있다."]], [["The lamplighter is skipping up and down his ladder on Mr. Tulkinghorn’s side of the Fields when that high-priest of noble mysteries arrives at his own dull court-yard.", "귀족적 비밀을 다루는 이 대제사장이 자기 칙칙한 안마당에 다다랐을 때, 점등부는 필즈의 토킹혼 씨 쪽 사다리를 오르락내리락하고 있다."], ["He ascends the door-steps and is gliding into the dusky hall when he encounters, on the top step, a bowing and propitiatory little man.", "그가 현관 계단을 올라 어슴푸레한 복도로 미끄러져 들어가려는 찰나, 맨 위 계단에서 굽실거리며 비위를 맞추는 자그마한 사내와 마주친다."]], [["“Is that Snagsby?”", "\"스내그스비 아닌가?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"예, 선생님."], ["I hope you are well, sir.", "안녕하셨습니까."], ["I was just giving you up, sir, and going home.”", "막 단념하고 집에 돌아가려던 참이었습니다.\""]], [["“Aye?", "\"그래?"], ["What is it?", "무슨 일인가?"], ["What do you want with me?”", "나한테 무슨 볼일이 있어서?\""]], [["“Well, sir,” says Mr.", "\"저, 선생님.\""], ["Snagsby, holding his hat at the side of his head in his deference towards his best customer, “I was wishful to say a word to you, sir.”", "스내그스비 씨가 자신의 가장 소중한 단골에게 보이는 공경의 표시로 모자를 머리 옆에 댄 채 말한다. \"한 말씀 여쭙고 싶었습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Can you say it here?”", "\"여기서 말해도 되겠나?\""]], [["“Perfectly, sir.”", "\"물론입니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Say it then.”", "\"그럼 말해보게.\""], ["The lawyer turns, leans his arms on the iron railing at the top of the steps, and looks at the lamplighter lighting the court-yard.", "변호사는 몸을 돌려 계단 꼭대기의 쇠난간에 팔을 기대고는, 안마당에 불을 밝히는 점등부를 바라본다."]], [["“It is relating,” says Mr.", "\"실은 관련된 일입니다.\""], ["Snagsby in a mysterious low voice, “it is relating—not to put too fine a point upon it—to the foreigner, sir!”", "스내그스비 씨가 비밀스럽게 낮은 목소리로 말한다. \"그 외국 여자와—이렇게 거두절미하고 말씀드리자면—관련된 일입니다, 선생님!\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn eyes him with some surprise.", "토킹혼 씨가 다소 놀란 눈으로 그를 본다."], ["“What foreigner?”", "\"무슨 외국 여자?\""]], [["“The foreign female, sir. French, if I don’t mistake?", "\"그 외국 여자 말입니다, 선생님."], ["I am not acquainted with that language myself, but I should judge from her manners and appearance that she was French; anyways, certainly foreign.", "제가 잘못 안 게 아니라면 프랑스 사람이지요? 저야 그 나라 말은 모릅니다만, 거동이며 생김새로 미루어 프랑스 사람일 거라고 짐작합니다."], ["Her that was upstairs, sir, when Mr.", "아무튼 외국인은 분명합니다."], ["Bucket and me had the honour of waiting upon you with the sweeping-boy that night.”", "그날 밤 버킷 씨하고 제가 그 청소부 소년을 데리고 선생님을 모시는 영광을 누렸을 때, 위층에 있던 바로 그 여자 말입니다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Yes, yes.", "그래, 그래."], ["Mademoiselle Hortense.”", "오르탕스 양 말이로군.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir?” Mr.", "\"그렇습니까, 선생님?\""], ["Snagsby coughs his cough of submission behind his hat.", "스내그스비 씨는 모자 뒤에서 굴종의 기침을 한 번 한다."], ["“I am not acquainted myself with the names of foreigners in general, but I have no doubt it WOULD be that.”", "\"저야 외국인 이름은 통 모릅니다만, 틀림없이 그 사람일 겁니다.\""], ["Mr. Snagsby appears to have set out in this reply with some desperate design of repeating the name, but on reflection coughs again to excuse himself.", "스내그스비 씨는 이 대답을 하면서 그 이름을 따라 발음해보겠다는 결사적인 작정을 했던 듯하나, 다시 생각하더니 또 한 번 기침으로 얼버무린다."]], [["“And what can you have to say, Snagsby,” demands Mr.", "\"그래, 그 여자에 대해 무슨 할 말이 있다는 건가, 스내그스비?\""], ["Tulkinghorn, “about her?”", "토킹혼 씨가 캐묻는다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"저, 선생님.\""], ["returns the stationer, shading his communication with his hat, “it falls a little hard upon me.", "문구상은 모자로 자신의 이야기를 가리며 대답한다. \"이게 제게는 좀 난처한 일이올시다."], ["My domestic happiness is very great—at least, it’s as great as can be expected, I’m sure—but my little woman is rather given to jealousy.", "제 가정의 행복이야 더없이 크지요—적어도 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 크다고 봅니다, 정말로—하지만 제 마누라가 좀 질투심이 많은 편이라서요."], ["Not to put too fine a point upon it, she is very much given to jealousy.", "거두절미하고 말씀드리자면, 질투심이 아주 심합니다."], ["And you see, a foreign female of that genteel appearance coming into the shop, and hovering—I should be the last to make use of a strong expression if I could avoid it, but hovering, sir—in the court—you know it is—now ain’t it?", "그런데 보십시오, 그렇게 점잖아 보이는 외국 여자가 가게로 들어와서는, 어슬렁어슬렁—피할 수만 있다면 저야 그런 강한 표현은 절대 쓰지 않을 사람입니다만, 어슬렁어슬렁, 선생님—골목을 맴도니—그게 어디 보통 일입니까—안 그렇습니까?"], ["I only put it to yourself, sir.”", "그저 선생님 판단에 맡기겠습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby, having said this in a very plaintive manner, throws in a cough of general application to fill up all the blanks.", "이 말을 무척 애처로운 투로 한 스내그스비 씨는 모든 빈틈을 메우려 두루 쓰이는 기침을 한 번 던져 넣는다."]], [["“Why, what do you mean?”", "\"아니, 그게 대체 무슨 소린가?\""], ["asks Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨가 묻는다."]], [["“Just so, sir,” returns Mr.", "\"바로 그겁니다, 선생님.\""], ["Snagsby; “I was sure you would feel it yourself and would excuse the reasonableness of MY feelings when coupled with the known excitableness of my little woman.", "스내그스비 씨가 대답한다. \"선생님께서도 직접 느끼시리라 확신했고, 또 제 마누라의 잘 알려진 흥분 기질과 더불어 제 심정의 마땅함도 헤아려주시리라 믿었습니다."], ["You see, the foreign female—which you mentioned her name just now, with quite a native sound I am sure—caught up the word Snagsby that night, being uncommon quick, and made inquiry, and got the direction and come at dinner-time.", "보십시오, 그 외국 여자가—방금 선생님께서 아주 본토박이 같은 발음으로 이름을 말씀하셨지요—그날 밤 워낙 눈치가 빨라 스내그스비라는 말을 알아챘는지, 캐묻고 다녀 주소를 알아내서는 저녁 시간에 찾아왔지 뭡니까."], ["Now Guster, our young woman, is timid and has fits, and she, taking fright at the foreigner’s looks—which are fierce—and at a grinding manner that she has of speaking—which is calculated to alarm a weak mind—gave way to it, instead of bearing up against it, and tumbled down the kitchen stairs out of one into another, such fits as I do sometimes think are never gone into, or come out of, in any house but ours.", "그런데 우리 집 어린 하녀 거스터가 겁이 많고 발작을 일으키는 아이라, 그 외국 여자의 험상궂은—사나운—얼굴과, 무언가를 가는 듯한 말투에—여린 마음을 놀라게 하기 딱 좋은 말투지요—기겁을 해서는, 견디기는커녕 그만 무너져, 부엌 계단을 데굴데굴 굴러 한 발작에서 또 다른 발작으로 넘어갔는데, 그런 발작은 저희 집 말고는 어느 집에서도 시작되지도 끝나지도 않을 거라고 가끔 생각할 정도입니다."], ["Consequently there was by good fortune ample occupation for my little woman, and only me to answer the shop.", "그러니 다행인지 불행인지 제 마누라가 그 일에 매달릴 거리가 넘쳐났고, 가게는 저 혼자 봐야 했지요."], ["When she DID say that Mr. Tulkinghorn, being always denied to her by his employer (which I had no doubt at the time was a foreign mode of viewing a clerk), she would do herself the pleasure of continually calling at my place until she was let in here.", "그 여자가 글쎄, 토킹혼 씨는 고용주 측에서 늘 자기를 따돌린다며(그때는 저도 외국식으로 서기를 보는 방식인가 보다 했습니다만), 여기 들어올 수 있을 때까지 저희 집에 계속 찾아오는 즐거움을 누리겠다는 겁니다."], ["Since then she has been, as I began by saying, hovering, hovering, sir”—Mr. Snagsby repeats the word with pathetic emphasis—“in the court.", "그 뒤로 줄곧, 처음에 말씀드린 대로, 어슬렁어슬렁 맴돌고 있습니다, 선생님\"—스내그스비 씨는 애처로운 강조를 담아 그 말을 되풀이한다—\"골목에서 말이지요."], ["The effects of which movement it is impossible to calculate.", "그게 어떤 영향을 미칠지 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없습니다."], ["I shouldn’t wonder if it might have already given rise to the painfullest mistakes even in the neighbours’ minds, not mentioning (if such a thing was possible) my little woman.", "어쩌면 벌써 이웃 사람들 마음에까지 더없이 고약한 오해를 불러일으켰을지도 모릅니다. 제 마누라야(그럴 수만 있다면) 말할 것도 없고요."], ["Whereas, goodness knows,” says Mr.", "그런데, 하느님 아실 일이지만,\""], ["Snagsby, shaking his head, “I never had an idea of a foreign female, except as being formerly connected with a bunch of brooms and a baby, or at the present time with a tambourine and earrings.", "스내그스비 씨는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말한다. \"저는 외국 여자라고 하면 예전에는 빗자루 다발에 갓난애를 안은 모습으로, 요새는 탬버린에 귀고리를 단 모습으로밖에 떠올려본 적이 없습니다."], ["I never had, I do assure you, sir!”", "정말이지 그밖엔 없었습니다, 선생님!\""]], [["Mr.", "토킹혼 씨는 이 하소연을 진지하게 듣고는, 문구상이 말을 마치자 묻는다."], ["Tulkinghorn had listened gravely to this complaint and inquires when the stationer has finished, “And that’s all, is it, Snagsby?”", "\"그래서 그게 다인가, 스내그스비?\""]], [["“Why yes, sir, that’s all,” says Mr.", "\"예, 그렇습니다, 선생님, 그게 답니다.\""], ["Snagsby, ending with a cough that plainly adds, “and it’s enough too—for me.”", "스내그스비 씨는 \"게다가 이만하면 저로서는 충분하고도 남습니다\"라고 또렷이 덧붙이는 듯한 기침으로 말을 맺는다."]], [["“I don’t know what Mademoiselle Hortense may want or mean, unless she is mad,”", "\"오르탕스 양이 뭘 원하는지, 무슨 속셈인지 나로서는 모르겠군. 미친 게 아니라면 말이야.\""], ["says the lawyer.", "변호사가 말한다."]], [["“Even if she was, you know, sir,” Mr.", "\"설령 미쳤다 한들 말입니다, 선생님.\""], ["Snagsby pleads, “it wouldn’t be a consolation to have some weapon or another in the form of a foreign dagger planted in the family.”", "스내그스비 씨가 통사정한다. \"외국 단검 같은 무기가 우리 집안에 꽂힌다고 해서 그게 위안이 되지는 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그렇지.\""], ["says the other.", "상대가 말한다."], ["“Well, well!", "\"자, 자!"], ["This shall be stopped.", "이 일은 멈추도록 하겠네."], ["I am sorry you have been inconvenienced.", "자네가 불편을 겪었다니 안됐군."], ["If she comes again, send her here.”", "그 여자가 또 오거든 이리로 보내게.\""]], [["Mr. Snagsby, with much bowing and short apologetic coughing, takes his leave, lightened in heart.", "스내그스비 씨는 연신 굽실거리고 짧게 사과하는 기침을 하며 한결 가벼워진 마음으로 물러간다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn goes upstairs, saying to himself, “These women were created to give trouble the whole earth over.", "토킹혼 씨는 위층으로 올라가며 혼잣말을 한다."], ["The mistress not being enough to deal with, here’s the maid now!", "\"이 여자들이란 온 세상에 말썽을 일으키려고 태어난 족속이야. 마님 하나로도 모자라더니, 이번엔 하녀까지!"], ["But I will be short with THIS jade at least!”", "하지만 이 계집한테만은 단호하게 잘라버리겠어!\""]], [["So saying, he unlocks his door, gropes his way into his murky rooms, lights his candles, and looks about him.", "이렇게 중얼거리며 그는 문을 열고, 어두컴컴한 방을 더듬어 들어가, 촛불을 밝히고 주위를 둘러본다."], ["It is too dark to see much of the Allegory overhead there, but that importunate Roman, who is for ever toppling out of the clouds and pointing, is at his old work pretty distinctly. Not honouring him with much attention, Mr.", "머리 위의 우의화는 너무 어두워 잘 보이지 않지만, 늘 구름에서 굴러떨어지며 손가락질하는 그 끈질긴 로마인만은 꽤 또렷이 제 일을 하고 있다."], ["Tulkinghorn takes a small key from his pocket, unlocks a drawer in which there is another key, which unlocks a chest in which there is another, and so comes to the cellar-key, with which he prepares to descend to the regions of old wine.", "별로 주의를 기울이지 않은 채 토킹혼 씨는 주머니에서 작은 열쇠를 꺼내 서랍을 여니 그 안에 또 다른 열쇠가 있고, 그 열쇠로 궤를 여니 또 다른 열쇠가 있어, 마침내 지하 저장실 열쇠에 이른다. 그 열쇠로 묵은 포도주가 있는 곳으로 내려갈 채비를 한다."], ["He is going towards the door with a candle in his hand when a knock comes.", "촛불을 손에 들고 문 쪽으로 가는데 노크 소리가 난다."]], [["“Who’s this?", "\"누구지?"], ["Aye, aye, mistress, it’s you, is it?", "아, 아, 마님이로군, 자네인가?"], ["You appear at a good time.", "마침 잘 왔어."], ["I have just been hearing of you.", "방금 자네 이야기를 듣던 참이거든."], ["Now!", "자!"], ["What do you want?”", "무슨 볼일인가?\""]], [["He stands the candle on the chimney-piece in the clerk’s hall and taps his dry cheek with the key as he addresses these words of welcome to Mademoiselle Hortense.", "그는 서기방 벽난로 선반에 촛불을 세워두고, 오르탕스 양에게 이런 환영의 말을 건네며 열쇠로 자신의 메마른 뺨을 톡톡 두드린다."], ["That feline personage, with her lips tightly shut and her eyes looking out at him sideways, softly closes the door before replying.", "입술을 꼭 다물고 곁눈질로 그를 노려보는 그 고양이 같은 여자는, 대답하기 전에 살그머니 문을 닫는다."]], [["“I have had great deal of trouble to find you, sir.”", "\"당신을 찾느라 엄청 애먹었어요, 선생님.\""]], [["“HAVE you!”", "\"그랬어!\""]], [["“I have been here very often, sir.", "\"여기 아주 자주 왔어요, 선생님."], ["It has always been said to me, he is not at home, he is engage, he is this and that, he is not for you.”", "그때마다 안 계신다, 바쁘시다, 이렇다 저렇다, 당신을 만날 수 없다고만 하더군요.\""]], [["“Quite right, and quite true.”", "\"옳은 말이고, 사실이지.\""]], [["“Not true.", "\"사실 아니에요."], ["Lies!”", "거짓말!\""]], [["At times there is a suddenness in the manner of Mademoiselle Hortense so like a bodily spring upon the subject of it that such subject involuntarily starts and falls back.", "오르탕스 양의 태도에는 이따금 어찌나 느닷없는 데가 있는지, 마치 상대에게 몸을 날려 덮치는 것 같아 그 상대가 저도 모르게 흠칫 물러설 정도다."], ["It is Mr. Tulkinghorn’s case at present, though Mademoiselle Hortense, with her eyes almost shut up (but still looking out sideways), is only smiling contemptuously and shaking her head.", "지금 토킹혼 씨가 바로 그 꼴인데, 정작 오르탕스 양은 눈을 거의 감다시피 하고서(여전히 곁눈질로 노려보면서) 그저 경멸하듯 웃으며 고개를 절레절레 흔들 뿐이다."]], [["“Now, mistress,”", "\"자, 마님.\""], ["says the lawyer, tapping the key hastily upon the chimney-piece.", "변호사가 벽난로 선반에 열쇠를 황급히 톡톡 두드리며 말한다."], ["“If you have anything to say, say it, say it.”", "\"할 말이 있거든 하게, 어서 하라고.\""]], [["“Sir, you have not use me well.", "\"선생님, 당신은 나를 함부로 대했어요."], ["You have been mean and shabby.”", "비열하고 추잡하게 굴었다고요.\""]], [["“Mean and shabby, eh?”", "\"비열하고 추잡하게, 그래?\""], ["returns the lawyer, rubbing his nose with the key.", "변호사가 열쇠로 코를 문지르며 대꾸한다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["What is it that I tell you?", "내가 무슨 말 하는지 알잖아요?"], ["You know you have.", "당신도 그랬다는 거 알잖아요."], ["You have attrapped me—catched me—to give you information; you have asked me to show you the dress of mine my Lady must have wore that night, you have prayed me to come in it here to meet that boy.", "당신은 나를 옭아맸어요—꾀어냈다고요—정보를 얻어내려고. 그날 밤 마님이 입었을 게 분명한 내 옷을 보여달라고 했고, 그 옷을 입고 여기 와서 그 소년을 만나달라고 빌었잖아요."], ["Say! Is it not?”", "말해봐요!"], ["Mademoiselle Hortense makes another spring.", "안 그래요?\" 오르탕스 양이 또 한 번 몸을 날릴 듯 덤빈다."]], [["“You are a vixen, a vixen!” Mr.", "\"독한 계집, 독한 계집이로군!\""], ["Tulkinghorn seems to meditate as he looks distrustfully at her, then he replies, “Well, wench, well.", "토킹혼 씨는 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 곰곰이 따져보는 듯하다가 대답한다. \"그래, 계집, 그래."], ["I paid you.”", "그 값은 치렀잖나.\""]], [["“You paid me!”", "\"값을 치렀다고요!\""], ["she repeats with fierce disdain.", "그녀가 사나운 멸시를 담아 되받는다."], ["“Two sovereign!", "\"고작 이 소버린!"], ["I have not change them, I re-fuse them, I des-pise them, I throw them from me!”", "거슬러 받지도 않았고, 거절했고, 경멸하고, 내던져버렸어요!\""], ["Which she literally does, taking them out of her bosom as she speaks and flinging them with such violence on the floor that they jerk up again into the light before they roll away into corners and slowly settle down there after spinning vehemently.", "그러면서 그녀는 말하는 그대로 가슴팍에서 동전을 꺼내 바닥에 얼마나 거세게 내던지는지, 동전이 촛불 빛 속으로 다시 튀어 올랐다가 구석으로 굴러가 한참을 격하게 빙글빙글 돌다 천천히 멈춘다."]], [["“Now!”", "\"자!\""], ["says Mademoiselle Hortense, darkening her large eyes again.", "오르탕스 양이 큰 눈을 다시 흐리며 말한다. \"당신이 값을 치렀다고요?"], ["“You have paid me? Eh, my God, oh yes!”", "어머, 세상에, 오 그래요!\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn rubs his head with the key while she entertains herself with a sarcastic laugh.", "그녀가 비웃듯 한바탕 웃어대는 동안 토킹혼 씨는 열쇠로 머리를 긁적인다."]], [["“You must be rich, my fair friend,”", "\"부자인가 보군, 곱디고운 친구여.\""], ["he composedly observes, “to throw money about in that way!”", "그가 침착하게 말한다. \"돈을 그런 식으로 내던지는 걸 보니!\""]], [["“I AM rich,”", "\"부자 맞아요.\""], ["she returns.", "그녀가 대꾸한다."], ["“I am very rich in hate.", "\"증오라면 아주 차고 넘치게 부자죠."], ["I hate my Lady, of all my heart.", "나는 마님을 미워해요, 진심으로 온 마음을 다해서."], ["You know that.”", "당신도 알잖아요.\""]], [["“Know it?", "\"안다고?"], ["How should I know it?”", "내가 그걸 어찌 알겠나?\""]], [["“Because you have known it perfectly before you prayed me to give you that information.", "\"그 정보를 얻으려고 나한테 빌기 전부터 당신은 그걸 빤히 알고 있었으니까요."], ["Because you have known perfectly that I was en-r-r-r-raged!” It appears impossible for mademoiselle to roll the letter “r”", "내가 펄펄 끓어오를 만큼 화났다는 걸 빤히 알고 있었으니까요!\""], ["sufficiently in this word, notwithstanding that she assists her energetic delivery by clenching both her hands and setting all her teeth.", "오르탕스 양은 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 이를 악물어 격렬한 전달을 돕건만, 이 단어에서 'ㄹ' 소리를 충분히 굴리기란 도무지 불가능해 보인다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I knew that, did I?” says Mr.", "내가 그걸 알았다고?\""], ["Tulkinghorn, examining the wards of the key.", "토킹혼 씨가 열쇠의 톱니를 살피며 말한다."]], [["“Yes, without doubt.", "\"그래요, 의심할 여지 없이."], ["I am not blind.", "나는 눈먼 사람이 아니에요."], ["You have made sure of me because you knew that. You had reason!", "그걸 알았으니까 당신은 나를 손에 넣은 거예요."], ["I det-est her.”", "그럴 만했죠!"], ["Mademoiselle Hortense folds her arms and throws this last remark at him over one of her shoulders.", "나는 그 여자가 진절머리 나게 싫어요.\" 오르탕스 양은 팔짱을 끼고 어깨 너머로 이 마지막 말을 그에게 던진다."]], [["“Having said this, have you anything else to say, mademoiselle?”", "\"그 말을 했으니, 또 할 말이 더 있나, 아가씨?\""]], [["“I am not yet placed. Place me well.", "\"나는 아직 자리를 못 잡았어요."], ["Find me a good condition!", "좋은 자리를 마련해줘요."], ["If you cannot, or do not choose to do that, employ me to pursue her, to chase her, to disgrace and to dishonour her.", "번듯한 처지를 찾아달라고요! 그게 안 되거나 그럴 마음이 없거든, 나를 시켜 그 여자를 쫓고, 추적하고, 망신 주고, 모욕하게 해요."], ["I will help you well, and with a good will.", "내가 톡톡히, 기꺼이 도와줄게요."], ["It is what YOU do.", "당신이 하는 일이 그런 거잖아요."], ["Do I not know that?”", "내가 그걸 모를 줄 알아요?\""]], [["“You appear to know a good deal,”", "\"꽤 많이 아는 것 같군.\""], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn retorts.", "토킹혼 씨가 받아친다."]], [["“Do I not?", "\"안 그래요?"], ["Is it that I am so weak as to believe, like a child, that I come here in that dress to rec-eive that boy only to decide a little bet, a wager? Eh, my God, oh yes!”", "내가 그 옷을 입고 여기 와서 그 소년을 만난 게 고작 사소한 내기, 노름을 가리려고 한 짓이라고, 어린애처럼 믿을 만큼 내가 그렇게 어리석을까요?"], ["In this reply, down to the word “wager”", "어머, 세상에, 오 그래요!\""], ["inclusive, mademoiselle has been ironically polite and tender, then as suddenly dashed into the bitterest and most defiant scorn, with her black eyes in one and the same moment very nearly shut and staringly wide open.", "이 대답에서 '노름'이라는 말까지는 그녀가 반어적으로 정중하고 다정했다가, 곧장 더없이 신랄하고 도전적인 경멸로 돌변하는데, 검은 두 눈이 한순간에 거의 감기는 동시에 휘둥그레 부릅떠진다."]], [["“Now, let us see,” says Mr.", "\"자, 어디 보세.\""], ["Tulkinghorn, tapping his chin with the key and looking imperturbably at her, “how this matter stands.”", "토킹혼 씨가 열쇠로 턱을 톡톡 두드리며 태연히 그녀를 바라보고 말한다. \"이 일이 어떻게 돌아가고 있는지.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Let us see,”", "어디 봐요.\""], ["mademoiselle assents, with many angry and tight nods of her head.", "오르탕스 양이 성난 듯 야무지게 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이며 맞장구친다."]], [["“You come here to make a remarkably modest demand, which you have just stated, and it not being conceded, you will come again.”", "\"자네는 여기 와서 방금 말한 대로 놀랄 만큼 소박한 요구를 하고, 그게 받아들여지지 않으니 또 오겠다 이거지.\""]], [["“And again,”", "\"또 오고말고요.\""], ["says mademoiselle with more tight and angry nods.", "오르탕스 양이 한층 더 야무지고 성난 끄덕임으로 말한다."], ["“And yet again.", "\"또 오고."], ["And yet again.", "또 오고."], ["And many times again.", "또 몇 번이고 오죠."], ["In effect, for ever!”", "사실은, 영원히!\""]], [["“And not only here, but you will go to Mr. Snagsby’s too, perhaps?", "\"여기뿐 아니라 스내그스비 씨네도 가겠다는 거고, 아마?"], ["That visit not succeeding either, you will go again perhaps?”", "그 방문도 성공하지 못하면 또 가겠지, 아마?\""]], [["“And again,”", "\"또 가죠.\""], ["repeats mademoiselle, cataleptic with determination.", "오르탕스 양이 결의로 굳어진 채 되풀이한다."], ["“And yet again.", "\"또 가고."], ["And yet again.", "또 가고."], ["And many times again.", "또 몇 번이고 가죠."], ["In effect, for ever!”", "사실은, 영원히!\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아."], ["Now, Mademoiselle Hortense, let me recommend you to take the candle and pick up that money of yours.", "자, 오르탕스 양, 한 가지 권하지. 촛불을 들고 자네 돈을 주워가게."], ["I think you will find it behind the clerk’s partition in the corner yonder.”", "저쪽 구석 서기 칸막이 뒤에 있을 거야.\""]], [["She merely throws a laugh over her shoulder and stands her ground with folded arms.", "그녀는 그저 어깨 너머로 웃음을 던지고, 팔짱을 낀 채 그 자리에 버티고 선다."]], [["“You will not, eh?”", "\"안 줍겠다, 그거지?\""]], [["“No, I will not!”", "\"네, 안 주워요!\""]], [["“So much the poorer you; so much the richer I!", "\"그럴수록 자네는 더 가난해지고, 나는 더 부자가 되지!"], ["Look, mistress, this is the key of my wine-cellar.", "보게, 마님, 이게 내 포도주 저장실 열쇠야."], ["It is a large key, but the keys of prisons are larger.", "큰 열쇠지만, 감옥 열쇠는 더 크지."], ["In this city there are houses of correction (where the treadmills are, for women), the gates of which are very strong and heavy, and no doubt the keys too.", "이 도시에는 교화소가 있어서(여자들이 쓰는 디딜방아가 그곳에 있지), 그 문이 무척 튼튼하고 묵직한데, 틀림없이 열쇠도 그렇겠지."], ["I am afraid a lady of your spirit and activity would find it an inconvenience to have one of those keys turned upon her for any length of time.", "자네처럼 기개 넘치고 활발한 숙녀가 그런 열쇠를 한참 동안 등 뒤에서 잠그고 지내려면 꽤 불편할 거야."], ["What do you think?”", "어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["“I think,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["mademoiselle replies without any action and in a clear, obliging voice, “that you are a miserable wretch.”", "오르탕스 양이 아무런 동작 없이 또랑또랑하고 상냥한 목소리로 대답한다. \"당신은 비참하고 비열한 인간이에요.\""]], [["“Probably,” returns Mr.", "\"아마 그렇겠지.\""], ["Tulkinghorn, quietly blowing his nose.", "토킹혼 씨가 조용히 코를 풀며 대꾸한다."], ["“But I don’t ask what you think of myself; I ask what you think of the prison.”", "\"하지만 나는 자네가 나를 어찌 생각하는지 묻는 게 아니야. 그 감옥을 어찌 생각하는지 묻는 거지.\""]], [["“Nothing.", "\"아무 생각 없어요."], ["What does it matter to me?”", "그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠?\""]], [["“Why, it matters this much, mistress,”", "\"왜, 이만큼 상관있지, 마님.\""], ["says the lawyer, deliberately putting away his handkerchief and adjusting his frill; “the law is so despotic here that it interferes to prevent any of our good English citizens from being troubled, even by a lady’s visits against his desire.", "변호사가 손수건을 천천히 치우고 가슴 주름 장식을 매만지며 말한다. \"이 나라 법은 어찌나 강압적인지, 우리 선량한 영국 시민이 원치 않는데도 숙녀의 방문 따위로 시달리는 걸 막으려고 끼어들거든."], ["And on his complaining that he is so troubled, it takes hold of the troublesome lady and shuts her up in prison under hard discipline.", "그리고 그 시민이 그렇게 시달린다고 호소하면, 법은 그 성가신 숙녀를 붙잡아 엄한 규율 아래 감옥에 가둬버려."], ["Turns the key upon her, mistress.”", "그 여자한테 열쇠를 채우는 거지, 마님.\""], ["Illustrating with the cellar-key.", "저장실 열쇠로 그 시늉을 해 보인다."]], [["“Truly?”", "\"정말요?\""], ["returns mademoiselle in the same pleasant voice.", "오르탕스 양이 여전히 상냥한 목소리로 받는다."], ["“That is droll!", "\"거참 우습네요!"], ["But—my faith!—still what does it matter to me?”", "하지만—어머나!—그래도 그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠?\""]], [["“My fair friend,” says Mr.", "\"곱디고운 친구여,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, “make another visit here, or at Mr.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"여기든 스내그스비 씨네든 한 번만 더 찾아와보게."], ["Snagsby’s, and you shall learn.”", "그럼 알게 될 테니.\""]], [["“In that case you will send me to the prison, perhaps?”", "\"그럼 당신이 나를 감옥에 보내겠다, 그거예요, 아마?\""]], [["“Perhaps.”", "\"아마도.\""]], [["It would be contradictory for one in mademoiselle’s state of agreeable jocularity to foam at the mouth, otherwise a tigerish expansion thereabouts might look as if a very little more would make her do it.", "오르탕스 양처럼 흐뭇하게 농을 즐기는 처지에 입에 거품을 무는 건 앞뒤가 안 맞는 일이겠으나, 그게 아니라면 그 언저리에 번지는 호랑이 같은 표정으로 보아 조금만 더 건드리면 정말 그럴 듯도 하다."]], [["“In a word, mistress,” says Mr.", "\"한마디로, 마님,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, “I am sorry to be unpolite, but if you ever present yourself uninvited here—or there—again, I will give you over to the police.", "토킹혼 씨가 말한다. \"무례해서 미안하네만, 자네가 부르지도 않았는데 여기—혹은 거기—다시 나타나는 날엔, 나는 자네를 경찰에 넘기겠어."], ["Their gallantry is great, but they carry troublesome people through the streets in an ignominious manner, strapped down on a board, my good wench.”", "경찰이 워낙 정중하긴 하지만, 성가신 자들을 판자에 끈으로 묶어 거리로 끌고 다니는 게 그 꼴이 영 볼품없거든, 착한 계집아.\""]], [["“I will prove you,”", "\"어디 두고 봐요.\""], ["whispers mademoiselle, stretching out her hand, “I will try if you dare to do it!”", "오르탕스 양이 손을 뻗으며 속삭인다. \"당신이 정말 그럴 배짱이 있는지 시험해보겠어요!\""]], [["“And if,”", "\"그리고 만일,\""], ["pursues the lawyer without minding her, “I place you in that good condition of being locked up in jail, it will be some time before you find yourself at liberty again.”", "변호사가 그녀에게 아랑곳없이 말을 잇는다. \"내가 자네를 감옥에 갇히는 그 번듯한 처지에 앉혀준다면, 다시 자유의 몸이 되기까진 꽤 오래 걸릴 거야.\""]], [["“I will prove you,”", "\"어디 두고 봐요.\""], ["repeats mademoiselle in her former whisper.", "오르탕스 양이 아까처럼 속삭여 되풀이한다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"그러니 이제,\""], ["proceeds the lawyer, still without minding her, “you had better go.", "변호사가 여전히 아랑곳없이 이어간다. \"그만 가는 게 좋겠어."], ["Think twice before you come here again.”", "여기 다시 오기 전에 두 번 생각하라고.\""]], [["“Think you,”", "\"두 번이라고요.\""], ["she answers, “twice two hundred times!”", "그녀가 대답한다. \"두 곱하기 이백 번을 생각해도 모자라요!\""]], [["“You were dismissed by your lady, you know,” Mr.", "\"자네 마님이 자네를 내쫓았지, 알다시피.\""], ["Tulkinghorn observes, following her out upon the staircase, “as the most implacable and unmanageable of women.", "토킹혼 씨가 계단으로 그녀를 따라 나가며 말한다. \"여자 중에서 가장 무자비하고 가장 다루기 힘든 여자라고 말이야."], ["Now turn over a new leaf and take warning by what I say to you.", "이제 마음을 고쳐먹고 내 말을 새겨듣게."], ["For what I say, I mean; and what I threaten, I will do, mistress.”", "내가 한 말은 진심이고, 내가 위협한 건 반드시 행하니까, 마님.\""]], [["She goes down without answering or looking behind her.", "그녀는 대답도 없이, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 내려간다."], ["When she is gone, he goes down too, and returning with his cobweb-covered bottle, devotes himself to a leisurely enjoyment of its contents, now and then, as he throws his head back in his chair, catching sight of the pertinacious Roman pointing from the ceiling.", "그녀가 떠나자 그도 내려가, 거미줄로 뒤덮인 술병을 들고 돌아와, 그 안의 내용물을 느긋이 음미하는 데 몰두한다. 이따금 의자에 머리를 젖힐 때면, 천장에서 손가락질하는 그 집요한 로마인이 눈에 들어온다."]], [["Esther’s Narrative\nIt matters little now how much I thought of my living mother who had told me evermore to consider her dead.", "이제 와서, 나에게 늘 죽은 사람으로 여기라고 일렀던 살아 있는 어머니를 내가 얼마나 자주 생각했는지는 그리 중요하지 않습니다."], ["I could not venture to approach her or to communicate with her in writing, for my sense of the peril in which her life was passed was only to be equalled by my fears of increasing it.", "나는 감히 어머니에게 다가가거나 편지로 연락을 취할 엄두를 내지 못했으니, 어머니의 목숨이 처한 위험에 대한 내 감각은 오로지 그 위험을 더 키우게 될까 하는 두려움과 맞먹을 뿐이었기 때문입니다."], ["Knowing that my mere existence as a living creature was an unforeseen danger in her way, I could not always conquer that terror of myself which had seized me when I first knew the secret.", "살아 숨 쉬는 한 생명체로서 내가 존재한다는 사실 자체가 어머니 앞길에 예기치 못한 위험이라는 것을 알았기에, 그 비밀을 처음 알았을 때 나를 사로잡았던 나 자신에 대한 공포를 나는 늘 이겨내지는 못했습니다."], ["At no time did I dare to utter her name.", "어느 때든 나는 감히 어머니의 이름을 입에 올리지 못했습니다."], ["I felt as if I did not even dare to hear it.", "심지어 그 이름을 듣는 것조차 감히 못 할 것만 같았습니다."], ["If the conversation anywhere, when I was present, took that direction, as it sometimes naturally did, I tried not to hear: I mentally counted, repeated something that I knew, or went out of the room.", "내가 있는 자리에서 어디든 대화가 그쪽으로 흘러가면, 때로 자연스레 그러기도 했는데, 나는 듣지 않으려 애썼습니다. 속으로 수를 세거나, 아는 무언가를 되뇌거나, 방을 나가버렸습니다."], ["I am conscious now that I often did these things when there can have been no danger of her being spoken of, but I did them in the dread I had of hearing anything that might lead to her betrayal, and to her betrayal through me.", "지금 와 생각하면 어머니가 화제에 오를 위험이 전혀 없었을 때조차 나는 종종 이런 짓을 했지만, 어머니를 누설로 이끌 만한, 그것도 나를 통한 누설로 이끌 만한 무언가를 듣게 될까 하는 두려움에 그리한 것입니다."]], [["It matters little now how often I recalled the tones of my mother’s voice, wondered whether I should ever hear it again as I so longed to do, and thought how strange and desolate it was that it should be so new to me.", "이제 와서, 내가 얼마나 자주 어머니 목소리의 가락을 떠올렸는지, 그토록 간절히 바라던 대로 그 목소리를 다시 들을 날이 올까 궁금해했는지, 그리고 그것이 나에게 이토록 낯설다는 것이 얼마나 이상하고 쓸쓸한 일인지 생각했는지는 그리 중요하지 않습니다."], ["It matters little that I watched for every public mention of my mother’s name; that I passed and repassed the door of her house in town, loving it, but afraid to look at it; that I once sat in the theatre when my mother was there and saw me, and when we were so wide asunder before the great company of all degrees that any link or confidence between us seemed a dream.", "내가 어머니의 이름이 세상에 거론될 때마다 귀를 곤두세웠다는 것, 도시에 있는 어머니의 집 문 앞을 사랑하는 마음으로 오가면서도 차마 쳐다보지 못했다는 것, 한번은 극장에 앉아 있는데 마침 거기 있던 어머니가 나를 보았고, 온갖 신분의 거대한 무리 앞에서 우리가 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어 우리 사이의 어떤 연결이나 신뢰도 한낱 꿈만 같았다는 것, 이런 것들도 그리 중요하지 않습니다."], ["It is all, all over.", "그것은 다, 다 지나간 일입니다."], ["My lot has been so blest that I can relate little of myself which is not a story of goodness and generosity in others.", "내 운명은 너무도 큰 축복을 받아서, 나 자신에 관해 들려줄 이야기 가운데 남의 선함과 너그러움에 관한 이야기가 아닌 것이 거의 없을 정도입니다."], ["I may well pass that little and go on.", "그러니 그 자그마한 부분은 그냥 지나치고 이야기를 계속하는 것이 좋겠습니다."]], [["When we were settled at home again, Ada and I had many conversations with my guardian of which Richard was the theme.", "우리가 다시 집에 자리를 잡고 나서, 에이다와 나는 후견인과 여러 차례 대화를 나누었는데 그 주제는 리처드였습니다."], ["My dear girl was deeply grieved that he should do their kind cousin so much wrong, but she was so faithful to Richard that she could not bear to blame him even for that.", "사랑하는 내 친구는 리처드가 친절한 사촌에게 그토록 큰 부당함을 저지른다는 사실에 깊이 마음 아파했지만, 워낙 리처드에게 충실한지라 그 일로조차 그를 탓하는 것을 견디지 못했습니다."], ["My guardian was assured of it, and never coupled his name with a word of reproof.", "후견인도 그것을 잘 알았기에, 리처드의 이름을 단 한마디 질책과도 엮지 않았습니다."], ["“Rick is mistaken, my dear,”", "\"릭이 잘못 생각하는 거란다, 얘야.\""], ["he would say to her. “Well, well!", "후견인은 에이다에게 이렇게 말하곤 했습니다."], ["We have all been mistaken over and over again.", "\"괜찮아, 괜찮아! 우리도 다들 몇 번이고 거듭 잘못 생각해왔으니까."], ["We must trust to you and time to set him right.”", "너와 세월에 맡기고 그 애가 바로잡히기를 기다려야지.\""]], [["We knew afterwards what we suspected then, that he did not trust to time until he had often tried to open Richard’s eyes.", "당시 우리가 짐작만 하던 것을 나중에 알게 되었는데, 후견인은 리처드의 눈을 뜨게 하려고 여러 번 애쓰기 전까지는 세월에 맡기지 않았다는 사실입니다."], ["That he had written to him, gone to him, talked with him, tried every gentle and persuasive art his kindness could devise.", "후견인은 리처드에게 편지를 쓰고, 찾아가고, 이야기를 나누고, 그 친절한 마음이 궁리해낼 수 있는 온갖 부드럽고 설득력 있는 방법을 다 써보았습니다."], ["Our poor devoted Richard was deaf and blind to all.", "가엾도록 외곬으로 빠진 우리 리처드는 그 모든 것에 귀먹고 눈멀었습니다."], ["If he were wrong, he would make amends when the Chancery suit was over.", "자기가 틀렸다면 대법원 소송이 끝난 뒤에 보상하겠다는 것이었습니다."], ["If he were groping in the dark, he could not do better than do his utmost to clear away those clouds in which so much was confused and obscured.", "자기가 어둠 속을 더듬고 있다면, 그토록 많은 것이 뒤엉키고 흐려진 그 구름을 걷어내려 온 힘을 다하는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없다는 것이었습니다."], ["Suspicion and misunderstanding were the fault of the suit?", "의심과 오해가 소송의 잘못이라고?"], ["Then let him work the suit out and come through it to his right mind.", "그렇다면 소송을 끝까지 밀어붙여 그것을 통과해 제정신으로 돌아오게 하면 된다는 것이었습니다."], ["This was his unvarying reply.", "이것이 리처드의 한결같은 대답이었습니다."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce had obtained such possession of his whole nature that it was impossible to place any consideration before him which he did not, with a distorted kind of reason, make a new argument in favour of his doing what he did.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송이 그의 본성 전체를 어찌나 단단히 사로잡았는지, 그 앞에 어떤 고려 사항을 내놓아도 그는 비뚤어진 논리로 그것을 자기가 하는 일을 정당화하는 새로운 논거로 바꿔버리니, 도무지 어쩔 도리가 없었습니다."], ["“So that it is even more mischievous,”", "\"그러니 그 가엾은 친구에게 따져 묻는 것이,\""], ["said my guardian once to me, “to remonstrate with the poor dear fellow than to leave him alone.”", "한번은 후견인이 나에게 이렇게 말했습니다. \"그냥 내버려두는 것보다 오히려 더 해롭단 말이지.\""]], [["I took one of these opportunities of mentioning my doubts of Mr. Skimpole as a good adviser for Richard.", "나는 이런 기회를 빌려, 스킴폴 씨가 리처드에게 좋은 조언자가 될 수 있을지 내 의구심을 털어놓았습니다."]], [["“Adviser!”", "\"조언자!\""], ["returned my guardian, laughing, “My dear, who would advise with Skimpole?”", "후견인이 웃으며 받았습니다. \"얘야, 스킴폴한테 누가 조언을 구하겠니?\""]], [["“Encourager would perhaps have been a better word,”", "\"부추기는 사람이라는 말이 더 나았을지도 모르겠네요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Encourager!”", "\"부추기는 사람이라!\""], ["returned my guardian again.", "후견인이 다시 받았습니다."], ["“Who could be encouraged by Skimpole?”", "\"스킴폴한테 부추김을 받을 사람이 누가 있겠니?\""]], [["“Not Richard?”", "\"리처드는 아니란 말씀인가요?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“No,” he replied.", "\"그래.\""], ["“Such an unworldly, uncalculating, gossamer creature is a relief to him and an amusement.", "후견인이 대답했습니다. \"그렇게 세상 물정 모르고, 셈할 줄 모르고, 거미줄처럼 허허로운 인간은 그 애한테 위안이고 즐길 거리지."], ["But as to advising or encouraging or occupying a serious station towards anybody or anything, it is simply not to be thought of in such a child as Skimpole.”", "하지만 누군가에게, 혹은 무언가에 대해 조언을 하거나 부추기거나 진지한 자리를 맡는다는 건, 스킴폴 같은 어린애한테는 도무지 생각조차 할 수 없는 일이란다.\""]], [["“Pray, cousin John,”", "\"저, 존 사촌 오라버니.\""], ["said Ada, who had just joined us and now looked over my shoulder, “what made him such a child?”", "마침 우리 곁에 와서 내 어깨너머로 들여다보던 에이다가 말했습니다. \"그분은 왜 그렇게 어린애가 됐을까요?\""]], [["“What made him such a child?”", "\"왜 그렇게 어린애가 됐느냐고?\""], ["inquired my guardian, rubbing his head, a little at a loss.", "후견인이 머리를 문지르며, 살짝 난감해하며 되물었습니다."]], [["“Yes, cousin John.”", "\"네, 존 사촌 오라버니.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"글쎄,\" 후견인은 머리를 점점 더 헝클어뜨리며 천천히 대답했습니다."], ["he slowly replied, roughening his head more and more, “he is all sentiment, and—and susceptibility, and—and sensibility, and—and imagination.", "\"그 친구는 온통 감상이고, 그리고… 감수성이고, 그리고…"], ["And these qualities are not regulated in him, somehow.", "정서고, 그리고… 상상력이야."], ["I suppose the people who admired him for them in his youth attached too much importance to them and too little to any training that would have balanced and adjusted them, and so he became what he is.", "그런데 그런 자질이 어쩐 일인지 그 안에서 조절이 안 돼. 젊은 시절에 그를 그 자질로 칭찬하던 사람들이 그것에 너무 큰 가치를 두고, 그 자질을 균형 있게 다듬어줄 어떤 훈련에는 너무 적은 가치를 둔 모양이라, 그래서 그가 지금 같은 사람이 된 거겠지."], ["Hey?”", "응?\""], ["said my guardian, stopping short and looking at us hopefully.", "후견인은 말을 뚝 멈추고 기대에 차서 우리를 보았습니다."], ["“What do you think, you two?”", "\"너희 둘은 어떻게 생각하니?\""]], [["Ada, glancing at me, said she thought it was a pity he should be an expense to Richard.", "에이다는 나를 흘끗 보고는, 그가 리처드에게 비용 부담이 되는 것이 안타깝다고 생각한다고 말했습니다."]], [["“So it is, so it is,”", "\"그래, 그렇지, 그렇고말고.\""], ["returned my guardian hurriedly.", "후견인이 황급히 받았습니다."], ["“That must not be.", "\"그래선 안 되지."], ["We must arrange that.", "그건 우리가 처리해야 해."], ["I must prevent it.", "내가 막아야겠어."], ["That will never do.”", "그건 절대 안 될 일이야.\""]], [["And I said I thought it was to be regretted that he had ever introduced Richard to Mr. Vholes for a present of five pounds.", "그리고 나는, 그가 오 파운드를 얻으려고 리처드를 볼스 씨에게 소개한 일이 유감스럽다고 생각한다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Did he?”", "\"그가 그랬다고?\""], ["said my guardian with a passing shade of vexation on his face.", "후견인은 얼굴에 잠깐 언짢은 기색을 스치며 말했습니다."], ["“But there you have the man.", "\"하지만 그게 바로 그 사람이야."], ["There you have the man!", "그게 바로 그 사람이라고!"], ["There is nothing mercenary in that with him.", "그 친구한테 그건 돈 욕심하고는 아무 상관 없는 일이야."], ["He has no idea of the value of money.", "그는 돈의 가치라는 걸 몰라."], ["He introduces Rick, and then he is good friends with Mr. Vholes and borrows five pounds of him.", "릭을 소개해주고는, 그러고 나서 볼스 씨와 좋은 친구가 돼서 그에게 오 파운드를 빌리는 거지."], ["He means nothing by it and thinks nothing of it.", "거기엔 아무 속셈도 없고, 대수롭게 여기지도 않아."], ["He told you himself, I’ll be bound, my dear?”", "분명 그 친구가 너한테 직접 그 얘길 했겠지, 얘야?\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 네!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Exactly!”", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["cried my guardian, quite triumphant.", "후견인이 자못 의기양양하게 외쳤습니다."], ["“There you have the man!", "\"그게 바로 그 사람이라니까!"], ["If he had meant any harm by it or was conscious of any harm in it, he wouldn’t tell it.", "거기에 무슨 해를 끼칠 속셈이 있었거나 그 안에 어떤 해가 있다는 걸 의식했다면, 그는 말하지 않았을 거야."], ["He tells it as he does it in mere simplicity.", "그는 그저 천진하게, 행동하는 그대로 말하는 거지."], ["But you shall see him in his own home, and then you’ll understand him better.", "하지만 너희가 그를 그의 집에서 보면, 그땐 그를 더 잘 이해하게 될 거야."], ["We must pay a visit to Harold Skimpole and caution him on these points.", "해럴드 스킴폴을 찾아가서 이런 점들을 주의시켜야겠다."], ["Lord bless you, my dears, an infant, an infant!”", "어이구 얘들아, 갓난애야, 갓난애!\""]], [["In pursuance of this plan, we went into London on an early day and presented ourselves at Mr. Skimpole’s door.", "이 계획에 따라, 우리는 어느 이른 날 런던으로 들어가 스킴폴 씨의 집 앞에 나타났습니다."]], [["He lived in a place called the Polygon, in Somers Town, where there were at that time a number of poor Spanish refugees walking about in cloaks, smoking little paper cigars.", "그는 서머스 타운의 폴리곤이라는 곳에 살았는데, 그 무렵 거기엔 가난한 스페인 망명객 여럿이 망토를 두르고 작은 종이 궐련을 피우며 돌아다녔습니다."], ["Whether he was a better tenant than one might have supposed, in consequence of his friend Somebody always paying his rent at last, or whether his inaptitude for business rendered it particularly difficult to turn him out, I don’t know; but he had occupied the same house some years.", "그의 친구 누군가가 늘 끝에 가서는 집세를 대신 내준 덕에 그가 생각보다 나은 세입자였던 것인지, 아니면 사업에 도무지 서툰 탓에 그를 내쫓기가 유난히 어려웠던 것인지는 모르겠으나, 어쨌든 그는 같은 집에서 몇 해를 살고 있었습니다."], ["It was in a state of dilapidation quite equal to our expectation.", "집은 우리 예상에 꼭 들어맞게 다 허물어져가는 꼴이었습니다."], ["Two or three of the area railings were gone, the water-butt was broken, the knocker was loose, the bell-handle had been pulled off a long time to judge from the rusty state of the wire, and dirty footprints on the steps were the only signs of its being inhabited.", "지하 채광창 난간 두세 개가 사라졌고, 빗물통은 깨졌고, 문고리는 헐거웠고, 초인종 손잡이는 철사가 녹슨 상태로 보아 오래전에 뜯겨나갔으며, 계단에 찍힌 더러운 발자국만이 사람이 산다는 유일한 표시였습니다."]], [["A slatternly full-blown girl who seemed to be bursting out at the rents in her gown and the cracks in her shoes like an over-ripe berry answered our knock by opening the door a very little way and stopping up the gap with her figure.", "지나치게 익은 산딸기처럼 옷의 터진 틈과 신발의 갈라진 곳으로 살이 비어져 나올 것 같은, 칠칠치 못하게 푸짐한 처녀가 문을 아주 조금만 열고 그 틈을 제 몸으로 틀어막은 채 우리 노크에 응했습니다."], ["As she knew Mr. Jarndyce (indeed Ada and I both thought that she evidently associated him with the receipt of her wages), she immediately relented and allowed us to pass in.", "그 처녀는 잔다이스 씨를 알아보았기에(사실 에이다와 나는 둘 다, 처녀가 분명 그를 제 품삯을 받는 일과 연관 짓는다고 생각했습니다), 곧바로 누그러져 우리를 안으로 들였습니다."], ["The lock of the door being in a disabled condition, she then applied herself to securing it with the chain, which was not in good action either, and said would we go upstairs?", "문의 자물쇠가 망가진 상태라, 처녀는 그제야 사슬로 문을 잠그려 들었는데 그 사슬도 영 신통치 않았고, 위층으로 올라가시겠느냐고 물었습니다."]], [["We went upstairs to the first floor, still seeing no other furniture than the dirty footprints.", "우리는 이층으로 올라갔는데, 여전히 더러운 발자국 말고는 다른 가구라고는 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Mr. Jarndyce without further ceremony entered a room there, and we followed.", "잔다이스 씨는 더 격식 차릴 것도 없이 거기 있는 방으로 들어갔고, 우리도 뒤따랐습니다."], ["It was dingy enough and not at all clean, but furnished with an odd kind of shabby luxury, with a large footstool, a sofa, and plenty of cushions, an easy-chair, and plenty of pillows, a piano, books, drawing materials, music, newspapers, and a few sketches and pictures.", "방은 꽤 칙칙하고 전혀 깨끗하지 않았지만, 묘하게 초라한 호사로 꾸며져 있었습니다. 커다란 발받침과 소파와 잔뜩 쌓인 방석, 안락의자와 잔뜩 쌓인 베개, 피아노, 책, 그림 도구, 악보, 신문, 그리고 스케치와 그림 몇 점이 있었습니다."], ["A broken pane of glass in one of the dirty windows was papered and wafered over, but there was a little plate of hothouse nectarines on the table, and there was another of grapes, and another of sponge-cakes, and there was a bottle of light wine. Mr.", "더러운 창문 중 하나에 깨진 유리는 종이를 풀로 발라 막아놓았지만, 탁자 위에는 온실에서 키운 천도복숭아가 담긴 작은 접시가 있었고, 포도가 담긴 접시도, 스펀지케이크가 담긴 접시도 또 있었으며, 가벼운 와인 한 병도 있었습니다."], ["Skimpole himself reclined upon the sofa in a dressing-gown, drinking some fragrant coffee from an old china cup—it was then about mid-day—and looking at a collection of wallflowers in the balcony.", "스킴폴 씨는 손수 실내복 차림으로 소파에 비스듬히 기대어, 낡은 도자기 잔에 담긴 향긋한 커피를 마시며—그때가 한낮쯤이었습니다—발코니에 모아둔 계란풀 무더기를 바라보고 있었습니다."]], [["He was not in the least disconcerted by our appearance, but rose and received us in his usual airy manner.", "그는 우리가 나타난 것에 조금도 당황하지 않고, 일어나서 평소의 가뿐한 태도로 우리를 맞았습니다."]], [["“Here I am, you see!”", "\"보다시피, 내가 여기 이러고 있습니다!\""], ["he said when we were seated, not without some little difficulty, the greater part of the chairs being broken.", "우리가 자리에 앉자 그가 말했는데, 의자 대부분이 부서져 있어 앉기가 적잖이 어려웠습니다. \"내가 이러고 있어요!"], ["“Here I am! This is my frugal breakfast.", "이게 내 소박한 아침 식사랍니다."], ["Some men want legs of beef and mutton for breakfast; I don’t.", "어떤 사람들은 아침으로 쇠고기나 양고기 다릿살을 찾지요. 나는 안 그래요."], ["Give me my peach, my cup of coffee, and my claret; I am content.", "복숭아 한 알, 커피 한 잔, 클라레 한 잔이면 나는 만족합니다."], ["I don’t want them for themselves, but they remind me of the sun.", "그것들이 그 자체로 좋아서가 아니라, 그것들이 나에게 태양을 떠올리게 해주니까요."], ["There’s nothing solar about legs of beef and mutton.", "쇠고기나 양고기 다릿살에는 태양 같은 데가 전혀 없어요."], ["Mere animal satisfaction!”", "그저 짐승 같은 만족일 뿐이지요!\""]], [["“This is our friend’s consulting-room (or would be, if he ever prescribed), his sanctum, his studio,”", "\"여기가 우리 친구의 진료실이라네(처방이란 걸 한 적이 있다면 말이지만), 그의 성소이자 작업실이지.\""], ["said my guardian to us.", "후견인이 우리에게 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes,” said Mr.", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["Skimpole, turning his bright face about, “this is the bird’s cage.", "스킴폴 씨가 환한 얼굴을 우리 쪽으로 돌리며 말했습니다. \"여기가 새장입니다."], ["This is where the bird lives and sings.", "새가 여기 살면서 노래하는 곳이지요."], ["They pluck his feathers now and then and clip his wings, but he sings, he sings!”", "사람들이 이따금 새의 깃털을 뽑고 날개를 자르지만, 그래도 새는 노래합니다, 노래해요!\""]], [["He handed us the grapes, repeating in his radiant way, “He sings!", "그는 우리에게 포도를 건네면서 그 빛나는 어조로 거듭 말했습니다. \"노래한답니다!"], ["Not an ambitious note, but still he sings.”", "야심 찬 가락은 아니지만, 그래도 노래해요.\""]], [["“These are very fine,”", "\"아주 좋은 포도로군.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“A present?”", "\"선물인가?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“No!", "\"아니에요!"], ["Some amiable gardener sells them.", "어느 상냥한 정원사가 파는 겁니다. 어젯밤에 그 정원사의 하인이 이걸 가져오면서, 돈을 받아 갈 때까지 기다려야 하느냐고 묻더군요."], ["His man wanted to know, when he brought them last evening, whether he should wait for the money.", "'이보게, 친구,' 내가 말했지요."], ["‘Really, my friend,’ I said, ‘I think not—if your time is of any value to you.’ I suppose it was, for he went away.”", "'안 그러는 게 좋겠네—자네 시간이 자네한테 조금이라도 값어치가 있다면 말이야.' 값어치가 있었던 모양인지, 그는 가버렸어요.\""]], [["My guardian looked at us with a smile, as though he asked us, “Is it possible to be worldly with this baby?”", "후견인은 미소를 띠고 우리를 바라보았는데, 마치 \"이 갓난애를 상대로 세속적일 수가 있겠나?\" 하고 묻는 듯했습니다."]], [["“This is a day,” said Mr.", "\"오늘은,\""], ["Skimpole, gaily taking a little claret in a tumbler, “that will ever be remembered here.", "스킴폴 씨가 유쾌하게 텀블러에 클라레를 조금 따르며 말했습니다. \"이 집에서 길이길이 기억될 날이 될 겁니다."], ["We shall call it Saint Clare and Saint Summerson day.", "우리는 이날을 성 클레어와 성 서머슨의 날이라고 부르겠어요."], ["You must see my daughters.", "내 딸들을 꼭 봐야 합니다."], ["I have a blue-eyed daughter who is my Beauty daughter, I have a Sentiment daughter, and I have a Comedy daughter.", "나에겐 파란 눈을 가진 딸이 있는데 내 미녀 딸이고, 감상 딸이 하나 있고, 희극 딸이 하나 있어요."], ["You must see them all.", "셋 다 봐야 합니다."], ["They’ll be enchanted.”", "다들 황홀해할 거예요.\""]], [["He was going to summon them when my guardian interposed and asked him to pause a moment, as he wished to say a word to him first.", "그가 딸들을 부르려는데 후견인이 가로막으며, 먼저 한마디 할 말이 있으니 잠깐 멈춰달라고 청했습니다."], ["“My dear Jarndyce,”", "\"이보게 잔다이스,\""], ["he cheerfully replied, going back to his sofa, “as many moments as you please.", "그가 소파로 돌아가며 명랑하게 대답했습니다. \"원하는 만큼 얼마든지 멈추지."], ["Time is no object here.", "여기선 시간이 아무 문제가 안 돼."], ["We never know what o’clock it is, and we never care.", "우리는 지금이 몇 시인지도 통 모르고, 신경도 안 쓰니까."], ["Not the way to get on in life, you’ll tell me?", "그게 인생에서 성공하는 길은 아니라고 자네는 말하겠지?"], ["Certainly.", "물론 그래."], ["But we DON’T get on in life.", "하지만 우린 인생에서 성공하지 않아."], ["We don’t pretend to do it.”", "그러는 척도 안 하고.\""]], [["My guardian looked at us again, plainly saying, “You hear him?”", "후견인은 다시 우리를 보았는데, 분명히 \"저 친구 말 들었지?\" 하는 표정이었습니다."]], [["“Now, Harold,”", "\"자, 해럴드,\""], ["he began, “the word I have to say relates to Rick.”", "그가 말을 꺼냈습니다. \"내가 할 말은 릭에 관한 거야.\""]], [["“The dearest friend I have!”", "\"내가 가진 가장 소중한 친구지!\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole cordially.", "스킴폴 씨가 다정하게 받았습니다."], ["“I suppose he ought not to be my dearest friend, as he is not on terms with you.", "\"그 친구가 자네하고 사이가 안 좋으니, 내 가장 소중한 친구여서는 안 될 텐데 말이야."], ["But he is, I can’t help it; he is full of youthful poetry, and I love him.", "하지만 그렇게 됐으니 나도 어쩔 수가 없어. 그 친구는 젊음의 시정으로 가득 차 있고, 나는 그를 사랑하거든."], ["If you don’t like it, I can’t help it.", "자네가 싫어해도 나는 어쩔 수가 없어."], ["I love him.”", "나는 그를 사랑한다네.\""]], [["The engaging frankness with which he made this declaration really had a disinterested appearance and captivated my guardian, if not, for the moment, Ada too.", "이런 선언을 하는 그 매력적인 솔직함에는 정말로 사심 없어 보이는 구석이 있어서, 잠시나마 에이다는 몰라도 후견인의 마음을 사로잡았습니다."]], [["“You are welcome to love him as much as you like,”", "\"자네가 그 애를 얼마든지 사랑하는 건 괜찮네.\""], ["returned Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 받았습니다."], ["Jarndyce, “but we must save his pocket, Harold.”", "\"하지만 우리는 그 애 주머니를 지켜줘야 해, 해럴드.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“His pocket?", "\"그 친구 주머니라고?"], ["Now you are coming to what I don’t understand.”", "이제 내가 통 이해 못 하는 데로 들어가는군.\""], ["Taking a little more claret and dipping one of the cakes in it, he shook his head and smiled at Ada and me with an ingenuous foreboding that he never could be made to understand.", "클라레를 조금 더 따라 케이크 하나를 거기에 적시면서, 그는 고개를 저으며 자기는 도무지 이해할 수 없으리라는 천진한 예감을 품고 에이다와 나를 보며 미소 지었습니다."]], [["“If you go with him here or there,”", "\"자네가 그 애와 함께 여기저기 다니거든,\""], ["said my guardian plainly, “you must not let him pay for both.”", "후견인이 솔직하게 말했습니다. \"그 애가 두 사람 몫을 다 내게 하면 안 돼.\""]], [["“My dear Jarndyce,” returned Mr.", "\"이보게 잔다이스,\""], ["Skimpole, his genial face irradiated by the comicality of this idea, “what am I to do?", "스킴폴 씨가 받았는데, 이 발상이 우스워서 그 너그러운 얼굴이 환히 빛났습니다. \"그럼 나더러 어쩌라는 건가?"], ["If he takes me anywhere, I must go.", "그 친구가 나를 어디로 데려가면, 나는 가야지."], ["And how can I pay?", "그런데 내가 어떻게 돈을 내?"], ["I never have any money.", "나는 돈이라곤 한 푼도 없어."], ["If I had any money, I don’t know anything about it.", "설사 돈이 있다 해도, 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모른다네."], ["Suppose I say to a man, how much?", "어떤 사람한테 내가 얼마요? 하고 묻는다고 쳐."], ["Suppose the man says to me seven and sixpence?", "그 사람이 나한테 칠 실링 육 펜스요, 한다고 쳐."], ["I know nothing about seven and sixpence.", "나는 칠 실링 육 펜스에 대해 아무것도 모른다네."], ["It is impossible for me to pursue the subject with any consideration for the man.", "그 사람을 조금이라도 배려하면서 그 문제를 따져나갈 수가 없어."], ["I don’t go about asking busy people what seven and sixpence is in Moorish—which I don’t understand.", "나는 바쁜 사람들을 붙잡고 칠 실링 육 펜스가 무어인 말로 뭐냐고—내가 모르는 그 무어인 말로—묻고 다니지 않아."], ["Why should I go about asking them what seven and sixpence is in Money—which I don’t understand?”", "그런데 왜 그들을 붙잡고 칠 실링 육 펜스가 돈으로 뭐냐고—내가 모르는 그 돈으로—묻고 다녀야 하지?\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["said my guardian, by no means displeased with this artless reply, “if you come to any kind of journeying with Rick, you must borrow the money of me (never breathing the least allusion to that circumstance), and leave the calculation to him.”", "후견인이 이 천진한 대답에 조금도 언짢아하지 않고 말했습니다. \"자네가 릭과 무슨 여행 비슷한 걸 하게 되거든, 그 돈은 나한테서 빌리고(그 사정은 결코 조금도 입 밖에 내지 말고), 셈은 그 애한테 맡기게.\""]], [["“My dear Jarndyce,” returned Mr.", "\"이보게 잔다이스,\""], ["Skimpole, “I will do anything to give you pleasure, but it seems an idle form—a superstition.", "스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다. \"자네를 기쁘게 하는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠네만, 그건 부질없는 형식—일종의 미신 같군."], ["Besides, I give you my word, Miss Clare and my dear Miss Summerson, I thought Mr. Carstone was immensely rich.", "게다가 내 장담하건대, 클레어 양과 친애하는 서머슨 양, 나는 카스톤 씨가 엄청난 부자인 줄 알았답니다."], ["I thought he had only to make over something, or to sign a bond, or a draft, or a cheque, or a bill, or to put something on a file somewhere, to bring down a shower of money.”", "무언가를 양도하거나, 증서나 어음이나 수표나 환어음에 서명하거나, 어딘가 서류철에 무언가를 끼워 넣기만 하면 돈벼락이 쏟아질 거라고 생각했어요.\""]], [["“Indeed it is not so, sir,”", "\"정말로 그렇지 않답니다, 선생님.\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했습니다."], ["“He is poor.”", "\"그분은 가난해요.\""]], [["“No, really?”", "\"아니, 정말요?\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole with his bright smile.", "스킴폴 씨가 그 환한 미소를 띠고 받았습니다."], ["“You surprise me.”", "\"놀랍군요.\""]], [["“And not being the richer for trusting in a rotten reed,”", "\"썩은 갈대를 믿어봐야 부유해질 리도 없으니,\""], ["said my guardian, laying his hand emphatically on the sleeve of Mr.", "후견인이 스킴폴 씨의 실내복 소매에 힘주어 손을 얹으며 말했습니다."], ["Skimpole’s dressing-gown, “be you very careful not to encourage him in that reliance, Harold.”", "\"그 애가 그것에 기대도록 부추기는 일은 절대 없도록 각별히 조심하게, 해럴드.\""]], [["“My dear good friend,”", "\"이보게 친애하는 벗,\""], ["returned Mr.", "스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다."], ["Skimpole, “and my dear Miss Simmerson, and my dear Miss Clare, how can I do that?", "\"그리고 친애하는 시머슨 양, 친애하는 클레어 양, 내가 어떻게 그럴 수 있겠나?"], ["It’s business, and I don’t know business.", "그건 사업이고, 나는 사업이란 걸 모른다네."], ["It is he who encourages me.", "오히려 나를 부추기는 건 그 친구 쪽이야."], ["He emerges from great feats of business, presents the brightest prospects before me as their result, and calls upon me to admire them.", "그 친구는 대단한 사업적 위업에서 솟아 나와, 그 결과랍시고 더없이 환한 전망을 내 앞에 펼쳐놓고는, 그걸 감탄하라고 나를 부른다네."], ["I do admire them—as bright prospects.", "나는 정말 감탄해—환한 전망이라고 말이지."], ["But I know no more about them, and I tell him so.”", "하지만 나는 그 이상은 아무것도 모르고, 그 친구한테도 그렇게 말한다네.\""]], [["The helpless kind of candour with which he presented this before us, the light-hearted manner in which he was amused by his innocence, the fantastic way in which he took himself under his own protection and argued about that curious person, combined with the delightful ease of everything he said exactly to make out my guardian’s case.", "그가 이것을 우리 앞에 내놓는 그 무력한 솔직함, 자기 천진함에 스스로 재미있어하는 그 가벼운 태도, 자기 자신을 제 보호 아래 두고 그 별난 인물에 대해 따지는 그 기상천외한 방식이, 그가 하는 모든 말의 그 유쾌한 거침없음과 어우러져, 후견인의 주장을 꼭 들어맞게 입증해주었습니다."], ["The more I saw of him, the more unlikely it seemed to me, when he was present, that he could design, conceal, or influence anything; and yet the less likely that appeared when he was not present, and the less agreeable it was to think of his having anything to do with any one for whom I cared.", "그를 보면 볼수록, 그가 있는 자리에서는 그가 무언가를 꾸미거나 감추거나 좌우할 수 있다는 것이 더욱 있을 법하지 않게 여겨졌습니다. 그런데도 그가 없는 자리에서는 그것이 더욱 있을 법하게 여겨졌고, 내가 아끼는 누군가와 그가 어떤 관계를 맺는다는 생각이 더욱 달갑지 않았습니다."]], [["Hearing that his examination (as he called it) was now over, Mr. Skimpole left the room with a radiant face to fetch his daughters (his sons had run away at various times), leaving my guardian quite delighted by the manner in which he had vindicated his childish character.", "자기 심문(그가 그렇게 부른 것입니다)이 이제 끝났다는 말을 듣고, 스킴폴 씨는 환한 얼굴로 딸들을 데리러 방을 나갔는데(아들들은 이런저런 때에 다들 달아나버렸습니다), 후견인은 그가 자기 어린애 같은 성격을 옹호하는 방식에 자못 즐거워하며 남았습니다."], ["He soon came back, bringing with him the three young ladies and Mrs. Skimpole, who had once been a beauty but was now a delicate high-nosed invalid suffering under a complication of disorders.", "그는 곧 세 아가씨와 스킴폴 부인을 데리고 돌아왔는데, 부인은 한때 미인이었으나 이제는 갖가지 병이 겹쳐 시달리는 콧대 높고 가냘픈 환자였습니다."]], [["“This,” said Mr.", "\"이쪽은,\""], ["Skimpole, “is my Beauty daughter, Arethusa—plays and sings odds and ends like her father.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"내 미녀 딸 아레투사인데—제 아비처럼 이것저것 연주하고 노래하지요."], ["This is my Sentiment daughter, Laura—plays a little but don’t sing.", "이쪽은 내 감상 딸 로라인데—조금 연주하지만 노래는 안 해요."], ["This is my Comedy daughter, Kitty—sings a little but don’t play.", "이쪽은 내 희극 딸 키티인데—조금 노래하지만 연주는 안 하고요."], ["We all draw a little and compose a little, and none of us have any idea of time or money.”", "우린 다들 조금씩 그림을 그리고 조금씩 작곡을 하는데, 누구 하나 시간이나 돈에 대한 개념이 없답니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Skimpole sighed, I thought, as if she would have been glad to strike out this item in the family attainments.", "스킴폴 부인은, 내 생각에, 이 가족의 재능 목록에서 그 항목만은 지워버릴 수 있었으면 싶은 듯 한숨을 쉬었습니다."], ["I also thought that she rather impressed her sigh upon my guardian and that she took every opportunity of throwing in another.", "또 내 생각에 부인은 그 한숨을 후견인에게 자못 강하게 들이밀었고, 틈만 나면 한숨을 한 번씩 더 끼워 넣었습니다."]], [["“It is pleasant,” said Mr.", "\"가족마다 특이한 점들을 더듬어보는 건,\""], ["Skimpole, turning his sprightly eyes from one to the other of us, “and it is whimsically interesting to trace peculiarities in families.", "스킴폴 씨가 그 발랄한 눈을 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 옮기며 말했습니다. \"유쾌하기도 하고, 묘하게 흥미롭기도 하지요."], ["In this family we are all children, and I am the youngest.”", "이 가족에선 우리가 다 어린애인데, 내가 제일 어립니다.\""]], [["The daughters, who appeared to be very fond of him, were amused by this droll fact, particularly the Comedy daughter.", "그를 무척 따르는 듯한 딸들은 이 익살스러운 사실에 즐거워했는데, 특히 희극 딸이 그러했습니다."]], [["“My dears, it is true,” said Mr.", "\"얘들아, 그게 사실이잖니,\""], ["Skimpole, “is it not?", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"안 그래?"], ["So it is, and so it must be, because like the dogs in the hymn, ‘it is our nature to.’ Now, here is Miss Summerson with a fine administrative capacity and a knowledge of details perfectly surprising.", "사실이고, 그럴 수밖에 없는 게, 찬송가에 나오는 개들처럼 '그게 우리 본성'이거든. 자, 여기 서머슨 양은 빼어난 살림 솜씨에다 세세한 부분에 대한 지식이 놀라울 만큼 훌륭하지요."], ["It will sound very strange in Miss Summerson’s ears, I dare say, that we know nothing about chops in this house.", "우리가 이 집에서 양고기 토막에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 게 서머슨 양 귀엔 무척 이상하게 들리겠지요."], ["But we don’t, not the least.", "하지만 우린 정말 모른답니다, 손톱만큼도."], ["We can’t cook anything whatever.", "우린 무어든 요리할 줄을 몰라요."], ["A needle and thread we don’t know how to use.", "바늘과 실도 쓸 줄 모르고."], ["We admire the people who possess the practical wisdom we want, but we don’t quarrel with them.", "우린 우리한테 없는 실용적 지혜를 가진 사람들을 우러러보지만, 그들과 다투지는 않아요."], ["Then why should they quarrel with us?", "그런데 왜 그들이 우리와 다퉈야 하지요?"], ["Live and let live, we say to them.", "살고 또 살게 하라, 우린 그들에게 이렇게 말합니다."], ["Live upon your practical wisdom, and let us live upon you!”", "당신들은 실용적 지혜로 살고, 우리는 당신들에 기대어 살게 해달라!\""]], [["He laughed, but as usual seemed quite candid and really to mean what he said.", "그는 웃었지만, 늘 그렇듯 자못 솔직했고 정말로 제 말 그대로를 뜻하는 것 같았습니다."]], [["“We have sympathy, my roses,” said Mr.", "\"우리에겐 공감이 있단다, 내 장미꽃들아,\""], ["Skimpole, “sympathy for everything.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"모든 것에 대한 공감이지."], ["Have we not?”", "안 그러냐?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, papa!”", "\"오, 그럼요, 아빠!\""], ["cried the three daughters.", "세 딸이 외쳤습니다."]], [["“In fact, that is our family department,”", "\"사실 그게 우리 가족이 맡은 부문이지요,\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole, “in this hurly-burly of life.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["We are capable of looking on and of being interested, and we DO look on, and we ARE interested.", "\"이 아수라장 같은 삶 속에서 말입니다. 우리는 지켜보고 관심을 가질 줄 알고, 또 정말로 지켜보고 정말로 관심을 갖습니다."], ["What more can we do?", "그 이상 우리가 무얼 더 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Here is my Beauty daughter, married these three years.", "여기 내 미녀 딸은 결혼한 지 삼 년이 됐어요."], ["Now I dare say her marrying another child, and having two more, was all wrong in point of political economy, but it was very agreeable.", "자, 이 애가 또 다른 어린애와 결혼해서 어린것을 둘이나 더 두었으니 정치경제학으로 따지면 다 잘못된 일이라고 나는 말하겠지만, 그래도 무척 흐뭇한 일이었지요."], ["We had our little festivities on those occasions and exchanged social ideas.", "그럴 때마다 우린 자그마한 잔치를 벌이고 사교적인 생각을 주고받았답니다."], ["She brought her young husband home one day, and they and their young fledglings have their nest upstairs.", "어느 날 이 애가 어린 남편을 집에 데려왔고, 그들과 어린 새끼들은 위층에 둥지를 틀었지요."], ["I dare say at some time or other Sentiment and Comedy will bring THEIR husbands home and have THEIR nests upstairs too.", "감상이와 희극이도 언젠가 제 남편들을 집에 데려와서 위층에 제 둥지를 틀겠지요."], ["So we get on, we don’t know how, but somehow.”", "그렇게 우린 살아갑니다, 어떻게 사는지는 모르지만, 그래도 어떻게든 말이에요.\""]], [["She looked very young indeed to be the mother of two children, and I could not help pitying both her and them.", "그 애는 두 아이의 어머니라기엔 정말이지 무척 어려 보였고, 나는 그 애도 아이들도 가엾게 여기지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["It was evident that the three daughters had grown up as they could and had had just as little haphazard instruction as qualified them to be their father’s playthings in his idlest hours.", "세 딸이 되는대로 자라났고, 한가한 시간에 아비의 노리갯감 노릇이나 할 만큼만 어쩌다 손에 잡히는 가르침을 받았다는 것이 분명했습니다."], ["His pictorial tastes were consulted, I observed, in their respective styles of wearing their hair, the Beauty daughter being in the classic manner, the Sentiment daughter luxuriant and flowing, and the Comedy daughter in the arch style, with a good deal of sprightly forehead, and vivacious little curls dotted about the corners of her eyes.", "그의 회화적 취향이, 내가 보기에, 딸들이 머리를 매만진 저마다의 모양새에 반영되어 있었으니, 미녀 딸은 고전적인 모양으로, 감상 딸은 풍성하게 흘러내리는 모양으로, 희극 딸은 발랄한 이마를 한껏 드러내고 생기 있는 작은 곱슬머리를 눈가에 점점이 흩뜨린 짓궂은 모양이었습니다."], ["They were dressed to correspond, though in a most untidy and negligent way.", "옷도 거기에 맞춰 입었는데, 더없이 너저분하고 부주의한 차림새였습니다."]], [["Ada and I conversed with these young ladies and found them wonderfully like their father. In the meanwhile Mr.", "에이다와 나는 이 아가씨들과 이야기를 나누었고, 그들이 놀랍도록 아비를 닮았다는 것을 알았습니다."], ["Jarndyce (who had been rubbing his head to a great extent, and hinted at a change in the wind) talked with Mrs. Skimpole in a corner, where we could not help hearing the chink of money.", "그러는 동안 잔다이스 씨는(머리를 한참이나 문지르며 바람의 방향이 바뀐다고 넌지시 비쳤습니다) 한쪽 구석에서 스킴폴 부인과 이야기를 나누었는데, 우리 귀에 돈이 짤랑거리는 소리가 들리지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["Mr. Skimpole had previously volunteered to go home with us and had withdrawn to dress himself for the purpose.", "스킴폴 씨는 앞서 우리와 함께 집에 가겠노라 자청해두었던 터라, 그러려고 옷을 갈아입으러 물러가 있었습니다."]], [["“My roses,”", "\"내 장미꽃들아,\""], ["he said when he came back, “take care of mama.", "그가 돌아와서 말했습니다. \"엄마를 잘 보살펴드려라."], ["She is poorly to-day.", "오늘 엄마가 편찮으시니까."], ["By going home with Mr. Jarndyce for a day or two, I shall hear the larks sing and preserve my amiability.", "잔다이스 씨네 집에 하루 이틀 가 있으면, 나는 종달새 노랫소리도 듣고 내 상냥함도 지킬 수 있을 거야."], ["It has been tried, you know, and would be tried again if I remained at home.”", "알다시피 전에 한번 시험에 들었으니, 집에 그냥 있으면 또 시험에 들 테지.\""]], [["“That bad man!”", "\"그 못된 사람!\""], ["said the Comedy daughter.", "희극 딸이 말했습니다."]], [["“At the very time when he knew papa was lying ill by his wallflowers, looking at the blue sky,”", "\"바로 아빠가 계란풀 옆에 누워 앓으면서 파란 하늘을 바라보고 있을 때 말이에요.\""], ["Laura complained.", "로라가 하소연했습니다."]], [["“And when the smell of hay was in the air!”", "\"건초 냄새가 공기에 가득할 때 말이에요!\""], ["said Arethusa.", "아레투사가 말했습니다."]], [["“It showed a want of poetry in the man,” Mr.", "\"그건 그 사람한테 시정이 없다는 걸 보여준 거지.\""], ["Skimpole assented, but with perfect good humour.", "스킴폴 씨가 맞장구쳤는데, 더없이 좋은 기분이었습니다."], ["“It was coarse.", "\"그건 천박했어."], ["There was an absence of the finer touches of humanity in it!", "거기엔 인간미의 섬세한 결이 없었지!"], ["My daughters have taken great offence,”", "내 딸들이,\" 그가 우리에게 설명했습니다."], ["he explained to us, “at an honest man—”", "\"어느 정직한 사람한테 몹시 화가 나 있답니다—\""]], [["“Not honest, papa.", "\"정직하지 않아요, 아빠."], ["Impossible!”", "말도 안 돼요!\""], ["they all three protested.", "셋이 모두 항의했습니다."]], [["“At a rough kind of fellow—a sort of human hedgehog rolled up,” said Mr.", "\"어느 거친 부류의 친구한테 말이지요—말하자면 몸을 둥글게 만 인간 고슴도치 같은.\""], ["Skimpole, “who is a baker in this neighbourhood and from whom we borrowed a couple of arm-chairs.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 동네에서 빵집을 하는 사람인데, 우리가 그한테서 안락의자 두 개를 빌렸어요."], ["We wanted a couple of arm-chairs, and we hadn’t got them, and therefore of course we looked to a man who HAD got them, to lend them.", "우린 안락의자가 두 개 필요한데 우리한테는 없었으니, 당연히 그게 있는 사람을 찾아 빌리려 한 거지요."], ["Well!", "그런데!"], ["This morose person lent them, and we wore them out.", "이 뚱한 인간이 빌려주긴 했고, 우린 그걸 닳도록 썼습니다."], ["When they were worn out, he wanted them back.", "다 닳자 그는 그걸 돌려받고 싶어 했어요."], ["He had them back.", "그래서 돌려받았지요."], ["He was contented, you will say.", "그럼 만족했겠거니 하겠지요."], ["Not at all.", "천만에."], ["He objected to their being worn.", "그는 그게 닳았다고 시비를 걸더군요."], ["I reasoned with him, and pointed out his mistake.", "내가 그와 이치를 따지며 그의 잘못을 짚어주었습니다."], ["I said, ‘Can you, at your time of life, be so headstrong, my friend, as to persist that an arm-chair is a thing to put upon a shelf and look at?", "내가 말했지요. '이보게 친구, 자네 나이쯤 돼서, 안락의자란 선반에 올려놓고 바라보는 물건이라고 끝까지 우길 만큼 그렇게 고집스러울 수가 있나?"], ["That it is an object to contemplate, to survey from a distance, to consider from a point of sight?", "그게 관조하고, 멀찍이서 바라보고, 어느 시점에서 가늠해보는 대상이란 말인가?"], ["Don’t you KNOW that these arm-chairs were borrowed to be sat upon?’ He was unreasonable and unpersuadable and used intemperate language.", "이 안락의자들을 앉으라고 빌려준 줄 자네는 모른단 말인가?' 그는 도무지 이치도 모르고 설득도 안 되는 데다 막말까지 하더군요."], ["Being as patient as I am at this minute, I addressed another appeal to him.", "지금 이 순간만큼이나 인내심을 발휘하며, 나는 그에게 또 한 번 호소했습니다."], ["I said, ‘Now, my good man, however our business capacities may vary, we are all children of one great mother, Nature.", "내가 말했지요. '이보게 선량한 친구, 우리 사업 능력이 아무리 다르다 해도, 우린 다 한 위대한 어머니, 자연의 자식들 아닌가."], ["On this blooming summer morning here you see me’ (I was on the sofa) ‘with flowers before me, fruit upon the table, the cloudless sky above me, the air full of fragrance, contemplating Nature.", "꽃이 만발한 이 여름 아침에 자네는 여기 나를 보고 있네'(나는 소파에 있었습니다) '내 앞엔 꽃이, 탁자엔 과일이, 머리 위엔 구름 한 점 없는 하늘이, 공기엔 향기가 가득한데, 나는 자연을 관조하고 있어."], ["I entreat you, by our common brotherhood, not to interpose between me and a subject so sublime, the absurd figure of an angry baker!’ But he did,”", "우리 공통의 형제애를 걸고 부탁하네만, 나와 이토록 숭고한 대상 사이에 화난 빵장수라는 우스꽝스러운 형상을 끼워 넣지 말아주게!'"], ["said Mr.", "그런데도 그자는,\""], ["Skimpole, raising his laughing eyes in playful astonishment; “he did interpose that ridiculous figure, and he does, and he will again.", "스킴폴 씨가 웃음 띤 눈을 들어 장난스러운 놀라움을 지으며 말했습니다. \"끼워 넣었어요, 그 가당찮은 형상을, 그리고 지금도 끼워 넣고, 앞으로도 또 끼워 넣을 겁니다."], ["And therefore I am very glad to get out of his way and to go home with my friend Jarndyce.”", "그래서 나는 그를 피해 내 친구 잔다이스와 함께 집에 가게 되어 정말 기쁘답니다.\""]], [["It seemed to escape his consideration that Mrs. Skimpole and the daughters remained behind to encounter the baker, but this was so old a story to all of them that it had become a matter of course.", "스킴폴 부인과 딸들이 남아서 빵장수와 맞닥뜨려야 한다는 사실은 그의 고려에서 벗어난 듯했지만, 이것은 그들 모두에게 워낙 케케묵은 이야기인지라 당연한 일이 되어 있었습니다."], ["He took leave of his family with a tenderness as airy and graceful as any other aspect in which he showed himself and rode away with us in perfect harmony of mind.", "그는 그 자신이 보여준 다른 어떤 면 못지않게 가뿐하고 우아한 다정함으로 가족과 작별하고는, 더없이 화목한 마음으로 우리와 함께 말을 타고 떠났습니다."], ["We had an opportunity of seeing through some open doors, as we went downstairs, that his own apartment was a palace to the rest of the house.", "우리는 아래층으로 내려가면서 열린 문 몇 개 너머로, 그의 방이 집의 나머지에 견주면 궁전이나 다름없다는 것을 엿볼 수 있었습니다."]], [["I could have no anticipation, and I had none, that something very startling to me at the moment, and ever memorable to me in what ensued from it, was to happen before this day was out.", "이날이 저물기 전에, 그 순간 나에게는 무척 놀라운 일이, 그리고 거기에서 비롯된 일로 인해 내내 잊지 못할 일이 일어나리라고는 나로서는 예감할 수도 없었고, 또 예감하지도 못했습니다."], ["Our guest was in such spirits on the way home that I could do nothing but listen to him and wonder at him; nor was I alone in this, for Ada yielded to the same fascination.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 우리 손님이 어찌나 들떠 있던지, 나는 그저 그의 말에 귀 기울이며 그를 신기해할 수밖에 없었습니다. 게다가 이런 사람이 나만은 아니었으니, 에이다도 똑같은 매혹에 빠져들었습니다."], ["As to my guardian, the wind, which had threatened to become fixed in the east when we left Somers Town, veered completely round before we were a couple of miles from it.", "후견인으로 말하면, 서머스 타운을 떠날 때만 해도 동쪽에 굳어버릴 듯 위협하던 바람이, 거기서 채 이삼 킬로미터도 가기 전에 완전히 방향을 틀었습니다."]], [["Whether of questionable childishness or not in any other matters, Mr. Skimpole had a child’s enjoyment of change and bright weather.", "다른 어떤 일에서든 그 어린애 같음이 미심쩍은 것이든 아니든, 스킴폴 씨는 변화와 화창한 날씨를 어린애처럼 즐겼습니다."], ["In no way wearied by his sallies on the road, he was in the drawing-room before any of us; and I heard him at the piano while I was yet looking after my housekeeping, singing refrains of barcaroles and drinking songs, Italian and German, by the score.", "길에서 늘어놓은 재담에도 조금도 지치지 않은 채, 그는 우리 누구보다 먼저 응접실에 들어가 있었습니다. 그래서 내가 아직 살림을 둘러보는 동안에도, 그가 피아노 앞에 앉아 이탈리아어와 독일어로 뱃노래며 권주가의 후렴을 수없이 부르는 소리가 들렸습니다."]], [["We were all assembled shortly before dinner, and he was still at the piano idly picking out in his luxurious way little strains of music, and talking between whiles of finishing some sketches of the ruined old Verulam wall to-morrow, which he had begun a year or two ago and had got tired of, when a card was brought in and my guardian read aloud in a surprised voice, “Sir Leicester Dedlock!”", "저녁 식사 조금 전에 우리는 다 모였고, 그는 여전히 피아노 앞에서 그 호사스러운 식으로 한가로이 짤막한 가락을 뜯어내며, 한두 해 전에 시작했다가 싫증 났던 옛 베룰람 성벽 폐허 스케치를 내일 마저 끝내겠다는 둥 틈틈이 이야기하고 있었습니다. 그때 명함 한 장이 들어왔고, 후견인이 놀란 목소리로 소리 내어 읽었습니다. \"레스터 데드록 경!\""]], [["The visitor was in the room while it was yet turning round with me and before I had the power to stir.", "방이 아직 나와 함께 빙빙 돌고 있고 내가 채 몸을 움직일 힘도 없는 사이에, 방문객은 이미 방 안에 들어와 있었습니다."], ["If I had had it, I should have hurried away.", "힘이 있었다면 나는 서둘러 빠져나갔을 것입니다."], ["I had not even the presence of mind, in my giddiness, to retire to Ada in the window, or to see the window, or to know where it was.", "어지러운 와중에 나는 창가의 에이다에게 물러갈 침착함도, 창문을 볼 침착함도, 창문이 어디 있는지 알아챌 침착함도 없었습니다."], ["I heard my name and found that my guardian was presenting me before I could move to a chair.", "내 이름이 들렸고, 내가 의자 쪽으로 움직이기도 전에 후견인이 나를 소개하고 있다는 것을 알았습니다."]], [["“Pray be seated, Sir Leicester.”", "\"앉으시지요, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 씨,\""], ["said Sir Leicester in reply as he bowed and seated himself, “I do myself the honour of calling here—”", "레스터 경이 절을 하고 자리에 앉으며 대답했습니다. \"외람되이 이렇게 찾아온 까닭은—\""]], [["“You do ME the honour, Sir Leicester.”", "\"외람되다니요, 영광입니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“Thank you—of calling here on my road from Lincolnshire to express my regret that any cause of complaint, however strong, that I may have against a gentleman who—who is known to you and has been your host, and to whom therefore I will make no farther reference, should have prevented you, still more ladies under your escort and charge, from seeing whatever little there may be to gratify a polite and refined taste at my house, Chesney Wold.”", "\"고맙습니다—링컨셔에서 가는 길에 이렇게 들른 까닭은, 내가 어느 신사에게 품은 불만이, 그것이 아무리 강하다 한들, 그 신사로 말하면—그대도 아는 사람이자 그대를 손님으로 맞은 적이 있고, 그러니 더는 거론하지 않겠습니다만—그 불만 때문에 그대가, 더구나 그대의 보호와 인솔을 받는 숙녀분들이 내 집 체스니 월드에서 점잖고 세련된 취향을 만족시킬 만한 자그마한 것들이나마 둘러보지 못하게 된 데 대해 유감을 표하기 위함입니다.\""]], [["“You are exceedingly obliging, Sir Leicester, and on behalf of those ladies (who are present) and for myself, I thank you very much.”", "\"참으로 친절하십니다, 레스터 경. 여기 계신 저 숙녀들을 대신해서, 또 저 자신을 위해서도, 진심으로 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“It is possible, Mr. Jarndyce, that the gentleman to whom, for the reasons I have mentioned, I refrain from making further allusion—it is possible, Mr.", "\"잔다이스 씨, 어쩌면 내가 앞서 말한 까닭으로 더는 언급을 삼가는 그 신사가—잔다이스 씨, 어쩌면 그 신사가 그렇게까지 내 됨됨이를 오해하는 무례를 범하여, 그대로 하여금, 그대가 내 링컨셔 영지 사람들로부터 그 집을 찾는 모든 신사 숙녀에게 보이도록 일러둔 그 정중함과 그 예의로 영접받지 못하리라 믿게 만들었을 수도 있겠습니다."], ["Jarndyce, that that gentleman may have done me the honour so far to misapprehend my character as to induce you to believe that you would not have been received by my local establishment in Lincolnshire with that urbanity, that courtesy, which its members are instructed to show to all ladies and gentlemen who present themselves at that house. I merely beg to observe, sir, that the fact is the reverse.”", "나는 그저, 선생, 실상은 그 반대라는 점을 일러두고자 할 따름입니다.\""]], [["My guardian delicately dismissed this remark without making any verbal answer.", "후견인은 한마디 말로도 대답하지 않은 채 이 말을 부드럽게 흘려보냈습니다."]], [["“It has given me pain, Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"이 일은 나에게 고통을 안겼습니다, 잔다이스 씨.\""], ["Sir Leicester weightily proceeded.", "레스터 경이 무겁게 말을 이었습니다."], ["“I assure you, sir, it has given—me—pain—to learn from the housekeeper at Chesney Wold that a gentleman who was in your company in that part of the county, and who would appear to possess a cultivated taste for the fine arts, was likewise deterred by some such cause from examining the family pictures with that leisure, that attention, that care, which he might have desired to bestow upon them and which some of them might possibly have repaid.”", "\"장담하건대, 선생, 그것은—나에게—고통을—안겼습니다. 그 고장에서 그대와 함께 있던 어느 신사가, 게다가 미술에 대한 교양 있는 취향을 갖춘 듯한 분이, 그 또한 그 비슷한 까닭으로, 본래 들이고 싶었을, 또 그 가운데 몇몇은 어쩌면 그만한 값어치를 했을, 그런 여유와 그런 주의와 그런 정성으로 가문의 그림들을 살피지 못하고 단념했다는 것을 체스니 월드의 가정부에게서 듣고 말입니다.\""], ["Here he produced a card and read, with much gravity and a little trouble, through his eye-glass, “Mr.", "여기서 그는 명함 한 장을 꺼내, 자못 근엄하게, 외알 안경 너머로 약간 더듬으며 읽었습니다."], ["Hirrold—Herald—Harold—Skampling—Skumpling—I beg your pardon—Skimpole.”", "\"히롤드—허럴드—해럴드—스캠플링—스컴플링—실례했습니다—스킴폴 씨.\""]], [["“This is Mr. Harold Skimpole,”", "\"이분이 해럴드 스킴폴 씨입니다.\""], ["said my guardian, evidently surprised.", "후견인이 분명 놀란 기색으로 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["exclaimed Sir Leicester, “I am happy to meet Mr.", "레스터 경이 외쳤습니다."], ["Skimpole and to have the opportunity of tendering my personal regrets.", "\"스킴폴 씨를 만나, 직접 유감을 표할 기회를 얻게 되어 기쁩니다."], ["I hope, sir, that when you again find yourself in my part of the county, you will be under no similar sense of restraint.”", "선생, 다음에 다시 내 고장에 오실 때는 부디 그와 같은 거리낌을 느끼지 마시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["“You are very obliging, Sir Leicester Dedlock.", "\"참으로 친절하십니다, 레스터 데드록 경."], ["So encouraged, I shall certainly give myself the pleasure and advantage of another visit to your beautiful house.", "그렇게 격려해주시니, 나는 반드시 그 아름다운 댁을 다시 한번 찾아뵙는 기쁨과 영광을 누리겠습니다."], ["The owners of such places as Chesney Wold,” said Mr.", "체스니 월드 같은 곳의 소유주들은,\""], ["Skimpole with his usual happy and easy air, “are public benefactors.", "스킴폴 씨가 평소의 흐뭇하고 거침없는 태도로 말했습니다. \"공공의 은인이지요."], ["They are good enough to maintain a number of delightful objects for the admiration and pleasure of us poor men; and not to reap all the admiration and pleasure that they yield is to be ungrateful to our benefactors.”", "그들은 친절하게도, 우리 가난한 사람들이 우러러보고 즐길 수 있도록 숱한 즐거운 것들을 간직해줍니다. 그러니 그것들이 주는 그 모든 감탄과 즐거움을 거두어들이지 않는다는 것은 우리 은인에게 배은망덕한 짓이지요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester seemed to approve of this sentiment highly.", "레스터 경은 이 견해를 대단히 흡족해하는 듯했습니다."], ["“An artist, sir?”", "\"예술가시오, 선생?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["returned Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 받았습니다."], ["“A perfectly idle man.", "\"완전히 한가한 사람이지요."], ["A mere amateur.”", "한낱 애호가일 뿐입니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester seemed to approve of this even more.", "레스터 경은 이것을 더더욱 흡족해하는 듯했습니다."], ["He hoped he might have the good fortune to be at Chesney Wold when Mr. Skimpole next came down into Lincolnshire.", "그는 스킴폴 씨가 다음에 링컨셔로 내려올 때 자기가 체스니 월드에 있는 행운을 누릴 수 있기를 바란다고 했습니다."], ["Mr. Skimpole professed himself much flattered and honoured.", "스킴폴 씨는 무척 우쭐하고 영광이라는 뜻을 내비쳤습니다."]], [["“Mr. Skimpole mentioned,”", "\"스킴폴 씨가 언급하기를,\""], ["pursued Sir Leicester, addressing himself again to my guardian, “mentioned to the housekeeper, who, as he may have observed, is an old and attached retainer of the family—”", "레스터 경이 다시 후견인을 향해 말을 이었습니다. \"가정부에게 언급하기를, 그도 알아챘겠지만 그 가정부는 가문의 오래되고 충직한 가신인데—\""]], [["(“That is, when I walked through the house the other day, on the occasion of my going down to visit Miss Summerson and Miss Clare,”", "(\"그러니까 며칠 전 서머슨 양과 클레어 양을 찾아뵈러 내려간 김에 그 집을 둘러볼 때 말이지요.\""], ["Mr. Skimpole airily explained to us.)", "스킴폴 씨가 우리에게 가뿐하게 설명했습니다.)"]], [["“—That the friend with whom he had formerly been staying there was Mr.", "\"—거기서 예전에 함께 머물렀던 친구가 잔다이스 씨라고 언급했다더군요.\""], ["Jarndyce.” Sir Leicester bowed to the bearer of that name.", "레스터 경은 그 이름을 지닌 사람에게 고개를 숙였습니다."], ["“And hence I became aware of the circumstance for which I have professed my regret.", "\"그래서 내가, 앞서 유감을 표한 그 사정을 알게 된 것입니다."], ["That this should have occurred to any gentleman, Mr. Jarndyce, but especially a gentleman formerly known to Lady Dedlock, and indeed claiming some distant connexion with her, and for whom (as I learn from my Lady herself) she entertains a high respect, does, I assure you, give—me—pain.”", "이런 일이 어느 신사에게든 일어났다는 것, 잔다이스 씨, 더구나 예전에 데드록 부인과 알고 지냈고, 실은 부인과 먼 친척뻘이라 하며, 부인이(이건 부인에게 직접 들은 바입니다만) 깊이 존경하는 그런 신사에게 일어났다는 것은, 장담하건대, 나에게—고통을—안깁니다.\""]], [["“Pray say no more about it, Sir Leicester,”", "\"그 일은 더 말씀하지 마시지요, 레스터 경.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 받았습니다."], ["“I am very sensible, as I am sure we all are, of your consideration.", "\"저는, 또 우리 모두 분명 그러하듯, 경의 배려에 깊이 감복하고 있습니다."], ["Indeed the mistake was mine, and I ought to apologize for it.”", "사실 잘못은 제게 있었으니, 마땅히 제가 사과드려야 합니다.\""]], [["I had not once looked up.", "나는 한 번도 고개를 들지 않았습니다."], ["I had not seen the visitor and had not even appeared to myself to hear the conversation.", "방문객을 보지도 않았고, 심지어 나 자신에게조차 그 대화가 들리는 것 같지도 않았습니다."], ["It surprises me to find that I can recall it, for it seemed to make no impression on me as it passed.", "그런데도 그것을 떠올릴 수 있다는 것이 신기한데, 오가는 동안에는 나에게 아무 인상도 남기지 않는 것 같았기 때문입니다."], ["I heard them speaking, but my mind was so confused and my instinctive avoidance of this gentleman made his presence so distressing to me that I thought I understood nothing, through the rushing in my head and the beating of my heart.", "그들이 말하는 소리가 들리긴 했지만, 내 머릿속은 너무도 혼란스러웠고 이 신사를 본능적으로 피하려는 마음에 그의 존재가 어찌나 고통스러웠는지, 머리가 쿵쿵 울리고 심장이 두근거리는 통에 나는 아무것도 알아듣지 못하는 것만 같았습니다."]], [["“I mentioned the subject to Lady Dedlock,”", "\"이 문제를 데드록 부인에게 이야기했더니,\""], ["said Sir Leicester, rising, “and my Lady informed me that she had had the pleasure of exchanging a few words with Mr.", "레스터 경이 일어서며 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce and his wards on the occasion of an accidental meeting during their sojourn in the vicinity. Permit me, Mr.", "\"부인이 알려주기를, 그들이 그 근방에 머무는 동안 우연히 만나, 잔다이스 씨와 그 피후견인들과 몇 마디 나누는 기쁨을 누렸다더군요."], ["Jarndyce, to repeat to yourself, and to these ladies, the assurance I have already tendered to Mr. Skimpole.", "잔다이스 씨, 내가 이미 스킴폴 씨에게 드린 그 다짐을, 그대 자신과 저 숙녀들에게도 거듭 전하게 해주십시오."], ["Circumstances undoubtedly prevent my saying that it would afford me any gratification to hear that Mr. Boythorn had favoured my house with his presence, but those circumstances are confined to that gentleman himself and do not extend beyond him.”", "사정상 보이손 씨가 내 집에 들러주면 내가 흐뭇하겠다는 말은 분명 할 수 없습니다만, 그 사정은 오직 그 신사 한 사람에게 국한되며 그를 넘어서지는 않습니다.\""]], [["“You know my old opinion of him,” said Mr.", "\"그 사람에 대한 내 오랜 생각은 당신도 아시잖습니까.\""], ["Skimpole, lightly appealing to us.", "스킴폴 씨가 가볍게 우리에게 호소하며 말했습니다."], ["“An amiable bull who is determined to make every colour scarlet!”", "\"온갖 색을 다 진홍빛으로 만들기로 작심한 상냥한 황소지요!\""]], [["Sir Leicester Dedlock coughed as if he could not possibly hear another word in reference to such an individual and took his leave with great ceremony and politeness.", "레스터 데드록 경은 그런 인물에 대한 말은 단 한마디도 더 들을 수 없다는 듯 헛기침을 하고는, 자못 격식과 예의를 갖추어 작별했습니다."], ["I got to my own room with all possible speed and remained there until I had recovered my self-command.", "나는 되도록 빨리 내 방으로 가서, 마음의 평정을 되찾을 때까지 거기 머물렀습니다."], ["It had been very much disturbed, but I was thankful to find when I went downstairs again that they only rallied me for having been shy and mute before the great Lincolnshire baronet.", "마음이 무척 흐트러져 있었지만, 다시 아래층으로 내려갔을 때 그들이 그저 내가 링컨셔의 그 위대한 준남작 앞에서 수줍어하며 말 한마디 못 했다고 놀려대기만 하는 것을 보고 나는 안도했습니다."]], [["By that time I had made up my mind that the period was come when I must tell my guardian what I knew.", "그 무렵 나는, 내가 아는 것을 후견인에게 털어놓아야 할 시기가 왔다고 마음을 정했습니다."], ["The possibility of my being brought into contact with my mother, of my being taken to her house, even of Mr. Skimpole’s, however distantly associated with me, receiving kindnesses and obligations from her husband, was so painful that I felt I could no longer guide myself without his assistance.", "내가 어머니와 마주치게 될 가능성, 어머니의 집으로 끌려가게 될 가능성, 심지어 나와는 아무리 먼 관계라 해도 스킴폴 씨가 어머니의 남편에게서 친절과 신세를 입게 될 가능성이 너무도 괴로워서, 나는 후견인의 도움 없이는 더 이상 나 자신을 추스를 수 없을 것 같았습니다."]], [["When we had retired for the night, and Ada and I had had our usual talk in our pretty room, I went out at my door again and sought my guardian among his books.", "밤이 되어 잠자리에 들 채비를 하고, 에이다와 내가 우리의 예쁜 방에서 늘 하던 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 나는 다시 방문을 나서 책들 사이에 있는 후견인을 찾아갔습니다."], ["I knew he always read at that hour, and as I drew near I saw the light shining out into the passage from his reading-lamp.", "그가 늘 그 시각에 책을 읽는다는 것을 알고 있었는데, 다가가면서 보니 그의 독서등 불빛이 복도로 새어 나오고 있었습니다."]], [["“May I come in, guardian?”", "\"들어가도 될까요, 후견인?\""]], [["“Surely, little woman.", "\"그럼, 들어오렴, 꼬마 아가씨."], ["What’s the matter?”", "무슨 일이니?\""]], [["“Nothing is the matter.", "\"아무 일도 아니에요."], ["I thought I would like to take this quiet time of saying a word to you about myself.”", "이 조용한 시간에 제 이야기를 좀 드리고 싶어서요.\""]], [["He put a chair for me, shut his book, and put it by, and turned his kind attentive face towards me.", "그는 나에게 의자를 내주고, 책을 덮어 옆에 치워두고는, 그 친절하고 주의 깊은 얼굴을 나에게 돌렸습니다."], ["I could not help observing that it wore that curious expression I had observed in it once before—on that night when he had said that he was in no trouble which I could readily understand.", "나는 그 얼굴에, 전에 한 번 본 적 있는 그 묘한 표정이 어려 있는 것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었습니다—그날 밤, 그가 내가 쉽게 이해할 만한 어떤 괴로움도 자기에게는 없다고 말했던 그날 밤에 본 표정이었습니다."]], [["“What concerns you, my dear Esther,”", "\"너에게 관계되는 일은, 사랑하는 에스더,\" 그가 말했습니다."], ["said he, “concerns us all.", "\"우리 모두에게 관계되는 일이란다."], ["You cannot be more ready to speak than I am to hear.”", "네가 말하고 싶은 마음보다 내가 듣고 싶은 마음이 결코 덜하지 않아.\""]], [["“I know that, guardian.", "\"저도 알아요, 후견인."], ["But I have such need of your advice and support.", "하지만 저는 후견인의 조언과 힘이 너무도 절실해요."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["You don’t know how much need I have to-night.”", "오늘 밤 제게 그게 얼마나 절실한지 모르실 거예요.\""]], [["He looked unprepared for my being so earnest, and even a little alarmed.", "그는 내가 이토록 진지한 데 마음의 준비가 안 된 듯했고, 심지어 조금 놀란 기색이었습니다."]], [["“Or how anxious I have been to speak to you,”", "\"제가 후견인께 말씀드리려고 얼마나 마음 졸였는지도요.\""], ["said I, “ever since the visitor was here to-day.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"오늘 그 방문객이 다녀간 뒤로 줄곧 말이에요.\""]], [["“The visitor, my dear!", "\"방문객이라니, 얘야!"], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock?”", "레스터 데드록 경 말이냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["He folded his arms and sat looking at me with an air of the profoundest astonishment, awaiting what I should say next.", "그는 팔짱을 끼고 앉아 더없이 깊은 놀라움이 어린 얼굴로 나를 바라보며, 내가 다음에 무슨 말을 할지 기다렸습니다."], ["I did not know how to prepare him.", "나는 그에게 어떻게 마음의 준비를 시켜야 할지 알 수 없었습니다."]], [["“Why, Esther,”", "\"아니, 에스더,\""], ["said he, breaking into a smile, “our visitor and you are the two last persons on earth I should have thought of connecting together!”", "그가 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. \"우리 방문객과 너야말로 이 세상에서 내가 가장 마지막으로나 한데 엮어 생각할 두 사람인데!\""]], [["“Oh, yes, guardian, I know it.", "\"오, 네, 후견인, 저도 알아요."], ["And I too, but a little while ago.”", "저도 바로 얼마 전까지는 그랬어요.\""]], [["The smile passed from his face, and he became graver than before.", "미소가 그의 얼굴에서 사라지고, 그는 전보다 더 진지해졌습니다."], ["He crossed to the door to see that it was shut (but I had seen to that) and resumed his seat before me.", "그는 문으로 건너가 닫혔는지 확인하고는(하지만 그건 내가 이미 살펴두었습니다), 다시 내 앞 자리로 돌아왔습니다."]], [["“Guardian,”", "\"후견인,\""], ["said I, “do you remember, when we were overtaken by the thunder-storm, Lady Dedlock’s speaking to you of her sister?”", "내가 말했습니다. \"우리가 뇌우를 만났을 때, 데드록 부인이 자기 언니 이야기를 하던 것 기억하세요?\""]], [["“Of course.", "\"물론이지."], ["Of course I do.”", "물론 기억한단다.\""]], [["“And reminding you that she and her sister had differed, had gone their several ways?”", "\"그리고 부인과 그 언니가 사이가 틀어져 제각각 길을 갔다고 일러주던 것도요?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론.\""]], [["“Why did they separate, guardian?”", "\"그분들은 왜 갈라섰나요, 후견인?\""]], [["His face quite altered as he looked at me.", "나를 바라보는 그의 얼굴이 싹 달라졌습니다."], ["“My child, what questions are these!", "\"얘야, 그게 무슨 질문이냐!"], ["I never knew.", "나는 도무지 모른단다."], ["No one but themselves ever did know, I believe.", "그들 자신 말고는 아무도 끝내 몰랐을 거야."], ["Who could tell what the secrets of those two handsome and proud women were!", "그 아름답고 도도한 두 여인의 비밀이 무엇이었는지 누가 알 수 있겠니!"], ["You have seen Lady Dedlock.", "너도 데드록 부인을 보았지."], ["If you had ever seen her sister, you would know her to have been as resolute and haughty as she.”", "만일 그 언니를 본 적이 있다면, 그 언니가 동생만큼이나 단호하고 오만했다는 걸 알았을 게다.\""]], [["“Oh, guardian, I have seen her many and many a time!”", "\"오, 후견인, 저는 그분을 보고 또 보았어요, 수없이요!\""]], [["“Seen her?”", "\"보았다고?\""]], [["He paused a little, biting his lip.", "그는 잠깐 멈추어 입술을 깨물었습니다."], ["“Then, Esther, when you spoke to me long ago of Boythorn, and when I told you that he was all but married once, and that the lady did not die, but died to him, and that that time had had its influence on his later life—did you know it all, and know who the lady was?”", "\"그렇다면, 에스더, 네가 오래전 나에게 보이손 이야기를 했을 때, 그리고 내가 너에게 그가 한때 결혼 직전까지 갔으나 그 여인이 죽은 건 아니지만 그에게는 죽은 사람이 되었고, 그 일이 그의 후일에 영향을 미쳤다고 일러주었을 때—너는 그걸 다 알고 있었던 거냐, 그 여인이 누구인지 알고 있었던 거냐?\""]], [["“No, guardian,”", "\"아니에요, 후견인.\""], ["I returned, fearful of the light that dimly broke upon me.", "나는 어렴풋이 밝아오는 그 빛이 두려워 받았습니다."], ["“Nor do I know yet.”", "\"지금도 모르고요.\""]], [["“Lady Dedlock’s sister.”", "\"데드록 부인의 언니란다.\""]], [["“And why,”", "\"그런데 왜,\""], ["I could scarcely ask him, “why, guardian, pray tell me why were THEY parted?”", "나는 차마 그에게 묻기조차 어려웠습니다. \"왜, 후견인, 부디 말씀해주세요, 그분들은 왜 갈라선 건가요?\""]], [["“It was her act, and she kept its motives in her inflexible heart.", "\"그건 그 언니가 한 일이었고, 그 동기는 굽힐 줄 모르는 가슴속에 묻어두었지."], ["He afterwards did conjecture (but it was mere conjecture) that some injury which her haughty spirit had received in her cause of quarrel with her sister had wounded her beyond all reason, but she wrote him that from the date of that letter she died to him—as in literal truth she did—and that the resolution was exacted from her by her knowledge of his proud temper and his strained sense of honour, which were both her nature too.", "나중에 그가 추측하기로는(그저 추측일 뿐이지만) 동생과 다투던 그 일로 그 오만한 자존심이 어떤 상처를 입어, 이성을 다 잃을 만큼 깊이 베인 거라고 했어. 하지만 그 언니는 그 편지를 쓴 날부터 자기는 그에게 죽은 사람이라고—말 그대로 정말 그렇게 되었지—써 보냈고, 그 결심은 그의 도도한 기질과 팽팽한 명예심을 자기가 잘 알기에 어쩔 수 없이 내린 것이라고 했단다."], ["In consideration for those master points in him, and even in consideration for them in herself, she made the sacrifice, she said, and would live in it and die in it.", "그 두 가지는 그 언니의 천성이기도 했지. 그에게 있는 그 핵심을 헤아려서, 또 자기 안에 있는 그것까지 헤아려서, 자기가 그 희생을 치르는 것이며, 그 안에서 살다가 그 안에서 죽겠다고 했어."], ["She did both, I fear; certainly he never saw her, never heard of her from that hour.", "그 언니는 그 둘을 다 한 모양이야, 안타깝지만. 분명 그는 그 시각 이후로 두 번 다시 그 언니를 보지도, 소식을 듣지도 못했지."], ["Nor did any one.”", "그 누구도 못 들었고.\""]], [["“Oh, guardian, what have I done!”", "\"오, 후견인, 제가 무슨 짓을 한 걸까요!\""], ["I cried, giving way to my grief; “what sorrow have I innocently caused!”", "나는 슬픔에 못 이겨 외쳤습니다. \"제가 아무것도 모른 채 얼마나 큰 슬픔을 일으킨 걸까요!\""]], [["“You caused, Esther?”", "\"네가 일으켰다고, 에스더?\""]], [["“Yes, guardian.", "\"네, 후견인."], ["Innocently, but most surely.", "아무것도 모른 채, 그러나 더없이 분명하게요."], ["That secluded sister is my first remembrance.”", "그 외따로 지내던 언니가 제 첫 기억이에요.\""]], [["“No, no!”", "\"아니, 아니야!\""], ["he cried, starting.", "그가 화들짝 놀라며 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Yes, guardian, yes!", "\"그래요, 후견인, 그래요!"], ["And HER sister is my mother!”", "그리고 그분의 동생이 제 어머니예요!\""]], [["I would have told him all my mother’s letter, but he would not hear it then.", "나는 그에게 어머니의 편지를 다 이야기하려 했지만, 그는 그때 그것을 들으려 하지 않았습니다."], ["He spoke so tenderly and wisely to me, and he put so plainly before me all I had myself imperfectly thought and hoped in my better state of mind, that, penetrated as I had been with fervent gratitude towards him through so many years, I believed I had never loved him so dearly, never thanked him in my heart so fully, as I did that night.", "그는 너무도 다정하고 지혜롭게 나에게 말해주었고, 내 마음이 더 나은 상태에 있을 때 나 스스로 어렴풋이 생각하고 바랐던 모든 것을 너무도 또렷이 내 앞에 펼쳐 보여주어서, 그토록 오랜 세월 그에게 뜨거운 감사로 가득 차 있던 나였지만, 그날 밤만큼 그를 깊이 사랑한 적도, 마음속으로 그토록 온전히 그에게 감사한 적도 없었던 것 같았습니다."], ["And when he had taken me to my room and kissed me at the door, and when at last I lay down to sleep, my thought was how could I ever be busy enough, how could I ever be good enough, how in my little way could I ever hope to be forgetful enough of myself, devoted enough to him, and useful enough to others, to show him how I blessed and honoured him.", "그리고 그가 나를 내 방으로 데려다주고 문 앞에서 입 맞추어주었을 때, 마침내 내가 잠자리에 누웠을 때, 내 생각은 이러했습니다. 내가 어떻게 해야 충분히 부지런할 수 있을까, 어떻게 해야 충분히 선해질 수 있을까, 나의 보잘것없는 방식으로나마 어떻게 해야 나 자신을 충분히 잊고, 그에게 충분히 헌신하고, 다른 이들에게 충분히 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어, 내가 그를 얼마나 축복하고 우러르는지 보여드릴 수 있을까."]], [["The Letter and the Answer\nMy guardian called me into his room next morning, and then I told him what had been left untold on the previous night.", "이튿날 아침 후견인이 나를 방으로 불렀고, 나는 그제야 어젯밤 다 하지 못했던 이야기를 그에게 들려주었습니다."], ["There was nothing to be done, he said, but to keep the secret and to avoid another such encounter as that of yesterday.", "후견인은 비밀을 지키는 것과 어제 같은 만남을 다시는 겪지 않도록 피하는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없다고 말했습니다."], ["He understood my feeling and entirely shared it.", "그는 내 마음을 이해했고 전적으로 함께 느꼈습니다."], ["He charged himself even with restraining Mr. Skimpole from improving his opportunity.", "심지어 스킴폴 씨가 그 기회를 함부로 이용하지 못하도록 단속하는 일까지 자신이 떠맡았습니다."], ["One person whom he need not name to me, it was not now possible for him to advise or help.", "나에게 굳이 이름을 댈 필요가 없는 그 한 사람에 대해서는, 이제 그가 조언하거나 도울 길이 없었습니다."], ["He wished it were, but no such thing could be.", "그러기를 바랐지만 그럴 수 없는 일이었습니다."], ["If her mistrust of the lawyer whom she had mentioned were well-founded, which he scarcely doubted, he dreaded discovery.", "그녀가 언급했던 변호사를 향한 불신이 근거 있는 것이라면, 그리고 그는 그것을 거의 의심하지 않았는데, 그는 발각을 두려워했습니다."], ["He knew something of him, both by sight and by reputation, and it was certain that he was a dangerous man.", "그는 그 변호사에 대해 얼굴로도 평판으로도 어느 정도 알고 있었고, 그가 위험한 인물이라는 것만큼은 분명했습니다."], ["Whatever happened, he repeatedly impressed upon me with anxious affection and kindness, I was as innocent of as himself and as unable to influence.", "무슨 일이 벌어지든, 나는 그 자신과 마찬가지로 결백하며 그 일에 아무런 영향도 미칠 수 없다는 것을, 그는 근심 어린 애정과 다정함으로 거듭 나에게 새겨주었습니다."]], [["“Nor do I understand,”", "\"게다가 내가 알기로는,\""], ["said he, “that any doubts tend towards you, my dear.", "그가 말했습니다, \"어떤 의심도 네 쪽으로 향하지는 않는단다, 얘야."], ["Much suspicion may exist without that connexion.”", "그런 연관이 없어도 의심은 얼마든지 있을 수 있는 거야.\""]], [["“With the lawyer,”", "\"변호사 말고도요,\""], ["I returned.", "나는 대답했습니다."], ["“But two other persons have come into my mind since I have been anxious.", "\"하지만 마음이 불안해진 뒤로 다른 두 사람이 떠올랐어요.\" 그러고 나서 나는 거피 씨에 대한 모든 것을 그에게 털어놓았습니다."], ["Then I told him all about Mr. Guppy, who I feared might have had his vague surmises when I little understood his meaning, but in whose silence after our last interview I expressed perfect confidence.”", "내가 그의 뜻을 거의 알아차리지 못하던 시절에 그가 막연한 추측을 품었을지 모른다는 게 두려웠지만, 지난번 만남 이후 그가 침묵을 지키는 것에는 완전한 신뢰를 보낸다고 말했습니다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“Then we may dismiss him for the present.", "\"그렇다면 그 사람은 당분간 제쳐두어도 되겠구나."], ["Who is the other?”", "다른 한 사람은 누구냐?\""]], [["I called to his recollection the French maid and the eager offer of herself she had made to me.", "나는 프랑스인 하녀와, 그녀가 나에게 자신을 써달라며 보였던 그 간절한 제안을 그에게 떠올리게 했습니다."]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["he returned thoughtfully.", "그가 생각에 잠겨 대답했습니다."], ["“That is a more alarming person than the clerk.", "\"그 여자는 서기보다 더 경계해야 할 사람이로구나."], ["But after all, my dear, it was but seeking for a new service.", "하지만 얘야, 결국 그건 새 일자리를 구하려던 것뿐이었어."], ["She had seen you and Ada a little while before, and it was natural that you should come into her head.", "그 여자는 얼마 전에 너와 에이다를 본 적이 있으니, 네 생각이 머릿속에 떠오른 건 자연스러운 일이지."], ["She merely proposed herself for your maid, you know.", "그저 네 하녀가 되겠다고 제 발로 나선 것뿐이잖니."], ["She did nothing more.”", "그 이상은 아무것도 하지 않았어.\""]], [["“Her manner was strange,”", "\"그 여자의 태도가 이상했어요,\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, and her manner was strange when she took her shoes off and showed that cool relish for a walk that might have ended in her death-bed,”", "\"그래, 그 여자가 신발을 벗어 던지고 제 죽음의 자리로 끝날 수도 있었던 그 걸음을 태연히 즐기겠다고 했을 때도 태도가 이상했지,\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“It would be useless self-distress and torment to reckon up such chances and possibilities.", "\"그런 가능성과 우연을 일일이 따지는 건 쓸데없이 스스로를 괴롭히고 고통스럽게 하는 일이야."], ["There are very few harmless circumstances that would not seem full of perilous meaning, so considered.", "그렇게 따지고 들면, 위태로운 의미로 가득 차 보이지 않을 무해한 정황이란 거의 없단다."], ["Be hopeful, little woman.", "희망을 갖거라, 우리 작은 아가씨."], ["You can be nothing better than yourself; be that, through this knowledge, as you were before you had it.", "너는 너 자신보다 더 나은 무엇이 될 수는 없어. 그러니 이 사실을 알기 전과 똑같이, 이 사실을 알고도 그저 너 자신으로 있으렴."], ["It is the best you can do for everybody’s sake.", "그것이 모두를 위해 네가 할 수 있는 최선이야."], ["I, sharing the secret with you—”", "너와 비밀을 나누어 가진 나로서는—\""]], [["“And lightening it, guardian, so much,”", "\"게다가 그 비밀을 이렇게나 가볍게 덜어주시는 후견인께서는,\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["“—will be attentive to what passes in that family, so far as I can observe it from my distance.", "\"—내가 멀리서나마 살필 수 있는 한, 그 집안에서 벌어지는 일에 마음을 쓰마."], ["And if the time should come when I can stretch out a hand to render the least service to one whom it is better not to name even here, I will not fail to do it for her dear daughter’s sake.”", "그리고 여기서조차 이름을 부르지 않는 게 나은 그분께 손을 내밀어 아주 작은 도움이라도 드릴 수 있는 때가 온다면, 그분의 사랑하는 따님을 위해서라도 나는 반드시 그 일을 하겠다.\""]], [["I thanked him with my whole heart.", "나는 온 마음을 다해 그에게 고마워했습니다."], ["What could I ever do but thank him!", "고마워하는 것 말고 내가 무엇을 더 할 수 있었겠습니까!"], ["I was going out at the door when he asked me to stay a moment.", "내가 문을 나서려는데 그가 잠깐만 머물라고 했습니다."], ["Quickly turning round, I saw that same expression on his face again; and all at once, I don’t know how, it flashed upon me as a new and far-off possibility that I understood it.", "얼른 돌아서니, 그의 얼굴에 또다시 그 같은 표정이 떠올라 있는 것이 보였습니다. 그러자 별안간, 어떻게 그랬는지는 모르지만, 그 표정의 의미를 내가 안다는 것이 새롭고도 아득한 가능성으로 머릿속에 번뜩였습니다."]], [["“My dear Esther,”", "\"사랑하는 에스더야,\""], ["said my guardian, “I have long had something in my thoughts that I have wished to say to you.”", "후견인이 말했습니다, \"너에게 하고 싶은 이야기가 오래전부터 내 마음속에 있었단다.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말요?\""]], [["“I have had some difficulty in approaching it, and I still have.", "\"그 이야기를 꺼내기가 어려웠고, 지금도 여전히 어렵구나."], ["I should wish it to be so deliberately said, and so deliberately considered.", "그것을 아주 신중하게 말하고 또 아주 신중하게 헤아려보았으면 한다."], ["Would you object to my writing it?”", "내가 그것을 글로 써도 괜찮겠니?\""]], [["“Dear guardian, how could I object to your writing anything for ME to read?”", "\"사랑하는 후견인, 제가 읽을 것을 후견인께서 무엇이든 써주시겠다는데 제가 어떻게 마다하겠어요?\""]], [["“Then see, my love,”", "\"그럼 보거라, 얘야,\""], ["said he with his cheery smile, “am I at this moment quite as plain and easy—do I seem as open, as honest and old-fashioned—as I am at any time?”", "그가 명랑한 미소를 지으며 말했습니다, \"지금 이 순간 내가 여느 때처럼 더없이 솔직하고 편안해 보이느냐—내가 평소와 똑같이 열려 있고, 정직하고, 예스럽게 보이느냐?\""]], [["I answered in all earnestness, “Quite.”", "나는 더없이 진지하게 대답했습니다, \"그럼요.\""], ["With the strictest truth, for his momentary hesitation was gone (it had not lasted a minute), and his fine, sensible, cordial, sterling manner was restored.", "그것은 가장 엄밀한 진실이었습니다. 그의 순간적인 망설임은 사라졌고(그것은 일 분도 가지 않았습니다), 그의 훌륭하고 분별 있고 다정하고 진실한 태도가 되돌아와 있었기 때문입니다."]], [["“Do I look as if I suppressed anything, meant anything but what I said, had any reservation at all, no matter what?”", "\"내가 무언가를 감추거나, 말한 것과 다른 뜻을 품거나, 무엇이든 조금이라도 숨기는 것이 있어 보이느냐?\""], ["said he with his bright clear eyes on mine.", "그가 맑고 밝은 눈으로 내 눈을 들여다보며 말했습니다."]], [["I answered, most assuredly he did not.", "나는 결단코 그렇지 않다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“Can you fully trust me, and thoroughly rely on what I profess, Esther?”", "\"너는 나를 온전히 믿을 수 있겠느냐, 그리고 내가 다짐하는 바에 철저히 기댈 수 있겠느냐, 에스더?\""]], [["“Most thoroughly,”", "\"더없이 철저히요,\""], ["said I with my whole heart.", "나는 온 마음을 담아 말했습니다."]], [["“My dear girl,”", "\"착한 아이로구나,\""], ["returned my guardian, “give me your hand.”", "후견인이 대답했습니다, \"네 손을 다오.\""]], [["He took it in his, holding me lightly with his arm, and looking down into my face with the same genuine freshness and faithfulness of manner—the old protecting manner which had made that house my home in a moment—said, “You have wrought changes in me, little woman, since the winter day in the stage-coach.", "그는 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고, 팔로 나를 가볍게 받친 채, 그 변함없이 진실하고 신선하며 한결같은 태도로—한순간에 그 집을 내 보금자리로 만들어준 그 옛날의 보살피는 태도로—내 얼굴을 내려다보며 말했습니다. \"역마차를 타고 가던 그 겨울날 이후로, 너는 나를 바꾸어놓았단다, 작은 아가씨."], ["First and last you have done me a world of good since that time.”", "그때부터 줄곧, 처음부터 끝까지, 너는 나에게 한없는 좋은 일을 해주었어.\""]], [["“Ah, guardian, what have you done for me since that time!”", "\"아, 후견인, 그때부터 후견인께서 저에게 해주신 게 얼마나 많은데요!\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said he, “that is not to be remembered now.”", "그가 말했습니다, \"지금은 그건 떠올릴 일이 아니야.\""]], [["“It never can be forgotten.”", "\"결코 잊을 수 없는걸요.\""]], [["“Yes, Esther,”", "\"그래, 에스더,\""], ["said he with a gentle seriousness, “it is to be forgotten now, to be forgotten for a while.", "그가 부드러운 진지함으로 말했습니다, \"지금은 그걸 잊어야 한다, 잠시 동안만이라도 잊어야 해."], ["You are only to remember now that nothing can change me as you know me.", "지금 네가 기억해야 할 건 오직, 네가 아는 그대로의 나를 그 무엇도 바꿀 수 없다는 것뿐이야."], ["Can you feel quite assured of that, my dear?”", "그것만큼은 확실히 믿을 수 있겠느냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“I can, and I do,”", "\"믿을 수 있고, 또 믿어요,\""], ["I said.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["“That’s much,” he answered.", "\"그것만으로도 대단한 거야,\""], ["“That’s everything.", "그가 대답했습니다. \"그게 전부지."], ["But I must not take that at a word.", "하지만 나는 그 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들이지는 않으마."], ["I will not write this something in my thoughts until you have quite resolved within yourself that nothing can change me as you know me.", "네가 아는 그대로의 나를 그 무엇도 바꿀 수 없다는 걸 네 마음속에서 온전히 결심하기 전까지는, 내 마음속의 이 이야기를 쓰지 않겠다. 네가 그것을 조금이라도 의심한다면, 나는 결코 그것을 쓰지 않을 거야."], ["If you doubt that in the least degree, I will never write it.", "잘 헤아려본 끝에 그것을 확신한다면, 이레 뒤 오늘 밤 찰리를 나에게 보내거라—'편지를 가지러'"], ["If you are sure of that, on good consideration, send Charley to me this night week—‘for the letter.’ But if you are not quite certain, never send.", "말이다. 하지만 네가 온전히 확신하지 못한다면, 절대 보내지 마라."], ["Mind, I trust to your truth, in this thing as in everything.", "명심하렴, 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 이 일에서도 나는 네 진실함을 믿는다."], ["If you are not quite certain on that one point, never send!”", "그 한 가지 점에 대해 온전히 확신이 서지 않거든, 절대 보내지 마라!\""]], [["“Guardian,”", "\"후견인,\""], ["said I, “I am already certain, I can no more be changed in that conviction than you can be changed towards me.", "나는 말했습니다, \"저는 이미 확신해요. 후견인께서 저를 향한 마음을 바꿀 수 없는 것과 마찬가지로, 저 역시 그 믿음을 바꿀 수 없어요."], ["I shall send Charley for the letter.”", "편지를 가지러 찰리를 보내겠어요.\""]], [["He shook my hand and said no more.", "그는 내 손을 흔들고는 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다."], ["Nor was any more said in reference to this conversation, either by him or me, through the whole week.", "그도 나도, 그 한 주 내내 이 대화에 관해서는 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않았습니다."], ["When the appointed night came, I said to Charley as soon as I was alone, “Go and knock at Mr. Jarndyce’s door, Charley, and say you have come from me—‘for the letter.’”", "약속한 밤이 오자, 나는 혼자가 되자마자 찰리에게 말했습니다, \"잔다이스 씨 방문을 두드리고 가서, 내가 보냈다고 말씀드려—'편지를 가지러'"], ["Charley went up the stairs, and down the stairs, and along the passages—the zig-zag way about the old-fashioned house seemed very long in my listening ears that night—and so came back, along the passages, and down the stairs, and up the stairs, and brought the letter.", "왔다고.\" 찰리는 계단을 오르고, 계단을 내려가고, 복도를 따라 걸어갔습니다—그 예스러운 집의 구불구불한 길이 그날 밤 귀를 기울이고 있던 나에게는 무척 길게만 느껴졌습니다—그러고는 복도를 따라, 계단을 내려가, 계단을 올라 돌아와서 편지를 가져왔습니다."], ["“Lay it on the table, Charley,” said I.", "\"탁자 위에 놓아두렴, 찰리,\""], ["So Charley laid it on the table and went to bed, and I sat looking at it without taking it up, thinking of many things.", "나는 말했습니다. 찰리는 그것을 탁자 위에 놓고 잠자리에 들었고, 나는 그것을 집어 들지도 않은 채 그저 바라보며 앉아 온갖 것들을 생각했습니다."]], [["I began with my overshadowed childhood, and passed through those timid days to the heavy time when my aunt lay dead, with her resolute face so cold and set, and when I was more solitary with Mrs.", "나는 그늘에 덮였던 어린 시절에서 시작해, 그 주눅 든 나날들을 거쳐, 이모가 그 단호한 얼굴을 차갑고 굳은 채 죽어 누워 있던 그 무거운 시절에 이르렀습니다."], ["Rachael than if I had had no one in the world to speak to or to look at.", "그때 나는 세상에 말을 건넬 사람도, 바라볼 사람도 하나 없는 것보다 더 외롭게 레이철 부인과 함께 있었습니다."], ["I passed to the altered days when I was so blest as to find friends in all around me, and to be beloved.", "그러고는 내가 둘러싼 모든 이들에게서 벗을 찾고 사랑을 받는 큰 복을 누렸던, 달라진 나날로 넘어갔습니다."], ["I came to the time when I first saw my dear girl and was received into that sisterly affection which was the grace and beauty of my life.", "사랑하는 그 아이를 처음 보고, 내 삶의 우아함이자 아름다움이었던 그 자매 같은 애정 속으로 받아들여진 그때에 이르렀습니다."], ["I recalled the first bright gleam of welcome which had shone out of those very windows upon our expectant faces on that cold bright night, and which had never paled.", "그 춥고 맑던 밤, 우리의 기대에 찬 얼굴들 위로 바로 그 창문에서 빛나던, 한 번도 빛바랜 적 없는 환영의 첫 밝은 빛줄기를 떠올렸습니다."], ["I lived my happy life there over again, I went through my illness and recovery, I thought of myself so altered and of those around me so unchanged; and all this happiness shone like a light from one central figure, represented before me by the letter on the table.", "나는 그곳에서 보낸 행복한 삶을 다시 살아냈고, 병과 회복을 다시 겪었으며, 그토록 달라진 나 자신과 그토록 변함없는 내 주위 사람들을 생각했습니다. 그리고 이 모든 행복은 하나의 중심 인물에게서 비치는 빛처럼 빛났으니, 그 인물은 탁자 위의 편지로 내 앞에 놓여 있었습니다."]], [["I opened it and read it.", "나는 편지를 열어 읽었습니다."], ["It was so impressive in its love for me, and in the unselfish caution it gave me, and the consideration it showed for me in every word, that my eyes were too often blinded to read much at a time.", "그것은 나를 향한 사랑에서, 또 나에게 베푼 사심 없는 당부에서, 한 마디 한 마디에 담긴 나를 향한 배려에서 너무도 절절해, 내 눈은 한 번에 많이 읽지 못하도록 자꾸만 흐려졌습니다."], ["But I read it through three times before I laid it down.", "그래도 나는 그것을 내려놓기 전에 세 번 통독했습니다."], ["I had thought beforehand that I knew its purport, and I did.", "미리부터 나는 그 내용을 안다고 생각했고, 정말 알고 있었습니다."], ["It asked me, would I be the mistress of Bleak House.", "그것은 나에게, 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되어주겠느냐고 묻고 있었습니다."]], [["It was not a love letter, though it expressed so much love, but was written just as he would at any time have spoken to me.", "그것은 그토록 많은 사랑을 담고 있으면서도 연애편지는 아니었으니, 그가 언제든 나에게 말하던 그대로 쓰여 있었습니다."], ["I saw his face, and heard his voice, and felt the influence of his kind protecting manner in every line.", "나는 모든 줄에서 그의 얼굴을 보았고, 그의 목소리를 들었으며, 그 다정하고 보살피는 태도의 영향을 느꼈습니다."], ["It addressed me as if our places were reversed, as if all the good deeds had been mine and all the feelings they had awakened his.", "그것은 마치 우리의 처지가 뒤바뀐 듯이, 마치 모든 선행이 나의 것이고 그 선행이 일깨운 모든 감정이 그의 것인 듯이 나에게 말을 건넸습니다."], ["It dwelt on my being young, and he past the prime of life; on his having attained a ripe age, while I was a child; on his writing to me with a silvered head, and knowing all this so well as to set it in full before me for mature deliberation.", "그것은 내가 젊고 그는 한창때를 지났다는 것, 그가 무르익은 나이에 이른 반면 나는 아이였다는 것, 그가 은빛 머리로 나에게 글을 쓰고 있으며 이 모든 것을 너무도 잘 알기에 신중히 헤아려보라고 빠짐없이 펼쳐 보인다는 것을 거듭 짚었습니다."], ["It told me that I would gain nothing by such a marriage and lose nothing by rejecting it, for no new relation could enhance the tenderness in which he held me, and whatever my decision was, he was certain it would be right.", "그것은, 그런 결혼으로 내가 얻을 것은 아무것도 없고 그것을 거절한다 해도 잃을 것은 아무것도 없으니, 어떤 새로운 관계도 그가 나를 품은 그 다정함을 더 키울 수 없으며, 내 결정이 무엇이든 그것이 옳으리라 그는 확신한다고 했습니다."], ["But he had considered this step anew since our late confidence and had decided on taking it, if it only served to show me through one poor instance that the whole world would readily unite to falsify the stern prediction of my childhood.", "다만 그는 우리가 최근에 나눈 속내 이후 이 걸음을 새로이 헤아려보았고, 비록 그것이 단 한 가지 보잘것없는 사례를 통해서라도 온 세상이 내 어린 시절의 그 가혹한 예언을 기꺼이 거짓으로 만들어줄 것임을 나에게 보여주는 데 도움이 된다면, 그 걸음을 내딛기로 마음먹었다고 했습니다."], ["I was the last to know what happiness I could bestow upon him, but of that he said no more, for I was always to remember that I owed him nothing and that he was my debtor, and for very much.", "내가 그에게 어떤 행복을 줄 수 있는지를 가장 늦게 알아차린 건 나였지만, 그것에 대해서는 그가 더 말하지 않았으니, 나는 그에게 빚진 것이 아무것도 없으며 오히려 그가 나에게 빚진 자이고, 그것도 아주 많이 빚졌다는 것을 늘 기억해야 하기 때문이라고 했습니다."], ["He had often thought of our future, and foreseeing that the time must come, and fearing that it might come soon, when Ada (now very nearly of age) would leave us, and when our present mode of life must be broken up, had become accustomed to reflect on this proposal.", "그는 우리의 앞날을 자주 생각해왔는데, (이제 거의 성년이 다 된) 에이다가 우리를 떠나고 지금의 생활 방식이 깨질 수밖에 없는 때가 반드시 오리라는 것을, 게다가 어쩌면 머지않아 닥칠지도 모른다는 것을 내다보고서, 이 제안을 곱씹는 것이 익숙해졌다고 했습니다. 그렇게 해서 그가 이 제안을 한 것이었습니다."], ["Thus he made it. If I felt that I could ever give him the best right he could have to be my protector, and if I felt that I could happily and justly become the dear companion of his remaining life, superior to all lighter chances and changes than death, even then he could not have me bind myself irrevocably while this letter was yet so new to me, but even then I must have ample time for reconsideration.", "만약 내가 그에게 나의 보호자가 될 더없이 마땅한 권리를 언젠가 줄 수 있다고 느낀다면, 그리고 죽음 말고는 그 어떤 사소한 우연이나 변화도 넘어서서, 그의 남은 삶의 사랑스러운 동반자가 행복하고 떳떳하게 될 수 있다고 느낀다면, 그렇다 해도 이 편지가 아직 이토록 새로운 동안에는 나를 돌이킬 수 없이 옭아매게 할 수 없으며, 그런 경우에도 다시 헤아려볼 넉넉한 시간을 반드시 가져야 한다고 했습니다."], ["In that case, or in the opposite case, let him be unchanged in his old relation, in his old manner, in the old name by which I called him.", "그 경우든 그 반대의 경우든, 그는 옛 관계에서, 옛 태도에서, 내가 그를 부르던 옛 이름에서 변함이 없도록 해달라고 했습니다."], ["And as to his bright Dame Durden and little housekeeper, she would ever be the same, he knew.", "그리고 그의 밝은 더든 아주머니이자 작은 살림꾼에 대해서는, 그녀가 언제까지나 한결같으리라는 것을 자기는 안다고 했습니다."]], [["This was the substance of the letter, written throughout with a justice and a dignity as if he were indeed my responsible guardian impartially representing the proposal of a friend against whom in his integrity he stated the full case.", "이것이 편지의 알맹이였습니다. 그것은 처음부터 끝까지, 마치 그가 정말로 나의 책임진 후견인으로서 한 벗의 제안을 공정하게 대변하면서도, 그 청렴함으로 그 벗에게 불리한 사정까지 빠짐없이 진술하기라도 하듯, 그런 공정함과 품위로 쓰여 있었습니다."]], [["But he did not hint to me that when I had been better looking he had had this same proceeding in his thoughts and had refrained from it.", "그러나 그는, 내가 더 보기 좋았던 시절에도 이와 똑같은 일을 마음에 품었다가 삼갔다는 것은 내비치지 않았습니다."], ["That when my old face was gone from me, and I had no attractions, he could love me just as well as in my fairer days.", "내 옛 얼굴이 사라지고 내가 아무 매력도 없게 되었어도, 그가 더 곱던 시절과 똑같이 나를 사랑할 수 있다는 것도."], ["That the discovery of my birth gave him no shock.", "내 출생의 비밀이 밝혀진 일이 그에게 아무런 충격도 주지 않았다는 것도."], ["That his generosity rose above my disfigurement and my inheritance of shame.", "그의 너그러움이 내 흉한 모습과 내가 물려받은 수치를 넘어선다는 것도."], ["That the more I stood in need of such fidelity, the more firmly I might trust in him to the last.", "내가 그런 신의를 필요로 하면 할수록, 끝까지 그를 더 굳게 믿어도 된다는 것도, 그는 내비치지 않았습니다."]], [["But I knew it, I knew it well now.", "그러나 나는 알았습니다, 이제는 그것을 잘 알았습니다."], ["It came upon me as the close of the benignant history I had been pursuing, and I felt that I had but one thing to do.", "그것은 내가 좇아온 그 자애로운 이야기의 마무리로 나에게 다가왔고, 나는 내가 할 일이 단 하나뿐임을 느꼈습니다."], ["To devote my life to his happiness was to thank him poorly, and what had I wished for the other night but some new means of thanking him?", "내 삶을 그의 행복에 바치는 것은 그에게 보잘것없이 고마워하는 일에 지나지 않았건만, 며칠 전 그날 밤 내가 바란 것이 바로 그에게 고마워할 어떤 새로운 길이 아니었던가요?"]], [["Still I cried very much, not only in the fullness of my heart after reading the letter, not only in the strangeness of the prospect—for it was strange though I had expected the contents—but as if something for which there was no name or distinct idea were indefinitely lost to me.", "그래도 나는 무척 많이 울었습니다. 편지를 읽은 뒤 벅차오른 가슴 때문만도, 그 앞날의 낯섦 때문만도 아니었습니다—내용을 예상하고 있었는데도 그것은 낯설었지만—이름도 또렷한 형체도 없는 무언가가 어렴풋이 나에게서 사라져버린 것만 같았기 때문이었습니다."], ["I was very happy, very thankful, very hopeful; but I cried very much.", "나는 무척 행복했고, 무척 고마웠고, 무척 희망에 차 있었습니다. 그런데도 나는 무척 많이 울었습니다."]], [["By and by I went to my old glass.", "이윽고 나는 내 오래된 거울 앞으로 갔습니다."], ["My eyes were red and swollen, and I said, “Oh, Esther, Esther, can that be you!”", "두 눈이 붉게 부어 있어서 나는 말했습니다, \"오, 에스더, 에스더, 그게 정말 너란 말이니!\""], ["I am afraid the face in the glass was going to cry again at this reproach, but I held up my finger at it, and it stopped.", "거울 속 얼굴이 이 책망에 또 울려는 듯했지만, 나는 그것을 향해 손가락을 치켜들었고, 그러자 그것은 멈추었습니다."]], [["“That is more like the composed look you comforted me with, my dear, when you showed me such a change!”", "\"네가 그토록 달라진 모습을 나에게 보여주며 나를 위로하던 그 차분한 표정에 한결 가깝구나, 얘야!\""], ["said I, beginning to let down my hair.", "나는 머리를 풀어 내리기 시작하며 말했습니다."], ["“When you are mistress of Bleak House, you are to be as cheerful as a bird.", "\"네가 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되거든 새처럼 명랑해야 한다."], ["In fact, you are always to be cheerful; so let us begin for once and for all.”", "사실은 언제까지나 명랑해야지. 그러니 이번 한 번으로 영원히, 그렇게 시작해보자꾸나.\""]], [["I went on with my hair now, quite comfortably.", "나는 이제 아주 편안하게 머리를 매만져 나갔습니다."], ["I sobbed a little still, but that was because I had been crying, not because I was crying then.", "아직 조금 흐느끼긴 했지만, 그건 내가 울었기 때문이었지 그때 울고 있었기 때문은 아니었습니다."]], [["“And so Esther, my dear, you are happy for life.", "\"그러니 에스더야, 얘야, 너는 평생 행복한 거란다."], ["Happy with your best friends, happy in your old home, happy in the power of doing a great deal of good, and happy in the undeserved love of the best of men.”", "더없이 좋은 벗들과 함께 행복하고, 정든 보금자리에서 행복하고, 많은 선을 베풀 힘을 지녀 행복하고, 가장 훌륭한 분의 분에 넘치는 사랑을 받아 행복하지.\""]], [["I thought, all at once, if my guardian had married some one else, how should I have felt, and what should I have done!", "문득 나는, 후견인이 다른 누군가와 결혼했더라면 나는 어떤 기분이었을지, 또 나는 어떻게 했을지를 생각했습니다!"], ["That would have been a change indeed.", "그것이야말로 정말 큰 변화였을 것입니다."], ["It presented my life in such a new and blank form that I rang my housekeeping keys and gave them a kiss before I laid them down in their basket again.", "그것은 내 삶을 너무도 새롭고 텅 빈 모습으로 그려 보여서, 나는 살림 열쇠들을 흔들어 본 뒤 다시 바구니에 내려놓기 전에 그것들에 입을 맞추었습니다."]], [["Then I went on to think, as I dressed my hair before the glass, how often had I considered within myself that the deep traces of my illness and the circumstances of my birth were only new reasons why I should be busy, busy, busy—useful, amiable, serviceable, in all honest, unpretending ways.", "그러고 나서 거울 앞에서 머리를 매만지며, 나는 계속 생각해 나갔습니다. 내 병의 깊은 자국과 내 출생의 사정이야말로 내가 부지런하고, 부지런하고, 또 부지런해야 할—정직하고 겸손한 온갖 방식으로 쓸모 있고, 상냥하고, 도움이 되어야 할—새로운 이유에 지나지 않는다고, 나는 얼마나 자주 마음속으로 생각해왔던가요."], ["This was a good time, to be sure, to sit down morbidly and cry!", "정말이지, 지금이야말로 병적으로 주저앉아 울기에 딱 좋은 때로군요!"], ["As to its seeming at all strange to me at first (if that were any excuse for crying, which it was not) that I was one day to be the mistress of Bleak House, why should it seem strange?", "내가 언젠가 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되리라는 것이 처음에 조금이라도 낯설게 느껴졌다는 것(그것이 울 핑계가 된다면 말이지만, 그렇지도 않았습니다)에 대해서라면, 그게 왜 낯설게 느껴져야 하나요?"], ["Other people had thought of such things, if I had not.", "내가 그런 것을 생각해보지 못했다 해도, 다른 사람들은 그런 일들을 생각해보았으니까요."], ["“Don’t you remember, my plain dear,”", "\"기억나지 않니, 못난 우리 아가씨,\""], ["I asked myself, looking at the glass, “what Mrs. Woodcourt said before those scars were there about your marrying—”", "나는 거울을 보며 나 자신에게 물었습니다, \"이 흉터들이 생기기 전에 우드코트 부인이 네 결혼에 대해 뭐라고 말했는지—\""]], [["Perhaps the name brought them to my remembrance.", "아마 그 이름이 그것들을 내 기억으로 불러왔나 봅니다."], ["The dried remains of the flowers.", "그 꽃의 말라붙은 잔해 말입니다."], ["It would be better not to keep them now.", "이제는 그것을 간직하지 않는 편이 나을 것입니다."], ["They had only been preserved in memory of something wholly past and gone, but it would be better not to keep them now.", "그것은 다만 완전히 지나가 사라진 무언가를 기억하기 위해 보관해온 것이었지만, 이제는 간직하지 않는 편이 나을 것입니다."]], [["They were in a book, and it happened to be in the next room—our sitting-room, dividing Ada’s chamber from mine.", "그것들은 어느 책 속에 끼워져 있었는데, 마침 그 책은 옆방에—에이다의 침실과 내 침실을 가르는 우리 거실에 있었습니다."], ["I took a candle and went softly in to fetch it from its shelf.", "나는 촛불을 들고 살그머니 들어가 그것을 선반에서 가져왔습니다."], ["After I had it in my hand, I saw my beautiful darling, through the open door, lying asleep, and I stole in to kiss her.", "그것을 손에 쥐고 나니, 열린 문 너머로 아름다운 내 사랑하는 아이가 잠들어 누워 있는 것이 보여, 나는 살며시 들어가 그 아이에게 입을 맞추었습니다."]], [["It was weak in me, I know, and I could have no reason for crying; but I dropped a tear upon her dear face, and another, and another.", "내가 마음이 약했다는 걸 압니다, 그리고 울 까닭이라곤 도무지 없었습니다. 그런데도 나는 그 사랑스러운 얼굴 위에 눈물 한 방울을, 또 한 방울을, 또 한 방울을 떨구었습니다."], ["Weaker than that, I took the withered flowers out and put them for a moment to her lips.", "그보다 더 약하게도, 나는 그 시든 꽃들을 꺼내어 잠깐 그 아이의 입술에 갖다 대었습니다."], ["I thought about her love for Richard, though, indeed, the flowers had nothing to do with that.", "나는 리처드를 향한 그 아이의 사랑을 생각했습니다. 사실 그 꽃들은 그것과 아무 상관이 없었지만요."], ["Then I took them into my own room and burned them at the candle, and they were dust in an instant.", "그러고는 그것들을 내 방으로 가져가 촛불에 태웠고, 그것들은 순식간에 재가 되었습니다."]], [["On entering the breakfast-room next morning, I found my guardian just as usual, quite as frank, as open, and free.", "이튿날 아침 식당으로 들어가 보니, 후견인은 여느 때와 똑같이, 더없이 솔직하고, 열려 있고, 거리낌이 없었습니다."], ["There being not the least constraint in his manner, there was none (or I think there was none) in mine.", "그의 태도에 조금의 어색함도 없었기에, 내 태도에도 어색함이 없었습니다(아니, 없었다고 나는 생각합니다)."], ["I was with him several times in the course of the morning, in and out, when there was no one there, and I thought it not unlikely that he might speak to me about the letter, but he did not say a word.", "오전 내내 나는 아무도 없을 때 여러 번 그와 드나들며 함께 있었고, 어쩌면 그가 편지에 대해 나에게 말을 꺼낼지도 모르겠다고 생각했지만, 그는 한마디도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["So, on the next morning, and the next, and for at least a week, over which time Mr. Skimpole prolonged his stay.", "그렇게, 다음 날 아침에도, 그다음 날에도, 스킴폴 씨가 묵어가던 적어도 한 주 내내 그러했습니다."], ["I expected, every day, that my guardian might speak to me about the letter, but he never did.", "나는 날마다 후견인이 편지에 대해 말을 꺼낼지도 모른다고 기대했지만, 그는 끝내 말하지 않았습니다."]], [["I thought then, growing uneasy, that I ought to write an answer.", "그러자 나는 마음이 불안해지면서, 내가 답장을 써야 한다고 생각했습니다."], ["I tried over and over again in my own room at night, but I could not write an answer that at all began like a good answer, so I thought each night I would wait one more day.", "밤마다 내 방에서 몇 번이고 거듭 써보았지만, 좋은 답장처럼 시작이라도 되는 답장을 도무지 쓸 수가 없어, 밤마다 하루만 더 기다리자고 생각했습니다."], ["And I waited seven more days, and he never said a word.", "그렇게 나는 이레를 더 기다렸지만, 그는 끝내 한마디도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["At last, Mr.", "마침내, 스킴폴 씨가 떠난 뒤 어느 오후, 우리 셋은 말을 타러 나가려던 참이었습니다."], ["Skimpole having departed, we three were one afternoon going out for a ride; and I, being dressed before Ada and going down, came upon my guardian, with his back towards me, standing at the drawing-room window looking out.", "나는 에이다보다 먼저 채비를 마치고 내려가다가, 응접실 창가에 서서 등을 내 쪽으로 돌린 채 밖을 내다보고 있는 후견인과 마주쳤습니다."]], [["He turned on my coming in and said, smiling, “Aye, it’s you, little woman, is it?”", "내가 들어서자 그는 돌아서며 미소를 지으며 말했습니다, \"오, 너로구나, 작은 아가씨, 그렇지?\""], ["and looked out again.", "그러고는 다시 밖을 내다보았습니다."]], [["I had made up my mind to speak to him now.", "나는 이제 그에게 말을 하기로 마음을 굳혔습니다."], ["In short, I had come down on purpose.", "사실은, 일부러 그러려고 내려온 것이었습니다."], ["“Guardian,”", "\"후견인,\""], ["I said, rather hesitating and trembling, “when would you like to have the answer to the letter Charley came for?”", "나는 조금 망설이며 떨리는 목소리로 말했습니다, \"찰리가 가지러 갔던 그 편지의 답을 언제 받고 싶으세요?\""]], [["“When it’s ready, my dear,”", "\"준비되거든 받지, 얘야,\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했습니다."]], [["“I think it is ready,”", "\"준비된 것 같은데요,\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["“Is Charley to bring it?”", "\"찰리가 가져오느냐?\""], ["he asked pleasantly.", "그가 다정하게 물었습니다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["I have brought it myself, guardian,”", "제가 직접 가져왔어요, 후견인,\""], ["I returned.", "나는 대답했습니다."]], [["I put my two arms round his neck and kissed him, and he said was this the mistress of Bleak House, and I said yes; and it made no difference presently, and we all went out together, and I said nothing to my precious pet about it.", "나는 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸 안고 그에게 입을 맞추었습니다. 그러자 그가 이 사람이 황폐한 집의 안주인이냐고 물었고, 나는 그렇다고 말했습니다."], ["In Trust", "그리고 그것은 곧 아무런 달라짐도 가져오지 않았으니, 우리는 다 함께 밖으로 나갔고, 나는 사랑스러운 그 아이에게는 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["One morning when I had done jingling about with my baskets of keys, as my beauty and I were walking round and round the garden I happened to turn my eyes towards the house and saw a long thin shadow going in which looked like Mr. Vholes.", "어느 날 아침, 내가 열쇠 바구니를 짤랑짤랑 흔들며 정리를 끝낸 뒤, 내 어여쁜 사람과 함께 정원을 빙글빙글 돌며 거닐고 있을 때, 우연히 집 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸다가 길고 가느다란 그림자 하나가 안으로 들어가는 것을 보았는데, 영락없이 볼스 씨 같았다."], ["Ada had been telling me only that morning of her hopes that Richard might exhaust his ardour in the Chancery suit by being so very earnest in it; and therefore, not to damp my dear girl’s spirits, I said nothing about Mr. Vholes’s shadow.", "에이다는 바로 그날 아침에야, 리처드가 대법원 소송에 그토록 열성을 쏟느라 그 열정을 다 소진하기를 바란다는 희망을 내게 털어놓았던 터라, 나는 사랑하는 그 애의 기운을 꺾지 않으려고 볼스 씨의 그림자에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Presently came Charley, lightly winding among the bushes and tripping along the paths, as rosy and pretty as one of Flora’s attendants instead of my maid, saying, “Oh, if you please, miss, would you step and speak to Mr.", "이윽고 찰리가 덤불 사이를 가볍게 헤치며 오솔길을 종종걸음으로 달려왔는데, 내 하녀라기보다 플로라 여신을 모시는 시녀들 가운데 하나처럼 발그레하고 어여뻤다. 그러면서 \"아, 아가씨, 괜찮으시면, 잔다이스 씨께 가서 말씀 좀 나눠보시겠어요!\""], ["Jarndyce!”", "하고 말했다."]], [["It was one of Charley’s peculiarities that whenever she was charged with a message she always began to deliver it as soon as she beheld, at any distance, the person for whom it was intended.", "찰리에게는 별난 버릇이 하나 있었으니, 전갈을 맡기만 하면 아무리 멀리 떨어져 있어도 대상이 눈에 띄는 순간부터 곧장 그것을 전하기 시작한다는 것이었다."], ["Therefore I saw Charley asking me in her usual form of words to “step and speak” to Mr.", "그래서 나는 찰리가 늘 쓰는 말투로 잔다이스 씨께 \"가서 말씀 좀 나눠보시라\"고 청하는 모습을 그 소리가 들리기 한참 전부터 보았다."], ["Jarndyce long before I heard her. And when I did hear her, she had said it so often that she was out of breath.", "그리고 막상 소리가 들렸을 때는, 그 애가 같은 말을 어찌나 여러 번 되뇌었던지 숨이 차 있었다."]], [["I told Ada I would make haste back and inquired of Charley as we went in whether there was not a gentleman with Mr. Jarndyce.", "나는 에이다에게 얼른 다녀오겠다고 말하고, 안으로 들어가면서 찰리에게 잔다이스 씨와 함께 있는 신사가 있지 않으냐고 물었다."], ["To which Charley, whose grammar, I confess to my shame, never did any credit to my educational powers, replied, “Yes, miss.", "그러자 찰리는—부끄럽지만 고백하건대, 그 애의 문법은 내 가르치는 능력에 도무지 빛을 보태준 적이 없었다—\"네, 아가씨."], ["Him as come down in the country with Mr. Richard.”", "리처드 씨랑 같이 시골에 내려왔던 그분이요\"라고 대답했다."]], [["A more complete contrast than my guardian and Mr.", "내 후견인과 볼스 씨보다 더 완벽한 대조는 아마 있을 수 없으리라."], ["Vholes I suppose there could not be. I found them looking at one another across a table, the one so open and the other so close, the one so broad and upright and the other so narrow and stooping, the one giving out what he had to say in such a rich ringing voice and the other keeping it in in such a cold-blooded, gasping, fish-like manner that I thought I never had seen two people so unmatched.", "두 사람은 탁자를 사이에 두고 서로를 마주 보고 있었는데, 한 사람은 그토록 활짝 트였고 다른 한 사람은 그토록 꽉 닫혔으며, 한 사람은 그토록 넓고 곧았고 다른 한 사람은 그토록 좁고 구부정했으며, 한 사람은 할 말을 그토록 풍부하게 울리는 목소리로 쏟아내는데 다른 한 사람은 그토록 냉혈하고 헐떡이는 물고기 같은 태도로 그것을 안에 가두고 있어서, 나는 이토록 어울리지 않는 두 사람을 본 적이 없다고 생각했다."]], [["“You know Mr. Vholes, my dear,”", "\"너도 볼스 씨를 알지, 얘야.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했다."], ["Not with the greatest urbanity, I must say.", "솔직히 말해, 그다지 정중한 어조는 아니었다."]], [["Mr. Vholes rose, gloved and buttoned up as usual, and seated himself again, just as he had seated himself beside Richard in the gig.", "볼스 씨는 평소처럼 장갑을 끼고 단추를 단단히 채운 채 일어섰다가, 마차에서 리처드 곁에 앉던 그 모습 그대로 다시 자리에 앉았다."], ["Not having Richard to look at, he looked straight before him.", "바라볼 리처드가 없으니, 그는 그저 정면을 똑바로 응시했다."]], [["“Mr. Vholes,”", "\"볼스 씨가,\""], ["said my guardian, eyeing his black figure as if he were a bird of ill omen, “has brought an ugly report of our most unfortunate Rick.” Laying a marked emphasis on “most unfortunate”", "후견인은 그 검은 형체를 흉조의 새라도 보듯 바라보며 말했다, \"우리의 가엾기 짝이 없는 릭에 대해 흉한 소식을 가지고 왔구나.\""], ["as if the words were rather descriptive of his connexion with Mr. Vholes.", "'가엾기 짝이 없는'이라는 말에 또렷이 힘을 주었는데, 마치 그 말이 볼스 씨와 릭의 관계를 묘사하는 표현이라도 되는 듯했다."]], [["I sat down between them; Mr.", "나는 두 사람 사이에 앉았다."], ["Vholes remained immovable, except that he secretly picked at one of the red pimples on his yellow face with his black glove.", "볼스 씨는 검은 장갑을 낀 손으로 누런 얼굴에 돋은 붉은 여드름 하나를 몰래 만지작거리는 것 말고는 미동도 하지 않았다."]], [["“And as Rick and you are happily good friends, I should like to know,”", "\"릭과 자네가 다행히 좋은 친구 사이이니, 나는 네 생각을 알고 싶구나, 얘야.\""], ["said my guardian, “what you think, my dear.", "후견인이 말했다."], ["Would you be so good as to—as to speak up, Mr. Vholes?”", "\"그러니 좀—좀 터놓고 말씀해 주시겠소, 볼스 씨?\""]], [["Doing anything but that, Mr. Vholes observed, “I have been saying that I have reason to know, Miss Summerson, as Mr.", "볼스 씨는 그것만 빼고 다른 모든 짓을 하면서 입을 열었다."], ["C.’s professional adviser, that Mr. C.’s circumstances are at the present moment in an embarrassed state.", "\"저는 카스톤 씨의 법률 고문으로서, 서머슨 양, 카스톤 씨의 형편이 지금 이 순간 곤란한 상태에 놓여 있다는 것을 알 만한 근거가 있다고 말씀드리던 참입니다."], ["Not so much in point of amount as owing to the peculiar and pressing nature of liabilities Mr. C.", "액수가 크다기보다는, 카스톤 씨가 짊어진 부채의 특수하고 절박한 성격과, 그것을 청산하거나 감당할 수단이 마땅찮은 데서 비롯된 곤란이지요."], ["has incurred and the means he has of liquidating or meeting the same. I have staved off many little matters for Mr.", "저는 카스톤 씨를 위해 자잘한 일들을 여럿 막아드렸습니다만, 막아내는 데도 한계가 있는 법이고, 이제 그 한계에 다다랐습니다."], ["C., but there is a limit to staving off, and we have reached it. I have made some advances out of pocket to accommodate these unpleasantnesses, but I necessarily look to being repaid, for I do not pretend to be a man of capital, and I have a father to support in the Vale of Taunton, besides striving to realize some little independence for three dear girls at home.", "이 불쾌한 일들을 메우려고 제 주머니에서 얼마간 융통해 드리기도 했습니다만, 저도 응당 변제를 바라볼 수밖에 없으니, 제가 무슨 자본가 행세를 하는 것도 아니고, 톤턴 골짜기에서 부양해야 할 아버지가 있는 데다 집에는 어엿한 독립을 마련해 주려 애쓰는 사랑하는 세 딸까지 있는 처지라서요."], ["My apprehension is, Mr. C.’s circumstances being such, lest it should end in his obtaining leave to part with his commission, which at all events is desirable to be made known to his connexions.”", "제가 우려하는 바는, 카스톤 씨의 형편이 이러하니, 결국 임관 매각 허가를 받는 것으로 끝나지나 않을까 하는 점인데, 어쨌거나 이 일은 그분의 가까운 분들께 알려드리는 게 바람직하다고 보았습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Vholes, who had looked at me while speaking, here emerged into the silence he could hardly be said to have broken, so stifled was his tone, and looked before him again.", "말하는 내내 나를 바라보던 볼스 씨는, 여기서 깨뜨렸다고 하기도 어려울 만큼—그의 어조가 워낙 답답하게 막혀 있었으므로—그 침묵 속으로 다시 잠겨 들더니, 또다시 정면을 응시했다."]], [["“Imagine the poor fellow without even his present resource,”", "\"가엾은 저 친구가 지금 가진 그 밑천마저 없어진다고 상상해 보거라.\""], ["said my guardian to me.", "후견인이 내게 말했다."], ["“Yet what can I do?", "\"하지만 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠느냐?"], ["You know him, Esther.", "너도 그 애를 알잖느냐, 에스더."], ["He would never accept of help from me now.", "그 애는 이제 나한테서 어떤 도움도 결코 받지 않으려 할 게다."], ["To offer it or hint at it would be to drive him to an extremity, if nothing else did.”", "도움을 내밀거나 그런 기미만 비쳐도, 다른 무엇이 그 애를 막다른 데로 몰지 않더라도, 바로 그 도움이 그 애를 극단으로 몰아붙일 게야.\""]], [["Mr. Vholes hereupon addressed me again.", "이에 볼스 씨가 다시 내게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“What Mr. Jarndyce remarks, miss, is no doubt the case, and is the difficulty.", "\"잔다이스 씨께서 하신 말씀은, 아가씨, 의심할 바 없이 사실이고, 바로 그것이 난관입니다."], ["I do not see that anything is to be done.", "저는 무슨 수가 있다고 보지 않습니다."], ["I do not say that anything is to be done.", "무슨 수를 써야 한다고 말하는 것도 아닙니다."], ["Far from it.", "천만의 말씀이지요."], ["I merely come down here under the seal of confidence and mention it in order that everything may be openly carried on and that it may not be said afterwards that everything was not openly carried on.", "저는 그저 비밀을 지킨다는 약속 아래 이곳에 내려와 이 일을 말씀드리는 것뿐이니, 모든 일이 떳떳하게 처리되도록 하고, 나중에 가서 모든 일이 떳떳하게 처리되지 않았다는 소리가 나오지 않도록 하기 위함입니다."], ["My wish is that everything should be openly carried on.", "제 바람은 모든 일이 떳떳하게 처리되는 것입니다."], ["I desire to leave a good name behind me.", "저는 좋은 이름을 남기고 떠나기를 바랍니다."], ["If I consulted merely my own interests with Mr. C., I should not be here.", "만약 제가 카스톤 씨와의 일에서 오로지 제 이익만 따졌다면 여기 와 있지도 않았을 겁니다."], ["So insurmountable, as you must well know, would be his objections.", "아가씨도 잘 아시다시피, 그분의 반대는 그만큼 넘어설 수 없을 만큼 완강할 테니까요."], ["This is not a professional attendance.", "이것은 업무상 방문이 아닙니다."], ["This can he charged to nobody.", "누구에게도 청구할 수 없는 일입니다."], ["I have no interest in it except as a member of society and a father—AND a son,” said Mr.", "저는 사회의 한 구성원이자 한 아버지로서—그리고 한 아들로서 외에는 이 일에 아무런 이해관계가 없습니다.\""], ["Vholes, who had nearly forgotten that point.", "볼스 씨는 하마터면 그 대목을 잊을 뻔했다."]], [["It appeared to us that Mr. Vholes said neither more nor less than the truth in intimating that he sought to divide the responsibility, such as it was, of knowing Richard’s situation.", "볼스 씨가 리처드의 처지를 안다는 책임—그것이 어떤 것이든—을 나눠 지고자 한다고 넌지시 비친 것은, 우리가 보기에 사실보다 더도 덜도 아닌 말 같았다."], ["I could only suggest that I should go down to Deal, where Richard was then stationed, and see him, and try if it were possible to avert the worst. Without consulting Mr.", "나로서는, 그때 리처드가 주둔하고 있던 딜로 내려가 그를 만나, 최악의 사태를 막을 수 있을지 시도해 보겠다고 제안하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Vholes on this point, I took my guardian aside to propose it, while Mr. Vholes gauntly stalked to the fire and warmed his funeral gloves.", "이 점에 대해서는 볼스 씨와 상의하지 않고, 후견인을 한쪽으로 데려가 그 뜻을 전했는데, 그동안 볼스 씨는 깡마른 모습으로 벽난로 쪽으로 성큼성큼 걸어가 그 장례식 같은 장갑을 데우고 있었다."]], [["The fatigue of the journey formed an immediate objection on my guardian’s part, but as I saw he had no other, and as I was only too happy to go, I got his consent.", "여정의 고단함이 곧바로 후견인 쪽의 반대 이유가 되었지만, 그 밖에 다른 이유는 없는 듯 보였고, 나 또한 가게 되어 더없이 기뻤으므로, 나는 그의 허락을 받아냈다."], ["We had then merely to dispose of Mr. Vholes.", "그러고 나니 이제 볼스 씨만 처리하면 되었다."]], [["“Well, sir,” said Mr.", "\"그럼, 선생.\""], ["Jarndyce, “Miss Summerson will communicate with Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 말했다."], ["Carstone, and you can only hope that his position may be yet retrievable.", "\"서머슨 양이 카스톤 군과 이야기를 나눌 것이고, 선생으로서는 그의 처지가 아직 만회될 수 있기를 바라는 수밖에 없겠소."], ["You will allow me to order you lunch after your journey, sir.”", "먼 길 오셨으니 점심을 내오라 하겠소, 선생.\""]], [["“I thank you, Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"감사합니다, 잔다이스 씨.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes, putting out his long black sleeve to check the ringing of the bell, “not any.", "볼스 씨가 긴 검은 소맷자락을 내밀어 종 울리는 것을 막으며 말했다."], ["I thank you, no, not a morsel.", "\"아무것도 됐습니다."], ["My digestion is much impaired, and I am but a poor knife and fork at any time.", "감사합니다만, 한 입도 됐습니다. 제 소화 기능이 많이 상해서, 평소에도 변변치 못한 식객이거든요."], ["If I was to partake of solid food at this period of the day, I don’t know what the consequences might be.", "하루 중 이 시각에 단단한 음식을 먹었다가는 무슨 탈이 날지 모릅니다."], ["Everything having been openly carried on, sir, I will now with your permission take my leave.”", "모든 일을 떳떳하게 처리했으니, 선생, 이제 허락하신다면 물러가겠습니다.\""]], [["“And I would that you could take your leave, and we could all take our leave, Mr. Vholes,”", "\"부디 선생이 물러가고, 우리 모두가 물러갈 수 있다면 좋겠소, 볼스 씨.\""], ["returned my guardian bitterly, “of a cause you know of.”", "후견인이 씁쓸하게 받았다. \"선생도 잘 아는 그 소송에서 말이오.\""]], [["Mr. Vholes, whose black dye was so deep from head to foot that it had quite steamed before the fire, diffusing a very unpleasant perfume, made a short one-sided inclination of his head from the neck and slowly shook it.", "볼스 씨는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 검은 물이 어찌나 짙게 들었던지 벽난로 앞에서 김이 모락모락 피어오르며 몹시 불쾌한 냄새를 풍겼는데, 목을 까딱하듯 고개를 한쪽으로 짧게 숙였다가 천천히 가로저었다."]], [["“We whose ambition it is to be looked upon in the light of respectable practitioners, sir, can but put our shoulders to the wheel.", "\"존경받는 법조인으로 비치는 것을 목표로 삼는 저희 같은 사람들로서는, 선생, 그저 어깨를 바퀴에 들이대고 밀 수밖에 없습니다. 저희는 그렇게 합니다, 선생."], ["We do it, sir. At least, I do it myself; and I wish to think well of my professional brethren, one and all.", "적어도 저 자신은 그렇게 하고 있고, 제 동업자들 하나하나 모두를 좋게 생각하고 싶습니다."], ["You are sensible of an obligation not to refer to me, miss, in communicating with Mr. C.?”", "카스톤 씨와 이야기를 나누실 때 저를 언급하지 않으셔야 한다는 점, 아가씨도 유념하고 계시겠지요?\""]], [["I said I would be careful not to do it.", "나는 그러지 않도록 조심하겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Just so, miss.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 아가씨."], ["Good morning. Mr.", "안녕히 계십시오."], ["Jarndyce, good morning, sir.” Mr.", "잔다이스 씨, 안녕히 계십시오, 선생.\""], ["Vholes put his dead glove, which scarcely seemed to have any hand in it, on my fingers, and then on my guardian’s fingers, and took his long thin shadow away.", "볼스 씨는 안에 손이 들어 있는지조차 의심스러운 그 죽은 장갑을 내 손가락에, 이어서 후견인의 손가락에 갖다 댔고, 그러고는 자신의 길고 가느다란 그림자를 거두어 떠나갔다."], ["I thought of it on the outside of the coach, passing over all the sunny landscape between us and London, chilling the seed in the ground as it glided along.", "나는 그 그림자가 역마차 바깥쪽에 앉아 우리와 런던 사이에 펼쳐진 햇살 가득한 풍경을 가로질러 가며, 미끄러지는 자리마다 땅속의 씨앗을 얼어붙게 하는 모습을 떠올렸다."]], [["Of course it became necessary to tell Ada where I was going and why I was going, and of course she was anxious and distressed.", "물론 에이다에게 내가 어디로, 무엇 때문에 가는지를 말해야 했고, 물론 그 애는 애를 태우며 괴로워했다."], ["But she was too true to Richard to say anything but words of pity and words of excuse, and in a more loving spirit still—my dear devoted girl!—she wrote him a long letter, of which I took charge.", "그러나 그 애는 리처드에게 너무도 진실하여 가엾어하는 말과 두둔하는 말밖에 하지 않았고, 더더욱 사랑이 넘치는 마음으로—나의 헌신적이고 사랑스러운 그 애는!—그에게 긴 편지를 한 통 써서 내게 맡겼다."]], [["Charley was to be my travelling companion, though I am sure I wanted none and would willingly have left her at home.", "찰리가 내 길동무가 되기로 했는데, 사실 길동무 따위는 필요 없었고 기꺼이 집에 두고 갔을 것이다."], ["We all went to London that afternoon, and finding two places in the mail, secured them.", "우리는 그날 오후 다 함께 런던으로 가, 우편 마차에 두 자리가 비어 있는 것을 발견하고 그것을 잡아두었다."], ["At our usual bed-time, Charley and I were rolling away seaward with the Kentish letters.", "평소 잠자리에 들 시각에, 찰리와 나는 켄트로 가는 우편물과 함께 바닷가를 향해 덜컹덜컹 굴러가고 있었다."]], [["It was a night’s journey in those coach times, but we had the mail to ourselves and did not find the night very tedious.", "그 마차 시절에는 밤을 새우는 여정이었지만, 우편 마차를 우리끼리 차지한 데다 밤이 그리 지루하게 느껴지지도 않았다."], ["It passed with me as I suppose it would with most people under such circumstances.", "그런 처지에 놓인 대부분의 사람이 그러하리라 짐작되는 식으로 밤은 내게 흘러갔다."], ["At one while my journey looked hopeful, and at another hopeless.", "어떤 때는 내 여정이 희망차 보였고, 또 어떤 때는 가망 없어 보였다."], ["Now I thought I should do some good, and now I wondered how I could ever have supposed so.", "어느 순간에는 내가 무언가 도움이 되리라 싶다가도, 다음 순간에는 어떻게 그런 생각을 다 했을까 의아해졌다."], ["Now it seemed one of the most reasonable things in the world that I should have come, and now one of the most unreasonable.", "어느 때는 내가 이렇게 온 것이 세상에서 가장 사리에 맞는 일 같다가도, 다음 순간에는 가장 터무니없는 일 같았다."], ["In what state I should find Richard, what I should say to him, and what he would say to me occupied my mind by turns with these two states of feeling; and the wheels seemed to play one tune (to which the burden of my guardian’s letter set itself) over and over again all night.", "리처드를 어떤 상태로 보게 될지, 내가 그에게 무슨 말을 할지, 그가 내게 무슨 말을 할지가 이 두 갈래 감정과 더불어 번갈아 내 마음을 사로잡았고, 바퀴는 밤새도록 단 하나의 곡조를(후견인이 보낸 편지의 내용이 그 곡조에 얹혔다) 몇 번이고 되풀이해 연주하는 듯했다."]], [["At last we came into the narrow streets of Deal, and very gloomy they were upon a raw misty morning.", "마침내 우리는 딜의 좁은 거리로 들어섰는데, 차고 안개 낀 아침이라 거리는 무척이나 음산했다."], ["The long flat beach, with its little irregular houses, wooden and brick, and its litter of capstans, and great boats, and sheds, and bare upright poles with tackle and blocks, and loose gravelly waste places overgrown with grass and weeds, wore as dull an appearance as any place I ever saw.", "길고 평평한 해변에는 나무와 벽돌로 지은 작고 들쭉날쭉한 집들과, 어수선하게 널린 캡스턴이며 큰 배들, 헛간들, 도르래와 활차를 매단 헐벗은 기둥들, 그리고 풀과 잡초가 무성하게 뒤덮인 자갈투성이 황무지가 펼쳐져 있었는데, 내가 본 어느 곳 못지않게 음울한 모습이었다."], ["The sea was heaving under a thick white fog; and nothing else was moving but a few early ropemakers, who, with the yarn twisted round their bodies, looked as if, tired of their present state of existence, they were spinning themselves into cordage.", "바다는 짙고 흰 안개 아래서 일렁이고 있었고, 그 밖에 움직이는 것이라곤 일찌감치 일을 나온 밧줄 짜는 일꾼 몇뿐이었는데, 그들은 실타래를 몸에 칭칭 감고 있어, 지금의 자기 처지에 진력이 나서 스스로를 꼬아 밧줄로 만들고 있는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["But when we got into a warm room in an excellent hotel and sat down, comfortably washed and dressed, to an early breakfast (for it was too late to think of going to bed), Deal began to look more cheerful.", "그러나 우리가 어느 훌륭한 호텔의 따뜻한 방에 들어가, 말끔히 씻고 옷을 갈아입은 뒤 편안히 앉아 이른 아침 식사를(잠자리에 들기엔 이미 너무 늦은 시각이었으므로) 마주하자, 딜은 한결 명랑해 보이기 시작했다."], ["Our little room was like a ship’s cabin, and that delighted Charley very much.", "우리 자그마한 방은 배의 선실 같았는데, 찰리는 그것을 무척이나 좋아했다."], ["Then the fog began to rise like a curtain, and numbers of ships that we had had no idea were near appeared.", "이윽고 안개가 막처럼 걷히기 시작하더니, 근처에 있는 줄도 몰랐던 수많은 배가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["I don’t know how many sail the waiter told us were then lying in the downs.", "그때 다운스에 정박해 있는 배가 몇 척이라고 급사가 일러주었는지는 기억나지 않는다."], ["Some of these vessels were of grand size—one was a large Indiaman just come home; and when the sun shone through the clouds, making silvery pools in the dark sea, the way in which these ships brightened, and shadowed, and changed, amid a bustle of boats pulling off from the shore to them and from them to the shore, and a general life and motion in themselves and everything around them, was most beautiful.", "그 배들 가운데 일부는 어마어마하게 컸는데—한 척은 마침 본국으로 돌아온 큼직한 동인도 무역선이었다—구름 사이로 햇살이 비쳐 들어 어두운 바다에 은빛 웅덩이를 만들 때, 해변에서 배로, 또 배에서 해변으로 노 저어 오가는 보트들의 부산함 속에서, 그리고 배 자체와 그 주위 모든 것의 활기차고 분주한 움직임 속에서, 그 배들이 환히 빛났다가 그늘졌다가 시시각각 달라지는 모습은 더없이 아름다웠다."]], [["The large Indiaman was our great attraction because she had come into the downs in the night.", "그 큼직한 동인도 무역선이 우리의 가장 큰 구경거리였던 것은 그 배가 간밤에 다운스로 들어왔기 때문이다."], ["She was surrounded by boats, and we said how glad the people on board of her must be to come ashore.", "배는 보트들에 둘러싸여 있었고, 우리는 배에 탄 사람들이 뭍에 오르게 되어 얼마나 기쁠까 이야기했다."], ["Charley was curious, too, about the voyage, and about the heat in India, and the serpents and the tigers; and as she picked up such information much faster than grammar, I told her what I knew on those points.", "찰리는 또 그 항해에 대해, 그리고 인도의 더위와 뱀과 호랑이에 대해 궁금해했는데, 그 애는 그런 지식이라면 문법보다 훨씬 빨리 주워 담았으므로, 나는 그런 것들에 대해 아는 바를 일러주었다."], ["I told her, too, how people in such voyages were sometimes wrecked and cast on rocks, where they were saved by the intrepidity and humanity of one man.", "또한 그런 항해를 하다 보면 사람들이 때로 난파를 당해 바위에 떠밀려 올라가기도 하는데, 그럴 때 한 사람의 담대함과 인정으로 목숨을 건지기도 한다는 이야기도 들려주었다."], ["And Charley asking how that could be, I told her how we knew at home of such a case.", "찰리가 어떻게 그럴 수 있느냐고 묻기에, 나는 우리가 집에서 그런 사례를 알게 된 일이 있다고 일러주었다."]], [["I had thought of sending Richard a note saying I was there, but it seemed so much better to go to him without preparation.", "리처드에게 내가 와 있다는 쪽지를 보낼까 생각해 보았지만, 미리 알리지 않고 그냥 찾아가는 편이 훨씬 나을 듯했다."], ["As he lived in barracks I was a little doubtful whether this was feasible, but we went out to reconnoitre.", "그가 병영에서 지내고 있었으므로 이것이 가능한 일인지 다소 미심쩍었으나, 우리는 사정을 살피러 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Peeping in at the gate of the barrack-yard, we found everything very quiet at that time in the morning, and I asked a sergeant standing on the guardhouse-steps where he lived.", "병영 마당 문 안을 들여다보니, 아침 그 시각에는 모든 것이 무척 고요했고, 나는 위병소 계단에 서 있는 병장에게 그가 어디 사느냐고 물었다."], ["He sent a man before to show me, who went up some bare stairs, and knocked with his knuckles at a door, and left us.", "병장은 길을 안내하도록 한 사람을 앞세워 보냈는데, 그는 휑한 계단을 올라 어느 문을 손가락 마디로 두드리고는 우리를 두고 가버렸다."]], [["“Now then!”", "\"들어와요!\""], ["cried Richard from within.", "리처드가 안에서 외쳤다."], ["So I left Charley in the little passage, and going on to the half-open door, said, “Can I come in, Richard?", "그래서 나는 찰리를 좁은 통로에 남겨두고, 반쯤 열린 문 쪽으로 다가가 말했다. \"들어가도 될까, 리처드?"], ["It’s only Dame Durden.”", "더든 아줌마일 뿐이야.\""]], [["He was writing at a table, with a great confusion of clothes, tin cases, books, boots, brushes, and portmanteaus strewn all about the floor.", "그는 탁자에서 무언가를 쓰고 있었는데, 옷가지며 양철 상자, 책, 장화, 솔, 여행 가방이 바닥 여기저기에 어수선하게 널려 있었다."], ["He was only half dressed—in plain clothes, I observed, not in uniform—and his hair was unbrushed, and he looked as wild as his room.", "그는 옷을 절반밖에 걸치지 않은 채였고—군복이 아니라 평상복 차림이라는 것을 나는 알아챘다—머리도 빗질하지 않아, 제 방만큼이나 헝클어진 모습이었다."], ["All this I saw after he had heartily welcomed me and I was seated near him, for he started upon hearing my voice and caught me in his arms in a moment.", "이 모든 것은 그가 나를 진심으로 반기고 내가 그 곁에 앉은 뒤에야 눈에 들어왔는데, 그가 내 목소리를 듣더니 화들짝 놀라 순식간에 나를 품에 끌어안았기 때문이다."], ["Dear Richard!", "사랑하는 리처드!"], ["He was ever the same to me.", "그는 내게 늘 한결같았다."], ["Down to—ah, poor poor fellow!—to the end, he never received me but with something of his old merry boyish manner.", "그—아, 가엾고 가엾은 친구!—그 끝까지, 그는 옛날의 그 명랑하고 소년 같은 태도 없이 나를 맞이한 적이 한 번도 없었다."]], [["“Good heaven, my dear little woman,”", "\"세상에, 나의 어여쁜 아가씨,\""], ["said he, “how do you come here?", "그가 말했다. \"어떻게 여기까지 온 거야?"], ["Who could have thought of seeing you!", "누가 너를 보게 되리라 생각이나 했겠어!"], ["Nothing the matter?", "무슨 일 있는 건 아니지?"], ["Ada is well?”", "에이다는 잘 있고?\""]], [["“Quite well.", "\"아주 잘 있어."], ["Lovelier than ever, Richard!”", "그 어느 때보다 어여쁘단다, 리처드!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he said, leaning back in his chair.", "그가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다."], ["“My poor cousin!", "\"내 가엾은 사촌!"], ["I was writing to you, Esther.”", "마침 너한테 편지를 쓰고 있었어, 에스더.\""]], [["So worn and haggard as he looked, even in the fullness of his handsome youth, leaning back in his chair and crushing the closely written sheet of paper in his hand!", "잘생긴 젊음이 한창인 가운데서도, 의자에 등을 기댄 채 빽빽이 글이 적힌 종이를 손에 움켜쥐고 있는 그가 어찌나 지치고 초췌해 보이던지!"]], [["“Have you been at the trouble of writing all that, and am I not to read it after all?”", "\"그렇게 애써 그걸 다 써놓고선, 결국 나는 못 읽게 하는 거야?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, my dear,”", "\"아, 얘,\""], ["he returned with a hopeless gesture.", "그가 가망 없다는 몸짓을 하며 받았다."], ["“You may read it in the whole room.", "\"이 방 전체를 보면 그게 읽혀."], ["It is all over here.”", "여기선 다 끝났어.\""]], [["I mildly entreated him not to be despondent.", "나는 부디 낙담하지 말라고 부드럽게 간청했다."], ["I told him that I had heard by chance of his being in difficulty and had come to consult with him what could best be done.", "우연히 그가 곤경에 처했다는 소식을 들어, 무엇이 최선일지 함께 의논하러 왔다고 말했다."]], [["“Like you, Esther, but useless, and so NOT like you!”", "\"너답구나, 에스더, 하지만 소용없는 일이고, 그러니 너답지 않아!\""], ["said he with a melancholy smile.", "그가 우울한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“I am away on leave this day—should have been gone in another hour—and that is to smooth it over, for my selling out.", "\"오늘 휴가를 받아 나와 있어—한 시간 더 있으면 떠났어야 했는데—임관을 매각하는 일을 매끄럽게 마무리하기 위해서지."], ["Well!", "뭐!"], ["Let bygones be bygones.", "지난 일은 지난 일로 묻어두자."], ["So this calling follows the rest.", "그래서 이 직업도 나머지 것들과 똑같은 신세가 됐어."], ["I only want to have been in the church to have made the round of all the professions.”", "이제 성직에만 몸담아 봤더라면 온갖 직업을 한 바퀴 다 돌아본 셈이 됐을 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["“Richard,”", "\"리처드,\""], ["I urged, “it is not so hopeless as that?”", "내가 다그쳤다. \"그렇게 가망 없는 건 아니잖아?\""]], [["“Esther,”", "\"에스더,\""], ["he returned, “it is indeed.", "그가 받았다. \"정말 그래."], ["I am just so near disgrace as that those who are put in authority over me (as the catechism goes) would far rather be without me than with me.", "나는 망신살이 뻗치기 직전이라, (교리문답 식으로 말하자면) 내 위에 권한을 가진 자들이 나를 데리고 있느니 차라리 없는 편을 훨씬 바랄 지경이야."], ["And they are right.", "그리고 그들이 옳아."], ["Apart from debts and duns and all such drawbacks, I am not fit even for this employment.", "빚이며 빚쟁이며 그런 온갖 걸림돌은 차치하고라도, 나는 이 일조차 감당할 위인이 못 돼."], ["I have no care, no mind, no heart, no soul, but for one thing.", "나한테는 오직 한 가지 말고는, 마음도, 정신도, 가슴도, 영혼도 없어."], ["Why, if this bubble hadn’t broken now,”", "그러니, 만약 이 거품이 지금 터지지 않았더라면,\""], ["he said, tearing the letter he had written into fragments and moodily casting them away, by driblets, “how could I have gone abroad?", "그가 자기가 쓴 편지를 갈가리 찢어 침울하게 한 조각씩 흩뿌리며 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 외국으로 나갈 수 있었겠어?"], ["I must have been ordered abroad, but how could I have gone?", "외국 파병 명령을 받았어야 했겠지만, 어떻게 나갈 수 있었겠느냐고?"], ["How could I, with my experience of that thing, trust even Vholes unless I was at his back!”", "그 일에 대한 내 경험으로 미루어, 내가 등 뒤에 바짝 붙어 지키고 있지 않는 한, 어떻게 볼스조차 믿을 수 있었겠어!\""]], [["I suppose he knew by my face what I was about to say, but he caught the hand I had laid upon his arm and touched my own lips with it to prevent me from going on.", "내 표정을 보고 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 그가 알아챘던 모양인지, 그는 자기 팔 위에 얹어둔 내 손을 잡아 내 입술에 갖다 대어 내가 말을 잇지 못하게 했다."]], [["“No, Dame Durden!", "\"안 돼, 더든 아줌마!"], ["Two subjects I forbid—must forbid.", "두 가지 화제는 금지야—금지할 수밖에 없어."], ["The first is John Jarndyce.", "첫째는 존 잔다이스."], ["The second, you know what.", "둘째는, 네가 아는 그것."], ["Call it madness, and I tell you I can’t help it now, and can’t be sane.", "이걸 광기라 불러도 좋아. 나는 이제 어쩔 수가 없고, 제정신일 수도 없다고 말할 테니까."], ["But it is no such thing; it is the one object I have to pursue.", "하지만 그런 게 아니야. 이건 내가 좇아야만 하는 단 하나의 목표야."], ["It is a pity I ever was prevailed upon to turn out of my road for any other.", "내가 다른 어떤 것 때문에라도 내 길에서 벗어나도록 설득당했다는 게 한스러울 뿐이야."], ["It would be wisdom to abandon it now, after all the time, anxiety, and pains I have bestowed upon it!", "그동안 거기에 쏟아부은 그 모든 시간과 노심초사와 노고를 생각하면, 지금 그것을 포기하는 게 현명한 짓이겠지!"], ["Oh, yes, true wisdom.", "아, 그래, 참된 지혜겠지."], ["It would be very agreeable, too, to some people; but I never will.”", "어떤 사람들한테는 더없이 흡족한 일이기도 할 테고. 하지만 나는 결코 그러지 않을 거야.\""]], [["He was in that mood in which I thought it best not to increase his determination (if anything could increase it) by opposing him.", "그는 어떤 기분에 빠져 있었는데, 나는 그것을 반대해서 (무엇으로든 그것을 더 키울 수 있다면) 그의 결심을 더 굳히지 않는 편이 상책이라고 생각했다."], ["I took out Ada’s letter and put it in his hand.", "나는 에이다의 편지를 꺼내 그의 손에 쥐여주었다."]], [["“Am I to read it now?”", "\"지금 읽으라는 거야?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["As I told him yes, he laid it on the table, and resting his head upon his hand, began.", "내가 그렇다고 하자, 그는 그것을 탁자 위에 놓고 한 손으로 머리를 받친 채 읽기 시작했다."], ["He had not read far when he rested his head upon his two hands—to hide his face from me.", "얼마 읽지 않아 그는 두 손으로 머리를 받쳤다—내게서 얼굴을 가리려는 것이었다."], ["In a little while he rose as if the light were bad and went to the window.", "잠시 후 그는 빛이 나쁘다는 듯 일어나 창가로 갔다."], ["He finished reading it there, with his back towards me, and after he had finished and had folded it up, stood there for some minutes with the letter in his hand.", "그는 내게 등을 돌린 채 거기서 편지를 다 읽었고, 다 읽고 나서 그것을 접은 뒤에도 편지를 손에 쥐고 몇 분 동안 그 자리에 서 있었다."], ["When he came back to his chair, I saw tears in his eyes.", "그가 다시 의자로 돌아왔을 때, 나는 그의 눈에 어린 눈물을 보았다."]], [["“Of course, Esther, you know what she says here?”", "\"물론, 에스더, 너도 그 애가 여기서 뭐라고 하는지 알겠지?\""], ["He spoke in a softened voice and kissed the letter as he asked me.", "그는 한결 부드러워진 목소리로 말하며, 그렇게 물으면서 편지에 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“Yes, Richard.”", "\"응, 리처드.\""]], [["“Offers me,”", "\"그 애가 내게 주겠다는 거야,\""], ["he went on, tapping his foot upon the floor, “the little inheritance she is certain of so soon—just as little and as much as I have wasted—and begs and prays me to take it, set myself right with it, and remain in the service.”", "그가 발로 바닥을 톡톡 구르며 말을 이었다. \"머잖아 확실히 받게 될 그 자그마한 유산을—내가 탕진해 버린 만큼이나 적고도 많은 그것을—제발 받아서, 그걸로 처지를 바로잡고, 계속 복무하라고 빌고 또 비는 거야.\""]], [["“I know your welfare to be the dearest wish of her heart,”", "\"네 잘됨이 그 애 마음에서 가장 간절한 소원이라는 걸 나도 알아.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“And, oh, my dear Richard, Ada’s is a noble heart.”", "\"그리고, 아, 사랑하는 리처드, 에이다는 고결한 마음을 지녔어.\""]], [["“I am sure it is.", "\"나도 그런 줄 알아."], ["I—I wish I was dead!”", "나는—나는 차라리 죽어버렸으면 좋겠어!\""]], [["He went back to the window, and laying his arm across it, leaned his head down on his arm.", "그는 다시 창가로 가, 창턱에 팔을 가로 얹고 그 팔에 머리를 떨구었다."], ["It greatly affected me to see him so, but I hoped he might become more yielding, and I remained silent.", "그런 그의 모습을 보니 마음이 몹시 아팠지만, 그가 좀 더 누그러질지도 모른다는 희망에 나는 잠자코 있었다."], ["My experience was very limited; I was not at all prepared for his rousing himself out of this emotion to a new sense of injury.", "내 경험은 무척 일천했던 터라, 그가 이런 감정을 떨치고 일어서서 새삼 억울하다는 감정에 휩싸일 줄은 전혀 예상치 못했다."]], [["“And this is the heart that the same John Jarndyce, who is not otherwise to be mentioned between us, stepped in to estrange from me,”", "\"그리고 이게 바로, 우리 사이에서 달리 입에 올릴 것도 없는 바로 그 존 잔다이스가 끼어들어 나한테서 떼어놓으려 한 그 마음이야.\""], ["said he indignantly.", "그가 분개하여 말했다."], ["“And the dear girl makes me this generous offer from under the same John Jarndyce’s roof, and with the same John Jarndyce’s gracious consent and connivance, I dare say, as a new means of buying me off.”", "\"그리고 그 사랑스러운 아이가 나한테 이 너그러운 제안을 하는 게, 바로 그 존 잔다이스의 지붕 아래서, 그리고 분명코 그 존 잔다이스의 인자한 동의와 묵인 아래서, 나를 새로운 방식으로 돈으로 떼어내려는 수작이라고.\""]], [["“Richard!”", "\"리처드!\""], ["I cried out, rising hastily.", "나는 황급히 일어서며 소리쳤다."], ["“I will not hear you say such shameful words!”", "\"그런 부끄러운 말을 하는 걸 듣고 있지 않겠어!\""], ["I was very angry with him indeed, for the first time in my life, but it only lasted a moment.", "나는 평생 처음으로 그에게 정말로 화가 났지만, 그것은 한순간뿐이었다."], ["When I saw his worn young face looking at me as if he were sorry, I put my hand on his shoulder and said, “If you please, my dear Richard, do not speak in such a tone to me.", "그가 미안해하는 듯한 표정으로 지친 젊은 얼굴을 들어 나를 바라보는 것을 보자, 나는 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다. \"부디, 사랑하는 리처드, 나한테 그런 어조로 말하지 마."], ["Consider!”", "좀 생각해 봐!\""]], [["He blamed himself exceedingly and told me in the most generous manner that he had been very wrong and that he begged my pardon a thousand times.", "그는 자신을 몹시 책망하며, 더없이 너그러운 태도로 자기가 크게 잘못했고 천번 만번 용서를 빈다고 내게 말했다."], ["At that I laughed, but trembled a little too, for I was rather fluttered after being so fiery.", "그 말에 나는 웃었지만, 한편으로는 조금 떨기도 했는데, 그토록 불같이 화를 낸 뒤라 적잖이 마음이 흔들렸기 때문이다."]], [["“To accept this offer, my dear Esther,”", "\"이 제안을 받아들이는 건, 사랑하는 에스더,\""], ["said he, sitting down beside me and resuming our conversation, “—once more, pray, pray forgive me; I am deeply grieved—to accept my dearest cousin’s offer is, I need not say, impossible.", "그가 내 곁에 앉아 우리의 대화를 다시 이어가며 말했다. \"—다시 한번, 제발, 제발 나를 용서해 줘."], ["Besides, I have letters and papers that I could show you which would convince you it is all over here.", "나는 마음 깊이 슬프단다—내 더없이 소중한 사촌의 제안을 받아들이는 건, 말할 것도 없이 불가능해. 게다가, 여기선 다 끝났다는 걸 네게 납득시켜 줄 편지며 서류도 보여줄 수 있어."], ["I have done with the red coat, believe me.", "군복하고는 이제 끝이야, 정말로."], ["But it is some satisfaction, in the midst of my troubles and perplexities, to know that I am pressing Ada’s interests in pressing my own.", "하지만 이 고난과 혼란의 와중에도, 내 이익을 밀어붙이는 게 곧 에이다의 이익을 밀어붙이는 거라는 걸 아니까 그나마 위안이 돼."], ["Vholes has his shoulder to the wheel, and he cannot help urging it on as much for her as for me, thank God!”", "볼스가 어깨를 바퀴에 들이대고 밀고 있으니, 나를 위해서나 그 애를 위해서나 똑같이 그것을 힘껏 밀어붙일 수밖에 없겠지, 천만다행으로!\""]], [["His sanguine hopes were rising within him and lighting up his features, but they made his face more sad to me than it had been before.", "희망에 부푼 그의 기대가 마음속에서 솟아올라 그의 이목구비를 환하게 밝혔지만, 그 때문에 그의 얼굴은 내게 이전보다 더 슬프게 보였다."]], [["“No, no!”", "\"아니, 아니!\""], ["cried Richard exultingly.", "리처드가 의기양양하게 외쳤다."], ["“If every farthing of Ada’s little fortune were mine, no part of it should be spent in retaining me in what I am not fit for, can take no interest in, and am weary of.", "\"에이다의 자그마한 재산이 한 푼도 남김없이 내 것이 된대도, 그중 단 한 푼도 내가 감당할 수도 없고, 아무런 흥미도 못 느끼고, 신물이 난 이 일에 나를 붙들어두는 데 쓰여서는 안 돼."], ["It should be devoted to what promises a better return, and should be used where she has a larger stake.", "그건 더 나은 보답을 약속하는 일에 바쳐야 하고, 그 애가 더 큰 몫을 건 곳에 쓰여야 해."], ["Don’t be uneasy for me!", "나 때문에 마음 졸이지 마!"], ["I shall now have only one thing on my mind, and Vholes and I will work it.", "이제 나한테는 마음에 둘 일이 단 하나뿐일 테고, 볼스와 내가 그 일을 해낼 거야."], ["I shall not be without means.", "나도 빈털터리는 아닐 거야."], ["Free of my commission, I shall be able to compound with some small usurers who will hear of nothing but their bond now—Vholes says so.", "임관에서 벗어나면, 지금 와선 채권 말고는 아무 소리도 들으려 하지 않는 자잘한 고리대금업자들과 합의를 볼 수 있을 거야—볼스가 그렇게 말했어."], ["I should have a balance in my favour anyway, but that would swell it.", "어차피 내 쪽에 잔액이 남을 테지만, 그렇게 하면 그 잔액이 더 불어날 거야."], ["Come, come!", "자, 자!"], ["You shall carry a letter to Ada from me, Esther, and you must both of you be more hopeful of me and not believe that I am quite cast away just yet, my dear.”", "너는 나한테서 에이다한테 보내는 편지를 가져가 줘, 에스더. 그리고 너희 둘 다 나에 대해 좀 더 희망을 품어주고, 내가 아직 완전히 내버려진 신세는 아니라고 믿어줘야 해, 얘야.\""]], [["I will not repeat what I said to Richard.", "내가 리처드에게 한 말을 여기 옮기지는 않겠다."], ["I know it was tiresome, and nobody is to suppose for a moment that it was at all wise.", "그것이 지루한 소리였다는 걸 알고, 그것이 조금이라도 현명했으리라고는 아무도 한순간도 생각해서는 안 된다."], ["It only came from my heart.", "그것은 그저 내 가슴에서 우러난 말이었다."], ["He heard it patiently and feelingly, but I saw that on the two subjects he had reserved it was at present hopeless to make any representation to him.", "그는 그것을 참을성 있게, 그리고 마음을 담아 들어주었지만, 그가 따로 봉인해 둔 그 두 가지 화제에 대해서는 지금으로선 어떤 말을 해도 가망이 없다는 것을 나는 알았다."], ["I saw too, and had experienced in this very interview, the sense of my guardian’s remark that it was even more mischievous to use persuasion with him than to leave him as he was.", "또한 나는, 바로 이 면담에서 직접 겪어보고서, 그를 그대로 내버려두는 것보다 설득하려 드는 게 오히려 더 해롭다던 후견인의 말이 무슨 뜻인지를 절감했다."]], [["Therefore I was driven at last to asking Richard if he would mind convincing me that it really was all over there, as he had said, and that it was not his mere impression.", "그리하여 나는 결국, 그가 말한 대로 거기선 정말로 모든 게 끝났으며 그것이 그의 단순한 짐작이 아니라는 점을 내게 납득시켜 주겠느냐고 리처드에게 물을 수밖에 없었다."], ["He showed me without hesitation a correspondence making it quite plain that his retirement was arranged.", "그는 망설임 없이, 자신의 퇴역이 마무리되었음을 아주 분명하게 보여주는 서신들을 내게 내보였다."], ["I found, from what he told me, that Mr. Vholes had copies of these papers and had been in consultation with him throughout.", "나는 그가 들려준 이야기로 미루어, 볼스 씨가 이 서류들의 사본을 가지고 있으며 처음부터 끝까지 그와 상의해 왔다는 것을 알았다."], ["Beyond ascertaining this, and having been the bearer of Ada’s letter, and being (as I was going to be) Richard’s companion back to London, I had done no good by coming down.", "이 점을 확인한 것과, 에이다의 편지를 전한 심부름꾼 노릇을 한 것과, (앞으로 그러기로 했듯이) 리처드와 함께 런던으로 돌아가는 길동무가 되어주는 것 말고는, 내가 내려와서 한 좋은 일은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Admitting this to myself with a reluctant heart, I said I would return to the hotel and wait until he joined me there, so he threw a cloak over his shoulders and saw me to the gate, and Charley and I went back along the beach.", "못내 아쉬운 마음으로 이 사실을 스스로 인정하며, 나는 호텔로 돌아가 그가 그리로 합류할 때까지 기다리겠다고 말했고, 그러자 그는 어깨에 망토를 두르고 문까지 나를 배웅해 주었으며, 찰리와 나는 해변을 따라 돌아갔다."]], [["There was a concourse of people in one spot, surrounding some naval officers who were landing from a boat, and pressing about them with unusual interest.", "한곳에 사람들이 잔뜩 모여, 보트에서 막 내리는 몇몇 해군 장교를 에워싸고는 유난한 관심으로 그들 주위로 몰려들고 있었다."], ["I said to Charley this would be one of the great Indiaman’s boats now, and we stopped to look.", "나는 찰리에게 이것이 그 큰 동인도 무역선의 보트일 거라고 말하고, 우리는 멈춰 서서 구경했다."]], [["The gentlemen came slowly up from the waterside, speaking good-humouredly to each other and to the people around and glancing about them as if they were glad to be in England again.", "신사들은 물가에서 천천히 올라오며, 서로에게, 또 주위 사람들에게 유쾌하게 말을 건네고, 다시 영국에 돌아와 기쁜 듯 사방을 둘러보고 있었다. \"찰리, 찰리,\""], ["“Charley, Charley,”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I, “come away!”", "\"어서 가자!\""], ["And I hurried on so swiftly that my little maid was surprised.", "그러고는 어찌나 잽싸게 서둘렀던지 내 어린 하녀가 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["It was not until we were shut up in our cabin-room and I had had time to take breath that I began to think why I had made such haste.", "내가 왜 그토록 서둘렀는지 곰곰이 생각해 보기 시작한 것은, 우리가 선실 같은 방에 틀어박혀 내가 숨을 고를 겨를을 얻고 나서였다."], ["In one of the sunburnt faces I had recognized Mr. Allan Woodcourt, and I had been afraid of his recognizing me.", "그 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴들 가운데서 나는 앨런 우드코트 씨를 알아보았고, 그가 나를 알아볼까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."], ["I had been unwilling that he should see my altered looks.", "나는 그가 변해버린 내 얼굴을 보는 게 싫었다."], ["I had been taken by surprise, and my courage had quite failed me.", "나는 불시에 허를 찔렸고, 내 용기가 완전히 꺾여버렸던 것이다."]], [["But I knew this would not do, and I now said to myself, “My dear, there is no reason—there is and there can be no reason at all—why it should be worse for you now than it ever has been.", "그러나 나는 이래서는 안 된다는 것을 알았기에, 이제 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다. \"얘야, 지금이 예전 어느 때보다 너한테 더 나쁠 까닭은 없어—전혀 없고, 있을 수도 없어."], ["What you were last month, you are to-day; you are no worse, you are no better.", "네가 지난달의 너였던 그대로 오늘의 너인 거야. 더 나빠진 것도 없고, 더 나아진 것도 없어."], ["This is not your resolution; call it up, Esther, call it up!”", "이건 네 결심이 아니잖니. 결심을 불러내, 에스더, 불러내라고!\""], ["I was in a great tremble—with running—and at first was quite unable to calm myself; but I got better, and I was very glad to know it.", "나는—달리느라—몹시 떨고 있었고, 처음에는 도무지 마음을 가라앉힐 수가 없었지만, 차츰 나아졌고, 그렇게 되었다는 걸 알게 되어 무척 기뻤다."]], [["The party came to the hotel.", "일행이 호텔로 왔다."], ["I heard them speaking on the staircase.", "나는 그들이 계단에서 이야기하는 소리를 들었다."], ["I was sure it was the same gentlemen because I knew their voices again—I mean I knew Mr. Woodcourt’s.", "그들의 목소리를 다시 알아들었으므로 그 신사들이 틀림없다고 확신했는데—내 말은, 우드코트 씨의 목소리를 알아들었다는 뜻이다."], ["It would still have been a great relief to me to have gone away without making myself known, but I was determined not to do so.", "정체를 밝히지 않고 그냥 떠나버린다면 여전히 내게는 큰 안도가 되었겠지만, 나는 그러지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었다. \"안 돼, 얘야, 안 돼."], ["“No, my dear, no. No, no, no!”", "안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼!\""]], [["I untied my bonnet and put my veil half up—I think I mean half down, but it matters very little—and wrote on one of my cards that I happened to be there with Mr. Richard Carstone, and I sent it in to Mr.", "나는 보닛 끈을 풀고 베일을 반쯤 올렸는데—반쯤 내렸다는 뜻인 것 같지만, 그건 아무래도 좋다—내 명함 한 장에 마침 리처드 카스톤 씨와 함께 이곳에 와 있다고 적어, 그것을 우드코트 씨에게 들여보냈다."], ["Woodcourt. He came immediately.", "그는 곧바로 왔다."], ["I told him I was rejoiced to be by chance among the first to welcome him home to England.", "나는 우연히도 그가 영국으로 돌아온 것을 가장 먼저 맞이하는 사람들 축에 끼게 되어 기쁘다고 말했다."], ["And I saw that he was very sorry for me.", "그리고 나는 그가 나를 무척 가엾이 여기고 있다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“You have been in shipwreck and peril since you left us, Mr.", "\"우리 곁을 떠나신 뒤로 난파와 위험을 겪으셨군요, 우드코트 씨.\""], ["Woodcourt,”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I, “but we can hardly call that a misfortune which enabled you to be so useful and so brave.", "\"하지만 그토록 쓸모 있고 용감하게 처신하실 수 있게 해준 그 일을 두고 불운이라 부르기는 어렵겠지요."], ["We read of it with the truest interest.", "저희는 그 소식을 더없이 진심 어린 관심으로 읽었답니다."], ["It first came to my knowledge through your old patient, poor Miss Flite, when I was recovering from my severe illness.”", "제가 그 일을 처음 알게 된 건, 제가 그 모진 병에서 회복하던 무렵, 당신의 옛 환자였던 가엾은 플라이트 양을 통해서였어요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Little Miss Flite!”", "자그마한 플라이트 양!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“She lives the same life yet?”", "\"그분은 아직도 그대로 살고 계십니까?\""]], [["“Just the same.”", "\"꼭 그대로예요.\""]], [["I was so comfortable with myself now as not to mind the veil and to be able to put it aside.", "이제 나는 스스로에게 무척이나 편안해져서, 베일 따위는 신경 쓰이지 않았고 그것을 옆으로 젖혀둘 수도 있었다."]], [["“Her gratitude to you, Mr. Woodcourt, is delightful.", "\"그분이 당신께 품은 고마움은 참 보기 좋더군요, 우드코트 씨."], ["She is a most affectionate creature, as I have reason to say.”", "그분은 더없이 정 많은 분이세요. 제가 그렇게 말할 만한 까닭이 있답니다.\""]], [["“You—you have found her so?”", "\"그분이—그분이 그렇게 정이 많던가요?\""], ["he returned.", "그가 받았다."], ["“I—I am glad of that.”", "\"저는—저는 그것 참 기쁩니다.\""], ["He was so very sorry for me that he could scarcely speak.", "그는 나를 너무도 가엾이 여겨 말을 거의 잇지 못했다."]], [["“I assure you,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["said I, “that I was deeply touched by her sympathy and pleasure at the time I have referred to.”", "내가 말했다. \"제가 말씀드린 그 무렵에 그분이 보여준 동정과 기쁨에 저는 깊이 감동했답니다.\""]], [["“I was grieved to hear that you had been very ill.”", "\"많이 편찮으셨다는 소식을 듣고 마음이 아팠습니다.\""]], [["“I was very ill.”", "\"많이 아팠지요.\""]], [["“But you have quite recovered?”", "\"하지만 이제 완전히 회복하셨고요?\""]], [["“I have quite recovered my health and my cheerfulness,”", "\"건강도 명랑함도 완전히 되찾았어요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“You know how good my guardian is and what a happy life we lead, and I have everything to be thankful for and nothing in the world to desire.”", "\"제 후견인이 얼마나 좋은 분인지, 또 저희가 얼마나 행복하게 사는지 아시잖아요. 저는 감사할 일은 다 갖췄고, 세상에 더 바랄 게 아무것도 없답니다.\""]], [["I felt as if he had greater commiseration for me than I had ever had for myself.", "나는 마치 그가 나를, 내가 나 자신을 가엾이 여겨본 그 어느 때보다 더 가엾이 여기는 것처럼 느껴졌다."], ["It inspired me with new fortitude and new calmness to find that it was I who was under the necessity of reassuring him.", "그를 안심시켜야 하는 처지에 놓인 사람이 바로 나라는 것을 깨닫자, 그것이 내게 새로운 꿋꿋함과 새로운 평온함을 불어넣었다."], ["I spoke to him of his voyage out and home, and of his future plans, and of his probable return to India.", "나는 그에게 나갈 때와 돌아올 때의 항해 이야기며, 앞으로의 계획이며, 인도로 다시 돌아갈 가능성에 대해 물었다."], ["He said that was very doubtful.", "그는 그것이 무척 불확실하다고 했다."], ["He had not found himself more favoured by fortune there than here.", "그는 그곳에서도 이곳에서보다 더 운이 따르지는 않았던 것이다."], ["He had gone out a poor ship’s surgeon and had come home nothing better.", "그는 가난한 선의(船醫)로 나갔다가 조금도 나을 것 없는 처지로 돌아온 것이었다."], ["While we were talking, and when I was glad to believe that I had alleviated (if I may use such a term) the shock he had had in seeing me, Richard came in.", "우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안, 그리고 그가 나를 보고 받은 충격을 내가 (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 누그러뜨렸다고 기쁘게 믿고 있을 때, 리처드가 들어왔다."], ["He had heard downstairs who was with me, and they met with cordial pleasure.", "그는 아래층에서 누가 나와 함께 있는지 들은 터였고, 두 사람은 진심 어린 반가움으로 만났다."]], [["I saw that after their first greetings were over, and when they spoke of Richard’s career, Mr. Woodcourt had a perception that all was not going well with him.", "처음의 인사가 끝나고 리처드의 진로에 관한 이야기가 오갈 때, 나는 우드코트 씨가 그의 일이 잘 풀리지 않고 있음을 알아챘다는 것을 보았다."], ["He frequently glanced at his face as if there were something in it that gave him pain, and more than once he looked towards me as though he sought to ascertain whether I knew what the truth was.", "그는 마치 리처드의 얼굴에 자신을 아프게 하는 무언가가 있다는 듯 자주 그 얼굴을 흘끔거렸고, 한 번 이상 내 쪽을 바라보았는데, 마치 진실이 무엇인지를 내가 알고 있는지 가늠하려는 듯했다."], ["Yet Richard was in one of his sanguine states and in good spirits and was thoroughly pleased to see Mr. Woodcourt again, whom he had always liked.", "그러나 리처드는 희망에 부푼 상태들 가운데 하나에 빠져 기분이 좋았고, 언제나 좋아했던 우드코트 씨를 다시 만나 진심으로 기뻐했다."]], [["Richard proposed that we all should go to London together; but Mr. Woodcourt, having to remain by his ship a little longer, could not join us.", "리처드는 우리 모두 함께 런던으로 가자고 제안했지만, 우드코트 씨는 자기 배 곁에 좀 더 머물러야 했으므로 우리와 동행할 수 없었다."], ["He dined with us, however, at an early hour, and became so much more like what he used to be that I was still more at peace to think I had been able to soften his regrets.", "그래도 그는 이른 시각에 우리와 함께 식사를 했고, 옛날의 그 모습과 한결 더 비슷해져서, 내가 그의 회한을 누그러뜨릴 수 있었다고 생각하니 나는 더더욱 마음이 놓였다."], ["Yet his mind was not relieved of Richard.", "그러나 그의 마음은 리처드에 대한 걱정에서 놓여나지 못했다."], ["When the coach was almost ready and Richard ran down to look after his luggage, he spoke to me about him.", "마차가 거의 채비를 마치고 리처드가 자기 짐을 살피러 아래층으로 달려 내려갔을 때, 그가 내게 리처드에 관해 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["I was not sure that I had a right to lay his whole story open, but I referred in a few words to his estrangement from Mr Jarndyce and to his being entangled in the ill-fated Chancery suit. Mr.", "내게 그의 사연을 통째로 펼쳐 보일 권리가 있는지는 확신할 수 없었지만, 나는 그가 잔다이스 씨와 사이가 틀어진 일과, 그 운수 사나운 대법원 소송에 얽혀든 일에 대해 몇 마디로 언급했다."], ["Woodcourt listened with interest and expressed his regret.", "우드코트 씨는 관심을 가지고 들으며 안타까움을 드러냈다."]], [["“I saw you observe him rather closely,”", "\"당신이 그를 꽤 유심히 살피시는 걸 봤어요.\""], ["said I, “Do you think him so changed?”", "내가 말했다. \"그가 그렇게나 변했다고 보세요?\""]], [["“He is changed,”", "\"변했습니다.\""], ["he returned, shaking his head.", "그가 고개를 가로저으며 받았다."]], [["I felt the blood rush into my face for the first time, but it was only an instantaneous emotion.", "나는 난생처음으로 얼굴로 피가 확 솟구치는 것을 느꼈지만, 그것은 그저 한순간의 동요였다."], ["I turned my head aside, and it was gone.", "나는 고개를 옆으로 돌렸고, 그것은 사라졌다."]], [["“It is not,” said Mr.", "\"그가 훨씬 젊어졌다거나 늙었다거나, 야위었다거나 살쪘다거나, 창백해졌다거나 혈색이 좋아졌다거나 하는 게 아닙니다.\""], ["Woodcourt, “his being so much younger or older, or thinner or fatter, or paler or ruddier, as there being upon his face such a singular expression.", "우드코트 씨가 말했다. \"그의 얼굴에 그토록 묘한 표정이 어려 있다는 거지요."], ["I never saw so remarkable a look in a young person.", "저는 젊은 사람한테서 그렇게 두드러진 표정을 본 적이 없습니다."], ["One cannot say that it is all anxiety or all weariness; yet it is both, and like ungrown despair.”", "그게 전적으로 불안 탓이라거나 전적으로 피로 탓이라고 말할 수는 없어요. 하지만 그 둘 다이고, 마치 다 자라지 못한 절망 같은 것이지요.\""]], [["“You do not think he is ill?”", "\"그가 병든 것 같지는 않고요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["No.", "아니다."], ["He looked robust in body.", "그는 몸은 튼튼해 보였다."]], [["“That he cannot be at peace in mind, we have too much reason to know,”", "\"그가 마음이 편할 리 없다는 건, 우리도 너무나 잘 알고 있지요.\""], ["I proceeded. “Mr.", "내가 말을 이었다."], ["Woodcourt, you are going to London?”", "\"우드코트 씨, 런던으로 가시는 거지요?\""]], [["“To-morrow or the next day.”", "\"내일이나 모레 갑니다.\""]], [["“There is nothing Richard wants so much as a friend.", "\"리처드가 무엇보다 절실히 바라는 건 친구예요."], ["He always liked you.", "그는 늘 당신을 좋아했어요."], ["Pray see him when you get there.", "부디 그곳에 가시거든 그를 만나주세요."], ["Pray help him sometimes with your companionship if you can.", "부디 가능하다면 이따금 곁에 있어주는 것으로 그를 도와주세요."], ["You do not know of what service it might be. You cannot think how Ada, and Mr.", "그게 얼마나 큰 도움이 될지 당신은 모르실 거예요."], ["Jarndyce, and even I—how we should all thank you, Mr. Woodcourt!”", "에이다와, 잔다이스 씨와, 그리고 저까지도—우리 모두가 당신께 얼마나 고마워할지, 당신은 짐작도 못 하실 거예요, 우드코트 씨!\""]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양,\""], ["he said, more moved than he had been from the first, “before heaven, I will be a true friend to him!", "그가 처음보다 한결 더 마음이 동한 모습으로 말했다. \"하늘에 맹세코, 저는 그에게 참된 친구가 되겠습니다!"], ["I will accept him as a trust, and it shall be a sacred one!”", "그를 제게 맡겨진 사람으로 받아들이겠고, 그것을 신성한 소임으로 삼겠습니다!\""]], [["“God bless you!”", "\"하느님의 은총이 있기를!\""], ["said I, with my eyes filling fast; but I thought they might, when it was not for myself.", "내가 말했는데, 눈에는 눈물이 빠르게 차올랐다. 하지만 그것이 나 자신을 위한 게 아닐 때는 그래도 괜찮으리라 생각했다."], ["“Ada loves him—we all love him, but Ada loves him as we cannot.", "\"에이다가 그를 사랑해요—우리 모두 그를 사랑하지만, 에이다는 우리로서는 흉내 낼 수 없을 만큼 그를 사랑해요."], ["I will tell her what you say.", "당신이 하신 말씀을 그 애에게 전할게요."], ["Thank you, and God bless you, in her name!”", "그 애의 이름으로, 고맙습니다, 그리고 하느님의 은총이 있기를!\""]], [["Richard came back as we finished exchanging these hurried words and gave me his arm to take me to the coach.", "우리가 이 황급한 말들을 주고받기를 마칠 무렵 리처드가 돌아와, 나를 마차로 데려가려고 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["“Woodcourt,”", "\"우드코트,\""], ["he said, unconscious with what application, “pray let us meet in London!”", "그가 그 말이 어디에 쓰일지도 모른 채 말했다. \"런던에서 꼭 만나자고!\""]], [["“Meet?”", "\"만나자고?\""], ["returned the other.", "상대가 받았다."], ["“I have scarcely a friend there now but you.", "\"이제 거기엔 자네 말고는 친구라 할 사람이 거의 없네."], ["Where shall I find you?”", "어디로 가면 자넬 찾을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“Why, I must get a lodging of some sort,”", "\"글쎄, 어디든 하숙을 하나 구해야겠지.\""], ["said Richard, pondering.", "리처드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."], ["“Say at Vholes’s, Symond’s Inn.”", "\"사이먼즈 인의 볼스 사무소로 연락하게.\""]], [["“Good!", "\"좋아!"], ["Without loss of time.”", "지체 없이 그러겠네.\""]], [["They shook hands heartily.", "두 사람은 진심으로 악수를 나누었다."], ["When I was seated in the coach and Richard was yet standing in the street, Mr. Woodcourt laid his friendly hand on Richard’s shoulder and looked at me.", "내가 마차에 자리를 잡고 리처드가 아직 거리에 서 있을 때, 우드코트 씨가 다정한 손을 리처드의 어깨에 얹고 나를 바라보았다."], ["I understood him and waved mine in thanks.", "나는 그 뜻을 알아채고, 고마움을 담아 손을 흔들었다."]], [["And in his last look as we drove away, I saw that he was very sorry for me.", "그리고 우리가 마차를 몰아 떠날 때 그가 보낸 마지막 눈길에서, 나는 그가 나를 무척 가엾이 여기고 있다는 것을 보았다."], ["I was glad to see it.", "나는 그것을 보게 되어 기뻤다."], ["I felt for my old self as the dead may feel if they ever revisit these scenes.", "나는 죽은 이들이 만약 이 세상의 장면들을 다시 찾아온다면 느낄 법한 그런 심정으로, 나의 옛 자신을 떠올렸다."], ["I was glad to be tenderly remembered, to be gently pitied, not to be quite forgotten. Stop Him!", "다정하게 기억되고, 부드럽게 가엾이 여겨지고, 아주 잊히지는 않았다는 것이 나는 기뻤다."]], [["Darkness rests upon Tom-All-Alone’s.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳에 어둠이 내려앉는다."], ["Dilating and dilating since the sun went down last night, it has gradually swelled until it fills every void in the place.", "어젯밤 해가 진 뒤로 자꾸만 부풀고 또 부풀더니, 어둠은 차츰 불어나 이곳의 빈자리란 빈자리를 죄다 채워버린다."], ["For a time there were some dungeon lights burning, as the lamp of life hums in Tom-all-Alone’s, heavily, heavily, in the nauseous air, and winking—as that lamp, too, winks in Tom-all-Alone’s—at many horrible things.", "한동안은 지하 감옥 같은 불빛 몇 점이 타올랐다. 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 생명의 등불이 메스꺼운 공기 속에서 무겁게, 무겁게 윙윙대듯이, 그리고 그 등불도 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 깜빡이듯이, 숱한 끔찍한 것들을 향해 깜빡이듯이."], ["But they are blotted out.", "그러나 그 불빛마저 지워졌다."], ["The moon has eyed Tom with a dull cold stare, as admitting some puny emulation of herself in his desert region unfit for life and blasted by volcanic fires; but she has passed on and is gone.", "달은 둔하고 차가운 눈초리로 톰을 흘끔 보았으니, 화산 불길에 그을려 생명이 깃들 수 없는 그 황량한 땅에서 제 자신의 가냘픈 모방이라도 발견한 듯했다. 그러나 달은 그대로 지나쳐 사라졌다."], ["The blackest nightmare in the infernal stables grazes on Tom-all-Alone’s, and Tom is fast asleep.", "지옥의 마구간에서 가장 시커먼 악몽이 톰 혼자 사는 곳을 풀밭처럼 뜯어먹고, 톰은 깊이 잠들어 있다."]], [["Much mighty speech-making there has been, both in and out of Parliament, concerning Tom, and much wrathful disputation how Tom shall be got right.", "톰을 두고 의회 안팎에서 거창한 연설이 무수히 쏟아졌고, 톰을 어떻게 바로잡을지를 둘러싼 노여운 논쟁도 무수히 벌어졌다."], ["Whether he shall be put into the main road by constables, or by beadles, or by bell-ringing, or by force of figures, or by correct principles of taste, or by high church, or by low church, or by no church; whether he shall be set to splitting trusses of polemical straws with the crooked knife of his mind or whether he shall be put to stone-breaking instead.", "톰을 큰길로 끌어내는 데 순경을 쓸지, 교구 직원을 쓸지, 종을 울려 쫓을지, 숫자의 힘으로 할지, 올바른 취향의 원칙으로 할지, 고교회로 할지, 저교회로 할지, 아니면 아예 교회 없이 할지. 톰의 마음에 깃든 그 구부러진 칼로 논쟁의 지푸라기 다발이나 쪼개게 만들지, 아니면 그 대신 돌을 깨게 시킬지."], ["In the midst of which dust and noise there is but one thing perfectly clear, to wit, that Tom only may and can, or shall and will, be reclaimed according to somebody’s theory but nobody’s practice.", "그 먼지와 소음 한복판에서 완벽하게 분명한 것은 단 하나뿐이니, 곧 톰은 누군가의 이론에 따라서는 구제될 수도 있고 구제되어야 하고 구제될 테지만, 그 누구의 실천으로도 구제되지 못한다는 것이다. 그리고 희망찬 그동안에, 톰은 예전 그대로의 완강한 기세로 머리부터 먼저 파멸로 곤두박질친다."], ["And in the hopeful meantime, Tom goes to perdition head foremost in his old determined spirit.", "* 고교회·저교회: 영국 국교회 안의 두 흐름. 고교회는 전례와 성직 권위를 중시하고, 저교회는 복음과 개인 신앙을 중시한다."]], [["But he has his revenge.", "그러나 톰에게는 복수가 있다."], ["Even the winds are his messengers, and they serve him in these hours of darkness.", "바람마저 그의 전령이 되어, 이 어둠의 시간에 그를 섬긴다."], ["There is not a drop of Tom’s corrupted blood but propagates infection and contagion somewhere.", "톰의 썩은 피 한 방울도 어딘가에 감염과 전염을 퍼뜨리지 않는 것이 없다."], ["It shall pollute, this very night, the choice stream (in which chemists on analysis would find the genuine nobility) of a Norman house, and his Grace shall not be able to say nay to the infamous alliance.", "그 피는 바로 오늘 밤, 어느 노르만 가문의 고귀한 핏줄을(화학자가 분석하면 진짜 귀족의 성분을 찾아낼 그 핏줄을) 더럽힐 것이며, 공작 각하조차 그 수치스러운 결합에 아니라고 말하지 못하리라."], ["There is not an atom of Tom’s slime, not a cubic inch of any pestilential gas in which he lives, not one obscenity or degradation about him, not an ignorance, not a wickedness, not a brutality of his committing, but shall work its retribution through every order of society up to the proudest of the proud and to the highest of the high.", "톰의 진창에서 떨어진 티끌 하나, 그가 사는 역병의 기체 한 세제곱인치, 그에게 붙은 외설과 타락 하나, 무지 하나, 사악함 하나, 그가 저지른 야만 하나도, 사회의 모든 계층을 거쳐 가장 도도한 자 가운데 도도한 자, 가장 높은 자 가운데 높은 자에게까지 그 응보를 미치지 않는 것이 없다."], ["Verily, what with tainting, plundering, and spoiling, Tom has his revenge.", "진실로, 오염시키고 약탈하고 망쳐놓는 일로, 톰에게는 복수가 있다."]], [["It is a moot point whether Tom-all-Alone’s be uglier by day or by night, but on the argument that the more that is seen of it the more shocking it must be, and that no part of it left to the imagination is at all likely to be made so bad as the reality, day carries it. The day begins to break now; and in truth it might be better for the national glory even that the sun should sometimes set upon the British dominions than that it should ever rise upon so vile a wonder as Tom.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳이 낮에 더 흉한지 밤에 더 흉한지는 다툼의 여지가 있는 문제다. 하지만 그곳을 많이 볼수록 더 충격적일 수밖에 없고, 상상에 맡겨진 어느 부분도 실제만큼 끔찍하게 그려지기는 어렵다는 논리로 보면, 낮이 이긴다. 이제 날이 밝아오기 시작한다. 사실 국가의 영광을 위해서라면, 톰처럼 그토록 비루한 진풍경 위로 태양이 떠오르느니, 차라리 대영제국의 영토에 가끔은 해가 지는 편이 나을지도 모른다. * 대영제국의 영토에 가끔은 해가 지는: \"대영제국에는 해가 지지 않는다\"라는 당대의 자부심 어린 표현을 뒤집은 반어."]], [["A brown sunburnt gentleman, who appears in some inaptitude for sleep to be wandering abroad rather than counting the hours on a restless pillow, strolls hitherward at this quiet time.", "햇볕에 그을린 갈색 얼굴의 신사 하나가, 잠을 청하지 못한 채 뒤척이는 베개 위에서 시간을 헤아리느니 차라리 바깥을 헤매기로 한 듯한 모습으로, 이 고요한 시각에 이쪽으로 거닐어 온다."], ["Attracted by curiosity, he often pauses and looks about him, up and down the miserable by-ways.", "호기심에 이끌려 그는 자주 걸음을 멈추고 사방을, 그 비참한 뒷골목 위아래를 둘러본다."], ["Nor is he merely curious, for in his bright dark eye there is compassionate interest; and as he looks here and there, he seems to understand such wretchedness and to have studied it before.", "그저 호기심만은 아니다. 빛나는 검은 눈동자에 측은히 여기는 관심이 어려 있으니, 이리저리 둘러보는 그는 그런 비참함을 이해하는 듯하고, 전에도 그것을 살펴본 적이 있는 듯하다."]], [["On the banks of the stagnant channel of mud which is the main street of Tom-all-Alone’s, nothing is to be seen but the crazy houses, shut up and silent.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳의 큰길이라 할 진창 고인 도랑 둑에는 미친 듯이 낡아빠진 집들이, 굳게 닫힌 채 말없이 늘어선 것 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는다."], ["No waking creature save himself appears except in one direction, where he sees the solitary figure of a woman sitting on a door-step.", "그 자신 말고는 깨어 있는 생명이라곤 한 방향에서밖에 보이지 않으니, 그곳에 한 여인의 외로운 모습이 문간 계단에 앉아 있다."], ["He walks that way.", "그는 그쪽으로 걸어간다."], ["Approaching, he observes that she has journeyed a long distance and is footsore and travel-stained.", "가까이 다가가서 그는, 그녀가 먼 길을 걸어와 발이 부르트고 여행에 찌든 모습임을 알아챈다."], ["She sits on the door-step in the manner of one who is waiting, with her elbow on her knee and her head upon her hand.", "그녀는 무언가를 기다리는 사람처럼 문간 계단에 앉아 있으니, 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 머리를 얹고 있다."], ["Beside her is a canvas bag, or bundle, she has carried.", "곁에는 그녀가 들고 온 천 가방인지 보따리인지가 놓여 있다."], ["She is dozing probably, for she gives no heed to his steps as he comes toward her.", "그가 다가가는 발소리에도 그녀가 아무 기척이 없는 걸 보면, 아마도 졸고 있는 듯하다."]], [["The broken footway is so narrow that when Allan Woodcourt comes to where the woman sits, he has to turn into the road to pass her.", "부서진 보도가 너무 좁아서, 앨런 우드코트가 여인이 앉은 곳에 이르렀을 때, 그는 그녀를 지나치려고 차도로 돌아서야 한다."], ["Looking down at her face, his eye meets hers, and he stops.", "그녀의 얼굴을 내려다보다가, 그의 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주치고, 그는 걸음을 멈춘다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["“Nothing, sir.”", "\"아무 일도 아닙니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Can’t you make them hear?", "\"안에서 못 듣는 모양이오?"], ["Do you want to be let in?”", "들어가고 싶은 거요?\""]], [["“I’m waiting till they get up at another house—a lodging-house—not here,”", "\"다른 집에서 사람들이 일어나기를 기다리는 거예요. 하숙집인데, 여기는 아니에요.\""], ["the woman patiently returns.", "여인이 참을성 있게 대답한다."], ["“I’m waiting here because there will be sun here presently to warm me.”", "\"여기서 기다리는 건, 곧 여기에 햇볕이 들어 몸을 녹여줄 테니까요.\""]], [["“I am afraid you are tired.", "\"피곤한 모양이오."], ["I am sorry to see you sitting in the street.”", "길거리에 앉아 있는 걸 보니 마음이 아프오.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리."], ["It don’t matter.”", "괜찮아요.\""]], [["A habit in him of speaking to the poor and of avoiding patronage or condescension or childishness (which is the favourite device, many people deeming it quite a subtlety to talk to them like little spelling books) has put him on good terms with the woman easily.", "가난한 이들에게 말을 건넬 때 생색이나 깔보는 태도나 어린애 다루듯 하는 짓을(많은 사람이 마치 작은 철자 교본을 읽어주듯 그들에게 말하는 걸 무슨 세련된 기술쯤으로 여기는데) 피하는 그의 습관 덕분에, 그는 손쉽게 여인과 친밀해진다."]], [["“Let me look at your forehead,”", "\"이마 좀 봅시다.\""], ["he says, bending down.", "그가 몸을 숙이며 말한다."], ["“I am a doctor.", "\"나는 의사요."], ["Don’t be afraid.", "겁내지 마시오."], ["I wouldn’t hurt you for the world.”", "결코 당신을 해치지 않을 테니.\""]], [["He knows that by touching her with his skilful and accustomed hand he can soothe her yet more readily.", "그는 능숙하고 익숙한 손으로 그녀를 만지면 그녀를 한결 쉽게 다독일 수 있음을 안다."], ["She makes a slight objection, saying, “It’s nothing”; but he has scarcely laid his fingers on the wounded place when she lifts it up to the light.", "그녀는 \"아무것도 아니에요\"라며 살짝 마다하지만, 그가 다친 자리에 손가락을 대자마자 그녀는 이마를 빛 쪽으로 들어올린다."]], [["“Aye!", "\"이런!"], ["A bad bruise, and the skin sadly broken.", "멍이 심하고, 살갗이 안타깝게도 찢어졌군."], ["This must be very sore.”", "이거 무척 아프겠소.\""]], [["“It do ache a little, sir,”", "\"좀 쑤시긴 해요, 나리.\""], ["returns the woman with a started tear upon her cheek.", "여인이 대답하는데 뺨에 눈물이 한 방울 돋는다."]], [["“Let me try to make it more comfortable.", "\"좀 더 편하게 해드리리다."], ["My handkerchief won’t hurt you.”", "내 손수건이 당신을 아프게 하진 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Oh, dear no, sir, I’m sure of that!”", "\"오, 그럼요, 나리, 그건 분명해요!\""]], [["He cleanses the injured place and dries it, and having carefully examined it and gently pressed it with the palm of his hand, takes a small case from his pocket, dresses it, and binds it up.", "그는 다친 자리를 씻어내고 닦은 뒤, 조심스레 살피고 손바닥으로 부드럽게 눌러본 다음, 주머니에서 작은 약상자를 꺼내 처치하고 붕대를 감는다."], ["While he is thus employed, he says, after laughing at his establishing a surgery in the street, “And so your husband is a brickmaker?”", "그렇게 일하면서, 길거리에 진료소를 차렸다며 웃고는 말한다. \"그래, 당신 남편은 벽돌공이오?\""]], [["“How do you know that, sir?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 아세요, 나리?\""], ["asks the woman, astonished.", "여인이 놀라 묻는다."]], [["“Why, I suppose so from the colour of the clay upon your bag and on your dress.", "\"아니, 가방이며 옷에 묻은 진흙 빛깔을 보고 짐작했소."], ["And I know brickmakers go about working at piecework in different places.", "게다가 벽돌공들이 이곳저곳 다니며 삯일을 하는 걸 알고 있고."], ["And I am sorry to say I have known them cruel to their wives too.”", "그리고 안타깝게도, 그들이 아내에게 모질게 구는 것도 본 적이 있소.\""]], [["The woman hastily lifts up her eyes as if she would deny that her injury is referable to such a cause.", "여인은 자기가 다친 게 그런 까닭이 아니라고 부인이라도 하려는 듯 황급히 눈을 든다."], ["But feeling the hand upon her forehead, and seeing his busy and composed face, she quietly drops them again.", "그러나 이마에 놓인 손길을 느끼고, 분주하면서도 차분한 그의 얼굴을 보고는, 가만히 다시 눈을 내린다."]], [["“Where is he now?”", "\"그자는 지금 어디 있소?\""], ["asks the surgeon.", "외과의가 묻는다."]], [["“He got into trouble last night, sir; but he’ll look for me at the lodging-house.”", "\"어젯밤 말썽을 일으켰어요, 나리. 그래도 하숙집으로 저를 찾으러 올 거예요.\""]], [["“He will get into worse trouble if he often misuses his large and heavy hand as he has misused it here.", "\"여기서 그랬듯이 그 크고 묵직한 손을 자꾸 함부로 휘두르면, 더 큰 말썽에 빠질 거요. 하지만 당신은 그 짐승 같은 자를 용서하는군."], ["But you forgive him, brutal as he is, and I say no more of him, except that I wish he deserved it.", "그러니 나도 더는 말하지 않으리다. 다만 그가 용서받을 자격이 있는 사람이면 좋겠소."], ["You have no young child?”", "어린 자식은 없소?\""]], [["The woman shakes her head.", "여인이 고개를 젓는다."], ["“One as I calls mine, sir, but it’s Liz’s.”", "\"제 아이라고 부르는 애가 하나 있긴 한데, 리즈네 아이예요, 나리.\""]], [["“Your own is dead.", "\"당신 아이는 죽었군."], ["I see!", "알겠소!"], ["Poor little thing!”", "가엾기도 하지!\""]], [["By this time he has finished and is putting up his case.", "이쯤 되자 그는 일을 마치고 약상자를 챙긴다."], ["“I suppose you have some settled home.", "\"정착해 사는 집은 있겠지요."], ["Is it far from here?”", "여기서 멉니까?\""], ["he asks, good-humouredly making light of what he has done as she gets up and curtsys.", "그녀가 일어나 무릎을 굽혀 인사하는 사이, 그는 자기가 한 일을 너스레로 가볍게 넘기며 묻는다."]], [["“It’s a good two or three and twenty mile from here, sir.", "\"여기서 한 스물두세 마일은 족히 돼요, 나리."], ["At Saint Albans.", "세인트올번스에 있어요."], ["You know Saint Albans, sir?", "세인트올번스 아세요, 나리?"], ["I thought you gave a start like, as if you did.”", "마치 아시는 것처럼 흠칫하시는 것 같던데요.\""]], [["“Yes, I know something of it.", "\"그렇소, 좀 알지."], ["And now I will ask you a question in return.", "자, 이제 나도 답례로 하나 물읍시다."], ["Have you money for your lodging?”", "하숙비 낼 돈은 있소?\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["she says, “really and truly.”", "그녀가 말한다. \"정말이지 진짜로요.\""], ["And she shows it.", "그러면서 돈을 보여준다."], ["He tells her, in acknowledgment of her many subdued thanks, that she is very welcome, gives her good day, and walks away.", "그는 그녀가 거듭 나직이 건네는 감사에 답하여, 천만의 말씀이라 하고는 잘 가라고 인사하고 걸어간다."], ["Tom-all-Alone’s is still asleep, and nothing is astir.", "톰 혼자 사는 곳은 여전히 잠들어 있고, 움직이는 것이라곤 없다."]], [["Yes, something is!", "아니, 무언가 움직인다!"], ["As he retraces his way to the point from which he descried the woman at a distance sitting on the step, he sees a ragged figure coming very cautiously along, crouching close to the soiled walls—which the wretchedest figure might as well avoid—and furtively thrusting a hand before it.", "그가 멀리서 계단에 앉은 여인을 처음 발견한 자리로 되돌아가는데, 누더기를 걸친 형체 하나가 더없이 조심스럽게 다가오는 게 보인다. 더러운 벽에 바짝 웅크려서, 아무리 비참한 형체라도 차라리 피하고 싶을 그 벽에 붙어, 한 손을 슬그머니 앞으로 내밀며 온다."], ["It is the figure of a youth whose face is hollow and whose eyes have an emaciated glare.", "얼굴이 움푹 꺼지고 두 눈에 야윈 광기가 번득이는 소년의 형체다."], ["He is so intent on getting along unseen that even the apparition of a stranger in whole garments does not tempt him to look back.", "들키지 않고 가려는 데에만 골몰한 나머지, 멀쩡한 옷을 차려입은 낯선 이가 나타나도 돌아볼 생각조차 않는다."], ["He shades his face with his ragged elbow as he passes on the other side of the way, and goes shrinking and creeping on with his anxious hand before him and his shapeless clothes hanging in shreds.", "길 건너편을 지나치며 그는 누더기 팔꿈치로 얼굴을 가리고, 불안한 손을 앞에 내민 채, 형체도 알 수 없는 옷을 너덜너덜 늘어뜨린 채로 움츠리며 기어가듯 나아간다."], ["Clothes made for what purpose, or of what material, it would be impossible to say.", "무슨 용도로 만든 옷인지, 무슨 천으로 만든 옷인지 도무지 말할 수 없다."], ["They look, in colour and in substance, like a bundle of rank leaves of swampy growth that rotted long ago.", "빛깔이며 재질이 마치 오래전에 썩어버린 늪지의 무성한 잎사귀 뭉치 같다."]], [["Allan Woodcourt pauses to look after him and note all this, with a shadowy belief that he has seen the boy before.", "앨런 우드코트는 걸음을 멈추고 그를 좇아 보며 이 모든 것을 눈여겨보는데, 전에 그 아이를 본 적이 있다는 어렴풋한 느낌이 든다."], ["He cannot recall how or where, but there is some association in his mind with such a form.", "어떻게, 어디서였는지 떠오르지는 않지만, 그런 모습과 얽힌 무언가가 마음에 걸린다."], ["He imagines that he must have seen it in some hospital or refuge, still, cannot make out why it comes with any special force on his remembrance.", "어느 병원이나 보호소에서 본 게 틀림없다 싶으면서도, 그것이 왜 이리 유난히 강렬하게 기억에 떠오르는지 도무지 알 수 없다."]], [["He is gradually emerging from Tom-all-Alone’s in the morning light, thinking about it, when he hears running feet behind him, and looking round, sees the boy scouring towards him at great speed, followed by the woman.", "아침 햇살 속에서 톰 혼자 사는 곳을 차츰 빠져나오며 그 일을 생각하던 차에, 그는 뒤에서 달려오는 발소리를 듣고 돌아본다. 그 소년이 엄청난 속도로 그를 향해 내달리고, 그 뒤를 여인이 쫓아온다."]], [["“Stop him, stop him!”", "\"붙잡아요, 붙잡아요!\""], ["cries the woman, almost breathless.", "여인이 숨이 턱에 차서 외친다."], ["“Stop him, sir!”", "\"저 애 좀 붙잡아요, 나리!\""]], [["He darts across the road into the boy’s path, but the boy is quicker than he, makes a curve, ducks, dives under his hands, comes up half-a-dozen yards beyond him, and scours away again.", "그는 길을 가로질러 소년의 앞길을 막지만, 소년이 그보다 빠르다. 곡선을 그리며 몸을 낮추고 그의 손 밑으로 빠져나가, 예닐곱 미터 너머에서 솟아올라 다시 내달린다."], ["Still the woman follows, crying, “Stop him, sir, pray stop him!”", "여인은 여전히 쫓아오며 외친다. \"저 애 붙잡아요, 나리, 제발 붙잡아주세요!\""], ["Allan, not knowing but that he has just robbed her of her money, follows in chase and runs so hard that he runs the boy down a dozen times, but each time he repeats the curve, the duck, the dive, and scours away again.", "앨런은 소년이 방금 그녀의 돈을 훔쳤는지도 모른다는 생각에 추격에 나서, 어찌나 세차게 내달리는지 열두 번이나 소년을 따라잡지만, 그때마다 소년은 곡선을 그리고 몸을 낮추고 빠져나가, 다시 내달린다."], ["To strike at him on any of these occasions would be to fell and disable him, but the pursuer cannot resolve to do that, and so the grimly ridiculous pursuit continues.", "이럴 때마다 그를 후려치면 쓰러뜨려 꼼짝 못 하게 할 수 있겠지만, 추격자는 차마 그러기로 마음먹지 못하니, 그렇게 음산하고도 우스꽝스러운 추격이 이어진다."], ["At last the fugitive, hard-pressed, takes to a narrow passage and a court which has no thoroughfare.", "마침내 도망자는 몰릴 대로 몰린 끝에 좁은 골목으로, 막다른 안마당으로 들어선다."], ["Here, against a hoarding of decaying timber, he is brought to bay and tumbles down, lying gasping at his pursuer, who stands and gasps at him until the woman comes up.", "여기 썩어가는 목재 울타리를 등지고 궁지에 몰린 그는 털썩 쓰러져, 숨을 헐떡이며 추격자를 올려다보고 누워 있고, 추격자도 서서 그를 향해 헐떡이다가 마침내 여인이 다가온다."]], [["“Oh, you, Jo!”", "\"오, 너, 조!\""], ["cries the woman.", "여인이 외친다."], ["“What?", "\"뭐야?"], ["I have found you at last!”", "드디어 널 찾았구나!\""]], [["“Jo,”", "\"조라.\""], ["repeats Allan, looking at him with attention, “Jo!", "앨런이 그를 유심히 보며 되뇐다. \"조!"], ["Stay.", "가만."], ["To be sure!", "그렇지!"], ["I recollect this lad some time ago being brought before the coroner.”", "이 녀석을 얼마 전에 검시관 앞에 끌려나왔을 때 봤어.\""]], [["“Yes, I see you once afore at the inkwhich,”", "\"네, 댁을 그 검시판에서 한 번 봤구먼유.\""], ["whimpers Jo. “What of that?", "조가 훌쩍이며 말한다."], ["Can’t you never let such an unfortnet as me alone?", "\"그게 뭐 어때서유? 나 같은 불쌍헌 놈 좀 가만 못 놔둬유?"], ["An’t I unfortnet enough for you yet?", "나 아직두 충분히 불쌍허덜 않아유?"], ["How unfortnet do you want me fur to be?", "얼매나 불쌍해져야 속이 시원허것슈?"], ["I’ve been a-chivied and a-chivied, fust by one on you and nixt by another on you, till I’m worritted to skins and bones.", "댁들헌테 쫓기구 또 쫓기구, 처음엔 이놈헌테 쫓기구 담엔 저놈헌테 쫓기구, 그러다 살가죽이랑 뼈만 남게 시달렸구먼유."], ["The inkwhich warn’t MY fault.", "그 검시판은 내 잘못이 아니었슈."], ["I done nothink.", "난 아무것두 안 혔슈."], ["He wos wery good to me, he wos; he wos the only one I knowed to speak to, as ever come across my crossing.", "그 사람은 나헌테 잘혀줬슈, 잘혀줬다구유. 내 건널목에 온 사람덜 중에 나헌테 말 걸어준 유일헌 사람이었슈."], ["It ain’t wery likely I should want him to be inkwhiched.", "내가 그 사람 검시판에 부쳐지길 바랐을 리가 없잖유."], ["I only wish I wos, myself.", "차라리 내가 그렇게 되구 싶구먼유."], ["I don’t know why I don’t go and make a hole in the water, I’m sure I don’t.”", "어째서 내가 물에 빠져 죽지 않는지 모르것슈, 정말 모르것슈.\""]], [["He says it with such a pitiable air, and his grimy tears appear so real, and he lies in the corner up against the hoarding so like a growth of fungus or any unwholesome excrescence produced there in neglect and impurity, that Allan Woodcourt is softened towards him.", "그가 어찌나 애처로운 꼴로 그 말을 하는지, 그리고 그 꾀죄죄한 눈물이 어찌나 진짜처럼 보이는지, 또 울타리에 기대 구석에 누운 그 모습이 어찌나 방치와 더러움 속에서 자라난 곰팡이나 어떤 해로운 혹덩이 같은지, 앨런 우드코트는 그를 향해 마음이 누그러진다."], ["He says to the woman, “Miserable creature, what has he done?”", "그가 여인에게 말한다. \"가엾은 것, 이 애가 뭘 했단 말이오?\""]], [["To which she only replies, shaking her head at the prostrate figure more amazedly than angrily, “Oh, you Jo, you Jo.", "여인은 그 말에 대꾸 대신, 노여움보다는 어이없다는 표정으로 엎드린 형체를 보며 고개를 흔들 뿐이다. \"오, 너 조, 너 조."], ["I have found you at last!”", "드디어 널 찾았구나!\""]], [["“What has he done?”", "\"이 애가 뭘 했소?\""], ["says Allan.", "앨런이 말한다."], ["“Has he robbed you?”", "\"당신 걸 훔쳤소?\""]], [["“No, sir, no.", "\"아니에요, 나리, 아니에요."], ["Robbed me?", "제 걸 훔쳐요?"], ["He did nothing but what was kind-hearted by me, and that’s the wonder of it.”", "이 애는 저한테 마음씨 고운 짓밖에 한 게 없어요. 바로 그게 놀라운 일이지요.\""]], [["Allan looks from Jo to the woman, and from the woman to Jo, waiting for one of them to unravel the riddle.", "앨런은 조에서 여인으로, 여인에서 조로 번갈아 보며, 둘 중 하나가 이 수수께끼를 풀어주기를 기다린다."]], [["“But he was along with me, sir,”", "\"이 애가 저랑 함께 있었거든요, 나리.\""], ["says the woman.", "여인이 말한다."], ["“Oh, you Jo!", "\"오, 너 조!"], ["He was along with me, sir, down at Saint Albans, ill, and a young lady, Lord bless her for a good friend to me, took pity on him when I durstn’t, and took him home—”", "이 애가 저랑 함께 있었어요, 나리, 저 아래 세인트올번스에서, 앓고 있었지요. 그런데 어느 아가씨가, 저한테 좋은 벗이 되어주신 그분께 주님의 축복이 있기를, 제가 차마 못 할 때 이 애를 가엾이 여겨 집으로 데려갔는데요—\""]], [["Allan shrinks back from him with a sudden horror.", "앨런이 갑작스러운 공포에 그에게서 움찔 물러난다."]], [["“Yes, sir, yes.", "\"네, 나리, 네."], ["Took him home, and made him comfortable, and like a thankless monster he ran away in the night and never has been seen or heard of since till I set eyes on him just now.", "집으로 데려가서, 편히 지내게 해줬는데, 이 배은망덕한 괴물 같은 놈이 밤중에 달아나서는, 방금 제 눈에 띌 때까지 코빼기도 안 보이고 소식도 없었어요."], ["And that young lady that was such a pretty dear caught his illness, lost her beautiful looks, and wouldn’t hardly be known for the same young lady now if it wasn’t for her angel temper, and her pretty shape, and her sweet voice.", "그리고 그렇게 어여쁜 아가씨가 이 애의 병이 옮아서, 그 고운 얼굴을 잃고, 천사 같은 성품이랑 어여쁜 몸매랑 다정한 목소리만 아니었으면 지금은 같은 아가씨라고 알아보기도 힘들 지경이에요."], ["Do you know it?", "알기나 해?"], ["You ungrateful wretch, do you know that this is all along of you and of her goodness to you?”", "이 배은망덕한 놈아, 이게 다 너 때문이고, 너한테 베푼 그분의 친절 때문이라는 걸 알기나 하냐고?\""], ["demands the woman, beginning to rage at him as she recalls it and breaking into passionate tears.", "여인이 그 일을 떠올리며 그에게 화를 내기 시작하더니 격한 눈물을 터뜨린다."]], [["The boy, in rough sort stunned by what he hears, falls to smearing his dirty forehead with his dirty palm, and to staring at the ground, and to shaking from head to foot until the crazy hoarding against which he leans rattles.", "소년은 자기가 듣는 말에 거칠게 얻어맞은 듯 멍해져서는, 더러운 손바닥으로 더러운 이마를 문질러대고, 땅바닥을 멀거니 쳐다보며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨다 못해 기댄 미친 듯이 낡은 울타리가 덜컹덜컹 흔들린다."]], [["Allan restrains the woman, merely by a quiet gesture, but effectually.", "앨런은 그저 조용한 손짓 하나로 여인을 말리는데, 그게 효과가 있다."]], [["“Richard told me—”", "\"리처드가 나한테 말하기를—\""], ["He falters.", "그가 말을 더듬는다."], ["“I mean, I have heard of this—don’t mind me for a moment, I will speak presently.”", "\"내 말은, 이 이야기를 들은 적이 있다는 거요. 잠깐 나한테 신경 쓰지 마시오, 곧 말하리다.\""]], [["He turns away and stands for a while looking out at the covered passage.", "그는 돌아서서 한동안 지붕 덮인 골목 바깥을 내다보며 서 있다."], ["When he comes back, he has recovered his composure, except that he contends against an avoidance of the boy, which is so very remarkable that it absorbs the woman’s attention.", "다시 돌아왔을 때 그는 평정을 되찾았다. 다만 소년을 피하려는 마음과 맞서 싸우고 있는데, 그게 어찌나 두드러지는지 여인의 주의를 온통 끌어당긴다."]], [["“You hear what she says.", "\"저 사람이 하는 말을 들었지."], ["But get up, get up!”", "어서 일어나라, 일어나!\""]], [["Jo, shaking and chattering, slowly rises and stands, after the manner of his tribe in a difficulty, sideways against the hoarding, resting one of his high shoulders against it and covertly rubbing his right hand over his left and his left foot over his right.", "조가 떨면서 이를 딱딱 부딪치며 천천히 일어서더니, 곤경에 처한 제 동족의 방식대로 울타리에 모로 기대서서, 한쪽 솟은 어깨를 거기 받치고는 슬며시 오른손으로 왼손을, 왼발로 오른발을 비빈다."]], [["“You hear what she says, and I know it’s true.", "\"저 사람이 하는 말 들었지. 그게 사실이라는 걸 나도 안다."], ["Have you been here ever since?”", "줄곧 여기 있었느냐?\""]], [["“Wishermaydie if I seen Tom-all-Alone’s till this blessed morning,”", "\"오늘 이 좋은 아침까장 톰 혼자 사는 데를 본 적 없으면 천벌 받지유.\""], ["replies Jo hoarsely.", "조가 쉰 목소리로 대답한다."]], [["“Why have you come here now?”", "\"그럼 왜 지금 여기 왔느냐?\""]], [["Jo looks all round the confined court, looks at his questioner no higher than the knees, and finally answers, “I don’t know how to do nothink, and I can’t get nothink to do.", "조는 비좁은 안마당을 빙 둘러보고, 질문한 이를 무릎 위로는 쳐다보지도 못한 채 마침내 대답한다. \"난 아무것두 헐 줄 모르구, 헐 일두 못 구해유."], ["I’m wery poor and ill, and I thought I’d come back here when there warn’t nobody about, and lay down and hide somewheres as I knows on till arter dark, and then go and beg a trifle of Mr. Snagsby.", "난 무척 가난허구 아픈디, 아무두 없을 때 여기루 다시 와서, 어두워질 때까장 내가 아는 어디 한 군데 드러누워 숨어 있다가, 그담에 가서 스내그스비 나리헌테 푼돈이라두 빌어볼까 혔구먼유."], ["He wos allus willin fur to give me somethink he wos, though Mrs.", "그 나리는 늘 나헌테 뭔가 줄 마음이 있었슈, 그랬다구유."], ["Snagsby she was allus a-chivying on me—like everybody everywheres.”", "스내그스비 부인은 늘 날 쫓아내려구 혔지만유, 어디서나 누구나 다 그러듯이유.\""]], [["“Where have you come from?”", "\"어디서 왔느냐?\""]], [["Jo looks all round the court again, looks at his questioner’s knees again, and concludes by laying his profile against the hoarding in a sort of resignation.", "조는 다시 안마당을 빙 둘러보고, 다시 질문한 이의 무릎을 쳐다보다가, 결국 일종의 체념한 태도로 옆얼굴을 울타리에 갖다 댄다."]], [["“Did you hear me ask you where you have come from?”", "\"내가 어디서 왔느냐고 물은 걸 못 들었느냐?\""]], [["“Tramp then,”", "\"그냥 떠돌았슈.\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."]], [["“Now tell me,”", "\"자, 말해보아라.\""], ["proceeds Allan, making a strong effort to overcome his repugnance, going very near to him, and leaning over him with an expression of confidence, “tell me how it came about that you left that house when the good young lady had been so unfortunate as to pity you and take you home.”", "앨런이 거부감을 억누르려 안간힘을 쓰며, 그에게 바짝 다가가, 신뢰의 표정으로 그를 굽어보며 말을 잇는다. \"그 착한 아가씨가 너를 가엾이 여겨 집으로 데려가는 불운을 겪었는데, 어쩌다 그 집을 떠나게 됐는지 말해보아라.\""]], [["Jo suddenly comes out of his resignation and excitedly declares, addressing the woman, that he never known about the young lady, that he never heern about it, that he never went fur to hurt her, that he would sooner have hurt his own self, that he’d sooner have had his unfortnet ed chopped off than ever gone a-nigh her, and that she wos wery good to him, she wos.", "조가 별안간 체념에서 깨어나 흥분하여, 여인을 향해 단언한다. 자기는 그 아가씨에 대해 전혀 몰랐고, 그런 얘기는 들어본 적도 없으며, 그분을 해칠 마음은 결코 없었고, 차라리 제 몸을 해쳤으면 했지, 그분 곁에 가느니 차라리 제 불쌍한 머리통을 잘렸을 거라고, 그분은 자기한테 무척 잘해줬다고 말한다."], ["Conducting himself throughout as if in his poor fashion he really meant it, and winding up with some very miserable sobs.", "시종일관 그 가엾은 방식으로나마 정말 진심인 듯이 굴다가, 무척 비참한 흐느낌으로 끝맺는다."]], [["Allan Woodcourt sees that this is not a sham.", "앨런 우드코트는 이것이 거짓이 아님을 안다."], ["He constrains himself to touch him.", "그는 억지로 자신을 다잡아 그를 만진다."], ["“Come, Jo.", "\"자, 조."], ["Tell me.”", "말해다오.\""]], [["“No. I dustn’t,”", "\"안 돼유."], ["says Jo, relapsing into the profile state.", "그럴 수 없슈.\" 조가 다시 옆얼굴 자세로 돌아가며 말한다."], ["“I dustn’t, or I would.”", "\"그럴 수 없슈, 안 그러면 말허것지만유.\""]], [["“But I must know,”", "\"하지만 나는 알아야겠다.\""], ["returns the other, “all the same.", "다른 이가 대답한다. \"그래도 말이다."], ["Come, Jo.”", "자, 조.\""]], [["After two or three such adjurations, Jo lifts up his head again, looks round the court again, and says in a low voice, “Well, I’ll tell you something.", "이렇게 두세 번 다그치자, 조가 다시 고개를 들고, 다시 안마당을 둘러보더니, 나직한 목소리로 말한다. \"그럼, 한 가지만 말헐게유."], ["I was took away.", "난 끌려갔슈."], ["There!”", "자, 됐쥬!\""]], [["“Took away?", "\"끌려가?"], ["In the night?”", "밤중에?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["Very apprehensive of being overheard, Jo looks about him and even glances up some ten feet at the top of the hoarding and through the cracks in it lest the object of his distrust should be looking over or hidden on the other side.", "누가 엿들을까 무척 겁먹은 조는 사방을 둘러보고, 심지어 울타리 꼭대기까지 삼 미터쯤 위로 흘끔 올려다보고는 그 틈새까지 살핀다. 자기가 믿지 못하는 그 대상이 넘겨다보거나 반대편에 숨어 있을까 봐서다."]], [["“Who took you away?”", "\"누가 너를 끌고 갔느냐?\""]], [["“I dustn’t name him,”", "\"그 이름은 못 대유.\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."], ["“I dustn’t do it, sir.", "\"못 대유, 나리.\""]], [["“But I want, in the young lady’s name, to know.", "\"하지만 나는 그 아가씨의 이름으로 알아야겠다."], ["You may trust me.", "나를 믿어도 좋다."], ["No one else shall hear.”", "다른 누구도 듣지 못할 테니.\""]], [["“Ah, but I don’t know,”", "\"아, 허지만 모르것슈.\""], ["replies Jo, shaking his head fearfully, “as he DON’T hear.”", "조가 겁에 질려 고개를 흔들며 대답한다. \"그자가 안 듣는다는 걸 말이유.\""]], [["“Why, he is not in this place.”", "\"아니, 그자는 여기 없잖느냐.\""]], [["“Oh, ain’t he though?”", "\"오, 없을 것 같쥬?\""], ["says Jo.", "조가 말한다."], ["“He’s in all manner of places, all at wanst.”", "\"그자는 온갖 데 다 있슈, 한꺼번에 다유.\""]], [["Allan looks at him in perplexity, but discovers some real meaning and good faith at the bottom of this bewildering reply.", "앨런은 어리둥절하여 그를 바라보지만, 이 어리둥절한 대답 밑바닥에 어떤 진짜 의미와 진심이 깔려 있음을 알아챈다."], ["He patiently awaits an explicit answer; and Jo, more baffled by his patience than by anything else, at last desperately whispers a name in his ear.", "그는 참을성 있게 명확한 답을 기다린다. 그리고 조는 그 무엇보다 그의 참을성에 더 어쩔 줄 몰라 하다가, 마침내 절박하게 그의 귀에 어떤 이름을 속삭인다."]], [["“Aye!”", "\"이런!\""], ["says Allan.", "앨런이 말한다."], ["“Why, what had you been doing?”", "\"아니, 너는 무슨 짓을 했길래?\""]], [["“Nothink, sir.", "\"아무것두 안 혔슈, 나리."], ["Never done nothink to get myself into no trouble, ’sept in not moving on and the inkwhich.", "말썽에 휘말릴 짓은 결코 안 혔슈. 자리를 안 비킨 거랑 그 검시판 말구는유."], ["But I’m a-moving on now.", "허지만 지금은 비키구 있슈."], ["I’m a-moving on to the berryin ground—that’s the move as I’m up to.”", "매장지루 비켜 가구 있슈, 그게 내가 허는 비키기유.\""]], [["“No, no, we will try to prevent that.", "\"아니, 아니, 그건 막아보자꾸나."], ["But what did he do with you?”", "한데 그자가 너를 어떻게 했느냐?\""]], [["“Put me in a horsepittle,”", "\"날 병원에 처넣었슈.\" 조가 속삭이며 대답한다."], ["replied Jo, whispering, “till I was discharged, then giv me a little money—four half-bulls, wot you may call half-crowns—and ses ‘Hook it!", "\"퇴원헐 때까장유. 그담에 푼돈을, 반 황소 네 닢, 그러니께 댁들이 말허는 반 크라운 네 닢을 주구는, '꺼져!"], ["Nobody wants you here,’ he ses.", "여긴 아무두 너 따위 필요 없어' 허구는, '꺼지란 말여."], ["‘You hook it.", "가서 떠돌어'"], ["You go and tramp,’ he ses.", "허구는, '비켜' 허구는, '런던에서 사십 마일 안쪽 어디서두 두 번 다시 내 눈에 띄지 마라, 안 그러면 후회헐 거다'"], ["‘You move on,’ he ses.", "혔슈. 그러것쥬, 만약 그자가 날 보면유."], ["‘Don’t let me ever see you nowheres within forty mile of London, or you’ll repent it.’ So I shall, if ever he doos see me, and he’ll see me if I’m above ground,”", "내가 땅 위에 살아 있는 한 그자는 날 볼 거구유.\" 조는 신경질적으로 아까의 그 조심성과 살핌을 모두 되풀이하며 끝맺는다."], ["concludes Jo, nervously repeating all his former precautions and investigations.", "* 반 황소: 조의 거리 은어. 반 크라운(은화)을 가리킨다."]], [["Allan considers a little, then remarks, turning to the woman but keeping an encouraging eye on Jo, “He is not so ungrateful as you supposed.", "앨런은 잠시 생각하더니, 여인 쪽으로 돌아서되 조에게는 격려의 눈길을 거두지 않은 채 말한다. \"이 애는 당신이 생각한 것만큼 배은망덕하진 않소."], ["He had a reason for going away, though it was an insufficient one.”", "떠난 데는 까닭이 있었던 거요. 충분치는 않은 까닭이었지만.\""]], [["“Thankee, sir, thankee!”", "\"고맙구먼유, 나리, 고맙구먼유!\""], ["exclaims Jo.", "조가 외친다."], ["“There now!", "\"거 보슈!"], ["See how hard you wos upon me.", "댁이 나헌테 얼매나 모질게 굴었는지."], ["But ony you tell the young lady wot the genlmn ses, and it’s all right.", "허지만 그저 그 아가씨헌테 이 나리가 허신 말씀만 전혀주면, 다 괜찮유."], ["For YOU wos wery good to me too, and I knows it.”", "댁두 나헌테 무척 잘혀줬으니께유, 나두 알아유.\""]], [["“Now, Jo,”", "\"자, 조.\""], ["says Allan, keeping his eye upon him, “come with me and I will find you a better place than this to lie down and hide in.", "앨런이 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말한다. \"나랑 같이 가자."], ["If I take one side of the way and you the other to avoid observation, you will not run away, I know very well, if you make me a promise.”", "여기보다 누워 숨기에 더 나은 데를 찾아주마. 남들 눈에 안 띄게 나는 길 한쪽으로, 너는 다른 쪽으로 가면, 네가 약속만 한다면 달아나지 않으리란 걸 나는 잘 안다.\""]], [["“I won’t, not unless I wos to see HIM a-coming, sir.”", "\"안 달아나유, 그자가 오는 게 보이지 않는 한은유, 나리.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋다."], ["I take your word.", "네 말을 믿으마."], ["Half the town is getting up by this time, and the whole town will be broad awake in another hour.", "이맘때쯤이면 마을 절반이 일어나고, 한 시간만 더 지나면 마을 전체가 완전히 깰 거다."], ["Come along.", "가자."], ["Good day again, my good woman.”", "잘 가시오, 착한 아주머니.\""]], [["“Good day again, sir, and I thank you kindly many times again.”", "\"안녕히 가세요, 나리, 거듭거듭 진심으로 고맙습니다.\""]], [["She has been sitting on her bag, deeply attentive, and now rises and takes it up.", "여인은 가방 위에 앉아 깊이 귀 기울이고 있다가, 이제 일어나 그것을 집어 든다."], ["Jo, repeating, “Ony you tell the young lady as I never went fur to hurt her and wot the genlmn ses!”", "조는 \"그저 그 아가씨헌테 내가 그분 해칠 맘은 결코 없었다구, 이 나리가 허신 말씀을 꼭 전혀줘유!\""], ["nods and shambles and shivers, and smears and blinks, and half laughs and half cries, a farewell to her, and takes his creeping way along after Allan Woodcourt, close to the houses on the opposite side of the street.", "하고 되뇌며, 고개를 끄덕이고 비척거리고 부르르 떨고, 얼굴을 문지르고 눈을 깜빡이고, 반은 웃고 반은 울며 그녀에게 작별을 고하고는, 앨런 우드코트의 뒤를 따라 길 건너편 집들에 바짝 붙어 기어가듯 길을 나선다."], ["In this order, the two come up out of Tom-all-Alone’s into the broad rays of the sunlight and the purer air.", "이런 차림새로 두 사람은 톰 혼자 사는 곳에서 빠져나와 환한 햇살과 한결 맑은 공기 속으로 올라온다."]], [["Jo’s Will\nAs Allan Woodcourt and Jo proceed along the streets where the high church spires and the distances are so near and clear in the morning light that the city itself seems renewed by rest, Allan revolves in his mind how and where he shall bestow his companion.", "앨런 우드코트와 조가 거리를 따라 걸어간다. 아침 햇살 속에서 우뚝 솟은 교회 첨탑들과 먼 풍경이 어찌나 가깝고 또렷한지 도시 전체가 휴식으로 새로 태어난 듯하다."], ["“It surely is a strange fact,”", "앨런은 이 동행을 어디에 어떻게 맡길지 머릿속으로 궁리한다. \"참으로 묘한 일이다.\""], ["he considers, “that in the heart of a civilized world this creature in human form should be more difficult to dispose of than an unowned dog.”", "그는 생각한다. \"문명 세계 한복판에서 사람 모습을 한 이 가련한 존재를 처리하는 일이, 주인 없는 개를 처리하는 것보다 더 어렵다니.\""], ["But it is none the less a fact because of its strangeness, and the difficulty remains.", "그러나 묘하다고 해서 사실이 아닌 건 아니며, 어려움은 여전히 남아 있다."]], [["At first he looks behind him often to assure himself that Jo is still really following.", "처음에 그는 조가 정말로 따라오고 있는지 확인하려고 뒤를 자주 돌아본다."], ["But look where he will, he still beholds him close to the opposite houses, making his way with his wary hand from brick to brick and from door to door, and often, as he creeps along, glancing over at him watchfully.", "그러나 어디를 보든 조는 늘 맞은편 집들에 바싹 붙은 채, 조심스러운 손길로 벽돌에서 벽돌로, 문에서 문으로 더듬으며 길을 헤쳐 나오고, 살금살금 기어오면서도 그를 향해 경계하듯 흘끔거린다."], ["Soon satisfied that the last thing in his thoughts is to give him the slip, Allan goes on, considering with a less divided attention what he shall do.", "곧 그를 따돌릴 생각 따위는 조의 머릿속에 전혀 없음을 확인하자, 앨런은 한층 덜 산만한 정신으로 어떻게 해야 할지 궁리하며 걸음을 옮긴다."]], [["A breakfast-stall at a street-corner suggests the first thing to be done.", "길모퉁이의 간이 아침 식사 가판대가 가장 먼저 할 일을 일러준다."], ["He stops there, looks round, and beckons Jo.", "그는 거기 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보고 조에게 손짓한다."], ["Jo crosses and comes halting and shuffling up, slowly scooping the knuckles of his right hand round and round in the hollowed palm of his left, kneading dirt with a natural pestle and mortar.", "조가 길을 건너 절뚝거리고 발을 끌며 다가온다. 오른손 마디를 왼손 오목한 손바닥에 대고 천천히 빙빙 문지르며, 타고난 절구와 절굿공이로 때를 반죽한다."], ["What is a dainty repast to Jo is then set before him, and he begins to gulp the coffee and to gnaw the bread and butter, looking anxiously about him in all directions as he eats and drinks, like a scared animal.", "조에게는 진수성찬일 음식이 그의 앞에 차려지고, 그는 먹고 마시는 내내 사방을 불안하게 두리번거리며, 겁먹은 짐승처럼 커피를 꿀꺽꿀꺽 삼키고 버터 바른 빵을 갉아 먹기 시작한다."]], [["But he is so sick and miserable that even hunger has abandoned him.", "그러나 그는 너무 아프고 비참해서 허기조차 그를 떠나버렸다."], ["“I thought I was amost a-starvin, sir,”", "\"거의 굶어 죽을 지경인 줄 알았어요, 나리.\""], ["says Jo, soon putting down his food, “but I don’t know nothink—not even that.", "조가 곧 음식을 내려놓으며 말한다. \"근데 아무것도 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t care for eating wittles nor yet for drinking on ’em.”", "그것조차 모르겠어요. 음식을 먹고 싶은 마음도, 마시고 싶은 마음도 없어요.\""], ["And Jo stands shivering and looking at the breakfast wonderingly.", "그러고는 조가 부들부들 떨며 의아한 듯 아침 식사를 바라본다."]], [["Allan Woodcourt lays his hand upon his pulse and on his chest.", "앨런 우드코트가 그의 맥을 짚고 가슴에 손을 댄다. \"숨을 들이쉬어 봐, 조!\""], ["“Draw breath, Jo!”", "\"들이쉬어지긴 해요.\""], ["“It draws,”", "조가 말한다."], ["says Jo, “as heavy as a cart.”", "\"수레만큼이나 무겁게요.\""], ["He might add, “And rattles like it,”", "\"수레처럼 덜컹거리기도 한다\"고 덧붙일 법한데도, 그는 그저 중얼거릴 뿐이다."], ["but he only mutters, “I’m a-moving on, sir.”", "\"비키며 가는 중이에요, 나리.\""]], [["Allan looks about for an apothecary’s shop.", "앨런이 약방을 찾아 주위를 둘러본다."], ["There is none at hand, but a tavern does as well or better.", "가까이엔 없지만 술집이 그만큼, 아니 그보다 더 쓸모가 있다."], ["He obtains a little measure of wine and gives the lad a portion of it very carefully.", "그는 포도주를 조금 받아와 아주 조심스럽게 소년에게 한 모금 먹인다."], ["He begins to revive almost as soon as it passes his lips.", "포도주가 입술을 지나자마자 조는 거의 곧바로 기운을 차리기 시작한다."], ["“We may repeat that dose, Jo,”", "\"이 약을 한 번 더 줘도 되겠다, 조.\""], ["observes Allan after watching him with his attentive face.", "앨런이 세심한 얼굴로 그를 지켜본 뒤 말한다."], ["“So!", "\"자!"], ["Now we will take five minutes’ rest, and then go on again.”", "이제 오 분만 쉬었다가, 다시 가자.\""]], [["Leaving the boy sitting on the bench of the breakfast-stall, with his back against an iron railing, Allan Woodcourt paces up and down in the early sunshine, casting an occasional look towards him without appearing to watch him.", "소년을 간이 가판대 의자에 앉히고 등을 쇠난간에 기대게 한 뒤, 앨런 우드코트는 이른 햇살 속을 오르락내리락 거닐며, 지켜보는 티는 내지 않은 채 이따금 그를 향해 눈길을 던진다."], ["It requires no discernment to perceive that he is warmed and refreshed.", "소년이 따뜻해지고 기운을 차렸음을 알아채는 데는 별다른 통찰이 필요하지 않다."], ["If a face so shaded can brighten, his face brightens somewhat; and by little and little he eats the slice of bread he had so hopelessly laid down.", "저토록 그늘진 얼굴이 환해질 수 있다면, 그의 얼굴이 다소 환해진다. 그리고 그토록 절망스럽게 내려놓았던 빵 한 조각을 조금씩 조금씩 먹는다."], ["Observant of these signs of improvement, Allan engages him in conversation and elicits to his no small wonder the adventure of the lady in the veil, with all its consequences.", "이렇게 호전되는 기색을 지켜보다가, 앨런이 그에게 말을 붙여, 적잖이 놀랍게도 베일 쓴 부인의 모험담을 그 모든 결말까지 끌어낸다."], ["Jo slowly munches as he slowly tells it.", "조는 느릿느릿 이야기하며 느릿느릿 우물거린다."], ["When he has finished his story and his bread, they go on again.", "이야기와 빵을 다 마치자, 둘은 다시 길을 떠난다."]], [["Intending to refer his difficulty in finding a temporary place of refuge for the boy to his old patient, zealous little Miss Flite, Allan leads the way to the court where he and Jo first foregathered.", "소년을 잠시 묵게 할 곳을 찾기가 어렵다는 이 난제를, 앨런은 자신의 오랜 환자이자 열성적인 자그마한 플라이트 양에게 상의하려 마음먹고, 그와 조가 처음 마주쳤던 그 뜰로 앞장서 간다."], ["But all is changed at the rag and bottle shop; Miss Flite no longer lodges there; it is shut up; and a hard-featured female, much obscured by dust, whose age is a problem, but who is indeed no other than the interesting Judy, is tart and spare in her replies.", "그러나 넝마와 병을 파는 가게는 모든 게 달라져 있다. 플라이트 양은 더 이상 거기 묵지 않는다."], ["These sufficing, however, to inform the visitor that Miss Flite and her birds are domiciled with a Mrs.", "가게는 닫혀 있다. 먼지를 잔뜩 뒤집어써 나이를 가늠하기 어려운, 그러나 실은 다름 아닌 그 흥미로운 인물 주디인, 모진 인상의 여자가 무뚝뚝하고 인색하게 대꾸한다."], ["Blinder, in Bell Yard, he repairs to that neighbouring place, where Miss Flite (who rises early that she may be punctual at the divan of justice held by her excellent friend the Chancellor) comes running downstairs with tears of welcome and with open arms.", "그래도 그 대꾸만으로 플라이트 양이 새들과 함께 벨 야드의 블라인더 부인 댁에 자리 잡았음을 알아낸 그는, 인근의 그곳으로 향한다. 거기서 플라이트 양이(훌륭한 친구인 대법관이 여는 정의의 회의에 늦지 않으려고 일찍 일어나는 그녀가) 환영의 눈물을 글썽이며 두 팔을 벌리고 계단을 뛰어 내려온다."]], [["“My dear physician!”", "\"내 친애하는 의사 선생님!\""], ["cries Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 외친다."], ["“My meritorious, distinguished, honourable officer!”", "\"공로 많고 출중하고 명예로운 장교님!\""], ["She uses some odd expressions, but is as cordial and full of heart as sanity itself can be—more so than it often is.", "그녀는 이상한 표현을 쓰긴 하지만, 멀쩡한 정신이 보일 수 있는 만큼이나, 아니 그보다도 더 다정하고 마음이 가득하다."], ["Allan, very patient with her, waits until she has no more raptures to express, then points out Jo, trembling in a doorway, and tells her how he comes there.", "앨런은 그녀에게 무척 인내심을 발휘하며, 그녀가 더 이상 토해낼 황홀경이 남지 않을 때까지 기다린 뒤, 문간에서 떨고 있는 조를 가리키며 어쩌다 그가 거기 오게 되었는지 들려준다."]], [["“Where can I lodge him hereabouts for the present?", "\"이 근처에서 당분간 이 아이를 어디 묵게 할 수 있을까요?"], ["Now, you have a fund of knowledge and good sense and can advise me.”", "부인은 지식과 분별이 풍부하니, 제게 조언을 해주실 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["Miss Flite, mighty proud of the compliment, sets herself to consider; but it is long before a bright thought occurs to her.", "플라이트 양은 그 칭찬에 잔뜩 우쭐해져 골똘히 생각에 잠긴다. 그러나 좋은 생각이 떠오르기까지는 한참이 걸린다."], ["Mrs. Blinder is entirely let, and she herself occupies poor Gridley’s room.", "블라인더 부인 댁은 방이 모조리 나가 있고, 그녀 자신은 가엾은 그리들리의 방을 쓰고 있다."], ["“Gridley!”", "\"그리들리!\""], ["exclaims Miss Flite, clapping her hands after a twentieth repetition of this remark.", "이 말을 스무 번째쯤 되풀이한 끝에 플라이트 양이 손뼉을 치며 외친다."], ["“Gridley!", "\"그리들리!"], ["To be sure!", "그렇고말고!"], ["Of course!", "물론이지!"], ["My dear physician!", "내 친애하는 의사 선생님!"], ["General George will help us out.”", "조지 장군이 우리를 도와줄 거예요.\""]], [["It is hopeless to ask for any information about General George, and would be, though Miss Flite had not already run upstairs to put on her pinched bonnet and her poor little shawl and to arm herself with her reticule of documents.", "조지 장군이 누구인지 알아보려고 묻는 건 부질없는 일이고, 설령 플라이트 양이 벌써 위층으로 뛰어 올라가 꼭 끼는 보닛과 초라하고 자그마한 숄을 두르고 서류 가방으로 무장하지 않았더라도 마찬가지였을 것이다."], ["But as she informs her physician in her disjointed manner on coming down in full array that General George, whom she often calls upon, knows her dear Fitz Jarndyce and takes a great interest in all connected with her, Allan is induced to think that they may be in the right way.", "그러나 그녀가 만반의 채비를 하고 내려오면서 두서없는 말투로, 자기가 자주 찾아가는 조지 장군이 친애하는 피츠 잔다이스를 알고 그녀와 관련된 모든 일에 큰 관심을 가지고 있다고 알려주자, 앨런은 둘이 옳은 길을 가고 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 든다."], ["So he tells Jo, for his encouragement, that this walking about will soon be over now; and they repair to the general’s.", "그래서 그는 조를 격려하느라, 이렇게 돌아다니는 일도 이제 곧 끝날 거라고 말한다. 그러고 둘은 장군의 집으로 향한다."], ["Fortunately it is not far.", "다행히 멀지 않다."]], [["From the exterior of George’s Shooting Gallery, and the long entry, and the bare perspective beyond it, Allan Woodcourt augurs well. He also descries promise in the figure of Mr.", "조지의 사격 연습장 겉모습과, 긴 입구와, 그 너머로 휑하니 펼쳐진 풍경을 보고, 앨런 우드코트는 길조를 점친다."], ["George himself, striding towards them in his morning exercise with his pipe in his mouth, no stock on, and his muscular arms, developed by broadsword and dumbbell, weightily asserting themselves through his light shirt-sleeves.", "아침 운동으로 그들을 향해 성큼성큼 다가오는 조지 씨 본인의 모습에서도 그는 희망을 엿본다. 입에는 파이프를 물고, 목에는 깃을 두르지 않았으며, 양날 검과 아령으로 단련한 우람한 두 팔이 얇은 셔츠 소매를 뚫고 묵직하게 그 존재감을 드러낸다."]], [["“Your servant, sir,”", "\"안녕하십니까, 선생.\""], ["says Mr. George with a military salute.", "조지 씨가 군대식 경례를 붙이며 말한다."], ["Good-humouredly smiling all over his broad forehead up into his crisp hair, he then defers to Miss Flite, as, with great stateliness, and at some length, she performs the courtly ceremony of presentation.", "그러고 너른 이마부터 곱슬머리까지 온통 흐뭇하게 웃다가, 플라이트 양이 위엄을 잔뜩 갖추고 한참에 걸쳐 정중한 소개 의식을 치르는 동안 그녀에게 정중히 양보한다."], ["He winds it up with another “Your servant, sir!” and another salute.", "그는 또 한 번 \"안녕하십니까, 선생!\"과 또 한 번의 경례로 그것을 마무리한다."]], [["“Excuse me, sir.", "\"실례지만, 선생."], ["A sailor, I believe?”", "뱃사람이신 듯한데요?\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I am proud to find I have the air of one,”", "\"제게 그런 풍모가 있다니 자랑스럽습니다.\""], ["returns Allan; “but I am only a sea-going doctor.”", "앨런이 대꾸한다. \"하지만 저는 그저 배를 타는 의사일 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir!", "\"그렇습니까, 선생!"], ["I should have thought you was a regular blue-jacket myself.”", "저는 정식 해군이신 줄로 알았는데요.\""]], [["Allan hopes Mr. George will forgive his intrusion the more readily on that account, and particularly that he will not lay aside his pipe, which, in his politeness, he has testified some intention of doing.", "앨런은 그래서 더더욱 조지 씨가 자신의 무례한 방문을 너그러이 용서해주기를 바라며, 특히 파이프를 치우지 말아 주기를 바란다고 말한다."], ["“You are very good, sir,”", "조지 씨가 예의상 파이프를 치울 기색을 보였기 때문이다."], ["returns the trooper.", "\"참 친절하십니다, 선생.\""], ["“As I know by experience that it’s not disagreeable to Miss Flite, and since it’s equally agreeable to yourself—”", "기병이 대꾸한다. \"플라이트 양이 싫어하지 않는 줄은 제 경험으로 잘 알고 있고, 선생께도 마찬가지로 괜찮으시다니—\""], ["and finishes the sentence by putting it between his lips again.", "그러고는 파이프를 다시 입에 물어 문장을 끝맺는다."], ["Allan proceeds to tell him all he knows about Jo, unto which the trooper listens with a grave face.", "앨런이 이어서 조에 대해 자기가 아는 모든 것을 들려주고, 기병은 진지한 얼굴로 귀를 기울인다."]], [["“And that’s the lad, sir, is it?”", "\"그러니까 저 아이가 그 녀석이로군요, 선생?\""], ["he inquires, looking along the entry to where Jo stands staring up at the great letters on the whitewashed front, which have no meaning in his eyes.", "그가 묻는다. 그러고는 입구를 따라, 회칠한 정면에 큼지막한 글자들이 적혀 있지만 자기 눈에는 아무 뜻도 없는 그것을 멍하니 올려다보고 선 조 쪽으로 시선을 던진다."]], [["“That’s he,”", "\"바로 그 녀석입니다.\" 앨런이 말한다."], ["says Allan. “And, Mr.", "\"그런데 조지 씨, 저 아이 문제로 제가 곤경에 빠져 있습니다."], ["George, I am in this difficulty about him.", "저는 저 아이를 병원에 넣고 싶지 않습니다. 당장 입원시킬 수 있다 해도요."], ["I am unwilling to place him in a hospital, even if I could procure him immediate admission, because I foresee that he would not stay there many hours if he could be so much as got there.", "거기 들여보낼 수만 있다 쳐도, 몇 시간을 못 버티고 나와버릴 게 뻔하기 때문입니다. 구빈원도 마찬가지예요."], ["The same objection applies to a workhouse, supposing I had the patience to be evaded and shirked, and handed about from post to pillar in trying to get him into one, which is a system that I don’t take kindly to.”", "저 아이를 거기 넣으려고 이리저리 따돌림당하고 외면당하며 이 기둥에서 저 기둥으로 떠넘겨지는 수고를 참아낸다 쳐도 말입니다. 그건 제가 도무지 정이 가지 않는 방식이라서요.\""]], [["“No man does, sir,”", "\"누군들 정이 가겠습니까, 선생.\""], ["returns Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 대꾸한다."]], [["“I am convinced that he would not remain in either place, because he is possessed by an extraordinary terror of this person who ordered him to keep out of the way; in his ignorance, he believes this person to be everywhere, and cognizant of everything.”", "\"저 아이는 어느 쪽에도 머물지 않으리라고 확신합니다. 자기더러 비켜 있으라고 명령한 사람에 대한 유별난 공포에 사로잡혀 있거든요. 무지한 탓에, 저 아이는 그 사람이 어디에나 있고 모든 것을 다 알고 있다고 믿고 있습니다.\""]], [["“I ask your pardon, sir,”", "\"실례지만, 선생.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“But you have not mentioned that party’s name.", "\"그자의 이름을 아직 말씀하지 않으셨군요."], ["Is it a secret, sir?”", "비밀입니까, 선생?\""]], [["“The boy makes it one.", "\"아이가 비밀로 삼고 있지요."], ["But his name is Bucket.”", "하지만 그 사람 이름은 버킷입니다.\""]], [["“Bucket the detective, sir?”", "\"형사 버킷 말입니까, 선생?\""]], [["“The same man.”", "\"바로 그 사람입니다.\""]], [["“The man is known to me, sir,”", "\"제가 아는 사람입니다, 선생.\" 기병이 연기를 한 모금 내뿜고 가슴을 떡 펴며 대꾸한다."], ["returns the trooper after blowing out a cloud of smoke and squaring his chest, “and the boy is so far correct that he undoubtedly is a—rum customer.”", "\"그리고 그 아이 말이 거기까진 옳아요. 그자는 분명—만만찮은 작자거든요.\""], ["Mr. George smokes with a profound meaning after this and surveys Miss Flite in silence.", "조지 씨는 이 말 뒤에 깊은 뜻을 담아 담배를 피우며, 말없이 플라이트 양을 바라본다."]], [["“Now, I wish Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson at least to know that this Jo, who tells so strange a story, has reappeared, and to have it in their power to speak with him if they should desire to do so.", "\"그래서 저는 적어도 잔다이스 씨와 서머슨 양이 이 사실을, 그러니까 그토록 기이한 이야기를 들려주는 이 조가 다시 나타났다는 사실을 알게 되고, 만약 원한다면 이 아이와 이야기를 나눌 수 있도록 해주고 싶습니다."], ["Therefore I want to get him, for the present moment, into any poor lodging kept by decent people where he would be admitted.", "그래서 당분간 점잖은 사람들이 운영하는, 이 아이를 받아줄 만한 허름한 거처에라도 넣어두고 싶은 겁니다."], ["Decent people and Jo, Mr. George,”", "조지 씨, 보시다시피 점잖은 사람들과 조는.\""], ["says Allan, following the direction of the trooper’s eyes along the entry, “have not been much acquainted, as you see.", "앨런이 입구를 따라 향한 기병의 눈길을 좇으며 말한다. \"그다지 가깝게 지낸 사이가 못 됩니다."], ["Hence the difficulty.", "그래서 이게 어려운 거지요."], ["Do you happen to know any one in this neighbourhood who would receive him for a while on my paying for him beforehand?”", "혹시 이 근처에 제가 미리 비용을 치르면 얼마간 이 아이를 받아줄 만한 사람을 아십니까?\""]], [["As he puts the question, he becomes aware of a dirty-faced little man standing at the trooper’s elbow and looking up, with an oddly twisted figure and countenance, into the trooper’s face.", "이 질문을 던지면서 그는 기병의 팔꿈치께에 더러운 얼굴의 자그마한 사내가 서서, 기묘하게 비틀린 몸매와 표정으로 기병의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있음을 알아챈다."], ["After a few more puffs at his pipe, the trooper looks down askant at the little man, and the little man winks up at the trooper.", "파이프를 몇 모금 더 빨고 나서, 기병이 그 자그마한 사내를 비스듬히 내려다보고, 자그마한 사내가 기병을 향해 눈을 찡긋한다."]], [["“Well, sir,” says Mr.", "\"그러시다면, 선생.\""], ["George, “I can assure you that I would willingly be knocked on the head at any time if it would be at all agreeable to Miss Summerson, and consequently I esteem it a privilege to do that young lady any service, however small.", "조지 씨가 말한다. \"분명히 말씀드리는데, 서머슨 양에게 조금이라도 좋은 일이 된다면 저는 언제든 기꺼이 머리통을 한 대 얻어맞을 수도 있습니다."], ["We are naturally in the vagabond way here, sir, both myself and Phil.", "그러니 그 아가씨께 아무리 사소한 거라도 도움을 드리는 일을 영광으로 여깁니다. 여기 저랑 필은 천성이 떠돌이 신세라서요, 선생."], ["You see what the place is.", "보시다시피 이 꼴인 곳입니다."], ["You are welcome to a quiet corner of it for the boy if the same would meet your views.", "선생 뜻에 맞으신다면 이곳 조용한 구석을 이 아이에게 기꺼이 내드리지요."], ["No charge made, except for rations.", "식대 말고는 따로 받을 게 없습니다."], ["We are not in a flourishing state of circumstances here, sir.", "여기 형편이 그리 넉넉지가 못해서요, 선생."], ["We are liable to be tumbled out neck and crop at a moment’s notice.", "언제 통고 한마디에 멱살 잡혀 쫓겨날지 모르는 신세입니다."], ["However, sir, such as the place is, and so long as it lasts, here it is at your service.”", "하지만 선생, 이런 곳이나마, 그리고 여기가 버티는 동안은, 얼마든지 쓰시지요.\""]], [["With a comprehensive wave of his pipe, Mr. George places the whole building at his visitor’s disposal.", "조지 씨가 파이프를 한 바퀴 휘둘러, 건물 전체를 방문객 마음대로 쓰라고 내준다."]], [["“I take it for granted, sir,”", "\"당연한 얘기겠습니다만, 선생.\""], ["he adds, “you being one of the medical staff, that there is no present infection about this unfortunate subject?”", "그가 덧붙인다. \"선생이 의료진의 한 분이시니, 이 가엾은 아이가 당장 옮길 만한 전염병 같은 건 없겠지요?\""]], [["Allan is quite sure of it.", "앨런은 확실히 없다고 한다."]], [["“Because, sir,” says Mr.", "\"그게 말입니다, 선생.\""], ["George, shaking his head sorrowfully, “we have had enough of that.”", "조지 씨가 슬프게 고개를 저으며 말한다. \"그런 건 이미 충분히 겪을 만큼 겪었거든요.\""]], [["His tone is no less sorrowfully echoed by his new acquaintance.", "그의 어조에 못지않게 슬픈 어조로 새로 사귄 지인도 맞장구를 친다."], ["“Still I am bound to tell you,”", "\"그래도 말씀드려야겠습니다.\""], ["observes Allan after repeating his former assurance, “that the boy is deplorably low and reduced and that he may be—I do not say that he is—too far gone to recover.”", "앨런이 아까의 확언을 거듭한 뒤 말한다. \"이 아이는 한심할 만큼 쇠약하고 망가져 있어서, 어쩌면—꼭 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만—회복하기엔 너무 깊이 가버렸을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Do you consider him in present danger, sir?”", "\"지금 위독하다고 보십니까, 선생?\" 기병이 묻는다."], ["inquires the trooper.", "\"네, 그런 것 같아 두렵습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, I fear so.” “Then, sir,”", "\"그렇다면, 선생.\""], ["returns the trooper in a decisive manner, “it appears to me—being naturally in the vagabond way myself—that the sooner he comes out of the street, the better.", "기병이 단호한 어조로 대꾸한다. \"제 생각에는—저 자신이 천성이 떠돌이 신세이니 하는 말입니다만—저 아이를 길거리에서 끌어들이는 게 빠르면 빠를수록 좋겠습니다."], ["You, Phil!", "야, 필!"], ["Bring him in!”", "데리고 들어와!\""]], [["Mr. Squod tacks out, all on one side, to execute the word of command; and the trooper, having smoked his pipe, lays it by.", "스쿼드 씨가 한쪽으로 몸을 기울인 채 명령을 수행하러 종종걸음으로 나간다."], ["Jo is brought in. He is not one of Mrs.", "기병은 파이프를 다 피우고 한쪽에 내려놓는다."], ["Pardiggle’s Tockahoopo Indians; he is not one of Mrs.", "조가 들어온다. 조는 파디글 부인의 토커후포 인디언이 아니다."], ["Jellyby’s lambs, being wholly unconnected with Borrioboola-Gha; he is not softened by distance and unfamiliarity; he is not a genuine foreign-grown savage; he is the ordinary home-made article.", "조는 보리오불라가와는 전혀 무관하니, 젤리비 부인의 어린양도 아니다. 조는 거리와 낯섦으로 미화된 존재가 아니다."], ["Dirty, ugly, disagreeable to all the senses, in body a common creature of the common streets, only in soul a heathen.", "조는 진짜 외국에서 자란 야만인이 아니다. 조는 평범한 국산품이다."], ["Homely filth begrimes him, homely parasites devour him, homely sores are in him, homely rags are on him; native ignorance, the growth of English soil and climate, sinks his immortal nature lower than the beasts that perish.", "더럽고, 추하고, 모든 감각에 거슬리며, 몸뚱이는 흔한 거리의 흔한 짐승이요, 영혼만 이교도다. 토종 오물이 그를 더럽히고, 토종 기생충이 그를 파먹고, 토종 종기가 그의 몸에 돋고, 토종 누더기가 그의 몸에 걸쳐 있다."], ["Stand forth, Jo, in uncompromising colours!", "영국 땅과 기후에서 자란 토종 무지가 그의 불멸하는 본성을 스러질 짐승보다 더 낮은 데로 가라앉힌다."], ["From the sole of thy foot to the crown of thy head, there is nothing interesting about thee.", "조여, 한 점 에누리 없는 본색으로 나서라! 네 발바닥부터 정수리까지, 네게는 흥미로울 것이라곤 하나도 없다."]], [["He shuffles slowly into Mr. George’s gallery and stands huddled together in a bundle, looking all about the floor.", "그는 발을 끌며 천천히 조지 씨의 연습장 안으로 들어와, 보따리처럼 잔뜩 웅크린 채 바닥만 두루 살핀다."], ["He seems to know that they have an inclination to shrink from him, partly for what he is and partly for what he has caused.", "그는 사람들이 자기를 꺼리고 싶어 한다는 걸 아는 듯하다. 자기가 어떤 존재인지 때문이기도 하고, 자기가 무슨 일을 저질렀는지 때문이기도 하다."], ["He, too, shrinks from them.", "그 역시 그들을 꺼린다."], ["He is not of the same order of things, not of the same place in creation.", "그는 같은 부류에 속하지 않고, 창조의 질서 안에서 같은 자리를 차지하지 않는다."], ["He is of no order and no place, neither of the beasts nor of humanity.", "그는 어떤 부류에도, 어떤 자리에도 속하지 않으니, 짐승에도 인간에도 속하지 않는다."]], [["“Look here, Jo!”", "\"이리 봐라, 조!\""], ["says Allan.", "앨런이 말한다."], ["“This is Mr. George.”", "\"이분은 조지 씨다.\""]], [["Jo searches the floor for some time longer, then looks up for a moment, and then down again.", "조는 한동안 더 바닥을 살피다가, 잠깐 올려다보고, 다시 내려다본다."]], [["“He is a kind friend to you, for he is going to give you lodging room here.”", "\"이분은 네게 친절한 친구다. 너한테 여기 묵을 방을 내주실 거니까.\""]], [["Jo makes a scoop with one hand, which is supposed to be a bow.", "조가 한 손으로 한 번 떠올리는 시늉을 하는데, 그게 절인 모양이다."], ["After a little more consideration and some backing and changing of the foot on which he rests, he mutters that he is “wery thankful.”", "잠시 더 궁리하고 발을 이리저리 바꿔 디딘 끝에, 그는 \"정말 고맙습니다\" 하고 중얼거린다."]], [["“You are quite safe here.", "\"여기선 아주 안전하다."], ["All you have to do at present is to be obedient and to get strong.", "지금 네가 할 일은 그저 말 잘 듣고 기운을 차리는 것뿐이야."], ["And mind you tell us the truth here, whatever you do, Jo.”", "그리고 명심해라, 조, 무슨 일이 있어도 여기선 우리한테 사실대로 말해야 한다.\""]], [["“Wishermaydie if I don’t, sir,”", "\"안 그러면 죽어버릴게요, 나리.\""], ["says Jo, reverting to his favourite declaration.", "조가 즐겨 쓰는 맹세로 되돌아가 말한다."], ["“I never done nothink yit, but wot you knows on, to get myself into no trouble.", "\"전 여태껏 나리가 아시는 거 말고는 말썽 부릴 짓을 한 적이 한 번도 없어요."], ["I never was in no other trouble at all, sir, ’sept not knowin’ nothink and starwation.”", "다른 말썽 같은 건 아예 없었어요, 나리. 아무것도 모르는 거랑 굶주리는 거 말곤요.\""]], [["“I believe it, now attend to Mr. George.", "\"믿는다, 이제 조지 씨 말씀을 들어라."], ["I see he is going to speak to you.”", "너한테 하실 말씀이 있으신 것 같다.\""]], [["“My intention merely was, sir,” observes Mr.", "\"제 뜻은 그저, 선생.\""], ["George, amazingly broad and upright, “to point out to him where he can lie down and get a thorough good dose of sleep.", "조지 씨가 놀랍도록 떡 벌어진 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 말한다. \"이 녀석한테 어디서 누워 흠뻑 한숨 잘 수 있는지 일러주려는 거였습니다."], ["Now, look here.”", "자, 이리 보거라.\""], ["As the trooper speaks, he conducts them to the other end of the gallery and opens one of the little cabins.", "기병은 말하면서 그들을 연습장 저쪽 끝으로 데려가, 자그마한 칸막이 방 하나를 연다."], ["“There you are, you see!", "\"자, 보거라!"], ["Here is a mattress, and here you may rest, on good behaviour, as long as Mr., I ask your pardon, sir”—he refers apologetically to the card Allan has given him—“Mr.", "여기 매트리스가 있고, 행실만 바르면, 여기서 쉬어도 된다."], ["Woodcourt pleases.", "저, 실례지만, 선생—\""], ["Don’t you be alarmed if you hear shots; they’ll be aimed at the target, and not you.", "그가 앨런이 건넨 명함을 미안한 듯 들여다본다. \"우드코트 씨가 좋다고 하시는 동안은 말이다."], ["Now, there’s another thing I would recommend, sir,”", "총소리가 들려도 놀라지 마라. 표적을 겨눈 거지 너를 겨눈 게 아니니까."], ["says the trooper, turning to his visitor.", "자, 한 가지 더 권하고 싶은 게 있습니다, 선생.\" 기병이 방문객 쪽으로 돌아서며 말한다."], ["“Phil, come here!”", "\"필, 이리 와!\""]], [["Phil bears down upon them according to his usual tactics.", "필이 늘 하던 식으로 그들을 향해 비스듬히 다가온다."], ["“Here is a man, sir, who was found, when a baby, in the gutter.", "\"여기 이 사람은 말입니다, 선생, 갓난아기 때 시궁창에서 발견된 자입니다."], ["Consequently, it is to be expected that he takes a natural interest in this poor creature.", "그러니 이 가엾은 아이한테 자연히 마음이 가는 게 당연하지요."], ["You do, don’t you, Phil?”", "그렇지, 필?\""]], [["“Certainly and surely I do, guv’ner,”", "\"확실히, 틀림없이 그렇습니다요, 대장.\""], ["is Phil’s reply.", "필이 대답한다."]], [["“Now I was thinking, sir,” says Mr.", "\"그래서 제 생각엔 말입니다, 선생.\""], ["George in a martial sort of confidence, as if he were giving his opinion in a council of war at a drum-head, “that if this man was to take him to a bath and was to lay out a few shillings in getting him one or two coarse articles—”", "마치 북 위에 펼쳐진 군사 회의에서 의견을 내놓듯, 조지 씨가 군인다운 비밀스러운 어조로 말한다. \"이 사람이 저 아이를 목욕탕에 데려가서, 몇 실링쯤 들여 거친 옷가지 한두 벌을 마련해준다면—\""]], [["“Mr. George, my considerate friend,”", "\"조지 씨, 사려 깊은 친구분.\""], ["returns Allan, taking out his purse, “it is the very favour I would have asked.”", "앨런이 지갑을 꺼내며 대꾸한다. \"바로 제가 부탁드리려던 일입니다.\""]], [["Phil Squod and Jo are sent out immediately on this work of improvement.", "필 스쿼드와 조는 이 단장(端裝)의 임무를 띠고 즉시 내보내진다. 자기 성공에 흠뻑 취한 플라이트 양은 부랴부랴 법정으로 향한다."], ["Miss Flite, quite enraptured by her success, makes the best of her way to court, having great fears that otherwise her friend the Chancellor may be uneasy about her or may give the judgment she has so long expected in her absence, and observing “which you know, my dear physician, and general, after so many years, would be too absurdly unfortunate!”", "안 그러면 친구인 대법관이 자기 걱정을 하거나, 자기가 없는 사이에 그토록 오래 기다려온 판결을 내려버릴까 봐 몹시 두려운 것이다. 그러면서 그녀는 한마디 덧붙인다."], ["Allan takes the opportunity of going out to procure some restorative medicines, and obtaining them near at hand, soon returns to find the trooper walking up and down the gallery, and to fall into step and walk with him.", "\"아시다시피, 내 친애하는 의사 선생님이자 장군님, 이렇게 오랜 세월이 흘렀는데 그리되면 너무 어처구니없이 불운한 일이잖아요!\" 앨런은 이 틈을 타 회복제 몇 가지를 사러 나갔다가, 가까운 데서 그것을 구해 곧 돌아와, 기병이 연습장을 오르락내리락 거니는 모습을 보고는 보조를 맞춰 함께 걷는다."]], [["“I take it, sir,” says Mr.", "\"제 짐작입니다만, 선생.\""], ["George, “that you know Miss Summerson pretty well?”", "조지 씨가 말한다. \"서머슨 양을 꽤 잘 아시지요?\""]], [["Yes, it appears.", "그렇다는 것 같다."]], [["“Not related to her, sir?”", "\"친척은 아니시고요, 선생?\""]], [["No, it appears.", "아니라는 것 같다."]], [["“Excuse the apparent curiosity,”", "\"호기심을 부리는 것 같아 실례입니다.\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."], ["“It seemed to me probable that you might take more than a common interest in this poor creature because Miss Summerson had taken that unfortunate interest in him.", "\"서머슨 양이 이 가엾은 아이에게 그토록 안타까운 관심을 쏟았으니, 선생도 이 아이한테 보통 이상의 관심을 가지셨을 법하다는 생각이 들어서요."], ["’Tis MY case, sir, I assure you.”", "저야 그렇거든요, 선생, 정말입니다.\""]], [["“And mine, Mr. George.”", "\"저도 그렇습니다, 조지 씨.\""]], [["The trooper looks sideways at Allan’s sunburnt cheek and bright dark eye, rapidly measures his height and build, and seems to approve of him.", "기병은 햇볕에 그을린 앨런의 뺨과 빛나는 검은 눈을 곁눈질하고, 그의 키와 체격을 재빨리 가늠하더니, 그가 마음에 드는 모양이다."]], [["“Since you have been out, sir, I have been thinking that I unquestionably know the rooms in Lincoln’s Inn Fields, where Bucket took the lad, according to his account.", "\"선생이 나가 계신 동안, 제가 곰곰 생각해보니, 버킷이 그 아이를 데려갔다는 링컨스 인 필즈의 그 방을, 아이 말대로라면, 틀림없이 제가 알고 있습니다."], ["Though he is not acquainted with the name, I can help you to it.", "그 아이는 이름을 모르지만, 제가 알려드릴 수 있어요."], ["It’s Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼입니다."], ["That’s what it is.”", "바로 그자예요.\""]], [["Allan looks at him inquiringly, repeating the name.", "앨런이 미심쩍은 얼굴로 그를 보며 그 이름을 되뇐다."]], [["“Tulkinghorn.", "\"토킹혼."], ["That’s the name, sir.", "그 이름입니다, 선생."], ["I know the man, and know him to have been in communication with Bucket before, respecting a deceased person who had given him offence.", "제가 그자를 압니다. 게다가 그자가 자기한테 밉보인 어느 고인 일로 전에 버킷과 연락을 주고받은 사이라는 것도 압니다."], ["I know the man, sir.", "제가 그자를 알지요, 선생."], ["To my sorrow.”", "안타깝게도요.\""]], [["Allan naturally asks what kind of man he is.", "앨런은 자연히 그가 어떤 사람이냐고 묻는다."]], [["“What kind of man!", "\"어떤 사람이냐고요!"], ["Do you mean to look at?”", "생김새 말입니까?\""]], [["“I think I know that much of him.", "\"그건 얼추 아는 듯합니다."], ["I mean to deal with.", "상대하기에 어떤 사람인가 하는 거지요."], ["Generally, what kind of man?”", "통틀어서, 어떤 사람입니까?\""]], [["“Why, then I’ll tell you, sir,”", "\"그러시다면, 말씀드리지요, 선생.\""], ["returns the trooper, stopping short and folding his arms on his square chest so angrily that his face fires and flushes all over; “he is a confoundedly bad kind of man.", "기병이 우뚝 멈춰 서서 떡 벌어진 가슴 위에 팔짱을 끼는데, 어찌나 성이 났는지 얼굴이 온통 벌겋게 달아오른다. \"지긋지긋하게 못된 부류의 인간입니다."], ["He is a slow-torturing kind of man.", "천천히 사람을 고문하는 부류지요."], ["He is no more like flesh and blood than a rusty old carbine is.", "녹슨 낡은 기병총만큼이나 피도 눈물도 없는 인간입니다."], ["He is a kind of man—by George!—that has caused me more restlessness, and more uneasiness, and more dissatisfaction with myself than all other men put together.", "맹세코, 그자는—다른 모든 인간을 합친 것보다도 저를 더 안절부절못하게, 더 불안하게, 그리고 제 자신에게 더 불만스럽게 만든 부류의 인간입니다."], ["That’s the kind of man Mr. Tulkinghorn is!”", "토킹혼 씨가 바로 그런 인간이에요!\""]], [["“I am sorry,”", "\"죄송합니다.\""], ["says Allan, “to have touched so sore a place.”", "앨런이 말한다. \"이렇게 아픈 데를 건드려서요.\""]], [["“Sore?”", "\"아픈 데라고요?\""], ["The trooper plants his legs wider apart, wets the palm of his broad right hand, and lays it on the imaginary moustache.", "기병이 다리를 더 넓게 벌리고 떡 벌어진 오른손바닥에 침을 적셔, 있지도 않은 콧수염에 갖다 댄다."], ["“It’s no fault of yours, sir; but you shall judge.", "\"선생 잘못은 아니지요. 하지만 직접 판단해보십시오."], ["He has got a power over me.", "그자는 저를 휘어잡을 힘을 쥐고 있어요."], ["He is the man I spoke of just now as being able to tumble me out of this place neck and crop.", "아까 제가 이곳에서 저를 멱살 잡아 내쫓을 수 있다고 한 그자가 바로 토킹혼입니다."], ["He keeps me on a constant see-saw.", "그자는 저를 끊임없이 시소 위에 올려놓고 흔들어대요."], ["He won’t hold off, and he won’t come on.", "손을 떼지도 않고, 본격적으로 나서지도 않습니다."], ["If I have a payment to make him, or time to ask him for, or anything to go to him about, he don’t see me, don’t hear me—passes me on to Melchisedech’s in Clifford’s Inn, Melchisedech’s in Clifford’s Inn passes me back again to him—he keeps me prowling and dangling about him as if I was made of the same stone as himself.", "그자에게 갚을 돈이 있거나, 기한을 부탁해야 하거나, 무슨 일로든 찾아가야 하면, 그자는 저를 보지도, 제 말을 듣지도 않은 채—클리퍼드 인의 멜키세덱한테로 떠넘기고, 클리퍼드 인의 멜키세덱은 다시 저를 그자한테로 떠넘기지요. 마치 제가 자기랑 똑같은 돌덩이로 만들어진 양, 저를 자기 주위에서 어슬렁거리고 매달리게 만듭니다."], ["Why, I spend half my life now, pretty well, loitering and dodging about his door.", "글쎄, 저는 이제 거의 인생의 절반을 그자의 문간을 서성이고 피해 다니는 데 허비하고 있어요."], ["What does he care?", "그자가 신경이나 쓰겠습니까?"], ["Nothing.", "천만에요."], ["Just as much as the rusty old carbine I have compared him to.", "제가 그자를 빗댄 녹슨 낡은 기병총만큼도 신경 안 씁니다."], ["He chafes and goads me till—Bah!", "그자가 저를 어찌나 안달 나게 들들 볶는지—쳇!"], ["Nonsense!", "헛소리!"], ["I am forgetting myself.", "제가 정신을 놓고 있었군요. 우드코트 씨.\""], ["Mr. Woodcourt,”", "기병이 다시 걸음을 옮긴다."], ["the trooper resumes his march, “all I say is, he is an old man; but I am glad I shall never have the chance of setting spurs to my horse and riding at him in a fair field.", "\"제 말은 그저, 그자가 노인이라는 겁니다. 하지만 탁 트인 벌판에서 말에 박차를 가해 그자에게 돌격할 기회가 영영 없으리란 게 다행이에요."], ["For if I had that chance, in one of the humours he drives me into—he’d go down, sir!”", "만약 그자가 저를 몰아넣는 그런 격분에 휩싸여 그런 기회를 잡았더라면—그자는 끝장이었을 겁니다, 선생!\""]], [["Mr. George has been so excited that he finds it necessary to wipe his forehead on his shirt-sleeve.", "조지 씨는 어찌나 흥분했는지 셔츠 소매로 이마를 닦아야 할 지경이다."], ["Even while he whistles his impetuosity away with the national anthem, some involuntary shakings of his head and heavings of his chest still linger behind, not to mention an occasional hasty adjustment with both hands of his open shirt-collar, as if it were scarcely open enough to prevent his being troubled by a choking sensation.", "국가(國歌)를 휘파람으로 불며 격정을 날려 보내는 와중에도, 자기도 모르게 고개를 몇 번 흔들고 가슴을 들썩이는 잔여 동작이 여전히 남아 있고, 더구나 이따금 두 손으로 풀어 헤친 셔츠 깃을 황급히 매만지는데, 마치 질식할 것 같은 답답함을 막기에는 깃이 충분히 풀려 있지 않은 듯하다."], ["In short, Allan Woodcourt has not much doubt about the going down of Mr. Tulkinghorn on the field referred to.", "한마디로, 앨런 우드코트는 방금 말한 그 벌판에서 토킹혼 씨가 끝장날 것임을 별로 의심하지 않는다."]], [["Jo and his conductor presently return, and Jo is assisted to his mattress by the careful Phil, to whom, after due administration of medicine by his own hands, Allan confides all needful means and instructions.", "조와 그를 데려간 사람이 곧 돌아오고, 조는 꼼꼼한 필의 부축을 받아 매트리스로 옮겨진다. 앨런은 자기 손으로 약을 제대로 먹인 뒤, 필에게 필요한 모든 수단과 지침을 일러준다."], ["The morning is by this time getting on apace.", "어느덧 아침이 빠르게 깊어간다."], ["He repairs to his lodgings to dress and breakfast, and then, without seeking rest, goes away to Mr. Jarndyce to communicate his discovery.", "그는 옷을 갈아입고 아침을 먹으러 자기 거처로 돌아갔다가, 쉴 생각도 않고 자신의 발견을 알리러 잔다이스 씨에게 떠난다."]], [["With him Mr. Jarndyce returns alone, confidentially telling him that there are reasons for keeping this matter very quiet indeed and showing a serious interest in it.", "잔다이스 씨가 그와 함께 단둘이 돌아오는데, 이 일을 정말이지 아주 조용히 묻어두어야 할 사정이 있다고 은밀히 일러주며, 이에 진지한 관심을 보인다."], ["To Mr. Jarndyce, Jo repeats in substance what he said in the morning, without any material variation.", "잔다이스 씨에게 조는 아침에 한 이야기를 실질적으로 똑같이, 별다른 차이 없이 되풀이한다."], ["Only that cart of his is heavier to draw, and draws with a hollower sound.", "다만 그 수레가 끌기에 더 무거워졌고, 더 텅 빈 소리를 내며 끌릴 뿐이다."]], [["“Let me lay here quiet and not be chivied no more,”", "\"여기 가만히 누워서, 이제 더는 쫓겨 다니지 않게 해주세요.\" 조가 가까스로 말한다."], ["falters Jo, “and be so kind any person as is a-passin nigh where I used fur to sleep, as jist to say to Mr. Sangsby that Jo, wot he known once, is a-moving on right forards with his duty, and I’ll be wery thankful.", "\"그리고 제가 늘 자던 데 근처를 지나가는 누구라도 친절을 베풀어서, 한때 알던 조가 제 할 일을 다하러 똑바로 비키며 가고 있다고 스내그스비 씨한테 그저 한마디만 전해주면, 정말 고맙겠어요."], ["I’d be more thankful than I am aready if it wos any ways possible for an unfortnet to be it.”", "불쌍한 놈한테 그게 어떻게든 가능하다면, 지금보다 더 고맙겠어요.\""]], [["He makes so many of these references to the law-stationer in the course of a day or two that Allan, after conferring with Mr. Jarndyce, good-naturedly resolves to call in Cook’s Court, the rather, as the cart seems to be breaking down.", "조가 하루 이틀 사이에 이렇게 그 법률 문구상을 하도 여러 번 들먹이는 통에, 앨런은 잔다이스 씨와 의논한 끝에, 수레가 무너져 내리는 듯하기도 해서, 마음씨 좋게 쿡스 코트에 들르기로 마음먹는다."]], [["To Cook’s Court, therefore, he repairs. Mr.", "그리하여 그는 쿡스 코트로 향한다."], ["Snagsby is behind his counter in his grey coat and sleeves, inspecting an indenture of several skins which has just come in from the engrosser’s, an immense desert of law-hand and parchment, with here and there a resting-place of a few large letters to break the awful monotony and save the traveller from despair.", "스내그스비 씨는 회색 외투에 토시를 끼고 계산대 뒤에 서서, 정서공한테서 막 들어온 여러 장짜리 증서를 검사하고 있다. 끔찍한 단조로움을 깨고 길손을 절망에서 구해줄 큼지막한 글자 몇 개가 군데군데 쉼터처럼 박혀 있을 뿐, 법률 글씨와 양피지가 광막한 사막처럼 펼쳐져 있다."], ["Mr Snagsby puts up at one of these inky wells and greets the stranger with his cough of general preparation for business.", "스내그스비 씨가 그 잉크 우물 가운데 한 군데서 멈춰 서서, 으레 업무 채비로 짓는 헛기침을 하며 낯선 이를 맞는다."]], [["“You don’t remember me, Mr. Snagsby?”", "\"저를 기억 못 하시겠지요, 스내그스비 씨?\""]], [["The stationer’s heart begins to thump heavily, for his old apprehensions have never abated.", "문구상의 심장이 무겁게 쿵쿵거리기 시작한다. 그 해묵은 불안이 한 번도 가라앉은 적이 없기 때문이다."], ["It is as much as he can do to answer, “No, sir, I can’t say I do.", "그가 겨우 할 수 있는 대답은 이뿐이다. \"아니요, 선생, 기억난다고는 못 하겠는데요."], ["I should have considered—not to put too fine a point upon it—that I never saw you before, sir.”", "제 생각엔—너무 까다롭게 따지자는 건 아닙니다만—전에 한 번도 뵌 적이 없는 것 같은데요, 선생.\""]], [["“Twice before,”", "\"두 번 뵈었지요.\""], ["says Allan Woodcourt.", "앨런 우드코트가 말한다."], ["“Once at a poor bedside, and once—”", "\"한 번은 어느 가엾은 자의 병상 곁에서, 또 한 번은—\""]], [["“It’s come at last!”", "'드디어 올 게 왔구나!'"], ["thinks the afflicted stationer, as recollection breaks upon him.", "시달리는 문구상이 기억이 번뜩 떠오르며 생각한다."], ["“It’s got to a head now and is going to burst!”", "'이제 곪을 대로 곪아서 터지려는구나!'"], ["But he has sufficient presence of mind to conduct his visitor into the little counting-house and to shut the door.", "그래도 그는 방문객을 자그마한 회계실로 안내해 문을 닫을 만큼의 정신은 가까스로 붙들고 있다."]], [["“Are you a married man, sir?”", "\"결혼하셨습니까, 선생?\""]], [["“No, I am not.”", "\"아니요, 안 했습니다.\""]], [["“Would you make the attempt, though single,” says Mr.", "\"미혼이시긴 해도, 한번 애써주시겠습니까.\""], ["Snagsby in a melancholy whisper, “to speak as low as you can?", "스내그스비 씨가 우울한 속삭임으로 말한다. \"되도록 낮은 목소리로 말씀해주시면요."], ["For my little woman is a-listening somewheres, or I’ll forfeit the business and five hundred pound!”", "제 마누라가 어딘가에서 엿듣고 있거든요. 안 그러면 제 가게랑 오백 파운드를 다 내놓아도 좋습니다!\""]], [["In deep dejection Mr. Snagsby sits down on his stool, with his back against his desk, protesting, “I never had a secret of my own, sir.", "깊은 낙심에 빠진 스내그스비 씨는 책상에 등을 기대고 의자에 앉아 항변한다."], ["I can’t charge my memory with ever having once attempted to deceive my little woman on my own account since she named the day.", "\"저는 제 비밀이라곤 한 번도 가져본 적이 없습니다, 선생. 마누라가 혼인 날짜를 잡은 이래로, 제 일로 마누라를 속이려 든 적이 단 한 번이라도 있었다고는 도무지 기억이 안 납니다."], ["I wouldn’t have done it, sir.", "그러지 않았을 거예요, 선생."], ["Not to put too fine a point upon it, I couldn’t have done it, I dursn’t have done it.", "너무 까다롭게 따지자는 건 아닙니다만, 그럴 수도 없었고, 감히 그럴 엄두도 못 냈을 겁니다."], ["Whereas, and nevertheless, I find myself wrapped round with secrecy and mystery, till my life is a burden to me.”", "그런데도, 그럼에도 불구하고, 제 인생이 짐이 될 만큼 저는 비밀과 수수께끼에 칭칭 휘감겨 있는 처지가 되어버렸지요.\""]], [["His visitor professes his regret to hear it and asks him does he remember Jo. Mr.", "방문객은 그 말을 들으니 안타깝다고 하고는, 조를 기억하느냐고 묻는다."], ["Snagsby answers with a suppressed groan, oh, don’t he!", "스내그스비 씨가 억눌린 신음으로 답한다. 아, 어찌 잊겠습니까!"]], [["“You couldn’t name an individual human being—except myself—that my little woman is more set and determined against than Jo,”", "\"제 마누라가 조만큼—저 자신을 빼고요—이를 갈고 단단히 벼르는 인간은 따로 없을 겁니다, 선생.\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["Allan asks why.", "앨런이 까닭을 묻는다."]], [["“Why?” repeats Mr.", "\"까닭이요?\""], ["Snagsby, in his desperation clutching at the clump of hair at the back of his bald head.", "스내그스비 씨가, 절망에 빠진 채 벗어진 뒤통수의 머리카락 한 줌을 움켜쥐며 되뇐다."], ["“How should I know why?", "\"제가 까닭을 어찌 알겠습니까?"], ["But you are a single person, sir, and may you long be spared to ask a married person such a question!”", "하지만 선생은 미혼이시니, 결혼한 사람한테 그런 질문을 던질 수 있는 그 처지가 부디 오래오래 이어지길 빕니다!\""]], [["With this beneficent wish, Mr. Snagsby coughs a cough of dismal resignation and submits himself to hear what the visitor has to communicate.", "이 자비로운 축원과 함께, 스내그스비 씨는 음울한 체념의 헛기침을 한 번 하고는, 방문객이 전할 말을 들으려 몸을 맡긴다."]], [["“There again!” says Mr.", "\"또 그거예요!\""], ["Snagsby, who, between the earnestness of his feelings and the suppressed tones of his voice is discoloured in the face.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다. 그는 감정의 절절함과 억누른 목소리 사이에서 얼굴이 잿빛이 되었다."], ["“At it again, in a new direction!", "\"또 그거라니까요, 이번엔 새로운 방향에서!"], ["A certain person charges me, in the solemnest way, not to talk of Jo to any one, even my little woman.", "어떤 사람이 더없이 엄숙하게, 조 얘기를 누구한테도, 심지어 제 마누라한테도 하지 말라고 못을 박습니다."], ["Then comes another certain person, in the person of yourself, and charges me, in an equally solemn way, not to mention Jo to that other certain person above all other persons.", "그러고는 또 다른 어떤 사람이, 바로 선생 모습으로 나타나, 똑같이 엄숙하게, 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 그 또 다른 어떤 사람한테는 절대로 조 얘기를 꺼내지 말라고 못을 박는군요."], ["Why, this is a private asylum!", "글쎄, 이건 사설 정신병원이에요!"], ["Why, not to put too fine a point upon it, this is Bedlam, sir!”", "너무 까다롭게 따지자는 건 아닙니다만, 이건 정신병원이라고요, 선생!\""], ["says Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 말한다."]], [["But it is better than he expected after all, being no explosion of the mine below him or deepening of the pit into which he has fallen.", "그래도 막상 일은 그가 예상한 것보다 낫다. 발밑의 지뢰가 터지는 것도, 자기가 빠진 구덩이가 더 깊어지는 것도 아니기 때문이다."], ["And being tender-hearted and affected by the account he hears of Jo’s condition, he readily engages to “look round”", "게다가 마음이 여린 그는 조의 처지에 대해 들은 이야기에 마음이 움직여, 되도록 조용히 짬을 낼 수 있는 대로 저녁에 일찍 \"들러보겠다\"고 선뜻 약속한다."], ["as early in the evening as he can manage it quietly.", "저녁이 오자 그는 아주 조용히 들른다."], ["He looks round very quietly when the evening comes, but it may turn out that Mrs. Snagsby is as quiet a manager as he.", "다만 스내그스비 부인도 그 못지않게 조용히 일을 꾸미는 사람일지 모르는 노릇이다."]], [["Jo is very glad to see his old friend and says, when they are left alone, that he takes it uncommon kind as Mr.", "조는 옛 친구를 보고 무척 반가워한다."], ["Sangsby should come so far out of his way on accounts of sich as him. Mr.", "그러고는 둘만 남게 되자, 자기 같은 놈 때문에 스내그스비 씨가 이렇게 멀리까지 일부러 걸음을 해주시다니 더없이 고맙다고 말한다."], ["Snagsby, touched by the spectacle before him, immediately lays upon the table half a crown, that magic balsam of his for all kinds of wounds.", "눈앞의 광경에 마음이 짠해진 스내그스비 씨는 즉시 식탁 위에 반 크라운을 내려놓는다. 온갖 상처에 듣는 그의 마법의 약이다."]], [["“And how do you find yourself, my poor lad?”", "\"그래, 몸은 좀 어떠냐, 가엾은 녀석아?\""], ["inquires the stationer with his cough of sympathy.", "문구상이 측은해하는 헛기침을 하며 묻는다."]], [["“I am in luck, Mr. Sangsby, I am,”", "\"전 운이 좋아요, 스내그스비 씨, 정말요.\""], ["returns Jo, “and don’t want for nothink.", "조가 대답한다. \"아쉬운 게 하나도 없어요."], ["I’m more cumfbler nor you can’t think.", "생각도 못 하실 만큼 편안해요."], ["Mr. Sangsby!", "스내그스비 씨!"], ["I’m wery sorry that I done it, but I didn’t go fur to do it, sir.”", "제가 그 일을 저질러서 정말 죄송해요. 하지만 일부러 그러려던 건 아니었어요, 나리.\""]], [["The stationer softly lays down another half-crown and asks him what it is that he is sorry for having done.", "문구상이 가만히 반 크라운을 하나 더 내려놓고는, 저질러서 미안하다는 게 대체 무슨 일이냐고 묻는다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"스내그스비 씨.\""], ["Sangsby,”", "조가 말한다. \"제가 그 부인한테 병을 옮겼어요."], ["says Jo, “I went and giv a illness to the lady as wos and yit as warn’t the t’other lady, and none of ’em never says nothink to me for having done it, on accounts of their being ser good and my having been s’unfortnet.", "그 다른 부인인 듯하면서도 그 다른 부인은 아니었던 그 부인한테요. 근데 아무도 제가 그 짓을 했다고 저한테 뭐라 하질 않아요."], ["The lady come herself and see me yesday, and she ses, ‘Ah, Jo!’ she ses.", "다들 어찌나 좋은 분들인지, 또 제가 어찌나 불쌍한 놈인지 그래서요. 그 부인이 어제 직접 찾아와서 절 봤어요."], ["‘We thought we’d lost you, Jo!’ she ses.", "그러고는 '아, 조!' 하고, '우린 널 잃은 줄 알았단다, 조!'"], ["And she sits down a-smilin so quiet, and don’t pass a word nor yit a look upon me for having done it, she don’t, and I turns agin the wall, I doos, Mr.", "하더라고요. 그러고 어찌나 조용히 미소 지으며 앉던지, 제가 그 짓을 했다고 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번 안 주더라니까요, 정말이에요."], ["Sangsby. And Mr.", "그래서 전 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠어요, 정말로요, 스내그스비 씨."], ["Jarnders, I see him a-forced to turn away his own self. And Mr.", "그리고 잔다이스 씨도, 자기도 모르게 고개를 돌리는 걸 봤어요."], ["Woodcot, he come fur to giv me somethink fur to ease me, wot he’s allus a-doin’ on day and night, and wen he come a-bending over me and a-speakin up so bold, I see his tears a-fallin, Mr. Sangsby.”", "우드코트 씨는 절 편하게 해주려고 뭘 주러 왔는데, 그분은 늘 밤낮으로 그러시거든요, 근데 제 위로 몸을 숙이고 그렇게 씩씩하게 말을 거는데, 눈물이 떨어지는 걸 봤어요, 스내그스비 씨.\""]], [["The softened stationer deposits another half-crown on the table.", "마음이 누그러진 문구상이 식탁에 반 크라운을 하나 더 내려놓는다."], ["Nothing less than a repetition of that infallible remedy will relieve his feelings.", "그 어김없는 명약을 거듭 처방하는 것 말고는 그의 감정을 달랠 길이 없다."]], [["“Wot I was a-thinkin on, Mr. Sangsby,”", "\"제가 생각한 건요, 스내그스비 씨.\""], ["proceeds Jo, “wos, as you wos able to write wery large, p’raps?”", "조가 이어 말한다. \"혹시 글씨를 아주 크게 쓰실 수 있나, 하는 거였어요?\""]], [["“Yes, Jo, please God,”", "\"그럼, 조, 하느님이 도우신다면야.\""], ["returns the stationer.", "문구상이 대답한다."]], [["“Uncommon precious large, p’raps?”", "\"엄청나게, 굉장히 크게, 어쩌면요?\""], ["says Jo with eagerness.", "조가 간절하게 묻는다."]], [["“Yes, my poor boy.”", "\"그럼, 가엾은 녀석아.\""]], [["Jo laughs with pleasure. “Wot I wos a-thinking on then, Mr.", "조가 기뻐서 웃는다."], ["Sangsby, wos, that when I wos moved on as fur as ever I could go and couldn’t be moved no furder, whether you might be so good p’raps as to write out, wery large so that any one could see it anywheres, as that I wos wery truly hearty sorry that I done it and that I never went fur to do it, and that though I didn’t know nothink at all, I knowd as Mr.", "\"그러니까 제가 생각한 건요, 스내그스비 씨, 제가 갈 수 있는 데까지 다 비키며 가서 더는 비킬 수 없게 됐을 때, 혹시 친절을 베풀어서, 누구든 어디서든 볼 수 있게 아주 크게 써주실 수 있나, 하는 거였어요. 제가 그 짓을 한 걸 정말 진심으로 미안해하고, 일부러 그러려던 게 아니었다고요."], ["Woodcot once cried over it and wos allus grieved over it, and that I hoped as he’d be able to forgive me in his mind.", "그리고 제가 아무것도 모르긴 해도, 우드코트 씨가 한번은 그 일로 울었고 늘 그 일로 가슴 아파했다는 건 알았다고요. 그리고 그분이 마음속으로 절 용서해주실 수 있길 바란다고요."], ["If the writin could be made to say it wery large, he might.”", "글씨로 그걸 아주 크게 써주실 수 있으면, 그분이 용서해주실지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“It shall say it, Jo.", "\"써주마, 조."], ["Very large.”", "아주 크게.\""]], [["Jo laughs again.", "조가 다시 웃는다."], ["“Thankee, Mr. Sangsby.", "\"고맙습니다, 스내그스비 씨."], ["It’s wery kind of you, sir, and it makes me more cumfbler nor I was afore.”", "참 친절하세요, 나리. 덕분에 전보다 더 편안해졌어요.\""]], [["The meek little stationer, with a broken and unfinished cough, slips down his fourth half-crown—he has never been so close to a case requiring so many—and is fain to depart.", "온순한 자그마한 문구상은 끊기다 만 어설픈 헛기침과 함께, 네 번째 반 크라운을 슬쩍 내려놓는다—이토록 여러 개를 필요로 하는 경우와 마주한 적은 그로서도 처음이다—그러고는 떠나는 수밖에 없다."], ["And Jo and he, upon this little earth, shall meet no more.", "그리고 조와 그는, 이 자그마한 지상에서, 두 번 다시 만나지 못하리라."], ["No more.", "두 번 다시."]], [["For the cart so hard to draw is near its journey’s end and drags over stony ground.", "끌기 그토록 힘든 그 수레가 여정의 끝에 다다라 돌투성이 길 위로 끌려가고 있기 때문이다."], ["All round the clock it labours up the broken steps, shattered and worn.", "하루 종일, 수레는 부서지고 닳은 채 망가진 계단을 힘겹게 기어오른다."], ["Not many times can the sun rise and behold it still upon its weary road.", "해가 떠올라 그 수레가 여전히 그 고단한 길 위에 있음을 보게 될 날도 이제 몇 번 남지 않았다."]], [["Phil Squod, with his smoky gunpowder visage, at once acts as nurse and works as armourer at his little table in a corner, often looking round and saying with a nod of his green-baize cap and an encouraging elevation of his one eyebrow, “Hold up, my boy!", "화약 연기에 그을린 얼굴의 필 스쿼드가, 한쪽 구석 자그마한 작업대에서 총포 수리공으로 일하는 동시에 간병인 노릇을 한다. 이따금 돌아보며 초록빛 방모 모자를 한 번 까딱이고 하나뿐인 눈썹을 격려하듯 치켜올리며 이렇게 말한다."], ["Hold up!” There, too, is Mr.", "\"기운 내, 얘야!"], ["Jarndyce many a time, and Allan Woodcourt almost always, both thinking, much, how strangely fate has entangled this rough outcast in the web of very different lives.", "기운 내!\" 거기엔 잔다이스 씨도 여러 번 와 있고, 앨런 우드코트는 거의 늘 와 있는데, 둘 다 운명이 어쩌면 이토록 기이하게 이 거친 버림받은 아이를 전혀 다른 삶들의 그물 속에 얽어 넣었는지 깊이 곱씹는다."], ["There, too, the trooper is a frequent visitor, filling the doorway with his athletic figure and, from his superfluity of life and strength, seeming to shed down temporary vigour upon Jo, who never fails to speak more robustly in answer to his cheerful words.", "거기엔 기병도 자주 들르는데, 떡 벌어진 몸으로 문간을 가득 채우고서, 넘쳐나는 생명과 힘으로 조에게 잠시나마 활기를 흘려보내는 듯하다. 그러면 조는 그 쾌활한 말에 어김없이 더 기운찬 목소리로 대답한다."]], [["Jo is in a sleep or in a stupor to-day, and Allan Woodcourt, newly arrived, stands by him, looking down upon his wasted form.", "오늘 조는 잠들었는지 아니면 혼수에 빠졌는지 모르겠고, 막 도착한 앨런 우드코트가 그 곁에 서서 야윈 몸을 내려다본다."], ["After a while he softly seats himself upon the bedside with his face towards him—just as he sat in the law-writer’s room—and touches his chest and heart.", "잠시 후 그는 가만히 침대 가에 앉아 조 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌린다—그 법률 필경사의 방에 앉았던 그 모습 그대로—그러고는 그의 가슴과 심장을 만져본다."], ["The cart had very nearly given up, but labours on a little more.", "수레는 거의 멈출 뻔하다가, 조금 더 힘겹게 나아간다."]], [["The trooper stands in the doorway, still and silent.", "기병이 문간에 가만히 말없이 서 있다."], ["Phil has stopped in a low clinking noise, with his little hammer in his hand.", "필은 자그마한 망치를 손에 든 채, 낮게 딸각거리던 소리를 멈췄다."], ["Mr. Woodcourt looks round with that grave professional interest and attention on his face, and glancing significantly at the trooper, signs to Phil to carry his table out.", "우드코트 씨가 진지하고 직업적인 관심과 주의가 어린 얼굴로 둘러보더니, 기병에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던지고, 필에게 작업대를 들고 나가라고 손짓한다."], ["When the little hammer is next used, there will be a speck of rust upon it.", "그 자그마한 망치를 다음에 쓰게 될 때면, 그 위에 녹이 한 점 슬어 있으리라."]], [["“Well, Jo!", "\"자, 조!"], ["What is the matter?", "왜 그러느냐?"], ["Don’t be frightened.”", "무서워 마라.\""]], [["“I thought,”", "\"제가요.\""], ["says Jo, who has started and is looking round, “I thought I was in Tom-all-Alone’s agin.", "조가 화들짝 놀라 두리번거리며 말한다. \"톰 혼자 사는 곳에 다시 와 있는 줄 알았어요."], ["Ain’t there nobody here but you, Mr.", "여긴 나리 말고는 아무도 없는 거지요, 우드코트 씨?\""], ["Woodcot?”", "\"아무도 없다.\""]], [["“Nobody.” “And I ain’t took back to Tom-all-Alone’s.", "\"그리고 전 톰 혼자 사는 곳으로 다시 끌려가는 거 아니지요."], ["Am I, sir?”", "그렇지요, 나리?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"그래.\""], ["Jo closes his eyes, muttering, “I’m wery thankful.”", "조가 눈을 감으며 중얼거린다. \"정말 고맙습니다.\""]], [["After watching him closely a little while, Allan puts his mouth very near his ear and says to him in a low, distinct voice, “Jo!", "잠시 그를 가까이서 지켜본 뒤, 앨런이 그의 귀에 입을 바싹 대고, 낮고 또렷한 목소리로 말한다. \"조!"], ["Did you ever know a prayer?”", "너 기도 하나라도 알았던 적이 있느냐?\""]], [["“Never knowd nothink, sir.”", "\"아무것도 몰랐어요, 나리.\""]], [["“Not so much as one short prayer?”", "\"짤막한 기도 하나조차도?\""]], [["“No, sir.", "\"네, 나리."], ["Nothink at all. Mr.", "아무것도요."], ["Chadbands he wos a-prayin wunst at Mr. Sangsby’s and I heerd him, but he sounded as if he wos a-speakin’ to hisself, and not to me.", "한번은 채드밴드 씨가 스내그스비 씨네서 기도를 하기에 들었는데, 마치 저한테가 아니라 자기 혼자한테 말하는 것 같았어요."], ["He prayed a lot, but I couldn’t make out nothink on it.", "기도를 한참 했는데, 무슨 소린지 통 알 수가 없었어요."], ["Different times there was other genlmen come down Tom-all-Alone’s a-prayin, but they all mostly sed as the t’other ’wuns prayed wrong, and all mostly sounded to be a-talking to theirselves, or a-passing blame on the t’others, and not a-talkin to us.", "다른 때엔 다른 양반들이 톰 혼자 사는 곳에 기도하러 내려왔는데, 다들 거의 다른 양반들이 기도를 틀리게 한다고 하고, 다들 거의 자기들끼리 떠들거나 다른 양반들 탓을 하는 것 같았지, 우리한테 말하는 것 같진 않았어요."], ["WE never knowd nothink.", "우린 아무것도 몰랐어요."], ["I never knowd what it wos all about.”", "전 그게 다 무슨 소린지 도통 몰랐어요.\""]], [["It takes him a long time to say this, and few but an experienced and attentive listener could hear, or, hearing, understand him.", "이 말을 하는 데 그는 한참이 걸리고, 노련하고 주의 깊은 청자가 아니고서는 그 말을 들을 수도, 듣는다 해도 알아들을 수도 없으리라."], ["After a short relapse into sleep or stupor, he makes, of a sudden, a strong effort to get out of bed.", "잠시 다시 잠인지 혼수인지에 빠졌다가, 그는 느닷없이 침대에서 빠져나오려고 안간힘을 쓴다."]], [["“Stay, Jo!", "\"가만있어라, 조!"], ["What now?”", "이번엔 또 뭐냐?\""]], [["“It’s time for me to go to that there berryin ground, sir,”", "\"저 묘지로 갈 때가 됐어요, 나리.\""], ["he returns with a wild look.", "그가 사나운 눈빛으로 대답한다."]], [["“Lie down, and tell me.", "\"누워라, 그리고 말해보거라."], ["What burying ground, Jo?”", "무슨 묘지 말이냐, 조?\""]], [["“Where they laid him as wos wery good to me, wery good to me indeed, he wos.", "\"저한테 잘해준 사람을 묻은 데요, 저한테 정말 잘해줬어요, 그 사람이요."], ["It’s time fur me to go down to that there berryin ground, sir, and ask to be put along with him.", "저 묘지로 내려가서, 그 사람 곁에 묻어달라고 부탁할 때가 됐어요, 나리."], ["I wants to go there and be berried.", "거기 가서 묻히고 싶어요."], ["He used fur to say to me, ‘I am as poor as you to-day, Jo,’ he ses.", "그 사람은 저한테 늘 이러곤 했어요. '난 오늘 너만큼이나 가난하단다, 조.'"], ["I wants to tell him that I am as poor as him now and have come there to be laid along with him.”", "이제 저도 그 사람만큼 가난하고, 그 사람 곁에 묻히려고 거기 왔다고 말해주고 싶어요.\""]], [["“By and by, Jo.", "\"머잖아, 조."], ["By and by.”", "머잖아 그러자.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["P’raps they wouldn’t do it if I wos to go myself.", "제가 직접 가면 그렇게 안 해줄지도 몰라요."], ["But will you promise to have me took there, sir, and laid along with him?”", "그래도 저를 거기 데려가서 그 사람 곁에 묻어주겠다고 약속해주실래요, 나리?\""]], [["“I will, indeed.”", "\"그러마, 꼭.\""]], [["“Thankee, sir. Thankee, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리."], ["They’ll have to get the key of the gate afore they can take me in, for it’s allus locked.", "고맙습니다, 나리. 거긴 늘 잠겨 있어서, 절 들이려면 먼저 문 열쇠를 가져와야 할 거예요."], ["And there’s a step there, as I used for to clean with my broom.", "그리고 거기 계단이 하나 있는데, 제가 빗자루로 늘 쓸던 거예요."], ["It’s turned wery dark, sir.", "무척 어두워졌어요, 나리."], ["Is there any light a-comin?”", "무슨 빛이 오고 있나요?\""]], [["“It is coming fast, Jo.”", "\"빠르게 오고 있다, 조.\""]], [["Fast.", "빠르게."], ["The cart is shaken all to pieces, and the rugged road is very near its end.", "수레는 산산이 부서져 흔들리고, 험한 길은 이제 그 끝에 무척 가까워졌다."]], [["“Jo, my poor fellow!”", "\"조, 가엾은 녀석아!\""]], [["“I hear you, sir, in the dark, but I’m a-gropin—a-gropin—let me catch hold of your hand.”", "\"어둠 속에서 나리 목소리가 들려요, 근데 전 더듬거리고 있어요—더듬거려요—나리 손을 잡게 해주세요.\""]], [["“Jo, can you say what I say?”", "\"조, 내가 하는 말을 따라 할 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“I’ll say anythink as you say, sir, for I knows it’s good.”", "\"나리가 하시는 말은 뭐든 따라 할게요, 좋은 거라는 걸 아니까요.\" \"하늘에 계신 우리 아버지.\""]], [["“Our Father.” “Our Father!", "\"하늘에 계신 우리 아버지!"], ["Yes, that’s wery good, sir.”", "네, 그거 참 좋아요, 나리.\""]], [["“Which art in heaven.”", "\"하늘에 계신.\""]], [["“Art in heaven—is the light a-comin, sir?”", "\"하늘에 계시—오며—빛이 오고 있나요, 나리?\""]], [["“It is close at hand.", "\"바로 가까이 와 있다."], ["Hallowed be thy name!”", "이름이 거룩히 여김을 받으시오며!\""]], [["“Hallowed be—thy—”", "\"이름이 거룩히—여김을—\""]], [["The light is come upon the dark benighted way.", "빛이 그 어둡고 캄캄한 길 위에 다다랐다."], ["Dead!", "죽었다!"]], [["Dead, your Majesty.", "죽었습니다, 폐하."], ["Dead, my lords and gentlemen.", "죽었습니다, 경들과 신사 여러분."], ["Dead, right reverends and wrong reverends of every order.", "죽었습니다, 온갖 직위의 옳은 성직자와 그른 성직자 여러분."], ["Dead, men and women, born with heavenly compassion in your hearts.", "죽었습니다, 가슴에 천상의 연민을 품고 태어난 남녀 여러분."], ["And dying thus around us every day.", "그리고 날마다 우리 주변에서 이렇게 죽어갑니다."]], [["Closing In\nThe place in Lincolnshire has shut its many eyes again, and the house in town is awake.", "링컨셔의 저택은 다시 그 숱한 눈을 감았고, 도시의 저택은 깨어났다."], ["In Lincolnshire the Dedlocks of the past doze in their picture-frames, and the low wind murmurs through the long drawing-room as if they were breathing pretty regularly.", "링컨셔에서는 지난날의 데드록 가문 사람들이 액자 속에서 졸고, 나직한 바람이 긴 응접실을 따라 웅얼거리니 마치 그들이 제법 규칙적으로 숨 쉬는 듯하다."], ["In town the Dedlocks of the present rattle in their fire-eyed carriages through the darkness of the night, and the Dedlock Mercuries, with ashes (or hair-powder) on their heads, symptomatic of their great humility, loll away the drowsy mornings in the little windows of the hall.", "도시에서는 오늘날의 데드록 가문 사람들이 불 같은 눈을 단 마차를 타고 한밤의 어둠을 덜컹덜컹 가르며 달리고, 머리에 잿가루(아니면 분가루)를 뒤집어쓰고 한없는 겸손을 드러내는 데드록 가문의 헤르메스들은 현관의 작은 창가에서 졸린 아침나절을 늘어지게 흘려보낸다."], ["The fashionable world—tremendous orb, nearly five miles round—is in full swing, and the solar system works respectfully at its appointed distances.", "유행을 좇는 세계는—둘레가 거의 다섯 마일에 이르는 거대한 천체는—한창 돌아가고, 태양계는 정해진 거리에서 공손하게 운행한다."]], [["Where the throng is thickest, where the lights are brightest, where all the senses are ministered to with the greatest delicacy and refinement, Lady Dedlock is.", "* 헤르메스: 그리스 신화의 전령 신. 여기서는 화려한 제복을 입은 데드록 가문의 하인들을 풍자적으로 가리킨다."], ["From the shining heights she has scaled and taken, she is never absent.", "무리가 가장 빽빽한 곳, 불빛이 가장 환한 곳, 모든 감각이 더없이 섬세하고 세련되게 시중받는 곳, 그곳에 데드록 마님이 있다."], ["Though the belief she of old reposed in herself as one able to reserve whatsoever she would under her mantle of pride is beaten down, though she has no assurance that what she is to those around her she will remain another day, it is not in her nature when envious eyes are looking on to yield or to droop.", "마님은 자기가 기어올라 차지한 그 빛나는 정점에서 한 번도 자리를 비우지 않는다. 무엇이든 제 자존심의 망토 아래 감출 수 있는 사람이라며 오래도록 자신에게 품어온 믿음은 무너졌고, 주위 사람들에게 비치는 지금 모습이 하루만 더 지나도 그대로일지 장담할 수 없게 되었지만, 시샘하는 눈길이 지켜볼 때 굽히거나 처지는 것은 그녀의 천성에 없다."], ["They say of her that she has lately grown more handsome and more haughty.", "사람들은 마님이 요즘 들어 더 아름다워지고 더 오만해졌다고 한다."], ["The debilitated cousin says of her that she’s beauty nough—tsetup shopofwomen—but rather larming kind—remindingmanfact—inconvenient woman—who WILL getoutofbedandbawthstahlishment—Shakespeare.", "그 비실거리는 사촌은 마님을 두고 이렇게 말한다. 미모치곤—여자가게차릴—만하지만—좀—섬뜩한—데가있어—사람생각나게하는—거북한여자야—침대에서일어나—집안을발칵—뒤집어놓을—셰익스피어말이야."]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn says nothing, looks nothing.", "토킹혼 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 아무 표정도 짓지 않는다."], ["Now, as heretofore, he is to be found in doorways of rooms, with his limp white cravat loosely twisted into its old-fashioned tie, receiving patronage from the peerage and making no sign.", "지금도 예전처럼 그는 방문간에 서서, 흐물거리는 흰 크라바트를 구식 매듭으로 느슨하게 비틀어 맨 채 귀족들의 후원을 받으면서도 아무런 내색을 하지 않는다."], ["Of all men he is still the last who might be supposed to have any influence upon my Lady.", "모든 남자 가운데 마님에게 무슨 영향력이라도 가졌으리라 짐작할 수 있는 마지막 사람이 그다."], ["Of all women she is still the last who might be supposed to have any dread of him.", "모든 여자 가운데 그를 조금이라도 두려워하리라 짐작할 수 있는 마지막 사람이 마님이다."]], [["One thing has been much on her mind since their late interview in his turret-room at Chesney Wold.", "체스니 월드의 첨탑 방에서 둘이 나눈 지난번 면담 이후로 마님의 마음을 줄곧 짓누르는 일이 하나 있다."], ["She is now decided, and prepared to throw it off.", "마님은 이제 결심했고, 그 일을 떨쳐낼 채비를 마쳤다."]], [["It is morning in the great world, afternoon according to the little sun.", "큰 세계에서는 아침이지만, 작은 태양으로 치자면 오후다."], ["The Mercuries, exhausted by looking out of window, are reposing in the hall and hang their heavy heads, the gorgeous creatures, like overblown sunflowers.", "헤르메스들은 창밖을 내다보느라 지친 나머지 현관에서 쉬며 그 화려한 몸뚱이의 무거운 머리를 떨군 모습이, 활짝 피었다 시든 해바라기 같다."], ["Like them, too, they seem to run to a deal of seed in their tags and trimmings.", "또 그들처럼, 술과 장식 갓끈에 씨앗이 잔뜩 맺힌 것처럼도 보인다."], ["Sir Leicester, in the library, has fallen asleep for the good of the country over the report of a Parliamentary committee.", "서재에서는 레스터 경이 나라를 위한답시고 어느 의회 위원회 보고서를 읽다가 잠들었다."], ["My Lady sits in the room in which she gave audience to the young man of the name of Guppy.", "마님은 거피라는 이름의 그 젊은이를 접견했던 바로 그 방에 앉아 있다."], ["Rosa is with her and has been writing for her and reading to her.", "로자가 곁에 있어 마님 대신 글을 적어주고 책을 읽어주었다."], ["Rosa is now at work upon embroidery or some such pretty thing, and as she bends her head over it, my Lady watches her in silence.", "로자는 지금 자수인지 그 비슷한 고운 일감에 매달려 있고, 그녀가 고개를 숙여 그 위로 손을 놀리는 사이 마님은 말없이 지켜본다."], ["Not for the first time to-day.", "오늘 처음 있는 일이 아니다."]], [["“Rosa.”", "\"로자.\""]], [["The pretty village face looks brightly up.", "그 어여쁜 시골 처녀의 얼굴이 환하게 위를 올려다본다."], ["Then, seeing how serious my Lady is, looks puzzled and surprised.", "그러다가 마님이 얼마나 심각한지 알아채고는 어리둥절하고 놀란 표정이 된다."]], [["“See to the door.", "\"문을 살펴보아라."], ["Is it shut?”", "닫혀 있느냐?\""]], [["Yes.", "네."], ["She goes to it and returns, and looks yet more surprised.", "로자가 문가로 갔다가 돌아오는데, 더욱 놀란 기색이다."]], [["“I am about to place confidence in you, child, for I know I may trust your attachment, if not your judgment.", "\"내 너에게 속을 털어놓으려 한다, 얘야. 네 판단력은 몰라도 네 충심만은 믿을 수 있음을 알기 때문이다."], ["In what I am going to do, I will not disguise myself to you at least.", "내가 이제 하려는 일에서, 적어도 너에게만은 나를 감추지 않겠다."], ["But I confide in you.", "하지만 너를 믿고 하는 말이다."], ["Say nothing to any one of what passes between us.”", "우리 둘 사이에 오가는 이야기는 누구에게도 발설하지 마라.\""]], [["The timid little beauty promises in all earnestness to be trustworthy.", "겁 많은 작은 미인은 더없이 진지하게 비밀을 지키겠노라 약속한다."]], [["“Do you know,”", "\"너는 아느냐,\""], ["Lady Dedlock asks her, signing to her to bring her chair nearer, “do you know, Rosa, that I am different to you from what I am to any one?”", "데드록 마님이 의자를 더 가까이 가져오라고 손짓하며 묻는다. \"로자, 너는 내가 다른 누구를 대할 때와 너를 대할 때가 다르다는 걸 아느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, my Lady.", "\"네, 마님."], ["Much kinder.", "훨씬 다정하시지요."], ["But then I often think I know you as you really are.”", "하지만 저는 마님의 참모습을 안다고 자주 생각하곤 해요.\""]], [["“You often think you know me as I really am?", "\"내 참모습을 안다고 자주 생각한다?"], ["Poor child, poor child!”", "가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!\""]], [["She says it with a kind of scorn—though not of Rosa—and sits brooding, looking dreamily at her.", "마님은 일종의 경멸을 담아 이 말을 내뱉지만—로자를 향한 것은 아니다—그러고는 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 꿈결처럼 그녀를 바라보며 앉아 있다."]], [["“Do you think, Rosa, you are any relief or comfort to me?", "\"로자, 너는 네가 나에게 위안이나 위로가 된다고 생각하느냐?"], ["Do you suppose your being young and natural, and fond of me and grateful to me, makes it any pleasure to me to have you near me?”", "네가 젊고 꾸밈없으며 나를 따르고 고마워하는 것이, 너를 곁에 두는 일을 나에게 조금이라도 즐거운 일로 만든다고 여기느냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know, my Lady; I can scarcely hope so.", "\"모르겠어요, 마님. 감히 그러기를 바랄 수도 없고요."], ["But with all my heart, I wish it was so.”", "하지만 진심으로, 그랬으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“It is so, little one.”", "\"그렇단다, 얘야.\""]], [["The pretty face is checked in its flush of pleasure by the dark expression on the handsome face before it.", "어여쁜 얼굴이 기쁨에 발그레 물들려다가, 눈앞의 그 아름다운 얼굴에 어린 어두운 표정에 가로막힌다."], ["It looks timidly for an explanation.", "그 얼굴이 겁먹은 듯 설명을 구한다."]], [["“And if I were to say to-day, ‘Go!", "\"그런데 내가 오늘 너에게 '가거라!"], ["Leave me!’ I should say what would give me great pain and disquiet, child, and what would leave me very solitary.”", "나를 떠나라!' 한다면, 그것은 나에게 큰 아픔과 불안을 안기는 말일 테고, 나를 무척 외롭게 만드는 말일 테다, 얘야.\""]], [["“My Lady!", "\"마님!"], ["Have I offended you?”", "제가 마님께 잘못을 저질렀나요?\""]], [["“In nothing.", "\"전혀 아니란다."], ["Come here.”", "이리 오너라.\""]], [["Rosa bends down on the footstool at my Lady’s feet.", "로자가 마님 발치의 발받침 위로 몸을 굽힌다."], ["My Lady, with that motherly touch of the famous ironmaster night, lays her hand upon her dark hair and gently keeps it there.", "마님은 그날 밤 그 이름난 철물 제조업자가 보여준 어머니 같은 손길로 로자의 검은 머리에 손을 얹고는 가만히 그대로 둔다."]], [["“I told you, Rosa, that I wished you to be happy and that I would make you so if I could make anybody happy on this earth. I cannot.", "\"로자, 내 너에게 네가 행복하기를 바라며, 이 세상에서 누군가 하나라도 행복하게 해줄 수 있다면 너를 그렇게 해주겠다고 했지."], ["There are reasons now known to me, reasons in which you have no part, rendering it far better for you that you should not remain here.", "그런데 그럴 수가 없구나. 지금 내가 아는 어떤 사정들이 있는데, 네가 끼어들 일은 전혀 없는 그 사정들 때문에 네가 여기 머물지 않는 편이 너에게 훨씬 낫다."], ["You must not remain here.", "너는 여기 머물러서는 안 된다."], ["I have determined that you shall not.", "그러지 않기로 내가 정했다."], ["I have written to the father of your lover, and he will be here to-day.", "네 연인의 아버지에게 편지를 보냈고, 그분이 오늘 이리로 올 게다."], ["All this I have done for your sake.”", "이 모든 걸 너를 위해 했다.\""]], [["The weeping girl covers her hand with kisses and says what shall she do, what shall she do, when they are separated!", "우는 처녀가 마님의 손에 입맞춤을 퍼부으며, 헤어지면 자기는 어찌해야 하나, 어찌해야 하나 한다!"], ["Her mistress kisses her on the cheek and makes no other answer.", "안주인은 그녀의 뺨에 입을 맞출 뿐 다른 대답은 하지 않는다."]], [["“Now, be happy, child, under better circumstances.", "\"자, 행복해지거라, 얘야. 더 나은 처지에서."], ["Be beloved and happy!”", "사랑받고 행복하거라!\""]], [["“Ah, my Lady, I have sometimes thought—forgive my being so free—that YOU are not happy.” “I!”", "\"아, 마님, 저는 가끔 이런 생각을 했어요—이렇게 함부로 말하는 걸 용서해 주세요—마님께서 행복하지 않으신 게 아닐까 하고요.\""]], [["“Will you be more so when you have sent me away?", "\"내가!\" \"저를 보내고 나면 더 행복해지실까요?"], ["Pray, pray, think again.", "부디, 부디 다시 생각해 주세요."], ["Let me stay a little while!”", "조금만 더 머물게 해주세요!\""]], [["“I have said, my child, that what I do, I do for your sake, not my own.", "\"내 말했지, 얘야. 내가 하는 일은 나 자신이 아니라 너를 위해 하는 것이라고."], ["It is done.", "이미 정해진 일이다."], ["What I am towards you, Rosa, is what I am now—not what I shall be a little while hence.", "로자, 너를 향한 내 마음은 지금 이대로의 나란다—조금만 지나면 어찌 될지 모를 그런 나가 아니라."], ["Remember this, and keep my confidence.", "이것을 기억하고, 내 비밀을 지켜다오."], ["Do so much for my sake, and thus all ends between us!”", "그만큼만 나를 위해 해주면, 우리 둘 사이는 이로써 다 끝나는 것이다!\""]], [["She detaches herself from her simple-hearted companion and leaves the room.", "마님은 천진한 동무에게서 몸을 떼고 방을 나선다."], ["Late in the afternoon, when she next appears upon the staircase, she is in her haughtiest and coldest state.", "오후도 저물 무렵, 마님이 다시 계단에 모습을 드러낼 때 그녀는 더없이 오만하고 차가운 상태다."], ["As indifferent as if all passion, feeling, and interest had been worn out in the earlier ages of the world and had perished from its surface with its other departed monsters.", "마치 온갖 정념과 감정과 관심이 세상의 더 이른 시대에 다 닳아 없어져 다른 사라진 괴물들과 함께 지표면에서 자취를 감추기라도 한 듯, 무심하기 그지없다."]], [["Mercury has announced Mr. Rouncewell, which is the cause of her appearance.", "헤르메스가 라운스웰 씨의 도착을 알렸으니, 그것이 마님이 모습을 드러낸 까닭이다."], ["Mr. Rouncewell is not in the library, but she repairs to the library.", "라운스웰 씨가 서재에 있는 것은 아니지만, 마님은 서재로 향한다."], ["Sir Leicester is there, and she wishes to speak to him first.", "거기에는 레스터 경이 있고, 마님은 그에게 먼저 이야기하고 싶다."]], [["“Sir Leicester, I am desirous—but you are engaged.”", "\"레스터 경, 제가 청이 있는데—한데 손님이 계시네요.\""]], [["Oh, dear no!", "오, 천만에!"], ["Not at all.", "전혀 아니오."], ["Only Mr. Tulkinghorn.", "토킹혼 씨뿐이라오."]], [["Always at hand.", "언제나 가까이 있다."], ["Haunting every place.", "어느 자리에나 망령처럼 따라붙는다."], ["No relief or security from him for a moment.", "한순간도 그에게서 벗어나거나 안심할 수 없다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Lady Dedlock.", "\"실례합니다, 데드록 마님."], ["Will you allow me to retire?”", "제가 물러가도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["With a look that plainly says, “You know you have the power to remain if you will,”", "\"마음만 먹으면 남아 있을 힘이 당신에게 있다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소\""], ["she tells him it is not necessary and moves towards a chair. Mr.", "하고 또렷이 말하는 듯한 눈길로, 마님은 그럴 필요 없다고 이르고는 의자 쪽으로 걸음을 옮긴다."], ["Tulkinghorn brings it a little forward for her with his clumsy bow and retires into a window opposite.", "토킹혼 씨가 어설픈 인사와 함께 의자를 그녀 앞으로 조금 끌어다 놓고는 맞은편 창가로 물러난다."], ["Interposed between her and the fading light of day in the now quiet street, his shadow falls upon her, and he darkens all before her.", "이제 고요해진 거리에 사위어가는 한낮의 빛과 그녀 사이를 가로막고 선 그의 그림자가 그녀 위로 드리워, 그가 그녀 앞의 모든 것을 어둡게 한다."], ["Even so does he darken her life.", "꼭 그렇게 그는 그녀의 삶을 어둡게 한다."]], [["It is a dull street under the best conditions, where the two long rows of houses stare at each other with that severity that half-a-dozen of its greatest mansions seem to have been slowly stared into stone rather than originally built in that material.", "아무리 좋은 조건에서도 음울한 거리다. 길게 늘어선 두 줄의 집들이 어찌나 매섭게 서로를 노려보는지, 그중 가장 큰 저택 대여섯 채는 본래 돌로 지어졌다기보다 노려보임을 당하다가 서서히 돌로 굳어버린 듯하다."], ["It is a street of such dismal grandeur, so determined not to condescend to liveliness, that the doors and windows hold a gloomy state of their own in black paint and dust, and the echoing mews behind have a dry and massive appearance, as if they were reserved to stable the stone chargers of noble statues.", "어찌나 음산하게 웅장한 거리인지, 활기 따위에 굽힐 생각이 도무지 없어서 문과 창은 검은 칠과 먼지로 제 나름의 침울한 위세를 지키고, 뒤편의 메아리치는 마구간 골목은 메마르고 묵직한 모양새가 마치 고귀한 동상들의 돌 군마를 매어둘 자리로 남겨둔 듯하다."], ["Complicated garnish of iron-work entwines itself over the flights of steps in this awful street, and from these petrified bowers, extinguishers for obsolete flambeaux gasp at the upstart gas.", "이 끔찍한 거리에서는 정교하게 장식한 철세공이 층계마다 휘감겨 있고, 이 돌로 굳은 정자에서 한물간 횃불용 소화기들이 벼락출세한 가스등을 보고 입을 떡 벌린다."], ["Here and there a weak little iron hoop, through which bold boys aspire to throw their friends’ caps (its only present use), retains its place among the rusty foliage, sacred to the memory of departed oil.", "군데군데 약하고 작은 쇠고리가 녹슨 잎사귀 장식 사이에 자리를 지키며 사라진 기름불의 기억을 모시고 있는데, 대담한 사내아이들이 친구 모자를 던져 넣으려 애쓰는 것이 그것의 유일한 지금 쓰임새다."], ["Nay, even oil itself, yet lingering at long intervals in a little absurd glass pot, with a knob in the bottom like an oyster, blinks and sulks at newer lights every night, like its high and dry master in the House of Lords.", "아니, 기름불 자체조차 긴 간격을 두고 군데군데, 바닥에 굴 껍데기 같은 혹이 달린 우스꽝스러운 작은 유리 단지에 머뭇거리며 남아, 밤마다 더 새로운 불빛들을 향해 눈을 깜빡이며 부루퉁한 꼴이, 마치 상원에 들어앉은 한물가고 메마른 제 주인을 닮았다."]], [["Therefore there is not much that Lady Dedlock, seated in her chair, could wish to see through the window in which Mr. Tulkinghorn stands.", "그러니 의자에 앉은 데드록 마님이 토킹혼 씨가 서 있는 그 창 너머로 보고 싶어 할 만한 것은 별로 없다."], ["And yet—and yet—she sends a look in that direction as if it were her heart’s desire to have that figure moved out of the way.", "그런데도—그런데도—마님은 저 인물이 길에서 비켜나기를 마음 깊이 바라기라도 하듯 그쪽으로 눈길을 보낸다."]], [["Sir Leicester begs his Lady’s pardon.", "레스터 경이 마님에게 양해를 구한다."], ["She was about to say?", "무슨 말을 하려던 참이었소?"]], [["“Only that Mr. Rouncewell is here (he has called by my appointment) and that we had better make an end of the question of that girl.", "\"다름이 아니라 라운스웰 씨가 와 있어요(제가 약속해서 찾아온 거예요)."], ["I am tired to death of the matter.”", "그 처녀 문제는 이쯤에서 마무리 짓는 게 좋겠어요. 그 일이라면 진절머리가 나도록 지쳤거든요.\""]], [["“What can I do—to—assist?”", "\"내가 뭘 하면—그—도움이 되겠소?\""], ["demands Sir Leicester in some considerable doubt.", "레스터 경이 적잖이 미심쩍어하며 묻는다."]], [["“Let us see him here and have done with it.", "\"그를 이리로 들여서 이 일을 끝내버려요."], ["Will you tell them to send him up?”", "그를 올려 보내라고 일러주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Mr. Tulkinghorn, be so good as to ring.", "\"토킹혼 씨, 미안하지만 종을 좀 울려주시오."], ["Thank you.", "고맙소."], ["Request,”", "그—\""], ["says Sir Leicester to Mercury, not immediately remembering the business term, “request the iron gentleman to walk this way.”", "레스터 경이 헤르메스에게 일렀으나 사무적인 호칭이 얼른 떠오르지 않아, \"그 철물 양반더러 이리로 오시라 청하게.\""]], [["Mercury departs in search of the iron gentleman, finds, and produces him.", "헤르메스가 철물 양반을 찾으러 떠나, 그를 찾아내 데려온다."], ["Sir Leicester receives that ferruginous person graciously.", "레스터 경이 그 무쇠 같은 인물을 정중히 맞이한다."]], [["“I hope you are well, Mr. Rouncewell.", "\"강녕하시기 바랍니다, 라운스웰 씨."], ["Be seated. (My solicitor, Mr.", "앉으시지요."], ["Tulkinghorn.) My Lady was desirous, Mr. Rouncewell,”", "(내 법률 고문, 토킹혼 씨요.) 마님께서, 라운스웰 씨,\""], ["Sir Leicester skilfully transfers him with a solemn wave of his hand, “was desirous to speak with you.", "레스터 경이 한 손을 엄숙하게 내저으며 능숙하게 그를 마님에게 넘긴다. \"당신과 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하셨소."], ["Hem!”", "험!\""]], [["“I shall be very happy,”", "\"데드록 마님께서 황송하게도 말씀하시는 것이라면 무엇이든 정성을 다해 귀 기울이겠습니다.\""], ["returns the iron gentleman, “to give my best attention to anything Lady Dedlock does me the honour to say.”", "철물 양반이 대답한다."]], [["As he turns towards her, he finds that the impression she makes upon him is less agreeable than on the former occasion.", "마님 쪽으로 돌아서며 그는, 마님이 자기에게 주는 인상이 지난번보다 덜 유쾌함을 느낀다."], ["A distant supercilious air makes a cold atmosphere about her, and there is nothing in her bearing, as there was before, to encourage openness.", "거리감 있고 거만한 분위기가 그녀 둘레에 싸늘한 기운을 빚어내고, 지난번과 달리 그녀의 거동에는 솔직함을 북돋울 만한 구석이 전혀 없다."]], [["“Pray, sir,”", "\"여쭙겠는데요, 선생,\""], ["says Lady Dedlock listlessly, “may I be allowed to inquire whether anything has passed between you and your son respecting your son’s fancy?”", "데드록 마님이 맥없이 말한다. \"선생과 아드님 사이에 아드님의 그 연정에 관해 무슨 이야기라도 오갔는지 물어봐도 될까요?\""]], [["It is almost too troublesome to her languid eyes to bestow a look upon him as she asks this question.", "이 질문을 던지며 그에게 눈길 한 번 주는 것조차 그녀의 나른한 눈에는 너무 성가신 일이다."]], [["“If my memory serves me, Lady Dedlock, I said, when I had the pleasure of seeing you before, that I should seriously advise my son to conquer that—fancy.”", "\"제 기억이 옳다면, 데드록 마님, 지난번 마님을 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때 제가 말씀드렸지요. 아들에게 그—연정을 이겨내라고 진지하게 충고하겠노라고요.\""], ["The ironmaster repeats her expression with a little emphasis.", "철물 제조업자가 그녀의 표현을 살짝 힘주어 되풀이한다."]], [["“And did you?”", "\"그래서 그러셨나요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Of course I did.”", "물론 그랬지요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester gives a nod, approving and confirmatory. Very proper.", "레스터 경이 흡족해하며 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["The iron gentleman, having said that he would do it, was bound to do it.", "아주 마땅한 일이다. 철물 양반은 그러겠다고 말했으니 그럴 의무가 있다."], ["No difference in this respect between the base metals and the precious.", "이 점에서는 천한 금속과 귀한 금속 사이에 차이가 없다."], ["Highly proper.", "지극히 마땅한 일이다."]], [["“And pray has he done so?”", "\"그래서 아드님이 그리하던가요?\""]], [["“Really, Lady Dedlock, I cannot make you a definite reply. I fear not.", "\"솔직히, 데드록 마님, 확실한 대답을 드릴 수가 없습니다."], ["Probably not yet.", "안 그런 듯합니다."], ["In our condition of life, we sometimes couple an intention with our—our fancies which renders them not altogether easy to throw off.", "아마 아직은 아니지요. 저희 같은 처지의 사람들은 가끔 우리—우리 연정에 어떤 결심을 결부하는데, 그러면 좀처럼 떨쳐내기가 쉽지 않습니다."], ["I think it is rather our way to be in earnest.”", "진지하게 구는 것이 저희 방식인가 봅니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester has a misgiving that there may be a hidden Wat Tylerish meaning in this expression, and fumes a little.", "레스터 경은 이 표현에 왓 타일러 같은 불순한 속뜻이 숨어 있을지 모른다는 의구심에 약간 부아가 끓는다. 라운스웰 씨는 더없이 쾌활하고 정중하지만, 그런 한도 안에서 분명히 자기를 대하는 태도에 맞춰 어조를 조절한다."], ["Mr.", "* 왓 타일러: 1381년 잉글랜드 농민 봉기를 이끈 인물."], ["Rouncewell is perfectly good-humoured and polite, but within such limits, evidently adapts his tone to his reception.", "레스터 경은 라운스웰의 말에서 신분 질서에 도전하는 평민적 기운을 의심한다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"그게 말이죠,\""], ["proceeds my Lady, “I have been thinking of the subject, which is tiresome to me.”", "마님이 말을 잇는다. \"제가 이 문제를 줄곧 생각해 왔는데, 저로선 지긋지긋한 일이거든요.\""]], [["“I am very sorry, I am sure.”", "\"진심으로 송구스럽습니다.\""]], [["“And also of what Sir Leicester said upon it, in which I quite concur”—Sir Leicester flattered—“and if you cannot give us the assurance that this fancy is at an end, I have come to the conclusion that the girl had better leave me.”", "\"또 레스터 경이 이 일에 대해 하신 말씀도 생각했고, 저도 전적으로 같은 뜻이에요\"—레스터 경이 우쭐해한다—\"그래서 만일 선생이 이 연정이 끝났다고 장담해 주실 수 없다면, 저는 그 처녀가 제 곁을 떠나는 편이 낫다는 결론에 이르렀어요.\""]], [["“I can give no such assurance, Lady Dedlock.", "\"그런 장담은 드릴 수가 없습니다, 데드록 마님."], ["Nothing of the kind.”", "그 비슷한 것조차요.\""]], [["“Then she had better go.”", "\"그럼 그 애가 떠나는 게 낫겠군요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, my Lady,”", "\"실례합니다, 마님,\""], ["Sir Leicester considerately interposes, “but perhaps this may be doing an injury to the young woman which she has not merited.", "레스터 경이 사려 깊게 끼어든다. \"하지만 어쩌면 이것이 그 젊은 여인에게 마땅찮은 해를 입히는 일이 될지도 모르오."], ["Here is a young woman,”", "여기 한 젊은 여인이 있소,\""], ["says Sir Leicester, magnificently laying out the matter with his right hand like a service of plate, “whose good fortune it is to have attracted the notice and favour of an eminent lady and to live, under the protection of that eminent lady, surrounded by the various advantages which such a position confers, and which are unquestionably very great—I believe unquestionably very great, sir—for a young woman in that station of life.", "레스터 경이 한 벌의 은식기라도 펼쳐 보이듯 오른손으로 그 사정을 장엄하게 펼친다. \"어느 고귀한 부인의 눈에 들어 그 총애를 받는 행운을 누리고, 그 고귀한 부인의 보호 아래 그런 지위가 베푸는 갖가지 혜택에 둘러싸여 사는 여인 말이오."], ["The question then arises, should that young woman be deprived of these many advantages and that good fortune simply because she has”—Sir Leicester, with an apologetic but dignified inclination of his head towards the ironmaster, winds up his sentence—“has attracted the notice of Mr Rouncewell’s son?", "그 혜택이란 의심할 바 없이 대단한 것이오—내 믿건대 의심할 바 없이 대단한 것이오, 선생—그런 신분의 젊은 여인에게는 말이오. 그러면 이런 의문이 생기오."], ["Now, has she deserved this punishment?", "그저 그 여인이 라운스웰 씨 아드님의—\" 레스터 경이 철물 제조업자를 향해 미안하다는 듯하면서도 위엄 있게 고개를 기울이며 문장을 마무리한다."], ["Is this just towards her?", "\"—눈에 들었다는 이유만으로 이 숱한 혜택과 행운을 빼앗아야 하겠소? 한데, 그 여인이 이런 벌을 받아 마땅하오?"], ["Is this our previous understanding?”", "이것이 그 여인에게 공정하오? 이것이 우리가 앞서 합의한 바이오?\""]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"송구합니다만,\""], ["interposes Mr. Rouncewell’s son’s father.", "라운스웰 씨 아드님의 아버지가 끼어든다."], ["“Sir Leicester, will you allow me?", "\"레스터 경, 한 말씀 드려도 되겠습니까?"], ["I think I may shorten the subject.", "제가 이 이야기를 간추려도 될 듯합니다."], ["Pray dismiss that from your consideration.", "부디 그건 고려에서 빼주십시오."], ["If you remember anything so unimportant—which is not to be expected—you would recollect that my first thought in the affair was directly opposed to her remaining here.”", "그토록 사소한 것을 기억하신다면—기억하시리라 기대할 수도 없지만—이 일에 대한 저의 첫 생각이 그 애가 여기 머무는 것에 곧장 반대하는 쪽이었음을 떠올리실 겁니다.\""]], [["Dismiss the Dedlock patronage from consideration?", "데드록 가문의 후원을 고려에서 뺀다?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["Sir Leicester is bound to believe a pair of ears that have been handed down to him through such a family, or he really might have mistrusted their report of the iron gentleman’s observations.", "레스터 경은 그런 가문을 통해 자기에게 물려 내려온 두 귀를 믿지 않을 수 없으니, 그렇지 않았다면 철물 양반이 한 말을 그 귀가 전한 대로 정말이지 의심했을지도 모른다."]], [["“It is not necessary,”", "\"이런 문제들은 어느 쪽에서도 굳이 파고들 필요가 없어요.\""], ["observes my Lady in her coldest manner before he can do anything but breathe amazedly, “to enter into these matters on either side.", "그가 놀라 숨이나 겨우 내쉴 뿐 아무것도 못 하는 사이, 마님이 더없이 차가운 태도로 말한다. \"그 애는 아주 착한 애예요."], ["The girl is a very good girl; I have nothing whatever to say against her, but she is so far insensible to her many advantages and her good fortune that she is in love—or supposes she is, poor little fool—and unable to appreciate them.”", "저로선 그 애를 두고 나무랄 게 조금도 없어요. 다만 그 애가 자기에게 주어진 숱한 혜택과 행운을 도무지 깨닫지 못한 채 사랑에 빠져 있다는—아니, 빠졌다고 여기는 거겠지요, 가엾고 어리석은 것—그래서 그것들을 고마워할 줄 모른다는 게 문제일 뿐이에요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester begs to observe that wholly alters the case.", "레스터 경은 그렇다면 사정이 완전히 달라진다고 한마디 보탠다."], ["He might have been sure that my Lady had the best grounds and reasons in support of her view.", "마님이 자기 견해를 뒷받침할 더없이 훌륭한 근거와 이유를 갖추셨으리라 짐작했어야 옳았다."], ["He entirely agrees with my Lady.", "그는 마님과 전적으로 뜻을 같이한다."], ["The young woman had better go.", "그 젊은 여인은 떠나는 편이 낫겠다."]], [["“As Sir Leicester observed, Mr. Rouncewell, on the last occasion when we were fatigued by this business,”", "\"지난번 우리가 이 일로 지쳐 있을 때 레스터 경이 말씀하셨듯이, 라운스웰 씨,\""], ["Lady Dedlock languidly proceeds, “we cannot make conditions with you.", "데드록 마님이 나른하게 말을 잇는다. \"저희는 선생과 조건을 내걸고 흥정할 수가 없어요."], ["Without conditions, and under present circumstances, the girl is quite misplaced here and had better go.", "조건 없이, 지금 형편으로 보아 그 애는 여기 도무지 어울리지 않으니 떠나는 게 낫겠어요."], ["I have told her so.", "그 애에게도 그리 일러두었고요."], ["Would you wish to have her sent back to the village, or would you like to take her with you, or what would you prefer?”", "그 애를 마을로 돌려보내기를 원하시나요, 아니면 데려가고 싶으신가요, 어느 쪽이 좋으시겠어요?\""]], [["“Lady Dedlock, if I may speak plainly—”", "\"데드록 마님, 솔직히 말씀드려도 된다면—\""]], [["“By all means.”", "\"얼마든지요.\""]], [["“—I should prefer the course which will the soonest relieve you of the incumbrance and remove her from her present position.”", "\"—저는 마님께서 그 짐을 가장 빨리 더시고, 또 그 애를 지금 처지에서 가장 빨리 벗어나게 할 방도를 택하고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“And to speak as plainly,”", "\"그럼 저도 솔직히 말하자면,\""], ["she returns with the same studied carelessness, “so should I.", "마님이 똑같이 꾸며낸 무심함으로 받는다. \"저 역시 그래요."], ["Do I understand that you will take her with you?”", "그 애를 데려가시겠다는 말씀으로 알아들으면 되겠지요?\""]], [["The iron gentleman makes an iron bow.", "철물 양반이 무쇠 같은 인사를 한다."]], [["“Sir Leicester, will you ring?” Mr.", "\"레스터 경, 종을 울려주시겠어요?\""], ["Tulkinghorn steps forward from his window and pulls the bell.", "토킹혼 씨가 창가에서 앞으로 나서며 종을 당긴다."], ["“I had forgotten you.", "\"당신을 깜빡했군요."], ["Thank you.”", "고맙습니다.\""], ["He makes his usual bow and goes quietly back again.", "그가 늘 하던 인사를 하고는 조용히 다시 물러난다."], ["Mercury, swift-responsive, appears, receives instructions whom to produce, skims away, produces the aforesaid, and departs.", "헤르메스가 잽싸게 응답하여 나타나, 누구를 데려올지 지시받고는 쏜살같이 가서 앞서 말한 그 처녀를 데려온 뒤 떠난다."]], [["Rosa has been crying and is yet in distress.", "로자는 울고 있었고 지금도 마음이 괴롭다."], ["On her coming in, the ironmaster leaves his chair, takes her arm in his, and remains with her near the door ready to depart.", "그녀가 들어서자 철물 제조업자가 의자에서 일어나 그녀의 팔을 끼고는, 떠날 채비를 한 채 문 가까이에서 그녀와 함께 선다."]], [["“You are taken charge of, you see,”", "\"보다시피 네게는 돌봐줄 사람이 있고,\""], ["says my Lady in her weary manner, “and are going away well protected.", "마님이 지친 태도로 말한다. \"넌 든든히 보호받으며 떠나는 거란다."], ["I have mentioned that you are a very good girl, and you have nothing to cry for.”", "네가 아주 착한 애라고 말해 두었으니, 울 일은 하나도 없다.\""]], [["“She seems after all,” observes Mr.", "\"그런데도 이 아인 떠나는 게 슬퍼서 우는 것 같군요.\""], ["Tulkinghorn, loitering a little forward with his hands behind him, “as if she were crying at going away.”", "토킹혼 씨가 뒷짐을 진 채 조금 앞으로 어슬렁거리며 한마디 한다."]], [["“Why, she is not well-bred, you see,” returns Mr.", "\"뭐, 보시다시피 이 애가 가정교육을 제대로 못 받았고,\""], ["Rouncewell with some quickness in his manner, as if he were glad to have the lawyer to retort upon, “and she is an inexperienced little thing and knows no better.", "라운스웰 씨가 변호사를 받아칠 상대가 생겨 반가운 듯 다소 날쌘 태도로 응수한다. \"세상 물정 모르는 어린것이라 더 나은 처신을 모르는 거지요."], ["If she had remained here, sir, she would have improved, no doubt.”", "여기 남았더라면 분명 나아졌을 텐데요, 선생.\""]], [["“No doubt,” is Mr.", "\"분명 그랬겠지요.\""], ["Tulkinghorn’s composed reply.", "토킹혼 씨가 침착하게 받는다."]], [["Rosa sobs out that she is very sorry to leave my Lady, and that she was happy at Chesney Wold, and has been happy with my Lady, and that she thanks my Lady over and over again.", "로자가 흐느끼며, 마님 곁을 떠나는 게 너무도 슬프고, 체스니 월드에서 행복했으며 마님과 함께 있어 행복했다고, 마님께 거듭거듭 감사드린다고 한다."], ["“Out, you silly little puss!”", "\"이런, 어리석은 새끼 고양이 같으니!\""], ["says the ironmaster, checking her in a low voice, though not angrily.", "철물 제조업자가 낮은 소리로, 그러나 성내지 않고 그녀를 다독인다."], ["“Have a spirit, if you’re fond of Watt!”", "\"왓을 사랑한다면, 기개를 좀 가져야지!\""], ["My Lady merely waves her off with indifference, saying, “There, there, child!", "마님은 그저 무심하게 그녀를 물리며 말한다. \"됐다, 됐어, 얘야!"], ["You are a good girl.", "넌 착한 애다."], ["Go away!”", "가거라!\""], ["Sir Leicester has magnificently disengaged himself from the subject and retired into the sanctuary of his blue coat.", "레스터 경은 이 문제에서 장엄하게 손을 떼고 제 푸른 외투의 성소로 물러나 있다."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn, an indistinct form against the dark street now dotted with lamps, looms in my Lady’s view, bigger and blacker than before.", "이제 등불이 점점이 박힌 어두운 거리를 배경으로 흐릿한 윤곽이 된 토킹혼 씨가, 전보다 더 크고 더 검게 마님의 시야에 우뚝 솟는다."]], [["“Sir Leicester and Lady Dedlock,” says Mr.", "\"레스터 경, 데드록 마님,\""], ["Rouncewell after a pause of a few moments, “I beg to take my leave, with an apology for having again troubled you, though not of my own act, on this tiresome subject.", "라운스웰 씨가 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말한다. \"이제 작별을 고하고자 합니다."], ["I can very well understand, I assure you, how tiresome so small a matter must have become to Lady Dedlock.", "제 뜻으로 한 일은 아닙니다만, 이 성가신 문제로 또 한 번 폐를 끼쳐 송구합니다."], ["If I am doubtful of my dealing with it, it is only because I did not at first quietly exert my influence to take my young friend here away without troubling you at all.", "이토록 사소한 일이 데드록 마님께 얼마나 진절머리 나는 일이 되었을지, 정말이지 잘 헤아립니다. 제가 이 일을 다룬 방식에 미심쩍은 데가 있다면, 그건 다만 제가 처음부터 두 분께 폐를 끼치지 않고 조용히 제 영향력을 써서 여기 이 어린 벗을 데려가지 않았기 때문일 뿐입니다."], ["But it appeared to me—I dare say magnifying the importance of the thing—that it was respectful to explain to you how the matter stood and candid to consult your wishes and convenience.", "하지만 제게는—아마 일의 무게를 과장하는 거겠지요—두 분께 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 설명드리는 것이 예의이고, 두 분의 뜻과 편의를 여쭙는 것이 도리로 여겨졌습니다."], ["I hope you will excuse my want of acquaintance with the polite world.”", "사교계 예법에 어두운 점을 부디 너그러이 봐주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester considers himself evoked out of the sanctuary by these remarks.", "레스터 경은 이 말에 성소에서 불려 나왔다고 느낀다. \"라운스웰 씨,\""], ["“Mr. Rouncewell,”", "그가 답한다."], ["he returns, “do not mention it.", "\"그런 말씀 마시오."], ["Justifications are unnecessary, I hope, on either side.”", "변명은 어느 쪽에서도 필요 없으리라 보오.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear it, Sir Leicester; and if I may, by way of a last word, revert to what I said before of my mother’s long connexion with the family and the worth it bespeaks on both sides, I would point out this little instance here on my arm who shows herself so affectionate and faithful in parting and in whom my mother, I dare say, has done something to awaken such feelings—though of course Lady Dedlock, by her heartfelt interest and her genial condescension, has done much more.”", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 기쁩니다, 레스터 경. 끝으로 한 말씀만 더 드리자면, 앞서 제 어머니가 이 가문과 오래 인연을 맺어온 일과 그것이 양쪽 모두의 값어치를 말해 준다고 했던 그 이야기로 돌아가, 제 팔에 매달린 이 작은 본보기를 가리켜 보이고 싶습니다. 이 애는 헤어지는 마당에 이토록 정 깊고 한결같은 모습을 보이는데, 제 어머니가 이런 마음을 일깨우는 데 얼마간 거들었으리라 짐작합니다—물론 데드록 마님께서 진심 어린 관심과 자애로운 베풂으로 훨씬 더 많이 거드셨겠지만요.\""]], [["If he mean this ironically, it may be truer than he thinks.", "그가 이 말을 비꼬아 한 것이라면, 본인 생각보다 더 사실에 가까울지 모른다."], ["He points it, however, by no deviation from his straightforward manner of speech, though in saying it he turns towards that part of the dim room where my Lady sits. Sir Leicester stands to return his parting salutation, Mr.", "그러나 그는 거침없는 말투에서 조금도 벗어남 없이 이 말을 가리키니, 다만 이 말을 하면서 그 어둑한 방에서 마님이 앉아 있는 쪽으로 몸을 돌릴 뿐이다."], ["Tulkinghorn again rings, Mercury takes another flight, and Mr. Rouncewell and Rosa leave the house.", "레스터 경이 그의 작별 인사에 답하려 일어서고, 토킹혼 씨가 다시 종을 울리고, 헤르메스가 또 한 번 날아가고, 라운스웰 씨와 로자는 저택을 떠난다."]], [["Then lights are brought in, discovering Mr. Tulkinghorn still standing in his window with his hands behind him and my Lady still sitting with his figure before her, closing up her view of the night as well as of the day.", "그러자 등불이 들어오는데, 토킹혼 씨는 여전히 뒷짐을 진 채 창가에 서 있고 마님은 여전히 제 앞에 선 그의 윤곽이 밤도 낮도 가려버린 자리에 그대로 앉아 있다."], ["She is very pale. Mr.", "마님은 무척 창백하다."], ["Tulkinghorn, observing it as she rises to retire, thinks, “Well she may be!", "토킹혼 씨가 마님이 물러가려고 일어서는 것을 보며 이렇게 생각한다."], ["The power of this woman is astonishing.", "\"그럴 만도 하지! 이 여자의 힘은 놀랍군."], ["She has been acting a part the whole time.”", "줄곧 한 편의 연기를 펼쳐온 거야.\""], ["But he can act a part too—his one unchanging character—and as he holds the door open for this woman, fifty pairs of eyes, each fifty times sharper than Sir Leicester’s pair, should find no flaw in him.", "하지만 그 역시 연기를 할 줄 안다—한 번도 변치 않는 그 한 가지 배역을—그래서 그가 이 여자를 위해 문을 잡아주는 동안, 레스터 경의 눈보다 저마다 쉰 배는 더 매서운 쉰 쌍의 눈이 들여다본대도 그에게서 어떤 흠도 찾지 못할 것이다."]], [["Lady Dedlock dines alone in her own room to-day.", "데드록 마님은 오늘 자기 방에서 홀로 저녁을 든다."], ["Sir Leicester is whipped in to the rescue of the Doodle Party and the discomfiture of the Coodle Faction.", "레스터 경은 두들 당을 구하고 쿠들 파를 무너뜨리는 자리로 불려 나갔다. 마님은 여전히 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴로(비실거리는 사촌의 그 표현을 그대로 그려낸 채) 저녁상에 앉으며, 그가 외출했는지 묻는다."], ["Lady Dedlock asks on sitting down to dinner, still deadly pale (and quite an illustration of the debilitated cousin’s text), whether he is gone out?", "네. 토킹혼 씨는 아직 갔는지 묻는다."], ["Yes.", "아니요."], ["Whether Mr. Tulkinghorn is gone yet?", "잠시 뒤 다시 묻는다, 그가 아직도 안 갔느냐?"], ["No.", "아니요."], ["Presently she asks again, is he gone YET?", "무얼 하고 있느냐? 서재에서 편지를 쓰고 있는 것 같다고 헤르메스가 답한다."], ["No. What is he doing?", "마님께서 그를 만나기를 원하시는지?"], ["Mercury thinks he is writing letters in the library.", "그것만은 절대 아니다."], ["Would my Lady wish to see him? Anything but that.", "* 두들 당과 쿠들 파: 디킨스가 만들어낸 가상의 정파 이름으로, 거기서 거기인 영국 양대 정당을 풍자한다."]], [["But he wishes to see my Lady.", "그러나 그가 마님을 뵙고자 한다."], ["Within a few more minutes he is reported as sending his respects, and could my Lady please to receive him for a word or two after her dinner?", "몇 분 더 지나자, 그가 정중히 안부를 전하며 저녁 식사 뒤에 마님이 한두 마디 나누는 자리를 허락해 주실 수 있는지 묻는다는 전갈이 온다."], ["My Lady will receive him now.", "마님은 지금 그를 맞겠다고 한다."], ["He comes now, apologizing for intruding, even by her permission, while she is at table.", "그가 들어오며, 마님이 허락했다 해도 식사 중에 끼어들어 죄송하다고 사죄한다."], ["When they are alone, my Lady waves her hand to dispense with such mockeries.", "단둘이 되자 마님이 그런 입에 발린 인사 따위는 그만두라고 손을 내젓는다."]], [["“What do you want, sir?”", "\"무슨 일이시죠, 선생?\""]], [["“Why, Lady Dedlock,”", "\"그게, 데드록 마님,\""], ["says the lawyer, taking a chair at a little distance from her and slowly rubbing his rusty legs up and down, up and down, up and down, “I am rather surprised by the course you have taken.”", "변호사가 그녀에게서 조금 떨어진 자리에 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 녹슨 다리를 위아래로, 위아래로, 위아래로 천천히 문지른다. \"마님이 취하신 처사에 좀 놀랐습니다.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""]], [["“Yes, decidedly.", "\"네, 정말로요."], ["I was not prepared for it.", "미처 예상치 못한 일이었거든요."], ["I consider it a departure from our agreement and your promise.", "저는 그것이 우리 합의와 마님의 약속에서 벗어난 것이라고 봅니다."], ["It puts us in a new position, Lady Dedlock.", "그것은 우리를 새로운 처지에 놓는 일입니다, 데드록 마님."], ["I feel myself under the necessity of saying that I don’t approve of it.”", "저로서는 그것을 찬성할 수 없다고 말씀드리지 않을 수 없군요.\""]], [["He stops in his rubbing and looks at her, with his hands on his knees.", "그가 문지르던 손을 멈추고 무릎에 손을 얹은 채 그녀를 바라본다."], ["Imperturbable and unchangeable as he is, there is still an indefinable freedom in his manner which is new and which does not escape this woman’s observation.", "흔들림 없고 변함없는 사람이지만, 그의 태도에는 여전히 뭐라 꼬집을 수 없는 거리낌 없음이 새로이 배어 있고, 그것이 이 여자의 관찰을 비껴가지 못한다."]], [["“I do not quite understand you.”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 잘 모르겠군요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes you do, I think.", "\"오, 아니, 아실 텐데요."], ["I think you do.", "아시리라 봅니다."], ["Come, come, Lady Dedlock, we must not fence and parry now.", "자, 자, 데드록 마님, 이제 칼을 막고 찌르는 시늉은 그만둡시다."], ["You know you like this girl.”", "마님이 이 처녀를 아끼시는 걸 마님도 아시지요.\""]], [["“Well, sir?”", "\"그래서요, 선생?\""]], [["“And you know—and I know—that you have not sent her away for the reasons you have assigned, but for the purpose of separating her as much as possible from—excuse my mentioning it as a matter of business—any reproach and exposure that impend over yourself.”", "\"그리고 마님도—저도—압니다. 마님이 그 애를 둘러대신 이유 때문이 아니라, 마님 자신에게 닥쳐오는 어떤 비난과 폭로로부터—사무적인 일로 이런 말씀을 드리는 걸 용서하십시오—그 애를 되도록 멀리 떼어놓으려는 속셈으로 그 애를 보내셨다는 것을요.\""]], [["“Well, sir?”", "\"그래서요, 선생?\""]], [["“Well, Lady Dedlock,”", "\"그게 말이죠, 데드록 마님,\""], ["returns the lawyer, crossing his legs and nursing the uppermost knee.", "변호사가 다리를 꼬고 위에 얹힌 무릎을 감싸 쥐며 받는다."], ["“I object to that.", "\"저는 그것에 반대합니다."], ["I consider that a dangerous proceeding.", "저는 그것을 위험한 처사로 봅니다."], ["I know it to be unnecessary and calculated to awaken speculation, doubt, rumour, I don’t know what, in the house.", "그것은 불필요하며, 이 집안에 억측과 의심과 소문, 그밖에 뭐가 될지 모를 것들을 불러일으키기 십상이라는 걸 압니다."], ["Besides, it is a violation of our agreement.", "게다가 그것은 우리 합의를 어기는 일입니다."], ["You were to be exactly what you were before.", "마님은 예전과 똑같으셔야 했습니다."], ["Whereas, it must be evident to yourself, as it is to me, that you have been this evening very different from what you were before.", "그런데 마님 자신에게도, 저에게도 분명하듯이, 오늘 저녁 마님은 예전과는 무척 달랐습니다."], ["Why, bless my soul, Lady Dedlock, transparently so!”", "아니, 맙소사, 데드록 마님, 빤히 들여다보이게 그러셨지요!\""]], [["“If, sir,”", "\"선생, 만일,\""], ["she begins, “in my knowledge of my secret—”", "그녀가 입을 연다. \"제 비밀을 알고 있는 제가—\""], ["But he interrupts her.", "그러나 그가 그녀의 말을 끊는다."]], [["“Now, Lady Dedlock, this is a matter of business, and in a matter of business the ground cannot be kept too clear.", "\"자, 데드록 마님, 이건 사무적인 일이고, 사무적인 일에서는 바탕을 더없이 깨끗이 해두어야 합니다."], ["It is no longer your secret.", "그것은 더 이상 마님의 비밀이 아닙니다."], ["Excuse me.", "용서하십시오."], ["That is just the mistake.", "바로 그게 잘못 짚으신 겁니다."], ["It is my secret, in trust for Sir Leicester and the family.", "그것은 제 비밀입니다, 레스터 경과 이 가문을 위해 맡아둔 것이지요."], ["If it were your secret, Lady Dedlock, we should not be here holding this conversation.”", "만일 그것이 마님의 비밀이었다면, 데드록 마님, 우리가 여기서 이런 대화를 나누고 있지는 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“That is very true.", "\"지당한 말이에요."], ["If in my knowledge of THE secret I do what I can to spare an innocent girl (especially, remembering your own reference to her when you told my story to the assembled guests at Chesney Wold) from the taint of my impending shame, I act upon a resolution I have taken.", "그 비밀을 아는 제가, 죄 없는 처녀 하나를 제게 닥쳐올 치욕의 오점에서 지켜주려고 할 수 있는 일을 한다면(더구나 체스니 월드에 모인 손님들에게 당신이 제 이야기를 들려줄 때 그 애를 언급했던 걸 떠올리면 더욱), 그건 제가 굳힌 결심에 따라 하는 거예요."], ["Nothing in the world, and no one in the world, could shake it or could move me.”", "이 세상 어떤 것도, 이 세상 누구도 그것을 흔들거나 저를 움직이지 못해요.\""], ["This she says with great deliberation and distinctness and with no more outward passion than himself.", "마님은 그 못지않게 겉으로 아무런 정념도 드러내지 않은 채, 신중하고 또렷하게 이 말을 한다."], ["As for him, he methodically discusses his matter of business as if she were any insensible instrument used in business.", "그로 말하자면, 그녀가 사무에 쓰이는 무슨 감각 없는 도구라도 되는 양 제 사무를 차근차근 논한다."]], [["“Really?", "\"정말입니까?"], ["Then you see, Lady Dedlock,”", "그렇다면 보십시오, 데드록 마님,\""], ["he returns, “you are not to be trusted.", "그가 받는다. \"마님은 믿을 수가 없는 분이군요."], ["You have put the case in a perfectly plain way, and according to the literal fact; and that being the case, you are not to be trusted.”", "마님은 사정을 더없이 명료하게, 글자 그대로의 사실대로 말씀하셨고, 그렇다면 마님은 믿을 수가 없는 분입니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps you may remember that I expressed some anxiety on this same point when we spoke at night at Chesney Wold?”", "\"우리가 밤에 체스니 월드에서 이야기 나눌 때, 제가 바로 이 점에 대해 얼마간 걱정을 비쳤던 걸 기억하실는지요?\""]], [["“Yes,” says Mr.", "\"네,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, coolly getting up and standing on the hearth.", "토킹혼 씨가 냉정하게 일어나 벽난로 앞에 서며 말한다. \"네."], ["“Yes.", "기억합니다, 데드록 마님."], ["I recollect, Lady Dedlock, that you certainly referred to the girl, but that was before we came to our arrangement, and both the letter and the spirit of our arrangement altogether precluded any action on your part founded upon my discovery.", "마님이 분명 그 처녀를 언급하셨지요. 하지만 그건 우리가 협정에 이르기 전이었고, 우리 협정의 글자와 정신은 제 발견에 기대어 마님이 무슨 행동을 하는 것을 죄다 막아두었습니다."], ["There can be no doubt about that.", "그건 의심할 여지가 없지요."], ["As to sparing the girl, of what importance or value is she?", "그 처녀를 지켜준다는 것은—그 애가 무슨 대수이고 무슨 값어치가 있단 말입니까? 지켜준다고요!"], ["Spare! Lady Dedlock, here is a family name compromised.", "데드록 마님, 여기 한 가문의 이름이 위태로워졌습니다."], ["One might have supposed that the course was straight on—over everything, neither to the right nor to the left, regardless of all considerations in the way, sparing nothing, treading everything under foot.”", "그렇다면 길은 곧장—모든 것을 짓밟으며, 오른쪽으로도 왼쪽으로도 비끼지 않고, 앞을 가로막는 온갖 사정 따위는 아랑곳없이, 아무것도 봐주지 않고, 모든 것을 발밑에 짓이기며 나아가는 거라고 봐야 마땅했지요.\""]], [["She has been looking at the table.", "그녀는 식탁을 바라보고 있었다."], ["She lifts up her eyes and looks at him.", "그녀가 눈을 들어 그를 바라본다."], ["There is a stern expression on her face and a part of her lower lip is compressed under her teeth.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 준엄한 표정이 어려 있고, 아랫입술 한쪽이 이 아래에 깨물려 있다."], ["“This woman understands me,” Mr.", "\"이 여자가 내 말을 알아듣는군.\""], ["Tulkinghorn thinks as she lets her glance fall again.", "그녀가 다시 눈길을 떨구는 사이 토킹혼 씨가 생각한다."], ["“SHE cannot be spared.", "\"이 여자는 봐줄 수가 없어."], ["Why should she spare others?”", "그러니 왜 이 여자가 남을 봐줘야 하나?\""]], [["For a little while they are silent.", "잠시 둘은 말이 없다."], ["Lady Dedlock has eaten no dinner, but has twice or thrice poured out water with a steady hand and drunk it.", "데드록 마님은 저녁을 한 술도 들지 않았지만, 흔들림 없는 손으로 두세 번 물을 따라 마셨다."], ["She rises from table, takes a lounging-chair, and reclines in it, shading her face.", "마님이 식탁에서 일어나 안락의자를 끌어다 거기 기대앉아 얼굴을 가린다."], ["There is nothing in her manner to express weakness or excite compassion.", "그녀의 태도에는 나약함을 드러내거나 동정을 자아낼 만한 구석이 전혀 없다."], ["It is thoughtful, gloomy, concentrated.", "골똘하고 침울하며 한곳에 모여 있다."], ["“This woman,” thinks Mr.", "\"이 여자는,\""], ["Tulkinghorn, standing on the hearth, again a dark object closing up her view, “is a study.”", "다시 그녀의 시야를 가로막는 어두운 물체가 되어 벽난로 앞에 선 토킹혼 씨가 생각한다. \"하나의 연구 대상이로군.\""]], [["He studies her at his leisure, not speaking for a time.", "그는 한동안 말없이 여유롭게 그녀를 연구한다."], ["She too studies something at her leisure.", "그녀 역시 여유롭게 무언가를 연구한다."], ["She is not the first to speak, appearing indeed so unlikely to be so, though he stood there until midnight, that even he is driven upon breaking silence.", "그녀는 좀처럼 먼저 입을 열 사람으로 보이지 않으니, 그가 자정까지 거기 서 있는다 해도 그러지 않을 듯하여, 결국 그조차 침묵을 깨지 않을 수 없게 된다."]], [["“Lady Dedlock, the most disagreeable part of this business interview remains, but it is business.", "\"데드록 마님, 이 사무적 면담에서 가장 거북한 대목이 남아 있습니다만, 어쨌든 사무지요."], ["Our agreement is broken.", "우리 합의는 깨졌습니다."], ["A lady of your sense and strength of character will be prepared for my now declaring it void and taking my own course.”", "마님 같은 분별과 강단을 지닌 분이라면, 제가 이제 그것을 무효라 선언하고 제 나름의 길을 가더라도 마음의 준비가 되어 있으시겠지요.\""]], [["“I am quite prepared.”", "\"충분히 되어 있어요.\""]], [["Mr. Tulkinghorn inclines his head.", "토킹혼 씨가 고개를 숙인다."], ["“That is all I have to trouble you with, Lady Dedlock.”", "\"이것이 제가 마님께 끼칠 폐의 전부입니다, 데드록 마님.\""]], [["She stops him as he is moving out of the room by asking, “This is the notice I was to receive?", "그가 방을 나서려는 참에 그녀가 그를 멈춰 세우며 묻는다. \"이것이 제가 받기로 한 그 통고인가요?"], ["I wish not to misapprehend you.”", "잘못 알아듣고 싶지 않아서요.\""]], [["“Not exactly the notice you were to receive, Lady Dedlock, because the contemplated notice supposed the agreement to have been observed.", "\"마님이 받기로 한 그 통고와 꼭 같지는 않습니다, 데드록 마님. 본디 예정했던 통고는 합의가 지켜졌다는 전제 위에 있었으니까요."], ["But virtually the same, virtually the same.", "하지만 사실상 같은 것입니다, 사실상 같지요."], ["The difference is merely in a lawyer’s mind.”", "차이라야 한낱 변호사의 머릿속에나 있을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“You intend to give me no other notice?”", "\"제게 달리 통고는 하지 않으실 생각인가요?\""]], [["“You are right.", "\"옳게 보셨습니다."], ["No.”", "안 합니다.\""]], [["“Do you contemplate undeceiving Sir Leicester to-night?”", "\"오늘 밤 레스터 경의 눈을 뜨게 하실 작정인가요?\""]], [["“A home question!” says Mr.", "\"정곡을 찌르는 질문이군요!\""], ["Tulkinghorn with a slight smile and cautiously shaking his head at the shaded face.", "토킹혼 씨가 살짝 웃으며, 가려진 얼굴을 향해 조심스레 고개를 가로젓는다."], ["“No, not to-night.”", "\"아니요, 오늘 밤은 아닙니다.\""]], [["“To-morrow?”", "\"내일은요?\""]], [["“All things considered, I had better decline answering that question, Lady Dedlock.", "\"이것저것 따져보면, 그 질문에는 대답하지 않는 편이 낫겠습니다, 데드록 마님."], ["If I were to say I don’t know when, exactly, you would not believe me, and it would answer no purpose.", "제가 정확히 언제인지 모른다고 말하면 마님은 믿지 않으실 테고, 그래봐야 아무 소용도 없을 테니까요."], ["It may be to-morrow.", "내일이 될 수도 있지요."], ["I would rather say no more.", "그 이상은 말씀드리지 않으렵니다."], ["You are prepared, and I hold out no expectations which circumstances might fail to justify.", "마님은 마음의 준비가 되어 있으시고, 저는 형편이 받쳐주지 못할 어떤 기대도 내비치지 않겠습니다."], ["I wish you good evening.”", "안녕히 주무십시오.\""]], [["She removes her hand, turns her pale face towards him as he walks silently to the door, and stops him once again as he is about to open it.", "그녀가 손을 치우고 창백한 얼굴을 그에게로 돌리는 사이 그가 말없이 문 쪽으로 걸어가고, 그가 막 문을 열려는 참에 그녀가 다시 한번 그를 멈춰 세운다."]], [["“Do you intend to remain in the house any time?", "\"이 집에 한동안 더 머무실 작정인가요?"], ["I heard you were writing in the library.", "서재에서 글을 쓰고 계셨다고 들었어요."], ["Are you going to return there?”", "거기로 돌아가실 건가요?\""]], [["“Only for my hat.", "\"모자를 가지러 갈 뿐입니다."], ["I am going home.”", "저는 집으로 갑니다.\""]], [["She bows her eyes rather than her head, the movement is so slight and curious, and he withdraws.", "그녀가 고개라기보다 눈을 숙이니, 그 움직임이 어찌나 미미하고 야릇한지, 그가 물러난다."], ["Clear of the room he looks at his watch but is inclined to doubt it by a minute or thereabouts.", "방을 벗어나 그가 회중시계를 들여다보지만, 일 분쯤 틀린 게 아닌가 의심한다."], ["There is a splendid clock upon the staircase, famous, as splendid clocks not often are, for its accuracy.", "계단에는 훌륭한 시계가 하나 있는데, 훌륭한 시계치고는 드물게도 정확하기로 이름났다."], ["“And what do YOU say,” Mr.", "\"그래 너는 뭐라 하느냐,\""], ["Tulkinghorn inquires, referring to it.", "토킹혼 씨가 그 시계를 두고 묻는다."], ["“What do you say?”", "\"너는 뭐라 하느냐?\""]], [["If it said now, “Don’t go home!”", "만일 그것이 지금 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 말한다면."], ["What a famous clock, hereafter, if it said to-night of all the nights that it has counted off, to this old man of all the young and old men who have ever stood before it, “Don’t go home!”", "만일 그것이 헤아려온 그 숱한 밤 가운데 하필 오늘 밤, 그 앞에 서본 적 있는 그 숱한 젊은이와 늙은이 가운데 하필 이 늙은이에게 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 말한다면, 훗날 얼마나 이름난 시계가 되겠는가!"], ["With its sharp clear bell it strikes three quarters after seven and ticks on again.", "시계가 날카롭고 또렷한 종소리로 일곱 시 사십오 분을 치고는 다시 똑딱똑딱 간다."], ["“Why, you are worse than I thought you,”", "\"원, 내가 보던 것보다 더 못쓰겠군.\""], ["says Mr. Tulkinghorn, muttering reproof to his watch.", "토킹혼 씨가 제 회중시계를 나무라듯 중얼거린다."], ["“Two minutes wrong?", "\"이 분이나 틀려?"], ["At this rate you won’t last my time.”", "이런 식이면 너는 내 수명도 못 채우겠다.\""], ["What a watch to return good for evil if it ticked in answer, “Don’t go home!”", "만일 그것이 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 똑딱여 답함으로써 악을 선으로 갚는다면, 그 얼마나 갸륵한 회중시계인가!"]], [["He passes out into the streets and walks on, with his hands behind him, under the shadow of the lofty houses, many of whose mysteries, difficulties, mortgages, delicate affairs of all kinds, are treasured up within his old black satin waistcoat.", "그가 거리로 나서서, 뒷짐을 진 채 높다란 집들의 그늘 아래로 걸어간다. 그 집들의 숱한 비밀과 골칫거리, 저당, 온갖 미묘한 사정들이 그의 낡은 검은 새틴 조끼 안에 고이 간직되어 있다."], ["He is in the confidence of the very bricks and mortar.", "그는 그 벽돌과 회반죽 자체의 속내까지 꿰고 있다."], ["The high chimney-stacks telegraph family secrets to him.", "높다란 굴뚝들이 가문의 비밀을 그에게 전신으로 알린다."], ["Yet there is not a voice in a mile of them to whisper, “Don’t go home!”", "그런데도 그 일 마일 안에는 \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 속삭일 목소리가 단 하나도 없다."]], [["Through the stir and motion of the commoner streets; through the roar and jar of many vehicles, many feet, many voices; with the blazing shop-lights lighting him on, the west wind blowing him on, and the crowd pressing him on, he is pitilessly urged upon his way, and nothing meets him murmuring, “Don’t go home!”", "더 평범한 거리들의 북적임과 움직임을 가로질러, 숱한 마차와 숱한 발과 숱한 목소리의 우렁찬 소음과 덜컹거림을 헤치며, 환하게 타오르는 상점 불빛이 그의 앞길을 밝히고 서풍이 그를 떠밀고 군중이 그를 밀어붙이는 가운데, 그는 무자비하게 제 갈 길로 내몰리지만, \"집에 가지 마시오!\" 하고 웅얼대며 그를 마주하는 것은 아무것도 없다."], ["Arrived at last in his dull room to light his candles, and look round and up, and see the Roman pointing from the ceiling, there is no new significance in the Roman’s hand to-night or in the flutter of the attendant groups to give him the late warning, “Don’t come here!”", "마침내 그의 음울한 방에 이르러 촛불을 밝히고 둘레와 위쪽을 둘러보며 천장에서 손가락질하는 로마인을 올려다보지만, 오늘 밤 그 로마인의 손에도, 시중드는 무리의 펄럭임에도 \"이리 오지 마시오!\" 하는 때늦은 경고를 줄 만한 새로운 의미는 없다."]], [["It is a moonlight night, but the moon, being past the full, is only now rising over the great wilderness of London.", "달밤이지만, 보름을 지난 달은 이제야 런던의 거대한 황야 위로 떠오른다."], ["The stars are shining as they shone above the turret-leads at Chesney Wold.", "별들은 체스니 월드 첨탑의 납지붕 위에서 빛나던 그대로 빛난다."], ["This woman, as he has of late been so accustomed to call her, looks out upon them.", "요즘 그가 줄곧 부르는 호칭대로 하자면 이 여자가, 그 별들을 내다본다."], ["Her soul is turbulent within her; she is sick at heart and restless.", "그녀의 영혼은 안에서 들끓고, 그녀는 가슴이 답답하며 안절부절못한다."], ["The large rooms are too cramped and close.", "그 큰 방들이 너무도 비좁고 갑갑하다."], ["She cannot endure their restraint and will walk alone in a neighbouring garden.", "그녀는 방의 옥죔을 견딜 수 없어 근처 정원을 홀로 거닐려 한다."]], [["Too capricious and imperious in all she does to be the cause of much surprise in those about her as to anything she does, this woman, loosely muffled, goes out into the moonlight.", "하는 일마다 너무도 변덕스럽고 위세 등등하여 무슨 짓을 하든 주위 사람을 별로 놀라게 하지 않는 이 여자가, 헐겁게 몸을 감싼 채 달빛 속으로 나선다."], ["Mercury attends with the key.", "헤르메스가 열쇠를 들고 따라간다."], ["Having opened the garden-gate, he delivers the key into his Lady’s hands at her request and is bidden to go back.", "정원 문을 연 뒤 그는 마님의 청에 따라 열쇠를 마님 손에 건네고 돌아가라는 분부를 받는다."], ["She will walk there some time to ease her aching head.", "마님은 지끈거리는 머리를 식히려 한동안 거기를 거닐 것이다."], ["She may be an hour, she may be more.", "한 시간쯤 걸릴 수도, 더 걸릴 수도 있다."], ["She needs no further escort.", "더는 누가 따라올 필요가 없다."], ["The gate shuts upon its spring with a clash, and he leaves her passing on into the dark shade of some trees.", "문이 용수철 소리와 함께 철컥 닫히고, 그는 마님이 어느 나무들의 어두운 그늘 속으로 들어가도록 두고 떠난다."]], [["A fine night, and a bright large moon, and multitudes of stars. Mr.", "좋은 밤이고, 밝고 커다란 달이며, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 별이다."], ["Tulkinghorn, in repairing to his cellar and in opening and shutting those resounding doors, has to cross a little prison-like yard.", "토킹혼 씨는 제 지하 술 저장고로 가서 그 울리는 문들을 여닫느라, 감옥 같은 작은 마당을 가로질러야 한다."], ["He looks up casually, thinking what a fine night, what a bright large moon, what multitudes of stars!", "그가 무심코 위를 올려다보며 생각한다, 참 좋은 밤이군, 참 밝고 커다란 달이군, 별이 참 무수하군!"], ["A quiet night, too.", "게다가 고요한 밤이군."]], [["A very quiet night.", "참으로 고요한 밤이다."], ["When the moon shines very brilliantly, a solitude and stillness seem to proceed from her that influence even crowded places full of life.", "달이 유난히 찬란하게 빛날 때면, 그 달에서 어떤 적막과 정적이 흘러나와 생기로 북적이는 곳마저 물들이는 듯하다."], ["Not only is it a still night on dusty high roads and on hill-summits, whence a wide expanse of country may be seen in repose, quieter and quieter as it spreads away into a fringe of trees against the sky with the grey ghost of a bloom upon them; not only is it a still night in gardens and in woods, and on the river where the water-meadows are fresh and green, and the stream sparkles on among pleasant islands, murmuring weirs, and whispering rushes; not only does the stillness attend it as it flows where houses cluster thick, where many bridges are reflected in it, where wharves and shipping make it black and awful, where it winds from these disfigurements through marshes whose grim beacons stand like skeletons washed ashore, where it expands through the bolder region of rising grounds, rich in cornfield wind-mill and steeple, and where it mingles with the ever-heaving sea; not only is it a still night on the deep, and on the shore where the watcher stands to see the ship with her spread wings cross the path of light that appears to be presented to only him; but even on this stranger’s wilderness of London there is some rest.", "먼지 인 큰길 위에도, 또 드넓은 시골 들녘이 잠든 듯 한눈에 들어오는 언덕마루에도 고요한 밤일 뿐 아니라—거기서는 들판이 멀어지며 하늘을 등진 나무들의 띠에 이르고, 그 위로 잿빛 유령 같은 꽃빛이 어리며 점점 더 고요해진다—정원과 숲에도, 물풀밭이 싱그럽게 푸르고 시냇물이 정겨운 섬들 사이로 반짝이며 둑을 넘어 웅얼대고 갈대 사이로 속삭이며 흐르는 강 위에도 고요한 밤일 뿐 아니라, 집들이 빽빽이 들어찬 곳을 지나며, 숱한 다리가 그 위에 비치는 곳을 지나며, 부두와 선박이 그 강을 검고 으스스하게 만드는 곳을 지나며, 이 흉물들을 벗어나 해변에 떠밀려온 해골처럼 음산한 봉화탑이 늘어선 늪지를 굽이쳐 지나며, 옥수수밭과 풍차와 첨탑이 풍성한 더 호방한 구릉지대를 통과해 펼쳐지며, 마침내 쉴 새 없이 일렁이는 바다와 뒤섞이는 그곳까지, 강물이 흐르는 동안 줄곧 적막이 그것을 따른다. 깊은 바다 위에도, 또 펼친 날개를 단 배가 오직 자기에게만 깔린 듯한 빛의 길을 가로지르는 것을 지켜보려 파수꾼이 서 있는 그 해변에도 고요한 밤일 뿐 아니라, 이 이방인의 황야 같은 런던에조차 얼마간의 안식이 깃든다."], ["Its steeples and towers and its one great dome grow more ethereal; its smoky house-tops lose their grossness in the pale effulgence; the noises that arise from the streets are fewer and are softened, and the footsteps on the pavements pass more tranquilly away.", "그 첨탑과 탑들과 하나뿐인 거대한 둥근 지붕이 한층 영묘해지고, 연기에 그을린 지붕들은 그 창백한 광휘 속에서 거친 티를 벗으며, 거리에서 일어나는 소음은 줄고 누그러지고, 보도 위의 발소리는 한결 잔잔하게 멀어진다."], ["In these fields of Mr. Tulkinghorn’s inhabiting, where the shepherds play on Chancery pipes that have no stop, and keep their sheep in the fold by hook and by crook until they have shorn them exceeding close, every noise is merged, this moonlight night, into a distant ringing hum, as if the city were a vast glass, vibrating.", "토킹혼 씨가 거니는 이 들판, 곧 멈춤구멍 하나 없는 대법원 피리를 양치기들이 불어대고, 양들을 멈춤도 굽힘도 없이 우리에 가두어 두었다가 마침내 바싹 털을 깎아버리는 이 들판에서는, 이 달밤에 모든 소음이 한데 녹아들어 멀리서 울리는 웅웅거림이 되니, 마치 도시가 떨고 있는 거대한 유리잔이라도 되는 듯하다."]], [["What’s that?", "저게 뭐지?"], ["Who fired a gun or pistol?", "누가 총인지 권총인지를 쏘았나?"], ["Where was it?", "어디였지?"]], [["The few foot-passengers start, stop, and stare about them.", "몇 안 되는 보행자들이 흠칫 놀라 멈춰 서서 사방을 두리번거린다."], ["Some windows and doors are opened, and people come out to look.", "창문과 문이 더러 열리고, 사람들이 보려고 나온다."], ["It was a loud report and echoed and rattled heavily.", "요란한 발포음이었고, 메아리치며 무겁게 덜컹거렸다."], ["It shook one house, or so a man says who was passing.", "지나가던 어떤 사내의 말로는 집 한 채가 흔들렸다고도 한다."], ["It has aroused all the dogs in the neighbourhood, who bark vehemently.", "그 소리에 동네의 개들이 죄다 깨어나 사납게 짖어댄다."], ["Terrified cats scamper across the road.", "겁먹은 고양이들이 길을 가로질러 줄행랑친다."], ["While the dogs are yet barking and howling—there is one dog howling like a demon—the church-clocks, as if they were startled too, begin to strike.", "개들이 아직 짖고 울부짖는 동안에도—악마처럼 울부짖는 개가 한 마리 있다—교회 종들이 저마다 놀라기라도 한 듯 치기 시작한다."], ["The hum from the streets, likewise, seems to swell into a shout.", "거리에서 이는 웅웅거림 역시 외침으로 부풀어 오르는 듯하다."], ["But it is soon over.", "그러나 그것도 곧 잦아든다."], ["Before the last clock begins to strike ten, there is a lull.", "마지막 종이 열 시를 치기 시작하기도 전에 잠잠해진다."], ["When it has ceased, the fine night, the bright large moon, and multitudes of stars, are left at peace again.", "그것이 멎고 나자, 좋은 밤과 밝고 커다란 달과 무수한 별들이 다시 평온히 남는다."]], [["Has Mr. Tulkinghorn been disturbed?", "토킹혼 씨는 방해를 받았을까?"], ["His windows are dark and quiet, and his door is shut.", "그의 창은 어둡고 고요하며, 문은 닫혀 있다."], ["It must be something unusual indeed to bring him out of his shell.", "그를 제 껍데기 밖으로 끌어내려면 정말이지 어지간히 예사롭지 않은 일이라야 한다."], ["Nothing is heard of him, nothing is seen of him.", "그에 관해서는 아무 소리도 들리지 않고, 아무 모습도 보이지 않는다."], ["What power of cannon might it take to shake that rusty old man out of his immovable composure?", "그 녹슨 늙은이를 그 미동 없는 침착함에서 흔들어내려면 대포를 얼마나 쏘아야 할까?"]], [["For many years the persistent Roman has been pointing, with no particular meaning, from that ceiling.", "여러 해 동안 그 끈질긴 로마인은 별 뜻 없이 저 천장에서 손가락질해 왔다."], ["It is not likely that he has any new meaning in him to-night.", "오늘 밤이라 해서 그에게 무슨 새로운 뜻이 생겼을 리 없다."], ["Once pointing, always pointing—like any Roman, or even Briton, with a single idea.", "한번 가리키면 언제나 가리킨다—한 가지 생각밖에 없는 여느 로마인처럼, 아니 브리튼인이라 한들 마찬가지로."], ["There he is, no doubt, in his impossible attitude, pointing, unavailingly, all night long.", "거기 그가, 불가능한 자세로 밤새도록 부질없이 손가락질하며 있는 게 분명하다."], ["Moonlight, darkness, dawn, sunrise, day.", "달빛, 어둠, 동틀 녘, 해돋이, 한낮."], ["There he is still, eagerly pointing, and no one minds him.", "거기 그가 여전히 열심히 손가락질하고 있건만, 아무도 그를 거들떠보지 않는다."]], [["But a little after the coming of the day come people to clean the rooms.", "그러나 날이 밝고 조금 지나 사람들이 방을 치우러 온다."], ["And either the Roman has some new meaning in him, not expressed before, or the foremost of them goes wild, for looking up at his outstretched hand and looking down at what is below it, that person shrieks and flies.", "그런데 그 로마인에게 전에는 드러나지 않던 무슨 새로운 뜻이 생겼는지, 아니면 그들 가운데 맨 앞에 선 자가 미쳐버린 것인지, 그자가 뻗은 그 손을 올려다보고 그 아래에 놓인 것을 내려다보더니 비명을 지르며 달아난다."], ["The others, looking in as the first one looked, shriek and fly too, and there is an alarm in the street.", "나머지 사람들도 첫 번째 사람이 들여다본 대로 안을 들여다보다가 똑같이 비명을 지르며 달아나고, 거리에 소동이 인다."]], [["What does it mean?", "이게 무슨 일인가?"], ["No light is admitted into the darkened chamber, and people unaccustomed to it enter, and treading softly but heavily, carry a weight into the bedroom and lay it down.", "어두컴컴한 방에는 빛이 한 줄기도 들지 않고, 익숙지 않은 사람들이 들어가 사뿐하면서도 무겁게 걸으며 무언가 무거운 것을 침실로 옮겨 내려놓는다."], ["There is whispering and wondering all day, strict search of every corner, careful tracing of steps, and careful noting of the disposition of every article of furniture.", "온종일 수군거림과 의아함이 가득하고, 구석구석 샅샅이 뒤지며, 발자국을 조심스레 좇고, 가구 한 점 한 점의 놓인 자리를 꼼꼼히 적어둔다."], ["All eyes look up at the Roman, and all voices murmur, “If he could only tell what he saw!”", "모두의 눈이 그 로마인을 올려다보고, 모두의 입이 웅얼거린다. \"저자가 본 것을 말해 줄 수만 있다면!\""]], [["He is pointing at a table with a bottle (nearly full of wine) and a glass upon it and two candles that were blown out suddenly soon after being lighted.", "그가 가리키는 것은 (포도주가 거의 가득 찬) 병 하나와 잔 하나가 놓이고, 불을 붙인 지 얼마 안 되어 느닷없이 꺼져버린 촛불 두 자루가 놓인 탁자다."], ["He is pointing at an empty chair and at a stain upon the ground before it that might be almost covered with a hand.", "그가 가리키는 것은 빈 의자 하나와 그 앞 바닥에 진 얼룩 하나니, 손 하나로 거의 가려질 만한 얼룩이다."], ["These objects lie directly within his range.", "이 물건들은 바로 그가 가리키는 범위 안에 놓여 있다."], ["An excited imagination might suppose that there was something in them so terrific as to drive the rest of the composition, not only the attendant big-legged boys, but the clouds and flowers and pillars too—in short, the very body and soul of Allegory, and all the brains it has—stark mad.", "흥분한 상상력이라면, 거기에 무언가 너무도 끔찍한 것이 있어 그 구성의 나머지를—시중드는 굵은 다리의 사내아이들뿐 아니라 구름과 꽃과 기둥들까지, 한마디로 알레고리의 바로 그 몸과 영혼, 그리고 거기 깃든 온갖 머리까지—깡그리 미치게 했다고 여길지도 모른다."], ["It happens surely that every one who comes into the darkened room and looks at these things looks up at the Roman and that he is invested in all eyes with mystery and awe, as if he were a paralysed dumb witness.", "어두컴컴한 방에 들어와 이 물건들을 보는 사람마다 그 로마인을 올려다보고, 그리하여 그가 마치 마비되어 말 못 하는 증인이라도 되는 양 모든 눈에 신비와 경외에 휩싸이는 일이, 어김없이 벌어진다."]], [["So it shall happen surely, through many years to come, that ghostly stories shall be told of the stain upon the floor, so easy to be covered, so hard to be got out, and that the Roman, pointing from the ceiling shall point, so long as dust and damp and spiders spare him, with far greater significance than he ever had in Mr.", "이렇게 어김없이 벌어지리라. 앞으로 숱한 세월에 걸쳐, 그토록 가리기는 쉬워도 지워내기는 그토록 어려운 바닥의 그 얼룩을 두고 으스스한 이야기들이 떠돌리라."], ["Tulkinghorn’s time, and with a deadly meaning. For Mr.", "그리고 그 로마인은 천장에서 손가락질하되, 먼지와 습기와 거미가 그를 비껴두는 한, 토킹혼 씨 시절에 지녔던 것보다 훨씬 큰 의미로, 죽음의 뜻을 담아 가리키리라."], ["Tulkinghorn’s time is over for evermore, and the Roman pointed at the murderous hand uplifted against his life, and pointed helplessly at him, from night to morning, lying face downward on the floor, shot through the heart. Dutiful Friendship", "토킹혼 씨의 시절은 영영 끝나버렸으니, 그 로마인은 그의 목숨을 노려 치켜든 살인의 손을 가리켰고, 또 가슴을 관통당해 바닥에 얼굴을 묻고 쓰러진 그를, 밤부터 아침까지 속절없이 가리켰던 것이다."]], [["A great annual occasion has come round in the establishment of Mr. Matthew Bagnet, otherwise Lignum Vitae, ex-artilleryman and present bassoon-player.", "매튜 배그넷 씨, 다른 이름으로는 리그넘 비타이라 불리는, 전직 포병이자 현직 바순 연주자의 가정에 해마다 돌아오는 큰 행사가 또 찾아왔다."], ["An occasion of feasting and festival.", "잔치와 축제의 날이다."], ["The celebration of a birthday in the family.", "가족 가운데 한 사람의 생일을 기리는 자리다."]], [["It is not Mr.", "* 리그넘 비타이: 라틴어로 '생명의 나무'를 뜻하며, 단단하기로 이름난 열대 목재의 이름이기도 하다."], ["Bagnet’s birthday. Mr.", "배그넷의 우직하고 굳센 성품을 빗댄 별명이다."], ["Bagnet merely distinguishes that epoch in the musical instrument business by kissing the children with an extra smack before breakfast, smoking an additional pipe after dinner, and wondering towards evening what his poor old mother is thinking about it—a subject of infinite speculation, and rendered so by his mother having departed this life twenty years.", "배그넷 씨의 생일은 아니다. 배그넷 씨는 악기 장사의 그 시기를 그저 아침을 먹기 전에 아이들에게 입을 한 번 더 쪽 맞추고, 저녁을 먹은 뒤 담배를 한 대 더 피우고, 해 질 무렵이면 가엾은 노모가 이걸 어떻게 생각할까 곰곰 떠올리는 것으로 표시할 따름이다."], ["Some men rarely revert to their father, but seem, in the bank-books of their remembrance, to have transferred all the stock of filial affection into their mother’s name.", "끝없이 곱씹게 되는 주제인데, 그 어머니가 세상을 떠난 지 이십 년이 되었으니 더욱 그러하다. 더러 어떤 사내들은 아버지는 좀처럼 떠올리지 않으면서, 추억이라는 통장 속 효심의 자산을 몽땅 어머니 이름으로 옮겨놓은 듯하다."], ["Mr. Bagnet is one of these.", "배그넷 씨가 바로 그런 사람이다."], ["Perhaps his exalted appreciation of the merits of the old girl causes him usually to make the noun-substantive “goodness” of the feminine gender.", "어쩌면 노모의 미덕을 워낙 드높이 받드는 탓에, 그는 으레 \"선함\"이라는 명사를 여성형으로 다루는지도 모른다."]], [["It is not the birthday of one of the three children.", "세 아이 가운데 하나의 생일도 아니다."], ["Those occasions are kept with some marks of distinction, but they rarely overleap the bounds of happy returns and a pudding. On young Woolwich’s last birthday, Mr.", "그런 날들도 어느 정도 특별하게 챙기긴 하지만, 무사히 또 한 살 먹은 것을 축하하며 푸딩을 차리는 경계를 넘어서는 일은 드물다."], ["Bagnet certainly did, after observing on his growth and general advancement, proceed, in a moment of profound reflection on the changes wrought by time, to examine him in the catechism, accomplishing with extreme accuracy the questions number one and two, “What is your name?” and “Who gave you that name?”", "어린 울리치의 지난 생일에 배그넷 씨는 아이가 자란 것과 두루 발전한 것을 두고 한마디 한 뒤, 세월이 빚어내는 변화를 깊이 곱씹던 차에 교리문답을 시켜보는 데까지 나아간 게 분명하다."], ["but there failing in the exact precision of his memory and substituting for number three the question “And how do you like that name?” which he propounded with a sense of its importance, in itself so edifying and improving as to give it quite an orthodox air.", "첫째와 둘째 물음, \"네 이름이 무엇이냐?\"와 \"누가 네게 그 이름을 주었느냐?\"는 더없이 정확하게 해냈으나, 거기서 기억의 정밀함이 어긋나 셋째 물음 자리에 \"그래서 그 이름이 마음에 드느냐?\"라는 물음을 갈아 끼웠으니, 그는 그 질문을 무게감 있게 던졌고, 그 자체로 어찌나 교화적이고 향상에 이로운지 자못 정통적인 느낌마저 풍겼다."], ["This, however, was a speciality on that particular birthday, and not a general solemnity.", "그러나 이건 그 특정 생일에만 있었던 특별 사례일 뿐, 늘 치르는 의식은 아니었다."]], [["It is the old girl’s birthday, and that is the greatest holiday and reddest-letter day in Mr. Bagnet’s calendar.", "노모의 생일이다."], ["The auspicious event is always commemorated according to certain forms settled and prescribed by Mr. Bagnet some years since.", "그리고 그날은 배그넷 씨의 달력에서 가장 큰 명절이자 가장 붉은 글자로 적힌 날이다."], ["Mr. Bagnet, being deeply convinced that to have a pair of fowls for dinner is to attain the highest pitch of imperial luxury, invariably goes forth himself very early in the morning of this day to buy a pair; he is, as invariably, taken in by the vendor and installed in the possession of the oldest inhabitants of any coop in Europe.", "이 경사스러운 행사는 몇 해 전 배그넷 씨가 정해 규정해놓은 일정한 격식에 따라 늘 기념한다."], ["Returning with these triumphs of toughness tied up in a clean blue and white cotton handkerchief (essential to the arrangements), he in a casual manner invites Mrs.", "저녁상에 닭 두 마리를 올리는 것이야말로 제왕에 버금가는 사치의 절정에 이르는 일이라고 깊이 믿는 배그넷 씨는, 이날이면 어김없이 몸소 아주 이른 아침에 나가 닭 두 마리를 사 온다."], ["Bagnet to declare at breakfast what she would like for dinner. Mrs.", "그리고 어김없이 장사꾼에게 속아서, 유럽의 어느 닭장에서든 가장 오래 묵은 터줏대감을 손에 넣고야 만다."], ["Bagnet, by a coincidence never known to fail, replying fowls, Mr. Bagnet instantly produces his bundle from a place of concealment amidst general amazement and rejoicing.", "이 질김의 승리물을 깨끗한 청백 무명 손수건에(이 손수건이 이 행사의 필수품이다) 싸 들고 돌아와서는, 짐짓 무심한 척 배그넷 부인에게 아침상에서 저녁으로 뭘 먹고 싶은지 말해보라고 청한다."], ["He further requires that the old girl shall do nothing all day long but sit in her very best gown and be served by himself and the young people.", "한 번도 어긋난 적 없는 우연으로, 배그넷 부인이 닭이라고 대답하면, 배그넷 씨는 곧장 숨겨둔 곳에서 보따리를 꺼내 들어 온 식구의 경탄과 환호를 자아낸다."], ["As he is not illustrious for his cookery, this may be supposed to be a matter of state rather than enjoyment on the old girl’s part, but she keeps her state with all imaginable cheerfulness.", "그는 한술 더 떠 노모가 하루 종일 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입고 앉아만 있으면서 자기와 아이들에게 시중을 받아야 한다고 못 박는다. 그의 요리 솜씨가 이름난 게 아닌지라, 노모로서는 즐거움이라기보다 격식을 차린 노릇에 가까울 법하지만, 그래도 그녀는 상상할 수 있는 한 더없이 명랑하게 그 격식을 지킨다."]], [["On this present birthday, Mr.", "이번 생일에도 배그넷 씨는 늘 하던 준비를 마쳤다."], ["Bagnet has accomplished the usual preliminaries.", "그는 닭 두 마리를 사 왔는데, 속담에 진실이 조금이라도 담겨 있다면 이놈들은 결코 겨로 꾀어 잡힌 놈들이 아니다."], ["He has bought two specimens of poultry, which, if there be any truth in adages, were certainly not caught with chaff, to be prepared for the spit; he has amazed and rejoiced the family by their unlooked-for production; he is himself directing the roasting of the poultry; and Mrs.", "그놈들을 꼬챙이에 꿸 채비를 시켰고, 뜻밖의 등장으로 식구들을 놀라게 하고 기쁘게 했으며, 손수 닭 굽는 일을 진두지휘하고 있다. 한편 배그넷 부인은 일이 잘못 돌아가는 걸 보고 손이 근질근질해 막고 싶어 하면서도, 격식 차린 옷을 입고 귀빈으로 앉아 있다."], ["Bagnet, with her wholesome brown fingers itching to prevent what she sees going wrong, sits in her gown of ceremony, an honoured guest.", "* 겨로 꾀어 잡힌 놈들이 아니다: \"늙은 새는 겨로 꾀어 잡지 못한다\"는 영어 속담을 빗댄 것으로, 닭들이 그만큼 늙고 노회하다는 풍자."]], [["Quebec and Malta lay the cloth for dinner, while Woolwich, serving, as beseems him, under his father, keeps the fowls revolving.", "퀘벡과 몰타가 저녁상에 식탁보를 깔고, 울리치는 제 신분에 걸맞게 아버지 밑에서 거들며 닭을 빙빙 돌린다. 이 어린 일꾼들이 실수를 저지를 때마다 배그넷 부인은 이따금 윙크를 보내거나 고개를 절레절레 젓거나 얼굴을 찡그려 보인다."], ["To these young scullions Mrs. Bagnet occasionally imparts a wink, or a shake of the head, or a crooked face, as they made mistakes.", "* 퀘벡, 몰타: 배그넷 부부의 두 딸로, 아버지가 군 복무하던 주둔지의 지명을 따서 붙인 이름이다."]], [["“At half after one.”", "\"한 시 반이다.\""], ["Says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["“To the minute.", "\"일 분도 안 틀리게."], ["They’ll be done.”", "그때면 다 익는다.\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet, with anguish, beholds one of them at a standstill before the fire and beginning to burn.", "배그넷 부인은 가슴 졸이며, 닭 한 마리가 불 앞에서 돌기를 멈추고 타기 시작하는 걸 바라본다."]], [["“You shall have a dinner, old girl,” says Mr.", "\"임자, 여왕한테나 차릴 만한 저녁상을 받게 될 거야.\""], ["Bagnet. “Fit for a queen.”", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."]], [["Mrs. Bagnet shows her white teeth cheerfully, but to the perception of her son, betrays so much uneasiness of spirit that he is impelled by the dictates of affection to ask her, with his eyes, what is the matter, thus standing, with his eyes wide open, more oblivious of the fowls than before, and not affording the least hope of a return to consciousness.", "배그넷 부인은 하얀 이를 명랑하게 드러내 보이지만, 아들의 눈에는 마음의 불안이 너무 또렷이 비쳐, 아들은 애정에 떠밀려 눈으로 무슨 일이냐고 어머니에게 묻는다."], ["Fortunately his elder sister perceives the cause of the agitation in Mrs.", "그렇게 두 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 선 채 아까보다 더 닭을 까맣게 잊어버려, 정신을 차릴 기미라곤 조금도 보이지 않는다."], ["Bagnet’s breast and with an admonitory poke recalls him. The stopped fowls going round again, Mrs.", "다행히 누나가 배그넷 부인의 가슴속 동요의 까닭을 알아채고는, 타이르듯 콕 찔러 동생을 제정신으로 돌려놓는다."], ["Bagnet closes her eyes in the intensity of her relief.", "멈췄던 닭이 다시 돌아가자 배그넷 부인은 안도가 사무쳐 눈을 감는다."]], [["“George will look us up,”", "\"조지가 우릴 찾아올 거야.\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["“At half after four.", "\"네 시 반에."], ["To the moment.", "어김없이."], ["How many years, old girl.", "임자, 조지가 우릴 찾아온 지가."], ["Has George looked us up.", "몇 해째지."], ["This afternoon?”", "오늘 오후로?\""]], [["“Ah, Lignum, Lignum, as many as make an old woman of a young one, I begin to think.", "\"아아, 리그넘, 리그넘, 젊은 처녀가 늙은 할멈이 되고도 남을 만큼 됐지, 슬슬 그런 생각이 드네."], ["Just about that, and no less,” returns Mrs.", "딱 그쯤이지, 그보다 적지는 않을 거야.\""], ["Bagnet, laughing and shaking her head.", "배그넷 부인이 웃으며 고개를 저으며 대답한다."]], [["“Old girl,”", "\"임자.\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet, “never mind.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["You’d be as young as ever you was.", "\"괜찮아. 당신은 옛날만큼 젊어."], ["If you wasn’t younger.", "더 젊어지진 않았다 쳐도."], ["Which you are.", "사실은 더 젊어졌지."], ["As everybody knows.”", "다들 아는 일이야.\""]], [["Quebec and Malta here exclaim, with clapping of hands, that Bluffy is sure to bring mother something, and begin to speculate on what it will be.", "이 대목에서 퀘벡과 몰타가 손뼉을 치며, 블러피가 분명 엄마한테 뭔가를 가져올 거라고 소리치고는, 그게 뭘까 점치기 시작한다."]], [["“Do you know, Lignum,” says Mrs.", "* 블러피: 조지를 가리키는 아이들의 애칭."], ["Bagnet, casting a glance on the table-cloth, and winking “salt!”", "\"있잖아요, 리그넘.\" 배그넷 부인이 식탁보에 눈길을 던지며, 오른쪽 눈으로 몰타에게 \"소금!\""], ["at Malta with her right eye, and shaking the pepper away from Quebec with her head, “I begin to think George is in the roving way again.”", "하고 윙크하고, 고개를 까딱여 퀘벡한테서 후추를 치우게 하면서 말한다. \"조지가 또 떠돌 마음이 든 것 같아요.\""]], [["“George,”", "\"조지는.\""], ["returns Mr. Bagnet, “will never desert.", "배그넷 씨가 대답한다."], ["And leave his old comrade.", "\"절대 등 돌리지 않아."], ["In the lurch.", "옛 전우를 두고."], ["Don’t be afraid of it.”", "곤경에 내버려두는 일은. 그런 걱정은 말아.\""]], [["“No, Lignum. No.", "\"아니, 리그넘."], ["I don’t say he will.", "아니에요."], ["I don’t think he will.", "그가 그럴 거라는 말은 아니에요."], ["But if he could get over this money trouble of his, I believe he would be off.”", "그러리라곤 생각 안 해요. 하지만 이 돈 문제만 넘긴다면, 떠나버릴 것 같아요.\""]], [["Mr. Bagnet asks why.", "배그넷 씨가 왜냐고 묻는다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["returns his wife, considering, “George seems to me to be getting not a little impatient and restless.", "아내가 곰곰 생각하며 대답한다. \"조지가 영 안달이 나고 안절부절못하는 것 같단 말이에요."], ["I don’t say but what he’s as free as ever.", "옛날만큼 거리낌 없지 않다는 건 아니에요."], ["Of course he must be free or he wouldn’t be George, but he smarts and seems put out.”", "당연히 자유로워야지, 안 그러면 조지가 아니지, 그래도 어딘가 쓰리고 속이 뒤틀린 듯해요.\""]], [["“He’s extra-drilled,” says Mr.", "\"변호사한테 호되게 단련당해서 그래.\""], ["Bagnet. “By a lawyer.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["Who would put the devil out.”", "\"악마라도 화나게 할 변호사한테.\""]], [["“There’s something in that,”", "\"그 말도 일리가 있긴 해요.\""], ["his wife assents; “but so it is, Lignum.”", "아내가 수긍한다. \"하지만 사실이 그런걸요, 리그넘.\""]], [["Further conversation is prevented, for the time, by the necessity under which Mr.", "이때 더 이상의 대화는 잠시 가로막힌다."], ["Bagnet finds himself of directing the whole force of his mind to the dinner, which is a little endangered by the dry humour of the fowls in not yielding any gravy, and also by the made gravy acquiring no flavour and turning out of a flaxen complexion.", "배그넷 씨가 온 정신력을 저녁 식사에 쏟아부어야 할 처지에 놓인 까닭이다. 닭들이 메마른 익살을 부려 국물 한 방울 내놓지 않는 데다, 따로 만든 그레이비마저 도통 맛이 들지 않고 누리끼리한 빛으로 나오는 탓에 식사가 살짝 위태로워졌다."], ["With a similar perverseness, the potatoes crumble off forks in the process of peeling, upheaving from their centres in every direction, as if they were subject to earthquakes. The legs of the fowls, too, are longer than could be desired, and extremely scaly.", "비슷하게 심술궂게도, 감자는 껍질을 벗기는 와중에 포크에서 부스러져, 마치 지진이라도 겪는 양 한가운데서 사방으로 갈라져 솟구친다."], ["Overcoming these disadvantages to the best of his ability, Mr.", "닭다리 또한 바라는 것보다 길고, 비늘이 잔뜩 일어 있다."], ["Bagnet at last dishes and they sit down at table, Mrs. Bagnet occupying the guest’s place at his right hand.", "이 온갖 불리한 점들을 최선을 다해 극복하고서, 배그넷 씨는 마침내 음식을 접시에 담고, 모두 식탁에 둘러앉으니, 배그넷 부인이 그의 오른편 귀빈석을 차지한다."]], [["It is well for the old girl that she has but one birthday in a year, for two such indulgences in poultry might be injurious.", "노모로서는 생일이 일 년에 한 번뿐인 게 다행이다. 이런 닭 호사를 두 번이나 누렸다간 몸에 해로울 수도 있을 테니까."], ["Every kind of finer tendon and ligament that is in the nature of poultry to possess is developed in these specimens in the singular form of guitar-strings.", "닭의 본성이 지닐 수 있는 온갖 가느다란 힘줄과 인대가 이 표본들에서는 기타 줄이라는 별난 형태로 발달해 있다."], ["Their limbs appear to have struck roots into their breasts and bodies, as aged trees strike roots into the earth.", "사지는 마치 늙은 나무가 흙에 뿌리를 뻗듯, 가슴팍과 몸통에 뿌리를 박은 듯하다."], ["Their legs are so hard as to encourage the idea that they must have devoted the greater part of their long and arduous lives to pedestrian exercises and the walking of matches.", "다리는 어찌나 단단한지, 이놈들이 길고 고된 평생의 태반을 도보 운동과 걷기 시합에 바쳤음이 틀림없다는 생각이 들 정도다."], ["But Mr. Bagnet, unconscious of these little defects, sets his heart on Mrs.", "그러나 배그넷 씨는 이런 자잘한 흠은 까맣게 모른 채, 배그넷 부인이 앞에 놓인 진미를 어마어마하게 먹어치우는 데 온 마음을 쏟는다."], ["Bagnet eating a most severe quantity of the delicacies before her; and as that good old girl would not cause him a moment’s disappointment on any day, least of all on such a day, for any consideration, she imperils her digestion fearfully.", "그리고 마음씨 고운 노모는 어떤 날에도, 더구나 이런 날에는 무슨 일이 있어도 그를 단 한순간도 실망시키지 않으려는지라, 제 소화력을 무섭도록 위태롭게 한다."], ["How young Woolwich cleans the drum-sticks without being of ostrich descent, his anxious mother is at a loss to understand.", "어린 울리치가 타조 핏줄도 아니면서 어떻게 닭다리를 그토록 말끔히 발라먹는지, 노심초사하는 어머니로서는 도무지 알 길이 없다."]], [["The old girl has another trial to undergo after the conclusion of the repast in sitting in state to see the room cleared, the hearth swept, and the dinner-service washed up and polished in the backyard.", "식사가 끝난 뒤에도 노모에게는 또 하나의 시련이 남아 있으니, 격식을 차려 앉아서 방이 치워지고 난로가 쓸리고 식기들이 뒷마당에서 씻기고 닦이는 광경을 지켜보는 일이다."], ["The great delight and energy with which the two young ladies apply themselves to these duties, turning up their skirts in imitation of their mother and skating in and out on little scaffolds of pattens, inspire the highest hopes for the future, but some anxiety for the present.", "두 어린 아가씨가 이 일들에 매달리는 대단한 즐거움과 기운—어머니를 흉내 내어 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고, 나막신을 받친 작은 발판을 타고 들락날락 미끄러지듯 누비는 모습—은 앞날에는 더없는 희망을, 그러나 당장에는 약간의 불안을 안긴다. 같은 까닭으로 혀가 뒤엉켜 떠들어대고, 사기그릇이 달그락거리고, 양철 컵이 덜그럭거리고, 빗자루가 휙휙 날고, 물이 펑펑 쓰이는데, 죄다 지나치게 과하다."], ["The same causes lead to confusion of tongues, a clattering of crockery, a rattling of tin mugs, a whisking of brooms, and an expenditure of water, all in excess, while the saturation of the young ladies themselves is almost too moving a spectacle for Mrs. Bagnet to look upon with the calmness proper to her position.", "한편 어린 아가씨들 자신이 흠뻑 젖어버린 꼴은, 배그넷 부인이 제 자리에 어울리는 평정심을 지키며 바라보기에는 거의 너무도 가슴 뭉클한 광경이다."], ["At last the various cleansing processes are triumphantly completed; Quebec and Malta appear in fresh attire, smiling and dry; pipes, tobacco, and something to drink are placed upon the table; and the old girl enjoys the first peace of mind she ever knows on the day of this delightful entertainment.", "마침내 갖가지 청소 과정이 의기양양하게 마무리되고, 퀘벡과 몰타가 보송보송하게 마른 새 옷차림으로 생글거리며 나타난다. 식탁에는 담뱃대와 담배와 마실 것이 놓이고, 노모는 이 즐거운 잔칫날에 처음으로 마음의 평온을 맛본다."]], [["When Mr. Bagnet takes his usual seat, the hands of the clock are very near to half-past four; as they mark it accurately, Mr.", "배그넷 씨가 늘 앉는 자리에 앉을 때, 시곗바늘은 네 시 반에 거의 다 와 있다."], ["Bagnet announces, “George!", "바늘이 정확히 그 시각을 가리키자, 배그넷 씨가 알린다. \"조지!"], ["Military time.”", "군대 시간이군.\""]], [["It is George, and he has hearty congratulations for the old girl (whom he kisses on the great occasion), and for the children, and for Mr.", "조지다. 그는 노모에게(이런 큰 날이라 입을 맞춘다), 아이들에게, 배그넷 씨에게 진심 어린 축하를 건넨다."], ["Bagnet. “Happy returns to all!”", "\"모두 또 한 해 잘 맞으시길!\""], ["says Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말한다."]], [["“But, George, old man!”", "\"근데, 조지, 이 사람아!\""], ["cries Mrs. Bagnet, looking at him curiously.", "배그넷 부인이 그를 의아한 눈으로 바라보며 외친다."], ["“What’s come to you?”", "\"무슨 일 있었어?\""]], [["“Come to me?”", "\"무슨 일이라뇨?\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아니!"], ["You are so white, George—for you—and look so shocked.", "얼굴이 어찌 그리 핼쑥해, 조지—자네답지 않게—게다가 무슨 충격받은 얼굴이야."], ["Now don’t he, Lignum?”", "안 그래요, 리그넘?\""]], [["“George,” says Mr.", "\"조지.\""], ["Bagnet, “tell the old girl.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다. \"노모한테 말하게."], ["What’s the matter.”", "무슨 일인지.\""]], [["“I didn’t know I looked white,”", "\"제 얼굴이 핼쑥한 줄은 몰랐네요.\""], ["says the trooper, passing his hand over his brow, “and I didn’t know I looked shocked, and I’m sorry I do.", "기병이 손으로 이마를 쓸며 말한다. \"충격받은 얼굴인 줄도 몰랐고요, 그렇다니 미안합니다."], ["But the truth is, that boy who was taken in at my place died yesterday afternoon, and it has rather knocked me over.”", "하지만 사실은, 제 가게에 받아들였던 그 소년이 어제 오후에 죽었어요. 그게 저를 좀 무너뜨렸나 봅니다.\""]], [["“Poor creetur!”", "\"가엾은 것!\""], ["says Mrs. Bagnet with a mother’s pity.", "배그넷 부인이 어머니다운 연민으로 말한다."], ["“Is he gone?", "\"그 애가 갔어?"], ["Dear, dear!”", "저런, 저런!\""]], [["“I didn’t mean to say anything about it, for it’s not birthday talk, but you have got it out of me, you see, before I sit down.", "\"이 얘긴 꺼낼 생각이 없었어요, 생일에 할 얘긴 아니니까요. 그런데 보세요, 제가 앉기도 전에 부인이 기어이 끌어내셨네요."], ["I should have roused up in a minute,”", "금방 기운 차렸을 텐데.\" 기병이 일부러 더 쾌활하게 말한다."], ["says the trooper, making himself speak more gaily, “but you’re so quick, Mrs. Bagnet.”", "\"부인은 워낙 눈치가 빠르셔서요, 배그넷 부인.\""]], [["“You’re right.", "\"옳은 말이야."], ["The old girl,”", "우리 노모는.\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Is as quick.", "\"화약처럼."], ["As powder.”", "빠르지.\""]], [["“And what’s more, she’s the subject of the day, and we’ll stick to her,”", "\"게다가 무엇보다, 오늘의 주인공은 부인이시니, 우린 부인께 매달려야지요.\""], ["cries Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 외친다."], ["“See here, I have brought a little brooch along with me.", "\"이거 보세요, 작은 브로치를 하나 가져왔어요."], ["It’s a poor thing, you know, but it’s a keepsake.", "변변찮은 거예요, 그래도 정표지요."], ["That’s all the good it is, Mrs. Bagnet.”", "그게 이 물건의 쓸모 전부예요, 배그넷 부인.\""]], [["Mr. George produces his present, which is greeted with admiring leapings and clappings by the young family, and with a species of reverential admiration by Mr.", "조지 씨가 선물을 내놓자, 어린 식구들은 감탄하며 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰고 손뼉을 치며 맞이하고, 배그넷 씨는 일종의 경건한 찬탄으로 맞이한다."], ["Bagnet.", "\"임자.\""], ["“Old girl,” says Mr.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["Bagnet. “Tell him my opinion of it.”", "\"이 사람한테 이것에 대한 내 견해를 전해줘.\""]], [["“Why, it’s a wonder, George!”", "\"세상에, 이거 정말 굉장한 거잖아, 조지!\""], ["Mrs. Bagnet exclaims.", "배그넷 부인이 외친다."], ["“It’s the beautifullest thing that ever was seen!”", "\"이렇게 아름다운 건 난생처음 봐!\""]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["“My opinion.”", "\"그게 내 견해야.\""]], [["“It’s so pretty, George,” cries Mrs.", "\"어찌나 예쁜지, 조지.\""], ["Bagnet, turning it on all sides and holding it out at arm’s length, “that it seems too choice for me.”", "배그넷 부인이 이리저리 돌려보고 팔을 쭉 뻗어 들어 보며 외친다. \"나한테는 너무 과분할 만큼 곱구먼.\""]], [["“Bad!”", "\"틀렸어!\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Not my opinion.”", "\"그건 내 견해가 아니야.\""]], [["“But whatever it is, a hundred thousand thanks, old fellow,” says Mrs.", "\"하지만 어쨌든, 백만 번 고마워, 이 사람아.\""], ["Bagnet, her eyes sparkling with pleasure and her hand stretched out to him; “and though I have been a crossgrained soldier’s wife to you sometimes, George, we are as strong friends, I am sure, in reality, as ever can be.", "배그넷 부인이 기쁨에 눈을 반짝이며 그에게 손을 내밀며 말한다. \"그리고 내가 가끔은 자네한테 까칠한 군인 마누라 노릇을 했지만, 조지, 우린 진짜로는 더없이 단단한 친구지, 그건 분명해."], ["Now you shall fasten it on yourself, for good luck, if you will, George.”", "자, 이제 운이 따르라고 자네가 직접 채워주게, 그래줄 거지, 조지.\""]], [["The children close up to see it done, and Mr. Bagnet looks over young Woolwich’s head to see it done with an interest so maturely wooden, yet pleasantly childish, that Mrs.", "아이들이 그 광경을 보려고 바싹 다가서고, 배그넷 씨도 어린 울리치의 머리 너머로 그 광경을 지켜보는데, 그 관심이 어찌나 다 자란 어른답게 뻣뻣하면서도 흐뭇하게 아이 같은지, 배그넷 부인은 특유의 경쾌한 투로 웃음을 터뜨리며 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없다."], ["Bagnet cannot help laughing in her airy way and saying, “Oh, Lignum, Lignum, what a precious old chap you are!”", "\"어머, 리그넘, 리그넘, 당신은 어쩜 그리 귀여운 영감이람!\""], ["But the trooper fails to fasten the brooch.", "그러나 기병은 브로치를 채우는 데 실패한다."], ["His hand shakes, he is nervous, and it falls off.", "손이 떨리고, 그는 불안하고, 브로치는 떨어져버린다."], ["“Would any one believe this?”", "\"이거 참, 누가 믿겠어요?\""], ["says he, catching it as it drops and looking round.", "떨어지는 브로치를 잡아채며 그가 둘러보며 말한다."], ["“I am so out of sorts that I bungle at an easy job like this!”", "\"내가 이렇게 안절부절못해서, 이런 쉬운 일도 서투르게 망치다니!\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet concludes that for such a case there is no remedy like a pipe, and fastening the brooch herself in a twinkling, causes the trooper to be inducted into his usual snug place and the pipes to be got into action.", "배그넷 부인은 이런 경우에는 담배만 한 약이 없다고 결론짓고는, 눈 깜짝할 새에 브로치를 손수 채운 뒤, 기병을 늘 앉던 아늑한 자리에 모셔 앉히고 담뱃대를 가동시킨다."], ["“If that don’t bring you round, George,”", "\"그래도 기운이 안 나거든, 조지.\""], ["says she, “just throw your eye across here at your present now and then, and the two together MUST do it.”", "그녀가 말한다. \"이따금 여기 자네 선물 쪽으로 눈을 던져봐, 그럼 둘이 합쳐서 틀림없이 효험을 볼 테니까.\""]], [["“You ought to do it of yourself,”", "\"부인이 직접 채우셔야 마땅했죠.\""], ["George answers; “I know that very well, Mrs.", "조지가 대답한다. \"저도 그건 잘 알아요, 배그넷 부인."], ["Bagnet. I’ll tell you how, one way and another, the blues have got to be too many for me.", "이런저런 일로 우울이 너무 겹쳐 견디기 힘들어졌다고나 할까요."], ["Here was this poor lad.", "그 가엾은 녀석 일이 있었죠."], ["’Twas dull work to see him dying as he did, and not be able to help him.”", "그 애가 그렇게 죽어가는 걸 지켜보면서도 도와줄 수 없는 게, 참 답답한 노릇이었어요.\""]], [["“What do you mean, George?", "\"그게 무슨 소리야, 조지?"], ["You did help him.", "자넨 그 애를 도왔잖아."], ["You took him under your roof.”", "자네 지붕 아래 거둬줬으니.\""]], [["“I helped him so far, but that’s little.", "\"그만큼은 도왔죠, 하지만 그건 사소한 거예요."], ["I mean, Mrs. Bagnet, there he was, dying without ever having been taught much more than to know his right hand from his left.", "제 말은, 배그넷 부인, 그 애가 거기 누워서, 오른손과 왼손을 구별하는 것 말고는 더 배운 것도 별로 없이 죽어갔다는 거예요."], ["And he was too far gone to be helped out of that.”", "게다가 이미 너무 늦어서, 그 처지에서 건져낼 수도 없었고요.\""]], [["“Ah, poor creetur!”", "\"아아, 가엾은 것!\""], ["says Mrs. Bagnet.", "배그넷 부인이 말한다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그러고 나니.\""], ["says the trooper, not yet lighting his pipe, and passing his heavy hand over his hair, “that brought up Gridley in a man’s mind.", "기병이 아직 담뱃대에 불도 붙이지 않은 채 묵직한 손으로 머리칼을 쓸어 넘기며 말한다. \"그리들리가 떠오르더군요."], ["His was a bad case too, in a different way.", "그 사람 경우도 결이 다르긴 해도 딱한 처지였죠."], ["Then the two got mixed up in a man’s mind with a flinty old rascal who had to do with both.", "그러더니 그 둘이, 둘 다와 얽혔던 어느 매정한 늙은 악당과 머릿속에서 뒤섞이는 거예요."], ["And to think of that rusty carbine, stock and barrel, standing up on end in his corner, hard, indifferent, taking everything so evenly—it made flesh and blood tingle, I do assure you.”", "그리고 그 녹슨 카빈총이, 개머리판이며 총신이며, 그자의 구석에 똑바로 서서, 단단하고 무심하게, 모든 걸 그토록 한결같이 받아넘기는 꼴을 떠올리면—정말이지, 살과 피가 다 찌릿해졌어요.\""]], [["“My advice to you,”", "\"내 충고는.\""], ["returns Mrs.", "배그넷 부인이 대답한다."], ["Bagnet, “is to light your pipe and tingle that way.", "\"담뱃대에 불을 붙여서 그쪽으로 찌릿하라는 거야."], ["It’s wholesomer and comfortabler, and better for the health altogether.”", "그게 더 건강에 좋고 마음도 편하고, 어느 모로 보나 몸에 이로우니까.\""]], [["“You’re right,”", "\"옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["says the trooper, “and I’ll do it.”", "기병이 말한다. \"그렇게 하지요.\""]], [["So he does it, though still with an indignant gravity that impresses the young Bagnets, and even causes Mr. Bagnet to defer the ceremony of drinking Mrs.", "그래서 그는 그렇게 하지만, 여전히 분이 서린 엄숙함이 어려 있어 어린 배그넷들에게 인상을 남기고, 심지어 배그넷 씨가 배그넷 부인의 건강을 비는 건배 의식을 미루게까지 한다."], ["Bagnet’s health, always given by himself on these occasions in a speech of exemplary terseness. But the young ladies having composed what Mr.", "이 건배는 이런 자리마다 늘 그가 본보기로 삼을 만큼 간결한 연설로 올려온 것이다."], ["Bagnet is in the habit of calling “the mixtur,” and George’s pipe being now in a glow, Mr.", "그러나 어린 아가씨들이 배그넷 씨가 으레 \"혼합주\"라 부르는 것을 다 타고, 조지의 담뱃대도 이제 발갛게 달아오르자, 배그넷 씨는 그날 저녁의 건배사로 나아가는 것을 제 의무로 여긴다."], ["Bagnet considers it his duty to proceed to the toast of the evening. He addresses the assembled company in the following terms.", "그는 모인 일행에게 다음과 같은 말로 연설한다."]], [["“George.", "\"조지."], ["Woolwich.", "울리치."], ["Quebec.", "퀘벡."], ["Malta.", "몰타."], ["This is her birthday.", "오늘은 이 사람 생일이다."], ["Take a day’s march.", "하루를 꼬박 행군해봐라."], ["And you won’t find such another.", "그래도 이만한 사람은 못 찾을 거다."], ["Here’s towards her!”", "이 사람을 위하여!\""]], [["The toast having been drunk with enthusiasm, Mrs. Bagnet returns thanks in a neat address of corresponding brevity.", "건배를 열렬히 들이켠 뒤, 배그넷 부인은 그에 걸맞게 간결한 답사로 감사를 표한다."], ["This model composition is limited to the three words “And wishing yours!” which the old girl follows up with a nod at everybody in succession and a well-regulated swig of the mixture.", "이 본보기 같은 명문은 \"그쪽들도 잘되시길!\"이라는 세 마디로 그치고, 노모는 거기에 이어 모두에게 차례차례 고개를 끄덕이고는 혼합주를 절제 있게 한 모금 들이켠다."], ["This she again follows up, on the present occasion, by the wholly unexpected exclamation, “Here’s a man!”", "그러고는 이날 자리에서, 다시 이어, 전혀 뜻밖의 탄성을 내뱉는다. \"어머, 손님이네!\""]], [["Here IS a man, much to the astonishment of the little company, looking in at the parlour-door.", "손님이 있다. 작은 모임이 깜짝 놀라게도, 거실 문 안을 들여다보는 한 사내가 있다."], ["He is a sharp-eyed man—a quick keen man—and he takes in everybody’s look at him, all at once, individually and collectively, in a manner that stamps him a remarkable man.", "눈매가 날카로운 사내—재빠르고 예리한 사내—인데, 그는 모두가 자기를 바라보는 표정을, 단번에, 개개인 것이고 전체의 것이고 모조리 받아들이는데, 그 솜씨가 그를 비범한 사내임을 새겨두게 한다."]], [["“George,”", "\"조지.\""], ["says the man, nodding, “how do you find yourself?”", "사내가 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다. \"어떻게 지내나?\""]], [["“Why, it’s Bucket!”", "\"아니, 버킷이잖나!\""], ["cries Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 외친다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["says the man, coming in and closing the door.", "사내가 들어와 문을 닫으며 말한다."], ["“I was going down the street here when I happened to stop and look in at the musical instruments in the shop-window—a friend of mine is in want of a second-hand wiolinceller of a good tone—and I saw a party enjoying themselves, and I thought it was you in the corner; I thought I couldn’t be mistaken.", "\"이 거리를 내려가던 참에 어쩌다 멈춰서 가게 진열창의 악기들을 들여다봤지—친구 하나가 음색 좋은 중고 비올론첼로를 구하고 있어서 말이야—그러다 보니 일행이 즐겁게 어울리는 게 눈에 들어왔고, 저 구석에 있는 게 자네구나 싶었거든. 잘못 봤을 리 없다 싶었지."], ["How goes the world with you, George, at the present moment? Pretty smooth?", "요즘 자네는 세상살이가 어떤가, 조지?"], ["And with you, ma’am?", "그럭저럭 매끄러운가?"], ["And with you, governor?", "부인은요? 주인장은요?"], ["And Lord,”", "아이고.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, opening his arms, “here’s children too!", "버킷 씨가 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 말한다."], ["You may do anything with me if you only show me children.", "\"여기 아이들도 있네! 나한테는 아이들만 보여주면 무슨 짓이든 하게 만들 수 있어요."], ["Give us a kiss, my pets.", "입 좀 맞춰보자, 요 귀염둥이들."], ["No occasion to inquire who YOUR father and mother is.", "너희 아버지 어머니가 누군지는 물어볼 것도 없겠다."], ["Never saw such a likeness in my life!”", "평생 이렇게 빼닮은 건 본 적이 없어!\""]], [["Mr. Bucket, not unwelcome, has sat himself down next to Mr.", "버킷 씨는, 환영받지 못할 사람도 아닌지라, 조지 씨 옆에 앉아 퀘벡과 몰타를 무릎에 앉혔다."], ["George and taken Quebec and Malta on his knees.", "\"요 예쁜 것들.\" 버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“You pretty dears,” says Mr.", "\"한 번 더 뽀뽀해주렴."], ["Bucket, “give us another kiss; it’s the only thing I’m greedy in.", "내가 욕심내는 건 이것 하나뿐이란다."], ["Lord bless you, how healthy you look!", "아이고, 어쩜 이리 건강해 보일까!"], ["And what may be the ages of these two, ma’am?", "이 둘은 나이가 몇이나 됩니까, 부인?"], ["I should put ’em down at the figures of about eight and ten.”", "내가 보기엔 한 여덟 살, 열 살쯤 되겠는데요.\""]], [["“You’re very near, sir,”", "\"거의 맞히셨네요, 선생님.\""], ["says Mrs. Bagnet.", "배그넷 부인이 말한다."]], [["“I generally am near,” returns Mr.", "\"내가 보통 잘 맞히지요.\""], ["Bucket, “being so fond of children.", "버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"워낙 아이들을 좋아해서요."], ["A friend of mine has had nineteen of ’em, ma’am, all by one mother, and she’s still as fresh and rosy as the morning.", "친구 하나는 애가 열아홉이나 됩니다, 부인, 다 한 어머니한테서 났는데, 그 어머니가 아직도 아침처럼 싱싱하고 발그레해요."], ["Not so much so as yourself, but, upon my soul, she comes near you!", "부인만큼은 못해도, 정말이지 부인 못지않게 가깝게 갑니다!"], ["And what do you call these, my darling?”", "그래, 이건 뭐라고 부르지, 우리 귀염둥이?\""], ["pursues Mr. Bucket, pinching Malta’s cheeks.", "버킷 씨가 몰타의 볼을 꼬집으며 잇는다."], ["“These are peaches, these are.", "\"이건 복숭아네, 복숭아야."], ["Bless your heart!", "아이고 예뻐라!"], ["And what do you think about father?", "그래, 아빠는 어떻게 생각하니?"], ["Do you think father could recommend a second-hand wiolinceller of a good tone for Mr. Bucket’s friend, my dear?", "아빠가 버킷 아저씨 친구한테 음색 좋은 중고 비올론첼로를 추천해줄 수 있을 것 같아, 얘야?"], ["My name’s Bucket.", "내 이름은 버킷이란다."], ["Ain’t that a funny name?”", "우습지 않니, 그 이름?\""]], [["These blandishments have entirely won the family heart. Mrs.", "이 살가운 너스레가 온 식구의 마음을 완전히 사로잡았다."], ["Bagnet forgets the day to the extent of filling a pipe and a glass for Mr. Bucket and waiting upon him hospitably.", "배그넷 부인은 오늘이 무슨 날인지도 잊을 만큼, 버킷 씨에게 담뱃대를 채우고 잔을 채워주며 정성껏 대접한다."], ["She would be glad to receive so pleasant a character under any circumstances, but she tells him that as a friend of George’s she is particularly glad to see him this evening, for George has not been in his usual spirits.", "그녀는 어떤 사정에서든 이리도 유쾌한 인물을 맞으면 반가웠을 테지만, 조지의 친구이니 오늘 저녁에 만나뵙게 되어 특히 기쁘다고 그에게 말한다. 조지가 여느 때 같은 기분이 아니어서라는 것이다."]], [["“Not in his usual spirits?”", "\"여느 때 같은 기분이 아니라고요?\""], ["exclaims Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 외친다."], ["“Why, I never heard of such a thing!", "\"아니, 그런 소린 처음 듣는데요!"], ["What’s the matter, George?", "무슨 일인가, 조지?"], ["You don’t intend to tell me you’ve been out of spirits.", "설마 자네가 기분이 처져 있었다는 말은 아니겠지."], ["What should you be out of spirits for?", "자네가 처질 까닭이 어디 있어?"], ["You haven’t got anything on your mind, you know.”", "마음에 걸리는 게 있을 리도 없잖나, 안 그래.\""]], [["“Nothing particular,”", "\"별것 아닐세.\""], ["returns the trooper.", "기병이 대답한다."]], [["“I should think not,”", "\"그렇겠지.\""], ["rejoins Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 받는다."], ["“What could you have on your mind, you know!", "\"자네가 마음에 걸릴 게 뭐가 있겠나!"], ["And have these pets got anything on THEIR minds, eh?", "그래, 이 귀염둥이들은 마음에 걸리는 게 있을까, 응?"], ["Not they, but they’ll be upon the minds of some of the young fellows, some of these days, and make ’em precious low-spirited.", "없지, 하지만 언젠가는 이 아이들이 어느 젊은 녀석들 마음에 걸려서, 그 녀석들 기분을 아주 푹 가라앉히겠지요."], ["I ain’t much of a prophet, but I can tell you that, ma’am.”", "내가 예언자는 못 돼도, 그건 장담할 수 있습니다, 부인.\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet, quite charmed, hopes Mr. Bucket has a family of his own.", "배그넷 부인은 아주 흐뭇해하며, 버킷 씨도 제 가정이 있기를 바란다고 한다."]], [["“There, ma’am!”", "\"아이고, 부인!\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Would you believe it?", "\"믿어지세요?"], ["No, I haven’t.", "없어요, 저는."], ["My wife and a lodger constitute my family.", "아내와 하숙인 하나가 제 식구의 전부랍니다."], ["Mrs. Bucket is as fond of children as myself and as wishful to have ’em, but no.", "버킷 부인도 저만큼이나 아이들을 좋아하고 그만큼 갖고 싶어 하지만, 안 생기더군요."], ["So it is.", "그게 그래요."], ["Worldly goods are divided unequally, and man must not repine.", "세상의 재물은 고르지 않게 나뉘는 법이고, 사람은 그걸 두고 투덜대선 안 되지요."], ["What a very nice backyard, ma’am!", "참 좋은 뒷마당이네요, 부인!"], ["Any way out of that yard, now?”", "그런데 저 마당에서 빠져나가는 길이 따로 있나요?\""]], [["There is no way out of that yard.", "저 마당에서 빠져나가는 길은 없다."]], [["“Ain’t there really?”", "\"정말 없어요?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“I should have thought there might have been.", "\"있을 법도 한데 말이죠."], ["Well, I don’t know as I ever saw a backyard that took my fancy more.", "글쎄, 이만큼 내 마음에 든 뒷마당은 본 적이 없는 것 같네요."], ["Would you allow me to look at it?", "좀 봐도 될까요? 고맙습니다."], ["Thank you. No, I see there’s no way out.", "아니, 보니까 빠져나갈 길이 없군요."], ["But what a very good-proportioned yard it is!”", "그래도 참 균형이 잘 잡힌 마당이에요!\""]], [["Having cast his sharp eye all about it, Mr. Bucket returns to his chair next his friend Mr. George and pats Mr. George affectionately on the shoulder.", "마당을 날카로운 눈으로 두루 살펴본 뒤, 버킷 씨는 친구 조지 씨 옆 의자로 돌아와 조지 씨의 어깨를 다정하게 토닥인다."]], [["“How are your spirits now, George?”", "\"이제 기분이 어떤가, 조지?\""]], [["“All right now,”", "\"이제 괜찮네.\""], ["returns the trooper.", "기병이 대답한다."]], [["“That’s your sort!”", "\"그래야지!\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Why should you ever have been otherwise?", "\"애초에 안 좋을 까닭이 어디 있겠나?"], ["A man of your fine figure and constitution has no right to be out of spirits.", "자네처럼 훤칠한 체격에 건장한 사내가 기분이 처질 권리는 없어."], ["That ain’t a chest to be out of spirits, is it, ma’am?", "이게 기분이 처질 가슴팍이오, 부인?"], ["And you haven’t got anything on your mind, you know, George; what could you have on your mind!”", "게다가 자넨 마음에 걸리는 게 없잖나, 조지. 자네가 마음에 걸릴 게 뭐가 있겠어!\""]], [["Somewhat harping on this phrase, considering the extent and variety of his conversational powers, Mr. Bucket twice or thrice repeats it to the pipe he lights, and with a listening face that is particularly his own.", "그 말솜씨의 폭과 다채로움을 생각하면 다소 이 한마디에 자꾸 매달리는 셈인데, 버킷 씨는 불을 붙이는 담뱃대에 대고 그 말을 두세 번 되뇌는데, 그 표정은 무언가에 귀를 기울이는 듯 그만의 독특한 얼굴이다."], ["But the sun of his sociality soon recovers from this brief eclipse and shines again.", "그러나 그의 사교성이라는 태양은 이 짧은 일식에서 곧 회복되어 다시 빛난다."]], [["“And this is brother, is it, my dears?” says Mr.", "\"그래, 이 애가 오빠로구나, 얘들아?\""], ["Bucket, referring to Quebec and Malta for information on the subject of young Woolwich.", "버킷 씨가 어린 울리치에 관한 정보를 얻으려 퀘벡과 몰타에게 물으며 말한다."], ["“And a nice brother he is—half-brother I mean to say.", "\"참 멋진 오빠야—그러니까 배다른 오빠 말이다."], ["For he’s too old to be your boy, ma’am.”", "부인이 낳은 아들이라기엔 너무 나이가 들었으니까요.\""]], [["“I can certify at all events that he is not anybody else’s,”", "\"적어도 남의 아들이 아니라는 것만은 보증할 수 있답니다.\""], ["returns Mrs. Bagnet, laughing.", "배그넷 부인이 웃으며 대답한다."]], [["“Well, you do surprise me!", "\"이거 놀랍군요!"], ["Yet he’s like you, there’s no denying.", "하기야 부인을 닮긴 했어요, 그건 부인할 수 없겠네요."], ["Lord, he’s wonderfully like you!", "아이고, 어쩜 이리 부인을 빼닮았을까!"], ["But about what you may call the brow, you know, THERE his father comes out!” Mr.", "그래도 이마라고 부를 만한 데를 보면, 거기서 아버지가 드러납니다!\""], ["Bucket compares the faces with one eye shut up, while Mr. Bagnet smokes in stolid satisfaction.", "버킷 씨는 한쪽 눈을 감고 두 얼굴을 견주어보고, 한편 배그넷 씨는 무덤덤한 만족 속에 담배를 피운다."]], [["This is an opportunity for Mrs. Bagnet to inform him that the boy is George’s godson.", "이때야말로 배그넷 부인이 그에게 그 소년이 조지의 대자라는 걸 알릴 기회다."]], [["“George’s godson, is he?”", "\"조지의 대자라고요?\""], ["rejoins Mr. Bucket with extreme cordiality.", "버킷 씨가 더없이 살갑게 받는다."], ["“I must shake hands over again with George’s godson.", "\"조지의 대자라면 다시 한번 악수를 해야지."], ["Godfather and godson do credit to one another.", "대부와 대자가 서로 빛을 더해주는군요."], ["And what do you intend to make of him, ma’am?", "그래, 이 아이를 어떻게 키우실 작정입니까, 부인?"], ["Does he show any turn for any musical instrument?”", "무슨 악기에든 소질을 보이나요?\""]], [["Mr. Bagnet suddenly interposes, “Plays the fife.", "배그넷 씨가 불쑥 끼어든다."], ["Beautiful.”", "\"피리를 분다오. 멋지게.\""]], [["“Would you believe it, governor,” says Mr.", "\"이거 믿어지십니까, 주인장.\""], ["Bucket, struck by the coincidence, “that when I was a boy I played the fife myself?", "그 우연에 마음이 동한 버킷 씨가 말한다. \"제가 어릴 적엔 저도 피리를 불었답니다?"], ["Not in a scientific way, as I expect he does, but by ear.", "이 아이처럼 정식으로 배운 건 아니고, 그저 귀로 익혔지만요."], ["Lord bless you!", "아이고!"], ["‘British Grenadiers’—there’s a tune to warm an Englishman up!", "'영국 척탄병'—그건 영국인의 피를 끓게 하는 곡이지요!"], ["COULD you give us ‘British Grenadiers,’ my fine fellow?”", "우리한테 '영국 척탄병'을 한 곡 불어줄 수 있겠니, 멋진 친구야?\""]], [["Nothing could be more acceptable to the little circle than this call upon young Woolwich, who immediately fetches his fife and performs the stirring melody, during which performance Mr.", "이 어린 모임에 어린 울리치를 향한 이 청보다 더 반가운 일은 없을 것이니, 아이는 곧장 피리를 가져와 그 흥겨운 가락을 연주한다."], ["Bucket, much enlivened, beats time and never fails to come in sharp with the burden, “British Gra-a-anadeers!”", "그 연주 내내 버킷 씨는 한껏 신이 나 박자를 맞추고, 후렴 \"영국 처어어언탄병!\"을 한 번도 놓치지 않고 야무지게 받아넘긴다."], ["In short, he shows so much musical taste that Mr. Bagnet actually takes his pipe from his lips to express his conviction that he is a singer.", "요컨대 그는 음악적 취향을 어찌나 한껏 드러내는지, 배그넷 씨는 정말로 담뱃대를 입에서 떼고는 그가 가수임에 틀림없다는 확신을 표한다."], ["Mr. Bucket receives the harmonious impeachment so modestly, confessing how that he did once chaunt a little, for the expression of the feelings of his own bosom, and with no presumptuous idea of entertaining his friends, that he is asked to sing.", "버킷 씨는 그 가락에 얽힌 혐의를 어찌나 겸손하게 받아들이는지—한때 제 가슴속 감정을 풀어내려 노래를 좀 흥얼거린 적은 있으나 벗들을 즐겁게 해드릴 만하다는 주제넘은 생각은 추호도 없었다고 털어놓으며—급기야 노래를 청받는다."], ["Not to be behindhand in the sociality of the evening, he complies and gives them “Believe Me, if All Those Endearing Young Charms.” This ballad, he informs Mrs.", "그날 저녁의 흥겨움에 뒤처지지 않으려고, 그는 응하여 \"그대의 사랑스러운 젊은 매력이 모두 사라진대도 나를 믿어주오\"를 들려준다."], ["Bagnet, he considers to have been his most powerful ally in moving the heart of Mrs. Bucket when a maiden, and inducing her to approach the altar—Mr.", "이 발라드야말로 버킷 부인이 처녀였을 적 그녀의 마음을 움직여 그녀를 제단으로 다가오게 하는 데—버킷 씨 본인의 표현으로는 \"각오를 굳히게\""], ["Bucket’s own words are “to come up to the scratch.”", "하는 데—가장 강력한 아군이었다고 그는 배그넷 부인에게 일러준다."]], [["This sparkling stranger is such a new and agreeable feature in the evening that Mr. George, who testified no great emotions of pleasure on his entrance, begins, in spite of himself, to be rather proud of him.", "이 반짝이는 손님은 그날 저녁의 새롭고 유쾌한 면모가 되어, 그가 들어설 때만 해도 별다른 기쁨의 기색을 보이지 않던 조지 씨조차 저도 모르게 그를 자못 자랑스러워하기 시작한다."], ["He is so friendly, is a man of so many resources, and so easy to get on with, that it is something to have made him known there. Mr.", "그는 어찌나 다정하고, 어찌나 재간이 많고, 어찌나 어울리기 편한지, 그를 여기 소개한 것이 보람된 일로 여겨질 정도다."], ["Bagnet becomes, after another pipe, so sensible of the value of his acquaintance that he solicits the honour of his company on the old girl’s next birthday.", "배그넷 씨는 담배를 한 대 더 피운 뒤, 그를 알게 된 것의 가치를 절실히 깨달아, 노모의 다음 생일에 그가 함께해주는 영광을 청한다."], ["If anything can more closely cement and consolidate the esteem which Mr. Bucket has formed for the family, it is the discovery of the nature of the occasion.", "버킷 씨가 이 가족에게 품게 된 존경을 더욱 단단히 굳히고 다질 만한 것이 있다면, 그건 바로 이 자리가 무슨 날인지 알게 된 것이다."], ["He drinks to Mrs. Bagnet with a warmth approaching to rapture, engages himself for that day twelvemonth more than thankfully, makes a memorandum of the day in a large black pocket-book with a girdle to it, and breathes a hope that Mrs.", "그는 환희에 가까운 따스함으로 배그넷 부인을 위해 잔을 들고, 일 년 뒤 그날을 위해 더없이 고맙게 약속하고, 허리띠가 달린 큼직한 검정 수첩에 그날을 적어넣고, 그때까지는 버킷 부인과 배그넷 부인이 어찌 보면 자매 같은 사이가 되기를 바란다고 말한다."], ["Bucket and Mrs. Bagnet may before then become, in a manner, sisters.", "그 자신의 말마따나, 사사로운 정 없는 공적인 삶이 다 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["As he says himself, what is public life without private ties? He is in his humble way a public man, but it is not in that sphere that he finds happiness.", "그도 변변찮으나마 공직에 몸담은 사람이지만, 그가 행복을 찾는 곳은 그 영역이 아니다."], ["No, it must be sought within the confines of domestic bliss.", "아니, 행복은 가정의 단란함이라는 울타리 안에서 찾아야 마땅하다."]], [["It is natural, under these circumstances, that he, in his turn, should remember the friend to whom he is indebted for so promising an acquaintance.", "이런 상황에서 그가 그렇게 유망한 친분을 맺게 해준 벗을 이번에는 떠올리는 것도 자연스러운 일이다. 그리고 그는 그를 떠올린다."], ["And he does. He keeps very close to him.", "그는 그의 곁에 바짝 붙어 있다."], ["Whatever the subject of the conversation, he keeps a tender eye upon him.", "대화의 주제가 무엇이든, 그는 그에게 다정한 눈길을 떼지 않는다."], ["He waits to walk home with him.", "그는 그와 함께 집까지 걸어가려고 기다린다."], ["He is interested in his very boots and observes even them attentively as Mr. George sits smoking cross-legged in the chimney-corner.", "그는 그의 장화에까지 관심을 두어, 조지 씨가 굴뚝 옆에서 책상다리를 하고 앉아 담배를 피우는 동안 그 장화마저 유심히 살핀다."]], [["At length Mr. George rises to depart.", "마침내 조지 씨가 떠나려고 일어선다."], ["At the same moment Mr. Bucket, with the secret sympathy of friendship, also rises.", "같은 순간, 버킷 씨도 우정의 은밀한 공감으로 함께 일어선다."], ["He dotes upon the children to the last and remembers the commission he has undertaken for an absent friend.", "그는 끝까지 아이들을 어여삐 여기고, 자리에 없는 친구를 위해 맡은 부탁을 잊지 않는다."]], [["“Respecting that second-hand wiolinceller, governor—could you recommend me such a thing?”", "\"그 중고 비올론첼로 말인데요, 주인장—그런 걸 하나 추천해주실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Scores,”", "\"수십 개도 됩니다.\""], ["says Mr. Bagnet.", "배그넷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I am obliged to you,”", "\"감사합니다.\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket, squeezing his hand.", "버킷 씨가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 대답한다."], ["“You’re a friend in need. A good tone, mind you!", "\"어려울 때 도와주는 친구가 진짜 친구지요."], ["My friend is a regular dab at it.", "음색이 좋아야 해요, 명심하시고!"], ["Ecod, he saws away at Mozart and Handel and the rest of the big-wigs like a thorough workman.", "제 친구가 그쪽으로는 보통 솜씨가 아니거든요. 정말이지, 모차르트며 헨델이며 그 거장들을 영락없는 장인처럼 슥슥 켜낸답니다."], ["And you needn’t,” says Mr.", "그리고,\""], ["Bucket in a considerate and private voice, “you needn’t commit yourself to too low a figure, governor.", "버킷 씨가 사려 깊고 은근한 목소리로 말한다. \"값을 너무 낮게 부르실 필요는 없어요, 주인장."], ["I don’t want to pay too large a price for my friend, but I want you to have your proper percentage and be remunerated for your loss of time.", "제 친구한테 너무 큰돈을 치르게 하고 싶진 않지만, 주인장도 마땅한 이문을 챙기고 들인 시간값을 받으셔야지요. 그게 공평한 거니까요."], ["That is but fair. Every man must live, and ought to it.”", "누구나 먹고살아야 하고, 또 마땅히 그래야지요.\""]], [["Mr. Bagnet shakes his head at the old girl to the effect that they have found a jewel of price.", "배그넷 씨가 노모를 향해 고개를 젓는데, 값진 보배를 하나 찾아냈다는 뜻이다."]], [["“Suppose I was to give you a look in, say, at half arter ten to-morrow morning.", "\"그럼 제가 가령 내일 아침 열 시 반쯤 들러볼까요?"], ["Perhaps you could name the figures of a few wiolincellers of a good tone?”", "음색 좋은 비올론첼로 몇 개의 값을 일러주실 수 있겠지요?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["Nothing easier. Mr.", "그보다 쉬운 일은 없다."], ["and Mrs. Bagnet both engage to have the requisite information ready and even hint to each other at the practicability of having a small stock collected there for approval.", "배그넷 부부는 둘 다 필요한 정보를 마련해두기로 약속하고, 심지어 검토하시라고 작은 재고를 그쪽에 좀 모아두는 것도 해볼 만하다고 서로 슬쩍 귀띔까지 한다."]], [["“Thank you,” says Mr.", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["Bucket, “thank you.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["Good night, ma’am.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["Good night, governor.", "안녕히 주무세요, 부인."], ["Good night, darlings.", "안녕히 주무세요, 주인장."], ["I am much obliged to you for one of the pleasantest evenings I ever spent in my life.”", "잘 자라, 귀염둥이들. 평생 보낸 가장 즐거운 저녁의 하나를 누리게 해주셔서 정말 감사합니다.\""]], [["They, on the contrary, are much obliged to him for the pleasure he has given them in his company; and so they part with many expressions of goodwill on both sides.", "그들은 그들대로, 그가 함께해준 즐거움에 대해 그에게 큰 신세를 졌다고 한다. 그래서 양쪽 모두 숱한 호의의 말을 주고받으며 헤어진다."], ["“Now George, old boy,” says Mr.", "\"자, 조지, 이 사람아.\""], ["Bucket, taking his arm at the shop-door, “come along!”", "가게 문 앞에서 그의 팔을 잡으며 버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["As they go down the little street and the Bagnets pause for a minute looking after them, Mrs.", "\"가세!\""], ["Bagnet remarks to the worthy Lignum that Mr. Bucket “almost clings to George like, and seems to be really fond of him.”", "그들이 작은 거리를 내려가고, 배그넷 부부가 잠시 멈춰 그 뒷모습을 바라보는 동안, 배그넷 부인은 의젓한 리그넘에게 버킷 씨가 \"조지한테 거의 들러붙다시피 하는 게, 정말로 그를 좋아하는 것 같다\"고 한마디 한다."]], [["The neighbouring streets being narrow and ill-paved, it is a little inconvenient to walk there two abreast and arm in arm. Mr.", "근방의 거리들은 좁고 길이 험해서, 둘이 나란히 팔짱을 끼고 걷기에는 좀 불편하다."], ["George therefore soon proposes to walk singly. But Mr.", "그래서 조지 씨가 곧 한 줄로 걷자고 제안한다."], ["Bucket, who cannot make up his mind to relinquish his friendly hold, replies, “Wait half a minute, George.", "그러나 정다운 팔짱을 놓을 마음이 좀처럼 서지 않는 버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"잠깐만, 조지."], ["I should wish to speak to you first.”", "먼저 자네한테 할 말이 있어.\""], ["Immediately afterwards, he twists him into a public-house and into a parlour, where he confronts him and claps his own back against the door.", "그러고는 곧장 그를 비틀어 어느 술집 안으로, 거실 안으로 데려가서는, 그와 마주 선 채 제 등을 문에 척 붙인다."]], [["“Now, George,” says Mr.", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["Bucket, “duty is duty, and friendship is friendship.", "버킷 씨가 말한다. \"의무는 의무고, 우정은 우정이지."], ["I never want the two to clash if I can help it.", "난 어쩔 수 없는 게 아니라면 이 둘이 부딪치는 걸 결코 바라지 않아."], ["I have endeavoured to make things pleasant to-night, and I put it to you whether I have done it or not.", "오늘 저녁 난 일을 즐겁게 만들려고 애썼고, 내가 그렇게 했는지 안 했는지는 자네 판단에 맡기지."], ["You must consider yourself in custody, George.”", "자네는 이제 체포된 몸이라고 여겨야겠네, 조지.\""]], [["“Custody?", "\"체포?"], ["What for?”", "무슨 죄로?\""], ["returns the trooper, thunderstruck.", "기병이 벼락 맞은 듯 되묻는다."]], [["“Now, George,” says Mr.", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["Bucket, urging a sensible view of the case upon him with his fat forefinger, “duty, as you know very well, is one thing, and conversation is another.", "버킷 씨가 통통한 집게손가락으로 사태를 분별 있게 바라보라고 그를 다그치며 말한다. \"자네도 잘 알다시피, 의무는 의무고 잡담은 잡담이야."], ["It’s my duty to inform you that any observations you may make will be liable to be used against you.", "자네가 무슨 말을 하든 그것이 자네한테 불리하게 쓰일 수 있다는 걸 알리는 게 내 의무지."], ["Therefore, George, be careful what you say.", "그러니, 조지, 말조심해."], ["You don’t happen to have heard of a murder?”", "혹시 살인 사건 얘기를 들은 적 있나?\""]], [["“Murder!”", "\"살인이라니!\""]], [["“Now, George,” says Mr.", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["Bucket, keeping his forefinger in an impressive state of action, “bear in mind what I’ve said to you.", "버킷 씨가 집게손가락을 인상적으로 놀린 채 말한다. \"내가 한 말을 명심해."], ["I ask you nothing.", "난 자네한테 아무것도 묻지 않아."], ["You’ve been in low spirits this afternoon.", "자네는 오늘 오후 내내 기분이 처져 있었지."], ["I say, you don’t happen to have heard of a murder?”", "다시 묻는데, 혹시 살인 사건 얘기를 들은 적 있나?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["Where has there been a murder?”", "어디서 살인이 났는데?\""]], [["“Now, George,” says Mr.", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["Bucket, “don’t you go and commit yourself.", "버킷 씨가 말한다. \"괜히 불리한 말을 하지 말게."], ["I’m a-going to tell you what I want you for.", "자네를 왜 찾는지 내가 일러주지."], ["There has been a murder in Lincoln’s Inn Fields—gentleman of the name of Tulkinghorn.", "링컨스 인 필즈에서 살인이 있었어—토킹혼이라는 이름의 신사가."], ["He was shot last night.", "어젯밤 총에 맞았지."], ["I want you for that.”", "그 일로 자네를 찾는 거야.\""]], [["The trooper sinks upon a seat behind him, and great drops start out upon his forehead, and a deadly pallor overspreads his face.", "기병은 뒤에 있던 의자에 털썩 주저앉고, 이마에는 굵은 땀방울이 솟아나며, 죽은 듯한 창백함이 얼굴을 뒤덮는다."]], [["“Bucket!", "\"버킷!"], ["It’s not possible that Mr. Tulkinghorn has been killed and that you suspect ME?”", "토킹혼 씨가 살해당했고, 자네가 나를 의심한다는 게 말이 되나?\""]], [["“George,” returns Mr.", "\"조지.\""], ["Bucket, keeping his forefinger going, “it is certainly possible, because it’s the case.", "버킷 씨가 집게손가락을 계속 놀리며 대답한다. \"확실히 말이 되지, 실제로 그러니까."], ["This deed was done last night at ten o’clock.", "이 일은 어젯밤 열 시에 벌어졌어."], ["Now, you know where you were last night at ten o’clock, and you’ll be able to prove it, no doubt.”", "그래, 자네는 어젯밤 열 시에 어디 있었는지 알 테고, 의심할 바 없이 그걸 입증할 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“Last night!", "\"어젯밤!"], ["Last night?”", "어젯밤이라고?\""], ["repeats the trooper thoughtfully.", "기병이 골똘히 되뇐다."], ["Then it flashes upon him.", "그러더니 퍼뜩 떠오른다."], ["“Why, great heaven, I was there last night!”", "\"이런, 맙소사, 나 어젯밤 거기 있었어!\""]], [["“So I have understood, George,”", "\"나도 그렇게 알고 있어, 조지.\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket with great deliberation.", "버킷 씨가 아주 신중하게 대답한다."], ["“So I have understood.", "\"나도 그렇게 알고 있지."], ["Likewise you’ve been very often there.", "마찬가지로 자네는 거기 자주 갔어."], ["You’ve been seen hanging about the place, and you’ve been heard more than once in a wrangle with him, and it’s possible—I don’t say it’s certainly so, mind you, but it’s possible—that he may have been heard to call you a threatening, murdering, dangerous fellow.”", "그 근처를 어슬렁대는 게 목격됐고, 그와 언쟁을 벌이는 게 한두 번 들리지도 않았어. 그리고 어쩌면—분명 그렇다는 말은 아냐, 명심하게, 하지만 어쩌면—그가 자네더러 위협적이고 살기 어린 위험한 놈이라고 부르는 게 들렸을지도 몰라.\""]], [["The trooper gasps as if he would admit it all if he could speak.", "기병은 말을 할 수만 있다면 그걸 죄다 인정하기라도 할 듯 숨을 헐떡인다."]], [["“Now, George,” continues Mr.", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["Bucket, putting his hat upon the table with an air of business rather in the upholstery way than otherwise, “my wish is, as it has been all the evening, to make things pleasant.", "버킷 씨가 모자를 가구상이나 할 법한 사무적인 태도로 탁자에 내려놓으며 잇는다. \"내 바람은, 오늘 저녁 내내 그래왔듯이, 일을 즐겁게 만드는 거야."], ["I tell you plainly there’s a reward out, of a hundred guineas, offered by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.", "솔직히 말하지, 현상금이 걸렸어, 백 기니짜리로, 레스터 데드록 준남작 경이 내건 거지."], ["You and me have always been pleasant together; but I have got a duty to discharge; and if that hundred guineas is to be made, it may as well be made by me as any other man.", "자네와 난 늘 즐겁게 지내온 사이지만, 난 다해야 할 의무가 있고, 그 백 기니를 누가 챙겨야 한다면, 다른 누구 못지않게 내가 챙기는 편이 나아."], ["On all of which accounts, I should hope it was clear to you that I must have you, and that I’m damned if I don’t have you.", "이 모든 까닭에서, 내가 자네를 잡아야 한다는 게, 그리고 잡지 않으면 천벌을 받을 거라는 게 자네한테도 분명히 보였으면 해."], ["Am I to call in any assistance, or is the trick done?”", "내가 누구 도움을 부를까, 아니면 일을 곱게 끝낼까?\""]], [["Mr. George has recovered himself and stands up like a soldier.", "조지 씨는 정신을 가다듬고 군인처럼 일어선다."], ["“Come,”", "\"갑시다.\""], ["he says; “I am ready.”", "그가 말한다. \"준비됐소.\""]], [["“George,”", "\"조지.\""], ["continues Mr.", "버킷 씨가 잇는다."], ["Bucket, “wait a bit!”", "\"잠깐만!\""], ["With his upholsterer manner, as if the trooper were a window to be fitted up, he takes from his pocket a pair of handcuffs.", "마치 기병이 손볼 창문이라도 되는 양 그 가구상 같은 태도로, 그는 호주머니에서 수갑 한 벌을 꺼낸다."], ["“This is a serious charge, George, and such is my duty.”", "\"이건 중대한 혐의야, 조지, 그리고 이게 내 의무지.\""]], [["The trooper flushes angrily and hesitates a moment, but holds out his two hands, clasped together, and says, “There!", "기병은 성이 나 얼굴이 벌게져 잠시 망설이지만, 두 손을 맞붙여 내밀고는 말한다. \"자!"], ["Put them on!”", "채우게!\""]], [["Mr. Bucket adjusts them in a moment.", "버킷 씨가 단번에 그것을 채운다."], ["“How do you find them?", "\"어떤가? 편한가?"], ["Are they comfortable? If not, say so, for I wish to make things as pleasant as is consistent with my duty, and I’ve got another pair in my pocket.”", "안 그러면 말하게, 난 내 의무에 어긋나지 않는 선에서 최대한 일을 편하게 만들고 싶고, 호주머니에 한 벌이 더 있으니까.\""], ["This remark he offers like a most respectable tradesman anxious to execute an order neatly and to the perfect satisfaction of his customer.", "이 말을 그는 주문을 깔끔하게, 손님이 완벽히 만족하도록 처리하려 애쓰는 더없이 점잖은 장사꾼처럼 건넨다."], ["“They’ll do as they are?", "\"이대로 괜찮겠나?"], ["Very well!", "아주 좋아!"], ["Now, you see, George”—he takes a cloak from a corner and begins adjusting it about the trooper’s neck—“I was mindful of your feelings when I come out, and brought this on purpose.", "자, 보게, 조지\"—그는 구석에서 외투 하나를 가져와 기병의 목둘레에 둘러주기 시작한다—\"내가 나올 때 자네 기분을 헤아려서, 이걸 일부러 가져왔지."], ["There!", "자!"], ["Who’s the wiser?”", "누가 알아채겠나?\""]], [["“Only I,”", "\"나만 알지.\""], ["returns the trooper, “but as I know it, do me one more good turn and pull my hat over my eyes.”", "기병이 대답한다. \"하지만 내가 아는 일이니, 한 번만 더 호의를 베풀어서 모자를 눈까지 끌어내려주게.\""]], [["“Really, though!", "\"이거 참, 정말로?"], ["Do you mean it?", "진심인가? 안됐잖아."], ["Ain’t it a pity? It looks so.”", "그렇게 보이니까.\""]], [["“I can’t look chance men in the face with these things on,” Mr.", "\"이런 걸 차고는 지나가는 사람들 얼굴을 똑바로 볼 수가 없어.\""], ["George hurriedly replies.", "조지 씨가 다급히 대답한다."], ["“Do, for God’s sake, pull my hat forward.”", "\"제발, 부디 모자를 앞으로 끌어내려주게.\""]], [["So strongly entreated, Mr. Bucket complies, puts his own hat on, and conducts his prize into the streets, the trooper marching on as steadily as usual, though with his head less erect, and Mr.", "그토록 간곡히 청하니, 버킷 씨는 응하여, 제 모자를 쓰고는 제 노획물을 거리로 인도한다."], ["Bucket steering him with his elbow over the crossings and up the turnings.", "기병은 여느 때처럼 꿋꿋이 행진하되 다만 고개는 덜 꼿꼿하고, 버킷 씨는 팔꿈치로 그를 이끌어 길목을 건너고 모퉁이를 돈다."]], [["Esther’s Narrative\nIt happened that when I came home from Deal I found a note from Caddy Jellyby (as we always continued to call her), informing me that her health, which had been for some time very delicate, was worse and that she would be more glad than she could tell me if I would go to see her.", "딜에서 집으로 돌아왔을 때 마침 캐디 젤리비(우리는 늘 그녀를 이렇게 불렀습니다)에게서 쪽지가 한 장 와 있었습니다. 얼마 전부터 몹시 약해진 그녀의 건강이 더 나빠졌으니, 와서 봐준다면 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 기쁘겠다는 내용이었습니다."], ["It was a note of a few lines, written from the couch on which she lay and enclosed to me in another from her husband, in which he seconded her entreaty with much solicitude.", "몇 줄짜리 쪽지로, 그녀가 누워 있는 긴 의자에서 쓴 것이었고, 남편이 쓴 다른 편지에 동봉되어 있었는데, 남편은 그 안에서 무척 애틋하게 아내의 부탁을 거들었습니다."], ["Caddy was now the mother, and I the godmother, of such a poor little baby—such a tiny old-faced mite, with a countenance that seemed to be scarcely anything but cap-border, and a little lean, long-fingered hand, always clenched under its chin.", "캐디는 이제 그토록 가여운 작은 아기의 어머니였고, 나는 그 아기의 대모였습니다. 모자 테두리 말고는 얼굴이라곤 거의 없어 보이는 조그맣고 늙은 티가 나는 핏덩이로, 가늘고 긴 손가락이 달린 작은 손을 늘 턱 아래 움켜쥐고 있었습니다."], ["It would lie in this attitude all day, with its bright specks of eyes open, wondering (as I used to imagine) how it came to be so small and weak.", "아기는 하루 종일 이런 자세로 누워, 반짝이는 점 같은 두 눈을 뜨고서, (나는 늘 이렇게 상상했습니다만) 어쩌다 자기가 이렇게 작고 약하게 태어났는지 궁금해하는 듯했습니다."], ["Whenever it was moved it cried, but at all other times it was so patient that the sole desire of its life appeared to be to lie quiet and think.", "자리를 옮길 때마다 울었지만, 그 밖의 모든 순간에는 어찌나 참을성이 많은지, 평생의 유일한 소망이 가만히 누워 생각하는 것인 듯싶었습니다."], ["It had curious little dark veins in its face and curious little dark marks under its eyes like faint remembrances of poor Caddy’s inky days, and altogether, to those who were not used to it, it was quite a piteous little sight.", "얼굴에는 묘한 작은 검은 핏줄이 어렸고, 눈 밑에는 가여운 캐디가 잉크에 절어 지내던 나날의 희미한 기억 같은 묘하고 작은 검은 자국이 있어서, 통틀어 보면 익숙하지 않은 사람에게는 참으로 가엾고 애처로운 광경이었습니다."]], [["But it was enough for Caddy that SHE was used to it.", "하지만 캐디로서는 자신이 그 아기에게 익숙하다는 것으로 충분했습니다."], ["The projects with which she beguiled her illness, for little Esther’s education, and little Esther’s marriage, and even for her own old age as the grandmother of little Esther’s little Esthers, was so prettily expressive of devotion to this pride of her life that I should be tempted to recall some of them but for the timely remembrance that I am getting on irregularly as it is.", "병석에 누워 그녀가 시름을 달래며 세운 계획들, 어린 에스더의 교육과 어린 에스더의 결혼, 심지어 어린 에스더가 낳을 어린 에스더들의 할머니가 될 자신의 노년에 관한 계획들은, 이 생애의 자랑거리를 향한 헌신을 어찌나 어여쁘게 드러내던지, 나도 그중 몇 가지를 옮겨놓고 싶은 마음이 들 정도였습니다. 다만 안 그래도 이야기가 두서없이 흘러가고 있다는 걸 때맞춰 떠올린 덕분에 그러지 않았을 뿐입니다."]], [["To return to the letter.", "다시 편지 이야기로 돌아가지요."], ["Caddy had a superstition about me which had been strengthening in her mind ever since that night long ago when she had lain asleep with her head in my lap.", "캐디는 나에 대해 일종의 미신 같은 믿음을 품고 있었는데, 오래전 그날 밤 내 무릎에 머리를 누이고 잠들었던 이래로 그 믿음이 마음속에서 점점 굳어졌습니다."], ["She almost—I think I must say quite—believed that I did her good whenever I was near her.", "그녀는 내가 곁에 있기만 하면 자기에게 좋은 일이 생긴다고 거의—사실 완전히라고 해야겠지요—믿었습니다."], ["Now although this was such a fancy of the affectionate girl’s that I am almost ashamed to mention it, still it might have all the force of a fact when she was really ill.", "이것이 그 다정한 아가씨의 한낱 공상에 지나지 않아 입에 올리기조차 부끄럽긴 하지만, 그녀가 정말로 아플 때라면 그것이 사실만큼의 힘을 발휘할 수도 있는 노릇이었습니다."], ["Therefore I set off to Caddy, with my guardian’s consent, post-haste; and she and Prince made so much of me that there never was anything like it.", "그래서 나는 후견인의 허락을 받아 부랴부랴 캐디에게 달려갔고, 그녀와 프린스가 어찌나 나를 반기던지 그런 환대는 어디에도 없을 정도였습니다."]], [["Next day I went again to sit with her, and next day I went again.", "이튿날 나는 다시 그녀 곁을 지키러 갔고, 그다음 날도 또 갔습니다."], ["It was a very easy journey, for I had only to rise a little earlier in the morning, and keep my accounts, and attend to housekeeping matters before leaving home.", "아주 수월한 길이었으니, 아침에 조금만 일찍 일어나 장부를 정리하고 집안일을 챙긴 뒤 집을 나서기만 하면 되었기 때문입니다."]], [["But when I had made these three visits, my guardian said to me, on my return at night, “Now, little woman, little woman, this will never do.", "그런데 이렇게 세 번째 다녀오자, 밤에 돌아온 나에게 후견인이 말했습니다. \"자, 꼬마 부인, 꼬마 부인, 이래서는 안 되겠어."], ["Constant dropping will wear away a stone, and constant coaching will wear out a Dame Durden.", "낙숫물이 끊임없이 떨어지면 돌도 닳는 법이고, 마차를 끊임없이 타다 보면 더든 아주머니도 닳아 없어지겠구나."], ["We will go to London for a while and take possession of our old lodgings.”", "우리 한동안 런던에 가서 옛 하숙집을 차지하고 지내자꾸나.\""]], [["“Not for me, dear guardian,”", "\"저 때문이라면 그러실 것 없어요, 후견인.\""], ["said I, “for I never feel tired,”", "내가 말했습니다. \"저는 한 번도 피곤한 줄 모르거든요.\""], ["which was strictly true.", "이건 한 치도 거짓이 아니었습니다."], ["I was only too happy to be in such request.", "나는 그렇게 필요한 사람이 된 것이 그저 더없이 기쁠 따름이었습니다."]], [["“For me then,”", "\"그럼 나를 위해서다.\""], ["returned my guardian, “or for Ada, or for both of us.", "후견인이 받았습니다. \"아니면 에이다를 위해서거나, 우리 둘 다를 위해서지."], ["It is somebody’s birthday to-morrow, I think.”", "내 기억에 내일이 누군가의 생일이지 싶은데.\""]], [["“Truly I think it is,”", "\"정말 그러네요.\""], ["said I, kissing my darling, who would be twenty-one to-morrow.", "나는 내일이면 스물한 살이 되는 사랑하는 친구에게 입을 맞추며 말했습니다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["observed my guardian, half pleasantly, half seriously, “that’s a great occasion and will give my fair cousin some necessary business to transact in assertion of her independence, and will make London a more convenient place for all of us.", "후견인이 반은 즐겁게, 반은 진지하게 말했습니다. \"그건 대단한 날이지."], ["So to London we will go.", "내 어여쁜 사촌이 독립을 주장하느라 처리해야 할 일도 좀 생길 테고, 그러면 런던이 우리 모두에게 더 편한 곳이 되겠구나."], ["That being settled, there is another thing—how have you left Caddy?”", "그러니 런던으로 가자. 그건 그렇게 정해두고, 또 한 가지가 있다—캐디는 어떤 상태로 두고 왔니?\""]], [["“Very unwell, guardian.", "\"몹시 안 좋아요, 후견인."], ["I fear it will be some time before she regains her health and strength.”", "건강과 기력을 되찾으려면 한참 걸릴까 봐 걱정이에요.\""]], [["“What do you call some time, now?”", "\"한참이라면 어느 정도를 말하는 거냐?\""], ["asked my guardian thoughtfully.", "후견인이 생각에 잠겨 물었습니다."]], [["“Some weeks, I am afraid.”", "\"몇 주는 될 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["He began to walk about the room with his hands in his pockets, showing that he had been thinking as much.", "후견인은 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 방 안을 거닐기 시작했습니다. 자기도 그만큼은 생각하고 있었다는 표시였습니다."], ["“Now, what do you say about her doctor?", "\"그래, 그 집 의사는 어떠냐?"], ["Is he a good doctor, my love?”", "좋은 의사니, 얘야?\""]], [["I felt obliged to confess that I knew nothing to the contrary but that Prince and I had agreed only that evening that we would like his opinion to be confirmed by some one.", "나는 달리 흠잡을 만한 건 모르겠다고 인정할 수밖에 없었습니다. 다만 바로 그날 저녁 프린스와 나는, 누군가가 그 의사의 소견을 확인해주면 좋겠다고 의견을 모은 참이라고 했습니다."]], [["“Well, you know,”", "\"그래, 알다시피,\""], ["returned my guardian quickly, “there’s Woodcourt.”", "후견인이 얼른 받았습니다. \"우드코트가 있잖니.\""]], [["I had not meant that, and was rather taken by surprise.", "나는 그런 뜻으로 한 말이 아니어서 적잖이 놀랐습니다."], ["For a moment all that I had had in my mind in connexion with Mr. Woodcourt seemed to come back and confuse me.", "한순간 우드코트 씨와 관련해 마음속에 담아두었던 모든 것이 되살아나 나를 어지럽히는 듯했습니다."]], [["“You don’t object to him, little woman?”", "\"그 사람한테 무슨 거리낌이라도 있니, 꼬마 부인?\""]], [["“Object to him, guardian?", "\"거리낌이라뇨, 후견인?"], ["Oh no!”", "아, 그럴 리가요!\""]], [["“And you don’t think the patient would object to him?”", "\"그럼 환자가 그 사람을 꺼리진 않을 것 같으냐?\""]], [["So far from that, I had no doubt of her being prepared to have a great reliance on him and to like him very much.", "꺼리기는커녕, 나는 그녀가 그를 크게 의지할 채비가 되어 있고 무척 좋아하리라는 것을 의심치 않았습니다."], ["I said that he was no stranger to her personally, for she had seen him often in his kind attendance on Miss Flite.", "나는 그가 그녀에게 낯선 사람이 아니라고 말했습니다. 플라이트 양을 친절히 보살피는 모습을 그녀가 여러 번 보았으니까요."]], [["“Very good,”", "\"아주 좋아.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“He has been here to-day, my dear, and I will see him about it to-morrow.”", "\"그 사람이 오늘 여기 다녀갔다, 얘야. 내일 그 일로 만나보마.\""]], [["I felt in this short conversation—though I did not know how, for she was quiet, and we interchanged no look—that my dear girl well remembered how merrily she had clasped me round the waist when no other hands than Caddy’s had brought me the little parting token.", "이 짧은 대화를 나누면서 나는 느꼈습니다—어떻게 느꼈는지는 모르겠습니다. 그녀는 잠자코 있었고, 우리는 눈길도 주고받지 않았으니까요—내 사랑하는 친구가, 캐디의 손이 아니면 아무도 그 작별의 정표를 내게 가져다주지 못하던 그날, 얼마나 즐겁게 내 허리를 끌어안았는지 또렷이 기억하고 있다는 것을요."], ["This caused me to feel that I ought to tell her, and Caddy too, that I was going to be the mistress of Bleak House and that if I avoided that disclosure any longer I might become less worthy in my own eyes of its master’s love.", "그래서 나는 그녀에게도, 또 캐디에게도 내가 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되리라는 것을 알려야 한다고 느꼈습니다. 그 사실을 더 이상 숨긴다면, 그 집 주인의 사랑을 받을 자격이 내 스스로의 눈에 덜한 사람이 될지도 모를 일이었으니까요."], ["Therefore, when we went upstairs and had waited listening until the clock struck twelve in order that only I might be the first to wish my darling all good wishes on her birthday and to take her to my heart, I set before her, just as I had set before myself, the goodness and honour of her cousin John and the happy life that was in store for me.", "그래서 우리가 위층으로 올라가, 오로지 나만이 사랑하는 친구의 생일을 맨 먼저 축하하고 그녀를 가슴에 품을 수 있도록, 시계가 열두 시를 칠 때까지 귀 기울이며 기다린 뒤, 나는 내가 스스로에게 말했던 그대로 그녀에게도 일러주었습니다. 사촌 존의 선량함과 고결함을, 그리고 나를 기다리는 행복한 삶을 말입니다."], ["If ever my darling were fonder of me at one time than another in all our intercourse, she was surely fondest of me that night.", "우리가 함께한 모든 시간을 통틀어 사랑하는 친구가 다른 어느 때보다 나를 더 애틋하게 여긴 적이 있다면, 그건 분명 그날 밤이었습니다."], ["And I was so rejoiced to know it and so comforted by the sense of having done right in casting this last idle reservation away that I was ten times happier than I had been before.", "그리고 나는 그것을 알게 되어 너무나 기뻤고, 이 마지막 부질없는 망설임을 떨쳐버린 것이 옳은 일이었다는 생각에 너무나 마음이 놓여서, 전보다 열 배는 더 행복했습니다."], ["I had scarcely thought it a reservation a few hours ago, but now that it was gone I felt as if I understood its nature better.", "몇 시간 전만 해도 그것을 망설임이라 여기지도 않았건만, 막상 사라지고 나니 나는 그 본질을 더 잘 이해하게 된 듯한 기분이었습니다."]], [["Next day we went to London.", "이튿날 우리는 런던으로 갔습니다."], ["We found our old lodging vacant, and in half an hour were quietly established there, as if we had never gone away.", "옛 하숙집이 비어 있는 것을 확인하고는, 반 시간 만에 마치 한 번도 떠난 적 없었던 것처럼 조용히 자리를 잡았습니다."], ["Mr. Woodcourt dined with us to celebrate my darling’s birthday, and we were as pleasant as we could be with the great blank among us that Richard’s absence naturally made on such an occasion.", "우드코트 씨가 사랑하는 친구의 생일을 축하하러 와서 우리와 함께 저녁을 들었고, 우리는 그런 자리에 리처드가 없으면 으레 생기는 커다란 빈자리를 안고서도 할 수 있는 한 즐겁게 지냈습니다."], ["After that day I was for some weeks—eight or nine as I remember—very much with Caddy, and thus it fell out that I saw less of Ada at this time than any other since we had first come together, except the time of my own illness.", "그날 이후 몇 주 동안—내 기억으로는 여덟이나 아홉 주쯤—나는 캐디와 거의 붙어 지냈고, 그러다 보니 이 무렵 나는, 우리가 처음 함께한 이래 내가 앓아누웠던 때를 빼면 어느 때보다도 에이다를 적게 보게 되었습니다."], ["She often came to Caddy’s, but our function there was to amuse and cheer her, and we did not talk in our usual confidential manner.", "에이다는 캐디네 집에 자주 왔지만, 거기서 우리가 할 일은 그녀를 즐겁게 해주고 기운을 북돋우는 것이었으므로, 평소처럼 속내를 터놓고 이야기하지는 못했습니다."], ["Whenever I went home at night we were together, but Caddy’s rest was broken by pain, and I often remained to nurse her.", "밤에 집에 돌아가면 우리는 늘 함께였지만, 캐디는 통증 때문에 잠을 깊이 못 잤고, 나는 그녀를 간호하느라 그 집에 남는 일이 잦았습니다."]], [["With her husband and her poor little mite of a baby to love and their home to strive for, what a good creature Caddy was!", "사랑할 남편과 가여운 핏덩이 같은 아기가 있고, 또 그들을 위해 가꿔야 할 가정이 있으니, 캐디는 참으로 갸륵한 사람이었습니다!"], ["So self-denying, so uncomplaining, so anxious to get well on their account, so afraid of giving trouble, and so thoughtful of the unassisted labours of her husband and the comforts of old Mr. Turveydrop; I had never known the best of her until now.", "그토록 자신을 희생하고, 그토록 불평이 없고, 그들을 위해 그토록 낫고 싶어 하고, 폐 끼치는 것을 그토록 두려워하고, 도와주는 이 없이 홀로 애쓰는 남편의 노고와 늙은 터비드롭 씨의 안락을 그토록 배려하다니, 나는 지금에서야 비로소 그녀의 가장 좋은 면모를 알게 되었습니다."], ["And it seemed so curious that her pale face and helpless figure should be lying there day after day where dancing was the business of life, where the kit and the apprentices began early every morning in the ball-room, and where the untidy little boy waltzed by himself in the kitchen all the afternoon.", "그리고 어찌나 묘하던지요. 춤이 곧 생업인 곳, 무도장에서 작은 바이올린과 견습생들이 아침마다 일찍부터 시작하는 곳, 지저분한 어린 사내아이가 오후 내내 부엌에서 혼자 왈츠를 추는 그곳에, 그녀의 창백한 얼굴과 무력한 몸이 날마다 누워 있다는 것이 말입니다."]], [["At Caddy’s request I took the supreme direction of her apartment, trimmed it up, and pushed her, couch and all, into a lighter and more airy and more cheerful corner than she had yet occupied; then, every day, when we were in our neatest array, I used to lay my small small namesake in her arms and sit down to chat or work or read to her.", "캐디의 부탁으로 나는 그녀의 방을 도맡아 관리하며 말끔히 정돈했고, 긴 의자째로 그녀를 밀어, 지금껏 차지하고 있던 곳보다 더 밝고 바람 잘 통하고 더 명랑한 구석으로 옮겨주었습니다. 그러고는 날마다 우리가 가장 단정한 차림을 하고 나면, 나는 나와 이름이 같은 그 자그마하고 자그마한 아기를 그녀의 품에 안겨주고서, 곁에 앉아 이야기를 나누거나 바느질을 하거나 책을 읽어주곤 했습니다."], ["It was at one of the first of these quiet times that I told Caddy about Bleak House.", "이렇게 조용한 시간 중에서도 거의 첫 무렵에, 나는 캐디에게 황폐한 집 이야기를 들려주었습니다."]], [["We had other visitors besides Ada.", "에이다 말고도 다른 손님들이 있었습니다."], ["First of all we had Prince, who in his hurried intervals of teaching used to come softly in and sit softly down, with a face of loving anxiety for Caddy and the very little child.", "우선 프린스가 있었지요. 그는 교습 사이사이 짬이 날 때면 살그머니 들어와 살그머니 앉아서는, 캐디와 그 조그마한 아기를 향한 사랑 어린 근심으로 가득한 얼굴을 하고 있었습니다."], ["Whatever Caddy’s condition really was, she never failed to declare to Prince that she was all but well—which I, heaven forgive me, never failed to confirm.", "캐디의 상태가 실제로 어떻든 간에, 그녀는 프린스에게는 늘 거의 다 나았다고 한결같이 말했습니다—그리고 나도, 하늘이 용서하시길, 한결같이 그 말을 거들었습니다."], ["This would put Prince in such good spirits that he would sometimes take the kit from his pocket and play a chord or two to astonish the baby, which I never knew it to do in the least degree, for my tiny namesake never noticed it at all.", "그러면 프린스는 어찌나 기분이 좋아지던지, 이따금 주머니에서 작은 바이올린을 꺼내 한두 가락 켜며 아기를 놀라게 해보려 했지만, 내가 보기에 아기가 조금이라도 놀라는 일은 한 번도 없었습니다. 나와 이름이 같은 그 자그마한 아기는 아예 거들떠보지도 않았으니까요."]], [["Then there was Mrs. Jellyby.", "다음으로 젤리비 부인이 있었습니다."], ["She would come occasionally, with her usual distraught manner, and sit calmly looking miles beyond her grandchild as if her attention were absorbed by a young Borrioboolan on its native shores.", "부인은 이따금, 여느 때처럼 정신이 딴 데 가 있는 태도로 와서는, 마치 멀고 먼 고향 땅의 어린 보리오불라 사람에게 온통 정신이 팔리기라도 한 듯, 손주를 까마득히 지나친 어딘가를 태연히 바라보며 앉아 있곤 했습니다."], ["As bright-eyed as ever, as serene, and as untidy, she would say, “Well, Caddy, child, and how do you do to-day?”", "여전히 눈을 반짝이며, 여전히 평온하게, 여전히 단정치 못한 차림으로 부인은 말하곤 했습니다. \"그래, 캐디야, 오늘은 좀 어떠니?\""], ["And then would sit amiably smiling and taking no notice of the reply or would sweetly glide off into a calculation of the number of letters she had lately received and answered or of the coffee-bearing power of Borrioboola-Gha.", "그러고는 상냥하게 미소 지으며 앉아 대답에는 아랑곳하지 않거나, 아니면 사뿐히 미끄러지듯 화제를 바꿔 요사이 받고 답한 편지가 몇 통이나 되는지, 혹은 보리오불라가의 커피 산출 능력이 어느 정도인지 따져보는 데로 흘러가곤 했습니다."], ["This she would always do with a serene contempt for our limited sphere of action, not to be disguised.", "부인은 늘 우리의 좁디좁은 활동 영역을 향한, 도무지 감출 수 없는 평온한 경멸을 품고 이렇게 했습니다."]], [["Then there was old Mr.", "다음으로 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 있었습니다."], ["Turveydrop, who was from morning to night and from night to morning the subject of innumerable precautions.", "그는 아침부터 밤까지, 밤부터 아침까지 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 조심거리의 대상이었습니다."], ["If the baby cried, it was nearly stifled lest the noise should make him uncomfortable.", "아기가 울면, 그 소리에 그가 불편해질세라 거의 숨이 막힐 지경으로 달랬습니다."], ["If the fire wanted stirring in the night, it was surreptitiously done lest his rest should be broken.", "밤중에 불을 뒤적여야 하면, 그의 안식이 깨질세라 몰래 했습니다."], ["If Caddy required any little comfort that the house contained, she first carefully discussed whether he was likely to require it too.", "캐디가 집 안에 있는 자그마한 편의품 하나라도 필요로 하면, 먼저 그도 그것을 필요로 할 법한지부터 조심스레 의논했습니다."], ["In return for this consideration he would come into the room once a day, all but blessing it—showing a condescension, and a patronage, and a grace of manner in dispensing the light of his high-shouldered presence from which I might have supposed him (if I had not known better) to have been the benefactor of Caddy’s life.", "이런 배려에 대한 보답으로 그는 하루에 한 번 방에 들어왔는데, 그 방에 거의 축복을 내리다시피 했습니다—어깨가 솟은 자신의 존재에서 흘러나오는 빛을 베풀면서, 어찌나 은혜를 베푸는 듯한 겸양과 후원과 우아한 태도를 보이던지, 내가 (속사정을 몰랐더라면) 그를 캐디의 삶에 은혜를 베푼 사람으로 여겼을 정도였습니다."]], [["“My Caroline,”", "\"내 캐롤라인.\""], ["he would say, making the nearest approach that he could to bending over her.", "그는 그녀에게 몸을 굽힌다고 굽혀 그나마 가장 가까이 다가서며 말하곤 했습니다."], ["“Tell me that you are better to-day.”", "\"오늘은 좀 나아졌다고 말해주렴.\""]], [["“Oh, much better, thank you, Mr. Turveydrop,”", "\"아, 훨씬 나아졌어요, 고맙습니다, 터비드롭 씨.\""], ["Caddy would reply.", "캐디가 대답하곤 했습니다."]], [["“Delighted!", "\"기쁘구나!"], ["Enchanted!", "더없이 기쁘다!"], ["And our dear Miss Summerson.", "그리고 우리의 사랑스러운 서머슨 양."], ["She is not quite prostrated by fatigue?”", "피곤에 아주 지쳐 쓰러진 건 아니겠지?\""], ["Here he would crease up his eyelids and kiss his fingers to me, though I am happy to say he had ceased to be particular in his attentions since I had been so altered.", "이 대목에서 그는 눈꺼풀을 잔뜩 찡그리며 나에게 손끝으로 입맞춤을 보내곤 했습니다. 그래도 내가 그토록 달라진 뒤로는 나에 대한 살가운 관심을 거두었다는 것을 다행으로 여기며 말씀드립니다."]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"전혀 아니에요.\""], ["I would assure him.", "나는 그를 안심시키곤 했습니다."]], [["“Charming!", "\"훌륭하구나!"], ["We must take care of our dear Caroline, Miss Summerson.", "우리의 사랑스러운 캐롤라인을 잘 보살펴야 해요, 서머슨 양."], ["We must spare nothing that will restore her.", "저 아이를 낫게 하는 일이라면 무엇 하나 아껴서는 안 됩니다."], ["We must nourish her.", "잘 먹여 기운을 차리게 해야지요."], ["My dear Caroline”—he would turn to his daughter-in-law with infinite generosity and protection—“want for nothing, my love.", "내 사랑하는 캐롤라인\"—그는 한없는 너그러움과 비호의 태도로 며느리에게로 몸을 돌리곤 했습니다—\"무엇이든 부족함이 없도록 해라, 얘야."], ["Frame a wish and gratify it, my daughter.", "원하는 게 있거든 말하고 그대로 이루렴, 내 딸아."], ["Everything this house contains, everything my room contains, is at your service, my dear.", "이 집에 있는 모든 것, 내 방에 있는 모든 것이 다 네 것이란다, 얘야."], ["Do not,”", "행여,\""], ["he would sometimes add in a burst of deportment, “even allow my simple requirements to be considered if they should at any time interfere with your own, my Caroline.", "그는 이따금 격식이 북받쳐 이렇게 덧붙이곤 했습니다. \"내 소박한 필요가 어느 때든 네 필요와 부딪히거든, 그것일랑 아예 헤아리지도 말려무나, 내 캐롤라인."], ["Your necessities are greater than mine.”", "네 필요가 내 것보다 더 중하니까.\""]], [["He had established such a long prescriptive right to this deportment (his son’s inheritance from his mother) that I several times knew both Caddy and her husband to be melted to tears by these affectionate self-sacrifices.", "그는 이런 격식 차린 태도(그의 아들이 어머니에게서 물려받은 그 유산)에 대해 어찌나 오래도록 굳어진 권리를 누리고 있던지, 나는 이렇게 애정 어린 자기희생을 늘어놓는 그를 보고 캐디와 그녀의 남편이 눈물을 흘리는 광경을 여러 번 보았습니다."]], [["“Nay, my dears,”", "\"아니란다, 얘들아.\""], ["he would remonstrate; and when I saw Caddy’s thin arm about his fat neck as he said it, I would be melted too, though not by the same process.", "그는 만류하곤 했습니다. 그가 그렇게 말할 때 캐디의 가느다란 팔이 그의 살진 목을 감는 것을 보면, 나도 마음이 녹곤 했습니다."], ["“Nay, nay!", "같은 연유는 아니었지만요."], ["I have promised never to leave ye.", "\"아니야, 아니야! 나는 결코 너희를 떠나지 않겠다고 약속했어."], ["Be dutiful and affectionate towards me, and I ask no other return.", "나에게 도리를 다하고 정을 베풀어다오, 그 외에 다른 보답은 바라지 않으마."], ["Now, bless ye!", "자, 너희에게 복이 있기를!"], ["I am going to the Park.”", "나는 공원에 다녀오마.\""]], [["He would take the air there presently and get an appetite for his hotel dinner.", "그는 곧 거기서 바람을 쐬며 호텔 저녁 식사를 위한 입맛을 돋우곤 했습니다."], ["I hope I do old Mr.", "내가 늙은 터비드롭 씨를 부당하게 깎아내리는 일이 없기를 바랍니다만, 나는 여기 충실히 적은 것들 말고는 그에게서 더 나은 면모를 본 적이 없습니다."], ["Turveydrop no wrong, but I never saw any better traits in him than these I faithfully record, except that he certainly conceived a liking for Peepy and would take the child out walking with great pomp, always on those occasions sending him home before he went to dinner himself, and occasionally with a halfpenny in his pocket.", "다만 한 가지, 그가 피피를 분명히 마음에 들어 해서, 그 아이를 데리고 거창하게 산책을 나가곤 했다는 점은 빼고 말이지요. 그럴 때면 그는 늘 자기가 저녁을 들러 가기 전에 아이를 집으로 돌려보냈고, 이따금 그 아이 주머니에 반 페니쯤 넣어주기도 했습니다."], ["But even this disinterestedness was attended with no inconsiderable cost, to my knowledge, for before Peepy was sufficiently decorated to walk hand in hand with the professor of deportment, he had to be newly dressed, at the expense of Caddy and her husband, from top to toe.", "하지만 이런 무사무욕한 행동조차도, 내가 아는 한 적잖은 비용이 따랐습니다. 피피가 격식의 대가와 손잡고 걸어도 될 만큼 말끔히 차려입으려면, 캐디와 그녀 남편의 돈으로 머리끝부터 발끝까지 새로 입혀야 했으니까요."]], [["Last of our visitors, there was Mr. Jellyby.", "우리 손님들 가운데 마지막으로 젤리비 씨가 있었습니다."], ["Really when he used to come in of an evening, and ask Caddy in his meek voice how she was, and then sit down with his head against the wall, and make no attempt to say anything more, I liked him very much.", "정말이지 그가 저녁에 들어와, 온순한 목소리로 캐디에게 좀 어떠냐고 묻고는, 벽에 머리를 기대고 앉아 그 이상 무슨 말을 하려는 기색도 없을 때, 나는 그가 무척 좋았습니다."], ["If he found me bustling about doing any little thing, he sometimes half took his coat off, as if with an intention of helping by a great exertion; but he never got any further.", "내가 부산히 자그마한 일을 하고 있는 것을 보면, 그는 가끔 무슨 대단한 힘이라도 보태려는 듯 외투를 반쯤 벗기도 했습니다. 하지만 그 이상으로는 결코 나아가지 못했지요."], ["His sole occupation was to sit with his head against the wall, looking hard at the thoughtful baby; and I could not quite divest my mind of a fancy that they understood one another.", "그의 유일한 소일거리는 벽에 머리를 기대고 앉아, 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 아기를 뚫어지게 바라보는 것이었습니다. 그래서 나는 그 둘이 서로 통하는 데가 있다는 공상을 좀처럼 떨쳐버릴 수가 없었습니다."]], [["I have not counted Mr. Woodcourt among our visitors because he was now Caddy’s regular attendant.", "우드코트 씨는 우리 손님으로 꼽지 않았는데, 이제 그가 캐디의 단골 주치의가 되었기 때문입니다."], ["She soon began to improve under his care, but he was so gentle, so skilful, so unwearying in the pains he took that it is not to be wondered at, I am sure. I saw a good deal of Mr.", "그녀는 그의 보살핌 아래 곧 차도를 보이기 시작했는데, 그가 어찌나 다정하고, 어찌나 솜씨가 좋고, 그토록 지치는 기색 없이 정성을 쏟던지, 그건 당연한 일이었지요."], ["Woodcourt during this time, though not so much as might be supposed, for knowing Caddy to be safe in his hands, I often slipped home at about the hours when he was expected.", "이 무렵 나는 우드코트 씨를 꽤 자주 보았습니다. 다만 사람들이 짐작할 만큼은 아니었는데, 캐디가 그의 손에 맡겨져 안전하다는 것을 알았으므로, 나는 그가 올 시각이 되면 슬그머니 집으로 빠져나가는 일이 잦았기 때문입니다."], ["We frequently met, notwithstanding.", "그래도 우리는 자주 마주쳤습니다."], ["I was quite reconciled to myself now, but I still felt glad to think that he was sorry for me, and he still WAS sorry for me I believed. He helped Mr.", "이제 나는 나 자신과 충분히 화해한 상태였지만, 그가 나를 가엾이 여긴다고 생각하면 여전히 기뻤고, 그가 여전히 나를 가엾이 여긴다고 나는 믿었습니다."], ["Badger in his professional engagements, which were numerous, and had as yet no settled projects for the future.", "그는 배저 씨의 진료 일을 거들고 있었는데, 그 일이 워낙 많았습니다. 그리고 앞날에 대해서는 아직 정해진 계획이 없었습니다."]], [["It was when Caddy began to recover that I began to notice a change in my dear girl.", "캐디가 회복되기 시작할 무렵, 나는 사랑하는 친구에게서 어떤 변화를 알아차리기 시작했습니다."], ["I cannot say how it first presented itself to me, because I observed it in many slight particulars which were nothing in themselves and only became something when they were pieced together.", "그것이 처음에 어떻게 내 눈에 들어왔는지는 말하기 어렵습니다. 그 자체로는 아무것도 아니어서 여럿이 모여야만 비로소 무언가가 되는 자잘한 정황들을 통해 알아차렸기 때문입니다."], ["But I made it out, by putting them together, that Ada was not so frankly cheerful with me as she used to be.", "하지만 그것들을 하나하나 맞춰본 끝에 나는, 에이다가 예전만큼 나에게 거리낌 없이 명랑하지 않다는 것을 알아냈습니다. 나를 향한 그녀의 다정함은 여전히 애틋하고 진실했습니다."], ["Her tenderness for me was as loving and true as ever; I did not for a moment doubt that; but there was a quiet sorrow about her which she did not confide to me, and in which I traced some hidden regret.", "그것은 한순간도 의심하지 않았습니다. 다만 그녀에게는 나에게 털어놓지 않는 조용한 슬픔이 어려 있었고, 그 속에서 나는 어떤 감춰진 회한의 자취를 더듬을 수 있었습니다."]], [["Now, I could not understand this, and I was so anxious for the happiness of my own pet that it caused me some uneasiness and set me thinking often.", "그런데 나는 이것을 도무지 이해할 수가 없었고, 내 소중한 친구의 행복이 너무나 걱정스러운 나머지, 마음이 적잖이 불편해져 자주 생각에 잠기게 되었습니다."], ["At length, feeling sure that Ada suppressed this something from me lest it should make me unhappy too, it came into my head that she was a little grieved—for me—by what I had told her about Bleak House.", "마침내 에이다가 이 무언가를, 그것이 나까지 불행하게 만들세라 나에게 감추고 있는 것이 틀림없다고 확신하게 되자, 문득 이런 생각이 들었습니다. 그녀가—나를 위해서—내가 황폐한 집에 관해 들려준 이야기 때문에 조금 슬퍼하고 있는 것이라고요."]], [["How I persuaded myself that this was likely, I don’t know.", "내가 어떻게 이것을 그럴듯하다고 스스로를 설득했는지는 모르겠습니다."], ["I had no idea that there was any selfish reference in my doing so.", "내가 그렇게 한 데에 어떤 이기적인 속내가 있다고는 생각지 못했습니다."], ["I was not grieved for myself: I was quite contented and quite happy.", "나는 나 자신 때문에 슬프지 않았으니까요. 나는 더없이 만족스럽고 더없이 행복했습니다."], ["Still, that Ada might be thinking—for me, though I had abandoned all such thoughts—of what once was, but was now all changed, seemed so easy to believe that I believed it.", "그래도 에이다가—나를 위해서, 나는 그런 생각을 다 버렸건만—한때는 그러했으나 이제는 모두 달라져버린 것을 떠올리고 있을지도 모른다는 것은, 너무나 믿기 쉬워서 나는 그만 그것을 믿어버렸습니다."]], [["What could I do to reassure my darling (I considered then) and show her that I had no such feelings?", "사랑하는 친구를 안심시키고 나에게 그런 마음이 없다는 것을 보여주려면 어떻게 해야 할까(그때 나는 곰곰이 생각했습니다)?"], ["Well!", "글쎄요!"], ["I could only be as brisk and busy as possible, and that I had tried to be all along.", "나는 그저 최대한 활기차고 분주하게 지낼 수밖에 없었고, 그건 줄곧 애써온 일이기도 했습니다."], ["However, as Caddy’s illness had certainly interfered, more or less, with my home duties—though I had always been there in the morning to make my guardian’s breakfast, and he had a hundred times laughed and said there must be two little women, for his little woman was never missing—I resolved to be doubly diligent and gay.", "다만 캐디의 병 때문에 내 집안일이 이러나저러나 다소 방해를 받은 건 분명했지요—그래도 나는 늘 아침이면 거기서 후견인의 아침상을 차렸고, 그는 백 번도 더 웃으며 꼬마 부인이 둘은 있어야겠다고, 자기 꼬마 부인은 한 번도 자리를 비운 적이 없으니 그렇다고 말했지만요. 어쨌든 나는 갑절로 부지런하고 명랑해지기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["So I went about the house humming all the tunes I knew, and I sat working and working in a desperate manner, and I talked and talked, morning, noon, and night.", "그래서 나는 아는 노래란 노래는 다 흥얼거리며 집 안을 돌아다녔고, 죽기 살기로 바느질을 하고 또 했으며, 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 이야기하고 또 이야기했습니다."]], [["And still there was the same shade between me and my darling.", "그런데도 나와 사랑하는 친구 사이에는 여전히 똑같은 그늘이 드리워 있었습니다."]], [["“So, Dame Trot,”", "\"자, 더든 아주머니.\""], ["observed my guardian, shutting up his book one night when we were all three together, “so Woodcourt has restored Caddy Jellyby to the full enjoyment of life again?”", "어느 날 밤, 우리 셋이 함께 있을 때 후견인이 읽던 책을 덮으며 말했습니다. \"그래, 우드코트가 캐디 젤리비를 다시 온전히 삶을 누릴 수 있게 되돌려놓았다고?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["I said; “and to be repaid by such gratitude as hers is to be made rich, guardian.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그리고 그녀 같은 사람의 고마움으로 보답받는다면 그건 부자가 되는 일이지요, 후견인.\""]], [["“I wish it was,”", "\"진심으로 그랬으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["he returned, “with all my heart.”", "그가 받았습니다."]], [["So did I too, for that matter.", "사실 나도 그랬으면 했습니다."], ["I said so.", "나도 그렇다고 말했지요."]], [["“Aye!", "\"그래!"], ["We would make him as rich as a Jew if we knew how.", "방법만 안다면 우리가 그를 유대인만큼이나 부자로 만들어줄 텐데."], ["Would we not, little woman?”", "안 그러냐, 꼬마 부인?\""]], [["I laughed as I worked and replied that I was not sure about that, for it might spoil him, and he might not be so useful, and there might be many who could ill spare him.", "나는 바느질을 하며 웃었고, 그건 잘 모르겠다고 대답했습니다. 그러면 그를 망쳐놓을지도 모르고, 그러면 그가 그리 쓸모 있는 사람이 못 될지도 모르며, 그가 없으면 곤란해질 사람도 많을 테니까요."], ["As Miss Flite, and Caddy herself, and many others.", "플라이트 양이며, 캐디 본인이며, 그 밖에도 많은 이들이 말입니다."]], [["“True,”", "\"맞는 말이다.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“I had forgotten that.", "\"내가 그걸 잊었구나."], ["But we would agree to make him rich enough to live, I suppose?", "하지만 그래도 먹고살 만큼은 부자로 만들어주는 데는 다들 동의하겠지?"], ["Rich enough to work with tolerable peace of mind?", "그럭저럭 마음 편히 일할 만큼은 부자로?"], ["Rich enough to have his own happy home and his own household gods—and household goddess, too, perhaps?”", "자기만의 행복한 가정과 자기만의 가정의 수호신들을—어쩌면 가정의 여신까지도—거느릴 만큼은 부자로 말이다.\""]], [["That was quite another thing, I said.", "그건 전혀 다른 이야기라고 나는 말했습니다."], ["We must all agree in that.", "그건 우리 모두 동의해야 한다고요."]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“All of us.", "\"우리 모두 말이다."], ["I have a great regard for Woodcourt, a high esteem for him; and I have been sounding him delicately about his plans.", "나는 우드코트를 무척 아끼고, 그를 높이 평가한단다. 그래서 그의 계획에 대해 조심스레 떠보고 있었지."], ["It is difficult to offer aid to an independent man with that just kind of pride which he possesses.", "그가 지닌 그 올곧은 자존심을 가진 독립적인 사람에게 도움을 건네기란 어려운 일이야."], ["And yet I would be glad to do it if I might or if I knew how.", "그래도 할 수만 있다면, 또 방법만 안다면 기꺼이 그러고 싶구나."], ["He seems half inclined for another voyage.", "그는 또 한 번 항해를 떠날 마음이 반쯤 있는 듯하더라."], ["But that appears like casting such a man away.”", "하지만 그건 그런 사람을 내쳐버리는 일 같기도 하고.\""]], [["“It might open a new world to him,”", "\"새로운 세상이 그에게 열릴지도 모르지요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“So it might, little woman,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지, 꼬마 부인.\""], ["my guardian assented.", "후견인이 동의했습니다."], ["“I doubt if he expects much of the old world.", "\"그가 이 낡은 세상에 큰 기대를 거는 것 같지도 않고."], ["Do you know I have fancied that he sometimes feels some particular disappointment or misfortune encountered in it.", "너도 아니? 나는 그가 이 세상에서 어떤 각별한 실망이나 불운을 겪은 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들 때가 있단다."], ["You never heard of anything of that sort?”", "그런 일에 대해 무슨 이야기를 들은 적은 없니?\""]], [["I shook my head.", "나는 고개를 가로저었습니다."]], [["“Humph,”", "\"흠.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“I am mistaken, I dare say.”", "\"내가 잘못 짚었나 보다.\""], ["As there was a little pause here, which I thought, for my dear girl’s satisfaction, had better be filled up, I hummed an air as I worked which was a favourite with my guardian.", "여기서 잠시 침묵이 흘렀는데, 나는 사랑하는 친구를 위해서라도 그 틈을 메우는 게 낫겠다 싶어, 바느질을 하면서 후견인이 좋아하는 곡조 하나를 흥얼거렸습니다."]], [["“And do you think Mr. Woodcourt will make another voyage?”", "\"그래서 우드코트 씨가 또 항해를 떠날 거라고 보세요?\""], ["I asked him when I had hummed it quietly all through.", "나는 그 곡을 처음부터 끝까지 나직이 흥얼거리고 나서 그에게 물었습니다."]], [["“I don’t quite know what to think, my dear, but I should say it was likely at present that he will give a long trip to another country.”", "\"뭐라 단정하기는 어렵구나, 얘야. 하지만 지금으로서는 그가 다른 나라로 긴 여행을 떠날 공산이 크다고 봐야겠지.\""]], [["“I am sure he will take the best wishes of all our hearts with him wherever he goes,”", "\"그가 어디로 가든 우리 모두의 진심 어린 축원이 함께할 거예요.\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I; “and though they are not riches, he will never be the poorer for them, guardian, at least.”", "\"그게 재물은 아니지만, 그래도 그것 때문에 그가 더 가난해지는 일은 결코 없을 거예요, 후견인. 적어도 그건 분명하지요.\""]], [["“Never, little woman,”", "\"결코 그럴 일은 없지, 꼬마 부인.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했습니다."]], [["I was sitting in my usual place, which was now beside my guardian’s chair.", "나는 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아 있었는데, 이제 그곳은 후견인 의자 옆이었습니다."], ["That had not been my usual place before the letter, but it was now.", "그 편지가 오기 전까지는 그게 내 늘 앉던 자리가 아니었지만, 이제는 그랬습니다."], ["I looked up to Ada, who was sitting opposite, and I saw, as she looked at me, that her eyes were filled with tears and that tears were falling down her face.", "나는 맞은편에 앉은 에이다를 올려다보았고, 그녀가 나를 바라볼 때 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 고이고 그 눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 보았습니다."], ["I felt that I had only to be placid and merry once for all to undeceive my dear and set her loving heart at rest.", "나는 단번에 차분하고 명랑해지기만 하면 사랑하는 친구의 오해를 풀어주고 그 사랑 가득한 마음을 편안하게 해줄 수 있다고 느꼈습니다."], ["I really was so, and I had nothing to do but to be myself.", "나는 정말로 그러했으므로, 그저 나 자신답게 있기만 하면 되었습니다."]], [["So I made my sweet girl lean upon my shoulder—how little thinking what was heavy on her mind!—and I said she was not quite well, and put my arm about her, and took her upstairs.", "그래서 나는 사랑스러운 친구를 내 어깨에 기대게 했습니다—그녀의 마음을 짓누르는 것이 무엇인지는 까맣게 모른 채 말이지요!—그러고는 몸이 좀 안 좋은 거라고 하며 그녀를 팔로 감싸 위층으로 데려갔습니다."], ["When we were in our own room, and when she might perhaps have told me what I was so unprepared to hear, I gave her no encouragement to confide in me; I never thought she stood in need of it.", "우리 둘만의 방에 들어와, 어쩌면 그녀가 내가 도무지 들을 채비가 되어 있지 않은 그 이야기를 털어놓았을지도 모를 그때, 나는 그녀가 내게 속내를 털어놓도록 부추기지 않았습니다. 그녀에게 그럴 필요가 있다고는 생각조차 못 했으니까요."]], [["“Oh, my dear good Esther,”", "\"아, 사랑하는 착한 에스더.\""], ["said Ada, “if I could only make up my mind to speak to you and my cousin John when you are together!”", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"너랑 사촌 존이 함께 있을 때, 두 사람한테 말할 결심만 설 수 있다면!\""]], [["“Why, my love!”", "\"왜 그래, 얘?\""], ["I remonstrated.", "내가 만류했습니다."], ["“Ada, why should you not speak to us!”", "\"에이다, 왜 우리한테 말을 못 한다는 거니!\""]], [["Ada only dropped her head and pressed me closer to her heart.", "에이다는 그저 고개를 떨구고 나를 가슴에 더 꼭 끌어안았습니다."]], [["“You surely don’t forget, my beauty,”", "\"넌 설마 잊지 않았겠지, 내 어여쁜 친구야.\""], ["said I, smiling, “what quiet, old-fashioned people we are and how I have settled down to be the discreetest of dames?", "내가 미소 지으며 말했습니다. \"우리가 얼마나 조용하고 예스러운 사람들인지, 또 내가 어떻게 더없이 신중한 안주인으로 자리를 잡았는지 말이야."], ["You don’t forget how happily and peacefully my life is all marked out for me, and by whom?", "내 삶이 얼마나 행복하고 평온하게 다 정해져 있는지, 또 누구에 의해 그런지도 잊지 않았겠지?"], ["I am certain that you don’t forget by what a noble character, Ada.", "분명 너는 얼마나 고결한 분에 의해서인지 잊지 않았을 거야, 에이다."], ["That can never be.”", "그건 결코 잊을 수 없는 일이지.\""]], [["“No, never, Esther.”", "\"응, 결코 잊지 않았어, 에스더.\""]], [["“Why then, my dear,”", "\"그렇다면 얘야,\""], ["said I, “there can be nothing amiss—and why should you not speak to us?”", "내가 말했습니다. \"잘못될 일이라곤 아무것도 없잖니—그러니 너는 왜 우리한테 말을 못 하는 거야?\""]], [["“Nothing amiss, Esther?”", "\"잘못될 일이 없다고, 에스더?\""], ["returned Ada.", "에이다가 받았습니다."], ["“Oh, when I think of all these years, and of his fatherly care and kindness, and of the old relations among us, and of you, what shall I do, what shall I do!”", "\"아, 지난 그 모든 세월을 생각하면, 그분의 아버지 같은 보살핌과 다정함을, 우리 사이의 그 오랜 정을, 또 너를 생각하면, 나는 어떡하면 좋아, 어떡하면 좋아!\""]], [["I looked at my child in some wonder, but I thought it better not to answer otherwise than by cheering her, and so I turned off into many little recollections of our life together and prevented her from saying more.", "나는 다소 의아한 눈으로 내 아이를 바라보았지만, 그녀를 위로하는 것 말고는 달리 대답하지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 우리가 함께한 삶의 자잘한 추억들로 화제를 돌려 그녀가 더 말하지 못하게 막았습니다."], ["When she lay down to sleep, and not before, I returned to my guardian to say good night, and then I came back to Ada and sat near her for a little while.", "그녀가 잠자리에 누운 뒤에야, 그제야 비로소 나는 후견인에게 가서 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸고, 그런 다음 다시 에이다에게 돌아와 잠시 그녀 곁에 앉아 있었습니다."]], [["She was asleep, and I thought as I looked at her that she was a little changed.", "그녀는 잠들어 있었고, 나는 그녀를 바라보면서 그녀가 조금 달라졌다고 생각했습니다."], ["I had thought so more than once lately.", "요즘 들어 한두 번 그런 생각을 한 게 아니었지요."], ["I could not decide, even looking at her while she was unconscious, how she was changed, but something in the familiar beauty of her face looked different to me.", "그녀가 의식 없이 누워 있는 모습을 들여다보면서도, 나는 그녀가 어떻게 달라졌는지 도무지 가늠할 수가 없었습니다. 다만 낯익은 그 얼굴의 아름다움 속 무언가가 나에게는 달라 보였습니다."], ["My guardian’s old hopes of her and Richard arose sorrowfully in my mind, and I said to myself, “She has been anxious about him,”", "그녀와 리처드에 대한 후견인의 옛 바람이 내 마음속에 서글프게 떠올랐고, 나는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸습니다. \"이 아이가 그 사람 때문에 마음을 졸여왔구나.\""], ["and I wondered how that love would end.", "그리고 나는 그 사랑이 어떻게 끝날지 헤아려보았습니다."]], [["When I had come home from Caddy’s while she was ill, I had often found Ada at work, and she had always put her work away, and I had never known what it was.", "캐디가 앓는 동안 그 집에서 돌아오면, 나는 에이다가 무언가를 바느질하고 있는 것을 자주 보았는데, 그녀는 늘 그 바느질감을 치워버렸고, 그래서 나는 그게 무엇인지 한 번도 알지 못했습니다."], ["Some of it now lay in a drawer near her, which was not quite closed.", "지금 그중 일부가 그녀 곁의 서랍 안에 놓여 있었는데, 서랍이 완전히 닫혀 있지 않았습니다."], ["I did not open the drawer, but I still rather wondered what the work could be, for it was evidently nothing for herself.", "나는 서랍을 열지는 않았지만, 그래도 그 바느질감이 대체 무엇일까 적잖이 궁금했습니다. 그것이 그녀 자신을 위한 것이 아님은 분명했으니까요."]], [["And I noticed as I kissed my dear that she lay with one hand under her pillow so that it was hidden.", "그리고 사랑하는 친구에게 입을 맞추면서 나는, 그녀가 한 손을 베개 밑에 넣어 보이지 않게 한 채 누워 있는 것을 알아챘습니다."]], [["How much less amiable I must have been than they thought me, how much less amiable than I thought myself, to be so preoccupied with my own cheerfulness and contentment as to think that it only rested with me to put my dear girl right and set her mind at peace!", "내가 그들이 생각한 것보다, 또 내가 스스로 생각한 것보다 얼마나 덜 사랑스러운 사람이었던가요. 내 자신의 명랑함과 만족에만 그토록 골몰한 나머지, 사랑하는 친구를 바로잡고 그 마음을 평안하게 하는 일이 오로지 나에게 달려 있다고 여겼으니 말입니다!"]], [["But I lay down, self-deceived, in that belief.", "하지만 나는 스스로를 속인 채, 그 믿음 속에 자리에 누웠습니다."], ["And I awoke in it next day to find that there was still the same shade between me and my darling. Enlightened", "그리고 이튿날에도 그 믿음 속에서 잠을 깼지만, 나와 사랑하는 친구 사이에는 여전히 똑같은 그늘이 드리워 있다는 것을 알게 되었을 뿐입니다."]], [["When Mr. Woodcourt arrived in London, he went, that very same day, to Mr.", "우드코트 씨는 런던에 도착하자 바로 그날로 사이먼드 법학원에 있는 볼스 씨를 찾아갔습니다."], ["Vholes’s in Symond’s Inn. For he never once, from the moment when I entreated him to be a friend to Richard, neglected or forgot his promise.", "내가 그에게 리처드의 친구가 되어달라고 간청한 그 순간부터, 그는 단 한 번도 약속을 소홀히 하거나 잊은 적이 없었습니다."], ["He had told me that he accepted the charge as a sacred trust, and he was ever true to it in that spirit.", "그는 그 부탁을 신성한 책무로 받아들였다고 나에게 말했고, 언제나 그 정신에 충실했습니다."]], [["He found Mr. Vholes in his office and informed Mr. Vholes of his agreement with Richard that he should call there to learn his address.", "그는 사무실에서 볼스 씨를 만나, 리처드의 주소를 알아내려고 들르기로 한 약속을 볼스 씨에게 알렸습니다."]], [["“Just so, sir,” said Mr.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생님.\""], ["Vholes. “Mr.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["C.’s address is not a hundred miles from here, sir, Mr.", "\"C 씨 주소는 여기서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않았습니다, 선생님."], ["C.’s address is not a hundred miles from here.", "C 씨 주소는 여기서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않았어요."], ["Would you take a seat, sir?”", "자리에 앉으시겠습니까, 선생님?\""]], [["Mr. Woodcourt thanked Mr. Vholes, but he had no business with him beyond what he had mentioned.", "우드코트 씨는 볼스 씨에게 고맙다고 했지만, 앞서 말한 것 외에는 그와 볼일이 없었습니다."]], [["“Just so, sir.", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 선생님."], ["I believe, sir,” said Mr.", "제 생각에 선생님은,\""], ["Vholes, still quietly insisting on the seat by not giving the address, “that you have influence with Mr.", "볼스 씨가 주소를 알려주지 않은 채 여전히 조용하게 자리를 권하며 말했습니다. \"C 씨에게 영향력이 있는 듯합니다."], ["C. Indeed I am aware that you have.”", "사실 저는 선생님께 그런 영향력이 있다는 걸 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“I was not aware of it myself,”", "\"나 자신은 몰랐는데요.\""], ["returned Mr.", "우드코트 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["Woodcourt; “but I suppose you know best.”", "\"하지만 당신이 제일 잘 안다니 그런가 봅니다.\""]], [["“Sir,” rejoined Mr.", "\"선생님,\""], ["Vholes, self-contained as usual, voice and all, “it is a part of my professional duty to know best.", "볼스 씨가 여느 때처럼 목소리까지 차분하게 다잡으며 받았습니다. \"제일 잘 아는 것이 제 직업적 의무의 한 부분입니다."], ["It is a part of my professional duty to study and to understand a gentleman who confides his interests to me.", "자기 이익을 저에게 맡긴 신사를 연구하고 이해하는 것이 제 직업적 의무의 한 부분이지요."], ["In my professional duty I shall not be wanting, sir, if I know it.", "제가 아는 한, 선생님, 저는 직업적 의무를 게을리하지 않습니다."], ["I may, with the best intentions, be wanting in it without knowing it; but not if I know it, sir.”", "좋은 뜻을 품고도 모르는 사이에 게을리할 수는 있겠지요. 하지만 제가 안다면, 선생님, 그러지 않습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Woodcourt again mentioned the address.", "우드코트 씨가 다시 주소를 언급했습니다."]], [["“Give me leave, sir,”", "\"잠깐만요, 선생님.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Bear with me for a moment. Sir, Mr.", "\"잠시만 참아주십시오."], ["C. is playing for a considerable stake, and cannot play without—need I say what?”", "선생님, C 씨는 상당한 판돈을 걸고 게임을 하고 있는데, 그러려면 빠질 수 없는 것이 있습니다 — 그게 무엇인지 제가 굳이 말해야 할까요?\""]], [["“Money, I presume?”", "\"돈, 이겠지요?\""]], [["“Sir,” said Mr.", "\"선생님,\""], ["Vholes, “to be honest with you (honesty being my golden rule, whether I gain by it or lose, and I find that I generally lose), money is the word.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"솔직하게 말씀드리면(솔직함은 제 황금률입니다, 그것으로 득을 보든 손해를 보든 말이지요."], ["Now, sir, upon the chances of Mr.", "그런데 보통은 손해를 보더군요), 돈이 바로 그 말입니다."], ["C.’s game I express to you no opinion, NO opinion. It might be highly impolitic in Mr.", "자, 선생님, C 씨의 게임이 어찌 될지에 대해 저는 아무 의견도 드리지 않겠습니다, 아무 의견도요."], ["C., after playing so long and so high, to leave off; it might be the reverse; I say nothing.", "그토록 오래, 그토록 크게 걸어온 마당에 C 씨가 손을 떼는 것이 대단히 어리석을 수도 있고, 그 반대일 수도 있겠지요. 저는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["No, sir,” said Mr.", "그렇습니다, 선생님.\""], ["Vholes, bringing his hand flat down upon his desk in a positive manner, “nothing.”", "볼스 씨가 단호하게 손바닥을 책상에 탁 내리치며 말했습니다. \"아무 말도요.\""]], [["“You seem to forget,”", "\"잊으신 모양인데요.\""], ["returned Mr.", "우드코트 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["Woodcourt, “that I ask you to say nothing and have no interest in anything you say.”", "\"나는 당신에게 아무 말도 하지 말라고 부탁했고, 당신이 무슨 말을 하든 관심이 없습니다.\""]], [["“Pardon me, sir!”", "\"실례지만, 선생님!\""], ["retorted Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 받아쳤습니다."], ["“You do yourself an injustice.", "\"스스로 부당하게 구시는군요."], ["No, sir! Pardon me!", "아니요, 선생님!"], ["You shall not—shall not in my office, if I know it—do yourself an injustice.", "실례지만! 제가 아는 한 — 제 사무실에서 — 스스로를 부당하게 대하시게 둘 수 없습니다."], ["You are interested in anything, and in everything, that relates to your friend.", "선생님은 친구와 관련된 일이라면 무엇이든, 모든 것에 관심이 있으십니다."], ["I know human nature much better, sir, than to admit for an instant that a gentleman of your appearance is not interested in whatever concerns his friend.”", "선생님, 저는 인간 본성을 훨씬 더 잘 압니다. 선생님 같은 풍모의 신사가 친구와 관계된 일에 관심이 없다고는 한순간도 인정할 수 없지요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"뭐,\""], ["replied Mr. Woodcourt, “that may be.", "우드코트 씨가 대답했습니다."], ["I am particularly interested in his address.”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지요. 나는 특히 그의 주소에 관심이 있습니다.\""]], [["“The number, sir,” said Mr.", "\"호수는, 선생님,\""], ["Vholes parenthetically, “I believe I have already mentioned. If Mr.", "볼스 씨가 덧붙이듯 말했습니다."], ["C. is to continue to play for this considerable stake, sir, he must have funds.", "\"이미 말씀드린 것 같습니다."], ["Understand me! There are funds in hand at present.", "C 씨가 이 상당한 판돈을 걸고 계속 게임을 하려면, 선생님, 자금이 있어야 합니다."], ["I ask for nothing; there are funds in hand.", "제 말을 이해해주십시오! 지금 당장은 수중에 자금이 있습니다."], ["But for the onward play, more funds must be provided, unless Mr.", "저는 아무것도 요구하지 않습니다. 수중에 자금이 있어요."], ["C. is to throw away what he has already ventured, which is wholly and solely a point for his consideration.", "하지만 게임을 계속하려면 더 많은 자금을 마련해야 합니다."], ["This, sir, I take the opportunity of stating openly to you as the friend of Mr.", "그러지 않으면 C 씨가 이미 건 것을 다 내던지는 셈이 되지요. 그건 전적으로, 오로지 C 씨가 고려할 문제입니다만."], ["C. Without funds I shall always be happy to appear and act for Mr.", "이 점을, 선생님, 저는 C 씨의 친구이신 선생님께 이렇게 솔직하게 말씀드릴 기회로 삼습니다."], ["C. to the extent of all such costs as are safe to be allowed out of the estate, not beyond that.", "자금이 없어도 저는 언제든 기꺼이 C 씨를 위해 나서고 행동하겠습니다 — 재산에서 안전하게 인정받을 수 있는 비용의 범위까지만요, 그 이상은 아닙니다."], ["I could not go beyond that, sir, without wronging some one.", "그 이상은, 선생님, 누군가에게 해를 끼치지 않고는 할 수 없습니다."], ["I must either wrong my three dear girls or my venerable father, who is entirely dependent on me, in the Vale of Taunton; or some one.", "저는 사랑하는 세 딸이나, 톤턴 골짜기에서 전적으로 저에게 의지하는 노쇠한 아버지나, 아니면 누군가에게 해를 끼쳐야 하지요."], ["Whereas, sir, my resolution is (call it weakness or folly if you please) to wrong no one.”", "그런데, 선생님, 제 결심은(약하다거나 어리석다고 하셔도 좋습니다) 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않는 것입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Woodcourt rather sternly rejoined that he was glad to hear it.", "우드코트 씨는 다소 엄하게, 그 말을 들으니 기쁘다고 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“I wish, sir,” said Mr.", "\"저는, 선생님,\""], ["Vholes, “to leave a good name behind me.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"좋은 이름을 남기고 싶습니다."], ["Therefore I take every opportunity of openly stating to a friend of Mr. C.", "그래서 C 씨가 어떤 처지에 있는지 그분 친구분께 솔직하게 말씀드릴 기회를 빠짐없이 잡는 것이지요."], ["how Mr. C.", "저 자신으로 말하자면, 선생님, 일꾼이 품삯을 받는 건 당연합니다."], ["is situated. As to myself, sir, the labourer is worthy of his hire.", "제가 수레바퀴에 어깨를 들이대기로 했으면 그렇게 하고, 받는 만큼 벌어들입니다."], ["If I undertake to put my shoulder to the wheel, I do it, and I earn what I get. I am here for that purpose.", "저는 그 목적으로 여기 있는 것이지요."], ["My name is painted on the door outside, with that object.”", "바깥문에 제 이름이 칠해져 있는 것도 그 목적에서입니다.\""]], [["“And Mr. Carstone’s address, Mr. Vholes?”", "\"그럼 카스톤 씨 주소는요, 볼스 씨?\""]], [["“Sir,” returned Mr.", "\"선생님,\""], ["Vholes, “as I believe I have already mentioned, it is next door.", "볼스 씨가 대답했습니다. \"이미 말씀드린 것 같습니다만, 바로 옆집입니다."], ["On the second story you will find Mr.", "이 층에 가시면 C 씨 거처가 있습니다."], ["C.’s apartments. Mr.", "C 씨가 자기 법률 자문 가까이 있고 싶어 하시는데, 저는 결코 마다하지 않습니다."], ["C. desires to be near his professional adviser, and I am far from objecting, for I court inquiry.”", "저는 오히려 조사받기를 청하니까요.\""]], [["Upon this Mr. Woodcourt wished Mr.", "이 말을 듣고 우드코트 씨는 볼스 씨에게 작별 인사를 하고 리처드를 찾아 나섰습니다."], ["Vholes good day and went in search of Richard, the change in whose appearance he began to understand now but too well.", "그는 이제 리처드의 달라진 모습을 너무도 잘 이해하기 시작했습니다."]], [["He found him in a dull room, fadedly furnished, much as I had found him in his barrack-room but a little while before, except that he was not writing but was sitting with a book before him, from which his eyes and thoughts were far astray.", "그는 칙칙한 방에서, 빛바랜 가구가 놓인 그 방에서 리처드를 만났습니다. 얼마 전 내가 막사 같은 그의 방에서 보았던 모습과 거의 같았지만, 다만 그는 글을 쓰고 있지 않고 앞에 책을 펼쳐둔 채 앉아 있었습니다."], ["As the door chanced to be standing open, Mr.", "그 책에서 그의 눈과 생각은 저 멀리 헤매고 있었지요."], ["Woodcourt was in his presence for some moments without being perceived, and he told me that he never could forget the haggardness of his face and the dejection of his manner before he was aroused from his dream.", "마침 문이 열려 있던 터라, 우드코트 씨는 잠시 그에게 들키지 않은 채 그 앞에 서 있었습니다. 그는 리처드가 몽상에서 깨어나기 전 그 수척한 얼굴과 풀 죽은 태도를 결코 잊을 수 없었다고 나에게 말했습니다."]], [["“Woodcourt, my dear fellow,”", "\"우드코트, 이 친구야.\""], ["cried Richard, starting up with extended hands, “you come upon my vision like a ghost.”", "리처드가 두 손을 내밀며 벌떡 일어나 외쳤습니다. \"자네는 유령처럼 내 눈앞에 나타나는군.\""]], [["“A friendly one,”", "\"친절한 유령이지.\""], ["he replied, “and only waiting, as they say ghosts do, to be addressed.", "그가 대답했습니다. \"그리고 유령이 으레 그러듯, 누가 말 걸어주기만 기다리고 있었네."], ["How does the mortal world go?”", "인간 세상은 어떻게 돌아가나?\""], ["They were seated now, near together.", "이제 두 사람은 가까이 마주 앉았습니다."]], [["“Badly enough, and slowly enough,”", "\"꽤나 나쁘게, 꽤나 더디게 돌아가지.\""], ["said Richard, “speaking at least for my part of it.”", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"적어도 내가 속한 쪽은 말이야.\""]], [["“What part is that?”", "\"그쪽이 어딘데?\""]], [["“The Chancery part.”", "\"대법원 쪽.\""]], [["“I never heard,” returned Mr.", "\"내 아직 그쪽이,\""], ["Woodcourt, shaking his head, “of its going well yet.”", "우드코트 씨가 고개를 저으며 대꾸했습니다. \"잘 돌아간다는 소리는 들어본 적이 없네.\""]], [["“Nor I,”", "\"나도 그래.\""], ["said Richard moodily.", "리처드가 시무룩하게 말했습니다."], ["“Who ever did?”", "\"그런 소릴 들어본 사람이 있겠나?\""], ["He brightened again in a moment and said with his natural openness, “Woodcourt, I should be sorry to be misunderstood by you, even if I gained by it in your estimation.", "그는 곧 다시 환해져서 타고난 솔직함으로 말했습니다. \"우드코트, 자네한테 오해받는 건 싫네, 설령 그 덕에 자네 눈에 더 좋게 비친다 해도 말이야."], ["You must know that I have done no good this long time.", "내가 오랫동안 아무 쓸모 있는 일도 못 했다는 건 자네도 알아야 하네."], ["I have not intended to do much harm, but I seem to have been capable of nothing else.", "큰 해를 끼치려던 건 아니었지만, 그 외엔 아무것도 할 줄 모르는 사람이 된 것 같아. 운명이 나를 몰아넣은 이 그물에서 빠져나와 있었다면 더 나았을지도 모르지."], ["It may be that I should have done better by keeping out of the net into which my destiny has worked me, but I think not, though I dare say you will soon hear, if you have not already heard, a very different opinion.", "하지만 그렇게 생각하진 않아. 자네가 곧, 아니 이미 들었을지도 모르지만, 아주 다른 의견을 듣게 되리란 건 분명하지만 말이야."], ["To make short of a long story, I am afraid I have wanted an object; but I have an object now—or it has me—and it is too late to discuss it.", "긴 이야기를 짧게 줄이자면, 내겐 목표가 없었던 것 같네. 하지만 이제는 목표가 있어 — 아니, 그 목표가 나를 붙들고 있다고 해야겠지 — 그리고 그걸 논하기엔 너무 늦었어."], ["Take me as I am, and make the best of me.”", "나를 있는 그대로 받아주고, 좋게 봐주게.\""]], [["“A bargain,”", "\"좋네, 약속이야.\""], ["said Mr. Woodcourt.", "우드코트 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Do as much by me in return.”", "\"그 대신 자네도 나한테 그래주게.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You,”", "자네는,\""], ["returned Richard, “you can pursue your art for its own sake, and can put your hand upon the plough and never turn, and can strike a purpose out of anything.", "리처드가 대꾸했습니다. \"자네는 예술 그 자체를 위해 자네 길을 추구할 수 있고, 쟁기에 손을 얹으면 결코 돌아서지 않으며, 무엇에서든 목적을 끌어낼 수 있는 사람이지."], ["You and I are very different creatures.”", "자네와 나는 아주 다른 존재야.\""]], [["He spoke regretfully and lapsed for a moment into his weary condition.", "그는 아쉬운 듯 말하고는 잠시 다시 그 지친 상태로 가라앉았습니다."]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"뭐, 됐어, 됐어!\""], ["he cried, shaking it off.", "그가 그 기분을 떨쳐내며 외쳤습니다."], ["“Everything has an end.", "\"모든 일엔 끝이 있는 법."], ["We shall see!", "두고 보자고!"], ["So you will take me as I am, and make the best of me?”", "그래, 자네는 나를 있는 그대로 받아주고, 좋게 봐주겠다는 거지?\""]], [["“Aye!", "\"그럼!"], ["Indeed I will.”", "정말 그러겠네.\""], ["They shook hands upon it laughingly, but in deep earnestness.", "두 사람은 웃으며 그 일을 두고 악수를 나누었지만, 그 속엔 깊은 진심이 담겨 있었습니다."], ["I can answer for one of them with my heart of hearts.", "그중 한 사람에 대해서라면 나는 진심을 다해 보증할 수 있습니다."]], [["“You come as a godsend,”", "\"자넨 하늘이 보내준 선물 같아.\""], ["said Richard, “for I have seen nobody here yet but Vholes.", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"여기서 아직 볼스 말고는 아무도 못 봤거든."], ["Woodcourt, there is one subject I should like to mention, for once and for all, in the beginning of our treaty.", "우드코트, 우리 약속을 시작하는 마당에 단 한 번, 영영 못 박아두고 싶은 이야기가 하나 있네."], ["You can hardly make the best of me if I don’t.", "그러지 않으면 자네가 나를 좋게 봐주기 어려울 테니까."], ["You know, I dare say, that I have an attachment to my cousin Ada?”", "내가 사촌 에이다에게 마음을 두고 있다는 건 자네도 알 테지?\""]], [["Mr. Woodcourt replied that I had hinted as much to him.", "우드코트 씨는 내가 그런 낌새를 비친 적이 있다고 대답했습니다."], ["“Now pray,”", "\"그럼 부탁인데,\""], ["returned Richard, “don’t think me a heap of selfishness.", "리처드가 받았습니다. \"나를 이기심 덩어리로 보진 말아주게."], ["Don’t suppose that I am splitting my head and half breaking my heart over this miserable Chancery suit for my own rights and interests alone.", "내가 이 비참한 대법원 소송에 머리를 쪼개고 가슴을 반쯤 찢어가며 매달리는 게 오로지 내 권리와 이익만을 위해서라고는 생각지 말게. 에이다의 것이 내 것과 묶여 있어."], ["Ada’s are bound up with mine; they can’t be separated; Vholes works for both of us.", "떼어낼 수가 없네. 볼스는 우리 둘 모두를 위해 일하는 거야."], ["Do think of that!”", "그걸 좀 생각해주게!\""]], [["He was so very solicitous on this head that Mr. Woodcourt gave him the strongest assurances that he did him no injustice.", "이 대목에 대해 그가 어찌나 간곡하던지, 우드코트 씨는 결코 그를 부당하게 보지 않는다고 가장 강하게 다짐해주었습니다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"있잖아,\" 리처드가 말했습니다."], ["said Richard, with something pathetic in his manner of lingering on the point, though it was off-hand and unstudied, “to an upright fellow like you, bringing a friendly face like yours here, I cannot bear the thought of appearing selfish and mean.", "무심하고 꾸밈없는 태도이긴 했지만 그 점에 매달리는 그의 모습엔 어딘가 애처로운 데가 있었지요. \"자네처럼 올곧은 친구가, 자네처럼 다정한 얼굴을 하고 여기까지 와줬는데, 내가 이기적이고 비열하게 보인다는 건 견딜 수가 없네."], ["I want to see Ada righted, Woodcourt, as well as myself; I want to do my utmost to right her, as well as myself; I venture what I can scrape together to extricate her, as well as myself.", "우드코트, 나는 나만큼이나 에이다도 정당한 몫을 받는 걸 보고 싶어. 나만큼이나 그녀를 위해서도 최선을 다하고 싶고, 나만큼이나 그녀를 구해내려고 긁어모을 수 있는 걸 다 걸고 있는 거야."], ["Do, I beseech you, think of that!”", "제발, 부탁이니 그걸 좀 생각해주게!\""]], [["Afterwards, when Mr. Woodcourt came to reflect on what had passed, he was so very much impressed by the strength of Richard’s anxiety on this point that in telling me generally of his first visit to Symond’s Inn he particularly dwelt upon it.", "나중에 우드코트 씨가 그동안 있었던 일을 곰곰이 되새겨보았을 때, 이 점에 대한 리처드의 불안이 어찌나 강렬했던지 깊은 인상을 받아서, 사이먼드 법학원을 처음 찾아간 일을 나에게 대강 들려주면서도 유독 그 대목에 오래 머물렀습니다."], ["It revived a fear I had had before that my dear girl’s little property would be absorbed by Mr.", "그것은 전부터 품고 있던 두려움을 되살렸습니다."], ["Vholes and that Richard’s justification to himself would be sincerely this.", "사랑하는 에이다의 그 작은 재산이 볼스 씨에게 다 빨려 들어가고, 리처드는 그것을 진심으로 이렇게 정당화하리라는 두려움 말입니다."], ["It was just as I began to take care of Caddy that the interview took place, and I now return to the time when Caddy had recovered and the shade was still between me and my darling.", "이 만남이 있었던 것은 마침 내가 캐디를 돌보기 시작했을 무렵이었습니다. 이제 나는 캐디가 회복되고도 나와 사랑하는 친구 사이에 여전히 그늘이 드리워 있던 그때로 돌아갑니다."]], [["I proposed to Ada that morning that we should go and see Richard.", "그날 아침 나는 에이다에게 리처드를 보러 가자고 했습니다."], ["It a little surprised me to find that she hesitated and was not so radiantly willing as I had expected.", "그런데 에이다가 머뭇거리며 내가 예상했던 것만큼 환하게 내켜 하지 않는 걸 보고 조금 놀랐습니다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘,\""], ["said I, “you have not had any difference with Richard since I have been so much away?”", "내가 말했습니다. \"내가 한참 집을 비운 사이에 리처드랑 무슨 다툼이라도 있었던 건 아니지?\""]], [["“No, Esther.”", "\"아니야, 에스더.\""]], [["“Not heard of him, perhaps?”", "\"그럼 혹시 그이 소식도 못 들었어?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, I have heard of him,”", "\"아니, 소식은 들었어.\""], ["said Ada.", "에이다가 말했습니다."]], [["Such tears in her eyes, and such love in her face.", "그 눈에 그렁그렁한 눈물, 그 얼굴에 어린 사랑."], ["I could not make my darling out.", "나는 도무지 사랑하는 친구의 속을 알 수 없었습니다."], ["Should I go to Richard’s by myself?", "내가 혼자 리처드네 갈까? 내가 물었습니다."], ["I said. No, Ada thought I had better not go by myself.", "아니, 에이다는 내가 혼자 가지 않는 게 낫겠다고 했습니다."], ["Would she go with me?", "그럼 같이 갈래?"], ["Yes, Ada thought she had better go with me.", "응, 에이다는 나와 함께 가는 게 낫겠다고 했습니다."], ["Should we go now?", "지금 갈까?"], ["Yes, let us go now.", "응, 지금 가자."], ["Well, I could not understand my darling, with the tears in her eyes and the love in her face!", "글쎄, 나는 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁하고 얼굴엔 사랑이 어린 사랑하는 친구의 속을 알 수가 없었습니다!"]], [["We were soon equipped and went out.", "우리는 곧 채비를 마치고 나섰습니다."], ["It was a sombre day, and drops of chill rain fell at intervals.", "음울한 날이었고, 차가운 빗방울이 이따금 떨어졌습니다."], ["It was one of those colourless days when everything looks heavy and harsh.", "모든 것이 무겁고 거칠어 보이는, 그런 빛깔 없는 날 가운데 하루였지요."], ["The houses frowned at us, the dust rose at us, the smoke swooped at us, nothing made any compromise about itself or wore a softened aspect.", "집들은 우리를 향해 찌푸리고, 먼지는 우리를 향해 일어나고, 연기는 우리를 향해 덮쳐들었으며, 무엇 하나 제 모습에 타협하거나 누그러진 얼굴을 하지 않았습니다."], ["I fancied my beautiful girl quite out of place in the rugged streets, and I thought there were more funerals passing along the dismal pavements than I had ever seen before.", "나는 험한 거리에서 아름다운 친구가 영 어울리지 않는다는 생각이 들었고, 그 음침한 보도 위로 여태 본 것보다 더 많은 장례 행렬이 지나가는 것만 같았습니다."]], [["We had first to find out Symond’s Inn.", "우리는 먼저 사이먼드 법학원을 찾아내야 했습니다."], ["We were going to inquire in a shop when Ada said she thought it was near Chancery Lane.", "어느 가게에 들어가 물어보려는데 에이다가 챈서리 레인 근처인 것 같다고 했습니다."], ["“We are not likely to be far out, my love, if we go in that direction,”", "\"그쪽으로 가면, 얘, 그리 멀리 벗어나진 않겠다.\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["said I. So to Chancery Lane we went, and there, sure enough, we saw it written up.", "그래서 우리는 챈서리 레인으로 갔고, 거기서 정말로 그 이름이 적혀 있는 걸 보았습니다."], ["Symond’s Inn.", "사이먼드 법학원."]], [["We had next to find out the number.", "다음으로는 호수를 찾아야 했습니다."], ["“Or Mr. Vholes’s office will do,”", "\"아니면 볼스 씨 사무실도 괜찮겠다.\""], ["I recollected, “for Mr.", "나는 떠올렸습니다."], ["Vholes’s office is next door.”", "\"볼스 씨 사무실이 바로 옆집이니까.\""], ["Upon which Ada said, perhaps that was Mr. Vholes’s office in the corner there.", "그러자 에이다가 저기 모퉁이에 있는 게 볼스 씨 사무실인지도 모르겠다고 했습니다."], ["And it really was.", "그리고 정말 그랬습니다."]], [["Then came the question, which of the two next doors?", "그다음엔 옆집 둘 중 어느 쪽이냐 하는 문제가 남았습니다."], ["I was going for the one, and my darling was going for the other; and my darling was right again.", "나는 이쪽으로 가려 했고 사랑하는 친구는 저쪽으로 가려 했는데, 이번에도 사랑하는 친구가 옳았습니다."], ["So up we went to the second story, when we came to Richard’s name in great white letters on a hearse-like panel.", "그래서 우리는 삼 층으로 올라갔고, 거기서 영구차 같은 검은 판자에 큼직한 흰 글씨로 적힌 리처드의 이름과 마주쳤습니다."]], [["I should have knocked, but Ada said perhaps we had better turn the handle and go in.", "내가 문을 두드리려 했지만, 에이다가 그냥 손잡이를 돌리고 들어가는 게 낫겠다고 했습니다."], ["Thus we came to Richard, poring over a table covered with dusty bundles of papers which seemed to me like dusty mirrors reflecting his own mind.", "그렇게 우리는 리처드에게 이르렀습니다. 그는 먼지투성이 서류 뭉치로 뒤덮인 책상을 골똘히 들여다보고 있었는데, 그 서류들은 마치 그의 마음을 비추는 먼지투성이 거울처럼 보였습니다."], ["Wherever I looked I saw the ominous words that ran in it repeated.", "어디를 보든 그 속에 흐르는 불길한 글자가 되풀이되어 보였지요."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스."]], [["He received us very affectionately, and we sat down.", "그는 우리를 아주 다정하게 맞았고, 우리는 자리에 앉았습니다."], ["“If you had come a little earlier,”", "\"조금만 더 일찍 왔으면,\""], ["he said, “you would have found Woodcourt here.", "그가 말했습니다. \"우드코트를 봤을 텐데."], ["There never was such a good fellow as Woodcourt is.", "우드코트 같은 좋은 친구는 세상에 다시없어."], ["He finds time to look in between-whiles, when anybody else with half his work to do would be thinking about not being able to come.", "다른 사람 같으면 그 절반의 일만 있어도 도저히 못 온다고 생각할 텐데, 그 친구는 짬짬이 들여다볼 시간을 내거든."], ["And he is so cheery, so fresh, so sensible, so earnest, so—everything that I am not, that the place brightens whenever he comes, and darkens whenever he goes again.”", "게다가 어찌나 쾌활하고, 어찌나 생기 넘치고, 어찌나 분별 있고, 어찌나 진실하고, 어찌나 — 내가 못 가진 모든 걸 다 갖췄는지, 그 친구가 오면 이곳이 환해지고, 다시 가버리면 어두워진다네.\""]], [["“God bless him,”", "\"그가 나에게 보여준 진심을 봐서라도, 하느님께서 그를 축복하시기를.\""], ["I thought, “for his truth to me!”", "나는 생각했습니다."]], [["“He is not so sanguine, Ada,”", "\"그 친구는 에이다, 볼스나 나만큼 낙관하진 않아.\""], ["continued Richard, casting his dejected look over the bundles of papers, “as Vholes and I are usually, but he is only an outsider and is not in the mysteries.", "리처드가 서류 뭉치 위로 풀 죽은 시선을 던지며 말을 이었습니다. \"하지만 그 친구는 그저 외부인이고 그 비밀 속에 들어와 있지 않으니까."], ["We have gone into them, and he has not.", "우린 그 속으로 들어갔지만 그 친구는 안 그랬거든."], ["He can’t be expected to know much of such a labyrinth.”", "이런 미궁을 그 친구가 많이 알 거라고 기대할 수야 없지.\""]], [["As his look wandered over the papers again and he passed his two hands over his head, I noticed how sunken and how large his eyes appeared, how dry his lips were, and how his finger-nails were all bitten away.", "그의 시선이 다시 서류 위를 헤매고 두 손으로 머리를 쓸어 넘기는 동안, 나는 그의 눈이 얼마나 퀭하고 커 보이는지, 입술이 얼마나 메말랐는지, 손톱이 죄다 물어뜯겨 사라진 것을 알아챘습니다."]], [["“Is this a healthy place to live in, Richard, do you think?”", "\"리처드, 여기가 살기에 건강한 곳이라고 생각해?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Why, my dear Minerva,”", "\"아니, 내 사랑스러운 미네르바,\" 리처드가 예전의 명랑한 웃음을 띠며 대답했습니다."], ["answered Richard with his old gay laugh, “it is neither a rural nor a cheerful place; and when the sun shines here, you may lay a pretty heavy wager that it is shining brightly in an open spot.", "\"전원도 아니고 유쾌한 곳도 아니지. 여기 해가 비치면, 어디 탁 트인 곳엔 해가 쨍쨍하게 비치고 있을 거라고 꽤 큰돈을 걸어도 좋아."], ["But it’s well enough for the time.", "하지만 당분간은 그런대로 괜찮아."], ["It’s near the offices and near Vholes.”", "사무소들이 가깝고 볼스가 가깝거든.\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["I hinted, “a change from both—”", "내가 넌지시 말했습니다. \"둘 다에서 좀 떨어지는 게—\""]], [["“Might do me good?”", "\"나한테 좋을 거란 말이지?\""], ["said Richard, forcing a laugh as he finished the sentence.", "리처드가 그 문장을 끝맺으며 억지로 웃었습니다."], ["“I shouldn’t wonder!", "\"그럴 만도 하지!"], ["But it can only come in one way now—in one of two ways, I should rather say.", "하지만 이젠 한 가지 길로만 그렇게 될 수 있어 — 두 가지 길 중 하나라고 해야겠군."], ["Either the suit must be ended, Esther, or the suitor.", "에스더, 소송이 끝나거나, 아니면 소송인이 끝나거나 둘 중 하나야."], ["But it shall be the suit, my dear girl, the suit, my dear girl!”", "하지만 끝나는 건 소송일 거야, 내 사랑스러운 친구야, 소송 말이야, 내 사랑스러운 친구야!\""]], [["These latter words were addressed to Ada, who was sitting nearest to him.", "이 마지막 말은 그에게 가장 가까이 앉아 있던 에이다에게 한 것이었습니다."], ["Her face being turned away from me and towards him, I could not see it.", "에이다의 얼굴이 내게서 돌려져 그를 향하고 있어서, 나는 그 표정을 볼 수 없었습니다."]], [["“We are doing very well,”", "\"우린 아주 잘하고 있어.\""], ["pursued Richard.", "리처드가 말을 이었습니다."], ["“Vholes will tell you so.", "\"볼스가 그렇게 말해줄 거야."], ["We are really spinning along.", "우린 정말 술술 나아가고 있다고."], ["Ask Vholes.", "볼스한테 물어봐."], ["We are giving them no rest.", "우린 저들에게 쉴 틈을 안 줘."], ["Vholes knows all their windings and turnings, and we are upon them everywhere.", "볼스는 저들의 온갖 굽이와 길목을 다 알고 있고, 우린 어디서든 저들을 바짝 쫓고 있어."], ["We have astonished them already.", "우린 벌써 저들을 깜짝 놀라게 했어."], ["We shall rouse up that nest of sleepers, mark my words!”", "저 잠꾸러기 둥지를 발칵 뒤집어놓고 말 테니, 내 말 명심해!\""]], [["His hopefulness had long been more painful to me than his despondency; it was so unlike hopefulness, had something so fierce in its determination to be it, was so hungry and eager, and yet so conscious of being forced and unsustainable that it had long touched me to the heart.", "그의 희망찬 모습은 이미 오래전부터 그의 절망보다도 나를 더 아프게 했습니다. 그것은 희망과는 너무도 달랐고, 기어이 희망이고자 하는 결의에 어딘가 사납기까지 했으며, 너무도 굶주리고 간절하면서도, 그러면서도 그것이 억지로 짜낸 것이고 지탱될 수 없는 것임을 너무도 잘 알고 있어서, 오래도록 내 가슴을 저몄습니다."], ["But the commentary upon it now indelibly written in his handsome face made it far more distressing than it used to be.", "그러나 이제 그 잘생긴 얼굴에 지울 수 없이 새겨진 그 주석은, 그것을 예전보다 훨씬 더 가슴 아프게 만들었습니다."], ["I say indelibly, for I felt persuaded that if the fatal cause could have been for ever terminated, according to his brightest visions, in that same hour, the traces of the premature anxiety, self-reproach, and disappointment it had occasioned him would have remained upon his features to the hour of his death.", "지울 수 없다고 말하는 까닭은, 설령 그 운명의 소송이 그가 가장 환하게 그리던 대로 바로 그 시각에 영영 끝맺어진다 해도, 그것이 그에게 안겨준 때 이른 불안과 자책과 실망의 흔적은 죽는 순간까지 그 얼굴에 남아 있으리라고 나는 굳게 믿었기 때문입니다."]], [["“The sight of our dear little woman,”", "\"우리 사랑스러운 꼬마 아주머니를 보고 있으면,\" 리처드가 말했습니다."], ["said Richard, Ada still remaining silent and quiet, “is so natural to me, and her compassionate face is so like the face of old days—”", "에이다는 여전히 말없이 가만히 있었지요. \"어찌나 마음이 편한지, 그 가엾어하는 얼굴이 옛날 그 얼굴과 어찌나 똑같은지—\""]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["No, no.", "아니, 아니에요."], ["I smiled and shook my head.", "나는 미소 지으며 고개를 저었습니다."]], [["“—So exactly like the face of old days,”", "\"—옛날 그 얼굴과 어찌나 꼭 같은지,\""], ["said Richard in his cordial voice, and taking my hand with the brotherly regard which nothing ever changed, “that I can’t make pretences with her.", "리처드가 무엇으로도 변치 않는 형제 같은 정으로 내 손을 잡으며 다정한 목소리로 말했습니다. \"이 아주머니한테는 꾸며댈 수가 없어."], ["I fluctuate a little; that’s the truth.", "솔직히 말하면 난 좀 흔들려."], ["Sometimes I hope, my dear, and sometimes I—don’t quite despair, but nearly.", "어떤 땐 희망을 품다가, 어떤 땐 — 아주 절망하진 않지만, 거의 그래."], ["I get,”", "난 말이야,\""], ["said Richard, relinquishing my hand gently and walking across the room, “so tired!”", "리처드가 내 손을 가만히 놓고 방을 가로질러 걸으며 말했습니다. \"너무 지쳐!\""]], [["He took a few turns up and down and sunk upon the sofa.", "그는 방 안을 몇 번 오가다가 소파에 털썩 주저앉았습니다."], ["“I get,”", "\"난 너무,\""], ["he repeated gloomily, “so tired.", "그가 침울하게 되뇌었습니다. \"지쳤어."], ["It is such weary, weary work!”", "이건 정말 고되고 고된 일이야!\""]], [["He was leaning on his arm saying these words in a meditative voice and looking at the ground when my darling rose, put off her bonnet, kneeled down beside him with her golden hair falling like sunlight on his head, clasped her two arms round his neck, and turned her face to me.", "그가 팔에 기댄 채 생각에 잠긴 목소리로 이 말을 하며 바닥을 내려다보고 있을 때, 사랑하는 친구가 일어나 모자를 벗고는 그의 곁에 무릎을 꿇었습니다. 황금빛 머리카락이 햇살처럼 그의 머리 위로 흘러내렸지요."], ["Oh, what a loving and devoted face I saw!", "그녀는 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸고 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 돌렸습니다. 오, 내가 본 그 얼굴은 얼마나 사랑이 가득하고 헌신적이던지!"]], [["“Esther, dear,”", "\"에스더, 얘.\""], ["she said very quietly, “I am not going home again.”", "그녀가 아주 조용히 말했습니다. \"나, 다시는 집에 안 갈 거야.\""]], [["A light shone in upon me all at once.", "문득 한 줄기 빛이 내 안으로 비쳐 들었습니다."]], [["“Never any more.", "\"이제 두 번 다시."], ["I am going to stay with my dear husband.", "나는 사랑하는 남편과 함께 있을 거야."], ["We have been married above two months.", "우린 결혼한 지 두 달이 넘었어."], ["Go home without me, my own Esther; I shall never go home any more!”", "나 없이 집에 가, 내 에스더. 나는 두 번 다시 집에 안 갈 거야!\""], ["With those words my darling drew his head down on her breast and held it there.", "이 말과 함께 사랑하는 친구는 그의 머리를 자기 가슴으로 끌어당겨 거기 꼭 안았습니다."], ["And if ever in my life I saw a love that nothing but death could change, I saw it then before me.", "그리고 내 평생 죽음 말고는 무엇으로도 변할 수 없는 사랑을 본 적이 있다면, 바로 그때 내 눈앞에서 그것을 보았습니다."]], [["“Speak to Esther, my dearest,”", "\"에스더한테 말해, 여보.\""], ["said Richard, breaking the silence presently.", "리처드가 이윽고 침묵을 깨며 말했습니다."], ["“Tell her how it was.”", "\"어떻게 된 일인지 들려줘.\""]], [["I met her before she could come to me and folded her in my arms.", "그녀가 내게 오기도 전에 내가 먼저 다가가 두 팔로 끌어안았습니다."], ["We neither of us spoke, but with her cheek against my own I wanted to hear nothing.", "우리 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 그녀의 뺨이 내 뺨에 닿아 있으니 나는 아무 말도 듣고 싶지 않았습니다. \"우리 아가.\""], ["“My pet,” said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“My love.", "\"내 사랑."], ["My poor, poor girl!”", "가엾은, 가엾은 우리 아가!\""], ["I pitied her so much.", "나는 그녀가 너무도 가여웠습니다."], ["I was very fond of Richard, but the impulse that I had upon me was to pity her so much.", "나는 리처드를 무척 아꼈지만, 그 순간 나를 사로잡은 충동은 그녀가 너무도 가엾다는 것이었습니다."]], [["“Esther, will you forgive me?", "\"에스더, 날 용서해줄래?"], ["Will my cousin John forgive me?”", "사촌 존도 날 용서해줄까?\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘,\""], ["said I, “to doubt it for a moment is to do him a great wrong.", "내가 말했습니다. \"그걸 한순간이라도 의심하는 건 그분께 큰 잘못을 저지르는 거야."], ["And as to me!”", "그리고 나로 말하면!\""], ["Why, as to me, what had I to forgive!", "아니, 나로 말하면, 내가 뭘 용서할 게 있다고!"]], [["I dried my sobbing darling’s eyes and sat beside her on the sofa, and Richard sat on my other side; and while I was reminded of that so different night when they had first taken me into their confidence and had gone on in their own wild happy way, they told me between them how it was.", "나는 흐느끼는 사랑하는 친구의 눈물을 닦아주고 소파에서 그녀 곁에 앉았으며, 리처드는 내 다른 쪽에 앉았습니다. 그리고 그토록 다른 그날 밤이 떠올랐지요. 두 사람이 처음 나에게 속내를 털어놓고 그들 특유의 분방하고 행복한 방식으로 이야기를 이어가던 그 밤 말입니다. 두 사람은 번갈아가며 어떻게 된 일인지 들려주었습니다."]], [["“All I had was Richard’s,”", "\"내가 가진 건 다 리처드 거였어.\" 에이다가 말했습니다."], ["Ada said; “and Richard would not take it, Esther, and what could I do but be his wife when I loved him dearly!”", "\"그런데 리처드가 받으려 하지 않았어, 에스더. 그토록 사랑하는데, 그이의 아내가 되는 것 말고 내가 뭘 할 수 있었겠어!\""]], [["“And you were so fully and so kindly occupied, excellent Dame Durden,”", "\"게다가 당신은 그토록 알차고 그토록 친절하게 바빴잖아, 훌륭한 더든 아주머니.\""], ["said Richard, “that how could we speak to you at such a time!", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"그런 때에 어떻게 당신한테 말을 꺼낼 수 있었겠어!"], ["And besides, it was not a long-considered step.", "게다가 오래 고민한 일도 아니었어."], ["We went out one morning and were married.”", "우린 어느 날 아침 나가서 결혼해버렸거든.\""]], [["“And when it was done, Esther,”", "\"그리고 그 일을 치르고 나서, 에스더.\""], ["said my darling, “I was always thinking how to tell you and what to do for the best.", "사랑하는 친구가 말했습니다. \"나는 늘 너한테 어떻게 말할까, 어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을까 고민했어."], ["And sometimes I thought you ought to know it directly, and sometimes I thought you ought not to know it and keep it from my cousin John; and I could not tell what to do, and I fretted very much.”", "어떤 땐 네가 당장 알아야 한다고 생각했고, 어떤 땐 네가 몰라야 하고 사촌 존한테도 숨겨야 한다고 생각했어. 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수가 없어서 무척 애를 태웠지.\""]], [["How selfish I must have been not to have thought of this before!", "이걸 진작 생각지 못했다니 나는 얼마나 이기적이었던 걸까요!"], ["I don’t know what I said now.", "그때 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 모르겠습니다."], ["I was so sorry, and yet I was so fond of them and so glad that they were fond of me; I pitied them so much, and yet I felt a kind of pride in their loving one another.", "나는 너무도 미안했지만, 그러면서도 두 사람이 너무도 좋았고 그들이 나를 좋아해줘서 너무도 기뻤습니다. 두 사람이 너무도 가여웠지만, 그러면서도 그들이 서로 사랑한다는 데 일종의 자랑스러움을 느꼈지요."], ["I never had experienced such painful and pleasurable emotion at one time, and in my own heart I did not know which predominated.", "한꺼번에 그토록 괴로우면서도 즐거운 감정을 느껴본 적은 없었고, 내 마음속에서도 어느 쪽이 더 큰지 알 수 없었습니다."], ["But I was not there to darken their way; I did not do that.", "하지만 나는 그들의 앞길을 어둡게 하려고 거기 있는 게 아니었습니다. 나는 그러지 않았습니다."]], [["When I was less foolish and more composed, my darling took her wedding-ring from her bosom, and kissed it, and put it on.", "내가 덜 어리석어지고 좀 더 차분해지자, 사랑하는 친구가 가슴에서 결혼반지를 꺼내 입을 맞추고는 손가락에 끼웠습니다."], ["Then I remembered last night and told Richard that ever since her marriage she had worn it at night when there was no one to see.", "그제야 나는 지난밤이 떠올라, 리처드에게 그녀가 결혼한 뒤로 줄곧 아무도 보지 않는 밤이면 그 반지를 끼고 있었다고 말했습니다."], ["Then Ada blushingly asked me how did I know that, my dear.", "그러자 에이다가 얼굴을 붉히며, 얘, 그걸 어떻게 알았느냐고 물었습니다."], ["Then I told Ada how I had seen her hand concealed under her pillow and had little thought why, my dear.", "그래서 나는 그녀가 베개 밑에 손을 감추고 있는 걸 보고도, 얘, 왜인지는 거의 짐작도 못 했다고 말했지요."], ["Then they began telling me how it was all over again, and I began to be sorry and glad again, and foolish again, and to hide my plain old face as much as I could lest I should put them out of heart.", "그러자 두 사람은 다시 처음부터 어떻게 된 일인지 들려주기 시작했고, 나는 다시 미안하고 또 기뻤다가, 다시 어리석어졌고, 두 사람의 기를 꺾을세라 못생긴 늙은 내 얼굴을 한껏 감추려 했습니다."]], [["Thus the time went on until it became necessary for me to think of returning.", "그렇게 시간이 흘러 마침내 나는 돌아갈 일을 생각해야 했습니다."], ["When that time arrived it was the worst of all, for then my darling completely broke down.", "그때가 닥치니 무엇보다도 견디기 힘들었습니다. 사랑하는 친구가 완전히 무너져버렸거든요."], ["She clung round my neck, calling me by every dear name she could think of and saying what should she do without me!", "그녀는 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 이름으로 나를 부르며 내 목에 매달려, 나 없이 어떻게 살겠느냐고 했습니다! 리처드도 별반 낫지 않았지요."], ["Nor was Richard much better; and as for me, I should have been the worst of the three if I had not severely said to myself, “Now Esther, if you do, I’ll never speak to you again!”", "그리고 나로 말하면, 만약 내가 스스로에게 엄하게 \"자, 에스더, 그러면 너랑 다시는 말도 안 할 거야!\" 하고 다그치지 않았다면 셋 중 가장 형편없었을 겁니다."]], [["“Why, I declare,”", "\"아니, 세상에.\""], ["said I, “I never saw such a wife.", "내가 말했습니다. \"이런 아내는 처음 봐."], ["I don’t think she loves her husband at all.", "남편을 도무지 사랑하지 않는 것 같다니까."], ["Here, Richard, take my child, for goodness’ sake.”", "자, 리처드, 제발 이 애를 좀 데려가요.\""], ["But I held her tight all the while, and could have wept over her I don’t know how long.", "하지만 나는 내내 그녀를 꼭 끌어안고 있었고, 얼마나 오래인지 모르게 그녀를 붙들고 울 수도 있었습니다."]], [["“I give this dear young couple notice,”", "\"이 사랑스러운 젊은 부부에게 일러두는데,\""], ["said I, “that I am only going away to come back to-morrow and that I shall be always coming backwards and forwards until Symond’s Inn is tired of the sight of me.", "내가 말했습니다. \"나는 그저 내일 다시 오려고 가는 거고, 사이먼드 법학원이 내 꼴을 지겨워할 때까지 늘 들락날락할 거예요."], ["So I shall not say good-bye, Richard.", "그러니 잘 있으라는 말은 안 할래, 리처드."], ["For what would be the use of that, you know, when I am coming back so soon!”", "이렇게 곧 돌아올 텐데 그런 말이 무슨 소용이겠어!\""]], [["I had given my darling to him now, and I meant to go; but I lingered for one more look of the precious face which it seemed to rive my heart to turn from.", "이제 나는 사랑하는 친구를 그에게 넘겨주었고, 그만 가려 했습니다. 하지만 등을 돌리려니 가슴이 찢어지는 것만 같은 그 소중한 얼굴을 한 번 더 보려고 머뭇거렸습니다."]], [["So I said (in a merry bustling manner) that unless they gave me some encouragement to come back, I was not sure that I could take that liberty, upon which my dear girl looked up, faintly smiling through her tears, and I folded her lovely face between my hands, and gave it one last kiss, and laughed, and ran away.", "그래서 나는 (명랑하게 부산을 떨며) 돌아오라고 좀 부추겨주지 않으면 내가 그렇게 마음대로 굴어도 될지 모르겠다고 말했고, 그러자 사랑하는 친구가 눈물 사이로 희미하게 미소 지으며 올려다보았습니다. 나는 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 두 손으로 감싸고 마지막으로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 웃으며 달려나왔습니다."]], [["And when I got downstairs, oh, how I cried!", "그리고 아래층에 내려와서는, 오, 얼마나 울었던지!"], ["It almost seemed to me that I had lost my Ada for ever.", "마치 내 에이다를 영영 잃어버린 것만 같았습니다."], ["I was so lonely and so blank without her, and it was so desolate to be going home with no hope of seeing her there, that I could get no comfort for a little while as I walked up and down in a dim corner sobbing and crying.", "그녀가 없으니 너무도 외롭고 텅 빈 것 같았고, 거기서 그녀를 볼 희망도 없이 집으로 돌아간다는 게 너무도 쓸쓸해서, 어둑한 구석을 오가며 흐느껴 우는 동안 한동안은 도무지 마음을 달랠 수 없었습니다."]], [["I came to myself by and by, after a little scolding, and took a coach home. The poor boy whom I had found at St.", "이윽고 나는 스스로를 조금 나무라고 나서 정신을 차렸고, 삯마차를 타고 집으로 갔습니다."], ["Albans had reappeared a short time before and was lying at the point of death; indeed, was then dead, though I did not know it.", "세인트 올번스에서 만났던 그 가엾은 소년이 얼마 전 다시 나타나 죽음의 문턱에 누워 있었지요. 실은 그때 이미 죽었지만, 나는 그걸 몰랐습니다."], ["My guardian had gone out to inquire about him and did not return to dinner.", "후견인은 그 아이 소식을 알아보러 나가서 저녁 식사 때까지 돌아오지 않았습니다."], ["Being quite alone, I cried a little again, though on the whole I don’t think I behaved so very, very ill.", "혼자가 되니 나는 또 조금 울었지만, 대체로 보면 그렇게 아주아주 못나게 군 것 같지는 않습니다."]], [["It was only natural that I should not be quite accustomed to the loss of my darling yet.", "사랑하는 친구를 잃은 일에 아직 도무지 익숙해지지 못한 건 당연한 노릇이었지요."], ["Three or four hours were not a long time after years.", "여러 해를 함께한 뒤의 서너 시간은 긴 시간이 아니었으니까요."], ["But my mind dwelt so much upon the uncongenial scene in which I had left her, and I pictured it as such an overshadowed stony-hearted one, and I so longed to be near her and taking some sort of care of her, that I determined to go back in the evening only to look up at her windows.", "하지만 내 마음은 내가 그녀를 두고 온 그 정 없는 풍경에 너무도 자꾸 머물렀고, 나는 그곳을 그토록 그늘지고 매정한 곳으로 그려보았으며, 그녀 곁에서 어떻게든 그녀를 돌봐주고 싶은 마음이 너무도 간절해서, 저녁에 그저 그녀의 창문이라도 올려다보러 다시 가기로 마음먹었습니다."]], [["It was foolish, I dare say, but it did not then seem at all so to me, and it does not seem quite so even now.", "어리석은 짓이었겠지요. 하지만 그때는 전혀 그렇게 여겨지지 않았고, 지금도 그리 어리석게만 여겨지지는 않습니다."], ["I took Charley into my confidence, and we went out at dusk.", "나는 찰리에게만 속내를 털어놓고, 우리는 땅거미 질 무렵 나섰습니다."], ["It was dark when we came to the new strange home of my dear girl, and there was a light behind the yellow blinds.", "사랑하는 친구의 낯설고 새로운 보금자리에 이르렀을 때는 어두웠고, 노란 차양 뒤로 불빛이 비쳤습니다."], ["We walked past cautiously three or four times, looking up, and narrowly missed encountering Mr.", "우리는 올려다보며 서너 차례 조심스레 그 앞을 지나쳤는데, 하마터면 볼스 씨와 마주칠 뻔했습니다."], ["Vholes, who came out of his office while we were there and turned his head to look up too before going home.", "우리가 거기 있는 동안 그가 사무실에서 나와, 집으로 가기 전에 그 역시 고개를 돌려 위를 올려다보았거든요."], ["The sight of his lank black figure and the lonesome air of that nook in the dark were favourable to the state of my mind.", "그 깡마른 검은 형체와 어둠 속 그 후미진 곳의 쓸쓸한 분위기는 내 마음 상태에 들어맞았습니다."], ["I thought of the youth and love and beauty of my dear girl, shut up in such an ill-assorted refuge, almost as if it were a cruel place.", "나는 그토록 어울리지 않는 피난처에 갇힌 사랑하는 친구의 젊음과 사랑과 아름다움을 떠올렸는데, 거의 그곳이 잔인한 곳이기라도 한 것만 같았습니다."]], [["It was very solitary and very dull, and I did not doubt that I might safely steal upstairs.", "거기는 무척 외지고 무척 칙칙했고, 나는 들키지 않고 위층으로 살그머니 올라가도 되겠다고 의심치 않았습니다."], ["I left Charley below and went up with a light foot, not distressed by any glare from the feeble oil lanterns on the way.", "나는 찰리를 아래에 두고 가벼운 발걸음으로 올라갔는데, 오는 길에 희미한 기름 등불의 불빛이 비쳐도 거북하지 않았습니다."], ["I listened for a few moments, and in the musty rotting silence of the house believed that I could hear the murmur of their young voices.", "잠시 귀를 기울이니, 그 집의 곰팡내 나는 썩어가는 정적 속에서 두 사람의 젊은 목소리가 두런대는 소리가 들리는 듯했습니다."], ["I put my lips to the hearse-like panel of the door as a kiss for my dear and came quietly down again, thinking that one of these days I would confess to the visit.", "나는 사랑하는 이에게 보내는 입맞춤으로 영구차 같은 문 판자에 입술을 댄 뒤, 조용히 다시 내려왔습니다. 언젠가 이 방문을 고백하리라 생각하면서요."]], [["And it really did me good, for though nobody but Charley and I knew anything about it, I somehow felt as if it had diminished the separation between Ada and me and had brought us together again for those moments.", "그리고 그 일은 정말로 나에게 위안이 되었습니다. 찰리와 나 말고는 아무도 그 일을 몰랐지만, 그래도 어쩐지 그것이 에이다와 나 사이의 거리를 좁혀주고, 그 잠깐 동안만이라도 우리를 다시 이어준 것만 같았거든요."], ["I went back, not quite accustomed yet to the change, but all the better for that hovering about my darling.", "나는 그 변화에 아직 도무지 익숙해지지 못한 채 돌아왔지만, 그래도 사랑하는 친구의 곁을 그렇게 맴돌고 나니 한결 나았습니다."]], [["My guardian had come home and was standing thoughtfully by the dark window.", "후견인은 집에 돌아와 어두운 창가에 생각에 잠겨 서 있었습니다."], ["When I went in, his face cleared and he came to his seat, but he caught the light upon my face as I took mine.", "내가 들어가자 그의 얼굴이 밝아지며 그는 자기 자리로 왔지만, 내가 자리에 앉을 때 내 얼굴에 비친 빛을 보았습니다."]], [["“Little woman,”", "\"꼬마 아주머니,\""], ["said he, “You have been crying.”", "그가 말했습니다. \"울었구나.\""]], [["“Why, yes, guardian,”", "\"네, 그게, 후견인.\""], ["said I, “I am afraid I have been, a little.", "내가 말했습니다. \"조금 운 것 같아요."], ["Ada has been in such distress, and is so very sorry, guardian.”", "에이다가 너무 괴로워하고, 너무 미안해해서요, 후견인.\""]], [["I put my arm on the back of his chair, and I saw in his glance that my words and my look at her empty place had prepared him.", "나는 그의 의자 등받이에 팔을 얹었고, 내 말과 그녀의 빈자리를 바라보는 내 눈길이 그를 미리 마음의 준비를 시켰음을 그의 눈빛에서 보았습니다."]], [["“Is she married, my dear?”", "\"그 애가 결혼했니, 얘야?\""]], [["I told him all about it and how her first entreaties had referred to his forgiveness.", "나는 그 모든 일과, 그녀가 맨 먼저 간청한 것이 그의 용서에 관한 것이었음을 들려주었습니다."]], [["“She has no need of it,”", "\"용서받을 일이 없지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“Heaven bless her and her husband!”", "\"하늘이 그 애와 그 남편을 축복하시기를!\""], ["But just as my first impulse had been to pity her, so was his.", "하지만 내 첫 충동이 그녀를 가여워하는 것이었듯, 그의 충동도 그러했습니다."], ["“Poor girl, poor girl!", "\"가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!"], ["Poor Rick!", "가엾은 릭!"], ["Poor Ada!”", "가엾은 에이다!\""]], [["Neither of us spoke after that, until he said with a sigh, “Well, well, my dear!", "그 뒤로 우리 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 그가 한숨을 쉬며 말했습니다. \"그래, 그래, 얘야!"], ["Bleak House is thinning fast.”", "황폐한 집이 빠르게 비어가는구나.\""]], [["“But its mistress remains, guardian.”", "\"하지만 안주인은 남아 있어요, 후견인.\""], ["Though I was timid about saying it, I ventured because of the sorrowful tone in which he had spoken.", "그렇게 말하기가 조심스러웠지만, 그가 말한 슬픈 어조 때문에 용기를 냈습니다. \"안주인이 이 집을 행복하게 만들려고 할 수 있는 건 다 할 거예요.\""], ["“She will do all she can to make it happy,” said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“She will succeed, my love!”", "\"그 애는 해낼 거다, 내 사랑!\""]], [["The letter had made no difference between us except that the seat by his side had come to be mine; it made none now.", "그 편지는 우리 사이에 아무것도 달라지게 하지 않았습니다. 다만 그의 곁자리가 내 자리가 되었다는 것뿐이었지요."], ["He turned his old bright fatherly look upon me, laid his hand on my hand in his old way, and said again, “She will succeed, my dear.", "지금도 달라진 건 없었습니다. 그는 예전의 그 환하고 자애로운 눈길을 내게 돌리고, 예전 그대로 내 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 다시 말했습니다."], ["Nevertheless, Bleak House is thinning fast, O little woman!”", "\"그 애는 해낼 거다, 얘야. 그래도 황폐한 집은 빠르게 비어가는구나, 오, 꼬마 아주머니!\""]], [["I was sorry presently that this was all we said about that.", "이내 나는 그 일에 대해 우리가 나눈 말이 이게 다라는 것이 아쉬웠습니다."], ["I was rather disappointed.", "다소 실망스러웠지요."], ["I feared I might not quite have been all I had meant to be since the letter and the answer.", "그 편지와 그 답장 이후로 내가 되고자 했던 그 모든 모습이 되지 못한 건 아닐까 두려웠습니다."]], [["Obstinacy\nBut one other day had intervened when, early in the morning as we were going to breakfast, Mr.", "하루를 더 사이에 두고서야, 아침 일찍 우리가 막 아침을 먹으러 가려던 참에, 우드코트 씨가 황급히 들어와 끔찍한 소식을 전했습니다."], ["Woodcourt came in haste with the astounding news that a terrible murder had been committed for which Mr. George had been apprehended and was in custody.", "무시무시한 살인 사건이 벌어졌고, 그 일로 조지 씨가 붙잡혀 구금되어 있다는 것이었습니다."], ["When he told us that a large reward was offered by Sir Leicester Dedlock for the murderer’s apprehension, I did not in my first consternation understand why; but a few more words explained to me that the murdered person was Sir Leicester’s lawyer, and immediately my mother’s dread of him rushed into my remembrance.", "레스터 데드록 경이 살인범을 잡으려고 막대한 현상금을 내걸었다는 말을 들었을 때, 처음 놀란 가운데에는 그 까닭을 알지 못했습니다. 그러나 몇 마디 더 듣고서야, 살해된 사람이 레스터 경의 변호사라는 사실이 밝혀졌고, 그 순간 어머니가 그를 두려워했다는 기억이 와락 떠올랐습니다."]], [["This unforeseen and violent removal of one whom she had long watched and distrusted and who had long watched and distrusted her, one for whom she could have had few intervals of kindness, always dreading in him a dangerous and secret enemy, appeared so awful that my first thoughts were of her.", "오랫동안 어머니가 지켜보며 불신했고 또한 오랫동안 그가 지켜보며 불신했던 사람, 언제나 위험하고 은밀한 적으로 두려워하느라 어머니가 그를 다정하게 대할 틈이라곤 거의 없었을 그 사람이 이토록 뜻밖에 폭력으로 사라졌다는 사실이 너무도 끔찍해서, 제 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 것은 어머니였습니다."], ["How appalling to hear of such a death and be able to feel no pity!", "그런 죽음의 소식을 듣고도 한 점 연민을 느낄 수 없다는 것은 얼마나 소름 끼치는 일인지요!"], ["How dreadful to remember, perhaps, that she had sometimes even wished the old man away who was so swiftly hurried out of life!", "그토록 순식간에 목숨을 빼앗긴 그 노인을, 어머니가 어쩌면 더러는 사라져버렸으면 하고 바란 적이 있었을지도 모른다는 것을 떠올리는 일은 얼마나 무서운지요!"]], [["Such crowding reflections, increasing the distress and fear I always felt when the name was mentioned, made me so agitated that I could scarcely hold my place at the table.", "이렇게 한꺼번에 밀려드는 생각이, 그 이름이 입에 오를 때마다 늘 느끼던 괴로움과 두려움을 더욱 키워서, 저는 식탁에 제대로 앉아 있기조차 어려울 만큼 동요했습니다."], ["I was quite unable to follow the conversation until I had had a little time to recover.", "잠시 마음을 추스를 틈을 갖기 전까지는 대화를 도무지 따라갈 수가 없었습니다."], ["But when I came to myself and saw how shocked my guardian was and found that they were earnestly speaking of the suspected man and recalling every favourable impression we had formed of him out of the good we had known of him, my interest and my fears were so strongly aroused in his behalf that I was quite set up again.", "그러나 제정신이 돌아와 후견인이 얼마나 충격을 받았는지 보고, 모두가 그 혐의를 받는 사람을 진지하게 이야기하며, 우리가 알던 그의 선한 됨됨이에서 받은 좋은 인상을 하나하나 되짚고 있다는 것을 알게 되자, 그를 위하는 관심과 두려움이 너무도 강하게 일어 저는 다시금 기운을 차렸습니다."]], [["“Guardian, you don’t think it possible that he is justly accused?”", "\"후견인, 그분이 정당하게 고발당했을 가능성이 있다고는 생각하지 않으시죠?\""]], [["“My dear, I CAN’T think so.", "\"얘야, 나는 그렇게 생각할 수가 없구나."], ["This man whom we have seen so open-hearted and compassionate, who with the might of a giant has the gentleness of a child, who looks as brave a fellow as ever lived and is so simple and quiet with it, this man justly accused of such a crime?", "그토록 마음을 열고 인정 많은 모습을 보여준 사람, 거인 같은 힘에 어린아이 같은 부드러움을 지닌 사람, 둘도 없이 용감한 사내처럼 보이면서도 그토록 소박하고 차분한 사람, 그 사람이 그런 죄로 정당하게 고발당했다고?"], ["I can’t believe it.", "나는 믿을 수가 없어."], ["It’s not that I don’t or I won’t.", "안 믿는 것도, 안 믿으려는 것도 아니야."], ["I can’t!”", "믿을 수가 없는 거야!\""]], [["“And I can’t,”", "\"저도 믿을 수가 없습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Woodcourt.", "우드코트 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Still, whatever we believe or know of him, we had better not forget that some appearances are against him.", "\"그래도 우리가 그분을 어떻게 믿고 어떻게 알든, 몇 가지 정황이 그분에게 불리하다는 것은 잊지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["He bore an animosity towards the deceased gentleman.", "그분은 고인이 된 그 신사에게 적의를 품고 있었습니다."], ["He has openly mentioned it in many places.", "여러 곳에서 그것을 공공연히 드러냈고요."], ["He is said to have expressed himself violently towards him, and he certainly did about him, to my knowledge.", "그 사람에게 격하게 말했다고들 하는데, 제가 아는 한 그 사람에 대해서 격하게 말한 것은 분명한 사실입니다."], ["He admits that he was alone on the scene of the murder within a few minutes of its commission.", "그분 스스로 살인이 벌어지고 몇 분 안에 그 현장에 혼자 있었다고 인정했습니다."], ["I sincerely believe him to be as innocent of any participation in it as I am, but these are all reasons for suspicion falling upon him.”", "저는 진심으로 그분이 저만큼이나 이 일에 가담하지 않았다고 믿지만, 이 모든 것이 그분에게 의심이 쏠리는 까닭입니다.\""]], [["“True,”", "\"맞는 말이야.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["And he added, turning to me, “It would be doing him a very bad service, my dear, to shut our eyes to the truth in any of these respects.”", "그러고는 제 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였습니다. \"얘야, 이런 점들에서 진실에 눈을 감는다면 그건 그 사람을 아주 못되게 돕는 일이 될 게다.\""]], [["I felt, of course, that we must admit, not only to ourselves but to others, the full force of the circumstances against him.", "저는 물론, 우리 자신에게뿐만 아니라 다른 사람들에게도 그에게 불리한 정황의 무게를 온전히 인정해야 한다고 느꼈습니다."], ["Yet I knew withal (I could not help saying) that their weight would not induce us to desert him in his need.", "그러면서도 (저는 말하지 않을 수 없었습니다만) 그 무게 때문에 곤경에 처한 그를 우리가 저버리는 일은 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었습니다."]], [["“Heaven forbid!”", "\"천만에, 결코 그럴 수 없지!\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대답했습니다."], ["“We will stand by him, as he himself stood by the two poor creatures who are gone.” He meant Mr.", "\"그가 세상을 떠난 두 가엾은 사람 곁을 지켰듯이, 우리도 그의 곁을 지킬 게다.\""], ["Gridley and the boy, to both of whom Mr. George had given shelter.", "그가 말한 두 사람은 그리들리 씨와 그 소년이었으니, 조지 씨가 두 사람 모두에게 거처를 내준 적이 있었습니다."]], [["Mr. Woodcourt then told us that the trooper’s man had been with him before day, after wandering about the streets all night like a distracted creature.", "이어서 우드코트 씨가 들려준 이야기로는, 기병의 하인이 날이 밝기도 전에 그를 찾아왔는데, 밤새도록 정신 나간 사람처럼 거리를 헤매고 다닌 끝이었다고 했습니다."], ["That one of the trooper’s first anxieties was that we should not suppose him guilty.", "그 기병이 가장 먼저 걱정한 것 가운데 하나가, 우리가 그를 죄인으로 여기지 않을까 하는 것이었다고 했습니다."], ["That he had charged his messenger to represent his perfect innocence with every solemn assurance he could send us. That Mr.", "그래서 자기가 보낸 심부름꾼에게, 보낼 수 있는 온갖 엄숙한 다짐을 다해 자신의 완전한 무죄를 전하라고 일렀다고 했습니다."], ["Woodcourt had only quieted the man by undertaking to come to our house very early in the morning with these representations.", "우드코트 씨는 아침 일찍 우리 집에 와서 이 이야기를 전하겠다고 약속하고서야 겨우 그 사내를 진정시켰다고 했습니다."], ["He added that he was now upon his way to see the prisoner himself.", "그러면서 이제 자기가 직접 그 죄수를 보러 가는 길이라고 덧붙였습니다."]], [["My guardian said directly he would go too.", "후견인은 곧바로 자기도 가겠다고 했습니다."], ["Now, besides that I liked the retired soldier very much and that he liked me, I had that secret interest in what had happened which was only known to my guardian.", "그런데 저는 그 퇴역 군인을 무척 좋아했고 그도 저를 좋아했을 뿐 아니라, 벌어진 일에 후견인만이 아는 은밀한 관심을 품고 있었습니다."], ["I felt as if it came close and near to me.", "마치 그 일이 제게 바싹 가까이 다가온 것처럼 느껴졌습니다."], ["It seemed to become personally important to myself that the truth should be discovered and that no innocent people should be suspected, for suspicion, once run wild, might run wilder.", "진실이 밝혀지고 무고한 사람이 의심받지 않는 것이, 저 개인에게도 중요한 일처럼 여겨졌습니다. 한번 걷잡을 수 없이 날뛴 의심은 더욱 사납게 날뛸 수도 있으니까요."]], [["In a word, I felt as if it were my duty and obligation to go with them.", "한마디로, 저는 그들과 함께 가는 것이 제 의무이자 책임처럼 느껴졌습니다."], ["My guardian did not seek to dissuade me, and I went.", "후견인은 저를 말리려 하지 않았고, 저는 따라나섰습니다."]], [["It was a large prison with many courts and passages so like one another and so uniformly paved that I seemed to gain a new comprehension, as I passed along, of the fondness that solitary prisoners, shut up among the same staring walls from year to year, have had—as I have read—for a weed or a stray blade of grass.", "그곳은 큰 감옥이었습니다. 안마당과 통로가 수없이 많은데 하나같이 서로 닮았고 한결같이 똑같은 돌이 깔려 있어서, 그 길을 지나는 동안 저는 새삼 깨닫는 듯한 느낌이 들었습니다."], ["In an arched room by himself, like a cellar upstairs, with walls so glaringly white that they made the massive iron window-bars and iron-bound door even more profoundly black than they were, we found the trooper standing in a corner.", "해가 바뀌고 또 바뀌도록 똑같이 노려보는 벽 사이에 갇혀 지내는 외로운 죄수들이, 책에서 읽었듯이 잡초 한 포기나 어쩌다 돋아난 풀 한 잎에 어째서 그토록 애착을 품게 되는지를요. 그가 홀로 갇힌 곳은 아치형 천장의 방으로, 위층에 있는 지하 저장고 같았는데, 벽이 어찌나 눈부시게 희던지 그 때문에 육중한 무쇠 창살과 무쇠를 두른 문이 실제보다 한층 더 시커멓게 보였습니다."], ["He had been sitting on a bench there and had risen when he heard the locks and bolts turn.", "그 안 한쪽 구석에 기병이 서 있는 것을 우리는 보았습니다. 그는 거기 놓인 의자에 앉아 있다가 자물쇠와 빗장이 돌아가는 소리를 듣고 일어선 참이었습니다."]], [["When he saw us, he came forward a step with his usual heavy tread, and there stopped and made a slight bow.", "우리를 보자 그는 여느 때처럼 묵직한 걸음으로 한 발 다가왔다가 멈추어 서서 가볍게 고개를 숙였습니다."], ["But as I still advanced, putting out my hand to him, he understood us in a moment.", "그러나 제가 손을 내밀며 계속 다가가자, 그는 곧바로 우리 마음을 알아챘습니다."]], [["“This is a load off my mind, I do assure you, miss and gentlemen,”", "\"이거 정말이지 마음의 짐을 덜었습니다, 아가씨, 그리고 신사 양반들.\""], ["said he, saluting us with great heartiness and drawing a long breath.", "그가 더없이 진심을 담아 우리에게 인사하며 길게 숨을 내쉬었습니다."], ["“And now I don’t so much care how it ends.”", "\"이제는 이 일이 어떻게 끝나든 그리 마음 쓰지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["He scarcely seemed to be the prisoner.", "그는 도무지 죄수처럼 보이지 않았습니다."], ["What with his coolness and his soldierly bearing, he looked far more like the prison guard.", "침착한 태도와 군인다운 자세로 보아, 오히려 감옥의 간수에 훨씬 가까워 보였습니다."]], [["“This is even a rougher place than my gallery to receive a lady in,”", "\"제 사격장보다도 더 누추한 데서 숙녀분을 맞게 됐습니다만,\" 조지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mr. George, “but I know Miss Summerson will make the best of it.”", "\"서머슨 양은 이 정도쯤 너그럽게 봐주시리라 믿습니다.\""], ["As he handed me to the bench on which he had been sitting, I sat down, which seemed to give him great satisfaction.", "그가 자기가 앉아 있던 의자로 저를 안내하기에 제가 앉자, 그는 무척 흡족해하는 것 같았습니다."]], [["“I thank you, miss,”", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."]], [["“Now, George,”", "\"자, 조지.\""], ["observed my guardian, “as we require no new assurances on your part, so I believe we need give you none on ours.”", "후견인이 입을 열었습니다. \"자네 쪽에서 우리에게 새삼 다짐할 필요가 없듯이, 우리 쪽에서도 자네에게 굳이 다짐할 필요는 없으리라 보네.\""]], [["“Not at all, sir.", "\"천만에요, 나리."], ["I thank you with all my heart.", "진심으로 감사드립니다."], ["If I was not innocent of this crime, I couldn’t look at you and keep my secret to myself under the condescension of the present visit.", "제가 이 죄에 대해 무고하지 않았다면, 이렇게 황송하게 찾아와 주신 마당에 나리를 똑바로 쳐다보며 제 속을 감출 수는 없었을 겁니다."], ["I feel the present visit very much.", "이번 방문이 제게는 정말 큰 의미입니다."], ["I am not one of the eloquent sort, but I feel it, Miss Summerson and gentlemen, deeply.”", "제가 말주변이 좋은 축은 아닙니다만, 그래도 절실히 느낍니다, 서머슨 양, 그리고 신사 양반들, 깊이 느낍니다.\""]], [["He laid his hand for a moment on his broad chest and bent his head to us.", "그는 잠시 한 손을 자기 넓은 가슴에 얹고 우리에게 고개를 숙였습니다."], ["Although he squared himself again directly, he expressed a great amount of natural emotion by these simple means.", "곧바로 다시 어깨를 펴긴 했지만, 그 소박한 몸짓으로도 그는 자연스러운 감정을 한껏 드러냈습니다."]], [["“First,”", "\"우선,\""], ["said my guardian, “can we do anything for your personal comfort, George?”", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"자네 개인의 편의를 위해 우리가 해줄 만한 일이 있겠나, 조지?\""]], [["“For which, sir?”", "\"무엇 말씀이십니까, 나리?\""], ["he inquired, clearing his throat.", "그가 목을 가다듬으며 물었습니다."]], [["“For your personal comfort.", "\"자네 개인의 편의 말일세."], ["Is there anything you want that would lessen the hardship of this confinement?”", "이렇게 갇혀 지내는 고생을 조금이나마 덜어줄 만한, 뭐 필요한 게 없나?\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"글쎄요, 나리.\""], ["replied George, after a little cogitation, “I am equally obliged to you, but tobacco being against the rules, I can’t say that there is.”", "조지가 잠시 생각하더니 대답했습니다. \"그것도 똑같이 고맙습니다만, 담배는 규칙에 어긋나서, 딱히 필요한 건 없다고 해야겠습니다.\""]], [["“You will think of many little things perhaps, by and by.", "\"앞으로 자질구레한 게 여럿 생각날지도 모르지."], ["Whenever you do, George, let us know.”", "언제든 생각나거든, 조지, 우리에게 알려주게.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir. Howsoever,”", "\"고맙습니다, 나리."], ["observed Mr.", "어쨌거나,\""], ["George with one of his sunburnt smiles, “a man who has been knocking about the world in a vagabond kind of a way as long as I have gets on well enough in a place like the present, so far as that goes.”", "조지 씨가 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴로 웃으며 말했습니다. \"저처럼 오랫동안 떠돌이 신세로 세상을 헤매고 다닌 사람이라면 이런 곳에서도, 그런 면에서는 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다.\""]], [["“Next, as to your case,”", "\"다음으로, 자네 사건 말인데.\""], ["observed my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Exactly so, sir,”", "\"그렇지요, 나리.\""], ["returned Mr. George, folding his arms upon his breast with perfect self-possession and a little curiosity.", "조지 씨가 완전히 침착한 태도로, 약간의 호기심을 띠며 가슴 앞에 팔짱을 끼고 대답했습니다."]], [["“How does it stand now?”", "\"지금 어떻게 돌아가고 있나?\""]], [["“Why, sir, it is under remand at present.", "\"그게 말입니다, 나리, 지금은 구류 연장 중입니다."], ["Bucket gives me to understand that he will probably apply for a series of remands from time to time until the case is more complete.", "버킷 말로는, 사건이 더 갖춰질 때까지 때때로 구류 연장을 거듭 신청할 것 같다고 합니다."], ["How it is to be made more complete I don’t myself see, but I dare say Bucket will manage it somehow.”", "어떻게 더 갖춘다는 건지 저로서는 모르겠습니다만, 버킷이 어떻게든 해내겠지요.\""]], [["“Why, heaven save us, man,”", "\"이런, 맙소사, 이 사람아.\""], ["exclaimed my guardian, surprised into his old oddity and vehemence, “you talk of yourself as if you were somebody else!”", "후견인이 옛날의 그 별스럽고 격한 기질을 못 이겨 소리쳤습니다. \"자네는 마치 자네가 딴 사람인 양 자네 이야기를 하는구먼!\""]], [["“No offence, sir,” said Mr.", "\"기분 상하셨다면 죄송합니다, 나리.\""], ["George. “I am very sensible of your kindness.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["But I don’t see how an innocent man is to make up his mind to this kind of thing without knocking his head against the walls unless he takes it in that point of view.”", "\"나리의 친절은 잘 알고 있습니다. 하지만 무고한 사람이 이런 일을 그렇게 받아들이지 않고서야, 벽에 머리를 들이받지 않고 어떻게 마음을 추스를 수 있겠습니까.\""]], [["“That is true enough to a certain extent,”", "\"어느 정도는 맞는 말이긴 하네.\""], ["returned my guardian, softened.", "후견인이 누그러져서 대답했습니다."], ["“But my good fellow, even an innocent man must take ordinary precautions to defend himself.”", "\"하지만 이보게, 무고한 사람이라도 자기를 지키려면 으레 해야 할 대비는 해야 하는 법일세.\""]], [["“Certainly, sir.", "\"물론입니다, 나리."], ["And I have done so.", "그래서 저도 그렇게 했습니다. 치안판사들께 이렇게 진술했지요."], ["I have stated to the magistrates, ‘Gentlemen, I am as innocent of this charge as yourselves; what has been stated against me in the way of facts is perfectly true; I know no more about it.’ I intend to continue stating that, sir.", "'여러분, 저는 여러분만큼이나 이 혐의에 결백합니다. 사실관계로 저에게 제기된 것은 모두 틀림없는 진실입니다."], ["What more can I do?", "저는 그 이상은 아무것도 모릅니다.' 저는 앞으로도 계속 그렇게 진술할 작정입니다, 나리."], ["It’s the truth.”", "그 이상 제가 무얼 할 수 있겠습니까? 그게 진실인걸요.\""]], [["“But the mere truth won’t do,”", "\"하지만 그저 진실만으로는 안 되네.\""], ["rejoined my guardian.", "후견인이 받아쳤습니다."]], [["“Won’t it indeed, sir?", "\"정말 안 되는 겁니까, 나리?"], ["Rather a bad look-out for me!”", "저한테는 영 안 좋은 형국이군요!\""], ["Mr. George good-humouredly observed.", "조지 씨가 너스레를 떨며 말했습니다."]], [["“You must have a lawyer,”", "\"자네는 변호사를 두어야 하네.\""], ["pursued my guardian.", "후견인이 이어갔습니다."], ["“We must engage a good one for you.”", "\"우리가 자네를 위해 솜씨 좋은 사람을 하나 선임해줘야겠어.\""]], [["“I ask your pardon, sir,”", "\"송구합니다, 나리.\""], ["said Mr. George with a step backward.", "조지 씨가 한 발 물러서며 말했습니다."], ["“I am equally obliged.", "\"그것도 똑같이 고맙습니다."], ["But I must decidedly beg to be excused from anything of that sort.”", "하지만 그런 종류의 일은 단호히 사양하게 해주시기를 간청드립니다.\""]], [["“You won’t have a lawyer?”", "\"변호사를 두지 않겠다고?\""]], [["“No, sir.” Mr.", "\"네, 나리.\""], ["George shook his head in the most emphatic manner.", "조지 씨는 더없이 단호하게 고개를 저었습니다."], ["“I thank you all the same, sir, but—no lawyer!”", "\"그래도 고맙습니다, 나리, 하지만, 변호사는 안 됩니다!\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“I don’t take kindly to the breed,”", "\"저는 그 족속을 곱게 보지 않습니다.\""], ["said Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Gridley didn’t.", "\"그리들리도 그랬고요."], ["And—if you’ll excuse my saying so much—I should hardly have thought you did yourself, sir.”", "그리고, 이런 말씀 드려 죄송합니다만, 나리께서도 그러시리라고는 좀처럼 생각되지 않습니다.\""]], [["“That’s equity,”", "\"그건 형평법일세.\""], ["my guardian explained, a little at a loss; “that’s equity, George.”", "후견인이 약간 난처해하며 설명했습니다. \"그건 형평법이야, 조지.\""]], [["“Is it, indeed, sir?”", "\"그렇습니까, 나리?\""], ["returned the trooper in his off-hand manner.", "기병이 무심한 투로 대답했습니다."], ["“I am not acquainted with those shades of names myself, but in a general way I object to the breed.”", "\"저야 그런 이름의 미묘한 갈래까지는 잘 모릅니다만, 대체로 말해 저는 그 족속이 싫습니다.\""]], [["Unfolding his arms and changing his position, he stood with one massive hand upon the table and the other on his hip, as complete a picture of a man who was not to be moved from a fixed purpose as ever I saw.", "그는 팔짱을 풀고 자세를 바꾸어, 한쪽 육중한 손을 탁자에 얹고 다른 손은 허리에 댄 채 섰습니다. 한번 정한 뜻에서 결코 움직이지 않을 사람의 본보기로, 제가 본 가운데 그토록 완벽한 그림은 없었습니다."], ["It was in vain that we all three talked to him and endeavoured to persuade him; he listened with that gentleness which went so well with his bluff bearing, but was evidently no more shaken by our representations that his place of confinement was.", "우리 셋이 그에게 이런저런 말로 설득하려 애써봐야 헛일이었습니다. 그는 무뚝뚝한 태도와 그토록 잘 어울리는 부드러움으로 귀를 기울였지만, 그가 갇힌 그 방만큼이나 우리 말에는 조금도 흔들리지 않는 것이 분명했습니다."]], [["“Pray think, once more, Mr.", "\"제발 다시 한번 생각해주세요, 조지 씨.\""], ["George,” said I.", "제가 말했습니다."], ["“Have you no wish in reference to your case?”", "\"당신 사건과 관련해서 바라는 것이 정말 하나도 없나요?\""]], [["“I certainly could wish it to be tried, miss,”", "\"군법회의에서 재판받을 수 있다면야 그러기를 바라겠지요, 아가씨.\""], ["he returned, “by court-martial; but that is out of the question, as I am well aware.", "그가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 그건 어림없는 일이라는 걸 저도 잘 압니다."], ["If you will be so good as to favour me with your attention for a couple of minutes, miss, not more, I’ll endeavour to explain myself as clearly as I can.”", "아가씨께서 부디 한 이삼 분만, 그 이상은 말고, 제 말에 귀 기울여주신다면, 제가 할 수 있는 한 또렷이 제 뜻을 밝혀보겠습니다.\""]], [["He looked at us all three in turn, shook his head a little as if he were adjusting it in the stock and collar of a tight uniform, and after a moment’s reflection went on.", "그는 우리 셋을 차례로 바라보고는, 마치 빳빳한 군복의 깃과 목띠에 목을 맞추기라도 하듯 고개를 약간 가다듬은 다음, 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“You see, miss, I have been handcuffed and taken into custody and brought here.", "\"보십시오, 아가씨, 저는 수갑이 채워진 채 붙들려서 이리로 끌려왔습니다."], ["I am a marked and disgraced man, and here I am.", "저는 낙인찍히고 망신당한 사람으로, 이렇게 여기 있습니다."], ["My shooting gallery is rummaged, high and low, by Bucket; such property as I have—’tis small—is turned this way and that till it don’t know itself; and (as aforesaid) here I am!", "제 사격장은 버킷한테 위아래 샅샅이 뒤지어졌고, 제 재산이라고 해봐야 보잘것없지만, 그것도 이리저리 뒤집혀서 제 것인지조차 모를 지경이 되었고, 그래서 (앞서 말씀드렸듯이) 이렇게 여기 있습니다!"], ["I don’t particular complain of that.", "그렇다고 그걸 딱히 불평하는 건 아닙니다."], ["Though I am in these present quarters through no immediately preceding fault of mine, I can very well understand that if I hadn’t gone into the vagabond way in my youth, this wouldn’t have happened.", "바로 앞선 제 잘못 때문에 이런 곳에 들어온 건 아니라 해도, 제가 젊은 시절에 떠돌이 길로 들어서지 않았더라면 이런 일은 없었으리라는 건 저도 충분히 압니다. 어쨌든 일은 벌어졌습니다."], ["It HAS happened. Then comes the question how to meet it.”", "그러면 이걸 어떻게 마주할 것이냐, 그게 문제지요.\""]], [["He rubbed his swarthy forehead for a moment with a good-humoured look and said apologetically, “I am such a short-winded talker that I must think a bit.”", "그는 잠시 거무스름한 이마를 문지르며 너그러운 표정으로, 변명하듯 말했습니다. \"제가 숨 가쁘게 더듬는 말꾼이라서, 좀 생각을 해야겠습니다.\""], ["Having thought a bit, he looked up again and resumed.", "그러고는 잠시 생각하더니 다시 고개를 들고 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“How to meet it.", "\"이걸 어떻게 마주할 것이냐."], ["Now, the unfortunate deceased was himself a lawyer and had a pretty tight hold of me.", "자, 불운하게도 세상을 떠난 그 사람은 그 자신이 변호사였고, 저를 단단히 옭아매고 있었습니다."], ["I don’t wish to rake up his ashes, but he had, what I should call if he was living, a devil of a tight hold of me.", "망자의 재를 들쑤시고 싶지는 않습니다만, 그자가 살아 있다면 저는 이렇게 말하겠습니다. 그자는 저를 지독하리만치 옭아매고 있었다고요."], ["I don’t like his trade the better for that.", "그렇다고 그 직업이 더 좋아진 건 아닙니다."], ["If I had kept clear of his trade, I should have kept outside this place.", "제가 그 직업을 멀리했더라면, 이런 곳 밖에 있었겠지요."], ["But that’s not what I mean.", "하지만 그건 제가 하려는 말이 아닙니다."], ["Now, suppose I had killed him.", "자, 제가 그자를 죽였다고 쳐봅시다."], ["Suppose I really had discharged into his body any one of those pistols recently fired off that Bucket has found at my place, and dear me, might have found there any day since it has been my place.", "버킷이 제 집에서 찾아낸, 최근에 발사된 그 권총 가운데 하나를 제가 정말로 그자 몸에다 쏘았다고 쳐봅시다. 그런데 이런, 그 권총이야 제 집이 된 뒤로는 언제든 거기서 찾아낼 수 있었던 물건이지요."], ["What should I have done as soon as I was hard and fast here?", "그렇게 여기 옴짝달싹 못 하게 갇히자마자 제가 무얼 했겠습니까?"], ["Got a lawyer.”", "변호사를 구했겠지요.\""]], [["He stopped on hearing some one at the locks and bolts and did not resume until the door had been opened and was shut again.", "자물쇠와 빗장에서 누군가 인기척이 나자 그는 말을 멈추었고, 문이 열렸다가 다시 닫힐 때까지 말을 잇지 않았습니다."], ["For what purpose opened, I will mention presently.", "무슨 일로 문이 열렸는지는 곧 말씀드리겠습니다."]], [["“I should have got a lawyer, and he would have said (as I have often read in the newspapers), ‘My client says nothing, my client reserves his defence’: my client this, that, and t’other.", "\"저는 변호사를 구했을 거고, 그 변호사는 (신문에서 자주 읽었듯이) 이렇게 말했겠지요. '제 의뢰인은 아무 말도 하지 않습니다, 제 의뢰인은 변론을 유보합니다.'"], ["Well, ’tis not the custom of that breed to go straight, according to my opinion, or to think that other men do.", "제 의뢰인은 이렇다, 저렇다, 이러쿵저러쿵. 자, 제 생각에 그 족속은 곧이곧대로 가는 게 본성도 아니고, 남들이 그러리라 여기지도 않습니다."], ["Say I am innocent and I get a lawyer.", "제가 결백하다고 칩시다, 그래서 변호사를 구합니다."], ["He would be as likely to believe me guilty as not; perhaps more.", "그자는 저를 죄인으로 믿는 쪽이 안 믿는 쪽만큼이나, 어쩌면 그보다 더 그럴 겁니다. 그러면 그자가 어떻게 하겠습니까?"], ["What would he do, whether or not?", "어떻든 간에, 제가 죄인인 양 굴겠지요."], ["Act as if I was—shut my mouth up, tell me not to commit myself, keep circumstances back, chop the evidence small, quibble, and get me off perhaps!", "제 입을 틀어막고, 함부로 말하지 말라 이르고, 정황을 감추고, 증거를 잘게 토막 내고, 말꼬리를 잡고 늘어지고, 그래서 어쩌면 저를 빼내주겠지요! 하지만 서머슨 양, 제가 그런 식으로 빠져나오는 것에 마음을 쓰겠습니까?"], ["But, Miss Summerson, do I care for getting off in that way; or would I rather be hanged in my own way—if you’ll excuse my mentioning anything so disagreeable to a lady?”", "아니면 차라리 제 방식대로 교수형을 당하는 편을 택하겠습니까? 숙녀분께 이런 거북한 얘기를 꺼내는 걸 너그러이 봐주신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["He had warmed into his subject now, and was under no further necessity to wait a bit.", "이제 그는 자기 이야기에 한껏 달아올라, 더는 잠시 멈추어 생각할 필요가 없었습니다."]], [["“I would rather be hanged in my own way.", "\"저는 차라리 제 방식대로 교수형을 당하겠습니다."], ["And I mean to be!", "그러고야 말 작정입니다!"], ["I don’t intend to say,”", "그렇다고 해서,\""], ["looking round upon us with his powerful arms akimbo and his dark eyebrows raised, “that I am more partial to being hanged than another man.", "그가 힘센 두 팔을 허리에 짚고 짙은 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 우리를 둘러보며 말했습니다. \"제가 남들보다 교수형 당하는 걸 더 좋아한다는 뜻은 아닙니다."], ["What I say is, I must come off clear and full or not at all.", "제가 하는 말은, 저는 깨끗하고 온전하게 풀려나든가, 아니면 아예 안 풀려나든가 둘 중 하나여야 한다는 겁니다."], ["Therefore, when I hear stated against me what is true, I say it’s true; and when they tell me, ‘whatever you say will be used,’ I tell them I don’t mind that; I mean it to be used.", "그러니 제게 불리한 진술이 사실일 때는, 그건 사실이라고 말합니다. 그리고 그들이 '당신이 하는 말은 무엇이든 증거로 쓰일 거요'라고 하면, 저는 상관없다고, 부디 그렇게 쓰이게 하라고 말합니다."], ["If they can’t make me innocent out of the whole truth, they are not likely to do it out of anything less, or anything else.", "온전한 진실로도 저를 결백하게 만들지 못한다면, 그보다 덜한 것이나 다른 무엇으로 그리해낼 리도 없을 테니까요."], ["And if they are, it’s worth nothing to me.”", "설령 그리해낸들, 제게는 아무 값어치도 없습니다.\""]], [["Taking a pace or two over the stone floor, he came back to the table and finished what he had to say.", "그는 돌바닥을 한두 걸음 거닐다가 탁자로 돌아와 하려던 말을 마쳤습니다."]], [["“I thank you, miss and gentlemen both, many times for your attention, and many times more for your interest.", "\"아가씨, 그리고 신사 양반들 두 분, 귀 기울여주셔서 거듭 감사드리고, 관심 가져주셔서 더더욱 감사드립니다."], ["That’s the plain state of the matter as it points itself out to a mere trooper with a blunt broadsword kind of a mind.", "무딘 폭검 같은 머리를 가진 일개 기병에게 비치는 대로, 이게 이 일의 있는 그대로의 사정입니다."], ["I have never done well in life beyond my duty as a soldier, and if the worst comes after all, I shall reap pretty much as I have sown.", "저는 군인으로서의 본분 말고는 살면서 변변히 잘해본 적이 없으니, 끝내 최악의 일이 닥친다 해도, 뿌린 대로 거두는 셈이 되겠지요."], ["When I got over the first crash of being seized as a murderer—it don’t take a rover who has knocked about so much as myself so very long to recover from a crash—I worked my way round to what you find me now.", "살인자로 붙잡히는 첫 충격을 넘기고 나니, 저처럼 그토록 많이 떠돌아다닌 부랑자는 충격에서 벗어나는 데 그리 오래 걸리지 않는 법인지라, 지금 보시는 이 마음가짐에 다다랐습니다."], ["As such I shall remain.", "저는 이대로 있겠습니다."], ["No relations will be disgraced by me or made unhappy for me, and—and that’s all I’ve got to say.”", "저 때문에 망신당하거나 불행해질 친척도 없고요, 그리고, 그게 제가 드릴 수 있는 말 전부입니다.\""]], [["The door had been opened to admit another soldier-looking man of less prepossessing appearance at first sight and a weather-tanned, bright-eyed wholesome woman with a basket, who, from her entrance, had been exceedingly attentive to all Mr.", "문이 열려 들어온 사람은 또 한 사람의 군인 같은 남자였는데, 첫눈에는 그리 호감 가는 인상이 아니었습니다. 그리고 햇볕에 그을리고 눈이 초롱초롱한, 건강한 여인이 바구니를 들고 함께였는데, 그녀는 들어선 순간부터 조지 씨가 하는 말 하나하나에 무척이나 귀를 기울이고 있었습니다."], ["George had said. Mr.", "조지 씨는 두 사람을 허물없는 끄덕임과 다정한 눈길로 맞이했지만, 이야기를 하던 도중이라 그 이상 각별한 인사는 건네지 못했습니다."], ["George had received them with a familiar nod and a friendly look, but without any more particular greeting in the midst of his address.", "이제 그는 두 사람과 정답게 악수를 나누며 말했습니다."], ["He now shook them cordially by the hand and said, “Miss Summerson and gentlemen, this is an old comrade of mine, Matthew Bagnet.", "\"서머슨 양, 그리고 신사 양반들, 이쪽은 제 오랜 전우 매슈 배그넷입니다."], ["And this is his wife, Mrs. Bagnet.”", "그리고 이쪽은 그 부인 배그넷 부인이고요.\""]], [["Mr. Bagnet made us a stiff military bow, and Mrs. Bagnet dropped us a curtsy.", "배그넷 씨는 우리에게 뻣뻣하게 군대식으로 절을 했고, 배그넷 부인은 무릎을 굽혀 인사를 했습니다."]], [["“Real good friends of mine, they are,”", "\"제 진짜 좋은 벗들입니다.\""], ["said Mr. George.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“It was at their house I was taken.”", "\"제가 붙잡힌 게 이 사람들 집에서였지요.\""]], [["“With a second-hand wiolinceller,”", "\"중고 첼로 때문에.\""], ["Mr. Bagnet put in, twitching his head angrily.", "배그넷 씨가 화난 듯 고개를 홱 움직이며 끼어들었습니다."], ["“Of a good tone.", "\"음색이 좋은 놈으로."], ["For a friend.", "친구한테 주려고."], ["That money was no object to.”", "돈은 아깝지 않은 거였소.\""]], [["“Mat,” said Mr.", "\"맷,\""], ["George, “you have heard pretty well all I have been saying to this lady and these two gentlemen.", "조지 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 아가씨하고 이 두 신사분께 내가 한 말을 자네도 얼추 다 들었을 텐데."], ["I know it meets your approval?”", "자네도 옳다고 여기겠지?\""]], [["Mr. Bagnet, after considering, referred the point to his wife.", "배그넷 씨는 잠시 헤아려보더니 그 문제를 아내에게 넘겼습니다."], ["“Old girl,”", "\"여보.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“Tell him.", "\"당신이 말해."], ["Whether or not.", "옳다고 보는지 아닌지를."], ["It meets my approval.”", "나는 옳다고 보네.\""]], [["“Why, George,”", "\"이런, 조지.\""], ["exclaimed Mrs. Bagnet, who had been unpacking her basket, in which there was a piece of cold pickled pork, a little tea and sugar, and a brown loaf, “you ought to know it don’t.", "그 사이 바구니를 풀고 있던 배그넷 부인이 소리쳤는데, 바구니 안에는 차가운 절인 돼지고기 한 덩이와 약간의 차와 설탕, 그리고 거무스레한 빵 한 덩이가 들어 있었습니다."], ["You ought to know it’s enough to drive a person wild to hear you.", "\"당신도 알아야지, 안 옳다는 걸. 당신 얘기를 듣고 있자면 사람 속이 다 뒤집힌다는 걸 알아야 한다고."], ["You won’t be got off this way, and you won’t be got off that way—what do you mean by such picking and choosing?", "이렇게도 안 풀려나겠다, 저렇게도 안 풀려나겠다, 그렇게 골라잡고 까탈을 부려서 대체 어쩌자는 거예요?"], ["It’s stuff and nonsense, George.”", "다 부질없는 헛소리예요, 조지.\""]], [["“Don’t be severe upon me in my misfortunes, Mrs. Bagnet,”", "\"이 곤경에 빠진 사람한테 너무 모질게 굴지 마세요, 배그넷 부인.\""], ["said the trooper lightly.", "기병이 가볍게 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Bother your misfortunes,”", "곤경이고 뭐고,\" 배그넷 부인이 외쳤습니다."], ["cried Mrs. Bagnet, “if they don’t make you more reasonable than that comes to.", "\"그 곤경이 당신을 그 모양보다 좀 더 분별 있게 만들어주지 못한다면 무슨 소용이람."], ["I never was so ashamed in my life to hear a man talk folly as I have been to hear you talk this day to the present company.", "살면서 사내가 어리석은 소리를 하는 걸 듣고 오늘처럼 부끄러웠던 적이 없어요, 이 자리에서 당신이 이분들께 하는 얘기를 들으면서 말이에요."], ["Lawyers?", "변호사라뇨?"], ["Why, what but too many cooks should hinder you from having a dozen lawyers if the gentleman recommended them to you.”", "사공이 많아서 배가 산으로 가는 게 아니고서야, 이 신사분께서 추천해주시는 변호사를 열 명이고 못 둘 까닭이 어디 있어요.\""]], [["“This is a very sensible woman,”", "\"이분은 참으로 분별 있는 분이로군.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“I hope you will persuade him, Mrs. Bagnet.”", "\"부디 저 사람을 설득해주시기 바랍니다, 배그넷 부인.\""]], [["“Persuade him, sir?”", "\"설득이요, 나리?\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대답했습니다."], ["“Lord bless you, no.", "\"원, 천만에요."], ["You don’t know George.", "나리는 조지를 몰라서 그래요."], ["Now, there!”", "자, 저기!\""], ["Mrs. Bagnet left her basket to point him out with both her bare brown hands.", "배그넷 부인이 바구니를 내버려 두고 햇볕에 그을린 두 맨손으로 그를 가리켰습니다."], ["“There he stands!", "\"저기 저렇게 서 있잖아요!"], ["As self-willed and as determined a man, in the wrong way, as ever put a human creature under heaven out of patience!", "엉뚱한 쪽으로는, 하늘 아래 어떤 사람의 인내심도 바닥나게 만들 만큼 고집스럽고 막무가내인 사람이라고요!"], ["You could as soon take up and shoulder an eight and forty pounder by your own strength as turn that man when he has got a thing into his head and fixed it there.", "저 사람이 일단 머릿속에 뭘 집어넣고 못 박아버리면, 그걸 돌려놓느니 차라리 제 힘으로 사십팔 파운드짜리 대포를 번쩍 들어 어깨에 메는 게 빠를 거예요."], ["Why, don’t I know him!”", "원, 내가 저 사람을 모를까!\""], ["cried Mrs. Bagnet.", "배그넷 부인이 소리쳤습니다."], ["“Don’t I know you, George!", "\"내가 당신을 모를까, 조지!"], ["You don’t mean to set up for a new character with ME after all these years, I hope?”", "이 오랜 세월 끝에 와서 나한테 새삼 딴사람 행세를 하려는 건 아니겠지, 설마?\""]], [["Her friendly indignation had an exemplary effect upon her husband, who shook his head at the trooper several times as a silent recommendation to him to yield.", "그녀의 다정한 분노는 그 남편에게 본보기 같은 효과를 미쳐서, 그는 기병에게 여러 번 고개를 저어, 그만 굽히라고 말없이 권했습니다."], ["Between whiles, Mrs. Bagnet looked at me; and I understood from the play of her eyes that she wished me to do something, though I did not comprehend what.", "그 틈틈이 배그넷 부인은 저를 바라보았는데, 저는 그 눈짓에서 그녀가 제게 무언가를 해주기를 바란다는 것을 알아챘지만, 그게 무엇인지는 알지 못했습니다."]], [["“But I have given up talking to you, old fellow, years and years,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만 나는 당신한테 말하길 그만둔 지가 벌써 몇 년이오, 이 양반아.\""], ["Bagnet as she blew a little dust off the pickled pork, looking at me again; “and when ladies and gentlemen know you as well as I do, they’ll give up talking to you too.", "배그넷 부인이 절인 돼지고기에서 먼지를 조금 후 불어 날리며, 다시 저를 바라보며 말했습니다. \"그리고 신사 숙녀분들도 나만큼 당신을 잘 알게 되면, 당신한테 말하길 그만두실 거예요."], ["If you are not too headstrong to accept of a bit of dinner, here it is.”", "만약 당신이 너무 고집을 부려서 식사 한 술 못 받아먹을 지경만 아니라면, 여기 가져왔어요.\""]], [["“I accept it with many thanks,”", "\"고맙게 잘 먹겠습니다.\""], ["returned the trooper.", "기병이 대답했습니다."]], [["“Do you though, indeed?” said Mrs.", "\"정말 그러시겠어요, 응?\""], ["Bagnet, continuing to grumble on good-humouredly.", "배그넷 부인이 너스레를 떨며 투덜투덜 이어갔습니다."], ["“I’m sure I’m surprised at that.", "\"참 별일이네요."], ["I wonder you don’t starve in your own way also.", "당신 식으로라면 굶지 않는 게 신기할 지경인데."], ["It would only be like you.", "그게 딱 당신다운 짓일 텐데."], ["Perhaps you’ll set your mind upon THAT next.”", "어쩌면 다음번엔 그러기로 작정할지도 모르지.\""], ["Here she again looked at me, and I now perceived from her glances at the door and at me, by turns, that she wished us to retire and to await her following us outside the prison.", "여기서 그녀가 다시 저를 바라보았는데, 저는 이제 그녀가 번갈아 문과 저를 흘끔거리는 눈짓에서, 우리가 자리를 뜨고 감옥 밖에서 그녀가 따라 나오기를 기다려주기를 바란다는 것을 알아챘습니다."], ["Communicating this by similar means to my guardian and Mr. Woodcourt, I rose.", "비슷한 방법으로 이를 후견인과 우드코트 씨에게 알리고, 저는 일어섰습니다."]], [["“We hope you will think better of it, Mr. George,”", "\"부디 다시 생각해주시기 바랍니다, 조지 씨.\""], ["said I, “and we shall come to see you again, trusting to find you more reasonable.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"그리고 다시 찾아뵙겠습니다, 그때는 좀 더 분별 있어지셨기를 바라면서요.\""]], [["“More grateful, Miss Summerson, you can’t find me,”", "\"저보다 더 고마워하는 사람은 못 만나실 겁니다, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."]], [["“But more persuadable we can, I hope,”", "\"하지만 더 설득에 응하는 분은 만날 수 있기를 바라요.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."], ["“And let me entreat you to consider that the clearing up of this mystery and the discovery of the real perpetrator of this deed may be of the last importance to others besides yourself.”", "\"그리고 부디 헤아려주세요. 이 수수께끼를 풀고 이 일을 실제로 저지른 자를 찾아내는 것이, 당신뿐 아니라 다른 사람들에게도 더없이 중대한 일일 수 있다는 것을요.\""]], [["He heard me respectfully but without much heeding these words, which I spoke a little turned from him, already on my way to the door; he was observing (this they afterwards told me) my height and figure, which seemed to catch his attention all at once.", "그는 공손하게 제 말을 들었지만 그 말에 그리 마음을 두지는 않았습니다. 저는 이미 문 쪽으로 가던 길이라 몸을 약간 돌린 채 이 말을 했습니다. 그는 (나중에 사람들이 제게 전해준 바로는) 제 키와 몸매를 눈여겨보고 있었는데, 그것이 갑자기 그의 눈길을 사로잡은 모양이었습니다."]], [["“’Tis curious,”", "\"참 묘하군요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“And yet I thought so at the time!”", "\"그때도 그렇게 생각했었는데!\""]], [["My guardian asked him what he meant.", "후견인이 그게 무슨 말이냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, sir,”", "\"그게 말입니다, 나리.\""], ["he answered, “when my ill fortune took me to the dead man’s staircase on the night of his murder, I saw a shape so like Miss Summerson’s go by me in the dark that I had half a mind to speak to it.”", "그가 대답했습니다. \"제 불운이 저를 그 사람이 살해되던 밤 그 사람 계단으로 이끌었을 때, 어둠 속에서 서머슨 양과 어찌나 닮은 형체가 제 곁을 지나가던지, 하마터면 말을 걸 뻔했습니다.\""]], [["For an instant I felt such a shudder as I never felt before or since and hope I shall never feel again.", "순간 저는 그때까지 한 번도 느껴본 적 없는, 그리고 그 뒤로도 두 번 다시 느낀 적 없는, 부디 앞으로도 결코 느끼지 않기를 바라는 그런 전율에 휩싸였습니다."]], [["“It came downstairs as I went up,”", "\"제가 올라가는 동안 그건 아래로 내려와서,\""], ["said the trooper, “and crossed the moonlighted window with a loose black mantle on; I noticed a deep fringe to it.", "기병이 말했습니다. \"달빛이 비치는 창문을 가로질러 갔는데, 헐렁한 검은 망토를 두르고 있었지요."], ["However, it has nothing to do with the present subject, excepting that Miss Summerson looked so like it at the moment that it came into my head.”", "술이 깊게 달린 게 눈에 띄었습니다. 어쨌든 지금 이야기와는 상관없는 일입니다만, 그 순간 서머슨 양이 그것과 어찌나 닮아 보이던지 문득 떠올랐던 겁니다.\""]], [["I cannot separate and define the feelings that arose in me after this; it is enough that the vague duty and obligation I had felt upon me from the first of following the investigation was, without my distinctly daring to ask myself any question, increased, and that I was indignantly sure of there being no possibility of a reason for my being afraid.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 제 안에 일어난 감정들을 저는 따로 가려내어 또렷이 말할 수가 없습니다. 다만 이것만은 분명합니다. 처음부터 수사를 좇아야 한다고 느꼈던 그 막연한 의무와 책임이, 감히 제 자신에게 어떤 물음도 똑똑히 던지지 못한 채로 더욱 커졌다는 것, 그리고 제가 두려워할 만한 까닭이라곤 도무지 있을 수 없다고 분개하듯 확신했다는 것입니다."]], [["We three went out of the prison and walked up and down at some short distance from the gate, which was in a retired place. We had not waited long when Mr.", "우리 셋은 감옥에서 나와, 외진 곳에 있는 정문에서 조금 떨어져 이리저리 거닐었습니다."], ["and Mrs. Bagnet came out too and quickly joined us.", "오래 기다리지 않아 배그넷 부부도 나와서 곧 우리에게 다가왔습니다."]], [["There was a tear in each of Mrs. Bagnet’s eyes, and her face was flushed and hurried.", "배그넷 부인의 두 눈에는 저마다 눈물이 맺혀 있었고, 얼굴은 상기되고 다급해 보였습니다."], ["“I didn’t let George see what I thought about it, you know, miss,”", "\"조지한테는 내가 어떻게 생각하는지 안 내비쳤지요, 아가씨.\""], ["was her first remark when she came up, “but he’s in a bad way, poor old fellow!”", "다가오면서 그녀가 처음 꺼낸 말이었습니다. \"하지만 저 사람, 가엾게도 영 위태로운 처지예요!\""]], [["“Not with care and prudence and good help,”", "\"잘 보살피고 신중을 기하고 든든히 돕는다면 그렇지 않을 겁니다.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“A gentleman like you ought to know best, sir,” returned Mrs.", "\"나리 같은 신사분께서 더 잘 아시겠지요, 나리.\""], ["Bagnet, hurriedly drying her eyes on the hem of her grey cloak, “but I am uneasy for him.", "배그넷 부인이 잿빛 망토 자락으로 황급히 눈물을 닦으며 대답했습니다. \"하지만 저는 저 사람이 걱정돼요."], ["He has been so careless and said so much that he never meant.", "저렇게 무심하게, 본심도 아닌 말을 그렇게나 많이 했잖아요."], ["The gentlemen of the juries might not understand him as Lignum and me do.", "배심원 신사들은 저를, 아니 저 사람을, 리그넘하고 저처럼 그렇게 알아들어 주지 않을지도 몰라요."], ["And then such a number of circumstances have happened bad for him, and such a number of people will be brought forward to speak against him, and Bucket is so deep.”", "게다가 저 사람한테 불리한 정황이 어찌나 많이 일어났는지, 또 저 사람한테 불리하게 증언하려고 나설 사람도 어찌나 많은지, 그리고 버킷은 어찌나 음흉한지요.\""]], [["“With a second-hand wiolinceller.", "\"중고 첼로 가지고. 게다가 어릴 때."], ["And said he played the fife. When a boy,”", "피리를 불었다고 말했지.\""], ["Mr. Bagnet added with great solemnity.", "배그넷 씨가 자못 엄숙하게 덧붙였습니다."]], [["“Now, I tell you, miss,” said Mrs.", "\"자, 들어보세요, 아가씨.\""], ["Bagnet; “and when I say miss, I mean all!", "배그넷 부인이 말했습니다. \"아가씨라고 했지만, 다들 들으시라는 거예요!"], ["Just come into the corner of the wall and I’ll tell you!”", "이 담벼락 모퉁이로 좀 오세요, 말씀드릴 테니!\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet hurried us into a more secluded place and was at first too breathless to proceed, occasioning Mr.", "배그넷 부인이 우리를 좀 더 으슥한 곳으로 급히 끌고 갔는데, 처음엔 너무 숨이 차서 말을 잇지 못했고, 그 바람에 배그넷 씨가 한마디 했습니다."], ["Bagnet to say, “Old girl!", "\"여보!"], ["Tell ’em!”", "어서 말해줘!\""]], [["“Why, then, miss,”", "\"그러니까요, 아가씨.\""], ["the old girl proceeded, untying the strings of her bonnet for more air, “you could as soon move Dover Castle as move George on this point unless you had got a new power to move him with.", "그녀가 바람을 더 쐬려고 보닛 끈을 풀면서 말을 이었습니다. \"조지를 이 일에서 돌려놓으려면, 저 사람을 움직일 새로운 힘이 없는 한 차라리 도버성을 옮기는 게 빠를 거예요."], ["And I have got it!”", "그런데 그 힘을 제가 손에 넣었답니다!\""]], [["“You are a jewel of a woman,”", "\"당신은 보석 같은 여인이로군요.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“Go on!”", "\"어서 말해보세요!\""]], [["“Now, I tell you, miss,”", "\"자, 들어보세요, 아가씨.\""], ["she proceeded, clapping her hands in her hurry and agitation a dozen times in every sentence, “that what he says concerning no relations is all bosh.", "그녀가 한 문장마다 열두 번씩 다급하게, 동요한 듯 두 손을 마주치며 말을 이었습니다. \"친척이 없다는 저 사람 말은 죄다 헛소리예요."], ["They don’t know of him, but he does know of them.", "저쪽이 저 사람을 모르는 거지, 저 사람은 저쪽을 안다고요."], ["He has said more to me at odd times than to anybody else, and it warn’t for nothing that he once spoke to my Woolwich about whitening and wrinkling mothers’ heads.", "저 사람이 다른 누구한테보다 저한테 이따금 말을 더 많이 했는데, 언젠가 우리 울리치한테 어머니 머리를 세게 하고 주름지게 만든다는 얘기를 한 게 괜한 게 아니었어요."], ["For fifty pounds he had seen his mother that day.", "오십 파운드를 걸어도 좋아요, 그날 저 사람은 어머니를 본 거예요."], ["She’s alive and must be brought here straight!”", "어머니가 살아 계셔요, 그러니 당장 이리 모셔 와야 해요!\""]], [["Instantly Mrs. Bagnet put some pins into her mouth and began pinning up her skirts all round a little higher than the level of her grey cloak, which she accomplished with surpassing dispatch and dexterity.", "배그넷 부인은 곧바로 핀 몇 개를 입에 물고, 잿빛 망토 높이보다 조금 더 위로 치맛자락을 빙 둘러 핀으로 걷어 올리기 시작했는데, 그 일을 비할 데 없이 날쌔고 능란하게 해냈습니다."]], [["“Lignum,” said Mrs.", "\"리그넘,\""], ["Bagnet, “you take care of the children, old man, and give me the umbrella!", "배그넷 부인이 말했습니다. \"당신은 애들을 잘 보고 있어요, 여보, 그리고 나한테 우산을 줘요!"], ["I’m away to Lincolnshire to bring that old lady here.”", "나는 그 노부인을 모셔 오러 링컨셔로 떠날 테니까.\""]], [["“But, bless the woman,”", "\"아니, 이런 부인 좀 보게.\""], ["cried my guardian with his hand in his pocket, “how is she going?", "후견인이 주머니에 손을 넣으며 외쳤습니다. \"그분이 어떻게 가신다는 겁니까?"], ["What money has she got?”", "돈은 얼마나 있고요?\""]], [["Mrs. Bagnet made another application to her skirts and brought forth a leathern purse in which she hastily counted over a few shillings and which she then shut up with perfect satisfaction.", "배그넷 부인이 다시 치맛자락을 더듬어 가죽 지갑을 꺼내, 그 안에서 실링 몇 닢을 황급히 세어보고는 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 도로 닫았습니다."]], [["“Never you mind for me, miss.", "\"제 걱정은 마세요, 아가씨."], ["I’m a soldier’s wife and accustomed to travel my own way.", "저는 군인의 아내라 제 방식대로 여행하는 데 익숙하답니다."], ["Lignum, old boy,”", "리그넘, 여보,\""], ["kissing him, “one for yourself, three for the children.", "그를 입맞추며 말했습니다. \"이건 당신 거고, 셋은 애들 거예요."], ["Now I’m away into Lincolnshire after George’s mother!”", "자, 나는 조지 어머니를 찾아 링컨셔로 떠나요!\""]], [["And she actually set off while we three stood looking at one another lost in amazement.", "그러고는 우리 셋이 어안이 벙벙해서 서로를 멀거니 바라보고 서 있는 사이에, 그녀는 정말로 길을 나섰습니다."], ["She actually trudged away in her grey cloak at a sturdy pace, and turned the corner, and was gone.", "잿빛 망토를 두르고 씩씩한 걸음으로 정말 터덜터덜 걸어가, 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌습니다."]], [["“Mr. Bagnet,”", "\"배그넷 씨.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“Do you mean to let her go in that way?”", "\"부인을 저대로 보내실 작정입니까?\""]], [["“Can’t help it,”", "\"어쩔 수 없습니다.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“Made her way home once from another quarter of the world.", "\"한번은 세상 저편에서도 혼자 집까지 찾아왔으니까요."], ["With the same grey cloak.", "똑같은 잿빛 망토 두르고."], ["And same umbrella.", "똑같은 우산 들고."], ["Whatever the old girl says, do. Do it!", "저 사람이 무슨 말을 하든, 따르시오."], ["Whenever the old girl says, I’LL do it.", "그냥 따르시오! 저 사람이 무슨 말을 하든, 나는 따른다오."], ["She does it.”", "저 사람이 해내거든요.\""]], [["“Then she is as honest and genuine as she looks,”", "\"그렇다면 부인은 보기만큼이나 정직하고 진실한 분이로군요.\""], ["rejoined my guardian, “and it is impossible to say more for her.”", "후견인이 받아 말했습니다. \"그 이상 더 좋은 말을 할 수 없겠습니다.\""]], [["“She’s Colour-Sergeant of the Nonpareil battalion,”", "\"저 사람은 둘도 없는 부대의 선임 부사관이지요.\""], ["said Mr. Bagnet, looking at us over his shoulder as he went his way also.", "배그넷 씨가 자기도 길을 나서며 어깨너머로 우리를 돌아보고 말했습니다."], ["“And there’s not such another.", "\"저런 사람이 또 없습니다."], ["But I never own to it before her.", "하지만 저 사람 앞에서는 절대 그런 말 안 합니다."], ["Discipline must be maintained.”", "군기는 지켜야 하니까요.\""]], [["The Track\nMr. Bucket and his fat forefinger are much in consultation together under existing circumstances.", "버킷 씨와 그 통통한 집게손가락은 지금 같은 상황에서 자주 머리를 맞대고 의논을 나눈다."], ["When Mr. Bucket has a matter of this pressing interest under his consideration, the fat forefinger seems to rise, to the dignity of a familiar demon.", "버킷 씨가 이처럼 다급한 문제를 골똘히 궁리할 때면 그 통통한 집게손가락은 마치 친근한 정령의 품격으로 곧추서는 듯하다."], ["He puts it to his ears, and it whispers information; he puts it to his lips, and it enjoins him to secrecy; he rubs it over his nose, and it sharpens his scent; he shakes it before a guilty man, and it charms him to his destruction.", "손가락을 귀에 갖다 대면 손가락이 정보를 속삭이고, 입술에 갖다 대면 비밀을 지키라 명령하고, 코를 문지르면 후각을 예리하게 다듬어주고, 죄지은 자 앞에서 흔들면 그자를 홀려 파멸로 이끈다."], ["The Augurs of the Detective Temple invariably predict that when Mr. Bucket and that finger are in much conference, a terrible avenger will be heard of before long.", "형사 신전의 점쟁이들은 한결같이 예언하니, 버킷 씨와 그 손가락이 한참 회의를 벌이면 머지않아 무시무시한 복수자의 소식이 들려오리라는 것이다."]], [["Otherwise mildly studious in his observation of human nature, on the whole a benignant philosopher not disposed to be severe upon the follies of mankind, Mr. Bucket pervades a vast number of houses and strolls about an infinity of streets, to outward appearance rather languishing for want of an object.", "평소에는 인간 본성을 온화하게 탐구하는 편이라, 대체로 인류의 어리석음을 호되게 나무라지 않는 너그러운 철학자인 버킷 씨는, 숱한 집을 두루 누비고 끝도 없는 거리를 어슬렁거리는데, 겉보기엔 마치 목적을 잃고 시들시들 헤매는 사람 같다."], ["He is in the friendliest condition towards his species and will drink with most of them.", "그는 자기 동족을 더없이 다정하게 대하며 거의 누구와도 함께 술잔을 기울인다."], ["He is free with his money, affable in his manners, innocent in his conversation—but through the placid stream of his life there glides an under-current of forefinger.", "돈을 쓰는 데 인색하지 않고, 태도가 상냥하며, 대화는 천진난만하다—그러나 잔잔히 흐르는 그의 삶 밑바닥에는 집게손가락의 저류가 미끄러져 흐른다."]], [["Time and place cannot bind Mr. Bucket.", "시간도 장소도 버킷 씨를 묶어두지 못한다."], ["Like man in the abstract, he is here to-day and gone to-morrow—but, very unlike man indeed, he is here again the next day.", "추상적인 인간처럼 그는 오늘 여기 있고 내일은 사라진다—그러나 인간과는 사뭇 달리, 다음 날이면 다시 여기 와 있다."], ["This evening he will be casually looking into the iron extinguishers at the door of Sir Leicester Dedlock’s house in town; and to-morrow morning he will be walking on the leads at Chesney Wold, where erst the old man walked whose ghost is propitiated with a hundred guineas.", "오늘 저녁이면 그는 도심에 있는 레스터 데드록 경 저택 문간의 철제 횃불 소화기를 무심한 듯 들여다볼 것이고, 내일 아침이면 체스니 월드의 납지붕 위를 거닐 것이니, 그곳은 옛날 그 노인이 거닐던 곳, 백 기니로 그 유령을 달랜다는 바로 그 자리다."], ["Drawers, desks, pockets, all things belonging to him, Mr. Bucket examines.", "서랍이며 책상이며 호주머니며, 그자에게 딸린 모든 물건을 버킷 씨는 샅샅이 살핀다."], ["A few hours afterwards, he and the Roman will be alone together comparing forefingers.", "그러고 몇 시간 뒤면 그와 로마인이 단둘이 마주 앉아 집게손가락을 견주고 있으리라."]], [["It is likely that these occupations are irreconcilable with home enjoyment, but it is certain that Mr.", "* 로마인: 토킹혼 씨 방 천장에 그려진 로마인 그림으로, 아래쪽을 손가락으로 가리키고 있다."], ["Bucket at present does not go home. Though in general he highly appreciates the society of Mrs.", "이런 일들이 가정의 단란함과는 도무지 어울리지 않으리라는 건 짐작할 만하지만, 버킷 씨가 지금 집에 들어가지 않는다는 것만은 분명하다."], ["Bucket—a lady of a natural detective genius, which if it had been improved by professional exercise, might have done great things, but which has paused at the level of a clever amateur—he holds himself aloof from that dear solace. Mrs.", "버킷 부인의 동무 노릇을 평소엔 그가 높이 사면서도—타고난 형사 천재성을 지닌 부인이라, 직업적 단련으로 갈고닦았다면 큰일을 해냈을 테지만 영리한 아마추어 수준에서 멈춰버린 분이다—그 정겨운 위안만큼은 멀찍이 물려둔다."], ["Bucket is dependent on their lodger (fortunately an amiable lady in whom she takes an interest) for companionship and conversation.", "버킷 부인은 동무와 대화를 그 집 하숙인(다행히 부인이 마음을 쓰는 상냥한 여인이다)에게 기대고 있다."]], [["A great crowd assembles in Lincoln’s Inn Fields on the day of the funeral.", "장례식 날 링컨스 인 광장에 큰 군중이 모여든다. 레스터 데드록 경이 몸소 의식에 참석한다."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock attends the ceremony in person; strictly speaking, there are only three other human followers, that is to say, Lord Doodle, William Buffy, and the debilitated cousin (thrown in as a make-weight), but the amount of inconsolable carriages is immense.", "엄밀히 말하면 그 밖에 사람으로 따라온 이는 셋뿐이니, 두들 경과 윌리엄 버피, 그리고 (머릿수 채우려 끼워 넣은) 골골한 친척이다. 그러나 비탄에 잠긴 마차의 수효는 어마어마하다."], ["The peerage contributes more four-wheeled affliction than has ever been seen in that neighbourhood.", "귀족 사회가 이 동네에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 네 바퀴 달린 애도를 한껏 쏟아낸다."], ["Such is the assemblage of armorial bearings on coach panels that the Herald’s College might be supposed to have lost its father and mother at a blow.", "마차 측면에 새긴 문장이 어찌나 빼곡한지, 마치 문장원이 하루아침에 부모를 한꺼번에 잃기라도 한 듯하다."], ["The Duke of Foodle sends a splendid pile of dust and ashes, with silver wheel-boxes, patent axles, all the last improvements, and three bereaved worms, six feet high, holding on behind, in a bunch of woe.", "푸들 공작은 은제 바퀴통과 특허받은 차축에 최신 개량을 모조리 갖춘 호화로운 먼지와 재 한 무더기를 보내고, 그 뒤에 키 큰 슬픔의 벌레 셋이 비탄의 다발을 이루어 매달려 있다."], ["All the state coachmen in London seem plunged into mourning; and if that dead old man of the rusty garb be not beyond a taste in horseflesh (which appears impossible), it must be highly gratified this day.", "런던의 모든 의전 마부가 죄다 상복에 잠긴 듯하다. 만일 녹슨 옷차림의 그 죽은 노인에게 말 보는 안목이 남아 있다면(그럴 리야 없겠지만), 오늘 같은 날엔 무척이나 흡족하리라."]], [["Quiet among the undertakers and the equipages and the calves of so many legs all steeped in grief, Mr. Bucket sits concealed in one of the inconsolable carriages and at his ease surveys the crowd through the lattice blinds.", "장의사들과 마차 행렬, 그리고 슬픔에 흠뻑 젖은 그 숱한 종아리 사이에 끼어, 버킷 씨는 비탄에 잠긴 마차 한 대 속에 몸을 숨기고 앉아 격자 차양 너머로 느긋하게 군중을 살핀다."], ["He has a keen eye for a crowd—as for what not?—and looking here and there, now from this side of the carriage, now from the other, now up at the house windows, now along the people’s heads, nothing escapes him.", "그는 군중을 보는 눈썰미가 날카롭다—하기야 못 보는 게 어디 있으랴?—이쪽저쪽 둘러보며, 한 번은 마차 이쪽에서, 한 번은 저쪽에서, 한 번은 저택 창문을 올려다보고, 한 번은 사람들 머리 위를 훑으니, 그의 눈을 벗어나는 것이 없다."]], [["“And there you are, my partner, eh?” says Mr.", "\"옳지, 거기 있구먼, 내 짝꿍, 응?\""], ["Bucket to himself, apostrophizing Mrs. Bucket, stationed, by his favour, on the steps of the deceased’s house.", "버킷 씨는 혼잣말로, 자기가 일러둔 대로 고인의 저택 계단에 자리 잡은 버킷 부인에게 말을 건넨다."], ["“And so you are.", "\"그래, 거기 있구먼."], ["And so you are!", "거기 있어!"], ["And very well indeed you are looking, Mrs. Bucket!”", "아주 멀쩡해 보이는걸, 버킷 부인!\""]], [["The procession has not started yet, but is waiting for the cause of its assemblage to be brought out.", "행렬은 아직 출발하지 않고, 모임의 까닭이 된 인물이 운구되어 나오기를 기다리고 있다."], ["Mr. Bucket, in the foremost emblazoned carriage, uses his two fat forefingers to hold the lattice a hair’s breadth open while he looks.", "가장 앞쪽 문장 새긴 마차에 탄 버킷 씨는 통통한 집게손가락 두 개로 격자를 머리카락 한 올만큼 벌려 잡고 바깥을 내다본다."]], [["And it says a great deal for his attachment, as a husband, that he is still occupied with Mrs. B.", "그리고 그가 여전히 버킷 부인에게 골몰해 있다는 것은, 남편으로서 그 정이 얼마나 깊은지를 잘 보여준다."], ["“There you are, my partner, eh?”", "\"거기 있구먼, 내 짝꿍, 응?\""], ["he murmuringly repeats.", "그는 중얼중얼 되뇐다."], ["“And our lodger with you.", "\"우리 하숙인도 같이 있고."], ["I’m taking notice of you, Mrs.", "내가 자네를 눈여겨보고 있다네, 버킷 부인."], ["Bucket; I hope you’re all right in your health, my dear!”", "몸성히 잘 있길 바라네, 여보!\""]], [["Not another word does Mr. Bucket say, but sits with most attentive eyes until the sacked depository of noble secrets is brought down—Where are all those secrets now?", "버킷 씨는 더는 한마디도 하지 않고, 더없이 주의 깊은 눈으로 앉아 있다가, 귀족의 비밀이 가득 담겼던 자루 같은 그 보관소가 운구되어 내려오고—그 모든 비밀은 지금 어디 있을까?"], ["Does he keep them yet?", "그자가 아직 간직하고 있을까?"], ["Did they fly with him on that sudden journey?—and until the procession moves, and Mr. Bucket’s view is changed.", "그 느닷없는 여정에 함께 날아가버렸을까?—이윽고 행렬이 움직여 버킷 씨의 시야가 바뀔 때까지 그대로 있다."], ["After which he composes himself for an easy ride and takes note of the fittings of the carriage in case he should ever find such knowledge useful.", "그러고 나서 그는 편안한 승차에 몸을 맡길 채비를 하고, 혹시 이런 지식이 쓸모 있을 날이 올까 싶어 마차의 비품을 눈여겨봐 둔다."]], [["Contrast enough between Mr.", "어두운 마차에 갇힌 토킹혼 씨와 자기 마차에 갇힌 버킷 씨, 그 대비란!"], ["Tulkinghorn shut up in his dark carriage and Mr. Bucket shut up in HIS.", "한쪽은 그 작은 상처 하나가 그를 옴짝달싹 못 하는 잠 속으로 던져넣어 거리의 돌바닥 위에서 그토록 무겁게 덜컹대게 하고, 그 상처 너머로는 헤아릴 수 없이 광막한 공간이 펼쳐져 있다."], ["Between the immeasurable track of space beyond the little wound that has thrown the one into the fixed sleep which jolts so heavily over the stones of the streets, and the narrow track of blood which keeps the other in the watchful state expressed in every hair of his head!", "다른 한쪽은 머리카락 한 올 한 올마다 깃든 그 빈틈없는 경계 상태로 그를 붙잡아두는, 좁다란 핏줄기의 자취가 있을 뿐이다! 그러나 두 사람에겐 어차피 매한가지다."], ["But it is all one to both; neither is troubled about that.", "어느 쪽도 그런 일로 마음 쓰지 않는다."]], [["Mr. Bucket sits out the procession in his own easy manner and glides from the carriage when the opportunity he has settled with himself arrives.", "버킷 씨는 특유의 느긋한 태도로 행렬을 끝까지 지켜보다가, 미리 마음먹어둔 기회가 오자 마차에서 슬그머니 빠져나온다."], ["He makes for Sir Leicester Dedlock’s, which is at present a sort of home to him, where he comes and goes as he likes at all hours, where he is always welcome and made much of, where he knows the whole establishment, and walks in an atmosphere of mysterious greatness.", "그는 레스터 데드록 경의 저택으로 향하는데, 그곳은 지금 그에게 일종의 제집 같은 곳이라, 시간을 가리지 않고 마음대로 드나들며, 늘 환대받고 떠받들어지며, 집안 전체를 훤히 꿰고서, 신비로운 위엄의 분위기 속을 거니는 곳이다."]], [["No knocking or ringing for Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨는 노크도 초인종도 필요 없다."], ["He has caused himself to be provided with a key and can pass in at his pleasure.", "그는 미리 손써서 열쇠를 마련해두었기에 마음 내키는 대로 들어갈 수 있다. 그가 현관을 가로지르는데 머큐리가 알린다."], ["As he is crossing the hall, Mercury informs him, “Here’s another letter for you, Mr.", "\"또 편지 한 통 왔습니다, 버킷 씨, 우편으로요.\" 그러면서 편지를 건넨다."], ["Bucket, come by post,”", "* 머큐리: 화려한 제복을 입은 하인을 빗댄 표현."], ["and gives it him.", "본디 로마 신화의 전령 신."]], [["“Another one, eh?”", "\"또 한 통이라, 응?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["If Mercury should chance to be possessed by any lingering curiosity as to Mr. Bucket’s letters, that wary person is not the man to gratify it.", "행여 머큐리가 버킷 씨의 편지를 두고 무슨 호기심을 조금이라도 품고 있더라도, 그 빈틈없는 인물은 그 호기심을 채워줄 위인이 아니다."], ["Mr. Bucket looks at him as if his face were a vista of some miles in length and he were leisurely contemplating the same.", "버킷 씨는 마치 머큐리의 얼굴이 몇 마일이나 뻗은 풍경이고 자기는 그것을 한가로이 감상하기라도 하는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라본다."]], [["“Do you happen to carry a box?”", "\"혹시 코담뱃갑 가지고 다니나?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 묻는다."]], [["Unfortunately Mercury is no snuff-taker.", "공교롭게도 머큐리는 코담배를 하지 않는다."]], [["“Could you fetch me a pinch from anywheres?”", "\"어디서든 한 줌 가져다줄 수 있겠나?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Thankee.", "\"고맙네."], ["It don’t matter what it is; I’m not particular as to the kind.", "무슨 종류든 상관없네. 가리지 않으니까."], ["Thankee!”", "고맙네!\""]], [["Having leisurely helped himself from a canister borrowed from somebody downstairs for the purpose, and having made a considerable show of tasting it, first with one side of his nose and then with the other, Mr. Bucket, with much deliberation, pronounces it of the right sort and goes on, letter in hand.", "누군가 아래층에서 일부러 빌려온 통에서 느긋하게 한 줌 떠넣고, 한 번은 콧구멍 이쪽으로 한 번은 저쪽으로 제법 거하게 음미하는 시늉을 하고 나서, 버킷 씨는 무척 신중하게 제대로 된 물건이라 선언하고는, 편지를 손에 든 채 걸음을 옮긴다."]], [["Now although Mr. Bucket walks upstairs to the little library within the larger one with the face of a man who receives some scores of letters every day, it happens that much correspondence is not incidental to his life.", "그런데 버킷 씨가 큰 서재 안의 작은 서재로 올라가는 그 얼굴은 하루에 편지를 수십 통씩 받는 사람의 얼굴이건만, 사실 그의 삶에 서신 왕래가 흔한 일은 아니다."], ["He is no great scribe, rather handling his pen like the pocket-staff he carries about with him always convenient to his grasp, and discourages correspondence with himself in others as being too artless and direct a way of doing delicate business.", "그는 글쓰기에 능한 사람이 아니라, 늘 손닿는 곳에 지니고 다니는 호주머니 지팡이를 다루듯 펜을 다루며, 자기에게 편지를 보내는 일을 남들에게 권하지도 않으니, 그것이 미묘한 일을 처리하기엔 너무 어수룩하고 곧이곧대로인 방식이라 여기기 때문이다."], ["Further, he often sees damaging letters produced in evidence and has occasion to reflect that it was a green thing to write them.", "게다가 그는 증거로 제출된 불리한 편지들을 숱하게 봐왔기에, 그런 걸 써댄다는 게 얼마나 풋내기 짓인지 곱씹을 일이 많았다."], ["For these reasons he has very little to do with letters, either as sender or receiver.", "이런 까닭에 그는 편지와는, 보내는 쪽이든 받는 쪽이든, 거의 인연이 없다."], ["And yet he has received a round half-dozen within the last twenty-four hours.", "그런데도 지난 스물네 시간 동안 무려 여섯 통이나 받았다."]], [["“And this,” says Mr.", "\"그리고 이것은,\""], ["Bucket, spreading it out on the table, “is in the same hand, and consists of the same two words.”", "버킷 씨가 편지를 탁자 위에 펼쳐놓으며 말한다. \"똑같은 필체에다, 똑같은 두 단어로 되어 있군.\""]], [["What two words?", "무슨 두 단어인가?"]], [["He turns the key in the door, ungirdles his black pocket-book (book of fate to many), lays another letter by it, and reads, boldly written in each, “Lady Dedlock.”", "그는 문에 열쇠를 돌려 잠그고, (숱한 이들에게 운명의 책인) 검은 수첩의 끈을 풀고서, 그 옆에 또 다른 편지 한 통을 나란히 놓고는, 둘 다에 굵직하게 적힌 글씨를 읽는다. \"데드록 마님.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“But I could have made the money without this anonymous information.”", "\"하지만 이런 익명의 정보가 없었어도 그 돈은 벌 수 있었을걸.\""]], [["Having put the letters in his book of fate and girdled it up again, he unlocks the door just in time to admit his dinner, which is brought upon a goodly tray with a decanter of sherry.", "편지들을 운명의 책에 도로 넣고 다시 끈을 묶고 나서, 그가 문을 따자 마침 저녁 식사가 들어온다. 셰리주 한 병을 곁들인 그득한 쟁반에 받쳐 들어온다."], ["Mr. Bucket frequently observes, in friendly circles where there is no restraint, that he likes a toothful of your fine old brown East Inder sherry better than anything you can offer him.", "버킷 씨는 거리낌 없는 친구들 사이에서 곧잘 말하곤 하니, 잘 익은 갈색 동인도 셰리주를 한 모금 들이켜는 것이 세상 무엇보다 좋다고 한다."], ["Consequently he fills and empties his glass with a smack of his lips and is proceeding with his refreshment when an idea enters his mind.", "그리하여 그는 입맛을 쩝쩝 다시며 잔을 채웠다 비웠다 하며 다과를 즐기던 중에 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠오른다."]], [["Mr. Bucket softly opens the door of communication between that room and the next and looks in.", "버킷 씨는 그 방과 옆방을 잇는 문을 살며시 열고 안을 들여다본다."], ["The library is deserted, and the fire is sinking low.", "서재는 텅 비어 있고, 난롯불은 잦아들고 있다."], ["Mr. Bucket’s eye, after taking a pigeon-flight round the room, alights upon a table where letters are usually put as they arrive.", "버킷 씨의 눈은 방 안을 비둘기처럼 한 바퀴 휙 돌고 나서, 편지가 도착하면 으레 올려두는 탁자 위에 내려앉는다."], ["Several letters for Sir Leicester are upon it. Mr.", "그 위에 레스터 경 앞으로 온 편지 몇 통이 놓여 있다."], ["Bucket draws near and examines the directions. “No,”", "버킷 씨는 가까이 다가가 수신인을 살핀다."], ["he says, “there’s none in that hand.", "\"아니로군.\" 그가 말한다."], ["It’s only me as is written to.", "\"그 필체로 된 건 없어."], ["I can break it to Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to-morrow.”", "편지 받는 건 나뿐이군. 레스터 데드록 준남작께는 내일 알려드리면 되겠어.\""]], [["With that he returns to finish his dinner with a good appetite, and after a light nap, is summoned into the drawing-room.", "그러고 그는 입맛 좋게 저녁 식사를 마저 끝내러 돌아갔다가, 한숨 가볍게 자고 나서 응접실로 불려간다."], ["Sir Leicester has received him there these several evenings past to know whether he has anything to report.", "레스터 경은 지난 며칠 저녁 내내 그를 그곳에서 맞아, 보고할 만한 게 있는지 묻곤 했다."], ["The debilitated cousin (much exhausted by the funeral) and Volumnia are in attendance.", "(장례식으로 몹시 지친) 골골한 친척과 볼럼니아가 곁에 시중들고 있다."]], [["Mr. Bucket makes three distinctly different bows to these three people.", "버킷 씨는 이 세 사람에게 저마다 뚜렷이 다른 절을 세 번 한다."], ["A bow of homage to Sir Leicester, a bow of gallantry to Volumnia, and a bow of recognition to the debilitated Cousin, to whom it airily says, “You are a swell about town, and you know me, and I know you.”", "레스터 경에게는 공경의 절을, 볼럼니아에게는 정중한 흠모의 절을, 골골한 친척에게는 알아본다는 절을 하는데, 그 절은 가볍게 이렇게 말하는 듯하다. \"당신은 도심의 멋쟁이고, 당신은 나를 알고, 나도 당신을 알지요.\""], ["Having distributed these little specimens of his tact, Mr. Bucket rubs his hands.", "이렇게 자기 처세술의 작은 표본들을 골고루 나눠주고 나서, 버킷 씨는 두 손을 비빈다."]], [["“Have you anything new to communicate, officer?”", "\"새로 전할 소식이라도 있나, 경관?\""], ["inquires Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 묻는다."], ["“Do you wish to hold any conversation with me in private?”", "\"나와 따로 나눌 이야기라도 있는가?\""]], [["“Why—not to-night, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”", "\"글쎄요—오늘 밤은 아닙니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\""]], [["“Because my time,”", "\"왜냐하면 내 시간은,\""], ["pursues Sir Leicester, “is wholly at your disposal with a view to the vindication of the outraged majesty of the law.”", "레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"능욕당한 법의 존엄을 되찾는 일이라면 전적으로 자네 마음대로 쓸 수 있으니 말이네.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket coughs and glances at Volumnia, rouged and necklaced, as though he would respectfully observe, “I do assure you, you’re a pretty creetur.", "버킷 씨는 헛기침을 하고는 연지를 바르고 목걸이를 두른 볼럼니아를 흘끔 보는데, 마치 정중하게 이렇게 말하려는 듯하다."], ["I’ve seen hundreds worse looking at your time of life, I have indeed.”", "\"정말이지, 당신은 어여쁜 분이외다. 당신 연배에 당신보다 못한 얼굴을 수백 명은 봤습니다, 정말이지.\""]], [["The fair Volumnia, not quite unconscious perhaps of the humanizing influence of her charms, pauses in the writing of cocked-hat notes and meditatively adjusts the pearl necklace. Mr.", "어여쁜 볼럼니아는 자기 매력이 인간미를 자아내는 힘이 있다는 걸 어쩌면 어렴풋이 알고 있는지, 삼각모 모양으로 접던 쪽지 쓰기를 멈추고 생각에 잠긴 듯 진주 목걸이를 매만진다."], ["Bucket prices that decoration in his mind and thinks it as likely as not that Volumnia is writing poetry.", "버킷 씨는 속으로 그 장신구의 값을 어림하고는, 볼럼니아가 시를 쓰고 있을 법도 하다고 짐작한다."]], [["“If I have not,”", "\"만일 내가,\""], ["pursues Sir Leicester, “in the most emphatic manner, adjured you, officer, to exercise your utmost skill in this atrocious case, I particularly desire to take the present opportunity of rectifying any omission I may have made.", "레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"이 극악무도한 사건에 자네의 온갖 기량을 다 쏟으라고 더없이 단호하게 당부하지 않았다면, 나는 지금 이 자리를 빌려 혹 빠뜨린 것이 있다면 바로잡고자 하네."], ["Let no expense be a consideration.", "비용은 일절 따지지 말게."], ["I am prepared to defray all charges.", "모든 경비를 내가 치를 작정이네."], ["You can incur none in pursuit of the object you have undertaken that I shall hesitate for a moment to bear.”", "자네가 맡은 목적을 좇느라 쓰는 비용이라면, 그 어떤 것이라도 내가 단 한순간이라도 망설이며 떠맡지 않을 까닭이 없네.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket made Sir Leicester’s bow again as a response to this liberality.", "버킷 씨는 이 너그러움에 답하여 다시 한번 레스터 경에게 올리던 절을 한다."]], [["“My mind,”", "\"내 마음은,\""], ["Sir Leicester adds with a generous warmth, “has not, as may be easily supposed, recovered its tone since the late diabolical occurrence.", "레스터 경이 너그러운 온정을 담아 덧붙인다. \"쉬 짐작하겠지만, 그 끔찍한 사건 이후로 평정을 되찾지 못했네."], ["It is not likely ever to recover its tone.", "앞으로도 결코 되찾지 못하겠지."], ["But it is full of indignation to-night after undergoing the ordeal of consigning to the tomb the remains of a faithful, a zealous, a devoted adherent.”", "그러나 오늘 밤은, 충직하고 열성스럽고 헌신적이던 한 추종자의 유해를 무덤에 묻는 시련을 겪고 나니, 분노로 가슴이 가득하네.\""]], [["Sir Leicester’s voice trembles and his grey hair stirs upon his head.", "레스터 경의 목소리가 떨리고 잿빛 머리카락이 머리 위에서 흔들린다."], ["Tears are in his eyes; the best part of his nature is aroused.", "두 눈에 눈물이 고이고, 그의 본성에서 가장 고결한 부분이 일깨워진다."]], [["“I declare,”", "\"단언하건대,\""], ["he says, “I solemnly declare that until this crime is discovered and, in the course of justice, punished, I almost feel as if there were a stain upon my name.", "그가 말한다. \"엄숙히 단언하건대, 이 범죄가 밝혀지고 정의의 절차에 따라 처벌되기 전까지는, 내 이름에 오점이 묻은 것만 같은 심정이네."], ["A gentleman who has devoted a large portion of his life to me, a gentleman who has devoted the last day of his life to me, a gentleman who has constantly sat at my table and slept under my roof, goes from my house to his own, and is struck down within an hour of his leaving my house.", "자기 인생의 큰 부분을 내게 바친 신사, 자기 인생의 마지막 날을 내게 바친 신사, 늘 내 식탁에 앉고 내 지붕 아래 잠들던 신사가, 내 집에서 제집으로 떠났다가, 내 집을 나선 지 한 시간도 안 되어 쓰러졌네."], ["I cannot say but that he may have been followed from my house, watched at my house, even first marked because of his association with my house—which may have suggested his possessing greater wealth and being altogether of greater importance than his own retiring demeanour would have indicated.", "그가 내 집에서부터 미행당했을 수도, 내 집에서 감시당했을 수도, 심지어 내 집과 관계가 있다는 이유로 처음부터 표적이 되었을 수도 있다는 걸 부정할 수 없네—그 관계 탓에, 그가 제 몸을 사리던 처신으로는 짐작도 못 할 만큼 더 큰 재산을 지녔고 훨씬 더 중요한 인물이라 여겨졌을지도 모르니 말이네."], ["If I cannot with my means and influence and my position bring all the perpetrators of such a crime to light, I fail in the assertion of my respect for that gentleman’s memory and of my fidelity towards one who was ever faithful to me.”", "내가 가진 재력과 영향력과 지위로도 이런 범죄를 저지른 자들을 모조리 밝혀내지 못한다면, 나는 그 신사의 추억을 향한 존경도, 늘 내게 충직하던 이에 대한 신의도 지켜내지 못하는 것이네.\""]], [["While he makes this protestation with great emotion and earnestness, looking round the room as if he were addressing an assembly, Mr. Bucket glances at him with an observant gravity in which there might be, but for the audacity of the thought, a touch of compassion.", "그가 큰 감정과 진심을 담아 이렇게 단언하며, 마치 회중을 앞에 두고 연설하듯 방 안을 둘러보는 동안, 버킷 씨는 관찰하는 듯한 진중한 눈으로 그를 흘끔 보는데, 무엄한 생각인지 몰라도 그 눈에는 어쩌면 한 줄기 연민이 어려 있을지도 모른다."]], [["“The ceremony of to-day,”", "\"오늘의 의식은,\""], ["continues Sir Leicester, “strikingly illustrative of the respect in which my deceased friend”—he lays a stress upon the word, for death levels all distinctions—“was held by the flower of the land, has, I say, aggravated the shock I have received from this most horrible and audacious crime.", "레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"고인이 된 내 벗이\"—그는 죽음이 모든 차별을 평등하게 만든다는 듯 그 말에 힘을 준다—\"이 나라의 정화 같은 분들께 어떤 존경을 받았는지를 놀랍도록 잘 보여주었거니와, 그것이, 말하건대, 이 더없이 끔찍하고 대담한 범죄로 내가 받은 충격을 한층 더 키웠네."], ["If it were my brother who had committed it, I would not spare him.”", "설령 이 짓을 저지른 자가 내 형제라 해도, 나는 용서치 않을 것이네.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket looks very grave.", "버킷 씨는 자못 진중한 표정을 짓는다."], ["Volumnia remarks of the deceased that he was the trustiest and dearest person!", "볼럼니아는 고인이 더없이 믿음직스럽고 더없이 소중한 분이었노라 한마디 한다!"]], [["“You must feel it as a deprivation to you, miss,” replies Mr.", "\"아가씨께는 큰 상실로 느껴지시겠지요.\""], ["Bucket soothingly, “no doubt.", "버킷 씨가 달래듯 대답한다."], ["He was calculated to BE a deprivation, I’m sure he was.”", "\"당연하고말고요. 그분은 상실감을 자아낼 만한 분이었지요, 정말 그러셨고말고요.\""]], [["Volumnia gives Mr. Bucket to understand, in reply, that her sensitive mind is fully made up never to get the better of it as long as she lives, that her nerves are unstrung for ever, and that she has not the least expectation of ever smiling again.", "볼럼니아는 그 대답으로, 자기 섬세한 마음이 평생토록 이 슬픔을 결코 떨쳐내지 못하리라 굳게 마음먹었으며, 신경이 영영 곤두서버렸고, 다시 미소 지을 날이 오리라곤 조금도 기대하지 않는다는 것을 버킷 씨에게 알린다."], ["Meanwhile she folds up a cocked hat for that redoubtable old general at Bath, descriptive of her melancholy condition.", "그러는 동안에도 그녀는 바스에 사는 그 무시무시한 늙은 장군에게 보낼 삼각모 쪽지를 접으며, 자기의 우울한 처지를 그려 보인다."]], [["“It gives a start to a delicate female,” says Mr.", "\"섬세한 여성분께는 충격이지요.\""], ["Bucket sympathetically, “but it’ll wear off.”", "버킷 씨가 공감하며 말한다. \"허나 차차 가실 겁니다.\""]], [["Volumnia wishes of all things to know what is doing?", "볼럼니아는 무엇보다도 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 알고 싶어 한다."], ["Whether they are going to convict, or whatever it is, that dreadful soldier?", "그들이 그 무시무시한 군인을 유죄로 만들려는 것인지, 아니면 뭐라고 부르든 그런 걸 하려는 것인지?"], ["Whether he had any accomplices, or whatever the thing is called in the law?", "그자에게 공범이 있었는지, 아니면 법률 용어로 뭐라고 하든 그런 게 있었는지?"], ["And a great deal more to the like artless purpose.", "그러고는 그런 식의 천진한 질문을 한참 더 늘어놓는다."]], [["“Why you see, miss,”", "\"글쎄요, 아가씨.\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket, bringing the finger into persuasive action—and such is his natural gallantry that he had almost said “my dear”—“it ain’t easy to answer those questions at the present moment.", "버킷 씨가 그 손가락을 설득하듯 움직이며 대답한다—그리고 타고난 정중함이 어찌나 몸에 뱄는지 하마터면 \"여보\"라고 할 뻔한다—\"지금 이 순간엔 그런 질문에 답하기가 쉽지 않습니다."], ["Not at the present moment.", "지금 이 순간엔 말이지요."], ["I’ve kept myself on this case, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” whom Mr.", "저는 이 사건에 매달려왔습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["Bucket takes into the conversation in right of his importance, “morning, noon, and night.", "버킷 씨는 레스터 경의 중요한 신분에 걸맞게 그를 대화에 끌어들인다."], ["But for a glass or two of sherry, I don’t think I could have had my mind so much upon the stretch as it has been.", "\"아침이고 한낮이고 한밤이고요. 셰리주 한두 잔이 아니었다면, 제 머리를 이토록 팽팽히 당겨두지는 못했을 겁니다."], ["I COULD answer your questions, miss, but duty forbids it.", "저는 아가씨의 질문에 답할 수도 있지만, 직무가 그것을 금합니다."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, will very soon be made acquainted with all that has been traced.", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께서는 머지않아 밝혀진 모든 것을 알게 되실 겁니다."], ["And I hope that he may find it”—Mr. Bucket again looks grave—“to his satisfaction.”", "그리고 부디 그것이\"—버킷 씨가 다시 진중한 표정을 짓는다—\"나리의 마음에 흡족하길 바랍니다.\""]], [["The debilitated cousin only hopes some fler’ll be executed—zample.", "골골한 친척은 그저 누구든 하나 처형되길—본보기로—바랄 뿐이다."], ["Thinks more interest’s wanted—get man hanged presentime—than get man place ten thousand a year.", "만 파운드짜리 자리에 사람 앉히는 것보다, 사람 하나 당장 목매다는 게 더 어려운 노릇이라고—더 손써야 한다고 본다."], ["Hasn’t a doubt—zample—far better hang wrong fler than no fler.", "의심할 바 없으니—본보기로—엉뚱한 놈이라도 하나 목매다는 게 아무도 안 매다는 것보다 훨씬 낫다는 것이다."]], [["“YOU know life, you know, sir,” says Mr.", "\"당신은 세상 물정을 아시지요, 안 그렇습니까, 나리.\""], ["Bucket with a complimentary twinkle of his eye and crook of his finger, “and you can confirm what I’ve mentioned to this lady.", "버킷 씨가 칭찬하듯 눈을 반짝이고 손가락을 구부리며 말한다. \"그러니 제가 이 아가씨께 말씀드린 것을 당신이 거들어주실 수 있겠지요."], ["YOU don’t want to be told that from information I have received I have gone to work.", "당신께는 제가 입수한 정보에 따라 일에 착수했다고 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없습니다."], ["You’re up to what a lady can’t be expected to be up to. Lord!", "당신은 여성분께 기대할 수 없는 것까지도 꿰뚫고 계시니까요."], ["Especially in your elevated station of society, miss,” says Mr.", "원, 당신처럼 높은 사회적 지위에 계신 분이야 더더욱 그렇지요, 아가씨.\""], ["Bucket, quite reddening at another narrow escape from “my dear.”", "버킷 씨가 또 한 번 \"여보\" 소리가 아슬아슬하게 새어 나올 뻔한 통에 얼굴이 발그레해지며 말한다."]], [["“The officer, Volumnia,”", "\"이 경관은, 볼럼니아,\""], ["observes Sir Leicester, “is faithful to his duty, and perfectly right.”", "레스터 경이 말한다. \"제 직무에 충실하니, 더없이 옳은 일이지.\""]], [["Mr.", "버킷 씨가 중얼거린다."], ["Bucket murmurs, “Glad to have the honour of your approbation, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”", "\"나리의 인정을 받는 영광을 누리니 기쁩니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\""]], [["“In fact, Volumnia,”", "\"실은, 볼럼니아,\""], ["proceeds Sir Leicester, “it is not holding up a good model for imitation to ask the officer any such questions as you have put to him.", "레스터 경이 말을 잇는다. \"이 경관에게 네가 던진 그런 질문을 하는 것은 본받을 만한 본보기가 못 되네."], ["He is the best judge of his own responsibility; he acts upon his responsibility.", "그는 자기 책임을 누구보다 잘 판단하는 사람이고, 자기 책임으로 행동하는 사람이네."], ["And it does not become us, who assist in making the laws, to impede or interfere with those who carry them into execution.", "또 법을 만드는 일을 거드는 우리가, 그 법을 집행하는 이들을 가로막거나 간섭하는 것은 도리가 아니지."], ["Or,”", "아니면,\""], ["says Sir Leicester somewhat sternly, for Volumnia was going to cut in before he had rounded his sentence, “or who vindicate their outraged majesty.”", "레스터 경이 다소 엄하게 말하니, 그가 문장을 매듭짓기도 전에 볼럼니아가 끼어들려 했기 때문이다. \"아니면 능욕당한 법의 존엄을 되찾는 이들을 말이네.\""]], [["Volumnia with all humility explains that she had not merely the plea of curiosity to urge (in common with the giddy youth of her sex in general) but that she is perfectly dying with regret and interest for the darling man whose loss they all deplore.", "볼럼니아는 더없이 겸손하게 해명하니, 자기는 (대체로 경박한 또래 여성들과 마찬가지로) 단지 호기심을 핑계로 댄 것이 아니라, 모두가 그 상실을 애통해하는 그 사랑스러운 분을 향한 안타까움과 관심으로 그야말로 애가 타 죽을 지경이라는 것이다."]], [["“Very well, Volumnia,”", "\"좋아, 볼럼니아.\""], ["returns Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 대답한다."], ["“Then you cannot be too discreet.”", "\"그렇다면 아무리 신중해도 지나치지 않으니라.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket takes the opportunity of a pause to be heard again.", "버킷 씨는 말이 끊긴 틈을 타 다시 입을 연다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I have no objections to telling this lady, with your leave and among ourselves, that I look upon the case as pretty well complete.", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 나리께서 허락하신다면 우리끼리 이 아가씨께 말씀드리는 것이 거리낄 것 없겠습니다만, 저는 이 사건이 거의 다 마무리되었다고 봅니다."], ["It is a beautiful case—a beautiful case—and what little is wanting to complete it, I expect to be able to supply in a few hours.”", "멋진 사건입니다—멋진 사건이지요—그리고 마무리에 모자란 약간의 것들은, 몇 시간 안에 채워넣을 수 있으리라 기대합니다.\""]], [["“I am very glad indeed to hear it,”", "\"그 말을 들으니 참으로 기쁘네.\""], ["says Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 말한다."], ["“Highly creditable to you.”", "\"자네에게 무척 큰 공이 되겠군.\""]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” returns Mr.", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["Bucket very seriously, “I hope it may at one and the same time do me credit and prove satisfactory to all.", "버킷 씨가 자못 진지하게 대답한다. \"그것이 제게 공이 되는 동시에 모두에게도 흡족한 일로 드러나길 바랍니다."], ["When I depict it as a beautiful case, you see, miss,” Mr.", "제가 이걸 멋진 사건이라고 말씀드릴 때는, 아시겠지만, 아가씨,\""], ["Bucket goes on, glancing gravely at Sir Leicester, “I mean from my point of view.", "버킷 씨가 레스터 경을 진중하게 흘끔 보며 말을 잇는다. \"제 관점에서 그렇다는 겁니다."], ["As considered from other points of view, such cases will always involve more or less unpleasantness.", "다른 관점에서 보자면, 이런 사건들은 으레 어느 정도 언짢은 구석이 따르게 마련이지요."], ["Very strange things comes to our knowledge in families, miss; bless your heart, what you would think to be phenomenons, quite.”", "집안 일이란 게 참 별별 희한한 게 다 우리 귀에 들어옵니다, 아가씨. 원, 아가씨가 보시기엔 그야말로 괴이쩍은 일이라 여기실 만한 것들이 말입니다.\""]], [["Volumnia, with her innocent little scream, supposes so.", "볼럼니아가 천진하게 가벼운 비명을 지르며 그러리라 짐작한다."]], [["“Aye, and even in gen-teel families, in high families, in great families,”", "\"예, 게다가 점잖은 집안에서도, 고귀한 집안에서도, 대단한 집안에서도 그렇습니다.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, again gravely eyeing Sir Leicester aside.", "버킷 씨가 또 한 번 레스터 경을 곁눈으로 진중하게 살피며 말한다."], ["“I have had the honour of being employed in high families before, and you have no idea—come, I’ll go so far as to say not even YOU have any idea, sir,”", "\"저는 전에도 고귀한 집안에 고용되는 영광을 누려본 적이 있는데, 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다—아니, 당신마저도 짐작 못 하실 거라고까지 말씀드리겠습니다, 나리,\""], ["this to the debilitated cousin, “what games goes on!”", "이건 골골한 친척에게 하는 말이다. \"어떤 짓거리들이 벌어지는지 말입니다!\""]], [["The cousin, who has been casting sofa-pillows on his head, in a prostration of boredom yawns, “Vayli,” being the used-up for “very likely.”", "소파 쿠션을 제 머리 위로 끌어다 덮으며 지루함에 늘어져 있던 친척이 하품하며 \"그렇겠지\"라고 말하는데, 그건 \"아주 그럼직하다\"를 닳고 닳게 줄인 말이다."]], [["Sir Leicester, deeming it time to dismiss the officer, here majestically interposes with the words, “Very good.", "레스터 경이 이제 경관을 물러나게 할 때라 여기고, 여기서 위엄 있게 끼어들어 이렇게 말한다. \"잘했네."], ["Thank you!”", "고맙네!\""], ["and also with a wave of his hand, implying not only that there is an end of the discourse, but that if high families fall into low habits they must take the consequences.", "그리고 손을 한 번 내젓는데, 이는 이야기가 여기서 끝났다는 뜻일 뿐 아니라, 고귀한 집안이 천한 버릇에 빠지면 그 대가를 치러야 마땅하다는 뜻도 담겨 있다. \"잊지 말게, 경관.\""], ["“You will not forget, officer,”", "그가 짐짓 너그럽게 덧붙인다."], ["he adds with condescension, “that I am at your disposal when you please.”", "\"원할 때 언제든 나를 마음대로 쓸 수 있다는 것을.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket (still grave) inquires if to-morrow morning, now, would suit, in case he should be as for’ard as he expects to be.", "버킷 씨가 (여전히 진중하게) 묻기를, 자기가 예상하는 만큼 일이 진척되어 있다면, 내일 아침이 괜찮으신지 여쭙는다."], ["Sir Leicester replies, “All times are alike to me.”", "레스터 경이 대답한다. \"내게는 어느 때나 매한가지네.\""], ["Mr. Bucket makes his three bows and is withdrawing when a forgotten point occurs to him.", "버킷 씨가 세 번 절을 하고 물러나려는데 깜빡 잊은 점 하나가 떠오른다."]], [["“Might I ask, by the by,”", "\"그런데, 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까.\""], ["he says in a low voice, cautiously returning, “who posted the reward-bill on the staircase.”", "그가 낮은 목소리로 조심스레 되돌아오며 말한다. \"계단에 그 현상금 벽보를 누가 붙였습니까?\""]], [["“I ordered it to be put up there,”", "\"내가 거기에 붙이라 지시했네.\""], ["replies Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 대답한다."]], [["“Would it be considered a liberty, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, if I was to ask you why?”", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 외람된 일이 아니라면, 왜 그러셨는지 여쭤봐도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀 외람되지 않네."], ["I chose it as a conspicuous part of the house.", "나는 집 안에서 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리로 그곳을 골랐네."], ["I think it cannot be too prominently kept before the whole establishment.", "집안 전체에 그것을 아무리 도드라지게 내걸어도 지나치지 않다고 보네."], ["I wish my people to be impressed with the enormity of the crime, the determination to punish it, and the hopelessness of escape.", "나는 우리 식솔들이 이 범죄의 극악함과, 그것을 기어이 처벌하겠다는 결의와, 도망쳐봐야 가망 없다는 것을 마음 깊이 새기길 바라네."], ["At the same time, officer, if you in your better knowledge of the subject see any objection—”", "한편으로는, 경관, 자네가 이 일에 더 밝은 식견으로 무슨 거리낌이라도 본다면—\""]], [["Mr. Bucket sees none now; the bill having been put up, had better not be taken down.", "버킷 씨는 이제 아무 거리낌도 없다."], ["Repeating his three bows he withdraws, closing the door on Volumnia’s little scream, which is a preliminary to her remarking that that charmingly horrible person is a perfect Blue Chamber.", "벽보가 이미 붙은 마당이니 떼지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 것이다. 세 번 절을 거듭하고 그가 물러나며 문을 닫는데, 그 뒤로 볼럼니아의 가벼운 비명이 새어 나오니, 그 매력적으로 끔찍한 사람은 영락없는 푸른 방이라는 그녀의 평이 그에 뒤따른다."]], [["In his fondness for society and his adaptability to all grades, Mr.", "* 푸른 방: 동화 「푸른 수염」에서 살해당한 아내들의 시신이 감춰진 금단의 방."], ["Bucket is presently standing before the hall-fire—bright and warm on the early winter night—admiring Mercury.", "무시무시하면서도 묘하게 끌리는 비밀의 존재를 빗댄 말. 사교를 좋아하고 어떤 계층에도 능히 어울리는 버킷 씨는, 이내 현관 난롯불—초겨울 밤에 밝고 따스하다—앞에 서서 머큐리를 감탄하며 바라본다."]], [["“Why, you’re six foot two, I suppose?”", "\"이봐, 자네 키가 육 피트 이 인치쯤 되겠지?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Three,”", "\"삼 인치입니다.\""], ["says Mercury.", "머큐리가 말한다."]], [["“Are you so much?", "\"그렇게나 크다고?"], ["But then, you see, you’re broad in proportion and don’t look it.", "하지만 자네는 그만큼 떡 벌어져서 그렇게 안 보이는군."], ["You’re not one of the weak-legged ones, you ain’t.", "자네는 다리 약한 축이 아니야, 암 아니지."], ["Was you ever modelled now?” Mr.", "본뜬 적 있나, 모델 말이야?\""], ["Bucket asks, conveying the expression of an artist into the turn of his eye and head.", "버킷 씨가 묻는데, 눈빛과 고갯짓에 예술가 같은 분위기를 실어 보인다."]], [["Mercury never was modelled.", "머큐리는 모델이 되어본 적이 없다."]], [["“Then you ought to be, you know,” says Mr.", "\"그럼 그래야 해, 알겠나.\""], ["Bucket; “and a friend of mine that you’ll hear of one day as a Royal Academy sculptor would stand something handsome to make a drawing of your proportions for the marble.", "버킷 씨가 말한다. \"언젠가 왕립 미술원 조각가로 이름 날릴 내 친구가 하나 있는데, 자네 그 균형 잡힌 몸을 대리석에 새기겠다고 데생 한 장 하는 데 두둑이 사례할 걸세."], ["My Lady’s out, ain’t she?”", "마님은 외출하셨나, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“Out to dinner.”", "\"만찬에 나가셨습니다.\""]], [["“Goes out pretty well every day, don’t she?”", "\"거의 날마다 나가시지, 안 그래?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Not to be wondered at!”", "\"놀랄 일도 아니지!\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Such a fine woman as her, so handsome and so graceful and so elegant, is like a fresh lemon on a dinner-table, ornamental wherever she goes.", "\"그분처럼 고운 여인이, 그리도 아리땁고 그리도 우아하고 그리도 기품 있는 분이, 만찬 식탁 위의 싱싱한 레몬 같아서, 가는 곳마다 좌중을 빛내니 말이야."], ["Was your father in the same way of life as yourself?”", "자네 부친도 자네처럼 이 일을 하셨나?\""]], [["Answer in the negative.", "아니라는 대답."]], [["“Mine was,”", "\"우리 아버지는 그러셨지.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“My father was first a page, then a footman, then a butler, then a steward, then an inn-keeper.", "\"우리 아버지는 처음엔 시동, 다음엔 하인, 다음엔 집사, 다음엔 가령, 다음엔 여관 주인이셨지."], ["Lived universally respected, and died lamented.", "두루 존경받으며 사시다, 애도 속에 돌아가셨어."], ["Said with his last breath that he considered service the most honourable part of his career, and so it was.", "마지막 숨을 거두며 하시는 말씀이, 봉직이야말로 당신 인생에서 가장 명예로운 부분이라 여긴다 하셨는데, 정말 그러했네."], ["I’ve a brother in service, AND a brother-in-law.", "내 형제 하나도 봉직 일을 하고, 매부도 하나 있지."], ["My Lady a good temper?”", "마님은 성품이 좋으신가?\""]], [["Mercury replies, “As good as you can expect.”", "머큐리가 대답한다. \"기대하실 만큼은요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“A little spoilt?", "\"조금 응석받이시고?"], ["A little capricious?", "조금 변덕스럽고?"], ["Lord!", "원!"], ["What can you anticipate when they’re so handsome as that?", "그리도 아리따운 분에게 무얼 더 바라겠나?"], ["And we like ’em all the better for it, don’t we?”", "그래서 우리는 더 좋아하는 거 아니겠나, 안 그래?\""]], [["Mercury, with his hands in the pockets of his bright peach-blossom small-clothes, stretches his symmetrical silk legs with the air of a man of gallantry and can’t deny it.", "머큐리는 환한 복숭아꽃 빛 반바지 호주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채, 균형 잡힌 비단 다리를 한량 같은 분위기로 쭉 뻗으며, 그 말을 부정하지 못한다."], ["Come the roll of wheels and a violent ringing at the bell.", "바퀴 구르는 소리가 들려오고 초인종이 거세게 울린다."], ["“Talk of the angels,”", "\"천사 이야기를 하니,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Here she is!”", "\"이렇게 나타나는군!\""]], [["The doors are thrown open, and she passes through the hall.", "문이 활짝 열리고, 그녀가 현관을 가로질러 지나간다."], ["Still very pale, she is dressed in slight mourning and wears two beautiful bracelets.", "여전히 무척 창백한 그녀는 가벼운 상복 차림에 아름다운 팔찌 두 개를 끼고 있다."], ["Either their beauty or the beauty of her arms is particularly attractive to Mr. Bucket.", "그 팔찌의 아름다움인지 그녀 팔의 아름다움인지, 어느 쪽이 버킷 씨의 마음을 유난히 끄는 듯하다."], ["He looks at them with an eager eye and rattles something in his pocket—halfpence perhaps.", "그는 간절한 눈빛으로 그것을 바라보며 호주머니 속에서 무언가를 짤랑인다—아마도 반 페니 동전이리라."]], [["Noticing him at his distance, she turns an inquiring look on the other Mercury who has brought her home.", "멀찍이 있는 그를 알아채고, 그녀는 자기를 데려다준 또 다른 머큐리에게 묻는 듯한 눈길을 돌린다."]], [["“Mr. Bucket, my Lady.”", "\"버킷 씨입니다, 마님.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket makes a leg and comes forward, passing his familiar demon over the region of his mouth.", "버킷 씨가 한쪽 다리를 빼며 절을 하고 앞으로 나서며, 그 친근한 정령을 입 언저리에 슬쩍 문지른다."]], [["“Are you waiting to see Sir Leicester?”", "\"레스터 경을 뵈려고 기다리는 건가요?\""]], [["“No, my Lady, I’ve seen him!”", "\"아닙니다, 마님. 이미 뵈었습니다!\""]], [["“Have you anything to say to me?”", "\"내게 할 말이라도 있나요?\""]], [["“Not just at present, my Lady.”", "\"지금 당장은 아닙니다, 마님.\""]], [["“Have you made any new discoveries?”", "\"무슨 새로운 사실이라도 알아냈나요?\""]], [["“A few, my Lady.”", "\"몇 가지요, 마님.\""]], [["This is merely in passing.", "이건 그저 지나가며 던지는 말일 뿐이다."], ["She scarcely makes a stop, and sweeps upstairs alone. Mr.", "그녀는 거의 멈추지 않고, 홀로 계단을 휩쓸 듯 올라간다."], ["Bucket, moving towards the staircase-foot, watches her as she goes up the steps the old man came down to his grave, past murderous groups of statuary repeated with their shadowy weapons on the wall, past the printed bill, which she looks at going by, out of view.", "버킷 씨는 계단 발치로 다가가, 그 노인이 무덤으로 가려고 내려왔던 그 계단을 그녀가 올라가는 모습을 지켜본다. 벽 위로 어슴푸레한 무기를 든 채 거듭 새겨진 살벌한 조각상 무리를 지나, 그녀가 지나가며 흘끗 보는 그 인쇄된 벽보를 지나, 시야에서 사라질 때까지."]], [["“She’s a lovely woman, too, she really is,”", "\"저분도 사랑스러운 여인이야, 정말이지.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, coming back to Mercury.", "버킷 씨가 머큐리에게 돌아오며 말한다."], ["“Don’t look quite healthy though.”", "\"그래도 그리 건강해 보이진 않는군.\""]], [["Is not quite healthy, Mercury informs him.", "그리 건강하지 못하시다고, 머큐리가 일러준다."], ["Suffers much from headaches.", "두통으로 자주 고생하신다고."]], [["Really?", "정말로?"], ["That’s a pity!", "그것참 안됐군!"], ["Walking, Mr. Bucket would recommend for that.", "그런 데는 산책이 좋다고 버킷 씨가 권하겠다."], ["Well, she tries walking, Mercury rejoins.", "그런데 마님도 산책을 해보신다고 머큐리가 받는다."], ["Walks sometimes for two hours when she has them bad.", "두통이 심하면 더러 두 시간씩 걸으신다고."], ["By night, too.", "게다가 밤중에도."]], [["“Are you sure you’re quite so much as six foot three?”", "\"자네 정말 키가 육 피트 삼 인치나 되는 거 확실한가?\""], ["asks Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 묻는다."], ["“Begging your pardon for interrupting you a moment?”", "\"잠깐 말을 끊어 미안하네만?\""]], [["Not a doubt about it.", "의심할 여지 없다."]], [["“You’re so well put together that I shouldn’t have thought it.", "\"자네는 워낙 균형이 잘 잡혀서 그렇게는 안 보였네."], ["But the household troops, though considered fine men, are built so straggling.", "하지만 근위병들은 다들 미남으로 치는데도 어찌나 휘청대게들 생겼는지."], ["Walks by night, does she?", "밤중에 걸으신다고, 그분이?"], ["When it’s moonlight, though?”", "그래도 달밤에 말이지?\""]], [["Oh, yes.", "오, 그럼요."], ["When it’s moonlight!", "달밤에! 물론이지요."], ["Of course. Oh, of course!", "오, 물론입니다!"], ["Conversational and acquiescent on both sides.", "양쪽 다 이야기를 주고받으며 맞장구친다."]], [["“I suppose you ain’t in the habit of walking yourself?”", "\"자네야 직접 걸어 다니는 버릇은 없겠지?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Not much time for it, I should say?”", "\"그럴 틈도 별로 없을 테고, 안 그래?\""]], [["Besides which, Mercury don’t like it.", "게다가 머큐리는 걷는 걸 좋아하지 않는다."], ["Prefers carriage exercise.", "마차 타는 운동을 더 좋아한다."]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"하긴 그렇지.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“That makes a difference.", "\"그게 다르긴 하지."], ["Now I think of it,” says Mr.", "가만 생각해보니,\""], ["Bucket, warming his hands and looking pleasantly at the blaze, “she went out walking the very night of this business.”", "버킷 씨가 두 손을 녹이며 활활 타는 불길을 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말한다. \"마님이 그 사건이 있던 바로 그날 밤에도 산책을 나가셨더군.\""]], [["“To be sure she did!", "\"그럼요, 나가셨고말고요!"], ["I let her into the garden over the way.”", "제가 길 건너 정원으로 들여보내드렸지요.\""]], [["“And left her there.", "\"그러고 거기 두고 왔지."], ["Certainly you did.", "그랬고말고."], ["I saw you doing it.”", "자네가 그러는 걸 내가 봤거든.\""]], [["“I didn’t see YOU,”", "\"저는 당신을 못 봤는데요.\""], ["says Mercury.", "머큐리가 말한다."]], [["“I was rather in a hurry,” returns Mr.", "\"내가 좀 바빴거든.\""], ["Bucket, “for I was going to visit a aunt of mine that lives at Chelsea—next door but two to the old original Bun House—ninety year old the old lady is, a single woman, and got a little property.", "버킷 씨가 대답한다. \"첼시에 사는 우리 이모님을 뵈러 가는 길이었는데—그 오래된 원조 빵집에서 두 집 건너에 사시지—아흔 살 된 노인이고, 독신이고, 자그마한 재산도 좀 있으시지."], ["Yes, I chanced to be passing at the time.", "그래, 마침 그때 지나가던 길이었거든."], ["Let’s see.", "가만있자."], ["What time might it be?", "몇 시쯤이었더라?"], ["It wasn’t ten.”", "열 시는 아니었지.\""]], [["“Half-past nine.”", "\"아홉 시 반이었습니다.\""]], [["“You’re right.", "\"자네 말이 맞아."], ["So it was.", "그랬지."], ["And if I don’t deceive myself, my Lady was muffled in a loose black mantle, with a deep fringe to it?”", "그리고 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 마님이 술 장식이 깊게 달린 헐렁한 검은 망토를 두르고 계셨지?\""]], [["“Of course she was.”", "\"그럼요, 그러셨습니다.\""]], [["Of course she was. Mr.", "그럼, 그러셨고말고."], ["Bucket must return to a little work he has to get on with upstairs, but he must shake hands with Mercury in acknowledgment of his agreeable conversation, and will he—this is all he asks—will he, when he has a leisure half-hour, think of bestowing it on that Royal Academy sculptor, for the advantage of both parties?", "버킷 씨는 위층에서 마저 처리할 자그마한 일이 있어 돌아가야 하지만, 그래도 머큐리와 악수를 나누며 그 유쾌한 대화에 고마움을 표해야겠다 하고는, 부탁하건대—이것 하나만 부탁한다며—한가한 반 시간이 나거든, 양쪽 다 득이 될 테니, 그 왕립 미술원 조각가에게 그 시간을 한번 내어줄 생각을 해보지 않겠느냐 한다."]], [["Springing a Mine\nRefreshed by sleep, Mr. Bucket rises betimes in the morning and prepares for a field-day.", "잠으로 기운을 차린 버킷 씨는 아침 일찍 일어나 결전의 하루를 준비한다."], ["Smartened up by the aid of a clean shirt and a wet hairbrush, with which instrument, on occasions of ceremony, he lubricates such thin locks as remain to him after his life of severe study, Mr.", "깨끗한 셔츠와 물 묻힌 머리빗의 도움으로 말끔하게 매무새를 가다듬는데, 의례를 차려야 할 때면 이 도구로 고된 연구의 세월을 보낸 뒤 머리에 남은 몇 가닥 안 되는 머리칼에 윤기를 입힌다."], ["Bucket lays in a breakfast of two mutton chops as a foundation to work upon, together with tea, eggs, toast, and marmalade on a corresponding scale.", "버킷 씨는 일을 시작할 토대로 양고기 갈비 두 점을 차린 아침을 먹는데, 거기에 차와 달걀과 토스트와 마멀레이드도 그에 걸맞은 규모로 곁들인다."], ["Having much enjoyed these strengthening matters and having held subtle conference with his familiar demon, he confidently instructs Mercury “just to mention quietly to Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, that whenever he’s ready for me, I’m ready for him.”", "이 든든한 음식을 흠뻑 즐기고, 제 마음속 친숙한 악마와 은밀한 회의를 거친 뒤, 그는 자신만만하게 시종에게 지시한다. \"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께 그저 조용히 전해주게."], ["A gracious message being returned that Sir Leicester will expedite his dressing and join Mr.", "나리께서 준비되시면 저도 준비됐다고 말이야.\""], ["Bucket in the library within ten minutes, Mr. Bucket repairs to that apartment and stands before the fire with his finger on his chin, looking at the blazing coals.", "레스터 경이 옷차림을 서둘러 마치고 십 분 안에 서재에서 버킷 씨를 만나겠다는 정중한 전갈이 돌아오자, 버킷 씨는 그 방으로 가서 손가락을 턱에 댄 채 불 앞에 서서 활활 타오르는 석탄을 바라본다."]], [["Thoughtful Mr. Bucket is, as a man may be with weighty work to do, but composed, sure, confident.", "버킷 씨는 무거운 일을 앞둔 사람이 으레 그러하듯 생각에 잠겨 있지만, 침착하고, 확신에 차 있고, 자신만만하다."], ["From the expression of his face he might be a famous whist-player for a large stake—say a hundred guineas certain—with the game in his hand, but with a high reputation involved in his playing his hand out to the last card in a masterly way.", "그 얼굴 표정으로 보자면, 큰돈 — 이를테면 백 기니쯤은 확실히 — 을 걸고 카드 패를 손에 쥔 채 솜씨 좋게 마지막 한 장까지 풀어내는 데 높은 명성이 달려 있는, 이름난 휘스트 노름꾼처럼 보일지도 모른다."], ["Not in the least anxious or disturbed is Mr. Bucket when Sir Leicester appears, but he eyes the baronet aside as he comes slowly to his easy-chair with that observant gravity of yesterday in which there might have been yesterday, but for the audacity of the idea, a touch of compassion.", "레스터 경이 모습을 드러내도 버킷 씨는 조금도 불안하거나 동요하지 않지만, 준남작이 천천히 안락의자로 다가오는 동안 어제 보였던 그 관찰자다운 진지함으로 그를 곁눈질하는데, 그 속에는 — 그런 생각이 무례하지만 않다면 — 어제와 마찬가지로 한 줄기 연민이 깃들어 있었을지도 모른다."]], [["“I am sorry to have kept you waiting, officer, but I am rather later than my usual hour this morning.", "\"기다리시게 해서 죄송합니다, 형사 양반. 오늘 아침엔 평소 시각보다 좀 늦었군요."], ["I am not well.", "몸이 좋질 않아요."], ["The agitation and the indignation from which I have recently suffered have been too much for me.", "요즘 겪은 동요와 분노가 내겐 너무 버거웠소. 나는 — 통풍이 있어서요\""], ["I am subject to—gout”—Sir Leicester was going to say indisposition and would have said it to anybody else, but Mr. Bucket palpably knows all about it—“and recent circumstances have brought it on.”", "— 레스터 경은 몸이 편치 않다고 말하려던 참이었고 다른 누구에게라면 그렇게 말했겠으나, 버킷 씨는 그 일을 빤히 다 알고 있다 — \"최근 사정 때문에 도졌소이다.\""]], [["As he takes his seat with some difficulty and with an air of pain, Mr. Bucket draws a little nearer, standing with one of his large hands on the library-table.", "레스터 경이 다소 힘겹게, 고통스러운 기색으로 자리에 앉자, 버킷 씨는 큼직한 손 하나를 서재 탁자에 올린 채 조금 더 가까이 다가선다."]], [["“I am not aware, officer,”", "\"잘 모르겠소만, 형사 양반,\""], ["Sir Leicester observes; raising his eyes to his face, “whether you wish us to be alone, but that is entirely as you please.", "레스터 경이 그의 얼굴로 눈을 들며 말한다. \"우리 둘만 있기를 바라는지 어떤지 말이오."], ["If you do, well and good.", "허나 그건 전적으로 그쪽 뜻에 맡기겠소."], ["If not, Miss Dedlock would be interested—”", "그러기를 원한다면 좋소. 아니라면, 데드록 양도 흥미를 가질 텐데—\""]], [["“Why, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” returns Mr.", "\"그게 말입니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\""], ["Bucket with his head persuasively on one side and his forefinger pendant at one ear like an earring, “we can’t be too private just at present.", "버킷 씨가 머리를 설득하듯 한쪽으로 기울이고 귀고리처럼 집게손가락을 한쪽 귀에 늘어뜨린 채 대답한다. \"지금 이 순간만큼은 아무리 은밀해도 지나치지 않습니다."], ["You will presently see that we can’t be too private.", "곧 아시게 되겠지만 아무리 은밀해도 지나치지 않아요."], ["A lady, under the circumstances, and especially in Miss Dedlock’s elevated station of society, can’t but be agreeable to me, but speaking without a view to myself, I will take the liberty of assuring you that I know we can’t be too private.”", "이런 상황에서, 더구나 데드록 양처럼 사교계에서 높은 신분에 계신 숙녀분이라면 저로서야 함께해 기쁘기 그지없겠습니다만, 제 사사로운 마음을 떠나서 감히 단언컨대, 우리는 아무리 은밀해도 지나치지 않다는 것을 저는 압니다.\""]], [["“That is enough.”", "\"그만하면 됐소.\""]], [["“So much so, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” Mr.", "\"하도 그러해서 말입니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["Bucket resumes, “that I was on the point of asking your permission to turn the key in the door.”", "버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"마침 문에 자물쇠를 채워도 되겠냐고 여쭈려던 참이었습니다.\""]], [["“By all means.”", "\"얼마든지.\""], ["Mr. Bucket skilfully and softly takes that precaution, stooping on his knee for a moment from mere force of habit so to adjust the key in the lock as that no one shall peep in from the outerside.", "버킷 씨는 능숙하고 부드럽게 그 조처를 취하는데, 순전히 습관의 힘으로 잠시 무릎을 굽혀 바깥쪽에서 아무도 들여다보지 못하도록 자물쇠 안에서 열쇠를 맞춘다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I mentioned yesterday evening that I wanted but a very little to complete this case.", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 어제저녁에 말씀드렸지요. 이 사건을 마무리 짓는 데 아주 조금만 더 필요하다고 말입니다."], ["I have now completed it and collected proof against the person who did this crime.”", "이제 그것을 마무리 지었고, 이 범행을 저지른 자에 대한 증거를 모았습니다.\""]], [["“Against the soldier?”", "\"그 군인 말이오?\""]], [["“No, Sir Leicester Dedlock; not the soldier.”", "\"아닙니다, 레스터 데드록 나리. 군인이 아닙니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester looks astounded and inquires, “Is the man in custody?”", "레스터 경은 깜짝 놀란 표정으로 묻는다. \"그자가 구금되었소?\""]], [["Mr.", "버킷 씨는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 그에게 말한다."], ["Bucket tells him, after a pause, “It was a woman.”", "\"여자였습니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester leans back in his chair, and breathlessly ejaculates, “Good heaven!”", "레스터 경은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 숨을 멈추고 외친다. \"맙소사!\""]], [["“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” Mr.", "\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["Bucket begins, standing over him with one hand spread out on the library-table and the forefinger of the other in impressive use, “it’s my duty to prepare you for a train of circumstances that may, and I go so far as to say that will, give you a shock.", "버킷 씨가 한 손을 서재 탁자에 활짝 펴 짚고 다른 손 집게손가락을 인상 깊게 놀리며 그를 굽어보고 선 채 입을 연다. \"충격을 줄지도 모르는, 아니 감히 말씀드리건대 분명히 충격을 줄 일련의 사정에 대비하시도록 마음의 준비를 시켜드리는 게 제 의무입니다."], ["But Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, you are a gentleman, and I know what a gentleman is and what a gentleman is capable of.", "허나 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 나리는 신사이시고, 저는 신사가 어떤 분인지, 신사가 무엇을 감당할 수 있는지 압니다."], ["A gentleman can bear a shock when it must come, boldly and steadily.", "신사는 충격이 닥칠 수밖에 없을 때 그것을 대담하고 꿋꿋하게 견딥니다."], ["A gentleman can make up his mind to stand up against almost any blow.", "신사는 거의 어떤 타격에도 맞설 각오를 다질 수 있지요."], ["Why, take yourself, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.", "가령 나리 자신을 봅시다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리."], ["If there’s a blow to be inflicted on you, you naturally think of your family.", "나리께 타격이 가해진다면, 나리는 자연히 가문을 떠올리실 겁니다."], ["You ask yourself, how would all them ancestors of yours, away to Julius Caesar—not to go beyond him at present—have borne that blow; you remember scores of them that would have borne it well; and you bear it well on their accounts, and to maintain the family credit.", "율리우스 카이사르까지 거슬러 올라가는 — 지금은 그 위로는 가지 않겠습니다만 — 그 모든 조상께서 그 타격을 어떻게 견뎌내셨을까 스스로 물으시겠지요. 그것을 잘 견뎌냈을 분이 수십 명은 떠오르실 테고, 나리는 그분들을 위해, 또 가문의 명예를 지키기 위해 그것을 잘 견뎌내시는 겁니다."], ["That’s the way you argue, and that’s the way you act, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”", "그것이 나리께서 판단하시는 방식이고, 행동하시는 방식이지요, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리.\""]], [["Sir Leicester, leaning back in his chair and grasping the elbows, sits looking at him with a stony face.", "레스터 경은 의자에 등을 기댄 채 팔걸이를 움켜쥐고, 돌처럼 굳은 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 앉아 있다."]], [["“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock,” proceeds Mr.", "\"자, 레스터 데드록 나리,\""], ["Bucket, “thus preparing you, let me beg of you not to trouble your mind for a moment as to anything having come to MY knowledge.", "버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"이렇게 마음의 준비를 시켜드렸으니, 무엇이 제 귀에 들어왔든 잠시도 마음 쓰지 마시기를 간청드립니다."], ["I know so much about so many characters, high and low, that a piece of information more or less don’t signify a straw.", "저는 높고 낮은 숱한 인물에 대해 워낙 많은 걸 알고 있어서, 정보 한 조각쯤 많거나 적은 건 지푸라기만큼도 대수롭지 않습니다."], ["I don’t suppose there’s a move on the board that would surprise ME, and as to this or that move having taken place, why my knowing it is no odds at all, any possible move whatever (provided it’s in a wrong direction) being a probable move according to my experience.", "판 위에서 어떤 수가 나온들 저를 놀라게 할 건 없을 듯하고, 이런저런 수가 두어졌다 한들 제가 그걸 안다는 게 조금도 이상할 게 없으니, (그릇된 방향이기만 하다면) 그 어떤 수든 제 경험으로 보면 충분히 둘 법한 수이기 때문입니다."], ["Therefore, what I say to you, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, is, don’t you go and let yourself be put out of the way because of my knowing anything of your family affairs.”", "그러니 제가 나리께 드리는 말씀은, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 제가 나리 가문의 일을 좀 안다고 해서 마음을 흩뜨리지 마시라는 겁니다.\""]], [["“I thank you for your preparation,”", "\"대비시켜준 점은 고맙소,\""], ["returns Sir Leicester after a silence, without moving hand, foot, or feature, “which I hope is not necessary; though I give it credit for being well intended.", "손도 발도 표정도 까딱하지 않은 채 잠시 침묵하던 레스터 경이 대답한다. \"필요 없는 일이기를 바라지만 말이오."], ["Be so good as to go on.", "다만 선의에서 나온 것임은 인정하겠소. 부디 계속하시오."], ["Also”—Sir Leicester seems to shrink in the shadow of his figure—“also, to take a seat, if you have no objection.”", "또한—\" 레스터 경은 제 그림자 속에서 움츠러드는 듯이 보인다 \"—또한, 괜찮다면 자리에 앉으시오.\""]], [["None at all. Mr.", "조금도 마다할 일이 없다."], ["Bucket brings a chair and diminishes his shadow.", "버킷 씨는 의자를 가져와 제 그림자를 줄인다."], ["“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, with this short preface I come to the point.", "\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이 짤막한 서론을 끝내고 본론으로 들어가겠습니다."], ["Lady Dedlock—”", "데드록 마님께서—\""]], [["Sir Leicester raises himself in his seat and stares at him fiercely.", "레스터 경은 자리에서 몸을 일으키며 그를 매섭게 노려본다."], ["Mr. Bucket brings the finger into play as an emollient.", "버킷 씨는 누그러뜨리는 약처럼 손가락을 놀린다."]], [["“Lady Dedlock, you see she’s universally admired.", "\"데드록 마님은, 보십시오, 만인의 흠모를 받으시지요."], ["That’s what her ladyship is; she’s universally admired,”", "마님이 바로 그런 분입니다. 만인의 흠모를 받는 분이에요,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I would greatly prefer, officer,”", "\"형사 양반, 나는 차라리,\""], ["Sir Leicester returns stiffly, “my Lady’s name being entirely omitted from this discussion.”", "레스터 경이 뻣뻣하게 대답한다. \"이 논의에서 마님의 이름은 통째로 빼주었으면 하오.\""]], [["“So would I, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, but—it’s impossible.”", "\"저도 그러고 싶습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 허나 — 그건 불가능합니다.\""]], [["“Impossible?”", "\"불가능하다고?\""]], [["Mr. Bucket shakes his relentless head.", "버킷 씨는 가차 없이 고개를 젓는다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, it’s altogether impossible.", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 그건 도무지 불가능한 일입니다."], ["What I have got to say is about her ladyship.", "제가 말씀드려야 할 것이 바로 마님에 관한 것이니까요."], ["She is the pivot it all turns on.”", "마님이 이 모든 일이 돌아가는 축이십니다.\""]], [["“Officer,”", "\"형사 양반,\""], ["retorts Sir Leicester with a fiery eye and a quivering lip, “you know your duty.", "레스터 경이 불타는 눈과 떨리는 입술로 쏘아붙인다. \"그대는 제 의무를 알고 있소."], ["Do your duty, but be careful not to overstep it.", "의무를 다하시오. 허나 그 선을 넘지 않도록 조심하시오."], ["I would not suffer it.", "나는 용납하지 않을 것이오."], ["I would not endure it.", "나는 견디지 않을 것이오."], ["You bring my Lady’s name into this communication upon your responsibility—upon your responsibility.", "마님의 이름을 이 이야기에 끌어들이는 건 그대의 책임이오 — 그대의 책임이란 말이오."], ["My Lady’s name is not a name for common persons to trifle with!”", "마님의 이름은 천한 자들이 함부로 입에 올릴 이름이 아니란 말이오!\""]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I say what I must say, and no more.”", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 저는 말씀드려야 할 것만 말씀드리지, 그 이상은 하지 않습니다.\""]], [["“I hope it may prove so.", "\"그러기를 바라오. 좋소."], ["Very well.", "계속하시오."], ["Go on. Go on, sir!”", "계속하란 말이오!\""], ["Glancing at the angry eyes which now avoid him and at the angry figure trembling from head to foot, yet striving to be still, Mr. Bucket feels his way with his forefinger and in a low voice proceeds.", "이제 그를 피하는 성난 눈과, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서도 가만히 있으려 애쓰는 성난 몸뚱이를 흘끔 보며, 버킷 씨는 집게손가락으로 더듬듯 나직한 목소리로 말을 잇는다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, it becomes my duty to tell you that the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn long entertained mistrusts and suspicions of Lady Dedlock.”", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨께서 오래전부터 데드록 마님에게 불신과 의심을 품어왔다는 것을 말씀드리는 게 제 의무가 되었습니다.\""]], [["“If he had dared to breathe them to me, sir—which he never did—I would have killed him myself!”", "\"감히 그자가 내게 그런 걸 발설했더라면 — 결코 그러진 않았소만 — 내 손으로 그자를 죽였을 것이오!\""], ["exclaims Sir Leicester, striking his hand upon the table.", "레스터 경이 탁자를 손으로 내리치며 외친다."], ["But in the very heat and fury of the act he stops, fixed by the knowing eyes of Mr. Bucket, whose forefinger is slowly going and who, with mingled confidence and patience, shakes his head.", "그러나 그 행위의 열기와 분노가 한창일 때, 그는 천천히 움직이는 집게손가락과 함께 확신과 인내가 뒤섞인 채 고개를 젓는 버킷 씨의 다 안다는 듯한 눈에 붙들려 멈춘다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn was deep and close, and what he fully had in his mind in the very beginning I can’t quite take upon myself to say.", "\"레스터 데드록 나리, 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨는 속이 깊고 입이 무거운 분이라, 그분이 맨 처음부터 마음속에 무엇을 온전히 품고 계셨는지는 제가 감히 단정할 수가 없습니다."], ["But I know from his lips that he long ago suspected Lady Dedlock of having discovered, through the sight of some handwriting—in this very house, and when you yourself, Sir Leicester Dedlock, were present—the existence, in great poverty, of a certain person who had been her lover before you courted her and who ought to have been her husband.” Mr.", "허나 그분 입으로 직접 들은 바, 그분은 오래전부터 데드록 마님이 어떤 필체를 보고 — 바로 이 집에서, 그것도 레스터 데드록 나리께서 친히 그 자리에 계실 때 — 나리께서 마님께 구애하시기 전에 그분의 연인이었고 마땅히 그분의 남편이 되었어야 할 어떤 사람이 지독한 가난 속에 살아 있다는 사실을 알아차렸으리라 의심하셨습니다.\""], ["Bucket stops and deliberately repeats, “Ought to have been her husband, not a doubt about it.", "버킷 씨는 잠시 멈췄다가 신중하게 되뇐다. \"마땅히 그분의 남편이 되었어야 할 사람, 그건 의심할 여지가 없습니다."], ["I know from his lips that when that person soon afterwards died, he suspected Lady Dedlock of visiting his wretched lodging and his wretched grave, alone and in secret.", "그분 입으로 들은 바, 그 사람이 얼마 안 가 죽자, 그분은 데드록 마님이 그자의 누추한 하숙집과 누추한 무덤을 홀로 몰래 찾아갔으리라 의심하셨습니다."], ["I know from my own inquiries and through my eyes and ears that Lady Dedlock did make such visit in the dress of her own maid, for the deceased Mr.", "또 제 나름의 조사와 제 눈과 귀로 알아낸 바, 데드록 마님은 정말로 자신의 하녀 옷을 입고 그런 방문을 하셨습니다."], ["Tulkinghorn employed me to reckon up her ladyship—if you’ll excuse my making use of the term we commonly employ—and I reckoned her up, so far, completely.", "고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 마님을 — 저희가 흔히 쓰는 표현을 빌리는 걸 양해해주신다면 — '꿰뚫어보라'고 저를 고용하셨고, 저는 거기까지는 마님을 완전히 꿰뚫어보았습니다."], ["I confronted the maid in the chambers in Lincoln’s Inn Fields with a witness who had been Lady Dedlock’s guide, and there couldn’t be the shadow of a doubt that she had worn the young woman’s dress, unknown to her.", "저는 링컨스 인 광장의 그 방에서, 데드록 마님의 길잡이였던 증인과 함께 그 하녀를 대면시켰는데, 마님이 그 젊은 처자의 옷을 그 처자도 모르게 입었다는 데는 한 점의 의심도 있을 수 없었습니다."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I did endeavour to pave the way a little towards these unpleasant disclosures yesterday by saying that very strange things happened even in high families sometimes.", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 저는 어제 이런 불쾌한 사실들을 밝히는 데 조금이나마 길을 닦아보려고, 때로는 명문가에서도 아주 기이한 일이 벌어진다고 말씀드렸던 겁니다."], ["All this, and more, has happened in your own family, and to and through your own Lady.", "이 모든 일이, 그리고 그 이상이, 나리의 가문에서, 나리의 마님께, 마님을 통해 벌어졌습니다."], ["It’s my belief that the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn followed up these inquiries to the hour of his death and that he and Lady Dedlock even had bad blood between them upon the matter that very night.", "제 믿음으로는 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 돌아가시는 그 순간까지 이 조사를 밀어붙이셨고, 바로 그날 밤 그분과 데드록 마님 사이에 그 일을 두고 험악한 기류까지 흘렀습니다."], ["Now, only you put that to Lady Dedlock, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and ask her ladyship whether, even after he had left here, she didn’t go down to his chambers with the intention of saying something further to him, dressed in a loose black mantle with a deep fringe to it.”", "자, 나리께서 데드록 마님께 그 점을 여쭤보십시오, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 그분이 이곳을 떠나신 뒤에도, 마님께서 깊은 술이 달린 헐렁한 검은 망토를 두르고 그분께 무언가 더 할 말이 있어 그분 방으로 내려가지 않으셨는지를 마님께 여쭤보십시오.\""]], [["Sir Leicester sits like a statue, gazing at the cruel finger that is probing the life-blood of his heart.", "레스터 경은 조각상처럼 앉아, 제 심장의 생피를 후벼 파는 그 잔인한 손가락을 응시한다."]], [["“You put that to her ladyship, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, from me, Inspector Bucket of the Detective.", "\"마님께 그 점을 여쭤보십시오, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 저, 형사반의 버킷 경위가 드리는 말씀이라고 말입니다."], ["And if her ladyship makes any difficulty about admitting of it, you tell her that it’s no use, that Inspector Bucket knows it and knows that she passed the soldier as you called him (though he’s not in the army now) and knows that she knows she passed him on the staircase.", "그리고 마님께서 그 일을 인정하기를 곤란해하시거든, 소용없다고 전하십시오. 버킷 경위가 그 일을 알고 있으며, 마님께서 나리가 그 군인이라 부르신 자(지금은 군에 있지 않습니다만)와 스쳐 지나가셨다는 것도, 마님 자신이 계단에서 그자와 스쳐 지나갔음을 알고 계신다는 것도 안다고 말입니다."], ["Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, why do I relate all this?”", "자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 제가 왜 이 모든 걸 늘어놓겠습니까?\""]], [["Sir Leicester, who has covered his face with his hands, uttering a single groan, requests him to pause for a moment.", "레스터 경은 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 신음을 한 번 토하더니, 잠시 멈춰달라고 청한다."], ["By and by he takes his hands away, and so preserves his dignity and outward calmness, though there is no more colour in his face than in his white hair, that Mr. Bucket is a little awed by him.", "이윽고 그는 두 손을 내리는데, 얼굴에 핏기라곤 그의 백발만큼도 남아 있지 않으면서도 위엄과 겉으로의 평정을 어찌나 잘 지키는지, 버킷 씨가 그에게 조금은 경외감을 느낄 정도다."], ["Something frozen and fixed is upon his manner, over and above its usual shell of haughtiness, and Mr. Bucket soon detects an unusual slowness in his speech, with now and then a curious trouble in beginning, which occasions him to utter inarticulate sounds.", "그의 태도에는 평소의 오만이라는 껍질 위에 무언가 얼어붙고 굳어버린 기운이 어려 있고, 버킷 씨는 곧 그의 말투에 평소답지 않은 더딤이 있음을, 이따금 말을 시작하는 데 묘하게 애를 먹어 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 내는 것을 알아챈다."], ["With such sounds he now breaks silence, soon, however, controlling himself to say that he does not comprehend why a gentleman so faithful and zealous as the late Mr.", "그는 지금 그런 소리로 침묵을 깨더니, 이내 마음을 다잡고 이렇게 말한다."], ["Tulkinghorn should have communicated to him nothing of this painful, this distressing, this unlooked-for, this overwhelming, this incredible intelligence.", "고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨처럼 그토록 충직하고 열성적인 신사가 어찌하여 이토록 고통스럽고, 이토록 비통하고, 이토록 뜻밖이고, 이토록 압도적이고, 이토록 믿기 힘든 소식을 자신에게 한마디도 전하지 않았는지 도무지 이해가 가지 않는다고."]], [["“Again, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,”", "\"그것도 말입니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["returns Mr.", "버킷 씨가 대답한다."], ["Bucket, “put it to her ladyship to clear that up.", "\"마님께 그 점을 분명히 밝혀달라고 여쭤보십시오."], ["Put it to her ladyship, if you think it right, from Inspector Bucket of the Detective.", "옳다고 여기신다면, 형사반의 버킷 경위가 드리는 말이라며 마님께 여쭤보십시오."], ["You’ll find, or I’m much mistaken, that the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn had the intention of communicating the whole to you as soon as he considered it ripe, and further, that he had given her ladyship so to understand.", "제가 크게 잘못 짚은 게 아니라면, 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 때가 무르익었다 여기는 즉시 모든 걸 나리께 알릴 작정이셨고, 더 나아가 마님께도 그렇게 알아듣도록 일러두셨다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다."], ["Why, he might have been going to reveal it the very morning when I examined the body!", "아니, 어쩌면 제가 시신을 살피던 바로 그 아침에 그걸 밝히려던 참이었을지도 모릅니다!"], ["You don’t know what I’m going to say and do five minutes from this present time, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet; and supposing I was to be picked off now, you might wonder why I hadn’t done it, don’t you see?”", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 지금 이 순간으로부터 오 분 뒤에 제가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 할지 나리는 모르십니다. 만약 제가 지금 당장 쓰러진다면, 나리는 제가 왜 진작 그 일을 해두지 않았을까 의아해하시겠지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["True.", "옳은 말이다."], ["Sir Leicester, avoiding, with some trouble those obtrusive sounds, says, “True.”", "레스터 경은 그 거슬리는 소리를 다소 힘겹게 억누르며 \"옳소\""], ["At this juncture a considerable noise of voices is heard in the hall.", "하고 말한다. 이때 현관에서 상당한 말소리가 들려온다."], ["Mr. Bucket, after listening, goes to the library-door, softly unlocks and opens it, and listens again.", "버킷 씨는 귀를 기울이더니 서재 문으로 가서, 살며시 자물쇠를 풀어 문을 열고 다시 귀를 기울인다."], ["Then he draws in his head and whispers hurriedly but composedly, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, this unfortunate family affair has taken air, as I expected it might, the deceased Mr.", "그러고는 머리를 안으로 들이고 황급하지만 침착하게 속삭인다. \"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이 불행한 집안일이 새어 나가고 말았습니다."], ["Tulkinghorn being cut down so sudden.", "고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨가 그토록 급작스레 쓰러지셨으니 그러리라 짐작은 했지요."], ["The chance to hush it is to let in these people now in a wrangle with your footmen.", "이걸 덮을 기회는, 지금 나리의 하인들과 옥신각신하는 저 사람들을 들이는 데 있습니다."], ["Would you mind sitting quiet—on the family account—while I reckon ’em up?", "제가 저들을 꿰뚫어보는 동안 — 집안을 위해서 — 가만히 앉아 계시겠습니까?"], ["And would you just throw in a nod when I seem to ask you for it?”", "그리고 제가 끄덕여달라고 청하는 듯하거든 그냥 고개만 한 번 끄덕여주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Sir Leicester indistinctly answers, “Officer.", "레스터 경은 또렷하지 않게 대답한다. \"형사 양반."], ["The best you can, the best you can!” and Mr.", "할 수 있는 한 잘, 할 수 있는 한 잘 해주시오!\""], ["Bucket, with a nod and a sagacious crook of the forefinger, slips down into the hall, where the voices quickly die away.", "그러자 버킷 씨는 고개를 한 번 끄덕이고 집게손가락을 슬기롭게 한 번 구부려 보이며 현관으로 슬쩍 내려가는데, 그곳의 말소리는 이내 잦아든다."], ["He is not long in returning; a few paces ahead of Mercury and a brother deity also powdered and in peach-blossomed smalls, who bear between them a chair in which is an incapable old man.", "그가 돌아오기까지는 오래 걸리지 않는다. 시종과, 역시 머리에 분을 뿌리고 복숭아꽃빛 반바지를 입은 또 한 명의 신령 같은 자가 그보다 몇 걸음 앞서며, 둘이서 의자 하나를 맞들고 오는데 그 위에는 거동 못 하는 노인이 앉아 있다."], ["Another man and two women come behind.", "또 다른 남자 하나와 여자 둘이 뒤따른다."], ["Directing the pitching of the chair in an affable and easy manner, Mr. Bucket dismisses the Mercuries and locks the door again.", "버킷 씨는 상냥하고 느긋한 태도로 의자를 내려놓는 자리를 지시하더니, 두 시종을 물리고 다시 문을 잠근다."], ["Sir Leicester looks on at this invasion of the sacred precincts with an icy stare.", "레스터 경은 신성한 영역을 침범당하는 이 광경을 얼음장 같은 눈초리로 지켜본다."]], [["“Now, perhaps you may know me, ladies and gentlemen,”", "\"자, 어쩌면 여러분은 저를 아실 겁니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분,\" 버킷 씨가 은밀한 목소리로 말한다."], ["says Mr. Bucket in a confidential voice.", "\"저는 형사반의 버킷 경위입니다, 그렇고말고요."], ["“I am Inspector Bucket of the Detective, I am; and this,”", "그리고 이것이,\" 가슴 주머니에서 편리한 작은 지팡이 끝을 꺼내며 \"제 권한의 증표지요."], ["producing the tip of his convenient little staff from his breast-pocket, “is my authority.", "자, 여러분은 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리를 뵙고 싶어 하셨습니다. 좋습니다!"], ["Now, you wanted to see Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. Well!", "지금 나리를 뵙고 계시지요."], ["You do see him, and mind you, it ain’t every one as is admitted to that honour.", "명심하십시오, 그런 영광을 누리는 사람이 아무나 있는 게 아닙니다. 노신사 양반, 당신 성함은 스몰위드지요."], ["Your name, old gentleman, is Smallweed; that’s what your name is; I know it well.”", "그게 당신 성함입니다. 잘 알고 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, and you never heard any harm of it!” cries Mr.", "\"흥, 그래서 그게 어디 나쁜 소리 들은 적이라도 있나!\""], ["Smallweed in a shrill loud voice.", "스몰위드 씨가 째지듯 큰 목소리로 외친다."]], [["“You don’t happen to know why they killed the pig, do you?”", "\"혹시 사람들이 왜 돼지를 잡아 죽였는지 아십니까?\""], ["retorts Mr. Bucket with a steadfast look, but without loss of temper.", "버킷 씨가 흔들림 없는 눈빛으로, 그러나 성을 내지는 않고 받아친다."]], [["“No!”", "\"몰라!\""]], [["“Why, they killed him,” says Mr.", "\"왜냐하면 말입니다,\""], ["Bucket, “on account of his having so much cheek.", "버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그놈이 하도 뻔뻔하게 굴어서 잡아 죽인 겁니다."], ["Don’t YOU get into the same position, because it isn’t worthy of you.", "당신은 그런 처지에 빠지지 마세요. 당신답지 못한 짓이니까요."], ["You ain’t in the habit of conversing with a deaf person, are you?”", "당신, 귀먹은 사람하고 대화하는 버릇이 있는 건 아니지요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞아,\""], ["snarls Mr.", "스몰위드 씨가 으르렁거린다."], ["Smallweed, “my wife’s deaf.”", "\"내 마누라가 귀가 먹었어.\""]], [["“That accounts for your pitching your voice so high.", "\"그래서 그렇게 목청을 높이시는군요."], ["But as she ain’t here; just pitch it an octave or two lower, will you, and I’ll not only be obliged to you, but it’ll do you more credit,”", "허나 부인은 여기 안 계시니, 한두 옥타브쯤 낮춰주시겠습니까? 그래주시면 저야 고맙기도 하거니와, 당신 체면도 더 서실 겁니다,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“This other gentleman is in the preaching line, I think?”", "\"이 다른 신사분은 설교하는 분이신 것 같은데요?\""]], [["“Name of Chadband,” Mr.", "\"이름은 채드밴드요,\""], ["Smallweed puts in, speaking henceforth in a much lower key.", "스몰위드 씨가 끼어드는데, 이때부터는 한결 낮은 음으로 말한다."]], [["“Once had a friend and brother serjeant of the same name,” says Mr.", "\"예전에 같은 이름을 가진 친구이자 동료 부사관이 하나 있었지요,\""], ["Bucket, offering his hand, “and consequently feel a liking for it.", "버킷 씨가 손을 내밀며 말한다. \"그래서 그 이름에 정이 갑니다."], ["Mrs. Chadband, no doubt?”", "채드밴드 부인이시겠지요?\""]], [["“And Mrs. Snagsby,”", "\"그리고 스내그스비 부인이오,\""], ["Mr. Smallweed introduces.", "스몰위드 씨가 소개한다."]], [["“Husband a law-stationer and a friend of my own,”", "\"남편이 법률 문방구상이고 제 친구지요,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Love him like a brother!", "\"그를 형제처럼 아낍니다!"], ["Now, what’s up?”", "자, 무슨 일이십니까?\""]], [["“Do you mean what business have we come upon?”", "\"우리가 무슨 용건으로 왔느냐는 말이오?\""], ["Mr. Smallweed asks, a little dashed by the suddenness of this turn.", "스몰위드 씨가 이 갑작스러운 전환에 다소 기가 꺾여 묻는다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["You know what I mean.", "무슨 뜻인지 아시잖습니까."], ["Let us hear what it’s all about in presence of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리 앞에서 그게 다 무슨 일인지 들어봅시다."], ["Come.”", "자.\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed, beckoning Mr.", "스몰위드 씨는 채드밴드 씨를 손짓으로 부르더니, 잠시 속삭이며 그와 상의한다."], ["Chadband, takes a moment’s counsel with him in a whisper. Mr.", "채드밴드 씨는 이마와 손바닥 땀구멍에서 상당량의 기름기를 짜내며 큰 소리로 \"그래요."], ["Chadband, expressing a considerable amount of oil from the pores of his forehead and the palms of his hands, says aloud, “Yes. You first!”", "당신 먼저!\""], ["and retires to his former place.", "하고는 본디 자리로 물러난다."]], [["“I was the client and friend of Mr. Tulkinghorn,”", "\"나는 토킹혼 씨의 의뢰인이자 친구였소,\""], ["pipes Grandfather Smallweed then; “I did business with him.", "이윽고 스몰위드 영감이 새된 소리로 말한다. \"그와 거래를 했지."], ["I was useful to him, and he was useful to me.", "나는 그에게 쓸모가 있었고, 그도 내게 쓸모가 있었소."], ["Krook, dead and gone, was my brother-in-law.", "죽고 없는 크룩은 내 처남이었소."], ["He was own brother to a brimstone magpie—leastways Mrs. Smallweed.", "유황 까치 같은 — 어쨌든 우리 스몰위드 마누라 말이오 — 그 여편네의 친오빠였단 말이오."], ["I come into Krook’s property.", "내가 크룩의 재산을 물려받았소."], ["I examined all his papers and all his effects.", "나는 그의 서류며 유품을 죄다 살펴봤지."], ["They was all dug out under my eyes.", "다 내 눈앞에서 파헤쳐 나왔소."], ["There was a bundle of letters belonging to a dead and gone lodger as was hid away at the back of a shelf in the side of Lady Jane’s bed—his cat’s bed.", "죽고 없는 어떤 하숙인의 편지 한 묶음이 있었는데, 레이디 제인 — 그 고양이 잠자리 말이오 — 그 잠자리 옆쪽 선반 뒤에 숨겨져 있었소."], ["He hid all manner of things away, everywheres.", "그자는 별별 것을 다, 사방에 숨겨뒀소."], ["Mr. Tulkinghorn wanted ’em and got ’em, but I looked ’em over first.", "토킹혼 씨가 그걸 원해서 가져갔지만, 내가 먼저 훑어봤지."], ["I’m a man of business, and I took a squint at ’em.", "나는 사업하는 사람이라, 그것들을 슬쩍 들여다봤소."], ["They was letters from the lodger’s sweetheart, and she signed Honoria.", "그 하숙인의 연인이 보낸 편지였는데, 여자는 '호노리아'라고 서명했더군."], ["Dear me, that’s not a common name, Honoria, is it?", "이런, 호노리아라니 흔한 이름이 아니지 않소?"], ["There’s no lady in this house that signs Honoria is there?", "이 집에 호노리아라고 서명하는 마님은 안 계시겠지요?"], ["Oh, no, I don’t think so!", "아, 없겠지, 아무렴!"], ["Oh, no, I don’t think so!", "아, 없겠지, 아무렴!"], ["And not in the same hand, perhaps?", "게다가 같은 필체일 리도 없겠지?"], ["Oh, no, I don’t think so!”", "아, 없겠지, 아무렴!\""]], [["Here Mr. Smallweed, seized with a fit of coughing in the midst of his triumph, breaks off to ejaculate, “Oh, dear me!", "이 대목에서 스몰위드 씨는 의기양양해하던 와중에 기침 발작에 사로잡혀, 말을 끊고 외친다."], ["Oh, Lord!", "\"아이고 맙소사!"], ["I’m shaken all to pieces!”", "아, 주여! 온몸이 산산이 흔들리는구나!\""]], [["“Now, when you’re ready,” says Mr.", "\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리와 관계되는 무언가로,\""], ["Bucket after awaiting his recovery, “to come to anything that concerns Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, here the gentleman sits, you know.”", "버킷 씨가 그가 회복하기를 기다렸다가 말한다. \"넘어갈 준비가 되시거든, 신사분이 여기 앉아 계시니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Haven’t I come to it, Mr. Bucket?”", "\"아직 거기까지 안 왔다는 거요, 버킷 씨?\""], ["cries Grandfather Smallweed.", "스몰위드 영감이 외친다."], ["“Isn’t the gentleman concerned yet?", "\"신사분이 아직 관계가 없다는 거요?"], ["Not with Captain Hawdon, and his ever affectionate Honoria, and their child into the bargain?", "하든 대위하고, 그자의 변함없이 애틋한 호노리아하고, 게다가 그 둘의 자식까지 있는데도?"], ["Come, then, I want to know where those letters are.", "자, 그렇다면 나는 그 편지들이 어디 있는지 알고 싶소."], ["That concerns me, if it don’t concern Sir Leicester Dedlock.", "그건 나하고 관계된 일이오, 레스터 데드록 나리하고는 관계가 없더라도 말이오."], ["I will know where they are.", "나는 그것들이 어디 있는지 알아야겠소."], ["I won’t have ’em disappear so quietly.", "그렇게 조용히 사라지게 놔두지 않을 테요."], ["I handed ’em over to my friend and solicitor, Mr. Tulkinghorn, not to anybody else.”", "나는 그걸 내 친구이자 사무 변호사인 토킹혼 씨에게 넘겼지, 다른 누구한테 넘긴 게 아니란 말이오.\""]], [["“Why, he paid you for them, you know, and handsome too,”", "\"아니, 그가 그 값을 치렀잖습니까, 그것도 후하게 말입니다,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I don’t care for that.", "\"그건 상관없소."], ["I want to know who’s got ’em.", "나는 누가 그걸 가졌는지 알고 싶소."], ["And I tell you what we want—what we all here want, Mr. Bucket.", "그리고 우리가 뭘 원하는지 말해주지 — 여기 있는 우리 모두가 원하는 걸 말이오, 버킷 씨."], ["We want more painstaking and search-making into this murder.", "우리는 이 살인 사건을 더 공들여 더 샅샅이 파헤치기를 원하오."], ["We know where the interest and the motive was, and you have not done enough.", "우리는 그 이해관계와 동기가 어디 있었는지 알고 있고, 당신은 아직 충분히 하질 않았소."], ["If George the vagabond dragoon had any hand in it, he was only an accomplice, and was set on.", "그 떠돌이 용기병 조지가 거기 손을 댔다 한들, 그자는 한낱 공범일 뿐이고 누가 시킨 거요."], ["You know what I mean as well as any man.”", "당신도 누구 못지않게 내 말뜻을 알 것이오.\""]], [["“Now I tell you what,” says Mr.", "\"자, 내 한마디 하지요,\""], ["Bucket, instantaneously altering his manner, coming close to him, and communicating an extraordinary fascination to the forefinger, “I am damned if I am a-going to have my case spoilt, or interfered with, or anticipated by so much as half a second of time by any human being in creation.", "버킷 씨가 순식간에 태도를 바꾸어 그에게 바짝 다가서며, 집게손가락에 야릇한 흡인력을 실어 말한다. \"이 세상 그 어떤 인간이든 단 반 초라도 내 사건을 망치거나, 끼어들거나, 앞질러 가게 한다면 내가 빌어먹을 놈이 될 거요."], ["YOU want more painstaking and search-making!", "당신이 더 공들이고 더 샅샅이 파헤치기를 원한다고?"], ["YOU do?", "당신이?"], ["Do you see this hand, and do you think that I don’t know the right time to stretch it out and put it on the arm that fired that shot?”", "이 손이 보이시오? 그리고 내가 언제 이 손을 뻗어 그 총을 쏜 자의 팔에 얹어야 할지를 모를 것 같소?\""]], [["Such is the dread power of the man, and so terribly evident it is that he makes no idle boast, that Mr. Smallweed begins to apologize.", "이 사내의 무시무시한 위세와, 그것이 결코 허튼소리가 아님이 어찌나 끔찍하리만치 분명한지, 스몰위드 씨는 사과를 늘어놓기 시작한다."], ["Mr. Bucket, dismissing his sudden anger, checks him.", "버킷 씨는 별안간 치솟은 노여움을 거두며 그를 제지한다."]], [["“The advice I give you is, don’t you trouble your head about the murder.", "\"내가 충고하건대, 그 살인 사건은 당신 머릿속에서 지우시오."], ["That’s my affair.", "그건 내 일이니까."], ["You keep half an eye on the newspapers, and I shouldn’t wonder if you was to read something about it before long, if you look sharp.", "당신은 신문이나 곁눈으로 봐두시오. 눈을 부릅뜨고 보면 머잖아 그 일에 관한 무언가를 읽게 되더라도 놀랍지 않을 거요."], ["I know my business, and that’s all I’ve got to say to you on that subject.", "나는 내 일을 알고 있고, 그 문제에 관해 당신한테 할 말은 그게 다요."], ["Now about those letters.", "자, 이제 그 편지들 말입니다."], ["You want to know who’s got ’em.", "당신은 누가 그걸 가졌는지 알고 싶다고 했지요."], ["I don’t mind telling you.", "말해주는 거야 상관없소."], ["I have got ’em.", "내가 가졌소."], ["Is that the packet?”", "이게 그 묶음이오?\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed looks, with greedy eyes, at the little bundle Mr. Bucket produces from a mysterious part of his coat, and identifies it as the same.", "스몰위드 씨는 버킷 씨가 외투의 알 수 없는 곳에서 꺼내 든 작은 꾸러미를 게걸스러운 눈으로 바라보며, 바로 그것이라고 알아본다."]], [["“What have you got to say next?”", "\"그다음엔 무슨 할 말이 있으십니까?\""], ["asks Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 묻는다."], ["“Now, don’t open your mouth too wide, because you don’t look handsome when you do it.”", "\"자, 입을 너무 크게 벌리지 마세요. 그러면 보기 흉하니까.\""]], [["“I want five hundred pound.”", "\"오백 파운드를 원하오.\""]], [["“No, you don’t; you mean fifty,”", "\"아니, 그럴 리가. 오십 파운드란 말이겠지요,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket humorously.", "버킷 씨가 익살스레 말한다."]], [["It appears, however, that Mr. Smallweed means five hundred.", "그러나 알고 보니 스몰위드 씨는 정말로 오백을 뜻하는 모양이다."]], [["“That is, I am deputed by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to consider (without admitting or promising anything) this bit of business,”", "\"그러니까, 나는 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께 위임받아 (아무것도 인정하거나 약속하는 게 아니라) 이 사소한 거래를 고려해보라는 거지요,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket—Sir Leicester mechanically bows his head—“and you ask me to consider a proposal of five hundred pounds.", "버킷 씨가 말한다 — 레스터 경은 기계적으로 고개를 숙인다 — \"그런데 당신은 오백 파운드짜리 제안을 고려해달라는 거고."], ["Why, it’s an unreasonable proposal!", "아니, 그건 터무니없는 제안이오!"], ["Two fifty would be bad enough, but better than that.", "이백오십도 충분히 고약하지만, 그래도 그건 낫지요."], ["Hadn’t you better say two fifty?”", "이백오십이라고 하시는 게 낫지 않겠소?\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed is quite clear that he had better not.", "스몰위드 씨는 그러지 않는 편이 낫다고 아주 단호하다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["says Mr.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["Bucket, “let’s hear Mr. Chadband.", "\"채드밴드 씨 말을 들어봅시다."], ["Lord!", "이런!"], ["Many a time I’ve heard my old fellow-serjeant of that name; and a moderate man he was in all respects, as ever I come across!”", "같은 이름을 가진 옛 동료 부사관 얘길 어찌나 자주 들었던지. 내가 만난 사람 중에 매사에 더없이 온건한 사람이었지요!\""]], [["Thus invited, Mr. Chadband steps forth, and after a little sleek smiling and a little oil-grinding with the palms of his hands, delivers himself as follows, “My friends, we are now—Rachael, my wife, and I—in the mansions of the rich and great.", "이렇게 청을 받자 채드밴드 씨가 앞으로 나서더니, 매끄럽게 잠깐 미소 짓고 손바닥으로 잠깐 기름을 비벼대고는 다음과 같이 입을 연다."], ["Why are we now in the mansions of the rich and great, my friends?", "\"벗들이여, 우리는 지금 — 내 아내 레이철과 나는 — 부유하고 위대한 자들의 저택에 와 있습니다. 우리는 어찌하여 지금 부유하고 위대한 자들의 저택에 와 있을까요, 벗들이여?"], ["Is it because we are invited?", "우리가 초대를 받아서일까요?"], ["Because we are bidden to feast with them, because we are bidden to rejoice with them, because we are bidden to play the lute with them, because we are bidden to dance with them?", "그들과 함께 잔치를 즐기라 청함을 받았기에, 그들과 함께 기뻐하라 청함을 받았기에, 그들과 함께 류트를 타라 청함을 받았기에, 그들과 함께 춤추라 청함을 받았기에? 아니지요."], ["No. Then why are we here, my friends?", "그렇다면 우리는 어찌하여 여기 있을까요, 벗들이여?"], ["Air we in possession of a sinful secret, and do we require corn, and wine, and oil, or what is much the same thing, money, for the keeping thereof?", "우리가 어떤 죄 많은 비밀을 쥐고 있어, 그것을 지키는 대가로 곡식과, 포도주와, 기름, 혹은 그와 거의 매한가지인 돈을 요구하기 때문일까요?"], ["Probably so, my friends.”", "아마 그럴 테지요, 벗들이여.\""]], [["“You’re a man of business, you are,” returns Mr.", "\"당신도 사업하는 사람이로군요,\""], ["Bucket, very attentive, “and consequently you’re going on to mention what the nature of your secret is.", "버킷 씨가 아주 주의 깊게 대답한다. \"그러니 이제 그 비밀의 성격이 무엇인지 말씀하시려는 거고요."], ["You are right.", "옳습니다."], ["You couldn’t do better.”", "그보다 나은 수가 없지요.\""]], [["“Let us then, my brother, in a spirit of love,” says Mr.", "\"그러면 우리 형제여, 사랑의 정신으로,\""], ["Chadband with a cunning eye, “proceed unto it.", "채드밴드 씨가 교활한 눈빛으로 말한다. \"그리로 나아갑시다."], ["Rachael, my wife, advance!”", "레이철, 내 아내여, 앞으로!\""]], [["Mrs. Chadband, more than ready, so advances as to jostle her husband into the background and confronts Mr. Bucket with a hard, frowning smile.", "채드밴드 부인은 진작부터 채비가 되어 있던 터라, 어찌나 앞으로 나서는지 제 남편을 뒤로 밀쳐내고는 딱딱하고 찌푸린 미소로 버킷 씨와 마주 선다."]], [["“Since you want to know what we know,”", "\"우리가 아는 걸 알고 싶다니,\""], ["says she, “I’ll tell you.", "그녀가 말한다. \"내 말해주지요."], ["I helped to bring up Miss Hawdon, her ladyship’s daughter.", "나는 하든 양을, 그 마님의 따님을 키우는 걸 거들었소."], ["I was in the service of her ladyship’s sister, who was very sensitive to the disgrace her ladyship brought upon her, and gave out, even to her ladyship, that the child was dead—she WAS very nearly so—when she was born. But she’s alive, and I know her.”", "나는 그 마님의 언니 밑에서 일했는데, 그분은 마님이 자기에게 끼친 치욕에 몹시 예민하셔서, 마님께까지도 그 아이가 죽었다고 둘러댔지요 — 사실 거의 죽다시피 했고요 — 태어났을 때 말이오."], ["With these words, and a laugh, and laying a bitter stress on the word “ladyship,” Mrs.", "허나 그 아이는 살아 있고, 나는 그 아이를 알고 있소.\""], ["Chadband folds her arms and looks implacably at Mr. Bucket.", "이 말과 함께, 한바탕 웃음과 함께, '마님'이라는 말에 신랄한 힘을 주며, 채드밴드 부인은 팔짱을 끼고 버킷 씨를 무자비하게 노려본다."]], [["“I suppose now,”", "\"그러니까 짐작건대,\""], ["returns that officer, “YOU will be expecting a twenty-pound note or a present of about that figure?”", "그 형사가 대답한다. \"당신은 이십 파운드짜리 지폐, 아니면 그쯤 되는 선물을 기대하시는 거겠지요?\""]], [["Mrs. Chadband merely laughs and contemptuously tells him he can “offer”", "채드밴드 부인은 그저 웃기만 하면서, 경멸하듯 그에게 이십 펜스나 '내놓아'"], ["twenty pence.", "보라고 한다."]], [["“My friend the law-stationer’s good lady, over there,” says Mr.", "\"저기 계신 내 친구 법률 문방구상의 마나님은,\""], ["Bucket, luring Mrs. Snagsby forward with the finger.", "버킷 씨가 손가락으로 스내그스비 부인을 앞으로 꾀어내며 말한다."], ["“What may YOUR game be, ma’am?”", "\"당신의 속셈은 무엇입니까, 부인?\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby is at first prevented, by tears and lamentations, from stating the nature of her game, but by degrees it confusedly comes to light that she is a woman overwhelmed with injuries and wrongs, whom Mr. Snagsby has habitually deceived, abandoned, and sought to keep in darkness, and whose chief comfort, under her afflictions, has been the sympathy of the late Mr. Tulkinghorn, who showed so much commiseration for her on one occasion of his calling in Cook’s Court in the absence of her perjured husband that she has of late habitually carried to him all her woes. Everybody it appears, the present company excepted, has plotted against Mrs. Snagsby’s peace. There is Mr. Guppy, clerk to Kenge and Carboy, who was at first as open as the sun at noon, but who suddenly shut up as close as midnight, under the influence—no doubt—of Mr. Snagsby’s suborning and tampering. There is Mr. Weevle, friend of Mr. Guppy, who lived mysteriously up a court, owing to the like coherent causes. There was Krook, deceased; there was Nimrod, deceased; and there was Jo, deceased; and they were “all in it.” In what, Mrs. Snagsby does not with particularity express, but she knows that Jo was Mr. Snagsby’s son, “as well as if a trumpet had spoken it,” and she followed Mr. Snagsby when he went on his last visit to the boy, and if he was not his son why did he go? The one occupation of her life has been, for some time back, to follow Mr. Snagsby to and fro, and up and down, and to piece suspicious circumstances together—and every circumstance that has happened has been most suspicious; and in this way she has pursued her object of detecting and confounding her false husband, night and day. Thus did it come to pass that she brought the Chadbands and Mr. Tulkinghorn together, and conferred with Mr. Tulkinghorn on the change in Mr. Guppy, and helped to turn up the circumstances in which the present company are interested, casually, by the wayside, being still and ever on the great high road that is to terminate in Mr. Snagsby’s full exposure and a matrimonial separation. All this, Mrs. Snagsby, as an injured woman, and the friend of Mrs. Chadband, and the follower of Mr. Chadband, and the mourner of the late Mr. Tulkinghorn, is here to certify under the seal of confidence, with every possible confusion and involvement possible and impossible, having no pecuniary motive whatever, no scheme or project but the one mentioned, and bringing here, and taking everywhere, her own dense atmosphere of dust, arising from the ceaseless working of her mill of jealousy.", "스내그스비 부인은 처음엔 눈물과 한탄에 막혀 제 속셈의 성격을 밝히지 못하지만, 차츰 어수선하게나마 진상이 드러나니, 그녀는 온갖 상처와 부당함에 짓눌린 여인으로, 스내그스비 씨가 늘 자기를 속이고, 저버리고, 어둠 속에 가둬두려 해왔으며, 그 고난 속에서 그녀의 가장 큰 위안은 고인이 된 토킹혼 씨의 동정이었으니, 그 위증쟁이 남편이 자리를 비운 사이 그분이 쿡스 코트에 들렀던 어느 날 그녀에게 어찌나 큰 연민을 보여주었던지, 그녀는 근래 들어 늘 자기의 온갖 시름을 그분께 가져가곤 했다는 것이다. 보아하니 모든 사람이 — 지금 이 자리에 있는 이들만 빼고 — 스내그스비 부인의 평화를 음모로 깨뜨려왔다. 켄지 앤 카보이의 사무원 거피 씨가 있는데, 처음엔 한낮의 태양처럼 활짝 열린 사람이었으나, 의심할 바 없이 스내그스비 씨가 매수하고 농간을 부린 탓에 별안간 한밤중처럼 꽉 닫혀버렸다. 거피 씨의 친구인 위블 씨가 있는데, 비슷하게 앞뒤가 들어맞는 이유로 어느 골목 위층에 수상쩍게 살고 있었다. 죽고 없는 크룩이 있었고, 죽고 없는 님로드가 있었고, 죽고 없는 조가 있었으니, 그들은 모두 \"한패였다.\" 무슨 한패였는지 스내그스비 부인은 콕 집어 말하지 못하지만, 조가 스내그스비 씨의 아들이라는 것은 \"나팔이 울려 알려준 듯이\" 확실히 알고 있고, 스내그스비 씨가 그 아이를 마지막으로 찾아갔을 때 그 뒤를 밟았으며, 그 아이가 그의 아들이 아니라면 어찌하여 그가 갔겠느냐는 것이다. 한동안 그녀의 삶에서 단 하나의 일거리는 스내그스비 씨를 이리저리, 위아래로 따라다니며 수상쩍은 정황들을 끼워 맞추는 것이었고 — 일어난 정황은 죄다 더없이 수상쩍었으며 — 이런 식으로 그녀는 거짓된 남편을 적발하고 망신 주려는 목표를 밤낮으로 쫓아왔다. 그리하여 채드밴드 부부와 토킹혼 씨를 한자리에 모으게 되었고, 거피 씨의 변화에 대해 토킹혼 씨와 의논했으며, 지금 이 자리에 있는 이들이 관심을 둔 정황들을 길가에서 우연히, 그러나 늘 변함없이 스내그스비 씨의 전면 폭로와 부부의 별거로 끝맺을 그 큰길 위에서 들춰내는 일을 거들었다. 이 모든 것을 스내그스비 부인은, 상처받은 여인으로서, 채드밴드 부인의 벗으로서, 채드밴드 씨의 추종자로서, 고인이 된 토킹혼 씨를 애도하는 자로서, 가능한 모든 그리고 불가능한 온갖 혼란과 뒤얽힘을 곁들여, 어떠한 금전적 동기도 없이, 앞서 말한 것 말고는 아무런 책략이나 계획도 없이, 끊임없이 돌아가는 제 질투의 방앗간에서 피어오르는 제 자신의 자욱한 먼지 안개를 여기 끌고 들어와 사방에 퍼뜨리며, 은밀한 봉인 아래 증언하러 이곳에 와 있는 것이다."]], [["While this exordium is in hand—and it takes some time—Mr. Bucket, who has seen through the transparency of Mrs. Snagsby’s vinegar at a glance, confers with his familiar demon and bestows his shrewd attention on the Chadbands and Mr. Smallweed. Sir Leicester Dedlock remains immovable, with the same icy surface upon him, except that he once or twice looks towards Mr. Bucket, as relying on that officer alone of all mankind.", "이 장황한 서설이 이어지는 동안 — 그것은 제법 시간이 걸린다 — 스내그스비 부인의 식초 같은 속내를 한눈에 꿰뚫어본 버킷 씨는 제 친숙한 악마와 회의를 하며, 채드밴드 부부와 스몰위드 씨에게 빈틈없는 주의를 기울인다. 레스터 데드록 경은 한두 번 버킷 씨 쪽을 바라보는 것 말고는, 온 인류 가운데 오직 그 형사만을 의지하기라도 하듯, 여전히 얼음장 같은 표면을 두른 채 꼼짝 않고 있다."]], [["“Very good,”", "\"아주 좋습니다,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Now I understand you, you know, and being deputed by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to look into this little matter,”", "\"이제 당신들 속내를 알겠군요. 그리고 나는 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리께 위임받아 이 사소한 문제를 들여다보는 자이니,\""], ["again Sir Leicester mechanically bows in confirmation of the statement, “can give it my fair and full attention.", "레스터 경은 다시 기계적으로 고개를 숙여 그 진술을 확인해준다. \"공정하고 충분한 주의를 기울일 수 있지요."], ["Now I won’t allude to conspiring to extort money or anything of that sort, because we are men and women of the world here, and our object is to make things pleasant.", "자, 돈을 뜯어내려는 공모니 뭐니 하는 따위는 들먹이지 않겠습니다. 여기 있는 우리는 세상 물정에 밝은 남녀이고, 우리 목적은 일을 원만하게 만드는 거니까요."], ["But I tell you what I DO wonder at; I am surprised that you should think of making a noise below in the hall.", "허나 정말 의아한 게 하나 있는데, 당신들이 아래 현관에서 소란을 피울 생각을 했다는 게 놀랍단 말입니다."], ["It was so opposed to your interests.", "그건 당신들 이익에 정면으로 어긋나는 짓이었어요."], ["That’s what I look at.”", "내가 보는 건 바로 그겁니다.\""]], [["“We wanted to get in,”", "\"우리는 안으로 들어오고 싶었던 거요,\""], ["pleads Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 변명한다."]], [["“Why, of course you wanted to get in,” Mr.", "\"아니, 물론 안으로 들어오고 싶으셨겠지요,\""], ["Bucket asserts with cheerfulness; “but for a old gentleman at your time of life—what I call truly venerable, mind you!—with his wits sharpened, as I have no doubt they are, by the loss of the use of his limbs, which occasions all his animation to mount up into his head, not to consider that if he don’t keep such a business as the present as close as possible it can’t be worth a mag to him, is so curious!", "버킷 씨가 쾌활하게 단언한다. \"허나 당신만 한 연세의 노신사가 — 진정 우러러볼 만한 분이라고 했잖습니까, 명심하세요!"], ["You see your temper got the better of you; that’s where you lost ground,”", "— 사지를 못 쓰게 된 탓에 — 그래서 모든 활력이 머리로 솟구쳐 의심할 바 없이 머리가 날카로워지셨을 텐데 — 지금 같은 거래는 가능한 한 비밀로 부치지 않으면 한 푼의 값어치도 없으리라는 걸 헤아리지 못하다니, 참으로 기묘하단 말입니다! 보세요, 당신은 성질이 당신을 이겨버린 겁니다."], ["says Mr.", "바로 거기서 발판을 잃은 거예요,\""], ["Bucket in an argumentative and friendly way.", "버킷 씨가 따지는 듯하면서도 다정한 투로 말한다."]], [["“I only said I wouldn’t go without one of the servants came up to Sir Leicester Dedlock,”", "\"나는 그저 하인 한 명이 레스터 데드록 나리께 올라가지 않으면 돌아가지 않겠다고 했을 뿐이오,\""], ["returns Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 대꾸한다."]], [["“That’s it!", "\"바로 그겁니다!"], ["That’s where your temper got the better of you.", "거기서 성질이 당신을 이겨버린 거지요."], ["Now, you keep it under another time and you’ll make money by it.", "자, 다음번엔 그걸 잘 다스리시오. 그럼 그걸로 돈을 버실 테니까."], ["Shall I ring for them to carry you down?”", "사람들을 불러 당신을 내려가게 할까요?\""]], [["“When are we to hear more of this?”", "\"이 일에 관해선 언제 더 듣게 되는 거죠?\""], ["Mrs. Chadband sternly demands.", "채드밴드 부인이 매섭게 따져 묻는다."]], [["“Bless your heart for a true woman!", "\"과연 진정한 여인이시군요, 복 받으세요!"], ["Always curious, your delightful sex is!” replies Mr.", "늘 호기심에 차 있는 게 당신들 사랑스러운 성이지요!\""], ["Bucket with gallantry.", "버킷 씨가 정중하게 대답한다."], ["“I shall have the pleasure of giving you a call to-morrow or next day—not forgetting Mr. Smallweed and his proposal of two fifty.”", "\"내일이나 모레쯤 당신을 찾아뵙는 기쁨을 누리겠습니다 — 스몰위드 씨와 그의 이백오십 파운드 제안도 잊지 않고요.\""]], [["“Five hundred!”", "\"오백이오!\""], ["exclaims Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 외친다."]], [["“All right!", "\"좋습니다!"], ["Nominally five hundred.” Mr.", "명목상 오백이라 합시다.\""], ["Bucket has his hand on the bell-rope.", "버킷 씨는 종 줄에 손을 얹고 있다."], ["“SHALL I wish you good day for the present on the part of myself and the gentleman of the house?”", "\"그럼 저 자신과 이 댁 신사분을 대신하여, 지금으로선 작별 인사를 드릴까요?\""], ["he asks in an insinuating tone.", "그가 슬쩍 구슬리는 투로 묻는다."]], [["Nobody having the hardihood to object to his doing so, he does it, and the party retire as they came up. Mr.", "아무도 그러지 말라고 우길 만한 배짱이 없으니, 그는 그렇게 하고, 일행은 올라왔던 대로 물러난다."], ["Bucket follows them to the door, and returning, says with an air of serious business, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, it’s for you to consider whether or not to buy this up.", "버킷 씨는 그들을 문까지 따라갔다가 돌아와, 진지한 업무를 보는 태도로 말한다. \"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이걸 사들일지 말지는 나리께서 판단하실 일입니다."], ["I should recommend, on the whole, it’s being bought up myself; and I think it may be bought pretty cheap.", "저로서야 대체로 사들이는 편을 권하고 싶고요."], ["You see, that little pickled cowcumber of a Mrs.", "또 제법 싸게 살 수 있을 듯합니다."], ["Snagsby has been used by all sides of the speculation and has done a deal more harm in bringing odds and ends together than if she had meant it. Mr.", "보십시오, 저 스내그스비 부인이라는 절인 오이 같은 여편네는 온갖 속셈에 두루 이용당해서, 일부러 그러려 했던 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 해를 끼쳤습니다, 자질구레한 것들을 한데 끌어모으면서 말이지요."], ["Tulkinghorn, deceased, he held all these horses in his hand and could have drove ’em his own way, I haven’t a doubt; but he was fetched off the box head-foremost, and now they have got their legs over the traces, and are all dragging and pulling their own ways.", "고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨는 이 모든 말을 손아귀에 쥐고 제 뜻대로 몰 수 있었을 겁니다, 그건 의심할 바 없지요. 허나 그분이 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 마부석에서 떨어지고 나니, 이제 말들이 줄을 넘어 다리를 걸치고는 저마다 제멋대로 끌고 당기고 있는 겁니다."], ["So it is, and such is life.", "그게 그렇고, 사는 게 그렇지요."], ["The cat’s away, and the mice they play; the frost breaks up, and the water runs.", "고양이가 없으니 쥐들이 노닐고, 서리가 풀리니 물이 흐르는 법입니다."], ["Now, with regard to the party to be apprehended.”", "자, 이제 잡아들일 자에 관해서 말입니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester seems to wake, though his eyes have been wide open, and he looks intently at Mr. Bucket as Mr. Bucket refers to his watch.", "레스터 경은 눈을 줄곧 부릅뜨고 있었으면서도 마치 잠에서 깨어나는 듯하더니, 버킷 씨가 회중시계를 들여다보는 동안 그를 뚫어지게 바라본다."]], [["“The party to be apprehended is now in this house,” proceeds Mr.", "\"잡아들일 자는 지금 이 집 안에 있습니다,\""], ["Bucket, putting up his watch with a steady hand and with rising spirits, “and I’m about to take her into custody in your presence.", "버킷 씨가 흔들림 없는 손으로 시계를 집어넣고 점점 기운이 오른 채 말을 잇는다. \"그리고 저는 나리께서 보시는 앞에서 그 여자를 구금하려 합니다."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, don’t you say a word nor yet stir.", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 한마디도 하지 마시고 까딱도 하지 마십시오."], ["There’ll be no noise and no disturbance at all.", "아무런 소동도 소란도 없을 겁니다."], ["I’ll come back in the course of the evening, if agreeable to you, and endeavour to meet your wishes respecting this unfortunate family matter and the nobbiest way of keeping it quiet.", "괜찮으시다면 저녁나절에 다시 와서, 이 불행한 집안일과 그것을 가장 말끔하게 덮을 방도에 관한 나리의 뜻을 받들도록 애써보겠습니다."], ["Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, don’t you be nervous on account of the apprehension at present coming off.", "자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 지금 곧 있을 체포 때문에 마음 졸이지 마십시오."], ["You shall see the whole case clear, from first to last.”", "처음부터 끝까지 이 사건 전모를 환히 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Bucket rings, goes to the door, briefly whispers Mercury, shuts the door, and stands behind it with his arms folded.", "버킷 씨는 종을 울리고, 문으로 가서, 시종에게 짤막하게 속삭이고는, 문을 닫고, 팔짱을 낀 채 그 뒤에 선다."], ["After a suspense of a minute or two the door slowly opens and a Frenchwoman enters.", "한두 분쯤 마음 졸이는 시간이 흐른 뒤 문이 천천히 열리고 한 프랑스 여자가 들어온다."], ["Mademoiselle Hortense.", "오르탕스 양이다."]], [["The moment she is in the room Mr. Bucket claps the door to and puts his back against it.", "그녀가 방에 들어선 순간 버킷 씨는 문을 탁 닫고 등을 기대 막아선다."], ["The suddenness of the noise occasions her to turn, and then for the first time she sees Sir Leicester Dedlock in his chair.", "그 소리의 느닷없음에 그녀는 돌아서고, 그제야 비로소 안락의자에 앉은 레스터 데드록 경을 본다."]], [["“I ask you pardon,”", "\"용서하세요,\""], ["she mutters hurriedly.", "그녀가 황급히 중얼거린다."], ["“They tell me there was no one here.”", "\"여기 아무도 없다고들 하기에.\""]], [["Her step towards the door brings her front to front with Mr. Bucket.", "문 쪽으로 내딛는 걸음이 그녀를 버킷 씨와 정면으로 마주 세운다."], ["Suddenly a spasm shoots across her face and she turns deadly pale.", "별안간 경련이 그녀의 얼굴을 가로지르더니 그녀는 새파랗게 질린다."]], [["“This is my lodger, Sir Leicester Dedlock,”", "\"이 여자는 제 하숙인입니다, 레스터 데드록 경,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, nodding at her.", "버킷 씨가 그녀를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말한다."], ["“This foreign young woman has been my lodger for some weeks back.”", "\"이 외국인 처자가 몇 주 전부터 제 하숙인이지요.\""]], [["“What do Sir Leicester care for that, you think, my angel?”", "\"레스터 경이 그딴 걸 무슨 상관 하겠어, 안 그래, 내 천사님?\""], ["returns mademoiselle in a jocular strain.", "오르탕스 양이 농지거리하듯 대꾸한다."]], [["“Why, my angel,”", "\"글쎄다, 내 천사님,\""], ["returns Mr.", "버킷 씨가 대꾸한다."], ["Bucket, “we shall see.”", "\"두고 봐야지.\""]], [["Mademoiselle Hortense eyes him with a scowl upon her tight face, which gradually changes into a smile of scorn, “You are very mysterieuse.", "오르탕스 양은 팽팽하게 굳은 얼굴에 잔뜩 인상을 쓰며 그를 노려보다가, 그 표정이 차츰 경멸의 미소로 바뀐다. \"당신 참 알 수 없는 사람이네."], ["Are you drunk?”", "술 취했어?\""]], [["“Tolerable sober, my angel,”", "\"멀쩡하다네, 내 천사님,\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 대꾸한다."]], [["“I come from arriving at this so detestable house with your wife.", "\"나는 당신 마누라하고 이 지긋지긋한 집에 막 도착했어."], ["Your wife have left me since some minutes.", "당신 마누라가 몇 분 전에 날 두고 가버렸어."], ["They tell me downstairs that your wife is here.", "아래층에서 당신 마누라가 여기 있다고 하더군."], ["I come here, and your wife is not here.", "그래서 올라왔는데, 당신 마누라는 여기 없잖아."], ["What is the intention of this fool’s play, say then?”", "대체 이 바보 같은 장난이 무슨 꿍꿍이야, 어디 말해봐?\""], ["mademoiselle demands, with her arms composedly crossed, but with something in her dark cheek beating like a clock.", "오르탕스 양이 팔짱을 차분히 낀 채, 그러나 그 거무스름한 뺨 어딘가가 시계처럼 똑딱이는 채로 따져 묻는다."]], [["Mr. Bucket merely shakes the finger at her.", "버킷 씨는 그저 그녀에게 손가락을 흔들어 보일 뿐이다."]], [["“Ah, my God, you are an unhappy idiot!”", "\"아, 맙소사, 당신 참 한심한 멍청이로군!\""], ["cries mademoiselle with a toss of her head and a laugh.", "오르탕스 양이 고개를 홱 젖히며 웃음과 함께 외친다."], ["“Leave me to pass downstairs, great pig.”", "\"비키라고, 이 큰 돼지야, 아래층으로 내려가게.\""], ["With a stamp of her foot and a menace.", "발을 쿵 구르고 으름장을 놓으며."]], [["“Now, mademoiselle,” says Mr.", "\"자, 아가씨,\""], ["Bucket in a cool determined way, “you go and sit down upon that sofy.”", "버킷 씨가 차분하고 단호하게 말한다. \"가서 저 소파에 앉으시오.\""]], [["“I will not sit down upon nothing,”", "\"나는 아무 데도 안 앉아,\""], ["she replies with a shower of nods.", "그녀가 고개를 마구 끄덕이며 대꾸한다."]], [["“Now, mademoiselle,” repeats Mr.", "\"자, 아가씨,\""], ["Bucket, making no demonstration except with the finger, “you sit down upon that sofy.”", "버킷 씨가 손가락 말고는 아무런 동작도 하지 않은 채 되풀이한다. \"저 소파에 앉으시오.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“Because I take you into custody on a charge of murder, and you don’t need to be told it.", "\"살인 혐의로 당신을 구금할 거니까. 굳이 일러줄 것도 없겠지."], ["Now, I want to be polite to one of your sex and a foreigner if I can.", "자, 나는 당신 같은 여자에게, 그것도 외국인에게 할 수 있는 한 예의를 차리고 싶소."], ["If I can’t, I must be rough, and there’s rougher ones outside.", "그게 안 되면 거칠게 굴 수밖에 없고, 밖에는 더 거친 자들이 있소."], ["What I am to be depends on you.", "내가 어떻게 나갈지는 당신한테 달렸소."], ["So I recommend you, as a friend, afore another half a blessed moment has passed over your head, to go and sit down upon that sofy.”", "그러니 친구로서 충고하건대, 당신 머리 위로 복된 반 순간이 더 지나가기 전에, 가서 저 소파에 앉으시오.\""]], [["Mademoiselle complies, saying in a concentrated voice while that something in her cheek beats fast and hard, “You are a devil.”", "오르탕스 양은 그 뺨 어딘가가 빠르고 세게 똑딱이는 채로, 응축된 목소리로 \"당신은 악마야\" 하고 말하며 따른다."]], [["“Now, you see,” Mr.", "\"자, 보십시오,\""], ["Bucket proceeds approvingly, “you’re comfortable and conducting yourself as I should expect a foreign young woman of your sense to do.", "버킷 씨가 흡족하게 말을 잇는다. \"이제 편안히, 당신 같은 분별 있는 외국인 처자에게 기대할 만한 대로 처신하시는군."], ["So I’ll give you a piece of advice, and it’s this, don’t you talk too much.", "그래서 충고를 하나 해주겠는데, 다름 아니라, 말을 너무 많이 하지 마시오."], ["You’re not expected to say anything here, and you can’t keep too quiet a tongue in your head.", "여기서 당신이 무슨 말을 하기를 바라는 사람도 없고, 입을 다물고 있을수록 좋소."], ["In short, the less you PARLAY, the better, you know.”", "한마디로, 떠벌릴수록 손해라는 거요, 알겠소?\""], ["Mr. Bucket is very complacent over this French explanation.", "버킷 씨는 이 프랑스어 설명을 무척이나 흡족해한다."]], [["Mademoiselle, with that tigerish expansion of the mouth and her black eyes darting fire upon him, sits upright on the sofa in a rigid state, with her hands clenched—and her feet too, one might suppose—muttering, “Oh, you Bucket, you are a devil!”", "오르탕스 양은 입을 호랑이처럼 잔뜩 벌리고 검은 눈에서 그를 향해 불을 뿜으며, 두 손을 — 또 짐작건대 두 발까지도 — 꽉 움켜쥔 채 소파에 뻣뻣하게 곧추앉아 중얼거린다. \"오, 이 버킷, 당신은 악마야!\""]], [["“Now, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,”", "\"자, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["says Mr.", "버킷 씨가 말하는데, 이때부터 그 손가락은 한시도 쉬지 않는다."], ["Bucket, and from this time forth the finger never rests, “this young woman, my lodger, was her ladyship’s maid at the time I have mentioned to you; and this young woman, besides being extraordinary vehement and passionate against her ladyship after being discharged—”", "\"제 하숙인인 이 처자는, 제가 말씀드린 그 무렵 마님의 하녀였습니다. 그리고 이 처자는 해고당한 뒤로 마님에게 유난히 격렬하고 사납게 굴었을 뿐 아니라—\""]], [["“Lie!”", "\"거짓말!\""], ["cries mademoiselle.", "오르탕스 양이 외친다."], ["“I discharge myself.”", "\"내가 내 발로 그만뒀어.\""]], [["“Now, why don’t you take my advice?”", "\"자, 왜 내 충고를 안 듣는 거요?\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket in an impressive, almost in an imploring, tone.", "버킷 씨가 인상 깊게, 거의 사정하다시피 한 어조로 대꾸한다."], ["“I’m surprised at the indiscreetness you commit.", "\"당신이 저지르는 경솔함에 놀랄 따름이오."], ["You’ll say something that’ll be used against you, you know.", "당신은 자기한테 불리하게 쓰일 말을 하고 말 거요, 알겠소."], ["You’re sure to come to it.", "틀림없이 그렇게 될 거요."], ["Never you mind what I say till it’s given in evidence.", "내 말이 증거로 제출되기 전까지는 신경 쓰지 마시오."], ["It is not addressed to you.”", "그건 당신더러 들으라는 말이 아니니까.\""]], [["“Discharge, too,”", "\"게다가 해고라니,\""], ["cries mademoiselle furiously, “by her ladyship!", "오르탕스 양이 사납게 외친다. \"마님한테 해고당했다고!"], ["Eh, my faith, a pretty ladyship!", "흥, 맙소사, 거룩하기도 한 마님이지!"], ["Why, I r-r-r-ruin my character by remaining with a ladyship so infame!”", "그렇게 파렴치한 마님 곁에 붙어 있다가 내 신상만 망치고 말 텐데!\""]], [["“Upon my soul I wonder at you!” Mr.", "\"맹세코 당신한테 놀랐소!\""], ["Bucket remonstrates.", "버킷 씨가 항변한다."], ["“I thought the French were a polite nation, I did, really.", "\"나는 프랑스인이 정중한 민족인 줄 알았는데 말이오, 정말로요."], ["Yet to hear a female going on like that before Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet!”", "헌데 여자가 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리 앞에서 저런 식으로 떠들어대는 꼴을 다 듣다니!\""]], [["“He is a poor abused!”", "\"이 양반은 가엾게 능욕당한 사람이야!\""], ["cries mademoiselle.", "오르탕스 양이 외친다."], ["“I spit upon his house, upon his name, upon his imbecility,”", "\"나는 이 양반 집에, 이 양반 이름에, 이 양반 멍청함에 침을 뱉어,\""], ["all of which she makes the carpet represent.", "이 모든 것을 그녀는 양탄자로 대신 표현한다."], ["“Oh, that he is a great man!", "\"오, 대단한 양반이라지!"], ["Oh, yes, superb!", "오, 그래, 거룩하기도 하셔라!"], ["Oh, heaven!", "오, 하느님!"], ["Bah!”", "흥!\""]], [["“Well, Sir Leicester Dedlock,”", "\"자, 레스터 데드록 경,\""], ["proceeds Mr.", "버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다."], ["Bucket, “this intemperate foreigner also angrily took it into her head that she had established a claim upon Mr. Tulkinghorn, deceased, by attending on the occasion I told you of at his chambers, though she was liberally paid for her time and trouble.”", "\"이 무절제한 외국 여자는 또, 제가 말씀드린 그 사건 때 토킹혼 씨의 방에서 시중을 든 일로 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨에게 무슨 권리를 세워놓았다고 성을 내며 멋대로 생각했습니다, 제 시간과 수고에 대해 후하게 값을 치렀는데도 말이지요.\""]], [["“Lie!”", "\"거짓말!\""], ["cries mademoiselle.", "오르탕스 양이 외친다."], ["“I ref-use his money all togezzer.”", "\"나는 그자 돈 몽땅 거절했어.\""]], [["“If you WILL PARLAY, you know,” says Mr.", "\"당신이 굳이 떠벌리겠다면 말이오,\""], ["Bucket parenthetically, “you must take the consequences.", "버킷 씨가 곁들이듯 말한다. \"그 결과를 감수해야 할 거요."], ["Now, whether she became my lodger, Sir Leicester Dedlock, with any deliberate intention then of doing this deed and blinding me, I give no opinion on; but she lived in my house in that capacity at the time that she was hovering about the chambers of the deceased Mr.", "자, 이 여자가 그때 이미 이 짓을 저지르고 저를 속일 작정으로 제 하숙인이 되었는지 어떤지는 제가 의견을 내지 않겠습니다."], ["Tulkinghorn with a view to a wrangle, and likewise persecuting and half frightening the life out of an unfortunate stationer.”", "허나 이 여자는 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨의 방을 다투려는 속셈으로 어슬렁거리던 무렵, 그리고 어떤 가엾은 문방구상을 들볶고 반쯤 혼을 빼놓던 무렵에 그런 신분으로 제 집에 살았습니다.\""]], [["“Lie!”", "\"거짓말!\""], ["cries mademoiselle.", "오르탕스 양이 외친다."], ["“All lie!”", "\"다 거짓말이야!\""]], [["“The murder was committed, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and you know under what circumstances.", "\"살인이 저질러졌습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리. 어떤 정황에서였는지는 나리도 아십니다."], ["Now, I beg of you to follow me close with your attention for a minute or two.", "자, 청컨대 일이 분만 제 말을 바짝 따라와 주십시오."], ["I was sent for, and the case was entrusted to me.", "저는 부름을 받았고, 이 사건이 제게 맡겨졌습니다."], ["I examined the place, and the body, and the papers, and everything.", "저는 현장과, 시신과, 서류와, 모든 것을 살펴봤습니다."], ["From information I received (from a clerk in the same house) I took George into custody as having been seen hanging about there on the night, and at very nigh the time of the murder, also as having been overheard in high words with the deceased on former occasions—even threatening him, as the witness made out.", "(같은 집에 사는 어느 사무원에게서) 제가 입수한 정보에 따라, 저는 조지가 그날 밤, 그것도 살인이 벌어진 바로 그 시각 무렵에 그 근처를 어슬렁대는 게 목격되었고, 또 이전에도 고인과 험한 말을 주고받는 게 — 증인의 진술로는 그를 위협하기까지 한 게 — 엿들렸다는 이유로 그를 구금했습니다."], ["If you ask me, Sir Leicester Dedlock, whether from the first I believed George to be the murderer, I tell you candidly no, but he might be, notwithstanding, and there was enough against him to make it my duty to take him and get him kept under remand.", "레스터 데드록 나리, 제가 처음부터 조지를 살인자로 믿었느냐고 물으신다면, 솔직히 아니라고 말씀드리겠습니다. 허나 그럼에도 그자가 범인일 수는 있었고, 그를 잡아 구류시킬 만큼 그에게 불리한 정황이 충분했기에 그러는 게 제 의무였습니다."], ["Now, observe!”", "자, 잘 보십시오!\""]], [["As Mr. Bucket bends forward in some excitement—for him—and inaugurates what he is going to say with one ghostly beat of his forefinger in the air, Mademoiselle Hortense fixes her black eyes upon him with a dark frown and sets her dry lips closely and firmly together.", "버킷 씨가 — 그로서는 — 다소 흥분하여 몸을 앞으로 기울이며, 하려는 말의 서두를 공중에 집게손가락을 유령처럼 한 번 까딱이는 것으로 여는 동안, 오르탕스 양은 검은 눈으로 그를 어둡게 노려보며 메마른 입술을 굳게 꾹 다문다."]], [["“I went home, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, at night and found this young woman having supper with my wife, Mrs.", "\"저는 밤에 집으로 갔습니다, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리."], ["Bucket. She had made a mighty show of being fond of Mrs.", "그런데 이 처자가 제 아내 버킷 부인과 저녁을 먹고 있더군요."], ["Bucket from her first offering herself as our lodger, but that night she made more than ever—in fact, overdid it.", "이 여자는 처음 제 하숙인으로 들어오겠다고 했을 때부터 버킷 부인을 끔찍이 좋아하는 척 요란을 떨었습니다만, 그날 밤엔 그 어느 때보다 더했지요 — 실은 도가 지나쳤습니다."], ["Likewise she overdid her respect, and all that, for the lamented memory of the deceased Mr.", "마찬가지로 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨의 애도할 만한 추억에 대한 존경이며 뭐며도 도가 지나쳤고요."], ["Tulkinghorn. By the living Lord it flashed upon me, as I sat opposite to her at the table and saw her with a knife in her hand, that she had done it!”", "살아 계신 주님께 맹세코, 제가 식탁에서 그 여자 맞은편에 앉아 손에 칼을 든 그 모습을 보았을 때, 이 여자가 그 짓을 했구나 하는 생각이 번뜩 떠올랐습니다!\""]], [["Mademoiselle is hardly audible in straining through her teeth and lips the words, “You are a devil.”", "오르탕스 양이 이를 악물고 입술 사이로 짜내는 말소리는 거의 들리지 않는다. \"당신은 악마야.\""]], [["“Now where,” pursues Mr.", "\"자, 살인이 있던 그날 밤,\""], ["Bucket, “had she been on the night of the murder?", "버킷 씨가 말을 잇는다. \"이 여자는 어디 있었을까요?"], ["She had been to the theayter.", "극장에 갔다고 했습니다."], ["(She really was there, I have since found, both before the deed and after it.) I knew I had an artful customer to deal with and that proof would be very difficult; and I laid a trap for her—such a trap as I never laid yet, and such a venture as I never made yet.", "(실은 정말로 거기 있었더군요, 그 짓 전에도 후에도요, 나중에 알아냈습니다.) 저는 제가 상대해야 할 자가 약삭빠른 손님이고 증거를 잡기가 무척 어려우리란 걸 알았습니다. 그래서 저는 그 여자에게 덫을 놓았지요 — 여태껏 한 번도 놓아본 적 없는 그런 덫을, 여태껏 한 번도 해본 적 없는 그런 모험을 말입니다."], ["I worked it out in my mind while I was talking to her at supper.", "저녁을 먹으며 그 여자와 이야기하는 동안 머릿속으로 그걸 짜냈습니다."], ["When I went upstairs to bed, our house being small and this young woman’s ears sharp, I stuffed the sheet into Mrs. Bucket’s mouth that she shouldn’t say a word of surprise and told her all about it.", "잠자리에 들려고 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 저희 집이 작은 데다 이 처자의 귀가 밝은지라, 저는 버킷 부인이 놀라서 한마디도 못 내뱉도록 입에 침대보를 틀어막고는 모든 걸 털어놓았지요."], ["My dear, don’t you give your mind to that again, or I shall link your feet together at the ankles.”", "이봐, 또 그딴 생각일랑 마. 안 그러면 당신 두 발목을 한데 묶어버릴 테니.\""], ["Mr. Bucket, breaking off, has made a noiseless descent upon mademoiselle and laid his heavy hand upon her shoulder.", "말을 끊은 버킷 씨는 소리 없이 오르탕스 양에게 내려가, 묵직한 손을 그녀의 어깨에 얹은 참이다."]], [["“What is the matter with you now?”", "\"이번엔 또 뭐가 문제야?\""], ["she asks him.", "그녀가 그에게 묻는다."]], [["“Don’t you think any more,” returns Mr.", "\"창밖으로 몸을 던질 생각일랑,\""], ["Bucket with admonitory finger, “of throwing yourself out of window.", "버킷 씨가 타이르듯 손가락을 세우며 대꾸한다. \"더는 마시오."], ["That’s what’s the matter with me.", "내가 문제 삼는 건 바로 그거니까."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Just take my arm.", "그냥 내 팔을 잡으시오."], ["You needn’t get up; I’ll sit down by you.", "일어설 것 없소. 내가 당신 곁에 앉을 테니."], ["Now take my arm, will you?", "자, 내 팔을 잡으시겠소?"], ["I’m a married man, you know; you’re acquainted with my wife.", "나는 결혼한 몸이오, 알다시피. 당신은 내 아내도 알지."], ["Just take my arm.”", "그냥 내 팔을 잡으시오.\""]], [["Vainly endeavouring to moisten those dry lips, with a painful sound she struggles with herself and complies.", "그 메마른 입술을 적셔보려 부질없이 애쓰며, 고통스러운 소리와 함께 그녀는 안간힘으로 자신과 씨름하다 따른다."]], [["“Now we’re all right again.", "\"자, 이제 다시 멀쩡해졌군요."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, this case could never have been the case it is but for Mrs.", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이 사건은 버킷 부인이 아니었다면 결코 지금 같은 사건이 되지 못했을 겁니다. 그 사람은 오만 명 가운데 하나 — 아니, 십오만 명 가운데 하나뿐인 여인이지요!"], ["Bucket, who is a woman in fifty thousand—in a hundred and fifty thousand!", "이 처자가 방심하도록, 저는 그 뒤로 한 번도 저희 집에 발을 들이지 않았습니다."], ["To throw this young woman off her guard, I have never set foot in our house since, though I’ve communicated with Mrs.", "다만 빵집 빵과 우유로 필요할 때마다 버킷 부인과 연락을 주고받았지요. 침대보를 입에 문 아내에게 제가 속삭인 말은 이러했습니다."], ["Bucket in the baker’s loaves and in the milk as often as required. My whispered words to Mrs.", "'여보, 조지에 대한 내 의심이니, 이거니, 저거니, 또 다른 거니 하는 그럴듯한 이야기로 저 여자를 줄곧 방심시킬 수 있겠소?"], ["Bucket when she had the sheet in her mouth were, ‘My dear, can you throw her off continually with natural accounts of my suspicions against George, and this, and that, and t’other?", "쉬지 않고 저 여자를 밤낮으로 감시할 수 있겠소? 「저 여자는 내가 모르는 사이에는 아무것도 못 하리라, 저 여자는 자기도 모르게 내 포로가 되리라, 죽음에서 못 벗어나듯 내게서 못 벗어나리라, 저 여자가 이 살인을 저질렀다면 내가 저 여자를 붙잡을 때까지 저 여자의 목숨은 내 목숨이요 저 여자의 영혼은 내 영혼이리라」"], ["Can you do without rest and keep watch upon her night and day?", "하고 단언할 수 있겠소?'"], ["Can you undertake to say, ‘She shall do nothing without my knowledge, she shall be my prisoner without suspecting it, she shall no more escape from me than from death, and her life shall be my life, and her soul my soul, till I have got her, if she did this murder?’ Mrs.", "버킷 부인은 침대보 때문에 겨우 입을 떼며 제게 말하더군요."], ["Bucket says to me, as well as she could speak on account of the sheet, ‘Bucket, I can!’ And she has acted up to it glorious!”", "'버킷, 할 수 있어요!' 그리고 그걸 훌륭하게 해냈지요!\""]], [["“Lies!”", "\"거짓말!\""], ["mademoiselle interposes.", "오르탕스 양이 끼어든다."], ["“All lies, my friend!”", "\"다 거짓말이야, 이 친구야!\""]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, how did my calculations come out under these circumstances?", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 이런 정황에서 제 계산은 어찌 들어맞았을까요?"], ["When I calculated that this impetuous young woman would overdo it in new directions, was I wrong or right?", "이 충동적인 처자가 새로운 방향으로 도를 넘으리라 제가 계산했을 때, 제가 틀렸을까요, 옳았을까요?"], ["I was right.", "옳았습니다."], ["What does she try to do?", "이 여자가 무슨 짓을 하려 들까요?"], ["Don’t let it give you a turn?", "충격받지 마십시오."], ["To throw the murder on her ladyship.”", "그 살인을 마님께 뒤집어씌우려 했습니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester rises from his chair and staggers down again.", "레스터 경은 의자에서 몸을 일으키다 다시 비틀거리며 주저앉는다."]], [["“And she got encouragement in it from hearing that I was always here, which was done a-purpose.", "\"게다가 이 여자는 제가 늘 여기 있다는 말을 듣고 더욱 부추겨졌는데, 그건 일부러 그리한 겁니다. 자, 제 수첩을 펼쳐보십시오, 레스터 데드록 나리."], ["Now, open that pocket-book of mine, Sir Leicester Dedlock, if I may take the liberty of throwing it towards you, and look at the letters sent to me, each with the two words ‘Lady Dedlock’ in it.", "외람되이 나리께 던져드려도 된다면 말입니다. 그리고 제게 온 편지들을 보십시오, 하나같이 '데드록 마님'이라는 두 단어가 들어 있지요."], ["Open the one directed to yourself, which I stopped this very morning, and read the three words ‘Lady Dedlock, Murderess’ in it.", "바로 오늘 아침 제가 가로챈, 나리 앞으로 온 편지를 펼쳐, 거기 적힌 '데드록 마님, 살인자'라는 세 단어를 읽어보십시오."], ["These letters have been falling about like a shower of lady-birds.", "이 편지들이 무당벌레 떼처럼 우수수 쏟아졌습니다."], ["What do you say now to Mrs. Bucket, from her spy-place having seen them all written by this young woman?", "버킷 부인이 제 감시 자리에서 이 처자가 그것들을 죄다 쓰는 걸 보았다면, 이제 뭐라 하시겠습니까?"], ["What do you say to Mrs. Bucket having, within this half-hour, secured the corresponding ink and paper, fellow half-sheets and what not?", "버킷 부인이 이 반 시간 안에 똑같은 잉크와 종이, 짝이 맞는 반장짜리 종이며 뭐며를 확보했다면, 뭐라 하시겠습니까?"], ["What do you say to Mrs. Bucket having watched the posting of ’em every one by this young woman, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet?”", "버킷 부인이 이 처자가 그 하나하나를 우편으로 부치는 걸 지켜봤다면 뭐라 하시겠습니까, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리?\""], ["Mr. Bucket asks, triumphant in his admiration of his lady’s genius.", "버킷 씨가 제 아내의 천재성에 대한 감탄으로 의기양양하여 묻는다."]], [["Two things are especially observable as Mr. Bucket proceeds to a conclusion.", "버킷 씨가 결론으로 나아가는 동안 유독 눈에 띄는 것이 두 가지 있다."], ["First, that he seems imperceptibly to establish a dreadful right of property in mademoiselle.", "첫째, 그가 어느새 오르탕스 양에 대해 무시무시한 소유권을 확립해가는 듯하다는 것."], ["Secondly, that the very atmosphere she breathes seems to narrow and contract about her as if a close net or a pall were being drawn nearer and yet nearer around her breathless figure.", "둘째, 그녀가 들이쉬는 공기 자체가 마치 촘촘한 그물이나 관 덮개가 그 숨 막혀 하는 몸뚱이를 점점 더 바짝 죄어 들어오기라도 하듯, 그녀를 둘러싸고 좁혀들고 오그라드는 듯하다는 것."]], [["“There is no doubt that her ladyship was on the spot at the eventful period,” says Mr.", "\"그 결정적인 시각에 마님이 그 현장에 계셨다는 건 의심할 여지가 없습니다,\""], ["Bucket, “and my foreign friend here saw her, I believe, from the upper part of the staircase.", "버킷 씨가 말한다. \"그리고 제 짐작으론 여기 이 외국인 친구가 계단 위쪽에서 마님을 봤습니다."], ["Her ladyship and George and my foreign friend were all pretty close on one another’s heels.", "마님과 조지와 이 외국인 친구는 모두 서로 바짝 뒤꿈치를 밟다시피 했지요. 허나 그건 더는 중요하지 않으니 깊이 들어가지 않겠습니다."], ["But that don’t signify any more, so I’ll not go into it.", "저는 고인이 되신 토킹혼 씨를 쏜 권총의 메움 솜뭉치를 찾아냈습니다."], ["I found the wadding of the pistol with which the deceased Mr. Tulkinghorn was shot.", "그건 나리의 체스니 월드 저택을 인쇄로 설명한 안내문 한 조각이었지요."], ["It was a bit of the printed description of your house at Chesney Wold.", "그게 뭐 대수냐 하시겠지요, 레스터 데드록 준남작 나리."], ["Not much in that, you’ll say, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet. No.", "그렇습니다."], ["But when my foreign friend here is so thoroughly off her guard as to think it a safe time to tear up the rest of that leaf, and when Mrs. Bucket puts the pieces together and finds the wadding wanting, it begins to look like Queer Street.”", "허나 여기 이 외국인 친구가 그 종잇장의 나머지를 찢어버려도 안전한 때라고 여길 만큼 완전히 방심했을 때, 그리고 버킷 부인이 그 조각들을 맞춰보고 솜뭉치만큼이 빠졌다는 걸 알아냈을 때, 일이 꽤나 수상쩍게 돌아가기 시작하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“These are very long lies,”", "\"이거 참 긴 거짓말이로군,\""], ["mademoiselle interposes.", "오르탕스 양이 끼어든다."], ["“You prose great deal.", "\"당신 말 참 길게도 늘어놓네."], ["Is it that you have almost all finished, or are you speaking always?”", "거의 다 끝난 거야, 아니면 계속 떠들 거야?\""]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet,” proceeds Mr.", "\"레스터 데드록 준남작 나리,\""], ["Bucket, who delights in a full title and does violence to himself when he dispenses with any fragment of it, “the last point in the case which I am now going to mention shows the necessity of patience in our business, and never doing a thing in a hurry.", "버킷 씨가 말을 잇는데, 그는 온전한 칭호를 즐기는지라 그 한 조각이라도 생략할 때면 스스로를 억지로 다잡는다. \"이제 말씀드릴 이 사건의 마지막 대목은, 우리 일에는 인내가 필요하며 결코 무슨 일이든 서둘러 하지 말아야 한다는 걸 보여줍니다."], ["I watched this young woman yesterday without her knowledge when she was looking at the funeral, in company with my wife, who planned to take her there; and I had so much to convict her, and I saw such an expression in her face, and my mind so rose against her malice towards her ladyship, and the time was altogether such a time for bringing down what you may call retribution upon her, that if I had been a younger hand with less experience, I should have taken her, certain.", "저는 어제 이 처자가 모르게 그녀를 지켜봤는데, 그때 그녀는 제 아내와 함께 장례식을 구경하고 있었지요. 제 아내가 그녀를 거기 데려가려고 꾸민 일이었습니다."], ["Equally, last night, when her ladyship, as is so universally admired I am sure, come home looking—why, Lord, a man might almost say like Venus rising from the ocean—it was so unpleasant and inconsistent to think of her being charged with a murder of which she was innocent that I felt quite to want to put an end to the job.", "저는 그녀를 옭아맬 거리가 어찌나 많았던지, 그녀 얼굴에 어린 그런 표정을 보았던지, 마님에 대한 그녀의 악의에 제 마음이 어찌나 곤두섰던지, 그리고 때가 그야말로 그녀에게 이른바 응보를 내릴 만한 그런 때였던지라, 제가 경험 적은 풋내기였다면 틀림없이 그녀를 잡아들였을 겁니다. 마찬가지로, 어젯밤 마님이 — 분명 만인의 흠모를 받는 분이지요 — 집에 돌아오시는데 그 모습이 — 아니, 이런, 거의 바다에서 솟아오르는 비너스 같다고 해도 좋을 만큼이라 — 죄 없는 분이 살인 누명을 쓴다고 생각하니 어찌나 불쾌하고 앞뒤가 안 맞던지, 저는 이 일을 당장 끝내버리고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았습니다."], ["What should I have lost?", "그랬다면 제가 무엇을 잃었을까요?"], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I should have lost the weapon.", "레스터 데드록 준남작 나리, 저는 흉기를 잃었을 겁니다."], ["My prisoner here proposed to Mrs. Bucket, after the departure of the funeral, that they should go per bus a little ways into the country and take tea at a very decent house of entertainment.", "여기 제 죄수는 장례 행렬이 떠난 뒤 버킷 부인에게, 함께 합승마차로 시골에 좀 나가 아주 점잖은 요릿집에서 차를 마시자고 청했습니다."], ["Now, near that house of entertainment there’s a piece of water.", "그런데 그 요릿집 근처에 물웅덩이가 하나 있지요."], ["At tea, my prisoner got up to fetch her pocket-handkercher from the bedroom where the bonnets was; she was rather a long time gone and came back a little out of wind.", "차를 마시다가 제 죄수는 보닛을 벗어둔 침실로 손수건을 가지러 일어났습니다. 그녀는 제법 오래 자리를 비웠다가 다소 숨이 가빠진 채 돌아왔지요."], ["As soon as they came home this was reported to me by Mrs. Bucket, along with her observations and suspicions.", "집에 돌아오자마자 버킷 부인이 자기가 관찰한 바와 의심을 곁들여 이걸 제게 보고했습니다."], ["I had the piece of water dragged by moonlight, in presence of a couple of our men, and the pocket pistol was brought up before it had been there half-a-dozen hours.", "저는 우리 사람 둘을 입회시키고 달빛 아래 그 물웅덩이를 훑게 했는데, 그게 거기 잠긴 지 여섯 시간도 안 되어 그 호신용 권총이 건져 올려졌습니다."], ["Now, my dear, put your arm a little further through mine, and hold it steady, and I shan’t hurt you!”", "자, 이봐, 당신 팔을 내 팔에 좀 더 깊이 끼고, 단단히 붙들고 있으시오. 그럼 다치게 하지 않을 테니!\""]], [["In a trice Mr. Bucket snaps a handcuff on her wrist.", "순식간에 버킷 씨는 그녀의 손목에 수갑을 철컥 채운다."], ["“That’s one,”", "\"이게 하나,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Now the other, darling.", "\"이제 나머지 하나요, 아가씨."], ["Two, and all told!”", "둘, 이로써 끝!\""]], [["He rises; she rises too.", "그가 일어선다. 그녀도 함께 일어선다."], ["“Where,”", "\"당신의,\""], ["she asks him, darkening her large eyes until their drooping lids almost conceal them—and yet they stare, “where is your false, your treacherous, and cursed wife?”", "그녀가 그에게 묻는데, 큰 두 눈이 어찌나 어두워지는지 처진 눈꺼풀이 그 눈을 거의 가릴 지경이면서도 — 그러면서도 그 눈은 노려본다. \"당신의 거짓되고, 배신을 일삼는, 저주받을 마누라는 어디 있지?\""]], [["“She’s gone forrard to the Police Office,”", "\"먼저 경찰서로 갔소,\""], ["returns Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 대답한다."], ["“You’ll see her there, my dear.”", "\"거기서 그 사람을 보게 될 거요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“I would like to kiss her!”", "\"그 여자한테 입을 맞춰주고 싶어!\""], ["exclaims Mademoiselle Hortense, panting tigress-like.", "오르탕스 양이 호랑이처럼 헐떡이며 외친다."]], [["“You’d bite her, I suspect,”", "\"물어뜯기야 하겠지, 내 짐작엔,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“I would!”", "\"그래!\""], ["making her eyes very large.", "두 눈을 한껏 크게 뜨며."], ["“I would love to tear her limb from limb.”", "\"사지를 갈가리 찢어놓고 싶어 죽겠다고.\""]], [["“Bless you, darling,” says Mr.", "\"복 받으시오, 아가씨,\""], ["Bucket with the greatest composure, “I’m fully prepared to hear that.", "버킷 씨가 더없이 침착하게 말한다. \"그런 말쯤은 들을 각오가 되어 있소."], ["Your sex have such a surprising animosity against one another when you do differ.", "당신네 성은 일단 사이가 틀어지면 서로에게 놀랄 만큼 적개심을 품으니 말이오."], ["You don’t mind me half so much, do you?”", "나한테는 그 절반만큼도 신경 안 쓰지, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"안 써."], ["Though you are a devil still.”", "그래도 당신은 여전히 악마야.\""]], [["“Angel and devil by turns, eh?”", "\"천사였다 악마였다, 그런 거요?\""], ["cries Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 외친다."], ["“But I am in my regular employment, you must consider.", "\"허나 나는 제 본분에 따라 일하는 중이란 걸 헤아려주시오."], ["Let me put your shawl tidy.", "숄을 단정히 매만져드리리다."], ["I’ve been lady’s maid to a good many before now.", "나는 전에도 여러 사람의 시중을 들어줬소."], ["Anything wanting to the bonnet?", "보닛엔 더 손볼 데 없소?"], ["There’s a cab at the door.”", "문 앞에 마차가 와 있소.\""]], [["Mademoiselle Hortense, casting an indignant eye at the glass, shakes herself perfectly neat in one shake and looks, to do her justice, uncommonly genteel.", "오르탕스 양은 거울에 분개한 눈길을 던지더니, 단번에 몸을 흔들어 완벽하게 매무새를 가다듬는데, 공평하게 말하자면 유달리 기품 있어 보인다."]], [["“Listen then, my angel,”", "\"그럼 들어봐, 내 천사님,\""], ["says she after several sarcastic nods.", "그녀가 비아냥대듯 여러 번 고개를 끄덕인 뒤 말한다."], ["“You are very spiritual.", "\"당신 참 영리해."], ["But can you restore him back to life?”", "그런데 그 사람을 도로 살려낼 수 있어?\""]], [["Mr.", "버킷 씨가 대답한다."], ["Bucket answers, “Not exactly.”", "\"딱히 그렇진 못하지.\""]], [["“That is droll.", "\"그것참 우습네."], ["Listen yet one time.", "한 번만 더 들어봐."], ["You are very spiritual.", "당신 참 영리해."], ["Can you make a honourable lady of her?”", "저 여자를 명예로운 마님으로 만들 수 있어?\""]], [["“Don’t be so malicious,”", "\"그렇게 심술부리지 마시오,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Or a haughty gentleman of HIM?”", "\"아니면 저 양반을 도도한 신사로 만들든가?\""], ["cries mademoiselle, referring to Sir Leicester with ineffable disdain.", "오르탕스 양이 형언할 수 없는 경멸을 담아 레스터 경을 가리키며 외친다."], ["“Eh!", "\"흥!"], ["Oh, then regard him!", "오, 저 꼴 좀 봐!"], ["The poor infant!", "가엾은 갓난애 같으니!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Ha!”", "하!\""]], [["“Come, come, why this is worse PARLAYING than the other,”", "\"이런, 이런, 이건 아까보다 더 고약한 떠벌림이로군,\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“Come along!”", "\"자, 갑시다!\""]], [["“You cannot do these things?", "\"당신은 그런 일들을 못 하잖아?"], ["Then you can do as you please with me.", "그럼 나야 당신 마음대로 해."], ["It is but the death, it is all the same.", "기껏해야 죽음일 뿐이고, 다 매한가지지."], ["Let us go, my angel.", "가자고, 내 천사님."], ["Adieu, you old man, grey.", "잘 있으시오, 늙고 머리 센 양반."], ["I pity you, and I despise you!”", "당신이 가엾고, 또 경멸스러워!\""]], [["With these last words she snaps her teeth together as if her mouth closed with a spring.", "이 마지막 말과 함께 그녀는 입이 용수철로 닫히기라도 하듯 이를 딱 맞물린다."], ["It is impossible to describe how Mr. Bucket gets her out, but he accomplishes that feat in a manner so peculiar to himself, enfolding and pervading her like a cloud, and hovering away with her as if he were a homely Jupiter and she the object of his affections.", "버킷 씨가 어떻게 그녀를 데리고 나가는지는 도무지 형언할 수 없으나, 그는 그 일을 제 나름으로 무척이나 독특한 방식으로 해내니, 구름처럼 그녀를 감싸고 에워싼 채, 마치 그가 수수한 유피테르이고 그녀가 그 사랑의 대상이기라도 한 양 그녀와 더불어 두둥실 떠나간다."]], [["Sir Leicester, left alone, remains in the same attitude, as though he were still listening and his attention were still occupied.", "홀로 남은 레스터 경은, 여전히 귀 기울이고 있는 듯, 여전히 정신을 쏟고 있는 듯, 같은 자세로 그대로 있다."], ["At length he gazes round the empty room, and finding it deserted, rises unsteadily to his feet, pushes back his chair, and walks a few steps, supporting himself by the table.", "이윽고 그는 텅 빈 방을 둘러보고, 아무도 없다는 걸 알자 비틀비틀 일어나 의자를 뒤로 밀고, 탁자에 몸을 의지한 채 몇 걸음 걷는다."], ["Then he stops, and with more of those inarticulate sounds, lifts up his eyes and seems to stare at something.", "그러다 멈춰 서서, 그 알아들을 수 없는 소리를 더 내며, 눈을 들어 무언가를 응시하는 듯하다."]], [["Heaven knows what he sees.", "그가 무엇을 보는지는 하늘만이 안다."], ["The green, green woods of Chesney Wold, the noble house, the pictures of his forefathers, strangers defacing them, officers of police coarsely handling his most precious heirlooms, thousands of fingers pointing at him, thousands of faces sneering at him.", "체스니 월드의 푸르디푸른 숲, 그 고귀한 저택, 조상들의 초상화, 그것을 훼손하는 낯선 자들, 그의 가장 소중한 가보를 거칠게 다루는 경찰관들, 그를 가리키는 수천 개의 손가락, 그를 비웃는 수천 개의 얼굴."], ["But if such shadows flit before him to his bewilderment, there is one other shadow which he can name with something like distinctness even yet and to which alone he addresses his tearing of his white hair and his extended arms.", "허나 그런 그림자들이 그의 눈앞을 어지러이 스치며 그를 혼란에 빠뜨릴지언정, 그가 지금도 어느 정도 또렷하게 이름 부를 수 있는, 그리하여 오직 그 하나에게만 백발을 쥐어뜯고 두 팔을 뻗어 호소하는 또 다른 그림자가 하나 있다."]], [["It is she in association with whom, saving that she has been for years a main fibre of the root of his dignity and pride, he has never had a selfish thought.", "그것은 바로, 그녀가 여러 해 동안 그의 위엄과 자부심이라는 뿌리의 중심 줄기였다는 점을 빼면, 그가 더불어 단 한 번도 이기적인 생각을 품어본 적 없는 그 사람이다."], ["It is she whom he has loved, admired, honoured, and set up for the world to respect.", "그것은 바로 그가 사랑하고, 흠모하고, 존중하고, 세상이 우러르도록 떠받들어온 그 사람이다."], ["It is she who, at the core of all the constrained formalities and conventionalities of his life, has been a stock of living tenderness and love, susceptible as nothing else is of being struck with the agony he feels.", "그것은 바로 그의 삶을 옭아맨 그 모든 격식과 인습의 핵심에서, 살아 숨 쉬는 다정함과 사랑의 원천이 되어준, 다른 무엇도 그러지 못하리만치 지금 그가 느끼는 고통에 함께 찔릴 줄 아는 그 사람이다."], ["He sees her, almost to the exclusion of himself, and cannot bear to look upon her cast down from the high place she has graced so well.", "그는 거의 자기 자신은 잊은 채 그녀를 보고 있으며, 그녀가 그토록 잘 빛내온 그 높은 자리에서 굴러떨어지는 모습을 차마 바라보지 못한다."]], [["And even to the point of his sinking on the ground, oblivious of his suffering, he can yet pronounce her name with something like distinctness in the midst of those intrusive sounds, and in a tone of mourning and compassion rather than reproach.", "그리하여 그가 제 고통도 잊은 채 바닥에 쓰러지는 그 순간까지도, 그는 그 비집고 드는 소리들 한가운데서 어느 정도 또렷하게 그녀의 이름을 부를 수 있으니, 그것도 책망이 아니라 차라리 애도와 연민의 어조로."]], [["Flight\nInspector Bucket of the Detective has not yet struck his great blow, as just now chronicled, but is yet refreshing himself with sleep preparatory to his field-day, when through the night and along the freezing wintry roads a chaise and pair comes out of Lincolnshire, making its way towards London.", "수사반 버킷 형사는 방금 기록한 대로 아직 큰 한 방을 날리지 않았고, 결전의 날을 앞두고 잠으로 원기를 보충하는 중이다. 그러나 밤을 가로질러, 얼어붙은 겨울 길을 따라, 이두마차 한 대가 링컨셔에서 빠져나와 런던 쪽으로 길을 잡는다."]], [["Railroads shall soon traverse all this country, and with a rattle and a glare the engine and train shall shoot like a meteor over the wide night-landscape, turning the moon paler; but as yet such things are non-existent in these parts, though not wholly unexpected.", "머지않아 철도가 이 온 시골을 가로지를 것이고, 덜컹거리는 소리와 번쩍이는 불빛 속에 기관차와 객차가 너른 밤 풍경 위로 유성처럼 내달리며 달빛마저 창백하게 만들 것이다. 그러나 아직은 이 일대에 그런 것이 존재하지 않으니, 그렇다고 전혀 예상하지 못할 일도 아니다."], ["Preparations are afoot, measurements are made, ground is staked out.", "준비는 진행되고, 측량은 끝났으며, 땅에는 말뚝이 박힌다. 다리는 짓기 시작했고, 아직 이어지지 않은 교각들은 길과 개천 너머로 서로를 황량하게 바라보는데, 마치 결합을 가로막는 장애물을 사이에 둔 벽돌과 회반죽의 한 쌍 같다."], ["Bridges are begun, and their not yet united piers desolately look at one another over roads and streams like brick and mortar couples with an obstacle to their union; fragments of embankments are thrown up and left as precipices with torrents of rusty carts and barrows tumbling over them; tripods of tall poles appear on hilltops, where there are rumours of tunnels; everything looks chaotic and abandoned in full hopelessness.", "둑은 조각조각 쌓다가 그대로 버려져 절벽이 되었고, 그 위로 녹슨 손수레와 외바퀴차가 우르르 굴러떨어진다. 굴이 뚫린다는 소문이 도는 언덕 꼭대기마다 키 큰 장대로 세운 삼각대가 솟아 있다."], ["Along the freezing roads, and through the night, the post-chaise makes its way without a railroad on its mind.", "모든 것이 혼란스럽고, 완전히 희망 없이 버려진 듯 보인다. 얼어붙은 길을 따라, 밤을 가로질러, 역마차는 철도 따위는 안중에도 없이 길을 잡는다."]], [["Mrs. Rouncewell, so many years housekeeper at Chesney Wold, sits within the chaise; and by her side sits Mrs.", "여러 해 동안 체스니 월드의 가정부로 지낸 라운스웰 부인이 마차 안에 앉아 있고, 그 곁에는 회색 망토에 우산을 챙긴 배그넷 부인이 앉아 있다."], ["Bagnet with her grey cloak and umbrella. The old girl would prefer the bar in front, as being exposed to the weather and a primitive sort of perch more in accordance with her usual course of travelling, but Mrs.", "늙은 여인은 날씨에 그대로 드러나는 데다 평소 여행 방식에 더 어울리는 원시적인 횃대 같은, 앞쪽 좌석을 더 좋아할 테지만, 라운스웰 부인이 그녀의 편안함을 어찌나 헤아리는지 그 제안을 입에 올릴 틈조차 주지 않는다."], ["Rouncewell is too thoughtful of her comfort to admit of her proposing it.", "늙은 마님은 늙은 여인을 어떻게 대접해야 할지 모를 정도다."], ["The old lady cannot make enough of the old girl. She sits, in her stately manner, holding her hand, and regardless of its roughness, puts it often to her lips.", "그녀는 위엄 있는 몸가짐으로 앉아 상대의 손을 잡고, 그 거친 살결도 아랑곳하지 않은 채 자주 입술에 가져다 댄다."], ["“You are a mother, my dear soul,”", "\"당신은 어머니예요, 고마운 분.\""], ["says she many times, “and you found out my George’s mother!”", "그녀가 몇 번이고 말한다. \"그런 당신이 우리 조지의 어머니를 찾아주었어요!\""]], [["“Why, George,”", "\"아, 조지는,\""], ["returns Mrs.", "배그넷 부인이 대꾸한다."], ["Bagnet, “was always free with me, ma’am, and when he said at our house to my Woolwich that of all the things my Woolwich could have to think of when he grew to be a man, the comfortablest would be that he had never brought a sorrowful line into his mother’s face or turned a hair of her head grey, then I felt sure, from his way, that something fresh had brought his own mother into his mind.", "\"저한테 늘 스스럼없었답니다, 부인. 그이가 우리 집에서 우리 울위치한테, 어른이 되어 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것 중에 제일 마음 편한 건 어머니 얼굴에 시름의 주름 한 줄 새긴 적 없고 머리카락 한 올 세게 만든 적 없다는 거라고 했을 때, 저는 그 말투에서 뭔가가 새삼 그이 어머니를 머릿속에 떠올리게 했구나 하고 확신했지요."], ["I had often known him say to me, in past times, that he had behaved bad to her.”", "지난날에도 그이가 어머니께 못되게 굴었다고 저한테 종종 말하곤 했거든요.\""]], [["“Never, my dear!” returns Mrs.", "\"그런 적 없어요, 부인!\""], ["Rouncewell, bursting into tears.", "라운스웰 부인이 눈물을 터뜨리며 대꾸한다."], ["“My blessing on him, never!", "\"그 애한테 복이 있기를, 절대 그런 적 없어요!"], ["He was always fond of me, and loving to me, was my George!", "그 애는 늘 나를 좋아했고, 다정했어요, 우리 조지는!"], ["But he had a bold spirit, and he ran a little wild and went for a soldier.", "다만 기개가 당찬 애라서, 조금 거칠게 굴다가 군인이 되겠다고 떠난 거예요. 그 애가 처음엔 자기 소식을 알리는 걸 미뤘던 거, 다 알아요."], ["And I know he waited at first, in letting us know about himself, till he should rise to be an officer; and when he didn’t rise, I know he considered himself beneath us, and wouldn’t be a disgrace to us.", "장교로 올라설 때까지 기다린 거지요. 그러다 올라서지 못하니까 자기가 우리 밑이라고 여기고, 우리한테 수치가 되지 않으려 했던 거예요."], ["For he had a lion heart, had my George, always from a baby!”", "우리 조지는 사자의 심장을 가진 애였거든요, 갓난아기 적부터 늘!\""]], [["The old lady’s hands stray about her as of yore, while she recalls, all in a tremble, what a likely lad, what a fine lad, what a gay good-humoured clever lad he was; how they all took to him down at Chesney Wold; how Sir Leicester took to him when he was a young gentleman; how the dogs took to him; how even the people who had been angry with him forgave him the moment he was gone, poor boy.", "늙은 마님의 두 손은 예전처럼 정처 없이 떠돌고, 그러는 사이 그녀는 온몸을 떨며 회상한다. 그 애가 얼마나 싹수 있는 녀석이었는지, 얼마나 훤칠한 녀석이었는지, 얼마나 명랑하고 너그럽고 영리한 녀석이었는지를."], ["And now to see him after all, and in a prison too!", "체스니 월드 사람들이 죄다 그 애를 얼마나 따랐는지, 레스터 경이 젊은 신사이던 그 애를 얼마나 아꼈는지, 개들까지 그 애를 얼마나 따랐는지, 그 애한테 화내던 사람들조차 그 애가 떠나자마자 어찌 그리 용서했는지, 가엾은 녀석."], ["And the broad stomacher heaves, and the quaint upright old-fashioned figure bends under its load of affectionate distress.", "그런데 이제 와서, 그것도 감옥에서 그 애를 보게 되다니! 넓은 가슴받이가 들썩이고, 기묘하게 꼿꼿하고 고풍스러운 자태가 애정 어린 비탄의 무게에 짓눌려 휜다."]], [["Mrs.", "배그넷 부인은 따뜻하고 선한 마음에서 우러난 본능적인 솜씨로, 늙은 가정부를 잠시 그 감정에 내버려둔다. 손등으로 제 어미다운 두 눈을 슬쩍 훔치는 일은 빠뜨리지 않으면서."], ["Bagnet, with the instinctive skill of a good warm heart, leaves the old housekeeper to her emotions for a little while—not without passing the back of her hand across her own motherly eyes—and presently chirps up in her cheery manner, “So I says to George when I goes to call him in to tea (he pretended to be smoking his pipe outside), ‘What ails you this afternoon, George, for gracious sake?", "이윽고 그녀는 명랑한 투로 새가 지저귀듯 이야기를 꺼낸다. \"그래서 차 마시러 들어오라고 조지를 부르러 갔을 때(그이는 밖에서 담배 피우는 척하고 있었지요), 제가 그랬어요."], ["I have seen all sorts, and I have seen you pretty often in season and out of season, abroad and at home, and I never see you so melancholy penitent.’ ‘Why, Mrs.", "'대체 오늘 오후엔 무슨 일이오, 조지, 맙소사? 별의별 사람을 다 봤고, 당신도 좋을 때 궂을 때, 외국에서 집에서 꽤 자주 봤지만, 이렇게 시름에 잠겨 뉘우치는 꼴은 처음 보네.'"], ["Bagnet,’ says George, ‘it’s because I AM melancholy and penitent both, this afternoon, that you see me so.’ ‘What have you done, old fellow?’ I says.", "'아, 배그넷 부인,' 조지가 그래요."], ["‘Why, Mrs.", "'오늘 오후엔 정말로 시름에 잠겨 뉘우치고 있어서 그렇게 보이는 거랍니다.' '대체 뭘 했길래 그러오, 이 양반아?'"], ["Bagnet,’ says George, shaking his head, ‘what I have done has been done this many a long year, and is best not tried to be undone now.", "제가 물었지요. '아, 배그넷 부인,'"], ["If I ever get to heaven it won’t be for being a good son to a widowed mother; I say no more.’ Now, ma’am, when George says to me that it’s best not tried to be undone now, I have my thoughts as I have often had before, and I draw it out of George how he comes to have such things on him that afternoon.", "조지가 고개를 흔들며 그래요. '내가 한 일은 오래전, 아주 오래전에 한 일이라, 이제 와서 되돌리려 해도 안 되는 게 상책입니다."], ["Then George tells me that he has seen by chance, at the lawyer’s office, a fine old lady that has brought his mother plain before him, and he runs on about that old lady till he quite forgets himself and paints her picture to me as she used to be, years upon years back.", "내가 천국에 가게 된다면, 그건 홀어머니께 효도한 덕분은 아닐 거예요. 더는 말 안 하렵니다.'"], ["So I says to George when he has done, who is this old lady he has seen?", "자, 부인, 조지가 이제 와서 되돌리려 해도 안 되는 게 상책이라고 하니, 저는 전부터 자주 그랬듯이 짚이는 데가 있어서, 대체 그날 오후에 무슨 일로 그런 생각에 잠기게 됐는지 캐물었지요. 그러자 조지가, 변호사 사무실에서 우연히 어떤 점잖은 노부인을 봤는데 그분이 자기 어머니를 눈앞에 또렷이 떠올리게 했다지 뭐예요."], ["And George tells me it’s Mrs.", "그러더니 그 노부인 얘기를 줄줄이 늘어놓다가 완전히 넋이 나가서, 수십 년 전 그분 모습까지 저한테 그려 보이더라고요. 그래서 다 말하고 나니까 제가 물었지요, 본 노부인이 누구냐고."], ["Rouncewell, housekeeper for more than half a century to the Dedlock family down at Chesney Wold in Lincolnshire.", "조지가 그러는데, 링컨셔 체스니 월드의 데드록 가문에서 반세기 넘게 가정부로 지낸 라운스웰 부인이라잖아요. 조지가 전에도 자기는 링컨셔 사람이라고 자주 말했거든요."], ["George has frequently told me before that he’s a Lincolnshire man, and I says to my old Lignum that night, ‘Lignum, that’s his mother for five and for-ty pound!’”", "그래서 제가 그날 밤 우리 리그넘 영감한테 그랬어요. '리그넘, 사십오 파운드를 걸고서라도 저게 그이 어머니야!'\""]], [["All this Mrs. Bagnet now relates for the twentieth time at least within the last four hours.", "이 모든 이야기를 배그넷 부인은 이제 지난 네 시간 동안 적어도 스무 번째로 풀어놓는 중이다."], ["Trilling it out like a kind of bird, with a pretty high note, that it may be audible to the old lady above the hum of the wheels.", "일종의 새처럼 꽤 높은 음으로 곱게 지저귀어, 바퀴 굴러가는 소리 너머로도 늙은 마님이 들을 수 있도록."]], [["“Bless you, and thank you,”", "\"고마워요, 정말 고마워요.\""], ["says Mrs. Rouncewell.", "라운스웰 부인이 말한다."], ["“Bless you, and thank you, my worthy soul!”", "\"고맙고 또 고마워요, 갸륵한 분!\""]], [["“Dear heart!”", "\"어머나!\""], ["cries Mrs. Bagnet in the most natural manner.", "배그넷 부인이 더없이 자연스러운 투로 외친다."], ["“No thanks to me, I am sure.", "\"저한테 고마워하실 거 없어요, 정말로."], ["Thanks to yourself, ma’am, for being so ready to pay ’em!", "부인 자신한테 고마워하셔야지요, 이리 선뜻 고마워할 줄 아시니!"], ["And mind once more, ma’am, what you had best do on finding George to be your own son is to make him—for your sake—have every sort of help to put himself in the right and clear himself of a charge of which he is as innocent as you or me.", "그리고 부인, 다시 한 번 명심하세요. 조지가 부인의 친아들로 밝혀진 마당에 부인이 하실 일은, 부인을 봐서라도 그이가 온갖 도움을 다 받아 떳떳이 처신하고, 부인이나 저처럼 결백한데도 뒤집어쓴 혐의를 벗게 하는 거예요."], ["It won’t do to have truth and justice on his side; he must have law and lawyers,”", "진실과 정의가 그이 편이라고 다 되는 게 아니거든요. 법과 변호사가 있어야 해요.\""], ["exclaims the old girl, apparently persuaded that the latter form a separate establishment and have dissolved partnership with truth and justice for ever and a day.", "늙은 여인이 외친다. 보아하니 그녀는 법과 변호사라는 게 진실, 정의와는 별개의 살림이라, 그 둘과 영원토록 손을 끊었다고 믿는 모양이다."]], [["“He shall have,” says Mrs.", "\"그 애한테는,\""], ["Rouncewell, “all the help that can be got for him in the world, my dear.", "라운스웰 부인이 말한다. \"세상에서 구할 수 있는 도움을 죄다 받게 해줄 거예요, 부인."], ["I will spend all I have, and thankfully, to procure it.", "가진 걸 다 쓰겠어요, 기꺼이, 그걸 마련하느라."], ["Sir Leicester will do his best, the whole family will do their best.", "레스터 경께서도 최선을 다하실 테고, 온 집안이 최선을 다할 거예요. 나는, 나는 뭔가 좀 아는 게 있어요, 부인."], ["I—I know something, my dear; and will make my own appeal, as his mother parted from him all these years, and finding him in a jail at last.”", "그래서 나도 나대로 호소할 거예요. 그 애 어미로서, 이 긴 세월 그 애와 헤어져 지내다 끝내 감옥에서 그 애를 찾았으니까요.\""]], [["The extreme disquietude of the old housekeeper’s manner in saying this, her broken words, and her wringing of her hands make a powerful impression on Mrs. Bagnet and would astonish her but that she refers them all to her sorrow for her son’s condition. And yet Mrs. Bagnet wonders too why Mrs. Rouncewell should murmur so distractedly, “My Lady, my Lady, my Lady!” over and over again.", "이 말을 하면서 늙은 가정부가 보이는 극심한 동요, 끊어지는 말투, 두 손을 비트는 모습은 배그넷 부인에게 강한 인상을 남기고, 만일 그 모두를 아들 처지를 슬퍼한 탓으로 돌리지 않았다면 그녀를 깜짝 놀라게 했을 것이다. 그래도 배그넷 부인은 라운스웰 부인이 왜 저리 넋 나간 듯 \"마님, 마님, 마님!\"을 거듭 중얼거리는지 의아하긴 하다."]], [["The frosty night wears away, and the dawn breaks, and the post-chaise comes rolling on through the early mist like the ghost of a chaise departed.", "서리 내린 밤이 저물어가고 동이 트며, 역마차는 이른 안개를 헤치고 굴러간다. 마치 떠나간 마차의 유령처럼."], ["It has plenty of spectral company in ghosts of trees and hedges, slowly vanishing and giving place to the realities of day.", "그 곁에는 나무와 산울타리의 유령이 잔뜩 유령 같은 동행이 되어, 서서히 사라지며 한낮의 실체에 자리를 내준다. 런던에 닿자 길손들이 내린다."], ["London reached, the travellers alight, the old housekeeper in great tribulation and confusion, Mrs.", "늙은 가정부는 큰 시름과 혼란에 잠겨 있고, 배그넷 부인은 더없이 말끔하고 침착하다."], ["Bagnet quite fresh and collected—as she would be if her next point, with no new equipage and outfit, were the Cape of Good Hope, the Island of Ascension, Hong Kong, or any other military station.", "다음 행선지가 새 마차도 행장도 없이 곧장 희망봉이든 어센션 섬이든 홍콩이든, 그 밖의 어느 주둔지든 마찬가지였을 그런 태도다."]], [["But when they set out for the prison where the trooper is confined, the old lady has managed to draw about her, with her lavender-coloured dress, much of the staid calmness which is its usual accompaniment.", "그러나 기병이 갇힌 감옥으로 길을 나설 무렵, 늙은 마님은 라벤더빛 드레스와 더불어 으레 거기 따라붙는 차분한 평정을 한껏 끌어모은 참이다."], ["A wonderfully grave, precise, and handsome piece of old china she looks, though her heart beats fast and her stomacher is ruffled more than even the remembrance of this wayward son has ruffled it these many years.", "놀랍도록 진중하고 단정하고 우아한 옛 도자기처럼 보이지만, 정작 가슴은 빠르게 뛰고 가슴받이는 이 변덕쟁이 아들에 대한 기억이 지난 숱한 세월 헝클어뜨린 것보다도 더 헝클어져 있다."]], [["Approaching the cell, they find the door opening and a warder in the act of coming out.", "감방에 다가가니 문이 열리고 간수 하나가 막 나오는 참이다."], ["The old girl promptly makes a sign of entreaty to him to say nothing; assenting with a nod, he suffers them to enter as he shuts the door.", "늙은 여인이 얼른 아무 말 말라고 간청하는 손짓을 보낸다. 간수는 고개를 끄덕여 응하고는 문을 닫으며 둘을 들여보낸다."]], [["So George, who is writing at his table, supposing himself to be alone, does not raise his eyes, but remains absorbed.", "그래서 자기 혼자뿐인 줄 알고 책상에서 글을 쓰던 조지는 눈을 들지 않고 골똘히 빠져 있다."], ["The old housekeeper looks at him, and those wandering hands of hers are quite enough for Mrs.", "늙은 가정부가 그를 바라보는데, 정처 없이 떠도는 그 두 손만으로도 배그넷 부인의 확신은 충분하다."], ["Bagnet’s confirmation, even if she could see the mother and the son together, knowing what she knows, and doubt their relationship.", "설령 아는 바를 알면서 어미와 아들을 나란히 두고 본다 한들 둘의 핏줄을 의심할 수 있을까마는."]], [["Not a rustle of the housekeeper’s dress, not a gesture, not a word betrays her.", "가정부의 옷자락이 바스락거리는 소리도, 몸짓 하나도, 말 한마디도 그녀를 드러내지 않는다."], ["She stands looking at him as he writes on, all unconscious, and only her fluttering hands give utterance to her emotions.", "그녀는 아무것도 모른 채 글을 써내려가는 그를 그저 바라보고 서 있고, 오직 파닥거리는 두 손만이 감정을 토로한다. 그러나 그 손은 무척이나 웅변적이다, 무척이나, 무척이나 웅변적이다."], ["But they are very eloquent, very, very eloquent.", "배그넷 부인은 그 손을 알아듣는다."], ["Mrs. Bagnet understands them.", "그 손은 감사를, 기쁨을, 비탄을, 희망을 말한다."], ["They speak of gratitude, of joy, of grief, of hope; of inextinguishable affection, cherished with no return since this stalwart man was a stripling; of a better son loved less, and this son loved so fondly and so proudly; and they speak in such touching language that Mrs.", "이 건장한 사내가 풋내기였던 적부터 보답 한 번 받지 못한 채 품어온, 결코 꺼지지 않는 애정을 말한다. 덜 사랑한 더 나은 아들과, 그토록 애틋하고 그토록 자랑스럽게 사랑한 이 아들을 말한다."], ["Bagnet’s eyes brim up with tears and they run glistening down her sun-brown face.", "그 손은 어찌나 가슴 저미게 말하는지, 배그넷 부인의 두 눈에 눈물이 차올라 볕에 그을린 갈색 얼굴을 타고 반짝이며 흘러내린다."]], [["“George Rouncewell!", "\"조지 라운스웰!"], ["Oh, my dear child, turn and look at me!”", "오, 내 사랑하는 아들아, 돌아서서 나를 봐다오!\""]], [["The trooper starts up, clasps his mother round the neck, and falls down on his knees before her.", "기병이 펄쩍 일어나 어머니의 목을 끌어안고는 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇는다."], ["Whether in a late repentance, whether in the first association that comes back upon him, he puts his hands together as a child does when it says its prayers, and raising them towards her breast, bows down his head, and cries.", "뒤늦은 뉘우침인지, 그에게 되살아난 첫 기억의 끌림인지, 그는 아이가 기도할 때처럼 두 손을 모으고 그것을 어머니의 가슴 쪽으로 들어 올린 채 고개를 숙이고 운다."]], [["“My George, my dearest son!", "\"우리 조지, 내 가장 소중한 아들!"], ["Always my favourite, and my favourite still, where have you been these cruel years and years?", "늘 내 귀염둥이였고 지금도 여전히 내 귀염둥이인데, 이 모진 세월 동안 어디 있었니?"], ["Grown such a man too, grown such a fine strong man.", "이렇게 어른이 되어서, 이렇게 훤칠하고 늠름한 사내가 되어서."], ["Grown so like what I knew he must be, if it pleased God he was alive!”", "하느님 뜻으로 살아만 있다면 분명 이리 됐으리라고 내가 알던 그 모습 그대로 자랐구나!\""]], [["She can ask, and he can answer, nothing connected for a time.", "한동안은 그녀도 무엇 하나 조리 있게 묻지 못하고, 그도 무엇 하나 조리 있게 답하지 못한다."], ["All that time the old girl, turned away, leans one arm against the whitened wall, leans her honest forehead upon it, wipes her eyes with her serviceable grey cloak, and quite enjoys herself like the best of old girls as she is.", "그동안 내내 늙은 여인은 돌아서서 한쪽 팔을 희게 칠한 벽에 기대고, 그 위에 정직한 이마를 묻고는, 요긴한 회색 망토로 눈물을 훔치며, 더없이 좋은 늙은 여인답게 한껏 그 순간을 즐긴다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\" 둘이 좀 더 진정했을 때 기병이 말한다."], ["says the trooper when they are more composed, “forgive me first of all, for I know my need of it.”", "\"무엇보다 먼저 저를 용서해주세요. 제게 그게 필요한 줄 아니까요.\""]], [["Forgive him!", "용서라니!"], ["She does it with all her heart and soul.", "그녀는 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 용서한다."], ["She always has done it.", "늘 용서해왔다."], ["She tells him how she has had it written in her will, these many years, that he was her beloved son George.", "그녀는 이 숱한 세월 유언장에 그가 자기 사랑하는 아들 조지라고 적어두었다고 그에게 말한다."], ["She has never believed any ill of him, never.", "그에 대해 나쁜 소문은 한 번도 믿은 적이 없다고, 한 번도."], ["If she had died without this happiness—and she is an old woman now and can’t look to live very long—she would have blessed him with her last breath, if she had had her senses, as her beloved son George.", "만약 이 행복을 누리지 못한 채 죽었더라면 — 이제 그녀는 늙은 여인이라 그리 오래 살 가망도 없다 — 정신만 온전했다면 마지막 숨으로라도 그를 사랑하는 아들 조지로서 축복했을 거라고."]], [["“Mother, I have been an undutiful trouble to you, and I have my reward; but of late years I have had a kind of glimmering of a purpose in me too. When I left home I didn’t care much, mother—I am afraid not a great deal—for leaving; and went away and ’listed, harum-scarum, making believe to think that I cared for nobody, no not I, and that nobody cared for me.”", "\"어머니, 저는 어머니께 효도 모르는 골칫거리였으니, 이런 벌도 마땅하지요. 하지만 근래 몇 해 동안엔 제게도 무언가 어렴풋한 목적 같은 게 깃들기 시작했답니다. 집을 떠날 적엔 떠나는 것에 별로 마음 쓰지 않았어요, 어머니. 거의 아무렇지도 않았던 것 같아 두렵습니다. 그저 무작정 떠나서 입대했지요, 정신없이. 나는 아무도 신경 안 쓴다고, 암 그렇고말고, 나를 신경 쓸 사람도 없다고 믿는 척하면서요.\""]], [["The trooper has dried his eyes and put away his handkerchief, but there is an extraordinary contrast between his habitual manner of expressing himself and carrying himself and the softened tone in which he speaks, interrupted occasionally by a half-stifled sob.", "기병은 눈물을 닦고 손수건을 치웠지만, 평소 그가 말하고 처신하던 투와 지금 그가 말하는 누그러진 음색 사이에는 유난한 대조가 있다. 그 음색은 이따금 반쯤 삼킨 흐느낌에 끊긴다."]], [["“So I wrote a line home, mother, as you too well know, to say I had ’listed under another name, and I went abroad. Abroad, at one time I thought I would write home next year, when I might be better off; and when that year was out, I thought I would write home next year, when I might be better off; and when that year was out again, perhaps I didn’t think much about it. So on, from year to year, through a service of ten years, till I began to get older, and to ask myself why should I ever write.”", "\"그래서 집에 편지 한 줄 띄웠지요, 어머니. 어머니도 너무 잘 아시다시피, 다른 이름으로 입대했다고요. 그러고는 외국으로 나갔습니다. 외국에서, 한때는 내년에 형편이 나아지면 집에 편지하리라 생각했어요. 그러다 그 한 해가 지나면 또, 내년에 형편이 나아지면 집에 편지하리라 생각했지요. 그렇게 또 한 해가 지나니, 어쩌면 별로 생각조차 안 하게 됐어요. 그런 식으로 해가 가고 또 가, 십 년을 복무하는 사이 저도 나이가 들기 시작했고, 대체 내가 편지를 쓸 까닭이 무어냐고 스스로에게 묻기에 이르렀지요.\""]], [["“I don’t find any fault, child—but not to ease my mind, George?", "\"널 탓하는 게 아니란다, 얘야. 그래도 이 어미 마음 좀 놓아주려고는 안 했니, 조지?"], ["Not a word to your loving mother, who was growing older too?”", "같이 나이 들어가던 이 사랑하는 어미한테 한마디도?\""]], [["This almost overturns the trooper afresh, but he sets himself up with a great, rough, sounding clearance of his throat.", "이 말에 기병은 다시 무너질 뻔하지만, 크고 거친 헛기침으로 목청을 쿵 가다듬으며 마음을 다잡는다."]], [["“Heaven forgive me, mother, but I thought there would be small consolation then in hearing anything about me.", "\"하느님, 저를 용서하소서. 어머니, 하지만 그땐 제 소식을 들어봤자 작은 위안밖에 안 되리라 여겼답니다."], ["There were you, respected and esteemed.", "어머니는 존경받고 우러름 받는 분이셨지요."], ["There was my brother, as I read in chance North Country papers now and then, rising to be prosperous and famous.", "형은, 이따금 북부 지방 신문에서 우연히 읽었듯, 번창하고 이름난 사람으로 올라서고 있었고요. 저는 떠돌고 뿌리 없는 기병이었어요."], ["There was I a dragoon, roving, unsettled, not self-made like him, but self-unmade—all my earlier advantages thrown away, all my little learning unlearnt, nothing picked up but what unfitted me for most things that I could think of.", "형처럼 자수성가한 게 아니라 스스로 망가뜨린 신세였지요. 어릴 적 누리던 온갖 이점은 다 내던졌고, 보잘것없는 배움도 다 까먹었고, 떠올릴 수 있는 웬만한 일에 죄다 어울리지 않게 만드는 것 말곤 아무것도 익히지 못했으니까요."], ["What business had I to make myself known?", "제가 무슨 자격으로 제 존재를 알린단 말입니까?"], ["After letting all that time go by me, what good could come of it?", "그 긴 세월을 다 흘려보내고 나서, 그게 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["The worst was past with you, mother.", "어머니, 최악은 이미 지나간 뒤였어요."], ["I knew by that time (being a man) how you had mourned for me, and wept for me, and prayed for me; and the pain was over, or was softened down, and I was better in your mind as it was.”", "그때쯤 저도 (어른이 되었으니) 어머니가 저를 어떻게 애도하고, 저를 위해 울고, 저를 위해 기도했는지 알았지요. 그 아픔은 이미 지나갔거나 무뎌졌을 테고, 차라리 그대로 두는 편이 어머니 마음에 낫겠다 싶었던 겁니다.\""]], [["The old lady sorrowfully shakes her head, and taking one of his powerful hands, lays it lovingly upon her shoulder.", "늙은 마님은 서글피 고개를 젓고는, 그의 억센 손 하나를 잡아 사랑스레 제 어깨에 얹는다."]], [["“No, I don’t say that it was so, mother, but that I made it out to be so.", "\"아니에요, 정말 그랬다는 게 아니라, 제가 그렇게 짜맞춘 거예요, 어머니."], ["I said just now, what good could come of it?", "방금 제가 그랬지요, 그게 무슨 소용이 있겠냐고."], ["Well, my dear mother, some good might have come of it to myself—and there was the meanness of it.", "그런데 사랑하는 어머니, 그게 저 자신한테는 무슨 소용이 됐을지도 모르지요. 바로 거기에 제 비열함이 있었던 겁니다."], ["You would have sought me out; you would have purchased my discharge; you would have taken me down to Chesney Wold; you would have brought me and my brother and my brother’s family together; you would all have considered anxiously how to do something for me and set me up as a respectable civilian.", "어머니는 저를 찾아내셨을 테고, 제 제대를 돈 주고 사주셨을 테고, 저를 체스니 월드로 데려가셨을 테지요. 저랑 형이랑 형네 식구들을 한자리에 모으셨을 테고요."], ["But how could any of you feel sure of me when I couldn’t so much as feel sure of myself?", "다들 저를 위해 무얼 해줄지, 어떻게 저를 번듯한 시민으로 세워줄지 애태우며 궁리하셨을 거예요."], ["How could you help regarding as an incumbrance and a discredit to you an idle dragooning chap who was an incumbrance and a discredit to himself, excepting under discipline?", "하지만 제가 저 자신조차 미덥지 않은데 어머니인들 어찌 저를 미덥다 여기시겠어요? 규율 아래에 있을 때 말고는 제 한 몸에도 짐이요 수치인, 빈둥대는 기병 녀석을 어머니인들 어찌 짐이요 수치로 여기지 않으시겠어요?"], ["How could I look my brother’s children in the face and pretend to set them an example—I, the vagabond boy who had run away from home and been the grief and unhappiness of my mother’s life?", "제가 무슨 낯으로 형의 아이들을 마주 보고 본보기인 척하겠어요? 집에서 도망쳐 나와 어머니 평생의 슬픔이요 불행이던 이 떠돌이 녀석이 말입니다."], ["‘No, George.’ Such were my words, mother, when I passed this in review before me: ‘You have made your bed.", "'안 돼, 조지.' 어머니, 이 모든 걸 눈앞에 죽 떠올렸을 때 제가 한 말이 그거였어요."], ["Now, lie upon it.’”", "'네가 잠자리를 깔았으니, 이제 거기 누워라.'\""]], [["Mrs.", "라운스웰 부인이 위엄 있는 자태를 곧추세우며, 부풀어 오르는 자긍심을 안고 늙은 여인을 향해 고개를 끄덕인다."], ["Rouncewell, drawing up her stately form, shakes her head at the old girl with a swelling pride upon her, as much as to say, “I told you so!”", "\"내가 그랬잖아요!\" 하고 말하듯이."], ["The old girl relieves her feelings and testifies her interest in the conversation by giving the trooper a great poke between the shoulders with her umbrella; this action she afterwards repeats, at intervals, in a species of affectionate lunacy, never failing, after the administration of each of these remonstrances, to resort to the whitened wall and the grey cloak again.", "늙은 여인은 우산으로 기병의 양 어깨 사이를 콱 찔러 제 심정을 풀고 대화에 쏠린 관심을 드러낸다. 이 동작을 그녀는 그 뒤로도 일종의 애정 어린 광기를 부려 틈틈이 되풀이하는데, 매번 이 항의를 가하고 나면 어김없이 다시 희게 칠한 벽과 회색 망토로 돌아간다."]], [["“This was the way I brought myself to think, mother, that my best amends was to lie upon that bed I had made, and die upon it.", "\"이런 식으로 제 마음을 다잡았지요, 어머니. 제가 깔아놓은 그 잠자리에 누워서 거기서 죽는 것이 제 최선의 속죄라고요."], ["And I should have done it (though I have been to see you more than once down at Chesney Wold, when you little thought of me) but for my old comrade’s wife here, who I find has been too many for me.", "그리고 실제로 그렇게 했을 거예요(어머니는 까맣게 모르셨지만, 체스니 월드로 어머니를 보러 한 번 넘게 내려간 적도 있답니다). 여기 이 옛 전우의 아내만 아니었다면요."], ["But I thank her for it.", "알고 보니 이분이 저보다 한 수 위였더군요."], ["I thank you for it, Mrs.", "그래도 이분께 감사합니다."], ["Bagnet, with all my heart and might.”", "진심으로, 온 힘을 다해 감사드려요, 배그넷 부인.\""]], [["To which Mrs. Bagnet responds with two pokes.", "이에 배그넷 부인은 우산으로 두 번 찌르는 것으로 응한다."]], [["And now the old lady impresses upon her son George, her own dear recovered boy, her joy and pride, the light of her eyes, the happy close of her life, and every fond name she can think of, that he must be governed by the best advice obtainable by money and influence, that he must yield up his case to the greatest lawyers that can be got, that he must act in this serious plight as he shall be advised to act and must not be self-willed, however right, but must promise to think only of his poor old mother’s anxiety and suffering until he is released, or he will break her heart.", "이제 늙은 마님은 아들 조지에게 — 되찾은 자기 사랑하는 아들, 자신의 기쁨이요 자랑이요 눈의 빛이요 생의 행복한 끝맺음이며, 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 애틋한 이름의 그 아들에게 — 신신당부한다. 돈과 인맥으로 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 조언을 따라야 한다고, 구할 수 있는 가장 뛰어난 변호사들에게 사건을 맡겨야 한다고, 이 엄중한 곤경에서는 시키는 대로 처신해야 하며, 제아무리 옳더라도 고집을 부려선 안 된다고, 풀려날 때까지는 오직 가엾은 늙은 어미의 근심과 고통만 생각하겠다고 약속해야 한다고, 안 그러면 어미의 가슴이 찢어진다고."]], [["“Mother, ’tis little enough to consent to,”", "\"어머니, 그쯤 따르는 거야 어렵지 않지요.\" 기병이 입맞춤으로 그녀의 말을 막으며 대꾸한다."], ["returns the trooper, stopping her with a kiss; “tell me what I shall do, and I’ll make a late beginning and do it.", "\"제가 뭘 하면 될지 일러주세요. 뒤늦게나마 시작해서 그대로 하겠습니다."], ["Mrs. Bagnet, you’ll take care of my mother, I know?”", "배그넷 부인, 제 어머니를 보살펴주시겠지요, 그러시리라 압니다.\""]], [["A very hard poke from the old girl’s umbrella.", "늙은 여인의 우산이 아주 세게 한 번 찌른다."]], [["“If you’ll bring her acquainted with Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson, she will find them of her way of thinking, and they will give her the best advice and assistance.”", "\"어머니를 잔다이스 씨와 서머슨 양에게 소개해주시면, 어머니도 그분들이 같은 생각인 걸 아실 테고, 그분들이 가장 좋은 조언과 도움을 주실 거예요.\""]], [["“And, George,”", "\"그리고 조지,\""], ["says the old lady, “we must send with all haste for your brother.", "늙은 마님이 말한다. \"한시바삐 네 형을 부르러 사람을 보내야겠다."], ["He is a sensible sound man as they tell me—out in the world beyond Chesney Wold, my dear, though I don’t know much of it myself—and will be of great service.”", "사람들 말로는 분별 있고 견실한 사람이라더구나. 체스니 월드 너머 바깥세상에 있어서 나도 잘은 모르지만, 얘야, 큰 도움이 될 게다.\""]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["returns the trooper, “is it too soon to ask a favour?”", "기병이 대꾸한다. \"부탁 하나 드리기엔 너무 이른가요?\""]], [["“Surely not, my dear.”", "\"이르긴, 절대 아니란다, 얘야.\""]], [["“Then grant me this one great favour.", "\"그럼 이 큰 부탁 하나만 들어주세요."], ["Don’t let my brother know.”", "형한테는 알리지 말아주세요.\""]], [["“Not know what, my dear?”", "\"무얼 알리지 말라는 거니, 얘야?\""]], [["“Not know of me.", "\"저에 관한 걸요."], ["In fact, mother, I can’t bear it; I can’t make up my mind to it.", "사실은요, 어머니, 견딜 수가 없어요. 도무지 마음을 다잡을 수가 없습니다."], ["He has proved himself so different from me and has done so much to raise himself while I’ve been soldiering that I haven’t brass enough in my composition to see him in this place and under this charge.", "형은 저와는 영 다른 사람임을 증명해 보였고, 제가 군바리 노릇 하는 동안 자기를 그토록 끌어올렸으니, 이런 곳에서 이런 혐의를 뒤집어쓴 제 꼴을 형에게 보일 만한 뱃심이 제겐 없어요."], ["How could a man like him be expected to have any pleasure in such a discovery?", "형 같은 사람이 그런 사실을 알고 무슨 기쁨을 느끼겠어요? 어림없는 일이지요."], ["It’s impossible.", "안 돼요, 형한테는 제 비밀을 지켜주세요, 어머니."], ["No, keep my secret from him, mother; do me a greater kindness than I deserve and keep my secret from my brother, of all men.”", "제가 받을 자격보다 더 큰 은혜를 베풀어주세요. 하고많은 사람 중에 형한테만은 제 비밀을 지켜주세요.\""]], [["“But not always, dear George?”", "\"하지만 영영은 아니지, 조지?\""]], [["“Why, mother, perhaps not for good and all—though I may come to ask that too—but keep it now, I do entreat you. If it’s ever broke to him that his rip of a brother has turned up, I could wish,” says the trooper, shaking his head very doubtfully, “to break it myself and be governed as to advancing or retreating by the way in which he seems to take it.”", "\"글쎄요, 어머니, 어쩌면 영영 안 알리는 건 아닐 수도 있어요. 저도 언젠가 그렇게 부탁드리게 될지 모르지요. 하지만 지금은 지켜주세요, 간곡히 빕니다. 만약 언젠가 못난 동생 놈이 나타났다는 사실을 형한테 알리게 된다면, 저는 차라리,\" 기병이 영 미덥잖다는 듯 고개를 흔들며 말한다. \"제 입으로 알리고 싶어요. 그래서 형이 그걸 받아들이는 태도를 보고 나아갈지 물러설지 정하고 싶습니다.\""]], [["As he evidently has a rooted feeling on this point, and as the depth of it is recognized in Mrs. Bagnet’s face, his mother yields her implicit assent to what he asks.", "이 점에 관해 그의 마음이 단단히 뿌리내린 것이 분명하고, 그 깊이가 배그넷 부인의 얼굴에도 읽히는지라, 어머니는 그가 청하는 바에 두말없이 동의한다."], ["For this he thanks her kindly.", "이에 그는 다정하게 감사한다."]], [["“In all other respects, my dear mother, I’ll be as tractable and obedient as you can wish; on this one alone, I stand out.", "\"다른 모든 일에서는, 사랑하는 어머니, 어머니가 바라시는 만큼 고분고분 따르겠어요. 다만 이 하나만은 제 고집을 부립니다."], ["So now I am ready even for the lawyers. I have been drawing up,”", "자, 이제 변호사들을 만날 채비까지 됐어요."], ["he glances at his writing on the table, “an exact account of what I knew of the deceased and how I came to be involved in this unfortunate affair.", "제가 죽은 양반에 대해 아는 바와, 어쩌다 이 불운한 사건에 얽혀들게 됐는지를 정확히 적어두는 중이었거든요.\" 그가 책상 위 자기 글을 흘끔 본다."], ["It’s entered, plain and regular, like an orderly-book; not a word in it but what’s wanted for the facts.", "\"당번병 일지처럼 분명하고 조리 있게 기재해뒀지요. 사실에 필요한 것 말곤 한마디도 안 들어갔어요."], ["I did intend to read it, straight on end, whensoever I was called upon to say anything in my defence.", "저를 변호하라는 요청이 오면 언제든 처음부터 끝까지 죽 읽어줄 작정이었습니다."], ["I hope I may be let to do it still; but I have no longer a will of my own in this case, and whatever is said or done, I give my promise not to have any.”", "지금도 그렇게 하도록 허락받기를 바라지만, 이 사건에서는 더 이상 제 고집을 부리지 않을 테니, 무슨 말을 듣고 무슨 일을 하든 제 뜻은 내세우지 않겠다고 약속드리지요.\""]], [["Matters being brought to this so far satisfactory pass, and time being on the wane, Mrs. Bagnet proposes a departure.", "일이 이쯤 흡족하게 매듭지어지고 시간도 기울어가자, 배그넷 부인이 떠나자고 제안한다."], ["Again and again the old lady hangs upon her son’s neck, and again and again the trooper holds her to his broad chest.", "늙은 마님은 거듭거듭 아들의 목에 매달리고, 기병은 거듭거듭 어머니를 제 넓은 가슴에 끌어안는다."]], [["“Where are you going to take my mother, Mrs. Bagnet?”", "\"제 어머니를 어디로 모셔갈 건가요, 배그넷 부인?\""]], [["“I am going to the town house, my dear, the family house.", "\"시내 저택으로 가요, 얘야, 그 집안 저택 말이다."], ["I have some business there that must be looked to directly,”", "거기 당장 살펴야 할 볼일이 좀 있단다.\""], ["Mrs. Rouncewell answers.", "라운스웰 부인이 답한다."]], [["“Will you see my mother safe there in a coach, Mrs. Bagnet?", "\"마차로 어머니를 거기까지 무사히 모셔다드리겠지요, 배그넷 부인?"], ["But of course I know you will.", "하기야 당연히 그러실 줄 압니다."], ["Why should I ask it!”", "제가 왜 그런 걸 묻겠어요!\""]], [["Why indeed, Mrs. Bagnet expresses with the umbrella.", "정말이지 왜 묻겠나, 하고 배그넷 부인은 우산으로 표현한다."]], [["“Take her, my old friend, and take my gratitude along with you.", "\"제 어머니를 모셔다드려요, 오랜 벗이여. 그리고 제 감사도 함께 가져가세요."], ["Kisses to Quebec and Malta, love to my godson, a hearty shake of the hand to Lignum, and this for yourself, and I wish it was ten thousand pound in gold, my dear!”", "퀘벡과 몰타한테 입맞춤을, 제 대자한테 사랑을, 리그넘한테는 진심 어린 악수를, 그리고 이건 부인 몫이에요. 이게 황금 일만 파운드였으면 좋겠습니다, 부인!\""], ["So saying, the trooper puts his lips to the old girl’s tanned forehead, and the door shuts upon him in his cell.", "이렇게 말하며 기병은 늙은 여인의 그을린 이마에 입술을 갖다 댄다. 그러고는 그를 가둔 감방의 문이 닫힌다."]], [["No entreaties on the part of the good old housekeeper will induce Mrs. Bagnet to retain the coach for her own conveyance home.", "마음씨 좋은 늙은 가정부가 아무리 간청해도, 배그넷 부인은 자기 귀갓길에 마차를 붙들어두려 하지 않는다."], ["Jumping out cheerfully at the door of the Dedlock mansion and handing Mrs. Rouncewell up the steps, the old girl shakes hands and trudges off, arriving soon afterwards in the bosom of the Bagnet family and falling to washing the greens as if nothing had happened.", "데드록 저택 문 앞에서 명랑하게 폴짝 뛰어내려 라운스웰 부인을 계단까지 부축해 올린 늙은 여인은, 악수를 나누고 터덜터덜 걸어가, 곧 배그넷 가족의 품에 이르러 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 푸성귀를 씻기 시작한다."]], [["My Lady is in that room in which she held her last conference with the murdered man, and is sitting where she sat that night, and is looking at the spot where he stood upon the hearth studying her so leisurely, when a tap comes at the door.", "마님은 살해된 사내와 마지막으로 담판을 벌였던 바로 그 방에 있다. 그날 밤 앉았던 자리에 앉아, 그가 난롯가에 서서 그토록 한가로이 자신을 살피던 그 자리를 바라보는데, 문에 똑똑 소리가 난다."], ["Who is it?", "누구인가?"], ["Mrs. Rouncewell.", "라운스웰 부인이다."], ["What has brought Mrs. Rouncewell to town so unexpectedly?", "무슨 일로 라운스웰 부인이 이리도 뜻밖에 상경했는가?"]], [["“Trouble, my Lady.", "\"걱정거리예요, 마님."], ["Sad trouble.", "슬픈 걱정거리지요."], ["Oh, my Lady, may I beg a word with you?”", "오, 마님, 한 말씀 여쭤도 될까요?\""]], [["What new occurrence is it that makes this tranquil old woman tremble so?", "이 차분한 늙은 여인을 이토록 떨게 하는 새 사달이 대체 무엇인가?"], ["Far happier than her Lady, as her Lady has often thought, why does she falter in this manner and look at her with such strange mistrust?", "마님이 늘 생각해왔듯 마님보다 훨씬 행복한 사람이 어찌하여 이리도 머뭇거리며, 그런 이상한 의심의 눈초리로 마님을 바라보는가?"]], [["“What is the matter?", "\"무슨 일이오?"], ["Sit down and take your breath.”", "앉아서 숨을 좀 돌리시오.\""]], [["“Oh, my Lady, my Lady.", "\"오, 마님, 마님."], ["I have found my son—my youngest, who went away for a soldier so long ago.", "제 아들을 찾았어요. 오래전에 군인이 되겠다고 떠난 막내를요."], ["And he is in prison.”", "그런데 그 애가 감옥에 있답니다.\""]], [["“For debt?”", "\"빚 때문에?\""]], [["“Oh, no, my Lady; I would have paid any debt, and joyful.”", "\"오, 아니에요, 마님. 빚이라면 무슨 빚이든 갚았겠지요, 그것도 기쁘게요.\""]], [["“For what is he in prison then?”", "\"그럼 무슨 일로 감옥에 있다는 거요?\""]], [["“Charged with a murder, my Lady, of which he is as innocent as—as I am.", "\"살인 혐의예요, 마님. 그 애는 저처럼 결백한데요."], ["Accused of the murder of Mr. Tulkinghorn.”", "토킹혼 씨를 살해했다고 고발당했답니다.\""]], [["What does she mean by this look and this imploring gesture?", "이 눈빛과 이 애원하는 몸짓은 무엇을 뜻하는가?"], ["Why does she come so close?", "어찌하여 이리도 가까이 다가오는가?"], ["What is the letter that she holds?", "그녀가 손에 쥔 저 편지는 무엇인가?"]], [["“Lady Dedlock, my dear Lady, my good Lady, my kind Lady!", "\"데드록 마님, 사랑하는 마님, 어지신 마님, 인자하신 마님!"], ["You must have a heart to feel for me, you must have a heart to forgive me.", "마님께는 저를 가엾이 여길 마음이 있으실 거예요, 저를 용서하실 마음이 있으실 거예요."], ["I was in this family before you were born.", "저는 마님이 태어나시기 전부터 이 집안에 있었어요."], ["I am devoted to it.", "이 집안에 헌신해왔지요."], ["But think of my dear son wrongfully accused.”", "하지만 억울하게 고발당한 제 사랑하는 아들을 생각해주세요.\""]], [["“I do not accuse him.”", "\"나는 그를 고발하지 않소.\""]], [["“No, my Lady, no.", "\"네, 마님, 그러시지요."], ["But others do, and he is in prison and in danger.", "하지만 다른 이들이 고발했고, 그 애는 감옥에 갇혀 위태롭답니다."], ["Oh, Lady Dedlock, if you can say but a word to help to clear him, say it!”", "오, 데드록 마님, 그 애의 누명을 벗기는 데 보탬이 될 한마디라도 하실 수 있다면, 부디 해주세요!\""]], [["What delusion can this be?", "이게 무슨 미혹이란 말인가?"], ["What power does she suppose is in the person she petitions to avert this unjust suspicion, if it be unjust?", "그녀가 호소하는 사람에게, 만약 이 부당한 의심이 부당한 것이라면, 그것을 막을 무슨 힘이 있다고 여기는가?"], ["Her Lady’s handsome eyes regard her with astonishment, almost with fear.", "마님의 아름다운 두 눈이 놀라움으로, 거의 두려움으로 그녀를 바라본다."]], [["“My Lady, I came away last night from Chesney Wold to find my son in my old age, and the step upon the Ghost’s Walk was so constant and so solemn that I never heard the like in all these years.", "\"마님, 저는 늘그막에 아들을 찾으려고 어젯밤 체스니 월드를 떠나왔는데요, 유령의 길에 울리던 발소리가 어찌나 끊임없고 어찌나 무겁던지 이 숱한 세월 그런 건 들어본 적이 없었답니다."], ["Night after night, as it has fallen dark, the sound has echoed through your rooms, but last night it was awfullest.", "밤마다, 날이 어두워지면, 그 소리가 마님의 방들에 메아리쳤지만, 어젯밤이 가장 무시무시했어요."], ["And as it fell dark last night, my Lady, I got this letter.”", "그리고 어젯밤 날이 어두워질 무렵, 마님, 제가 이 편지를 받았지요.\""]], [["“What letter is it?”", "\"무슨 편지요?\""]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿! 쉿!\""], ["Hush!”", "가정부가 주위를 둘러보며 겁먹은 속삭임으로 답한다."], ["The housekeeper looks round and answers in a frightened whisper, “My Lady, I have not breathed a word of it, I don’t believe what’s written in it, I know it can’t be true, I am sure and certain that it is not true.", "\"마님, 저는 이 일을 누구한테도 한마디 발설하지 않았어요. 거기 적힌 걸 믿지도 않고요."], ["But my son is in danger, and you must have a heart to pity me.", "사실일 리 없다는 걸 알아요. 사실이 아니라고 확신하고 또 확신해요."], ["If you know of anything that is not known to others, if you have any suspicion, if you have any clue at all, and any reason for keeping it in your own breast, oh, my dear Lady, think of me, and conquer that reason, and let it be known!", "하지만 제 아들이 위태로우니, 마님께는 저를 가엾이 여길 마음이 있으셔야 해요. 만약 마님이 남들은 모르는 무언가를 아신다면, 무슨 의심이라도 품고 계시다면, 무슨 단서라도 갖고 계시다면, 그걸 마님 가슴속에만 묻어둘 무슨 까닭이 있으시다면, 오, 사랑하는 마님, 저를 생각하셔서 그 까닭을 이겨내시고, 부디 그걸 알려주세요!"], ["This is the most I consider possible.", "제가 바랄 수 있는 건 이게 고작이에요. 마님이 모진 분이 아니신 줄은 알아요."], ["I know you are not a hard lady, but you go your own way always without help, and you are not familiar with your friends; and all who admire you—and all do—as a beautiful and elegant lady, know you to be one far away from themselves who can’t be approached close.", "하지만 마님은 늘 도움 없이 혼자 길을 가시고, 벗들과도 허물없이 지내지 않으시지요. 마님을 우러르는 모든 이가 — 다들 우러르지요 — 아름답고 우아한 마님을 자기들과는 동떨어진, 가까이 다가갈 수 없는 분으로 압니다."], ["My Lady, you may have some proud or angry reasons for disdaining to utter something that you know; if so, pray, oh, pray, think of a faithful servant whose whole life has been passed in this family which she dearly loves, and relent, and help to clear my son!", "마님, 마님께는 아시는 무언가를 입에 올리길 꺼리실 만한 어떤 자존심이나 노여움의 까닭이 있을지도 몰라요. 만약 그렇다면, 부디, 오, 부디, 평생을 이 집안에서 보낸, 이 집안을 진심으로 사랑하는 충직한 하인을 생각하셔서, 마음을 누그러뜨리시고 제 아들의 누명 벗기는 걸 도와주세요!"], ["My Lady, my good Lady,”", "마님, 어지신 마님,\" 늙은 가정부가 진솔하고 꾸밈없이 간청한다."], ["the old housekeeper pleads with genuine simplicity, “I am so humble in my place and you are by nature so high and distant that you may not think what I feel for my child, but I feel so much that I have come here to make so bold as to beg and pray you not to be scornful of us if you can do us any right or justice at this fearful time!”", "\"저는 제 자리에서 이토록 미천하고 마님은 천성이 이토록 높고 멀리 계시니, 제가 제 자식을 두고 느끼는 마음을 마님은 헤아리지 못하실지도 몰라요. 하지만 그 마음이 어찌나 큰지, 이 끔찍한 때에 마님이 우리에게 조금이라도 옳고 정당한 일을 베푸실 수 있다면 부디 우리를 업신여기지 말아달라고, 이렇게 외람되이 빌고 청하러 여기까지 온 거랍니다!\""]], [["Lady Dedlock raises her without one word, until she takes the letter from her hand.", "데드록 마님은 그녀의 손에서 편지를 받아 들 때까지 한마디 말도 없이 그녀를 일으켜 세운다."]], [["“Am I to read this?”", "\"이걸 읽어야 하오?\""]], [["“When I am gone, my Lady, if you please, and then remembering the most that I consider possible.”", "\"제가 떠난 뒤에요, 마님, 괜찮으시다면요. 그러고 나서 제가 바랄 수 있는 게 고작 그것임을 기억해주세요.\""]], [["“I know of nothing I can do.", "\"내가 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 모르오."], ["I know of nothing I reserve that can affect your son.", "당신 아들에게 영향을 줄 만한, 내가 감춘 것도 아무것도 모르고."], ["I have never accused him.”", "나는 한 번도 그를 고발하지 않았소.\""]], [["“My Lady, you may pity him the more under a false accusation after reading the letter.”", "\"마님, 편지를 읽으시면 거짓 고발을 당한 그 애를 한층 더 가엾이 여기실지도 몰라요.\""]], [["The old housekeeper leaves her with the letter in her hand.", "늙은 가정부는 마님의 손에 편지를 쥐여둔 채 떠난다."], ["In truth she is not a hard lady naturally, and the time has been when the sight of the venerable figure suing to her with such strong earnestness would have moved her to great compassion.", "사실 마님은 천성이 모진 여인이 아니어서, 한때는 저토록 절절한 진심으로 호소하는 그 존경스러운 모습이 그녀를 크나큰 연민으로 움직였을 때도 있었다."], ["But so long accustomed to suppress emotion and keep down reality, so long schooled for her own purposes in that destructive school which shuts up the natural feelings of the heart like flies in amber and spreads one uniform and dreary gloss over the good and bad, the feeling and the unfeeling, the sensible and the senseless, she had subdued even her wonder until now.", "그러나 너무나 오래 감정을 억누르고 실제를 짓눌러온 데다, 너무나 오래 제 잇속에 따라 그 파괴적인 학교에서 길들여진 탓에 — 마음의 자연스러운 감정을 호박 속 파리처럼 가두고, 선과 악, 정 있는 것과 정 없는 것, 분별 있는 것과 분별없는 것 위에 한결같고 음울한 윤기 하나를 고루 발라버리는 그런 학교에서 — 그녀는 이제껏 놀라움마저 억눌러왔던 것이다."]], [["She opens the letter.", "그녀가 편지를 펼친다. 종이 위에는 시신이 마룻바닥에 엎어진 채, 심장을 관통당해 발견된 정황이 인쇄되어 펼쳐져 있다."], ["Spread out upon the paper is a printed account of the discovery of the body as it lay face downward on the floor, shot through the heart; and underneath is written her own name, with the word “murderess” attached.", "그 아래에는 그녀 자신의 이름이, \"살인자\"라는 단어와 함께 적혀 있다."]], [["It falls out of her hand.", "편지가 그녀의 손에서 떨어진다."], ["How long it may have lain upon the ground she knows not, but it lies where it fell when a servant stands before her announcing the young man of the name of Guppy.", "그것이 얼마나 오래 바닥에 놓여 있었는지 그녀는 알지 못하나, 하인 하나가 그녀 앞에 서서 거피라는 이름의 젊은이가 왔음을 알릴 때까지도 떨어진 자리에 그대로 놓여 있다."], ["The words have probably been repeated several times, for they are ringing in her head before she begins to understand them.", "그 말은 아마 여러 번 되풀이됐을 것이다. 그녀가 그 뜻을 알아듣기 전부터 머릿속에 울리고 있으니."]], [["“Let him come in!”", "\"들라 하시오!\""]], [["He comes in.", "그가 들어온다."], ["Holding the letter in her hand, which she has taken from the floor, she tries to collect her thoughts.", "바닥에서 집어 든 편지를 손에 쥔 채, 그녀는 마음을 추스르려 애쓴다."], ["In the eyes of Mr. Guppy she is the same Lady Dedlock, holding the same prepared, proud, chilling state.", "거피 씨의 눈에 그녀는 여전히 똑같은 데드록 마님이다, 여전히 준비된, 도도하고 싸늘한 태도를 지닌."]], [["“Your ladyship may not be at first disposed to excuse this visit from one who has never been welcome to your ladyship”—which he don’t complain of, for he is bound to confess that there never has been any particular reason on the face of things why he should be—“but I hope when I mention my motives to your ladyship you will not find fault with me,”", "\"마님께서는 한 번도 마님의 환영을 받은 적 없는 자가 이리 찾아온 걸 처음엔 너그러이 봐주실 기분이 아니실지도 모르겠습니다.\" — 그는 이를 불평하는 게 아니다, 겉으로 보아 그가 환영받을 만한 무슨 특별한 까닭이 있던 적은 없음을 인정할 수밖에 없으니까 — \"하지만 제가 마님께 그 동기를 말씀드리면, 저를 탓하지 않으시리라 바랍니다.\""], ["says Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Do so.”", "\"말해보시오.\""]], [["“Thank your ladyship.", "\"고맙습니다, 마님."], ["I ought first to explain to your ladyship,” Mr.", "먼저 마님께 말씀드려야겠는데요,\""], ["Guppy sits on the edge of a chair and puts his hat on the carpet at his feet, “that Miss Summerson, whose image, as I formerly mentioned to your ladyship, was at one period of my life imprinted on my ’eart until erased by circumstances over which I had no control, communicated to me, after I had the pleasure of waiting on your ladyship last, that she particularly wished me to take no steps whatever in any manner at all relating to her.", "거피 씨가 의자 끄트머리에 앉아 모자를 제 발치 양탄자에 내려놓는다. \"이전에 마님께 말씀드린 대로 한때 제 심장에 그 모습이 새겨졌다가 제가 어찌할 수 없는 사정으로 지워진 서머슨 양이, 지난번 제가 마님을 뵙는 영광을 누린 뒤에, 자기와 관련된 일이라면 어떤 식으로든 무슨 조치도 일절 취하지 말기를 특별히 바란다고 제게 전했습니다."], ["And Miss Summerson’s wishes being to me a law (except as connected with circumstances over which I have no control), I consequently never expected to have the distinguished honour of waiting on your ladyship again.”", "그리고 서머슨 양의 뜻은 제게 법이나 마찬가지인지라(제가 어찌할 수 없는 사정과 얽힌 경우는 빼고요), 그래서 저는 다시 마님을 뵙는 영예를 누리리라곤 결코 기대하지 않았지요.\""]], [["And yet he is here now, Lady Dedlock moodily reminds him.", "그런데도 지금 그가 여기 있다고, 데드록 마님이 시무룩하게 일깨운다."]], [["“And yet I am here now,”", "\"그런데도 지금 제가 여기 있지요.\""], ["Mr. Guppy admits.", "거피 씨가 인정한다."], ["“My object being to communicate to your ladyship, under the seal of confidence, why I am here.”", "\"제 목적이 마님께, 비밀 엄수를 전제로, 제가 왜 여기 왔는지를 알려드리는 것이니까요.\""]], [["He cannot do so, she tells him, too plainly or too briefly.", "그 점이라면 아무리 분명하게, 아무리 간략하게 말해도 부족함이 없겠다고, 그녀가 그에게 이른다."], ["“Nor can I,” Mr.", "\"저도 그렇습니다.\""], ["Guppy returns with a sense of injury upon him, “too particularly request your ladyship to take particular notice that it’s no personal affair of mine that brings me here.", "거피 씨가 무언가 억울하다는 기색으로 대꾸한다. \"제가 여기 온 것이 제 개인적인 일 때문이 아니라는 점을 마님께 각별히 유념해주십사 아무리 각별히 청해도 부족함이 없지요."], ["I have no interested views of my own to serve in coming here.", "여기 오는 데 제 사사로운 잇속이라곤 없습니다."], ["If it was not for my promise to Miss Summerson and my keeping of it sacred—I, in point of fact, shouldn’t have darkened these doors again, but should have seen ’em further first.”", "서머슨 양과의 약속, 그리고 그 약속을 신성히 지키려는 마음만 아니었다면, 솔직히 말해 저는 이 문턱을 다시 넘기는커녕 그러느니 차라리 멀찌감치 사라지는 꼴을 봤을 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy considers this a favourable moment for sticking up his hair with both hands.", "거피 씨는 이 대목이 두 손으로 머리카락을 곧추세우기에 좋은 순간이라 여긴다."]], [["“Your ladyship will remember when I mention it that the last time I was here I run against a party very eminent in our profession and whose loss we all deplore.", "\"제가 말씀드리면 마님께서도 기억하시겠지만, 지난번 제가 여기 왔을 때 저는 우리 업계에서 아주 저명한, 그리고 그 죽음을 우리 모두가 애도하는 어떤 양반과 부딪쳤습니다."], ["That party certainly did from that time apply himself to cutting in against me in a way that I will call sharp practice, and did make it, at every turn and point, extremely difficult for me to be sure that I hadn’t inadvertently led up to something contrary to Miss Summerson’s wishes.", "그 양반은 그때부터 분명 제게 맞서 끼어들었어요, 제가 악랄한 수법이라 부를 만한 방식으로요. 그러고는 매 길목마다 매 고비마다, 제가 무심코 서머슨 양의 뜻에 어긋나는 무언가로 이어지는 짓을 하지 않았다고 확신하기를 지독히 어렵게 만들었지요."], ["Self-praise is no recommendation, but I may say for myself that I am not so bad a man of business neither.”", "자화자찬은 권할 게 못 되지만, 저 자신을 두고 한마디 하자면, 저도 그리 못난 사업가는 아니랍니다.\""]], [["Lady Dedlock looks at him in stern inquiry. Mr.", "데드록 마님이 준엄하게 따지는 눈으로 그를 본다."], ["Guppy immediately withdraws his eyes from her face and looks anywhere else.", "거피 씨는 즉시 그녀의 얼굴에서 눈길을 거두어 아무 데로나 돌린다."]], [["“Indeed, it has been made so hard,” he goes on, “to have any idea what that party was up to in combination with others that until the loss which we all deplore I was gravelled—an expression which your ladyship, moving in the higher circles, will be so good as to consider tantamount to knocked over.", "\"정말이지, 그 양반이 다른 이들과 짜고 무슨 꿍꿍이를 꾸미는지 도무지 짐작조차 할 수 없게 만들어놔서요.\""], ["Small likewise—a name by which I refer to another party, a friend of mine that your ladyship is not acquainted with—got to be so close and double-faced that at times it wasn’t easy to keep one’s hands off his ’ead.", "그가 말을 잇는다. \"우리 모두가 애도하는 그 죽음이 있기 전까지 저는 그야말로 막혀버렸지요."], ["However, what with the exertion of my humble abilities, and what with the help of a mutual friend by the name of Mr.", "높은 사교계에서 노니시는 마님께서는 너그러이, 그 표현이 나가떨어졌다는 말과 매한가지라고 여겨주십시오."], ["Tony Weevle (who is of a high aristocratic turn and has your ladyship’s portrait always hanging up in his room), I have now reasons for an apprehension as to which I come to put your ladyship upon your guard.", "스몰 — 이건 마님이 모르시는 또 다른 양반, 제 친구를 가리키는 이름인데요 — 도 어찌나 입을 다물고 두 얼굴로 굴던지, 때로는 그놈 머리통에 손을 안 대기가 쉽지 않을 지경이었답니다. 그렇지만 제 변변찮은 재간을 쥐어짜고, 또 토니 위블 씨라는 한 공통의 벗(고매한 귀족 취향을 지닌 분으로, 마님의 초상화를 늘 제 방에 걸어두고 있지요)의 도움을 받아, 이제 저는 마님께 경계하시라 일러드리러 온 어떤 우려의 근거를 갖게 됐습니다."], ["First, will your ladyship allow me to ask you whether you have had any strange visitors this morning?", "우선, 마님, 오늘 아침 이상한 방문객이 있었는지 여쭤봐도 될까요?"], ["I don’t mean fashionable visitors, but such visitors, for instance, as Miss Barbary’s old servant, or as a person without the use of his lower extremities, carried upstairs similarly to a guy?” “No!”", "사교계의 방문객 말고요, 이를테면 바버리 양의 늙은 하인이라든가, 다리를 쓰지 못해서 무슨 허수아비처럼 위층으로 들려 올라가는 사람 같은 그런 방문객 말입니다.\""]], [["“Then I assure your ladyship that such visitors have been here and have been received here.", "\"없었소!\" \"그렇다면 단언컨대, 마님, 그런 방문객들이 여기 와서 여기서 맞이를 받았습니다."], ["Because I saw them at the door, and waited at the corner of the square till they came out, and took half an hour’s turn afterwards to avoid them.”", "제가 문간에서 그들을 봤고, 그들이 나올 때까지 광장 모퉁이에서 기다렸다가, 그들을 피하느라 그 뒤로 삼십 분쯤 더 돌아다녔으니까요.\""]], [["“What have I to do with that, or what have you?", "\"그게 나와 무슨 상관이며, 당신과는 또 무슨 상관이오?"], ["I do not understand you.", "무슨 말인지 모르겠소."], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“Your ladyship, I come to put you on your guard.", "\"마님, 저는 마님께 경계하시라 일러드리러 온 겁니다."], ["There may be no occasion for it.", "그럴 까닭이 없을 수도 있지요."], ["Very well.", "좋습니다."], ["Then I have only done my best to keep my promise to Miss Summerson.", "그렇다면 저는 서머슨 양과의 약속을 지키려 최선을 다한 것뿐이고요."], ["I strongly suspect (from what Small has dropped, and from what we have corkscrewed out of him) that those letters I was to have brought to your ladyship were not destroyed when I supposed they were.", "제가 짐작하기로는(스몰이 흘린 말과, 우리가 그놈한테서 비틀어 짜낸 걸로 봐서) 제가 마님께 가져다드리기로 했던 그 편지들이, 제가 그런 줄로만 알았던 것과 달리, 그때 없애지지 않았던 모양입니다."], ["That if there was anything to be blown upon, it IS blown upon.", "폭로될 만한 게 있었다면, 이미 폭로됐다는 거지요."], ["That the visitors I have alluded to have been here this morning to make money of it.", "제가 말씀드린 그 방문객들이 오늘 아침 여기 와서 그걸로 돈을 챙기려 한 거고요."], ["And that the money is made, or making.”", "그리고 그 돈은 이미 챙겨졌거나, 챙겨지는 중이라는 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy picks up his hat and rises.", "거피 씨가 모자를 집어 들고 일어선다."]], [["“Your ladyship, you know best whether there’s anything in what I say or whether there’s nothing.", "\"마님, 제가 드린 말씀에 뭔가 있는지 아무것도 없는지는 마님이 가장 잘 아시겠지요."], ["Something or nothing, I have acted up to Miss Summerson’s wishes in letting things alone and in undoing what I had begun to do, as far as possible; that’s sufficient for me.", "있든 없든, 저는 서머슨 양의 뜻에 따라 일을 가만 내버려두고, 이미 벌여놓은 일을 가능한 한 되물렸으니, 그걸로 저는 족합니다."], ["In case I should be taking a liberty in putting your ladyship on your guard when there’s no necessity for it, you will endeavour, I should hope, to outlive my presumption, and I shall endeavour to outlive your disapprobation.", "혹시 그럴 필요도 없는데 마님께 경계하시라 일러드려 외람을 범한 거라면, 마님께서 부디 제 주제넘음을 너그러이 견뎌내주시기를 바라고, 저도 마님의 못마땅함을 견뎌내도록 애쓰겠습니다."], ["I now take my farewell of your ladyship, and assure you that there’s no danger of your ever being waited on by me again.”", "이제 마님께 작별을 고하며, 다시는 마님이 저한테서 시중받으실 일이 없으리라는 걸 장담드리지요.\""]], [["She scarcely acknowledges these parting words by any look, but when he has been gone a little while, she rings her bell.", "그녀는 이 작별의 말을 눈빛으로조차 거의 알은체하지 않지만, 그가 떠나고 조금 지나자 종을 울린다."]], [["“Where is Sir Leicester?”", "\"레스터 경은 어디 계시지?\""]], [["Mercury reports that he is at present shut up in the library alone.", "머큐리가 지금 서재에 혼자 틀어박혀 계신다고 아뢴다."]], [["“Has Sir Leicester had any visitors this morning?”", "\"레스터 경께 오늘 아침 방문객이 있었나?\""]], [["Several, on business.", "볼일로 몇 사람 있었습니다."], ["Mercury proceeds to a description of them, which has been anticipated by Mr. Guppy.", "머큐리가 그들을 묘사하기 시작하는데, 그건 거피 씨가 이미 예고한 바다."], ["Enough; he may go.", "됐다, 가도 좋다."]], [["So!", "그래!"], ["All is broken down.", "모든 게 무너졌다."], ["Her name is in these many mouths, her husband knows his wrongs, her shame will be published—may be spreading while she thinks about it—and in addition to the thunderbolt so long foreseen by her, so unforeseen by him, she is denounced by an invisible accuser as the murderess of her enemy.", "그녀의 이름이 이 숱한 입에 오르내리고, 남편은 자기가 입은 모욕을 알며, 그녀의 수치가 세상에 알려질 것이다 — 어쩌면 그녀가 이러는 사이에도 퍼지고 있을지 모른다 — 그리고 그녀가 그토록 오래 내다본, 그러나 그는 그토록 내다보지 못한 그 날벼락에 더하여, 그녀는 보이지 않는 고발자에게 제 원수를 죽인 살인자로 지목당했다."]], [["Her enemy he was, and she has often, often, often wished him dead.", "그는 그녀의 원수였고, 그녀는 자주, 자주, 자주 그가 죽기를 바랐다."], ["Her enemy he is, even in his grave.", "그는 여전히 그녀의 원수다, 무덤 속에서까지도."], ["This dreadful accusation comes upon her like a new torment at his lifeless hand.", "이 무시무시한 고발이 그 싸늘한 손에서 새로운 고문처럼 그녀에게 들이닥친다."], ["And when she recalls how she was secretly at his door that night, and how she may be represented to have sent her favourite girl away so soon before merely to release herself from observation, she shudders as if the hangman’s hands were at her neck.", "그리고 그날 밤 자신이 몰래 그의 문 앞에 갔던 일, 단지 남의 눈을 피하려고 직전에 그토록 일찍 아끼는 하녀를 멀리 보냈다고 둘러대질 수 있겠다는 생각이 떠오르자, 그녀는 마치 교수형 집행인의 손이 제 목을 죄는 듯 진저리친다."]], [["She has thrown herself upon the floor and lies with her hair all wildly scattered and her face buried in the cushions of a couch.", "그녀는 바닥에 몸을 내던지고, 머리카락을 온통 마구 흩뜨린 채 긴 의자의 방석에 얼굴을 묻고 엎드린다."], ["She rises up, hurries to and fro, flings herself down again, and rocks and moans.", "일어나 이리저리 서성이다가 다시 몸을 내던지고, 흔들며 신음한다."], ["The horror that is upon her is unutterable.", "그녀를 짓누르는 공포는 이루 말할 수 없다."], ["If she really were the murderess, it could hardly be, for the moment, more intense.", "그녀가 정말로 살인자라 한들, 지금 이 순간의 그것보다 더 격렬할 수는 없으리라."]], [["For as her murderous perspective, before the doing of the deed, however subtle the precautions for its commission, would have been closed up by a gigantic dilatation of the hateful figure, preventing her from seeing any consequences beyond it; and as those consequences would have rushed in, in an unimagined flood, the moment the figure was laid low—which always happens when a murder is done; so, now she sees that when he used to be on the watch before her, and she used to think, “if some mortal stroke would but fall on this old man and take him from my way!”", "범행 전 그녀의 살의 어린 시야가, 그 실행을 위한 방비책이 제아무리 교묘했던들, 그 가증스러운 형상의 거대한 팽창에 가려져 그 너머의 어떤 결과도 보지 못하게 막혔을 것이고 — 살인이 저질러질 때면 늘 그러하듯 — 그 형상이 거꾸러지는 순간 상상조차 못 한 홍수처럼 그 결과들이 밀려들었을 것이듯, 그렇게 그녀는 이제 깨닫는다. 그가 늘 그녀 앞에서 망보던 시절, 그녀가 \"이 늙은이한테 죽음의 일격이라도 떨어져 내 앞길에서 치워준다면!\""], ["it was but wishing that all he held against her in his hand might be flung to the winds and chance-sown in many places.", "하고 생각하곤 했던 그것이, 실은 그가 손에 쥐고 그녀를 옭아매던 모든 것이 바람결에 흩날려 사방에 우연히 흩뿌려지기를 바란 것에 지나지 않았음을."], ["So, too, with the wicked relief she has felt in his death.", "그의 죽음에서 그녀가 느낀 그 사악한 안도 또한 마찬가지다."], ["What was his death but the key-stone of a gloomy arch removed, and now the arch begins to fall in a thousand fragments, each crushing and mangling piecemeal!", "그의 죽음이란 음울한 아치의 쐐기돌이 빠진 것 말고 무엇이었던가. 이제 그 아치가 천 조각으로 무너져 내리기 시작하며, 그 조각마다 그녀를 짓이기고 갈가리 찢는다!"]], [["Thus, a terrible impression steals upon and overshadows her that from this pursuer, living or dead—obdurate and imperturbable before her in his well-remembered shape, or not more obdurate and imperturbable in his coffin-bed—there is no escape but in death.", "그리하여 끔찍한 생각 하나가 그녀에게 슬며시 스며들어 그늘을 드리운다. 살았든 죽었든 이 추적자에게서 — 또렷이 기억나는 그 모습으로 그녀 앞에 완강하고 흔들림 없이 서 있든, 관 속 침상에 누워서도 그에 못지않게 완강하고 흔들림 없든 — 죽음 말고는 벗어날 길이 없다는 생각이."], ["Hunted, she flies.", "쫓기는 그녀가 달아난다."], ["The complication of her shame, her dread, remorse, and misery, overwhelms her at its height; and even her strength of self-reliance is overturned and whirled away like a leaf before a mighty wind.", "그녀의 수치와 두려움, 회한과 비참이 한데 뒤엉켜 절정에서 그녀를 집어삼킨다. 그녀의 자립심마저 거센 바람에 날리는 잎새처럼 뒤집혀 휩쓸려 날아간다."]], [["She hurriedly addresses these lines to her husband, seals, and leaves them on her table:", "그녀는 황급히 남편에게 이 몇 줄을 적어 봉인하고는 책상 위에 남긴다."]], [["If I am sought for, or accused of, his murder, believe that I am wholly innocent.", "만약 제가 그분의 살해범으로 수색되거나 고발된다면, 제가 전적으로 결백함을 믿어주세요."], ["Believe no other good of me, for I am innocent of nothing else that you have heard, or will hear, laid to my charge.", "그 밖에 저에 대한 어떤 좋은 점도 믿지 마세요. 당신이 들었거나 앞으로 들으실, 제게 씌워진 다른 어떤 일에 대해서도 저는 결백하지 않으니까요."], ["He prepared me, on that fatal night, for his disclosure of my guilt to you.", "그 끔찍한 밤, 그분은 제 죄를 당신께 폭로하겠노라고 저를 다그쳤습니다."], ["After he had left me, I went out on pretence of walking in the garden where I sometimes walk, but really to follow him and make one last petition that he would not protract the dreadful suspense on which I have been racked by him, you do not know how long, but would mercifully strike next morning.", "그분이 떠난 뒤, 저는 가끔 거니는 정원을 산책한다는 핑계로 나갔지만, 실은 그분을 뒤따라가, 그분이 저를 옭아매어온 그 끔찍한 마음 졸임을 — 얼마나 오래였는지 당신은 모르세요 — 더는 끌지 말고 자비롭게 이튿날 아침 결판을 내달라고 마지막 한 번 청하기 위해서였습니다."]], [["I found his house dark and silent.", "그분의 집은 어둡고 고요했습니다."], ["I rang twice at his door, but there was no reply, and I came home.", "저는 두 번 문 앞에서 종을 울렸지만 아무 응답이 없어 집으로 돌아왔습니다."]], [["I have no home left. I will encumber you no more.", "이제 제게는 돌아갈 집이 없습니다."], ["May you, in your just resentment, be able to forget the unworthy woman on whom you have wasted a most generous devotion—who avoids you only with a deeper shame than that with which she hurries from herself—and who writes this last adieu.", "더는 당신께 짐이 되지 않겠습니다. 부디 당신이 정당한 분노 속에서, 더없이 너그러운 헌신을 헛되이 쏟아부은 이 못난 여인을 잊으실 수 있기를 — 제 자신에게서 황급히 달아날 때보다 더 깊은 수치로 당신을 피하는, 그리고 이 마지막 작별을 적는 이 여인을."]], [["She veils and dresses quickly, leaves all her jewels and her money, listens, goes downstairs at a moment when the hall is empty, opens and shuts the great door, flutters away in the shrill frosty wind.", "그녀는 황급히 너울을 쓰고 옷을 갖춰 입고는 보석과 돈을 죄다 남겨둔 채 귀를 기울이다가, 현관이 텅 빈 틈에 아래층으로 내려가, 큰 문을 열었다 닫고는, 매서운 서리 바람 속으로 훌쩍 사라진다."]], [["Pursuit\nImpassive, as behoves its high breeding, the Dedlock town house stares at the other houses in the street of dismal grandeur and gives no outward sign of anything going wrong within.", "높은 가문에 걸맞게 무표정한 데드록 저택은 음울한 위엄이 깃든 거리의 다른 집들을 무심히 바라보며, 안에서 뭔가 잘못되어 간다는 기색을 겉으로 조금도 내비치지 않는다."], ["Carriages rattle, doors are battered at, the world exchanges calls; ancient charmers with skeleton throats, and peachy cheeks that have a rather ghastly bloom upon them seen by daylight, when indeed these fascinating creatures look like Death and the Lady fused together, dazzle the eyes of men.", "마차가 덜컹거리고, 문이 쾅쾅 두들겨지고, 세상은 서로 방문을 주고받는다. 해골 같은 목과, 대낮에 보면 어쩐지 섬뜩한 화색이 도는 복숭앗빛 뺨을 지닌 늙은 미녀들이, 정말이지 이 매혹적인 존재들이 죽음과 귀부인이 한 몸으로 녹아붙은 듯 보이는 그들이, 사내들의 눈을 호린다."], ["Forth from the frigid mews come easily swinging carriages guided by short-legged coachmen in flaxen wigs, deep sunk into downy hammercloths, and up behind mount luscious Mercuries bearing sticks of state and wearing cocked hats broadwise, a spectacle for the angels.", "싸늘한 마구간에서는 아마빛 가발을 쓴 짧은 다리의 마부들이 모는 마차가 부드럽게 흔들리며 나오는데, 마부들은 푹신한 마부석 덮개에 깊숙이 파묻혀 있고, 뒤에는 탐스러운 머큐리들이 올라타 권위의 지팡이를 받쳐 들고 삼각모를 옆으로 비스듬히 눌러쓴 채, 천사들이나 볼 법한 장관을 이룬다."]], [["The Dedlock town house changes not externally, and hours pass before its exalted dullness is disturbed within.", "데드록 저택은 겉으로는 조금도 변하지 않고, 그 드높은 권태가 안에서 흐트러지기까지는 몇 시간이 흐른다."], ["But Volumnia the fair, being subject to the prevalent complaint of boredom and finding that disorder attacking her spirits with some virulence, ventures at length to repair to the library for change of scene.", "그러나 어여쁜 볼럼니아는 만연한 병폐인 지루함에 시달리고, 그 무질서가 제법 독하게 기분을 덮쳐오는 것을 느끼자, 마침내 기분 전환을 위해 서재로 발걸음을 옮기는 모험을 감행한다."], ["Her gentle tapping at the door producing no response, she opens it and peeps in; seeing no one there, takes possession.", "문을 살며시 두드려도 아무 대답이 없자, 그녀는 문을 열고 안을 들여다본다. 아무도 없는 것을 보고는 안으로 들어가 차지한다."]], [["The sprightly Dedlock is reputed, in that grass-grown city of the ancients, Bath, to be stimulated by an urgent curiosity which impels her on all convenient and inconvenient occasions to sidle about with a golden glass at her eye, peering into objects of every description.", "이 발랄한 데드록 여인은 풀이 무성한 옛 도시 바스에서, 편하든 불편하든 온갖 자리에서 황금 안경을 눈에 갖다 대고 모로 비집고 다니며 온갖 물건을 들여다보게 만드는 그 다급한 호기심에 휘둘린다는 평을 듣는다."], ["Certain it is that she avails herself of the present opportunity of hovering over her kinsman’s letters and papers like a bird, taking a short peck at this document and a blink with her head on one side at that document, and hopping about from table to table with her glass at her eye in an inquisitive and restless manner.", "분명한 것은, 그녀가 지금 이 기회를 틈타 친척의 편지와 서류 위를 새처럼 맴돌며, 이 문서를 쪼아보고 저 문서를 고개를 갸웃이 기울인 채 곁눈질하고, 안경을 눈에 댄 채 캐묻듯 안절부절못하며 탁자에서 탁자로 폴짝폴짝 옮겨 다닌다는 사실이다."], ["In the course of these researches she stumbles over something, and turning her glass in that direction, sees her kinsman lying on the ground like a felled tree.", "이렇게 뒤지던 중에 그녀는 무언가에 걸려 비틀거리고, 그쪽으로 안경을 돌리자, 친척이 베어 넘긴 나무처럼 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 모습을 본다."]], [["Volumnia’s pet little scream acquires a considerable augmentation of reality from this surprise, and the house is quickly in commotion.", "볼럼니아가 즐겨 내지르는 자그마한 비명이 이 놀라움에 힘입어 상당히 실감 나는 비명으로 부풀어 오르고, 집안은 순식간에 발칵 뒤집힌다."], ["Servants tear up and down stairs, bells are violently rung, doctors are sent for, and Lady Dedlock is sought in all directions, but not found.", "하인들이 계단을 정신없이 오르내리고, 종이 격렬하게 울리고, 의사를 부르러 사람이 달려가고, 데드록 마님을 사방으로 찾지만 보이지 않는다."], ["Nobody has seen or heard her since she last rang her bell.", "마지막으로 종을 울린 뒤로 그녀를 보거나 그녀의 기척을 들은 사람이 아무도 없다."], ["Her letter to Sir Leicester is discovered on her table, but it is doubtful yet whether he has not received another missive from another world requiring to be personally answered, and all the living languages, and all the dead, are as one to him.", "마님이 레스터 경에게 남긴 편지가 탁자 위에서 발견되지만, 정작 그가 또 다른 세상에서 보낸, 몸소 답해야 할 또 다른 전갈을 이미 받은 것은 아닌지 아직 알 수 없으니, 그에게는 살아 있는 온갖 언어도 죽어버린 온갖 언어도 다 매한가지다."]], [["They lay him down upon his bed, and chafe, and rub, and fan, and put ice to his head, and try every means of restoration.", "사람들은 그를 침대에 누이고, 문지르고, 비비고, 부채질하고, 머리에 얼음을 대고, 온갖 회복 수단을 시도한다."], ["Howbeit, the day has ebbed away, and it is night in his room before his stertorous breathing lulls or his fixed eyes show any consciousness of the candle that is occasionally passed before them.", "그래도 하루가 다 저물고 방 안에 밤이 깃들고 나서야 그의 그렁그렁한 숨소리가 가라앉고, 이따금 눈앞을 스치는 촛불을 향해 그 고정된 두 눈이 어렴풋한 의식을 비친다."], ["But when this change begins, it goes on; and by and by he nods or moves his eyes or even his hand in token that he hears and comprehends.", "그러나 이 변화가 시작되자 이어진다. 이윽고 그는 고개를 끄덕이거나 눈을 움직이거나 심지어 손을 움직여 듣고 알아듣는다는 표시를 한다."]], [["He fell down, this morning, a handsome stately gentleman, somewhat infirm, but of a fine presence, and with a well-filled face.", "오늘 아침 쓰러질 때만 해도 그는 다소 노쇠했으나 풍채가 훌륭하고 얼굴에 살이 통통히 오른, 잘생기고 위엄 있는 신사였다."], ["He lies upon his bed, an aged man with sunken cheeks, the decrepit shadow of himself.", "지금 침대에 누운 그는 뺨이 움푹 꺼진 노인이요, 제 모습의 노쇠한 그림자다."], ["His voice was rich and mellow and he had so long been thoroughly persuaded of the weight and import to mankind of any word he said that his words really had come to sound as if there were something in them.", "그의 목소리는 풍부하고 부드러웠고, 자신이 내뱉는 어떤 말이든 인류에게 무게와 의미가 있다고 너무 오래 굳게 확신해온 나머지, 그의 말은 정말로 그 안에 무언가가 들어 있는 양 들리기에 이르렀다."], ["But now he can only whisper, and what he whispers sounds like what it is—mere jumble and jargon.", "그러나 이제 그는 속삭일 수밖에 없고, 그 속삭임은 있는 그대로, 그저 뒤죽박죽 횡설수설로 들린다."]], [["His favourite and faithful housekeeper stands at his bedside.", "그가 아끼는 충직한 가정부가 침대맡에 서 있다."], ["It is the first act he notices, and he clearly derives pleasure from it.", "그가 알아챈 첫 번째 일이 바로 이것이고, 그는 분명히 거기서 위안을 얻는다."], ["After vainly trying to make himself understood in speech, he makes signs for a pencil.", "말로 자기 뜻을 전하려다 헛수고로 끝나자, 그는 연필을 달라는 시늉을 한다."], ["So inexpressively that they cannot at first understand him; it is his old housekeeper who makes out what he wants and brings in a slate.", "너무도 알아듣기 힘들게 해서 처음에는 그 뜻을 아무도 헤아리지 못한다. 그가 원하는 것을 알아채고 석판을 가져오는 사람은 바로 그 늙은 가정부다."]], [["After pausing for some time, he slowly scrawls upon it in a hand that is not his, “Chesney Wold?”", "한참 멈칫거린 뒤, 그는 제 것이 아닌 듯한 글씨로 천천히 그 위에 갈겨쓴다. \"체스니 월드?\""]], [["No, she tells him; he is in London.", "아니라고, 그녀가 그에게 일러준다."], ["He was taken ill in the library this morning.", "여기는 런던이라고. 오늘 아침 서재에서 발작을 일으켜 쓰러졌다고."], ["Right thankful she is that she happened to come to London and is able to attend upon him.", "마침 자기가 런던에 와 있어 그를 돌볼 수 있게 된 것이 정말로 다행이라고."]], [["“It is not an illness of any serious consequence, Sir Leicester.", "\"심각한 병이 아니랍니다, 레스터 경. 내일이면 훨씬 나아지실 거예요, 레스터 경."], ["You will be much better to-morrow, Sir Leicester.", "의사 선생님들이 다 그렇게 말씀하시는걸요.\""], ["All the gentlemen say so.” This, with the tears coursing down her fair old face.", "그렇게 말하는 동안 그녀의 고운 늙은 얼굴 위로 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내린다."]], [["After making a survey of the room and looking with particular attention all round the bed where the doctors stand, he writes, “My Lady.”", "방 안을 둘러보고, 의사들이 둘러선 침대 주위를 유난히 주의 깊게 살핀 뒤, 그는 적는다. \"마님.\""]], [["“My Lady went out, Sir Leicester, before you were taken ill, and don’t know of your illness yet.”", "\"마님은 외출하셨어요, 레스터 경. 어르신이 쓰러지시기 전에 나가셔서, 아직 어르신 일을 모르신답니다.\""]], [["He points again, in great agitation, at the two words.", "그는 몹시 동요하며 그 두 글자를 다시 가리킨다."], ["They all try to quiet him, but he points again with increased agitation.", "사람들이 모두 그를 진정시키려 하지만, 그는 더욱 격하게 동요하며 다시 가리킨다."], ["On their looking at one another, not knowing what to say, he takes the slate once more and writes “My Lady.", "무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 서로 얼굴만 쳐다보자, 그는 다시 한번 석판을 들고 적는다."], ["For God’s sake, where?”", "\"마님. 제발, 어디에?\""], ["And makes an imploring moan.", "그러고는 애원하듯 신음을 낸다."]], [["It is thought better that his old housekeeper should give him Lady Dedlock’s letter, the contents of which no one knows or can surmise.", "그 늙은 가정부가 그에게 데드록 마님의 편지를 건네주는 편이 낫겠다고 판단되는데, 그 내용은 아무도 모르고 짐작조차 못 한다."], ["She opens it for him and puts it out for his perusal.", "그녀가 편지를 펼쳐 그가 읽을 수 있게 내놓는다."], ["Having read it twice by a great effort, he turns it down so that it shall not be seen and lies moaning.", "그는 안간힘을 다해 두 번 읽고 나서, 보이지 않도록 편지를 엎어놓고 신음하며 누워 있다."], ["He passes into a kind of relapse or into a swoon, and it is an hour before he opens his eyes, reclining on his faithful and attached old servant’s arm.", "그는 다시 도지는 듯한 상태로, 혹은 혼절 상태로 빠져들고, 충직하고 정 깊은 늙은 하인의 팔에 기댄 채 다시 눈을 뜨기까지 한 시간이 걸린다."], ["The doctors know that he is best with her, and when not actively engaged about him, stand aloof.", "의사들은 그가 그녀 곁에 있을 때 가장 편안하다는 것을 알기에, 그를 직접 돌볼 일이 없을 때는 멀찍이 물러서 있다."]], [["The slate comes into requisition again, but the word he wants to write he cannot remember.", "석판이 다시 쓰임을 받지만, 그가 적고 싶은 말이 떠오르지 않는다."], ["His anxiety, his eagerness, and affliction at this pass are pitiable to behold.", "이 지경에 이르러 그의 불안과 조바심과 고뇌는 차마 보기 딱하다."], ["It seems as if he must go mad in the necessity he feels for haste and the inability under which he labours of expressing to do what or to fetch whom.", "서둘러야 한다는 절박함과, 무엇을 하라거나 누구를 데려오라거나 표현해낼 수 없는 무력함 속에서 그는 미쳐버릴 것만 같다."], ["He has written the letter B, and there stopped.", "그는 B라는 글자를 적고는 거기서 멈춘다."], ["Of a sudden, in the height of his misery, he puts Mr. before it.", "별안간, 고통이 절정에 달한 순간, 그는 그 앞에 '미스터'를 붙인다."], ["The old housekeeper suggests Bucket.", "늙은 가정부가 버킷을 떠올려 말한다."], ["Thank heaven!", "천만다행이다!"], ["That’s his meaning.", "바로 그 뜻이다."]], [["Mr. Bucket is found to be downstairs, by appointment.", "버킷 씨는 약속대로 아래층에 있는 것으로 확인된다."], ["Shall he come up?", "올라오라고 할까요?"]], [["There is no possibility of misconstruing Sir Leicester’s burning wish to see him or the desire he signifies to have the room cleared of every one but the housekeeper.", "그를 보고 싶어 하는 레스터 경의 불타는 소망이나, 가정부 한 사람만 남기고 방 안의 모두를 내보내고 싶어 하는 그의 뜻은 도무지 잘못 해석할 여지가 없다."], ["It is speedily done, and Mr. Bucket appears.", "그것은 신속히 이루어지고, 버킷 씨가 나타난다."], ["Of all men upon earth, Sir Leicester seems fallen from his high estate to place his sole trust and reliance upon this man.", "세상 모든 사람 가운데, 레스터 경은 자신의 드높은 지위에서 굴러떨어져 오직 이 사내에게만 신뢰와 의지를 두는 듯하다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I’m sorry to see you like this.", "\"준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 이런 모습을 뵙게 되어 마음이 안 좋습니다."], ["I hope you’ll cheer up.", "부디 기운 내시기를 바랍니다."], ["I’m sure you will, on account of the family credit.”", "가문의 명예를 생각하시면 분명 그러시겠지요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester puts her letter in his hands and looks intently in his face while he reads it.", "레스터 경이 그녀의 편지를 그의 손에 쥐여주고, 그가 읽는 동안 그의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라본다."], ["A new intelligence comes into Mr.", "버킷 씨가 읽어 내려갈수록 그의 눈에 새로운 깨달음이 어린다."], ["Bucket’s eye as he reads on; with one hook of his finger, while that eye is still glancing over the words, he indicates, “Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I understand you.”", "그 눈이 아직 글자를 훑는 사이, 그는 손가락을 한 번 까딱하여 일러준다. \"준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 무슨 말씀이신지 알겠습니다.\""]], [["Sir Leicester writes upon the slate.", "레스터 경이 석판에 쓴다."], ["“Full forgiveness.", "\"전부 용서한다."], ["Find—” Mr.", "찾아—\""], ["Bucket stops his hand.", "버킷 씨가 그의 손을 멈춘다."]], [["“Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I’ll find her.", "\"준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 제가 마님을 찾아내겠습니다."], ["But my search after her must be begun out of hand.", "다만 수색은 지금 당장 시작해야 합니다."], ["Not a minute must be lost.”", "일 분도 허비해선 안 됩니다.\""]], [["With the quickness of thought, he follows Sir Leicester Dedlock’s look towards a little box upon a table.", "생각만큼 잽싸게, 그는 탁자 위 작은 상자를 향한 레스터 데드록 경의 눈길을 좇는다."]], [["“Bring it here, Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet?", "\"이걸 이리 가져올까요, 준남작 레스터 데드록 경?"], ["Certainly.", "그러죠."], ["Open it with one of these here keys?", "여기 이 열쇠 중 하나로 열까요?"], ["Certainly.", "그러죠."], ["The littlest key?", "제일 작은 열쇠로?"], ["TO be sure.", "알겠습니다."], ["Take the notes out?", "지폐를 꺼낼까요?"], ["So I will.", "그러겠습니다."], ["Count ’em?", "세어볼까요?"], ["That’s soon done.", "금방 끝납니다."], ["Twenty and thirty’s fifty, and twenty’s seventy, and fifty’s one twenty, and forty’s one sixty.", "이십에 삼십이면 오십, 다시 이십이면 칠십, 오십이면 백이십, 사십이면 백육십."], ["Take ’em for expenses?", "경비로 가져가라고요?"], ["That I’ll do, and render an account of course.", "그렇게 하고, 물론 나중에 셈을 보고드리겠습니다."], ["Don’t spare money?", "돈을 아끼지 말라고요?"], ["No I won’t.”", "아끼지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["The velocity and certainty of Mr. Bucket’s interpretation on all these heads is little short of miraculous.", "이 모든 대목을 풀어내는 버킷 씨의 속도와 확신은 거의 기적에 가깝다."], ["Mrs. Rouncewell, who holds the light, is giddy with the swiftness of his eyes and hands as he starts up, furnished for his journey.", "불을 들고 있는 라운스웰 부인은 그가 여정에 나설 채비를 갖추고 벌떡 일어서며 보이는 눈과 손의 날렵함에 어질어질해진다."]], [["“You’re George’s mother, old lady; that’s about what you are, I believe?”", "\"댁이 조지의 어머니로군요, 노부인. 내 짐작에 그렇지 싶습니다만?\""], ["says Mr. Bucket aside, with his hat already on and buttoning his coat.", "버킷 씨가 벌써 모자를 쓰고 외투 단추를 채우며 한쪽으로 비켜 말한다."]], [["“Yes, sir, I am his distressed mother.”", "\"네, 그래요, 제가 그 애의 애끓는 어미랍니다.\""]], [["“So I thought, according to what he mentioned to me just now.", "\"그럴 줄 알았습니다. 좀 전에 그가 내게 한 말로 보아서요."], ["Well, then, I’ll tell you something.", "그럼, 한 가지 일러드리지요. 더는 애태우실 것 없습니다."], ["You needn’t be distressed no more.", "아드님은 아무 문제 없어요."], ["Your son’s all right.", "자, 이제 우시면 안 됩니다."], ["Now, don’t you begin a-crying, because what you’ve got to do is to take care of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and you won’t do that by crying.", "댁이 해야 할 일은 준남작 레스터 데드록 경을 보살피는 것인데, 운다고 그 일이 되는 게 아니니까요. 아드님으로 말하자면, 아무 문제 없다니까요."], ["As to your son, he’s all right, I tell you; and he sends his loving duty, and hoping you’re the same.", "그리고 사랑을 담아 안부를 전하면서, 어머님도 무탈하시기를 바란다더군요. 그는 떳떳하게 풀려났습니다."], ["He’s discharged honourable; that’s about what HE is; with no more imputation on his character than there is on yours, and yours is a tidy one, I’LL bet a pound.", "바로 그렇습니다. 그의 인품에 흠 잡힐 데가 없는 것이, 댁의 인품에 흠 잡힐 데 없는 것과 똑같지요."], ["You may trust me, for I took your son.", "그리고 댁의 인품이 야무지다는 데 일 파운드 걸지요."], ["He conducted himself in a game way, too, on that occasion; and he’s a fine-made man, and you’re a fine-made old lady, and you’re a mother and son, the pair of you, as might be showed for models in a caravan.", "저를 믿으셔도 됩니다. 제가 아드님을 잡아들인 사람이니까요."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, what you’ve trusted to me I’ll go through with.", "그때도 그는 사내답게 처신했고요. 그는 잘 빠진 사내고, 댁은 잘 빠진 노부인이니, 두 분 모자는 천막 흥행에 본보기로 내걸어도 될 만합니다."], ["Don’t you be afraid of my turning out of my way, right or left, or taking a sleep, or a wash, or a shave till I have found what I go in search of.", "준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 제게 맡기신 일은 끝까지 해내겠습니다. 제가 좌로든 우로든 길을 벗어나거나, 한숨 자거나, 씻거나, 면도하거나 하는 일은 찾으러 나선 것을 손에 넣기 전까지는 없을 테니 염려 마십시오."], ["Say everything as is kind and forgiving on your part?", "경의 다정하고 너그러운 뜻을 다 전하라고요?"], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, I will.", "준남작 레스터 데드록 경, 그러겠습니다."], ["And I wish you better, and these family affairs smoothed over—as, Lord, many other family affairs equally has been, and equally will be, to the end of time.”", "그리고 어서 쾌차하시고 이 집안일도 잘 수습되기를 바랍니다. 아이고, 다른 숱한 집안일이 그렇게 수습되어 왔고, 또 세상 끝날 때까지 그렇게 수습될 테니까요.\""]], [["With this peroration, Mr. Bucket, buttoned up, goes quietly out, looking steadily before him as if he were already piercing the night in quest of the fugitive.", "이 한바탕 연설을 마치고, 단단히 단추를 채운 버킷 씨는 마치 벌써 밤을 꿰뚫고 달아난 여인을 좇기라도 하는 듯 앞만 똑바로 응시하며 조용히 밖으로 나간다."]], [["His first step is to take himself to Lady Dedlock’s rooms and look all over them for any trifling indication that may help him.", "그가 맨 먼저 한 일은 데드록 마님의 방으로 가서 도움이 될 만한 사소한 단서라도 없는지 구석구석 살피는 것이다."], ["The rooms are in darkness now; and to see Mr.", "방은 이제 어둠에 잠겨 있다."], ["Bucket with a wax-light in his hand, holding it above his head and taking a sharp mental inventory of the many delicate objects so curiously at variance with himself, would be to see a sight—which nobody DOES see, as he is particular to lock himself in.", "그리고 밀랍 초를 손에 들고 머리 위로 치켜든 채, 자기와는 묘하게 어울리지 않는 그 숱한 섬세한 물건들을 날카롭게 머릿속으로 헤아리는 버킷 씨를 본다면 그것은 진풍경일 텐데, 그가 안에서 문을 잠그는 데 신경을 쓴 까닭에 그 광경은 아무도 보지 못한다."]], [["“A spicy boudoir, this,” says Mr.", "\"운치 있는 규방이로군.\""], ["Bucket, who feels in a manner furbished up in his French by the blow of the morning.", "버킷 씨가 말한다. 오늘 아침의 충격으로 그의 프랑스어 솜씨가 어쩐지 닦여나간 기분이다."], ["“Must have cost a sight of money.", "\"돈깨나 들었겠어."], ["Rum articles to cut away from, these; she must have been hard put to it!”", "두고 떠나기 아까운 진귀한 물건들이군. 마음 단단히 먹어야 했을 거야!\""]], [["Opening and shutting table-drawers and looking into caskets and jewel-cases, he sees the reflection of himself in various mirrors, and moralizes thereon.", "탁자 서랍을 여닫고 작은 함과 보석 상자를 들여다보다가, 그는 여러 거울에 비친 제 모습을 보고는 거기에 빗대어 인생을 곱씹는다."]], [["“One might suppose I was a-moving in the fashionable circles and getting myself up for Almack’s,”", "\"이러다 내가 사교계라도 드나들면서 올맥스 무도회 채비라도 하는 줄 알겠어.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말한다."], ["“I begin to think I must be a swell in the Guards without knowing it.”", "\"이러다 나도 모르는 새에 근위대 멋쟁이라도 된 줄 알겠는걸.\""]], [["Ever looking about, he has opened a dainty little chest in an inner drawer.", "연신 여기저기 둘러보던 그는 안쪽 서랍에서 앙증맞은 작은 함을 열었다."], ["His great hand, turning over some gloves which it can scarcely feel, they are so light and soft within it, comes upon a white handkerchief.", "너무 가볍고 부드러워 손에 잡히는 감조차 거의 없는 장갑 몇 짝을 그의 큼직한 손이 뒤적이다가, 흰 손수건 한 장에 닿는다."]], [["“Hum!", "\"흠!"], ["Let’s have a look at YOU,”", "어디 너를 좀 보자.\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, putting down the light.", "버킷 씨가 불을 내려놓으며 말한다."], ["“What should YOU be kept by yourself for?", "\"왜 너만 따로 간직했을까?"], ["What’s YOUR motive?", "무슨 까닭이지?"], ["Are you her ladyship’s property, or somebody else’s?", "넌 마님 것이냐, 아니면 다른 누구 것이냐?"], ["You’ve got a mark upon you somewheres or another, I suppose?”", "어딘가에 표시가 박혀 있겠지, 아마?\""]], [["He finds it as he speaks, “Esther Summerson.”", "말하는 사이 그는 그것을 찾아낸다. \"에스더 서머슨.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["says Mr. Bucket, pausing, with his finger at his ear.", "버킷 씨가 손가락을 귀에 댄 채 멈칫하며 말한다."], ["“Come, I’ll take YOU.”", "\"그래, 너를 가져가야겠다.\""]], [["He completes his observations as quietly and carefully as he has carried them on, leaves everything else precisely as he found it, glides away after some five minutes in all, and passes into the street.", "그는 시작할 때처럼 조용하고 신중하게 관찰을 마무리하고, 나머지 것들은 발견한 그대로 한 치도 어긋남 없이 두고서, 통틀어 오 분쯤 지나 슬며시 빠져나가 거리로 나선다."], ["With a glance upward at the dimly lighted windows of Sir Leicester’s room, he sets off, full-swing, to the nearest coach-stand, picks out the horse for his money, and directs to be driven to the shooting gallery. Mr.", "레스터 경의 방, 희미하게 불 켜진 창문을 한번 올려다보고는, 가장 가까운 마차 정류장으로 전속력으로 달려가 자기 돈값에 맞는 말을 골라내고는, 사격장으로 몰아달라고 이른다."], ["Bucket does not claim to be a scientific judge of horses, but he lays out a little money on the principal events in that line, and generally sums up his knowledge of the subject in the remark that when he sees a horse as can go, he knows him.", "버킷 씨가 말에 관한 전문가를 자처하는 것은 아니지만, 그쪽 주요 경주에 푼돈쯤은 걸어보는 터라, 그 방면의 식견을 대개 한마디로 간추린다. 잘 달리는 말을 보면 그는 그놈을 알아본다는 것이다."]], [["His knowledge is not at fault in the present instance.", "이번 경우에 그의 식견은 틀리지 않는다."], ["Clattering over the stones at a dangerous pace, yet thoughtfully bringing his keen eyes to bear on every slinking creature whom he passes in the midnight streets, and even on the lights in upper windows where people are going or gone to bed, and on all the turnings that he rattles by, and alike on the heavy sky, and on the earth where the snow lies thin—for something may present itself to assist him, anywhere—he dashes to his destination at such a speed that when he stops the horse half smothers him in a cloud of steam.", "위험천만한 속도로 돌바닥 위를 덜커덩거리며 달리면서도, 자정 거리에서 스쳐 지나가는 살금살금 움직이는 온갖 것들에, 심지어 사람들이 자리에 들고 있거나 이미 든 위층 창의 불빛에, 그리고 덜컹덜컹 지나치는 온갖 길모퉁이에, 또 무거운 하늘에도, 눈이 엷게 깔린 땅바닥에도 그 예리한 눈을 빈틈없이 던지며—어디서든 도움이 될 무언가가 눈에 띌지 모르니—그는 목적지를 향해 어찌나 빠르게 내달리는지, 멈춰 섰을 때 말이 뿜어내는 김 구름에 그가 반쯤 파묻힌다."]], [["“Unbear him half a moment to freshen him up, and I’ll be back.”", "\"잠깐만 마구를 풀어 한숨 돌리게 해줘요. 곧 돌아올 테니.\""]], [["He runs up the long wooden entry and finds the trooper smoking his pipe.", "그는 길게 뻗은 나무 통로를 뛰어올라가, 파이프 담배를 피우고 있는 기병을 찾아낸다."]], [["“I thought I should, George, after what you have gone through, my lad.", "\"이럴 줄 알았네, 조지. 자네가 겪은 일을 생각하면 말이야, 이 사람아."], ["I haven’t a word to spare.", "한가하게 말 나눌 틈이 없네."], ["Now, honour!", "자, 약속이네!"], ["All to save a woman.", "다 어떤 여인을 살리려는 일일세."], ["Miss Summerson that was here when Gridley died—that was the name, I know—all right—where does she live?”", "그리들리가 죽을 때 여기 와 있던 미스 서머슨—그게 이름이었지, 나도 알아—맞아—그분이 어디 사나?\""]], [["The trooper has just come from there and gives him the address, near Oxford Street.", "기병은 마침 그곳에서 막 돌아온 참이라, 옥스퍼드 거리 근처의 주소를 일러준다."]], [["“You won’t repent it, George.", "\"후회할 일 없을 걸세, 조지."], ["Good night!”", "잘 있게!\""]], [["He is off again, with an impression of having seen Phil sitting by the frosty fire staring at him open-mouthed, and gallops away again, and gets out in a cloud of steam again.", "그는 입을 헤벌린 채 자기를 멍하니 바라보며 서리 낀 난로 곁에 앉아 있던 필을 본 듯한 인상을 품고서 다시 떠나, 또다시 말을 몰아 달려가, 또다시 김 구름 속에서 내린다."]], [["Mr. Jarndyce, the only person up in the house, is just going to bed, rises from his book on hearing the rapid ringing at the bell, and comes down to the door in his dressing-gown.", "집 안에서 유일하게 깨어 있던 잔다이스 씨는 막 자리에 들려던 참인데, 급하게 울리는 종소리를 듣고 책에서 눈을 떼 일어나, 잠옷 바람으로 문간까지 내려온다."]], [["“Don’t be alarmed, sir.”", "\"놀라지 마십시오, 선생.\""], ["In a moment his visitor is confidential with him in the hall, has shut the door, and stands with his hand upon the lock.", "어느새 그의 방문객이 현관에서 그와 은밀히 이야기를 나누며 문을 닫고, 자물쇠에 손을 얹은 채 서 있다."], ["“I’ve had the pleasure of seeing you before.", "\"전에 한번 뵌 적이 있지요."], ["Inspector Bucket.", "버킷 경위입니다."], ["Look at that handkerchief, sir, Miss Esther Summerson’s.", "이 손수건을 보십시오, 선생. 미스 에스더 서머슨의 것입니다."], ["Found it myself put away in a drawer of Lady Dedlock’s, quarter of an hour ago.", "십오 분 전에 데드록 마님의 서랍에 따로 넣어둔 것을 제가 직접 찾아냈습니다."], ["Not a moment to lose.", "한시도 지체할 수 없습니다."], ["Matter of life or death.", "생사가 걸린 일입니다."], ["You know Lady Dedlock?”", "데드록 마님을 아십니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"압니다.\""]], [["“There has been a discovery there to-day.", "\"오늘 그 댁에서 어떤 사실이 드러났습니다."], ["Family affairs have come out.", "집안일이 불거진 거지요."], ["Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, has had a fit—apoplexy or paralysis—and couldn’t be brought to, and precious time has been lost.", "준남작 레스터 데드록 경이 발작을 일으켰습니다—뇌졸중인지 마비인지—깨어나질 못해서 귀한 시간을 허비했습니다."], ["Lady Dedlock disappeared this afternoon and left a letter for him that looks bad.", "데드록 마님은 오늘 오후에 자취를 감추셨는데, 그분에게 좋지 않아 보이는 편지를 한 장 남겼습니다."], ["Run your eye over it.", "한번 훑어보십시오."], ["Here it is!”", "여기 있습니다!\""]], [["Mr. Jarndyce, having read it, asks him what he thinks.", "잔다이스 씨가 그것을 다 읽고는, 어떻게 생각하느냐고 그에게 묻는다."]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠습니다."], ["It looks like suicide.", "자살처럼 보입니다."], ["Anyways, there’s more and more danger, every minute, of its drawing to that.", "어쨌든 시간이 갈수록 그쪽으로 치달을 위험이 점점 커집니다."], ["I’d give a hundred pound an hour to have got the start of the present time. Now, Mr.", "지금보다 한 시간만 일찍 시작할 수 있었다면 한 시간에 백 파운드라도 내놓겠습니다."], ["Jarndyce, I am employed by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, to follow her and find her, to save her and take her his forgiveness.", "자, 잔다이스 선생, 저는 준남작 레스터 데드록 경의 의뢰로 그분을 뒤쫓아 찾아내, 살려서 그분의 용서를 전하려는 것입니다."], ["I have money and full power, but I want something else.", "돈도 있고 전권도 있습니다만, 한 가지가 더 필요합니다."], ["I want Miss Summerson.”", "미스 서머슨이 필요합니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "잔다이스 씨가 곤혹스러운 목소리로 되뇐다."], ["Jarndyce in a troubled voice repeats, “Miss Summerson?”", "\"미스 서머슨이라고요?\""]], [["“Now, Mr. Jarndyce”—Mr.", "\"자, 잔다이스 선생\"—버킷 씨는 줄곧 더없이 주의 깊게 그의 얼굴을 읽어왔다—\"인정 많은 마음을 지닌 신사로 알고 말씀드리는 겁니다."], ["Bucket has read his face with the greatest attention all along—“I speak to you as a gentleman of a humane heart, and under such pressing circumstances as don’t often happen.", "그것도 흔치 않게 다급한 상황이라서요. 지체가 위험했던 적이 언제고 있었다면 바로 지금이 그렇고, 그 지체를 자초한 일로 나중에 자신을 도저히 용서 못 할 때가 언제고 있다면 바로 지금입니다."], ["If ever delay was dangerous, it’s dangerous now; and if ever you couldn’t afterwards forgive yourself for causing it, this is the time.", "데드록 마님이 사라진 뒤로, 한 시간에 적어도 백 파운드씩이라 말씀드린 그 여덟아홉 시간이 이미 허비됐습니다."], ["Eight or ten hours, worth, as I tell you, a hundred pound apiece at least, have been lost since Lady Dedlock disappeared. I am charged to find her.", "저는 그분을 찾으라는 임무를 맡았습니다."], ["I am Inspector Bucket.", "저는 버킷 경위입니다."], ["Besides all the rest that’s heavy on her, she has upon her, as she believes, suspicion of murder.", "그분을 짓누르는 다른 모든 것에 더해, 그분은 자기 생각에 살인 혐의까지 뒤집어쓰고 있습니다."], ["If I follow her alone, she, being in ignorance of what Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, has communicated to me, may be driven to desperation.", "제가 혼자 뒤쫓으면, 그분은 준남작 레스터 데드록 경이 제게 전한 바를 모르는 터라 절망에 내몰릴지도 모릅니다."], ["But if I follow her in company with a young lady, answering to the description of a young lady that she has a tenderness for—I ask no question, and I say no more than that—she will give me credit for being friendly.", "하지만 제가 어떤 젊은 아가씨와 함께, 그분이 애틋하게 여기는 한 젊은 아가씨의 인상과 들어맞는 분과 함께 뒤쫓으면—저는 아무것도 묻지 않고, 그 이상은 말하지 않겠습니다—그분은 저를 우호적인 사람으로 믿어줄 겁니다."], ["Let me come up with her and be able to have the hold upon her of putting that young lady for’ard, and I’ll save her and prevail with her if she is alive.", "제가 그분을 따라잡아, 그 젊은 아가씨를 앞세워 그분을 붙잡을 수 있게 해주십시오. 그러면 그분이 살아 있는 한, 제가 그분을 구하고 설득해내겠습니다."], ["Let me come up with her alone—a hard matter—and I’ll do my best, but I don’t answer for what the best may be.", "저 혼자서 따라잡으라 하시면—어려운 일이지요—최선을 다하겠습니다만, 그 최선이 어떨지는 장담 못 합니다."], ["Time flies; it’s getting on for one o’clock.", "시간이 흐릅니다. 한 시가 다 되어 갑니다."], ["When one strikes, there’s another hour gone, and it’s worth a thousand pound now instead of a hundred.”", "한 시 종이 울리면 또 한 시간이 가는 것이고, 이제 그 한 시간은 백 파운드가 아니라 천 파운드짜리입니다.\""]], [["This is all true, and the pressing nature of the case cannot be questioned.", "이 모든 것이 사실이고, 사안의 다급한 성격은 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["Mr. Jarndyce begs him to remain there while he speaks to Miss Summerson.", "잔다이스 씨는 자기가 미스 서머슨에게 이야기하는 동안 그에게 여기 있어 달라고 청한다."], ["Mr. Bucket says he will, but acting on his usual principle, does no such thing, following upstairs instead and keeping his man in sight.", "버킷 씨는 그러겠다고 하지만, 평소 원칙에 따라 그러지 않고, 대신 계단을 따라 올라가 그 사람을 시야에 둔다."], ["So he remains, dodging and lurking about in the gloom of the staircase while they confer. In a very little time Mr.", "그렇게 그는 두 사람이 의논하는 동안 계단의 어둠 속에서 요리조리 몸을 숨기고 도사린 채 머문다."], ["Jarndyce comes down and tells him that Miss Summerson will join him directly and place herself under his protection to accompany him where he pleases. Mr.", "얼마 안 가 잔다이스 씨가 내려와, 미스 서머슨이 곧 그와 함께하여 그가 가자는 곳 어디든 동행하도록 그의 보호 아래 몸을 맡기겠다고 일러준다."], ["Bucket, satisfied, expresses high approval and awaits her coming at the door.", "만족한 버킷 씨는 크게 찬동을 표하며 문간에서 그녀가 오기를 기다린다."]], [["There he mounts a high tower in his mind and looks out far and wide.", "거기서 그는 마음속으로 높은 망루에 올라 멀리, 넓게 내다본다."], ["Many solitary figures he perceives creeping through the streets; many solitary figures out on heaths, and roads, and lying under haystacks.", "거리를 따라 살금살금 움직이는 외로운 인영을 그는 여럿 알아본다. 황야와 길 위에 나와 있고, 건초더미 밑에 누워 있는 외로운 인영도 여럿이다."], ["But the figure that he seeks is not among them.", "그러나 그가 찾는 인영은 그들 가운데 없다."], ["Other solitaries he perceives, in nooks of bridges, looking over; and in shadowed places down by the river’s level; and a dark, dark, shapeless object drifting with the tide, more solitary than all, clings with a drowning hold on his attention.", "다리 후미진 곳에서 아래를 내려다보고 있는, 강물 높이까지 내려간 어둑한 자리에 있는 또 다른 외로운 이들도 그는 알아본다. 그리고 그 무엇보다 외로이, 물살에 떠밀려가는 검고 검은, 형체 없는 물체 하나가 빠져 죽어가는 손길로 그의 주의를 움켜쥔다."]], [["Where is she? Living or dead, where is she?", "그녀는 어디 있는가?"], ["If, as he folds the handkerchief and carefully puts it up, it were able with an enchanted power to bring before him the place where she found it and the night-landscape near the cottage where it covered the little child, would he descry her there?", "살았든 죽었든, 그녀는 어디 있는가? 손수건을 접어 조심스레 갈무리하는 지금, 만약 그것이 마법의 힘으로 그녀가 그것을 주운 장소와, 그것으로 어린아이를 덮어주었던 그 오두막 근처의 밤 풍경을 그의 눈앞에 불러올 수 있다면, 그는 거기서 그녀를 알아볼 수 있을까?"], ["On the waste where the brick-kilns are burning with a pale blue flare, where the straw-roofs of the wretched huts in which the bricks are made are being scattered by the wind, where the clay and water are hard frozen and the mill in which the gaunt blind horse goes round all day looks like an instrument of human torture—traversing this deserted, blighted spot there is a lonely figure with the sad world to itself, pelted by the snow and driven by the wind, and cast out, it would seem, from all companionship.", "벽돌 가마가 파리한 푸른 불꽃으로 타오르고, 벽돌을 빚어내는 초라한 오두막의 짚지붕이 바람에 흩날리고, 진흙과 물이 꽝꽝 얼어붙고, 비쩍 마른 눈먼 말이 온종일 돌리는 방아가 흡사 인간을 고문하는 기구처럼 보이는 그 황무지—이 버려지고 메마른 곳을 가로질러, 슬픈 세상을 홀로 지닌 채 눈발에 두들겨 맞고 바람에 떠밀리며, 모든 동행에게서 내쫓긴 듯한 외로운 인영 하나가 있다. 그것 또한 여인의 모습이다."], ["It is the figure of a woman, too; but it is miserably dressed, and no such clothes ever came through the hall and out at the great door of the Dedlock mansion. Esther’s Narrative", "그러나 비참한 차림새이니, 그런 옷이 데드록 저택의 현관을 지나 그 큰 문을 나선 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["I had gone to bed and fallen asleep when my guardian knocked at the door of my room and begged me to get up directly.", "잠자리에 들어 잠이 들었는데 후견인이 제 방문을 두드리며 곧장 일어나달라고 했습니다."], ["On my hurrying to speak to him and learn what had happened, he told me, after a word or two of preparation, that there had been a discovery at Sir Leicester Dedlock’s.", "무슨 일이냐고 여쭙고 까닭을 알아보려 서둘러 나가니, 후견인은 마음의 준비를 시키는 말을 한두 마디 건넨 뒤, 레스터 데드록 경의 댁에서 어떤 사실이 드러났다고 말했습니다."], ["That my mother had fled, that a person was now at our door who was empowered to convey to her the fullest assurances of affectionate protection and forgiveness if he could possibly find her, and that I was sought for to accompany him in the hope that my entreaties might prevail upon her if his failed.", "어머니가 달아났다는 것, 만에 하나라도 찾아낼 수만 있다면 애정 어린 보호와 용서를 더없이 굳건히 다짐하라는 권한을 받은 사람이 지금 우리 집 문 앞에 와 있다는 것, 그리고 그의 간청이 통하지 않을 때 제 간청이라면 어머니의 마음을 돌릴 수 있으리라는 기대로 제가 그를 따라가주기를 바란다는 것이었습니다."], ["Something to this general purpose I made out, but I was thrown into such a tumult of alarm, and hurry and distress, that in spite of every effort I could make to subdue my agitation, I did not seem, to myself, fully to recover my right mind until hours had passed.", "대략 이런 취지의 말을 알아듣긴 했으나, 저는 경악과 다급함과 비탄의 소용돌이에 휩싸여, 동요를 가라앉히려 안간힘을 다 썼는데도, 몇 시간이 지나고 나서야 비로소 온전히 제정신을 되찾은 듯했습니다."]], [["But I dressed and wrapped up expeditiously without waking Charley or any one and went down to Mr. Bucket, who was the person entrusted with the secret.", "그래도 저는 찰리든 누구든 깨우지 않고 잽싸게 옷을 입고 몸을 둘러싼 뒤, 그 비밀을 맡은 사람인 버킷 씨에게로 내려갔습니다."], ["In taking me to him my guardian told me this, and also explained how it was that he had come to think of me. Mr.", "후견인은 저를 그에게 데려가며 이 사실을 일러주었고, 어쩌다 제 생각이 떠올랐는지도 설명해주었습니다."], ["Bucket, in a low voice, by the light of my guardian’s candle, read to me in the hall a letter that my mother had left upon her table; and I suppose within ten minutes of my having been aroused I was sitting beside him, rolling swiftly through the streets.", "버킷 씨는 후견인의 촛불 빛에 기대어 낮은 목소리로, 어머니가 탁자 위에 남겨두고 간 편지를 현관에서 제게 읽어주었습니다. 그리고 제가 잠에서 깨어난 지 십 분도 채 되지 않아, 저는 그의 곁에 앉아 거리를 빠르게 굴러가고 있었던 것 같습니다."]], [["His manner was very keen, and yet considerate when he explained to me that a great deal might depend on my being able to answer, without confusion, a few questions that he wished to ask me.", "그의 태도는 무척 날카로우면서도 사려 깊었으니, 자기가 묻고 싶은 몇 가지 질문에 제가 혼란 없이 대답할 수 있느냐에 많은 것이 달려 있을지도 모른다고 설명할 때 그랬습니다."], ["These were, chiefly, whether I had had much communication with my mother (to whom he only referred as Lady Dedlock), when and where I had spoken with her last, and how she had become possessed of my handkerchief.", "질문은 주로 이런 것이었습니다. 어머니(그는 데드록 마님이라고만 칭했습니다)와 많은 이야기를 나누었는지, 마지막으로 이야기한 것이 언제 어디서였는지, 어머니가 어떻게 제 손수건을 갖게 되었는지."], ["When I had satisfied him on these points, he asked me particularly to consider—taking time to think—whether within my knowledge there was any one, no matter where, in whom she might be at all likely to confide under circumstances of the last necessity.", "이 점들에 대해 제가 그를 흡족하게 해주자, 그는 특별히—시간을 두고 잘 생각해서—제가 아는 사람 가운데 어디에 있든, 막다른 궁지에 몰린 처지에서 어머니가 속마음을 털어놓을 만한 이가 조금이라도 있는지 곰곰이 생각해보라고 했습니다."], ["I could think of no one but my guardian.", "후견인 말고는 떠오르는 이가 없었습니다."], ["But by and by I mentioned Mr. Boythorn.", "하지만 이윽고 저는 보이손 씨를 언급했습니다."], ["He came into my mind as connected with his old chivalrous manner of mentioning my mother’s name and with what my guardian had informed me of his engagement to her sister and his unconscious connexion with her unhappy story.", "그는 예전에 기사도적인 태도로 어머니의 이름을 입에 올렸던 일, 그리고 후견인이 일러준 그가 어머니의 언니와 약혼했었다는 사실, 또 그가 자기도 모르는 사이에 어머니의 불행한 사연에 얽혀 있다는 사실과 연결되어 제 마음에 떠올랐습니다."]], [["My companion had stopped the driver while we held this conversation, that we might the better hear each other.", "이 대화를 나누는 동안 제 동행은 서로의 말을 더 잘 들으려고 마부를 멈춰 세웠습니다."], ["He now told him to go on again and said to me, after considering within himself for a few moments, that he had made up his mind how to proceed.", "이제 그는 다시 가자고 마부에게 이르고는, 잠시 속으로 궁리한 끝에 어떻게 할지 마음을 정했다고 제게 말했습니다."], ["He was quite willing to tell me what his plan was, but I did not feel clear enough to understand it.", "자기 계획이 무엇인지 기꺼이 알려주겠다고 했으나, 저는 그것을 이해할 만큼 머리가 맑지 않았습니다."]], [["We had not driven very far from our lodgings when we stopped in a by-street at a public-looking place lighted up with gas.", "숙소에서 그리 멀리 가지 않아 우리는 가스등으로 환히 밝혀진, 술집처럼 보이는 곳 앞의 골목길에 멈췄습니다."], ["Mr. Bucket took me in and sat me in an arm-chair by a bright fire.", "버킷 씨는 저를 안으로 데리고 들어가 환하게 타오르는 난롯가의 안락의자에 앉혔습니다."], ["It was now past one, as I saw by the clock against the wall.", "벽에 걸린 시계로 보니 이제 한 시가 지난 시각이었습니다."], ["Two police officers, looking in their perfectly neat uniform not at all like people who were up all night, were quietly writing at a desk; and the place seemed very quiet altogether, except for some beating and calling out at distant doors underground, to which nobody paid any attention.", "더없이 말끔한 제복 차림으로 밤새 깨어 있던 사람들 같지 않은 경찰관 두 명이 책상에서 조용히 무언가를 쓰고 있었습니다. 지하 어딘가의 먼 문에서 두드리고 외치는 소리가 들려왔으나 아무도 거기에 관심을 두지 않았고, 그 소리만 빼면 그곳은 전체적으로 무척 고요해 보였습니다."]], [["A third man in uniform, whom Mr. Bucket called and to whom he whispered his instructions, went out; and then the two others advised together while one wrote from Mr.", "버킷 씨가 부르더니 귓속말로 지시를 내린 또 다른 제복 차림의 사내가 밖으로 나갔습니다."], ["Bucket’s subdued dictation. It was a description of my mother that they were busy with, for Mr.", "그러자 나머지 두 사람이 함께 상의했고, 그중 하나가 버킷 씨가 나직이 불러주는 대로 받아 적었습니다."], ["Bucket brought it to me when it was done and read it in a whisper.", "그들이 분주히 매달린 것은 어머니의 인상착의였으니, 버킷 씨가 다 되자 그것을 제게 가져와 속삭이듯 읽어주었기 때문입니다."], ["It was very accurate indeed.", "정말이지 무척 정확했습니다."]], [["The second officer, who had attended to it closely, then copied it out and called in another man in uniform (there were several in an outer room), who took it up and went away with it.", "그것을 꼼꼼히 들여다보던 두 번째 경관이 그것을 베껴 적고는, 또 다른 제복 차림의 사내를(바깥방에 여럿이 있었습니다) 불러들였고, 그 사내가 그것을 받아들고 가지고 나갔습니다."], ["All this was done with the greatest dispatch and without the waste of a moment; yet nobody was at all hurried.", "이 모든 일이 한순간도 허비하지 않고 더없이 신속하게 이루어졌습니다. 그런데도 아무도 서두르는 기색이 없었습니다."], ["As soon as the paper was sent out upon its travels, the two officers resumed their former quiet work of writing with neatness and care. Mr.", "그 종이가 제 갈 길을 떠나 보내지자마자 두 경관은 다시 말끔하고 정성스럽게 무언가를 쓰던 아까의 조용한 일로 돌아갔습니다."], ["Bucket thoughtfully came and warmed the soles of his boots, first one and then the other, at the fire.", "버킷 씨는 골똘히 생각하며 다가와 난롯불에 장화 밑창을 한쪽씩 차례로 데웠습니다."]], [["“Are you well wrapped up, Miss Summerson?”", "\"몸은 단단히 둘러쌌습니까, 서머슨 양?\""], ["he asked me as his eyes met mine.", "그의 눈이 제 눈과 마주치자 물었습니다."], ["“It’s a desperate sharp night for a young lady to be out in.”", "\"젊은 아가씨가 밖에 나돌기엔 지독하게 매서운 밤이라서요.\""]], [["I told him I cared for no weather and was warmly clothed.", "저는 날씨 따위는 개의치 않으며 따뜻하게 입었다고 말했습니다."]], [["“It may be a long job,”", "\"긴 일이 될지도 모릅니다.\""], ["he observed; “but so that it ends well, never mind, miss.”", "그가 한마디 했습니다. \"하지만 끝만 좋으면 되니, 마음 쓰지 마십시오, 아가씨.\""]], [["“I pray to heaven it may end well!”", "\"부디 좋게 끝나기를 하늘에 기도드립니다!\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["He nodded comfortingly.", "그가 위로하듯 고개를 끄덕였습니다."], ["“You see, whatever you do, don’t you go and fret yourself.", "\"보세요, 무슨 일이 있어도 절대 안달하지 마십시오."], ["You keep yourself cool and equal for anything that may happen, and it’ll be the better for you, the better for me, the better for Lady Dedlock, and the better for Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.”", "무슨 일이 닥치든 침착하고 평온하게 마음을 다잡으세요. 그래야 아가씨한테도 좋고, 저한테도 좋고, 데드록 마님한테도 좋고, 준남작 레스터 데드록 경한테도 좋습니다.\""]], [["He was really very kind and gentle, and as he stood before the fire warming his boots and rubbing his face with his forefinger, I felt a confidence in his sagacity which reassured me.", "그는 정말로 무척 친절하고 다정했고, 난롯불 앞에 서서 장화를 데우며 집게손가락으로 얼굴을 문지르는 그를 보고 있자니, 그의 명민함에 대한 믿음이 솟아 마음이 놓였습니다."], ["It was not yet a quarter to two when I heard horses’ feet and wheels outside.", "밖에서 말발굽 소리와 바퀴 소리가 들렸을 때는 아직 두 시 십오 분 전도 되지 않은 시각이었습니다."], ["“Now, Miss Summerson,”", "\"자, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said he, “we are off, if you please!”", "그가 말했습니다. \"괜찮으시다면, 출발합시다!\""]], [["He gave me his arm, and the two officers courteously bowed me out, and we found at the door a phaeton or barouche with a postilion and post horses.", "그가 제게 팔을 내밀었고, 두 경관이 정중히 인사하며 저를 배웅했습니다. 문 앞에는 마부 한 명과 역마(驛馬)가 딸린 페이튼인지 바루슈인지 하는 마차가 있었습니다."], ["Mr. Bucket handed me in and took his own seat on the box.", "버킷 씨는 저를 마차에 태우고 자기는 마부석에 자리를 잡았습니다."], ["The man in uniform whom he had sent to fetch this equipage then handed him up a dark lantern at his request, and when he had given a few directions to the driver, we rattled away.", "그가 이 마차를 가져오라고 보냈던 제복 차림의 사내가 그의 청에 따라 그에게 어둑한 등을 건넸고, 마부에게 몇 가지 지시를 내린 뒤, 우리는 덜컹거리며 떠났습니다."]], [["I was far from sure that I was not in a dream.", "저는 이것이 꿈이 아니라고 도무지 확신할 수가 없었습니다."], ["We rattled with great rapidity through such a labyrinth of streets that I soon lost all idea where we were, except that we had crossed and re-crossed the river, and still seemed to be traversing a low-lying, waterside, dense neighbourhood of narrow thoroughfares chequered by docks and basins, high piles of warehouses, swing-bridges, and masts of ships.", "우리는 어찌나 빠르게 미로 같은 거리를 덜컹거리며 달렸던지, 강을 건너고 또 건넜다는 것 말고는 우리가 어디 있는지 곧 종잡을 수 없게 되었습니다. 그래도 부두와 선창이 군데군데 박히고 높이 쌓인 창고들과 도개교들과 배의 돛대들이 뒤섞인, 좁은 길들이 얽힌 강가의 낮고 빽빽한 동네를 여전히 가로지르고 있는 듯했습니다."], ["At length we stopped at the corner of a little slimy turning, which the wind from the river, rushing up it, did not purify; and I saw my companion, by the light of his lantern, in conference with several men who looked like a mixture of police and sailors.", "마침내 우리는 강에서 불어오는 바람이 휩쓸고 올라와도 깨끗해지지 않는, 진창에 미끄러운 작은 골목 모퉁이에 멈췄습니다. 등불 빛에 비친 제 동행은 경찰과 선원이 뒤섞인 듯 보이는 여러 사내들과 의논하고 있었습니다."], ["Against the mouldering wall by which they stood, there was a bill, on which I could discern the words, “Found Drowned”; and this and an inscription about drags possessed me with the awful suspicion shadowed forth in our visit to that place.", "그들이 서 있는 허물어져가는 벽에는 벽보가 한 장 붙어 있었는데, 저는 \"익사체 발견\"이라는 글자를 알아볼 수 있었습니다. 이것과 갈고리에 관한 어떤 문구가, 우리가 그곳을 찾아온 까닭에 어렴풋이 드리워진 끔찍한 의심으로 저를 사로잡았습니다."]], [["I had no need to remind myself that I was not there by the indulgence of any feeling of mine to increase the difficulties of the search, or to lessen its hopes, or enhance its delays.", "수색을 어렵게 만들거나, 그 희망을 꺾거나, 그 지체를 늘리려고 제 감정에 휘둘려 그곳에 와 있는 게 아니라는 사실을 굳이 스스로에게 일깨울 필요는 없었습니다."], ["I remained quiet, but what I suffered in that dreadful spot I never can forget.", "저는 잠자코 있었지만, 그 끔찍한 곳에서 제가 겪은 고통은 결코 잊을 수 없습니다."], ["And still it was like the horror of a dream.", "그리고 그것은 여전히 악몽의 공포 같았습니다."], ["A man yet dark and muddy, in long swollen sodden boots and a hat like them, was called out of a boat and whispered with Mr. Bucket, who went away with him down some slippery steps—as if to look at something secret that he had to show.", "아직 어둡고 진흙투성이인 한 사내가, 길쭉하게 부풀어 물에 젖은 장화를 신고 그와 똑같은 모자를 쓴 채 배에서 불려 나와 버킷 씨와 귓속말을 나누었고, 버킷 씨는 그와 함께 미끄러운 계단을 따라 내려갔습니다—마치 그가 보여줄 어떤 은밀한 것을 살피러 가는 듯이."], ["They came back, wiping their hands upon their coats, after turning over something wet; but thank God it was not what I feared!", "그들은 무언가 젖은 것을 뒤집어본 뒤 코트에 손을 닦으며 돌아왔습니다. 하지만 다행히도 그것은 제가 두려워하던 것이 아니었습니다!"]], [["After some further conference, Mr. Bucket (whom everybody seemed to know and defer to) went in with the others at a door and left me in the carriage, while the driver walked up and down by his horses to warm himself.", "좀 더 의논을 나눈 뒤, 버킷 씨는(누구나 그를 알고 그에게 머리를 숙이는 듯했습니다) 다른 이들과 함께 어떤 문 안으로 들어가며 저를 마차에 남겨두었고, 마부는 몸을 녹이려고 말 옆을 오락가락 걸었습니다."], ["The tide was coming in, as I judged from the sound it made, and I could hear it break at the end of the alley with a little rush towards me.", "소리로 짐작건대 밀물이 들어오고 있었고, 골목 끝에서 제 쪽으로 자그맣게 밀려들며 물결이 부서지는 소리가 들렸습니다."], ["It never did so—and I thought it did so, hundreds of times, in what can have been at the most a quarter of an hour, and probably was less—but the thought shuddered through me that it would cast my mother at the horses’ feet.", "길어야 십오 분, 어쩌면 그보다 짧았을 그 시간 동안, 실제로는 한 번도 그러지 않았는데도 저는 수백 번이나 물결이 그렇게 밀려든다고 생각했습니다. 그리고 그 물결이 어머니를 말발굽 앞으로 던져놓을 거라는 생각이 몸서리치며 저를 훑고 지나갔습니다."]], [["Mr. Bucket came out again, exhorting the others to be vigilant, darkened his lantern, and once more took his seat.", "버킷 씨가 다시 나와 다른 이들에게 빈틈없이 경계하라고 당부하고는, 등불을 어둡게 가린 뒤 다시 한번 자리에 앉았습니다."], ["“Don’t you be alarmed, Miss Summerson, on account of our coming down here,”", "\"우리가 여기까지 내려온 일로 놀라지 마십시오, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he said, turning to me.", "그가 제 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했습니다."], ["“I only want to have everything in train and to know that it is in train by looking after it myself.", "\"저는 그저 만사를 빈틈없이 준비해두고, 직접 챙겨보아 그게 제대로 진행되고 있음을 확인하고 싶을 뿐입니다."], ["Get on, my lad!”", "가자, 이 사람아!\""]], [["We appeared to retrace the way we had come.", "우리는 왔던 길을 되짚어가는 듯했습니다."], ["Not that I had taken note of any particular objects in my perturbed state of mind, but judging from the general character of the streets.", "어지러운 마음에 어떤 특정한 사물을 눈여겨봐서가 아니라, 거리의 전반적인 분위기로 미루어 그렇게 판단했습니다."], ["We called at another office or station for a minute and crossed the river again.", "우리는 또 다른 사무소인지 초소인지에 잠깐 들렀다가 다시 강을 건넜습니다."], ["During the whole of this time, and during the whole search, my companion, wrapped up on the box, never relaxed in his vigilance a single moment; but when we crossed the bridge he seemed, if possible, to be more on the alert than before.", "이 시간 내내, 그리고 수색이 이어지는 내내, 마부석에 몸을 둘러싸고 앉은 제 동행은 단 한순간도 경계를 늦추지 않았습니다. 그런데 다리를 건널 때 그는, 가능하다면, 전보다 더 바짝 긴장하는 것 같았습니다."], ["He stood up to look over the parapet, he alighted and went back after a shadowy female figure that flitted past us, and he gazed into the profound black pit of water with a face that made my heart die within me.", "그는 일어서서 난간 너머를 살폈고, 우리 곁을 휙 스쳐간 어슴푸레한 여자의 모습을 쫓아 마차에서 내려 되돌아갔으며, 시커멓고 깊은 물웅덩이를 들여다볼 때는 제 가슴이 철렁 내려앉게 하는 표정을 지었습니다."], ["The river had a fearful look, so overcast and secret, creeping away so fast between the low flat lines of shore—so heavy with indistinct and awful shapes, both of substance and shadow; so death-like and mysterious.", "강은 무서운 모습이었으니, 어찌나 어둡게 깔리고 은밀한지, 낮고 평평한 강기슭 사이로 어찌나 빠르게 흘러 빠져나가는지, 실체든 그림자든 흐릿하고 끔찍한 형상들로 어찌나 묵직한지, 죽음 같고 신비스러웠습니다."], ["I have seen it many times since then, by sunlight and by moonlight, but never free from the impressions of that journey.", "그 뒤로 저는 햇빛 아래서도 달빛 아래서도 그 강을 여러 번 보았으나, 그날 밤 여정의 인상에서 결코 벗어난 적이 없습니다."], ["In my memory the lights upon the bridge are always burning dim, the cutting wind is eddying round the homeless woman whom we pass, the monotonous wheels are whirling on, and the light of the carriage-lamps reflected back looks palely in upon me—a face rising out of the dreaded water.", "제 기억 속에서 다리 위의 불빛들은 늘 흐릿하게 타오르고, 우리가 스쳐가는 집 없는 여인 주위로 살을 에는 바람이 소용돌이치고, 단조로운 바퀴들이 빙빙 굴러가며, 마차 등불의 빛이 되비쳐 제게 창백하게 비쳐듭니다—그 두려운 물에서 솟아오르는 얼굴 하나가."]], [["Clattering and clattering through the empty streets, we came at length from the pavement on to dark smooth roads and began to leave the houses behind us.", "텅 빈 거리를 덜컹덜컹 끝없이 달려, 우리는 마침내 포장도로에서 어둡고 매끄러운 길로 접어들어 집들을 뒤로하기 시작했습니다."], ["After a while I recognized the familiar way to Saint Albans.", "얼마 지나 저는 세인트올번스로 가는 낯익은 길임을 알아보았습니다."], ["At Barnet fresh horses were ready for us, and we changed and went on.", "바닛에서는 새 말이 우리를 기다리고 있어, 말을 바꾸어 다시 갔습니다."], ["It was very cold indeed, and the open country was white with snow, though none was falling then.", "정말이지 무척 추웠고, 탁 트인 들판은 눈으로 하얗게 덮여 있었으나, 그때는 눈이 내리지 않았습니다."]], [["“An old acquaintance of yours, this road, Miss Summerson,”", "\"이 길은 아가씨한테 오랜 벗이로군요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket cheerfully.", "버킷 씨가 명랑하게 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["I returned.", "제가 대답했습니다."], ["“Have you gathered any intelligence?”", "\"무슨 소식이라도 모으셨습니까?\""]], [["“None that can be quite depended on as yet,”", "\"아직 온전히 믿을 만한 건 없습니다.\""], ["he answered, “but it’s early times as yet.”", "그가 대답했습니다. \"하지만 아직 이른 때이니까요.\""]], [["He had gone into every late or early public-house where there was a light (they were not a few at that time, the road being then much frequented by drovers) and had got down to talk to the turnpike-keepers.", "그는 불빛이 켜진 늦은 시각이나 이른 시각의 술집마다 다 들어가보았고(그 무렵에는 소몰이꾼들이 그 길을 무척 자주 다녀서 술집이 적지 않았습니다), 통행료 징수원들과 이야기하려고 마차에서 내리곤 했습니다. 그가 술을 주문하고, 돈을 짤랑거리고, 어디서나 싹싹하고 흥겹게 구는 소리를 저는 들었습니다."], ["I had heard him ordering drink, and chinking money, and making himself agreeable and merry everywhere; but whenever he took his seat upon the box again, his face resumed its watchful steady look, and he always said to the driver in the same business tone, “Get on, my lad!”", "하지만 다시 마부석에 자리를 잡을 때마다 그의 얼굴은 빈틈없이 한결같은 표정으로 되돌아갔고, 그는 늘 똑같이 일하는 어조로 마부에게 말했습니다. \"가자, 이 사람아!\""]], [["With all these stoppages, it was between five and six o’clock and we were yet a few miles short of Saint Albans when he came out of one of these houses and handed me in a cup of tea.", "이렇게 자주 멈춰 선 탓에, 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이가 되어서도 우리는 아직 세인트올번스에서 몇 마일 못 미친 지점에 있었는데, 그때 그가 그런 집들 가운데 하나에서 나와 제게 차 한 잔을 건넸습니다."]], [["“Drink it, Miss Summerson, it’ll do you good.", "\"드세요, 서머슨 양, 몸에 좋을 겁니다."], ["You’re beginning to get more yourself now, ain’t you?”", "이제 점점 본래의 아가씨로 돌아오고 있지요, 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["I thanked him and said I hoped so.", "저는 고맙다고 인사하며 그러기를 바란다고 말했습니다."]], [["“You was what you may call stunned at first,”", "\"처음에는 그야말로 얼이 빠지셨었지요.\" 그가 대답했습니다."], ["he returned; “and Lord, no wonder!", "\"그러니, 세상에, 무리도 아니지요!"], ["Don’t speak loud, my dear.", "큰 소리로 말하지 마십시오, 아가씨."], ["It’s all right.", "다 괜찮습니다."], ["She’s on ahead.”", "그분은 앞쪽에 계십니다.\""]], [["I don’t know what joyful exclamation I made or was going to make, but he put up his finger and I stopped myself.", "제가 무슨 기쁨에 찬 탄성을 질렀는지, 아니면 지르려 했는지 모르겠으나, 그가 손가락을 세우자 저는 입을 다물었습니다."]], [["“Passed through here on foot this evening about eight or nine.", "\"오늘 저녁 여덟 시인가 아홉 시쯤 걸어서 이곳을 지나갔습니다."], ["I heard of her first at the archway toll, over at Highgate, but couldn’t make quite sure.", "하이게이트 너머 아치형 통행료 징수소에서 처음 그분 소식을 들었는데, 썩 확실하지는 않았습니다."], ["Traced her all along, on and off.", "끊겼다 이어졌다 하면서 줄곧 자취를 좇았지요."], ["Picked her up at one place, and dropped her at another; but she’s before us now, safe.", "한 군데서는 자취를 잡았다가 또 한 군데서는 놓쳤지만, 지금은 우리보다 앞에, 무사히 계십니다."], ["Take hold of this cup and saucer, ostler.", "이 찻잔과 받침을 받게나, 마부."], ["Now, if you wasn’t brought up to the butter trade, look out and see if you can catch half a crown in your t’other hand.", "자, 자네가 버터 장사로 자라지 않았다면, 정신 차리고 다른 손으로 반 크라운을 받아챌 수 있는지 보게. 하나, 둘, 셋, 옜네!"], ["One, two, three, and there you are! Now, my lad, try a gallop!”", "자, 이 사람아, 한번 전속력으로 달려보게!\""]], [["We were soon in Saint Albans and alighted a little before day, when I was just beginning to arrange and comprehend the occurrences of the night and really to believe that they were not a dream.", "우리는 곧 세인트올번스에 들어서서 날이 밝기 조금 전에 내렸는데, 그제야 저는 비로소 밤사이의 일들을 정리하고 이해하며, 그것이 꿈이 아니었음을 정말로 믿기 시작했습니다."], ["Leaving the carriage at the posting-house and ordering fresh horses to be ready, my companion gave me his arm, and we went towards home.", "역참에 마차를 두고 새 말을 준비해두라고 일러둔 뒤, 제 동행은 제게 팔을 내밀었고, 우리는 집을 향해 걸어갔습니다."]], [["“As this is your regular abode, Miss Summerson, you see,”", "\"보세요, 여기가 아가씨의 본거지이니까요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he observed, “I should like to know whether you’ve been asked for by any stranger answering the description, or whether Mr.", "그가 한마디 했습니다. \"그 인상착의에 들어맞는 낯선 사람이 아가씨를 찾아왔는지, 아니면 잔다이스 씨를 찾아왔는지 알고 싶습니다."], ["Jarndyce has. I don’t much expect it, but it might be.”", "그럴 거라고 크게 기대하지는 않습니다만, 그럴 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["As we ascended the hill, he looked about him with a sharp eye—the day was now breaking—and reminded me that I had come down it one night, as I had reason for remembering, with my little servant and poor Jo, whom he called Toughey.", "언덕을 오르며 그는 날카로운 눈으로 사방을 둘러보았고—이제 날이 밝아오고 있었습니다—제가 어느 날 밤 어린 하녀와 가엾은 조와 함께 이 언덕을 내려갔던 일을 일깨워주었으니, 그는 조를 터피라고 불렀습니다. 그 일을 저는 기억할 만한 까닭이 있었습니다."]], [["I wondered how he knew that.", "저는 그가 그것을 어떻게 알았는지 의아했습니다."]], [["“When you passed a man upon the road, just yonder, you know,”", "\"저기 바로 저쯤에서, 길에서 한 사내를 지나치셨지요, 기억나시지요.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["Yes, I remembered that too, very well.", "네, 그것도 무척 잘 기억하고 있었습니다."]], [["“That was me,”", "\"그게 저였습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["Seeing my surprise, he went on, “I drove down in a gig that afternoon to look after that boy.", "제가 놀라는 것을 보고 그가 말을 이었습니다. \"그날 오후에 저는 그 아이를 살피러 이륜마차를 몰고 내려왔습니다."], ["You might have heard my wheels when you came out to look after him yourself, for I was aware of you and your little maid going up when I was walking the horse down.", "아가씨가 직접 그 아이를 살피러 나오셨을 때 제 바퀴 소리를 들으셨을지도 모릅니다. 제가 말을 끌고 언덕을 내려갈 때 아가씨와 어린 하녀가 올라가는 걸 알아챘으니까요."], ["Making an inquiry or two about him in the town, I soon heard what company he was in and was coming among the brick-fields to look for him when I observed you bringing him home here.”", "마을에서 그 아이에 대해 한두 가지 물어보고, 저는 곧 그 아이가 어떤 무리와 어울리는지 듣고서, 그 아이를 찾으러 벽돌 굽는 들판으로 들어서던 참에, 아가씨가 그 아이를 이리로 데려오는 것을 보았던 겁니다.\""]], [["“Had he committed any crime?”", "\"그 아이가 무슨 죄라도 지었던가요?\""], ["I asked.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“None was charged against him,” said Mr.", "\"그 아이한테 씌워진 죄는 없었습니다.\""], ["Bucket, coolly lifting off his hat, “but I suppose he wasn’t over-particular.", "버킷 씨가 태연히 모자를 벗으며 말했습니다. \"하지만 그리 깔끔한 처지는 아니었던 것 같습니다."], ["No.", "아니지요."], ["What I wanted him for was in connexion with keeping this very matter of Lady Dedlock quiet.", "제가 그 아이를 원한 건 바로 이 데드록 마님 일을 조용히 묻어두는 것과 관련이 있었습니다."], ["He had been making his tongue more free than welcome as to a small accidental service he had been paid for by the deceased Mr.", "그 아이가 고인이 된 토킹혼 씨한테서 어떤 사소한 우연한 심부름 값을 받은 일에 대해, 달갑잖을 만큼 입을 함부로 놀려대고 있었거든요."], ["Tulkinghorn; and it wouldn’t do, at any sort of price, to have him playing those games.", "어떤 값을 치르더라도 그 아이가 그런 장난을 치게 둘 수는 없었습니다."], ["So having warned him out of London, I made an afternoon of it to warn him to keep out of it now he WAS away, and go farther from it, and maintain a bright look-out that I didn’t catch him coming back again.”", "그래서 그 아이한테 런던을 떠나라고 경고해두었다가, 그가 정말로 떠났으니 이제 거기서 멀리 떨어져 지내고, 더 멀리 가고, 다시 돌아오다가 제 눈에 띄지 않도록 정신을 바짝 차리라고 경고하려고 하루 오후를 들였던 겁니다.\""]], [["“Poor creature!”", "\"가엾은 것!\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Poor enough,” assented Mr.", "\"가엾기 짝이 없었지요.\""], ["Bucket, “and trouble enough, and well enough away from London, or anywhere else.", "버킷 씨가 동의했습니다. \"골칫거리이기도 했고, 런던이든 어디든 떠나 있는 게 잘된 일이었지요."], ["I was regularly turned on my back when I found him taken up by your establishment, I do assure you.”", "단언컨대, 그 아이가 아가씨네 집에 거두어졌다는 걸 알았을 때 저는 그야말로 자빠질 뻔했습니다.\""]], [["I asked him why.", "저는 왜냐고 물었습니다."], ["“Why, my dear?”", "\"왜냐고요, 아가씨?\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Naturally there was no end to his tongue then.", "\"그렇게 되니 당연히 그 아이 혀끝에 끝이 없어졌으니까요."], ["He might as well have been born with a yard and a half of it, and a remnant over.”", "그 아이는 한 자 반쯤 되는 혀를 달고, 남은 토막까지 더해서 태어난 거나 다름없었습니다.\""]], [["Although I remember this conversation now, my head was in confusion at the time, and my power of attention hardly did more than enable me to understand that he entered into these particulars to divert me.", "지금이야 이 대화를 기억하지만, 그때는 머릿속이 어지러웠고, 제 주의력이라곤 그가 저를 다른 데로 돌리려고 이런 자질구레한 이야기를 늘어놓는다는 것을 알아챌 정도밖에 되지 않았습니다."], ["With the same kind intention, manifestly, he often spoke to me of indifferent things, while his face was busy with the one object that we had in view.", "분명히 같은 친절한 의도로, 그는 우리가 눈앞에 둔 단 하나의 목표에 얼굴을 골몰히 두면서도, 제게 자주 별것 아닌 이야기를 건넸습니다."], ["He still pursued this subject as we turned in at the garden-gate.", "정원 문을 들어설 때까지도 그는 여전히 이 화제를 이어갔습니다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Here we are, and a nice retired place it is.", "\"다 왔군요, 한적하니 참 좋은 곳입니다."], ["Puts a man in mind of the country house in the Woodpecker-tapping, that was known by the smoke which so gracefully curled.", "저 우아하게 피어오르는 연기로 알려졌다던 〈딱따구리 두드리네〉 노래 속 시골집을 떠올리게 하는군요."], ["They’re early with the kitchen fire, and that denotes good servants.", "부엌 불을 일찍 지폈으니, 좋은 하인들이라는 표시지요."], ["But what you’ve always got to be careful of with servants is who comes to see ’em; you never know what they’re up to if you don’t know that.", "하지만 하인들에 관해 늘 조심해야 할 건 누가 그들을 찾아오느냐 하는 겁니다. 그걸 모르면 그들이 무슨 꿍꿍이인지 절대 알 수 없으니까요."], ["And another thing, my dear.", "그리고 또 한 가지, 아가씨."], ["Whenever you find a young man behind the kitchen-door, you give that young man in charge on suspicion of being secreted in a dwelling-house with an unlawful purpose.”", "부엌문 뒤에서 젊은 사내를 발견하거든, 불법한 목적으로 주택 안에 숨어든 혐의로 그 젊은 사내를 잡아두십시오.\""]], [["We were now in front of the house; he looked attentively and closely at the gravel for footprints before he raised his eyes to the windows.", "이제 우리는 집 앞에 이르렀습니다. 그는 창문으로 눈을 들기 전에, 자갈밭에 발자국이 있는지 주의 깊게 찬찬히 살폈습니다."]], [["“Do you generally put that elderly young gentleman in the same room when he’s on a visit here, Miss Summerson?”", "\"여기 손님으로 오면 저 늙은 젊은 신사분을 늘 같은 방에 묵게 하십니까, 서머슨 양?\""], ["he inquired, glancing at Mr. Skimpole’s usual chamber.", "그가 스킴폴 씨가 늘 쓰는 방을 흘끔 보며 물었습니다."]], [["“You know Mr.", "\"스킴폴 씨를 아세요!\""], ["Skimpole!” said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“What do you call him again?”", "\"그분 이름이 뭐라고 하셨지요?\""], ["returned Mr. Bucket, bending down his ear.", "버킷 씨가 귀를 기울이며 되물었습니다."], ["“Skimpole, is it?", "\"스킴폴이라고요?"], ["I’ve often wondered what his name might be.", "저분 이름이 뭘까 자주 궁금했었습니다."], ["Skimpole.", "스킴폴."], ["Not John, I should say, nor yet Jacob?”", "존은 아니고, 그렇다고 제이콥도 아닐 텐데?\""]], [["“Harold,”", "\"해럴드예요.\""], ["I told him.", "제가 일러주었습니다."]], [["“Harold. Yes.", "\"해럴드."], ["He’s a queer bird is Harold,”", "그렇군요. 해럴드는 별난 새이지요.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket, eyeing me with great expression.", "버킷 씨가 의미심장한 표정으로 저를 바라보며 말했습니다."]], [["“He is a singular character,”", "\"독특한 분이지요.\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“No idea of money,”", "\"돈이라곤 도무지 모르고요.\""], ["observed Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 한마디 했습니다."], ["“He takes it, though!”", "\"그러면서도 받기는 받지만요!\""]], [["I involuntarily returned for answer that I perceived Mr. Bucket knew him.", "저는 저도 모르게 버킷 씨가 그분을 안다는 걸 알아챘다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“Why, now I’ll tell you, Miss Summerson,”", "\"그럼, 자, 말씀드리지요, 서머슨 양.\" 그가 대답했습니다."], ["he replied.", "\"한 가지 일에 너무 줄곧 골몰하지 않는 편이 마음에 한결 나을 테니, 기분 전환 삼아 들려드리지요."], ["“Your mind will be all the better for not running on one point too continually, and I’ll tell you for a change.", "터피가 어디 있는지 제게 가리켜준 게 바로 그분입니다. 그날 밤 저는 다른 수가 없으면 그저 문으로 가서 터피를 내달라고 할 작정이었는데, 무슨 수가 있을지 우선 한두 번 움직여보려고, 그림자가 보이는 저 창문에 자갈 한 알을 던져 올렸습니다."], ["It was him as pointed out to me where Toughey was.", "해럴드가 그걸 열자마자 그를 한번 보고는, 옳거니, 당신이 내 사람이로군, 하고 생각했지요. 그래서 가족들이 잠자리에 든 뒤에 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않다는 둥, 자비로운 젊은 아가씨들이 부랑자를 거두는 건 안타까운 일이라는 둥 그를 슬슬 구슬렸습니다."], ["I made up my mind that night to come to the door and ask for Toughey, if that was all; but willing to try a move or so first, if any such was on the board, I just pitched up a morsel of gravel at that window where I saw a shadow.", "그러고 나서 그의 됨됨이를 어지간히 파악하고는, 소란이나 말썽 없이 이 집에서 터피를 데려갈 수만 있다면 5파운드짜리 지폐를 잘 쓰는 셈치겠다고 했지요. 그러자 그가 더없이 쾌활하게 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말하더군요."], ["As soon as Harold opens it and I have had a look at him, thinks I, you’re the man for me.", "'내게 5파운드짜리 지폐 얘기를 해봐야 소용없네, 이 사람아. 그런 일에 나는 그저 어린아이라 돈이라곤 도무지 모르니까.'"], ["So I smoothed him down a bit about not wanting to disturb the family after they was gone to bed and about its being a thing to be regretted that charitable young ladies should harbour vagrants; and then, when I pretty well understood his ways, I said I should consider a fypunnote well bestowed if I could relieve the premises of Toughey without causing any noise or trouble.", "물론 저는 그가 그렇게 태평하게 구는 게 무슨 뜻인지 알아챘지요. 이제 그가 영락없이 내 사람이라고 확신하고서, 저는 지폐를 작은 돌멩이에 둘둘 말아 그에게 던져 올렸습니다."], ["Then says he, lifting up his eyebrows in the gayest way, ‘It’s no use mentioning a fypunnote to me, my friend, because I’m a mere child in such matters and have no idea of money.’ Of course I understood what his taking it so easy meant; and being now quite sure he was the man for me, I wrapped the note round a little stone and threw it up to him.", "그랬더니! 그가 웃고 환히 빛나며, 더없이 천진한 얼굴로 말하더군요."], ["Well!", "'하지만 난 이런 것들의 값어치를 몰라."], ["He laughs and beams, and looks as innocent as you like, and says, ‘But I don’t know the value of these things.", "이걸 가지고 대체 뭘 하란 말인가?' '쓰십시오, 선생님.'"], ["What am I to DO with this?’ ‘Spend it, sir,’ says I.", "제가 말했지요. '하지만 난 속아 넘어갈 걸세.'"], ["‘But I shall be taken in,’ he says, ‘they won’t give me the right change, I shall lose it, it’s no use to me.’ Lord, you never saw such a face as he carried it with!", "그가 말하더군요. '거스름돈을 제대로 안 줄 테고, 잃어버릴 테고, 내게는 아무 쓸모가 없어.'"], ["Of course he told me where to find Toughey, and I found him.”", "세상에, 그가 그러면서 짓던 그런 얼굴은 본 적이 없을 겁니다! 물론 그는 제게 터피가 어디 있는지 일러주었고, 저는 그를 찾아냈습니다.\""]], [["I regarded this as very treacherous on the part of Mr. Skimpole towards my guardian and as passing the usual bounds of his childish innocence.", "저는 이것이 후견인에 대한 스킴폴 씨의 무척 배신적인 처사이며, 그의 어린아이 같은 천진함의 통상적인 한도를 넘어선 일이라고 여겼습니다."]], [["“Bounds, my dear?”", "\"한도라고요, 아가씨?\""], ["returned Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Bounds?", "\"한도라?"], ["Now, Miss Summerson, I’ll give you a piece of advice that your husband will find useful when you are happily married and have got a family about you.", "자, 서머슨 양, 아가씨가 행복하게 결혼해서 가족을 거느리게 되었을 때 아가씨 남편한테 쓸모 있을 충고 한마디 드리지요. 돈에 관한 한 자기는 더없이 순진하다고 누가 말하거든, 자기 돈을 단단히 챙기십시오."], ["Whenever a person says to you that they are as innocent as can be in all concerning money, look well after your own money, for they are dead certain to collar it if they can.", "그런 자들은 할 수만 있으면 영락없이 그 돈을 가로채니까요. 누가 '세상일에 나는 어린아이라네'"], ["Whenever a person proclaims to you ‘In worldly matters I’m a child,’ you consider that that person is only a-crying off from being held accountable and that you have got that person’s number, and it’s Number One.", "하고 떠벌리거든, 그 사람은 그저 책임지지 않으려고 발뺌하는 것이며, 아가씨가 그 사람의 정체를 꿰뚫었다고 여기십시오. 그 정체는 바로 '제 잇속 일등'입니다."], ["Now, I am not a poetical man myself, except in a vocal way when it goes round a company, but I’m a practical one, and that’s my experience.", "자, 저야 시 짓는 사람은 아닙니다, 좌중을 돌며 노래 한 곡 뽑을 때 말고는요. 하지만 저는 실용적인 사람이고, 이게 제 경험입니다."], ["So’s this rule.", "이 법칙도 그렇고요."], ["Fast and loose in one thing, fast and loose in everything.", "한 가지에서 들쭉날쭉하는 자는 만사에 들쭉날쭉합니다. 이게 어긋나는 걸 본 적이 없습니다."], ["I never knew it fail.", "아가씨도 마찬가지일 겁니다."], ["No more will you. Nor no one.", "누구라도 마찬가지일 거고요."], ["With which caution to the unwary, my dear, I take the liberty of pulling this here bell, and so go back to our business.”", "방심하는 이들을 향한 이 경고와 함께, 아가씨, 실례를 무릅쓰고 이 종을 한번 당기고는, 다시 우리 일로 돌아가겠습니다.\""]], [["I believe it had not been for a moment out of his mind, any more than it had been out of my mind, or out of his face.", "그 일이 제 마음에서 한순간도 떠나지 않았듯이, 그의 마음에서도, 그의 얼굴에서도 한순간도 떠난 적이 없었던 것 같습니다."], ["The whole household were amazed to see me, without any notice, at that time in the morning, and so accompanied; and their surprise was not diminished by my inquiries.", "그날 아침 그 시각에, 아무 기별도 없이, 그런 사람과 함께 나타난 저를 보고 온 집안이 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 게다가 제 질문에도 그들의 놀라움은 가시지 않았습니다."], ["No one, however, had been there.", "하지만 아무도 거기 오지 않았다고 했습니다."], ["It could not be doubted that this was the truth.", "이것이 사실임은 의심할 여지가 없었습니다."]], [["“Then, Miss Summerson,”", "\"그렇다면, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said my companion, “we can’t be too soon at the cottage where those brickmakers are to be found.", "제 동행이 말했습니다. \"그 벽돌공들이 있다는 오두막으로 한시바삐 가야겠습니다."], ["Most inquiries there I leave to you, if you’ll be so good as to make ’em.", "거기서의 질문은 거의 다 아가씨께 맡기지요, 친절히 맡아주신다면요."], ["The naturalest way is the best way, and the naturalest way is your own way.”", "가장 자연스러운 방식이 가장 좋은 방식이고, 가장 자연스러운 방식은 곧 아가씨 자신의 방식이니까요.\""]], [["We set off again immediately.", "우리는 곧장 다시 길을 나섰습니다."], ["On arriving at the cottage, we found it shut up and apparently deserted, but one of the neighbours who knew me and who came out when I was trying to make some one hear informed me that the two women and their husbands now lived together in another house, made of loose rough bricks, which stood on the margin of the piece of ground where the kilns were and where the long rows of bricks were drying.", "오두막에 다다라보니 문이 닫혀 있고 버려진 듯했으나, 저를 알아보는 이웃 한 사람이, 제가 안에서 누구든 불러보려고 애쓰는 동안 나와서 일러주기를, 그 두 여자와 남편들은 이제 다른 집에서 함께 산다고 했습니다. 그 집은 가마들이 있고 긴 벽돌 줄이 마르고 있는 땅뙈기의 가장자리에 헐겁게 쌓은 거친 벽돌로 지어진 것이었습니다."], ["We lost no time in repairing to this place, which was within a few hundred yards; and as the door stood ajar, I pushed it open.", "우리는 지체 없이 그곳으로 갔는데, 그곳은 몇백 야드 안에 있었습니다. 문이 빠끔히 열려 있어 저는 그것을 밀어 열었습니다."]], [["There were only three of them sitting at breakfast, the child lying asleep on a bed in the corner.", "안에는 세 사람만이 아침을 먹으며 앉아 있었고, 아이는 구석 침대에 잠들어 누워 있었습니다."], ["It was Jenny, the mother of the dead child, who was absent.", "자리에 없는 사람은 죽은 아이의 어머니인 제니였습니다."], ["The other woman rose on seeing me; and the men, though they were, as usual, sulky and silent, each gave me a morose nod of recognition.", "다른 여자가 저를 보고 일어섰고, 두 사내는 여느 때처럼 뚱하고 말이 없었으나, 저마다 못마땅한 듯 까닥하며 알은체를 했습니다."], ["A look passed between them when Mr. Bucket followed me in, and I was surprised to see that the woman evidently knew him.", "버킷 씨가 저를 따라 들어서자 그들 사이에 눈빛이 오갔고, 저는 그 여자가 분명 그를 안다는 것을 보고 놀랐습니다."]], [["I had asked leave to enter of course.", "물론 저는 들어가도 되겠느냐고 청했었습니다."], ["Liz (the only name by which I knew her) rose to give me her own chair, but I sat down on a stool near the fire, and Mr. Bucket took a corner of the bedstead.", "리즈가(제가 아는 그녀의 유일한 이름이었습니다) 자기 의자를 제게 내주려고 일어섰으나, 저는 난롯가의 걸상에 앉았고, 버킷 씨는 침대틀 한 귀퉁이에 앉았습니다."], ["Now that I had to speak and was among people with whom I was not familiar, I became conscious of being hurried and giddy.", "이제 말을 해야 하는 데다 낯선 사람들 사이에 있게 되자, 저는 다급하고 어질어질해지는 것을 느꼈습니다."], ["It was very difficult to begin, and I could not help bursting into tears.", "입을 떼기가 무척 어려웠고, 저는 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았습니다."]], [["“Liz,”", "\"리즈.\""], ["said I, “I have come a long way in the night and through the snow to inquire after a lady—”", "제가 말했습니다. \"저는 어떤 부인을 찾으러 밤새 눈을 헤치고 먼 길을 왔어요—\""]], [["“Who has been here, you know,” Mr.", "\"여기 다녀가셨던 분 말입니다.\""], ["Bucket struck in, addressing the whole group with a composed propitiatory face; “that’s the lady the young lady means.", "버킷 씨가 침착하고 누그러뜨리는 얼굴로 무리 전체를 향해 끼어들었습니다. \"이 아가씨가 말하는 게 그 부인입니다."], ["The lady that was here last night, you know.”", "어젯밤 여기 다녀간 그 부인 말이오.\""]], [["“And who told YOU as there was anybody here?”", "\"그리고 여기 누가 있었다고 누가 댁한테 말하던가?\""], ["inquired Jenny’s husband, who had made a surly stop in his eating to listen and now measured him with his eye.", "제니의 남편이 물었으니, 그는 귀를 기울이느라 먹던 것을 뚱하게 멈추었다가 이제 그를 눈으로 가늠하고 있었습니다."]], [["“A person of the name of Michael Jackson, with a blue welveteen waistcoat with a double row of mother of pearl buttons,”", "\"마이클 잭슨이라는 사람인데, 자개단추가 두 줄 달린 파란 골덴 조끼를 입었지요.\""], ["Mr. Bucket immediately answered.", "버킷 씨가 즉시 대답했습니다."]], [["“He had as good mind his own business, whoever he is,”", "\"그자가 누구든, 제 일에나 신경 쓰는 게 좋을 거요.\""], ["growled the man.", "사내가 으르렁거렸습니다."]], [["“He’s out of employment, I believe,” said Mr.", "\"실직했다더군요.\""], ["Bucket apologetically for Michael Jackson, “and so gets talking.”", "버킷 씨가 마이클 잭슨을 두둔하듯 말했습니다. \"그래서 그렇게 입을 놀리는 거지요.\""]], [["The woman had not resumed her chair, but stood faltering with her hand upon its broken back, looking at me.", "여자는 다시 의자에 앉지 않고, 부서진 등받이에 손을 얹은 채 머뭇거리며 서서 저를 바라보았습니다."], ["I thought she would have spoken to me privately if she had dared.", "저는 그녀가 감히 그럴 수만 있다면 제게 따로 이야기를 했으리라고 생각했습니다."], ["She was still in this attitude of uncertainty when her husband, who was eating with a lump of bread and fat in one hand and his clasp-knife in the other, struck the handle of his knife violently on the table and told her with an oath to mind HER own business at any rate and sit down.", "그녀가 여전히 이렇게 망설이는 자세로 서 있는데, 한 손에는 빵과 비계 덩어리를, 다른 손에는 접칼을 들고 먹던 그녀의 남편이 칼자루로 식탁을 세게 내리치고는, 욕설을 내뱉으며 어쨌든 네 일에나 신경 쓰고 앉으라고 그녀에게 말했습니다."]], [["“I should like to have seen Jenny very much,”", "\"제니를 꼭 좀 보고 싶었어요.\" 제가 말했습니다."], ["said I, “for I am sure she would have told me all she could about this lady, whom I am very anxious indeed—you cannot think how anxious—to overtake.", "\"이 부인에 대해 아는 걸 죄다 제게 일러주었을 게 분명하니까요. 저는 그분을 따라잡고 싶은 마음이 정말 간절하답니다—얼마나 간절한지 모르실 거예요."], ["Will Jenny be here soon?", "제니가 곧 올까요?"], ["Where is she?”", "어디 있나요?\""]], [["The woman had a great desire to answer, but the man, with another oath, openly kicked at her foot with his heavy boot.", "여자는 무척 대답하고 싶어 했으나, 사내가 또 한 번 욕설을 내뱉으며 묵직한 장화로 대놓고 그녀의 발을 걷어찼습니다."], ["He left it to Jenny’s husband to say what he chose, and after a dogged silence the latter turned his shaggy head towards me.", "그는 무어라 말할지를 제니의 남편에게 넘겼고, 고집스러운 침묵 끝에 후자가 텁수룩한 머리를 제 쪽으로 돌렸습니다."]], [["“I’m not partial to gentlefolks coming into my place, as you’ve heerd me say afore now, I think, miss.", "\"전에도 제가 말한 걸로 아는데요, 아가씨, 저는 신사 양반들이 제 집에 들어오는 게 달갑지 않습니다."], ["I let their places be, and it’s curious they can’t let my place be.", "저는 그분들 집을 가만 놔두는데, 그분들은 제 집을 가만 못 놔두니 희한한 노릇이지요."], ["There’d be a pretty shine made if I was to go a-wisitin THEM, I think.", "제가 그분들을 찾아가기라도 한다면 어지간히 야단법석이 날 텐데 말입니다."], ["Howsoever, I don’t so much complain of you as of some others, and I’m agreeable to make you a civil answer, though I give notice that I’m not a-going to be drawed like a badger.", "하기야, 아가씨한테는 다른 몇몇한테만큼 불평은 없으니, 점잖게 대답해드리는 데는 동의하겠습니다. 다만, 오소리처럼 끌려 나올 생각은 없다는 걸 미리 일러둡니다."], ["Will Jenny be here soon?", "제니가 곧 오겠냐고요?"], ["No she won’t.", "안 옵니다."], ["Where is she?", "어디 있냐고요?"], ["She’s gone up to Lunnun.”", "런던으로 올라갔습니다.\""]], [["“Did she go last night?”", "\"어젯밤에 갔나요?\""], ["I asked.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“Did she go last night?", "\"어젯밤에 갔냐고요?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["She went last night,”", "어젯밤에 갔습니다.\""], ["he answered with a sulky jerk of his head.", "그가 뚱하게 머리를 홱 젖히며 대답했습니다."]], [["“But was she here when the lady came?", "\"그런데 그 부인이 왔을 때 제니가 여기 있었나요?"], ["And what did the lady say to her?", "그리고 그 부인이 제니한테 뭐라고 했나요?"], ["And where is the lady gone?", "그 부인은 어디로 갔나요?"], ["I beg and pray you to be so kind as to tell me,”", "부디 친절을 베풀어 제게 일러주시기를 빌고 또 청해요.\""], ["said I, “for I am in great distress to know.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"저는 그걸 알고 싶어 몹시 애가 타니까요.\""]], [["“If my master would let me speak, and not say a word of harm—”", "\"제 주인 양반이 말하게 해주신다면, 해될 말은 한마디도 안 하고—\""], ["the woman timidly began.", "여자가 머뭇머뭇 말을 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“Your master,”", "\"네 주인은,\""], ["said her husband, muttering an imprecation with slow emphasis, “will break your neck if you meddle with wot don’t concern you.”", "그녀의 남편이 천천히 힘주어 저주를 중얼거리며 말했습니다. \"네가 상관없는 일에 끼어들면 네 목을 부러뜨릴 거다.\""]], [["After another silence, the husband of the absent woman, turning to me again, answered me with his usual grumbling unwillingness.", "또 한 번 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 자리에 없는 여자의 남편이 다시 제 쪽으로 돌아서서, 여느 때처럼 투덜대며 마지못해 제게 대답했습니다."]], [["“Wos Jenny here when the lady come?", "\"그 부인이 왔을 때 제니가 여기 있었냐고요?"], ["Yes, she wos here when the lady come.", "네, 그 부인이 왔을 때 여기 있었습니다."], ["Wot did the lady say to her?", "그 부인이 제니한테 뭐라고 했냐고요?"], ["Well, I’ll tell you wot the lady said to her.", "좋습니다, 그 부인이 뭐라고 했는지 일러드리지요."], ["She said, ‘You remember me as come one time to talk to you about the young lady as had been a-wisiting of you?", "그분이 말하더군요. '댁을 찾아왔던 젊은 아가씨에 대해 한번 이야기하러 왔던 나를 기억하나요?"], ["You remember me as give you somethink handsome for a handkercher wot she had left?’ Ah, she remembered.", "그 아가씨가 두고 간 손수건 값으로 후한 것을 댁한테 준 나를 기억하나요?' 아, 그녀는 기억하더군요."], ["So we all did.", "우리 모두 기억했고요."], ["Well, then, wos that young lady up at the house now?", "그래, 그럼, 그 젊은 아가씨가 지금 저 위 집에 있냐고요?"], ["No, she warn’t up at the house now.", "아니요, 지금 저 위 집에 없었습니다."], ["Well, then, lookee here.", "그래, 그럼, 들어보세요."], ["The lady was upon a journey all alone, strange as we might think it, and could she rest herself where you’re a setten for a hour or so.", "그 부인은, 우리야 이상하다 싶었지만, 혼자 길을 가는 중이었는데, 댁이 앉아 있는 거기서 한 시간쯤 쉬어가도 되겠냐고요."], ["Yes she could, and so she did.", "네, 되고말고요, 그래서 그렇게 했습니다."], ["Then she went—it might be at twenty minutes past eleven, and it might be at twenty minutes past twelve; we ain’t got no watches here to know the time by, nor yet clocks.", "그러고는 떠났지요—열한 시 이십 분이었을 수도 있고, 열두 시 이십 분이었을 수도 있습니다. 여기는 시간을 알 시계도 없고, 벽시계도 없으니까요."], ["Where did she go?", "어디로 갔냐고요?"], ["I don’t know where she go’d.", "어디로 갔는지 모릅니다."], ["She went one way, and Jenny went another; one went right to Lunnun, and t’other went right from it.", "그분은 한쪽 길로 갔고, 제니는 다른 쪽 길로 갔지요. 하나는 곧장 런던으로 갔고, 다른 하나는 곧장 그 반대로 갔습니다."], ["That’s all about it.", "그게 다입니다."], ["Ask this man.", "이 사람한테 물어보세요."], ["He heerd it all, and see it all.", "다 듣고 다 봤으니까요."], ["He knows.”", "이 사람이 압니다.\""]], [["The other man repeated, “That’s all about it.”", "다른 사내가 되풀이했습니다. \"그게 다요.\""]], [["“Was the lady crying?”", "\"그 부인이 울던가요?\""], ["I inquired.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“Devil a bit,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["returned the first man.", "첫 번째 사내가 대답했습니다."], ["“Her shoes was the worse, and her clothes was the worse, but she warn’t—not as I see.”", "\"신발은 엉망이고 옷도 엉망이었지만, 울지는—내가 본 바로는, 아니었습니다.\""]], [["The woman sat with her arms crossed and her eyes upon the ground.", "여자는 팔짱을 끼고 눈을 바닥에 둔 채 앉아 있었습니다."], ["Her husband had turned his seat a little so as to face her and kept his hammer-like hand upon the table as if it were in readiness to execute his threat if she disobeyed him.", "그녀의 남편은 그녀를 마주 보도록 자리를 조금 틀고는, 그녀가 말을 듣지 않으면 위협을 실행에 옮길 채비라도 된 듯이 망치 같은 손을 식탁에 얹어두고 있었습니다."]], [["“I hope you will not object to my asking your wife,”", "\"실례가 안 된다면, 부인께 그 부인이 어떤 모습이었는지 여쭙고 싶은데요.\""], ["said I, “how the lady looked.”", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Come, then!”", "\"자, 그럼!\""], ["he gruffly cried to her.", "그가 그녀에게 퉁명스레 외쳤습니다."], ["“You hear what she says.", "\"이 아가씨가 하는 말 들었지."], ["Cut it short and tell her.”", "짧게 끊어서 일러줘.\""]], [["“Bad,”", "\"안 좋았어요.\""], ["replied the woman.", "여자가 대답했습니다."], ["“Pale and exhausted.", "\"창백하고 기진맥진하셨지요."], ["Very bad.”", "아주 안 좋았어요.\""]], [["“Did she speak much?”", "\"말은 많이 했나요?\""]], [["“Not much, but her voice was hoarse.”", "\"많지는 않았지만, 목소리가 쉬어 있었어요.\""]], [["She answered, looking all the while at her husband for leave.", "그녀는 줄곧 남편에게서 허락을 구하듯 그를 쳐다보며 대답했습니다."]], [["“Was she faint?”", "\"기력이 없었나요?\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."], ["“Did she eat or drink here?”", "\"여기서 뭘 먹거나 마셨나요?\""]], [["“Go on!”", "\"말해!\""], ["said the husband in answer to her look.", "남편이 그녀의 눈길에 대답해 말했습니다."], ["“Tell her and cut it short.”", "\"일러주고 짧게 끊어.\""]], [["“She had a little water, miss, and Jenny fetched her some bread and tea.", "\"물을 조금 드셨어요, 아가씨. 제니가 빵하고 차를 좀 가져다드렸고요."], ["But she hardly touched it.”", "하지만 거의 입을 안 대셨어요.\""]], [["“And when she went from here,”", "\"그리고 그 부인이 여기서 떠날 때는—\""], ["I was proceeding, when Jenny’s husband impatiently took me up.", "제가 말을 이어가는데, 제니의 남편이 참지 못하고 제 말을 가로챘습니다."]], [["“When she went from here, she went right away nor’ard by the high road.", "\"여기서 떠날 때, 그분은 큰길로 곧장 북쪽으로 갔습니다."], ["Ask on the road if you doubt me, and see if it warn’t so.", "제 말이 못 미덥거든 길에서 물어보세요, 그렇지 않은지."], ["Now, there’s the end.", "자, 이게 끝입니다."], ["That’s all about it.”", "그게 다라고요.\""]], [["I glanced at my companion, and finding that he had already risen and was ready to depart, thanked them for what they had told me, and took my leave.", "저는 제 동행을 흘끔 보았는데, 그가 이미 일어서서 떠날 채비를 한 것을 알고, 그들이 일러준 것에 고맙다고 인사하고 자리를 떴습니다."], ["The woman looked full at Mr. Bucket as he went out, and he looked full at her.", "그가 나갈 때 여자가 버킷 씨를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 그도 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보았습니다."]], [["“Now, Miss Summerson,”", "\"자, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he said to me as we walked quickly away.", "우리가 빠르게 걸어 멀어지는데 그가 제게 말했습니다."], ["“They’ve got her ladyship’s watch among ’em.", "\"저자들이 마님의 시계를 가지고 있습니다."], ["That’s a positive fact.”", "그건 확실한 사실입니다.\""]], [["“You saw it?”", "\"보셨나요?\""], ["I exclaimed.", "제가 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Just as good as saw it,”", "\"본 거나 다름없지요.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“Else why should he talk about his ‘twenty minutes past’ and about his having no watch to tell the time by?", "\"안 그러면 왜 그자가 '이십 분'이 어쩌고 하면서, 시간을 알 시계가 없다는 둥 떠들었겠습니까?"], ["Twenty minutes!", "이십 분이라니!"], ["He don’t usually cut his time so fine as that.", "그자는 보통 시간을 그렇게 잘게 자르지 않습니다."], ["If he comes to half-hours, it’s as much as HE does.", "그자가 반 시간 단위까지 따진다면 그게 고작이지요."], ["Now, you see, either her ladyship gave him that watch or he took it.", "자, 보세요, 마님이 그자한테 그 시계를 주었거나, 아니면 그자가 빼앗은 겁니다."], ["I think she gave it him.", "제 생각엔 마님이 준 것 같습니다."], ["Now, what should she give it him for?", "자, 그럼 마님이 무엇 때문에 그걸 주었을까요?"], ["What should she give it him for?”", "무엇 때문에 줬을까요?\""]], [["He repeated this question to himself several times as we hurried on, appearing to balance between a variety of answers that arose in his mind.", "서둘러 가는 동안 그는 이 질문을 스스로에게 여러 번 되풀이했는데, 마음속에 떠오르는 여러 답들 사이에서 저울질하는 듯했습니다."]], [["“If time could be spared,” said Mr.", "\"시간을 낼 수만 있다면,\""], ["Bucket, “which is the only thing that can’t be spared in this case, I might get it out of that woman; but it’s too doubtful a chance to trust to under present circumstances.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"—이 사건에서 낼 수 없는 유일한 게 바로 시간이지만요—저 여자한테서 알아낼 수도 있을 겁니다."], ["They are up to keeping a close eye upon her, and any fool knows that a poor creetur like her, beaten and kicked and scarred and bruised from head to foot, will stand by the husband that ill uses her through thick and thin.", "하지만 지금 상황에서 믿고 맡기기엔 너무 미덥잖은 기회지요. 저들은 그 여자를 바짝 감시하는 데 능하고, 머리부터 발끝까지 두들겨 맞고 걷어차이고 흉터투성이에 멍투성이인 저 여자 같은 가엾은 것이, 그렇게 자기를 학대하는 남편 편을 무슨 일이 있어도 들 거라는 건 바보라도 압니다."], ["There’s something kept back.", "감춰둔 게 있어요."], ["It’s a pity but what we had seen the other woman.”", "다른 여자를 만나지 못한 게 안타깝군요.\""]], [["I regretted it exceedingly, for she was very grateful, and I felt sure would have resisted no entreaty of mine.", "저는 그것을 몹시 안타까워했으니, 그녀는 무척 고마워했었고, 제 어떤 간청에도 거역하지 않았으리라고 확신했기 때문입니다."]], [["“It’s possible, Miss Summerson,” said Mr.", "\"이럴 수도 있습니다, 서머슨 양.\""], ["Bucket, pondering on it, “that her ladyship sent her up to London with some word for you, and it’s possible that her husband got the watch to let her go.", "버킷 씨가 그것을 곰곰이 따지며 말했습니다. \"마님이 아가씨한테 전할 말을 들려서 그 여자를 런던으로 올려보냈고, 남편이 그녀를 보내주는 대가로 시계를 챙긴 것일 수도 있지요."], ["It don’t come out altogether so plain as to please me, but it’s on the cards.", "제 마음에 흡족할 만큼 죄다 명확히 드러나지는 않지만, 그럴 법은 합니다."], ["Now, I don’t take kindly to laying out the money of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, on these roughs, and I don’t see my way to the usefulness of it at present.", "자, 저는 준남작 레스터 데드록 경의 돈을 저런 거친 자들한테 뿌리는 게 달갑지 않고, 지금으로선 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있는지도 모르겠습니다. 아니요!"], ["No! So far our road, Miss Summerson, is for’ard—straight ahead—and keeping everything quiet!”", "지금까지로 보면 우리가 갈 길은, 서머슨 양, 앞으로—곧장 앞으로—그리고 만사를 조용히 하는 겁니다!\""]], [["We called at home once more that I might send a hasty note to my guardian, and then we hurried back to where we had left the carriage.", "우리는 후견인에게 급히 쪽지를 보내려고 다시 한번 집에 들렀다가, 마차를 두고 온 곳으로 서둘러 돌아갔습니다."], ["The horses were brought out as soon as we were seen coming, and we were on the road again in a few minutes.", "우리가 오는 것이 보이자마자 말이 끌려 나왔고, 우리는 몇 분 만에 다시 길에 올랐습니다."]], [["It had set in snowing at daybreak, and it now snowed hard.", "날이 밝을 무렵부터 눈이 내리기 시작했는데, 이제는 거세게 쏟아졌습니다."], ["The air was so thick with the darkness of the day and the density of the fall that we could see but a very little way in any direction.", "낮의 어둠과 쏟아지는 눈의 자욱함으로 공기가 어찌나 빽빽한지, 어느 쪽으로도 아주 조금밖에 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Although it was extremely cold, the snow was but partially frozen, and it churned—with a sound as if it were a beach of small shells—under the hoofs of the horses into mire and water.", "몹시 추웠는데도 눈은 부분적으로만 얼어, 말발굽에 짓이겨지며—작은 조개껍데기가 깔린 바닷가 같은 소리를 내며—진창과 물이 되었습니다."], ["They sometimes slipped and floundered for a mile together, and we were obliged to come to a standstill to rest them.", "말들은 이따금 1마일을 내리 미끄러지고 허우적댔고, 우리는 말을 쉬게 하려고 멈춰 서야 했습니다."], ["One horse fell three times in this first stage, and trembled so and was so shaken that the driver had to dismount from his saddle and lead him at last.", "한 마리는 이 첫 구간에서 세 번이나 넘어졌고, 어찌나 떨고 흔들리는지 마부가 결국 안장에서 내려 끌고 가야 했습니다."]], [["I could eat nothing and could not sleep, and I grew so nervous under those delays and the slow pace at which we travelled that I had an unreasonable desire upon me to get out and walk.", "저는 아무것도 먹을 수 없었고 잠도 잘 수 없었으며, 그런 지체와 우리가 가는 더딘 속도에 어찌나 초조해졌던지, 마차에서 내려 걷고 싶은 터무니없는 마음이 일었습니다."], ["Yielding to my companion’s better sense, however, I remained where I was.", "하지만 제 동행의 더 나은 분별에 따라 저는 그대로 머물렀습니다."], ["All this time, kept fresh by a certain enjoyment of the work in which he was engaged, he was up and down at every house we came to, addressing people whom he had never beheld before as old acquaintances, running in to warm himself at every fire he saw, talking and drinking and shaking hands at every bar and tap, friendly with every waggoner, wheelwright, blacksmith, and toll-taker, yet never seeming to lose time, and always mounting to the box again with his watchful, steady face and his business-like “Get on, my lad!”", "이 시간 내내 그는 자기가 하는 일에서 어떤 즐거움을 느끼며 생기를 잃지 않은 채, 우리가 다다르는 집마다 오르내리며, 한 번도 본 적 없는 사람들을 오랜 친지인 양 대했고, 보이는 불마다 뛰어들어 몸을 녹였으며, 술집과 선술집마다 이야기하고 마시고 악수하면서, 짐마차꾼이며 수레바퀴장이며 대장장이며 통행료 징수원과 누구하고나 살갑게 굴었지만, 한 번도 시간을 허비하는 법이 없었고, 늘 빈틈없이 한결같은 얼굴과 일하는 듯한 \"가자, 이 사람아!\"와 함께 다시 마부석에 올랐습니다."]], [["When we were changing horses the next time, he came from the stable-yard, with the wet snow encrusted upon him and dropping off him—plashing and crashing through it to his wet knees as he had been doing frequently since we left Saint Albans—and spoke to me at the carriage side.", "다음번에 말을 바꿀 때, 그는 마구간 마당에서 나왔는데, 젖은 눈이 그에게 더께처럼 엉겨 붙었다가 떨어지고 있었고—세인트올번스를 떠난 뒤로 자주 그랬듯이 젖은 무릎까지 눈을 철벅철벅 으스러뜨리며 헤치고 다녔습니다—그가 마차 옆에서 제게 말했습니다."]], [["“Keep up your spirits.", "\"기운 내십시오."], ["It’s certainly true that she came on here, Miss Summerson.", "그분이 여기까지 오신 건 분명 사실입니다, 서머슨 양."], ["There’s not a doubt of the dress by this time, and the dress has been seen here.”", "이제 옷차림에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없고, 그 옷차림이 여기서 목격되었습니다.\""]], [["“Still on foot?”", "\"여전히 걸어서요?\""], ["said I.", "제가 말했습니다."]], [["“Still on foot.", "\"여전히 걸어서요."], ["I think the gentleman you mentioned must be the point she’s aiming at, and yet I don’t like his living down in her own part of the country neither.”", "아가씨가 말씀하신 그 신사분이 그분이 향하는 지점인 것 같긴 합니다만, 그래도 그분이 마님 자신의 고장 쪽에 사는 게 영 마음에 걸립니다.\""]], [["“I know so little,” said I.", "\"제가 아는 게 너무 없어서요.\""], ["“There may be some one else nearer here, of whom I never heard.”", "제가 말했습니다. \"이 근처에 제가 들어본 적 없는 다른 누군가가 있을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“That’s true.", "\"그렇긴 합니다."], ["But whatever you do, don’t you fall a-crying, my dear; and don’t you worry yourself no more than you can help.", "하지만 무슨 일이 있어도 울지는 마십시오, 아가씨. 그리고 어쩔 수 없는 것 이상으로 애태우지도 마시고요."], ["Get on, my lad!”", "가자, 이 사람아!\""]], [["The sleet fell all that day unceasingly, a thick mist came on early, and it never rose or lightened for a moment.", "진눈깨비가 그날 온종일 쉴 새 없이 내렸고, 짙은 안개가 일찍 끼어, 한순간도 걷히거나 옅어지지 않았습니다."], ["Such roads I had never seen.", "그런 길은 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["I sometimes feared we had missed the way and got into the ploughed grounds or the marshes.", "저는 이따금 우리가 길을 잘못 들어 갈아엎은 밭이나 늪으로 들어선 게 아닌가 두려웠습니다."], ["If I ever thought of the time I had been out, it presented itself as an indefinite period of great duration, and I seemed, in a strange way, never to have been free from the anxiety under which I then laboured.", "제가 나와 있은 시간을 떠올려보려 하면, 그것은 한없이 길게 이어진 막연한 기간으로 다가왔고, 이상하게도 저는 그때 시달리던 불안에서 한 번도 벗어나본 적이 없는 것만 같았습니다."]], [["As we advanced, I began to feel misgivings that my companion lost confidence.", "앞으로 나아갈수록 저는 제 동행이 자신감을 잃어가는 게 아닌가 하는 불안한 예감이 들기 시작했습니다."], ["He was the same as before with all the roadside people, but he looked graver when he sat by himself on the box.", "그는 길가의 사람들을 대할 때는 전과 다름없었으나, 마부석에 혼자 앉아 있을 때는 더 심각한 얼굴이었습니다."], ["I saw his finger uneasily going across and across his mouth during the whole of one long weary stage.", "길고 지루한 한 구간 내내 그의 손가락이 불안하게 입가를 가로질러 오가는 것을 저는 보았습니다."], ["I overheard that he began to ask the drivers of coaches and other vehicles coming towards us what passengers they had seen in other coaches and vehicles that were in advance.", "우리 쪽으로 다가오는 마차와 다른 탈것의 마부들에게, 앞서가는 다른 마차와 탈것에서 어떤 승객들을 보았는지 묻기 시작하는 것도 엿들었습니다."], ["Their replies did not encourage him.", "그들의 대답은 그를 북돋아주지 못했습니다."], ["He always gave me a reassuring beck of his finger and lift of his eyelid as he got upon the box again, but he seemed perplexed now when he said, “Get on, my lad!”", "그는 다시 마부석에 오를 때마다 늘 손가락을 까딱이고 눈꺼풀을 들어 올리며 저를 안심시켰으나, 이제는 \"가자, 이 사람아!\" 하고 말할 때 당황한 듯했습니다."]], [["At last, when we were changing, he told me that he had lost the track of the dress so long that he began to be surprised.", "마침내 말을 바꿀 때, 그는 옷차림의 자취를 너무 오래 놓쳐 슬슬 의아해지기 시작했다고 제게 말했습니다."], ["It was nothing, he said, to lose such a track for one while, and to take it up for another while, and so on; but it had disappeared here in an unaccountable manner, and we had not come upon it since.", "그런 자취를 한동안 놓쳤다가 또 한동안 잡고, 그렇게 이어가는 거야 별일 아니라고 했습니다. 하지만 여기서는 영문 모르게 자취가 사라졌고, 그 뒤로 다시 잡지 못했다는 것이었습니다."], ["This corroborated the apprehensions I had formed, when he began to look at direction-posts, and to leave the carriage at cross roads for a quarter of an hour at a time while he explored them.", "이것은 그가 이정표를 살펴보기 시작하고, 갈림길마다 한 번에 십오 분씩 마차에서 내려 길을 탐색하기 시작했을 때 제가 품었던 불안을 뒷받침해주었습니다."], ["But I was not to be down-hearted, he told me, for it was as likely as not that the next stage might set us right again.", "하지만 그는 제게 낙심하지 말라고 했으니, 다음 구간에서 다시 제대로 길을 잡게 될 공산이 충분하다는 것이었습니다."]], [["The next stage, however, ended as that one ended; we had no new clue.", "그러나 다음 구간도 그 구간이 끝난 것처럼 끝났습니다. 우리는 새로운 실마리를 얻지 못했습니다."], ["There was a spacious inn here, solitary, but a comfortable substantial building, and as we drove in under a large gateway before I knew it, where a landlady and her pretty daughters came to the carriage-door, entreating me to alight and refresh myself while the horses were making ready, I thought it would be uncharitable to refuse.", "거기에는 외딴, 그러나 아늑하고 듬직한 건물인 널찍한 여관이 하나 있었습니다. 제가 미처 알아채기도 전에 큰 대문 아래로 마차를 몰고 들어서자, 여주인과 그녀의 예쁜 딸들이 마차 문으로 나와, 말을 준비하는 동안 내려서 몸을 녹이시라고 제게 청했습니다."], ["They took me upstairs to a warm room and left me there.", "저는 거절하는 게 매정한 일일 것 같았습니다. 그들은 저를 따뜻한 방으로 데려가 위층에 남겨두었습니다."]], [["It was at the corner of the house, I remember, looking two ways.", "기억하기로 그곳은 집 모퉁이여서 두 방향으로 내다보였습니다."], ["On one side to a stable-yard open to a by-road, where the ostlers were unharnessing the splashed and tired horses from the muddy carriage, and beyond that to the by-road itself, across which the sign was heavily swinging; on the other side to a wood of dark pine-trees.", "한쪽으로는 샛길로 트인 마구간 마당이 보였는데, 거기서 마부들이 진흙투성이 마차에서 흙탕물을 뒤집어쓴 지친 말들의 마구를 풀고 있었고, 그 너머로는 샛길 자체가 보였는데 그 위로 간판이 묵직하게 흔들리고 있었습니다. 다른 쪽으로는 어두운 소나무 숲이 보였습니다."], ["Their branches were encumbered with snow, and it silently dropped off in wet heaps while I stood at the window.", "그 가지들은 눈을 잔뜩 이고 있었고, 제가 창가에 서 있는 동안 그 눈이 젖은 무더기로 소리 없이 떨어졌습니다."], ["Night was setting in, and its bleakness was enhanced by the contrast of the pictured fire glowing and gleaming in the window-pane.", "밤이 깃들고 있었고, 창유리에 어리어 환히 타오르며 어른거리는 난롯불의 영상과 대비되어 그 황량함이 한층 짙어졌습니다."], ["As I looked among the stems of the trees and followed the discoloured marks in the snow where the thaw was sinking into it and undermining it, I thought of the motherly face brightly set off by daughters that had just now welcomed me and of MY mother lying down in such a wood to die.", "나무 줄기들 사이를 바라보며, 눈 속에 스며들어 그것을 무너뜨리는 해빙의 흔적을 따라 변색된 자국들을 좇으며, 저는 방금 저를 맞아준, 딸들 덕에 환하게 빛나던 그 자애로운 얼굴과, 그런 숲에 누워 죽어가는 제 어머니를 떠올렸습니다."]], [["I was frightened when I found them all about me, but I remembered that before I fainted I tried very hard not to do it; and that was some little comfort.", "그들이 모두 제 주위에 둘러서 있는 것을 깨닫고 저는 깜짝 놀랐는데, 정신을 잃기 전에 그러지 않으려고 무던히 애썼던 것이 떠올랐습니다. 그것이 조금은 위안이 되었습니다."], ["They cushioned me up on a large sofa by the fire, and then the comely landlady told me that I must travel no further to-night, but must go to bed.", "그들은 난롯가의 큰 소파에 저를 방석으로 받쳐 앉혔고, 그러고 나서 그 인상 좋은 여주인이 오늘 밤은 더 가지 말고 잠자리에 들어야 한다고 제게 말했습니다."], ["But this put me into such a tremble lest they should detain me there that she soon recalled her words and compromised for a rest of half an hour.", "하지만 이 말에 저는 그들이 저를 거기 붙잡아둘까 봐 어찌나 떨었던지, 그녀는 곧 그 말을 거두고 반 시간 쉬는 것으로 타협을 보았습니다."]], [["A good endearing creature she was.", "참 정겹고 사랑스러운 사람이었습니다."], ["She and her three fair girls, all so busy about me. I was to take hot soup and broiled fowl, while Mr.", "그녀와 그녀의 예쁜 세 딸이 모두 저를 그토록 분주히 보살폈습니다."], ["Bucket dried himself and dined elsewhere; but I could not do it when a snug round table was presently spread by the fireside, though I was very unwilling to disappoint them.", "버킷 씨가 몸을 말리며 다른 데서 식사하는 동안 저는 뜨거운 수프와 닭구이를 먹기로 되어 있었습니다. 하지만 이내 난롯가에 아늑하고 둥근 식탁이 차려졌을 때, 저는 그들을 실망시키고 싶지 않았는데도 그것을 먹을 수가 없었습니다."], ["However, I could take some toast and some hot negus; and as I really enjoyed that refreshment, it made some recompense.", "그래도 토스트 조금과 뜨거운 니거스는 들 수 있었고, 그 음료를 정말로 맛있게 즐겼기에 어느 정도 보답이 되었습니다."]], [["Punctual to the time, at the half-hour’s end the carriage came rumbling under the gateway, and they took me down, warmed, refreshed, comforted by kindness, and safe (I assured them) not to faint any more.", "시간에 꼭 맞추어, 반 시간이 다 되었을 때 마차가 대문 아래로 덜컹거리며 다가왔고, 그들은 저를 아래층으로 데려갔습니다. 몸이 따뜻해지고 기운을 차리고 그들의 친절에 위로받은 저는, (다시는 정신을 잃지 않을 것이라고 그들에게 장담하며) 무사히 내려갔습니다."], ["After I had got in and had taken a grateful leave of them all, the youngest daughter—a blooming girl of nineteen, who was to be the first married, they had told me—got upon the carriage step, reached in, and kissed me.", "마차에 올라 그들 모두와 고마운 작별을 나눈 뒤, 막내딸이—열아홉의 활짝 핀 처녀로, 가장 먼저 결혼하게 될 거라고 그들이 일러주었습니다—마차 발판에 올라 안으로 손을 뻗어 제게 입을 맞추었습니다."], ["I have never seen her, from that hour, but I think of her to this hour as my friend.", "그 시각 이후로 저는 그녀를 다시 본 적이 없으나, 지금 이 순간까지도 그녀를 제 벗으로 떠올립니다."]], [["The transparent windows with the fire and light, looking so bright and warm from the cold darkness out of doors, were soon gone, and again we were crushing and churning the loose snow.", "난롯불과 등불이 어린, 바깥의 차가운 어둠에서 그토록 환하고 따뜻해 보이던 그 투명한 창문들은 곧 사라졌고, 우리는 다시 헐거운 눈을 으스러뜨리고 짓이기며 나아갔습니다."], ["We went on with toil enough, but the dismal roads were not much worse than they had been, and the stage was only nine miles.", "우리는 어지간히 고생하며 갔으나, 그 음울한 길들은 여태까지보다 그리 더 나쁘지는 않았고, 그 구간은 9마일밖에 되지 않았습니다."], ["My companion smoking on the box—I had thought at the last inn of begging him to do so when I saw him standing at a great fire in a comfortable cloud of tobacco—was as vigilant as ever and as quickly down and up again when we came to any human abode or any human creature.", "마부석에서 담배를 피우는 제 동행은—아까 그 여관에서 그가 아늑한 담배 연기 구름에 싸여 활활 타는 난롯불 앞에 서 있는 것을 보고, 그렇게 피우시라고 청할 생각을 했었습니다—여전히 빈틈없이 경계했고, 우리가 사람 사는 집이나 사람을 마주칠 때마다 여전히 잽싸게 내렸다가 올랐습니다."], ["He had lighted his little dark lantern, which seemed to be a favourite with him, for we had lamps to the carriage; and every now and then he turned it upon me to see that I was doing well.", "그는 자기가 아끼는 듯한 그 작고 어둑한 등에 불을 켜두었는데—마차에는 등불이 따로 있었는데도요—이따금 그것을 제 쪽으로 돌려 제가 괜찮은지 살폈습니다."], ["There was a folding-window to the carriage-head, but I never closed it, for it seemed like shutting out hope.", "마차 덮개에는 접이식 창문이 있었으나, 저는 한 번도 그것을 닫지 않았으니, 그것은 희망을 닫아거는 것만 같았기 때문입니다."]], [["We came to the end of the stage, and still the lost trace was not recovered.", "우리는 구간 끝에 다다랐으나, 여전히 잃어버린 자취를 되찾지 못했습니다."], ["I looked at him anxiously when we stopped to change, but I knew by his yet graver face as he stood watching the ostlers that he had heard nothing.", "말을 바꾸려고 멈췄을 때 저는 그를 애타게 바라보았으나, 마부들을 지켜보며 서 있는 그의 한층 심각해진 얼굴로 그가 아무것도 듣지 못했음을 알았습니다."], ["Almost in an instant afterwards, as I leaned back in my seat, he looked in, with his lighted lantern in his hand, an excited and quite different man.", "그러고 나서 거의 곧바로, 제가 자리에 등을 기대고 있는데, 그가 켜둔 등불을 손에 들고 들여다보았으니, 흥분한, 사뭇 딴사람 같은 모습이었습니다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이에요?\""], ["said I, starting.", "제가 깜짝 놀라 말했습니다."], ["“Is she here?”", "\"그분이 여기 계세요?\""]], [["“No, no.", "\"아니요, 아니요."], ["Don’t deceive yourself, my dear.", "착각하지 마십시오, 아가씨."], ["Nobody’s here.", "아무도 여기 없습니다."], ["But I’ve got it!”", "하지만 알아냈습니다!\""]], [["The crystallized snow was in his eyelashes, in his hair, lying in ridges on his dress.", "결정처럼 얼어붙은 눈이 그의 속눈썹에, 머리카락에 박혀 있었고, 옷에는 이랑처럼 쌓여 있었습니다."], ["He had to shake it from his face and get his breath before he spoke to me.", "그는 제게 말하기 전에 얼굴에서 그것을 털어내고 숨을 돌려야 했습니다."]], [["“Now, Miss Summerson,”", "\"자, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said he, beating his finger on the apron, “don’t you be disappointed at what I’m a-going to do.", "그가 마차 앞치마를 손가락으로 두드리며 말했습니다. \"제가 이제 하려는 일에 실망하지 마십시오."], ["You know me.", "아가씨는 저를 아시잖습니까."], ["I’m Inspector Bucket, and you can trust me.", "저는 버킷 경위이고, 아가씨는 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다."], ["We’ve come a long way; never mind.", "먼 길을 왔지만, 괜찮습니다."], ["Four horses out there for the next stage up!", "다음 구간으로 올라갈 말 네 마리 거기 대령하라!"], ["Quick!”", "어서!\""]], [["There was a commotion in the yard, and a man came running out of the stables to know if he meant up or down.", "마당에 소동이 일었고, 한 사내가 마구간에서 뛰쳐나와 위로 가시는 거냐 아래로 가시는 거냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Up, I tell you!", "\"위라고, 이 사람아!"], ["Up!", "위로!"], ["Ain’t it English?", "영어로 못 알아듣나?"], ["Up!”", "위로!\""]], [["“Up?”", "\"위로요?\""], ["said I, astonished.", "제가 놀라서 말했습니다."], ["“To London!", "\"런던으로요!"], ["Are we going back?”", "우리 돌아가는 건가요?\""]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["he answered, “back.", "그가 대답했습니다. \"돌아갑니다."], ["Straight back as a die.", "화살처럼 곧장 되돌아갑니다."], ["You know me.", "아가씨는 저를 아시잖습니까."], ["Don’t be afraid.", "두려워하지 마십시오."], ["I’ll follow the other, by G——”", "다른 쪽을 쫓겠습니다, 맹세코—\""]], [["“The other?”", "\"다른 쪽이요?\""], ["I repeated.", "제가 되물었습니다."], ["“Who?”", "\"누구를요?\""]], [["“You called her Jenny, didn’t you?", "\"그 여자를 제니라고 부르셨지요, 그렇지요?"], ["I’ll follow her.", "그 여자를 쫓겠습니다."], ["Bring those two pair out here for a crown a man.", "저기 그 두 쌍을 한 사람당 1크라운에 끌어내!"], ["Wake up, some of you!”", "정신들 차려, 이 사람들아!\""]], [["“You will not desert this lady we are in search of; you will not abandon her on such a night and in such a state of mind as I know her to be in!”", "\"우리가 찾는 이 부인을 저버리시면 안 됩니다. 제가 아는바 그분이 처한 이런 밤에, 이런 마음 상태에서 그분을 내버리시면 안 됩니다!\""], ["said I, in an agony, and grasping his hand.", "제가 괴로움에 차서, 그의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했습니다."]], [["“You are right, my dear, I won’t.", "\"옳으신 말씀입니다, 아가씨, 저버리지 않을 겁니다."], ["But I’ll follow the other.", "하지만 다른 쪽을 쫓겠습니다."], ["Look alive here with them horses.", "여기 말들, 어서 서두르게."], ["Send a man for’ard in the saddle to the next stage, and let him send another for’ard again, and order four on, up, right through.", "다음 구간까지 안장에 사람 하나를 앞서 보내고, 그 사람한테 또 하나를 앞서 보내라고 해서, 위로, 곧장 내처 네 마리씩 대령하라고 일러."], ["My darling, don’t you be afraid!”", "아가씨, 두려워하지 마십시오!\""]], [["These orders and the way in which he ran about the yard urging them caused a general excitement that was scarcely less bewildering to me than the sudden change.", "이 지시들과 그가 마당을 뛰어다니며 그들을 다그치는 모습은, 그 갑작스러운 방향 전환 못지않게 저를 어리둥절하게 하는 전반적인 흥분을 불러일으켰습니다."], ["But in the height of the confusion, a mounted man galloped away to order the relays, and our horses were put to with great speed.", "하지만 한창 혼란이 일던 중에, 말 탄 사내 하나가 갈아탈 말을 준비시키러 달려 나갔고, 우리 말들은 무척 빠르게 매여졌습니다."]], [["“My dear,” said Mr.", "\"아가씨.\""], ["Bucket, jumping to his seat and looking in again, “—you’ll excuse me if I’m too familiar—don’t you fret and worry yourself no more than you can help.", "버킷 씨가 자기 자리로 뛰어오르며 다시 들여다보고 말했습니다. \"—너무 허물없이 굴어도 용서하십시오—어쩔 수 없는 것 이상으로 애태우고 걱정하지 마십시오."], ["I say nothing else at present; but you know me, my dear; now, don’t you?”", "지금은 더 아무 말도 않겠습니다. 하지만 아가씨는 저를 아시잖습니까, 그렇지요?\""]], [["I endeavoured to say that I knew he was far more capable than I of deciding what we ought to do, but was he sure that this was right? Could I not go forward by myself in search of—I grasped his hand again in my distress and whispered it to him—of my own mother.", "저는 우리가 어떻게 해야 할지 결정하는 데 그가 저보다 훨씬 더 능하다는 것은 안다고 말하려 했으나, 정말 이게 옳은지 그가 확신하느냐고, 제가 혼자라도 앞으로 나아가 찾아보면 안 되느냐고—괴로움에 저는 다시 그의 손을 움켜쥐고 그에게 속삭였습니다—제 친어머니를 찾아 나서면 안 되느냐고 했습니다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"아가씨.\" 그가 대답했습니다."], ["he answered, “I know, I know, and would I put you wrong, do you think?", "\"압니다, 압니다. 제가 아가씨를 그르치게 하겠습니까, 그럴 것 같습니까?"], ["Inspector Bucket.", "버킷 경위입니다."], ["Now you know me, don’t you?”", "자, 아가씨는 저를 아시지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["What could I say but yes!", "네, 말고 무어라 할 수 있었겠습니까!"]], [["“Then you keep up as good a heart as you can, and you rely upon me for standing by you, no less than by Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet.", "\"그러면 되도록 마음을 굳게 다잡으시고, 준남작 레스터 데드록 경 못지않게 아가씨 곁을 지킬 저를 믿으십시오."], ["Now, are you right there?”", "자, 거기 괜찮으십니까?\""]], [["“All right, sir!”", "\"다 괜찮아요!\""]], [["“Off she goes, then.", "\"그럼, 출발이다."], ["And get on, my lads!”", "그리고 가자, 이 사람들아!\""]], [["We were again upon the melancholy road by which we had come, tearing up the miry sleet and thawing snow as if they were torn up by a waterwheel.", "우리는 다시 우리가 왔던 그 처량한 길에 올라, 마치 물레방아에 뜯겨 나가듯 진창 진눈깨비와 녹아내리는 눈을 헤집으며 나아갔습니다."]], [["A Wintry Day and Night\nStill impassive, as behoves its breeding, the Dedlock town house carries itself as usual towards the street of dismal grandeur.", "조상 대대로 물려받은 기품이 명하는 대로 여전히 무표정한 채, 데드록 저택은 음울한 위엄으로 가득한 거리를 향해 평소와 다름없는 자태를 유지한다."], ["There are powdered heads from time to time in the little windows of the hall, looking out at the untaxed powder falling all day from the sky; and in the same conservatory there is peach blossom turning itself exotically to the great hall fire from the nipping weather out of doors.", "현관의 작은 창마다 이따금 분 바른 머리가 내다보이며, 종일 하늘에서 떨어지는 세금 없는 가루눈을 바라본다. 같은 온실 안에서는 복숭아꽃이 바깥의 살을 에는 날씨를 등지고 커다란 홀의 난롯불 쪽으로 이국적인 자태를 돌리고 있다."], ["It is given out that my Lady has gone down into Lincolnshire, but is expected to return presently.", "마님은 링컨셔로 내려갔으나 머지않아 돌아오리라고 알려져 있다."]], [["Rumour, busy overmuch, however, will not go down into Lincolnshire.", "그러나 지나치게 부산한 소문은 링컨셔로 내려가려 하지 않는다."], ["It persists in flitting and chattering about town.", "소문은 한사코 도시 안을 날아다니며 재잘거린다."], ["It knows that that poor unfortunate man, Sir Leicester, has been sadly used.", "소문은 그 가엾고 불운한 사내, 레스터 경이 딱하게 봉변당했음을 안다."], ["It hears, my dear child, all sorts of shocking things.", "소문은, 가엾은 그대여, 온갖 충격적인 이야기를 듣는다."], ["It makes the world of five miles round quite merry.", "소문은 반경 오 마일의 세상을 흥겨움으로 들썩이게 한다."], ["Not to know that there is something wrong at the Dedlocks’ is to augur yourself unknown.", "데드록 가에 무언가 잘못된 일이 벌어졌음을 모른다는 것은, 곧 자기 자신이 무명인사임을 떠벌리는 꼴이다."], ["One of the peachy-cheeked charmers with the skeleton throats is already apprised of all the principal circumstances that will come out before the Lords on Sir Leicester’s application for a bill of divorce.", "해골처럼 앙상한 목에 복숭앗빛 뺨을 가진 매혹적인 여인 가운데 하나는, 레스터 경이 이혼 청원서를 내면 상원에서 드러날 주요 정황을 벌써 죄다 꿰고 있다."]], [["At Blaze and Sparkle’s the jewellers and at Sheen and Gloss’s the mercers, it is and will be for several hours the topic of the age, the feature of the century.", "보석상 블레이즈앤드스파클에서도, 포목상 신앤드글로스에서도, 이 일은 몇 시간 동안 시대의 화두요 세기의 사건이 되며 또 될 것이다."], ["The patronesses of those establishments, albeit so loftily inscrutable, being as nicely weighed and measured there as any other article of the stock-in-trade, are perfectly understood in this new fashion by the rawest hand behind the counter.", "그 가게의 후원자인 귀부인들은 비록 그토록 도도하고 속을 알 수 없을지언정, 거기서는 다른 어떤 진열 상품 못지않게 꼼꼼히 무게가 달리고 치수가 재어지는 터라, 계산대 뒤에 선 가장 풋내기 점원까지도 이 새로운 유행을 완벽히 알아챈다."], ["“Our people, Mr. Jones,”", "\"우리 손님은 말일세, 존스 군.\""], ["said Blaze and Sparkle to the hand in question on engaging him, “our people, sir, are sheep—mere sheep.", "블레이즈앤드스파클이 그 점원을 채용하면서 일러둔 말이다. \"우리 손님은, 여보게, 양 떼라네, 그저 양 떼야."], ["Where two or three marked ones go, all the rest follow.", "표시 박힌 두세 마리가 가는 데로 나머지가 죄다 따라가지."], ["Keep those two or three in your eye, Mr.", "그 두세 마리만 잘 지켜보게, 존스 군, 그러면 자네는 양 떼를 손에 쥔 거야.\""], ["Jones, and you have the flock.”", "신앤드글로스 또한 자기네 존스에게 똑같이 일러둔다."], ["So, likewise, Sheen and Gloss to THEIR Jones, in reference to knowing where to have the fashionable people and how to bring what they (Sheen and Gloss) choose into fashion.", "어디 가야 유행을 좇는 사람들을 붙잡을지, 자기네(신앤드글로스)가 점찍은 물건을 어떻게 유행시킬지를 두고. 똑같이 빗나가는 법 없는 원칙에 따라, 도서관 주인이자 실로 화려한 양 떼를 길러내는 거물 사육사인 슬래더리 씨도 바로 이날 인정한다."], ["On similar unerring principles, Mr.", "\"아, 그렇고말고요, 손님."], ["Sladdery the librarian, and indeed the great farmer of gorgeous sheep, admits this very day, “Why yes, sir, there certainly ARE reports concerning Lady Dedlock, very current indeed among my high connexion, sir.", "데드록 마님에 관한 소문이 확실히 떠돕니다, 제가 거느린 상류층 단골 사이에서 정말 한창이지요. 보십시오, 제 상류층 단골은 무언가 입에 올릴 거리가 있어야 하니까요."], ["You see, my high connexion must talk about something, sir; and it’s only to get a subject into vogue with one or two ladies I could name to make it go down with the whole.", "그저 제가 이름 댈 수 있는 부인 한둘 사이에 어떤 화제를 유행시키기만 하면 그게 전체로 퍼져나가는 겁니다."], ["Just what I should have done with those ladies, sir, in the case of any novelty you had left to me to bring in, they have done of themselves in this case through knowing Lady Dedlock and being perhaps a little innocently jealous of her too, sir.", "손님께서 제게 어떤 신상품을 들여놓으라 맡기셨을 때 제가 그 부인들에게 했을 바로 그 일을, 이번 경우엔 그분들이 스스로 해낸 셈이지요. 데드록 마님을 알고 또 그분을 두고 어쩌면 조금은 천진하게 질투도 한 까닭에 말입니다, 손님."], ["You’ll find, sir, that this topic will be very popular among my high connexion.", "두고 보십시오, 손님, 이 화제는 제 상류층 단골 사이에서 아주 인기를 끌 겁니다."], ["If it had been a speculation, sir, it would have brought money.", "만약 이게 투기였다면, 손님, 돈을 벌어들였을 겁니다."], ["And when I say so, you may trust to my being right, sir, for I have made it my business to study my high connexion and to be able to wind it up like a clock, sir.”", "제가 이렇게 말씀드리면, 제 말이 옳다고 믿으셔도 됩니다, 손님. 저는 제 상류층 단골을 연구하고 시계 태엽 감듯 마음대로 다루는 것을 본업으로 삼아온 사람이니까요, 손님.\""]], [["Thus rumour thrives in the capital, and will not go down into Lincolnshire.", "이렇게 소문은 수도에서 무성히 번창하며, 링컨셔로는 내려가려 하지 않는다."], ["By half-past five, post meridian, Horse Guards’ time, it has even elicited a new remark from the Honourable Mr. Stables, which bids fair to outshine the old one, on which he has so long rested his colloquial reputation.", "근위 기병대 시계로 오후 다섯 시 반쯤에, 소문은 명예로운 스테이블스 씨에게서 새로운 한마디까지 이끌어냈으니, 그가 오랫동안 입담의 명성을 기대온 옛 농담을 능가할 만하다."], ["This sparkling sally is to the effect that although he always knew she was the best-groomed woman in the stud, he had no idea she was a bolter.", "이 번뜩이는 재담인즉, 그는 그녀가 마구간에서 가장 손질 잘 된 암말인 줄은 늘 알았지만 도망치는 말일 줄은 미처 몰랐다는 것이다."], ["It is immensely received in turf-circles.", "경마판에서 엄청난 갈채를 받는다."]], [["At feasts and festivals also, in firmaments she has often graced, and among constellations she outshone but yesterday, she is still the prevalent subject.", "연회와 축제에서도, 그녀가 자주 빛내던 창공에서도, 어제만 해도 그녀가 압도하던 별자리들 사이에서도, 그녀는 여전히 단연 화제다. 무슨 일일까?"], ["What is it?", "누구일까?"], ["Who is it?", "언제였을까?"], ["When was it?", "어디였을까?"], ["Where was it? How was it?", "어떻게 된 일일까?"], ["She is discussed by her dear friends with all the genteelest slang in vogue, with the last new word, the last new manner, the last new drawl, and the perfection of polite indifference.", "그녀의 다정한 벗들은 한창 유행하는 가장 점잖은 은어로, 가장 최신의 말투로, 가장 최신의 몸짓으로, 가장 최신의 느릿한 어조로, 그리고 완벽하게 세련된 무관심으로 그녀를 입에 올린다."], ["A remarkable feature of the theme is that it is found to be so inspiring that several people come out upon it who never came out before—positively say things!", "이 화제의 주목할 만한 특징은, 영감을 어찌나 불어넣는지 여태 한 번도 입을 열지 않던 사람들까지 입을 연다는 것이다. 정말로 한마디씩 한다!"], ["William Buffy carries one of these smartnesses from the place where he dines down to the House, where the Whip for his party hands it about with his snuff-box to keep men together who want to be off, with such effect that the Speaker (who has had it privately insinuated into his own ear under the corner of his wig) cries, “Order at the bar!”", "윌리엄 버피는 만찬 자리에서 이런 재담 하나를 들고 하원으로 내려가고, 거기서 그가 속한 당의 원내총무가 자기 코담뱃갑과 함께 그것을 돌려, 자리를 뜨고 싶어 하는 의원들을 붙잡아둔다. 그 효과가 어찌나 대단한지 의장이(가발 한 귀퉁이 밑으로 그 말이 슬며시 제 귀에 들어왔던 터라) \"정숙하시오!\""], ["three times without making an impression.", "하고 세 번을 외쳐도 아무 소용이 없다."]], [["And not the least amazing circumstance connected with her being vaguely the town talk is that people hovering on the confines of Mr.", "게다가 그녀가 막연히 도시의 화젯거리가 된 것과 관련해 가장 놀라운 정황은 이것이다."], ["Sladdery’s high connexion, people who know nothing and ever did know nothing about her, think it essential to their reputation to pretend that she is their topic too, and to retail her at second-hand with the last new word and the last new manner, and the last new drawl, and the last new polite indifference, and all the rest of it, all at second-hand but considered equal to new in inferior systems and to fainter stars.", "슬래더리 씨가 거느린 상류층 단골의 변두리를 어슬렁대는 사람들, 그녀에 대해 아무것도 모르고 여태 한 번도 안 적이 없는 사람들이, 자기네도 그녀를 화제로 삼는 척하는 것이 제 평판에 필수라고 여겨, 가장 최신의 말투로, 가장 최신의 몸짓으로, 가장 최신의 느릿한 어조로, 가장 최신의 세련된 무관심으로, 그 밖의 모든 것을 곁들여 그녀를 재탕으로 팔아넘긴다는 점이다. 죄다 재탕이지만 더 열등한 체계와 더 희미한 별들 사이에서는 새것이나 진배없이 통한다."], ["If there be any man of letters, art, or science among these little dealers, how noble in him to support the feeble sisters on such majestic crutches!", "만약 이 하찮은 장사꾼들 가운데 문필가나 예술가나 학자가 하나라도 있다면, 그토록 위풍당당한 목발로 가냘픈 자매들을 떠받쳐주다니 얼마나 고귀한 일인가!"]], [["So goes the wintry day outside the Dedlock mansion.", "데드록 저택 바깥으로는 겨울날이 이렇게 흘러간다."], ["How within it?", "안은 어떨까?"]], [["Sir Leicester, lying in his bed, can speak a little, though with difficulty and indistinctness.", "침대에 누운 레스터 경은 힘겹고 또렷하지 못할망정 조금은 말을 할 수 있다."], ["He is enjoined to silence and to rest, and they have given him some opiate to lull his pain, for his old enemy is very hard with him.", "그에게는 침묵과 안정이 처방되었고, 사람들은 고통을 달래려 아편을 조금 먹였으니, 오랜 숙적이 그를 호되게 다루는 까닭이다."], ["He is never asleep, though sometimes he seems to fall into a dull waking doze.", "그는 결코 잠들지 않으나, 이따금 멍하게 깨어 있는 선잠에 빠지는 듯도 하다."], ["He caused his bedstead to be moved out nearer to the window when he heard it was such inclement weather, and his head to be so adjusted that he could see the driving snow and sleet.", "이토록 험악한 날씨라는 말을 듣고 그는 침대를 창가로 더 가까이 옮기게 했고, 머리를 휘날리는 눈과 진눈깨비가 보이도록 손보게 했다."], ["He watches it as it falls, throughout the whole wintry day.", "그는 그것이 떨어지는 모습을 겨울날 내내 지켜본다."]], [["Upon the least noise in the house, which is kept hushed, his hand is at the pencil.", "쥐죽은 듯 조용히 유지되는 집 안에서 아주 작은 소리만 나도, 그의 손은 연필로 향한다."], ["The old housekeeper, sitting by him, knows what he would write and whispers, “No, he has not come back yet, Sir Leicester.", "곁에 앉은 늙은 가정부는 그가 무엇을 쓰려는지 알기에 속삭인다. \"아닙니다, 그분은 아직 돌아오지 않으셨어요, 레스터 경."], ["It was late last night when he went.", "어젯밤 늦게야 떠나셨답니다."], ["He has been but a little time gone yet.”", "떠난 지 아직 얼마 되지 않았어요.\""]], [["He withdraws his hand and falls to looking at the sleet and snow again until they seem, by being long looked at, to fall so thick and fast that he is obliged to close his eyes for a minute on the giddy whirl of white flakes and icy blots.", "그는 손을 거두고 다시 진눈깨비와 눈을 바라보기 시작하는데, 오래 바라본 탓에 그것이 어찌나 빽빽하고 빠르게 떨어지는 듯하던지, 그는 어지러이 소용돌이치는 흰 눈송이와 얼음 얼룩 앞에서 잠깐 눈을 감을 수밖에 없다."]], [["He began to look at them as soon as it was light.", "그는 날이 밝자마자 그것을 바라보기 시작했다."], ["The day is not yet far spent when he conceives it to be necessary that her rooms should be prepared for her.", "그녀의 방을 그녀를 위해 준비해두어야겠다는 생각이 들 때만 해도 날은 아직 많이 가지 않았다."], ["It is very cold and wet.", "몹시 춥고 축축하다."], ["Let there be good fires.", "난롯불을 잘 피워두어라."], ["Let them know that she is expected.", "그녀가 돌아오시기로 되어 있음을 모두에게 알려라."], ["Please see to it yourself.", "부디 그대가 직접 살펴라."], ["He writes to this purpose on his slate, and Mrs. Rouncewell with a heavy heart obeys.", "그는 석판에 이런 뜻을 적고, 라운스웰 부인은 무거운 마음으로 따른다."]], [["“For I dread, George,”", "\"이런 예감이 드는구나, 조지.\" 늙은 부인이 아들에게 말한다."], ["the old lady says to her son, who waits below to keep her company when she has a little leisure, “I dread, my dear, that my Lady will never more set foot within these walls.”", "그녀가 잠깐 짬이 날 때 곁을 지켜주려고 아래층에서 기다리던 아들이다. \"이런 예감이 들어, 얘야, 마님이 이 벽 안에 다시는 발을 들이지 못하실 것만 같구나.\""]], [["“That’s a bad presentiment, mother.”", "\"불길한 예감이군요, 어머니.\""]], [["“Nor yet within the walls of Chesney Wold, my dear.”", "\"체스니 월드 벽 안에도 다시는 못 들어오실 게다, 얘야.\""]], [["“That’s worse.", "\"그건 더 안 좋네요."], ["But why, mother?”", "한데 왜 그러세요, 어머니?\""]], [["“When I saw my Lady yesterday, George, she looked to me—and I may say at me too—as if the step on the Ghost’s Walk had almost walked her down.”", "\"어제 마님을 뵈었을 때 말이다, 조지, 내게는 그렇게 보였어, 아니 나를 보고 그러시는 것도 같았어. 유령의 길에 울리는 발소리가 마님을 거의 짓밟아버린 것만 같았단다.\""]], [["“Come, come!", "\"이런, 이런!"], ["You alarm yourself with old-story fears, mother.”", "어머니, 옛이야기에 나오는 두려움으로 공연히 마음을 졸이시네요.\""]], [["“No I don’t, my dear.", "\"아니다, 얘야."], ["No I don’t.", "아니야."], ["It’s going on for sixty year that I have been in this family, and I never had any fears for it before.", "내가 이 집안에 들어온 지 예순 해가 다 되어가건만, 그동안 한 번도 두려웠던 적이 없었어."], ["But it’s breaking up, my dear; the great old Dedlock family is breaking up.”", "그런데 무너지고 있구나, 얘야. 위대한 데드록 가문이 무너지고 있어.\""]], [["“I hope not, mother.”", "\"그렇지 않기를 바라요, 어머니.\""]], [["“I am thankful I have lived long enough to be with Sir Leicester in this illness and trouble, for I know I am not too old nor too useless to be a welcomer sight to him than anybody else in my place would be.", "\"이 병환과 환난 중에 레스터 경 곁을 지킬 만큼 오래 살았으니 감사할 따름이다. 내 자리에 있을 다른 누구보다 내가 그분께 더 반가운 모습이 될 만큼은, 내가 그리 늙지도 쓸모없지도 않다는 걸 아니까."], ["But the step on the Ghost’s Walk will walk my Lady down, George; it has been many a day behind her, and now it will pass her and go on.”", "하지만 유령의 길에 울리는 발소리가 마님을 짓밟고 말 거야, 조지. 그 소리는 오랜 세월 마님 뒤를 따라왔는데, 이제 마님을 앞질러 지나가버릴 게다.\""]], [["“Well, mother dear, I say again, I hope not.”", "\"글쎄요, 어머니, 다시 말씀드리지만, 그렇지 않기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Ah, so do I, George,”", "\"아, 나도 그렇단다, 조지.\""], ["the old lady returns, shaking her head and parting her folded hands.", "늙은 부인이 고개를 가로젓고 맞잡은 두 손을 풀며 대꾸한다."], ["“But if my fears come true, and he has to know it, who will tell him!”", "\"하지만 내 두려움이 사실로 드러나, 그분이 그걸 아셔야만 한다면, 누가 그분께 말씀드리겠니!\""]], [["“Are these her rooms?”", "\"이게 마님의 방인가요?\""]], [["“These are my Lady’s rooms, just as she left them.”", "\"여기가 마님의 방이란다, 떠나실 때 그대로지.\""]], [["“Why, now,”", "\"이런, 그러고 보니,\""], ["says the trooper, glancing round him and speaking in a lower voice, “I begin to understand how you come to think as you do think, mother.", "기병이 주위를 둘러보고 목소리를 낮추어 말한다. \"어머니가 왜 그런 생각을 하시게 됐는지 이제 알 것 같네요, 어머니."], ["Rooms get an awful look about them when they are fitted up, like these, for one person you are used to see in them, and that person is away under any shadow, let alone being God knows where.”", "방이란 게 늘 거기서 보던 누군가를 위해 이렇게 꾸며져 있는데, 그 사람이 무슨 그늘 밑으로 떠나버리면 무서운 기운이 감돌게 마련이지요. 어디로 갔는지 하느님만 아시는 처지라면 더 말할 것도 없고요.\""]], [["He is not far out.", "그의 말은 그리 빗나가지 않았다."], ["As all partings foreshadow the great final one, so, empty rooms, bereft of a familiar presence, mournfully whisper what your room and what mine must one day be.", "모든 이별이 저 위대한 마지막 이별을 예고하듯, 익숙한 존재를 잃은 텅 빈 방은 그대의 방과 나의 방이 언젠가 어떤 모습이 될지를 구슬프게 속삭인다."], ["My Lady’s state has a hollow look, thus gloomy and abandoned; and in the inner apartment, where Mr.", "이렇게 음울하고 버려진 채로, 마님의 거처는 휑한 기운을 띤다."], ["Bucket last night made his secret perquisition, the traces of her dresses and her ornaments, even the mirrors accustomed to reflect them when they were a portion of herself, have a desolate and vacant air.", "어젯밤 버킷 씨가 은밀히 뒤졌던 안쪽 방에는 그녀의 옷가지와 장신구의 자취가, 심지어 그것들이 그녀의 일부였을 때 비추던 데 익숙한 거울들마저 황량하고 공허한 기운을 풍긴다."], ["Dark and cold as the wintry day is, it is darker and colder in these deserted chambers than in many a hut that will barely exclude the weather; and though the servants heap fires in the grates and set the couches and the chairs within the warm glass screens that let their ruddy light shoot through to the furthest corners, there is a heavy cloud upon the rooms which no light will dispel.", "겨울날이 어둡고 추운 만큼, 이 버려진 방은 비바람이나 겨우 막아낼 숱한 오두막보다도 더 어둡고 더 춥다. 하인들이 화로마다 불을 가득 지피고, 발그레한 빛이 가장 먼 구석까지 쏘아 보내지는 따뜻한 유리 가리개 안쪽에 긴 의자와 안락의자를 들여놓아도, 방마다 어떤 빛으로도 걷어낼 수 없는 무거운 먹구름이 드리워 있다."]], [["The old housekeeper and her son remain until the preparations are complete, and then she returns upstairs.", "늙은 가정부와 아들은 준비가 다 끝날 때까지 머물렀다가, 그녀가 위층으로 돌아간다."], ["Volumnia has taken Mrs. Rouncewell’s place in the meantime, though pearl necklaces and rouge pots, however calculated to embellish Bath, are but indifferent comforts to the invalid under present circumstances.", "그사이 볼럼니아가 라운스웰 부인의 자리를 대신했는데, 진주 목걸이와 연지 단지가 바스 온천을 꾸미는 데는 제격일지언정 지금 같은 처지의 환자에게는 그리 신통한 위안거리가 못 된다."], ["Volumnia, not being supposed to know (and indeed not knowing) what is the matter, has found it a ticklish task to offer appropriate observations and consequently has supplied their place with distracting smoothings of the bed-linen, elaborate locomotion on tiptoe, vigilant peeping at her kinsman’s eyes, and one exasperating whisper to herself of, “He is asleep.”", "무슨 일인지 알 도리가 없다고(실은 정말 모른다) 여겨지는 볼럼니아는 적절한 말을 건네기가 영 까다로운 일임을 깨닫고, 그 대신 침대보를 정신없이 매만지고, 발끝으로 요란하게 오가고, 친척의 눈을 빈틈없이 들여다보고, 혼잣말로 \"주무시네.\" 하고 사람 속을 긁는 한마디를 속삭이는 것으로 자리를 메웠다."], ["In disproof of which superfluous remark Sir Leicester has indignantly written on the slate, “I am not.”", "이 쓸데없는 말을 반박하듯 레스터 경은 석판에 분개하며 적었다. \"안 잔다.\""]], [["Yielding, therefore, the chair at the bedside to the quaint old housekeeper, Volumnia sits at a table a little removed, sympathetically sighing.", "그리하여 볼럼니아는 머리맡 의자를 그 기묘하고 늙은 가정부에게 내어주고, 조금 떨어진 탁자에 앉아 동정 어린 한숨을 내쉰다."], ["Sir Leicester watches the sleet and snow and listens for the returning steps that he expects.", "레스터 경은 진눈깨비와 눈을 지켜보며 기다리던 발소리가 돌아오기를 귀 기울인다."], ["In the ears of his old servant, looking as if she had stepped out of an old picture-frame to attend a summoned Dedlock to another world, the silence is fraught with echoes of her own words, “Who will tell him!”", "마치 소환된 데드록 가 사람을 저세상으로 안내하려고 낡은 액자에서 걸어 나온 듯한 늙은 하녀의 귀에, 그 침묵은 제 입에서 나왔던 말의 메아리로 가득하다. \"누가 그분께 말씀드리겠니!\""]], [["He has been under his valet’s hands this morning to be made presentable and is as well got up as the circumstances will allow.", "그는 오늘 아침 시종의 손에 맡겨져 사람들 앞에 내보일 만하게 단장되었고, 형편이 허락하는 한 깔끔하게 차려졌다."], ["He is propped with pillows, his grey hair is brushed in its usual manner, his linen is arranged to a nicety, and he is wrapped in a responsible dressing-gown.", "베개로 몸을 받쳤고, 백발은 평소대로 빗질되었으며, 속옷은 흠잡을 데 없이 매만져졌고, 무게 있는 실내복으로 몸을 감쌌다."], ["His eye-glass and his watch are ready to his hand.", "외알 안경과 회중시계도 손닿는 데 놓여 있다."], ["It is necessary—less to his own dignity now perhaps than for her sake—that he should be seen as little disturbed and as much himself as may be.", "그가 되도록 동요한 기색 없이, 되도록 평소의 자신다운 모습으로 보이는 것은 필요한 일이다. 어쩌면 이제 자기 자신의 위엄을 위해서라기보다 그녀를 위해서."], ["Women will talk, and Volumnia, though a Dedlock, is no exceptional case.", "여자들이란 입을 놀리게 마련이고, 볼럼니아도 데드록 가 사람이지만 예외는 아니다."], ["He keeps her here, there is little doubt, to prevent her talking somewhere else.", "그가 그녀를 여기 붙들어두는 것은 다른 데서 입을 놀리지 못하게 하려는 뜻이 분명하다."], ["He is very ill, but he makes his present stand against distress of mind and body most courageously.", "그는 몹시 아프지만, 마음과 몸의 고통에 맞서 더없이 의연하게 버텨내고 있다."]], [["The fair Volumnia, being one of those sprightly girls who cannot long continue silent without imminent peril of seizure by the dragon Boredom, soon indicates the approach of that monster with a series of undisguisable yawns. Finding it impossible to suppress those yawns by any other process than conversation, she compliments Mrs.", "어여쁜 볼럼니아는 오래 입을 다물고 있으면 권태라는 용에게 당장 잡아먹힐 위험에 처하는 발랄한 아가씨 가운데 하나인지라, 이내 감추지 못할 하품을 연달아 쏟아내며 그 괴물이 다가옴을 드러낸다."], ["Rouncewell on her son, declaring that he positively is one of the finest figures she ever saw and as soldierly a looking person, she should think, as what’s his name, her favourite Life Guardsman—the man she dotes on, the dearest of creatures—who was killed at Waterloo.", "대화 말고는 그 하품을 억누를 도리가 없음을 깨닫고, 그녀는 라운스웰 부인에게 아들을 칭찬하며, 그가 단연 여태껏 본 가장 멋진 장부 가운데 하나요, 그러니까 이름이 뭐더라, 자기가 끔찍이 좋아하던 그 근위 기병만큼이나 군인다운 풍채라고 떠벌린다. 자기가 애지중지하던 사람, 더없이 사랑스러운 존재, 워털루에서 전사한 그 사내 말이다."]], [["Sir Leicester hears this tribute with so much surprise and stares about him in such a confused way that Mrs. Rouncewell feels it necessary to explain.", "레스터 경은 이 찬사를 어찌나 놀라워하며 듣는지, 어리둥절한 얼굴로 주위를 두리번거려, 라운스웰 부인은 설명할 필요를 느낀다."]], [["“Miss Dedlock don’t speak of my eldest son, Sir Leicester, but my youngest.", "\"데드록 아가씨가 말씀하시는 건 제 큰아들이 아니랍니다, 레스터 경. 막내아들이지요."], ["I have found him.", "그 애를 찾았습니다."], ["He has come home.”", "집으로 돌아왔어요.\""]], [["Sir Leicester breaks silence with a harsh cry.", "레스터 경이 거친 외침으로 침묵을 깬다."], ["“George?", "\"조지?"], ["Your son George come home, Mrs. Rouncewell?”", "그대의 아들 조지가 돌아왔다고, 라운스웰 부인?\""]], [["The old housekeeper wipes her eyes.", "늙은 가정부가 눈물을 훔친다."], ["“Thank God.", "\"하느님 감사합니다."], ["Yes, Sir Leicester.”", "그렇습니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["Does this discovery of some one lost, this return of some one so long gone, come upon him as a strong confirmation of his hopes?", "잃었던 누군가를 다시 찾았다는 이 소식, 그토록 오래 떠나 있던 누군가가 돌아왔다는 이 일이, 그에게는 제 희망을 굳건히 뒷받침하는 증거로 다가오는 것일까? 그는 이렇게 생각하는 것일까."], ["Does he think, “Shall I not, with the aid I have, recall her safely after this, there being fewer hours in her case than there are years in his?”", "\"내게 주어진 도움으로, 나 또한 이번 일 뒤에 그녀를 무사히 데려올 수 있지 않을까. 그녀의 경우는 이 사내의 세월보다 짧은 시간밖에 흐르지 않았으니?\""]], [["It is of no use entreating him; he is determined to speak now, and he does.", "그를 만류해봐야 소용없다. 그는 지금 말하기로 작정했고, 그래서 말한다."], ["In a thick crowd of sounds, but still intelligibly enough to be understood.", "두텁게 뭉뚱그려진 소리 속에서도, 알아들을 만큼은 또렷이."]], [["“Why did you not tell me, Mrs. Rouncewell?”", "\"왜 내게 말하지 않았는가, 라운스웰 부인?\""]], [["“It happened only yesterday, Sir Leicester, and I doubted your being well enough to be talked to of such things.”", "\"바로 어제 일어난 일입니다, 레스터 경. 그리고 그런 이야기를 들으실 만큼 정정하실지 의심스러웠습니다.\""]], [["Besides, the giddy Volumnia now remembers with her little scream that nobody was to have known of his being Mrs.", "게다가 들뜬 볼럼니아가 이제 작게 비명을 지르며 떠올린다."], ["Rouncewell’s son and that she was not to have told. But Mrs.", "그가 라운스웰 부인의 아들이라는 사실을 아무도 알아선 안 되었고, 자기도 그걸 발설해선 안 되었다는 것을."], ["Rouncewell protests, with warmth enough to swell the stomacher, that of course she would have told Sir Leicester as soon as he got better.", "하지만 라운스웰 부인은 가슴받이가 부풀어 오를 만큼 열을 올려, 물론 레스터 경이 차도를 보이는 대로 곧장 말씀드릴 작정이었다고 항변한다."]], [["“Where is your son George, Mrs. Rouncewell?”", "\"그대의 아들 조지는 어디 있는가, 라운스웰 부인?\""], ["asks Sir Leicester,", "레스터 경이 묻는다."]], [["Mrs. Rouncewell, not a little alarmed by his disregard of the doctor’s injunctions, replies, in London.", "라운스웰 부인은 그가 의사의 당부를 아랑곳하지 않는 데 적잖이 놀라며, 런던에 있다고 대답한다."]], [["“Where in London?”", "\"런던 어디에?\""]], [["Mrs. Rouncewell is constrained to admit that he is in the house.", "라운스웰 부인은 그가 이 집 안에 있다고 마지못해 털어놓는다."]], [["“Bring him here to my room.", "\"그 사람을 이 방으로 데려오게."], ["Bring him directly.”", "당장 데려오게.\""]], [["The old lady can do nothing but go in search of him.", "늙은 부인은 아들을 찾으러 가는 수밖에 없다."], ["Sir Leicester, with such power of movement as he has, arranges himself a little to receive him.", "레스터 경은 자신에게 남은 만큼의 움직임을 짜내어, 그를 맞으려고 몸을 조금 추스른다."], ["When he has done so, he looks out again at the falling sleet and snow and listens again for the returning steps.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 떨어지는 진눈깨비와 눈을 내다보며, 돌아오는 발소리를 다시 귀 기울인다."], ["A quantity of straw has been tumbled down in the street to deaden the noises there, and she might be driven to the door perhaps without his hearing wheels.", "거리에는 소음을 죽이려고 짚을 잔뜩 깔아두었으니, 그녀가 바퀴 소리도 들리지 않은 채 문 앞에 당도할지도 모를 일이다."]], [["He is lying thus, apparently forgetful of his newer and minor surprise, when the housekeeper returns, accompanied by her trooper son.", "그가 이렇게, 좀 더 사소한 새 놀라움은 잊어버린 듯 누워 있을 때, 가정부가 기병인 아들과 함께 돌아온다."], ["Mr. George approaches softly to the bedside, makes his bow, squares his chest, and stands, with his face flushed, very heartily ashamed of himself.", "조지 씨는 살그머니 침대맡으로 다가가, 고개 숙여 인사하고, 가슴을 펴고 서는데, 얼굴이 벌게진 채 몹시도 진심으로 자신을 부끄러워한다."]], [["“Good heaven, and it is really George Rouncewell!”", "\"맙소사, 정말로 조지 라운스웰이로구나!\""], ["exclaims Sir Leicester.", "레스터 경이 외친다."], ["“Do you remember me, George?”", "\"나를 기억하는가, 조지?\""]], [["The trooper needs to look at him and to separate this sound from that sound before he knows what he has said, but doing this and being a little helped by his mother, he replies, “I must have a very bad memory, indeed, Sir Leicester, if I failed to remember you.”", "기병은 그를 한참 바라보고 이 소리와 저 소리를 갈라내고서야 그가 무슨 말을 했는지 알아채는데, 그러고 나서 어머니의 도움도 조금 받아 대답한다. \"제가 레스터 경을 기억하지 못한다면, 그건 정말이지 제 기억력이 형편없다는 뜻이겠지요.\""]], [["“When I look at you, George Rouncewell,” Sir Leicester observes with difficulty, “I see something of a boy at Chesney Wold—I remember well—very well.”", "\"그대를 보고 있자니, 조지 라운스웰,\" 레스터 경이 힘겹게 말한다. \"체스니 월드의 한 소년이 어렴풋이 떠오르네. 또렷이 기억하지. 아주 또렷이.\""]], [["He looks at the trooper until tears come into his eyes, and then he looks at the sleet and snow again.", "그는 눈에 눈물이 차오를 때까지 기병을 바라보다가, 다시 진눈깨비와 눈으로 눈길을 돌린다."]], [["“I ask your pardon, Sir Leicester,”", "\"외람되오나, 레스터 경,\""], ["says the trooper, “but would you accept of my arms to raise you up?", "기병이 말한다. \"제 팔로 일으켜 드려도 되겠습니까?"], ["You would lie easier, Sir Leicester, if you would allow me to move you.”", "제가 옮겨드리도록 허락해주시면 좀 더 편히 누우실 수 있을 겁니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“If you please, George Rouncewell; if you will be so good.”", "\"그래주게, 조지 라운스웰. 그리 친절을 베풀어준다면.\""]], [["The trooper takes him in his arms like a child, lightly raises him, and turns him with his face more towards the window.", "기병은 그를 어린아이처럼 두 팔로 안아 가뿐히 들어 올리고, 얼굴이 창 쪽으로 더 향하도록 돌려 눕힌다."], ["“Thank you.", "\"고맙네."], ["You have your mother’s gentleness,”", "그대는 어머니의 다정함을,\""], ["returns Sir Leicester, “and your own strength.", "레스터 경이 대꾸한다. \"그리고 제 몫의 힘을 지녔구먼."], ["Thank you.”", "고맙네.\""]], [["He signs to him with his hand not to go away.", "그는 손짓으로 가지 말라는 뜻을 전한다."], ["George quietly remains at the bedside, waiting to be spoken to.", "조지는 말이 떨어지기를 기다리며 조용히 침대맡에 머문다."]], [["“Why did you wish for secrecy?”", "\"왜 비밀로 하기를 바랐는가?\""], ["It takes Sir Leicester some time to ask this.", "레스터 경이 이 말을 묻기까지 시간이 좀 걸린다."]], [["“Truly I am not much to boast of, Sir Leicester, and I—I should still, Sir Leicester, if you was not so indisposed—which I hope you will not be long—I should still hope for the favour of being allowed to remain unknown in general.", "\"솔직히 저는 내세울 게 별로 없는 사람입니다, 레스터 경. 그리고 저는, 레스터 경, 혹여 이리 편찮으시지만 않다면, 부디 오래가지 않기를 바라옵니다만, 저는 그래도 전반적으로 무명인 채 남게 해주시는 호의를 바라고 싶습니다."], ["That involves explanations not very hard to be guessed at, not very well timed here, and not very creditable to myself.", "거기엔 짐작하기 그리 어렵지 않은 사연이 얽혀 있는데, 여기서 꺼내기에 그리 적절치도 않거니와, 제게 그리 떳떳한 일도 못 됩니다."], ["However opinions may differ on a variety of subjects, I should think it would be universally agreed, Sir Leicester, that I am not much to boast of.”", "여러 주제를 두고 의견이 제각각일지언정, 제가 내세울 게 별로 없는 사람이라는 데는 만장일치로 동의하시리라 봅니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“You have been a soldier,”", "\"그대는 군인이었지,\""], ["observes Sir Leicester, “and a faithful one.”", "레스터 경이 말한다. \"그것도 충실한 군인이었어.\""]], [["George makes his military bow.", "조지가 군인답게 인사한다."], ["“As far as that goes, Sir Leicester, I have done my duty under discipline, and it was the least I could do.”", "\"거기까지야, 레스터 경, 저는 규율 아래 제 본분을 다했을 뿐이고, 그건 제가 할 수 있는 최소한이었습니다.\""]], [["“You find me,”", "\"보다시피,\" 레스터 경이 말한다."], ["says Sir Leicester, whose eyes are much attracted towards him, “far from well, George Rouncewell.”", "그의 눈은 자꾸만 기병에게 이끌린다. \"내 형편이 영 좋지 못하네, 조지 라운스웰.\""]], [["“I am very sorry both to hear it and to see it, Sir Leicester.”", "\"그 말씀을 듣는 것도, 또 직접 뵙는 것도 몹시 안타깝습니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“I am sure you are.", "\"그럴 테지. 아닐세."], ["No. In addition to my older malady, I have had a sudden and bad attack.", "오래된 지병에 더해, 갑작스럽고 고약한 발작을 겪었네."], ["Something that deadens,”", "무언가 마비시키고,\""], ["making an endeavour to pass one hand down one side, “and confuses,”", "한 손으로 한쪽 옆구리를 쓸어내리려 애쓰며, \"또 헝클어뜨리는 것이.\""], ["touching his lips.", "제 입술을 짚으며."]], [["George, with a look of assent and sympathy, makes another bow.", "조지가 알겠다는, 또 동정 어린 표정으로 다시 고개 숙여 인사한다."], ["The different times when they were both young men (the trooper much the younger of the two) and looked at one another down at Chesney Wold arise before them both and soften both.", "둘 다 젊은이였던 시절(기병이 둘 중 한참 어렸다) 체스니 월드 아래쪽에서 서로를 바라보던 그 다른 시절들이 두 사람 앞에 떠올라, 둘 다의 마음을 누그러뜨린다."]], [["Sir Leicester, evidently with a great determination to say, in his own manner, something that is on his mind before relapsing into silence, tries to raise himself among his pillows a little more.", "레스터 경은 다시 침묵에 빠져들기 전에, 제 마음에 담긴 무언가를 제 방식대로 기어이 말하겠다는 굳센 결의로, 베개 사이에서 몸을 조금 더 일으키려 한다."], ["George, observant of the action, takes him in his arms again and places him as he desires to be.", "그 동작을 알아챈 조지가 다시 그를 두 팔로 안아 원하는 자세로 눕혀준다."], ["“Thank you, George.", "\"고맙네, 조지."], ["You are another self to me.", "그대는 내게 또 다른 나일세."], ["You have often carried my spare gun at Chesney Wold, George.", "그대는 체스니 월드에서 내 여분의 사냥총을 곧잘 들어주었지, 조지."], ["You are familiar to me in these strange circumstances, very familiar.”", "이 야릇한 처지에서 그대는 내게 낯익은 사람이야, 아주 낯익어.\""], ["He has put Sir Leicester’s sounder arm over his shoulder in lifting him up, and Sir Leicester is slow in drawing it away again as he says these words.", "그를 일으키며 레스터 경의 성한 팔을 제 어깨에 둘러주었던 터라, 그가 이 말을 하는 동안 레스터 경은 그 팔을 도로 거두는 데 더디다."]], [["“I was about to add,”", "\"내가 덧붙이려던 말은,\""], ["he presently goes on, “I was about to add, respecting this attack, that it was unfortunately simultaneous with a slight misunderstanding between my Lady and myself.", "그가 이내 이어간다. \"이 발작에 관해 덧붙이려던 말은, 공교롭게도 그것이 마님과 나 사이의 사소한 오해와 때를 같이했다는 거였네."], ["I do not mean that there was any difference between us (for there has been none), but that there was a misunderstanding of certain circumstances important only to ourselves, which deprives me, for a little while, of my Lady’s society.", "우리 사이에 어떤 불화가 있었다는 뜻이 아닐세(그런 건 전혀 없었어). 다만 우리 둘에게만 중요한 어떤 정황을 두고 오해가 있었고, 그 탓에 잠시 마님과 함께하지 못하게 되었다는 거지."], ["She has found it necessary to make a journey—I trust will shortly return.", "마님은 여행을 떠나야 할 사정이 생겼는데, 부디 곧 돌아오시리라 믿네."], ["Volumnia, do I make myself intelligible?", "볼럼니아, 내 말이 알아들을 만한가?"], ["The words are not quite under my command in the manner of pronouncing them.”", "발음하는 데 말이 통 내 뜻대로 듣질 않는구나.\""]], [["Volumnia understands him perfectly, and in truth he delivers himself with far greater plainness than could have been supposed possible a minute ago.", "볼럼니아는 그의 말을 완벽히 알아듣는데, 실은 그가 일 분 전만 해도 도저히 가능하다 싶지 않았을 만큼 훨씬 또렷하게 말을 풀어낸다."], ["The effort by which he does so is written in the anxious and labouring expression of his face.", "그렇게 해내는 데 든 안간힘이 그의 불안하고 애쓰는 표정에 고스란히 적혀 있다."], ["Nothing but the strength of his purpose enables him to make it.", "오로지 그 의지의 힘만이 그것을 해내게 한다."]], [["“Therefore, Volumnia, I desire to say in your presence—and in the presence of my old retainer and friend, Mrs.", "\"그러니, 볼럼니아, 나는 너 앞에서, 그리고 진실됨과 충직함을 누구도 의심할 수 없는 나의 오랜 가신이자 벗인 라운스웰 부인 앞에서, 또 체스니 월드의 조상 대대로 물려받은 집에서 내 젊은 시절의 낯익은 추억처럼 돌아온 그녀의 아들 조지 앞에서 말하고자 하네."], ["Rouncewell, whose truth and fidelity no one can question, and in the presence of her son George, who comes back like a familiar recollection of my youth in the home of my ancestors at Chesney Wold—in case I should relapse, in case I should not recover, in case I should lose both my speech and the power of writing, though I hope for better things—”", "혹여 내가 다시 쓰러지거든, 혹여 내가 회복하지 못하거든, 혹여 내가 말과 글 쓰는 힘을 둘 다 잃거든, 비록 나은 일을 바라고는 있다만.\""]], [["The old housekeeper weeping silently; Volumnia in the greatest agitation, with the freshest bloom on her cheeks; the trooper with his arms folded and his head a little bent, respectfully attentive.", "늙은 가정부는 소리 없이 울고, 볼럼니아는 더없이 동요한 채 뺨에 그 어느 때보다 생생한 홍조를 띠고, 기병은 팔짱을 끼고 고개를 조금 숙인 채 공손히 귀를 기울인다."]], [["“Therefore I desire to say, and to call you all to witness—beginning, Volumnia, with yourself, most solemnly—that I am on unaltered terms with Lady Dedlock.", "\"그러니 나는 말하고, 그대들 모두를 증인으로 부르고자 하네. 볼럼니아, 너부터 시작해, 더없이 엄숙하게."], ["That I assert no cause whatever of complaint against her.", "나는 데드록 마님과 변함없는 관계임을. 나는 그녀에게 그 어떤 불평거리도 내세우지 않음을."], ["That I have ever had the strongest affection for her, and that I retain it undiminished.", "나는 늘 그녀에게 더없이 강한 애정을 품어왔고, 그것을 조금도 줄지 않은 채 간직하고 있음을."], ["Say this to herself, and to every one.", "이 말을 그녀 본인에게, 또 모두에게 전하라."], ["If you ever say less than this, you will be guilty of deliberate falsehood to me.”", "만약 그대가 이보다 조금이라도 덜 말한다면, 그것은 나를 향한 고의적인 거짓을 범하는 죄가 될 것이다.\""]], [["Volumnia tremblingly protests that she will observe his injunctions to the letter.", "볼럼니아는 그의 당부를 글자 하나 어김없이 지키겠노라고 떨면서 다짐한다."]], [["“My Lady is too high in position, too handsome, too accomplished, too superior in most respects to the best of those by whom she is surrounded, not to have her enemies and traducers, I dare say.", "\"마님은 지위가 너무 높고, 너무 아름답고, 너무 교양 있고, 대개의 일에서 둘러싼 이들 가운데 가장 뛰어난 자들보다도 너무 우월하시니, 적과 비방하는 자가 없을 리 없겠지."], ["Let it be known to them, as I make it known to you, that being of sound mind, memory, and understanding, I revoke no disposition I have made in her favour.", "내가 그대들에게 알리듯, 그자들에게도 알려라. 나는 온전한 정신과 기억과 분별을 갖춘 채로, 그녀에게 베푼 어떤 처분도 철회하지 않음을."], ["I abridge nothing I have ever bestowed upon her.", "나는 그녀에게 내린 어떤 것도 줄이지 않음을."], ["I am on unaltered terms with her, and I recall—having the full power to do it if I were so disposed, as you see—no act I have done for her advantage and happiness.”", "나는 그녀와 변함없는 관계이며, 그러기로 마음먹었다면 그럴 충분한 권한이 있음에도 불구하고, 보다시피, 그녀의 이익과 행복을 위해 내가 한 어떤 행위도 거두지 않음을.\""]], [["His formal array of words might have at any other time, as it has often had, something ludicrous in it, but at this time it is serious and affecting.", "그의 격식 차린 말의 나열은 다른 때였다면, 흔히 그러했듯, 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 구석이 있었을지 모르나, 이때만은 진지하고 가슴을 울린다."], ["His noble earnestness, his fidelity, his gallant shielding of her, his generous conquest of his own wrong and his own pride for her sake, are simply honourable, manly, and true.", "그의 고결한 진심, 그의 충직함, 그녀를 향한 그의 의연한 비호, 그녀를 위해 제 억울함과 제 자존심을 너그러이 이겨낸 그 마음은 그저 명예롭고, 사내답고, 참되다."], ["Nothing less worthy can be seen through the lustre of such qualities in the commonest mechanic, nothing less worthy can be seen in the best-born gentleman.", "더없이 흔한 직공에게서든, 더없이 고귀하게 태어난 신사에게서든, 그런 자질의 광채를 뚫고 이보다 못한 것이라곤 찾아볼 수 없다."], ["In such a light both aspire alike, both rise alike, both children of the dust shine equally.", "그런 빛 속에서 둘은 똑같이 우러르고, 똑같이 일어서며, 똑같이 흙의 자식으로서 똑같이 빛난다."]], [["Overpowered by his exertions, he lays his head back on his pillows and closes his eyes for not more than a minute, when he again resumes his watching of the weather and his attention to the muffled sounds.", "안간힘에 짓눌린 그는 베개에 머리를 도로 누이고 일 분도 채 안 되게 눈을 감았다가, 다시 날씨를 지켜보며 먹먹한 소리에 귀 기울이기 시작한다."], ["In the rendering of those little services, and in the manner of their acceptance, the trooper has become installed as necessary to him.", "그 자잘한 시중을 들어주는 모습에서, 또 그것을 받아들이는 태도에서, 기병은 그에게 없어서는 안 될 사람으로 자리 잡았다."], ["Nothing has been said, but it is quite understood.", "아무 말도 오가지 않았으나, 충분히 이해되었다."], ["He falls a step or two backward to be out of sight and mounts guard a little behind his mother’s chair.", "그는 눈에 띄지 않게 한두 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 어머니 의자 조금 뒤쪽에서 보초를 선다."]], [["The day is now beginning to decline.", "이제 날이 저물기 시작한다."], ["The mist and the sleet into which the snow has all resolved itself are darker, and the blaze begins to tell more vividly upon the room walls and furniture.", "눈이 죄다 녹아내려 변한 안개와 진눈깨비가 더 어두워지고, 불길은 방 벽과 가구에 더 또렷이 어른거리기 시작한다. 어스름이 짙어진다."], ["The gloom augments; the bright gas springs up in the streets; and the pertinacious oil lamps which yet hold their ground there, with their source of life half frozen and half thawed, twinkle gaspingly like fiery fish out of water—as they are.", "거리에는 밝은 가스등이 솟아오르고, 아직 그 자리를 지키는 끈질긴 기름 등잔들은, 그 생명의 근원이 절반은 얼고 절반은 녹은 채로, 물 밖에 나온 불붙은 물고기처럼 가쁘게 반짝인다."], ["The world, which has been rumbling over the straw and pulling at the bell, “to inquire,”", "실제로 그러하니까. 짚 위로 덜컹거리며 굴러와 종을 잡아당기고 \"안부를 묻던\""], ["begins to go home, begins to dress, to dine, to discuss its dear friend with all the last new modes, as already mentioned.", "세상은 이제 집으로 돌아가, 옷을 차려입고, 만찬을 들고, 앞서 말했듯 온갖 최신 유행을 곁들여 제 다정한 벗을 입에 올리기 시작한다."]], [["Now does Sir Leicester become worse, restless, uneasy, and in great pain.", "이제 레스터 경은 더 나빠져, 안절부절못하고, 불안해하며, 심한 고통에 시달린다."], ["Volumnia, lighting a candle (with a predestined aptitude for doing something objectionable), is bidden to put it out again, for it is not yet dark enough.", "볼럼니아가 (꼭 거슬리는 짓을 하도록 점지받은 사람답게) 초에 불을 붙이자, 도로 꺼두라는 명을 받는데, 아직 그만큼 어둡지 않아서다."], ["Yet it is very dark too, as dark as it will be all night.", "그렇지만 사실 무척 어둡기도 하니, 밤새 어두울 만큼 어둡다."], ["By and by she tries again. No!", "이윽고 그녀가 다시 시도한다."], ["Put it out.", "안 돼! 꺼두어라."], ["It is not dark enough yet.", "아직 그만큼 어둡지 않다."]], [["His old housekeeper is the first to understand that he is striving to uphold the fiction with himself that it is not growing late.", "날이 늦어가고 있음을 인정하지 않으려고 그가 스스로 그런 허구를 붙들고 버틴다는 것을, 가장 먼저 알아챈 것은 늙은 가정부다."]], [["“Dear Sir Leicester, my honoured master,”", "\"존경하는 주인님, 레스터 경,\" 그녀가 나직이 속삭인다."], ["she softly whispers, “I must, for your own good, and my duty, take the freedom of begging and praying that you will not lie here in the lone darkness watching and waiting and dragging through the time.", "\"주인님을 위해서, 또 제 본분으로서, 외람되이 빌고 또 비옵니다. 이 외로운 어둠 속에 누워 지켜보고 기다리며 시간을 질질 끌지 마옵소서."], ["Let me draw the curtains, and light the candles, and make things more comfortable about you.", "커튼을 치고, 초에 불을 밝히고, 주변을 좀 더 편안하게 해드리도록 해주십시오."], ["The church-clocks will strike the hours just the same, Sir Leicester, and the night will pass away just the same.", "교회 시계는 똑같이 시각을 알릴 것이고, 레스터 경, 밤도 똑같이 지나갈 것입니다."], ["My Lady will come back, just the same.”", "마님도 똑같이 돌아오실 겁니다.\""]], [["“I know it, Mrs.", "\"나도 안다, 라운스웰 부인."], ["Rouncewell, but I am weak—and she has been so long gone.”", "하지만 내가 약해진 데다, 마님이 떠난 지 너무 오래되었어.\""]], [["“Not so very long, Sir Leicester.", "\"그리 오래된 건 아닙니다, 레스터 경."], ["Not twenty-four hours yet.”", "아직 스물네 시간도 안 되었습니다.\""]], [["“But that is a long time.", "\"하지만 그건 긴 시간이야."], ["Oh, it is a long time!”", "아, 긴 시간이지!\""]], [["He says it with a groan that wrings her heart.", "그가 그녀의 가슴을 후벼파는 신음과 함께 그 말을 한다."]], [["She knows that this is not a period for bringing the rough light upon him; she thinks his tears too sacred to be seen, even by her.", "그녀는 지금이 그에게 거친 빛을 비출 때가 아님을 안다. 그의 눈물이 너무 거룩해 자기조차도 보아선 안 된다고 여긴다."], ["Therefore she sits in the darkness for a while without a word, then gently begins to move about, now stirring the fire, now standing at the dark window looking out.", "그래서 그녀는 한동안 말없이 어둠 속에 앉아 있다가, 가만히 움직이기 시작해, 불을 들쑤시기도 하고, 어두운 창가에 서서 바깥을 내다보기도 한다."], ["Finally he tells her, with recovered self-command, “As you say, Mrs.", "마침내 그가 추슬러진 자제력으로 그녀에게 말한다. \"그대 말이 맞네, 라운스웰 부인."], ["Rouncewell, it is no worse for being confessed.", "인정한다고 더 나빠질 것도 없지."], ["It is getting late, and they are not come.", "날이 늦어가는데, 그들은 오지 않았어."], ["Light the room!”", "방에 불을 밝히게!\""], ["When it is lighted and the weather shut out, it is only left to him to listen.", "방에 불이 밝혀지고 날씨가 가려지자, 그에게 남은 일이라곤 귀 기울이는 것뿐이다."]], [["But they find that however dejected and ill he is, he brightens when a quiet pretence is made of looking at the fires in her rooms and being sure that everything is ready to receive her.", "하지만 사람들은 알아챈다. 그가 아무리 풀이 죽고 병들었어도, 그녀의 방에 켜둔 불을 들여다보며 그녀를 맞을 채비가 다 되었는지 확인하는 조용한 시늉을 해 보이면 그가 환해진다는 것을."], ["Poor pretence as it is, these allusions to her being expected keep up hope within him.", "가련한 시늉일망정, 그녀가 돌아오시기로 되어 있다는 이런 언급은 그의 마음속에 희망을 지펴둔다."]], [["Midnight comes, and with it the same blank.", "자정이 오고, 그와 함께 똑같은 공허가 온다."], ["The carriages in the streets are few, and other late sounds in that neighbourhood there are none, unless a man so very nomadically drunk as to stray into the frigid zone goes brawling and bellowing along the pavement.", "거리에 마차는 드물고, 그 동네에 늦은 다른 소리라곤 없다. 어쩌다 너무도 정처 없이 취해 그 혹한 지대로 흘러든 사내가 인도를 따라 악다구니를 쓰고 고래고래 소리치는 것 말고는."], ["Upon this wintry night it is so still that listening to the intense silence is like looking at intense darkness.", "이 겨울밤은 어찌나 고요한지, 그 짙은 침묵에 귀 기울이는 것이 마치 짙은 어둠을 바라보는 것만 같다."], ["If any distant sound be audible in this case, it departs through the gloom like a feeble light in that, and all is heavier than before.", "혹여 이런 침묵 속에 먼 소리 하나가 들려온다면, 그것은 저 어둠 속의 희미한 빛처럼 어스름을 가르며 사라지고, 모든 것이 전보다 더 무거워진다."]], [["The corporation of servants are dismissed to bed (not unwilling to go, for they were up all last night), and only Mrs. Rouncewell and George keep watch in Sir Leicester’s room.", "하인들 무리는 잠자리로 물러가고(어젯밤 내내 깨어 있었으니 마다하지 않는다), 오직 라운스웰 부인과 조지만이 레스터 경의 방을 지킨다."], ["As the night lags tardily on—or rather when it seems to stop altogether, at between two and three o’clock—they find a restless craving on him to know more about the weather, now he cannot see it.", "밤이 더디게 흘러가며, 아니 오히려 두 시와 세 시 사이에 아예 멈춰 선 듯할 무렵, 두 사람은 그가 이제 볼 수 없는 날씨에 대해 더 알고 싶어 안달하는 것을 알아챈다."], ["Hence George, patrolling regularly every half-hour to the rooms so carefully looked after, extends his march to the hall-door, looks about him, and brings back the best report he can make of the worst of nights, the sleet still falling and even the stone footways lying ankle-deep in icy sludge.", "그래서 조지는 그토록 정성껏 보살펴둔 방들을 반 시간마다 어김없이 순찰하던 길에, 발걸음을 현관문까지 넓혀, 주위를 둘러보고, 더없이 고약한 밤에 관해 그가 전할 수 있는 가장 나은 보고를 들고 돌아온다. 진눈깨비는 여전히 떨어지고, 심지어 돌로 깐 보도마저 얼음 진창에 발목까지 잠겨 있다고."]], [["Volumnia, in her room up a retired landing on the staircase—the second turning past the end of the carving and gilding, a cousinly room containing a fearful abortion of a portrait of Sir Leicester banished for its crimes, and commanding in the day a solemn yard planted with dried-up shrubs like antediluvian specimens of black tea—is a prey to horrors of many kinds.", "볼럼니아는 계단의 외진 층계참 위쪽 자기 방에 있는데, 조각 장식과 금박 입힌 부분의 끝을 지나 두 번째 모퉁이를 돈 곳이다. 친척용 방인 이곳에는 죄를 지어 추방된 레스터 경의 끔찍하게 망쳐진 초상화 한 점이 걸려 있고, 낮에는 태곳적 표본 같은 시커먼 찻잎처럼 바싹 마른 관목들이 심긴 엄숙한 안뜰이 내려다보인다."], ["Not last nor least among them, possibly, is a horror of what may befall her little income in the event, as she expresses it, “of anything happening”", "거기서 그녀는 온갖 두려움의 먹잇감이 된다. 그 가운데서도 마지막이 아니요 결코 작지 않은 것은, 어쩌면 그녀의 표현대로 레스터 경에게 \"무슨 일이 생길\""], ["to Sir Leicester.", "경우 자기 쥐꼬리만 한 수입이 어찌 될까 하는 두려움이다."], ["Anything, in this sense, meaning one thing only; and that the last thing that can happen to the consciousness of any baronet in the known world.", "이런 맥락에서 무슨 일이란 오직 한 가지만을 뜻하니, 곧 알려진 세상의 어느 준남작에게든 그의 의식에 일어날 수 있는 마지막 일이다."]], [["An effect of these horrors is that Volumnia finds she cannot go to bed in her own room or sit by the fire in her own room, but must come forth with her fair head tied up in a profusion of shawl, and her fair form enrobed in drapery, and parade the mansion like a ghost, particularly haunting the rooms, warm and luxurious, prepared for one who still does not return.", "이 두려움이 빚어낸 결과로, 볼럼니아는 자기 방에서 도무지 잠자리에 들 수도 없고 자기 방 난롯가에 앉아 있을 수도 없음을 깨닫는다. 그래서 어여쁜 머리를 숄로 잔뜩 동여매고 어여쁜 몸을 천으로 휘감고는, 유령처럼 저택을 떠돌아야만 하는데, 특히 따뜻하고 호사스럽게 꾸며진, 여태 돌아오지 않는 이를 위한 방들을 즐겨 맴돈다."], ["Solitude under such circumstances being not to be thought of, Volumnia is attended by her maid, who, impressed from her own bed for that purpose, extremely cold, very sleepy, and generally an injured maid as condemned by circumstances to take office with a cousin, when she had resolved to be maid to nothing less than ten thousand a year, has not a sweet expression of countenance.", "이런 처지에 혼자 있는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없는지라, 볼럼니아는 하녀를 거느리는데, 이 하녀는 그 일을 위해 제 잠자리에서 끌려나온 데다, 몹시 춥고, 무척 졸리고, 게다가 본디 연봉 만 파운드는 족히 되는 사람의 하녀가 되겠다고 마음먹었건만 처지에 떠밀려 친척 신세인 분의 시중을 들게 된 박복한 하녀인지라, 얼굴빛이 그리 곱지 못하다."]], [["The periodical visits of the trooper to these rooms, however, in the course of his patrolling is an assurance of protection and company both to mistress and maid, which renders them very acceptable in the small hours of the night.", "그러나 기병이 순찰하는 길에 이 방들을 주기적으로 들르는 것은 마님에게도 하녀에게도 보호와 동행을 보장하는 일이라, 깊은 밤 시각에는 무척 반가운 일이 된다."], ["Whenever he is heard advancing, they both make some little decorative preparation to receive him; at other times they divide their watches into short scraps of oblivion and dialogues not wholly free from acerbity, as to whether Miss Dedlock, sitting with her feet upon the fender, was or was not falling into the fire when rescued (to her great displeasure) by her guardian genius the maid.", "그가 다가오는 소리가 들릴 때마다 둘은 그를 맞으려고 조금씩 매무새를 꾸미는 채비를 하고, 그 밖의 시간에는 짤막한 망각의 토막과, 아주 가시 없지는 않은 대화로 보초 시간을 나눈다. 발받침에 두 발을 올리고 앉았던 데드록 아가씨가, 수호신인 하녀에게 (그녀로서는 몹시 못마땅하게도) 구해지던 순간 불 속으로 빠지려던 참이었는지 아닌지를 두고서."]], [["“How is Sir Leicester now, Mr. George?”", "\"레스터 경은 지금 좀 어떠신가요, 조지 씨?\""], ["inquires Volumnia, adjusting her cowl over her head.", "볼럼니아가 머리에 두건을 매만지며 묻는다."]], [["“Why, Sir Leicester is much the same, miss.", "\"글쎄요, 레스터 경은 별 차도가 없으십니다, 아가씨."], ["He is very low and ill, and he even wanders a little sometimes.”", "무척 기운이 없고 편찮으시고, 이따금 정신이 조금 오락가락하시기도 합니다.\""]], [["“Has he asked for me?”", "\"나를 찾으셨나요?\""], ["inquires Volumnia tenderly.", "볼럼니아가 다정하게 묻는다."]], [["“Why, no, I can’t say he has, miss.", "\"글쎄요, 아니요, 그러셨다고는 못 하겠습니다, 아가씨."], ["Not within my hearing, that is to say.”", "그러니까 제가 듣기로는 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“This is a truly sad time, Mr. George.”", "\"참으로 슬픈 때로군요, 조지 씨.\""]], [["“It is indeed, miss.", "\"정말 그렇습니다, 아가씨."], ["Hadn’t you better go to bed?”", "이제 그만 주무시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“You had a deal better go to bed, Miss Dedlock,”", "\"잠자리에 드시는 게 훨씬 나을 텐데요, 데드록 아가씨.\""], ["quoth the maid sharply.", "하녀가 톡 쏘아붙인다."]], [["But Volumnia answers No! No!", "하지만 볼럼니아는 싫다고, 싫다고 대답한다."], ["She may be asked for, she may be wanted at a moment’s notice.", "그녀를 찾을지도 모르고, 언제든 당장 필요로 할지도 모른다는 것이다. \"무슨 일이 생기기라도\""], ["She never should forgive herself “if anything was to happen” and she was not on the spot.", "했는데 자기가 그 자리에 없다면 결코 자신을 용서하지 못하리라는 것이다."], ["She declines to enter on the question, mooted by the maid, how the spot comes to be there, and not in her room (which is nearer to Sir Leicester’s), but staunchly declares that on the spot she will remain.", "그러면서 하녀가 들고나온 물음, 곧 어째서 그 자리가 (레스터 경 방에 더 가까운) 자기 방이 아니라 거기인가 하는 물음에는 답하기를 사양하고, 다만 그 자리에 남겠노라고 꿋꿋이 선언한다."], ["Volumnia further makes a merit of not having “closed an eye”—as if she had twenty or thirty—though it is hard to reconcile this statement with her having most indisputably opened two within five minutes.", "볼럼니아는 한술 더 떠 \"한숨도 못 잤음\"을 마치 무슨 큰 공처럼 내세우는데, 마치 스무 번이나 서른 번은 잘 수 있었던 양 군다. 하지만 오 분 안에 두 눈을 분명코 떴음을 따져보면, 이 주장과 좀처럼 아귀가 맞지 않는다."]], [["But when it comes to four o’clock, and still the same blank, Volumnia’s constancy begins to fail her, or rather it begins to strengthen, for she now considers that it is her duty to be ready for the morrow, when much may be expected of her, that, in fact, howsoever anxious to remain upon the spot, it may be required of her, as an act of self-devotion, to desert the spot.", "그러나 네 시가 되도록 여전히 똑같은 공허가 이어지자, 볼럼니아의 한결같음이 흔들리기 시작한다. 아니 오히려 굳건해지기 시작한다."], ["So when the trooper reappears with his, “Hadn’t you better go to bed, miss?”", "이제 그녀는 많은 일이 그녀에게 기대될 내일을 위해 채비하는 것이 제 본분이라 여기기 때문이다. 실은 아무리 그 자리에 남고 싶을지언정, 자기희생의 행위로서 그 자리를 떠나는 것이 요구될지도 모른다고."], ["and when the maid protests, more sharply than before, “You had a deal better go to bed, Miss Dedlock!”", "그래서 기병이 다시 나타나 \"이제 그만 주무시는 게 낫지 않겠습니까, 아가씨?\" 하고, 하녀가 전보다 더 톡 쏘아붙이며 \"잠자리에 드시는 게 훨씬 나을 텐데요, 데드록 아가씨!\""], ["she meekly rises and says, “Do with me what you think best!”", "하고 항변하자, 그녀는 얌전히 일어서며 말한다. \"두 사람이 가장 좋다고 여기는 대로 나를 하구려!\""]], [["Mr. George undoubtedly thinks it best to escort her on his arm to the door of her cousinly chamber, and the maid as undoubtedly thinks it best to hustle her into bed with mighty little ceremony.", "조지 씨는 그녀를 제 팔에 의지하게 해 그녀의 친척용 방문까지 데려다주는 것이 가장 좋다고 의심 없이 여기고, 하녀는 그녀를 거의 격식 없이 침대로 떠밀어 넣는 것이 가장 좋다고 의심 없이 여긴다."], ["Accordingly, these steps are taken; and now the trooper, in his rounds, has the house to himself.", "그리하여 이 절차가 행해지고, 이제 기병은 순찰 도는 동안 저택을 홀로 차지한다."]], [["There is no improvement in the weather.", "날씨는 조금도 나아지지 않았다."], ["From the portico, from the eaves, from the parapet, from every ledge and post and pillar, drips the thawed snow.", "현관 차양에서, 처마에서, 난간에서, 모든 턱과 기둥과 지주에서, 녹은 눈이 뚝뚝 떨어진다."], ["It has crept, as if for shelter, into the lintels of the great door—under it, into the corners of the windows, into every chink and crevice of retreat, and there wastes and dies.", "그것은 마치 피난처를 찾듯 커다란 문의 상인방 안으로, 그 밑으로, 창문 구석으로, 물러설 만한 온갖 틈과 갈라진 자리로 기어들어, 거기서 스러지고 죽는다."], ["It is falling still; upon the roof, upon the skylight, even through the skylight, and drip, drip, drip, with the regularity of the Ghost’s Walk, on the stone floor below.", "눈은 여전히 떨어지고 있다. 지붕 위로, 천창 위로, 심지어 천창을 뚫고, 유령의 길에 울리는 그 규칙적인 발소리처럼 뚝, 뚝, 뚝, 아래쪽 돌바닥 위로."]], [["The trooper, his old recollections awakened by the solitary grandeur of a great house—no novelty to him once at Chesney Wold—goes up the stairs and through the chief rooms, holding up his light at arm’s length. Thinking of his varied fortunes within the last few weeks, and of his rustic boyhood, and of the two periods of his life so strangely brought together across the wide intermediate space; thinking of the murdered man whose image is fresh in his mind; thinking of the lady who has disappeared from these very rooms and the tokens of whose recent presence are all here; thinking of the master of the house upstairs and of the foreboding, “Who will tell him!” he looks here and looks there, and reflects how he MIGHT see something now, which it would tax his boldness to walk up to, lay his hand upon, and prove to be a fancy. But it is all blank, blank as the darkness above and below, while he goes up the great staircase again, blank as the oppressive silence.", "기병은 큰 저택의 외로운 위용에 옛 기억이 일깨워져, 한때 체스니 월드에서 익히 보았던 터라 그에게는 새로울 것도 없건만, 등불을 팔 길이만큼 치켜들고 계단을 올라 주요 방들을 지나간다. 지난 몇 주 사이 겪은 갖가지 운명을 떠올리고, 시골에서 보낸 제 소년 시절을 떠올리며, 그 사이의 너른 세월을 가로질러 그토록 야릇하게 한데 묶인 제 삶의 두 시절을 떠올리며. 그 기억이 마음에 생생한 살해당한 사내를 떠올리며. 바로 이 방들에서 사라진, 그러나 최근의 자취가 죄다 여기 남은 그 마님을 떠올리며. 위층에 있는 이 집의 주인을, 그리고 \"누가 그분께 말씀드리겠니!\" 하던 그 불길한 예감을 떠올리며. 그는 여기를 보고 저기를 보며 곰곰 생각한다. 지금이라도 무언가가 보일지 모른다고, 다가가서 손을 얹고 그것이 헛것임을 입증하기에는 제 담력이 시험에 들 그런 무언가가. 하지만 위에도 아래에도 온통 공허뿐, 어둠처럼 공허할 뿐이다. 그가 다시 큰 계단을 올라가는 동안, 짓누르는 침묵처럼 공허할 뿐이다."]], [["“All is still in readiness, George Rouncewell?”", "\"모두 빈틈없이 채비되었는가, 조지 라운스웰?\""]], [["“Quite orderly and right, Sir Leicester.”", "\"아주 가지런하고 빈틈없습니다, 레스터 경.\""]], [["“No word of any kind?”", "\"무슨 기별도 없고?\""]], [["The trooper shakes his head.", "기병이 고개를 가로젓는다."]], [["“No letter that can possibly have been overlooked?”", "\"행여 빠뜨렸을 만한 편지도 없는가?\""]], [["But he knows there is no such hope as that and lays his head down without looking for an answer.", "하지만 그는 그런 희망이란 없음을 알기에, 대답을 기다리지도 않고 머리를 도로 누인다."]], [["Very familiar to him, as he said himself some hours ago, George Rouncewell lifts him into easier positions through the long remainder of the blank wintry night, and equally familiar with his unexpressed wish, extinguishes the light and undraws the curtains at the first late break of day.", "몇 시간 전 그 자신이 말했듯 그에게 무척 낯익은 조지 라운스웰은, 공허한 겨울밤이 길게 남은 내내 그를 더 편한 자세로 들어 올려주고, 그의 입 밖에 내지 않은 바람에도 똑같이 익숙하여, 늦게야 동이 처음 트자 등불을 끄고 커튼을 걷는다."], ["The day comes like a phantom.", "날은 유령처럼 다가온다."], ["Cold, colourless, and vague, it sends a warning streak before it of a deathlike hue, as if it cried out, “Look what I am bringing you who watch there!", "차갑고, 빛깔 없고, 어렴풋한 그 날은, 죽음 같은 빛깔의 경고 줄무늬를 앞세워 보내니, 마치 이렇게 외치는 듯하다. \"거기서 지켜보는 그대여, 내가 무엇을 가져오는지 보라!"], ["Who will tell him!”", "누가 그분께 말씀드리겠는가!\""]], [["Esther’s Narrative\nIt was three o’clock in the morning when the houses outside London did at last begin to exclude the country and to close us in with streets.", "런던 외곽의 집들이 마침내 시골을 밀어내고 우리를 거리로 에워싸기 시작한 것은 새벽 세 시였습니다. 우리는 낮에 지나왔을 때보다 훨씬 험한 길을 헤치며 나아갔습니다."], ["We had made our way along roads in a far worse condition than when we had traversed them by daylight, both the fall and the thaw having lasted ever since; but the energy of my companion never slackened.", "그날 이후로 눈도 계속 내리고 해빙도 줄곧 이어진 탓이었지요. 하지만 내 동행의 기력은 조금도 수그러들지 않았습니다."], ["It had only been, as I thought, of less assistance than the horses in getting us on, and it had often aided them.", "내 생각에 그 기력이 우리를 앞으로 나아가게 하는 데에 말들보다 보탬이 덜 됐던 것은 그저 잠깐이었고, 오히려 말들을 도와준 적이 더 많았습니다. 말들은 비탈 중턱에서 기진해 멈춰 섰고, 거센 물줄기를 헤치며 몰렸으며, 미끄러져 마구에 뒤엉키기도 했지요."], ["They had stopped exhausted half-way up hills, they had been driven through streams of turbulent water, they had slipped down and become entangled with the harness; but he and his little lantern had been always ready, and when the mishap was set right, I had never heard any variation in his cool, “Get on, my lads!”", "하지만 그와 그의 작은 등불은 언제나 채비를 갖추고 있었고, 사고가 수습되고 나면 나는 그의 침착한 \"가자, 이놈들아!\" 소리에서 한 번도 흐트러짐을 들은 적이 없었습니다."]], [["The steadiness and confidence with which he had directed our journey back I could not account for.", "그가 돌아오는 길을 이끈 그 한결같음과 확신을 나로서는 도무지 설명할 수가 없었습니다."], ["Never wavering, he never even stopped to make an inquiry until we were within a few miles of London.", "그는 한 번도 흔들리지 않았고, 우리가 런던에서 몇 마일 안쪽에 들어설 때까지 묻는답시고 멈추는 일조차 없었습니다."], ["A very few words, here and there, were then enough for him; and thus we came, at between three and four o’clock in the morning, into Islington.", "그러고 나서는 여기저기서 짤막한 몇 마디면 충분했지요. 그렇게 해서 우리는 새벽 세 시에서 네 시 사이에 이즐링턴으로 들어섰습니다."]], [["I will not dwell on the suspense and anxiety with which I reflected all this time that we were leaving my mother farther and farther behind every minute.", "그동안 내내 우리가 매분 매분 어머니를 점점 더 뒤로 멀리 떼어놓고 있다는 생각에 짓눌리던 그 초조와 불안을 여기서 길게 늘어놓지는 않겠습니다."], ["I think I had some strong hope that he must be right and could not fail to have a satisfactory object in following this woman, but I tormented myself with questioning it and discussing it during the whole journey.", "내 생각엔, 그가 옳을 수밖에 없고 이 여인을 뒤쫓는 데에 분명 흡족한 목적이 있을 거라는 강한 희망을 어느 정도 품고 있었던 것 같습니다. 그러면서도 나는 그것을 의심하고 따져 묻느라 가는 길 내내 스스로를 들볶았지요."], ["What was to ensue when we found her and what could compensate us for this loss of time were questions also that I could not possibly dismiss; my mind was quite tortured by long dwelling on such reflections when we stopped.", "그 여인을 찾아내면 무슨 일이 벌어질까, 이렇게 잃어버린 시간을 무엇으로 메울 수 있을까 하는 물음 또한 도저히 떨쳐낼 수 없었습니다. 우리가 멈춰 섰을 때 내 마음은 그런 생각들에 오래 매달린 탓에 완전히 시달려 있었습니다."]], [["We stopped in a high-street where there was a coach-stand.", "마차 정거장이 있는 어느 큰길에서 우리는 멈췄습니다."], ["My companion paid our two drivers, who were as completely covered with splashes as if they had been dragged along the roads like the carriage itself, and giving them some brief direction where to take it, lifted me out of it and into a hackney-coach he had chosen from the rest.", "내 동행은 두 마부에게 삯을 치렀는데, 두 사람은 마치 마차처럼 길바닥에 끌려다니기라도 한 양 온몸이 흙탕물 범벅이었지요. 그는 마차를 어디로 가져다 놓으라고 짤막하게 일러준 뒤 나를 그 안에서 들어 올려, 나머지 가운데서 자기가 골라둔 삯마차로 옮겨 태웠습니다."]], [["“Why, my dear!”", "\"아니, 아가씨!\""], ["he said as he did this.", "그가 그러면서 말했습니다."], ["“How wet you are!”", "\"온통 젖었군요!\""]], [["I had not been conscious of it.", "나는 그걸 의식하지 못하고 있었습니다."], ["But the melted snow had found its way into the carriage, and I had got out two or three times when a fallen horse was plunging and had to be got up, and the wet had penetrated my dress.", "하지만 녹은 눈이 마차 안으로 스며들었고, 게다가 말이 쓰러져 허우적대는 통에 일으켜 세워야 했던 두세 차례 내가 마차에서 내렸던 터라, 물기가 내 옷에 배어들었던 겁니다."], ["I assured him it was no matter, but the driver, who knew him, would not be dissuaded by me from running down the street to his stable, whence he brought an armful of clean dry straw.", "나는 그에게 아무렇지도 않다고 했지만, 그를 아는 마부는 내 만류에도 아랑곳없이 거리를 달려 자기 마구간으로 가더니 거기서 깨끗하고 마른 짚을 한 아름 가져왔습니다."], ["They shook it out and strewed it well about me, and I found it warm and comfortable.", "두 사람은 그것을 털어 내 둘레에 잘 깔아주었고, 나는 그게 따뜻하고 아늑하다는 걸 알게 되었지요."]], [["“Now, my dear,”", "\"자, 아가씨.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket, with his head in at the window after I was shut up.", "버킷 씨가 나를 안에 태우고 문을 닫은 뒤 창문으로 머리를 들이밀며 말했습니다."], ["“We’re a-going to mark this person down.", "\"우리는 이제 이 사람을 잡으러 갑니다."], ["It may take a little time, but you don’t mind that.", "시간이 좀 걸릴지도 모르지만, 아가씨는 그쯤은 개의치 않을 거고요."], ["You’re pretty sure that I’ve got a motive.", "내게 다 생각이 있다는 걸 꽤 확실히 믿고 있죠."], ["Ain’t you?”", "안 그래요?\""]], [["I little thought what it was, little thought in how short a time I should understand it better, but I assured him that I had confidence in him.", "그게 무엇인지 나는 거의 짐작도 못 했고, 얼마나 짧은 시간 안에 그걸 더 잘 알게 될지도 거의 몰랐지만, 나는 그를 믿는다고 그에게 다짐했습니다."]], [["“So you may have, my dear,”", "\"믿어도 좋아요, 아가씨.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“And I tell you what!", "\"그리고 내 말해 두는데!"], ["If you only repose half as much confidence in me as I repose in you after what I’ve experienced of you, that’ll do.", "내가 아가씨를 겪어보고 아가씨한테 두는 만큼의 절반만 아가씨가 나한테 믿음을 두어도, 그걸로 충분합니다. 세상에!"], ["Lord! You’re no trouble at all.", "아가씨는 손톱만큼도 폐가 안 돼요."], ["I never see a young woman in any station of society—and I’ve seen many elevated ones too—conduct herself like you have conducted yourself since you was called out of your bed.", "나는 어느 신분의 젊은 여자든—높으신 분들도 숱하게 봤지만—잠자리에서 불려 나온 뒤로 아가씨처럼 처신하는 사람을 본 적이 없습니다."], ["You’re a pattern, you know, that’s what you are,” said Mr.", "본보기예요, 알아요, 아가씨가 바로 그래요.\""], ["Bucket warmly; “you’re a pattern.”", "버킷 씨가 따뜻하게 말했습니다. \"본보기라니까요.\""]], [["I told him I was very glad, as indeed I was, to have been no hindrance to him, and that I hoped I should be none now.", "나는 그에게 폐가 안 되었다니 정말 기쁘다고, 사실 정말로 기뻤으니까요, 그리고 앞으로도 그러지 않기를 바란다고 말했습니다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"아가씨.\""], ["he returned, “when a young lady is as mild as she’s game, and as game as she’s mild, that’s all I ask, and more than I expect.", "그가 대답했습니다. \"젊은 숙녀가 담대한 만큼 온순하고, 온순한 만큼 담대하다면, 그게 내가 바라는 전부고 기대 이상이지요."], ["She then becomes a queen, and that’s about what you are yourself.”", "그럼 그 사람은 여왕이 되는 겁니다. 아가씨가 바로 그래요.\""]], [["With these encouraging words—they really were encouraging to me under those lonely and anxious circumstances—he got upon the box, and we once more drove away.", "이런 격려의 말과 함께—그 외롭고 불안한 처지에서 그 말은 정말로 내게 격려가 되었습니다—그는 마부석에 올랐고, 우리는 다시 한번 달려 나갔습니다."], ["Where we drove I neither knew then nor have ever known since, but we appeared to seek out the narrowest and worst streets in London.", "어디로 달렸는지는 그때도 몰랐고 그 뒤로도 끝내 알지 못했지만, 우리는 런던에서 가장 좁고 가장 후미진 거리들만 골라 찾아드는 듯했습니다."], ["Whenever I saw him directing the driver, I was prepared for our descending into a deeper complication of such streets, and we never failed to do so.", "그가 마부에게 길을 일러주는 걸 볼 때마다 나는 그런 거리의 더 깊은 미궁 속으로 내려가리라 짐작했고, 그건 어김없이 들어맞았지요."]], [["Sometimes we emerged upon a wider thoroughfare or came to a larger building than the generality, well lighted.", "이따금 우리는 좀 더 너른 큰길로 빠져나오거나, 여느 건물보다 큼직하고 환히 불을 밝힌 건물에 이르렀습니다."], ["Then we stopped at offices like those we had visited when we began our journey, and I saw him in consultation with others.", "그러면 우리는 처음 길을 나설 때 들렀던 것 같은 사무소들 앞에 멈췄고, 그가 다른 이들과 의논하는 모습을 보았지요."], ["Sometimes he would get down by an archway or at a street corner and mysteriously show the light of his little lantern.", "더러는 그가 아치 통로 옆이나 길모퉁이에서 내려 작은 등불을 슬쩍 비추어 보였습니다."], ["This would attract similar lights from various dark quarters, like so many insects, and a fresh consultation would be held.", "그러면 그 불빛이 마치 무수한 벌레들처럼 여기저기 어두운 구석에서 비슷한 불빛들을 불러냈고, 새로운 의논이 벌어졌습니다."], ["By degrees we appeared to contract our search within narrower and easier limits.", "차츰 우리는 수색의 범위를 더 좁고 손쉬운 한계 안으로 좁혀가는 듯했습니다."], ["Single police-officers on duty could now tell Mr. Bucket what he wanted to know and point to him where to go.", "이제는 근무 중인 순경 한 사람 한 사람이 버킷 씨가 알고 싶어 하는 걸 일러주고 어디로 가야 할지 가리켜줄 수 있었지요."], ["At last we stopped for a rather long conversation between him and one of these men, which I supposed to be satisfactory from his manner of nodding from time to time.", "마침내 우리는 그가 그런 사람 가운데 하나와 꽤 긴 이야기를 나누느라 멈춰 섰는데, 그가 이따금 고개를 끄덕이는 모양새로 보아 만족스러운 모양이라고 짐작했습니다."], ["When it was finished he came to me looking very busy and very attentive.", "이야기가 끝나자 그는 무척 바쁘고 무척 주의 깊은 얼굴로 내게 다가왔습니다."]], [["“Now, Miss Summerson,”", "\"자, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he said to me, “you won’t be alarmed whatever comes off, I know.", "그가 내게 말했습니다. \"무슨 일이 벌어지든 아가씨는 놀라지 않으리란 걸 압니다."], ["It’s not necessary for me to give you any further caution than to tell you that we have marked this person down and that you may be of use to me before I know it myself.", "더 당부할 것도 없이, 우리가 이 사람을 잡으러 들어간다는 것과, 나 자신이 미처 알기도 전에 아가씨가 내게 도움이 될지도 모른다는 것만 일러두지요."], ["I don’t like to ask such a thing, my dear, but would you walk a little way?”", "이런 부탁은 하고 싶지 않지만, 아가씨, 조금만 걸어주시겠어요?\""]], [["Of course I got out directly and took his arm.", "물론 나는 곧장 내려서 그의 팔을 잡았습니다."]], [["“It ain’t so easy to keep your feet,” said Mr.", "\"발을 디디기가 쉽지 않을 겁니다.\""], ["Bucket, “but take time.”", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"천천히 하세요.\""]], [["Although I looked about me confusedly and hurriedly as we crossed the street, I thought I knew the place.", "거리를 건너는 동안 나는 어수선하고 황급하게 주위를 둘러보았지만, 그곳을 아는 것 같았습니다. \"여기 홀본인가요?\""], ["“Are we in Holborn?” I asked him.", "나는 그에게 물었습니다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Do you know this turning?”", "\"이 골목길을 아시나요?\""]], [["“It looks like Chancery Lane.”", "\"챈서리 레인 같은데요.\""]], [["“And was christened so, my dear,”", "\"바로 그렇게 이름 붙은 곳이지요, 아가씨.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["We turned down it, and as we went shuffling through the sleet, I heard the clocks strike half-past five.", "우리는 그 길로 접어들었고, 진눈깨비를 헤치며 발을 끌며 가는데 시계가 다섯 시 반을 치는 소리가 들렸습니다."], ["We passed on in silence and as quickly as we could with such a foot-hold, when some one coming towards us on the narrow pavement, wrapped in a cloak, stopped and stood aside to give me room.", "우리는 그런 발판으로 갈 수 있는 한 빠르게, 말없이 나아갔는데, 그때 외투를 두른 누군가가 좁은 보도에서 우리 쪽으로 다가오다가 걸음을 멈추고 비켜서서 내게 길을 내주었습니다."], ["In the same moment I heard an exclamation of wonder and my own name from Mr.", "바로 그 순간 나는 우드코트 씨의 입에서 놀라움의 탄성과 내 이름을 듣게 되었지요."], ["Woodcourt. I knew his voice very well.", "나는 그의 목소리를 아주 잘 알았습니다."]], [["It was so unexpected and so—I don’t know what to call it, whether pleasant or painful—to come upon it after my feverish wandering journey, and in the midst of the night, that I could not keep back the tears from my eyes.", "그토록 뜻밖이라, 그리고 너무도—뭐라 불러야 할지, 반가운 건지 가슴 아픈 건지 모르겠는, 열병처럼 헤매던 그 여정 끝에, 그것도 한밤중에 그를 마주치자, 나는 눈에서 솟는 눈물을 도저히 참을 수가 없었습니다."], ["It was like hearing his voice in a strange country.", "마치 낯선 나라에서 그의 목소리를 듣는 것 같았지요."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson, that you should be out at this hour, and in such weather!”", "\"서머슨 양, 이런 시각에, 이런 날씨에 밖에 나와 계시다니요!\""]], [["He had heard from my guardian of my having been called away on some uncommon business and said so to dispense with any explanation.", "그는 내가 어떤 심상찮은 일로 불려 나갔다는 걸 내 후견인에게서 들었던지라, 어떤 설명도 면해주려고 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["I told him that we had but just left a coach and were going—but then I was obliged to look at my companion.", "나는 우리가 방금 마차에서 내려 어디로 가는 길이라고 말하려다—거기서 내 동행을 쳐다보지 않을 수 없었습니다."]], [["“Why, you see, Mr. Woodcourt”—he had caught the name from me—“we are a-going at present into the next street.", "\"아, 그게 말이죠, 우드코트 씨\"—그는 나한테서 그 이름을 알아챈 거였습니다—\"우리는 지금 옆 거리로 가는 참입니다."], ["Inspector Bucket.”", "나는 버킷 경위라고 합니다.\""]], [["Mr. Woodcourt, disregarding my remonstrances, had hurriedly taken off his cloak and was putting it about me.", "우드코트 씨는 내 만류는 아랑곳하지 않고 황급히 자기 외투를 벗어 내 둘레에 둘러주었습니다."], ["“That’s a good move, too,” said Mr.", "\"그것도 잘하신 겁니다.\""], ["Bucket, assisting, “a very good move.”", "버킷 씨가 거들며 말했습니다. \"아주 잘하신 거예요.\""]], [["“May I go with you?”", "\"같이 가도 되겠습니까?\""], ["said Mr. Woodcourt.", "우드코트 씨가 말했습니다."], ["I don’t know whether to me or to my companion.", "내게 묻는 건지 내 동행에게 묻는 건지 모르겠더군요."]], [["“Why, Lord!”", "\"아니, 세상에!\""], ["exclaimed Mr. Bucket, taking the answer on himself.", "버킷 씨가 그 대답을 자기가 떠맡으며 외쳤습니다."], ["“Of course you may.”", "\"물론 되고말고요.\""]], [["It was all said in a moment, and they took me between them, wrapped in the cloak.", "이 모든 게 순식간에 오갔고, 두 사람은 외투에 싸인 나를 가운데 두고 데려갔습니다."]], [["“I have just left Richard,”", "\"방금 리처드한테서 오는 길입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Woodcourt.", "우드코트 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I have been sitting with him since ten o’clock last night.”", "\"어젯밤 열 시부터 그와 함께 앉아 있었지요.\""]], [["“Oh, dear me, he is ill!”", "\"아, 저런, 그가 아픈가요!\""]], [["“No, no, believe me; not ill, but not quite well.", "\"아니요, 아닙니다, 정말로요. 아픈 건 아니고, 그저 그리 좋지 않을 뿐입니다."], ["He was depressed and faint—you know he gets so worried and so worn sometimes—and Ada sent to me of course; and when I came home I found her note and came straight here.", "그가 기운이 빠지고 가물가물하기에—아시다시피 그는 이따금 그렇게 애를 태우고 진이 빠지지요—에이다가 당연히 내게 사람을 보냈고요. 집에 돌아와 그 쪽지를 보고 곧장 이리로 온 겁니다."], ["Well!", "자!"], ["Richard revived so much after a little while, and Ada was so happy and so convinced of its being my doing, though God knows I had little enough to do with it, that I remained with him until he had been fast asleep some hours.", "리처드는 조금 지나자 무척 기운을 차렸고, 에이다는 그게 다 내 덕이라고 어찌나 행복해하고 어찌나 굳게 믿던지요, 하느님은 아시지만 내가 한 일이라곤 거의 없는데도 말입니다. 그래서 나는 그가 몇 시간 푹 잠들 때까지 그와 함께 있었지요."], ["As fast asleep as she is now, I hope!”", "지금 그녀가 자고 있는 만큼이나 푹 잠들었기를 바랍니다!\""]], [["His friendly and familiar way of speaking of them, his unaffected devotion to them, the grateful confidence with which I knew he had inspired my darling, and the comfort he was to her; could I separate all this from his promise to me?", "그들을 두고 그토록 다정하고 허물없이 이야기하는 그 말투며, 꾸밈없는 헌신이며, 그가 내 사랑하는 벗에게 불어넣었음을 내가 아는 그 고마운 믿음이며, 그가 그녀에게 주는 위안이며, 내가 이 모든 것을 그가 내게 한 약속과 떼어 생각할 수 있었을까요? 내 모습이 변한 것에 그토록 마음 아파하며 그가 내게 한 말을 이것이 떠올리게 해주지 않았다면 나는 얼마나 배은망덕했을지요."], ["How thankless I must have been if it had not recalled the words he said to me when he was so moved by the change in my appearance: “I will accept him as a trust, and it shall be a sacred one!”", "\"나는 그를 맡겨진 신뢰로 받아들이겠습니다. 그리고 그것은 거룩한 신뢰가 될 겁니다!\""]], [["We now turned into another narrow street. “Mr.", "이제 우리는 또 다른 좁은 거리로 접어들었습니다."], ["Woodcourt,” said Mr.", "\"우드코트 씨.\""], ["Bucket, who had eyed him closely as we came along, “our business takes us to a law-stationer’s here, a certain Mr.", "오는 동안 그를 유심히 살피던 버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"우리 일은 여기 어느 법률 문구상한테 가는 겁니다."], ["Snagsby’s.", "스내그스비라는 분이지요."], ["What, you know him, do you?”", "아니, 그를 아십니까?\""], ["He was so quick that he saw it in an instant.", "그는 어찌나 눈치가 빠른지 그 자리에서 그걸 알아챘습니다."]], [["“Yes, I know a little of him and have called upon him at this place.”", "\"네, 조금 압니다. 이곳으로 그를 찾아온 적도 있고요.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir?”", "\"그렇습니까, 선생?\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Then you will be so good as to let me leave Miss Summerson with you for a moment while I go and have half a word with him?”", "\"그럼 내가 가서 그와 한두 마디 나누는 동안, 잠깐만 서머슨 양을 선생께 맡겨도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["The last police-officer with whom he had conferred was standing silently behind us.", "그가 마지막으로 의논했던 순경이 우리 뒤에 말없이 서 있었습니다."], ["I was not aware of it until he struck in on my saying I heard some one crying.", "나는 누군가 우는 소리가 들린다고 말했을 때 그가 끼어들기 전까지는 그를 알아채지 못했지요."]], [["“Don’t be alarmed, miss,”", "\"놀라지 마십시오, 아가씨.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“It’s Snagsby’s servant.”", "\"스내그스비네 하녀입니다.\""]], [["“Why, you see,” said Mr.", "\"아, 그게 말이죠.\""], ["Bucket, “the girl’s subject to fits, and has ’em bad upon her to-night.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"그 계집애가 발작 증세가 있는데, 오늘 밤 심하게 도졌어요."], ["A most contrary circumstance it is, for I want certain information out of that girl, and she must be brought to reason somehow.”", "더없이 거북한 상황입니다. 그 애한테서 알아내야 할 게 있는데, 어떻게든 정신을 차리게 해야 하니까요.\""]], [["“At all events, they wouldn’t be up yet if it wasn’t for her, Mr. Bucket,”", "\"어쨌든, 그 애 때문이 아니었으면 저 사람들도 아직 안 일어났을 겁니다, 버킷 씨.\""], ["said the other man.", "다른 사람이 말했습니다."], ["“She’s been at it pretty well all night, sir.”", "\"밤새도록 거의 내내 저러고 있었거든요, 선생.\""]], [["“Well, that’s true,”", "\"음, 그건 사실이지.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“My light’s burnt out.", "\"내 등불이 다 탔군."], ["Show yours a moment.”", "자네 것 좀 잠깐 비춰주게.\""]], [["All this passed in a whisper a door or two from the house in which I could faintly hear crying and moaning.", "이 모든 게, 안에서 우는 소리와 신음 소리가 어렴풋이 들려오는 그 집에서 한두 집 떨어진 곳에서 속삭임으로 오갔습니다."], ["In the little round of light produced for the purpose, Mr. Bucket went up to the door and knocked.", "그러려고 만들어진 그 작고 둥근 불빛 속에서 버킷 씨는 문으로 다가가 두드렸습니다."], ["The door was opened after he had knocked twice, and he went in, leaving us standing in the street.", "두 번 두드리자 문이 열렸고, 그는 우리를 거리에 세워둔 채 안으로 들어갔습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,” said Mr.", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["Woodcourt, “if without obtruding myself on your confidence I may remain near you, pray let me do so.”", "우드코트 씨가 말했습니다. \"당신의 믿음에 주제넘게 끼어들지 않으면서 곁에 있어도 된다면, 부디 그렇게 하게 해주십시오.\""]], [["“You are truly kind,”", "\"정말 친절하시군요.\""], ["I answered.", "나는 대답했습니다."], ["“I need wish to keep no secret of my own from you; if I keep any, it is another’s.”", "\"내 일이라면 당신께 비밀로 할 것이 없습니다. 만약 무엇이든 숨긴다면, 그건 다른 사람의 것이지요.\""]], [["“I quite understand.", "\"잘 알겠습니다."], ["Trust me, I will remain near you only so long as I can fully respect it.”", "믿어주십시오, 그것을 온전히 존중할 수 있는 동안만 당신 곁에 있겠습니다.\""]], [["“I trust implicitly to you,”", "\"전적으로 당신을 믿습니다.\" 나는 말했습니다."], ["I said. “I know and deeply feel how sacredly you keep your promise.”", "\"당신이 얼마나 거룩하게 약속을 지키는지 나는 알고 깊이 느끼고 있어요.\""]], [["After a short time the little round of light shone out again, and Mr. Bucket advanced towards us in it with his earnest face.", "잠시 뒤 그 작고 둥근 불빛이 다시 환히 비쳤고, 버킷 씨가 그 빛 속에서 진지한 얼굴로 우리에게 다가왔습니다."], ["“Please to come in, Miss Summerson,”", "\"들어오시지요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["he said, “and sit down by the fire. Mr.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["Woodcourt, from information I have received I understand you are a medical man.", "\"그리고 불가에 앉으세요. 우드코트 씨, 내가 들은 바로는 의사라고 하시던데."], ["Would you look to this girl and see if anything can be done to bring her round.", "이 계집애를 좀 봐주시고, 정신 차리게 할 무슨 방법이 없는지 봐주시겠습니까?"], ["She has a letter somewhere that I particularly want.", "이 애한테 내가 특별히 원하는 편지가 어딘가 있어요."], ["It’s not in her box, and I think it must be about her; but she is so twisted and clenched up that she is difficult to handle without hurting.”", "그 애 상자 안에는 없으니 분명 몸에 지니고 있을 텐데, 어찌나 비틀리고 잔뜩 움켜쥐고 있는지 다치게 하지 않고 다루기가 어렵군요.\""]], [["We all three went into the house together; although it was cold and raw, it smelt close too from being up all night.", "우리 셋은 함께 집 안으로 들어갔습니다. 춥고 으슬으슬한데도 밤새 사람들이 깨어 있었던 탓에 공기가 텁텁했지요."], ["In the passage behind the door stood a scared, sorrowful-looking little man in a grey coat who seemed to have a naturally polite manner and spoke meekly.", "문 뒤 복도에는 회색 외투를 입은, 겁먹고 슬퍼 보이는 작은 남자가 서 있었는데, 천성이 공손해 보였고 말투도 온순했습니다."]], [["“Downstairs, if you please, Mr.", "\"아래층으로 가시지요, 버킷 씨.\""], ["Bucket,” said he.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“The lady will excuse the front kitchen; we use it as our workaday sitting-room.", "\"숙녀분께서는 앞쪽 부엌을 양해해 주시겠지요. 거기를 평소 거실로 쓰고 있습니다."], ["The back is Guster’s bedroom, and in it she’s a-carrying on, poor thing, to a frightful extent!”", "뒤쪽은 거스터의 침실인데, 거기서 저렇게, 가엾게도, 끔찍할 만큼 난리를 치고 있어요!\""]], [["We went downstairs, followed by Mr.", "우리는 스내그스비 씨를 뒤따라 아래층으로 내려갔습니다."], ["Snagsby, as I soon found the little man to be.", "그 작은 남자가 곧 그 사람이라는 걸 알게 됐지요."], ["In the front kitchen, sitting by the fire, was Mrs. Snagsby, with very red eyes and a very severe expression of face.", "앞쪽 부엌에서는 스내그스비 부인이 불가에 앉아 있었는데, 두 눈이 몹시 빨갛고 얼굴은 무척이나 매서운 표정이었습니다."]], [["“My little woman,” said Mr.", "\"여보.\""], ["Snagsby, entering behind us, “to wave—not to put too fine a point upon it, my dear—hostilities for one single moment in the course of this prolonged night, here is Inspector Bucket, Mr.", "스내그스비 씨가 우리 뒤로 들어서며 말했습니다. \"이 기나긴 밤 중에 단 한순간만이라도—너무 까다롭게 굴지 말고, 여보—적대를 거두어 주오."], ["Woodcourt, and a lady.”", "여기 버킷 경위와 우드코트 씨, 그리고 어느 숙녀분이 오셨소.\""]], [["She looked very much astonished, as she had reason for doing, and looked particularly hard at me.", "그녀는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있는지라 무척 놀란 표정으로, 유독 나를 매섭게 노려보았습니다."]], [["“My little woman,”", "\"여보.\""], ["said Mr. Snagsby, sitting down in the remotest corner by the door, as if he were taking a liberty, “it is not unlikely that you may inquire of me why Inspector Bucket, Mr.", "스내그스비 씨가 마치 외람된 짓이라도 하는 양 문 옆 가장 외진 구석에 앉으며 말했습니다."], ["Woodcourt, and a lady call upon us in Cook’s Court, Cursitor Street, at the present hour.", "\"어쩌면 당신은 어째서 버킷 경위와 우드코트 씨와 어느 숙녀분이 이 시각에 커시터 가의 쿡스 코트로 우리를 찾아왔느냐고 내게 물을지도 모르겠소."], ["I don’t know.", "나는 모르오."], ["I have not the least idea.", "조금도 짐작이 안 가오."], ["If I was to be informed, I should despair of understanding, and I’d rather not be told.”", "설령 듣게 된다 해도 나는 이해할 가망이 없을 테니, 차라리 듣지 않는 편이 낫겠소.\""]], [["He appeared so miserable, sitting with his head upon his hand, and I appeared so unwelcome, that I was going to offer an apology when Mr. Bucket took the matter on himself.", "그가 머리를 손에 괴고 앉은 모습이 어찌나 참담해 보이고, 나는 어찌나 달갑잖은 손님처럼 보였는지, 내가 사과를 하려던 참에 버킷 씨가 그 일을 떠맡았습니다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Snagsby,”", "\"자, 스내그스비 씨.\""], ["said he, “the best thing you can do is to go along with Mr.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["Woodcourt to look after your Guster—”", "\"당신이 할 수 있는 최선은 우드코트 씨와 함께 가서 댁의 거스터를 돌보는 겁니다—\""]], [["“My Guster, Mr. Bucket!”", "\"내 거스터라니요, 버킷 씨!\""], ["cried Mr. Snagsby.", "스내그스비 씨가 외쳤습니다."], ["“Go on, sir, go on.", "\"계속하시오, 선생, 계속해요."], ["I shall be charged with that next.”", "이제 그것까지 내 죄로 뒤집어쓰겠군요.\""]], [["“And to hold the candle,”", "\"그리고 촛불을 들어주거나,\""], ["pursued Mr.", "버킷 씨는 자기 말을 바로잡지도 않고 이어갔습니다."], ["Bucket without correcting himself, “or hold her, or make yourself useful in any way you’re asked.", "\"그 애를 붙들어주거나, 시키는 대로 무엇이든 거들어주는 겁니다. 그렇게 기꺼이 해줄 사람이 세상에 또 없지요."], ["Which there’s not a man alive more ready to do, for you’re a man of urbanity and suavity, you know, and you’ve got the sort of heart that can feel for another. Mr.", "당신은 점잖고 상냥한 사람이고, 남을 헤아릴 줄 아는 그런 마음을 지녔으니까요."], ["Woodcourt, would you be so good as see to her, and if you can get that letter from her, to let me have it as soon as ever you can?”", "우드코트 씨, 저 애를 좀 봐주시겠습니까? 그리고 그 애한테서 편지를 얻어낼 수 있거든, 되도록 빨리 내게 건네주시겠습니까?\""]], [["As they went out, Mr. Bucket made me sit down in a corner by the fire and take off my wet shoes, which he turned up to dry upon the fender, talking all the time.", "두 사람이 나가자 버킷 씨는 나를 불가 구석에 앉히고 젖은 신발을 벗기게 했는데, 그 신발을 난로 가리개 위에 거꾸로 얹어 말리면서 내내 이야기를 했습니다."]], [["“Don’t you be at all put out, miss, by the want of a hospitable look from Mrs.", "\"저기 스내그스비 부인이 손님을 반기는 낯빛이 아니라고 조금도 마음 쓰지 마세요, 아가씨."], ["Snagsby there, because she’s under a mistake altogether.", "부인이 완전히 오해하고 있어서 그러니까요."], ["She’ll find that out sooner than will be agreeable to a lady of her generally correct manner of forming her thoughts, because I’m a-going to explain it to her.”", "부인은 그게 오해라는 걸, 평소 생각을 반듯하게 정리하는 숙녀로서는 달갑지 않을 만큼 빨리 깨닫게 될 겁니다. 내가 지금부터 부인한테 설명할 테니까요.\""], ["Here, standing on the hearth with his wet hat and shawls in his hand, himself a pile of wet, he turned to Mrs. Snagsby.", "그는 젖은 모자와 숄을 손에 든 채, 제 몸이 한 무더기 물기 그 자체인 모습으로 난롯가에 서서 스내그스비 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰습니다."], ["“Now, the first thing that I say to you, as a married woman possessing what you may call charms, you know—‘Believe Me, if All Those Endearing,’ and cetrer—you’re well acquainted with the song, because it’s in vain for you to tell me that you and good society are strangers—charms—attractions, mind you, that ought to give you confidence in yourself—is, that you’ve done it.”", "\"자, 내가 부인께 처음으로 드릴 말씀은, 이를테면 매력이라 할 만한 걸 지닌 기혼 여성으로서 말이죠—'믿어주오, 그 모든 사랑스러운' 어쩌고 하는, 그 노래 잘 아시잖아요, 부인이 상류 사회와 남이라고 내게 우긴들 소용없으니까요—매력 말입니다, 자신감을 가질 만한 그런 매력과 끌림을 지닌 분으로서, 부인은 일을 저질렀다는 겁니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby looked rather alarmed, relented a little and faltered, what did Mr. Bucket mean.", "스내그스비 부인은 자못 놀란 표정으로, 조금 누그러져 더듬더듬 버킷 씨가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말이냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“What does Mr.", "\"버킷 씨가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말이냐고요?\""], ["Bucket mean?”", "그가 되받았습니다."], ["he repeated, and I saw by his face that all the time he talked he was listening for the discovery of the letter, to my own great agitation, for I knew then how important it must be; “I’ll tell you what he means, ma’am.", "그가 이야기하는 내내 편지가 발견되기를 귀 기울이고 있다는 걸 그 얼굴에서 알 수 있었지요. 나도 무척 마음이 떨렸습니다."], ["Go and see Othello acted.", "그게 얼마나 중요한 것인지 그제야 알았으니까요. \"내가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 알려드리지요, 부인."], ["That’s the tragedy for you.”", "가서 『오셀로』 공연을 보세요. 그게 부인한테 딱 맞는 비극입니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby consciously asked why.", "스내그스비 부인은 뜨끔한 듯 왜냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜냐고요?\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Because you’ll come to that if you don’t look out.", "\"조심하지 않으면 부인도 그 꼴이 될 테니까요."], ["Why, at the very moment while I speak, I know what your mind’s not wholly free from respecting this young lady.", "아니, 내가 이렇게 말하는 바로 이 순간에도 부인 마음이 이 젊은 아가씨에 대해 무엇으로부터 온전히 자유롭지 못한지 나는 압니다."], ["But shall I tell you who this young lady is?", "헌데 이 젊은 아가씨가 누구인지 내가 말씀드릴까요?"], ["Now, come, you’re what I call an intellectual woman—with your soul too large for your body, if you come to that, and chafing it—and you know me, and you recollect where you saw me last, and what was talked of in that circle.", "자, 보세요, 부인은 내가 지성적인 여성이라 부르는 그런 사람—굳이 따지자면 영혼이 몸에 비해 너무 커서 몸을 비비적댈 만큼이지요—이고, 나를 알고, 나를 마지막으로 어디서 봤는지, 그 자리에서 무슨 이야기가 오갔는지 기억하실 겁니다. 안 그래요?"], ["Don’t you?", "그렇지요!"], ["Yes! Very well.", "아주 좋습니다."], ["This young lady is that young lady.”", "이 젊은 아가씨가 바로 그 젊은 아가씨입니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby appeared to understand the reference better than I did at the time.", "스내그스비 부인은 그 말뜻을 그때의 나보다 더 잘 알아듣는 것 같았습니다."]], [["“And Toughey—him as you call Jo—was mixed up in the same business, and no other; and the law-writer that you know of was mixed up in the same business, and no other; and your husband, with no more knowledge of it than your great grandfather, was mixed up (by Mr.", "\"그리고 터피—부인이 조라고 부르는 그 애—도 바로 그 일에 얽혀 있었고, 다른 일이 아니었습니다. 부인이 아는 그 법률 필경사도 바로 그 일에 얽혀 있었고, 다른 일이 아니었지요."], ["Tulkinghorn, deceased, his best customer) in the same business, and no other; and the whole bileing of people was mixed up in the same business, and no other.", "부인의 남편도, 부인의 증조부만큼이나 아무것도 모른 채, 바로 그 일에(고인이 된 토킹혼 씨, 그의 가장 큰 단골 손님 때문에) 얽혀 있었고, 다른 일이 아니었습니다."], ["And yet a married woman, possessing your attractions, shuts her eyes (and sparklers too), and goes and runs her delicate-formed head against a wall.", "그리고 그 일에 얽힌 사람이라면 모조리 다 바로 그 일이었지, 다른 일이 아니었어요. 그런데도 부인의 그 매력을 지닌 기혼 여성이 눈을(그리고 반짝이는 눈동자까지) 질끈 감고는, 그 곱게 빚은 머리를 벽에다 들이박고 있는 겁니다."], ["Why, I am ashamed of you!", "원, 부끄러운 줄 아세요!"], ["(I expected Mr. Woodcourt might have got it by this time.)”", "(나는 이쯤이면 우드코트 씨가 그걸 손에 넣었으리라 생각하고 있었습니다.)\""]], [["Mrs. Snagsby shook her head and put her handkerchief to her eyes.", "스내그스비 부인은 고개를 가로젓고 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔습니다."]], [["“Is that all?”", "\"이게 전부일까요?\""], ["said Mr. Bucket excitedly.", "버킷 씨가 격앙되어 말했습니다."], ["“No.", "\"아니죠."], ["See what happens.", "무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보세요."], ["Another person mixed up in that business and no other, a person in a wretched state, comes here to-night and is seen a-speaking to your maid-servant; and between her and your maid-servant there passes a paper that I would give a hundred pound for, down.", "바로 그 일에 얽힌, 다른 일이 아닌, 또 한 사람, 비참한 처지에 놓인 사람이 오늘 밤 여기 와서 댁의 하녀와 이야기하는 게 목격됩니다. 그리고 그녀와 댁의 하녀 사이에 종이 한 장이 건네지는데, 내가 당장 백 파운드라도 내놓을 만한 종이지요."], ["What do you do?", "부인은 어떻게 합니까?"], ["You hide and you watch ’em, and you pounce upon that maid-servant—knowing what she’s subject to and what a little thing will bring ’em on—in that surprising manner and with that severity that, by the Lord, she goes off and keeps off, when a life may be hanging upon that girl’s words!”", "숨어서 그들을 지켜보다가 그 하녀를 덮칩니다—그 애가 무슨 증세가 있는지, 사소한 일에도 그게 도진다는 걸 뻔히 알면서요—그것도 그토록 소스라치게, 그토록 매섭게 덮친 통에, 맙소사, 그 애 입에서 나올 말에 한 목숨이 달려 있을지도 모르는데 그 애가 정신을 놓아버리고 그대로 있는 겁니다!\""]], [["He so thoroughly meant what he said now that I involuntarily clasped my hands and felt the room turning away from me.", "이제 그가 하는 말이 어찌나 절절하게 진심이던지, 나는 나도 모르게 두 손을 맞잡았고 방이 내게서 빙그르르 멀어지는 듯했습니다."], ["But it stopped.", "하지만 멈췄지요."], ["Mr. Woodcourt came in, put a paper into his hand, and went away again.", "우드코트 씨가 들어와 그의 손에 종이 한 장을 쥐여주고는 다시 나갔습니다."]], [["“Now, Mrs. Snagsby, the only amends you can make,”", "\"자, 스내그스비 부인, 부인이 할 수 있는 유일한 보상은,\""], ["said Mr. Bucket, rapidly glancing at it, “is to let me speak a word to this young lady in private here.", "버킷 씨가 그것을 휙 훑어보며 말했습니다."], ["And if you know of any help that you can give to that gentleman in the next kitchen there or can think of any one thing that’s likelier than another to bring the girl round, do your swiftest and best!”", "\"내가 이 젊은 아가씨와 여기서 단둘이 한마디 나누게 해주는 겁니다. 그리고 저 옆 부엌에 있는 저 신사분께 부인이 줄 수 있는 도움이 뭔지 아시거나, 그 애를 정신 차리게 할 만한 무언가가 딱 하나라도 떠오른다면, 부디 가장 빠르고 가장 잘 해주세요!\""], ["In an instant she was gone, and he had shut the door.", "순식간에 그녀는 나갔고, 그는 문을 닫았습니다."], ["“Now my dear, you’re steady and quite sure of yourself?”", "\"자, 아가씨, 마음을 다잡고 정신을 꼭 차리고 있겠지요?\""]], [["“Quite,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["“Whose writing is that?”", "\"이게 누구 필체죠?\""]], [["It was my mother’s.", "어머니의 것이었습니다."], ["A pencil-writing, on a crushed and torn piece of paper, blotted with wet.", "구겨지고 찢긴 종잇조각에 연필로 쓴 글씨였는데, 물기에 얼룩져 있었지요."], ["Folded roughly like a letter, and directed to me at my guardian’s.", "편지처럼 대충 접혀, 내 후견인 댁으로 내게 부쳐진 것이었습니다."]], [["“You know the hand,”", "\"이 필체를 아는군요.\""], ["he said, “and if you are firm enough to read it to me, do!", "그가 말했습니다. \"그리고 내게 읽어줄 만큼 마음이 단단하다면, 그렇게 해주세요!"], ["But be particular to a word.”", "다만 한 마디 한 마디 정확히 해야 합니다.\""]], [["It had been written in portions, at different times.", "그것은 여러 차례에 걸쳐, 서로 다른 때에 나뉘어 쓴 것이었습니다."], ["I read what follows:", "나는 다음을 읽었습니다."]], [["I came to the cottage with two objects.", "나는 두 가지 목적으로 그 오두막에 갔다."], ["First, to see the dear one, if I could, once more—but only to see her—not to speak to her or let her know that I was near.", "첫째는, 할 수만 있다면 그 사랑하는 아이를 한 번만 더 보려고—그러나 그저 보기만 하려고—그 애에게 말을 걸거나 내가 가까이 있음을 알리지는 않으려고."], ["The other object, to elude pursuit and to be lost.", "다른 목적은, 추적을 따돌리고 자취를 감추려는 것이었다."], ["Do not blame the mother for her share.", "그 어미가 한 몫을 두고 그녀를 탓하지 말아라."], ["The assistance that she rendered me, she rendered on my strongest assurance that it was for the dear one’s good.", "그녀가 내게 베푼 도움은, 이것이 그 사랑하는 아이를 위한 일이라는 나의 가장 굳은 다짐을 듣고서 베푼 것이다."], ["You remember her dead child.", "그녀의 죽은 아이를 기억하겠지."], ["The men’s consent I bought, but her help was freely given.", "남자들의 동의는 내가 돈으로 샀지만, 그녀의 도움은 거저 받은 것이다."]], [["“‘I came.’ That was written,”", "\"'나는 갔다.' 이건,\""], ["said my companion, “when she rested there.", "내 동행이 말했습니다. \"그녀가 거기서 쉴 때 쓴 겁니다."], ["It bears out what I made of it.", "내가 짐작했던 걸 뒷받침하는군요."], ["I was right.”", "내가 옳았어요.\""]], [["The next was written at another time:", "다음 것은 또 다른 때에 쓴 것이었습니다."]], [["I have wandered a long distance, and for many hours, and I know that I must soon die.", "나는 먼 거리를, 여러 시간 동안 헤맸고, 내가 곧 죽으리라는 걸 안다. 이 거리들!"], ["These streets! I have no purpose but to die.", "나에게는 죽는 것 말고는 아무 목적도 없다."], ["When I left, I had a worse, but I am saved from adding that guilt to the rest.", "떠나올 때 나에게는 더 나쁜 목적이 있었지만, 그 죄까지 나머지에 보태는 것은 면하게 되었다."], ["Cold, wet, and fatigue are sufficient causes for my being found dead, but I shall die of others, though I suffer from these.", "추위와 젖음과 피로만으로도 내가 죽은 채 발견될 충분한 까닭이 되겠지만, 나는 이것들로 고통받으면서도 다른 것들로 죽을 것이다."], ["It was right that all that had sustained me should give way at once and that I should die of terror and my conscience.", "나를 지탱해 주던 모든 것이 한꺼번에 무너지고, 내가 공포와 양심으로 죽는 것이 마땅했다."]], [["“Take courage,”", "\"기운을 내세요.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“There’s only a few words more.”", "\"이제 몇 마디 안 남았습니다.\""]], [["Those, too, were written at another time.", "그것 역시 또 다른 때에 쓴 것이었습니다."], ["To all appearance, almost in the dark:", "보아하니, 거의 어둠 속에서 쓴 듯했지요."]], [["I have done all I could do to be lost.", "나는 자취를 감추려고 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했다."], ["I shall be soon forgotten so, and shall disgrace him least.", "그렇게 나는 곧 잊힐 것이고, 그분에게 가장 적은 수치만을 끼칠 것이다."], ["I have nothing about me by which I can be recognized.", "나에게는 나를 알아볼 만한 어떤 것도 지니고 있지 않다."], ["This paper I part with now.", "이 종이를 이제 내려놓는다."], ["The place where I shall lie down, if I can get so far, has been often in my mind.", "거기까지 갈 수만 있다면, 내가 누울 그 자리는 줄곧 내 마음속에 있었다."], ["Farewell.", "잘 있어라."], ["Forgive.", "용서해다오."]], [["Mr. Bucket, supporting me with his arm, lowered me gently into my chair.", "버킷 씨는 팔로 나를 부축하여 의자에 가만히 내려 앉혔습니다."], ["“Cheer up!", "\"기운 내세요!"], ["Don’t think me hard with you, my dear, but as soon as ever you feel equal to it, get your shoes on and be ready.”", "내가 모질다고 여기지 마세요, 아가씨, 하지만 견딜 만하다 싶거든 곧바로 신발을 신고 채비를 갖추세요.\""]], [["I did as he required, but I was left there a long time, praying for my unhappy mother.", "나는 그가 시키는 대로 했지만, 거기서 오랫동안 홀로 남아 불행한 어머니를 위해 기도했습니다."], ["They were all occupied with the poor girl, and I heard Mr. Woodcourt directing them and speaking to her often.", "다들 가엾은 그 애한테 매달려 있었고, 우드코트 씨가 그들에게 이래라저래라 일러주며 그 애에게 자주 말을 거는 소리가 들렸지요."], ["At length he came in with Mr. Bucket and said that as it was important to address her gently, he thought it best that I should ask her for whatever information we desired to obtain.", "마침내 그가 버킷 씨와 함께 들어와서, 그 애에게 부드럽게 말을 거는 것이 중요하니 우리가 알아내고자 하는 것이 무엇이든 내가 그 애한테 물어보는 게 가장 좋겠다고 했습니다."], ["There was no doubt that she could now reply to questions if she were soothed and not alarmed.", "그 애를 다독여 놀라게 하지만 않는다면 이제 질문에 대답할 수 있으리라는 건 의심의 여지가 없었지요."], ["The questions, Mr. Bucket said, were how she came by the letter, what passed between her and the person who gave her the letter, and where the person went.", "버킷 씨가 말하기로 질문은, 그 애가 어떻게 편지를 손에 넣었는지, 편지를 준 사람과 그 애 사이에 무슨 일이 오갔는지, 그리고 그 사람이 어디로 갔는지였습니다."], ["Holding my mind as steadily as I could to these points, I went into the next room with them. Mr.", "나는 이 점들에 정신을 최대한 단단히 붙들고 그들과 함께 옆방으로 들어갔습니다."], ["Woodcourt would have remained outside, but at my solicitation went in with us.", "우드코트 씨는 밖에 남아 있으려 했지만, 내가 청하자 우리와 함께 들어갔지요."]], [["The poor girl was sitting on the floor where they had laid her down.", "가엾은 그 애는 사람들이 눕혀둔 바닥에 앉아 있었습니다."], ["They stood around her, though at a little distance, that she might have air.", "다들 그 애 곁에 둘러서 있었으나, 그 애가 숨을 쉴 수 있도록 조금 떨어져 있었지요."], ["She was not pretty and looked weak and poor, but she had a plaintive and a good face, though it was still a little wild.", "그 애는 예쁘지 않았고 약하고 가난해 보였지만, 아직 조금 정신 나간 듯하긴 해도 애처롭고 선한 얼굴을 하고 있었습니다."], ["I kneeled on the ground beside her and put her poor head upon my shoulder, whereupon she drew her arm round my neck and burst into tears.", "나는 그 애 곁 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 그 가엾은 머리를 내 어깨에 기대게 했는데, 그러자 그 애는 한 팔을 내 목에 두르고 와락 울음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["“My poor girl,”", "\"가엾은 것.\" 나는 그 애 이마에 얼굴을 갖다 대며 말했습니다."], ["said I, laying my face against her forehead, for indeed I was crying too, and trembling, “it seems cruel to trouble you now, but more depends on our knowing something about this letter than I could tell you in an hour.”", "정말이지 나도 울며 떨고 있었으니까요. \"지금 너를 괴롭히는 게 모질게 보이겠지만, 우리가 이 편지에 관해 무언가를 알아내는 데 달린 것이 한 시간을 들여도 다 말 못 할 만큼 크단다.\""]], [["She began piteously declaring that she didn’t mean any harm, she didn’t mean any harm, Mrs.", "그 애는 자기는 나쁜 뜻이 없었다고, 나쁜 뜻이 없었다고, 스내그스비 부인!"], ["Snagsby!", "하고 애처롭게 외치기 시작했습니다."]], [["“We are all sure of that,”", "\"우리 모두 그건 다 안단다.\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."], ["“But pray tell me how you got it.”", "\"하지만 제발 그걸 어떻게 손에 넣었는지 말해다오.\""]], [["“Yes, dear lady, I will, and tell you true.", "\"네, 친절하신 아씨, 그럴게요, 사실대로 말씀드릴게요."], ["I’ll tell true, indeed, Mrs. Snagsby.”", "정말로 사실대로 말할게요, 스내그스비 부인.\""]], [["“I am sure of that,”", "\"그건 나도 안단다.\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."], ["“And how was it?”", "\"그래, 어떻게 된 거니?\""]], [["“I had been out on an errand, dear lady—long after it was dark—quite late; and when I came home, I found a common-looking person, all wet and muddy, looking up at our house.", "\"심부름을 나갔었어요, 친절하신 아씨—날이 어두워진 지 한참 뒤에—아주 늦게요. 그러고 집에 돌아오니 흔해 빠진 행색의, 온통 젖고 진흙투성이인 사람이 우리 집을 올려다보고 있더라고요."], ["When she saw me coming in at the door, she called me back and said did I live here.", "그 여자는 내가 문으로 들어가는 걸 보더니 나를 불러 세우고는, 여기 사느냐고 물었어요."], ["And I said yes, and she said she knew only one or two places about here, but had lost her way and couldn’t find them.", "그래서 나는 그렇다고 했고, 그 여자는 이 근처에 한두 군데밖에 모르는데 길을 잃어 찾을 수가 없다고 했어요."], ["Oh, what shall I do, what shall I do!", "아, 어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["They won’t believe me!", "사람들이 내 말을 안 믿을 거야!"], ["She didn’t say any harm to me, and I didn’t say any harm to her, indeed, Mrs. Snagsby!”", "그 여자는 나한테 나쁜 말 하나 안 했고, 나도 그 여자한테 나쁜 말 하나 안 했어요, 정말이에요, 스내그스비 부인!\""]], [["It was necessary for her mistress to comfort her—which she did, I must say, with a good deal of contrition—before she could be got beyond this.", "그 애가 이 대목을 넘어서기까지는 안주인이 그 애를 달래줄 필요가 있었는데—꽤나 뉘우치는 태도로 그렇게 해주었다는 걸 나는 말해두어야겠습니다."]], [["“She could not find those places,”", "\"그 여자가 그곳들을 못 찾았구나.\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했습니다."]], [["“No!”", "\"네!\""], ["cried the girl, shaking her head.", "그 애가 고개를 가로저으며 외쳤습니다."], ["“No!", "\"네!"], ["Couldn’t find them.", "못 찾았어요."], ["And she was so faint, and lame, and miserable, Oh so wretched, that if you had seen her, Mr. Snagsby, you’d have given her half a crown, I know!”", "그리고 그 여자는 어찌나 기진하고, 다리를 절고, 비참하던지, 아 어찌나 가엾던지, 그 여자를 보셨더라면, 스내그스비 씨, 분명히 반 크라운은 주셨을 거예요, 정말로요!\""]], [["“Well, Guster, my girl,”", "\"그래, 거스터.\""], ["said he, at first not knowing what to say.", "그가 처음에는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 하며 말했습니다."], ["“I hope I should.”", "\"나도 그랬으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["“And yet she was so well spoken,”", "\"그런데도 어찌나 말씨가 고운지,\""], ["said the girl, looking at me with wide open eyes, “that it made a person’s heart bleed.", "그 애가 두 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 나를 보며 말했습니다. \"듣는 사람 가슴이 미어질 정도였어요."], ["And so she said to me, did I know the way to the burying ground?", "그러더니 나한테, 매장지로 가는 길을 아느냐고 물었어요."], ["And I asked her which burying ground.", "그래서 어느 매장지냐고 물었지요."], ["And she said, the poor burying ground.", "그랬더니 가난한 사람들 매장지라고 했어요."], ["And so I told her I had been a poor child myself, and it was according to parishes.", "그래서 나는 나도 가난한 아이였는데 그건 교구마다 다르다고 말해줬어요."], ["But she said she meant a poor burying ground not very far from here, where there was an archway, and a step, and an iron gate.”", "하지만 그 여자는 여기서 그리 멀지 않은 가난한 사람들 매장지를 말하는 거라고, 아치 통로와 계단 하나와 쇠문이 있는 데라고 했어요.\""]], [["As I watched her face and soothed her to go on, I saw that Mr. Bucket received this with a look which I could not separate from one of alarm.", "내가 그 애 얼굴을 살피며 계속 말하도록 다독이는데, 버킷 씨가 이 말을 받아들이는 표정에서 나는 경악이라고밖에 볼 수 없는 기색을 읽었습니다."]], [["“Oh, dear, dear!”", "\"아, 어떡해, 어떡해!\""], ["cried the girl, pressing her hair back with her hands.", "그 애가 두 손으로 머리카락을 뒤로 쓸어 넘기며 외쳤습니다."], ["“What shall I do, what shall I do!", "\"어쩌면 좋아, 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["She meant the burying ground where the man was buried that took the sleeping-stuff—that you came home and told us of, Mr. Snagsby—that frightened me so, Mrs.", "그 여자가 말한 건 그 남자가 묻힌 매장지였어요—수면제를 먹은 그 남자요—스내그스비 씨가 집에 와서 우리한테 얘기해 준 그 사람, 나를 그렇게 무섭게 만든 그 사람이요, 스내그스비 부인."], ["Snagsby.", "아, 또 무서워."], ["Oh, I am frightened again. Hold me!”", "나 좀 붙잡아 주세요!\""]], [["“You are so much better now,”", "\"이제 한결 좋아졌잖니.\" 나는 말했습니다."], ["said I. “Pray, pray tell me more.”", "\"제발, 제발 더 말해다오.\""]], [["“Yes I will, yes I will!", "\"네, 그럴게요, 그럴게요!"], ["But don’t be angry with me, that’s a dear lady, because I have been so ill.”", "하지만 나한테 화내지 마세요, 친절하신 아씨, 내가 너무 아팠으니까요.\""]], [["Angry with her, poor soul!", "그 애한테 화를 내다니, 가엾은 것!"]], [["“There!", "\"자!"], ["Now I will, now I will.", "이제 말할게요, 말할게요."], ["So she said, could I tell her how to find it, and I said yes, and I told her; and she looked at me with eyes like almost as if she was blind, and herself all waving back.", "그래서 그 여자가 그곳을 찾는 길을 알려줄 수 있느냐고 했고, 나는 그렇다고 하면서 알려줬어요. 그러자 그 여자는 거의 앞을 못 보는 사람처럼 그런 눈으로 나를 보더니, 제 몸이 온통 휘청휘청하더라고요."], ["And so she took out the letter, and showed it me, and said if she was to put that in the post-office, it would be rubbed out and not minded and never sent; and would I take it from her, and send it, and the messenger would be paid at the house.", "그러고는 그 편지를 꺼내 나한테 보여주면서, 이걸 우체국에 넣으면 지워지고 무시당해서 영영 부쳐지지 않을 거라고, 그러니 자기한테서 이걸 받아 부쳐주겠느냐고, 심부름 삯은 그 집에서 치를 거라고 했어요."], ["And so I said yes, if it was no harm, and she said no—no harm.", "그래서 나는 나쁜 일이 아니라면 그러겠다고 했고, 그 여자는 아니라고—나쁜 일이 아니라고 했어요."], ["And so I took it from her, and she said she had nothing to give me, and I said I was poor myself and consequently wanted nothing.", "그래서 나는 그걸 받았고, 그 여자는 나한테 줄 게 아무것도 없다고 했고, 나는 나도 가난하니 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 했어요."], ["And so she said God bless you, and went.”", "그러자 그 여자는 신의 가호가 있기를, 하고는 갔어요.\""]], [["“And did she go—”", "\"그래서 그 여자가 갔니—\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["cried the girl, anticipating the inquiry.", "그 애가 질문을 앞질러 외쳤습니다."], ["“Yes!", "\"네!"], ["She went the way I had shown her.", "내가 알려준 길로 갔어요."], ["Then I came in, and Mrs. Snagsby came behind me from somewhere and laid hold of me, and I was frightened.”", "그러고 나서 나는 들어왔는데, 스내그스비 부인이 어디선가 내 뒤로 와서 나를 붙들었고, 나는 무서웠어요.\""]], [["Mr. Woodcourt took her kindly from me.", "우드코트 씨가 그 애를 내게서 다정하게 떼어내 맡았습니다."], ["Mr. Bucket wrapped me up, and immediately we were in the street.", "버킷 씨는 나를 감싸주었고, 우리는 곧바로 거리로 나섰습니다."], ["Mr. Woodcourt hesitated, but I said, “Don’t leave me now!”", "우드코트 씨가 머뭇거렸지만, 나는 \"지금 나를 두고 가지 마세요!\""], ["and Mr.", "하고 말했고, 버킷 씨도 \"우리와 함께 있는 게 나을 겁니다, 당신이 필요할지도 모르니까요."], ["Bucket added, “You’ll be better with us, we may want you; don’t lose time!”", "지체하지 맙시다!\" 하고 덧붙였습니다."]], [["I have the most confused impressions of that walk.", "그 걸음에 대해 나는 더없이 어수선한 인상만을 간직하고 있습니다."], ["I recollect that it was neither night nor day, that morning was dawning but the street-lamps were not yet put out, that the sleet was still falling and that all the ways were deep with it.", "밤도 낮도 아니었고, 아침이 밝아오고 있었지만 가로등은 아직 꺼지지 않았고, 진눈깨비가 여전히 내리고 있었고 모든 길이 그것으로 깊이 잠겨 있었던 것이 기억납니다."], ["I recollect a few chilled people passing in the streets.", "추위에 떨며 거리를 지나가던 몇몇 사람들이 기억납니다."], ["I recollect the wet house-tops, the clogged and bursting gutters and water-spouts, the mounds of blackened ice and snow over which we passed, the narrowness of the courts by which we went.", "젖은 지붕들, 막혀 터질 듯한 도랑과 빗물받이들, 우리가 밟고 지난 거뭇한 얼음과 눈 더미들, 우리가 지나간 안마당의 비좁음이 기억납니다."], ["At the same time I remember that the poor girl seemed to be yet telling her story audibly and plainly in my hearing, that I could feel her resting on my arm, that the stained house-fronts put on human shapes and looked at me, that great water-gates seemed to be opening and closing in my head or in the air, and that the unreal things were more substantial than the real.", "그러면서도 가엾은 그 애가 여전히 내 귓가에서 또렷하고 분명하게 자기 이야기를 하고 있는 것 같았고, 그 애가 내 팔에 기대고 있는 것이 느껴졌고, 얼룩진 집들의 정면이 사람의 형상을 띠고 나를 바라보았으며, 거대한 수문들이 내 머릿속에서, 혹은 허공에서 열렸다 닫혔다 하는 것 같았고, 그 허깨비 같은 것들이 현실보다 더 실체 같았던 것이 기억납니다."]], [["At last we stood under a dark and miserable covered way, where one lamp was burning over an iron gate and where the morning faintly struggled in.", "마침내 우리는 어둡고 음산한 지붕 덮인 통로 아래 서게 되었는데, 쇠문 위로 등불 하나가 타오르고 아침이 희미하게 비집고 들어오는 곳이었습니다."], ["The gate was closed.", "문은 닫혀 있었지요."], ["Beyond it was a burial ground—a dreadful spot in which the night was very slowly stirring, but where I could dimly see heaps of dishonoured graves and stones, hemmed in by filthy houses with a few dull lights in their windows and on whose walls a thick humidity broke out like a disease.", "그 너머는 매장지였습니다—밤이 아주 더디게 꿈틀거리는 끔찍한 곳이었지만, 멸시받은 무덤과 비석 더미가 어렴풋이 보였고, 그것들은 창마다 흐릿한 불빛 몇 개를 단 더러운 집들에 둘러싸여 있었으며, 그 집들의 벽에는 짙은 습기가 병처럼 배어 나오고 있었습니다."], ["On the step at the gate, drenched in the fearful wet of such a place, which oozed and splashed down everywhere, I saw, with a cry of pity and horror, a woman lying—Jenny, the mother of the dead child.", "그 문 옆 계단 위, 어디에나 배어 나와 튀어 흐르는 그런 곳의 무서운 물기에 흠뻑 젖은 채, 나는 가엾고 끔찍한 비명과 함께 한 여인이 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다—죽은 아이의 어머니인 제니였습니다."]], [["I ran forward, but they stopped me, and Mr. Woodcourt entreated me with the greatest earnestness, even with tears, before I went up to the figure to listen for an instant to what Mr.", "나는 앞으로 달려갔지만 그들이 나를 막았고, 우드코트 씨는 내가 그 형상에게 다가가기 전에 버킷 씨가 하는 말을 잠깐만 들어달라고 더없이 간절하게, 눈물까지 글썽이며 애원했습니다."], ["Bucket said. I did so, as I thought.", "나는 그렇게 했지요, 그랬다고 생각합니다."], ["I did so, as I am sure.", "그렇게 했습니다, 분명히 그랬습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson, you’ll understand me, if you think a moment.", "\"서머슨 양, 잠깐만 생각해 보면 내 말을 이해할 겁니다."], ["They changed clothes at the cottage.”", "그들은 오두막에서 옷을 바꿔 입었어요.\""]], [["They changed clothes at the cottage.", "그들은 오두막에서 옷을 바꿔 입었다."], ["I could repeat the words in my mind, and I knew what they meant of themselves, but I attached no meaning to them in any other connexion.", "나는 그 말을 마음속으로 되뇔 수 있었고, 그 말 자체가 무슨 뜻인지 알았지만, 다른 어떤 연관 속에서도 거기에 아무 의미를 붙이지 못했습니다."]], [["“And one returned,” said Mr.", "\"그러고는 하나가 돌아왔고,\""], ["Bucket, “and one went on.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["And the one that went on only went on a certain way agreed upon to deceive and then turned across country and went home.", "\"하나는 계속 갔지요. 그리고 계속 간 쪽은 속이려고 합의된 어느 길까지만 가다가, 들판을 가로질러 집으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["Think a moment!”", "잠깐만 생각해 보세요!\""]], [["I could repeat this in my mind too, but I had not the least idea what it meant.", "이것도 나는 마음속으로 되뇔 수 있었지만, 그게 무슨 뜻인지는 도무지 알 수 없었습니다."], ["I saw before me, lying on the step, the mother of the dead child.", "나는 내 앞 계단에 죽은 아이의 어머니가 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["She lay there with one arm creeping round a bar of the iron gate and seeming to embrace it.", "그녀는 한 팔을 쇠문 창살에 감아 그것을 끌어안은 듯한 모습으로 거기 누워 있었지요."], ["She lay there, who had so lately spoken to my mother.", "그토록 얼마 전에 내 어머니에게 말을 걸었던 그녀가 거기 누워 있었습니다."], ["She lay there, a distressed, unsheltered, senseless creature.", "비탄에 잠긴, 비바람을 가릴 데 없는, 정신을 놓은 가엾은 것이 거기 누워 있었습니다."], ["She who had brought my mother’s letter, who could give me the only clue to where my mother was; she, who was to guide us to rescue and save her whom we had sought so far, who had come to this condition by some means connected with my mother that I could not follow, and might be passing beyond our reach and help at that moment; she lay there, and they stopped me!", "내 어머니의 편지를 가져온 그녀, 어머니가 어디 있는지에 대한 유일한 실마리를 내게 줄 수 있는 그녀, 우리가 그토록 멀리까지 찾아 헤맨 그분을 구하고 살려내도록 우리를 이끌어줄 그녀, 내가 따라잡을 수 없는 어떤 식으로든 내 어머니와 얽힌 까닭에 이런 지경에 이르렀고 바로 그 순간 우리 손이 닿지 않고 도움이 미치지 않는 곳으로 넘어가고 있을지도 모르는 그녀가, 거기 누워 있었는데, 그들이 나를 막았습니다!"], ["I saw but did not comprehend the solemn and compassionate look in Mr. Woodcourt’s face.", "나는 우드코트 씨의 얼굴에 어린 엄숙하고 가엾어하는 표정을 보았으나 이해하지 못했습니다."], ["I saw but did not comprehend his touching the other on the breast to keep him back.", "그가 다른 한 사람의 가슴에 손을 대어 그를 붙드는 것을 보았으나 이해하지 못했습니다."], ["I saw him stand uncovered in the bitter air, with a reverence for something.", "나는 그가 무언가에 대한 경의로 매서운 공기 속에 모자를 벗고 서 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["But my understanding for all this was gone.", "하지만 이 모든 것에 대한 내 이해력은 사라지고 없었지요."]], [["I even heard it said between them, “Shall she go?”", "나는 그들 사이에 이런 말이 오가는 것까지 들었습니다. \"그녀를 가게 할까요?\""]], [["“She had better go.", "\"가는 게 낫겠지요."], ["Her hands should be the first to touch her.", "저 여자한테 먼저 손을 대야 할 사람은 그녀입니다."], ["They have a higher right than ours.”", "우리보다 더 높은 권리가 그녀에게 있어요.\""]], [["I passed on to the gate and stooped down.", "나는 문으로 다가가 몸을 굽혔습니다."], ["I lifted the heavy head, put the long dank hair aside, and turned the face.", "무거운 머리를 들어 올리고, 길게 늘어진 축축한 머리카락을 옆으로 치우고, 얼굴을 돌렸습니다."], ["And it was my mother, cold and dead. Perspective", "그건 내 어머니였습니다, 차갑게 식어 죽은."]], [["I proceed to other passages of my narrative.", "이야기의 다른 대목으로 넘어가겠습니다."], ["From the goodness of all about me I derived such consolation as I can never think of unmoved.", "나는 주위 사람들의 다정함에서, 떠올릴 때마다 가슴이 먹먹해지는 위안을 얻었습니다."], ["I have already said so much of myself, and so much still remains, that I will not dwell upon my sorrow.", "나 자신에 관한 이야기는 이미 충분히 했고 앞으로도 할 이야기가 많으니, 내 슬픔만 붙들고 늘어지지는 않겠습니다."], ["I had an illness, but it was not a long one; and I would avoid even this mention of it if I could quite keep down the recollection of their sympathy.", "나는 한 차례 앓았습니다만 오래 가지는 않았습니다. 사람들의 따뜻한 마음에 대한 기억을 깨끗이 눌러둘 수만 있다면 이 병 이야기조차 꺼내지 않았을 것입니다."]], [["I proceed to other passages of my narrative.", "이야기의 다른 대목으로 넘어가겠습니다."]], [["During the time of my illness, we were still in London, where Mrs. Woodcourt had come, on my guardian’s invitation, to stay with us.", "내가 앓는 동안 우리는 여전히 런던에 머물렀고, 그곳에 우드코트 부인이 후견인의 초대를 받아 우리와 함께 지내러 와 있었습니다."], ["When my guardian thought me well and cheerful enough to talk with him in our old way—though I could have done that sooner if he would have believed me—I resumed my work and my chair beside his.", "후견인이 보기에 내가 예전처럼 그와 이야기를 나눌 만큼 건강하고 명랑해졌다고 여겼을 때―그가 내 말을 믿어주기만 했다면 나는 더 일찍 그럴 수 있었을 텐데―나는 다시 일감을 잡고 그의 곁 의자에 앉았습니다."], ["He had appointed the time himself, and we were alone.", "시간은 그가 직접 정했고, 우리 둘뿐이었습니다."]], [["“Dame Trot,”", "\"트롯 아주머니.\""], ["said he, receiving me with a kiss, “welcome to the growlery again, my dear.", "후견인이 입맞춤으로 나를 맞으며 말했습니다. \"투덜방에 다시 온 걸 환영하네, 그래."], ["I have a scheme to develop, little woman.", "펼쳐 보일 계획이 하나 있어, 우리 꼬마 아가씨."], ["I propose to remain here, perhaps for six months, perhaps for a longer time—as it may be.", "나는 이곳에 머물 작정이야, 어쩌면 여섯 달, 어쩌면 더 길게―형편 닿는 대로."], ["Quite to settle here for a while, in short.”", "한마디로, 한동안 여기 아주 눌러앉을 셈이지.\""]], [["“And in the meanwhile leave Bleak House?”", "\"그럼 그동안 황폐한 집은 떠나는 건가요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“Aye, my dear?", "\"그래, 얘야?"], ["Bleak House,”", "황폐한 집은.\""], ["he returned, “must learn to take care of itself.”", "그가 대꾸했습니다. \"제 앞가림을 제가 하는 법을 배워야지.\""]], [["I thought his tone sounded sorrowful, but looking at him, I saw his kind face lighted up by its pleasantest smile.", "그의 목소리가 서글프게 들리는 듯해서 그를 바라보았더니, 그 다정한 얼굴이 더없이 환한 미소로 밝아 있었습니다."]], [["“Bleak House,”", "\"황폐한 집은.\""], ["he repeated—and his tone did NOT sound sorrowful, I found—“must learn to take care of itself.", "그가 거듭 말했는데, 이번에는 목소리가 서글프게 들리지 않았습니다. \"제 앞가림을 제가 하는 법을 배워야지."], ["It is a long way from Ada, my dear, and Ada stands much in need of you.”", "거기는 에이다한테서 너무 멀어, 그래. 그리고 에이다한테는 네가 무척 필요하단다.\""]], [["“It’s like you, guardian,”", "\"후견인다우세요.\""], ["said I, “to have been taking that into consideration for a happy surprise to both of us.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"우리 둘 다 기쁘게 놀랄 일을 마련하시려고 그런 생각까지 다 하시다니요.\""]], [["“Not so disinterested either, my dear, if you mean to extol me for that virtue, since if you were generally on the road, you could be seldom with me.", "\"그렇게 사심 없는 일도 아니야, 얘야, 네가 그 미덕으로 나를 치켜세울 작정이라면 말이지. 네가 줄곧 길 위에 나가 있으면 나랑 함께 있는 일이 드물 테니까."], ["And besides, I wish to hear as much and as often of Ada as I can in this condition of estrangement from poor Rick.", "게다가 나는 가엾은 릭과 이렇게 서먹해진 처지에, 에이다 소식을 되도록 많이, 되도록 자주 듣고 싶구나."], ["Not of her alone, but of him too, poor fellow.”", "그 애만이 아니라 그 친구 소식도, 가엾은 녀석 말이야.\""]], [["“Have you seen Mr. Woodcourt, this morning, guardian?”", "\"오늘 아침에 우드코트 씨를 만나셨어요, 후견인?\""]], [["“I see Mr. Woodcourt every morning, Dame Durden.”", "\"우드코트 씨라면 아침마다 만나지, 더든 아주머니.\""]], [["“Does he still say the same of Richard?”", "\"그분이 여전히 리처드를 두고 똑같은 말을 하시던가요?\""]], [["“Just the same.", "\"그대로야."], ["He knows of no direct bodily illness that he has; on the contrary, he believes that he has none.", "그 친구한테 무슨 직접적인 몸의 병이 있는 건 모르겠다더구나. 오히려 그런 병은 없다고 믿고 있어."], ["Yet he is not easy about him; who CAN be?”", "그런데도 그 친구가 마음에 걸린다는 거야. 안 걸릴 사람이 누가 있겠니?\""]], [["My dear girl had been to see us lately every day, some times twice in a day.", "내 사랑하는 아이는 요즈음 날마다, 어떤 때는 하루에 두 번씩 우리를 보러 왔습니다."], ["But we had foreseen, all along, that this would only last until I was quite myself.", "하지만 우리는 이것이 내가 완전히 회복할 때까지만 이어지리란 것을 처음부터 내다보고 있었습니다."], ["We knew full well that her fervent heart was as full of affection and gratitude towards her cousin John as it had ever been, and we acquitted Richard of laying any injunctions upon her to stay away; but we knew on the other hand that she felt it a part of her duty to him to be sparing of her visits at our house.", "그 애의 뜨거운 마음이 사촌 존을 향한 애정과 고마움으로 예전과 다름없이 가득하다는 것을 우리는 잘 알고 있었고, 리처드가 그 애더러 발길을 끊으라고 어떤 명령을 내린 것이 아니라는 점도 우리는 인정했습니다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 그 애가 우리 집 방문을 자제하는 것이 그에 대한 자기 도리의 한 부분이라 여긴다는 것도 우리는 알았습니다."], ["My guardian’s delicacy had soon perceived this and had tried to convey to her that he thought she was right.", "후견인의 섬세한 마음은 이를 곧 알아채고, 그 애가 옳다고 생각한다는 뜻을 그 애에게 전하려 애썼습니다."]], [["“Dear, unfortunate, mistaken Richard,” said I.", "\"가엾고 불운하고 그릇된 리처드.\""], ["“When will he awake from his delusion!”", "내가 말했습니다. \"그 미망에서 언제나 깨어날까요!\""]], [["“He is not in the way to do so now, my dear,”", "\"지금은 그럴 처지가 아니야, 얘야.\""], ["replied my guardian.", "후견인이 대답했습니다."], ["“The more he suffers, the more averse he will be to me, having made me the principal representative of the great occasion of his suffering.”", "\"고통이 커질수록 그 친구는 나를 더 멀리할 거야. 자기 고통의 그 거대한 빌미를 대표하는 인물로 나를 삼아버렸으니까.\""]], [["I could not help adding, “So unreasonably!”", "나는 한마디 보태지 않을 수 없었습니다. \"그렇게 터무니없이!\""]], [["“Ah, Dame Trot, Dame Trot,”", "\"아아, 트롯 아주머니, 트롯 아주머니.\""], ["returned my guardian, “what shall we find reasonable in Jarndyce and Jarndyce!", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다. \"잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서 터무니없지 않은 게 어디 있겠니!"], ["Unreason and injustice at the top, unreason and injustice at the heart and at the bottom, unreason and injustice from beginning to end—if it ever has an end—how should poor Rick, always hovering near it, pluck reason out of it?", "꼭대기에도 부조리와 불의, 한복판과 밑바닥에도 부조리와 불의, 처음부터 끝까지―끝이라는 게 있기나 하다면―부조리와 불의뿐인데, 늘 그 언저리를 맴도는 가엾은 릭이 어떻게 거기서 이치를 뽑아내겠니?"], ["He no more gathers grapes from thorns or figs from thistles than older men did in old times.”", "그 친구가 가시덤불에서 포도를, 엉겅퀴에서 무화과를 거둘 수 없는 건, 옛 시절 더 나이 든 사람들이 그러지 못한 거나 매한가지지.\""]], [["His gentleness and consideration for Richard whenever we spoke of him touched me so that I was always silent on this subject very soon.", "리처드 이야기가 나올 때마다 보여주는 그의 너그러움과 배려가 어찌나 가슴에 사무치던지, 나는 이 화제에서는 늘 금세 입을 다물고 말았습니다."]], [["“I suppose the Lord Chancellor, and the Vice Chancellors, and the whole Chancery battery of great guns would be infinitely astonished by such unreason and injustice in one of their suitors,”", "\"대법원장님이나, 부원장님들이나, 대법원의 그 거창한 대포들이 죄다 모인다 해도, 자기네 소송인 가운데 한 사람이 이렇게 터무니없고 부당한 꼴을 본다면 한없이 놀랄 테지.\""], ["pursued my guardian.", "후견인이 말을 이었습니다."], ["“When those learned gentlemen begin to raise moss-roses from the powder they sow in their wigs, I shall begin to be astonished too!”", "\"그 학식 높은 양반들이 자기네 가발에 뿌리는 분으로 이끼장미를 키워내기 시작하면, 그때는 나도 놀라기 시작하마!\""]], [["He checked himself in glancing towards the window to look where the wind was and leaned on the back of my chair instead.", "그는 바람이 어느 쪽에서 부는지 보려고 창 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리다 말고, 대신 내 의자 등받이에 기댔습니다."]], [["“Well, well, little woman!", "\"자, 자, 꼬마 아가씨!"], ["To go on, my dear.", "계속하자꾸나, 그래."], ["This rock we must leave to time, chance, and hopeful circumstance.", "이 바위는 시간과 운수와 희망 어린 형편에 맡길 수밖에 없어."], ["We must not shipwreck Ada upon it.", "우리가 거기에 에이다를 좌초시켜선 안 돼."], ["She cannot afford, and he cannot afford, the remotest chance of another separation from a friend.", "그 애도, 그 친구도, 또 한 번 벗과 헤어지는 일이라면 아주 작은 가능성조차 감당할 수 없으니까."], ["Therefore I have particularly begged of Woodcourt, and I now particularly beg of you, my dear, not to move this subject with Rick.", "그래서 나는 우드코트한테 특별히 당부했고, 이제 너한테도 특별히 당부하마, 얘야. 릭한테 이 문제를 꺼내지 말아라."], ["Let it rest.", "그냥 두렴."], ["Next week, next month, next year, sooner or later, he will see me with clearer eyes.", "다음 주든, 다음 달이든, 내년이든, 늦든 이르든, 그 친구는 더 맑은 눈으로 나를 보게 될 게다."], ["I can wait.”", "나는 기다릴 수 있어.\""]], [["But I had already discussed it with him, I confessed; and so, I thought, had Mr.", "하지만 나는 이미 그와 이 문제를 의논했노라고 털어놓았습니다."], ["Woodcourt.", "그리고 내 짐작에 우드코트 씨도 그랬을 것이라고 했습니다."]], [["“So he tells me,”", "\"그렇다고 하더구나.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대답했습니다."], ["“Very good.", "\"아주 좋아."], ["He has made his protest, and Dame Durden has made hers, and there is nothing more to be said about it.", "그 사람도 자기 뜻을 밝혔고, 더든 아주머니도 자기 뜻을 밝혔으니, 그 이야긴 더 할 게 없겠다."], ["Now I come to Mrs. Woodcourt.", "자, 이제 우드코트 부인 이야기를 해보자."], ["How do you like her, my dear?”", "그분이 마음에 드니, 얘야?\""]], [["In answer to this question, which was oddly abrupt, I said I liked her very much and thought she was more agreeable than she used to be.", "뜬금없이 불쑥 튀어나온 이 물음에, 나는 그분이 무척 마음에 들고 예전보다 한결 다정해진 것 같다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“I think so too,”", "\"나도 그렇게 생각해.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“Less pedigree?", "\"족보 얘기가 줄어서?"], ["Not so much of Morgan ap—what’s his name?”", "모건 앱―그 이름이 뭐더라?―얘기가 덜해서 말이지?\""]], [["That was what I meant, I acknowledged, though he was a very harmless person, even when we had had more of him.", "내 말뜻이 바로 그것이었다고 나는 인정했습니다. 비록 그 양반 이야기를 더 많이 들었을 때도 그분은 아주 해롭잖은 인물이긴 했지만요."]], [["“Still, upon the whole, he is as well in his native mountains,”", "\"그래도, 어쨌든, 그 양반은 제 고향 산속에 그냥 있는 편이 낫겠어.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“I agree with you.", "\"네 말에 동의한다."], ["Then, little woman, can I do better for a time than retain Mrs. Woodcourt here?”", "그러면 꼬마 아가씨, 한동안 우드코트 부인을 여기 머물게 하는 것보다 더 나은 방도가 있겠니?\""]], [["No.", "없습니다."], ["And yet—", "그런데도―"]], [["My guardian looked at me, waiting for what I had to say.", "후견인은 내가 무슨 말을 할지 기다리며 나를 바라보았습니다."]], [["I had nothing to say.", "나는 할 말이 없었습니다."], ["At least I had nothing in my mind that I could say.", "적어도 입 밖에 낼 만한 말은 마음속에 없었습니다."], ["I had an undefined impression that it might have been better if we had had some other inmate, but I could hardly have explained why even to myself.", "다른 누군가가 함께 지냈다면 더 나았을지도 모른다는 어렴풋한 느낌이 들긴 했지만, 그 까닭은 나 자신에게조차 도무지 설명할 수 없었습니다."], ["Or, if to myself, certainly not to anybody else.", "설령 나 자신에게는 설명할 수 있다 해도, 다른 누구에게는 분명 그러지 못했을 것입니다."]], [["“You see,” said my guardian, “our neighbourhood is in Woodcourt’s way, and he can come here to see her as often as he likes, which is agreeable to them both; and she is familiar to us and fond of you.”", "\"보렴.\" 후견인이 말했습니다. \"우리 동네는 우드코트가 다니는 길목이니, 그 사람이 마음 내킬 때마다 어머니를 보러 여기 올 수 있어. 두 사람한테 다 반가운 일이지. 게다가 부인은 우리한테 친숙하고 너를 아주 좋아하잖니.\""]], [["Yes. That was undeniable.", "그렇습니다."], ["I had nothing to say against it.", "부정할 수 없는 일이었습니다."], ["I could not have suggested a better arrangement, but I was not quite easy in my mind.", "나는 거기에 반대할 말이 없었습니다. 더 나은 방도를 내놓을 수도 없었지만, 마음이 영 편치는 않았습니다."], ["Esther, Esther, why not?", "에스더, 에스더, 왜 그럴까?"], ["Esther, think!", "에스더, 생각해봐!"]], [["“It is a very good plan indeed, dear guardian, and we could not do better.”", "\"정말 아주 좋은 계획이에요, 후견인. 이보다 나을 수는 없겠어요.\""]], [["“Sure, little woman?”", "\"정말이지, 꼬마 아가씨?\""]], [["Quite sure.", "정말입니다."], ["I had had a moment’s time to think, since I had urged that duty on myself, and I was quite sure.", "나 자신에게 그 도리를 다그친 뒤로 잠깐 생각할 틈이 있었으니, 나는 정말로 확신했습니다."]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“It shall be done.", "\"그렇게 하자."], ["Carried unanimously.”", "만장일치로 가결.\""]], [["“Carried unanimously,”", "\"만장일치로 가결.\""], ["I repeated, going on with my work.", "나는 다시 일감을 손에 들며 그렇게 되뇌었습니다."]], [["It was a cover for his book-table that I happened to be ornamenting.", "마침 내가 수를 놓고 있던 것은 그의 책상에 덮을 덮개였습니다."], ["It had been laid by on the night preceding my sad journey and never resumed.", "슬픈 여행을 떠나기 전날 밤에 한쪽에 치워두었다가 다시 손대지 못했던 것이었지요."], ["I showed it to him now, and he admired it highly.", "나는 이제 그것을 그에게 보여주었고, 그는 무척 감탄했습니다."], ["After I had explained the pattern to him and all the great effects that were to come out by and by, I thought I would go back to our last theme.", "무늬를 설명하고 차차 드러날 멋진 효과를 죄다 일러준 다음, 나는 우리가 나누던 마지막 화제로 되돌아가기로 했습니다."]], [["“You said, dear guardian, when we spoke of Mr. Woodcourt before Ada left us, that you thought he would give a long trial to another country.", "\"에이다가 우리를 떠나기 전에 우드코트 씨 이야기를 하실 때, 그분이 다른 나라에서 오래 시험 삼아 일해볼 거라 생각하신다고 하셨잖아요."], ["Have you been advising him since?”", "그 뒤로 그분께 조언을 해주셨나요?\""]], [["“Yes, little woman, pretty often.”", "\"그럼, 꼬마 아가씨, 꽤 자주.\""]], [["“Has he decided to do so?”", "\"그분이 그렇게 하기로 정하셨대요?\""]], [["“I rather think not.”", "\"아닌 것 같구나.\""]], [["“Some other prospect has opened to him, perhaps?”", "\"어쩌면 다른 길이 열린 건가요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“Why—yes—perhaps,”", "\"글쎄―그래―어쩌면.\""], ["returned my guardian, beginning his answer in a very deliberate manner.", "후견인이 아주 신중한 투로 대답을 시작했습니다."], ["“About half a year hence or so, there is a medical attendant for the poor to be appointed at a certain place in Yorkshire.", "\"지금부터 반년쯤 뒤에, 요크셔의 어느 곳에 빈민을 돌볼 의료 담당자를 한 사람 두기로 되어 있어."], ["It is a thriving place, pleasantly situated—streams and streets, town and country, mill and moor—and seems to present an opening for such a man.", "번창하는 고장에다 자리도 쾌적하지―시내를 가르는 물줄기와 거리들, 읍내와 들판, 방앗간과 황야가 있는 곳이야. 그런 사람한테 길이 열리는 자리인 듯싶더구나."], ["I mean a man whose hopes and aims may sometimes lie (as most men’s sometimes do, I dare say) above the ordinary level, but to whom the ordinary level will be high enough after all if it should prove to be a way of usefulness and good service leading to no other.", "내가 말하는 사람이란, 그 희망과 목표가 더러는 (대개 사람들이 그렇듯이 말이야) 보통 수준을 넘어서지만, 그것이 다른 데로 이어지지 않더라도 유익하고 좋은 봉사의 길로 드러나기만 한다면 그 보통 수준만으로도 충분히 높다고 여길 사람을 말해."], ["All generous spirits are ambitious, I suppose, but the ambition that calmly trusts itself to such a road, instead of spasmodically trying to fly over it, is of the kind I care for.", "너그러운 영혼은 다 야심이 있게 마련이지, 아마. 하지만 그 위로 발작하듯 날아오르려 애쓰는 대신, 그런 길에 차분히 자기를 맡기는 야심―그게 바로 내가 아끼는 종류야."], ["It is Woodcourt’s kind.”", "그게 우드코트 같은 사람의 야심이지.\""]], [["“And will he get this appointment?”", "\"그분이 그 자리를 얻으실까요?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“Why, little woman,”", "\"글쎄다, 꼬마 아가씨.\""], ["returned my guardian, smiling, “not being an oracle, I cannot confidently say, but I think so.", "후견인이 미소 지으며 대답했습니다. \"내가 점쟁이가 아니니 자신 있게 말할 순 없다만, 그러리라 본다."], ["His reputation stands very high; there were people from that part of the country in the shipwreck; and strange to say, I believe the best man has the best chance.", "그 사람 평판은 아주 높아. 그 고장 사람들이 그 난파선에 타고 있었고, 이상하게 들리겠지만, 가장 훌륭한 사람한테 가장 좋은 기회가 온다고 나는 믿는단다."], ["You must not suppose it to be a fine endowment.", "그게 무슨 대단한 자리라고 생각하면 안 돼. 아주아주 평범한 일이야, 얘야."], ["It is a very, very commonplace affair, my dear, an appointment to a great amount of work and a small amount of pay; but better things will gather about it, it may be fairly hoped.”", "일은 산더미인데 보수는 쥐꼬리만 한 자리지. 하지만 거기에 더 나은 것들이 모여들 거라고, 그건 충분히 바랄 만하지.\""]], [["“The poor of that place will have reason to bless the choice if it falls on Mr. Woodcourt, guardian.”", "\"우드코트 씨가 뽑힌다면, 그 고장 빈민들은 그 선택을 두고 고마워할 까닭이 있겠네요, 후견인.\""]], [["“You are right, little woman; that I am sure they will.”", "\"네 말이 옳다, 꼬마 아가씨. 틀림없이 그럴 게야.\""]], [["We said no more about it, nor did he say a word about the future of Bleak House.", "우리는 그 이야기를 더 하지 않았고, 그도 황폐한 집의 앞날에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았습니다."], ["But it was the first time I had taken my seat at his side in my mourning dress, and that accounted for it, I considered.", "하지만 내가 상복을 입고 그의 곁에 자리를 잡은 것은 그때가 처음이었으니, 그 때문이려니 하고 나는 생각했습니다."]], [["I now began to visit my dear girl every day in the dull dark corner where she lived.", "이제 나는 날마다 내 사랑하는 아이를, 그 애가 사는 침침하고 어두운 구석으로 찾아가기 시작했습니다."], ["The morning was my usual time, but whenever I found I had an hour or so to spare, I put on my bonnet and bustled off to Chancery Lane.", "아침이 으레 가는 때였지만, 한 시간쯤 짬이 난다 싶으면 나는 보닛을 쓰고 부랴부랴 챈서리 거리로 달려갔습니다."], ["They were both so glad to see me at all hours, and used to brighten up so when they heard me opening the door and coming in (being quite at home, I never knocked), that I had no fear of becoming troublesome just yet.", "두 사람은 어느 시각에든 나를 보면 어찌나 반가워하던지, 또 내가 문을 열고 들어오는 소리가 나면(완전히 내 집처럼 드나들었기에 나는 노크라곤 하지 않았습니다) 어찌나 얼굴이 환해지던지, 나는 아직은 폐가 되지 않을까 하는 걱정이 들지 않았습니다."]], [["On these occasions I frequently found Richard absent.", "그렇게 갈 때면 나는 리처드가 자리에 없는 경우를 자주 보았습니다."], ["At other times he would be writing or reading papers in the cause at that table of his, so covered with papers, which was never disturbed.", "어떤 때는 서류로 뒤덮여 한 번도 치워진 적 없는 그 책상 앞에서 소송 서류를 쓰거나 읽고 있었습니다."], ["Sometimes I would come upon him lingering at the door of Mr. Vholes’s office.", "어떤 때는 볼스 씨 사무실 문가에서 서성이는 그를 마주치기도 했습니다."], ["Sometimes I would meet him in the neighbourhood lounging about and biting his nails.", "어떤 때는 동네에서 빈둥거리며 손톱을 물어뜯는 그와 마주치기도 했습니다."], ["I often met him wandering in Lincoln’s Inn, near the place where I had first seen him, oh how different, how different!", "또 자주, 내가 그를 처음 보았던 곳 근처, 링컨스 인을 헤매는 그와 마주치곤 했는데, 아, 어쩌면 그리도 달라졌던지, 어쩌면 그리도 달라졌던지!"]], [["That the money Ada brought him was melting away with the candles I used to see burning after dark in Mr. Vholes’s office I knew very well.", "에이다가 그에게 가져다준 돈이, 해 진 뒤 볼스 씨 사무실에서 타오르던 그 촛불과 함께 녹아 사라지고 있다는 것을 나는 잘 알았습니다."], ["It was not a large amount in the beginning, he had married in debt, and I could not fail to understand, by this time, what was meant by Mr. Vholes’s shoulder being at the wheel—as I still heard it was.", "처음부터 큰 액수도 아니었던 데다 그는 빚을 안고 결혼했으니, 볼스 씨가 어깨로 수레바퀴를 떠받친다는―여전히 내 귀에 들려오던―말이 무슨 뜻인지를 이제는 모를 수 없었습니다."], ["My dear made the best of housekeepers and tried hard to save, but I knew that they were getting poorer and poorer every day.", "내 사랑하는 아이는 더할 나위 없는 살림꾼이 되어 악착같이 아끼려 애썼지만, 그들이 날마다 점점 더 가난해진다는 것을 나는 알고 있었습니다."]], [["She shone in the miserable corner like a beautiful star.", "그 애는 그 비참한 구석에서 아름다운 별처럼 빛났습니다."], ["She adorned and graced it so that it became another place.", "그 애가 그곳을 꾸미고 빛내니 그곳은 아주 딴 데가 되었습니다."], ["Paler than she had been at home, and a little quieter than I had thought natural when she was yet so cheerful and hopeful, her face was so unshadowed that I half believed she was blinded by her love for Richard to his ruinous career.", "집에 있을 때보다 핼쑥해지고, 그토록 명랑하고 희망에 차 있을 때 자연스럽다 여겼던 것보다 조금 더 조용해지긴 했지만, 그 애의 얼굴에는 그늘이 어찌나 없던지, 나는 그 애가 리처드를 향한 사랑에 눈이 멀어 그의 파멸로 치닫는 행로를 보지 못하는 게 아닐까 하고 반쯤 믿게 되었습니다."]], [["I went one day to dine with them while I was under this impression.", "이런 인상에 사로잡혀 있던 무렵의 어느 날, 나는 그들과 저녁을 함께하러 갔습니다."], ["As I turned into Symond’s Inn, I met little Miss Flite coming out.", "사이먼즈 인으로 꺾어 드는데, 마침 나오던 자그마한 플라이트 양과 마주쳤습니다."], ["She had been to make a stately call upon the wards in Jarndyce, as she still called them, and had derived the highest gratification from that ceremony.", "그녀는 여전히 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인이라 부르는 두 사람에게 격식 갖춘 방문을 하고 오는 길이었고, 그 의식에서 더없는 만족을 얻은 모양이었습니다."], ["Ada had already told me that she called every Monday at five o’clock, with one little extra white bow in her bonnet, which never appeared there at any other time, and with her largest reticule of documents on her arm.", "에이다가 이미 일러주기를, 그녀는 월요일마다 다섯 시에 찾아오는데, 다른 때는 결코 달지 않는 작고 하얀 나비 매듭 하나를 보닛에 더 달고, 서류를 잔뜩 담은 가장 큰 손가방을 팔에 끼고 온다고 했습니다."]], [["“My dear!”", "\"아, 얘야!\""], ["she began. “So delighted!", "그녀가 입을 열었습니다."], ["How do you do!", "\"어찌나 반가운지!"], ["So glad to see you.", "안녕하세요! 만나서 정말 기뻐요."], ["And you are going to visit our interesting Jarndyce wards?", "우리 흥미로운 잔다이스 피후견인들을 만나러 가는 길이죠?"], ["TO be sure!", "그렇고말고요!"], ["Our beauty is at home, my dear, and will be charmed to see you.”", "우리 미인은 집에 있어요, 얘야, 당신을 보면 황홀해할 거예요.\""]], [["“Then Richard is not come in yet?” said I.", "\"그럼 리처드는 아직 안 들어왔나요?\""], ["“I am glad of that, for I was afraid of being a little late.”", "내가 물었습니다. \"잘됐네요, 제가 좀 늦은 게 아닐까 걱정했거든요.\""]], [["“No, he is not come in,”", "\"아뇨, 아직 안 들어왔어요.\""], ["returned Miss Flite.", "플라이트 양이 대답했습니다."], ["“He has had a long day in court.", "\"오늘 법정에서 긴 하루를 보냈답니다."], ["I left him there with Vholes.", "거기 볼스랑 같이 있는 걸 두고 왔어요."], ["You don’t like Vholes, I hope?", "당신은 볼스를 좋아하지 않으면 좋겠는데요?"], ["DON’T like Vholes.", "볼스를 좋아하지 마세요."], ["Dan-gerous man!”", "위험한 사람이에요!\""]], [["“I am afraid you see Richard oftener than ever now,”", "\"이젠 리처드를 그 어느 때보다 자주 보시겠네요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“My dearest,”", "\"내 사랑.\""], ["returned Miss Flite, “daily and hourly.", "플라이트 양이 대답했습니다. \"날마다, 시시각각이지요."], ["You know what I told you of the attraction on the Chancellor’s table?", "대법원장 책상 위의 그 끌림에 대해 내가 들려준 얘기 기억나죠?"], ["My dear, next to myself he is the most constant suitor in court.", "얘야, 나 다음으로 그 사람이 법정에서 가장 한결같은 소송인이에요."], ["He begins quite to amuse our little party.", "우리 작은 모임에 아주 즐거움을 주기 시작했답니다."], ["Ve-ry friendly little party, are we not?”", "무척 정다운 작은 모임이지요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["It was miserable to hear this from her poor mad lips, though it was no surprise.", "가엾고 미친 그 입에서 이런 말을 듣자니 비참했습니다. 비록 놀랄 일은 아니었지만요."]], [["“In short, my valued friend,”", "\"한마디로, 나의 소중한 벗.\""], ["pursued Miss Flite, advancing her lips to my ear with an air of equal patronage and mystery, “I must tell you a secret.", "플라이트 양이 후견하는 듯하면서도 은밀한 태도로 입술을 내 귀에 들이대며 말을 이었습니다. \"비밀을 하나 알려줘야겠어요."], ["I have made him my executor.", "나, 그 사람을 내 유언 집행인으로 삼았어요."], ["Nominated, constituted, and appointed him.", "지명하고, 선임하고, 임명했답니다."], ["In my will.", "내 유언장에다요."], ["Ye-es.”", "그-럼요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"정말요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Ye-es,”", "\"그-럼요.\""], ["repeated Miss Flite in her most genteel accents, “my executor, administrator, and assign.", "플라이트 양이 가장 우아한 말씨로 거듭 말했습니다. \"내 유언 집행인, 재산 관리인, 양수인으로요."], ["(Our Chancery phrases, my love.) I have reflected that if I should wear out, he will be able to watch that judgment.", "(우리 대법원 표현이랍니다, 내 사랑.) 내가 닳아 없어진다 해도 그 사람이라면 그 판결을 지켜볼 수 있겠다 싶었지요."], ["Being so very regular in his attendance.”", "출석이 그토록 한결같으니까요.\""]], [["It made me sigh to think of him.", "그를 떠올리니 한숨이 나왔습니다."]], [["“I did at one time mean,” said Miss Flite, echoing the sigh, “to nominate, constitute, and appoint poor Gridley.", "\"한때는 가엾은 그리들리를 지명하고, 선임하고, 임명할 작정이었답니다.\""], ["Also very regular, my charming girl.", "플라이트 양이 그 한숨에 화답하듯 말했습니다."], ["I assure you, most exemplary!", "\"그이도 아주 한결같았거든요, 사랑스러운 아가씨."], ["But he wore out, poor man, so I have appointed his successor.", "정말이지 더없이 모범적이었어요! 하지만 그이는 닳아 없어졌지요, 가엾은 사람."], ["Don’t mention it.", "그래서 그 후임을 임명한 거예요."], ["This is in confidence.”", "발설하지 말아요. 이건 비밀이니까요.\""]], [["She carefully opened her reticule a little way and showed me a folded piece of paper inside as the appointment of which she spoke.", "그녀는 손가방을 조심스레 조금 열어, 안에 든 접힌 종이 한 장을 자기가 말한 그 임명장이라며 내게 보여주었습니다."]], [["“Another secret, my dear.", "\"또 다른 비밀이 있어요, 얘야."], ["I have added to my collection of birds.”", "내 새 수집품을 늘렸답니다.\""]], [["“Really, Miss Flite?”", "\"정말요, 플라이트 양?\""], ["said I, knowing how it pleased her to have her confidence received with an appearance of interest.", "내가 말했습니다. 그녀의 속내를 관심 어린 기색으로 받아주는 것이 그녀를 얼마나 기쁘게 하는지 알았기에 그렇게 말한 것이지요."]], [["She nodded several times, and her face became overcast and gloomy.", "그녀는 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕였고, 얼굴은 어두워지며 침울해졌습니다. \"두 마리가 더 늘었어요."], ["“Two more. I call them the Wards in Jarndyce.", "나는 그것들을 잔다이스 사건의 피후견인이라 부른답니다."], ["They are caged up with all the others.", "다른 새들과 함께 다 가둬뒀어요."], ["With Hope, Joy, Youth, Peace, Rest, Life, Dust, Ashes, Waste, Want, Ruin, Despair, Madness, Death, Cunning, Folly, Words, Wigs, Rags, Sheepskin, Plunder, Precedent, Jargon, Gammon, and Spinach!”", "희망, 기쁨, 청춘, 평화, 안식, 생명, 먼지, 잿더미, 황폐, 궁핍, 파멸, 절망, 광기, 죽음, 교활, 어리석음, 말, 가발, 누더기, 양피지, 약탈, 판례, 횡설수설, 허튼소리, 시금치와 함께요!\""]], [["The poor soul kissed me with the most troubled look I had ever seen in her and went her way.", "가엾은 그녀는 내가 그녀에게서 본 가장 괴로운 표정으로 내게 입을 맞추고 제 갈 길을 갔습니다."], ["Her manner of running over the names of her birds, as if she were afraid of hearing them even from her own lips, quite chilled me.", "자기 새들의 이름을 죽 외워 내리는 그 모양새가, 마치 제 입에서 나오는 이름조차 듣기가 두려운 듯해서, 나는 온몸이 오싹했습니다."]], [["This was not a cheering preparation for my visit, and I could have dispensed with the company of Mr. Vholes, when Richard (who arrived within a minute or two after me) brought him to share our dinner.", "이것은 방문을 앞둔 마음을 북돋아주는 채비는 못 되었고, 리처드가(나보다 한두 분 뒤에 도착했습니다) 볼스 씨를 데려와 우리 저녁 식사를 함께하게 했을 때 나는 그의 동석을 사양했더라도 좋았을 것입니다."], ["Although it was a very plain one, Ada and Richard were for some minutes both out of the room together helping to get ready what we were to eat and drink. Mr.", "아주 소박한 식사였는데도, 에이다와 리처드는 몇 분 동안 함께 방을 비우고 우리가 먹고 마실 것을 차리는 일을 거들었습니다."], ["Vholes took that opportunity of holding a little conversation in a low voice with me.", "볼스 씨는 그 틈을 타 나직한 목소리로 나와 잠시 이야기를 나누었습니다."], ["He came to the window where I was sitting and began upon Symond’s Inn.", "그는 내가 앉아 있던 창가로 다가와 사이먼즈 인 이야기부터 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“A dull place, Miss Summerson, for a life that is not an official one,”", "\"공무에 매인 인생이 아니라면 따분한 곳이지요, 서머슨 양.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes, smearing the glass with his black glove to make it clearer for me.", "볼스 씨가 검은 장갑으로 유리를 문질러 나를 위해 더 맑게 닦아주며 말했습니다."]], [["“There is not much to see here,”", "\"여기는 볼 게 별로 없네요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Nor to hear, miss,”", "\"들을 것도 없지요, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“A little music does occasionally stray in, but we are not musical in the law and soon eject it.", "\"이따금 음악이 좀 흘러들긴 합니다만, 우리 법조계는 음악과는 거리가 멀어서 금세 내쫓아버린답니다."], ["I hope Mr. Jarndyce is as well as his friends could wish him?”", "잔다이스 씨가 친구분들이 바라는 만큼 건강하시길 빕니다만?\""]], [["I thanked Mr. Vholes and said he was quite well.", "나는 볼스 씨에게 고맙다고 하며 그분은 아주 건강하시다고 했습니다."]], [["“I have not the pleasure to be admitted among the number of his friends myself,” said Mr.", "\"나 자신은 그분의 벗들 축에 끼는 영광을 누리지 못합니다.\""], ["Vholes, “and I am aware that the gentlemen of our profession are sometimes regarded in such quarters with an unfavourable eye.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다. \"그리고 우리 업계 사람들이 그런 자리에서는 더러 곱지 않은 눈길로 비치곤 한다는 것도 압니다."], ["Our plain course, however, under good report and evil report, and all kinds of prejudice (we are the victims of prejudice), is to have everything openly carried on. How do you find Mr.", "그렇지만 좋은 평이든 나쁜 평이든, 온갖 편견 아래서든(우리는 편견의 희생자랍니다), 우리가 갈 길은 모든 일을 떳떳이 처리하는 것이지요."], ["C. looking, Miss Summerson?”", "시(C) 씨는 안색이 어떻습디까, 서머슨 양?\""]], [["“He looks very ill.", "\"무척 안 좋아 보여요."], ["Dreadfully anxious.”", "끔찍하게 초조해하시고요.\""]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["He stood behind me with his long black figure reaching nearly to the ceiling of those low rooms, feeling the pimples on his face as if they were ornaments and speaking inwardly and evenly as though there were not a human passion or emotion in his nature.", "그는 그 길고 검은 형상을 그 낮은 방의 천장에 닿을 듯 세운 채 내 뒤에 서서, 제 얼굴의 여드름을 무슨 장식이라도 되는 양 만지작거리며, 천성에 인간다운 정념이나 감정이라곤 하나도 없는 듯 속으로 가라앉은 평탄한 목소리로 말했습니다."]], [["“Mr. Woodcourt is in attendance upon Mr.", "\"우드코트 씨가 시 씨를 돌보고 계신 줄로 압니다만?\""], ["C., I believe?” he resumed.", "그가 다시 말했습니다."]], [["“Mr. Woodcourt is his disinterested friend,”", "\"우드코트 씨는 그분의 사심 없는 벗이에요.\""], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했습니다."]], [["“But I mean in professional attendance, medical attendance.”", "\"내 말은 직업상의 돌봄, 의료상의 돌봄을 뜻한 거랍니다.\""]], [["“That can do little for an unhappy mind,”", "\"그것이 불행한 마음에는 별 도움이 못 되지요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["So slow, so eager, so bloodless and gaunt, I felt as if Richard were wasting away beneath the eyes of this adviser and there were something of the vampire in him.", "어찌나 굼뜨고, 어찌나 게걸스럽고, 어찌나 피 한 방울 없이 수척한지, 나는 리처드가 이 조언자의 눈길 아래서 시들어가고 있고, 그에게는 흡혈귀 같은 데가 있다는 느낌이 들었습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes, very slowly rubbing his gloved hands, as if, to his cold sense of touch, they were much the same in black kid or out of it, “this was an ill-advised marriage of Mr.", "볼스 씨가, 자기의 무딘 촉감으로는 검은 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼었든 벗었든 마찬가지라는 듯, 장갑 낀 두 손을 아주 천천히 비비며 말했습니다."], ["C.’s.”", "\"이건 시 씨에게 잘못 권해진 결혼이었습니다.\""]], [["I begged he would excuse me from discussing it.", "나는 그 이야기는 논하지 않게 해달라고 청했습니다."], ["They had been engaged when they were both very young, I told him (a little indignantly) and when the prospect before them was much fairer and brighter.", "두 사람은 아주 어렸을 때 약혼했고(나는 조금 발끈하며 그에게 말했습니다), 그때는 앞날이 훨씬 더 곱고 환했노라고요."], ["When Richard had not yielded himself to the unhappy influence which now darkened his life.", "리처드가, 지금은 그의 인생을 어둡게 하는 그 불행한 영향에 자신을 내맡기기 전이었노라고요."]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["assented Mr. Vholes again.", "볼스 씨가 다시 맞장구쳤습니다."], ["“Still, with a view to everything being openly carried on, I will, with your permission, Miss Summerson, observe to you that I consider this a very ill-advised marriage indeed. I owe the opinion not only to Mr.", "\"그래도 모든 일을 떳떳이 처리한다는 견지에서, 허락하신다면, 서머슨 양, 나는 이것이 정말이지 매우 잘못 권해진 결혼이라 본다는 점을 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["C.’s connexions, against whom I should naturally wish to protect myself, but also to my own reputation—dear to myself as a professional man aiming to keep respectable; dear to my three girls at home, for whom I am striving to realize some little independence; dear, I will even say, to my aged father, whom it is my privilege to support.”", "이 의견은 내가 마땅히 나 자신을 지켜야 할 시 씨의 친척들에게 빚지고 있을 뿐 아니라, 내 자신의 평판에도 빚지고 있지요. 그것은 점잖음을 지키려 애쓰는 업계 사람으로서 나에게 소중하고, 내가 약간의 자립을 마련해주려 분투하고 있는 집안의 세 딸에게 소중하며, 감히 말하건대, 내가 부양하는 영예를 누리는 늙으신 아버지에게도 소중합니다.\""]], [["“It would become a very different marriage, a much happier and better marriage, another marriage altogether, Mr.", "\"그건 아주 다른 결혼, 훨씬 더 행복하고 더 나은 결혼, 완전히 딴 결혼이 될 거예요, 볼스 씨.\" 내가 말했습니다."], ["Vholes,” said I, “if Richard were persuaded to turn his back on the fatal pursuit in which you are engaged with him.”", "\"만약 리처드가 당신이 그와 함께 매달려 있는 그 치명적인 추구에 등을 돌리도록 설득될 수만 있다면요.\""]], [["Mr. Vholes, with a noiseless cough—or rather gasp—into one of his black gloves, inclined his head as if he did not wholly dispute even that.", "볼스 씨는 검은 장갑 한쪽에 대고 소리 없는 기침을―아니, 헐떡임을―한 뒤, 그것조차 전적으로 부정하지는 않는다는 듯 고개를 숙였습니다."]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양.\""], ["he said, “it may be so; and I freely admit that the young lady who has taken Mr.", "그가 말했습니다. \"그럴지도 모르지요."], ["C.’s name upon herself in so ill-advised a manner—you will I am sure not quarrel with me for throwing out that remark again, as a duty I owe to Mr. C.’s connexions—is a highly genteel young lady.", "그리고 시 씨의 이름을 그토록 잘못 권해진 방식으로 짊어진 그 젊은 아가씨가―시 씨의 친척들에게 내가 마땅히 다해야 할 도리로서 그 말을 다시 꺼내더라도 당신은 분명 나를 탓하지 않으시겠지요―더없이 품위 있는 젊은 아가씨라는 점은 나도 선뜻 인정합니다."], ["Business has prevented me from mixing much with general society in any but a professional character; still I trust I am competent to perceive that she is a highly genteel young lady.", "업무 탓에 나는 직업상의 성격으로가 아니고는 일반 사교계에 많이 어울려본 적이 없습니다만, 그래도 그분이 더없이 품위 있는 젊은 아가씨라는 것쯤은 알아볼 능력이 있다고 믿습니다."], ["As to beauty, I am not a judge of that myself, and I never did give much attention to it from a boy, but I dare say the young lady is equally eligible in that point of view.", "미모로 말하자면 나 자신은 그것을 가늠하는 사람이 못 되고, 어릴 적부터 거기엔 별로 마음을 쓴 적이 없습니다만, 그분이 그 점에서도 못지않게 합당하리라 짐작은 합니다."], ["She is considered so (I have heard) among the clerks in the Inn, and it is a point more in their way than in mine.", "인(Inn)의 서기들 사이에서도 그렇게 여긴다(고 들었습니다)는데, 그건 나보다 그들 쪽에 더 어울리는 사안이지요."], ["In reference to Mr. C.’s pursuit of his interests—”", "시 씨가 자기 이익을 추구하는 것에 관해서는―\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["His interests, Mr. Vholes!”", "그분의 이익이라뇨, 볼스 씨!\""]], [["“Pardon me,” returned Mr.", "\"실례합니다.\""], ["Vholes, going on in exactly the same inward and dispassionate manner. “Mr.", "볼스 씨가 똑같이 속으로 가라앉은 무덤덤한 투를 그대로 이어가며 대꾸했습니다."], ["C. takes certain interests under certain wills disputed in the suit.", "\"시 씨는 이 소송에서 다투어지는 어떤 유언장들에 따라 어떤 이익을 취하지요."], ["It is a term we use.", "우리가 쓰는 용어랍니다."], ["In reference to Mr. C,’s pursuit of his interests, I mentioned to you, Miss Summerson, the first time I had the pleasure of seeing you, in my desire that everything should be openly carried on—I used those words, for I happened afterwards to note them in my diary, which is producible at any time—I mentioned to you that Mr.", "시 씨가 자기 이익을 추구하는 것에 관해서는, 서머슨 양, 내가 당신을 뵙는 기쁨을 처음 누렸을 때 말씀드린 바 있지요."], ["C. had laid down the principle of watching his own interests, and that when a client of mine laid down a principle which was not of an immoral (that is to say, unlawful) nature, it devolved upon me to carry it out.", "모든 일을 떳떳이 처리하고 싶다는 내 바람에서요―나는 바로 그 말을 썼는데, 마침 그 뒤에 그것을 내 일기에 적어두었고 그 일기는 언제든 제시할 수 있습니다―시 씨가 자기 이익을 지켜보겠다는 원칙을 세웠고, 내 의뢰인이 부도덕한(다시 말해 불법적인) 성질이 아닌 원칙을 세웠을 때 그것을 실행하는 것은 나에게 맡겨진 일이라고 말씀드렸지요."], ["I HAVE carried it out; I do carry it out.", "나는 그것을 실행해왔고, 지금도 실행하고 있습니다."], ["But I will not smooth things over to any connexion of Mr. C.’s on any account.", "그러나 나는 어떤 경우에도 시 씨의 어느 친척에게도 일을 얼버무리지 않을 것입니다."], ["As open as I was to Mr. Jarndyce, I am to you.", "잔다이스 씨에게 떳떳했던 것처럼, 당신에게도 그렇습니다."], ["I regard it in the light of a professional duty to be so, though it can be charged to no one.", "그렇게 하는 것을 나는 직업상의 도리로 여깁니다."], ["I openly say, unpalatable as it may be, that I consider Mr.", "누구에게 청구할 수 있는 일은 아니지만요."], ["C.’s affairs in a very bad way, that I consider Mr. C.", "듣기에 거북할지라도 나는 떳떳이 말합니다."], ["himself in a very bad way, and that I regard this as an exceedingly ill-advised marriage.", "나는 시 씨의 일이 매우 나쁜 형국에 있다고 보고, 시 씨 자신도 매우 나쁜 처지에 있다고 보며, 이것을 지극히 잘못 권해진 결혼이라 여긴다고요. 제가 여기 있나요, 선생?"], ["Am I here, sir?", "예, 고맙습니다."], ["Yes, I thank you; I am here, Mr.", "여기 있습니다, 시 씨."], ["C., and enjoying the pleasure of some agreeable conversation with Miss Summerson, for which I have to thank you very much, sir!”", "서머슨 양과 유쾌한 담소를 나누는 기쁨을 누리고 있었지요. 그 점은 당신께 매우 감사드려야겠습니다, 선생!\""]], [["He broke off thus in answer to Richard, who addressed him as he came into the room.", "그는 방으로 들어서며 말을 건넨 리처드에게 대답하느라 이렇게 말을 끊었습니다."], ["By this time I too well understood Mr. Vholes’s scrupulous way of saving himself and his respectability not to feel that our worst fears did but keep pace with his client’s progress.", "이때쯤 나는 볼스 씨가 자기 자신과 자기 점잖음을 지키는 그 빈틈없는 방식을 너무도 잘 알고 있었기에, 우리의 가장 어두운 두려움이 그 의뢰인의 진행과 꼭 발맞추어 나아가고 있을 뿐이라는 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었습니다."]], [["We sat down to dinner, and I had an opportunity of observing Richard, anxiously.", "우리는 저녁상에 둘러앉았고, 나는 리처드를 근심스레 살필 기회를 얻었습니다."], ["I was not disturbed by Mr. Vholes (who took off his gloves to dine), though he sat opposite to me at the small table, for I doubt if, looking up at all, he once removed his eyes from his host’s face.", "볼스 씨가(식사하느라 장갑을 벗었습니다) 그 작은 식탁에서 내 맞은편에 앉았는데도 나는 방해받지 않았으니, 그가 고개를 들기는 했더라도 주인의 얼굴에서 눈을 한 번이라도 뗐을지 의심스러웠기 때문입니다."], ["I found Richard thin and languid, slovenly in his dress, abstracted in his manner, forcing his spirits now and then, and at other intervals relapsing into a dull thoughtfulness.", "리처드는 여위고 기운이 없어 보였고, 옷차림은 단정치 못했으며, 태도는 멍하니 딴 데 가 있었고, 이따금 억지로 기운을 짜내다가도 또 어느 틈엔가 둔중한 골몰 속으로 다시 가라앉았습니다."], ["About his large bright eyes that used to be so merry there was a wanness and a restlessness that changed them altogether.", "그토록 즐겁던 그 크고 빛나던 두 눈가에는, 그 눈을 아주 딴판으로 바꿔놓은 핏기 없음과 안절부절이 어려 있었습니다."], ["I cannot use the expression that he looked old.", "늙어 보였다는 표현은 쓸 수가 없습니다."], ["There is a ruin of youth which is not like age, and into such a ruin Richard’s youth and youthful beauty had all fallen away.", "노년과는 다른 청춘의 폐허라는 것이 있는데, 리처드의 청춘과 젊은 아름다움은 죄다 바로 그런 폐허로 무너져 내렸던 것입니다."]], [["He ate little and seemed indifferent what it was, showed himself to be much more impatient than he used to be, and was quick even with Ada.", "그는 거의 먹지 않았고 무엇을 먹든 무심해 보였으며, 예전보다 훨씬 더 참을성이 없었고, 에이다에게마저 퉁명스러웠습니다."], ["I thought at first that his old light-hearted manner was all gone, but it shone out of him sometimes as I had occasionally known little momentary glimpses of my own old face to look out upon me from the glass.", "처음에는 그 옛날의 명랑한 태도가 죄다 사라진 줄 알았지만, 그것은 이따금씩 그에게서 환히 비쳐 나왔으니, 마치 거울 속에서 내 옛 얼굴이 잠깐잠깐 나를 내다보던 것을 더러 보았던 것처럼요."], ["His laugh had not quite left him either, but it was like the echo of a joyful sound, and that is always sorrowful.", "그의 웃음도 아주 떠나버린 것은 아니었지만, 그것은 기쁜 소리의 메아리 같았고, 그런 것은 언제나 서글픈 법입니다."]], [["Yet he was as glad as ever, in his old affectionate way, to have me there, and we talked of the old times pleasantly.", "그래도 그는 변함없이 기뻐했습니다. 그 옛날의 다정한 투로 내가 거기 있는 것을 반가워했고, 우리는 옛 시절 이야기를 즐겁게 나누었습니다."], ["These did not appear to be interesting to Mr. Vholes, though he occasionally made a gasp which I believe was his smile.", "이런 이야기는 볼스 씨에게는 흥미롭지 않은 듯했으나, 그래도 그는 이따금 헐떡임을 내뱉었는데 그게 그의 미소였던 모양입니다."], ["He rose shortly after dinner and said that with the permission of the ladies he would retire to his office.", "그는 저녁을 먹은 뒤 이내 일어서더니, 숙녀분들이 허락하신다면 자기 사무실로 물러가겠다고 했습니다."]], [["“Always devoted to business, Vholes!”", "\"늘 업무에 헌신이로군, 볼스!\""], ["cried Richard.", "리처드가 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Yes, Mr. C.,”", "\"그렇습니다, 시 씨.\""], ["he returned, “the interests of clients are never to be neglected, sir.", "그가 대답했습니다. \"의뢰인의 이익은 결코 소홀히 해선 안 되지요, 선생."], ["They are paramount in the thoughts of a professional man like myself, who wishes to preserve a good name among his fellow-practitioners and society at large.", "동업자들과 사회 전반에서 좋은 이름을 지키고자 하는, 나 같은 업계 사람의 머릿속에서는 그것이 가장 우선이랍니다."], ["My denying myself the pleasure of the present agreeable conversation may not be wholly irrespective of your own interests, Mr. C.”", "지금 이 유쾌한 담소의 기쁨을 스스로 마다하는 것이, 당신 자신의 이익과 전혀 무관하지만은 않을지도 모르지요, 시 씨.\""]], [["Richard expressed himself quite sure of that and lighted Mr. Vholes out.", "리처드는 그 점은 충분히 확신한다고 말하며 볼스 씨를 등불로 비추어 배웅했습니다."], ["On his return he told us, more than once, that Vholes was a good fellow, a safe fellow, a man who did what he pretended to do, a very good fellow indeed!", "돌아와서는 볼스가 좋은 친구라고, 미덥다고, 하겠다고 한 일은 해내는 사람이라고, 정말이지 아주 좋은 친구라고 우리에게 거듭 말했습니다."], ["He was so defiant about it that it struck me he had begun to doubt Mr. Vholes.", "그가 어찌나 도전적으로 그러던지, 나는 그가 볼스 씨를 의심하기 시작한 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들었습니다."]], [["Then he threw himself on the sofa, tired out; and Ada and I put things to rights, for they had no other servant than the woman who attended to the chambers.", "이윽고 그는 녹초가 되어 소파에 몸을 던졌습니다. 에이다와 나는 자질구레한 것들을 정리했는데, 그들에게는 방을 돌봐주는 여자 말고 다른 하인이 없었기 때문입니다."], ["My dear girl had a cottage piano there and quietly sat down to sing some of Richard’s favourites, the lamp being first removed into the next room, as he complained of its hurting his eyes.", "내 사랑하는 아이는 그곳에 작은 코티지 피아노를 두고 있었는데, 조용히 앉아 리처드가 좋아하는 곡 몇 가지를 부르려 했습니다. 그가 등불이 눈을 아프게 한다고 투덜대서, 먼저 등불을 옆방으로 옮겨놓고요."]], [["I sat between them, at my dear girl’s side, and felt very melancholy listening to her sweet voice.", "나는 두 사람 사이, 내 사랑하는 아이 곁에 앉아, 그 애의 고운 목소리를 들으며 무척 우울해졌습니다."], ["I think Richard did too; I think he darkened the room for that reason.", "리처드도 그랬으리라 생각합니다. 그 때문에 그가 방을 어둡게 한 것이라 생각합니다."], ["She had been singing some time, rising between whiles to bend over him and speak to him, when Mr. Woodcourt came in.", "그 애는 한동안 노래를 부르다가도 틈틈이 일어서서 그에게 몸을 숙여 말을 건넸는데, 그러던 차에 우드코트 씨가 들어왔습니다."], ["Then he sat down by Richard and half playfully, half earnestly, quite naturally and easily, found out how he felt and where he had been all day.", "그는 리처드 곁에 앉아, 반은 장난스럽게 반은 진지하게, 아주 자연스럽고 편안한 태도로, 그의 기분이 어떤지, 하루 종일 어디 있었는지를 알아냈습니다."], ["Presently he proposed to accompany him in a short walk on one of the bridges, as it was a moonlight airy night; and Richard readily consenting, they went out together.", "이내 그는 달빛 환하고 바람 시원한 밤이니 다리 한 곳을 잠깐 함께 거닐자고 제안했고, 리처드가 선뜻 응하여 둘은 함께 나갔습니다."]], [["They left my dear girl still sitting at the piano and me still sitting beside her.", "그들은 여전히 피아노 앞에 앉은 내 사랑하는 아이와, 여전히 그 곁에 앉은 나를 남겨두고 떠났습니다."], ["When they were gone out, I drew my arm round her waist.", "그들이 나가자 나는 그 애의 허리에 팔을 둘렀습니다."], ["She put her left hand in mine (I was sitting on that side), but kept her right upon the keys, going over and over them without striking any note.", "그 애는 왼손을 내 손에 얹었지만(나는 그쪽에 앉아 있었습니다) 오른손은 건반 위에 둔 채, 어떤 음도 누르지 않으면서 그 위를 자꾸자꾸 더듬었습니다."]], [["“Esther, my dearest,”", "\"에스더, 내 사랑.\""], ["she said, breaking silence, “Richard is never so well and I am never so easy about him as when he is with Allan Woodcourt.", "그 애가 침묵을 깨고 말했습니다. \"리처드가 가장 편안할 때도, 내 마음이 그이를 두고 가장 놓일 때도, 다 그이가 앨런 우드코트와 함께 있을 때야."], ["We have to thank you for that.”", "그건 다 너한테 고마운 일이지.\""]], [["I pointed out to my darling how this could scarcely be, because Mr.", "나는 사랑하는 아이에게 그럴 리가 없다고 일러주었습니다."], ["Woodcourt had come to her cousin John’s house and had known us all there, and because he had always liked Richard, and Richard had always liked him, and—and so forth.", "우드코트 씨는 그 애의 사촌 존의 집에 왔던 분이고 거기서 우리 모두를 알게 되었으며, 늘 리처드를 좋아했고 리처드도 늘 그를 좋아했으니―그러니 어쩌고저쩌고 하면서요."]], [["“All true,”", "\"다 맞는 말이야.\""], ["said Ada, “but that he is such a devoted friend to us we owe to you.”", "에이다가 말했습니다. \"하지만 그이가 우리한테 그토록 헌신적인 벗인 건, 다 너 덕분이야.\""]], [["I thought it best to let my dear girl have her way and to say no more about it.", "나는 사랑하는 아이가 자기 뜻대로 하게 두고 더는 그 이야기를 하지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했습니다."], ["So I said as much.", "그래서 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["I said it lightly, because I felt her trembling.", "가볍게 말했습니다. 그 애가 떨고 있는 것을 느꼈기 때문입니다."]], [["“Esther, my dearest, I want to be a good wife, a very, very good wife indeed.", "\"에스더, 내 사랑. 나는 좋은 아내가 되고 싶어, 정말이지 아주아주 좋은 아내가."], ["You shall teach me.”", "네가 가르쳐줘야 해.\""]], [["I teach!", "내가 가르치다니!"], ["I said no more, for I noticed the hand that was fluttering over the keys, and I knew that it was not I who ought to speak, that it was she who had something to say to me.", "나는 더 말하지 않았습니다. 건반 위에서 파닥이는 그 손을 보았고, 말해야 할 사람은 내가 아니라, 나에게 할 말이 있는 사람은 그 애라는 것을 알았기 때문입니다."]], [["“When I married Richard I was not insensible to what was before him.", "\"내가 리처드와 결혼할 때, 그이 앞에 놓인 일을 몰랐던 건 아니야."], ["I had been perfectly happy for a long time with you, and I had never known any trouble or anxiety, so loved and cared for, but I understood the danger he was in, dear Esther.”", "너랑 함께 오랫동안 더없이 행복했고, 그토록 사랑받고 보살핌을 받으며 근심이나 걱정이라곤 모르고 지냈지만, 그래도 나는 그이가 처한 위험을 알고 있었어, 에스더.\""]], [["“I know, I know, my darling.”", "\"알아, 알아, 내 사랑.\""]], [["“When we were married I had some little hope that I might be able to convince him of his mistake, that he might come to regard it in a new way as my husband and not pursue it all the more desperately for my sake—as he does.", "\"우리가 결혼했을 때, 나는 그이의 잘못을 깨우쳐줄 수 있을지도 모른다고, 그이가 내 남편으로서 그 일을 새로운 눈으로 보게 될지도, 그래서 나를 위해 그 일을 더 필사적으로 좇지 않게 될지도 모른다고―지금 그러는 것과는 달리―작은 희망을 품었어."], ["But if I had not had that hope, I would have married him just the same, Esther.", "하지만 그런 희망이 없었더라도, 나는 똑같이 그이와 결혼했을 거야, 에스더."], ["Just the same!”", "똑같이!\""]], [["In the momentary firmness of the hand that was never still—a firmness inspired by the utterance of these last words, and dying away with them—I saw the confirmation of her earnest tones.", "한시도 가만있지 않던 그 손에 잠깐 깃든 단호함―이 마지막 말을 내뱉음으로써 솟아났다가 그 말과 함께 스러진 단호함―속에서, 나는 그 애의 간절한 어조가 진심임을 확인했습니다."]], [["“You are not to think, my dearest Esther, that I fail to see what you see and fear what you fear.", "\"내가 보는 것을 내가 못 본다거나, 네가 두려워하는 것을 내가 안 두려워한다고는 생각하지 마, 내 사랑 에스더."], ["No one can understand him better than I do.", "누구도 나만큼 그이를 잘 이해할 순 없어."], ["The greatest wisdom that ever lived in the world could scarcely know Richard better than my love does.”", "세상에 살았던 가장 위대한 지혜라 해도, 내 사랑이 아는 만큼 리처드를 잘 알기는 어려울 거야.\""]], [["She spoke so modestly and softly and her trembling hand expressed such agitation as it moved to and fro upon the silent notes!", "그 애는 어찌나 겸손하고 부드럽게 말하던지, 또 그 떨리는 손은 소리 없는 음 위를 오가며 어찌나 큰 동요를 드러내던지!"], ["My dear, dear girl!", "내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아이!"]], [["“I see him at his worst every day.", "\"나는 날마다 그이의 가장 나쁜 모습을 봐."], ["I watch him in his sleep.", "잠든 그이를 지켜보지."], ["I know every change of his face.", "그이 얼굴의 변화를 하나하나 다 알아."], ["But when I married Richard I was quite determined, Esther, if heaven would help me, never to show him that I grieved for what he did and so to make him more unhappy.", "하지만 리처드와 결혼할 때 나는 굳게 다짐했어, 에스더, 하늘이 도와주신다면, 그이가 한 일을 두고 내가 슬퍼한다는 걸 결코 그이한테 보이지 않겠다고, 그래서 그이를 더 불행하게 만들지 않겠다고."], ["I want him, when he comes home, to find no trouble in my face.", "나는 그이가 집에 돌아왔을 때 내 얼굴에서 어떤 근심도 보지 않길 바라."], ["I want him, when he looks at me, to see what he loved in me.", "나를 볼 때, 그이가 내게서 사랑했던 것을 보길 바라."], ["I married him to do this, and this supports me.”", "나는 이걸 하려고 그이와 결혼한 거야, 그리고 이게 나를 버티게 해.\""]], [["I felt her trembling more.", "나는 그 애가 더 떠는 것을 느꼈습니다."], ["I waited for what was yet to come, and I now thought I began to know what it was.", "나는 아직 나올 말을 기다렸고, 이제 그것이 무엇인지 알아차리기 시작한 듯했습니다."]], [["“And something else supports me, Esther.”", "\"그리고 또 한 가지가 나를 버티게 해, 에스더.\""]], [["She stopped a minute.", "그 애는 잠시 멈췄습니다."], ["Stopped speaking only; her hand was still in motion.", "말만 멈췄을 뿐, 손은 여전히 움직이고 있었습니다."]], [["“I look forward a little while, and I don’t know what great aid may come to me.", "\"나는 조금 앞을 내다보는데, 어떤 커다란 도움이 내게 올지 모르겠어."], ["When Richard turns his eyes upon me then, there may be something lying on my breast more eloquent than I have been, with greater power than mine to show him his true course and win him back.”", "그때 리처드가 나에게 눈길을 돌리면, 내 가슴에 무언가가 안겨 있을지도 몰라. 지금껏 나보다 더 잘 말하는, 나보다 더 큰 힘으로 그이에게 참된 길을 보여주고 그이를 되돌려놓을 무언가가.\""]], [["Her hand stopped now.", "이제 그 애의 손이 멈췄습니다."], ["She clasped me in her arms, and I clasped her in mine.", "그 애가 두 팔로 나를 끌어안았고, 나도 두 팔로 그 애를 끌어안았습니다."]], [["“If that little creature should fail too, Esther, I still look forward.", "\"그 작은 생명마저 안 된다면, 에스더, 그래도 나는 앞을 내다봐."], ["I look forward a long while, through years and years, and think that then, when I am growing old, or when I am dead perhaps, a beautiful woman, his daughter, happily married, may be proud of him and a blessing to him.", "아주 먼 앞을, 여러 해, 또 여러 해 너머를 내다보며 생각해. 그때, 내가 늙어갈 무렵, 어쩌면 내가 죽은 뒤에, 아름다운 여인이, 그이의 딸이, 행복하게 시집가서 그이를 자랑스러워하고 그이에게 축복이 되어줄지도 모른다고."], ["Or that a generous brave man, as handsome as he used to be, as hopeful, and far more happy, may walk in the sunshine with him, honouring his grey head and saying to himself, ‘I thank God this is my father!", "아니면 너그럽고 용감한 남자가, 그이가 옛날에 그랬듯 잘생기고, 희망에 차 있고, 그이보다 훨씬 더 행복한 그런 남자가, 그이와 함께 햇살 속을 거닐며 그이의 센 머리를 공경하고 속으로 이렇게 말할지도 모른다고. '하느님 감사합니다, 이분이 제 아버지입니다!"], ["Ruined by a fatal inheritance, and restored through me!’”", "치명적인 유산으로 무너졌다가, 저를 통해 회복되셨지요!'\""]], [["Oh, my sweet girl, what a heart was that which beat so fast against me!", "오, 나의 다정한 아이여, 내게 그토록 빠르게 뛰던 그 가슴은 어떤 가슴이었던가요!"]], [["“These hopes uphold me, my dear Esther, and I know they will.", "\"이런 희망들이 나를 떠받쳐, 사랑하는 에스더, 그리고 앞으로도 그러리란 걸 알아."], ["Though sometimes even they depart from me before a dread that arises when I look at Richard.”", "비록 가끔은 그 희망들마저, 리처드를 바라볼 때 솟아나는 어떤 두려움 앞에서는 나를 떠나버리지만.\""]], [["I tried to cheer my darling, and asked her what it was.", "나는 사랑하는 아이를 달래보려 그것이 무엇이냐고 물었습니다. 그 애는 흐느끼고 울먹이며 대답했습니다."], ["Sobbing and weeping, she replied, “That he may not live to see his child.” A Discovery", "\"그이가 자기 아이를 보지 못하고 죽을지도 모른다는 두려움이야.\""]], [["The days when I frequented that miserable corner which my dear girl brightened can never fade in my remembrance.", "내 사랑하는 아가씨가 환하게 밝혀주던 그 비참한 구석에서 보낸 나날은 내 기억에서 영영 바래지 않을 것입니다."], ["I never see it, and I never wish to see it now; I have been there only once since, but in my memory there is a mournful glory shining on the place which will shine for ever.", "이제는 그곳을 다시 보지도 않고, 보고 싶지도 않습니다. 그 뒤로 딱 한 번 가보았을 뿐이지만, 내 기억 속에는 그 자리를 비추는 슬픈 영광이 깃들어 있고, 그 빛은 영원히 사라지지 않을 것입니다."]], [["Not a day passed without my going there, of course.", "물론 하루도 거르지 않고 그곳을 찾았습니다."], ["At first I found Mr. Skimpole there, on two or three occasions, idly playing the piano and talking in his usual vivacious strain.", "처음에는 두세 번쯤 스킴폴 씨가 거기 있는 걸 보았는데, 한가로이 피아노를 치며 늘 그렇듯 활기찬 어조로 떠들고 있었습니다."], ["Now, besides my very much mistrusting the probability of his being there without making Richard poorer, I felt as if there were something in his careless gaiety too inconsistent with what I knew of the depths of Ada’s life.", "그런데 그가 거기 있으면서 리처드를 더 가난하게 만들지 않을 리 없다는 점을 몹시 미심쩍게 여기는 데다가, 그의 태평한 명랑함이 내가 아는 에이다의 깊은 속내와 너무나 어긋나는 무언가를 품고 있는 것처럼 느껴졌습니다."], ["I clearly perceived, too, that Ada shared my feelings.", "에이다도 나와 같은 심정이라는 걸 똑똑히 알 수 있었습니다."], ["I therefore resolved, after much thinking of it, to make a private visit to Mr. Skimpole and try delicately to explain myself.", "그래서 한참을 고민한 끝에, 나는 스킴폴 씨를 따로 찾아가 조심스럽게 내 뜻을 설명해보기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["My dear girl was the great consideration that made me bold.", "내가 그렇게 대담해질 수 있었던 건 무엇보다 사랑하는 아가씨를 생각해서였습니다."]], [["I set off one morning, accompanied by Charley, for Somers Town.", "어느 날 아침, 나는 찰리를 데리고 서머스 타운으로 나섰습니다."], ["As I approached the house, I was strongly inclined to turn back, for I felt what a desperate attempt it was to make an impression on Mr. Skimpole and how extremely likely it was that he would signally defeat me.", "집이 가까워지자 그냥 돌아가고 싶은 마음이 강하게 들었는데, 스킴폴 씨에게 어떤 인상을 남기려는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 시도인지, 그리고 그가 나를 보기 좋게 꺾어버릴 가능성이 얼마나 큰지 절감했기 때문입니다."], ["However, I thought that being there, I would go through with it. I knocked with a trembling hand at Mr.", "하지만 여기까지 온 마당이니 끝까지 해보자고 생각했습니다."], ["Skimpole’s door—literally with a hand, for the knocker was gone—and after a long parley gained admission from an Irishwoman, who was in the area when I knocked, breaking up the lid of a water-butt with a poker to light the fire with.", "나는 떨리는 손으로 스킴폴 씨의 문을 두드렸는데—말 그대로 손으로 두드렸으니, 문고리가 떨어져 나가고 없었기 때문입니다—한참을 옥신각신한 끝에 아일랜드 여인의 허락을 받고서야 들어갈 수 있었습니다. 그 여인은 내가 문을 두드릴 때 마당에 나와, 불을 지피려고 부지깽이로 물통 뚜껑을 부수고 있었습니다."]], [["Mr. Skimpole, lying on the sofa in his room, playing the flute a little, was enchanted to see me.", "스킴폴 씨는 자기 방 소파에 누워 플루트를 조금 불고 있다가, 나를 보더니 더없이 반가워했습니다."], ["Now, who should receive me, he asked.", "그러면서 자기를 누가 맞이하면 좋겠느냐고 물었습니다."], ["Who would I prefer for mistress of the ceremonies?", "진행 역으로 누구를 더 원하느냐는 것이었습니다."], ["Would I have his Comedy daughter, his Beauty daughter, or his Sentiment daughter?", "희극 딸을 부를까, 미인 딸을 부를까, 감성 딸을 부를까?"], ["Or would I have all the daughters at once in a perfect nosegay?", "아니면 세 딸을 한꺼번에 한 다발 꽃처럼 부를까?"]], [["I replied, half defeated already, that I wished to speak to himself only if he would give me leave.", "이미 반쯤 기가 꺾인 채로 나는, 허락만 해주신다면 오직 그분과만 이야기하고 싶다고 대답했습니다."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson, most joyfully!", "\"친애하는 서머슨 양, 더없이 기쁘오!"], ["Of course,”", "물론이지.\""], ["he said, bringing his chair nearer mine and breaking into his fascinating smile, “of course it’s not business.", "그가 의자를 내 쪽으로 바싹 당기며 그 매혹적인 미소를 활짝 지으며 말했습니다. \"물론 용무는 아니겠지."], ["Then it’s pleasure!”", "그럼 즐거운 일이로군!\""]], [["I said it certainly was not business that I came upon, but it was not quite a pleasant matter.", "나는 용무로 온 건 분명히 아니지만, 그렇다고 썩 즐거운 일도 아니라고 말했습니다."]], [["“Then, my dear Miss Summerson,”", "\"그렇다면, 친애하는 서머슨 양,\""], ["said he with the frankest gaiety, “don’t allude to it.", "그가 더없이 솔직한 명랑함으로 말했습니다. \"그 얘긴 꺼내지 마시오."], ["Why should you allude to anything that is NOT a pleasant matter?", "즐겁지 않은 일을 어째서 굳이 입에 올리겠소? 나는 절대 그러지 않소."], ["I never do. And you are a much pleasanter creature, in every point of view, than I.", "게다가 그대는 어느 모로 보나 나보다 훨씬 더 유쾌한 사람이오."], ["You are perfectly pleasant; I am imperfectly pleasant; then, if I never allude to an unpleasant matter, how much less should you!", "그대는 완벽하게 유쾌하고, 나는 불완전하게 유쾌하지. 그러니 나조차 불쾌한 일을 입에 올리지 않는데, 하물며 그대가 어찌 올리겠소!"], ["So that’s disposed of, and we will talk of something else.”", "자, 이 문제는 이걸로 정리됐으니, 우리 다른 얘기나 합시다.\""]], [["Although I was embarrassed, I took courage to intimate that I still wished to pursue the subject.", "당황스러웠지만, 나는 용기를 내어 그래도 그 이야기를 계속하고 싶다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤습니다."]], [["“I should think it a mistake,” said Mr.", "\"서머슨 양이 실수를 저지를 만한 사람이라고 여겼다면, 나는 이게 실수라고 생각했을 거요.\""], ["Skimpole with his airy laugh, “if I thought Miss Summerson capable of making one.", "스킴폴 씨가 경쾌하게 웃으며 말했습니다."], ["But I don’t!”", "\"하지만 난 그렇게 생각하지 않소!\""]], [["“Mr. Skimpole,”", "\"스킴폴 씨,\""], ["said I, raising my eyes to his, “I have so often heard you say that you are unacquainted with the common affairs of life—”", "나는 그를 향해 눈을 들며 말했습니다. \"당신은 세상의 흔한 일들엔 도통 어둡다고 자주 말씀하셨지요—\""]], [["“Meaning our three banking-house friends, L, S, and who’s the junior partner?", "\"우리의 은행가 친구 셋, 그러니까 파운드 군과 실링 군, 그리고 막내 동업자가 누구더라? 펜스 군?"], ["D?” said Mr.", "그 얘기로군.\""], ["Skimpole, brightly.", "스킴폴 씨가 환하게 말했습니다."], ["“Not an idea of them!”", "\"그 친구들에 대해선 아무것도 모르오!\""]], [["“—That perhaps,” I went on, “you will excuse my boldness on that account.", "\"—그래서 그 점을 핑계 삼아 제 무례를 너그럽게 봐주시리라 믿습니다.\""], ["I think you ought most seriously to know that Richard is poorer than he was.”", "나는 말을 이었습니다. \"리처드가 예전보다 가난해졌다는 사실을, 부디 진지하게 아셔야 한다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"저런!\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“So am I, they tell me.”", "\"나도 그렇다고들 하더군.\""]], [["“And in very embarrassed circumstances.”", "\"그리고 형편이 몹시 곤란해졌고요.\""]], [["“Parallel case, exactly!”", "\"내 처지랑 똑같구먼!\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole with a delighted countenance.", "스킴폴 씨가 기쁜 낯빛으로 말했습니다."]], [["“This at present naturally causes Ada much secret anxiety, and as I think she is less anxious when no claims are made upon her by visitors, and as Richard has one uneasiness always heavy on his mind, it has occurred to me to take the liberty of saying that—if you would—not—”", "\"지금 이 일이 자연히 에이다에게 남모를 근심을 안겨주고 있어요. 게다가 손님들이 찾아와 무언가를 요구하지 않을 때 그 애가 덜 불안해하는 것 같고, 또 리처드는 늘 마음에 무거운 걱정 하나를 짊어지고 있으니—그래서 외람되지만 한 말씀 드리려고요. 만약 당신이—그러지—않으신다면—\""]], [["I was coming to the point with great difficulty when he took me by both hands and with a radiant face and in the liveliest way anticipated it.", "내가 그 말을 가까스로 꺼내려는 참에, 그가 내 두 손을 덥석 잡고 환한 얼굴로 더없이 발랄하게 내 말을 가로챘습니다."]], [["“Not go there?", "\"거기 가지 말라고?"], ["Certainly not, my dear Miss Summerson, most assuredly not.", "물론이오, 친애하는 서머슨 양, 두말할 것 없소. 내가 왜 거길 가겠소?"], ["Why SHOULD I go there?", "나는 어딜 가든 즐거우려고 가는 거요."], ["When I go anywhere, I go for pleasure.", "고통을 맛보려고 어딜 가진 않소."], ["I don’t go anywhere for pain, because I was made for pleasure.", "나는 즐거움을 위해 태어난 사람이니까. 고통은 나를 원할 때 제 발로 찾아오는 법이오."], ["Pain comes to ME when it wants me.", "그런데 요즘 사랑하는 리처드네 집에선 도무지 즐거움을 누리지 못했고, 그대의 실용적인 통찰이 그 까닭을 똑똑히 짚어주는구려."], ["Now, I have had very little pleasure at our dear Richard’s lately, and your practical sagacity demonstrates why.", "우리 젊은 친구들이 한때 그토록 사람을 사로잡던 젊은 시의 정취를 잃어버리고는, '이 사람은 파운드를 원하는 자야' 하고 생각하기 시작했소."], ["Our young friends, losing the youthful poetry which was once so captivating in them, begin to think, ‘This is a man who wants pounds.’ So I am; I always want pounds; not for myself, but because tradespeople always want them of me.", "사실 그렇소. 나는 늘 파운드를 원하지."], ["Next, our young friends begin to think, becoming mercenary, ‘This is the man who HAD pounds, who borrowed them,’ which I did.", "내가 쓰려는 게 아니라, 상인들이 늘 나한테서 파운드를 받으려 하니까. 그다음엔 우리 젊은 친구들이 속물이 되어, '이 사람은 파운드를 가졌던 자, 그걸 빌렸던 자야'"], ["I always borrow pounds.", "하고 생각하기 시작하지. 정말 그랬소."], ["So our young friends, reduced to prose (which is much to be regretted), degenerate in their power of imparting pleasure to me.", "나는 늘 파운드를 빌리니까. 그래서 우리 젊은 친구들이 산문으로 전락해버려서는(참으로 안타까운 일이오) 나에게 즐거움을 안겨주는 힘이 퇴화하고 만 거요."], ["Why should I go to see them, therefore?", "그러니 내가 왜 그들을 보러 가겠소?"], ["Absurd!”", "어불성설이오!\""]], [["Through the beaming smile with which he regarded me as he reasoned thus, there now broke forth a look of disinterested benevolence quite astonishing.", "이렇게 따져가며 나를 바라보던 그 환한 미소 사이로, 이제 사심 없는 자비의 표정이 떠올랐는데, 그 모습이 어찌나 놀랍던지요."]], [["“Besides,”", "\"게다가,\""], ["he said, pursuing his argument in his tone of light-hearted conviction, “if I don’t go anywhere for pain—which would be a perversion of the intention of my being, and a monstrous thing to do—why should I go anywhere to be the cause of pain?", "그가 가벼운 확신의 어조로 자기 논리를 이어갔습니다. \"고통을 맛보려고 어딜 가지 않는 내가—그건 내 존재의 본뜻을 거스르는 일이고, 끔찍한 짓이지—고통의 원인이 되려고 어딜 가야 할 까닭이 어디 있겠소?"], ["If I went to see our young friends in their present ill-regulated state of mind, I should give them pain.", "지금처럼 마음이 흐트러진 우리 젊은 친구들을 보러 간다면, 나는 그들에게 고통을 안기게 될 거요. 나와 얽힌 기억들이 그들에겐 불쾌하게 다가올 테지."], ["The associations with me would be disagreeable.", "그들은 '이 사람은 파운드를 가졌었는데 이제 파운드를 갚지 못하는 자야' 하고 말할지도 모르오."], ["They might say, ‘This is the man who had pounds and who can’t pay pounds,’ which I can’t, of course; nothing could be more out of the question!", "물론 나는 갚을 수 없소. 그건 애초에 말도 안 되는 얘기지!"], ["Then kindness requires that I shouldn’t go near them—and I won’t.”", "그러니 그들 곁에 가지 않는 것이야말로 친절이오—그래서 나는 가지 않겠소.\""]], [["He finished by genially kissing my hand and thanking me.", "그는 다정하게 내 손에 입을 맞추고 고맙다는 말로 끝을 맺었습니다."], ["Nothing but Miss Summerson’s fine tact, he said, would have found this out for him.", "서머슨 양의 그 빼어난 재치가 아니었다면 결코 이 점을 깨닫지 못했을 거라고 했습니다."]], [["I was much disconcerted, but I reflected that if the main point were gained, it mattered little how strangely he perverted everything leading to it.", "나는 무척 당혹스러웠지만, 핵심만 얻어낸다면 그가 거기에 이르는 모든 과정을 아무리 괴상하게 비틀어놓든 별로 중요치 않다고 마음을 다잡았습니다."], ["I had determined to mention something else, however, and I thought I was not to be put off in that.", "그래도 다른 한 가지를 말하기로 작정한 터라, 그 일만큼은 그냥 넘어갈 수 없다고 생각했습니다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"스킴폴 씨,\""], ["Skimpole,”", "나는 말했습니다. \"방문을 마치기 전에 외람되지만 한 말씀 드려야겠습니다."], ["said I, “I must take the liberty of saying before I conclude my visit that I was much surprised to learn, on the best authority, some little time ago, that you knew with whom that poor boy left Bleak House and that you accepted a present on that occasion.", "얼마 전 가장 확실한 소식통에게서, 그 가엾은 소년이 누구와 함께 황폐한 집을 떠났는지 당신이 알고 있었고, 그때 사례를 받았다는 사실을 듣고 몹시 놀랐습니다. 후견인께는 이 일을 말씀드리지 않았어요."], ["I have not mentioned it to my guardian, for I fear it would hurt him unnecessarily; but I may say to you that I was much surprised.”", "공연히 마음만 상하실까 봐서요. 하지만 당신께는, 제가 무척 놀랐다는 말씀은 드릴 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["“No?", "\"아니라고?"], ["Really surprised, my dear Miss Summerson?”", "정말로 놀랐단 말이오, 친애하는 서머슨 양?\""], ["he returned inquiringly, raising his pleasant eyebrows.", "그가 그 유쾌한 눈썹을 치켜올리며 캐묻듯 되물었습니다."]], [["“Greatly surprised.”", "\"크게 놀랐습니다.\""]], [["He thought about it for a little while with a highly agreeable and whimsical expression of face, then quite gave it up and said in his most engaging manner, “You know what a child I am.", "그는 더없이 호감 가고 익살스러운 표정으로 잠시 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하다가, 이내 포기하고 그 누구보다 사람을 끄는 태도로 말했습니다. \"내가 얼마나 어린애 같은 사람인지 알잖소."], ["Why surprised?”", "왜 놀랐다는 거요?\""]], [["I was reluctant to enter minutely into that question, but as he begged I would, for he was really curious to know, I gave him to understand in the gentlest words I could use that his conduct seemed to involve a disregard of several moral obligations.", "나는 그 문제를 시시콜콜 따지고 들기가 꺼려졌지만, 그가 정말로 궁금해하며 제발 말해달라고 하기에, 내가 쓸 수 있는 가장 부드러운 말로 그의 행동이 여러 도덕적 의무를 저버린 일처럼 보인다는 뜻을 전했습니다."], ["He was much amused and interested when he heard this and said, “No, really?”", "그는 이 말을 듣고 무척 재미있어하며 흥미를 보였고, 순진할 만큼 천진하게 \"아니, 정말로?\""], ["with ingenuous simplicity.", "하고 말했습니다."]], [["“You know I don’t intend to be responsible. I never could do it.", "\"내가 무슨 책임을 질 생각이 없는 사람이란 걸 알잖소."], ["Responsibility is a thing that has always been above me—or below me,”", "나는 도저히 그런 걸 할 수 없는 사람이오. 책임이란 늘 내 위에 있거나—아니면 내 아래에 있는 것이었소.\""], ["said Mr. Skimpole.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“I don’t even know which; but as I understand the way in which my dear Miss Summerson (always remarkable for her practical good sense and clearness) puts this case, I should imagine it was chiefly a question of money, do you know?”", "\"어느 쪽인지조차 모르겠소. 하지만 친애하는 서머슨 양이(언제나 그 실용적인 양식과 명석함이 돋보이는 분이지) 이 일을 풀어놓는 방식을 헤아려보건대, 결국 주로 돈 문제인 것 같은데, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["I incautiously gave a qualified assent to this.", "나는 경솔하게도 거기에 어정쩡하게 그렇다고 동의하고 말았습니다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아하!"], ["Then you see,” said Mr.", "그럼 보시오,\""], ["Skimpole, shaking his head, “I am hopeless of understanding it.”", "스킴폴 씨가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했습니다. \"나는 그런 건 도무지 이해할 가망이 없는 사람이오.\""]], [["I suggested, as I rose to go, that it was not right to betray my guardian’s confidence for a bribe.", "나는 자리에서 일어서며, 뇌물을 받고 후견인의 신뢰를 저버리는 건 옳지 않다고 넌지시 일렀습니다."]], [["“My dear Miss Summerson,”", "\"친애하는 서머슨 양,\""], ["he returned with a candid hilarity that was all his own, “I can’t be bribed.”", "그가 자기만의 그 솔직한 쾌활함으로 되받았습니다. \"나는 뇌물에 넘어갈 수 있는 사람이 아니오.\""]], [["“Not by Mr.", "\"버킷 씨에게도요?\""], ["Bucket?” said I.", "내가 말했습니다."]], [["“No,” said he.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["“Not by anybody.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["I don’t attach any value to money.", "\"누구에게도 안 넘어가지."], ["I don’t care about it, I don’t know about it, I don’t want it, I don’t keep it—it goes away from me directly.", "나는 돈에 아무런 가치를 두지 않으니까. 돈에 관심도 없고, 돈을 알지도 못하고, 원하지도 않고, 가지고 있지도 않소—돈은 내게서 곧장 달아나버린다오."], ["How can I be bribed?”", "그러니 내가 어떻게 뇌물에 넘어가겠소?\""]], [["I showed that I was of a different opinion, though I had not the capacity for arguing the question.", "나는 그 문제를 따져 논할 능력은 없었지만, 내 생각은 다르다는 뜻을 내비쳤습니다."]], [["“On the contrary,” said Mr.", "\"오히려 정반대요,\""], ["Skimpole, “I am exactly the man to be placed in a superior position in such a case as that.", "스킴폴 씨가 말했습니다. \"그런 경우라면 나야말로 남보다 우월한 위치에 설 만한 사람이오."], ["I am above the rest of mankind in such a case as that.", "그런 경우라면 나는 나머지 인류보다 높은 데 있소."], ["I can act with philosophy in such a case as that.", "그런 경우라면 나는 철학적으로 행동할 수 있소."], ["I am not warped by prejudices, as an Italian baby is by bandages.", "나는 이탈리아 갓난아이가 포대기에 묶이듯 편견에 묶여 있지 않소."], ["I am as free as the air.", "나는 공기처럼 자유로운 사람이오."], ["I feel myself as far above suspicion as Caesar’s wife.”", "카이사르의 아내처럼 의심을 훌쩍 뛰어넘은 곳에 있다고 느낀다오.\""]], [["Anything to equal the lightness of his manner and the playful impartiality with which he seemed to convince himself, as he tossed the matter about like a ball of feathers, was surely never seen in anybody else!", "깃털 공을 가지고 놀듯 이 문제를 이리저리 던지면서, 스스로를 설득해 보이는 그 가벼운 태도와 장난스러운 공정함에 견줄 만한 모습은, 다른 누구에게서도 결코 본 적이 없는 것이었습니다!"]], [["“Observe the case, my dear Miss Summerson.", "\"이 일을 잘 보시오, 친애하는 서머슨 양."], ["Here is a boy received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to.", "여기 한 소년이 집에 받아들여져, 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여졌소."], ["The boy being in bed, a man arrives—like the house that Jack built.", "소년이 침대에 누워 있는데, 한 남자가 들이닥치오—마치 '잭이 지은 집'처럼 말이지."], ["Here is the man who demands the boy who is received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to.", "여기 그 남자가 있소. 집에 받아들여져 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여진 소년을 내놓으라고 요구하는 그 남자 말이오."], ["Here is a bank-note produced by the man who demands the boy who is received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to.", "여기 은행권 한 장이 있소. 집에 받아들여져 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여진 소년을 내놓으라고 요구하는 그 남자가 내민 은행권이오."], ["Here is the Skimpole who accepts the bank-note produced by the man who demands the boy who is received into the house and put to bed in a state that I strongly object to.", "여기 스킴폴이 있소. 집에 받아들여져 내가 강하게 반대하는 상태로 침대에 뉘여진 소년을 내놓으라고 요구하는 그 남자가 내민 은행권을 받는 그 스킴폴 말이오."], ["Those are the facts.", "이것이 사실이오."], ["Very well.", "좋소."], ["Should the Skimpole have refused the note?", "그래서 스킴폴이 그 돈을 거절했어야 했소?"], ["WHY should the Skimpole have refused the note?", "어째서 스킴폴이 그 돈을 거절했어야 한단 말이오?"], ["Skimpole protests to Bucket, ‘What’s this for?", "스킴폴은 버킷에게 항변하지. '이게 무슨 돈이오?"], ["I don’t understand it, it is of no use to me, take it away.’ Bucket still entreats Skimpole to accept it.", "도통 모르겠소, 나한테는 아무 쓸모가 없으니 도로 가져가시오.' 그래도 버킷은 스킴폴에게 받아달라고 간청하오."], ["Are there reasons why Skimpole, not being warped by prejudices, should accept it?", "편견에 묶이지 않은 스킴폴이 그걸 받아야 할 이유가 있겠소? 그렇소, 있소."], ["Yes. Skimpole perceives them.", "스킴폴은 그 이유들을 알아채지."], ["What are they?", "그게 무엇이겠소? 스킴폴은 스스로 따져보오."], ["Skimpole reasons with himself, this is a tamed lynx, an active police-officer, an intelligent man, a person of a peculiarly directed energy and great subtlety both of conception and execution, who discovers our friends and enemies for us when they run away, recovers our property for us when we are robbed, avenges us comfortably when we are murdered.", "이 사람은 길들여진 스라소니, 발 빠른 경찰관, 영리한 사람, 발상과 실행 양쪽에서 비범하게 집중된 정력과 대단한 치밀함을 지닌 인물이라고. 우리 친구와 적이 달아나면 우리를 위해 찾아주고, 우리가 도둑맞으면 재산을 되찾아주고, 우리가 살해당하면 속 시원히 복수해주는 자라고 말이오."], ["This active police-officer and intelligent man has acquired, in the exercise of his art, a strong faith in money; he finds it very useful to him, and he makes it very useful to society.", "이 발 빠른 경찰관이자 영리한 사람은 자기 일을 해나가면서 돈에 대한 굳은 믿음을 얻었소. 그는 돈이 자기에게 무척 쓸모 있다는 걸 알고, 또 그것을 사회에 무척 쓸모 있게 쓰지."], ["Shall I shake that faith in Bucket because I want it myself; shall I deliberately blunt one of Bucket’s weapons; shall I positively paralyse Bucket in his next detective operation?", "그런데 내가 그 돈을 직접 원하는 마당에, 버킷의 그 믿음을 흔들어놓아야 하겠소? 내가 일부러 버킷의 무기 하나를 무디게 만들어야 하겠소?"], ["And again.", "내가 버킷의 다음 수사 작업을 아주 마비시켜놓아야 하겠소? 또 이런 것도 있소."], ["If it is blameable in Skimpole to take the note, it is blameable in Bucket to offer the note—much more blameable in Bucket, because he is the knowing man.", "스킴폴이 그 돈을 받는 게 탓할 일이라면, 버킷이 그 돈을 내미는 것도 탓할 일이오—오히려 버킷 쪽이 훨씬 더 탓할 일이지. 그는 사정을 다 아는 사람이니까."], ["Now, Skimpole wishes to think well of Bucket; Skimpole deems it essential, in its little place, to the general cohesion of things, that he SHOULD think well of Bucket.", "그런데 스킴폴은 버킷을 좋게 생각하고 싶소. 스킴폴은, 그 자그마한 자리에서나마, 만물이 두루 어우러지는 데에 자기가 버킷을 좋게 생각하는 것이 긴요하다고 여기오."], ["The state expressly asks him to trust to Bucket. And he does.", "국가가 그에게 버킷을 믿으라고 명백히 요청하니, 그는 그대로 믿는 것이오."], ["And that’s all he does!”", "그가 한 일이라곤 그게 전부요!\""]], [["I had nothing to offer in reply to this exposition and therefore took my leave. Mr.", "이 장광설에 나는 도무지 대꾸할 말이 없어서 자리를 떴습니다."], ["Skimpole, however, who was in excellent spirits, would not hear of my returning home attended only by “Little Coavinses,”", "그러나 더없이 기분이 좋아진 스킴폴 씨는, 내가 '꼬마 코빈시스' 하나만 데리고 집으로 돌아간다는 건 말도 안 된다며 직접 나를 바래다주었습니다."], ["and accompanied me himself. He entertained me on the way with a variety of delightful conversation and assured me, at parting, that he should never forget the fine tact with which I had found that out for him about our young friends.", "그는 가는 길 내내 갖가지 유쾌한 이야기로 나를 즐겁게 해주었고, 헤어질 때는 우리 젊은 친구들에 관한 그 일을 자기 대신 알아내 준 내 그 빼어난 재치를 결코 잊지 않겠노라고 다짐했습니다."]], [["As it so happened that I never saw Mr. Skimpole again, I may at once finish what I know of his history.", "그 뒤로 스킴폴 씨를 다시 만나는 일이 없었으니, 내가 아는 그의 내력을 여기서 마저 끝맺어도 되겠습니다."], ["A coolness arose between him and my guardian, based principally on the foregoing grounds and on his having heartlessly disregarded my guardian’s entreaties (as we afterwards learned from Ada) in reference to Richard.", "그와 후견인 사이에 냉랭한 기류가 생겼는데, 그 까닭은 주로 앞서 말한 사정들과, 리처드 일을 두고 그가 후견인의 간청을(이건 나중에 에이다에게 들어 알게 된 것입니다) 매정하게 무시한 데에 있었습니다."], ["His being heavily in my guardian’s debt had nothing to do with their separation.", "그가 후견인에게 큰 빚을 지고 있었다는 점은 두 사람이 갈라선 일과는 아무 상관이 없었습니다."], ["He died some five years afterwards and left a diary behind him, with letters and other materials towards his life, which was published and which showed him to have been the victim of a combination on the part of mankind against an amiable child.", "그는 그로부터 오 년쯤 뒤에 죽으면서 일기를 남겼는데, 거기엔 편지들과 자기 생애를 정리하려는 다른 자료들이 함께 있었고, 그것이 책으로 출간되었습니다. 그 책은 그가 사랑스러운 아이 하나를 겨눈 인류의 합작 음모에 희생된 사람이었던 양 그려놓았습니다."], ["It was considered very pleasant reading, but I never read more of it myself than the sentence on which I chanced to light on opening the book.", "사람들은 무척 재미난 읽을거리로 여겼지만, 나는 책을 펼쳤다가 우연히 눈에 들어온 한 문장 말고는 더 읽지 않았습니다."], ["It was this: “Jarndyce, in common with most other men I have known, is the incarnation of selfishness.”", "그 문장은 이러했습니다. \"잔다이스는 내가 아는 대다수 사람과 마찬가지로, 이기심의 화신이다.\""]], [["And now I come to a part of my story touching myself very nearly indeed, and for which I was quite unprepared when the circumstance occurred.", "이제 나는 내 이야기에서 나 자신과 참으로 가깝게 맞닿은 대목에 이르렀는데, 정작 그 일이 벌어졌을 때 나는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았습니다."], ["Whatever little lingerings may have now and then revived in my mind associated with my poor old face had only revived as belonging to a part of my life that was gone—gone like my infancy or my childhood.", "내 가엾은 옛 얼굴과 얽혀 이따금 마음속에 되살아나곤 하던 자그마한 미련들이 있었지만, 그것들은 이미 지나가 버린 내 삶의 한 부분에 속한 것으로서만 되살아났을 뿐입니다—내 갓난 시절이나 어린 시절처럼 지나가 버린 것으로요."], ["I have suppressed none of my many weaknesses on that subject, but have written them as faithfully as my memory has recalled them.", "그 일에 관한 나의 숱한 약한 마음들을 나는 하나도 감추지 않고, 기억이 떠올려주는 그대로 충실히 적어왔습니다."], ["And I hope to do, and mean to do, the same down to the last words of these pages, which I see now not so very far before me.", "그리고 이제 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 보이는 이 책의 마지막 구절에 이르기까지, 나는 한결같이 그렇게 하기를 바라고 또 그럴 작정입니다."]], [["The months were gliding away, and my dear girl, sustained by the hopes she had confided in me, was the same beautiful star in the miserable corner.", "세월은 미끄러지듯 흘러갔고, 나에게 털어놓은 희망에 의지하며 버티던 사랑하는 아가씨는, 그 비참한 구석에서 여전히 한결같이 아름다운 별이었습니다."], ["Richard, more worn and haggard, haunted the court day after day, listlessly sat there the whole day long when he knew there was no remote chance of the suit being mentioned, and became one of the stock sights of the place.", "리처드는 갈수록 야위고 초췌해진 모습으로 날마다 법정을 맴돌았고, 소송이 거론될 가망이라곤 손톱만큼도 없는 줄 알면서도 온종일 멍하니 앉아 있다가, 그곳의 붙박이 구경거리 가운데 하나가 되어버렸습니다."], ["I wonder whether any of the gentlemen remembered him as he was when he first went there.", "그곳 신사들 중에 그가 처음 발을 들여놓았을 적 모습을 기억하는 이가 한 사람이라도 있을까 싶었습니다."]], [["So completely was he absorbed in his fixed idea that he used to avow in his cheerful moments that he should never have breathed the fresh air now “but for Woodcourt.”", "그는 자신의 고정관념에 어찌나 완전히 사로잡혀 있던지, 기분이 좋을 때면 \"우드코트가 아니었다면\" 지금 이렇게 맑은 공기를 마시지도 못했을 거라고 털어놓곤 했습니다."], ["It was only Mr. Woodcourt who could occasionally divert his attention for a few hours at a time and rouse him, even when he sunk into a lethargy of mind and body that alarmed us greatly, and the returns of which became more frequent as the months went on.", "그가 한 번에 몇 시간씩이나마 마음을 다른 데로 돌리고 그를 다시 일으켜 세울 수 있는 사람은 오직 우드코트 씨뿐이었는데, 우리를 몹시 놀라게 한 그 심신의 무기력에 가라앉을 때조차 그러했습니다."], ["My dear girl was right in saying that he only pursued his errors the more desperately for her sake.", "게다가 그 무기력이 도지는 일은 세월이 갈수록 점점 잦아졌습니다. 그가 자신을 위해 오히려 더 필사적으로 잘못된 길을 좇는 거라던 사랑하는 아가씨의 말은 옳았습니다."], ["I have no doubt that his desire to retrieve what he had lost was rendered the more intense by his grief for his young wife, and became like the madness of a gamester.", "잃어버린 것을 되찾으려는 그의 갈망이 젊은 아내에 대한 슬픔으로 한층 더 격렬해져서, 마치 노름꾼의 광기처럼 변해버렸다는 데 나는 의심의 여지가 없습니다."]], [["I was there, as I have mentioned, at all hours.", "앞서 말했듯이 나는 시도 때도 없이 그곳에 있었습니다."], ["When I was there at night, I generally went home with Charley in a coach; sometimes my guardian would meet me in the neighbourhood, and we would walk home together.", "밤에 그곳에 있을 때면 대개 찰리와 함께 마차를 타고 집으로 돌아왔습니다. 이따금 후견인이 근처에서 나를 마중 나오면, 우리는 함께 걸어서 집으로 돌아오곤 했습니다."], ["One evening he had arranged to meet me at eight o’clock.", "어느 날 저녁, 그는 여덟 시에 나를 만나기로 약속해두었습니다."], ["I could not leave, as I usually did, quite punctually at the time, for I was working for my dear girl and had a few stitches more to do to finish what I was about; but it was within a few minutes of the hour when I bundled up my little work-basket, gave my darling my last kiss for the night, and hurried downstairs.", "그날은 평소처럼 시간에 딱 맞춰 자리를 뜰 수가 없었는데, 사랑하는 아가씨를 위해 무언가를 만들던 중이라 손대고 있던 것을 마무리하려면 몇 바늘 더 떠야 했기 때문입니다. 하지만 약속 시각을 몇 분 앞두고 나는 자그마한 반짇고리를 챙겨 들고, 그 밤의 마지막 입맞춤을 사랑하는 아가씨에게 남긴 뒤 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔습니다."], ["Mr. Woodcourt went with me, as it was dusk.", "땅거미가 진 터라 우드코트 씨가 나와 함께 가주었습니다."]], [["When we came to the usual place of meeting—it was close by, and Mr. Woodcourt had often accompanied me before—my guardian was not there.", "늘 만나기로 한 자리에 다다랐을 때—그곳은 바로 가까이였고, 우드코트 씨도 전에 여러 번 나와 동행한 적이 있었습니다—후견인은 거기 없었습니다."], ["We waited half an hour, walking up and down, but there were no signs of him.", "우리는 삼십 분 동안 이리저리 거닐며 기다렸지만, 그의 자취는 보이지 않았습니다."], ["We agreed that he was either prevented from coming or that he had come and gone away, and Mr. Woodcourt proposed to walk home with me.", "우리는 그가 사정이 생겨 오지 못했거나, 아니면 왔다가 그냥 가버렸으리라는 데 뜻을 모았고, 우드코트 씨가 나와 함께 걸어서 집까지 가자고 했습니다."]], [["It was the first walk we had ever taken together, except that very short one to the usual place of meeting.", "늘 만나기로 한 자리까지 가던 그 아주 짧은 걸음을 빼면, 우리가 함께 걸어본 것은 그때가 처음이었습니다."], ["We spoke of Richard and Ada the whole way.", "우리는 가는 길 내내 리처드와 에이다 이야기를 나누었습니다."], ["I did not thank him in words for what he had done—my appreciation of it had risen above all words then—but I hoped he might not be without some understanding of what I felt so strongly.", "나는 그가 해준 일에 대해 말로 고마움을 전하지는 않았습니다—그때 그 일에 대한 내 감사의 마음은 어떤 말로도 다 담을 수 없을 만큼 부풀어 있었으니까요—그래도 내가 그토록 강하게 느끼던 마음을 그가 어느 정도는 헤아려주기를 바랐습니다."]], [["Arriving at home and going upstairs, we found that my guardian was out and that Mrs. Woodcourt was out too.", "집에 다다라 위층으로 올라가 보니, 후견인은 나가고 없었고 우드코트 부인도 외출 중이었습니다."], ["We were in the very same room into which I had brought my blushing girl when her youthful lover, now her so altered husband, was the choice of her young heart, the very same room from which my guardian and I had watched them going away through the sunlight in the fresh bloom of their hope and promise.", "우리가 들어선 곳은, 그 옛날 풋풋한 연인이었으나 지금은 너무도 달라진 남편이 된 그 사람을 사랑하는 아가씨가 어린 마음으로 택했을 때, 내가 얼굴 붉히던 그 아이를 데리고 들어왔던 바로 그 방이었습니다. 후견인과 내가 햇살을 받으며 떠나가는 두 사람을, 희망과 약속이 갓 피어난 그 모습을 지켜보았던 바로 그 방이었습니다."]], [["We were standing by the opened window looking down into the street when Mr. Woodcourt spoke to me.", "우리가 열린 창가에 서서 거리를 내려다보고 있을 때, 우드코트 씨가 나에게 말을 건넸습니다."], ["I learned in a moment that he loved me.", "나는 순식간에 그가 나를 사랑한다는 걸 알았습니다."], ["I learned in a moment that my scarred face was all unchanged to him.", "나는 순식간에 흉터로 일그러진 내 얼굴이 그에게는 조금도 달라지지 않았다는 걸 알았습니다."], ["I learned in a moment that what I had thought was pity and compassion was devoted, generous, faithful love.", "나는 순식간에 내가 동정이며 연민이라 여겼던 것이, 실은 헌신적이고 너그럽고 변함없는 사랑이었다는 걸 알았습니다."], ["Oh, too late to know it now, too late, too late.", "아, 이제야 그걸 알다니 너무 늦었습니다, 너무 늦었어요, 너무 늦었습니다."], ["That was the first ungrateful thought I had.", "그것이 나에게 처음으로 떠오른, 은혜를 모르는 생각이었습니다."], ["Too late.", "너무 늦었다고요."]], [["“When I returned,”", "\"내가 돌아왔을 때,\""], ["he told me, “when I came back, no richer than when I went away, and found you newly risen from a sick bed, yet so inspired by sweet consideration for others and so free from a selfish thought—”", "그가 나에게 말했습니다. \"떠날 때보다 조금도 더 가멸지 못한 채 돌아와서, 당신이 병상에서 갓 일어난 모습을 보았을 때, 그러면서도 남을 향한 그 다정한 배려로 그토록 빛나고 이기적인 생각이라곤 그토록 없는 모습을 보았을 때—\""]], [["“Oh, Mr. Woodcourt, forbear, forbear!”", "\"오, 우드코트 씨, 그만, 그만하세요!\""], ["I entreated him.", "나는 그에게 간청했습니다."], ["“I do not deserve your high praise.", "\"저는 당신의 그 높은 칭찬을 받을 자격이 없어요."], ["I had many selfish thoughts at that time, many!”", "그때 저는 이기적인 생각을 참으로 많이 했답니다, 아주 많이요!\""]], [["“Heaven knows, beloved of my life,”", "\"하늘이 알다시피, 내 생의 사랑하는 사람이여,\""], ["said he, “that my praise is not a lover’s praise, but the truth.", "그가 말했습니다. \"내 칭찬은 연인의 칭찬이 아니라 진실이오."], ["You do not know what all around you see in Esther Summerson, how many hearts she touches and awakens, what sacred admiration and what love she wins.”", "당신 주변의 모든 사람이 에스더 서머슨에게서 무엇을 보는지, 그녀가 얼마나 많은 마음을 어루만지고 일깨우는지, 얼마나 거룩한 흠모와 얼마나 큰 사랑을 얻는지, 당신은 모르오.\""]], [["“Oh, Mr.", "\"오, 우드코트 씨,\""], ["Woodcourt,”", "나는 외쳤습니다."], ["cried I, “it is a great thing to win love, it is a great thing to win love!", "\"사랑을 얻는다는 건 대단한 일이에요, 사랑을 얻는다는 건 정말 대단한 일이에요! 저는 그것이 자랑스럽고, 그것으로 영광스러워요."], ["I am proud of it, and honoured by it; and the hearing of it causes me to shed these tears of mingled joy and sorrow—joy that I have won it, sorrow that I have not deserved it better; but I am not free to think of yours.”", "그 말을 들으니 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 이 눈물이 흐르네요—그 사랑을 얻었다는 기쁨, 그리고 그것을 더 받을 만하게 살지 못했다는 슬픔이요. 하지만 저는 당신의 사랑을 마음에 둘 처지가 못 됩니다.\""]], [["I said it with a stronger heart, for when he praised me thus and when I heard his voice thrill with his belief that what he said was true, I aspired to be more worthy of it.", "나는 한층 굳센 마음으로 그 말을 했습니다. 그가 나를 그렇게 칭찬하고, 자기가 한 말이 참이라는 믿음으로 그의 목소리가 떨리는 걸 들으니, 그 칭찬에 더 어울리는 사람이 되고 싶다는 마음이 솟았기 때문입니다."], ["It was not too late for that.", "그것만큼은 너무 늦지 않았습니다."], ["Although I closed this unforeseen page in my life to-night, I could be worthier of it all through my life.", "비록 오늘 밤 내 삶의 이 뜻밖의 한 장을 덮어버린다 해도, 나는 살아가는 내내 그 모든 것에 더 어울리는 사람이 될 수 있었습니다."], ["And it was a comfort to me, and an impulse to me, and I felt a dignity rise up within me that was derived from him when I thought so.", "그리고 그렇게 생각하니 그것이 내게 위안이 되고 또 힘이 되어, 그에게서 비롯된 어떤 기품이 내 안에서 솟아오르는 것을 느꼈습니다."]], [["He broke the silence.", "그가 침묵을 깼습니다."]], [["“I should poorly show the trust that I have in the dear one who will evermore be as dear to me as now”—and the deep earnestness with which he said it at once strengthened me and made me weep—“if, after her assurance that she is not free to think of my love, I urged it.", "\"지금처럼 앞으로도 영원히 소중할 사랑하는 분에 대한 내 믿음을, 내가 너무도 초라하게 보이게 하는 셈이 될 거요\"—그가 그 말을 하던 깊은 진지함은 나를 단번에 굳세게 하면서도 눈물짓게 했습니다—\"내 사랑을 마음에 둘 처지가 못 된다고 그녀가 분명히 말한 뒤에도 내가 그 사랑을 들이댄다면 말이오."], ["Dear Esther, let me only tell you that the fond idea of you which I took abroad was exalted to the heavens when I came home.", "사랑하는 에스더, 다만 이 말만은 하게 해주오. 내가 멀리 떠나며 품고 간 당신에 대한 그 애틋한 마음이, 집으로 돌아오니 하늘에 닿을 만큼 드높아져 있더이다."], ["I have always hoped, in the first hour when I seemed to stand in any ray of good fortune, to tell you this.", "나는 행운의 한 줄기 빛 속에 처음 서 있는 듯한 그 순간에 당신에게 이 말을 하리라고 늘 바라왔소."], ["I have always feared that I should tell it you in vain.", "그러면서도 그 말이 헛되이 끝날까 늘 두려워했소."], ["My hopes and fears are both fulfilled to-night.", "내 바람과 두려움이 오늘 밤 둘 다 이루어졌구려."], ["I distress you.", "내가 당신을 괴롭히는군."], ["I have said enough.”", "이만하면 충분히 말했소.\""]], [["Something seemed to pass into my place that was like the angel he thought me, and I felt so sorrowful for the loss he had sustained!", "그가 나를 천사라 여겨주던 그 모습 같은 무언가가 내 안으로 스며드는 듯했고, 나는 그가 겪은 그 상실이 너무도 안타깝게 느껴졌습니다!"], ["I wished to help him in his trouble, as I had wished to do when he showed that first commiseration for me.", "그가 나에게 처음 그 연민을 내비쳤을 때 그러고 싶었던 것처럼, 나는 그의 괴로움을 덜어주고 싶었습니다."]], [["“Dear Mr.", "\"우드코트 씨,\""], ["Woodcourt,”", "나는 말했습니다."], ["said I, “before we part to-night, something is left for me to say.", "\"오늘 밤 우리가 헤어지기 전에, 제가 드려야 할 말씀이 하나 남아 있어요."], ["I never could say it as I wish—I never shall—but—”", "제가 바라는 만큼 잘 표현하지는 결코 못할 테고—앞으로도 결코 못하겠지만—그래도—\""]], [["I had to think again of being more deserving of his love and his affliction before I could go on.", "말을 더 잇기 전에, 나는 그의 사랑과 그의 아픔에 더 어울리는 사람이 되어야 한다고 다시 한번 마음을 다잡아야 했습니다."]], [["“—I am deeply sensible of your generosity, and I shall treasure its remembrance to my dying hour.", "\"—저는 당신의 너그러움을 마음 깊이 느끼고 있고, 그 기억을 죽는 순간까지 소중히 간직할 거예요."], ["I know full well how changed I am, I know you are not unacquainted with my history, and I know what a noble love that is which is so faithful.", "제가 얼마나 달라졌는지 저는 너무도 잘 알고, 당신이 제 내력을 모르지 않는다는 것도 알고, 그토록 변함없는 그 사랑이 얼마나 고귀한 것인지도 압니다."], ["What you have said to me could have affected me so much from no other lips, for there are none that could give it such a value to me.", "당신이 제게 해주신 그 말씀은 다른 어떤 입에서 나왔다면 저를 이토록 깊이 움직이지 못했을 거예요. 저에게 그만한 값어치를 안겨줄 입은 달리 없으니까요."], ["It shall not be lost.", "그 말씀은 헛되이 사라지지 않을 거예요."], ["It shall make me better.”", "그것이 저를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어줄 테니까요.\""]], [["He covered his eyes with his hand and turned away his head.", "그는 손으로 두 눈을 가리고 고개를 돌렸습니다."], ["How could I ever be worthy of those tears?", "내가 어찌 저 눈물을 받을 만한 사람이 될 수 있을까요?"]], [["“If, in the unchanged intercourse we shall have together—in tending Richard and Ada, and I hope in many happier scenes of life—you ever find anything in me which you can honestly think is better than it used to be, believe that it will have sprung up from to-night and that I shall owe it to you.", "\"앞으로도 변함없이 함께할 우리의 교제 속에서—리처드와 에이다를 보살피면서, 그리고 부디 더 행복한 삶의 여러 장면 속에서도—혹시 당신이 제게서 예전보다 나아졌다고 진심으로 여길 만한 무언가를 발견하신다면, 그건 오늘 밤에서 싹튼 것이고 제가 당신께 빚진 것이라고 믿어주세요."], ["And never believe, dear dear Mr. Woodcourt, never believe that I forget this night or that while my heart beats it can be insensible to the pride and joy of having been beloved by you.”", "그리고 결코 믿지 마세요, 사랑하고 사랑하는 우드코트 씨, 제가 이 밤을 잊으리라고는, 또는 제 심장이 뛰는 한 당신께 사랑받았다는 그 자랑과 기쁨에 무감할 수 있으리라고는 결코 믿지 마세요.\""]], [["He took my hand and kissed it.", "그가 내 손을 잡고 입을 맞추었습니다."], ["He was like himself again, and I felt still more encouraged.", "그는 다시 예전의 그 사람으로 돌아왔고, 나는 한층 더 용기를 얻었습니다."]], [["“I am induced by what you said just now,”", "\"방금 하신 말씀을 듣고,\" 나는 말했습니다."], ["said I, “to hope that you have succeeded in your endeavour.”", "\"당신이 그 일에 성공하셨기를 바라게 되네요.\""]], [["“I have,” he answered.", "\"성공했소.\""], ["“With such help from Mr.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["Jarndyce as you who know him so well can imagine him to have rendered me, I have succeeded.”", "\"그분을 잘 아는 당신이라면 능히 짐작할 만한 그런 도움을 잔다이스 씨에게서 받아, 나는 성공했소.\""]], [["“Heaven bless him for it,”", "\"그분께 하늘의 축복이 있기를.\""], ["said I, giving him my hand; “and heaven bless you in all you do!”", "나는 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했습니다. \"그리고 당신이 하시는 모든 일에 하늘의 축복이 함께하기를!\""]], [["“I shall do it better for the wish,”", "\"그 바람 덕분에 나는 그 일을 더 잘 해낼 거요.\""], ["he answered; “it will make me enter on these new duties as on another sacred trust from you.”", "그가 대답했습니다. \"그 덕분에 나는 이 새로운 임무를, 당신이 내게 맡긴 또 하나의 거룩한 소임처럼 받들며 시작하게 될 테니까.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Richard!”", "리처드는요!\""], ["I exclaimed involuntarily, “What will he do when you are gone!”", "나도 모르게 외쳤습니다. \"당신이 떠나면 그이는 어떻게 하나요!\""]], [["“I am not required to go yet; I would not desert him, dear Miss Summerson, even if I were.”", "\"아직은 떠날 필요가 없소. 설령 떠나야 한다 해도, 친애하는 서머슨 양, 나는 그를 저버리지 않을 거요.\""]], [["One other thing I felt it needful to touch upon before he left me.", "그가 나를 떠나기 전에, 한 가지 더 짚어두어야 할 일이 있다고 느꼈습니다."], ["I knew that I should not be worthier of the love I could not take if I reserved it.", "받을 수 없는 그 사랑을, 만일 내가 마음속에 묻어둔다면 그 사랑에 더 어울리는 사람이 되지 못하리라는 걸 알았기 때문입니다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"우드코트 씨,\""], ["Woodcourt,”", "나는 말했습니다."], ["said I, “you will be glad to know from my lips before I say good night that in the future, which is clear and bright before me, I am most happy, most fortunate, have nothing to regret or desire.”", "\"안녕히 가시라는 인사를 드리기 전에, 제 입으로 직접 들려드리면 당신이 기뻐하실 거예요. 제 앞에 또렷하고 환하게 펼쳐진 미래 속에서, 저는 더없이 행복하고 더없이 복되며, 후회할 것도 바랄 것도 없답니다.\""]], [["It was indeed a glad hearing to him, he replied.", "그건 정말로 자기에게 기쁜 소식이라고 그가 대답했습니다."]], [["“From my childhood I have been,”", "\"저는 어린 시절부터,\" 나는 말했습니다."], ["said I, “the object of the untiring goodness of the best of human beings, to whom I am so bound by every tie of attachment, gratitude, and love, that nothing I could do in the compass of a life could express the feelings of a single day.”", "\"더없이 훌륭한 인간 한 분의 지칠 줄 모르는 자애를 받는 사람이었어요. 애정과 감사와 사랑의 온갖 끈으로 그분께 단단히 묶여 있어서, 제가 한평생을 바쳐 무엇을 한들 단 하루치의 마음조차 다 표현하지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“I share those feelings,”", "\"나도 그 마음을 함께 느끼오.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["“You speak of Mr. Jarndyce.”", "\"당신은 잔다이스 씨를 말하는 거로군요.\""]], [["“You know his virtues well,”", "\"당신은 그분의 미덕을 잘 알지요.\""], ["said I, “but few can know the greatness of his character as I know it.", "나는 말했습니다. \"하지만 제가 아는 만큼 그분 인품의 위대함을 아는 사람은 드물답니다."], ["All its highest and best qualities have been revealed to me in nothing more brightly than in the shaping out of that future in which I am so happy.", "그분의 가장 드높고 가장 훌륭한 자질이, 제가 이토록 행복해하는 그 미래를 빚어내신 일에서보다 더 환하게 드러난 적은 없었어요."], ["And if your highest homage and respect had not been his already—which I know they are—they would have been his, I think, on this assurance and in the feeling it would have awakened in you towards him for my sake.”", "그리고 설령 당신의 그 더없는 경의와 존경이 이미 그분의 것이 아니었더라도—실은 그분의 것임을 저는 알지만—이 다짐과, 또 저를 위해 그것이 당신 마음속에 그분을 향해 불러일으켰을 그 감정으로 인해, 그분의 것이 되었으리라고 저는 생각해요.\""]], [["He fervently replied that indeed indeed they would have been.", "그는 진정으로, 진정으로 그러했을 거라고 열렬히 대답했습니다."], ["I gave him my hand again.", "나는 다시 그에게 손을 내밀었습니다."]], [["“Good night,”", "\"안녕히 가세요.\""], ["I said, “Good-bye.”", "나는 말했습니다. \"잘 가세요.\""]], [["“The first until we meet to-morrow, the second as a farewell to this theme between us for ever.”", "\"앞엣것은 내일 다시 만날 때까지의 인사, 뒤엣것은 우리 사이에서 이 이야기를 영영 마무리하는 작별 인사로군요.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Good night; good-bye.”", "\"안녕히, 잘 가세요.\""]], [["He left me, and I stood at the dark window watching the street.", "그는 나를 떠났고, 나는 어두운 창가에 서서 거리를 바라보았습니다."], ["His love, in all its constancy and generosity, had come so suddenly upon me that he had not left me a minute when my fortitude gave way again and the street was blotted out by my rushing tears.", "한결같고도 너그러운 그의 사랑이 너무도 갑작스레 내게 밀려든 터라, 그가 나를 떠난 지 일 분도 채 안 되어 내 굳센 마음은 또다시 무너졌고, 거리는 쏟아지는 눈물에 가려 보이지 않았습니다."]], [["But they were not tears of regret and sorrow. No.", "그러나 그것은 후회와 슬픔의 눈물이 아니었습니다."], ["He had called me the beloved of his life and had said I would be evermore as dear to him as I was then, and I felt as if my heart would not hold the triumph of having heard those words.", "아니고말고요. 그는 나를 자기 생의 사랑하는 사람이라 불렀고, 지금 그러하듯 앞으로도 영원히 내가 그에게 소중하리라고 말했습니다."], ["My first wild thought had died away.", "나는 그 말을 들었다는 그 벅찬 기쁨을 내 심장이 다 담아내지 못할 것만 같았습니다."], ["It was not too late to hear them, for it was not too late to be animated by them to be good, true, grateful, and contented.", "처음의 그 걷잡을 수 없던 생각은 스러지고 없었습니다. 그 말을 듣기에 너무 늦지 않았으니, 그 말에 힘입어 선하고 참되고 감사하며 만족하는 사람이 되기에도 너무 늦지 않았기 때문입니다."], ["How easy my path, how much easier than his! Another Discovery", "내 앞길은 얼마나 평탄한지요, 그의 길보다 얼마나 더 평탄한지요!"]], [["I had not the courage to see any one that night.", "그날 밤 나는 누구도 마주할 용기가 없었습니다."], ["I had not even the courage to see myself, for I was afraid that my tears might a little reproach me.", "심지어 내 모습을 들여다볼 용기조차 없었으니, 내 눈물이 나를 조금이나마 나무랄까 두려웠기 때문입니다."], ["I went up to my room in the dark, and prayed in the dark, and lay down in the dark to sleep.", "나는 어둠 속에서 방으로 올라가, 어둠 속에서 기도하고, 어둠 속에 누워 잠을 청했습니다."], ["I had no need of any light to read my guardian’s letter by, for I knew it by heart.", "후견인의 편지를 읽는 데에는 어떤 불빛도 필요 없었으니, 나는 그 내용을 외우고 있었기 때문입니다."], ["I took it from the place where I kept it, and repeated its contents by its own clear light of integrity and love, and went to sleep with it on my pillow.", "나는 늘 간직해두는 자리에서 편지를 꺼내, 그 편지가 스스로 발하는 진실과 사랑의 맑은 빛으로 내용을 되뇌었고, 그것을 베개에 둔 채 잠이 들었습니다."]], [["I was up very early in the morning and called Charley to come for a walk.", "이튿날 아침 나는 아주 일찍 일어나 찰리를 불러 산책을 나섰습니다."], ["We bought flowers for the breakfast-table, and came back and arranged them, and were as busy as possible.", "우리는 아침 식탁에 올릴 꽃을 사 와서 꽂아 두었고, 더없이 분주하게 움직였습니다."], ["We were so early that I had a good time still for Charley’s lesson before breakfast; Charley (who was not in the least improved in the old defective article of grammar) came through it with great applause; and we were altogether very notable.", "어찌나 일찍 일어났는지 아침 식사 전에 찰리에게 공부를 가르칠 시간까지 넉넉히 남았습니다. 찰리는(예전부터 그토록 부실하던 문법 실력은 조금도 나아지지 않은 채로) 큰 박수를 받으며 그 시간을 견뎌냈고, 우리는 온통 대단한 사람들이 된 기분이었습니다."], ["When my guardian appeared he said, “Why, little woman, you look fresher than your flowers!”", "후견인이 나타나서 말했습니다. \"이런, 꼬마 아씨, 네가 네 꽃들보다 더 싱그러워 보이는구나!\""], ["And Mrs. Woodcourt repeated and translated a passage from the Mewlinnwillinwodd expressive of my being like a mountain with the sun upon it.", "그러자 우드코트 부인이 무얼린윌린워드의 한 구절을 되뇌고 번역해주었는데, 내가 햇살을 받은 산과 같다는 뜻이었습니다."]], [["This was all so pleasant that I hope it made me still more like the mountain than I had been before.", "이 모든 것이 너무도 즐거워서, 부디 이 덕분에 내가 전보다 더 그 산을 닮게 되었기를 바랐습니다."], ["After breakfast I waited my opportunity and peeped about a little until I saw my guardian in his own room—the room of last night—by himself.", "아침 식사를 마친 뒤 나는 기회를 기다리며 이리저리 살짝 엿보다가, 후견인이 자기 방에—어젯밤의 그 방에—혼자 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Then I made an excuse to go in with my housekeeping keys, shutting the door after me.", "그래서 살림 열쇠 꾸러미를 들고 들어갈 핑계를 만들어, 들어가서는 등 뒤로 문을 닫았습니다."]], [["“Well, Dame Durden?”", "\"그래, 더든 아주머니?\""], ["said my guardian; the post had brought him several letters, and he was writing.", "후견인이 말했습니다. 우편으로 편지가 여러 통 와 있었고, 그는 글을 쓰던 참이었습니다."], ["“You want money?”", "\"돈이 필요하니?\""]], [["“No, indeed, I have plenty in hand.”", "\"아니에요, 정말로 손에 넉넉히 있는걸요.\""]], [["“There never was such a Dame Durden,”", "\"더든 아주머니처럼 돈을 오래 쓰는 사람은 또 없을 거야.\""], ["said my guardian, “for making money last.”", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["He had laid down his pen and leaned back in his chair looking at me.", "그는 펜을 내려놓고 의자에 등을 기댄 채 나를 바라보았습니다."], ["I have often spoken of his bright face, but I thought I had never seen it look so bright and good.", "나는 그의 환한 얼굴을 자주 이야기해왔지만, 그날처럼 그렇게 환하고 선해 보인 적은 없는 것 같았습니다."], ["There was a high happiness upon it which made me think, “He has been doing some great kindness this morning.”", "그 얼굴에는 드높은 행복이 어려 있어서 나는 이렇게 생각했습니다. '오늘 아침 무슨 큰 선행을 베푸셨구나.'"]], [["“There never was,”", "\"더든 아주머니처럼 돈을 오래 쓰는 사람은,\""], ["said my guardian, musing as he smiled upon me, “such a Dame Durden for making money last.”", "후견인이 나를 보고 미소 지으며 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했습니다. \"정말 또 없을 거야.\""]], [["He had never yet altered his old manner.", "그는 아직도 그 오래된 태도를 한 번도 바꾸지 않았습니다."], ["I loved it and him so much that when I now went up to him and took my usual chair, which was always put at his side—for sometimes I read to him, and sometimes I talked to him, and sometimes I silently worked by him—I hardly liked to disturb it by laying my hand on his breast.", "나는 그 태도와 그를 너무도 사랑했기에, 이제 그에게 다가가 늘 그의 곁에 놓이는 내 의자에—이따금 나는 그에게 책을 읽어주고, 이따금 이야기를 나누고, 이따금 그의 곁에서 말없이 일을 했습니다—앉으면서, 그의 가슴에 손을 얹어 그 태도를 흩뜨리고 싶지 않았습니다."], ["But I found I did not disturb it at all.", "하지만 나는 조금도 그것을 흩뜨리지 않았음을 알게 되었습니다."]], [["“Dear guardian,”", "\"후견인님,\""], ["said I, “I want to speak to you.", "내가 말했습니다. \"드릴 말씀이 있어요."], ["Have I been remiss in anything?”", "제가 무언가 소홀했던 게 있나요?\""]], [["“Remiss in anything, my dear!”", "\"무언가 소홀했다니, 얘야!\""]], [["“Have I not been what I have meant to be since—I brought the answer to your letter, guardian?”", "\"제가—후견인님의 편지에 답을 가져다드린 뒤로—되고자 마음먹은 그 사람이 되지 못한 건가요?\""]], [["“You have been everything I could desire, my love.”", "\"너는 내가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이었단다, 얘야.\""]], [["“I am very glad indeed to hear that,”", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 정말로 무척 기뻐요.\""], ["I returned.", "내가 대답했습니다."], ["“You know, you said to me, was this the mistress of Bleak House.", "\"아시잖아요, 후견인님이 제게 이 사람이 황폐한 집의 안주인이 맞느냐고 물으셨지요."], ["And I said, yes.”", "그리고 제가 그렇다고 했고요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said my guardian, nodding his head.", "후견인이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했습니다."], ["He had put his arm about me as if there were something to protect me from and looked in my face, smiling.", "그는 무언가로부터 나를 지켜주려는 듯 내 어깨에 팔을 둘렀고, 미소 지으며 내 얼굴을 들여다보았습니다."]], [["“Since then,”", "\"그 뒤로,\""], ["said I, “we have never spoken on the subject except once.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"우리는 그 이야기를 딱 한 번 말고는 한 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“And then I said Bleak House was thinning fast; and so it was, my dear.”", "\"그때 내가 황폐한 집이 점점 비어간다고 했지. 정말 그랬으니까, 얘야.\""]], [["“And I said,”", "\"그리고 제가 말했어요,\""], ["I timidly reminded him, “but its mistress remained.”", "나는 머뭇거리며 그에게 일깨워주었습니다. \"그래도 그 안주인은 남아 있다고요.\""]], [["He still held me in the same protecting manner and with the same bright goodness in his face.", "그는 여전히 똑같이 지켜주는 태도로 나를 안은 채, 얼굴에는 여전히 똑같이 환한 선함을 띠고 있었습니다."]], [["“Dear guardian,”", "\"후견인님,\""], ["said I, “I know how you have felt all that has happened, and how considerate you have been.", "내가 말했습니다. \"그동안 일어난 모든 일에 대해 후견인님이 어떻게 느끼셨는지, 또 얼마나 배려해주셨는지 저는 알아요."], ["As so much time has passed, and as you spoke only this morning of my being so well again, perhaps you expect me to renew the subject.", "시간이 이렇게 많이 흘렀고, 게다가 바로 오늘 아침 제가 다시 건강해졌다고 말씀하셨으니, 어쩌면 제가 그 이야기를 다시 꺼내기를 기다리시는지도 모르겠어요."], ["Perhaps I ought to do so.", "어쩌면 제가 그래야 하는 거겠지요."], ["I will be the mistress of Bleak House when you please.”", "후견인님이 원하실 때 저는 황폐한 집의 안주인이 되겠어요.\""]], [["“See,”", "\"보렴,\""], ["he returned gaily, “what a sympathy there must be between us!", "그가 명랑하게 대답했습니다. \"우리 사이에 얼마나 마음이 통하는지!"], ["I have had nothing else, poor Rick excepted—it’s a large exception—in my mind.", "가엾은 릭을 빼면—그게 큰 예외이긴 하다만—내 머릿속엔 다른 생각이 없었단다."], ["When you came in, I was full of it.", "네가 들어왔을 때 나는 온통 그 생각뿐이었어."], ["When shall we give Bleak House its mistress, little woman?”", "황폐한 집에 언제 안주인을 들일까, 꼬마 아씨?\""]], [["“When you please.”", "\"원하실 때요.\""]], [["“Next month?”", "\"다음 달은 어떠니?\""]], [["“Next month, dear guardian.”", "\"다음 달에요, 후견인님.\""]], [["“The day on which I take the happiest and best step of my life—the day on which I shall be a man more exulting and more enviable than any other man in the world—the day on which I give Bleak House its little mistress—shall be next month then,”", "\"내가 내 평생 가장 행복하고 가장 멋진 한 걸음을 내딛는 날—이 세상 어느 누구보다 더 기뻐하고 더 부러움받을 사람이 되는 날—황폐한 집에 그 어여쁜 안주인을 들이는 날, 그렇다면 그날은 다음 달이로구나.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["I put my arms round his neck and kissed him just as I had done on the day when I brought my answer.", "나는 그의 목을 두 팔로 감싸 안고, 내가 답을 가져다드린 그날 그랬던 것처럼 그에게 입을 맞추었습니다."]], [["A servant came to the door to announce Mr. Bucket, which was quite unnecessary, for Mr.", "하인 하나가 문가로 와서 버킷 씨의 방문을 알렸는데, 그것은 전혀 필요 없는 일이었습니다."], ["Bucket was already looking in over the servant’s shoulder.", "버킷 씨가 이미 하인의 어깨너머로 안을 들여다보고 있었으니까요. \"잔다이스 씨, 그리고 서머슨 양.\""], ["“Mr. Jarndyce and Miss Summerson,”", "그가 다소 숨을 헐떡이며 말했습니다."], ["said he, rather out of breath, “with all apologies for intruding, WILL you allow me to order up a person that’s on the stairs and that objects to being left there in case of becoming the subject of observations in his absence?", "\"불쑥 들어와 죄송합니다만, 계단에 있는 사람 하나를 올려 보내도록 명해도 되겠습니까? 그 사람이 없는 사이에 자기가 입길에 오를까 봐 거기 혼자 남는 걸 마다하는지라 말입니다."], ["Thank you.", "고맙습니다."], ["Be so good as chair that there member in this direction, will you?”", "자, 저기 저 양반을 이쪽으로 모셔 오시오, 알겠소?\""], ["said Mr. Bucket, beckoning over the banisters.", "버킷 씨가 난간 너머로 손짓하며 말했습니다."]], [["This singular request produced an old man in a black skull-cap, unable to walk, who was carried up by a couple of bearers and deposited in the room near the door.", "이 묘한 요청 끝에 검은 두건을 쓴 노인 하나가 나타났는데, 걸을 수 없는 처지라 짐꾼 둘이 들쳐 메고 올라와 문 가까이에 내려놓았습니다."], ["Mr. Bucket immediately got rid of the bearers, mysteriously shut the door, and bolted it.", "버킷 씨는 곧장 짐꾼들을 내보내고, 알 수 없는 태도로 문을 닫더니 빗장을 질렀습니다."]], [["“Now you see, Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"자, 보십시오, 잔다이스 씨.\""], ["he then began, putting down his hat and opening his subject with a flourish of his well-remembered finger, “you know me, and Miss Summerson knows me.", "그가 모자를 내려놓고 그 유명한 손가락을 한 번 멋들어지게 휘두르며 본론을 꺼냈습니다. \"당신은 저를 아시고, 서머슨 양도 저를 아십니다."], ["This gentleman likewise knows me, and his name is Smallweed.", "이 양반도 마찬가지로 저를 알지요. 이름은 스몰위드입니다."], ["The discounting line is his line principally, and he’s what you may call a dealer in bills.", "주로 어음 할인이 이 양반 일이라, 말하자면 어음 장수라고나 할까요."], ["That’s about what YOU are, you know, ain’t you?” said Mr.", "당신이 하는 게 대체로 그것 아니오, 안 그렇소?\""], ["Bucket, stopping a little to address the gentleman in question, who was exceedingly suspicious of him.", "버킷 씨는 잠시 멈추고, 자신을 몹시 미심쩍어하는 그 양반에게 말을 건넸습니다."]], [["He seemed about to dispute this designation of himself when he was seized with a violent fit of coughing.", "그는 자신에 대한 이런 호칭에 이의를 제기하려는 듯하더니, 갑자기 격렬한 기침 발작에 사로잡혔습니다."]], [["“Now, moral, you know!” said Mr.", "\"자, 교훈입니다, 아시겠소!\""], ["Bucket, improving the accident.", "버킷 씨가 그 우연을 활용해 말했습니다."], ["“Don’t you contradict when there ain’t no occasion, and you won’t be took in that way.", "\"그럴 일도 아닌데 반박하지 마시오. 그러면 그런 식으로 당하지 않을 테니."], ["Now, Mr. Jarndyce, I address myself to you.", "자, 잔다이스 씨, 당신께 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["I’ve been negotiating with this gentleman on behalf of Sir Leicester Dedlock, Baronet, and one way and another I’ve been in and out and about his premises a deal.", "저는 준남작 레스터 데드록 경을 대신해 이 양반과 협상을 해왔고, 이래저래 이 양반 거처를 꽤 들락거렸습니다."], ["His premises are the premises formerly occupied by Krook, marine store dealer—a relation of this gentleman’s that you saw in his lifetime if I don’t mistake?”", "그 거처라는 게 예전에 크룩이라는 자가 쓰던 데인데요, 선박 잡화상이었지요—이 양반의 친척으로, 제가 잘못 안 게 아니라면 살아생전에 당신도 본 적이 있는 자입니다만?\""]], [["My guardian replied, “Yes.”", "후견인이 대답했습니다. \"그렇소.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"그렇지요!"], ["You are to understand,” said Mr.", "이렇게 이해하시면 됩니다.\""], ["Bucket, “that this gentleman he come into Krook’s property, and a good deal of magpie property there was.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다. \"이 양반이 크룩의 재산을 물려받았는데, 까치가 모아둔 것 같은 잡동사니가 한가득이었지요."], ["Vast lots of waste-paper among the rest.", "그중에 폐지가 엄청난 더미로 있었습니다."], ["Lord bless you, of no use to nobody!”", "맙소사, 누구한테도 아무 쓸모없는 것들이었지요!\""]], [["The cunning of Mr. Bucket’s eye and the masterly manner in which he contrived, without a look or a word against which his watchful auditor could protest, to let us know that he stated the case according to previous agreement and could say much more of Mr. Smallweed if he thought it advisable, deprived us of any merit in quite understanding him. His difficulty was increased by Mr. Smallweed’s being deaf as well as suspicious and watching his face with the closest attention.", "버킷 씨의 눈에 어린 교활함이며, 경계를 늦추지 않는 청자가 트집 잡을 만한 눈짓 하나 말 한마디 없이도, 자기가 사전에 합의한 대로 사건을 진술하는 것이며 마음만 먹으면 스몰위드 씨에 대해 훨씬 많은 것을 말할 수 있다는 사실을 우리에게 슬쩍 알려주는 그 능란한 솜씨 탓에, 우리가 그를 제대로 이해하는 데에는 우리 자신의 공이 한 톨도 들어가지 않았습니다. 스몰위드 씨가 의심이 많은 데다 귀까지 먹어, 버킷 씨의 얼굴을 더없이 바짝 주의 깊게 지켜보는 통에 그의 어려움은 한층 커졌습니다."]], [["“Among them odd heaps of old papers, this gentleman, when he comes into the property, naturally begins to rummage, don’t you see?”", "\"그 헌 서류 더미 속을, 이 양반이 재산을 물려받았으니 당연히 뒤지기 시작하지요, 안 그렇겠습니까?\""], ["said Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“To which?", "\"뭐를 어쩐다고?"], ["Say that again,”", "다시 말해보시오.\""], ["cried Mr. Smallweed in a shrill, sharp voice.", "스몰위드 씨가 새되고 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤습니다."]], [["“To rummage,”", "\"뒤진다고요.\""], ["repeated Mr. Bucket.", "버킷 씨가 되풀이했습니다."], ["“Being a prudent man and accustomed to take care of your own affairs, you begin to rummage among the papers as you have come into; don’t you?”", "\"신중한 사람인 데다 제 일을 챙기는 데 익숙하니, 물려받은 서류들 사이를 뒤지기 시작하지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“Of course I do,”", "\"당연히 그러지.\""], ["cried Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 외쳤습니다."]], [["“Of course you do,” said Mr.", "\"당연히 그러시지요.\""], ["Bucket conversationally, “and much to blame you would be if you didn’t.", "버킷 씨가 한담하듯 말했습니다. \"안 그러면 오히려 크게 나무랄 일이고요."], ["And so you chance to find, you know,” Mr.", "그렇게 해서 우연히 발견하게 되지요, 아시겠습니까,\""], ["Bucket went on, stooping over him with an air of cheerful raillery which Mr.", "버킷 씨가 명랑하게 놀리는 투로—스몰위드 씨는 결코 그 투에 장단을 맞추지 않았습니다만—그에게 몸을 숙이며 말을 이었습니다."], ["Smallweed by no means reciprocated, “and so you chance to find, you know, a paper with the signature of Jarndyce to it.", "\"그렇게 해서 우연히, 잔다이스라는 서명이 든 서류 한 장을 발견하지요."], ["Don’t you?”", "안 그렇소?\""]], [["Mr. Smallweed glanced with a troubled eye at us and grudgingly nodded assent.", "스몰위드 씨가 불안한 눈으로 우리를 흘끔거리더니, 마지못해 고개를 끄덕여 수긍했습니다."]], [["“And coming to look at that paper at your full leisure and convenience—all in good time, for you’re not curious to read it, and why should you be?—what do you find it to be but a will, you see.", "\"그래서 짬을 내 느긋하게 그 서류를 들여다보는데—천천히요, 당신은 그걸 읽는 데 호기심이 없으니까요, 또 그럴 이유가 어디 있겠습니까?—그게 다름 아닌 유언장이라는 걸 알게 되지요."], ["That’s the drollery of it,”", "그게 우스운 대목입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Bucket with the same lively air of recalling a joke for the enjoyment of Mr.", "버킷 씨가 스몰위드 씨를 즐겁게 해주려고 농담을 떠올리는 듯 여전히 활기찬 태도로 말했습니다."], ["Smallweed, who still had the same crest-fallen appearance of not enjoying it at all; “what do you find it to be but a will?”", "스몰위드 씨는 여전히 전혀 즐겁지 않은 듯 풀이 죽은 모습이었습니다. \"그게 다름 아닌 유언장이라는 걸 알게 되지요?\""]], [["“I don’t know that it’s good as a will or as anything else,”", "\"그게 유언장으로든 뭐로든 효력이 있는지 난 모르오.\""], ["snarled Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 으르렁대듯 말했습니다."]], [["Mr.", "버킷 씨는 잠시 그 노인을 노려보았는데—그는 의자 속으로 미끄러지듯 쪼그라들어 그저 한 보따리 같은 꼴이 되어 있었습니다—마치 당장이라도 덮칠 기세였습니다."], ["Bucket eyed the old man for a moment—he had slipped and shrunk down in his chair into a mere bundle—as if he were much disposed to pounce upon him; nevertheless, he continued to bend over him with the same agreeable air, keeping the corner of one of his eyes upon us.", "그래도 그는 한쪽 눈꼬리로 우리를 살피며, 여전히 상냥한 태도로 그에게 몸을 숙인 채 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“Notwithstanding which,”", "\"그런데도 말입니다,\""], ["said Mr.", "버킷 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Bucket, “you get a little doubtful and uncomfortable in your mind about it, having a very tender mind of your own.”", "\"당신은 그것 때문에 마음이 좀 미심쩍고 불편해집니다. 워낙 여린 마음을 지닌 분이니까요.\""]], [["“Eh?", "\"엥?"], ["What do you say I have got of my own?”", "내가 뭘 지녔다고?\""], ["asked Mr. Smallweed with his hand to his ear.", "스몰위드 씨가 한 손을 귀에 대고 물었습니다."]], [["“A very tender mind.”", "\"아주 여린 마음 말입니다.\""]], [["“Ho!", "\"호!"], ["Well, go on,”", "그래, 계속하시오.\""], ["said Mr. Smallweed.", "스몰위드 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“And as you’ve heard a good deal mentioned regarding a celebrated Chancery will case of the same name, and as you know what a card Krook was for buying all manner of old pieces of furniter, and books, and papers, and what not, and never liking to part with ’em, and always a-going to teach himself to read, you begin to think—and you never was more correct in your born days—‘Ecod, if I don’t look about me, I may get into trouble regarding this will.’”", "\"그리고 똑같은 이름의 유명한 대법원 유언 소송에 관해 이런저런 말을 많이 들어왔고, 또 크룩이라는 자가 온갖 헌 가구며 책이며 서류며 별별 것을 다 사들이고는 절대 내놓지 않으려 하고, 늘 제 손으로 글을 깨치겠다고 벼르던 위인이었다는 걸 아니, 당신은 생각하기 시작하지요—그리고 당신 평생 이보다 더 옳았던 적은 없을 겁니다—'이런, 정신 똑바로 차리지 않으면 이 유언장 때문에 곤경에 빠질지도 모르겠군.'\""]], [["“Now, mind how you put it, Bucket,”", "\"자, 말조심해서 하시오, 버킷.\""], ["cried the old man anxiously with his hand at his ear.", "노인이 한 손을 귀에 댄 채 불안하게 외쳤습니다. \"또렷이 말하시오."], ["“Speak up; none of your brimstone tricks.", "당신 그 유황불 같은 잔꾀는 그만두고."], ["Pick me up; I want to hear better.", "나를 일으켜 주시오. 더 잘 듣고 싶소."], ["Oh, Lord, I am shaken to bits!”", "오, 맙소사, 몸이 다 산산조각 나는구나!\""]], [["Mr. Bucket had certainly picked him up at a dart.", "버킷 씨는 분명 잽싸게 그를 일으켜 주었습니다."], ["However, as soon as he could be heard through Mr.", "그러나 스몰위드 씨의 기침과 \"오, 내 뼈야!"], ["Smallweed’s coughing and his vicious ejaculations of “Oh, my bones! Oh, dear!", "오, 이런!"], ["I’ve no breath in my body!", "숨이 막혀 죽겠다!"], ["I’m worse than the chattering, clattering, brimstone pig at home!”", "집에서 떠들고 덜그럭거리는 그 유황불 돼지보다 내 꼴이 더 못하구나!\""], ["Mr. Bucket proceeded in the same convivial manner as before.", "하는 사나운 외침을 뚫고 말이 들릴 만해지자, 버킷 씨는 앞서처럼 다정한 태도로 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“So, as I happen to be in the habit of coming about your premises, you take me into your confidence, don’t you?”", "\"그래서, 제가 마침 당신 거처를 들락거리는 처지이다 보니, 당신은 저를 믿고 속내를 털어놓지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["I think it would be impossible to make an admission with more ill will and a worse grace than Mr. Smallweed displayed when he admitted this, rendering it perfectly evident that Mr.", "스몰위드 씨가 이것을 인정할 때 드러낸 것보다 더 마지못해하고 더 볼썽사납게 무언가를 시인하기란 불가능하지 싶었습니다."], ["Bucket was the very last person he would have thought of taking into his confidence if he could by any possibility have kept him out of it.", "그 모습을 보면, 버킷 씨를 어떻게든 떼어낼 수만 있었다면 그가 결코 속내를 털어놓을 생각조차 안 했을 마지막 인물이라는 게 더없이 분명히 드러났습니다."]], [["“And I go into the business with you—very pleasant we are over it; and I confirm you in your well-founded fears that you will get yourself into a most precious line if you don’t come out with that there will,”", "\"그래서 저는 당신과 함께 이 일에 뛰어듭니다—아주 화기애애하게요. 그리고 그 유언장을 내놓지 않으면 더없이 골치 아픈 처지에 빠질 거라는, 근거 있는 당신의 두려움을 제가 굳혀주지요.\""], ["said Mr.", "버킷 씨가 힘주어 말했습니다."], ["Bucket emphatically; “and accordingly you arrange with me that it shall be delivered up to this present Mr. Jarndyce, on no conditions.", "\"그래서 당신은 그것을 지금 이 자리에 계신 잔다이스 씨께 아무 조건 없이 넘기기로 저와 약조합니다."], ["If it should prove to be valuable, you trusting yourself to him for your reward; that’s about where it is, ain’t it?”", "만약 그게 값진 것으로 드러나면, 보상은 잔다이스 씨를 믿고 맡기기로 하고요. 대략 그렇지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“That’s what was agreed,”", "\"그게 합의한 거요.\""], ["Mr. Smallweed assented with the same bad grace.", "스몰위드 씨가 여전히 볼썽사납게 수긍했습니다."]], [["“In consequence of which,” said Mr.", "\"그렇기에,\""], ["Bucket, dismissing his agreeable manner all at once and becoming strictly business-like, “you’ve got that will upon your person at the present time, and the only thing that remains for you to do is just to out with it!”", "버킷 씨가 그 상냥한 태도를 단번에 거두고 철저히 사무적으로 변하며 말했습니다. \"당신은 지금 그 유언장을 몸에 지니고 있고, 이제 당신이 할 일은 그저 그것을 꺼내는 것뿐입니다!\""]], [["Having given us one glance out of the watching corner of his eye, and having given his nose one triumphant rub with his forefinger, Mr.", "경계하는 눈꼬리로 우리에게 한 번 눈길을 던지고, 집게손가락으로 의기양양하게 코를 한 번 비빈 뒤, 버킷 씨는 그 속내를 나눈 벗에게 시선을 못 박은 채, 서류를 받아 후견인에게 건넬 채비로 한 손을 내밀고 서 있었습니다."], ["Bucket stood with his eyes fastened on his confidential friend and his hand stretched forth ready to take the paper and present it to my guardian. It was not produced without much reluctance and many declarations on the part of Mr.", "그것은 스몰위드 씨가 자기는 가난하고 부지런한 사람이라느니, 정직하게 굴다가 손해 보지 않게 해주는 일은 잔다이스 씨의 명예에 맡기겠다느니 하는 숱한 항변 끝에야, 그것도 몹시 마지못해 나왔습니다."], ["Smallweed that he was a poor industrious man and that he left it to Mr. Jarndyce’s honour not to let him lose by his honesty.", "그는 조금씩, 아주 천천히 가슴 주머니에서 얼룩지고 빛바랜 서류 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 겉은 심하게 그을렸고 가장자리는 조금 탔으니, 마치 오래전에 불 속에 던져졌다가 황급히 다시 낚아챈 것 같았습니다."], ["Little by little he very slowly took from a breast-pocket a stained, discoloured paper which was much singed upon the outside and a little burnt at the edges, as if it had long ago been thrown upon a fire and hastily snatched off again. Mr.", "버킷 씨는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 마술사 같은 손놀림으로 그 서류를 스몰위드 씨에게서 잔다이스 씨에게로 옮겼습니다."], ["Bucket lost no time in transferring this paper, with the dexterity of a conjuror, from Mr. Smallweed to Mr.", "그는 그것을 후견인에게 건네며 손가락 뒤로 속삭였습니다."], ["Jarndyce. As he gave it to my guardian, he whispered behind his fingers, “Hadn’t settled how to make their market of it.", "\"이걸로 어떻게 한몫 챙길지 아직 정하지 못한 자들입니다."], ["Quarrelled and hinted about it.", "서로 다투고 들먹였지요."], ["I laid out twenty pound upon it.", "제가 이십 파운드를 썼습니다."], ["First the avaricious grandchildren split upon him on account of their objections to his living so unreasonably long, and then they split on one another.", "처음엔 욕심 사나운 손주들이 저자가 어처구니없이 오래 산다고 못마땅해하며 등을 돌렸고, 그다음엔 저희끼리 등을 돌렸지요."], ["Lord!", "맙소사!"], ["There ain’t one of the family that wouldn’t sell the other for a pound or two, except the old lady—and she’s only out of it because she’s too weak in her mind to drive a bargain.”", "저 집안엔 일이 파운드에 제 식구를 팔아넘기지 않을 자가 하나도 없습니다. 저 노파만 빼고요—그것도 머리가 너무 흐려져서 흥정을 못 하니 끼지 못하는 것뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Mr Bucket,”", "\"버킷 씨,\""], ["said my guardian aloud, “whatever the worth of this paper may be to any one, my obligations are great to you; and if it be of any worth, I hold myself bound to see Mr.", "후견인이 소리 내어 말했습니다. \"이 서류가 누구에게 어떤 값어치가 있든, 나는 당신에게 큰 신세를 졌소."], ["Smallweed remunerated accordingly.”", "그리고 만약 이게 조금이라도 값어치가 있다면, 스몰위드 씨가 그에 걸맞게 보상받도록 챙기는 것을 내 의무로 여기겠소.\""]], [["“Not according to your merits, you know,” said Mr.", "\"당신이 한 만큼이 아니라, 아시겠소,\""], ["Bucket in friendly explanation to Mr. Smallweed.", "버킷 씨가 스몰위드 씨에게 다정하게 설명하듯 말했습니다."], ["“Don’t you be afraid of that.", "\"그 점은 걱정 마시오."], ["According to its value.”", "그 값어치만큼 말입니다.\""]], [["“That is what I mean,”", "\"내 말이 그 뜻이오.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“You may observe, Mr. Bucket, that I abstain from examining this paper myself.", "\"버킷 씨, 보다시피 나는 이 서류를 내 손으로 살펴보는 것을 삼가고 있소."], ["The plain truth is, I have forsworn and abjured the whole business these many years, and my soul is sick of it.", "솔직히 말하면, 나는 이 일 전체를 여러 해 동안 끊고 멀리해왔고, 내 영혼은 그것에 진저리가 나 있소."], ["But Miss Summerson and I will immediately place the paper in the hands of my solicitor in the cause, and its existence shall be made known without delay to all other parties interested.”", "하지만 서머슨 양과 나는 곧장 이 서류를 그 소송을 맡은 내 사무 변호사에게 넘길 것이고, 그 존재는 지체 없이 이해관계가 있는 다른 모든 당사자에게 알릴 것이오.\""]], [["“Mr. Jarndyce can’t say fairer than that, you understand,”", "\"잔다이스 씨가 이보다 더 공정하게 말씀하실 수는 없지요, 아시겠소.\""], ["observed Mr. Bucket to his fellow-visitor.", "버킷 씨가 함께 온 사람에게 말했습니다."], ["“And it being now made clear to you that nobody’s a-going to be wronged—which must be a great relief to YOUR mind—we may proceed with the ceremony of chairing you home again.”", "\"그리고 누구도 부당한 일을 당하지 않으리라는 게 이제 당신께 분명해졌으니—그건 당신 마음에 큰 위안이 될 게 틀림없습니다만—이제 당신을 다시 가마에 태워 댁으로 모셔 가는 의식으로 넘어가도 되겠지요.\""]], [["He unbolted the door, called in the bearers, wished us good morning, and with a look full of meaning and a crook of his finger at parting went his way.", "그는 빗장을 풀고 짐꾼들을 불러들여, 우리에게 아침 인사를 건네고는, 의미심장한 눈빛과 함께 헤어지면서 손가락을 한 번 까딱이고 제 갈 길을 갔습니다."]], [["We went our way too, which was to Lincoln’s Inn, as quickly as possible. Mr.", "우리도 우리 갈 길을 갔으니, 곧 링컨 법학원으로 되도록 빨리 향했습니다."], ["Kenge was disengaged, and we found him at his table in his dusty room with the inexpressive-looking books and the piles of papers.", "켄지 씨는 마침 한가했고, 우리는 그가 먼지 낀 방의 책상 앞에 앉아 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Chairs having been placed for us by Mr. Guppy, Mr.", "표정 없어 보이는 책들과 서류 더미에 둘러싸인 채였습니다."], ["Kenge expressed the surprise and gratification he felt at the unusual sight of Mr. Jarndyce in his office.", "거피 씨가 우리에게 의자를 놓아주자, 켄지 씨는 잔다이스 씨가 자기 사무실에 나타난 보기 드문 광경에 느낀 놀라움과 기쁨을 표했습니다."], ["He turned over his double eye-glass as he spoke and was more Conversation Kenge than ever.", "그는 말하는 동안 이중 안경을 만지작거렸고, 그 어느 때보다 더 입심 좋은 켄지다웠습니다."]], [["“I hope,” said Mr.", "\"바라건대,\""], ["Kenge, “that the genial influence of Miss Summerson,”", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양의 다정한 감화가,\""], ["he bowed to me, “may have induced Mr.", "그가 내게 고개를 숙였습니다."], ["Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 씨로 하여금,\""], ["he bowed to him, “to forego some little of his animosity towards a cause and towards a court which are—shall I say, which take their place in the stately vista of the pillars of our profession?”", "그가 그에게 고개를 숙였습니다. \"어떤 소송과 어떤 법정—말하자면, 우리 직업이라는 기둥들이 늘어선 그 위풍당당한 회랑에 한자리를 차지하는—에 대한 그 적의를 조금이나마 누그러뜨리도록 이끌었기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“I am inclined to think,”", "\"내 생각에는,\""], ["returned my guardian, “that Miss Summerson has seen too much of the effects of the court and the cause to exert any influence in their favour.", "후견인이 대답했습니다. \"서머슨 양이 그 법정과 그 소송의 폐해를 너무 많이 보아왔기에, 그것들에 유리한 어떤 영향도 끼치지 못할 것 같소."], ["Nevertheless, they are a part of the occasion of my being here.", "그렇지만 그것들도 내가 여기 온 까닭의 한 부분이긴 하오."], ["Mr. Kenge, before I lay this paper on your desk and have done with it, let me tell you how it has come into my hands.”", "켄지 씨, 내가 이 서류를 당신 책상에 내려놓고 이 일에서 손을 떼기 전에, 이것이 어떻게 내 손에 들어왔는지 말해두겠소.\""]], [["He did so shortly and distinctly.", "그는 그것을 짧고 또렷하게 이야기했습니다."]], [["“It could not, sir,”", "\"선생님,\" 켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["said Mr. Kenge, “have been stated more plainly and to the purpose if it had been a case at law.”", "\"그게 소송 사건이었다 한들, 이보다 더 명확하고 요령 있게 진술될 수는 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Did you ever know English law, or equity either, plain and to the purpose?”", "\"영국 법이든 형평법이든, 명확하고 요령 있던 적이 있던가요?\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Oh, fie!”", "\"오, 저런!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["At first he had not seemed to attach much importance to the paper, but when he saw it he appeared more interested, and when he had opened and read a little of it through his eye-glass, he became amazed.", "처음에 그는 그 서류에 별 중요성을 두지 않는 듯했으나, 막상 그것을 보자 더 흥미를 느끼는 것 같았고, 안경 너머로 그것을 펼쳐 조금 읽고 나자 깜짝 놀랐습니다."], ["“Mr. Jarndyce,”", "\"잔다이스 씨,\""], ["he said, looking off it, “you have perused this?”", "그가 서류에서 눈을 떼며 말했습니다. \"이걸 읽어보셨습니까?\""]], [["“Not I!”", "\"천만에!\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대답했습니다."]], [["“But, my dear sir,”", "\"하지만, 선생님,\""], ["said Mr. Kenge, “it is a will of later date than any in the suit.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["It appears to be all in the testator’s handwriting.", "\"이건 그 소송에 걸린 어떤 것보다도 나중 날짜의 유언장입니다."], ["It is duly executed and attested.", "전부 유언자의 친필인 것으로 보이고요."], ["And even if intended to be cancelled, as might possibly be supposed to be denoted by these marks of fire, it is NOT cancelled.", "정식으로 작성되고 증인까지 갖추었습니다. 그리고 설령 이 불에 탄 흔적이 어쩌면 그렇게 추정될 만큼 폐기하려던 의도였다 하더라도, 폐기된 것은 아닙니다."], ["Here it is, a perfect instrument!”", "자, 여기 완벽한 증서가 있습니다!\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래서!\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“What is that to me?”", "\"그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?\""]], [["“Mr. Guppy!”", "\"거피 군!\""], ["cried Mr. Kenge, raising his voice.", "켄지 씨가 목소리를 높여 외쳤습니다."], ["“I beg your pardon, Mr.", "\"실례했습니다, 잔다이스 씨.\""], ["Jarndyce.”", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["“Sir.” “Mr.", "\"사이먼드 법학원의 볼스 씨께."], ["Vholes of Symond’s Inn. My compliments.", "안부 전하고."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "잔다이스 대 잔다이스."], ["Glad to speak with him.”", "만나서 얘기하고 싶다고.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy disappeared.", "거피 씨가 사라졌습니다."]], [["“You ask me what is this to you, Mr. Jarndyce.", "\"잔다이스 씨, 이게 당신과 무슨 상관이냐고 물으셨지요."], ["If you had perused this document, you would have seen that it reduces your interest considerably, though still leaving it a very handsome one, still leaving it a very handsome one,” said Mr.", "만약 이 문서를 읽어보셨다면, 이것이 당신의 지분을 상당히 줄이긴 하나, 그래도 여전히 아주 후한 몫을 남겨준다는 것을, 여전히 아주 후한 몫을 남겨준다는 것을 보셨을 겁니다.\""], ["Kenge, waving his hand persuasively and blandly. “You would further have seen that the interests of Mr.", "켄지 씨가 손을 설득하듯 부드럽게 내저으며 말했습니다."], ["Richard Carstone and of Miss Ada Clare, now Mrs. Richard Carstone, are very materially advanced by it.”", "\"더 나아가 이것이 리처드 카스톤 씨와 에이다 클레어 양, 이제는 리처드 카스톤 부인이 된 분의 지분을 아주 크게 높여준다는 것도 보셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Kenge,”", "\"켄지 씨,\""], ["said my guardian, “if all the flourishing wealth that the suit brought into this vile court of Chancery could fall to my two young cousins, I should be well contented.", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"그 소송이 이 비열한 대법원으로 끌어들인 그 번지르르한 재산이 전부 내 두 어린 사촌에게 돌아갈 수 있다면, 나는 더없이 만족하겠소."], ["But do you ask ME to believe that any good is to come of Jarndyce and Jarndyce?”", "하지만 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서 무슨 좋은 것이 나올 거라고 나더러 믿으라는 거요?\""]], [["“Oh, really, Mr. Jarndyce!", "\"오, 정말이지, 잔다이스 씨!"], ["Prejudice, prejudice.", "편견이군요, 편견입니다."], ["My dear sir, this is a very great country, a very great country.", "선생님, 여긴 아주 위대한 나라입니다, 아주 위대한 나라예요."], ["Its system of equity is a very great system, a very great system.", "그 형평법 제도는 아주 위대한 제도이고요, 아주 위대한 제도입니다."], ["Really, really!”", "정말로, 정말이라니까요!\""]], [["My guardian said no more, and Mr. Vholes arrived.", "후견인은 더 말하지 않았고, 볼스 씨가 도착했습니다."], ["He was modestly impressed by Mr. Kenge’s professional eminence.", "그는 켄지 씨의 직업적 명성에 겸손하게 감복한 듯했습니다."]], [["“How do you do, Mr. Vholes?", "\"안녕하시오, 볼스 씨?"], ["Will you be so good as to take a chair here by me and look over this paper?”", "여기 내 옆자리에 앉아서 이 서류를 좀 살펴봐 주시겠소?\""]], [["Mr. Vholes did as he was asked and seemed to read it every word. He was not excited by it, but he was not excited by anything. When he had well examined it, he retired with Mr. Kenge into a window, and shading his mouth with his black glove, spoke to him at some length. I was not surprised to observe Mr. Kenge inclined to dispute what he said before he had said much, for I knew that no two people ever did agree about anything in Jarndyce and Jarndyce. But he seemed to get the better of Mr. Kenge too in a conversation that sounded as if it were almost composed of the words “Receiver-General,” “Accountant-General,” “report,” “estate,” and “costs.” When they had finished, they came back to Mr. Kenge’s table and spoke aloud.", "볼스 씨는 청한 대로 했고, 한 단어 한 단어 읽는 듯했습니다. 그는 그것에 흥분하지 않았는데, 사실 그는 무엇에도 흥분하는 법이 없었습니다. 충분히 살펴본 뒤, 그는 켄지 씨와 함께 창가로 물러나 검은 장갑을 낀 손으로 입을 가린 채 한참 동안 이야기를 나누었습니다. 켄지 씨가 그의 말이 채 끝나기도 전에 반박하려 드는 것을 보고도 나는 놀라지 않았으니, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스에서는 어떤 두 사람도 무엇 하나 의견이 일치한 적이 없음을 알기 때문이었습니다. 하지만 그는 켄지 씨까지도 이겨내는 듯했는데, 그 대화는 거의 \"재산관리관\", \"회계관\", \"보고서\", \"재산\", \"비용\"이라는 말들로만 이루어진 것처럼 들렸습니다. 이야기를 마치자 그들은 켄지 씨의 책상으로 돌아와 소리 내어 말했습니다."]], [["“Well!", "\"그래!"], ["But this is a very remarkable document, Mr. Vholes,”", "하지만 이건 아주 주목할 만한 문서입니다, 볼스 씨.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["Mr.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Vholes said, “Very much so.”", "\"정말 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“And a very important document, Mr. Vholes,”", "\"그리고 아주 중요한 문서이고요, 볼스 씨.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["Again Mr.", "볼스 씨가 다시 말했습니다."], ["Vholes said, “Very much so.”", "\"정말 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“And as you say, Mr. Vholes, when the cause is in the paper next term, this document will be an unexpected and interesting feature in it,”", "\"그리고 볼스 씨 말씀대로, 다음 개정 기간에 그 소송이 사건 목록에 오르면, 이 문서는 거기서 뜻밖이면서도 흥미로운 대목이 되겠지요.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge, looking loftily at my guardian.", "켄지 씨가 거만하게 후견인을 바라보며 말했습니다."]], [["Mr. Vholes was gratified, as a smaller practitioner striving to keep respectable, to be confirmed in any opinion of his own by such an authority.", "볼스 씨는, 체면을 지키려 애쓰는 변변찮은 개업 변호사로서, 이런 권위자에게서 자기 견해를 어떤 식으로든 확인받자 흡족해했습니다."]], [["“And when,”", "\"그런데,\""], ["asked my guardian, rising after a pause, during which Mr.", "후견인이 잠시 침묵한 끝에 일어서며 물었습니다."], ["Kenge had rattled his money and Mr.", "그 침묵 동안 켄지 씨는 돈을 짤랑거렸고 볼스 씨는 여드름을 짜고 있었습니다."], ["Vholes had picked his pimples, “when is next term?”", "\"그 다음 개정 기간은 언제요?\""]], [["“Next term, Mr. Jarndyce, will be next month,”", "\"다음 개정 기간은, 잔다이스 씨, 다음 달입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“Of course we shall at once proceed to do what is necessary with this document and to collect the necessary evidence concerning it; and of course you will receive our usual notification of the cause being in the paper.”", "\"물론 우리는 곧장 이 문서로 필요한 절차를 밟고 그것에 관한 필요한 증거를 모을 것입니다. 그리고 물론 그 소송이 사건 목록에 올랐다는 통상의 통지를 받으시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“To which I shall pay, of course, my usual attention.”", "\"그러면 나는 물론, 그것에 늘 하던 대로 관심을 기울이겠소.\""]], [["“Still bent, my dear sir,” said Mr.", "\"여전히 고집하시는군요, 선생님,\""], ["Kenge, showing us through the outer office to the door, “still bent, even with your enlarged mind, on echoing a popular prejudice?", "켄지 씨가 우리를 바깥 사무실을 지나 문까지 배웅하며 말했습니다. \"그 넓은 식견을 지니시고도, 여전히 세간의 편견을 따라 메아리치기로 마음먹으셨군요?"], ["We are a prosperous community, Mr. Jarndyce, a very prosperous community.", "우리는 번창하는 공동체입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 아주 번창하는 공동체예요."], ["We are a great country, Mr. Jarndyce, we are a very great country.", "우리는 위대한 나라입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 우리는 아주 위대한 나라입니다."], ["This is a great system, Mr. Jarndyce, and would you wish a great country to have a little system?", "이것은 위대한 제도입니다, 잔다이스 씨, 그런데 위대한 나라가 자그마한 제도를 갖기를 바라십니까?"], ["Now, really, really!”", "정말이지, 정말이라니까요!\""]], [["He said this at the stair-head, gently moving his right hand as if it were a silver trowel with which to spread the cement of his words on the structure of the system and consolidate it for a thousand ages.", "그는 계단 꼭대기에서 이렇게 말했는데, 오른손을 마치 은빛 흙손이라도 되는 양 부드럽게 놀려, 그 말의 회반죽을 제도라는 구조물에 펴 발라 천년만년 굳히려는 듯했습니다."]], [["Steel and Iron\nGeorge’s Shooting Gallery is to let, and the stock is sold off, and George himself is at Chesney Wold attending on Sir Leicester in his rides and riding very near his bridle-rein because of the uncertain hand with which he guides his horse.", "조지의 사격장은 세놓이고, 재고는 처분되었으며, 조지 본인은 체스니 월드에서 레스터 경의 승마를 수행하며 말고삐를 잡은 손이 미덥지 못한 까닭에 그 곁에 바짝 붙어 말을 몬다."], ["But not to-day is George so occupied.", "하지만 오늘 조지는 그 일에 매여 있지 않다."], ["He is journeying to-day into the iron country farther north to look about him.", "오늘 그는 좀 더 북쪽 철강 지대로 길을 나서, 두루 살펴보러 가는 길이다."]], [["As he comes into the iron country farther north, such fresh green woods as those of Chesney Wold are left behind; and coal pits and ashes, high chimneys and red bricks, blighted verdure, scorching fires, and a heavy never-lightening cloud of smoke become the features of the scenery.", "좀 더 북쪽 철강 지대로 접어들면, 체스니 월드의 그 싱그러운 초록 숲 같은 것은 뒤로 멀어진다. 그리고 탄광과 잿더미, 높다란 굴뚝과 붉은 벽돌, 시들어버린 초목, 살을 태우는 불길, 결코 걷히지 않는 묵직한 연기 구름이 풍경의 특징이 된다."], ["Among such objects rides the trooper, looking about him and always looking for something he has come to find.", "그런 것들 사이로 기병이 말을 몰며 사방을 둘러보는데, 줄곧 자기가 찾으러 온 무언가를 찾고 있다."]], [["At last, on the black canal bridge of a busy town, with a clang of iron in it, and more fires and more smoke than he has seen yet, the trooper, swart with the dust of the coal roads, checks his horse and asks a workman does he know the name of Rouncewell thereabouts.", "마침내, 쇳소리가 쩌렁쩌렁 울리고 지금껏 본 것보다 더 많은 불길과 더 많은 연기가 자욱한, 분주한 어느 읍내의 시커먼 운하 다리 위에서, 석탄길 먼지에 거뭇해진 기병이 말을 멈추고 한 일꾼에게 이 근방에서 라운스웰이라는 이름을 아느냐고 묻는다."]], [["“Why, master,”", "\"아니, 나리,\""], ["quoth the workman, “do I know my own name?”", "일꾼이 대꾸한다. \"내 이름도 내가 모르겠소?\""]], [["“’Tis so well known here, is it, comrade?”", "\"여기서 그렇게 잘 알려진 이름인가, 친구?\""], ["asks the trooper.", "기병이 묻는다."]], [["“Rouncewell’s?", "\"라운스웰 말이오?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["You’re right.”", "바로 맞혔수다.\""]], [["“And where might it be now?”", "\"그럼 지금 어디로 가야 하나?\""], ["asks the trooper with a glance before him.", "기병이 앞쪽을 흘끔 보며 묻는다."]], [["“The bank, the factory, or the house?”", "\"은행, 공장, 아니면 저택?\""], ["the workman wants to know.", "일꾼이 알고 싶어 한다."]], [["“Hum!", "\"흠!"], ["Rouncewell’s is so great apparently,”", "라운스웰이 그렇게나 대단한 모양이로군.\""], ["mutters the trooper, stroking his chin, “that I have as good as half a mind to go back again.", "기병이 턱을 쓸며 중얼거린다. \"이거 그냥 발길을 돌릴까 하는 마음이 절반은 드는걸."], ["Why, I don’t know which I want.", "원, 어딜 찾아야 할지조차 모르겠으니."], ["Should I find Mr. Rouncewell at the factory, do you think?”", "라운스웰 씨를 공장에서 만날 수 있을 것 같소?\""]], [["“Tain’t easy to say where you’d find him—at this time of the day you might find either him or his son there, if he’s in town; but his contracts take him away.”", "\"어디서 만날지는 말하기가 쉽지 않수다—이 시간이면 그가 읍내에 있을 경우 그든 그 아들이든 거기서 만날 수 있겠지만, 계약 일로 자주 떠나 있으니.\""]], [["And which is the factory?", "그럼 공장은 어디인가?"], ["Why, he sees those chimneys—the tallest ones!", "아니, 저 굴뚝들이 보이지 않소—제일 높은 것들 말이오!"], ["Yes, he sees THEM. Well!", "그렇소, 저것들이 보이는군."], ["Let him keep his eye on those chimneys, going on as straight as ever he can, and presently he’ll see ’em down a turning on the left, shut in by a great brick wall which forms one side of the street.", "좋수다! 저 굴뚝들에 눈을 박고 곧장 갈 수 있는 만큼 곧장 가시오, 그러면 이내 왼쪽으로 꺾이는 길 아래로 거리 한쪽을 이루는 커다란 벽돌담에 둘러싸인 그것들이 보일 거요."], ["That’s Rouncewell’s.", "거기가 라운스웰이오."]], [["The trooper thanks his informant and rides slowly on, looking about him.", "기병은 알려준 이에게 고마워하고 사방을 둘러보며 천천히 말을 몬다."], ["He does not turn back, but puts up his horse (and is much disposed to groom him too) at a public-house where some of Rouncewell’s hands are dining, as the ostler tells him.", "그는 발길을 돌리지 않고, 마부 말마따나 라운스웰의 일꾼 몇이 점심을 먹고 있는 어느 선술집에 말을 맡긴다(게다가 손수 빗질이라도 해주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같다)."], ["Some of Rouncewell’s hands have just knocked off for dinner-time and seem to be invading the whole town.", "라운스웰의 일꾼 몇이 마침 점심시간이라 일을 멈추었는데, 읍내 전체를 점령하다시피 한 듯하다."], ["They are very sinewy and strong, are Rouncewell’s hands—a little sooty too.", "힘줄이 단단하고 억세다, 라운스웰의 일꾼들은—더러 검댕도 묻었고."]], [["He comes to a gateway in the brick wall, looks in, and sees a great perplexity of iron lying about in every stage and in a vast variety of shapes—in bars, in wedges, in sheets; in tanks, in boilers, in axles, in wheels, in cogs, in cranks, in rails; twisted and wrenched into eccentric and perverse forms as separate parts of machinery; mountains of it broken up, and rusty in its age; distant furnaces of it glowing and bubbling in its youth; bright fireworks of it showering about under the blows of the steam-hammer; red-hot iron, white-hot iron, cold-black iron; an iron taste, an iron smell, and a Babel of iron sounds.", "그는 벽돌담의 출입구에 이르러 안을 들여다보고, 온갖 단계와 갖가지 형태로 어지러이 널린 쇠붙이의 난장판을 본다—막대로, 쐐기로, 판으로, 물탱크로, 보일러로, 차축으로, 바퀴로, 톱니로, 크랭크로, 레일로. 기계의 따로따로인 부품으로서 기괴하고 비뚤어진 형태로 비틀리고 뒤틀린 채로. 산더미처럼 쌓인 부서진 쇠는 세월에 녹슬어 있고, 저 멀리 용광로 속 쇠는 젊은 시절을 누리며 이글이글 부글부글 끓는다. 증기 해머의 타격 아래 밝은 불꽃놀이처럼 쇠가 사방으로 흩날리고, 시뻘겋게 단 쇠, 새하얗게 단 쇠, 식어서 검은 쇠가 있다. 쇠 맛이 나고, 쇠 냄새가 나며, 쇠 소리가 바벨탑처럼 뒤엉킨다."]], [["“This is a place to make a man’s head ache too!”", "\"여기도 사람 골치를 지끈거리게 하는 데로군!\""], ["says the trooper, looking about him for a counting-house.", "기병이 사무실을 찾아 두리번거리며 말한다. \"여기 오는 게 누구지?"], ["“Who comes here? This is very like me before I was set up.", "옛날, 내가 자리 잡기 전의 나와 어찌나 닮았는지."], ["This ought to be my nephew, if likenesses run in families.", "닮은 게 집안 내력이라면, 저 사람은 내 조카가 분명한데."], ["Your servant, sir.”", "안녕하시오, 선생.\""]], [["“Yours, sir.", "\"안녕하십니까, 선생."], ["Are you looking for any one?”", "누구를 찾으십니까?\""]], [["“Excuse me. Young Mr.", "\"실례지만."], ["Rouncewell, I believe?”", "젊은 라운스웰 씨, 맞소?\" \"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Yes.” “I was looking for your father, sir.", "\"댁의 부친을 찾고 있었소, 선생."], ["I wish to have a word with him.”", "그분과 한마디 나누고 싶어서.\""]], [["The young man, telling him he is fortunate in his choice of a time, for his father is there, leads the way to the office where he is to be found.", "젊은이가, 마침 부친이 계시니 시간을 잘 골라 오셨다고 말하며, 부친을 만날 수 있는 사무실로 길을 안내한다."], ["“Very like me before I was set up—devilish like me!”", "\"내가 자리 잡기 전의 나와 어찌나 닮았는지—기막히게 닮았어!\""], ["thinks the trooper as he follows.", "기병은 뒤따르며 생각한다."], ["They come to a building in the yard with an office on an upper floor.", "두 사람은 마당에 있는, 위층에 사무실이 딸린 건물에 이른다."], ["At sight of the gentleman in the office, Mr. George turns very red.", "사무실 안의 신사를 보는 순간, 조지 씨는 얼굴이 새빨개진다."]], [["“What name shall I say to my father?”", "\"부친께 누구라고 전할까요?\""], ["asks the young man.", "젊은이가 묻는다."]], [["George, full of the idea of iron, in desperation answers “Steel,” and is so presented.", "쇠 생각으로 머릿속이 가득 찬 조지는 자포자기하여 \"스틸이라고\"라 답하고, 그렇게 소개된다."], ["He is left alone with the gentleman in the office, who sits at a table with account-books before him and some sheets of paper blotted with hosts of figures and drawings of cunning shapes.", "그는 사무실 안의 신사와 단둘이 남겨지는데, 신사는 책상 앞에 앉아 회계 장부들을 펼쳐놓았고, 숱한 숫자와 정교한 도형 스케치로 얼룩진 종잇장 몇 장이 놓여 있다."], ["It is a bare office, with bare windows, looking on the iron view below.", "휑한 사무실이고, 창문도 휑하니 아래쪽 쇠붙이 풍경을 내다본다."], ["Tumbled together on the table are some pieces of iron, purposely broken to be tested at various periods of their service, in various capacities.", "책상 위에는 쓰임에 따라 여러 시기에 시험하려고 일부러 부러뜨린 쇳조각 몇 개가 한데 뒹군다. 모든 것에 쇳가루가 앉아 있다."], ["There is iron-dust on everything; and the smoke is seen through the windows rolling heavily out of the tall chimneys to mingle with the smoke from a vaporous Babylon of other chimneys.", "창문 너머로는 높다란 굴뚝에서 묵직하게 굴러 나온 연기가 안개처럼 자욱한 다른 굴뚝들의 바빌론 같은 연기와 뒤섞이는 모습이 보인다."]], [["“I am at your service, Mr. Steel,”", "* 스틸: 영어로 '강철(steel)'을 뜻한다."], ["says the gentleman when his visitor has taken a rusty chair.", "조지가 쇠붙이 풍경에 정신이 팔린 나머지 본명 대신 둘러댄 가명이다."]], [["“Well, Mr. Rouncewell,”", "\"무슨 용건이신지요, 스틸 씨.\""], ["George replies, leaning forward with his left arm on his knee and his hat in his hand, and very chary of meeting his brother’s eye, “I am not without my expectations that in the present visit I may prove to be more free than welcome.", "손님이 녹슨 의자에 앉자 신사가 말한다. \"음, 라운스웰 씨.\""], ["I have served as a dragoon in my day, and a comrade of mine that I was once rather partial to was, if I don’t deceive myself, a brother of yours.", "조지가 왼팔을 무릎에 올리고 모자를 손에 든 채 몸을 앞으로 기울이며, 형의 눈을 마주치기를 한사코 꺼리며 대답한다. \"이번 방문이 환영받기보다 주제넘은 짓으로 드러날 수도 있겠다는 예감이 없지 않소이다."], ["I believe you had a brother who gave his family some trouble, and ran away, and never did any good but in keeping away?”", "나는 한때 용기병으로 복무했는데, 내가 꽤 마음을 주었던 전우 하나가, 내 착각이 아니라면, 댁의 형제였소. 내가 알기로 댁에게는 집안을 좀 골치 아프게 하고 달아나, 멀리 떨어져 있는 것 말고는 아무런 쓸모도 없었던 형제가 있었지요?\""]], [["“Are you quite sure,”", "\"정말 확실하십니까,\""], ["returns the ironmaster in an altered voice, “that your name is Steel?”", "철강업자가 달라진 목소리로 되묻는다. \"댁의 성함이 스틸이라는 게 말입니다?\""]], [["The trooper falters and looks at him.", "기병이 머뭇거리며 그를 바라본다."], ["His brother starts up, calls him by his name, and grasps him by both hands.", "형이 벌떡 일어나 그의 이름을 부르며 두 손을 덥석 잡는다."]], [["“You are too quick for me!”", "\"형은 나보다 너무 빠르구려!\""], ["cries the trooper with the tears springing out of his eyes.", "기병이 눈에서 눈물을 쏟으며 외친다."], ["“How do you do, my dear old fellow?", "\"어떻게 지냈소, 그리운 형님?"], ["I never could have thought you would have been half so glad to see me as all this.", "형이 나를 보고 이렇게나 반가워할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐소."], ["How do you do, my dear old fellow, how do you do!”", "어떻게 지냈소, 그리운 형님, 어떻게 지냈소!\""]], [["They shake hands and embrace each other over and over again, the trooper still coupling his “How do you do, my dear old fellow!” with his protestation that he never thought his brother would have been half so glad to see him as all this!", "두 사람은 거듭거듭 악수하고 서로 끌어안는데, 기병은 여전히 \"어떻게 지냈소, 그리운 형님!\"이라는 말에다, 형이 나를 보고 이렇게나 반가워할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다는 다짐을 짝지어 붙인다!"]], [["“So far from it,”", "\"그렇기는커녕,\""], ["he declares at the end of a full account of what has preceded his arrival there, “I had very little idea of making myself known.", "그는 그곳에 도착하기까지의 사연을 모조리 털어놓은 끝에 단언한다. \"나는 내 정체를 밝힐 생각이 거의 없었소."], ["I thought if you took by any means forgivingly to my name I might gradually get myself up to the point of writing a letter.", "형이 어떻게든 내 이름을 너그럽게 받아들인다면, 차츰 마음을 다잡아 편지 한 통 쓸 수 있는 데까지 가볼까 했지."], ["But I should not have been surprised, brother, if you had considered it anything but welcome news to hear of me.”", "하지만 형, 형이 내 소식을 전혀 반갑잖은 것으로 여겼다 해도, 나는 놀라지 않았을 거요.\""]], [["“We will show you at home what kind of news we think it, George,”", "\"우리가 그걸 어떤 소식으로 여기는지는 집에 가서 보여주마, 조지.\""], ["returns his brother.", "형이 대답한다."], ["“This is a great day at home, and you could not have arrived, you bronzed old soldier, on a better.", "\"오늘은 집안의 큰 경삿날이니, 햇볕에 그을린 늙은 군인 양반아, 너는 이보다 더 좋은 날에 올 수가 없었어."], ["I make an agreement with my son Watt to-day that on this day twelvemonth he shall marry as pretty and as good a girl as you have seen in all your travels.", "오늘 나는 내 아들 와트와 약조를 맺는데, 오늘로부터 일 년 뒤 이날, 네가 그 숱한 여행길에서 본 그 누구 못지않게 예쁘고 착한 아가씨와 결혼하기로 했단다."], ["She goes to Germany to-morrow with one of your nieces for a little polishing up in her education.", "그 애는 내일 네 조카딸 하나와 함께 독일로 가서, 교육의 마무리 손질을 좀 받을 거야."], ["We make a feast of the event, and you will be made the hero of it.”", "우리는 이 일을 잔치로 치를 참인데, 너를 그 주인공으로 삼으마.\""]], [["Mr. George is so entirely overcome at first by this prospect that he resists the proposed honour with great earnestness.", "조지 씨는 처음에 이 제안에 완전히 압도되어 그 영예를 한사코 마다한다."], ["Being overborne, however, by his brother and his nephew—concerning whom he renews his protestations that he never could have thought they would have been half so glad to see him—he is taken home to an elegant house in all the arrangements of which there is to be observed a pleasant mixture of the originally simple habits of the father and mother with such as are suited to their altered station and the higher fortunes of their children.", "그러나 형과 조카에게—이들에 관해서도 이렇게나 반가워할 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐다는 다짐을 거듭하는데—떠밀려, 우아한 저택으로 끌려간다. 그 저택의 온갖 살림에는 부모의 본디 소박한 습관과, 달라진 신분 및 자녀들의 높아진 형편에 어울리는 것들이 흐뭇하게 뒤섞여 있는 게 눈에 띈다."], ["Here Mr. George is much dismayed by the graces and accomplishments of his nieces that are and by the beauty of Rosa, his niece that is to be, and by the affectionate salutations of these young ladies, which he receives in a sort of dream.", "여기서 조지 씨는 지금의 조카딸들이 지닌 우아함과 교양에, 그리고 장차 조카며느리가 될 로사의 아름다움에, 또 이 젊은 숙녀들의 다정한 인사에 적잖이 당황하여, 그 인사를 일종의 꿈결 속에서 받는다."], ["He is sorely taken aback, too, by the dutiful behaviour of his nephew and has a woeful consciousness upon him of being a scapegrace.", "그는 조카의 깍듯한 처신에도 호되게 기가 꺾이고, 자기가 망나니라는 서글픈 자각에 사로잡힌다."], ["However, there is great rejoicing and a very hearty company and infinite enjoyment, and Mr. George comes bluff and martial through it all, and his pledge to be present at the marriage and give away the bride is received with universal favour.", "그래도 큰 즐거움과 더없이 화기애애한 자리와 끝없는 흥겨움이 펼쳐지고, 조지 씨는 그 모든 것을 무뚝뚝하고 군인답게 헤쳐나가며, 결혼식에 참석해 신부를 인도하겠다는 그의 약속은 만장일치의 호응을 얻는다."], ["A whirling head has Mr. George that night when he lies down in the state-bed of his brother’s house to think of all these things and to see the images of his nieces (awful all the evening in their floating muslins) waltzing, after the German manner, over his counterpane.", "그날 밤, 조지 씨가 형네 집의 가장 좋은 침대에 누워 이 모든 일을 떠올리며, 조카딸들의(저녁 내내 하늘하늘한 모슬린 차림으로 위풍당당하던) 모습이 독일식으로 왈츠를 추며 그의 침대보 위를 누비는 환영을 보노라니, 머릿속이 빙글빙글 돈다."]], [["The brothers are closeted next morning in the ironmaster’s room, where the elder is proceeding, in his clear sensible way, to show how he thinks he may best dispose of George in his business, when George squeezes his hand and stops him.", "이튿날 아침, 형제는 철강업자의 방에 틀어박힌다. 거기서 형이 특유의 또렷하고 분별 있는 어조로, 자기 사업 안에서 조지를 어떻게 가장 잘 자리 잡게 해줄 수 있을지 자기 생각을 펼치려는데, 조지가 그의 손을 꽉 쥐며 말을 막는다."]], [["“Brother, I thank you a million times for your more than brotherly welcome, and a million times more to that for your more than brotherly intentions.", "\"형, 형제 이상으로 따뜻한 환대에 백만 번 고맙고, 형제 이상으로 깊은 그 뜻에는 또 백만 번 더 고맙소. 하지만 내 계획은 이미 정해져 있소."], ["But my plans are made. Before I say a word as to them, I wish to consult you upon one family point.", "그 얘기를 꺼내기 전에, 집안 문제 한 가지를 형과 상의하고 싶소."], ["How,”", "어떻게 해야,\""], ["says the trooper, folding his arms and looking with indomitable firmness at his brother, “how is my mother to be got to scratch me?”", "기병이 팔짱을 끼고 굽힐 줄 모르는 단호함으로 형을 바라보며 말한다. \"어떻게 해야 어머니가 나를 지워버리게 할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“I am not sure that I understand you, George,”", "\"네 말을 제대로 알아들었는지 모르겠구나, 조지.\""], ["replies the ironmaster.", "철강업자가 대꾸한다."]], [["“I say, brother, how is my mother to be got to scratch me?", "\"내 말은, 형, 어떻게 해야 어머니가 나를 지워버리게 할 수 있느냐는 거요."], ["She must be got to do it somehow.”", "어떻게든 그렇게 하시도록 해야 하오.\""]], [["“Scratch you out of her will, I think you mean?”", "\"어머니 유언장에서 너를 지운다는 뜻이냐?\""]], [["“Of course I do.", "\"당연히 그렇소."], ["In short,”", "한마디로,\""], ["says the trooper, folding his arms more resolutely yet, “I mean—TO—scratch me!”", "기병이 한층 더 단호하게 팔짱을 끼며 말한다. \"내 말은—나를—지워버리라는 거요!\""]], [["“My dear George,”", "\"이보게 조지,\""], ["returns his brother, “is it so indispensable that you should undergo that process?”", "형이 대꾸한다. \"네가 꼭 그런 절차를 겪어야만 하느냐?\""]], [["“Quite!", "\"그렇소!"], ["Absolutely!", "반드시!"], ["I couldn’t be guilty of the meanness of coming back without it.", "그것 없이 돌아오는 비열한 짓은 저지를 수 없소."], ["I should never be safe not to be off again.", "그러지 않고는 다시 떠나지 않으리란 보장이 영영 안 서니까."], ["I have not sneaked home to rob your children, if not yourself, brother, of your rights.", "나는, 형이든 형의 자식들이든 그 권리를 빼앗으려고 슬그머니 집에 기어든 게 아니오."], ["I, who forfeited mine long ago!", "내 권리야 오래전에 스스로 내던졌고!"], ["If I am to remain and hold up my head, I must be scratched.", "내가 여기 남아 떳떳이 고개를 들려면, 지워져야만 하오."], ["Come.", "자."], ["You are a man of celebrated penetration and intelligence, and you can tell me how it’s to be brought about.”", "형은 명석한 통찰과 지혜로 이름난 사람이니, 어떻게 하면 그 일을 이룰 수 있는지 말해줄 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“I can tell you, George,”", "\"내가 말해주마, 조지.\""], ["replies the ironmaster deliberately, “how it is not to be brought about, which I hope may answer the purpose as well.", "철강업자가 신중하게 대답한다. \"그 일을 어떻게 하면 이룰 수 없는지를."], ["Look at our mother, think of her, recall her emotion when she recovered you.", "그게 오히려 목적에 들어맞기를 바란다. 우리 어머니를 보아라, 어머니를 생각해라, 너를 되찾았을 때 어머니의 그 격정을 떠올려보아라."], ["Do you believe there is a consideration in the world that would induce her to take such a step against her favourite son?", "어머니가 가장 아끼는 아들을 상대로 그런 짓을 하도록 이끌 만한 사정이 이 세상에 있다고 믿느냐?"], ["Do you believe there is any chance of her consent, to balance against the outrage it would be to her (loving dear old lady!) to propose it?", "(그 사랑 많고 다정한 노부인께!) 그런 제안을 하는 것 자체가 어머니께 얼마나 모욕일지, 그것과 견주어 어머니가 동의할 가능성이 조금이라도 있다고 믿느냐?"], ["If you do, you are wrong.", "그렇게 믿는다면 너는 틀렸다."], ["No, George!", "안 돼, 조지!"], ["You must make up your mind to remain UNscratched, I think.”", "너는 지워지지 않은 채로 남기로 마음을 굳혀야 한다.\""], ["There is an amused smile on the ironmaster’s face as he watches his brother, who is pondering, deeply disappointed.", "철강업자는 깊이 실망하여 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴 동생을 지켜보며, 얼굴에 흥겨운 미소를 띤다."], ["“I think you may manage almost as well as if the thing were done, though.”", "\"하지만 그 일이 이루어진 것이나 거의 다름없이 처리할 방도는 있을 거다.\""]], [["“How, brother?”", "\"어떻게요, 형?\""]], [["“Being bent upon it, you can dispose by will of anything you have the misfortune to inherit in any way you like, you know.”", "\"네가 정 그러기로 작정했다면, 불운하게도 무엇을 물려받든 그걸 유언으로 네가 원하는 대로 처분할 수 있잖느냐.\""]], [["“That’s true!”", "\"그건 그렇구려!\""], ["says the trooper, pondering again.", "기병이 다시 곰곰이 생각하며 말한다. 그러고는 형의 손에 자기 손을 얹고 아쉬운 듯 묻는다."], ["Then he wistfully asks, with his hand on his brother’s, “Would you mind mentioning that, brother, to your wife and family?” “Not at all.”", "\"형, 그 얘기를 형수와 식구들한테 좀 해주면 안 되겠소?\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있겠느냐.\""], ["You wouldn’t object to say, perhaps, that although an undoubted vagabond, I am a vagabond of the harum-scarum order, and not of the mean sort?”", "\"고맙소. 그리고 어쩌면, 내가 의심할 바 없는 떠돌이이긴 해도, 좀스러운 부류가 아니라 무모하고 덜렁대는 부류의 떠돌이라고 한마디 해줄 수도 있겠소?\""]], [["The ironmaster, repressing his amused smile, assents.", "철강업자가 흥겨운 미소를 누르며 그러마고 한다."]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고맙소."], ["Thank you.", "고맙소."], ["It’s a weight off my mind,”", "한시름 놓이는구려.\""], ["says the trooper with a heave of his chest as he unfolds his arms and puts a hand on each leg, “though I had set my heart on being scratched, too!”", "기병이 팔짱을 풀고 두 손을 각각 무릎에 얹으며 가슴을 한 번 들썩이고 말한다. \"지워지는 데에도 마음을 단단히 두긴 했지만!\""]], [["The brothers are very like each other, sitting face to face; but a certain massive simplicity and absence of usage in the ways of the world is all on the trooper’s side.", "마주 앉은 형제는 서로 무척 닮았다. 다만 묵직한 소박함과 세상 물정에 닳지 않은 구석은 온전히 기병 쪽에만 있다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["he proceeds, throwing off his disappointment, “next and last, those plans of mine.", "그가 실망을 떨치며 말을 잇는다. \"다음이자 마지막으로, 그 내 계획 말이오."], ["You have been so brotherly as to propose to me to fall in here and take my place among the products of your perseverance and sense.", "형은 형제답게도 나더러 여기에 들어와, 형의 끈기와 분별이 일군 성과들 한가운데에서 한자리를 차지하라고 권했소."], ["I thank you heartily.", "진심으로 고맙소."], ["It’s more than brotherly, as I said before, and I thank you heartily for it,”", "아까도 말했듯 형제 이상가는 일이고, 진심으로 고맙소.\""], ["shaking him a long time by the hand.", "그가 오랫동안 형의 손을 흔든다."], ["“But the truth is, brother, I am a—I am a kind of a weed, and it’s too late to plant me in a regular garden.”", "\"하지만 사실은 말이오, 형, 나는—나는 말하자면 잡초 같은 놈이라, 제대로 된 정원에 옮겨 심기엔 이미 너무 늦었소.\""]], [["“My dear George,”", "\"이보게 조지,\""], ["returns the elder, concentrating his strong steady brow upon him and smiling confidently, “leave that to me, and let me try.”", "형이 그 강인하고 듬직한 이마를 동생에게로 모으며 미더운 미소를 띠고 대답한다. \"그건 내게 맡기고, 한번 해보게 해다오.\""]], [["George shakes his head.", "조지가 고개를 젓는다."], ["“You could do it, I have not a doubt, if anybody could; but it’s not to be done.", "\"누군가 할 수 있다면 형이 해낼 수 있을 거요, 그건 의심치 않소. 하지만 그건 될 일이 아니오."], ["Not to be done, sir!", "될 일이 아니라니까, 선생!"], ["Whereas it so falls out, on the other hand, that I am able to be of some trifle of use to Sir Leicester Dedlock since his illness—brought on by family sorrows—and that he would rather have that help from our mother’s son than from anybody else.”", "그런데 반대로 일이 이렇게 풀렸소. 나는 레스터 데드록 경께—집안의 비통한 일들로 병을 얻으신 그분께—얼마간 사소한 도움이라도 드릴 수 있게 되었고, 그분은 그 도움을 다른 누구보다 우리 어머니의 아들에게서 받고 싶어 하신다오.\""]], [["“Well, my dear George,”", "\"음, 이보게 조지,\""], ["returns the other with a very slight shade upon his open face, “if you prefer to serve in Sir Leicester Dedlock’s household brigade—”", "형이 그 트인 얼굴에 아주 옅은 그늘을 드리우며 대답한다. \"네가 레스터 데드록 경 댁의 식솔 부대에서 봉직하기를 더 바란다면—\""]], [["“There it is, brother,”", "\"바로 그거요, 형.\""], ["cries the trooper, checking him, with his hand upon his knee again; “there it is!", "기병이 다시 손을 무릎에 얹으며 형의 말을 막고 외친다. \"바로 그거!"], ["You don’t take kindly to that idea; I don’t mind it.", "형은 그 생각이 영 내키지 않는 거요. 나는 개의치 않소."], ["You are not used to being officered; I am.", "형은 누군가의 지휘를 받는 데 익숙지 않지만, 나는 익숙하오."], ["Everything about you is in perfect order and discipline; everything about me requires to be kept so.", "형의 주변은 모든 것이 완벽한 질서와 규율 속에 있소. 내 주변은 모든 것을 그렇게 잡아둘 필요가 있고."], ["We are not accustomed to carry things with the same hand or to look at ’em from the same point.", "우리는 같은 손으로 일을 처리하거나 같은 자리에서 사물을 바라보는 데 익숙지 않소."], ["I don’t say much about my garrison manners because I found myself pretty well at my ease last night, and they wouldn’t be noticed here, I dare say, once and away.", "내 병영식 몸가짐에 대해선 길게 말하지 않겠소. 어젯밤엔 제법 마음 편히 지냈고, 또 어쩌다 한 번쯤이야 여기서도 눈에 띄지 않을 테니까."], ["But I shall get on best at Chesney Wold, where there’s more room for a weed than there is here; and the dear old lady will be made happy besides.", "하지만 나는 체스니 월드에서 가장 잘 지낼 거요. 거기엔 잡초가 자랄 자리가 여기보다 더 넓으니까."], ["Therefore I accept of Sir Leicester Dedlock’s proposals.", "게다가 그 다정한 노부인도 행복해지실 테고."], ["When I come over next year to give away the bride, or whenever I come, I shall have the sense to keep the household brigade in ambuscade and not to manoeuvre it on your ground.", "그러니 나는 레스터 데드록 경의 제안을 받아들이겠소. 내년에 신부를 인도하러 건너올 때, 아니 언제 오든, 나는 그 식솔 부대를 매복시켜둘 만큼의 분별은 있을 테니, 형의 땅에서 그걸 부려먹진 않으리다."], ["I thank you heartily again and am proud to think of the Rouncewells as they’ll be founded by you.”", "다시 한번 진심으로 고맙소. 그리고 형이 세워나갈 라운스웰 가문을 생각하면 자랑스럽소.\""]], [["“You know yourself, George,”", "\"너는 너 자신을 알지, 조지.\""], ["says the elder brother, returning the grip of his hand, “and perhaps you know me better than I know myself.", "형이 동생의 손을 마주 쥐며 말한다. \"어쩌면 내가 나 자신을 아는 것보다 너를 더 잘 알지도 모르겠구나."], ["Take your way.", "네 길을 가거라."], ["So that we don’t quite lose one another again, take your way.”", "다만 우리가 다시 서로를 영영 잃지만 않도록, 네 길을 가거라.\""]], [["“No fear of that!”", "\"그럴 염려는 없소!\""], ["returns the trooper.", "기병이 대답한다."], ["“Now, before I turn my horse’s head homewards, brother, I will ask you—if you’ll be so good—to look over a letter for me.", "\"자, 말머리를 집 쪽으로 돌리기 전에, 형, 한 가지 부탁이 있소—괜찮다면—편지 한 통을 좀 봐주시오."], ["I brought it with me to send from these parts, as Chesney Wold might be a painful name just now to the person it’s written to.", "이 근방에서 부치려고 가져왔는데, 체스니 월드라는 이름이 지금 이 편지를 받을 사람에겐 아픈 이름일 수도 있어서요."], ["I am not much accustomed to correspondence myself, and I am particular respecting this present letter because I want it to be both straightforward and delicate.”", "나도 편지 쓰는 일엔 그리 익숙지 않은 데다, 이번 편지는 솔직하면서도 섬세하기를 바라는 까닭에 각별히 마음을 쓰고 있소.\""]], [["Herewith he hands a letter, closely written in somewhat pale ink but in a neat round hand, to the ironmaster, who reads as follows:", "이러면서 그는 다소 흐린 잉크로 빽빽하되 단정하고 둥근 필체로 쓴 편지 한 통을 철강업자에게 건네고, 철강업자는 다음과 같이 읽는다."]], [["Miss Esther Summerson,", "에스더 서머슨 양께,"]], [["A communication having been made to me by Inspector Bucket of a letter to myself being found among the papers of a certain person, I take the liberty to make known to you that it was but a few lines of instruction from abroad, when, where, and how to deliver an enclosed letter to a young and beautiful lady, then unmarried, in England.", "버킷 형사로부터, 어떤 사람의 서류 가운데 저에게 보낸 편지 한 통이 발견되었다는 전갈을 받고서, 저는 외람되이 양께 다음을 알려드립니다. 그것은 다만 외국에서 보내온 몇 줄의 지시문으로, 그 무렵 영국에 있던 젊고 아리따운 한 숙녀, 당시 미혼이었던 그분께 동봉된 편지를 언제, 어디서, 어떻게 전할지에 관한 것이었습니다."], ["I duly observed the same.", "저는 그대로 충실히 이행하였습니다."]], [["I further take the liberty to make known to you that it was got from me as a proof of handwriting only and that otherwise I would not have given it up, as appearing to be the most harmless in my possession, without being previously shot through the heart.", "또한 저는 외람되이 양께 알려드립니다. 그것은 오로지 필적의 증거로서 저에게서 받아간 것이며, 그렇지 않았다면 저는 제 손에 있는 것 중 가장 해롭지 않아 보이는 그것을, 심장을 먼저 총으로 꿰뚫리기 전에는 결코 내주지 않았을 것입니다."]], [["I further take the liberty to mention that if I could have supposed a certain unfortunate gentleman to have been in existence, I never could and never would have rested until I had discovered his retreat and shared my last farthing with him, as my duty and my inclination would have equally been. But he was (officially) reported drowned, and assuredly went over the side of a transport-ship at night in an Irish harbour within a few hours of her arrival from the West Indies, as I have myself heard both from officers and men on board, and know to have been (officially) confirmed.", "또한 저는 외람되이 말씀드립니다. 어느 불운한 신사가 살아 있으리라고 짐작이라도 할 수 있었다면, 저는 그분이 숨은 곳을 찾아낼 때까지 결코 쉴 수 없었고 쉬지도 않았을 것이며, 제 마지막 한 푼까지 그분과 나누었을 것입니다. 그것이 저의 의무이자 또한 저의 바람이었을 테니까요. 그러나 그분은 (공식적으로) 익사한 것으로 보고되었고, 서인도제도에서 돌아온 지 몇 시간도 안 되어 아일랜드의 어느 항구에서 밤중에 수송선 뱃전 너머로 떨어진 것이 분명합니다. 이는 제가 그 배에 탔던 장교와 병사 양쪽에게서 직접 들은 바이며, (공식적으로) 확인된 일임을 저는 알고 있습니다."]], [["I further take the liberty to state that in my humble quality as one of the rank and file, I am, and shall ever continue to be, your thoroughly devoted and admiring servant and that I esteem the qualities you possess above all others far beyond the limits of the present dispatch.", "또한 저는 외람되이 밝힙니다. 졸병 가운데 하나라는 미천한 처지로나마, 저는 양의 철저히 헌신하고 흠모하는 종이며 또 영원히 그러할 것이고, 양께서 지니신 자질을 다른 무엇보다도, 이 짧은 글의 한계를 훨씬 넘어서도록 떠받든다는 것을."]], [["I have the honour to be,", "삼가 올립니다,\n조지"]], [["“A little formal,”", "\"좀 격식을 차렸구나.\""], ["observes the elder brother, refolding it with a puzzled face.", "형이 어리둥절한 얼굴로 편지를 다시 접으며 한마디 한다."]], [["“But nothing that might not be sent to a pattern young lady?”", "\"하지만 모범적인 젊은 숙녀에게 보내선 안 될 만한 구석은 없겠소?\""], ["asks the younger.", "동생이 묻는다."]], [["“Nothing at all.”", "\"전혀 없다.\""]], [["Therefore it is sealed and deposited for posting among the iron correspondence of the day.", "그리하여 편지는 봉인되어, 그날의 철강 관련 우편물 사이에 끼워 부쳐질 채비를 갖춘다."], ["This done, Mr. George takes a hearty farewell of the family party and prepares to saddle and mount.", "이 일을 마치고 조지 씨는 가족 일행에게 진심 어린 작별을 고하고 안장을 얹어 말에 오르려 한다."], ["His brother, however, unwilling to part with him so soon, proposes to ride with him in a light open carriage to the place where he will bait for the night, and there remain with him until morning, a servant riding for so much of the journey on the thoroughbred old grey from Chesney Wold.", "그러나 형은 그를 그리 빨리 떠나보내기가 아쉬워, 가벼운 무개마차로 그가 하룻밤 묵으며 말을 쉬게 할 곳까지 함께 가서, 아침까지 그와 머물자고 제안한다. 그 여정의 상당 부분 동안 하인 하나가 체스니 월드에서 데려온 순종 늙은 잿빛 말을 타고 따라온다."], ["The offer, being gladly accepted, is followed by a pleasant ride, a pleasant dinner, and a pleasant breakfast, all in brotherly communion.", "이 제안은 기쁘게 받아들여지고, 이어 흐뭇한 승마와 흐뭇한 만찬과 흐뭇한 아침 식사가, 모두 형제간의 정담 속에 이어진다."], ["Then they once more shake hands long and heartily and part, the ironmaster turning his face to the smoke and fires, and the trooper to the green country.", "그런 다음 두 사람은 다시 한번 오래 진심으로 악수하고 헤어지는데, 철강업자는 연기와 불길 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 기병은 푸른 들판 쪽으로 향한다."], ["Early in the afternoon the subdued sound of his heavy military trot is heard on the turf in the avenue as he rides on with imaginary clank and jingle of accoutrements under the old elm-trees. Esther’s Narrative", "이른 오후, 그가 오래된 느릅나무 아래로 마구가 절그렁절그렁 짤랑짤랑 울리는 상상에 잠겨 말을 몰아가노라니, 가로수 길의 잔디 위로 그 묵직한 군대식 빠른 걸음의 잦아든 소리가 들려온다."]], [["Soon after I had that conversation with my guardian, he put a sealed paper in my hand one morning and said, “This is for next month, my dear.”", "후견인과 그런 대화를 나눈 지 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 아침, 후견인은 봉인한 종이 한 장을 내 손에 쥐여주며 말했습니다. \"이건 다음 달에 쓸 거란다, 얘야.\""], ["I found in it two hundred pounds.", "그 안에는 이백 파운드가 들어 있었습니다."]], [["I now began very quietly to make such preparations as I thought were necessary.", "나는 이제 아주 조용히 필요하다고 여겨지는 준비를 시작했습니다."], ["Regulating my purchases by my guardian’s taste, which I knew very well of course, I arranged my wardrobe to please him and hoped I should be highly successful.", "후견인의 취향에 맞추어 물건을 골랐는데, 그분 취향이야 물론 잘 알고 있었으므로, 그분 마음에 들도록 옷가지를 갖추었고 무척 잘 해내기를 바랐습니다."], ["I did it all so quietly because I was not quite free from my old apprehension that Ada would be rather sorry and because my guardian was so quiet himself.", "이 모든 일을 그토록 조용히 한 까닭은, 에이다가 못내 서운해할지도 모른다는 예전 그 걱정에서 아직 완전히 벗어나지 못한 데다, 후견인 자신이 워낙 조용한 분이었기 때문입니다."], ["I had no doubt that under all the circumstances we should be married in the most private and simple manner.", "모든 사정을 헤아릴 때 우리는 가장 은밀하고 소박한 방식으로 결혼하게 되리라는 데 의심이 없었습니다."], ["Perhaps I should only have to say to Ada, “Would you like to come and see me married to-morrow, my pet?”", "어쩌면 나는 에이다에게 이렇게 말하기만 하면 될지도 몰랐습니다. \"내일 내가 결혼하는 걸 보러 올래, 얘야?\""], ["Perhaps our wedding might even be as unpretending as her own, and I might not find it necessary to say anything about it until it was over.", "어쩌면 우리 결혼식도 에이다 자신의 것만큼 꾸밈없을 테고, 다 끝날 때까지 그 일에 대해 굳이 한마디도 할 필요가 없을지도 몰랐습니다."], ["I thought that if I were to choose, I would like this best.", "내가 고를 수 있다면 이렇게 하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했습니다."]], [["The only exception I made was Mrs. Woodcourt.", "내가 유일하게 예외로 둔 사람은 우드코트 부인이었습니다."], ["I told her that I was going to be married to my guardian and that we had been engaged some time.", "나는 부인에게 후견인과 결혼할 예정이며 우리가 약혼한 지 얼마 되었다고 말씀드렸습니다."], ["She highly approved.", "부인은 크게 찬성했습니다."], ["She could never do enough for me and was remarkably softened now in comparison with what she had been when we first knew her.", "부인은 나에게 아무리 잘해줘도 모자라다고 여겼고, 우리가 처음 알았을 때에 견주면 지금은 놀라우리만치 누그러져 있었습니다."], ["There was no trouble she would not have taken to have been of use to me, but I need hardly say that I only allowed her to take as little as gratified her kindness without tasking it.", "나에게 도움이 되는 일이라면 부인이 마다할 수고가 없었지만, 굳이 말할 것도 없이 나는 그 친절을 시험에 들게 하지 않는 선에서, 부인의 호의를 흡족하게 할 만큼만 받아들였습니다."]], [["Of course this was not a time to neglect my guardian, and of course it was not a time for neglecting my darling.", "물론 지금은 후견인을 소홀히 할 때가 아니었고, 물론 내 사랑하는 친구를 소홀히 할 때도 아니었습니다."], ["So I had plenty of occupation, which I was glad of; and as to Charley, she was absolutely not to be seen for needlework.", "그래서 나는 할 일이 잔뜩 있었는데, 그게 오히려 기뻤습니다. 찰리로 말하자면, 바느질에 파묻혀 도무지 보이지가 않을 지경이었습니다."], ["To surround herself with great heaps of it—baskets full and tables full—and do a little, and spend a great deal of time in staring with her round eyes at what there was to do, and persuade herself that she was going to do it, were Charley’s great dignities and delights.", "자기 둘레에 바느질감을 산더미처럼 쌓아 두고—바구니 가득, 탁자 가득—조금만 손을 대고, 그 동그란 눈으로 해야 할 일거리를 멀거니 바라보며 한참을 보내고, 곧 그 일을 하리라고 스스로를 다독이는 것이야말로 찰리의 크나큰 위엄이자 기쁨이었습니다."]], [["Meanwhile, I must say, I could not agree with my guardian on the subject of the will, and I had some sanguine hopes of Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "한편 나는 솔직히 유언장 문제를 두고는 후견인과 뜻을 같이할 수가 없었으니, 잔다이스 대 잔다이스 소송을 두고 다소 낙관적인 기대를 품고 있었습니다."], ["Which of us was right will soon appear, but I certainly did encourage expectations.", "우리 둘 가운데 누가 옳았는지는 곧 드러나겠지만, 나는 분명 기대를 부추기는 쪽이었습니다."], ["In Richard, the discovery gave occasion for a burst of business and agitation that buoyed him up for a little time, but he had lost the elasticity even of hope now and seemed to me to retain only its feverish anxieties.", "리처드에게는 그 발견이 한바탕 분주함과 들뜸을 불러일으켜 한동안 그를 떠받쳐주었으나, 이제 그는 희망의 탄력마저 잃은 채 내가 보기에 오직 그 열병 같은 불안만 붙들고 있는 듯했습니다."], ["From something my guardian said one day when we were talking about this, I understood that my marriage would not take place until after the term-time we had been told to look forward to; and I thought the more, for that, how rejoiced I should be if I could be married when Richard and Ada were a little more prosperous.", "어느 날 우리가 이 문제를 이야기할 때 후견인이 한 어떤 말로 미루어, 나는 내 결혼이 우리가 고대하라는 말을 들었던 그 개정 기간이 지난 다음에야 이루어지리라는 걸 알았습니다. 그래서 나는 더욱더, 리처드와 에이다가 조금이라도 더 형편이 펴졌을 때 결혼할 수 있다면 얼마나 기쁠까 생각했습니다."]], [["The term was very near indeed when my guardian was called out of town and went down into Yorkshire on Mr. Woodcourt’s business.", "개정 기간이 정말 코앞에 다가왔을 무렵, 후견인은 시외로 불려가 우드코트 씨 일로 요크셔까지 내려갔습니다."], ["He had told me beforehand that his presence there would be necessary.", "그분은 미리 자기가 그곳에 가 있어야 한다고 나에게 말해두었습니다."], ["I had just come in one night from my dear girl’s and was sitting in the midst of all my new clothes, looking at them all around me and thinking, when a letter from my guardian was brought to me.", "어느 밤 사랑하는 친구네에서 막 돌아와, 내 새 옷가지를 한가운데 두고 앉아 사방에 둘러놓은 그것들을 바라보며 생각에 잠겨 있을 때, 후견인의 편지 한 통이 나에게 전해졌습니다."], ["It asked me to join him in the country and mentioned by what stage-coach my place was taken and at what time in the morning I should have to leave town.", "편지는 시골로 자기에게 와달라고 청하면서, 어느 역마차에 내 자리를 잡아두었고 아침 몇 시에 시내를 떠나야 하는지를 일러주었습니다."], ["It added in a postscript that I would not be many hours from Ada.", "추신에는 에이다와 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있게 되리라는 말이 덧붙어 있었습니다."]], [["I expected few things less than a journey at that time, but I was ready for it in half an hour and set off as appointed early next morning.", "그때 나는 여행만큼 예상 밖인 일도 없었지만, 반 시간 만에 채비를 갖추고 이튿날 이른 아침 지시받은 대로 길을 나섰습니다."], ["I travelled all day, wondering all day what I could be wanted for at such a distance; now I thought it might be for this purpose, and now I thought it might be for that purpose, but I was never, never, never near the truth.", "나는 온종일 길을 달리며, 도대체 무슨 일로 이렇게 먼 곳까지 나를 부른 걸까 종일토록 궁금해했습니다. 이런 까닭이려나 했다가, 또 저런 까닭이려나 했다가 했지만, 나는 결코, 결코, 결코 진실 근처에도 가닿지 못했습니다."]], [["It was night when I came to my journey’s end and found my guardian waiting for me.", "여정의 끝에 다다랐을 때는 밤이었고, 후견인이 나를 기다리고 있었습니다."], ["This was a great relief, for towards evening I had begun to fear (the more so as his letter was a very short one) that he might be ill.", "이건 큰 위안이었으니, 저녁 무렵부터 나는 (편지가 워낙 짧았던 탓에 더더욱) 그분이 편찮으신 게 아닐까 걱정하기 시작했던 것입니다. 그러나 그분은 그 자리에, 더없이 건강한 모습으로 있었습니다."], ["However, there he was, as well as it was possible to be; and when I saw his genial face again at its brightest and best, I said to myself, he has been doing some other great kindness.", "그 정겨운 얼굴이 가장 밝고 환한 빛을 띤 모습을 다시 보았을 때, 나는 속으로 말했습니다. 또 무슨 크나큰 선행을 베푸셨구나, 하고요."], ["Not that it required much penetration to say that, because I knew that his being there at all was an act of kindness.", "그렇게 말하는 데 대단한 통찰이 필요한 건 아니었습니다. 그분이 거기 있다는 사실 자체가 친절을 베푸는 행위임을 알고 있었으니까요."]], [["Supper was ready at the hotel, and when we were alone at table he said, “Full of curiosity, no doubt, little woman, to know why I have brought you here?”", "호텔에 저녁상이 차려졌고, 우리 둘만 식탁에 앉았을 때 그분이 말했습니다. \"내가 너를 왜 여기로 데려왔는지 궁금해 죽겠지, 우리 꼬마 아가씨?\""]], [["“Well, guardian,”", "\"글쎄요, 후견인님.\""], ["said I, “without thinking myself a Fatima or you a Blue Beard, I am a little curious about it.”", "내가 말했습니다. \"제가 저를 파티마라고, 후견인님을 푸른수염이라고 여기는 건 아니지만, 좀 궁금하긴 해요.\""]], [["“Then to ensure your night’s rest, my love,”", "* 파티마와 푸른수염: 페로의 동화 '푸른수염'에 나오는 인물들."], ["he returned gaily, “I won’t wait until to-morrow to tell you.", "호기심에 못 이겨 들어가서는 안 될 방을 열어보는 아내가 파티마다."], ["I have very much wished to express to Woodcourt, somehow, my sense of his humanity to poor unfortunate Jo, his inestimable services to my young cousins, and his value to us all.", "\"그렇다면 네가 밤에 편히 쉴 수 있도록, 얘야,\" 그분이 명랑하게 대꾸했습니다."], ["When it was decided that he should settle here, it came into my head that I might ask his acceptance of some unpretending and suitable little place to lay his own head in.", "\"내일까지 기다리지 않고 말해주마. 나는 어떻게든 우드코트에게, 가엾고 불운한 조에게 베푼 그의 인정과, 내 어린 사촌들에게 베푼 값을 매길 수 없는 봉사와, 우리 모두에게 그가 지니는 소중함에 대해 내 마음을 전하고 싶었단다."], ["I therefore caused such a place to be looked out for, and such a place was found on very easy terms, and I have been touching it up for him and making it habitable.", "그가 이곳에 정착하기로 정해졌을 때, 나는 그가 제 몸 누일 소박하고 적당한 작은 보금자리 하나를 받아주지 않겠느냐고 청해볼까 하는 생각이 떠올랐지."], ["However, when I walked over it the day before yesterday and it was reported ready, I found that I was not housekeeper enough to know whether things were all as they ought to be.", "그래서 그런 곳을 찾아보게 했더니, 아주 손쉬운 조건으로 그런 곳을 하나 찾아냈고, 나는 그를 위해 그곳을 손질해 사람이 살 만하게 만들어 왔단다. 그런데 그저께 그곳을 둘러보며 다 됐다는 보고를 받고 보니, 내가 살림을 아는 사람이 못 되어서 모든 게 마땅한 대로 되었는지 어떤지를 알 수가 없더구나."], ["So I sent off for the best little housekeeper that could possibly be got to come and give me her advice and opinion.", "그래서 어떻게든 구할 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 꼬마 살림꾼을 불러다 조언과 의견을 듣기로 한 거지. 그리고 여기 그 사람이 있구나.\""], ["And here she is,”", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["said my guardian, “laughing and crying both together!”", "\"웃으면서 울고, 둘을 한꺼번에 하면서 말이야!\""]], [["Because he was so dear, so good, so admirable.", "그분이 너무나 다정하고, 너무나 선하고, 너무나 존경스러웠기 때문입니다."], ["I tried to tell him what I thought of him, but I could not articulate a word.", "나는 그분을 어떻게 생각하는지 말하려 했지만, 한마디도 또렷이 내뱉을 수가 없었습니다."]], [["“Tut, tut!”", "\"쯧, 쯧!\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."], ["“You make too much of it, little woman.", "\"별것도 아닌 일에 너무 호들갑이로구나, 꼬마 아가씨."], ["Why, how you sob, Dame Durden, how you sob!”", "아니, 더든 아주머니, 어쩜 그리 흐느끼니, 그렇게 흐느껴서야!\""]], [["“It is with exquisite pleasure, guardian—with a heart full of thanks.”", "\"이건 더없는 기쁨에서 나오는 거예요, 후견인님—감사한 마음으로 가슴이 벅차서요.\""]], [["“Well, well,” said he.", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["“I am delighted that you approve.", "그분이 말했습니다. \"네 마음에 들었다니 기쁘구나."], ["I thought you would.", "그럴 줄 알았지."], ["I meant it as a pleasant surprise for the little mistress of Bleak House.”", "황폐한 집의 꼬마 안주인에게 즐거운 깜짝 선물이 되라고 한 거란다.\""]], [["I kissed him and dried my eyes.", "나는 그분에게 입을 맞추고 눈물을 닦았습니다."], ["“I know now!”", "\"이제 알겠어요!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["“I have seen this in your face a long while.”", "\"오래전부터 후견인님 얼굴에서 이걸 봐왔거든요.\""]], [["“No; have you really, my dear?”", "\"아니, 정말 그랬니, 얘야?\""], ["said he.", "그분이 말했습니다."], ["“What a Dame Durden it is to read a face!”", "\"사람 얼굴을 읽어내는 더든 아주머니라니, 참!\""]], [["He was so quaintly cheerful that I could not long be otherwise, and was almost ashamed of having been otherwise at all.", "그분이 어찌나 별스럽게 쾌활했던지 나도 오래 그렇지 않을 수 없었고, 조금이라도 그렇지 않았던 것이 거의 부끄러울 지경이었습니다."], ["When I went to bed, I cried.", "잠자리에 들었을 때 나는 울었습니다."], ["I am bound to confess that I cried; but I hope it was with pleasure, though I am not quite sure it was with pleasure.", "울었다고 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다. 다만 그것이 기쁨에서 나온 것이기를 바라지만, 기쁨에서 나온 것인지는 그리 확신할 수 없습니다."], ["I repeated every word of the letter twice over.", "나는 편지의 한마디 한마디를 두 번씩 거듭 외었습니다."]], [["A most beautiful summer morning succeeded, and after breakfast we went out arm in arm to see the house of which I was to give my mighty housekeeping opinion.", "더없이 아름다운 여름 아침이 뒤따랐고, 아침을 먹은 뒤 우리는 팔짱을 끼고 나가 내가 그 대단한 살림꾼의 의견을 내놓아야 할 그 집을 보러 갔습니다."], ["We entered a flower-garden by a gate in a side wall, of which he had the key, and the first thing I saw was that the beds and flowers were all laid out according to the manner of my beds and flowers at home.", "우리는 옆 담장에 난 문으로 화원에 들어섰는데, 그 열쇠를 그분이 갖고 있었습니다. 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은, 화단과 꽃들이 모두 우리 집 내 화단과 꽃들과 똑같은 방식으로 꾸며져 있다는 것이었습니다."]], [["“You see, my dear,”", "\"보다시피, 얘야,\""], ["observed my guardian, standing still with a delighted face to watch my looks, “knowing there could be no better plan, I borrowed yours.”", "후견인이 기쁨에 찬 얼굴로 내 표정을 살피려 가만히 서서 말했습니다. \"이보다 나은 본보기는 없을 줄 알기에 네 것을 빌렸단다.\""]], [["We went on by a pretty little orchard, where the cherries were nestling among the green leaves and the shadows of the apple-trees were sporting on the grass, to the house itself—a cottage, quite a rustic cottage of doll’s rooms; but such a lovely place, so tranquil and so beautiful, with such a rich and smiling country spread around it; with water sparkling away into the distance, here all overhung with summer-growth, there turning a humming mill; at its nearest point glancing through a meadow by the cheerful town, where cricket-players were assembling in bright groups and a flag was flying from a white tent that rippled in the sweet west wind. And still, as we went through the pretty rooms, out at the little rustic verandah doors, and underneath the tiny wooden colonnades garlanded with woodbine, jasmine, and honey-suckle, I saw in the papering on the walls, in the colours of the furniture, in the arrangement of all the pretty objects, MY little tastes and fancies, MY little methods and inventions which they used to laugh at while they praised them, my odd ways everywhere.", "우리는 푸른 잎새 사이로 버찌가 옹기종기 깃들어 있고 사과나무 그림자가 풀밭에서 장난치는 어여쁜 과수원을 지나 집 자체로 나아갔습니다. 인형의 방들로 이루어진 오두막, 그야말로 시골티 나는 오두막이었지만, 그토록 사랑스러운 곳, 그토록 고요하고 그토록 아름다운 곳, 그 둘레로 그토록 풍요롭고 미소 짓는 들녘이 펼쳐진 곳이었습니다. 물줄기는 저 멀리로 반짝이며 흘러가는데, 한쪽은 온통 여름 초목에 뒤덮였고 다른 한쪽은 윙윙대는 물방앗간을 돌렸으며, 가장 가까운 지점에서는 명랑한 마을 곁 풀밭을 가로질러 어른거렸습니다. 그 마을에서는 크리켓 선수들이 화사한 무리를 이루어 모여들고, 향긋한 서풍에 나부끼는 하얀 천막에서 깃발 하나가 펄럭이고 있었습니다. 그리고 우리가 그 어여쁜 방들을 지나, 작은 시골풍 베란다 문을 빠져나가, 인동덩굴과 재스민과 인동꽃으로 화관처럼 엮인 자그마한 나무 기둥 회랑 아래를 걷는 동안에도, 나는 벽지 무늬에서, 가구 빛깔에서, 그 모든 어여쁜 물건의 배치에서, 그들이 칭찬하면서도 웃어넘기곤 했던 나의 자그마한 취향과 공상을, 나의 자그마한 방식과 궁리를, 사방 어디서나 나의 별난 버릇들을 보았습니다."]], [["I could not say enough in admiration of what was all so beautiful, but one secret doubt arose in my mind when I saw this, I thought, oh, would he be the happier for it!", "이 모든 아름다운 것에 대해 아무리 감탄해도 모자랄 지경이었지만, 이것을 보았을 때 내 마음에는 남모를 의심 하나가 솟았습니다. 아, 이걸로 그분이 더 행복해질까, 하고 나는 생각했습니다."], ["Would it not have been better for his peace that I should not have been so brought before him?", "내가 이렇게 그분 앞에 나서지 않는 편이 그분의 평안을 위해 더 낫지 않았을까?"], ["Because although I was not what he thought me, still he loved me very dearly, and it might remind him mournfully of what be believed he had lost.", "내가 그분이 생각하던 그런 사람은 아니었어도, 그분은 여전히 나를 무척이나 깊이 사랑했으니, 이것이 그분이 잃었다고 믿는 것을 슬프게 일깨울지도 모르는 일이었습니다."], ["I did not wish him to forget me—perhaps he might not have done so, without these aids to his memory—but my way was easier than his, and I could have reconciled myself even to that so that he had been the happier for it.", "나는 그분이 나를 잊기를 바라지는 않았습니다—그분은 이런 기억의 보조 장치가 없었더라면 어쩌면 잊지 않았을지도 모릅니다—그러나 내 길이 그분의 길보다 수월했으니, 그분이 그걸로 더 행복해질 수만 있다면 나는 그 정도는 기꺼이 받아들일 수 있었을 것입니다."]], [["“And now, little woman,”", "\"자, 이제, 꼬마 아가씨,\""], ["said my guardian, whom I had never seen so proud and joyful as in showing me these things and watching my appreciation of them, “now, last of all, for the name of this house.”", "이런 것들을 나에게 보여주며 내가 그것들을 마음에 들어 하는 모습을 지켜보는 그 순간만큼 자랑스럽고 즐거워하는 후견인을 나는 본 적이 없었습니다. \"이제 마지막으로, 이 집 이름이란다.\""]], [["“What is it called, dear guardian?”", "\"뭐라고 부르나요, 후견인님?\""]], [["“My child,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["said he, “come and see,”", "그분이 말했습니다. \"와서 보렴.\""]], [["He took me to the porch, which he had hitherto avoided, and said, pausing before we went out, “My dear child, don’t you guess the name?”", "그분은 그때까지 피해 왔던 현관으로 나를 데려갔고, 우리가 나가기 전에 멈춰 서서 말했습니다. \"얘야, 이름을 짐작하지 못하겠니?\" \"모르겠어요!\" 내가 말했습니다."]], [["“No!” said I.", "우리는 현관을 나섰고, 그분은 그 위에 적힌 글자를 나에게 보여주었습니다."], ["We went out of the porch and he showed me written over it, Bleak House.", "황폐한 집."]], [["He led me to a seat among the leaves close by, and sitting down beside me and taking my hand in his, spoke to me thus, “My darling girl, in what there has been between us, I have, I hope, been really solicitous for your happiness.", "그분은 바로 곁 잎새 사이의 자리로 나를 이끌어, 내 곁에 앉아 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 이렇게 말했습니다. \"사랑하는 내 딸아, 그동안 우리 사이에 있었던 일에서, 나는 진정으로 네 행복을 마음 깊이 바랐단다, 바라건대 말이지."], ["When I wrote you the letter to which you brought the answer,”", "네가 답을 가져왔던 그 편지를 내가 썼을 때,\""], ["smiling as he referred to it, “I had my own too much in view; but I had yours too.", "그분은 그 편지를 떠올리며 미소 지었습니다. \"나는 내 행복을 너무 많이 염두에 두었지."], ["Whether, under different circumstances, I might ever have renewed the old dream I sometimes dreamed when you were very young, of making you my wife one day, I need not ask myself.", "하지만 네 행복도 마음에 두었단다. 다른 사정이었다면 내가 언젠가 너를 아내로 맞으리라는, 네가 아주 어렸을 적 이따금 꾸곤 했던 그 옛 꿈을 다시 꾸었을지 어떨지는 굳이 나 자신에게 물을 필요가 없겠지."], ["I did renew it, and I wrote my letter, and you brought your answer.", "나는 그걸 다시 꾸었고, 편지를 썼고, 너는 답을 가져왔어."], ["You are following what I say, my child?”", "내 말을 잘 따라오고 있니, 얘야?\""]], [["I was cold, and I trembled violently, but not a word he uttered was lost.", "나는 몸이 차가워졌고 심하게 떨렸지만, 그분이 내뱉는 한마디도 놓치지 않았습니다."], ["As I sat looking fixedly at him and the sun’s rays descended, softly shining through the leaves upon his bare head, I felt as if the brightness on him must be like the brightness of the angels.", "내가 앉아 그분을 뚫어지게 바라보고 햇살이 잎새 사이로 부드럽게 비쳐 그분의 맨머리 위로 내려앉는 동안, 나는 그분 위에 어린 그 광채가 마치 천사들의 광채와도 같다고 느꼈습니다."]], [["“Hear me, my love, but do not speak.", "\"내 말을 듣되, 말은 하지 말거라, 얘야."], ["It is for me to speak now.", "이제 말할 차례는 나란다."], ["When it was that I began to doubt whether what I had done would really make you happy is no matter.", "내가 한 일이 정말 너를 행복하게 해줄지를 의심하기 시작한 게 언제였는지는 중요치 않아."], ["Woodcourt came home, and I soon had no doubt at all.”", "우드코트가 돌아왔고, 나는 이내 아무 의심도 남지 않았단다.\""]], [["I clasped him round the neck and hung my head upon his breast and wept.", "나는 두 팔로 그분의 목을 끌어안고 그분의 가슴에 고개를 묻고 울었습니다."], ["“Lie lightly, confidently here, my child,”", "\"여기 가볍게, 마음 놓고 기대거라, 얘야.\""], ["said he, pressing me gently to him.", "그분이 나를 부드럽게 끌어당기며 말했습니다."], ["“I am your guardian and your father now.", "\"나는 이제 네 후견인이자 네 아버지란다."], ["Rest confidently here.”", "마음 놓고 여기 쉬렴.\""]], [["Soothingly, like the gentle rustling of the leaves; and genially, like the ripening weather; and radiantly and beneficently, like the sunshine, he went on.", "잎새의 부드러운 살랑임처럼 다독이며, 무르익는 날씨처럼 정겹게, 햇살처럼 환하고 자애롭게, 그분은 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“Understand me, my dear girl.", "\"내 말을 알아듣거라, 사랑하는 내 딸아."], ["I had no doubt of your being contented and happy with me, being so dutiful and so devoted; but I saw with whom you would be happier.", "너처럼 그토록 효성스럽고 그토록 헌신적인 사람이 나와 함께 만족하고 행복하리라는 데는 의심이 없었단다. 하지만 나는 네가 누구와 함께라면 더 행복할지를 보았지."], ["That I penetrated his secret when Dame Durden was blind to it is no wonder, for I knew the good that could never change in her better far than she did.", "더든 아주머니가 그걸 까맣게 모르고 있을 때 내가 그의 비밀을 꿰뚫어 본 건 이상한 일이 아니야. 그 안에서 결코 변할 수 없는 선함을, 정작 그 자신보다 내가 훨씬 더 잘 알고 있었으니까."], ["Well!", "그래! 나는 오래도록 앨런 우드코트의 속내를 들어왔지만, 정작 그는 어제, 네가 여기 오기 몇 시간 전에야 내 속내를 들었단다."], ["I have long been in Allan Woodcourt’s confidence, although he was not, until yesterday, a few hours before you came here, in mine.", "그러나 나는 내 에스더의 빛나는 본보기를 그냥 잃어버릴 수는 없었어. 사랑하는 내 딸의 미덕을 한 점이라도 눈에 띄지 않고 기림받지 못한 채로 둘 수는 없었지."], ["But I would not have my Esther’s bright example lost; I would not have a jot of my dear girl’s virtues unobserved and unhonoured; I would not have her admitted on sufferance into the line of Morgan ap-Kerrig, no, not for the weight in gold of all the mountains in Wales!”", "내 딸이 모건 앱 케리그의 혈통에 그저 너그러운 묵인으로 받아들여지게 둘 수는 없었단다. 아니, 웨일스의 모든 산을 황금으로 달아 준다 해도 그럴 수는 없었어!\""]], [["He stopped to kiss me on the forehead, and I sobbed and wept afresh.", "그분은 잠시 멈추고 내 이마에 입을 맞추었고, 나는 다시금 흐느끼며 울었습니다."], ["For I felt as if I could not bear the painful delight of his praise.", "그분의 칭찬이 주는 고통스러운 기쁨을 차마 견딜 수 없을 것만 같았기 때문입니다."]], [["“Hush, little woman!", "\"쉿, 꼬마 아가씨! 울지 말거라."], ["Don’t cry; this is to be a day of joy.", "오늘은 기쁨의 날이 되어야 해. 나는 이날을 손꼽아 기다려왔단다,\""], ["I have looked forward to it,”", "그분이 의기양양하게 말했습니다."], ["he said exultingly, “for months on months!", "\"몇 달이고 몇 달이고 말이야! 몇 마디만 더 하면, 종종걸음 아주머니, 내 할 말은 끝난단다."], ["A few words more, Dame Trot, and I have said my say.", "내 에스더의 가치를 티끌만큼도 헛되이 버리지 않겠다고 마음먹고, 나는 우드코트 부인을 따로 불러 속내를 털어놓았지. '자, 부인,'"], ["Determined not to throw away one atom of my Esther’s worth, I took Mrs.", "내가 말했네. '나는 분명히 알아챘소—아니, 사실은 확실히 알고 있소—당신 아들이 내 피후견인을 사랑한다는 걸 말이오."], ["Woodcourt into a separate confidence.", "게다가 내 피후견인이 당신 아들을 사랑한다는 것도 아주 확신하오."], ["‘Now, madam,’ said I, ‘I clearly perceive—and indeed I know, to boot—that your son loves my ward.", "하지만 그 애는 의무감과 애정 때문에 제 사랑을 희생할 것이고, 어찌나 완전하게, 어찌나 철저하게, 어찌나 경건하게 희생할지, 당신이 밤낮으로 지켜본다 해도 결코 눈치채지 못할 것이오.' 그러고는 부인에게 우리 이야기를 다 들려주었단다—우리의—너와 나의 이야기 말이야."], ["I am further very sure that my ward loves your son, but will sacrifice her love to a sense of duty and affection, and will sacrifice it so completely, so entirely, so religiously, that you should never suspect it though you watched her night and day.’ Then I told her all our story—ours—yours and mine.", "'자, 부인,' 내가 말했지."], ["‘Now, madam,’ said I, ‘come you, knowing this, and live with us.", "'이걸 다 알고서도, 우리와 함께 와서 사시오. 와서 내 아이를 시시각각 보시오."], ["Come you, and see my child from hour to hour; set what you see against her pedigree, which is this, and this’—for I scorned to mince it—‘and tell me what is the true legitimacy when you shall have quite made up your mind on that subject.’ Why, honour to her old Welsh blood, my dear,”", "당신이 본 것을, 그 애의 혈통—그건 이러이러하오—과 견주어 보고,'—나는 그걸 에둘러 말하기를 경멸했으니까—'그 문제에 대해 마음을 온전히 정한 다음에, 무엇이 참된 정통인지 나에게 말해 보시오.' 아니, 부인의 그 오랜 웨일스 핏줄에 영광 있으라, 얘야,\""], ["cried my guardian with enthusiasm, “I believe the heart it animates beats no less warmly, no less admiringly, no less lovingly, towards Dame Durden than my own!”", "후견인이 열정에 차 외쳤습니다. \"내 믿건대, 그 핏줄이 고동치게 하는 그 가슴은, 더든 아주머니를 향해 내 가슴 못지않게 따뜻하게, 못지않게 감탄하며, 못지않게 사랑스럽게 뛰고 있단다!\""]], [["He tenderly raised my head, and as I clung to him, kissed me in his old fatherly way again and again.", "그분은 다정하게 내 고개를 들어 올렸고, 내가 그분에게 매달리자, 옛 아버지 같은 그 다정한 방식으로 거듭거듭 입을 맞추었습니다."], ["What a light, now, on the protecting manner I had thought about!", "내가 곰곰이 생각하곤 했던 그 보호자다운 태도 위로, 이제 얼마나 환한 빛이 비추던지요!"]], [["“One more last word.", "\"마지막으로 한마디만 더."], ["When Allan Woodcourt spoke to you, my dear, he spoke with my knowledge and consent—but I gave him no encouragement, not I, for these surprises were my great reward, and I was too miserly to part with a scrap of it.", "앨런 우드코트가 너에게 말을 건넸을 때, 얘야, 그는 내가 알고 동의한 가운데 그렇게 한 것이란다—하지만 나는 그에게 아무런 부추김도 주지 않았지, 그럼, 안 했고말고. 이 깜짝 선물들이야말로 나에게 크나큰 보답이었으니, 나는 그 한 조각이라도 내놓기에는 너무 인색했단 말이다."], ["He was to come and tell me all that passed, and he did.", "그는 오가는 모든 이야기를 나에게 와서 전하기로 되어 있었고, 그렇게 했지."], ["I have no more to say.", "더 할 말은 없구나."], ["My dearest, Allan Woodcourt stood beside your father when he lay dead—stood beside your mother.", "사랑하는 내 딸아, 앨런 우드코트는 네 아버지가 숨을 거두고 누웠을 때 그 곁에 서 있었단다—네 어머니 곁에 서 있었지."], ["This is Bleak House.", "이곳이 황폐한 집이란다."], ["This day I give this house its little mistress; and before God, it is the brightest day in all my life!”", "오늘 나는 이 집에 그 꼬마 안주인을 주노라. 그리고 하느님 앞에 맹세컨대, 이날은 내 평생에 가장 환한 날이로구나!\""]], [["He rose and raised me with him.", "그분이 일어서며 나를 함께 일으켜 세웠습니다."], ["We were no longer alone.", "우리는 더는 둘만이 아니었습니다."], ["My husband—I have called him by that name full seven happy years now—stood at my side.", "내 남편이—나는 이제 꼬박 칠 년의 행복한 세월 동안 그 이름으로 그를 불러왔습니다—내 곁에 서 있었습니다."]], [["“Allan,”", "\"앨런,\""], ["said my guardian, “take from me a willing gift, the best wife that ever man had.", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"나로부터 기꺼운 선물을 받게나, 사내가 가질 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 아내일세."], ["What more can I say for you than that I know you deserve her!", "자네가 이 사람을 받을 자격이 있음을 내가 안다는 것 말고 자네에게 무슨 말을 더 하겠나!"], ["Take with her the little home she brings you.", "이 사람이 자네에게 가져다주는 이 작은 보금자리도 함께 받게."], ["You know what she will make it, Allan; you know what she has made its namesake.", "이 사람이 이곳을 어떻게 만들지는 자네가 알겠지, 앨런. 같은 이름의 그 집을 어떻게 만들어 왔는지 자네가 아니까."], ["Let me share its felicity sometimes, and what do I sacrifice?", "이따금 그 행복을 나에게도 나눠 주게나, 그런들 내가 무얼 희생하겠나?"], ["Nothing, nothing.”", "아무것도, 아무것도 없네.\""]], [["He kissed me once again, and now the tears were in his eyes as he said more softly, “Esther, my dearest, after so many years, there is a kind of parting in this too.", "그분은 한 번 더 나에게 입을 맞추었고, 이제 두 눈에 눈물이 어린 채 한결 부드럽게 말했습니다. \"에스더, 사랑하는 내 딸아, 이토록 오랜 세월이 지나, 여기에도 일종의 작별이 있구나."], ["I know that my mistake has caused you some distress.", "내 잘못이 너에게 얼마간 괴로움을 안겼다는 걸 안다."], ["Forgive your old guardian, in restoring him to his old place in your affections; and blot it out of your memory.", "네 마음속 옛자리로 이 늙은 후견인을 다시 들여, 그를 용서해 주려무나. 그리고 그건 네 기억에서 지워 버리렴."], ["Allan, take my dear.”", "앨런, 내 소중한 사람을 받게나.\""]], [["He moved away from under the green roof of leaves, and stopping in the sunlight outside and turning cheerfully towards us, said, “I shall be found about here somewhere.", "그분은 푸른 잎의 지붕 아래에서 걸어 나와, 바깥 햇살 속에 멈춰 서서 우리 쪽으로 명랑하게 돌아서며 말했습니다. \"나는 이 근방 어딘가에 있을 테니."], ["It’s a west wind, little woman, due west!", "서풍이로구나, 꼬마 아가씨, 정서풍이야!"], ["Let no one thank me any more, for I am going to revert to my bachelor habits, and if anybody disregards this warning, I’ll run away and never come back!”", "이제 누구도 나에게 더 고마워하지 말거라, 나는 다시 홀아비 시절 버릇으로 돌아갈 참이니까. 누구든 이 경고를 무시한다면, 나는 달아나서 다시는 돌아오지 않을 테다!\""]], [["What happiness was ours that day, what joy, what rest, what hope, what gratitude, what bliss!", "그날 우리의 행복은 얼마나 컸던지, 그 기쁨, 그 안식, 그 희망, 그 감사, 그 더없는 복락이라니!"], ["We were to be married before the month was out, but when we were to come and take possession of our own house was to depend on Richard and Ada.", "우리는 그달이 가기 전에 결혼하기로 했지만, 우리 집으로 와서 거기 살림을 차릴 시기는 리처드와 에이다에게 달려 있었습니다."]], [["We all three went home together next day.", "이튿날 우리 셋은 함께 집으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["As soon as we arrived in town, Allan went straight to see Richard and to carry our joyful news to him and my darling.", "시내에 닿자마자 앨런은 리처드를 보러, 그리고 우리의 기쁜 소식을 그와 내 사랑하는 친구에게 전하러 곧장 갔습니다. 늦은 시각이긴 했지만, 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 잠깐 에이다에게 가볼 작정이었습니다."], ["Late as it was, I meant to go to her for a few minutes before lying down to sleep, but I went home with my guardian first to make his tea for him and to occupy the old chair by his side, for I did not like to think of its being empty so soon.", "그래도 먼저 후견인과 함께 집으로 가서 그분의 차를 끓여 드리고 그분 곁 낡은 의자에 앉으려 했습니다. 그 의자가 이렇게 빨리 비게 될 거라고는 생각하고 싶지 않았기 때문입니다."]], [["When we came home we found that a young man had called three times in the course of that one day to see me and that having been told on the occasion of his third call that I was not expected to return before ten o’clock at night, he had left word that he would call about then.", "집에 돌아와 보니, 어느 젊은이가 그 하루 동안에만 세 번이나 나를 찾아왔는데, 세 번째 방문 때 내가 밤 열 시 전에는 돌아오지 않을 거라는 말을 듣고는, 그쯤 다시 들르겠다는 말을 남겼다고 했습니다."], ["He had left his card three times.", "그는 세 번 명함을 두고 갔습니다."], ["Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨였습니다."]], [["As I naturally speculated on the object of these visits, and as I always associated something ludicrous with the visitor, it fell out that in laughing about Mr. Guppy I told my guardian of his old proposal and his subsequent retraction.", "그 방문의 목적이 궁금해진 데다, 나는 늘 그 방문객을 무언가 우스꽝스러운 것과 결부시켜 떠올리곤 했던 터라, 거피 씨를 두고 웃다가 그만 후견인에게 그의 옛 청혼과 그 뒤의 철회 이야기를 하게 되었습니다."], ["“After that,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said my guardian, “we will certainly receive this hero.”", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"이 영웅을 꼭 맞아들여야겠구나.\""], ["So instructions were given that Mr. Guppy should be shown in when he came again, and they were scarcely given when he did come again.", "그래서 거피 씨가 다시 오면 안으로 들이라는 지시가 내려졌는데, 그 지시가 떨어지기가 무섭게 그가 다시 왔습니다."]], [["He was embarrassed when he found my guardian with me, but recovered himself and said, “How de do, sir?”", "그는 후견인이 나와 함께 있는 것을 보고 당황했지만, 이내 정신을 차리고 말했습니다. \"안녕하십니까, 선생님?\""]], [["“How do you do, sir?”", "\"안녕하시오, 선생?\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“Thank you, sir, I am tolerable,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님, 그럭저럭 지냅니다.\""], ["returned Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Will you allow me to introduce my mother, Mrs. Guppy of the Old Street Road, and my particular friend, Mr.", "\"제 어머니, 올드스트리트로드의 거피 부인과, 제 각별한 벗 위블 씨를 소개해도 되겠습니까."], ["Weevle. That is to say, my friend has gone by the name of Weevle, but his name is really and truly Jobling.”", "다시 말해, 제 벗이 위블이라는 이름으로 통해 왔습니다만, 그 이름은 사실 진짜로 조블링입니다.\""]], [["My guardian begged them to be seated, and they all sat down.", "후견인이 그들에게 앉으라고 청했고, 그들은 모두 자리에 앉았습니다."]], [["“Tony,”", "\"토니,\""], ["said Mr. Guppy to his friend after an awkward silence.", "어색한 침묵 끝에 거피 씨가 자기 벗에게 말했습니다."], ["“Will you open the case?”", "\"자네가 사건을 열어 주겠나?\""]], [["“Do it yourself,”", "\"자네가 직접 하게나.\""], ["returned the friend rather tartly.", "그 벗이 다소 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“Well, Mr. Jarndyce, sir,”", "\"그럼, 잔다이스 씨, 선생님,\""], ["Mr. Guppy, after a moment’s consideration, began, to the great diversion of his mother, which she displayed by nudging Mr.", "잠시 생각한 끝에 거피 씨가 입을 떼었습니다."], ["Jobling with her elbow and winking at me in a most remarkable manner, “I had an idea that I should see Miss Summerson by herself and was not quite prepared for your esteemed presence.", "그의 어머니는 그게 무척이나 즐거웠던지, 팔꿈치로 조블링 씨를 쿡쿡 찌르고 나에게 더없이 별난 방식으로 윙크하며 그 즐거움을 드러냈습니다. \"저는 서머슨 양을 단둘이 만나뵐 줄 알았던 터라, 존경하올 선생님이 자리하실 줄은 미처 예상하지 못했습니다."], ["But Miss Summerson has mentioned to you, perhaps, that something has passed between us on former occasions?”", "그렇지만 서머슨 양이 어쩌면 선생님께 말씀드렸을지 모르겠습니다만, 예전에 저희 사이에 무슨 일이 좀 오갔다는 것을요?\""]], [["“Miss Summerson,”", "\"서머슨 양이,\""], ["returned my guardian, smiling, “has made a communication to that effect to me.”", "후견인이 미소 지으며 대꾸했습니다. \"그런 취지의 이야기를 나에게 전한 바 있소.\""]], [["“That,” said Mr.", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["Guppy, “makes matters easier.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Sir, I have come out of my articles at Kenge and Carboy’s, and I believe with satisfaction to all parties.", "\"일이 한결 수월해지는군요. 선생님, 저는 켄지 앤드 카보이 사무소에서 견습 계약을 마쳤습니다."], ["I am now admitted (after undergoing an examination that’s enough to badger a man blue, touching a pack of nonsense that he don’t want to know) on the roll of attorneys and have taken out my certificate, if it would be any satisfaction to you to see it.”", "모든 당사자가 만족할 만하게 마무리되었다고 믿습니다. 저는 이제 (사람을 진이 빠지도록 들볶는 시험을, 알 필요도 없는 잡다한 헛소리에 관해 치른 끝에) 변호사 명부에 등재되어 자격증을 받았으니, 선생님께서 보시는 게 무슨 흡족함이 된다면 보여 드리지요.\""]], [["“Thank you, Mr. Guppy,”", "\"고맙소, 거피 씨.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다."], ["“I am quite willing—I believe I use a legal phrase—to admit the certificate.”", "\"나는 기꺼이—내가 법률 용어를 쓰는 것 같소만—그 자격증을 인정하겠소.\""]], [["Mr. Guppy therefore desisted from taking something out of his pocket and proceeded without it.", "그리하여 거피 씨는 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내려던 것을 그만두고 그것 없이 말을 이었습니다."]], [["“I have no capital myself, but my mother has a little property which takes the form of an annuity”—here Mr.", "\"저 자신은 자본이 없습니다만, 제 어머니께서 연금 형태로 된 약간의 재산을 가지고 계십니다\"—여기서 거피 씨의 어머니는 그 말을 아무리 즐겨도 모자란다는 듯 고개를 절레절레 흔들고, 손수건을 입에 갖다 대며 다시금 나에게 윙크했습니다—\"그러니 사업을 꾸리며 들어가는 자질구레한 경비로 몇 파운드쯤은 결코 모자랄 일이 없습니다."], ["Guppy’s mother rolled her head as if she never could sufficiently enjoy the observation, and put her handkerchief to her mouth, and again winked at me—“and a few pounds for expenses out of pocket in conducting business will never be wanting, free of interest, which is an advantage, you know,”", "게다가 무이자이니, 아시다시피 그게 이점이지요.\""], ["said Mr. Guppy feelingly.", "거피 씨가 감정을 실어 말했습니다."]], [["“Certainly an advantage,”", "\"분명 이점이오.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“I HAVE some connexion,” pursued Mr.", "\"저에게는 약간의 연줄이 있습니다,\""], ["Guppy, “and it lays in the direction of Walcot Square, Lambeth.", "거피 씨가 말을 이었습니다. \"그게 램버스의 월컷 광장 쪽으로 뻗어 있지요."], ["I have therefore taken a ’ouse in that locality, which, in the opinion of my friends, is a hollow bargain (taxes ridiculous, and use of fixtures included in the rent), and intend setting up professionally for myself there forthwith.”", "그래서 저는 그 동네에 집을 한 채 얻었는데, 제 벗들의 의견으로는 거저나 다름없는 거래입니다 (세금이 우습도록 적고, 붙박이 가구 사용료까지 집세에 포함되어 있거든요). 거기서 곧바로 제 사업을 차릴 작정입니다.\""]], [["Here Mr. Guppy’s mother fell into an extraordinary passion of rolling her head and smiling waggishly at anybody who would look at her.", "여기서 거피 씨의 어머니는 또 한바탕 유난스럽게 고개를 절레절레 흔들고, 자기를 봐주는 사람이라면 누구에게나 짓궂게 미소 짓기 시작했습니다."]], [["“It’s a six-roomer, exclusive of kitchens,” said Mr.", "\"부엌을 빼고 방이 여섯입니다,\""], ["Guppy, “and in the opinion of my friends, a commodious tenement.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"제 벗들의 의견으로는 널찍한 집이지요."], ["When I mention my friends, I refer principally to my friend Jobling, who I believe has known me,” Mr.", "제가 벗들이라고 말할 때, 그건 주로 제 벗 조블링을 가리키는데, 제가 믿기로 이 친구는 저를,\""], ["Guppy looked at him with a sentimental air, “from boyhood’s hour.”", "거피 씨가 감상적인 분위기로 그를 바라보았습니다. \"어린 시절부터 알아온 사이입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Jobling confirmed this with a sliding movement of his legs.", "조블링 씨가 두 다리를 슬쩍 미끄러뜨리는 동작으로 이를 확인해 주었습니다."]], [["“My friend Jobling will render me his assistance in the capacity of clerk and will live in the ’ouse,”", "\"제 벗 조블링이 사무원 자격으로 저를 도울 것이며, 그 집에 함께 살 것입니다,\""], ["said Mr. Guppy.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다."], ["“My mother will likewise live in the ’ouse when her present quarter in the Old Street Road shall have ceased and expired; and consequently there will be no want of society.", "\"제 어머니께서도 올드스트리트로드의 현재 거처가 분기 임대를 마치고 만료되면 그 집에 함께 사실 것입니다. 그러니 어울릴 사람이 모자랄 일은 없겠지요."], ["My friend Jobling is naturally aristocratic by taste, and besides being acquainted with the movements of the upper circles, fully backs me in the intentions I am now developing.”", "제 벗 조블링은 천성이 취향상 귀족적인 데다, 상류 사회의 동향에도 밝아서, 제가 지금 펼쳐 보이는 이 계획을 전적으로 뒷받침해 줍니다.\""]], [["Mr. Jobling said “Certainly” and withdrew a little from the elbow of Mr Guppy’s mother.", "조블링 씨가 \"분명히 그렇습니다\"라고 말하며 거피 씨 어머니의 팔꿈치로부터 조금 물러났습니다."]], [["“Now, I have no occasion to mention to you, sir, you being in the confidence of Miss Summerson,” said Mr.", "\"자, 선생님께서는 서머슨 양의 속내를 들으시는 분이니, 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠습니다만,\""], ["Guppy, “(mother, I wish you’d be so good as to keep still), that Miss Summerson’s image was formerly imprinted on my ’eart and that I made her a proposal of marriage.”", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"(어머니, 부디 좀 가만히 계셔 주셨으면 합니다만) 서머슨 양의 모습이 한때 제 가슴에 새겨져 있었고, 제가 그분께 청혼을 했더랬습니다.\""]], [["“That I have heard,”", "\"그건 들었소.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다."]], [["“Circumstances,” pursued Mr.", "\"제가 어찌할 수 없었던, 아니 오히려 정반대였던 사정들이,\""], ["Guppy, “over which I had no control, but quite the contrary, weakened the impression of that image for a time.", "거피 씨가 말을 이었습니다. \"한동안 그 모습의 인상을 흐려놓았지요."], ["At which time Miss Summerson’s conduct was highly genteel; I may even add, magnanimous.”", "그때 서머슨 양의 처신은 더없이 품위 있었습니다. 한술 더 떠 대범했다고까지 말할 수 있겠습니다.\""]], [["My guardian patted me on the shoulder and seemed much amused.", "후견인이 내 어깨를 토닥였는데 무척이나 재미있어하는 눈치였습니다."]], [["“Now, sir,” said Mr.", "\"자, 선생님,\""], ["Guppy, “I have got into that state of mind myself that I wish for a reciprocity of magnanimous behaviour.", "거피 씨가 말했습니다. \"이제 저 자신이 그런 마음가짐에 이르러서, 대범한 처신을 서로 주고받기를 바라게 되었습니다."], ["I wish to prove to Miss Summerson that I can rise to a heighth of which perhaps she hardly thought me capable.", "저는 서머슨 양께, 어쩌면 그분이 제게 그럴 능력이 있다고는 거의 여기지 않으셨을 그런 높은 경지로 제가 올라설 수 있음을 증명하고 싶습니다."], ["I find that the image which I did suppose had been eradicated from my ’eart is NOT eradicated.", "제 가슴에서 뽑혀 나갔으리라 짐작했던 그 모습이, 알고 보니 뽑혀 나가지 않았더군요."], ["Its influence over me is still tremenjous, and yielding to it, I am willing to overlook the circumstances over which none of us have had any control and to renew those proposals to Miss Summerson which I had the honour to make at a former period.", "그것이 제게 미치는 영향력은 여전히 어마어마합니다. 그래서 그것에 굴복하여, 저는 우리 가운데 누구도 어찌할 수 없었던 그 사정들을 못 본 척 넘기고, 예전에 영광스럽게도 제가 서머슨 양께 드렸던 그 청혼을 다시금 드리고자 합니다."], ["I beg to lay the ’ouse in Walcot Square, the business, and myself before Miss Summerson for her acceptance.”", "월컷 광장의 집과, 사업과, 저 자신을 서머슨 양 앞에 바치오니, 부디 받아 주시기를 청합니다.\""]], [["“Very magnanimous indeed, sir,”", "\"참으로 대범하구려, 선생.\""], ["observed my guardian.", "후견인이 말했습니다."]], [["“Well, sir,” replied Mr.", "\"네, 선생님,\""], ["Guppy with candour, “my wish is to BE magnanimous.", "거피 씨가 솔직하게 대답했습니다."], ["I do not consider that in making this offer to Miss Summerson I am by any means throwing myself away; neither is that the opinion of my friends.", "\"제 바람은 대범하게 처신하자는 것입니다. 저는 서머슨 양께 이런 제안을 드리는 것이 결코 제 몸값을 떨어뜨리는 일이라고 여기지 않으며, 제 벗들의 의견도 그렇지 않습니다."], ["Still, there are circumstances which I submit may be taken into account as a set off against any little drawbacks of mine, and so a fair and equitable balance arrived at.”", "그래도 제 자잘한 결점들을 상쇄할 만한 것으로 헤아려 주실 만한 사정들이 있으니, 그렇게 하여 공정하고 형평에 맞는 균형에 이를 수 있겠다는 점을 말씀드립니다.\""]], [["“I take upon myself, sir,”", "\"내가 직접,\""], ["said my guardian, laughing as he rang the bell, “to reply to your proposals on behalf of Miss Summerson.", "후견인이 종을 울리며 웃으며 말했습니다. \"서머슨 양을 대신해 그대의 청혼에 답하리다."], ["She is very sensible of your handsome intentions, and wishes you good evening, and wishes you well.”", "그녀는 그대의 훌륭한 뜻을 매우 고맙게 여기며, 그대에게 좋은 밤 되기를 빌고, 그대가 잘되기를 빈다오.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said Mr. Guppy with a blank look.", "거피 씨가 멍한 표정으로 말했습니다."], ["“Is that tantamount, sir, to acceptance, or rejection, or consideration?”", "\"그게, 선생님, 수락에 해당합니까, 거절입니까, 아니면 검토입니까?\""]], [["“To decided rejection, if you please,”", "\"단호한 거절에 해당하오, 괜찮다면.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다."]], [["Mr. Guppy looked incredulously at his friend, and at his mother, who suddenly turned very angry, and at the floor, and at the ceiling.", "거피 씨는 못 믿겠다는 눈으로 자기 벗을 보았다가, 갑자기 몹시 화가 난 자기 어머니를 보았다가, 바닥을 보았다가, 천장을 보았습니다."]], [["“Indeed?” said he.", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["“Then, Jobling, if you was the friend you represent yourself, I should think you might hand my mother out of the gangway instead of allowing her to remain where she ain’t wanted.”", "그가 말했습니다. \"그렇다면, 조블링, 자네가 스스로 자처하는 그런 벗이라면, 어머니를 원치도 않는 곳에 그대로 두지 말고 통로 밖으로 모셔 내드릴 만도 한데 말이지.\""]], [["But Mrs. Guppy positively refused to come out of the gangway.", "그러나 거피 부인은 통로 밖으로 나오기를 한사코 거부했습니다."], ["She wouldn’t hear of it.", "들으려고도 하지 않았습니다."], ["“Why, get along with you,”", "\"아니, 썩 꺼지지 못해,\""], ["said she to my guardian, “what do you mean?", "그녀가 후견인에게 말했습니다. \"무슨 뜻이야?"], ["Ain’t my son good enough for you?", "내 아들이 당신한테 모자란단 말이야?"], ["You ought to be ashamed of yourself.", "부끄러운 줄 알아야지."], ["Get out with you!”", "썩 나가!\""]], [["“My good lady,”", "\"이보시오, 부인.\""], ["returned my guardian, “it is hardly reasonable to ask me to get out of my own room.”", "후견인이 대꾸했습니다. \"내 집 내 방에서 나가라고 청하는 건 도무지 사리에 맞지 않소.\""]], [["“I don’t care for that,” said Mrs.", "\"그딴 건 모르겠고,\""], ["Guppy. “Get out with you.", "거피 부인이 말했습니다."], ["If we ain’t good enough for you, go and procure somebody that is good enough.", "\"썩 나가. 우리가 당신한테 모자라거든, 가서 모자라지 않은 사람을 구해 와."], ["Go along and find ’em.”", "어서 가서 찾으라고.\""]], [["I was quite unprepared for the rapid manner in which Mrs. Guppy’s power of jocularity merged into a power of taking the profoundest offence.", "거피 부인의 그 익살 부리는 재간이 더없이 깊은 모욕감으로 돌변하는 그 잽싼 모양새에 나는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았습니다."]], [["“Go along and find somebody that’s good enough for you,”", "\"가서 당신한테 모자라지 않을 사람을 찾으라니까,\""], ["repeated Mrs. Guppy.", "거피 부인이 거듭 말했습니다."], ["“Get out!”", "\"썩 꺼져!\""], ["Nothing seemed to astonish Mr. Guppy’s mother so much and to make her so very indignant as our not getting out.", "거피 씨의 어머니를 그토록 놀라게 하고 그토록 분개하게 만드는 일은, 우리가 나가지 않는 것만 한 게 없는 듯했습니다."], ["“Why don’t you get out?”", "\"왜 안 나가는 거야?\""], ["said Mrs. Guppy.", "거피 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“What are you stopping here for?”", "\"여기서 뭘 꾸물대고 있는 거냐고?\""]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["interposed her son, always getting before her and pushing her back with one shoulder as she sidled at my guardian, “WILL you hold your tongue?”", "그녀가 후견인에게로 슬금슬금 다가갈 때마다 늘 앞을 막아서서 한쪽 어깨로 밀어내며 아들이 끼어들었습니다. \"제발 입 좀 다물어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“No, William,”", "\"안 돼, 윌리엄,\""], ["she returned, “I won’t!", "그녀가 대꾸했습니다. \"안 다물 거야!"], ["Not unless he gets out, I won’t!”", "저 사람이 나가지 않는 한, 안 다물 거라고!\""]], [["However, Mr. Guppy and Mr.", "그러나 거피 씨와 조블링 씨는 둘이 함께 (이제 막 험한 말을 퍼붓기 시작한) 거피 씨의 어머니를 에워싸, 그녀의 거센 뜻을 거슬러 가며 아래층으로 데려갔습니다."], ["Jobling together closed on Mr. Guppy’s mother (who began to be quite abusive) and took her, very much against her will, downstairs, her voice rising a stair higher every time her figure got a stair lower, and insisting that we should immediately go and find somebody who was good enough for us, and above all things that we should get out.", "그녀의 몸이 계단을 한 칸씩 내려갈 때마다 목소리는 한 칸씩 높아져 갔고, 우리더러 당장 우리한테 모자라지 않을 사람을 가서 찾으라고, 무엇보다도 우리더러 썩 나가라고 줄곧 우겨 댔습니다."]], [["Beginning the World\nThe term had commenced, and my guardian found an intimation from Mr. Kenge that the cause would come on in two days.", "개정 기간이 시작되었고, 후견인은 켄지 씨로부터 이틀 뒤면 소송이 진행된다는 통지를 받았습니다."], ["As I had sufficient hopes of the will to be in a flutter about it, Allan and I agreed to go down to the court that morning.", "나는 그 유언장에 충분히 큰 희망을 품고 있어 마음이 들떠 있었기에, 앨런과 나는 그날 아침 법정에 가보기로 했습니다."], ["Richard was extremely agitated and was so weak and low, though his illness was still of the mind, that my dear girl indeed had sore occasion to be supported.", "리처드는 몹시 심란해했고, 병이 아직 마음의 병이긴 했어도 어찌나 쇠약하고 기운이 없는지, 내 사랑하는 아이는 정말이지 그를 받쳐줘야 할 절박한 처지에 있었습니다."], ["But she looked forward—a very little way now—to the help that was to come to her, and never drooped.", "그러나 그녀는 이제 머지않아—아주 가까이 다가온—자기에게 찾아올 도움을 내다보며, 결코 시들지 않았습니다."]], [["It was at Westminster that the cause was to come on.", "소송이 진행될 곳은 웨스트민스터였습니다."], ["It had come on there, I dare say, a hundred times before, but I could not divest myself of an idea that it MIGHT lead to some result now.", "장담하건대 그곳에서 이미 백 번도 넘게 진행되었겠지만, 나는 이번에는 어떤 결말로 이어질지도 모른다는 생각을 떨쳐낼 수 없었습니다."], ["We left home directly after breakfast to be at Westminster Hall in good time and walked down there through the lively streets—so happily and strangely it seemed!—together.", "우리는 시간을 넉넉히 잡아 웨스트민스터 홀에 닿으려고 아침 식사를 마치자마자 집을 나섰고, 활기 넘치는 거리를 지나 그곳으로 함께 걸어갔습니다—어찌나 행복하고 묘하게 느껴지던지!"]], [["As we were going along, planning what we should do for Richard and Ada, I heard somebody calling “Esther!", "길을 걸으며 리처드와 에이다를 위해 무엇을 해줄 수 있을지 궁리하던 차에, 누군가 \"에스더!"], ["My dear Esther!", "사랑하는 에스더!"], ["Esther!”", "에스더!\""], ["And there was Caddy Jellyby, with her head out of the window of a little carriage which she hired now to go about in to her pupils (she had so many), as if she wanted to embrace me at a hundred yards’ distance.", "하고 부르는 소리가 들렸습니다. 캐디 젤리비였습니다."], ["I had written her a note to tell her of all that my guardian had done, but had not had a moment to go and see her.", "그녀가(워낙 학생이 많아서) 이제 학생들을 찾아다니느라 빌려 타고 다니는 작은 마차의 창밖으로 고개를 내밀고는, 백 야드나 떨어진 거리에서 나를 와락 끌어안기라도 할 것 같았습니다. 나는 후견인이 해주신 모든 일을 알리려고 그녀에게 짧은 편지를 써 보냈지만, 직접 찾아가 만날 틈이 도무지 없던 터였습니다."], ["Of course we turned back, and the affectionate girl was in that state of rapture, and was so overjoyed to talk about the night when she brought me the flowers, and was so determined to squeeze my face (bonnet and all) between her hands, and go on in a wild manner altogether, calling me all kinds of precious names, and telling Allan I had done I don’t know what for her, that I was just obliged to get into the little carriage and calm her down by letting her say and do exactly what she liked.", "우리는 당연히 발길을 돌렸고, 그 다정한 아이는 황홀경에 빠진 상태였습니다. 나에게 꽃을 가져다주던 그날 밤 이야기를 어찌나 신나서 떠들어대던지, 두 손으로 내 얼굴을(보닛까지 통째로) 감싸 쥐려고 어찌나 작정하던지, 온갖 사랑스러운 이름으로 나를 불러대며 앨런에게는 내가 자기한테 무슨 무슨 일을 해주었다고—나도 모를 일까지—늘어놓으며 그토록 마구잡이로 굴어대는 통에, 나는 그만 그 작은 마차에 올라타 그녀가 하고 싶은 말과 행동을 마음껏 하도록 내버려두며 진정시킬 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["Allan, standing at the window, was as pleased as Caddy; and I was as pleased as either of them; and I wonder that I got away as I did, rather than that I came off laughing, and red, and anything but tidy, and looking after Caddy, who looked after us out of the coach-window as long as she could see us.", "창가에 선 앨런은 캐디만큼이나 기뻐했고, 나는 또 두 사람 못지않게 기뻤습니다. 그러니 내가 웃음을 터뜨리며, 얼굴이 붉어진 채, 차림새는 엉망진창이 되어, 보이는 한 끝까지 마차 창밖으로 우리를 지켜보던 캐디를 돌아보며 나왔다는 게 아니라, 그래도 그 자리를 빠져나왔다는 게 신기할 따름입니다."]], [["This made us some quarter of an hour late, and when we came to Westminster Hall we found that the day’s business was begun.", "이 일로 우리는 십오 분쯤 늦어버렸고, 웨스트민스터 홀에 닿았을 때는 그날의 일정이 이미 시작된 뒤였습니다."], ["Worse than that, we found such an unusual crowd in the Court of Chancery that it was full to the door, and we could neither see nor hear what was passing within.", "더 나쁜 것은, 대법정에 어찌나 유난스러운 인파가 몰려들었는지 문간까지 꽉 들어차서, 안에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보지도 듣지도 못했다는 사실이었습니다. 무언가 우스운 일인 듯했습니다."], ["It appeared to be something droll, for occasionally there was a laugh and a cry of “Silence!”", "이따금 웃음소리가 터지고 \"정숙!\" 하는 외침이 따랐으니까요."], ["It appeared to be something interesting, for every one was pushing and striving to get nearer.", "무언가 흥미로운 일인 듯했습니다. 다들 더 가까이 가려고 떠밀고 안간힘을 썼으니까요."], ["It appeared to be something that made the professional gentlemen very merry, for there were several young counsellors in wigs and whiskers on the outside of the crowd, and when one of them told the others about it, they put their hands in their pockets, and quite doubled themselves up with laughter, and went stamping about the pavement of the Hall.", "무언가 법조계 신사들을 잔뜩 신나게 하는 일인 듯했습니다. 인파 바깥쪽에 가발에 구레나룻을 한 젊은 변호사가 여럿 있었는데, 그중 한 사람이 다른 이들에게 그 이야기를 들려주자 다들 주머니에 손을 찔러 넣고 허리가 꺾이도록 웃어대며 홀 바닥을 쿵쿵 구르며 돌아다녔으니까요."]], [["We asked a gentleman by us if he knew what cause was on.", "우리는 곁에 있던 신사에게 무슨 소송이 진행 중인지 아느냐고 물었습니다."], ["He told us Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "그는 잔다이스 대 잔다이스라고 일러주었습니다."], ["We asked him if he knew what was doing in it.", "우리는 그 소송에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 아느냐고 물었습니다."], ["He said really, no he did not, nobody ever did, but as well as he could make out, it was over.", "그는 정말이지, 아니, 모르겠다고, 아무도 안 적이 없다고 했지만, 자기가 알아낸 바로는 끝났다고 했습니다."], ["Over for the day? we asked him.", "오늘치가 끝난 거냐고 우리는 물었습니다."], ["No, he said, over for good.", "아니, 그가 말했습니다, 아주 끝났다고."]], [["Over for good!", "아주 끝났다니!"]], [["When we heard this unaccountable answer, we looked at one another quite lost in amazement.", "이 영문 모를 대답을 듣고서 우리는 어리둥절함에 사로잡혀 서로를 바라보았습니다."], ["Could it be possible that the will had set things right at last and that Richard and Ada were going to be rich?", "유언장이 마침내 모든 걸 바로잡아 리처드와 에이다가 부자가 될 수 있다는 게 정말 가능한 일일까? 너무 좋아서 도무지 사실 같지 않았습니다."], ["It seemed too good to be true. Alas it was!", "아아, 정말 그랬습니다!"]], [["Our suspense was short, for a break-up soon took place in the crowd, and the people came streaming out looking flushed and hot and bringing a quantity of bad air with them.", "우리의 마음 졸임은 짧게 끝났습니다. 이내 인파가 흩어지더니 사람들이 줄지어 쏟아져 나왔는데, 다들 상기되고 달아오른 얼굴에 탁한 공기까지 잔뜩 몰고 나왔습니다."], ["Still they were all exceedingly amused and were more like people coming out from a farce or a juggler than from a court of justice.", "그래도 하나같이 더없이 즐거워하는 모습이라, 법정이 아니라 어떤 소극이나 요술 구경에서 나오는 사람들 같았습니다."], ["We stood aside, watching for any countenance we knew, and presently great bundles of paper began to be carried out—bundles in bags, bundles too large to be got into any bags, immense masses of papers of all shapes and no shapes, which the bearers staggered under, and threw down for the time being, anyhow, on the Hall pavement, while they went back to bring out more.", "우리는 한쪽으로 비켜서서 아는 얼굴이 없나 살폈는데, 곧 큼직한 서류 뭉치들이 들려 나오기 시작했습니다—가방에 든 뭉치, 어떤 가방에도 들어가지 않을 만큼 큰 뭉치, 갖가지 모양에 아예 형체도 없는 어마어마한 종이 더미였습니다. 운반하는 사람들은 그 무게에 비틀거리며 우선 아무렇게나 홀 바닥에 내던져 놓고는, 더 가지고 나오려고 되돌아갔습니다."], ["Even these clerks were laughing.", "그 서기들조차 웃고 있었습니다."], ["We glanced at the papers, and seeing Jarndyce and Jarndyce everywhere, asked an official-looking person who was standing in the midst of them whether the cause was over.", "우리는 그 서류들을 흘끗 들여다보았고, 어디에나 잔다이스 대 잔다이스라고 적혀 있는 것을 보고는, 그 한복판에 서 있던 관리처럼 보이는 사람에게 소송이 끝났느냐고 물었습니다."], ["Yes, he said, it was all up with it at last, and burst out laughing too.", "그렇다고, 마침내 다 끝장났다고 그가 말하고는, 그 역시 웃음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["At this juncture we perceived Mr. Kenge coming out of court with an affable dignity upon him, listening to Mr.", "바로 그때 우리는 켄지 씨가 점잖고 상냥한 위엄을 두른 채 법정에서 나오는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Vholes, who was deferential and carried his own bag. Mr.", "그는 공손하게 제 가방을 직접 들고 따르는 볼스 씨의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었습니다."], ["Vholes was the first to see us.", "우리를 먼저 알아본 건 볼스 씨였습니다."], ["“Here is Miss Summerson, sir,”", "\"여기 서머슨 양이 있군요, 변호사님.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“And Mr. Woodcourt.”", "\"그리고 우드코트 씨도요.\""]], [["“Oh, indeed!", "\"오, 그렇군!"], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["Truly!”", "정말이지!\""], ["said Mr. Kenge, raising his hat to me with polished politeness.", "켄지 씨가 세련된 정중함으로 나에게 모자를 들어 올리며 말했습니다."], ["“How do you do?", "\"안녕하셨습니까?"], ["Glad to see you.", "만나서 반갑습니다."], ["Mr. Jarndyce is not here?”", "잔다이스 씨는 여기 안 계시오?\""]], [["No.", "네."], ["He never came there, I reminded him.", "그분은 결코 이런 곳에 오시지 않는다고 나는 그에게 일러주었습니다."]], [["“Really,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["returned Mr.", "켄지 씨가 대꾸했습니다. \"오늘 그분이 여기 안 계신 게 차라리 다행이오."], ["Kenge, “it is as well that he is NOT here to-day, for his—shall I say, in my good friend’s absence, his indomitable singularity of opinion?—might have been strengthened, perhaps; not reasonably, but might have been strengthened.”", "그분의—내 좋은 벗이 안 계신 자리이니 이렇게 말해도 될까요, 굽힐 줄 모르는 그 독특한 견해 말이오—어쩌면 더 굳어졌을지도 모를 테니까. 사리에 닿게는 아니지만, 더 굳어졌을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Pray what has been done to-day?”", "\"실례지만 오늘 무슨 일이 처리된 겁니까?\""], ["asked Allan.", "앨런이 물었습니다."]], [["“I beg your pardon?”", "\"뭐라고 하셨소?\""], ["said Mr. Kenge with excessive urbanity.", "켄지 씨가 지나칠 만큼 점잖게 되물었습니다."]], [["“What has been done to-day?”", "\"오늘 무슨 일이 처리됐습니까?\""]], [["“What has been done,”", "\"무슨 일이 처리됐느냐,\""], ["repeated Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 되뇌었습니다."], ["“Quite so.", "\"그렇지."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["Why, not much has been done; not much.", "음, 처리된 건 별로 없소. 별로 없어요."], ["We have been checked—brought up suddenly, I would say—upon the—shall I term it threshold?”", "우리는 제동이 걸렸소—느닷없이 멈춰 섰다고 해야겠군요—그 무엇이라고 할까요, 문턱에서 말이오.\""]], [["“Is this will considered a genuine document, sir?”", "\"이 유언장이 진짜 문서로 인정됩니까, 변호사님?\""], ["said Allan.", "앨런이 물었습니다."], ["“Will you tell us that?”", "\"그것 좀 말씀해 주시겠소?\""]], [["“Most certainly, if I could,”", "\"내가 할 수만 있다면야 더없이 기꺼이,\""], ["said Mr.", "켄지 씨가 말했습니다."], ["Kenge; “but we have not gone into that, we have not gone into that.”", "\"하지만 우리는 거기까지 들어가지 못했소. 거기까지 들어가지 못했어요.\""]], [["“We have not gone into that,”", "\"거기까지 들어가지 못했지요.\""], ["repeated Mr. Vholes as if his low inward voice were an echo.", "볼스 씨가 그 낮고 속에서 울리는 목소리를 메아리처럼 되뇌었습니다."]], [["“You are to reflect, Mr. Woodcourt,”", "\"잘 헤아려 보셔야 하오, 우드코트 씨.\""], ["observed Mr. Kenge, using his silver trowel persuasively and smoothingly, “that this has been a great cause, that this has been a protracted cause, that this has been a complex cause.", "켄지 씨가 그 은빛 흙손 같은 말솜씨를 설득조로 매끄럽게 놀리며 말했습니다."], ["Jarndyce and Jarndyce has been termed, not inaptly, a monument of Chancery practice.”", "\"이것은 위대한 소송이었고, 길게 끌어온 소송이었으며, 복잡한 소송이었소. 잔다이스 대 잔다이스는 대법원 관행의 기념비라 일컬어져 왔는데, 그 표현이 영 틀린 것도 아니지요.\""]], [["“And patience has sat upon it a long time,”", "\"게다가 인내심이 그 위에 오래도록 앉아 있었지요.\""], ["said Allan.", "앨런이 말했습니다."]], [["“Very well indeed, sir,” returned Mr.", "* 인내심: 셰익스피어의 「십이야」에 나오는 \"기념비 위에 앉은 인내처럼\"이라는 구절을 빗댄 말이다."], ["Kenge with a certain condescending laugh he had.", "\"참으로 훌륭하시오, 우드코트 씨.\" 켄지 씨가 특유의 어딘가 거들먹거리는 웃음을 흘리며 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Very well!", "\"참 훌륭해요!"], ["You are further to reflect, Mr. Woodcourt,”", "더 헤아려 보셔야 하오, 우드코트 씨.\""], ["becoming dignified almost to severity, “that on the numerous difficulties, contingencies, masterly fictions, and forms of procedure in this great cause, there has been expended study, ability, eloquence, knowledge, intellect, Mr.", "거의 준엄해 보일 만큼 위엄을 갖추며 말을 이었습니다. \"이 위대한 소송에 얽힌 수많은 난관과 우발 사태와 빼어난 의제(擬制)와 절차 형식에는, 학식과 능력과 웅변과 지식과 지성이, 우드코트 씨, 고매한 지성이 쏟아부어졌소."], ["Woodcourt, high intellect. For many years, the—a—I would say the flower of the bar, and the—a—I would presume to add, the matured autumnal fruits of the woolsack—have been lavished upon Jarndyce and Jarndyce.", "여러 해에 걸쳐, 그—에—말하자면 법조계의 정화(精華)와, 그—에—감히 덧붙이자면, 대법관석의 무르익은 가을 결실이—잔다이스 대 잔다이스에 아낌없이 베풀어졌단 말이오."], ["If the public have the benefit, and if the country have the adornment, of this great grasp, it must be paid for in money or money’s worth, sir.”", "만약 대중이 이 위대한 손길의 혜택을 누리고, 나라가 그 장식을 얻는다면, 그것은 돈으로든 돈에 값하는 것으로든 치러져야 하는 법이오, 우드코트 씨.\""]], [["“Mr. Kenge,”", "\"켄지 씨.\""], ["said Allan, appearing enlightened all in a moment.", "앨런이 순간 모든 것을 깨달은 표정으로 말했습니다."], ["“Excuse me, our time presses.", "\"실례지만 우리는 시간이 촉박합니다."], ["Do I understand that the whole estate is found to have been absorbed in costs?”", "그러니까 재산 전체가 소송 비용으로 다 삼켜진 것으로 드러났다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Hem!", "\"흠!"], ["I believe so,”", "그런 것 같소이다.\""], ["returned Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Mr. Vholes, what do YOU say?”", "\"볼스 씨, 댁은 어찌 생각하오?\""]], [["“I believe so,”", "\"그런 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“And that thus the suit lapses and melts away?”", "\"그래서 소송이 그렇게 효력을 잃고 녹아 없어진다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Probably,”", "\"아마 그렇겠지요.\""], ["returned Mr. Kenge.", "켄지 씨가 대꾸했습니다."], ["“Mr. Vholes?”", "\"볼스 씨?\""]], [["“Probably,”", "\"아마 그렇겠지요.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes.", "볼스 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“My dearest life,”", "\"내 가장 사랑하는 사람.\""], ["whispered Allan, “this will break Richard’s heart!”", "앨런이 속삭였습니다. \"이건 리처드의 가슴을 무너뜨릴 거요!\""]], [["There was such a shock of apprehension in his face, and he knew Richard so perfectly, and I too had seen so much of his gradual decay, that what my dear girl had said to me in the fullness of her foreboding love sounded like a knell in my ears.", "그의 얼굴에는 그런 두려움의 충격이 어려 있었고, 그가 리처드를 워낙 속속들이 알았으며, 나 또한 리처드가 차츰 쇠해가는 모습을 그토록 많이 지켜본 터라, 내 사랑하는 아이가 불길한 사랑의 예감을 가득 담아 나에게 했던 말이 귓가에 조종(弔鐘)처럼 울렸습니다."]], [["“In case you should be wanting Mr. C., sir,”", "\"혹 C씨를 찾으신다면, 변호사님.\""], ["said Mr. Vholes, coming after us, “you’ll find him in court.", "볼스 씨가 우리 뒤를 따라오며 말했습니다."], ["I left him there resting himself a little.", "\"법정 안에 계실 겁니다."], ["Good day, sir; good day, Miss Summerson.”", "좀 쉬게 그곳에 두고 왔어요."], ["As he gave me that slowly devouring look of his, while twisting up the strings of his bag before he hastened with it after Mr.", "안녕히 가십시오, 변호사님. 안녕히 가십시오, 서머슨 양.\""], ["Kenge, the benignant shadow of whose conversational presence he seemed afraid to leave, he gave one gasp as if he had swallowed the last morsel of his client, and his black buttoned-up unwholesome figure glided away to the low door at the end of the Hall.", "그는 가방 끈을 비비 꼬아 매며 켄지 씨를 서둘러 뒤따라가려 했는데—그 상냥한 대화 상대의 그늘을 떠나기가 두려운 듯했습니다—나에게 그 느릿느릿 집어삼키는 듯한 눈길을 던지더니, 마치 의뢰인의 마지막 한 점을 삼켜버린 양 한 번 숨을 헐떡였습니다. 그러고는 단추를 목까지 채운 그 검고 음산한 형체가 홀 끝의 낮은 문 쪽으로 미끄러져 사라졌습니다."]], [["“My dear love,”", "\"내 사랑.\""], ["said Allan, “leave to me, for a little while, the charge you gave me.", "앨런이 말했습니다. \"당신이 나에게 맡긴 임무를 잠시 동안만 내게 맡겨주오."], ["Go home with this intelligence and come to Ada’s by and by!”", "이 소식을 가지고 집으로 갔다가, 이따 에이다네로 와요!\""]], [["I would not let him take me to a coach, but entreated him to go to Richard without a moment’s delay and leave me to do as he wished.", "나는 그가 나를 마차에 태우게 두지 않고, 한순간도 지체 말고 리처드에게 가달라고, 그가 바라는 대로 하게 나를 내버려두라고 간청했습니다."], ["Hurrying home, I found my guardian and told him gradually with what news I had returned.", "서둘러 집으로 와서 후견인을 찾아, 내가 어떤 소식을 안고 돌아왔는지 차근차근 말씀드렸습니다."], ["“Little woman,”", "\"꼬마 아주머니.\""], ["said he, quite unmoved for himself, “to have done with the suit on any terms is a greater blessing than I had looked for.", "그가 자신을 위해서는 조금도 동요하지 않고 말했습니다. \"어떤 조건으로든 그 소송이 끝났다는 건 내가 바랐던 것보다 더 큰 축복이로구나."], ["But my poor young cousins!”", "하지만 내 가엾은 어린 조카들은!\""]], [["We talked about them all the morning and discussed what it was possible to do.", "우리는 오전 내내 그들 이야기를 나누며 무엇을 할 수 있을지 의논했습니다."], ["In the afternoon my guardian walked with me to Symond’s Inn and left me at the door. I went upstairs.", "오후에 후견인은 나와 함께 사이먼드 법학원까지 걸어와 문 앞에서 나를 내려주었습니다."], ["When my darling heard my footsteps, she came out into the small passage and threw her arms round my neck, but she composed herself directly and said that Richard had asked for me several times.", "나는 위층으로 올라갔습니다. 사랑하는 그 아이는 내 발소리를 듣자 좁은 복도로 나와 내 목을 끌어안았지만, 곧 마음을 가다듬고는 리처드가 나를 여러 번 찾았다고 말했습니다."], ["Allan had found him sitting in the corner of the court, she told me, like a stone figure.", "앨런이 법정 한구석에 앉아 있는 그를 발견했는데, 마치 돌로 만든 석상 같았다고 그녀가 일러주었습니다."], ["On being roused, he had broken away and made as if he would have spoken in a fierce voice to the judge.", "깨워서 정신을 차리게 하자 그가 벌떡 일어나 사나운 목소리로 판사에게 무슨 말을 하려는 듯했답니다."], ["He was stopped by his mouth being full of blood, and Allan had brought him home.", "하지만 입안에 피가 가득 차오르는 바람에 멈췄고, 앨런이 그를 집으로 데려왔다고 했습니다."]], [["He was lying on a sofa with his eyes closed when I went in.", "내가 들어갔을 때 그는 눈을 감은 채 소파에 누워 있었습니다."], ["There were restoratives on the table; the room was made as airy as possible, and was darkened, and was very orderly and quiet.", "탁자에는 강심제가 놓여 있었고, 방은 되도록 바람이 잘 통하게 하면서도 어둑하게 해두었으며, 더없이 정돈되고 조용했습니다."], ["Allan stood behind him watching him gravely.", "앨런은 그의 뒤에 서서 무겁게 그를 지켜보고 있었습니다."], ["His face appeared to me to be quite destitute of colour, and now that I saw him without his seeing me, I fully saw, for the first time, how worn away he was.", "그의 얼굴은 내게 핏기라곤 전혀 없어 보였고, 그가 나를 보지 못하는 채로 내가 그를 바라보게 되자, 비로소 처음으로 그가 얼마나 야위었는지 똑똑히 보였습니다."], ["But he looked handsomer than I had seen him look for many a day.", "그래도 그는 오랜만에 보는 그 어느 때보다 잘생겨 보였습니다."]], [["I sat down by his side in silence.", "나는 말없이 그의 곁에 앉았습니다."], ["Opening his eyes by and by, he said in a weak voice, but with his old smile, “Dame Durden, kiss me, my dear!”", "이윽고 눈을 뜬 그가 기운 없는 목소리로, 그러나 예전의 그 미소를 지으며 말했습니다. \"더든 아주머니, 키스해 줘요, 그대여!\""]], [["It was a great comfort and surprise to me to find him in his low state cheerful and looking forward.", "이토록 쇠약한 상태에서도 그가 명랑하게 앞날을 내다보는 모습을 보니, 나에게는 큰 위안이자 놀라움이었습니다."], ["He was happier, he said, in our intended marriage than he could find words to tell me.", "우리가 곧 결혼할 거라는 사실에, 말로는 다 표현할 수 없을 만큼 행복하다고 그가 말했습니다."], ["My husband had been a guardian angel to him and Ada, and he blessed us both and wished us all the joy that life could yield us.", "내 남편은 자기와 에이다에게 수호천사였다며, 우리 둘을 축복하고 인생이 우리에게 베풀 수 있는 모든 기쁨을 빌어주었습니다."], ["I almost felt as if my own heart would have broken when I saw him take my husband’s hand and hold it to his breast.", "그가 내 남편의 손을 잡아 제 가슴에 가져다 대는 모습을 보았을 때, 나는 내 심장마저 터져버릴 것만 같았습니다."]], [["We spoke of the future as much as possible, and he said several times that he must be present at our marriage if he could stand upon his feet.", "우리는 되도록 앞날에 관해 이야기했고, 그는 발로 설 수만 있다면 우리 결혼식에 꼭 참석해야겠다고 여러 번 말했습니다."], ["Ada would contrive to take him, somehow, he said.", "에이다가 어떻게든 자기를 데려갈 방도를 마련해줄 거라고 그가 말했습니다."], ["“Yes, surely, dearest Richard!”", "\"그럼요, 물론이에요, 사랑하는 리처드!\""], ["But as my darling answered him thus hopefully, so serene and beautiful, with the help that was to come to her so near—I knew—I knew!", "그러나 사랑하는 그 아이가 그토록 평온하고 아름답게, 그토록 희망에 차서—이제 자기에게 곧 찾아올 도움을 그토록 가까이 둔 채—그에게 대답하는 동안, 나는 알았습니다—나는 알고 있었습니다!"]], [["It was not good for him to talk too much, and when he was silent, we were silent too.", "그가 너무 많이 말하는 것은 좋지 않았기에, 그가 잠잠해지면 우리도 잠잠해졌습니다."], ["Sitting beside him, I made a pretence of working for my dear, as he had always been used to joke about my being busy.", "그의 곁에 앉아, 나는 사랑하는 사람을 위해 바느질하는 척했습니다. 그가 늘 내가 바쁜 걸 두고 농담을 하곤 했으니까요."], ["Ada leaned upon his pillow, holding his head upon her arm.", "에이다는 그의 머리를 제 팔에 받쳐 안고 베개에 기대어 있었습니다."], ["He dozed often, and whenever he awoke without seeing him, said first of all, “Where is Woodcourt?”", "그는 자주 깜빡 졸았고, 깨어날 때마다 그가 보이지 않으면 무엇보다 먼저 \"우드코트는 어디 있지?\" 하고 물었습니다."]], [["Evening had come on when I lifted up my eyes and saw my guardian standing in the little hall.", "내가 눈을 들어 후견인이 작은 현관에 서 있는 것을 보았을 때는 어느덧 저녁이 찾아온 뒤였습니다."], ["“Who is that, Dame Durden?”", "\"저기 누구죠, 더든 아주머니?\""], ["Richard asked me.", "리처드가 나에게 물었습니다."], ["The door was behind him, but he had observed in my face that some one was there.", "문은 그의 등 뒤에 있었지만, 그는 내 얼굴에서 누군가 와 있다는 것을 알아챘던 것입니다."]], [["I looked to Allan for advice, and as he nodded “Yes,”", "나는 앨런에게 어찌하면 좋을지 눈짓으로 물었고, 그가 \"그래요\""], ["bent over Richard and told him.", "하고 고갯짓하자, 리처드에게 몸을 굽혀 일러주었습니다."], ["My guardian saw what passed, came softly by me in a moment, and laid his hand on Richard’s.", "후견인은 무슨 일인지 알아채고는 곧장 내 곁으로 가만히 다가와 리처드의 손에 자기 손을 얹었습니다."], ["“Oh, sir,”", "\"오, 어르신.\""], ["said Richard, “you are a good man, you are a good man!”", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"어르신은 좋은 분이세요, 좋은 분이에요!\""], ["and burst into tears for the first time.", "그러고는 처음으로 울음을 터뜨렸습니다."]], [["My guardian, the picture of a good man, sat down in my place, keeping his hand on Richard’s.", "좋은 사람의 표상 같은 후견인은 내 자리에 앉아, 리처드의 손을 자기 손으로 계속 잡았습니다."]], [["“My dear Rick,”", "\"릭, 얘야.\""], ["said he, “the clouds have cleared away, and it is bright now.", "그가 말했습니다. \"구름이 걷혔고 이제 환해졌구나."], ["We can see now.", "이제 우리는 볼 수 있어."], ["We were all bewildered, Rick, more or less.", "우리는 다들 어리둥절했지, 릭, 너나 나나 더하고 덜할 뿐."], ["What matters!", "그게 무슨 대수겠니!"], ["And how are you, my dear boy?”", "그래, 좀 어떠냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“I am very weak, sir, but I hope I shall be stronger.", "\"몹시 기운이 없어요, 어르신. 하지만 더 튼튼해지길 바라요."], ["I have to begin the world.”", "저는 이제 세상을 다시 시작해야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Aye, truly; well said!”", "\"그래, 그럼! 옳은 말이다!\""], ["cried my guardian.", "후견인이 외쳤습니다."]], [["“I will not begin it in the old way now,”", "\"이제 옛날 방식으로 시작하지는 않을 거예요.\""], ["said Richard with a sad smile.", "리처드가 서글픈 미소를 지으며 말했습니다."], ["“I have learned a lesson now, sir.", "\"이제 교훈을 하나 얻었어요, 어르신."], ["It was a hard one, but you shall be assured, indeed, that I have learned it.”", "혹독한 교훈이었지만, 제가 정말로 그걸 배웠다는 건 믿으셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"됐다, 됐어.\""], ["said my guardian, comforting him; “well, well, well, dear boy!”", "후견인이 그를 다독이며 말했습니다. \"됐다, 됐어, 됐어, 얘야!\""]], [["“I was thinking, sir,”", "\"제가 생각해 봤는데요, 어르신.\""], ["resumed Richard, “that there is nothing on earth I should so much like to see as their house—Dame Durden’s and Woodcourt’s house.", "리처드가 말을 이었습니다. \"이 세상에서 제가 무엇보다도 보고 싶은 건 그들의 집이에요—더든 아주머니와 우드코트의 집 말이에요."], ["If I could be removed there when I begin to recover my strength, I feel as if I should get well there sooner than anywhere.”", "기운을 차리기 시작할 때 그리로 옮겨갈 수만 있다면, 어디서보다도 거기서 더 빨리 나을 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“Why, so have I been thinking too, Rick,”", "\"실은 나도 그 생각을 하고 있었단다, 릭.\" 후견인이 말했습니다."], ["said my guardian, “and our little woman likewise; she and I have been talking of it this very day.", "\"우리 꼬마 아주머니도 마찬가지고. 그애와 나는 바로 오늘 그 이야기를 했지."], ["I dare say her husband won’t object.", "그애 남편이 반대하지 않을 게다."], ["What do you think?”", "어떠냐?\""]], [["Richard smiled and lifted up his arm to touch him as he stood behind the head of the couch.", "리처드가 미소 지으며, 소파 머리맡에 서 있는 그에게 닿으려고 팔을 들어 올렸습니다."]], [["“I say nothing of Ada,”", "\"에이다 이야기는 하지 않겠어요.\""], ["said Richard, “but I think of her, and have thought of her very much.", "리처드가 말했습니다. \"하지만 저는 그녀를 생각해요, 정말 많이 생각해 왔어요."], ["Look at her!", "저 사람을 보세요!"], ["See her here, sir, bending over this pillow when she has so much need to rest upon it herself, my dear love, my poor girl!”", "여기 좀 보세요, 어르신. 정작 자기야말로 이 베개에 기대 쉬어야 할 처지인데도 이렇게 몸을 굽히고 있잖아요, 내 사랑, 내 가엾은 사람!\""]], [["He clasped her in his arms, and none of us spoke.", "그가 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 우리 가운데 누구도 입을 열지 않았습니다."], ["He gradually released her, and she looked upon us, and looked up to heaven, and moved her lips.", "그는 천천히 그녀를 놓아주었고, 그녀는 우리를 바라보다가 하늘을 올려다보며 입술을 움직였습니다."]], [["“When I get down to Bleak House,”", "\"제가 황폐한 집으로 내려가면요, 어르신.\" 리처드가 말했습니다."], ["said Richard, “I shall have much to tell you, sir, and you will have much to show me.", "\"어르신께 들려드릴 이야기가 많을 거예요. 어르신도 제게 보여주실 게 많으실 테고요."], ["You will go, won’t you?”", "가실 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Undoubtedly, dear Rick.”", "\"틀림없이 가고말고, 릭.\""]], [["“Thank you; like you, like you,”", "\"고맙습니다. 어르신답네요, 어르신다워요.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“But it’s all like you.", "\"하지만 다 어르신다운 일이죠."], ["They have been telling me how you planned it and how you remembered all Esther’s familiar tastes and ways.", "어르신이 어떻게 그 집을 꾸미셨는지, 에스더가 늘 좋아하던 취향과 버릇을 어떻게 다 기억해 두셨는지 사람들이 제게 얘기해 줬어요."], ["It will be like coming to the old Bleak House again.”", "마치 옛날 그 황폐한 집으로 다시 돌아가는 것 같겠어요.\""]], [["“And you will come there too, I hope, Rick.", "\"너도 거기 오면 좋겠구나, 릭."], ["I am a solitary man now, you know, and it will be a charity to come to me.", "알다시피 나는 이제 외로운 사람이니, 나를 찾아오는 건 자선을 베푸는 일이지."], ["A charity to come to me, my love!”", "나를 찾아와 주는 자선 말이다, 얘야!\""], ["he repeated to Ada as he gently passed his hand over her golden hair and put a lock of it to his lips.", "그가 에이다의 황금빛 머리칼을 가만히 쓰다듬으며, 그 한 줌을 입술에 가져다 대고는 그녀에게 다시 말했습니다."], ["(I think he vowed within himself to cherish her if she were left alone.)", "(그녀가 홀로 남겨진다면 그녀를 보살피겠노라고 그는 마음속으로 다짐했던 것 같습니다.)"]], [["“It was a troubled dream?”", "\"그것은 괴로운 꿈이었나요?\""], ["said Richard, clasping both my guardian’s hands eagerly.", "리처드가 후견인의 두 손을 간절히 움켜쥐며 말했습니다."]], [["“Nothing more, Rick; nothing more.”", "\"그뿐이란다, 릭. 그뿐이야.\""]], [["“And you, being a good man, can pass it as such, and forgive and pity the dreamer, and be lenient and encouraging when he wakes?”", "\"그러니 좋은 분이신 어르신은 그것을 한낱 꿈으로 흘려보내고, 그 꿈꾼 자를 용서하고 가엾이 여겨, 그가 깨어날 때 너그럽게 북돋아 주실 수 있겠지요?\""]], [["“Indeed I can.", "\"그럼, 할 수 있고말고."], ["What am I but another dreamer, Rick?”", "나라고 또 다른 꿈꾸는 자가 아니고 무엇이겠니, 릭?\""]], [["“I will begin the world!”", "\"저는 세상을 다시 시작할 거예요!\""], ["said Richard with a light in his eyes.", "리처드가 눈에 빛을 띠며 말했습니다."]], [["My husband drew a little nearer towards Ada, and I saw him solemnly lift up his hand to warn my guardian.", "내 남편이 에이다 쪽으로 조금 더 다가섰고, 나는 그가 후견인에게 경계하라는 듯 엄숙하게 손을 들어 올리는 것을 보았습니다."]], [["“When shall I go from this place to that pleasant country where the old times are, where I shall have strength to tell what Ada has been to me, where I shall be able to recall my many faults and blindnesses, where I shall prepare myself to be a guide to my unborn child?”", "\"제가 언제쯤 이곳을 떠나 그 정겨운 고장으로, 옛 시절이 있는 그곳으로 갈 수 있을까요? 에이다가 제게 어떤 사람이었는지 말할 기운을 얻을 그곳으로, 제 숱한 잘못과 어리석음을 떠올릴 수 있을 그곳으로, 아직 태어나지 않은 제 아이를 이끌 사람이 되도록 저를 가다듬을 그곳으로요.\""], ["said Richard.", "리처드가 말했습니다."], ["“When shall I go?”", "\"언제쯤 갈 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Dear Rick, when you are strong enough,”", "\"릭, 얘야, 네가 충분히 튼튼해지면 가는 거란다.\""], ["returned my guardian.", "후견인이 대답했습니다."]], [["“Ada, my darling!”", "\"에이다, 내 사랑!\""]], [["He sought to raise himself a little.", "그가 몸을 조금 일으키려 했습니다."], ["Allan raised him so that she could hold him on her bosom, which was what he wanted.", "앨런이 그를 일으켜 그녀의 품에 안기게 해주었습니다. 그것이 그가 바라던 바였으니까요."]], [["“I have done you many wrongs, my own.", "\"내 사랑, 나는 당신에게 못할 짓을 많이 했소."], ["I have fallen like a poor stray shadow on your way, I have married you to poverty and trouble, I have scattered your means to the winds.", "나는 당신이 가는 길에 가엾은 떠돌이 그림자처럼 드리웠고, 당신을 가난과 고생에 시집보냈으며, 당신의 재산을 바람결에 흩어버렸소."], ["You will forgive me all this, my Ada, before I begin the world?”", "내가 세상을 다시 시작하기 전에, 이 모든 걸 용서해 주겠소, 나의 에이다?\""]], [["A smile irradiated his face as she bent to kiss him.", "그녀가 몸을 굽혀 그에게 입 맞추자 그의 얼굴에 미소가 환히 번졌습니다."], ["He slowly laid his face down upon her bosom, drew his arms closer round her neck, and with one parting sob began the world.", "그는 천천히 얼굴을 그녀의 품에 묻고, 두 팔로 그녀의 목을 더 가까이 끌어안고는, 한 번 작별의 흐느낌과 함께 세상을 시작했습니다."], ["Not this world, oh, not this!", "이 세상이 아니라, 오, 이 세상이 아니라!"], ["The world that sets this right.", "이 세상을 바로잡는 그 세상을."]], [["When all was still, at a late hour, poor crazed Miss Flite came weeping to me and told me she had given her birds their liberty.", "깊은 밤, 모든 것이 고요해졌을 때, 가엾이 정신이 나간 플라이트 양이 울며 나에게 다가와, 자기 새들을 모두 풀어주었다고 일러주었습니다."]], [["Down in Lincolnshire\nThere is a hush upon Chesney Wold in these altered days, as there is upon a portion of the family history.", "링컨셔의 시골에는 이 달라진 나날에 정적이 감돈다. 가문의 내력 한 자락에 정적이 감돌듯이."], ["The story goes that Sir Leicester paid some who could have spoken out to hold their peace; but it is a lame story, feebly whispering and creeping about, and any brighter spark of life it shows soon dies away.", "떠도는 이야기로는 레스터 경이 입을 열 만한 자들에게 돈을 쥐여 침묵을 사들였다고 하지만, 이건 어딘가 절뚝거리는 이야기로, 힘없이 속닥거리며 기어 다닐 뿐, 그나마 좀 더 또렷이 빛나는 생명의 불씨를 보일라치면 이내 사그라든다. 아름다운 데드록 마님이 공원의 영묘에 잠들어 있다는 것만은 분명하다."], ["It is known for certain that the handsome Lady Dedlock lies in the mausoleum in the park, where the trees arch darkly overhead, and the owl is heard at night making the woods ring; but whence she was brought home to be laid among the echoes of that solitary place, or how she died, is all mystery.", "그곳은 나무들이 머리 위로 어둑하게 가지를 드리우고, 밤이면 올빼미 소리가 숲을 울리는 곳이다. 그러나 그녀가 어디서 옮겨져 이 외딴 자리의 메아리 사이에 눕게 되었는지, 또 어떻게 죽었는지는 온통 수수께끼다."], ["Some of her old friends, principally to be found among the peachy-cheeked charmers with the skeleton throats, did once occasionally say, as they toyed in a ghastly manner with large fans—like charmers reduced to flirting with grim death, after losing all their other beaux—did once occasionally say, when the world assembled together, that they wondered the ashes of the Dedlocks, entombed in the mausoleum, never rose against the profanation of her company.", "그녀의 옛 벗 가운데 몇몇은—주로 해골 같은 목에 복숭앗빛 뺨을 한 요염한 부인들 가운데서 찾아볼 만한 이들로, 다른 사내들을 죄다 잃은 뒤 음산한 죽음을 상대로 추파를 던지는 요부 같은 꼴로 커다란 부채를 으스스하게 만지작거리며—세상 사람들이 한데 모일 때면 이따금 이런 말을 흘리곤 했다. 영묘에 묻힌 데드록 가문의 유골이 그녀가 끼어든 그 모독에 들고일어나지 않는 게 이상하다고."], ["But the dead-and-gone Dedlocks take it very calmly and have never been known to object.", "그러나 죽어 사라진 데드록 가문 사람들은 그것을 아주 담담히 받아들이고, 항의했다는 소문은 한 번도 들린 적이 없다."]], [["Up from among the fern in the hollow, and winding by the bridle-road among the trees, comes sometimes to this lonely spot the sound of horses’ hoofs.", "움푹 팬 땅의 고사리 덤불 사이에서 올라와 나무들 사이로 난 승마길을 굽이쳐, 이따금 이 적막한 곳으로 말발굽 소리가 다가온다."], ["Then may be seen Sir Leicester—invalided, bent, and almost blind, but of worthy presence yet—riding with a stalwart man beside him, constant to his bridle-rein.", "그러면 레스터 경의 모습이 보인다—병들어 등이 굽고 거의 앞을 못 보지만 여전히 위엄을 갖춘 자태로, 한결같이 고삐 곁을 지키는 건장한 사내를 옆에 거느린 채 말을 몬다."], ["When they come to a certain spot before the mausoleum-door, Sir Leicester’s accustomed horse stops of his own accord, and Sir Leicester, pulling off his hat, is still for a few moments before they ride away.", "영묘 문 앞 어느 자리에 이르면 레스터 경의 길든 말이 스스로 걸음을 멈추고, 레스터 경은 모자를 벗어 든 채 잠시 가만히 서 있다가 다시 말을 몰아 떠난다."]], [["War rages yet with the audacious Boythorn, though at uncertain intervals, and now hotly, and now coolly, flickering like an unsteady fire.", "대담한 보이손과의 전쟁은 아직도 한창인데, 다만 들쭉날쭉한 간격을 두고 때로는 뜨겁게, 때로는 차갑게, 흔들리는 불꽃처럼 깜박인다. 진상은 이렇다고 한다."], ["The truth is said to be that when Sir Leicester came down to Lincolnshire for good, Mr.", "레스터 경이 아주 링컨셔로 내려오자, 보이손 씨가 통행권을 포기하고 레스터 경이 바라는 대로 무엇이든 하려는 기색을 뚜렷이 내비쳤다는 것이다."], ["Boythorn showed a manifest desire to abandon his right of way and do whatever Sir Leicester would, which Sir Leicester, conceiving to be a condescension to his illness or misfortune, took in such high dudgeon, and was so magnificently aggrieved by, that Mr.", "그런데 레스터 경은 이것을 자신의 병이나 불운에 대한 동정쯤으로 여겨 어찌나 발끈하고 어찌나 거창하게 모욕감을 느꼈던지, 보이손 씨는 이웃을 본래 모습으로 되돌리려면 노골적인 침입 행위를 저지를 수밖에 없는 처지에 놓이고 말았다."], ["Boythorn found himself under the necessity of committing a flagrant trespass to restore his neighbour to himself. Similarly, Mr.", "그리하여 보이손 씨는 여전히 분쟁의 통행로에 어마어마한 경고문을 내붙이고, (머리 위에 새를 올린 채) 제 집 안 성역에서 레스터 경을 격렬하게 성토한다."], ["Boythorn continues to post tremendous placards on the disputed thoroughfare and (with his bird upon his head) to hold forth vehemently against Sir Leicester in the sanctuary of his own home; similarly, also, he defies him as of old in the little church by testifying a bland unconsciousness of his existence.", "또한 여전히 작은 교회에서는 옛날처럼 그의 존재를 천연덕스럽게 모르는 척하며 그에게 맞선다."], ["But it is whispered that when he is most ferocious towards his old foe, he is really most considerate, and that Sir Leicester, in the dignity of being implacable, little supposes how much he is humoured.", "그러나 수군대는 말로는, 그가 옛 원수에게 가장 사납게 굴 때가 사실은 가장 깊이 배려하는 때이며, 레스터 경은 끝까지 화해하지 않는 위엄에 젖어 자신이 얼마나 너그러운 대접을 받고 있는지 거의 짐작도 못 한다고 한다."], ["As little does he think how near together he and his antagonist have suffered in the fortunes of two sisters, and his antagonist, who knows it now, is not the man to tell him.", "그가 자신의 적수와 두 자매의 운명 속에서 얼마나 가까이 함께 아파했는지도 거의 모른다. 이제 그 사실을 아는 적수는 그에게 그것을 일러줄 위인이 못 된다."], ["So the quarrel goes on to the satisfaction of both.", "그렇게 다툼은 양쪽 모두 만족스럽게 이어진다."]], [["In one of the lodges of the park—that lodge within sight of the house where, once upon a time, when the waters were out down in Lincolnshire, my Lady used to see the keeper’s child—the stalwart man, the trooper formerly, is housed.", "공원의 문지기 오두막 가운데 하나—바로 그 옛날 링컨셔에 강물이 넘쳤을 때 마님이 문지기의 아이를 바라보곤 하던, 저택이 보이는 그 오두막에—건장한 사내, 옛날의 기병이 살고 있다."], ["Some relics of his old calling hang upon the walls, and these it is the chosen recreation of a little lame man about the stable-yard to keep gleaming bright.", "그의 지난 직업의 유물 몇 가지가 벽에 걸려 있는데, 마구간 마당을 돌아다니는 절름발이 작은 사내가 그것들을 번쩍번쩍 닦아 두는 일을 더없는 낙으로 삼는다."], ["A busy little man he always is, in the polishing at harness-house doors, of stirrup-irons, bits, curb-chains, harness bosses, anything in the way of a stable-yard that will take a polish, leading a life of friction.", "그는 늘 부지런한 작은 사내로, 마구간 문간에서 등자쇠며 재갈이며 굴레사슬이며 마구 장식이며, 마구간 마당에서 닦이는 것이라면 무엇이든 윤을 내며 문지르는 삶을 산다."], ["A shaggy little damaged man, withal, not unlike an old dog of some mongrel breed, who has been considerably knocked about.", "게다가 텁수룩하고 어딘가 망가진 작은 사내로, 어지간히 두들겨 맞은 잡종 늙은 개와 영락없이 닮았다."], ["He answers to the name of Phil.", "그는 필이라는 이름에 대답한다."]], [["A goodly sight it is to see the grand old housekeeper (harder of hearing now) going to church on the arm of her son and to observe—which few do, for the house is scant of company in these times—the relations of both towards Sir Leicester, and his towards them.", "위풍당당한 늙은 가정부가(이제는 귀가 더 어두워졌다) 아들의 팔에 기대 교회에 가는 모습을 보는 것은 흐뭇한 광경이며, 또—요즘은 저택에 찾는 손님이 드물어 이를 지켜보는 이가 별로 없지만—두 사람이 레스터 경을 대하는 태도와 레스터 경이 그들을 대하는 태도를 살피는 것도 그러하다."], ["They have visitors in the high summer weather, when a grey cloak and umbrella, unknown to Chesney Wold at other periods, are seen among the leaves; when two young ladies are occasionally found gambolling in sequestered saw-pits and such nooks of the park; and when the smoke of two pipes wreathes away into the fragrant evening air from the trooper’s door.", "한여름이면 손님들이 찾아오는데, 다른 철에는 체스니 월드에 낯선 잿빛 외투와 우산이 나뭇잎 사이로 보이고, 두 젊은 아가씨가 외딴 톱질 구덩이며 공원의 그런 구석진 곳에서 뛰노는 모습이 이따금 눈에 띄며, 기병의 문간에서 파이프 두 대의 연기가 향긋한 저녁 공기 속으로 굽이굽이 피어오른다."], ["Then is a fife heard trolling within the lodge on the inspiring topic of the “British Grenadiers”; and as the evening closes in, a gruff inflexible voice is heard to say, while two men pace together up and down, “But I never own to it before the old girl.", "그럴 때면 오두막 안에서 \"영국 척탄병\"이라는 신명 나는 가락을 노래하는 피리 소리가 들리고, 저녁이 짙어지면 두 사내가 함께 이리저리 거니는 동안 무뚝뚝하고 완고한 목소리로 이렇게 말하는 게 들린다. \"하지만 난 절대 할멈 앞에선 인정 안 해."], ["Discipline must be maintained.”", "규율은 지켜야 하는 법이니까.\""]], [["The greater part of the house is shut up, and it is a show-house no longer; yet Sir Leicester holds his shrunken state in the long drawing-room for all that, and reposes in his old place before my Lady’s picture.", "저택의 대부분은 닫혀 있고, 이제는 더 이상 구경거리 집이 아니다. 그래도 레스터 경은 긴 응접실에서 쪼그라든 위세나마 지키며, 마님의 초상화 앞 옛 자리에서 쉰다."], ["Closed in by night with broad screens, and illumined only in that part, the light of the drawing-room seems gradually contracting and dwindling until it shall be no more.", "밤이면 넓은 가리개로 둘러막고 오직 그 한 부분만 밝혀 두니, 응접실의 불빛은 점점 오그라들고 줄어들다가 마침내 사라져 버릴 듯 보인다."], ["A little more, in truth, and it will be all extinguished for Sir Leicester; and the damp door in the mausoleum which shuts so tight, and looks so obdurate, will have opened and received him.", "사실 조금만 더 지나면 레스터 경에게도 그 불빛은 모두 꺼질 것이고, 그토록 빈틈없이 닫혀 그토록 완고해 보이는 영묘의 축축한 문이 열려 그를 맞아들일 것이다."]], [["Volumnia, growing with the flight of time pinker as to the red in her face, and yellower as to the white, reads to Sir Leicester in the long evenings and is driven to various artifices to conceal her yawns, of which the chief and most efficacious is the insertion of the pearl necklace between her rosy lips.", "세월이 흐를수록 얼굴의 붉은 자리는 더 분홍빛으로, 흰 자리는 더 누렇게 물들어 가는 볼럼니아는 긴긴 저녁이면 레스터 경에게 책을 읽어 주며, 하품을 감추려 갖은 잔꾀를 부리는데, 그 가운데 으뜸가는 데다 가장 잘 듣는 수법이 진주 목걸이를 발그레한 입술 사이에 물어 두는 것이다."], ["Long-winded treatises on the Buffy and Boodle question, showing how Buffy is immaculate and Boodle villainous, and how the country is lost by being all Boodle and no Buffy, or saved by being all Buffy and no Boodle (it must be one of the two, and cannot be anything else), are the staple of her reading.", "버피 대 부들 문제를 다룬 장황한 논설들—버피가 얼마나 흠 하나 없이 깨끗하고 부들이 얼마나 악당인지, 나라가 죄다 부들이고 버피가 없어 어떻게 망하는지, 아니면 죄다 버피고 부들이 없어 어떻게 구원받는지(둘 중 하나여야 하며 그 밖에 다른 길은 있을 수 없다)를 늘어놓는 글들—이 그녀가 읽는 글의 주된 거리다."], ["Sir Leicester is not particular what it is and does not appear to follow it very closely, further than that he always comes broad awake the moment Volumnia ventures to leave off, and sonorously repeating her last words, begs with some displeasure to know if she finds herself fatigued.", "레스터 경은 그것이 무슨 내용인지 따지지 않고, 그다지 새겨듣는 기색도 없다. 다만 볼럼니아가 슬쩍 읽기를 멈추려 들면 그 순간 번쩍 정신을 차려, 그녀가 마지막으로 읽은 말을 우렁우렁 되뇌고는 다소 못마땅한 투로 혹시 피곤하냐고 묻는다는 점만은 예외다."], ["However, Volumnia, in the course of her bird-like hopping about and pecking at papers, has alighted on a memorandum concerning herself in the event of “anything happening”", "그래도 볼럼니아는 새처럼 폴짝폴짝 뛰어다니며 서류들을 쪼아 대던 끝에, 친척에게 \"무슨 일이 생길\""], ["to her kinsman, which is handsome compensation for an extensive course of reading and holds even the dragon Boredom at bay.", "경우 자신에 관해 적어 둔 비망록 한 장에 내려앉았으니, 이것이야말로 방대한 독서에 대한 후한 보상이요, 권태라는 용마저 가까이 못 오게 막아 준다."]], [["The cousins generally are rather shy of Chesney Wold in its dullness, but take to it a little in the shooting season, when guns are heard in the plantations, and a few scattered beaters and keepers wait at the old places of appointment for low-spirited twos and threes of cousins.", "친척들은 대체로 따분한 체스니 월드를 꺼리지만, 사냥철에는 조금 발길을 들인다. 그때면 농장에 총소리가 들리고, 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 몰이꾼들과 사냥터지기들이 옛 약속 장소에서 풀 죽은 친척들이 두셋씩 오기를 기다린다."], ["The debilitated cousin, more debilitated by the dreariness of the place, gets into a fearful state of depression, groaning under penitential sofa-pillows in his gunless hours and protesting that such fernal old jail’s—nough t’sew fler up—frever.", "그 쇠약한 친척은 이곳의 음울함에 더욱 쇠약해져, 총을 들지 못하는 시간이면 참회하듯 소파 쿠션에 묻혀 끙끙 앓으며, 이런 빌어먹을 늙은 감옥은—사람을—꿰매—버려야—영영—그래야 한다고 하소연한다."]], [["The only great occasions for Volumnia in this changed aspect of the place in Lincolnshire are those occasions, rare and widely separated, when something is to be done for the county or the country in the way of gracing a public ball.", "링컨셔의 이렇게 달라진 풍경 속에서 볼럼니아에게 유일하게 큰 행사라면, 드물고 멀찍이 떨어진 채 가끔 찾아오는 그 순간들, 곧 공개 무도회를 빛내는 일로 주(州)나 나라를 위해 무언가 해야 할 때다."], ["Then, indeed, does the tuckered sylph come out in fairy form and proceed with joy under cousinly escort to the exhausted old assembly-room, fourteen heavy miles off, which, during three hundred and sixty-four days and nights of every ordinary year, is a kind of antipodean lumber-room full of old chairs and tables upside down.", "그럴 때면 정말이지 주름 장식을 두른 요정 같은 그녀가 선녀 같은 자태로 나타나, 친척의 호위를 받으며 기쁘게 열네 마일이나 떨어진 낡고 지친 무도회장으로 향한다. 그곳은 평범한 한 해의 삼백예순나흘 밤낮 동안은 거꾸로 뒤집힌 낡은 의자와 탁자로 가득한, 지구 반대편의 헛간 같은 곳이다."], ["Then, indeed, does she captivate all hearts by her condescension, by her girlish vivacity, and by her skipping about as in the days when the hideous old general with the mouth too full of teeth had not cut one of them at two guineas each.", "그럴 때면 정말이지 그녀는 겸손한 몸가짐과 소녀 같은 활기로, 그리고 이빨이 너무 빽빽이 들어찬 흉측한 늙은 장군이 한 개에 이 기니씩 받고 그 이빨들을 아직 한 개도 뽑지 않았던 그 시절처럼 폴짝폴짝 뛰노는 모습으로 뭇사람의 마음을 사로잡는다."], ["Then does she twirl and twine, a pastoral nymph of good family, through the mazes of the dance.", "그럴 때면 그녀는 좋은 가문 출신의 목가적 요정처럼 춤의 미궁 속을 빙글빙글 돌고 휘감는다."], ["Then do the swains appear with tea, with lemonade, with sandwiches, with homage.", "그럴 때면 시골 총각들이 차를, 레모네이드를, 샌드위치를, 경의를 바치며 나타난다."], ["Then is she kind and cruel, stately and unassuming, various, beautifully wilful.", "그럴 때면 그녀는 다정하다가도 매정하고, 도도하다가도 수수하며, 변화무쌍하고, 곱게 제멋대로다."], ["Then is there a singular kind of parallel between her and the little glass chandeliers of another age embellishing that assembly-room, which, with their meagre stems, their spare little drops, their disappointing knobs where no drops are, their bare little stalks from which knobs and drops have both departed, and their little feeble prismatic twinkling, all seem Volumnias.", "그럴 때면 그녀와, 그 무도회장을 장식하는 옛 시대의 작은 유리 샹들리에들 사이에는 묘한 닮음이 생긴다. 그것들은 빈약한 줄기와, 듬성듬성한 작은 유리 방울들과, 방울이 매달려야 할 자리에 방울 없이 실망스럽게 박힌 마디들과, 마디도 방울도 죄다 떨어져 나간 앙상한 작은 가지들과, 가냘프게 프리즘처럼 반짝이는 작은 빛들로 하여, 하나같이 볼럼니아를 닮은 듯하다."]], [["For the rest, Lincolnshire life to Volumnia is a vast blank of overgrown house looking out upon trees, sighing, wringing their hands, bowing their heads, and casting their tears upon the window-panes in monotonous depressions.", "그 밖에 볼럼니아에게 링컨셔의 삶이란, 나무들을 내다보는 우거진 저택의 광막한 공백이다. 그 나무들은 한숨짓고, 두 손을 비틀고, 고개를 떨구며, 단조로운 우울 속에 창유리에 눈물을 흩뿌린다."], ["A labyrinth of grandeur, less the property of an old family of human beings and their ghostly likenesses than of an old family of echoings and thunderings which start out of their hundred graves at every sound and go resounding through the building.", "웅장함의 미궁이지만, 이는 인간으로 이루어진 어느 옛 가문과 그 유령 같은 닮은꼴들의 것이라기보다, 메아리와 우렛소리로 이루어진 어느 옛 가문의 것이니, 그것들은 어떤 소리든 들릴 때마다 백 개의 무덤에서 뛰쳐나와 온 건물을 울리며 메아리쳐 간다."], ["A waste of unused passages and staircases in which to drop a comb upon a bedroom floor at night is to send a stealthy footfall on an errand through the house.", "쓰이지 않는 복도와 계단의 황량한 벌판, 그곳에서 밤중에 침실 바닥에 빗 하나만 떨어뜨려도 은밀한 발소리가 저택을 가로질러 심부름을 떠나는 셈이 된다."], ["A place where few people care to go about alone, where a maid screams if an ash drops from the fire, takes to crying at all times and seasons, becomes the victim of a low disorder of the spirits, and gives warning and departs.", "사람들이 혼자 돌아다니기를 꺼리는 곳, 하녀가 불에서 재 한 점만 떨어져도 비명을 지르고, 때와 철을 가리지 않고 울음을 터뜨리며, 천한 신경 쇠약의 희생양이 되어 일을 그만두겠다 알리고 떠나 버리는 곳."]], [["Thus Chesney Wold.", "이것이 체스니 월드다."], ["With so much of itself abandoned to darkness and vacancy; with so little change under the summer shining or the wintry lowering; so sombre and motionless always—no flag flying now by day, no rows of lights sparkling by night; with no family to come and go, no visitors to be the souls of pale cold shapes of rooms, no stir of life about it—passion and pride, even to the stranger’s eye, have died away from the place in Lincolnshire and yielded it to dull repose.", "제 몸의 그토록 많은 부분이 어둠과 공허에 내맡겨진 채, 여름 햇살 아래서나 겨울 잿빛 하늘 아래서나 거의 달라지는 것 없이, 늘 그토록 음침하고 미동조차 없다—이제는 낮에 펄럭이는 깃발도 없고, 밤에 줄지어 반짝이는 불빛도 없으며, 오가는 가족도 없고, 차고 창백한 방의 형상에 혼을 불어넣을 손님도 없으며, 둘레에 생명의 기척이라곤 없다."], ["The Close of Esther’s Narrative", "격정도 자긍심도, 낯선 이의 눈에까지도, 이 링컨셔의 시골에서는 사그라지고, 그곳을 흐릿한 안식에 내어 주고 말았다."]], [["Full seven happy years I have been the mistress of Bleak House.", "꼬박 칠 년이라는 행복한 세월 동안 나는 황폐한 집의 안주인으로 지냈습니다."], ["The few words that I have to add to what I have written are soon penned; then I and the unknown friend to whom I write will part for ever.", "지금까지 적은 것에 덧붙일 몇 마디는 금세 끝납니다. 그러고 나면 나와, 내가 이 글을 띄우는 알지 못하는 벗은 영영 헤어지겠지요."], ["Not without much dear remembrance on my side.", "내 쪽에서는 정겨운 추억을 잔뜩 품은 채로 말입니다."], ["Not without some, I hope, on his or hers.", "그리고 그분 쪽에서도 얼마간은, 부디 그러하기를 바랍니다."]], [["They gave my darling into my arms, and through many weeks I never left her.", "사람들이 내 사랑하는 친구를 내 품에 안겨주었고, 나는 여러 주 동안 그녀 곁을 떠나지 않았습니다."], ["The little child who was to have done so much was born before the turf was planted on its father’s grave.", "그토록 많은 일을 해내야 했던 그 어린아이는 제 아버지 무덤에 잔디가 깔리기도 전에 태어났습니다. 사내아이였습니다."], ["It was a boy; and I, my husband, and my guardian gave him his father’s name.", "나와 내 남편과 후견인은 그 아이에게 제 아버지의 이름을 붙여주었습니다."]], [["The help that my dear counted on did come to her, though it came, in the eternal wisdom, for another purpose.", "내 사랑하는 친구가 믿고 기대했던 그 도움은 정말로 그녀에게 찾아왔습니다. 다만 그것은 영원하신 섭리 안에서 다른 목적을 위해 온 것이었습니다."], ["Though to bless and restore his mother, not his father, was the errand of this baby, its power was mighty to do it.", "이 아기에게 맡겨진 일은 제 아버지가 아니라 제 어머니를 축복하고 다시 일으키는 것이었으나, 그 일을 해내는 힘만큼은 대단했습니다."], ["When I saw the strength of the weak little hand and how its touch could heal my darling’s heart and raised hope within her, I felt a new sense of the goodness and the tenderness of God.", "그 가냘프고 작은 손의 힘을, 그리고 그 손길이 어떻게 내 사랑하는 친구의 마음을 어루만져 낫게 하고 그 안에 희망을 일으키는지를 보았을 때, 나는 하느님의 선하심과 자애로우심을 새삼 새롭게 느꼈습니다."]], [["They throve, and by degrees I saw my dear girl pass into my country garden and walk there with her infant in her arms.", "두 사람은 무럭무럭 자랐고, 나는 이윽고 사랑하는 그 아이가 내 시골 정원으로 들어와 아기를 품에 안고 그곳을 거니는 모습을 보게 되었습니다."], ["I was married then.", "그 무렵 나는 이미 결혼한 몸이었습니다."], ["I was the happiest of the happy.", "행복한 사람들 가운데서도 가장 행복했습니다."]], [["It was at this time that my guardian joined us and asked Ada when she would come home.", "바로 이 무렵 후견인이 우리에게 와서 에이다에게 언제 집으로 돌아올 거냐고 물었습니다."]], [["“Both houses are your home, my dear,”", "\"두 집 다 네 집이란다, 얘야.\""], ["said he, “but the older Bleak House claims priority.", "후견인이 말했습니다. \"그래도 더 오래된 황폐한 집이 우선이지."], ["When you and my boy are strong enough to do it, come and take possession of your home.”", "너와 내 아이가 옮길 만큼 충분히 튼튼해지거든 와서 네 집을 차지하려무나.\""]], [["Ada called him “her dearest cousin, John.”", "에이다는 그를 \"더없이 사랑하는 사촌 오라버니 존\"이라고 불렀습니다."], ["But he said, no, it must be guardian now.", "하지만 그는 안 된다고, 이제는 후견인이어야 한다고 했습니다."], ["He was her guardian henceforth, and the boy’s; and he had an old association with the name.", "이제부터 그는 에이다의 후견인이요 그 아이의 후견인이며, 그 이름과는 오래 묵은 인연이 있다는 것이었습니다."], ["So she called him guardian, and has called him guardian ever since.", "그래서 에이다는 그를 후견인이라고 불렀고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 후견인이라고 부릅니다."], ["The children know him by no other name.", "아이들은 그를 다른 이름으로는 알지 못합니다."], ["I say the children; I have two little daughters.", "아이들이라고 했지요. 나에게는 어린 딸이 둘 있습니다."]], [["It is difficult to believe that Charley (round-eyed still, and not at all grammatical) is married to a miller in our neighbourhood; yet so it is; and even now, looking up from my desk as I write early in the morning at my summer window, I see the very mill beginning to go round.", "찰리가(여전히 눈이 동그랗고 문법은 도무지 맞지 않는 그 찰리가) 우리 이웃의 방앗간 주인과 결혼했다니 도무지 믿기지 않습니다. 그래도 사실이 그렇습니다."], ["I hope the miller will not spoil Charley; but he is very fond of her, and Charley is rather vain of such a match, for he is well to do and was in great request.", "지금도 이렇게 이른 아침 여름 창가의 책상에서 글을 쓰다가 고개를 들면, 바로 그 방앗간이 돌기 시작하는 게 보입니다. 그 방앗간 주인이 찰리의 버릇을 버려놓지나 않으면 좋으련만, 그는 찰리를 무척 아낍니다."], ["So far as my small maid is concerned, I might suppose time to have stood for seven years as still as the mill did half an hour ago, since little Emma, Charley’s sister, is exactly what Charley used to be.", "찰리는 이런 혼처에 좀 우쭐대기도 하는데, 그가 형편이 넉넉하고 청혼이 줄을 섰던 사람이기 때문입니다. 내 어린 하녀로 말하자면, 마치 시간이 칠 년 동안 반 시간 전 멈춰 있던 방앗간처럼 가만히 멈춰 있었던 게 아닌가 싶을 정도입니다."], ["As to Tom, Charley’s brother, I am really afraid to say what he did at school in ciphering, but I think it was decimals.", "찰리의 동생 어린 에마가 예전의 찰리와 어쩜 그리도 똑같은지요. 찰리의 남동생 톰으로 말하자면, 그 애가 학교에서 셈을 어떻게 했는지는 차마 입에 올리기 두렵지만, 아마 소수였던 것 같습니다."], ["He is apprenticed to the miller, whatever it was, and is a good bashful fellow, always falling in love with somebody and being ashamed of it.", "어쨌든 그 애는 그 방앗간 주인에게 도제로 들어갔는데, 착하고 수줍음 많은 녀석이라 늘 누군가에게 반하고는 그걸 부끄러워합니다."]], [["Caddy Jellyby passed her very last holidays with us and was a dearer creature than ever, perpetually dancing in and out of the house with the children as if she had never given a dancing-lesson in her life.", "캐디 젤리비는 가장 마지막 휴가를 우리와 함께 보냈는데, 어느 때보다 더 사랑스러운 사람이 되어 있었습니다. 마치 평생 무용 강습 한 번 해본 적 없다는 듯, 아이들과 어울려 끊임없이 집 안팎을 춤추며 드나들었습니다."], ["Caddy keeps her own little carriage now instead of hiring one, and lives full two miles further westward than Newman Street.", "캐디는 이제 마차를 빌리는 대신 자기 작은 마차를 두고 살며, 뉴먼 거리보다 서쪽으로 꼬박 이 마일이나 더 떨어진 곳에 삽니다."], ["She works very hard, her husband (an excellent one) being lame and able to do very little.", "캐디는 정말 열심히 일합니다. 남편이(더없이 훌륭한 사람이지요) 다리를 절어 거의 아무것도 하지 못하기 때문입니다."], ["Still, she is more than contented and does all she has to do with all her heart. Mr.", "그래도 캐디는 더없이 만족스러워하며 자기가 해야 할 일을 온 마음을 다해 합니다."], ["Jellyby spends his evenings at her new house with his head against the wall as he used to do in her old one.", "젤리비 씨는 예전에 딸의 옛집에서 그랬던 것처럼, 새집에서도 벽에 머리를 기댄 채 저녁을 보냅니다."], ["I have heard that Mrs. Jellyby was understood to suffer great mortification from her daughter’s ignoble marriage and pursuits, but I hope she got over it in time.", "듣자 하니 젤리비 부인은 딸의 변변찮은 결혼과 그 일거리 때문에 크게 자존심이 상한 모양이지만, 시간이 지나면 그 일을 잘 넘겼기를 바랍니다."], ["She has been disappointed in Borrioboola-Gha, which turned out a failure in consequence of the king of Borrioboola wanting to sell everybody—who survived the climate—for rum, but she has taken up with the rights of women to sit in Parliament, and Caddy tells me it is a mission involving more correspondence than the old one.", "부인은 보리오불라가 일에 실망했는데, 그곳은 보리오불라 왕이(그 풍토에서 살아남은) 사람들을 모조리 럼주에 팔아넘기려 한 탓에 결국 실패로 돌아갔습니다. 그래서 부인은 여성의 의회 진출권 문제를 새로 붙잡았는데, 캐디 말로는 그게 예전 일보다 더 많은 서신 왕래가 필요한 사명이라고 합니다."], ["I had almost forgotten Caddy’s poor little girl.", "캐디의 가엾은 어린 딸을 하마터면 잊을 뻔했습니다."], ["She is not such a mite now, but she is deaf and dumb.", "이제 그 아이는 그렇게 조그맣지는 않지만, 듣지도 말하지도 못합니다."], ["I believe there never was a better mother than Caddy, who learns, in her scanty intervals of leisure, innumerable deaf and dumb arts to soften the affliction of her child.", "나는 캐디만 한 어머니가 또 없으리라 믿습니다. 캐디는 얼마 안 되는 짬짬이 시간에 제 아이의 고통을 덜어주려고 농아를 위한 온갖 기술을 헤아릴 수 없이 익히고 있습니다."]], [["As if I were never to have done with Caddy, I am reminded here of Peepy and old Mr. Turveydrop.", "마치 캐디 이야기는 영영 끝나지 않을 것처럼, 여기서 나는 피피와 늙은 터비드롭 씨가 떠오릅니다."], ["Peepy is in the Custom House, and doing extremely well.", "피피는 세관에서 일하며 아주 잘해내고 있습니다."], ["Old Mr. Turveydrop, very apoplectic, still exhibits his deportment about town, still enjoys himself in the old manner, is still believed in in the old way.", "늙은 터비드롭 씨는 뇌졸중 기운이 심해졌으면서도 여전히 거리에서 우아한 몸가짐을 뽐내고, 여전히 옛날 방식대로 즐기며, 여전히 옛날 방식대로 떠받들어집니다."], ["He is constant in his patronage of Peepy and is understood to have bequeathed him a favourite French clock in his dressing-room—which is not his property.", "그는 한결같이 피피를 후원하는데, 자기 화장방에 있는 아끼는 프랑스 시계를 피피에게 물려주기로 했다고들 합니다. 그런데 그건 그의 것이 아닙니다."]], [["With the first money we saved at home, we added to our pretty house by throwing out a little growlery expressly for my guardian, which we inaugurated with great splendour the next time he came down to see us.", "집에서 처음 모은 돈으로 우리는 오로지 후견인을 위해 작은 투덜방을 덧대어 우리의 아담한 집을 늘렸고, 다음번 그가 우리를 보러 내려왔을 때 성대하게 그곳을 처음으로 열어 보였습니다."], ["I try to write all this lightly, because my heart is full in drawing to an end, but when I write of him, my tears will have their way.", "끝맺음에 다가가니 가슴이 벅차올라 나는 이 모든 것을 가볍게 쓰려고 애씁니다만, 그분에 대해 쓰자니 눈물이 제멋대로 흘러내립니다."]], [["I never look at him but I hear our poor dear Richard calling him a good man.", "나는 그를 바라볼 때마다 우리의 가엾고 사랑스러운 리처드가 그를 두고 좋은 사람이라 부르던 소리를 듣습니다."], ["To Ada and her pretty boy, he is the fondest father; to me he is what he has ever been, and what name can I give to that?", "에이다와 그 예쁜 사내아이에게 그는 더없이 다정한 아버지입니다. 나에게 그는 언제나 그랬던 그 모습 그대로인데, 그것에 무슨 이름을 붙일 수 있을까요?"], ["He is my husband’s best and dearest friend, he is our children’s darling, he is the object of our deepest love and veneration.", "그는 내 남편의 가장 좋고 가장 소중한 벗이며, 우리 아이들이 사랑하는 사람이며, 우리가 가장 깊이 사랑하고 우러르는 분입니다. 그러면서도 나는 그를 마치 더 높은 존재처럼 느끼면서도 그와 너무나 허물없고 편해서, 나 자신이 다 신기할 정도입니다."], ["Yet while I feel towards him as if he were a superior being, I am so familiar with him and so easy with him that I almost wonder at myself.", "나는 옛 이름들을 한 번도 잃은 적이 없고, 그도 제 이름을 잃은 적이 없습니다. 그가 우리와 함께 있을 때면 나는 늘 그의 곁 내 낡은 의자 말고 다른 자리에 앉는 법이 없습니다."], ["I have never lost my old names, nor has he lost his; nor do I ever, when he is with us, sit in any other place than in my old chair at his side, Dame Trot, Dame Durden, Little Woman—all just the same as ever; and I answer, “Yes, dear guardian!”", "종종걸음 아주머니, 더든 아주머니, 꼬마 아낙—모두 예전 그대로입니다. 그리고 나는 \"네, 후견인!\""], ["just the same.", "하고 예전과 똑같이 대답합니다."]], [["I have never known the wind to be in the east for a single moment since the day when he took me to the porch to read the name.", "그가 나를 현관으로 데려가 그 이름을 읽게 한 그날 이후로, 나는 단 한순간도 바람이 동쪽에서 부는 것을 알아챈 적이 없습니다."], ["I remarked to him once that the wind seemed never in the east now, and he said, no, truly; it had finally departed from that quarter on that very day.", "언젠가 나는 그에게 요즘은 바람이 도무지 동쪽에서 부는 것 같지 않다고 말했고, 그는 그렇다고, 정말 그렇다고, 바람은 바로 그날 그 방향에서 영영 떠나버렸다고 했습니다."]], [["I think my darling girl is more beautiful than ever.", "내 사랑하는 그 아이는 어느 때보다 더 아름다워진 것 같습니다."], ["The sorrow that has been in her face—for it is not there now—seems to have purified even its innocent expression and to have given it a diviner quality.", "그녀의 얼굴에 깃들어 있던 슬픔은—이제는 거기에 없습니다만—그 순결한 표정마저 정화하여 더욱 거룩한 기품을 부여한 듯합니다."], ["Sometimes when I raise my eyes and see her in the black dress that she still wears, teaching my Richard, I feel—it is difficult to express—as if it were so good to know that she remembers her dear Esther in her prayers.", "이따금 눈을 들어, 그녀가 아직도 입고 있는 검은 옷차림으로 내 리처드를 가르치는 모습을 보면, 나는 느낍니다—말로 표현하기 어렵지만—그녀가 기도 속에서 사랑하는 에스더를 기억해준다는 걸 안다는 게 얼마나 좋은 일인지를요."]], [["I call him my Richard!", "나는 그 아이를 내 리처드라고 부릅니다!"], ["But he says that he has two mamas, and I am one.", "하지만 그 애는 자기에게 엄마가 둘 있다고, 그중 하나가 나라고 합니다."]], [["We are not rich in the bank, but we have always prospered, and we have quite enough.", "우리는 은행에 돈이 넉넉하지는 않지만 늘 형편이 나아져 왔고, 부족함 없이 살 만큼은 충분히 가졌습니다."], ["I never walk out with my husband but I hear the people bless him.", "나는 남편과 함께 바깥을 거닐 때면 언제나 사람들이 그를 축복하는 소리를 듣습니다."], ["I never go into a house of any degree but I hear his praises or see them in grateful eyes.", "어느 신분의 집에 들어가든 그를 칭송하는 말을 듣거나 고마워하는 눈빛 속에서 그것을 봅니다."], ["I never lie down at night but I know that in the course of that day he has alleviated pain and soothed some fellow-creature in the time of need.", "밤에 잠자리에 누울 때면 언제나, 그날 하루 동안 그가 고통을 덜어주고 절박한 처지에 놓인 어느 동료 인간을 다독여주었음을 압니다."], ["I know that from the beds of those who were past recovery, thanks have often, often gone up, in the last hour, for his patient ministration.", "회복할 가망이 없던 이들의 병상에서, 그 마지막 순간에 그의 끈기 있는 보살핌에 감사하는 마음이 자주, 또 자주 하늘로 올라갔음을 압니다."], ["Is not this to be rich?", "이것이야말로 부유한 게 아니겠습니까?"]], [["The people even praise me as the doctor’s wife.", "사람들은 의사의 아내라며 나마저 칭송합니다."], ["The people even like me as I go about, and make so much of me that I am quite abashed.", "사람들은 내가 돌아다니면 나마저 좋아하고, 나를 어찌나 떠받드는지 도리어 무안할 지경입니다."], ["I owe it all to him, my love, my pride!", "이 모든 것이 다 그 사람 덕입니다, 내 사랑, 내 자랑인 그 사람 말입니다!"], ["They like me for his sake, as I do everything I do in life for his sake.", "사람들이 그 사람 때문에 나를 좋아하듯, 나도 내 삶에서 하는 모든 일을 그 사람을 위해 합니다."]], [["A night or two ago, after bustling about preparing for my darling and my guardian and little Richard, who are coming to-morrow, I was sitting out in the porch of all places, that dearly memorable porch, when Allan came home.", "하루 이틀 전, 내일 내려오기로 한 내 사랑하는 친구와 후견인과 어린 리처드를 맞을 채비를 부산스레 마치고 나서, 나는 하필이면 그 현관에, 더없이 잊지 못할 그 현관에 나와 앉아 있었습니다. 그때 앨런이 집에 돌아왔습니다."], ["So he said, “My precious little woman, what are you doing here?”", "그래서 그가 말했습니다. \"내 소중한 꼬마 아낙, 여기서 뭘 하고 있소?\""], ["And I said, “The moon is shining so brightly, Allan, and the night is so delicious, that I have been sitting here thinking.”", "그래서 나는 말했습니다. \"앨런, 달빛이 어찌나 환하게 비치는지, 밤이 어찌나 감미로운지, 여기 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있었어요.\""]], [["“What have you been thinking about, my dear?”", "\"무슨 생각을 하고 있었소, 여보?\""], ["said Allan then.", "그러자 앨런이 물었습니다."]], [["“How curious you are!” said I.", "\"당신도 참 궁금한 것도 많으셔라!\""], ["“I am almost ashamed to tell you, but I will.", "내가 말했습니다. \"말씀드리기 좀 부끄럽지만, 그래도 할게요."], ["I have been thinking about my old looks—such as they were.”", "내 옛날 얼굴을—그게 어떠했든 간에 말이에요—생각하고 있었어요.\""]], [["“And what have you been thinking about THEM, my busy bee?”", "\"그 얼굴에 대해 무슨 생각을 했소, 내 부지런한 꿀벌 아씨?\""], ["said Allan.", "앨런이 물었습니다."]], [["“I have been thinking that I thought it was impossible that you COULD have loved me any better, even if I had retained them.”", "\"이런 생각을 했어요. 설령 내가 그 얼굴을 그대로 간직했다 한들, 당신이 지금보다 나를 더 사랑할 수는 도저히 없었으리라고요.\""]], [["“‘Such as they were’?”", "\"'그게 어떠했든 간에'라고?\""], ["said Allan, laughing.", "앨런이 웃으며 말했습니다."]], [["“Such as they were, of course.”", "\"물론, 어떠했든 간에 말이에요.\""]], [["“My dear Dame Durden,”", "\"내 사랑하는 더든 아주머니.\""], ["said Allan, drawing my arm through his, “do you ever look in the glass?”", "앨런이 내 팔을 제 팔에 끼우며 말했습니다. \"당신은 거울을 들여다보기는 하오?\""]], [["“You know I do; you see me do it.”", "\"당신도 알잖아요. 내가 그러는 걸 보면서요.\""]], [["“And don’t you know that you are prettier than you ever were?”", "\"그러면 당신이 그 어느 때보다 더 예뻐졌다는 걸 모른단 말이오?\""]], [["I did not know that; I am not certain that I know it now. But I know that my dearest little pets are very pretty, and that my darling is very beautiful, and that my husband is very handsome, and that my guardian has the brightest and most benevolent face that ever was seen, and that they can very well do without much beauty in me—even supposing—.", "나는 그걸 알지 못했습니다. 지금도 그걸 알고 있는지 잘 모르겠습니다. 하지만 나는 압니다. 더없이 사랑스러운 내 아이들이 무척 예쁘고, 내 사랑하는 친구가 무척 아름답고, 내 남편이 무척 잘생겼고, 내 후견인이 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 환하고 자애로운 얼굴을 지녔다는 것을요. 그러니 그들은 내가 그리 아름답지 않아도 얼마든지 잘 지낼 수 있다는 것을—설령 정말 그렇다손 치더라도—."]]];
